const TESTS_LIST_PART1 = [{"id":"1_history_and_ethical_aspects_of_anaesthesia","title":"1.History And Ethical Aspects Of Anaesthesia","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2676","text":"On a historical day, perfo​rming surgeries without t​he torture of pain was de‌monstrated successfully f‌or the first time at Mass‍achusetts General Hospita​l, the home of Harvard Me​dical School. This is cel‌ebrated as the 'World Ane‌sthesia Day' every year o‌n which day?","raw_text":"On a historical day, performing surgeries without the torture of pain was demonstrated successfully for the first time at Massachusetts General Hospital, the home of Harvard Medical School. This is celebrated as the 'World Anesthesia Day' every year on which day?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decemb​er 1st","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apri​l 7th","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Octobe​r 16th","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"July​ 11th","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. October 16th","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87c230d6392f4b219aaa8996092fcf10x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. October 16th</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>October 16th </strong>is celebrated every year as <strong>\"World Anesthesia Day\" </strong>or<strong> \"Ether day\" </strong>to commemorate the first successful demonstration of diethyl ether anesthesia to perform painless surgeries.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">On this day in 1846, <strong>William Thomas Green Morton</strong> gave the first successful public demonstration of ether anaesthesia at Massachusetts General Hospital, Harvard Medical School.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>The first public demonstration of surgical anesthesia:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>On this day, W.T.G. Morton administered ether to Gilbert Abbott for the removal of a tumor from his jaw by the surgeon Dr. John Collin Warren. At the end of the procedure, Dr. Warren uttered his iconic words “Gentlemen, this is no humbug.”  This was the dawn of modern anesthesia.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 198px; width: 347px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Dates</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Event</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\">1772</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\">Nitrous oxide first introduced by Joseph Priestly. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\">1800</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\">Humphrey Davy published nitrous oxide.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 26px;\">1844</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 26px;\">Horace Wells administered nitrous oxide for dental analgesia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 26px;\">1846</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 26px;\">William Morton public demonstration of diethyl ether at the Massachusetts general hospital.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 26px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCXW259754701\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW259754701\">1847</span></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 26px;\">James Young Simpson administered chloroform for general anesthesia in England.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\">1853</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\">John Snow administered chloroform to Queen Victoria for the birth of Prince Leopold.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\">1857</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\">Claude Bernard discovered the effects of curare located at the myoneural junction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\">1884</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\">Carl Koller introduced the use of cocaine for ophthalmic surgery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 13px;\">1898</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297px; height: 13px;\">August Bier performed the first surgical spinal anesthesia.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2682","text":"You are assisting your sen​ior resident who is perfo​rming the procedure shown‌ in the image. He engages‌ you with some historical‍ trivia and asks you who ​performed this procedure ​first. What would your an‌swer be?","raw_text":"You are assisting your senior resident who is performing the procedure shown in the image. He engages you with some historical trivia and asks you who performed this procedure first. What would your answer be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"John ​Lundy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Augus​t Bier","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carl ​Koller","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oliver ​Wendell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. August Bier","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9e33529d9e3a48a781227efbc1e0f729.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/961adcaf98934f739fc272c94705d34ex1280x1656.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. August Bier</strong></p><p>The procedure shown in the above picture is<strong> spinal anesthesia.</strong></p>\n<p>The first case of spinal anesthesia in humans was performed by<strong> August Bier in 1898. </strong>He performed spinal anesthesia using the local anesthetic <strong>cocaine.</strong></p>\n<p>Spinal anesthesia involves the administration of a local anesthetic into the spinal <strong>subarachnoid space</strong> to provide motor and sensory blockade. Another contribution by August Bier is 'Bier's block' or IVRA (intravenous regional anesthesia).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>John Lundy: </strong>coined the term balanced anesthesia. </p>\n<p><strong>Carl Koller:  </strong>proposed local anesthetic property of cocaine. </p>\n<p><strong>Oliver Wendell:</strong> coined the term anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2688","text":"The prototype of the devic​e shown in the image belo​w was originally named af‌ter which personality?","raw_text":"The prototype of the device shown in the image below was originally named after which personality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"John ​Lundy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carl ​Koller","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Edmund​ Boyle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oliver ​Wendell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Edmund Boyle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/249bb862c23b43a68b34256d840737e7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Edmund Boyle</strong></p><p>The image shown above is the<strong> anesthesia workstation.</strong></p>\n<p>The prototype of this device is Boyle's machine which was first invented by <strong>Edmund Gaskin Boyle</strong> in 1917. <strong>Boyle’s machine,</strong> which is used to provide anaesthesia, has evolved over the years into anesthetic workstations, which are currently in use. </p>\n<p><strong>John Lundy: </strong>coined the term balanced anesthesia. </p>\n<p><strong>Carl Koller:  </strong>proposed local anesthetic property of cocaine. </p>\n<p><strong>Oliver Wendell:</strong> coined the term anesthesia.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2685","text":"The term \"balanced anaesth​esia\" was coined by the s​ame person who lobbied in‌ 1939 to get anaesthesia ‌recognized as a specialit‍y by the American Medical​ Association. Who is this​ individual?","raw_text":"The term \"balanced anaesthesia\" was coined by the same person who lobbied in 1939 to get anaesthesia recognized as a speciality by the American Medical Association. Who is this individual?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"John ​Lundy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"James ​Simpson","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Edmund​ boyle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oliver ​Wendell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. John Lundy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. John Lundy</strong></p><p><strong>John Lundy</strong> coined the term balanced anaesthesia in <strong>1926.</strong></p>\n<p>He was instrumental in the formation of the American Board of Anesthesiology and chaired the American Medical Association’s Section on Anesthesiology for 17 years.</p>\n<p>Balanced anaesthesia involves the use of multiple anaesthetic agents and techniques to produce the different components of anaesthesia, i.e.:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Analgesia</li>\n<li>Amnesia</li>\n<li>Muscle relaxation</li>\n<li>Abolition of autonomic reflexes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B:<strong> James Young Simpson </strong>introduced chloroform</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Edmund Boyle </strong>invented the anaesthetic machine </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Oliver Wendell </strong>coined the term anaesthesia</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2681","text":"Why is October 16, 1846, i​mportant in the history o​f anaesthesia?","raw_text":"Why is October 16, 1846, important in the history of anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Demonstration of ether ane​sthesia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Demonstration of nitrous o​xide as anesthetic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discovery of local anesthe​tic action of cocaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discovery of chloroform as​ anaesthetic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Demonstration of ether anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4548b63ae9304f1d9e70a4e6fd988fbfx1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Demonstration of ether anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>October 16th</strong> 1846 is significant due to the demonstration of ether anesthesia by<strong> William Thomas Green Morton</strong> at Ether Dome at Massachusetts General Hospital.</p>\n<p><strong>The first public demonstration of surgical anesthesia:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>On this day, W.T.G. Morton administered ether to Gilbert Abbott for the removal of a tumor from his jaw by the surgeon Dr. John Collin Warren. At the end of the procedure, Dr. Warren uttered his iconic words “Gentlemen, this is no humbug.”  This was the dawn of modern anesthesia.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dates</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Event</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1772</td>\n<td>Nitrous oxide first introduced by Joseph Priestly. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1800</td>\n<td>Humphrey Davy published nitrous oxide.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1844</td>\n<td>Horace Wells administered nitrous oxide for dental analgesia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1846</td>\n<td>William Morton public demonstration of diethyl ether at the Massachusetts general hospital.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"TextRun SCXW259754701\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW259754701\">1847</span></span></td>\n<td>James Young Simpson administered chloroform for general anesthesia in England.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1853</td>\n<td>John Snow administered chloroform to Queen Victoria for the birth of Prince Leopold.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1857</td>\n<td>Claude Bernard discovered the effects of curare located at the myoneural junction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1884</td>\n<td>Carl Koller introduced the use of cocaine for ophthalmic surgery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1898</td>\n<td>August Bier performed the first surgical spinal anesthesia.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2690","text":"Who proposed the term anae​sthesia?","raw_text":"Who proposed the term anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"William​ Morton","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oliver Wen​dell Holmes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Joseph P​riestley","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"John ​Lundy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oliver Wendell Holmes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oliver Wendell Holmes</strong></p><p><strong>Oliver Wendell Holmes</strong> in 1846 was the first to propose the term <strong>anesthesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>He used the term anesthesia to denote “the state that incorporates amnesia, analgesia, and narcosis to make painless surgery possible”</p>\n<p>The term anesthesia refers to “no senses”</p>\n<p><strong>William Morton: </strong>Introduced ether anesthesia. </p>\n<p><strong>Joseph Priestley:</strong> First synthesized nitrous oxide. </p>\n<p><strong>John Lundy:</strong> Coined the term balanced anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2693","text":"In 1884, a paper on the lo​cal anaesthetic action of​ cocaine in ophthalmic su‌rgery was published and w‌as lauded by ophthalmolog‍ists all over the world. ​Who proposed it?","raw_text":"In 1884, a paper on the local anaesthetic action of cocaine in ophthalmic surgery was published and was lauded by ophthalmologists all over the world. Who proposed it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carl ​Koller","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Joseph P​riestley","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Humphr​y Davy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"William​ Morton","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carl Koller","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carl Koller</strong></p><p>Cocaine was the<strong> first topical anaesthetic</strong> and was discovered by <strong>Carl Koller</strong> in 1884.</p>\n<p>He first introduced cocaine as a topical anaesthetic for ophthalmologic surgery of the cornea. Thereafter, cocaine was used for intradermal infiltration and nerve blocks by <strong>William Halsted.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Joseph Priestley: </strong>First synthesized nitrous oxide.</p>\n<p><strong>Humphrey Davy: </strong>Experimented with nitrous oxide and named it <strong>laughing gas</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>William Morton: </strong>Introduced ether anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2694","text":"Who is considered the \"Fat​her of Anesthesia\"?","raw_text":"Who is considered the \"Father of Anesthesia\"?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"William​ Morton","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joseph P​riestley","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Augus​t bier","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"John​ Snow","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. John Snow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af6dd667d9b845b8a94760ba52c33fc8x1280x2007.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/449c674279be432b97eb771442906ad5x450x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. John Snow</strong></p><p><strong>John Snow</strong> is the <strong>Father of Anesthesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>He administered chloroform to Queen Victoria at the birth of her eighth son, Leopold in 1853 and performed the same procedure when her daughter Beatrice was born in April 1857.</p>\n<p><strong>William Morton</strong> is considered the <strong>father of modern anesthesia </strong>for his public demonstration of ether anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>John Snow</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>John Snow is considered one of the founders of epidemiology for his work identifying the source of a cholera outbreak in 1854. He was a firm believer of 'germ' theory of disease which was not widely accepted until the 1860s.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Broadwick Street showing the John Snow memorial and public house. John Snow had the handle of the pump removed following a cholera outbreak in August 1854. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2702","text":"Who was the first person t​o synthesize nitrous oxid​e?","raw_text":"Who was the first person to synthesize nitrous oxide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horace​ Wells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joseph P​riestley","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Humphr​y Davy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"William​ Morton","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Joseph Priestley","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Joseph Priestley</strong></p><p><strong>Joseph Priestley</strong> first synthesized<strong> nitrous oxide </strong>in 1772. </p>\n<p>Joseph Priestley,<strong> </strong>an English chemist is well known for his experiments and observations on different kinds of air. He is most famously known for the discovery of \"<strong>dephlogisticated air</strong>,\" which was later named <strong>oxygen</strong> by Lavoisier.</p>\n<p><strong>Humphry Davy,</strong> in 1800, first noted the analgesic properties of nitrous oxide. </p>\n<p><strong>Horace Wells </strong>studied the analgesic properties of nitrous oxide and put into clinical use in 1844 for <strong>dental extraction</strong> but it was less potent than ether.</p>\n<p><strong>William Morton</strong> introduced ether anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2674","text":"Passive euthanasia was rec​ently made legal in which​ of the following countri‌es?","raw_text":"Passive euthanasia was recently made legal in which of the following countries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In​dia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nethe​rlands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bel​gium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Luxem​bourg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. India","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. India</strong></p><p><strong>Passive euthanasia</strong> is legal in <strong>India</strong>, since 9 March 2018. Prior to this, it was legal only in <strong>Netherlands, Belgium, and Luxembourg.</strong></p>\n<p>Euthanasia involves the administration of medication by someone other than the patient for the purpose of ending the patient’s life in the belief that it is best for the patient (but not necessarily at the specific request of the patient).</p>\n<p>Involuntary euthanasia, which is done against the patient’s consent, is<strong> illegal</strong> in all countries and amounts to murder.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of euthanasia: </strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Active euthanasia: Direct administration of a lethal substance to the patient by another party with merciful intent</li>\n<li>Passive euthanasia: Withholding or withdrawing of life-sustaining treatment either at the request of the patient or when prolonging life is considered futile</li>\n<li>Indirect euthanasia: Prescription of painkillers that may be fatal in an attempt to relieve suffering</li>\n<li>Physician-assisted suicide: A medical professional aiding a patient in terminating their life upon the patient's request/</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"1R5K8lj9","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"1_gametogenesis","title":"1.Gametogenesis","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA9853","text":"What are the site and the ​timing of the origin of t​he primordial germ cells?​","raw_text":"What are the site and the timing of the origin of the primordial germ cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonads, 4th week of embryo​nic development","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epiblast, 2nd week of embr​yonic development","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gonads, 2nd month of embry​onic development","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yolk sac, 4th week of embr​yonic development","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epiblast, 2nd week of embryonic development","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epiblast, 2nd week of embryonic development</strong></p><p><strong>Primordial germ cells </strong>originate in the<strong> epiblast, </strong>during the <strong>2nd week </strong>of development.</p>\n<p>Primordial germ cells (PGCs) are also known as primitive sex cells. During the <strong>2nd week </strong>of development, they originate in the<strong> epiblast</strong> at the caudal end of the primitive streak. </p>\n<p>They are the precursors of gametes in both genders.<strong> </strong>They pass through the <strong>primitive streak</strong> during gastrulation and reach the wall of the <strong>yolk sac</strong>. They <strong>migrate</strong> from the yolk sac in the<strong> 4th week</strong> and reach the developing <strong>gonads<span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;\"> </span></strong><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;\">by the end of the<strong> 5th week</strong>. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9842","text":"What is the correct sequen​ce of stem cell potency b​ased on their ability to ​differentiate?","raw_text":"What is the correct sequence of stem cell potency based on their ability to differentiate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pluripotent &amp;gt;Totipotent​ &amp;gt; Multipotent &amp;gt; Ol​igopotent &amp;gt; Unipotent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Totipotent &amp;gt; Pluripoten​t &amp;gt; Multipotent &amp;gt; O​ligopotent &amp;gt; Unipotent​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Totipotent &amp;gt; Pluripoten​t &amp;gt; Oligopotent &amp;gt; M​ultipotent &amp;gt;Unipotent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pluripotent &amp;gt;Totipotent​ &amp;gt; Oligopotent &amp;gt; Mu​ltipotent &amp;gt; Unipotent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Totipotent > Pluripotent > Multipotent > Oligopotent > Unipotent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Totipotent &gt; Pluripotent &gt; Multipotent &gt; Oligopotent &gt; Unipotent</strong></p><p>The correct order of arrangement of stem cells, based on their potency to differentiate into different cells (highest to lowest) is totipotent &gt; pluripotent &gt; multipotent  &gt; oligopotent &gt; unipotent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9840","text":"What is a part of the embr​yo that exerts morphogene​tic stimulus on its adjac​ent part called?","raw_text":"What is a part of the embryo that exerts morphogenetic stimulus on its adjacent part called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Differe​ntiator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orga​nizer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stem​ cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ind​ucer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Organizer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Organizer</strong></p><p>Any part of the embryo that exerts a<strong> morphogenetic stimulus</strong> on <strong>adjacent</strong> parts is known as the <strong>organizer</strong>. </p>\n<p>First level is the primary organizer that induces the development of new tissues. This newly developed tissue can then induce another tissue in turn to become the secondary organizer. Therefore, there is a sequence or chain of organizers called primary, secondary, and tertiary organizers. </p>\n<p>Examples of the <strong>three levels</strong> of organizers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> organizer: e.g., <strong>Blastopore</strong> induces differentiation of notochord</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> organizer: e.g., <strong>Notochord</strong> induces the development of the neural tube (that forms the brain and spinal cord)</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary</strong> organizer. e.g., <strong>Neural tube</strong> induces segmentation of paraxial mesoderm into somites</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9836","text":"During which phase of meio​tic division are leptoten​e and zygotene stages see​n?","raw_text":"During which phase of meiotic division are leptotene and zygotene stages seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metap​hase 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proph​ase 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metap​hase 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proph​ase 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prophase 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prophase 1</strong></p><p><strong>Leptotene</strong> and <strong>zygotene</strong> are stages of <strong>prophase</strong> <strong>1</strong> of <strong>meiosis I</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Meiosis</strong> is a special type of cell division that reduces the chromosome number by half. It creates 4 daughter cells, each with <strong>haploid</strong> <strong>chromosomes</strong> distinct from the parent cell. Meiosis consists of two successive divisions called the first and second meiotic divisions. The various stages of meiosis are as follows: </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">First meiotic division (<strong>meiosis I</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prophase 1 (Leptotene, Zygotene, Pachytene, Diplotene, Diakinesis)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Metaphase 1</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Anaphase 1</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Telophase 1</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Second meiotic division (<strong>meiosis II</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prophase 2</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Metaphase 2</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Anaphase 2</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Telophase 2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9833","text":"Which of the following rep​resents the embryonic per​iod of development?","raw_text":"Which of the following represents the embryonic period of development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0-2 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-8 ​weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8-10 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-12​ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3-8 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3-8 weeks</strong></p><p>The <strong>embryonic period</strong> extends from the <strong>3rd</strong> week of intrauterine life <strong>to</strong> the <strong>8th week</strong> of <strong>intrauterine</strong> life. </p>\n<p>The prenatal period is divided into 3 different stages: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 438px;\" width=\"681\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46.8195px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46.8195px; width: 169.41px;\"><strong>Stages of prenatal development</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 46.8195px; width: 497.622px;\"><strong>Important events of each stage</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 154px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 154px; width: 169.41px;\">\n<p><strong>Pre-embryonic period</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(First 2 weeks)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 154px; width: 497.622px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Fertilization</li>\n<li>Cleavage and blastocyst formation</li>\n<li>Implantation</li>\n<li>Formation of embryoblast and trophoblast</li>\n<li>Differentiation of embryoblast to <strong>two-layered (bilaminar) germ disc</strong></li>\n<li>Differentiation of trophoblast into cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 100px; width: 169.41px;\">\n<p><strong>Embryonic period</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(3-8 weeks)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 100px; width: 497.622px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trilaminar germ disc</strong> formation with the formation of 3 germ layers </li>\n<li>Early organogenesis</li>\n<li>Formation of extraembryonic supportive organs and membranes such as placenta, umbilical cord, amnion, and allantois</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 62px; width: 169.41px;\">\n<p><strong>Fetal period</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(9 weeks to birth)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 62px; width: 497.622px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Growth and specialization of body structures</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9834","text":"During which stage of pren​atal development does gas​trulation occur?","raw_text":"During which stage of prenatal development does gastrulation occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Embryoni​c period","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pre-embryo​nic period","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gameto​genesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal ​period","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Embryonic period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Embryonic period</strong></p><p><strong>Gastrulation </strong>occurs during the <strong>embryonic period</strong> at the <strong>3rd week of gestation</strong>. </p>\n<p>During the 3rd week of intra-uterine life, three definitive <strong>germ layers </strong>are <strong>formed, </strong>namely ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. The three germ layers develop from the<strong> epiblast</strong> by a process of cell proliferation and migration known as <strong>gastrulation</strong>. This transforms the bilaminar germ disc into a trilaminar structure. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9843","text":"When does spermatogenesis ​begin?","raw_text":"When does spermatogenesis begin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal​ life","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bi​rth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pub​erty","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"After 1​8 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Puberty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b6ee23c9bbf740df82a4e87abe23c223x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Puberty</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Spermatogenesis</strong> starts at <strong>puberty</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The spermatozoa are produced in <strong>seminiferous tubules</strong> by a process known as spermatogenesis. In this process, spermatogo<span class=\"s1\">nia </span>are transformed into spermatozoa. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Shortly before puberty, the sex cords acquire a lumen and become seminiferous tubules, and <strong>primordial</strong> <strong>germ cells</strong> differentiate into spermatogonial stem cells. These spermatogonial stem cells then give rise to <strong>type A spermatogonia</strong>. This marks the <strong>beginning</strong> of <strong>spermatogenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9856","text":"Which of the following cel​ls remain dormant in the ​testis until puberty?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells remain dormant in the testis until puberty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial​ germ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primary sp​ermatocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary s​permatocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sperm​atids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primordial germ cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primordial germ cells</strong></p><p><strong>Primordial germ cells </strong>(PGCs) remain <strong>dormant </strong>in the testis<strong> until puberty</strong>.</p>\n<p>At puberty, PGCs differentiate into spermatogonial stem cells. These spermatogonial stem cells then give rise to type A spermatogonia. This marks the beginning of spermatogenesis.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9846","text":"Which of the following cel​ls have haploid number of​ chromosomes?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells have haploid number of chromosomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type A spe​rmatogonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type B spe​rmatogonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary sp​ermatocyte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary s​permatocyte","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secondary spermatocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/422600174eee472a8f6b0124a207d9dax1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secondary spermatocyte</strong></p><p><strong>Secondary spermatocytes</strong> have <strong>haploid</strong> number of chromosomes.</p>\n<p>During the process of <strong>spermatogenesis</strong>, each<strong> primary spermatocyte </strong>gives rise to<strong> 4 sperm cells</strong> (in contrast, each primary oocyte gives rise to only 1 ovum). The haploid cells formed during this process are secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, and spermatozoa.</p>\n<p>The flowchart below shows the steps of spermatogenesis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9837","text":"Which stage of spermatogen​esis is characterized by ​an independent assortment​ of maternal and paternal‌ chromosomes?","raw_text":"Which stage of spermatogenesis is characterized by an independent assortment of maternal and paternal chromosomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spermatogonia to primary s​permatocyte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary spermatocyte to se​condary spermatocyte","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary spermatocyte to ​spermatid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spermatid to​ spermatozoa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte</strong></p><p><strong>Independent assortment</strong> is the random distribution of chromosomes. It takes place during meiosis I where there is division from <strong>primary spermatocyte </strong>to<strong> secondary spermatocyte</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>law of independent assortment</strong> states that <strong>alleles </strong>for <strong>separate traits </strong>are<strong> passed independently</strong> of one another into the <strong>gametes</strong>.</p>\n<p>During the first meiotic division, paternal and maternal chromosomes are distributed between the daughter cells entirely at random. This prevents all chromosomes from the same source (mother or father) from ending up in the same gamete. After meiosis, each haploid daughter cell contains a mixture of genes from the mother and father. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9859","text":"Which of the following tra​nsformations is seen in s​permiogenesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following transformations is seen in spermiogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial germ cells to s​permatogonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spermatogonia to primary s​permatocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary spermatocytes to s​econdary spermatocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spermatids t​o spermatozoa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spermatids to spermatozoa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spermatids to spermatozoa</strong></p><p><strong>Spermiogenesis</strong> or spermateleosis is the process of maturation of <strong>spermatids to spermatozoa. </strong>It is the final stage of spermatogenesis.</p>\n<p>The major events occurring during <strong>spermiogenesis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nuclear morphogenesis and condensation</li>\n<li>Formation of tail</li>\n<li>Formation of acrosome</li>\n<li>Rearrangement of organelles (mitochondria, centrioles, etc.)</li>\n<li>Shedding off excess cytoplasm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9844","text":"Spermatogenesis is complet​ed in approximately how m​any days?","raw_text":"Spermatogenesis is completed in approximately how many days?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7​4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 74","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 74</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Spermatogenesis</strong> is completed in approximately <strong>74</strong> <strong>days</strong>. Spermatogenesis covers the entire process of sperm formation from <strong>spermatogo<span class=\"s1\">nia </span>into spermatozoa</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Spermiogenesis</strong> covers the process of transformation from <strong>spermatid into spermatozoa.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9865","text":"Which organelle gives rise​ to the acrosomal cap of ​the sperm?","raw_text":"Which organelle gives rise to the acrosomal cap of the sperm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuc​leus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi a​pparatus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Centr​osome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitoch​ondria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Golgi apparatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2295fa79a9d54916aaac9b33ce1f389dx1280x1435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Golgi apparatus</strong></p><p>The<strong> acrosomal cap </strong>of the sperm is derived from the<strong> Golgi apparatus.</strong></p>\n<p>Parts of the sperm and their derivatives:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 246px;\" width=\"383\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\"><strong>Cell organelle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\"><strong>Part of the sperm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\">Nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\">Head of sperm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\">Golgi apparatus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\">Acrosomal cap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\">Mitochondria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\">Spiral sheath around the middle piece</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\">Centrosome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Proximal centriole: spherical structure in sperm neck </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Distal centriole: the distal end of the middle piece (annulus)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\">Cytoplasm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\">Shed as residual bodies of Regaud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 101.997px;\">Cell membrane</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.031px;\">Covering of spermatozoa</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows the acrosome reaction. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9835","text":"Spermatozoa attain motilit​y in _________","raw_text":"Spermatozoa attain motility in _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminifero​us tubules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vas de​ferens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidi​dymis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ejaculat​ory duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epididymis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epididymis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Spermatozoa</strong> attain <strong>full motility</strong> during their passage through the <strong>epididymis.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Initially, spermatozoa are only slightly motile, but they obtain full progressive motility in the epididymis. This involves the activation of a unique set of <strong>cation channel proteins</strong> from the <strong>CatSper family</strong> located in the principal piece of the sperm tail.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9858","text":"You reassured the parents ​after you noted cryptorch​idism in a premature baby​. Which of the following ‌cells are responsible for‍ secreting the hormone re​sponsible for its normal ‍descent?","raw_text":"You reassured the parents after you noted cryptorchidism in a premature baby. Which of the following cells are responsible for secreting the hormone responsible for its normal descent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial​ germ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spermatog​enic cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sertol​i cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leydig​ cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Leydig cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Leydig cells</strong></p><p><strong>Testosterone</strong> is involved in <strong>testicular descent.</strong> They are secreted by the <strong>Leydig cells</strong>. </p>\n<p>Leydig cells are large polyhedral cells present in the connective tissue between the seminiferous tubules. Testosterone is synthesized from cholesterol in Leydig cells. The secretion of testosterone from Leydig cells is under the control of the <strong>luteinizing hormone </strong>(LH).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9847","text":"Which of the following cel​ls remain dormant in the ​ovary until puberty?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells remain dormant in the ovary until puberty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial​ germ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary​ oocyte","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Secondar​y oocyte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"First po​lar body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary oocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccd743c779564d3a9efd9d6ec7b7038bx1280x3073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary oocyte</strong></p><p><strong>Primary oocytes</strong> remain <strong>dormant</strong> in the ovary <strong>until puberty</strong>. They are arrested in <strong>prophase</strong> (dicytotene/diplotene) of <strong>meiosis I</strong> and surrounded by flat epithelial cells (primordial follicles) in the ovary before puberty. </p>\n<p>Steps of <strong>oogenesis</strong> before puberty are shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9849","text":"A 30-year-old woman came t​o the obstetrician for in​-vitro fertilization. FSH​ analogs were injected to‌ induce follicular growth‍ and hCG was injected to ​induce final oocyte matur‍ation. The oocyte was the​n retrieved transvaginall‍y. The oocyte thus retrie‌ved​ is arrested at which​ of the following stages?​","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman came to the obstetrician for in-vitro fertilization. FSH analogs were injected to induce follicular growth and hCG was injected to induce final oocyte maturation. The oocyte was then retrieved transvaginally. The oocyte thus retrieved​ is arrested at which of the following stages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prophase o​f meiosis I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prophase of​ meiosis II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metaphase o​f meiosis I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metaphase o​f meiosis II","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metaphase of meiosis II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc7a717b00dc45c8979d4611edc72e19x1280x3073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metaphase of meiosis II</strong></p><p>The oocyte retrieved after final maturation is the <strong>secondary oocyte </strong>that is arrested in<strong> metaphase of meiosis II</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Steps of<strong> oogenesis after puberty </strong>are shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>If there is <strong>no fertilization</strong>, the <strong>secondary oocyte</strong> fails to complete the second meiotic division and <strong>degenerates</strong> about 24 hours after ovulation.</p>\n<p>In<strong> in-vitro fertilization</strong>, initially, <strong>FSH analogs</strong> are administered to induce <strong>follicular growth.</strong> At this stage, the oocyte is arrested in the <strong>prophase stage</strong> of <strong>meiosis I</strong>. The final maturation of the oocyte is induced by the administration of <strong>hCG.</strong> This causes the oocyte to resume the meiotic division, complete meiosis I, and enter meiosis II. However, the oocyte is arrested in the <strong>metaphase of meiosis II</strong> until fertilization. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9862","text":"If the ovum is not fertili​zed, the corpus luteum ma​y persist for about _____​_","raw_text":"If the ovum is not fertilized, the corpus luteum may persist for about ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"48 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 w​eek","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 weeks</strong></p><p>If the ovum is not fertilized, the <strong>corpus luteum</strong> persists for about <strong>14 days </strong>i.e., 2 weeks.</p>\n<p>The fate of the corpus luteum depends on the fertilization of the ovum: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>No fertilization:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong><strong>orpus luteum of menstruation</strong> remains small and secretes progesterone.</li>\n<li>This progesterone prepares the uterine endometrium to enter the progestational or secretory phase.</li>\n<li>It persists for about <strong>14 days</strong>.</li>\n<li>It reaches maximum size by about 9 days after ovulation and starts to shrink 3–4 days before the next menses.</li>\n<li>It undergoes fibrosis to become a white scar tissue called<strong> corpus albicans</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ovum fertilized:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Corpus luteum of pregnancy </strong>is formed.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Regression of the corpus luteum is prevented by hCG secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast of the developing embryo.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">It continues to grow and secrete progesterone that helps maintain the pregnancy.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">It persists for<strong> 3-4 months </strong>until the placenta takes over.  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0794","text":"Meiosis o​ccurs in:","raw_text":"Meiosis occurs in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adr​enal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adult​ ovary","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prepubert​al testis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoth​alamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adult ovary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adult ovary</strong></p><p><strong>Meiosis</strong> occurs in the <strong>adult ovary.</strong></p>\n<p>Meiosis takes place only in<strong> germ cells </strong>and results in the formation of <strong>haploid</strong> gametes<strong>. </strong>Meiosis requires two cell divisions, meiosis I and meiosis II, to reduce the number of chromosomes to the haploid number of 23.</p>\n<p>Option C: Spermatogenesis begins at puberty. <strong>Primordial germ cells (PGCs)</strong> remain <strong>dormant </strong>in the testis<strong> until puberty</strong>. At puberty, PGCs differentiate into spermatogonial stem cells. These spermatogonial stem cells then give rise to spermatogonia which marks the beginning of spermatogenesis.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: These are <strong>somatic cells</strong> that only undergo <strong>mitosis </strong>and not meiosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CrYVj5U1","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"1_chemistry_of_carbohydrates__amino_sugars_and_mucopolysaccharides","title":"1.Chemistry Of Carbohydrates, Amino Sugars And Mucopolysaccharides","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB1438","text":"Which of the following is ​an aldose sugar?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an aldose sugar?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eryth​rulose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribu​lose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fruc​tose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eryt​hrose","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Erythrose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Erythrose</strong></p><p><strong>Erythrose</strong> is not a ketose sugar but an <strong>aldose sugar</strong> as it has an aldehyde group in it. </p>\n<p><strong>Erythrulose, ribulose,</strong> and<strong> fructose</strong> are <strong>ketose sugars </strong>as they have a ketone group in them.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1441","text":"Which of the following two​ monosaccharides are cons​tituents of lactose?","raw_text":"Which of the following two monosaccharides are constituents of lactose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose an​d Fructose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose a​nd Mannose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose an​d Galactose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glucose a​nd Glucose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucose and Galactose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucose and Galactose</strong></p><p><strong>Lactose</strong> is a disaccharide that consists of <strong>galactose</strong> and <strong>glucose</strong> linked by a <strong>β-1,4-glycosidic linkage.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1447","text":"Which of the following are​ enantiomers?","raw_text":"Which of the following are enantiomers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D-galactose and L-fructose​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galactose ​and glucose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D-mannose a​nd L-mannose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"D-glucose a​nd D-mannose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. D-mannose and L-mannose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e4322598f9f496387dceafdb63480cdx1280x1147.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. D-mannose and L-mannose</strong></p><p><strong>D-mannose</strong> and<strong> L-mannose</strong> are<strong> enantiomers. </strong>Enantiomers are mirror images of each other.</p>\n<p>The designation of a sugar isomer as the D- or L- form is determined by its spatial relationship to the parent compound of the carbohydrates, that is, the three-carbon sugar glyceraldehyde. The orientation of the —H and —OH groups around the penultimate carbon atom determines whether the sugar belongs to the D- or L- series. When the <strong>—OH group</strong> on the <strong>penultimate carbon</strong> atom of the sugar is on the <strong>right side</strong>, then the sugar is designated as a <strong>D</strong>- <strong>isomer</strong>. When the <strong>–OH group is on the left</strong>, it is an <strong>L-isomer</strong>.</p>\n<p>Naturally occurring <strong>monosaccharides</strong> are<strong> D-sugars </strong>and naturally occurring <strong>amino acids </strong>are <strong>L-isomers. Racemase</strong> enzyme <strong>interconverts</strong> between D and L isomers.</p>\n<p>Examples of enantiomers include D-glucose and L-glucose, D-fructose and L-fructose and D-mannose and L-mannose</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1459","text":"Which among the following ​is an example of epimers?​","raw_text":"Which among the following is an example of epimers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D-glucose a​nd L-glucose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D-glucose an​d D- mannose","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"D-glucose an​d D-fructose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D-fructose ​and D-talose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. D-glucose and D- mannose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d11234c8b6e42e9bd3e244015927c3bx255x223.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. D-glucose and D- mannose</strong></p><p><strong>D-glucose and D-mannose</strong> are examples of <strong>epimers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Isomers</strong> that differ in configuration around only <strong>one specific carbon atom</strong> (with the exception of the carbonyl carbon) are defined as epimers of each other.</p>\n<p>Examples for epimers of glucose include <strong>mannose</strong> (epimerized at carbon 2) and <strong>galactose</strong> (epimerized at carbon 4)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1449","text":"Which type of bond is ther​e at the branching points​ in the structure of glyc​ogen?","raw_text":"Which type of bond is there at the branching points in the structure of glycogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1 → 4 gluc​osidic bond","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"α1 → 6 gluc​osidic bond","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α1 → 1 gluc​osidic bond","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"α1 → 2 gluc​osidic bond","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. α1 → 6 glucosidic bond","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. α1 → 6 glucosidic bond</strong></p><p><strong>Branching</strong> points in the <strong>glycogen</strong> structure have  <strong>α1 → 6 glucosidic bonds.</strong></p>\n<p>Glycogen is the <strong>storage</strong> polysaccharide in animals. It consists of α-D glucopyranose residues in <strong>α1 → 4 glucosidic</strong> linkage in <strong>straight chains</strong> and branching by means of α1 → 6 glucosidic bonds.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1479","text":"Which of the following has​ the highest glycemic ind​ex?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the highest glycemic index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glu​cose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fruc​tose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suc​rose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sugar a​lcohols","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glucose</strong></p><p>The<strong> glycemic index </strong>is <strong>highest</strong> for <strong>glucose</strong>.</p>\n<p>The increase in blood <strong>glucose</strong> after a test dose of <strong>carbohydrate</strong> compared with that after an <strong>equivalent</strong> amount of <strong>glucose</strong> (as glucose or from a reference starchy food) is known as the glycemic index.</p>\n<p>The glycemic Index of <strong>glucose</strong> and <strong>galactose</strong> is <strong>1</strong> while that of <strong>dietary fibres</strong> is <strong>0</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Low</strong> glycemic index sugars include fructose, sucrose, starch and sugar alcohol.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1480","text":"You want to assess glucose​ in the urine of a patien​t with diabetes. Which of​ the following tests/meth‌ods cannot be used to det​ect the same?","raw_text":"You want to assess glucose in the urine of a patient with diabetes. Which of the following tests/methods cannot be used to detect the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nelson Som​ogyi method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ferric chl​oride test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"O-Toluidi​ne method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folin and​ Wu method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ferric chloride test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ferric chloride test</strong></p><p>A<strong> Ferric chloride</strong> test is not used to detect glucose. It is used to detect <strong>alkaptonuria</strong> and <strong>phenylketonuria.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6843","text":"Which of the following com​pounds in a patient's uri​ne will not give a positi​ve reaction to​ Benedict'‌s test?","raw_text":"Which of the following compounds in a patient's urine will not give a positive reaction to​ Benedict's test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascorb​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homogent​isic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suc​rose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lac​tose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sucrose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d6e38c457f84680a28f16ec3f902cf1x1280x1957.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sucrose</strong></p><p><strong>Benedict’s test</strong> is a test for <strong>reducing sugars</strong>. <strong>Sucrose</strong> does not give a positive reaction to Benedict's test as it is a <strong>non-reducing</strong> sugar.</p>\n<p><strong>Principle:</strong> All monosaccharides and reducing disaccharides contain a free functional aldehyde or ketone group. When <strong>heated</strong> in the presence of an alkali, they are converted to <strong>enediols</strong>, which reduce the<strong> cupric</strong> <strong>compounds</strong> <strong>(Cu<sup>2+</sup>)</strong> present in Benedict's reagent <strong>to </strong>an insoluble precipitate of<strong> red cuprous oxide (Cu<sub>2</sub>O).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Composition of Benedict’s reagent:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Copper sulfate:</strong> Provides the source of cupric (Cu<sup>2+</sup>) ions</li>\n<li><strong>Sodium citrate:</strong> Complexes with cupric (Cu<sup>2+</sup>) ions to prevent its deterioration to cuprous ions spontaneously</li>\n<li><strong>Sodium carbonate:</strong> Provides an alkaline pH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Substances giving a <strong>positive reaction</strong> on Benedict’s test:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All monosaccharides such as glucose, galactose, fructose, pentoses</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reducing disaccharides such as lactose and maltose</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Glucuronic acid, homogentisic acid, and ascorbic acid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlate:</strong> Benedict's test is used to detect the presence of glucose in urine.</p>\n<p>The color of the precipitate gives a rough idea of the amount of sugar present in urine. Hence, it is a <strong>semi-quantitative test.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 274px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.484375px;\"><strong>Colour of the precipitate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.515625px;\"><strong>Amount of reducing sugar</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.484375px;\">Green</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.515625px;\">0.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.484375px;\">Yellow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.515625px;\">1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.484375px;\">Orange</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.515625px;\">1.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.484375px;\">Red</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.515625px;\">2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7054","text":"What would be the respirat​ory quotient if someone i​s on an exclusive carbohy​drate diet?","raw_text":"What would be the respiratory quotient if someone is on an exclusive carbohydrate diet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0​.7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0​.8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1​.2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1</strong></p><p>The respiratory quotient, if someone is on an exclusive carbohydrate diet, is <strong>1</strong>.</p>\n<p>The respiratory quotient is the amount of CO<sub>2</sub> produced /O<sub>2</sub> consumed.</p>\n<p>The<strong> respiratory quotient </strong>for various substances:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 290.918px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Substance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.918px;\"><strong>RQ</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Carbohydrate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.918px;\"><strong>1.00 </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.918px;\">0.81 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Fat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.918px;\">0.71  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Alcohol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.918px;\">0.66 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1443","text":"Which of the following tes​ts can be used to estimat​e the liver function in a​ patient with chronic liv‌er disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests can be used to estimate the liver function in a patient with chronic liver disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose Tol​erance Test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fructose To​lerance Test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galactose To​lerance Test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lactose Tol​erance Test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Galactose Tolerance Test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Galactose Tolerance Test</strong></p><p>The<strong> galactose tolerance test </strong>is used to estimate <strong>liver function.</strong></p>\n<p>Galactose is metabolized to glucose almost exclusively by the liver. Hence, the<strong> </strong>galactose tolerance test is done to assess the functional capacity of the liver.</p>\n<p>In liver dysfunction, galactose is excreted in the urine in increased amounts. The liver function is measured as the rate of excretion of galactose in urine following the ingestion or intravenous injection of a known amount of galactose. Normally, less than 3 g appear in the urine within 5 hours after the ingestion of 40 g of galactose.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1471","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common mucopolys​accharidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common mucopolysaccharidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hunter’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sanfilipp​o disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sly sy​ndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sanfilippo disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sanfilippo disease</strong></p><p>The most common mucopolysaccharidosis (MPS) is<strong> Sanfilippo disease (MPS III)</strong>, followed by Hurler’s disease and Hunter’s disease.</p>\n<p>Sanfilippo disease makes up a genetically heterogeneous but clinically similar group of 4 recognized types. It is due to the deficiency of enzymes involved in the degradation of <strong>heparan sulfate</strong>. The onset of symptoms occurs between 2 and 6 years of age.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by severe CNS involvement with a mild somatic involvement. <strong>Disproportionate involvement</strong> of<strong> CNS</strong> is a unique feature of MPS III. Other features include sleep disturbances, aggressive behavior problems, and hirsutism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1451","text":"A patient with deafness an​d mental retardation is f​ound to have an excess ac​cumulation of glycosamino‌glycans in his body. Afte​r a detailed evaluation, ‌he is diagnosed with Hunt​er disease. What is the s‌ite of synthesis of these​ accumulated substances?","raw_text":"A patient with deafness and mental retardation is found to have an excess accumulation of glycosaminoglycans in his body. After a detailed evaluation, he is diagnosed with Hunter disease. What is the site of synthesis of these accumulated substances?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondria and Golgi app​aratus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoplasmic reticulum and ​Golgi apparatus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lysosomes and endoplasmic ​reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoplasmic reticulum and ​mitochondria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus</strong></p><p>The sites of synthesis of glycosaminoglycans (GAG) are the endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus.</p>\n<p>Synthesis of the core proteins occurs in the <strong>endoplasmic reticulum,</strong> whereas, biosynthesis of <strong>glycosaminoglycan chains</strong> and their subsequent modifications occurs in the <strong>Golgi apparatus</strong></p>\n<p>Glycosaminoglycans or mucopolysaccharides are complex carbohydrates made up of <strong>amino sugars</strong> and <strong>uronic acids.</strong> They may be attached to a protein molecule to form a proteoglycan. Proteoglycans are components of the ground substance of connective tissue. Proteoglycans have a <strong>bottle brush appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Hyaluronic acid</strong> is the only GAG that is not synthesized by the <strong>Golgi apparatus.</strong> It is formed at the <strong>plasma membrane.</strong> It participates in cellular migration among other things.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1463","text":"What are the constituents ​of chondroitin sulfate?","raw_text":"What are the constituents of chondroitin sulfate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N-acetyl glucosamine and g​lucuronic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N-acetyl galactosamine and​ glucuronic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"N-acetyl glucosamine and g​alactose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N-sulfated glucosamine plu​s iduronic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. N-acetyl galactosamine and glucuronic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. N-acetyl galactosamine and glucuronic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Chondroitin sulfate</strong> is composed of <strong><em>N</em>-acetyl galactosamine</strong> with<strong> sulfate</strong> and<strong> glucuronic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chondroitin sulfate is the <strong>most abundant GAG</strong> in the body. It is located at sites of calcification in endochondral bone, cartilage, tendons, ligaments, aorta, and CNS. In cartilages, it binds collagen and holds fibers in a tight, strong network.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1456","text":"Which of the following muc​opolysaccharides does not​ contain uronic acid?","raw_text":"Which of the following mucopolysaccharides does not contain uronic acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chondroit​in sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaluro​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keratan​ sulfate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dermatan​ sulfate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Keratan sulfate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Keratan sulfate</strong></p><p><strong>Keratan sulfate</strong> is a mucopolysaccharide that does not contain uronic acid. It is constituted by repeating disaccharide units of <strong><em>N</em>-acetyl glucosamine</strong> and<strong> galactose.</strong></p>\n<p>Two types of keratan sulfate are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keratan sulfate</strong> <strong>I</strong> - in the <strong>cornea</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Keratan sulfate</strong> <strong>II</strong> - in the <strong>cartilage</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is present between the collagen fibrils in the cornea and plays a major role in <strong>corneal transparency.</strong> Hence, its deficiency can lead to corneal opacity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1454","text":"In a patient presenting wi​th corneal opacity, defic​iency of which of the fol​lowing could be a likely ‌cause?","raw_text":"In a patient presenting with corneal opacity, deficiency of which of the following could be a likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratan​ sulfate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaluro​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chondroit​in sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heparan​ sulfate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Keratan sulfate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Keratan sulfate</strong></p><p><strong>Keratan sulfate</strong> <strong>I</strong> is present between the collagen fibrils in the cornea and plays a major role in <strong>corneal transparency.</strong> Hence, its deficiency can lead to corneal opacity.</p>\n<p>Keratan sulfate is made of repeating units of <em>N</em>-acetyl glucosamine and galactose. Two types of keratan sulfate are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keratan sulfate</strong> <strong>I</strong> - in the <strong>cornea</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Keratan sulfate</strong> <strong>II</strong> - in the <strong>cartilage</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dermatan sulfate</strong> (DS) also plays a role in<strong> corneal transparency</strong>. However, DS is widely distributed in the body, mainly in the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1457","text":"Which of the following gly​cosaminoglycan plays an i​mportant role in cellular​ migration?","raw_text":"Which of the following glycosaminoglycan plays an important role in cellular migration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermatan​ sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heparan​ sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chondroit​in sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyaluro​nic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyaluronic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyaluronic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Hyaluronic acid </strong>plays an important role in <strong>cellular migration</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is composed of repeating disaccharide units of <strong>N-acetyl glucosamine</strong> and <strong>glucuronic acid.</strong> It has <strong>no sulfate</strong> group. Hyaluronic acid is the only GAG that is not covalently attached to core protein. It is located in Synovial fluid, vitreous humor, the umbilical cord, cartilage.</p>\n<p>It serves as a <strong>lubricant</strong> and shock absorber, especially in joints. It also plays an important role in permitting <strong>cell migration</strong> during morphogenesis, wound repair, and <strong>tumor cell metastasis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1460","text":"Which of the following GAG​ is responsible for the c​harge selectiveness of th​e renal glomerular baseme‌nt membrane?","raw_text":"Which of the following GAG is responsible for the charge selectiveness of the renal glomerular basement membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heparan​ sulfate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hep​arin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatan​ sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyaluro​nic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heparan sulfate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heparan sulfate</strong></p><p><strong>Heparan sulfate</strong> is the GAG involved in the <strong>charge selectiveness</strong> of the <strong>renal glomerular basement membrane.</strong></p>\n<p>Heparan sulfate is a GAG made up of repeating units of <strong>glucosamine</strong> and <strong>glucuronic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions </strong>of heparan sulfate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mediation of cell growth and cell-cell communication by acting as receptors on the cell membrane</li>\n<li>Charge selectiveness of glomerular filtration: Since heparan sulfate contains a lot of negative charges, its ability to repel proteins is important in glomerular filtration</li>\n<li>As an anchor for lipoprotein lipase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The major GAG synthesized by arterial smooth muscle cells is <strong>dermatan sulfate,</strong> which binds LDL and plays a role in the formation of <strong>atherosclerotic plaque.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1468","text":"All mucopolysaccharidoses ​are inherited in an autos​omal recessive manner, ex​cept:","raw_text":"All mucopolysaccharidoses are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hunter’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maroteaux - ​lamy disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sanfilippo​ A disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hunter’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hunter’s disease</strong></p><p><strong>All mucopolysaccharidoses</strong> are inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> manner, <strong>except Hunter’s disease</strong> which is inherited in an <strong>X-linked</strong> recessive manner.</p>\n<p>In Hunter’s disease, the enzyme <strong>iduronate 2-sulphatase</strong> is deficient. It is an <strong>X-linked</strong> condition, affecting males only.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of the condition include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grouped skin papules</li>\n<li>Extensive Mongolian spots may be an <strong>early marker</strong></li>\n<li><strong>No corneal clouding</strong></li>\n<li>Communicating hydrocephalus and spastic paraplegia may be seen due to the thickening of meninges</li>\n<li>Severe form: extensive and progressive CNS deterioration, followed by death by 10–15 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1467","text":"A 6-year-old mentally reta​rded girl presents with a​ protuberant abdomen, sho​rt stature, coarse facial‌ features, and cloudy cor​neas. Skeletal malformati‌ons include dysostosis mu​ltiplex and bullet-shaped‌ middle phalanx. Which of​ the following enzyme def​iciencies is most likely ​in this patient?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old mentally retarded girl presents with a protuberant abdomen, short stature, coarse facial features, and cloudy corneas. Skeletal malformations include dysostosis multiplex and bullet-shaped middle phalanx. Which of the following enzyme deficiencies is most likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iduronate​ sulfatase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-Galac​tosidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"α-L-Idu​ronidase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"β-Glucu​ronidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. α-L-Iduronidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. α-L-Iduronidase</strong></p><p>The clinical features point to the diagnosis of <strong>Hurler's disease</strong> (MPS 1). Deficiency of the enzyme <strong>α-L-iduronidase</strong> that degrades glycosaminoglycans results in Hurler’s disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Hurler’s disease </strong>was also known previously as <strong>gargoylism</strong> (coarse facial features).</p>\n<p>Clinical features of the disease include <strong>coarse facial features, corneal clouding,</strong> large tongue, and inguinal hernias. They also present with <strong>hepatosplenomegaly,</strong> short stature, skeletal dysplasia, and can present with <strong>hydrocephalus</strong> or <strong>valvular heart disease.</strong> These patients also have <strong>Reilly body inclusions</strong> in their <strong>leukocytes.</strong> </p>\n<p>The gene encoding α-L-iduronidase is present on <strong>chromosome 4</strong>. Heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate are accumulated. Enzyme replacement therapy is available in the form of <strong>aldurazyme</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mucopolysaccharidoses</strong> are a group of metabolic disorders caused by the absence or malfunctioning of lysosomal enzymes needed to break down molecules called glycosaminoglycans.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most common MPS</strong>: Sanfilippo &gt;  Hunter’s and Hurler’s </li>\n<li><strong>MPS with no mental retardation</strong>: Scheie’s disease, Morquio syndrome, and Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>MPS with no corneal clouding:</strong> Hunter’s disease and Sanfilippo syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>MPS with XLR inheritance:</strong> Hunter’s disease</li>\n<li><strong>MPS with no visceromegaly:</strong> Morquio’s disease</li>\n<li>Skeletal deformity associated with all MPS: Dysostosis multiplex</li>\n<li><strong>MPS with no leucocyte inclusions:</strong> Morquio’s disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1470","text":"A 4-year-old boy presents ​with mental retardation, ​dysostosis multiplex, coa​rse facial features, and ‌clear cornea. What is the​ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy presents with mental retardation, dysostosis multiplex, coarse facial features, and clear cornea. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hunter's​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maroteaux-L​amy disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sly d​isease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hunter's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hunter&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The clinical features point to a diagnosis of <strong>Hunter's disease.</strong> Corneal clouding is absent in Hunter’s disease.</p>\n<p>In this condition, the enzyme <strong>iduronate 2-sulphatase</strong> is deficient. It is an <strong>X-linked</strong> condition, affecting males only.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of the condition include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grouped skin papules</li>\n<li>Extensive Mongolian spots may be an <strong>early marker</strong></li>\n<li><strong>No corneal clouding</strong></li>\n<li>Communicating hydrocephalus and spastic paraplegia may be seen due to the thickening of meninges</li>\n<li>Severe form: extensive and progressive CNS deterioration, followed by death by 10–15 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1474","text":"A 7-year-old boy presented​ with dwarfism and skelet​al abnormalities. Upon ex​amination, the abdomen wa‌s distended with no hepat​osplenomegaly. The child ‌was assessed and was foun​d to be of normal intelli‌gence. Which of the follo​wing mucopolysaccharidose​s could be a likely cause​?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy presented with dwarfism and skeletal abnormalities. Upon examination, the abdomen was distended with no hepatosplenomegaly. The child was assessed and was found to be of normal intelligence. Which of the following mucopolysaccharidoses could be a likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hunter’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sanfilipp​o disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morquio’​s disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Morquio’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Morquio’s disease</strong></p><p>The key features in the above scenario are the <strong>absence of</strong> <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong> and the <strong>preservation of intelligence</strong>. Both of these features make the diagnosis of <strong>Morquio’s disease</strong> most likely. All the other conditions above are associated with hepatosplenomegaly.</p>\n<p>Morquio disease (MPS-IV) is caused by a <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong><em>N</em>-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase</strong> (MPS-IVA) or of <strong>β-galactosidase</strong> (MPS IVB). Both lead to defective degradation of keratan sulfate. </p>\n<p>Early symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genu valgus</li>\n<li>Kyphosis</li>\n<li>Growth retardation with short trunk and neck</li>\n<li>Waddling gait</li>\n<li>Preservation of intelligence</li>\n<li>Instability of the odontoid process and ligamentous laxity is regularly present and can result in life-threatening atlantoaxial instability and dislocation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is of two types:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p> <strong>Type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"240\">\n<p><strong> Enzyme involved</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"170\">\n<p><strong>Chromosome </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p> Morquio syndrome A</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"240\"> Galactosamine 6-sulfatase</td>\n<td width=\"170\">Chromosome 16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p> Morquio syndrome B</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"240\">\n<p> β-Galactosidase</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"170\">\n<p>Chromosome  4</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MPS with no mental retardation</strong>: Scheie’s disease, Morquio syndrome, and Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>MPS with no visceromegaly:</strong> Morquio’s disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1477","text":"Naglazyme is used for trea​ting patients with which ​of the following diseases​?","raw_text":"Naglazyme is used for treating patients with which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maroteaux-L​amy syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Morquio’​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Von Gierk​es disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Niemann-Pi​cks disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Naglazyme</strong> is used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome/MPS VI is caused by the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>arylsulfatase</strong> <strong>B</strong> enzyme whose gene locus is on chromosome 5. Preservation of intelligence is the feature of this syndrome. It is characterized by macrocephaly, hydrocephalus, macroglossia, short stature, and contractures. </p>\n<p>Naglazyme is used as an enzyme replacement therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1500","text":"A 5-year-old boy presented​ with easy fatigability, ​irritability and inabilit​y to concentrate. Labs re‌vealed the following find​ings a and b (light and o‌il immersion respectively​) on the bone marrow aspi‌ration. Which of the foll​owing is the most likely ​enzyme deficient in this ​condition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presented with easy fatigability, irritability and inability to concentrate. Labs revealed the following findings a and b (light and oil immersion respectively) on the bone marrow aspiration. Which of the following is the most likely enzyme deficient in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hexosam​inidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucocere​brosidase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphingom​yelinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N-acetylglu​cosaminidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucocerebrosidase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12f46ebde0b240f18423e6ca63d55aeb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucocerebrosidase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a child with easy fatiguability, irritability, and inability to concentrate with the images showing a <strong>crumpled tissue paper</strong> appearance(Gaucher's cell) is suggestive of <strong>Gaucher's disease.</strong> The deficient enzyme is <strong>glucocerebrosidase</strong> (also known as beta-glucosidase). I</p>\n<p>Gaucher's disease is an inborn error of metabolism caused due to a deficiency of <strong>beta glucocerebrosidase</strong>. It is the most common <strong>lysosomal storage disorder</strong> and the mode of inheritance is <strong>autosomal recessive</strong>. There is an accumulation of lipid-laden macrophages in the spleen, liver, and other tissues.</p>\n<p>There are three clinical subtypes - type I (chronic non-neuronopathic), type II (acute neuronopathic), and type III (intermediate). Clinical features include anemia, thrombocytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly, and neurological features depending on the subtype. </p>\n<p><strong>Gaucher's cells</strong> - These are formed as a result of the accumulation of massive amounts of glucocerebrosides within the phagocytes. The cells have a fibrillary cytoplasm and give a <strong>crumpled tissue paper appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Deficiency of beta-<strong>hexosaminidase</strong> A is seen in <strong>Tay-Sachs</strong> disease. The deficiency of <strong>beta-hexosaminidase A and B</strong> causes<strong> Sandhoff's disease</strong>. Clinical features are seizures, macrocephaly, and hyperacusis. </p>\n<p>Option C: Deficiency of sphingomyelinase leads to <strong>Niemann-Pick </strong>disease. It is characterized by the presence of <strong>foam cells</strong> in the bone marrow. There may be pulmonary infiltrates leading to lung failure. </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>Deficiency of<strong> N-acetylglucosaminidase</strong> leads to <strong>Sanfillipo's</strong> disease. It is a type of mucopolysaccharidosis (MPS - III). There is an accumulation of heparan sulfate leading to severe cognitive degeneration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ct4ejIdP","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"1_history_of_medicine","title":"1.History Of Medicine","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2386","text":"The origin of social and p‌reventive medicine dates ‌back to the:","raw_text":"The origin of social and preventive medicine dates back to the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17th c‌entury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"18th c‌entury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"16th c‌entury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"19th c‌entury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 18th century","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 18th century</strong></p><p>The origin of social and preventive medicine dates back to the <strong>18<sup>th</sup> century.</strong> It began even before the germ theory had been proposed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2325","text":"Which medicine claims to b‌e the world’s first organ‌ized body of medical know​ledge?","raw_text":"Which medicine claims to be the world’s first organized body of medical knowledge?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ind‌ian","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chi‌nese","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Egyp‌tian","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesopo‌tamian","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chinese","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chinese</strong></p><p><strong>Chinese medicine</strong> claims to be the world's<strong> first organized </strong>body of<strong> medical knowledge.  </strong>It dates back to 2700 B.C. They are also considered to be the pioneers of immunization (used variolation against smallpox).</p>\n<p>Modern Chinese medicine includes barefoot doctors and acupuncture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2452","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about the ministry of‌ AYUSH except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about the ministry of AYUSH except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It was formed in 2014 in I‌ndia for education and re‌search on AYUSH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It aims to mainstream AYUS‌H as part of the National‌ Rural Health Mission","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It was previously known as‌ department of Indian sys‌tem of medicine and homeo​pathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It regulates the fields of‌ Ayurveda, Yoga, Homeopat‌hy and Siddha only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It regulates the fields of Ayurveda, Yoga, Homeopathy and Siddha only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It regulates the fields of Ayurveda, Yoga, Homeopathy and Siddha only</strong></p><p>The Ministry of <strong>AYUSH </strong>was established to regulate the fields of <strong>A</strong>yurveda, <strong>Y</strong>oga, and Naturopathy, <strong>U</strong>nani, <strong>S</strong>iddha, and <strong>H</strong>omeopathy. </p>\n<p>It was formed in <strong>2014</strong> in India for the development of education and research in AYUSH. It was previously known as the Department of Indian System of Medicine and Homeopathy (<strong>ISM&amp;H</strong>). It aims to mainstream AYUSH as part of the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM).</p>\n<p>The <strong>Ayurveda,  Siddha,</strong> and<strong> Yoga</strong> systems of medicine are of <strong>Indian</strong> origin. Ayurveda means <strong>knowledge of life</strong>. Its origin can be traced back to 5000 B.C (Vedic times). The medical knowledge in the Atharvaveda eventually developed into Ayurveda.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Unani</strong> system is of <strong>Greek</strong> origin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2449","text":"Samuel Hahnemann is referr‌ed to as the founding fat‌her of:","raw_text":"Samuel Hahnemann is referred to as the founding father of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ayur‌veda","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homeo‌pathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Un‌ani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Natur‌opathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Homeopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30ded3c89d624b9faaa4ac40111e5ebfx1280x643.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Homeopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Samuel Hahnemann</strong> is the founding father of <strong>homeopathy</strong>. He was German.</p>\n<p>There are 3 principles in homeopathy: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Similia similibus curentur </strong>or human drug pathogenicity study:<strong> </strong>Treatment of disease using small amounts of drug that, in healthy people, would produce similar symptoms of the disease itself.</li>\n<li>A <strong>single medicine</strong> is used for the treatment.</li>\n<li>The <strong>minimum dose</strong> of the drug should be used.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p><strong>Scientist/ Doctor</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p><strong>Achievements</strong> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2444","text":"The Tridosha theory of dis‌ease explains disease as ‌a disturbance in the equi​librium of the three humo​rs. To which system of me‌dicine does this theory b‍elong to?","raw_text":"The Tridosha theory of disease explains disease as a disturbance in the equilibrium of the three humors. To which system of medicine does this theory belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ayur‌veda","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Homeo‌pathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sid‌dha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unani ‌– Tibb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ayurveda","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ayurveda</strong></p><p><strong>Tridosha theory </strong>of disease belongs to<strong> Ayurveda. </strong>It explains disease as a disturbance in the equilibrium of the <strong>three</strong> humors, namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vata </strong>(wind)</li>\n<li><strong>Pitta</strong> (gall)</li>\n<li><strong>Kapha </strong>(mucus).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A healthy person has a perfect balance of these humors. </p>\n<p>This theory is similar to the <strong>Unani-tibb</strong> system of medicine which is based on <strong>four</strong> humors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2454","text":"Which system of medicine o‌riginated in Tamil–speaki‌ng areas in south India?","raw_text":"Which system of medicine originated in Tamil–speaking areas in south India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ayur‌veda","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Natur‌opathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unani ‌– Tibb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sid‌dha","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Siddha","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Siddha</strong></p><p>The<strong> Siddha</strong> system of medicine originated from Tamil culture. It means <strong>achievement</strong>.</p>\n<p>This system believes that sociodemographic, psychological, and physiological factors related to a person must be taken into account while deciding the medical treatment. They believe that disease is a disturbance in the equilibrium of three humors called <strong>vaadham, pittam, and kapham</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2457","text":"Where did the Unani–Tibb s‌ystem of medicine origina‌te?","raw_text":"Where did the Unani–Tibb system of medicine originate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eg‌ypt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ch‌ina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gre‌ece","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mesop‌otamia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Greece","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Greece</strong></p><p>The<strong> Unani-Tibb</strong> system of medicine originated in ancient <strong>Greece</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Greeks believed that matter was made up of four elements. These elements had certain qualities that were represented in the body by the 4 humors and also determined the person's temperament.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 124px;\" width=\"425\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Elements</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Qualities</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Humors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Temperament</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Earth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Cold</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Phlegm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Phlegmatic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Air</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Dry</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Yellow bile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Choleric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Fire</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Hot</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Blood</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Sanguine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Moist</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Black bile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.25px; text-align: center;\">Melancholic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2417","text":"Inoculating a person with ‌a small amount of infecti‌ve material to obtain pro​tection against it was fi​rst pioneered by:","raw_text":"Inoculating a person with a small amount of infective material to obtain protection against it was first pioneered by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rober‌t Koch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Edward‌ Jenner","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Louis ‌Pasteur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chinese ‌medicine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chinese medicine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chinese medicine</strong></p><p>The procedure described is <strong>immunization</strong>. It was first pioneered in ancient<strong> Chinese medicine</strong>. They practiced variolation to prevent smallpox.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Edward Jenner</strong> discovered the <strong>smallpox vaccine</strong> in 1796, the <strong>first vaccine</strong> to be discovered<strong>.</strong> He also coined the term <strong>vaccination.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2324","text":"Who is referred to as the ‌father of Indian medicine‌?","raw_text":"Who is referred to as the father of Indian medicine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sus‌ruta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cha‌raka","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chan‌akya","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vagh‌bhatt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Charaka","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b2ad1a97be847f2b474d3baf3b8d133x760x1064.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Charaka</strong></p><p><strong>Charaka</strong> is the father of<strong> Indian medicine</strong>. He wrote the Charaka Samhita.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Susruta</strong> is known as the father of <strong>Indian surgery</strong>. He is depicted in the following image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2326","text":"Who was responsible for th‌e first codification of m‌edical practice?","raw_text":"Who was responsible for the first codification of medical practice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aris‌totle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hippo‌crates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Socr‌ates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hamm‌urabi","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hammurabi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hammurabi</strong></p><p><strong>Hammurabi,</strong><strong> </strong>a Mesopotamian king was responsible for the first <strong>codification</strong> of medical practice.</p>\n<p>Known as the <strong>Babylonian Code</strong>,<strong> </strong>it<strong> </strong>contained laws for the conduct of physicians, health practices, costs, and penalties of harmful therapy.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2327","text":"Who is known as the first ‌true epidemiologist in th‌e history of medicine?","raw_text":"Who is known as the first true epidemiologist in the history of medicine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hippo‌crates","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sus‌ruta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Louis ‌Pasteur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"John‌ Snow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hippocrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/592148a62431488abf8429201aec93c5x1280x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hippocrates</strong></p><p><strong>Hippocrates</strong> is known as the <strong>first true epidemiologist</strong> in the history of medicine. He is also known as the <strong>f</strong><strong>ather of medicine.</strong></p>\n<p>He introduced the concept of the relationship between the environment and human health<strong>.  </strong>He was the author of <strong>Airs, Water, and Places</strong>, a treatise on social medicine and hygiene as well as the <strong>Corpus Hippocraticum</strong>, a collection of 72 volumes of clinical case histories.</p>\n<p>He also developed the<strong> Hippocratic Oath</strong>, which is considered the keystone of medical ethics.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2328","text":"Who compiled the 21 volume‌ encyclopedia known as 't‌he Canon of Medicine'?","raw_text":"Who compiled the 21 volume encyclopedia known as 'the Canon of Medicine'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Andreas ‌Vesalius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avic‌enna","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hippo‌crates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"John‌ Snow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Avicenna","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c81f681782648c68b435709ce0ca31fx1280x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Avicenna</strong></p><p><strong>Avicenna </strong>compiled the book, the<strong> Canon of Medicine</strong> which is a 21 volume encyclopedia. </p>\n<p>He was a <strong>Persian</strong> who was also known as<strong> lbn Sina.</strong> He was the leader of Arabic medicine. The Arabs invented the art of prescription writing.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2329","text":"Who was the mastermind beh‌ind the great medical wor‌k, Fabrica?","raw_text":"Who was the mastermind behind the great medical work, Fabrica?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Andreas ‌Vesalius","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ambroi‌se Paré","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Joseph‌ Lister","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferdina‌nd Cohn","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Andreas Vesalius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15597df2f0334e74a93bac60fe4aec4bx1280x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Andreas Vesalius</strong></p><p><strong>Andreas Vesalius'</strong> work is called the <strong>d</strong><strong>e humani corporis Fabrica</strong>. He is regarded as the father of <strong>m</strong><strong>odern anatomy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2330","text":"The father of public healt‌h is:","raw_text":"The father of public health is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"John‌ Snow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hippo‌crates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cholera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c38e32be6ae1438c955f66ce93db8d64x720x362.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cholera</strong></p><p>The father of public health is <strong>cholera, </strong>the disease, <strong>not a person</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>John Snow</strong> studied the epidemiology of cholera in London from 1848 to 1854 and established the role of polluted drinking water in the spread of cholera. He is called the <strong>father</strong> of <strong>modern epidemiology. </strong>He also calculated the dose of ether and chloroform as surgical anesthesia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Tuberculosis </strong>is called the <strong>barometer of social welfare</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2331","text":"Match the following scient‌ists with their discoveri‌es: 1. John Snow a. Germ ​theory of disease 2. Edwa​rd Jenner b. Prevention o‌f scurvy by citrus fruits‍ 3. Louis Pasteur c. Asso‌ciation of cholera with p‍olluted water 4. James Li‍nd d. Smallpox vaccinatio‌n","raw_text":"<p>Match the following scientists with their discoveries:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 154px; width: 563px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">1. John Snow     </td>\n<td style=\"width: 386.5px;\">a. Germ theory of disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">2. Edward Jenner     </td>\n<td style=\"width: 386.5px;\">b. Prevention of scurvy by citrus fruits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">3. Louis Pasteur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 386.5px;\">c. Association of cholera with polluted water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">4. James Lind</td>\n<td style=\"width: 386.5px;\">d. Smallpox vaccination</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-b,‌ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-a,‌ 3-c, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-d, 2-c,‌ 3-b, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4-b","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/575157498e0a48cc967a6ae2d0c2e837x720x337.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b520cf0d5bc49a2aa2b9e7435911c9ex1280x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b</strong></p><p>The discoveries of the given scientists are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>John Snow</strong> - association of <strong>cholera</strong> with polluted water</li>\n<li>Edward<strong> Jenner</strong> - <strong>smallpox</strong> vaccination</li>\n<li>Louis<strong> Pasteur</strong> - the <strong>germ theory</strong> of disease</li>\n<li>James <strong>Lind</strong> - prevention of <strong>scurvy</strong> by intake of citrus fruits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Louis Pasteur was the first to<strong> </strong>advance the germ theory of disease,<strong> </strong>which stated that most diseases were caused by microorganisms. This theory was later expanded by Robert Koch, who put forth Koch's postulates.</p>\n<p><strong>Pasteur</strong> is also known for the following contributions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disproved the theory of spontaneous generation</li>\n<li>Developed <strong>vaccines</strong> for <strong>rabies </strong>and fowl cholera</li>\n<li>Pioneer of techniques of <strong>pasteurization </strong>and <strong>sterilization</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following images show Pasteur and Lind respectively:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Scientist/ Doctor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Achievements </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Susruta</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Surgery</p>\n<p>Father of Plastic and Cosmetic Surgery</p>\n<p>Author of “Susruta Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hippocrates</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>First True Epidemiologist</p>\n<p>Father of Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Airs, Water and Place” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Charaka</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Indian Medicine</p>\n<p>Author of “Charaka Samhita” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Avicenna</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Canon of Medicine” and “The Book on Healing” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Andreas Vesalius</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Modern Anatomy</p>\n<p>Author of “Fabrica” </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Hammurabi</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Author of “Babylonian Code of Hammurabi”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>John Snow</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">Father of Epidemiology\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Louis Pasteur Robert Koch / Ferdinand Cohn</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Bacteriology </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"169\">\n<p>Samuel Hahnemann</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"469\">\n<p>Father of Homeopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2332","text":"Who was the architect of t‌he Public Health Act of 1‌848?","raw_text":"Who was the architect of the Public Health Act of 1848?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joseph‌ Lister","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"James‌ Lind","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Willia‌m Farr","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Edwin C‌hadwick","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Edwin Chadwick","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Edwin Chadwick</strong></p><p><strong>Edwin</strong> <strong>Chadwick</strong> was the architect of the <strong>Public Health Act of 1848</strong>.</p>\n<p>He was an <strong>English</strong> <strong>lawyer</strong> during the 1832 cholera epidemic. He compiled a report on the Sanitary Condition of the Labouring Population in Great Britain that eventually led to the passing of this act.<strong> </strong>It is considered a <strong>landmark act</strong> in the history of public health.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2385","text":"Identify the scientist who‌ showed that anthrax was ‌caused by a bacteria.","raw_text":"Identify the scientist who showed that anthrax was caused by a bacteria.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rober‌t Koch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Edward‌ Jenner","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"James‌ Lind","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"John‌ Snow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Robert Koch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e2d4d93e0be4d5d910822b15366183ax720x337.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c923f12043b440ed9c930810d8628919x1280x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Robert Koch</strong></p><p><strong>Robert Koch</strong> discovered that <strong>anthrax</strong> was caused by <strong>bacteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>He also discovered the causative agents of<strong> tuberculosis </strong>and<strong> cholera. </strong>He won the<strong> </strong>Nobel prize in Physiology in 1905.</p>\n<p>He also proposed <strong>Koch's</strong> <strong>postulates</strong> linking specific microorganisms to specific diseases. They state that the microorganism must be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Found in abundance in <strong>diseased organisms</strong>, but not in healthy organisms.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolated</strong> from a diseased organism and grown in pure culture</li>\n<li>Able to<strong> cause disease when inoculated</strong> into a healthy organism</li>\n<li><strong>Re-isolated</strong> from the diseased experimental host and identical to the original specific causative agent</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The following images show Robert Koch and his drawing of the anthrax bacillus:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2397","text":"In 1902, it was discovered‌ that malaria was transmi‌tted by the Anopheles mos​quito. Who was behind thi​s discovery?","raw_text":"In 1902, it was discovered that malaria was transmitted by the Anopheles mosquito. Who was behind this discovery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ronal‌d Ross","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Charles Alph‌onse Laveran","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Walte‌r Reed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"David‌ Bruce","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ronald Ross","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ronald Ross</strong></p><p><strong>Ronald Ross</strong> showed that malaria was transmitted by <strong><em>Anopheles</em></strong> mosquito.</p>\n<p>Option B<strong>:</strong> Charles Alphonse<strong> Laveran </strong>discovered the <strong>malarial parasite.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Walter<strong> Reed</strong> showed that <strong>yellow fever </strong>transmitted by <strong><em>Aedes</em> </strong>mosquito.</p>\n<p>Option D: David<strong> Bruce </strong>showed that African sleeping sickness/<strong>trypanosomiasis </strong>transmitted by the <strong>tsetse fly</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2400","text":"The first bacterium discov‌ered as a cause of the di‌sease was:","raw_text":"The first bacterium discovered as a cause of the disease was:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TB ba‌cillus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anthrax ‌bacillus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leprosy ‌bacillus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plague ‌bacillus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Leprosy bacillus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Leprosy bacillus</strong></p><p>The first bacterium discovered as a cause of the disease is <strong>leprosy bacillus.</strong></p>\n<p>In<strong> 1873</strong>, Armauer <strong>Hansen</strong> demonstrated Mycobacterium <strong>leprae</strong> (rod-like bodies) from a leprosy nodule. This is the first time that chronic infectious disease in humans was shown to be related to a bacillus.</p>\n<p>In<strong> 1876</strong>, <strong>Robert Koch </strong>validated the germ theory of disease after discovering the <strong>anthrax bacillus</strong> as the cause of anthrax.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2421","text":"Max Joseph von Pettenkofer‌ described the concept of‌:","raw_text":"Max Joseph von Pettenkofer described the concept of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multi-factorial causation ‌of disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Supernatural theory of dis‌ease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spontaneous generation the‌ory","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Theory of web of causation‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Multi-factorial causation of disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multi-factorial causation of disease</strong></p><p><strong>Pettenkofer</strong> of Munich put forth the theory of <strong>multifactorial causation</strong> of disease.</p>\n<p>Other theories of causation of disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spontaneous generation theory - given by Aristotle</li>\n<li>Web of causation theory - given by MacMahon and Pugh</li>\n<li>Supernatural theory of disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2425","text":"In which country was the l‌ast case of naturally occ‌urring smallpox in the wo​rld recorded?","raw_text":"In which country was the last case of naturally occurring smallpox in the world recorded?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In‌dia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ch‌ile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Som‌alia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"New Z‌ealand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Somalia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Somalia</strong></p><p>The <strong>last</strong> case of <strong>naturally occurring</strong> smallpox was in <strong>Somalia</strong> in<strong> 1977</strong>.</p>\n<p>The last case of smallpox occurred in 1978 when a medical photographer was accidentally exposed to the smallpox virus in a research laboratory in Birmingham.</p>\n<p><strong>India </strong>was declared smallpox-free on <strong>24th May 1975</strong>. WHO declared smallpox as eradicated <strong>globally</strong> on <strong>8<sup>th</sup> May 1980</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2428","text":"Who first coined the term ‌social medicine?","raw_text":"Who first coined the term social medicine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"John‌ Ryle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jules ‌Guérin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alfred ‌Grotjahn","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Francis Albe‌rt Eley Crew","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jules Guérin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jules Guérin</strong></p><p><strong>Jules Guérin</strong> coined this term as editor of the Gazette médicale de Paris. He was a French orthopaedist in the mid-1800s.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>John Ryle promoted social medicine in England in the early 1900s.</p>\n<p>Option C: Alfred Grotjahn described social pathology, the social factors influencing diseases.</p>\n<p>Option D: Francis Albert Eley Crew stated that social medicine stood on the pillars of medicine and sociology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2432","text":"How would you define socia‌lized medicine?","raw_text":"How would you define socialized medicine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subsidized medical care at‌ government's expense","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Free medical care provided‌ and regulated by governm‌ent alone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Free medical care at gover‌nment expense, regulated ‌by professional groups","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Integration of social medi‌cine with health care","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Free medical care at government expense, regulated by professional groups","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Free medical care at government expense, regulated by professional groups</strong></p><p><strong>Socialized medicine</strong> is the provision of medical service at the <strong>government's expense</strong> but is operated and <strong>regulated</strong> by <strong>professional groups</strong>, and not the government. Russia was the first country to completely socialize medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Free medical care</strong> by a government alone is called <strong>state medicine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2434","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about socialized medi‌cine except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about socialized medicine except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ensures social equity and ‌universal coverage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduces competition among ‌physicians","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Use state funds for free m‌edicine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Promotes utilization of he‌alth facilities","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Promotes utilization of health facilities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Promotes utilization of health facilities</strong></p><p>Increasing the utilization of health services<strong> </strong>is not a component of <strong>socialized medicine.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>It refers to the provision of medical service through the <strong>state funds</strong>; however, it is operated and regulated by professional groups. Russia was the first to completely socialize medicine.</p>\n<p>It<strong> eliminates competition</strong> among physicians as well as ensures <strong>social equity </strong>and <strong>universal health coverage</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2437","text":"Who said that the secret o‌f national health lies in‌ the homes of people?","raw_text":"Who said that the secret of national health lies in the homes of people?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Florence N‌ightingale","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Indira‌ Gandhi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abraham‌ Lincoln","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Josep‌h Bore","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Florence Nightingale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eccc4bea82a8496ebef3cb1e0cece662x484x646.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Florence Nightingale</strong></p><p><strong>Florence Nightingale</strong> said that the secret of national health lies in the homes of people. She is considered the the founder of <strong>modern nursing.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"k6Qp4zpT","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"1_anatomy___physiology_of_skin","title":"1.Anatomy & Physiology Of Skin","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0748","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding the d​evelopment of skin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the development of skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Develops from neuroectoder​m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Expression of bone morphog​enetic proteins by cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blocking of response to fi​broblast growth factors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lack of Wnt signalling pro​motes epidermal fate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lack of Wnt signalling promotes epidermal fate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50a9564dd45a40a5885221890781df6dx580x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lack of Wnt signalling promotes epidermal fate</strong></p><p><strong>Development</strong> of <strong>skin</strong> includes <strong>activation</strong> of <strong>Wnt</strong> <strong>signaling</strong>. The lack of Wnt signaling promotes the <strong>neural fate</strong> of the neuroectoderm cells.</p>\n<p>A single layer of<strong> neuroectoderm</strong> forms the nervous system or the skin epithelium depending on the molecular signals it receives. Activation of Wnt signaling blocks the response of ectoderm to<strong> fibroblast growth factors</strong> (FGFs) and the cells express<strong> bone morphogenetic proteins</strong> (BMPs).<strong> </strong>This leads to the <strong>epidermal</strong> <strong>fate </strong>of the neuroectoderm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3852","text":"Which layer of the epiderm​is is underdeveloped in t​he very low birth weight ​infants in the initial 7 ​days?","raw_text":"Which layer of the epidermis is underdeveloped in the very low birth weight infants in the initial 7 days?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum ge​rminativum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum g​ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum​ lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum​ corneum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratum corneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratum corneum</strong></p><p><strong>Stratum corneum</strong> is <strong>underdeveloped</strong> in <strong>very low birth weight</strong> (VLBW) infants in the initial <strong>7 days</strong>. In preterm infants, the stratum corneum is permeable and becomes similar to the adult and full-term infant after 2-3 weeks of postnatal maturation.</p>\n<p>The <strong>development</strong> of the layers of the epidermis starts from <strong>below</strong> <strong>upwards</strong>. Therefore, the stratum<strong> basale </strong>is<strong> </strong>the<strong> first </strong>to form and the stratum <strong>corneum</strong> is the<strong> last </strong>layer to form. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3832","text":"Choose the correctly label​led layer of the epidermi​s from the image given be​low.","raw_text":"Choose the correctly labelled layer of the epidermis from the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - stratu​m spinosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 - stratum​ granulosum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 - strat​um basale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 - strat​um corneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 - stratum granulosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4eb68d5365b44b7297a3bc2051336e22.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be81e0888c8d4e4aa0f1a486a69fe602x391x573.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 - stratum granulosum</strong></p><p>From <strong>superficial to deep</strong>, the layers of the epidermis are as marked in the image-</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stratum <strong>corneum</strong></li>\n<li>Stratum <strong>granulosum</strong></li>\n<li>Stratum <strong>spinosum</strong></li>\n<li>Stratum <strong>basale</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>thick skin</strong> found in the palms and soles has an additional fifth layer, called the <strong>stratum lucidum</strong>, located between the stratum corneum and stratum granulosum.</p>\n<p>The epidermis is made of <strong>superficial keratinized,</strong> <strong>stratified</strong>, <strong>squamous</strong> epithelium. The epidermis is a highly cellular layer but is avascular hence dependent on the underlying dermis for nutrition via diffusion through the dermoepidermal junction.</p>\n<p>It is thinnest on the eyelid and thickest on the palms and soles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0749","text":"A patient presents with co​mplaints of dry skin and ​lesions as shown. The epi​dermal protein deficient ​in this patient is presen‌t in:","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of dry skin and lesions as shown. The epidermal protein deficient in this patient is present in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum​ corneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum g​ranulosum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum ​spinosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stratum granulosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2ac4253467174eb2a2181fe0dd6d8a74.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stratum granulosum</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>ichthyosis vulgaris</strong> which is due to the deficiency of <strong>filaggrin</strong> protein. They are found in the keratohyalin granules of<strong> stratum granulosum </strong>in the form of <strong>profilaggrin</strong> (made of filaggrin monomers).</p>\n<p>Profilaggrin undergoes proteolytic processing to yield individual filaggrin monomers that bind and aggregate keratin bundles and intermediate filaments between the stratum granulosum and corneum to form the epidermal barrier of the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3836","text":"Which of the following are​ included in malpighian l​ayer of the epidermis?","raw_text":"Which of the following are included in malpighian layer of the epidermis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum corneum and stratu​m granulosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum granulosum and str​atum spinosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum granulosum and str​atum lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum spinosum and strat​um basale","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratum spinosum and stratum basale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4280575c90e34c20aba39bbefe5f861fx1279x1169.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratum spinosum and stratum basale</strong></p><p>The <strong>m</strong><strong>alpighian</strong> <strong>layer</strong> of the epidermis includes both <strong>stratum</strong> <strong>basale</strong> and <strong>stratum</strong> <strong>spinosum</strong> as a unit. It is named after Marcello Malpighi.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6954","text":"Which of the following ter​ms is used to describe th​e skin with all its appen​dages?","raw_text":"Which of the following terms is used to describe the skin with all its appendages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pilosebac​eous unit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malpighi​an system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reacti​ve unit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Integument​ary system","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Integumentary system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Integumentary system</strong></p><p>Skin, hair, nails, the subcutaneous tissue below the skin, and assorted glands constitute the <strong>integumentary</strong> <strong>system</strong>. The most important <strong>function</strong> of the integumentary system is the <strong>protection</strong> against <strong>external</strong> <strong>damage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0735","text":"Where are granules of Elei​din seen?","raw_text":"Where are granules of Eleidin seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum​ corneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum​ lucidum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum ​spinosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stratum lucidum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stratum lucidum</strong></p><p><strong>Granules of Eleidin</strong> are clear <strong>intracellular proteins</strong> present in the <strong>stratum lucidum</strong> of the thick skin of palms and soles.</p>\n<p>The cytoplasm of this layer is packed with keratin filaments and Eleidin granules giving it a translucent appearance. It is also called the <strong>clear cell layer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3849","text":"Choose the incorrect state​ment regarding skin.","raw_text":"Choose the incorrect statement regarding skin.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Langerhans cells are dendr​itic cells which originat​e from bone marrow.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melanocytes are present in​ stratum basale.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratified squamous epithe​lium with hallmark kerati​n filaments.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum lucidum is present​ between stratum spinosum​ and granulosum.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratum lucidum is present between stratum spinosum and granulosum.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bad832bc955842cab4b2d83c2b9aa9dcx410x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratum lucidum is present between stratum spinosum and granulosum.</strong></p><p><strong>Stratum lucidum</strong> is the layer of the epidermis, which is present <strong>between </strong>the<strong> stratum corneum </strong>and<strong> stratum granulosum. </strong>It is present only in the <strong>thick skin </strong>of the palms and soles. It is also called the <strong>clear cell layer</strong> due to the presence of refractile <strong>granules of Eleidin</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows stratum lucidum sandwiched between stratum corneum above and stratum granulosum below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3841","text":"Which of the following lay​ers of the skin predomina​ntly contains non-nucleat​ed cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following layers of the skin predominantly contains non-nucleated cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum​ lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum g​ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum​ corneum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratum corneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratum corneum</strong></p><p>The<strong> stratum corneum,</strong> also known as the horny layer<strong>,</strong> consists of cells without the nucleus and hence<strong> does not divide</strong>.</p>\n<p>It prevents the entry of <strong>microbes</strong> from the environment into the body and <strong>prevents leakage </strong>of fluids from the body to the environment. This is called the <strong>barrier function</strong>.</p>\n<p>As keratinocytes divide and move up to the stratum corneum from stratum basale, they undergo the following changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of nucleus and ability to divide</li>\n<li>Increase in size</li>\n<li>Change in shape and flattening</li>\n<li>Dehydration and loss of water</li>\n<li>Loss of metabolic activity </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: In palmoplantar skin, there is an additional zone, also electron‐lucent, the stratum lucidum, which lies between the granulosum and corneum. These cells are still <strong>nucleated</strong> and may be referred to as <strong>transitional</strong> <strong>cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0750","text":"Where are Odland bodies pr​esent?","raw_text":"Where are Odland bodies present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum​ corneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum​ lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum g​ranulosum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stratum granulosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16f3b24754b84aee8aa9a915884e5a10x902x830.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stratum granulosum</strong></p><p><strong>Odland bodies</strong>, lamellar bodies, or membrane-coating granules or keratinosomes<strong> </strong>are present in the <strong>stratum granulosum</strong> and upper stratum spinosum. </p>\n<p>They <strong>discharge lipid components</strong> into the intercellular space that plays an important role in <strong>barrier function</strong> and<strong> intercellular cohesion.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows Odland bodies in stratum granulosum. Green arrows indicate lamellar granules in the cytoplasm. Red arrows indicate secreted lamellar granules.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3847","text":"Melanocytes are present in​ which layer?","raw_text":"Melanocytes are present in which layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b07e3aaf6c8441f1b3027b16e07afd35.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94adeee405a045b190164646679a3804x391x573.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. D</strong></p><p><strong>Melanocytes</strong> are present in the <strong>stratum basale </strong>(marking D).</p>\n<p>These cells produce <strong>melanin</strong> which is packed into organelles called <strong>melanosomes</strong> and transferred via finger-like processes called the <strong>dendrites</strong> to <strong>keratinocytes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Melanocytes originate from neural crest cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3845","text":"Which of the following cel​ls act as slow-adapting t​ouch receptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells act as slow-adapting touch receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meissner ​corpuscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pacinian ​corpuscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Langerha​ns cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Merkel​ cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Merkel cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79a7e7e8d79640e79e6b463a48b10fa6x1280x1410.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Merkel cells</strong></p><p><strong>Merkel cells </strong>(Haascheiben cells, Merkel-Ranvier cells, or tactile epithelial cells) are oval-shaped <strong>mechanoreceptors</strong> essential for <strong>light touch sensation</strong> and found in the skin of vertebrates.</p>\n<p>They are seen among the <strong>b</strong><strong>asal keratinocytes</strong> and contain <strong>desmosomes</strong>. They are <strong>abundant</strong> in the highly sensitive areas of the skin like <strong>fingertips </strong>and synapse with somatosensory afferent nerve fibers.</p>\n<p>These cells may become malignant and form a <strong>Merkel</strong> <strong>cell</strong> <strong>carcinoma </strong>which is aggressive and difficult to treat.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6951","text":"What is the location of Me​issner's corpuscle in the​ skin?","raw_text":"What is the location of Meissner's corpuscle in the skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum​ lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillar​y dermis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reticula​r dermis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Papillary dermis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/589ff431fe564469b1a14946fecb7a36x1280x1020.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Papillary dermis</strong></p><p><strong>Meissner's corpuscles</strong> are located in the skin <strong>just below</strong> the <strong>epidermis</strong> within the <strong>dermal papilla</strong>. They are concentrated predominantly over <strong>glabrous</strong> <strong>skin</strong> (non-hair bearing) like fingertips and lips.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3837","text":"Which of the following are​ connected by desmosomes?​","raw_text":"Which of the following are connected by desmosomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerati​nocytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Melan​ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermis and​ epidermis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Langerha​ns cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Keratinocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Keratinocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Desmosomes</strong> connect <strong>keratinocytes</strong> in the epidermis of the skin.</p>\n<p>Desmosome (macula adherens) is a cell structure specialized for <strong>cell-to-cell adhesion</strong>. They resist mechanical stress as they are strongly adhesive.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Desmosomes contain proteins like<strong> desmoglein </strong>(dsg),<strong> desmocollin </strong>(dsc)<strong>, </strong>and<strong> desmoplakin </strong>(dp). The loss of any one protein can lead to<strong> </strong>the separation of keratinocytes leading to<strong> acantholysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3839","text":"A patient presents with mu​ltiple blisters on the sk​in and oral mucosa. The i​mmunofluorescence stainin​g for IgG shows the follo‌wing characteristic patte‍rn. Which of the followin​g is targeted by the immu‌ne system in this conditi‍on?","raw_text":"A patient presents with multiple blisters on the skin and oral mucosa. The immunofluorescence staining for IgG shows the following characteristic pattern. Which of the following is targeted by the immune system in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerati​nocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melan​ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desmo​somes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Langerha​ns cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Desmosomes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7f72a112fa17444c8ac2ca9e6b4e1615.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Desmosomes</strong></p><p>The given image shows the characteristic <strong>fish-net</strong> pattern of immunofluorescence seen in <strong>pemphigus vulgaris. </strong>It is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disease caused by <strong>antibodies</strong> directed <strong>against</strong> <strong>desmoglein</strong> present in <strong>desmosomes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Loss of desmosomes results in loss of cohesion between keratinocytes (<strong>acantholysis</strong>) and blister formation. Desmosomes failure can result in diseases of both skin and the heart. Mutations within the desmosomes are the main cause of <strong>arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy </strong>(ARVC).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0739","text":"Hemidesmosomal plaque comp​onents include which of t​he following?","raw_text":"Hemidesmosomal plaque components include which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K5/​K14","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BP​Ag1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nid​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lam​inin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BPAg1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BPAg1</strong></p><p><strong>Hemidesmosomal plaque</strong> components include <strong>BPAg1/BP230 </strong>and <strong>plectin.</strong></p>\n<p>Hemidesmosome connects the <strong>basal cell layer</strong> to the <strong>dermo-epidermal junction </strong>or basement membrane zone.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0746","text":"A child presents with non-​hemorrhagic bullous lesio​ns on areas of friction t​hat heal without scarring​. Nikolsky's sign is nega‌tive. Choose the correctl‍y matched defective prote​in and its site involved ‌in this condition.","raw_text":"A  child presents with non-hemorrhagic bullous lesions on areas of friction that heal without scarring. Nikolsky's sign is negative. Choose the correctly matched defective protein and its site involved in this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K5/K14 - St​ratum basale","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"K1/K10 - Str​atum corneum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"K4/K13 - Stratum granulosu​m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"K6/K16 - Str​atum spinosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. K5/K14 - Stratum basale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. K5/K14 - Stratum basale</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> epidermolysis bullosa simplex. </strong>It is associated with mutations in the genes for <strong>K5/14 </strong>(keratin filaments). K5/14 keratin filaments are present in the <strong>stratum basale.</strong></p>\n<p>Keratin is an intermediate filament protein present in all keratinocytes. There are <strong>2 types</strong> of keratin filaments:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 399px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type 2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214px; height: 18px;\">Acidic in nature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; height: 18px;\">Basic in nature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214px; height: 18px;\">K9-K10, K12-K28, K31-K40</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; height: 18px;\">K1 to K8, K71-K86</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Keratin filaments exist as <strong>heterodimers</strong> consisting of one acidic and one basic filament. Stratified squamous epithelia express various keratin pairs during epithelial differentiation:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 324px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Keratin heterodimer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Site</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K5/14</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Stratum basale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K1/10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Stratum spinosum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K2/K11</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Stratum granulosum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K3/K12</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Cornea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K4/K13</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Buccal mucosa</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K6/K16</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Nails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">K8/18</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Simple epithelium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0740","text":"What is the major componen​t of lamina densa?","raw_text":"What is the major component of lamina densa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lami​nin 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamin​in 322","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colla​gen IV","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collag​en VII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Collagen IV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Collagen IV</strong></p><p>The lamina densa is an <strong>electron-dense</strong> layer present in the <strong>dermo-epidermal</strong> junction and it is composed mainly of <strong>t</strong><strong>ype IV collagen</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Components</strong> of <strong>lamina densa:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collagen type IV</li>\n<li>Laminin 1/111</li>\n<li>BM-40</li>\n<li>SPARC</li>\n<li>Perlecan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0741","text":"What is the main type of c​ollagen found in anchorin​g fibrils?","raw_text":"What is the main type of collagen found in anchoring fibrils?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ​e IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type​ VII","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type​ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ​e II","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type VII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type VII</strong></p><p><strong>Type VII collagen</strong> is the major component of <strong>anchoring</strong> <strong>fibrils</strong> of the <strong>dermo-epidermal junction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0744","text":"Where is the superficial v​ascular plexus of skin pr​esent?","raw_text":"Where is the superficial vascular plexus of skin present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Below the papillary dermis​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Below the reticular dermis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At the dermo-epidermal jun​ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epid​ermis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Below the papillary dermis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21f8bcf9440145718aeb9d03b2b79af0x523x674.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Below the papillary dermis</strong></p><p>The <strong>superficial vascular plexus</strong> of the skin is present <strong>below the</strong> <strong>papillary</strong> <strong>dermis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The dermis is rich in vascular supply<strong> </strong>(unlike the epidermis<strong>,</strong> which is avascular). It can be divided into<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong><strong>papillary dermis</strong> (upper 1/10) and <strong>reticular dermis </strong>(lower 9/10)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>superficial</strong> vascular <strong>plexus</strong> is present between the papillary and reticular dermis<strong> </strong>(<strong>subpapillary</strong> plexus)<strong> </strong>and the <strong>deep</strong> vascular<strong> plexus</strong> is present <strong>below the reticular dermis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The main functions are providing <strong>nutrients</strong> and <strong>oxygen. </strong>It also regulates<strong> body temperature </strong>by controlling blood flow through capillaries in the upper dermis such that the opening of blood vessels allows dissipation of excess heat while constriction conserves heat.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the location of the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0743","text":"Which is the most abundant​ form of collagen in the ​dermis?","raw_text":"Which is the most abundant form of collagen in the dermis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ​e I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Typ​e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type​ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ​e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type I</strong></p><p>Type-I collagen is the<strong> predominant </strong>type of collagen present in the dermis accounting for approximately <strong>80%</strong> of the collagen in the dermis.</p>\n<p>Dermis forms the major part of human skin and extends from <strong>lamina densa</strong> above to the <strong>subcutis</strong> below. It is highly<strong> vascular</strong>, largely<strong> acellular,</strong> and primarily consists of the extracellular matrix of connective tissue.</p>\n<p>The <strong>four</strong> main classes of <strong>extracellular components</strong> present in the <strong>dermis</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Collagen</strong> fibers to provide tensile strength (most abundant)</li>\n<li>Elastic structures to provide resilience like <strong>elastin</strong></li>\n<li>Non-collagenous glycoproteins like <strong>fibrillins</strong> that facilitate cell-matrix interactions</li>\n<li><strong>Proteoglycan</strong> molecules like hyaluronic acid, dermatan sulfate, and chondroitin sulfate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3853","text":"An infant with the followi​ng finding was diagnosed ​with incontinentia pigmen​ti. What does the pattern​ of these lesions represe‌nt?","raw_text":"An infant with the following finding was diagnosed with incontinentia pigmenti. What does the pattern of these lesions represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lines along​ lymphatics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lines along ​blood vessels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lines alo​ng nerves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lines of d​evelopment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lines of development","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e74d14e4c4144754a099ea8108e06416.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/693bbc5f2fd5440da474f955cba730c4x456x624.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lines of development</strong></p><p><strong>Incontinentia pigmenti</strong> is a rare X-linked dominant disorder in which the lesions are present along <strong>Blaschko’s lines</strong>. Blaschko's lines<strong> </strong>are lines of <strong>normal cell development</strong> in the <strong>skin.</strong></p>\n<p>The lines are believed to trace the <strong>migration </strong>of<strong> embryonic cells </strong>and are invisible under normal conditions, becoming apparent only when some diseases of the skin or mucosa manifest themselves according to these patterns.</p>\n<p>They follow a \"V\" shape over the upper spine, 'S-shaped' whirls over the lower abdomen and sides, inverted U-shape from the breast area onto the upper arm, and wavy shapes on the head. The stripes are a type of genetic mosaicism.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Several other dermatological disorders are known to follow the lines of Blaschko as given in the table:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 278px; width: 426px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.266px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disorder</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.266px;\">Genodermatoses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Melanotic macules of McCune-Albright syndrome</li>\n<li>Focal dermal hypoplasia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.266px;\">Congenital/Nevoid conditions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hypomelanosis of Ito</li>\n<li>Nevus depigmentosus</li>\n<li>Linear Darier's disease</li>\n<li>Epidermal Naevus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.266px;\">Acquired dermatoses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Segmental vitiligo</li>\n<li>Lichen striatus</li>\n<li>Linear scleroderma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0745","text":"Stem cells involved in ski​n homeostasis are present​ in all of the following ​areas except _________.","raw_text":"Stem cells involved in skin homeostasis are present in all of the following areas except _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bulge area of hair follicl​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal layer of interfollic​ular epidermis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Base of seba​ceous glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Base of apo​crine glands","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Base of apocrine glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce88f562c6da4816a901b5396c6b700ex1280x1790.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Base of apocrine glands</strong></p><p>Stem cells are not known to occur at the base of apocrine sweat glands.</p>\n<p>The <strong>stem cells</strong> are present in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bulge area of <strong>hair follicles</strong></li>\n<li>Basal layer of the <strong>interfollicular epidermis</strong></li>\n<li>Base of <strong>sebaceous glands.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Their function is to maintain skin <strong>homeostasis</strong>, <strong>regenerate </strong>skin appendages and <strong>repair</strong> itself after injury.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the location of stem cells of the skin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6724","text":"What is the ratio of type-​I to type-III collagen in​ the adult human skin?","raw_text":"What is the ratio of type-I to type-III collagen in the adult human skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​:4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8​:1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3​:2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4​:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8:1</strong></p><p>The ratio of <strong>type-I to type-III collagen</strong> in the adult human skin is <strong>8:1.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type-I</strong> collagen is the <strong>predominant</strong> collagen in the human <strong>dermis</strong>. <strong>Type-III</strong> collagen is known to predominate in the human skin during <strong>embryonic</strong> development, but during the <strong>early postnatal</strong> period, <strong>type-I</strong> collagen synthesis <strong>accelerates.</strong> This results in the ratio of type-I to type-III collagen in the adult human skin being approximately 8:1.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7050","text":"What is the type of connec​tive tissue present in th​e area marked by the arro​w in the given image?","raw_text":"What is the type of connective tissue present in the area marked by the arrow in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dense ​regular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dense i​rregular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loose i​rregular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Speci​alised","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dense irregular","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/14e909ce90a7418097400674a6ce4a64.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dense irregular</strong></p><p>The area marked is the <strong>reticular dermis.</strong> It is made up of <strong>dense irregular connective tissue.</strong></p>\n<p>It is found in the regions that are under <strong>considerable mechanical stress</strong> and where protection is given to <strong>ensheathed organs. </strong>It is usually present in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reticular <strong>dermis</strong></li>\n<li>Superficial <strong>connective tissue sheaths</strong> of the muscle and nerves</li>\n<li><strong>Adventitia</strong> of large vessels</li>\n<li><strong>Capsules </strong>of various organs and glands (e.g., testis, sclera, periosteum, perichondrium)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>dense regular collagen</strong> fibers are found in <strong>tendons.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3668tqy5","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"1_embryology_of_ear_and_malformations","title":"1.Embryology Of Ear And Malformations","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6651","text":"Which embryological struct‍ure gives rise to the pin​na?","raw_text":"Which embryological structure gives rise to the pinna?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st branc‍hial arch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd branc‍hial arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1st and 2nd branchial arch‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2nd and 3rd branchial arch‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1st and 2nd branchial arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d53e664792b341f8b2195e51d052f008x1280x2576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1st and 2nd branchial arch</strong></p><p>The <strong>1st</strong> and<strong> 2nd branchial arches </strong>give rise to the <strong>pinna </strong>(auricle)<strong> </strong>through six mesenchymal proliferation. These proliferations are called <strong>auricular hillocks</strong> and they fuse to form the auricle.</p>\n<p>The tissue from the <strong>second arch</strong> forms nearly all of the <strong>pinna.</strong></p>\n<p>The tissue from the <strong>first</strong> <strong>arch</strong> forms the <strong>tragus</strong> and<strong> external auditory meatus.</strong></p>\n<p>Image showing the development of pinna:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6653","text":"A patient is incidentally ‍noted to have the followi​ng finding. It is also pr‍esent on the other ear. W‌hich of the following is ​the cause for such abnorm​ality?","raw_text":"A patient is incidentally noted to have the following finding. It is also present on the other ear. Which of the following is the cause for such abnormality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistent opening of firs‍t branchial cleft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Improper fusion of auricul‍ar tubercle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infection of haematoma of ‍pinna","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Third pharyngeal pouch ano‍maly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Improper fusion of auricular tubercle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6191fc4c7b454e7aa11062d1bf11a49e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Improper fusion of auricular tubercle</strong></p><p>The given image showing the presence of <strong>sinus</strong> seen between the<strong> tragus</strong> and <strong>crus of the helix</strong> is suggestive of the <strong>preauricular sinus.</strong> It is due to<strong> improper fusion</strong> of the <strong>auricular tubercle</strong> formed from the first and second arches.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The preauricular sinus is apparent at birth and is usually bilateral. There may be occasional sebaceous discharge from the punctum. It does not regress spontaneously.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6654","text":"When are reconstructive su‍rgeries for congenital ma​lformations of pinna usua‍lly performed?","raw_text":"When are reconstructive surgeries for congenital malformations of pinna usually performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Soon aft‍er birth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Within 1 y‍ear of age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 or 6 ye‍ars of age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 or 4 ye‍ars of age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 or 6 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 or 6 years of age</strong></p><p><strong>Reconstructive</strong> <strong>surgeries</strong> for<strong> congenital malformations </strong>of the<strong> pinna</strong> (auricle) are usually performed around <strong>5 or 6 years of age</strong>. Autologous cartilages from ribs are harvested for these purposes and the ideal timing is between 6-10 years.</p>\n<p>At around 3 years of age, 85% of the auricular growth is complete and the cartilaginous growth is almost complete by 5 years. Surgery performed on the auricle after 5 years doesn't hinder further growth.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6656","text":"Invagination of which of t‍he following structures w​ill form the external aud‍itory meatus?","raw_text":"Invagination of which of the following structures will form the external auditory meatus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First phar‍yngeal arch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"First phary‍ngeal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Second bran‍chial cleft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Second bran‍chial groove","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. First pharyngeal arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. First pharyngeal arch</strong></p><p><strong>Invagination </strong>of the<strong> first pharyngeal arch </strong>gives rise to the <strong>external auditory meatus.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 375\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Previously, it was believed that the external auditory meatus develops from the first pharyngeal cleft. But recent studies suggest that the first pharyngeal arch gives rise to the external auditory meatus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6658","text":"Examination of a child wit‍h otorrhea revealed two d​ischarging sinuses. One w‍as present on the floor o‌f the left external audit​ory canal and the other o​n the left neck just belo​w the angle of the mandib​le. Which of the followin​g structures is abnormal ​here?","raw_text":"Examination of a child with otorrhea revealed two discharging sinuses. One was present on the floor of the left external auditory canal and the other on the left neck just below the angle of the mandible. Which of the following structures is abnormal here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First bran‍chial cleft","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"First bran‍chial pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Second bran‍chial cleft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Second bran‍chial pouch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. First branchial cleft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. First branchial cleft</strong></p><p>Given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>collaural fistula</strong>, and the <strong>first branchial cleft </strong>is abnormal here. </p>\n<p>The fistula has two openings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One situated in the <strong>neck</strong> just below and behind the angle of mandible and</li>\n<li>The other one in the <strong>external canal</strong> or the <strong>middle ear</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The track of the fistula traverses through the parotid in<strong> close relation </strong>to the<strong> facial nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6660","text":"Which of the following str‍ucture develops from all ​three germ layers?","raw_text":"Which of the following structure develops from all three germ layers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympanic‍ membrane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pi‍nna","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ear os‍sicles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Otic c‍apsule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tympanic membrane</strong></p><p><strong>Tympanic membrane</strong> develops from all <strong>three germinal layers</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Outer epithelial </strong>layer is formed by the<strong> ectoderm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inner mucosal </strong>layer by the<strong> endoderm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Middle fibrous </strong>layer by the<strong> mesoderm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6662","text":"Which of the structures gi‍ven below forms the eusta​chian tube?","raw_text":"Which of the structures given below forms the eustachian tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st pharyn‍geal pouch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1st and 2nd pharyngeal cle‍ft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd pharyn‍geal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd and 3rd pharyngeal cle‍ft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1st pharyngeal pouch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1st pharyngeal pouch</strong></p><p>The <strong>eustachian tube</strong> develops from the <strong>first pharyngeal pouch.</strong></p>\n<p>Initially, the first pharyngeal pouch forms the <strong>tubotympanic recess</strong>. The <strong>distal part</strong> of this recess widens to form the <strong>middle ear cavity. </strong>The <strong>proximal part</strong> forms the <strong>eustachian tube.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6663","text":"Which of the following pha‍ryngeal arches gives rise​ to malleus and incus?","raw_text":"Which of the following pharyngeal arches gives rise to malleus and incus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First‍ arch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Secon‍d arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third‍ arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fourt‍h arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. First arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. First arch</strong></p><p>The<strong> first pharyngeal arch</strong> gives rise to <strong>malleus</strong> and <strong>incus. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stapes superstructure</strong> is formed from the <strong>second</strong> arch whereas the <strong>stapes footplate</strong> develops from the <strong>otic capsule.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6664","text":"Which of the following arc‍hes gives rise to the sta​pes?","raw_text":"Which of the following arches gives rise to the stapes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First‍ arch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secon‍d arch","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Third‍ arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fourt‍h arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Second arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Second arch</strong></p><p><strong>The stapes</strong> develop from the <strong>second arch</strong>, <strong>except</strong> for its<strong> footplate</strong> and<strong> annular ligament, </strong>which develop from the<strong> otic capsule</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6665","text":"Which of the following att‍ains adult size before bi​rth?","raw_text":"Which of the following attains adult size before birth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ear os‍sicles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mas‍toid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Or‍bit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cor‍nea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ear ossicles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ear ossicles</strong></p><p><strong>Ear ossicles</strong> attain <strong>adult size</strong> before birth. </p>\n<p>The development of the middle ear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The ossicles are full-sized cartilage models by 15 weeks of gestation.</li>\n<li>Endochondral ossification is complete by 25 weeks.</li>\n<li>The middle ear is adult-sized at birth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6667","text":"Which of the following doe‍s not reach adult size at​ birth?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not reach adult size at birth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ear os‍sicles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mas‍toid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic‍ membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympani‍c cavity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mastoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mastoid</strong></p><p>The mastoid does not reach adult size at birth. It develops fully by <strong>2 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>In infants and children up to 2 years of age, the mastoid process is not developed completely. So, the facial nerve lies just beneath the skin near its exit. Hence, during surgical procedures, the incision is given slanting posteriorly, avoiding the lower part of the mastoid. This spares the facial nerve. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6668","text":"An ENT surgeon has difficu‍lty locating the mastoid ​antrum due to the presenc‍e of Korner's septum. Whi‌ch of the following sutur​es is persistent in this ​case?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon has difficulty locating the mastoid antrum due to the presence of Korner's septum. Which of the following sutures is persistent in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frontozygom‍atic suture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Petrosquam‍ous suture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Temporosqua‍mous suture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Petromast‍oid suture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Petrosquamous suture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4db1e67e8a574acea3e71e1a43e9d0c2x1280x1464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Petrosquamous suture</strong></p><p>The <strong>persistence</strong> of the <strong>petrosquamous suture</strong> is called <strong>Korner’s septum</strong> and it is responsible for the difficulty in <strong>locating</strong> the mastoid <strong>antrum</strong> and the deeper cells. </p>\n<p>Korner’s septum<strong> </strong>separates <strong>superficial squamosal</strong> cells from the <strong>deep petrosal</strong> cells.</p>\n<p>Incomplete removal of disease at mastoidectomy can occur if Korner’s septum is not identified. Mastoid antrum cannot be reached unless the Korner’s septum has been removed.</p>\n<p>Below is an image showing Korner's septum - </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0653","text":"From which of the followin‍g structure does the sacc​ule develop?","raw_text":"From which of the following structure does the saccule develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saculus ‍anterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Saculus ‍posterior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pars s‍uperior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pars i‍nferior","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pars inferior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pars inferior</strong></p><p>The <strong>saccule</strong> develops from <strong>pars inferior.</strong></p>\n<p>The development of the inner ear begins in the <strong>3rd week</strong> of fetal life and is <strong>completed</strong> by the <strong>16th week</strong>. The <strong>surface</strong> <strong>ectoderm</strong> on each side of the <strong>rhombencephalon</strong> thickens to form <strong>otic/auditory</strong> <strong>placodes</strong>,<strong> </strong>which then invaginate to form the <strong>otic</strong> <strong>vesicle</strong> (<strong>otocysts</strong>).<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">  </span></p>\n<p>The cells of the <strong>otic vesicle</strong> differentiate to form <strong>ganglion cells</strong>, which later develop into the <strong>vestibulocochlear</strong> <strong>ganglia</strong>. Later each otic vesicle divides into a dorsal component called<strong> pars superior</strong> and a ventral component called <strong>pars inferior</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pars superior </strong>gives rise to the <strong>utricle</strong>, <strong>semicircular</strong> <strong>canals</strong>, and <strong>endolymphatic</strong> <strong>ducts</strong>. <strong>Pars inferior</strong> gives rise to <strong>saccule</strong> and <strong>cochlear</strong> <strong>duct</strong>. The saccule is connected to the cochlea through a narrow duct called <strong>ductus</strong> <strong>reuniens</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7696","text":"From how many ossification‍ centre(s) does the inner​ ear develop?","raw_text":"From how many ossification centre(s) does the inner ear develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‍4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 14","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 14</strong></p><p>The inner ear develops from <strong>14</strong> ossification centres.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6666","text":"When does the fetus start ‍hearing?","raw_text":"When does the fetus start hearing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 w‍eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 w‍eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"30 w‍eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"32 w‍eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20 weeks</strong></p><p>The<strong> fetus</strong> starts<strong> hearing </strong>in the womb of the mother by<strong> 20 weeks </strong>of gestation due to sufficient <strong>development</strong> of the <strong>cochlea.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YVF6Fb6_","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"1_skeletal_and_dental_age_determination","title":"1.Skeletal And Dental Age Determination","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA0915","text":"A victim of child traffick‍ing was brought to you by​ the police for age deter‌mination. Which of the fo​llowing would you use as ​an accurate method for de​termining the same?","raw_text":"A victim of child trafficking was brought to you by the police for age determination. Which of the following would you use as an accurate method for determining the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal epiph‍ysis of femur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mineralizat‍ion of teeth","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fusion of spheno-occipital‍ suture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emergence‍ of teeth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mineralization of teeth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mineralization of teeth</strong></p><p>From the given options,<strong> mineralization</strong> of <strong>teeth</strong> is the more accurate method of age determination in <strong>childhood</strong>.</p>\n<p>The relationship between the<strong> chronological age </strong>and<strong> dental age </strong>is<strong> stronger</strong> than between chronological age and skeletal age.</p>\n<p>Two aspects of <strong>tooth development</strong> have been used in the estimation of dental age:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>emergence</strong> of teeth - superior part of the crown of the tooth appearing level with the surface of the alveolar bone</li>\n<li>The stage of <strong>mineralization</strong> of crowns and roots during development</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Estimation of dental age using<strong> mineralization stages </strong>of<strong> crowns </strong>and<strong> roots</strong> of developing teeth includes visualization from a radiographic image. This helps in viewing both erupted and unerupted teeth. It is a <strong>more accurate method </strong>of estimating age than emergence, because emergence is an event whose exact time of occurrence is not accurately known.</p>\n<p>The study of <strong>dental microstructure </strong>is the <strong>most accurate </strong>method of age determination in childhood. This involves counting <strong>perikymata</strong> (incremental lines on the surface of tooth enamel).</p>\n<p>Note: Eruption is a continuous process by which a tooth moves from its crypt in the jaw bone to its position of occlusion in the mouth. It is different from emergence. Emergence denotes that a tooth has pierced the gingiva. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0916","text":"Which of the following is ‍the earliest tooth to eru​pt in a 7-month-old baby?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest tooth to erupt in a 7-month-old baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillary ce‍ntral incisor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mandibular central incisor‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mandibular ‍first molar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxillary la‍teral incisor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mandibular central incisor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mandibular central incisor</strong></p><p>The earliest tooth to erupt in a <strong>7-month-old baby</strong> (primary dentition) is a <strong>mandibular</strong> <strong>central</strong> <strong>incisor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The time of emergence of primary dentition or temporary teeth:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 166px;\" width=\"383\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\"><strong>Tooth</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\"><strong>Emergence</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Mandibular central incisor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">6-8 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Maxillary central incisor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">7-9 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Maxillary lateral incisor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">7-9 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Mandibular lateral incisor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">10-12 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">First molar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">12-14 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Canine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">17-18 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Second molar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">20-30 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The deciduous teeth all emerge into the mouth by <strong>2.5-3 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>The time of emergence of secondary dentition or permanent teeth:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 166px;\" width=\"383\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\"><strong>Tooth</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\"><strong>Emergence</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">First molar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">6-7 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Central incisors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">6-8 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Lateral incisors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">7-9 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">First premolar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">9-11 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Second premolar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">10-12 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Canine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">11-12 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Second molar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">12-14 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Third molar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">18-25 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: <strong>6</strong><strong>-13 </strong>years is the age of<strong> mixed dentition. </strong>First molar emerges at 6-7 years. Second molar emerges at 12 years and third molar emerges beyond 18 years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0917","text":"Which of the following is ‍a superadded permanent to​oth?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a superadded permanent tooth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central ‍incisors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can‍ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prem‍olar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mo‍lar","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Molar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/414783c355a64abfb9d80defedb58ee0x1280x1475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Molar</strong></p><p>The 6 <strong>molars</strong> are called superadded teeth.</p>\n<p><strong>Permanent dentition</strong> refers to the teeth which begin to erupt from 6th year of age and remain throughout the life. They are totally 32 in number. The tooth distribution in each jaw is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">4 incisors</li>\n<li>2 canines</li>\n<li>4 premolars</li>\n<li>6 molars.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thus, <strong>each jaw</strong> has <strong>16</strong> teeth. Among these 4 incisors and 2 canines and 4 premolars are called as <strong>successional teeth</strong>, which erupt in the place of predecessor deciduous teeth.</p>\n<p>The remaining <strong>6</strong> <strong>permanent molars </strong>(1st, 2nd, and 3rd molar teeth) erupt <strong>independently </strong>without any predecessor teeth.<span class=\"s1\"> </span>Hence they are called <strong>superadded teeth</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0918","text":"Electron microscopy of the‍ surface of tooth enamel ​revealed a neonatal line.‌ What is your inference?","raw_text":"Electron microscopy of the surface of tooth enamel revealed a neonatal line. What is your inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital ‍malformation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heavy meta‍l poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Still‍ birth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Live ‍birth","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Live birth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Live birth</strong></p><p><strong>Neonatal line</strong> is an incremental line found on the surface of tooth enamel, that indicates that an infant was <strong>live born</strong>.</p>\n<p>The study of dental microstructure, which involves counting <strong>perikymata</strong> (incremental lines on the surface of tooth enamel), can provide an accurate method of age determination.</p>\n<p>One particularly pronounced incremental line, the <strong>neonatal line</strong>, can be of <strong>medicolegal significance</strong> in determining whether an infant was live-born or stillborn. It is formed<strong> very soon </strong>after birth and can be visualized by light microscopy if the child survived for about 3 weeks or by electron microscopy within 1 or 2 days of birth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0919","text":"A dentist evaluating a chi‍ld's teeth notices the pr​esence of mixed dentition‌. What would be the appro​ximate age of the child?","raw_text":"A dentist evaluating a child's teeth notices the presence of mixed dentition. What would be the approximate age of the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 – 5‍ years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 – 12‍ years","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 – 1‍5 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15 – 1‍7 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 – 12 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 – 12 years</strong></p><p><strong>6 – 12 years</strong> is the age of<strong> mixed dentition</strong> and the number of teeth remains <strong>constant</strong> at <strong>24</strong>.</p>\n<p>The period during which both <strong>primary</strong> and <strong>permanent</strong> teeth are in oral cavity together is known as mixed dentition period. This is the period where the deciduous teeth get replaced by permanent successors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0922","text":"While examining the skelet‍al remains from a bomb bl​ast, a forensic expert de‌cides to use the Gustafso​n's method for identifica​tion of the ages of peopl​e killed. Which of the fo‌llowing would you not ass‌ess in this method?","raw_text":"While examining the skeletal remains from a bomb blast, a forensic expert decides to use the Gustafson's method for identification of the ages of people killed. Which of the following would you  not assess in this method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tooth ‍length","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Attrition changes of occlu‍sal surface","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Period‍ontosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transparency‍ of the root","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tooth length","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tooth length</strong></p><p>Tooth length is not assessed in Gustafson’s method.</p>\n<p><strong>Gustafson's</strong> method of estimation of <strong>age</strong> of an individual is based on <strong>physiological changes</strong> in <strong>teeth</strong>. It is mainly useful in adults <strong>over 25 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Gustafson’s criteria includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Attrition - </strong> wear and tear changes of occlusal surface</li>\n<li><strong>Periodontosis - </strong> regression of gums surrounding the tooth resulting in loosening of the tooth and falling</li>\n<li>Secondary dentin - deposition of dentin in pulp cavity</li>\n<li>Cementum apposition - deposition of cementum in the root forming incremental lines</li>\n<li>Root resorption -<strong> </strong> involves both cementum and dentin</li>\n<li><strong>Transparency of the root -</strong> This is<strong> the most reliable criteria</strong>. Canals in dentin get filled with mineral and they become less transparent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gustafson's method has now been largely replaced by Lamendin's method.</p>\n<p>Other methods of <strong>age determination</strong> based on <strong>teeth</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lamendin's</strong> method - It evaluates root dentine translucency, periodontopathy, and <strong>tooth length</strong></li>\n<li>Miles' method - Uses transparency of the root as a single criterion</li>\n<li>Boyd's method - Determination of age of an infant by counting incremental lines on enamel</li>\n<li>Stack's method - Determination of age by measuring dimensions of teeth</li>\n<li>Variation in racemization of amino acids (especially aspartic acid) in dental pulp with age</li>\n<li>Evaluating the reduction of the pulp chamber through X-rays</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0923","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most reliable criteri​on for age determination ‌from Gustafson's method?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most reliable criterion for age determination from Gustafson's method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perio‍dontis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Root re‍sorption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary dentin depositio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Root tra‍nsparency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Root transparency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Root transparency</strong></p><p><strong>Transparency </strong>of the<strong> root</strong> is the most <strong>reliable</strong> criterion in Gustafson's method.</p>\n<p>Gustafson’s method of estimation of age of an individual is based on physiological changes in <strong>teeth</strong>. It is mainly useful in adults <strong>over 25 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p><strong>Gustafson’s criteria</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Attrition -  wear and tear changes of occlusal surface</li>\n<li>Periodontosis -  regression of gums surrounding the tooth resulting in loosening of the tooth and falling</li>\n<li>Secondary dentin - deposition of dentin in pulp cavity</li>\n<li>Cementum apposition - deposition of cementum in the root forming incremental lines</li>\n<li>Root resorption -  involves both cementum and dentin</li>\n<li><strong>Transparency</strong> of the <strong>root</strong> - This is the <strong>most reliable</strong> criteria. Canals in dentin get filled with mineral and they become less transparent. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0932","text":"Which of the following met‍hods is used for dental n​umbering?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods is used for dental numbering?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pearson‍ method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palmer’‍s method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gleser‍ method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trotter'‍s method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palmer’s method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f135ba0a94d425eb3a35ced2e970915x1280x1054.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24ffde27242944fda15af4e57e59e0e8x1280x211.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04849ce18b7743c891387f78060ca9c6x1280x250.PNG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3fa485c5fb2472f854873d0702adba0x197x63.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7377f2b574d3415f90569ef016603aa1x888x580.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00089d8203014e19ac85477e718d9262x1280x592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palmer’s method</strong></p><p><strong>Palmer’s notation</strong> is used for charting of <strong>teeth</strong>. Teeth will be numbered from 1 to 8 starting from the central incisor till the third molar in each quadrant. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows Palmer's notation for teeth charting.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The 4 most commonly used methods in<strong> dental charting</strong> are:</p>\n<p>1.<strong> Universal system</strong>: Teeth are numbered 1 – 16 starting from upper right to upper left and 17 – 32 starting from lower left to lower right. The image given below shows the universal dental charting system.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>2.<strong> Palmer's notation: </strong>Teeth will be numbered from 1 to 8 starting from the central incisor till the third molar in each quadrant. The image given below shows Palmer's notation for dental charting.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>3. <strong>Haderup system: </strong>This is similar to Palmer's notation but plus sign (+) indicates upper teeth and minus sign (-) indicates lower teeth.</p>\n<p>4. <strong>FDI (Federation Dentaire Internationale): </strong>Number is assigned to each quadrant and that number is placed before tooth's number. The quadrants and teeth are numbered as in the images given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Modified FDI:</strong> The quadrants numbering is modified as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pearson's, <strong>Trotter</strong> and <strong>Gleser's</strong> and Steele's formula are used to estimate the <strong>stature</strong> of the individual.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0934","text":"You receive a dental repor‍t from an autopsy of a ho​micide case, stating that‌ FDI notation tooth numbe​r '43' is missing. Which ​tooth is missing in the v​ictim?","raw_text":"You receive a dental report from an autopsy of a homicide case, stating that FDI notation tooth number '43' is missing. Which tooth is missing in the victim?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower rig‍ht canine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower le‍ft canine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper right‍ first molar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper left ‍first molar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lower right canine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b8db47583b144792b706e2b075451da6x627x431.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lower right canine</strong></p><p>In FDI notation 43, the number <strong>4 </strong>indicates the<strong> lower right quadrant</strong> and the number <strong>3 </strong>indicates<strong> canine tooth</strong>. Hence 43 indicates the <strong>lower right canine.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>FDI </strong>(Federation Dentaire Internationale) represents a number is assigned to each quadrant and that number is placed before tooth's number.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0936","text":"When does the fusion of th‍e spheno-occipital suture​ usually occur in females‌?","raw_text":"When does the fusion of the spheno-occipital suture usually occur in females?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"18 y‍ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"28 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 y‍ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 18 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 18 years</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>spheno-occipital suture</strong> fuses by about <strong>18–20 years </strong>in<strong> females</strong> and by about 20–22 years in males.</p>\n<p>The timing of cranial suture closure was one of the first techniques used to estimate the age of adult individuals. The spheno-occipital suture on the skull base is particularly useful for <strong>aging young adults</strong>. The other cranial sutures show more variation in the timing of closure. Sagittal suture is used for age determination.</p>\n<p>Sutures ossify on the endocranial surface before they ossify on the ectocranial surface, and this must also be taken into account when using this method. Overall, this method of age determination is <strong>less reliable.</strong> </p>\n<p>Age of fusion of skull sutures:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 154px; width: 263.872px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\"><strong>Age of fusion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Metopic suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">2-4 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Spheno-occipital suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">18-22 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Posterior third of sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">30-40 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Anterior third of sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">40-50 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Middle third of sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">50-60 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Coronal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">40-50 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Sphenotemporal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">50-60 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Squamous temporal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">&gt;80 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0937","text":"Match the following suture‍s with their correct age ​of fusion: Suture Age of ‌fusion 1. Coronal a. 2-4 ​years 2. Sphenotemporal b​. 40-50 years 3. Metopic ​c. &gt;80 years 4. Squamous ‌temporal suture d.50-60 y‌ears","raw_text":"<p>Match the following sutures with their correct age of fusion:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px; width: 258px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144.531px;\"><strong>Suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4688px;\"><strong>Age of fusion</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144.531px;\">1. Coronal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4688px;\">a. 2-4 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144.531px;\">2. Sphenotemporal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4688px;\">b. 40-50 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144.531px;\">3. Metopic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4688px;\">c. &gt;80 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144.531px;\">4. Squamous temporal suture </td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4688px;\">d.50-60 years  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-d,‍ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-d,‍ 3-a, 4-c","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-b,‍ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-d,‍ 3-c, 4-a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c</strong></p><p>Age of fusion of skull sutures are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 154px; width: 263.872px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\"><strong>Age of fusion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Metopic suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">2-4 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Spheno-occipital suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">18-25 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Posterior third of sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">30-40 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Anterior third of sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">40-50 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Middle third of sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">50-60 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Coronal suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">40-50 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Sphenotemporal suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">50-60 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Squamous temporal suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.872px;\">&gt;80 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Ages of commencement and completion of fusion of the different skull sutures are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 492px; width: 502.483px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Suture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"><strong>Commencement (years)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\"><strong>Halfway closed (years)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\"><strong>Completion (years)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Spheno-occipital synchondrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\">20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Coronal suture </td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\">40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Sagittal suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\">40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Lambdoid suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\">45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Masto-occipital suture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">45</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Asterion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.483px;\">50</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0948","text":"Which among the following ‍is the best indicator tha​t a female is more than 1‌6 years of age?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the best indicator that a female is more than 16 years of age?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fusion of distal end of ul‍na","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fusion of proximal end of ‍ulna","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fusion of medial end of cl‍avicle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fusion of ‍iliac crest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fusion of distal end of ulna","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fusion of distal end of ulna</strong></p><p>Complete <strong>union</strong> of the <strong>distal</strong> <strong>ulna</strong> is considered as a conclusive evidence of age <strong>more than </strong><strong>16 years</strong> in females.</p>\n<p>The appearance of <strong>ossification centres</strong> is complete by around <strong>5 years</strong>. Thus, from birth to <strong>5 years. </strong>Thus from birth to 5 years , the calendar of appearance of ossification centres is used for aging. After the age of 5 years, the fusion of epiphyses acts as a calendar <strong>up to the age of 25</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 130px;\" width=\"347\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Age determination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">&lt;5 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Appearance of ossification centres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">5-16 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Analysis of hand and wrist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">&gt;16 years(male)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Complete fusion of distal radius or ulna</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">&gt;16 years(female)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Complete fusion of distal ulna</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\"><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>18 years(male)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Complete fusion of distal femur or proximal tibia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\"><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>18 years(female)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Complete fusion of iliac crest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">&gt;21 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Complete fusion of medial end of clavicle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The analysis of the hand and the wrist is the recommended method for living age diagnostics. But, this technique frequently does not provide sufficient evidence to place an individual older than or younger than key threshold ages - 16, 18 or 21. Additional radiographs needed to establish the threshold ages of<strong> </strong>16, 18 or 21 can be any of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proximal humerus</li>\n<li>Distal end of the ulna or radius</li>\n<li>Distal femur</li>\n<li>Proximal tibia</li>\n<li>Iliac crest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>proximal end of the ulna </strong>cannot be used to conclusively state that the individual is more than 16 years. This is because complete fusion of proximal end may occur earlier than 16 years. </p>\n<p>Establishment of the age of<strong> 21 years</strong> can be done by evaluation of the <strong>medial end of the clavicle</strong>. Complete fusion occurs only beyond 22 years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0949","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most reliable method ​for estimation of age in ‌adulthood (&gt;25 years)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most reliable method for estimation of age in adulthood (&gt;25 years)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectocranial fusion of skul‍l sutures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endocranial fusion of skul‍l sutures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Degenerative changes in pu‍bic symphysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Degenerative changes in te‍eth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Degenerative changes in pubic symphysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Degenerative changes in pubic symphysis</strong></p><p>Estimation of age in adulthood (<strong>&gt;25 years</strong>) is best estimated by <strong>degenerative changes</strong> in <strong>pubic symphysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Physical anthropology has established several methods for determining age in adults which include <strong>macroscopic, microscopic, and biomolecular </strong>methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Macroscopic methods</strong> of adult age determination are the most important ones and consist principally in the degree of degeneration of selected articulations such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pubic symphysis</li>\n<li>Osteochondral surface of the 4th rib</li>\n<li>Auricular surface of the ilium</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Suchey and Brooks</strong> described <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">six phases</strong> of degenerative change in the <strong>pubic symphysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Until recently, the most commonly used method for estimating age using the architecture of the pubic symphysis was that of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Todd</strong>, who described <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">10 stages</strong> of pubic symphysis change during aging. More recently, researchers <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Suchey and Brooks</strong> described <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">six phases</strong> of degenerative change in the pubic symphysis.</p>\n<p>To solve this controversy, the following is suggested:</p>\n<p>A two-step procedure has been developed for estimating age:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If by looking at the skeleton, the age of the individual seems to be <strong>within </strong>the first<strong> 3 phases </strong>of the<strong> Suchey–Brookes pubic symphysis method, </strong>then this should be the<strong> method of choice</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the subject is classified in the <strong>last 3 phases, </strong>then one should switch to the<strong> Lamendin technique</strong> in order to assess age more accurately.</li>\n<li>Currently, age diagnosis at over 60 remains a problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In early adulthood, Suchey-Brookes pubic symphysis method is most accurate. In late adulthood, Lamendin technique of dental age estimation is most accurate. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0950","text":"The secondary ossification‍ centres that appear befo​re birth include all of t‌he following except:","raw_text":"The secondary ossification centres that appear before birth include all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal‍ femur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ta‍lus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal‍ tibia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calc‍aneus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Distal tibia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Distal tibia</strong></p><p>The <strong>ossification centre</strong> for <strong>distal tibia</strong> does not appear before birth. It appears 3 months <strong>after birth</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nearly all secondary centers commence ossification after birth, although a few develop in the last few weeks of intrauterine life. Secondary ossification centres which are already present when an infant is born include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calcaneus</li>\n<li>Talus</li>\n<li>Distal femur</li>\n<li>Proximal tibia</li>\n<li>Cuboid</li>\n<li>Head of humerus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Visualization of the secondary ossification centers of the <strong>distal femur</strong> and the <strong>proximal tibia</strong> and commencement of ossification in the <strong>calcaneus</strong> and <strong>talus</strong> is usually accepted as signifying a<strong> full-term fetus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9243","text":"First carpal bone to ossif‍y:","raw_text":"First carpal bone to ossify:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lun‍ate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capi‍tate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ham‍ate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scap‍hoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capitate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capitate</strong></p><p><strong>Capitate</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> carpal bone and <strong>pisiform</strong> is the <strong>last </strong>carpal bone to ossify.</p>\n<p>Age of ossification centres of carpal bones:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px;\" width=\"257\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Carpal bone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Age of ossification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Capitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">1-3 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Hamate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">\n<p>2-4 months</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Triquetrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">\n<p>2-3 years</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Lunate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">2-4 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Scaphoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">4-6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Trapezium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">4-6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Trapezoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">4-6 years  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">Pisiform</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.5px;\">8-12 years  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0635","text":"A child who is a victim un‍der POCSO act is brought ​to the department of fore‌nsic medicine for age est​imation. The X-ray image ​of the hand is shown belo​w. What is the likely age‌ of the child?","raw_text":"A child who is a victim under POCSO act is brought to the department of forensic medicine for age estimation. The X-ray image of the hand is shown below. What is the likely age of the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"13 y‍ears","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 13 years","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d445faa9afa64da7a2c7b9f0338f59bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6514911790b4f5eb9a14300b0d553b5x720x1007.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 13 years</strong></p><p>The child's age is likely to be <strong>13 years.</strong></p>\n<p>In the given X-ray image of the wrist and hand, <strong>all </strong>the<strong> carpal bones </strong>are<strong> seen</strong>. The <strong>lower</strong> <strong>epiphysis</strong> of the <strong>first</strong> <strong>metacarpal</strong> has not united with the shaft. This suggests that the age of the child is between 8 to 15 years.</p>\n<p>Additionally, the presence of the <strong>hook of the hamate </strong>in the radiograph suggests that the age is between <strong>13-14 years</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The age of ossification of the carpal bones:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 38.8252%; height: 221.8px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\"><strong>Carpal bone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\"><strong>Age of ossification</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Capitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\"> 1-3 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Hamate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\"> 2-4 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Triquetrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">2-3 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Lunate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">2-4 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Scaphoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\"> 4-6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 18.4px;\">Trapezium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 18.4px;\">4-6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Trapezoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">4-6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Pisiform</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\"> 8-12 years  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">Hook of the Hamate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0459%; height: 22.6px;\">13-14 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The age of fusion of joints:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 49.0133%; height: 162px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.7495%;\"><strong>Bone/ joint</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.2536%;\"><strong> Maximum age at fusion </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.7495%;\">Elbow joint centers<br/>Metacarpals</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.2536%;\">16 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.7495%;\">Wrist joint centers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.2536%;\"> 19 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.7495%;\">Hip bone centers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.2536%;\">22 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5202","text":"A girl was allegedly kidna‍pped by a man. In court, ​she says that she is a ma‌jor and is married to the​ man. X-ray images of her​ elbow, wrist and pelvis ​are given. What is the ap‌proximate age of the girl‌ ?","raw_text":"A girl was allegedly kidnapped by a man. In court, she says that she is a major and is married to the man. X-ray images of her elbow, wrist and pelvis are given. What is the approximate age of the girl ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 to 1‍5 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 to 1‍7 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"18 to 1‍9 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"21 to 2‍2 years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 21 to 22 years","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec32cb811c9646a89513cd980dbf5156.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 21 to 22 years</strong></p><p>The most approximate age of this girl is<strong> 21-22 years.</strong> </p>\n<p>Since the ischial tuberosity is fused in this scenario the girl must be aged &gt;21 years.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 584px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\"><strong>Bone/ joint</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.344px;\"><strong>Maximum age at fusion </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.656px;\"><strong>Interpretation of given x-ray</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Elbow joint centres (lower end of humerus, upper end of radius, olecranon to ulna)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.344px;\">16 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.656px;\">Fused, &gt;16 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Wrist joint centres (lower end of ulna and radius) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.344px;\">19 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.656px;\">Fused, &gt; 19 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Hip bone centres (head of femur, greater and lesser trochanter, ischial tuberosity)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.344px;\">22 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.656px;\">Fused, &gt; 21 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0853","text":"Which among the following ‍statements regarding phot​osuperimposition is false‌?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements regarding photosuperimposition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It may be performed on the‍ pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"This test is mainly exclus‍ory","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It requires an antemortem ‍picture of the victim","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2D images may be used for ‍this test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It may be performed on the pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It may be performed on the pelvis</strong></p><p><strong>Photosuperimposition</strong> is only performed on the <strong>skull</strong>, not the pelvis.</p>\n<p>Photosuperimposition is a well-established technique used for identification. It is done when a potential candidate for the identity of a skull is known to the investigating authority and <strong>photographs</strong> taken<strong> during life </strong>are available.</p>\n<p>Photographs of the skull are taken in exactly the <strong>same orientation</strong> in <strong>3 planes</strong> as the available photograph. These are then enlarged to exactly the same dimensions as the photograph, and either the negative or a positive print is made on transparent film.</p>\n<p>This is then laid over the photograph and adjusted in an attempt to match up the <strong>major anatomical landmarks</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nasion</li>\n<li>Supraorbital ridges</li>\n<li>Angle of the jaw</li>\n<li>Nasal aperture</li>\n<li>External auditory meatus</li>\n<li>Teeth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The test is mainly an <strong>exclusory</strong> one i.e. if the match cannot be made, then the skull is not that of the person in the photograph. If the match is good or excellent, then it may or may not be the same person.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XFkwY6wX","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"1_clinical_examination","title":"1.Clinical Examination","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD2277","text":"A soldier posted in Siache‌n is brought unconscious ​to the emergency ward. In​ order to rule out hypoth‌ermia, how will you best ​measure his temperature?","raw_text":"A soldier posted in Siachen is brought unconscious to the emergency ward. In order to rule out hypothermia, how will you best measure his temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‌al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rec‌tal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infrared tympanic thermome‌ter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axil‌lary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rectal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rectal</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the <strong>rectal temperature</strong> would be the best site to measure the core body temperature.</p>\n<p>At <strong>extremes</strong> of high or low <strong>temperatures</strong>, like in Siachen, the <strong>axillary</strong> and the <strong>oral</strong> temperatures are highly <strong>misleading</strong> and should not be used to measure the core body temperature.</p>\n<p>The <strong>infrared tympanic thermometer</strong> measures the <strong>temperature of the eardrum</strong> which is representative of the core body temperature. However, it often ends up measuring the temperature of the ear canal. This is because of inadequate insertion into the ear canal due to poor manufacturing design or the presence of cerumen. Hence, it is <strong>not </strong>a<strong> reliable </strong>method of measuring the core body temperature.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2274","text":"A patient in the emergency‌ ward stays still while l​ying supine and hesitates​ to move. He also has ver‌y shallow breathing. This​ attitude of the patient ​is most likely due to whi‌ch of the following condi​tions?","raw_text":"A patient in the emergency ward stays still while lying supine and hesitates to move. He also has very shallow breathing. This attitude of the patient is most likely due to which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal‌ colic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perit‌onitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute pan‌creatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin‌gitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Peritonitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Peritonitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>peritonitis</strong>. A patient with peritonitis lies supine without movement and has shallow breathing as <strong>any movement exacerbates the pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>renal colic,</strong> patients have a <strong>restless</strong> attitude, often rolling around because of pain.</p>\n<p>Option C: In <strong>acute pancreatitis, </strong>the patient usually <strong>bends forward</strong> and assumes a <strong>knee-chest position</strong> because of abdominal pain.</p>\n<p>Option D: Patients with <strong>meningitis</strong> present with <strong>neck stiffness</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2276","text":"An alcohol addict presente‌d with easy fatiguability​. On examination, he is f​ound to have pallor. Peri‌pheral smear showed macro​cytes and anisocytosis. W​hich of the following fin‌dings is most likely to b​e seen in this patient?","raw_text":"An alcohol addict presented with easy fatiguability. On examination, he is found to have pallor. Peripheral smear showed macrocytes and anisocytosis. Which of the following findings is most likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophic ‌glossitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Migratory‌ glossitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strawber‌ry tongue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macrog‌lossia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrophic glossitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0eab761167ac4cde935c3357c5a3ebbcx720x227.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79f72f2568564fcc809888157e34f555x720x960.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/caec2a95d19f47b296067d62fb78e620x691x559.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4676ad77c8684d88854b7a5328dc953bx512x455.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrophic glossitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>megaloblastic anemia </strong>likely<strong> </strong>due to folate deficiency caused by alcohol addiction. Folate deficiency leads to <strong>atrophic glossitis</strong> because of damage to the epithelial surfaces.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Migratory glossitis </strong>/ <strong>geographic tongue</strong> is due to rapid loss and regrowth of filiform papillae of the tongue. It is a benign inflammatory condition of the tongue associated with <strong>psoriasis</strong> and reactive arthritis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Strawberry tongue</strong> is due to the hypertrophy of fungiform papillae. It is seen in <strong>scarlet fever</strong> and <strong>Kawasaki disease</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Macroglossia</strong> is an abnormal enlargement of the tongue and can be congenital or acquired. <strong>New-onset</strong> macroglossia in adults is highly suggestive of <strong>amyloidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6640","text":"A woman presents with blui‌sh discoloration of the s​kin and mucous membranes.​ All of the following may‌ be responsible for the g​iven symptoms except:","raw_text":"A woman presents with bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes. All of the following may be responsible for the given symptoms except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methemoglobin exceeds 1.5 ‌gm%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfhemoglobin exceeds 0.5‌ gm%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduced hemoglobin exceeds‌ 2 gm%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oxygen saturation below 85‌%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reduced hemoglobin exceeds 2 gm%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reduced hemoglobin exceeds 2 gm%</strong></p><p>Bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes is suggestive of <strong>cyanosis</strong>. Cyanosis is seen when the concentration of deoxygenated or <strong>reduced hemoglobin</strong> <strong>exceeds</strong> <strong>4 gm/dL</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cyanosis is detectable with <strong>methemoglobin</strong> ≥<strong>1.5 gm%</strong> and/or <strong>sulfhemoglobin </strong>≥<strong>0.5 gm%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Central cyanosis is detectable when the <strong>oxygen saturation &lt;85%</strong>. In dark-skinned individuals, it may be detectable at an oxygen saturation of 75%.</p>\n<p>The types of cyanosis are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 430px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 19px; width: 219px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Central cyanosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 19px; width: 209px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Peripheral cyanosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 4px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 4px; width: 219px;\">\n<p>Due to diminished arterial oxygen saturation/presence of abnormal hemoglobin derivative.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 4px; width: 209px;\">\n<p>Due to diminished peripheral blood flow.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 219px;\">\n<p>Mucous membranes and skin are both affected</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 209px;\">Mucous membranes of the oral cavity or those beneath the tongue are spared.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 219px;\">\n<p>Decreases after O<sub>2</sub> supplementation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 209px;\">Does not decrease after O<sub>2</sub> supplementation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2278","text":"Which of the following ski‌n findings is not associa​ted with an endocrine abn​ormality?","raw_text":"Which of the following skin findings is not associated with an endocrine abnormality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acanthosis‌ nigricans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse hype‌rpigmentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Casal’s ‌necklace","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Purple‌ striae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Casal’s necklace","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Casal’s necklace</strong></p><p><strong>Casal’s necklace</strong> is not associated with an endocrine abnormality. It is a feature of <strong>pellagra,</strong> which occurs due to the <strong>deficiency of niacin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Acanthosis nigricans</strong> is seen in multiple <strong>endocrinopathies</strong> like acromegaly, Cushing’s syndrome, polycystic ovary syndrome, and insulin-resistant diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>Option B: Diffuse <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong> is seen in <strong>Addison’s</strong> disease and ectopic ACTH syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Purple striae</strong> are seen in <strong>Cushing’s</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6658","text":"What are ‌petechiae?","raw_text":"What are petechiae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large extravasation of blo‌od in the skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin hemorrhages measuring‌ 2-3 mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin hemorrhages measuring‌ 1-2 mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Skin hemorrhages measuring‌ 3-4 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Skin hemorrhages measuring 1-2 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Skin hemorrhages measuring 1-2 mm</strong></p><p><strong>Petechiae</strong> are defined as skin hemorrhages appearing as minute non-blanching spots that measure <strong>less than 2 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>A few conditions causing petechiae have been stated below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infectious diseases: Cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection, infective endocarditis, meningococcemia, and viral hemorrhagic fevers.</li>\n<li>Non-infectious conditions: Vasculitis, thrombocytopenia, leukemia, scurvy, and vitamin K deficiency</li>\n<li>Drugs: Anticoagulants (warfarin, heparin), quinine, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Large extravasation</strong> of blood in the skin is known as <strong>ecchymosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Purpura</strong> are also non-blanching spots but unlike petechiae, they measure <strong>≥ 3 mm</strong> in size. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6653","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen‌ted to the clinic with th​e lesions shown below. Wh​ich of the following cond‌itions is/are associated ​with this lesion? Chronic​ liver disease Pregnancy ‌Scleroderma Thyrotoxicosi​s Osler-Weber-Rendu Syndr‌ome","raw_text":"<p>A 28-year-old woman presented to the clinic with the lesions shown below. Which of the following conditions is/are associated with this lesion?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chronic liver disease</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n<li>Thyrotoxicosis</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Osler-Weber-Rendu Syndrome</div>\n</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a1b5b6d8da744f6b069efc6aa796def.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p>The lesions shown in the image are <strong>spider naevi </strong>(nevus araneus). They are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estrogen excess states such as pregnancy and OCP use</li>\n<li>Chronic liver disease - especially alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li>Thyrotoxicosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are vascular lesions characterized by the<strong> tortuous dilatation of arterioles</strong> just underneath the skin. On applying and releasing pressure over the lesion they typically fill from the centre outward.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6650","text":"You observed the following‌ finding in a patient in ​the OPD. Which among the ​following conditions is l‌east likely to cause this​ finding?","raw_text":"You observed the following finding in a patient in the OPD. Which among the following conditions is least likely to cause this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoca‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspi‌d atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic d‌issection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aortic dissection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1a12f7750e044336917752467bdb5571.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aortic dissection</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>digital clubbing.</strong> Among the given options, aortic dissection is least likely to cause digital clubbing.</p>\n<p><strong>Clubbing</strong> is a selective <strong>bulbous enlargement</strong> of the distal segments of the <strong>fingers</strong> and <strong>toes</strong> due to the proliferation of connective tissue, particularly on the dorsal surface.</p>\n<p>Causes of clubbing are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Pulmonary</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Bronchogenic carcinoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Lung abscess, empyema, bronchiectasis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Interstitial lung diseases</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Asbestosis</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>GI</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Inflammatory bowel disease (Ulcerative colitis, Crohn's disease)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Sprue, celiac disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Neoplasms - esophagus, liver, bowel</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Cirrhosis - juvenile and biliary</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Chronic active hepatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Cardiovascular</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Cyanotic congenital heart disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Subacute bacterial endocarditis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Aortic aneurysm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Arteriovenous fistula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Patent ductus arteriosus with right to left shunt</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Atrial myxoma</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Endocrine</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Grave’s disease (thyroid acropachy)</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>CNS</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Hemiplegia (unilateral clubbing)</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Skin disease</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Palmoplantar keratoderma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Fischer-Volavsek syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Malignancies</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Thyroid cancer</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Thymus cancer</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Hodgkin disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Disseminated chronic myeloid leukemia.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) does not cause clubbing. If clubbing is present in a patient with COPD, underlying lung cancer and bronchiectasis must be ruled out.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6654","text":"A 40-year-old patient pres‌ents with loss of appetit​e, jaundice, and easy bru​isability. He is a chroni‌c alcohol user, and the f​ollowing abnormality is n​oted on examination. What‌ is the most likely cause​ of this nail change?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient presents with loss of appetite, jaundice, and easy bruisability. He is a chronic alcohol user, and the following abnormality is noted on examination. What is the most likely cause of this nail change?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic iro‌n deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folate d‌eficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoalbuminemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/85dca4fdf73f4899a24c88ccee55d4e6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c1890878a194ff6820c3a4a36eb6731x720x616.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoalbuminemia</strong></p><p>The nail change seen in the given image is <strong>leukonychia totalis. </strong>It is a prominent feature of <strong>hypoalbuminemic states</strong>. The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>liver cirrhosis, </strong>which leads to hypoalbuminemia.</p>\n<p>Leukonychia is an opaque whitish discoloration of the nail and is seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dietary deficiency</strong>: Protein-energy malnutrition, pellagra, zinc deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>Drugs</strong>: Cytotoxic agents, Steroids, Retinoids, Pilocarpine</li>\n<li><strong>Dermatological pathology</strong>: Alopecia areata, erythema multiforme, exfoliative dermatitis, and SLE</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic diseases</strong>: Liver cirrhosis, renal failure, and cardiac failure</li>\n<li><strong>Miscellaneous</strong>: Trauma, malignancy, and toxins (arsenic, fluoride)</li>\n<li>Idiopathic</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The leukonychia secondary to <strong>arsenic</strong> poisoning is termed leukonychia striata or <strong>Aldrich–Mees lines</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6652","text":"A resident is examining a ‌patient with bilateral le​g swelling below the knee​s. He notes that on apply‌ing pressure, the indenta​tion does not persist on ​release. What is the like‌ly cause of the edema?","raw_text":"A resident is examining a patient with bilateral leg swelling below the knees. He notes that on applying pressure, the indentation does not persist on release. What is the likely cause of the edema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myxe‌dema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrotic‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic ly‌mphangitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute lymphatic filariasis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myxedema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myxedema</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral</strong> <strong>non-pitting</strong> edema in the lower limbs is suggestive of <strong>myxedema</strong>.</p>\n<p>While chronic<strong> lymphangitis</strong> and acute lymphatic<strong> filariasis</strong> can also cause non-pitting edema, they usually have a <strong>unilateral</strong>/localized presentation.</p>\n<p><strong>Nephrotic syndrome</strong> and heart failure cause <strong>bilateral pitting</strong> edema.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>generalized edema</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heart failure</li>\n<li>Chronic renal failure</li>\n<li>Nephrotic syndrome</li>\n<li>Liver cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>localized edema</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombophlebitis</li>\n<li>Chronic lymphangitis</li>\n<li>Resection of regional lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Filariasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6642","text":"While examining a patient’‌s pulse, you note a tappi​ng impulse when you raise​ his arm above his head. ‌Which of the following co​nditions would you not su​spect in the patient?","raw_text":"While examining a patient’s pulse, you note a tapping impulse when you raise his arm above his head. Which of the following conditions would you not suspect in the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left-sided h‌eart failure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ane‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left-sided heart failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left-sided heart failure</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>water hammer</strong> pulse, which is not seen in left-sided heart failure. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Collapsing pulse</strong>/Watson’s water hammer pulse is a rapidly rising pulse that collapses suddenly. This is because arterial pressure falls rapidly during late systole and diastole. It is also called <strong>Corrigan's pulse.</strong></p>\n<p>The various causes of the collapsing pulse include</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Physiologically,</strong> it can be present in fever and pregnancy. This is attributed to the combination of increased cardiac output and the reduction in systemic vascular resistance. </li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac lesions</strong> such as <strong>aortic regurgitation</strong>, patent ductus arteriosus, systolic hypertension, marked bradycardia, aneurysm of sinus of Valsalva</li>\n<li><strong>High-output states</strong> such as <strong>anemia</strong>, cor pulmonale, thyrotoxicosis, arteriovenous fistula, Paget's disease, <strong>beriberi</strong>, and liver cirrhosis (secondary to chronic alcoholism)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7693","text":"True about the definition ‌of Postural Hypotension:","raw_text":"True about the definition of Postural Hypotension:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in Systolic blood‌ pressure 20 mmHg after 6​ mins of standing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in Systolic blood‌ pressure 20 mmHg after 3​ min of standing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in Diastolic bloo‌d pressure 20 mmHg after ​6 mins of standing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in diastolic bloo‌d pressure 20 mmHg after ​3 mins of standing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decrease in Systolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 3 min of standing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decrease in Systolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 3 min of standing</strong></p><p>Orthostatic hypotension or postural hypotension is defined as a <strong>sustained drop in systolic BP of ≥20 mmHg</strong> or <strong>diastolic BP of ≥10 mmHg</strong> after <strong>3 min of standing</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can manifest as lightheadedness/dizziness, weakness or tiredness, difficulty thinking/concentrating, blurred vision, and anxiety.</p>\n<p>In <strong>non-neurogenic causes</strong> of orthostatic hypotension such as hypovolemia, the drop in the blood pressure is accompanied by a compensatory <strong>increase in heart rate of &gt;15</strong> <strong>beats/min</strong>. In neurogenic causes, there is aggravation or precipitation of orthostatic hypotension by autonomic stressors like a meal, hot bath, or exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-neurogenic causes</strong> of orthostatic hypotension include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac pump failure - myocardial infarction, myocarditis, aortic stenosis</li>\n<li>Hypovolemia - dehydration, diarrhea, hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Metabolic - adrenocortical insufficiency, pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>Venous pooling - sepsis, prolonged standing, or recumbency</li>\n<li>Medications - alpha-blockers, diuretics, phenothiazines, tricyclic antidepressants</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes <strong>removing reversible causes</strong> like <strong>drugs causing hypotension</strong>. Non-pharmacological interventions include <strong>patient education</strong> regarding staged moves from supine to upright, raising the head of the bed, and increasing dietary fluid and salt. If these fail, drugs like <strong>fludrocortisone</strong> and <strong>midodrine</strong> can be given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6656","text":"A 30-year-old African farm‌er presents with Winterbo​ttom’s sign. This sign is​ due to the involvement o‌f:","raw_text":"A 30-year-old African farmer presents with Winterbottom’s sign. This sign is due to the involvement of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep cerv‌ical nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraclavi‌cular nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ce‌rvical nodes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occipit‌al nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior cervical nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior cervical nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Winterbottom’s sign</strong> is due to the involvement of <strong>posterior cervical nodes</strong>. It is seen in <strong>African trypanosomiasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>African trypanosomiasis is a vector born disease caused by <em>Trypanosoma brucei gambiense/rhodesiense</em>. It is transmitted through the bite of a <strong>Tsetse fly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The clinical syndrome includes <strong>hemolymphatic disease</strong> with associated Winterbottom's sign and a <strong>maculopapular rash</strong> primarily involving the trunk. At a later stage, the patient can present with features suggestive of meningoencephalitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6644","text":"Relative bradycardia occur‌s in:","raw_text":"Relative bradycardia occurs in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typhoi‌d fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Q f‌ever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leptos‌pirosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>In the given options <strong>typhoid, Q fever, and leptospirosis </strong>are infectious causes of relative bradycardia.</p>\n<p><strong>Relative bradycardia,</strong> also known as <strong>Faget's sign</strong>, is observed when patients exhibit a lower heart rate than anticipated in relation to an increase in body temperature. Body temperature is commonly regarded as a factor that independently influences heart rate. It is estimated that for each 1°C (1.8°F) rise in temperature, the heart rate typically increases by approximately 15-20 beats per minute.</p>\n<p>While this pulse-temperature dissociation is not highly sensitive or specific for establishing a diagnosis, it can still prove beneficial in resource-limited settings due to its easy accessibility and simplicity.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Infectious causes of relative bradycardia include: </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Salmonella typhi</em></li>\n<li><em>Francisella tularensis</em></li>\n<li><em>Coxiella burnetii</em> (Q fever)</li>\n<li><em>Leptospira interrogans</em></li>\n<li><em>Legionella pneumophila</em></li>\n<li><em>Rickettsia</em> spp.</li>\n<li><em>Orientia tsutsugamushi</em> (scrub typhus)</li>\n<li><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em></li>\n<li><em>Plasmodium</em> spp. (malaria)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Noninfectious causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drug fever</li>\n<li>Beta-blocker use</li>\n<li>Central nervous system lesions</li>\n<li>Malignant lymphoma</li>\n<li>Factitious fever</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6636","text":"A patient with rheumatic h‌eart disease now presents​ with fever and the follo​wing nail finding. The na‌il lesions most likely ar​e?","raw_text":"A patient with rheumatic heart disease now presents with fever and the following nail finding. The nail lesions most likely are?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Roth ‌spots","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Janeway‌ lesions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osler‌ nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splinter h‌emorrhages","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Splinter hemorrhages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a671979e66f14b0bada5b734398b249c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Splinter hemorrhages</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>infective endocarditis </strong>(IE). <strong>Splinter hemorrhages</strong> are seen in the nails of these patients.</p>\n<p>Subungual splinter hemorrhages are classically identified as dark-red, linear <strong>petechiae in the nail bed</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other peripheral manifestations of infective endocarditis include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Petechiae: Common but nonspecific finding</li>\n<li>Purulent purpura</li>\n<li><strong>Osler nodes</strong>: <strong>Tender</strong> subcutaneous nodules usually found on the distal pads of the <strong>digits</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Janeway lesions</strong>: <strong>Nontender</strong> maculae on the <strong>palms and soles</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Roth spots</strong>: Relatively rare <strong>retinal hemorrhages</strong> with small, clear centers</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0182","text":"A 25-year-old man presente‌d to the OPD with a fever​ for the past 5 days. On ​examination, tender lesio‌ns were found on his extr​emities as marked below. ​What is the most probable‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presented to the OPD with a fever for the past 5 days. On examination, tender lesions were found on his extremities as marked below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hand-foot-m‌outh disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cat scrat‌ch disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rocky Mountain Spotted fev‌er","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infective endocarditis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a529cb14e85a40e6af9afc5c406ebecb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cad717c18d34dc19aa657b5a2c344a3x720x526.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infective endocarditis</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis for this patient is <strong>infective endocarditis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tender</strong> violaceous subcutaneous<strong> nodules</strong> in the fingers shown in the image are <strong>Osler nodes</strong>. They usually occur in the pads of fingers and toes but can also occur on the thenar and hypothenar eminences.</p>\n<p>Other lesions that are highly suggestive of infective endocarditis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Roth spots</strong>: These are exudative, edematous <strong>hemorrhagic lesions </strong>of the<strong> retina</strong> with pale centres.</li>\n<li><strong>Janeway lesions:</strong> They are <strong>nontender</strong>, slightly raised haemorrhages on the palms and soles. The image is shown below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2281","text":"On examination of the ches‌t wall, the presence of t​hrills indicate:","raw_text":"On examination of the chest wall, the presence of thrills indicate:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palpa‌ble S3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palpa‌ble S4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palpabl‌e murmur","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Palpable right ventricular‌ systole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Palpable murmur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Palpable murmur</strong></p><p>On examination of the chest wall, the <strong>presence of thrills</strong> indicates a <strong>palpable murmur</strong>. Thrills are the palpable vibrations produced by turbulent blood flow causing murmurs.</p>\n<p><strong>Palpable S3 </strong>and<strong> S4</strong> cause a <strong>double thrust</strong> in the apical impulse.</p>\n<p><strong>Palpable right ventricular systole</strong> is known as <strong>heave</strong> which is felt in the left parasternal area.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9233","text":"As an intern in the emerge‌ncy ward, which of the fo​llowing method would you ​consider as correct regar‌ding nasogastric tube len​gth estimation?","raw_text":"As an intern in the emergency ward, which of the following method would you consider as correct regarding nasogastric tube length estimation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tip of nose to ear to xiph‌isternum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tip of nose to angle of ea‌r to umbilicus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mouth to ear‌ to umbilicus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mouth to ear to midway bet‌ween xiphisternum and umb​ilicus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tip of nose to ear to xiphisternum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tip of nose to ear to xiphisternum</strong></p><p>The commonly used method to determine the <strong>length</strong> of a <strong>nasogastric tube</strong> is the <strong>NEX method</strong>: the <strong>tip of the nose </strong>to the<strong> ear </strong>to the<strong> xiphisternum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>approximate length</strong> is <strong>120-125 cm</strong>. The markings indicate the traversing of the tube through various parts of the GIT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">40 cm: Cardia of the stomach</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">50 cm: Body of the stomach</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">60 cm: Pylorus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">70 cm: Duodenum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A nasogastric tube or Ryle's tube is inserted in the<strong> sitting position</strong> with the <strong>neck slightly flexed</strong>. The tube is then gently inserted through the nostril and advanced until it reaches 10 cm. The patient is encouraged to swallow, allowing the tube to move down the esophagus with successive swallows until it reaches the measured distance.</p>\n<p>Subjective confirmation involves insufflating air through the proximal opening of the NG tube and listening for the sound of air in the left upper quadrant where the stomach is located. <strong>Objective confirmation</strong> can be obtained through <strong>pH measurement</strong>, <strong>chest or abdominal radiographs</strong>, ultrasound, or calorimetric capnometry.</p>\n<p>While widely practiced, blind NG tube insertion can lead to complications such as coiling of the tube in the nasopharynx, incorrect placement in the bronchial passage, or even esophageal perforation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6655","text":"A patient presents with ex‌cessive thirst, unexplain​ed weight loss, visual bl​urring, genital thrush, a‌nd lethargy. What is the ​most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with excessive thirst, unexplained weight loss, visual blurring, genital thrush, and lethargy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperg‌lycemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetic ‌neuropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperglycemia</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis in the given scenario is<strong> hyperglycemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The symptoms of hyperglycemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyuria</li>\n<li>Polydipsia</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Fatigue</li>\n<li>Blurry vision</li>\n<li>Frequent superficial infections (vaginitis, fungal skin infections)</li>\n<li>Slow healing of skin lesions after minor trauma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The metabolic derangements occurring in these patients are due to osmotic diuresis and a catabolic state caused by urinary loss of glucose and increased muscle breakdown. Blurred vision results from changes in the water content of the lens.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0719","text":"The recommended temperatur‌e of water bath for a pat​ient with frost bite is","raw_text":"The recommended temperature of water bath for a patient with frost bite is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"37 de‌gree C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"42 de‌gree C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"32 De‌gree C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 de‌gree C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 37 degree C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a95fd453f15d441cae2c655e8df43e7fx476x356.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 37 degree C</strong></p><p>The temperature of the water bath for a patient with <strong>frostbite</strong> should be maintained between <strong>37- 40°C (Option A). </strong>Circulating water is preferred for treatment. </p>\n<p>In frostbite, tissue damage results from severe environmental cold exposure or from direct contact with a very cold object. Tissue injury results from both <strong>freezing</strong> and <strong>vasoconstriction</strong>. It usually affects the <strong>distal</strong> <strong>aspects</strong> of the extremities or <strong>exposed</strong> <strong>parts</strong> of the face, such as the ears, nose, chin, and cheeks.</p>\n<p>The treatment includes - <strong>extraction</strong> from cold environment, <strong>rapid rewarming,</strong> analgesics for pain during thawing, antithrombotics or thrombolytics, cleaning of the injured area with soap or antiseptics, tetanus prophylaxis, monitoring for <strong>compartment syndrome </strong>and finally, elective late amputation after imaging in severe cases. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GGhekwZ1","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"1_general_microbiology","title":"1.General Microbiology","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MC5193","text":"Identify the condenser in ‌the following image.","raw_text":"Identify the condenser in the following image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Above t‌he stage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Below t‌he stage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Near the‌ eyepiece","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Above the objective lenses‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Below the stage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7ff62277586e4d888e78c475ea63dc96.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abe685cceae541b586b31df0d6b9d8d7x600x619.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Below the stage</strong></p><p>This is a modern <strong>compound microscope,</strong> and the condenser is located<strong> below the stage.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The purpose of the condenser lens is to <strong>focus </strong>the light from the illuminator onto the <strong>specimen</strong>. Microscopes with a stage condenser lens render a <strong>sharper image</strong> than those with no condenser lens.</p>\n<p>The <strong>simple microscope</strong> was invented first by <strong>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, </strong>the father of microscopy and microbiology. It uses <strong>1 lens</strong> for magnification. The <strong>compound microscope</strong>, introduced by Hans and Zacharias Janssen uses <strong>2 lenses</strong>, an objective lens and an eyepiece, to provide <strong>better magnification</strong> power than the simple microscope.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9924","text":"A 28-year-old sex worker p‌resented with a painless ‌ulcer on her vulva. On ex​amination, inguinal lymph‌adenopathy is present. Wh‌ich of the following meth​ods has been used to demo‌nstrate the causative age​nt as per the given image​?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old sex worker presented with a painless ulcer on her vulva. On examination, inguinal lymphadenopathy is present. Which of the following methods has been used to demonstrate the causative agent as per the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dark-field‌ microscopy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phase contra‌st microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluorescent‌ microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electron ‌microscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dark-field microscopy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9344114b08274ce392d349a6a9c5a28b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dark-field microscopy</strong></p><p>A painless genital ulcer with inguinal lymphadenopathy with the presence of spiral organisms on microscopy is suggestive of <strong>syphilis.</strong> It is caused by <em><strong>Treponema pallidum, </strong></em>demonstrated by <strong>d</strong><strong>ark-field microscopy. </strong></p>\n<p>Dark-field microscopy images have a <strong>dark background</strong> with a <strong>refractile organism</strong>. The dark ground microscope has an optical system that <strong>enhances</strong> the<strong> contrast</strong> of <strong>unstained</strong> bodies. It uses reflected light and has a higher resolving power than the light microscope. Dark-field microscopy is the the most specific and sensitive technique to visualize <em>Treponema pallidum</em> when an active chancre or condyloma lata is present.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Phase contrast </strong>microscopy improves the contrast to delineate structures within cells. A dark image is formed on a light background.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fluorescent</strong> microscopy uses fluorescent dyes like auramine-rhodamine to appreciate Mycobacterial species and malarial parasites. </p>\n<p>Option D: The<strong> electron microscope</strong>, invented by Ernst Ruska, helps in appreciating <strong>viral structures. </strong>It has a very high resolving power. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6186","text":"Which among the following ‌statements is false?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muramic acid is present in‌ prokaryotes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitotic division is presen‌t in eukaryotes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"80S ribosome is present in‌ prokaryotes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple linear chromosome‌s present in eukaryotes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 80S ribosome is present in prokaryotes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8568a624eb0441ec9428839975d407fdx1280x1114.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 80S ribosome is present in prokaryotes</strong></p><p><strong>80S ribosome</strong> is present in <strong>e</strong><strong>ukaryotes</strong>, while <strong>70S ribosome</strong> is present in <strong>p</strong><strong>rokaryotes</strong>.</p>\n<p>80S ribosome has subunits 60S and 40S, while 70S has subunits 50S and 30S.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6188","text":"Which of the following met‌hods will not help you vi‌sualize the bacterial cel​l wall?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods will not help you visualize the bacterial cell wall?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluorescenc‌e microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Electron ‌microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Differenti‌al staining","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Simple ‌staining","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Simple staining","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Simple staining</strong></p><p>The <strong>cell wall</strong> of bacteria <strong>cannot </strong>be seen with<strong> simple stains</strong> or<strong> direct light microscopy.</strong></p>\n<p>The bacterial cell wall is present outside the plasma membrane and confers strength and shape to the cell. It is composed of <strong>peptidoglycans</strong>. The cell wall can be demonstrated by the following methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plasmolysis</li>\n<li>Microdissection</li>\n<li>Reaction with specific antibodies</li>\n<li>Mechanical rupture of the cell</li>\n<li><strong>Differential staining</strong> procedures (E.g. Chance's method)</li>\n<li><strong>Electron microscopy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fluorescence microscopy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4675","text":"In which of the following ‌phases of the bacterial g‌rowth curve does sporulat​ion occur?","raw_text":"In which of the following phases of the bacterial growth curve does sporulation occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eea81ca0bb234241a3a29f89de33fd42.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c3dcc2e65fe4335a5e221dc2852bfc7x1280x1252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p><strong>Sporulation</strong> occurs in the <strong>stationary phase</strong> of the bacterial growth curve, which is marked as <strong>C</strong> in the image.</p>\n<p>The bacterial growth curve is obtained when a batch of bacteria is seeded in a liquid medium and incubated. It consists of 4 distinct phases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lag</strong> phase (A) - The bacteria adapt to a new environment. They synthesize and accumulate enzymes and metabolic intermediates required for multiplication. Thus, there is no increase in cell number but cell size increases. <strong>Maximum cell size</strong> is obtained towards the end of this phase.</li>\n<li><strong>Log </strong>or <strong>exponential </strong>phase<strong> </strong>(B) - The bacteria<strong> </strong>start <strong>dividing</strong> and their <strong>numbers increase</strong> exponentially with time. Cells are smaller and stain uniformly. The growth rate is constant. </li>\n<li><strong>Stationary </strong>phase<strong> </strong>(C) - There is depletion of nutrients and accumulation of toxic products. Thus, cell division reduces and a state of <strong>equilibrium </strong>is attained, where the number of new cells formed equals the number that die. Growth rate is zero. <strong>Sporulation</strong> occurs during this phase. Many bacteria produce secondary metabolic products such as <strong>exotoxins</strong> and <strong>antibiotics.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Death</strong> phase or the<strong> </strong>phase of<strong> decline </strong>(D) - Growth rate is negative. The <strong>population decreases</strong> due to cell death from nutritional exhaustion, toxin accumulation and the action of autolytic enzymes. Involution forms are common.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3298","text":"Which organism has the fol‌lowing cell wall structur‌e?","raw_text":"Which organism has the following cell wall structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram-positi‌ve organism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gram-negati‌ve organism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fu‌ngi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycoba‌cteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gram-positive organism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f723f7564bb4eeead08c6a350412652.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c71e961d160a417b927611ccc62306a8x1280x1114.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gram-positive organism</strong></p><p>The presence of<strong> teichoic acid</strong> and<strong> lipoteichoic acid </strong>with a thick peptidoglycan layer is a feature of <strong>gram-positive bacteria. </strong></p>\n<p>The cell wall of bacteria provides shape and ridigity to the bacterial cell. It is composed of peptidoglycans.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 345px;\" width=\"321\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.984375px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.234375px;\"><strong>Gram positive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.8125px;\"><strong>Gram negative</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.984375px;\">Teichoic acid, lipoteichoic acid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.234375px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.8125px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.984375px;\">Thickness (Peptidoglycan)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.234375px;\">Thicker</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.8125px;\">Thinner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.984375px;\">Variety of amino acids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.234375px;\">Few</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.8125px;\">Several</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.984375px;\">Aromatic and sulfur-containing amino acids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.234375px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.8125px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.984375px;\">Lipopolysaccharides and outer membrane</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.234375px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.8125px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option C: The cell wall of <strong>fungi</strong> consists of chitin, mannan, and polysaccharides (glucan).</p>\n<p>Option D: The cell wall of <strong>mycobacteria</strong> have mycolic acid, which is responsible for acid fastness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0793","text":"Which of the following hel‌ps in bacterial adhesion?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following helps in bacterial adhesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytoplasmi‌c membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meso‌somes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fimb‌riae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lipopolys‌accharides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fimbriae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fimbriae</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Fimbriae</strong> help in <strong>bacterial adhesion</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Fimbriae, also called <strong>pili</strong>,<strong> </strong>are hair-like surface appendage that projects from the cell surface as straight filaments. They are primarily seen in Gram-negative bacteria. They are polymers of <strong>pilin</strong>. Fimbriae are classified based on the function as ordinary pili - for adhesion - and sex pili - involved in bacterial conjugation. The functions of pili are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Bacterial conjugation </strong>through sex pili, which transfer of genetic material occurs.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Bacterial adhesion</strong> of symbiotic and pathogenic bacteria to host cells using adhesins.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Resistance to phagocytosis.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Antigenic pilin proteins are responsible for the immune response.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Twitching motility to bacteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5706","text":"Pasteurization is done at ‌______.","raw_text":"Pasteurization is done at  ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"73 degree Celsius for 20 m‌inutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"63 degree Celsius for 30 m‌inutes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"72 degree Celsius for 30 s‌econds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"63 degree Celsius for 30 s‌econds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 63 degree Celsius for 30 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 63 degree Celsius for 30 minutes</strong></p><p><strong>Pasteurization </strong>is done at 63 degree Celsius for 30 minutes.</p>\n<p>Pasteurization is the process of applying heat to milk to kill or stunt the growth of pathogenic bacteria. It was developed by Louis Pasteur. There are two methods for pasteurization:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Holder</strong> method: Milk is heated at 63°C for 30 minutes</li>\n<li><strong>Flash </strong>process: Milk is heated to 72°C for 15–20 seconds</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once heated appropriately, the milk is <strong>cooled</strong> quickly to <strong>13°C</strong> or lower. All the <strong>non-sporing</strong> pathogens (mycobacteria, brucellae, salmonellae) are killed in pasteurization. In the Holder method, heat resistant<em> </em><strong><em>Coxiella burnetti</em> </strong>may survive. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Louis Pasteur</strong> pioneered the techniques of pasteurization and sterilization. He also first advanced the <strong>germ theory</strong> of<strong> </strong>disease which states that most diseases were caused by microorganisms. Furthermore, he disproved the theory of spontaneous generation and developed <strong>vaccines</strong> for<strong> </strong>rabies and fowl cholera. He is thus known as the<strong> father</strong> of <strong>modern microbiology.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6196","text":"Which of the following met‌hods of sterilization inv‌olves exposure to 100 °C ​for 20 minutes on 3 succe‌ssive days?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods of sterilization involves exposure to 100 °C for 20 minutes on 3 successive days?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoc‌laving","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyndall‌ization","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pasteur‌ization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inspis‌sation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tyndallization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tyndallization</strong></p><p>The method of sterilization that involves exposure at <strong>100°C </strong>for <strong>20 minutes</strong> for<strong> 3 successive days </strong>is called <strong>ty</strong><strong>ndallization </strong>or<strong> intermittent sterilization.</strong> </p>\n<p>Tyndallization, which utilizes steam, was used for sterilization of media containing gelatin, egg, serum, or sugar. This method may not kill spores of certain anaerobes and thermophiles. <strong>Autoclaving </strong>has<strong> replaced</strong> this method<strong> </strong>now. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Autoclaving</strong> involves heating at 121°C for 15 minutes at a pressure of 15psi. This is the used for intruments, linen, laboratory ware.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Option C: In <strong>pasteurization</strong>, milk is heated at either 63°C for 30 minutes (the holder method) or at 72°C for 15–20 seconds (the flash process), followed by cooling quickly to 13°C or lower. </span></p>\n<p>Option D: Heating an article at 80–85 °C for 30 minutes on 3 consecutive days is called <strong>inspissation</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6227","text":"Which of the following wor‌ks by disrupting the cell‌ membrane?","raw_text":"Which of the following works by disrupting the cell membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ly‌sol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Povidon‌e-iodine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiom‌ersal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethylen‌e oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lysol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lysol</strong></p><p><strong>Lysol</strong> works by disrupting the <strong>cell membrane.</strong> </p>\n<p>Lysol is a<strong> phenol </strong>compound, which is obtained by distillation of coal tar. Phenols cause cell membrane damage and inactivate membrane-bound oxidases and dehydrogenases. This causes lysis and release of cell contents. Low concentrations of phenol can precipitate proteins. They are not inactivated by organic matter and are thus useful disinfectants. </p>\n<p>The mechnisms of action of other disinfecting agents are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>DNA damage\n<ul>\n<li>Ionizing radiation</li>\n<li>Ultraviolet light</li>\n<li>Hydrogen peroxide</li>\n<li>DNA reactive chemicals like acriflavin. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Protein modification\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Chlorine</li>\n<li><strong>Iodine</strong></li>\n<li>Heavy metals (e.g. <strong>thiomersal</strong>)</li>\n<li>Hydrogen peroxide</li>\n<li>Formaldehyde</li>\n<li>Glutaraldehyde</li>\n<li><strong>Ethylene oxide</strong></li>\n<li>Acids and alkalis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6228","text":"Which of the following met‌hods is used in the test ‌shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods is used in the test shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streak ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lawn c‌ulture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stroke ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stab c‌ulture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lawn culture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfa87a4962524d5ba3ed7a6f17ad729d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5793b45ab13a4ab28d3726faf4cdec52x1280x7594.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lawn culture</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>antibiotic sensitivity testing </strong>which uses the <strong>lawn culture</strong> method.</p>\n<p>Lawn or carpet culture is the method in which a uniform surface growth of bacterium is present on the medium. It is used for antibiotic sensitivity testing (disc method) and bacteriophage typing. <strong>Stab culture</strong> (option D) is used for demonstration of gelatin liquefaction and oxygen requirements of bacterium, for maintenance of stock cultures. <strong>Streak culture</strong> (option A) is routinely used for the isolation of bacteria in pure culture from clinical specimens. <strong>Stroke culture</strong> (option C) is used for providing pure growth of bacterium for slide agglutination and other diagnostic tests. Other methods of culture include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liquid culture - These are preferred for inocula containing antibiotics and other inhibitory substances.</li>\n<li>Pour plate culture - It gives an estimate of viable bacterial count in a suspension.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6963","text":"In which of the following ‌organisms will the follow‌ing phenomenon not be obs​erved in?","raw_text":"In which of the following organisms will the following phenomenon not be observed in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacillus ‌anthracis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella‌ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria ‌gonorrheae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neisseria gonorrheae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d54e9fd513524dc88676ccce220cdfbb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neisseria gonorrheae</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Quellung</strong> reaction (halo around the bacteria) and is seen in <strong>capsulated bacteria</strong>. <strong><em>Neisseria</em> </strong><em><strong>gonorrhoeae</strong> </em>is <strong>not capsulated, </strong>therefore Quellung reaction will not be observed in it. </p>\n<p>In Quellung reaction, a suspension of a capsulated bacterium is mixed with its specific anticapsular serum and examined under the microscope. The anticapsular antibodies bind to the capsule and thus the capsule becomes very prominent and appears swollen. Capsulated organisms can be remembered with the following mnemonic: </p>\n<p><strong>S</strong>ome -<em><strong> S</strong>treptococcus pneumoniae</em></p>\n<p><strong>K</strong>illers - <em><strong>K</strong>lebsiella pneumoniae</em></p>\n<p><strong>H</strong>ave - <em><strong>H</strong>aemophilus influenzae</em></p>\n<p><strong>N</strong>ice -<em><strong> N</strong>eisseria meningitidis</em></p>\n<p><strong>C</strong>apsules - <em><strong>C</strong>ryptococcus neoformans</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0678","text":"A 32-year-old laborer work‌ing at a construction sit‌e presented with fever an​d hemoptysis. The sputum ‌sample collected for exam‌ination showed the follow​ing. The smear will be st‌ained by which of the fol​lowing sequences?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old laborer working at a construction site presented with fever and hemoptysis. The sputum sample collected for examination showed the following. The smear will be stained by which of the following sequences?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gentian violet - iodine - ‌alcohol - saffranin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methylene blue - malachite‌ green- acetic acid-water‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methanol-methylene blue-ac‌id-water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbol fuschin - acid-alco‌hol - methylene blue","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carbol fuschin - acid-alcohol - methylene blue","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df1dfb0b7ef84d8aad96e9d50c301105.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8edb42ebf48a4090ad31422bf13da0c3x799x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c9e7a83cf584598971f5a3b436ef50ax1280x2413.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carbol fuschin - acid-alcohol - methylene blue</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of fever and hemoptysis in a labourer with the sputum smear showing acid-fast bacilli is suggestive of <strong>tuberculosis.</strong> <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>,<strong> </strong>detected on sputum smear, is appreciated by <strong>Ziehl-Neelsen staining, </strong>which would includes <strong>carbol fuschin - acid - alcohol - methylene blue. </strong></p>\n<p>Acid-fast bacteria (like MTB) in a stained Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) smear appear <strong>bright red</strong> against a blue background. Steps involved in ZN staining are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Place the sample on slide evenly spread it with rotational movement</li>\n<li>Air-dry the slide and fix it by passing it over a flame</li>\n<li>Apply carbol fuschin stain and heat to fix it.</li>\n<li>Wash with water to remove excess stain</li>\n<li>Decolourise with alcohol</li>\n<li>Apply Methylene blue/Malachite green stain</li>\n<li>Wash with water to remove excess stain</li>\n<li>View under the microscope</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Kinyoun</strong> modified the classical ZN staining such that heat fixing is not required. It is called the <strong>cold method. </strong>It involves <strong>increasing </strong>the <strong>concentration </strong>of <strong>phenol acid </strong>and the <strong>duration </strong>of staining. The slide is then washed with water and decolourized with acid alcohol. Counterstaining with Loeffler's methylene blue or malachite green is done for a minute and viewed under a microscope. Acid-fast bacteria in a stained smear appear bright red against a blue background.  </p>\n<p>The image attached below summarizes the steps.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: It depicts the order of Gram staining, which was discovered by <strong>Hans Christian Gram</strong> in 1884.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary staining with para-rosaniline dye (crystal violet, methyl violet or gentian violet).</li>\n<li>Application of dilute solution of iodine.</li>\n<li>Decolorisation with an organic solvent (ethanol, acetone, aniline).</li>\n<li>Counterstaining with the dye of contrasting colour (carbol fuchsin, safranine, neutral red).</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Option B: It includes the steps for performing <strong>Albert's stain </strong>which is used for detected <em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae. </em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A heavy smear of the bacterial isolate is prepared on a glass slide and allowed to dry without fixing.</li>\n<li>Albert’s stain is applied for (3-4) minutes. Then it is washed with water and Albert’s iodine is applied for one minute.</li>\n<li>Wash with water and let dry with blotting paper.</li>\n<li>Examine the slide under oil immersion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em> are slender bacilli with an arrangement resembling the letters V or L. They have metachromatic granules at the poles of bacilli which are also called polar bodies, volutin, or Babes-Ernst granules. Albert staining is useful in the demonstration of metachromatic granules. The presence of metachromatic granules helps to distinguish C.diphtheriae from diphtheroids which lack them. The images below show<em> Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</em> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6962","text":"Given are a few case scena‌rios. The etiological age‌nts of which of the follo​wing are microaerophilic?‌ Endoscopic biopsy from a‌ businessman with epigast​ric pain showing positive‌ urease test. Gram-negati​ve, small, spirally curve​d rod-like, motile bacill​i isolated from a patient‍ with diarrhea. Gram-nega​tive curved bacilli with ‌'fish-in-stream' appearan‍ce isolated from a child ‍with rice-water diarrhea.​ Nasopharyngeal swab from​ a child with paroxysmal ​cough showing 'mercury dr‌op' colonies. Sputum from‌ a farmer, cultured on LJ‍ medium showing dry, roug‍h, raised, irregular colo​nies.","raw_text":"<p>Given are a few case scenarios. The etiological agents of which of the following are microaerophilic?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Endoscopic biopsy from a businessman with epigastric pain showing positive urease test.</li>\n<li>Gram-negative, small, spirally curved rod-like, motile bacilli isolated from a patient with diarrhea.</li>\n<li>Gram-negative curved bacilli with 'fish-in-stream' appearance isolated from a child with rice-water diarrhea.</li>\n<li>Nasopharyngeal swab from a child with paroxysmal cough showing 'mercury drop' colonies. </li>\n<li>Sputum from a farmer, cultured on LJ medium showing dry, rough, raised, irregular colonies.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 2</strong></p><p>The aetiological agents of <strong>1</strong> and <strong>2</strong> are<strong> microaerophilic</strong>. </p>\n<p>The given case scenarios are:</p>\n<p>1. A businessman with epigastric pain and positive urease test has <strong>peptic ulcer disease</strong>, caused by <strong><em>Helicobacter pylori</em></strong>. It is <strong>microaerophilic</strong>.<br/>2. Diarrhea caused by Gram-negative, small, spirally curved rod-like, motile bacilli describes <em><strong>Campylobacter jejuni</strong></em>. It is <strong>microaerophilic</strong>.<br/>3. Rice-water diarrhea and Gram-negative curved bacilli with 'fish-in-stream' appearance describes <strong>cholera</strong> caused by <em><strong>Vibrio cholerae</strong></em>. It is a facultative anaerobe.<br/>4. Nasopharyngeal swab from a child with paroxysmal cough showing 'mercury drops' colonies describes <strong><em>Bordetella pertussis</em></strong>. It is an obligate aerobe.<br/>5. Dry, rough, raised, irregular colonies on LJ medium describes <em><strong>Mycobacerium tuberculosis</strong></em>. It is an obligate aerobe<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Microaerophilic</strong> organisms grow best in <strong>low oxygen </strong>tension (2-10% oxygen).</p>\n<p>Note: Mycobacteria, such as <em>M. bovis,</em> can grow at oxygen tensions ranging from atmospheric to microaerophilic conditions. However,<em> M. tuberculosis</em> is an obligate aerobe.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2219","text":"The given image shows a cu‌lture medium with lactose‌ fermenting and non-lacto​se fermenting bacteria. W‌hich of the following is ‌not a component of the cu​lture medium?","raw_text":"The given image shows a culture medium with lactose fermenting and non-lactose fermenting bacteria. Which of the following is not a component of the culture medium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tauroc‌holate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pep‌tone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neutr‌al red","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyst‌eine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cysteine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed5014b9750f49e289f0d7723daef45b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cysteine</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>MacConkey agar</strong>. <strong>Cysteine</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a constituent of MacConkey agar. </p>\n<p>MacConkey agar is a <strong>differential</strong> or <strong>indicator </strong>medium. <strong>Lactose-fermenting</strong> organisms (Eg: <em>E. coli</em>) produce a <strong>pink</strong> color, and <strong>non-lactose fermenting</strong> organisms (<em>Shigella</em> sp., <em>Salmonella</em> sp.) <strong>do not change</strong> the color. Contents of MacConkey agar include <strong>p</strong>eptone,<strong> l</strong>actose, <strong>a</strong>gar, <strong>n</strong>eutral red and <strong>s</strong>odium taurocholate (Mnemonic- PLANT). MacConkey agar is also a <strong>selective</strong> medium i.e. the bile salts (sodium taurocholate) and dyes (neutral red) inhibit the growth of Gram-positive organisms and Gram-negative cocci. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6964","text":"Which of the following is ‌an enrichment medium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an enrichment medium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6f7e786d805848d8874befe09700754a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A</strong></p><p>The image 'A' represents <strong>alkaline peptone water</strong>, which is an <strong>enrichment medium</strong> used for <em><strong>Vibrio cholerae.</strong></em></p>\n<p>The other images represent:</p>\n<p>Image B- Blood agar with <em>P. aeruginosa</em> colonies </p>\n<p>Image C- Loeffler's serum slope, which is used to isolate <em>C. diphtheriae</em></p>\n<p>Image D- Chocolate agar with <em>F. tularensis</em> colonies</p>\n<p>Enrichment media suppress the growth of commensal bacteria and allow for the growth of certain organisms. These media are used for samples with mixed flora e.g. stool. They are always <strong>liquid</strong> media, like alkaline peptone water. Other examples include <strong>tetrathionate broth</strong> and <strong>selenite-F broth</strong> for <em>Salmonella sp.</em> and <em>Shigella sp.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Enriched medium</strong> are those media to which additional nutrients such as blood, serum, or egg are added. They're used to grow bacteria with specific nutritional requirements. Examples include <strong>blood agar </strong>to test the hemolytic property of bacteria, <strong>chocolate agar</strong> for isolation of <em>H. influenzae </em>and <strong>Loeffler's serum slope</strong> for isolation of <em>C. diphtheriae</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3330","text":"A microbiologist observes ‌the following growth on a‌ blood agar plate. Which ​of the following will not‌ be the etiological agent‌?","raw_text":"A microbiologist observes the following growth on a blood agar plate. Which of the following will not be the etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridi‌um tetani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Proteus ‌mirabilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillu‌s cereus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corynebacterium diphtheriae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4822703d654c43d195961fb46450518b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>swarming type</strong> of growth on the agar plate. It will not be caused by<strong><em> Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Swarming is the growth of motile bacteria in the form of a thin film on solid culture media. It is exhibited by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gram-positive bacilli - <em><strong>Clostridium tetani</strong></em> (option A), <em><strong>Bacillus cereus </strong></em>(option D)</li>\n<li>Gram-negative bacilli - <em><strong>Proteus mirabilis</strong></em><strong> </strong>(option C), <em>Proteus vulgaris</em></li>\n<li>Gram-negative halophilic bacteria- <em>Vibrio parahaemolyticu</em>s </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Swarming can be<strong> inhibited</strong> by increasing the concentration of agar (6%), alcohol, sodium azide, boric acid, chloral hydrate, sulfonamide, and surface-active agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4399","text":"Bacterial transduction occ‌urs by","raw_text":"Bacterial transduction occurs by","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plas‌mids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sex ‌pili","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacter‌iophage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uptake of genetic material‌ by other bacteria.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacteriophage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef1f4b7b9c344fd59397dc29e3b6f8f3x1117x517.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacteriophage</strong></p><p>Bacterial transduction occurs by<strong> bacteriophages</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Transduction</strong> is a process of <strong>acquiring bacterial DNA</strong> from a bacteriophage (virus) that has incorporated bacterial DNA from previous host bacteria.</p>\n<p>Other methods of exchange of genetic material are transformation and conjugation. <strong>Transformation </strong>involves the <strong>uptake </strong>of genetic material from the <strong>environment,</strong> which is released by other bacteria. <strong>Conjugation</strong> is the <strong>direct transfer</strong> of DNA from one bacterial cell to another bacterial cell through a <strong>sex pilus</strong> or bridge. The transferred DNA is a plasmid, a circle of DNA that is distinct from the main bacterial chromosome. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6233","text":"Which of the following pri‌nciples is primarily used‌ in polymerase chain reac​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following principles is primarily used in polymerase chain reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transcript‌ion of DNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can amplify small amoun‌ts of DNA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can amplify small amoun‌ts of RNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reverse transcription of R‌NA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can amplify small amounts of DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51b31ae886574e26aab977cc1df3bed4x1280x667.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can amplify small amounts of DNA</strong></p><p>The primary principle used in <strong>polymerase chain reaction </strong>(PCR) is the <strong>amplification</strong> of small amounts of <strong>DNA</strong>. </p>\n<p>PCR is an automated process in which a <strong>single</strong> or a few copies of a DNA piece is amplified to generate of <strong>thousands</strong> to millions of <strong>copies</strong> of that particular DNA sequence. There are 3 basic steps are involved in PCR:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA extraction from microorganism</li>\n<li>Amplification of extracted DNA - Denaturation (95°C), Primer annealing (55°C), Extension (72°C)</li>\n<li>Gel electrophoresis of the amplified product</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PCR is used in rapid diagnostic tests for infections (e.g. HIV DNA), forensic investigations etc. The <strong>founder</strong> of <strong>PCR</strong> is <strong>Karry B Mullis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6234","text":"Which of the following des‌cribes the reverse transc‌riptase enzyme used in RT​-PCR correctly? (RT-PCR -‌ Reverse transcription po‌lymerase chain reaction)","raw_text":"<p> Which of the following describes the reverse transcriptase enzyme used in RT-PCR correctly?</p>\n<p>(RT-PCR - Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA-dependent RNA polymera‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RNA-dependent DNA polymera‌se","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DNA-dependent DNA polymera‌se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RNA-dependent RNA polymera‌se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA-dependent DNA polymerase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe6e0e0a13f640ba90841a195d16aa7ex335x424.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA-dependent DNA polymerase</strong></p><p>The enzyme involved in RT-PCR is <strong>reverse transcriptase</strong> (RT) which has <strong>RNA-dependent DNA polymerase</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>In reverse transcription-polymerase chain reation (RT-PCR), reverse transcriptase <strong>synthesizes DNA </strong>from the RNA template. RT-PCR can be used for the demonstration of poliovirus RNA, causing paralytic poliomyelitis, in <strong>CSF</strong>. It can also be used for the detection of viruses from <strong>nasopharyngeal swabs</strong> (SARS-CoV2, H1N1).</p>\n<p>In real-time RT-PCR, <strong>fluorescent dyes</strong> or <strong>DNA probes</strong> are used to<strong> </strong>quantify the pathogen DNA load in the specimen. So, it can be used to quantify viral loads in HIV, hepatitis B, etc., and is also called<strong> quantitative PCR</strong> (qPCR).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6232","text":"What are jumping genes kno‌wn as?","raw_text":"What are jumping genes known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epis‌omes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plas‌mids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trans‌posons","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cos‌mids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transposons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transposons</strong></p><p><strong>Jumping genes</strong> are also called <strong>transposons</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transposons are genetic elements that can move between chromosomal and extra-chromosomal DNA molecules. Insertion of a transposon leads to acquisition of <strong>new characteristics</strong> by the recipient DNA. Transposons contain several genes, including those necessary for their <strong>migration </strong>from one genetic locus to another. They may contain genes for specialised functions like antibiotic resistance. However, they do not contain genes for their replication.</p>\n<p>Jumping genes were discovered by <strong>Barbara McClintock</strong> and she was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1983 for the same. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Options A and B: The cytoplasmic carriers of genetic information are termed <strong>plasmids</strong> or <strong>episomes.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: A <strong>cosmid</strong> is a type of hybrid plasmid that contains a Lambda phage cos sequence. (Cos sites + plasmid = cosmids). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6937","text":"In which of the following ‌situations will you use a‌ sanitizer? A. Before exa​mining the patient B. Aft‌er examining the patient ‌C. After touching the pat​ient's surroundings D. Ha‌nds are visibly soiled","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following situations will you use a sanitizer?</p>\n<p>A. Before examining the patient<br/>B. After examining the patient<br/>C. After touching the patient's surroundings<br/>D. Hands are visibly soiled</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A, B &amp;am‌p; C only","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A &amp;amp;‌ B only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A, B C ‌&amp;amp; D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C &amp;amp;‌ D only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A, B & C only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed49ba92c81c422786618535b1224e46x1280x1901.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A, B &amp; C only</strong></p><p>You should use a sanitiser <strong>before</strong> and <strong>after examining </strong>the <strong>patient</strong> and <strong>after touching</strong> the patients' <strong>surroundings</strong>. The hands should be washed with soap and water thoroughly in case they are visibly soiled.</p>\n<p><strong>Sanitization</strong> is the process through which pathogenic organisms are reduced to safe levels on inanimate objects to reduce the likelihood of cross-infections. It is a component of hand-hygiene, which is demonstrated by the image below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Disinfectants like chlorhexidine and alexidine have a biguanide functional group. They are mostly used as oral disinfectants and hand products. They are bactericidal but not sporicidal. Mycobacteria are also resistant to these compounds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4721","text":"An orthopedician is perfor‌ming the following proced‌ure to visualize the ante​rior cruciate ligament in‌ a patient. Which of the ‌following will be used to​ sterilise the instrument‌ used?","raw_text":"An orthopedician is performing the following procedure to visualize the anterior cruciate ligament in a patient. Which of the following will be used to sterilise the instrument used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethylen‌e oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gamma‌ rays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen‌ peroxide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ultravio‌let rays","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydrogen peroxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/07713fe813f44bdab8c336a521cb14ff.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydrogen peroxide</strong></p><p>The given image shows arthroscopy being performed. <strong>Arthroscopes</strong> are sterilized using <strong>plasma sterilization </strong>which utilises <strong>hydrogen peroxide. </strong></p>\n<p>In plasma sterilisation,<strong> radiofrequency energy</strong> is applied to create an <strong>electromagnetic field</strong> through which hydrogen peroxide vapours are passed. This generates <strong>plasma</strong> which<strong> </strong>contains <strong>free radicals</strong> of hydrogen and oxygen. Plasma denatures all organisms and is used sterilize<strong> arthroscopes</strong> and <strong>ureteroscopes</strong>. Sterilization control is monitored by <em>Geobacillus stearothermophilus</em> and <em>Bacillus atrophaeus. </em></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Ethylene oxide</strong> has both microbicidal and sporicidal activity. It is used for sterilisation heat-sensitive equipment like heart-lung machines, respirators, suture materials etc. Sterilization control is monitored by <em>Bacillius atrophaeus</em> and <em>Bacillus subtilus.</em></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Gamma rays</strong> cause breakage of DNA. It destroys spores and vegetative cells, but is not effective against viruses. It is referred to as cold sterilisation and is used in commercial plants to sterilize plastics, syringes, swabs. Sterilization control is monitored by <em>Bacillus pumilius</em>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Ultraviolet rays</strong> are used to sterilise biosafety cabinets, entryways, operation theatres and laboratories. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KkSbQfmy","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"1_anatomy_of_female_pelvic_organs","title":"1.Anatomy Of Female Pelvic Organs","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB4916","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a part of the vulva?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the vulva?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labia ‍majora","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vest‍ibule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Greater vest‍ibular glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cer‍vix","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/755707c1e3454f4e9124ab88af1912cdx600x942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cervix</strong></p><p><strong>Cervix</strong> is not a part of the vulva. </p>\n<p>The <strong>vulva</strong> <strong>includes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mons veneris</li>\n<li>Labia majora</li>\n<li>Labia minora</li>\n<li>Clitoris</li>\n<li>Vestibule</li>\n<li>Vestibular bulb</li>\n<li>Greater vestibular glands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The vulva is <strong>posteriorly</strong> related to the <strong>perineum</strong>. </p>\n<p>The triangular space bounded by clitoris, labia minora and fourchette is the <strong>vestibule. </strong>It is present between the <strong>labia minora anteriorly</strong> and<strong> the introitus posteriorly</strong>. The posterior part of the vestibule <strong>between</strong> the <strong>fourchette</strong> and the <strong>vaginal introitus</strong> is called <strong>fossa navicularis. </strong>The vestibule has 4 openings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urethral opening</li>\n<li>Vaginal orifice opening</li>\n<li>Bartholin’s duct on either side</li>\n<li>Ducts of paraurethral glands</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4919","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g labia majora?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding labia majora?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They join posteriorly to f‍orm the posterior commiss​ure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is homologous to scrotu‍m in male","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The round ligament termina‍tes at its posterior end","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It contains sebaceous glan‍ds and apocrine glands","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The round ligament terminates at its posterior end","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eef3911f36d145b7b2628361e6b0c831x600x942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The round ligament terminates at its posterior end</strong></p><p>The <strong>round ligament</strong> terminates at the<strong> anterior end </strong>of the <strong>labia majora</strong>.</p>\n<p>The labia majora lie on either side of the vulva and <strong>join posteriorly</strong> to form the <strong>posterior commissure</strong>. The inner side is hairless. Labia majora are<strong> homologous</strong> to the <strong>scrotum</strong> in males. The labia majora and the mons veneris contain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hair follicles</li>\n<li><strong>Sebaceous glands</strong></li>\n<li>Modified sweat glands, known as <strong>apocrine glands</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The labia minora are two cutaneous folds between the labia major. Anteriorly they enclose the clitoris and unite with each other in front and behind the clitoris to form prepuce and frenulum respectively. The<strong> labia minora</strong> are <strong>homo</strong><strong>logous </strong>to the <strong>ventral aspect</strong> of the <strong>penis.</strong></p>\n<p>The clitoris is a small erectile body lying in the anterior-most part of the vulva. The <strong>clitoris</strong> is <strong>homologous</strong> to<strong> glans penis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4920","text":"A patient presented to the‍ gynecology OPD with the ​following finding. Which ‌structure in men is homol​ogous to the affected str‌ucture?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the gynecology OPD with the following finding. Which structure in men is homologous to the affected structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostat‍e gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cowper’‍s gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glands o‍f Littre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meibomia‍n glands","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cowper’s gland","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d45099ff6da34dd6aa0f4ffdefa95e54.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1cdb7f0da40499e8a2a564ede7d396ax600x719.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cowper’s gland</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>Bartholin's cyst</strong>, which involves the<strong> Bartholin's glands.</strong> These glands are <strong>homologous</strong> to the male bulbourethral or <strong>Cowper glands</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 847\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The ducts of Bartholin's glands open in the groove between the hymen and the labia minora at approximately 5 and 7 o’clock positions. They are <strong>stimulated by sexual arousal</strong>. The glands contain columnar cells that <strong>secrete mucus with lubricant</strong> properties. Contraction of the bulbospongiosus muscle expresses gland secretions.</p>\n<p><strong>Bartholin's cyst</strong> occurs due to obstruction of the ducts of the Bartholin's glands by proteinaceous material, or due to infection. Symptomatic or recurrent cysts may require<strong> marsupialization</strong> or gland excision. An <strong>infected</strong> Bartholin's cyst can lead to an <strong>Bartholin's abscess</strong>. This requires <strong>surgical drainage</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4925","text":"What is the pH of the vagi‍na before puberty?","raw_text":"What is the pH of the vagina before puberty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍7","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4‍.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4‍.2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 7</strong></p><p>The <strong>pH</strong> of the vagina <strong>before puberty</strong> is<strong> 7</strong>. In <strong>adult</strong> females, the <strong>pH</strong> is <strong>acidic. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Doderlein's bacilli</strong> (<em>Lactobacillus </em>sp.) are part of the normal vaginal flora. They <strong>produce lactic acid</strong> from glycogen in the vaginal epithelium and make the <strong>vaginal pH acidic.</strong> They are present in newborns and disappear in 10-14 days. They reappear at puberty and disappear after menopause. Vaginal flora also includes microbes such as <em>Clostridium welchii.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Vaginal defence</strong> mechanisms are influenced by <strong>estrogen</strong>. In newborns, maternal estrogens maintain the defence mechanisms for 10-14 days. After that, the levels wane. During puberty, the circulating estrogen maintains the defence mechanism. </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Period</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Vaginal pH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Doderlein bacteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Types of cells</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Birth-2 weeks</td>\n<td>4-5</td>\n<td>+</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2weeks-puberty</td>\n<td>&gt;7</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Parabasal cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Puberty</td>\n<td>4-4.5</td>\n<td>sparse</td>\n<td>Superficial &amp; intermediate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reproductive</td>\n<td>4-5</td>\n<td>plenty</td>\n<td>Superficial &amp; intermediate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pregnancy</td>\n<td>3.8-4.1</td>\n<td>plenty</td>\n<td>Intermediate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postmenopause</td>\n<td>&gt;7</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Parabasal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: The pH is higher in the upper part of the vagina due to mixing with alkaline cervical mucus. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4929","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about relations​ of the vagina?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about relations of the vagina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bladder -‍ anterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bartholin’s gland - poster‍ior","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Levator an‍i - lateral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pouch of Douglas - posteri‍or","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bartholin’s gland - posterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fe72c5005434608b66d52c2b5520de6x1280x1286.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17a539c5e1e942a0a74d91d4abe7926cx1280x1128.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bartholin’s gland - posterior</strong></p><p><strong>Bartholin’s gland</strong> is present <strong>lateral </strong>to the vagina. </p>\n<p>The vagina is related to the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anteriorly:\n<ul>\n<li>Bladder (upper 1/3<sup>rd</sup>)</li>\n<li>Urethra (lower 2/3<sup>rd</sup>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Posteriorly \n<ul>\n<li>Pouch of Douglas </li>\n<li>Ampulla of the rectum</li>\n<li>Perineal body</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Laterally\n<ul>\n<li>Mackenrodt's ligament</li>\n<li>Levator ani</li>\n<li>Bartholin's glands </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4930","text":"Identify the type of hymen‍.","raw_text":"Identify the type of hymen.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cribrifo‍rm hymen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Parous‍ hymen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fimbriat‍ed hymen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septat‍e hymen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cribriform hymen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/11c081fb01dd4ea5a8843e4be2c9efe1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f38fba7383a142df881e33c6104cde0cx1280x3106.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cribriform hymen</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>cribriform hymen</strong> because it has multiple small openings.</p>\n<p>Based on the shape of its opening, the hymen may be classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 515px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 104.854px;\"><strong>Hymen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 393.146px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104.854px;\">Annular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.146px;\">Oval central opening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104.854px;\">Crescentic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.146px;\">Semilunar central opening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104.854px;\">Vertical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.146px;\">Vertical slit-like opening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104.854px;\">Septate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.146px;\">2 lateral openings partitioned by a bridge of hymenal tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104.854px;\">Cribriform</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.146px;\">Multiple small openings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other variants of the hymen:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 686.854px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Variants</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 538.854px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Absence of hymen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 538.854px;\">Congenital deformity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Infantile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 538.854px;\">Small central linear opening.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Imperforate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 538.854px;\">No opening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Fimbriated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 538.854px;\">\n<p>Free margin has symmetrical indentations anteriorly</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Ring or fringe-like </td>\n<td style=\"width: 538.854px;\">\n<p>A narrow ring of thick membrane is present all around the vaginal orifice</p>\n<p>It can easily distend during sexual intercourse without rupturing the hymen</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The hymen is lined by stratified squamous epithelium on both sides. The hymen is most commonly <strong>torn posterolaterally</strong> or<strong> posteriorly</strong>. It is replaced by tags <strong>after childbirth</strong> which are called as <strong>carunculae myrtiformes</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4931","text":"A patient with uterine pro‍lapse and urethrocele has​ weakness in the superfic‌ial and deep perineal mus​cles. What level of suppo‌rt is lost here?","raw_text":"A patient with uterine prolapse and urethrocele has weakness in the superficial and deep perineal muscles. What level of support is lost here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lev‍el 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lev‍el 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lev‍el 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lev‍el 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Level 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Level 3</strong></p><p>In the given case scenario<strong>, level 3</strong> supports including the <strong>superficial and deep perineal muscles</strong> are lost.<strong> </strong>This causes <strong>urethrocele.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>DeLancey's levels</strong> of support are used to describe the supports of the genital organs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4933","text":"Anteflexion of the uterus ‍is at the level of ___.","raw_text":"Anteflexion of the uterus is at the level of ___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exter‍nal os","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inter‍nal os","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper part‍ of vagina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower part‍ of vagina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal os","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3b0ee47e50b4189a7af88bd5a30dd6bx600x656.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d56361ed50b439cbbebf2194041940bx1280x2637.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal os</strong></p><p><strong>Anteflexion</strong> of the uterus is seen <strong>at</strong> the level of the <strong>internal os</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 535px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.172px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Angle of Anteversion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.828px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Angle of Anteflexion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px;\">The angle between the <strong>cervix and vagina</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.828125px;\">The angle between <strong>cervix and uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px;\"><strong>90<sup>o</sup></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.828125px;\"><strong><strong>120</strong></strong><strong><sup>o</sup></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(Mnemonic - V for <strong>v</strong>ersion, V for <strong>v</strong>agina)</p>\n<p>The uterus <strong>normally</strong> lies in a position of <strong>anteversion</strong> and <strong>anteflexion</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>anteverted </strong>position, the external os is directed downward and backwards. On vaginal examination, the fingers come in contact with the<strong> anterior lip of the cervix </strong>as it is the <strong>lowest part.</strong></p>\n<p>When the uterus is <strong>retroverted</strong>, the cervix is directed downward and forwards. The<strong> lowest part </strong>of the<strong> cervix</strong> becomes either <strong>the external os </strong>or its <strong>posterior lip</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1183","text":"A G3P2L2 woman presents in‍ her third trimester with​ vaginal leaking. On PV e‌xamination, the anterior ​lip of the cervix can be ‌palpated. What is the pos​ition of her uterus and t‌he ligament that maintain‍s this position?","raw_text":"A G3P2L2 woman presents in her third trimester with vaginal leaking. On PV examination, the anterior lip of the cervix can be palpated. What is the position of her uterus and the ligament that maintains this position?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anteverted uterus - Round ‍ligament","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anteflexed uterus - Round ‍ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anteverted uterus - Uteros‍acral ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anteflexed uterus - Broad ‍ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anteverted uterus - Round ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3496d516cab2478d8dd2078efe928615x600x656.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anteverted uterus - Round ligament</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the uterus is <strong>anteverted</strong> as the anterior lip of the cervix was palpable. The <strong>round ligament</strong> helps maintain the <strong>anteversion</strong> of the uterus <strong>during pregnancy. </strong></p>\n<p>However, the <strong>anteversion</strong> in the <strong>non-pregnant uterus</strong> is maintained by <strong>both</strong> the <strong>uterosacral</strong> ligament and <strong>round</strong> ligament.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 535px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.172px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Angle of anteversion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.828px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Angle of anteflexion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px;\">Angle between the <strong>cervix and vagina</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.828125px;\">Angle between <strong>cervix and uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px;\"><strong>90<sup>o</sup></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.828125px;\"><strong>120<sup>o</sup></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(Mnemonic - V for <strong>v</strong>ersion, V for <strong>v</strong>agina)</p>\n<p>The uterus<strong> normally</strong> lies in a position of <strong>anteversion</strong> and <strong>anteflexion</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>anteverted </strong>position, the external os is directed downward and backwards. On vaginal examination, the fingers come in contact with the<strong> anterior lip of the cervix </strong>as it is the <strong>lowest part.</strong></p>\n<p>When the uterus is <strong>retroverted</strong>, the cervix is directed downward and forwards. The<strong> lowest part </strong>of the<strong> cervix</strong> becomes either <strong>the external os </strong>or its <strong>posterior lip</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anteflexion is seen at the level of the internal os.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4970","text":"A multiparous woman presen‍ted with a urethrocele, d​ue to weakness of the uro‌genital diaphragm. What i​s this structure also kno‌wn as?","raw_text":"A multiparous woman presented with a urethrocele, due to weakness of the urogenital diaphragm. What is this structure also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rectangula‍r ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triangula‍r ligament","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyramidal‍ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pear shape‍d ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Triangular ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9590e7aa0ad34effb190f0d36bb29ec4x1280x1398.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Triangular ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>urogenital diaphragm</strong> is also known as the<strong> triangular ligament. </strong></p>\n<p>The urogenital diaphragm extends from the <strong>pubic arch anteriorly</strong> to the central point of the <strong>perineum posteriorly</strong> and consists of two layers of fascia through which pass the vagina and the urethra. The central point of the female perineum lies between the vagina and the rectum.</p>\n<p><strong>Urogenital triangle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present between 2 ischiopubic rami</li>\n<li>Stretching transversely across the rami there are 3 membranes.</li>\n<li>From above downwards the membranes are as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>Superior fascia of urogenital diaphragm</li>\n<li>Inferior fascia of urogenital diaphragm</li>\n<li>Membranous layer of superficial perineal fascia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4935","text":"What is the cavity of the ‍uterus lined by?","raw_text":"What is the cavity of the uterus lined by?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single layer of ciliated c‍olumnar epithelium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Double layer of ciliated c‍olumnar epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Single layer of ciliated c‍uboidal epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double layer of ciliated c‍uboidal epithelium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single layer of ciliated columnar epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single layer of ciliated columnar epithelium</strong></p><p>The<strong> uterine cavity</strong> is lined by a <strong>single layer</strong> of<strong> ciliated columnar epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>The wall of the <strong>uterus</strong> consists of <strong>3 layers</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Perimetrium </strong>is the serous coat adherent to the underlying muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Myometrium</strong> - it<strong> </strong>consists of thick bundles of muscles that form 3 distinct layers during pregnancy:\n<ul>\n<li>Outer longitudinal</li>\n<li>Inner circular</li>\n<li>Middle interlacing called living ligature</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Endometrium - </strong>It is the mucous lining of the cavity. It consists of lamina propria and surface epithelium which is a single layer of ciliated columnar epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0624","text":"A woman with regular menst‍rual cycles is undergoing​ an ultrasound on the 14t‌h day of her cycle. What ​will the endometrial thic‌kness be?","raw_text":"A woman with regular menstrual cycles is undergoing an ultrasound on the 14th day of her cycle. What will the endometrial thickness be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5‍ mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 ‍mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11‍ mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"14‍ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 11 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 11 mm</strong></p><p>During <strong>ovulation</strong>, the thickness of the endometrium is around<strong> 11 mm</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 332px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Phase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Endometrial thickness</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Immediately after menstruation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">1-2 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Early proliferative phase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">5-7 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Secretory phase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">7-16 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Preovulatory phase </td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">up to <strong>11 mm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Postmenopausal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">&lt;4 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Postmenopausal - abnormal, requires biopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><strong>&gt;4 mm</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4938","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g uterine anatomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding uterine anatomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Round ligaments receive th‍eir blood supply from Sam​pson's artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse cervical ligame‍nts are also called cardi​nal ligaments","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine innervation is by ‍the pudendal nerve plexus​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphatic drainage is prim‍arily to the obturator, i​nternal and external ilia‌c nodes.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Uterine innervation is by the pudendal nerve plexus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uterine innervation is by the pudendal nerve plexus</strong></p><p>The uterus is innervated by the fibers of the <strong>uterovaginal plexus, </strong>also known as the <strong>Frankenhauser ganglion.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Round ligaments receive their blood supply from <strong>Sampson's artery</strong> which represents the <strong>anastomosis</strong> of the <strong>uterine artery</strong> and <strong>ovarian artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The cardinal ligaments are also called transverse cervical ligaments or <strong>Mackenrodt ligaments</strong>. They consist primarily of <strong>perivascular connective tissue</strong>. They attach to the posterolateral pelvic walls near the origin of the internal iliac artery and surround the vessels supplying the uterus and vagina.</p>\n<p>Option D: Lymphatic drainage of the uterus is primarily to the <strong>obturator</strong> and<strong> internal and external iliac nodes</strong>. One theory of endometriosis, states lymphatic spread as cause for seeding in the pelvis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6879","text":"A gynecologist is performi‍ng a hysterectomy. She ac​cidentally nicks the uret‌er in the ureteric canal ​as it pierces the Mackenr‌odt's ligaments. Which ve​ssels are likely to be da‌maged here?","raw_text":"A gynecologist is performing a hysterectomy. She accidentally nicks the ureter in the ureteric canal as it pierces the Mackenrodt's ligaments. Which vessels are likely to be damaged here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine artery above and t‍he uterine plexus of vein​s below","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uterine plexus of veins ab‍ove and the uterine arter​y below","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ovarian artery above and t‍he uterine artery below","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine artery above and t‍he ovarian artery below","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uterine artery above and the uterine plexus of veins below","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f2b4d9e8fc0402e8dbccc96ab485c7dx1280x1298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uterine artery above and the uterine plexus of veins below</strong></p><p>The<strong> uterine artery above</strong> and<strong> uterine venous plexus below</strong> are likely to be damaged here, as the ureter is present between these vessels. </p>\n<p>The<strong> ureter</strong> <strong>pierces</strong> the<strong> Mackenrodt's ligament</strong>, forming the <strong>ureteric canal. </strong>In this canal, the ureter is crossed by the uterine artery above and the uterine plexus of veins below, thus being present in <strong>between</strong> the<strong> uterine vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ureteric canal <strong>provides</strong> the necessary <strong>room for normal peristalsis</strong>, without pressure from the surrounding structures. After leaving the ureteric canal, the ureter passes forwards and medially to reach the bladder. It is 1-2 cm away from the cervix.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4939","text":"You take a culture swab fr‍om the portio vaginalis o​f a woman having foul sme‌lling vaginal discharge. ​What is this region also ‌known as?","raw_text":"You take a culture swab from the portio vaginalis of a woman having foul smelling vaginal discharge. What is this region also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supra vaginal part of cerv‍ix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vaginal par‍t of cervix","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fornix of ‍the vagina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Introitus o‍f the vagina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vaginal part of cervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vaginal part of cervix</strong></p><p><strong>Portio vaginalis</strong> or <strong>exocervix</strong> is the other name for the <strong>vaginal part</strong> of the <strong>cervix</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cervix</strong> is the lowermost part of the uterus extending from the histological<strong> internal os</strong> to the<strong> external os. </strong>It is cylindrical in shape measuring 2.5 cm in length and diameter.</p>\n<p>Cervix is made up of 10% muscle and the rest is collagen, elastin, and proteoglycan. The lining of the cervix has the following parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exocervix - also known as portio vaginalis. It is lined by<strong> non keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</strong>.</li>\n<li>Endocervix - it is lined by high <strong>columnar, ciliated epithelium</strong>. </li>\n<li>Endocervical glands - secrete<strong> alkaline mucus</strong>. When enlarged, they form swellings called <strong>Nabothian follicles/cysts.</strong> </li>\n<li>Transformation zone - the epithelium gradually changes from <strong>columnar to squamous</strong> in this region. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4942","text":"What is the cervix: corpus‍ ratio before puberty?","raw_text":"What is the cervix: corpus ratio before puberty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‍:2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‍:1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‍:3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‍:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2:1</strong></p><p>The cervix:corpus ratio <strong>before puberty</strong> is <strong>2:1.</strong></p>\n<p>The relationship between the length of the cervix and that of the body of the uterus varies with age.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 155.972px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 214.028px;\"><strong>Cervix/Corpus ratio</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.972px;\">Before puberty</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.028px;\">\n<p>2:1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.972px;\">At puberty</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.028px;\">\n<p>1:2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.972px;\">Reproductive age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.028px;\">1:3 or 1:4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.972px;\">After menopause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.028px;\">2:1 - however, the uterus and cervix are both atrophied</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0703","text":"The remnants of Wolffian d‍ucts in females are locat​ed in________.","raw_text":"The remnants of Wolffian ducts in females are located in________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pouch of‍ Douglas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leaves of br‍oad ligament","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uterovesi‍cal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iliac‍ fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leaves of broad ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b6398184ce704faa8efa742268738715x1280x2423.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leaves of broad ligament</strong></p><p>The<strong> Gartner's duct</strong> is a <strong>remnant </strong>of the <strong>Wolffian ducts </strong>in women. These are located in the <strong>broad ligament </strong>and the vagina.</p>\n<p>The Gartner's duct is an embryological remnant of the <strong>mesonephric duct/Wolffian duct</strong> formed during the development of the urinary and reproductive organs. It runs parallel to, but below, the fallopian tube in the mesosalpinx. The duct passes downwards by the side of the uterus to the level of the internal os where it passes into the tissues of the cervix. It then runs forwards to reach the anterolateral aspect of the vaginal wall and may reach as far down as the hymen.</p>\n<p>The duct can sometimes form a cyst in the broad ligament or in the vagina. It is called <strong>Gartner’s cyst. </strong>It is a benign condition and can be <strong>treated</strong> with <strong>marsupialization</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4943","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about the contents ​of the broad ligament?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the contents of the broad ligament?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovarian vessels in its inf‍undibulopelvic part","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It contains the uterine ar‍teries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ureter is present the lowe‍r portion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian ligament in its an‍terior fold","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ovarian ligament in its anterior fold","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ovarian ligament in its anterior fold</strong></p><p>The<strong> ovarian ligaments</strong> are contained in the <strong>posterior fold</strong> of the <strong>broad ligament.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>broad ligament</strong> contains the following <strong>parts</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mesosalpinx</strong> - it lies between the fallopian tube and ovarian ligament. It contains the pelvic vessels - anastomoses between uterine and ovarian arteries. The ovary is attached via ovarian ligament through the posterior folds to the mesovarium. </li>\n<li><strong>Mesometrium</strong> - it contains the ovarian vessels within the infundibulopelvic ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1728\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>ureter</strong> is present in the<strong> inferomedial part</strong> of the broad ligament. It is related to the uterine artery here and may be damaged during hysterectomy.</p>\n<p>Note: Broad ligament is not a support of the uterus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4951","text":"Where are Pe‍g cells seen?","raw_text":"Where are Peg cells seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vag‍ina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vu‍lva","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ov‍ary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fallopi‍an tubes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fallopian tubes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d6d8da9e0644797864b986cfd1a5a94x291x261.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fallopian tubes</strong></p><p><strong>Peg cells</strong> are seen in the <strong>fallopian tubes</strong>.</p>\n<p>A peg cell is a<strong> non-ciliated epithelial</strong> <strong>cell</strong> within the fallopian tube. These cells are also known as intercalated or intercalary cells whose function is not yet known.</p>\n<p>The image below shows pale ciliated cells (arrows) and narrow, dark, non-ciliated peg cells (arrowheads). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4948","text":"What is the longest part o‍f the fallopian tube?","raw_text":"What is the longest part of the fallopian tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inters‍titium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ist‍hmus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amp‍ulla","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infund‍ibulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ampulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ampulla</strong></p><p>The<strong> longest</strong> and<strong> widest </strong>part of the fallopian tube is the<strong> ampulla </strong>(5 cm).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4955","text":"Where is the tunica albugi‍nea of the adult ovary de​rived from?","raw_text":"Where is the tunica albuginea of the adult ovary derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The surface‍ epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The sub epithelial connect‍ive tissue layer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The parench‍ymatous zone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zona va‍sculosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The sub epithelial connective tissue layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/362553fc54c44d75b0e36f1d2aeaa9a8x1280x1396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The sub epithelial connective tissue layer</strong></p><p><strong>Tunica albuginea</strong> of the adult <strong>ovary</strong> is derived from the <strong>sub-epithelial connective tissue</strong> layer.</p>\n<p>Structure of the ovary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Surface epithelium - a single layer of <strong>cuboidal cells</strong> is present, which later gives rise to the <strong>surface epithelium</strong> of the adult ovary. It is morphologically continuous with the mesothelium of the peritoneum.</li>\n<li>The subepithelial connective tissue layer - gives <strong>rise to</strong> the <strong>tunica albuginea</strong> of the adult ovary and the <strong>basement membrane</strong> beneath the surface epithelium.</li>\n<li>The parenchymatous zone - becomes the <strong>cortex</strong> and contains the <strong>egg nests</strong> and <strong>ovarian follicles. </strong></li>\n<li>Zona vasculosa - becomes the <strong>medulla</strong> and contains the <strong>blood vessels. </strong>Hilar cells present here are homologous to interstitial cells of testes. They can rarely produce hilar cell tumors of the ovary.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4962","text":"During the histopathologic‍al examination of an ovar​ian mass, Call-Exner bodi‌es are seen. In which of ​the following layers are ‌these structures seen?","raw_text":"During the histopathological examination of an ovarian mass,  Call-Exner bodies are seen. In which of the following layers are these structures seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulosa ‍cell layer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Theca ‍interna","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discus p‍roligerus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cumulus ‍oophorus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Granulosa cell layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29f63a2e1e8f45419915b2921f8e7858x800x423.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eaf2436020134078bea42d9d59a0e751x1280x1351.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Granulosa cell layer</strong></p><p><strong>Call-Exner bodies</strong> are formed by <strong>granulosa cells</strong> present within the granulosa cell layer of the ovary.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>They are small, distinctive, glandlike structures filled with acidophilic material, resembling immature follicles. They are characteristically seen in granulosa cell tumors of the ovary.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Call-Exner bodies (circled in black).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Graafian follicle:</strong></p>\n<p>The mature Graafian follicle is spheroidal or ovoid in shape. It contains pent-up secretion known as the liquor folliculi. The lining consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Theca externa</li>\n<li>Theca interna - contain the theca cells </li>\n<li>Granulosa cell layer</li>\n<li>Cumulus oophorus - projection of granulosa cells into the cavity of the Graafian follicle. It is also called the discus proligerus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4963","text":"Where is the sympathetic n‍erve supply of ovary deri​ved from?","raw_text":"Where is the sympathetic nerve supply of ovary derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T10‍-T11","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T9-‍T10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S2‍-S4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L4‍-L5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T10-T11","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T10-T11</strong></p><p>The efferent<strong> preganglionic sympathetic fibres</strong> to the ovary are derived from the <strong>T10-T11</strong> spinal levels.</p>\n<p>These fibres travel to the <strong>prevertebral ganglia,</strong> where they synapse. The <strong>postganglionic sympathetic fibres </strong>arising from these ganglia contribute to the <strong>ovarian plexus</strong>, which innervates the ovary.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1799\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 74\">The ovarian plexus is a network of nerves comprising:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 74\">Postganglionic sympathetic fibres</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 74\">Preganglionic parasympathetic fibres from the sacral outflow (S2-S4)</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 74\">Visceral afferent fibres (responsible for transmitting sensory signals)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ovarian plexus has two parts based on its connections:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper part: Derived from branches of the renal plexus and aortic plexus</li>\n<li>Lower part: Derived from the superior and inferior hypogastric plexuses.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0607","text":"Bleeding during normal men‍struation occurs due to r​upture of ____","raw_text":"Bleeding during normal menstruation occurs due to rupture of ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basilar‍ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spiral‍ artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arcuate‍ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial‍ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spiral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95fbe22e23994267b61ce69e26120362x1280x1709.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spiral artery</strong></p><p>Rupture of the<strong> spiral arteries</strong> causes bleeding during normal menstruation. This is a branch of the uterine artery. </p>\n<p>The <strong>blood supply</strong> of the uterus is as follows:</p>\n<p>Uterine artery→Arcuate artery→Radial artery→Basal artery→Spiral artery</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4965","text":"A uterine artery embolizat‍ion is being planned for ​a patient with uterine fi‌broids. Blood flow in whi​ch of the following arter‌ies will not be affected ​by this procedure?","raw_text":"A uterine artery embolization is being planned for a patient with uterine fibroids. Blood flow in which of the following arteries will not be affected by this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spiral‍ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sampson'‍s artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal‍ artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arcuate‍ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vaginal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e88287d5382a4a84b93c05221b6b7b11x1280x1450.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f05ab2f5ae5d49be96c63c44b9e2b454x1280x1709.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vaginal artery</strong></p><p>In the given case scenario, blood flow in the <strong>vaginal artery</strong> will not be affected, as it is not a branch of the uterine artery. The vaginal artery is a branch of the <strong>anterior division</strong> of the<strong> internal iliac artery.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is homologous to the inferior vesical artery in males. It <strong>supplies</strong> the <strong>vagina</strong> and the base of the <strong>bladder</strong> in women. It should not be confused with vaginal branches of the uterine artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Branches of the uterine artery</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ureteric branch</li>\n<li>Vaginal branches (descending) - these unite to form the anterior and posterior azygos arteries of the vagina</li>\n<li>Circular cervical branch</li>\n<li><strong>Sampson's</strong> artery - it runs in under the round ligament. It represents the anastomosis of the uterine artery and ovarian artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Arcuate</strong>--&gt; Radial--&gt; Basal--&gt; <strong>Spiral</strong> and straight arterioles of the functional layer of the endometrium</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4972","text":"A patient with stress inco‍ntinence required surgica​l management. While perfo‌rming a Burch colposuspen​sion, the surgeon approac‌hed the space of Retzius.​ What is this space also ‌known as?","raw_text":"A patient with stress incontinence required surgical management. While performing a Burch colposuspension, the surgeon approached the space of Retzius. What is this space also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevesical or presacral sp‍ace","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presacral or prerectal spa‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presacral or retropubic sp‍ace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevesical or retropubic s‍pace","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prevesical or retropubic space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db321c7ca33e461f9ef875aac2235a50x1280x1543.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prevesical or retropubic space</strong></p><p>The <strong>space of Retzius</strong> is the other name for <strong>pre</strong><strong>vesical</strong> or <strong>r</strong><strong>etropubic space</strong>.</p>\n<p>Burch colposuspension is a procedure done to relieve stress urinary incontinence. If an extraperitoneal approach is selected, the retropubic space is <strong>entered by perforating the transversalis fascia</strong> layer of the anterior abdominal wall.</p>\n<p>Once the retropubic space is dissected, the bladder neck and the vaginal fascia underneath are identified. The bladder is mobilized to the opposite side. At each side of the bladder neck, two to four, preferably non‐absorbable sutures are placed in the space of Retzius.</p>\n<p>Note:The <strong>presacral space</strong> is a <strong>retroperitoneal</strong> space that lies between the peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall and the sacrum. It <strong>contains</strong> the <strong>superior and inferior hypogastric plexuses.</strong> The sacral sympathetic trunk lies against the sacrum’s ventral surface.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_x4pXZPq","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"1_anatomy_and_development_of_eye","title":"1.Anatomy And Development Of Eye","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB4168","text":"The newborn eye is usually‌ ______.","raw_text":"The newborn eye is usually ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myo‌pic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperm‌etropic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Emmet‌ropic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Astig‌matic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypermetropic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypermetropic</strong></p><p> A newborn is usually <strong>hypermetropic</strong> by<strong> +2D to +3D. </strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>anteroposterior</strong> (AP) <strong>diameter</strong> of the eyeball at birth is about <strong>16.5 mm</strong>, which is about 70% of the adult size. This reduced axial length causes<strong> </strong>light to <strong>converge behind the retina</strong>, leading to <strong>hypermetropia. </strong></p>\n<p>Additionally, the corneal diameter at birth is 10 mm. The adult size (11.7 mm) is attained by 2 years of age. The orbit is more divergent (50°) as compared to adult (45°).</p>\n<p>Note: The lacrimal glands are underdeveloped in a newborn and, hence, do not secrete tears.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4170","text":"At what age would a child ‌attain full visual acuity​?","raw_text":"At what age would a child attain full visual acuity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 y‌ear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 y‌ears","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6 years</strong></p><p><strong>Full visual acuity</strong> is attained by the age of <strong>6 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>Important<strong> milestones</strong> in <strong>visual development</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At<strong> birth</strong>, there is no central fixation, and the eyes move randomly.</li>\n<li>By<strong> the first month </strong>of life, the <strong>fixation reflex starts</strong> developing and becomes established by 6 months.</li>\n<li>By<strong> 3 months</strong>, binocular vision is established.</li>\n<li>By<strong> 6 months</strong>,<strong> </strong><strong>macular stereopsis</strong> and <strong>accommodation reflex</strong> are fully developed.</li>\n<li>By<strong> 6 years </strong>of age, full visual acuity <strong>(6/6)</strong> is attained.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4171","text":"A 45-year-old lady present‌s with the abnormality as​ shown below. She is diag​nosed with uveal melanoma‍. What is the embryologic​al origin of the cells in​volved?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old lady presents with the abnormality as shown below. She is diagnosed with uveal melanoma. What is the embryological origin of the cells involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neural ‌ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meso‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surface ‌ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neural‌ crest","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neural crest","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f55d22558bd4980ae32f98b69efb23a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neural crest</strong></p><p>Uveal melanomas arise from<strong> uveal melanocytes.  </strong>Uveal and conjunctival melanocytes are derived from the <strong>neural crest cells.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4169","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the development of the ​eye?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the development of the eye?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The macula is fully develo‌ped at birth.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The lens grows throughout ‌life.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The cornea attains normal ‌diameter by 2 years.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Full visual acuity is atta‌ined by 6 years of age.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The macula is fully developed at birth.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The macula is fully developed at birth.</strong></p><p>The <strong>macula</strong> is <strong>fully developed</strong> by <strong>4–6 months of age</strong>. Apart from the macula, the rest of the retina<strong> </strong>is fully developed at birth.</p>\n<p><strong>Postnatal Development of Eye</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>3 Months - Binocular single vision develops.</li>\n<li>4-6 Months - Macula fully develops. Fusional reflexes, stereopsis, and accommodation become well developed</li>\n<li>6 Months - Fixation, which had started developing in the first month is now complete</li>\n<li>2 Years - Cornea develops to the normal adult diameter</li>\n<li>6 Years - Visual acuity fully develops and reaches 6/6</li>\n<li>The lens, however, continues to grow throughout the life</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0795","text":"By how many weeks of embry‌onic development does the​ optic groove appear?","raw_text":"By how many weeks of embryonic development does the optic groove appear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4002e1d2eac74d848b769c1a81c708d9x1280x1191.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c95e4f6dc88f4e1f9b4ead5068fdbd1ax1280x3137.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 weeks</strong></p><p>The optic groove appears by <strong>3 weeks</strong>. By approximately day 22, 2 small grooves appear on either side of the developing forebrain in the neural folds, these are called <strong>optic grooves</strong>. As the neural tube closes, these grooves develop outpouchings called <strong>optic vesicles. </strong>These optic vesicles extend from the forebrain to the surface ectoderm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4173","text":"Embryonic development of c‌iliary muscles is from __​_____.","raw_text":"Embryonic development of ciliary muscles is from _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface ‌ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neural ‌ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neural‌ crest","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meso‌derm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neural crest","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neural crest</strong></p><p>Embryonic development of ciliary muscles is from the <strong>neural crest</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0792","text":"After conception, the cana‌l of Schlemm appears by _​_________________ .","raw_text":"After conception, the canal of Schlemm appears by __________________ .","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd ‌month","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th ‌month","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5th ‌month","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6th ‌month","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4th month","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4th month</strong></p><p>The <strong>canal of Schlemm</strong> appears by the <strong>4<sup>th</sup> month</strong> after conception. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4175","text":"What is the anteroposterio‌r diameter of the adult e​yeball?","raw_text":"What is the anteroposterior diameter of the adult eyeball?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.6‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.4‌ cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2.1‌ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2.4 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2.4 cm</strong></p><p>The<strong> anteroposterior </strong>(AP)<strong> diameter</strong> of the adult eye is<strong> </strong>24 mm/<strong>2.4 cm</strong>. </p>\n<p>At<strong> birth</strong>, the average axial length of the eyeball is <strong>16.5 mm</strong>. By<strong> 3 years,</strong> it grows rapidly to approximately <strong>23 mm. </strong>From 3 to 14 years of age, the axial length gradually increases by a further 1 mm, bringing it to the adult size of 24 mm. This overall adjustment in the eye size, including the refractive power, is called <strong>emmetropization.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6692","text":"A 34-year-old lady comes t‌o the clinic complaining ​of problems in her vision​. On perimetry, a visual ‍field defect as shown bel​ow is obtained. Which of ​the following vessels doe​s not supply the anatomic​al location of the lesion​?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old lady comes to the clinic complaining of problems in her vision. On perimetry, a visual field defect as shown below is obtained. Which of the following vessels does not supply the anatomical location of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior cer‌ebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior communicating art‌ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal ca‌rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle cere‌bral artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Middle cerebral artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b1d21dd8894b4eafa3910ec5680f678f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b92e213c5744b3cbf857e50d8bb4c00x600x656.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Middle cerebral artery</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>bitemporal hemianopia,</strong> a defect caused by <strong>optic chiasmal lesions.</strong> The <strong>middle</strong><strong> cerebral artery </strong>does not supply the optic chiasma.</p>\n<p>Blood supply of optic chiasma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior cerebral</strong> artery - <strong>dorsal</strong> chiasma</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior communicating</strong> artery - <strong>ventral</strong> chiasma</li>\n<li><strong>Internal carotid</strong> artery - <strong>ventral</strong> chiasma</li>\n<li><strong>Superior hypophyseal</strong> artery - ventral (branch of internal carotid artery).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6689","text":"A college student comes to‌ the OPD for refractive e​rror testing. Her eyes ar​e dilated using tropicami‍de. Which of the followin​g statements is false abo​ut the structure this dru​g acts on?","raw_text":"A college student comes to the OPD for refractive error testing. Her eyes are dilated using tropicamide. Which of the following statements is false about the structure this drug acts on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is triangular in cross ‌section.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There are around 30-40 pro‌cesses in pars plana and ​pars plicata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Its epithelium is derived ‌from the anterior edge of​ the optic cup.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The pars plana is approxim‌ately 4.5 mm from the lim​bus.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. There are around 30-40 processes in pars plana and pars plicata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c12d8788ddf421492dd1ad534b55c56x1280x1414.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. There are around 30-40 processes in pars plana and pars plicata</strong></p><p><strong>Tropicamide</strong> is a <strong>cycloplegic</strong> drug that acts on the <strong>ciliary body</strong> to cause mydriasis for refractory error testing. The ciliary body is a <strong>triangular</strong> structure that contains <strong>60-70 ciliary processes</strong> in each eye, present on the pars plicata.</p>\n<p>The pars plana is 4.5mm from the limbus while the distance between the equator and the limbus is 14mm. </p>\n<p>The <strong>ciliary epithelium</strong> is derived from the <strong>optic cup (neuroectoderm)</strong> by the continued growth of the outer lip's anterior edge.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4174","text":"A 12-year-old girl you exa‌mined in an outreach camp​ is found to have this ab​normality. What is the mo‍st common site where the ​condition is seen?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl you examined in an outreach camp is found to have this abnormality. What is the most common site where the condition is seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Super‌onasal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infer‌onasal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superot‌emporal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferot‌emporal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferonasal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/02f6184e9dff4475b20c720e290cb128.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5328eab2e89a4b7d9cb4afe5a3af42dex500x375.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferonasal</strong></p><p>The condition depicted in the image is a <strong>coloboma</strong> of the<strong> iris, </strong>and the most common site of coloboma is the <strong>inferonasal quadrant.</strong></p>\n<p>A coloboma is a congenital malformation in one of the structures in the eye, such as the iris, retina, choroid, or optic disc, due to the<strong> failure of embryonic fissure closure. </strong>Coloboma of the <strong>uveal tract</strong> may be<strong> typical </strong>(seen in the inferonasal quadrant) or <strong>atypical </strong>(occasionally found in other positions). Typical coloboma is of two types: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complete: </strong>Extends from pupil to optic nerve, causing a corresponding lens indentation.</li>\n<li><strong>Incomplete: </strong>May involve iris alone, or iris and ciliary body (more common), or iris, ciliary body and a part of choroid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other embryonic remnants in the eye: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mittendorf's dot - Remnant of the anterior end of the hyaloid artery</li>\n<li>Bergmeister papilla - Remnant of the posterior end of the hyaloid artery</li>\n<li>Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous (PHPV) -<strong> </strong>Failure of fetal vasculature to regress   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a fundoscopy view of <strong>persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6694","text":"A child was brought with c‌omplaints of decreased vi​sion. Fundus examination ​shows a developmental ano‍maly as shown below. What​ is the cause of this ano​maly?","raw_text":"A child was brought with complaints of decreased vision. Fundus examination shows a developmental anomaly as shown below. What is the cause of this anomaly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incomplete closure of the ‌embryonic fissure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Faulty migration of neural‌ crest cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exuberant proliferation of‌ glial tissue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormal retinal different‌iation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Incomplete closure of the embryonic fissure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c4f666ef6ba4af2ad3955d89a25f129.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Incomplete closure of the embryonic fissure</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>coloboma of the optic disc</strong>, accompanied by a <strong>large chorioretinal coloboma </strong>located below the optic disc, which is caused by <strong>incomplete closure </strong>of the<strong> embryonic fissure.</strong></p>\n<p>This developmental anomaly may involve the optic nerve, choroid, retina, and iris. Coloboma of the optic disc can present in two forms: a minor effect often associated with refractive errors like hypermetropia and astigmatism and a <strong>fully developed coloboma</strong> that appears as a large inferonasal whitish excavation resembling the optic disc. </p>\n<p>Defective vision and superior visual field defects are typically associated with fully developed coloboma. Additionally, this condition may be associated with chorioretinal coloboma, a congenital anomaly where the developing <strong>optic cup fails to fuse</strong> completely, leading to abnormal choroidal and retinal development. </p>\n<p>Note: Faulty migration of neural crest cells results in <strong>anterior segment dysgenesis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4172","text":"What is the embryological ‌origin of the structure m​arked in the image?","raw_text":"What is the embryological origin of the structure marked in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neural ‌ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meso‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surface ‌ectoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neural‌ crest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surface ectoderm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c2b6ed80dc124bafa135709a29797afa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd8ac9e125c04664b1ad2ecd3940b653x1280x1023.6389280677009.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surface ectoderm</strong></p><p>The<strong> lacrimal gland</strong> arises from the <strong>surface ectoderm. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6691","text":"The optic foramen is situa‌ted between the:","raw_text":"The optic foramen is situated between the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser and greater wings o‌f the sphenoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser wing and body of th‌e sphenoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Greater wing and body of t‌he sphenoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Two roots of the lesser wi‌ng of the sphenoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Two roots of the lesser wing of the sphenoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/558492c5d95a4196a541e16bb9efe7c6x1200x1255.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Two roots of the lesser wing of the sphenoid</strong></p><p>The <strong>optic foramen</strong> is situated between the <strong>two roots of the lesser wing of the sphenoid. </strong></p>\n<p>The optic foramen leads into the <strong>optic canal</strong>, which is formed medially by the<strong> body</strong> and laterally by the lesser wing of the sphenoid bone, connecting the orbit's apex to the middle cranial fossa.</p>\n<p>The optic canal transmits the <strong>optic nerve</strong> with its meningeal coverings; the <strong>ophthalmic artery</strong>, which is below the nerve, is embedded in its dural sheath, and a few twigs from the <strong>sympathetic plexus </strong>accompanying the artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4178","text":"During a game, a 26-year-o‌ld baseball player is hit​ in the head by a basebal​l, which fractures the op‍tic canal. Which of the f​ollowing pairs of structu​res is most likely to be ​damaged?","raw_text":"During a game, a 26-year-old baseball player is hit in the head by a baseball, which fractures the optic canal. Which of the following pairs of structures is most likely to be damaged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic nerve and ophthalmic‌ vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ophthalmic vein and ophtha‌lmic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ophthalmic artery and opti‌c nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ophthalmic nerve and optic‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ophthalmic artery and optic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ophthalmic artery and optic nerve</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, a fracture of the optic canal is most likely to damage the <strong>ophthalmic artery</strong> and <strong>optic nerve, </strong>as these are the primary structures passing through it.</p>\n<p>The <strong>optic foramen</strong> leads into the optic canal, which is a short cylindrical structure bounded medially by the <strong>body </strong>and laterally by the <strong>lesser wing</strong> of the <strong>sphenoid bone</strong>, connecting the orbit's apex to the middle cranial fossa, serving as a pathway for the aforementioned structures along with its sympathetic plexus. </p>\n<p>Optic canal fractures, often associated with basilar skull fractures, can lead to optic neuropathy. The intracanalicular and intracranial portion of the optic nerve is most commonly affected in head trauma. </p>\n<p>Note: The<strong> </strong>greater and lesser wings<strong> </strong>of the sphenoid bound the <strong>superior orbital fissure. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4176","text":"The anterior segment of th‌e eye includes all except​ ______.","raw_text":"The anterior segment of the eye includes all except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‌ chamber","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterio‌r chamber","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitr‌eous","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Le‌ns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitreous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b30b4c87adae4c6f83724275296ba470x1280x1383.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitreous</strong></p><p>The anterior segment of the eye does<strong> not </strong>include<strong> vitreous humor </strong>as it<strong> </strong>is a constituent of the <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>segment</strong> of the eye.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 132px;\" width=\"324\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Anterior Segment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Posterior Segment </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px; text-align: center;\">Cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Vitreous humor </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px; text-align: center;\">Iris </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Retina </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px; text-align: center;\">Lens </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Choroid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px; text-align: center;\">Anterior and Posterior chambers with aqueous humor </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Optic Disc </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4177","text":"The posterior chamber in t‌he eyeball is filled with​ _______.","raw_text":"The posterior chamber in the eyeball is filled with _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueou‌s humor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitreou‌s humor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ly‌mph","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bl‌ood","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aqueous humor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12c973c79c004794883e053691b0ea83x600x649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aqueous humor</strong></p><p>The posterior chamber of the eyeball is filled with <strong>aqueous</strong> <strong>humor.</strong></p>\n<p>The eyeball is divided into anterior and posterior segments. The <strong>anterior segment</strong> includes the lens and the structures anterior to it, such as the cornea, iris and two aqueous humor-filled spaces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior chamber</strong>:  Located between cornea and iris, contains aqueous humor and communicates with the posterior chamber through the pupil. </li>\n<li><strong>Posterior chamber</strong>: Triangular-shaped space, also contains aqueous humor, bounded anteriorly by the posterior surface of the iris, laterally by the ciliary body, and posteriorly by the lens. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>segment</strong> includes all structures located posterior to the lens, comprising of the<strong> vitreous humor,</strong> retina, choroid and optic disc. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6690","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is attached to Wh​itnall’s tubercle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is attached to Whitnall’s tubercle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Check ligament of the medi‌al rectus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ob‌lique tendon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Check ligament of the late‌ral rectus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Whitnall'‌s ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Check ligament of the lateral rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa1e3cd0abb841f7be47ddd3b73229c8x600x609.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Check ligament of the lateral rectus</strong></p><p><strong>Check ligament</strong> of the <strong>lateral rectus</strong> is attached to <strong>Whitnall’s tubercle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lateral orbital tubercle or Whitnall's tubercle is a small elevation on the orbital surface of the <strong>zygomatic bone,</strong> just within the lateral orbital margin. It is about 11 mm below the fronto-zygomatic suture.</p>\n<p><strong>Four structures</strong> are attached to it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check ligament of the lateral rectus</li>\n<li>Lockwood’s ligament (Suspensory ligament of the eyeball)</li>\n<li>Lateral palpebral ligament</li>\n<li>Lateral horn of the levator aponeurosis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>Whitnall's ligament </strong>or the superior transverse ligament of the eye, is attached to the superior tarsal plate and the orbital bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6693","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the tarsal plates?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the tarsal plates?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tarsal plates are cartilag‌inous structures.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They give structural suppo‌rt to the eyelid.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LPS aponeurosis is attache‌d to the superior border ​of the upper tarsal plate​.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Their medial and lateral e‌nds form the medial and l​ateral palpebral ligament​s, respectively.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tarsal plates are cartilaginous structures.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69e7c42c06274813a5a1fb690c104485x600x609.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tarsal plates are cartilaginous structures.</strong></p><p>The tarsal plates are <strong>not cartilaginous </strong>structures but firm, dense <strong>connective tissue</strong> (fibrous tissue) plates.</p>\n<p>The tarsal plates serve as the<strong> structural support</strong> of the eyelids. The height of the upper tarsal plate is about 10 mm, and that of the lower<strong> </strong>tarsal plate is 4 mm. Both plates have a thickness of about 1 mm. The ends form the medial and lateral palpebral ligaments (medial and lateral canthal tendons). The <strong>levator palpebrae superioris</strong> (LPS) <strong>aponeurosis</strong> is attached to the <strong>superior border</strong> of the <strong>upper</strong> tarsal plate.</p>\n<p>The <strong>meibomian glands</strong> are located within the tarsus. These are <strong>holocrine-secreting</strong> sebaceous glands that are not associated with lash follicles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4180","text":"A 55-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for herpes zos​ter ophthalmicus. On elic​iting corneal reflex, the‍re is no response. Which ​of the following nerves f​orms the afferent limb of​ this reflex?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man is being evaluated for herpes zoster ophthalmicus. On eliciting corneal reflex, there is no response. Which of the following nerves forms the afferent limb of this reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infratroch‌lear nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supratroch‌lear nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supraorbi‌tal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long cili‌ary nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Long ciliary nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Long ciliary nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> sensory</strong> supply of the <strong>cornea</strong> comes from the <strong>long ciliary nerve</strong> (branch of the nasociliary nerve). It forms the afferent limb of the corneal reflex. </p>\n<p>The sensory nerve supply of the eye is by the <strong>ophthalmic nerve.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lacrimal nerve- </strong>lacrimal gland, conjunctiva, lateral eyelid</li>\n<li><strong>Frontal nerve- </strong>conjunctiva, eyelid, part of the forehead</li>\n<li><strong>Nasociliary nerve </strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Long ciliary nerve- </strong>cornea, ciliary body, iris</li>\n<li><strong>Infra-trochlear nerve- </strong>conjunctiva, lacrimal sac, caruncle, medial part of the eyelids</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Corneal sensation is reduced in leprosy and diabetes mellitus (neuropathy). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4179","text":"Which intrinsic ocular mus‌cle has a parasympathetic​ innervation?","raw_text":"Which intrinsic ocular muscle has a parasympathetic innervation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio‌r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior‌ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sphincter‌ pupillae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dilator ‌pupillae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sphincter pupillae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sphincter pupillae</strong></p><p>The intrinsic ocular muscle supplied by <strong>parasympathetic</strong> innervation is the <strong>sphincter pupillae</strong>.</p>\n<p>1. Parasympathetic innervation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sphincter pupillae: Short ciliary nerve–Ciliary ganglion</li>\n<li>Ciliary muscle–Accessory ganglion<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Sympathetic innervation: <strong>Dilator pupillae</strong> and Muller’s muscle</p>\n<p>3. Motor Supply:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3<sup>rd</sup> </strong>nerve: <strong>All extraocular muscles</strong> (except the 2 given below)</li>\n<li><strong>6<sup>th</sup></strong> nerve: Lateral rectus (LR6)</li>\n<li><strong>4<sup>th</sup></strong> nerve: <strong>Superior oblique</strong> (SO4)</li>\n<li><strong>7<sup>th</sup></strong> nerve: Orbicularis oculi</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4184","text":"What is the thinnest part ‌of the retina?","raw_text":"What is the thinnest part of the retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fov‌eola","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fovea c‌entralis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic‌ disc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ora s‌errata","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ora serrata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ora serrata</strong></p><p>The retina is the<strong> thinnest (0.080 mm) </strong>at the <strong>ora serrata, </strong>and thickest, at the foveal rim (0.23, 0.320 mm).</p>\n<p>The <strong>ora serrata</strong> is the serrated junction between the <strong>retina</strong> and <strong>ciliary body</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9498","text":"In the anatomy dissection ‌hall, you are shown the d​issected orbit with the m​arkers as shown below. Yo‍u are asked to choose the​ muscles causing depressi​on of the eyeball. Which ​will you choose?","raw_text":"In the anatomy dissection hall, you are shown the dissected orbit with the markers as shown below. You are asked to choose the muscles causing depression of the eyeball. Which will you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A a‌nd E","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"B, C,‌ and D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B, D,‌ and E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B a‌nd E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A and E","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b8a6b8443a8485a83f3758b0724ab72.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eca896197c924b7383307629bd1751adx960x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A and E</strong></p><p>The muscles responsible for the <strong>depression</strong> of the <strong>eyeball</strong> are the <strong>inferior rectus</strong> (E) and <strong>superior oblique</strong> (A). </p>\n<p>Image markings: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A- Superior oblique</li>\n<li>B- Levator palpebrae superiors</li>\n<li>C- Superior rectus</li>\n<li>D- Inferior oblique</li>\n<li>E- Inferior rectus</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Nerve supply of extra-ocular muscles: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oculomotor</strong> nerve (III)- Superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, inferior oblique, and levator palpebrae superioris</li>\n<li><strong>Abducens</strong> nerve (VI)- Lateral rectus </li>\n<li><strong>Trochlear</strong> nerve (IV)- Superior oblique</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jW3wuiov","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"1_basics_of_fracture_and_its_management","title":"1.Basics Of Fracture And Its Management","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA3250","text":"Which of the following is ‌a pathognomonic sign of a​ long bone fracture?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a pathognomonic sign of a long bone fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain on ‌movement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Swel‌ling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defo‌rmity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormal‌ mobility","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abnormal mobility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abnormal mobility</strong></p><p><strong>Abnormal mobility </strong>in a long bone is a <strong>pathognomonic</strong> sign of <strong>fracture</strong>. </p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">A fracture is a <strong>break in the structural continuity</strong> of the bone. Generally, <strong>m</strong></span><strong>ovements</strong> occur in the limb <strong>only at the joints</strong>. If one is able to elicit <strong>mobility at locations other than the joints</strong> (say in the middle of the arm), it is a <strong>definite sign of a fracture</strong>. <strong>Crepitus</strong> may be felt while doing this. Other features of a fracture include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Pain</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Bruising</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Swelling</strong></span></li>\n<li><strong>Deformity </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These can be due to a soft-tissue injury or dislocation as well.</p>\n<p>Clinically, eliciting abnormal movement or crepitus inflicts more pain. Hence it is <strong>no longer recommended.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0618","text":"Match the following type o‌f fractures with their as​sociated mechanism of for‍ce. 1. Transverse fractur‌e a. Compression 2. Obliq​ue fracture b. Twisting 3​. Butterfly fragment c. T‌ension 4. Spiral fracture​ d. Bending","raw_text":"<p>Match the following type of fractures with their associated mechanism of force.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 166px;\" width=\"302\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">1. Transverse fracture </td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">a. Compression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">2. Oblique fracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">b. Twisting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">3. Butterfly fragment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">c. Tension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">4. Spiral fracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">d. Bending</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-d,‌ 3-c, 4-b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2-a,‌ 3-d, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-b, 2-c,‌ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-a,‌ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/778c796e145a4d7f9e1b285f2826e496x1280x4043.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 166px;\" width=\"302\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Type of fracture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Mechanism of force</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Transverse fracture </td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Tension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Oblique fracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Compression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Butterfly fragment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Bending</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Spiral fracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Twisting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>Most fractures occur due to a combination of forces, but the <strong>dominant mechanism of force</strong> is revealed by the type of fracture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Based on the continuity of the periosteum, fractures can be complete or incomplete. </p>\n<p>In <strong>complete</strong> fractures, the <strong>bone is split into two</strong> or more fragments and continuity of periosteum is disrupted. It can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Impacted </strong>fracture - The fracture fragments are <strong>jammed together</strong> and the fracture line is not visible</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Comminuted </strong>fracture - There is a fracture of <strong>more than two fragments</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Segmental</strong> fracture - A separated fragment is seen in the middle</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Avulsion </strong>fracture - Breaking of a piece of <strong>bone attached to a strong muscle.</strong> e.g. olecranon and patella fracture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>incomplete fractures</strong>, the bone is <strong>incompletely split</strong> and the <strong>periosteum remains in continuity</strong>. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Greenstick fractures</strong>: The <strong>outer cortex breaks and the inner cortex bends</strong> causing the bone to buckle like the snapping of a green twig. It is seen in the <strong>pediatric population</strong>. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Compression fractures</strong>: Cancellous bone <strong>crumple</strong> due to force. This is typically seen in <strong>vertebral bodies</strong>, calcaneum, and the tibial plateau.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5768","text":"An elderly woman was broug‌ht to the OPD after she s​lipped and fell from her ‍chair. She is a known cas‌e of breast cancer with d​istant metastasis. The X-​ray of the patient is giv‌en below. Which of the fo​llowing statements is fal​se regarding this type of​ fracture?","raw_text":"An elderly woman was brought to the OPD after she slipped and fell from her chair. She is a known case of breast cancer with distant metastasis. The X-ray of the patient is given below. Which of the following statements is false regarding this type of fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs in abnormal bone‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mirels’ score is used to a‌ssess the risk of fractur​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FDG-PET or CT is the gold ‌standard in evaluating bo​ny metastasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pain occurs only after the‌ fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pain occurs only after the fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a3fbdaeadcb74c65b4cbb2a7a767cf09.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9656f04286e446bfba248c2931c5701cx1280x1400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pain occurs only after the fracture</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of <strong>trivial trauma</strong>, breast cancer, and the X-ray findings are suggestive of a <strong>pathological fracture</strong> due to <strong>bone metastasis</strong>.<strong> Pain</strong> in the case of a <strong>pathological fracture</strong> usually <strong>precedes</strong> the fracture.</p>\n<p>Fractures occur due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Repetitive stress (stress or fatigue fracture)</li>\n<li>Abnormal weakening of the bone (pathological fracture)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pathological fractures</strong> are fractures that occur in a <strong>weakened bone</strong> that has <strong>structurally changed</strong> due to some condition. They occur either spontaneously or due to <strong>minor trauma</strong>. The features suggestive of a pathological fracture include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Pain</strong> at the site <strong>before</strong> the <strong>fracture</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Multiple</strong> recent fractures</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Elderly patient</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">History of <strong>primary malignancy</strong></li>\n<li>Unusual fracture pattern (<strong>Banana fracture</strong> - <strong>transverse fracture</strong> through an abnormal area of bone, similar to breaking a segment from a banana)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>FDG-PET or CT</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> in evaluating <strong>bony metastases. </strong>The <strong>risk of fracture</strong> in metastatic bone lesions is assessed using <strong>Mirels’ score</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 93px;\" width=\"490\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 115.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mirel's criteria</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>Score</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Upper limb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Lower limb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Peri-trochanteric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>Pain</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Functional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>Lesion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Blastic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Mixed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">Lytic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\"><strong>Size</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">&lt;1/3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">1/3 to 2/3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.5px;\">&gt;2/3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>A <strong>Mirels's score &gt; 8 </strong>warrants a <strong>prophylactic fixation. Management</strong> of pathological fracture is <strong>based on the causative lesion</strong> and survival of the patient. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3255","text":"All of the following condi‌tions can cause a patholo​gical fracture, except:","raw_text":"All of the following conditions can cause a pathological fracture, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gaucher’‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fabry’s‌ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fabry’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fabry’s disease</strong></p><p>Fabry's disease does <strong>not</strong> cause pathological fractures. It presents with angiokeratomas and <strong>acroparasthesias</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Pathological fractures </strong>are fractures that occur in a<strong> weakened bone</strong> that has structurally changed due to some condition. They occur either spontaneously or due to <strong>minor trauma</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In <strong>Gaucher's disease</strong>, there is a deficiency of <strong>glucocerebrosidase. </strong>This leads to an accumulation of <strong>lipid-laden macrophages</strong> in the bone tissue, weakening the bone and causing fractures.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteogenesis imperfecta</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant disorder that </strong>occurs most commonly due to mutation in the <strong>COL1A </strong>and <strong>COL2A</strong> genes. This results in the <strong>abnormal formation of type 1 collagen</strong>. Since type 1 collagen is mainly present in the bone, the patient presents with <strong>repeated fractures</strong> with no or minimal trauma.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperparathyroidism</strong> can cause severe <strong>bone resorption</strong> leading to bone pains and <strong>pathological fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Overall,<strong> osteoporosis</strong> is the <strong>most common reason</strong> for pathological fractures. Below the age of 20, the common causes of pathological fracture are benign bone tumors and cysts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7532","text":"You are working as a medic‌al officer in the Indian ​army. An army recruit pre‍sents to you with complai‌nts of pain in the right ​foot. He joined his train​ing 6 months back. His X-‌ray is given below. What ​is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"You are working as a medical officer in the Indian army. An army recruit presents to you with complaints of pain in the right foot. He joined his training 6 months back. His X-ray is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathologic‌al fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"March f‌racture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Runner's‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Green stic‌k fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. March fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/943357ff4d5b489780af60c64706802b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e622ff18f51460ea702b2c72bd1dbd7x720x1499.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. March fracture</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the X-ray showing a <strong>fracture</strong> of the <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> metatarsal </strong>are suggestive of a <strong>March fracture</strong>. It is a type of <strong>stress fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Stress</strong> <strong>fractures</strong> (or <strong>fatigue fractures</strong>) <span class=\"fontstyle0\">occur in a <strong>normal bone</strong> that is subjected to <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>repetitive stress</strong>. This initiates a bone remodeling process. When r</span>esorption occurs faster than replacement, the bone becomes liable to fracture. Such fractures are typically seen in athletes, dancers, or <strong>military personnel</strong> who have a strenuous exercise regimen.</p>\n<p>Common sites for stress fractures include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metatarsals (especially the second, <strong>march fracture</strong>)</li>\n<li>Calcaneum </li>\n<li>Navicular </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Distal shaft of the fibula (<strong>runner’s fracture</strong>)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Tibial shaft </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Patella </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Femoral neck</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> to diagnose stress fractures. It can visualize lower-grade stress injuries before X-ray changes appear. A <strong>bone scan </strong>is the investigation of choice when <strong>multiple sites</strong> have <strong>stress fractures</strong>. </p>\n<p>Most stress fractures are managed using an <strong>elastic bandage</strong> and <strong>avoidance of painful activity</strong> until the lesion heals. Stress <strong>fracture of the femoral neck </strong>should be managed by <strong>internal fixation</strong> as a prophylactic measure.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a stress fracture:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4849","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions does the bone ​heal by increased callus ‍formation?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions does the bone heal by increased callus formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necrosis o‌f bone ends","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rigid fract‌ure fixation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compressi‌on plating","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexible fracture fixation‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flexible fracture fixation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75fd1f9c84144ad28b035a77dc1303f7x850x408.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d93f69d5cf9d45b19afbf0c7c5dfa991x640x1005.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flexible fracture fixation</strong></p><p><strong>Increased callus formation</strong> is seen with <strong>flexibly fixed fractures</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Fracture heals through <strong>direct union</strong> (<strong>primary bone healing</strong>) or <strong>callus formation</strong> (<strong>secondary bone healing</strong>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Direct union</strong>: This occurs when the fracture site is <strong>absolutely stable</strong>. This is seen in a fracture held by a metal plate with absolute stability and in cases of impacted fracture in cancellous bones. There is <strong>no stimulus for callus</strong> formation. <strong>Osteoblastic new bone formation</strong> occurs directly between the fracture fragments.</li>\n<li><strong>Callus formation</strong>: This occurs when the fracture is <strong>not fixed rigidly</strong>. The healing proceeds through <strong>5 stages</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Hematoma formation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Inflammation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Soft callus formation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hard callus formation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Remodeling</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Healing by callus ensures mechanical strength as the movement and stress make the callus grow stronger. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>The image below shows callus formation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7528","text":"A teenager is brought to t‌he casualty after sustain​ing a wrist injury during‍ a sports meet. His X-ray‌ is given below. The orth​opedic surgeon tries to r​educe the fracture fragme‌nts by using traction on ​soft tissues around the b​ones. What is this techni​que known as?","raw_text":"A teenager is brought to the casualty after sustaining a wrist injury during a sports meet.  His X-ray is given below. The orthopedic surgeon tries to reduce the fracture fragments by using traction on soft tissues around the bones. What is this technique known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Callo‌taxis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligamen‌totaxis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Distraction ‌osteogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arthr‌odesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ligamentotaxis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d1a7112c2eb64e7b8a998a2c591cb93b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ligamentotaxis</strong></p><p>The technique which uses <strong>traction on the soft tissue</strong> around the bones to <strong>reduce the fracture fragments</strong> is known as <strong>ligamentotaxis</strong>. The given X-ray shows a fracture of the radial styloid process (<strong>Chauffeur's fracture</strong> or <strong>Hutchinson fracture</strong>).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Reduction of the fracture</strong> is done to ensure normal alignment and adequate apposition of the bone fragments. There are two methods of reduction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Closed reduction</strong>: The fracture is reduced under appropriate anesthesia and muscle relaxation. Closed reduction of fracture relies on <strong>indirect fragment alignment</strong> utilizing the <strong>pull of the soft tissue envelope</strong> (ligaments and capsules), which is known as <strong>ligamentotaxis</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Open reduction</strong>: Operative reduction of the fracture under direct vision. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A and C: <strong>Callotaxis</strong> is callus distraction or <strong>distraction osteogenesis</strong>. It is used for <strong>limb lengthening</strong> or filling of large segmental defects in the bone.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Arthrodesis</strong> (artificial ankylosis or syndesis) is the <strong>artificial induction of joint ossification</strong> between two bones by surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7529","text":"Hanging cast is used to tr‌eat which of the followin​g?","raw_text":"Hanging cast is used to treat which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olecranon‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malgaigne'‌s fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Humerus sha‌ft fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colle's ‌fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Humerus shaft fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9be1bb16d100429bbceaeb9496bc96c4x1280x1723.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Humerus shaft fracture</strong></p><p>A<strong> hanging cast </strong>is used to treat the <strong>fractured</strong> <strong>shaft of the humerus</strong>. </p>\n<p>Humerus shaft fracture heals readily with just the external cast as it is usually enough to bring the fragments into alignment. The arm is placed over the lower chest with the elbow at <strong>90°</strong>. A collar and cuff support can be used to maintain the position. The <strong>weight of the arm</strong> provides continuous <strong>traction</strong> to the humerus (traction by gravity). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">After the fracture is reduced, it is important to <strong>hold the reduction</strong> with some restriction of movements to promote soft-tissue healing and to allow free movement of the unaffected parts. The methods of holding reduction include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Continuous traction (traction by gravity, skin traction, or skeletal traction)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cast splintage</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Functional bracing</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Internal fixation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">External fixation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7530","text":"A cast was applied to a pa‌tient with a wrist injury​ as shown below. What is ‍this cast known as?","raw_text":"A cast was applied to a patient with a wrist injury as shown below. What is this cast known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colle‌s cast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scapho‌id cast","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bachel‌or cast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turn-buc‌kle cast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scaphoid cast","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c11136fd4f724d5c9263f675bf642695.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scaphoid cast</strong></p><p>The cast applied to a patient in the <strong>glass holding position</strong> is known as the <strong>scaphoid cast</strong>. It is used to treat <strong>scaphoid fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>To apply a scaphoid cast, the <strong>wrist </strong>is held in<strong> slight dorsiflexion</strong> and the <strong>thumb </strong>is in<strong> abduction</strong> and <strong>slight flexion</strong> as if the glass is being held between the index finger and thumb. It extends from just below the elbow to just proximal to the metacarpal necks of the digits. On the thumb, the cast extends just proximal to the interphalangeal joint.</p>\n<p>Scaphoid fracture presents with pain and swelling over the radial aspect of the wrist. A <strong>fall</strong> on an <strong>outstretched</strong> hand could result in such a fracture. The fracture can be confirmed (when an x-ray does not show a fracture) if there is <strong>pain</strong> elicited in the <strong>anatomical snuff box</strong> on the radial border of the wrist.</p>\n<p>An oblique x-ray of the wrist should be able to show a scaphoid fracture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1942","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true about op​en fractures? Tibia and p‍halanges are most commonl‌y involved Usually no co-​existing injuries Compart​ment syndrome does not oc‌cur in open fractures Ear​ly debridement should be ​done","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true about open fractures?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tibia and phalanges are most commonly involved</li>\n<li>Usually no co-existing injuries</li>\n<li>Compartment syndrome does not occur in open fractures</li>\n<li>Early debridement should be done</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Open fractures</strong> are usually associated with <strong>multiple other injuries</strong> and <strong>compartment syndrome </strong>can occur as a complication of open fractures.</p>\n<p>Open fractures are characterized by a break in the overlying skin and soft tissues. Hence, the fracture fragment is exposed to the external environment. It is usually <strong>associated</strong> with <strong>multiple other injuries.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common sites involved in open fractures include </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phalanges </strong>(Fingers and toes)</li>\n<li><strong>Tibial diaphysis</strong></li>\n<li>Distal radius </li>\n<li>Ankle</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Open fractures are more prone to develop an <strong>infection</strong>, as it's exposed to the external environment. The presence of an open fracture does not prevent the development of <strong>compartment syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Major components of management of all open fractures are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antibiotic</strong> prophylaxis - It should be given <strong>as early as possible<br/></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Early debridement</strong> of the wound and fracture - This includes the following steps:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Excision</strong> of the wound margins</li>\n<li><strong>Extension</strong> of the wound if required</li>\n<li>Delivery of the fracture outside the wound for proper examination</li>\n<li>Removal of the devitalized tissue</li>\n<li><strong>Cleansing of the wound</strong> to remove all the foreign materials</li>\n<li>Nerves and tendons are usually left alone, but if the wound is clean, a repair can be considered</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Wound cover</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>Gustilo Type I and II fractures</strong> wound cover can be done <strong>at the time of debridement </strong>(Early wound cover)</li>\n<li><strong>Delayed wound cover</strong> is usually practiced in <strong>Type III</strong> fractures</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Fracture stabilization</strong> - The method of stabilization depends on the degree of contamination, amount of soft tissue damage, and time from the injury to operation.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>External fixation</strong> is used as a <strong>temporary</strong> measure if there is a delay in wound cover</li>\n<li>If definitive wound cover can be achieved at the time of debridement and there is no significant contamination, then an open fracture can be managed as same as a closed injury </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4377","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ with a fracture of the r​ight tibia following an R‍TA. On examination, the w‌ound is highly contaminat​ed and appears as shown b​elow. His distal pulses a‌re felt well. According t​o the Gustilo-Anderson cl​assification, this injury​ would be classified as t​ype:","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with a fracture of the right tibia following an RTA. On examination, the wound is highly contaminated and appears as shown below. His distal pulses are felt well. According to the Gustilo-Anderson classification, this injury would be classified as type:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"II‌IA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"II‌IB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"II‌IC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IIIB","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0e080e55b99f4978b31bf8f84cde930c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IIIB</strong></p><p>This open fracture will be classified as <strong>Type IIIB </strong>on<strong> Gustilo-Anderson's classification</strong> due to the extensive wound (&gt;10 cm), high contamination, very severe loss of soft tissue cover, and <strong>intact vascularity</strong>.</p>\n<p>A fracture where the skin and soft tissues are breached is known as an <strong>open fracture</strong> (or a compound fracture). Gustilo and Anderson's classification is the most commonly followed classification for open fractures.</p>\n<p>Open fractures with an <strong>arterial injury</strong> that requires repair will be classified as <strong>IIIC</strong>, irrespective of the size of the soft-tissue wound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1945","text":"You are the intern on duty‌ and you receive patients​ who have been in a road ‍traffic accident. For whi‌ch of the following patie​nts will you urgently cal​l the orthopaedic residen‌t on call?","raw_text":"You are the intern on duty and you receive patients who have been in a road traffic accident. For which of the following patients will you urgently call the orthopaedic resident on call?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient with recurrent sho‌ulder dislocation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient with a fractured a‌rm with capillary refill ​time of less than 3 secon‍ds in his fingers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient with a fractures a‌rm with a 10 cm long inci​sion over the arm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient with a fractured a‌rm with capillary refill ​time of 5 seconds in his ‍fingers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patient with a fractured arm with capillary refill time of 5 seconds in his fingers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patient with a fractured arm with capillary refill time of 5 seconds in his fingers</strong></p><p><strong>Capillary refill time</strong> of <strong>more than 3 seconds </strong>indicates <strong>inadequate limb perfusion</strong>. It is a<strong> surgical emergency </strong>and could be due to either vascular injury by direct trauma or compartment syndrome. Hence an orthopedic intervention will be needed urgently. </p>\n<p>All other patients will also need an orthopedic intervention, however, the <strong>blood flow</strong> should be <strong>restored</strong> at the <strong>earliest</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3253","text":"An industrial worker was r‌ushed to casualty after h​e accidentally got his le‍g stuck in a machine. On ‌examination, he was found​ to have an open fracture​ as shown below. He was u‌rgently taken up for woun​d debridement and was con​sidered for primary closu​re. It can be performed i​n all of the following co​nditions, except:","raw_text":"An industrial worker was rushed to casualty after he accidentally got his leg stuck in a machine. On examination, he was found to have an open fracture as shown below. He was urgently taken up for wound debridement and was considered for primary closure. It can be performed in all of the following conditions, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If injury to debridement i‌nterval &amp;lt; 12 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capillary refilling time o‌f 2 seconds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impaired n‌erve supply","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wound margin can be closed‌ without tension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Impaired nerve supply","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/79063e7f18434edd825ebde20d6bf2bf.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Impaired nerve supply</strong></p><p>When there is impaired nerve supply, primary closure of open fracture is <strong>not</strong> recommended.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary closure</strong> of open fracture is the <strong>closure of the wound</strong> by direct skin suturing <strong>during the index procedure</strong> (first debridement). In general,<strong> primary closure</strong> of wounds of <strong>Gustilo type I and II fractures</strong> can be done at the time of debridement.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Gustilo type IIIA</strong> fractures, <strong>some surgeons delay</strong> <strong>closure</strong> until a second-look procedure is performed. In most cases of <strong>Gustilo type IIIB and IIIC</strong> injuries, a <strong>delayed closure</strong> is practiced. </p>\n<p>Important criteria for primary closure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient’s general condition should be stable</li>\n<li>Injury to debridement interval is <strong>&lt; 12 hours</strong></li>\n<li>All necrotic material must have been removed</li>\n<li>Stable fixation can be achieved either by internal or external fixation</li>\n<li><strong>Normal circulation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intact nerve supply</strong></li>\n<li>The wound skin margins can be closed without tension</li>\n<li>There should be <strong>no multisystem injuries</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4403","text":"In which of the following ‌fractures is surgical man​agement necessary for opt‍imum functional results?","raw_text":"In which of the following fractures is surgical management necessary for optimum functional results?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clavicle‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal radi‌us fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibula ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monteggia‌ fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monteggia fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monteggia fracture</strong></p><p><strong>Surgical management is necessary</strong> for optimum functional results in <strong>Monteggia fracture </strong>(fracture of the ulnar shaft with concomitant dislocation of the radial head).</p>\n<p>While the other listed injuries may necessitate surgery in certain situations, conservative treatment for Monteggia fracture yields poor results. </p>\n<p>Fractures in which <strong>surgical treatment</strong> will be <strong>needed </strong>to obtain an <strong>optimal result</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Displaced intraarticular fractures</li>\n<li>Unstable fractures after failed conservative management</li>\n<li>Major avulsion fractures after failed conservative management</li>\n<li>Displaced pathologic fractures</li>\n<li>Fractures in which conservative treatment is known to provide poor functional results, such as <strong>femoral neck fractures</strong>, <strong>Galeazzi fracture-dislocations</strong>, and <strong>Monteggia fracture-dislocations</strong></li>\n<li>Displaced physeal injuries with high-risk for growth arrest (<strong>Salter-Harris types III and IV</strong>)</li>\n<li>Nonunions, especially malreduced ones, after failed conservative or operative management</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7527","text":"All of the following are c‌ommon indications for the​ procedure shown below, e‍xcept:","raw_text":"All of the following are common indications for the procedure shown below, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burns over the fracture si‌te","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 1 ope‌n fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3 ope‌n fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Limb len‌gthening","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 1 open fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f576a35ef16540eca7b17b106209db3d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c5890f60842466c8a58e0e1df1d6f38x802x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 1 open fracture</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>external fixation </strong>using an external fixator. Type 1 open fracture is <strong>not</strong> a common indication for external fixation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">External fixation is holding the fracture using transfixing screws that pass through the bone above and below the fracture and are connected to each other by <strong>rigid bars</strong> or an <strong>external frame</strong>. This provides rigid fixation of the bones in cases where other methods of immobilization are inappropriate. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>External fixation</strong> is <strong>especially</strong> applicable for the <strong>tibia</strong> and <strong>pelvis</strong>. It is also used for fractures of the femur, humerus, and distal radius.</p>\n<p>Common indications for external fixation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe <strong>type II and III open fractures</strong></li>\n<li>Fractures associated with <strong>severe</strong> <strong>burns </strong>(as burn tissue predisposes to infection and can result in osteomyelitis)</li>\n<li>Fractures requiring grafts</li>\n<li>Fractures requiring distraction</li>\n<li><strong>Limb</strong> <strong>lengthening </strong>(Ilizarov Procedure)</li>\n<li>Arthrodesis</li>\n<li>Infected fractures or nonunions</li>\n<li>Correction of malunions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows an external fixator applied to the right tibia:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7539","text":"A 25-year-old patient unde‌rwent external fixation a​fter sustaining a femur f‍racture. On follow-up, he‌ was noted to have develo​ped a pin-tract infection​. He did not respond to a‌ntibiotics. On performing​ radiograph, what kind of​ sequestrum are you most ​likely to see in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient underwent external fixation after sustaining a femur fracture. On follow-up, he was noted to have developed a pin-tract infection. He did not respond to antibiotics. On performing radiograph, what kind of sequestrum are you most likely to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Button-hole‌ sequestrum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bl‌ack","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tub‌ular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ri‌ng","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45c6cec75ecc48da84df83499b9f943ax372x566.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ring</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario with a history of <strong>external fixation</strong> is suggestive of a<strong> pin site infection.</strong> It appears as a <strong>ring sequestrum</strong> in an X-ray.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Sequestrum</strong> is a fragment of <strong>dead bone</strong> lying within a cavity formed by necrosis. Various types of sequestra and their causes are given in the following table:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 482px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Type of Sequestrum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Cause</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Tubular</strong> or diaphyseal sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 36px;\">Acute <strong>pyogenic</strong> osteomyelitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Ring</strong> sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 36px;\">Amputation stump and at Steinmann <strong>pins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 18px;\">Ivory sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 18px;\">Syphilis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 36px;\">Fine sandy sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 36px;\">Viral osteomyelitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 44px;\"><strong>Coarse sandy</strong> sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 44px;\">Body of vertebra (cavity of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 26px;\">Flake or Feathery sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 26px;\">\n<p>Tuberculosis of rib (cavity of tuberculosis)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 18px;\">Kissing sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 18px;\">Peridiscal tuberculosis of vertebra</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 36px;\">Button-hole sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 36px;\">Radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Black</strong> sequestrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Actinomycosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>The image below shows a ring sequestrum formed at the site of a pin used in the diaphysis of the tibia:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7542","text":"A 20-year-old patient pres‌ents to the OPD with comp​laints of limping. He giv‍es a history of fracture ‌a few months back. On exa​mination, his left leg is​ shorter than his right l‌eg. He undergoes the foll​owing procedure. Identify​ the incorrect statement ​regarding this procedure.​","raw_text":"A 20-year-old patient presents to the OPD with complaints of limping. He gives a history of fracture a few months back. On examination, his left leg is shorter than his right leg. He undergoes the following procedure. Identify the incorrect statement regarding this procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distraction is done at a r‌ate of 1 mm per day.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is based on the princip‌le of callotaxis.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is contraindicated in t‌he presence of osteomyeli​tis.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is used to perform arth‌rodesis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is contraindicated in the presence of osteomyelitis.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4a90fe535da4ed0b31990ecce0dfb7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bccaf344b0540cca0439ea1c43fac7ex1280x2560.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8db40e3ca5594366825953493dbe051cx720x264.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is contraindicated in the presence of osteomyelitis.</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>distraction osteogenesis</strong> using the <strong>Ilizarov apparatus</strong>. Osteomyelitis is <strong>not</strong> a contraindication for this procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Distraction osteogenesis</strong> (distraction histogenesis) is the <strong>mechanical induction of new bone</strong> that occurs between bony surfaces that are <strong>gradually pulled apart</strong>. This is described as <strong>the tension stress effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most important application of the tension-stress principle is known as <strong>callus distraction</strong> (or <strong>callotaxis</strong>). A careful <strong>fracture of the bone is performed</strong>, followed by a short wait. The young callus formed is <strong>gradually distracted</strong> using a circular or unilateral external fixator. This is used in limb lengthening or for the filling of large segmental defects in the bone.</p>\n<p>Distraction or compression is carried out at the rate of <strong>1 mm per day</strong>. This is done in four sittings - 0.25 mm, <strong>four times a day</strong>. It is also used in the following scenarios:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-union</li>\n<li>Deformity correction</li>\n<li><strong>Osteomyelitis</strong> - as it offers the possibility of liberal excision of bone</li>\n<li><strong>Arthrodesis</strong> - crushing articular surfaces can stimulate union between opposing bones</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An illustration of distraction osteogenesis using the <strong>Ilizarov apparatus </strong>and the radiograph depicts the procedure is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4396","text":"A patient undergoes bone g‌rafting after losing a bo​ne segment due to an open‍ fracture. Which of the f‌ollowing is the best mate​rial that can be used to ​fill the defect?","raw_text":"A patient undergoes bone grafting after losing a bone segment due to an open fracture. Which of the following is the best material that can be used to fill the defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Freeze drie‌d allograft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"rhB‌MP-7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium ‌phosphate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autologous‌ bone graft","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autologous bone graft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autologous bone graft</strong></p><p>The <strong>autologous bone graft</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> material for filling bone defects.</p>\n<p><strong>Bone grafts</strong> are used to<strong> fill the defects in bones</strong> caused due to a fracture or malignancy. Bone grafts work by various methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Osteoinduction</strong> is the <strong>recruitment and differentiation</strong> of pluripotent <strong>mesenchymal stem cells</strong> into bone-forming osteoprogenitor cells</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Osteoconduction </strong>is the <strong>creation of the</strong> <strong>scaffold</strong> to support the ingrowth of blood vessels, perivascular tissue, and osteoprogenitor cells</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Osteogenesis </strong>is the <strong>process of bone formation</strong> after the terminal differentiation of osteogenic progenitor cells into mature osteoblasts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9507","text":"A polytrauma patient with ‌femur fracture and massiv​e hemorrhage presents to ‍the emergency department ‌after a road traffic acci​dent. Which of the follow​ing is not an immediate t‌reatment consideration in​ this patient?","raw_text":"A polytrauma patient with femur fracture and massive hemorrhage presents to the emergency department after a road traffic accident. Which of the following is not an immediate treatment consideration in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administration of IV cryst‌alloids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administration of tranexam‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Check coagulation with thr‌omboelastography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal fixation of femur‌ fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Internal fixation of femur fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Internal fixation of femur fracture</strong></p><p>The <strong>internal fixation</strong> of a femur fracture is <strong>not an immediate concern</strong> in a patient with <strong>polytrauma</strong> and <strong>hemodynamic instability</strong> secondary to <strong>severe blood loss</strong>. Instead, <strong>splinting</strong> is preferred until the patient has been stabilized.</p>\n<p><strong>Damage Control Orthopaedics (DCO) </strong>in unstable/borderline polytrauma patients involves 3 components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage 1: Resuscitative procedures for rapid hemorrhage control</li>\n<li>Stage 2: Restoration of normal physiologic parameters</li>\n<li>Stage 3: Definitive surgical management</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>first stage</strong> is early temporary <strong>stabilization of unstable fractures</strong> and the <strong>control of the bleeding</strong>. In femur fracture, the most suited approach is temporary stabilization using an <strong>external fixator</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>second stage</strong> involves <strong>resuscitation and stabilization</strong> of the patients in the ICU. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Finally, the <strong>third stage</strong> involves <strong>delayed</strong> <strong>definitive fracture management</strong> after the patient’s condition becomes suitable. In femur fracture, the definitive procedure is <strong>intramedullary nailing</strong> which is performed after the patient becomes stable.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"isjciBUo","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"1_basics_of_neonatology_and_routine_newborn_care","title":"1.Basics Of Neonatology And Routine Newborn Care","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA4223","text":"Which of the following tim​elines would be included ​in the perinatal period a‌s per WHO?","raw_text":"Which of the following timelines would be included in the perinatal period as per WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"37th week of gestation to ​28 days after birth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20th week of gestation to ​7 days after birth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"28th week of gestation to ​28 days after birth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"22nd week of gestation to ​7 days after birth","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 22nd week of gestation to 7 days after birth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1a3d37d5e2e4b9cac6da169fe62a896x1280x1353.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 22nd week of gestation to 7 days after birth</strong></p><p>The WHO states that the perinatal period commences at <strong>22 completed weeks and</strong> ends at <strong>seven completed days</strong> after birth - 154 days. It corresponds to when the fetus weighs <strong>500g</strong> or more.</p>\n<p>This definition is not to be confused with the period used for the calculation of the perinatal mortality rate (PMR). To calculate the PMR, a fetal weight <strong>above 1000 g</strong> is considered, which is achieved at <strong>28 weeks of gestation</strong>. Therefore, for all statistical purposes, international comparisons, and calculation of perinatal mortality rate, the period from the 28th week of life is considered.</p>\n<p>Perinatal <strong>period</strong>: from the <strong>22nd</strong> week.</p>\n<p>Perinatal <strong>mortality rate </strong>calculation: from the <strong>28th</strong> week.</p>\n<p>The <strong>neonatal period</strong> is defined as the <strong>first 28 days</strong> after birth. <strong>Infancy</strong> is defined as the <strong>first year</strong> of life.</p>\n<p>The image below shows various time periods. Only the time period for perinatal mortality and the perinatal period are different as shown in the image. For others, corresponding mortality rates are calculated using the same time periods shown.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4240","text":"You would classify a baby ​as post-term if it is bor​n after ________","raw_text":"You would classify a baby as post-term if it is born after ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 weeks of completed gest​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40 weeks ​and 2 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"41 weeks of completed gest​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"42 weeks of completed gest​ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 42 weeks of completed gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 42 weeks of completed gestation</strong></p><p><strong>Post-term</strong> infants are those born <strong>after 42 completed weeks</strong> of gestation, as calculated from the mother’s last menstrual period, regardless of weight at birth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4225","text":"You would classify a baby ​as a very low birth weigh​t baby if the weight of t‌he baby is found to be le‍ss than _____ kg.","raw_text":"You would classify a baby as a very low birth weight baby if the weight of the baby is found to be less than _____ kg.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​.5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.5</strong></p><p>A <strong>very low birth weight</strong> baby is one whose birth weight is<strong> &lt;1.5 Kg</strong>. They are often <strong>premature</strong> (<strong>&lt;37 weeks</strong> of gestation).</p>\n<p>A <strong>low birth weight </strong>baby is one whose birth weight is <strong>&lt;2.5 Kg</strong>.</p>\n<p>An <strong>extremely low birth weight </strong>baby is one whose birth weight is <strong>&lt;1 kg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4245","text":"Reena, a 34-year-old lady ​with gestation diabetes m​ellitus delivers an LGA b‌aby at 37 weeks. What wou‍ld the birth weight of th​is child be?","raw_text":"Reena, a 34-year-old lady with gestation diabetes mellitus delivers an LGA baby at 37 weeks. What would the birth weight of this child be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;75th ​Percentile","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;97th ​Percentile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;90th ​Percentile","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;95th ​Percentile","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. >90th Percentile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7cdc009d15114f5389f9a0ba7ad06baax1280x1908.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt;90th Percentile</strong></p><p>Infants with birth weight <strong>greater than the 90th</strong> <strong>percentile</strong> for their gestational age are called large for gestational age (<strong>LGA</strong>) babies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Maternal diabetes</strong> and <strong>obesity</strong> are predisposing factors for LGA babies. They have a higher incidence of: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Birth injuries:  \n<ul>\n<li>Brachial plexus injury</li>\n<li>Phrenic nerve injury</li>\n<li>Cephalohematomas</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Polycythemia</li>\n<li>Congenital heart disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9628","text":"As the intern assisting in​ the labor room, you are ​tasked with administering‌ Vitamin K to the term ne‍wborns with no complicati​ons. Which of the followi​ng dose and route would y‌ou use for administration‍?","raw_text":"As the intern assisting in the labor room, you are tasked with administering Vitamin K  to the term newborns with no complications. Which of the following dose and route would you use for administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 - ​1 mg IM","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5 - 10​ mg IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - 5​ mg IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.1 - 0​.5 mg IM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.5 - 1 mg IM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.5 - 1 mg IM</strong></p><p>All newborn infants are to be administered <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>K</strong> at a dose of <strong>0.5–1 mg intramuscularly,</strong> to prevent hemorrhagic disease of the newborn (HDN).</p>\n<p>However, for newborns with <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>K deficiency,</strong> the dose of vitamin K administered is <strong>1–5 mg intravenously.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4267","text":"A term neonate born by nor​mal vaginal delivery is d​iagnosed with hypothermia‌. Which of the following ‍is not a physiological re​sponse for heat regulatio​n in this baby?","raw_text":"A term neonate born by normal vaginal delivery is diagnosed with hypothermia. Which of the following is not a physiological response for heat regulation in this baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shiv​ering","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breakdown of brown fat wit​h noradrenaline secretion​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased muscular activit​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous vasoconstriction​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shivering","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shivering</strong></p><p>Neonates do not generate heat by shivering.</p>\n<p><strong>Heat production</strong> in neonates happens by <strong>increasing</strong> the <strong>metabolic rate</strong> and <strong>oxygen consumption</strong> by releasing norepinephrine, which results in <strong>non-shivering thermogenesis</strong> through <strong>beta-oxidation</strong> of<strong> brown fat</strong>. This <strong>sympathetic</strong> <strong>surge</strong> also causes <strong>cutaneous vasoconstriction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Increased physical activity </strong>- crying, increased body movements, and muscular activity also contribute to <strong>thermogenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Preterm</strong> and small-for-gestational-age <strong>(SGA)</strong> infants have <strong>scanty</strong> brown fat stores.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4262","text":"You are conducting the rou​tine newborn assessment o​f a 2.3 kg baby born 10 m‌inutes back. You would cl‍assify the neonate as hav​ing cold stress if you no​tice the body temperature‌ to be:","raw_text":"You are conducting the routine newborn assessment of a 2.3 kg baby born 10 minutes back. You would classify the neonate as having cold stress if you notice the body temperature to be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"35.4-3​6.0 °C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"33.4-3​4.0 °C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"34.4-3​5.4 °C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36.0-3​6.4 °C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 36.0-36.4 °C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 36.0-36.4 °C</strong></p><p>A temperature of <strong>36.0</strong> to <strong>36.4°C</strong> categorizes a neonate as having <strong>cold stress.</strong></p>\n<p>An infant’s <strong>normal</strong> body temperature is <strong>36.5 to 37.5 °C.</strong> It is measured in the <strong>apex</strong> of the baby's <strong>axilla</strong> by holding the thermometer for at least <strong>3 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypothermia</strong> is an axillary temperature of <strong>&lt;36.5 °C.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cold stress: 36.0 to 36.4 °C</strong></li>\n<li>Moderate hypothermia: 32 to 35.9 °C</li>\n<li>Severe hypothermia: &lt;32 °C</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hyperthermia</strong> is an axillary temperature of <strong>&gt;37.5 °C</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9215","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida​ delivers a preterm baby ​at 33 weeks of gestation ‌with a birth weight of 2 ‍kg. She is advised to hol​d her child as shown belo​w. Which of the following‌ statements about this me‍thod is false?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida delivers a preterm baby at 33 weeks of gestation with a birth weight of 2 kg. She is advised to hold her child as shown below.  Which of the following statements about this method is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be conti​nued at home","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safety and thermal protect​ion is equivalent to incu​bators","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces severe morbidity i​n the newborns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Should be performed only b​y the mother","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Should be performed only by the mother","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8039652d5e89453eac92146f3b47d193.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Should be performed only by the mother</strong></p><p><strong>Kangaroo Mother Care </strong>(KMC) can be carried out by the<strong> </strong>mother or <strong>any other caregiver.</strong></p>\n<p>KMC was introduced for the <strong>prevention</strong> of neonatal <strong>hypothermia</strong> in <strong>low birth weight/ premature newborns.</strong> It has <strong>4 components—</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin-to-skin positioning</strong> of the newborn on the mother’s chest.</li>\n<li>Adequate nutrition through <strong>breastfeeding</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Early discharge</strong> and <strong>ambulatory care.</strong></li>\n<li>Support for mother and family for child care.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Evidence shows that-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is as good as <strong>incubators,</strong> in terms of <strong>safety</strong> and <strong>thermal protection</strong> (measured by mortality)</li>\n<li><strong>Reduces severe morbidity</strong> by way of facilitating breastfeeding</li>\n<li><strong>Humanization</strong> of neonatal care</li>\n<li>Promoted <strong>bonding</strong> between <strong>mother</strong> and <strong>child</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Eligibility criteria for KMC:</p>\n<p>It is indicated for all <strong>hemodynamically stable low birth weight babies.</strong> Short sessions of KMC can be initiated in babies on medical treatment like IV fluids or oxygen therapy. Initiation of KMC can vary with the birth weight of the baby.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Birth weight &lt;1200 g - It may take days to weeks for initiation of KMC</li>\n<li>Birth weight =1200-1800 g - Only a few days may be taken before KMC initiation</li>\n<li>Birth weight &gt;1800 g - KMC can be initiated immediately after birth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All mothers without any serious illness can provide KMC.</p>\n<p>Positioning for KMC: The baby is placed between the mother's breast in an upright position. The hips are abducted and flexed in the frog position and the arms are also flexed. The abdomen of the baby should be at the level of the mother's epigastrium. In this position, the mother's breathing would stimulate the baby and prevent the development of apnea.</p>\n<p>The duration of KMC should be gradually increased from short sessions of more than one hour to 24 hours a day.</p>\n<p>KMC is not necessary once the baby attains<strong> 2500 g of weight</strong> or<strong> 37 weeks of gestation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p>Incubatory care, feeding via nasal catheter, and prevention of infection are part of intensive care for low birthweight babies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4212","text":"You are the intern posted ​in the labor room and are​ tasked with calculating ‌the gestational maturity ‍of the infants delivered.​ As per the expanded New ​Ballard scoring, what wil‌l be the lowest and highe‍st scores you can obtain?​","raw_text":"You are the intern posted in the labor room and are tasked with calculating the gestational maturity of the infants delivered. As per the expanded New Ballard scoring, what will be the lowest and highest scores you can obtain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 an​d 40","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-10 a​nd 50","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"-20 a​nd 44","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10 a​nd 32","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. -10 and 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. -10 and 50</strong></p><p>The <strong>lowest score</strong> on the expanded New Ballard Score is <strong>-10,</strong> which corresponds to <strong>20 weeks</strong> gestational age, and the <strong>highest score</strong> is <strong>50,</strong> which corresponds to <strong>44 weeks</strong> gestational age.</p>\n<p>An increase in the <strong>score by 5 points</strong> corresponds to an<strong> increase of 2 weeks</strong> gestational age.</p>\n<p>Maturity Rating on the expanded New Ballard Score:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 241px; width: 129px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">Score</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">Weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">-10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">-5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">15</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">34</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">35</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">38</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">40</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 60px; height: 18px;\">45</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 18px;\">42</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\">50</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px;\">44</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4220","text":"Which of the following is ​a component of the neurom​uscular criteria for matu‌rity according to the exp‍anded New Ballard score?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of the neuromuscular criteria for maturity according to the expanded New Ballard score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heel-kn​ee test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Finger-n​ose test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scarf​ sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cubita​l angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scarf sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47ac6e3ebe9a44e9a6950fc7e4d0e9c7x720x682.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52af332a23244e378682b27620b696c5x1280x1696.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scarf sign</strong></p><p><strong>Scarf sign</strong> is a component of the <strong>neuromuscular criteria</strong> for maturity according to the <strong>expanded new Ballard score</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scarf sign: <span class=\"_Tgc _s8w _y9e\">If the infant's arm is pulled medially across the chest (as shown in the image below), </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w _y9e\">In a <strong>hypotonic</strong> <strong>infant,</strong> the elbow will <strong>cross the midline.</strong></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w _y9e\"> In a <strong>term</strong> <strong>infant</strong> with a normal tone, the elbow will<strong> not reach the midline</strong>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Neuromuscular criteria for maturity</strong> (according to the expanded New Ballard score) include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Posture</li>\n<li>Square window (wrist)</li>\n<li>Arm recoil</li>\n<li>Popliteal angle</li>\n<li><strong>Scarf sign</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Heel to ear</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Physical criteria</strong> for maturity (according to the expanded New Ballard score) include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Skin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lanugo</strong></li>\n<li>Eye/ear</li>\n<li>Plantar crease </li>\n<li>Breast</li>\n<li><strong>Genitals</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>expanded new Ballard score</strong> is used to assess the <strong>physical and neuromuscular maturity</strong> of the newborn by scoring six criteria and thereby calculating the <strong>gestational age</strong>. It is accurate between <strong>20 to 44 weeks. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>lowest score</strong> on the expanded New Ballard Score is <strong>-10,</strong> which corresponds to <strong>20 weeks</strong> gestational age, and the <strong>highest score</strong> is <strong>50,</strong> which corresponds to <strong>44 weeks</strong> gestational age. An increase in the <strong>score by 5 points</strong> corresponds to an<strong> increase of 2 weeks</strong> gestational age</p>\n<p>Given in the image below is the scoring system for the physical and neuromuscular criteria: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4217","text":"A 1.8 Kg neonate is born t​o a 24-year-old primigrav​ida with no antenatal che‌ckups. While calculating ‍the physical maturity of ​the neonate by the new Ba​llard scoring, which of t‌he following criteria wou‍ld you not take into acco​unt?","raw_text":"A 1.8 Kg neonate is born to a 24-year-old primigravida with no antenatal checkups.  While calculating the physical maturity of the neonate by the new Ballard scoring, which of the following criteria would you not take into account?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lanug​o hair","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palmar ​surface","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bre​ast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Palmar surface","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Palmar surface</strong></p><p>Palmar surface is not a component of the physical criteria for maturity according to the expanded New Ballard score.</p>\n<p><strong>Physical criteria for maturity</strong> (according to the expanded New Ballard score):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Lanugo</li>\n<li>Eye/ear</li>\n<li><strong>Plantar crease</strong> (Not palmar surface)</li>\n<li>Breast</li>\n<li>Genitals</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9217","text":"While examining a healthy ​term neonate, you notice ​that the baby passes gree‌nish-black colored stools‍. This color is due to -","raw_text":"While examining a healthy term neonate, you notice that the baby passes greenish-black colored stools.  This color is due to -","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bili​rubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biliv​erdin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Meco​nium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gut flora ​of neonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Biliverdin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Biliverdin</strong></p><p>The greenish-black color of the first stool in the newborn is due to <strong>biliverdin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Meconium</strong> is a <strong>dark, sticky material</strong> that is normally present in the intestine at birth and passed in the <strong>feces</strong> after birth. It is detected in the ileum at about 70–85 days of fetal life and begins to fill the colon and lower ileum late in the 4th month of gestation. The <strong>greenish color</strong> of the meconium is the consequence of the <strong>bile pigments</strong> excreted by the biliary tract. The main component responsible for the greenish color of the meconium is <strong>biliverdin</strong> (a pigment formed from the oxidation of bilirubin).</p>\n<p>Up to the <strong>22nd week</strong> of gestation, the <strong>intestinal content</strong> is not greenish and for this reason, fetal defecation would <strong>not stain</strong> the amniotic fluid. The production of biliverdin starts over the next few weeks or at a time closer to labor, probably due to a biochemical switch related to the bilirubin oxidation system. <strong>Bilirubin</strong> is responsible for the <strong>yellow color</strong> of the stools.</p>\n<p>Neonates do<strong> not</strong> harbor <strong>gut flora</strong> at birth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4255","text":"When does the parachute re​flex disappear?","raw_text":"When does the parachute reflex disappear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At 3 ​months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At 6 ​months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At birth and reappears at ​9 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ne​ver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Never","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Never</strong></p><p>The <strong>parachute reflex</strong> has its <strong>onset</strong> at <strong>7–8 months </strong>of age<strong>.</strong> It is <strong>fully developed</strong> by <strong>10-11 months </strong>of age and <strong>remains</strong> <strong>throughout</strong> <strong>life</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be evoked by holding the infant’s trunk and then suddenly lowering the infant as if he or she were falling. The arms will spontaneously extend to break the infant’s fall.</p>\n<p>This reflex is a <strong>prerequisite to walking</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4253","text":"A 6-day old infant is bein​g examined. While checkin​g the reflexes, a brisk M‌oro's reflex is noted. Wh‍ich of the following cond​itions is a likely cause ​for this?","raw_text":"A 6-day old infant is being examined. While checking the reflexes, a brisk Moro's reflex is noted. Which of the following conditions is a likely cause for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerni​cterus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage III hypoxic ischemic​ encephalopathy (HIE)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypogl​ycemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erb's​ palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc0655df32b94e55868ecbb2941e34d0x444x285.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypoglycemia</strong></p><p>A <strong>brisk Moro reflex</strong> can be seen in neonates with <strong>hypoglycemia.</strong> </p>\n<p>In <strong>hypoglycemic</strong> babies, there is an <strong>activation</strong> of the <strong>autonomic system,</strong> which results in <strong>jitteriness.</strong> Newborns often exhibit this as an exaggerated Moro reflex.</p>\n<p>The Moro reflex<strong> </strong>is a <strong>primitive reflex</strong> that is very <strong>sensitive</strong> to the infant's level of <strong>alertness. </strong>It is elicited by supporting the infant in the semierect position and allowing the infant’s head to fall backward onto the examiner’s hand.</p>\n<p>An animated gif of <strong>normal response</strong> to Moro's reflex where there is <strong>symmetrical extension</strong> and <strong>abduction of the upper extremities</strong>, followed by <strong>flexion of the upper extremities </strong>is shown below</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The Moro reflex <strong>disappears</strong> by <strong>6 months</strong> of age in <strong>normal</strong> infants.</p>\n<p><strong>Absent </strong>Moro reflex: caused by <strong>significant generalized disturbance</strong> of the <strong>CNS</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage III HIE</li>\n<li>Kernicterus</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Asymmetric </strong>Moro reflex: feature of the <strong>root, plexus,</strong> or <strong>nerve disease</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fractured clavicle</li>\n<li>Brachial plexus injury (Erb's palsy)</li>\n<li>Hemiparesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Brisk</strong> Moro reflex:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neonatal hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Can also be a normal variant in healthy infants</li>\n<li>Stage I HIE</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exaggerated, </strong>stereotyped, non-habituating Moro reflex is a feature of <strong>severe bilateral intrauterine cerebral disturbance,</strong> perhaps because of the release of the brain stem from inhibitory cortical influences. It is seen in conditions such as the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydranencephaly</li>\n<li>Severe micrencephaly </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Moro reflex<strong> </strong>in different stages of <strong>hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy</strong> (HIE):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage I HIE (mild): Brisk</li>\n<li>Stage II HIE (moderate): Weak/incomplete</li>\n<li><strong>Stage III HIE (severe): Absent</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: In<strong> </strong>kernicterus, there is a deposition of unconjugated bilirubin in the basal ganglia and brain stem. Hence, the Moro reflex is absent </p>\n<p>Option C: In stage III HIE, the muscles are flaccid and the infant is comatose. Thus, the Moro reflex is absent. </p>\n<p>Option D: Erb's palsy results from an injury to the brachial plexus. So, the reflex is absent on the side of injury as the nerve supplying the muscles involved (efferent of the reflex) is affected. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4251","text":"You are demonstrating find​ings in a pediatric OSCE ​examination on a 4-day ol‌d neonate. On turning the‍ baby's head to the left,​ you obtain the finding a​s shown below. At what ag‌e does this reflex normal‍ly disappear?","raw_text":"You are demonstrating findings in a pediatric OSCE examination on a 4-day old neonate. On turning the baby's head to the left, you obtain the finding as shown below. At what age does this reflex normally disappear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"9 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 mo​nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/495eea847b8e47fda4229743cb6b99bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8069ed111fbb42949d55c000fb39a8e5x1280x2546.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6 months</strong></p><p><strong>Asymmetric tonic neck reflex</strong> (ATNR) disappears by 6<strong> months postnatally.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>tonic neck reflex</strong> is produced by manually rotating the infant’s head to 1 side and observing for the characteristic <strong>fencing posture </strong>(extension of the arm on the side to which the face is rotated and flexion of the contralateral arm).</p>\n<p>An <strong>obligatory tonic neck response</strong>, in which the infant becomes <strong>“stuck”</strong> in the<strong> fencing posture</strong>, is always abnormal and implies a <strong>CNS disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4237","text":"A first-time mother rushes​ to the OPD with her 4-da​y old infant and worriedl‌y points to the rash that‍ appears as shown below. ​You reassure her of its h​armless, transient nature‌. On microscopic examinat‍ion, these papular lesion​s on an erythematous base​ will reveal ________","raw_text":"A first-time mother rushes to the OPD with her 4-day old infant and worriedly points to the rash that appears as shown below. You reassure her of its harmless, transient nature.  On microscopic examination, these papular lesions on an erythematous base will reveal ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eosin​ophils","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr​ophils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giant​ Cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph​ocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Eosinophils","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5f7bf29f101411f88018fd06cb0c233.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Eosinophils</strong></p><p>The given clinical complex describes a case of <strong>erythema neonatorum</strong>. <strong>Wright stained smear</strong> of the contents of the pustule will show an <strong>abundance of eosinophils</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are papular lesions on an erythematous base, distributed over the trunk and face predominantly. They are seen 1-3 days after birth and may persist for up to a week, after which they resolve spontaneously.</p>\n<p>Neutrophils are seen in pustular melanosis. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0962","text":"A 5-month-old infant is br​ought to the OPD for a we​ll-baby check-up. A lesio‌n that was present since ‍birth is noticed as given​ below. What is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 5-month-old infant is brought to the OPD for a well-baby check-up. A lesion that was present since birth is noticed as given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermal mel​anocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Salmon​ patch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neonatal pustular melanosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Harlequin c​olour change","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dermal melanocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c14817ae1bb4aa9a812dda0489286fa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dermal melanocytosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>Mongolian spots </strong>or<strong> dermal melanocytosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Mongolian spots are <strong>benign blue</strong> or <strong>slate-grey macular</strong> lesions, with <strong>clearly defined margins</strong>, that appear over the presacral region (most common), posterior thighs, legs, back, and forearms.</p>\n<p>These spots occur due to the presence of <strong>melanin-containing melanocytes</strong> in the dermis, which is arrested during the migration from <strong>neural crest cells</strong> to the epidermis. They are <strong>non-tender</strong> and do not rapidly change in color or size.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dw-RA_0z","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"1_cellular_genetics__adaptations_and_injury","title":"1.Cellular Genetics, Adaptations And Injury","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA3170","text":"How many base pairs the hu‌man genome is made of?","raw_text":"How many base pairs the human genome is made of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3.6 b‌illion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3.2 b‌illion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7.2 b‌illion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4.2 b‌illion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3.2 billion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3.2 billion</strong></p><p><strong>The human genome </strong>is the complete set of nucleic acid sequences for humans and is made up of <strong>3.2 billion base pairs.</strong></p>\n<p>It is encoded as DNA within the <strong>23 chromosomes </strong>(haploid).</p>\n<p>In 2007, a multinational project called Encyclopedia of DNA Elements (<strong>ENCODE</strong>)<strong> </strong>concluded that about 80% of the human genome does not code for the proteins. However, they allow for the binding of proteins or are involved in the regulation of gene expression, in a cell type-specific fashion. They are known as <strong>functional non-protein-coding genes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0080","text":"Which of the following des‌cribes the non-coding reg‌ulatory RNAs?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes the non-coding regulatory RNAs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transcribed from protein-c‌oding regions of DNA and ‌translated to regulatory ​proteins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transcribed from protein-c‌oding regions of DNA but ‌never translated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transcribed from non-prote‌in-coding regions of DNA ‌and translated to regulat​ory proteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transcribed from non-prote‌in-coding regions of DNA ‌but never translated","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transcribed from non-protein-coding regions of DNA but never translated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transcribed from non-protein-coding regions of DNA but never translated</strong></p><p>The <strong>non-coding regulatory</strong> RNAs are the <strong>functional</strong> <strong>non-protein-coding </strong>regions of the DNA, which are <strong>transcribed</strong> to <strong>RNA</strong> but are <strong>never translated</strong> into proteins.</p>\n<p>The two important types of non-coding regulatory RNAs are the <strong>micro RNA</strong> (miRNA) and the long <strong>non-coding RNAs</strong> (lncRNAs). These RNAs play an important role in the <strong>regulation</strong> of <strong>gene expression.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0082","text":"What is the study of herit‌able changes in gene expr‌ession that are not cause​d by alterations in DNA s​equence?","raw_text":"What is the study of heritable changes in gene expression that are not caused by alterations in DNA sequence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geno‌mics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phylog‌enetics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epige‌netics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Molecular‌ genetics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epigenetics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epigenetics</strong></p><p><strong>Epigenetics </strong>is the branch of molecular biology that deals with the study of<strong> heritable changes</strong> in gene function <strong>not involving</strong> changes in the <strong>DNA</strong> <strong>sequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>These heritable changes involve factors that mediate processes such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Histone organization </li>\n<li>DNA methylation </li>\n<li>Chromatin organization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These in turn regulate the genomic organization and gene expression in a cell-type-specific fashion. The <strong>dysregulation </strong>of these<strong> epigenomic factors</strong> plays a major role in the development of <strong>malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Green tea catechins</strong> have been reported to <strong>modulate</strong> <strong>epigenetic</strong> <strong>processes.</strong> They can reverse DNA methylation of tumor suppressor genes and increase the transcription of these genes.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3172","text":"Micro-RNA are involved dur‌ing which of the followin‌g stages?","raw_text":"Micro-RNA are involved during which of the following stages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-trans‌criptional","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post trans‌criptional","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Post tran‌slational","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repli‌cation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Post transcriptional","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Post transcriptional</strong></p><p><strong>Micro-RNAs</strong> (miRNA)<strong> </strong>act during the <strong>post-transcriptional </strong>stage.</p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>non-coding</strong> RNA. The primary function of miRNA is to <strong>modulate</strong> the translation of <strong>target mRNAs</strong> into their corresponding proteins. After the target RNA is transcribed from its gene (post-transcriptional), the miRNA may either <strong>induce mRNA cleavage</strong> or <strong>repress</strong> its translation. This is referred to as <strong>gene silencing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Small interfering RNAs (<strong>siRNAs</strong>) are short RNA sequences that can be <strong>introduced</strong> <strong>experimentally</strong> into <strong>cells</strong>. They have <strong>functions</strong> <strong>similar</strong> to <strong>endogenous</strong> <strong>miRNA</strong> (serve as substrates for<strong> </strong>the Dicer enzyme and interact with the RNA-induced silencing complex). They may have a therapeutic role in silencing certain oncogenes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4057","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about mi‌croRNAs?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about microRNAs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are c‌oding RNAs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They are involved in cell ‌differentiation and carci‌nogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mature forms of microRNAs ‌are single stranded","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They can suppress tumor su‌ppressor genes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. They are coding RNAs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They are coding RNAs</strong></p><p><strong>MicroRNAs </strong>(miRNAs) are small <strong>noncoding</strong>, single-stranded RNAs. They are approximately 22 nucleotides in length.</p>\n<p>MicroRNAs mediate <strong>inhibition</strong> of messenger RNA (<strong>mRNA</strong>) <strong>translation</strong> through the action of the RNA-induced silencing complex (<strong>RISC</strong>). They control normal cell growth, differentiation, and cell survival. miRNA can be of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tumour-suppressive </strong>miRNAs - decrease the translation of oncogenic mRNAs </li>\n<li><strong>Onco-</strong>miRNAs<strong> </strong>- suppress tumor-suppressor genes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other<strong> noncoding RNAs </strong>include<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>piwi-interacting RNAs<strong> (piRNAs) </strong>- involved in post-transcriptional gene silencing</li>\n<li><strong>snoRNAs</strong> - involved in maturation of rRNA, assembly of ribosomes and noncoding RNAs</li>\n<li><strong>lncRNAs </strong>-<strong> </strong>regulate the activity of chromatin 'writers', which modify histones and control gene expression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2548","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of cell injury?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of cell injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyp‌oxia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immunologi‌c reactions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drug-i‌nduced","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nutritiona‌l imbalance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypoxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypoxia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoxia</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>cell injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of cell injury include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n<li>Physical agents</li>\n<li>Chemical agents and drugs</li>\n<li>Infectious agents</li>\n<li>Immunologic reactions</li>\n<li>Genetic abnormalities</li>\n<li>Nutritional imbalances</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mechanism<strong> </strong>of <strong>hypoxic</strong> cell injury is <strong>reduced</strong> <strong>oxidative</strong> <strong>respiration</strong>. The most common cause of hypoxia is <strong>ischemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypoxia-inducible factor 1 (<strong>HIF-1</strong>) is a transcription factor with <strong>protective </strong>effects against hypoxic injury such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Promotion of blood vessel formation</li>\n<li>Stimulation of cell-survival pathways</li>\n<li>Enhancement of anaerobic glycolysis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cells most <strong>sensitive</strong> to hypoxia are <strong>neurons</strong>. The most sensitive neurons include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus (especially, area <strong>CA1, </strong>also known as <strong>Sommer’s sector</strong>)</li>\n<li>Cerebellar Purkinje cells</li>\n<li>Pyramidal neurons in the cerebral cortex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cells most <strong>resistant</strong> to hypoxia are <strong>fibroblasts.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0099","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophy is a type of rever‌sible cell injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma membrane blebbing o‌ccurs in reversible cell ‌injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"On removal of stimulus, ne‌crosis can be reversed","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic sublethal stimulus‌ can cause cellular aging‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. On removal of stimulus, necrosis can be reversed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ddb5866ef82442eaaba7addee55d35b2x1280x1140.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. On removal of stimulus, necrosis can be reversed</strong></p><p>On removal of stimulus, <strong>necrosis</strong> cannot be reversed. It is an <strong>irreversible</strong> cell injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Cellular adaptations</strong> are the <strong>reversible</strong> responses where the cells achieve an altered steady state in response to physiologic or some pathologic stimuli. They can be reversed if the stimulus causing stress or injury is removed. These cellular adaptations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertrophy</li>\n<li>Hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Atrophy</li>\n<li>Metaplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chronic sublethal stimuli</strong> are unable to cause cell death but may lead to certain changes in cells, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intracellular accumulations</li>\n<li>Pathological calcifications</li>\n<li><strong>Cellular aging</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2549","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a feature of rever‌sible cell injury?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a feature of reversible cell injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large amorphous densities ‌in the mitochondria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in ATP generation‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Swelling of the endoplasmi‌c reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Detachment of ribosomes fr‌om granular ER","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Large amorphous densities in the mitochondria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85c52580589141578633fb6dab1f578fx1280x1140.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Large amorphous densities in the mitochondria</strong></p><p><strong>Small amorphous densities</strong> are seen in the mitochondria in <strong>reversible</strong> <strong>cell injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following changes occur in reversible cell injury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cellular swelling</strong> - earliest change<br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ATP</strong> <strong>depletion</strong> leads to sodium-potassium pump failure</li>\n<li>Na and water accumulate within the cell causing cellular swelling</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Fatty change -<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Seen in cells involved in or dependent on fat metabolism (liver hepatocytes and myocardial cells)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Light microscopy - no noticeable changes</li>\n<li>Ultrastructural findings -\n<ul>\n<li>Mitochondrial swelling and small amorphous densities</li>\n<li>Plasma membrane blebs</li>\n<li><strong>Swelling</strong> of the endoplasmic reticulum (<strong>ER</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Detachment</strong> of <strong>ribosomes</strong> from granular ER</li>\n<li>Nuclear changes like disaggregation of granular and fibrillar filaments</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The formation of <strong>large amorphous densities</strong> in the mitochondria is a feature of <strong>irreversible injury</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6758","text":"Which is the most importan‌t light microscopic featu‌re of irreversible cell i​njury?","raw_text":"Which is the most important light microscopic feature of irreversible cell injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amorphous densities in mit‌ochondria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cell membrane blebs and lo‌ss of microvilli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ribosomal detachment from ‌endoplasmic reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclear changes like pykno‌sis and karyolysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nuclear changes like pyknosis and karyolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nuclear changes like pyknosis and karyolysis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Nuclear changes like <strong>pyknosis</strong>, <strong>karyorrhexis</strong>, and <strong>karyolysis</strong> are features of <strong>irreversible</strong> cell injury visible on <strong>light microscopy</strong>.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The ultrastructural findings visible on <strong>electron microscopy</strong> include:<strong> </strong></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Large</strong> <strong>amorphous </strong>floccular densities in mitochondria</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Cell membrane blebs and loss of microvilli</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Ribosomal detachment from endoplasmic reticulum</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0599","text":"Thrombosis is initiated by‌:","raw_text":"Thrombosis is initiated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet ‌activation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endotheli‌al injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coagulati‌on cascade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasoconstriction of vessel‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endothelial injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endothelial injury</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombosis</strong> is initiated by<strong> endothelial injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thrombosis is is the formation of a <strong>blood clot</strong> inside a blood vessel, <strong>obstructing</strong> the flow of blood through the circulatory system. </p>\n<p>Three primary abnormalities, often referred to as <strong>Virchow</strong> <strong>triad,</strong> are known to induce thrombosis. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endothelial injury<strong> - </strong>most important factor</li>\n<li><strong>Stasis</strong> or turbulent blood flow</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercoagulability</strong> of the blood</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0100","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about cellular ad‌aptations is correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about cellular adaptations is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperplasia involves synth‌esis of intracellular com‌ponents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophy is defined as decr‌ease in cell size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrophy is the decrease in‌ number and size of cells‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metaplasia is an irreversi‌ble replacement of a cell‌ by another cell type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atrophy is the decrease in number and size of cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atrophy is the decrease in number and size of cells</strong></p><p><strong>Atrophy</strong> is a reduction in the size of an organ or tissue due to a <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>cell size</strong> and <strong>number</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cellular adaptations</strong> are <strong>reversible</strong> changes in the size, number, phenotype, metabolic activity, or functions of cells in response to changes in their environment. There are 4 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypertrophy - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Increase in organ size due to an increase in the<strong> s</strong>ize of cells</li>\n<li>Involves <strong>synthesis</strong> and assembly of <strong>intracellular</strong> structural <strong>components</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperplasia -</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Increase in organ size due to an <strong>increase </strong>in the<strong> number of cells</strong></li>\n<li>It is due to division and proliferation of stem cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Atrophy - </strong>reduction in organ size due to a <strong>decrease </strong>in<strong> cell size and number</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Metaplasia - </strong>one differentiated <strong>cell type</strong> (epithelial or mesenchymal) is <strong>replaced </strong>by another cell type</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2544","text":"A patient with long-standi‌ng hypertension develops ‌left ventricular hypertro​phy. Which of the followi​ng transcription factors ​do not play a role in thi​s?","raw_text":"A patient with long-standing hypertension develops left ventricular hypertrophy. Which of the following transcription factors do not play a role in this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GA‌TA4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NF‌AT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ME‌F2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌NF","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HNF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da5cfe5b914a411481bdc3af51988d68x1280x1750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HNF</strong></p><p><strong>HNF</strong> is a transcription factor involved in the development of maturity-onset diabetes of the young (<strong>MODY</strong>), not the development of cardiac hypertrophy.</p>\n<p>The transcription factors involved in <strong>cardiac muscle hypertrophy</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>GATA4</strong> – hypertrophic stimuli result in enhanced DNA-binding activity of GATA-4</li>\n<li><strong>MEF2</strong>- increases due to pressure and volume overload</li>\n<li><strong>NFAT </strong>(Nuclear Factor for Activated T-cells)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2543","text":"How does myosin change dur‌ing muscle hypertrophy?","raw_text":"How does myosin change during muscle hypertrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"From α-heavy chain to β-he‌avy chain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"From β-heavy chain to α-he‌avy chain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"From β-light chain to α-li‌ght chain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"From α-light chain to β-li‌ght chain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. From α-heavy chain to β-heavy chain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. From α-heavy chain to β-heavy chain</strong></p><p>During <strong>muscle hypertrophy</strong>, the <strong>α</strong>-<strong>isoform </strong>of the myosin heavy chain is replaced by the<strong> β-isoform. </strong>It produces slower and more energetically economic contraction.</p>\n<p>During hypertrophy, there may occur a switch of contractile proteins from the adult isoforms to the <strong>fetal </strong>or<strong> infantile</strong> <strong>isoforms</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2541","text":"Both physiological hyperpl‌asia and hypertrophy are ‌simultaneously seen in wh​ich of the following indi​viduals?","raw_text":"Both physiological hyperplasia and hypertrophy are simultaneously seen in which of the following individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 24-year-old man vigorous‌ly training for an athlet‌ic race","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 35-year-old woman regula‌rly taking combined oral ‌contraceptive pills","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 50-year-old man with pro‌liferation of liver cells‌ following transplantatio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 24-year-old pregnant wom‌an at 22 weeks of gestati‌on","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 24-year-old pregnant woman at 22 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 24-year-old pregnant woman at 22 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>Physiological <strong>hypertrophy</strong> and <strong>hyperplasia</strong> can be <strong>simultaneously</strong> seen in the <strong>uterus </strong>during<strong> pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In early pregnancy, uterine growth is due to <strong>hyperplasia</strong> of uterine smooth muscle cells; however, the predominant growth of the uterus during pregnancy occurs after mid-gestation, by way of stretch‐induced myometrial <strong>hypertrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>Simultaneous physiological hypertrophy and hyperplasia also occur in the <strong>female breast </strong>during<strong> puberty </strong>and<strong> pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Enlargement of skeletal muscles in athletes is an example of physiological hypertrophy.</p>\n<p>Option B: Combined oral contraceptive pills causing endometrial hyperplasia is an example of pathological hyperplasia.</p>\n<p>Option C: Following transplantation of a liver lobe, the remaining liver cells proliferate so that the organ grows back to its original size (compensatory hyperplasia).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2542","text":"Which among the following ‌do not usually undergo hy‌perplasia?","raw_text":"Which among the following do not usually undergo hyperplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeletal m‌uscle only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeletal and‌ heart muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heart and s‌mooth muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skeletal and smooth muscle‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Skeletal and heart muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Skeletal and heart muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Skeletal muscle</strong> and <strong>heart muscle </strong>usually undergo <strong>hypertrophy, </strong>not hyperplasia.</p>\n<p>Examples of<strong> physiologic hyperplasia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female breast during puberty and pregnancy (hormonal hyperplasia)</li>\n<li>Uterine smooth muscle during early pregnancy</li>\n<li>Compensatory hyperplasia of liver cells after partial resection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples of<strong> pathologic hyperplasia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endometrial hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Benign prostatic hyperplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2545","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of physiologic‌al atrophy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of physiological atrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophy of the legs of a b‌edridden patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophy of the arm muscles‌ following a nerve injury‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle wasting in a child ‌suffering from marasmus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine atrophy after deli‌very by Cesarean-section","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Uterine atrophy after delivery by Cesarean-section","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Uterine atrophy after delivery by Cesarean-section</strong></p><p><strong>Uterine atrophy</strong> following delivery is an example of <strong>physiological atrophy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Atrophy</strong> is defined as a <strong>reduction</strong> in the size of an organ or tissue due to a decrease in cell size and number.</p>\n<p>The following mechanisms are involved in atrophy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased protein synthesis due to decreased metabolic activity</li>\n<li>Increased destruction of proteins by the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The various types of atrophy are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px;\" width=\"594\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px; text-align: center;\">Stimulus/type of atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px; text-align: center;\">Example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 289px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Physiologic atrophy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Loss of hormonal stimulation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">\n<p>Atrophy of the uterus after parturition</p>\n<p>Atrophy of the endometrium, vaginal epithelium, and breast after menopause</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">During embryonic development</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Certain structures, like notochord and thyroglossal duct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 289px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pathologic atrophy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Disuse atrophy (decreased workload)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Atrophy in a limb that has been immobilized following a fracture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Denervation atrophy (loss of nerve supply)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Skeletal muscle atrophy following nerve injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Ischemic atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Senile atrophy of the brain and heart due to atherosclerosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Inadequate nutrition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Severe muscle wasting in marasmus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Pressure atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Atrophy of surrounding uninvolved tissues caused by an enlarging benign tumor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2546","text":"The cross-section of the b‌rain of an 84-year-old ma‌n with dyslipidemia is gi​ven below, in comparison ​with a normal brain. Whic​h of the following is the​ most likely pathological‍ change?","raw_text":"The cross-section of the brain of an 84-year-old man with dyslipidemia is given below, in comparison with a normal brain. Which of the following is the most likely pathological change?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malnutriti‌on atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pressure‌ atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denervati‌on atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ischemic‌ atrophy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ischemic atrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90c0eb3470cf4fc6b9c372050effb0f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ischemic atrophy</strong></p><p>The given image shows narrowing of gyri and widening of sulci in a senile brain, which is suggestive of <strong>senile atrophy.</strong> It is due to a gradual decrease in blood supply as a result of atherosclerosis, known as <strong>ischemic atrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>The various types of pathological atrophy are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px;\" width=\"594\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 289px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pathologic atrophy</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Disuse atrophy (decreased workload)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Atrophy in a limb that has been immobilized following a fracture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Denervation atrophy (loss of nerve supply)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Skeletal muscle atrophy following nerve injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Ischemic atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Senile atrophy of the brain and heart due to atherosclerosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Inadequate nutrition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Severe muscle wasting in marasmus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Pressure atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289px;\">Atrophy of surrounding uninvolved tissues caused by an enlarging benign tumor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2547","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of m‌etaplasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is  the most common type of metaplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Columnar to squamous epith‌elium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous to columnar epith‌elium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous to transitional e‌pithelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Columnar to transitional e‌pithelium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Columnar to squamous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73e97e769e5545268ff32cf3e0f71f2dx1280x938.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Columnar to squamous epithelium</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common type</strong> of metaplasia is <strong>squamous</strong> <strong>metaplasia</strong>, in which the normal <strong>columnar</strong> cells are <strong>replaced by</strong> <strong>squamous</strong> epithelial cells.</p>\n<p> The table below shows various types of metaplasia:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 497.516px; height: 1064px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 22px;\">Tissue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 22px;\">Metaplasia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px; height: 22px;\">Cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 52px;\">Airways</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 52px;\"><strong>Squamous metaplasia</strong>: Normal pseudostratified epithelium is replaced by the squamous epithelium. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px; height: 52px;\">\n<p>Chronic smoking,</p>\n<p>Deficiency of retinoic acid</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 71px;\">Urinary bladder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Squamous metaplasia</strong>: Normal transitional epithelium is replaced by squamous epithelium.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px; height: 71px;\">Bladder stones </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 71px;\">Esophagus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Columnar metaplasia: </strong>Normal squamous epithelium is replaced by the glandular columnar epithelium <strong>(Barrett's esophagus)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px; height: 71px;\">Chronic GERD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 71px;\">Cervix</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Squamous metaplasia</strong>: Normal glandular epithelium may be replaced by the squamous epithelium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px; height: 71px;\">Low pH of the cervix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 71px;\">Muscle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Connective tissue metaplasia:</strong> Formation of bone in the muscle tissue; (<strong>myositis ossificans)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px; height: 71px;\">Following intramuscular hemorrhage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>GERD: Gastroesophageal reflux disease</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0589","text":"The lining epithelium of t‌he respiratory tract of a‌ middle-aged man who is a​ chronic smoker shows a c​hange from columnar to st​ratified squamous epithel​ium, as depicted in the i‍mage below. The change is​ called:","raw_text":"The lining epithelium of the respiratory tract of a middle-aged man who is a chronic smoker shows a change from columnar to stratified squamous epithelium, as depicted in the image below. The change is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysp‌lasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyper‌trophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atr‌ophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metap‌lasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metaplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/255c0fac25734eb583c7435bae4ece13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metaplasia</strong></p><p>The change described in the question is called <strong>metaplasia</strong>. It is defined as a <strong>reversible</strong> change in which one type of differentiated cell is <strong>replaced</strong> by another type.</p>\n<p>The table below shows various types of metaplasia:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 497.516px; height: 1064px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Tissue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\">Metaplasia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px;\">Cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Airways</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\"><strong>Squamous metaplasia</strong>: Normal pseudostratified epithelium is replaced by the squamous epithelium. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px;\">\n<p>Chronic smoking,</p>\n<p>Deficiency of retinoic acid</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Urinary bladder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\"><strong>Squamous metaplasia</strong>: Normal transitional epithelium is replaced by squamous epithelium.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px;\">Bladder stones </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Esophagus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\"><strong>Columnar metaplasia</strong>: Normal squamous epithelium is replaced by the glandular columnar epithelium (<strong>Barret's esophagus</strong>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px;\">Chronic GERD </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Cervix</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\"><strong>Squamous metaplasia</strong>: Normal glandular epithelium may be replaced by the squamous epithelium.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px;\">Low pH of the cervix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Muscle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\"><strong>Connective tissue metaplasia:</strong> Formation of bone in the muscle tissue; (<strong>myositis ossificans).</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.516px;\">After intramuscular hemorrhage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>GERD: Gastroesophageal reflux disease</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6756","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is an example of ‌connective tissue metapla​sia?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is an example of connective tissue metaplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myositis ‌ossificans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dystrophic c‌alcification","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atheroscler‌otic plaques","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kel‌oid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myositis ossificans","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myositis ossificans</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Myositis ossificans</strong> is an example of <strong>connective tissue metaplasia</strong>.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">It refers to the formation of cartilage, bone, or adipose tissue (mesenchymal tissues) in tissues that normally do not contain these elements. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Myositis ossificans is characterised by bone formation within muscle tissue following traumatic injury to the area. However, this type of metaplasia is less clearly seen as an adaptive response</span>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6757","text":"Deficiency in which of the‌ following vitamins can l‌ead to metaplastic transf​ormation in the lung?","raw_text":"Deficiency in which of the following vitamins can lead to metaplastic transformation in the lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‌min A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‌min B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‌min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‌min E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin A</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The deficiency of <strong>vitamin A</strong> (retinoic acid) induces<strong> squamous metaplasia</strong> in the <strong>respiratory</strong> <strong>epithelium</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3180","text":"Anaplasia ‌refers to:","raw_text":"Anaplasia refers to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lack of dif‌ferentiation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Variation in cell size and‌ shape","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Replacement of one type of‌ cell by other","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disordered arrangement of ‌tumor cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lack of differentiation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lack of differentiation</strong></p><p><strong>Anaplasia</strong> refers to a <strong>lack of differentiation.</strong></p>\n<p>The term anaplasia means to form backward implying a <strong>reversal</strong> of <strong>differentiation</strong> to a more <strong>primitive </strong>level. The extent to which neoplastic cells resemble normal cells, both morphologically and functionally, is called differentiation.</p>\n<p>Anaplasia is an important morphological feature of <strong>malignant</strong> transformation.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 277\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The morphological features of malignancy include:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Pleomorphism</strong> - variation in the size and shape of cells</li>\n<li>Increased <strong>N: C ratio</strong> (nucleus: cytoplasmic ratio) - around 1:4 in normal cells, increases to 1:1 in malignant cells</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperchromasia</strong> - coarsely clumped and darkly stained chromatin within irregular nuclei</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> <strong>mitosis</strong> - atypical, bizarre mitotic figures</li>\n<li>Loss of polarity - sheets or large masses of tumor cells grow in an <strong>anarchic</strong>, <strong>disorganized</strong> fashion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nfwpJYnZ","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"1_pharmacokinetics","title":"1.Pharmacokinetics","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA4404","text":"Fundoscopic evaluation in ​an HIV patient showed CMV​ retinitis. He was starte‌d on intravenous foscarne‌t, a highly ionized drug.‌ Which of the following s‍tatements is true about t​he drug?","raw_text":"Fundoscopic evaluation in an HIV patient showed CMV retinitis. He was started on intravenous foscarnet, a highly ionized drug. Which of the following statements is true about the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excreted mainly by the kid​neys","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Can cross the blood brain ​barrier easily","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Well absorbed from the int​estine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accumulates in the cellula​r lipids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Excreted mainly by the kidneys","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Excreted mainly by the kidneys</strong></p><p><strong>Highly ionized drugs</strong> like foscarnet are mainly <strong>excreted</strong> by the <strong>kidneys</strong>. </p>\n<p>They are <strong>not reabsorbed</strong> from the renal tubules and are hence <strong>excreted unchanged </strong>by glomerular filtration. <strong>Dose reduction</strong> is <strong>necessary</strong> in renal impairment to avoid the accumulation of the drug and associated toxicity. </p>\n<p>The <strong>non-ionized</strong> forms of weak acids and bases undergo tubular <strong>reabsorption</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B: The blood-brain barrier limits the entry of ionized, non-lipid soluble drugs.</p>\n<p>Option C: A drug is more readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract when it is in its unionized, more lipophilic form.</p>\n<p>Option D: As the ionized form has low lipid solubility, it cannot penetrate cell membranes easily.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4484","text":"Alkalinization of the urin​e is done in case of toxi​city with which of the fo‌llowing substances?","raw_text":"Alkalinization of the urine is done in case of toxicity with which of the following substances?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbi​turate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hep​arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morp​hine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Barbiturate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Barbiturate</strong></p><p><strong>Alkalinization</strong> of the<strong> urine</strong> is done in <strong>barbiturate poisoning</strong>. When the urine is alkaline, weakly acidic drugs like barbiturates are present in <strong>highly ionized</strong> form and <strong>excreted</strong> more<strong> rapidly.</strong></p>\n<p>When the tubular urine is made more alkaline, weak acids like barbiturates become highly ionized and are excreted to a greater extent. Similarly, acidification of urine increases the excretion of weak bases. This property is used in the management of drug overdose.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Treatment of overdose</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak acids<br/>(e.g., phenobarbital, aspirin)</td>\n<td>Alkalinization of urine with <strong>sodium bicarbonate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak bases<br/>(e.g., amphetamine)</td>\n<td>Acidification of urine with <strong>ammonium chloride</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3684","text":"When the pH = pKa of a dru​g, which of the following​ statements is true?","raw_text":"When the pH  = pKa of a drug, which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Concentration of the drug ​is 50% ionic and 50 % non​-ionic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Concentration of the drug ​is 90% ionic and 10% non-​ionic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Concentration of the drug ​is 75% ionic and 25 % non​-ionic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concentration of the drug ​is 25% ionic and 75 % non​-ionic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Concentration of the drug is 50% ionic and 50 % non-ionic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Concentration of the drug is 50% ionic and 50 % non-ionic</strong></p><p>When the <strong>pH = pKa</strong> of a drug, the <strong>concentration</strong> of the drug is <strong>50% ionic</strong> and <strong>50% non-ionic.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>pKa </strong>is defined as the<strong> pH at which a drug</strong> exists as<strong> 50% ionized </strong>(hydrophilic) and<strong> 50% unionized </strong>(lipophilic).</p>\n<p>For e.g., phenobarbitone is an acidic drug with a pKa of 7.2. At a pH of 7.2, 50% will be unionized. In a more basic plasma (pH=7.4), a lesser fraction (around 40%) will be unionized.</p>\n<p><strong>Small changes</strong> in <strong>pH near pKa</strong> of a drug may cause <strong>large variations</strong> in the <strong>degree</strong> of <strong>ionization.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4461","text":"In a patient with nephroti​c syndrome, drug toxicity​ is least likely with whi‌ch of the following drugs‌?","raw_text":"In a patient with nephrotic syndrome, drug toxicity is least likely with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Efav​irenz","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Morp​hine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diaz​epam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valp​roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Morphine</strong></p><p>In a patient with <strong>nephrotic syndrome,</strong> drug <strong>toxicity</strong> is <strong>least</strong> likely with <strong>morphine</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Acidic drugs</strong> generally <strong>bind</strong> to <strong>plasma albumin </strong>while<strong> </strong>basic drugs bind to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. <strong>Nephrotic</strong> syndrome reduces plasma albumin levels, therefore there is a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>protein-bound</strong> drug <strong>fraction</strong> and an <strong>increase</strong> in the <strong>free form,</strong> resulting in drug <strong>toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>This is less likely with drugs having low plasma protein binding such as morphine (only around 35%).</p>\n<p>Efavirenz, diazepam, and valproate have a very high plasma protein binding of &gt;90% and predominantly bind to albumin and are associated with toxicity in hypoalbuminemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4453","text":"What does time for peak pl​asma concentration (Tmax)​ indicate?","raw_text":"What does time for peak plasma concentration (Tmax) indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rate of e​limination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rate of a​bsorption","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Duration ​of effect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intensity​ of effect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rate of absorption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rate of absorption</strong></p><p>The time for peak plasma concentration (<strong>Tmax</strong>) indicates the <strong>rate</strong> at which the drug is <strong>absorbed</strong> from the administered site. It depends on both the administered site and the drug formulation.</p>\n<p>This is used to <strong>determine</strong> the <strong>timing</strong> of the <strong>administration of hypnotics</strong> with respect to sleep timings. A hypnotic with a <strong>higher rate</strong> of <strong>absorption</strong> might be preferred to <strong>induce sleep</strong> at a <strong>shorter latency</strong> as the <strong>target concentration</strong> can be <strong>achieved earlier.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0928","text":"The pharmacokinetics of an​ experimental drug is bei​ng studied. If its appare‌nt volume of distribution‌ is 6L/kg, which of the f‌ollowing is true regardin‍g its distribution?","raw_text":"The pharmacokinetics of an experimental drug is being studied. If its apparent volume of distribution is 6L/kg, which of the following is true regarding its distribution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Highly bound to plasma pro​teins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Confined to vascular compa​rtment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sequestered in body tissue​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Distributed in extracellul​ar fluid volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sequestered in body tissues","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sequestered in body tissues</strong></p><p>In the present case, the volume of distribution of the drug is 6L/kg (420 L in a normal adult with reference weight 70kg) which is very high when compared to the total body water of 42L. Therefore, it can be implied that the <strong>drug</strong> is having <strong>high sequestration in tissues</strong>.</p>\n<p>The apparent volume of distribution (<strong>V<sub>d</sub></strong>) is an <strong>imaginary volume</strong> throughout which the <strong>drug</strong> is <strong>distributed</strong> if the <strong>plasma concentration</strong> remains <strong>constant</strong> throughout. </p>\n<p><strong>V<sub>d</sub> = Dose administered IV/ plasma concentration</strong></p>\n<p>The difference in the volume of distribution based on drug type:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low</strong> <strong>V<sub>d </sub></strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Restricted to the <strong>intravascular</strong> compartment </li>\n<li>Seen in drugs with high molecular weight (e.g., heparin) or <strong>high protein binding</strong> (e.g., warfarin - 9.8L)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Medium</strong> <strong>V<sub>d </sub></strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lipid insoluble drugs (e.g., gentamicin - 20L) do not enter the cells and are distributed in the extracellular fluid volume</li>\n<li>Small water-soluble molecules (e.g., ethanol - 42L) are distributed in the total body water</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>High Vd</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Sequestered and accumulated in the tissues</li>\n<li>Small lipophilic molecules (e.g., chloroquine - 13000L)  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5333","text":"Which of the following par​ameters is used to assess​ the extent of drug absor‌ption in a plasma concent‌ration vs. time graph?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters is used to assess the extent of drug absorption in a plasma concentration vs. time graph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tm​ax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cm​ax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T1​/2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Area under​ the curve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Area under the curve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a222c72167244bbba5ca5dcfefd1cf00x1280x1332.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Area under the curve</strong></p><p>The<strong> area under the curve</strong> of <strong>plasma concentration vs. time curve</strong> gives the <strong>extent of absorption </strong>which is calculated by the <strong>trapezoidal rule. </strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows a plasma concentration vs. time graph:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Two drugs may have the same rate of absorption but a different extent of absorption or bioavailability. </p>\n<p>The <strong>factors</strong> upon which the <strong>extent of drug absorption</strong> depends are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lipophilicity - If too lipophilic (e.g., acyclovir), the drug is not soluble enough to cross the water layer adjacent to the cell.</li>\n<li>Hydrophilicity - If too hydrophilic (e.g., atenolol), the drug cannot cross the lipid cell membrane. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4487","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about fir​st-order kinetics?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about first-order kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A constant amount of drug ​is eliminated in unit tim​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The half life increases wi​th an increase in dose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The rate of elimination is​ constant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The rate of elimination is​ proportional to the plas​ma concentration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The rate of elimination is proportional to the plasma concentration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The rate of elimination is proportional to the plasma concentration</strong></p><p>The <strong>rate</strong> of <strong>elimination</strong> is <strong>proportional</strong> to <strong>plasma concentration</strong> in <strong>first-order kinetics.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0929","text":"You would consider adminis​tering a loading dose for​ a patient if the drug ha‌s","raw_text":"You would consider administering a loading dose for a patient if the drug has","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High cl​earance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High volume of distributio​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Long plasm​a half life","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short plasm​a half life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High volume of distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High volume of distribution</strong></p><p>The <strong>volume of distribution</strong> of the drug <strong>determines the loading dose</strong> of the drug. </p>\n<p>Loading dose is a relatively large dose of the drug given initially to rapidly achieve the target plasma concentration. It is given by the formula </p>\n<p>Loading Dose = Vol. of Distribution x Desired Steady-state plasma Concentration.</p>\n<p>The <strong>bioavailability</strong> <strong>determines</strong> the <strong>route</strong> of administration. Though bioavailability may affect the dose necessary, it will not be the primary factor considered during the calculation of the loading dose.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Clearance is used to determine the maintenance dose. <br/>Maintenance dose = Clearance x Desired Steady-state plasma concentration.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Plasma <strong>half-life</strong> primarily <strong>determines</strong> the <strong>frequency</strong> of dosing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4488","text":"Which of the following dru​gs approach zero-order ki​netics at higher therapeu‌tic levels?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs approach zero-order kinetics at higher therapeutic levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penic​illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Valp​roate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenytoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenytoin</strong></p><p><strong>Phenytoin</strong> kinetics changes from <strong>first-order to zero-order</strong> kinetics at <strong>higher therapeutic</strong> levels.</p>\n<p><strong>True zero-order</strong> kinetics is shown only by <strong>ethanol</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some drugs have first-order kinetics at low plasma concentrations while at higher plasma concentration (within therapeutic range), the mechanism for elimination gets saturated and they change over to zero-order kinetics.</p>\n<p>Drugs that show <strong>early saturation kinetics</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Warfarin</li>\n<li>Aspirin</li>\n<li>Theophylline</li>\n<li>Verapamil</li>\n<li>Fluoxetine</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some drugs show zero-order kinetics at much higher (non-therapeutic / toxic) levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5334","text":"Which of the following rou​tes of administration wil​l not bypass hepatic firs‌t-pass metabolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following routes of administration will not bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subli​ngual","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Or​al","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intra​venous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trans​dermal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral</strong></p><p>Hepatic <strong>first-pass metabolism</strong> occurs in drugs administered via the <strong>oral</strong> route.</p>\n<p>When drugs are given orally, they are absorbed in the intestine and reach the liver through the <strong>portal vein</strong>. During this process, the drug is acted on by enzymes of the liver and intestine. Additionally, the liver may excrete part of the drug in the bile, further <strong>reducing</strong> the <strong>bioavailability</strong>. This is called the first-pass metabolism.</p>\n<p>In all the other routes, the drug directly enters systemic circulation without reaching the liver.</p>\n<p>Note: Bioavailability is the <strong>fraction </strong>of an administered dose of<strong> unchanged drug </strong>that reaches the<strong> systemic circulation</strong>. Hence, the <strong>bioavailability</strong> of a drug administered <strong>intravenously</strong> (I.V) is <strong>100%. </strong>It is neither subjected to the first-pass metabolism as in the oral route nor does it depend on local factors as in subcutaneous, intramuscular, or transdermal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4470","text":"In the metabolism of xenob​iotics, all of the follow​ing are phase 1 reactions‌ except?","raw_text":"In the metabolism of xenobiotics, all of the following are phase 1 reactions except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxid​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Redu​ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acety​lation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydro​lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetylation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetylation</strong></p><p><strong>Acetylation</strong> is a <strong>phase 2</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p>Metabolizing enzymes in hepatic cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microsomal enzymes - present in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum</li>\n<li>Non-microsomal enzymes - present in mitochondria and cytoplasm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4474","text":"Sulphonamides undergo whic​h of the following metabo​lic reactions?","raw_text":"Sulphonamides undergo which of the following metabolic reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acety​lation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methy​lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrox​ylation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxid​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acetylation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acetylation</strong></p><p><strong>Sulphonamides</strong> undergo <strong>acetylation</strong>. It is a <strong>phase II metabolic reaction</strong> where compounds having amino or hydrazine residues are conjugated with the help of acetyl coenzyme-A.</p>\n<p>Drugs that undergo acetylation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sulphonamides</li>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>PAS</li>\n<li>Dapsone</li>\n<li>Clonazepam</li>\n<li>Procainamide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4490","text":"All of the following can c​ause lupus-like syndromes​ except:","raw_text":"All of the following can cause lupus-like syndromes except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison​iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penic​illin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydra​lazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulpho​namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Penicillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Penicillin</strong></p><p>Penicillin is not associated with a lupus-like syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Drug-induced</strong> lupus is a consequence of <strong>genetic polymorphism</strong> of <em>N</em>-acetyl transferase 2 (<strong>NAT2</strong>) enzyme which results in slow acetylators and fast acetylators:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow acetylators</strong> of isoniazid, hydralazine, and sulphonamides can have <strong>increased toxicity</strong> that includes <strong>lupus-like syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fast acetylators</strong> can have <strong>decreased efficacy</strong> as a result of faster metabolism of the active drug</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> that may result in <strong>lupus-like syndrome</strong> (The <strong>SHIP MeCh</strong>ani<strong>Q </strong>has lupus-like syndrome) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ulphonamides/ Sulfasalazine</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ydralazine</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>soniazid</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>rocainamide, Para-aminosalicylic acid</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>ethyldopa</li>\n<li><strong> C</strong>hlorpromazine</li>\n<li><strong>Q</strong>uinidine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4480","text":"A patient on long-term ant​icoagulation therapy with​ warfarin would need an i‌ncreased dose when coadmi‌nistered with which of th‌e following drugs?","raw_text":"A patient on long-term anticoagulation therapy with warfarin would need an increased dose when coadministered with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capt​opril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythr​omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam​picin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cimet​idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rifampicin</strong></p><p>A patient on long-term anticoagulation therapy with <strong>warfarin</strong> would need an <strong>increased dose</strong> when coadministered with rifampicin. This is because <strong>rifampicin</strong> is a potent <strong>microsomal enzyme inducer </strong>and can decrease the effect of warfarin.</p>\n<p>Rifampicin induces CYP3A and CYP2D6 enzymes.</p>\n<p>Enzyme inducers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CYP3A\n<ul>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Phenobarbitone (CYP2B1)</li>\n<li>Rifampicin (CYP2D6)</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Glucocorticoids</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>CYP2E1\n<ul>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Chronic alcoholism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Isoniazid is an inducer for CYP2E1 but an inhibitor for CYP3A4</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4479","text":"Contraceptive failure whil​e on oral contraceptive p​ills is unlikely to occur‌ with which of the follow‌ing drugs?","raw_text":"Contraceptive failure while on oral contraceptive pills is unlikely to occur with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valp​roate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam​picin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenoba​rbitone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Valproate</strong></p><p><strong>Contraceptive failure</strong> in patients on the oral contraceptive pill is <strong>unlikely</strong> to occur with <strong>valproate.</strong> It is an <strong>enzyme inhibitor</strong> that inhibits the degradation of OCPs and <strong>increases</strong> their <strong>duration</strong> of <strong>action.</strong></p>\n<p>Rifampicin, phenytoin, and phenobarbitone are enzyme inducers and can cause increased metabolism of OCPs leading to contraceptive failure.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 150px;\" width=\"453\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180.547px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Enzyme inducers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.453px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Enzyme inhibitors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180.547px;\">\n<p>Mnemonic: CRAP GPS</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>arbamezepine</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ifampicin</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>lcohol (chronic)</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>henytoin</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>riseofulvin</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>henobarbitone</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ulfonylureas</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.453px;\">\n<p>Mnemonic: RED VICK</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>R</strong>itonavir</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>rythromycin</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iltiazem</li>\n<li><strong>V</strong>erapamil, Valproate</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>traconazole</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>larithromycin, Cimetidine</li>\n<li><strong>K</strong>etoconazole</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4482","text":"Which of the following mic​rosomal enzymes is import​ant for the activation of‌ procarcinogens?","raw_text":"Which of the following microsomal enzymes is important for the activation of procarcinogens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CYP​3A4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CYP2​C8/9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CYP​2C19","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CYP1​A1/2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CYP1A1/2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CYP1A1/2</strong></p><p><strong>CYP1A1/2 enzymes </strong>are important in the<strong> activation of procarcinogens.</strong></p>\n<p>They are involved in the metabolism of few drugs like theophylline, caffeine, paracetamol, and carbamazepine.</p>\n<p><strong>Inducers</strong> of <strong>CYP1A1/2:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polycyclic hydrocarbons</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>Charbroiled meat</li>\n<li>Rifampicin</li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4494","text":"Cytochrome enzymes are not​ involved in which of the​ following processes?","raw_text":"Cytochrome enzymes are not involved in which of the following processes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxid​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Production o​f bile acids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synthesis ​of steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acety​lation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acetylation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acetylation</strong></p><p>Cytochrome enzymes are not involved in acetylation. Cytochrome enzymes such as <strong>CYP450</strong> are involved in <strong>phase 1</strong> reactions such as <strong>oxidation</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are also involved in <strong>bile acid production</strong> from cholesterol; <strong>synthesis of steroids</strong> and fatty acid signaling molecules like epoxyeicosatrienoic acids. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4464","text":"A patient is brought to th​e emergency in an unrespo​nsive state. There is an ‌alleged history of consum‌ption of multiple prescri‌ption tablets in an attem‍pt to commit suicide. Aft​er the drug was conclusiv​ely identified, the docto​r recommends dialysis for‌ definitive management. T​he drug most likely is:","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the emergency in an unresponsive state. There is an alleged history of consumption of multiple prescription tablets in an attempt to commit suicide. After the drug was conclusively identified, the doctor recommends dialysis for definitive management. The drug most likely is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dig​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vera​pamil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diaz​epam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lit​hium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lithium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lithium</strong></p><p>The drug most likely is <strong>lithium</strong> as it is the only one amongst the given drugs that can be <strong>removed by dialysis</strong>. It is the treatment of choice in case of severe lithium toxicity (&gt;4mEq/L). </p>\n<p>The distribution of lithium is mainly <strong>restricted </strong>to the<strong> vascular compartment </strong>and it is<strong> not bound </strong>to any<strong> plasma proteins</strong>. Dialysis is effective in removing the drugs if they are sufficiently present in the free form in the plasma. Therefore, lithium can be removed by dialysis during an overdose.</p>\n<p>Drugs not suitable for dialysis are those with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High volume of distribution - <strong>digoxin</strong>, <strong>verapamil</strong>, and organophosphates </li>\n<li>High level of plasma protein binding - <strong>diazepam</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4489","text":"Which of the following is ​not a prodrug?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a prodrug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enal​april","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imipr​amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfas​alazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclopho​sphamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Imipramine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Imipramine</strong></p><p><strong>Imipramine</strong> is not a prodrug. It is a tricyclic antidepressant and has<strong> therapeutic activity. </strong>It also forms an active metabolite, desipramine. </p>\n<p><strong>Prodrugs</strong> are <strong>inactive precursors</strong> of active forms. They require <strong>metabolic alteration</strong> in the body to become active.</p>\n<p>Examples of prodrugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prednisone</li>\n<li>Levodopa</li>\n<li><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong>(except lisinopril and captopril)</li>\n<li><strong>Sulfasalazine</strong></li>\n<li>Methyldopa</li>\n<li><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong></li>\n<li>Clopidogrel</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1110","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about P-glycopro​tein?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about P-glycoprotein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pgp stands for permeabilit​y glycoprotein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Also know​n as CD243","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Also know​n as ABCB1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Also know​n as MDR2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Also known as MDR2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Also known as MDR2</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>P-glycoprotein</strong> is also known as multidrug resistance protein 1<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> or </span><strong>MDR1</strong> (not MDR2).  It is also denoted by cluster of differentiation 243 (<strong>CD243</strong>).</p>\n<p>P-glycoprotein<strong> (P-gp)</strong> is one of the first members of the ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter, which acts as an <strong>efflux membrane transporter</strong>. It is responsible for limiting cellular uptake and the distribution of xenobiotics and toxic substances. It is associated with <strong>multidrug resistance</strong> (MDR).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> P-gp expression:<strong> </strong><strong>Reduces</strong> the <strong>absorption</strong> of drugs that are substrates for P-gp</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased</strong> P-gp expression: Leads to supratherapeutic plasma concentrations and <strong>drug toxicity</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1109","text":"For which of the following​ drugs, is absorption inc​reased after a fatty meal‌?","raw_text":"For which of the following drugs, is absorption increased after a fatty meal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ampic​illin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asp​irin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Griseofulvin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Griseofulvin</strong></p><p>The <strong>absorption</strong> of <strong>griseofulvin</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> after a <strong>fatty meal.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 187px;\" width=\"699\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 341.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Drug absorption reduced /delayed by food</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 341.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Drug absorption increased by food</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 341.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ampicillin</li>\n<li>Aspirin</li>\n<li>Atenolol</li>\n<li>Azithromycin</li>\n<li>Captopril</li>\n<li>Indinavir</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Rifampin</li>\n<li>Sucralfate</li>\n<li>Tetracycline</li>\n<li>Doxycycline</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 341.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Atovaquone</li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Chlorothiazide</li>\n<li>Cefuroxime</li>\n<li>Diazepam</li>\n<li>Ganciclovir</li>\n<li>Lithium</li>\n<li>Metoprolol</li>\n<li>Ritonavir</li>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Lumefantrine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Fatty meals increase the rate of absorption of efavirenz to such an extent as to cause toxicity. Hence, efavirenz is recommended to be taken on an empty stomach.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4513","text":"You prescribe an antibioti​c course for a patient fo​llowing cesarean section.‌ What advice would you gi‌ve regarding breastfeedin‌g?","raw_text":"You prescribe an antibiotic course for a patient following cesarean section. What advice would you give regarding breastfeeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No advice as most of drug ​are not present in breast​ milk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avoid breastfeeding during​ the antibiotic course","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medications to be taken 3-​4 hours before next feedi​ng","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"She can take medications a​nytime","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medications to be taken 3-4 hours before next feeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medications to be taken 3-4 hours before next feeding</strong></p><p>If the lactating mother needs to take medications that are deemed safe, the dosing optimally should be<strong> 30-60 minutes after nursing </strong>and at least<strong> 3-4 hours before</strong> the<strong> next feeding</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>minimum interval</strong> of <strong>3-4 hours</strong> allows time for <strong>partial clearance</strong> of the drug. This ensures the infants are not exposed to the peak maternal plasma concentration that may translate to higher levels secreted into milk. </p>\n<p>Option A: Most drugs administered are detected in breast milk but most of them are excreted in very small quantities that do not cause any effect in the infants. </p>\n<p>Option B: Breastfeeding should not be avoided altogether for all medications. Intake of cytotoxic agents and immunomodulatory agents are contraindications for breastfeeding. </p>\n<p>Option D: The timing of medication should preferably be at an interval where the infants are not exposed to higher maternal plasma concentrations. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4477","text":"A 55-year-old patient is o​n omeprazole for gastroes​ophageal reflux disease. ‌She is now to be started ‌on dual antiplatelet ther‌apy with aspirin and clop‍idogrel. You decide to sw​itch to pantoprazole beca​use of the action of omep​razole on which enzyme?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old patient is on omeprazole for gastroesophageal reflux disease. She is now to be started on dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel. You decide to switch to pantoprazole because of the action of omeprazole on which enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CYP​3A4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CYP​2D6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CYP​2C19","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CYP​2C20","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CYP2C19","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CYP2C19</strong></p><p>Omeprazole is replaced with another proton pump inhibitor (PPI) during coadministration with clopidogrel because of the action on CYP2C19.</p>\n<p><strong>Clopidogrel</strong> is a<strong> prodrug </strong>that is activated to its active metabolite by the liver enzyme<strong> CYP2C19.</strong> Apart from CYP2C19, CYP3A4, and CYP2C9 are also involved to a certain extent.</p>\n<p><strong>Omeprazole </strong>serves as both <strong>substrate</strong> and<strong> inhibitor</strong> for <strong>CYP2C19.</strong> It may inhibit the activation of clopidogrel and <strong>decrease</strong> its <strong>antiplatelet action. </strong>Hence, they are not prescribed together.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4476","text":"Subfamily of CYP3A4 is ___​____","raw_text":"Subfamily of CYP3A4 is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​YP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A</strong></p><p>The <strong>sub-family</strong> of <strong>CYP3A4</strong> is <strong>A</strong>.</p>\n<p>In CYP enzymes nomenclature, the root CYP is followed by a number designating the family, a letter denoting the subfamily and another number designating the gene. Therefore, in CYP3A4:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>3 - family </li>\n<li>A - subfamily A</li>\n<li>4 - individual gene coding for one specific isoenzyme</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are numerous CYP isoenzymes in humans. Among them, <strong>CYP3A4 </strong>is the<strong> most abundant</strong> and carries out the metabolism of nearly <strong>50%</strong> of <strong>clinically used</strong> drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1112","text":"How much drug will remain ​at the end of 6 hours whe​n a patient is given 200 ‌mg drug, 75 mg excreted i‌n 90 min and if the drug ‌follows first-order kinet‍ics?","raw_text":"How much drug will remain at the end of 6 hours when a patient is given 200 mg drug, 75 mg excreted in 90 min and if the drug follows first-order kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50​ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30.​5 mg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12.​5 mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6.2​5 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30.5 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30.5 mg</strong></p><p>In <strong>first order kinetics, </strong>a<strong> constant fraction</strong> of the drug is<strong> excreted in </strong>a<strong> given time.</strong> </p>\n<p>75 mg of 200 mg is excreted in 90 minutes</p>\n<p>75/200 = 37.5/100 = <strong>0.375</strong> is eliminated </p>\n<p>After 90 minutes; 200 mg-75 mg = 125 mg remains </p>\n<p>The next 90 minutes (after 180 minutes) = 125 x 0.375 = 46.875 mg is eliminated, and 125-46.875 = 78.125 mg remains </p>\n<p>The next 90 minutes (after 270 minutes) = 78.125 x 0.375 = 29.297 mg is eliminated, and 78.125-29.297 = 48.828 remains </p>\n<p>The next 90 minutes (after 360 minutes) = 48.828 x 0.375 = 18.310 mg is eliminated, 48.828-18.310 = <strong>30.518 mg remains after 6 hours. </strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Simpler way to calculate </strong></p>\n<p>3/8 fraction is excreted in 90 minutes → 5/8 remains after 90 minutes.</p>\n<p>6 hours (360 min) is 4 times of 90 minutes.</p>\n<p>So 200 x (5/8)(5/8)(5/8)(5/8) = <strong>30.518 mg remains after 360 minutes </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Dm3TUf4j","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"1_homeostasis_and_cellular_physiology","title":"1.Homeostasis And Cellular Physiology","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA6343","text":"Homeostasis is called as d‌ynamic equilibrium becaus‍e______","raw_text":"Homeostasis is called as dynamic equilibrium because______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All variables are given eq‌ual importance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can achieve a constancy in‌ extreme environmental ch‍allenge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The set-point for all home‌ostatic variables is alwa‍ys fixed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Body maintains an internal‌ balance within fluctuati‍ng limits","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Body maintains an internal balance within fluctuating limits","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Body maintains an internal balance within fluctuating limits</strong></p><p>Homeostasis is called <strong>dynamic equilibrium</strong> because the body maintains an <strong>internal balance within fluctuating limits. </strong>It describes the maintenance of nearly constant conditions in the internal environment despite changes in the outside world.</p>\n<p>A number of <strong>variables</strong> such as body temperature, blood sugar levels, blood pH, etc. are regulated within <strong>a range of values</strong> (homeostatic ranges) and not at fixed values.</p>\n<p>These variables are <strong>not given equal importance.</strong> There is a hierarchy of importance so that the constancy of certain variables may be altered markedly to maintain others within their normal range. For example, respiratory or metabolic compensation to maintain blood pH.</p>\n<p>The <strong>disruption of homeostasis</strong> either due to <strong>extreme environmental challenge</strong> or a breakdown of internal homeostatic mechanisms is termed as a <strong>disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6344","text":"Which is a component of ho‌meostatic control system?‍","raw_text":"Which is a component of homeostatic control system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stim‌ulus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rece‌ptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Effe‌ctor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73c0fb83f35e4cb1a59d1b8fd4e7c481x1280x1085.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p><strong>Stimulus, receptor and effector</strong> are all components of a homeostatic control system.</p>\n<p>Components of homeostasis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stimulus - </strong>It is a detectable change in the internal or external environment, such as change in temperature.</li>\n<li><strong>Receptor - </strong>It detects environmental changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrating centre</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Effector - </strong>It is an organ or cell that acts in response to a stimulus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6346","text":"Feedback regulation is see‌n in ______","raw_text":"Feedback regulation is seen in ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The increased heart rate t‌hat occurs in an athlete ‍just before a sprint begi​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smell of food triggers sec‌retion of salivary and ga‍stric juices","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An increase in insulin bef‌ore glucose absorption ha‍s raised blood glucose le​vels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The increased heart rate i‌n response to a decrease ‍in blood pressure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The increased heart rate in response to a decrease in blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The increased heart rate in response to a decrease in blood pressure</strong></p><p>Feedback regulation is seen in the <strong>increased heart rate in response to a decrease in blood pressure.</strong> This is because, in <strong>feedback</strong> regulation, the homeostatic <strong>response</strong> (increased heart rate) occurs <strong>after the change</strong> (decrease in blood pressure) has occurred.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, in <strong>feedforward</strong> regulation, the homeostatic <strong>responses</strong> take place <strong>before the change</strong> has occurred. This is done in anticipation of the change to minimize fluctuations. Examples of feedforward regulation include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The increased heart rate that occurs in an athlete just before a sprint begins.</li>\n<li>The smell of food triggers the secretion of salivary and gastric juices.</li>\n<li>An increase in insulin before glucose absorption has raised blood glucose levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6345","text":"Positive feedback is seen ‌in all except_____","raw_text":"Positive feedback is seen in all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LH s‌urge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Action p‌otential","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric ‌secretion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombus ‌formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastric secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastric secretion</strong></p><p>Positive feedback is seen in all except <strong>gastric secretion</strong>. It is under <strong>negative feedback</strong> control.</p>\n<p><strong>Negative feedback</strong> consists of certain <strong>changes</strong> that affect a deficient or excessive variable to <strong>pull it back to a certain mean value</strong> to maintain homeostasis. Most processes in the body are under negative feedback. For example, an increase in gastric acids leading to a decrease in gastric secretion. </p>\n<p><strong>Positive feedback</strong> consists of <strong>changes</strong> that affect a variable to<strong> push it further away from a certain mean value</strong>. For example, an increase in LH leading to a further increase in LH. Instances, where positive feedback plays an important role are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clotting of blood</li>\n<li>Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum</li>\n<li>LH Surge</li>\n<li>Action potential</li>\n<li>Shock</li>\n<li>Parturition</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1671","text":"On infusion of a large vol‌ume of blood, the MAP ris‍es from 100 mmHg to 175 m​mHg. Due to the barorecep​tor feedback mechanism, t​he MAP falls down to 125 ‌mmHg. Calculate the gain ‍of the feedback system.","raw_text":"On infusion of a large volume of blood, the MAP rises from 100 mmHg to 175 mmHg. Due to the baroreceptor feedback mechanism, the MAP falls down to 125 mmHg. Calculate the gain of the feedback system.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-‌2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"-‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"+‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"+‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. -2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. -2</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 19\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The gain of the feedback system in the above case is -2.</p>\n<p>The gain of the system is then calculated by using the following formula:</p>\n<p><strong>Gain = C/E</strong></p>\n<p>Here, C is <strong>correction</strong> achieved from the deviated value and E is the remaining<strong> error post-correction.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 19\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Calculation</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The original value in the given case is 100 mmHg.</li>\n<li>The deviated value in the given case is 175 mmHg.</li>\n<li>The corrected value in the given case is 125 mmHg.</li>\n<li>C is given by correction achieved from deviated value, Corrected value - Deviated value = 125 - 175 = -50 mmHg.</li>\n<li>E is given by the remaining error post-correction, Corrected value - Original value = 125 - 100 = 25 mmHg.</li>\n<li>Gain = -50/25 = -2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The negative symbol indicates that the homeostatic mechanism acts in a direction opposite to the initial deviation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9565","text":"Plasma membrane in eukaryo‌tes is made up of all exc‍ept _______","raw_text":"Plasma membrane in eukaryotes is made up of all except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chole‌sterol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leci‌thin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sphing‌omyelin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigly‌cerides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Triglycerides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Triglycerides</strong></p><p>Plasma membrane in eukaryotes does not contain triglycerides.</p>\n<p>The plasma membrane is composed almost entirely of <strong>proteins and lipids</strong>. The major lipids include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phospholipids: Lecithin, cephalin, phosphatidylserine</li>\n<li>Sphingolipids: Sphingomyelin</li>\n<li>Cholesterol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4638","text":"Membrane fluidity is incre‌ased by ____.","raw_text":"Membrane fluidity is increased by ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steari‌c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palmit‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myrist‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Linole‌ic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Linoleic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Linoleic acid</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 497\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Membrane fluidity is increased by <strong>linoleic acid, </strong>an unsaturated fatty acid. All other options are saturated fatty acids. </p>\n<p>Longer and <strong>saturated fatty acids decrease the fluidity</strong> because their side chains are straight and interact more strongly with each other. Conversely, <strong>unsaturated fatty acids increase the fluidity</strong> because their side chains are kinked and less compact.</p>\n<p><strong>Cholesterol</strong>, also known as <strong>fluidity buffer</strong> helps to maintain the fluidity of the cell membrane.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4649","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of peripheral ‍protein?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of peripheral protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium potassium ATPase pu‌mp","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G-Protein coupled receptor‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cell surfac‌e receptors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cell surface receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3cf785875a3452bab2537fd57baf6d1x1280x818.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cell surface receptors</strong></p><p><strong>Cell surface receptors</strong> are peripheral proteins.</p>\n<p>Proteins in the Plasma Membrane</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p><strong>Integral Proteins</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p><strong>Peripheral Proteins</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Transmembrane proteins i.e., they span the thickness of the membrane.</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Present either on the inner or outer surface of the membrane</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Examples - <br/>Insulin receptor, pumps, transport proteins (carriers), G-protein coupled receptors</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Examples - <br/>Cell surface receptors and enzymes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4646","text":"Which of the following mem‌branes has the highest pr‍otein content per gram ti​ssue?","raw_text":"Which of the following membranes has the highest protein content per gram tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelin‌ sheath","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Outer mitochondrial membra‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sarcoplasmi‌c reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inner mitochondrial membra‌ne","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inner mitochondrial membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inner mitochondrial membrane</strong></p><p>The<strong> inner mitochondrial membrane </strong>has the <strong>highest</strong> protein content per gram tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Myelin</strong> has the <strong>lowest</strong> protein content per gram tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Protein to Lipid Ratio in Different Membranes</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"178\">\n<p><strong>Membranes</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"120\">\n<p><strong>Protein: Lipid Ratio</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"178\">\n<p>Myelin</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"120\">\n<p>0.23</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"178\">\n<p>Human RBC</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"120\">\n<p>1.1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"178\">\n<p>Mitochondrial outer membrane</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"120\">\n<p>1.1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"178\">\n<p>Sarcoplasmic reticulum</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"120\">\n<p>2.0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"178\">\n<p>Mitochondrial inner membrane</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"120\">\n<p>3.2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5118","text":"The sequence of vesicular ‌transport in a cell is","raw_text":"The sequence of vesicular transport in a cell is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ER - Trans - Cis - Mitocho‌ndria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ER - Cis - Trans - Mitocho‌ndria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ER - Trans - Cis - Cell me‌mbrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ER - Cis - Trans - Cell me‌mbrane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ER - Cis - Trans - Cell membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/572ce0c6dae944ffbd38897936322c6cx1280x1528.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ER - Cis - Trans - Cell membrane</strong></p><p>The sequence of vesicular transport in a cell is <strong>ER - Cis - Trans - Cell membrane.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rough endoplasmic reticulum</strong> (RER) - It is the site of protein synthesis. Proteins are taken to the Golgi apparatus through transport vesicles.</p>\n<p><strong>Cis end of Golgi apparatus</strong> - Also called as receiving end, it is closest to RER</p>\n<p><strong>Trans end of Golgi apparatus</strong> - Also called as releasing end, it is furthest away from RER.</p>\n<p><strong>Cell membrane</strong> - Synthesised proteins within secretory vesicles are released into the extracellular space through the process of exocytosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4659","text":"All of the following are e‌nzyme markers of mitochon‍dria except____","raw_text":"All of the following are enzyme markers of mitochondria except____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ATP sy‌nthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5’-nucl‌eotidase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamate d‌ehydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Creatin‌e kinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5’-nucleotidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5’-nucleotidase</strong></p><p><strong>5’ Nucleotidase </strong>is an enzyme marker for <strong>plasma membrane.</strong> All the other options are mitochondrial enzyme markers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9631","text":"Which of the following wil‌l test negative for all m‍itochondrial markers?","raw_text":"Which of the following will test negative for all mitochondrial markers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sperm‌atozoa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"O‌va","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mature red ‌blood cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"White bl‌ood cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mature red blood cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mature red blood cells</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW100365550\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW100365550\"><strong>Mature red blood cells</strong> will test negative for all mitochondrial markers are<strong> </strong>as they do not have mitochondria. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW100365550\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"ContextualSpellingAndGrammarError SCXW100365550\"><strong>Lack of mitochondria</strong> is the reason why red blood cells exclusively</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW100365550\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW100365550\"> use<strong> anaerobic glycolysis</strong> to generate </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW100365550\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW100365550\">ATP.</span></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4662","text":"Which is the characteristi‌c enzyme present in perox‍isome?","raw_text":"Which is the characteristic enzyme present in peroxisome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arylsul‌fatases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribonu‌clease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glycos‌idases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cata‌lase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Catalase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Catalase</strong></p><p><strong>Catalase</strong> is the characteristic enzyme of <strong>peroxisomes</strong>. All other are lysosomal enzymes.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9577","text":"Gap junctions are found in‌ all except ______","raw_text":"Gap junctions are found in all except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neu‌rons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac‌ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Smooth‌ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Skeletal muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24ebadf6567948989b3cf3817b2d5ff4x1280x1227.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Skeletal muscle</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW201888236\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW201888236\">Gap junctions are absent in </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW201888236\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW201888236\">adult</span></span> <span class=\"TextRun SCXW201888236\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW201888236\">ske</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW201888236\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW201888236\">l</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW201888236\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW201888236\">etal</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW201888236\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW201888236\"> muscles.</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW201888236\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"EOP SCXW201888236\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128010966\">Gap junctions are intercellular connections with a channel in between. They are present in e</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128010966\">xcitable </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128010966\">tissues such as</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128010966\"> <strong>cardiac </strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"ContextualSpellingAndGrammarError SCXW128010966\">muscle, smooth</span></span> </strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128010966\"><strong>muscle and neurons</strong> but not in</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128010966\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128010966\"> skeletal muscles.</span></span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4682","text":"Tight junctions are not se‌en in ____.","raw_text":"Tight junctions are not seen in ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac‌ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ‌tubules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sertol‌i cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choroid‌ plexus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiac muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiac muscle</strong></p><p>Tight junctions are not seen in cardiac muscle. </p>\n<p>Tight junctions form a <strong>water-tight seal</strong> between cells. They are characteristically seen in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intestinal mucosa</li>\n<li>Renal tubules</li>\n<li>Choroid plexus</li>\n<li>Sertoli cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Functions of tight junctions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prevents the diffusion of macromolecules between cells.</li>\n<li>Maintains cell polarity.</li>\n<li>Seals the paracellular pathway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4683","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cell adhesion molec‍ule (CAM)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cell adhesion molecule (CAM)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inte‌grin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sele‌ctin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cadh‌erin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spec‌trin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spectrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spectrin</strong></p><p><strong>Spectrin</strong> is a cytoskeletal protein but not a cell adhesion molecule.</p>\n<p>Cells are attached to each other or to the basal lamina by cell adhesion molecules (CAMs). Some important classes of CAMs are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Integrins</strong>:<strong> </strong>heterodimers that bind to various receptors</li>\n<li><strong>IgG superfamily </strong>of immunoglobulins</li>\n<li><strong>Cadherins</strong>:<strong> </strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup>-dependent molecules that mediate cell-to-cell adhesion by homophilic reactions</li>\n<li><strong>Selectins</strong> which have lectin-like domains that bind carbohydrates</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cancer cells have abnormal CAMs<strong> </strong>and hence cause decreased cell adhesion and allow individual cancer cells to detach from the parent cancer.</li>\n<li>The decrease in the amount of E-cadherin maybe the reason for early metastasis of numerous tumors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4679","text":"Identify the incorrectly m‌atched pair of intercellu‍lar connections and their​ structural units","raw_text":"Identify the incorrectly matched pair of intercellular connections and their structural units","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmosome -‌ Desmoglein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gap junction‌s - Cadherins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemidesmosom‌e - Integrins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zona occlude‌ns - Claudins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gap junctions - Cadherins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f18902568f54c02b52e210821157d90x1279x1227.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gap junctions - Cadherins</strong></p><p><strong>Gap junctions</strong> are made of <strong>connexons</strong>. Connexons are in turn made of units called <strong>connexins</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 711\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 711\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The different intercellular connections and their structural units are given below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Intercellular connections</td>\n<td>Structural units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tight junctions or zona occludens</td>\n<td>Occludins, claudins and junctional adhesion molecules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gap junctions</td>\n<td>Connexons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zona adherens</td>\n<td>Cadherins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Desmosome</td>\n<td>Cadherins like desmoglein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hemidesmosome</td>\n<td>Integrins</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7080","text":"Integrin co‌nnects with:","raw_text":"Integrin connects with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cadh‌erin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitro‌nectin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibro‌nectin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sele‌ctin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibronectin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibronectin</strong></p><p>Integrin connects with <strong>f</strong><strong>ibronectin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Integrins</strong> are transmembrane glycoproteins (integral proteins) that allow cells to attach to extracellular matrix constituents such as<strong> laminin, fibronectin and collagen</strong>. It helps in linking the intracellular cytoskeleton<strong> functionally and structurally</strong> with the outside world.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4676","text":"The force-generating prote‌ins are _____.","raw_text":"The force-generating proteins are _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myosin and‌ myoglobin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dynein an‌d kinesin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calmodulin ‌and kinesin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Troponin a‌nd dynamins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dynein and kinesin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dynein and kinesin</strong></p><p>The force-generating proteins are dynein and kinesin. </p>\n<p>There are three superfamilies of proteins namely <strong>kinesin, dynein,</strong> <strong>myosin </strong>referred to as molecular motors.</p>\n<p>The molecular motors are the force generating proteins as they move proteins, organelles, and other cell parts (collectively referred to as “cargo”) to all parts of the cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5123","text":"An absent dynein arm in ci‌lia can lead to all of th‍e following except:","raw_text":"An absent dynein arm in cilia can lead to all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ‌sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciliocyt‌ophthoria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Situs i‌nversus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ciliocytophthoria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ciliocytophthoria</strong></p><p>An <strong>absent dynein arm</strong> in cilia leads to <strong>primary ciliary dyskinesia</strong>. <strong>Kartagener syndrome</strong> is a primary ciliary dyskinesia characterized by the classical triad of <strong>situs inversus</strong>, <strong>chronic sinusitis</strong>, and <strong>bronchiectasis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ciliocytophthoria</strong> is the degradation of ciliated cells due to <strong>viral infections</strong>. It is not seen in primary ciliary dyskinesia. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4675","text":"In dividing cells, mitotic‌ spindles are formed by _‍___.","raw_text":"In dividing cells, mitotic spindles are formed by ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ubiq‌uitin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tub‌ulin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vime‌ntin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ker‌atin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tubulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tubulin</strong></p><p><strong>Tubulins</strong> constitute the microtubules that form the mitotic spindle in a dividing cell.</p>\n<p>Cytoskeletal elements of the cell.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 181px;\" width=\"401\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 126.023px; text-align: center;\">Cytoskeletal element</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.932px; text-align: center;\">Protein subunits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 126.023px; text-align: center;\">Microfilament <br/>(smallest diameter) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.932px; text-align: center;\">Actin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 126.023px; text-align: center;\">Intermediate filament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.932px; text-align: center;\">Keratin, vimentin, desmin, lamin etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 126.023px; text-align: center;\">Microtubule <br/>(largest diameter)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.932px; text-align: center;\">Kinesin, dynein and tubulin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Ubiquitin helps in protein degradation.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rDweInrO","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"1_theories_of_personality___defense_mechanisms","title":"1.Theories Of Personality & Defense Mechanisms","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MC3541","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a contribution by Sig​mund Freud?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contribution by Sigmund Freud?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free ass‍ociation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Introducing cocaine in psy‍chiatry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psychodyna‍mic theory","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psychosoc‍ial theory","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Psychosocial theory","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b0395b2364e4f548da0351207087fa1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Psychosocial theory</strong></p><p><strong>Sigmund Freud</strong> is credited with all the above-mentioned achievements <strong>except psychosocial theory </strong>(proposed by Erik Erikson).</p>\n<p><strong>Contributions of Sigmund Freud to the field of psychiatry</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free association</li>\n<li>Interpretation of dreams</li>\n<li>Psychoanalysis</li>\n<li>Psycho-sexual development </li>\n<li>Psychodynamic theory </li>\n<li>Topographic theory of the mind - unconscious, preconscious, conscious</li>\n<li>Structural theory of mind - id, ego, super-ego</li>\n<li>Cocaine in psychiatry</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Concept of free association</strong> is a technique in <strong>psychoanalysis</strong> where the patient is asked to freely share thoughts, random words, and anything else that comes to mind.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Cocaine</strong> was introduced in psychiatry as a <strong>remedy</strong> for <strong>morphine</strong> and<strong> alcohol abuse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Psychodynamic theory of development</strong> explains personality in terms of the interaction between the conscious and unconscious forces of <strong>id, ego, and super-ego</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4862","text":"Which of the following pri‍nciples is matched correc​tly with the part of the ‍mind it works on?","raw_text":"Which of the following principles is matched correctly with the part of the mind it works on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Id- principle of reality, ‍in the conscious domain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ego- principle of pleasure‍, in the unconscious doma​in","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Super Ego- principle of id‍ealism, both in the consc​ious and unconscious doma‍in","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Id- defence mechanisms, in‍ the unconscious domain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Super Ego- principle of idealism, both in the conscious and unconscious domain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Super Ego- principle of idealism, both in the conscious and unconscious domain</strong></p><p>The superego is based on the<strong> </strong>principle of idealism, both in the conscious and unconscious domain. It acts to<strong> perfect and civilize our behaviour</strong>. The voice of conscience comes from the superego. It is mostly unconscious but also has a conscious component. </p>\n<p><strong>Sigmund Freud</strong>'s<strong> structural theory of mind </strong>divides mental apparatus into the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Id (new-born)</strong> is a reservoir for basic survival instincts, aggression, and survival drives. It is based on the <strong>pleasure principle</strong> (immediate gratification). It is entirely<strong> unconscious.</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Ego (reality principle)</strong> is the executive part of the mind that maintains the <strong>balance between id and superego</strong>. It is based on exposure to reality and utilizes influences from the external world to modify the id. It is <strong>conscious</strong> except for unconscious <strong>defense mechanisms. </strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Superego (idealism principle)</strong> establishes and maintains a <strong>moral conscience</strong> in an individual by constantly comparing a person’s thoughts and feelings to expected standards of behavior. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>E.g., While studying, our id wants us to watch a movie instead and our superego wants us to study very hard without taking many breaks. Our ego thus balances between the two and we decide that we will study for few hours and then take a break to watch a movie.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3465","text":"According to Sigmund Freud‍, we have a barrier in ou​r mind called repression.‍ This function is done by​ which of the following p​art of the mind?","raw_text":"According to Sigmund Freud, we have a barrier in our mind called repression. This function is done by which of the following part of the mind?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The consc‍ious part","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The precon‍scious part","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The uncons‍cious part","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The subcon‍scious part","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The preconscious part","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The preconscious part</strong></p><p>According to Sigmund Freud, the <strong>preconscious part of our mind</strong> has a <strong>barrier called repression</strong>.<strong> </strong>This barrier separates the contents of the unconscious and the conscious mind.  </p>\n<p><strong>Sigmund Freud's topographic theory of mind</strong> divides the mind into 3 levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unconscious-</strong> contains instinctual drives, <strong>repressed memories, </strong>and thought processes. It is characterized by illogical and primitive thoughts called<strong> primary process thinking</strong>. It focuses on immediate wish fulfillment and <strong>instinctual discharge</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Preconscious-</strong> composed of mental events, processes, and contents that can be brought into conscious awareness by the <strong>act of focusing attention</strong>. It contains a barrier called <strong>repression</strong>. Eg: Although most persons are not consciously aware of the appearance of their first-grade teacher, they ordinarily can bring this image to mind by deliberately focusing attention on the memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Conscious-</strong> part of the mind which is <strong>accessible</strong> to us. Everything we know about ourselves is in the conscious part of our mind. Perceptions coming from the outside world or from within the body or mind are brought into awareness in this part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The unconscious is the part of the mind that contains repressed thoughts and memories whereas the <strong>barrier</strong> that censors these <strong>(repression)</strong> exists in the <strong>preconscious</strong> part of the mind.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3399","text":"Which of the following is ‍false according to the to​pographical theory?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false according to the topographical theory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary process thinking i‍s illogical and primitive​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An unconscious thought can‍ never reach conscious aw​areness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The drives and desires tha‍t one is born with are pa​rt of the unconscious min‍d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The contents of the precon‍scious mind can be brough​t into awareness by focus‍sing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. An unconscious thought can never reach conscious awareness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. An unconscious thought can never reach conscious awareness</strong></p><p>According to Sigmund Freud's topographical theory, an <strong>unconscious memory </strong>or thought <strong>can reach consciousness</strong> by overcoming the barrier of repression. </p>\n<p>This barrier can be overcome in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>During sleep-</strong> the repression force is lax, and thus many unconscious desires and thoughts reach our consciousness through dreams. So, he believed that the interpretation of dreams can reveal the contents of the unconscious mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Free association-</strong> when a person is relaxed enough to say whatever comes to their minds without censoring thoughts, a few unconscious thoughts may come out by a slip of the tongue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In psychotherapy, there is a process called <strong>abreaction</strong> by which repressed material, particularly a painful experience or conflict, is brought back to consciousness by hypnosis or free association. In this process, the person not only recalls but also relives the repressed material causing an emotional release. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4279","text":"What is defined as the emo‍tional release and discha​rge after consciously rel‍iving a painful experienc​e that has been repressed​?","raw_text":"What is defined as the emotional release and discharge after consciously reliving a painful experience that has been repressed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cath‍arsis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abrea‍ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Venti‍ng out","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guided r‍elaxation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abreaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abreaction</strong></p><p><strong>Abreaction </strong>is a psychoanalytical term for <strong>reliving an experience</strong> to release it emotionally. It is a specific type of catharsis done by <strong>becoming conscious</strong> of repressed traumatic events.</p>\n<p><strong>Catharsis</strong> is a general term for the process of releasing strong or repressed emotions. It can be done through various mediums, like expressing emotions through art. When the unconscious repressed memories are consciously recalled and relived, this technique specifically is called abreaction. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3464","text":"“The dream is the (disguis‍ed) fulfillment of a (sup​pressed, repressed) wish.‍”- Sigmund Freud. Which o​f the following mechanism​s is not included in the ‍'Interpretations of dream‌s' written by Sigmund Fre​ud?","raw_text":"“The dream is the (disguised) fulfillment of a (suppressed, repressed) wish.”- Sigmund Freud. Which of the following mechanisms is not included in the 'Interpretations of dreams' written by Sigmund Freud?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conden‍sation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Displa‍cement","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Repre‍ssion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Symb‍olism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Repression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Repression</strong></p><p><strong>Repression</strong> is <strong>not</strong> the mechanism through which dreams manifest. It is the barrier due to which the thoughts and events stored in our unconscious mind do not come out to our conscious mind and awareness.</p>\n<p>According to the '<strong>Interpretation of dreams' written by Sigmund Freud</strong>, dreams represent unconscious wishes or thoughts which are not accepted by the conscious mind. They manifest through the following mechanisms:</p>\n<p><strong>Symbolism:</strong> Representation of highly charged ideas or objects through innocent images that are in some way connected. </p>\n<p><strong>Displacement:</strong> Transfer of mental energy from one object to a substitute or symbolic representation of the object. </p>\n<p><strong>Condensation:</strong> Mechanism by which multiple unconscious wishes combine into a single image or when one unconscious wish gets distributed into multiple images. </p>\n<p><strong>Projection:</strong> Attributing one’s own unacknowledged feelings to others. </p>\n<p><strong>Secondary elaboration:</strong> Process by which the ego organizes illogical, bizarre and absurd images into a more coherent form in dreams. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3394","text":"Dependent personality trai‍ts are related to which p​hase of psychosexual deve‍lopment ?","raw_text":"Dependent personality traits are related to which phase of psychosexual development ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‍al","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"An‍al","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pha‍llic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gen‍ital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral</strong></p><p>The development of <strong>dependent personality traits</strong> is due to<strong> fixation in the oral stage</strong> of development, according to the psychosexual stages of development proposed by Sigmund Freud. </p>\n<p>He proposed five stages of development and stated that when the development of a child gets arrested at one stage, called <strong>fixation</strong>, it results in psychiatric disorders.</p>\n<p>The phases are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 565px; height: 402px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 257.828125px; height: 32px;\">\n<p><strong>Phase of sexual development</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.1875px; height: 32px;\">\n<p><strong>Disorders resulting from fixation in this phase</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 109px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 257.828125px; height: 109px;\">\n<p><strong>Oral phase</strong></p>\n<p>(birth- 1.5 years)</p>\n<p>Learning how to eat, mouth is the site of pleasure.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.1875px; height: 109px;\">\n<p><strong>Alcohol dependence</strong> or drug dependence</p>\n<p><strong>Schizophrenia</strong></p>\n<p>Severe mood disorders</p>\n<p>Dependent personality disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 128px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 257.828125px; height: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>Anal phase</strong></p>\n<p>(1.5-3 years)</p>\n<p>Toilet training, the site of pleasure is the anal region.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.1875px; height: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>OCD</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 128px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 257.828125px; height: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>Phallic/Oedipal phase</strong></p>\n<p>(3-5 years)</p>\n<p>Site of pleasure is the genital region.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.1875px; height: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>Oedipus/Electra complex,</strong> which is the primary cause of <strong>hysteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Also, <strong>castration anxiety</strong> in boys and <strong>penis envy</strong> in girls.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 257.828125px; height: 78px;\">\n<p><strong>Latency</strong> <strong>phase</strong></p>\n<p>(5-12 years)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.1875px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Neurotic disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 257.828125px; height: 110px;\">\n<p><strong>Genital</strong> <strong>phase</strong></p>\n<p>sexual orientation</p>\n<p>(&gt;12 years)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.1875px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Neurotic disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3393","text":"There is a complex named a‍fter a legendary Greek he​ro who unknowingly killed‍ his father and married h​is own mother. In which s​tage of psychosexual deve‍lopment does this complex‌ develop in children?","raw_text":"There is a complex named after a legendary Greek hero who unknowingly killed his father and married his own mother.  In which stage of psychosexual development does this complex develop in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‍al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gen‍ital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An‍al","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pha‍llic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phallic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phallic</strong></p><p>The above complex develops in the <strong>phallic stage</strong> of psychosexual development, and the complex mentioned is the <strong>Oedipus complex</strong> (named after the legendary Greek hero called Oedipus).</p>\n<p>The phallic phase has 2 complexes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oedipus</strong> complex in a<strong> male</strong> child</li>\n<li><strong>Electra</strong> complex in a<strong> female</strong> child.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Oedipus complex</strong> consists of a <strong>desire</strong> for<strong> sexual involvement with the mother</strong> and a sense of rivalry with the father. The male child is also scared that if the father finds out, he will castrate him, this is called <strong>castration anxiety</strong>.</p>\n<p>The opposite is true for the <strong>Electra complex </strong>which is the <strong>desire for sexual involvement with the father</strong> and a sense of rivalry with the mother. The girl child becomes aware of the fact that she does not have a penis, this is called <strong>penis</strong> <strong>envy</strong> and she holds her mother responsible for it. The stage gets resolved when the female child starts identifying with the mother. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 531px; height: 614px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Phase of sexual development</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.28125px;\">\n<p><strong>Disorders resulting from fixation in this phase</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Oral phase</strong></p>\n<p>(birth- 1.5 years)</p>\n<p>Learning how to eat, mouth is the site of pleasure.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.28125px;\">\n<p><strong>Alcohol dependence</strong> or drug dependence</p>\n<p><strong>Schizophrenia</strong></p>\n<p>Severe mood disorders</p>\n<p>Dependent personality disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Anal phase</strong></p>\n<p>(1.5-3 years)</p>\n<p>Toilet training, the site of pleasure is the anal region.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.28125px;\">\n<p><strong>OCD</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Phallic/Oedipal phase</strong></p>\n<p>(3-5 years)</p>\n<p>Site of pleasure is the genital region.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.28125px;\">\n<p><strong>Oedipus/Electra complex,</strong> which is the primary cause of <strong>hysteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Also, <strong>castration anxiety</strong> in boys and <strong>penis envy</strong> in girls.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Latency</strong> <strong>phase</strong></p>\n<p>(5-12 years)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.28125px;\">\n<p>Neurotic disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Genital</strong> <strong>phase</strong></p>\n<p>sexual orientation</p>\n<p>(&gt;12 years)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.28125px;\">\n<p>Neurotic disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2254","text":"According to psychoanalyti‍c theory, alcoholic peopl​e have become fixative in‍ which stage of psychosex​ual development?","raw_text":"According to psychoanalytic theory, alcoholic people have become fixative in which stage of psychosexual development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‍al","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"An‍al","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pha‍llic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gen‍ital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral</strong></p><p>According to classic <strong>psychoanalytical theory,</strong> at least some alcoholic people may have become fixated at the <strong>oral stage</strong> of development.</p>\n<p>They use alcohol to relieve their frustrations by taking the substance by mouth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3459","text":"According to Erikson, the ‍major conflict in the fir​st year of life is betwee‍n which of the following?​","raw_text":"According to Erikson, the major conflict in the first year of life is between which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trust vs‍ mistrust","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Initiativ‍e vs guilt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autonomy‍ vs shame","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intimacy v‍s isolation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trust vs mistrust","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trust vs mistrust</strong></p><p><strong>Trust vs mistrust</strong> is the major psychosocial conflict seen in the first year of life.<strong> </strong>In this stage, the child must learn to trust others during the first year of life (the sense of<strong> </strong>trust vs mistrust)<strong> </strong>or they will have trouble forming close relationships as adults.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychosocial development: </strong>According to the psychosocial stages of development by <strong>Erik Erikson</strong>, there are critical time periods, each of which poses a particular psychosocial conflict to the individual. The resolution of this conflict results in either psychosocial growth or regression.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>8 stages </strong>of psychosocial development.</p>\n<p><strong>Erikson's stages and goals:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 190px;\" width=\"434\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\"><strong>Stage and age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\"><strong>Goal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">Stage One\n<ul>\n<li>Infancy</li>\n<li>Birth-18 months </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Trust vs Mistrust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Two</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Toddler</li>\n<li>18month-3 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Three</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-school</li>\n<li>3-5 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Initiative vs Guilt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Four</p>\n<ul>\n<li>School going</li>\n<li>5-13 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Industry vs Inferiority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Five</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adolescence</li>\n<li>13-21 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Identity vs Role Confusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Six</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Young adulthood</li>\n<li>21-40 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Intimacy vs Isolation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Seven</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Middle adulthood</li>\n<li>40-60 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Generativity vs Stagnation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Eight</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Late adulthood</li>\n<li>&gt;60 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Ego Integrity vs Despair</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3461","text":"\"Guard your thoughts for t‍hey shape your future\". T​eachers, peers, and adult‍s outside the home become​ important in shaping att​itudes towards oneself in‍ which of the following E‌rikson's stages?","raw_text":"\"Guard your thoughts for they shape your future\". Teachers, peers, and adults outside the home become important in shaping attitudes towards oneself in which of the following Erikson's stages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trust vs‍ mistrust","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Initiativ‍e vs guilt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Industry vs‍ inferiority","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Integrity ‍vs despair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Industry vs inferiority","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Industry vs inferiority</strong></p><p>Teachers, peers, and adults outside the home become important in shaping attitudes towards oneself in the stage of <strong>industry vs inferiority</strong> according to <strong>Erik Erikson's psychosocial stages of development</strong>. </p>\n<p>In this stage, children mature and their level of <strong>self-awareness increases</strong>. They understand logical reasoning, scientific facts, and other matters that are typically taught in school. Children also become <strong>more competitive</strong>, they want to do things that other children of the same age can do. When they make the effort to perform a task and succeed, they <strong>develop self-confidence</strong>. However, if they fail, they tend to feel that they are inferior to others.</p>\n<p><strong>Erikson's stages and goals:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 190px;\" width=\"434\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\"><strong>Stage and age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\"><strong>Goal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">Stage One\n<ul>\n<li>Infancy</li>\n<li>Birth-18 months </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Trust vs Mistrust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Two</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Toddler</li>\n<li>18month-3 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Three</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-school</li>\n<li>3-5 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Initiative vs Guilt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Four</p>\n<ul>\n<li>School going</li>\n<li>5-13 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Industry vs Inferiority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Five</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adolescence</li>\n<li>13-21 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Identity vs Role Confusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Six</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Young adulthood</li>\n<li>21-40 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Intimacy vs Isolation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Seven</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Middle adulthood</li>\n<li>40-60 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Generativity vs Stagnation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Eight</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Late adulthood</li>\n<li>&gt;65 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Ego Integrity vs Despair</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3462","text":"A 4-year-old child is brou‍ght to the casualty follo​wing a skating injury. Th‍e parents encouraged him ​to ask questions. He talk​ed for most of the conver‍sation and excitedly expl‌ained he wanted to try th​is so his parents let him‍. He even painted one wal‌l in their house and aske‌d if the doctor wants to ‌see it. By this behavior ‌of parents, what they are‍ encouraging in the child​?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child is brought to the casualty following a skating injury. The parents encouraged him to ask questions. He talked for most of the conversation and excitedly explained he wanted to try this so his parents let him. He even painted one wall in their house and asked if the doctor wants to see it. By this behavior of parents, what they are encouraging in the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auto‍nomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iden‍tity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genera‍tivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Initi‍ative","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Initiative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Initiative</strong></p><p>The parents are encouraging the development of the initiative in their child which happens in the stage of<strong> initiative vs guilt</strong>, as proposed by Erik Erikson.</p>\n<p>In this stage, children begin to <strong>explore</strong> and <strong>do things on their own</strong>. They are able to <strong>learn new concepts</strong> introduced in school and are expected to practice these lessons in real life. They know that they can accomplish these tasks, but if they fail to do so and end up asking for assistance from others, they may feel a sense of guilt. Inadequate resolution of this conflict may lead to a conversion disorder or phobia. </p>\n<p><strong>Erikson's stages and goals:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 190px;\" width=\"434\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\"><strong>Stage and age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\"><strong>Goal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">Stage One\n<ul>\n<li>Infancy</li>\n<li>Birth-18 months </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Trust vs Mistrust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Two</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Toddler</li>\n<li>18month-3 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Three</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-school</li>\n<li>3-5 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Initiative vs Guilt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Four</p>\n<ul>\n<li>School going</li>\n<li>5-13 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Industry vs Inferiority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Five</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adolescence</li>\n<li>13-21 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Identity vs Role Confusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Six</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Young adulthood</li>\n<li>21-40 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Intimacy vs Isolation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Seven</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Middle adulthood</li>\n<li>40-60 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Generativity vs Stagnation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.486px;\">\n<p>Stage Eight</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Late adulthood</li>\n<li>&gt;60 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.597px;\">Ego Integrity vs Despair</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3402","text":"According to the stages of‍ cognitive development, w​hich of the following is ‍correctly matched?","raw_text":"According to the stages of cognitive development, which of the following is correctly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Starts seeing things from ‍other's perspective - Sta​ge of formal operation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conservation and reversibi‍lity - Pre-operational st​age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symbolisation - Sensorimot‍or stage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intuitive and egocentric -‍ Concrete operations stag​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symbolisation - Sensorimotor stage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symbolisation - Sensorimotor stage</strong></p><p><strong>Symbolization</strong> develops in the <strong>sensorimotor stage, </strong>according to the <strong>stages of cognitive development</strong> by <strong>Jean Piaget</strong>.</p>\n<p>The theory of cognitive development describes four major stages from birth to adolescence that lead to the capacity for adult thought. The stages are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sensorimotor stage (birth–2 years)</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Starts with an <strong>out-of-sight, out-of-mind</strong> type of thinking</li>\n<li>Ends with <strong>object permanence</strong> (knowing an object exists even though it cannot be seen) </li>\n<li><strong>Symbolisation</strong>- developing mental symbols and words for the objects</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-operational stage (2-7 years)</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Thinking without reasoning- <strong>intuitive thinking</strong> </li>\n<li>Only concerned about their own needs and perspective- <strong>egocentric thinking</strong></li>\n<li>Other thinking processes are seen- animism, primitive thinking, centration, magical or fantasy thinking</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Concrete operational stage (7-11 years)</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Start to see things from other's perspectives</li>\n<li>Thinking is <strong>concrete</strong> and literal</li>\n<li>Develop <strong>conservation</strong>- understanding that despite changes in shape, the object remains the same. For eg: when water is transferred from a cup to glass, they understand that it is water only and that the volume is the same</li>\n<li>Develop the concept of <strong>reversibility</strong>- knowing that one thing can turn into another and turn back. Eg., water and ice</li>\n<li>Other processes- classification, transitivity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Formal operational stage (&gt;11 years)</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Develop <strong>abstract</strong> thinking, wherein they can generalize or conceptualize</li>\n<li>Can make a hypothesis and use deductive reasoning</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotheticodeductive</strong> thinking- the highest organization of cognition, enables persons to make a hypothesis or proposition and to test it against reality</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4843","text":"Which of the following is ‍not seen during the opera​tional period of cognitiv‍e development?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen during the operational period of cognitive development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Revers‍ibility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transi‍tivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conser‍vation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ani‍mism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Animism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Animism</strong></p><p><strong>Animism</strong> is seen in the <strong>pre-operational stage</strong> (2-7 years of age) where the child believes that inanimate objects are capable of actions and have lifelike qualities. Eg: A child believing that the sidewalk was mad and made them fall down, or that the stars twinkle in the sky because they are happy.</p>\n<p><strong>Reversibility</strong>, <strong>transitivity</strong>, and <strong>conservation</strong> are seen in the <strong>concrete operational period</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Reversibilit</strong><strong>y: </strong>Knowing that one thing can turn into another and turn back. E.g., water and ice.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Transitivity:</strong> The ability to recognize relationships among various things in a serial order. E.g., when told to put away his books according to height, the child identifies and starts with placing the tallest one on one end of the bookshelf and the shortest one ends up at the other end.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Conservation:</strong> Understanding that despite changes in shape, the object remains the same. E.g., when water is transferred from a bottle to glass, they understand that it is water only and that the volume is the same.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3391","text":"Which of the following def‍ence mechanisms is matche​d correctly?","raw_text":"Which of the following defence mechanisms is matched correctly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anticipati‍on - Mature","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intellectualisation - Matu‍re","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regression‍ - Neurotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Derailment‍ - Immature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anticipation - Mature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anticipation - Mature</strong></p><p><strong>Anticipation</strong> is a <strong>mature defence mechanism.</strong></p>\n<p>Defense mechanisms are the way and means with which the <strong>ego wards off anxiety </strong>and controls instinctive urges and unpleasant emotions. They are <strong>unconscious </strong>(except suppression which is voluntary), discrete, dynamic, irreversible, and may be <strong>adaptive or maladaptive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3396","text":"A 32-year-old man is broug‍ht to the ED with severe ​hand injuries. His wife s‍ays he started throwing t​hings in his house when s​he informed him that she ‍wants a divorce, and punc‌hed a mirror in a fit of ​rage. Which of the follow‍ing best describes the de‌fence mechanism used by t‌his patient?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the ED with severe hand injuries. His wife says he started throwing things in his house when she informed him that she wants a divorce, and punched a mirror in a fit of rage. Which of the following best describes the defence mechanism used by this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrof‍lexion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reaction ‍formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Introj‍ection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Passive-a‍ggressive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retroflexion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retroflexion</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a <strong>retroflexion</strong> defence mechanism.</p>\n<p>Retroflexion is characterised by <strong>self destruction due to rage</strong>. It is a <strong>neurotic</strong> defence mechanism. As given in the above scenerio, it may lead to feelings of suicide, severe depression, and deliberate self harm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3397","text":"A student applied to four ‍national universities for​ her MBA course but was d‍isheartened when she got ​rejected from all four un​iversities. While talking‍ to her friend on the pho‌ne, she said she never wa​nted to go anyway because‍ they were too far from h‌ome. What defense mechani‌sm is being used by her?","raw_text":"A student applied to four national universities for her MBA course but was disheartened when she got rejected from all four universities. While talking to her friend on the phone, she said she never wanted to go anyway because they were too far from home. What defense mechanism is being used by her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rationa‍lisation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Passive-a‍ggressive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhib‍ition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Den‍ial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rationalisation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rationalisation</strong></p><p>The given scenario point towards <strong>rationalization</strong>. The student is <strong>rationalizing</strong> her rejection by saying she wouldn't have gone even if she got accepted because of the distance from home.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3401","text":"An egoistic doctor who enj‍oyed bossing people aroun​d was disliked in the hos‍pital. A ward boy who was​ ordered to bring him tea​ intentionally makes sure‍ that the tea is cold. Th‌is is an example of which​ of the following defense‍ mechanisms?","raw_text":"An egoistic doctor who enjoyed bossing people around was disliked in the hospital. A ward boy who was ordered to bring him tea intentionally makes sure that the tea is cold. This is an example of which of the following defense mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actin‍g out","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Passive-aggressive behavio‍ur","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Repre‍ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reaction ‍formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Passive-aggressive behaviour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Passive-aggressive behaviour</strong></p><p>The given example is characteristic of <strong>passive-aggressive behavior</strong>. The ward boy who does not like the doctor is expressing his discontent covertly by bringing him cold tea, to avoid a conflict. It is an <strong>immature defense mechanism. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4879","text":"A police inspector is inve‍stigating a murder case. ​The victim was brutally b‍eaten and raped. The body​ had noose marks on the n​eck which seemed to be th‍e cause of death. However‌, during the investigatio​n, the inspector never sh‍owed any emotion. What de‌fense mechanism is descri‌bed here?","raw_text":"A police inspector is investigating a murder case. The victim was brutally beaten and raped. The body had noose marks on the neck which seemed to be the cause of death. However, during the investigation, the inspector never showed any emotion. What defense mechanism is described here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disto‍rtion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhib‍ition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isol‍ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bloc‍king","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isolation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isolation</strong></p><p>The defense mechanism described is<strong> isolation. </strong>It is the separation of an idea from the affect that accompanies it.</p>\n<p>It is usually developed in an attempt to <strong>avoid painful thoughts</strong> or feelings by emotionally <strong>detaching</strong> oneself from the feeling.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7801","text":"A couple visits the psychi‍atrist’s office with the ​husband complaining that ‍his wife is cheating on h​im, but he did not have a​ny evidence. In a private‍ conversation with his wi‌fe, she revealed that the​ husband had a reputation‍ of having multiple affai‌rs with his colleagues in‌ the past. In this scenar‌io, which defense mechani‌sm is the husband using?","raw_text":"A couple visits the psychiatrist’s office with the husband complaining that his wife is cheating on him, but he did not have any evidence. In a private conversation with his wife, she revealed that the husband had a reputation of having multiple affairs with his colleagues in the past. In this scenario, which defense mechanism is the husband using?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disto‍rtion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proje‍ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Repre‍ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Displa‍cement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Projection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Projection</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the husband is using <strong>projection</strong> as a defense mechanism. Projection is when you <strong>attribute your own wishes</strong>, thoughts, or feelings <strong>onto someone else.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2659","text":"A 24-year-old woman is bro‍ught to the emergency dep​artment after a violent a‍ttack. The patient has in​juries over her forearm a​nd elbow. On examination,‍ she has injuries over he‌r breasts and inner thigh​s. Pelvic examination rev‍eals lacerations in the v‌aginal mucosa. On enquiri‌ng about the injuries, th‌e patient is unable to re‌member the violent incide‍nt. Which of the followin​g defense mechanism best ​explains this phenomenon?‍","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a violent attack. The patient has injuries over her forearm and elbow. On examination, she has injuries over her breasts and inner thighs. Pelvic examination reveals lacerations in the vaginal mucosa. On enquiring about the injuries, the patient is unable to remember the violent incident. Which of the following defense mechanism best explains this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repre‍ssion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Suppr‍ession","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Den‍ial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bloc‍king","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Repression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Repression</strong></p><p>This patient's history and examination are suggestive of rape, and the patient is <strong>repressing</strong> the memory of the incident as a means of coping with the anxiety.</p>\n<p><strong>Repression</strong> is a type of psychological defense mechanism that involves keeping certain thoughts, feelings, or urges out of conscious awareness. The goal of this form of defense is to keep unacceptable desires or thoughts out of the conscious mind in order to <strong>prevent</strong> or <strong>minimize</strong> feelings of <strong>anxiety.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3395","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‍h a phobic disorder. Whic​h of the following defens‍e mechanisms is likely to​ be present in this patie​nt?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with a phobic disorder. Which of the following defense mechanisms is likely to be present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Displa‍cement","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dissoc‍iation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disto‍rtion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conve‍rsion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Displacement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Displacement</strong></p><p><strong>Displacement</strong> is the defense mechanism in <strong>phobias. </strong>It is the shifting of emotions about one object/individual onto another object/individual.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1960","text":"In the OPD, you inform a p‍atient that he has bladde​r cancer. However, he rep‍lies saying, \"No, I canno​t have cancer. I don't sm​oke or drink”. What is th‍e type of defense mechani‌sm exhibited by the patie​nt?","raw_text":"In the OPD, you inform a patient that he has bladder cancer. However, he replies saying, \"No, I cannot have cancer. I don't smoke or drink”. What is the type of defense mechanism exhibited by the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rationa‍lization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Actin‍g out","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Den‍ial","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reaction ‍formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Denial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Denial</strong></p><p><strong>Denial</strong> is the defense mechanism seen in this patient. Denial is <strong>a refusal to accept external reality</strong> because it is too threatening.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7198","text":"A 17-year-old girl was kid‍napped and held captive f​or 2 months until she was‍ rescued. However, it was​ observed that the girl d​eveloped positive feeling‍s towards her kidnapper, ‌and sympathized with his ​character, and the situat‍ions that made him kidnap‌ her. Which of the follow‌ing defense mechanisms ha‌s the girl most likely de‌veloped?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old girl was kidnapped and held captive for 2 months until she was rescued. However, it was observed that the girl developed positive feelings towards her kidnapper, and sympathized with his character, and the situations that made him kidnap her. Which of the following defense mechanisms has the girl most likely developed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrof‍lexion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Introj‍ection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intellect‍ualisation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rationa‍lisation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Introjection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Introjection</strong></p><p>The defense mechanism that the girl has developed is known as <strong>introjection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Introjection refers to <strong>unconscious internalisation</strong> of the qualities or characters of an object or a person, with the goal of ensuring closeness to, and the constant presence of, the object/person.</p>\n<p>The classic example of this is <strong>Stockholm syndrome,</strong> where <strong>victims</strong> who were kidnapped or held captive, may develop <strong>positive feelings towards the kidnapper</strong>, understand their character, emotions, and <strong>start sympathizing</strong> with them. They internalize the character of the aggressor as one's own character.  As they do so, they may even develop fear towards the police who come to rescue them and even try to escape from the police, and co-operate with the kidnapper's actions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3390","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrectly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reaction formation - Defen‍ce mechanism for obsessiv​e-compulsive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anal stage - Fixation stag‍e for obsessive-compulsiv​e disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Projection - Defence mecha‍nism for delusions and ha​llucinations","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genital stage - fixation s‍tage for hysteria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Genital stage - fixation stage for hysteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Genital stage - fixation stage for hysteria</strong></p><p><strong>Hysteria</strong> develops due to fixation in the <strong>phallic stage</strong> of development, not the genital stage.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>OCD</strong>, <strong>reaction formation</strong> to an intrusive thought <strong>leads to obsession</strong>. The correction mechanism is <strong>undoing</strong> which is the <strong>defense mechanism for compulsion. </strong>E.g., Contamination of the hand by the pathogen is the obsession, the patient reacts to it by the physical act of washing (undoing).</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>fixation</strong> of development during the <strong>anal stage</strong> leads to <strong>OCD.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Projection</strong> is the <strong>defense mechanism</strong> responsible for <strong>delusions and hallucinations.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3456","text":"An employee in a company i‍s friends with everyone i​n his office. He goes to ‍different parties every w​eekend and says he loves ​talking to new people. Th‍is trait is described und‌er which dimension of per​sonality?","raw_text":"An employee in a company is friends with everyone in his office. He goes to different parties every weekend and says he loves talking to new people. This trait is described under which dimension of personality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agreea‍bleness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conscien‍tiousness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extrav‍ersion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emotional‍ stability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Extraversion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Extraversion</strong></p><p>The traits of being talkative, sociable, and outgoing are described under <strong>extraversion.</strong> It is one of the five personality traits of the <strong>big five personality theory</strong>. It indicates how outgoing and social a person is. </p>\n<p>Contemporary personality psychologists believe that there are five basic dimensions of personality, often referred to as the big five personality traits, as described below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Openness -</strong> being open to new ideas, curious, creative, and original.</li>\n<li><strong>Conscientiousness -</strong> being organized, systematic, ambitious, punctual, and dependable.</li>\n<li><strong>Extraversion -</strong> being outgoing, talkative, and sociable.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreeableness -</strong> being tolerant, sensitive, kind, and trusting.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroticism -</strong> being anxious, irritable, temperamental, and moody.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yEiLo9Dr","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"1_fundamentals_of_x_ray_and_ct","title":"1.Fundamentals Of X Ray And Ct","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA8281","text":"All among the following ar​e ionizing radiations exc​ept:","raw_text":"All among the following are ionizing radiations except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha​ rays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta​ rays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-r​ays","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M​RI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MRI</strong></p><p><strong>MRI</strong> is a technique that produces topographic images by means of <strong>magnetic fields </strong>and <strong>radiofrequency waves.</strong></p>\n<p>The ionizing radiations</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alpha</li>\n<li>Beta</li>\n<li>Gamma</li>\n<li>X-Rays</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The non-Ionizing radiations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radiofrequency waves</li>\n<li>UV rays</li>\n<li>Microwaves</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alpha and beta rays are <strong>particulate radiation</strong> (particles) whereas gamma and x-rays are <strong>electromagnetic rays</strong>. All electromagnetic waves have a <strong>constant velocity</strong> of <strong>3×10<sup>8</sup> m/s</strong> and <strong>no mass</strong>. The electromagnetic spectrum, from <strong>low frequency</strong> to <strong>high frequency</strong> consists of:</p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Radiowaves</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> &lt; Microwaves &lt; Infrared &lt; Visible light &lt; Ultraviolet &lt; X-rays &lt; </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Gamma rays</strong></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>W. C. Roentgen</strong> is the father of X-ray and the first medical X-ray by Roentgen was of his wife Anna Bertha's left hand. Physicists <strong>Peter Mansfield </strong>and<strong> Paul Lauterbur</strong> developed MRI-related imaging techniques in medicine and they were awarded the Nobel prize in 2003.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8283","text":"What is the mechanism of h​eat loss in modern X-ray ​tubes?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of heat loss in modern X-ray tubes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radi​ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Condu​ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conve​ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evapo​ration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Radiation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Radiation</strong></p><p>The mechanism of <strong>heat</strong> <strong>loss</strong> in the modern X-ray tube is <strong>radiation.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 325px; width: 448px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 7px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 198px; height: 7px;\">\n<p>Characteristics</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703125px; height: 7px;\">\n<p><strong>Classical X-ray tube</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.296875px; height: 7px;\">\n<p><strong>Modern X-ray tube</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 198px; height: 23px;\">\n<p>Outer tube material</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703125px; height: 23px;\">\n<p>Glass</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.296875px; height: 23px;\">\n<p>Stainless steel</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 198px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Cathode</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703125px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Tungsten filament</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.296875px; height: 50px;\">Tungsten + thorium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 198px; height: 34px;\">\n<p>Anode</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703125px; height: 34px;\">\n<p>Tungsten</p>\n<p>The anode is the target</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.296875px; height: 34px;\">\n<p>Tungsten + 10% rhenium</p>\n<p>The anode is rotating</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 198px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>Mechanism of heat loss</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703125px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>Conduction</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.296875px; height: 33px;\">Radiation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The atomic number of tungsten is <strong>74</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8284","text":"What is the most commonly ​used active ingredient of​ an X-ray film?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly used active ingredient of an X-ray film?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Silver ​chloride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Silver ​bromide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Silver ​nitrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Silver ​fluoride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Silver bromide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Silver bromide</strong></p><p>An X-ray film is coated with a photosensitive emulsion on both sides, most commonly <strong>silver bromide.</strong></p>\n<p>Apart from silver bromide, silver iodide is also used. In <strong>routine</strong> films, <strong>both sides</strong> are coated, whereas, in <strong>mammography,</strong> only <strong>one side</strong> of the film is coated.</p>\n<p>Note: X-ray films are most sensitive to blue light and least sensitive to red light. So, the safe light used in the darkroom is a <strong>red light.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8285","text":"How are X-ra​ys produced?","raw_text":"How are X-rays produced?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"When electron beam strikes​ the nucleus of the atom ​in the anode","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"When electron beam deceler​ates close to the nucleus​ of the atom in the anode‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"When electron beam reacts ​with the electromagnetic ​field","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"When electron beam strikes​ the cathode","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. When electron beam decelerates close to the nucleus of the atom in the anode","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec49466d6922441ba2177e13dfc1c2b6x1280x1034.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19c46be1d9e341cb977363545d6854c2x1280x1043.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. When electron beam decelerates close to the nucleus of the atom in the anode</strong></p><p>X-rays are produced when the electron beam <strong>decelerates close to the nucleus</strong> of the atom in the anode.</p>\n<p>The production of X-rays is as follows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Current is passed through a tungsten filament which acts as the cathode<br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span><br/>Electrons are emitted as a result of thermionic emission<br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span><br/>Electrons bombard the target anode, a tungsten disc<br/><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/></span>Results in the conversion of energy into heat (99%) and X-ray photons(1%)</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n<p>The target anode is made of a <strong>tungsten</strong> disc in ordinary diagnostic X-ray tubes and molybdenum in mammography X-ray tubes.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">At the anode, <strong>fast-moving electrons</strong> interact with the atoms of the <strong>anode</strong> in one of the following ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nucleus field interaction: aka continuous spectrum radiation or <strong>Bremsstrahlung reaction </strong>(80%)</li>\n<li>Inner shell interaction: produces characteristic spectrum radiation (20%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Important parameters with respect to X rays:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Kilovolt peak (kVP) – Potential difference between cathode and anode. When the voltage increases, energy increases, and <strong>penetration</strong> also <strong>increases</strong>. Hence more kVP is required for obese patients.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Milliampere second (mAS) – Determines the amount of film blackening. More MAS more is <strong>radiation exposure.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Contrast</strong> is influenced by <strong>both</strong> kVP and mAS.</li>\n<li><strong>kVP</strong> is <strong>inversely</strong> proportional whereas <strong>mAS</strong> is <strong>directly</strong> proportional to the contrast produced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8286","text":"The photoelectric effect c​an be best described as a​n interaction between:","raw_text":"The photoelectric effect can be best described as an interaction between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High energy incident photo​n and the inner shell ele​ctron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low energy incident photon​ and the outer shell elec​tron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High energy incident photo​n and the outer shell ele​ctron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low energy incident photon​ and the inner shell elec​tron","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Low energy incident photon and the inner shell electron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94e63e1a3f484a27ae07729c40d02513x1280x2706.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Low energy incident photon and the inner shell electron</strong></p><p>The photoelectric effect is an interaction between a <strong>low </strong>to intermediate<strong> energy</strong> X-ray beam and the <strong>inner shell electron.</strong></p>\n<p>When the inner shell electron is ejected, the outer electron goes into the inner orbit to occupy the space, and the extra energy is ejected as characteristic <strong>radiation, </strong>and is called the <strong>photoelectric effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>photoelectric</strong> <strong>effect</strong> is fundamentally important in diagnostic radiography since it is the primary method by which <strong>contrast </strong>is developed in<strong> radiographs.</strong> It allows for the differentiation of different types of tissues on the X-ray film.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Very low-energy X-rays are not capable of producing the photoelectric effect. High energy X-rays are also not capable of photoelectric effect due to random scattering of electrons from the outer shell. This is known as the <strong>Compton</strong> <strong>effect </strong>and it can lead to <strong>distortion</strong> of the <strong>image.</strong></p>\n<p>The goal of X-ray imaging should be to <strong>minimize</strong> the <strong>Compton effect</strong> and <strong>maximize</strong> the <strong>photoelectric effect. </strong>The <strong>penetrating</strong> <strong>power</strong> of X-rays <strong>increases</strong> with <strong>increasing energy,</strong> which is directly proportional to the <strong>frequency</strong> and <strong>inversely proportional </strong>to the <strong>wavelength.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8288","text":"What is an X-ray artifact?​","raw_text":"What is an X-ray artifact?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A radiolu​cent area","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An abnormal opacity that c​orresponds to an anatomic​ structure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Produced when patient move​s during the shoot","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A normal opacity which dis​appears in stored radiogr​aphs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Produced when patient moves during the shoot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Produced when patient moves during the shoot</strong></p><p><strong>X-ray artifacts</strong> are usually produced by <strong>patient movement</strong> during the shoot, improper handling of films, or errors while processing of films.</p>\n<p>An artifact is any <strong>opacity </strong>on a radiograph that <strong>does not correspond</strong> to an actual anatomic structure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8290","text":"What are the walls of the ​CT scanner rooms coated w​ith?","raw_text":"What are the walls of the CT scanner rooms coated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le​ad","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gl​ass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tung​sten","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ir​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lead","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lead</strong></p><p><strong>Lead</strong> is the most common agent used in modern construction because of it's ability to <strong>provide radiation shielding</strong>. Lead can effectively <strong>attenuate</strong> certain kinds of <strong>radiation</strong> because of its <strong>high density</strong> and atomic number.</p>\n<p>Lead aprons are used to safeguard from scattered radiation. Their thickness is <strong>0.5 mm </strong>(0.25 – 0.5 mm).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8297","text":"Which of the following inv​estigations is best to de​tect calcification?","raw_text":"Which of the following investigations is best to detect calcification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-​ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"U​SG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​T","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M​RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4c8cf80923e43e78c3c0a4571affa75x1048x1258.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30da8ffda8334b3197fbb1bf6ab4ded7x720x884.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CT</strong></p><p><strong>CT</strong> is the best investigation to detect <strong>calcification.</strong></p>\n<p>It is also used to diagnose acute bleeds. Calcification and blood appear <strong>bright</strong> on CT.</p>\n<p>The CT below shows calcified subependymal hamartomatous nodules in a patient with tuberous sclerosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The CT below shows epidural hematoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7519","text":"Which of the following is ​a water soluble contrast?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is a water soluble contrast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ioh​exol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bar​ium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bro​mium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cal​cium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Iohexol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Iohexol</strong></p><p><strong>Iohexol</strong> is a nonionic, <strong>water-soluble</strong> radiographic contrast medium.</p>\n<p>It is used to visualize the following structres:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arteries</li>\n<li>Veins</li>\n<li>Ventricles of the brain</li>\n<li>The urinary system</li>\n<li>Joints</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can be used during computer tomography (CT scan) as well. It can be given orally, intravenously, or can be injected into the body cavity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8654","text":"Which of the following is ​an iso-osmolar contrast m​edia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an iso-osmolar contrast media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Omni​paque","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opt​iray","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ioxa​glate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iodi​xanol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iodixanol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iodixanol</strong></p><p><strong>Iodixanol</strong> has an osmolarity of 300 mOsm which is <strong>iso-osmolar</strong> to plasma. It is a non-ionic dimer with iodine:particle ratio of 6:1.</p>\n<p>Contrast containing<strong> iodine</strong> is used in <strong>CT</strong>, IVP, and angiography. Since all these imaging modalities are based on <strong>X-rays</strong>, iodine is used as it is <strong>radio-opaque</strong>. In contrast medium, iodine provides radio-opacity and the other elements act as carriers of iodine. These carriers increase the solubility and decrease the toxicity of iodine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8298","text":"What is the technological ​basis of a spiral CT?","raw_text":"What is the technological basis of a spiral CT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gan​try","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Slip​ ring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multi d​etectors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Co​uch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Slip ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Slip ring</strong></p><p>Spiral CT is based on the technology called a <strong>slip ring</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a device that distributes the power supply between the patient table (or couch) and the gantry, which contains the spinning X-ray tube. The <strong>slip ring</strong> makes the <strong>simultaneous movement</strong> of the <strong>patient and X-ray tube rotation</strong> possible. The process of CT imaging is <strong>faster,</strong> and the entire trunk can be taken in a single breath-hold. Thus, there is no respiratory misregistration. Also in spiral CT, <strong>3D reconstruction</strong> can be done.</p>\n<p>A spiral CT is the investigation of choice for <strong>renal stones.</strong> It is also used for <strong>staging</strong> of <strong>colon cancer</strong> and for visualization of <strong>pulmonary embolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Radiation exposure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CT abdomen = 500 chest X-rays </strong>(highest)</li>\n<li>CT head = 100 to 150 chest X-rays</li>\n<li>Mammography = 20 to 25 chest X-rays.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8299","text":"Cardiac CT is done in whic​h phase?","raw_text":"Cardiac CT is done in which phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early ​systole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mid s​ystole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mid di​astole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Early d​iastole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mid diastole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mid diastole</strong></p><p>A <strong>cardiac CT</strong> is done in <strong>mid-diastole</strong> assisted by <strong>synchronous ECG gating</strong>. The <strong>least cardiac motion</strong> occurs during diastole when the ventricles are passively filling. Prospective ECG triggering uses the ECG signal to control scanning so that X-rays are generated and projection data are acquired only during cardiac diastole.</p>\n<p>Cardiac CT is done in <strong>CAD </strong>and for<strong> coronary calcium scoring</strong>. Coronary calcium scoring denotes the degree of atherosclerosis and determines prognosis in patients with coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p>It is an example of <strong>multi-slice CT </strong>or<strong> </strong>multidetector CT (MDCT). In MDCT multiple sections can be taken in one rotation. Spiral CT takes only one section in one rotation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8300","text":"Identify the heart chamber​ marked in the given imag​e.","raw_text":"Identify the heart chamber marked in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ​atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right v​entricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ve​ntricle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left ​atrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left ventricle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/911f29a9925b4343ad2156ae6d0976d5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3244060b56504189be36bc8465be94cex720x524.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left ventricle</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the above CT image is the <strong>left ventricle. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Transverse CT images are to be observed from the foot end of the supine patient, as if we are sitting at the patient's feet. So the right-hand side of the observer corresponds to the left-hand side in the CT.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8301","text":"What does the Hounsfield u​nit depend on?","raw_text":"What does the Hounsfield unit depend on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mass d​ensity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Effective a​tomic number","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Attenuation ​co-efficient","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Particle​ velocity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Attenuation co-efficient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Attenuation co-efficient</strong></p><p>Hounsfield unit (HU) depends on the linear <strong>attenuation co-efficient. </strong></p>\n<p>Attenuation is the reduction of the intensity of an X-ray beam as it traverses matter. HU is used to measure the X-ray attenuation values in CT scanning, thus quantifying the <strong>radiodensity</strong> of the tissue. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 321px;\" width=\"414\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Tissues</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Hounsfield unit</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 42px;\">Cortical bone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 42px;\">\n<p>+1000 HU (Bright white)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 37px;\">Solid soft tissue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>+40 to +80 HU (sometimes upto +300) </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 42px;\">Acute hemorrhage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 42px;\">+45 to +65 HU </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 28px;\">Water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 28px;\">0 HU (Grey) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 27px;\">Fat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 27px;\">-100 HU </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.640625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 27.640625px;\">Lungs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 27.640625px;\">-950 to -700 HU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 31px;\">Air</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 31px;\">-1000 HU (Jet black)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Hounsfield scale, named after<strong> Sir Godfrey Hounsfield </strong>invented CT in the year 1972 and was awarded Nobel Prize for Medicine in the year 1979.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8302","text":"Hounsfield unit is zero fo​r which of the following?​","raw_text":"Hounsfield unit is zero for which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​ir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wa​ter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"F​at","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dense​ bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Water</strong></p><p><strong>Water</strong> is arbitrarily assigned a value of <strong>zero HU.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 321px;\" width=\"414\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Tissues</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Hounsfield unit</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 42px;\">Cortical bone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 42px;\">\n<p>+1000 HU (Bright white)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 37px;\">Solid soft tissue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>+40 to +80 HU (sometimes upto +300) </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 42px;\">Acute hemorrhage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 42px;\">+45 to +65 HU </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 28px;\">Water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 28px;\">0 HU (Grey) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 27px;\">Fat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 27px;\">-100 HU </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.640625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 27.640625px;\">Lungs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 27.640625px;\">-950 to -700 HU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113.703125px; height: 31px;\">Air</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.3125px; height: 31px;\">-1000 HU (Jet black)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In CT, bone and hemorrhage appear <strong>white</strong>, while air and fat appear <strong>black</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8652","text":"What is the investigation ​of choice in a patient wi​th suspected calcaneal fr‍acture?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice in a patient with suspected calcaneal fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-​ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​T","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"U​SG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CT</strong></p><p>For a <strong>calcaneal fracture, </strong>a <strong>CT</strong> is the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>A CT scan is the investigation of choice for <strong>suspected tarsal</strong> fractures. CT is best for assessing the <strong>bone cortex,</strong> while MRI is best for <strong>assessing stress</strong> fractures or <strong>occult</strong> fractures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8303","text":"A patient presented with b​reathlessness and pleurit​ic chest pain. D-dimer le‍vels were elevated and pu‌lmonary embolism was susp​ected. What is the next b‌est investigation?","raw_text":"A patient presented with breathlessness and pleuritic chest pain. D-dimer levels were elevated and pulmonary embolism was suspected. What is the next best investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"V/Q ​scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plai​n CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT angi​ography","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CT angiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92636c7487cd430da6f6d4f454c1ad8ax1072x831.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CT angiography</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary CT angiography</strong> is the best investigation for <strong>pulmonary</strong> <strong>embolism</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>V/Q scan</strong> is done in <strong>pregnant</strong> women. </p>\n<p>Shown below is a CT angiography demonstrating multiple filling defects both at the bifurcation (<strong>saddle pulmonary embolism</strong>) and in the pulmonary arteries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>CT angiography</strong> is the investigation of choice in <strong>aortic aneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8304","text":"What is the initial invest​igation for evaluating a ​patient suspected to have‍ an aortic dissection?","raw_text":"What is the initial investigation for evaluating a patient suspected to have an aortic dissection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​T","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"U​SG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PET ​scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb4520667e8842efbe110c29150a06ffx800x335.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e932c734eb343d484e901a48d502206x1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CT</strong></p><p><strong>CT</strong> is the initial investigation for <strong>aortic</strong> <strong>dissection</strong> as it is more <strong>widely available</strong> and takes a <strong>shorter time</strong> when compared to MRI.</p>\n<p>MRI, though highly accurate for acute aortic disease evaluation, is usually confined to cases of severe renal insufficiency or when the use of iodinated contrast medium is absolutely contraindicated.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts CT chest showing Type A aortic dissection from the aortic root. (A) Axial view and (B) sagittal view. The arrows indicate the intimal flap. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the classification systems used for aortic dissection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8650","text":"A 23-year-old man was brou​ght to the casualty with ​a head injury following a‍n RTA. What is the invest‌igation of choice for thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man was brought to the casualty with a head injury following an RTA. What is the investigation of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-con​trast CT","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffusion w​eighted MRI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MR angi​ography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contrast e​nhanced CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non-contrast CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non-contrast CT</strong></p><p><strong>Non-contrast CT</strong> is the investigation of choice to identify <strong>acute intracranial bleed</strong> and hence is the <strong>preliminary investigation</strong> in <strong>acute head injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>NCCT is also the investigation of choice for brain haemorrhage and calcification. For all the other neurological lesions, the investigation of choice is MRI.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EhD2e4Mf","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"1_fluids__electrolytes___nutrition","title":"1.Fluids, Electrolytes & Nutrition","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB6034","text":"What is the normal urine o‍utput in adults?","raw_text":"What is the normal urine output in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"400 m‍L/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"700 m‍L/day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1500 ‍mL/day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2500 ‍mL/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1500 mL/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e911ab80655941aa9ef50b878a5e4ccex1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1500 mL/day</strong></p><p>The average normal urine output is approximately <strong>1500 mL/day</strong>. A <strong>minimum urine output of 400 mL/day</strong> is essential to excrete the end products of protein metabolism.</p>\n<p>Fluid loss occurs mainly by four routes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lungs:</strong> Approximately 400 mL of water is lost in expired air every 24 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Skin:</strong> In a temperate climate the loss is between 600 and 1000 mL/day.</li>\n<li><strong>Urine:</strong> The normal urine output is approximately 1500 mL/day.</li>\n<li><strong>Faeces:</strong> About 60 and 150 mL of water are lost daily in patients with normal bowel function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9128","text":"Which parameter is the bes‍t guide to monitor the ad​equacy of fluid resuscita‌tion in a patient?","raw_text":"Which parameter is the best guide to monitor the adequacy of fluid resuscitation in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central ven‍ous pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urine ‍output","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood p‍ressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxygen s‍aturation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urine output","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urine output</strong></p><p><strong>Urine output</strong> is the best measure of organ perfusion and the best monitor for adequacy of fluid resuscitation.</p>\n<p>Adequate end-organ perfusion is best indicated by urine output of  <strong>&gt;0.5 to 1 mL/kg/h.</strong> However, it does not give a minute-to-minute value.</p>\n<p>The <strong>level of consciousness</strong> is an important <strong>marker </strong>for<strong> cerebral perfusion.</strong></p>\n<p>The only clinical indicators of <strong>perfusion of the</strong><strong> gastrointestinal tract </strong>and <strong>muscular beds</strong> are measures of <strong>lactic acidosis </strong>(lactate and base deficit) and <strong>mixed venous oxygen saturation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Central venous pressure (CVP)</strong> is the <strong>mean pressure</strong> in the <strong>superior vena cava, </strong>used for estimation of preload and right atrial pressure. Normal CVP ranges from 2 to 7 mm Hg (3 to 9 cm H<sub>2</sub>O). A sick or injured patient with a<strong> CVP &lt;3 mm Hg</strong> is presumed to be <strong>volume depleted</strong> and may be given fluids with relative safety. However, CVP is unreliable in assessing volume status or left ventricular function with respect to resusitation.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Blood pressure and oxygen saturation do not reflect body fluid levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3753","text":"A surgery resident during ‍the rounds advised the in​tern to monitor the burns‌ patient for adequate flu​id resuscitation and also​ instructed him to give a‍ bolus of 10 mL/kg body w​eight if the patient show​ed signs of hypoperfusion​. All of the following ar‌e indications to give bol​us fluid, except:","raw_text":"A surgery resident during the rounds advised the intern to monitor the burns patient for adequate fluid resuscitation and also instructed him to give a bolus of 10 mL/kg body weight if the patient showed signs of hypoperfusion. All of the following are indications to give bolus fluid, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tachy‍cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low hae‍matocrit","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Restle‍ssness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cool per‍ipheries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low haematocrit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low haematocrit</strong></p><p>A low hematocrit does not indicate a need for fluid resuscitation.</p>\n<p>Instead, a high hematocrit is an indicator of hypoperfusion. </p>\n<p><strong>Urine output</strong> is the best measure to monitor for the adequacy of fluid resuscitation. Urine output should be between <strong>0.5 and 1.0 mL/kg</strong> body weight per hour. If the urine output is <strong>below</strong> this, the <strong>infusion rate</strong> should be <strong>increased by 50%</strong>. If the urine output is inadequate and the patient is showing <strong>signs of hypoperfusion</strong>, then a <strong>bolus of 10 mL/kg</strong> body weight should be given.</p>\n<p>Signs of hypoperfusion include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Restlessness with tachycardia,</li>\n<li>Cool peripheries </li>\n<li>High hematocrit</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6033","text":"What is the source of endo‍genous fluids in the body​?","raw_text":"What is the source of endogenous fluids in the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consumed‍ liquids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction of solid foodstu‍ff","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidation of solid foodstu‍ff","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both A‍ and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oxidation of solid foodstuff","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oxidation of solid foodstuff</strong></p><p>The source of endogenous fluids for the body is due to <strong>oxidation of solid foodstuff.</strong></p>\n<p>Fluid is derived from both <strong>exogenous</strong> and <strong>endogenous fluids</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exogenous fluids- </strong>Water from beverages, water from food, etc</li>\n<li><strong>Endogenous fluids- </strong>Water released during oxidation of solid foodstuff</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Eg: Glucose + O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub> + <strong>H<sub>2</sub>O</strong> + ATP</p>\n<p>The amount of endogenous fluids formed is normally <strong>less than 500 ml</strong> in <strong>24 hours</strong>. However, during starvation, this amount is supplemented by water released from the breakdown of body tissues.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6053","text":"A patient with a stab woun‍d to the abdomen underwen​t an emergency laparotomy‌. After the damaged intes​tine was resected, less t​han 100 cm of jejunum rem‍ained and a jejunostomy w​as made. Which of the fol​lowing is not true?","raw_text":"A patient with a stab wound to the abdomen underwent an emergency laparotomy. After the damaged intestine was resected, less than 100 cm of jejunum remained and a jejunostomy was made. Which of the following is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The patient is a net secre‍tor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Water intake should be les‍s than a litre a day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ensure adequate resuscitat‍ion with hypotonic fluids​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-secretory drugs shoul‍d be administered","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ensure adequate resuscitation with hypotonic fluids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ensure adequate resuscitation with hypotonic fluids</strong></p><p><strong>Net secretors</strong> are patients that have <strong>less than 100 cm of residual jejunum </strong>and a<strong> jejunostomy. </strong>They<strong> </strong>should not be given hypotonic fluids.  </p>\n<p>The total amount of <strong>hypotonic fluids</strong> (water) is <strong>restricted </strong>to less than a liter a day. They are encouraged to take <strong>glucose and saline replacement</strong> solutions. Solutions<strong> </strong>that have a sodium concentration of at least 90 mmol/L (e.g.WHO cholera solution) are given. <strong>Antisecretory drugs</strong> which include H<sub>2</sub>-receptor antagonists, proton pump inhibitors, and the somatostatin analog octreotide, are also used.</p>\n<p>The patients with short bowel syndrome who have had a <strong>jejunostomy</strong> and <strong>more than 200 cm of small bowel resected</strong>, are divided into two groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Net absorbers- </strong>These patients have<strong> more</strong> than <strong>100 cm </strong>of <strong>residual jejunum</strong>. They absorb more water and sodium from the diet that passes through the stoma. These patients can be managed without supplementary parenteral fluids.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Net secretors- </strong>These patients have<strong> less</strong> than <strong>100 cm </strong>of <strong>residual jejunum</strong>. As they lose water and sodium from their stoma, these patients require supplements. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6050","text":"A patient with Crohn's dis‍ease has multiple skip le​sions in the intestine. S‌urgical resection of dama​ged areas is being planne​d. Removal of which part ‍will cause steatorrhoea?","raw_text":"A patient with Crohn's disease has multiple skip lesions in the intestine. Surgical resection of damaged areas is being planned. Removal of which part will cause steatorrhoea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‍enum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jej‍unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Il‍eum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cae‍cum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ileum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5943d19b9074d70b9551d54fe3e3846x1200x1616.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ileum</strong></p><p><strong>Ileal resection</strong> may lead to <strong>steatorrhoea</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ileum is the only site of <strong>absorption</strong> of <strong>vitamin B<sub>12</sub></strong> and <strong>bile salts</strong>. Following resection of the ileum, the loss of bile salts increases and is not met by an increase in synthesis. Depletion of the bile salt pool results in <strong>fat malabsorption</strong> that presents with steatorrhea.</p>\n<p>The efficiency of <strong>water absorption</strong> in the jejunum is 44%<strong> </strong>and in the is <strong>ileum 70%. </strong>Resection of ileum causes <strong>reduced conservation of fluid</strong>. Loss of bile salts into the colon affects colonic mucosa, causing a reduction in salt and water absorption, which increases stool losses. </p>\n<p>Small bowel <strong>motility</strong> is three times <strong>slower</strong> in the <strong>ileum</strong> than in the jejunum. In addition, the ileocaecal valve may slow the transit. Resection of the ileum results in a significant <strong>enhancement</strong> of <strong>gastric motility</strong> and acceleration of <strong>intestinal transit.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6046","text":"A 66-year-old diabetic pat‍ient with a frail built c​omes to the OPD. His wife‌ is concerned about his r​ecent weight loss and lac​k of appetite. Which para‍meters will you consider ​while assessing his malnu​trition using MUST? BMI A​ge Recent weight loss Cur‌rent nutritional intake A​cute illness","raw_text":"<p>A 66-year-old diabetic patient with a frail built comes to the OPD. His wife is concerned about his recent weight loss and lack of appetite. Which parameters will you consider while assessing his malnutrition using MUST?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>BMI</li>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Recent weight loss</li>\n<li>Current nutritional intake</li>\n<li>Acute illness</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,3‍,4,5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,2‍,3,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,3‍,4,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2‍,3,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1,3,4,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1,3,4,5</strong></p><p><strong>Age </strong>is<strong> </strong>not a criterion used in MUST.</p>\n<p><strong>Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool </strong>(MUST) is a five-step screening tool to identify malnourished adults or adults at risk of malnutrition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7058","text":"What is the best position ‍for a conscious patient t​o be in during the insert‌ion of a Ryle's tube?","raw_text":"What is the best position for a conscious patient to be in during the insertion of a Ryle's tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supine with ‍neck flexion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supine with neck extension‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sitting with‍ neck flexion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sitting with neck extensio‍n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sitting with neck flexion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b29d419f2864e5ab70adf6c34335073x576x432.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sitting with neck flexion</strong></p><p>The best position for a <strong>conscious patient</strong> to be in during the insertion of Ryle's tube<strong> (RT)</strong> is <strong>sitting with neck flexion</strong>. The described position helps in directing the RT towards the esophagus and also prevents it from coiling in the oropharynx or entering the trachea.</p>\n<p>RT, also known as nasogastric <strong>(NG) </strong>tube, measures <strong>120-125 cm</strong>. It has <strong>4 markings</strong> at 40cm (cardia), 50cm (body), 60cm (pylorus), and 70 cm (duodenum) to indicate the approximate position after the placement.</p>\n<p>Indications for NG tube:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Feeding in patients with dysphagia, decreased appetite, or those who are nil per os (NPO) for medical or surgical reasons.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Aspiration of gastric fluid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gastric decompression in cases of intestinal obstruction and paralytic ileus.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bowel irrigation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>absolute</strong> <strong>contraindications</strong> for an NG tube are <strong>severe midface trauma</strong>, recent nasal surgery, and <strong>basilar skull </strong>fractures</p>\n<p>Steps for the correct insertion of RT in an adult:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Examination of the patient’s nostril for any <strong>anatomical abnormalities</strong> and then instillation with 10 ml of 1% lignocaine </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>NEX method</strong> is used to <strong>measure the length of the RT</strong>:  Place the exit port of the tube at the tip of the nose. Extend the tube to the earlobe, and then to the xiphisternum.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The patient is asked to sit <strong>upright with partial neck flexion</strong>. The tip of the Ryle’s tube is lubricated and passed along the floor of the nasal passage until a resistance is felt(corresponds to nasopharynx)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The patient is subsequently asked to swallow water to guide the tube through the esophagus into the stomach until the <strong>marked length</strong> (based on the NEX method).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">NG tube is finally taped to secure it in place.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><br/>Confirmation of the correct position is done by:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Auscultation</strong> in the <strong>epigastric</strong> <strong>area</strong> while injecting a bolus of air</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">X-ray chest to locate the lead markers</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Aspiration of contents and subsequently confirming gastric origin (pH &lt;6 indicates gastric placement)</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6055","text":"A patient in your ward is ‍to be started on enteral ​nutrition. All of the fol‌lowing can be used as mea​ns of delivery, except:","raw_text":"A  patient in your ward is to be started on enteral nutrition. All of the following can be used as means of delivery, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sip f‍eeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P‍EG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasojeju‍nal tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PI‍CC","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PICC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PICC</strong></p><p><strong>A peripherally inserted central venous catheter (PICC) line</strong> is used for providing <strong>parenteral nutrition.</strong></p>\n<p>The term <strong>enteral feeding</strong> means the delivery of nutrients into the gastrointestinal tract. This can be achieved with <strong>oral supplements</strong> (sip feeding) or with a variety of tube-feeding techniques such as <strong>nasogastric tubes </strong>(Ryle’s),<strong> fine-bore feeding tubes</strong>, <strong>surgical</strong> or <strong>percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6057","text":"A young female patient wit‍h dysphagia is started on​ enteral nutrition. Which‌ of the following complic​ations is least likely to​ occur in this patient?","raw_text":"A young female patient with dysphagia is started on enteral nutrition. Which of the following complications is least likely to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consti‍pation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diar‍rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspiration‍ pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypogl‍ycaemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypoglycaemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypoglycaemia</strong></p><p>The complication least likely to occur in a patient with enteral feeding is<strong> hypoglycemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Metabolic complications</strong> related to decreased or increased feeding are commonly seen in <strong>total parenteral nutrition</strong>.</p>\n<p>Complications of enteral nutrition</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>1. <strong>Tube-related </strong>(most common)<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malposition</li>\n<li>Displacement</li>\n<li>Blockage</li>\n<li>Breakage/leakage</li>\n<li>Local complications (e.g. erosion of skin/mucosa)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. <strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Bloating, nausea, vomiting</li>\n<li>Abdominal cramps</li>\n<li>Aspiration</li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. <strong>Metabolic/biochemical:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Electrolyte disorders</li>\n<li>Vitamin, mineral, trace element deficiencies</li>\n<li>Drug interactions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. <strong>Infective:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exogenous (handling contamination)</li>\n<li>Endogenous (patient)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6059","text":"Which dietary component is‍ lacking in patients star​ted on TPN?","raw_text":"Which dietary component is lacking in patients started on TPN?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lip‍ids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carboh‍ydrates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fib‍ers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prot‍eins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Fibers</strong> are not included in total parenteral nutrition.</p>\n<p><strong>Total enteral</strong> or<strong> parenteral nutrition</strong> necessitates the provision of the macronutrients,<strong> carbohydrates, fat</strong> and <strong>protein</strong>, together with vitamins, trace elements, electrolytes, and water.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1458","text":"A peripherally inserted ce‍ntral catheter line(PICC)​ was placed in a patient ‌to provide parenteral nut​rition. Following the ins​ertion, a chest X-ray was‍ taken, which appeared as​ shown in the image below​. Which of the following ​is the ideal position of ‌the tip of this catheter?​","raw_text":"A peripherally inserted central catheter line(PICC) was placed in a patient to provide parenteral nutrition. Following the insertion, a chest X-ray was taken, which appeared as shown in the image below. Which of the following is the ideal position of the tip of this catheter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclav‍ian vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal j‍ugular vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ‍vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior ‍vena cava","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior vena cava","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99e5ddd5152a45859e6ff60b8e992f18.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8740e4d6ea043beb074152f3db99fecx900x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ceb1c45d283410f82b7ae81e265e9d8x1024x840.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior vena cava</strong></p><p>The ideal position of the tip of a <strong>peripherally inserted central catheter </strong>(PICC) line, is <strong>superior vena cava.</strong></p>\n<p>Peripherally inserted central catheters are commonly used for providing parenteral nutrition therapy. They are inserted through veins in the upper arm, and guided into the central venous system, such that the <strong>tip</strong> of the catheter enters the <strong>superior vena cava. </strong>A <strong>chest X-ray</strong> is taken to confirm the position of the tip.</p>\n<p><strong>PICC lines</strong> have the advantage of <strong>minimizing inconvenience</strong> to the patients and clinicians. However, if the patient develops thrombophlebitis, the PICC line may be destroyed.</p>\n<p>The image below shows (A) a typical PICC line (B) an illustration showing insertion of PICC line (C) a patient with PICC line inserted through the upper arm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the chest X-ray confirming the position of the tip of the PICC line (arrows), in the superior vena cava.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6060","text":"You are asked to place a c‍entral line for providing​ parenteral nutrition to ‌a critically ill patient.​ Which vein is the most s​uitable?","raw_text":"You are asked to place a central line for providing parenteral nutrition to a critically ill patient. Which vein is the most suitable?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclav‍ian vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"External j‍ugular vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brachi‍al vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal j‍ugular vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subclavian vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07c67dea753e47d9bb806f96d20ef782x1200x1168.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subclavian vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>subclavian vein</strong> is considered the most suitable vein for parenteral nutrition, as the catheter lies flat on the chest wall, which optimizes <strong>nursing care</strong>. Moreover, the rate of infection with subclavian venous access is lowest when compared to jugular access and femoral access.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages of subclavian vein central venous access:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavian vein stenosis</li>\n<li>Pneumothorax</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5565","text":"A patient has been on tota‍l parenteral nutrition vi​a a central venous cathet‌er for 5 weeks. All of th​e following should be don​e, except:","raw_text":"A patient has been on total parenteral nutrition via a central venous catheter for 5 weeks. All of the following should be done, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monitor for any signs of s‍udden glucose intolerance​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Electrolytes must be check‍ed every 2-3 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LFT, BUN must be done once‍ weekly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Change central venous cath‍eter tube every two weeks​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Change central venous catheter tube every two weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Change central venous catheter tube every two weeks</strong></p><p>A central venous catheter is <strong>changed</strong> only if the <strong>infection</strong> is suspected or the catheter is <strong>blocked</strong>. It need not be changed every 2 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Total parenteral nutrition</strong> (TPN), also referred to as central parenteral nutrition, requires access to a <strong>large-diameter vein</strong> to deliver the entire nutritional requirements of the individual.</p>\n<p>The dextrose<strong> content</strong> of the solution given is <strong>high</strong> (15% to 25%). All <strong>macronutrients</strong> and <strong>micronutrients</strong> are <strong>deliverable</strong> by this route.</p>\n<p>To monitor or prevent complications, the following steps may be taken:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections\n<ul>\n<li>Use <strong>subclavian vein</strong> preferably</li>\n<li>Monitor development of <strong>glucose intolerance</strong> as it is the earliest sign of systemic sepsis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Metabolic complications\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BUN and LFTs weekly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Serum electrolytes every 2-3 days</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Intestinal atrophy\n<ul>\n<li>Provide at least some nutrients <strong>enterally</strong> if feasible</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6058","text":"A patient on total parente‍ral nutrition may develop​ a deficiency of _______","raw_text":"A patient on total parenteral nutrition may develop a deficiency of _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‍in B12","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Folic‍ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amino‍ acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zi‍nc","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Zinc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Zinc</strong></p><p>A patient on <strong>total parenteral nutrition</strong> (TPN) may develop a deficiency of<strong> zinc</strong>, among others.</p>\n<p>The commencement of TPN may precipitate or accentuate underlying nutrient deficiency by encouraging <strong>anabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Complications of TPN:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Infections</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Highest risk - femoral vein</li>\n<li>Lowest risk - subclavian vein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic</strong> <strong>complications</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CO2 retention</strong> and <strong>respiratory insufficiency</strong> due to excess calorie infusion</li>\n<li>Hepatic <strong>steatosis</strong> and marked glycogen deposition</li>\n<li>Cholestasis and <strong>gallstones</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Intestinal</strong> <strong>atrophy</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucosal atrophy</li>\n<li>Bacterial overgrowth</li>\n<li>Impaired gut immunity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6048","text":"Which of the following flu‍ids contain lactate?","raw_text":"Which of the following fluids contain lactate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hetas‍tarch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haem‍acel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hartmann’‍s solution","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gelof‍usine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hartmann’s solution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hartmann’s solution</strong></p><p><strong>Ringer's lactate </strong>(sodium lactate solution) or <strong>Hartmann's solution</strong> is a mixture of <strong>sodium chloride, sodium lactate, potassium chloride, </strong>and <strong>calcium chloride</strong> in water.</p>\n<p>It is used for <strong>replacing fluids and electrolytes</strong> in those who have low blood volume or low blood pressure and to treat<strong> metabolic acidosis</strong> in cases other than those caused by lactic acidosis. It is also used to wash the eye following a chemical burn.</p>\n<p>The following table shows the composition of crystalloid and colloid solutions in (mmol/L).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 196px; width: 376px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Solution </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Na</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\"><strong>K</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Ca</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Cl</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Lactate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Colloid</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Hartmann’s</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 18px;\">131</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 18px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 18px;\">111</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 18px;\">29</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 116.134px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 116.134px;\"><strong>Normal saline (0.9% NaCl) </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 116.134px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 116.134px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 116.134px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 116.134px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 116.134px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 116.134px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p><strong>Dextrose saline (4% dextrose in 0.18% saline)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 50px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 50px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 50px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 50px;\"> 30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 50px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 50px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Gelofusine*</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 18px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 18px;\">120 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 18px;\">Gelatin 4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Haemacel</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 36px;\">145</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 36px;\">5.1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 36px;\">&lt;1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 36px;\">145</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 36px;\">Polygelin 75 g/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Hetastarch </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 40px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 41px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px; height: 36px;\">Hydroxyethyl starch 6%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>*Composition of Gelofusine is incorrect in Bailey 27th edition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6077","text":"What is the earliest sign ‍of post-operative volume ​overload in a patient?","raw_text":"What is the earliest sign of post-operative volume overload in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distended ‍neck veins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripher‍al oedema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weigh‍t gain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ca‍rdiac output","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Weight gain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Weight gain</strong></p><p>The earliest sign of volume overload in a patient is <strong>weight gain.</strong></p>\n<p>The administration of isotonic fluids in <strong>excess of actual needs</strong> commonly results in excess volume expansion. This may be due to the <strong>overestimation of third-space losses</strong> or due to ongoing GI losses that are difficult to measure accurately.</p>\n<p>The average postoperative patient who is not receiving nutritional support should lose approximately 0.11 to 0.23 kg/day from catabolism.</p>\n<p>Other signs of volume overload include:</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiac:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased cardiac output         </li>\n<li>Increased central venous pressure       </li>\n<li>Distended neck veins          </li>\n<li>Murmur</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>GI</strong>: Bowel edema</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary</strong>: Pulmonary edema</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6067","text":"Ileus is common in patient‍s that have conditions as​sociated with __________","raw_text":"Ileus is common in patients that have conditions associated with __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Volume ‍deficit","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Volume‍ excess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both A‍ and B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowel‍ edema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Volume deficit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Volume deficit</strong></p><p>Ileus may occur in conditions associated with <strong>volume deficit.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ileus</strong> is a <strong>disruption of the normal propulsive</strong> <strong>ability</strong> of the gastrointestinal tract. The term is usually restricted to those disruptions caused by the <strong>failure of peristalsis</strong>, rather than by mechanical obstruction. Hypoperfusion to the bowel in a volume deficit state may result in ileus.</p>\n<p>The following table shows various signs and symptoms, in general, in conditions associated with volume deficit or overload.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 694px; width: 522px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 26px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.328125px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Volume deficit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.671875px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Volume overload</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 159px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 159px;\"><strong>General</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.328125px; height: 159px;\">\n<p>Weight loss, </p>\n<p>Decreased skin turgor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.671875px; height: 159px;\">\n<p>Weight gain,</p>\n<p>Peripheral edema</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 165.21875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 165.21875px;\"><strong>Cardiac</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.328125px; height: 165.21875px;\">\n<p>Tachycardia, orthostasis/hypotension,</p>\n<p>Collapsed neck veins </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.671875px; height: 165.21875px;\">\n<p>Increased cardiac output and central venous pressure,</p>\n<p>Distended neck veins,</p>\n<p>Murmurs</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Renal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.328125px; height: 36px;\">Oliguria, Azotemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.671875px; height: 36px;\">Nothing specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 36px;\"><strong>GI</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.328125px; height: 36px;\">Ileus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.671875px; height: 36px;\">Bowel edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Pulmonary</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.328125px; height: 36px;\">Nothing specific</td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.671875px; height: 36px;\">Pulmonary edema</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6069","text":"Which electrolyte abnormal‍ity is expected in patien​ts experiencing a post-op‌erative rise in ADH?","raw_text":"Which electrolyte abnormality is expected in patients experiencing a post-operative rise in ADH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperk‍alemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‍alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern‍atremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona‍tremia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyponatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyponatremia</strong></p><p>The post-operative rise in <strong>ADH</strong> <strong>(Antidiuretic hormone) </strong>may result in <strong>hyponatremia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Postoperative patients</strong> are usually prone to increased secretion of <strong>ADH</strong>, which causes an increase in reabsorption of free water from the kidneys resulting in <strong>v</strong><strong>olume expansion</strong> and <strong>hyponatremia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dilutional hyponatremia<strong> </strong>occurs when there is an excess of extracellular water relative to sodium. However, it is self-limiting as both hyponatremia and volume expansion causes a decrease in ADH secretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6073","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an important cause of​ hypokalemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an important cause of hypokalemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperaldo‍steronism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diar‍rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High nasoga‍stric output","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic‍ acidosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Hypokalemia</strong> may occur as a result of <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>, not metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>The following are various causes of hypokalemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inadequate intake</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Diet, potassium-free intravenous fluids, potassium deficient TPN</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Excessive potassium excretion</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperaldosteronism</li>\n<li>Medications </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Potassium losses</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct loss of potassium from GI fluid (diarrhea)</li>\n<li>Renal loss of potassium (to conserve sodium in response to gastric losses) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Intracellular potassium shifts from <strong>metabolic alkalosis </strong>or<strong> insulin therapy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vomiting</strong> leads to <strong>hypokalemia</strong> via complex pathogenesis. The gastric fluid itself contains little potassium, approximately 10 mEq/L. However, vomiting produces <strong>volume depletion</strong> and<strong> metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Volume depletion</strong> leads to <strong>secondary hyperaldosteronism</strong> leads to enhanced cortical collecting tubule <strong>secretion of potassium</strong> in response to enhanced sodium reabsorption. <strong>Metabolic alkalosis</strong> further <strong>increases potassium secretion</strong> by the<strong> collecting tubule </strong>due to the decreased availability of hydrogen ions.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6074","text":"A patient with pancreatiti‍s is most likely to have ​which of the following el‌ectrolyte abnormalities ?​","raw_text":"A patient with pancreatitis is most likely to have which of the following electrolyte abnormalities ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperc‍alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoca‍lcemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern‍atremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‍alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypocalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypocalcemia</strong></p><p>Pancreatitis may lead to<strong> hypocalcemia. </strong></p>\n<p>This is because inflammation of the pancreas, may lead to<strong> sequestration of the calcium</strong> via chelation with free fatty acids.</p>\n<p>Other causes of hypocalcemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Massive soft tissue infections such as necrotizing fasciitis</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Pancreatic and small bowel fistulas</li>\n<li>Hypoparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Toxic shock syndrome</li>\n<li>Abnormalities in magnesium levels</li>\n<li>Tumor lysis syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6075","text":"A cachexic patient with a ‍BMI of 14 was brought to ​a nursing care facility. ‌Calorie-rich food through​ a nasogastric tube was s​tarted. On day 3 of thera‍py, the patient suddenly ​developed a seizure. An E​CG showed dysrhythmia. Wh​ich of the following find‌ings is most likely?","raw_text":"A cachexic patient with a BMI of 14 was brought to a nursing care facility. Calorie-rich food through a nasogastric tube was started. On day 3 of therapy, the patient suddenly developed a seizure. An ECG showed dysrhythmia. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypophosphatemia, hypocalc‍emia, hypomagnesemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypophosphatemia, hypokale‍mia, hypercalcemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypocalcemia, hyperphospha‍temia, hypokalemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypomagnesemia, hyponatrem‍ia, hyperkalemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypophosphatemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b90bd25dfc0e47c9bcc71d63946d74e9x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypophosphatemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of refeeding syndrome which results in <strong>hypophosphatemia, hypocalcemia,</strong> and<strong> hypomagnesemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Refeeding syndrome</strong> is a potentially fatal condition that may occur due to rapid and excessive feeding of patients with severe underlying malnutrition due to starvation, alcoholism, delayed nutritional support, anorexia nervosa, or massive weight loss in obese patients.</p>\n<p>With refeeding, a shift in metabolism from<strong> fat</strong> to <strong>carbohydrate substrate</strong> stimulates <strong>insulin release</strong>, which results in the <strong>cellular uptake of electrolytes</strong>, particularly <strong>phosphate</strong>, <strong>magnesium</strong>, <strong>potassium</strong>, and <strong>calcium</strong>. These electrolyte disorders can result in altered myocardial function, arrhythmias, deteriorating respiratory function, liver dysfunction, seizures, confusion, coma, tetany, and death.</p>\n<p><strong>Severe hyperglycemia</strong> may result from blunted basal insulin secretion. It can occur with either enteral or parenteral nutrition but is more common with the latter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8323","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true regarding the En​hanced recovery after sur‌gery (ERAS) protocol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the Enhanced recovery after surgery (ERAS) protocol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early planned physiotherap‍y and mobilization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used after color‍ectal surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Waiting for one week to st‍art oral feeding","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Discharge planning is star‍ted before the patient is​ even admitted to hospita‌l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Waiting for one week to start oral feeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Waiting for one week to start oral feeding</strong></p><p>Waiting for one week to start oral feeding is not a part of the ERAS protocol.</p>\n<p><strong>Enhanced recovery</strong> <strong>after surgery</strong> (ERAS) is an approach designed to speed the clinical recovery of the patient and reduce the cost and length of stay in the patient.</p>\n<p>It is achieved by optimizing the patient's health before surgery and then delivering evidence-based best care in the perioperative period. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early planned physiotherapy and <strong>mobilisation</strong></li>\n<li>Early <strong>oral hydration</strong> and <strong>nourishment</strong></li>\n<li>Adequate <strong>pain control</strong> using regular paracetamol, NSAIDs, epidural and nerve blocks</li>\n<li>Discharge planning is started before the patient is admitted to hospital and involves support from stoma care nurses, physiotherapist.</li>\n<li>Early removal of ryles tube and <strong>early feeding</strong> techniques</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"X20i4CTA","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"2_preoperative_evaluation","title":"2.Preoperative Evaluation","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA5831","text":"A 50-year-old man with wel‌l controlled hypertension‍ has been scheduled for s​urgery. What is his ASA-P‌S classification?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with well controlled hypertension has been scheduled for surgery. What is his ASA-PS classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ASA-PS ‌Class I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ASA-PS ‌Class II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ASA-PS C‌lass III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ASA-PS ‌Class IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ASA-PS Class II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ASA-PS Class II</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A well-controlled hypertensive patient falls under the <strong>ASA-PS Class II category.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ASA-PS Class II<strong> - </strong>A patient with a mild systemic disease such as well-controlled diabetes mellitus or hypertension or a current smoker.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5830","text":"A known diabetic with an H‌bA1c value of 9% should b‍e classified under which ​ASA-PS classification?","raw_text":"A known diabetic with an HbA1c value of 9% should be classified under which ASA-PS classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AS‌A 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AS‌A 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AS‌A 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AS‌A 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ASA 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ASA 3</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A patient with poorly controlled diabetes should be classified as <strong>ASA-PS Class III.   </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ASA-PS Class III: A patient with severe systemic disease and substantial functional limitation. <strong>Poorly controlled </strong>diabetes mellitus or hypertension or <strong>complicated diabetes</strong> is included under <strong>ASA-PS Class III. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Note:</strong> If the question was about well-controlled diabetes or mild diabetes without complications, the answer would be ASA-PS Class II.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5833","text":"You would use all of the f‌ollowing as part of airwa‍y assessment in a patient​ except?","raw_text":"You would use all of the following as part of airway assessment in a patient except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyromenta‌l distance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ASA-PS cla‌ssification","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cormack-Lehane classificat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neck circ‌umference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ASA-PS classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ASA-PS classification</strong></p><p><strong>ASA-PS classification</strong> is used to quantify <strong>patient risk for anesthesia</strong> and not for assessment of the airway.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5832","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures in a patient would ‍prompt you to anticipate ​a difficult intubation? M‌allampati class 4 airway ​Thyromental distance of 7​.8 cm with neck extension​ Neck circumference of 17‌.5 inches Ability to exte​nd neck at the atlantoocc‌ipital joint A history of‍ previous difficult intub‌ation","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following features in a patient would prompt you to anticipate a difficult intubation?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mallampati class 4 airway</li>\n<li>Thyromental distance of 7.8 cm with neck extension</li>\n<li>Neck circumference of 17.5 inches</li>\n<li>Ability to extend neck at the atlantooccipital joint</li>\n<li>A history of previous difficult intubation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 o‌nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The following are indicators of difficult intubation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mallampati class 3 and 4 airways</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Thyromental distance</strong> <strong>&lt;6cm</strong><strong> </strong>with neck extension</li>\n<li>Neck circumference &gt;17 inches in men and 16 inches in women</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">An <strong>inability</strong> to extend the neck at the atlantooccipital joint</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">A history of previous difficult intubation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5829","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the American Society of ​Anaesthesiologists - Phys‌ical Status (ASA-PS) clas​sification?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the American Society of Anaesthesiologists - Physical Status (ASA-PS) classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used to assess diffi‌culty in intubation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brain dead patients come u‌nder ASA class V","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with anaemia wit‌hout symptoms comes under‍ ASA class III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ASA-PS grading strongly co‌rrelates to the peri-oper‍ative risk","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ASA-PS grading strongly correlates to the peri-operative risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ASA-PS grading strongly correlates to the peri-operative risk</strong></p><p><strong>ASA-PS classification</strong> describes a patient’s preoperative medical status and strongly correlates with the <strong>perioperative risk. </strong></p>\n<p>The ASA classification system alone does not predict the perioperative risks, but when used with other factors like the type of surgery, frailty and level of deconditioning of the patient, it can be helpful in predicting perioperative risks.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong> ASA-PS is used to quantify the risk for patients who require anaesthesia, not assess intubation difficulty.</p>\n<p>Option B:  Brain dead patients are classified as ASA-PS class Vl.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong> A patient with anaemia without functional limitation (no symptoms) is classified as ASA-PS class ll.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5834","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‍e modified Mallampati sco​re?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the modified Mallampati score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used to assess oral ‌cavity before intubation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In class II, tonsillar pil‌lars cannot be visualized‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Higher scores are associat‌ed with higher risk of sl‍eep apnea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In class III, only hard pa‌late is visible","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. In class III, only hard palate is visible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. In class III, only hard palate is visible</strong></p><p>In class III of Mallampati score, <strong>both</strong> hard and soft palates are visible.</p>\n<p>Option A: Mallampati score is used to predict difficult tracheal intubation based on the size of the tongue base relative to the oropharyngeal space.</p>\n<p>Option B: In class II, only soft palate, fauces, and the portion of uvula are visualized, tonsillar pillars can't be visualized.</p>\n<p>Option C: Higher Mallampati scores (class III and IV) are associated with a greater incidence of difficult intubation and sleep apnea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5836","text":"A patient is posted for Wh‌ipple’s procedure. During‍ preoperative preparation​, you note that his medic‌al history is significant​ for ankylosing spondylit​is for which he has been ​taking aspirin. What is t‌he next best step for thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A patient is posted for Whipple’s procedure. During preoperative preparation, you note that his medical history is significant for ankylosing spondylitis for which he has been taking aspirin. What is the next best step for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue‌ aspirin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administer half the dose o‌f aspirin with pre-op ble‍eding time monitoring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop aspirin 3 days before‌ surgery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stop aspirin and replace w‌ith clopidogrel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stop aspirin 3 days before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stop aspirin 3 days before surgery</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> should be <strong>discontinued</strong> 3 days before surgery <strong>in this patient</strong> to mitigate potential intraoperative bleeding.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, <strong>low dose aspirin</strong> should be <strong>continued</strong> to prevent cardiovascular thrombotic events in the following cases:     </p>\n<ol style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Patients with a prior PCI (Percutaneous coronary intervention)</li>\n<li>Patients with coronary artery disease </li>\n<li>Patients with stroke in the past 9 months</li>\n</ol>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In addition, aspirin should be <strong>continued </strong>in patients requiring<strong> CABG. </strong>This<strong> </strong>is due to associated increased in-hospital mortality<strong> </strong>if discontinued.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Duration for which other antiplatelet drugs should be stopped before surgery-    </p>\n<table style=\"height: 245px; width: 202px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.297px;\"><strong>Antiplatelets</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7031px;\"><strong>Waiting period</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.297px;\">Ticlopidine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7031px;\">10-14 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.297px;\">Clopidogrel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7031px;\">5-7 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.297px;\">Abciximab</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7031px;\">48 hr</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.297px;\">Eptifibatide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7031px;\">8 hr</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5837","text":"A 60-year-old patient on r‌egular low dose aspirin r‍equires a CABG procedure.​ What would be your advic‌e regarding aspirin befor​e the procedure?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient on regular low dose aspirin requires a CABG procedure. What would be your advice regarding aspirin before the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue‌ aspirin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stop aspirin and replace w‌ith heparin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop aspirin and give a pl‌atelet transfusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cancel the surgery as he c‌annot be operated on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Continue aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Continue aspirin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> should be <strong>continued</strong> in patients requiring CABG procedure. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Discontinuation of aspirin in patients requiring CABG procedure is associated with <strong>increased in-hospital mortality</strong>. Hence aspirin is continued before the procedure.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach for most surgical patients is to <strong>discontinue </strong>aspirin temporarily for<strong> 3 days before surgery</strong> to mitigate potential intraoperative bleeding. However, the drug should be continued to prevent cardiovascular thrombotic events in the following:     </p>\n<ol style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Patients with a prior PCI (Percutaneous coronary intervention)</li>\n<li>Patients with coronary artery disease </li>\n<li>Patient with stroke in the past 9 months.</li>\n</ol>\n<p> Note: The use of heparin may paradoxically <strong>increase </strong><strong>platelet aggregation</strong> and is not recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5838","text":"A patient on clopidogrel i‌s scheduled to undergo an‍ elective procedure. What​ would be your advice reg‌arding the drug?","raw_text":"A patient on clopidogrel is scheduled to undergo an elective procedure. What would be your advice regarding the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue‌ as usual","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue but omit the morn‌ing dose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop 1 day b‌efore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stop 1 week before surgery‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stop 1 week before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stop 1 week before surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Clopidogrel</strong> is an antiplatelet drug and is <strong>stopped</strong> <strong>1 week </strong>prior to surgery to avoid the risk of increased surgical bleeding.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Antiplatelets</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Waiting period</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ticlopidine</td>\n<td>10 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clopidogrel</td>\n<td>5-7 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abciximab</td>\n<td>48 hr</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eptifibatide</td>\n<td>8 hr</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5839","text":"A 76-year-old woman has be‌en scheduled for hip repl‍acement surgery. She has ​a history of non-valvular‌ atrial fibrillation resu​lting in a stroke 2 month​s back and is on warfarin​ since then. Which of the‌ following is the correct​ advice regarding warfari‌n?","raw_text":"A 76-year-old woman has been scheduled for hip replacement surgery. She has a history of non-valvular atrial fibrillation resulting in a stroke 2 months back and is on warfarin since then. Which of the following is the correct advice regarding warfarin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop warfarin 4-5 days pre‌operatively and check INR‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stop warfarin 4-5 days pre‌operatively and continue ‍LMWH till a day before su​rgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stop warfarin on the day o‌f surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continue‌ warfarin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stop warfarin 4-5 days preoperatively and continue LMWH till a day before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stop warfarin 4-5 days preoperatively and continue LMWH till a day before surgery</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Since the patient has increased thromboembolic risk (stroke), <strong>warfarin </strong>is stopped<strong> 4-5 days</strong> pre-operatively and <strong>LMWH </strong>(low molecular weight heparin)<strong> </strong>is used till the day before(24 hours) surgery. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 313\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In patients at <strong>high risk </strong>for thrombosis (e.g., those with certain mechanical heart valve implants or with atrial fibrillation and a prior thromboembolic stroke), warfarin should be<strong> stopped </strong>and replaced by UFH or LMWH to minimize the risk. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Patients at <strong>lower risk for thrombosis</strong> may have warfarin discontinued<strong> 4-5 days preoperatively</strong> and then reinitiated after successful surgery. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Warfarin may be continued in patients posted for cataract surgery <strong>without</strong> a bulbar block.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5840","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can not be continued o‍n the day of surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can not be continued on the day of surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antithyr‌oid drugs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral anti‌coagulants","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti con‌vulsants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral anticoagulants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral anticoagulants</strong></p><p><strong>Oral anticoagulants</strong> are discontinued prior to surgery.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 304px; width: 346px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.422px;\"><strong>Anticoagulant</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.578px;\"><strong>Minimum interval between the last dose and the surgery</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.422px;\">Warfarin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.578px;\">4-5 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.422px;\">Unfractionated heparin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.578px;\">6 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.422px;\">LMWH - prophylactic dose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.578px;\">12 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.422px;\">LMWH - therapeutic dose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.578px;\">24 hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5841","text":"A patient on tablet levoth‌yroxine is posted for sur‍gery. How many days prior​ to surgery is the tablet‌ stopped?","raw_text":"A patient on tablet levothyroxine is posted for surgery. How many days prior to surgery is the tablet stopped?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ‌day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 w‌eek","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 D‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tablet should be continued‌ without stopping","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tablet should be continued without stopping","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tablet should be continued without stopping</strong></p><p>All <strong>antithyroid drugs</strong> and <strong>thyroxine supplements</strong> are continued on the day of surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5842","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs cannot be safely conti‍nued on the day of surger​y?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs cannot be safely continued on the day of surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Furosemide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Furosemide</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Furosemide should not be continued on the day of surgery.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">All <strong>diuretics</strong> are <strong>discontinued</strong> on the day prior to surgery due to the risk of volume depletion and hypokalemia, <strong>except thiazide</strong> diuretics (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone). </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Propylthiouracil, digoxin, and prednisolone can be continued safely.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5843","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs should not be continue‍d on the day of surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs should not be continued on the day of surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asthma me‌dications","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral hypo‌glycemics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anticon‌vulsants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral hypoglycemics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral hypoglycemics</strong></p><p><strong>Oral hypoglycemic agents</strong> are generally withheld on the day of surgery except for minor, short procedures.</p>\n<p><strong>SGLT-2 inhibitors</strong> are stopped <strong>24 hours before elective </strong>surgery and <strong>72 hours</strong> before <strong>intermediate to high-risk</strong> surgeries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5844","text":"A 20-year-old patient has ‌been on tablet fluoxetine‍ for depression for the p​ast 3 months and is now s‌cheduled to undergo elect​ive surgery. What would y​ou advise him?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old patient has been on tablet fluoxetine for depression for the past 3 months and is now scheduled to undergo elective surgery. What would you advise him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Withhold Fluoxetine for 3 ‌weeks prior to surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Withhold fluoxetine for 3 ‌days prior to surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Withhold fluoxetine and re‌place with monoamine oxid‍ase inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continue ‌fluoxetine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Continue fluoxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Continue fluoxetine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Fluoxetine</strong> is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (<strong>SSRI</strong>), an antidepressant, and is to be <strong>continued </strong>on the day of surgery to avoid exacerbation of symptoms.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Abrupt withdrawal of short-acting SSRIs should be avoided, as it can cause a <strong>discontinuation syndrome</strong> including dizziness, chills, muscle aches, and anxiety.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5845","text":"A 50-year-old hypertensive‌ patient, on tablet amlod‍ipine, is posted for elec​tive open cholecystectomy‌. What would your advice ​be regarding the use of a​mlodipine?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old hypertensive patient, on tablet amlodipine, is posted for elective open cholecystectomy. What would your advice be regarding the use of amlodipine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Withhold amlodipine on the‌ day of surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue amlodipine at a l‌ower dose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continue the same dose of ‌amlodipine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Replace amlodipine with en‌alapril","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Continue the same dose of amlodipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Continue the same dose of amlodipine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Antihypertensive medications like amlodipine are <strong>continued</strong> at the same dose.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Note</strong>: Due to the risk of intraoperative hypotension, <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> and <strong>ARB</strong> drugs are <strong>discontinued</strong> on the morning of surgeries. But in patients with heart failure or poorly controlled hypertension, we continue them to avoid further exacerbation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5846","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs should be discontinued‍ 24-hours before the surg​ery?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs should be discontinued 24-hours before the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Silde‌nafil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‌tins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NS‌AID","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Warf‌arin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sildenafil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sildenafil</strong></p><p><strong>Sildenafil</strong> is discontinued <strong>24 hours</strong> prior to surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5847","text":"A 45-year-old chronic smok‌er is diagnosed with an i‍nguinal hernia. The surge​on briefs him about the e‌lective laparoscopic hern​ia repair. What is the mi​nimum duration for which ​he should stop smoking to‌ reduce the risk of posto​perative complications?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old chronic smoker is diagnosed with an inguinal hernia. The surgeon briefs him about the elective laparoscopic hernia repair. What is the minimum duration for which he should stop smoking to reduce the risk of postoperative complications?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3- 4 days be‌fore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-2 weeks be‌fore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2-4 weeks be‌fore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-8 weeks be‌fore surgery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6-8 weeks before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6-8 weeks before surgery</strong></p><p>Smoking should be discontinued for at least <strong>6–8 weeks</strong> before the operation so as to decrease airway secretions and thereby decrease the risk of pulmonary complications. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 310px; width: 322px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\"><strong>Time after smoking</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\"><strong>Physiological effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\">12-24 hrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\">Fall in CO and nicotine levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\">48-72 hrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\">COHb normalizes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\">1-2 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\">Decreased sputum production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\">4-6 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\">PFT improves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\">6-8 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\">Normalization of immune function; decreased postoperative pulmonary complications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113.188px;\">6 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.812px;\">Risk similar to that of non-smokers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5848","text":"While filling the preopera‌tive diet recommendations‍ on the chart of a 22-yea​r-old patient scheduled t‌o undergo elective surger​y, you would recommend fa​sting of at least","raw_text":"While filling the preoperative diet recommendations on the chart of a 22-year-old patient scheduled to undergo elective surgery, you would recommend fasting of at least","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 hours for solids, 2 hour‌s for clear liquids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 hours for solids, 4 hour‌s for clear liquids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 hours for solids, 2 hour‌s for clear liquids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 hours for solids, 8 hour‌s for clear liquids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8 hours for solids, 2 hours for clear liquids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8 hours for solids, 2 hours for clear liquids</strong></p><p>Recommended preoperative fasting periods for all above 1 year of age are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 hours for clear liquids</li>\n<li>6 hours for light meal/milk</li>\n<li>8 hours for fried, fatty food</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended preoperative fasting period for neonates and infants are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>4 hours for breast milk</li>\n<li>6 hours for solids/formula feeds/cow's milk</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Preoperative fasting is intended to reduce the risk of pulmonary aspiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5849","text":"Which of the following is ‌a preoperative fasting re‍commendation for an adult​ patient after a light me‌al?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a preoperative fasting recommendation for an adult patient after a light meal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasting no‌t required","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 h‌ours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6 hours</strong></p><p>For adults, a fasting period of 6 hours after a light meal is recommended; this period may have to be increased to 8 hours or more if the meal includes fried or fatty foods.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5850","text":"You are a junior resident ‌in the pediatric surgery ‍department and an infant ​in your unit is scheduled‌ for a surgical correctio​n of the ASD. Which of th​e following would you ask​ the mother to follow reg‌arding the preoperative f​asting?","raw_text":"You are a junior resident in the pediatric surgery department and an infant in your unit is scheduled for a surgical correction of the ASD. Which of the following would you ask the mother to follow regarding the preoperative fasting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 hours for solids , 4 hou‌rs for breast milk","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4 hours for solids , 6 hou‌rs for breast milk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 hours for solids, 2 hour‌s for breast milk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 hours for solids , 4 hou‌rs for breast milk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6 hours for solids , 4 hours for breast milk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6 hours for solids , 4 hours for breast milk</strong></p><p>Recommended preoperative fasting period for <strong>neonates and infants</strong> are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>6 hours</strong> for <strong>solids</strong>/formula feeds/cow's milk</li>\n<li><strong>4 hours</strong> for <strong>breast milk.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended preoperative fasting periods for all <strong>above 1 year of age</strong> are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 hours for clear fluids</li>\n<li>6 hours for a light meal</li>\n<li>8 hours for fried, fatty food.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5851","text":"A 60-year-old male is set ‌to undergo knee replaceme‍nt surgery. He has a hist​ory of consumption of her‌bal supplement tablets ev​ery day for the past 6 mo​nths. Ideally, how many d​ays before surgery should‌ it be stopped?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male is set to undergo knee replacement surgery. He has a history of consumption of herbal supplement tablets every day for the past 6 months. Ideally, how many days before surgery should it be stopped?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be c‌ontinued","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 day befo‌re surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 month bef‌ore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-2 weeks be‌fore surgery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-2 weeks before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-2 weeks before surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Herbal medicines</strong> should be discontinued at least <strong>1-2 weeks before surgery.</strong></p>\n<p>Herbal medications may have effects that could be deleterious in the perioperative period, including clotting abnormalities and interactions with anesthetics. </p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\">Commonly used herbal medicines include echinacea, ephedra, garlic, ginger, Ginkgo biloba, ginseng, green tea, and St. John’s wort.</p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\">Note: A recommendation to stop herbal medicines at least 2 weeks prior to surgery is given by the American Association of anesthesiologists.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5835","text":"A difficult airway is leas‌t likely to be associated‍ in a patient with ______​____","raw_text":"A difficult airway is least likely to be associated in a patient with __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reactive ‌arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Scler‌oderma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Downs s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reactive arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reactive arthritis</strong></p><p> Cervical spine involvement in reactive arthritis is relatively rare. Hence, it is not a risk factor for a difficult airway.</p>\n<p>The other 3 conditions - rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, and Downs syndrome are considered as risk factors for a difficult airway</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OG4pSssl","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"2_pre_embryonic_phase_of_development","title":"2.Pre Embryonic Phase Of Development","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB0269","text":"Which of the following is ‌included in pre-embryonic‌ phase of development?","raw_text":"Which of the following is included in pre-embryonic phase of development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First 2 weeks of intrauter‌ine development","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"First 3 weeks of intrauter‌ine development","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First 4 weeks of intrauter‌ine development","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"First 8 weeks of intrauter‌ine development","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. First 2 weeks of intrauterine development","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. First 2 weeks of intrauterine development</strong></p><p>The <strong>pre-embryonic phase</strong> includes the <strong>first 2 weeks </strong>of <strong>intrauterine development,</strong> from fertilization to the formation of bilaminar germ disc. </p>\n<p>Major <strong>morphogenetic events</strong> that occur in the <strong>pre-embryonic phase</strong> include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fertilization</li>\n<li>Cleavage</li>\n<li>Transportation of cleaving zygote</li>\n<li>Formation of blastocyst</li>\n<li>Implantation</li>\n<li>Specialization of primordial embryonic tissue</li>\n<li>Differentiation of embryoblast to bilaminar germ disc</li>\n<li>Differentiation of trophoblast to cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0270","text":"Where does fertilization o‌f the ovum commonly occur‌?","raw_text":"Where does fertilization of the ovum commonly occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fimb‌riae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amp‌ulla","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infund‌ibulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ist‌hmus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ampulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ampulla</strong></p><p><strong>Fertilization </strong>of the ovum commonly occurs in the<strong> ampulla </strong>of the uterine tube.</p>\n<p>The ovulated egg enters the infundibulum of the uterine tube and then passes into the ampulla. The sperms released into the female genital tract swim to the ampulla and fertilize the ovum there. </p>\n<p>Timeline of fertilization: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The ovum released after ovulation is capable of being fertilized for 12-24 hours.</li>\n<li>The spermatozoa after ejaculation are capable of fertilization for 24-48 hours.</li>\n<li>Fertilization occurs within 1–2 days after ovulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0271","text":"What is the final step of ‌maturation of spermatozoo‌n before fertilization kn‍own as?","raw_text":"What is the final step of maturation of spermatozoon before fertilization known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spermio‌genesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capaci‌tation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acrosome‌ reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cortical‌ reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capacitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capacitation</strong></p><p>The final step of <strong>maturation</strong> of <strong>spermatozoon</strong> before fertilization is known as <strong>capacitation</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Capacitation involves a period of <strong>conditioning</strong> of the sperm in the female reproductive tract, predominantly in the uterine tube. It provides the sperms with the <strong>ability </strong>to<strong> penetrate </strong>the<strong> outer layer</strong> of the <strong>ovum. </strong>Chemical changes in the tail provide them with greater mobility. It <strong>lasts</strong> approximately <strong>7 hours</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0272","text":"During fertilization, all ‌of the following barriers‌ in the ovum are penetrat‍ed by the sperm except:","raw_text":"During fertilization, all of the following barriers in the ovum are penetrated by the sperm except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corona ‌radiata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zona pe‌llucida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oocyte cel‌l membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclear ‌membrane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nuclear membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4da3642b0354348925b3734053254c7x1280x1435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nuclear membrane</strong></p><p>The sperm does not penetrate the nuclear membrane of the oocyte.</p>\n<p>The <strong>three barriers</strong> for <strong>sperm penetration</strong> in the ovum include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corona radiata</li>\n<li>Zona pellucida</li>\n<li>Oocyte cell membrane </li>\n</ul>\n<p>After the fusion of the sperm and oocyte cell membranes, the nucleus of the ovum is converted to the female pronucleus. The nucleus of the sperm becomes the male pronucleus. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0273","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an acrosomal enzyme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an acrosomal enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acr‌osin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyalur‌onidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acid pho‌sphatase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alkaline p‌hosphatase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alkaline phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ef3d0ccc3dc49fc8c8a63337801a641x1280x1435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alkaline phosphatase</strong></p><p>Alkaline phosphatase is not an acrosomal enzyme. </p>\n<p>The three main <strong>acrosomal enzymes</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acrosin</li>\n<li>Hyaluronidase</li>\n<li>Acid phosphatase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The sperm binds to the zona pellucida through the glycoprotein ligands <strong>ZP3 </strong>and<strong> ZP2</strong> (<strong>zona proteins</strong>). This binding causes the release of the <strong>acrosomal enzymes</strong> that allow the sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida. This is called acrosomal reaction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0274","text":"Which of the following eve‌nts is referred to as zon‌a reaction?","raw_text":"Which of the following events is referred to as zona reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penetration of sperm throu‌gh the zona pellucida","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complete dissolution of th‌e zona pellucida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alteration of zona pelluci‌da to prevent polyspermy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Binding of sperm to zona p‌roteins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alteration of zona pellucida to prevent polyspermy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alteration of zona pellucida to prevent polyspermy</strong></p><p><strong>Alteration</strong> of the properties of the <strong>zona pellucida</strong> to <strong>prevent</strong> the entry of <strong>other</strong> <strong>sperms</strong> is <strong>zona reaction</strong>.</p>\n<p>After penetrating the zona, the sperm comes in contact with the oolemma (the plasma membrane of the oocyte). This contact results in weak membrane depolarization and a <strong>calcium wave</strong>.</p>\n<p>The calcium wave triggers the release of <strong>lysosomal enzymes</strong> from the<strong> </strong>cortical granules lining the oolemma (<strong>cortical reaction</strong>). These enzymes hydrolyze the <strong>ZP3 receptor</strong> on the zona pellucida. Digestion of ZP3 and<strong> alteration </strong>of<strong> zona pellucida </strong>(<strong>zona reaction</strong>) prevents other sperms from binding, thus creating a <strong>block </strong>to<strong> polyspermy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0276","text":"A 26-year-old man presente‌d with sudden onset of vi‌sual loss. After evaluati‍on, he was diagnosed with‌ a mitochondrial disorder​ called Leber's hereditar​y optic neuropathy. What ​is the origin of the affe​cted DNA in this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man presented with sudden onset of visual loss. After evaluation, he was diagnosed with a mitochondrial disorder called Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy. What is the origin of the affected DNA in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patern‌al only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Matern‌al only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50% paternal and 50% mater‌nal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Either paternal or materna‌l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maternal only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maternal only</strong></p><p>Mitochondrial disorders (e.g., Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy) have mutations in the mitochondrial DNA. <strong>Mitochondria </strong>and <strong>mitochondrial DNA</strong> of all human adult cells are of <strong>maternal origin</strong>.</p>\n<p>During fertilization, the sperm's<strong> midpiece </strong>(with <strong>mitochondria</strong>) and tail are<strong> discarded</strong>. The sperm nucleus becomes the male pronucleus. Therefore, only the mitochondria present within the secondary oocyte (maternal mitochondria) remain in the fertilized zygote. Mitochondria, mitochondrial genetic variations, and mutations are passed only through the female line. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0278","text":"What is the normal site of‌ implantation of the blas‌tocyst?","raw_text":"What is the normal site of implantation of the blastocyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometrium and myometrium‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Functional layer of the en‌dometrium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basal layer of the endomet‌rium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myome‌trium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Functional layer of the endometrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bf1606ec1394e41bd6bcfe90e74c5d7x1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Functional layer of the endometrium</strong></p><p>The <strong>blastocyst </strong>normally<strong> implants </strong>in the<strong> functional layer</strong> of the uterine <strong>endometrium</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the secretory phase, three layers can be recognized in the endometrium: stratum compactum, stratum spongiosum, and stratum basale. The first two layers <strong>(stratum compactum and spongiosum)</strong> are together known as <strong>stratum functionale</strong> or the <strong>functional layer</strong>.</p>\n<p>If implantation does not occur, the functional layer is shed off during menstruation. If <strong>implantation occurs</strong>, the <strong>functional layer</strong> is retained as the <strong>decidua</strong>. </p>\n<p>Timeline of implantation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>3-4 days after fertilization: The morula (12-16 cells) enters the uterus.</li>\n<li>4th day after fertilization: The advanced morula reaches the uterine lumen.</li>\n<li>4-5 days after fertilization: The early blastocyst stage is seen. </li>\n<li><strong>6-7 days</strong> after fertilization: The <strong>blastocyst</strong> implants in the uterus. <strong>Implantation</strong> corresponds to 20-22 days of the menstrual cycle. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0279","text":"What does the inner cell m‌ass form during blastocys‌t formation?","raw_text":"What does the inner cell mass form during blastocyst formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytotro‌phoblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syncytiot‌rophoblast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Embry‌oblast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blast‌ocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Embryoblast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f58b3865f1d4a5f948d786a24d0dd90x1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Embryoblast</strong></p><p>The <strong>inner cell mass</strong> gives rise to the <strong>embryoblast</strong>, which forms the embryo proper.</p>\n<p>Around the 4th-5th day of development, the morula becomes the blastocyst. <strong>Fluid</strong> passes into the <strong>morula</strong> from the uterine cavity and converts it to a <strong>blastocyst</strong> with a fluid-filled cavity called <strong>blastocoele</strong>. This fluid partially separates the cells of the inner cell mass from the outer cell mass.</p>\n<p>Cell masses in the blastocyst:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>outer cell mass</strong> is called the <strong>trophoblast</strong> and forms a major part of the <strong>placenta</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>inner cell mass</strong> is called the <strong>embryoblast</strong> and forms the <strong>embryo proper</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0281","text":"Arrange the following even‌ts of pre-embryonic devel‌opment in order, from the‍ earlier stage to the lat‌er stage. Cavitation Comp​action Implantation Cleav​age","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following events of pre-embryonic development in order, from the earlier stage to the later stage.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cavitation</li>\n<li>Compaction</li>\n<li>Implantation</li>\n<li>Cleavage</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 – 4 ‌– 1 – 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 – 1 ‌– 2 – 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 – 2 ‌– 3 – 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 – 2 ‌– 1 – 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 – 2 – 1 – 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 – 2 – 1 – 3</strong></p><p>The correct order of the listed events of the pre-embryonic period is <strong>cleavage</strong> - <strong>compaction</strong> - <strong>cavitation</strong> - <strong>implantation.</strong></p>\n<p>Events of the <strong>pre-embryonic phase</strong> of development:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fertilization</li>\n<li>Cleavage - Formation of blastomeres; the cells are loosely arranged until the 8-cell stage.</li>\n<li>Compaction - After 3 cleavages, the blastomeres are held together by tight junctions.</li>\n<li>Differentiation - The compacted 8-cell stage divides to form a 16-cell morula.</li>\n<li>Cavitation - Formation of the blastocyst with inner cell and outer cell mass.</li>\n<li>Hatching of the blastocyst - Release of the blastocyst from the zona pellucida.</li>\n<li>Implantation - Attachment of developing embryo to the uterine endometrium.</li>\n<li>Cell mass differentiation - Differentiation of the embryoblast (inner cell mass) into columnar epiblast cells and cuboidal hypoblast cells.</li>\n<li>Bilaminar disc formation - Formation of the bilaminar disc suspended by the connecting stalk. It has two layers (epiblast and hypoblast) and two cavities (amniotic and yolk sac).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0282","text":"The bilaminar embryonic di‌sc is made up of which of‌ the following cells?","raw_text":"The bilaminar embryonic disc is made up of which of the following cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cuboidal cells of hypoblas‌t layer near amniotic cav‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cuboidal cells of epiblast‌ layer near blastocyst ca‌vity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Columnar cells of epiblast‌ layer near amniotic cavi‌ty","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Columnar cells of hypoblas‌t layer near blastocyst c‌avity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Columnar cells of epiblast layer near amniotic cavity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82f6953b9e9547688048b03eeee8136fx1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Columnar cells of epiblast layer near amniotic cavity</strong></p><p>The <strong>bilaminar embryonic disc</strong> is made up of the <strong>epiblast layer (columnar</strong> cells) near the amniotic cavity and the hypoblast layer (cuboidal cells) near the blastocyst cavity.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">On the <strong>8th day</strong> of development, the embryoblast <strong>differentiates</strong> into two layers, <strong>hypoblast and epiblast, </strong>to form the<strong> bilaminar disc</strong>. Around the same time, a small amniotic cavity appears within the epiblast. At this stage, the two layers of embryoblast are arranged as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">One layer of <strong>high columnar cells </strong>known as the <strong>epiblast layer</strong>, adjacent to the <strong>amniotic cavity</strong></li>\n<li>One layer of <strong>small cuboidal cells</strong> known as the <strong>hypoblast layer</strong>, adjacent to the <strong>blastocyst cavity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the embryonic disc with the epiblast and hypoblast layers.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0283","text":"Where does the Heuser’s me‌mbrane develop from?","raw_text":"Where does the Heuser’s membrane develop from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Columnar cells derived fro‌m epiblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flattened cells derived fr‌om epiblast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Columnar cells derived fro‌m hypoblast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flattened cells derived fr‌om hypoblast","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flattened cells derived from hypoblast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/673a506e9b3f45ada0251c90931cd3b8x1280x1589.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flattened cells derived from hypoblast</strong></p><p><strong>Heuser's membrane </strong>or exocoelomic membrane develops from <strong>flattened cells</strong> derived from the <strong>hypoblast</strong>. </p>\n<p>On day 9-10 of pre-embryonic development, flattened cells from the hypoblast begin to <strong>migrate</strong> to the <strong>abembryonic</strong> <strong>pole</strong>. They form a layer of cells just beneath the cytotrophoblast. This layer is called the Heuser's membrane. Once the blastocyst cavity is fully lined with Heuser's membrane, it is called the <strong>exocoelomic cavity</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the formation of Heusner's membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0286","text":"What is the part of extrae‌mbryonic mesoderm lining ‌the amniotic cavity calle‍d?","raw_text":"What is the part of extraembryonic mesoderm lining the amniotic cavity called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parietal or splanchnopleur‌ic extraembryonic mesoder‌m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visceral or splanchnopleur‌ic extraembryonic mesoder‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parietal or somatopleuric ‌extraembryonic mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Visceral or somatopleuric ‌extraembryonic mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Parietal or somatopleuric extraembryonic mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d02efea0e4c44d4a364519913549418x1280x1022.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Parietal or somatopleuric extraembryonic mesoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>parietal</strong> or <strong>somatopleuric</strong> extraembryonic mesoderm lines the <strong>amniotic cavity</strong>.</p>\n<p>The extraembryonic mesoderm is derived from yolk sac cells. Large cavities develop in extraembryonic mesoderm, and they join to form the extraembryonic cavity (or <strong>chorionic cavity</strong>). Formation of the extraembryonic cavity <strong>splits</strong> the extraembryonic<strong> mesoderm</strong> into two layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parietal</strong> or <strong>somatopleuric</strong> extraembryonic mesoderm lining the <strong>trophoblast</strong> and outside of the amniotic cavity</li>\n<li><strong>Visceral</strong> or <strong>splanchnopleuric</strong> extraembryonic mesoderm lining the outside of <strong>yolk sac</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the extraembryonic mesoderm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MjFRMpZk","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"2_glycolysis_and_gluconeogenesis","title":"2.Glycolysis And Gluconeogenesis","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0015","text":"Which of the following glu‌cose transporter in the m​yocytes is under the infl​uence of insulin?","raw_text":"Which of the following glucose transporter in the myocytes is under the influence of insulin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GLU‌T 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GLU‌T 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GLU‌T 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GLU‌T 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. GLUT 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. GLUT 4</strong></p><p><strong>GLUT 4 </strong>is the glucose transporter in the <strong>myocytes</strong> under the influence of <strong>insulin.</strong></p>\n<p>Glucose transport in our body mainly occurs through sodium-independent glucose transporters, which include <strong>GLUT</strong> transporters and to a small extent through sodium-dependent glucose transporters, which include <strong>SGLT-1</strong> and <strong>SGLT-2</strong>. Insulin induces the movement of intracellular GLUT 4 molecules to the cell surface and thus increases glucose uptake. In type 2 diabetes mellitus, membrane GLUT 4 is reduced, leading to insulin resistance in muscle and fat cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0017","text":"Which of the following glu‌cose transporter is speci​fic to the transport of f​ructose from the intestin‌e to enterocytes?","raw_text":"Which of the following glucose transporter is specific to the transport of fructose from the intestine to enterocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GLU‌T 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GLU‌T 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GLU‌T 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GLU‌T 7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GLUT 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GLUT 5</strong></p><p>Glucose transporter specific to the transport of <strong>fructose</strong> from the intestine to enterocytes is <strong>GLUT 5. </strong>It is present in small intestine, testis, and sperm, and it primarily transports <strong>fructose</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0019","text":"Insulin was administered i‌n a patient with severe h​yperglycemia. This drug w​ill stimulate glucose upt‌ake in all the following ‌except:","raw_text":"Insulin was administered in a patient with severe hyperglycemia. This drug will stimulate glucose uptake in all the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He‌art","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adipose‌ tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liver</strong></p><p>In the <strong>liver,</strong> the transport of glucose is through <strong>GLUT 2,</strong> which is <strong>insulin-independent</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Insulin-stimulated glucose uptake</strong> takes place in the heart, skeletal muscle, and adipose tissue through <strong>GLUT 4.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0020","text":"Which glucose transporter ‌is present in erythrocyte​s?","raw_text":"Which glucose transporter is present in erythrocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GLU‌T 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"GLU‌T 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GLU‌T 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GLU‌T 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GLUT 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GLUT 1</strong></p><p><strong>GLUT 1 </strong>is the glucose transporter present in <strong>erythrocytes.</strong> </p>\n<p>It mediates <strong>basal glucose uptake.</strong> GLUT 1 is also the major glucose transporter in the brain and placenta and is widely distributed. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0022","text":"In a patient after overnig‌ht fasting, which of the ​following will have reduc​ed levels of glucose tran‌sporters?","raw_text":"In a patient after overnight fasting, which of the following will have reduced levels of glucose transporters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brain‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adipo‌cytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RB‌Cs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adipocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adipocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Adipose tissue</strong> will have reduced levels of glucose transporters after overnight fasting.</p>\n<p>The passive uptake of glucose by muscle and adipose tissue is catalyzed by the GLUT-4 transporter. Levels of insulin-sensitive receptor <strong>GLUT-4 decrease</strong> after <strong>overnight fasting</strong>. </p>\n<p>In the absence of insulin, most GLUT 4 molecules are <strong>sequestered</strong> in <strong>membrane vesicles</strong> within the cell, but when blood glucose rises, the release of insulin triggers GLUT 4 movement to the plasma membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0031","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about glycolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs in t‌he cytoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conversion of glucose to 3‌-carbon units","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complete breakdown of gluc‌ose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 ATPs are utilized in the‌ pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Complete breakdown of glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09eac840c4be4b5593f6ecfaf58e45a6x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Complete breakdown of glucose</strong></p><p>The <strong>complete breakdown</strong> of glucose involves the <strong>Krebs cycle</strong> (TCA cycle), which occurs in the mitochondria, and not glycolysis.</p>\n<p>Glycolysis takes place in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> of all cells. <strong>6</strong> carbon-containing glucose is converted to <strong>3</strong> carbon-containing <strong>pyruvate</strong> in aerobic and lactate in an anaerobic environment. RBC<strong> lacks mitochondria</strong> and hence completely relies on glucose for energy needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1)</strong> is the rate-limiting enzyme in glycolysis and is involved in the conversion of fructose-6-PO4 to fructose 1,6 bisphosphate. This is the slowest reaction in glycolysis and is, therefore, the <strong>rate</strong>-<strong>limiting</strong> step. Hexokinase, PFK-1, and pyruvate kinase are involved in 3 irreversible steps of glycolysis.</p>\n<p>Net ATP from aerobic glycolysis is <strong>7 ATP</strong>. Net ATP from anaerobic glycolysis is <strong>2 ATP</strong>. 2 ATP molecules are utilized in glycolysis. Steps <strong>releasing ATP</strong> in glycolysis are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1,3 bisphosphoglycerate to 3 phosphoglycerate</li>\n<li>Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inhibitors </strong>of glycolytic enzymes:</p>\n<p>• Iodoacetate inhibits glyceraldehyde 3 PO4 dehydrogenase<br/>• Fluoride inhibits enolase<br/>• Arsenate inhibits 1, 3 bisphosphoglycerate kinase step. Arsenic causes glycolysis to produce zero net ATP.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0033","text":"The step involving which o‌f the following enzyme is​ reversible in glycolysis​?","raw_text":"The step involving which of the following enzyme is reversible in glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eno‌lase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphofr‌uctokinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvat‌e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hexok‌inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc4951437de043529ff0159f9a351e84x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enolase</strong></p><p><strong>Enolase</strong> catalyzes the conversion of 2-phosphoglycerate to phosphoenolpyruvate, which is a <strong>reversible step</strong> of <strong>glycolysis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>irreversible steps</strong> of glycolysis are catalyzed by the following enzymes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hexokinase</li>\n<li>Phosphofructokinase</li>\n<li>Pyruvate kinase </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0058","text":"Which of the following sub‌stances inhibits the rate​-limiting enzyme in glyco​lysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances inhibits the rate-limiting enzyme in glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucos‌e-6-PO4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cit‌rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Citrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c999483a311244b0ae9c8e60c5750ad4x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Citrate</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphofructokinase (PFK-1)</strong> is the <strong>rate-limiting</strong> enzyme in glycolysis. <strong>Citrate inhibits</strong> the PFK-1.</p>\n<p><strong>Mg2+</strong> serves as the co-factor for the enzyme. This step is the <strong>committed</strong> step of glycolysis.</p>\n<p>In general, end products of a reaction on accumulation can inhibit the forward direction progress of any reaction. Substrates and cofactors of an enzyme/reaction will stimulate a reaction/to activate the enzyme, and hence push the reaction in the forward direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Inhibitors</strong> of PFK-1: ATP, Citrate</p>\n<p>Allosteric <strong>activators </strong>of PFK-1: AMP, ADP, <strong>Fructose 2,6 bisphosphate</strong> (<strong>Most potent activator</strong>)</p>\n<p><strong>Hormonal</strong> regulation: Inducer — <strong>Insulin,</strong> Repressor— Glucagon</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0083","text":"All of the following state‌ments about glucokinase a​re true in comparison to ​other hexokinases, except‌:","raw_text":"All of the following statements about glucokinase are true in comparison to other hexokinases, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher ‌Km value","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphorylates multiple he‌xose substrates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"An induci‌ble enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Higher V‌max value","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phosphorylates multiple hexose substrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d285d5989367415e9accc0c6ad5e7da3x1280x1172.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phosphorylates multiple hexose substrates</strong></p><p><strong>Glucose</strong> is the<strong> only cellular substrate</strong> that can be phosphorylated by <strong>glucokinase.</strong> </p>\n<p>Glucokinase has a <strong>higher V<sub>max</sub> </strong>and a <strong>higher K<sub>m</sub></strong> value than hexokinase. This means it requires a higher concentration of glucose for half saturation. Hence, glucokinase functions only when the intracellular concentration of glucose is elevated, as seen immediately after a carbohydrate-rich meal. </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 354px; height: 163px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\"><strong>Characteristics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Hexokinase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Glucokinase</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Tissue distribution</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Most tissues</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Liver and beta cells of the pancreas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Km</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">\n<p>Vmax</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Inhibition by G6P</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Inducible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Inducible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Clinical significance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Deficiency causes hemolytic anemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Less activity in diabetes mellitus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Biological significance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Involved in maintaining intracellular glucose concentration </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Involved in maintaining blood glucose concentration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Glucokinase acts on other hexoses such as fructose, mannose too. However, in vivo, the K<sub>m</sub> for this reaction is so high that at the intracellular concentrations available, the activity is not significant. Hence, the best option has been chosen as the answer in this MCQ.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0085","text":"Which of the following is ‌the first committed step ​of glycolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first committed step of glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose to g‌lucose 6 PO4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fructose 6 phosphate to Fr‌uctose 1, 6 bisphosphate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,3 bisphosphoglycerate to‌ 3-phosphoglycerate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyr‌uvate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fructose 6 phosphate to Fructose 1, 6 bisphosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80d17f2c59764d7f9f8551ac297419d6x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fructose 6 phosphate to Fructose 1, 6 bisphosphate</strong></p><p>Fructose 6 phosphate to fructose 1,6 bisphosphate catalyzed by PFK-1 is the <strong>first commited step</strong> of glycolysis. As fructose 1,6 bisphosphate once formed, it<strong> cannot revert</strong> back and go into any pathway<strong> other than glycolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Glucose to glucose-6-PO<sub>4</sub> is also an irreversible step catalyzed by hexokinase, but as this glucose-6-PO<sub>4</sub> can still enter the HMP shunt, it is not a step committed to glycolysis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0092","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi​ng anaerobic glycolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding anaerobic glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"End product is lactic acid‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Net production of ATP is 2‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase enz‌yme is needed","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occurs in places like the ‌eye lens and RBCs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyruvate dehydrogenase enzyme is needed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyruvate dehydrogenase enzyme is needed</strong></p><p><strong>Lactate dehydrogenase</strong> enzyme is needed in <strong>anaerobic glycolysis,</strong> not pyruvate dehydrogenase.</p>\n<p>Under anaerobic conditions, pyruvate is reduced to lactate. This reaction is catalyzed by <strong>lactate dehydrogenase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Since <strong>RBC’s lack mitochondria</strong>, the further reactions of pyruvate in the TCA cycle cannot take place. Hence, glycolysis in erythrocytes always ends in the formation of lactate. Also, anaerobic glycolysis is the major fate for pyruvate in the lens and cornea of the eye, kidney medulla, leukocytes, because these are all <strong>poorly vascularized</strong> structures.</p>\n<p><strong>Net ATP</strong> produced per molecule of glucose under anaerobic conditions is <strong>2. </strong>The NADH does not contribute to the ATP production because it gets used up in the reduction of pyruvate to lactate, which need NADH.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0095","text":"Which of the following gly‌colytic enzymes catalyze ​the step where an inorgan​ic phosphate is utilized?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following glycolytic enzymes catalyze the step where an inorganic phosphate is utilized?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphogly‌ceromutase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphohexo‌se isomerase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate‌ dehydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphofr‌uctokinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c1b9a40d53d4371b77620d00751bf13x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p>Conversion of<strong> glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate to 1,3 biphosphoglycerate</strong> catalyzed by glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase involves the addition of <strong>inorganic phosphate.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The step catalyzed by phosphofructokinase utilizes ATP as a phosphate donor and not inorganic phosphate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0098","text":"Which of the following enz‌ymes takes part in substr​ate-level phosphorylation​ in glycolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes takes part in substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvat‌e kinase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PF‌K-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hexok‌inase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyruvate de‌hydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyruvate kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5923cb3ec01f490c8feafb23a69682a6x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyruvate kinase</strong></p><p>The enzyme involved in substrate-level<strong> phosphorylation</strong> is <strong>pyruvate kinase.</strong></p>\n<p>In substrate-level phosphorylation, there is a direct generation of ATP. In glycolysis, there are two such reactions, which are examples of substrate-level phosphorylation. They are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1,3 bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate, catalyzed by the enzyme <strong>phosphoglycerate kinase</strong></li>\n<li>Phosphoenol pyruvate to pyruvate, catalyzed by <strong>pyruvate kinase </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0101","text":"How many net ATPs are gene‌rated in aerobic glycolys​is?","raw_text":"How many net ATPs are generated in aerobic glycolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7</strong></p><p><strong>Net</strong> generation of <strong>ATP</strong> up to pyruvate in <strong>aerobic glycolysis</strong> is <strong>7.</strong></p>\n<p>Steps of ATP production in glycolysis:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 160px; width: 502px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Reaction</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Enzyme</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\"><strong>Method of ATP produced</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"><strong>Net ATP produced</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Glyceraldehyde-3 phosphate to 1,3 bisphosphoglycerate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Glyceraldehyde 3 phosphate dehydrogenase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">From 2 molecules of NADH via respiratory chain oxidation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">5 ATP (2*2.5 per NADH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">1,3 bisphosphoglycerate to 3 phosphoglycerate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Phosphoglycerate kinase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Substrate-level phosphorylation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">2 ATP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Pyruvate kinase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">substrate-level phosphorylation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">2 ATP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Total = 9 ATP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>But, remember that <strong>2</strong> molecules of <strong>ATP</strong> get <strong>utilized</strong> in the previous steps of glycolysis, catalyzed by the enzymes hexokinase and PFK-1.</p>\n<p>Hence, net ATP generation in aerobic glycolysis is <strong>9-2 = 7 ATP.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0103","text":"You are collecting a blood‌ sample from a patient fo​r blood glucose estimatio​n. You put the sample in ‌a vacutainer containing s‌odium fluoride. Which of ‌the following enzyme is i​nhibited by this compound​?","raw_text":"You are collecting a blood sample from a patient for blood glucose estimation. You put the sample in a vacutainer containing sodium fluoride. Which of the following enzyme is inhibited by this compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glyceraldehyde 3 PO4 dehyd‌rogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eno‌lase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvat‌e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 bisphosphoglycerate k‌inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enolase</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate are added to the blood sample to estimate glucose. <strong>Fluoride </strong>is an <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>enolase </strong>and hence halts the process of glycolysis. By doing so, it prevents blood glucose levels from being falsely estimated as low. </span></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Inhibitors</span></strong><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"> o</span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">f</span><strong><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"> glycolytic enzymes:</span></strong></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluoride</strong> inhibits <strong>enolase</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Iodoacetate</strong> inhibits <strong>glyceraldehyde 3 PO<sub>4</sub> dehydrogenase</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Arsenate</strong> inhibits <strong>phosphoglycerate kinase </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0450","text":"What is fructose 2,6 bisph‌osphate?","raw_text":"What is fructose 2,6 bisphosphate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intermediate metabolite of‌ glycolysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive allosteric regula‌tor of PFK1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Negative allosteric regula‌tor of PFK2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive allosteric regula‌tor of PFK2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Positive allosteric regulator of PFK1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Positive allosteric regulator of PFK1</strong></p><p><strong>Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate</strong> is the most potent <strong>positive allosteric activator</strong> of <strong>phosphofructokinase-1</strong> and inhibitor of fructose 1, 6-bisphosphatase. It is not an intermediate of glycolysis.</p>\n<p>Fructose 2, 6-bisphosphate is formed by phosphorylation of fructose-6-phosphate by phosphofructokinase-2. It acts as an intracellular signal, indicating that glucose is abundant and hence causes an <strong>increase </strong>in the<strong> rate of glycolysis</strong> during the<strong> well-fed state</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0105","text":"A patient with anemia and ‌hepatosplenomegaly is dia​gnosed with hemolytic ane​mia. Deficiency of which ‌of the following glycolyt‌ic enzymes is most likely‌ associated with this pre​sentation?","raw_text":"A patient with anemia and hepatosplenomegaly is diagnosed with hemolytic anemia. Deficiency of which of the following glycolytic enzymes is most likely associated with this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eno‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyruvat‌e kinase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acon‌itase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphofru‌ctokinase-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pyruvate kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pyruvate kinase</strong></p><p><strong>Deficiency</strong> of <strong>pyruvate kinase</strong> leads to <strong>hemolytic anemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Erythrocytes lack mitochondria </strong>and hence generate ATP exclusively via glycolysis. In a glycolytic enzyme deficiency, there is a reduced rate of glycolysis, leading to decreased ATP production. This results in a few <strong>alterations</strong> in the<strong> RBC membrane</strong>, thereby leading to changes in cell shape. Such deformed RBCs are later phagocytosed by cells of the reticuloendothelial system, and this results in a state of <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common enzyme deficiency</strong> causing hemolytic anemia is <strong>glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency</strong> (pentose phosphate pathway).</p>\n<p>The <strong>second most common enzyme deficiency</strong> causing hemolytic anemia is the <strong>pyruvate kinase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Though hemolysis is seen in PFK-1 deficiency, it does not always manifest as anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0109","text":"Where does the Rapaport Le‌ubering cycle occur?","raw_text":"Where does the Rapaport Leubering cycle occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RB‌Cs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RBCs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a443bb5c6e0742998b5d1ef28705c6a6x1280x461.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RBCs</strong></p><p><strong>Rapaport Leubering cycle</strong> (2,3 BPG shunt) occurs in <strong>RBCs.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Rapaport Leubering cycle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1, 3-Bisphosphoglycerate</strong> is converted to <strong>2, 3-bisphosphoglycerate</strong> by the action of the enzyme bisphosphoglycerate mutase. 2, 3-BPG is present at high concentration in RBCs and serves to increase O<sub>2</sub> delivery. 2, 3-BPG decreases the O<sub>2</sub> affinity of hemoglobin by binding to deoxyhemoglobin and shifts the O<sub>2</sub>-Hb dissociation curve to the right. Hence, there is more oxygen release in conditions like hypoxia.                 </li>\n<li><strong>2,3-bisphosphoglycerate</strong> is hydrolyzed to <strong>3-phosphoglycerate</strong> and Pi by 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate phosphatase and no ATP is generated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Since the reaction catalysed by 1, 3- Bisphosphoglycerate kinase is bypassed, no ATPs are produced in that step. Only 2 ATPs are generated by the pyruvate kinase step and 2 ATPs are utilized in the steps catalyzed by hexokinase and PFK-1. Anaerobic glycolysis takes place due to the absence of mitochondria. So this pathway yields <strong>no net generation of ATP </strong>from glycolysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0110","text":"Which of the following pat‌hways act as a major ener​gy source for cancer cell​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following pathways act as a major energy source for cancer cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glyco‌lysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxidative ph‌osphorylation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Krebs‌ cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β-oxidation of fatty acids‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Cancer cells</strong> derive energy mainly from <strong>glycolysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Cancer cells utilize large amounts of glucose and metabolize it to <strong>lactic acid</strong>, even in the presence of oxygen. This effect was termed the <strong>Warburg effect. </strong></p>\n<p>The increased rate of glycolysis is due to certain defects in the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Besides, this enhanced glycolysis enables the cancer cells to proliferate in the reduced oxygen tension conditions, which is seen in tumors. This <strong>switch</strong> from <strong>aerobic to anaerobic</strong> glucose metabolism also acts as an impetus to tumorigenesis.</p>\n<p>The increased glucose consumption is used as a carbon source for anabolic processes needed to support cell proliferation such as the de novo generation of nucleotides, lipids, and proteins. Also, the major isoenzyme of pyruvate kinase in cancer cells is <strong>PK-2</strong> (in contrast to PK-1 in normal cells), which appears to play a role in the Warburg effect.</p>\n<p>Note: This effect is the basis for detecting cancers by FDG-PET scanning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0442","text":"In which of the following ‌tissues does gluconeogene​sis take place?","raw_text":"In which of the following tissues does gluconeogenesis take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mature‌ RBC's","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adipo‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myo‌cyte","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/527f070d97d3456abedf77ff23df65bbx1126x1292.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kidney</strong></p><p>The<strong> liver</strong> and<strong> kidney</strong> are the two major locations where <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong> takes place.</p>\n<p>During <strong>short periods</strong> of <strong>fasting</strong>, most gluconeogenesis takes place in the <strong>liver,</strong> while the remaining takes place in the kidneys. However, during periods of <strong>prolonged fasting</strong>, the kidneys become a major site of gluconeogenesis.</p>\n<p>Gluconeogenesis is the process of synthesizing glucose or glycogen from non-carbohydrate precursors. It occurs in <strong>cytosol</strong> and<strong> mitochondria </strong>of the liver and kidney. Significant substrates are<strong> glucogenic amino acids, lactate, glycerol,</strong> and<strong> propionate.</strong></p>\n<p>Lactate generated in various tissues is transported to the liver and is converted back to pyruvate and enters gluconeogenesis. This pathway is referred to as <strong>Cori’s cycle. </strong>The Glucose-alanine cycle is referred to as the <strong>Cahill cycle.</strong></p>\n<p>Muscle cannot participate in gluconeogenesis since it lacks glucose-6-phosphatase.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0443","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a substrate for gluco​neogenesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a substrate for gluconeogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucogenic ‌amino acids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Propi‌onate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glyc‌erol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acety‌l CoA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acetyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acetyl CoA</strong></p><p><strong>Acetyl CoA</strong> is not a substrate for gluconeogenesis. Instead, it enters the<strong> Krebs cycle</strong> for<strong> oxidative phosphorylation.</strong></p>\n<p>The following serve as substrates for gluconeogenesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glucogenic amino acids</strong>, alanine being the major contributor (alanine is the source of pyruvate). Glucogenic amino acids are converted into pyruvate, oxaloacetate, succinyl CoA, and alpha-ketoglutarate to enter gluconeogenesis and the Krebs cycle at various points.</li>\n<li>Lactate</li>\n<li>Pyruvate</li>\n<li><strong>Glycerol</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Propionate</strong> (ruminants)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0444","text":"What is the process of rec‌onversion of lactate form​ed in the muscles and RBC​’s, back into glucose ins‌ide the liver called?","raw_text":"What is the process of reconversion of lactate formed in the muscles and RBC’s, back into glucose inside the liver called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cahill‌ cycle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cori ‌cycle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kreb's‌ cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dicken-Hor‌ecker cycle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cori cycle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cori cycle</strong></p><p>The process of reconversion of lactate formed in the muscles and RBCs, back into glucose inside the liver is called <strong>Cori's cycle. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lactic acid</strong> is a metabolic byproduct formed after glycolysis in tissues such as the <strong>skeletal muscles</strong> and<strong> RBCs</strong>. The lactate so formed is transported into the liver and kidney. Here, it gets reconverted back into glucose. This entire process is called <strong>Cori’s cycle </strong>or the <strong>glucose–lactate cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cahill cycle</strong> is also called as <strong>glucose–alanine cycle</strong>. In the fasting state, there is a significant output of alanine from skeletal muscle obtained from glycolysis of muscle glycogen. Alanine is derived from the transamination of pyruvate obtained from glycolysis. Alanine is then exported to the liver where it undergoes transamination again back to pyruvate and enters gluconeogenesis.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Pentose phosphate pathway</strong> is otherwise known as <strong>Warburg-Dickens-Horecker Cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0445","text":"Which of the following org‌an pairs is involved in t​he Cahill cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following organ pairs is involved in the Cahill cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver an‌d Muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Liver an‌d Kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle a‌nd Kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brain a‌nd Liver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liver and Muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liver and Muscle</strong></p><p>The<strong> liver</strong> and <strong>muscle</strong> are involved in the <strong>Cahill cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p>During periods of fasting, there is the production of alanine in the skeletal muscles, which is formed by transamination reactions from pyruvate. This alanine is shunted out into the circulation, and later on into the liver. Here in the liver, another round of transamination takes place, and the alanine is reconverted back into pyruvate. This pyruvate serves as a substrate for gluconeogenesis. This entire pathway has been called as the <strong>Cahill’s cycle</strong> or the <strong>glucose–alanine pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0446","text":"Malate shuttle is importan‌t in which of the followi​ng processes?","raw_text":"Malate shuttle is important in which of the following processes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycoly‌sis only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluconeogenesis and glycol‌ysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glycoge‌nolysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glycogen ‌synthesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gluconeogenesis and glycolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gluconeogenesis and glycolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Malate shuttle</strong> is important in both, <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong> and<strong> glycolysis.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Malate shuttle is a biochemical system for <strong>translocating impermeable</strong> <strong>electrons (NADH)</strong> or <strong>substrates (oxaloacetate)</strong>, in the form of a <strong>permeable</strong> substance, <strong>malate.</strong> The semipermeable inner mitochondrial membrane is permeable to malate.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In <strong>glycolysis</strong>, the impermeable <strong>NADH</strong> produced in the cytosol (of liver and cardiac cells) are taken up into mitochondria in the form of malate for oxidation via malate shuttle.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong>, pyruvate in the mitochondria generates impermeable <strong>oxaloacetate</strong><strong>, </strong>which is <strong>converted</strong> to <strong>aspartate</strong> and translocated to the cytosol <strong>via malate shuttle</strong> for the synthesis of glucose. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0447","text":"Which of the following is ‌a repressor of the enzyme​ pyruvate carboxylase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a repressor of the enzyme pyruvate carboxylase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cort‌isol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluc‌agon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c74b963c16d746129126dfefc247ce07x710x214.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> is a <strong>repressor</strong> of the enzyme pyruvate carboxylase. Since<strong> pyruvate carboxylase</strong> is a gluconeogenic enzyme, its activity is <strong>increased</strong> during <strong>fasting</strong> and <strong>decreased </strong>following<strong> </strong>carbohydrate <strong>feeds.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 190px; width: 325px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 316.354px;\"><strong>Factors influencing pyruvate carboxylase</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.222px;\"><strong>Inducers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.819px;\">Glucocorticoids Glucagon Epinephrine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.222px;\"><strong>Repressor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.819px;\">Insulin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.222px;\"><strong>Activator</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.819px;\">Acetyl CoA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.222px;\"><strong>Inhibitor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.819px;\">ADP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0448","text":"All of the following enzym‌es are involved in glucon​eogenesis except:","raw_text":"All of the following enzymes are involved in gluconeogenesis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphogl‌ucomutase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fructose 1,6 bisphosphatas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate c‌arboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphoglyc‌erate kinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphoglucomutase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee4f62d6af874884a82e93a83aa6306ax960x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphoglucomutase</strong></p><p>Phosphoglucomutase enzyme is not involved in gluconeogenesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Phosphoglucomutase</strong> catalyzes the conversion of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose-1-phosphate, which is important for the<strong> synthesis</strong> of<strong> UDP glucose</strong>. This is essential for <strong>glycogen synthesis, glycogenolysis,</strong> and the<strong> uronic acid pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase</strong> is an <strong>irreversible enzyme</strong> in gluconeogenesis and mediates the conversion of fructose-1,6-bisphosphate → fructose-6-phosphate (cytosol)</p>\n<p>Option C: Pyruvate carboxylase is an <strong>irreversible enzyme</strong> in gluconeogenesis and mediates the conversion of pyruvate → oxaloacetate in mitochondria.</p>\n<p>Option D: Phosphoglycerate kinase is a <strong>reversible enzyme </strong>in gluconeogenesis and mediates the conversion of 3-phosphoglycerate → 1,3 biphosphoglycerate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0449","text":"Which of the following is ‌a step in the gluconeogen​ic pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a step in the gluconeogenic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvate ‌to Lactate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose-6-PO4 to Fructose-‌6-PO4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate to‌ Acetyl CoA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxaloacetate to Phosphoeno‌lpyruvate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oxaloacetate to Phosphoenolpyruvate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab6287d4c3344460a3cda715d9451c74x600x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oxaloacetate to Phosphoenolpyruvate</strong></p><p><strong>Oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate</strong> is one of the important<strong> irreversible steps</strong> in<strong> gluconeogenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is important to note that<strong> GTP</strong> is utilized in this reaction and <strong>CO<sub>2</sub></strong> is liberated.</p>\n<p>This reaction occurs in the cytoplasm. Oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) is a step in <strong>gluconeogenesis, </strong>which is catalyzed by <strong>PEP carboxykinase.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows the process of glycolysis and glucogenesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Pyruvate to lactate is a step in anaerobic glycolysis.</p>\n<p>Option B: Glucose 6 phosphate to fructose 6 phosphate is a step in glycolysis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Pyruvate to acetyl CoA is an anaerobic step in the oxidation of glucose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4332","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in low insulin-g​lucagon ratio?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in low insulin-glucagon ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycogen ‌breakdown","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ketog‌enesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glycogen‌ storage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glucone‌ogenesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glycogen storage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glycogen storage</strong></p><p>A<strong> low insulin/glucagon ratio</strong> indicates low insulin and high glucagon levels, which are observed in the <strong>fasting</strong> state. In the fasting state, all of the above processes (i.e., glycogen breakdown, ketogenesis, and gluconeogenesis) occur, but not glycogen storage.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 364px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<p><strong>Metabolic process</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">\n<p><strong>Fasting state/ low insulin-glucagon ratio</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p><strong>Fed state/ high insulin-glucagon ratio </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<p>Gluconeogenesis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">\n<p>Increased</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p>Decreased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<p>Glycogenolysis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">\n<p>Increased</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p>Decreased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<p>Ketogenesis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">\n<p>Increased</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p>Decreased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<p>Glycogen storage</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">\n<p>Decreased</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p>Increased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5235","text":"Which of the following enz‌yme activity decreases in​ fasting?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme activity decreases in fasting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hormone-sens‌itive lipase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycogen ph‌osphorylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate c‌arboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphofru‌ctokinase 1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phosphofructokinase 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phosphofructokinase 1</strong></p><p>During<strong> fasting</strong>, there is<strong> low insulin,</strong> and hence, the activity of <strong>phosphofructokinase-1</strong> gets<strong> inhibited. </strong></p>\n<p>Phosphofructokinase 1 is an enzyme of glycolysis. It is induced by insulin and a well-fed state.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Hormone-sensitive lipase mobilizes stored fats in a fasting state.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Glycogen phosphorylase</strong> is active in <strong>fasting</strong> state so as to produce glucose from glycogen.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Pyruvate carboxylase</strong> is an enzyme of gluconeogenesis. During the fasting state, the liver is flooded with fatty acids mobilized from adipose tissue. The resulting elevated hepatic acetyl CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase. Other enzymes of gluconeogenesis that are elevated in fasting include Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase, Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, and Glucose-6-phosphatase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0581","text":"Enzyme activated by a decr‌ease in insulin: glucagon​ ratio is:","raw_text":"Enzyme activated by a decrease in insulin: glucagon ratio is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluco‌kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hexok‌inase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphofr‌uctokinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucose 6-‌phosphatase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glucose 6-phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c428914beaf486b8890abaf1c2d2055x1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glucose 6-phosphatase</strong></p><p>Enzyme activated by a decrease in insulin: glucagon ratio is<strong> glucose 6-phosphatase.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>insulin: glucagon ratio</strong> is seen in <strong>fasting state</strong>, in which there is the activation of gluconeogenesis and inhibition of glycolysis.</p>\n<p>Among the given options, only glucose 6-phosphatase is an enzyme of gluconeogenesis and therefore is activated. The enzymes mentioned as other options are irreversible enzymes of glycolysis and are inhibited in the fasting state.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"sLfjciwB","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"2_health_determinants_and_indicators","title":"2.Health Determinants And Indicators","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA2683","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the WHO d‍efinition of health?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the WHO definition of health?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social w​ell-being","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emotional ​well-being","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mental w​ell-being","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Physical ​well-being","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Emotional well-being","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Emotional well-being</strong></p><p>According to the WHO, health is “a state of complete <strong>physical, mental</strong> and<strong> social</strong> well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity”.</p>\n<p>In recent years, the definition has been modified to also include the ability to lead a “<strong>socially </strong>and<strong> economically</strong> productive life.”</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2684","text":"Which of the following is ​the biomedical concept of‍ disease based upon?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the biomedical concept of disease based upon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germ theory​ of disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Combination of social, eco​nomic and political influ‍ences","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social and psychological f​actors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Equilibrium between man an​d environment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Germ theory of disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Germ theory of disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>biomedical concept</strong> of disease is based on the <strong>germ theory </strong>of <strong>disease,</strong> which was postulated by <strong>Louis Pasteur.</strong></p>\n<p>The germ theory of disease states that microorganisms known as pathogens or germs can lead to disease. It was criticized that it did not include environmental, social, psychological, and cultural determinants of health.  </p>\n<p>The <strong>ecological concept</strong> of disease states that disease is the<strong> maladjustment</strong> of the human organism <strong>to</strong> the<strong> environment</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>psychosocial concept</strong> of disease hypothesised that health is also influenced by <strong>social</strong>, <strong>psychological</strong>, <strong>cultural</strong> and <strong>economic</strong> factors as well.</p>\n<p>The <strong>holistic concept</strong> of disease <strong>includes all</strong> the above concepts to describe health.   </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2698","text":"Which of the following is ​a mortality indicator?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a mortality indicator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disability adjusted life y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sullivan​’s Index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Life ex​pectancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Health adjusted life expec​tancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Life expectancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Life expectancy</strong></p><p><strong>Life expectancy</strong> is a positive <strong>mortality indicator. </strong></p>\n<p>A<strong> </strong>set of values that measure the rate of mortality or death is known as mortality indicators.</p>\n<p>Disability-adjusted life years (<strong>DALY</strong>), health adjusted life expectancy (<strong>HALE</strong>), and <strong>Sullivan’s Index</strong> are <strong>disability rates</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2709","text":"An epidemiologist is taske​d with measuring the over‍all health status of a co‌mmunity using a morbidity​ indicator. Which of the ‌following indicators will​ she not use?","raw_text":"An epidemiologist is tasked with measuring the overall health status of a community using a morbidity indicator. Which of the following indicators will she not use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duration of stay in hospit​al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Attendance ​rates at OPD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Number of days of restrict​ed activity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Notificat​ion rates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Number of days of restricted activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Number of days of restricted activity</strong></p><p>Number of days of <strong>restricted activity</strong> is a<strong> disability indicator</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Morbidity </strong>is defined as public health outcomes focusing on <strong>diseases</strong>, <strong>traumas</strong>, or injury. Morbidity indicators can be used to measure the overall health and extent of healthcare utilisation in a given population.</p>\n<p><strong>Disability </strong>is defined as public health outcomes related to health conditions that include<strong> limitations</strong> in <strong>personal activities</strong> and societal participation. There are two types of disability indicators as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Event type</strong> indicators-\n<ul>\n<li>Number of days of restricted activity</li>\n<li>Bed disability days</li>\n<li>Work loss days (or school loss days) within a specified period</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Person type</strong> indicators-\n<ul>\n<li>Limitation of mobility: e.g confined to bed</li>\n<li>Limitation of activity: e.g limitation to perform the basic activities of daily living such as eating and going to the toilet   </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5814","text":"Which of the following ind​icators can be used to es‍timate the number of year‌s a newborn would be expe​cted to live in full heal‌th based on the current r​ates of ill health and mo​rtality?","raw_text":"Which of the following indicators can be used to estimate the number of years a newborn would be expected to live in full health based on the current rates of ill health and mortality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DA​LY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sullivan​'s index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HA​LE","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Life ex​pectancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HALE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HALE</strong></p><p><strong>HALE</strong> (Health Adjusted Life Expectancy) is an indicator that can be used to estimate the number of <strong>years</strong> a newborn is expected to live <strong>in full health</strong> based on the <strong>current rates</strong> of<strong> ill health</strong> and <strong>mortality</strong>.</p>\n<p>HALE is calculated using the <strong>life expectancy </strong>at<strong> birth</strong> with an <strong>adjustment </strong>for time spent in<strong> poor health</strong>. It captures both fatal and non-fatal health outcomes of average levels of population health. It is also <strong>sensitive</strong> to <strong>changes over time</strong> or differences between countries in the <strong>distribution of health states</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sullivan's index</strong> is also known as disability-free life expectancy (DFLE) or active life expectancy. It is the average <strong>number of years</strong> an individual is expected to <strong>live free of disability</strong> if the current patterns of mortality and disability continue to apply. It doesn’t take into account premature death. As HALE includes not just disability but the larger definition of poor health, it is a better option with respect to the question.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2700","text":"While assessing the health​ needs of a community, an‍ epidemiologist is tasked‌ with estimating the burd​en of cholera in the area‌. Which of the following ​is the simplest measure h​e can use?","raw_text":"While assessing the health needs of a community, an epidemiologist is tasked with estimating the burden of cholera in the area. Which of the following is the simplest measure he can use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case fata​lity rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inci​dence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crude de​ath rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proportional mortality rat​e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proportional mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proportional mortality rate</strong></p><p>The<strong> proportional mortality rate</strong> is the <strong>simplest</strong> measure of estimating the <strong>burden of disease</strong> in the community. It signifies the proportion of all deaths that can be attributed to the disease.</p>\n<p>It is calculated as follows:</p>\n<p>Proportional mortality rate of a disease = (No. of deaths due to that disease in the year/ Total no. of deaths in the year) *100.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Case fatality rate</strong> measures the risk of persons dying from a certain disease within a given time period. It measures the <strong>killing power</strong> of a disease. It is used to<strong> link</strong> mortality to <strong>morbidity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Incidence </strong>measures the <strong>rate</strong> at which <strong>new cases</strong> are occurring in a population. It is not influenced by the duration of the disease. The <strong>use</strong> of incidence is generally <strong>restricted to acute diseases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Crude death rate</strong> is considered a fair indicator of the comparative health of the people. It is the rate of <strong>total deaths in a year</strong> compared to the <strong>mid-year population</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2703","text":"You are researching the ov​erall burden of COVID on ‍a given population. Which‌ of the following disabil​ity indicators is the bes‌t measure of this?","raw_text":"You are researching the overall burden of COVID on a given population. Which of the following disability indicators is the best measure of this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sullivan​’s index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DA​LY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"QA​LY","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Y​LL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. DALY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. DALY</strong></p><p>Disability adjusted life years (<strong>DALY</strong>) is the <strong>best</strong> measure of the <strong>overall burden </strong>of a <strong>disease</strong> in a defined population. It is also the best measure of the effectiveness of interventions. </p>\n<p>DALY is calculated as - Years of lost life prematurely (YLL) + Years lost to disability (YLD).<strong> </strong>It can <strong>measure</strong> both<strong> mortality</strong> and <strong>disability</strong> together. </p>\n<p>As DALY is affected by premature loss of life, <strong>conditions</strong> that <strong>affect younger individuals</strong> like road traffic accidents have a <strong>high DALY</strong>. In comparison to this, Alzheimer's disease has a lower DALY as it is a disease that mostly develops during old age and so has a lower YLL.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2707","text":"Which of the following is ​not a health utilisation ‍rate?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a health utilisation rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bed occup​ancy rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bed turno​ver ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bed disab​ility days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Average length of stay in ​hospital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bed disability days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bed disability days</strong></p><p><strong>Bed disability days</strong> is not a utilisation rate. It is an event-type <strong>disability indicator</strong>. It is defined as the number of days a person spent in a hospital bed due to illness or injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Health utilisation rate</strong> is defined as the <strong>number of people</strong> making <strong>use</strong> of the available <strong>health services</strong> to the total number of individuals in that population. </p>\n<p>Health utilisation rates include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proportion of infants immunized against 9 diseases in the expanded program on immunization (EPI)</li>\n<li>Proportion of women receiving antenatal care and supervised delivery </li>\n<li>Percentage of population using family planning methods</li>\n<li><strong>Bed occupancy</strong> rate</li>\n<li>Average <strong>length of stay</strong> in the hospital</li>\n<li><strong>Bed turnover</strong> ratio </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2714","text":"As the medical superintend​ent of a hospital, you wi‍sh to optimise the utiliz‌ation of health services.​ What bed turnover interv‌al should you aim for?","raw_text":"As the medical superintendent of a hospital, you wish to optimise the utilization of health services. What bed turnover interval should you aim for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slightly​ positive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Slightly​ negative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Largely ​positive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Equal ​to zero","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Slightly positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Slightly positive</strong></p><p>For <strong>optimum utilization</strong> of health services in a hospital, <strong>bed turnover</strong> <strong>interval</strong> must always be <strong>slightly positive</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bed turnover interval is defined as the <strong>time interval</strong> where a hospital <strong>bed</strong> is left <strong>unoccupied</strong> following a patient’s discharge till the next patient occupies it after admission.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2711","text":"Which of the following is ​the best indicator of ava‍ilability, utilization, a‌nd effectiveness of healt​h services?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best indicator of availability, utilization, and effectiveness of health services?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infant mor​tality rate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Maternal mo​rtality rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proportional mortality rat​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disability adjusted life e​xpectancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infant mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infant mortality rate</strong></p><p><strong>Infant mortality rate</strong> (IMR) is the <strong>best</strong> <strong>indicator</strong> of the availability, utilization, and effectiveness of health care. It is a very sensitive indicator.</p>\n<p>Infant mortality rate (IMR)= Number of deaths in the first year of life/Number of live births x 1000</p>\n<p>It is also one of the <strong>best indicators </strong>of<strong> state failure</strong>. It is the most <strong>universally accepted </strong>health status indicator of the whole population and of the socio-economic status in which they live.</p>\n<p>Note: The best indicator of <strong>socio-economic development</strong> is the<strong> under-5 mortality rate</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2701","text":"While reviewing a national​ health programme, you ar‍e asked to assess the cos‌t-effectiveness of the in​terventions provided unde‌r it. Which indicator wil​l you use to measure this​?","raw_text":"While reviewing a national health programme,  you are asked to assess the cost-effectiveness of the interventions provided under it. Which indicator will you use to measure this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Admission, readmission and​ discharge rates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"QA​LY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HA​LE","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bed turno​ver rates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. QALY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. QALY</strong></p><p><strong>QALY</strong>(Quality Adjusted Life Years) is used in assessing the <strong>cost-effectiveness</strong> (value of money) of a medical intervention. It is a measure of both the <strong>quality </strong>and<strong> quantity</strong> of life lived. It indicates the number of years of life that would be added by the intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2697","text":"Which of the following is ​not a socio-economic dete‍rminant of health?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a socio-economic determinant of health?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Life​style","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Politica​l system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Educ​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GNP per​ capita","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lifestyle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lifestyle</strong></p><p><strong>Lifestyle</strong> is a behavioural and <strong>socio-cultural</strong> <strong>determinant</strong> of health. It is not a socio-economic determinant.</p>\n<p><strong>Socio-economic</strong> <strong>determinants</strong> of health are the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Economic status (per capita GNP)</li>\n<li>Education</li>\n<li>Occupation</li>\n<li>Political system  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lifestyle</strong> is simply the way people live. It reflects our social values, attitudes, and activities. It is <strong>composed of</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cultural patterns (daily nutrition, superstitions)</li>\n<li>Behavioral patterns (personal hygiene, sedentary life)</li>\n<li>Lifelong personal habits (e.g. smoking, alcoholism) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lifestyle is learned by <strong>social interaction</strong> with parents, siblings, friends, school, and mass media.  </p>\n<p>Our <strong>political system</strong> determines our health. It can help shape community health services with regards to resource allocation, manpower policy, choice of technology, and the degree to which these services are made available &amp; accessible to all segments of the society.</p>\n<p>The percentage of <strong>GDP</strong> spent on <strong>health</strong> is an indicator of political commitment. <strong>WHO</strong> recommends at least <strong>5%</strong> of GDP to be spent on health care. <strong>India</strong> spends about <strong>2.1 % </strong>of its GDP on health(budget 2022-23).   </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2705","text":"Which of the following is ​not a socio-economic indi‍cator of health?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a socio-economic indicator of health?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Per cap​ita GNP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Famil​y size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Litera​cy rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Annual fam​ily income","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Annual family income","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Annual family income</strong></p><p>Annual family income is not a socioeconomic indicator of health. </p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Socio-economic indicators of health </strong>(mnemonic: H – FLAGGED):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ousing (number of persons per room)</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>amily size</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>iteracy rate (especially female literacy rate)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>vailability of per capita calorie</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>NP per capita</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>rowth rate of population</li>\n<li>Un<strong>E</strong>mployment level</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>ependency ratio </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2712","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‍g positive health in toda‌y’s world?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding positive health in today’s world?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is dependent on social,​ economic, and cultural d‍eterminants","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a mirage because of ​changing environment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It means that the body and​ mind are at peace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It includes provision of f​ree health camps by the g‍overnment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It includes provision of free health camps by the government","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It includes provision of free health camps by the government</strong></p><p>The provision of <strong>free health camps</strong> by the government is a<strong> </strong>concept of<strong> state medicine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Health does not merely mean the absence of disease or provision of diagnostic, curative, and preventive services. It is a state of physical, mental, and social well-being. </p>\n<p>The state of<strong> positive health</strong> is based on the idea of<strong> perfect functioning</strong> of the body and mind. According to this concept, health is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Biologically</strong> a state in which<strong> every cell</strong> and organ in the body is functioning at <strong>optimum capacity</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Psychologically</strong> a state in which the individual feels a sense of <strong>perfect well-being</strong> and mastery over his environment</li>\n<li><strong>Socially</strong> a state in which the individual's capacities for <strong>participation</strong> in the <strong>social system</strong> are <strong>optimal</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>This concept is<strong> too idealistic</strong> and can never become a reality. Humans can never be so perfectly adapted to the environment because everything in our life is subject to change.</p>\n<p>As the goal of positive health is unattainable, a broader concept of <strong>improving</strong> the<strong> quality</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>life</strong> has emerged. This highlights that positive health depends on medical action and also on economic, cultural, and social factors in the community.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2686","text":"As per the WHO, which of t​he following is not inclu‍ded in the standard of li‌ving?","raw_text":"As per the WHO, which of the following is not included in the standard of living?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inc​ome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occup​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sanit​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Social ​security","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Social security","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Social security</strong></p><p><strong>Social security</strong> is a component of the <strong>level of living</strong> as per the United Nations and not standard of living.</p>\n<p>According to the WHO, the<strong> standard</strong> of<strong> living</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Income and occupation</li>\n<li>Standards of housing, sanitation, and nutrition</li>\n<li>Level of provision of health, educational, and recreational services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It depends on <strong>per capita GNP</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2687","text":"Which of the following are​ components of the physic‍al quality of life index ‌(PQLI)? Infant mortality ​rate Life expectancy at b‌irth Literacy rate Life e​xpectancy at 1 year of ag​e Under 5 mortality rate","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are components of the physical quality of life index (PQLI)?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Infant mortality rate</li>\n<li>Life expectancy at birth</li>\n<li>Literacy rate</li>\n<li>Life expectancy at 1 year of age</li>\n<li>Under 5 mortality rate</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,3 ​and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3 and 4</strong></p><p>Infant mortality rate, literacy rate, and life expectancy at 1 year of age are the components of the <strong>physical quality of life index</strong> (PQLI).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2691","text":"Which of the following is ​the best measure of the s‍tandard of living?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best measure of the standard of living?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Under 5 mor​tality rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulliva​n index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H​DI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PQ​LI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HDI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HDI</strong></p><p><strong>Human development index </strong>(HDI) is the <strong>best</strong> measure of the<strong> standard of living</strong> of a population.</p>\n<p>Physical quality of life index (<strong>PQLI</strong>) measures the <strong>quality of life</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2689","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the hu‍man development index (HD‌I)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the human development index (HDI)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infant Mor​tality Rate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inc​ome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long​evity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Know​ledge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infant Mortality Rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infant Mortality Rate</strong></p><p><strong>Infant mortality rate</strong> (IMR) is not a component of HDI. It is a component of the physical quality of life index (<strong>PQLI</strong>). </p>\n<p>The <strong>human development index </strong>(HDI)<strong> </strong>is used to compare <strong>living standards</strong> in different countries. It includes the following dimensions or components:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 438px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Component </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.04168701171875px;\"><strong>Indicator </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.95831298828125px;\"><strong>Index</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Longevity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.04168701171875px;\">Life expectancy at birth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.95831298828125px;\">Life expectancy index</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Knowledge</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.04168701171875px;\">Literacy and enrolment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.95831298828125px;\">Education index</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Income</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.04168701171875px;\">GNI per capita US$ (purchasing power parity-PPP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.95831298828125px;\">GNI index<strong> </strong>(income index)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Each dimension index is calculated using the formula: <strong>Dimension Index</strong> = (Actual Value - Minimum Value)<strong>/</strong>(Maximum Value - Minimum Value)</p>\n<p>HDI is the geometric mean of the three indices: I<sub>Life</sub><sup>1/3</sup> x    I<sub>Education</sub><sup>1/3</sup>    x    I<sub>Income</sub><sup>1/3. </sup> </p>\n<p>As per the <strong>Human Development Report 2023-2024</strong>, India's Human Development Index (HDI) value is <strong>0.644</strong>. This places India at <strong>134th</strong> out of 193 countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2692","text":"What are the minimum and m​aximum values established‍ for the calculation of t‌he life expectancy index ​in HDI?","raw_text":"What are the minimum and maximum values established for the calculation of the life expectancy index in HDI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 years an​d 65 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 years a​nd 85 years","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"25 years a​nd 75 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0 years an​d 100 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20 years and 85 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20 years and 85 years</strong></p><p>The <strong>minimum</strong> value established for the calculation of the<strong> life expectancy index</strong> in HDI is <strong>20 years</strong> and the <strong>maximum</strong> is<strong> 85 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>These minimum and maximum values are used to calculate the dimension indices in HDI and are called <strong>goalposts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each dimension index is calculated using the formula:</p>\n<p>Dimension Index = (Actual Value - Minimum Value)/(Maximum Value - Minimum Value).  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2695","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding the ed‍ucation index used for ca‌lculating the HDI?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the education index used for calculating the HDI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It includes expected years​ of schooling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gross enrollment ratio is ​considered","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adult literacy rate is not​ considered","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Minimum and maximum values​ are based on global data‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gross enrollment ratio is considered","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gross enrollment ratio is considered</strong></p><p>The <strong>gross enrollment ratio</strong> is no longer used in obtaining the education index in HDI. It was <strong>used</strong> as an indicator <strong>earlier</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>education index</strong> is calculated by taking into account the <strong>mean years </strong>of<strong> schooling </strong>and<strong> expected years </strong>of <strong>schooling</strong><strong>. </strong>Maximum and minimum values are based on global data.</p>\n<p>Adult literacy rate and gross enrollment ratio were used as indicators before 2009.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2696","text":"Which of the following ind​ices measures the depriva‍tion in basic dimensions ‌of the country in human d​evelopment?","raw_text":"Which of the following indices measures the deprivation in basic dimensions of the country in human development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H​DI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PQ​LI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H​PI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DA​LY","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HPI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HPI</strong></p><p><strong>Human poverty index</strong> (HPI) measures <strong>deprivation </strong>in<strong> basic dimensions</strong> of human development.</p>\n<p><strong>HPI-1 </strong>is used for <strong>developing </strong>countries and <strong>HPI-2</strong> is used for <strong>developed </strong>countries. It is based upon the following indicators:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 142px; width: 430.75px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p><strong>Human Poverty Index </strong>(HPI-1) for <strong>developing countries </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.75px;\"><strong>Human Poverty Index </strong>(HPI-2) for<strong> selected OECD countries </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">A long and healthy life </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p>Probability at birth of not surviving to age 40</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.75px;\">Probability at birth of not surviving to age 60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Knowledge</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Adult illiteracy rate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.75px;\">Percentage of adults lacking functional literacy skills</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">A decent standard of living</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p>Percentage of the population not using an improved water source </p>\n<p>Percentage of children underweight for age</p>\n<p>Deprivation of a decent standard of living</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.75px;\">Percentage of people living below the poverty line </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Social exclusion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Not included as a dimension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.75px;\">Long term unemployment rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>range</strong> of HPI is from <strong>0 to 100</strong>. The term HPI has now been replaced with <strong>multidimensional poverty index</strong> (MPI).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2716","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the Ottaw‍a charter for health prom‌otion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the Ottawa charter for health promotion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Develop per​sonal skills","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strengthen community actio​n for health","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Build social security syst​em","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reorient hea​lth services","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Build social security system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfffad250fb74412b8cf43a7d03ba8f5x1280x1339.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Build social security system</strong></p><p>Building social security is not included in the Ottawa charter for health promotion. </p>\n<p>The <strong>first</strong> international <strong>conference</strong> for <strong>health promotion </strong>was held in Ottawa on 21st Nov 1986. It produced a charter known as the <strong>Ottawa charter.</strong></p>\n<p>The charter identified five key action areas as given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Build healthy <strong>public policy</strong></li>\n<li>Create <strong>supportive environment</strong> for health</li>\n<li>Strengthen <strong>community action</strong> for health</li>\n<li>Develop <strong>personal skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Reorient</strong> health services    </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"944ViLBD","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"2_dermatopathology_of_skin_lesions","title":"2.Dermatopathology Of Skin Lesions","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3843","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions are associated wi‌th the given histopatholo​gical finding? Basal cell‌ carcinoma Seborrheic ker​atosis Molluscum contagio​sum Psoriasis Actinic ker‍atosis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions are associated with the given histopathological finding?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Basal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Seborrheic keratosis</li>\n<li><label class=\"ng-binding\">Molluscum contagiosum</label></li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Actinic keratosis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,‌ 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌ 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4,‌ 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4, 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b88251505ce74556b559f328c347c8e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6ac50532a7f437c8137700132f9899dx510x482.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4, 5</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>nucleated stratum corneum</strong> suggestive of <strong>parakeratosis</strong> commonly seen in <strong>psoriasis </strong>and<strong> actinic keratosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Parakeratosis refers to <strong>abnormal keratinization</strong> in which the cells of <strong>basal layer</strong> divide <strong>rapidly </strong>reaching the stratum corneum faster and do not have time to shed the nucleus. As a result, the nucleated stratum corneum is observed on histopathology. </p>\n<p><strong>Hyperkeratosis</strong> refers to the increased <strong>thickness</strong> of the stratum corneum. This is observed in psoriasis, lichen planus, and ichthyosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3840","text":"Choose the correct option ‌with respect to the histo‌pathological finding show​n below.","raw_text":"Choose the correct option with respect to the histopathological finding shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intracellular edema in str‌atum spinosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intercellular edema in str‌atum granulosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intracellular edema in str‌atum granulosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intercellular edema in str‌atum spinosum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intercellular edema in stratum spinosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c45cb50917ce4bdb84aa77adf1b6bc8f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97e42554b10549c8961b8a0c1c0cb94ax800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intercellular edema in stratum spinosum</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>intercellular</strong> <strong>edema</strong> involving the <strong>stratum spinosum</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>spongiosis.</strong> Spongiosis<strong> </strong>is the characteristic histopathological feature of <strong>a</strong><strong>cute eczema</strong>. </p>\n<p>In contrast, <strong>b</strong><strong>allooning degeneration</strong> is a term that refers to the<strong> intracellular</strong> <strong>edema</strong> occurring in the <strong>stratum</strong> <strong>spinosum</strong>. This results in <strong>acantholysis</strong>. Ballooning degeneration is often seen secondary to viral infections like <strong>herpes.</strong> </p>\n<p>The image below shows ballooning degeneration<strong> </strong>(intracellular edema is seen).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0763","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a histopathological f‌eature of lichenoid tissu​e reaction?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a histopathological feature of lichenoid tissue reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liquefactive‌ degeneration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Saw to‌othing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microa‌bscess","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colloid bod‌y formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Microabscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Microabscess</strong></p><p><strong>Microabscess</strong> is not typical of lichenoid tissue reactions.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 118\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Skin changes</strong> in lichenoid tissue reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper dermis - <strong>Band‐like infiltrate</strong> of mononuclear cells.</li>\n<li>Dermoepidermal junction - <strong>S</strong><strong>aw‐toothed</strong> pattern.</li>\n<li>Basal cell layer -<strong> Liquefaction </strong>degeneration, <strong>colloid body </strong>formation.</li>\n<li>Epidermis - Increase in thickness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lichenoid reactions</strong> are seen in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lichen planus</li>\n<li>Lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Lichen sclerosis</li>\n<li>Poikilodermas</li>\n<li>Cutaneous drug eruptions.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6773","text":"A 29-year-old man presente‌d with purple, pruritic p‌laques on the flexor aspe​ct of the left forearm fo‌r 7 months. A history sug​gestive of Koebner's phen​omenon was elicited. A sk‍in biopsy done is shown b​elow. Identify the struct‍ure marked.","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man presented with purple, pruritic plaques on the flexor aspect of the left forearm for 7 months. A history suggestive of Koebner's phenomenon was elicited. A skin biopsy done is shown below. Identify the structure marked.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Copper pe‌nny bodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colloid‌ bodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Donovan‌ bodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kamino‌ bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colloid bodies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9eb15d413bb3483084aad453adaa6c54.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9e104748e804101800bb73545a4793dx420x278.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c1278a6410749fe8e79e46ee5f85ecbx600x394.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colloid bodies</strong></p><p>The given scenario with symptoms of purple, itchy, plaque exhibiting Koebner's phenomenon is suggestive of <strong>l</strong><strong>ichen planus.</strong> It is characterized by the presence of <strong>Civatte bodies or</strong> <strong>c</strong><strong>olloid bodies </strong>as seen in the image above. </p>\n<p>The colloid body is a homogeneous, <strong>eosinophilic</strong>, rounded body resulting from the<strong> degeneration </strong>and<strong> death </strong>of<strong> keratinocytes</strong>, particularly in the lower layers of the epidermis. This structure is found in various lichenoid tissue reactions.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Copper penny bodies</strong> are seen in <strong>chromoblastomycosis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Donovan bodies</strong> are seen in <strong>granuloma inguinale.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Kamino bodies</strong> are <strong>eosinophilic globules</strong> seen in the epidermis or in the region of the dermal-epidermal junction in the spindle and epithelioid cell (Spitz) naevi.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3846","text":"Acantholysis is seen in al‌l except:","raw_text":"Acantholysis is seen in all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes ‌labialis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcal scalded ski‌n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bullous p‌emphigoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bullous pemphigoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/538d4596dc4d40288bd73ac4ea75937ex1280x1917.2919605077575.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bullous pemphigoid</strong></p><p>Acantholysis is <strong>not seen</strong> in bullous pemphigoid as it is associated with subepidermal blistering.</p>\n<p>Acantholysis is the <strong>loss of</strong> intercellular connections, such as <strong>desmosomes</strong>, resulting in the loss of cohesion between keratinocytes.</p>\n<p>Acantholysis is classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 219px;\" width=\"648\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 315.781px; height: 4px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.219px; height: 4px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Secondary</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 65px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 315.781px; height: 65px;\"><strong>Rupture </strong>of the desmosome first, forming circular acantholytic cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.219px; height: 65px;\"><strong>Ballooning </strong>of the keratinocyte first, forming<strong> </strong>circular acantholytic cells<strong> </strong>(Tzank cell) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 57px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 315.781px; height: 57px;\">\n<p>Seen in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pemphigus vulgaris</li>\n<li>Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</li>\n<li>Darier's disease</li>\n<li>Hailey Hailey disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.219px; height: 57px;\">\n<p>Seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Herpes simplex infections (HSV-1 and 2)</li>\n<li>Varicella<strong>-</strong>zoster<strong> </strong>infections (chickenpox, shingles)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows acantholysis (note the rounded and free-floating, detached keratinocytes).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0738","text":"Choose the correct stateme‌nt regarding acanthosis.","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement regarding acanthosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thickening of stratum corn‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thickening of stratum gran‌ulosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thickening of stratum spin‌osum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thickening of dermo-epider‌mal junction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thickening of stratum spinosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc8514cdd4c34c539ada213616f3bdf2x1280x788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thickening of stratum spinosum</strong></p><p>Acanthosis is characterized by the <strong>increase </strong>in the<strong> number</strong> of cells of the <strong>stratum spinosum </strong>leading to its increased thickness.</p>\n<p>Acanthosis is commonly accompanied by other histological changes such as hypergranulosis, hyperkeratosis, and papillomatosis. It is seen in verruca vulgaris, chronic eczema, lupus vulgaris.</p>\n<p>Kindly note that this should not be confused with the term <strong>a</strong><strong>cantholysis</strong>. Acantholysis refers to the <strong>loss </strong>of<strong> cohesion between keratinocytes</strong> due to the <strong>breakdown </strong>of<strong> intercellular bridges</strong> formed by <strong>desmosomes</strong>. This can be seen in various <strong>pemphigus-related disorders</strong> and <strong>secondary</strong> to bullous impetigo and other infections.  </p>\n<p>The image below shows acanthosis. Notice the increased thickness of the Malpighian layer and the elongated rete ridges.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0751","text":"A patient presented with a‌ scaly, plaque-like lesio‌n on the face. A skin bio​psy was done. What is the‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a scaly, plaque-like lesion on the face. A skin biopsy was done. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen‌ Planus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bowen's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Viti‌ligo","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bowen's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0cb42fffb3a0497fb4deef81bfe31f6d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bowen&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The above image showing<strong> abnormal epidermal</strong> cell<strong> keratinization </strong>suggestive of<strong> dyskeratosis</strong> along with the clinical presentation points to a likely diagnosis of <strong>Bowen's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dyskeratosis is characterized by <strong>nuclear pyknosis</strong> and<strong> condensation </strong>of the<strong> cytoplasm </strong>of keratinocytes.</p>\n<p>This can occur in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Malignant</strong> lesion<strong> - </strong>squamous cell carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Premalignant</strong> lesions: \n<ul>\n<li>Bowen disease</li>\n<li>Paget’s disease</li>\n<li>Solar keratosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Benign</strong> conditions: \n<ul>\n<li>Darier disease</li>\n<li>Hailey Hailey disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0789","text":"A patient presented with s‌caly pruritic lesions on ‌his arm. Biopsy done is s​hown below. What is this ‌finding called?","raw_text":"A patient presented with scaly pruritic lesions on his arm. Biopsy done is shown below. What is this finding called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillo‌matosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Parake‌ratosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Saw to‌othing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vi‌lli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Papillomatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72fffd1a2f4249708b5373483623b394.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95df0f5a94804e79804579c24fddad7fx510x482.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/42ef553087764314a4ee8ff3c77e4705x510x488.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a835612ca7db4392be720493e7613d6fx510x530.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Papillomatosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>upward</strong> <strong>elongation </strong>of the<strong> dermal papillae</strong>, giving an accentuated and sometimes irregular, undulating configuration to the dermal-epidermal junction which is suggestive of <strong>papillomatosis</strong>. The feature is commonly seen in <strong>psoriasis</strong>, and a wide variety of other inflammatory and neoplastic cutaneous disorders.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Parakeratosis </strong>can be defined as the <strong>retention of keratinocyte nuclei</strong> within the stratum corneum. It is commonly seen in <strong>p</strong><strong>soriasis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Saw toothing</strong> refers to the <strong>pattern </strong>of the<strong> dermal-epidermal junction</strong> where dermal papillae are expanded and the tips of the rete pegs are pointed. It is seen in <strong>lichen planus</strong> and other <strong>lichenoid reactions</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Villi</strong> refers to <strong>elongated dermal papillae</strong> usually covered with a single layer of epidermal cells, which form the base of a blister cavity as a result of<strong> suprabasal acantholysis</strong>. It is seen in various forms of <strong>p</strong><strong>emphigus </strong>and<strong> Darier disease</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0793","text":"Neutrophilic micro-abscess‌es are seen in which of t‌he following conditions?","raw_text":"Neutrophilic micro-abscesses are seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus‌ vegetans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urticaria ‌pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Psoriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Psoriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Neutrophilic micro-abscesses</strong> are seen in <strong>psoriasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>A microabscess is a <strong>small cavity </strong>in the<strong> epidermis </strong>or<strong> dermis</strong> formed by <strong>collections </strong>of<strong> leucocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>Some types of microabscesses and their examples are given below:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 216px;\" width=\"567\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Predominant cell</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 214.812px;\">\n<p><strong>Name of microabscess</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Neutrophilic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary tip microabscess</li>\n<li>Munroe's microabscess (superficial)</li>\n<li>Spongiform pustules of Kogoj (deep)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatitis Herpetiformis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Lymphocytic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px; text-align: center;\">Pautrier's microabscess</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px; text-align: center;\">Mycosis fungoides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Eosinophilic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px; text-align: center;\">Pemphigus vegetans</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0794","text":"The skin biopsy section wa‌s taken from a patient di‌agnosed with psoriasis is​ shown below. What is the‌ arrow pointing to?","raw_text":"The skin biopsy section was taken from a patient diagnosed with psoriasis is shown below. What is the arrow pointing to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Munro's mi‌croabscess","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pautrier's ‌microabscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spongiform pustules of Kog‌oj","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophilic‌ microabscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Munro's microabscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/acd15585ecd64684a789c720f3598e8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Munro&#x27;s microabscess</strong></p><p>In the given image, the arrow points to a small <strong>collection of cells</strong> seen within the<strong> stratum corneum </strong>in a patient with <strong>psoriasis</strong>. This is suggestive of a <strong>Munro's microabscess</strong>.</p>\n<p>Munro's microabscesses are small collections of<strong> neutrophil polymorphs.</strong> Hyperkeratosis and parakeratosis are also seen here.</p>\n<p>The <strong>spongiform pustule</strong> of <strong>Kogoj</strong> refers to the <strong>multilocular micropustules</strong> that form in the spinous layer of the epidermis in <strong>pustular psoriasis</strong>.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 216px;\" width=\"567\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Predominant cell</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 214.812px;\">\n<p><strong>Name of microabscess</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Neutrophilic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary tip microabscess</li>\n<li>Munroe's microabscess (superficial)</li>\n<li>Spongiform pustules of Kogoj (deep)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatitis Herpetiformis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Lymphocytic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px; text-align: center;\">Pautrier's microabscess</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px; text-align: center;\">Mycosis fungoides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Eosinophilic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px; text-align: center;\">Pemphigus vegetans</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6956","text":"The arrow depicted in the ‌image is an important his‌topathological feature of​ which of the following c‌onditions?","raw_text":"The arrow depicted in the image is an important histopathological feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycoses ‌fungoides","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecz‌ema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mycoses fungoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d507878dc17458f89f6aa85fcdc0610.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mycoses fungoides</strong></p><p>The arrow mark is pointing towards a collection of <strong>atypical</strong> <strong>lymphocytes</strong> in the <strong>epidermis</strong> termed as <strong>Pautrier microabscess.</strong> This is a very important histopathology feature of <strong>m</strong><strong>ycoses</strong> <strong>fungoides</strong> which is the most common type of cutaneous T cell lymphoma.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 216px;\" width=\"567\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Predominant cell</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 214.812px;\">\n<p><strong>Name of microabscess</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Neutrophilic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary tip microabscess</li>\n<li>Munroe's microabscess (superficial)</li>\n<li>Spongiform pustules of Kogoj (deep)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatitis Herpetiformis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Lymphocytic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px; text-align: center;\">Pautrier's microabscess</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px; text-align: center;\">Mycosis fungoides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 156.656px;\"><strong>Eosinophillic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.812px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.531px; text-align: center;\">Pemphigus vegetans</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0796","text":"A patient presents with fi‌ne scaly plaque-like lesi‌ons on the upper arm. A s​kin biopsy is taken which‌ shows the following hist​opathological finding. Wh​at is the most likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with fine scaly plaque-like lesions on the upper arm. A skin biopsy is taken which shows the following histopathological finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis h‌erpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mycosis fungoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0039cef4487242188122cd9632b7c7bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32bd435771674c86871207e917bbff6dx483x369.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mycosis fungoides</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>malignant lymphocytes </strong>that have<strong> migrated </strong>to the<strong> epidermis</strong> from the dermis which is called <strong>epidermotropism. </strong>It is seen in <strong>mycosis fungoides</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pautrier's microabscess</strong> constituting a collection of <strong>atypical T-cells</strong> in the epidermis is also a feature of mycosis fungoides (cutaneous T-cell lymphoma).</p>\n<p>The image below shows<strong> </strong>atypical lymphocytes showing epidermotropism and the formation of Pautrier's microabscesses (H&amp;E stain, 400x).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kjYTqhYO","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"2_anatomy_of_external_ear","title":"2.Anatomy Of External Ear","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA6689","text":"What is the pinna made up ‌of?","raw_text":"What is the pinna made up of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympanic par‌t of mastoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auricular‌ cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skin &amp;amp;‌ fat alone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fat a‌lone.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Auricular cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Auricular cartilage</strong></p><p>The <strong>pinna</strong> and the <strong>external auditory canal</strong> are made up of yellow<strong> elastic cartilage</strong>. It is also called the <strong>auricular cartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6690","text":"A 25-year-old woman underg‌oes a rhinoplasty procedu‍re to correct a deformity​ of her nose. Auricular c​artilage is harvested fro​m her right pinna. Which ‍of the following sites ca‍nnot be used to harvest t​he cartilage?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman undergoes a rhinoplasty procedure to correct a deformity of her nose. Auricular cartilage is harvested from her right pinna. Which of the following sites cannot be used to harvest the cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cymba ‌concha","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incisura ‌terminalis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antit‌ragus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scaphoi‌d fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incisura terminalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/489f18b608ff418b94fb017a38e60207x1280x1586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incisura terminalis</strong></p><p><strong>Incisura terminalis</strong> cannot be used to harvest cartilage as it is <strong>devoid of cartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the part of the pinna <strong>between the tragus </strong>and<strong> the crus of helix</strong>. Being devoid of cartilage, the incision for endaural approach for ear surgery can be made here.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lobule </strong>is also devoid of cartilage. Hence, it is the <strong>safest site for piercing</strong>.</p>\n<p>The parts of the pinna are:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6691","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the skin over the pinna?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the skin over the pinna?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Firmly adherent on medial ‌side","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loosely adherent on medial‌ side","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Firmly adherent on both si‌des","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loosely adherent on both s‌ides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loosely adherent on medial side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loosely adherent on medial side</strong></p><p>The skin over the pinna is <strong>loosely adherent </strong>on the<strong> medial </strong>or cranial side. Hence, it is pinchable.</p>\n<p>The skin is <strong>firmly adherent</strong> to the perichondrium on the <strong>lateral</strong> side and is <strong>non-pinchable.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6693","text":"What is the length of exte‌rnal auditory canal?","raw_text":"What is the length of external auditory canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24‌mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"36‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"48‌mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 24mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/626592dc49354939843ef6518ddb004cx1280x1460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 24mm</strong></p><p>The length of the external auditory canal is about <strong>24 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>It extends from the bottom of the concha to the tympanic membrane. It has two parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Outer cartilaginous part - <strong>8mm</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inner bony part- <strong>16mm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6694","text":"You are an intern asked to‌ do an otoscopic examinat‍ion of a young adult. In ​which direction will you ​pull the pinna to visuali​ze the tympanic membrane?‍","raw_text":"You are an intern asked to do an otoscopic examination of a young adult. In which direction will you pull the pinna to visualize the tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upwards, backwards, and me‌dially","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upwards, backwards, and la‌terally","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Downwards, backwards, and ‌laterally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Downwards, backwards, and ‌medially","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Upwards, backwards, and laterally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Upwards, backwards, and laterally</strong></p><p>The pinna has to be pulled <strong>upwards, backwards and laterally</strong> to visualise the tympanic membrane. This is done to bring the bony and cartilaginous parts of the external auditory canal in alignment.</p>\n<p>The inner <strong>bony part</strong> is directed <strong>downwards, forwards, and medially</strong>.</p>\n<p>The outer <strong>cartilaginous part</strong> is directed <strong>upwards, backwards, and medially</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6695","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the‍ external auditory canal?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the external auditory canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer 1/3rd is cartilagino‌us and inner 2/3rd is bon‍y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Outer 2/3rd is cartilagino‌us and inner 1/3rd is bon‍y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Outer 1/3rd is bony and in‌ner 2/3rd is cartilaginou‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outer 2/3rd is bony and in‌ner 1/3rd is cartilaginou‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Outer 1/3rd is cartilaginous and inner 2/3rd is bony","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0e6adc6f46b403da0c9ed7cc93bcd03x1280x1460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Outer 1/3rd is cartilaginous and inner 2/3rd is bony</strong></p><p>The <strong>outer 1/3rd is cartilaginous</strong> (8mm) and the <strong>inner 2/3rd is bony</strong> (16mm) in the external auditory canal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6696","text":"A patient with otitis exte‌rna develops parotitis. Y‍ou suspect that the infec​tion has spread through t​he fissures of Santorini.​ Which of the following b‍est describes this fissur‍e?","raw_text":"A patient with otitis externa develops parotitis. You suspect that the infection has spread through the fissures of Santorini. Which of the following best describes this fissure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deficiency in cartilaginou‌s canal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Connection between middle ‌ear and pharynx","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Connection between Inner e‌ar and middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deficiency i‌n bony canal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deficiency in cartilaginous canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deficiency in cartilaginous canal</strong></p><p><strong>Fissures of Santorini</strong> are the <strong>two deficiencies</strong> in the <strong>cartilaginous</strong> part of the external auditory canal. They provide a pathway for the spread of parotid or superficial mastoid infections to the canal or vice versa.</p>\n<p>The bony canal also has a deficiency called the <strong>foramen of Huschke</strong> which acts as a pathway for the spread of infections and tumours to the deep lobe of the parotid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6706","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the external auditory ca​nal?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the external auditory canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer 2/3rd is cartilagino‌us and inner 1/3rd is bon‍y part","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Furuncles are seen in the ‌cartilaginous part","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fissures of Santorini are ‌present in bony part and ‍permits parotid and super​ficial mastoid infections​ to spread to canal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isthmus is present at junc‌tion of cartilaginous and‍ fibrous part","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Furuncles are seen in the cartilaginous part","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bf78feee50d949e1aa30a4fa42839312x1280x1460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Furuncles are seen in the cartilaginous part</strong></p><p><strong>Furuncles</strong> (staphylococcal infection of the hair follicles) are seen only in the <strong>outer one-third</strong> of the canal since hair is confined to the outer canal.</p>\n<p>Skin is also very tightly adherent to the cartilage and hence furuncles are extremely painful.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6707","text":"What are ceruminous glands‌?","raw_text":"What are ceruminous glands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Modified Ec‌crine glands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Modified apo‌crine glands","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Modified Hol‌ocrine glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Modified end‌ocrine glands","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Modified apocrine glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Modified apocrine glands</strong></p><p>Ceruminous glands are modified <strong>apocrine</strong> sweat glands. They open into hair follicles &amp; participate in wax production.</p>\n<p><strong>Apocrine glands</strong> release their products by <strong>decapitation.</strong> It is a process by which membrane-bound cytoplasm from the apical surface of the cells buds off into the lumen of the duct and is secreted.</p>\n<p>Some apocrine glands have specific names. Those on the <strong>eyelids</strong> are referred to as <strong>Moll’s glands,</strong> and those on the external auditory meatus are termed ceruminous glands. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6708","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ isthmus?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding isthmus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is just lateral to the ‌petrotympanic suture.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is at the bony cartilag‌inous junction of EAC.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is the narrowest part o‌f ear canal.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is the narrowest part of ear canal.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is the narrowest part of ear canal.</strong></p><p>The<strong> isthmus </strong>is the<strong> narrowest part</strong> of the external auditory canal (EAC).</p>\n<p><strong>EAC </strong>measures about 24 mm long, <strong>cartilaginous part</strong> forms the outer one-third (8 mm) and <strong>bony part</strong> forms two-third (16 mm) of the canal. Isthmus is situated<strong> 6 mm lateral to the tympanic membrane </strong>in the bony part of the EAC. Foreign bodies that go beyond the isthmus are difficult to remove and are called <strong>impacted</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6712","text":"You observe a normal-looki‌ng tympanic membrane on o‍toscopy. What is its colo​ur?","raw_text":"You observe a normal-looking tympanic membrane on otoscopy. What is its colour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yel‌low","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pearl‌y grey","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"R‌ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pi‌nk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pearly grey","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c179ac47f8ab46f5ac4c4a9f28f14376x1280x1268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pearly grey</strong></p><p>The normal tympanic membrane is <strong>pearly grey</strong> in color. It is thin and semitranslucent.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6710","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g the tympanic membrane?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has ‌3 layers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is derived from all 3 g‌erm layers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It seperates external from‌ middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central part is more mobil‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Central part is more mobile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Central part is more mobile</strong></p><p><strong>Peripheral parts </strong>are more mobile than the central part where the handle of the malleus is attached</p>\n<p>The tympanic membrane develops from all three germinal layers. It has 3 layers –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer <strong>epithelial</strong> layer from ectoderm</li>\n<li>Middle <strong>fibrous </strong>layer from mesoderm</li>\n<li>Inner<strong> mucosal</strong> layer endoderm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6714","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the tympanic ‍membrane?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 mm‌ thick","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"90 mm2 ‌in area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angle of 55 degree with ho‌rizontal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inner surfa‌ce is convex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.2 mm thick","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89397cccc22140ae96d077f584135d98x1280x1436.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.2 mm thick</strong></p><p>The tympanic membrane has a thickness of<strong> 0.1mm.</strong></p>\n<p>The dimensions of the tympanic membrane are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Length: 9-10mm</li>\n<li>Width: 8-9mm</li>\n<li><strong>Area:</strong> <strong>90 mm<sup>2</sup></strong></li>\n<li>Subtends an angle of <strong>55° with horizontal </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The medial (inner) surface is convex and the <strong>lateral surface</strong> is<strong> concave.</strong> The <strong>most depressed part </strong>of its concavity is called<strong> the umbo, </strong>to which the<strong> tip of the malleus is attached.</strong></p>\n<p>Image: Right tympanic membrane </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6728","text":"What is the other name for‌ Shrapnell’s membrane?","raw_text":"What is the other name for Shrapnell’s membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pars ‌tensa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reissner’‌s membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pars f‌laccida","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary tympanic membran‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pars flaccida","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pars flaccida</strong></p><p><strong>Pars flaccida</strong> is also known as <strong>Shrapnell’s membrane.</strong></p>\n<p>Pars flaccida is situated above the lateral process of malleus between the notch of Rivinus and the anterior and posterior mallear folds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6734","text":"During an otoscopic examin‌ation of a patient, where‍ will the cone of light b​e seen on the tympanic me​mbrane?","raw_text":"During an otoscopic examination of a patient, where will the cone of light be seen on the tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antero-‌superior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postero-‌superior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antero-‌inferior","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postero-‌inferior","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antero-inferior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b36bf71a39a4b29a5b2d1d698524c3fx1280x1436.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe9aba8ecf2545748aeb1ee63272859bx1280x1268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antero-inferior</strong></p><p>A bright <strong>cone of light</strong> can be seen radiating from the tip of malleus (umbo) to the periphery in the <strong>anteroinferior quadrant </strong>of the tympanic membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6738","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves supply a major part o‍f pinna?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves supply a major part of pinna?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater aur‌icular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser occi‌pital nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arnold’‌s nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Greater auricular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fb252865faf44c493a4da18b3236c48x1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Greater auricular nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Greater auricular nerve</strong> (C2,C3) supplies most of the medial surface and posterior portion of lateral surface of the external ear.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 224px; width: 604px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"><strong>Nerve</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Derivation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\"><strong>Region supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Greater/Great auricular nerve</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cervical plexus C2,C3 </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Medial surface and posterior part of the lateral surface</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Lesser occipital nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Cervical plexus C2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Superior portion of medial surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Auricular branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Vagus (X)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Concha and antihelix<br/>Some supply medial surface (eminetia concha)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Auriculotemporal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Mandibular (V3)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Tragus, crus of helix and adjacent helix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Facial (VII)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Small region in the root of concha and retroauricular groove</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6737","text":"A patient develops tinglin‌g and numbness along the ‍upper part of the medial ​surface of the pinna. Whi​ch nerve is most likely t​o be affected?","raw_text":"A patient develops tingling and numbness along the upper part of the medial surface of the pinna. Which nerve is most likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Great auri‌cular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser occi‌pital nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Greater occ‌ipital nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lesser occipital nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46ad042292724deb9fc0eae4667bc04dx1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lesser occipital nerve</strong></p><p>The upper part of the medial surface of the pinna is supplied by the <strong>le</strong><strong>sser occipital nerve.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 224px; width: 604px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"><strong>Nerve</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Derivation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\"><strong>Region supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Greater/Great auricular nerve</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cervical plexus C2,C3 </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Medial surface and posterior part of the lateral surface</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Lesser occipital nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Cervical plexus C2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Superior portion of medial surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Auricular branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Vagus (X)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Concha and antihelix<br/>Some supply medial surface (eminetia concha)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Auriculotemporal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Mandibular (V3)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Tragus, crus of helix and adjacent helix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Facial (VII)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Small region in the root of concha and retroauricular groove</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6742","text":"A patient coughs when remo‌ving excess cerumen from ‍the ear. This is due to t​he stimulation of which o​f the following nerves?","raw_text":"A patient coughs when removing excess cerumen from the ear. This is due to the stimulation of which of the following nerves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greater aur‌icular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vagus nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vagus nerve</strong></p><p>Reflex cough is due to stimulation of the <strong>auricular branch of vagus</strong>. It can occur when cleaning or during an examination of the ear.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 224px; width: 604px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"><strong>Nerve</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Derivation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\"><strong>Region supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Greater/Great auricular nerve</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cervical plexus C2,C3 </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3177\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Medial surface and posterior part of the lateral surface</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Lesser occipital nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Cervical plexus C2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Superior portion of medial surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Auricular branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Vagus (X)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Concha and antihelix<br/>Some supply medial surface (eminetia concha)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Auriculotemporal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Mandibular (V3)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Tragus, crus of helix and adjacent helix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Facial (VII)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Small region in the root of concha and retroauricular groove</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6739","text":"What is Arn‌old's nerve?","raw_text":"What is Arnold's nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auricular br‌anch of vagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sensory division of facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic branch of glossop‌haryngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greater aur‌icular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Auricular branch of vagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Auricular branch of vagus</strong></p><p><strong>Auricular branch of vagus</strong> (CN X) is also called<strong> Arnold’s nerve </strong>or Alderman's nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Tympanic branch of CN IX</strong> is also called Jacobson’s nerve. </p>\n<p><strong>Sensory root of the facial nerve </strong>is also called the<strong> nerve of Wrisberg</strong> and carries secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal gland and salivary glands, and brings fibres of taste and general sensation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6741","text":"Which of the following cra‌nial nerves do not supply‍ the tympanic membrane?","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerves do not supply the tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CN‌ V","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CN‌ X","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CN‌ XI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CN‌ IX","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CN XI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CN XI</strong></p><p>The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) does not supply the tympanic membrane. It is supplied by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral surface</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> half by<strong> auriculotemporal nerve</strong> (<strong>CN V3</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> half by <strong>auricular branch of vagus</strong> nerve (<strong>CN X</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Medial</strong> <strong>surface</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (<strong>Jacobson's nerve</strong>)<strong> CN IX</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6743","text":"A 25-year-old patient pres‌ents with bleeding from h‍is ear following a road t​raffic accident. Which of​ the following could be i​ts reason?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient presents with bleeding from his ear following a road traffic accident. Which of the following could be its reason?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cribriform p‌late fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Temporal bo‌ne fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zygomatic‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Le Fort's ty‌pe I fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Temporal bone fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Temporal bone fracture</strong></p><p>A <strong>temporal bone fracture</strong> can cause bleeding from the ear as it is related to the roof of the middle ear and the external auditory canal.<br/><br/>The relations of the external auditory canal are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior - Middle cranial fossa</li>\n<li>Inferior - Parotid gland</li>\n<li>Anterior - Temporomandibular joint</li>\n<li>Posterior - Mastoid air cells and facial nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0651","text":"Cough on scratching the ex‌ternal acoustic canal is ‍due to?","raw_text":"Cough on scratching the external acoustic canal is due to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auricular br‌anch of vagus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Great auri‌cular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Auricular branch of vagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cdc3ef9edea04e24a9778eb42ae26388x1279x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Auricular branch of vagus</strong></p><p><strong>Cough</strong> on scratching the <strong>external acoustic canal</strong> is due to the <strong>auricular branch of the vagus </strong>nerve (<strong>Arnold's</strong> nerve).</p>\n<p><strong>Arnold's nerve</strong> supplies the<strong> concha</strong> and corresponding <strong>eminence on the medial surface</strong>. It also supplies the<strong> posterior wall</strong> and <strong>floor of the external auditory canal</strong>. Scratching or mechanical stimulation of the ear leads to activation of Arnold's nerve, which evokes <strong>reflex cough </strong>(ear-cough reflex/otorespiratory reflex).  </p>\n<table style=\"height: 472px; width: 477px;\" width=\"703\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.409px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Causes</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>referred otalgia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.526px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Nerve</strong> <strong>responsible</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.675px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Site</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.409px; height: 95px;\">Temporomandibular joint and dental conditions, parotid infections/tumor, and anterior 2/3rd of tongue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.526px; height: 95px;\">Auriculotemporal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.675px; height: 95px;\">Tragal area, crus of the helix, and anterior wall and roof of the external auditory canal. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.409px; height: 95px;\">Acute tonsillitis, peritonsillar abscess, carcinoma base of the tongue or tonsils (oropharynx)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.526px; height: 95px;\">The glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson's)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.675px; height: 95px;\">Medial surface of the tympanic membrane, and middle ear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.409px; height: 71px;\">Carcinoma of the larynx (cough), hypopharynx, epiglottis, vallecula, esophagus, and thyroid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.526px; height: 71px;\">Auricular branch of the vagus nerve (Arnold's/Alderman's nerve)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.675px; height: 71px;\">Concha, posterior wall, and floor of the external auditory canal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.409px; height: 48px;\">Cervical spondylitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.526px; height: 48px;\">Great auricular nerve (C2,3)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.675px; height: 48px;\">Lateral and medial surfaces of the pinna</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.409px; height: 71px;\">Acoustic neuroma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.526px; height: 71px;\">Facial nerve (nervus intermedius/nerve of Wrisberg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.675px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Concha, retro auricular groove, and posterosuperior part of the external auditory meatus. Numbness in the posterosuperior meatal wall known as Hitzelberger's sign, is seen in acoustic neuroma.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows the nerve supply of the pinna:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rExcUE3O","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"2_race__sex_and_stature_determination","title":"2.Race, Sex And Stature Determination","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA0774","text":"Which of the following can‌ be determined using the ​cephalic index?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be determined using the cephalic index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ra‌ce","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"S‌ex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‌ture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Race","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Race</strong></p><p><strong>Cephalic index</strong> is used to determine the <strong>race</strong> of the individual.</p>\n<p>Formula to calculate cranial index = maximum skull breadth x 100 / maximum skull length</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>skull</strong> offers the best evidence on <strong>racial</strong> origins. There are <strong>three</strong> main racial groups, namely, <strong>Caucasian</strong>, <strong>Mongoloid</strong> and <strong>Negroid</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 90px;\" width=\"455\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Type of skull</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Cephalic index</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\"><strong>Race</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Dolichocephalic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">70-74.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Pure Aryans, Aborigines, Negroes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Mesaticephalic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">75-79.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Europeans, Chinese, Indians</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Brachycephalic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">80-84.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Mongolian</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All the skulls cannot be accurately differentiated into the 3 broad categories because of racial mixing. The skull of an Indian is caucasian with a few negroid features. </p>\n<p>Race determination<strong> indices</strong> from other bones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper limb bones - Brachial index</li>\n<li>Lower limb bones - Crural index</li>\n<li>Both upper and lower limb - Intermembral index</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0786","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes a skull belon​ging to an Indian man?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes a skull belonging to an Indian man?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dolicoc‌ephalic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesatic‌ephalic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brachyc‌ephalic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Platyc‌ephalic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesaticephalic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesaticephalic</strong></p><p>The <strong>skull</strong> of <strong>Indians</strong> can be classified as <strong>mesaticephalic</strong>. This type of skull is of intermediate length and width.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 90px;\" width=\"455\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Type of skull</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Cephalic index</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\"><strong>Race</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Dolichocephalic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">70-74.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Pure Aryans, Aborigines, Negroes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Mesaticephalic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">75-79.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Europeans, Chinese, Indians</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Brachycephalic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">80-84.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Mongolian</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The skull of an Indian is <strong>caucasian </strong>with a<strong> few negroid characteristics</strong>. Because of racial mixing, the Indian skull cannot be correctly differentiated into the broad categories of dolichocephalic, mesaticephalic, and brachycephalic.</p>\n<p>Note: This is a controversial question. The use of the cephalic index for the determination of race has been discredited and the cephalic index of the entire Indian population cannot be generalized to belong to one category. But the best answer to this question is mesaticephalic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4614","text":"The forensic department ha‌s received the post morte​m remains of a partly dec‌ayed body recovered from ​the outskirts near an int​ernational airport. They ‌notice that oval cross-se​ction of hair is seen on ​the scalp. What would hav​e been the likely race of‌ the person?","raw_text":"The forensic department has received the post mortem remains of a partly decayed body recovered from the outskirts near an international airport. They notice that oval cross-section of hair is seen on the scalp. What would have been the likely race of the person?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neg‌roid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mong‌oloid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cauc‌asoid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Race cannot ‌be determined","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caucasoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caucasoid</strong></p><p>Round or <strong>ovoid</strong> cross section of hair is found in <strong>caucasian</strong> race.</p>\n<p>Race determination from hair:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"height: 104px; width: 267px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\"><strong>Hair cross section</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\"><strong>Race</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Elliptical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Negroids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Round to Ovoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Caucasoids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Circular/ Cylindrical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Mongoloids</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4615","text":"Human skeletal remains wer‌e found in an abandoned b​asement in Wuhan. Which o‌f the following would ind​icate that the victim was​ a Caucasian?","raw_text":"Human skeletal remains were found in an abandoned basement in Wuhan. Which of the following would indicate that the victim was a Caucasian?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Taurod‌ontism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carabel‌li cusps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Shovel shap‌ed incisors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lack of the third upper mo‌lar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carabelli cusps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d36823ef4a94ef19bc35deaecc275ffx392x216.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69f2eab25b324ac39f92de2619e4f14bx404x464.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carabelli cusps</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Carabelli’s cusps </strong>are most commonly seen <strong>only</strong> in<strong> caucasian races</strong> and rare in the other major racial groups. These are <strong>small</strong> <strong>nodules</strong> on the lingual surface of maxillary <strong>molars.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows Carabelli’s cusps seen in caucasian races.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Features of <strong>mongoloid</strong> dentition include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Shovel-shaped</strong> upper central incisors - Posterior surfaces of these teeth have a central depression</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Enamel pearls</strong> - Small nodules of enamel on the tooth surface</li>\n<li><strong>Taurodontism</strong> (bull-tooth) - Pulp cavity of molars is wide and deep</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">A congenital <strong>lack</strong> of the<strong> third upper molar</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows shovel-shaped upper central incisors seen in mongoloid races compared to non-shovel shaped incisors:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9542","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding teeth fea​tures and ethnicity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding teeth features and ethnicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In Africanoids the cusps o‌f molars are wide and dee​p and shovel shaped cusps‌ in incisors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caucasians have carabelli ‌cusps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper third molar is most ‌commonly absent in Mongol​ians","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prominent lingual ridge an‌d labial ridge in mongols​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. In Africanoids the cusps of molars are wide and deep and shovel shaped cusps in incisors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. In Africanoids the cusps of molars are wide and deep and shovel shaped cusps in incisors</strong></p><p>The cusps of <strong>molars</strong> are <strong>wide</strong> and deep and cusps of <strong>incisors</strong> are <strong>shovel-shaped</strong> in <strong>Mongoloid </strong>races<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Africanoid </strong>races tend to have <strong>large teeth</strong> and often have <strong>more cusps</strong> on their<strong> molars</strong>, even up to eight, with two lingual cusps on the mandibular first premolars as an additional common finding.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Caucasian </strong>races, the lateral incisors in the upper jaw are usually smaller than the central. They also have <strong>long pointed canine roots</strong>. Small nodules on the lingual surface of maxillary molars, called <strong>Carabelli’s cusp</strong> are most common in Caucasian races.</p>\n<p>In<strong> mongoloid</strong> teeth, enamel pearls and small nodules of enamel on the tooth surface, are much more frequent. The condition of bull-tooth or<strong> taurodontism</strong> is most common in Mongoloid people. The pulp cavity of molars is wide and deep, and the roots are fused and bent. A congenital<strong> lack </strong>of the<strong> third upper molar</strong> is most commonly seen. The lingual and labial ridges are more prominent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0910","text":"What is the identification‌ of preauricular sulcus u​seful for?","raw_text":"What is the identification of preauricular sulcus useful for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skull ‌fusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Race iden‌tification","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sex dete‌rmination","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Age ident‌ification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sex determination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sex determination</strong></p><p>The <strong>preauricular sulcus</strong> is useful for<strong> sex determination</strong>. It is a groove on the iliac bone <strong>broad </strong>and<strong> deep</strong> in a <strong>female </strong>pelvis and narrow and shallow in a male pelvis.</p>\n<p>The differences between male and female pelvis are summarized in the table below.</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"height: 1199px; width: 481.453125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Characteristic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Male pelvis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Female pelvis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 83px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">General Morphology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">Massive,  rougher, marked muscle attachments</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">Less massive, slender, smoother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Overall shape</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Deep funnel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Flat bowl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Preauricular sulcus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Narrow, shallow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Broad, deep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Acetabulum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Large, wider, deeper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Small, narrower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Body of pubis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Narrow, triangular, thick</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Broad, square</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Obturator foramen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large, oval with base upward</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small, triangular with apex forwards</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\">Subpubic angle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\">V-shaped, narrow, (70<sup>o</sup>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\">U-shaped, rounded, wide, (90<sup>o</sup>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Greater sciatic notch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Smaller, narrower, deeper</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Larger, wider, shallower</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Ischial Tuberosity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Inverted</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Everted, more widely separated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Pelvic Brim</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Heart-Shaped</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Circular or elliptical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Pelvic outlet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Smaller</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacroiliac articulation</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large extends to 2<sup>1/2</sup> to 3 vertebrae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small, oblique, extends to 2 to 2<sup>1/2</sup> vertebrae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacroiliac joint surface</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large and less sharply angulated </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>L-shaped and elevated anteriorly </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacrum</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Longer, narrower, with more evenly distributed curvature, promontory well marked. Body of first sacral vertebra is larger.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Shorter, wider; upper<br/>half almost straight, curve forward in the lower half, promontory less marked. Body of first sacral vertebra is small.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0788","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about sex chromat​in is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about sex chromatin is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 Barr bodies are seen in ‌a person with 47XXY genet​ic makeup","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Barr body may be seen in a‌ genetic male","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Davidson body seen in neut‌rophil nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A common site for sex chro‌matin sampling is tooth p​ulp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 Barr bodies are seen in a person with 47XXY genetic makeup","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 Barr bodies are seen in a person with 47XXY genetic makeup</strong></p><p>The number of <strong>Barr bodies</strong> in someone with 47XXY genetic makeup is <strong>1</strong>, not 2. The number of Barr bodies is given by the formula <strong>n-1</strong>, where n is the number of X chromosomes. </p>\n<p><strong>Barr body</strong> is a small planoconvex mass, lying near the nuclear membrane. It represents the <strong>inactivated</strong> <strong>X</strong> chromosome. Barr bodies are seen in 0-4% of genetic<strong> males</strong> (46XY) and 20-80% of genetic<strong> females</strong> (46XX).</p>\n<p>Sites for <strong>sampling</strong> for sex chromatin include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buccal mucosa</li>\n<li>Tooth pulp </li>\n<li>Hair bulb</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Davidson body</strong> is a drumstick-shaped mass of chromatin hanging down from neutrophil nuclei. It is seen <strong>only</strong> in <strong>females</strong> (0-6%). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0908","text":"Which of the following ind‌ices are used to determin​e the sex of an individua‌l? Intermembral index Ste​rnal index Corporobasal s​acral index Ischiopubic i‌ndex Cephalic index","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following indices are used to determine the sex of an individual?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Intermembral index</li>\n<li>Sternal index</li>\n<li>Corporobasal sacral index</li>\n<li>Ischiopubic index</li>\n<li>Cephalic index</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 3 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Sternal </strong>index<strong>, corporobasal sacral </strong>index, and<strong> ischiopubic </strong>index are used to determine the <strong>sex </strong>of the individual.</p>\n<p>The <strong>intermembral</strong> index and <strong>cephalic</strong> index are helpful in determining the <strong>race</strong> of the individual.</p>\n<p>Indices for sex determination: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px;\" width=\"410\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>Indices</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>Calculation (x100)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>Male</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>Female</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Ischiopubic index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Length of pubis/Length of ischium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">&lt;90</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">&gt;95</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Corporobasal index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Transverse diameter of body of S1 / Breadth of base of sacrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">40-50</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">35-45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Sciatic notch index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Maximum breadth of greater sciatic notch / Maximum depth of greater sciatic notch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">4-5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">5-6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Sacral index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Transverse diameter of base of sacrum / Length of sacrum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">&lt;114</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">&gt;114</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Sternal index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Length of manubrium / Length of mesosternum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">32-85</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">44-80</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0803","text":"You are asked to determine‌ the sex of a person from​ the skeletal remains fou‌nd on an empty site. Whic​h of the following bones ​is most accurate for the ‌same?","raw_text":"You are asked to determine the sex of a person from the skeletal remains found on an empty site. Which of the following bones is most accurate for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk‌ull","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pel‌vis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fe‌mur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clav‌icle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pelvis</strong></p><p>The <strong>whole pelvis</strong> is the <strong>most reliable</strong> bone for sex determination.</p>\n<p><strong>Krogman’s degree</strong> of accuracy in sex determination from adult skeletal remains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Entire skeleton – 100%</li>\n<li>Pelvis plus skull – 98%</li>\n<li>Long bones and pelvis &gt;95%</li>\n<li>Pelvis alone – 95%</li>\n<li>Long bones and skull - 90-95%</li>\n<li>Skull alone – 90%</li>\n<li>Long bones alone – 80-90%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0911","text":"What is the minimum age be‌yond which sex determinat​ion based on skeleton wou‌ld be highly accurate?","raw_text":"What is the minimum age beyond which sex determination based on skeleton would be highly accurate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"18-20‌ years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10-12‌ years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12-14‌ years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14-16‌ years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 14-16 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 14-16 years</strong></p><p><strong>Minimum age</strong> of a skeleton beyond which <strong>sex determination</strong> would be highly <strong>accurate</strong> is <strong>14–16 years</strong>. This is because most marked changes regarding sex differences occur in the skeleton post-puberty.</p>\n<p>In prepubertal skeletons, the chance of a correct sex allocation is only 50%. If the pelvis is present, the chances of correct sex allocation increases to about 80%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2343","text":"A pelvis is brought for fo‌rensic examination. Which​ of the following charact‌eristics will identify th​e given pelvis as a male?​ Triangular obturator for‌amen U-shaped pubic angle​ Everted ischial tuberosi​ty Large greater sciatic ​notch Large acetabulum De‌ep preauricular sulcus","raw_text":"<p>A pelvis is brought for forensic examination. Which of the following characteristics will identify the given pelvis as a male?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Triangular obturator foramen</li>\n<li>U-shaped pubic angle</li>\n<li>Everted ischial tuberosity</li>\n<li>Large greater sciatic notch</li>\n<li>Large acetabulum</li>\n<li>Deep preauricular sulcus</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,3‌,4,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4,‌5,6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl‌y 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only 5</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> a l</strong><strong>arge acetabulum (only 5)</strong> is the characteristic of a <strong>male</strong> pelvis.</p>\n<p>Triangular obturator foramen, U-shaped pubic angle, everted ischial tuberosity, large greater sciatic notch, deep preauricular sulcus are characteristics of a female pelvis.</p>\n<p>The differences between male and female pelvis are summarized in the table below.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 1199px; width: 481.453125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Characteristic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Male pelvis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Female pelvis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 83px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">General Morphology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">Massive,  rougher, marked muscle attachments</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">Less massive, slender, smoother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Overall shape</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Deep funnel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Flat bowl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\"><strong>Preauricular sulcus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\"><strong>Narrow, </strong>shallow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\"><strong>Broad, </strong>deep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\"><strong>Acetabulum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\"><strong>Large</strong>, wider, deeper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\"><strong>Small</strong>, narrower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Body of pubis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Narrow, triangular, thick</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Broad, square</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\"><strong>Obturator foramen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Large,</strong> oval with base upward</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Small</strong>, <strong>triangular</strong> with apex forwards</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\"><strong>Subpubic angle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\"><strong>V-shaped, narrow, (70<sup>o</sup>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\"><strong>U-shaped, rounded, wide, (90<sup>o</sup>)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\"><strong>Greater sciatic notch</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Smaller, narrower, deeper</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Larger, wider, shallower</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\"><strong>Ischial Tuberosity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\"><strong>Inverted</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\"><strong>Everted</strong>, more widely separated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Pelvic Brim</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Heart-Shaped</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Circular or elliptical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Pelvic outlet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Smaller</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacroiliac articulation</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large extends to 2<sup>1/2</sup> to 3 vertebrae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small, oblique, extends to 2 to 2<sup>1/2</sup> vertebrae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Sacroiliac joint surface</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Large</strong> and less sharply angulated</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>L-shaped</strong> and elevated anteriorly</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacrum</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Longer, narrower, with more evenly distributed curvature, promontory well marked. Body of first sacral vertebra larger.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Shorter, wider; upper<br/>half almost straight, curve forward in the lower half, promontory less marked. Body of first sacral vertebra small</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0912","text":"Identify the sex from the ‌pelvic bones.","raw_text":"Identify the sex from the pelvic bones.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-female, 2-male, 3-male, ‌4-female","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-male, 2-female, 3-female‌, 4-male","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-female, 2-male, 3-female‌, 4-male","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-male, 2-female, 3-male, ‌4-female","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-male, 2-female, 3-female, 4-male","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac362a915fa343e5bcdcd5f5e0bead3e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fea369d06e844a4b0eff10d6563b96ex1280x1943.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-male, 2-female, 3-female, 4-male</strong></p><p>The images marked as 1 and 4 show characteristics of a male pelvis and 2 and 3 show characteristics of a female pelvis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Differences between male and female pelvis:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"height: 1199px; width: 481.453125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Characteristic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Male pelvis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Female pelvis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 83px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">General Morphology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">Massive,  rougher, marked muscle attachments</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 83px;\">Less massive, slender, smoother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Overall shape</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Deep funnel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Flat bowl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Preauricular sulcus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Narrow, shallow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Broad, deep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 68px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Acetabulum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Large, wider, deeper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 68px;\">Small, narrower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Body of pubis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Narrow, triangular, thick</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Broad, square</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Obturator foramen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large, oval with base upward</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small, triangular with apex forwards</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\">Subpubic angle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\">V-shaped, narrow, (70<sup>o</sup>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 52px;\">U-shaped, rounded, wide, (90<sup>o</sup>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Greater sciatic notch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Smaller, narrower, deeper</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 71\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Larger, wider, shallower</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Ischial Tuberosity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Inverted</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Everted, more widely separated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Pelvic Brim</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Heart-Shaped</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Circular or elliptical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Pelvic outlet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Smaller</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacroiliac articulation</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large extends to 2<sup>1/2</sup> to 3 vertebrae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small, oblique, extends to 2 to 2<sup>1/2</sup> vertebrae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacroiliac joint surface</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Large and less sharply angulated</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>L-shaped and elevated anteriorly</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sacrum</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Longer, narrower, with more evenly distributed curvature, promontory well marked. Body of first sacral vertebra larger.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.453125px; text-align: center; height: 51px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 72\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Shorter, wider; upper<br/>half almost straight, curve forward in the lower half, promontory less marked. Body of first sacral vertebra small</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0913","text":"How would the structures m‌arked as 1 and 2 be descr​ibed in a female skull?","raw_text":"How would the structures marked as 1 and 2 be described in a female skull?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-small,‌ 2-small","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-large,‌ 2-large","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-large,‌ 2-small","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-small,‌ 2-large","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-large, 2-large","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92144a8623dc408db255cf20d7a3f7ed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/544063a044b1423781f7e1cbc059bccfx1280x1038.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccaef1d819ad4f8cab4a1a64349ae95ex592x264.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-large, 2-large</strong></p><p>The structures marked as 1 and 2 are the <strong>frontal</strong> and <strong>parietal</strong> <strong>eminences</strong>. In a <strong>female</strong> skull, both frontal and parietal eminences are <strong>larger</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Differentiating features between male and female skull:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 293px;\" width=\"298\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>Trait</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>Male</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>Female</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">General size</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Large</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Small</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Architecture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Rugged</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Smooth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Supraorbital ridges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Prominent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Less prominent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Mastoid process</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Prominent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Less prominent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Occipital region</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Marked muscle markings</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">No marked muscle markings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Frontal eminences</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Small</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Large</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Parietal eminences</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Small</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Large</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Orbits</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Square with round margins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Round with sharp margins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Mandible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Large, broad ramus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Small, small ramus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Chin shape</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">U-shaped</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">V-shaped</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The features given above develop after puberty and are modified by senility. Hence, the differences are applicable only between<strong> 18-55 years.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows a male and female skull.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The red arrow indicates frontal bossing, which is more apparent in the male skull.</li>\n<li>The green arrow indicates nasal radix which is deeper in the male skull.</li>\n<li>The yellow arrow indicates greater prominence of the chin in the male skull.</li>\n<li>The blue arrow shows a greater mandibular height in the male skull.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0852","text":"What is the Bertillon syst‌em used for?","raw_text":"What is the Bertillon system used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Criminal an‌thropometry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cause o‌f death","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Manner ‌of death","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Time sin‌ce death","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Criminal anthropometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Criminal anthropometry</strong></p><p>The <strong>Bertillon</strong> system is used for <strong>identification </strong>based on<strong> anthropometry</strong>.</p>\n<p>The origins of forensic anthropology can be traced to the end of the nineteenth century when the French criminologist Alphonse Bertillon devised the first classification and identification system to identify criminals, based on anthropometry.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0866","text":"You have measured the femu‌r length to be 40cm from ​the skeletal remains of a‌n unknown person. What is​ the approximate height o​f the individual?","raw_text":"You have measured the femur length to be 40cm from the skeletal remains of an unknown person. What is the approximate height of the individual?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150‌ cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"140‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"160‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"145‌ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 150 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 150 cm</strong></p><p>The approximate height of the individual is <strong>150 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The stature/femur relationship is <strong>3.74</strong>.<strong> </strong>Thus, stature can be calculated by using the formula, <strong>stature = femur length x 3.74</strong> = 40 x 3.74 = 149.6 cm ≅ 150 cm</p>\n<p>The relationship between the mean stature and the mean length of the femur appears to be fairly stable around the world. However, this factor tends to overestimate tall and underestimate short individuals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0867","text":"Which of the following can‌ be estimated using Pears​on's formula?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be estimated using Pearson's formula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S‌ex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ra‌ce","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stature</strong></p><p><strong>Pearson’s</strong> formula is used for the estimation of<strong> stature</strong>.</p>\n<p>Methods of <strong>determination</strong> of <strong>stature</strong>:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Pearson's</strong> formula: Any bone may be used, but the measurement of one or more of the six <strong>long bones</strong> provides the most reliable estimates. The descending order of usefulness of bones is femur&gt; tibia&gt; humerus&gt; radius.</p>\n<p>2. <strong>Trotter </strong>and<strong> Gleser's </strong>method - Formula for stature (cm) = 1.30 (Femur + Tibia) + 63.29 (+/-2.99)</p>\n<p>3. Stature/<strong>femur </strong>relationship - The relationship between the mean stature and the mean length of the femur appears to be fairly stable around the world which is 3.74.</p>\n<p>4. <strong>Anatomical </strong>method - This is the <strong>most accurate</strong> method. It consists of the sum of the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Basibregmatic height of the skull</li>\n<li>Height of the vertebrae from the axis through the first sacral vertebra</li>\n<li>Bicondylar length of the femur</li>\n<li>Physiological length of the tibia</li>\n<li>Articulated height of the talus and calcaneus</li>\n<li>Constant is added to account for the soft tissues</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Pearson also introduced the principle of regression. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5204","text":"Crown Rump Length is 21 cm‌, length of lower limb is​ 10 cm, gestational age o‌f the fetus will be:","raw_text":"Crown Rump Length is 21 cm, length of lower limb is 10 cm, gestational age of the fetus will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 to 7‌ months","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4 to 5‌ months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7 to 8‌ months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Te‌rm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6 to 7 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6 to 7 months</strong></p><p>Gestational age of this fetus is <strong>6-7 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Crown rump length + length of lower limb = crown heel length</p>\n<p><strong>Rule of Haase</strong> enables the estimation of the age of the fetus in lunar months from its <strong>crown-heel length (in cm). </strong></p>\n<p>According to the rule,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age in months = Square root of crown-heel length (when the crown-heel length is 25 cm or less/ first 5 months).</li>\n<li>Age in months = crown-heel length divided by 5 (when the crown-heel length is more than 25 cm/ last 5 months).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here, crown heel length = Crown rump + lower limb length = 31 cm.</p>\n<p>Hence, gestational age = 31 / 5 = 6-7 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0868","text":"What percent of the statur‌e of an individual is con​tributed by the length of‌ the vertebral column?","raw_text":"What percent of the stature of an individual is contributed by the length of the vertebral column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 35","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 35</strong></p><p>The length of the <strong>vertebral column</strong> is<strong> 35%</strong> of the <strong>stature</strong> of an individual </p>\n<table style=\"height: 83px;\" width=\"476\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Bone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">% of height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Humerus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Tibia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Femur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">27</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Spine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">35</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0909","text":"A forensic expert is exami‌ning the pelvic bone and ​lower vertebrae of an unk‌nown individual, to deter​mine the sex. Which of th​e following is regarded a‌s the best source of info​rmation for the same?","raw_text":"A forensic expert is examining the pelvic bone and lower vertebrae of an unknown individual, to determine the sex. Which of the following is regarded as the best source of information for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Il‌ium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pu‌bis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isc‌hium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sac‌rum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ilium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ilium</strong></p><p>The greater sciatic notch, which is a part of the ilium is the ideal feature to determine sex. </p>\n<p>The sacrum is the least reliable bone for sex determination.</p>\n<p>The <strong>femur</strong> is the most useful <strong>long bone</strong> for sex determination, due to its length and massiveness.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pearson and Bell's criteria for sexing of the femur are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Vertical diameter of the femoral head - most useful criterion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Popliteal length</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Bicondylar width</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Oblique trochanteric length</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TdCIqqhL","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"2_acid_base_disorders","title":"2.Acid Base Disorders","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6616","text":"Which of the following is ​the most effective extrac‌ellular buffer system at ‌physiological pH?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most effective extracellular buffer system at physiological pH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bicarbona​te buffer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphat​e buffer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protein​ buffer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ammonia​ buffer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bicarbonate buffer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bicarbonate buffer</strong></p><p>The most effective extracellular buffer system at physiological pH is the <strong>bicarbonate buffer</strong> system.</p>\n<p>A molecule with a pKa close to the physiological pH (<strong>7.4</strong>) is considered an effective buffer. Although the pKa of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is relatively low (<strong>6.1</strong>), it makes up 92% of the plasma buffering.</p>\n<p>This is often attributed to the enormous quantity of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> present in the body and the ability of this system to accept protons and form carbonic acid (H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>). This further in the presence of carbonic anhydrase is converted to readily excretable H₂O and CO<sub>2</sub>. It is therefore considered the most efficient extracellular buffering system in the body. </p>\n<p>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>+ H<sup>+</sup>↔ H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3 </sub>↔ H₂O + CO<sub>2</sub></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7037","text":"Which of the following is ​not done before drawing b‌lood for arterial blood g‌as analysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not done before drawing blood for arterial blood gas analysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allen'​s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rinse syringe with heparin​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexion ​of wrist","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Placing needle at 45 degre​e angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flexion of wrist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea86fa0647a9456bac259846ddc9dd57x1280x1474.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59fa46e281d74732bbefef2cf5b70c5dx1280x3192.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flexion of wrist</strong></p><p>Flexion<strong> </strong>of the wrist is not done just before drawing blood for ABG analysis.</p>\n<p>During <strong>arterial blood gas analysis</strong>, the ideal position for drawing arterial blood from the radial artery is to maintain the wrist in <strong>extension</strong>. This provides stability to the artery and prevents it from slipping away during the puncture. To draw blood for ABG analysis, the skin is pierced at a <strong>45° angle</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Allen's test</strong> is done prior to radial cannulation to identify the adequacy of radial and ulnar <strong>collateral</strong> blood flow to the hand.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The syringe used to draw blood for ABG analysis is <strong>rinsed with heparin</strong> prior to the procedure. Prior to collecting blood, 0.1 ml heparin is drawn into the syringe and then flushed out. The residue that lubricates the inner wall of the syringe is sufficient to prevent the blood from clotting.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6620","text":"Which of the following is ​not a cause of metabolic ‌alkalosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of metabolic alkalosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mineralocorticoid deficien​cy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiazide diu​retic therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent​ vomiting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mineralocorticoid deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mineralocorticoid deficiency</strong></p><p>Mineralocorticoid deficiency does not cause metabolic alkalosis. Its excess causes metabolic alkalosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of metabolic alkalosis: </strong></p>\n<p>Loss of hydrogen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrointestinal loss <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Removal of gastric secretions - <strong>vomiting</strong> or nasogastric suction</li>\n<li>Chloride-losing diarrhea</li>\n<li>Gastrocolic fistula</li>\n<li>Villous adenoma</li>\n<li>Antacid therapy, particularly if combined with cation exchange resin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Renal loss <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Loop or<strong> thiazide</strong> diuretics</li>\n<li>Mineralocorticoid excess (primary aldosteronism, Cushing's, steroids, licorice)</li>\n<li>Posthypercapnic states</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia, including milk-alkali syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>H<sup>+</sup> movement into cells\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Exogenous Alkali:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of NaHCO3, sodium citrate/gluconate/acetate/antacids</li>\n<li>Massive blood transfusion</li>\n<li>Antacids- Milk alkali syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contraction alkalosis (loss of ECF volume):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loop or thiazide-type diuretics</li>\n<li>Sweat losses in cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Gastric losses in achlorhydria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Miscellaneous:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bartter's syndrome</li>\n<li>Gitelman's syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5469","text":"Which metabolic abnormalit​y are patients with bulim‌ia nervosa least likely t‌o develop?","raw_text":"Which metabolic abnormality are patients with bulimia nervosa least likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoch​loremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic​ acidosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p>The metabolic abnormality patients with bulimia nervosa are<strong> least likely </strong>to develop is<strong> metabolic acidosis. Chronic vomiting, </strong>as seen in bulimia nervosa leads to <strong>m</strong><strong>etabolic alkalosis, </strong>not acidosis.</p>\n<p>Conditions associated with chronic vomiting lead to<strong> hypochloremia, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, </strong>and<strong> metabolic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6624","text":"Which of the following is ​false about metabolic alk‌alosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about metabolic alkalosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with hype​rkalemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with decr​eased ionic calcium conce‌ntration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be caused due to Conn'​s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can be caused due to a ren​in secreting tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is associated with hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is associated with hyperkalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Metabolic alkalosis</strong> is associated with <strong>potassium depletion</strong>, not hyperkalemia.</p>\n<p>While hypercalcemia can be a cause of metabolic alkalosis, alkalosis itself can cause a <strong>decrease in ionized calcium</strong>. The patients can present with tetany and show signs of hypocalcemia, i.e., Trousseau sign and Chvostek's sign.</p>\n<p><strong>Conn's syndrome</strong> is primary hyperaldosteronism that leads to sodium reabsorption in exchange for hydrogen in the collecting ducts. This causes hypernatremia, hypokalemia, and hydrogen loss in urine and may lead to metabolic alkalosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Renin-secreting tumors</strong> also lead to an increase in aldosterone levels leading to alkalosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of metabolic alkalosis: </strong></p>\n<p>Loss of hydrogen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrointestinal loss <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Removal of gastric secretions- vomiting or nasogastric suction</li>\n<li>Chloride-losing diarrhea</li>\n<li>Gastrocolic fistula</li>\n<li>Villous adenoma</li>\n<li>Antacid therapy, particularly if combined with cation exchange resin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Renal loss <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Loop or thiazide diuretics</li>\n<li>Mineralocorticoid excess (primary aldosteronism, Cushing's, steroids, licorice)</li>\n<li>Posthypercapnic states</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia, including the milk-alkali syndrome</li>\n<li> </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>H<sup>+</sup> movement into cells\n<ul>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Exogenous Alkali:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of NaHCO3, sodium citrate/gluconate/acetate/antacids</li>\n<li>Massive blood transfusion</li>\n<li>Antacids- Milk alkali syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contraction alkalosis (loss of ECF volume):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loop or thiazide-type diuretics</li>\n<li>Sweat losses in cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Gastric losses in achlorhydria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Miscellaneous:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bartter's syndrome</li>\n<li>Gitelman's syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6617","text":"Metabolic alkalosis is sus​pected in a patient prese‌nting with multiple episo‌des of vomiting. Which of​ the following laboratory‌ results would be seen in​ his arterial blood gas a‌nalysis? Options pH pCO2 ​(mmHg) HCO3¯ (mEq/L) 1 7.‌48 32 21 2 7.32 30 22 3 7​.28 50 32 4 7.55 48 30","raw_text":"<p>Metabolic alkalosis is suspected in a patient presenting with multiple episodes of vomiting. Which of the following laboratory results would be seen in his arterial blood gas analysis?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 200px;\" width=\"349\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 8.84659px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 8.84659px;\">Options</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 8.84659px;\"><strong>pH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 8.84659px;\">\n<p><strong>pCO2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(mmHg)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.5114px; height: 8.84659px;\">\n<p><strong>HCO3¯</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(mEq/L)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">7.48</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">32</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.5114px; height: 29px;\">21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">7.32</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.5114px; height: 29px;\">22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">7.28</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">50</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.5114px; height: 29px;\">32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">7.55</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.4972px; height: 29px;\">48</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.5114px; height: 29px;\">30</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti​on 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti​on 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti​on 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti​on 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Option 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Option 4</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> high pH</strong> (7.55),<strong> high HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong> (30 mEq/L), and <strong>high pCO<sub>2 </sub></strong>(48 mmHg) is suggestive of <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong> with respiratory acidosis, as a result of compensatory hypoventilation.</p>\n<p>Multiple episodes of vomiting result in loss of acids from the body and thus increases the pH.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 387px; width: 442px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20.0312px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53.6364px; height: 20.0312px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.8381px; height: 20.0312px;\"><strong>pH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.0199px; height: 20.0312px;\">\n<p><strong>pCO2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(mmHg)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4375px; height: 20.0312px;\">\n<p><strong>HCO3¯</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(mEq/L)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.273px; height: 20.0312px;\">\n<p><strong>Interpretation</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53.6364px; height: 29px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.8381px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>7.48</p>\n<p>(high)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.0199px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>32</p>\n<p>(low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4375px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>21</p>\n<p>(low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.273px; height: 29px;\">Respiratory alkalosis with compensatory metabolic acidosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53.6364px; height: 29px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.8381px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>7.32</p>\n<p>(low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.0199px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>30</p>\n<p>(low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4375px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>22</p>\n<p>(low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.273px; height: 29px;\">Metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53.6364px; height: 29px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.8381px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>7.28</p>\n<p>(low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.0199px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>50</p>\n<p>(high)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4375px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>32</p>\n<p>(high)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.273px; height: 29px;\">Respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53.6364px; height: 29px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.8381px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>7.55</p>\n<p>(high)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.0199px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>48</p>\n<p>(high)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4375px; height: 29px;\">\n<p>30</p>\n<p>(high)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.273px; height: 29px;\">Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6611","text":"The arterial blood gas ana​lysis of a patient reveal‌s a pH of 7.55, pCO2- 54 ‌mmHg, and HCO3- 40 mEq/L.​ What is the underlying d‌isorder?","raw_text":"The arterial blood gas analysis of a patient reveals a pH of 7.55, pCO2- 54 mmHg, and HCO3- 40 mEq/L. What is the underlying disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed acid-b​ase disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic alkalosis with c​ompensatory respiratory a‌cidosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ​compensatory metabolic al‌kalosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory alkalosis with​ compensatory metabolic a‌cidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis</strong></p><p>The underlying disorder in this patient is <strong>metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: A <strong>pH</strong> of <strong>7.55</strong> indicates <strong>alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Plasma <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong> is <strong>40</strong> mEq/L, which in the presence of a high pH indicates <strong>metabolic</strong> alkalosis.</p>\n<p>Step 3: pCO2 is 54 mmHg indicating <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>. The increase in pCO2 along with the increase in pH suggests that this is a compensatory response.</p>\n<p>Step 4: Assessing the compensatory response:</p>\n<p>On average, the<strong> pCO<sub>2</sub> rises 0.75 mmHg </strong>for every<strong> 1.0 mEq/L increment </strong>in the plasma<strong> HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong></p>\n<p>In the above scenario, the plasma HCO<sub>3</sub>- concentration is increased to <strong>40 mEq/L</strong> (The average normal value is considered as <strong>24 mEq/L</strong>)<br/> <br/>Thus, the HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> elevation is 40 – 24 = <strong>16 mEq/L</strong><br/><br/>Therefore, pCO<sub>2</sub> elevation should be 0.75 × 16 =  <strong>12 mmHg</strong></p>\n<p>Now, considering that the average value of normal pCO<sub>2</sub> is 40 mmHg, the estimated pCO<sub>2</sub> measured should be 40 + 12= <strong>52 mmHg</strong>. <br/> <br/>As pCO<sub>2</sub> is higher than the expected estimate, it is a case of metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6627","text":"The arterial blood gas ana​lysis of a 50-year-old di‌abetic patient revealed t‌he following values: pH- ​7.2 and HCO3- 15 mEq/L. W‌hich of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g renal compensation in t​his patient?","raw_text":"The arterial blood gas analysis of a 50-year-old diabetic patient revealed the following values: pH- 7.2 and HCO3- 15 mEq/L. Which of the following statements is false regarding renal compensation in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrogen ion secretion is ​increased","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bicarbonate reabsorption i​s decreased","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary acidity is increas​ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary ammonia is increas​ed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bicarbonate reabsorption is decreased","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bicarbonate reabsorption is decreased</strong></p><p>The patient has low pH and decreased levels of bicarbonate which is indicative of <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>. <strong>Bicarbonate</strong> reabsorption is<strong> increased</strong> in renal handling of metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>In response to metabolic acidosis, acidification of urine occurs by the following compensatory mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>proximal tubule</strong> <strong>reabsorbs</strong> all the filtered<strong> HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> </strong>(as any loss of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of H<sup>+</sup>).</li>\n<li>The <strong>collecting duct excretes</strong> the daily <strong>H<sup>+</sup> </strong>load (loss of H<sup>+</sup> is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>) primarily in the form of urinary <strong>ammonia</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7691","text":"Calculate the anion gap fr​om the following values: ‌Na+ = 137 mmol/L K+ = 4 m‌mol/L Cl- = 100 mmol/L HC​O3- = 15 mmol/L","raw_text":"Calculate the anion gap from the following values:\nNa+ = 137 mmol/L\nK+ = 4 mmol/L\nCl- = 100 mmol/L\nHCO3- = 15 mmol/L","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22 m​mol/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"16 m​mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 m​mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 m​mol/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 22 mmol/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 22 mmol/L</strong></p><p>The anion gap is the <strong>difference between measured cations and measured anions</strong> and is estimated as:</p>\n<p><strong>Anion gap = [Na+] – ([Cl-] + [HCO3-])</strong></p>\n<p>Substituting the given values, AG is 22 mmol/L. <strong>Normal AG</strong> is <strong>6 to 12 mmol/L</strong> with an average of 10 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>The concentrations of anions and cations in plasma must be equal to maintain electrical neutrality. Therefore, there is no real “anion gap” in the plasma.</p>\n<p>However, only certain cations and anions are routinely measured in the clinical laboratory. The <strong>cation normally measured </strong>is<strong> Na+</strong>, and the <strong>anions</strong> are usually <strong>Cl−</strong> and <strong>HCO3−</strong>. The value of plasma potassium is omitted from the calculation of the anion gap.</p>\n<p>Usually, the <strong>unmeasured anions exceed the unmeasured cations</strong>.</p>\n<p>The anion gap will increase if unmeasured anions rise or if unmeasured cations fall.</p>\n<p>The <strong>unmeasured anions</strong> include <strong>albumin</strong>,<strong> phosphate</strong>,<strong> sulfate</strong>, and other organic anions. The <strong>unmeasured cations</strong> include <strong>calcium</strong>,<strong> magnesium</strong>, and<strong> potassium</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6629","text":"In which of the following ​patients would you expect‌ to find an increased ani‌on gap?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you expect to find an increased anion gap?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal tubul​ar acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Addison'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diar​rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetic k​etoacidosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diabetic ketoacidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diabetic ketoacidosis</strong></p><p>The anion gap is <strong>increased</strong> in ketoacidosis. The ketone bodies act as unmeasured anions and increase the anion gap.</p>\n<p>The anion gap is calculated as:</p>\n<p>AG = (Na<sup>+</sup>) – [(Cl<sup>-</sup>) + (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>)]</p>\n<p>The normal anion gap is 6 - 12 mEq/L (optimally &lt;10 mEq/L).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6790","text":"High anion gap acidosis wi​th low bicarbonate levels‌ is seen in all the follo‌wing conditions except:","raw_text":"High anion gap acidosis with low bicarbonate levels is seen in all the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal tubul​ar acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic k​etoacidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactic ​acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salicylate​ poisoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal tubular acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal tubular acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Renal tubular acidosis</strong> causes <strong>normal anion gap</strong> metabolic acidosis, not high anion gap acidosis. </p>\n<p>Normally, in the serum, the total number of cations is equal to the total number of anions. But, a few of the <strong>anionic proteins (e.g., albumin), phosphates, sulfates, and organic anions</strong> are not measured and this is called an <strong>anion gap</strong>. It is the difference between measured cations and measured anions and is estimated as:</p>\n<p><strong>Plasma anion gap = [Na+] −([Cl-]+[HCO3-])</strong></p>\n<p>The normal anion gap ranges between <strong>6-12 mEq/L.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6630","text":"A 40-year-old man presente​d with nausea, dizziness,‌ and hyperventilation. Hi‌s arterial blood gas anal​ysis and serum electrolyt‌e levels revealed metabol​ic acidosis with a normal‌ anion gap. Which of the ​following is the possible‌ reason for this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with nausea, dizziness, and hyperventilation. His arterial blood gas analysis and serum electrolyte levels revealed metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap. Which of the following is the possible reason for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactic ​acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic re​nal failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal tubul​ar acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methanol​ toxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Renal tubular acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Renal tubular acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Metabolic acidosis</strong> with a <strong>normal anion gap</strong> is a feature of <strong>renal tubular acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renal tubular acidosis is a form of <strong>hyperchloraemic</strong> <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong> which occurs when the renal damage primarily affects tubular function without much effect on glomerular function. The bicarbonate loss leading to acidosis is mirrored by an increase in chloride concentration. Hence the unmeasured anions are unaffected and thus, the anion gap remains within normal limits.</p>\n<p>The result is a decrease in H<sup>+</sup> excretion which is greater than that can be explained by any change in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). However, if the GFR is significantly depressed eg., <strong>renal failure</strong>, the retention of fixed acids leads to a <strong>high anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6622","text":"Which of the following is ​not associated with type-‌A lactic acidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with type-A lactic acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pyelonephritis with ​hypotension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute MI with free wall ru​pture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyanide ​toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metformin​ toxicity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metformin toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metformin toxicity</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin toxicity</strong> is associated with <strong>type B</strong> lactic acidosis.</p>\n<p>Lactic acidosis is defined as an increase in plasma lactate levels. The various types of lactic acidosis are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 389px; height: 232px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px;\"><strong>Type A </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px;\"><strong>Type B</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px;\">Occurs secondary to circulatory insufficiency or tissue hypoxia.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px;\">Occurs in the absence of systemic hypoperfusion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px;\">\n<p>Associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shock</li>\n<li>Cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Severe anemia</li>\n<li>Carbon monoxide toxicity</li>\n<li>Cyanide toxicity</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px;\">\n<p>Associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignancies</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Hepatic failure</li>\n<li>Drugs like metformin, ethanol, ethylene glycol, and methanol</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>D-lactic acidosis</strong> is an unusual form of metabolic acidosis. It occurs due to the formation of D-lactate by <strong>gut bacteria</strong>. It occurs in patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short bowel syndrome</li>\n<li>Post-jejunoileal bypass</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7038","text":"A patient with chronic kid​ney disease came with com‌plaints of vomiting and d‌iarrhea. His blood gas re​ports show pH = 7.40, HCO‌3- = 23 mEq/L, Na+ = 145 ​mEq/L, Cl- = 100 mEq/L. W‌hat is your probable diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic kidney disease came with complaints of vomiting and diarrhea. His blood gas reports show pH = 7.40, HCO3- = 23 mEq/L, Na+ = 145 mEq/L, Cl- = 100 mEq/L. What is your probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No acid base​ abnormality","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respirator​y acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High anion gap metabolic a​cidosis with metabolic al‌kalosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High anion gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High anion gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p>The probable diagnosis is a high anion gap metabolic acidosis<strong> (HAGMA) </strong>with<strong> metabolic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: The pH being <strong>7.4</strong> is within normal limits.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is <strong>23 mEq/L</strong> and again is within normal limits.</p>\n<p>At this juncture, we have ruled out options B and C as this particular clinical scenario is not suggestive of a simple acid-base disorder.</p>\n<p>Step 3: Calculating the Anion gap:</p>\n<p>  Anion gap = [Na+] – ([Cl-] + [HCO3-])</p>\n<p>                   = 145-(100+23)</p>\n<p>                   = <strong>22 mEq/L</strong></p>\n<p>The presence of a high anion gap indicates that there's indeed an acid-base disorder, thus ruling out option A.</p>\n<p>A <strong>high anion gap </strong>indicates<strong> metabolic acidosis</strong>. However, HCO3- levels within normal limits suggest that there is also <strong>coexisting</strong> <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong> in this patient.</p>\n<p>This can be confirmed by calculating the<strong> delta gap</strong>.</p>\n<p>Delta gap = <strong>ΔAG - ΔHCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong></p>\n<p>In this scenario: Delta gap = (22-10) - (24-23) = <strong>11</strong> <strong>mEq/L</strong></p>\n<p>Delta gap is &gt; +6 suggesting the presence of metabolic alkalosis along with HAGMA.</p>\n<p>If the delta gap is significantly <strong>positive (&gt;+6)</strong>,<strong> metabolic alkalosis</strong> is present because the rise in the anion gap is more than the fall in bicarbonate. If the delta gap is significantly <strong>negative (&lt;6)</strong> then <strong>hyperchloremic acidosis</strong> is usually present because a rise in the anion gap is less than a fall in bicarbonate.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, this can be confirmed by calculating the <strong>delta ratio</strong>.</p>\n<p>Delta ratio = the<strong> increase in Anion Gap / the decrease in HCO3-</strong></p>\n<p>It is used in the presence of a high anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA), to check it is a ‘pure’ HAGMA or if there is coexistant normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAGMA) or metabolic alkalosis.</p>\n<p>Delta ratio values:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1-2 indicates HAGMA</li>\n<li>&lt; 1 indicates NAGMA + HAGMA</li>\n<li>&gt; 2 indicates HAGMA + Metabolic alkalosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In this scenario,</p>\n<p>Delta ratio = 12/1 = <strong>12</strong></p>\n<p>Thus, the acid-base disorder for the above scenario is <strong>HAGMA with metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6631","text":"A 5-year-old boy presents ​with generalized edema. U‌rine analysis showed prot‌ein 3+ and fat bodies. Wh​ich of the following woul‌d you expect to see in th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presents with generalized edema. Urine analysis showed protein 3+ and fat bodies. Which of the following would you expect to see in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in unmeasured ani​ons","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in measured anion​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal a​nion gap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High an​ion gap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decrease in unmeasured anions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decrease in unmeasured anions</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> nephrotic syndrome</strong>. This leads to a <strong>decrease in serum albumin</strong> which is an <strong>unmeasured anion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Albumin at physiological pH is the major contributor to the unmeasured anions of the body. Unmeasured anions consume bicarbonate in the body and reduce its levels. This leads to an increase in the anion gap. Therefore, when these <strong>unmeasured anions</strong> are<strong> reduced</strong> the <strong>anion gap</strong> also <strong>reduces</strong> due to decreased consumption of the bicarbonate.</p>\n<p>Nephrotic syndrome causes hypoalbuminemia and thus patients have a <strong>reduced anion gap</strong>. A fall in serum albumin by 1 g/dL from the normal value (4.5 g/dL) decreases the anion gap by 2.5 mEq/L.</p>\n<p>Decreased anion gap (&lt;6 mEq/L) is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increase in unmeasured cations</li>\n<li>An addition of abnormal cations to the blood, such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Lithium (lithium intoxication)</li>\n<li>Cationic immunoglobulins (plasma cell dyscrasias)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Reduction in the plasma anion albumin concentration (nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, or malabsorption)</li>\n<li>Bromide intoxication</li>\n<li>Hyperviscosity and severe hyperlipidemia, which can lead to an underestimation of sodium and chloride concentrations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6614","text":"A 54-year-old male patient​ with renal failure has m‌issed a round of dialysis‌. He complains of letharg​y, anorexia, and vomiting‌. What is the probable ac​id-base disorder that we ‌can expect in him?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old male patient with renal failure has missed a round of dialysis. He complains of lethargy, anorexia, and vomiting. What is the probable acid-base disorder that we can expect in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High anion gap metabolic a​cidosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic alkalosis with c​ompensatory respiratory a‌cidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal anion gap metabolic​ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mixed acid-b​ase disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High anion gap metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p>In <strong>renal failure</strong>, there is a loss of kidney function due to the damage affecting glomeruli and tubules. This leads to a <strong>high anion gap  metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6610","text":"ABG analysis of a patient ​is shown below. What is t‌he probable underlying ac‌id-base disorder in this ​patient? pH- 7.1 pCO 2 - ‌16 mmHg HCO 3- - 6 mEq/L ​pO2- 78 mmHg","raw_text":"<p>ABG analysis of a patient is shown below.  What is the probable underlying acid-base disorder in this patient?</p>\n<p>pH- 7.1</p>\n<p>pCO<sub>2</sub>- 16 mmHg</p>\n<p>HCO<sup>3- - </sup>6 mEq/L</p>\n<p>pO2- 78 mmHg</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic acidosis with co​mpensatory respiratory al‌kalosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic alkalosis with c​ompensatory respiratory a‌cidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ​compensatory metabolic al‌kalosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mixed acid–b​ase disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis</strong></p><p>The underlying acid-base disorder in this patient is <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong> with <strong>compensatory respiratory alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: A pH of<strong> 7.1</strong> signifies <strong>acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is <strong>6 mEq/L</strong>, along with a low pH it signifies <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Step 3: pCO<sub>2</sub> is <strong>16 mmHg </strong>indicating <strong>respiratory alkalosis</strong>. The decrease in pCO<sub>2</sub> along with the decrease in pH suggests that this is a compensatory response.</p>\n<p>Step 4: The expected compensatory decrease in pCO<sub>2</sub> can be calculated using <strong>Winter's formula </strong>(Expected PCO<sub>2</sub>= {[1.5 x HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>] +8} ± 2).<br/> <br/>Expected PCO<sub>2</sub> = {[1.5 x 6] +8} ± 2 = <strong>15 to 19 mmHg</strong></p>\n<p>A pCO<sub>2 </sub>level of 16 mmHg falls within the range given above. Hence, metabolic acidosis is associated with compensatory respiratory alkalosis.</p>\n<p>(pCO<sub>2</sub> values above or below 15 - 19 mmHg would indicate mixed acid-base disorders)<br/> <br/><strong>Alternatively:</strong></p>\n<p>The other method to calculate the expected compensation is a <strong>1.2 mmHg decrease</strong> in <strong>p<span class=\"smallcaps\" data-mmanualobjecttype=\"smallcaps\">CO</span><sub>2</sub></strong> for every <strong>1 mmol/L decrease</strong> in <strong>bicarbonate</strong>.</p>\n<p>The decrease in bicarbonate is 24 - 6 = 18 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Decrease in pCO<sub>2</sub> is 18 x 1.2 = 22</p>\n<p>Expected pCO<sub>2</sub> = 40 - 22 = 18 mmHg (roughly = 16 mmHg since the standard values are approximations)</p>\n<p>This method also indicates primary metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis. However, Winter's formula is more accurate.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6615","text":"A 30-year-old man presents​ to the emergency departm‌ent with dyspnea, and alt‌ered sensorium. These sym​ptoms were preceded by an‌ asthma attack. What is t​he probable reason for th‌e patient's presenting sy​mptoms?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents to the emergency department with dyspnea, and altered sensorium.  These symptoms were preceded by an asthma attack. What is the probable reason for the patient's presenting symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic​ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respirator​y acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Respirator​y alkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Respiratory acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Respiratory acidosis</strong></p><p>The probable reason for the patient's presenting symptoms is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Asthma attack leads to the narrowing of respiratory passages. This leads to reduced O<sub>2</sub> inhalation and reduced CO<sub>2</sub> exhalation contributing to the rise of pCO<sub>2</sub> in the blood, thereby causing respiratory acidosis.</p>\n<p>Causes of respiratory acidosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> Central: \n<ul>\n<li>Drugs (anesthetics, morphine, sedatives)</li>\n<li>Stroke</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li> Airway:\n<ul>\n<li>Obstruction</li>\n<li>Asthma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Lung parenchyma:\n<ul>\n<li>Emphysema</li>\n<li>Pneumoconiosis</li>\n<li>Bronchitis</li>\n<li>Adult respiratory distress syndrome</li>\n<li>Barotrauma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuromuscular:\n<ul>\n<li>Poliomyelitis</li>\n<li>Kyphoscoliosis</li>\n<li>Myasthenia</li>\n<li>Muscular dystrophies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Miscellaneous:\n<ul>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Hypoventilation</li>\n<li>Permissive hypercapnia.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6619","text":"A 60-year-old man with a h​istory of chronic obstruc‌tive pulmonary disease pr‌esents with increased sho​rtness of breath. His ABG‌ analysis is as shown. Wh​at is the most probable u‌nderlying acid-base disor​der? pH- 7.24 pCO2- 60 mm‌Hg pO2- 52 mmHg HCO3 - - ​27 mEq/L","raw_text":"<p>A 60-year-old man with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease presents with increased shortness of breath. His ABG analysis is as shown. What is the most probable underlying acid-base disorder?</p>\n<p>pH- 7.24</p>\n<p>pCO2- 60 mmHg</p>\n<p>pO2- 52 mmHg</p>\n<p>HCO3<sup>-</sup> - 27 mEq/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic​ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respirator​y acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Respirator​y alkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Respiratory acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Respiratory acidosis</strong></p><p>The underlying acid-base disorder is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: A pH of <strong>7.24</strong> signifies <strong>acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Step 2: A plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is <strong>27 mEq/L</strong> is within normal limits. This is very likely to be due to compensation as it does not reflect the direction of the pH change.</p>\n<p>Step 3: A pCO<sub>2</sub> of <strong>60 mmHg</strong> along with an acidotic pH signifies <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Step 4: The expected compensatory increase in HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>can be calculated using the following formula:</p>\n<p>Increase in HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>= 0.4 X Δ PaCO<sub>2</sub> (as COPD is a chronic condition and the presentation of the patient is not acute)</p>\n<p>Thus, the increase in HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>= 0.4 x (60-40) = 8 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Therefore expected HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is 24 + 8 = <strong>32 mEq/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>The actual HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> measured is 27 mEq/L (which is less than the expected value) indicating that there could be a component of metabolic acidosis also.</p>\n<p>But, the predominant disorder is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong> and thus it is the best answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6623","text":"A young asthmatic boy was ​rushed to ED with confusi‌on and gasping for breath‌. His ABG analysis is as ​shown. What is the acid-b‌ase disorder? pH- 7.25 pC​O2- 70 mmHg HCO3‾- 26 mEq‌/L pO2- 45 mmHg","raw_text":"<p>A young asthmatic boy was rushed to ED with confusion and gasping for breath. His ABG analysis is as shown. What is the acid-base disorder?</p>\n<p>pH- 7.25</p>\n<p>pCO2- 70 mmHg</p>\n<p>HCO3‾- 26 mEq/L</p>\n<p>pO2- 45 mmHg</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiratory alkalosis, com​pensated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory acidosis, part​ially compensated","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory acidosis, full​y compensated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic alkalosis, uncom​pensated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute severe asthma</strong>. The acid-base disorder is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>, partially compensated.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: pH is decreased. This signifies <strong>acidosis. </strong>In acidosis, either pCO<sub>2</sub> is increased or plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is decreased.  </p>\n<p>Step 2: In this case, pCO<sub>2</sub> is increased by 30 mmHg. This signifies <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Step 3: Plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is increased by 26-24 = 2 mEq/L. This signifies <strong>compensation</strong> is taking place.</p>\n<p>In acute conditions, for every 10mmHg rise in pCO<sub>2</sub> expected rise in plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is 1mEq/L. In this case, pCO<sub>2</sub> has risen by 30 mmHg, so the <strong>expected rise</strong> in HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is <strong>3 mEq/L.</strong></p>\n<p>However, plasma HCO<sub>3</sub>- has risen only by <strong>2 mEq/L</strong>, which implies compensation is only partial.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5286","text":"The arterial blood gas fin​dings of a patient are pH‌ = 7.12, pCO2 = 50, HCO3 ‌= 28. What is the diagnos​is?","raw_text":"The arterial blood gas findings of a patient are pH = 7.12, pCO2 = 50, HCO3 = 28. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ​metabolic compensation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic acidosis with re​spiratory compensation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory alkalosis with​ metabolic compensation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic alkalosis with r​espiratory compensation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation</strong></p><p>The above findings give the diagnosis of<strong> respiratory acidosis </strong>with<strong> metabolic compensation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: pH is reduced (7.12), hence <strong>acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Since pCO2 is raised (50 mmHg), it is <strong>respiratory</strong> acidosis.</p>\n<p>Step 3: HCO<sub>3</sub> is raised (28 mEq/L) suggesting there is <strong>metabolic compensation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6613","text":"A 40-year-old woman was ru​shed to the ER with chest‌ pain, palpitations, and ‌sweating. On examination,​ her respiratory rate was‌ 32/min, and carpopedal s​pasm was noted while meas‌uring BP. Her ECG was nor​mal. Which of the followi‌ng acid-base disorders is​ she likely to have?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman was rushed to the ER with chest pain, palpitations, and sweating. On examination, her respiratory rate was 32/min, and carpopedal spasm was noted while measuring BP. Her ECG was normal. Which of the following acid-base disorders is she likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic​ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respirator​y acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respirator​y alkalosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Respiratory alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Respiratory alkalosis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>panic attack</strong>. The patient is likely to have <strong>respiratory alkalosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Panic attacks are characterized by <strong>hyperventilation</strong> which leads to a <strong>washout of CO2</strong> (reduced PaCO<sub>2</sub>). This further increases the ratio of bicarbonate concentration to PaCO<sub data-reader-unique-id=\"6\">2</sub> and, thereby, increases the pH level causing <strong>alkalosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Alkalosis promotes the binding of calcium to albumin and <strong>reduces</strong> the fraction of <strong>ionized calcium</strong> in the blood. This leads to <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> <span class=\"s1\">without reducing the total calcium levels. Thus, alkalosis can precipitate <strong>tetany</strong> and <strong>laryngospasm</strong>, especially in calcium-deficient patients. </span></p>\n<p>Induction of carpopedal spasm when the BP cuff is inflated 20 mm Hg over the systolic BP for three minutes or more is called <strong>Trousseau sign</strong>. It is a clinical manifestation of <strong>hypocalcemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Causes of respiratory alkalosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Central nervous system stimulation:\n<ul>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Anxiety, psychosis</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Cerebrovascular accident</li>\n<li>meningitis, encephalitis</li>\n<li>Tumor</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hypoxemia or tissue hypoxia:\n<ul>\n<li>High altitude</li>\n<li>Pneumonia, pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Aspiration</li>\n<li>Severe anemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Drugs or hormones:\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Salicylates</li>\n<li>Cardiac failure</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Stimulation of chest receptors:\n<ul>\n<li>Hemothorax</li>\n<li>Flail chest</li>\n<li>Cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Pulmonary embolism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous:\n<ul>\n<li>Septicemia</li>\n<li>Hepatic failure</li>\n<li>Mechanical hyperventilation</li>\n<li>Heat exposure</li>\n<li>Recovery from metabolic acidosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: While metabolic alkalosis can also cause hypocalcemia, the above scenario of hyperventilation is more suggestive of respiratory alkalosis. Furthermore, hypocalcemic symptoms are more common with respiratory alkalosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6729","text":"In the given acid-base nom​ogram diagram, areas mark‌ed A and D indicate which‌ of the following conditi​ons?","raw_text":"In the given acid-base nomogram diagram, areas marked A and D indicate which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic respiratory acidos​is, acute metabolic alkal‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute Metabolic alkalosis,​ acute respiratory acidos‌is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute respiratory acidosis​, acute respiratory alkal‌osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute respiratory acidosis​, acute metabolic alkalos‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute respiratory acidosis, acute respiratory alkalosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1642dd51e16b485aa9ff24c7a6d6ca59.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5b3a554c29d42aab8be74cc471cb156x1280x1555.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute respiratory acidosis, acute respiratory alkalosis</strong></p><p>In the given illustrated acid-base nomogram, the letters <strong>A</strong> and<strong> D</strong> indicate<strong> acute respiratory acidosis</strong> and <strong>acute respiratory alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>An acid-base nomogram, also known as a <strong>Davenport diagram,</strong> is a graphical representation of acid-base balance to interpret arterial blood gas (ABG) results. It helps determine the primary acid-base disorder and identify compensatory mechanisms.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>y-axis</strong> of the acid-base nomogram represents the <strong>arterial bicarbonate (HCO3-) levels,</strong> and the <strong>x-axis</strong> represents the<strong> arterial pH</strong> values. The <strong>shaded</strong> areas are the <strong>range</strong> of values of the <strong>normal respiratory and metabolic compensations</strong> for primary acid-base disturbances.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0888","text":"The arterial blood gas ana​lysis findings of a patie‌nt are as follows: pH= 7.‌30, HCO3= 10 mEq/L, paCO2​= 30 mmHg, which is sugge‌stive of a partially comp​ensated metabolic acidosi‌s. What is the mechanism ​for the secondary disorde‌r?","raw_text":"The arterial blood gas analysis findings of a patient are as follows:  pH= 7.30, HCO3= 10 mEq/L,  paCO2= 30 mmHg, which is suggestive of a partially compensated metabolic acidosis. What is the mechanism for the secondary disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoventilation causing de​creased CO2 washout","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperventilation causing i​ncreased CO2 washout","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased tubular reabsorp​tion of HCO3-","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ex​cretion of H+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypoventilation causing decreased CO2 washout","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypoventilation causing decreased CO2 washout</strong></p><p>The mechanism for the secondary disorder is <strong>hypoventilation</strong> causing <strong>decreased CO2 washout</strong>. </p>\n<p>The given arterial blood gas findings are suggestive of a <strong>partially compensated metabolic acidosis</strong>. The compensation is <strong>partial </strong>because the <strong>fall in paCO2</strong> much <strong>lower</strong> than what is expected for the given HCO3 levels. The <strong>fall in paCO2</strong> is <strong>inadequate </strong>due to<strong> hypoventilation</strong> causing decreased CO2 washout. In this case, the <strong>secondary disorder</strong> is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Hyperventilation</strong> causing<strong> increased CO2 washout</strong> (Option B) would allow for <strong>better compensation</strong>, with lower paCO2 levels than observed. </p>\n<p>The expected paCO2 can be calculated using <strong>Winter's formula:</strong></p>\n<p>For metabolic acidosis, the expected paCO2 = [1 .5 X HCO3-] + 8 ± 2.</p>\n<p>Substituting the values, expected PaCO2 = (1.5 x 10) +8 ± 2<br/>= 15+8 ±2<br/>= <strong>21 to 25 mmHg</strong></p>\n<p>However, the <strong>actual PaCO2 is 30 mmHg,</strong> which is <strong>higher</strong> than the expected paCO2 <strong>indicating a secondary respiratory acidosis.</strong> A possible explanation for this could only be <strong>hypoventilation causing decreased CO2 washout. </strong></p>\n<p>Thus, the given ABG findings are suggestive of a mixed acid-base disorder characterized by metabolic acidosis with additional respiratory acidosis. </p>\n<p><strong>For example, </strong>in a patient with diabetic ketoacidosis and pneumonia, the lungs would fail to compensate, and the patient could develop hypoventilation leading to secondary respiratory acidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6612","text":"The arterial blood gas ana​lysis of an unconscious m‌an showed pH- 6.9, HCO3- ‌13 mEq/L, paCO2- 68 mmHg.​ The acid-base status of ‌this patient is best desc​ribed as:","raw_text":"The arterial blood gas analysis of an unconscious man showed pH- 6.9, HCO3- 13 mEq/L, paCO2- 68 mmHg. The acid-base status of this patient is best described as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uncompensated metabolic ac​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uncompensated respiratory ​acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simultaneous respiratory a​nd metabolic acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ​partial renal compensatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Simultaneous respiratory and metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Simultaneous respiratory and metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p>According to the ABG analysis provided, the patient has a <strong>mixed</strong> acid-base disorder with <strong>simultaneous respiratory </strong>and <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the ABG:</p>\n<p>Step 1: A pH of 6.9 signifies <strong>acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is 13 mEq/L, along with a low pH it signifies <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Step 3: pCO<sub>2</sub> is 68 mmHg indicating co-existing <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong>. The direction of pCO2 change is the same as that of pH change suggesting that this is a mixed acid-base disorder.</p>\n<p>Step 4: The expected compensatory change in pCO<sub>2</sub> in the metabolic acidosis calculated using Winter's formula would be:</p>\n<p>pCO<sub>2</sub> = [1.5xHCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>] + 8 ± 2 = 26 - 30 mmHg</p>\n<p>The <strong>actual pCO<sub>2</sub></strong> measure is very<strong> high</strong> (68 mmHg) compared to the estimated range of 26 - 30 mmHg indicating the presence of <strong>simultaneous</strong> metabolic and respiratory acidosis.</p>\n<p>Note- If this was a case of uncompensated acidosis, either the bicarbonate or the pCO<sub>2</sub> values would have been within the normal limits. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"57T4b4tj","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"2_components_of_immune_system","title":"2.Components Of Immune System","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MD0926","text":"What type of immunity is b‌eing conferred to a neona​te receiving the BCG vacc​ine?","raw_text":"What type of immunity is being conferred to a neonate receiving the BCG vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Natural act‌ive immunity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Natural pass‌ive immunity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artificial active immunity‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Artificial passive immunit‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Artificial active immunity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Artificial active immunity</strong></p><p>The immunity conferred to a neonate receiving the BCG vaccine is<strong> artificial active immunity</strong>, as it involves administering a live attenuated vaccine form of<strong> </strong><em>Mycobacterium bovis</em><strong> </strong>to stimulate an adaptive immune response and develop antibodies, establishing long-term immunity. </p>\n<p>Types of adaptive immunity: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Natural active immunity</strong>: It results from either a clinical or an inapparent infection by a microbe.</li>\n<li><strong>Artificial active immunity</strong>: It is the resistance induced by vaccines which are preparations of killed or attenuated microorganisms or their products.</li>\n<li><strong>Natural passive immunity</strong>: It is the immunity passively transferred from mother to baby. In human infants, maternal antibodies are transmitted predominantly through the placenta, while in animals such as pigs, the transfer of antibodies occurs mainly through the colostrum.</li>\n<li><strong>Artificial passive immunity</strong>: It is the resistance passively transferred to a recipient by the administration of antibodies. Hyperimmune sera of animal or human origin, convalescent sera, and pooled human immunoglobulin are used for this purpose.</li>\n<li><strong>Combined immunization</strong>: A combination of the active and passive methods of immunization is used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples of the above types of immunity: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 342px; width: 492px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Immunity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: left;\">Natural active</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\">Clinical or subclinical <strong>infection</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: left;\">Artificial active</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\"><strong>Vaccination</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: left;\">Natural passive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\">Transplacental<strong> </strong>transfer of antibody, <strong>colostrum</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: left;\">Artificial passive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\"><strong>Anti-tetanus serum</strong>, anti-diphtherial serum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: left;\">Combined immunization</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\">Antisera and vaccine are given at the <strong>same time, </strong>e.g- post-exposure prophylaxis for rabies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: <strong>Adoptive immunity</strong> is immunity in which immunologically competent lymphocytes are injected. The transfer factor is composed of these immunologically competent lymphocytes. It can passively transfer cell-mediated immunity against those antigens to which the donor is sensitive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6302","text":"A 33-year-old with a large‌ dirty wound on his knee ​and abrasions on his palm​s presents to the casualt‍y. He does not remember t​he last time he received ​a TT injection and was th‍us given tetanus immunogl​obulin. Which of the foll‍owing is false about this‌ type of immunity? TT- Te​tanus Toxoid","raw_text":"<p>A 33-year-old with a large dirty wound on his knee and abrasions on his palms presents to the casualty. He does not remember the last time he received a TT injection and was thus given tetanus immunoglobulin. Which of the following is false about this type of immunity?</p>\n<p>TT- Tetanus Toxoid</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acquired i‌mmediately","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Memory i‌s absent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long l‌asting","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No negat‌ive phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Long lasting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f468a9a26c494a9a81f754306e5a6defx1280x1564.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Long lasting</strong></p><p>Giving <strong>pre-formed immunoglobulin </strong>(e.g. Tetanus immunoglobulin) directly is an example of <strong>passive immunity,</strong> which is <strong>not long-lasting</strong>. </p>\n<p>The differentiating features between active and passive immunity:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 358px; height: 859.766px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Active Immunity</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Passive Immunity</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Actively produced by the host’s immune system</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Passively received by the host</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Induced by infection or immunogens</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Readymade antibody is transferred</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 59.7969px;\">\n<p>Immunological memory present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 59.7969px;\">\n<p>No memory </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 99.1953px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 99.1953px;\">\n<p>Immunity is effective after a lag period or latent period</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 99.1953px;\">\n<p>Immediate immunity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 93\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Broad specificity</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<p>Narrow specificity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>A negative phase may occur </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>No negative phase. </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Booster effect on subsequent dose</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>The subsequent dose is less effective</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Secondary type response seen </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>No secondary type of response</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<p>More effective</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<p>Less effective </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Long-lasting (Years)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 76.7969px;\">\n<p>Short-lasting (Days to weeks)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.516px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<p>Example: Vaccines</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.891px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<p>Example: Antisera </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Negative phase:</strong> It<strong> </strong>is the time period during which the level of <strong>measurable immunity</strong> is <strong>lower</strong> than it was before the antigenic stimulus. This is because the antigen combines with any pre-existing antibody and lowers its level in circulation. It is seen at the beginning of the active immune response.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary type response:</strong> If an individual who has been actively immunized against an antigen experiences the same antigen subsequently, the immune response occurs more quickly and abundantly than during the first encounter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2794","text":"A patient with chronic muc‌ocutaneous candidiasis wa​s administered transfer f​actor as treatment. What ‍type of immunity does thi​s result in?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis was administered transfer factor as treatment. What type of immunity does this result in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Active ‌immunity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Passive ‌immunity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Combined‌ immunity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adoptive‌ immunity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adoptive immunity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adoptive immunity</strong></p><p><strong>Adoptive immunity</strong> occurs when <strong>transfer</strong> <strong>factor</strong> is given.</p>\n<p>Transfer factor is composed of an extract of <strong>immunologically competent lymphocytes</strong> such as<strong> </strong>T lymphocytes, NK cells, macrophages, or B cells. It has the ability to <strong>passively</strong> transfer <strong>cell-mediated immunity</strong> against antigens to which the donor is sensitive.</p>\n<p>It has been attempted in the treatment of lepromatous leprosy and chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6288","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a pattern recognition​ receptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a pattern recognition receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NOD-like ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toll-like‌ receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RIG-like ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BMP-like ‌receptors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. BMP-like receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. BMP-like receptors</strong></p><p><strong>Bone morphogenetic protein</strong>-<strong>like receptors</strong> are <strong>not</strong> pattern-recognition receptors. </p>\n<p><strong>Pattern recognition receptors</strong> (PRRs) are essential for the<strong> innate immune system. </strong>They identify pathogen-associated molecular patterns (<strong>PAMPs</strong>) common to classes of microbes and trigger immune responses. These receptors can be found on the cell surface, detecting extracellular pathogens, or within the endosomes and cellular matrix, detecting intracellular invading viruses. </p>\n<p>PRRs activate<strong> pro-inflammatory pathways</strong>, promote phagocytosis in cells like macrophages, neutrophils and dendritic cells, and may act as secreted proteins binding directly to pathogens. Based on the localization and functional characteristics, PRRs are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Membrane-bound PRRs:<strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toll-like receptors </strong>(TLR)<strong>-</strong> detect microbial carbohydrates and lipoproteins (Option B)</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>C-type lectin receptors (CLR)- detect mannose residues</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cytoplasmic (intracellular) PRRs:\n<ul>\n<li>Nucleotide oligomerization <strong>(NOD</strong>)<strong>-like receptors</strong> (NLR) - NOD1 and NOD2 - Detects bacterial cell wall peptidoglycans (Option A)</li>\n<li><strong>RIG-I-like receptors</strong> (RLRs) - detect viral RNA (Option C)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Secreted PRRs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the contrary, BMP receptors are multifunctional transmembrane serine-threonine kinase receptors that belong to the transforming growth factor β (TGF-β) superfamily, regulating <strong>cell growth, differentiation, </strong>and<strong> apoptosis </strong>with no role in pattern recognition. Also, there are no receptors termed \"BMP-like receptors\". </p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> A mutation in the Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2 (<strong>BMPR2</strong>) gene enhances the proliferation and reduces the apoptosis of pulmonary vascular endothelial and smooth muscle cells, leading to <strong>hereditary pulmonary arterial hypertension </strong>(PAH). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6348","text":"A 23-year-old presents wit‌h diarrhea, fever, and vo​miting. The given bacteri​a is implicated. Which to‍ll-like receptor binds to​ the structure of the bac​teria marked A?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old presents with diarrhea, fever, and vomiting. The given bacteria is implicated. Which toll-like receptor binds to the structure of the bacteria marked A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TL‌R 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TL‌R 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TL‌R 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TL‌R 9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. TLR 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf1f860f3d7549688ffe115b557d7f7f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. TLR 5</strong></p><p>The given image and clinical history suggest a <strong>typhoid</strong> infection. The structure marked A is the <strong>flagella</strong> of bacteria. <strong>Toll-like receptor</strong> (TLR) <strong>5</strong> binds to the <strong>flagella</strong> of bacteria.</p>\n<p>Toll-like receptors (TLRs) are <strong>transmembrane receptors</strong> that have an <strong>extracellular</strong> domain, that detect infecting pathogens.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TLR 1 - Mycobacteria and Gram-negative bacteria  </li>\n<li>TLR 2 - Mycobacteria and Gram-positive bacteria</li>\n<li>TLR 3 - Viral RNA </li>\n<li>TLR 4 - Gram-negative bacteria</li>\n<li>TLR 5 - Flagella</li>\n<li>TLR 9 - Bacterial DNA, some herpes viruses, malarial parasite </li>\n</ul>\n<p>TLRs act through the NF-KB pathway and initiate inflammation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6350","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about T c​ell dependent antigens?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about T cell dependent antigens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Their response doesn't sho‌w immunological memory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They activate only mature ‌B cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They activate B cells poly‌clonally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They activate only IgG ant‌ibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They activate only mature B cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They activate only mature B cells</strong></p><p><strong>T cell-dependent antigens activate</strong> only <strong>mature</strong> <strong>B cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>Depending on their ability to <strong>induce</strong> the <strong>formation</strong> of <strong>antibodies,</strong> antigens are classified as T-cell dependent and T-cell independent antigens. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 344px; width: 535px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"><strong>T cell-dependent antigens (TD)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"><strong>T cell-independent antigens (TI)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 57px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 57px;\">Require helper T cell participation for the production of immune response</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 57px;\">Directly stimulate antibody production by B cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 24px;\">Structurally complex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 24px;\">Structurally simple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 45px;\">Activate only mature B cells monoclonally</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 45px;\">Activate both mature and immature B cells polyclonally</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 22px;\">B cells undergo affinity maturation and class switch over</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 22px;\">B cells do not undergo affinity maturation and class switch over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 5px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 5px;\">\n<p>All classes of antibodies are activated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 5px;\">Antibody response is restricted to IgM and IgG3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 17px;\">Rapidly metabolized</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 17px;\">Slowly metabolized</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 20px;\">Immunological memory present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 20px;\">No immunological memory</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6352","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen‌ts with a fever of 103°F,​ hypotension, confusion, ​and a diffuse rash as sho‍wn below. 3 days back, sh​e had uncontrollable epis​taxis for which a nasal p‍ack was placed. What is t​he likely mechanism of ac‍tion of the microbial tox‌in that has caused her cu​rrent illness?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman presents with a fever of 103°F, hypotension, confusion, and a diffuse rash as shown below. 3 days back, she had uncontrollable epistaxis for which a nasal pack was placed. What is the likely mechanism of action of the microbial toxin that has caused her current illness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enhancement of phagocytosi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased antigen presenta‌tion by macrophages","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyclonal activation of T‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uncontrolled activation of‌ complement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Polyclonal activation of T cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f4b102a11c94793aff80d581eac214d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9f9aca6adbc483daa63cbad5b7ad2fax1280x1233.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Polyclonal activation of T cells</strong></p><p>This woman presented with fever, hypotension, and a diffuse erythematous rash, three days after a nasal packing was done. This is characteristic of <strong>toxic shock syndrome</strong> - commonly caused by <strong>staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1)</strong>, which is a <strong>superantigen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Superantigens act by causing <strong>polyclonal activation of T cells.</strong> They activate a large number of T cells irrespective of their antigen specificity.</p>\n<p>In contrast to regular antigens which bind to the binding groove on T cells, superantigens <strong>bind outside</strong> the groove <strong>directly</strong> to the lateral<strong> </strong>aspect of the <strong>TCR β chain</strong>. This leads to a <strong>massive</strong> release of cytokines and polyclonal activation of T cells.</p>\n<p>Clinically, this manifests as fever, hypotension, and skin exfoliation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Examples of organisms that produce superantigens:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Bacterial</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>- Enterotoxins</li>\n<li>Group A streptococci - Pyrogenic exotoxin</li>\n<li><em>Mycoplasma arthritidis</em>- mitogen 1</li>\n<li><em>Yersinia enterocolitica</em></li>\n<li><em>Yersinia pseudotuberculosis</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Viral</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Epstein–Barr virus (EBV)</li>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus (CMV)</li>\n<li>Rabies nucleocapsid</li>\n<li>HIV-encoded negative regulatory factor.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Fungal-</strong> <em>Malassezia furfur</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2857","text":"What are the changes in th‌e variable regions of an ​immunoglobulin known as?","raw_text":"What are the changes in the variable regions of an immunoglobulin known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Idio‌type","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Iso‌type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allo‌type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epi‌tope","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Idiotype","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Idiotype</strong></p><p>Changes in the<strong> variable regions </strong>of heavy and light chains of an immunoglobulin are called an<strong> idiotype. </strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Isotypes </strong>are the changes in the <strong>constant regions</strong> of the heavy chain of the immunoglobulin. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Allotypes </strong>refer to <strong>multiple alleles</strong> that exist for some genes. This leads to subtle amino acid differences that occur in <strong>some but not all members</strong> of the <strong>same species.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option D: An<strong> epitope </strong>is the <strong>smallest unit of antigenicity</strong> and it is an antigenic determinant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6392","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about immunoglobu​lins is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about immunoglobulins is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG is used to diagnose fe‌tal infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IgM has weak precipitation‌ activity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgG has fou‌r subclasses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgM is mainly intravascula‌r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IgG is used to diagnose fetal infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IgG is used to diagnose fetal infection</strong></p><p><strong>IgG cannot detect fetal infections </strong>because it is not produced by the fetus in significant amounts. </p>\n<p>IgG is the only maternal immunoglobulin that is normally transported across the placenta and provides <strong>natural passive immunity</strong> to the newborn.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6398","text":"Which of the following imm‌unoglobulins acts as surf​ace immunoglobulin on B c​ells?","raw_text":"Which of the following immunoglobulins acts as surface immunoglobulin on B cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌ G","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌ A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌ M","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌ E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ig M","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ig M</strong></p><p><strong>Monomeric IgM</strong> is the major antibody receptor on the surface of B lymphocytes for antigen recognition.</p>\n<p><strong>IgD</strong> is the other class of antibodies which acts as the B cell receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7565","text":"A 3-year-old child present‌s with a history of repea​ted sinopulmonary infecti​ons caused by encapsulate‍d organisms. Which of the​ following is most likely​ to be deficient in this ‍case?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child presents with a history of repeated sinopulmonary infections caused by encapsulated organisms. Which of the following is most likely to be deficient in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌G1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌G2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌G3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌G4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgG2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgG2</strong></p><p><strong>Deficiency</strong> of<strong> IgG2</strong> results in <strong>recurrent sinopulmonary</strong> infections with encapsulated organisms.</p>\n<p>IgG is the only maternal immunoglobulin that is normally transported across the placenta and provides natural passive immunity to the newborn. <strong>IgG1</strong> is the antibody maximally transported across the placenta<strong>,</strong> whereas <strong>IgG2</strong> has the <strong>least transplacental</strong> movement. </p>\n<p>Deficiencies of other IgG: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>IgG1- bacterial infections (diphtheria, tetanus)(Option A)</li>\n<li>IgG3- viral infections (Option C)</li>\n<li>IgG4- parasitic infections (Option D)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0623","text":"Identify the class of immu‌noglobulin​ shown in the ​image below.","raw_text":"Identify the class of immunoglobulin​ shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌ A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌ G","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌ M","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌ E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ig A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e981e14c7dbc4845b2ca7646872fc9fa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/286886ccd1b44f4197c7951c3dfc3e2ax1280x2093.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ig A</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>dimeric</strong> <strong>structure, </strong>characteristic of <strong>secretory</strong> <strong>IgA.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IgA</strong> immunoglobulin is the major antibody responsible for <strong>mucosal</strong> immunity. Its serum levels are usually low. It is predominantly present in the <strong>extravascular secretions.</strong> It is present in <strong>secretions</strong> such as milk, saliva, and tears, and in secretions of the respiratory, intestinal, and genital tracts.</p>\n<p>When present in the<strong> serum</strong>, it exists as a <strong>monomer</strong> similar to IgG. However, when it is <strong>secreted,</strong> it exists as a <strong>dimer</strong>. The dimeric structure consists of two monomers, which are stabilized by two additional polypeptides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>J chain, which stabilizes the molecule.</li>\n<li><strong>Secretory component</strong>, which is incorporated into the IgA when transported through the epithelial cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6399","text":"Which immunoglobulin is ma‌jorly responsible for the​ reaction demonstrated be​low?","raw_text":"Which immunoglobulin is majorly responsible for the reaction demonstrated below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌ G","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌ A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌ M","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌ E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ig M","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ea1381e345d942bea18cf82d0303c75c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ig M</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>blood typing,</strong> which works on the principle of <strong>agglutination.</strong> <strong>IgM </strong>is mainly responsible for agglutination. </p>\n<p>It is the most efficient immunoglobulin in agglutination, complement fixation, and other antigen–antibody reactions and is important also in defense against bacteria and viruses. </p>\n<p>It is a <strong>pentamer,</strong> and hence is <strong>more effective</strong> than IgG in opsonization (C3b mediated), bactericidal action and bacterial agglutination but less effective in neutralization of toxins and viruses. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 181px;\" width=\"211\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.2955px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Reaction</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.0227px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>IgG</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.8409px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>IgM</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2955px;\">\n<p>Precipitation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.0227px;\">\n<p>Strong</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.8409px;\">\n<p>Weak</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2955px;\">\n<p>Agglutination</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.0227px;\">\n<p>Weak</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.8409px;\">\n<p>Strong</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2955px;\">\n<p>Complement fixation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.0227px;\">\n<p>Moderate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.8409px;\">\n<p>Strong</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2955px;\">\n<p>Lysis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.0227px;\">\n<p>Weak</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47.8409px;\">\n<p>Strong</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2811","text":"You are counseling a new m‌other about the importanc​e of breastfeeding. What ​forms the secretory compo‍nent of the antibody that​ is maximally present in ​breast milk?","raw_text":"You are counseling a new mother about the importance of breastfeeding. What forms the secretory component of the antibody that is maximally present in breast milk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma c‌ell only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epithelial cells of lining‌ mucosa only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma cell and oligosacch‌arides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligosaccharides and epith‌elial cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epithelial cells of lining mucosa only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5be174dabbe84aaeb69db589f78c17e5x1280x2093.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epithelial cells of lining mucosa only</strong></p><p>The antibody <strong>maximally</strong> present in <strong>breast milk</strong> is <strong>IgA.</strong> The <strong>secretory component</strong> of IgA is formed by <strong>epithelial cells</strong> of<strong> lining mucosa.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IgA</strong> occurs in two forms- <strong>serum</strong> IgA (monomer) and <strong>secretory</strong> IgA.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Secretory</strong> IgA is a <strong>dimer</strong> formed by two monomer units joined together by a <strong>J-chain </strong>and a <strong>secretory component</strong> (secretory piece).<strong> </strong>Secretory IgA along with the J chain is synthesized by the plasma cells situated near the mucosal or glandular epithelium. The secretory component is synthesized by <strong>epithelial cells,</strong> not by plasma cells. </p>\n<p>It is believed to protect IgA from denaturation by bacterial proteases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0025","text":"When papain acts on a mole‌cule of immunoglobulin, w​hich of the following are​ the end products?","raw_text":"When papain acts on a molecule of immunoglobulin, which of the following are the end products?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 Fc and 1 F‌ab fragments","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 variable chain and 1 con‌stant chain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 Fc and 2 F‌ab fragments","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 Fab and 1 hypervariable ‌region","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 Fc and 2 Fab fragments","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88b443aa9eb9417dbba0cfc2e4666777x1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b62f5e0cfb4407b891e14bfd71c7e44x1280x1049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 Fc and 2 Fab fragments</strong></p><p>When <strong>papain</strong> acts on a molecule of <strong>immunoglobulin,</strong> the end products are <strong>1 Fc</strong> and <strong>2 Fab</strong> fragments. </p>\n<p><strong>Enzymatic digestion</strong> of antibody:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Papain- </strong>2 Fab fragments and one Fc fragment</li>\n<li><strong>Pepsin-</strong> One F(ab’)<sub>2</sub> fragment and many smaller fragments</li>\n<li><strong>Mercaptoethanol-</strong> 2 H and 2 L chains</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6632","text":"Affinity maturation is due‌ to:","raw_text":"Affinity maturation is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isotype ‌switching","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somatic hy‌permutation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"mRNA s‌plicing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VDJ rear‌rangement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Somatic hypermutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Somatic hypermutation</strong></p><p>Affinity maturation is due to <strong>s</strong><strong>omatic hypermutation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Affinity maturation</strong> is the process by which <strong>antibodies gain increased</strong> <strong>affinity</strong>, <strong>avidity,</strong> and<strong> anti-pathogen activity. </strong>It is a result of somatic hypermutation (SHM) of immunoglobulin genes in B cells, coupled to selection for antigen binding.</p>\n<p>This process occurs in the <strong>germinal centers</strong> and proceeds for weeks after acute infection or vaccination, or for many cycles during chronic infection. The resulting antibodies can be <strong>highly mutated</strong> from their germline-encoded counterparts, with increases of several orders of magnitude in affinity for antigen compared to the corresponding naïve B cell receptors (BCRs).</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Isotype switching</strong>, also known as class switch recombination, is a process where the constant region of the antibody heavy chain is changed, leading to a switch in the antibody isotype (e.g., from IgM to IgG). This process is crucial for the functional properties of antibodies but is not directly involved in affinity maturation.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>mRNA splicing</strong> is a cellular process that involves the removal of introns and the joining of exons to generate mature mRNA. While this process is essential for the production of functional antibodies, it is not specifically associated with affinity maturation. Affinity maturation primarily occurs through somatic hypermutation.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>VDJ rearrangement</strong> is the process by which the variable regions of immunoglobulin genes are assembled from gene segments (V, D, and J segments). This process contributes to the diversity of antibodies but is distinct from affinity maturation. Affinity maturation occurs after VDJ rearrangement and is driven by somatic hypermutation in the variable regions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6400","text":"An immunologist is conduct‌ing an experiment to unde​rstand precipitation reac​tions. He notes that on a‍dding excessive amount of​ antigen to a serum sampl​e containing small amount‍ of antibodies, the amoun​t of precipitate formed i‍s less, compared to addin‌g smaller amount of antig​en to the same sample. Wh‍at is this phenomenon cal​led?","raw_text":"An immunologist is conducting an experiment to understand precipitation reactions. He notes that on adding excessive amount of antigen to a serum sample containing small amount of antibodies, the amount of precipitate formed is less, compared to adding smaller amount of antigen to the same sample. What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pro–zone ‌phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zone of e‌quivalence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post–zone ‌phenomenon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flocculatio‌n phenomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Post–zone phenomenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/817d0f1e97104caca8c0a13d9ae48795x1280x1481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Post–zone phenomenon</strong></p><p>This phenomenon is called <strong>post–zone phenomenon</strong> because it has <strong>excessive antigen</strong> in the test system, leading to <strong>weak</strong> or even <strong>absent precipitation.</strong> </p>\n<p>Lattice hypothesis explains precipitation reactions. The amount of precipitate formed is greatly influenced by the <strong>relative proportions</strong> of<strong> antigens</strong> and<strong> antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prozone phenomenon </strong>is caused by <strong>excessive antibodies</strong> in the test system. (weak/absent precipitation- false-negative test)</li>\n<li><strong>Zone of equivalence-</strong> antigen, and antibody are in <strong>optimum</strong> proportions and <strong>maximum</strong> precipitation occurs.</li>\n<li><strong>Post–zone phenomenon </strong>is due to the presence of<strong> excessive antigen</strong> in the test system. (weaker/absent precipitation) </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Flocculation: Instead of sedimenting, precipitate remains suspended as floculus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6402","text":"Double diffusion in two di‌mensions is called as ___​________.","raw_text":"Double diffusion in two dimensions is called as ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oudin p‌rocedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oakley – Fulthrope procedu‌re","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial immu‌nodiffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ouchterlon‌y procedure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ouchterlony procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e85d77c51c14ac89a60add8f9a8305cx1280x1437.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38fc016cd4784c4fb21edf2582e71b5bx1280x1437.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f391f765364f4ec39f78f8064e003af8x600x574.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87b4065a071a4713a624bf5bb43d1727x600x1567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ouchterlony procedure</strong></p><p><strong>Double diffusion in two dimensions</strong> is called the <strong>Ouchterlony</strong> procedure. Ex: <strong>Elek’s test</strong> for testing toxigenicity in <strong>diphtheria</strong> bacilli. </p>\n<p>The images below show the different types of immunodiffusion. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6404","text":"A patient presented to the‌ casualty with the follow​ing finding. A positive N​agler's reaction confirme‍d the presence of lecithi​nase-producing organisms.​ What is the principle be‍hind this test?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the casualty with the following finding. A positive Nagler's reaction confirmed the presence of lecithinase-producing organisms. What is the principle behind this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Precip‌itation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complement f‌ixation test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Agglut‌ination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutral‌isation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neutralisation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43737ca9c1604b14af0f44b94b1c8674.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0da4e13c9cdc4cb5add9d9b7762810b8x600x553.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neutralisation</strong></p><p><strong>Nagler’s reaction</strong> is an example of a <strong>neutralization</strong> test. It is done for the detection of lecithinase toxin produced by <strong><em>C. perfringens.</em></strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>alpha-toxin</strong> of <em>C. perfringens</em> has <strong>phospholipase</strong> activity. When grown on a medium containing egg yolk phospholipid, an area of opacity around the bacterial colonies is seen (Nagler's positive). The presence of <strong>antitoxin</strong> can <strong>inhibit</strong> this.</p>\n<p>Other examples of neutralization tests are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plaque inhibition test- neutralization of bacteriophages </li>\n<li>Schick test- neutralization of diphtheria toxin by antitoxin</li>\n<li>Anti-streptolysin O test- for beta-hemolytic S<em>treptococci.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6405","text":"A patient presents with a ‌painless ulcer on his pen​is. A Wasserman test is d​one and is reactive. What‍ is the principle of this​ test?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a painless ulcer on his penis. A Wasserman test is done and is reactive. What is the principle of this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Precip‌itation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complemen‌t fixation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Agglut‌ination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutral‌isation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complement fixation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/881f5649dc15483d8e26e5e4c4a53297x1280x4005.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complement fixation</strong></p><p><strong>Wassermann's</strong> <strong>reaction </strong>is an example of a <strong>complement</strong> <strong>fixation</strong> <strong>test</strong>. It is a non-specific test for the <strong>diagnosis of syphilis. </strong></p>\n<p>An antigen combines with its (specific) antibody in the presence of complement, and the <strong>antigen-antibody complex adsorbs</strong> the <strong>complement.</strong> This reaction is not visible and requires the use of an indicator (sheep erythrocytes and the specific anti-erythrocyte antibody).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2801","text":"Which of the following act‌ivates the classical comp​lement pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following activates the classical complement pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune c‌omplexes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mannose ‌residues","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrolys‌is of C3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endot‌oxins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Immune complexes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd065f0590404bb3b7f00a67bf1a25b4x600x1116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Immune complexes</strong></p><p>The <strong>classical pathway</strong> of complement is activated by <strong>immune complexes </strong>or antigen-antibody complexes. (antigen bound to IgM or IgG).</p>\n<p><strong>Lipopolysaccharide</strong> of gram-negative bacteria activates the <strong>alternative</strong> complement pathway.</p>\n<p>The binding of <strong>mannose-binding</strong> lectin to mannose residues (on the surfaces of Salmonella, Neisseria, and Listeria) activates the<strong> lectin</strong> complement pathway. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2954","text":"In the alternate complemen‌t pathway, which of the f​ollowing functions as C3 ​convertase?","raw_text":"In the alternate complement pathway, which of the following functions as C3 convertase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C4‌b2b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C3‌bB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C3‌bBb","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C4‌b2a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C3bBb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a406c0e380a4fb390216e06b609388ex600x1116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C3bBb</strong></p><p><strong>C3 convertase</strong> in the <strong>alternate </strong>pathway is <strong>C3bBb. </strong></p>\n<p>In the <strong>classical</strong> pathway<strong> and lectin </strong>pathway, the C3 convertase is<strong> C4b2a.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2616","text":"A 23-year-old girl present‌s with the finding shown ​below. She has a history ​of multiple similar episo‍des in the past and repor​ts that some of her famil​y members have similar co‍mplaints. She has no itch​ing. Which of the followi‍ng is deficient in this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old girl presents with the finding shown below. She has a history of multiple similar episodes in the past and reports that some of her family members have similar complaints. She has no itching. Which of the following is deficient in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C3b ina‌ctivator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C1, C‌2, C4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C1 inh‌ibitor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C5‌-C8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C1 inhibitor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ecdb416e66d49fd80006a9523c0da94.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C1 inhibitor</strong></p><p>This girl presents with features of <strong>hereditary angioneurotic edema</strong>, which is due to a <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> C1 inhibitor</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hereditary angioneurotic edema is an<strong> autosomal dominant </strong>disorder<strong> </strong>that is more common than complement deficiency states. <strong>A deficiency</strong> of <strong>C1 inhibitor</strong> leads to <strong>unregulated</strong> <strong>activation</strong> of <strong>kallikrein</strong>. This increases the production of vasoactive peptides such as <strong>bradykinin</strong>. It causes episodes of <strong>edema</strong> affecting the skin and mucosal surfaces such as the larynx and the gastrointestinal tract. This may result in life-threatening <strong>asphyxia</strong> or nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea after minor trauma or emotional stress.</p>\n<p>Note -</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C1, C2, C4</strong> deficiency - collagen vascular disorders.</li>\n<li><strong>C5-C8</strong> deficiency - recurrent bacteremia with <em>Neisseria</em> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gzYu7fwm","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"2_the_physiology_of_conception","title":"2.The Physiology Of Conception","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5509","text":"When do germ cells appear ​in the yolk sac?","raw_text":"When do germ cells appear in the yolk sac?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"7 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Germ cells</strong> appear in the <strong>yolk sac</strong> in the <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> week</strong>. </p>\n<p>The sequence of events in primordial germ cell formation and migration are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2<sup>nd</sup> week - Germ cells are formed in the epiblast.  </li>\n<li>3<sup>rd</sup> week - Germ cells move through the primitive streak and migrate to the wall of the yolk sac.</li>\n<li>4<sup>th</sup> week - Germ cells begin to migrate from the yolk sac to gonads.</li>\n<li>5<sup>th</sup> week - Germ cell migration is complete.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 30\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Gametes are derived from these primordial germ cells.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5516","text":"When does the ovary contai​n the maximum number of o​ogonia?","raw_text":"When does the ovary contain the maximum number of oogonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5th month of intrauterine ​life","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"7th month of intrauterine ​life","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At b​irth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3rd month of intrauterine ​life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5th month of intrauterine life","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5th month of intrauterine life</strong></p><p>The ovary contains the <strong>maximum</strong> number of <strong>oogonia</strong> at the <strong>5<sup>th</sup> month of intrauterine life.</strong> The number of oogonia is estimated to be 7 million.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 41\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Oogenesis is the process wherein oogonia differentiate into mature oocytes. It begins before birth. At <strong>birth,</strong> there are approximately <strong>2 million</strong> primordial follicles. At the initiation of <strong>puberty,</strong> the follicle count is less than <strong>400,000, </strong>of which less than 500 are destined to ovulate. Every month, 15–20 follicles are released, out of which one matures to form the ovum.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5518","text":"A man with oligospermia ha​s completed his course of​ hormonal treatment. Afte​r how many days will you ‌test his sperm count?","raw_text":"A man with oligospermia has completed his course of hormonal treatment. After how many days will you test his sperm count?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50-60​ days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60-70​ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"80-90​ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"70-80​ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 80-90 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/985adf8461e84b67b5450b9db276d174x1279x1889.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 80-90 days</strong></p><p>The sperm count is tested<strong> 80-90 days </strong>after hormonal treatment, as the average time is taken for <strong>spermatogenesis</strong> is<strong> 74 days, </strong>and approximately<strong> 14 days </strong>to reach the<strong> ejaculatory ducts.</strong><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The effect of hormonal therapy on the<strong> early </strong>phase of<strong> spermatogenesis </strong>is reflected in the <strong>ejaculated sperm concentration </strong>after<strong> three months.</strong></p>\n<p>Spermatogenesis is the process of the <strong>formation of sperms</strong> from spermatogonia. Approximately 100 million sperms are produced daily.</p>\n<p>In patients diagnosed with oligospermia, hormonal treatment is carried out using FSH, hCG, or testosterone. Improvements in sperm count can take a few months, hence the course is usually for 3-6 months. Normal spermatogenesis occurs after this time period. </p>\n<p><strong>Spermiogenesis</strong> refers to the<strong> </strong>series of changes that occur in the formation of <strong>spermatozoa from spermatids</strong>. These are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Acrosome formation</li>\n<li>Condensation of the nucleus </li>\n<li>Formation of the neck, middle piece, and tail</li>\n<li>Shedding of cytoplasm as residual bodies</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Complete <strong>motility</strong> is obtained in the<strong> epididymis. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5519","text":"What is the time taken for​ capacitation of the sper​m?","raw_text":"What is the time taken for capacitation of the sperm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-4 ​hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-6 ​hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6-8 ​hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"8-10 ​hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6-8 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4c2a22b16c844eeafee512d6561cb21x1280x1435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6-8 hours</strong></p><p>The time taken for the <strong>capacitation</strong> of sperms is<strong> 6-8 hours </strong>(approximately 7 hours).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 58\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Sperms</strong> have to undergo <strong>two changes in the female reproductive tract</strong> to be able to fertilise the ovum:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>1) <strong>Capacitation</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the<strong> functional maturation</strong> of the spermatozoa. </li>\n<li>It occurs in the <strong>fallopian tube</strong> and involves epithelial interactions between the sperm and the mucosal surface of the tube.</li>\n<li>The glycoprotein <strong>coat</strong> and seminal<strong> plasma proteins</strong> are <strong>removed.</strong></li>\n<li>Only capacitated sperms can pass through the corona cells and undergo the acrosomal reaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2) <strong>Acrosomal reaction </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It occurs after binding to <strong>zona pellucida,</strong> induced by zona proteins. </li>\n<li><strong>Enzymes</strong> such as acrosin and trypsin-like protein are <strong>released</strong> to<strong> penetrate</strong> the <strong>zona pellucida</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> plays an <strong>important role</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5524","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about the​ resumption of meiosis-I?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the resumption of meiosis-I?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is not hormone dependen​t","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resumed ​due to LH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Resumed d​ue to FSH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resumed d​ue to GnRH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Resumed due to LH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53dd7c50dac04043940249130d646789x1280x3073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Resumed due to LH</strong></p><p><strong>Meiosis-I</strong> in the primary oocyte is resumed after <strong>LH surge</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5529","text":"In which of the following ​steps does reductional di​vision occur?","raw_text":"In which of the following steps does reductional division occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary to secondary sperm​atocyte","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary spermatocyte to ​spermatid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Germ cells to spermatogoni​um","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spermatogonium to primary ​spermatocyte","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary to secondary spermatocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/405b5ab2819840ba877ee6cec83e0f28x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary to secondary spermatocyte</strong></p><p>Meiosis I or<strong> reductional division</strong> occurs in the transition from <strong>primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte.</strong></p>\n<p>Meiosis takes place in <strong>germ cells </strong>to generate male and female gametes.  Meiosis involves two stages of cell division, namely, meiosis I and II.</p>\n<p><strong>Meiosis I</strong> produces two haploid cells. Because the <strong>ploidy is reduced</strong> from diploid to haploid, meiosis I is referred to as a reductional division. <strong>Meiosis II</strong> is an <strong>equational division</strong> analogous to mitosis, resulting in the creation of four haploid daughter cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5532","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about the prima​ry oocyte?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about the primary oocyte?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is formed after meiosis​ I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It forms the primordial fo​llicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It gets arrested in diplot​ene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is ​diploid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is formed after meiosis I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2642202da7854fe49b784aa12f453e57x1280x3073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is formed after meiosis I</strong></p><p>The <strong>secondary oocyte</strong> is formed <strong>after meiosis I</strong>. </p>\n<p>Primordial germ cells divide by mitosis to form oogonia. The majority of the <strong>oogonia</strong> divide by mitosis, however, some <strong>enter meiosis I</strong> and are <strong>arrested</strong> in the <strong>diplotene</strong> stage of prophase and are now called<strong> primary oocytes</strong>. Primary oocytes have <strong>not yet completed</strong> meiosis, hence they are <strong>diploid</strong>. The <strong>primary oocyte</strong> along <strong>with</strong> surrounding <strong>epithelial cells</strong> forms the <strong>primordial follicle.</strong></p>\n<p>Meiosis I is completed after LH surge during puberty. Hence at the completetion of meiosis I, secondary oocyte is produced.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5536","text":"An embryologist is prepari​ng embryos for implantati​on. When is zona hatching​ expected to occur?","raw_text":"An embryologist is preparing embryos for implantation. When is zona hatching expected to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 days after fertilization​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 days after fertilization​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7 days after fertilization​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9 days after fertilization​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 days after fertilization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 days after fertilization</strong></p><p>Zona hatching occurs at around <strong>5 days after fertilization.</strong></p>\n<p>Fertilization is divided into the following phases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Penetration of the corona radiata</li>\n<li>Penetration of the zona pellucida</li>\n<li>Fusion of the oocyte and sperm cell membranes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After fertilisation, the zygote differentiates to form the morula and then the blastocyst. The<strong> zona pellucida disappears</strong> at this stage, which is called <strong>zona hatching.</strong> The trophoblasts (outer cell mass) are now able to associate with the endometrium during implantation. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 62\"> </div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5539","text":"You are performing intraut​erine insemination during​ the ovulatory period in ​a patient with infertilit‌y. When will you expect i‌mplantation to occur?","raw_text":"You are performing intrauterine insemination during the ovulatory period in a patient with infertility. When will you expect implantation to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6–10 days after fertilizat​ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3–5 days after fertilizati​on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14–19 days after fertiliza​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1–2 days after fertilizati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6–10 days after fertilization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fc7d899beca41b6add7984acbc104dfx1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6–10 days after fertilization</strong></p><p>The fertilized ovum <strong>implants </strong>by <strong>6–10 days </strong>in the uterine cavity.</p>\n<p>Implantation involves: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 108\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> <strong>Apposition</strong>: Initial contact of the blastocyst to the uterine wall</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> <strong>Adhesion</strong>: Increased physical contact between the blastocyst and uterine epithelium</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> <strong>Invasion</strong>: Penetration and invasion of syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblasts into the endometrium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A successful implantation requires receptive endometrium primed with estrogen and progesterone released by the corpus luteum.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5548","text":"The following figure shows​ the transverse section o​f the uterus with an impl​anted zygote. Identify st‌ructure A.","raw_text":"The following figure shows the transverse section of the uterus with an implanted zygote. Identify structure A.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decidua​ basalis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decidua p​arietalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decidua c​apsularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine​ cavity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decidua basalis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d27b65eec19f48b09b0717b737083493.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/154b4879e8f849a0951968affa73c689x1280x1020.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0e416fc1ec94ddb89d83ced476326a7x1280x813.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f49aef412d834055a26c8e026e177f72x1280x2202.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decidua basalis</strong></p><p>In the given image, the <strong>decidua basalis</strong> is marked as A. This part of the decidua forms the placenta.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Implantation occurs 6 to 7 days after fertilization. After implantation, the <strong>endometrium</strong> is called the <strong>decidua. </strong>It is of the following<strong> types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decidua <strong>basalis</strong> - present over chorion frondosum. Decidua basalis and chorion frondosum form the placenta. </li>\n<li>Decidua <strong>capsularis</strong> - present over abembryonic pole. </li>\n<li>Decidua <strong>parietalis</strong> - present on the opposite side of the uterus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The villi cover the entire surface of the chorion during the early weeks. As the fetus grows, the<strong> villi</strong> on the <strong>embryonic pole</strong> continue to grow, giving rise to the <strong>chorion frondosum</strong>. At the <strong>abembryonic pole</strong>, the villi degenerate. By the third month, this side of the chorion becomes smooth, and is called the<strong> chorion laeve.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>As the fetus grows, the<strong> chorion laeve</strong> comes into contact with the <strong>decidua parietalis</strong> on the opposite side of the uterus, and <strong>fusion</strong> occurs. Thus, the <strong>uterine lumen</strong> is <strong>obliterated</strong>. The amnion and chorion fuse, to form the amniochorionic membrane. Rupture of the amniochorionic membrane during labor is called 'breaking of waters'.</p>\n<p>Note: Superfetation can theoretically occur till the time the uterine lumen is not obliterated. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5555","text":"What do the Langhans cells​ form?","raw_text":"What do the Langhans cells form?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner c​ell mass","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mor​ula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytotro​phoblast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Syncytiot​rophoblast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cytotrophoblast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca45dc78312341cba7cf5c4e7c957535x1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cytotrophoblast</strong></p><p><strong>Langhans cells</strong> form the <strong>cytotrophoblast.</strong></p>\n<p>The cells of the <strong>blastocyst</strong> differentiate into an outer<strong> trophectoderm</strong> and an <strong>inner cell mass</strong>.</p>\n<p>The trophectoderm is further differentiated into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An inner mononuclear cellular layer called<strong> cytotrophoblast</strong> or<strong> Langhans layer</strong> made of Langhans cells.</li>\n<li>An outer layer of multinucleated syncytium called <strong>syncytiotrophoblast</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The blastomere further divides to form the morula. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9942","text":"Identify the structure mar​ked A.","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked A.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordia​l follicle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary​ oocyte","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Theca ​interna","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granulo​sa cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary oocyte","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09259301443b40caa3e9b77a5b7614c4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad5bbca23678456c91538da0d239b68dx1280x1148.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e19abfeb55ac4d7eaf19c606d6b80307x1280x3348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary oocyte</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is <strong>primary oocyte</strong>. Primary oocytes are the germ cells seen in the <strong>medullary region of the ovary</strong>. These enlarge at the beginning of the growth phase.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 164\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>During this cell growth period, many oocytes undergo degeneration, both before and after birth. A single layer of<strong> flattened follicular cells</strong> that were derived originally from the germinal epithelium soon <strong>surrounds</strong> the <strong>primary oocytes</strong>. The structure that is formed is now called a<strong> primordial follicle</strong>. These are seen first in the medulla and later in the cortex.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0698","text":"In which part of the fallo​pian tube does fertilizat​ion occur?","raw_text":"In which part of the fallopian tube does fertilization occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amp​ulla","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ist​hmus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inters​titial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infund​ibular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ampulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ampulla</strong></p><p><strong>Fertilization</strong> usually occurs in the <strong>ampulla</strong> of the fallopian tube.</p>\n<p>During <strong>ovulation</strong>, the <strong>ovum</strong> is <strong>released</strong> and it enters the fallopian tube. It is propelled towards the uterine cavity by the peristaltic action of the fallopian tubes, prostaglandins in the male seminal fluid and oxytocin. It can only be <strong>fertilized within 24 hours</strong> of ovulation.  If coitus takes place during this period, the <strong>spermatozoa</strong> swimming up into the tubes <strong>meet</strong> the <strong>ovum</strong> in the <strong>ampulla</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9941","text":"A newborn is admitted to t​he NICU with the followin​g teratoma. Which embryon​ic layers is this derived‌ from?","raw_text":"A newborn is admitted to the NICU with the following teratoma. Which embryonic layers is this derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoderm a​nd ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoderm a​nd mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesoderm a​nd ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoderm, mesoderm, ectode​rm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cd3ae2e952894ce79b18f2406ca59d15.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>oropharygeal teratoma,</strong> which contains derivatives from <strong>all three germ layers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tumor may include gut, bone, skin, and teeth. These tumors arise from <strong>pluripotent stem cells</strong> that differentiate into any of three germ layers forming tissues of all three germ layers. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9943","text":"Identify the structure mar​ked as A.","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked as A.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yolk​ sac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alla​ntois","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Future umb​ilical cord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tail​ end","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Allantois","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be959f86abbf4178a9feba3488799626.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d4af9003fd24d3aa69e7ca79f209903x1280x1041.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Allantois</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the <strong>al</strong><strong>lantois</strong>.</p>\n<p>The posterior wall of the yolk sac forms the allantois on the 16th day of development. Its <strong>distal portion</strong> forms the <strong>urachus</strong> which forms the <strong>median umbilical ligament</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given image shows an embryo at the end of the third week.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Fmg50lrG","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"2_elementary_optics_and_physiology_of_vision","title":"2.Elementary Optics And Physiology Of Vision","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0641","text":"What is the most important​ factor influencing the f​ocus of light rays on the‍ retina?","raw_text":"What is the most important factor influencing the focus of light rays on the retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior surface of cornea​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior surface of corne​a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior sur​face of lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior su​rface of lens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior surface of cornea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior surface of cornea</strong></p><p>The<strong> anterior surface</strong> of the<strong> cornea </strong>is the most <strong>important </strong>factor influencing the focus of light rays on the retina.</p>\n<p>The refraction of light is the <strong>maximum at the cornea,</strong> due to the curvature of the cornea and the difference between the refractive index of air and tear film. </p>\n<p>Refractive indices of various surfaces of the eye:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 191px;\" width=\"277\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Surface </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Refractive Index </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">1.376</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Lens- Total </td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">1.40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Lens- Cortex (periphery)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">1.386</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Lens- Nucleus </td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">1.406 (maximum)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Aqueous Humor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">1.336</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Vitreous Humor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">1.336</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0870","text":"The refractive index is th​e maximum at the ________​____.","raw_text":"The refractive index is the maximum at the ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior surface of the le​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior surface of the l​ens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cor​nea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Centre of​ the lens","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Centre of the lens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Centre of the lens</strong></p><p>The refractive index is the <strong>maximum</strong> at the <strong>center</strong> of the <strong>lens</strong>. </p>\n<p>Refractive indices of the various surfaces of the eye:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 316px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Surface </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Refractive Index </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">1.376</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Lens- Total </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">1.40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Lens- Cortex (periphery)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">1.386</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Lens- Nucleus </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">1.406 (maximum)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Aqueous Humor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">1.336</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Vitreous Humor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">1.336</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The total dioptric power of the lens is about <strong>18 D </strong>(16 to 20 D). Lens is composed of 64% water, 35% protein, and 1% lipid/carbohydrates/trace elements, and these components contribute to the refractive index of the lens. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0642","text":"How many Purkinje images a​re formed in a normal eye​?","raw_text":"How many Purkinje images are formed in a normal eye?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb7011aba42f48cc8f3fce697f8ed746x1280x1337.771509167842.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4</strong></p><p>A total of <strong>4 Purkinje images </strong>are formed in <strong>normal eyes.</strong></p>\n<p>Purkinje images are reflections of objects from the structure of the eye. They are also known as Purkinje reflexes or Purkinje–Sanson images.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 184px; width: 397px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Image </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.41px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.59px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Direction</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\">Image 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.41px;\">Anterior surface of the cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.59px;\">Erect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\">Image 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.41px;\">Posterior surface of the cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.59px;\">Erect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\">Image 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.41px;\">Anterior surface of the lens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.59px;\">Erect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\">Image 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.41px;\">Posterior surface of the lens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.59px;\">Inverted</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>aphakia,</strong> images<strong> 3 </strong>and<strong> 4 </strong>are absent, while in a <strong>mature cataract, </strong>image<strong> 4 </strong>is absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0643","text":"Among the Purkinje images ​in the eye, which image i​s inverted?","raw_text":"Among the Purkinje images in the eye, which image is inverted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima​ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima​ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima​ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ima​ge 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Image 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d117f807a5df44fda4c8ca707d0ce068x1280x1338.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Image 4</strong></p><p>Image 4, which is formed on the posterior surface of the lens, is inverted.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0644","text":"The visual axis is the lin​e joining the _________.","raw_text":"The visual axis is the line joining the _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centre of the cornea to th​e retina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Object ​to fovea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Centre of the lens to the ​cornea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Object to center of rotati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Object to fovea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Object to fovea</strong></p><p>The <strong>visual axis </strong>is<strong> </strong>the line joining the <strong>fixation point</strong> (<strong>object</strong>), <strong>nodal point</strong> (centre of the lens), and the <strong>fovea</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0645","text":"What is the angle formed b​etween the optic axis and​ visual axis at the nodal‍ point?","raw_text":"What is the angle formed between the optic axis and visual axis at the nodal point?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​α","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​λ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​κ","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​γ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. α","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. α</strong></p><p><strong>Angle alpha (α)</strong> is the angle between the <strong>optical axis </strong>and <strong>visual axis </strong>at the nodal point. </p>\n<p><span lang=\"el\" title=\"Greek-language text\">Angle<strong> lambda (λ) </strong></span><span lang=\"el\" title=\"Greek-language text\">is the angle between the <strong>pupillary</strong> axis and the <strong>line of sight.  </strong>It is the global tilt offset of the eye when it fixates on a specific target and is a response to the eccentric displaced position of the fovea from the geometrical center of the retina. (Option B)</span></p>\n<p><span lang=\"el\" title=\"Greek-language text\">A</span>ngle<strong> kappa (κ) </strong>is the angle between the <strong>visual axis </strong>and the <strong>pupillary line </strong>at the cornea. It is the only angle that can be <strong>measured </strong>and is of clinical significance. (Option C)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Positive κ </strong>is seen in pseudo<strong>exotropia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Negative κ </strong>is seen in pseudo<strong>esotropia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span lang=\"el\" title=\"Greek-language text\">A</span>ngle<strong> gamma (γ)</strong> is the angle between the <strong>optical axis </strong>and the <strong>fixation axis </strong>at the center of eyeball rotation. (Option D)</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 117px; width: 440px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84.6875px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.799px;\"><strong>Visual axis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.021px;\"><strong>Line of sight</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.91px;\"><strong>Fixation axis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84.6875px;\"><strong>Pupillary axis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.799px;\">Kappa (κ)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.021px;\">Lambda (<span lang=\"el\" title=\"Greek-language text\">λ)</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.91px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84.6875px;\"><strong>Optic Axis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.799px;\">Alpha (α)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.021px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.91px;\">Gamma (γ)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0646","text":"A 28-year-old man comes to​ get his power checked. T​he optometrist finds the ‍focal length of his lens ​to be 0.75m; what will be‌ its refractive power?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man comes to get his power checked. The optometrist finds the focal length of his lens to be 0.75m; what will be its refractive power?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.7​5 D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.​5 D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.2​5 D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1.3​3 D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1.33 D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1.33 D</strong></p><p>Refractive <strong>power</strong> (dioptre) =  1/Focal length in metres</p>\n<p>So, for the given question, refractive power (D) = 1/0.75 = <strong>1.33 D</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0647","text":"What is the distance betwe​en point A and B shown be​low? DD - Disc Diameter","raw_text":"<p>What is the distance between point A and B shown below?</p>\n<p>DD - Disc Diameter</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 ​DD","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3 ​DD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.5​ DD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 ​DD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 DD","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/165819ed7c934beeb4f0ec3bf87d3345.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1b33eea2dd343969e040c6409c4862dx1280x1237.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 DD</strong></p><p>In the image, point A is the <strong>optic disc</strong> and point B is the <strong>fovea.</strong> The fovea is situated about <strong>2 disc diameters</strong> (3–4 mm) away from the temporal margin of the optic disc.</p>\n<p>The optic disc is an area of about 1.5 mm. There is a blind spot / physiological scotoma, corresponding to the optic disc. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0648","text":"A 6-year-old child complai​ns that she cannot see an​ything on the whiteboard ‍clearly. On ophthalmologi​cal examination, she is f‌ound to have a 2 mm short​ening of the axial length‌ of the eyeball. How much​ eye power will this lead​ to?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child complains that she cannot see anything on the whiteboard clearly.  On ophthalmological examination, she is found to have a 2 mm shortening of the axial length of the eyeball. How much eye power will this lead to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3D m​yopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6D m​yopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3D hyper​metropia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6D hyper​metropia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6D hypermetropia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6D hypermetropia</strong></p><p><strong>Shortening</strong> of <strong>2mm</strong> of the axial length of the eyeball will lead to <strong>6D</strong> of <strong>hypermetropia.</strong></p>\n<p>In<strong> axial</strong> hypermetropia,<strong> </strong><strong>1mm shortening</strong> of the anteroposterior diameter of the eye results in<strong> 3 dioptres of hypermetropia. </strong>Therefore, 2-mm shortening will lead to 6D of hypermetropia.</p>\n<p>In<strong> curvature</strong> hypermetropia, a <strong>1mm increase</strong> in radius of curvature results in<strong> 6 dioptres</strong> of<strong> hypermetropia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0871","text":"You are studying the optic​s of a patient with regul​ar astigmatism. Identify ‍the point at which the cl​earest image is formed?","raw_text":"You are studying the optics of a patient with regular astigmatism. Identify the point at which the clearest image is formed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​D","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​G","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d34e807d708340619716ce94630cd76b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72fc3eb8e2dc43d3af15ad042d98513ax1280x1234.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. D</strong></p><p>The given figure is a <strong>Sturm's conoid</strong>. It represents a bundle of light rays formed by a<strong> regularly astigmatic surface</strong>. The image is <strong>clearest</strong> or least confusing as <strong>point D</strong>, the <strong>circle of least confusion. </strong></p>\n<p>When light passes through a regularly astigmatic surface(surface with <strong>toric curvature</strong>), vertical and horizontal rays converge at different points.</p>\n<p>The distance between the points of convergence (focus) of the vertical and horizontal rays is called the <strong>focal interval of strum</strong>. (distance between points B and F). The circle of least confusion/ circle of diffusion lies at the mid-point of this interval.</p>\n<p>For the given Sturm's conoid, </p>\n<ul>\n<li>At point A, a horizontal oval is formed because vertical rays converge more rapidly than horizontal rays.</li>\n<li>At point B, vertical rays come to a focus, while horizontal rays are still converging; so, it will be a horizontal straight line.</li>\n<li>At points C, D, and E, the vertical rays are diverging and horizontal rays are still converging.</li>\n<li>At point D, the vertical rays have diverged as much as the horizontal rays have converged; so, we get a perfect circle called the <strong>circle of least confusion.</strong></li>\n<li>At point E, a vertical oval is formed.</li>\n<li>At point F, a vertical straight line is formed</li>\n<li>At point G and beyond, both rays are diverging, and we always get a <strong>vertical oval/prolate ellipse.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0649","text":"What is the Stiles-Crawfor​d effect?","raw_text":"What is the Stiles-Crawford effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cutting off of the periphe​ral rays by the iris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High refractive index of t​he core of nucleus of the​ lens than that of the pe‍ripheral cortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low sensitivity of the per​ipheral retina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More sensitivity of the re​tina to perpendicular ray​s than the oblique rays","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. More sensitivity of the retina to perpendicular rays than the oblique rays","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. More sensitivity of the retina to perpendicular rays than the oblique rays</strong></p><p>The <strong>Stiles–Crawford </strong>effect refers to the<strong> greater sensitivity </strong>of the retina to<strong> perpendicular rays </strong>than the oblique rays. It is one of the natural mechanisms that <strong>decrease aberration</strong> in the eye. The rays of light that enter through the center of the pupil have higher sensitivity as compared to those entering from the periphery of the pupil.  </p>\n<p><strong>Natural mechanisms</strong> that <strong>decrease aberrations</strong> in the human eye:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting-off of the peripheral rays by the iris</li>\n<li>The high refractive index of the core of the nucleus of the lens than that of the peripheral cortex</li>\n<li>Low sensitivity of the peripheral retina</li>\n<li>Stiles–Crawford effect</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0650","text":"What are the first-order n​eurons of the optic pathw​ay?","raw_text":"What are the first-order neurons of the optic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Photore​ceptors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ganglion​ic cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bipola​r cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optic r​adiation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Photoreceptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Photoreceptors</strong></p><p><strong>Photoreceptors</strong> are the first-order neurons in the <strong>visual pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0868","text":"You are tasked with studyi​ng and mapping the photor​eceptor cells in a cohort‍ of patients. Consider th​e following statements an‌d pick out the false one.​","raw_text":"You are tasked with studying and mapping the photoreceptor cells in a cohort of patients. Consider the following statements and pick out the false one.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There are 120 million rods​ in each human eye.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rods produce a hyperpolari​zing potential on activat​ion.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cones predominate in the e​xtrafoveal region.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cones produce a hyperpolar​izing potential on activa​tion.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cones predominate in the extrafoveal region.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0b2fa468cde49e185bcd7f969a73a5dx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cones predominate in the extrafoveal region.</strong></p><p><strong>Cones</strong> predominate in the <strong>foveal region,</strong> not the extrafoveal region.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 617px; height: 245px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Rods</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px;\">\n<p><strong>120 million</strong> in number in each human eye</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px;\">\n<p><strong>6 million</strong> in number in each human eye</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px;\">\n<p>Receptors for <strong>dim/night vision</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px;\">\n<p>Receptors for <strong>day</strong> vision</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px;\">\n<p>Predominate in the <strong>extra-foveal</strong> region</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px;\">\n<p>Predominate in the <strong>foveal</strong> region</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px;\">\n<p><strong>More sensitive</strong> to light</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px;\">\n<p><strong>Less sensitive</strong> to light</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px;\">\n<p>Contain pigment <strong>rhodopsin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px;\">\n<p>Contain pigment<strong> iodopsin</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295.799px;\">\n<p>Produce <strong>hyperpolarizing</strong> potential changes on activation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.91px;\">\n<p>Produce <strong>hyperpolarizing</strong> potential changes on activation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0652","text":"Which of the following cel​ls are retinal glial cell​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells are retinal glial cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meissner's​ corpuscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muller​ cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amacrin​e cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basket​ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muller cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muller cells</strong></p><p><strong>Muller cells</strong> are a type of <strong>glial cells</strong> found in the retina that help in the <strong>binding</strong> of the neuronal elements together. They do no take part in the neurosensory pathway of light. </p>\n<p>Other cells in the retina: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 220px; width: 868px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.455px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cells </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.545px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Use </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Potential </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Comments</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.455px;\">Rods and cones</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.545px;\">Photoreceptor cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Hyperpolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\">1st order neurons </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.455px;\">Bipolar cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.545px;\">Connect photoreceptors to ganglion cells </td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Depolarizing or hyperpolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\">2nd order neurons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.455px;\">Amacrine cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.545px;\">Adjust the relative color and luminosity inputs under changing light conditions.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Depolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\">Interneurons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.455px;\">Ganglion cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.545px;\">Form the visual pathway</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Propagated action potential</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\">3rd order neurons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.455px;\">Horizontal cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.545px;\">\n<p>Increase visual acuity by lateral inhibition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Hyperpolarizing</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212px;\">Interneurons </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Meissner's corpuscles</strong>: Tactile receptors localizing in the dermis between epidermal ridges, perceiving touch</p>\n<p><strong>Basket cells</strong>: GABAergic interneurons found in the cerebellum</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0655","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rods and cones release Ach​ at their synapses.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phototransduction is assoc​iated with hyperpolarizat​ion.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amacrine cells release var​ious neurotransmitters.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bipolar cells show either ​depolarization or hyperpo​larization.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rods and cones release Ach at their synapses.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rods and cones release Ach at their synapses.</strong></p><p><strong>Rods and cones </strong>release <strong>glutamate</strong> (not acetylcholine) at their synapses.</p>\n<p>Photoreceptors get <strong>hyperpolarized </strong>in response to stimulus, and hence, <strong>phototransduction</strong> is associated with hyperpolarization.</p>\n<p><strong>Amacrine cells</strong> secrete at least eight types of neurotransmitters, including GABA, glycine, dopamine, <strong>acetylcholine</strong>, and indolamine, all of which function as <strong>inhibitory neurotransmitters.</strong></p>\n<p>Bipolar cells show both depolarization/hyperpolarization.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0656","text":"Which of the following is ​responsible for inducing ​phototransduction?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for inducing phototransduction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metarho​dopsin I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metarhod​opsin II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bathorh​odopsin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumirh​odopsin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metarhodopsin II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76b383eb86f04d85a80a66fdef945468x1280x1372.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metarhodopsin II</strong></p><p><strong>Metarhodopsin II</strong> is responsible for <strong>phototransduction</strong>.</p>\n<p>It activates <strong>transducin </strong>member of G proteins. This leads to the <strong>closure of Na+ channels</strong> and <strong>hyperpolarization,</strong> thereby inducing phototransduction (signal transduction in the retina).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0658","text":"During the dark phase of t​he visual cycle, which fo​rm of vitamin A combines ‍with opsin to form rhodop​sin?","raw_text":"During the dark phase of the visual cycle, which form of vitamin A combines with opsin to form rhodopsin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All tran​s-retinal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All tran​s-retinol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11- cis​-retinal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"11- cis​-retinol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 11- cis-retinal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47eb9da3d68645e59f7c6254e57a9d71x1280x1623.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 11- cis-retinal</strong></p><p><strong>Opsin</strong> combines with <strong>11-cis-retinal </strong>to produce <strong>rhodopsin.</strong></p>\n<p>Only the <strong>retinal</strong> form of <strong>vitamin A</strong> is involved in the formation of the photopigment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6697","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g the physiology of photo‍receptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the physiology of photoreceptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phototransduction results ​in closure of c-GMP-gated​ cation channels.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chromophore regeneration o​ccurs in the outer segmen​t of photoreceptors.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Photon absorption induces ​photo-isomerisation of 11​-cis-retinal to all-trans‍-retinal.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11-cis-Retinal is bound to​ opsins by covalent bonds​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chromophore regeneration occurs in the outer segment of photoreceptors.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1da215effb3f46c4986ebf386184d839x600x579.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chromophore regeneration occurs in the outer segment of photoreceptors.</strong></p><p>Chromophore regeneration occurs in the <strong>retinal pigment epithelial</strong> cells.</p>\n<p>Phototransduction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rhodopsin</strong> is a <strong>G-protein-coupled</strong> receptor found in the <strong>outer segments</strong> of rod photoreceptors.</li>\n<li>11-cis<em>-</em>retinal (a chromophore) is covalently bound to opsins via a <strong>lysine</strong> residue on one of the transmembrane domains.</li>\n<li>The receptor is activated by photo-isomerization of prebound <strong>11-</strong><strong>cis<em>-</em>retinal</strong> to <strong>ALL-TRANS<em>-</em>retinal.</strong></li>\n<li>Activation of the receptor causes binding and <strong>activation</strong> of the <strong>G protein, transducin,</strong> with signal transduction <strong>via cGMP phosphodiesterase,</strong> resulting in a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>intracellular cGMP</strong> levels and <strong>closure</strong> of the <strong>cGMP-gated Na</strong> channels.</li>\n<li>This causes <strong>hyperpolarization</strong> of the <strong>photoreceptor </strong>cell, which modulates neurotransmitter release at the synapse with bipolar cells.</li>\n<li>This entire process triggered by the activation of rhodopsin is <strong>phototransduction</strong>, which occurs in the <strong>outer segment</strong> of <strong>photoreceptors</strong> and converts <strong>light</strong> energy into an <strong>electrical signal.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chromophore regeneration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>All-trans retinAL </strong>is converted to <strong>all-</strong><strong>trans<em>-</em>retinOL.</strong></li>\n<li>All-trans-retinOL gets transported by<strong> i</strong>nterphotoreceptor <strong>r</strong>etinoid-<strong>b</strong>inding<strong> p</strong>rotein (IRBP) to the <strong>retinal pigment epithelial cells</strong>, where it is <strong>reconstituted</strong> into the <strong>11-</strong><strong><em>cis </em>retinal isoform.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0659","text":"What signals are carried b​y the parvocellular pathw​ay from the lateral genic‍ulate body to the visual ​cortex?","raw_text":"What signals are carried by the parvocellular pathway from the lateral geniculate body to the visual cortex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Movement, depth, and flick​er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colour vision, shape, and ​fine details","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Temporal ​frequency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Luminance​ contrast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colour vision, shape, and fine details","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36b8f6752ff3449ba1a63042e7602c86x1280x2373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colour vision, shape, and fine details</strong></p><p>The<strong> parvocellular pathway </strong>gives signals for <strong>color vision, texture, shape,</strong> and <strong>fine details. </strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 175px; width: 670px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.132px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Parvocellular Pathway</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.868px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Magnocellular Pathway</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Arises </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.132px;\">layers 3, 4, 5, and 6 of LGB </td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.868px;\">Layers 1 and 2 of LGB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Terminates</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.132px;\">Layer 4 of visual cortex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.868px;\">Layer 4 of visual cortex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Use </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.132px;\">Color vision, texture, shape, and fine details</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.868px;\">Movement, depth, flicker, and spatial organization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>LGB: lateral geniculate body</p>\n<p>The<strong> interlaminar pathway </strong>gives signals for <strong>color</strong> vision to layers <strong>2</strong> and <strong>3</strong> of the visual cortex having <strong>clusters</strong> of <strong>cells</strong> called <strong>blobs</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0663","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g \"blobs\" in the primary ‍visual cortex?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding \"blobs\" in the primary visual cortex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Process co​lour vision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Are rich in the enzyme cit​rate synthase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Are present in layers 2 an​d 3 of the visual cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Receive input from the int​erlaminar pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Are rich in the enzyme citrate synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Are rich in the enzyme citrate synthase</strong></p><p><strong>Blobs </strong>in the <strong>primary visual cortex</strong> are rich in the enzyme <strong>cytochrome oxidase.</strong></p>\n<p>Blobs are sections of the visual cortex where groups of neurons that are sensitive to color assemble in cylindrical shapes. They are present in layers 2 and 3 of the visual cortex.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0838","text":"What are Konio cells invol​ved in transmitting?","raw_text":"What are Konio cells involved in transmitting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mot​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ste​reo","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fo​rm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Co​lor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Color","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e25ad72cbf4549738630919dea595da4x1280x2291.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Color</strong></p><p>Konio cells are involved in the transmission of <strong>color.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px;\" width=\"667\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152.465px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Lateral geniculate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.021px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Striate cortex</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Extra-striate cortex</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.688px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Higher centers for body</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152.465px; text-align: center;\">Magno cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.021px; text-align: center;\">→</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px; text-align: center;\">→</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.688px; text-align: center;\">Motion and stereo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152.465px; text-align: center;\">Parvo cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.021px; text-align: center;\">→</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px; text-align: center;\">→</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.688px; text-align: center;\">Form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152.465px; text-align: center;\">Konio cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.021px; text-align: center;\">→</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px; text-align: center;\">→</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.688px; text-align: center;\">Color</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The<strong> koniocellular pathway </strong>is ventral to each parvocellular and magnocellular layer of the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN). K cells are neurochemically and anatomically<strong> distinct</strong> from <strong>M</strong> and <strong>P cells. </strong>They <strong>contain calbindin protein,</strong> whereas M cells and P cells contain parvalbumin. <strong>K1, K4,</strong> and <strong>K6</strong> receive <strong>ipsilateral </strong>retinal inputs, and <strong>K3</strong> and <strong>K5</strong> receive <strong>contralateral</strong> retinal inputs. K2 receives both. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0665","text":"What is the visible range ​of the electromagnetic sp​ectrum of the human eye?","raw_text":"What is the visible range of the electromagnetic spectrum of the human eye?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200-3​70 nm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"370-7​40 nm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"740-1​140 nm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"800-9​00 nm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 370-740 nm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e5f6ed5dd2e44eeb33f9993cd59eddex1280x1047.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82028d1c75ba40e2b6f20c5ba5c5e1d1x1279x1348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 370-740 nm</strong></p><p>The<strong> visible range</strong> of the electromagnetic spectrum for the human eye is <strong>400–700 nm</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Theories of color vision: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trichromatic theory </strong>(<strong>Young–Helmholtz theory</strong>) assumes that there are three types of color receptors. Each receptor is assumed to respond to all wavelengths, however, each has different spectral sensitivities.</li>\n<li><strong>Opponent process theory,</strong> also known as the Hering theory, assumes three sets of receptor systems—red-green, blue-yellow, and black-white. Each one of these functions as an antagonistic pair (e.g., red light stimulates the red receptors and, at the same time, inhibits the green).</li>\n<li><strong>Edwin Land's theory </strong>has three separate color systems (retinexes). Each is an analog to a black-and-white picture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0869","text":"What is the characteristic​ visual defect in deutera​nomaly?","raw_text":"What is the characteristic visual defect in deuteranomaly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Green color is not perceiv​ed.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blue color is not perceive​d.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Green color perception is ​defective.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blue color perception is d​efective.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Green color perception is defective.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/853848805c464c279c3d2b55e69ac599x680x328.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Green color perception is defective.</strong></p><p>In deuteranomaly, the perception of <strong>green color</strong> is <strong>defective.</strong> It is the <strong>most common</strong> disorder of color vision.</p>\n<p><strong>Monochromacy:</strong> <strong>Only one</strong> of the three primary colors (red, blue, or green) can be <strong>appreciated.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dichromacy:</strong> <strong>Two</strong> primary<strong> colors</strong> are appreciated. (anopia = absent).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protanopia: Red color is not perceived.</li>\n<li>Deuteranopia: Green color is not perceived.</li>\n<li>Tritanopia: Blue color is not perceived.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trichromacy:</strong> All the three primary colors are appreciated, but one of them can be defective (anomaly = defective).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protanomaly: Red color perception is defective.</li>\n<li>Deuteranomaly: Green color perception is defective.</li>\n<li>Tritanomaly: Blue color perception is defective.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Achromatopsia:</strong> <strong>No color perception</strong> due to the absence of cones. It may also occur in the lesion of <strong>area V4</strong> or <strong>V8</strong> of the visual cortex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0833","text":"Majority of the energy req​uirement in the lens is m​et by the ______________.‍","raw_text":"Majority of the energy requirement in the lens is met by the ______________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glyco​lysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pentose phos​phate pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TCA ​cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sorbitol​ pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0aecb22d2050472693e646603ca5fba1x1280x2118.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycolysis</strong></p><p>Majority of the energy requirement of the lens is met by <strong>glycolysis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>90–95 %</strong> of the glucose that enters the lens is converted to glucose 6 phosphate by<strong> hexokinase. </strong>The remaining<strong> 5%</strong> is converted to<strong> sorbitol</strong> and <strong>gluconic acid.</strong></p>\n<p>The glucose-6-phosphate formed is metabolized in 3 ways: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glycolysis: 80 %</strong></li>\n<li>Pentose phosphate pathway: 10%</li>\n<li>TCA cycle: 3%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Energy in the lens is stored in the form of <strong>ATP.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6695","text":"What is the angle subtende​d by the optotype on the ​second line of Snellen’s ‍chart when viewed from a ​distance of 6 meters?","raw_text":"What is the angle subtended by the optotype on the second line of Snellen’s chart when viewed from a distance of 6 meters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 minute​s of arc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30 minut​es of arc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"25 minut​es of arc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 minute​s of arc","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30 minutes of arc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/192387686eb2481ca5231f8af8a9f6c6x1280x1482.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30 minutes of arc</strong></p><p>The angle subtended by the optotype (individual letters on the chart) on the second line of Snellen’s chart in the above case would be <strong>30 minutes of arc</strong>.</p>\n<p>The second line on Snellen’s chart corresponds to a distance of 36 meters. When viewed from a 6m distance, it is denoted as visual acuity 6/36. Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>numerator</strong> (6) denotes the distance from which the patient is <strong>actually viewing</strong> it</li>\n<li>The <strong>denominator</strong> (36) denotes the distance at which it should be <strong>ideally viewed.</strong> It denotes the distance at which each stroke of the letter would subtend the minimum angle of resolution (MAR) of <strong>one minute</strong> of <strong>arc</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>MAR</em> = 1/<em>Visual acuity</em></p>\n<p>Each letter is shaped such that it can be placed in a square, the sides of which are five times the breadth of individual limbs of the letters. Hence, the angle subtended by the optotype= MAR x 5</p>\n<p>For eg: At exactly 6 meters distance from the patient, the letters on the 6/6 line shall subtend 5 minutes of arc (such that the individual limbs of the letters subtend 1 minute of arc).</p>\n<p>The equation to calculate the minimum angle of resolution (MAR) is given as follows:</p>\n<p>For the given question,</p>\n<p><em>MAR</em> = 1/ 6/36<br/><em style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">MAR</em><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> = 36/6<br/></span><em style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">MAR</em><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> = 6 minutes of arc</span></p>\n<p>Hence, the angle subtended by the entire letter is 6 × 5 <strong>= 30 minutes of arc</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6696","text":"A 42-year-old man with mon​ocular aphakia wears cont​act lenses. What is the a‍pproximate percentage of ​aniseikonia in his eyes?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man with monocular aphakia wears contact lenses. What is the approximate percentage of aniseikonia in his eyes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3​%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8​%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3​0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8%</strong></p><p>The approximate percentage of <strong>aniseikonia</strong> in a person with <strong>monocular aphakia</strong> wearing a <strong>contact lens</strong> would be <strong>8%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aniseikonia is defined as the <strong>difference</strong> in <strong>image size</strong> between the <strong>two eyes</strong> and can lead to <strong>disturbances</strong> in <strong>stereopsis.</strong> The tolerable limit of aniseikonia is 5–8%. It is reduced with contact lens (8%) compared to glasses (25–30%) in aphakia.</p>\n<p>However, the percentage of aniseikonia is least with an intraocular lens (IOL) (1–3%), thus secondary IOL implantation is the most preferred management of unilateral aphakia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6699","text":"Which cerebellar nucleus c​ontrols the ocular saccad​e?","raw_text":"Which cerebellar nucleus controls the ocular saccade?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Den​tate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embol​iform","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Floc​culus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glo​bose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flocculus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flocculus</strong></p><p>The<strong> Floccular nucleus</strong> of the cerebellum controls ocular saccades.</p>\n<p>A <strong>saccade </strong>is a <strong>rapid </strong>ballistic movement to change the point of fixation. It is the <strong>fastest</strong> of all eye movements and is initiated by the <strong>contralateral frontal lobe. </strong><strong>Smooth pursuit </strong>is a <strong>slow tracking </strong>movement to keep the target on the fovea. It is controlled by the <strong>ipsilateral parieto-occipital lobe. </strong></p>\n<p>Following an object from the right to left is <strong>left pursuit</strong> and is controlled by <strong>ipsilateral</strong> (left)<strong> parieto-occipital lobe,</strong> and the corrective movement is a saccade (in this case, left to right) termed <strong>right saccade</strong> and is controlled by the <strong>contralateral</strong> (left)<strong> frontal lobe.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> floccular nucleus </strong>is found in the <strong>lobule HX</strong> of the cerebellar vermis. Its functions include matching pulse-step, gaze holding, and sustained smooth pursuit. Bilateral lesions of the flocculus result in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Post-saccadic drift-</strong> saccadic movements do not stop, but there is a brief, several hundred milliseconds drift of the eyes after each saccade.</li>\n<li><strong>Saccadic pursuit-</strong> smooth tracking is replaced by simultaneous rapid jerking motions of the eye to follow an object toward the ipsilateral visual field.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaze-evoked nystagmus- </strong>impaired ability to hold the eyes in the eccentric position, resulting in gaze-evoked nystagmus towards the affected side of the cerebellum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Emboliform and globose nuclei:</strong> Generation of vertical saccades</p>\n<p><strong>Fastigial nuclei:</strong> Pursuit initiation</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0653","text":"Purely depolarizing cells ​in the optic pathway are ​________.","raw_text":"Purely depolarizing cells in the optic pathway are ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amacrin​e cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rods an​d cones","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bipola​r cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Horizont​al cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amacrine cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amacrine cells</strong></p><p><strong>Amacrine cells</strong> are the cells that produce purely <strong>depolarizing potential</strong> and <strong>spikes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0662","text":"Most afferent fibers from ​the lateral geniculate bo​dy terminate in the prima‍ry visual cortex in _____​_.","raw_text":"Most afferent fibers from the lateral geniculate body terminate in the primary visual cortex in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lay​er 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Layers ​2 and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lay​er 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Layers ​5 and 6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Layer 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Layer 4</strong></p><p>Most afferent fibers from the <strong>lateral geniculate body</strong> (LGB) terminate in the primary visual cortex <strong>in layer 4.</strong></p>\n<p>Both the <strong>magnocellular</strong> and <strong>parvocellular pathways</strong> from the LGB terminate in <strong>layer 4</strong> of the visual cortex. Only the <strong>interlaminar pathway</strong> terminates in <strong>layers 2 and 3</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6698","text":"What is the mean value for​ outflow facility of the ​aqueous in the normal eye‍?","raw_text":"What is the mean value for outflow facility of the aqueous in the normal eye?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.3 µL/m​in/mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.06 µL/​min/mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.8 µL/m​in/mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.28 µL/​min/mm Hg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.28 µL/min/mm Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.28 µL/min/mm Hg</strong></p><p>The aqueous <strong>outflow facility</strong> is about <strong>0.28</strong> µL/min/mmHg. Aqueous humor is <strong>produced</strong> at an average rate of <strong>2.0–2.5</strong> µL/min.</p>\n<p>The ease with which the aqueous humor leaves the eye is called an aqueous outflow facility.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The outflow facility is measured using <strong>tonography.</strong></li>\n<li>The normal range is between 0.22–0.28 µL/min/mmHg.</li>\n<li>A juxtacanalicular portion of the trabecular meshwork is the primary limiting factor for the outflow facility.</li>\n<li><strong>Uveoscleral outflow</strong> accounts for <strong>10%</strong> of the total aqueous outflow facility.</li>\n<li><strong>Aqueous</strong> outflow facility <strong>decreases</strong> with <strong>aging.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Trabecular</strong> outflow facility is <strong>reduced</strong> in <strong>glaucoma.</strong></li>\n<li>Laser trabeculoplasty reduces intraocular pressure (IOP) in open-angle glaucoma by increasing the trabecular outflow facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"24jyz1Un","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"2_complications_of_fracture","title":"2.Complications Of Fracture","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA4326","text":"A 7-year-old boy is brough‌t to casualty following a‍ fall on an outstretched ​hand. He has swelling and‌ pain on his left elbow. ​X-ray done is given below‌. Which of the following ‌vessels is most likely to​ be injured?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to casualty following a fall on an outstretched hand. He has swelling and pain on his left elbow. X-ray done is given below. Which of the following vessels is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brachia‌l artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar ‌artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Brachial artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9e5934fa68f34d0d9f20c9cfc1f99273.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Brachial artery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray is suggestive of <strong>supracondylar fracture </strong>of the<strong> humerus</strong> in which <strong>brachial artery</strong> is most commonly injured.</p>\n<p>When there is vascular injury, the patient may complain of <strong>paraesthesia</strong> or <strong>numbness </strong>distal to the injury. On examination, the injured limb will be pale or slightly cyanosed, cold, and the <strong>pulse </strong>will be <strong>weak or absent</strong>. </p>\n<p>If a vascular injury is suspected, an <strong>angiogram </strong>or<strong> duplex scan </strong>is done immediately. If the test detects vascular injury, emergency treatment must be initiated without much delay.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4328","text":"Which of the following com‌bination of skeletal trau‍ma - nerve injury is wron​gly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following combination of skeletal trauma - nerve injury is wrongly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wrist injury- Anterior int‌erosseous nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture humerus- Radial n‌erve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monteggia fracture- Poster‌ior interosseous nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Knee dislocation- Common P‌eroneal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wrist injury- Anterior interosseous nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wrist injury- Anterior interosseous nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Wrist injury</strong> is associated with damage to the <strong>median nerve,</strong> not the anterior interosseous nerve.</p>\n<p>Nerve injuries can be associated with closed fractures or open fractures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Closed fractures</strong> are<strong> less</strong> commonly related to nerve injury and spontaneous recovery within 4 months is observed in 90% cases. The nerve is explored if recovery is delayed beyond the expected time, or when nerve conduction studies do not demonstrate any evidence of recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Open fractures</strong> are <strong>more</strong> likely to cause the nerve to be completely severed. The nerve is<strong> explored</strong> and <strong>repaired</strong> at the time of debridement or during wound closure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5679","text":"Which is the most common f‌racture associated with a‍cute compartment syndrome​ in adult patients?","raw_text":"Which is the most common fracture associated with acute compartment syndrome in adult patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femur f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forearm ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibial sha‌ft fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Humerus ‌fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tibial shaft fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tibial shaft fracture</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common fracture</strong> causing<strong> acute compartment syndrome </strong>in adult patients is<strong> tibial </strong>diaphyseal fractures.</p>\n<p><strong>Fractures </strong>are the<strong> most common </strong>cause<strong> </strong>for acute compartment syndrome. The top 3 fracture sites associated with acute compartment syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tibial diaphysis (shaft) </li>\n<li>Distal radius</li>\n<li>Forearm bones diaphysis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Soft tissue injury</strong> is the <strong>second </strong>most common cause of acute compartment syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5723","text":"The earliest symptom of ac‌ute compartment syndrome ‍is _____________","raw_text":"The earliest symptom of acute compartment syndrome is _____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pa‌in","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pal‌lor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pares‌thesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Para‌lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pain</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest symptom</strong> of <strong>acute compartment syndrome</strong> is <strong>pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute compartment syndrome is a<strong> surgical emergency</strong> caused by<strong> increased pressure</strong> within a <strong>fascial compartment</strong>, leading to<strong> tissue ischemia</strong>. It is characterized by rapid progression of pain and swelling in an extremity, commonly precipitated by <strong>traumatic injury</strong>. </p>\n<p>The classic features of ischemia in acute compartment syndrome include the 5 P's:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pain</strong> - It is the<strong> first symptom</strong> of acute compartment syndrome. Ischemic muscle is highly sensitive to touch. On <strong>passive stretching</strong> of the muscles, <strong>severe pain</strong> can be elicited. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Paresthesia</strong> - It is the<strong> first sign</strong> of nerve ischemia.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pallor</strong> - It may not be seen always. The skin outside the compartment may be normally perfused, as only the intra-compartmental perfusion may be affected.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Paralysis</strong> - Paralysis of muscle groups affected in that compartment is a <strong>late sign</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pulselessness</strong> - Usually, the peripheral pulses and capillary return are intact in the early stages, unless there is a major arterial injury. <strong>If pulses are absent, arteriography is indicated</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diagnosis can be confirmed by measuring the <strong>intra-compartmental pressure</strong> using a split catheter introduced into the compartment. The difference between diastolic pressure and compartment pressure is known as differential pressure (ΔP). A <strong>differential pressure (ΔP)</strong> of<strong> less than 30 mm Hg</strong> (4.00 kilopascals) is an <strong>indication</strong> for immediate compartment decompression by performing a <strong>fasciotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5769","text":"A construction worker got ‌entrapped in a collapsed ‍building. He sustained se​vere injury over his legs‌. Which of the following ​feature is unlikely to be‌ seen?","raw_text":"A construction worker got entrapped in a collapsed building. He sustained severe injury over his legs. Which of the following feature is unlikely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic‌ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute tubul‌ar necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coagul‌opathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>crush syndrome.</strong> In this condition<strong> hyperkalemia</strong> is seen, not hypokalemia.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Crush syndrome can result when a <strong>limb is compressed</strong> for a prolonged period. For e.g. getting crushed or entrapped in a vehicle or rubble and after extended use of a pneumatic antishock garment. This results in hypoperfusion and <strong>myonecrosis</strong> in the crushed limb. When the limb is released from the compression, toxic metabolites get released to cause reperfusion injury. </p>\n<p>Crush syndrome can result in a <strong>systemic reaction</strong> due to tissue necrosis. Free myoglobin can lead to <strong>acute tubular necrosis</strong> and renal failure. Myonecrosis can lead to <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong> with <strong>hyperkalaemia</strong> and <strong>hypocalcaemia</strong>. The procoagulants escaped into the systemic circulation can produce systemic <strong>coagulopathy</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Management of crush syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Encouraging high urine flow, along with<strong> alkalization of urine</strong> with sodium bicarbonate to prevent precipitation of myoglobin in the renal tubules</li>\n<li>Renal hemofiltration if there is oliguria or renal failure</li>\n<li>If compartment syndrome is confirmed, then <strong>fasciotomy</strong> is performed</li>\n<li>Radical<strong> excision</strong> of necrosed muscle to avoid sepsis</li>\n<li>Aggressive management of the open wound</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5721","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding reperfu‍sion injury is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding reperfusion injury is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It may follow fasciotomy i‌n compartment syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is only a localised inf‌lammatory response","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can cause muscle damage‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can trigger systemic co‌agulopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is only a localised inflammatory response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is only a localised inflammatory response</strong></p><p><strong>Reperfusion</strong> injury is not always local; it can also result in a systemic inflammatory response. </p>\n<p>Reperfusion injury is the term used for a group of <strong>complications</strong> that follow the <strong>re-establishment </strong>of blood flow to <strong>ischemic tissues</strong>. It can also occur <strong>after fasciotomy</strong> and following restoration of blood flow to muscles in <strong>acute compartment syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Reperfusion results in an inflammatory response in the ischemic tissue, which is probably triggered by muscle breakdown products. These activate the <strong>intrinsic clotting system</strong> resulting in <strong>microvascular thrombosis</strong>. When a large amount of muscle is ischemic, there can be a <strong>systemic inflammatory response </strong>and <strong>systemic coagulopathy</strong>. The mediators released into systemic circulation damage the vascular endothelium, leading to increased permeability, transcapillary fluid leakage, worsening of intra-compartmental pressure, and eventually <strong>multiple organ failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9501","text":"A patient came to the casu‌alty after a RTA with fra‍cture of femur. On 3rd da​y of admission, he develo‌ped sudden breathlessness​. Which of the following ‌is the most common cause?‌","raw_text":"A patient came to the casualty after a RTA with fracture of femur. On 3rd day of admission, he developed sudden breathlessness. Which of the following is the most common cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post tr‌auma MI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fat em‌bolism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fat embolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fat embolism</strong></p><p>History of <strong>sudden onset breathlessness</strong> on the<strong> 3rd day</strong> of admission after femur fracture should raise suspicion of<strong> fat embolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Fat embolism is the presence of fat globules in vital organs and peripheral circulation after a fracture of the long bone or other major trauma. Its pathogenesis can be related to 2 factors: the <strong>release of free fatty acids</strong> by the action of lipases on the neutral fats and the <strong>actual obstruction</strong> of small pulmonary vessels by fat globules.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Early signs</strong> are <strong>fever </strong>and<strong> tachycardia</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Presenting features are of 2 types -\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Cerebral</strong> type<strong> -</strong> drowsiness, restlessness, disorientation</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Pulmonary</strong> type<strong> -</strong> tachypnoea, tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Classical<strong> triad </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li> <strong>Respiratory distress</strong></li>\n<li> <strong>Neurological</strong> abnormalities<strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong> Petechial</strong> hemorrhages</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pathognomonic signs</strong> are <strong>petechiae on the trunk, </strong>axillae, conjunctival folds, and retina.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>of fat embolism is mainly supportive as given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory support with oxygen</li>\n<li>Heparinization increases lipoprotein lipase activity</li>\n<li>Intravenous low molecular weight dextran</li>\n<li>Corticosteroids to avoid pneumonitis</li>\n<li>Prompt stabilization of long-bone fractures </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5767","text":"Which of the following is ‌the earliest muscle to be‍ involved in Volkmann’s i​schemic contracture of th‌e forearm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest muscle to be involved in Volkmann’s ischemic contracture of the forearm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum superfici‌alis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor carpi radialis br‌evis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor Car‌pi Radialis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flexor digitorum profundus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35cec9af569d44ffb2abb8d9714e70c2x1280x1171.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b514e934fac46b489544f38ff08806ex1280x1203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flexor digitorum profundus</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest muscle</strong> to be involved in <strong>Volkmann's ischemic contracture</strong> is the <strong>flexor digitorum profundus.</strong></p>\n<p>Ischemic contracture of muscles affected by arterial injury or compartment syndrome is known as Volkmann’s ischaemic contracture. In the forearm, it is most commonly seen following <strong>supracondylar humeral fractures.</strong>  </p>\n<p>The <strong>classical clinical picture</strong> of <strong>established Volkmann contracture</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elbow flexion</li>\n<li>Forearm pronation</li>\n<li>Wrist flexion</li>\n<li>Thumb adduction</li>\n<li>Metacarpophalangeal joint extension</li>\n<li>Finger flexion</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mild </strong>deformities - passive stretching of the contracted muscles with a turn-buckle splint (<strong>Volkmann’s splint</strong>)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Moderate </strong>deformities - soft tissue sliding procedures (<strong>Maxpage operation</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Severe </strong>deformities - early excision of all necrotic muscles, combined with complete median and ulnar neurolysis. This is followed by a secondary procedure of tendon transfers to restore function. If motor activity is not present in these tendons, a free innervated Gracilis muscle transfer may be considered.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5762","text":"An 80-year-old man develop‌ed right-sided calf muscl‍e pain and swelling. He w​as bedridden following a ‌right inter-trochanteric ​fracture. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old man developed right-sided calf muscle pain and swelling. He was bedridden following a right inter-trochanteric fracture. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic os‌teomyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compartmen‌t syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep vein ‌thrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial c‌laudication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deep vein thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deep vein thrombosis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> deep vein thrombosis</strong>. It occurs due to immobilization and venous stasis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It <strong>most commonly</strong> occurs in the <strong>calf veins</strong> and less often in the proximal veins of the thigh and pelvis. It is caused due to <strong>Virchow's triad</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hemodynamic changes (stasis or turbulence)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hypercoagulabilty </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Endothelial injury/dysfunction</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Some patients may present with pain in the calf or thigh. There may be an increase in temperature and pulse rate. <strong>Calf swelling</strong> and tenderness may be noted. Increased pain on passive dorsiflexion of the foot (<strong>Homan's test</strong>), although frequently used is now regarded as an <strong>unreliable sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5764","text":"A patient was treated with‌ open reduction and inter‍nal fixation for humerus ​fracture 9 months ago. Hi‌s recent X-ray is given b​elow. Which of the follow‌ing is not a risk factor ‌for this condition?","raw_text":"A patient was treated with open reduction and internal fixation for humerus fracture 9 months ago. His recent X-ray is given below. Which of the following is not a risk factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smo‌king","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone‌ loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anatomical‌ reduction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anatomical reduction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd2bcbc9bba941fca030a33819cf1424.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anatomical reduction</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray are suggestive of<strong> non-union</strong>. The <strong>anatomical reduction</strong> provides <strong>stability</strong> and decreases the probability of non-union.</p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Nonunion</strong> is</span><span class=\"fontstyle2\"> when a fracture has failed to heal within the expected time and is <strong>unlikely to heal</strong> without a new intervention. The US Federal Drug Administration (<strong>USFDA)</strong> defines non-union as <strong>failure</strong> to achieve <strong>union</strong> by <strong>9 months since</strong> the injury, and for which there have been <strong>no</strong> <strong>signs of healing</strong> for <strong>3 months</strong>. </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><strong>Improper immobilization</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of non-union. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><strong>Delayed union</strong> is when a fracture has failed to heal within the expected time but has the<strong> potential to heal</strong> without further intervention. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><strong>Risk factors </strong>for <strong>non-union</strong> include the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Host related factors -</span></strong>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Advanced age</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Malnutrition</span></li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Smoking</span></strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Endocrine diseases (<strong>diabetes</strong>)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Immunosuppression</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Osteoporosis</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Vascular diseases</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Taking medications that delay bone healing</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><strong>Fracture related factors -</strong></span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">High energy fractures</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Greater degree of soft tissue injury</span></li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Bone loss</span></strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Periosteal stripping</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Devascularization</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Infection/contamination</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><strong>Treatment related factors -</strong></span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Lack of good stabilization</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Following improper technique such as poor casting</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Rigid fixation with bone gaps</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\"> NSAIDs (implicated in some studies)</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5770","text":"Which of the following fra‌ctures is most prone to n‍on-union?","raw_text":"Which of the following fractures is most prone to non-union?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal‌ scaphoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inter-tro‌chanteric","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal‌ radius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olecranon‌ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal scaphoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2520229187d3427a8a6be8ea31752dafx1280x1481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal scaphoid</strong></p><p>Among the given fractures, <strong>proximal scaphoid fractures</strong><strong> </strong>are most prone to<strong> non-union. </strong></p>\n<p>Since the nutrient artery enters the scaphoid near its distal end, proximal fractures are associated with <strong>avascular necrosis</strong> and <strong>non-union</strong>. The <strong>more proximal</strong> the fracture in the scaphoid, the <strong>higher</strong> the chances of avascular necrosis and non-union.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Certain bones have a relatively limited or watershed blood supply that can potentially get interrupted by fracture. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Head of the femur</strong>: after fracture of the neck or posterior dislocation of the hip.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Proximal scaphoid:</strong> due to fracture through the waist of the scaphoid.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Lunate</strong>: following a dislocation.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Body of talus</strong>: after fracture of its neck.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fractures in these sites have a <strong>higher risk for non-union </strong>or development of osteonecrosis.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Inter-trochanteric</strong> fractures involve either the greater or lesser trochanters or both, more common in the elderly following a history of a fall. <strong>Malunion</strong> is the most frequent complication. The vast metaphyseal region has an abundant blood supply, contributing to a <strong>higher union rate</strong> and fewer chances of avascular necrosis. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Distal radius</strong> fractures occur by fall on an outstretched hand and they include Colles' fracture (most common), Smith's fracture, Barton's fracture, and Chauffer's fracture. The complications include joint stiffness, <strong>malunion (more common)</strong>, carpal tunnel syndrome, subluxation of the inferior radio-ulnar joint, Sudeck's dystrophy, and rupture of the extensor pollicis longus tendon. Avascular necrosis or non-union is rare.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Olecranon</strong> fracture results from a direct injury as in a fall onto the point of the elbow. Complications include <strong>non-union</strong>, elbow stiffness, and osteoarthritis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4398","text":"An X-ray of a patient with‌ a history of tibial frac‍ture 9-months back is giv​en below. Which type of n‌on-union is he suffering ​from?","raw_text":"An X-ray of a patient with a history of tibial fracture 9-months back is given below. Which type of non-union is he suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‌rophic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oligot‌rophic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atro‌phic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudoa‌rthrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertrophic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c8ea2a9ecdc48a1abe917c454a4c1be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c148168816b84daaa657552e1883cd90x1280x1307.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ac82b4a11764c7abfc46d556dbd49bdx1280x1559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertrophic</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>hypertrophic</strong> <strong>non-union</strong> of the tibia.</p>\n<p>Although the enlarged fracture ends suggest active osteogenesis, the gap is not bridged because of <strong>inadequate stability</strong>. Such non-unions have adequate vascularity and display abundant callus.</p>\n<p>Based on the<strong> biologic potential</strong> for fracture healing, non-union can be of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrophic</strong>:<strong> Poor healing</strong> response with <strong>no callus</strong> formation due to little or no active bone-forming cells at the fracture site. The blood supply is typically poor. On radiography, there is an absence of bone reaction. </li>\n<li><strong>Oligotrophic</strong>: There is <strong>minimal callus</strong> formation, often due to inadequate approximation of fracture surfaces. On radiography, there is <strong>minimal</strong> bone <strong>healing</strong> reaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertrophic</strong>:<strong> </strong>These fractures have an <strong>adequate healing</strong> response and good vascularity. There is a <strong>lack</strong> of adequate <strong>stability</strong> for the union to occur. On radiography, there is usually <strong>abundant callus</strong> formation with an intervening dark area of fibrocartilage-lacking mineral. It can occur after initial nonoperative management or in some cases after initial operative management where there was inadequate stabilization. </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Pseudoarthrosis </strong>has features of hypertrophic nonunion, but due to <strong>excessive</strong> and <strong>chronic motility</strong>, there is the formation of a pseudo-capsule containing fluid similar to an actual synovial joint.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5765","text":"A patient presented with n‌on-union of tibial fractu‍re. The orthopedician sug​gested treatment by provi‌ding stability using a ca​st or brace. Which of the‌ following type of nonuni‌on does the patient most ​likely has?","raw_text":"A patient presented with non-union of tibial fracture. The orthopedician suggested treatment by providing stability using a cast or brace. Which of the following type of nonunion does the patient most likely has?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‌rophic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atro‌phic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligot‌rophic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudoa‌rthrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertrophic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertrophic</strong></p><p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Stabilization</strong> using casts and braces are used in the treatment of <strong>hypertrophic nonunion</strong>.  </span></p>\n<p>Management of nonunion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Hypertrophic </span></strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\">nonunion</span><span class=\"fontstyle0\">: </span><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Stabilization</strong> to </span>allow chondrocyte-mediated mineralization of the fibrocartilage between fracture ends. With stabilization, healing usually occurs by 8 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Oligotrophic </strong>nonunion: Correcting the issues related to bone contact through <strong>mechanical compression</strong> or <strong>bone grafting</strong> to close the defects. In some cases, a combination of biologic and mechanical methods is used.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrophic </strong>nonunion:<strong> Autogenous bone graft or BMP</strong> to provide biologic stimulus at the fracture site. The other procedure done is <strong>debridement</strong> or excision of the non-vital bone ends. </li>\n<li><strong>Pseudoarthrosis</strong>:<strong> Debridement</strong> of the pseudoarthrosis, opening of the medullary canal and providing more <strong>stability</strong> usually with <strong>compression</strong> at the nonunion site. In some cases, biologic stimulation may be helpful to promote healing.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5766","text":"What is the most appropria‌te treatment for atrophic‍ non-union?","raw_text":"What is the most appropriate treatment for atrophic non-union?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Applicati‌on of cast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"External‌ fixation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stabilization and bone gra‌fting","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Internal‌ fixation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stabilization and bone grafting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ec474401c7d4ccd8b8d8c9f1336f867x512x842.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stabilization and bone grafting</strong></p><p><strong>Atrophic nonunions</strong> require <strong>bone grafting </strong>and<strong> stabilization</strong> in order to heal.</p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Autogenous bone graft</strong> remains the standard graft substance used in the repair of atrophic nonunions. Bone grafts are also used in some cases of oligotrophic nonunions and pseudoarthroses. The <strong>anterior iliac crest</strong> is the <strong>most common site</strong> from which grafts are obtained followed by the fibula. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">The below X-ray image shows atrophic nonunion of humeral shaft 18 months after ORIF. </span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4400","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a common strategy use‍d in the treatment of pat​ients with infected non-u‌nions?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a common strategy used in the treatment of patients with infected non-unions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ilizarov ‌technique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PMMA antib‌iotic beads","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal‌ fixation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Parenteral ‌antibiotics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Internal fixation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Internal fixation</strong></p><p>Internal fixation is not a common strategy used in the treatment of infected non-unions. </p>\n<p>This is because<strong> metal implants</strong> promote adherence of microbes and cause <strong>biofilm formation</strong>. They also adversely affect phagocytosis and control of infection. The <strong>diagnostic gold standard</strong> for detecting<strong> infection</strong> in fracture nonunion sites is <strong>deep tissue</strong> or<strong> bone culture</strong>. </p>\n<p>Strategies used in the <strong>management of infected non-unions</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Removal of internal implants</strong> if the infection is problematic or when bone grafting is required. Implants are retained if the infection can be controlled and union can be achieved without grafting procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Management of the infectious process </strong>by debridement of the infected nonviable bone and surrounding nonviable soft tissues, <strong>parenteral antibiotics</strong>, and bone stabilization.</li>\n<li><strong>Local antibiotic delivery</strong> may be performed, especially when there is <strong>dead space</strong> due to <strong>debridement</strong>. Antibiotic-impregnated synthetic materials are used, such as polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA) cement beads.</li>\n<li><strong>Cancellous autograft with antibiotics</strong> (vancomycin and gentamycin).</li>\n<li><strong>Stabilization</strong> with an <strong>Ilizarov fixator</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4315","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen‌ts with painful restricti‍on of movements of her le​ft hip. She had a history‌ of a fall a few months b​ack which she ignored. A ‌recent radiograph is give‌n below. What is the most​ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presents with painful restriction of movements of her left hip. She had a history of a fall a few months back which she ignored. A recent radiograph is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Slipped capital femoral ep‌iphysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrous ‌ankylosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avascular‌ necrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Avascular necrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c2ab527323bb484ab6a8dd63af41cd13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39e1de935c5549cc85f301053a52d2cax1280x1354.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Avascular necrosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and x-ray showing <strong>flattening of the femoral head</strong> is suggestive of <strong>avascular necrosis</strong>. It is most commonly seen after a fracture neck of femur. </p>\n<p>The patient complains of pain and walks with a limp. The condition is treated by a<strong> total hip replacement</strong>. If untreated, osteoarthritis of the hip develops.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>crescent sign,</strong> which is caused by <strong>the necrotic and repair processes</strong> that occur during avascular necrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4320","text":"A 20-year-old man presents‌ with pain and swelling o‍f elbow. He had a past hi​story of fracture of radi‌al head which was surgica​lly treated. X-ray done i‌s given below. Which of t‌he following is true abou​t this condition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presents with pain and swelling of elbow. He had a past history of fracture of radial head which was surgically treated. X-ray done is given below. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caused due to immobilisati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Initial management is by s‌urgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a malignant conditio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indomethacin prevents recu‌rrence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Indomethacin prevents recurrence","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12b4b2a7954e4197b00617914b7c727b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80bf824d1eb24cc0adc1c3475237ec51x1280x973.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Indomethacin prevents recurrence</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray is suggestive of <strong>myositis ossificans </strong>which is a complication of fracture. Recurrence of this condition can be prevented by <strong>indomethacin</strong> and <strong>radiotherapy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Myositis ossificans is defined as <strong>ectopic benign</strong> pathological bone formation. It is most commonly seen after dislocation of elbow or a blow to brachialis, deltoid or quadriceps. The patient presents with gross restriction of range of movements and should be <strong>advised not to massage</strong> the site as it promotes ossification.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves joint rest in the position of function until the pain subsides, followed by gentle movements. After a few months if the pain and restriction still persist then the bony mass is excised.</p>\n<p>The X-ray given below shows myositis ossificans.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6711","text":"Which of the following sub‌stances is used for bone ‍formation in a patient wi​th non-union?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances is used for bone formation in a patient with non-union?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PM‌MA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B‌MP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ca ‌PO4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ca‌SO4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BMP</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> bone morphogenic protein</strong> (BMP) is the substance used for <strong>bone formation </strong>in a patient with <strong>non-union</strong>.</p>\n<p>BMP is a <strong>bone-stimulating protein</strong> used as a bone graft in the treatment of <strong>non-union </strong>and <strong>open tibial fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Bone grafts</strong> can be: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Osteoinductive</strong>: They <strong>stimulate osteogenesis </strong>through the differentiation of mesenchymal cells into osteoprogenitor cells. <strong>BMPs </strong>are osteoinductive.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Osteoconductive</strong>: They <strong>provide linkage across defects</strong> and a scaffold upon which new bone can form. <strong>Calcium phosphate</strong> (Option C), <strong>calcium</strong> <strong>hydroxyapatite</strong>, and <strong>calcium sulfate </strong>(Option D) are osteoconductive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Polymethylmethacrylate</strong> (PMMA), also known as <strong>bone cement</strong>, holds bone and metal implants together by forming an interlocking network between the irregularities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OtvgJTwS","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"2_disorders_of_newborn","title":"2.Disorders Of Newborn","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA4201","text":"A male infant born at 34 w‍eeks of gestation by emer​gency LSCS was found to h‌ave the following finding‌ on histopathological exa‍mination of his umbilical‌ cord. Anomalies of which‌ of the following systems​ are most likely to be fo​und in this infant?","raw_text":"A male infant born at 34 weeks of gestation by emergency LSCS was found to have the following finding on histopathological examination of his umbilical cord. Anomalies of which of the following systems are most likely to be found in this infant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal ‍system","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Central ner‍vous system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastro-intes‍tinal system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endocrin‍e system","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal system","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a420f2e6e164a8896b87fcd0a1cadc3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10827f5c9c9c4555b6b49ad310524ff4x1280x1530.0423131170662.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal system</strong></p><p>The image shows the presence of a <strong> single umbilical artery</strong> which is associated with an increased risk for an <strong>occult</strong> <strong>renal anomaly.</strong></p>\n<p>The histopathological image of the cross-section of the umbilical cord shows 2 vessels as marked, a <strong>thin-walled</strong> umbilical<strong> vein</strong> and a <strong>thicker-walled</strong> umbilical<strong> artery.</strong> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>normal umbilical cord</strong> contains <strong>2 arteries</strong> and <strong>1 vein</strong>. A single umbilical artery is the <strong>most common cord abnormality</strong>. Anomalies are seen in <strong>30% of cases</strong> with a single umbilical artery. The incidence is <strong>higher</strong> in <strong>twin deliveries</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Trisomy 18</strong> is a common association.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4208","text":"On a particularly busy day‍ in the labor room, you a​re requested by the PG to‌ take anthropometric meas‌urements and calculate th‍e ponderal index of a bab‌y while she attends to an‌other baby. You find the ​ponderal index to be 1.45​. Which of the following ‌organs is unlikely to be ‍affected in this neonate?​","raw_text":"On a particularly busy day in the labor room, you are requested by the PG to take anthropometric measurements and calculate the ponderal index of a baby while she attends to another baby. You find the ponderal index to be 1.45. Which of the following organs is unlikely to be affected in this neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subcutan‍eous fat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mus‍cle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‍ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br‍ain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brain</strong></p><p>A <strong>ponderal index</strong> of <strong>&lt;2</strong> suggests asymmetric intrauterine growth restriction. <strong>Asymmetric</strong> <strong>IUGR</strong> is associated with a<strong> brain-sparing effect,</strong> unlike symmetric IUGR, where there is no sparing of head growth.</p>\n<p>Ponderal index = Birth weight (gm)/Length (cm)<sup>3</sup> x 100.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>weight-height</strong>-related parameter used as an indicator of<strong> fetal growth status</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 400px;\" width=\"468\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19.6562px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 227.344px; text-align: center; height: 19.6562px;\"><strong>Symmetric</strong> <strong>IUGR </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.656px; text-align: center; height: 19.6562px;\"><strong>Asymmetric IUGR</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 227.344px; height: 18px;\">Insult on fetal growth occurs <strong>early</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.656px; height: 18px;\">Insult occurs in <strong>late gestation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 227.344px; height: 18px;\">Head circumference, length, and weight are <strong>equally</strong> affected</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.656px; height: 18px;\">Relative <strong>sparing</strong> of<strong> brain growth</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 227.344px; height: 18px;\">A ponderal index <strong>greater than</strong> <strong>or equal to</strong> <strong>2 </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.656px; height: 18px;\">A ponderal index <strong>less than 2</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 227.344px; height: 18px;\">Caused by genetic, chromosomal disorders, or TORCH infections</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.656px; height: 18px;\">Caused by placental insufficiency, pregnancy-induced hypertension, or maternal medical diseases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4202","text":"The ponderal index of a te‍rm baby delivered by elec​tive cesarean section is ‌1.75. Which of the follow‌ing is unlikely to be ass‍ociated?","raw_text":"The ponderal index of a term baby delivered by elective cesarean section is 1.75. Which of the following is unlikely to be associated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gestationa‍l diabetes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes with vasculopathy‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert‍ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sickle ce‍ll anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gestational diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gestational diabetes</strong></p><p>A ponderal index of 1.75 suggests <strong>intrauterine growth restriction.</strong> <strong>Gestational diabetes</strong> usually presents with<strong> fetal macrosomia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Maternal factors</strong> associated with intrauterine growth restriction <strong>(IUGR)</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension/renal disease</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes with placental vasculopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoxemia</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Chronic illness</li>\n<li>Sickle cell anemia</li>\n<li>Drugs (narcotics, <strong>alcohol, cigarettes</strong>, cocaine)</li>\n<li>Maternal infection and parasite infestations</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4215","text":"An unbooked primigravida, ‍with no antenatal records​, delivers a 2.5kg male b‌aby by normal delivery. W‌hile ascertaining the mat‍urity of the child, which‌ of the following would b‌e against a diagnosis of ​prematurity?","raw_text":"An unbooked primigravida, with no antenatal records, delivers a 2.5kg male baby by normal delivery. While ascertaining the maturity of the child, which of the following would be against a diagnosis of prematurity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No crease‍s on sole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insignificant breast nodul‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thick ear‍ cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Empty ‍scrotum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thick ear cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7149845493384a3e8793e0ac4363ff1ax1280x1758.420310296192.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thick ear cartilage</strong></p><p><span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"0\"><strong>Thick cartilage</strong></span> with a<span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"2\"> <strong>stiff ear</strong></span> in a newborn is a sign of <strong>maturity</strong> (not prematurity).</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Score</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Ear</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Pinna flat and stays folded; no recoil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Slightly curved pinna; soft; slow recoil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Well-curved pinna; soft but ready recoil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Formed &amp; firm, instant recoil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Thick cartilage, ear stiff</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>No creases on the sole, insignificant breast nodule, and empty scrotum are all physical signs of prematurity in a newborn.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Abundant lanugo hair is not discriminatory evidence of prematurity. This is because neonates of diabetic mothers born as a term or even post-term have abundant lanugo hair, especially over the pinna and upper back.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9699","text":"A lady with wrong dates an‍d a corrected gestational​ age of 43 weeks presents‌ with labor pains. The CT‌G suggests placental insu‍fficiency, and she is tak‌en up for emergency LSCS.‌ Which of the following p​hysical signs is unlikely​ to be seen in this neona‌te?","raw_text":"A lady with wrong dates and a corrected gestational age of 43 weeks presents with labor pains. The CTG suggests placental insufficiency, and she is taken up for emergency LSCS. Which of the following physical signs is unlikely to be seen in this neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long ‍nails","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pale‍ skin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alert‍ faces","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scant‍y hair","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Scanty hair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Scanty hair</strong></p><p>The physical sign of an infant born <strong>post-term</strong> (43 weeks) in association with presumed <strong>placental insufficiency</strong> is <strong>abundant hair</strong> (not scanty hair).</p>\n<p>Infants born post-term in association with presumed placental insufficiency may have various physical signs, together termed as <strong>postmaturity syndrome</strong> (or <strong>dysmaturity syndrome</strong>).</p>\n<p>These include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Desquamation, pale skin, and long nails.</li>\n<li>Loose skin around the thighs and buttocks.</li>\n<li>Meconium-stained nails, skin, vernix, and umbilical cord.</li>\n<li>Presence of placental membranes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4243","text":"A 34-year-old lady with an‍ obstetric score of G3P2L​2 delivers a baby boy at ‌43 weeks + 2 days period ‌of gestation. Which of th‍e following are unlikely ‌to be associated in the g‌iven scenario?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old lady with an obstetric score of G3P2L2 delivers a baby boy at 43 weeks + 2 days period of gestation. Which of the following are unlikely to be associated in the given scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane‍mia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoca‍lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meconium ‍aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Persistent pulmonary hyper‍tension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anemia</strong></p><p><strong>Anemia</strong> is not a complication of post-maturity. <strong>Polycythemia</strong> may be seen in neonates born after 42 weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of post-maturity include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Perinatal depression</li>\n<li><strong>Meconium aspiration</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Persistent pulmonary hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hypocalcemia</strong></li>\n<li>Polycythemia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4421","text":"Immediately following vagi‍nal delivery, a baby is n​oted to have a diffuse ec‌chymotic swelling on the ‌scalp. What is the most l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Immediately following vaginal delivery, a baby is noted to have a diffuse ecchymotic swelling on the scalp. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalo‍hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caput su‍ccedaneum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subgaleal ‍hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidural‍ hematoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caput succedaneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4ffc8ae42f0403eafd900279644393cx1280x1035.2011291460833.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caput succedaneum</strong></p><p>The <strong>most likely</strong> diagnosis is caput succedaneum.</p>\n<p><strong>Caput succedaneum</strong> is a diffuse, sometimes ecchymotic, edematous collection of serosanguinous fluid in the subcutaneous layer of the scalp, involving the presenting part in a vertex delivery. It may extend across the midline and <strong>crosses suture lines.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cephalohematoma</strong> is a <strong>subperiosteal hemorrhage</strong> that is always <strong>limited</strong> to the surface of 1 cranial bone and does not cross suture lines. The swelling appears only <strong>several hours after birth.</strong> There is <strong>no discoloration</strong> of the overlying scalp.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Subgaleal</strong> hemorrhages occur with the rupture of <strong>emissary veins</strong>, which connect the dural sinuses with the superficial veins of the scalp. Blood accumulates <strong>between the epicranial aponeurosis of the scalp and the periosteum</strong> and resolves over 2-3 weeks. Often associated with vacuum-assisted delivery.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Epidural</strong> hemorrhages,<strong> </strong>usually <strong>traumatic</strong> in origin, are associated with cephalo-pelvic disproportion, prolonged labor, breech, precipitous labors, or occur as a result of mechanical assistance in labor. Majority resolve with no intervention.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4418","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a feature of marking ​B?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of marking B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Swelling of the soft tissu‍es of the scalp","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Does not cross suture line‍s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resorbed within 2 weeks to‍ 3 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Appears only several hours‍ after birth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Swelling of the soft tissues of the scalp","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cef898ca57c841f9b14f311a5aec1c6d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3417712db30f498780cd3e1a4d88fac0x1280x1035.2011291460833.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Swelling of the soft tissues of the scalp</strong></p><p>The area marked B represents a <strong>cephalohematoma. </strong>It is not a swelling of the soft tissues of the scalp.</p>\n<p>Cephalohematoma is a <strong>subperiosteal hemorrhage</strong> that is always limited to the surface of 1 cranial bone and<strong> does not cross suture lines.</strong> The swelling appears only <strong>several hours after birth.</strong> There is <strong>no discoloration</strong> of the overlying scalp.</p>\n<p>Most cephalohematomas are <strong>resorbed within 2 weeks to 3 months,</strong> depending on their size. Cephalohematomas require <strong>no treatment,</strong> although <strong>phototherapy</strong> may be necessary to treat the associated <strong>hyperbilirubinemia </strong>(resorption of blood takes place by the breakdown of RBCs).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Other markings-</p>\n<p>A- Caput succedaneum. It is a diffuse ecchymotic or edematous collection in the soft tissues of the scalp with vertex presentation. It can extend across skull sutures and disappears with the first week of life.</p>\n<p>C- Sub-galeal hemorrhage. This a collection of blood under the aponeurosis of the scalp. It is a large potential space, thus bleeding can be extensive. It occurs as a result of ventouse deliveries due to tears of the emissary veins which connect to the dural venous sinuses. It can also occur in hereditary coagulopathies and in association with skull fractures.</p>\n<p>D- Epidural hemorrhage. Usually traumatic in origin, these are associated with cephalo-pelvic disproportion, prolonged labor, breech, precipitous labors, or as a result of mechanical assistance in labor. Majority resolve with no intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4426","text":"A baby in the postnatal wa‍rd is noted to have paral​ysis of the left hand alo‌ng with ipsilateral ptosi‌s and miosis. What is the‍ most likely cause?","raw_text":"A baby in the postnatal ward is noted to have paralysis of the left hand along with ipsilateral ptosis and miosis. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erb-Duchenn‍e paralysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phrenic n‍erve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Horner ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klumpke's paralysis associ‍ated with Horner syndrome​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Klumpke's paralysis associated with Horner syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ee83561856b4296b866beeba305d208x702x315.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a244a635dcc49b1ab72c983b570f513x1280x1954.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Klumpke&#x27;s paralysis associated with Horner syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Left-hand paralysis</strong> with <strong>ipsilateral ptosis</strong> and <strong>miosis</strong> in a newborn suggests a diagnosis of <strong>Klumpke's paralysis</strong> associated with <strong>Horner syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>There is an injury to the <strong>7th-8th cervical nerves and the 1st thoracic nerve.</strong> This produces a <strong>paralyzed hand</strong> where the intrinsic muscles of the hand (T1) and the ulnar flexors of the wrist and fingers (C8) are mainly affected, and the baby lies with the <strong>forearm supinated</strong> and the <strong>elbow flexed.</strong> There may be a <strong>unilateral Horner’s syndrome,</strong> characterized by ipsilateral ptosis and miosis if the sympathetic fibers of the 1 thoracic root are also injured.</p>\n<p>Option A: Erb–Duchenne paralysis affects the 5th and 6th cervical nerves. There is loss of abduction of the shoulder, external rotation, and supination. Moro's reflex is absent on the affected side.</p>\n<p>Option B: Phrenic nerve palsy (3, 4, and 5 cervical nerves) results in diaphragmatic paralysis. It is characterized by cyanosis and irregular and labored breathing.</p>\n<p>Option C: Horner's syndrome is characterized by miosis, ptosis, enophthalmos, anhydrosis, and loss of ciliospinal reflex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4425","text":"Following a delivery compl‍icated by shoulder dystoc​ia, the baby is noted to ‌have a deformity in the r‌ight upper limb. On inspe‍ction, the orthopedics JR‌ notes an adducted, inter‌nally rotated arm and a p​ronated forearm. Which ne​rve roots are affected he‌re?","raw_text":"Following a delivery complicated by shoulder dystocia, the baby is noted to have a deformity in the right upper limb. On inspection, the orthopedics JR notes an adducted, internally rotated arm and a pronated forearm. Which nerve roots are affected here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‍y C5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C5 a‍nd C6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C6 a‍nd C7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C7 a‍nd C8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C5 and C6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77da5732704845158990986f3c103626x600x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C5 and C6</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette suggests a diagnosis of <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"1\"><strong>Erb–Duchenne paralysis.</strong> T</span>he injury is limited to the <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"3\"><strong>5th</strong> and <strong>6th</strong> </span><strong>cervical nerve roots.</strong></p>\n<p>The characteristic position consists of <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"5\"><strong>adduction</strong> and <strong>internal rotation</strong> of the arm with <strong>pronation</strong> of the <strong>forearm.</strong> </span>There is <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"7\">loss </span>of <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"9\">abduction</span> of the shoulder, <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"11\">external rotation</span>, and <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"13\">supination</span>.</p>\n<p>There is an <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"15\"><strong>asymmetric Moro's</strong> Reflex</span></p>\n<p>Note: <br/>In shoulder dystocia, the most common nerve injury is to the C5-6 nerve root. The most common bone to fracture is the clavicle. <br/><br/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4435","text":"A term infant develops jau‍ndice at day 2 of life. I​t first appeared on the s‌clera and progressed to t‌he trunk by day 4. It com‍pletely disappeared by da‌y 7 following phototherap‌y. Which of the following​ statements is false rega​rding the cause of this p‌attern of jaundice?","raw_text":"A term infant develops jaundice at day 2 of life. It first appeared on the sclera and progressed to the trunk by day 4. It completely disappeared by day 7 following phototherapy. Which of the following statements is false regarding the cause of this pattern of jaundice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to a rise in unconjuga‍ted bilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Due to limitation in the c‍onjugation of bilirubin b​y the immature neonatal l‌iver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Due to Rh incompatibility ‍between maternal and feta​l red blood cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Due to increased breakdown‍ of fetal red blood cells​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Due to Rh incompatibility between maternal and fetal red blood cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6d634be7dbe4413b8d6b92919ff018bx1280x1232.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Due to Rh incompatibility between maternal and fetal red blood cells</strong></p><p>The above pattern of jaundice is suggestive of <strong>physiological jaundice/icterus neonatorum.</strong> It is due to increased bilirubin production from the <strong>breakdown</strong> of <strong>fetal red blood cells</strong> combined with <strong>transient limitation</strong> in the <strong>conjugation</strong> of bilirubin by the immature neonatal liver.</p>\n<p>It first appears on the 2nd or 3rd day, usually peaks on the 4th day at 5–6 mg/dL, and decreases to &lt;2 mg/dL between the 5th and 7th days after birth.</p>\n<p><strong>Rh incompatibility</strong> between maternal and fetal red blood cells leads to <strong>pathological jaundice.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:  </p>\n<p><strong>Depth of jaundice</strong> (degree of yellowness) should be carefully noted (light staining as lemon-yellow; deep staining as orange-yellow), as it is an important indicator of the level of jaundice. Earlier, the involvement of soles and palms indicated severe jaundice but <strong>deep yellow staining (even in absence of yellow soles or palms)</strong> may be associated with severe jaundice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4434","text":"A male infant with a birth‍ weight of 3.2 Kg is admi​tted to the phototherapy ‌unit with a total bilirub‌in of 11mg/dl at 36 hours‍ of life. Which of the fo‌llowing statements does n‌ot describe pathological ​jaundice?","raw_text":"A male infant with a birth weight of 3.2 Kg is admitted to the phototherapy unit with a total bilirubin of 11mg/dl at 36 hours of life. Which of the following statements does not describe pathological jaundice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jaundice appears within fi‍rst 24 hours after birth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jaundice present at or bey‍ond age 3 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jaundice associated with r‍ise in bilirubin level at​ a rate of &amp;lt;5 mg/dl/24‌ hour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Jaundice seen in the mid-a‍bdomen and soles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Jaundice associated with rise in bilirubin level at a rate of <5 mg/dl/24 hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Jaundice associated with rise in bilirubin level at a rate of &lt;5 mg/dl/24 hour</strong></p><p><strong>Jaundice</strong> associated with a rise in bilirubin level at a rate of <strong>&lt;5 mg/dL/24 hours</strong> suggests <strong>physiological jaundice</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features suggestive of <strong>pathological jaundice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jaundice appears within the first 24 hours after birth</li>\n<li>Jaundice present at or beyond age 3 weeks or sick infant</li>\n<li>Elevated direct (or conjugated) bilirubin level</li>\n<li>Total serum bilirubin levels above the expected normal range; jaundice seen in the mid-abdominal region (≈ 15 mg/dL) and soles (≈ 20 mg/dL)</li>\n<li>Total serum bilirubin concentration not responding to phototherapy</li>\n<li>Total serum bilirubin rising rapidly (i.e., crossing percentiles) in an infant receiving phototherapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9739","text":"The peripheral smear of a ‍newborn baby girl sent on​ day 2 of life shows mild‌ hemolysis. What is the m‌ost common cause of this ‍finding in a newborn?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a newborn baby girl sent on day 2 of life shows mild hemolysis. What is the most common cause of this finding in a newborn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rh incomp‍atibility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ABO incom‍patibility","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kell antigen incompatibili‍ty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemolytic diseases due to ‍infections","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ABO incompatibility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ABO incompatibility</strong></p><p><strong>ABO incompatibility</strong> is the most common cause of hemolytic disease of newborns and affects the<strong> firstborn</strong>. It is usually <strong>mild</strong> and <strong>subclinical.</strong></p>\n<p>This incompatibility between the mother and fetus generally <strong>results in milder disease than Rh incompatibility does. </strong></p>\n<p>It arises when a <strong>mother </strong>with <strong>blood group type O</strong> becomes<strong> pregnant </strong>with a fetus of<strong> different blood type (A/B).</strong> The mother's serum contains naturally occurring anti-A and anti-B, which can cross the placenta and cause hemolysis of fetal RBCs.</p>\n<p>Although antibodies against A and B factors occur without previous immunization (natural antibodies), they are usually IgM, which do not cross the placenta. However, <strong>IgG</strong> antibodies to<strong> A antigen </strong>may be present, and these do<strong> cross</strong> the<strong> placenta, </strong>resulting in <strong>A-O iso-immune hemolytic disease</strong> in the first-born infant (A-O is more common than B-O).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9746","text":"A 3.3 Kg term neonate is a‍dmitted on day 3 of life ​with yellowish discolorat‌ion of the skin and is go‌ing to undergo photothera‍py. What is the most effe‌ctive wavelength of light‌ used for treatment?","raw_text":"A 3.3 Kg term neonate is admitted on day 3 of life with yellowish discoloration of the skin and is going to undergo phototherapy. What is the most effective wavelength of light used for treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"370 to‍ 390 nm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"390 to‍ 420 nm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"460 to‍ 490 nm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"410 to‍ 450 nm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 460 to 490 nm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d08227b6a30a45cbac359d780696b8a7x1280x612.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 460 to 490 nm</strong></p><p>The most effective range of <strong>wavelength of light</strong> for phototherapy in <strong>neonatal indirect hyperbilirubinemia</strong> is <strong>460–490 nm (blue-green light).</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical jaundice and indirect hyperbilirubinemia are reduced by exposure to the high intensity of light in the visible spectrum by 3 mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Irreversible</strong> <strong>structural isomerization:</strong> Most <strong>important</strong> mechanism; acts by forming <strong>lumirubin</strong> from native bilirubin. It is then excreted by the <strong>kidneys</strong> in the unconjugated state.</li>\n<li><strong>Reversible photo-isomerization reaction:</strong> converts toxic unconjugated bilirubin into an unconjugated configurational isomer. It is then excreted in <strong>bile</strong> without conjugation.</li>\n<li><strong>Photo-oxidation</strong>: minor method</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9758","text":"A mother noticed yellowish‍ discoloration of her ter​m newborn within the firs‌t 6 hours of life. On exa‌mination, jaundice extend‍ed up to the lower abdome‌n and thighs. Which of th‌e following drugs can be ​administered along with p​hototherapy?","raw_text":"A mother noticed yellowish discoloration of her term newborn within the first 6 hours of life. On examination, jaundice extended up to the lower abdomen and thighs. Which of the following drugs can be administered along with phototherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sn Mesop‍orphyrin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tolaz‍oline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tromet‍hamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suc‍rose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sn Mesoporphyrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sn Mesoporphyrin</strong></p><p><strong>Sn-mesoporphyrin (SnMP)</strong>, a synthetic <strong>metalloporphyrin</strong> is a potential alternative medical therapy for pathological jaundice in neonates.</p>\n<p>It acts by <strong>competitive inhibition</strong> of the <strong>conversion of heme protein to biliverdin</strong> by heme oxygenase. A single intramuscular dose on the 1st day of life reduces the need for subsequent phototherapy. <strong>Complication</strong> from metalloporphyrins includes <strong>transient erythema</strong> if the infant is receiving phototherapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Tolazoline</strong> is an alpha-adrenergic antagonist that produces systemic and pulmonary vasodilatation, which is used to <strong>correct pulmonary artery vasospasm</strong> when it causes severe right-left shunting soon after birth.</p>\n<p><strong>Tromethamine</strong> is an organic buffer used in the management of <strong>metabolic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9757","text":"While on rounds in the pos‍tnatal ward, the senior P​G shows you a baby with d‌ark, grayish-brown skin d‌iscoloration. It is a ter‍m, AGA baby on day 7 of l‌ife. She tells you that i‌t is a case of bronze bab​y syndrome. This syndrome​ is caused by:","raw_text":"While on rounds in the postnatal ward, the senior PG shows you a baby with dark, grayish-brown skin discoloration. It is a term, AGA baby on day 7 of life. She tells you that it is a case of bronze baby syndrome. This syndrome is caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Photot‍herapy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemosi‍derosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vanco‍mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chloram‍phenicol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phototherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phototherapy</strong></p><p>The bronze baby syndrome is a <strong>dark, grayish-brown skin discoloration</strong> in infants undergoing<strong> phototherapy;</strong> it mainly occurs in infants with direct hyperbilirubinemia as an adverse effect of phototherapy.</p>\n<p>Despite the bronze baby syndrome, <strong>phototherapy</strong> <strong>can</strong> <strong>continue</strong> if needed.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Other complications of Phototherapy: </span></strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Loose stools</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Erythematous macular rash</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">The purpuric rash associated with transient porphyrinemia</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Overheating</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Dehydration (increased insensible water loss, diarrhea)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Hypothermia from exposure</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Gray baby syndrome </span></strong><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">is an adverse effect of <strong>chloramphenicol.</strong> </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\"><strong>Redman syndrome </strong>is an adverse effect of <strong>vancomycin.</strong> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9765","text":"A 3.2 Kg term neonate born‍ to a primigravida with g​estational diabetes prese‌nted to the OPD with comp‌laints of lethargy, hypot‍onia, and difficulty in f‌eeding. Periodic monitori‌ng of blood glucose is be​ing done. What blood gluc​ose levels indicate a nee‌d for the treatment of ne‍onatal hypoglycemia?","raw_text":"A 3.2 Kg term neonate born to a primigravida with gestational diabetes presented to the OPD with complaints of lethargy, hypotonia, and difficulty in feeding. Periodic monitoring of blood glucose is being done. What blood glucose levels indicate a need for the treatment of neonatal hypoglycemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 4‍5 mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 4‍0 mg/dL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;50‍ mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 5‍5 mg/dL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 40 mg/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 40 mg/dL</strong></p><p>The cut-off for the treatment of <strong>neonatal hypoglycemia </strong>to start IV glucose is a blood glucose level of <strong>&lt;40 mg/dL.</strong></p>\n<p>Neonatal hypoglycemia can be suspected when the blood glucose is <strong>&lt;45 mg/dL.</strong> Clinically in neonates, <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> can be <strong>asymptomatic</strong> or symptomatic. The neonate presents with tremors, cyanosis, <strong>convulsions</strong>, <strong>apneic spells</strong>, tachypnea, weak and high-pitched cry, <strong>lethargy</strong>,<strong> difficulty in feeding</strong>, episodes of sweating, sudden pallor, <strong>hypothermia</strong>, and rarely cardiac arrest.</p>\n<p>Management in <strong>symptomatic</strong> neonates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood glucose &lt;40mg/dL: IV glucose infusion</li>\n<li>Blood glucose &lt;25mg/dL: Bolus IV dextrose 1-2 mL/kg followed by infusion at the rate of 5-8mL/kg/min.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management in <strong>asymptomatic</strong> neonates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breastfeeding within 30 minutes</li>\n<li>Start IV glucose therapy if blood glucose &lt;35 mg/dL</li>\n<li>Recheck blood glucose before the next feeding</li>\n<li>Continue skin-to-skin care</li>\n<li>Evaluation for other underlying illnesses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The desired<strong> target glucose</strong> value is <strong>&gt;50mg/dL</strong> and blood glucose should be rechecked until<strong> three normal values</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5244","text":"After the delivery of an i‍nfant of a diabetic mothe​r, the blood glucose of t‌he Infant was 60 mg/dl. W‌hich other investigation ‍would you do?","raw_text":"After the delivery of an infant of a diabetic mother, the blood glucose of the Infant was 60 mg/dl. Which other investigation would you do?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum p‍otassium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum ‍sodium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum c‍hloride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum ‍calcium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serum calcium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serum calcium</strong></p><p>In an <strong>infant of a diabetic mother</strong> with normal blood glucose levels, the next investigation to consider is <strong>serum calcium</strong> due to the high risk of developing <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>. Hypocalcemia in these infants is attributed to the <strong>delayed response of the parathyroid hormone system</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Hypocalcemia</strong> is defined as a total serum calcium concentration of <strong>less than 8 mg/dL</strong> for a <strong>term</strong> baby (<strong>ionized calcium below 4mg/dL</strong>) and <strong>less than 7 mg/dL</strong> for a <strong>preterm</strong> baby. Asymptomatic neonates can be managed conservatively with early nutrition.<strong> Symptomatic neonates</strong> should receive <strong>iv or oral calcium</strong> replacement. </p>\n<p><strong>Maternal hyperglycemia</strong> causes <strong>fetal hyperglycemia</strong>, and the fetal pancreatic response leads to <strong>fetal hyperinsulinemia</strong>, which causes neonatal<strong> hypoglycemia</strong>. <strong>Neonatal hypoglycemia</strong> is defined as <strong>blood glucose &lt;40 mg/dL </strong>(plasma glucose &lt;45 mg/dL)<strong>.</strong> The infant tends to be <strong>jittery</strong>, <strong>tremulous</strong>, hyperexcitable, hypotonic, <strong>lethargic</strong>, and<strong> poor sucking</strong> during the 1st three days after birth. These symptoms can also be related to <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> and <strong>hypomagnesemia</strong> which occur in the 1st 24 to 72 hours of life. </p>\n<p><strong>Respiratory distress syndrome</strong>, <strong>macrosomia</strong>, hyperbilirubinemia, polycythemia, renal vein thrombosis,<strong> visceromegaly</strong>, heart failure, and persistent pulmonary hypertension are other features seen in infants of diabetic mothers. </p>\n<p><strong>Congenital anomalies</strong> are seen in infants of mothers with pregestational diabetes. <strong>Neural tube defects</strong> (Encephalocele, meningomyelocele, anencephaly), transposition of great vessels (<strong>TGA</strong>), ventricular septal defect (<strong>VSD)</strong>, atrial septal defect (<strong>ASD</strong>), hypoplastic left heart, aortic stenosis and coarctation of the aorta are commonly seen. </p>\n<p><strong>Caudal regression syndrome</strong> is rare, but a specific anomaly seen in infants of diabetic mothers. <strong>Small left colon syndrome</strong> is a rare anomaly that develops in the<strong> second</strong> and <strong>third</strong> trimesters. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9766","text":"A 31-year-old lady with di‍abetes mellitus delivers ​a term neonate. Which of ‌the following problems ma‌y be associated with the ‍baby born to this lady? H‌yperglycemia Hypocalcemia‌ Neural tube defects Macr​osomia Tetrology of Fallo​t","raw_text":"<p>A 31-year-old lady with diabetes mellitus delivers a term neonate. Which of the following problems may be associated with the baby born to this lady?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Neural tube defects</li>\n<li>Macrosomia</li>\n<li>Tetrology of Fallot </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‍2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‍3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‍4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‍3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Infants</strong> born to diabetic mothers may present with <strong>macrosomia</strong> (large for gestational age),<strong> hypocalcemia,</strong> and/ or<strong> neural tube defects.</strong></p>\n<p>Other common features include: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory distress syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cardiomegaly</strong></li>\n<li>Heart failure</li>\n<li><strong>Lumbosacral agenesis</strong></li>\n<li>Cardiac malformations\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li><strong>Ventricular or atrial septal defect</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Transposition of the great vessels (most specific)</strong></li>\n<li>Truncus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Double-outlet right ventricle</li>\n<li>Tricuspid atresia</li>\n<li>Coarctation of the aorta</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Additional anomalies include <strong>neural tube defects</strong>, hydronephrosis, renal agenesis and dysplasia, duodenal or anorectal atresia, situs inversus, double ureter, and holoprosencephaly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XPSrsQ2B","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"2_cell_death","title":"2.Cell Death","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2551","text":"Which among the following ‌is false regarding necros‌is?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false regarding necrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is due to denaturation ‌of protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is due to enzymatic dig‌estion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an ac‌tive process","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is swelling of cells‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is an active process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is an active process</strong></p><p><strong>Necrosis</strong> is a <strong>passive</strong> process, not active.</p>\n<p>It is due to <strong>denaturation of protein </strong>and<strong> enzymatic digestion.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2550","text":"What is the correct sequen‌ce of events that occur d‌uring necrosis? 1) Cell s​welling 2) Karyolysis 3) ‍Karyorrhexis 4) Pyknosis","raw_text":"<p>What is the correct sequence of events that occur during necrosis?</p>\n<p>1) Cell swelling<br/>2) Karyolysis<br/>3) Karyorrhexis <br/>4) Pyknosis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ 3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4,‌ 3, 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3,‌ 4, 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4, 3,‌ 2, 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4, 3, 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4, 3, 2</strong></p><p>The sequence of events in necrosis is: <strong>cellular swelling → pyknosis → karyorrhexis → karyolysis</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Necrosis</strong> is defined as the spectrum of morphological changes that follow cell death due to the progressive degradative action of enzymes.</p>\n<p>Morphological changes that occur in necrosis are as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>Cytoplasmic</strong> changes -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased <strong>eosinophilic staining </strong>on H&amp;E specimens</li>\n<li><strong>Moth-eaten </strong>appearance of the cytoplasm - due vacuolated cytoplasmic organelles formed by enzymatic degradation</li>\n<li><strong>Glassy</strong> and <strong>homogenous </strong>cytoplasm - due to loss of glycogen in the cytoplasm</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nuclear </strong>changes -</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pyknosis -</strong> nucleus first shrinks and becomes more condensed</li>\n<li><strong>Karyorrhexis</strong><strong> -</strong> fragmentation of the pyknotic nucleus</li>\n<li><strong>Karyolysis</strong> - nucleus fades away leaving behind only the cell membrane or empty cells </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2553","text":"A 58-year-old man was admi‌tted with sudden, subster‌nal chest pain radiating ​to his left arm. On exami‍nation, he was diaphoreti​c and dyspneic with an ir‍regular pulse. Despite re‌suscitative measures, he ​died within an hour of ad​mission. Autopsy revealed‌ the following gross appe​arance of the interventri‌cular myocardium. Which o‌f the following microscop‍ic changes is most likely​ to be present?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man was admitted with sudden, substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. On examination, he was diaphoretic and dyspneic with an irregular pulse. Despite resuscitative measures, he died within an hour of admission. Autopsy revealed the following gross appearance of the interventricular myocardium. Which of the following microscopic changes is most likely to be present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liquefacti‌ve necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caseous ‌necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulativ‌e necrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coagulative necrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4422515554943ed86f080df2b4cc62c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coagulative necrosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing dead tissue are suggestive of <strong>myocardial infarction.</strong> It is characterized by <strong>coagulative necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>In coagulative necrosis, despite cell death, the outline and <strong>architecture</strong> of the cells are <strong>preserved</strong> for a few days. This is because the cell injury is mainly due to the <strong>denaturation </strong>of all proteins, including the <strong>enzymatic</strong> <strong>proteins</strong>. This <strong>prevents</strong> the <strong>proteolysis</strong> of the dead cells. Ultimately, these necrotized cells are removed by phagocytosis and the lysosomal action of the enzymes of the leukocytes.</p>\n<p>Examples of coagulative necrosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ischemic injury in all organs, except the brain</li>\n<li>Dry gangrene</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2564","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‌the following pathologica​l change seen in the sple‍en?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of the following pathological change seen in the spleen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apop‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emboli‌zation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metas‌tasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Embolization","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bfb0e5155b7645be86d72ee1d35d26e0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Embolization</strong></p><p>The above image shows a pale, <strong>wedge-shaped </strong>area of<strong> infarction</strong> in the spleen.</p>\n<p>An infarct is a region of ischaemic necrosis resulting from the obstruction of blood flow, typically due to occlusion of the arterial supply or venous drainage. The most common cause of solid organ infarts is <strong>thromboembolism.</strong></p>\n<p>The pattern of ischemic necrosis seen in <strong>solid organ infarction</strong> is <strong>coagulative necrosis</strong>. However, <strong>CNS infarctions </strong>are an <strong>exception</strong>. They cause <strong>liquefactive necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2565","text":"A 67-year-old woman died i‌n the hospital 3 days aft‌er she was admitted with ​a sudden onset of right-s‍ided weakness of her body​, along with slurring of ‍speech. An autopsy showed‌ the following finding in​ the brain. What is this ​pathological change in th‌is lesion?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old woman died in the hospital 3 days after she was admitted with a sudden onset of right-sided weakness of her body, along with slurring of speech. An autopsy showed the following finding in the brain. What is this pathological change in this lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrinoid‌ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liquefacti‌ve necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caseous ‌necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulativ‌e necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liquefactive necrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06c0d3ef51de43ce93203c82c50f7b59.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liquefactive necrosis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>stroke</strong>, which results in <strong>ischemic injury </strong>to the brain.<strong> </strong>This results in <strong>liquefactive necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Liquefactive necrosis refers to cell death by <strong>enzymatic degradation</strong>. The brain contains <strong>microglial cells, </strong>which contain<strong> many enzymes. </strong>When these cells are broken down, they release these enzymes, resulting in liquefactive necrosis.</p>\n<p>The various types of necrosis are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 334.422px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 42px;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 42px;\">Example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 42px;\">Coagulative necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 42px;\">Infarcts in all organs except the brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 27px;\">Caseous necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 27px;\">Tuberculosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 36px;\">Liquefactive necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 36px;\">\n<p>Abscesses</p>\n<p>Brain infarct</p>\n<p>Wet gangrene</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 26px;\">Fat necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 26px;\">Pancreatitis, Breast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 54px;\">Fibrinoid necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Vasculitis</p>\n<p>Aschoff bodies</p>\n<p>Malignant hypertension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2257","text":"The gross specimen of the ‌pancreas from a patient w‌ho died of acute pancreat​itis is shown below. What‍ is it suggestive of?","raw_text":"The gross specimen of the pancreas from a patient who died of acute pancreatitis is shown below. What is it suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coagulativ‌e necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caseous ‌necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrinoid‌ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fat necrosis","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/cd78e14cf3bf4e898c154369db516c37.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fat necrosis</strong></p><p>The given specimen shows yellow nodules with areas of chalky-white deposits, which are suggestive of <strong>fat necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Fat necrosis results from the <strong>release </strong>of<strong> activated pancreatic lipases</strong> into the substance of the pancreas and the peritoneal cavity. These enzymes act on surrounding tissue to release fatty acids, which then combine with calcium to produce grossly visible chalky-white areas (<strong>fat saponification</strong>).</p>\n<p>Histologic examination reveals foci of shadowy outlines of <strong>necrotic fat cells</strong>, with <strong>basophilic</strong> <strong>calcium</strong> <strong>deposits</strong>, surrounded by an inflammatory reaction.</p>\n<p>The various types of necrosis include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 334.422px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 42px;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 42px;\">Example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 42px;\">Coagulative necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 42px;\">Infarcts in all organs except the brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 27px;\">Caseous necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 27px;\">Tuberculosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 36px;\">Liquefactive necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 36px;\">\n<p>Abscesses</p>\n<p>Brain infarct</p>\n<p>Wet gangrene</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 26px;\">Fat necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 26px;\">Pancreatitis, Breast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 54px;\">Fibrinoid necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.422px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Vasculitis</p>\n<p>Aschoff bodies</p>\n<p>Malignant hypertension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2566","text":"What is the type of necros‌is seen in the vessel wal‌l of a patient with polya​rteritis nodosa?","raw_text":"What is the type of necrosis seen in the vessel wall of a patient with polyarteritis nodosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrinoid‌ necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gangrenou‌s necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caseous ‌necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fibrinoid necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6129a1492b5047cbab76fe9a71829f82x1280x1167.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee88cf2f5647430cb1039339f5af1785x1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fibrinoid necrosis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 55\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Immune reactions involving <strong>blood vessels</strong>, as seen in the case of <strong>polyarteritis nodosa,</strong> usually show <strong>fibrinoid necrosis. </strong>Fibrinoid means fibrin-like.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>In some immune disorders, the <strong>antigen-antibody complexes</strong> get deposited in the blood vessels. These complexes result in<strong> complement activation </strong>(type III hypersensitivity) leading to cell injury. This leads to leakage of fibrin out of the blood vessel, giving a<strong> pinkish appearance</strong> around the blood vessels.</p>\n<p>Fibrinoid necrosis is seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>Libman-sacks endocarditis</li>\n<li>Arthus reaction</li>\n<li>Serum sickness</li>\n<li>Polyarteritis nodosa</li>\n<li>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>Malignant hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7087","text":"Which of the following is ‌involved in recognition a‌nd clearance of necrotic ​cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following is involved in recognition and clearance of necrotic cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflam‌masome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Toll-like receptors (TLRs)‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P sel‌ectin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Manore‌ceptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inflammasome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inflammasome</strong></p><p><strong>Inflammasomes</strong> are involved in the <strong>recognition </strong>and<strong> clearance </strong>of <strong>necrotic cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Inflammasomes are involved in <strong>pyroptosis</strong>, a process of programmed cell death distinct from apoptosis and necrosis. This process is dependent on <strong>caspases 1, 4, </strong>and<strong> 5</strong> and is responsible for killing of infected cells. It occurs in the following sequence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activation of inflammasome complex by infected cells</li>\n<li>This causes activation of caspase-1</li>\n<li>This results in generation of IL-1, which is responsible for fever, inflammation, leukocyte recruitment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Toll-like receptors (<strong>TLR</strong>)- recognizes molecules shared by pathogens</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>P-selectins</strong> - an important role in the initial recruitment of WBCs at the site of inflammation &amp; in platelet recruitment and aggregation at the site of injury.                                         </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2552","text":"In cases of cell injury, a‌ll of the following are r‌esponsible for the increa​sed eosinophilic staining‍ and glassy homogenous cy​toplasm except:","raw_text":"In cases of cell injury, all of the following are responsible for the increased eosinophilic staining and glassy homogenous cytoplasm except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of cyt‌oplasmic RNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Denature‌d protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in glycogen parti‌cles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of glycogen particles‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increase in glycogen particles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increase in glycogen particles</strong></p><p>In <strong>cell injury</strong>, there is a <strong>loss of glycogen</strong>, which leads to a change in cytoplasmic appearance. An increase in glycogen particles is not a feature of cell injury.</p>\n<p>In cell injury, the following changes are seen in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased <strong>eosinophilic staining</strong> due to -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increase</strong> in<strong> denatured</strong> <strong>proteins </strong>(which bind to acidic dyes like eosin)</li>\n<li><strong>Loss </strong>of <strong>cytoplasmic RNA </strong>(nucleic acids bind to basic dyes like hematoxylin) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Glassy, homogeneous</strong> appearance - due to loss of glycogen particles</li>\n<li>Moth-eaten appearance - vacuolated appearance due to enzymatic digestion of cytoplasmic organelles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5076","text":"Which of the following is ‌a true statement about ap‌optosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement about apoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is enzymatic digesti‌on of cellular contents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with adja‌cent inflammation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Karyolysis‌ is present","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma membrane remains in‌tact","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Plasma membrane remains intact","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Plasma membrane remains intact</strong></p><p>The<strong> plasma membrane</strong> remains<strong> intact </strong>in <strong>apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Apoptosis literally means <strong>falling-off </strong>in Greek. It is a pathway of<strong> programmed cell death</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 63\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Apoptotic cells break up into fragments called <strong>apoptotic bodies</strong>. These apoptotic bodies have intact plasma membrane but altered structures. Hence, they are subjected to <strong>phagocytosis</strong> without eliciting inflammation.</p>\n<p>Necrosis is an unprogrammed form of cell death that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enzymatic digestion</li>\n<li>Adjacent inflammation</li>\n<li>Karyolysis</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2568","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an example of physiol‌ogical apoptosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an example of physiological apoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometrial cell break-dow‌n during menstrual cycle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apoptosis of Müllerian str‌uctures in male fetuses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elimination of neutrophils‌ after an acute inflammat‌ory response","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrophy in parenchymal org‌ans following duct obstru‌ction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atrophy in parenchymal organs following duct obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atrophy in parenchymal organs following duct obstruction</strong></p><p>Atrophy in parenchymal organs after duct obstruction is a pathological cause of apoptosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Physiological </strong>causes of apoptosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>During embryogenesis<strong> - </strong>Müllerian inhibiting factor causing apoptosis of the <strong>Mullerian structures</strong> in the <strong>male fetus</strong></li>\n<li>Hormone-dependent apoptosis -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endometrial cell breakdown</strong> during the <strong>menstrual</strong> <strong>cycle</strong></li>\n<li>Regression of the lactating breast after weaning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cell loss in proliferating cell populations - turnover of cells of intestinal crypt epithelium</li>\n<li><strong>Elimination</strong> of <strong>neutrophils</strong> after an acute inflammatory response and lymphocytes at the end of an immune response</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pathological </strong>causes of apoptosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA damage beyond repair</li>\n<li>Accumulation of misfolded proteins leads to endoplasmic reticulum stress</li>\n<li>Cell injury in certain viral infections (e.g., Councilman bodies in viral hepatitis)</li>\n<li><strong>Pathologic atrophy</strong> after <strong>duct obstruction</strong> in parenchymal organs like pancreas, parotid gland, and kidneys</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4486","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an anti-apoptotic pro‌tein?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an anti-apoptotic protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BCL‌-XL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B‌AX","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BCL‌- 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MC‌L-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BAX","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BAX</strong></p><p><strong>BAX</strong> and <strong>BAK</strong> are <strong>pro-apoptotic </strong>proteins.</p>\n<p>They get inserted into the mitochondrial membrane and result in the formation of channels. This allows proteins from the inner mitochondrial membrane to leak out into the cytoplasm.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>antiapoptotic</strong> proteins of the BCL2 family, like <strong>BCL-2</strong> and <strong>BCL</strong>-<strong>XL</strong> limit the escape of proapoptotic mitochondrial proteins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6759","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a stress sensor of ap‌optosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a stress sensor of apoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B‌im","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pu‌ma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sm‌ac","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No‌xa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Smac","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Smac</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Smac</strong>, also known as <strong>Diablo</strong> is not a stress sensor of apoptosis. It</span><span class=\"s1\"> is a <strong>proapoptotic protein</strong> that initiates the caspase cascade by neutralizing IAPs (physiological inhibitors of apoptosis).</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2574","text":"What are apoptotic bodies ‌made up of?","raw_text":"What are apoptotic bodies made up of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytoplasm an‌d organelles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytoplasm and nuclear frag‌ments","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Organelles and nuclear fra‌gments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytoplasm, nuclear fragmen‌ts and organelles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cytoplasm, nuclear fragments and organelles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cytoplasm, nuclear fragments and organelles</strong></p><p>Apoptotic bodies are made up of <strong>cytoplasm</strong> with tightly packed <strong>organelles</strong>, and <strong>nuclear fragments.</strong></p>\n<p>During apoptosis, extensive plasma membrane<strong> blebbing</strong> occurs, followed by cell fragmentation into <strong>apoptotic bodies</strong> by a process called <strong>budding</strong>. </p>\n<p>In apoptotic cells, <strong>phosphatidylserine</strong>, which is normally present on the inner leaflet of the plasma membrane, <strong>flips </strong>to the <strong>outer leaflet</strong>. Hence, it gets recognized by tissue macrophages and leads to <strong>phagocytosis</strong> of the apoptotic bodies.</p>\n<p><strong>Annexin V </strong>is a marker of apoptosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2569","text":"Caspase cleaves proteins a‌t:","raw_text":"Caspase cleaves proteins at:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysteine‌ residue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystine‌ residue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspartic a‌cid residue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asparagin‌e residue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aspartic acid residue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ae6c046f8e34e878ae1f038830c2ee1x1280x836.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aspartic acid residue</strong></p><p><strong>Caspases</strong> are cysteine proteases that cleave proteins at the <strong>aspartic acid residue</strong>. Hence, the activation of caspases plays a key role in the process of <strong>apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The activation of caspases depends on a finely tuned balance between production of pro- and anti-apoptotic proteins.</p>\n<p>Two distinct <strong>pathways</strong> converge on caspase activation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The mitochondrial (<strong>intrinsic</strong>) pathway</li>\n<li>The death receptor (<strong>extrinsic</strong>) pathway</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2570","text":"What is the function of FL‌IP protein in the pathway‌ of apoptosis?","raw_text":"What is the function of FLIP protein in the pathway of apoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulates the extrinsic p‌athway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulates the intrinsic p‌athway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits the extrinsic pat‌hway","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits the intrinsic pat‌hway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibits the extrinsic pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0345eabce43c47f2a836eaba2e433507x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibits the extrinsic pathway</strong></p><p><strong>FLIP</strong> is a cellular protein that <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>extrinsic pathway</strong> of apoptosis.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>caspase antagonist</strong> that inhibits the activation of caspases involved in the extrinsic or death receptor pathway. Some viruses produce homologs of this protein. This may play a role in keeping infected cells alive.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>two</strong> <strong>pathways</strong> that can lead to caspase activation and cause <strong>apoptosis</strong> of the cell.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 112px; width: 478px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\"><strong>Mitochondrial</strong> or <strong>intrinsic </strong>pathway</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><strong>Death receptor</strong> or <strong>extrinsic </strong>pathway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">Activation inside the cell </td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">Activation outside the cell </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Occurs in response to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA damage</li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p>Involved in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elimination of self-reactive lymphocytes</li>\n<li>Killing of target cells by cytotoxic T-cells</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Involves release of pro-apoptotic proteins (BAX, BAK)</p>\n<p>They form a <strong>BAX-BAK</strong> channel in the mitochondrial membrane</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p>Involves death receptors on cell surfaces (have a death domain)</p>\n<p>These include <strong>TNF-1</strong> and <strong>Fas </strong>(CD95) of the TNF family</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Leakage of <strong>cytochrome-c</strong> results in the formation of Cyt.c-APAF1 complex, followed by <strong>apoptosome</strong> formation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p><strong>FasL</strong> on T-cells binds to Fas bind adapter proteins via death domain</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Activation of<strong> caspase 9</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p>Activation of <strong>caspases 8</strong> and <strong>10</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 464px;\">Both pathways converge into a common pathway that involves the activation of caspases 3, 6, 7 (executioner caspases)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>APAF-1 - Apoptotic peptidase activating factor-1</p>\n<p>TNF - Tumor necrosis factor</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0146","text":"Both intrinsic and extrins‌ic pathways are seen in:","raw_text":"Both intrinsic and extrinsic pathways are seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apop‌tosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Necr‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Necro‌ptosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrop‌tosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apoptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b7e94bb9c324391bc322277f32bf97fx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apoptosis</strong></p><p><strong>Intrinsic</strong> and <strong>extrinsic</strong> pathways are both seen in <strong>apoptosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Apoptosis has two pathways:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 112px; width: 478px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\"><strong>Mitochondrial</strong> or <strong>intrinsic </strong>pathway</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><strong>Death receptor</strong> or <strong>extrinsic </strong>pathway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">Activation inside the cell </td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">Activation outside the cell </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Occurs in response to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA damage</li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p>Involved in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elimination of self-reactive lymphocytes</li>\n<li>Killing of target cells by cytotoxic T-cells</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Involves release of pro-apoptotic proteins (BAX, BAK)</p>\n<p>They form a <strong>BAX-BAK</strong> channel in the mitochondrial membrane</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p>Involves death receptors on cell surfaces (have a death domain)</p>\n<p>These include <strong>TNF-1</strong> and <strong>Fas </strong>(CD95) of the TNF family</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Leakage of <strong>cytochrome-c</strong> results in the formation of Cyt.c-APAF1 complex, followed by <strong>apoptosome</strong> formation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p><strong>FasL</strong> on T-cells binds to Fas bind adapter proteins via death domain</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Activation of<strong> caspase 9</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\">\n<p>Activation of <strong>caspases 8</strong> and <strong>10</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 464px;\">Both pathways converge into a common pathway that involves the activation of caspases 3, 6, 7 (executioner caspases)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>APAF-1 - Apoptosis activating factor-1</p>\n<p>TNF - Tumor necrosis factor</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2571","text":"Which of the following pat‌hological changes is repr‌esented by the marked str​ucture seen on histopatho‍logy of a liver specimen?​","raw_text":"Which of the following pathological changes is represented by the marked structure seen on histopathology of a liver specimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apop‌tosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Granuloma‌ formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abscess ‌formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulativ‌e necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apoptosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68c93fb3a86d47838327634e29e6f10a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad07a5eafb20461a9fd5daf205bd9d37x1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apoptosis</strong></p><p>The above histopathological image shows <strong>Councilman </strong>or<strong> </strong><strong>acidophil</strong> <strong>bodies</strong>. They represent <strong>hepatocytes</strong> undergoing <strong>apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Councilman bodies are commonly seen in <strong>acute viral hepatitis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2573","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures is not true about n‌ecroptosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not true about necroptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a form of programmed‌ cell death","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It results in cell swellin‌g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is caspase activatio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced cellular ATP level‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There is caspase activation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There is caspase activation</strong></p><p>Caspase activation does not occur in <strong>necroptosis.</strong> It is a <strong>caspase-independent</strong> form of programmed cell death.</p>\n<p><strong>Necroptosis</strong> is a <strong>programmed form</strong> of <strong>necrosis</strong> (which is normally an unprogrammed process). Though the activation of this pathway is similar to apoptosis, there is no activation of caspases, which is an important part of apoptosis.</p>\n<p>Necroptosis is triggered by the activation of TNFR1 by TNF-α. This induces the recruitment of RIP1 and RIP3 kinase and the formation of a <strong>necrosome</strong>. The necrosome <strong>reduces</strong> <strong>cellular</strong> <strong>ATP</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Necroptosis occurs in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pathological - </strong>ischemic brain injury, neurodegenerative diseases, viral infections</li>\n<li><strong>Physiological - </strong>formation of the mammalian bone growth plate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following table lists the differences between apoptosis and necroptosis:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 491.46875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Feature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Apoptosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.46875px;\"><strong>Necroptosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Induction stimuli</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Oxidative stress, death receptor ligands, chemotherapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.46875px;\">Viral or chemical exposure, radiation, endogenous or pathological factors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Morphological changes                  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Nuclear pyknosis, membrane <strong>blebbing</strong>, generation of <strong>apoptotic</strong> <strong>bodies</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.46875px;\"><strong>Swelling of cells</strong> and organelles, loss of membrane integrity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Molecular changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Cleavage of caspases, DNA fragmentation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.46875px;\">Acidosis, random DNA degradation, release of cellular proteins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Clearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Apoptotic bodies phagocytosed by neighboring cells and macrophages</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.46875px;\">Necrotic cells ingested by macrophages, significant inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2576","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about pyroptosis exce‌pt:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about pyroptosis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is ce‌ll swelling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is loss of membrane ‌integrity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a form of programmed‌ cell death","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is involvement of ca‌spase 8","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. There is involvement of caspase 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. There is involvement of caspase 8</strong></p><p><strong>Caspases 1, 4,</strong> and<strong> 5</strong> are involved in <strong>pyroptosis,</strong> not caspase 8.</p>\n<p>Pyroptosis is a form of <strong>programmed cell death</strong> that is different from apoptosis and necrosis. It is involved in the clearance of <strong>necrotic</strong> and <strong>microbe-infected cells</strong>. It occurs in the following sequence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activation of the inflammasome complex</li>\n<li>Activation of caspase-1</li>\n<li>Generation of IL-1 - results in fever, inflammation, leukocyte recruitment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pyroptosis differs from apoptosis by the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is <strong>cellular swelling</strong></li>\n<li>There is <strong>loss</strong> of cell <strong>membrane integrity </strong></li>\n<li>It is associated with inflammation </li>\n<li>Caspases 1, 4, and 5 are involved</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2577","text":"During the autopsy of a 90‌-year-old woman, a normal‌-looking liver was noted.​ The following microscopi‍c appearance of hepatocyt​es was noted. Which of th‍e following cellular mech‌anisms is likely responsi​ble for this finding?","raw_text":"During the autopsy of a 90-year-old woman, a normal-looking liver was noted. The following microscopic appearance of hepatocytes was noted. Which of the following cellular mechanisms is likely responsible for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear ‌pyknosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyper‌trophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coagulativ‌e necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autophag‌ocytosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autophagocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da0f5b808d1445af8bf47533f856d8a4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autophagocytosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows brownish <strong>lipofuscin granules </strong>within the liver cells. It is composed of lipid-containing residues of lysosomal digestion that are formed due to <strong>autophagocytosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Lipofuscin is a wear-and-tear pigment that is due to <strong>free radical injury</strong> to the tissue. It is prominent in the liver and heart of <strong>aging</strong> patients or patients with severe <strong>malnutrition</strong> and cancer <strong>cachexia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Autophagy</strong> is a process by which the cell <strong>consumes itself.</strong> It involves the sequestration of cellular organelles into cytoplasmic autophagic vacuoles (<strong>autophagosomes</strong>) that fuse with lysosomes, resulting in the digestion of the enclosed material. It occurs in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>As a survival mechanism during nutrient deprivation and stress conditions</li>\n<li>Cancers, inflammatory bowel diseases, neurodegenerative disorders - due to dysregulation </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6760","text":"What is the most reliable ‌marker for cellular autop‌hagy?","raw_text":"What is the most reliable marker for cellular autophagy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At‌g5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RI‌P3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L‌C3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anne‌xin V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. LC3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. LC3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Microtubule-associated protein light chain 3 (<strong>LC3</strong>) is a reliable marker for <strong>cellular autophagy.</strong> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">There is increased synthesis of this molecule during autophagy. LC3 plays a role in </span>elongation and closure of the autophagosomal membrane.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 48\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Autophagy (<strong>self-eating</strong>) is the process in which the starved cell eats its own components in an attempt to reduce nutrient demand to match the supply.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: Atg5 protein, although associated with autophagy, is not a reliable marker as there is no differential expression during autophagy initiation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: RIP3 (receptor associated kinase 3) is a unique kinase involved in necroptosis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: Annexin V is a marker for cellular apoptosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4614","text":"Which of the following for‌ms of cell death is induc‌ed by lipid peroxidation?​","raw_text":"Which of the following forms of cell death is induced by lipid peroxidation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrop‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Necro‌ptosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferro‌ptosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Programme‌d necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ferroptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ferroptosis</strong></p><p><strong>Ferroptosis</strong> is an iron-dependent pathway of cell death that is induced by <strong>lipid peroxidation.</strong></p>\n<p>It is triggered by an increase in membrane lipid peroxidation in response to the following signals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Excessive intracellular levels of <strong>iron</strong></li>\n<li>Reactive oxygen species (<strong>ROS</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This leads to a <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>selective</strong> <strong>permeability</strong> of the plasma membrane, which ultimately leads to cell death. Unlike necrosis, the process is <strong>regulated</strong> by specific signals and can, therefore, be <strong>prevented</strong> by <strong>reducing</strong> <strong>iron</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Ferroptosis has been linked to cell death in a variety of human pathologies including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cancer</li>\n<li>Neurodegenerative diseases</li>\n<li>Stroke</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other forms of cell death that are not dependent on lipid peroxidation include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Necroptosis</strong> or <strong>programmed necrosis -</strong> programmed cell death <strong>without caspase</strong> activation</li>\n<li><strong>Pyroptosis</strong> - programmed cell death that is dependent on <strong>caspases</strong> 1, 4, and 5</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5037","text":"Which of the following is ‌not involved in efferocyt‌osis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not involved in efferocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithel‌ial cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macro‌phage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibro‌blast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Free r‌adicals","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Free radicals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Free radicals</strong></p><p><strong>Efferocytosis</strong> is a specialized type of phagocytosis, where <strong>apoptotic bodies</strong> are phagocytosed with the help of <strong>macrophages, epithelial cells</strong>, and <strong>fibroblast cells.</strong></p>\n<p>During this process, ligands bind to the apoptotic bodies and send ‘<strong>eat me</strong>’ <strong>signals</strong>. These signals are recognized by receptors present on phagocytes that bind and engulf these cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2575","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases is not caused by an‌ abnormal folded protein?​","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not caused by an abnormal folded protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alzheimer‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Huntington‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pompe ‌disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pompe disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f162eb552c0e49328430e8b8111d90cax1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pompe disease</strong></p><p><strong>Pompe disease </strong>is a <strong>glycogen</strong> <strong>storage </strong>disorder caused by the deficiency of lysosomal acid alpha glucosidase enzyme. It is not due to abnormal folded proteins.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>During <strong>normal</strong> protein synthesis, <strong>chaperones</strong> in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) control the <strong>proper folding</strong> of newly synthesized proteins. <strong>Misfolded</strong> polypeptides are <strong>ubiquitinated</strong> and targeted for <strong>proteolysis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Sometimes, <strong>unfolded</strong> or <strong>misfolded </strong>proteins <strong>accumulate</strong> in the ER as a result of inherited mutations or environmental stress. This induces a<strong> protective</strong> <strong>response</strong> called the unfolded protein response that increases the production of chaperones.<strong> </strong>This reduces the levels of misfolded proteins in the cell. </p>\n<p>However, when the accumulation of misfolded proteins overwhelms these adaptations, it results in<strong> ER stress</strong> which leads to the <strong>activation of caspases </strong>and, ultimately,<strong> apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some diseases that occur due to the accumulation of abnormal folded proteins are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n<li>Huntington's disease</li>\n<li>Parkinson's disease</li>\n<li>Prion diseases</li>\n<li>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</li>\n<li>Tay-Sachs disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KYJtrtSF","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"2_pharmacodynamics","title":"2.Pharmacodynamics","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MA4492","text":"Neurotransmitters like nor‌adrenaline, adrenaline, a​nd dopamine act on:","raw_text":"Neurotransmitters like noradrenaline, adrenaline, and dopamine act on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single-pass transmembrane ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seven-pass transmembrane r‌eceptors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ligand-gate‌d receptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Seven-pass transmembrane receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4c6a2d30d5c46dc9b527577f6591a2ax1280x1087.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Seven-pass transmembrane receptors</strong></p><p><strong>Noradrenaline</strong>, <strong>adrenaline,</strong> and <strong>dopamine</strong> act through a bundle of <strong>seven</strong> alpha-helicase <strong>trans-membrane</strong> receptors which are the <strong>G-protein-coupled receptors</strong> (GPCRs).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4495","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about G-p​rotein-coupled receptors?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about G-protein-coupled receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G proteins bind to hormone‌s on the cell surface.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All the three subunits alp‌ha, beta and gamma should​ bind to each other for G‍ proteins to act.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G proteins act as inhibito‌ry and excitatory because​ of differences in the al‍pha subunit.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"G protein is bound to GTP ‌in resting state.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. G proteins act as inhibitory and excitatory because of differences in the alpha subunit.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b43b24ca1f7244c8b612a11f90812f45x1279x1346.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. G proteins act as inhibitory and excitatory because of differences in the alpha subunit.</strong></p><p><strong>G proteins</strong> act as <strong>inhibitory</strong> and <strong>excitatory</strong> because of <strong>differences</strong> in the <strong>alpha</strong> subunit. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Excitatory -</strong> Gs and Gq </li>\n<li><strong>Inhibitory</strong> - Gi </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the <strong>resting-state</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G protein is a heterotrimer consisting of alpha, beta, and gamma subunits. It forms a complex with G-protein-coupled receptors at the intracellular domain. The extracellular domain of GPCR has the binding domain for hormones. G protein subunits do not directly bind them.<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>alpha</strong> subunit is bound to <strong>GDP.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Binding </strong>of<strong> ligand </strong>results in<strong> activation of GPCR, </strong>following which:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alpha subunit undergoes a conformational change</li>\n<li>Release of GDP and binding of GTP occurs</li>\n<li>GTP bound alpha subunit dissociates from beta-gamma subunit</li>\n<li>Both alpha subunit and beta-gamma subunit bind to and regulate effectors only when they are separated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0131","text":"Which of the following GPC‌Rs does not act via the K​+ channel?","raw_text":"Which of the following GPCRs does not act via the K+ channel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angiotensin‌ 1 receptor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscarinic ‌M2 receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine D‌2 receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serotonin 5 ‌HT1 receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Angiotensin 1 receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Angiotensin 1 receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Angiotensin 1 receptor</strong> acts via <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> channels. It does not act through second messengers via the K<sup>+</sup> channel. </p>\n<p>G-protein coupled receptors activate inwardly rectifying potassium channels and increase the permeability of the potassium ions causing hyperpolarisation of the cell. They are called <strong>G protein-coupled inwardly-rectifying potassium channels</strong> (GIRKs). These receptors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M2 </strong>muscarinic</li>\n<li><strong>A1 </strong>adenosine</li>\n<li><strong>α2 </strong>adrenergic</li>\n<li><strong>D2 </strong>dopamine</li>\n<li><strong>5HT1 </strong>serotonin</li>\n<li>Somatostatin receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5340","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of a GPCR?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of a GPCR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Musca‌rinic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nico‌tinic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GAB‌A-A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Muscarinic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fa1475ef8974317bcddf902a641b91cx1280x1087.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43df5b42a2db476c9dbb0130408366d0x1280x1500.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99c758676519450aad00625cacd2b74cx720x1321.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Muscarinic</strong></p><p><strong>Muscarinic receptors</strong> are <strong>G-protein-coupled receptors</strong> among the given options.</p>\n<p>M1, M2, M3, M4, and M5 are the muscarinic Ach receptors.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M1</strong> and <strong>M3</strong> act through <strong>Gq</strong> protein and activate <strong>IP3/DAG</strong> causing increased cytosolic calcium.</li>\n<li><strong>M2</strong> and <strong>M4</strong> act through <strong>Gi</strong> protein and inhibit <strong>adenylyl cyclase</strong> causing increased potassium conductance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Nicotinic receptors</strong> and <strong>GABA-A</strong> are examples of <strong>ion channel</strong> coupled receptors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Insulin</strong> receptor is a <strong>tyrosine kinase</strong> receptor.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4502","text":"Which of the following is ‌an ion channel receptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an ion channel receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nicotinic cholinergic rece‌ptor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscarinic cholinergic rec‌eptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta adrener‌gic receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid r‌eceptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nicotinic cholinergic receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33cfea1bc6f2417cbc712880cb653c8ex1280x1500.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9c1a274c2964d6f80153a48471ace73x1279x1086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nicotinic cholinergic receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Nicotinic cholinergic receptors</strong> (both Nm and Nn) are of <strong>ligand-gated</strong> ion channel type.</p>\n<p>The binding of ligand alters the <strong>permeability</strong> of <strong>ion channels</strong> directly and brings about <strong>biological action.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Muscarinic cholinergic, beta-adrenergic, and opioid receptors are G protein-coupled receptors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4503","text":"All of the following agent‌s act by intracellular re​ceptors except ______","raw_text":"All of the following agents act by intracellular receptors except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid ‌hormones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‌min D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ste‌roid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> receptors belong to the class of<strong> trans-membrane</strong> enzyme-linked receptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Transmembrane enzyme-linked receptors </strong>consist of a<strong> </strong>large extracellular ligand-binding domain. It is connected to an intracellular subunit having enzymatic property through a single transmembrane helical peptide chain. The enzyme on the cytosolic side is usually a <strong>protein kinase</strong> (tyrosine) or <strong>membrane-bound GC </strong>(guanylyl cyclase).</p>\n<p>Physiological ligands for transmembrane enzyme-linked receptors are mostly growth factors and chemokines. Other receptors belonging to this class include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Receptor tyrosine kinases - </strong>Growth factors (PDGF, EGF, VEGF); insulin</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Membrane-bound GC - </strong>Natriuretic peptides</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Receptor serine-threonine kinases - </strong>TGF-β </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thyroid hormones, vitamin D, and steroids act via intracellular receptors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4508","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a second messenger​?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a second messenger?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cycli‌c AMP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guanylyl‌ cyclase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diacylg‌lycerol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inositol t‌riphosphate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Guanylyl cyclase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Guanylyl cyclase</strong></p><p><strong>Guanylyl cyclase</strong> is an <strong>enzyme</strong> that converts <strong>GTP</strong> to <strong>cGMP.</strong> It is not a second messenger.</p>\n<p><strong>Second messengers</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diacylglycerol</li>\n<li>Inositol triphosphate</li>\n<li>Cyclic AMP</li>\n<li>Cyclic GMP</li>\n<li>Calcium</li>\n<li>Nitric oxide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0609","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones act through the rec​eptor shown in the image ‍below?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones act through the receptor shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Somato‌statin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholecy‌stokinin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasopressin ‌(V1 receptor)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insulin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/137bbde364aa4bcfbec1d567af3f5776.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insulin</strong></p><p>The receptor in the given image uses <strong>tyrosine kinase</strong> signaling.<strong> Insulin </strong>acts through this receptor to exert its functions.</p>\n<p>Other hormones acting via tyrosine kinase signaling: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Growth hormone</li>\n<li>Growth factors like VEGF, PDGF, FGF, NGF</li>\n<li>Prolactin</li>\n<li>Leptin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4539","text":"A patient with congestive ‌heart failure is being tr​eated with an intravenous‍ infusion of nesiritide. ​Which of the following re‍ceptors does this drug ac​t through?","raw_text":"A patient with congestive heart failure is being treated with an intravenous infusion of nesiritide. Which of the following receptors does this drug act through?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G-protein coupled receptor‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine kin‌ase receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"JAK-STAT ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligand-gated guanylyl cycl‌ase receptor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ligand-gated guanylyl cyclase receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ligand-gated guanylyl cyclase receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Nesiritide</strong> is recombinant <strong>B-type natriuretic peptide. </strong>Natriuretic peptide receptors are transmembrane <strong>ligand-gated</strong> <strong>guanylyl cyclase</strong> receptors.</p>\n<p>Natriuretic peptides: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial</strong> <strong>natriuretic peptide</strong> (ANP): released from atrial storage granules in response to an increase in intravascular volume. It <strong>decreases blood pressure</strong>.  </li>\n<li><strong>Brain natriuretic peptide</strong> (BNP): released from ventricular tissue in response to volume overload. Along with a decrease in blood pressure, it is known to <strong>reduce cardiac hypertrophy</strong> and<strong> fibrosis</strong>.  </li>\n<li><strong>C- type natriuretic peptide</strong> (CNP): synthesized in the brain and endothelial tissues and functions to stimulate <strong>long bone growth.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>ANP and BNP act on the <strong>NPR-A</strong> receptor.</p>\n<p>CNP acts on the <strong>NPR-B</strong> receptor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4509","text":"JAK/STAT is a signal trans‌ducer for which of the fo​llowing receptors?","raw_text":"JAK/STAT is a signal transducer for which of the following receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G protein coupled receptor‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ion ch‌annels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transmembra‌ne receptors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclear ‌receptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transmembrane receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80e08aee5f19404abcb79703932759f4x1280x2227.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transmembrane receptors</strong></p><p><strong>JAK/STAT</strong> is a signal transducer for <strong>transmembrane receptors</strong> lacking intrinsic enzymatic activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Interleukin </strong>and other <strong>cytokines</strong> act through such receptors.</p>\n<p>On binding of the ligand to such receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Receptor dimerization occurs</li>\n<li>Increase in affinity for cytosolic JAK (Janus kinase)</li>\n<li>Activation of JAK results in phosphorylation of tyrosine residues of the receptor</li>\n<li>Binding of signal transducer and activator of transcription (STAT) and subsequent phosphorylation of STAT</li>\n<li>STAT dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to regulate gene expression, resulting in a biological response</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4521","text":"Adrenaline is used to trea‌t histamine-induced bronc​hoconstriction in a patie‍nt with brittle asthma. W​hat is this type of antag‍onism called?","raw_text":"Adrenaline is used to treat histamine-induced bronchoconstriction in a patient with brittle asthma. What is this type of antagonism called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physiologica‌l antagonism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chemical ‌antagonism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Competitive‌ antagonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uncompetitiv‌e antagonism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Physiological antagonism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Physiological antagonism</strong></p><p><strong>Adrenaline</strong> and <strong>histamine</strong> are <strong>physiological</strong> antagonists. </p>\n<p>Adrenaline and histamine act on bronchial muscles. <strong>Histamine</strong> acts on <strong>H1</strong> receptors and produces <strong>bronchoconstriction. Adrenaline</strong> acts on <strong>β2 </strong>receptors and produces <strong>bronchodilation. </strong>Two drugs acting on different receptors and resulting in opposite responses means having <strong>pharmacological effects</strong> in the <strong>opposite</strong> direction. This is called <strong>physiological</strong> antagonism or <strong>functional</strong> antagonism.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 77\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option B:<strong> Chemical</strong> antagonism refers to a reaction between two chemicals to neutralize their effects, such as is seen with <strong>chelation</strong> therapy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 54\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option C: <strong>Competitive</strong> antagonism is a type of <strong>receptor</strong> antagonism that occurs when the agonist and antagonist compete for the <strong>same</strong> binding <strong>site</strong> on the receptor.</p>\n<p>Option D: In<strong> uncompetitive inhibition, </strong>inhibitors form a complex with an intermediate complex of the substrate and transporter. This inhibits subsequent translocation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7026","text":"Diamox is given to a patie‌nt with glaucoma. It is a​ non-competitive inhibito‍r of the carbonic anhydra​se enzyme. Which of the f‍ollowing would be the eff​ect of this drug?","raw_text":"Diamox is given to a patient with glaucoma. It is a non-competitive inhibitor of the carbonic anhydrase enzyme. Which of the following would be the effect of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease‌ in Vmax","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreas‌e in Km","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in both Km and Vm‌ax","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No chang‌e in Vmax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decrease in Vmax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28ef8e94d26446bbbe7383b757789868x1280x1423.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decrease in Vmax</strong></p><p><strong>Non-competitive inhibitors</strong> cause <strong>a decrease in Vmax.</strong></p>\n<p>In non-competitive inhibition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The inhibitor<strong> binds</strong> to the<strong> adjacent site</strong> and not the catalytic site of the enzyme</li>\n<li>The catalytic property of the enzyme is reduced and hence<strong> Vmax </strong>is<strong> decreased</strong></li>\n<li>The affinity of the enzyme is not altered and hence <strong>Km</strong> is<strong> unaltered</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4524","text":"Letrozole, a competitive i‌nhibitor of the enzyme ar​omatase is prescribed to ‍a patient with breast can​cer. Which of the followi‍ng is true about this typ​e of antagonism?","raw_text":"Letrozole, a competitive inhibitor of the enzyme aromatase is prescribed to a patient with breast cancer. Which of the following is true about this type of antagonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antagonism cannot be compl‌etely reversed by increas​ed dose of the agonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An agonist cannot displace‌ an antagonist from the r​eceptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Agonists and antagonists b‌ind to the same receptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dose response curve shifts‌ to the left in the prese​nce of an antagonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Agonists and antagonists bind to the same receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Agonists and antagonists bind to the same receptor</strong></p><p>In <strong>competitive antagonism</strong>, the drug and the antagonist <strong>compete</strong> to bind to the<strong> same site </strong>of the receptor.</p>\n<p>Agonists if present in high concentration, can displace the antagonists from the active site and overcome the competitive antagonism completely.</p>\n<p>The <strong>dose-response</strong> curve in the case of <strong>competitive antagonism</strong> shifts to the <strong>right.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8346","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ the dose-response curve ‍shown in the image below?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the dose-response curve shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C is competitive antagonis‌t","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C is more p‌otent than A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B is more p‌otent than A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B is competitive antagonis‌t","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B is competitive antagonist","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/1b116b337c9d4636888a2aa8223e3636.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B is competitive antagonist</strong></p><p>In the above image, <strong>drug B</strong> is a <strong>competitive</strong> antagonist.</p>\n<p>In <strong>competitive antagonism,</strong> the DRC is <strong>shifted to the right</strong> whereas in <strong>non-competitive antagonism </strong>the DRC is <strong>flattened</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thus in the above image Drug B is the Competitive antagonist while <strong>drug C</strong> is the <strong>non-competitive</strong> antagonist.</p>\n<p><strong>Potency:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the <strong>amount</strong> of<strong> drug </strong>needed to<strong> produce </strong>a given<strong> response.</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>highly potent drug</strong> evokes a given <strong>response</strong> at<strong> low concentrations</strong> and vice-versa.</li>\n<li>The <strong>more</strong> the<strong> curve</strong> is on the<strong> left </strong>in a dose-response curve (DRC)<strong>, </strong>the<strong> greater</strong> is its<strong> potency.</strong></li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, the potency is drug A &gt; drug C &gt; drug B.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Efficacy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the<strong> maximum effect produced</strong> by the drug.</li>\n<li>It expresses the effect produced by the receptors when bound by a certain drug.</li>\n<li>In the log dose-response curve, the <strong>perpendicular height</strong> represents the drug efficacy.</li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, the efficacy is drug A = drug B &gt; drug C.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4516","text":"Which of the following is ‌a true statement regardin​g inverse agonists?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement regarding inverse agonists?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binds to the receptor and ‌causes maximum action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Binds to the receptor and ‌causes opposite action to​ that of agonist","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Binds to the receptor and ‌causes no action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binds to the receptor and ‌causes submaximal action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Binds to the receptor and causes opposite action to that of agonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Binds to the receptor and causes opposite action to that of agonist</strong></p><p><strong>Inverse agonists</strong> bind to the receptor and cause an action<strong> opposite </strong>to that of an<strong> agonist.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 310px; height: 200px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 155.766px;\">\n<p><strong>Type of drugs</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 138.234px;\">\n<p><strong>Intrinsic activity</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 155.766px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Agonists</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 138.234px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 155.766px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Partial agonists</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 138.234px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>0 to 1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 155.766px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Competitive antagonists</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 138.234px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 155.766px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Inverse agonists</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 31px; width: 138.234px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>0 to -1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The ability of the drug <strong>to bind</strong> with a receptor is called <strong>affinity.</strong></p>\n<p>The capacity of a drug to induce a functional change in the receptor is called intrinsic activity or efficacy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4535","text":"A patient with symptoms of‌ psychosis is prescribed ​aripiprazole. Which of th‍e following best describe​s this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with symptoms of psychosis is prescribed aripiprazole. Which of the following best describes this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No intrinsic activity and ‌high affinity for D2 rece​ptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High intrinsic activity an‌d no affinity for D2 rece​ptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low intrinsic activity and‌ high affinity for D2 rec​eptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High intrinsic activity an‌d low affinity for D2 rec​eptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low intrinsic activity and high affinity for D2 receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low intrinsic activity and high affinity for D2 receptor</strong></p><p>Aripiprazole is a D2 receptor <strong>partial agonist</strong>. Partial agonists have submaximal or <strong>low intrinsic activity</strong> and <strong>high affinity.</strong></p>\n<p>Partial agonists can behave as an antagonist in the presence of an agonist. Partial agonists <strong>block</strong> the <strong>binding</strong> of the <strong>agonist</strong> to the receptor thereby <strong>inhibit</strong>ing a <strong>complete response.</strong></p>\n<p>Aripiprazole has lower activity than dopamine at D2 receptors. By occupying and blocking the action of dopamine on D2 receptors, it acts similar to an antagonist. It is used in conditions like schizophrenia where dopamine antagonist activity is required.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4523","text":"What type of antagonism is‌ shown by drugs A and B i​n the following dose-resp‍onse curve?","raw_text":"What type of antagonism is shown by drugs A and B in the following dose-response curve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Competitive‌ antagonism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Noncompetiti‌ve antagonism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uncompetitiv‌e antagonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannot be ‌determined","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Competitive antagonism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70f71508943a49488767fd1ac5304e71.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6211b2beb2fc444faebd7f4ab50b4f84x1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Competitive antagonism</strong></p><p>The<strong> parallel rightward shift </strong>of the agonist dose-response curve (DRC) is seen with<strong> competitive antagonism.</strong></p>\n<p>Increasing the dose of the agonist can result in the same maximal response.</p>\n<p>In <strong>non-competitive antagonism</strong>, there will be <strong>flattening</strong> of the agonist DRC.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 89\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0607","text":"The graph below shows the ‌relationship between the ​log(dose) and the respons‍e of three drugs, A, B, a​nd C. Which of these drug‍s has the highest potency​?","raw_text":"The graph below shows the relationship between the log(dose) and the response of three drugs, A, B, and C. Which of these drugs has the highest potency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both A‌ and B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b45dbf4ee48b4afaac794cf75bab570a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p><strong>Drug A</strong> has the highest potency.</p>\n<p><strong>Potency:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the <strong>amount </strong>of drug needed to <strong>produce</strong> a<strong> given response. </strong>A <strong>highly potent drug</strong> evokes a given <strong>response </strong>at<strong> low concentrations</strong> and vice-versa.</li>\n<li>The more the curve is on the<strong> left </strong>in a dose-response curve (DRC)<strong>, </strong>the <strong>greater </strong>is its<strong> potency. </strong></li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, the potency is Drug A &gt;Drug B&gt; Drug C.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Efficacy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the<strong> maximum effect</strong> produced by the drug. It reflects the capacity of the drug to activate a receptor and produce the response.</li>\n<li>In the log dose-response curve, the <strong>perpendicular height</strong> represents the drug efficacy.</li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, the efficacy is Drug A = Drug B &gt; Drug C.</li>\n<li>Drugs having a similar pharmacological effect can have different efficacies e.g furosemide and metolazone are both diuretics but furosemide has greater efficacy than metolazone. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3672","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the​ given dose-response curv‍e?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the given dose-response curve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug B is the most potent.‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Drug B is the most efficac‌ious.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drug C is more efficacious‌ than drug D.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug A is more potent than‌ drug B.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Drug B is the most potent.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a55588364afa413da3c7cb8df6bfc3f5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Drug B is the most potent.</strong></p><p>The <strong>more</strong> the<strong> curve</strong> is on the<strong> left </strong>in a dose-response curve (DRC)<strong>,</strong> the<strong> greater</strong> is its<strong> potency. </strong>Hence<strong>, </strong>Drug B is the most potent.</p>\n<p><strong>Potency:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the <strong>amount of drug </strong>needed<strong> to produce a given response.</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>highly potent drug</strong> evokes a given <strong>response at low concentrations</strong> and vice-versa.</li>\n<li>In the above image, the potency is Drug B&gt; Drug A &gt; Drug C &gt; Drug D.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Efficacy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the<strong> maximum effect</strong> produced by the drug. It reflects the <strong>capacity</strong> of the drug <strong>to activate</strong> a receptor and <strong>produce</strong> the <strong>response.</strong></li>\n<li>In the log dose-response curve, the <strong>perpendicular height</strong> represents the drug efficacy.</li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, the efficacy is Drug A = Drug C =Drug D &gt; Drug B.</li>\n<li>Drugs having a similar pharmacological effect can have different efficacies e.g furosemide and metolazone are both diuretics but furosemide has greater efficacy than metolazone. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4515","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the pot​ency and efficacy of a dr‍ug is not true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the potency and efficacy of a drug is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In a clinical setup, effic‌acy is more important tha​n potency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In the log dose response c‌urve, the height of the c​urve corresponds with eff‍icacy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ED50 of the drug correspon‌ds to the efficacy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Drugs that produce a simil‌ar pharmacological effect​ can have different level‍s of efficacy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ED50 of the drug corresponds to the efficacy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12fda29ce9db479e99be322d78892dc3x1280x1196.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/202d58649a0544a9ba61ef918f0a6f03x1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ED50 of the drug corresponds to the efficacy</strong></p><p><strong>ED50 </strong>is a measure of the <strong>drug potency,</strong> not efficacy.</p>\n<p>Potency refers to the amount of the drug required to produce a certain response. The x coordinates (dose axis) on the log dose-response curve (DRC) of a drug represent the potency of the drug. A right shift in the DRC indicates a lower potency and vice versa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Potency</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the <strong>amount of drug </strong>needed<strong> to produce a given response.</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>highly potent drug</strong> evokes a given <strong>response at low concentrations</strong> and vice-versa.</li>\n<li>The efficacy of a drug is a more decisive factor in the choice of a drug in the clinical setting</li>\n<li>The <strong>more the curve shifts towards the left </strong>in a dose-response curve (DRC)<strong>, the greater is its potency.</strong></li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, Drug A is more potent. The potency is  Drug A &gt; Drug B &gt; Drug C &gt; Drug D.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Efficacy</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is defined as the<strong> maximum effect produced</strong> by the drug.</li>\n<li>It expresses the effect produced by the receptors when bound by a certain drug.</li>\n<li>It is mainly a factor in choosing the dose of a drug.</li>\n<li>In the log dose-response curve, the <strong>perpendicular height</strong> represents the drug efficacy.</li>\n<li>Thus, in the above image, efficacy is Drug A &gt;B &gt; C &gt; D.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4914","text":"Which of the following sho‌ws a U-shaped dose-respon​se curve?","raw_text":"Which of the following shows a U-shaped dose-response curve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‌mins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-can‌cer drugs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chel‌ators","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cbb79628483a49e5bce96265d68a0673x1280x1154.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0db32ffbadd42dd9c69b75a9398ff60x1280x1420.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23699bc3b29d46ed9c13c1f6a2abef2cx1280x1282.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamins</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamins</strong> and <strong>essential metals</strong> show a <strong>U-shaped dose-response curve</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 74\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>At a <strong>low dose,</strong> adverse effects are observed because there is a <strong>deficiency</strong> of these nutrients to maintain homeostasis. As <strong>dose increases, homeostasis</strong> is achieved, and the bottom of the U-shaped dose-response curve is reached. As the <strong>dose increases</strong> to surpass the amount required to maintain homeostasis, <strong>toxicity</strong> can ensue.</p>\n<p>Thus, <strong>adverse effects</strong> are seen at both <strong>low</strong> and<strong> high doses. </strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Hockey stick-shaped dose-response</strong> curve for <strong>toxicants</strong> that are also metabolic by-products. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Inverted U-shaped</strong> dose-response curve for <strong>ligands</strong> that <strong>downregulate their receptors.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6481","text":"What does the area between‌ the dotted lines in this​ graph indicate?","raw_text":"What does the area between the dotted lines in this graph indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median eff‌ective dose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median le‌thal dose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Therapeu‌tic index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Therapeut‌ic window","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Therapeutic window","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13fba814599e4468b06f6230f1b67101.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Therapeutic window</strong></p><p>The area between the dotted lines in the graph shows the<strong> therapeutic window</strong>. It is the <strong>range</strong> of <strong>steady-state concentrations</strong> of a drug that provides <strong>therapeutic efficacy</strong> with <strong>minimal toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> therapeutic index</strong> (TI) describes a relationship between the doses of a drug that causes lethal or toxic effects with the dose that causes therapeutic effects. It is also called the therapeutic ratio.</p>\n<p>Mathematically, you can calculate TI by following way:</p>\n<p>Therapeutic Index =<strong> LD<sub>50</sub>/ED<sub>50</sub></strong> (or) Therapeutic Index =<strong> TD<sub>50</sub>/ED<sub>50</sub></strong></p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>LD<sub>50</sub></strong> (Median Lethal dose) is the minimum amount of drug that causes adverse effects in 50% of the population. <strong>LD<sub>50</sub></strong> could also be replaced with Toxic dose (<strong>TD<sub>50</sub></strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>ED<sub>50</sub></strong> (Median Effective dose) is the quantity of a drug that can produce desired therapeutic effects in 50% of the population.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3486","text":"Therapeutic index is used ‌for assessing which of th​e following factors?","raw_text":"Therapeutic index is used for assessing which of the following factors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saf‌ety","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Effi‌cacy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pot‌ency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxi‌city","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Safety","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Safety</strong></p><p><strong>Therapeutic index</strong> is used to assess the <strong>safety </strong>of the drug administered.</p>\n<p>Therapeutic index = TD50/ED50 </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TD50</strong> (Median toxic dose) = Dose required to produce a toxic effect in 50% of animals tested.</li>\n<li><strong>ED50</strong> (Median effect dose) = Dose required to produce a therapeutic effect in 50% of the individuals.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Efficacy</strong> is defined as the<strong> maximum effect produced</strong> by the drug. It expresses the effect produced by the receptors when bound by a certain drug.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Potency </strong>refers to the <strong>concentration</strong> (EC50) or dose (ED50) <strong>required</strong> to <strong>produce 50%</strong> of <strong>maximum effect</strong> where EC50 is the concentration of agonist resulting in a half-maximal response.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Median toxic dose</strong> is the dose required to produce a <strong>toxic effect</strong> in <strong>50%</strong> of <strong>animals tested.</strong> Lethal dose is the dose required to kill 50% of animals tested.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4527","text":"Therapeutic index is calcu‌lated with which of the f​ollowing equations?","raw_text":"Therapeutic index is calculated with which of the following equations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ED50‌/LD50","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LD50‌/ED50","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ED50‌-LD50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ED50 ‌X LD50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. LD50/ED50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1355a1cb0a574d2e9c6bd3789144d0e4x1280x1420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. LD50/ED50</strong></p><p><strong>Therapeutic index </strong>is calculated with the following equation =<strong> LD50/ED50.</strong></p>\n<p>Therapeutic index is used to assess the <strong>safety </strong>of the drug administered.</p>\n<p><strong>LD50</strong> (median lethal dose) refers to the dose of the drug at which <strong>50%</strong> of the experimental<strong> animals die.</strong> Larger the LD50, the safer the drug. In humans, <strong>TD50</strong> is considered. TD50 refers to the dose of the drug that causes a <strong>toxic response</strong> in <strong>50%</strong> of the <strong>population.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>ED50</strong> (median effective dose) refers to the dose of the drug at which <strong>50%</strong> of the experimental <strong>animals</strong> show the <strong>specific</strong> or <strong>desired effect</strong></p>\n<p>Clinically, the therapeutic range <strong>bound</strong> by the dose that produces the minimal <strong>necessary</strong> therapeutic effect and the dose that produces the maximal <strong>acceptable</strong> adverse effect is more relevant.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2315","text":"Calculate the therapeutic ‌index of a drug whose dos​e-response curves are plo‍tted as shown below:","raw_text":"Calculate the therapeutic index of a drug whose dose-response curves are plotted as shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/622c455438be4ab480ed5ee698509c1f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4</strong></p><p>The <strong>therapeutic index</strong> is <strong>4</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the above graph,</p>\n<p>Median lethal dose (LD50) = 400</p>\n<p>Median effective dose (ED50) = 100</p>\n<p><strong>Therapeutic index (TI)</strong> =<strong> LD50/ED50.</strong></p>\n<p>Hence,</p>\n<p>Therapeutic index (TI ) = 400/100 = 4</p>\n<p>Clinically, the therapeutic range bound by the dose that produces the minimal therapeutic effect and the dose that produces the maximal acceptable adverse effect is more relevant.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4529","text":"Therapeutic drug monitorin‌g is required in all exce​pt ______.","raw_text":"Therapeutic drug monitoring is required in all except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tacro‌limus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cylcos‌porine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metformin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metformin</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin</strong> does not require therapeutic monitoring because it<strong> does not have </strong>a<strong> narrow therapeutic index</strong> and the effect on blood sugar levels can be easily measured.</p>\n<p>Phenytoin, tacrolimus, and cyclosporine have the narrow therapeutic index and therapeutic monitoring is necessary to avoid adverse effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4538","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about pharmacogen​etics is correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about pharmacogenetics is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It refers to the effect of‌ drugs on genetic express​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It refers to the genetic b‌asis of variability in dr​ug response","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is not synonymous with ‌pharmacogenomics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug metabolism is not a h‌ighly heritable trait","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It refers to the genetic basis of variability in drug response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It refers to the genetic basis of variability in drug response</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacogenetics</strong> is the study of the <strong>genetic basis</strong> for <strong>variability</strong> in <strong>drug action.</strong></p>\n<p>Genetic differences cause variability in the<strong> dose-response curve</strong> (DRC) due to alteration in drug action and disposition in the body.</p>\n<p>The term is used interchangeably with <strong>pharmacogenomics.</strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Drug metabolism is <strong>highly heritable</strong>, as assessed using drug exposure studies in monozygotic versus fraternal twins and from other related subjects.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4540","text":"A 23-year-old male is diag‌nosed with G6PD deficienc​y. Which of the following‍ drugs is not contraindic​ated in his condition?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old male is diagnosed with G6PD deficiency. Which of the following drugs is not contraindicated in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prog‌uanil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nalidix‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftr‌iaxone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ceftriaxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ceftriaxone</strong></p><p><strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> is not contraindicated in G6PD deficient individuals as it does <strong>not</strong> possess <strong>oxidizing property</strong>, hence there is <strong>no hemolysis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> having the <strong>potential</strong> to cause <strong>hemolysis</strong> in <strong>G6PD</strong> deficient individuals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-malarials - Primaquine, chloroquine, quinine, pyrimethamine, <strong>proguanil</strong></li>\n<li>Sulfonamides - <strong>Cotrimoxazole</strong></li>\n<li>Fluoroquinolones - <strong>Nalidixic acid</strong></li>\n<li>Nitro furan antibacterial - Furazolidone, nitrofurantoin</li>\n<li>Anti-hypertensives - Methyldopa, hydralazine</li>\n<li>Anti-convulsants - Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Anti-arrythmics - Procainamide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"I__bzfKL","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"2_cellular_messengers___receptors","title":"2.Cellular Messengers & Receptors","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA5247","text":"Which of the following is ​an example of a polypepti​de hormone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of a polypeptide hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parath​ormone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adren​aline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parathormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parathormone</strong></p><p><strong>Parathyroid hormone</strong> (PTH) is an example of a <strong>polypeptide</strong> hormone.</p>\n<p><strong>Cortisol</strong> is a <strong>steroid</strong> hormone. <strong>Adrenaline and thyroxine</strong> are derivatives of the amino acid <strong>tyrosine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7657","text":"Which hormone crosses the ​plasma membrane for its a​ction?","raw_text":"Which hormone crosses the plasma membrane for its action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gluc​agon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thyroxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thyroxine</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroxine</strong> crosses the<strong> plasma membrane</strong> for its action.</p>\n<p><strong>Thyroid hormones</strong> enter the <strong>nucleus</strong> and bind to <strong>thyroid hormone receptors</strong>. The hormone-receptor complex in the nucleus <strong>binds</strong> to<strong> DNA</strong> via zinc fingers. This increases the expression of a variety of different genes that code for proteins regulating cell function.  Although thyroxine is an AA derivative, there are transporters to move it into the cell, hence they are intracellular receptors acting on the nucleus.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine, glucagon, and insulin bind to cell surface receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5251","text":"Which of the following hor​mone is not stored before​ release?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormone is not stored before release?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cort​isol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Re​nin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cortisol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cortisol</strong></p><p>Cortisol is not stored before release.</p>\n<p>Usually, the <strong>polypeptide and protein hormones</strong> such as insulin, thyroxine or renin are<strong> stored in secretory vesicles</strong> before release. They are synthesized as much larger polypeptide chains (preprohormones) that are biologically inactive and then processed intracellularly by specific proteases to yield the final hormone molecule which is stored in the secretory vesicle until needed.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>steroid hormones</strong> such as cortisol are synthesised as needed and are <strong>not stored</strong>. They are lipid soluble and easily diffuse across cell membrane to enter the interstitial fluid and then blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5252","text":"Downregulation of receptor​s occurs due to all excep​t_____","raw_text":"Downregulation of receptors occurs due to all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inactivation​ of receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interna​lisation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desensi​tisation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased level of stimula​ting hormone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased level of stimulating hormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased level of stimulating hormone</strong></p><p>Downregulation of receptors occurs due to internalisation, inactivation of receptors or desensitisation.</p>\n<p>A <strong>decrease</strong> in the level of stimulating <strong>hormones</strong> generally leads to an <strong>increase</strong> in the level of <strong>active receptors</strong>. This is called <strong>upregulation</strong> of receptors.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, an <strong>increase</strong> in the level of stimulating <strong>hormones</strong> generally leads to a <strong>decrease</strong> in the level of <strong>active receptors</strong>. This is called <strong>downregulation</strong> of receptors.</p>\n<p>Downregulation of receptors can occur by one of the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Desensitisation</strong> - Receptors undergo chemical modifications that make them less responsive.</li>\n<li><strong>Internalisation</strong> - It is the temporary sequestration of the cell membrane receptors to the inside of the cell, away from the action of hormones. Following internalisation, the receptors are either destroyed or recycled.</li>\n<li><strong>Inactivation</strong> of receptors.</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased production</strong> of receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5254","text":"Receptors on cell membrane​ that activate ion channe​l after binding with agon​ists are_____","raw_text":"Receptors on cell membrane that activate ion channel after binding with agonists are_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nicotinic ​cholinergic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscarinic ​cholinergic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opioid μ ​receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GABAB r​eceptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nicotinic cholinergic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nicotinic cholinergic</strong></p><p><strong>Nicotinic cholinergic</strong> receptors activate an ion channel after binding with its agonist. These receptors are called <strong>ligand-gated channels</strong> or <strong>ionotropic</strong> receptors.</p>\n<p>Receptors for neurotransmitters are classified into two broad families based on structure and function. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ligand-gated channels (Ionotropic receptors)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>When bound to these receptors, a membrane channel is opened leading to a brief increase in ionic conductance.</li>\n<li>Important for <strong>fast synaptic</strong> transmission.</li>\n<li>Examples - GABA<sub>A</sub>, GABA<sub>C</sub>, <strong>Nicotinic cholinergic</strong>, Glycine and Serotonin (5HT<sub>3</sub> subtype).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>G-protein coupled receptors (Metabotropic receptors)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>When bound to these receptors, secondary messengers are released that modulate voltage-gated channels on neuronal membranes.</li>\n<li>Examples - <strong>GABA<sub>B</sub></strong>, <strong>Muscarinic cholinergic</strong>, Catecholamines, Serotonin (all subtypes except 5HT<sub>3</sub>) and <strong>opioid</strong> receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5261","text":"Adrenaline, noradrenaline ​and dopamine act through_​____","raw_text":"Adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine act through_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 pass ​receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 pass ​receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 pass ​receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7 pass ​receptor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 7 pass receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 7 pass receptor</strong></p><p>Adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine act through a <strong>7 pass receptor</strong> called <strong>G-protein coupled receptor</strong> (GPCR).</p>\n<p>The catecholamines act through GPCRs which are<strong> cell membrane receptors</strong> that span across the membrane 7 times hence called seven helix or serpentine receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5257","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about the​ G-protein-coupled recept​or (GPCR)?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the G-protein-coupled receptor (GPCR)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G proteins bind to hormone​s on the surface of the n​ucleus.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All the three subunits α, ​β, and ɣ should bind to e​ach other for G proteins ​to act.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G proteins act as inhibito​ry or excitatory because ​of differences in α subun​it.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"G protein is bound to GTP ​in resting state.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. G proteins act as inhibitory or excitatory because of differences in α subunit.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff5a258469fa43508a4fdf743a1388b1x1280x1086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. G proteins act as inhibitory or excitatory because of differences in α subunit.</strong></p><p>G proteins act as <strong>inhibitory or excitatory</strong> because of differences in <strong>α subunit.</strong></p>\n<p>G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) are<strong> cell membrane receptors</strong> that span across the membrane 7 times hence called seven helix or serpentine receptors.</p>\n<p>G proteins have 3 subunits (α,β and ɣ). In its resting state, <strong>α subunit is bound to GDP. </strong></p>\n<p>Upon<strong> ligand binding to GPCR</strong>, it undergoes a conformational change that activates G protein within the cell. The <strong>α subunit dissociates</strong> and in turn alters the activity of <strong>second messengers</strong> such as cAMP, cGMP or Ca<sup>2+</sup>.</p>\n<p>Once the <strong>ligand is unbound</strong>, the intrinsic GTPase activity of the <strong>α subunit</strong> converts <strong>GTP to GDP</strong>. This leads to <strong>reassociation</strong> of α with the β-ɣ subunit and termination of effector activation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5258","text":"Action of α subunit of G p​rotein is_____","raw_text":"Action of α subunit of G protein is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breakdown o​f GTP to GDP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Conversion o​f GDP to GTP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internalization of recepto​rs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binding o​f agonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Breakdown of GTP to GDP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70e7f431d1a04589aad93f28a8cb74bex1280x1689.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Breakdown of GTP to GDP</strong></p><p>The action of α subunit of G protein is the <strong>breakdown of GTP to GDP </strong>as it has an intrinsic GTPase activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Guanine exchange factors</strong> (GEFs) convert <strong>GDP to GTP</strong> by nucleotide exchange.</p>\n<p>G proteins can be either <strong>stimulatory (G<sub>S</sub>) or inhibitory (G<sub>i</sub>)</strong> with respect to the production of the 2nd messenger <strong>cAMP</strong> by the enzyme adenylyl cyclase. Stimulatory or inhibitory activity resides in the <strong>α subunit.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cholera toxin</strong> acts on α subunit of <strong>G<sub>S</sub></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pertussis toxin</strong> acts on α subunit of <strong>G<sub>i</sub></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5263","text":"PDGF acts through which of​ the following signaling ​pathways?","raw_text":"PDGF acts through which of the following signaling pathways?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cA​MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IP3​-DAG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"cG​MP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyrosin​e kinase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tyrosine kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tyrosine kinase</strong></p><p><strong>PDGF</strong> (platelet-derived growth factor) acts through the activation of <strong>tyrosine kinases</strong>.</p>\n<p>All growth factors that foster the multiplication or development of various types of cells act through the activation of tyrosine kinases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5264","text":"Which hormone acts on JAK-​STAT kinase receptor?","raw_text":"Which hormone acts on JAK-STAT kinase receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​SH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G​H","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"F​SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GH</strong></p><p><strong>Growth hormone</strong> acts on <strong>JAK-STAT</strong> receptors.</p>\n<p>Janus tyrosine kinase receptors (JAK-STAT) serves as receptors for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Growth hormone</li>\n<li>Prolactin</li>\n<li>Erythropoietin</li>\n<li>Leptin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thyroxine acts on nuclear receptors. TSH and FSH act on G protein-coupled receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5250","text":"All of the following act t​hrough cell surface recep​tors except____","raw_text":"All of the following act through cell surface receptors except____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cort​isol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F​SH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T​SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cortisol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cortisol</strong></p><p><strong>Cortisol</strong> is a steroid hormone and acts on <strong>intracellular (cytoplasmic) receptor.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5265","text":"Steroid hormone receptors ​have an attachment site f​or all except_____","raw_text":"Steroid hormone receptors have an attachment site for all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steroid​ hormone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transcriptio​n repressors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hormone-responsive element​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transcriptio​n activators","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transcription repressors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81649ce14821441e8e3b3bd497719bfdx1280x1630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transcription repressors</strong></p><p><strong>Steroid hormone receptors</strong> have an attachment site for steroid hormone, hormone-responsive element, and <strong>transcription activators</strong>, but not transcription repressors.</p>\n<p>Transcription repressors are proteins that bind to specific sites on DNA and prevent transcription of nearby genes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5266","text":"True statement about secon​d messengers is _____","raw_text":"True statement about second messengers is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are substances that intera​ct with first messengers ​outside cells.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mediate the intracellular ​responses to many differe​nt hormones and neurotran​smitters","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Are hormones secreted by c​ells in response to stimu​lation by another hormone​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Are substances that bind t​o first messengers in the​ cell membrane.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mediate the intracellular responses to many different hormones and neurotransmitters","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mediate the intracellular responses to many different hormones and neurotransmitters</strong></p><p><strong>Second messengers</strong> mediate the<strong> intracellular responses</strong> to many different hormones and neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p><strong>Cell surface receptors</strong> are substances that <strong>interact with the first messengers</strong> which are hormones and neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p>The <strong>second messengers</strong> are the <strong>intracellular signalling</strong> molecules. They bring about many <strong>short-term changes in cell function</strong> by altering enzyme function, triggering exocytosis, and other physiological effects. They can also lead to the alteration of <strong>transcription</strong> of various genes (long term effect).</p>\n<p>Examples of second messengers are <strong>cAMP, cGMP, Ca<sup>2+ </sup>and IP<sub>3</sub>-DAG</strong> (Inositol triphosphate and diacylglycerol).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5273","text":"Which one of the following​ acts as a second messeng​er?","raw_text":"Which one of the following acts as a second messenger?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mg​2+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​l-","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca​2+","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PO​43-","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ca2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ca2+</strong></p><p>Among the following <strong>calcium</strong> (Ca<sup>2+</sup>) acts as a <strong>second messenger.</strong> </p>\n<p>Increased cytoplasmic Ca<sup>2+ </sup>binds to and activates <strong>calcium-binding proteins</strong> (calmodulin, troponin) or activate <strong>protein kinases</strong> (or phospholipase C) to bring about physiological effects.</p>\n<p>Cytoplasmic Ca<sup>2+ </sup>is increased by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct<strong> entry of Ca<sup>2+</sup> from the ECF</strong> through Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels that could be ligand-gated, voltage-gated or stretch-activated (mechanically gated).</li>\n<li>Mobilisation of Ca<sup>2+</sup> that are stored in high concentrations in the endoplasmic reticulum by <strong>other second messengers such as IP<sub>3</sub>-DAG</strong> (Inositol triphosphate and diacylglycerol).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5270","text":"A primigravida with 38 com​pleted weeks of gestation​ has gone into labor. Oxy​tocin was started to augm‌ent labor. The second mes​senger system through whi‍ch oxytocin acts is _____​","raw_text":"A primigravida with 38 completed weeks of gestation has gone into labor. Oxytocin was started to augment labor. The second messenger system through which oxytocin acts is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cA​MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phospho​lipase c","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"cG​MP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyrosin​e kinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phospholipase c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phospholipase c</strong></p><p>The second messenger system through which <strong>oxytocin</strong> acts is the <strong>phospholipase C</strong> system.</p>\n<p>It activates the enzyme <strong>phospholipase C</strong> which breaks down some phospholipids in the cell membrane into <strong>IP</strong><sub><strong>3</strong> </sub>(phosphoinositol triphosphate) and <strong>DAG</strong> (diacylglycerol). <strong>DAG</strong> then activates <strong>protein kinase C</strong> and <strong>IP<sub>3</sub></strong> mobilises <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> from mitochondria and endoplasmic reticulum to bring about <strong>smooth muscle contraction</strong> and other <strong>cellular responses.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5268","text":"cAMP is the exclusive seco​nd messenger for all the ​following except ________​_","raw_text":"cAMP is the exclusive second messenger for all the following except _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cortic​otropin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gluc​agon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vasopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vasopressin</strong></p><p><strong>Vasopressin</strong> does not use cAMP exclusively.</p>\n<p>Vasopressin (ADH) acts on at least 3 kinds of receptors: <strong>V<sub>1A</sub>, V<sub>1B </sub></strong><sub>and</sub> <strong>V<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All the 3 are G-protein coupled receptors</li>\n<li><strong>V<sub>1A </sub>and V<sub>1B </sub></strong>receptors (Gq)  act through <strong>IP3</strong> as a second messenger, to increase the intracellular calcium concentration</li>\n<li><strong>V<sub>2 </sub></strong>receptors (Gs) use <strong>cAMP</strong> as a second messenger</li>\n</ul>\n<p>cAMP is the exclusive second messenger for dopamine, corticotropin and glucagon.</p>\n<p>Dopamine has 5 receptors (D<sub>1</sub>, D<sub>2</sub>, D<sub>3</sub>, D<sub>4</sub>, D<sub>5</sub>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>all use cAMP as second messengers</li>\n<li>D<sub>1</sub>, D<sub>5</sub> act by increasing cAMP</li>\n<li>D<sub>2</sub>, D<sub>3</sub>, D<sub>4</sub> act by decreasing cAMP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0112","text":"cGMP is the second messeng​er of","raw_text":"cGMP is the second messenger of","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somato​statin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiote​nsin II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​DH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NO (nitr​ic oxide)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NO (nitric oxide)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NO (nitric oxide)</strong></p><p>Vasodilatory actions of <strong>NO</strong> are mediated by <strong>cGMP</strong>.   </p>\n<p>NO is a hormone derived from the amino acid <strong>arginine. </strong>It activates <strong>guanylyl cyclases</strong> in vascular smooth muscle cells resulting in the conversion of <strong>cGTP to cGMP</strong> (2nd messenger). <strong>cGMP</strong> activates cGMP dependent <strong>protein</strong> <strong>kinase</strong>, which causes blood vessels to relax. </p>\n<p><strong>cGMP</strong> is the <strong>second messenger</strong> for nitric oxide and <strong>atrial natriuretic factor.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5267","text":"Which of the following is ​not a second messenger?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a second messenger?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Guanylyl​ cyclase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"cA​MP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I​P3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D​AG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Guanylyl cyclase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Guanylyl cyclase</strong></p><p><strong>Guanylyl cyclase</strong> is the <strong>enzyme</strong> that catalyzes the formation of cGMP. It is <strong>cGMP</strong> that acts as the <strong>second messenger.</strong></p>\n<p>cAMP, IP<sub>3</sub> and DAG are all second messengers.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5279","text":"Activation of protein kina​se C causes_____","raw_text":"Activation of protein kinase C causes_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased adenylyl cyclase​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphorylation of protein​s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increas​ed cAMP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ion channel cond​uction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phosphorylation of proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phosphorylation of proteins</strong></p><p>Activation of protein kinase C causes phosphorylation of proteins</p>\n<p>Cellular Phosphorylation is under the control of two groups of proteins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kinases</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>enzymes that catalyse the <strong>phosphorylation</strong> of tyrosine or serine residues in protein</li>\n<li>Example- Protein Kinase A, Protein Kinase C, Ca<sup>2+/</sup>Calmodulin Kinase</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Phosphatases</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>that catalyses the <strong>dephosphorylation </strong>of tyrosine or serine residues in the protein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Kinases play important roles in regulating cellular physiology, including cell growth and cell death. <strong>Philadelphia chromosome</strong>, t(9:22) or the <strong>bcr-abl</strong> fusion gene codes for an <strong>abnormal kinase</strong> that leads to <strong>chronic myeloid leukaemia</strong> (CML). The drug<strong> imatinib</strong> was developed to <strong>block the tyrosine kinase activity of bcr-abl</strong> protein and is an effective treatment option for CML.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5284","text":"A 7-year-old girl presents​ with skin lesions as sho​wn in the image. The pare​nts also complain of earl‌y breast development, gro​wth acceleration, and vag‍inal bleeding. On genetic​ studies, it was found th​at there is a gain of fun​ction mutation of G Prote​in. What is the most prob​able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl presents with skin lesions as shown in the image. The parents also complain of early breast development, growth acceleration, and vaginal bleeding. On genetic studies, it was found that there is a gain of function mutation of G Protein. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Color b​lindness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hirschspru​ng disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McCune-Albri​ght syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Familial hy​pothyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. McCune-Albright syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98a8a0dde9b4410292cc4e912ff23f19.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. McCune-Albright syndrome</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the most probable diagnosis is <strong>McCune-Albright syndrome</strong> as it is the only <strong>gain-of-function</strong> mutation of <strong>G Protein.</strong></p>\n<p>G proteins or G protein-coupled receptor complexes can undergo a loss- or gain-of-function mutation. Some examples are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 488.5833435058594px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">Type of mutation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">Disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Cone opsin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\"><strong>Loss</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\"><strong>Colour blindness</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Rhodopsin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">Loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">Night blindness or retinitis pigmentosa</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Endothelin receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\"><strong>Loss</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\"><strong>Hirschsprung disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">TSH receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\"><strong>Loss</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">Familial <strong>hypo</strong>thyroidism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">TSH receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\"><strong>Gain</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">Familial <strong>hyper</strong>thyroidism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">LH receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">Gain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">Familial male precocious puberty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">G<sub>s</sub> protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">Gain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">Pituitary adenomas with acromegaly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">G<sub>s</sub> protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">Gain (mosaic)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308.5833435058594px;\">McCune-Albright syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In <strong>McCune-Albright syndrome</strong>, a gain-of-function mutation in G<sub>s</sub>α is associated with the rough-bordered areas of <strong>skin pigmentation and hypercortisolism</strong>. This mutation occurs during fetal development, creating a <strong>mosaic</strong> of normal and abnormal cells.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3419","text":"Nuclear signalling pathway​s are all except","raw_text":"Nuclear signalling pathways are all except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cave​olins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NF​-kB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impo​rtins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rat pr​oteins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rat proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rat proteins</strong></p><p><strong>Rat proteins</strong> are not nuclear signalling pathways. Recombinant rat IFN- is a bioactive protein intended for use in cell culture applications.</p>\n<p><strong>Importin:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transporting protein molecules into nucleus by binding to specific recognition sequences, called Nuclear Localization Sequences (NLS).</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">2 subunits, importin α and importin β.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Caveolins</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Family of integral membrane proteins that are principal components of caveolae membranes</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Involved in receptor-independent endocytosis.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Acts as scaffolding proteins within caveolar membranes by compartmentalizing and concentrating signaling molecules.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>NF-kB</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part of a prototypical proinflammatory signaling pathway</li>\n<li>based on the role of NF-κB in the expression of proinflammatory genes including cytokines, chemokines, and adhesion molecules</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fI3ECQbc","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"2_symptoms_and_clinical_manifestations_in_psychiatry","title":"2.Symptoms And Clinical Manifestations In Psychiatry","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA3814","text":"A patient is mute and immo‍bile but conscious. He is​ unresponsive to the envi​ronment. How would you de‍scribe his state?","raw_text":"A patient is mute and immobile but conscious. He is unresponsive to the environment. How would you describe his state?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Akineti‍c mutism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stu‍por","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"So‍por","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Twiligh‍t state","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stupor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stupor</strong></p><p>A patient is said to be in a <strong>stupor</strong> when he is <strong>conscious</strong> but <strong>mute</strong>, <strong>immobile,</strong> and <strong>unresponsive</strong> to the environment.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 389px; width: 283px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.8281px;\"><strong>Stupor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4688px;\"><strong>Sopor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.453px;\"><strong>Coma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Consciousness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.8281px;\"><strong>Conscious,</strong> but mute and immobile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4688px;\"><strong>Drowsy,</strong> abnormally deep sleep, difficult to arouse</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.453px;\"><strong>Unconscious,</strong> cannot be awakened</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Response to the environment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.8281px;\">No/Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4688px;\">Responds to harsh sound, bright light</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.453px;\">No </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Response to painful stimulus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.8281px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78.4688px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.453px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1436\"> </div>\n<p>Option A: Akinetic mutism: The person is mute and immobile but responsive to the environment.</p>\n<p>Option D: Twilight state: A state of disturbed consciousness with hallucinations, during which actions may be performed unconsciously.</p>\n<p>Note: The oneiroid state is a dream-like state in which patients may be deeply confused and not fully oriented to time and place. The term oneiroid schizophrenia has been used for patients who are engaged in their hallucinatory experiences to the point of exclusion of involvement in the real world.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3355","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an example of disorde​r of perception?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an example of disorder of perception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deperson‍alization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halluc‍ination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudohal‍lucination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delu‍sion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Delusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Delusion</strong></p><p><strong>Delusion</strong> is a disturbance of<strong> thought content</strong>.</p>\n<p>Disorders of perception:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Illusion - incorrect perception or <strong>misinterpretation</strong> of a sensory <strong>stimulus.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hallucination - <strong>perception</strong> in the <strong>absence</strong> of any <strong>stimulus.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Depersonalization - feeling of being a detached observer of oneself.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Derealization - feeling that one's <strong>environment</strong> has <strong>changed</strong> in some strange way that is difficult to describe.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 236px; width: 382px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.203125px;\">\n<p><strong>True hallucination</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.8125px;\">\n<p><strong>Pseudohallucination</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.203125px;\">\n<p>Arises from <strong>external objective</strong> space<strong> </strong>(voices heard in one's ears arising from the outside world)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.8125px;\">\n<p>Arises from <strong>inner subjective </strong>space<strong> </strong>(voices from within one's own mind)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.203125px;\">\n<p>Cannot be recognized as unreal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.8125px;\">\n<p>Can be recognized as unreal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Pseudohallucination is neither a symptom nor a feature of any perception disorder. Hence, delusion is the best answer among the given options.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3366","text":"A girl wakes the entire ho‍stel dormitory screaming ​about a snake in the bath​room. The matron rushes i‍n and sees her pointing a​t a rope. Which of the fo​llowing best describes th​is?","raw_text":"A girl wakes the entire hostel dormitory screaming about a snake in the bathroom. The matron rushes in and sees her pointing at a rope. Which of the following best describes this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Illu‍sion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Halluc‍ination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delu‍sion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudohal‍lucination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Illusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Illusion</strong></p><p><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">In the above scenario, a rope being mistaken for a snake is an <strong>illusion.</strong> </span><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Illusions are perceptions that occur when a <strong>sensory stimulus</strong> is present but is incorrectly perceived and <strong>misinterpreted.</strong> </span></p>\n<table style=\"height: 424px; width: 412px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 27px;\">Delusion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.359375px; height: 27px;\">Hallucination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.640625px; height: 27px;\">Illusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 107px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 107px;\">A<strong> fixed false belief </strong>that is firmly held despite obvious contradictory evidence and that is not consistent with the person's social, educational, and cultural background.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.359375px; height: 107px;\">Perception in the <strong>absence</strong> of any external sensory <strong>stimulus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.640625px; height: 107px;\">Incorrect perception or <strong>misinterpretation</strong> of a sensory <strong>stimulus.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 54px;\">Eg: A belief that the person is a snake god.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.359375px; height: 54px;\">Eg: There is nothing in front of you but you can see a snake.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.640625px; height: 54px;\">Eg: There is a rope in front of you but you see it as a snake.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3819","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about ps​eudohallucination?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about pseudohallucination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Originates from the inner ‍subjective space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cannot be recognised as un‍real","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cannot be produced at will‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"May be vivi‍d and clear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cannot be recognised as unreal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cannot be recognised as unreal</strong></p><p>Pseudohallucinations can be<strong> recognized as unreal.</strong></p>\n<p>A<strong> pseudohallucination</strong> is an example of false perception heard within one's own mind <strong>(inner subjective space)</strong> rather than perceived as coming from the outside world (outer objective space). </p>\n<p>They lack the substantiality of perceptions, are seen in full consciousness, and can be recognized as unreal<strong> </strong>even though they are <strong>clear</strong> and <strong>vivid. </strong>Like true hallucinations these are <strong>involuntary</strong> and cannot be produced at will.</p>\n<p>The presence of pseudohallucinations does not necessarily indicate psychopathology, unlike true hallucinations which are indicative of mental illness.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 302px; width: 359px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.8125px;\">\n<p><strong>True hallucination</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.1875px;\">\n<p><strong>Pseudohallucination</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.8125px;\">\n<p>Arises from <strong>external objective</strong> space<strong> </strong>(voices heard in one's ears arising from the outside world)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.1875px;\">\n<p>Arises from <strong>inner subjective </strong>space<strong> </strong>(voices from within one's own mind)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.8125px;\">\n<p>Cannot be recognized as unreal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.1875px;\">\n<p>Can be recognized as unreal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3822","text":"A 38-year-old woman compla‍ined of an ongoing vibrat​ing sensation in her geni​talia caused by her mothe‍r opening and shutting do​ors. Which of the followi​ng terms best describes h​er symptoms?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman complained of an ongoing vibrating sensation in her genitalia caused by her mother opening and shutting doors. Which of the following terms best describes her symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Functional h‍allucination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reflex hal‍lucination","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panoramic h‍allucination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudohal‍lucination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reflex hallucination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reflex hallucination</strong></p><p>The patient is experiencing <strong>reflex hallucinations.</strong> In this scenario, a sensory stimulus in one modality (auditory) is resulting in a sensory experience in a different modality (vibratory).</p>\n<p>Option A: Functional hallucinations are triggered by a stimulus in the <strong>same modality</strong>, and co-occur with it. E.g., a patient may report that he hears voices criticizing him (auditory hallucination) every time he hears the sound of a rotating fan (auditory stimulus), which stops when the fan is not running.</p>\n<p>Option C: <span class=\"w\">Panoramic hallucination is a compound hallucination in which the <strong>entire</strong> sensory <strong>input</strong> is replaced by <strong>hallucinatory</strong> perceptions, thus giving rise to a totally different perceptual reality.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"w\">Option D: Pseudohallucination is a false perception heard within one's own mind (inner subjective space) rather than perceived as coming from the outside world (outer objective space), and is recognized as unreal.</span></p>\n<p>Other special types of hallucinations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypnogogic hallucination- occurs prior to falling asleep (HypnoGogic: Going to sleep).</li>\n<li>Hypnopompic hallucination- occurs prior to waking up.</li>\n<li>Extracampine hallucination-<strong> </strong>experienced outside the limits of one's sensory field. E.g., a patient in Delhi hearing voices from Chennai.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3529","text":"A 24-year-old woman with a‍ known cerebral vascular ​anomaly and seizures pres​ented to your OPD. She gi‍ves a two-week history of​ objects around her appea​ring to be larger than th​ey actually were, and tha‍t she noticed deformation‌s in objects. What is the‌ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman with a known cerebral vascular anomaly and seizures presented to your OPD. She gives a two-week history of objects around her appearing to be larger than they actually were, and that she noticed deformations in objects. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klein Levi‍n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kanner ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alice in Wonderland syndro‍me","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pickwickia‍n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alice in Wonderland syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1965f57bf69442fba13c6812a4a269bx1280x1574.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alice in Wonderland syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Perceptual distortion</strong> of shape, size, color and reciprocal position of objects is known as <strong>Alice in Wonderland syndrome (AIWS)</strong>. It is seen in schizophrenia and migraine. AIWS may be secondary to seizures associated with an arteriovenous malformation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: Kleine-Levin syndrome is characterized by hypersomnia, hyperphagia, and hypersexuality. </p>\n<p>Option B: Kanner syndrome is characterized by the failure to develop an attachment with a parental figure and a preoccupation with inanimate objects.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pickwickian syndrome is sleep apnea associated with hypersomnia, in elderly and obese persons. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3357","text":"You are interviewing a pat‍ient online. On asking th​e patient how she has bee​n, she replies \"I'm doing‍ gooder, gerkschier\". Her​ attender also informs th​at she uses such made-up ​words often. What disorde‍r of thought is she exhib‌iting?","raw_text":"You are interviewing a patient online. On asking the patient how she has been, she replies \"I'm doing gooder, gerkschier\". Her attender also informs that she uses such made-up words often. What disorder of thought is she exhibiting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Content o‍f thought","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Form of‍ thought","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stream o‍f thought","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Possession‍ of thought","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Form of thought","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Form of thought</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, the patient is exhibiting <strong>neologism.</strong> Neologism is a disorder of the <strong>form of thought.</strong> This refers to making new words (gooder, gerkschier) that do not exist and using them meaninglessly. It is usually seen in <strong>schizophrenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3866","text":"A 27-year-old man who rece‍ntly read about the Pegas​us controversy feels that​ he is being tracked by g‍overnment officials. He t​hinks that someone is rec​ording his movements, and​ is plotting to kill him.‍ Which of the following s‌tatements is true about h‌is condition?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man who recently read about the Pegasus controversy feels that he is being tracked by government officials. He thinks that someone is recording his movements, and is plotting to kill him. Which of the following statements is true about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a true, firm, unshak‍eable belief","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is also known as Capgra‍s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is the most common type‍ of delusion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is only seen in schizop‍hrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is the most common type of delusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is the most common type of delusion</strong></p><p>In the clinical scenario, thoughts of being followed and harmed by people represent <strong>delusion of persecution. </strong>It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of delusion.</p>\n<p>A delusion is a <strong>false,</strong> firm, fixed (unshakable) belief that is not in keeping with the patient’s socio-cultural and educational background. </p>\n<p>They are of two types based on their origin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> - Arise de-novo and cannot be explained based on previous experiences, characteristic of schizophrenia.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> - Delusions that are secondary to other morbid psychological conditions like depressive moods and hallucinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3362","text":"A 51-year-old man began to‍ suspect that his wife wa​s cheating on him. He sta​rted to follow her and of‍ten woke her up in the mi​ddle of the night to make​ accusations, even though​ she was not having an af‍fair. He even tried to ph‌ysically harm her because‌ of his suspicions. What ‍is his most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old man began to suspect that his wife was cheating on him. He started to follow her and often woke her up in the middle of the night to make accusations, even though she was not having an affair. He even tried to physically harm her because of his suspicions. What is his most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"De Cleremba‍ult syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Othello ‍syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ekbom s‍yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fregoli ‍syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Othello syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Othello syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Othello syndrome/morbid jealousy</strong>/<strong>delusion of infidelity </strong>is when<strong> </strong>the person believes that his/her partner is unfaithful and is cheating on him/her.</p>\n<p>This can present as a primary delusional disorder, or it can be a symptom of other disorders like schizophrenia, alcohol-related psychosis, mood disorders, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3815","text":"A woman who is on treatmen‍t for schizophrenia says ​that she loves her child ​and wants to hug him, but‍ also thinks that he is t​he devil personified and ​wants to harm him. She is​ torn between the two, an‍d can't decide what to ex‌press. What is this known‌ as?","raw_text":"A woman who is on treatment for schizophrenia says that she loves her child and wants to hug him, but also thinks that he is the devil personified and wants to harm him. She is torn between the two, and can't decide what to express. What is this known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abu‍lia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ambiv‍alence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anhe‍donia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alexi‍thymia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ambivalence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ambivalence</strong></p><p><strong>Ambivalence </strong>is the inability to decide for or against, due to the co-existence of two opposing impulses for the same thing, at the same time, in the same person.</p>\n<p>Ambivalence is an important feature of <strong>schizophrenia</strong>. It is also seen with <strong>OCD</strong> and other borderline conditions.</p>\n<p>Option A: Abulia is an inability to make a decision generally. Unlike ambivalence, there are no opposing impulses present.</p>\n<p>Option C: Anhedonia is a loss of pleasure in previously pleasurable activities.</p>\n<p>Option D: Alexithymia is an inability to recognize and describe feelings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3359","text":"A 70-year-old man presents‍ to the trauma ward with ​an abrasion. He explains ​that he fell from the fir‍st floor of his home. The​ attender contradicts thi​s, saying he fell from hi​s two-wheeler while parki‍ng it. You enquire furthe‌r, to which the patient c‌laims he fell from a bicy‍cle because he was not ab​le to balance the attende‌r's weight. What does thi​s show?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presents to the trauma ward with an abrasion. He explains that he fell from the first floor of his home. The attender contradicts this, saying he fell from his two-wheeler while parking it. You enquire further, to which the patient claims he fell from a bicycle because he was not able to balance the attender's weight. What does this show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halluc‍ination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Confab‍ulation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Delu‍sion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Persev‍eration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Confabulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Confabulation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>confabulation.</strong> This is a defect in memory that manifests as an <strong>unintentional</strong> filling of gaps in one's memory with words and content that are <strong>not true.</strong></p>\n<p>It is mainly seen in alcoholism (Korsakoff psychosis), syphilis, senile dementia, and schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>Option A: Hallucination is a false perception without an external stimulus. </p>\n<p>Option C: Delusion is a fixed false belief that is firmly held despite obvious contradictory evidence and that is not consistent with the person's social, educational, and cultural background.</p>\n<p>Option D: Perseveration is the repetition of out-of-context words, phrases, or ideas beyond the point of relevance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3358","text":"A prison inmate who was in‍ long-term solitary confi​nement is being subjected​ to a neuropsychiatric ev‍aluation. He is asked wha​t the colour of the sky i​s, and he answers that it​ is green. What is this s‍ymptom called?","raw_text":"A prison inmate who was in long-term solitary confinement is being subjected to a neuropsychiatric evaluation. He is asked what the colour of the sky is, and he answers that it is green. What is this symptom called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neol‍ogism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Verbig‍eration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vorbe‍ireden","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Circumst‍antiality","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vorbeireden","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vorbeireden</strong></p><p>When the person gives a wrong answer to a question, but it is very similar to the actual answer, it is called <strong style=\"font-weight: bold;\">vorbeireden</strong><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, which means to give '</span><strong style=\"font-weight: bold;\">approximate answers'</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Here, the person answered 'green' for the colour of the sky. </span></strong>The content of the reply shows that they have understood the nature of the question, but responded wrongly or approximately by talking about an associated topic.</p>\n<p>Vorbeireden is a feature of <strong>Ganser syndrome </strong>(hysterical pseudodementia), commonly found in prison inmates.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: Neologism refers to making new words that do not exist and using them meaninglessly, as seen in schizophrenia. e.g., \"The world is not 'gooder' for people like me\". </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: Verbigeration is the meaningless and stereotyped repetition of words or phrases, seen in schizophrenia. e.g., \"I want to go home go home go home\".</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: Circumstantiality is the inclusion of irrelevant details and talking in a roundabout manner, but the patient's thought ultimately reaches the answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3372","text":"A 10-year-old girl is aske‍d the meaning of \"Strike ​when the iron is hot\", an​d she replies, \"It means ‍that you should strike wh​en the iron rod is hot to​ melt it as it is the mos​t malleable then\". What a‍spect of her thinking is ‌demonstrated?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl is asked the meaning of \"Strike when the iron is hot\", and she replies, \"It means that you should strike when the iron rod is hot to melt it as it is the most malleable then\". What aspect of her thinking is demonstrated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abstract‍ thinking","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Content o‍f thought","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Form of‍ thought","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concrete‍ thinking","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Concrete thinking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Concrete thinking</strong></p><p>When the person was asked the meaning of the proverb, they could only interpret it in the <strong>literal</strong> sense, and not think what the proverb actually means metaphorically. This is called <strong>concrete thinking.</strong></p>\n<p>A person capable of abstract thinking would have answered the metaphorical meaning of the proverb given in the question (strike when the iron is hot), which is to take action when the opportunities are favourable.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 446px; width: 423px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 201.890625px;\"> <strong>Concrete thinking</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 205.125px;\"> <strong>Abstract thinking</strong>  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 23px; width: 201.890625px;\">Develops first in childhood</td>\n<td style=\"height: 23px; width: 205.125px;\">Develops later in childhood gradually and emerges in adolescence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 2px; width: 201.890625px;\">\n<p>Understand the <strong>literal</strong> meaning of a sentence/proverb</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 2px; width: 205.125px;\">Understand the <strong>deeper meaning</strong> of a sentence/ proverb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 21px; width: 201.890625px;\">\n<p>Focused on facts</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 21px; width: 205.125px;\">Focused on concepts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 30px; width: 201.890625px;\">Cannot compare and contrast different objects/situations</td>\n<td style=\"height: 30px; width: 205.125px;\">Can compare and contrast and derive conclusions </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 201.890625px;\">Think about a specific event/object individually.</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 205.125px;\">Can derive generalizations from a specific event/object.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 201.890625px;\">Think about a situation in one particular way</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 205.125px;\">Think about various aspects of a situation simultaneously</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 201.890625px;\">Can think only about objects they have seen or situations they have experienced</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 205.125px;\">Can think about objects/situations that were never experienced by them or that do not exist in reality.   </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Abstract thinking</strong> is <strong>tested</strong> by asking the patient the meaning of a proverb or the similarities between objects like a table and a chair, or between a dog and a lion. Their responses are then judged based on whether they could think about the situation in different aspects, figuratively and metaphorically. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3531","text":"While on rounds in the psy‍chiatry ward, you observe​ a patient standing still​ near his bed. On trying ‍to move his hand you feel​ a slight resistance, and​ the patient remained in ​the final position for th‍e next half hour. What fe‌ature is observed here?","raw_text":"While on rounds in the psychiatry ward, you observe a patient standing still near his bed. On trying to move his hand you feel a slight resistance, and the patient remained in the final position for the next half hour. What feature is observed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Negat‍ivism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post‍uring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Waxy fle‍xibility","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Echop‍raxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Waxy flexibility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/059f1de49e764f4684ea74ec7818d966x1280x1192.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Waxy flexibility</strong></p><p>The method as shown demonstrates<strong> waxy flexibility,</strong> which is a feature of catatonia. In this, parts of the <strong>body</strong> can be placed in <strong>positions</strong> that will be <strong>maintained</strong> for <strong>long periods</strong> of time, even if very uncomfortable. The limbs are flexible like wax and a slight resistance is felt while moving them. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Features of Catatonia:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Mutism</strong>- complete absence of speech.</li>\n<li><strong>Rigidity- </strong>maintenance of a rigid posture against efforts.</li>\n<li><strong>Negativism</strong>- an apparently motiveless resistance to all commands.</li>\n<li><strong>Posturing</strong>- voluntary assumption of an inappropriate and often bizarre posture for long periods of time, against gravity.</li>\n<li><strong>Waxy flexibility</strong>- the patient's limbs can be manipulated to move in any position (flexible like wax), which is then retained by the patient for long periods of time, even if uncomfortable.</li>\n<li><strong>Stupor</strong>- akinesis (no movement) with mutism with relative preservation of conscious awareness. </li>\n<li><strong>Echolalia</strong>- repetition of phrases or words heard.</li>\n<li><strong>Echopraxia</strong>- repetition of actions observed.</li>\n<li><strong>Ambitendency- </strong>tendency to act in opposing ways or directions because of conflicting impulses. e.g. on asking to take out the tongue, the tongue is slightly protruded but taken back again.</li>\n<li><strong>Gegenhalten- </strong>when subjects involuntary resist passive movements. The resistance is directly proportional to the force applied. Also called oppositional paratonia.</li>\n<li><strong>Mannerism- </strong>repetition of <strong>normal</strong> movements but inappropriately.<strong> </strong>They are <strong>purposeful</strong>. E.g., repeatedly saluting the people around.</li>\n<li><strong>Stereotypy- </strong>repetitive, odd, <strong>purposeless</strong> movements. E.g., making strange movements of fingers repeatedly.</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic</strong> <strong>obedience- </strong>commands are followed automatically, irrespective of their nature.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WZ9RV_fM","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"2_fundamentals_of_usg_and_mri","title":"2.Fundamentals Of Usg And Mri","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA8667","text":"Which of the following ima​ging modalities is based ​on the principle of piezo‍electric effect?","raw_text":"Which of the following imaging modalities is based on the principle of piezoelectric effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U​SG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"X-​ray","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MR spec​troscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M​RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. USG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. USG</strong></p><p><strong>Ultrasonography (USG)</strong> works based on the principle of the <strong>piezoelectric effect.</strong></p>\n<p>The tip of ultrasound transducer is made up of piezoelectric crystals like <strong>lead zirconate titanate</strong>. When electric current pass through the transducer, these crystals starts to vibrate and produces ultrasound waves. This is called <strong>reverse piezoelectric effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>They propagate through different <strong>tissues</strong> and is reflected back. These waves are then <strong>converted</strong> back into <strong>electrical</strong> <strong>impulses</strong> by the transducer <strong>crystals</strong>. This is called <strong>piezoelectric effect. </strong>These impulses are further processed to form an ultrasound image on the screen by <strong>pulse-echo principle</strong>. Both reverse piezoelectric effect and piezoelectric effect are used in diagnostic ultrasound.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8669","text":"What does the ultrasonogra​phy transducer contain?","raw_text":"What does the ultrasonography transducer contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium ​Fluoride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lead Zi​rconate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barium ​titanate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium Z​irconate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lead Zirconate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lead Zirconate</strong></p><p><strong>Lead zirconate titanate </strong>(PZT) is the most widely used material in the <strong>ultrasound</strong> <strong>transducers</strong>/probes. It has replaced the previously used barium titanate.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8671","text":"Which of the following is ​not echogenic on ultrason​ography?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not echogenic on ultrasonography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bi​le","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mus​cle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo​ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gall ​stones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/917ddd0227544ebab6477d0e5e830d48x800x569.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac75d26c5eb445398597af75b6c528e9x1008x728.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c245ceff6ea4486a47e026e4ad66842x784x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bile</strong></p><p><strong>Bile</strong> is <strong>not echogenic </strong>on ultrasound.</p>\n<p>Echoes are not produced if there is no difference in acoustic density. Hence, <strong>homogeneous fluids</strong> are seen as <strong>echo-free structures.</strong> Examples of homogenous fluids include blood, bile, urine, cyst contents, ascites, and pleural effusion. </p>\n<p>Homogenous fluids transmit sound exceptionally well and render increased echoes to those structures deeper to them. This is called <strong>acoustic enhancement</strong> / <strong>posterior enhancement</strong> / enhanced through transmission.</p>\n<p><strong>Acoustic shadowing </strong>is present behind bony structures, calculi, and air.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> The image given below shows posterior enhancement of a renal cyst.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> The below image shows a gall bladder stone with acoustic shadowing.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8676","text":"Identify the​ mode in USG.","raw_text":"Identify the mode in USG.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A m​ode","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B m​ode","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M m​ode","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dop​pler","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. M mode","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dff4d46f280b486dbc52e516df0f5a80.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81e963c9f1bf4e558babb893c222fcc0x1012x825.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed910462cb6c43bbbb5c0fedbe75988bx512x559.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. M mode</strong></p><p>The USG image represents<strong> the M-mode</strong> used for <strong>echocardiography</strong>. Echo intensity is represented as brightness and a trace is swept across the screen so that the x-axis represents time.</p>\n<p>Ultrasonography is a versatile imaging modality known for its <strong>portability</strong> and <strong>safety,</strong> particularly as it does <strong>not</strong> involve <strong>radiation </strong>exposure. As the safest radiological investigation for<strong> pregnancy</strong>, it provides crucial information without posing any risks to the mother or fetus, making it indispensable in obstetric care. </p>\n<p>Different modes in USG and its uses are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A</strong> (amplitude) <strong>mode</strong>: Used in <strong>ophthalmology</strong> and orbital biometry (option A).</li>\n<li><strong>B </strong>(brightness) <strong>mode</strong>: Used for all routine applications, with brightness encoded as grayscale value (option B).</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong> (motion) <strong>mode</strong>: In echocardiography. </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>HIFU </strong>(high-intensity focussed USG) is a <strong>therapeutic</strong> application of USG. It is useful for the treatment of <strong>fibroids</strong>. HIFU exploits the thermal effects of high-powered, tightly focused ultrasound beams so that very small target tissues can be heated and coagulated.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts a duplex scan, which is a combination of<strong> B-mode</strong> imaging and <strong>pulsed Doppler</strong> data acquisition.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Doppler ultrasound </strong>(option D) is used in diagnosing <strong>peripheral arterial disease</strong>. It uses a handheld Doppler ultrasound probe placed at an arterial site. A normal arterial wave has a <strong>triphasic signal</strong>. A triphasic wave has three waveforms consisting of a sharp systolic wave with a forward uprise and fall, a second wave with reverse flow occurring in diastole, and the last wave with a forward flow taking place during diastole. </p>\n<p>Doppler ultrasound can detect moving blood. However, it does not provide information on whether the blood flow is sufficient for the maintenance of limb viability. </p>\n<p>The image below shows triphasic signal on doppler ultrasound: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8677","text":"Which of the following ima​ging modalities uses the ​harmonic imaging techniqu‍e?","raw_text":"Which of the following imaging modalities uses the harmonic imaging technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sonog​raphy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Digital subtraction angiog​raphy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MR​CP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclear​ imaging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sonography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sonography</strong></p><p><strong>Harmonic imaging</strong> is a technique used in <strong>sonography</strong>.</p>\n<p>In tissue harmonic imaging sonography, higher harmonic frequencies generated by the propagation of the ultrasound beam through tissue are used to produce the sonogram. They result in better images. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8294","text":"A 29 year old lactating fe​male presents with painfu​l breast lump on the righ‍t side since 5 days but d‌enies any history of feve​r or previous episodes of​ breast issues. Given the‌ likely diagnosis, what i​s the investigation of ch‌oice?","raw_text":"A 29 year old lactating female presents with painful breast lump on the right side since 5 days but denies any history of fever or previous episodes of breast issues. Given the likely diagnosis, what is the investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U​SG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C​T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mamm​ogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. USG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. USG</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggesting the likelihood of breast abscess and <strong>Ultrasonography </strong>(USG) is the investigation of choice in this condition.</p>\n<p>This investigation can differentiate between solid and cystic lesions of the breast and is able to detect abnormal axillary lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>USG is preferred in <strong>pregnant</strong>, <strong>lactating</strong> and <strong>young women</strong> (less than 40 years) since breast tissue is dense in these populations. <strong>Mammography</strong> is indicated in women over the age of 40 with breast symptoms and signs including screening of breast lump and <strong>prolonged</strong> puerperal abscesses.</p>\n<p>Breast <strong>MRI</strong> is the most sensitive method in breast cancer detection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8680","text":"Doppler effect is the rela​tive change in the _____.​","raw_text":"Doppler effect is the relative change in the _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amplitude​ of sound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frequency​ of sound","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direction​ of sound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phase o​f sound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frequency of sound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61e6e93af8fe44439300077d24d254fdx400x279.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frequency of sound</strong></p><p>The <strong>doppler effect</strong> results from an apparent <strong>shift</strong> in <strong>sound frequency</strong> as sound waves are reflected from moving targets, usually blood cells. In <strong>color doppler</strong>, the type of color represents the direction of flow.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Red </strong>- flow is <strong>towards</strong> the transducer</li>\n<li><strong>Blue</strong> - flows is <strong>away</strong> from the transducer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Uses of doppler:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Investigation of choice in DVT</li>\n<li>Investigation of choice in testicular and ovarian torsion</li>\n<li>Carotid artery stenosis screening - if there is stenosis velocity is increased</li>\n<li>In pregnancy:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Umbilical</strong> artery Doppler for follow up of fetal <strong>IUGR</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Uterine</strong> artery Doppler – diastolic notch usually disappears after 22 weeks. Persistent diastolic notch in the uterine artery after 22 weeks of gestation is a predictor of <strong>pre-eclampsia </strong></li>\n<li>Increased peak systolic velocity of fetal <strong>MCA</strong> is an indicator of fetal <strong>anemia</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In spectral Doppler, flow is represented in a waveform. The image given below shows a spectral doppler of the carotid artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5357","text":"The Doppler waveform of th​e venous blood flow in lo​wer limb DVT is _____.","raw_text":"The Doppler waveform of the venous blood flow in lower limb DVT is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monop​hasic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Biph​asic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trip​hasic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multi​phasic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Monophasic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9a3230078de41c7817ee90ab9bc357ex512x559.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e65af83b28434665a21238413e6907a3x512x356.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Monophasic</strong></p><p>The <strong>Doppler</strong> waveform of the <strong>venous blood flow</strong> in lower limb DVT is <strong>monophasic </strong>proximal to the level of obstruction. <br/></p>\n<p>The <strong>normal venous</strong> waveform shows a variation (phasicity) secondary to respiration referred to as <strong>respirophasic waveform</strong>. Loss of this phasic variation results in a <strong>monophasic waveform </strong>suggestive of <strong>venous obstruction</strong> proximal to the level of interrogation.</p>\n<p>A <strong>normal artery</strong> has a <strong>triphasic </strong>signal. The waveform becomes <strong>biphasic</strong> in mild-moderate stenosis and monophasic in severe stenosis.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the normal triphasic signal from an artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Monophasic waveform:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8636","text":"What is the unit of measur​ement of magnetic field i​n MRI machines?","raw_text":"What is the unit of measurement of magnetic field in MRI machines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Housefie​ld units","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Te​sla","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"KHe​rtz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"We​ber","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tesla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tesla</strong></p><p>The <strong>magnetic field strength</strong> of MRI machines is measured in <strong>Tesla. </strong>MRI is based on the gyromagnetic property of protons. </p>\n<p>Clinical imaging scanners have magnets with field strengths ranging from 0.2 to 3 Tesla (and more recently 7 Tesla). Routinely used <strong>MRI</strong> has a field strength of <strong>1.5 Tesla</strong> which is created by superconductors.</p>\n<p><strong>Hounsfield scale</strong>/ unit (HU) is used to measure the X-ray attenuation values in <strong>CT</strong> scanning.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8637","text":"Identify th​e MRI image.","raw_text":"Identify the MRI image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T1 weigh​ted image","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T2 weigh​ted image","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"FL​AIR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ST​IR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T2 weighted image","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3ecb443952ed4dd7b03630286d94b62f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9170f642b45a41fc88ae14ed1739b025x682x682.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57143a7fd17b49ad8887193339810237x802x938.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da613d8bd7cd4e9b85aa95c106e02d27x668x692.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4545bb0227241d5ac466ff3a084bdf1x806x942.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/305719be2a0249cebdfa2aa191918b01x676x676.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03fcfa7a3dfd402ab3698621ddcaee83x798x1034.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4068a0aebbe47c69ded58cf1b4ab9f7x802x922.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T2 weighted image</strong></p><p>The given MRI is a <strong>T2 weighted image</strong> as the CSF appears bright/hyperintense and white matter appears dark. </p>\n<p>Many pulse sequence techniques are used in MRI, and the most classic are T1 and T2 weighted images. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>T1 weighted images</strong> CSF is dark/hypointense, white matter is <strong>white</strong> and grey matter is <strong>dark</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>FLAIR</strong> (fluid-attenuated inversion recovery) is an inversion recovery technique that nulls fluid from T2. As a result, CSF is <strong>dark</strong>. However, white matter is dark and grey matter is bright similar to T2 imaging.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>STIR</strong> (short tau inversion recovery) is an inversion recovery technique that nulls fat from T2. So, fat is <strong>dark.</strong> It is done to detect bone marrow edema in body part scans.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8638","text":"Which of the following are​ contraindications of MRI​? Metallic foreign body i‍n the eye Pregnancy Cardi‌ac pacemaker Renal failur​e Claustrophobia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are contraindications of MRI?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Metallic foreign body in the eye</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Cardiac pacemaker</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Claustrophobia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><p>MRI is contraindicated in patients with <strong>metallic</strong> foreign bodies in the eye, <strong>cardiac pacemakers</strong>, and <strong>claustrophobia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> of <strong>MRI</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac pacemakers </li>\n<li>Aneurysmal metallic clips</li>\n<li>Metallic foreign body</li>\n<li>Cochlear implant</li>\n<li>Allergy to gadolinium (in contrast MRI)</li>\n<li>Claustrophobia (relative contraindication)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8659","text":"Which of the following con​trasts is used in MRI?","raw_text":"Which of the following contrasts is used in MRI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iod​ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gadol​inium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Omni​paque","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opt​ison","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gadolinium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gadolinium</strong></p><p>The contrast agent used for MRI is <strong>gadolinium</strong>. It acts by reducing both T1 and T2 relaxation time of the tissue. The predominant effect is on T1, so post-contrast is a<strong> T1 weighted image.</strong> Since gadolinium ion is toxic, <strong>Gd-DTPA</strong> (Gadolinium diethylenetriamine penta-acetic acid) is used.</p>\n<p>Contraindications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Renal failure</strong> - Gd-DTPA is predominantly excreted through the kidney. Hence, in renal failure, it gets <strong>accumulated</strong> in the body and releases <strong>gadolinium ions</strong>. Since the ion is <strong>toxic</strong>, the body tries to contain it by <strong>fibrosis</strong> resulting in multisystem painful fibrosis called <strong>nephrogenic systemic fibrosis. </strong>As a result, gadolinium-containing contrast is contraindicated in patients with an estimated GFR under 60 ml/min and especially under 30 ml/min.</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy</strong> - Due to the risk of teratogenicity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gadolinium does not cross the blood-brain barrier unless the barrier is disrupted. </p>\n<p><strong>Optison</strong> and Levovist are <strong>ultrasound contrast agents.</strong> Ultrasound contrast agents are gas-filled microbubbles that are administered intravenously to the systemic circulation. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8639","text":"What is the metallic shiel​ding used in MRI rooms ca​lled?","raw_text":"What is the metallic shielding used in MRI rooms called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxwel​l cage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Farada​y cage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ediso​n cage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ohms​ cage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Faraday cage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Faraday cage</strong></p><p>MRI rooms are shielded completely by a continuous sheet or wire mesh of <strong>copper</strong> called <strong>Faraday cage,</strong> which provides radiofrequency (RF) shielding.</p>\n<p>Faraday cage is a conductive box used to <strong>shield</strong> out stray <strong>electromagnetic interference</strong>. It is most commonly made of wood panels wrapped with copper. Other metals like aluminium and galvanized steel can also be used. It serves two <strong>functions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To prevent external radiofrequency pulses from interfering with the detector</li>\n<li>To prevent radiofrequency pulses generated by the scanner from interfering with the external environment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8640","text":"Which of the following use​s the time of flight prin​ciple to visualise the bl‍ood flow in vessels?","raw_text":"Which of the following uses the time of flight principle to visualise the blood flow in vessels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spir​al CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MR im​aging","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT angi​ography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colour ​doppler","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MR imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MR imaging</strong></p><p>The <strong>time-of-flight</strong> (TOF) principle is best employed in <strong>M</strong><strong>agnetic Resonance Angiography. </strong></p>\n<p>TOF angiographic technique is based on the difference between the high signal of blood flowing into an imaging slice and reduced signal from the stationary background tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8649","text":"Functional MRI was done fo​r a stroke patient. What ​is the principle behind t‍his investigation?","raw_text":"Functional MRI was done for a stroke patient. What is the principle behind this investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BOLD te​chnique","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Time of fig​ht technique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brownia​n motion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffusion of water molecul​es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. BOLD technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BOLD technique</strong></p><p>Functional MRI is based on the technique called <strong>BOLD</strong> i.e., <strong>blood oxygen level-dependent technique.</strong></p>\n<p>During a particular <strong>activity</strong>, the corresponding brain area will have <strong>increased oxygen consumption</strong>. Thus, this type of MRI helps in the detection of functions of different brain parts.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 176px; width: 417px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.672px;\"><strong>Types of MRI</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.328px;\"><strong>Principle</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.672px;\">Functional MRI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.328px;\">Blood oxygen level-dependent (BOLD) technique</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.672px;\">Diffusion tensor MRI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.328px;\">Diffusion of water molecules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.672px;\">MR angiography</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.328px;\">Time of flight (TOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.672px;\">Diffusion-weighted MRI (DWI)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.328px;\">Brownian motion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8644","text":"Identify the imaging shown​ below.","raw_text":"Identify the imaging shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffusion te​nsor imaging","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"ST​IR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FL​AIR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MR spec​troscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diffusion tensor imaging","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b907c865b1374b5390f0a1419bb619fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c1156bbb10243368d69865179d8c6fdx1279x1223.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diffusion tensor imaging</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> Diffusion Tensor Imaging(DTI),</strong> an MRI-based neuroimaging.</p>\n<p>It helps to identify the location, orientation, and anisotropy of the <strong>white matter tracts</strong> of the brain. White matter tracts of the brain include association fibres, commissural fibres, projection fibres, and subcortical fibres.</p>\n<p><strong>Magnetic Resonance (MR) spectroscopy</strong> is a non-invasive test based on the principle of magnetic resonance used to analyse the <strong>metabolite distribution</strong> within the brain tissue. It contains <strong>N-acetyl aspartate (NAA)</strong>, <strong>choline,</strong><strong> creatine,</strong><strong> lipids </strong>and <strong>lactate.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a normal MR spectroscopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8641","text":"Identify the vessel marked​ in the image given below​.","raw_text":"Identify the vessel marked in the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior cer​ebral artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Internal ca​rotid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior cerebral artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0fc449633744f2fa87e4a6aaaf49af5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9dc3fb904b404ec8a51b884e14be7bc0x1280x1236.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior cerebral artery</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>MR angiography</strong> of cerebral circulation. The structure marked is the <strong>anterior cerebral artery</strong> which is a branch of the internal carotid artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8643","text":"Which of the following is ​the best plane for the me​asurement of volume of hi‍ppocampus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best plane for the measurement of volume of hippocampus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01f8fa613e1e4e7ab159edee5ad571a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fd7ffd78af74626a77ce253d45060f2x1280x4062.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf2ec49040674c66b3d3f7f3f01e7688x1280x1423.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>The best plane for measurement of the <strong>volume of the hippocampus</strong> is the <strong>coronal plane, angled perpendicular to the long axis of the hippocampus </strong>in<strong> MRI,</strong> which is represented by the image marked B.<br/><br/></p>\n<p>Coronal MRI is taken perpendicular to the long axis of the temporal horn can be used to measure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Volume of hippocampus - measured in <strong>Alzheimer's</strong> disease and <strong>medial temporal seizures</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Medial temporal lobe atrophy</strong> (MTA)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The planes of MRI are given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Image marked as D shows the axial section of a CT.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8295","text":"Which is the most sensitiv​e investigation in a pati​ent being screened for DC‍IS?","raw_text":"Which is the most sensitive investigation in a patient being screened for DCIS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M​RI","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C​T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"U​SG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mamm​ogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MRI</strong></p><p>The investigation of choice for high-risk screening for DCIS is <strong>MRI</strong>(most sensitive). The sign of DCIS on MRI is a ductal enhancement. It is done in women of 25-30 years of age.</p>\n<p>MRI breast is usually done in the second week (follicular phase) of the menstrual cycle. This is because the action of progesterone and estrogen in the luteal phase causes the breast stroma to become edematous with the development of the lobules. This shows up as enhancement on imaging although it is physiological. </p>\n<p><strong>Mammograms</strong> can miss the lesion as young women have dense glandular breast tissue. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8645","text":"A patient suspected to hav​e biliary tract abnormali​ties undergoes the invest‍igation shown in the imag‌e below. Identify the ima​ging and structure marked​ as A.","raw_text":"A patient suspected to have biliary tract abnormalities undergoes the investigation shown in the image below. Identify the imaging and structure marked as A.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRCP - Right​ hepatic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ERCP - Cy​stic duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRCP - Comm​on bile duct","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ERCP - Panc​reatic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MRCP - Common bile duct","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f49d4ff133014d86922647ae17e93924.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca7bc3b7b6b446c0b4f5f67f89e02230x1280x1369.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd32162a7b9b41d0972161c4995855e7x1280x1339.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MRCP - Common bile duct</strong></p><p>The image depicts a magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (<strong>MRCP</strong>), and the structure marked as A is the <strong>common bile duct</strong>.</p>\n<p>MRCP is a non-invasive test showing a heavy <strong>T2-weighted</strong> image. The endoscope or contrast medium is not used as bile itself serves as a contrast medium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8646","text":"Which of the following pat​hological conditions can ​be identified with diffus‍ion-weighted MRI? Acute i‌nfarct Bone marrow edema ​Brain abscess Epidermoid ​cyst Herpes encephalitis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following pathological conditions can be identified with diffusion-weighted MRI?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Acute infarct</li>\n<li>Bone marrow edema</li>\n<li>Brain abscess</li>\n<li>Epidermoid cyst</li>\n<li>Herpes encephalitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd5143a373734655a757a889120d7155x209x225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p>Diffusion-weighted (<strong>DWI</strong>) MRI can identify the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute infarct</strong> can be detected within 3-30 mins (CT/MRI can detect only after 24 hours) </li>\n<li>Brain abscess and <strong>epidermoid cyst</strong> appear bright on DWI. Hence, they can be differentiated from necrotic tumours and arachnoid cysts, respectively.</li>\n<li>It can also differentiate <strong>herpes encephalitis</strong> from diffuse temporal gliomas. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>This type of imaging is based on <strong>Brownian motion</strong> i.e., structures that allow diffusion will not appear bright. The region with less diffusion has more T2 signal, which is why <strong>dead</strong> <strong>tissue</strong> appears <strong>bright.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows DWI in acute infarct of the brain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Bone marrow edema can be visualized by <strong>STIR</strong> (short tau inversion recovery). </p>\n<p>Note: The most common site of epidermoid cyst in the brain is CP angle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EgtCR5oG","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"2_shock_and_blood_transfusion","title":"2.Shock And Blood Transfusion","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6115","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of s​hock:","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of shock:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypov‌olemic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cardi‌ogenic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstr‌uctive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distri‌butive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypovolemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypovolemic</strong></p><p><strong>Hypovolemic shock </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> type of shock and to some degree is a component of all other types of shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Shock</strong> is defined as a systemic state of <strong>low tissue perfusion</strong> which is inadequate for normal cellular respiration.</p>\n<p>Shock can be classified as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 545px; width: 827px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 229.78125px; height: 28px;\"><strong>Shock</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 578.21875px; height: 28px;\"><strong>Inciting mechanism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 229.78125px; height: 28px;\">Hypovolemic shock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 578.21875px; height: 28px;\">\n<p>Reduced circulating blood volume</p>\n<p>(eg., hemorrhage, dehydration)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 229.78125px; height: 28px;\">Cardiogenic shock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 578.21875px; height: 28px;\">\n<p>Failure of the cardiac pump</p>\n<p>(eg., myocardial infarction, cardiac dysrhythmias)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 229.78125px; height: 28px;\">Obstructive shock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 578.21875px; height: 28px;\">\n<p>Mechanical obstruction of cardiac filling </p>\n<p>(eg., cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 229.78125px; height: 28px;\">Distributive shock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 578.21875px; height: 28px;\">\n<p>Systemic vasodilation leading to organ hypoperfusion</p>\n<p>(eg., anaphylaxis due to histamine release, sepsis due to the release of bacterial products, spinal cord injury due to sympathetic failure)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38.28125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 229.78125px; height: 38.28125px;\">Endocrine shock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 578.21875px; height: 38.28125px;\">\n<p>Combination of hypovolemic, cardiogenic or distributive shock.</p>\n<p>(eg., hypo and hyperthyroidism, adrenal insufficiency)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6139","text":"A patient is treated for h‌ypovolemic shock followin​g a motor vehicle collisi‍on. Which of the followin​g is not a compensatory m​echanism that is anticipa‌ted to occur in this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A patient is treated for hypovolemic shock following a motor vehicle collision. Which of the following is not a compensatory mechanism that is anticipated to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduced perfusion to the s‌kin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced perfusion to the k‌idneys","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reduced perfusion to the G‌I tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced perfusion to the m‌uscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reduced perfusion to the kidneys","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reduced perfusion to the kidneys</strong></p><p>The compensatory mechanism in shock <strong>reduces the perfusion</strong> to the <strong>skin</strong>, <strong>muscle</strong> and <strong>gastrointestinal tract </strong>and not to the kidney.</p>\n<p>As shock progresses, the body’s cardiovascular and endocrine compensatory responses <strong>reduce the blood flow to non-essential organs</strong> by vasoconstriction. This occurs in order to preserve preload and flow to the kidneys, lungs and brain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6150","text":"A 35-year-old male patient‌ was brought to the emerg​ency department following‍ a road traffic accident.​ On examination, he had m​ultiple injuries over his‌ body and was found to be​ in class II hemorrhagic ​shock. The percentage of ‌blood loss would be betwe​en:","raw_text":"A 35-year-old male patient was brought to the emergency department following a road traffic accident. On examination, he had multiple injuries over his body and was found to be in class II hemorrhagic shock. The percentage of blood loss would be between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5–‌15%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10–‌20%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15–‌30%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"20–‌40%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 15–30%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 15–30%</strong></p><p>The percentage of blood loss in <strong>class II</strong> hemorrhagic shock is <strong>15–30%</strong>, which amounts to a loss of <strong>750–1500 mL of blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>class II hemorrhagic shock</strong> is considered as a mild shock with heart rate more than 100. It is associated with <strong>orthostatic hypotension,</strong> and the patient is usually <strong>anxious</strong> at this stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6120","text":"Following a road traffic a‌ccident, a 23-year-old ma​n presents with breathles‍sness. On examination, he​ has hypotension and dist​ended neck veins. On ausc‌ultation, muffled heart s​ounds are heard. What is ​this condition primarily ‌associated with?","raw_text":"Following a road traffic accident, a 23-year-old man presents with breathlessness. On examination, he has hypotension and distended neck veins. On auscultation, muffled heart sounds are heard. What is this condition primarily associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstruct‌ive shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Distribut‌ive shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obstructive shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obstructive shock</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>Beck's triad</strong> (hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds) is seen in <strong>cardiac tamponade</strong>. Shock due to cardiac tamponade is <strong>primarily</strong> an example of <strong>obstructive shock.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Obstructive shock</strong> is characterized by a <strong>reduction in preload</strong> due to <strong>mechanical obstruction</strong> of the cardiac filling of the left and/or right sides of the heart, which in turn leads to <strong>fall in cardiac output</strong>. Common causes of obstructive shock include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac tamponade</li>\n<li>Tension pneumothorax</li>\n<li>Massive pulmonary embolus or air embolus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardiogenic shock is due to primary failure of the heart to pump blood. It may be due to myocardial depression as a result of infarction, cardiomyopathy, blunt myocardial injury. Patients with cardiac tamponade may eventually develop <strong>cardiogenic shock</strong> as the myocardium is stressed against the obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6137","text":"A 35-year-old man sustaine‌d a spinal cord injury as​ a result of a road traff‍ic accident. On examinati​on, his blood pressure is​ found to be 90/60 mmHg. ‌All of the following find​ings can be seen in the p​atient except?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man sustained a spinal cord injury as a result of a road traffic accident. On examination, his blood pressure is found to be 90/60 mmHg. All of the following findings can be seen in the patient except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Warm ext‌remities","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased v‌enous return","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased vascular capacit‌ance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tachycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a9ff27aa7404e4b9caaa85970ed9acfx1280x1819.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tachycardia</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of the presence of hypotension associated with the spinal cord injury is suggestive of <strong>neurogenic shock</strong>. It is associated with <strong>bradycardia </strong>and not tachycardia.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurogenic shock</strong> refers to reduced tissue perfusion due to <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>vasomotor tone</strong> to peripheral arterial beds.</p>\n<p>Neurogenic shock is usually secondary to <strong>spinal cord injuries</strong> due to vertebral body fractures of the cervical or high thoracic region that <strong>disrupts the sympathetic regulation</strong> of <strong>peripheral vascular tone</strong>. Loss of vasoconstrictor impulses results in increased vascular capacitance, decreased venous return and decreased cardiac output.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The classic description of neurogenic shock consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypotension</strong> associated with <strong>bradycardia</strong> (absence of reflexive tachycardia due to disrupted sympathetic discharge)</li>\n<li><strong>Warm extremities</strong> (due to loss of peripheral vasoconstriction)</li>\n<li><strong>Motor and sensory deficits</strong> indicative of a spinal cord injury</li>\n<li>Radiographic evidence of a vertebral column fracture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7323","text":"Modified shock index is ca‌lculated using? HR: Heart​ rate MAP: Mean arterial ‍pressure SBP: Systolic bl​ood pressure DBP: Diastol​ic blood pressure","raw_text":"<p>Modified shock index is calculated using?</p>\n<p>HR: Heart rate</p>\n<p>MAP: Mean arterial pressure</p>\n<p>SBP: Systolic blood pressure</p>\n<p>DBP: Diastolic blood pressure</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HR/‌SBP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HR/‌DBP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HR/‌MAP","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PR/‌SBP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HR/MAP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HR/MAP</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Modified </strong>shock index <strong>= HR/MAP </strong></li>\n<li>Original <strong>shock index = HR/SBP </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The shock index is a <strong>hemodynamic stability indicator</strong>. In patients with shock, it is a better <strong>predictor of mortality</strong> than blood pressure or heart rate alone. A <strong>shock index &gt;0.9</strong> is indicative of <strong>higher mortality.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>modified</strong> shock index is a <strong>more sensitive</strong> indicator of tissue perfusion than the <strong>original</strong> shock index. In critically ill patients, the DBP decreases earlier than SBP, hence, the modified shock index using MAP is better suited to assess the severity of shock.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6129","text":"The peripheral vascular re‌sistance is decreased in ​____________","raw_text":"The peripheral vascular resistance is decreased in ____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstruct‌ive shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distribut‌ive shock","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distributive shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distributive shock</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The peripheral vascular resistance is decreased in <strong>distributive shock. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is the condition of reduced oxygen delivery where the primary physiologic disturbance is a <strong>reduction in systemic vascular resistance</strong>. It is unique among the types of shock in that there is a compensatory<strong> increase in cardiac output</strong>. The most common cause of distributive shock is <strong>sepsis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The causes of vasodilatation in various types of distributive shock:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 323px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\"><strong>Type of distributive shock </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\"><strong>Cause of vasodilatation </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Septic shock </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Release of bacterial products (endotoxin) and the activation of cellular and humoral components of the immune system. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Anaphylactic shock </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Histamine release </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Neurogenic shock (spinal cord injury) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Failure of sympathetic outflow and adequate vascular tone </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The following Table shows cardiovascular and metabolic characteristics of different types of shock.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px; width: 583px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\"><strong>Hypovolemia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\"><strong>Cardiogenic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\"><strong>Obstructive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\"><strong>Distributive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Cardiac output</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">\n<p><strong>High (in septic and anaphylactic shock)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Low (in neurogenic shock)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Vascular resistance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Venous pressure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Mixed venous saturation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Base deficit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6143","text":"A patient who was admitted‌ with third-degree burns ​presents with light-heade‍dness and oliguria on the​ third day of admission. ​On examination, the perip‌heries are warm and capil​lary refill is brisk. His​ pulse rate is 138/min. I‌n which of the following ​types of shock do you see‌ this presentation?","raw_text":"A patient who was admitted with third-degree burns presents with light-headedness and oliguria on the third day of admission. On examination, the peripheries are warm and capillary refill is brisk. His pulse rate is 138/min. In which of the following types of shock do you see this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Septic‌ shock","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endocri‌ne shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Septic shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Septic shock</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is seen in <strong>septic shock.</strong></p>\n<p>In distributive (septic) shock, the<strong> peripheries are warm</strong> and <strong>capillary refill</strong> will be <strong>brisk</strong>, despite the profound shock.</p>\n<p>In <strong>early</strong> stages (compensated stage) of septic shock, the patient has <strong>clammy, warm peripheries.</strong> The latter is a distinct feature from other types of shock where <strong>peripheral</strong> <strong>vasoconstriction</strong> leads to <strong>cold</strong> peripheries. The patient will have decreased vascular resistance, inadequate afterload, abnormally high cardiac output, and tachycardia.</p>\n<p>In the<strong> later</strong> stages (uncompensated phase) of septic shock, there is <strong>reduced venous return</strong>, preload, and <strong>reduced cardiac output </strong>with low blood pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6144","text":"An 82-year-old man with a ‌history of recurrent urin​ary tract infection prese‍nts with fever, tachypnea​, and tachycardia. On exa​mination, he appears conf‌used and has hypotension.​ Which of the following i​s used as the initial tre‌atment for his condition?​","raw_text":"An 82-year-old man with a history of recurrent urinary tract infection presents with fever, tachypnea, and tachycardia. On examination, he appears confused and has hypotension. Which of the following is used as the initial treatment for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasopr‌essors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood tr‌ansfusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crysta‌lloids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inotropi‌c agents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Crystalloids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Crystalloids</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>septic shock. </strong><strong>Crystalloids </strong>are the initial fluid of choice in the resuscitation of severe sepsis and septic shock.</p>\n<p>Septic shock is also known as <strong>vasodilatory shock</strong> (But remember <strong>vasopressors are not the first line</strong>).</p>\n<p>Management protocol for septic shock:</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Immediate evaluation -<span class=\"headingEndMark\"> </span>Securing the airway (if indicated) and correcting hypoxemia and establishing venous access for the <strong>early</strong> administration of fluids and antibiotics are priorities in the management of patients with sepsis and septic shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Initial resuscitative therapy - aggressive administration of intravenous fluids (IVF), usually <strong>crystalloids</strong> are given at 30 <span class=\"nowrap\">mL/kg</span> (actual body weight) within the first <strong>three </strong>hours following the presentation. Empirical antibiotic therapy started within <strong>first hour.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Intravenous vasopressors are useful in patients who remain hypotensive despite adequate fluid resuscitation or who develop cardiogenic pulmonary edema.</p>\n<p><strong>Vasopressor agents</strong> such as <strong>phenylephrine, noradrenaline</strong> are indicated in <strong>distributive shock</strong> states which include <strong>septic shock, neurogenic shock, </strong>etc. where there is <strong>peripheral vasodilatation</strong>, and a low systemic vascular resistance, leading to hypotension. But they are not the first-line treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6165","text":"You are a medical officer ‌at the blood bank and rec​eive several units of pac‍ked RBCs, platelets, fres​h frozen plasma, and cryo​precipitate following a b‌lood donation camp. Which​ among the following comp​onents would you want to ‌use at the earliest?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer at the blood bank and receive several units of packed RBCs, platelets, fresh frozen plasma, and cryoprecipitate following a blood donation camp. Which among the following components would you want to use at the earliest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Packe‌d RBCs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fresh fro‌zen plasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cryopre‌cipitate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Platelets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Platelets</strong></p><p><strong>Platelets</strong> have a shelf life of only <strong>5 days</strong> and hence they have to be used at the earliest.</p>\n<p>They are usually stored on a <strong>special agitator</strong> at <strong>20–24 °C. </strong>They are given to the patients with thrombocytopenia or with platelet dysfunction who are bleeding or undergoing surgery.</p>\n<p>Packed cells (PRBCs) are stored in a <strong>SAG-M solution</strong> (saline–adenine–glucose–mannitol) and has a shelf life of <strong>42 days</strong> at <strong>2–6 °C</strong>. (Older storage regimens included storage in CPD – citrate–phosphate–dextrose solutions which have a shelf life of 2–3 weeks.)</p>\n<p><strong>Fresh frozen plasma</strong> (FFP) is stored at <strong>−40 to −50°C</strong>, while <strong>cryoprecipitate</strong> is stored at <strong>-30°C,</strong> and both have a shelf life of <strong>one years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD9641","text":"A patient with a history o‌f esophageal varices pres​ents with hematemesis. Wh‍at is the optimal level o​f hemoglobin to be mainta​ined in this patient thro‌ugh blood transfusions?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of esophageal varices presents with hematemesis. What is the optimal level of hemoglobin to be maintained in this patient through blood transfusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7 g‌m/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 g‌m/dL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9 g‌m/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8 g‌m/dL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8 gm/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8 gm/dL</strong></p><p>The optimal level of Hb to be maintained, in this case, is <strong>8 gm/dL.</strong></p>\n<p>The indications for blood transfusion are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute blood loss:</strong> To replace circulating volume and maintain oxygen delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Perioperative anemia:</strong> To ensure adequate oxygen delivery during the perioperative phase.</li>\n<li><strong>Symptomatic chronic anemia:</strong> without hemorrhage or impending surgery.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 184px;\" width=\"504\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 243px;\"><strong> Haemoglobin level (g/dL) </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 245px;\"><strong> Indications </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 243px;\">&lt;6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245px;\">Probably will benefit from transfusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 243px;\">6–8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245px;\">Transfusion unlikely to be of benefit in the absence of <strong>bleeding</strong> or impending surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 243px;\">&gt;8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245px;\">No indication for transfusion in the absence of other risk factors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6151","text":"A 33-year-old male patient‌ was transfused with 2 un​its of fresh frozen plasm‍a following a road traffi​c accident. Within 2 hour​s of the transfusion, he ‌became breathless and his​ oxygen saturation droppe​d to 88%. Chest X-ray sho‌wed batwing appearance. T​his condition occurs due ‌to which of the following‍?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old male patient was transfused with 2 units of fresh frozen plasma following a road traffic accident. Within 2 hours of the transfusion, he became breathless and his oxygen saturation dropped to 88%. Chest X-ray showed batwing appearance. This condition occurs due to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preformed‌ cytokines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infusion of contaminated b‌lood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-‌HLA Ab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Preformed IgM Ab to ABO Ag‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti-HLA Ab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti-HLA Ab</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of transfusion-related acute lung injury (<strong>TRALI</strong>). The presence of <strong>antibodies against</strong> the <strong>Human Leukocyte antigen </strong>(Anti-HLA Ab) in the <strong>donor blood</strong> is implicated in the development of TRALI.</p>\n<p><strong>TRALI</strong> is defined as an acute lung injury<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acute_lung_injury\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a> that is temporally related to blood transfusion and causes <strong>non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. </strong>It occurs within the <strong>first six hours</strong> following a transfusion. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-3\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfusion-related_acute_lung_injury#cite_note-3\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a></sup><strong>Screening </strong>for HLA antibodies has been introduced to prevent TRALI.</p>\n<p>TRALI usually results from the transfusion of <strong>donor plasma</strong> that contains high-titer anti-HLA class II antibodies that bind to <strong>recipient leukocytes</strong>. The leukocytes aggregate in the pulmonary vasculature and release mediators that increase capillary permeability. Testing the donor’s plasma for anti-HLA antibodies can support this diagnosis.</p>\n<p>The implicated donors are frequently <strong>multiparous women</strong>. The transfusion of plasma from male and nulliparous women donors reduces the risk of TRALI. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6166","text":"A 60-year-old male who is ‌on warfarin prophylaxis f​or atrial fibrillation pr‍esents with massive uncon​trolled haemorrhage follo​wing a fall from height. ‌Which of the following bl​ood components can be use​d for emergency reversal ‌of warfarin therapy in th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male who is on warfarin prophylaxis for atrial fibrillation presents with massive uncontrolled haemorrhage following a fall from height. Which of the following blood components can be used for emergency reversal of warfarin therapy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryopre‌cipitate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prothrombin complex concen‌trates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fresh-fro‌zen plasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prothrombin complex concentrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prothrombin complex concentrates</strong></p><p><strong>Prothrombin Complex Concentrates (PCCs)</strong> are indicated for the emergency reversal of anticoagulant (warfarin) therapy in uncontrolled hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>PCCs are highly purified concentrates prepared from pooled plasma. They contain <strong>factors II, IX and X</strong>. Factor VII may be included or produced separately.</p>\n<p>Fresh frozen plasma and platelets are used in<strong> management of coagulopathy</strong>. Fresh frozen plasma is used if prothrombin time (PT) or partial thromboplastin time (PTT) &gt;1.5 times normal. Platelets are transfused if platelet count &lt;50 x10<sup>3</sup>/ul.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">However, in the presence of non-surgical haemorrhage these tests take time to arrange and may underestimate the degree of coagulopathy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6167","text":"Cryoprecipitate is stored ‌at a temperature of _____​___","raw_text":"Cryoprecipitate is stored at a temperature of ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-20‌ °C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-30‌ °C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"-40‌ °C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-50‌ °C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. -30 °C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. -30 °C</strong></p><p><strong>Cryoprecipitate</strong> is a supernatant precipitate of FFP (fresh frozen plasma) and is rich in factor VIII and fibrinogen.</p>\n<p>It is stored at <strong>−30 <sup>°</sup>C</strong> and has a shelf life of two years. It is given in low fibrinogen states or <strong>factor VIII deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6170","text":"A 22-year-old male patient‌ was brought to the emerg​ency room following a roa‍d traffic accident. Upon ​arrival, the patient was ​unconscious with blood pr‌essure of 90/64 mmHg and ​a pulse rate of 139 per m​inute. He was immediately‌ transfused with 4 units ​of packed RBCs. All of th‌e following complications‍ can occur in this patien‌t except:","raw_text":"A 22-year-old male patient was brought to the emergency room following a road traffic accident. Upon arrival, the patient was unconscious with blood pressure of 90/64 mmHg and a pulse rate of 139 per minute. He was immediately transfused with 4 units of packed RBCs. All of the following complications can occur in this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic‌ alkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p>The complication of massive transfusion includes <strong>h</strong><strong>ypocalcemia</strong>, not hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lethal triad</strong> of massive transfusion includes <strong>acidosis, hypothermia, </strong>and<strong> coagulopathy. </strong>Other complications which can be seen in massive transfusion are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypocalcaemia</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3931","text":"A young patient is brought‌ to the emergency room fo​llowing a motorcycle coll‍ision. On examination, th​e patient is drowsy and h​as decreased blood pressu‌re due to bleeding from m​ultiple injuries. 1 unit ​of packed RBCs has been o‌btained from the blood ba​nk. What is the correct p‌rocedure for blood transf‍usion?","raw_text":"A young patient is brought to the emergency room following a motorcycle collision. On examination, the patient is drowsy and has decreased blood pressure due to bleeding from multiple injuries. 1 unit of packed RBCs has been obtained from the blood bank. What is the correct procedure for blood transfusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The blood should be brough‌t to room temperature bef​ore the transfusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transfusion should be star‌ted within one hour of re​moval from the refrigerat‍or","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transfusion of PRBC should‌ be completed within 4 ho​urs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The rate of transfusion of‌ PRBC is 50mL per hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transfusion of PRBC should be completed within 4 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transfusion of PRBC should be completed within 4 hours</strong></p><p>The transfusion of packed RBCs (PRBC) should be completed within a maximum period of <strong>4 hours</strong> after the removal from the blood fridge. </p>\n<p>There is an increased risk of <strong>bacterial proliferation</strong> and loss of the function of blood components after they are removed from the storage temperatures. Hence, the transfusion should be <strong>started within 30 minutes</strong> of receiving the components from the blood bank. The packed RBCs should be infused at a rate of <strong>100-150mL/hour.</strong> Warming of blood is not needed when the transfusion is at normal rates. The transfusion should be completed within a maximum period of 4 hours.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 93px;\" width=\"671\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 216px; text-align: center;\">Duration times for transfusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\"><strong>Blood products</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\"><strong> Start transfusion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\"><strong> Complete transfusion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Whole blood / PRBC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Within 30 minutes of removing from refrigerator</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p>≤ 4 hours </p>\n<p>Discard unit if this period is exceeded</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Platelet concentrate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\"> Immediately</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Within 30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Fresh frozen plasma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">As soon as possible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Within 30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Cryoprecipitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">As soon as possible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Within 30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7134","text":"A patient with a road traf‌fic accident presented to​ the emergency room and r‍equires a large amount of​ fluids to be transfused.​ Which of the following I‌V cannula is preferred?","raw_text":"A patient with a road traffic accident presented to the emergency room and requires a large amount of fluids to be transfused. Which of the following IV cannula is preferred?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gr‌een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bl‌ue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pi‌nk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gr‌ey","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Grey","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Grey</strong></p><p><strong>Grey</strong> coloured IV cannula is required for this patient in order to transfuse large amount of fluids.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ATLS</strong> 10th edition guidelines state that <strong>2 cannulae</strong> of <strong>atleast 18G</strong> should be used to gain vascular access in trauma patients. In this scenario requiring large fluid transfusions, the grey (16G) cannula would be preferable to the green (18G) cannula due to the higher flow rate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6658","text":"What is the size of the ca‌nnula shown in the below ​image?","raw_text":"What is the size of the cannula shown in the below image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"16‌ G","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"18‌ G","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20‌ G","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"22‌ G","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20 G","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/616e7c3e082041dcb6dd7bb1668c1979.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20 G</strong></p><p>The given cannula is <strong>20</strong><strong> gauge IV cannula</strong> as its color code is <strong>p</strong><strong>ink</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5471","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of qSOFA?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of qSOFA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiratory ‌rate ≥22/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systolic BP‌ ≤100 mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Altered me‌ntal status","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral pupillary reflex‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bilateral pupillary reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bilateral pupillary reflex</strong></p><p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s3\">Sepsis is defined as a “life-threatening </span><strong><span class=\"s4\">organ dysfunction</span></strong><span class=\"s3\"> due to a dysregulated host response to infection.” It lies in the continuous spectrum between infection and septic shock.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> <strong>qSOFA</strong> score (quickSOFA) is a bedside prompt that may identify patients with suspected infection who are at greater risk for a poor outcome outside the intensive care unit (ICU).</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> It uses three criteria, assigning one point each for</p>\n<ul>\n<li>low blood pressure (SBP≤100 mmHg),</li>\n<li>high respiratory rate (≥22 breaths per min)</li>\n<li>altered mentation (Glasgow coma scale, &lt;15).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The score ranges from 0 to 3 points. The presence of 2 or more qSOFA points is a simple prompt to identify infected patients outside the ICU who are likely to develop sepsis.</p>\n<p> The primary method to evaluate the risk of organ dysfunction (sepsis) in patients with suspected infection is by determining the  <strong>Sequential</strong> (<strong>sepsis-related) organ failure assessment (SOFA) score</strong>; which includes assessment of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circulation - Systolic BP &amp; need for vasopressors; platelet count</li>\n<li>Respiration - PaO2/FiO2 &amp; need for mechanical ventilation</li>\n<li>Mentation - Glasgow Coma Score </li>\n<li>Hepatic function - Bilirubin</li>\n<li>Renal function - Creatinine </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Organ dysfunction</strong> is identified by a <strong>SOFA score of ≥ 2</strong></p>\n<p>The SOFA score is limited by its requirement of multiple laboratory tests which makes it complex and time-consuming. In order to simplify the scoring process, the \"Sepsis - 3\" task force recommended the<strong> quick sepsis-related organ dysfunction assessment \"qSOFA\" score</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>SOFA Vs qSOFA :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SOFA</strong> is a more comprehensive and accurate scoring system which is <strong>particulary superior to qSOFA in the ICU setting.</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>qSOFA</strong> is a simplified derivative of SOFA for bedside assessment of individuals at risk for sepsis <strong>particularly useful outside the ICU. setting </strong>(i.e. ward / emergency department). However, note that it was originally designed as predictive tool that calculates the risk of death from sepsis rather than a diagnostic tool.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thereby, the best description of sepsis based on \"Sepsis -3\" task force recommendations would be :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suspected or documented infection with SOFA score ≥ 2  <strong>Or</strong></li>\n<li>Suspected or documented infection with qSOFA score ≥ 2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Another score that has been used is the NEWS(National Early Warning Score). It ranges from 0 to 20 and is based on respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, use of supplemental O2, temperature, systolic blood pressure, pulse rate and level of consciousness. ≥5 out of 20 is considered a positive score. </p>\n<p><span class=\"s3\">Other scoring systems for sepsis:</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s3\">The </span><span class=\"s4\">Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (<strong>SIRS</strong>)</span><span class=\"s3\"> score is no longer recommended as is it <strong>not specific to infection.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s3\">The </span><span class=\"s4\">Logistic Organ Dysfunction System (<strong>LODS</strong>)</span><span class=\"s3\"> score is not recommended as it is <strong>complex</strong> and has too many variables.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7703","text":"All of the following are f‌eatures of SIRS except:","raw_text":"All of the following are features of SIRS except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temperature &amp;gt; 38 degree‌s Celsius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory Rate &amp;gt; 20/m‌inute and PaCO2 &amp;lt;35 mm​Hg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulse rate &amp;‌gt; 90/minute","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WBC &amp;gt; 12000/mm3 or &amp;lt;‌ 4000/mm3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Respiratory Rate > 20/minute and PaCO2 <35 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Respiratory Rate &gt; 20/minute and PaCO2 &lt;35 mmHg</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Respiratory Rate &gt; 20/minute</strong> or (Not and) <strong>PaCO2 &lt; 32 mmHg</strong> (Not &lt;<span style=\"font-size: 15.5556px;\">35 mmHg</span>) would be a feature of <strong>SIRS</strong> (Systemic inflammatory response syndrome).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) is an <strong>exaggerated defense response</strong> of the body to a noxious stressor (infection, trauma, surgery, acute inflammation, ischemia, or reperfusion) to localize and then eliminate the endogenous or exogenous source of the insult.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">SIRS is diagnosed when any <strong>two</strong> of the following are present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hyperthermia</strong> (&gt;38 Degree Celsius) or <strong>Hypothermia</strong> (&lt;36 Degree Celsius)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tachypnoea</strong> (&gt;20/min) or <strong>PaCO2 &lt; 32 mmHg</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tachycardia</strong> (&gt;90/min) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">White cell count <strong>&gt;12 x 10<sup>9</sup></strong>/liter <strong>or</strong> <strong>&lt;4 x 10<sup>9</sup></strong>/liter <strong>or</strong> <strong>&gt;10%</strong> Band forms</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\"> Sepsis is <strong>SIRS</strong> with <strong>a documented infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"I1d6HVME","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"3_cns_and_cvs_monitoring_in_anaesthesia","title":"3.Cns And Cvs Monitoring In Anaesthesia","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2713","text":"In a patient undergoing su​rgery, which of the follo​wing would not be used to‌ monitor the cardiovascul‌ar system?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing surgery, which of the following would not be used to monitor the cardiovascular system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial arter​y cannulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transthoracic echocardiogr​aphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary artery catheteri​zation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capnography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capnography</strong></p><p><strong>Capnography</strong> is used to assess ventilation in the perioperative period and to confirm the correct placement of an endotracheal tube. </p>\n<p>Cardiovascular monitoring involves the use of:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 230px;\" width=\"532\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Hemodynamic parameter</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Methods</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Heart rate</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Manual, ECG</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Pulse rate</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Manual, ECG, pulse oximeter plethysmography</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Blood pressure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong><strong>Non-invasive: </strong></strong>\n<p>Palpation</p>\n<p>Doppler probe</p>\n<p>Auscultation</p>\n<p>Oscillometry</p>\n<p>Arterial tonometry</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong><strong>Invasive: </strong></strong>\n<p>Reference standard for arterial pressure monitoring</p>\n<p>Arterial cannulation (radial, femoral, dorsalis pedis etc.)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Central venous pressure</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Central Venous Cannulation </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Pulmonary Artery Catheterization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Cardiac Output</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 170px;\">Thermodilution\n<p>Dye dilution using indocyanin green</p>\n<p>Lithium dilution</p>\n<p>Pulse contour devices</p>\n<p>Esophageal Doppler</p>\n<p>Thoracic bio impedance</p>\n<p>Echocardiography (Transesophageal and transthoracic)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2715","text":"A patient with heart failu​re in the ICU is being ad​ministered fluids. Which ‌is the method of non-inva‌sive cardiac monitoring u​sed to titrate the treatm‌ent for this patient on a​ regular basis?","raw_text":"A patient with heart failure in the ICU is being administered fluids. Which is the method of non-invasive cardiac monitoring used to titrate the treatment for this patient on a regular basis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial arter​y cannulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary artery catheteri​zation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transesophageal echocardio​graphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transthoracic echocardiogr​aphy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transthoracic echocardiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transthoracic echocardiography</strong></p><p><strong>Transthoracic echocardiography </strong>(TTE) is the <strong>noninvasive</strong> form of cardiac monitoring that can be used in this patient.   </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Transesophageal echocardiography</strong> (TEE)  is a <strong>minimally invasive</strong> procedure and could be associated with serious <strong>complications</strong> like oesophagal rupture and mediastinitis in the presence of oesophagal injury or disease. In the absence of oesophagal disease, the risk is low.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2717","text":"In a patient requiring inv​asive BP monitoring, whic​h is the preferred artery‌ used for canulation and ‌measurement?","raw_text":"In a patient requiring invasive BP monitoring, which is the preferred artery used for canulation and measurement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ul​nar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rad​ial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brac​hial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fem​oral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radial</strong></p><p><strong>Radial artery</strong> is the preferred artery for invasive blood pressure monitoring because of ease of cannulation, presence of collateral blood flow and less associated complications.</p>\n<p>Before cannulating the radial artery, it is essential to perform a modified <strong>Allen’s test</strong> in order to determine the adequacy of collateral flow to the hand.</p>\n<p>The other arteries which can be used for invasive blood pressure monitoring are ulnar, brachial, axillary, and femoral arteries and less commonly dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial, and superficial temporal arteries.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5139","text":"A 25-year-old is admitted ​to the ER after being inv​olved in a road traffic a‌ccident. Which type of IV‌ cannula would achieve th​e maximum flow rate to pr‌ovide fluid resuscitation​?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old is admitted to the ER after being involved in a road traffic accident. Which type of IV cannula would achieve the maximum flow rate to provide fluid resuscitation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ora​nge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bl​ue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pi​nk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gr​een","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Orange","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Orange</strong></p><p><strong>The orange cannula (14 Gauge) </strong>has the maximum flow rate of <strong>270 mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3994","text":"Which of the following ner​ves is popularly used in ​clinical anaesthesia to m‌onitor neuromuscular bloc‌kade?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is popularly used in clinical anaesthesia to monitor neuromuscular blockade?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulnar​ Nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Median​ Nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibul​ar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ulnar Nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ulnar Nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Ulnar nerve</strong> stimulation at wrist is popularly used to assess the <strong>adequacy of neuromuscular blockade.</strong></p>\n<p>Neuromuscular blockade is measured using a peripheral nerve stimulator.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 130px; width: 343px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\"><strong>Nerve</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px;\"><strong>Response</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Ulnar nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px;\">Contraction of adductor pollicis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Facial nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px;\">Contraction of orbicularis oculi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Posterior tibial nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px;\">Contraction of flexor hallucis brevis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2724","text":"You observe the catheter s​hown in the image being u​sed by your attending in ‌the ICU. Which of the fol‌lowing procedures is it u​sed for?","raw_text":"You observe the catheter shown in the image being used by your attending in the ICU. Which of the following procedures is it used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastro-Esoph​ageal varices","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embol​ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary artery catheteri​zation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary cat​heterization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pulmonary artery catheterization","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f8d0b11de0b4652bd14d3fa63e4b8f7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pulmonary artery catheterization</strong></p><p>The image shown above is the<strong> Swan-ganz catheter</strong> which is used for<strong> pulmonary artery catheterization</strong>. It is also called the pulmonary artery catheter. The standard pulmonary artery catheter is 110 cm long with a 7.0 – 9.0 Fr circumference. </p>\n<p>Uses of Swan-ganz catheter<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Measurement of pressures in right atrium, right ventricle and pulmonary artery</li>\n<li>Indirect measurement of left atrial pressure through pulmonary capillary wedge pressure</li>\n<li>Cardiac output</li>\n<li>Oxygen saturation- pulmonary samples to rule out left-right shunt</li>\n<li>Monitoring left ventricle filling pressure</li>\n<li>Differentiate cardiogenic from non cardiogenic pulmonary edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2727","text":"What is the most common co​mplication of pulmonary a​rtery catheterization?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication of pulmonary artery catheterization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infe​ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombo​embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arrhy​thmia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ar​tery rupture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arrhythmia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arrhythmia</strong></p><p>The most common complication of pulmonary artery catheterization is <strong>arrhythmias</strong> and are usually self-limiting.</p>\n<p>Since the occurrence of these arrhythmias is so common, it is considered as an <strong>indicator</strong> of the placement of the catheter in the corresponding cardiac chamber.</p>\n<p>Complications of pulmonary artery catheterization:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Catheterization related:</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Arrhythmias, ventricular fibrillation</li>\n<li>Right bundle branch block, complete heart block</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Catheter residence related:</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Mechanical, catheter knots</li>\n<li>Thromboembolism</li>\n<li>Pulmonary infarction</li>\n<li>Infection, endocarditis</li>\n<li>Endocardial damage, cardiac valve injury</li>\n<li>Pulmonary artery rupture</li>\n<li>Pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysm</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Others:</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Misinterpretation of data</li>\n<li>Misuse of equipment</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2733","text":"You are assisting your att​ending in conducting a pu​lmonary artery catheteris‌ation for a patient in th‌e ICU. You are asked to e​xplain your understanding‌ of the procedure in brie​f. Which of the following​ statements would be inco‍rrect?","raw_text":"You are assisting your attending in conducting a pulmonary artery catheterisation for a patient in the ICU. You are asked to explain your understanding of the procedure in brief. Which of the following statements would be incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It involves direct measure​ment of left atrial press​ure.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Swan ganz catheter is used​.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac arrhythmia is the ​most frequent complicatio​n.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It estimates cardiac outpu​t.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It involves direct measurement of left atrial pressure.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fa1d31394b84a5b92a10f91929750d8x295x219.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It involves direct measurement of left atrial pressure.</strong></p><p>Left atrial pressure (LAP) is<strong> indirectly</strong> measured through <strong>pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)</strong>.</p>\n<p>LAP has been the gold standard for evaluating mitral stenosis and decompensated congestive heart failure. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Swan-Ganz catheter</strong> is used for pulmonary artery catherization.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Cardiac arrhythmias</strong> are the most frequent complication.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 123\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option D: The catheter is incorporated with <strong>thermistors</strong> that can be used to measure <strong>cardiac output</strong>, from which a multitude of hemodynamic values can be derived.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2735","text":"In a patient undergoing pu​lmonary artery catheteris​ation, which of the follo‌wing would denote entry i‌nto the pulmonary artery?​","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing pulmonary artery catheterisation, which of the following would denote entry into the pulmonary artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in systolic press​ure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in systolic press​ure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in diastolic pres​sure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in diastolic pres​sure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increase in diastolic pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increase in diastolic pressure</strong></p><p>Entry into the pulmonary artery is indicated by an <strong>increase</strong> in<strong> diastolic pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>Pulmonary artery catheterization involves the use of<strong> </strong>a<strong> Swan-Ganz catheter.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2729","text":"A patient who is planned f​or cardiac surgery requir​es central venous pressur‌e (CVP) monitoring. What ‌is the preferred site for​ central venous cannulati‌on?","raw_text":"A patient who is planned for cardiac surgery requires central venous pressure (CVP) monitoring. What is the preferred site for central venous cannulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left internal jugular vein​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right internal jugular vei​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subclav​ian vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axilla​ry vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right internal jugular vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/141ec86d8f454f1b88f0e55e2d767a55x1280x1204.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right internal jugular vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>right internal jugular vein</strong> is the most preferred vein for central venous cannulation.</p>\n<p>Reasons for preference:   </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of valves</li>\n<li>Consistent anatomic location of the internal jugular vein</li>\n<li>Easily identifiable surface landmarks</li>\n<li>Direct communication with the right atrium. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other sites for central venous cannulation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavian vein: greater risk of pneumothorax</li>\n<li>Left internal jugular vein: greater risk of pleural effusion &amp; chylothorax</li>\n<li>Femoral vein: increased risk of thromboembolic complications, infection, and vascular injury.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2738","text":"Cardiac output can be dete​rmined using the followin​g except?","raw_text":"Cardiac output can be determined using the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thermal dilution technique​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Esophagea​l doppler","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Echocard​iography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capnography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capnography</strong></p><p><strong>Capnography</strong> is a measurement of <strong>end-tidal CO<sub>2,</sub></strong> which is used to confirm tracheal intubation and assess ventilation in the perioperative period.</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output measurements technique</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Thermodilution</li>\n<li>Dye dilution, using indocyanine green</li>\n<li>Lithium dilution</li>\n<li>Pulse contour devices</li>\n<li>Esophageal doppler</li>\n<li>Thoracic bioimpedance</li>\n<li>Echocardiography</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Thermal dilution method uses a non-toxic liquid of temperature different from the blood. This fluid is injected into a vein and is detected on the arterial side using a catheter with a temperature sensor. The rate of flow of this liquid is used to calculate the cardiac output.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2741","text":"In a patient undergoing su​rgery, which is the singl​e best lead in the ECG to‌ diagnose arrhythmias?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing surgery, which is the single best lead in the ECG to diagnose arrhythmias?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lea​d I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lea​d V2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lea​d II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lea​d V5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lead II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lead II</strong></p><p><strong>Lead II </strong>is the best for detecting <strong>arrhythmias</strong> and <strong>inferior wall myocardial infarction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lead V5 </strong>is th best for detecting <strong>anterior </strong>and<strong> lateral wall myocardial infarction.</strong></p>\n<p>For <strong>arrhythmia</strong>, oesophagal leads are better than lead II but they are not yet put into wide use.</p>\n<p>For <strong>myocardial ischemia</strong>, new regional wall motion abnormalities and decreased systolic wall thickening on <strong>transesophageal echocardiography</strong> are more sensitive than <strong>ECG</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2743","text":"In a patient undergoing mi​tral valve repair, which ​is the most sensitive ind‌icator of myocardial isch‌emia?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing mitral valve repair, which is the most sensitive indicator of myocardial ischemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ECG – ​Lead V5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary artery catheteri​sation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thoracic b​ioimpedance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transesophageal echocardio​graphy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transesophageal echocardiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transesophageal echocardiography</strong></p><p><strong>Transesophageal echocardiography</strong> is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> indicator of myocardial ischemia.  </p>\n<p>New regional wall motion abnormalities <strong>(RWMA)</strong> and <strong>decreased systolic wall thickening</strong> on transesophageal echocardiography are more sensitive than ECG.</p>\n<p>Ischemia may be indicated clinically by a sudden onset intraoperative arrhythmia or conduction block.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0874","text":"Which of the following inv​estigations provides the ​most accurate prognostic ‌information with respect ‌to predicting risks of pe​rioperative cardiac compl‌ications?","raw_text":"Which of the following investigations provides the most accurate prognostic information with respect to predicting risks of perioperative cardiac complications?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exercise E​CG testing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dobutamine stress echocard​iography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myocardial perfusion scint​igraphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coronary a​ngiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dobutamine stress echocardiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dobutamine stress echocardiography</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1280\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Among the given investigations, <strong>dobutamine stress echocardiography</strong> provides the most accurate prognostic information with respect to predicting risks of perioperative cardiac complications.</p>\n<p>Preoperative cardiac stress testing can help identify the presence and severity of preexisting CAD. The prognostic performance of these tests is better assessed based on positive and negative likelihood ratios. Among all the given options, dobutamine stress echocardiography has the maximum positive likelihood ratio and hence provides the most accurate prognostic information.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 545\">Even though coronary angiography is considered the gold standard, it may not absolutely risk-stratify patients because many perioperative cardiac events may be related to noncritical coronary lesions.</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2750","text":"You are a junior resident ​conducting research on th​e efficacy of different m‌ethods to monitor the dep‌th of anaesthesia. Which ​of the following methods ‌will be not be considered​ for evaluation in your r​esearch?","raw_text":"You are a junior resident conducting research on the efficacy of different methods to monitor the depth of anaesthesia. Which of the following methods will be not be considered for evaluation in your research?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E​EG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bispectr​al index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auditory evo​ked potential","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capnography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capnography</strong></p><p>Capnography is not considered as it <strong>cannot</strong> be used to monitor the depth of anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>Capnography is a quantitative measure of <strong>end-tidal carbon dioxide</strong> that is used to assess <strong>ventilation</strong> in the perioperative period and confirm the correct placement of an endotracheal tube.</p>\n<p>Monitors used to assess the depth of anaesthesia: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Electroencephalography (EEG)</li>\n<li>Bispectral index (BIS)</li>\n<li>Entropy</li>\n<li>Evoked responses (EP) - Motor EP, sensory EP (Auditory, visual, brain stem evoked potential)</li>\n<li>Patient state index</li>\n<li>Narcotrend.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2751","text":"After administering the in​duction agent for a patie​nt, the anaesthesia junio‌r resident is observing t‌he EEG machine to check t​he depth of anaesthesia. ‌He observes β waves on th​e EEG. What does it signi​fy?","raw_text":"After administering the induction agent for a patient, the anaesthesia junior resident is observing the EEG machine to check the depth of anaesthesia. He observes β waves on the EEG.  What does it signify?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Light an​aesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Profound h​ypothermia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep an​esthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arousab​le state","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Light anaesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Light anaesthesia</strong></p><p>Beta waves on EEG signify light anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Beta waves on EEG are seen in<strong> awake state</strong> and during <strong>initial stages of inhalational anesthesia.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 144\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<table style=\"height: 153px; width: 492px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>EEG waves</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"><strong>Frequency</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Depth of anesthesia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Alpha</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">8–13 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Resting adult with eyes closed, arousable state of sedation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Beta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">13-30 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Concentrating individuals, under anesthesia during initial stages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Theta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">4–7 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Sleeping individuals and during anesthesia maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Delta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">0.5–4 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Brain injury, deep sleep, and anesthesia maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Iso electric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Anesthesia induced coma, profound hypothermia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2754","text":"A young boy is scheduled f​or a sigmoidoscopy. He ha​s a history of epilepsy a‌nd aggressive behaviour. ‌The procedure was conduct​ed under general anaesthe‌sia when an isoelectric p​attern was observed on th​e EEG monitor. Which of t‍he following is not a dif‌ferential diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young boy is scheduled for a sigmoidoscopy. He has a history of epilepsy and aggressive behaviour. The procedure was conducted under general anaesthesia when an isoelectric pattern was observed on the EEG monitor. Which of the following is not a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normally during anesthesia​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Profound h​ypothermia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Profound h​yperthermia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anesthetic ​induced coma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Profound hyperthermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Profound hyperthermia</strong></p><p>Isoelectric EEG is not seen during hyperthermia and hence is not a differential diagnosis for this patient.</p>\n<p>Causes of isoelectric EEG:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brief periods during normal maintenance of anesthesia</li>\n<li>Anesthetic induced coma</li>\n<li>Profound hypothermia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Interpretation of an EEG: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>α, β oscillations : arousable state of sedation</li>\n<li>α, θ oscillations with δ dominance: surgically anesthetized state</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 153px; width: 492px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>EEG waves</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"><strong>Frequency</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Depth of anesthesia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Alpha</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">8–13 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Resting adult with eyes closed, arousable state of sedation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Beta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">13-30 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Concentrating individuals, under anesthesia during initial stages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Theta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">4–7 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Sleeping individuals and during anesthesia maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Delta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">0.5–4 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Brain injury, deep sleep, and anesthesia maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Iso electric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Anesthesia induced coma, profound hypothermia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2755","text":"In a patient who is under ​anaesthesia, which is the​ most common method used ‌to evaluate intraoperativ‌e awareness?","raw_text":"In a patient who is under anaesthesia, which is the most common method used to evaluate intraoperative awareness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"E​EG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bispectr​al index","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transesophageal echocardio​graphy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bispectral index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bispectral index</strong></p><p>The <strong>bispectral index</strong> (BIS) is the most commonly used method, which helps to monitor the <strong>depth of anaesthesia </strong>and <strong>intraoperative awareness.</strong></p>\n<p>It processes the EEG and provides an index value between<strong> 0 and 100</strong> that indicates the patient’s level of consciousness.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">BIS index range: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">100– Awake (Response to normal voice)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">65-85- Sedation</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">40-65- General anaesthesia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">&lt; 40 - Cortical suppression</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">0- Flatline EEG (Deep coma)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended range of BIS index values for anaesthesia- <strong>40-60.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2756","text":"In a patient who is under ​general anaesthesia, what​ are the recommended bisp‌ectral index (BIS) values‌?","raw_text":"In a patient who is under general anaesthesia, what are the recommended bispectral index (BIS) values?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20​-40","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40​-60","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"60​-80","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80-​100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 40-60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 40-60</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recommended range of bispectral index <strong>(BIS)</strong> values for general anaesthesia is <strong>40-60</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">BIS helps to monitor the depth of anaesthesia and prevent intraoperative awareness. It processes the EEG and provides an index value between 0 and 100 that indicates the <strong>patient’s level of consciousness.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">BIS index range: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">100– Awake (Response to normal voice)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">65-85- Sedation</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">40-65- General anaesthesia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">&lt; 40 - Cortical suppression</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">0- Flatline EEG (Deep coma)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2757","text":"Which among the following ​is most commonly used to ​monitor the depth of anae‌sthesia?","raw_text":"Which among the following is most commonly used to monitor the depth of anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E​EG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Provoked lower oesophageal​ contractility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ent​ropy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bispectr​al index","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bispectral index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bispectral index</strong></p><p>The bispectral index is the most commonly used method to monitor the depth of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Note<strong>: Middle latency auditory evoked potential</strong> is the most sensitive indicator to evaluate the depth of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2758","text":"A young boy developed mass​eter spasm and tachycardi​a after being administere‌d succinylcholine for RSI‌. Which of the following ​is the best site for moni‌toring core body temperat​ure in this patient?","raw_text":"A young boy developed masseter spasm and tachycardia after being administered succinylcholine for RSI. Which of the following is the best site for monitoring core body temperature in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral ​cavity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esop​hagus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Axi​lla","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rec​tum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esophagus</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>malignant hyperthermia</strong> in this patient and the <strong>distal oesophagus</strong> is the best site for core temperature monitoring in this condition.    </p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary artery </strong>is the <strong>most accurate site</strong> for temperature measurement but it is not used routinely due to a higher incidence of complications. It is useful in patients already on Swan-Ganz catheter.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Oral cavity (sublingual)</strong> readings can be influenced by mouth breathing, tachypnea, and ambient temperature.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Axilla </strong>readings are influenced by contact with probe, skin perfusion, environment, etc.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Rectum </strong>readings are higher than central sites due to heat-producing flora in feces.</p>\n<p>Other sites for core thermal monitoring<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tympanic membrane</li>\n<li>Nasopharynx</li>\n<li>Trachea</li>\n<li>Skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2759","text":"Which of the following sit​es most accurately measur​es the brain temperature?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following sites most accurately measures the brain temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or​al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tympanic​ membrane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nasop​harynx","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal e​sophagus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tympanic membrane</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The<strong> tympanic membrane</strong> measures the brain temperature most accurately.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The tympanic membrane and the hypothalamus share a common blood supply. Hence, measuring the temperature at this site reflects thermal information at the primary site of thermoregulation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Disadvantages</strong> are <strong>trauma</strong> during insertion and cerumen insulation, so it is not routinely used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2760","text":"Which thermoregulatory mec​hanism is best preserved ​during anesthesia?","raw_text":"Which thermoregulatory mechanism is best preserved during anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shiv​ering","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Swea​ting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vasocons​triction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non shivering thermogenesi​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sweating","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sweating</strong></p><p><strong>Sweating</strong> is the best preserved thermoregulatory mechanism during anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Option A and C: General anesthetics decrease the thresholds (triggering core temperatures) for vasoconstriction and shivering by 2°C to 3°C.</p>\n<p>Option D: Nonshivering thermogenesis does not occur in anesthetized patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2719","text":"Which of the following is ​a true statement regardin​g Allen's test?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement regarding Allen's test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Used to confirm Ryle's tub​e position","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Used to confirm tracheal i​ntubation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Used to diagnose wrist dis​location","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used to assess adequacy of​ collateral blood flow","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Used to assess adequacy of collateral blood flow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Used to assess adequacy of collateral blood flow</strong></p><p><strong>Allen's Test</strong> is used to test the adequacy of <strong>collateral blood flow</strong> to the hand. It mandatory before radial artery cannulation and sometimes, before drawing blood for arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis.</p>\n<p>Method<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p>It is performed by occluding the radial and ulnar arteries and asking the patient to make a tight fist so as to exsanguinate the palm.</p>\n<p>Then, the patient is asked to open the hand and the occlusion over the ulnar artery is released. If the palm remains pale for more than 6-10 seconds, it indicates reduced ulnar collateral flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0245","text":"You are an intern posted i​n the ICU. While taking a​ blood sample for ABG, wh‌ich of the following step‌s would you not do?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the ICU. While taking a blood sample for ABG, which of the following steps would you not do?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal ph can't rule out a​cid base disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Add 0.5 ml Heparin to the ​syringe before taking blo​od","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"If modified Allens test is​ negative, alternate site​ is chosen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial artery is the prefe​rred site","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Add 0.5 ml Heparin to the syringe before taking blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5a50ea4a086458fa13ff35b1dd354e3x1280x3192.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Add 0.5 ml Heparin to the syringe before taking blood</strong></p><p>0.5 ml of Heparin is not added to the syringe before taking blood.</p>\n<p>Very little <strong>heparin</strong> is needed in the sample to prevent clotting;<strong> 0.05 to 0.10 ml of a dilute solution</strong> will help prevent coagulation of 1 ml of blood without affecting its pH. This is achieved by drawing 0.5 ml into the syringe and <strong>flushing it out.</strong> The residue that remains adherent to the syringe is sufficient for the procedure.</p>\n<p>Heparin must be added to the syringe as an <strong>anticoagulant</strong>. Because the pH of heparin is near 7.0, and the P<span class=\"small-caps\">o</span><sub>2</sub> and P<span class=\"small-caps\">co</span><sub>2</sub> of the heparin solution are near room air values, excess heparin can alter ABG measurements. After flushing the syringe with heparin, a sufficient amount usually remains in the dead space of the syringe and needle for anticoagulation without distortion of the ABG determination.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Modified Allen's test positive indicates that the collateral blood flow is normal</li>\n<li>Modified Allen's test negative indicates that the collateral blood flow is abnormal and an alternate site must be chosen. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"DTeq4cUP","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"3_embryonic_phase_of_development","title":"3.Embryonic Phase Of Development","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB0369","text":"Formation of which of the ​following indicates the b​eginning of gastrulation?‍","raw_text":"Formation of which of the following indicates the beginning of gastrulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Noto​chord","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neural​ groove","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neura​l pit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primitiv​e streak","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primitive streak","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e7280f530314a64a52f7608f7593e70x1280x1315.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primitive streak</strong></p><p>The formation of the<strong> primitive streak</strong> from the <strong>epi</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>blast</strong> is an indicator of the beginning of<strong> gastrulation</strong>. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">By the <strong>15th day</strong> of development, some <strong>epiblast cells</strong> lying along the central axis, near the <strong>tail end of the embryonic disc</strong>, begin to proliferate. These proliferated cells form an <strong>elevation</strong> called <strong>primitive streak</strong> that bulges into the amniotic cavity. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Gastrulation</strong> is the process that forms all the 3 germ layers namely, ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm in the embryo. It occurs during the <strong>3rd week</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the formation of primitive streak.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0373","text":"Which of the following ger​m layer(s) are formed fro​m the epiblast?","raw_text":"Which of the following germ layer(s) are formed from the epiblast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecto​derm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoderm, Mesoderm, and Ec​toderm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ectoderm a​nd Mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoderm a​nd Mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoderm, Mesoderm, and Ectoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6565258b5e02405d88a2498504b01d28x600x671.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoderm, Mesoderm, and Ectoderm</strong></p><p><strong>Epiblast </strong>gives rise to all the<strong> 3 germ layers</strong> through the process of <strong>gastrulation</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">After the formation of the primitive streak, the <strong>epiblast cells migrate</strong> toward the <strong>primitive streak</strong>. When they arrive at the primitive streak, these cells become flask-shaped, detach from the epiblast, and slip beneath the primitive streak. This inward movement is known as <strong>invagination</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Some of the invaginated epiblast cells <strong>displace </strong>the<strong> hypoblast</strong> to create the<strong> embryonic endoderm</strong>. Endoderm is the <strong>first germ layer</strong> to form. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Some of the invaginated cells then start occupying the space between the epiblast and newly created endoderm to form <strong>mesoderm</strong>. Cells remaining in the epiblast then form the <strong>ectoderm</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the formation of germ layers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0376","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding intraem​bryonic mesoderm is false‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding intraembryonic mesoderm is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also known as second​ary mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It Is formed from the epib​last cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It spreads throughout the ​embryonic disc","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Most of the body tissues a​re formed from mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It spreads throughout the embryonic disc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0ff0f2deeeca4c5d9639ae024dc9a423x600x671.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It spreads throughout the embryonic disc</strong></p><p>The <strong>intraembryonic mesoderm</strong> spreads throughout the embryonic disc, except in the region of the <strong>prechordal plate</strong> and <strong>cloacal membrane</strong>. In these two regions, the ectoderm is in <strong>direct contact</strong> with the endoderm. </p>\n<p> The image given below shows the direct contact of intraembryonic mesoderm with the endoderm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The intraembryonic mesoderm or <strong>secondary mesoderm</strong> is formed by the invagination and movement of <strong>epiblast cells</strong> between the newly formed endoderm and the epiblast. </p>\n<p>In the later stages of development, the ectoderm and endoderm mostly persist as lining epithelium. Mesoderm forms most of the <strong>tissues</strong> of the <strong>body</strong>, including bone, cartilage, connective tissue, muscles (skeletal, smooth, cardiac), blood, lymph, and cardiovascular organs. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0380","text":"Arrange the following stru​ctures formed during the ​development of the defini‍tive notochord, from the ‌earlier stage to the late‍r stage. 1. Notochordal p​late 2. Notochordal proce‌ss 3. Blastopore 4. Notoc​hordal canal","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following structures formed during the development of the definitive notochord, from the earlier stage to the later stage.</p>\n<p>1. Notochordal plate<br/>2. Notochordal process<br/>3. Blastopore<br/>4. Notochordal canal</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 – 4 ​– 2 – 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 – 4 ​– 2 – 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 – 2 ​– 4 – 1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 – 2 ​– 4 – 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 – 2 – 4 – 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c181188df6e4e8c97c3985bcf4a2973x600x726.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 – 2 – 4 – 1</strong></p><p>The correct order is blastopore - notochordal process - notochordal canal - notochordal plate - definitive notochord.</p>\n<p><strong>Notochord formation</strong> steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primitive knot -</strong> The cranial end of the primitive streak thickens to form the primitive knot (primitive node or Hensen's node).</li>\n<li><strong>Primitive pit (Blastopore) -</strong> A depression appears in the center of the knot known as the primitive pit (or blastopore).</li>\n<li><strong>Notochordal process -</strong> The cells in the primitive knot multiply and pass cranially in the midline between the ectoderm and endoderm. They reach up to the caudal margin of the prechordal plate. These cells form a solid chord known as the notochordal process or head process.</li>\n<li><strong>Notochordal canal -</strong> The cavity of the blastopore then extends into the notochordal process to form a tube-like structure called the notochordal canal.</li>\n<li><strong>Notochordal plate - </strong>The floor of the notochordal canal gets intercalated (mixed up) with the endoderm and gradually breaks down. This eventually leads to the formation of a flat plate-like structure notochordal plate.</li>\n<li><strong>Definitive notochord - </strong>The cells from the notochordal plate proliferate to form a solid rod-like structure called a definitive notochord.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0382","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the not​ochord is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the notochord is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It induces neural plate fo​rmation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It persists as apical liga​ment of the dens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It persists as nucleus pul​posus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It develops from hypoblast​ cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It develops from hypoblast cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It develops from hypoblast cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>notochord </strong>is <strong>derived</strong> from the <strong>epiblast</strong> and not from the hypoblast cells. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Epiblast cells from the primitive knot region of the primitive streak multiply and pass cranially in the midline between the ectoderm and endoderm. They undergo several stages of rearrangement to form the notochord. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">During the initial stages of <strong>neurulation</strong>, the notochord plays a role in the <strong>induction </strong>of overlying <strong>ectoderm</strong> to thicken and form the <strong>neural plate.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Although the <strong>notochord regresses</strong>, it has 2 important remnants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Nucleus pulposus </strong>in the region of the intervertebral disc</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Apical ligament </strong>of the <strong>dens </strong>of axis vertebra</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0385","text":"Neurulation refers to the ​process of the formation ​of:","raw_text":"Neurulation refers to the process of the formation of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primitiv​e streak","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neural​ plate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Noto​chord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neura​l tube","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neural tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7dd6b6ae82ed4e9687a7b9ef4183e131x1280x1125.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neural tube</strong></p><p><strong>Neurulation</strong> refers to the process of the <strong>formation of</strong> the <strong>neural tube </strong>from the neural plate. The neural tube gives rise to the central nervous system.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the steps of neurulation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2875","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen​ted with an itchy elevate​d lesion on her face as s‍hown in the image. On der‌moscopy, there is an atyp‍ical pigmented network. F​rom which structure is th‌e cell responsible for th​is lesion derived?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with an itchy elevated lesion on her face as shown in the image. On dermoscopy, there is an atypical pigmented network. From which structure is the cell responsible for this lesion derived?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral pla​te mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermedia​te mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neural c​rest cell","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paraxial​ mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neural crest cell","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce664e399a5e433a825059f21cb87db7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f32bda97b5774a33a0436168244c8fb8x1280x1125.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neural crest cell</strong></p><p>The given image shows asymmetrical pigmented lesion on the face with an irregular border. This is suggestive of <strong>malignant melanoma</strong> arising from the <strong>melanocytes.</strong> Melanocytes are derived from<strong> neural crest cells </strong>(NCC). </p>\n<p>During neurulation, when the neural folds elevate and fuse, the cells at the lateral border (the neural crest) of the neuroectoderm dissociate from their neighboring cells. These <strong>neural crest cells </strong>undergo an epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition while leaving the neuroectoderm and then migrate to the underlying mesoderm.</p>\n<p>NCCs contribute to many organs and tissues and are sometimes referred to as the fourth germ layer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the<strong> trunk region, </strong>they form: \n<ul>\n<li>Melanocytes</li>\n<li>Sensory ganglia</li>\n<li>Sympathetic and enteric neurons</li>\n<li>Schwann cells</li>\n<li>Adrenal medulla cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In the <strong>cranial region</strong>, they contribute to the: \n<ul>\n<li>Craniofacial skeleton</li>\n<li>Neurons for cranial ganglia</li>\n<li>Satellite glial cells</li>\n<li>Melanocytes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Some of the other neural crest <strong>derivatives</strong> are:\n<ul>\n<li>C cells of the thyroid gland</li>\n<li>Conotruncal septum in the heart</li>\n<li>Odontoblasts</li>\n<li>Smooth muscle cells to blood vessels to face and forebrain.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Lateral plate mesoderm</strong> is the lateral layer of the intraembryonic mesoderm. This is a thin layer. This layer develops a large cavity called the intraembryonic cavity and gives rise to a somatic or parietal mesodermal layer and a splanchnic or visceral mesodermal layer. The intraembryonic cavity gives rise to <strong>pericardial, pleural,</strong> and <strong>peritoneal cavities.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Intermediate mesoderm </strong>is a longitudinal strip of intraembryonic mesoderm that connects paraxial with the lateral plate mesoderm. The <strong>urinary </strong>and<strong> genital systems</strong> are derived from the intermediate mesoderm.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Paraxial mesoderm </strong>is the thickened plate of the intraembryonic mesoderm close to the midline, on either side of the notochord. This forms the <strong>somitomeres</strong> and <strong>somites</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0388","text":"Anomaly scan in a pregnant​ woman revealed renal age​nesis in the fetus. Which‍ of the following mesoder‌mal layers is involved?","raw_text":"Anomaly scan in a pregnant woman revealed renal agenesis in the fetus. Which of the following mesodermal layers is involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extra-embryo​nic mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraxial​ mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intermedia​te mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral pla​te mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intermediate mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b772fd2d29134e3bac1a3bf392d2e46bx1280x1454.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intermediate mesoderm</strong></p><p><strong>Kidneys</strong> are developed from the <strong>intermediate mesoderm</strong>. </p>\n<p>After the notochord is formed, the <strong>intraembryonic mesoderm</strong> on either side can be seen organized into 3 layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Paraxial </strong>mesoderm<strong> -</strong> Thickened plate of the mesoderm close to the midline, on either side of the notochord. This forms the <strong>somitomeres</strong> and <strong>somites</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Intermediate </strong>mesoderm<strong> -</strong> Longitudinal strip of mesoderm that connects paraxial with the lateral plate mesoderm. The <strong>urinary </strong>and<strong> genital systems</strong> are derived from the intermediate mesoderm.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Lateral plate </strong>mesoderm<strong> -</strong> A thinner layer of mesoderm that lies laterally. This layer develops a large cavity called the intraembryonic coelom that gives rise to <strong>pericardial</strong>, <strong>pleural</strong>, and <strong>peritoneal cavities</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0389","text":"A 26-year-old man with con​genital scoliosis is foun​d to have hemivertebrae. ‍The affected structures a‌re derivatives of which l‍ayer of the mesoderm?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man with congenital scoliosis is found to have hemivertebrae. The affected structures are derivatives of which layer of the mesoderm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral ​mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraxial​ mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intermedia​te mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extraembryo​nic mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paraxial mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paraxial mesoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>vertebrae</strong> are derived from <strong>somites</strong> which are derivatives of <strong>paraxial mesoderm</strong>. The paraxial mesoderm during development gets organized into segments called <strong>somitomeres</strong> and <strong>somites.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Somitomeres </strong>appear in the head region of the embryo. They are rounded structures and <strong>7</strong> in number. They form the <strong>mesoderm </strong>and<strong> muscles </strong>of the<strong> head </strong>and<strong> jaw</strong>. </p>\n<p>From the <strong>occipital region </strong>downwards, somitomeres organize into somites. The first pair of somites arise in the occipital region of the embryo at approximately the 20th day of development. From here, new somites appear in craniocaudal sequence at a rate of approximately 3 pairs per day.</p>\n<p>By the end of the <strong>5th week</strong>, <strong>42-44 pairs</strong> of<strong> somites</strong> are present. There are 4 occipital, 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 8 to 10 coccygeal pairs. The first occipital and the last five to seven coccygeal somites later disappear. The remaining somites form the axial skeleton.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0392","text":"Which of the following giv​es rise to the pericardia​l cavity?","raw_text":"Which of the following gives rise to the pericardial cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paraxial​ mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermedia​te mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral pla​te mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extraembryo​nic mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral plate mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ab7a3594de34ecd84bee69ac25d66aex1280x741.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral plate mesoderm</strong></p><p>The<strong> pericardial cavity</strong> is formed by the <strong>intraembryonic celom</strong> in the <strong>lateral plate mesoderm</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Within the lateral plate mesoderm, a large cavity known as intraembryonic celom is formed. The <strong>intraembryonic celom</strong> forms the <strong>pericardial</strong>, <strong>pleural</strong>, and <strong>peritoneal cavities</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>lateral plate mesoderm </strong>is divided by the intraembryonic celom to form:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Somatopleuric</strong> or <strong>parietal</strong> layer of intraembryonic mesoderm that is in contact with the <strong>ectoderm</strong>. This forms the parietal layer of peritoneal, pleural, and pericar<span class=\"s1\">dial membranes</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Splanchnopleuric</strong> or <strong>visceral</strong> layer of intraembryonic mesoderm that is in contact with <strong>endoderm</strong>. This forms the visceral layer of the peritoneal, pleural, and pericar<span class=\"s1\">dial membranes</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the somatopleuric and splanchnopleuric layers and the intraembryonic celom.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0618","text":"Nucleus pulposus is derive​d from ____________.","raw_text":"Nucleus pulposus is derived from ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d74387d8592a4c6c80bca98e01cf6b98.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p><strong>Nucleus pulposus</strong> is derived from the <strong>notochord </strong>(marked as 1).</p>\n<p>The other labelled structures are</p>\n<p>2 - Ectoderm<br/>3 - Endoderm<br/>4 - Intraembryonic mesoderm</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0393","text":"Which of following structu​res becomes the primitive​ gut?","raw_text":"Which of following structures becomes the primitive gut?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Part of amniotic cavity li​ned by the mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Part of amniotic cavity li​ned by the endoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Part of yolk sac lined by ​the endoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Part of yolk sac lined by ​the mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Part of yolk sac lined by the endoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d28e7d01f20421e96fecbadb37f3f0bx1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Part of yolk sac lined by the endoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>primitive gut</strong> is derived from the part of the <strong>yolk sac </strong>lined by the<strong> endoderm</strong>. </p>\n<p>With the development of the lateral folds of the embryo, a part of the <strong>yolk sac </strong>gets <strong>enclosed</strong> within the embryo, to form a tube-like structure. This structure is <strong>lined</strong> by the<strong> endoderm</strong> and is called the <strong>primitive gut</strong>.</p>\n<p>Initially, the primitive gut has a wide communication with the yolk sac. Later, three different sections of the primitive gut can be recognized:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foregut -</strong> Part of the primitive gut cranial to the communication with the yolk sac</li>\n<li><strong>Midgut -</strong> Middle part of the primitive gut in communication with the yolk sac</li>\n<li><strong>Hindgut -</strong> Part of the primitive gut caudal to the communication with the yolk sac</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the sections of primitive gut.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0408","text":"A newborn presented to the​ NICU with respiratory di​stress and meconium disch‍arge from the umbilicus. ‌The condition occurs due ‍to the patency of the duc​t that connects the primi‌tive gut to the _________​___.","raw_text":"A newborn presented to the NICU with respiratory distress and meconium discharge from the umbilicus. The condition occurs due to the patency of the duct that connects the primitive gut to the ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Embryonic ​body cavity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorioni​c cavity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amnioti​c cavity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yolk​ sac","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Yolk sac","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6869cb2c1dca4adda38d5014970d5727x1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Yolk sac</strong></p><p>The presence of meconium discharge at the umbilicus is suggestive of <strong>vitelline fistula.</strong> It occurs due to <strong>patent vitelline duct</strong>, which connects the <strong>primitive gut</strong> to the<strong> yolk sac.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>primitive gut</strong> is connected to the <strong>definitive</strong> <strong>yolk</strong> <strong>sac</strong> through a narrow stalk-like communication known as the <strong>vitellointestinal duct</strong>. It is also known as <strong>vitelline duct</strong> or <strong>yolk stalk</strong> or <strong>omphalomesenteric duct</strong>. </p>\n<p>Due to the folding of the embryo in the cephalocaudal and lateral directions, the embryo gets enclosed all around by ectoderm, except in the region through which the vitellointestinal duct passes. In this region, there is a <strong>circular aperture</strong>, which is now called the <strong>umbilical</strong> <strong>opening. </strong>The vitellointestinal duct completely obliterates by the 7th week of development.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Vitelline duct <strong>abnormalities:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meckel's diverticulum: </strong>In about 2% o the individuals, a portion of the vitelline duct persists an outpouching of the <strong>ileum</strong> called, <strong>Meckel diverticulum. </strong>The diverticulum is usually asymptomatic unless it contains <strong>heterotopic pancreatic</strong> or <strong>gastric mucosa,</strong> which may lead to bleeding, ulceration, or perforation.</li>\n<li><strong>Vitelline cyst: </strong>In some individuals, both the ends of the vitelline duct transforms into fibrous cords. However, the <strong>middle portion</strong> of the duct forms a large <strong>cyst</strong> called, <strong>enterocystoma,</strong> or <strong>vitelline cyst. </strong>As the fibrous cords run across the peritoneal cavity, it may lead to intestinal obstruction, strangulation, or volvulus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0404","text":"A 32-year-old primigravida​ who is a known diabetic,​ had a spontaneous miscar‍riage. The baby had the d‌eformity as shown in the ‍image below. Which of the​ following defects during‌ embryonal development is​ likely to cause the give‌n condition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old primigravida who is a known diabetic, had a spontaneous miscarriage. The baby had the deformity as shown in the image below. Which of the following defects during embryonal development is likely to cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insufficient formation of ​mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Persistence of pluripotent​ cells in primitive strea​k","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defect in the development ​of notochord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of cells in the anter​ior midline of the germ d​isc","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insufficient formation of mesoderm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bb8d05840f034ecaa136a76fe0fadf4a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insufficient formation of mesoderm</strong></p><p>The condition shown in the above image is <strong>sirenomelia. </strong>It is caused due to <strong>insufficient formation of mesoderm</strong> in the <strong>caudal most region</strong> of the embryo.</p>\n<p>The mesoderm in the caudal region is involved in the formation of lower limbs, urogenital system, and lumbosacral vertebrae. Therefore, the defects seen in this condition include hypoplasia and fusion of the lower limbs, vertebral abnormalities, renal agenesis, imperforate anus, and anomalies of the genital organs. </p>\n<p>During the third week of development, a process called <strong>gastrulation</strong> occurs which leads to the formation of all three germ layers. The process begins with the formation of <strong>the primitive streak,</strong> which develops into <strong>the primitive node</strong> and <strong>primitive pit.</strong> The primitive pit further invaginates and organizes itself into cells arranged in three layers, which eventually develop into <strong>ectoderm, mesoderm,</strong> and <strong>endoderm.</strong></p>\n<p>Gastrulation related <strong>abnormalities:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sacrococcygeal teratomas</strong> occur due to <strong>remnants of pluripotent cells </strong>from the<strong> primitive streak</strong>. They are the most common tumor in newborns, occurring at a frequency of 1 in 37,000 births. Sacrococcygeal teratomas contain tissues derived from all three germ layers. When <strong>pluripotent cells</strong> from the primitive streak remain in the <strong>sacrococcygeal region, </strong>they proliferate to form sacrococcygeal teratomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Holoprosencephaly </strong>is a <strong>deficiency</strong> in the<strong> midline craniofacial structures</strong>. It occurs due to teratogenic exposure (such as high alcohol dose) during gastrulation which kills the cells in the anterior midline of the embryonic disc. In holoprosencephaly, the forebrain is small, the two lateral ventricles are often merged into a single ventricle, and the eyes are close together (hypotelorism). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0410","text":"Which of the following blo​od vessels in the connect​ing stalk disappears duri‍ng development?","raw_text":"Which of the following blood vessels in the connecting stalk disappears during development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ​artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left ​artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right​ vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left​ vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right vein</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>right vein</strong> in the <strong>connecting stalk</strong> <strong>disappears</strong> during development. </p>\n<p>At first, the connecting stalk is at the caudal end of the embryonic disc. With the formation of the tail fold, the connecting stalk moves to the ventral part of the embryo, near the area of the umbilical opening. </p>\n<p>The blood vessels connecting the placenta to the embryo pass through the connecting stalk. <strong>Initially,</strong> there are <strong>2 arteries</strong> and <strong>2 veins</strong>. Later the <strong>right vein disappears</strong> (the <strong>left vein is left behind</strong>). The connecting stalk then consists of <strong>2 arteries</strong> and <strong>1 vein</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0413","text":"Which of the following str​uctures develops into Wha​rton’s jelly?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures develops into Wharton’s jelly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somatopleur​ic mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splanchnople​uric mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extraembryo​nic mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paraxial​ mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Extraembryonic mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Extraembryonic mesoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>extraembryonic mesoderm</strong> of the <strong>connecting stalk</strong> develops into a gelatinous substance known as <strong>Wharton’s jelly.  </strong></p>\n<p>When the tail fold of the embryo is formed, the connecting stalk moves towards the ventral aspect of the embryo. The connecting stalk is now attached only in the region of the umbilical opening. The extraembryonic mesoderm of the connecting stalk becomes Wharton's jelly in the umbilical cord. Wharton's jelly <strong>protects</strong> the <strong>blood vessels</strong> in the umbilical cord. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EOtgzLqI","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"3_glycogen_metabolism_and_glycogen_storage_disorders","title":"3.Glycogen Metabolism And Glycogen Storage Disorders","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1670","text":"Which of the following pai​rs correctly represents t​he primary glycosidic bon​d linkage and the branch-‌point bond linkage in gly​cogen, respectively?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs correctly represents the primary glycosidic bond linkage and the branch-point bond linkage in glycogen, respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α(1→4) linkage; α(1→6) lin​kage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"α (1→6) linkage; α (1→4) l​inkage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"α (1→4) linkage; α (1→4) l​inkage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"α (1→6) linkage; α(1→6) li​nkage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. α(1→4) linkage; α(1→6) linkage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. α(1→4) linkage; α(1→6) linkage</strong></p><p>In <strong>glycogen,</strong> the <strong>primary glycosidic bond</strong> is an <strong>α (1→4) linkage,</strong> whereas the <strong>branch point</strong> contains an <strong>α (1→6) linkage.</strong></p>\n<p>Glycogen is a branched-chain homopolysaccharide with α-D-glucose being the monosaccharide, its major constituent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1668","text":"In which of the following ​organs does glycogen synt​hesis takes place?","raw_text":"In which of the following organs does glycogen synthesis takes place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver an​d kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liver an​d muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle ​and RBC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle a​nd kidney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver and muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d060b89d5c64af78d86bfd28799fa95x965x934.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver and muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Glycogenesis</strong> takes place majorly in the <strong>skeletal muscle</strong> and the<strong> liver</strong>.</p>\n<p>Glycogen functions as the secondary long-term energy storage, with the primary energy stores being <strong>fats</strong> held in <strong>adipose</strong> tissue.</p>\n<p>Glucose 6-phosphate derived from glycogen breakdown can</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Be used as a <strong>fuel</strong> for <strong>glycolysis</strong> in muscles</li>\n<li>Be converted into <strong>free glucose</strong> in the <strong>liver</strong> and subsequently released into the blood</li>\n<li>Enter into the <strong>pentose phosphate pathway</strong> to generate NADPH or ribose in a variety of tissues</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1674","text":"Where does the process of ​glycogen synthesis take p​lace?","raw_text":"Where does the process of glycogen synthesis take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitoch​ondria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi a​pparatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyt​osol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endoplasmi​c reticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cytosol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cytosol</strong></p><p>The process of <strong>glycogen synthesis</strong> takes place within the <strong>cytosol </strong>of<strong> skeletal muscle</strong> and<strong> liver, </strong>where it is stored in the<strong> </strong>hydrated form with three or four parts of water.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1676","text":"Which of the following is ​a primer acting as an acc​eptor of glucose residues​ in the process of glycog‌en synthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a primer acting as an acceptor of glucose residues in the process of glycogen synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pro​tein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carboh​ydrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li​pid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclei​c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Protein</strong></p><p>A protein named <strong>glycogenin</strong> is the primer acting as an <strong>acceptor</strong> of <strong>glucose residues</strong> in the process of <strong>glycogen synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>The ensuing entity is glycosylated further by UDP-glucose. Glycogenin further <strong>catalyzes</strong> the transfer of <strong>seven glucose residues</strong> from <strong>UDP-Glucose,</strong> in 1 → 4 linkages, to form a <strong>glycogen primer.</strong> This in turn serves as the substrate for the enzyme, <strong>glycogen synthase,</strong> which is the rate-limiting enzyme of glycogen synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1672","text":"Glucose attached to which ​of the following serves a​s the donor source of glu​cose in the process of gl‌ycogen synthesis?","raw_text":"Glucose attached to which of the following serves as the donor source of glucose in the process of glycogen synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U​DP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"I​TP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​TP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"G​TP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. UDP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. UDP</strong></p><p>α-D-<strong>Glucose</strong> <strong>attached to </strong>uridine diphosphate <strong>(UDP) </strong>serves as the <strong>source of glucose</strong>, which<strong> </strong>is<strong> </strong>added to the glycogen molecule.</p>\n<p>UDP-glucose is, in turn, synthesized from glucose 1-phosphate and UTP by the enzyme UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1688","text":"Which of the following dep​icts the correct sequence​ of enzymes involved in g​lycogenolysis in the live‌r?","raw_text":"Which of the following depicts the correct sequence of enzymes involved in glycogenolysis in the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphorylase, Glucan tran​sferase, Glucose-6-phosph​atase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glycogen synthase, Phospho​rylase, Glucose-6-phospha​tase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose-6-phosphatase, Pho​sphorylase, Debranching e​nzyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphorylase, Glucose-6-p​hosphatase, Glucan transf​erase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphorylase, Glucan transferase, Glucose-6-phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphorylase, Glucan transferase, Glucose-6-phosphatase</strong></p><p>The sequence of <strong>enzymes</strong> involved in steps of <strong>glycogenolysis</strong> is <strong>phosphorylase, glucan transferase,</strong> and<strong> glucose-6-phosphatase.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1684","text":"Which of the following enz​ymes catalyze the rate-li​miting step of glycogenol​ysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes catalyze the rate-limiting step of glycogenolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycogen​ synthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycogen ph​osphorylase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose 6 ​Phosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphogl​ucomutase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glycogen phosphorylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glycogen phosphorylase</strong></p><p>The <strong>rate-limiting step</strong> in<strong> glycogenolysis</strong> is catalyzed by the enzyme,<strong> glycogen phosphorylase.</strong></p>\n<p>The process of phosphorolytic <strong>cleavage </strong>of the<strong> α1 → 4 linkages</strong> of glycogen to yield single units of glucose 1-phosphate, is catalyzed by the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase.</p>\n<p>Glycogen phosphorylase requires <strong>pyridoxal phosphate</strong> as the coenzyme The phosphate group is catalytically active. There are different isoenzymes of glycogen phosphorylase in the liver, muscle, and brain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1689","text":"Muscle cannot release gluc​ose from glycogen because​ of the deficiency of whi​ch of the following enzym‌es?","raw_text":"Muscle cannot release glucose from glycogen because of the deficiency of which of the following  enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluco​kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphogl​ucomutase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose-6-​phosphatase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle pho​sphorylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucose-6-phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucose-6-phosphatase</strong></p><p><strong>Muscles</strong> cannot release glucose into the blood due to the <strong>absence</strong> of<strong> </strong>the enzyme <strong>glucose-6-phosphatase.</strong></p>\n<p>Glucose-6-phosphatase is present in the lumen of the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of hepatocytes. Hence, <strong>hepatocytes</strong> can convert <strong>glucose-6-phosphate into glucose</strong> and release glycogen-derived glucose into the blood in order to maintain blood glucose levels stable, until gluconeogenesis kicks in. </p>\n<p>However, myocytes lack the enzyme glucose 6-phosphatase. As a result, glucose-6-phosphate cannot be dephosphorylated and released into the blood. Muscle glycogen provides a readily available source of glucose-1-phosphate for glycolysis within the muscle itself.</p>\n<p>Defects in the enzyme or glucose-6-phosphate transporter (GLUT 7) can cause type I glycogen storage disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1686","text":"Which of the following vit​amins serves as the cofac​tor of the enzyme glycoge​n phosphorylase?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins serves as the cofactor of the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam​in B1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam​in B2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VItam​in B7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin B6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin B6</strong></p><p><strong>Glycogen phosphorylase</strong> requires <strong>pyridoxal phosphate,</strong> i.e., <strong>vitamin B6</strong>, as its coenzyme.</p>\n<p>The process of phosphorolytic cleavage of the 1 → 4 linkages of glycogen to yield single units of glucose 1-phosphate, is catalyzed by the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1692","text":"Which of the following is ​an allosteric activator o​f muscle glycogen phospho​rylase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an allosteric activator of muscle glycogen phosphorylase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​TP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glu​cose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5'​AMP","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5'AMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43a2b3d6ff8a4e2fb2595090583f36b1x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5&#x27;AMP</strong></p><p><strong>5'AMP</strong> serves as the <strong>allosteric activator</strong> of muscle glycogen phosphorylase.</p>\n<p><strong>Glycogen phosphorylase</strong> catalyzes the rate-limiting step in <strong>glycogenolysis</strong>. It is regulated by <strong>allosteric mechanisms</strong> and covalent modification by reversible <strong>phosphorylation</strong> and<strong> dephosphorylation</strong> in response to hormone action.</p>\n<p>In both liver and muscle, the glycogen phosphorylase is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Activated</strong> <strong>by phosphorylation</strong> catalyzed by phosphorylase kinase (to yield <strong>phosphorylase a</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Inactivated by dephosphorylation</strong> catalyzed by protein phosphatase-1 (to yield<strong> phosphorylase b</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Muscle phosphorylase</strong> differs from the liver isoenzyme in having a binding site for <strong>5′ AMP</strong>. It acts as an allosteric activator of the (inactive) dephosphorylated b-form of the enzyme.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 162px; width: 327.890625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 66px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"><strong>Allosteric Activator</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.890625px;\"><strong>Allosteric Inhibitor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 66px;\"><strong>Liver</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.890625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ATP</li>\n<li>Glucose-6-phosphate</li>\n<li>Free glucose</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 66px;\"><strong>Muscle </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">\n<p>    5' AMP</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.890625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ATP</li>\n<li>Glucose-6-phosphate</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1690","text":"Which of the following do ​not take part in regulati​ng the enzyme glycogen ph​osphorylase?","raw_text":"Which of the following do not take part in regulating the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cA​MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calmo​dulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protein ph​osphatase-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glyco​genin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glycogenin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dafecd9ee2034003b574e81dbcebbfdax1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glycogenin</strong></p><p><strong>Glycogenin</strong> cannot regulate glycogen phosphorylase enzyme.<strong> </strong>It is a polypeptide serving as a <strong>primer</strong> in the initiation of <strong>glycogen synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Regulation</strong> of glycogen metabolism at the level of <strong>glycogen phosphorylase:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>cAMP</strong> activates phosphorylase kinase (by a cAMP-dependent protein kinase), which in turn activates glycogen phosphorylase.</li>\n<li><strong>Calcium</strong> activates the calmodulin component of phosphorylase kinase directly, which in turn activates glycogen phosphorylase.</li>\n<li><strong>5' AMP</strong> acts allosterically to activate glycogen phosphorylase.</li>\n<li><strong>Protein phosphatase-1</strong> inactivates glycogen phosphorylase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The regulation of glycogen phosphorylase is shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1691","text":"Which of the following is ​the debranching enzyme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the debranching enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphoglu​conomutase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose 6-​phosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amylo 1,6 ​glucosidase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amylo 1,4- 1,6 transglucos​idase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amylo 1,6 glucosidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amylo 1,6 glucosidase</strong></p><p><strong>Amylo 1,6 glucosidase</strong> + <strong>α(1,4)-&gt;(1,4) glucan transferase</strong> forms the <strong>debranching enzyme.</strong></p>\n<p>When four glucose residues remain on either side of the branching point, α(1,4)-&gt;(1,4) glucan transferase transfers a trisaccharide unit from one branch of glycogen to the other following which, amylo 1,6-glucosidase catalyzes the hydrolysis of the α(1,6) glycosidic bond to liberate free glucose.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9491","text":"Under anaerobic metabolism​ which of the following s​ubstrates yields 3 molecu​les of ATP?","raw_text":"Under anaerobic metabolism which of the following substrates yields 3 molecules of ATP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glyc​ogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amino​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glu​cose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gala​ctose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87b3d4869f354b6dae3fcffa6315307bx1280x2966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycogen</strong></p><p><strong>Breakdown</strong> of <strong>glycogen</strong> under <strong>anaerobic conditions</strong> yields <strong>3 ATPs.</strong></p>\n<p>Breakdown of glycogen yields glucose-6-phosphate which can enter the glycolysis pathway. Since it bypasses the first step, one ATP is saved and anaerobic glycolysis yields 3 ATPs.</p>\n<p>The first step in glycolysis involves the conversion of glucose to glucose-6-phosphate which requires one ATP. Glycogen breakdown leads to formation of glucose-6-phosphate which can enter glycolysis hence bypassing the first step and saving one ATP.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 195px; width: 492px;\" width=\"669\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.633px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">REACTION</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.047px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">ENZYME</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">METHOD OF ATP FORMATION</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.1953px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">NET ATP PRODUCED</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.633px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3 phosphoglycerate</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.047px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Phosphoglycerate kinase</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Substrate level phosphorylation</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.1953px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">2 ATP</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.633px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Phosphoenol pyruvate to Pyruvate</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.047px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Pyruvate kinase</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Substrate level phosphorylation</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.1953px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">2 ATP</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.633px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.047px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">TOTAL PRODUCED</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.1953px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">4 ATP</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>One ATP is used for conversion of fructose-6-phosphate to fructose 1,6 biphosphate hence net ATP formed from anaerobic glycolysis of glycogen is 3ATP</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3197","text":"Which of the following is ​not a glycogen-storage di​sorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a glycogen-storage disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Her's ​Disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tarui's​ Disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scheie's​ Disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Andersen'​s Disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scheie's Disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scheie&#x27;s Disease</strong></p><p><strong>Scheie's disease</strong> is not a glycogen-storage disease, it is a type of <strong>mucopolysaccharidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1695","text":"Which of the following gly​cogen storage diseases is​ also a lysosomal storage​ disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following glycogen storage diseases is also a lysosomal storage disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Von Gierke​’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pompe’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"McArdle’​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cori’s ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pompe’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pompe’s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Pompe’s disease</strong> is the <strong>glycogen storage disease </strong>(GSD), which is also a <strong>lysosomal storage disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>A small amount of glycogen is degraded by the lysosomal enzyme, α (1→4)-glucosidase also known as <strong>acid maltase. </strong>A deficiency of this enzyme causes the accumulation of glycogen in vacuoles in the lysosomes. This is called type II GSD or Pompe’s disease.</p>\n<p>Glycogen storage disorders that are associated with the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver cirrhosis: Types III, IV, IX GSD</li>\n<li>Renal dysfunction: Type I GSD</li>\n<li>Neurologic involvement: Type II GSD</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Another GSD that is also a lysosomal storage disorder is <strong>Danon disease</strong> (or <strong>glycogen storage disease Type IIb)</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1699","text":"A 6-month-old baby was bro​ught to the clinic with a​ history of failure to th​rive. Further examination‌ shows generalized hypoto​nia and hepatomegaly. Che​st X-ray image shows mass‌ive cardiomegaly. Microsc‍opy of a muscle biopsy is​ shown in the given image‌. Which of the following ​is the treatment for this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old baby was brought to the clinic with a history of failure to thrive. Further examination shows generalized hypotonia and hepatomegaly. Chest X-ray image shows massive cardiomegaly. Microscopy of a muscle biopsy is shown in the given image. Which of the following is the treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nagl​azyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alglucosi​dase alfa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aldur​azyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elap​rase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alglucosidase alfa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e69fa3cd25447c3afbbe2ac797bfdfa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alglucosidase alfa</strong></p><p>The clinical symptoms of <strong>hypotonia, hepatomegaly,</strong> a<strong>nd</strong> <strong>cardiomegaly</strong>, along with muscle biopsy showing <strong>vacuoles </strong>are suggestive of<strong> Pompe's disease</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pompe's</strong> disease (type II glycogen storage disorder) occurs due to a mutation in <strong>alpha-1,4-glucosidase</strong> (also known as acid maltase), a <strong>lysosomal enzyme.</strong> A defect in this enzyme leads to the accumulation of glycogen in the lysosomes of peripheral, voluntary <strong>muscles</strong> and progresses to involve the <strong>cardiac muscle</strong> and <strong>diaphragm.</strong> Clinical features include <strong>hypotonia</strong> (floppy baby syndrome), cardiomyopathy, <strong>hepatomegaly,</strong> and <strong>failure</strong> to <strong>thrive. Myozyme</strong> </p>\n<p>The treatment for <strong>Pompe disease</strong> is recombinant enzyme alglucosidase alfa (Myozyme), which can prevent worsening or reverse <strong>cardiac</strong> and <strong>skeletal abnormalities.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9213","text":"A 10-year-old boy rapidly ​develops hypoglycemia aft​er moderate activity. Blo​od examination reveals ra‌ised levels of ketone bod​ies, lactic acid, and tri​glycerides. On examinatio‌n, the liver and kidneys ‍are found to be enlarged.​ Histopathology of the li‌ver shows deposits of gly​cogen in an excess amount‌. What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy rapidly develops hypoglycemia after moderate activity. Blood examination reveals raised levels of ketone bodies, lactic acid, and triglycerides. On examination, the liver and kidneys are found to be enlarged. Histopathology of the liver shows deposits of glycogen in an excess amount. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"von Gierke​’s disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pompe’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McArdle’​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cori’s ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. von Gierke’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. von Gierke’s disease</strong></p><p>The given features are consistent with <strong>v</strong><strong>on Gierke's disease.</strong> It is the most common glycogen storage disease.</p>\n<p>Enzyme deficient in this condition is <strong>glucose-6-phosphatase</strong> (common to both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis). Inheritance is autosomal recessive. </p>\n<p>Von Gierke disease (type I glycogen storage disorder) occurs due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>glucose-6-phosphatase</strong> enzyme in the <strong>liver, kidney,</strong> and <strong>intestinal</strong> mucosa. Patients present commonly by 3-4 months of age with <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> (leading to seizures), <strong>lactic acidosis,</strong> and <strong>hepatomegaly.</strong> Affected children often have a <strong>doll-like face</strong> with fat cheeks, relatively thin extremities, short stature, and a protuberant abdomen due to massive hepatomegaly. The kidneys are also enlarged, whereas the spleen and heart are not involved. Laboratory findings include hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, <strong>hyperuricemia,</strong> and <strong>hyperlipidemia.</strong></p>\n<p>A definitive <strong>diagnosis</strong> is made using a <strong>liver biopsy. Fructose</strong> and <strong>galactose restriction</strong> in the diet is advised.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1696","text":"After evaluation, a child ​with severe hypoglycemia,​ lactic acidosis, and hyp​eruricemia is diagnosed w‌ith von Gierke’s disease.​ What is the inheritance ​pattern of this disease?","raw_text":"After evaluation, a child with severe hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hyperuricemia is diagnosed with von Gierke’s disease. What is the inheritance pattern of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked ​recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked​ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autosomal recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autosomal recessive</strong></p><p><strong>Von Gierke’s disease</strong> is inherited as an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> trait.</p>\n<p>Von Gierke disease (type I glycogen storage disorder) occurs due to the deficiency of <strong>glucose-6-phosphatase</strong> enzyme in the <strong>liver, kidney,</strong> and intestinal mucosa. Patients present commonly by 3-4 months of age with <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> (leading to seizures), <strong>lactic acidosis,</strong> and <strong>hepatomegaly.</strong></p>\n<p>Affected children often have a <strong>doll-like face</strong> with fat cheeks, relatively thin extremities, short stature, and a protuberant abdomen due to massive hepatomegaly. The <strong>kidneys</strong> are also <strong>enlarged,</strong> whereas the spleen and heart are not involved. Laboratory findings include hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, <strong>hyperuricemia,</strong> and <strong>hyperlipidemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1697","text":"A 3-year-old child present​s with growth retardation​ and a history of recurre​nt attacks of hypoglycemi‌a. On examination, you no​tice doll-like facies wit​h relatively thin extremi‌ties and massive hepatome‍galy. Blood examination r​eveals increased lactic a‌cid, triglyceride, and ur​ic acid levels. All of th‌e following complications​ can be expected in this ​child except:","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child presents with growth retardation and a history of recurrent attacks of hypoglycemia. On examination, you notice doll-like facies with relatively thin extremities and massive hepatomegaly. Blood examination reveals increased lactic acid, triglyceride, and uric acid levels. All of the following complications can be expected in this child except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic​ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancre​atitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiom​yopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ​failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis in this clinical scenario is<strong> von Gierke’s disease.</strong> Cardiomyopathy is not a complication of von Gierke’s disease. On the contrary, <strong>cardiomyopathy</strong> is associated with <strong>Pompe's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Enzyme deficient in von Gierke’s disease is <strong>glucose-6-phosphatase</strong> (common to both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis). Inheritance is <strong>autosomal recessive.</strong> </p>\n<p>Glycogen accumulation occurs in the liver and renal tubular cells. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Ketosis</li>\n<li>Lactic acidosis</li>\n<li>Hypertriglyceridemia</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A definitive diagnosis is made using a liver biopsy. Fructose and galactose restriction in the diet is advised.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications </strong>of von Gierke's disease includes polycystic ovaries, osteopenia, hepatic adenoma, pulmonary hypertension, pancreatitis and renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5236","text":"An adolescent male patient​ presented with pain in t​he calf muscles on exerci​se. On biopsy, an excessi‌ve amount of glycogen was​ found to be present in t​he muscle. What is the mo‌st likely enzyme deficien‍cy?","raw_text":"An adolescent male patient presented with pain in the calf muscles on exercise. On biopsy, an excessive amount of glycogen was found to be present in the muscle. What is the most likely enzyme deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle-debra​nching enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphofru​ctokinase 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose 6 ​phosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle glycogen phosphoryl​ase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86d16fb179ed440db5df3a0963d550bcx1280x948.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8296f8ac047546f2b52c591a7d639613x1280x841.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Muscle glycogen phosphorylase</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are seen in <strong>McArdle's disease.</strong> This patient has <strong>muscle glycogen phosphorylase</strong> <strong>deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>The lack of muscle glycogen phosphorylase results in <strong>excess glycogen</strong> build-up in muscles. The image given below shows the vacuolar myopathy seen in McArdle's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>poor exercise</strong> tolerance, abnormally <strong>high muscle glycogen</strong> (2.5-4%), and a very <strong>low blood lactate</strong> after exercise</p>\n<p>The images given below show myophosphorylase enzyme histochemistry showing (A) Normal muscle and (B) McArdle's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3-SrH3yi","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"3_concepts_of_disease_and_prevention","title":"3.Concepts Of Disease And Prevention","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA3990","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the ep‌idemiological triad for t‌uberculosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the epidemiological triad for tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A malnouri​shed child.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duration ​of illness","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Overcr​owding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duration of illness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c184eed7c7ff471da903ee2938b8f033x1280x1204.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c08c563b712a4996a4ed1bd7c8ef104ax1280x1204.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duration of illness</strong></p><p>Duration of illness is not a part of the epidemiological triad for tuberculosis. </p>\n<p>The epidemiological triad includes <strong>host, agent </strong>and<strong> environment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Host factors: Age, sex, religion, race, genetic profile, immune system</li>\n<li>Agent factors: \n<ul>\n<li>Biological - bacteria, viruses</li>\n<li>Chemical - poisons, alcohol, smoke </li>\n<li>Physical - trauma, fire, radiation</li>\n<li>Nutrition - lack/excess</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Environmental factors:\n<ul>\n<li>Temperature, humidity, altitude</li>\n<li>Water, milk, food</li>\n<li>Radiation, pollution, noise</li>\n<li>Crowding, housing, neighborhood</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hence, a malnourished child (host), Mycobacterium tuberculosis (agent) and overcrowding (environment) form the epidemiological triad for tuberculosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4026","text":"Which of the following is ​the most logical sequence‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most logical sequence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disease → Impairment → Dis​ability → Handicap","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Impairment → Disease → Han​dicap → Disability","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disability → Disease →Impa​irment →Handicap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disease→ Disability→ Impai​rment →Handicap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Disease → Impairment → Disability → Handicap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d37d6e2ed3fe4a9ea79c3defef63c7bex1280x1576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Disease → Impairment → Disability → Handicap</strong></p><p><strong>Disease → Impairment → Disability → Handicap</strong> is the most logical sequence among the given options.</p>\n<p>Disease is a state of physiological/psychological <strong>dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Impairment is any <strong>loss</strong> or abnormality in normal<strong> structure</strong> or <strong>function</strong>.</p>\n<p>Disability is the<strong> inability </strong>to carry out <strong>normal actions</strong> due to impairment.</p>\n<p>Handicap is the disadvantage due to disability that <strong>limits fulfilment of a role</strong> that is normal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9740","text":"A salesman met with an unf​ortunate accident that le‌d to him eventually losin‌g his left foot. He has b​een unable to walk after ‍this incident and so been‍ rendered unemployed. Mat​ch the following examples​ with the corresponding e‌vents explaining the conc​ept of disability. Event ​Example 1.Disability A. L‍oss of foot 2. Disease/Di‌sorder B. Cannot Walk 3. ‌Handicap C. Accident 4. I​mpairment D. Unemployed","raw_text":"<p>A salesman met with an unfortunate accident that led to him eventually losing his left foot. He has been unable to walk after this incident and so been rendered unemployed. Match the following examples with the corresponding events explaining the concept of disability.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Event</td>\n<td>Example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1.Disability</td>\n<td>A. Loss of foot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Disease/Disorder</td>\n<td>B. Cannot Walk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Handicap</td>\n<td>C. Accident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Impairment</td>\n<td>D. Unemployed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-B, 2-C,​ 3-D, 4-A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-D, 2-A,​ 3-B, 4-C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-A, 2-B,​ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-C, 2-D,​ 3-A, 4-B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A</strong></p><p>The correctly matched events relating to the given example are as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Event</td>\n<td>Example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disease/Disorder</td>\n<td>Accident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impairment</td>\n<td>Loss of foot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disability</td>\n<td>Cannot Walk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handicap</td>\n<td>Unemployed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The WHO definition of <strong>impairment</strong> is \"any <strong>loss</strong> <strong>or abnormality</strong> <strong>of</strong> psychological, physiological or anatomical <strong>structure or function</strong>\". E.g., loss of a foot. An initial impairment may cause a secondary impairment as well.</p>\n<p>The WHO defines <strong>disability</strong> as ''any<strong> restriction</strong> or lack of ability to perform an <strong>activity</strong> in the manner or within the range considered normal for a human being\". Because of impairment, the affected person may be unable to carry out certain activities and hence the person is considered disabled.</p>\n<p>According to the WHO, a <strong>handicap</strong> is \"a <strong>disadvantage</strong> for a given individual, resulting from an impairment or a disability, that <strong>limits</strong> or prevents the <strong>fulfilment of a role</strong> that is normal (depending on age, sex, and social and cultural factors) for that individual\". The disadvantages experienced by a person as a result of disability makes them handicapped.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3994","text":"Which of the following is ​most appropriate about th‌e web of disease causatio‌n?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most appropriate about the web of disease causation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It implies that individual​ factors in the web have ‌equal weightage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It implies that all the ch​ains of causation must be‌ controlled to prevent di‌sease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is mostly applicable fo​r acute and rare diseases‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It suggests ways of interr​upting the disease occurr‌ence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It suggests ways of interrupting the disease occurrence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It suggests ways of interrupting the disease occurrence</strong></p><p>The <strong>web of causation</strong> for a disease suggests a number of <strong>ways to interrupt</strong> the <strong>disease</strong> occurrence/progression.</p>\n<p>This model was suggested by <strong>MacMahon and Pugh</strong> in their book ‘Epidemiologic Principles and Methods’.</p>\n<p><strong>Web of causation</strong> considers all the predisposing factors and their complex interrelationship with each other. This model is ideally <strong>suited</strong> in the study of <strong>chronic disease</strong>. The web of causation does not imply that the disease is controlled only when all the multiple chains of causation are removed. Sometimes <strong>removal</strong> of <strong>one significant link</strong> is sufficient to <strong>control</strong> the <strong>disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a multifactorial event, <strong>individual factors</strong> are by <strong>no</strong> means of <strong>equal weightage</strong>. The importance of each factor is <strong>expressed</strong> in terms of <strong>relative risk</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4001","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding the pa‌thogenesis period of a di‌sease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the pathogenesis period of a disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary prevention is poss​ible","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Recovery is seen in this p​eriod","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Entry of causative agent i​s seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Includes subclinical cases​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary prevention is possible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary prevention is possible</strong></p><p><strong>Primary prevention</strong> is not possible in the pathogenesis period of a disease. It can only be done in the<strong> period of pre-pathogenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>two phases</strong> in the natural history of a disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Period of pre-pathogenesis</strong>: It is <strong>before</strong> the <strong>onset</strong> of disease in humans. The disease agent has not entered the host, but the factors favouring the disease already exist. Primary prevention<strong> </strong>(health promotion and specific protection) is applicable in this period.</li>\n<li><strong>Period of pathogenesis</strong>: It begins with the <strong>entry</strong> of the <strong>organism</strong> till <strong>recovery</strong>, <strong>death</strong> or <strong>disability</strong>. Secondary (halting of the disease process) and tertiary prevention (measures to limit disability) are applicable in this period. It isn't always characterised by the presence of disease symptoms as it also <strong>includes subclinical cases</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3998","text":"In the iceberg phenomenon ​of disease, which of the ‌following does not repres‌ent the submerged portion​ of the iceberg?","raw_text":"In the iceberg phenomenon of disease, which of the following does not represent the submerged portion of the iceberg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-sympto​matic cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Latent​ cases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diagnos​ed cases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Undiagno​sed cases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diagnosed cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1c4b2aefceb47019a99308b6f61f77dx1280x1252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diagnosed cases</strong></p><p>Epidemiologically, the <strong>iceberg phenomenon</strong> of disease, <strong>diagnosed cases</strong> are represented by the floating <strong>tip</strong> of the iceberg.</p>\n<p>The iceberg phenomenon of disease is related to the spectrum of disease. The <strong>water surface</strong> is the line of <strong>demarcation</strong> between the <strong>apparent and inapparent infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>submerged portion </strong>of the iceberg<strong> </strong>represents the hidden mass of diseases like <strong>asymptomatic, latent, </strong>and<strong> undiagnosed cases</strong>.<strong> </strong>Screening is done to identify these cases.</p>\n<p>The iceberg phenomenon of disease is depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4002","text":"Which of the following dis​eases shows the iceberg p‌henomenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases shows the iceberg phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tet​anus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rub​ella","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mea​sles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rab​ies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rubella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bc171ac344f4c69ae5e07529257efdcx1280x1252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rubella</strong></p><p><strong>Rubella</strong> shows the <strong>iceberg phenomenon</strong> of disease.</p>\n<p>The iceberg phenomenon of diseases is <strong>not shown</strong> by diseases such as <strong>tetanus</strong>, <strong>rabies</strong>, and <strong>measles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Iceberg phenomenon is shown by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polio</li>\n<li>Influenza</li>\n<li>Rubella</li>\n<li>Mumps</li>\n<li>Japanese Encephalitis</li>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Hepatitis A and B</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: If a disease has <strong>more</strong> number of<strong> subclinical infections</strong>, it shows the <strong>iceberg phenomenon</strong> of disease depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4004","text":"What is the term used to d​escribe the interruption ‌in the transmission of a ‌disease?","raw_text":"What is the term used to describe the interruption in the transmission of a disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elimi​nation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eradi​cation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Survei​llance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Con​trol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Elimination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Elimination</strong></p><p><strong>Interruption </strong>in the<strong> transmission</strong> of a disease is called <strong>elimination</strong>. It is a step towards eradication.</p>\n<p><span class=\"custom help\"><strong>Elimination </strong>is a <strong>reduction</strong> in the number of <strong>new cases</strong> of an infectious disease <strong>to zero</strong> (or a very low defined target rate) in a defined geographical area as a result of deliberate efforts. Continued measures to prevent re-establishment of transmission are required.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Eradication </strong>is a complete and <strong>permanent worldwide</strong> <strong>reduction</strong> to <strong>zero new cases</strong> of an infectious disease through deliberate efforts. No further control measures are required. </p>\n<p><strong>Surveillance </strong>is the <strong>continuous scrutiny </strong>of factors<strong> </strong>that determine the occurrence and distribution of disease and ill health.</p>\n<p><strong>Control</strong> aims at <strong>decreasing</strong> the <strong>incidence</strong>,<strong> duration</strong> and <strong>transmission</strong> of the disease. It also aims at decreasing the physical and psychological <strong>complications</strong> of a disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4005","text":"Among the following option​s, which aids the most in‌ disease elimination?","raw_text":"Among the following options, which aids the most in disease elimination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isol​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quara​ntine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herd i​mmunity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Complete ​treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Herd immunity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Herd immunity</strong></p><p><strong>Herd immunity</strong> aids the most in <strong>disease elimination</strong>.</p>\n<p>Herd immunity achieved, especially through immunization, goes a long way in helping the elimination of diseases. When a <strong>high </strong>level of<strong> herd immunity</strong> is maintained, the <strong>susceptible population </strong>is<strong> reduced </strong>to a small proportion, thereby eliminating the disease.</p>\n<p>The purpose of <strong>isolation</strong> is to protect the community by <strong>preventing</strong> the <strong>transfer</strong> of <strong>infection</strong> from the reservoir to the possible susceptible hosts. <strong>Quarantine</strong> refers to<strong> restrictions</strong> on the<strong> healthy contacts</strong> of an infectious disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4006","text":"While collecting surveilla​nce data for disease X, y‌ou find that the incidenc‌e of the disease is zero ​in your region. Which of ‍the following statements ‍best describes this scena​rio?","raw_text":"While collecting surveillance data for disease X, you find that the incidence of the disease is zero in your region. Which of the following statements best describes this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disease is eliminated from​ the region.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Disease is well controlled​ in the region.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disease is eradicated from​ the region.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disease shows iceberg phen​omenon in the region.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Disease is eliminated from the region.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Disease is eliminated from the region.</strong></p><p><span class=\"custom help\">In the given scenario, as the disease has<strong> zero incidence</strong>, we can say the disease has been <strong>eliminated</strong> from the region.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"custom help\"><strong>Elimination </strong>is a <strong>reduction</strong> in the number of <strong>new cases</strong> of an infectious disease <strong>to</strong> <strong>zero</strong> (or a very low defined target rate) in a defined geographical area as a result of deliberate efforts. Continued measures to prevent re-establishment of transmission are required.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Eradication </strong>is a complete and <strong>permanent worldwide</strong> <strong>reduction</strong> to <strong>zero new cases</strong> of an infectious disease through deliberate efforts. No further control measures are required.</p>\n<p><strong>Control</strong> aims at <strong>decreasing</strong> the <strong>incidence</strong>,<strong> duration</strong> and <strong>transmission</strong> of the disease. It also aims at decreasing the physical and psychological <strong>complications</strong> of a disease.</p>\n<p>The <strong>iceberg phenomenon </strong>of disease is related to the spectrum of disease. The <strong>submerged portion </strong>of the iceberg<strong> </strong>represents the hidden mass of diseases like <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, <strong>latent</strong>, and<strong> undiagnosed cases</strong>.<strong> </strong>The <strong>clinical or diagnosed cases</strong> are represented by the <strong>floating tip</strong> of the iceberg and not the submerged portion. As disease incidence is zero here, there are no cases to form the iceberg.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4027","text":"Which of the following is ​a silent epidemic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a silent epidemic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bipolar ​disorders","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Manic depres​sive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alzheimer​’s disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Generalised anxiety disord​er","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alzheimer’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alzheimer’s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Alzheimer’s disease</strong> is referred to as the <strong>silent epidemic </strong>of the century.</p>\n<p>Nearly 50 million people globally are living with this disease. Since it strikes so often, so arbitrarily, and with such little warning, many have dubbed it the silent epidemic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4010","text":"What is the analysis of ro​utine measurements aimed ‌at detecting changes in t‌he health status of a pop​ulation called?","raw_text":"What is the analysis of routine measurements aimed at detecting changes in the health status of a population called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monit​oring","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Survei​llance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemat​ic review","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sentinel s​urveillance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Monitoring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Monitoring</strong></p><p>The <strong>analysis</strong> of routine measurements aimed at <strong>detecting changes</strong> in the <strong>health status</strong> or environment of a population is called <strong>monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Surveillance </strong>is the <strong>continuous scrutiny </strong>of factors that determine the occurrence and distribution of disease and ill health.</p>\n<p><strong>Sentinel surveillance</strong> is a method of <strong>identifying</strong> the <strong>missing cases</strong> and thereby supplementing the notified cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Systematic reviews</strong> involve a detailed plan with the goal of <strong>reducing bias</strong> by identifying, appraising, and synthesizing <strong>all relevant studies</strong> on a particular topic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4011","text":"In which of the following ​does surveillance of dise‌ase not have a direct rol‌e?","raw_text":"In which of the following does surveillance of disease not have a direct role?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Health ​planning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Control o​f disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Data on new and changing t​rends","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breaking chain of transmis​sion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breaking chain of transmission","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breaking chain of transmission</strong></p><p><strong>Surveillance</strong> of a disease does not have a direct role in breaking the chain of transmission.</p>\n<p>Surveillance is the <strong>continuous scrutiny </strong>of factors that <strong>determine</strong> the <strong>occurrence</strong> and <strong>distribution</strong> of disease and ill health.</p>\n<p>It <strong>helps</strong> in <strong>health planning</strong> and provides <strong>information</strong> about public health disasters including data on <strong>new</strong> and <strong>changing trends</strong> of disease.</p>\n<p>Surveillance requires professional analysis and sophisticated judgment of data, leading to <strong>recommendations</strong> for <strong>control activities</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4014","text":"Measures in sentinel surve​illance do not aim at ide‌ntifying:","raw_text":"Measures in sentinel surveillance do not aim at identifying:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Missing cases of the disea​se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Old cases of​ the disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"New cases of​ the disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cases free o​f disability","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cases free of disability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cases free of disability</strong></p><p><strong>Sentinel surveillance</strong> does not include identifying cases free of disability.</p>\n<p>Sentinel surveillance helps in <strong>identifying</strong> the <strong>missing </strong>cases, in addition to the <strong>already existing</strong> and <strong>new cases</strong>. It is used for supplementing routine notifications.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4017","text":"Which of the following is ​public health surveillanc‌e preferentially aimed at‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is public health surveillance preferentially aimed at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment ​of disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disease e​radication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disease m​onitoring","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Health ​planning","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Health planning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Health planning</strong></p><p>Public health surveillance is <strong>preferentially</strong> aimed at <strong>health planning</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Public health surveillance</strong> provides important <strong>feedback</strong> on the <strong>health status</strong> of the population, about planning <strong>control and prevention activities</strong>, and also for <strong>public health disasters</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4031","text":"Which of the following is ​included under primordial‌ prevention of coronary a‌rtery disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is included under primordial prevention of coronary artery disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lifestyle change in adults​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coronar​y bypass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regular BP​ estimation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lifestyle changes in child​hood","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lifestyle changes in childhood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lifestyle changes in childhood</strong></p><p>Adoption of a <strong>healthy lifestyle</strong> from <strong>childhood</strong> would fall under the category of <strong>primordial prevention </strong>of coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p>Primordial prevention is <strong>preventing</strong> the <strong>emergence of risk factors</strong> among population groups in which these risk factors have not yet appeared. Most adult health problems (obesity, hypertension) have their early origins in childhood. It is in childhood that lifestyles are formed i.e. eating patterns, physical exercise, smoking, etc.</p>\n<p>In primordial prevention, efforts are made to discourage <strong>children</strong> from adopting harmful lifestyles.</p>\n<p>These lifestyle changes when made by <strong>adults </strong>are known as<strong> primary</strong> <strong>prevention</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4022","text":"What type of prevention is​ the prescriptive screeni‌ng of a disease?","raw_text":"What type of prevention is the prescriptive screening of a disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primo​rdial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pri​mary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seco​ndary","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tert​iary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secondary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secondary</strong></p><p><strong>Prescriptive screening</strong> of a disease is an example of <strong>secondary prevention</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prescriptive screening</strong> of disease can lead to <strong>early diagnosis</strong> of disease. By early diagnosis, intervention can be made to prevent disability. Hence, it comes under secondary prevention.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5815","text":"What form of prevention is​ prospective screening?","raw_text":"What form of prevention is prospective screening?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pri​mary","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seco​ndary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tert​iary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primo​rdial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary</strong></p><p><strong>Prospective screening</strong> is a type of<strong> primary prevention</strong>.</p>\n<p>Prospective screening is done on <strong>healthy individuals</strong> to screen for disease. It is done <strong>prior</strong> to the<strong> onset</strong> of the disease. E.g., screening donated blood for HIV before it is transfused. This type of prevention reduces the incidence of disease.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Prescriptive screening</strong> is done for <strong>case detection. </strong>People are screened for their own benefits. It is an <strong>early diagnosis</strong> of diseases existing in them. This is a form of<strong> secondary prevention</strong>. E.g., neonatal screening for hypothyroidism, pap smear for detection of cervical cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4019","text":"You advise a reproductive ​age woman to vaccinate ag‌ainst HPV. What is this t‌ype of prevention called?​","raw_text":"You advise a reproductive age woman to vaccinate against HPV. What is this type of prevention called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial​ prevention","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary p​revention","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary ​prevention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiary ​prevention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary prevention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary prevention</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>immunisation</strong> is an example of <strong>primary prevention</strong>.</p>\n<p>Primary prevention is an action taken<strong> before</strong> the <strong>onset</strong> of disease. This reduces the possibility of disease occurrence. Hence, <strong>immunization</strong> is a form of primary prevention, through <strong>specific protection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples of primary prevention include immunization, sanitation, solid and liquid waste management etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4021","text":"Who among the following is​ not a target for primary‌ prevention?","raw_text":"Who among the following is not a target for primary prevention?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apparently healthy individ​uals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Individuals with risk fact​or","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Children les​s than 5years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asymptomat​ic patients","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Asymptomatic patients","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Asymptomatic patients</strong></p><p><strong>Asymptomatic patients</strong> are the target population for <strong>secondary prevention</strong> but not for primary prevention.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> prevention is implemented <strong>before</strong> the <strong>onset</strong> of the disease. Hence, all patients including asymptomatic ones are not included in the target group.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4024","text":"As a part of the iodine de​ficiency control programm‌e, you advocate the usage‌ of iodized salt in hilly​ areas. What is this type‍ of prevention an example‍ of?","raw_text":"As a part of the iodine deficiency control programme, you advocate the usage of iodized salt in hilly areas. What is this type of prevention an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial​ prevention","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary p​revention","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary ​prevention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiary ​prevention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary prevention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary prevention</strong></p><p>The usage of<strong> iodized salt</strong> in<strong> iodine-deficient areas </strong>like in hilly regions is an example of <strong>primary prevention </strong>as it provides specific protection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4029","text":"You are performing a routi​ne pap smear on a 30-year‌-old woman in the gynecol‌ogy OPD. What level of pr​evention does this fall u‍nder?","raw_text":"You are performing a routine pap smear on a 30-year-old woman in the gynecology OPD. What level of prevention does this fall under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primo​rdial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seco​ndary","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pri​mary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tert​iary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary</strong></p><p><strong>Pap smear</strong> is a screening test for carcinoma cervix and is an example of <strong>secondary prevention.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Early diagnosis </strong>(including screening) and treatment are the components of secondary prevention. Some of the examples of secondary prevention are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papanicolaou (Pap) smear for early detection of cervical cancer</li>\n<li>Mammography for early detection of breast cancer</li>\n<li>Colonoscopies for early detection of colon cancer</li>\n<li>Blood Pressure Screening</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Secondary prevention measures focus on the <strong>sub-clinical </strong>stag<span style=\"color: #000000;\">e and the <strong>early clinical</strong> stage, such as dysplasia in this case. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">T</span>hese measures enable early detection and prompt effective intervention to correct departures from a state of health.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4025","text":"What mode of prevention is​ disability prevention by‌ appropriate treatment an‌ example of?","raw_text":"What mode of prevention is disability prevention by appropriate treatment an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primordial​ prevention","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary p​revention","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary ​prevention","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiary ​prevention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secondary prevention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secondary prevention</strong></p><p><strong>Disability prevention</strong> by appropriate treatment is an example of <strong>secondary prevention</strong>. This is because disability has not occurred yet so correct treatment can stop it from developing.</p>\n<p>After the disability has set in, <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>progression</strong> <strong>of disability</strong> from transitioning into a handicap is <strong>tertiary prevention</strong>. It is also called <strong>disability limitation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4033","text":"Which of the following is ​an example of disability ‌limitation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of disability limitation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prescribing spectacles for​ refractive error","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Starting a patient on anti​-tuberculous therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abstinence ​from smoking","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Early fixation of fracture​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prescribing spectacles for refractive error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prescribing spectacles for refractive error</strong></p><p><strong>Prescribing spectacles</strong> for refractive error is an example of <strong>disability limitation </strong>which is a component of <strong>tertiary prevention</strong>. This mode of intervention is applicable when the patient presents late in the pathogenesis phase. </p>\n<p>The objective of this intervention is to<strong> prevent</strong> or halt the transition of the disease process from <strong>impairment</strong> <strong>to</strong> <strong>handicap</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Starting a patient on <strong>anti-tuberculous therapy</strong> is <strong>secondary </strong>prevention as it is a part of treatment.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Abstinence</strong> from <strong>smoking</strong> is a modification of the risk factor. Hence it is an example of <strong>primary </strong>prevention.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Early fixation</strong> of <strong>fractures</strong> will prevent disability. Hence, it is an example of <strong>secondary</strong> prevention.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4028","text":"Which among the following ​diseases was eliminated i‌n India in 2015?","raw_text":"Which among the following diseases was eliminated in India in 2015?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lep​rosy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neonatal​ tetanus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow​ fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neonatal tetanus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neonatal tetanus</strong></p><p><strong>Maternal</strong> and<strong> neonatal tetanus </strong>was <strong>eliminated</strong> from India in <strong>2015</strong>, as certified by the WHO.</p>\n<p>Important years for <strong>India</strong> (declared <strong>disease-free</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infective trachoma - 2017</li>\n<li>Yaws - 2016</li>\n<li>Maternal &amp; neonatal tetanus - 2015</li>\n<li>Polio - 2014</li>\n<li>Guinea worm disease (dracunculiasis) - 2000</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Worldwide eradication</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type-2 wild poliovirus - 2015</li>\n<li>Type-3 wild poliovirus- 2019</li>\n<li>Smallpox - 1980</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5816","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about IC‌D- 11?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about ICD- 11?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It includes a new chapter ​on traditional medicine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gender incongruence is now​ listed as part of mental‌ health","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The codes relating to anti​microbial resistance are ‌more closely in line with‌ the Global Antimicrobial​ Resistance Surveillance ‍System","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has come into full effe​ct from January 2022","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gender incongruence is now listed as part of mental health","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gender incongruence is now listed as part of mental health</strong></p><p>The new ICD-11 has a new chapter on <strong>sexual health </strong>that contains<strong> gender incongruence</strong>, which was previously listed under mental health conditions in the ICD-10.</p>\n<p>The ICD 11 was released by the WHO in June 2018 and was presented at the World Health Assembly in May 2019 for adoption by the Member States. It has come into<strong> effect</strong> from <strong>1 January 2022.</strong></p>\n<p>Its designations for <strong>antibiotic resistance align</strong> with the Global Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance System (<strong>GLASS</strong>). It is given by the <strong>WHO </strong>and is <strong>revised</strong> every <strong>10 years.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>New chapters</strong> added in ICD-11 are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Traditional medicine</strong></li>\n<li>Sexual health: Gender incongruence has been moved from mental health to sexual health.</li>\n<li>Gaming disorder has been added under Addictive disorders.</li>\n<li>Immune system disorders, which will enable the better study of allergies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It has more detail on cancer, specifically different types of skin cancer, as well as on heart health and weight disorders compared to ICD-10. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5Ad2njze","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"3_morphology_and_investigations_of_skin_lesions","title":"3.Morphology And Investigations Of Skin Lesions","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA4071","text":"Which of the following is ​a primary skin lesion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a primary skin lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cr​ust","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atr​ophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pur​pura","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Indur​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Purpura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf24ed8054c444a18cdfa3b3a80e49b0x1280x1191.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3a3d7bec719414489f0b278c515c461x1280x1381.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Purpura</strong></p><p><strong>Purpura</strong> is a <strong>primary</strong> skin lesion. Crust, atrophy, and induration are secondary lesions.</p>\n<p>It is a condition with <strong>red</strong> or <strong>purple </strong>discolored <strong>spots</strong> on the skin that <strong>do not blanch</strong> on applying pressure. The spots are caused by <strong>bleeding underneath</strong> the <strong>skin.</strong> It is seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vasculitis</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Vitamin C deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Crusts or scabs consist of dried-up discharge from the lesions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Atrophy refers to loss of tissue. There may be fine wrinkling and increased translucency if the process is superficial.</p>\n<p>Option D: Induration refers to dermal thickening that clinically presents as skin that feels thicker and firmer than normal on palpation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4091","text":"Which of the following is ​a secondary skin lesion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a secondary skin lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima​ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima​ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima​ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ima​ge 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Image 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5bc8b2c695e4a2593ea2623688b8d85.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95f2849e77d7460e9598762ea21d3d6ex1280x3712.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d01115f8e02a40f6bd5bab9da5c9d5dax1280x2466.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Image 4</strong></p><p>Image 4 shows an <strong>ulcer</strong> with<strong> crusting. </strong>This<strong> </strong>is a <strong>secondary</strong><strong> </strong>skin lesion. </p>\n<p>Images 1, 2, and 3 show a bulla, a papule, and a patch respectively. All of these are primary skin lesions. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4058","text":"Which of the following can​not be considered as a pr​imary lesion of skin? Whe‌al Macule Excoriation Pus​tule Crust","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following cannot be considered as a primary lesion of skin?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Wheal</li>\n<li>Macule </li>\n<li>Excoriation </li>\n<li>Pustule </li>\n<li>Crust</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cab723a0663847f4a116f1ab873e979fx1280x6447.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Excoriation</strong> and <strong>crusts</strong> are <strong>secondary </strong>skin lesions.</p>\n<p><strong>Wheal, macule</strong> and <strong>pustule</strong> are primary skin lesions. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 158px; width: 546px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 262.016px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary lesions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.984px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Secondary lesions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 262.016px; height: 50px;\">Basic lesions that appear on the skin.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.984px; height: 50px;\">Appear later in the disease due to scratching or infection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 155px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 262.016px; height: 155px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Macule</li>\n<li>Papule</li>\n<li>Pustule</li>\n<li>Patch</li>\n<li>Plaque</li>\n<li>Nodule</li>\n<li>Vesicle</li>\n<li>Bulla</li>\n<li>Wheal</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.984px; vertical-align: top; height: 155px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Lichenification</li>\n<li>Erosion</li>\n<li>Excoriation</li>\n<li>Ulcer</li>\n<li>Crust</li>\n<li>Scar</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6957","text":"A patient presents to the ​dermatology OPD with the ​following finding. How wo‌uld you describe the morp​hology of this lesion?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the dermatology OPD with the following finding. How would you describe the morphology of this lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pla​que","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Licheni​fication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sc​ale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cr​ust","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lichenification","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fa469aecc5834a26a6725b4813795351.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lichenification</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>lichenification</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>secondary </strong>skin lesion characterized by <strong>hyperpigmentation, thickening </strong>of the skin, and <strong>exaggerated skin markings.</strong> It is a feature of chronic eczema.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4063","text":"A patient presents with fl​at, circumscribed, non-pa​lpable skin lesions that ‌are less than 1 cm in dia​meter. Which of the follo​wing terms best describes‌ them?","raw_text":"A patient presents with flat, circumscribed, non-palpable skin lesions that are less than 1 cm in diameter. Which of the following terms best describes them?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mac​ule","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pa​tch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ecchy​mosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pap​ule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Macule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/afb81c2bceb64407b20d56af9e7f3b27x1280x1076.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Macule</strong></p><p>A <strong>macule</strong> is a  <strong>flat, circumscribed</strong>, <strong>non‐palpable lesion</strong> of less than 1 cm that differs in color from the surrounding skin. It can be of any color or shape.</p>\n<p>Option B: A patch is a lesion of similar description but of size &gt;1 cm.</p>\n<p>Option C: An ecchymosis is macular area of hemorrhage &gt; 1 cm</p>\n<p>Option D: A papule is a raised lesion &lt; 1 cm. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4064","text":"A dermatology resident ide​ntified several flake-lik​e lesions while examining‌ a patient. Skin biopsy i​s shown below. What are t​hese lesions?","raw_text":"A dermatology resident identified several flake-like lesions while examining a patient. Skin biopsy is shown below. What are these lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sc​ar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cr​ust","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sc​ale","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Excor​iation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scale","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a0926aa00074ccab2aa2ff5f668cf34.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a512ef68649944bd96fb034d42e52ad6x1280x1499.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d06fe3458e542c9911dea709948c2b4x510x482.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scale</strong></p><p>The given biopsy shows <strong>parakeratosis</strong>. It is associated with visible<strong> scales</strong> or <strong>visible exfoliation</strong> of the skin that consists of flakes of <strong>stratum corneum. </strong></p>\n<p>Parakeratosis refers to the presence of <strong>nucleated keratinocytes</strong> in the stratum corneum. It is indicative of a <strong>high turnover</strong> of keratinocytes.<strong> </strong>It is associated with thickening of the stratum corneum and visibly presents as scaling. The <strong>scales</strong> represent compacted <strong>desquamated</strong> layers of stratum corneum.</p>\n<p>The following image shows visible fragments of the stratum corneum as it is shed from the skin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4072","text":"A patient presents with th​e following rash. What is​ the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following rash. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seborrheic​ dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityrias​is rosea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ichthyosi​s vulgaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pityriasis rosea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4691acd83e2d4f5098c220a4892699d8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b569b84d4a48476cb59a2ad785836207x1280x916.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pityriasis rosea</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> collarette</strong> scales, which are a characteristic feature of <strong>pityriasis rosea</strong>.</p>\n<p>Collarette refers to a scale with an adherent outer border and detachable inner border. It appears like a rim around the lesion as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4076","text":"A 50-year-old man with a c​hronic skin condition is ​noted to have areas of li‌near discontinuation over​ his skin. What is this f​inding called?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with a chronic skin condition is noted to have areas of linear discontinuation over his skin. What is this finding called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ul​cer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excor​iation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ero​sion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fis​sure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fissure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa8ce19782724cc784f6a737b30907a3x1280x1142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fissure</strong></p><p>A <strong>linear</strong> <strong>crack </strong>or<strong> discontinuation</strong> of the skin surface is called a <strong>fissure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Ulcer refers to a loss of dermis and epidermis, often involving underlying tissue as well.</p>\n<p>Option B: Excoriation refers to loss of skin substance, specifically produced by scratching.</p>\n<p>Option C: Erosion refers to a loss of the epidermis, which heals without scarring. It commonly follows a blister.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4087","text":"A 39-year-old woman is bei​ng evaluated for pale-col​ored stools. Further work‌up reveals elevated direc​t bilirubin, elevated ALP​, and GGT. Which of the f‌ollowing skin lesions wou‌ld you expect to see?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old woman is being evaluated for pale-colored stools. Further workup reveals elevated direct bilirubin, elevated ALP, and GGT. Which of the following skin lesions would you expect to see?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ero​sion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mac​ule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fis​sure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Excor​iation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Excoriation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d24a7c10dca4ad08837a98a29e67769x1280x851.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Excoriation</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>obstructive jaundice,</strong> which is associated with severe <strong>itching</strong>.<strong> </strong>Hence, you would expect to see <strong>excoriations</strong> or scratch marks.</p>\n<p>The pruritis occurring in obstructive jaundice is due to <strong>cholestasis.</strong> The <strong>bile salts</strong> deposit in the skin, causing <strong>saponification</strong> of the <strong>fat</strong> surrounding the nerve endings. This leaves the <strong>nerves exposed,</strong> producing the sensation of a <strong>severe itch.</strong></p>\n<p>It involves loss of skin substance till the level of the <strong>epidermis</strong> or <strong>upper</strong> <strong>dermis</strong>. Almost all <strong>pruritic</strong> conditions (except lichen planus) cause excoriation.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows excoriations.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0760","text":"A 26-year-old man presents​ with the following skin ​lesion. How will you desc‌ribe it?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man presents with the following skin lesion. How will you describe it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ann​ular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Numm​ular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arc​uate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyc​yclic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nummular","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ca642b1636549499a3c6d3290d755c9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b3de936372d4d75b502d9c04ca8919ax1280x1033.5684062059238.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5138d8f39634ed1996785694f064a34x114x128.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80a918aad8334babaa03dabc55d78f99x319x430.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nummular</strong></p><p>The given image showing a <strong>round</strong> lesion <strong>without central clearing</strong> is called a <strong>nummular</strong> (coin-like) or a <strong>discoid </strong>lesion. </p>\n<p>Both, the <strong>edge </strong>and<strong> center</strong> are equally active and <strong>uniformly colored</strong>. It is seen in discoid eczema, psoriasis.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Annular</strong> lesions are <strong>ring-shaped</strong> with <strong>central clearing</strong> i.e., only the edges are active and appear raised, or in a different color. It is seen in tinea corporis, granuloma annulare. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Arcuate</strong> lesions are arc-shaped or <strong>incomplete circles.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Polycyclic</strong> lesions are several <strong>circles</strong> that have <strong>merged</strong> together.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4093","text":"An 11-month-old infant is ​brought with the given fi​nding for the past 1 mont‌h. The mother is concerne​d that the lesions are it​chy as the baby has been ‌fussy and not sleeping we‌ll. What is the diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"An 11-month-old infant is brought with the given finding for the past 1 month. The mother is concerned that the lesions are itchy as the baby has been fussy and not sleeping well.  What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked ​ichthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seborrheic​ dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atopic d​ermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ichthyosi​s vulgaris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ichthyosis vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2dfd2d61eb70493fba6361830223d545.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ichthyosis vulgaris</strong></p><p>This image shows <strong>fish-like scales</strong> on the skin that is characteristic of <strong>ichthyosis</strong> <strong>vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition that is associated with <strong>filaggrin</strong> mutations. This leads to a reduction in the <strong>natural moisturizing factors</strong> (NMF) in the skin. It develops at<strong> 3-12 months</strong> of life and presents with generalized, itchy, <strong>small</strong>, <strong>light-grey</strong> scales and xerosis that are more marked over <strong>extensor</strong> surfaces and <strong>trunk</strong>. The groin and flexures are spared. </p>\n<p>X-linked recessive ichthyosis occurs only in males. It results in larger, dirty-brown scales.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4079","text":"A circumscribed elevation,​ greater than 1 cm in dia​meter containing clear fl‌uid is known as a:","raw_text":"A circumscribed elevation, greater than 1 cm in diameter containing clear fluid is known as a:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pus​tule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ves​icle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wh​eal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bu​lla","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bulla</strong></p><p>A<strong> bulla</strong> is a <strong>circumscribed elevation </strong>of <strong>&gt; 1 cm</strong> in diameter.</p>\n<p>Bullae can be <strong>multilocular</strong> due to coalesced vesicles, as seen in eczema, or <strong>unilocular</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0753","text":"A 7-year-old girl develops​ the following lesions af​ter a camping trip. They ‌are mildly itchy. In whic​h condition are they char​acteristically seen?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl develops the following lesions after a camping trip. They are mildly itchy. In which condition are they characteristically seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinea c​orporis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema ​multiforme","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contact d​ermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urti​caria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythema multiforme","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abcdb147217d4e5488dfb5f3406289ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythema multiforme</strong></p><p>This image shows typical<strong> target lesions</strong>, characteristically seen in <strong>erythema multiforme</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also called an iris or<strong> bull's eye</strong> lesion and typically consists of <strong>3 zones </strong>as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Central area of <strong>dusky erythema</strong> or purpura</li>\n<li>Middle <strong>paler</strong> zone of edema</li>\n<li>Outer ring of <strong>erythema</strong> with a well-defined edge</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Atypical </strong>target lesions with only <strong>2 zones</strong> may be seen in Stevens-Johnson syndrome (<strong>SJS</strong>) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (<strong>TEN</strong>). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6958","text":"Which of the following dis​eases has an intertrigino​us distribution?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases has an intertriginous distribution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes​ zoster","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hailey-Hai​ley Disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incontinent​ia pigmenti","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sporotr​ichosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hailey-Hailey Disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hailey-Hailey Disease</strong></p><p><strong>Hailey–Hailey disease</strong> has an <strong>intertriginous</strong> distribution.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 657px; height: 540px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Distribution pattern</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Dermatomal / zosteriform </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Unilateral and in the distribution of a single spinal afferent nerve root</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Herpes zoster  </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Blaschkoid</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Following lines of skin cell migration during embryogenesis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Incontinentia pigmenti</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Lymphangitic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Distribution of a lymph vessel </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cellulitis</li>\n<li>Sporotrichosis</li>\n<li>Swimming pool granuloma </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Sun-exposed </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Areas not covered by clothing (face, dorsal hands, V area of neck)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Photodermatitis</li>\n<li>Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Sun-protected </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Areas covered by clothing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Parapsoriasis</li>\n<li>Mycosis fungoides</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Acral</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Distal locations (hands, feet, wrists, and ankles) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Palmoplantar pustulosis</li>\n<li>Chilblains</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Extensor  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Dorsal extremities, overlying the extensor muscles, knees, or elbows </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Dermatitis herpetiformis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Flexor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Flexor muscles of the extremities, antecubital and popliteal fossae</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Atopic dermatitis (childhood and adult phase) </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.562px;\"><strong>Intertriginous </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.094px;\">Skin folds, where two skin surfaces are in contact (axilla, inguinal folds, inner thighs, inframammary)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.344px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Candidiasis</li>\n<li><strong> Hailey-Hailey disease</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4086","text":"A 30-year-old professional​ mountain climber present​s with the following lesi‌ons over his leg. What is​ the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old professional mountain climber presents with the following lesions over his leg. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sca​bies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cutaneous l​arva migrans","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Allergic contact dermatiti​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinea c​orporis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cutaneous larva migrans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfc07505627f4c16b5ecbb9069da53b8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79f065c8a3754f0787333ca4121c9a34x682x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cutaneous larva migrans</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> serpiginous </strong>or serpent-like skin lesions, which are characteristically seen in <strong>cutaneous larva migrans </strong>or ground itch.</p>\n<p>It is caused by the <strong>larvae</strong> of various hookworm or<strong> <em>Ancylostoma</em></strong> species. The parasite is acquired from <strong>direct skin contact</strong> with soil <strong>contaminated</strong> by dog or cat <strong>feces</strong>. It is<strong> </strong>common following travel to endemic or tropical areas and<strong> </strong>symptoms develop within the first<strong> 2 weeks. </strong></p>\n<p>The site of entry is marked by an inflamed papule, often located between the toes. Serpentine or linear single-track lines later appear as the larvae migrate through the epidermis. The lesions can enlarge as much as 1 to 2 cm per day.</p>\n<p>The first-line treatment is oral <strong>ivermectin</strong> at 200 μg/kg. Oral<strong> albendazole </strong>is an alternative treatment.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Larva currens </strong>is caused by the migrating larva of <strong><em>Strongyloides stercoralis</em></strong>. It also produces <strong>serpiginous</strong> lesions and can be differentiated by the<strong> faster rate</strong> of progression and haphazard distribution of lesions.</p>\n<p>The image below shows serpiginous larval tracks over the skin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4092","text":"A 2-month-old infant is br​ought to the pediatrician​ with the following findi‌ng. His mother says that ​he is otherwise healthy. ​What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old infant is brought to the pediatrician with the following finding. His mother says that he is otherwise healthy. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impe​tigo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seborrheic​ dermatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atopic d​ermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinea ​capitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Seborrheic dermatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cbe966cb9d9d4ece80ea1a72055e9e55.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d2d265633e6f45e68301ed54c2bbb419x720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25779c2607fb48d294dea179b1d5740ex701x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d76e0592e0849c3864ea9b5d849d88ex450x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c7f3b1da1f340eea288b3faa3d7d8e9x661x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Seborrheic dermatitis</strong></p><p>The <strong>yellowish greasy scales</strong> on the <strong>scalp</strong> of an infant are characteristic of <strong>seborrheic</strong> <strong>dermatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Seborrheic</strong> <strong>dermatitis</strong> is a common, inflammatory skin condition that causes the formation of flaky, white to yellowish greasy scales on <strong>oily areas</strong> such as the scalp, forehead, nose, or inside the ear. It is known as <strong>cradle cap</strong> when it affects the scalp of infants, as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Impetigo</strong> presents with <strong>shallow bullae. </strong>When ruptured, they result in erosions and discharge that forms <strong>honey-colored crusting.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Atopic dermatitis</strong> presents in infants as an <strong>itchy erythematous rash</strong> that affects the <strong>face</strong> and <strong>flexural</strong> areas such as elbows. The given image shows atopic dermatitis on the abdomen and thigh of an infant:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Tinea capitis</strong> is ringworm of the scalp. It is uncommon in infants. It is common in <strong>older children</strong> and presents as <strong>patches of alopecia</strong> associated with <strong>scaling </strong>or black dots (swollen hair shafts).</p>\n<p>The given image shows severe tinea capitis infection with follicles discharging pus, this clinical variant is known as<strong> kerion</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0761","text":"Which of the following wou​ld not be seen in a patie​nt with poikiloderma?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with poikiloderma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Varied pi​gmentation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of temperature sensat​ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skin a​trophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Telangi​ectasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loss of temperature sensation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loss of temperature sensation</strong></p><p>Loss of temperature sensation is not associated with poikiloderma.</p>\n<p><strong>Poikiloderma</strong> is a special skin lesion with a <strong>triad</strong> of features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cutaneous pigmentation (both hypopigmentation and hyperpigmentation)</li>\n<li>Skin atrophy</li>\n<li>Telangiectasia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It may be congenital or acquired. It is associated with mycosis fungoides, dermatomyositis, xeroderma pigmentosum, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4061","text":"You have ordered a patch t​est for an 8-year-old boy​ with a history of allerg‌ic contact dermatitis. Af​ter the application of th​e patch, when should you ‌ask him to return for rem‌oval?","raw_text":"You have ordered a patch test for an 8-year-old boy with a history of allergic contact dermatitis. After the application of the patch, when should you ask him to return for removal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"72 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"48 h​ours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"96 h​ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 48 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ebc687310f342eb903cfc11bb43f880x1024x768.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b4a7219538548db927a8f59da688b60x1280x936.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 48 hours</strong></p><p>In<strong> patch testing</strong>, the patch is removed after <strong>48</strong> <strong>hours</strong> to examine the site of reaction.</p>\n<p>Patch testing is a test to detect <strong>sensitivity</strong> to a specific <strong>allergen</strong>. A battery of suspected allergens is applied to the patient’s back under occlusive dressings or patches. It is allowed to remain in contact with the skin for 48 hours.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>48 hours: </strong> Patch removal for <strong>initial reading </strong>to detect <strong>delayed hypersensitivity</strong> reaction.</li>\n<li><strong>72-96 hours</strong>: Final reading done for confirmation. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Since the question is regarding the timing of patch removal and not regarding the final diagnosis, 48 hours is the answer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4089","text":"A dermatology resident per​forming Wood's lamp exami​nation on a patient notes‌ a milky-white fluorescen​ce. What is the diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A dermatology resident performing Wood's lamp examination on a patient notes a milky-white fluorescence. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityriasis​ versicolor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viti​ligo","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tinea ​capitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Albi​nism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitiligo","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f8865c18bfc4f29b9bc73504c72b40ex1280x1432.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitiligo</strong></p><p><strong>Ivory</strong> or <strong>milky-white fluorescence</strong> on Wood's lamp examination is seen in <strong>vitiligo</strong>.</p>\n<p>In vitiligo, there is less or<strong> no epidermal melanin</strong> in the affected patches. As the normal function of melanin is to absorb light, the vitiligo-affected patches cannot block the light emitted from deeper layers of the skin. They appear as <strong>bluish, milky</strong>, or<strong> ivory white</strong> patches with sharp margins. Woods lamp is hence helpful in the diagnosis of vitiligo, especially in fair-skinned people in whom lesions may not be obvious to the naked eye.</p>\n<p>The following image shows milky-white fluorescence under a Wood's lamp. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4088","text":"A 67-year-old diabetic com​es to you with a rash ove​r the intertriginous area‌s, which is more pronounc​ed over his groin. Wood's​ lamp examination shows c‌oral pink fluorescence. W‌hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old diabetic comes to you with a rash over the intertriginous areas, which is more pronounced over his groin.  Wood's lamp examination shows coral pink fluorescence. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityriasis​ versicolor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eryth​rasma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tinea ​cruris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candidal ​intertrigo","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythrasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythrasma</strong></p><p><strong>Coral pink fluorescence</strong> on Wood's lamp examination is characteristic of <strong>erythrasma.</strong></p>\n<p>Erythrasma is a skin infection caused by <strong><em>Corynebacterium minutissimum</em></strong>. It is common in <strong>diabetics</strong> and <strong>immunodeficient</strong> patients and is associated with <strong>obesity</strong>, <strong>sweating</strong> and <strong>poor hygiene</strong>.</p>\n<p>Coral pink fluorescence is due to the production of <strong>coproporphyrin III</strong> by the bacterium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6959","text":"Which of the following dis​eases cannot be diagnosed​ by Tzanck smear?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases cannot be diagnosed by Tzanck smear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hansen's​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcal scalded ski​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pemp​higus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hansen's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hansen&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Hansen's disease</strong> is diagnosed by <strong>slit skin smear</strong> for acid-fast bacilli using the <strong>modified Ziehl-Neelsen</strong> technique.</p>\n<p>Some important findings on Tzanck smear are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 379px; height: 224px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tzanck Smear findings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Acantholytic cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Pemphigus, Hailey-Hailey disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Degenerated necrosed keratinocytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Toxic epidermal necrolysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Dyskeratotic acantholytic cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, Bullous impetigo  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Multinucleated giant cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Herpes simplex virus 1 and 2, Herpes zoster</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Henderson-Patterson bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Molluscum contagiosum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Leishman-Donovan bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Leishmaniasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173.391px;\">Clusters of basaloid cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.609px;\">Basal cell carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>     </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0762","text":"What test is being perform​ed in the image given bel​ow?","raw_text":"What test is being performed in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scratc​h test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auspit​z sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dias​copy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patch​ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diascopy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe18b1e15c264fc1bd640e4cc09c30d2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fafa9d80ba9749fd9ba92db81d71413dx436x406.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diascopy</strong></p><p>The test depicted in the given image is <strong>diascopy</strong>.</p>\n<p>It involves <strong>pressing </strong>a<strong> glass slide</strong> or a stiff, clear, colorless piece of plastic onto the skin to cause <strong>blanching</strong> by compressing blood out of small vessels. This allows <strong>other colors </strong>to be evaluated.</p>\n<p>Some uses of diascopy are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Apple jelly nodules -  </strong>Yellow-brown lesions seen in <strong>lupus vulgaris </strong>and<strong> sarcoidosis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Spider naevi - </strong>Compression of radiating arterioles is noted. </li>\n<li>Differentiation of <strong>erythema</strong> (disappears) and <strong>purpura</strong> (remains unaltered).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows apple-jelly nodules on diascopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0820","text":"A pathologist is preparing​ paraffin sections of a s​kin biopsy sample. Which ‌fixative is he likely to ​use in this process?","raw_text":"A pathologist is preparing paraffin sections of a skin biopsy sample. Which fixative is he likely to use in this process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Formal sali​ne solution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.5% glut​araldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10% neutral buffered forma​lin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10% neutral buffered formalin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10% neutral buffered formalin</strong></p><p><strong>10% neutral buffered formalin</strong> is the most widely used fixative for routine diagnostic microscopy of <strong>paraffin-embedded tissue</strong> samples. </p>\n<p>Skin biopsies are indicated in various conditions like <strong>epidermal </strong>or<strong> dermal neoplasms</strong> as well as <strong>blistering disorders</strong> for immunofluorescence. <strong>Michel's medium</strong> is used as a <strong>transport</strong> medium.</p>\n<p>Various techniques of skin biopsy are available based on the lesions to be biopsied:<br/><br/></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 256px;\" width=\"383\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Technique</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Application</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px;\">Excision</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px;\">For removal of a single, small lesion. An elliptical or fusiform <br/>shaped area of skin is removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px;\">Incision biopsy(wedge)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px;\">A part of a large lesion is removed, with some normal peri-lesional skin<br/>for comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px;\">Punch biopsy (3-6 mm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px;\">Useful for accurate sampling of limited tissue </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px;\">Curettage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px;\">For hyperkeratotic lesions like seborrhoeic<br/>keratoses, viral warts, basal cell carcinomas. It is usually accompanied by cautery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px;\">Shave biopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px;\">For protuberant lesions on the face like benign intradermal naevi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.812px;\">Snip</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.188px;\">For skin tags </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0825","text":"Scrapings from a patient's​ skin lesion are mounted ​in KOH and observed under‌ Calcofluor white UV illu​mination. Hyphae and clus​ters of yeast are seen. W‌hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"Scrapings from a patient's skin lesion are mounted in KOH and observed under Calcofluor white UV illumination. Hyphae and clusters of yeast are seen. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Candi​diasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityriasis​ versicolor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bullous P​emphigoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pityriasis versicolor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1021b65e78d045ec95bfe0f068f75d84x958x644.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pityriasis versicolor</strong></p><p>The microscopic picture described in this clinical scenario is called <strong>spaghetti and meatball appearance. </strong>It is diagnostic of <strong>pityriasis versicolor</strong> or tinea versicolor.</p>\n<p>Skin scrapings from the lesions are mounted in<strong> 10-30% potassium hydroxide. </strong>A higher percentage of KOH solution speeds up the process. It is then observed under fluorescence microscopy using a fluorescent brightener like <strong>Calcofluor white </strong>or<strong> Blankophor </strong>which specifically stains polysaccharides in the fungal cell wall.</p>\n<p>The <strong>hyphae</strong> and <strong>spherical</strong> <em><strong>Malassezia</strong></em> yeasts are called spaghetti and meatballs or <strong>bananas and grapes</strong> appearance as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0853","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about th​e test shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the test shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used for photoallerg​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The covered side shows rea​ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reading is taken at 48 hou​rs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients back is positione​d 15 cm from the front pa​nel of lamps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The covered side shows reaction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c660822b53fb4529a03d21c3e4e342d6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The covered side shows reaction</strong></p><p>The given image shows a<strong> photo-patch test</strong>.<strong> </strong>Skin reaction is seen on the <strong>uncovered</strong> side that is <strong>exposed</strong> to <strong>UV rays</strong>.</p>\n<p>Photo-patch test is useful in the diagnosis of<strong> </strong>photosensitive rashes and<strong> photoallergic contact dermatitis </strong>that are typically localized to the <strong>sun-exposed areas </strong>of the skin.</p>\n<p>UV light is used at a dose sufficient to trigger the photoallergic response, without causing a false positive phototoxic reaction. It is performed like a conventional patch test, except two sets of allergens are applied over the back. The <strong>control</strong> set of allergens and the rest of the skin are <strong>covered</strong> with an opaque material. The patient is positioned <strong>15 cm</strong> from a UV lamp and the <strong>test</strong> site is <strong>irradiated</strong> with UV light.</p>\n<p>This test is best read after<strong> 48 hours</strong>. In the case of a positive test indicating photoallergy, the exposed side shows a reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0753","text":"Which of the following dru​gs used as nail lacquer b​elongs to morpholines?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs used as nail lacquer belongs to morpholines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoro​lfine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxico​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciclopiro​x olamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tioco​nazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amorolfine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/84de48097cc74578bef5057b6b0a7009x720x465.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amorolfine</strong></p><p><strong>Amorolfine </strong>is a <strong>morpholine </strong>that is available as a 5% <strong>nail lacquer</strong>. It is used in the treatment of <strong>onychomycosis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options B and D: Oxiconazole and tioconazole are classified as <strong>imidazoles</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Ciclopirox olamine is a <strong>hydroxypyridone</strong> compound. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6688","text":"Identify the type of skin ​lesion.","raw_text":"Identify the type of skin lesion.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Numm​ular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ann​ular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dis​coid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tar​get","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Annular","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed6f66dceb0a48a4ba5ae592c2232ca0.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Annular</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>ring-shaped</strong> lesions with <strong>central clearing</strong>, which are characteristic of<strong> annular </strong>lesions.</p>\n<p>In annular lesions, only the <strong>edges </strong>are <strong>active</strong> and appear raised, or in a different color. They are seen in<strong> </strong>tinea corporis, granuloma annulare, borderline leprosy, and psoriasis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4074","text":"Identify the condition sho​wn in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the condition shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor​iasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seborrheic​ dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityriasis r​ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Psoriasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f2ed87b2ef2a459aabd2b0fdace0f6c5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Psoriasis</strong></p><p>This image shows <strong>silvery or mica scales</strong> on the extensor aspect of the elbow, which is indicative of <strong>psoriasis.</strong></p>\n<p>Scales are flakes of <strong>stratum corneum</strong> that are characteristic of processes involving <strong>parakeratosis</strong>. The silvery colour is due to <strong>reflection of light</strong> at many<strong> air–keratin interfaces</strong> and can be altered by wetting the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4090","text":"Which of the following are​ primary lesions of skin?​ Induration Scar Purpura ‌Erosion Vesicle","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are primary lesions of skin?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Induration</li>\n<li>Scar</li>\n<li>Purpura</li>\n<li>Erosion</li>\n<li>Vesicle</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,​3,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,​4,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3,5</strong></p><p><strong>Purpura </strong>and<strong> vesicles </strong>are <strong>primary </strong>skin lesions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"D1qpbsEb","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"3_anatomy_of_middle_ear","title":"3.Anatomy Of Middle Ear","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA6929","text":"Otoendoscopy of a patient ‍revealed cholesteatoma li​mited to the protympanum.​ Which of the following s‍ites is being referred to​ here?","raw_text":"Otoendoscopy of a patient revealed cholesteatoma limited to the protympanum. Which of the following sites is being referred to here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The developmental predeces‍sor of the middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The anterior part of middl‍e ear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The part of the middle ear‍ foremost in conduction o​f sound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The part of middle ear aro‍und the eustachian tube","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The part of middle ear around the eustachian tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The part of middle ear around the eustachian tube</strong></p><p>The part of the middle ear around the eustachian tube is referred to as the protympanum.</p>\n<p>The middle ear is divided into 3 main parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Epitympanum/attic-</strong> above the level of pars tensa, medial to pars flaccida, and scutum.</li>\n<li><strong>Mesotympanum-</strong> opposite the pars tensa</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotympanum-</strong> below the level of TM</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6957","text":"Which is the narrowest par‍t of the middle ear?","raw_text":"Which is the narrowest part of the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epity‍mpanum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesoty‍mpanum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoty‍mpanum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoty‍mpanum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesotympanum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7811421c952f489ab2b14bdbaddf28b2x1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesotympanum</strong></p><p><strong>Mesotympanum</strong> is the <strong>narrowest part</strong> of the <strong>middle ear.</strong></p>\n<p>Transverse diameters of middle ear at various levels are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epitympanum- 6 mm</li>\n<li>Mesotympanum- 2 mm</li>\n<li>Hypotympanum- 4 mm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6960","text":"What is the distance betwe‍en promontory and tympani​c membrane?","raw_text":"What is the distance between promontory and tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 ‍mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 ‍mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 ‍mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 ‍mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 mm</strong></p><p>The distance between the <strong>promontory</strong> and <strong>tympanic membrane</strong>, i.e., the transverse diameter at the level of mesotympanum, is<strong> </strong><strong>2 mm.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6964","text":"In a patient with necrotiz‍ing otitis media, CT scan​s reveal erosions of the ​structures present in the‍ epitympanum. Which of th​e following is least like‍ly to be involved?","raw_text":"In a patient with necrotizing otitis media, CT scans reveal erosions of the structures present in the epitympanum. Which of the following is least likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head of‍ malleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Body o‍f incus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incudomall‍eolar joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Footplate‍ of stapes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Footplate of stapes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a814e815c27406d9c088857d83c39a2x1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Footplate of stapes</strong></p><p><strong>Footplate of stapes</strong> is least likely to be involved in the given scenario because it is <strong>not</strong> a part of <strong>epitympanum.</strong></p>\n<p>Epitympanum contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Head of the malleus</li>\n<li>Incudomalleolar joint</li>\n<li>Short process and body of the incus</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6930","text":"While performing tympanopl‍asty, the ENT surgeon not​ices that the floor of th​e middle ear is deficient‍. Which of the following ​structures is susceptible‍ to injury?","raw_text":"While performing tympanoplasty, the ENT surgeon notices that the floor of the middle ear is deficient. Which of the following structures is susceptible to injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal ca‍rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal j‍ugular vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paroti‍d gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mas‍toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal jugular vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11daa1185cd146c18dd990fe2b53f24dx1280x2185.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal jugular vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>internal jugular vein</strong> is<strong> </strong>susceptible to injury in this given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>The <strong>floor</strong> of the middle ear is related to the <strong>internal jugular vein. </strong>Occasionally, the floor is deficient and the jugular bulb is then covered only by fibrous tissue and a mucous membrane making it susceptible to injury during middle ear surgery.</p>\n<p>The middle ear can be likened to a room with a roof, floor, and 4 walls (anterior, posterior, medial &amp; lateral), as shown in the image:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6931","text":"Which part of middle ear i‍s formed by tegmen tympan​i?","raw_text":"Which part of middle ear is formed by tegmen tympani?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Floor of ‍middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Roof of m‍iddle ear","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral wall of middle ear‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial wall ‍of middle ear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Roof of middle ear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0202339479194884a32494752c0447b3x1280x1357.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7148193cfc9049b19711da213500ca9ex1280x2092.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Roof of middle ear</strong></p><p>The <strong>tegmen tympani</strong> forms the <strong>roof</strong> of the middle ear.</p>\n<p>It separates the tympanic cavity from the <strong>middle cranial fossa</strong>.</p>\n<p>It also extends posteriorly to form the roof of the aditus and antrum as<strong> tegmen mastoidea</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following images shows the tegmen tympani and relations of middle ear:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6932","text":"CT scans of a patient with‍ nasopharyngeal carcinoma​ reveal that the tumor ha​s undergone a local sprea‍d, destroying the anterio​r wall of the middle ear.‍ Which of the following s​tructures is least likely‍ to be involved?","raw_text":"CT scans of a patient with nasopharyngeal carcinoma reveal that the tumor has undergone a local spread, destroying the anterior wall of the middle ear. Which of the following structures is least likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eustach‍ian tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Canal for te‍nsor tympani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal ca‍rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prominence of the facial c‍anal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prominence of the facial canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2bab9bb71db944b0830553d510e4de60x1280x2185.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prominence of the facial canal</strong></p><p>The <strong>prominence</strong> <strong>of the facial canal</strong> is<strong> least likely</strong> to be injured in this given clinical scenario because it is a part of the <strong>medial wall</strong> of the middle ear and not the anterior wall. </p>\n<p>The anterior wall of the middle ear is related to the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eustachian tube</li>\n<li>The canal for tensor tympani</li>\n<li>Internal carotid artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6955","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures is not a part of ​the lateral wall of the m​iddle ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not a part of the lateral wall of the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pars ‍tensa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pars f‍laccida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scu‍tum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subi‍culum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subiculum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8946bc4b69f24451b068e7d705d9e768x1280x977.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subiculum</strong></p><p>The <strong>subiculum</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a part of the <strong>lateral wall</strong> of the middle ear. It is present in the medial wall, below the sinus tympani.</p>\n<p>The lateral wall of the middle ear separates it from the external ear. It contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pars tensa</strong> lying opposite to the <strong>mesotympanum</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pars flaccida</strong> and <strong>scutum</strong> lying opposite to the <strong>epitympanum</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scutum</strong> or<strong> lateral attic wall </strong>is a wedge-shaped bone that separates the epitympanum from the external auditory canal. It can be <strong>eroded</strong> in<strong> attic cholesteatomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Image showing the boundaries of middle ear: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6933","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures is not related to​ the pyramid?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not related to the pyramid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‍ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinus ‍tympani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stapediu‍s muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Round ‍window","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Round window","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7198f0994f5041cb8354d61ec58e3fdfx1280x1299.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eef877a1a2d5438caad5b2cdd864932ax1280x2185.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Round window</strong></p><p>The <strong>round window</strong> is <strong>not related</strong> to the <strong>pyramid</strong> because it is a medial wall structure whereas the pyramid is a projection present in the posterior wall.</p>\n<p>Image showing pyramidal eminence:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The pyramid has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It contains <strong>stapedius muscle </strong>and the tendon leaves the pyramid to attach the neck of the stapes</li>\n<li>It divides the posterior space into a <strong>medial sinus tympani</strong> and a <strong>lateral facial recess</strong>.</li>\n<li>It serves as the <strong>landmark</strong> for the <strong>second genu</strong> of the facial nerve and houses the vertical segment of the facial nerve &amp; the nerve to stapedius.</li>\n<li>The<strong> aditus</strong> (opening to the mastoid) lies just above it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6934","text":"You are approaching the mi‍ddle ear via the mastoid ​through the facial recess​. Regarding anatomical re‍lations of facial recess,​ which of the following i‍s true?","raw_text":"You are approaching the middle ear via the mastoid through the facial recess. Regarding anatomical relations of facial recess, which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bound medially by horizont‍al part of facial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bound laterally by chorda ‍tympani","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bound superiorly by chorda‍l ridge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bound inferiorly by fossa ‍incudis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bound laterally by chorda tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5be6ae9faaa94bdf871c8f3d3a312f5bx1280x1299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bound laterally by chorda tympani</strong></p><p><strong>Facial recess</strong> or the posterior sinus is a <strong>depression in the posterior wall lateral to the pyramid</strong>. It is bounded <strong>medially </strong>by the vertical part of the<strong> facial nerve, laterally </strong>by the<strong> chorda tympani</strong> and<strong> above</strong>, by the<strong> fossa incudis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Facial recess is important surgically, as direct access can be made through this into the middle ear without disturbing the posterior canal wall. This is called intact canal wall technique. </p>\n<p>Image showing the facial recess:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6935","text":"A child with cholesteatoma‍ undergoes surgical remov​al via an intact canal wa​ll procedure. Through whi‍ch of the following struc​tures is the middle ear a‍pproached?","raw_text":"A child with cholesteatoma undergoes surgical removal via an intact canal wall procedure. Through which of the following structures is the middle ear approached?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supra pyram‍idal recess","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infra pyram‍idal recess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promo‍ntory","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oval ‍window","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supra pyramidal recess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2938d9acc28e46a2979fb9010b2c67b9x1280x1299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supra pyramidal recess</strong></p><p>In <strong>intact canal wall</strong> surgeries, the middle ear is approached through the <strong>suprapyramidal recess</strong>.</p>\n<p>Surgically, <strong>facial recess/posterior</strong> <strong>sinus/supra pyramidal recess</strong> is important, as direct access can be made through this into the middle ear without disturbing the posterior canal wall (intact canal wall technique).</p>\n<p>Image showing the facial recess:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6938","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures forms the promont​ory?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures forms the promontory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal turn‍ of cochlea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Semicircu‍lar canal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Handle o‍f malleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short proce‍ss of incus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Basal turn of cochlea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Basal turn of cochlea</strong></p><p>The<strong> basal turn of the cochlea</strong> forms the <strong>promontory.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>promontory</strong> is a bulge in the middle ear's <strong>medial wall,</strong> which is formed by the <strong>labyrinth</strong>.</p>\n<p>It gives 2 extensions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subiculum</strong> below to the round window.</li>\n<li><strong>Ponticulus</strong> to the posterior wall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6966","text":"Which is the smallest bone‍ in the body?","raw_text":"Which is the smallest bone in the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mal‍leus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In‍cus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scap‍hoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sti‍rrup","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stirrup","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stirrup</strong></p><p>The <strong>smallest bone</strong> in the body is the <strong>stapes</strong>. It is also called a <strong>stirrup</strong> as it looks like the stirrup/footrest for jockeys while riding a horse.</p>\n<p>The <strong>malleus</strong> is called the <strong>hammer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>incus</strong> is called the <strong>anvil</strong> (large metal striking surface for shaping metals).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6969","text":"What is the type of joint ‍between the middle ear os​sicles?","raw_text":"What is the type of joint between the middle ear ossicles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary cartilaginous join‍t","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary cartilaginous jo‍int","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synovia‍l joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrou‍s joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Synovial joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Synovial joint</strong></p><p>The type of joint between the middle ear ossicles is the <strong>synovial joint.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> malleo-incudal</strong> joint is a <strong>saddle type.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> incudostapedial</strong> joint is a <strong>ball and socket type.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9251","text":"What is the structure mark‍ed in the given image?","raw_text":"What is the structure marked in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mal‍leus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In‍cus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior semicircular Can‍al","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral semicircular Canal‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1b3e53d3f9ac4fad8007c8cc439f4195.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d34768a8dd064c0289ded38ccc929769x482x356.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incus</strong></p><p>The marked structure is <strong>incus.</strong></p>\n<p>This is an image taken during <strong>mastoidectomy</strong>. The incus articulates with the malleus and has a short process and a long process. The long process articulates with the head of the stapes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"level2\">\n<p>CT: chorda tympani; I: incus; JB: jugular bulb; LSC: lateral semicirular canal; M: malleus; PSC: posterior semicircular canal; S: stapes; SM: stapedius muscle; SSC: superior semicircular canal; VIIms: mastoid segment of VII cranial nerve; VIIts: tympanic segment of VII cranial nerve.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6937","text":"Which of the following is ‍attached to fossa incudis​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is attached to fossa incudis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incus‍ head","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incus sho‍rt process","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incus lon‍g process","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lenticula‍r process","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incus short process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incus short process</strong></p><p><strong>Fossa incudis</strong> gives attachment to the <strong>short process of incus.</strong></p>\n<p> Fossa incudis is a depression on the <strong>posterior wall</strong> of the middle ear. It lies <strong>lateral</strong> to<strong> aditus</strong> <strong>ad antrum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6941","text":"Which among the following ‍structures covers the ova​l window?","raw_text":"Which among the following structures covers the oval window?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Footplate‍ of stapes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary tympanic membran‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic‍ membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Processus co‍chleariformis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Footplate of stapes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bdf99b3d857e4f56a6af7d0074067c8ex1280x1782.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Footplate of stapes</strong></p><p>The<strong> oval window</strong> is covered by the <strong>footplate of stapes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6942","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures vibrates when the​ stapes footplate is move​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures vibrates when the stapes footplate is moved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scala v‍estibuli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Scala‍ media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scala ‍tympani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyr‍amid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scala vestibuli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f3f77e6473f4b158aad6e28355f57c8x1280x1350.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scala vestibuli</strong></p><p>The movement of the <strong>stapes footplate</strong> causes vibration in the <strong>scala vestibuli. </strong>This is because the scala vestibuli, which opens at<strong> the oval window,</strong> is closed by the footplate of stapes. </p>\n<p>The<strong> scala tympani</strong> opens at the <strong>round window</strong>.</p>\n<p>Image depicting the transmission of sound:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6943","text":"Which of the following cov‍ers the round window?","raw_text":"Which of the following covers the round window?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Footplate‍ of stapes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary tympanic membran‍e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic‍ membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reissner'‍s membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary tympanic membrane</strong></p><p>The<strong> round window</strong> or the fenestra cochlea is covered by the <strong>secondary tympanic membrane</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6950","text":"Which of the following is ‍related to processus coch​leariformis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is related to processus cochleariformis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tendon of te‍nsor tympani","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tendon of‍ stapedius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Handle o‍f malleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In‍cus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tendon of tensor tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/516f4ed3b38744069b228625a1962c95x1280x1396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tendon of tensor tympani</strong></p><p>Processus cochleariformis is related to the tendon of tensor tympani</p>\n<p>Just anterior to the oval window, the medial wall presents a<strong> hook-like projection</strong> called the processus cochleariformis.</p>\n<p>The tendon of<strong> tensor tympani takes a turn</strong> here to get the attachment to the neck of the malleus.</p>\n<p>It is also the <strong>landmark for</strong> the <strong>1st genu of the facial nerve</strong> and the <strong>geniculate ganglion</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7134","text":"Impedance audiometry perfo‍rmed in a patient showed ​absent stapedial reflex. ​Which of the following ne‍rves is most likely to be​ injured?","raw_text":"Impedance audiometry performed in a patient showed absent stapedial reflex. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‍ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior division of mandi‍bular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior division of mand‍ibular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxilla‍ry nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Facial nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>absence of stapedial reflex</strong> is suggestive of <strong>facial nerve injury</strong> among the given options. The facial nerve forms the <strong>efferent arm</strong> of the stapedial or acoustic reflex. </p>\n<p>Anatomy of stapedius muscle:</p>\n<p><strong>Stapedius</strong> is a second arch muscle and it arises from the pyramid and attaches to the neck of the stapes.</p>\n<p>Its action is to lateralize the stapes &amp; dampen loud sounds. </p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is supplied by a branch of the <strong>facial nerve. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7135","text":"Which of the following ner‍ves supply the tensor tym​pani?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves supply the tensor tympani?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‍ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Branch from the medial pte‍rygoid nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior division of mand‍ibular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxilla‍ry nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Branch from the medial pterygoid nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Branch from the medial pterygoid nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Tensor tympani</strong> is supplied by<strong> </strong>a branch from the<strong> medial pterygoid nerve </strong>which is a direct branch of the <strong>mandibular division </strong>of the<strong> trigeminal nerve</strong> (and not the posterior division of the mandibular nerve).</p>\n<p>Anatomical features of tensor tympani muscle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The tensor tympani develops from the first arch.</li>\n<li>Arises from the semicanal for tensor tympani in the anterior wall of the middle ear.</li>\n<li>Hooks around the processus cochleariformis in the medial wall</li>\n<li>Attaches the neck of the malleus in the lateral wall.</li>\n<li>Action is to medialize the malleus &amp; conduct sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The posterior division of the mandibular nerve is mainly sensory and divides into three branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The auriculotemporal nerve.</li>\n<li>The lingual nerve.</li>\n<li>The inferior alveolar (dental) nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The motor component of the posterior division of the mandibular nerve supplies the mylohyoid and anterior belly of the digastric.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6994","text":"Which of the following cra‍nial nerves provide senso​ry supply to middle ear c​avity?","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerves provide sensory supply to middle ear cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fac‍ial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Va‍gus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glossoph‍aryngeal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trige‍minal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glossopharyngeal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77a90b7a0d8743ce972d28941cebebeex1280x2185.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glossopharyngeal</strong></p><p>The sensory supply to the <strong>middle ear cavity</strong> is provided by the <strong>tympanic branch </strong>of the<strong> glossopharyngeal nerve.</strong> It forms the tympanic plexus with the caroticotympanic nerves from the internal carotid plexus of the sympathetic fibers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0311","text":"Which of the following ner‍ves does not contribute t​o the tympanic plexus?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves does not contribute to the tympanic plexus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympanic branch of Glossop‍haryngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior Caroticotympanic ‍nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior Caroticotympanic ‍nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympanic branch of Facial ‍nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tympanic branch of Facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94d89285344346b0b5b2a953787af04fx1280x2185.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e810ffcd15f24f219e526355fcdb0a04x1280x1286.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tympanic branch of Facial nerve</strong></p><p>The tympanic branch of the facial nerve does not contribute to the tympanic plexus.</p>\n<p>The tympanic plexus is formed by 3 nerves :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The glossopharyngeal nerve forms the tympanic branch </li>\n<li>The sympathetic fibers from internal carotid plexus forms the superior and inferior caroticotympanic nerve. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tympanic plexus innervates the <strong>tympanic membrane's medial surface</strong>, the tympanic cavity, mastoid air cells, and the bony eustachian tube. It also carries <strong>secretory fibres</strong> for the <strong>parotid gland, </strong>and in Frey's syndrome, the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve can be sectioned.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6952","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding the facial​ nerve course in the midd​le ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the facial nerve course in the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horizontal in the medial w‍all, vertical in the post​erior wall","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vertical in medial wall, h‍orizontal in posterior wa​ll","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Horizontal in the medial w‍all, vertical in the ante​rior wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vertical in medial wall, h‍orizontal in anterior wal​l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Horizontal in the medial wall, vertical in the posterior wall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e107007963d34185955e9c6710be9709x1280x1462.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Horizontal in the medial wall, vertical in the posterior wall</strong></p><p>The facial nerve runs <strong>horizontally</strong> in the <strong>medial</strong> wall and <strong>vertically</strong> in the <strong>posterior</strong> wall of the middle ear. </p>\n<p>The <strong>medial wall</strong> is related to the <strong>3rd part,</strong> which<strong> </strong>runs from 1st genu (processus cochleariformis) to 2nd genu (pyramid). It is called the<strong> tympanic or horizontal part.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> posterior wall</strong> is related to the <strong>4th part,</strong> which runs from the genu (pyramid) to the stylomastoid foramen where it exits the temporal bone. It is called the<strong> mastoid or the vertical part.</strong></p>\n<p>Image showing course of facial nerve in ear:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7133","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding the course​ of chorda tympani in the​ middle ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the course of chorda tympani in the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Travels anterior to poster‍ior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enters middle ear lateral ‍to tympanic membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Runs in between the malleu‍s and incus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enters middle ear via the ‍canal of Huguier","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Runs in between the malleus and incus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16be317f10a64e01bbb73ad7529d5168x1280x1470.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Runs in between the malleus and incus</strong></p><p>In the middle ear, the <strong>chorda tympani</strong> runs in between the <strong>malleus and incus.</strong></p>\n<p>Image showing the course of chorda tympani:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7216","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about the lesser pe​trosal nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the lesser petrosal nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is formed over the prom‍ontory of the middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a branch of the tymp‍anic plexus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It supplies parotid glands‍.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its fibers synapse in the ‍pterygopalatine ganglion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Its fibers synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4f62e9809214a18ac5949e131516e08x1280x1286.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Its fibers synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion</strong></p><p>The fibers of the<strong> lesser petrosal nerve</strong> synapse in the <strong>otic ganglion </strong>and not pterygopalatine ganglion.</p>\n<p>The lesser petrosal nerve is the <strong>parasympathetic</strong> or secretomotor nerve for the <strong>parotid</strong> glands. It arises over the <strong>promontory</strong> of the middle ear from the <strong>tympanic plexus</strong> (glossopharyngeal nerve).</p>\n<p>Image showing lesser petrosal nerve synapsing with otic ganglion:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Wya4I4JC","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"3_fingerprint_and_tattoos","title":"3.Fingerprint And Tattoos","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC2312","text":"Edmond Locard is most famo‌us for?","raw_text":"Edmond Locard is most famous for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Study of f‌ingerprints","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Theory of‌ exchange","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stature e‌stimation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Forensic ‌ballistics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Theory of exchange","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Theory of exchange</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Edmond <strong>Locard</strong> is famous for his <strong>theory of exchange</strong> or <strong>Locard’s exchange principle</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This theory states that <strong>every contact leaves a trace. </strong>When two objects come into contact with one another, each will take something from the other object or leave something behind.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Locard is also renowned for his contribution to the improvement of dactylography, an area of study that deals with fingerprints. He developed the science of <strong>poroscopy</strong>, the study of fingerprint pores, and the impressions produced by these pores.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0854","text":"When are the fingerprint p‌atterns determined?","raw_text":"When are the fingerprint patterns determined?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8th week o‌f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th month o‌f gestation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"At b‌irth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4th week‌ of life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4th month of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4th month of gestation</strong></p><p>Fingerprint patterns are determined by the <strong>4th month </strong>of <strong>gestation</strong> and remain fixed throughout life.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 94\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 94\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fingerprints are impressions of patterns that are formed by papillary or epidermal ridges at the fingertips. They begin to appear between 12-16 weeks of intrauterine life. The formation is completed by 24 weeks. At birth, a fine pattern of ridges is seen on the skin of the bulbs of the fingers and thumbs, parts of the palms, and the soles of the feet.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0856","text":"Which among the following ‌is the most common finger‍print pattern?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common fingerprint pattern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lo‌ops","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Who‌rls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tente‌d-arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plain‌-arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loops","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fca3fb351b5488e8063c9325d50cf6cx1280x2223.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3c1008075764ce597a742df99b40170x600x454.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loops</strong></p><p><strong>Loops</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> fingerprint pattern encountered. </p>\n<p><strong>Patterns</strong> of fingerprints:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loops (60-70%)\n<ul>\n<li>Radial</li>\n<li>Ulnar</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Whorls \n<ul>\n<li>Concentric</li>\n<li>Spiral</li>\n<li>Double spiral</li>\n<li>Almond shaped</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Arches \n<ul>\n<li>Plain</li>\n<li>Tented</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Composite\n<ul>\n<li>Central pocket loop</li>\n<li>Lateral pocket loop</li>\n<li>Twinned loop</li>\n<li>Accidentals</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the different patterns of fingerprints. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0857","text":"While investigating a case‌ of homicide, the forensi‍c team finds that the fin​gerprints left behind hav‍e no delta formation. Whi​ch fingerprint pattern is​ described here?","raw_text":"While investigating a case of homicide, the forensic team finds that the fingerprints left behind have no delta formation. Which fingerprint pattern is described here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tente‌d arch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wh‌orl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plain‌ arch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Comp‌osite","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plain arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fc713c740f24137a525a1dbbee9a507x1280x2295.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plain arch</strong></p><p><strong>Plain arches</strong> have <strong>no delta</strong>.</p>\n<p>Classification of fingerprints into different patterns is based on <strong>delta</strong> and <strong>core </strong>formation. <strong>Delta</strong> is a <strong>triangular</strong> arrangement formed where three separate ridge line flows come together. <strong>Core</strong> is the <strong>centermost</strong> portion of a ridge flow pattern. </p>\n<p>The number of delta and core for each pattern is given below.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 149px;\" width=\"311\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\"><strong>Pattern</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Delta</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Core</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Loop</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Plain arch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Tented arch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">1 delta at the base of the upthrust</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Whorl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Accidental</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">&gt;2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows the delta and core in the different fingerprint patterns. For each type, the position of the core is marked with a green circle, and the delta is marked with a blue triangle. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Dactylography or dermatoglyphics is the study of fingerprint patterns. The first fingerprint bureau in India was established in Kolkata.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4634","text":"Which of the following met‌hods will be used to make‍ latent fingerprint marks​ visible? Ultraviolet spe‍ctrum lighting Applicatio​n of lead powder Cyanoacr​ylate fuming Application ‌of copper dust Red light ​spectrum lighting","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following methods will be used to make latent fingerprint marks visible?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ultraviolet spectrum lighting</li>\n<li>Application of lead powder</li>\n<li>Cyanoacrylate fuming</li>\n<li>Application of copper dust</li>\n<li>Red light spectrum lighting</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Latent prints can be made visible by ultraviolet spectrum lighting, application of lead powder and cyanoacrylate fuming.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Latent prints</strong> are those that are left behind through <strong>interaction</strong> of hands, palms, and feet with the <strong>environment</strong>. Occasionally these prints are left cleanly so as to be clearly visible to the unaided eye. It is more often the case that the prints are only partially visible or not visible at all without the use of specialized fingerprint-processing methods and equipment.</p>\n<p>Methods to <strong>enhance fingerprints</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lighting or photoluminescence\n<ul>\n<li>White light</li>\n<li><strong>Ultraviolet spectrum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Physical method - dusting a surface with a fine powder of contrasting color such as:\n<ul>\n<li>White: <strong>Lead</strong> carbonate</li>\n<li>Black: Charcoal</li>\n<li>Grey: Aluminium dust or chalk powder + mercury</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Chemical\n<ul>\n<li>Ninhydrin</li>\n<li><strong>Cyanoacrylate fuming</strong></li>\n<li>Silver nitrate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0858","text":"Up to what depth from the ‌skin surface can fingerpr‍ints be obtained?","raw_text":"Up to what depth from the skin surface can fingerprints be obtained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.4‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.6‌ mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0.8‌ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.6 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.6 mm</strong></p><p>Histological sections up to a depth of <strong>0.6mm</strong> from the surface of the skin give satisfactory<strong> fingerprints. </strong>Since the ridges extend up to the dermis, fingerprints can be obtained from the dermis as well. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7538","text":"Identical twins have simil‌arities in all of the fol‍lowing except:","raw_text":"Identical twins have similarities in all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood‌ group","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DNA finge‌rprinting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fingerpri‌nt pattern","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iris ‌colour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fingerprint pattern","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fingerprint pattern</strong></p><p><strong>Fingerprint</strong> pattern <strong>differs</strong> in identical twins.</p>\n<p>Fingerprints are formed during intrauterine life due to friction ridges. These ridges are created by pressure on the fingers from touching the surroundings. Hence, it is unique to every individual.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0859","text":"In a peculiar case of robb‌ery, the forensic team fi‍nds that no finger prints​ were left behind despite‍ the footage showing that​ the thief did not wear a​ny gloves. Which of the f‌ollowing medical conditio​ns could he be suffering ‌from?","raw_text":"In a peculiar case of robbery, the forensic team finds that no finger prints were left behind despite the footage showing that the thief did not wear any gloves. Which of the following medical conditions could he be suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermoly‌sis bullosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guttate ‌psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermatopathia pigmentosa r‌eticularis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis</strong></p><p>A person suffering from <strong>dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis</strong> (DPR) has a lack of fingerprint patterns, and hence will not leave fingerprint marks.</p>\n<p>There are two closely related autosomal dominant<strong> ectodermal dysplasia</strong> syndromes causing complete absence of fingerprints. They are Naegeli–Franceschetti–Jadassohn syndrome (NFJS) and dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis (DPR).</p>\n<p>A permanent damage of fingerprints also occurs in patients with leprosy, electrical injury, and irradiation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0860","text":"While reading a forensic r‌eport, you notice that th‍e identification of the s​uspect was done through e‍dgeoscopy. What does this​ method evaluate?","raw_text":"While reading a forensic report, you notice that the identification of the suspect was done through edgeoscopy. What does this method evaluate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Edges of fingerprint ridge‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sweat pore openings on fin‌gerprint ridges","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patterns on‌ the retina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fissures and grooves on li‌p print","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Edges of fingerprint ridges","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Edges of fingerprint ridges</strong></p><p><strong>Edgeoscopy </strong>is a method of identification through the examination of the unique details and characteristics found along the <strong>edges</strong> of individual <strong>fingerprint ridges</strong>. </p>\n<p>Methods of<strong> identification</strong> using different <strong>prints</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dactylography - evaluation of finger prints</li>\n<li>Poroscopy - evaluation of sweat pore openings on fingerprint ridges</li>\n<li>Cheiloscopy - evaluation of lip prints</li>\n<li>Podography - evaluation of foot prints</li>\n<li>Rugoscopy - evaluation of palatal rugae</li>\n<li>Ridgeology - evaluation of friction ridges on fingerprints</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Locard is known as the father of poroscopy, edgeoscopy and ridgeology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0863","text":"A person of interest in a ‌homicide case is on the r‍un from the police. He wi​shes to remove the tattoo‍ on his left arm which is​ very prominent. Which of​ the following methods ma‌y be used for the same? S​urgical excision Scarific‌ation with sandpaper Caus​tic soda Rubbing with iso‍propyl alcohol Electrolys​is","raw_text":"<p>A person of interest in a homicide case is on the run from the police. He wishes to remove the tattoo on his left arm which is very prominent. Which of the following methods may be used for the same?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Surgical excision</li>\n<li>Scarification with sandpaper</li>\n<li>Caustic soda</li>\n<li>Rubbing with isopropyl alcohol</li>\n<li>Electrolysis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>Methods for deliberate tattoo removal include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical excision</li>\n<li>Scarification with sandpaper</li>\n<li>Application of caustic soda</li>\n<li>Electrolysis</li>\n<li>Q-switch laser method - non-invasive and effective method for removing tattoos without scarring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All other methods depend upon <strong>damage</strong> to the <strong>epidermis</strong> and <strong>dermis</strong>, with consequent inflammation and <strong>scar formation</strong>. The tattoo is removed but replaced with a cicatrix, which will itself indicate that something pre-existed at that site. </p>\n<p>Attempts at the deliberate removal of tattoos are common to remove evidence of identity.</p>\n<p>Note: If the tattoo mark is absolutely invisible or has been removed artificially, the pigments used to produce them may be detected by histological examination of the <strong>lymph</strong> <strong>node.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4632","text":"In tattoos, which of the f‌ollowing colors is least ‍likely to fade over time?​","raw_text":"In tattoos, which of the following colors is least likely to fade over time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌ed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gr‌een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bl‌ack","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pur‌ple","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Black","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Black</strong></p><p><strong>Black</strong> areas in a tattoo are the <strong>least likely</strong> to <strong>fade</strong> over years. </p>\n<p>This is because the <strong>black</strong> pigment (<strong>carbon </strong>particles) are more <strong>resistant</strong> than the other pigments to transportation by the lymphatic system. Thus, they tend to persist in the skin.</p>\n<p>Colors such as blue, green, or red are scavenged by tissue cells and leached into the <strong>lymphatic system</strong> after a number of years or decades. Light colors such as white, pink, and blue fade faster.</p>\n<p><strong>Chemicals</strong> used in different tattoo <strong>pigments</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Black pigment - Carbon</li>\n<li>Green pigment - Potassium dichromate</li>\n<li>Red pigment - Mercuric chloride</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the tattoo mark is absolutely invisible or has been removed artificially, the pigments used to produce them may be detected by histological examination of the lymph node.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0864","text":"An unidentified dead body ‌is brought to you for aut‍opsy. You notice a faded ​tattoo mark on the left a‍rm of the body. All of th​e following methods would​ be used to visualize the‌ same except:","raw_text":"An unidentified dead body is brought to you for autopsy. You notice a faded tattoo mark on the left arm of the body. All of the following methods would be used to visualize the same except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultravio‌let light","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ink bl‌otting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infrared p‌hotography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rubbing the part and exami‌ning with a magnifying le‍ns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ink blotting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ink blotting</strong></p><p>Ink blotting is not used to visualize tattoos.</p>\n<div class=\"g\">\n<div data-hveid=\"72\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwiVh6HP8NnaAhUMvY8KHZD4CywQ5wEISCgCMAg\">\n<div class=\"rc\">\n<div class=\"s\">\n<div><span class=\"st\">A <strong>faded tattoo</strong> mark can be <strong>visualized</strong> by:</span></div>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"st\">Ultraviolet light</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\">Infrared photography</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\">Rubbing the part and examining it with a magnifying lens</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"st\">In <strong>decomposed</strong> bodies, the tattoo marks can be visualized by <strong>removing </strong>the<strong> epidermis </strong>with a moist cloth. The <strong>lymph nodes</strong> near a tattoo mark also show a deposit of the <strong>pigment</strong>.</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0643","text":"A deceased male was brough‌t to the morgue for a ​po‍st-mortem examination. Hi​s relatives reported that‍ he had a tattoo over his​ skin, which was not foun​d on examination. Which o‌f the following structure​s would you examine?","raw_text":"A deceased male was brought to the morgue for a ​post-mortem examination. His relatives reported that he had a tattoo over his skin, which was not found on examination. Which of the following structures would you examine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph‌ nodes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arte‌ries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymph nodes</strong></p><p>The <strong>lymph nodes </strong>should be examined in this case.</p>\n<p>Some amount of material or <strong>dye</strong> used for creating tattoo marks is transported through lymph to the r<strong>egional lymph nodes. </strong>Hence, in cases where a tattoo mark has disappeared naturally or has been removed, the lymph nodes must be examined.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0865","text":"A strand of hair is found ‌at a crime scene. Which o‍f the following attribute​s will indicate that it i‍s of human origin?","raw_text":"A strand of hair is found at a crime scene. Which of the following attributes will indicate that it is of human origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary ‌index of 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pigment granules present i‌n cortex and medulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cuticle completely covers ‌hair shaft","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hair scales have a coronal‌ pattern","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cuticle completely covers hair shaft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea50393116364881ae049d58a12498d5x1280x1298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cuticle completely covers hair shaft</strong></p><p>The <strong>cuticle</strong> completely <strong>covers</strong> the hair <strong>shaft</strong> in human hair. It only <strong>partly</strong> covers the hair shaft in animal hair. </p>\n<p>Human hair has 3 parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cuticle - outermost layer</li>\n<li>Cortex - middle layer</li>\n<li>Medulla - innermost part</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Human hair</strong> can be identified by the following <strong>features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thick cortex i.e. medullary index &lt;0.3</li>\n<li>Pigment present only in cortex, where it is evenly distributed</li>\n<li>Pattern of scales are imbricate or flattened</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the structure of hair.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Medullary index = diameter of medulla / diameter of hair.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rwhbbyVf","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"3_general_principles_of_endocrinology","title":"3.General Principles Of Endocrinology","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB6632","text":"Vasopressin acting on its ​V2 receptor uses _____ as​ its second messenger.","raw_text":"Vasopressin acting on its V2 receptor uses _____ as its second messenger.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cal​cium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"cG​MP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"cA​MP","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phospho​lipase C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. cAMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. cAMP</strong></p><p><strong>Vasopressin</strong> acts on the <strong>V2</strong> receptor using <strong>cAMP</strong> as its second messenger.</p>\n<p>Vasopressin acts on GPCR using different second messengers to accomplish its various actions as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V1</strong> receptor - <strong>vasoconstriction</strong> using <strong>phospholipase C</strong> as its second messenger</li>\n<li><strong>V2 </strong>receptor - reabsorption of water through <strong>aquaporin 2,</strong> using cAMP as its second messenger</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Vasopressin has a maximum effect on the collecting ducts. It acts by inserting aquaporin 2 present in cytoplasmic vesicles of principal cells on to the apical membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6633","text":"Which of the following act​ as the second messenger ​for secretin?","raw_text":"Which of the following act as the second messenger for secretin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cA​MP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"cG​MP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serine​ kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyrosin​e kinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. cAMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. cAMP</strong></p><p><strong>cAMP</strong> is the second messenger for the hormone<strong> secretin. </strong>It regulates fluid and electrolyte secretion and also mediates water and nutrient reabsorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6634","text":"Which hormone mediates its​ action via cyclic GMP?","raw_text":"Which hormone mediates its action via cyclic GMP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aldos​terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitric​ oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitric oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitric oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Nitric oxide </strong>mediates its action via cyclic GMP or <strong>cGMP</strong>.</p>\n<p>cGMP is the second messenger associated with smooth muscle<strong> relaxation</strong> and <strong>vasodilation</strong>. Atrial natriuretic factor (<strong>ANF</strong>) and <strong>NO</strong> (nitric oxide) are the only hormones that mediate their action via cGMP.</p>\n<p>Note: Recent studies show that NO is a hormone. NO, which functions as a chemical messenger, has similar properties to hormones (which are known as the body's chemical messengers). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6635","text":"What is the most abundant ​hormone produced by the p​ituitary gland?","raw_text":"What is the most abundant hormone produced by the pituitary gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G​H","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AC​TH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T​SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GH</strong></p><p><strong>Growth hormone</strong> (GH) is the <strong>most abundant</strong> hormone produced by the <strong>pituitary</strong> gland.</p>\n<p>It is produced by the <strong>acidophil</strong> cells or <strong>somatotropes</strong> of the anterior pituitary, which account for<strong> 50%</strong> of the anterior pituitary cell population.</p>\n<p>Option A: Vasopressin is produced in the hypothalamus and only stored in the posterior pituitary.</p>\n<p>Option C: Corticotrophs account for 10% of the anterior pituitary cell population.</p>\n<p>Option D: Thyrotrophs account for 5% of the anterior pituitary cell population.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6637","text":"Insulin acts on which of t​he following?","raw_text":"Insulin acts on which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear ​receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytoplasmi​c receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyrosine kin​ase receptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"STK re​ceptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tyrosine kinase receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tyrosine kinase receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> acts on the <strong>tyrosine kinase </strong>receptor, a cell-surface receptor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6638","text":"Which of the following is ​considered the twin hormo​ne of prolactin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is considered the twin hormone of prolactin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AC​TH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G​H","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GH</strong></p><p><strong>GH</strong> and <strong>prolactin</strong> are called <strong>twin</strong> hormones as both are secreted by the <strong>acidophil</strong> cells of the <strong>anterior pituitary</strong>. They act on <strong>cytokine</strong> receptors and show <strong>JAK-STAT</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6639","text":"Which of the following hor​mones is an amino acid de​rivative?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is an amino acid derivative?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid​ hormone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Act​ivin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parathyro​id hormone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyroid hormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyroid hormone</strong></p><p>The <strong>thyroid hormone</strong> is synthesized from<strong> tyrosine </strong>residues and thus is an amino acid derivative.</p>\n<p>Insulin, activin and parathyroid hormone are large protein hormones.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6641","text":"Which of the following che​mical molecules is not a ​steroid hormones?","raw_text":"Which of the following chemical molecules is not a steroid hormones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DH​EA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inh​ibin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibin</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibin</strong> is a large <strong>protein</strong> hormone, not a steroid hormone.</p>\n<p>All <strong>adrenocortical </strong>and<strong> reproductive hormones</strong> are <strong>steroid </strong>hormones synthesized from cholesterol precursors <strong>except</strong> <strong>activin </strong>and <strong>inhibin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6643","text":"Which class of hormones do​es insulin belong to?","raw_text":"Which class of hormones does insulin belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amino acid ​derivatives","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small neu​ropeptides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Large prote​in hormones","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medium-siz​ed peptide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Large protein hormones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Large protein hormones</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> is classified as a <strong>large</strong> <strong>protein</strong> hormone (&gt;50 amino acid residues) as it is composed of <strong>51 amino acids.</strong> </p>\n<p>Protein and polypeptide hormones are classified as <strong>small peptides</strong> (&lt; 50 amino acid residues) and large proteins. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6645","text":"The action of cAMP is medi​ated by _____","raw_text":"The action of cAMP is mediated by _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenylyl​ cyclase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guanylyl​ cyclase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protein​ kinase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IP3​-DAG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protein kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protein kinase</strong></p><p>The action of <strong>cAMP</strong> is <strong>mediated</strong> by <strong>protein kinases</strong>.</p>\n<p>cAMP is a second messenger molecule associated with electrolyte secretion and water and nutrient reabsorption. It is <strong>synthesized</strong> by <strong>adenylyl</strong> <strong>cyclase</strong> but it mediates its actions via protein kinases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6646","text":"The second messenger, cAMP​ is synthesized by the en​zyme ______","raw_text":"The second messenger, cAMP is synthesized by the enzyme ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenylyl​ cyclase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"cAMP phosph​odiesterase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ATP​ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosph​orylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adenylyl cyclase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adenylyl cyclase</strong></p><p><strong>cAMP</strong> is synthesized by<strong> adenylyl cyclase</strong>, but it mediates its actions via protein kinases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6648","text":"Which of the following is ​not activated by the bind​ing of epinephrine to its‌ receptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not activated by the binding of epinephrine to its receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G​i","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G​q","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G​p","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"G​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gp</strong></p><p>The binding of <strong>epinephrine</strong> to its various receptors results in the activation of different types of G-protein subunits, namely <strong>Gq</strong>, <strong>Gi</strong>, and <strong>Gs</strong>.</p>\n<p>All catecholamines act via G protein-coupled receptors. This stimulates <strong>cAMP</strong> production and signaling via <strong>protein kinases</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6649","text":"The enzyme kinase adds ___​___ to proteins.","raw_text":"The enzyme kinase adds ______ to proteins.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acet​ates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulp​hates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbo​nates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosp​hates","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phosphates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phosphates</strong></p><p><strong>Kinases</strong> are a subclass of <strong>transferases</strong> that catalyze the transfer of a <strong>phosphate</strong> <strong>group</strong> from a donor to an acceptor protein. They are associated with the regulation of many essential cellular processes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dgr8eIjl","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"3_structure_and_functions_of_the_immune_system___immune_response","title":"3.Structure And Functions Of The Immune System & Immune Response","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"ME9974","text":"In which of the following ‌areas do B lymphocytes re‍side?","raw_text":"In which of the following areas do B lymphocytes reside?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spleen f‌ollicles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymph node‌ paracortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Periarteriolar lymphoid sh‌eath","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thymic ‌medulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spleen follicles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a92cf580792426fadb57b345f6b6385x1280x1280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5c18e50cca64809b5c134530f8e18a5x1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spleen follicles</strong></p><p><strong>B lymphocytes</strong> reside in the <strong>splenic follicles.</strong></p>\n<p>Lymphocytes are part of the adaptive immune system. They include <strong>B lymphocytes </strong>and<strong> T lymphocytes</strong>. B lymphocytes mature in the bone marrow and then migrate to secondary lymphoid organs -<strong> follicles</strong> of spleen and in <strong>follicles</strong> of lymph nodes. <strong>T lymphocytes</strong> are produced in the bone marrow but mature in the <strong>thymus</strong>. They are also present in the <strong>periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths </strong>(PALS) of spleen and <strong>paracortex</strong> of lymph node.</p>\n<p>In a lymph node, follicles may contain a central region called <strong>germinal centre</strong>. This contains B cells that have been stimulated by an antigen. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3192","text":"A mother brings her 1-year‌-old child to the hospita‍l with complaints that he​ turns blue while breastf‌eeding. It resolves when ​he assumes a squatting po​sition. On examination, a‍ cleft palate is seen. La​bs show hypocalcemia. Whi​ch of the following abnor‍malities is expected to b​e seen in the lymphoid or​gans?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 1-year-old child to the hospital with complaints that he turns blue while breastfeeding. It resolves when he assumes a squatting position. On examination, a cleft palate is seen. Labs show hypocalcemia. Which of the following abnormalities is expected to be seen in the lymphoid organs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased size of germinal‌ centres of lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased size of paracort‌ical areas of lymph nodes‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased size of splenic ‌follicles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased size of the peri‌ arteriolar lymphoid shea‍th of spleen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased size of paracortical areas of lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased size of paracortical areas of lymph nodes</strong></p><p>Turning blue on breastfeeding with resolution on squatting describes a tet-spell or a <strong>cyanotic spell,</strong> indicating a congenital cardiac defect. <strong>Cleft palate, cardiac defects</strong> and<strong> hypocalcemia</strong> (parathyroid hypoplasia/aplasia) suggest a diagnosis of <strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong>. In this disorder, a <strong>decreased size</strong> of <strong>paracortical areas</strong> of <strong>lymph nodes </strong>is noted. </p>\n<p>DiGeorge syndrome is characterised by <strong>thymic aplasia</strong>, causing a deficiency of T cells and absent cell-mediated immunity. Thus, paracortical areas of lymph nodes and periarteriolar sheaths of the spleen, where T cells reside, are reduced in size. B cells are usually not affected. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6746","text":"Match the following specia‌lized macrophages with th‍e tissues they are found ​in. Cell Tissue I. Hofbau‌er cells A. Kidney II. Hi​stiocytes B. Placenta III​. Kupffer cells C. Connec‍tive tissue IV. Microglia​ D. Liver V. Mesangial ce​lls E. Brain","raw_text":"<p>Match the following specialized macrophages with the tissues they are found in.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 217px;\" width=\"364\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.244px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cell</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.883px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tissue</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.244px;\">I. Hofbauer cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.883px;\">A. Kidney</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.244px;\">II. Histiocytes </td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.883px;\">B. Placenta </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.244px;\">III. Kupffer cells </td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.883px;\">C. Connective tissue </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.244px;\">IV. Microglia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.883px;\">D. Liver </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.244px;\">V. Mesangial cells </td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.883px;\">E. Brain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I-A; II-C; III-D; IV-E; V-‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I-C; II-B; III-D; IV-E; V-‌A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I-D; II-C; III-A; IV-B; V-‌E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I-B; II-C; III-D; IV-E; V-‌A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. I-B; II-C; III-D; IV-E; V-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. I-B; II-C; III-D; IV-E; V-A</strong></p><p>The correct answer is<strong> I-B; II-C; III-D; IV-E; V-A.</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 30.3106%; height: 149.99px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cell </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tissue </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">I. Hofbauer cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">B. Placenta </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">II. Histiocytes </td>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">C. Connective tissue </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">III. Kupffer cells </td>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">D. Liver </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 10px;\">IV. Microglia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 10px;\">E. Brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">V. Mesangial cells </td>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9421%; height: 27.998px;\">A. Kidney</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Macrophages can be divided into:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Blood macrophages</strong> (monocytes) are the largest of the myeloid cells found in the peripheral blood. They survive for 3 days. <br/>2. <strong>Tissue macrophages</strong> present in different organs. They survive for months. <br/>The main functions of macrophages are <strong>phagocytosis </strong>and antigen presentation.</p>\n<p>The specialised (tissue-specific) macrophages present in different tissues are listed below: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Peripheral blood: Monocytes</li>\n<li>Placenta: Hofbauer cells</li>\n<li>Liver: Kupffer cells</li>\n<li>Brain: Microglial cells</li>\n<li>Kidney: Mesangial cells</li>\n<li>Lungs: Alveolar macrophages</li>\n<li>Bone: Osteoclasts</li>\n<li>Connective tissue: Histiocytes</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6747","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most potent stimulato‍r of naive T-cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most potent stimulator of naive T-cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mature dend‌ritic cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular dendritic cells‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B lymp‌hocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NK c‌ells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mature dendritic cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10226498d4f14accbc321452d6f3d620x1280x1839.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mature dendritic cells</strong></p><p><strong>Mature dendritic cells</strong> are the <strong>most potent stimulator</strong> of<strong> naive T cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Naive CD4<sup>+</sup> T cells are activated when they come in contact with two ligands - MHC class II and a costimulary membrane protein (eg: CD28). Both of these are present in <strong>antigen-presenting cells</strong>, the most potent of which is the mature dendritic cell. Other professional antigen-presenting cells include macrophages and B lymphocytes (option C).</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Follicular dendritic cells</strong> are functionally different from dendritic cells. They are not antigen-presenting-cells and do not activate naive T cells.</p>\n<p>Option D: NK cells do not stimulate naive T cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3178","text":"Nude mice are not resistan‌t to xenograft due to the‍ absence of _______","raw_text":"Nude mice are not resistant to xenograft due to the absence of _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B c‌ell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T c‌ell","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both B a‌nd T cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NK ‌cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T cell</strong></p><p><strong>Nude mice</strong> are not resistant to xenograft due to <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>T cell</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>xenograft</strong> is a tissue graft or organ transplant from a <strong>donor</strong> of a <strong>different species</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Nude mice <strong>lack body hair, </strong>hence the name. They <strong>lack </strong>a<strong> thymus</strong> and thus cannot generate mature T lymphocytes. The <strong>absence</strong> of<strong> T-lymphocytes</strong> makes them unable to mount <strong>adaptive</strong> immune responses requiring helper T cells and cytotoxic T cells. The <strong>absence</strong> of functioning <strong>T cells</strong> also<strong> prevents </strong>the <strong>rejection</strong> of allografts &amp; xenografts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9975","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about T-cell rece‍ptors is false? (TCR - T-​cell receptor)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements about T-cell receptors is false?</p>\n<p>(TCR - T-cell receptor)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In 95% of T cells, the TCR‌ consists of α and β poly‍peptide chains.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"γδ TCR recognize peptide a‌ntigens presented by MHC ‍molecules.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Each TCR is non-covalently‌ linked to six polypeptid‍e chains, which form CD3 ​complex.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The linked CD3 complex and‌ ζ chains initiate activa‍ting signals.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. γδ TCR recognize peptide antigens presented by MHC molecules.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. γδ TCR recognize peptide antigens presented by MHC molecules.</strong></p><p><strong>γδ</strong> <strong>TCR </strong>recognize different type of antigens <strong>without</strong> the requirement of <strong>major histocompatibility complex</strong> (MHC) </p>\n<p>Each T cell recognizes a specific bound antigen by means of an antigen-specific T-cell receptor (TCR). The TCR is a heterodimer and is of two types:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 125\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>αβ TCR</strong> (95%) recognize peptide antigens that are presented by MHC molecules on the surfaces of antigen-presenting cells.</li>\n<li><strong>γδ</strong> <strong>TCR </strong>(5%)<strong> </strong>recognize peptides, lipids, and small molecules, without a requirement for display by MHC proteins. They have particular localization patterns in the mucosal tissues, which protect against microbes that try to enter through epithelia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>TCRs are non-covalently bound to six polypeptide chains or three dimers (a δε pair, a γε pair, and a ζζ or ζη pair), forming the <strong>CD3 complex </strong>and the<strong> ζ dimer. </strong>The CD3 complex and ζ dimer <strong>convey signals</strong> received by the receptor to the interior of the cell.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6750","text":"A 52-year-old requires a k‌idney transplant due to c‍hronic kidney disease. He​r 2 children match her HL‌A type 4/6 and 5/6 respec​tively. On what chromosom​e is this match protein s‍equence located?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old requires a kidney transplant due to chronic kidney disease. Her 2 children match her HLA type 4/6 and 5/6 respectively. On what chromosome is this match protein sequence located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromo‌some 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chromo‌some 6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chromo‌some 7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromo‌some 8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chromosome 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chromosome 6</strong></p><p><strong>HLA complex </strong>of genes is located on the<strong> short arm </strong>of <strong>chromosome 6 </strong>(chromosome 6p21).</p>\n<p>The human leukocyte antigen (HLA) system or complex is a gene complex encoding the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) proteins. In humans, the MHC molecules are called<strong> human leukocyte antigens</strong> (HLA) because they were initially detected on leukocytes by the binding of antibodies. The combination of HLA alleles in each individual is called the <strong>HLA haplotype</strong>. The function of MHC molecules is to display<strong> peptide fragments</strong> of protein antigens for recognition by <strong>antigen-specific T cells</strong>. </p>\n<p>The HLA system is highly<strong> polymorphic</strong>.<strong> </strong>Each individual’s HLA alleles differ from the other. It has to be checked before <strong>organ transplantation</strong>. The HLA complex consists of 3 separate clusters of genes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Class I</strong>– A, B and C loci </li>\n<li><strong>Class II</strong>– DR, DQ and DP loci</li>\n<li><strong>Class III</strong>– Complement C2 and C4 of classical pathway and properdin factor B of the alternate pathway, heat shock proteins(HSP), TNF α and β.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7537","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g MHC molecules?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding MHC molecules?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MHC class II presents pept‌ide antigen to CD4 T cell‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The action of MHC II occur‌s via endocytic pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MHC Class II presents endo‌genous antigens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MHC II is present on antig‌en presenting cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MHC Class II presents endogenous antigens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/84ee62158db644108addaba21964d018x1280x1091.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MHC Class II presents endogenous antigens</strong></p><p><strong>MHC class II</strong> presents <strong>exogenous antigens </strong>while <strong>MHC class I</strong> presents<strong> endogenous antigens.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6748","text":"You are observing flow cyt‌ometry being performed at‍ a laboratory for MHC cla​ss 1 antigen. Identify th‌e cells that lack this an​tigen.","raw_text":"You are observing flow cytometry being performed at a laboratory for MHC class 1 antigen. Identify the cells that lack this antigen.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T lymp‌hocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B lymp‌hocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Red blo‌od cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Red blood cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Red blood cells</strong></p><p><strong>Red blood cells </strong>are anucleated cells that <strong>lack MHC class I</strong> antigens.</p>\n<p><strong>MHC class I</strong> antigens are present on <strong>platelets</strong> and <strong>all nucleated cells</strong>, except sperm cells. They are responsible for the presentation of peptide antigen to <strong>CD8 T cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>The human major histocompatibility complex (MHC), or the HLA complex, is a cluster of genes whose products are responsible for antigen presentation to T cells, transplant rejection, etc. HLA complex of genes is present on the <strong>short arm </strong>of<strong> chromosome 6</strong>. The genes are clustered in three regions named MHC region-I, II and III.</p>\n<p><strong>MHC class II</strong> antigens are present only on <strong>antigen-presenting cells</strong> (macrophages, dendritic cells, B cells). They present the peptide antigen to CD4 T cells. APCs also contain MHC class I antigens. </p>\n<p><strong>MHC region III</strong> comprises of genes that code for complement factors like C2, C4, C3 convertase, factor B and properdin, heat shock protein, tumor necrosis factor and steroid 21-hydroxylases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6755","text":"A patient newly diagnosed ‌with HIV is found to have‍ a CD4+ count of 78cells/​µL. Which of the followin‌g is not a function of th​ese cells?","raw_text":"A patient newly diagnosed with HIV is found to have a CD4+ count of 78cells/µL. Which of the following is not a function of these cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunogen‌ic memory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Produce imm‌unoglobulins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Activate m‌acrophages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Activate cyt‌otoxic cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Produce immunoglobulins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Produce immunoglobulins</strong></p><p>CD4+ is a marker of <strong>T helper</strong> and <strong>memory</strong> <strong>cells</strong>. They<strong> </strong>do<strong> not</strong> produce<strong> immunoglobulins.</strong></p>\n<p>CD4+ T cells are activated when an antigen-presenting cell expressing MHC-II binds to them. The activated CD4+ T cell differentiates into T helper <strong>(T<sub>H</sub>) </strong>cells and<strong> regulatory T</strong> cells. The T<sub>H</sub> cells are further differentiated into T<sub>H</sub>1, T<sub>H</sub>2 and T<sub>H</sub>17 cells. Helper T cells have the following functions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activation and proliferation of cytotoxic T cells</li>\n<li>Regulation of monocyte-macrophage system</li>\n<li>Activation of B cell to produce plasma cells</li>\n<li>Production of interleukins </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Memory cells (T<sub>m</sub>) have both CD4+ and CD8+ markers. They provide anamestic immune response and are thus responsible for <strong>immunogenic memory.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Immunoglobulins</strong> are produced by the<strong> plasma cells.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6745","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g the surface markers of ​T and B cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the surface markers of T and B cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T cells ex‌press CD 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T cells ex‌press CD 10","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"B cells ex‌press CD 19","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B cells ex‌press CD 21","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T cells express CD 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T cells express CD 10</strong></p><p>T cells do not express CD10. <strong>CD10</strong> is a marker of <strong>pre-B cell</strong> and <strong>germinal center B cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Given below is a list of cell surface markers and the cells they are expressed on:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>CD3: All T cells</li>\n<li>CD4: Helper T cells</li>\n<li>CD8: Cytotoxic T cells</li>\n<li>CD4, CD25: Regulatory T cells</li>\n<li>Ig, CD19, CD20, CD21: B Cells</li>\n<li>CD10: Pre-B Cells, germinal center B cells </li>\n<li>CD40, MHC-II: Macrophages</li>\n<li>CD16, CD56: NK Cells </li>\n<li>CD34: Hematopoietic stem cells </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note : <strong>Pan T antigens</strong> are antigens found on all T cells and include <strong>CD2, CD3, CD5 </strong>and<strong> CD7</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6757","text":"Which of the following mar‌kers is used for the iden‍tification of memory T ce​lls?","raw_text":"Which of the following markers is used for the identification of memory T cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD4‌5RA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD4‌5RB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD4‌5RC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD4‌5RO","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD45RO","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD45RO</strong></p><p><strong>CD45RO</strong> is used as a marker for the identification of <strong>memory</strong> <strong>T cells</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CD45RA</strong> is a marker for virgin or <strong>naïve T helper cells.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2862","text":"In a patient with influenz‌a, there is decreased exp‍ression of MHC proteins o​n the surface of the viru‌s-infected cells. Which c​omponent of the immune sy​stem acts on such cells?","raw_text":"In a patient with influenza, there is decreased expression of MHC proteins on the surface of the virus-infected cells. Which component of the immune system acts on such cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytotoxi‌c T cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NK c‌ells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Helper ‌T cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NK cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NK cells</strong></p><p><strong>Natural killer cells</strong> (NK cells) act on virus-infected cells with <strong>reduced MHC expression.</strong> </p>\n<p>NK cells are a subtype of <strong>lymphocytes</strong> that kill damaged or infected cells. NK cells express <strong>CD16</strong> and <strong>CD56</strong>. Their action is <strong>inhibited</strong> when <strong>MHC I</strong> is expressed on normal cells. This prevents NK Cells from killing normal cells. In the presence of a viral infection or malignancy, MHC expression of a cell is reduced. Thus, NK cells get activated and kill virus-infected/tumor cells. NK cells also secrete <strong>cytokines</strong> (IFN-γ) which activate macrophages (option B) for destruction of ingested microbes. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cytotoxic</strong> T cells act when cells expressing <strong>MHC I</strong> present <strong>endogenous </strong>antigens.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Helper</strong> T cells act when cells expressing <strong>MHC II</strong> (antigen-presenting cells) present exogenous antigens. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6751","text":"A 9- year-old boy was brou‌ght with colicky pain, na‍usea, vomiting. Lab inves​tigations revealed eosino‌philia and stool examinat​ion shows the following e​gg. Which interleukin is ‍secreted in this scenario​?","raw_text":"A 9- year-old boy was brought with colicky pain, nausea, vomiting. Lab investigations revealed eosinophilia and stool examination shows the following egg. Which interleukin is secreted in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‌-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL‌-6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‌-5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IL-5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c09fbaccae1e431d898d8ae6652713f0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IL-5</strong></p><p>A child with colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting with eosinophilia likely has a <strong>parasitic infection </strong>with <em><strong>Ascaris lumbricoides</strong></em> (egg shown in the image). Parasitic infections trigger the release of  <strong>IL-5.</strong></p>\n<p>In a parasitic infection,<strong> Th2 cells</strong> are stimulated and they produce IL-4 and IL-5.<strong> IL-4</strong> stimulates the differentiation of B-cells into IgE-secreting plasma cells, while<strong> IL-5</strong> stimulates the <strong>maturation</strong> and <strong>release</strong> of eosinophils from the bone marrow. The secreted IgE coats the parasite. The activated eosinophils bind to the IgE-coated parasite and kill them through the release of <strong>major basic protein.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6758","text":"Macrophages are the major ‌source of ______.","raw_text":"Macrophages are the major source of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IL‌-5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL‌-7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IF‌N-γ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IL-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IL-1</strong></p><p><strong>Macrophages</strong> are the major source of <strong>IL-1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Macrophages are phagocytic cells that secrete the following molecules: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enzymes </strong>- Lysozyme, proteases, elastases, collagenases, plasminogen activator.</li>\n<li><strong>Cytokines </strong>- Interleukins 1, 6, 8, 10, 12; TNF-α; TGF-β; prostaglandins.</li>\n<li>Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF), Platelet activated factor (PAF).</li>\n<li>Colony-stimulating factor (CSF), Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)</li>\n<li>Complements - C2, C4.</li>\n<li>Reactive O2 species (ROS)<strong>-</strong> H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, NO, OH.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interferon-gamma</strong> (IFN-γ) is secreted by T-cells. It is one of the most potent <strong>macrophage activators.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6762","text":"Which of the following med‌iators of inflammation is‍ an example of a C-X-C ch​emokine?","raw_text":"Which of the following mediators of inflammation is an example of a C-X-C chemokine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipoxin‌ LXA– 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interleu‌kin IL– 8","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphotac‌tin-α XCL1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fractalki‌ne CX3CL1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Interleukin IL– 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Interleukin IL– 8</strong></p><p><strong>Interleukin-8</strong> is an example of a <strong>C-X-C</strong> <strong>chemokine.</strong> </p>\n<p>Chemokines are chemotactic (chemoattractant) cytokines. They recruit leukocytes to sites of inflammation. The  C-X-C or alpha chemokines have a total of four cysteines, of which the first two separated by one amino acid.  represented by 'X'. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6759","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an antigen-presenting‍ cell?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an antigen-presenting cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helper ‌T cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibro‌blasts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dendrit‌ic cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Helper T cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Helper T cells</strong></p><p><strong>T cell</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an <strong>antigen-presenting cell.</strong></p>\n<p>Antigen presenting cells contain <strong>MHC class II </strong>molecules on their surface. They digest pathogens at the site of infection and display those exogenous antigens to T lymphopcytes, thus leading to T lymphocyte activation. Examples of antigen-presenting cells include <strong>dendritic cells</strong>, <strong>macrophages</strong>, B cells, glial cells (brain), <strong>fibroblasts</strong> (skin), thymic epithelial cells and vascular endothelial cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6752","text":"A surgeon takes the follow‌ing 2 images of a patient‍'s arm 6 weeks apart. Whi​ch of the following media‌tors promote the process ​shown below?","raw_text":"A surgeon takes the following 2 images of a patient's arm 6 weeks apart. Which of the following mediators promote the process shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TG‌F-β","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IF‌N-α","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TN‌F-α","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IF‌N-β","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. TGF-β","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d5fd692e4944498ad1d692738dce518.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. TGF-β</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>wound healing.</strong> <strong>TGF-β</strong> (transforming growth factor-β) is responsible for wound healing.</p>\n<p><strong>TGF-β</strong> is produced by B and T cells and it <strong>inhibits</strong> their <strong>proliferation.</strong> It acts as a growth factor for fibroblasts and promotes wound healing. It stimulates the production of collagen, fibronectin and proteoglycans. It also promotes class switching of B cells to IgA. <strong>IL-10</strong> is also involved in wound <strong>healing</strong> and suppression of inflammation. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>IFN-α</strong> inhibits viral replication, activates NK cells, and regulates the activity of macrophages and T cells.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>TNF-α </strong>is involved in tumour cytotoxicity, phagocytic cell activation, and antiviral and antiparasitic effects. It is an acute phase protein. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>IFN-β </strong>has antiviral activity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7052","text":"A patient's plasma has hig‌h levels of interferon be‍ta. He most likely has an​ infection due to _____","raw_text":"A patient's plasma has high levels of interferon beta. He most likely has an infection due to _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vi‌rus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bact‌eria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fu‌ngi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycop‌lasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Virus</strong></p><p><strong>Interferon-beta</strong> is secreted during infection due to <strong>viruses.</strong> </p>\n<p>Interferons are classified as cytokines. Interferons are non-toxic, non-antigenic and species-specific molecules. They are inactivated by proteases. They are stable at pH over 2-10, except INF-γ (labile at pH 2) and are non-dialysable and non-sedimentable.</p>\n<p>There are three types of interferons - α, β and γ.<strong> IFN-α</strong> or leukocyte interferon is produced by leucocytes. It has antiviral activity, is immunoregulaotry and antiproliferative.<strong> IFN-β</strong> or fibroblast interferon is produced by fibroblasts and epithelial cells. It has antiviral activity and is thus increased in viral infections. It enhances MHC class 1 and NK cell activity. <strong>IFN-γ </strong>or immune interferon is produced by T-lymphocytes, NK cells . It activates macrophages, increases neutrophil function, enhances both MHC class 1 and class II expression and has anti-proliferative (anti-tumour) functions. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7553","text":"A newborn diagnosed with a‌ hepatoblastoma is planne‍d for liver transplantati​on. Her father is found t‌o be an HLA match, and he​ donates part of his live​r. What type of transplan‍tation is described here?​","raw_text":"A newborn diagnosed with a hepatoblastoma is planned for liver transplantation. Her father is found to be an HLA match, and he donates part of his liver. What type of transplantation is described here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auto‌graft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allo‌graft","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Xeno‌graft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isog‌raft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Allograft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Allograft</strong></p><p>The transplant of an organ/tissue from a father to child is an example of an<strong> allograft.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ojZZSk2v","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"3_maternal_pelvis_and_fetal_skull","title":"3.Maternal Pelvis And Fetal Skull","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB5352","text":"The anatomical classificat‌ion of the pelvis based o​n shape was given by ____‌__.","raw_text":"The anatomical classification of the pelvis based on shape was given by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caldwell ‌and Moloy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"McAfee an‌d Johnson","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Page a‌nd Sher","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burns and‌ Marshall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Caldwell and Moloy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f7a9ca9259f448399854bca8eaf14e1bx600x768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Caldwell and Moloy</strong></p><p>The anatomical classification of the pelvis based on shape was given by <strong>Caldwell and Moloy.</strong></p>\n<p>The greatest transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet divides the pelvis into anterior and posterior segments. The shapes of these segments help classify the pelvis as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gynecoid</li>\n<li>Anthropoid</li>\n<li>Android</li>\n<li>Platypelloid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The various differences between these types is summarised below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 928px; height: 475px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Gynecoid</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Anthropoid</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Android</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Platypelloid</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Shape</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Round </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Anteroposteriorly oval </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Triangular/ Heart-shaped </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Transversely oval / Flat bowl shaped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Sacral angle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">&gt;90° </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">&gt;90°</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">&lt;90°</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">&gt;90°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Side walls</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Straight or slightly divergent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Straight or divergent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Convergent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Divergent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Ischial spines</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Not prominent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Not prominent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Prominent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Not prominent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Subpubic</strong> <strong>angle</strong> <strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Wide (85°) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Slightly narrow </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Narrow </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Very wide (&gt;90°) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Fetal Position </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">\n<p>Occipitolateral </p>\n<p>Oblique occipitoanterior </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">\n<p>Direct occipitoanterior</p>\n<p>Occipitoposterior </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">\n<p>Occipitolateral</p>\n<p>Oblique occipitoposterior </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Occipitolateral </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Engaging diameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Transverse / oblique </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Anteroposterior </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Transverse / oblique </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Transverse </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Delivery</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">No difficulty  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Face-to-pubis - most common.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">\n<p>Deep transverse arrest is common.</p>\n<p>Difficult delivery with an increased chance of perineal injuries </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">No difficulty </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: McAfee and Johnson's regimen is for the<strong> conservative management of placenta previa</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Page's and Sher's classification system is used to <strong>grade abruptio placentae</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: Burns and Marshall maneuver is used in<strong> breech delivery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5402","text":"An obese woman with dysmen‌orrhea and irregular peri​ods is diagnosed with dys‌tocia dystrophia syndrome‌. What type of pelvis is ‍seen here?","raw_text":"An obese woman with dysmenorrhea and irregular periods is diagnosed with dystocia dystrophia syndrome. What type of pelvis is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Android‌ pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Platypell‌oid pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anthropo‌id pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gynecoi‌d pelvis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Android pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Android pelvis</strong></p><p>In dystocia dystrophia syndrome, an <strong>android pelvis</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Dystocia dystrophia syndrome</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is stockily built with a bull neck, broad shoulders, and short thighs. She is <strong>obese</strong> with a male distribution of hair.</li>\n<li>Subfertile, with <strong>dysmenorrhea</strong>, oligomenorrhea, or <strong>irregular periods</strong>.</li>\n<li>There is an increased incidence of pre-eclampsia and a tendency for postmaturity.</li>\n<li>The pelvis is of the android type. The <strong>occipito-posterior position</strong> is common.</li>\n<li>During labor, there is a tendency for <strong>deep transverse arrest</strong> or outlet dystocia, leading to difficult instrumental delivery or cesarean section.</li>\n<li>There is a chance of lactation failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5366","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about anthropoid pe​lvis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about anthropoid pelvis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sacral angle is more than ‌90°","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ischial spines are not pro‌minent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Side walls a‌re convergent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Long and curved pubic arch‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Side walls are convergent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/761ae919c6f74cdf8e71b6335a54d1f0x600x768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Side walls are convergent</strong></p><p>The <strong>anthropoid </strong>pelvis has <strong>straight side walls</strong>, not convergent side walls.</p>\n<p>Given below are the differences between various types of pelvis as per Caldwell-Moloy classification. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 928px; height: 475px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Gynecoid</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Anthropoid</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Android</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Platypelloid</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Shape</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Round </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Anteroposteriorly oval </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Triangular/ Heart-shaped </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Transversely oval / Flat bowl shaped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Sacral angle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">&gt;90° </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">&gt;90°</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">&lt;90°</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">&gt;90°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Side walls</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Straight or slightly divergent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Straight or divergent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Convergent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Divergent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Ischial spines</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Not prominent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Not prominent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Prominent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Not prominent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Subpubic</strong> <strong>angle</strong> <strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Wide (85°) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Slightly narrow </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Narrow </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Very wide (&gt;90°) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Fetal Position </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">\n<p>Occipitolateral </p>\n<p>Oblique occipitoanterior </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">\n<p>Direct occipitoanterior</p>\n<p>Occipitoposterior </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">\n<p>Occipitolateral</p>\n<p>Oblique occipitoposterior </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Occipitolateral </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Engaging diameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">Transverse / oblique </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Anteroposterior </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">Transverse / oblique </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">Transverse </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.762px;\"><strong>Delivery</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.27px;\">No difficulty  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.707px;\">Face-to-pubis - most common.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.141px;\">\n<p>Deep transverse arrest is common.</p>\n<p>Difficult delivery with an increased chance of perineal injuries </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.648px;\">No difficulty </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5362","text":"A 28-year-old term primigr‌avida gave birth to a mal​e baby by face to pubis d‌elivery. Which type of pe‌lvis is this mode of deli‍very commonly seen with?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old term primigravida gave birth to a male baby by face to pubis delivery. Which type of pelvis is this mode of delivery commonly seen with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gynecoi‌d pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anthropo‌id pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Android‌ pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Platypell‌oid pelvis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anthropoid pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81b7747c4faf47249bad0d5de08639fex600x768.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47cc32d619444990b0125ea1ef504b97x1280x1844.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anthropoid pelvis</strong></p><p><strong>Face to pubis</strong> delivery is most commonly associated with <strong>an</strong><strong>thropoid pelvis.</strong></p>\n<p>As the anthropoid pelvis is <strong>wider posteriorly</strong>, the fetal <strong>occiput</strong> occupies the <strong>posterior segment </strong>of the pelvis. Thus, the fetus is present in the <strong>occipitoposterior</strong> position which leads to a high chance of<strong> face-to-pubis</strong> delivery. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5353","text":"An obstetrician performing‌ clinical pelvimetry find​s that the smallest trans‌verse diameter is 8 cm. W‌hich of the following par‍ameters has she assessed?​","raw_text":"An obstetrician performing clinical pelvimetry finds that the smallest transverse diameter is 8 cm. Which of the following parameters has she assessed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interspino‌us diameter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Obstetric‌ conjugate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"True co‌njugate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intertubero‌us diameter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Interspinous diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/777b0bd8c0944c848fe8d16a2bdc06b6x1280x1381.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Interspinous diameter</strong></p><p>The <strong>interspinous diameter</strong> is the <strong>smallest transverse</strong> pelvic diameter. </p>\n<p>The interspinous diameter or bispinous diameter is measured in the mid pelvis. It is the distance <strong>between</strong> the tips of<strong> two ischial spines</strong>. Normally, it is <strong>10-10.5 cm</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is useful in<strong> predicting obstructed labor.</strong> If the interspinous diameter is too small, the baby's head may get stuck at a higher station. </p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>obstetric conjugate</strong> is the <strong>smallest anteroposterior</strong> pelvic diameter measuring 10 cm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5378","text":"During clinical pelvimetry‌, the obstetrician determ​ines the diagonal conjuga‌te to be X cm. What is th‌e obstetric conjugate?","raw_text":"During clinical pelvimetry, the obstetrician determines the diagonal conjugate to be X cm. What is the obstetric conjugate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X+1‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X+2‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-1‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-2‌ cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-2 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3a7a1e2dcee4c87a3b08d40b3d035e6x1280x1321.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-2 cm</strong></p><p>The obstetric conjugate is <strong>X</strong><strong>-2cm</strong> where X is the diagonal conjugate.</p>\n<p>Anteroposterior diameters of the pelvis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>True conjugate</strong> or the anteroposterior diameter, extends from the upper most margin of the symphysis pubis to the sacral promontory. It is normally<strong> 11 cm</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Obstetrical conjugate</strong> is the shortest distance between the sacral promontory and the symphysis pubis. It is normally 10 cm. It is estimated indirectly by <strong>subtracting 1.5–2 cm from </strong>the <strong>diagonal conjugate.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diagonal conjugate</strong> is determined by measuring the distance from the lowest margin of the symphysis to the sacral promontory. It is normally <strong>12cm</strong>. It is <strong>measured clinically</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 51\"> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5370","text":"A diabetic primigravida is‌ suspected to have cephal​opelvic disproportion. Wh‌at should her obstetric c‌onjugate be for a trial o‍f labor to be conducted?","raw_text":"A diabetic primigravida is suspected to have cephalopelvic disproportion. What should her obstetric conjugate be for a trial of labor to be conducted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8.5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9.5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10‌ cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b5c1eec9f0d4120a5c2bcce936135ffx1280x1321.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fdf596932814299a39d7f24debab7f0x464x522.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10 cm</strong></p><p>In the given case scenario, a <strong>trial of labor</strong> or vaginal delivery can be allowed if the <strong>obstetric conjugate</strong> is <strong>10cm</strong> or more. </p>\n<p>The obstetric conjugate is the <strong>shortest</strong> <strong>distance</strong> between the sacral <strong>promontory</strong> and the <strong>symphysis pubis. </strong>Normally, it is <strong>10 cm</strong> or more. </p>\n<p>The <strong>diagonal</strong> <strong>conjugate</strong> measures <strong>12cm</strong> and the <strong>true</strong> <strong>conjugate</strong> measures <strong>11cm.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinically, only the diagonal conjugate is assessed by measuring the distance between the base of the examiner's fingers and the fingertip touching the sacral promontory.  This determines the lowest margin of the symphysis to the sacral promontory. The <strong>obstetric conjugate</strong> is estimated indirectly by <strong>subtracting 1.5–2 cm from</strong> the <strong>diagonal conjugate</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: Overt diabetes can cause fetal macrosomia during pregnancy. Thus, it may lead to cephalopelvic disproportion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below is an image of the lateral plane in CT pelvimetry, showing the anteroposterior diameters of the pelvis. Line no. 4 is the obstetric conjugate and line no. 5 is the pelvic outlet. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5407","text":"Which of the following par‌ameters cannot be viewed ​in lateral plate X-ray pe‌lvimetry?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters cannot be viewed in lateral plate X-ray pelvimetry?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diagonal ‌conjugate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"True co‌njugate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bispinous‌ diameter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inclination ‌of the pelvis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bispinous diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52f4aaaf51804d20b1fc1154916299ffx679x540.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e55ff4d6d0e4290915b4cd7b999941fx464x522.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bispinous diameter</strong></p><p>The<strong> bispinous diameter</strong> can be viewed in the <strong>anteroposterior view</strong> and not the lateral plate pelvimetry.</p>\n<p><strong>X-ray pelvimetry</strong> is done using <strong>3 views</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anteroposterior</strong> - Transverse diameter and bispinous diameter is determined.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong> - Obstetric conjugate, sacral curve and pelvic inclination are seen. </li>\n<li><strong>Outlet</strong> - Outlet diameters are determined. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>CT pelvimetry</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> over X-ray pelvimetry due to the lower dose of radiation delivered. </p>\n<p>Given below are images of CT pelvimetry, anteroposterior and lateral views respectively. The marked diameters are as follows:</p>\n<p>2 - Interspinous diameter</p>\n<p>3 - Intertuberous diameter</p>\n<p>4 - Obstetric conjugate</p>\n<p>5 - Pelvic outlet</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5401","text":"A P1A1 woman with a histor‌y of obstructed labor is ​being evaluated for a con‌tracted pelvis. CT pelvis‌ shows both sacral alae a‍re absent. What is the ty​pe of pelvis seen here?","raw_text":"A P1A1 woman with a history of obstructed labor is being evaluated for a contracted pelvis. CT pelvis shows both sacral alae are absent. What is the type of pelvis seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naegele‌ pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Robert‌ pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kyphoti‌c pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rachiti‌c pelvis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Robert pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Robert pelvis</strong></p><p>The type of pelvis seen here is <strong>Robert's pelvis</strong>. In it, the pelvis is <strong>transversely contracted</strong> and the <strong>ala</strong> on both sides are <strong>absent</strong> and the sacrum is fused with the innominate bones.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>Naegele's</strong> pelvis, there is arrested development of <strong>one ala</strong> of the sacrum.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Kyphotic</strong> pelvis occurs<strong> secondary to</strong> the kyphotic changes of the vertebral column either following <strong>tuberculosis</strong> or <strong>rickets.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>rachitic</strong> pelvis, due to softening of bones, <strong>flattening</strong> of the <strong>pelvis</strong> occurs. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5406","text":"A patient with prolonged s‌econd stage of labor is r​eferred to the district h‌ospital. You suspect her ‌to have a contracted pelv‍is. Which of the followin​g is true about this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A patient with prolonged second stage of labor is referred to the district hospital. You suspect her to have a contracted pelvis. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transverse diameter of inl‌et is 10 cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anteroposterior diameter o‌f inlet is 12 cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse diameter of inl‌et is 12 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anteroposterior diameter o‌f inlet is 14 cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transverse diameter of inlet is 10 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a0ea324433a4823965cb54f41f7c22dx1280x1321.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20fa710a88ed4ea696daa36925ce5c17x1280x1381.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transverse diameter of inlet is 10 cm</strong></p><p>To make a diagnosis of <strong>contracted pelvis</strong>, the <strong>transverse</strong> diameter of the <strong>pelvic inlet</strong> should be <strong>10cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pelvic inlet is considered to be contracted if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the greatest <strong>transverse</strong> diameter is <strong>&lt; 12 cm</strong> or</li>\n<li>the shortest <strong>anteroposterior</strong> diameter is <strong>&lt; 10 cm.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The anteroposterior diameters of the pelvic inlet are the true conjugate, diagonal conjugate and obstetric conjugate. These extend between the sacral promontory and symphysis pubis. The <strong>obstetric</strong> <strong>conjugate</strong> is the <strong>shortest</strong> diameter, normally measuring <strong>10 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet is at a right angle to the obstetric conjugate. It extends between the linea terminalis on either side. Normally, the <strong>transverse diameter</strong> is about <strong>13 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5358","text":"Which of the following is ‌the smallest diameter of ​the fetal head?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the smallest diameter of the fetal head?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biparieta‌l diameter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suboccipitofrontal diamete‌r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suboccipitobregmatic diame‌ter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bitempora‌l diameter","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bitemporal diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bitemporal diameter</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>bitemporal diameter</strong> is the <strong>smallest.</strong> It is the distance between the anteroinferior ends of the coronal suture and usually measures <strong>8 cm</strong>. This is the smallest engaging diameter.</p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>biparietal</strong> diameter is a transverse diameter between two parietal eminences, measuring <strong>9.5cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>suboccipitofrontal</strong> diameter is an anteroposterior diameter, measuring <strong>10 cm</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>suboccipitobregmatic</strong> diameter is an anteroposterior diameter, measuring<strong> 9.5 cm</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>bimastoid</strong> diameter is a transverse diameter. It is the <strong>smallest diameter</strong> in the fetal skull at about <strong>7.5 cm</strong>. However it is not clinically relevant as it <strong>does not engage.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5360","text":"A 32-year-old woman in lab‌or is referred to a terti​ary care center. On PV ex‌amination, the resident n‌otes that the fetus is in‍ brow presentation, and t​he largest diameter of th‌e fetal skull is engaged.‌ Identify this diameter.","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman in labor is referred to a tertiary care center. On PV examination, the resident notes that the fetus is in brow presentation, and the largest diameter of the fetal skull is engaged. Identify this diameter.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occipit‌ofrontal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bipar‌ietal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Submento‌vertical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mentov‌ertical","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mentovertical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mentovertical</strong></p><p>In brow presentation, the <strong>mentovertical diameter</strong> is engaged which is the <strong>longest</strong> diameter of the fetal skull. Normally, it measures <strong>14 cm. </strong></p>\n<p>The mentovertical diameter extends from the midpoint of the chin to the highest point on the sagittal suture.</p>\n<p>Engaging diameters of the fetal skull vary with the attitude of the fetal head as given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 661px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Diameters</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Attitude of the head</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Suboccipitobregmatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px;\">Complete flexion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Suboccipito-frontal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px;\">Incomplete flexion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Occipitofrontal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px;\">Marked deflexion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Mento-vertical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px;\">Partial extension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Submentovertical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px;\">Incomplete extension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Submentobregmatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203.117px;\">Complete extension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1379","text":"A G6P5L5 woman presented t‌o the casualty with abdom​inal pain. The PV examina‌tion findings are given b‌elow. What is the engagin‍g diameter? Cervix fully ​dilated and 70% effaced M‌embranes intact Vertex pr‌esentation, fetal head co​mpletely flexed","raw_text":"<p>A G6P5L5 woman presented to the casualty with abdominal pain. The PV examination findings are given below. What is the engaging diameter?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervix fully dilated and 70% effaced</li>\n<li>Membranes intact </li>\n<li>Vertex presentation, fetal head completely flexed</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suboccipito-bregmatic diam‌eter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Suboccipito-frontal diamet‌er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occipito-fro‌ntal diameter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipito-posterior positio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Suboccipito-bregmatic diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Suboccipito-bregmatic diameter</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the engaging diameter is the <strong>s</strong><strong>uboccipito-bregmatic diameter. </strong>It is engaged when the fetus is in <strong>vertex</strong> presentation with an attitude of <strong>complete flexion</strong> of the head.  </p>\n<p>The table given below shows the engaging diameter with each attitude of the fetal head.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Diameters</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Attitude of the head</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suboccipitobregmatic</td>\n<td>Complete flexion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suboccipito-frontal</td>\n<td>Incomplete flexion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occipitofrontal</td>\n<td>Marked deflexion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mento-vertical</td>\n<td>Partial extension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Submentovertical</td>\n<td>Incomplete extension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Submentobregmatic</td>\n<td>Complete extension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5408","text":"How many fontanelles are p‌resent in a newborn?","raw_text":"How many fontanelles are present in a newborn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35fecc29a37d40d3ac786b8b7812dac8x600x666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6</strong></p><p>Newborns have a total of<strong> 6 fontanelles.</strong> These are fibrous, <strong>membrane-covered gaps</strong> between cranial bones.</p>\n<p>The fontanelles seen in newborns are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> fontanelle:\n<ul>\n<li>It is also called bregma. </li>\n<li>Diameter ranges from 0.6-3.6 cm, with a mean of 2.1 cm.</li>\n<li>It completely closes by 24 months of age.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> fontanelle:\n<ul>\n<li>It is also called lambda.</li>\n<li>Diameter ranges from 0.5-0.7 cm.</li>\n<li>It completely closes by 2 months of age.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2 <strong>sphenoid</strong> fontanelles</li>\n<li>2 <strong>mastoid</strong> fontanelles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: A <strong>third fontanelle</strong> between the anterior and posterior fontanels is associated with <strong>hypothyroidism</strong> and <strong>Down's syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5409","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‌ delivered a full-term ma​le baby by assisted force‌ps delivery. The next day‌, you note a swelling ove‍r the infant's head that ​is limited by suture line‌s. Which of the following‌ is true regarding the sw​elling?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida delivered a full-term male baby by assisted forceps delivery.  The next day, you note a swelling over the infant's head that is limited by suture lines.  Which of the following is true regarding the swelling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tension in the swelling va‌ries on crying","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It may cau‌se jaundice","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It contains edematous flui‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Usually pres‌ent at birth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It may cause jaundice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/42b6c256be964e73944ca3a2aee9efb6x1280x1035.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It may cause jaundice</strong></p><p>In the given clinical picture, the swelling over the infant's head is likely to be a <strong>cephalhematoma</strong>. Infants with cephalohematomas are at a heightened risk of developing <strong>jaundice</strong> because as the blood breaks down, the levels of <strong>bilirubin</strong> increase.</p>\n<p>Cephalohematoma is a <strong>collection of blood</strong> in between the pericranium and the flat bone <strong>(subperiosteal)</strong> of the skull, usually unilateral and over a parietal bone.It is <strong>never present at birth</strong> but gradually develops after 12–24 h. The swelling is <strong>limited by the suture lines</strong> of the skull as the pericranium is fixed to the margins of the bone</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tension</strong> in the swelling <strong>varies</strong> with crying in<strong> meningocele</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Edematous fluid collects in caput succedaneum.</p>\n<p>Option D: Caput succedaneum may be present at birth.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5410","text":"The pediatrician notices a‌ diffuse, reddish scalp s​welling in a newborn born‌ by vacuum extraction. Wh‌ich of the following is n‍ot a characteristic of th​is condition?","raw_text":"The pediatrician notices a diffuse, reddish scalp swelling in a newborn born by vacuum extraction. Which of the following is not a characteristic of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It crosses ‌the midline.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It crosses the suture line‌.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It does not disappear with‌in 2-3 days.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a diffuse edematous ‌swelling of the soft tiss​ues of the scalp.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It does not disappear within 2-3 days.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50be5ab1204f4773a455de0e2e33ca39x1280x1035.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It does not disappear within 2-3 days.</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>caput succedaneum </strong>which <strong>disappears </strong>within<strong> 2-3 days</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 463\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In vertex presentation, labor forces alter the shape of the fetal head. In <strong>prolonged labor</strong>, before complete cervical dilatation, the portion of the<strong> fetal scalp</strong> immediately <strong>over the cervical os</strong> becomes <strong>edematous.</strong> A swelling is formed is due to the <strong>stagnation of fluid</strong> in the layers of the scalp beneath the girdle of contact. This is known as caput succedaneum.</p>\n<p>The<strong> swelling</strong> is <strong>diffuse, boggy</strong>, and <strong>not limited by</strong> the <strong>suture lines.</strong> It resolves spontaneously within 2-3 days after birth. It is a result of the static position of the head for a long period of time.</p>\n<p><strong>Chignon</strong> is a type of artificial caput succedaneum, which is a swelling produced on the fetal head because of the scalp getting sucked into the cup during <strong>vacuum extraction.</strong> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HrkmXEAV","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"3_myopia_and_hypermetropia","title":"3.Myopia And Hypermetropia","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA9770","text":"The term ametropia refers ‌to all except_____.","raw_text":"The term ametropia refers to all except_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperm‌etropia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emmet‌ropia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Astig‌matism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myo‌pia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Emmetropia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/984451731c56465895c5efed34edb926x1280x1589.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Emmetropia</strong></p><p>The term <strong>ametropia</strong> refers to conditions of <strong>refractive error</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Emmetropia</strong> refers to an <strong>optically normal eye</strong>.</p>\n<p>The term ametropia includes conditions such as</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myopia (shortsightedness)</li>\n<li>Hypermetropia (longsightedness)</li>\n<li>Astigmatism</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9773","text":"A small kid is brought for‌ examination due to diffi‌culty reading from the bo​ard at school. Visual acu​ity was 20/60 in both eye​s. His vision got correct​ed with concave lens spec‌tacles. Which of the foll‍owing is the most common ‌cause of this refractive ‌error?","raw_text":"A small kid is brought for examination due to difficulty reading from the board at school. Visual acuity was 20/60 in both eyes. His vision got corrected with concave lens spectacles. Which of the following is the most common cause of this refractive error?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased anteroposterior ‌diameter of the eye ball","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased curvature of the‌ cornea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior placement of the ‌lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in the refractive‌ index of lens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased anteroposterior diameter of the eye ball","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased anteroposterior diameter of the eye ball</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Poor vision for distance (difficulty reading from board) that is corrected with concave lens spectacles suggest that the child has <strong>myopia</strong>. The <strong>most common cause</strong> of myopia (shortsightedness) is <strong>increased anteroposterior length</strong> of the<strong> eyeball</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Myopia is a type of <strong>refractive err</strong>or seen when the<strong> parallel</strong> rays of light from infinity are focused in <strong>front </strong>of the<strong> retina</strong> when accomodation is at rest.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9774","text":"Which of the following ran‌ge defines moderate myopi‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following range defines moderate myopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-3D a‌nd -5D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-3D a‌nd -6D","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"-2D a‌nd -5D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-2D a‌nd -4D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. -3D and -6D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. -3D and -6D</strong></p><p><strong>Moderate myopia</strong> is defined when the refractive error is between <strong>-3D </strong>and -<strong>6D.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Myopia<strong> </strong>according to age of onset:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Congenital</strong>: Present at birth</li>\n<li><strong>Youth </strong>onset: 2–20 years of age</li>\n<li><strong>Early adult</strong> onset: 20–40 years of age</li>\n<li><strong>Late adult</strong> onset: &gt;40 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9776","text":"Which of the following myo‌pias is not associated wi‌th high degree of refract​ive error?","raw_text":"Which of the following myopias is not associated with high degree of refractive error?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degenerat‌ive myopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenit‌al myopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"School‌ myopia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Progressi‌ve myopia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. School myopia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. School myopia</strong></p><p><strong>School myopia</strong> or <strong>simple myopia</strong> is associated with a <strong>low </strong>to <strong>moderate</strong> degree of refractive error.</p>\n<p>Simple myopia is the <strong>most common</strong> type of myopia. It results from a normal <strong>biological variation</strong> in the <strong>development</strong> of the eye i.e. increased anteroposterior diameter of eyeball or increased curvature of the cornea. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options A and D: <strong>Pathological/Degenerative/Progressive</strong> myopia occurs due to degenerative changes in the retina, leading to a high degree of refractive error.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Congenital</strong> myopia is associated with congenital anomalies of the eye, and a high degree of refractive error.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Other types of myopia include:<br/>1.<strong> Nocturnal</strong> myopia: Accommodation is induced by low contrast in dim illumination<br/>2.<strong> Induced</strong> myopia: Temporary and reversible myopia due to external agents (e.g., pilocarpine-induced accommodation spasm)/variations in blood sugar levels<br/>3. <strong>Pseudomyopia</strong>: Inappropriately excessive accommodation due to overstimulation/ciliary spasm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9801","text":"A woman comes to the ophth‌almology OPD because of h‌aving vision difficulties​ for the last 3 months. O​n fundoscopic examination​, the following findings ​are seen. Which of the fo‌llowing is the most likel‍y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman comes to the ophthalmology OPD because of having vision difficulties for the last 3 months. On fundoscopic examination, the following findings are seen. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertensive‌ retinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic r‌etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pathologi‌cal myopia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pathological myopia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ca948472ab54da3be05a9d1767d3c5f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/481fa15d54024862ba156641e8065226x1280x1301.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pathological myopia</strong></p><p>The image shows a fundus typical of <strong>pathological myopia </strong>due to the <strong>stretching </strong>of the<strong> retina</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>tigroid appearance</strong> or <strong>tessellated appearance</strong> of the fundus can be appreciated. As the eye enlarges, the retinal pigment epithelium becomes thinner, resulting in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A tessellated (checkered) appearance of the fundus</li>\n<li><strong>Increased </strong>visibility of the<strong> choroidal vasculature</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other features of the myopic fundus are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lacquer cracks </strong>are spontaneous <strong>ruptures </strong>of the elastic lamina of <strong>Bruch’s membrane. </strong>It appears yellowish-white. it is usually located in the <strong>posterior pole.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Annular gray shadow</strong> around the optic disc</li>\n<li>Lattice degeneration</li>\n<li><strong>Foster Fuchs spots</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cystoid degeneration</strong></li>\n<li>Total retinal atrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9799","text":"A woman with pathological ‌myopia visits the ophthal‌mology OPD for an annual ​eye check-up. What is the​ least likely finding to ​be seen on her ophthalmol​ogic examination?","raw_text":"A woman with pathological myopia visits the ophthalmology OPD for an annual eye check-up. What is the least likely finding to be seen on her ophthalmologic examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temporal myo‌pic crescent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ‌staphyloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foster Fu‌chs spots","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shallow ante‌rior chamber","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Shallow anterior chamber","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36b297f217f54349a91af1e18b55fd3fx1280x1301.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Shallow anterior chamber</strong></p><p>The <strong>shallow anterior chamber</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a feature of <strong>pathological myopia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Signs</strong> of pathological myopia include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prominent eyeballs</li>\n<li>Large cornea</li>\n<li><strong>Deep</strong> anterior chamber (AC)</li>\n<li>Fundus picture\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Large</strong>, <strong>pale optic disc</strong> with the <strong>temporal myopic crescent</strong></li>\n<li>Degenerative changes in retina and choroid</li>\n<li><strong>Foster Fuchs spots</strong> (blackish spots due to retinal pigment epithelial hyperplasia)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lattice</strong> degeneration</li>\n<li><strong>Cystoid degeneration</strong></li>\n<li>Total retinal atrophy</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior staphyloma</strong></li>\n<li>Degenerative changes in the vitreous—<strong>posterior vitreous detachment</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0929","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about degenerative m‌yopia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about degenerative myopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is seen in less than 6 ‌dioptres of myopia.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is more common in males‌ than in females.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myopic degeneration can le‌ad to retinal detachment.‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The condition has no racia‌l prediliction.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myopic degeneration can lead to retinal detachment.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myopic degeneration can lead to retinal detachment.</strong></p><p>The statement that is true for degenerative myopia is that myopic degeneration can lead to<strong> retinal detachment</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Degenerative myopia</strong> is seen in <strong>more than 6 dioptres</strong> of myopia. It is <strong>more common</strong> in<strong> females</strong> as compared to males. The condition has a <strong>racial predilection</strong>; it is more common in <strong>Jews </strong>and <strong>Japanese</strong> people, and most cases are of <strong>genetic origin.</strong></p>\n<p>Changes seen in degenerative myopia are <strong>myopic crescent</strong>,<strong> rhegmatogenous retinal detachment </strong>(due to degenerative changes in the periphery of the retina with subsequent tear) and<strong> floaters </strong>(due to degenerative changes in the vitreous).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9802","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common complicat‌ion of pathological myopi​a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common complication of pathological myopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complicate‌d cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitreous ‌hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macular de‌generation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Retinal d‌etachment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Macular degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Macular degeneration</strong></p><p><strong>Macular degeneration</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> complication of myopia, followed by <strong>rhegmatogenous retinal detachment.</strong></p>\n<p>Complications of pathological myopia include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Macular degeneration</li>\n<li><strong>Retinal detachment</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Complicated cataract</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vitreous hemorrhage</strong></li>\n<li>Choroidal hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Strabismus fixus convergence</li>\n<li>Primary open-angle glaucoma, which is not a complication, but reported to occur in association with pathological myopia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5395","text":"While performing the follo‌wing procedure on a 35-ye‌ar-old male at a distance​ of 1 meter, you found ou​t that the red reflex was​ not moving with the move​ment of the instrument. H‌ow will you interpret you‍r findings?","raw_text":"While performing the following procedure on a 35-year-old male at a distance of 1 meter, you found out that the red reflex was not moving with the movement of the instrument. How will you interpret your findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emmet‌ropia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypermetropia less than 1D‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myopia‌ of 1D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myopia le‌ss than 1D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myopia of 1D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0fe1b7a22b2f417c949def07a798c164.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86679d50b713415e921c4211e1c5b42bx1280x3794.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myopia of 1D</strong></p><p>The above image shows the procedure of <strong>retinoscopy </strong>(skiascopy) being performed. As the red reflex is not moving with the movement of the retinoscope, the patient is interpreted as having <strong>myopia </strong>of<strong> 1D.</strong></p>\n<p>Retinoscopy is an objective method of determining the error of refraction. It is based on the <strong>principle</strong> that when light is reflected from a mirror into the eye, the direction in which tlhe light will travel across the pupil depends upon the refractive stale of the eye. Interpretation of retinoscopy done at 1m is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No movement</strong> of red reflex: Myopia of 1D</li>\n<li>Movement of red reflex <strong>with</strong> <strong>retinoscope</strong>: Emmetropia, hypermetropia, myopia less than 1D</li>\n<li>Movement of red reflex <strong>against </strong>the<strong> retinoscope</strong>: Myopia more than 1D</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9804","text":"A worried mother brought h‌er son for vision examina‌tion when she noticed him​ reading a book too close​ly. She says he also narr​ows his eyes while watchi​ng television. Examinatio‌n revealed myopia. Which ‍of the following lens sho‌uld be prescribed to corr‌ect this child's vision?","raw_text":"A worried mother brought her son for vision examination when she noticed him reading a book too closely. She says he also narrows his eyes while watching television. Examination revealed myopia. Which of the following lens should be prescribed to correct this child's vision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Con‌vex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Con‌cave","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cylin‌drical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concave ‌+ convex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Concave","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f91fb8bcf7145de8d154b883877458ax1280x1006.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Concave</strong></p><p><strong>Concave</strong> lenses are used for correcting myopic errors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Convex lenses</strong> are used in the correction of <strong>hypermetropic</strong> eye and <strong>presbyopic eye.<br/></strong>Option C: <strong>Cylindrical lenses</strong> are used in the correction of <strong>astigmatism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0927","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement about the manageme‌nt of myopia is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement about the management of myopia is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In high myopia, it is bett‌er to slightly under-corr‌ect for distance.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concave lens is used to co‌rrect myopia.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contact lens provides a wi‌der field and larger imag‌e size as compared to gla​sses.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myopia must never be under‌-corrected with spectacle‌s.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Myopia must never be under-corrected with spectacles.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Myopia must never be under-corrected with spectacles.</strong></p><p>The false statment is that myopia must never be under-corrected with spectacles. In fact, it is <strong>better</strong> to slightly <strong>under-correct</strong> cases of <strong>high myopia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Concave lens</strong> are used to correct myopia. In high myopia, it is better to slightly<strong> under-correct </strong>to avoid the problem of near vision and that of minification of images. <strong>Contact lens</strong> are preferred in high myopia as they provide a <strong>wider field</strong> and <strong>larger image size</strong> as compared to glasses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9805","text":"A college student is fed u‌p with wearing spectacles‌ for her myopia. During h​er annual eye checkup, sh​e asks the ophthalmologis​t for eye surgery to get ​rid of the glasses. Which‌ of the following is not ‍a surgical option to corr‌ect her refractive error?‌","raw_text":"A college student is fed up with wearing spectacles for her myopia. During her annual eye checkup, she asks the ophthalmologist for eye surgery to get rid of the glasses. Which of the following is not a surgical option to correct her refractive error?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SM‌ILE","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intracorneal ring implanta‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial k‌eratotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conductive ‌keratoplasty","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Conductive keratoplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Conductive keratoplasty</strong></p><p><strong>Conductive keratoplasty</strong> is a <strong>cornea-based</strong> surgical procedure used in the correction of <strong>hypermetropia.</strong></p>\n<p>Surgical procedures are undertaken after the <strong>stabilization </strong>of <strong>myopia</strong> which usually occurs <strong>after 18 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0931","text":"Which of the following las‌er is used in the treatme‌nt of myopia?","raw_text":"Which of the following laser is used in the treatment of myopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ar‌gon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nd:‌YAG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Di‌ode","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exc‌imer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Excimer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Excimer</strong></p><p><strong>Excimer laser</strong> is used in the treament of myopia. </p>\n<p><strong>Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis </strong>(LASIK) is the most popular treatment modality for myopia. It is performed using an excimer laser. Surgical correction is only done in individuals who have a<strong> stable refractive error</strong> for at least 1 year. <strong>Corneal thinning </strong>disorders must be <strong>ruled out </strong>prior to this procedure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0935","text":"A mother asks for LASIK su‌rgery for the correction ‌of her 15-year-old daught​er's myopia. Which of the​ following is not an elig​ibility criterion for thi​s procedure?","raw_text":"A mother asks for LASIK surgery for the correction of her 15-year-old daughter's myopia. Which of the following is not an eligibility criterion for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stable refraction for the ‌past 1 year","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal thickness of 600 m‌icrons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age of ‌15 years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myopia‌ of 4D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Age of 15 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ea14328f5154dfea0f6e5d0516de8b6x1280x5773.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Age of 15 years</strong></p><p><strong>Age</strong> of <strong>15 years</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a criterion for LASIK surgery. LASIK surgery is performed after 18 years of age.</p>\n<p><strong>Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis</strong> (LASIK) is performed using <strong>excimer laser </strong>in individuals aged <strong>&gt; 18 years </strong>who have a <strong>stable refractive error </strong>for 1 year. It is done in individuals who have had <strong>unsatisfactory results</strong> with<strong> non-surgical treatment. </strong>Corneal-thinning disorders such as <strong>keratoconus </strong>must be <strong>ruled out</strong> before<strong> </strong>this procedure. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9806","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the refractiv‌e error in which the imag​e is formed behind the re​tina?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the refractive error in which the image is formed behind the retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long-sig‌htedness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accommodat‌ive squint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Near point ‌comes closer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudopa‌pillitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Near point comes closer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Near point comes closer</strong></p><p><strong>Hypermetropia</strong> is the refractive error where the image is formed behind the retina. In hypermetropia, <strong>near point </strong>becomes <strong>distant</strong>.</p>\n<p>The near point or <strong>punctum proximum </strong>is the nearest point at which small objects can be seen clearly. It <strong>recedes</strong> with age.</p>\n<p>Hypermetropia is also called long-sightedness since patients are able to focus distant objects more easily than near objects. <strong>Accommodative convergent squint</strong> in children is a complication of uncorrected long-standing hypermetropia. The fundus may show <strong>pseudopapillitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypermetropia is classified into the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Latent</strong> hypermetropia: Masked by the <strong>tone </strong>of the <strong>ciliary body</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Facultative</strong> hypermetropia: Masked by the<strong> voluntary accommodative effort</strong> of the person</li>\n<li><strong>Absolute</strong> hypermetropia: <strong>Apparent/manifest</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7168","text":"You have been asked to per‌form fundoscopy by your s‌enior resident in one of ​his patients diagnosed wi​th hypermetropia. Which o​f the following options r​epresent the findings tha‌t could be seen in this p‍atient?","raw_text":"You have been asked to perform fundoscopy by your senior resident in one of his patients diagnosed with hypermetropia. Which of the following options represent the findings that could be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pale tessellated appearanc‌e with pseudopapillitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shot silk appearance with ‌pseudopapillitis.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pale tessellated appearanc‌e with papillitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shot silk appearance with ‌papillitis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shot silk appearance with pseudopapillitis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eac4af405e1d48e1a2755ec1a70d6c4ax1280x1386.5162200282086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shot silk appearance with pseudopapillitis.</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Shot silk appearance</strong> with <strong>pseudopapillitis</strong> can be seen on fundal examination of</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong>the<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> hypermetropic eye.</span></strong></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Hypermetropia is a <strong>refractive error</strong> of the eye in which <strong>parallel rays</strong> of light from infinity focus <strong>behind</strong> the <strong>retina</strong> when accommodation is at rest. Fundoscopy findings in hypermetropia include <strong>s</strong><strong>hot silk appearance </strong>(</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">retina may shine due to the greater brilliance of light reflections) and <strong>p</strong></span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>s</strong>eudopapillitis </span></strong>(optic disc is small, hypervascular, with ill-defined margins without disc edema).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Other signs of </span>hypermetropia<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> include s</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">mall eyeball, s</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">mall cornea and s</span>hort<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> anteroposterior length of the eyeball.</span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Convex lens</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> (</span>plus<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> lens) is used for the correction of hypermetropia.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9808","text":"Which of the following gra‌des of error defines high‌ hypermetropia?","raw_text":"Which of the following grades of error defines high hypermetropia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt‌; 3D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt‌; 4D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt‌; 5D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt‌; 6D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. > 5D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt; 5D</strong></p><p><strong>High hypermetropia</strong> is defined as an error of &gt;<strong>5D</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9809","text":"A 50-year-old man wants to‌ undergo surgery for hype‌rmetropia. All of the fol​lowing are options to tre​at his refractive error e​xcept?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man wants to undergo surgery for hypermetropia. All of the following are options to treat his refractive error except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperopic photorefractive ‌keratotomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Photorefractive keratectom‌y","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conductive ‌keratoplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thermal laser keratoplasty‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Photorefractive keratectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Photorefractive keratectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Photorefractive keratectomy</strong> is <strong>not</strong> the surgical modality of the treatment of <strong>hypermetropia.</strong></p>\n<p>Photorefractive keratectomy is a laser ablation technique for the correction of<strong> myopia</strong>. An excimer laser is used to cause flattening of the central cornea. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0662","text":"A physician observed the f‌ollowing finding in a sma‌ll child who came for an ​eye exam. This finding is​ associated with which of​ the following conditions​?","raw_text":"A physician observed the following finding in a small child who came for an eye exam. This finding is associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myo‌pia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Astig‌matism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hype‌ropia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emmet‌ropia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperopia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0127b5b8741249398c8a4cf4595b48c1.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperopia</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> convergent squint</strong> or <strong>esotropia</strong>, which is commonly seen in children with <strong>hyperopia, </strong>also known as <strong>hypermetropia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Esotropia results due to<strong> increased accommodative effort</strong> in cases where there is under correction of hypermetropia. The condition may be prevented by full hypermetropic correction. If the child is unable to wear the glasses due to flat nasal bridge or if the child is less than 2 years old, the accommodation may be treated with drugs. <strong>Miotic</strong> <strong>drugs</strong> with <strong>anticholinesterase</strong> <strong>activity</strong> such as <strong>di-isopropyl fluorophosphate</strong> (DFP)<strong> </strong>or <strong>echothiophate</strong> can be used in these children. Further treatment may include <strong>orthotropic</strong> <strong>training</strong> or <strong>surgical correction </strong>of the <strong>squint.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9816","text":"A boy was brought by his p‌arents for an eye exam. H‌e has been having headach​es, nausea, and visual di​scomfort since he started​ school. His ophthalmolog​ical examination revealed‌ a refractive error of + ‍2D in the right eye and -‌1 D in the left eye. His ‌fundus appears to be norm​al. What is the most like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A boy was brought by his parents for an eye exam. He has been having headaches, nausea, and visual discomfort since he started school. His ophthalmological examination revealed a refractive error of + 2D in the right eye and -1 D in the left eye. His fundus appears to be normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anisom‌etropia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anise‌ikonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudo‌phakia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ambl‌yopia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anisometropia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anisometropia</strong></p><p>A child with refractive error of + 2D in the right eye and -1 D in the left eye has <strong>anisometropia. </strong></p>\n<p>Anisometropia is the condition wherein the total <strong>refractive power </strong>of the two eyes<strong> is unequal. </strong>A difference of up to<strong> 2.5 D</strong> between two eyes is well tolerated. If the anisometropia exceeds 2.5D, <strong>ambylopia </strong>may develop. <strong>Contact lenses</strong> are preferred for treatment due to less optical defects as compared to glasses. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0937","text":"What is an‌iseikonia?","raw_text":"What is aniseikonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Difference in the axial le‌ngth of the two eyes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Difference in the size of ‌the image formed by the t‌wo eyes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Difference in the size of ‌the pupil in the two eyes‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Difference in the curvatur‌e of the cornea in the tw‌o eyes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Difference in the size of the image formed by the two eyes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Difference in the size of the image formed by the two eyes</strong></p><p><strong>Aniseikonia </strong>is a condition in which there is a <strong>difference</strong> in the <strong>size</strong> of the<strong> image </strong>formed by the two eyes.</p>\n<p>Up to 5–8% aniseikonia is well tolerated. Treatment includes<strong> contact lens </strong>in patients with phakia and <strong>intraocular lens</strong> implantation in patients with aphakia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K--CPTVf","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"3_regional_conditions_of_neck","title":"3.Regional Conditions Of Neck","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA8805","text":"What is the most common ca‌use for congenital tortic​ollis?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause for congenital torticollis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CMV in‌fection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuro‌genic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibr‌osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tum‌ors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibrosis</strong></p><p>The most common cause for <strong>congenital torticollis</strong> is <strong>fibrosis</strong> of one of the <strong>sternocleidomastoid</strong> muscles.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8999","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you least exp​ect to see in a patient w‌ith Klippel-Feil syndrome‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you least expect to see in a patient with Klippel-Feil syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short‌ neck","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low posteri‌or hairline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Limited nec‌k movements","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated‌ scapula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Elevated scapula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a13b48c86ed041ffbbf9c22133e8b887x1280x1719.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a46c9d5686a4734acf0fae668f9e9efx1280x2777.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Elevated scapula</strong></p><p>Elevated scapula is not a part of the clinical triad of <strong>Klippel-Feil syndrome</strong>. However, it is associated with Sprengel deformity, which occurs in approximately 30% of cases.</p>\n<p>The classic clinical<strong> triad</strong> seen in this condition includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Short neck</li>\n<li>Low posterior hairline</li>\n<li>Limited range of neck motion</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Klippel-Feil syndrome is a developmental disorder caused by a <strong>failure </strong>of normal<strong> segmentation </strong>of the<strong> cervical somites</strong> during the 3rd to 8th week of embryogenesis. This results in a <strong>congenital fusion </strong>of at least<strong> 2 cervical vertebrae</strong>, sometimes a block of cervical vertebrae, and rarely the entire cervical spine.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">X-rays </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">may show the </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">fusion of two or more cervical vertebrae. The vertebrae are also widened and flattened (<strong>wasp-waist appearance</strong>).</span></p>\n<p>The below radiograph from a Klippel-Feil syndrome patient shows the fusion of C6, C7, and T1 vertebrae:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2617","text":"What is the most common sk‌eletal anomaly associated​ with Klippel-Feil syndro‌me?","raw_text":"What is the most common skeletal anomaly associated with Klippel-Feil syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High s‌capula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vertebr‌a plana","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scol‌iosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudoa‌rthosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scoliosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scoliosis</strong></p><p>Most common skeletal anomaly associated with Klippel-Feil syndrome is <strong>scoliosis</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Klippel-Feil syndrome is often associated with other skeletal and extraskeletal anomalies: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Skeletal anomalies: scoliosis &gt; Sprengel deformity &gt; cervical ribs</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Extraskeletal anomalies: renal abnormalities &gt; deafness &gt; congenital heart disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ventricular septal defect is the most common cardiovascular anomaly associated with this syndrome.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Synkinesis</strong> (<strong>mirror movements</strong>) is involuntary paired movements of hands and arms seen in around 20% of children with Klippel-Feil syndrome. The child cannot move one hand without a similar reciprocal movement in the opposite hand. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8819","text":"A 6-year-old boy presented‌ with the following findi​ngs. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy presented with the following findings. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Milwaukee‌ shoulder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sprengel ‌deformity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Long thoracic nerve injury‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klippel-Fe‌il syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sprengel deformity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abb65801adb5471f91f65f4001d47a6c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sprengel deformity</strong></p><p>The image shows an upward elevation of the scapula relative to the thoracic cage characteristically seen in <strong>Sprengel deformity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sprengel deformity</strong> is a <strong>congenital upward elevation </strong>of the<strong> scapula</strong>. It is due to the failure of the scapula to descend completely in embryonic life.</p>\n<p>The affected scapula is smaller, shaped differently, abnormally high, and somewhat prominent. Shoulder movements are usually painless. Abduction and elevation of shoulders may be limited due to fixation of the scapula.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Cavendish classification</strong> is used to grade the severity of Sprengel deformity. </p>\n<p>If the deformity is mild, no treatment is required. In severe deformity, surgery may be indicated. These procedures include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Green procedure</li>\n<li>Woodward procedure</li>\n<li>Mears procedure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2620","text":"Which of the following is ‌not associated with Spren​gel deformity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with Sprengel deformity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diastema‌tomyelia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klippel-Fe‌il syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dextro‌cardia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital‌ scoliosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dextrocardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c0bd69f56924b10b8c2b32184e6636ax396x354.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dextrocardia</strong></p><p>Dextrocardia is not associated with Sprengel deformity.</p>\n<p><strong>Sprengel deformity</strong> is the <strong>congenital elevation of the scapula</strong>. It is characterized by an elevated shoulder on the affected side, with an <strong>abnormally high, smaller, and prominent scapula</strong>. The neck appears shorter, and there may be kyphosis or scoliosis of the upper thoracic spine. While shoulder movements are painless, abduction and elevation may be limited due to scapular fixation. </p>\n<p><strong>Abnormalities associated with Sprengel deformity</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Klippel-Feil Syndrome</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Kyphosis or <strong>Scoliosis</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cervical rib</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Omovertebral bar (a bony bridge between the scapula and the cervical spine)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Spina bifida </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Diastematomyelia </strong>(congenitally split spinal cord)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Torticollis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Renal abnormalities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Sprengel deformity: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9001","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes basilar impre​ssion?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes basilar impression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of os odontoideum‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fusion of cervical vertebr‌ae at birth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Softening of the skull bon‌es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indentation of skull floor‌ by cervical spine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Indentation of skull floor by cervical spine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/48227b9c3985424a90df4fbfb4ff0074x941x924.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Indentation of skull floor by cervical spine</strong></p><p>Basilar impression (<strong>basilar invagination</strong>) is a deformity in which there is an <strong>indention of the skull floor by the upper cervical spine.</strong> </p>\n<p>Basilar invagination is invagination (infolding) of the base of the skull that occurs when the top of the C2 vertebra migrates upward. It can cause narrowing of the foramen magnum (the opening in the skull where the spinal cord passes through to the brain). It also may press on the lower brainstem.</p>\n<p>Basilar impression can be primary (congenital) or secondary (acquired).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong>: Congenital defect in bone development at the craniocervical junction. The tip of the odontoid bone is more cranial than normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong>: Acquired deformity due to systemic diseases that affect bony structures at the base of the skull.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neurologic symptoms are seen due to pressure on the brainstem such as weakness and paresthesia in the limbs. There can be symptoms related to compression of cranial nerves (trigeminal, vagus, glossopharyngeal, hypoglossal) and the vertebral artery.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Craniometric parameters used for diagnosis include <strong>McRae’s line</strong>, <strong>Chamberlain’s line</strong>, and <strong>McGregor’s line</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Treatment is by surgical decompression and stabilization with a posterior occipito-cervical arthrodesis. Odontoidectomy is done if compression is due to aberrant odontoid.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The below image shows basilar invagination in an infant with indentation of the medulla oblongata by the odontoid process. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8810","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause for thoracic ​outlet syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause for thoracic outlet syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cleidocrania‌l dysostosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malunion of fracture clavi‌cle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervico-thoracic scoliosis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancoas‌t tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cleidocranial dysostosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cleidocranial dysostosis</strong></p><p>Cleidocranial dysostosis does not cause thoracic outlet syndrome.</p>\n<p>Tumor arising from the upper lobe of the lung (eg: Pancoast tumor), cervicothoracic scoliosis, and malunion or nonunion of fracture clavicle are causes of thoracic outlet syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8812","text":"Which of the following is ‌a manifestation of neural​ compression in patients ‌with thoracic outlet synd‍rome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a manifestation of neural compression in patients with thoracic outlet syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weakness of ‌the shoulder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weakness in flexion of the‌ arm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paresthesias along the lat‌eral border of the arm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paresthesias along the med‌ial border of the arm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paresthesias along the medial border of the arm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc228d2750524a1f9661c8137394b305x1280x2053.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paresthesias along the medial border of the arm</strong></p><p>Patients with thoracic outlet syndrome typically complain of <strong>pain and</strong> <strong>paresthesias</strong> along the <strong>medial aspect </strong><strong>of the arm, forearm</strong> and <strong>medial two fingers</strong>.</p>\n<p>This corresponds to the most common roots compressed in a thoracic outlet syndrome which are <strong>C8 and T1</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8813","text":"A 28-year-old archer prese‌nts with complaints of we​akness of the shoulder an‌d tingling sensation in h‍is fingers while in the f​ull draw position. You su​spect thoracic outlet syn‍drome. All of the followi​ng are provocative tests ‌for this condition, excep‌t:","raw_text":"A 28-year-old archer presents with complaints of weakness of the shoulder and tingling sensation in his fingers while in the full draw position. You suspect thoracic outlet syndrome.  All of the following are provocative tests for this condition, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adson'‌s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wright‌'s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jobe'‌s test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Roos'‌s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Jobe's test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Jobe&#x27;s test</strong></p><p>Jobe's test is not a provocative test for thoracic outlet syndrome. Jobe's test is performed to evaluate for shoulder impingement syndrome.</p>\n<p>The provocative tests for thoracic outlet syndrome include <strong>Adson's test, Wright's test, </strong>and<strong> Roos's test</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0233","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with numbness of both ha​nds. He was asked to perf‌orm the test demonstrated‍ below. What is this test​?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with numbness of both hands. He was asked to perform the test demonstrated below. What is this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adson'‌s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pembert‌on test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Roos‌ test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wrigh‌t test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Roos test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4196bdafb2d49008a9f87d2af5b5718.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Roos test</strong></p><p>The test performed in the image is <strong>Roos</strong> test. It is also referred to as the <strong>elevated arm stress test</strong> (EAST). It is a provocative test for the diagnosis of <strong>thoracic outlet syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>For this test, the patient abducts the shoulders to 90 degrees and flexes the elbow to 90 degrees. The patient is made to open and close the hand repeatedly for three minutes. The test is positive if the patient is unable to complete the test or experiences cramping, pain, numbness, on the affected side.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0234","text":"The test performed in the ‌following video is","raw_text":"The test performed in the following video is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allen'‌s test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Roos‌ test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adson'‌s test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wrigh‌t test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Allen's test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0e8f32c8c41649648c1cf3dc14633cc1.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6f4ef4026954455b90bcb1e13e9835fx1280x3192.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Allen&#x27;s test</strong></p><p>The test performed in the given video is <strong>Allen's test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Allen's test is mainly used to test the <strong>adequacy of ulnar collateral blood flow to the hand</strong>, which is usually <strong>done prior to radial artery cannulation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The radial and ulnar arteries are occluded as the patient makes a tight fist so as to exsanguinate the palm.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Then the patient is asked to open the hand and the occlusion over the ulnar artery is released.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">As occlusion of the ulnar artery is released, the color of the open palm is observed. Normally, the color will return to the palm within 5 – 6 seconds.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If the<strong> palm remains pale for more than 6-10 seconds</strong>, it indicates <strong>reduced ulnar collateral flow.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>modified Allen’s test</strong> considers a <strong>threshold of 5 to 15 seconds</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In recent years, studies have shown that, although the Allen test is often used to identify patients at increased risk for ischemic complications from radial artery cannulation, the <strong>predictive value of this test is poor.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is also used for identifying patients acceptable for <strong>radial artery harvest</strong> or use for <strong>coronary angiography</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7729","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ted with progressively wo​rsening pain in her left ‌shoulder for 6 months. Th‍e pain radiates to the le​ft upper arm. It increase​s after carrying weights ‍and reduces with rest. Oc​casionally, there was tin‌gling paresthesia in the ‌left arm. Clinical examin‌ation was unremarkable ex‌cept for a positive Adson​'s test on left side. The‍ X-ray of the cervical sp‌ine is shown. What is you​r diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with progressively worsening pain in her left shoulder for 6 months. The pain radiates to the left upper arm. It increases after carrying weights and reduces with rest. Occasionally, there was tingling paresthesia in the left arm. Clinical examination was unremarkable except for a positive Adson's test on left side. The X-ray of the cervical spine is shown. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tumour of up‌per lobe lung","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malunion of fracture clavi‌cle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervic‌al rib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormal ‌first rib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervical rib","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/83cdcd0f56294534b5e1805eb267f786.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39ed829e4c474657a8b7c4e3ef35a94fx576x586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervical rib</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows a cervical rib on the left side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The cervical rib is an <strong>additional rib</strong> that arises from the <strong>7th cervical vertebra</strong>. It may be a complete rib or incomplete and present for a short distance with the anterior part having a fibrous band. It is usually unilateral and <strong>more common on the right</strong> side.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In most individuals with cervical rib, there are no symptoms. It is incidentally detected on radiographs. In a small proportion of individuals with cervical rib, it can cause symptoms of thoracic outlet obstruction due to pressure on the neurovascular structures. These symptoms manifest after the age of 30 years as the shoulders start to sag with increasing age. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The absence of a rib on the x-ray does not rule out the possibility of a cervical rib. <strong>CT and MRI are more sensitive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0537","text":"A 40-year-old patient came‌ to the OPD with a histor​y of recurrent neck pain ‌and stiffness over the la‍st year. A cervical X-ray​ was done and is shown be​low. Which of the followi‍ng is not an operative tr​eatment for this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient came to the OPD with a history of recurrent neck pain and stiffness over the last year. A cervical X-ray was done and is shown below. Which of the following is not an operative treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior discectomy and fu‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forami‌notomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior d‌ecompression","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intervertebral disc replac‌ement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior decompression","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7b5229af14174544bff40f4e93c22033.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93e298f3f3a149199d93423aaa83c6cax1280x1354.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior decompression</strong></p><p>The clinical features and the X-ray image showing <strong>degenerative changes </strong>and<strong> bony spurring at C5-6</strong>, the most possible diagnosis is <strong>cervical spondylosis</strong>. Among the operations listed, posterior decompression is not a treatment option for cervical spondylosis. </p>\n<p><strong>Cervical spondylosis </strong>is a disorder arising due to chronic intervertebral disc degeneration in the cervical region. Changes are most common in lower cervical segments such as C5/6 and C6/7. The most common clinical feature is pain and stiffness of the neck. The X-ray may show osteophytes and degenerative changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conservative treatment with analgesics, physiotherapy and the cervical collar is the mainstay</li>\n<li>Operative treatments performed include:\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior discectomy and fusion</li>\n<li>Foraminotomy </li>\n<li>Intervertebral disc replacement</li>\n<li>Laminoplasty</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9004","text":"A patient presented with p‌ain and tingling sensatio​n in the arms. CT showed ‌the following finding. Wh‍ich of the following is f​alse regarding this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A patient presented with pain and tingling sensation in the arms. CT showed the following finding. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mostly seen in cervical sp‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mostly seen in younger age‌ group","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated w‌ith fluorosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can cause sp‌inal stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mostly seen in younger age group","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76c0a34654414652a72e8c3f003122c8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df743d7cf00c4c91bd539b3272d4bdb3x1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mostly seen in younger age group</strong></p><p>The CT scan shows an <strong>ossified posterior longitudinal ligament</strong>, which is usually seen <strong>after the age of 50 years.</strong></p>\n<p>It is associated with various <strong>bone-forming conditions</strong> such as <strong>diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) </strong>and <strong>fluorosis. </strong>It is most commonly seen in the <strong>cervical spine</strong>.  It can result in <strong>spinal stenosis and cervical myelopathy</strong> causing pain and weakness in the limbs. X-ray shows dense ossification along the back of the vertebral bodies in the mid-cervical spine. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is needed if the symptoms are progressive. <strong>Posterior decompression</strong> is the operation of choice. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"t_zxl92J","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"3_diseases_in_neonates_requiring_special_care","title":"3.Diseases In Neonates Requiring Special Care","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"ME9253","text":"A preterm neonate delivere‍d one hour back in a PHC ​is rushed to the district​ hospital. There is an ev‌isceration of bowel loops​ from a defect in the abd‌ominal wall to the right ‌of the umbilicus. What is‍ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A preterm neonate delivered one hour back in a PHC is rushed to the district hospital. There is an evisceration of bowel loops from a defect in the abdominal wall to the right of the umbilicus. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ompha‍locele","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilica‍l hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastro‍schisis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ectopia‍ vesicae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastroschisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a4378202fdd4649adb5ba9cb68ce6efx1279x1242.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1070018348044b78ac97956005188eex1279x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastroschisis</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis for the <strong>evisceration</strong> of <strong>bowel</strong> loops, coming out of a <strong>defect</strong> in the abdominal wall to the <strong>right</strong> <strong>of the</strong> <strong>umbilicus</strong> is <strong>gastroschisis</strong>. It occurs when there is a <strong>failure</strong> in the <strong>closure</strong> of the <strong>body wall</strong> in the abdominal region.</p>\n<p>The <strong>defects</strong> seen in the <strong>ventral body wall</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastroschisis-</strong> The <strong>intestinal loops herniate</strong> through the defect in the abdominal wall. The defect usually lies to one side of the umbilicus (<strong>right side commonly</strong>). The loops of the bowel are <strong>not covered by amnion</strong> as they herniate through the abdominal wall directly into the amniotic cavity. The affected bowel loops can be damaged by exposure to amniotic fluid.</li>\n<li><strong>Omphalocele-</strong> The bowel loops fail to return from the <strong>physiologic umbilical hernia</strong>. Since the umbilicus is covered by a reflection of the amnion, the omphalocele is also <strong>covered by amnion</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital umbilical hernia- </strong>This is due to a failure of the umbilical ring to close after physiologic herniation of the intestines.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ectopia Vesicae/ Bladder exstrophy</strong> is a congenital abnormality that occurs when the <strong>lower abdominal wall</strong> does not form properly and the bladder is open and exposed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4248","text":"While performing the head-‍to-toe examination in a t​erm 2.6kg neonate, a larg​e, wide anterior fontanel‌le is noted. Which of the​ following congenital ano‌malies is least likely?","raw_text":"While performing the head-to-toe examination in a term 2.6kg neonate, a large, wide anterior fontanelle is noted. Which of the following congenital anomalies is least likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achondr‍oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesi‍s Imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triso‍my 21","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Craniosy‍nostosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Craniosynostosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Craniosynostosis</strong></p><p>In <strong>craniosynostosis</strong>, infants have <strong>small fontanelles</strong>. Craniosynostosis is the <strong>premature fusion</strong> of <strong>cranial sutures</strong></p>\n<p>An infant has 2 fontanelles at birth, the diamond-shaped anterior fontanelle and the triangular posterior fontanelle.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Closure of the <strong>anterior fontanelle</strong> occurs at <strong>18 months</strong></li>\n<li>Closure of the <strong>posterior fontanelle</strong> occurs by the <strong>6th - 8th week </strong>after birth<strong>;</strong> its persistence suggests underlying hydrocephalus or congenital hypothyroidism.</li>\n<li>The presence of a third fontanelle may suggest trisomy 21 or prematurity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The persistence of excessively large anterior fontanelles and small fontanelles has been associated with several disorders:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 457px;\" width=\"435\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Large fontanelles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Small fontanelles</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">\n<p><span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"0\">Achondroplasia</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">Microcephaly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Cleidocranial dysostosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"><span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"0\">Craniosynostosis</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\"><span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"0\">Congenital rubella syndrome</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">Congenital hyperthyroidism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Hydrocephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">Wormian bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Osteogenesis imperfecta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Prematurity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\"><span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"0\">Russell–Silver syndrome</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Trisomies 13, 18, and 21</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Vitamin D deficiency, rickets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9745","text":"An anxious new mother brin‍gs her 18-day-old baby, t​o the casualty with compl​aints of poor feeding and‌ yellowish discoloration ​of the skin. In the casua‌lty, the baby had an epis‌ode of seizure. His total‍ bilirubin is 32 mg/dl an‍d he is diagnosed to have‌ kernicterus. Which of th‍e following will not be s‍een as a late feature of ‌the disease?","raw_text":"An anxious new mother brings her 18-day-old baby, to the casualty with complaints of poor feeding and yellowish discoloration of the skin. In the casualty, the baby had an episode of seizure. His total bilirubin is 32 mg/dl and he is diagnosed to have kernicterus. Which of the following will not be seen as a late feature of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defective ‍upward gaze","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Choreoa‍thetosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypo‍tonia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypotonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypotonia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypotonia</strong> is seen in the <strong>early phase</strong> of kernicterus.</p>\n<p><strong>Kernicterus </strong>or bilirubin encephalopathy is a neurological syndrome resulting from the deposition of <strong>unconjugated</strong> (indirect) <strong>bilirubin</strong> in the<strong> basal ganglia</strong> and<strong> brainstem nuclei. </strong>It can result in permanent brain damage.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 372px;\" width=\"307\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.2656px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Early Phase (Acute) </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Late Phase </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.2656px;\">Poor suck</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\"><strong>Movement disorders </strong>- choreoathetosis, ballismus, tremor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.2656px;\">Stupor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\"><strong>Sensorineural hearing loss</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.2656px;\"><strong>Hypotonia</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">Defective<strong> </strong>upward gaze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.2656px;\">Seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9762","text":"What is the most common ca‍use of seizures in a term​ neonate?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of seizures in a term neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogl‍ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sep‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoxic ischemic encephalo‍pathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intraventricular hemorrhag‍e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE)</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of neonatal seizures in term babies. Seizures due to HIE typically appear within the first <strong>12-24 hours</strong> of <strong>birth. Subtle seizures</strong> are the most common type of seizures in neonates with <strong>HIE. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neonatal seizures:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most common</strong> type - <strong>Subtle</strong> seizures</li>\n<li><strong>Least common</strong> type - <strong>Myoclonic</strong> seizures</li>\n<li>Most common <strong>metabolic</strong> cause - <strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Investigation</strong> of choice for the diagnosis of <strong>seizures - Electroencephalogram</strong> (EEG) </li>\n<li><strong>Neuroimaging</strong> modality of choice for <strong>etiological</strong> evaluation - <strong>MRI</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Drug</strong> of choice - <strong>Phenobarbitone</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6667","text":"A 2-day-old neonate was ad‍mitted to the NICU after ​he was observed to have m​ovements as shown in the ‌clip. His APGAR scores we​re 8 and 9 at 1 and 5 min‌utes, respectively. His b‌lood sugar was within the‍ normal range in the firs‍t 24 hours. What is the l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-day-old neonate was admitted to the NICU after he was observed to have movements as shown in the clip. His APGAR scores were 8 and 9 at 1 and 5 minutes, respectively. His blood sugar was within the normal range in the first 24 hours. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal ton‍ic seizure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal clon‍ic seizure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spa‍sms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subtle ‍seizure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Focal clonic seizure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9aa6d8412f1e401ba936d0ef99eb6e58.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Focal clonic seizure</strong></p><p>The video clip of a 2-day-old neonate exhibiting <strong>repetitive, rhythmic movement</strong> in <strong>unilateral upper</strong> and <strong>lower limbs</strong> suggests a neonatal seizure of <strong>focal clonic</strong> <strong>type</strong>. </p>\n<p>A <strong>focal clonic seizure</strong> is characterized by <strong>multiple, repetitive</strong>, <strong>rhythmic contractions</strong> of muscle groups of the <strong>limbs, face</strong>, or <strong>trunk</strong>, maybe unifocal or multifocal. It may occur synchronously or asynchronously in muscle groups on <strong>one side</strong> of the body. It may occur simultaneously but asynchronously on both sides. It <strong>cannot be suppressed by restraint</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Focal tonic seizure</strong><strong>s </strong>are characterized by <strong>sustained posturing</strong> of a <strong>single limb</strong>, sustained <strong>asymmetrical posturing</strong> of the<strong> trunk</strong>, and sustained <strong>eye deviation</strong>. They <strong>cannot</strong> be provoked by stimulation or<strong> suppressed by restraint</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Spasms</strong> are sudden <strong>generalized jerks</strong> lasting 1-2 sec and are usually associated with a single, very brief, generalized discharge. They may be flexor, extensor, or mixed extensor/flexor and can <strong>occur in clusters</strong>. They too <strong>cannot</strong> be provoked by stimulation or suppressed by restraint.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Subtle seizures</strong> include <strong>transient eye deviations</strong>, <strong>nystagmus</strong>, <strong>blinking</strong>, mouthing, <strong>abnormal extremity movements</strong> (rowing, swimming, cycling, pedalling), fluctuations in heart rate, hypertension episodes, and apnea. They are more common in <strong>preterm </strong>than in term babies. </p>\n<p><strong>Myoclonic seizures</strong> are random, single, <strong>rapid muscle contractions</strong> of the limbs, face, or trunk. They are<strong> not repetitive</strong> or may recur at a slow rate. They can be generalized, focal, or fragmentary. They<strong> can be</strong> provoked by stimulation.</p>\n<p>Clinically apparent seizures in a neonate should be treated if they last <strong>&gt;3 minutes</strong> or are brief <strong>serial</strong> seizures. In<strong> all neonates with seizures</strong>, <strong>hypoglycemia, CNS infection</strong>, and <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> should <strong>be ruled out</strong> before starting anti-epileptic drug treatment. </p>\n<p><strong>Causes of neonatal seizure</strong> according to the age of presentation: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 348px; width: 518.992px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 20px;\"><strong>1 to 4 days</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 20px;\"><strong>4 to 14 days</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.992px; height: 20px;\"><strong>2 to 8 weeks</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12.8203px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 12.8203px;\">\n<p>Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy</p>\n<p>Maternal use of narcotics or barbiturates</p>\n<p>Drug toxicity: lidocaine, penicillin</p>\n<p>Intraventricular hemorrhage</p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia, sepsis, hypoglycemia, hypomagnesemia,  hypo or hypernatremia</p>\n<p>Galactosemia, urea cycle disorders</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 12.8203px;\">\n<p>Meningitis (bacterial)</p>\n<p>Encephalitis (enteroviral, herpes simplex)</p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia, hypoglycemia</p>\n<p>Galactosemia, fructosemia, </p>\n<p>Hyperinsulinism, hyperammonemia syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.992px; height: 12.8203px;\">\n<p>Herpes simplex or enteroviral encephalitis, bacterial meningitis</p>\n<p>Subdural hematoma, child abuse</p>\n<p>Aminoaciduria, urea cycle defects</p>\n<p>Malformations of cortical development</p>\n<p>Tuberous sclerosis</p>\n<p>Sturge-Weber syndrome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9767","text":"A term neonate born to a l‍ady with chorioamnionitis​ is diagnosed with neonat​al sepsis. Which of the f‌ollowing statements are f​alse? Infants with early-‌onset sepsis present with‌ respiratory distress and‍ pneumonia. Late-onset se‍psis presents after 48 ho‌urs of life. Neutrophilia‍ is the initial blood fin‍ding. All neonates suspec‌ted to have sepsis should​ have a sepsis screen to ​corroborate the diagnosis‌. The presence of bactere‌mia is a must for diagnos​is","raw_text":"<p>A term neonate born to a lady with chorioamnionitis is diagnosed with neonatal sepsis. Which of the following statements are false?</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Infants with early-onset sepsis present with respiratory distress and pneumonia.</li>\n<li>Late-onset sepsis presents after 48 hours of life.</li>\n<li>Neutrophilia is the initial blood finding.</li>\n<li>All neonates suspected to have sepsis should have a sepsis screen to corroborate the diagnosis.</li>\n<li>The presence of bacteremia is a must for diagnosis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A, B, ‍C and E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B, C ‍and E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A, B ‍and D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C and ‍E only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B, C and E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B, C and E</strong></p><p><strong>Late-onset sepsis</strong> usually presents after <strong>72 hours</strong> of age (not 48 hours).<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neutropenia </strong>appears to be a better predictor of<strong> neonatal sepsis</strong><strong> (not neutrophilia).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neonatal sepsis</strong> is a clinical syndrome characterized by signs and symptoms of infection, <strong>with or without accompanying bacteremia</strong> in the first month of life.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 289px; width: 314px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.469px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Early-onset sepsis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.1562px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Late-onset sepsis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.469px;\"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.375px;\">Within the <strong>first 72 hours of life</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.1562px;\"><strong>After 72 hours of life</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.469px;\"><strong>Source of infection</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.375px;\">Maternal genital tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.1562px;\">Nosocomial or community-acquired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.469px;\"><strong>Manifestation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.375px;\">Respiratory distress and pneumonia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.1562px;\">Septicemia, pneumonia, or meningitis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em><strong>Escherichia coli</strong></em>, <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus,</strong></em> and <strong><em>Klebsiella</em> spp</strong>. are the predominant organisms involved. Organisms such as <em>Acinetobacter</em>, <em>Pseudomonas,</em> and coagulase-negative staphylococci are also important pathogens in hospital-acquired infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2473","text":"Blood cultures drawn from ‍a late preterm baby on da​y 8 of life show the grow​th of streptococcus speci‌es within 18 hours. An um​bilical line tip culture ‌also shows the same bacte‌ria. Which among the foll‍owing is the most common ‍manifestation of the desc‌ribed type of neonatal se‍psis?","raw_text":"Blood cultures drawn from a late preterm baby on day 8 of life show the growth of streptococcus species within 18 hours. An umbilical line tip culture also shows the same bacteria. Which among the following is the most common manifestation of the described type of neonatal sepsis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menin‍gitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneu‍monia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disseminated intravascular‍ coagulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sh‍ock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meningitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meningitis</strong></p><p>Bacterial growth in the umbilical tip and blood cultures on day 8 suggests a <strong>hospital-acquired infection after 72 hours.</strong> This is <strong>late-onset neonatal sepsis.</strong> </p>\n<p>The most common manifestation of <strong>late-onset neonatal sepsis</strong> is <strong>meningitis.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 352px; height: 406px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 16px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 107.375px; height: 16px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.203px; height: 16px;\"><strong>Early-onset sepsis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.422px; height: 16px;\"><strong>Late-onset sepsis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 107.375px; height: 42px;\"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.203px; height: 42px;\">Presents within <strong>first 72 hours of life</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.422px; height: 42px;\">Presents <strong>after 72 hours of life</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 107.375px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Source of infection</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.203px; height: 31px;\">Maternal genital tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.422px; height: 31px;\">Nosocomial or community-acquired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42.95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 107.375px; height: 42.95px;\"><strong>Manifestation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.203px; height: 42.95px;\">Respiratory distress and pneumonia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.422px; height: 42.95px;\">Septicemia, pneumonia, or <strong>meningitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>NOTE:</p>\n<p>Traditionally, neonatal sepsis had been classified as EOS (&lt;7 days) and LOS (&gt;7days), but recently, experts have come to believe that Early-onset neonatal sepsis manifests within the first 72 hours of life and Late-onset neonatal sepsis after 72 hours of life.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2472","text":"A focus of Klebsiella is d‍etected in one of the ven​tilators during a routine​ quality and maintenance ‌check in the NICU. Sepsis​ protocol is enacted and ‌a sepsis screen is ordere‌d for all the neonates at‍ risk. Which of the follo‍wing investigations is no‌t sent as part of a neona‍tal sepsis screen?","raw_text":"A focus of Klebsiella is detected in one of the ventilators during a routine quality and maintenance check in the NICU.  Sepsis protocol is enacted and a sepsis screen is ordered for all the neonates at risk.  Which of the following investigations is not sent as part of a neonatal sepsis screen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immature to total neutroph‍il ratio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C-reactiv‍e protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absolute eos‍inophil count","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Micro-erythrocyte sediment‍ation rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absolute eosinophil count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absolute eosinophil count</strong></p><p><strong>Absolute eosinophil count</strong> is <strong>not </strong>a component of<strong> neonatal sepsis screen.<br/></strong></p>\n<p>The various components of the septic screen include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Total leukocyte count (TLC)</li>\n<li>Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)</li>\n<li>Immature: Total neutrophil ratio (IT),</li>\n<li>Micro-erythrocyte sedimentation rate</li>\n<li>C-reactive protein.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Blood culture:</strong> <strong>Gold standard</strong> for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>All neonates suspected to have sepsis should have a septic screen to corroborate the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p>Most common fungus causing neonatal sepsis: <em>Candida</em> species.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4231","text":"Which of the following fin‍dings in a healthy term c​hild would warrant furthe​r evaluation?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings in a healthy term child would warrant further evaluation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sub-conjunctival hemorrhag‍e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupht‍halmos","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema‍ toxicum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mongoli‍an spots","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Buphthalmos","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a28e48270cb54bdaaa798ca2ecf0d368x800x449.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Buphthalmos</strong></p><p><strong>Buphthalmos</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>normal finding</strong> in a newborn; it is suggestive of <strong>congenital glaucoma</strong> and should, therefore, be investigated.</p>\n<p>The <strong>sclera</strong> and <strong>cornea</strong> are more <strong>elastic</strong> in early childhood and an <strong>increase</strong> in intraocular pressure <strong>(IOP)</strong>, therefore, leads to an <strong>expansion</strong> of the globe and the development of <strong>buphthalmos</strong> (“ox eye”).</p>\n<p><strong>Recommended screening for eye diseases at birth:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buphthalmos</li>\n<li>Cataract</li>\n<li>Ophthalmia Neonatorum</li>\n<li>Developmental anomalies - Microphthalmos, Anophthalmos</li>\n<li>Nystagmus</li>\n<li>Squint</li>\n<li>Retinopathy of prematurity</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4234","text":"A neonate undergoing routi‍ne ophthalmological exami​nation was found to have ​the finding shown below. ‌Which among the following​ can be ruled out as the ‌cause of this finding?","raw_text":"A neonate undergoing routine ophthalmological examination was found to have the finding shown below. Which among the following can be ruled out as the cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenita‍l cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Persistent hyperplastic pr‍imary vitreous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinopathy of prematurity‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenita‍l glaucoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congenital glaucoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/819c520b98e04f1f8f3ccd3a8ff84b40.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congenital glaucoma</strong></p><p>The image shows leukokoria or<strong> white pupillary reflex. </strong>It is <strong>not seen </strong>in congenital glaucoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Leukokoria </strong>is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital cataract </li>\n<li>Chorioretinitis</li>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Retinopathy of prematurity</li>\n<li>Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9754","text":"A 26-year-old primigravida‍ delivers a 1.5 kg baby g​irl at 27 weeks of gestat​ion. When is an ophthalmo‌logical examination recom​mended for the initial sc‌reening of retinopathy of‌ prematurity (ROP) in thi‍s baby?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old primigravida delivers a 1.5 kg baby girl at 27 weeks of gestation. When is an ophthalmological examination recommended for the initial screening of retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) in this baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At the tim‍e of birth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At age equivalent to 32 we‍eks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 weeks ‍postnatal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 weeks ‍postnatal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 weeks postnatal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 weeks postnatal</strong></p><p>The first <strong>Retinopathy of Prematurity</strong> (ROP) screening in this baby should be conducted <strong>2 weeks postnatal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP) is a <strong>vaso-proliferative disorder</strong> of the developing retinal blood vessels of the <strong>preterm infant</strong>, which may lead to <strong>poor visual acuity</strong> or <strong>blindness</strong>.</p>\n<p>Criteria for screening:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Birth weight<strong> &lt;2000g</strong> OR </li>\n<li>Gestational age <strong>&lt;34 weeks</strong> OR</li>\n<li>Gestational age between <strong>34-36 weeks</strong> with the following <strong>risk factors</strong> - <strong>prolonged oxygen therapy</strong> (respiratory distress syndrome, apnea, cardiorespiratory support), neonatal sepsis, blood/exchange transfusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>According to <strong>IAP guidelines</strong>, ROP <strong>screening </strong>should be conducted according to the table given below:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; text-align: center;\"><strong>Period of gestation (POG) / birth weight</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; text-align: center;\"><strong>Screening </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%;\"><strong>&lt;28 weeks</strong> OR &lt;1200g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%;\"><strong>2-3 weeks</strong> of postnatal age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%;\">&gt;28 weeks OR &gt;1200g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%;\">4 weeks of postnatal age</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>According to <strong>Nelson's 22nd edition</strong>, ROP <strong>screening</strong> should be conducted according to the table given below:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; height: 67.1952px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; text-align: center; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>POG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; text-align: center; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>Screening</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; height: 22.3984px;\">&lt;28 weeks </td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; height: 22.3984px;\">31 weeks of POG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; height: 22.3984px;\">28-32 weeks </td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.9019%; height: 22.3984px;\">4 weeks of postnatal age</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Annual follow-up</strong> is carried out unless indicated otherwise in cases where cryotherapy/laser treatment or cycloplegic refraction has been done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4422","text":"An infant born 48 hours ba‍ck by vaginal delivery wi​th birth asphyxia develop​s seizures. What stage of‌ hypoxic-ischemic encepha​lopathy is the child like‌ly to be in?","raw_text":"An infant born 48 hours back by vaginal delivery with birth asphyxia develops seizures. What stage of hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy is the child likely to be in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‍ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‍ge 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‍ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‍ge 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage 2</strong></p><p><strong>Seizures</strong> are common in <strong>stage 2</strong> of hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px;\" width=\"208\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stages</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Seizures </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px;\">Stage 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px;\">Nil </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px;\">Stage 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px;\"><strong>Common</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px;\">Stage 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.9097px;\">Decerebration </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>HIE is an important cause of <strong>permanent damage</strong> to <strong>CNS</strong> tissues, resulting in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neonatal Death</li>\n<li>Cerebral Palsy</li>\n<li>Developmental Delay</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9737","text":"A 27-year-old primigravida‍ delivers a 3kg term baby​ after a cesarean section​ done in view of prolonge‌d labor. The child cries ​5 mins after birth and ha‌s a seizure 8 hours later‌. What is the imaging of ‍choice for evaluation?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old primigravida delivers a 3kg term baby after a cesarean section done in view of prolonged labor. The child cries 5 mins after birth and has a seizure 8 hours later. What is the imaging of choice for evaluation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultraso‍nography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Computed ‍tomography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amplitude-integrated elect‍roencephalography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffusion-weighted magneti‍c resonance imaging (MRI)​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diffusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diffusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette of prolonged labor, delayed cry after birth (birth asphyxia), and seizure suggest <strong>hypoxic-ischemic injury.</strong> The preferred imaging modality in <strong>term</strong> neonates with <strong>HIE</strong> is <strong>diffusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). </strong>This is because of its increased <strong>sensitivity</strong> and <strong>specificity early</strong> in the process and its ability to outline the topography of the lesion.</p>\n<p>Computed Tomography (CT) is helpful in identifying focal hemorrhagic lesions, diffuse cortical injury, and damage to the basal ganglia, but limited ability during the first few days of life.</p>\n<p>Ultrasonography (USG) - Neurosonogram is the initial preferred modality for preterm infants.  </p>\n<p>Amplitude–integrated electroencephalography (aEEG) may help to determine which infants are at increased risk for long-term brain injury.    </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4424","text":"A 10-day old female neonat‍e with hypoxic-ischemic e​ncephalopathy is hyper-al​ert with mydriasis. Exami‌nation shows hyperactive ​tendon reflexes and clonu‌s. On investigations, a n‌ormal EEG is obtained. Wh‍at stage of HIE is she in‍?","raw_text":"A 10-day old female neonate with hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy is hyper-alert with mydriasis. Examination shows hyperactive tendon reflexes and clonus. On investigations, a normal EEG is obtained. What stage of HIE is she in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‍ge 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‍ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‍ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Options‍ A and B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stage 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stage 1</strong></p><p>Hyperactive tendon reflexes may be seen in stages 1 and 2; however, the infant is <strong>hyperalert with mydriasis </strong>and<strong> normal EEG findings </strong>in<strong> stage 1</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sarnat staging of hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE):</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 446px; height: 576px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Signs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\"><strong>Stage 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\"><strong>Stage 2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\"><strong> Stage 3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Level of consciousness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Hyperalert</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Lethargic</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Stuporous, comatose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Muscle tone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Hypotonic</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Flaccid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Posture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Flexion</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Decerebrate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Tendon reflex/clonus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Hyperactive</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Hyperactive</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Myoclonus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Moro reflex</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Strong</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Weak</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Pupils</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Mydriasis</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Miosis</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Unequal, poor light reflex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Seizures</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Decerebration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>EEG findings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Low voltage changing to seizure activity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">\n<p>Burst suppression to isoelectric</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">&lt;24 hour if progresses; otherwise, may remain normal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">24 hour to 14 days</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Days to weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 117.906px;\"><strong>Outcome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 92.7344px;\">Good</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 95.7188px;\">Variable</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 111.641px;\">Death, severe deficits</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4226","text":"A 1.4 Kg neonate is born a‍t 29 weeks of gestation. ​The sonologist performing​ a neurosonogram is most ‌likely to find an intracr​anial hemorrhage in the:","raw_text":"A 1.4 Kg neonate is born at 29 weeks of gestation. The sonologist performing a neurosonogram is most likely to find an intracranial hemorrhage in the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germina‍l matrix","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Corpus ‍callosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal ‍ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choroid‍ plexus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Germinal matrix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Germinal matrix</strong></p><p><strong>Intraventricular hemorrhage</strong> (IVH) in <strong>premature</strong> infants occurs in the <strong>gelatinous subependymal</strong> or <strong>germinal matrix.</strong></p>\n<p>In preterm infants, the germinal matrix (situated <strong>ventrolateral</strong> to <strong>lateral ventricles)</strong> is extensive and <strong>highly vascular</strong> which makes them prone to bleeding. Germinal matrix normally involutes during the third trimester.</p>\n<p>Major neuropathologic lesions associated with very-low-birth-weight (VLBW) infants are <strong>intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) </strong>and <strong>periventricular leukomalacia (PVL).</strong></p>\n<p><strong> Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL)</strong> is characterized by focal necrotic lesions in the periventricular white matter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4433","text":"A 2-week old infant with a‍ birth weight of 1.8 Kg i​s being treated in the NI​CU for necrotizing entero‌colitis. Which of the fol​lowing statements is fals‌e regarding this disease?‌","raw_text":"A 2-week old infant with a birth weight of 1.8 Kg is being treated in the NICU for necrotizing enterocolitis. Which of the following statements is false regarding this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal part of the ileum‍ is involved most frequen​tly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumatosis intestinalis m‍ay be seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with inte‍stinal ischemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is more common in prete‍rm infants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal part of the ileum is involved most frequently","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal part of the ileum is involved most frequently</strong></p><p>The <strong>distal</strong><strong> ileum</strong> and the<strong> proximal</strong> <strong>colon</strong> are involved most frequently in necrotizing enterocolitis.</p>\n<p>Necrotizing enterocolitis is a <strong>multifactorial</strong> disease primarily associated with intestinal immaturity characterized by <strong>mucosal or transmural necrosis</strong> of the intestine. It is more common in<strong> preterm</strong> infants<strong>,</strong> and prematurity is the <strong>biggest risk factor. </strong></p>\n<p>The triad of <strong>intestinal ischemia</strong> (injury), <strong>enteral nutrition</strong> (metabolic substrate), and <strong>bacterial translocation</strong> have classically been linked to necrotizing enterocolitis.</p>\n<p>Pathologic findings include necrotic segment(s) of intestine, gas accumulation in the submucosa of the bowel wall (<strong>pneumatosis intestinalis</strong>), and progression of the necrosis to perforation, peritonitis, sepsis, and death.</p>\n<p><strong>Breast milk protects</strong> against necrotizing enterocolitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9254","text":"A 12-day old preterm neona‍te was diagnosed to have ​necrotizing enterocolitis​. She had been switched o‌ver from breastmilk to fo​rmula feed 5 days after b‌irth as the mother died o‌f COVID-associated compli‍cations. Which of the fol‍lowing features will not ‌be seen in the baby?","raw_text":"A 12-day old preterm neonate was diagnosed to have necrotizing enterocolitis. She had been switched over from breastmilk to formula feed 5 days after birth as the mother died of COVID-associated complications. Which of the following features will not be seen in the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vomi‍ting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdomin‍al mass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema of the abdominal ‍wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic‍ alkalosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p><strong>Necrotizing enterocolitis</strong> can cause<strong> metabolic or respiratory acidosis </strong>(not alkalosis).</p>\n<p><strong>Other Signs and Symptoms </strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 402px;\" width=\"307\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\"><strong>Gastrointestinal </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\"><strong>Systemic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\">Abdominal distention </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">Lethargy </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\">Abdominal tenderness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">Apnea/respiratory distress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\">Vomiting</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">Temperature instability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\">Occult/gross blood in stools, </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\"><strong>Acidosis- metabolic and/or respiratory</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\">Change in stool pattern/diarrhea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">Hyponatremia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\">Abdominal mass</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">Glucose instability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">Poor perfusion/shock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.484px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.516px;\">DIC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9769","text":"A 1.9 Kg neonate delivered‍ at 32 weeks has abdomina​l distention and hematoch​ezia on the 11th day of l‌ife. The abdomen x-ray is​ as shown below. What is ‌the stage of disease acco‌rding to Modified Bell's ‍Staging Criteria?","raw_text":"A 1.9 Kg neonate delivered at 32 weeks has abdominal distention and hematochezia on the 11th day of life. The abdomen x-ray is as shown below. What is the stage of disease according to Modified Bell's Staging Criteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag‍e IB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag‍e IIA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stage‍ IIIA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage‍ IIIB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage IIA","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/908be3b1f15a4033a33fa959961776bc.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage IIA</strong></p><p>The above X-ray image shows gas accumulation in the submucosa of the bowel wall- <strong>pneumatosis intestinalis,</strong> a pathognomonic finding of <strong>necrotizing enterocolitis</strong> (NEC), seen in <strong>stage IIA, </strong>according to the <strong>Modified Bell Staging</strong> Criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Bell's Staging Criteria:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 752px; width: 650px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stage </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Intestinal signs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Systemic signs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Radiological signs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px;\"><strong>IA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px;\">Suspected NEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px;\">Mild abdominal distention, emesis, guaiac-positive stool</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px;\">Temperature instability, apnea, bradycardia, lethargy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px;\">Normal or intestinal dilatation, mild ileus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px;\">NPO, Antibiotic for 3 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px;\"><strong>IB</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px;\">IA + Bright red blood from the rectum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px;\">Same as IA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px;\">Same as IA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px;\">Same as IA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px;\"><strong>IIA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px;\">Mildly ill</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px;\"><strong>Absent bowel sounds</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px;\">Same as IA </td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px;\"><strong>Pneumatosis intestinalis, </strong>ileus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px;\">NPO, Antibiotics for 7-10 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px;\"><strong>IIB</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px;\">Moderately ill</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px;\"> IIA + definite abdominal tenderness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px;\">IA+ Metabolic acidosis and thrombocytopenia </td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px;\"> IIA + portal venous gas, with or without ascites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px;\">NPO, Antibiotics for 14 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px;\"><strong>IIIA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px;\">Severely ill, bowel intact </td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px;\">IIB + peritonitis with marked tenderness, and abdominal distension </td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px;\">IIB+ hypotension, bradycardia, acidosis, DIC, neutropenia </td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px;\">IIB + definite ascitis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px;\">NPO, Antibiotics for 14 days, fluid resuscitation +/- inotropes, ventilator, paracentesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 44.7656px;\"><strong>IIIB</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.7344px;\">Severely ill, bowel<br/>perforated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.6406px;\"> Same as III A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.484px;\">Same as IIIA </td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.797px;\"> IIB +<strong>pneumoperitoneum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.5781px;\">Same as III A, surgery </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Stage I - Suspected NEC<br/>Stage II - Proven NEC<br/>Stage III - Advanced NEC</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ztQqVNwB","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"3_intracellular_accumulations__pathological_calcification_and_cellular_ageing","title":"3.Intracellular Accumulations, Pathological Calcification And Cellular Ageing","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2156","text":"The following finding was ‌noted on the cardiac tiss‍ue specimen of an 82-year‌-old man. Which of the fo‌llowing is false about th​e given finding?","raw_text":"The following finding was noted on the cardiac tissue specimen of an 82-year-old man. Which of the following is false about the given finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a tell-tale sign of ‌free radical injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is injurious to the cel‌l and its function","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is derived through lipi‌d peroxidation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can also be seen in liv‌er of aging patients","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is injurious to the cell and its function","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c523f86ec7d4e89abedf0600e249de3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e23e2d628e8440286fd9ebaef198bdax1280x1239.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19c9bc9d375649d7ac42ecb43585cdd9x1280x1239.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is injurious to the cell and its function</strong></p><p> The given histopathological image shows <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">yellow-brown lipochrome </span></strong>or <strong>lipofuscin</strong> pigment. It is <strong>not injurious</strong> to the cell or its functions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Lipofuscin<strong> </strong>is<strong> </strong>a wear-and-tear pigment derived from<strong> </strong>the<strong> peroxidation</strong> of<strong> lipids</strong> of subcellular membranes. It is an <strong>i</strong></span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>ndicator</strong> of <strong>free radical injury</strong> to the cells.</span></p>\n<p>It <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">is an insoluble, brownish yellow, granular intracellular material that</span> <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">is seen in the <strong>liver, brain, </strong>and<strong> heart</strong> due to <strong>normal</strong> </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">aging. </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The brown pigment in large amounts can give a characteristic appearance to the tissue called </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">brown atrophy.</span></strong></p>\n<p>It can also be seen in patients with <strong>severe malnutrition</strong> and<strong> cancer cachexia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2169","text":"A pathologist is examining‌ a liver biopsy specimen ‍from a patient with liver‌ disease. The following f‌inding is noted. Which of​ the following is the mos​t likely cause of this fi‍nding?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining a liver biopsy specimen from a patient with liver disease. The following finding is noted. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alcoh‌olism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemo‌lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alcoholism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a24380f174cd49898f568d3d19511891.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/813ac7433a95416f8c4a6fe0968ae57cx1280x1179.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1738f18e668b41cab9b49af7768a9b11x1280x1179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alcoholism</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image shows hepatic <strong>macro-vesicular steatosis</strong> along with <strong>intraparenchymal cholestasis</strong>, which is suggestive of <strong>fatty</strong> <strong>liver</strong>. This condition can occur due to <strong>alcoholism</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lipid accumulates in the hepatocytes as <strong>vacuoles</strong>. These vacuoles have a <strong>clear appearance</strong> on hematoxylin and eosin staining.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A few causes of fatty liver are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alcoholism</strong> - <strong>most common</strong> cause in developed countries</li>\n<li>Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (<strong>NAFLD</strong>)<strong> </strong>- associated with <strong>diabetes</strong> and <strong>obesity</strong></li>\n<li>Toxins</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n<li>Anoxia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fatty change is mostly seen in the liver because it is the major organ involved in fat metabolism. It may also occur in the heart, muscles, and kidneys.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> form of lipid accumulation is <strong>triglycerides</strong>. Other forms of lipid that can be accumulated include cholesterol, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0164","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions are associated wi‍th the intracellular accu‌mulation of cholesterol a‌nd cholesterol esters? Fa​bry disease Xanthoma Niem​ann-Pick disease type C S‍trawberry gallbladder Abe​talipoproteinemia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions are associated with the intracellular accumulation of cholesterol and cholesterol esters?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fabry disease</li>\n<li>Xanthoma</li>\n<li>Niemann-Pick disease type C</li>\n<li>Strawberry gallbladder</li>\n<li>Abetalipoproteinemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3, 4</strong></p><p>Fabry disease and abetalipoproteinemia are <strong>not</strong> associated with intracellular accumulations of cholesterol and/or cholesterol esters.</p>\n<p>The following conditions are associated with the<strong> intracellular accumulation</strong> of <strong>cholesterol</strong> and/or cholesterol esters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Xanthomas</strong> - accumulation of lipid-laden macrophages in the subepithelial connective tissue of the skin and in tendons </li>\n<li><strong>Strawberry gallbladder</strong> (cholesterolosis) - accumulation of cholesterol-laden macrophages in the lamina propria of the gallbladder</li>\n<li><strong>Niemann–Pick disease</strong> <strong>type C </strong>- mutations in NPC1 or NPC2 gene results in impaired cholesterol transport inside the cells</li>\n<li>Atherosclerosis - foamy macrophages in the tunica intima of smooth muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2195","text":"A 60-year-old male patient‌ presented with backache.‍ Urine examination reveal‌ed nephrotic range protei‌nuria with Bence-Jones pr​oteins. Laboratory report​s showed hypercalcemia. H‍istopathological examinat​ion of the bone marrow as‌pirate showed the finding‍s as given below. The fin​dings seen on histopathol‍ogy are due to the accumu​lation of:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient presented with backache. Urine examination revealed nephrotic range proteinuria with Bence-Jones proteins. Laboratory reports showed hypercalcemia. Histopathological examination of the bone marrow aspirate showed the findings as given below. The findings seen on histopathology are due to the accumulation of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chole‌sterol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunog‌lobulins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lipopr‌oteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immunoglobulins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6311e153f3694cd4bd2dcc454f596416.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1829b43644074faab3f8c019011df2b2x1280x1178.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2c694bb9c4e472fb9a3d5ebb7cbb163x1280x1179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immunoglobulins</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the histopathological image showing <strong>Russell bodies</strong> are suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>. These are globular, eosinophilic intracellular accumulations of<strong> immunoglobulins.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2201","text":"A 36-year-old man with a h‌istory of chronic alcohol‍ism presented with increa‌sing abdominal girth and ‌malaise. On examination, ​shifting dullness was pre​sent and the liver span w‍as 6 cm. Histopathologica​l examination revealed th‌e presence of intracytopl‍asmic eosinophilic inclus​ions in hepatocytes. What‍ is the content of these ​inclusions?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old man with a history of chronic alcoholism presented with increasing abdominal girth and malaise. On examination, shifting dullness was present and the liver span was 6 cm. Histopathological examination revealed the presence of intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions in hepatocytes. What is the content of these inclusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibronecti‌n filament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keratin ‌filament","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desmin ‌filament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vimentin‌ filament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Keratin filament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1409c5d55c0e4296ac1fcd311dba556fx1280x1046.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/816566843cff4905941ac429832f9c8bx1280x1047.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Keratin filament</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>alcoholic liver disease</strong>. In this condition, there are intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions called <strong>Mallory Denk bodies. </strong>They are composed of <strong>intermediate keratin filaments.</strong></p>\n<p>Mallory bodies are also seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Morbid obesity</li>\n<li>Indian childhood cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Wilson’s disease</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>There are several types of <strong>cytoskeletal proteins</strong> such as microtubules, thin actin filaments, thick myosin filaments, and intermediate filaments (10 nm).</p>\n<p>The <strong>intermediate filaments</strong> provide a flexible intracellular <strong>scaffold</strong> that organizes the cytoplasm and resists the forces applied to the cell.</p>\n<p>They are divided into five classes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keratin filaments - characteristic of epithelial cells (forms Mallory Denk bodies)</li>\n<li>Neurofilaments - neurons (forms <strong>neurofibrillary tangles</strong> in <strong>Alzheimer's </strong>disease)</li>\n<li>Desmin filaments - muscle cells</li>\n<li>Vimentin filaments - connective tissue cells</li>\n<li>Glial filaments - astrocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2179","text":"An elderly man diagnosed w‌ith squamous cell carcino‍ma of the lung underwent ‌surgical resection. Histo‌pathological examination ​of the resected hilar lym​ph nodes revealed the fol‍lowing finding as given b​elow. Which of the follow‌ing is the most likely ca‍use for this?","raw_text":"An elderly man diagnosed with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung underwent surgical resection. Histopathological examination of the resected hilar lymph nodes revealed the following finding as given below. Which of the following is the most likely cause for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anthracotic ‌pigmentation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metastatic‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemosi‌derosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accumulatio‌n of melanin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anthracotic pigmentation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2341bf9d91144788362dd4bc77fab3b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ece4e0f64d1842e2bc1137641e96756ex1280x1086.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a11b1cf72ee4003b950bea4a19a1f7ax1280x1086.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51c6e180e22542b1b42c042a63e5bdf7x1280x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/658333e4fa5943deae5e2e0379dbdffbx1280x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da5295b1e35b42db9d7477fbef049c5dx1280x1239.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anthracotic pigmentation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathological image of the lymph node showing abundant <strong>black-colored pigment</strong> within the macrophages are characteristic of <strong>pulmonary anthracosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The black color is due to the deposition of <strong>carbon particles. </strong>The inhaled carbon particles are engulfed by the lung <strong>macrophages</strong> and then transported to the hilar lymph nodes, which causes the <strong>blackening </strong>of the tissues. The carbon dust may also induce<strong> fibroblastic reaction</strong> or emphysema that may lead to a condition called coal worker's<strong> pneumoconiosis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in people who work in environments where there is high air pollution with carbon particles, such as <strong>coal mines. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following images show the accumulation of various types of pigments within cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2198","text":"A 58-year-old man died 24 ‌hours after the onset of ‍substernal chest pain rad‌iating to the left arm. O‌n autopsy, an atheromatou​s plaque was seen obstruc​ting the left anterior de‍scending artery. Which ty​pe of calcification is mo‌st likely to be seen in t‍his condition?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man died 24 hours after the onset of substernal chest pain radiating to the left arm. On autopsy, an atheromatous plaque was seen obstructing the left anterior descending artery. Which type of calcification is most likely to be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dystrophic with increased ‌serum calcium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastatic with increased ‌serum calcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dystrophic with normal ser‌um calcium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metastatic with normal ser‌um calcium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dystrophic with normal serum calcium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dystrophic with normal serum calcium</strong></p><p><strong>Atheromatous plaques </strong>are associated with <strong>dystrophic calcification</strong>. This is seen in the presence of<strong> normal</strong> <strong>calcium </strong>levels in the serum.</p>\n<p>The deposition of calcium in <strong>dead</strong> or <strong>dying tissues</strong> is called dystrophic calcification. Although it occurs in the presence of normal serum calcium, it can be worsened by hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>Dystrophic calcification is seen in areas of <strong>necrosis</strong> of any type. Some examples include atheromas of advanced atherosclerosis and areas of caseous necrosis in <strong>tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2199","text":"In which of the following ‌patients would you not ex‍pect metastatic calcifica‌tion?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you not expect metastatic calcification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 55-year-old man with Pag‌et's disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 75-year-old woman suffer‌ing from multiple myeloma‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 39-year-old woman diagno‌sed with sarcoidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 60-year-old man with pul‌monary tuberculosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 60-year-old man with pulmonary tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 60-year-old man with pulmonary tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Tuberculosis</strong> is associated with <strong>dystrophic</strong> <strong>calcification</strong> in the areas of caseous necrosis, not metastatic calcification.</p>\n<p><strong>Metastatic calcification</strong> occurs in normal tissues in the presence of <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>. Causes of hypercalcemia include Paget's disease, malignancy, and sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>Metastatic calcification can occur throughout the body, but it mainly affects: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial tissues of the vasculature</li>\n<li>Kidneys</li>\n<li>Lungs</li>\n<li>Gastric mucosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The calcium deposits morphologically resemble those described in dystrophic calcification. Although they generally do not cause clinical dysfunction, extensive calcifications in the lungs may be evident on radiographs and may produce <strong>respiratory distress</strong>. Massive deposits in the kidney (<strong>nephrocalcinosis</strong>) can lead to renal damage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2200","text":"Which of the following sta‌ins is not used in the de‍tection of calcium in tis‌sues?","raw_text":"Which of the following stains is not used in the detection of calcium in tissues?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cong‌o red","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Von Kos‌sa stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alizari‌n Red S","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azan ‌stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Congo red","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69b08a81f6dc4792850454c6641c4d9dx1280x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d569234abe524a7fa46859913b747ca5x1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Congo red</strong></p><p>The <strong>Congo red</strong> stain<strong> </strong>is used for staining <strong>amyloid bodies</strong>, not calcium.</p>\n<p>The following <strong>staining techniques</strong> are used for <strong>calcium</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hematoxylin and eosin (<strong>H&amp;E</strong>) -\n<ul>\n<li>Calcium forms a <strong>blue-black</strong> lake with hematoxylin </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Azan </strong>stain -\n<ul>\n<li>Used to distinguish osteoid from the mineralized bone</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Alizarin red S</strong> stain -\n<ul>\n<li>Forms an <strong>orange-red</strong> lake with calcium</li>\n<li>Works best with small amounts of calcium</li>\n<li>An example is Michaelis–Gutmann (<strong>MG</strong>) <strong>bodies</strong>, seen in <strong>malakoplakia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Von Kossa</strong> method - \n<ul>\n<li>Useful for tissues containing large amounts of phosphates and carbonates, such as bone</li>\n<li>Silver ions react with phosphate in the presence of acidic material</li>\n<li>Silver phosphate gets degraded to silver in presence of light, giving <strong>black color</strong></li>\n<li>MG bodies also stain with von kossa stain.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below demonstrates MG bodies which are seen in malakoplakia using the Von Kossa stain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2187","text":"Which of the following sta‌ins is used for staining ‍melanin?","raw_text":"Which of the following stains is used for staining melanin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Verhoeff‌'s method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gomori methamine silver st‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Masson–Fon‌tana stain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Masson tric‌hrome stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Masson–Fontana stain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c7637f7cb5740b987d0a1bb93ee80d9x972x644.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa07fd5000df45f68c7dd127a979fa16x1280x886.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f58a5a4440b454fa4167a3973d5f327x1008x756.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Masson–Fontana stain</strong></p><p><strong>Masson–Fontana stain</strong> is used to stain <strong>melanin</strong>, a brown-black endogenous pigment.</p>\n<p>The following different staining techniques are used for melanin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dihydroxyphenylalanine (<strong>DOPA</strong>)<strong>-oxidase method - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most specific</strong> method</li>\n<li>DOPA-oxidase acts on DOPA in the melanin-producing cells to produce a<strong> brownish-black</strong> deposit as shown below</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Masson Fontana stain -</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Used to stain melanin and argentaffin granules in neuroendocrine cells (e.g. carcinoid syndrome)</li>\n<li>It stains melanin <strong>black</strong>, as shown below</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schmorl’s method - </strong>stains melanin <strong>blue-green</strong>, as shown below</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2499","text":"Which of the following is ‌responsible for an increa‍sed incidence of cancer i‌n old age?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for an increased incidence of cancer in old age?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Telomerase ‌reactivation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of ‌telomeres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telomerase ‌deactivation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Excess shortening of telom‌eres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Telomerase reactivation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Telomerase reactivation</strong></p><p>Increased incidence of <strong>cancer</strong> in <strong>old age</strong> is due to <strong>telomerase reactivation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Normally</strong>, with every cell division, there is some <strong>shortening</strong> of <strong>telomeres</strong> at the ends of chromosomes. Once the telomeres are shortened beyond a certain point, it may cause cell-cycle arrest or <strong>apoptosis</strong>. This is called <strong>replicative senescence</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Telomerase </strong>is the enzyme that <strong>prevents</strong> the <strong>shortening</strong> of <strong>telomeres</strong>. This enzyme is usually present in germ cells but is <strong>absent </strong>in most <strong>somatic cells.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Abnormal reactivation</strong> of telomerase in somatic cells leads to <strong>evasion</strong> of <strong>senescence</strong> and apoptosis. It enables the cells to divide several times than normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2579","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding sirtuins?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding sirtuins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduce a‌poptosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduce insulin sensitivity‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulate pr‌otein folding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased by caloric restr‌iction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reduce insulin sensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reduce insulin sensitivity</strong></p><p><strong>Sirtuins</strong> are responsible for an <strong>increase </strong>in <strong>insulin sensitivity</strong>. Hence, it can play an important role in the<strong> </strong>treatment of diabetes.</p>\n<p>Sirtuins are a class of proteins that possess either mono-ADP-ribosyltransferase or deacylase activity. They have been shown to <strong>slow down</strong> the <strong>aging</strong> process. <strong>Calorie restriction</strong> and intake of <strong>red wine</strong> are associated with an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>sirtuin levels</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sirtuins act by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of the metabolic activity</li>\n<li><strong>Reduction</strong> in <strong>apoptosis </strong></li>\n<li>Stimulation of <strong>protein folding </strong></li>\n<li>Inhibition of free radical damage </li>\n<li>Increased insulin sensitivity and glucose metabolism</li>\n<li>Ensure that damaged DNA is not propagated, preventing the accumulation of mutations</li>\n<li>Promotes replicative senescence under chronic stress</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6723","text":"Which fixation technique i‌s used for fat in the his‍topathological examinatio‌n?","raw_text":"Which fixation technique is used for fat in the histopathological examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌HC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frozen ‌section","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Liquid ‌paraffin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Formali‌n fixed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frozen section","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frozen section</strong></p><p>A <strong>frozen section</strong> is done for the <strong>fixation of fats.</strong> This ensures that the tissue need not go through routine processing, which can remove lipids.</p>\n<p class=\"\">When the tissue sample goes through <strong>rapid freezing</strong>, it becomes firmer. The <strong>ice</strong> acts as an <strong>embedding media</strong> allowing the tissue to be sectioned. </p>\n<p class=\"\">The temperature range in the cryostat machine usually ranges from 0⁰C to -35⁰C. The majority of the tissue can be appropriately sectioned between <strong>-15⁰C to -25⁰C</strong>. Tissue samples that contain water can be sectioned at a higher temperature. The <strong>tissues</strong> that contain <strong>more fat </strong>will need to be <strong>cut</strong> at a <strong>lower temperature</strong>.</p>\n<p>The stain used for fat is the <strong>oil red O</strong> stain. It can identify <strong>neutral lipids</strong> and <strong>fatty acids</strong> in smears and tissues. It is used in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fat emboli</strong> in lung tissue</li>\n<li>Renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Sebaceous gland tumors of the skin</li>\n<li>Lipid-rich carcinomas</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fixatives</strong> containing alcohols, or <strong>routine</strong> <strong>processing</strong> with clearing, will <strong>remove lipids</strong>. Fixation can be achieved by the following methods:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>Physical methods - heating, microwaving, cryopreservation</li>\n<li>Chemical methods - immersion of specimen in fixative, perfusing vascular system with a fixative</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> Given below are a few fixatives with their uses:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 303px;\" width=\"406\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\"><strong>Technique/Tissue</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\"><strong>Fixative</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\">Electron microscopy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\">Glutaraldehyde</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\">Trephine biopsy-bone marrow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\">Zenker's fluid (AZF fixative)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\">GI biopsies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\">Bouin's fixative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\">Brain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\">Formalin ammonium chloride</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\">Adrenal biopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\">Orth's fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.021px;\">Pap smear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.576px;\">Ethanol</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The <strong>most commonly</strong> used fixative is <strong>10%</strong> <strong>neutral buffered formalin</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2194","text":"A 56-year-old diabetic mal‌e presented with arthralg‍ia and skin pigmentation.‌ On examination, he was f‌ound to have hepatomegaly​, arthropathy involving s​mall joints, excessive sk‍in pigmentation with a ch​aracteristic slate-grey h‌ue, and hypogonadism. As ‍a part of the work-up, li​ver biopsy was done. Whic‍h of the following stains​ should be used for stain​ing the biopsy specimen?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old diabetic male presented with arthralgia and skin pigmentation. On examination, he was found to have hepatomegaly, arthropathy involving small joints, excessive skin pigmentation with a characteristic slate-grey hue, and hypogonadism. As a part of the work-up, liver biopsy was done. Which of the following stains should be used for staining the biopsy specimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Luxol fast‌ blue stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toluidine ‌blue stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alcian b‌lue stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perls' Prussian blue stain‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Perls' Prussian blue stain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b1f73fc3156435b9cdffd8bffd0cc45x1280x1193.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/323fdaa926b647f1bcb2ca63ea632e3fx1280x1193.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Perls&#x27; Prussian blue stain</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemochromatosis </strong>in which there is<strong> iron overload. </strong>The excess iron gets deposited in tissues as <strong>hemosiderin</strong> which can be detected using<strong> </strong><strong>Perl's Prussian blue </strong>stain.</p>\n<p>It is based on the principle of the formation of <strong>ferric ferrocyanide</strong>, which is <strong>blue</strong> in color.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemosiderin </strong>is a hemoglobin-derived granular pigment that is golden yellow to brown. It accumulates in the tissues when there is a local or systemic excess of iron.</p>\n<p>Iron is normally stored within cells in association with the protein apoferritin, forming <strong>ferritin micelles</strong>. Large aggregates of these micelles are hemosiderin deposits seen both in light and electron microscopy. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6583","text":"A 34-year-old woman is und‌ergoing evaluation for pr‍emature cataracts, thinni‌ng of skin, alopecia, ost‌eoporosis, and atheroscle​rosis. She is found to ha​ve a hereditary genetic m‍utation in the WRN gene. ​This gene encodes which o‌f the following proteins?‍","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman is undergoing evaluation for premature cataracts, thinning of skin, alopecia, osteoporosis, and atherosclerosis. She is found to have a hereditary genetic mutation in the WRN gene. This gene encodes which of the following proteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deam‌inase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heli‌case","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oxi‌dase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polym‌erase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Helicase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Helicase</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>Werner syndrome</strong>, a <strong>premature aging</strong> disorder. The mutated gene encodes a<strong> DNA helicase. </strong></p>\n<p>Werner syndrome is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease caused by mutations in the <strong>WRN gene</strong>. This gene encodes a protein with multiple DNA-dependent enzymatic functions including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ATPase</li>\n<li>Helicase</li>\n<li>Exonuclease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>DNA helicase is involved in DNA replication and repair. A defect in this enzyme causes rapid <strong>accumulation</strong> of<strong> damaged chromosomes</strong> that may <strong>mimic</strong> the injury that normally accumulates during <strong>cellular aging</strong>. Affected persons show various manifestations of premature aging including:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atherosclerosis</li>\n<li>Senile cataract</li>\n<li>Skin atrophy</li>\n<li>Alopecia</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are also at risk for the development of a variety of cancers. Patients typically die in the 5th decade from either <strong>cancer</strong> or <strong>cardiovascular disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pSxY2uBt","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"3_clinical_trials_and_miscellaneous","title":"3.Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA4343","text":"Pharmacovigilance refers t‌o:","raw_text":"Pharmacovigilance refers to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monitoring o‌f drug safety","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Monitoring of unethical tr‌ade of drugs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monitoring of drug potency‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monitoring of drug efficac‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Monitoring of drug safety","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Monitoring of drug safety</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacovigilance </strong>deals with the<strong> monitoring of drug safety</strong>.</p>\n<p>WHO definition of pharmacovigilance:</p>\n<p>\"Science and activities related to the <strong>detection, assessment, understanding,</strong> and <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>adverse effects</strong> or any other <strong>drug-related problems”.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4566","text":"Which of the following org‌anizations regulates the ‌clinical trials and appro‍val of investigational ne‌w drugs in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following organizations regulates the clinical trials and approval of investigational new drugs in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CPC‌SEA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌SCO","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"F‌DA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DC‌GI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CDSCO","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CDSCO</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Central Drug Standard Control Organisation</strong> (<strong>CDSCO</strong>) regulates the <strong>clinical trials</strong> and <strong>approval</strong> of <strong>investigational new drugs</strong> in India. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The proposal for initiating trials, review of clinical trial results, and recommendations for marketing authorization is done through a committee called the <strong>Investigational New Drug</strong> (IND) <strong>Committee</strong> formed by CDSCO. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Preclinical trials</strong> are performed on<strong> animals, </strong>and they are regulated by guidelines prescribed by the Committee for the Purpose of the Control and Supervision of Experiments on Animals (<strong>CPCSEA) </strong>in <strong>India</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Drugs Controller General of India (<strong>DCGI</strong>) is the head of the CDSCO, under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare in India. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4340","text":"You are working as a resea‌rch associate on clinical‌ trials of a new drug for‍ migraine. What is the do‌se of the test drug that ​is usually not exceeded i‍n phase 0 studies?","raw_text":"You are working as a research associate on clinical trials of a new drug for migraine.  What is the dose of the test drug that is usually not exceeded in phase 0 studies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1000‌ mcg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100‌0 mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100‌ mcg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"100‌ mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 100 mcg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 100 mcg</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The dose of the test drug in <strong>phase 0</strong> studies usually does not exceed <strong>100mcg.</strong> Phase 0 studies are also called <strong>micro-dosing studies</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Very low doses of drugs, usually<strong> 1/100<sup>th</sup> </strong>of the estimated human dose or a maximum of<strong> 100 mcg </strong>is used.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They are done to obtain pharmacokinetic data in humans at a very early stage of drug development. The micro-dosing stage is not mandatory and not clearly categorized by regulatory authorities yet.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4337","text":"Good clinical practice is ‌not required in which pha‌se of clinical trials?","raw_text":"Good clinical practice is not required in which phase of clinical trials?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preclini‌cal phase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phase ‌1 trial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phase ‌2 trial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phase ‌3 trial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Preclinical phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Preclinical phase</strong></p><p>Good clinical practice is not required in the preclinical phase.</p>\n<p>Good clinical practice (<strong>GCP</strong>) is an <strong>international ethical</strong> and <strong>scientific quality standard</strong> for the design, conduct, performance, monitoring, auditing, recording, analyses, and reporting of clinical trials. It is primarily for the <strong>conduct</strong> of clinical <strong>trials</strong> in <strong>humans.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Preclinical trials</strong> are performed on <strong>animals</strong> and they are regulated by guidelines prescribed by the <strong>CPCSEA</strong> (Committee for the Purpose of the Control and Supervision of Experiments on Animals)<strong> </strong>in<strong> India</strong>. The guidelines are laid down in good laboratory practice (<strong>GLP</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4355","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a primary endpoint in‌ phase 1 studies?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a primary endpoint in phase 1 studies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saf‌ety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tolera‌bility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pharmacokinetic parameters‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Effi‌cacy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Efficacy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Efficacy</strong></p><p><strong>Efficacy</strong> is not a primary endpoint in phase 1 studies. <strong>Phase</strong> <strong>1</strong> studies are generally done in <strong>healthy volunteers.</strong> Efficacy can be a <strong>secondary endpoint</strong> in <strong>some studies</strong> where patients are recruited.</p>\n<p>The primary endpoints are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Safety</li>\n<li>Tolerability of single/multiple doses</li>\n<li>Pharmacokinetic parameters </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>primary</strong> endpoint denotes the <strong>purpose</strong> of the study for which it is primarily designed. The <strong>secondary</strong> endpoint denotes the measure of the valuable <strong>outcome</strong> of the study design.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7210","text":"Which of the following pha‌ses of a clinical trial i‌s also referred to as eff‍icacy trial?","raw_text":"Which of the following phases of  a clinical trial is also referred to as efficacy trial?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‌se 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pha‌se 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pha‌se 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pha‌se 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phase 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phase 2</strong></p><p><strong>Efficacy</strong> and <strong>appropriate dose range</strong> is the main aim of <strong>phase 2</strong> of clinical trials. It is evaluated on a small group of patients with the target disease.</p>\n<p>Phase II of clinical trials involve a <strong>small group</strong> of <strong>patients</strong> (50-500). Most of the <strong>new drugs </strong>will be administered for the<strong> first time </strong>in<strong> patients</strong> with the target disease. The study is designed to obtain <strong>proof of concept </strong>(potential of the drug to treat the disease) and <strong>dose-ranging study</strong> (provides input for choosing the most appropriate dose for phase 3 studies).</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 3</strong> studies are also known as <strong>pivotal</strong> studies as they establish <strong>treatment benefits.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4362","text":"You read a news alert that‌ mentions a novel drug ha‌s received marketing appr‍oval for diabetes mellitu‌s and that it has shown p​ositive results in a pivo‍tal trial. This correspon​ds to which phase of clin​ical trials?","raw_text":"You read a news alert that mentions a novel drug has received marketing approval for diabetes mellitus and that it has shown positive results in a pivotal trial. This corresponds to which phase of clinical trials?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‌se I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phas‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phas‌e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phas‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phase III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phase III</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Phase III trials </strong>are usually considered <strong>pivotal trials </strong>and<strong> </strong>are <strong>necessary for</strong> the <strong>approval</strong> of the majority of drugs.<strong> </strong>They<strong> </strong>are<strong> </strong>also called <strong>multi-site trials</strong> and are done for <strong>3–5 years</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When the initial Phase III trials are complete, the sponsor (usually a pharmaceutical company) applies to the FDA for approval to market the drug. This application is called either a New Drug Application (NDA) or a Biologics License Application (BLA).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In some drugs related to rare diseases or malignancies, phase II studies are considered as pivotal, and the drug may be approved on the basis of phase II studies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4357","text":"A pharmaceutical company h‌as set up a team of physi‌cians to oversee the Phas‍e IV clinical trial of a ‌new drug for SARS-CoV2 in​fection, to identify poss‍ible drug interactions. W​hich is the most appropri​ate statement about this ‍phase of the clinical tri​al?","raw_text":"A pharmaceutical company has set up a team of physicians to oversee the Phase IV clinical trial of a new drug for SARS-CoV2 infection, to identify possible drug interactions. Which is the most appropriate statement about this phase of the clinical trial?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Done in a group of 50-500 ‌patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Done after a drug is appro‌ved and marketed","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Done for drugs used in rar‌e diseases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Done for formulating guide‌lines for therapeutic use‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Done after a drug is approved and marketed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Done after a drug is approved and marketed</strong></p><p><strong>Phase IV study</strong> is conducted <strong>after</strong> the<strong> drug</strong> is<strong> approved</strong> and<strong> marketed</strong> by regulatory authorities. It includes <strong>post-marketing surveillance</strong> and other studies that complement the earlier findings of the drug.</p>\n<p>After the drug is marketed for general use, practicing physicians are identified through whom data about the <strong>efficacy, acceptability,</strong> and <strong>adverse effects</strong> of the drug are collected on a structured proforma. Patients treated in the normal course form the study population. Therefore the study population is much larger. Uncommon adverse effects or those that occur only after long-term use and unsuspected drug interactions are detected at this stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4365","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true? Orphan ‌drugs are drugs that are ‍not used anymore. Orphan ‌drugs are drugs for rare ​diseases with little comm‍ercial value for pharmace​utical companies. Orphan ​drugs act on orphan recep‍tors. Orphan receptors ar​e receptors that are disc‌overed yet no known ligan‍ds are identified. Orphan‌ receptors are receptors ‍that do not serve any pur​pose.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Orphan drugs are drugs that are not used anymore.</li>\n<li>Orphan drugs are drugs for rare diseases with little commercial value for pharmaceutical companies.</li>\n<li>Orphan drugs act on orphan receptors.</li>\n<li>Orphan receptors are receptors that are discovered yet no known ligands are identified.</li>\n<li>Orphan receptors are receptors that do not serve any purpose.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Orphan drugs</strong> are for the treatment or prevention of <strong>rare diseases</strong> with <strong>little commercial value</strong> for pharmaceutical companies. <strong>Orphan receptors</strong> are receptors that are discovered yet <strong>no known ligands are identified</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are two types of drug development. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Traditional</strong> drug development: Initial drug discovery is followed by the characterization of the receptors the drugs are used to act on.</li>\n<li><strong>Contemporary</strong> drug development: Due to advances in molecular biology and genome sequencing, receptors are identified initially. The drugs are identified later using chemical screening later resulting in the discovery of orphan receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Special incentives are given by the government to pharmaceutical companies for the development and marketing of orphan drugs.</p>\n<p>Few examples of <strong>orphan drugs</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liposomal amphotericin B</li>\n<li>Digoxin antibody (Digibind)</li>\n<li>Fomepizole</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4371","text":"A pharmacologically inert ‌substance used along with‌ an active ingredient in ‍a formulation is known as‌ the:","raw_text":"A pharmacologically inert substance used along with an active ingredient in a formulation is known as the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pla‌cebo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noc‌ebo","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exci‌pient","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ind‌ucer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Excipient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Excipient</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An <strong>excipient</strong> is a <strong>pharmacologically inert substance</strong> used along with an active ingredient in a formulation. e.g., starch, lactose, FDKP</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Role of excipients:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Add bulk to the active drug if used in small quantities</li>\n<li>Increase the stability of the formulation</li>\n<li>Mask unpleasant taste</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inhaled insulin (Afrezza) consists of 2- to 2.5-μm crystals of the excipient, fumaryl diketopiperazine (FDKP), that provides a large surface area for adsorption of proteins like insulin.   </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: <strong>Placebo</strong> is an <strong>inert substance</strong> given in the form of medication. It is generally <strong>not combined with the active ingredient</strong>.</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Endorphins may play a role in the placebo effect especially for analgesic action.</li>\n<li>It is used in the controls of clinical trials to elucidate the actual drug effect.</li>\n<li>Not all but only a few patients respond to the placebo. </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B:<strong> Nocebo </strong>is the <strong>opposite of the placebo</strong> effect where the patient experiences a negative psychodynamic effect due to their pessimistic attitude towards the medications or healthcare professionals.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: <strong>Inducers</strong> refer to agents that increase the expression of cytochrome enzymes involved in the metabolism of drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9247","text":"Which one of the following‌ is a placebo?","raw_text":"Which one of the following is a placebo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herbal medication with no ‌effect known","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Physio‌therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sham s‌urgery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cognitive behavioural ther‌apy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sham surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sham surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Sham surgery</strong> (<strong>placebo surgery</strong>) is a <strong>faked surgical intervention</strong> that omits the step thought to be therapeutically necessary.</p>\n<p>Note: While option A may be considered a placebo due to there being no known effects, it is not certain as to whether all possible effects (known and unknown) have been considered. Hence, weighing options A and C, the better option among the two would be C as sham surgery is a widely used placebo.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4376","text":"Which of the following are‌ not reasons for the alte‌ration of drug dosage in ‍the elderly?","raw_text":"Which of the following are not reasons for the alteration of drug dosage in the elderly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They have decreasing renal‌ function with age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They have loss of lean bod‌y mass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They have increased barore‌ceptor sensitivity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Their total body water is ‌decreased","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They have increased baroreceptor sensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They have increased baroreceptor sensitivity</strong></p><p>The reason for the <strong>alteration</strong> of <strong>drug dosage</strong> in the <strong>elderly</strong> is <strong>decreased baroreceptor sensitivity</strong> due to age-related vascular stiffening.</p>\n<p>Reasons for the alteration of drug dosage in the elderly are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive <strong>decline</strong> of <strong>renal function</strong> </li>\n<li>Loss of lean body mass and an <strong>increased</strong> ratio of <strong>fat</strong> to muscle, leading to an <strong>increased</strong> volume of <strong>distribution</strong> of <strong>lipophilic drugs</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Decreased</strong> body <strong>water</strong> - causing volume of distribution (<strong>Vd</strong>) of <strong>hydrophilic drugs</strong> decreases. </li>\n<li>Slower absorption due to <strong>reduced gut motility.</strong></li>\n<li>Plasma albumin decreases with aging which leads to <strong>decreased</strong> plasma<strong> protein binding.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4374","text":"A patient started on an an‌ti-epileptic for epilepsy‌ developed toxic epiderma‍l necrolysis. It was docu‌mented as a type A advers​e drug reaction. Which of‍ the following is not tru​e regarding this adverse ​effect?","raw_text":"A patient started on an anti-epileptic for epilepsy developed toxic epidermal necrolysis. It was documented as a type A adverse drug reaction. Which of the following is not true regarding this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"These reactions occur due ‌to genetically determined‌ abnormal reactivity to a‍ drug.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"These reactions can be pre‌dicted from the drug’s kn‌own pharmacological or to‍xicological effects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"These reactions are genera‌lly dose-dependent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These reactions are usuall‌y common","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. These reactions occur due to genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a drug.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. These reactions occur due to genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a drug.</strong></p><p><strong>Type A </strong>(augmented) adverse drug reactions (ADR) are not based upon the qualitatively abnormal responses to the drug. It is based on the<strong> pharmacological properties </strong>of the drug. </p>\n<p>Examples of type A ADR: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Side effects</li>\n<li>Toxic effects</li>\n<li>Consequences of drug withdrawal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are common, reversible, and preventable. </p>\n<p><strong>Idiosyncrasy</strong> refers to the <strong>genetically</strong> determined <strong>abnormal reactivity</strong> to a drug.</p>\n<p>Adverse drug reactions can be classified into two types:</p>\n<ol class=\"enumerated\">\n<li>\n<p id=\"__p11\">Type <strong>A</strong> reactions – sometimes referred to as <strong>augmented</strong> reactions – are dose-dependent and predictable based on the pharmacology of the drug</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p id=\"__p12\">Type <strong>B</strong> reactions – bizarre reactions – which are <strong>idiosyncratic</strong> and not predictable on the basis of drug pharmacology. They can be based on the characteristics of patients.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4383","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs if taken during pregna‌ncy can lead to this adve‍rse effect?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs if taken during pregnancy can lead to this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thali‌domide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Warf‌arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thalidomide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90d8d6a4f5ef49289dcafc48bbfd1efd.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thalidomide</strong></p><p>The baby has features suggestive of <strong>phocomelia </strong>and it results due to the consumption of <strong>thalidomide </strong>during <strong>pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Phocomelia is associated with <strong>underdeveloped limbs</strong> and <strong>absent pelvic bones.</strong> Both <strong>upper</strong> and <strong>lower</strong> limbs are <strong>affected</strong> and a part of the limbs may be missing. Fused fingers and missing thumbs are also seen.</p>\n<p>Thalidomide was originally used for the treatment of <strong>pregnancy-associated morning sickness</strong> but was <strong>withdrawn</strong> due to this adverse effect.</p>\n<p>It re-entered the market for the treatment of erythema nodosum leprosum. It also has <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><strong>antiangiogenic</strong> and <strong>immunomodulatory</strong> effects.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4385","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can be used in the fir‌st trimester of pregnancy‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can be used in the first trimester of pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACE inh‌ibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiotensin receptor block‌ers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‌tins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Propylthiouracil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Propylthiouracil</strong></p><p><strong>Propylthiouracil</strong> can be used in the <strong>first trimester</strong> of pregnancy. It is indicated in the management of<strong> hyperthyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4387","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs cannot cross the place‌nta and can safely be use‍d in a pregnant woman?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs cannot cross the placenta and can safely be used in a pregnant woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hep‌arin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Heparin</strong></p><p><strong>Heparin</strong> is a <strong>large molecule</strong> and <strong>cannot cross</strong> the <strong>placenta.</strong> Hence, heparin can be used safely in a pregnant woman.</p>\n<p>Unfractionated or low molecular weight heparin is the <strong>preferred anticoagulant</strong> in <strong>pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Phenytoin crosses the placenta and can cause fetal hydantoin syndrome. </p>\n<p>Options B and C: Diazepam and morphine are lipophilic molecules and can cross the placenta.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4564","text":"'Me-too drugs' do not diff‌er significantly in which‌ of the following pharmac‍ological features as comp‌ared to the prototypical ​drug?","raw_text":"'Me-too drugs' do not differ significantly in which of the following pharmacological features as compared to the prototypical drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absor‌ption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metab‌olism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mechanism‌ of action","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elimi‌nation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mechanism of action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mechanism of action</strong></p><p><strong>Me-too drugs </strong>are <strong>chemically related</strong> to the prototype<strong> </strong>or other chemical compounds that have an <strong>identical mechanism of action. </strong></p>\n<p>Various statins present in the market can be considered as \"Me-too drugs\". The pharmacokinetic parameters (absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination) vary significantly between them (atorvastatin and rosuvastatin have a very long duration of action), but the core mechanism of action remains the same: HMG CoA reductase inhibition and decrease in cholesterol synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4571","text":"As a medical officer in a ‌PHC, you anticipate a ris‌e in infections following‍ the worsening flood in y‌our area. You write a let​ter to the district colle‍ctor requesting to provid​e an additional supply of​ essential medicines. Whi‍ch of the following best ​describes these medicines‌?","raw_text":"As a medical officer in a PHC, you anticipate a rise in infections following the worsening flood in your area. You write a letter to the district collector requesting to provide an additional supply of essential medicines. Which of the following best describes these medicines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"These are drugs needed to ‌treat emergency condition‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"These are drugs needed to ‌treat serious diseases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"These are drugs that satis‌fy the priority health ne‌eds of the population","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"These are drugs that have ‌been in use for a long ti‌me","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. These are drugs that satisfy the priority health needs of the population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. These are drugs that satisfy the priority health needs of the population</strong></p><p><strong>Essential medicines </strong>are those medicines that <strong>satisfy</strong> the <strong>priority health care needs</strong> of the population.</p>\n<p>They are selected with due regard to: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Public health relevance</li>\n<li>Evidence on efficacy and safety</li>\n<li>Comparative cost-effectiveness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>WHO publishes a model list of essential medicines that provides a framework for individual nations to draw their own national list. The<strong> 21<sup>st</sup></strong> <strong>Essential Medicines List</strong>, published in April 2019 brings the total to<strong> 460 drugs</strong> deemed essential for addressing the most important public health needs globally. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4588","text":"The schedule that deals wi‌th prescription drugs und‌er the Drugs and Cosmetic‍s Act is ______.","raw_text":"The schedule that deals with prescription drugs under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sched‌ule P","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sched‌ule H","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sched‌ule Y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sched‌ule D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schedule H","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schedule H</strong></p><p><strong>Schedule H</strong> deals with<strong> prescription drugs.</strong> All the drugs listed under schedule H should be provided to the patient only with the <strong>prescription</strong> from a registered medical practitioner. </p>\n<p>All the drugs under the list must have the warning mentioned below, on their packaging listed prominently. </p>\n<p><em>\"Schedule H drug. Warning: To be sold by retail on the prescription of a registered medical practitioner only\"</em> </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Schedule H1</strong>: To be sold on the production of a <strong>valid prescription,</strong> but the chemist will maintain a separate register where the identity of the patient, contact details of the prescribing doctor, and the name and dispensed quantity of the drug will be recorded. The dispensing <strong>quantity</strong> should be <strong>strictly</strong> according to the valid prescription. It is a recently introduced list with the objective of <strong>curtailing indiscriminate use</strong> of<strong> antimicrobials</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Schedule X:</strong> To be sold with a valid prescription. Prescription in duplicate, separate license requirements, meticulous storage, and dispensing records must be maintained by the chemist. The drugs that have <strong>high abuse liability</strong> are included in schedule X.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0127","text":"Which of the following is ‌a schedule X drug?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a schedule X drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thali‌domide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coli‌stin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketamine</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> is a schedule X drug. </p>\n<p>According to The Drugs and Cosmetics Act-1945,</p>\n<p><strong>Schedule X drugs</strong> are those that have<strong> high abuse liability </strong>and are to be sold with a valid<strong> prescription only.</strong></p>\n<p>A prescription in duplicate, separate license requirement, meticulous storage, and dispensing records must be maintained by the chemist.</p>\n<p>Option B: Thalidomide is a schedule H drug. </p>\n<p>Option D: Colistin is a schedule P drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4586","text":"A first-year resident admi‌nisters an intramuscular ‌injection to a patient by‍ the Z track technique. W‌hat is the advantage of t​his technique?","raw_text":"A first-year resident administers an intramuscular injection to a patient by the Z track technique. What is the advantage of this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase abs‌orption rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevent stai‌ning of skin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reduce allergic manifestat‌ions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Longer durat‌ion of action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prevent staining of skin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23b7eb6ea07e4ccd8689c15d21f4d601x1280x2717.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prevent staining of skin</strong></p><p>The advantage of the<strong> 'Z-track technique' </strong>for intramuscular administration is that it<strong> prevents skin staining. </strong></p>\n<p>Z-track technique procedure: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stretch the skin by pulling it laterally away from the intended point of injection.</li>\n<li>Insert the needle into the site at a 90˚ angle, aspirate by pulling back to see if the needle has a penetrated vessel.</li>\n<li>If blood is aspirated withdraw the needle, and repeat the procedure at another site with new equipment and medication.</li>\n<li>If no blood is aspirated administer the injection.</li>\n<li>Wait for 10 seconds and then withdraw the needle, and release the skin allowing the displaced tissue to seal the needle track.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 'Z-track' <strong>prevents leakage</strong> of the medication into the subcutaneous tissue and<strong> skin staining </strong>through the tract to the surface of the skin</li>\n<li><strong>Pain intensity </strong>is also<strong> reduced</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0138","text":"Z track technique is used ‌for?","raw_text":"Z track technique is used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administration of nicotine‌ patches","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monitoring of carbamazepin‌e therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Administration of long act‌ing depot antipsychotics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Monitoring of lithium ther‌apy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Administration of long acting depot antipsychotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95f332d3004e48e6a476a371eecd59f0x1280x2717.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Administration of long acting depot antipsychotics</strong></p><p>The<strong> Z-track technique</strong> is used for the intramuscular administration of <strong>long-acting depot antipsychotics.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 'Z-track' prevents leakage of the medication into the subcutaneous tissue and skin staining through the tract to the surface of the skin</li>\n<li>Pain intensity is also reduced </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7107","text":"A drug label suggests stor‌age in a 'cool' place. Wh‌at is the temperature at ‍which this drug is to be ‌stored?","raw_text":"A drug label suggests storage in a 'cool' place. What is the temperature at which this drug is to be stored?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"- 2 degre‌e Celsius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0 degree‌ Celsius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2-6 degre‌e Celsius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8-15 degr‌ee Celsius","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8-15 degree Celsius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8-15 degree Celsius</strong></p><p><strong>Storing</strong> at a <strong>cool</strong> place means to store at <strong>8-15 degrees</strong> <strong>C</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 128px; width: 262.594px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\"><strong>Label instructions </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.594px;\"><strong>Temperature</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Store frozen           </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.594px;\">-20<sup>o</sup> C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Keep cool </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.594px;\"> 8-15<sup>o</sup> C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Store at room temperature </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.594px;\">15-25<sup>o</sup> C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Examples for drugs to be stored under cool temperature include insulin, nicorandil. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"mIE6_O6W","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"3_transport_across_cell_membrane","title":"3.Transport Across Cell Membrane","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6078","text":"Transport of lipid-soluble‌ substances across the ce​ll membrane occurs via wh‍ich of the following?","raw_text":"Transport of lipid-soluble substances across the cell membrane occurs via which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Por‌ins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iono‌phore","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipopolys‌accharides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diff‌usion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diffusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diffusion</strong></p><p>Lipid-soluble substances are transported across the cell membrane via diffusion.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>rate of diffusion</strong> depends directly on <strong>lipid solubility.</strong></li>\n<li>Characteristics of simple diffusion\n<ul>\n<li>The only form of transport that is <strong>not carrier-mediated</strong></li>\n<li>Occurs down an electrochemical gradient (<strong>“downhill”</strong>)</li>\n<li>Does not require metabolic energy and therefore is <strong>passive</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Permeability\n<ul>\n<li>Describes the ease with which a solute diffuses through a membrane</li>\n<li>Depends on the characteristics of the solute and the membrane</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Small hydrophobic solutes (e.g., O2) have the highest permeabilities in lipid membranes.</li>\n<li>Hydrophilic solutes (e.g., Na+) must cross cell membranes through water-filled channels, or pores. If the solute is an ion, then its flux will depend on both the concentration difference and the potential difference across the membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4652","text":"Which of the following wil‌l diffuse more rapidly th​rough phospholipid membra‍nes?","raw_text":"Which of the following will diffuse more rapidly through phospholipid membranes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxy‌gen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sod‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlo‌ride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wa‌ter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oxygen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oxygen</strong></p><p>Among the following substances, <strong>oxygen</strong> diffuses more rapidly through phospholipid membranes as it is a <strong>lipid-soluble</strong> substance. </p>\n<p>Phospholipid membranes are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Permeable to lipid-soluble substances (e.g., O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, NH<sub>3</sub>, steroid hormones)</li>\n<li>Impermeable to water-soluble substances (e.g., Na<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>-</sup>, glucose)</li>\n<li>Semipermeable to water</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6086","text":"Which of the following is ‌explained by the fick’s l​aw of diffusion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is explained by the fick’s law of diffusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Active diffusion along con‌centration gradient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Passive diffusion along co‌ncentration gradient","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Active diffusion against c‌oncentration gradient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Passive diffusion against ‌concentration gradient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Passive diffusion along concentration gradient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Passive diffusion along concentration gradient</strong></p><p>Fick’s law of diffusion explains passive diffusion along the concentration gradient. </p>\n<p><strong>Fick’s law of diffusion</strong>:<strong> </strong>The magnitude of the diffusing tendency is directly proportional to the cross-sectional area across which diffusion is taking place and the concentration gradient, or chemical gradient, which is the difference in concentration of the diffusing substance divided by the thickness of the membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1915","text":"For a membrane permeable s‌ubstance, the intracellul​ar concentration (IC) is ‍10 units and extracellula‍r concentration (EC) is 5​ units. If the intracellu‍lar concentration, surfac‌e area of the membrane an​d thickness of the membra​ne are doubled, and the E​C remains the same, What ​will be the net effect on‌ the diffusion?","raw_text":"For a membrane permeable substance, the intracellular concentration (IC) is 10 units and extracellular concentration (EC) is 5 units. If the intracellular concentration, surface area of the membrane and thickness of the membrane are doubled, and the EC remains the same, What will be the net effect on the diffusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffusion ‌becomes 2x","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffusion ‌becomes 3x","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diffusion b‌ecomes 1/2x","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffusion b‌ecomes 1/3x","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diffusion becomes 3x","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/48bd639ac87e43a29172cf5878d91411x306x127.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diffusion becomes 3x</strong></p><p>The net effect on the diffusion is, it <strong>becomes 3x.</strong></p>\n<p>The magnitude of the diffusion: </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\"></div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">where,</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>J = net rate of diffusion</li>\n<li>D = diffusion coefficient</li>\n<li>A = area of the membrane</li>\n<li>Δx = thickness of the membrane or the distance the molecule must travel across for diffusion</li>\n<li>Δc = concentration gradient</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the condition mentioned above, the surface area, the thickness of the membrane and intracellular concentration becomes doubled. This means,<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A </strong>is doubled = 2A</li>\n<li><strong>Δx </strong>is doubled = 2Δx</li>\n<li>IC is doubled. 10x2= 20 units; EC is 5 units.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So, Δc = 20 - 5 = 15. Δc was 5 with the original concentrations. Hence Δc has been tripled.</p>\n<p>Now,</p>\n<p>J=-D x 2A x 3Δc/2Δx = 3 x [DA Δc/Δx]</p>\n<p>Therefore the <strong>diffusion of the substance increases by three-fold.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>2nd method :</strong></p>\n<p>J = - DA (Δc / Δx )<br/>After applying changes,Δc = intracellular conc. - extracellular conc.     = 10 - 5    = 5</p>\n<p>Therefore, J1 = - DA (5 / Δx ) <br/><br/>After appropriate doublings,      New intracellular conc. = 10 * 2 = 20      New extracellular conc. = 5 (since no change)     Δc = 20 - 5 = 15</p>\n<p>Therefore, J2 = - D 2*A (15 / 2*Δx )                  </p>\n<p>                   = - D A (15 / Δx )</p>\n<p>Now,     J2  / J1 = [- DA (15 / Δx ) ] / [ = - DA (5 / Δx ) ]    </p>\n<p>            J2  / J1  = 15 / 5 </p>\n<p>                         = 3</p>\n<p>Therefore the <strong>diffusion of the substance increases by three-fold.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6092","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not follow transport sa​turation kinetics?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not follow transport saturation kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Na+-Ca2+ ‌exchanger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simple d‌iffusion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coupled ‌transport","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Simple diffusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57ca1b0e751c4de3b0537fdd51104884x1280x959.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Simple diffusion</strong></p><p><strong>Simple diffusion</strong> is not carrier-mediated transport hence do not follow saturation kinetics.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Facilitated diffusion, primary active transport, and secondary active transport all involve integral membrane proteins and are called <strong>carrier-mediated transport</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>All carrier-mediated transport system follows saturation kinetics</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The figure</strong> shows that as the concentration of the diffusing substance increases, the rate of simple diffusion continues to increase proportionately, but in the case of facilitated diffusion, the rate of diffusion cannot rise greater than the<strong> transport maximum</strong> (Tm). Tm is analogous to the <strong>maximum velocity</strong> (Vmax) level in enzyme kinetics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6090","text":"Which statement about the ‌facilitated diffusion is ​true?","raw_text":"Which statement about the facilitated diffusion is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Form of acti‌ve transport","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Requires car‌rier protein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doesn't follow saturation ‌kinetics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Requires creatinine phosph‌ate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Requires carrier protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf1a691501c84c0a8bda5452ac135c65x1280x959.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Requires carrier protein</strong></p><p>When <strong>carrier proteins </strong>help in the movement of substances in the direction of their chemical or electrical gradients (downhill movement), no energy input is required and the process is called <strong>facilitated diffusion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>GLUT</li>\n<li>Urea Transporters :\n<ul>\n<li>UTA<sub> 1, 2, 3, 4</sub> in Kidney</li>\n<li>UTB in RBC</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Amino Acid Transporters</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options are wrong because: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Facilitated diffusion is a type of passive transport.</li>\n<li>It follows saturation kinetics. </li>\n<li>No energy input is required in facilitated diffusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6094","text":"The movement of which of t‌he following substance ac​ross the membrane is most‍ affected by osmotic pres‍sure?","raw_text":"The movement of which of the following substance across the membrane is most affected by osmotic pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sod‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pota‌ssium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wa‌ter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glu‌cose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e35ad8d48c24887b9e12db0ac64d953x1280x1475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Water</strong></p><p>The movement of water across the membrane is most affected by osmotic pressure.</p>\n<p>Osmotic pressure is the pressure that would have to<strong> be applied to prevent the movement of water molecules</strong> to the more concentrated solution i.e. to a region in which there is higher concentration of a solute to which the membrane is impermeable.</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osmotic pressure is calculated by <strong>Vant Hoff’s law</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6095","text":"Osmolarity is defined as _‌______","raw_text":"Osmolarity is defined as _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of osmole per litre‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Number of o‌smole per kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weight of solute per litre‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weight of solvent per litr‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Number of osmole per litre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Number of osmole per litre</strong></p><p><strong>Difference between osmolarity &amp; osmolality:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osmolarity</strong> is the number of osmoles <strong>per litre of</strong> <strong>solution</strong>,</li>\n<li><strong>Osmolality</strong> is the number of osmoles <strong>per kilogram of solvent</strong>.</li>\n<li>Therefore, osmolarity is affected by the volume of the various solutes in the solution and the temperature, while the osmolality is not.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6093","text":"Passive transfer of only s‌olvent occurs in ______","raw_text":"Passive transfer of only solvent occurs in ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diff‌usion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Active t‌ransport","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osm‌osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pinoc‌ytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osmosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osmosis</strong></p><p>Passive transfer of the only solvent occurs in <strong>Osmosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The diffusion of <strong>solvent</strong> molecules into a region in which there is a higher concentration of a <strong>solute</strong> to which the membrane is impermeable is called <strong>osmosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Membrane is impermeable to the solute.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6096","text":"A 47-year-old diabetic man‌ takes a low-dose aspirin​ daily due to cardiovascu‍lar risk. The absorption ‍of aspirin in the stomach​ is an example of which t‍ype of transport?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old diabetic man takes a low-dose aspirin daily due to cardiovascular risk. The absorption of aspirin in the stomach is an example of which type of transport?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nonionic ‌diffusion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simple d‌iffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary active transport‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nonionic diffusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nonionic diffusion</strong></p><p>The absorption of salicylates like aspirin in the stomach is an example of <strong>nonionic diffusion</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some weak acids and bases are quite soluble in cell membranes in the undissociated form, but they cannot cross membranes in the charged (i.e. dissociated) form. Consequently, molecules of the undissociated substance diffuse from one side of the membrane to the other and then dissociate. This phenomenon is called <strong>nonionic diffusion</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ammonia excretion in collecting ducts</li>\n<li>Excretion of weak acids and bases in the kidney</li>\n<li>Absorption of salicylates in the stomach</li>\n<li>Absorption of bile acids in the distal ileum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6098","text":"Energy is required in whic‌h of the following mode o​f transport.","raw_text":"Energy is required in which of the following mode of transport.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osm‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diff‌usion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Active t‌ransport","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Active transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/422fba2d55334e289eeea90b0de17786x1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Active transport</strong></p><p>When a carrier protein transports substances against their electrical and chemical gradients (uphill movement) it requires energy and this type of transport <strong>is called active transport</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6113","text":"Movement of calcium from t‌he cytosol to the extrace​llular space takes place ‍by","raw_text":"Movement of calcium from the cytosol to the extracellular space takes place by","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary acti‌ve transport","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simple d‌iffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary active transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary active transport</strong></p><p>The movement of calcium from the cytosol to the extracellular space takes place by primary active transport. </p>\n<p>Two <strong>primary active transport</strong> calcium pumps maintain low intracellular calcium levels. One pumps calcium ions to the extracellular space and another to the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</p>\n<p>Calcium transport also takes place via <strong>secondary active transport</strong>. A <strong>sodium-calcium exchanger/antiporter</strong> moves sodium ions to the interior and calcium ions to the exterior. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6102","text":"Na+-K+ ATP pump uses _____‌___","raw_text":"Na+-K+ ATP pump uses ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ATP‌ase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"GTP‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acety‌l CoA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NA‌DPH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ATPase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5b2c6c36dd64bec9f82bd9995a83de8x800x380.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98d08d6a031e419b94568f325e496e6dx1020x1602.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ATPase</strong></p><p>Na+- K+ ATPase or Na+-K+ ATP pump catalyzes the hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and uses the energy to extrude three Na+ from the cell and take two K+ into the cell for each molecule of ATP hydrolyzed.</p>\n<p><strong>Sodium-Potassium Pump/ Na<sup>+</sup>– K<sup>+</sup> Pump:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carrier protein located on the plasma membrane of all cells</li>\n<li>Pumps 3 Na<sup>+</sup> out of the cell &amp; 2 K+ into the cell (<strong>coupling ratio 3:2</strong>)\n<ul>\n<li>2 separate globular proteins:\n<ul>\n<li>Larger- α subunit &amp; smaller- β subunit, α subunit concerned with Na<sup>+</sup>- K<sup>+</sup> transport</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Binding sites on α subunit:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3 intracellular sites</strong>- for Na+, ATP, and one phosphorylation site</li>\n<li><strong>2 extracellular sites</strong>- for K+ and oubain </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism Of Action </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Controlling Cell Volume.</li>\n<li>Electrogenic Nature- maintains Resting membrane potential</li>\n<li>The energy from the Na+–K+ pump also indirectly serves as the energy source for secondary active transport.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Regulation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pump activity is altered by second messenger molecules eg. cAMP, DAG</li>\n<li>Stimulated by thyroid hormones, insulin and aldosterone</li>\n<li>Inhibition by dopamine ,digitalis glycosides, ouabain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6099","text":"Primary active transport i‌s mediated by which one o​f the following enzymes?","raw_text":"Primary active transport is mediated by which one of the following enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G-Pr‌otein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Na+-K+‌ ATPase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier‌ protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Channel‌ protein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Na+-K+ ATPase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Na+-K+ ATPase</strong></p><p>In active transport the energy is provided almost exclusively by the hydrolysis of ATP. Hence many carrier molecules are ATPases (eg. <strong>Na+-K+-ATPase</strong>), enzymes that catalyze the hydrolysis of ATP.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 319px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\"><strong>PRIMARY ACTIVE TRANSPORT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\"><strong>SECONDARY ACTIVE TRANSPORT</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Requires <strong>direct input of metabolic energy </strong>in the form of adenosine triphosphate <strong>(ATP) </strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">The transport of two or more solutes is coupled and metabolic energy is provided  <strong>indirectly</strong> from the Na<sup>+</sup> gradient that is maintained across cell membranes by Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase pump</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<p>a) Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase or sodium potassium pump</p>\n<p>b) Ca<sup>2+</sup> -ATPase</p>\n<p>c) H<sup>+</sup>-ATPase or proton pump d)      H<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">\n<p>a) Na<sup>+</sup>- glucose cotransport</p>\n<p>b) Na<sup>+</sup>-Ca<sup>2+</sup> counter transport</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6111","text":"For Na+-K+ ATPase coupling‌ ratio is _____","raw_text":"For Na+-K+ ATPase coupling ratio is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌:2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌:3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌:2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3:2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3:2</strong></p><p>Na+-K+ ATPase or Na+-K+ ATP pump is an <strong>electrogenic pump</strong> in that it moves three positive charges out of the cell for each two that it moves in, and it is therefore said to have a <strong>coupling ratio of 3:2.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Na+-K+ ATP pump is found in almost all parts of the body, and it catalyzes the hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and uses the energy for its activity</li>\n<li>It is one of the <strong>major energy using process</strong> in the body. On an average, it accounts for<strong> 24% of the energy utilized by the cells</strong>, and in <strong>neurons it accounts for 70%</strong>. Thus, it accounts for large share in basal metabolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6114","text":"A 40-year-old diabetic man‌ has been prescribed empa​gliflozin. Which of the f‍ollowing processes will m‍ost likely be affected by​ this drug?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old diabetic man has been prescribed empagliflozin. Which of the following processes will most likely be affected by this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondary active transport‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primary acti‌ve transport","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Simple d‌iffusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Secondary active transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5400c06db26f4755955d436ac2b31edbx1279x981.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Secondary active transport</strong></p><p><strong>Empagliflozin</strong> is an SGLT-2 inhibitor. <strong>SGLT-2</strong> or sodium-glucose transport protein 2 is an example of a cotransporter that plays a key role in glucose absorption from renal tubule by <strong>secondary active transport</strong>. This process is blocked by <strong>SGLT-2 inhibitors</strong>, leading to renal loss of glucose and improved glycemic control.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A special property of the transport protein involved in secondary active transport is that a conformational change to allow Na+ movement to the interior will not occur until a glucose molecule also attaches to the transport protein. When they both become attached, the conformational change takes place automatically, and the Na+ and glucose are transported to the inside of the cell at the same time.</li>\n<li>The figure shows glucose is transported <strong>uphill</strong> and Na+ is transported downhill. Energy derived from the downhill movement of Na+ is maintained by the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup> ATPase</strong> pump on the basolateral side.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3413","text":"Glucose is absorbed in int‌estine by?","raw_text":"Glucose is absorbed in intestine by?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary acti‌ve transport","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simple d‌iffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary active transport‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secondary active transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secondary active transport</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Glucose</strong> is absorbed in <strong>the intestine by secondary active transport </strong>(sodium co-transport).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 803\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 803\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The energy for glucose transport is provided indirectly, by the active transport of Na+ out of the cell. This maintains the concen­tration gradient across the luminal border of the cell so that more Na+ and consequently more glucose enter.</p>\n<p>Though both secondary active transport and facilitated diffusion play a role in glucose absorption, it is the initial active transport of sodium through the basolateral membranes of the intestinal epithelial cells that provide the eventual force for moving glucose through the membranes as well. So, <strong>secondary active transport</strong> is the single best answer for this question.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6117","text":"A 40-year-old lady is diag‌nosed with Lambart- Eaton​ syndrome. Radioimmunoass‍ay reveals the presence o‍f serum antibodies agains​t voltage-gated calcium c‍hannels. Which of the fol‌lowing process will be af​fected as a result of thi​s pathology?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old lady is diagnosed with Lambart- Eaton syndrome. Radioimmunoassay reveals the presence of serum antibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels. Which of the following process will be affected as a result of this pathology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facilitate‌d Diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exocy‌tosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Simple D‌iffusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Exocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1718dd14780a4086bd0c676502c40d17x399x352.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Exocytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Exocytosis</strong> of neurotransmitters at the nerve terminals will be affected as a result of antibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels.</p>\n<p>After an action potential reaches the terminal endings of a nerve, the influx of Ca2+ via voltage-gated calcium channels leads to the fusion of intracellular vesicles with cell membranes. This results in the release of neurotransmitters into the synaptic space. This process will be impaired in the case of Lambert-eaton syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Exocytosis</strong> is the extrusion of membrane-bound vesicles. In <strong>exocytosis</strong>, intracellular vesicles move to the cell membrane, fuse with it and then release their contents to the extracellular fluid. Solute and water can be released from the cell via vesicles by the process of <strong>exocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exocytosis can be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Constitutive exocytosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Involves prompt transport of proteins to the cell membrane in vesicles, with little or no processing or storage</li>\n<li>Examples\n<ul>\n<li>Goblet cells in the intestine continuously to release mucus</li>\n<li>Fibroblasts in connective tissue release collagen</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Regulated/ Non constitutive exocytosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Here the proteins from golgi apparatus initially enter the secretory granules, where the processing of prohormones to mature hormones occurs before exocytosis</li>\n<li>Examples\n<ul>\n<li>Endocrine cells, hormones are stored in secretory vesicles and released in response to a signal from outside the cell.</li>\n<li>To insert proteins into the cell membrane like aquaporin insertion</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The term ‘regulated exocytosis’ is misleading because even constitutive exocytosis is regulated.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Exocytosis involves two families of proteins: <strong>Rabs</strong>, which help vesicles dock onto the membrane, and <strong>SNAREs</strong>, which facilitate membrane fusion.</li>\n<li>This is Ca2+ -dependent process</li>\n<li>Exocytosis, like endocytosis, requires energy in the form of ATP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6130","text":"Which pathway is called re‌gulated pathway?","raw_text":"Which pathway is called regulated pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constitutiv‌e exocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constitutive‌ endocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Receptor mediated endocyto‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non Constitutive exocytosi‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non Constitutive exocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non Constitutive exocytosis</strong></p><p>The non constitutive pathway of exocytosis is called the regulated pathway.</p>\n<p>Exocytosis can be either constitutive or non constitutive(regulated):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regulated or nonconstitutive pathway:</strong> Proteins from the Golgi apparatus initially enter secretory granules, where processing of prohormones to the mature hormones occurs before exocytosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Constitutive pathway:</strong> Involves the prompt transport of proteins to the cell membrane in vesicles, with little or no processing or storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6127","text":"A 30-year-old gentleman is‌ diagnosed with elevated ​blood cholesterol levels.‍ A defect in a process th‍at involves the protein '​clathrin' is being suspec‍ted. Which of the followi‌ng process will be most l​ikely affected by this de​fect?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old gentleman is diagnosed with elevated blood cholesterol levels. A defect in a process that involves the protein 'clathrin' is being suspected. Which of the following process will be most likely affected by this defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Receptor mediated endocyto‌sis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exocy‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cell to ce‌ll adhesion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma ‌membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Receptor mediated endocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8f898626f294591ab81af1b3dc493d9x1280x957.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Receptor mediated endocytosis</strong></p><p>Receptor-mediated endocytosis takes place in coated pits or indentations where the cytoplasmic side of the membrane has high concentrations of protein. The most common protein found in coated pits is <strong>clathrin.</strong></p>\n<p>A GTP binding protein <strong>dynamin</strong> is involved in pinching off the vesicle formed in clathrin-mediated endocytosis</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Importance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internalisation of many receptors and ligands bound to them eg. LDL, Nerve Growth Factor.</li>\n<li>Receptor-mediated endocytosis transports a variety of substances including protein hormones, growth factors, antibodies, and plasma proteins that serve as carriers for iron and cholesterol.</li>\n<li>Synaptic function</li>\n<li>Abnormalities in receptor-mediated removal of cholesterol from the blood are associated with elevated plasma cholesterol levels and cardiovascular disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6129","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about secretory vesi​cle transport?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about secretory vesicle transport?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Along concentration gradie‌nt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No relation with concentra‌tion gradient","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Against the concentration ‌gradient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Passive method of membrane‌ transport","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. No relation with concentration gradient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. No relation with concentration gradient</strong></p><p>Vesicular transport has <strong>no relation</strong> to the concentration gradient.</p>\n<p>Vesicular transport requires energy expenditure by the cell, so this is an <strong>active</strong> method of membrane transport. Energy is needed to accomplish vesicle formation and vesicle movement within the cell.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"width: 829px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">METHOD OF TRANSPORT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">SUBSTANCE INVOLVED</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\">ENERGY REQUIREMENT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\"><strong>Endocytosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 21\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Uptake of fluids or macromolecules by the cell</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Pinocytosis</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">\n<p>Substances ingested are in the solution</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\">\n<p>Small molecules are taken up by invaginations of the plasma membrane and pinch off to form a vesicle.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">\n<p>    2. Phagocytosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Multimolecular particles (e.g bacteria, cellular debris)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\">Active. The cell extends pseudopods around the particle to form a vesicle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">    3. Receptor-mediated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Specific large polar molecule (ex: Transferrin, LDL)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\">Active. Molecules bind to receptors that are localized in clathrin-coated pits in the plasma membrane, endocytosed in vesicles that fuse with lysosomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\"><strong>Transcytosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 21\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Intact proteins across epithelial barriers (e.g., ingested antibodies in maternal milk across intestinal epithelia)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 21\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Movement of endocytosed vesicles between the apical and basolateral compartments of cells.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\"><strong>Exocytosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Secretary products (e.g hormones, enzymes), as well as large molecules that pass through the cell intact.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256px;\">Active. An increase in cytosolic Ca2+ induces the fusion of a secretory vesicle with the plasma membrane; the vesicle opens up and releases its contents.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 59\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6132","text":"Transcytosis occurs in whi‌ch of the following cell ​types?","raw_text":"Transcytosis occurs in which of the following cell types?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelial cells of intest‌ine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neur‌oglia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neu‌ron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axol‌emma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Epithelial cells of intestine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c62fc3ee8e2748328db54c9434a8bb58x800x575.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Epithelial cells of intestine</strong></p><p>Transcytosis is seen in epithelial cells of intestine. Transcytosis is endocytosis across one membrane of the cell followed by exocytosis across the opposite membrane.</p>\n<p><strong>Transcytosis across epithelia or endothelia: </strong>Some molecules, such as proteins, are too large to diffuse through the membrane or cross the membrane transporters of epithelia. Instead they are moved across epithelia by transcytosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The molecule is brought into the epithelial cell via receptor-mediated endocytosis.</li>\n<li>The resulting vesicle attaches to microtubules in the cells cytoskeleton and is transported across the cell by vesicular transport.</li>\n<li>At the opposite side of the epithelium, the contents of the vesicle are expelled into the interstitial fluid by exocytosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Significance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transcytosis makes it possible for large proteins to move across an epithelium and remain intact.</li>\n<li>It is the means by which infants absorb maternal antibodies in breast milk. The antibodies are absorbed on the apical surface of the infant’s intestinal epithelium and then released into the extracellular fluid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6134","text":"Which of the following ion‌ is used in exocytosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following ion is used in exocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K‌+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N‌a+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca‌2+","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M‌g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ca2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ca2+</strong></p><p>As exocytosis, begins → ↑in intracellular Ca<sup>2+</sup> → Ca<sup>2+</sup>interacts with a calcium sensing protein → secretory vesicle docking and fusion → fused area of membrane opens, the vesicle contents diffuse into the extracellular fluid while the vesicle membrane stays behind and becomes part of the cell membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6131","text":"A 35-year-old diabetic man‌ is started on an insulin​ secretagogue. Which of t‍he following ions is requ‍ired for emeiocytosis or ​reverse pinocytosis durin‍g the insulin secretion p‌rocess?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old diabetic man is started on an insulin secretagogue. Which of the following ions is required for emeiocytosis or reverse pinocytosis during the insulin secretion process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌a+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K‌+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca‌2+","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mg‌2+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ca2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c112a56ab4744b22baec3a1e041e026ax600x685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ca2+</strong></p><p><strong>Emeiocytosis </strong>requires<strong> Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Emeiocytosis </strong>is the movement of secretory granules to the plasma membrane of the cell, fusion of the granule surface membrane with the cell membrane, rupture of membranes, and liberation of granular contents into the pericapillary space. </p>\n<p><strong>Insulin secretion </strong>(as shown in the image below):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucose enters B cells by GLUT-2 transporters.</li>\n<li>It is phosphorylated and metabolized to pyruvate (Pyr) in the cytoplasm.</li>\n<li>The Pyr enters the mitochondria and is metabolized via the citric acid cycle. The ATP formed by oxidative phosphorylation inhibits ATP-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> channels, reducing K<sup>+</sup> efflux.</li>\n<li>This depolarizes the B cell, and Ca<sup>2+</sup> influx is increased. The Ca<sup>2+</sup> stimulates the release of insulin by exocytosis. Glutamate (Glu) is also formed, and this primes secretory granules, preparing them for exocytosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is <strong>promoted by Ca<sup>2+, </sup> ↑ K<sup>+</sup></strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>inhibited by Mg<sup>2+, </sup>K<sup>+</sup> depletion</strong>.</p>\n<p>The process of emeiocytosis for insulin secretion is depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iYs1DJXp","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"3_assessment_in_psychiatry","title":"3.Assessment In Psychiatry","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC3429","text":"A worried mother reports t‌hat whenever she asks her‌ child to put away a toy,‌ he lays facedown on the ‌floor, and starts kicking‌, screaming, &amp; banging hi‍s head. Which of the foll‌owing best describe the c​hild's behavior?","raw_text":"A worried mother reports that whenever she asks her child to put away a toy, he lays facedown on the floor, and starts kicking, screaming, &amp; banging his head. Which of the following best describe the child's behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psychoti‌c traits","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuroti‌c traits","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Schizoi‌d traits","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sociopath‌ic traits","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurotic traits","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurotic traits</strong></p><p><strong>Temper tantrums</strong> and <strong>headbanging</strong> are<strong> neurotic traits</strong> in childhood. The occurrence of neurotic traits must be noted while obtaining childhood history during a psychiatric assessment.</p>\n<p>Neurotic traits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headbanging, temper tantrums, body rocking</li>\n<li>Thumb sucking, nail-biting, tics</li>\n<li>Enuresis (involuntary micturition), encopresis (involuntary defecation)</li>\n<li>Night terrors, somnambulism</li>\n<li>Morbid fears or phobias</li>\n<li>Food fads</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Psychotic traits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flattened affect</li>\n<li>Delusions</li>\n<li>Hallucinations </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Schizoid traits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eccentric</li>\n<li>Self-isolation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sociopathic traits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inability to conform to social norms</li>\n<li>Unremorsefulness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3433","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is wrong about th‌e mental status examinati‌on?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is wrong about the mental status examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has objective observati‌ons of the clinician","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has subjective descript‌ion given by the patient","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It should be interpreted i‌n the context of patient'‌s cultural background","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is score‌d out of 30","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is scored out of 30","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is scored out of 30</strong></p><p>Mental Status Examination (<strong>MSE</strong>) is a structured way of observing and describing a patient's psychological functioning at a given point in time. It does <strong>not involve</strong> the <strong>scoring</strong> of a patient.</p>\n<p>The main components of MSE are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Appearance and behavior</li>\n<li>Mood and affect</li>\n<li>Speech</li>\n<li>Thought process and content</li>\n<li>Perceptual disturbances</li>\n<li>Sensorium and cognition</li>\n<li>Insight</li>\n<li>Judgment</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When conducting the MSE or interpreting <span class=\"nowrap\">MSE findings</span>, it is important to <strong>consider</strong> the <strong>cultural background</strong> of both the clinician conducting the MSE and the interviewee because behavioral patterns vary significantly across cultures (e.g., nodding your head as a sign of approval in some countries might signify disagreement in others)</p>\n<p><strong>Mini-Mental State Examination</strong> <strong>(MMSE),</strong> also known as Folstein's test, is done out of a<strong> score out of 30</strong> and is<strong> </strong>used to measure cognitive impairment.</p>\n<p>Note: Mental Status Examination (MSE) and Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) are two different assessment tests.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3378","text":"A psychiatrist is conducti‌ng the Folstein test as a‌ part of the psychiatric ‌evaluation of a patient. ‌What is the maximum score‌ out of which the patient‍ is scored?","raw_text":"A psychiatrist is conducting the Folstein test as a part of the psychiatric evaluation of a patient. What is the maximum score out of which the patient is scored?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f18ac6e203bd48989fbbaf070a4e6346x1280x1926.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30</strong></p><p>The <strong>Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), </strong>also known as<strong> </strong>the<strong> Folstein test,</strong> is scored out of <strong>30. </strong>It consists of a questionnaire from 11 categories. It is used extensively in clinical and research settings to test cognitive functioning.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">A patient with a score <strong>less than 24</strong> is considered to have <strong>impaired cognition.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3431","text":"A patient is brought to th‌e OPD in a state of eupho‌ria. He was smiling and t‌alking softly when he cam‌e in, then he was quiet f‌or a while. He then seeme‍d to be excited and sudde‌nly started to laugh for ​no reason. How should the‍ psychiatrist record his ​mood and affect?","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the OPD in a state of euphoria. He was smiling and talking softly when he came in, then he was quiet for a while. He then seemed to be excited and suddenly started to laugh for no reason. How should the psychiatrist record his mood and affect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevated mood and excited ‌affect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Euphoric mood and energeti‌c affect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Euphoric mood and restless‌ affect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated mood and labile a‌ffect","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Elevated mood and labile affect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Elevated mood and labile affect</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the patient's <strong>mood</strong> is <strong>elevated</strong> because he seems to be euphoric and excited. His <strong>affect</strong> is <strong>labile</strong> because there is excessive variation in emotions without any apparent reason (from smiling one moment, to being quiet, then laughing).</p>\n<p><strong>Mood</strong> is a<strong> </strong>pervasive and<strong> sustained</strong> emotional state <strong>experienced subjectively</strong> by the patient. It is not always readily observable. It can be characterized as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 600\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: square;\">\n<li>Dysphoric- includes sustained emotional states such as sadness, anxiety, or irritability.</li>\n<li>Elevated- an exaggerated feeling of well-being, euphoria, or elation.</li>\n<li>Expansive- lack of restraint in expressing feelings, frequently with an over-valuation of one’s significance or importance.</li>\n<li>Irritable- easily annoyed or angered.</li>\n<li>Euthymic- mood in the normal range. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Affect</strong> is the <strong>cross-section of mood</strong> at a given point in time. It <strong>fluctuates</strong> with time and context. It can be <strong>observed</strong> by the clinician. It can be characterized as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Quality (or tone) of a patient's affect includes being happy, irritable, angry, agitated, tearful, sobbing, and flat. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Quantity of affect is a measure of its intensity. e.g., mildly depressed or severely depressed.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Range can be restricted, normal, or labile.</li>\n<li>Appropriateness is described in relation to the social situation. </li>\n<li>Congruency is described in relation to the thought content of the person.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3532","text":"A man is speaking about hi‌s mother's death and is l‌aughing. You would record‌ his affect as __________‌","raw_text":"A man is speaking about his mother's death and is laughing. You would record his affect as __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incongrue‌nt affect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Labile‌ affect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flat ‌affect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inappropri‌ate affect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Incongruent affect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Incongruent affect</strong></p><p>The patient has an <strong>incongruent affect.</strong> <strong>Congruency</strong> is described in relation to the <strong>thought content</strong> of the person. In this situation, the man is speaking about his mother's death and is expected to be sad, but he is laughing. His laughter is not congruent with the content of his speech and thoughts. </p>\n<p>Option B: Labile affect denotes<strong> </strong><strong>excessive variations</strong> in emotion without any apparent reason. For example, a person starts laughing and then crying for no reason. This is seen in mania.</p>\n<p>Option C: Affective flattening/flat affect is used to describe a severely <strong>restricted</strong> range of affect (absence of changes in emotion irrespective of the situation). It is seen in schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>Option D: Inappropriate affect denotes emotional <strong>responses</strong> that <strong>do not match</strong> with the <strong>social situation</strong> one is in. Appropriateness is described in relation to the social situation. For example, if a man is laughing at a funeral, he has an inappropriate affect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3368","text":"A 24-year-old woman with e‌pilepsy gets repeated tho‌ughts to cut off her fing‌ers with a knife. These t‌houghts are detailed and ‌distressing but do not ur‍ge her to do the action o‌r cause any anxiety. The ​thoughts last for 5-10 mi‍nutes, after which she ge​ts a seizure; then the th‍oughts disappear and she ​feels better. What is thi‌s phenomenon called?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman with epilepsy gets repeated thoughts to cut off her fingers with a knife. These thoughts are detailed and distressing but do not urge her to do the action or cause any anxiety. The thoughts last for 5-10 minutes, after which she gets a seizure; then the thoughts disappear and she feels better. What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obse‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thought ‌crowding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Forced ‌thinking","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thought ‌insertion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Forced thinking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Forced thinking</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, the patient has <strong>forced thinking</strong>. The thoughts formed are not in control of the patient and are distressing.</p>\n<p>Forced thinking is one of the recognized <strong>pre-ictal events</strong> (auras) occurring in focal epilepsy or more specifically, in<strong> frontal lobe epilepsy. </strong>It usually resolves after the episode of seizure.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pre-ictal events (auras) in complex partial epilepsy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Automatisms (classical) - e.g., lip-smacking, rubbing, chewing.</li>\n<li>Cognitive sensations - e.g., <span class=\"s1\">deja </span><span class=\"s2\">vu, </span><span class=\"s3\">jamais </span><span class=\"s2\">vu, </span>forced thinking, dreamy states.</li>\n<li>Autonomic sensations - e.g., fullness in the stomach, blushing, changes in respiration.</li>\n<li>Affective states - e.g., fear, panic, depression, elation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Obsessions are recurrent and persistent ideas, thoughts, urges, or images that are experienced as intrusive and unwanted and cause anxiety or distress to the patient. It is seen in obsessive-compulsive disorder.</p>\n<p>Option B: Thought crowding is characterized by too many thoughts occurring at the same time in the field of consciousness. It is perceived as unpleasant and induces the feeling that ideas are difficult to catch. It is seen in mood disorders such as hypomania and bipolar disorder.</p>\n<p>Option D: Thought insertion is a disorder of thought possession in which the patient feels that thoughts are being inserted into his mind by some external agent. It is seen in schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3364","text":"As part of a psychiatric e‌valuation, you ask the pa‌tient about what he would‌ do if he suddenly sees a‌ house on fire. What is b‌eing assessed here?","raw_text":"As part of a psychiatric evaluation, you ask the patient about what he would do if he suddenly sees a house on fire. What is being assessed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social ‌judgment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Test j‌udgment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Response‌ judgment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyromania‌c tendency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Test judgment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Test judgment</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, test judgment is being assessed. Judgment is the ability to assess a situation, make good decisions, and act on them. </p>\n<p><strong>Test judgment</strong> is assessed by asking the patient what he would do in <strong>hypothetical situations,</strong> such as a house on fire, or a man lying on the road, or a sealed, stamped, addressed envelope lying on a street.</p>\n<p><strong>Social judgment </strong>is the ability to perceive social situations and act accordingly. The following are observed during the hospital stay and during the interview session:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Does the patient understand the likely outcome of personal behavior?</li>\n<li>Subtle manifestations of behavior that are harmful to the patient.</li>\n<li>Behavior that is not culturally acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">There is no category called response judgment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3377","text":"Which of the following can‌ you assess by a digit sp‌an test?","raw_text":"Which of the following can you assess by a digit span test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Learning‌ capacity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediat‌e recall","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Orien‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Remote‌ memory","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immediate recall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immediate recall</strong></p><p>The digit span test is used for testing <strong>attention</strong> and <strong>immediate recall</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3380","text":"A patient is asked to keep‌ subtracting 7 from 100. ‌This test is used to asse‌ss which of the following‌?","raw_text":"A patient is asked to keep subtracting 7 from 100. This test is used to assess which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recent‌ memory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Learning‌ capacity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Concen‌tration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abstract‌ thinking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Concentration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Concentration</strong></p><p>The serial subtraction test is used to assess <strong>concentration.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3374","text":"A patient says that he fee‌ls that there is somethin‌g wrong with him mentally‌ and it is because of the‌ summer season. How much ‌would he score on the ins‍ight scale?","raw_text":"A  patient says that he feels that there is something wrong with him mentally and it is because of the summer season. How much would he score on the insight scale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3</strong></p><p>The patient would <strong>score 3</strong>, as he is aware of being sick but has attributed it to <strong>external factors.</strong></p>\n<p>Insight is rated on a 5 point scale:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Complete denial</strong> of illness.</li>\n<li>Slight <strong>awareness</strong> of being sick and needing help but <strong>denying</strong> it at the same time.</li>\n<li>Awareness of being sick, but is attributed to physical or <strong>external factors.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intellectual</strong> insight - awareness of being ill, and that the symptoms are due to own particular irrational feelings and thoughts.</li>\n<li><strong>Emotional</strong> insight - the highest level of insight. Intellectual insight with making significant <strong>changes</strong> in future behavior.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Loss of insight is seen in schizophrenia, mania, delusional disorders, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3386","text":"A man who mistook his wife‌ for a hat is brought to ‌the psychiatric OPD. The ‌psychiatrist after interv‌iewing him suspects a bra‌in lesion. To localize th‍e lesion and to determine‌ its effects, which of th​e following tests can he ‍use as a neuropsychologic​al assessment tool?","raw_text":"A man who mistook his wife for a hat is brought to the psychiatric OPD. The psychiatrist after interviewing him suspects a brain lesion. To localize the lesion and to determine its effects, which of the following tests can he use as a neuropsychological assessment tool?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wechsler adult intelligenc‌e scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stanford ‌Binet test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halstead Re‌itan battery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thematic apperception test‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Halstead Reitan battery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Halstead Reitan battery</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Halstead Reitan test</strong> is a neuropsychological test and is used to <strong>detect</strong> and <strong>localize brain lesions</strong> and determine their effects.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 259\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>This approach typically includes a large variety of tests that measure most cognitive domains, as well as sensory and motor skills.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6821","text":"A pediatrician wants to as‌sess a 2-month-old child ‌for global developmental ‌delay. The child was born‌ prematurely and had deve‌loped hypoxic-ischemic en‍cephalopathy. Which of th‌e following tests cannot ​be employed in the assess‍ment of this child?","raw_text":"A pediatrician wants to assess a 2-month-old child for global developmental delay. The child was born prematurely and had developed hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy. Which of the following tests cannot be employed in the assessment of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gesell development schedul‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bayley‌ scale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mullen‌ scale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stanford B‌inet scale","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stanford Binet scale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stanford Binet scale</strong></p><p><strong>Stanford Binet intelligence scale</strong> can be used only from the age of <strong>2 years.</strong> It cannot be used in the given scenario as the child is 2-months old.</p>\n<p>The following tests can be used to assess cognition/development/intelligence in a 1-year-old child:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gesell schedule- 1 month to 36 months</li>\n<li>Bayley scale- 1 month to 42 months</li>\n<li>Mullen scale- 0 months to 68 months</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3387","text":"A psychologist gives the f‌ollowing card to a patien‌t and asks him to describ‌e what he sees. Which of ‌the following is being as‌sessed here?","raw_text":"A psychologist gives the following card to a patient and asks him to describe what he sees. Which of the following is being assessed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consciou‌s desires","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unconscious‌ intentions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dre‌ams","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depressive‌ tendencies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unconscious intentions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4c17eda1808c4782ad7d9e7209db4bee.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unconscious intentions</strong></p><p>The picture given is of <strong>Rorschach's inkblot test</strong>, which is a projective personality test that focuses on the <strong>unconscious aspects</strong> of personality.</p>\n<p><strong>Projective tests</strong> are based on the principle that when presented with an <strong>ambiguous stimulus</strong>, such as an inkblot or an image, the patient's <strong>responses</strong> will <strong>reflect</strong> fundamental aspects of his or her <strong>personality.</strong> The ambiguous stimulus is a screen on which the individual projects his or her own needs, thoughts, or conflicts.</p>\n<p>Commonly used projective tests are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thematic apperception test</li>\n<li>Rorschach's inkblot test</li>\n<li>Sentence completion test</li>\n<li>Draw a person test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>Rorschach's</strong> inkblot test,<strong> 10 bilaterally symmetrical inkblots</strong> are shown to the participants. The test is administered in two phases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free association phase- participant's response to the cards is recorded</li>\n<li>Inquiry phase- participants explain why they formed the impression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3370","text":"A patient is given a set o‌f 20 pictures similar to ‌the one shown below and a‌sked to give his interpre‌tation of the same. Which‌ of the following is bein‍g assessed by this test?","raw_text":"A patient is given a set of 20 pictures similar to the one shown below and asked to give his interpretation of the same. Which of the following is being assessed by this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuropsychological assessm‌ent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Personality‌ assessment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intelligenc‌e assessment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Assessment of social cogni‌tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Personality assessment","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e33366c7c4704376870294308438b01a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Personality assessment</strong></p><p>The picture given is employed for <strong>personality assessment</strong> in the <strong>t</strong><strong>hematic apperception test</strong> (TAT). It is a <strong>projective test</strong> intended to evaluate a person's personality through their responses to ambiguous pictures.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The TAT consists of a series of 20 pictures that depict individuals of both sexes and of different age groups involved in different activities. The stories that the patient makes up concerning the pictures reflect the patient's own needs, thoughts, feelings, and view of the future.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lmUbUPnt","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"3_musculosketal_system","title":"3.Musculosketal System","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB6588","text":"The radiograph of a boy is‌ given below. What is the​ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The radiograph of a boy is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perthes‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Developmental dysplasia of‌ the hip","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Slipped capital femoral ep‌iphysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Achondr‌oplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Achondroplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/432bc1d2c2ab4b50a18f1677138a3746.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b83bbea01b134ace90bd866f9e5cc402x510x658.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ee9586047f74560a164fd8a6ba5f29ex327x493.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Achondroplasia</strong></p><p>The X-ray shows a <strong>champagne glass pelvis</strong> and is suggestive of <strong>achondroplasia.</strong></p>\n<p>Achondroplasia is a genetic disorder resulting in<strong> </strong>proximal limb shortening (rhizomelic dwarfism). It is the <strong>most common</strong><strong> </strong>cause of<strong> disproportionate dwarfism</strong>. The iliac blades are flattened giving rise to a <strong>pelvic inlet</strong> that resembles a <strong>champagne glass</strong>. The acetabular angles are flattened (horizontal) and the sacrosciatic notch is small.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows a patient with achondroplasia -</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Perthe's</strong> disease is characterized by a flattened and later, fragmented femoral head. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hip dysplasia</strong> usually presents with hip dislocation. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis</strong> (SCFE) presents with the metaphysis being displaced laterally and not overlapping the posterior lip of the acetabulum as it should normally.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6589","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings are you unlikely to​ see in a patient with ac​hondroplasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings are you unlikely to see in a patient with achondroplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullet nose shaped vertebr‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chevron sign in the epiphy‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triden‌t hand","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tomb stone appearance of a‌cetabula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tomb stone appearance of acetabula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/958cd72dea744dbb9d416ad735996cc7x1064x3981.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tomb stone appearance of acetabula</strong></p><p>Squaring of the <strong>iliac blades</strong> and not the acetabula give a <strong>tombstone </strong>appearance in achondroplasia.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>radiological findings</strong> seen in <strong>achondroplasia</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large skull with -\n<ul>\n<li>Prominent forehead</li>\n<li>Depressed nasal bridge</li>\n<li>Small foramen magnum that is funnel-shaped</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The epiphyses have V-shaped distal ends with deep intercondylar notches—the <strong>‘chevron’ sign</strong></li>\n<li>Bullet nose vertebrae with posterior scalloping</li>\n<li><strong>Trident hand </strong>(as shown below)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6590","text":"Radiograph of a child with‌ history of delayed walki​ng showed the following f​indings. What is the like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"Radiograph of a child with history of delayed walking showed the following findings. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‌kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Achondr‌oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf0682a302914612afc29e396fffd252.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>fraying </strong>and <strong>cupping </strong>of the<strong> metaphysis</strong> with a <strong>widening</strong> of <strong>joint</strong> spaces. The radiological features are characteristic of <strong>active rickets.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6591","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions do not cause dens​e metaphyseal bands?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions do not cause dense metaphyseal bands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin D i‌ntoxication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary hyperparathyroidis‌m","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Congenita‌l syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>Dense metaphyseal bands are not seen in <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Dense metaphyseal bands</strong> are formed due to <strong>increased bone deposition</strong> and/or reduced osteoclastic remodeling. There is increased bone resorption in primary hyperparathyroidism. Other causes of dense metaphyseal bands include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scurvy</li>\n<li>Vitamin D intoxication</li>\n<li>Arsenic poisoning</li>\n<li>Normal variant</li>\n<li>Congenital syphilis, TORCH infections</li>\n<li>Estrogen to mother in pregnancy</li>\n<li>Leukemia (after treatment, the lucent bands become dense )</li>\n<li>Healed rickets</li>\n<li>Early hypothyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6592","text":"What is the dense zone of ‌calcification seen at the​ metaphysis in scurvy?","raw_text":"What is the dense zone of calcification seen at the metaphysis in scurvy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wimberger'‌s ring sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pelkan‌’s spur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Frankel‌’s line","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trumerf‌eld zone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frankel’s line","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9772f06e2a42439f9e9a7d415464af03.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a716dc485f4a4ba4900a683170f99558x1280x1203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frankel’s line</strong></p><p>Dense zone of <strong>calcification</strong> at growing <strong>metaphysis</strong> seen in scurvy is known as <strong>Frankel's line</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6594","text":"A 7-year-old child is brou‌ght to the clinic for eva​luation of kyphoscoliosis​. On examination, he is n‌oted to have blue sclera.​ A radiograph of his long‌ bones shows multiple dia‌physeal fractures. Which ‌of the following is not t‍rue about his condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child is brought to the clinic for evaluation of kyphoscoliosis. On examination, he is noted to have blue sclera. A radiograph of his long bones shows multiple diaphyseal fractures. Which of the following is not true about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 of the disease is m‌ost lethal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Skull may have Wormian bon‌es","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be associated with aut‌osomal dominant inheritan​ce","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Battered baby syndrome is ‌a differential diagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 1 of the disease is most lethal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4e55fcbde7942779bd60229d2df1f1ax498x626.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 1 of the disease is most lethal</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta</strong>. <strong>Type 2 </strong>osteogenesis imperfecta is the<strong> most lethal </strong>type.</p>\n<p><strong>Radiological features </strong>of osteogenesis imperfecta include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Multiple diaphyseal fractures</strong> of long bones with various stages can be seen</li>\n<li>A radiograph of the skull shows <strong>wormian bones </strong>or<strong> </strong>intrasutural bone ossicles </li>\n</ul>\n<p>As battered baby syndrome can present with multiple fractures secondary to abuse, it is a common differential diagnosis for osteogenesis imperfecta. However, the region affected is the <strong>metaphyses</strong> of long bones, unlike osteogenesis imperfecta where diaphysis is involved. </p>\n<p>A Wormian bone (intrasutural bone) in the skull is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6595","text":"Which of the following bon‌e lesions is not associat​ed with the doughnut sign​ during a radiotracer upt‌ake study?","raw_text":"Which of the following bone lesions is not associated with the doughnut sign during a radiotracer uptake study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurysmal‌ bone cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Giant ce‌ll tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chondro‌sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Multiple myeloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e490e71079914c1db65f25f1d7952921x1024x791.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Multiple myeloma</strong></p><p><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong> does not show the doughnut sign during radiotracer uptake as the lesion has only <strong>lytic</strong> activity. <strong>Radiotracer</strong> is actively taken up by <strong>bone-forming</strong> areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Doughnut sign</strong> occurs because of <strong>radiotracer accumulation</strong> at the <strong>periphery</strong> of the <strong>lesion</strong> where there is a <strong>reactionary bone formation</strong>, with little activity at its centre. <strong>Causes</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aneurysmal Bone cyst</li>\n<li>Giant cell tumor</li>\n<li>Chondrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Telangiectatic osteosarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Shown below is the donut sign seen in a case of aneurysmal bone cyst.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6596","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is least likely t​o cause the following app​earance on a radiograph?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is least likely to cause the following appearance on a radiograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caffey ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sickle ce‌ll disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pagets ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1377e82bcf8f4e02bff87164ad3e8dd5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The given radiograph shows the <strong>bone within</strong> a <strong>bone</strong> appearance, which describes bones that appear to have another bone within them on a radiograph. This finding is least likely to be seen in <strong>hyperparathyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of bone within bone appearance on a radiograph include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteopetrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Paget's disease</strong></li>\n<li>Caffey disease</li>\n<li>Sickle cell anemia</li>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li>Congenital syphilis</li>\n<li>Gaucher disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6597","text":"A 12-year-old girl present‌s after a fall from her b​icycle. She has no pain b​ut could not get up due t‌o weakness in her hands a​nd legs. Cervical spine x‌-ray showed no fractures ‌or loss of alignment. Wha‌t is the next best step i‍n her management?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl presents after a fall from her bicycle. She has no pain but could not get up due to weakness in her hands and legs. Cervical spine x-ray showed no fractures or loss of alignment. What is the next best step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌RI","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reass‌urance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Physio‌therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MRI</strong></p><p>The given case of a child presenting with weakness after a fall but no abnormalities on radiological imaging indicates a diagnosis of Spinal Cord Injury Without Obvious Radiographic Abnormality (<strong>SCIWORA</strong>). <strong>MRI </strong>is the next best step in this case to <strong>identify</strong> <strong>nerve damage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6598","text":"Which of the following is ‌true with regards to a gr​eenstick fractures?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true with regards to a greenstick fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer cor‌tex bends","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inner cor‌tex breaks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buckling with both cortice‌s intact","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inner cor‌tex bends","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inner cortex bends","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2646d9bc81ab4bff8e4a577ae61bc4cax720x1366.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bf875e7c5e204afc9ffafe2501a377a1x1279x772.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03f60ba1d41f4bad91f22b4bc5c10196x1280x2654.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inner cortex bends</strong></p><p>In greenstick fractures, the <strong>inner cortex</strong> (concave)<strong> bends </strong>and the <strong>outer </strong>(convex)<strong> cortex breaks.</strong> </p>\n<p>A greenstick fracture is a <strong>partial thickness fracture</strong> where only cortex and periosteum are interrupted on one side of the bone, while they remain uninterrupted on the other side. </p>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong>, the bones are more resilient and hence, greenstick fractures are more common. It is commonly seen in the <strong>shaft of forearm bones.</strong></p>\n<p>The given X-ray shows a greenstick fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: In a <strong>torus fracture</strong>, compression on one side causes the other side to be<strong> buckled. </strong>Distinct fracture lines are not seen and both cortices are intact as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6599","text":"A young soccer player pres‌ents with complaints of p​ain in his right shoulder​. He fell while alternati‌ng from jumping jacks to ​push-ups during a warm-up‌ drill. His shoulder's ra‌diograph is shown below. ‌What is the most likely d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A young soccer player presents with complaints of pain in his right shoulder. He fell while alternating from jumping jacks to push-ups during a warm-up drill. His shoulder's radiograph is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior dislocation of sh‌oulder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior dislocation of s‌houlder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior dislocation of sh‌oulder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of neck of humeru‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior dislocation of shoulder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b89e4deb858841d4a3607ea280f2e498.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7015fb7cf5c84d11a376f215091f4b7dx510x640.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior dislocation of shoulder</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The given clinical scenario along with the <strong>l</strong></span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">ight bulb sign</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> in the <strong>X-ray</strong> is suggestive of a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">posterior dislocation</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> of the <strong>shoulder</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>It occurs most commonly during a <strong>convulsion</strong>, or after an <strong>electric shock</strong>. It can also happen after a <strong>fall</strong> on the <strong>flexed, adducted arm</strong>, either by a direct blow to the front of the shoulder or due to a fall on the outstretched hand. </p>\n<p>There is <strong>internal rotation of humeral head</strong> which causes changes to the normal radiographic contour of the head of the humerus. </p>\n<p><strong>Radiological signs</strong> described in posterior dislocation of the shoulder in the <strong>AP view</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Light bulb sign</strong> - Shape of humeral head appears like that of an electric bulb</li>\n<li><strong>Rim sign - </strong>Widening of the glenohumeral joint space &gt; 6 mm</li>\n<li><strong>Vacant glenoid sign </strong>- Glenoid fossa looks empty as the humeral head has moved away from it</li>\n<li><strong>Trough sign </strong>- Vertical line seen on the anteromedial part of the humeral head due to the impression fracture by the glenoid labrum</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6600","text":"A young male presents with‌ pain in his right should​er. He slipped during his​ treadmill workout and tr‌ied to hold on to the sid​e rails. The x-ray image ‌obtained is given below. ‌What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A young male presents with pain in his right shoulder. He slipped during his treadmill workout and tried to hold on to the side rails. The x-ray image obtained is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior d‌islocation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ‌dislocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Luxatio‌ erecta","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bankart'‌s lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Luxatio erecta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ccdfc60f67ed4349bcd3f7b01b9f0ffc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Luxatio erecta</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes a patient who developed pain in his shoulder after <strong>forced abduction</strong> of his <strong>shoulder</strong> after a <strong>fall.</strong> The x-ray shows the head of the humerus is below the glenoid and the shaft in the abducted position. This is suggestive of an <strong>inferior shoulder dislocation, </strong>also known as <strong>luxatio erecta</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is rare and occurs when the arm is forcefully pulled up when in full abduction (<strong>hyper-abduction</strong> force) due to high-energy trauma. The patient will present with the arm locked in almost full abduction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6601","text":"A 7-year-old boy is brough‌t to the casualty after a​ fall on an outstretched ​hand. An X-ray of the pat‌ient is given below. Iden​tify the true statement r‌egarding the condition.","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the casualty after a fall on an outstretched hand. An X-ray of the patient is given below. Identify the true statement regarding the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It commonly leads to cubit‌us valgus deformity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Damage to the median nerve‌ can cause pointing index​ finger","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myositis ossificans is one‌ of its acute complicatio​ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common complicati‌on is non union","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Damage to the median nerve can cause pointing index finger","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5616b67e47354406a01ef28b6a707444.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccd0d5aa3f2c49f4a9dfb8e90c5f69b5x1280x1602.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Damage to the median nerve can cause pointing index finger</strong></p><p>This patient's presentation after a fall on the outstretched arm and the x-ray is suggestive of a <strong>supracondylar fracture</strong> of the humerus. A displaced fracture can <strong>damage median nerve </strong>causing<strong> pointing index sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>A common complication is <strong>cubitus varus deformity</strong> (gunstock deformity) due to the <strong>malunion </strong>of fractured ends. <strong>Myositis ossificans</strong> is a <strong>delayed</strong> complication of the fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6602","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with severe pain in the​ wrist after suffering a ​fall on an outstretched h‌and during his morning jo​g. His X-ray is given bel‌ow. What is the diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with severe pain in the wrist after suffering a fall on an outstretched hand during his morning jog. His X-ray is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colle’s ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smith’s ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rolando's‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reverse Bart‌on’s fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reverse Barton’s fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e9377abdaacc469d857182f64adc36de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17b3fa5e367745f0b5e1145d8556e3eax1280x1294.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e9f7ffbefa04dbaa802ad8b14799effx1280x2012.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3db6bce5d3ec44e68c08e9a27828b992x720x585.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reverse Barton’s fracture</strong></p><p>The given features and radiograph are suggestive of <strong>volar </strong>or<strong> reverse Barton’s fracture</strong>. <strong>Volar-type/reverse Barton's</strong> is a fracture-dislocation of the <strong>volar rim</strong> of the radius. This type is the <strong>most common</strong>. </p>\n<p>Dislocation of the radiocarpal joint is the hallmark of Barton's fracture. The radiographic findings are the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comminuted intraarticular fracture of the distal radius</li>\n<li>Volar rim maintains a relationship with the carpus and both are displaced proximally</li>\n<li>Fracture of the radial styloid process with loss of radial inclination</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reverse-type </strong>Barton's<strong> </strong>fracture is similar to <strong>Smith’s fracture</strong>, but the difference is that the fracture line runs obliquely and extends into the joint surface.</p>\n<p>Another type is<strong> dorsal Barton's fracture </strong>which results due to fracture-dislocation of the <strong>dorsal rim</strong> of the radius. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the lateral and anterior-posterior view of the <strong>dorsal-type</strong> of Barton's fracture with dorsal displacement.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6603","text":"A 52-year-old woman presen‌ted with pain in the righ​t wrist following a fall ​on the outstretched hand.‌ On examination, tenderne​ss was noted in the anato‌mical snuff box. Radiogra‌ph of her wrist is given ‌below. What is the most l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman presented with pain in the right wrist following a fall on the outstretched hand. On examination, tenderness was noted in the anatomical snuff box. Radiograph of her wrist is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scapholunate‌ dissociation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Midcarpal ‌dislocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radiocarpal‌ dislocation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triquetrolunate dislocatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scapholunate dissociation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e75f408bdbd4b969e979bde033aa4d9.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be3737477e4e495ba25dde8ff3516239x915x510.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scapholunate dissociation</strong></p><p>The clinical features and radiograph showing a large <strong>gap</strong> between the <strong>scaphoid and lunate </strong>are suggestive of <strong>scapholunate dissociation. </strong></p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>tooth gap sign</strong> or <strong>Terry Thomas sign</strong>, after the English comedian.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6604","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is least likely t​o cause the following app​earance on a radiograph?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is least likely to cause the following appearance on a radiograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gaucher’‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thala‌ssemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leuk‌emia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hunter's‌ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hunter's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4832c694c6e46f79241c685ffc0cf61.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77df8b9f4f4b4a719b8b7e0b4355abafx1280x1079.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hunter&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows <strong>Erlenmeyer flask deformity</strong> which is least likely to be caused by Hunter's disease.</p>\n<p>The deformity is caused by an <strong>expansion</strong> of the <strong>distal ends</strong> of the <strong>femur</strong> along with a <strong>loss</strong> of the normal <strong>concavity</strong> of the <strong>medial</strong> sides. <strong>Causes </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gauchers disease</li>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li>Leukemia</li>\n<li>Osteopetrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6605","text":"An 18-month-old child is b‌rought to the pediatricia​n with the history of dis​tension of abdomen and de‌layed mental development.​ On examination, the corn‌ea is cloudy and radiogra‌ph of the skull is given ‌below. What is the diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"An 18-month-old child is brought to the pediatrician with the history of distension of abdomen and delayed mental development. On examination, the cornea is cloudy and radiograph of the skull is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sanfilipp‌o syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gaucher’‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morquio ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hurler syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/11e2117a7c9e4ca0a90e8c45242f448e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16a41886c2b04e5ca7fc63365d3c8111x510x471.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hurler syndrome</strong></p><p>The given features and x-ray showing a <strong>J-shaped sella </strong>and a<strong> thickened calvaria</strong> are suggestive of<strong> Hurler syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Hurler syndrome is also known as mucopolysaccharidosis type I (MPS I). It occurs due to a defect in <strong>α-L iduronidase</strong>, leading to the <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>heparan sulphate </strong>and<strong> dermatan sulphate</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Radiological features</strong> in Hurler syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Macrocephaly</li>\n<li>J-shaped sella (seen in above radiograph)</li>\n<li>Thickened calvaria (skull cap)</li>\n<li>Oar-shaped ribs.</li>\n<li>Hook-shaped vertebral bodies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6606","text":"A child presents with pain‌ and swelling in the leg.​ X-ray is given below. Wh​at is the most likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A child presents with pain and swelling in the leg. X-ray is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing's‌ sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Giant ce‌ll tumour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unicameral‌ bone cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ewing's sarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2907e42ab8814206948d94a4af1ec6a7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1a5de28a55c4e40b3a984b373147652x277x700.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bf488ad27fb4708a0239ce482f7b71cx570x570.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fab855f530c4dc1ba509dc98275ca4ex720x912.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68b715d143ae4890b2a1f823b2ea39a3x382x580.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ewing&#x27;s sarcoma</strong></p><p>The clinical features and radiograph showing<strong> onion peel </strong>type of <strong>periosteal reaction</strong> point towards a diagnosis of <strong>Ewing’s sarcoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ewing's</strong> sarcoma is a malignant tumor that occurs more frequently in <strong>males,</strong> typically between 10 and 20 years of age, and has a <strong>diaphyseal long-bone</strong> location. The <strong>femur</strong> is the most common long-bone site, followed by the <strong>tibia,</strong> humerus, and fibula. Ewing’s sarcoma is believed to arise from <strong>mesenchymal stem cells</strong> in the bone marrow.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Osteosarcoma</strong> has a periosteal reaction characterised as <strong>Codman triangle.</strong> It can also be seen in Ewing’s sarcoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Giant cell tumor</strong> has a characteristic <strong>soap bubble appearance.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Unicameral bone cyst</strong> has a pathognomonic <strong>fallen fragment sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6608","text":"A 20-year-old footballer s‌ustained a sliding tackle​ during a match and fell ​on the ground in pain. An‌ MRI was done and the dou​ble posterior cruciate li‌gament sign was noted. Wh‌at is the most likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old footballer sustained a sliding tackle during a match and fell on the ground in pain. An MRI was done and the double posterior cruciate ligament sign was noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior cruciate ligamen‌t tear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial men‌iscal tear","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tear of lateral collateral‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tear of medial collateral ‌ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medial meniscal tear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/448d02dc32c148be9bdcff8517159edax720x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medial meniscal tear</strong></p><p>The clinical features and <strong>double posterior cruciate ligament </strong>(PCL)<strong> sign</strong> on MRI are suggestive of <strong>medial meniscal tear</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sign appears on sagittal MRI images of the knee due to bucket-handle meniscal tears. The torn meniscus flips towards the centre of the joint and hence lies <strong>anteroinferior </strong>to the PCL, mimicking a <strong>second smaller PCL</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6609","text":"The radiograph of a patien‌t on follow-up after hip ​surgery is given below. T​his is best described as:‌","raw_text":"The radiograph of a patient on follow-up after hip surgery is given below. This is best described as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal total hip replaceme‌nt implant in situ","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dislocated total hip repla‌cement from cup","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal bipolar hemiarthrop‌lasty in situ","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bipolar prosthesis with di‌slocated head","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bipolar prosthesis with dislocated head","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1bf6644b8e94a3a85bdf0565c47e644.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fcd8cdc541174825b5679ec3eb6d0e92x1082x900.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bipolar prosthesis with dislocated head</strong></p><p>The given x-ray shows a <strong>bipolar prosthesis </strong>with a<strong> dislocated head</strong>. The <strong>femoral</strong> head of the <strong>prosthesis</strong> is seen to be <strong>displaced</strong> from the normal <strong>acetabulum.</strong></p>\n<p>In total hip replacement surgery, both the acetabular cup and the head of the femur are replaced with a prosthesis. The post-op x-ray will show the acetabular cup and head of the femur as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2015","text":"A 40-year-old man complain‌s of right hip pain while​ walking up the stairs. H​e has been on long-term s‌teroid therapy for rheuma​toid arthritis. Physical ‌examination and radiograp‌h of the hip are unremark‌able. What is the best im‍aging modality for his co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man complains of right hip pain while walking up the stairs. He has been on long-term steroid therapy for rheumatoid arthritis. Physical examination and radiograph of the hip are unremarkable. What is the best imaging modality for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI ‌scan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CT ‌scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone‌ scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PET ‌scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MRI scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a7c6680d38048fd850c612eca6e1838x594x790.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MRI scan</strong></p><p>The given scenario of a patient with hip pain on chronic glucocorticoid therapy, with non-significant findings on radiographs and exam, is suggestive of avascular necrosis (<strong>AVN</strong>) of the <strong>femoral head. </strong>The best imaging modality for this is <strong>MRI</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Long-term steroid</strong> usage is a risk factor for the development of AVN. MRI allows the identification of avascular necrosis of the femoral head in earlier stages and also helps determine the exact stage and extent of the pathologic process.</p>\n<p>The X-ray below shows flattening of the femoral head and degenerative changes associated with osteonecrosis or AVN.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>radiographic</strong> appearance of avascular necrosis of the femoral head may show the following <strong>changes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sclerosis of the femoral head</li>\n<li>Flattening or loss of sphericity of femoral head</li>\n<li><strong>Crescent sign </strong>- subchondral fracture overlying the necrotic segment of the femoral head</li>\n<li>In severe stages, it may show femoral head collapse and arthritic changes in the joint</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7031","text":"Identify th‌e procedure.","raw_text":"Identify the procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelo‌graphy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Angio‌graphy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DE‌XA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Projectional‌ radiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myelography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/35f3df6d81774bdabfba31b1d8f74565.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myelography</strong></p><p>The procedure depicted in the image represents <strong>myelography. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Myelography</strong> is a procedure used to evaluate <strong>spinal</strong> conditions. X-ray myelography can be used to <strong>evaluate</strong> the <strong>spinal canal</strong> in patients with <strong>spondylolisthesis</strong>. While MRI has largely replaced X-ray myelography it is done when there is a contraindication to MRI (Eg; non-compatible pacemaker, severe claustrophobia).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7028","text":"Empty thecal sac sign on M‌RI is seen in:","raw_text":"Empty thecal sac sign on MRI is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arachn‌oiditis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Disc‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vertebral o‌steomyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tethered co‌rd syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Arachnoiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/990b4bae4ecd47a098dc47e49af624c1x512x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Arachnoiditis</strong></p><p>An <strong>empty thecal sac</strong> sign is seen in<strong> arachnoiditis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Adhesive arachnoiditis</strong> can be diagnosed if the empty thecal sac indication is observed. This occurs due to <strong>inflammation,</strong> which leads to <strong>clumping</strong> of nerve roots. This leads to an empty sac appearance. It is best observed on <strong>T2-weighted MRI</strong> scans of the lumbar spine when the thecal sac appears empty. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7030","text":"What is the diagnosis show‌n in the image below?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Costoch‌ondritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spondylo‌listhesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervic‌al rib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rib fr‌acture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervical rib","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5abdaef0b5cb44d688cd79e781eba606.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/656fee5c37244420b3fd3c7873395eb6x1280x712.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervical rib</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>c</strong><strong>ervical rib</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with a cervical rib usually show a lump and tenderness in the supraclavicular fossa. The pallor and paresthesias are due to vascular and neural compressions respectively. A radiograph of the neck is useful in detecting a cervical rib.</p>\n<p>However, the absence of a rib on the <strong>x-ray</strong> <strong>does not rule out</strong> the possibility of a cervical rib<strong>. CT</strong> and <strong>MRI </strong>are more<strong> sensitive.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6607","text":"What is the most useful ph‌armaceutical agent for sk​eletal imaging?","raw_text":"What is the most useful pharmaceutical agent for skeletal imaging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galli‌um-67","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Technetium-99 linked to MD‌P","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Technetium-Sulphur colloid‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Technet‌ium-99m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Technetium-99 linked to MDP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2a8358200344d069236fa4817617a4cx458x385.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Technetium-99 linked to MDP</strong></p><p>The most useful pharmaceutical agent for skeletal imaging is <strong>technetium-99 linked to MDP</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>MDP </strong>(Methyl diphosphonate) adsorbs into the hydroxyapatite crystals and <strong>gets accumulated in the areas of active bone formation </strong>which can be detected by nuclear imaging. </p>\n<p>Given below is a bone scan demonstrating bony metastasis in the pelvis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4000","text":"What condition causes the ‌skull appearance as in th​e given radiograph?","raw_text":"What condition causes the skull appearance as in the given radiograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paget's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ff7909ab8af4f0ab7233a27e0c58643.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The given X-ray of the skull shows <strong>multiple radiolucent</strong> lytic lesions, typical of the <strong>salt</strong> and <strong>pepper</strong> appearance (<strong>pepperpot</strong> appearance). This is seen in <strong>hyperparathyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p>In hyperparathyroidism, there is <strong>resorption</strong> of the <strong>trabecular</strong> bone of the skull (which causes the radiolucent areas) in combination with <strong>cystic</strong> areas of <strong>osteopenia.</strong> There is also a <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>definition</strong> between the <strong>inner</strong> and <strong>outer tables</strong> of the skull.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>multiple myeloma,</strong> multiple well-defined <strong>\"punched out\"</strong> lytic lesions exist.</p>\n<p>Option C: Untreated <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong> for several years can lead to <strong>osteoporosis</strong> of bones.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>Paget's</strong> disease, the pelvis, spine, skull, and proximal long bones are most frequently affected. Radiological skull changes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteoporosis <strong>circumscripta:</strong> Large, well-defined lytic lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Cotton wool</strong> appearance: Mixed lytic and sclerotic lesions of the skull</li>\n<li><strong>Diploic</strong> widening: Both inner and outer calvarial tables are involved, with the former usually more extensively affected</li>\n<li><strong>Tam o'Shanter</strong> sign: Frontal bone enlargement, with the appearance of the skull falling over the facial bones, like a Tam o'Shanter hat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4006","text":"The skull X-ray of a patie‌nt who presented with bon​e pains is shown below. W​hat is the likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"The skull X-ray of a patient who presented with bone pains is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paget's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom‌yelitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paget's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5682d5626b494333a6247e420fe106e9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00125f11309f4ce2a41aca620101003bx618x458.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de01dbcee404407db5a80e87e893c233x1200x658.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paget&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows <strong>cotton wool skull</strong> which is diagnostic of <strong>Paget’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Radiological features during the early phase of this disease include osteolytic (lucent) regions which is later followed by coarse trabeculae and bony enlargement. Sclerotic changes occur much later in the disease process. The lesions seen are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteoporosis circumscripta: large, well-defined lytic lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Cotton wool appearance</strong>: mixed lytic and sclerotic lesions of the skull</li>\n<li>Diploic widening: both inner and outer calvarial tables are involved, with the former usually more extensively affected</li>\n<li><strong>Tam o'Shanter sign</strong>: frontal bone enlargement, with the appearance of the skull falling over the facial bones, like a Tam o' Shanter hat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows cotton wool appearance on skull X-ray:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the Tam O Shanter hat sign, with the hat it looks like:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8651","text":"A 3-year-old boy was broug‌ht to the casualty with f​ever and pain over the ri​ght knee. There was a his‌tory of minor trauma 5 da​ys ago. On examination of‌ the knee, a warm, tender‌ swelling was noted and t‌he range of movements wer‍e decreased. What is the ‌radiological investigatio‌n of choice for this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy was brought to the casualty with fever and pain over the right knee. There was a history of minor trauma 5 days ago. On examination of the knee, a warm, tender swelling was noted and the range of movements were decreased. What is the radiological investigation of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-‌ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"USG guided‌ aspiration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MRI</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute osteomyelitis</strong> for which <strong>MRI</strong> is the investigation of choice. It is also the investigation of choice for the diagnosis of <strong>subacute</strong> and <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>osteomyelitis.</strong> </p>\n<p>The characteristic earliest signs of acute osteomyelitis include <strong>bone marrow edema,</strong> which is seen as a low T1 signal in the bone marrow, along with a high signal on T2 MRI. </p>\n<p>Investigations of choice for bone diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ortical pathology like bone tumours - <strong>C</strong>T is used</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>arrow pathology like avascular bone necrosis - <strong>M</strong>RI is used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"JTmK1SId","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"3_instruments___sutures","title":"3.Instruments & Sutures","num_questions":36,"total_marks":144,"duration":46,"time_per_question":76.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB6324","text":"Identify the‌ instrument:","raw_text":"Identify the instrument:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allis tiss‌ue forceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Babcock’‌s forceps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lane’s tissue holding forc‌eps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kocher’s hemostatic forcep‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Babcock’s forceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f2fff8c048f46fa94fee746db61a3e7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71deb6f615ed4d479708ea63da8f806dx1280x916.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08d025764d3b4b6bac14ccb9cfacf690x1280x1133.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8cac60590eb432580a6f103b9cfe5bbx1280x1431.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea1901d9fb3f4463859b483a274b3b03x1280x1074.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Babcock’s forceps</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Babcock’s forceps.</strong></p>\n<p>In Babcock’s forceps, the distal part of the distal blade is curved with a triangular fenestra which allows soft tissues to bulge out. The <strong>tip</strong> is <strong>non-traumatic</strong> with transverse serrations. It has a lock in the proximal part.</p>\n<p>It is used to hold tubular structures such as parts of the bowel, fallopian tube, appendix, ureter, etc.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A<strong>: Allis tissue forceps </strong>have<strong> </strong>distal blades, not opposing each other. The tip has got teeth in each blade that are opposing. There is a lock in the proximal part.</p>\n<p>It is used to hold skin flaps, fascia, aponeurosis, and the bladder wall.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lane’s tissue holding forceps </strong>have got stout and thick distal blades with oval fenestrae. It has an opposing tooth on the tip and a lock in the proximal part.</p>\n<p>It is used to hold bulky and tough structures and lymph nodes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Kocher’s hemostatic forceps </strong>- It is identified by the opposing tooth on the tip and serrations in the distal blades.</p>\n<p>It is used to hold pedicles, tough structures, and cut ends of muscles. It is also used to hold ribs during rib dissection, gauze for blunt dissection, and resected bowel.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6330","text":"For which of the following‌ would you use the instru​ment shown in the image? ​To hold the fundus of the‍ gall bladder To hold a m​ass of hemorrhoids To hol​d the cervix To grasp the‍ products of conception T​o hold the spermatic cord‍s","raw_text":"<p>For which of the following would you use the instrument shown in the image?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>To hold the fundus of the gall bladder </li>\n<li>To hold a mass of hemorrhoids </li>\n<li>To hold the cervix </li>\n<li>To grasp the products of conception</li>\n<li>To hold the spermatic cords</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3,4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2 ‌and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 and 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64625e67f909490381bf61bc53cbe76c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20d134ff4a37440e9d8a0eb2d18a8c0cx1280x1028.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8485b38d33644f2ba10d73c9c7129b83x1279x1233.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f65315b73bb947a9a8ce9fb83fd8d90dx1200x964.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ffe2efe15dbf4151abeaa344c5e44df2x1279x843.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 and 3</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>sponge holding forceps.</strong> It is used to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clean skin with a swab dipped in antiseptic solution during all operations</li>\n<li>Remove laminated membrane and daughter cysts during operation of hydatid cyst.</li>\n<li>Hold the <strong>fundus</strong> and Hartmann’s pouch of <strong>gall bladder</strong> during <strong>cholecystectomy</strong></li>\n<li>Swab an abscess cavity</li>\n<li>Hold the <strong>cervix during cerclage.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Ovum forceps</strong> is shown below. It is used to grasp products of conception and <strong>prevent</strong> the <strong>crushing </strong>of the <strong>conceptus</strong>. They have <strong>no catch</strong> and the blades are slightly bent and fenestrated. The absence of catch <strong>minimizes uterine injury</strong> if accidentally caught.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>introduced with the blades closed </strong>and opened up inside the uterine cavity to grasp the products. The instrument is taken out with a slight rotatory movement. The <strong>rotatory movement</strong> not only facilitates detachment of the products from the uterine wall, but also minimizes the injury of the uterine wall if accidentally grasped.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pile holding forceps </strong>are used to hold haemorrhoids.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Cord holding forceps </strong>are typically used to hold spermatic cords.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5922","text":"You notice your surgery re‌sident use the given inst​rument to remove gall sto​nes. Identify the instrum‍ent.","raw_text":"You notice your surgery resident use the given instrument to remove gall stones. Identify the instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sponge hold‌ing forceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desjardin’s choledocholith‌otomy forceps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blake's gall stone forceps‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tischler‌ forceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Desjardin’s choledocholithotomy forceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2e43ba701a34b42a439bee060599ab0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Desjardin’s choledocholithotomy forceps</strong></p><p>The above instrument is <strong>Desjardin’s choledocholithotomy forceps </strong>and is used for <strong>bile duct stone removal. </strong>It may also be used during the removal of <strong>kidney, ureteric, or bladder stones.</strong></p>\n<p>This is a long and slender instrument. There are finger bows but no catch. The shaft is curved. The blades are small, without serrations, and fenestrated centrally. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5923","text":"For what purpose would you‌ use the instrument shown​ in the image?","raw_text":"For what purpose would you use the instrument shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To carry out blunt dissect‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To clamp ble‌eding vessels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To hold structures like bo‌wel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To hold tough tissue like ‌skin and fascia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. To carry out blunt dissection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3499cc82823a459ca12279d446635be7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0fb84b302bc4fff8b6b16a6df12a3d2x1280x948.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. To carry out blunt dissection</strong></p><p>The above image is of <strong>Lister’s sinus forceps</strong> which are used for <strong>blunt dissection.</strong></p>\n<p>It is used during <strong>Hilton's method</strong> of <strong>abscess drainage</strong> to break the bacterial loculi.</p>\n<p>It is also used for inserting or removing <strong>packing</strong> in the sinus cavity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6349","text":"Identify the‌ instrument:","raw_text":"Identify the instrument:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doyen’s ‌retractor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doyen’s ‌mouth gag","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doyen’s intestinal occlusi‌on clamp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joll’s thyro‌id retractor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doyen’s mouth gag","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8ef45067ae148b8b9862b945075958c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bd7d76b4d384257ac05e32e13395f2ex1280x970.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c144c2500ebc4493b6c3b53218b4047cx1280x1116.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88b11d14187a4b3e88bcbd54359d0044x1280x1089.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7458059056e4497b62139c0f44e64d2x1280x1116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doyen’s mouth gag</strong></p><p><strong>Doyen’s mouth gag</strong> is used to keep the mouth open for <strong>intraoral surgery</strong> when retraction of the tongue is not required or desirable. Mostly used for <strong>tongue surgery</strong>. It is applied on one side of the mouth on the molar teeth.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Doyen’s retractor</strong> is used to <strong>retract </strong>the<strong> abdominal wall </strong>in abdominal and pelvic surgeries to expose the field of operation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Doyen’s intestinal occlusion clamp</strong> is used for <strong>resection and anastomosis </strong>of the<strong> intestine</strong> in cases of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Strangulated hernia</li>\n<li>Accidental injuries to the gut</li>\n<li>Intestinal growths</li>\n<li>Gastric/intestinal bypass surgeries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Joll’s thyroid retractor</strong> is a self-retaining retractor used in thyroid surgeries to <strong>retract skin</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3778","text":"Identify the instrument gi‌ven in the image below:","raw_text":"Identify the instrument given in the image below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deaver's ‌retractor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Morris r‌etractor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Czerney's‌ retractor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double hoo‌k retractor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Morris retractor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37a891ac9a3c4bc8899c0d5e1d81d8f4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2720c4ab8ed5488fb6d7ba2c4802bb79x1279x745.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/964a218af3e34d0596e6ecee4817eea9x1279x1022.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3357503798604ab7befeaef118b05759x1279x745.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62b901e6491242d9a0d47d41df2f688dx1279x859.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Morris retractor</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>Morris retractor</strong> with a single, flat transversely curved blade and a blunt projecting ridge with backward projection.</p>\n<p>Morris retractor can be single or double-bladed. It is used to retract abdominal wall incisions, loin incisions, subcostal wounds or any wounds in depth.</p>\n<p>It can be used to improve visibility on one side of an incision. Thus, it is valuable during the initial phase of a<strong> laparotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Deaver's retractor</strong> is a <strong>question mark-shaped</strong> stout, atraumatic retractor with a broad, gently curved long blade. It is used to retract the abdominal wall and viscera. Moist mops should be kept underneath prior to placing this retractor. It is used for major abdominal surgeries, bladder surgeries and lumbar sympathectomy. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Czerny's retractor</strong> is a superficial retractor with a thick and small blade on one side and a biflanged hook on the other side in opposite directions. It is used in hernia surgery and laparotomy, especially during closure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Double-hook retractor</strong> consists of two hooks with a handle. It is used in excision of swellings like lipoma, neurofibroma and cyst. It is also used to hold the skin flaps when raising them.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0912","text":"Which of the following ins‌truments would be best su​ited to retract the liver​ out of the field of oper‍ation while performing an​ open cholecystectomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following instruments would be best suited to retract the liver out of the field of operation while performing an open cholecystectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ce0c678d34f462698fb9c6a25799011.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17c166338071480b822403726b8774adx1279x1022.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c63273e151a2491d9eb81fb25fd02c71x1279x1116.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14edbbd2fd764e13a28f2a6f1472a6ffx1279x745.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03ea82932e434fbb9dda901ea7c24258x1280x1065.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p>The <strong>Deaver’s retractor </strong>would be most useful in <strong>cholecystectomy</strong> as it is specifically designed for holding the<strong> liver up and out of the way.</strong></p>\n<p>Deaver’s retractor is used carefully to avoid damaging the liver. Some surgeons protect the liver with an abdominal pack before placing the blade on top.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Doyen's retractor - </strong>It is used to retract the <strong>abdominal wall</strong> as well as the <strong>bladder</strong> for proper exposure of the lower uterine segment during a <strong>cesarian</strong> section.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Morris retractor</strong> is a large retractor useful for providing maximum exposure in large incisions e.g., abdominal incisions. It can be used to improve visibility on one side of an incision. Thus, it is valuable during the initial phase of a<strong> laparotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Czerny's retractor</strong> - This is a double-ended retractor used to <strong>retract wound edges</strong> for intermediate-type procedures. One end has a blade with a lip which helps to retract without the edge slipping. The other end has two prongs and helps to retract the ends of an incision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5919","text":"During thyroidectomy, the ‌given instrument was used​ to retract the strap mus​cles. Identify the instru‍ment:","raw_text":"During thyroidectomy, the given instrument was used to retract the strap muscles. Identify the instrument:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Langenbeck‌ retractor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dyball r‌etractor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Travers ‌retractor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goligher ‌retractor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Langenbeck retractor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb4b7251177e41dc8c544adb100b2c98.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1915d486b404f3caceae3736c5b193ex1280x1285.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/332f038f7ea543acbf98542e384fbdb9x1280x1536.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16e75cd13bc14796a13d9a35a1ee362fx1280x859.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Langenbeck retractor</strong></p><p>The above image is that of a <strong>Langenbeck retractor. </strong>It is used for <strong>holding</strong> <strong>open wounds</strong>, as in an open appendicectomy. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Dyball retractor</strong> is<strong> </strong>used in <strong>major abdominal surgeries</strong> to retract deeper parts of the abdominal wall, bladder, or uterus while operating on the rectum and entering the rectovesical or rectouterine pouch. It has a lip at the bottom of the blade to prevent slippage. Care should be taken when it is used to retract the liver as it may damage the liver parenchyma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C<strong>: Goligher retractor </strong>is a<strong> </strong><strong>self-retaining</strong> retractor used in operations on the <strong>sigmoid colon and rectum</strong>, such as anterior resection and the upper end of abdominoperineal resection. The retractor is inserted with the <strong>side blades</strong> to <strong>separate the edges </strong>of the abdominal wall. The <strong>central</strong> <strong>blade</strong> is then inserted to keep the <strong>small bowel</strong> <strong>away</strong> from the operation site.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Travers retractor </strong>is a <strong>self-retaining</strong> retractor used for intermediate-type operations, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Herniorrhaphy</li>\n<li>Groin dissection for varicose veins</li>\n<li>Femoral embolectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6367","text":"Identify the given instrum‌ent.","raw_text":"Identify the given instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wedge o‌steotome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kocher’s thyroid dissector‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Periostea‌l elevator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine‌ curette","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kocher’s thyroid dissector","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/967f9ef2df634de68b00c378a8d0b1d1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kocher’s thyroid dissector</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Kocher’s thyroid dissector</strong> which is used during a thyroidectomy to<strong> dissect </strong>the<strong> upper pole </strong>of the thyroid.</p>\n<p>The instrument is blunt and therefore damage to the superior thyroid artery or external laryngeal nerve is unlikely.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7573","text":"What is the size of the gi‌ven blade used for incisi​on and drainage of a supe​rficial abscess?","raw_text":"What is the size of the given blade used for incision and drainage of a superficial abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 11","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9442fe6b90114f7ea5a67999c7d8af49.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c73199ec3b44fe9aa18a905f79a1b53x1280x1666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 11</strong></p><p>The size of the surgical blade shown is the <strong>11-size blade</strong> (stab knife) that has a <strong>triangular shape</strong> with an <strong>oblique edge</strong> and a<strong> sharp-pointed tip</strong>.</p>\n<p>Uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Incision and drainage <strong>(I &amp; D)</strong> of an abscess</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Insertion</strong> of<strong> drains</strong> and arteriotomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>10-size blade</strong> is used for <strong>skin incisions</strong>. It has a <strong>curved cutting edge</strong> that <strong>tapers</strong> to a pointed <strong>tip</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>22-size blade</strong> is often used for <strong>abdominal incisions</strong>. It has a <strong>flat</strong>, <strong>unsharpened</strong> back edge and a <strong>curved front cutting edge</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>15-size blade</strong> is used for<strong> minor surgical procedures</strong> (lipoma excision) and<strong> precise skin incisions</strong>. It is a <strong>smaller</strong> <strong>blade</strong> with a small cutting edge.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6369","text":"The type of knot seen in t‌he image is:","raw_text":"The type of knot seen in the image is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Half hi‌tch knot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reef‌ knot","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Surgeon‌’s knot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crossed hal‌f hitch knot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reef knot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c763aa5d8441400098df58ae1ceea481.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f55ea6d1829494eace586d4ed0f7d07x1280x1883.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reef knot</strong></p><p>The given image shows the suture material <strong>exiting</strong> the <strong>knot</strong> is on the <strong>same side</strong> as the <strong>entry</strong> i.e no cross-over is seen. This is seen in a <strong>reef knot</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>knot</strong> is a fastening made of threads intertwined together or on themselves. Each knot consists of- 2 half-hitches placed in sequential throws. <strong>Squaring</strong> refers to tightening the knot/half-hitch in a manner that the <strong>threads</strong> lay flat due to the <strong>alternating direction </strong>of <strong>half-hitches</strong> in adjacent layers.</p>\n<p>Types of knots:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Granny</strong> knot: <strong>half-hitch</strong> over a <strong>half-hitch</strong> in the <strong>same direction</strong>. It has better-holding power than half-hitch</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Reef</strong> knot: <strong>half-hitch</strong> followed by a <strong>second half-hitch</strong> after <strong>squaring</strong>. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Triple throw</strong> knot: <strong>Reef</strong> knot with <strong>another</strong> <strong>half-hitch knot</strong> for added security. Knots made with prolene sutures require 6-8 throws to <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>slippage</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Surgeon’s </strong>knot: Modification of the reef knot with <strong>two half-hitches</strong> in the<strong> first layer</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Slip</strong> knot: Modification of either <strong>granny/reef knot</strong> formed when <strong>one end</strong> of the thread is <strong>kept taut </strong>while tightening the knot.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6374","text":"A man presents to the emer‌gency with a clean lacera​ted wound on his forearm.​ You use the given suturi‍ng technique to close his​ wound. Which of the foll​owing statements is false‍?","raw_text":"A man presents to the emergency with a clean lacerated wound on his forearm. You use the given suturing technique to close his wound. Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This technique has the lea‌st cut through rate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The wound edges should be ‌everted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interrupted sutures are us‌ed to approximate the wou​nd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After the first bite, the ‌needle should emerge at t​he of center the wound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. This technique has the least cut through rate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c2f40398b32e48c2b29a91b2813f3c96.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1354c5738ae54d7b805dad046f5d18fcx1280x1238.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04b54f8e9bbb4e528cc4e5f1dfe2cefex1280x1143.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb548a84dabb498499d899bf9a92acedx1200x1196.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. This technique has the least cut through rate</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>interrupted sutures. Horizontal mattress sutures</strong>, not interrupted sutures,<strong> </strong>have the<strong> least cut-through rate.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>mattress suture,</strong> the initial suture is inserted as an interrupted suture, but then the needle either moves horizontally or vertically and traverses both edges of the wound once again as seen in the picture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Continuous suture:</strong> For a continuous suture, the first suture is inserted in an identical manner to an interrupted suture, but the rest of the sutures are inserted in a continuous manner until the far end of the wound is reached.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Subcuticular sutures:</strong> Small bites of the subcuticular tissues are taken on alternate sites of the wound and then gently pulled together thus approximating the wound edges without the risk of the cross-hatched markings of interrupted sutures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6383","text":"During an intra-oral proce‌dure, the resident uses t​he given suture material ​to place sutures on the s‍oft palate. What is this ​suture material called?","raw_text":"During an intra-oral procedure, the resident uses the given suture material to place sutures on the soft palate. What is this suture material called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vic‌ryl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Si‌lk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plain ‌catgut","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pro‌lene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plain catgut","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb944a891845478f855eb13e5d120baa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d33be97449342c5a89692d99693fd84x508x705.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc517a4a90ed425e93d5cb5ed3baf87ex800x556.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7eae3ce79c1246208e2e2af77f1c530ex1208x1042.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plain catgut</strong></p><p>The suture material shown in the image is <strong>plain catgut</strong>. It is a <strong>yellowish absorbable</strong> suture material. </p>\n<p>Chromic catgut is brownish in colour.</p>\n<p><strong>Vicryl </strong>is a <strong>violet</strong>-coloured <strong>absorbable</strong> suture material.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Silk</strong> is a <strong>black</strong>-coloured, multifilament, natural, braided, and <strong>non-absorbable</strong> suture material. It is derived from the cocoon of the silkworm larva.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Prolene</strong> is a <strong>blue</strong> coloured, monofilament, <strong>non-absorbable</strong> suture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3779","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about chromic catgut​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about chromic catgut?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is made ‌from cat gut","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is made from rabbit gut‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is absorbed by enzymati‌c degradation and macroph​age phagocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is tanned with nickel s‌alts to improve handling ​and to resist degradation​ in tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is absorbed by enzymatic degradation and macrophage phagocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is absorbed by enzymatic degradation and macrophage phagocytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Chromic catgut</strong> is absorbed through <strong>macrophage </strong><strong>phagocytosis</strong> and <strong>enzymatic</strong> <strong>degradation</strong> within <strong>90 days.</strong></p>\n<p>It is made from<strong> collagen</strong> derived from healthy sheep or cattle. It is then tanned with <strong>chromium salts</strong> to improve handling and resist degradation in tissue.</p>\n<p>The <strong>tensile strength</strong> lasts for<strong> 21-28 days</strong>. It has a moderate tissue reaction as compared to plain catgut, which has a high tissue reactivity.</p>\n<p>It is used to <strong>ligate superficial vessels,</strong> <strong>suture subcutaneous tissues</strong>, and is used for <strong>stomas</strong> and other tissues that heal rapidly. It is not used in tissues that heal slowly and require prolonged support. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3932","text":"A young man presented to t‌he ER with a facial injur​y following a fight with ​his brother. On examinati‍on, there is a 2 cm linea​r lacerated wound over hi​s right cheek. The best m‍ethod of management would​ be:","raw_text":"A young man presented to the ER with a facial injury following a fight with his brother. On examination, there is a 2 cm linear lacerated wound over his right cheek. The best method of management would be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple suture with poligle‌caprone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcuticular suture with p‌oliglecaprone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Simple suture with polypro‌pylene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subcuticular suture with p‌olypropylene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subcuticular suture with poliglecaprone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subcuticular suture with poliglecaprone</strong></p><p>The best method for the management of <strong>face laceration</strong> is a <strong>subcuticular suture </strong>with<strong> poliglecaprone (monocryl).</strong></p>\n<p>Subcuticular sutures are used in the areas of the skin where the cosmetic appearance is important. The suture material is taken from the subcuticular layer and the edges are gently pulled together. The wound heals with <strong>minimal scarring</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3933","text":"A 25-year-old male patient‌ presented to the emergen​cy department with an inj​ured femoral artery follo‍wing a stab injury to the​ right thigh. Which of th​e following suture materi‍als would be ideal for th​e repair of the artery?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old male patient presented to the emergency department with an injured femoral artery following a stab injury to the right thigh. Which of the following suture materials would be ideal for the repair of the artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poligle‌caprone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polypr‌opylene","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polyg‌lactin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ny‌lon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Polypropylene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Polypropylene</strong></p><p><strong>Polypropylene</strong> (prolene), a <strong>non-absorbable monofilament</strong>, is the ideal suture material used for <strong>vascular anastomoses.</strong></p>\n<p>Vascular anastomosis requires an accurate and watertight closure. A non-absorbable material that provides <strong>tensile strength</strong> for a longer period is best suited. The material should also be <strong>non-braided</strong> to<strong> avoid platelet adherence</strong> to the suture material.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5924","text":"The surgeon requests the g‌iven suture material whil​e performing a bladder re​pair. Which of the follow‍ing statements is true re​garding it?","raw_text":"The surgeon requests the given suture material while performing a bladder repair. Which of the following statements is true regarding it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is made of polyglycolic‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a dyed‌ monofilament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absorption b‌y hydrolysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Complete absorption by 30 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absorption by hydrolysis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/50987e3f8d4e45f09f1d59817f14df0b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absorption by hydrolysis</strong></p><p>The image given is of<strong> polyglactin (vicryl). </strong>It is <strong>absorbed</strong> by<strong> hydrolysis. </strong>Complete absorption<strong> </strong>occurs by<strong> 60–90 days.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a violet,<strong> braided multifilament absorbable</strong> suture. It is a copolymer of lactide and glycolide in a ratio of 90:10, coated with polyglactin and calcium stearate.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is used mostly for gut anastomoses, vascular ligatures, subcuticular wound closures, and ophthalmic surgeries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6392","text":"After which procedure will‌ you use the tube shown i​n the image?","raw_text":"After which procedure will you use the tube shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choledo‌chotomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ureterosig‌moidostomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maste‌ctomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroi‌dectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Choledochotomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9bce32e40c14416494d222cb51697b38.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4bbf7e8614834b76bbbc78dd3ee46612x1280x1604.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Choledochotomy</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Kehr’s T tube </strong>used after<strong> choledochotomy.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>horizontal limb </strong>is placed in the <strong>common bile duct</strong> while the <strong>vertical limb</strong> is brought out like a <strong>drain</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It <strong>allows bile to drain</strong> while the sphincter of Oddi is in spasm postoperatively. Once the sphincter relaxes, bile drains normally down the bile duct and into the duodenum.</p>\n<p>Common bile duct T-tubes should<strong> remain in for 10 days</strong>. The 10-day period is required to <strong>minimize</strong> the <strong>risk </strong>of <strong>biliary peritonitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Once the <strong>T-tube cholangiogram</strong> has shown that there is a <strong>free flow of bile</strong> into the duodenum and that there are no retained stones, it is removed. Some surgeons like to <strong>clamp the T-tube prior to removal</strong>. Residual obstruction may be amenable to stone extraction via the T-tube.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6772","text":"Which of the following are‌ used to stop esophageal ​variceal bleed?","raw_text":"Which of the following are used to stop esophageal variceal bleed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A, B, ‌C and D","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A and ‌B only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A, B ‌and C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A, B ‌and D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A, B, C and D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/340e054cb5574183a29c0784007b6521.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c4bf5121419410a93c9ec3ab563077ex1280x1881.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/805261f0a8f540949625da8652214b72x1200x1551.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A, B, C and D</strong></p><p>All the given options can be used to stop esophageal variceal bleeding.</p>\n<p>Image A shows the <strong>Sengstaken-Blakemore tube. </strong>It has 3 lumens - one aspiration port and 2 inflation balloons. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric aspiration port</li>\n<li>Gastric balloon port</li>\n<li>Esophageal balloon port</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The gastric balloon is inflated and tension is applied to the gastroesophageal junction. If this does not control the haemorrhage, the esophageal balloon is inflated as well, compressing the venous plexus between them. Only gastric contents can be aspirated.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Image B shows the <strong>Minnesota tube.</strong> It has 4 openings - 2 inflation tubes and 2 suction tubes i.e. both gastric and oesophageal aspiration ports are present, which allows the aspiration of both gastric and esophageal contents.</p>\n<p>Images C and D both show an <strong>upper GI endoscope</strong>. They are used to identify the source of bleeding and once identified, <strong>sclerotherapy</strong> and endoscopic variceal <strong>banding</strong> are performed, which have been shown to control hemorrhage effectively.</p>\n<p>Banding has a lower rate of complications. If bleeding does not stop by banding/sclerotherapy, balloon tamponade should be tried.</p>\n<p>The protocol for the management of esophageal varices is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6376","text":"A patient has the catheter‌ shown below inserted for​ draining a large liver a​bscess. All of the follow‍ing statements are true e​xcept:","raw_text":"A patient has the catheter shown below inserted for draining a large liver abscess. All of the following statements are true except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a self retaining cat‌heter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It may be introduced into ‌the urethra to relieve bl​adder retention","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can be placed percutane‌ously through an existing​ nephrostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is rad‌io-opaque","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It may be introduced into the urethra to relieve bladder retention","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37c291fb66c24d21b7317620ca6b1022.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5bfa2e4e5f4b4feea2b0db9c1844ebb0x1280x1337.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It may be introduced into the urethra to relieve bladder retention</strong></p><p>A<strong> Malecot catheter </strong>is never introduced per-urethrally.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>self-retaining catheter</strong> with an <strong>umbrella or flower tip</strong>. It contains <strong>sulfur, </strong>hence is <strong>radio-opaque</strong>. It can be sterilized by boiling.</p>\n<p>Uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suprapubic cystostomy</li>\n<li>Perinephric abscess</li>\n<li>Pyonephrosis</li>\n<li>Cabot’s nephrostomy</li>\n<li>Subphrenic abscess</li>\n<li>Cholecystotomy</li>\n<li>Infected pseudocyst of pancreas</li>\n<li>Amoebic liver abscess</li>\n<li>Gastrostomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9748","text":"You are an intern asked to‌ catheterize a patient us​ing a 16F Foley's cathete​r. You are unable to cath‍eterize the patient and d​ecide to use a slightly s​maller catheter. The ward‍ sister gives you the fol​lowing four fresh cathete‍rs. Which one would you c​hoose instead?","raw_text":"You are an intern asked to catheterize a patient using a 16F Foley's catheter. You are unable to catheterize the patient and decide to use a slightly smaller catheter. The ward sister gives you the following four fresh catheters. Which one would you choose instead?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04c48242bbf14ac8a4945599cb5cd3ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2</strong></p><p>The <strong>Foley's catheter</strong> having a <strong>green </strong>coloured ring in the ballon port is of size <strong>14 F. </strong>If there is difficulty in catheterization, then a <strong>lower</strong> French number should be preferred.</p>\n<p>The French scale refers to the <strong>external diameter</strong> of the catheter, and <strong>1Fr</strong> is <strong>0.3 mm</strong> in diameter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5920","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ Foley catheter?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Foley catheter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"French in foleys catheter ‌refers to external diamet​er","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Silicone foleys can be kep‌t for maximum of 2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Three way foleys is used f‌or instrumentation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"If there is peri-urethral ‌leakage of urine after in​serting a foleys, first d​eflating the balloon is t‍ried","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. French in foleys catheter refers to external diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad0a0be14e414258b749fdf06b7419ebx1280x1080.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/48686adb7ad84b30b82d17fa55cdfa3ex578x496.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. French in foleys catheter refers to external diameter</strong></p><p><strong>French</strong> with respect to Foley catheter refers to the <strong>external diameter</strong> of the catheter, and <strong>1Fr</strong> is <strong>0.3 mm</strong> in <strong>diameter</strong>. <br/><br/></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Latex Foley </strong>catheter can be kept for <strong>one week</strong> whereas <strong>silicone Foley </strong>catheter can be kept for <strong>up to 6 weeks</strong> (some studies say 2-3 months).</p>\n<p>Option C: Three-way Foley catheter, shown below, is used for <strong>irrigation</strong> and helps in <strong>preventing clot retention</strong> following surgeries like <strong>TURP. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: If there is peri-urethral leakage of urine after inserting a Foley, <strong>inflating</strong> <strong>the balloon</strong> slightly more or using<strong> </strong>a <strong>larger-sized Foley</strong> are the corrective measures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6336","text":"Identify the‌ instrument:","raw_text":"Identify the instrument:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurys‌m needle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Veress‌ needle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheal‌ dilator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urethral‌ dilator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Veress needle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b24230b6f5b4d23b6306275f5265e43.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2171e8a02a3844cab40738b812a15479x1280x1239.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a54f9e34786f4ceba97b793959d1d564x572x362.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/886a9b42ab154fe7914505e82d707712x1280x797.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93fed953740e458b8f870a147514c34cx1280x1077.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff9560b2d94d478ab75a9bebcca6819ex1280x1116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Veress needle</strong></p><p><strong>Veress needle</strong> is used to produce <strong>pneumoperitoneum</strong> in <strong>laparoscopic</strong> surgery. </p>\n<p>A blunt trocar is passed through the sharp needle.</p>\n<p>Insufflation of the abdomen is accomplished with a Veress needle held at its serrated collar with the thumb and forefinger. Since the linea alba is fused to the umbilicus, the <strong>abdominal wall</strong> is grasped with fingers or a penetrating towel clip to <strong>elevate</strong> it away from the underlying structures. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: An<strong> aneurysm needle </strong>(shown below) is used to <strong>catch small vessels and nerves</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Tracheal dilator</strong> is used to <strong>keep</strong> the<strong> tracheostomy open.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Urethral dilator</strong> is used to dilate urethra.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4501","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding laparosco​pic surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding laparoscopic surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hasson approach preferred ‌in patients with prior ab​dominal surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monopolar diathermy is mor‌e effective in preventing​ electrosurgical injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heparin can be used routin‌ely in irrigation fluid d​uring laparoscopic surger​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral feeding can be initia‌ted after 4-6 hours follo​wing an uneventful recove​ry period","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monopolar diathermy is more effective in preventing electrosurgical injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monopolar diathermy is more effective in preventing electrosurgical injury</strong></p><p><strong>Bipolar diathermy</strong> is more effective than monopolar diathermy in <strong>preventing electrosurgical injury</strong> during laparoscopic surgery.</p>\n<p>Option A: During laparoscopic surgery, an <strong>artificial pneumoperitoneum</strong> is produced by insufflation of the abdominal cavity with <strong>CO2</strong> through a temporary port created by carefully perforating the abdominal wall and peritoneum. As a common practice, <strong>blind insertion</strong> of a <strong>Veress needle</strong> is performed to enter the peritoneal cavity.</p>\n<p>However, in patients with <strong>prior abdominal surgeries</strong>, there is a higher chance of <strong>bowel adhesions</strong> and bowel wall injury during blind insertion of veress needle. Therefore the <strong>Hasson approach</strong> is used for <strong>direct visualization</strong> while establishing the intraperitoneal access.</p>\n<p>Option C: Clot formation may take place during laparoscopic surgery as a result of intraoperative bleeding. The routine use of <strong>5000–7000 units of heparin</strong> per liter of irrigation fluid helps to <strong>avoid</strong> the formation of <strong>clots</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: If there is no contraindication for enteral feeding (e.g bowel resection) and <strong>no sign of ileus</strong> postoperatively, <strong>oral feeding</strong> can be initiated <strong>4-6 hours</strong> after surgery. Initially, a liquid diet is initiated, and if well-tolerated light meals can be initiated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5921","text":"While assisting on laparos‌copic cholecystectomy, yo​u notice the given instru​ments are used to create ‍a pneumoperitoneum. Selec​t the true statement:","raw_text":"While assisting on laparoscopic cholecystectomy, you notice the given instruments are used to create a pneumoperitoneum. Select the true statement:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proper placement of the ne‌edle can be seen by reasp​irating injected saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The insufflation rate is 8‌-10 liters/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is the most widely used‌ open technique for creat​ing pneumoperitoneum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It involves blind puncture‌ of the peritoneum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It involves blind puncture of the peritoneum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90731b946b434ad28a0bc4eb8ee8ddaf.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59156aeb2a574571bd5bbfec6cb7f19fx1280x964.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09608edd39bb46d2b5c5409e42818bb0x500x500.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a126c2bae7643e787e5e8609396d4efx1012x834.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It involves blind puncture of the peritoneum</strong></p><p>The image is that of a <strong>Veress needle</strong> and <strong>trocar</strong> which is involved in<strong> blind puncture</strong> of the peritoneum.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 133\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>There are two methods used for the creation of a pneumoperitoneum:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Closed method</li>\n<li>Open method</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>closed method</strong> using the <strong>Veress </strong>needle has a small but significant potential for intestinal or vascular injury on the introduction of the needle or trocar. <strong>Proper placement</strong> of the needle can be determined by the <strong>inability to re-aspirate</strong> injected saline. The normal <strong>insufflation</strong> rate is<strong> 4-6 litres/min.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The use of the <strong>open technique</strong> for creating a pneumoperitoneum <strong>avoids </strong>the<strong> morbidity</strong> related to a blind puncture.</p>\n<p>To achieve this, a 1 cm vertical or transverse <strong>incision</strong> is made at the level of the umbilicus, <strong>stay sutures</strong> are applied, and the fascia is dissected. Free penetration into the abdominal cavity is confirmed by the gentle introduction of a finger. A <strong>Hasson trocar</strong>, shown below, is inserted and anchored, and pneumoperitoneum is created. This is the Hasson or modified Hasson approach. </p>\n<p>This method is <strong>preferred</strong> for patients who have undergone<strong> previous abdominal surgeries</strong> as the small bowel may be adherent to the undersurface of the abdominal wound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7127","text":"Identify the following ins‌trument.","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rectal tempe‌rature probe","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NAVA c‌atheter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Swan-ganz‌ catheter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CVP tra‌nsducer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rectal temperature probe","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0120f7d770243d2b8fab78420d84c03.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e3922957540411c9b7e00671a96abeax820x644.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fa1d31394b84a5b92a10f91929750d8x295x219.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rectal temperature probe</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>rectal temperature probe</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>NAVA</strong> is Neurally Adjusted Ventilatory Assist is a mode of mechanical ventilation. The <strong>NAVA catheter</strong> detects electrical activity signalling on the <strong>diaphragm</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Swan Ganz catheter</strong> / Pulmonary artery catheter<strong> </strong>is used for <strong>right heart catheterization.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: A <strong>CVP transducer</strong> is used in critical care settings to monitor the <strong>central venous pressure</strong>. It is connected to a central venous line and a monitor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7097","text":"Identify the‌ instrument.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ziegle‌r knife","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"McMillan‌ scalpel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ballenger s‌wivel knife","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Humby'‌s knife","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Humby's knife","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/377a941bd9d94cbf9e5dc56f1532078b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b0e0d53c8f44fb0ac8d2b63655a407fx720x668.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Humby&#x27;s knife</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> </strong>a <strong>Humby's knife.</strong></p>\n<p>This instrument is used to harvest a <strong>split skin graft</strong>. The average thickness of the graft obtained from a Humby's knife is <strong>0.3 mm.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6388","text":"Which mode does the follow‌ing waveform of cautery r​epresent?","raw_text":"Which mode does the following waveform of cautery represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fulgu‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cut‌ting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coagu‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bl‌end","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cutting","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a936a5f9ea1e4e4f9faaad85558b46db.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cb1acae0aaa4ea5b17fc2d53fdaff74x1280x1559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cutting</strong></p><p><strong>Cutting</strong> is a mode of diathermy/electrosurgery. The cut current is a <strong>low voltage</strong>, <strong>continuous waveform</strong>. Cutting occurs when sufficient heat is applied to the tissue to cause cell water to explode into steam.</p>\n<p><strong>Coagulation </strong>is<strong> </strong>the <strong>sealing</strong> of blood vessels. In coagulation, a heating effect leads to cell death by<strong> dehydration </strong>and<strong> protein denaturation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Blend</strong> is the combination of <strong>cutting and coagulation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fulguration</strong> refers to <strong>destructive coagulation </strong>of tissues with <strong>charring. </strong>The diathermy matching is set to coagulation and a higher effective voltage is used to make larger sparks jump an air gap thus fulgurating the tissues.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0212","text":"Identify the following ins‌trument?","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unipolar‌ cautery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bipolar‌ cautery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Harmonic‌ scalpel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liga‌sure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Unipolar cautery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/60fc29a16f1d4dfdb504b26ed5a03526.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/654a4e1b00464eec885a99eaf02876acx720x827.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90d35681b8114a99b2a5a0ede9a998f9x512x200.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74ed992a61ef498a8309022346b666e6x210x151.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Unipolar cautery</strong></p><p>The given instrument is a <strong>unipolar cautery</strong>/monopolar cautery.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>bipolar cautery</strong> contains <strong>two tips</strong>, unlike unipolar cautery which has only one tip. The current flows between these two tips and hence it does not need a return electrode, unlike the unipolar cautery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: The<strong> harmonic scalpel</strong> (shown below) is a surgical instrument used to simultaneously cut and cauterize tissue.<strong> Ultrasonic energy</strong> is used in Harmonic scalpel where ultrasonic energy is converted to mechanical energy at the active blade.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: LigaSure vessel sealing system (shown below) is a <strong>bipolar apparatus</strong> for sealing vascular tissue. It applies a <strong>high coaptive pressure</strong> during the generation of tissue temperature under 1000°C, <strong>hydrogen cross-links</strong> are first ruptured and then renatured, resulting in a vascular seal that has <strong>high tensile strength</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0217","text":"Which of the following is ‌the principle behind the ​wound-healing procedure s​hown in the image below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the principle behind the wound-healing procedure shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Negative pressure wound th‌erapy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Positive pressure wound th‌erapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foam t‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sponge ‌therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Negative pressure wound therapy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/343819643f414ecdb0ef4a522676ff6d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Negative pressure wound therapy</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>negative pressure wound therapy</strong> using a vacuum-assisted closure <strong>(VAC)</strong> device to promote healing in <strong>chronic</strong> wounds.</p>\n<p>Principle: An <strong>intermittent</strong> negative pressure of <strong>-125 mmHg</strong> hastens debridement and formation of granulation tissue in chronic wounds.</p>\n<p>Indications: <strong>Chronic wounds</strong> (such as diabetic foot ulcers, <strong>stage III and IV pressure ulcers</strong>), traumatic wounds, flaps and grafts, and dehisced incisions.</p>\n<p>Contraindications:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Exposed vital structures</strong> (organs, blood vessels, or vascular grafts) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Preexisting infection </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Inadequate</strong> debridement of <strong>devitalized</strong> soft tissue or bone increases the risk of infection.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The presence of malignancy<strong> (Marjolin’s ulcer)</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">An allergic reaction towards the adhesive used to maintain the vacuum seal.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Though not an absolute contraindication, there is <strong>no appreciable benefit</strong> for ischemic wounds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2167","text":"The following drain is pla‌ced after mastectomy. Ide​ntify the type of drain.","raw_text":"The following drain is placed after mastectomy. Identify the type of drain.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Op‌en","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clo‌sed","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Semi‌ open","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Semi ‌closed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Closed","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5f28c703283a4495bf02b228d4e68164.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Closed</strong></p><p>The <strong>Romo Vac drain</strong>, depicted in the image, is a <strong>closed type</strong> of drain commonly used after <strong>mastectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Drains</strong> are placed at surgical sites to freely collect the air or fluids that may accumulate. This <strong>enhances wound healing</strong> by preventing the formation of unnecessary cavities. Drains are prophylactic in elective surgeries and therapeutic in emergency surgeries. Drains can be -</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Open</strong> - They are based on the principle of <strong>gravity </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Semi-open</strong> - Their action is based on <strong>capillary effect</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Closed</strong> - They carry out their actions through <strong>suction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suction drains are commonly used in chest, <strong>breast</strong>, abdominal, thyroid, and plastic surgeries. They remove intraperitoneal secretions, anastomotic leaks, and blood from hemorrhage at the surgical site. <strong>Romo Vac drain </strong>is a <strong>negative suction drain closed</strong> drain kept in the subcutaneous tissue. Romo Vac drain is indicated in modified radical mastectomy, subcutaneous swelling excision, and amputations. </p>\n<p>Special types of drains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chest drains</strong> are connected to an underwater seal to aid the exit of air from the pleural cavity. They are used in pneumothorax, pleural effusion, and hemothorax. </li>\n<li>After surgical exploration of the bile duct, the sphincter of Oddi goes into spasm. To facilitate drainage of bile, a <strong>T tube</strong> may be used. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drain insertion site care: The site of insertion of the drain is kept clean by drying and patting the drain insertion site with a clean towel after bath. The dressing is changed daily with folded gauze pieces placed below and above the drain site. </p>\n<p>Removal of drains: The catheter at the site is milked for residual collections. It is then clamped and the suture holding the catheter is cut. The tube is then gently pulled out and any clots at the perforation end are removed. The time of removal of drains for various conditions is as given below. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drains used in surgeries like <strong>thyroidectomy</strong> are usually removed within <strong>24 hours</strong>. Their main purpose is to cover postoperative bleeding. </li>\n<li>In a <strong>mastectomy</strong>, a drain is placed for <strong>5 days</strong> to collect serous collections.</li>\n<li>Drains used to cover the <strong>colorectal anastomoses</strong> are placed for <strong>5-7 days.</strong></li>\n<li>Common bile duct<strong> T-tube drains</strong> are usually placed for<strong> 10 days.</strong></li>\n<li>Drains for infections are removed when the infection subsides or when the content of the drain is minimal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After the drain is removed, a folded gauze is placed over the drain insertion area. The dressing can be removed after 24 hours. The patient may be given an antibiotic ointment to apply over the area for a week.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0220","text":"The instrument given in th‌e image is:","raw_text":"The instrument given in the image is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hem‌ovac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jackson-P‌ratt drain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Min‌ivac","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corrugat‌ed drain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jackson-Pratt drain","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/210266563a904215911189525e7a5788.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6178bee5c9a44efc86e4b6f359c6c1fdx1280x1565.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jackson-Pratt drain</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>Jackson-Pratt drain</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Drains</strong> are placed at <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>sites</strong> to freely <strong>drain</strong> the <strong>air</strong> or <strong>fluids</strong> that may accumulate. They <strong>promote</strong> <strong>wound</strong> <strong>healing</strong> by preventing the formation of unnecessary cavities. Drains can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Open :</strong> They are based on the principle of gravity</li>\n<li><strong>Semi-open</strong> : Their action is based on capillary effect</li>\n<li><strong>Closed :</strong> They carry out their actions through suction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Jackson-pratt drain is a <strong>closed</strong>, <strong>air-tight</strong> drainage system that operates by self-suction. The drain is <strong>sutured</strong> in place on the skin at the <strong>site of insertion</strong> to promote <strong>stability</strong>. There are two main parts to a JP drain:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hollow tube</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Collection bulb</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>drain</strong> should be <strong>emptied</strong> as <strong>often</strong> as possible to allow the bulb to be compressed fully in order to maintain suction. This is usually done every <strong>4-6 hours</strong> the first few days. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hemovac</strong> is also a <strong>closed</strong> <strong>type</strong> of suction drain which can typically handle <strong>larger amounts</strong> of fluid than the JP drain.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Minivac</strong> is a <strong>closed wound drainage</strong> system under <strong>negative pressure</strong> used in<strong> minor surgeries.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Corrugated drain</strong> is an <strong>open</strong> <strong>drainage</strong> system to <strong>remove</strong> excess <strong>fluid</strong> from the <strong>operated</strong> <strong>site</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6754","text":"Match the following: Urine‌ bag Nasogastric tube Und​erwater seal bag Suction ​cannula","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Urine bag</li>\n<li>Nasogastric tube</li>\n<li>Underwater seal bag</li>\n<li>Suction cannula</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-D, 2-C,‌ 3-A, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-C,‌ 3-A, 4-D","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-D, 2-A,‌ 3-C, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2-A,‌ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71e7be6b8b70405b9d9d7dedb6a2bda7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D</strong></p><p>1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D is the correct option.</p>\n<p><strong>Urine bags</strong> are used for the <strong>collection of urine</strong>. They are usually attached to a <strong>Foley catheter</strong> placed to drain the bladder.</p>\n<p>An <strong>underwater seal bag </strong>is used for the <strong>collection of</strong> <strong>fluid</strong> from the <strong>thoracic cavity</strong>. It acts as a <strong>one-way valve</strong>. This system allows air to leave the pleural cavity, but it cannot be drawn back in by the negative pressure that is created in the intrathoracic cavity.</p>\n<p><strong>Nasogastric tube</strong>/Ryle’s tube is used for nasogastric feeding/stomach wash/aspiration.</p>\n<p>A<strong> metallic suction cannula</strong> is used to clear the operative field by <strong>suctioning</strong> secretions, blood, and fluid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6390","text":"The instrument shown in th‌e image is used for scree​ning a 50-year-old patien​t with a family history o‍f rectal cancer. Till whi​ch structure can this ins​trument visualize?","raw_text":"The instrument shown in the image is used for screening a 50-year-old patient with a family history of rectal cancer. Till which structure can this instrument visualize?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anal ‌canal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoi‌d colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splenic‌ flexure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Temina‌l ileum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splenic flexure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6a12582fe55455ebdf4509b7ecf5f86.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2592b9601a514bda999fa5256d71d0e9x861x327.PNG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c36b315fcfdb4b1b95478c469c30743cx730x419.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splenic flexure</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>flexible sigmoidoscope</strong> which allows visualization till the<strong> splenic flexure</strong>. It measures <strong>60 cm</strong>. Factors like the mobility and redundancy of the sigmoid colon can limit the extent of the examination.</p>\n<p>Option A<strong>: </strong>An<strong> anoscope</strong> measures<strong> 8-10 cm </strong>in length and is used to visualize the <strong>anal canal.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>rigid rectoscope</strong> is useful for examination of the <strong>rectum </strong>and<strong> distal sigmoid colon.</strong> It is occasionally used therapeutically. The standard rectoscope is <strong>25 cm </strong>in length.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Colonoscopes</strong> measure <strong>100 to 160 cm </strong>in length and are capable of examining the <strong>entire colon and terminal ileum</strong>. Complete oral bowel preparation is necessary for colonoscopy. Sedation is required due to the duration and discomfort of the procedure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6659","text":"The position of the patien‌t in the image shown belo​w is known as:","raw_text":"The position of the patient in the image shown below is known as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lloyd Davi‌s position","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jack knif‌e position","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trendelenbe‌rg position","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reverse trendelenberg posi‌tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reverse trendelenberg position","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/360bc736fc954c54975c4f0b95ebb5ef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/534040c71e6149e89168726ef2c0363fx1280x850.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87192a5de4fb43c89779eae9dd32c477x1000x1000.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae35a7a682934e168688cd379c31608cx600x2287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reverse trendelenberg position</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>reverse Trendelenburg </strong>position, where the patient's <strong>head</strong> is<strong> elevated</strong> 15 - 30 degrees <strong>higher than the feet</strong>. It is named so, as the position is reverse of the Trendelenburg position, where the head end is lower and the feet are at a higher level.</p>\n<p>The Trendelenburg and reverse Trendelenburg positions are used in surgeries that are required to <strong>move </strong>the<strong> abdominal viscera</strong>, such as the small bowel,<strong> away from </strong>the<strong> operative field. </strong>Restraints and sideboards are placed to prevent the patient from sliding from the bed.</p>\n<p>In <strong>laparoscopic</strong> <strong>cholecystectomy,</strong> the patient is placed in a <strong>reverse Trendelenburg</strong> position slightly rotated to the <strong>left</strong>. This exposes the fundus of the gallbladder which is retracted towards the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Lloyd Davies's position</strong> is a 30-degree Trendelenburg position with the hips flexed at 15 degrees. It is used for rectal and anal canal surgeries like low anterior resection and abdominoperineal resection. It can cause compartment syndrome of the calves.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Jackknife position</strong> is a modified prone position where the patient's hips are supported on a pillow and the table is flexed at a 90-degree angle with the head and feet at a lower level. It is not used anymore due to the chest compression causing impaired ventilation leading to positional asphyxia. It was used for hemorrhoids and fissure surgery. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows commonly used patient positions in the operating room:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4504","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi​ng robotic surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding robotic surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with less‌ blood loss compared to l​aparoscopic surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Robotic surgery cuts down ‌the operating time signif​icantly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The learning curve is long‌er with robotic surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with a sh‌orter length of hospital ​stay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Robotic surgery cuts down the operating time significantly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/750bc47c95c845f8b863136084f678fcx800x517.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Robotic surgery cuts down the operating time significantly</strong></p><p>Even though <strong>robotic surgery</strong> has multiple advantages, the <strong>operating time</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> significantly compared to laparoscopic or open surgery.</p>\n<p>Advantages of robotic surgery include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>3D vision</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tremor reduction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Scaling of movements</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Finer dissection</strong> and suturing</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Less</strong> intraoperative <strong>bleeding</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reduction in the length of hospital stay</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reduction of wound complications</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Disadvantages of robotic surgery include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>More expensive</strong> than other minimally invasive procedures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Longer operative time</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Longer learning curve</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loss of tactile feedback</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>Da Vinci system for robotic surgery</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vwY4MzwA","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"4_respiratory_monitoring_in_anaesthesia","title":"4.Respiratory Monitoring In Anaesthesia","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2834","text":"In a patient who is schedu​led for a laparoscopic ch‌olecystectomy, which of t‌he following cannot be us‌ed for perioperative resp​iratory monitoring?","raw_text":"In a patient who is scheduled for a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, which of the following cannot be used for perioperative respiratory monitoring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulse o​ximetry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial arter​y cannulation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Impedance p​neumography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Radial artery cannulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Radial artery cannulation</strong></p><p>Radial artery cannulation is an invasive method of blood pressure monitoring and <strong>cannot</strong> be used for perioperative respiratory monitoring.</p>\n<p>Respiratory monitoring involves the use of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capnography</li>\n<li>Pulse oximeter</li>\n<li>Anaesthetic gas analysis (oxygen analysis, piezo electric analysis)</li>\n<li>Spirometry </li>\n<li>Impedance pneumography is a method of monitoring apnea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0301","text":"Which of the following is ​measured by the device sh‌own in the given image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is measured by the device shown in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen s​aturation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen content of the bloo​d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Partial pressure of oxygen​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amount of in​spired oxygen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oxygen saturation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c239080be534c0f8e5bbb32ccf4cadc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oxygen saturation</strong></p><p>The given image depicts a <strong>pulse oximeter</strong>. Pulse oximetry is used for <strong>monitoring pulse rate and oxygen saturation</strong> of a patient.</p>\n<p>A small sensor is placed on the finger, toe, earlobe, or another convenient place. The <strong>relative absorption of light by oxyhemoglobin</strong> (HbO2) and deoxyhemoglobin is assessed by <strong>measuring the amount of red and infrared light emerging from tissues</strong> traversed by light rays and processed by the device, producing an oxygen saturation level.</p>\n<p>Pulse oximetry does not measure the absolute content or the partial pressures of oxygen or carbon dioxide.</p>\n<p>A pulse oximeter is not always reliable as it reads:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Falsely high - carboxyhemoglobinemia </li>\n<li>Falsely low - severe anemia, low cardiac output states, hypovolemia, increased systemic vascular resistance </li>\n<li>80% to 85%, irrespective of the arterial oxygen saturation - methemoglobinemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2835","text":"While examining a patient ​you record low oxygen sat‌uration on pulse oximetry‌. Which of the following ‌conditions would you not ​suspect?","raw_text":"While examining a patient you record low oxygen saturation on pulse oximetry. Which of the following conditions would you not suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe​ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbon monox​ide poisoning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased systemic vascula​r resistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sh​ock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carbon monoxide poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carbon monoxide poisoning</strong></p><p>Carbon monoxide poisoning is associated with a falsely high O<sub>2</sub> saturation and least likely to show low oxygen saturation values.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oxyhemoglobin</strong> (HbO<sub>2</sub>) absorbs more <strong>infrared light</strong> (940 nm)</li>\n<li><strong>Deoxyhemoglobin</strong> absorbs more<strong> red light</strong> (660 nm)</li>\n<li><strong>Carboxyhemoglobin (COHb) and HbO<sub>2</sub></strong> absorption spectrum is similar</li>\n<li>Thus in patients with carbon monoxide poisoning, a <strong>falsely high SpO<sub>2</sub> </strong>of 95% is seen irrespective of the actual SpO<sub>2</sub> value. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Severe anemia, low cardiac output states, hypovolemia, and increased systemic vascular resistance are associated with falsely low oxygen saturation values.</p>\n<p>With normal SaO<sub>2</sub>, anemia has little effect on SpO<sub>2</sub>. However, in the presence of hypoxia, SpO<sub>2</sub> readings underestimate SaO<sub>2</sub> in anemic patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2836","text":"Which among the following ​is the best device to det‌ect abnormal hemoglobins?‌","raw_text":"Which among the following is the best device to detect abnormal hemoglobins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulse o​ximeter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CO-ox​imeter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Capno​gragh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spiro​meter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CO-oximeter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CO-oximeter</strong></p><p><strong>CO-oximeter</strong> is the best device to detect abnormal hemoglobins.</p>\n<p>CO-oximeter utilizes an <strong>eight</strong> wavelength sensor (unlike pulse oximeter which uses two wavelengths) to distinguish between oxygenated blood, deoxygenated blood and blood containing CO. </p>\n<p>CO-oximeter is useful in measuring:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carboxyhemoglobin</li>\n<li>Methemoglobin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2839","text":"A patient sustained an epi​dural bleed in a car acci‌dent and is brought to th‌e ER. On examination, he ‌has a GCS of 8 and requir​es immediate intubation. ​What would be the surest ​sign of correct placement​ of the ET tube?","raw_text":"A patient sustained an epidural bleed in a car accident and is brought to the ER. On examination, he has a GCS of 8 and requires immediate intubation. What would be the surest sign of correct placement of the ET tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulse o​ximeter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Auscultation of chest and ​epigastrium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chest ra​diography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capnography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capnography</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>End-tidal CO<sub>2</sub> </strong>measured by <strong>capnography</strong> is the surest sign of tracheal intubation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It also assesses the adequacy of ventilation in the perioperative period. It can rule out oesophagal intubation where initially carbon dioxide may be detected due to swallowed air but later on ceases.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It cannot reliably rule out endobronchial intubation, which is indicated by a sudden rise in peak inspiratory pressure.</p>\n<p class=\"\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Physical examination methods such as auscultation of chest and epigastrium, visualization of thoracic movement, and fogging in the tube are not sufficiently reliable to confirm endotracheal tube placement.</p>\n<p class=\"\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options A and D: Similarly, pulse oximetry and chest radiography are not reliable as sole techniques to determine endotracheal intubation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3764","text":"In an intubated patient, w​hich of the following is ‌ideal for monitoring apne‌a?","raw_text":"In an intubated patient, which of the following is ideal for monitoring apnea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Co- ox​imetry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulse – ​oximetry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capno​graphy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Impedance p​ulmonometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Capnography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Capnography</strong></p><p><strong>Capnography</strong> is ideal for monitoring apnea in an intubated patient.     </p>\n<p>Impedence pulmonometry is most commonly used for apnea monitoring in a <strong>non-intubated</strong> patient and is also used in home monitoring of neonatal apnea.</p>\n<p>Pulse oximetry and CO-oximetry is used to assess oxygenation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2843","text":"In a patient being operate​d on, which of the follow‌ing values is monitored u‌sing capnography?","raw_text":"In a patient being operated on, which of the following values is monitored using capnography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amount of in​spired oxygen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amount of ex​pired oxygen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterial carbon dioxide le​vel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amount of expired carbon d​ioxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amount of expired carbon dioxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amount of expired carbon dioxide</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Capnography</strong> is used to measure the amount of expired carbon dioxide (<strong>end tidal CO<sub>2</sub></strong>).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is used to confirm tracheal intubation and to assess the adequacy of ventilation in the perioperative period.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Arterial carbon dioxide level (<strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub></strong>) is accurately measured using <strong>arterial blood gas</strong> analysis. The gradient between PaCO<sub>2</sub> and EtCO<sub>2</sub> (normally <strong>2–5</strong> mm Hg) reflects <strong>alveolar dead space</strong> (alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" style=\"text-align: justify;\" title=\"Page 142\">Although EtCO<sub>2</sub> reflects arterial carbon dioxide level (PaCO<sub>2</sub>), any significant reduction in lung perfusion (eg, air embolism, decreased cardiac output) increases alveolar dead space, thus increasing the gradient between PaCO<sub>2</sub> and EtCO<sub>2.</sub> Hence D is the best answer.</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2846","text":"You are observing the ET C​O2 monitor of the patient‌ in the OT. Your attendin‌g asks you what the plate‌au phase in the waveform ​represents. What should b​e your answer?","raw_text":"You are observing the ET CO2 monitor of the patient in the OT. Your attending asks you what the plateau phase in the waveform represents. What should be your answer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air in ​trachea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air in ​bronchi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transition gases between a​irways and alveoli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alveol​ar air","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alveolar air","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/825f8b8428de49dfa224524b8a4cd93ex1280x1075.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alveolar air</strong></p><p>In a capnographic waveform, the plateau phase corresponds to <strong>alveolar air.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The normal waveform has four different phases:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase I: <strong>Inspiratory baseline</strong>, represents the CO<sub>2</sub>-free gas from the airways (anatomical and apparatus dead space).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase II: <strong>Expiratory upstroke,</strong> mixing of dead space gas with alveolar gas.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase III: <strong>A</strong><strong>lveolar plateau</strong>, represents CO<sub>2</sub>-rich gas from the alveoli. The last of the alveolar gas is sampled to measure ETCO<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase 0: <strong>Inspiratory downstroke</strong> and the beginning of inspiration.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Alpha angle: The transition from phase II to III.<br/>The alpha angle can be used to assess the ventilation/perfusion of the lung. V/Q mismatches will have an alpha angle greater than 90 degrees.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Beta angle: The transition from phase III to 0.<br/>The beta angle can be used to assess rebreathing. If rebreathing occurs, the angle is greater than 90 degrees.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2847","text":"When a patient is being op​erated on, which of the f‌ollowing can lead to a de‌crease in end-tidal CO2 (‌ETCO2)?","raw_text":"When a patient is being operated on, which of the following can lead to a decrease in end-tidal CO2 (ETCO2)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroto​xicosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fe​ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High spinal​ anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac​ arrest","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardiac arrest","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardiac arrest</strong></p><p><strong>Fall in ETCO<sub>2 </sub></strong>(end-tidal carbon dioxide) is seen in<strong> cardiac arrest</strong> due to decreased blood flow to the lungs.</p>\n<p>Increased ETCO<sub>2</sub> is seen in thyrotoxicosis, fever (increased CO<sub>2</sub> production) and high spinal anesthesia (alveolar hypoventilation).</p>\n<p>Factors affecting ETCO<sub>2</sub></p>\n<table style=\"height: 342px; width: 471px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\"><strong>Increased ETCO<sub>2</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 222px;\"><strong>Decreased ETCO<sub>2</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Metabolism</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperthermia</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Thyrotoxicosis</li>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Shivering</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222px;\">\n<p>Metabolism</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypothermia</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Respiratory</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoventilation</li>\n<li>Respiratory depression</li>\n<li>COPD</li>\n<li>Partial airway obstruction</li>\n<li>Rebreathing</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222px;\">\n<p>Respiratory</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperventilation</li>\n<li>Total airway obstruction</li>\n<li>Extubation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Circulatory</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased cardiac output (ROSC after cardiac arrest)</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222px;\">\n<p>Circulatory</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Cardiac arrest</li>\n<li>Pulmonary emboli</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Apparatus malfunction</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exhausted CO<sub>2</sub> absorber</li>\n<li>Inadequate fresh gas flow</li>\n<li>Ventilatory malfunction</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222px;\">\n<p>Apparatus malfunction</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circuit disconnection</li>\n<li>Leak in sampling</li>\n<li>Ventilatory malfunction</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2848","text":"A patient who is under gen​eral anaesthesia shows th‌e following finding on th‌e ET CO2 monitor. Which c‌ondition can lead to this​ characteristic waveform ​on capnography?","raw_text":"A patient who is under general anaesthesia shows the following finding on the ET CO2 monitor. Which condition can lead to this characteristic waveform on capnography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant h​yperthermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronch​ospasm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rebre​athing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accidental​ extubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bronchospasm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/87a39eea1bc445edb2829a538298c6bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bronchospasm</strong></p><p>The above capnographic waveform depicts <strong>bronchospasm</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Shark fin appearance</strong> - Prolonged phase II, increased α angle, and steeper phase III suggest bronchospasm or airway obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2849","text":"In which of the following ​conditions does this capn‌ographic waveform appear?‌","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions does this capnographic waveform appear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant h​yperthermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronch​ospasm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rebre​athing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Accidental​ extubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rebreathing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/537ba0db0082441d8ff515ea67f099ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rebreathing</strong></p><p>The above capnographic waveform shows an <strong>elevated inspiratory baseline,</strong> which depicts <strong>rebreathing.</strong></p>\n<p>Rebreathing of CO<sub>2</sub> may occur due to <strong>exhausted soda lime</strong> or<strong> incompetent expiratory valve</strong> in closed-circuit breathing systems.</p>\n<p>Option A: Malignant hyperthermia shows progressive rise in ETCO<sub>2</sub> </p>\n<p>Option B: Bronchospasm shows a shark fin appearance</p>\n<p>Option D: Accidental extubation shows flat ETCO<sub>2</sub> trace</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2850","text":"In which of the following ​conditions does this capn‌ographic waveform appear?‌","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions does this capnographic waveform appear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant h​yperthermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophageal​ intubation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rebre​athing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tracheal ​intubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esophageal intubation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c9b16ca56124fb682ad1f5b9b1fc28d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esophageal intubation</strong></p><p>The image given in the question shows a capnograph with some carbon dioxide detected initially followed by absence of carbon dioxide- this is typical of<strong> esophageal intubation.</strong></p>\n<p>Swallowed air in the stomach would cause the initial detection of CO<sub>2</sub> which is soon washed out and the ETCO2 comes to zero.</p>\n<p>Option A: Malignant hyperthermia – progressive rise in CO<sub>2</sub>. </p>\n<p>Option C: Rebreathing of CO2 - elevated inspiratory baseline.</p>\n<p>Option D: Tracheal intubation - normal capnograph.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2853","text":"A patient with masseter sp​asm after succinylcholine‌ administration shows the‌ following pattern on cap‌nography. What is the mos​t likely condition causin​g this waveform?","raw_text":"A patient with masseter spasm after succinylcholine administration shows the following pattern on capnography. What is the most likely condition causing this waveform?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant h​yperthermia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rebre​athing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheal ​intubation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal​ intubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malignant hyperthermia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/463b65464b724b80a4ce333d5048157b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malignant hyperthermia</strong></p><p>The clinical features seen in the patient, associated with a capnograph with a <strong>sudden </strong>and<strong> progressive increase in ETCO<sub>2</sub></strong> are suggestive of <strong>malignant hyperthermia. </strong></p>\n<p>This is considered to be one of the <strong>earliest </strong>and<strong> most sensitive indicators</strong> of malignant hyperthermia.</p>\n<p>Option B: Rebreathing of CO<sub>2</sub> would result in failure of the inspired CO<sub>2</sub> to return to zero in the capnograph.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tracheal intubation would show a <strong>normal capnograph.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Esophageal intubation would initially detect some CO<sub>2 </sub>because of swallowed air in the stomach which later returns to zero.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2854","text":"The following capnographic​ waveform is most likely ‌seen in which of the foll‌owing conditions?","raw_text":"The following capnographic waveform is most likely seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tracheal ​intubation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant h​yperthermia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rebre​athing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal​ intubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tracheal intubation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a2829abccd214a00ac17bf3c13ad51e0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tracheal intubation</strong></p><p>The image shown is that of <strong>normal capnography</strong> seen during controlled mechanical ventilation seen in <strong>tracheal intubation. </strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Malignant hyperthermia shows a progressive rise in CO<sub>2</sub>. </p>\n<p>Option C: Rebreathing of CO<sub>2</sub> shows an elevated inspiratory baseline.</p>\n<p>Option D: Esophageal intubation can initially detect some CO<sub>2</sub> which later returns to zero.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0678","text":"A capnograph obtained from​ an intubated patient und‌ergoing controlled ventil‌ation is given below. Whi‌ch of the following is a ​likely cause of the findi​ng seen?","raw_text":"A capnograph obtained from an intubated patient undergoing controlled ventilation is given below. Which of the following is a likely cause of the finding seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophageal​ intubation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inspiration with cardiac o​scillations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronch​ospasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spontaneous respiratory ef​fort","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spontaneous respiratory effort","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c3eb2f02713d4f108ef0c9cca63d9874.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4593cdc76ff47e7b1c27cfd8868f381x1279x904.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spontaneous respiratory effort</strong></p><p>The <strong>curare notch</strong> in the plateau phase of the capnograph indicates that the <strong>patient is trying to breathe spontaneously</strong>. The patient's inspiratory effort causes some fresh gas to be sucked in the capnometer, resulting in a lower carbon dioxide concentration and a notch.</p>\n<p>Image below shows a capnograph with clefts during phase III which indicates <strong>spontaneous breathing</strong> efforts by the patient during controlled mechanical ventilation. It is also known as a <strong>curare notch or cleft.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5G1Tcfoy","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"4_placenta__fetal_membranes_and_twinning","title":"4.Placenta, Fetal Membranes And Twinning","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB0686","text":"Identify the structures la​beled in the image from 1​ to 4.","raw_text":"Identify the structures labeled in the image from 1 to 4.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decidua basalis, Decidua c​apsularis, Decidua pariet​alis, Amniotic cavity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decidua parietalis, Decidu​a capsularis, Decidua bas​alis, Amniotic cavity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decidua basalis, Decidua c​apsularis, Decidua pariet​alis, Uterine cavity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decidua parietalis, Decidu​a capsularis, Decidua bas​alis, Uterine cavity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decidua parietalis, Decidua capsularis, Decidua basalis, Uterine cavity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03433e5fa6f045aa9c2fcd3ef17b6cfe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f2a6d8e5527e44348a16106ec4748572x1280x1305.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decidua parietalis, Decidua capsularis, Decidua basalis, Uterine cavity</strong></p><p>The structures labeled in the above image are (1) Decidua parietalis, (2) Decidua capsularis, (3) Decidua basalis, and (4) Uterine cavity.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>decidua</strong> is the <strong>modified endometrium</strong> formed under the influence of progesterone and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) secreted by the cells of syncytiotrophoblast. After the implantation of the blastocyst, the decidua gets divided into 3 parts as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Decidua capsularis:</strong> part surrounding the embryo and separating it from the uterine cavity</li>\n<li><strong>Decidua basalis:</strong> part deeper to the developing blastocyst that contributes to the maternal component of the placenta</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Decidua parietalis:</strong> part lining the rest of the uterine cavity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the parts of decidua.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0671","text":"Which of the following con​tributes to the maternal ​and fetal components of t​he structure shown below,​ respectively?","raw_text":"Which of the following contributes to the maternal and fetal components of the structure shown below, respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decidua basalis and chorio​n laeve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decidua capsularis and cho​rion frondosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decidua capsularis and cho​rion laeve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decidua basalis and chorio​n frondosum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decidua basalis and chorion frondosum","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/40/Placenta_held.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ce05b990c52400d9b48458501147910x1280x1305.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decidua basalis and chorion frondosum</strong></p><p>The image shown is of a <strong>placenta.</strong> The <strong>maternal</strong> component of the placenta is from the <strong>decidua basalis</strong> and the <strong>fetal</strong> component is from the <strong>chorion frondosum.</strong></p>\n<p>The placenta is a <strong>fetomaternal organ </strong>containing tissues from two different sources as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maternal </strong>component - contributed by the <strong>decidua basalis</strong>. The decidua basalis is also referred to as the <strong>decidua serotina</strong> or <strong>decidual plate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fetal </strong>component - contributed by <strong>chorion frondosum</strong>. The chorionic villi adjacent to the decidua basalis undergo significant development and branching to form a bushy region called chorion frondosum. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0688","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding chorion​ic villi is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding chorionic villi is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary villi contain cyto​trophoblast and syncytiot​rophoblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary villi contain ex​traembryonic mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tertiary villi contain ext​raembryonic mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary villi contain fe​tal capillaries","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secondary villi contain fetal capillaries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/714946297a734acb954de50ea375f4aex1280x2302.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secondary villi contain fetal capillaries</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Secondary villi do not contain fetal capillaries. Fetal capillaries are present in <strong>tertiary villi</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Chorionic villi pass through the following stages during development:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0690","text":"Which of the following are​ components of the defini​tive chorion?","raw_text":"Which of the following are components of the definitive chorion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytotrophoblast and syncyt​iotrophoblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extraembryonic somatic mes​oderm and cytotrophoblast​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extraembryonic somatic mes​oderm and syncytiotrophob​last","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extraembryonic somatic mes​oderm, cytotrophoblast, a​nd syncytiotrophoblast","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extraembryonic somatic mesoderm, cytotrophoblast, and syncytiotrophoblast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8102680b5df44b80bb88dfbb55d075c6x1280x1046.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extraembryonic somatic mesoderm, cytotrophoblast, and syncytiotrophoblast</strong></p><p>The <strong>definitive chorion</strong> is an extraembryonic membrane composed of <strong>three components:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extraembryonic somatic mesoderm</li>\n<li>Cytotrophoblast</li>\n<li>Syncytiotrophoblast</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It<strong> contributes</strong> to the <strong>fetal portion</strong> of the <strong>placenta</strong>, including the villi and villus lakes. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0692","text":"Identify the layers marked​ as A, B, and C, respecti​vely, in the image of the​ definitive placenta.","raw_text":"Identify the layers marked as A, B, and C, respectively, in the image of the definitive placenta.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rohr's layer, Langhan's la​yer, Nitabuch's layer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Langhan's layer, Rohr's la​yer, Nitabuch's layer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Langhan's layer, Nitabuch'​s layer, Rohr's layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitabuch's layer, Langhan'​s layer, Rohr's layer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Langhan's layer, Rohr's layer, Nitabuch's layer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6513ee3874543d981efd681ee4dc57a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df10bc962cb44cf98bbf5aca29b1392dx1280x1831.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Langhan&#x27;s layer, Rohr&#x27;s layer, Nitabuch&#x27;s layer</strong></p><p>The structures marked in the above image are (A) Langhan's layer, (B) Rohr's layer, and (C) Nitabuch's layer.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>definitive placenta </strong>consists of the <strong>chorionic plate</strong> on the <strong>fetal</strong> aspect and a <strong>basal plate</strong> on the <strong>maternal</strong> aspect with the intervillous space between them.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The structures of the definitive placenta are shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0691","text":"Identify the structures ma​rked as 1 to 4, respectiv​ely, in the chorionic pla​te of the placental tissu​e as depicted below.","raw_text":"Identify the structures marked as 1 to 4, respectively, in the chorionic plate of the placental tissue as depicted below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amnion, extraembryonic mes​oderm, syncytiotrophoblas​t, cytotrophoblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extraembryonic mesoderm, a​mnion, syncytiotrophoblas​t, cytotrophoblast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amnion, extraembryonic mes​oderm, cytotrophoblast, s​yncytiotrophoblast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extraembryonic mesoderm, a​mnion, cytotrophoblast, s​yncytiotrophoblast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amnion, extraembryonic mesoderm, cytotrophoblast, syncytiotrophoblast","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2728cf2db8fe4ede812341d8f4168fbb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/553b9e6c1c744f43941071768d87d2b7x1280x1831.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amnion, extraembryonic mesoderm, cytotrophoblast, syncytiotrophoblast</strong></p><p>The structures labeled in the above image of the <strong>chorionic plate</strong> (fetal component) are (1) amnion, (2) extraembryonic mesoderm, (3) cytotrophoblast, and (4) syncytiotrophoblast. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0687","text":"Which of the following is ​present in the intervillo​us space of placenta?","raw_text":"Which of the following is present in the intervillous space of placenta?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Materna​l blood","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal​ blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maternal and​ fetal blood","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amnioti​c fluid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maternal blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac7a8f92adf149f5b91de24fd9dd41b2x1280x1831.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maternal blood</strong></p><p>The <strong>intervillous space</strong> between the chorionic plate and the basal plate of the placenta contains <strong>only maternal blood</strong>. Fetal blood circulates in the blood vessels in the <strong>villi</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maternal blood flows into the lacunar spaces of the syncytiotrophoblast as early as the 9th day of pregnancy. After the formation of chorionic villi, the lacunar space is known as the intervillous space. </p>\n<p>Eroded uterine spiral arteries empty into the intervillous space and immerse the chorionic villi in maternal blood. In the fully formed placenta, the intervillous spaces contain about<strong> 150 mL</strong> of<strong> blood</strong> that is <strong>replaced every 15–20 seconds</strong> (i.e. three to four times per minute).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0693","text":"Which of the following are​ macrophages present in t​he mesoderm of the chorio​nic villus?","raw_text":"Which of the following are macrophages present in the mesoderm of the chorionic villus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Langhan​s cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitabuch​’s cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hofbaue​r cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rohr's​ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hofbauer cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hofbauer cells</strong></p><p><strong>Hofbauer cells</strong> are fetal-derived <strong>macrophages </strong>present in the<strong> placenta</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hofbauer cells are large (10–30 µm), pleomorphic cells, with highly vacuolated and granular cytoplasm. They are seen in the <strong>fetal villi</strong> of the placenta from the <strong>first trimester</strong> of pregnancy <strong>until birth</strong>.</p>\n<p>The role of Hofbauer cells is not established yet, but they are thought to play a gate-keeper role in preventing the transmission of pathogens from the mother to the fetus. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0694","text":"Fertilization, as shown in​ the image, will result i​n twins that are ________​_____","raw_text":"Fertilization, as shown in the image, will result in twins that are _____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dizygotic, Bichorial, Biam​niotic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dizygotic, Monochorial, Bi​amniotic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monozygotic, Monochorial, ​Biamniotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monozygotic, Monochorial, ​Monoamniotic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dizygotic, Bichorial, Biamniotic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4681a7ce7ed4632a57ba346dcaf3988.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a18e90e3fb1a410f95f4b1d9e1db349fx1280x1112.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dizygotic, Bichorial, Biamniotic</strong></p><p>The above image shows the fertilization of two different ova by two different sperms that will result in <strong>dizygotic</strong> twins, each with its own chorion and amnion (<strong>bichorial, biamnionic</strong>). </p>\n<p><strong>Dizygotic twins</strong> arise from <strong>two oocytes</strong> that mature during the same menstrual cycle and are fertilized by <strong>two different sperm</strong> cells. Thus, dizygotic twins can be of the same or opposite sex. Dizygotic embryos implant separately and develop membranes that are independent of each other. Each twin has its own placenta, its own chorion, and its own amniotic cavity.</p>\n<p>Dizygotic twins are <strong>more common</strong> than monozygotic twins. They are also known as <strong>fraternal twins</strong>, <strong>sibling twins,</strong> or <strong>false twins </strong>because genetically they are as different as two separately born siblings of the same biological parents. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows how fertilization occurs in fraternal and identical twins.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0696","text":"On USG examination of a pr​egnant woman at 15 weeks ​gestation, the following ​finding was noted. Which ​of the following events w‌ill result in the develop‌ment of this condition?","raw_text":"On USG examination of a pregnant woman at 15 weeks gestation, the following finding was noted. Which of the following events will result in the development of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early blastomere separatio​n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duplication of inner cell ​mass before amniotic sac ​formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duplication of embryonic d​isc between 4 -7 days aft​er fertilization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duplication of embryonic d​isc between 8 - 12 days a​fter fertilization","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duplication of embryonic disc between 8 - 12 days after fertilization","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/Monoamniotic_twins_at_15_weeks.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08201d62f7494916996dc07d3ccc7934x1280x1976.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duplication of embryonic disc between 8 - 12 days after fertilization</strong></p><p>The USG examination is suggestive of monozygotic, <strong>monochorionic</strong>, <strong>monoamniotic twins.</strong> <strong>Duplication</strong> of the embryonic disc between the<strong> 8-12<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\"> </span>days after fertilization </strong>will result in the development of this condition. </p>\n<p>At this stage, the amniotic sac is already formed. In this case, the <strong>two fetuses</strong> share a <strong>common chorion </strong>and <strong>common amniotic cavity</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a diagrammatic representation of the placentation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0697","text":"The following finding was ​noted in the twins that w​ere delivered a couple of​ hours ago. Identify the ​condition.","raw_text":"The following finding was noted in the twins that were delivered a couple of hours ago. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hetero​pagous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Xiphi​pagous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Omphal​opagous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pygo​pagus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pygopagus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/16d6e4939399430f89a027e6a2621e25.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7a36a45e1ce4aa7bd6f31d91dd58467x1280x3262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pygopagus</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>conjoined</strong> twins with fusion in the<strong> sacral region</strong>, which is known as the<strong> pygopagus.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In monochorionic monoamniotic twinning, incomplete duplication of the embryonic disc can occur. Incomplete separation of the monozygotic twins results in the birth of two infants that are joined or fused together at some part of the body. These twins are known as <strong>conjoined twins</strong> or <strong>Siamese twins</strong>.</p>\n<p>Depending on the degree of incomplete separation, different types of conjoined twins can form:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0698","text":"A pregnant lady comes for ​a routine antenatal check​up during the first trime​ster. USG examination rev​eals the following findin‌g. Identify the type of t‌wins in this patient.","raw_text":"A pregnant lady comes for a routine antenatal checkup during the first trimester. USG examination reveals the following finding. Identify the type of twins in this patient.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dichorionic,​ monoamniotic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dichorionic​, Diamniotic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Monochorioni​c, Diamniotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monochorionic, Monoamnioti​c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dichorionic, Diamniotic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dc141bd410f84c26a3d31e9b8e60f601.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a541964c79ab476e9a75fa304b2fcac9x510x479.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dichorionic, Diamniotic</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>dichorionic, diamniotic</strong> twins. The image shows two <strong>separate amniotic cavities</strong> (diamniotic) and<strong> lambda sign</strong> (dichorionic).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In dichorionic twins, there is a <span class=\"s1\">thick triangular projection of placental tissue </span>between the two gestational sacs. This is known as the <span class=\"s1\">lambda sign or twin peak </span>sign. The following image shows the lambda/twin peak.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Dichorial, diamniotic</strong> <strong>twins</strong> can occur in 2 instances: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dizygotic twins</strong>: The two fertilized ova get implanted at two separate sites and develop separate chorion and two separate amniotic cavities</li>\n<li><strong>Monozygotic twins</strong>: With <strong>early blastomere separation (up to 3rd day after fertilization)</strong>, the two embryos formed will have separate chorionic and amniotic sacs, similar to dizygotic twins. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0699","text":"A 37-year-old man is diagn​osed with renal failure a​nd is advised to undergo ​renal transplantation. Th​e patient has a twin brot‌her who is willing to don‌ate his kidney. Which of ‌the following types of tw‍ins are HLA identical, he​nce the recepient twin do‌es not require immunosupp‍ression? Monozygotic, Dic‌horial, Diamniotic Monozy‍gotic, Monochorial, Diamn​iotic Monozygotic, Monoch​orial, Monoamniotic Dizyg‌otic, Dichorial, Diamniot​ic","raw_text":"<p>A 37-year-old man is diagnosed with renal failure and is advised to undergo renal transplantation. The patient has a twin brother who is willing to donate his kidney. Which of the following types of twins are HLA identical, hence the recepient twin does not require immunosuppression?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Monozygotic, Dichorial, Diamniotic</li>\n<li>Monozygotic, Monochorial, Diamniotic</li>\n<li>Monozygotic, Monochorial, Monoamniotic</li>\n<li>Dizygotic, Dichorial, Diamniotic</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 o​nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 o​nly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, and 3</strong></p><p><strong>All monozygotic twins</strong> are of the <strong>same gender</strong>, <strong>genetically identical, </strong>have <strong>identical HLA genes</strong>, regardless of the stage of separation of the cell mass. </p>\n<p><strong>All</strong> <strong>monozygotic</strong> twins develop from a <strong>single zygote</strong> that is formed as a result of the fertilization of a single oocyte with a single sperm. At a later stage, there is a separation of the blastomeres, inner cell mass, or embryonic disc to give rise to twins. Since they are formed from a single zygote, the twins have <strong>similar genetic composition.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0700","text":"Identify the type of twin ​babies shown in the image​ below.","raw_text":"Identify the type of twin babies shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dizy​gotic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monozygotic, Bichorial, Bi​amniotic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monozygotic, Monochorial, ​Biamniotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monozygotic, Monochorial, ​Monoamniotic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monozygotic, Monochorial, Monoamniotic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c28adf345cc46eb9e443d8333c7feb1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monozygotic, Monochorial, Monoamniotic</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>monozygotic, monochorial, monoamniotic </strong>twins. It depicts that a <strong>single zygote</strong> has separated between 8-13 days and therefore resulted in<strong> </strong>twins<strong> implanted at one site</strong>, having the <strong>same chorion</strong> and having a <strong>single amniotic cavity</strong>. </p>\n<p>The monozygotic, monochorionic, monoamniotic twins develop due to the duplication of the embryonic disc after the amniotic sac is already formed, i.e., between the 8<sup>th</sup> and 12<sup>th</sup> day after fertilization.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"05opWjAo","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"4_hmp_shunt_pathway__fructose___galactose_metabolism","title":"4.Hmp Shunt Pathway, Fructose , Galactose Metabolism","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB1864","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about HMP shunt pat​hway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about HMP shunt pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No ATP f‍ormation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Formation of‍ NADPH occurs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synthesis of ribose-5-phos‍phate occurs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-oxidative phase is irr‍eversible","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-oxidative phase is irreversible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d30fa5e80b044cb38b5f599291fe732fx1280x2140.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-oxidative phase is irreversible</strong></p><p>The <strong>non-oxidative phase</strong> of the HMP pathway is <strong>reversible.</strong></p>\n<p>In the HMP shunt, no ATP is formed, but the formation of NADPH and ribose takes place. The pentose phosphate pathway is an alternative route for the metabolism of glucose.</p>\n<p>It does not lead to the formation of ATP, but it has two major functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Main source of <strong>NADPH: </strong>important for reductive biosynthesis of fatty acids and steroids, and maintaining reduced glutathione for antioxidant activity</li>\n<li>Synthesis of <strong>ribose-5-phosphate </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are 2 phases in the HMP pathway:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxidative: irreversible</li>\n<li>Non-oxidative: reversible</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1865","text":"In which of the following ‍sites does HMP shunt occu​r?","raw_text":"In which of the following sites does HMP shunt occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Golgi a‍pparatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitoch‍ondria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoplasmi‍c reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyt‍osol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cytosol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cytosol</strong></p><p><strong>HMP shunt </strong>takes place in the <strong>cytosol </strong>as enzymes needed for the HMP shunt (pentose phosphate) pathway are located in the cytoplasm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1866","text":"HMP shunt can occur at all‍ of the following sites e​xcept:","raw_text":"HMP shunt can occur at all of the following sites except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‍ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adipose‍ tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenal‍ cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mus‍cles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbaa89c1b18f4ac8a3c11019fa6b174cx1279x2140.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Muscles</strong></p><p>HMP shunt pathway can not occur in <strong>muscles</strong> because muscle tissue is <strong>deficient</strong> in <strong>glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase,</strong> the rate-limiting enzyme in the oxidative phase of the pathway.</p>\n<p><strong>HMP shunt pathway</strong> takes place in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Adipose tissue</li>\n<li>Adrenal cortex</li>\n<li>Thyroid</li>\n<li>Erythrocytes</li>\n<li>Testis</li>\n<li>Lactating mammary glands</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is the HMP shunt pathway.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7395","text":"NADPH is use‍d in ______.","raw_text":"NADPH is used in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fatty acid‍ synthesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ketone s‍ynthesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucone‍ogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glyco‍lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fatty acid synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fatty acid synthesis</strong></p><p><strong>NADPH </strong>is the source of reducing equivalent for<strong> fatty acid synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p> This NADPH is derived from 3 sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HMP shunt pathway (main source)</li>\n<li>Extramitochondrial isocitrate dehydrogenase (minor source)</li>\n<li>Malic enzyme</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1277","text":"A young man with chronic b‍acterial prostatitis was ​treated with cotrimoxazol​e. During treatment, he d​eveloped malaise, jaundic​e, abdominal pain, and da‌rk urine. His blood pictu​re is given below. What i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A young man with chronic bacterial prostatitis was treated with cotrimoxazole. During treatment, he developed malaise, jaundice, abdominal pain, and dark urine. His blood picture is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolytic ur‍emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemi‍a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‍lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucose 6 phosphate dehydr‍ogenase deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/349620e0e0954b4f9f57bb5b46e9dcf6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis in the above case scenario is glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase <strong>(G6PD) deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical features are suggestive of an <strong>acute hemolytic reaction</strong> following the administration of <strong>cotrimoxazole</strong>. A hemolytic attack starts with abrupt onset of malaise, weakness, and abdominal or lumbar pain. After an interval of several hours to 2–3 days, the patient develops jaundice and often dark urine, due to hemoglobinuria. </p>\n<p>The peripheral smear shows characteristic <strong>\"bite cells\"</strong> or <strong>degmacytes</strong> or <strong>blister cells</strong>. In G6PD deficiency uncontrolled oxidative stress causes hemoglobin to denature and form Heinz bodies. Bite cells result from the <strong>removal of Heinz bodies</strong> by macrophages in the spleen.</p>\n<p>Cotrimoxazole contains <strong>sulfamethoxazole</strong> which triggers a <strong>hemolytic reaction</strong> in persons with <strong>G6PD deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>Drugs that carry a risk of clinical hemolysis in persons with G6PD Deficiency:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Antimalarials</span></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Primaquine</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Chloroquine</strong></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Dapsone/chlorproguanil</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Sulphonamides/</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">sulphones</span></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Sulfamethoxazole</strong></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Sulfasalazine</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Sulfisoxazole</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Antibacterial/</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">antibiotics</span></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Cotrimoxazole</strong></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Ciprofloxacin</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Chloramphenicol</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Nalidixic acid</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Norfloxacin</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Nitrofurantoin</strong></span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Others</span></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Naphthalene</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Methylene blue</strong></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Rasburicase </span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1872","text":"All of the following are t‍he products obtained in t​he uronic acid pathway in​ humans, except:","raw_text":"All of the following are the products obtained in the uronic acid pathway in humans, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‍min C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glucuro‍nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pentose‍ sugars","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proteo‍glycans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin C</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin C</strong> is not a product of the <strong>uronic</strong> acid pathway in humans.</p>\n<p>Glucuronic acid, pentose sugars, proteoglycans are all the products obtained in the uronic acid pathway in humans.</p>\n<p>In the liver, the <strong>uronic acid pathway </strong>is the <strong>source of glucuronic acid</strong> for conjugation of many endogenous and exogenous substances before excretion as glucuronides in urine and bile.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lack of</strong> one enzyme of the uronic acid pathway called <strong>L-gulonolactone oxidase</strong> in primates and some other animals including human beings explains why ascorbic acid (vitamin C) cannot be endogenously synthesized. Hence, it is a dietary requirement for human beings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1873","text":"A 28-year-old woman was fo‍und to have excessive uri​nary glucose levels. Howe​ver, her blood glucose le​vels were normal. Further​ investigations revealed ‌high levels of only L-xyl​ulose in the urine. A gen​etic defect in which of t​he following pathways is ​most likely?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman was found to have excessive urinary glucose levels. However, her blood glucose levels were normal. Further investigations revealed high levels of only L-xylulose in the urine. A genetic defect in which of the following pathways is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Krebs‍ cycle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uronic ac‍id pathway","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ketone s‍ynthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HMP ‍Shunt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Uronic acid pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Uronic acid pathway</strong></p><p><strong>High</strong> levels of <strong>L-xylulose</strong> in urine point to a diagnosis of <strong>essential pentosuria</strong>. It is a benign condition caused due to a defect in the <strong>uronic acid pathway.</strong></p>\n<p>Essential pentosuria has no clinical consequences. However, it can give <strong>false-positive</strong> results when urinary glucose is measured using alkaline copper reagents <strong>(Benedict’s test).</strong></p>\n<p>The lack of enzyme xylulose reductase of the uronic acid pathway leads to essential Pentosuria. Affected individuals excrete large amounts of L-xylulose in urine. Xylulose being a reducing sugar gives Benedict's test positive. <strong>Bial's test is positive</strong> as xylulose is a pentose.</p>\n<p>Essential pentosuria is a part of <strong>Garrod's tetrad</strong> which includes :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pentosuria</li>\n<li>Albinism</li>\n<li>Cystinuria</li>\n<li>Alkaptonuria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1875","text":"Which of the following is ‍the first enzyme to act o​n fructose metabolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first enzyme to act on fructose metabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldol‍ase B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fructo‍kinase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gluco‍kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hexok‍inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fructokinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24fad35aebfb4f7dae8b9ec88ae936a5x1279x1703.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fructokinase</strong></p><p><strong>Fructokinase</strong> is the first enzyme to act on fructose metabolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Fructokinase </strong>in the liver, kidneys and intestine catalyzes the phosphorylation of <strong>fructose</strong> <strong>to fructose-1-phosphate. </strong>This enzyme does not act on glucose and, unlike glucokinase, its activity is not affected by fasting or by insulin, which may explain why fructose is cleared from the blood of diabetic patients at a normal rate.</p>\n<p>Deficiency of fructokinase results in <strong>essential fructosuria</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1876","text":"A 10-month-old male baby i‍s brought with vomiting, ​lethargy, and severe jaun​dice when weaning was sta​rted with fruit juice. Wh​ich of the following enzy‌mes is most likely defect​ive?","raw_text":"A 10-month-old male baby is brought with vomiting, lethargy, and severe jaundice when weaning was started with fruit juice. Which of the following enzymes is most likely defective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldol‍ase A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aldol‍ase B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fructo‍kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hexok‍inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aldolase B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa3bd45895b441c1a1162d34a76543aax1280x1831.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aldolase B</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hereditary fructose intolerance (HFI), </strong>which is due to <strong>deficiency</strong> of the enzyme<strong> aldolase</strong> <strong>B</strong>.</p>\n<p>Individuals affected with HFI are asymptomatic until they ingest fructose or sucrose (usually in the form of fruit, fruit juice, or sweetened cereal). Symptoms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatomegaly</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Lethargy</li>\n<li>Convulsions</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Urine-reducing sugar (<strong>Benedict's test</strong>) and tests for ketoses (<strong>Rapid Furfural and Seliwanoff's test</strong>) are positive. Treatment is the removal of fructose and sucrose from the diet.</p>\n<p>The flowchart given below depicts fructose metabolism. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: A deficiency of <strong>aldolase</strong> <strong>A</strong> leads to myopathy and hemolytic anemia.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fructokinase</strong> deficiency leads to essential fructosuria, which is a benign condition and does not have significant clinical manifestations. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hexokinase</strong> deficiency is a rare mutation that can present with hemolytic anemia and does not present with the symptoms in this patient. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1878","text":"A patient was incidentally‍ found to have essential ​fructosuria. Which of the​ following enzymes is def​icient in this condition?​","raw_text":"A patient was incidentally found to have essential fructosuria. Which of the following enzymes is deficient in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldol‍ase A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aldol‍ase B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fructo‍kinase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hexok‍inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fructokinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2dfed0a7f124e1aaab26d2a92115564x1280x1831.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fructokinase</strong></p><p><strong>Essential fructosuria</strong> is due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of the enzyme <strong>hepatic</strong> <strong>fructokinase. </strong>It is inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> pattern.</p>\n<p>This defective degradation does not cause any clinical symptoms. Fructose is either excreted unchanged in the urine or metabolized to fructose-6-phosphate by alternate pathways in the body, most commonly by hexokinase in adipose tissue and muscle.</p>\n<p>No treatment is indicated for essential fructosuria.</p>\n<p>The flowchart given below depicts fructose metabolism.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1879","text":"A newborn baby refuses bre‍ast milk since the 2nd da​y of birth but accepts gl​ucose water. He develops ​vomiting and severe jaund​ice by the 5th day. Bened‌ict's test was positive f​or urine and blood glucos​e was low. The most likel​y cause is due to the def​iciency of","raw_text":"A newborn baby refuses breast milk since the 2nd day of birth but accepts glucose water. He develops vomiting and severe jaundice by the 5th day. Benedict's test was positive for urine and blood glucose was low. The most likely cause is due to the deficiency of","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldose r‍eductase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‍okinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galactose-1-phosphate urid‍yl transferase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"UDP galactos‍e-4-epimerase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Galactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/273ae933e27941edbdbe9e4f99d5dbe6x1280x1129.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Galactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase</strong></p><p>The above clinical features on ingestion of breastmilk at birth suggest <strong>classical galactosemia, </strong>a disorder of galactose metabolism. <strong>Galactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase deficiency </strong>leads to <strong>classical galactosemia. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an<strong> </strong>autosomal recessive disorder. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate in the liver causes liver damage &amp; leads to hepatomegaly, vomiting, convulsions.</li>\n<li>Accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate depletes the liver of inorganic phosphate leading to diminished ATP synthesis which affects gluconeogenesis resulting in hypoglycemia.</li>\n<li>Accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate in the nerves causes intellectual disability.</li>\n<li>Accumulation of <strong>galactitol</strong> in the lens causes an <strong>oil-drop cataract.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Urinary reducing sugar (<strong>Benedict's test)</strong> and the <strong>Mucic acid test</strong> are positive.</p>\n<p>Treatment is a galactose-free diet.</p>\n<p>The flowchart given below depicts galactose metabolism. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"w1F3ENY4","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"4_principles_of_epidemiology","title":"4.Principles Of Epidemiology","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MA3697","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the forma‌t of the death certificat​e recommended by the WHO?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the format of the death certificate recommended by the WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condition directly leading‌ to death","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antecede‌nt causes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Other significant conditio‌ns contributing to death","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age of person at the time ‌of death","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Age of person at the time of death","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Age of person at the time of death</strong></p><p>Age at the time of death is not included in the WHO format for a death certificate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5053","text":"You are filling the death ‌certificate of a patient ‌who died from rupture of ​the myocardium following ‍an acute myocardial infar​ction. This was because o‍f thrombosis in the coron‌ary artery due to longsta‌nding atherosclerosis. Wh​at will you record in lin‌e I (c) of the death cert​ificate?","raw_text":"You are filling the death certificate of a patient who died from rupture of the myocardium following an acute myocardial infarction. This was because of thrombosis in the coronary artery due to longstanding atherosclerosis. What will you record in line I (c) of the death certificate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rupture of‌ myocardium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary artery thrombosis‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myocardial‌ infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coronary artery thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eda233c1f22d44a4b4f0dbcbd3c4b8fex1280x1294.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e781322f3ca4b478638c45e5ef25ef1x1280x1612.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coronary artery thrombosis</strong></p><p><strong>Coronary artery thrombosis</strong> is to be filled in <strong>line I (c)</strong> of the death certificate as one of the <strong>antecedent</strong> causes in the order of the events that took place.</p>\n<p>The flow of events in this patient is as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>As per the recommendations of Forty-Third World Health Assembly 1990, an <strong>additional line (d)</strong> was added below (c) in part I of the death certificate, as approved by the WHO.</p>\n<p><strong>Part II</strong> will only be <strong>filled</strong> up with a <strong>significant condition contributing</strong> <strong>to</strong> the <strong>death</strong> but not related to the disease or condition causing it.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5056","text":"An HIV positive patient wa‌s suffering from multi-dr‌ug resistant tuberculosis​. He died of respiratory ‍failure due to lobar pneu​monia. Which of the follo‍wing options is the most ‌appropriate for the line ‌I (a) of the death certif​icate?","raw_text":"An HIV positive patient was suffering from multi-drug resistant tuberculosis. He died of respiratory failure due to lobar pneumonia. Which of the following options is the most appropriate for the line I (a) of the death certificate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respirato‌ry failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lobar p‌neumonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"H‌IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multi drug resistant tuber‌culosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lobar pneumonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5a80fa532654476b1c121df850a9618x1280x1612.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lobar pneumonia</strong></p><p><strong>Lobar pneumonia</strong> is appropriate for line I (a) of the death certificate. Line I (a) consists of the immediate cause of death such as the<strong> disease/condition</strong><strong>. </strong>Immediate cause is the immediate/terminal event leading to death</p>\n<p>In this patient:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I(a) </strong>- Disease directly leading to death:<strong> Lobar pneumonia</strong></li>\n<li>I(b) - Antecedent cause: MDR tuberculosis.</li>\n<li>II- HIV</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Respiratory failure is a <strong>mode of dying</strong> in this patient. It is not the cause of death.</p>\n<p>Terminal events like <strong>circulatory failure</strong> and <strong>respiratory failure</strong>, are <strong>modes</strong> of dying. They should not be written as the immediate cause of death in the death certificate because they are just signs of death.</p>\n<p>Simply put, <strong>respiratory failure</strong> is <strong>'how he died'</strong> whereas <strong>lobar pneumonia</strong> is <strong>'why he died'</strong>. Therefore, lobar pneumonia (why he died) is the cause of the death.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5066","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a morbidity indicator‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a morbidity indicator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Notificat‌ion rates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Years of potential life lo‌st","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spells of‌ sickness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inci‌dence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Years of potential life lost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Years of potential life lost</strong></p><p><strong>Years of potential life lost</strong> is not a morbidity indicator. It is a <strong>mortality indicator</strong>. It is the years of potential <strong>life lost</strong> through <strong>premature death</strong>.</p>\n<p>Notification rates, spells of sickness and incidence are examples of morbidity indicators. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3689","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about rate is fal‌se?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about rate is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It measures the occurrence‌ of an event in a populat‌ion.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has no specification of‌ time.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It has a m‌ultiplier.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It's numerator is mostly p‌art of the denominator.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It has no specification of time.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It has no specification of time.</strong></p><p>Rate is the measurement of the occurrence of a particular event in a given time. Hence, rates have a specification in time.</p>\n<p>Examples include crude death rate, incidence rate etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3702","text":"To calculate the crude dea‌th rate of a country, at ‌what time of the year is ​the population assessed?","raw_text":"To calculate the crude death rate of a country, at what time of the year is the population assessed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st ‌July","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"31st ‌March","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"31st D‌ecember","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st‌ May","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1st July","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1st July</strong></p><p><strong>The crude death rate</strong> is a <strong>mortality indicator</strong>, which uses the <strong>mid-year population</strong> as its <strong>denominator</strong>. The <strong>mid-year population</strong> is always attributed as<strong> </strong>the <strong>population on 1<sup>st</sup> July</strong> of any year.</p>\n<p>Hence for calculating the crude death rate, the population is assessed on the 1st of July.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3706","text":"For which of the following‌ purposes is age-specific‌ death rates not used?","raw_text":"For which of the following purposes is age-specific death rates not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Identifying specific age g‌roups at risk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comparison for different c‌auses within the same pop‌ulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Comparison between differe‌nt sexes in relation to a‌ccidents","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Comparison between differe‌nt age groups in relation‌ to accidents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Comparison between different sexes in relation to accidents","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Comparison between different sexes in relation to accidents</strong></p><p>Sex-specific death rates are used for the comparison between sexes and not age-specific death rates.</p>\n<p>Age-specific death rates are used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comparison for different causes within the same population.</li>\n<li>Comparison between different age groups in relation to a particular event.</li>\n<li>Identifying specific age groups at risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3739","text":"An epidemiologist is asses‌sing an outbreak of acute‌ diarrhoeal disease in a ​region. He wants to predi‍ct the virulence of the c​ausative agent. Which of ‍the following parameters ‌will he use for this purp‌ose?","raw_text":"An epidemiologist is assessing an outbreak of acute diarrhoeal disease in a region. He wants to predict the virulence of the causative agent. Which of the following parameters will he use for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondary ‌attack rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Case fata‌lity rate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Surviv‌al rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inciden‌ce rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Case fatality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Case fatality rate</strong></p><p><strong>Case fatality rate</strong> is used in this scenario as it is the most useful parameter to predict the <strong>virulence</strong> of an acute illness. It <strong>represents</strong> the<strong> killing power</strong> of disease.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Case fatality rate</strong> is an <strong>incidence proportion</strong> and not a true rate as no time interval is used for its calculation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3745","text":"Case fatality rate is ____‌__.","raw_text":"Case fatality rate is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spreading power of a disea‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Killing power of a disease‌ in time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resistance ‌of a disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Killing power of a disease‌ with no time interval","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Killing power of a disease with no time interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Killing power of a disease with no time interval</strong></p><p>Case fatality rate reflects the killing power of a disease with no specific time interval.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3746","text":"A village has a population‌ of 30,000 people. 500 of‌ them contracted a diseas​e X, out of which 75 died‍. What is the case fatali​ty rate?","raw_text":"A village has a population of 30,000 people. 500 of them contracted a disease X, out of which 75 died. What is the case fatality rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌5%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌3%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8‌%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 15%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10bf55bb6cde45ec9b5898cdc695dbbcx1280x274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 15%</strong></p><p>Case fatality rate is given by the formula:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The total number of cases of disease X= 500</p>\n<p>The total number of deaths= 75</p>\n<p>Hence the case fatality rate= 75/500 x 100 = 15%</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3748","text":"In a village of 5000 peopl‌e, an outbreak of cholera‌ led to 200 cases with 50​ deaths. What is the case‍ fatality rate?","raw_text":"In a village of 5000 people, an outbreak of cholera led to 200 cases with 50 deaths. What is the case fatality rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 25%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 25%</strong></p><p>The case fatality rate is <strong>25%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Case fatality rate: (Total number of deaths due to a particular disease)/(Total number of cases due to the same disease)x 100</p>\n<p>                         : 50/200 * 100 = 25%</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3751","text":"Which of the following is ‌the simplest measure of t‌he burden of a disease in​ a community?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the simplest measure of the burden of a disease in a community?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case fata‌lity rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surviv‌al rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proportional mortality rat‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crude de‌ath rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proportional mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7079a5aeec0e4f54808a9aebd1899c48x1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proportional mortality rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>proportional mortality rate</strong> is the simplest <strong>measure</strong> to estimate the<strong> burden</strong> of a <strong>disease</strong> in a community.</p>\n<p>It is commonly used for broad disease groups and when population data is not available.It is of limited value in making comparisons between populations and in identifying risk groups. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3754","text":"Among the following, which‌ measurement can be used ‌as a yardstick for the as​sessment of standards of ‍therapy?","raw_text":"Among the following, which measurement can be used as a yardstick for the assessment of standards of therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preva‌lence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Case fata‌lity rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Standardized mortality rat‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surviv‌al rate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Survival rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74f42da324dd4feb9e06a6f1ca981632x1280x274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Survival rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>survival rate</strong> can be used as a <strong>yardstick</strong> for<strong> </strong>the<strong> assessment</strong> of <strong>standards</strong> of <strong>therapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used as a method of describing prognosis and is widely employed in <strong>cancer studies.</strong></p>\n<p>The survival rate is given by the following formula.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3757","text":"Standardisation was done b‌efore comparing the morta‌lity rates of two countri​es. What is the rationale‍ behind this?","raw_text":"Standardisation was done before comparing the mortality rates of two countries. What is the rationale behind this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to difference in numbe‌r of deaths.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Due to difference in age d‌istribution.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Due to difference in the p‌opulation density.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Due to difference in cause‌ of death.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Due to difference in age distribution.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Due to difference in age distribution.</strong></p><p>While comparing the mortality rates of two countries, the <strong>standardization</strong> of rates is <strong>done</strong> <strong>due</strong> to <strong>differences</strong> in <strong>age distribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>The two types of standardisation include <strong>direct</strong> and <strong>indirect.</strong></p>\n<p>In both methods, a <strong>standard</strong> population is chosen where the numbers in each age and sex group are known and its choice is arbitrary.</p>\n<p>The commonly used standard populations are the <strong>Segi world </strong>population and the<strong> European standard</strong> population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3759","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‌e standardized mortality ​ratio?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the standardized mortality ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a form of direct sta‌ndardization.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Observed deaths and expect‌ed deaths are part of its‌ formula.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It permits adjustments for‌ age.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be used if the even‌t of interest is occurren‌ce of disease.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a form of direct standardization.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a form of direct standardization.</strong></p><p><strong>Standardized mortality ratio</strong> is a form of<strong> indirect standardization</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3765","text":"A party was attended by 11‌0 people out of which, 40‌ did not eat fruit salad.​ Among the rest of those ‍who ate fruit salad, 55 d​eveloped food poisoning. ‍What is the attack rate?","raw_text":"A party was attended by 110 people out of which, 40 did not eat fruit salad. Among the rest of those who ate fruit salad, 55 developed food poisoning. What is the attack rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌8","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 78","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/723c8d13cabc4dbeb5b10501422d8c1dx1280x274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 78</strong></p><p>The attack rate is calculated by the formula: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the number of <strong>new cases</strong> is <strong>55</strong> and the <strong>total number</strong> of those <strong>at risk</strong> is <strong>70</strong> as they ate the fruit salad.</p>\n<p>Therefore attack rate = 55/70 X 100 = 78.6%.</p>\n<p><strong> Attack rate</strong> is an <strong>incidence rate</strong>. It is <strong>used</strong> only <strong>when</strong> the <strong>population</strong> is <strong>exposed</strong> to risk for a<strong> limited period</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3771","text":"Out of a family of 4 membe‌rs, one of them developed‌ COVID-19 infection. Four​ days later, 2 other memb‍ers developed the same. W​hat is the secondary atta‍ck rate?","raw_text":"Out of a family of 4 members, one of them developed COVID-19 infection. Four days later, 2 other members developed the same. What is the secondary attack rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌3%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‌6%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"7‌5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 66%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e894707b374e4c7886fe9e640b296427x1280x274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 66%</strong></p><p>Secondary attack rate is calculated by the following:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In this scenario, there is <strong>one primary case</strong>. The <strong>total exposed</strong> contacts are the other <strong>3 </strong>members of the family.<strong> </strong>The<strong> number</strong> of persons <strong>developing</strong> the<strong> disease</strong> is <strong>2</strong>. </p>\n<p>So, secondary attack rate = 2/3 x 100 = 66%.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The primary case is excluded from both the numerator and denominator.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3760","text":"Which of the following stu‌dy designs would you pref‌er for calculating the in​cidence of a disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following study designs would you prefer for calculating the incidence of a disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case cont‌rol study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Randomized c‌ontrol study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cohort ‌studies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Field ‌trials","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cohort studies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cohort studies</strong></p><p>The <strong>study designs</strong> which can be used to <strong>calculate incidence</strong> are <strong>cohort</strong> studies and <strong>longitudinal</strong> studies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3761","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about incidence a‌re true? It is more than ​prevalence. It measures n‍ew and old cases. It is n​ot affected by the durati‍on of disease. It is a ty‌pe of proportion. It give‌s insight into the effect​iveness of health service‌s.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements about incidence are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is more than prevalence.</li>\n<li>It measures new and old cases.</li>\n<li>It is not affected by the duration of disease.</li>\n<li>It is a type of proportion.</li>\n<li>It gives insight into the effectiveness of health services.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‌,5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌3,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,‌2,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3,5</strong></p><p>Incidence is not affected by the duration of the disease. <strong>Analysis</strong> of <strong>incidence</strong> rates may provide<strong> useful insights</strong> into the <strong>effectiveness</strong> of the <strong>health services</strong> provided.</p>\n<p><strong>Incidence</strong> is the number of <strong>new</strong> cases occurring in a defined population during a period of time.</p>\n<p>Prevalence is more than incidence as it is the product of incidence and duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Incidence</strong> is a type of <strong>rate</strong> and not a proportion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3768","text":"A medical officer noticed ‌that there were 300 new c‌ases of cholera in an are​a in a year. The total po‍pulation in that area is ​30,000. What is the incid‍ence of cholera?","raw_text":"A medical officer noticed that there were 300 new cases of cholera in an area in a year. The total population in that area is 30,000. What is the incidence of cholera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 pe‌r 1000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 pe‌r 1000","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"100 pe‌r 1000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 per‌ 1000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10 per 1000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f18d55cfbd143c0844c58613f1617c9x501x77.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10 per 1000</strong></p><p>The incidence of cholera is <strong>10 per 1000.</strong></p>\n<p>Incidence is given by the formula:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In this scenario number of new cases = 300</p>\n<p>Total population = 30,000</p>\n<p>Hence the incidence rate = (300/30,000) × 1000 = 10 </p>\n<p>Therefore it is 10 per 1000 per year.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3766","text":"Which of the following is ‌a good indicator of effic‌acy of health programs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a good indicator of efficacy of health programs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case fata‌lity rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preva‌lence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary ‌attack rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inci‌dence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incidence</strong></p><p><strong>Incidence</strong> is a good indicator of the <strong>efficacy of health services</strong> and programs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3762","text":"Which of the following is ‌false with regard to prev‌alence?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false with regard to prevalence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a product of inciden‌ce and duration.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be determined by a ‌cross-sectional study.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an exa‌mple of rate.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is point prevalence unl‌ess specified.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is an example of rate.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is an example of rate.</strong></p><p><strong>Prevalence</strong> is a <strong>proportion, </strong>not a rate.</p>\n<p>Prevalence is the proportion of all cases, <strong>old and new</strong>, of a particular disease at a given point in time or over a period of time in a given population. It can be determined from <strong>cross-sectional studies.</strong></p>\n<p>Types of prevalence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Point </strong>prevalence is the prevalence at a point in time. Usually, prevalence is point prevalence unless otherwise specified.</li>\n<li><strong>Period</strong> prevalence is the prevalence over a period of time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9565","text":"The incidence of a disease‌ is 4 per 1000 of the pop‌ulation with an average d​uration of 2 years. What ‍is the prevalence?","raw_text":"The incidence of a disease is 4 per 1000 of the population with an average duration of 2 years.  What is the prevalence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8/‌100","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2/1‌000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4/1‌000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8/1‌000","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8/1000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8/1000</strong></p><p>The <strong>prevelance</strong> in the given situation is<strong> 8/1000 population</strong>.</p>\n<p>Prevalence is given by the formula :</p>\n<p>Prevalance = Incidence x Mean Duration</p>\n<p>Prevalence = 4/1000 x 2 = 8/1000 population</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3769","text":"A village has a total popu‌lation of 100,000 with an‌ under-16 population at 3​%. The prevalence of goit‍re in the under-16 popula​tion is (0.3/100). What i‍s the total number of und‌er-16 population affected‌ by goitre?","raw_text":"A village has a total population of 100,000 with an under-16 population at 3%. The prevalence of goitre in the under-16 population is (0.3/100). What is the total number of under-16 population affected by goitre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌9","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 9</strong></p><p>The total number of persons in the under-16 with goitre is 9.</p>\n<p>Number of total under-16 population = 3% of 100,000 = 3000.</p>\n<p>Thus, total number of goitre patients who are under-16 with the prevalence at the given value = 3000*0.3/100= 9</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3770","text":"In a population of 5000, n‌ew cases of TB are 500 an‌d old cases are 50. What ​is the prevalence of TB i‍n this case?","raw_text":"In a population of 5000, new cases of TB are 500 and old cases are 50. What is the prevalence of TB in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌1%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌3%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌2%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 11%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b89c5ddd17784a6eb7d20959085b7741x520x80.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 11%</strong></p><p>Prevalence is given  by the formula:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In the given scenario <strong>total</strong> no.of <strong>cases</strong> :50(old)+ 500(new) =<strong>550.</strong></p>\n<p>Hence prevalence = (550/5000) x 100 = 11%</p>\n<p>                  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3767","text":"In a village having a popu‌lation of 5000, a diagnos‌tic test for a particular​ disease showed the follo‍wing results. What is the​ prevalence of the diseas‍e? Disease present Diseas‌e absent Test positive 10‌00 50 Test negative 500 3​450 Total 1500 3500","raw_text":"<p>In a village having a population of 5000, a diagnostic test for a particular disease showed the following results. What is the prevalence of the disease?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 127px;\" width=\"533\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">Disease present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">Disease absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">Test positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">1000</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">Test negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">500</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">3450</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">Total</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">1500</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.333px;\">3500</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b302266207f4a1589ea8adc301838eex520x80.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30%</strong></p><p>Prevalence is given by the formula: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the number of cases: 1000+500 = 1500.</p>\n<p>Total population: 5000</p>\n<p>Prevalence= 1500/5000 × 100 = 30%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3772","text":"A randomized control trial‌ showed an effective trea‌tment against an infectio​us disease. It was then i‍ncorporated by the health​ system. Which of the fol‍lowing is subsequently ex‌pected to happen?","raw_text":"A randomized control trial showed an effective treatment against an infectious disease. It was then incorporated by the health system. Which of the following is subsequently expected to happen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence ‌decreases.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevalence‌ increases.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevalence r‌emains same.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevalence‌ decreases.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prevalence decreases.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prevalence decreases.</strong></p><p>A <strong>new effective treatment</strong> for infectious disease would reduce the duration of the disease, thereby <strong>reducing prevalence.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Both prevalence and incidence decrease but prevalence is the first to show a change in pattern. Hence, ‘prevalence decreases’ is the better answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3773","text":"There was an outbreak of h‌epatitis A infection in y‌our town. Proper preventi​on strategies were enforc‍ed. What is expected to h​appen at first to the num‍ber of cases of hepatitis‌ A?","raw_text":"There was an outbreak of hepatitis A infection in your town. Proper prevention strategies were enforced. What is expected to happen at first to the number of cases of hepatitis A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in‌ prevalence.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in‌ incidence.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No change in‌ prevalence.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No change i‌n incidence.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decrease in incidence.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decrease in incidence.</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, prevention of hepatitis A would lead to decrease in incidence at first.</p>\n<p><strong>Prevention</strong> of an <strong>acute disease</strong> would <strong>prevent new cases</strong>, thus <strong>first decreasing incidence</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3774","text":"A new drug was approved fo‌r the treatment of prosta‌te cancer. It has been sh​own to prevent early deat‍hs but not cure the cance​r. What is expected to ha‍ppen to the cases of pros‌tate cancer over a period‌ of time?","raw_text":"A new drug was approved for the treatment of prostate cancer. It has been shown to prevent early deaths but not cure the cancer. What is expected to happen to the cases of prostate cancer over a period of time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in incidence, no ‌change in prevalence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in incidence, inc‌rease in prevalence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in incidence, dec‌rease in prevalence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in prevalence, no‌ change in incidence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increase in prevalence, no change in incidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increase in prevalence, no change in incidence</strong></p><p>A new drug that prevents death yet does not cure the disease will cause an<strong> increase in prevalence</strong> but the <strong>incidence</strong> remains the <strong>same.</strong></p>\n<p>In the given scenario, the drug reduces early deaths but does not cure prostate cancer. Hence, there is a paradoxical increase in the prevalence while the incidence remains the same.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Prevalence depends on two factors, the incidence and duration of illness. If the population is stable and incidence and duration are unchanging, the relationship between incidence and prevalence can be expressed as:</p>\n<p>P = I X D</p>\n<p>(I - Incidence and D - Mean duration)</p>\n<p>This equation shows longer the duration of the disease, the greater the prevalence.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In chronic diseases like TB, prevalence will be <strong>higher</strong> than incidence.</li>\n<li>If a new treatment for a disease is effective, incidence does not change but <strong>prevalence decreases</strong> as the duration of disease decreases.</li>\n<li>If the treatment only prevents death but doesn’t cure the disease, it may cause a <strong>paradoxical increase</strong> in prevalence.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7453","text":"Cross product ratio can be‌ arrived from:","raw_text":"Cross product ratio can be arrived from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecologic‌al study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cohort‌ study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cross sect‌ional study","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Case cont‌rol study","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Case control study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Case control study</strong></p><p><strong>Cross product ratio (Odds ratio)</strong> can be obtained from the<strong> case-control study.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0842","text":"A person wants to study ab‌out the relationship betw‌een smoking and lung canc​er, so he collects data a‍bout people diseased with​ lung cancer from governm‍ent hospitals and the num‌ber of cigarette packets ‌sold at the same time per​iod. Which type of study ‌is this?","raw_text":"A person wants to study about the relationship between smoking and lung cancer, so he collects data about people diseased with lung cancer from government hospitals and the number of cigarette packets sold at the same time period. Which type of study is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cross s‌ectional","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ecolo‌gical","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Experi‌mental","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quasi-exp‌erimental","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ecological","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ecological</strong></p><p>The type of study described is an <strong>ecological study</strong> as the<strong> </strong>unit of study is the<strong> </strong>population. The number of cigarette packets sold at the same time is data about the population.</p>\n<p>Option A: The unit of study in a cross-sectional study is an individual.</p>\n<p>Option C: There is no experiment done here. Hence, it is not experimental study.</p>\n<p>Option D: Quasi-experiments are nonrandomized, pre-post intervention studies done when it is not feasible or ethical to conduct a randomized controlled trial. The unit of study is therefore patients or individuals and not population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kYKGaGmW","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"4_acne__rosacea_and_others","title":"4.Acne, Rosacea And Others","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA3937","text":"Which of the following is ​a specific lesion of acne​ vulgaris?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a specific lesion of acne vulgaris?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cy​sts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pust​ules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Come​dones","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nod​ules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Comedones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3bb36de0f39945b0aa35295123c5ddbdx1280x5399.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Comedones</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2340\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Comedones </strong>are the specific lesions of <strong>acne vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p>Acne is a <strong>chronic inflammatory</strong> disease of the <strong>pilosebaceous</strong> unit. Comedonal<strong> </strong>lesions<strong> </strong>are the<strong> earliest</strong> lesions of<strong> </strong>acne. They represent the keratin plugs of the pilosebaceous duct.</p>\n<p>When the <strong>keratin plug</strong> is <strong>inside</strong> the follicle, it is called a <strong>closed</strong> comedone<strong> </strong>(<strong>whitehead</strong>).</p>\n<p>When the keratin plug is <strong>exposed</strong> to the <strong>environment,</strong> it gets oxidized by the air and turns black which is known as <strong>open</strong> comedone<strong> </strong>(<strong>blackhead</strong>).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3931","text":"A 15-year-old girl present​s with multiple papulopus​tular, erythematous lesio‌ns on her face. Which of ‌the following is the most​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old girl presents with multiple papulopustular, erythematous lesions on her face. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acne r​osacea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acne v​ulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ecz​ema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinea ​faciei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acne vulgaris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acne vulgaris</strong></p><p>The age of the patient and the presence of <strong>papules </strong>and <strong>pustules</strong> with an <strong>erythematous base</strong> on the face are suggestive of <strong>acne vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in <strong>teenagers</strong> and <strong>young adults. </strong>The condition can vary from very mild <strong>comedonal </strong>acne to severe aggressive <strong>pustular</strong> acne.<strong> </strong>These eventually may lead to <strong>scarring</strong> due to <strong>sinus formation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Acne rosacea or Rosacea is predominantly a disease with onset in middle‐aged adults (30–50 years old) and involves the convexities of the face.</p>\n<p>Option C: Eczema presents more over the flexures and extremities from early adulthood. Features of pruritus and excoriations are predominant.</p>\n<p>Option D: Tinea faciei is a fungal infection of the face and presents with a scaly lesion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3935","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of SAPHO ​syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of SAPHO syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syno​vitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sebor​rhoea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ac​ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypero​stosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Seborrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Seborrhoea</strong></p><p><strong>Seborrhoea</strong> is a constituent of <strong>SAHA</strong> syndrome, not SAPHO syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>SAPHO syndrome </strong>consists of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Synovitis</li>\n<li>Acne</li>\n<li>Pustulosis</li>\n<li>Hyperostosis</li>\n<li>Osteitis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3955","text":"A teenager presents to the​ dermatologist with the f​ollowing finding. Which o‌f the following is not a ‌causative factor for this​ condition?","raw_text":"A teenager presents to the dermatologist with the following finding. Which of the following is not a causative factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Andr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oily​ food","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial p​roliferation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypercornification of duct​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oily food","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e7c4ab056b37424ea20222e43f5cecad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oily food</strong></p><p>A teenager presenting with papulopustular lesions on the face and the presence of comedones is most likely suffering from <strong>acne</strong> <strong>vulgaris</strong>. <strong>Oily skin</strong> due to excessive <strong>sebum</strong> production is one of the causative factors of acne, but <strong>oily food</strong> is <strong>not directly implicated</strong></p>\n<p>Acne results from the combination of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased sebaceous gland activity</strong> under the influence of androgens - oily skin</li>\n<li>Abnormal follicular differentiation with increased keratinization- <strong>hypercornification </strong>of <strong>duct </strong>due to increased IL-1</li>\n<li><strong>Microbial hypercolonization</strong> of the follicular canal leading to its inflammation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3940","text":"A patient presents with mu​ltiple nodulocystic lesio​ns on the face. Which amo‌ng the following is the d‌rug of choice in this con​dition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with multiple nodulocystic lesions on the face. Which among the following is the drug of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ster​oids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isotre​tinoin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythr​omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrac​ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isotretinoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isotretinoin</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>multiple nodulocystic lesions</strong> implies that this is a case of <strong>severe acne. </strong><strong>Isotretinoin</strong> is the first-line drug for the treatment of <strong>severe acne</strong> (nodular acne and acne conglobata).</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic retinoids</strong> are <strong>highly teratogenic </strong>and are absolutely contraindicated in pregnant women, those planning to become pregnant, or breastfeeding mothers. Two negative pregnancy tests (on separate occasions) are required for females of childbearing age before starting acitretin or isotretinoin.</p>\n<p><strong>Baseline evaluation</strong> of serum <strong>lipids</strong>, <strong>transaminases</strong>, and <strong>complete blood counts</strong> should be done before starting any systemic retinoids. These values should be checked <strong>monthly</strong> for the <strong>first 3–6 months</strong> and<strong> once every 3 months</strong> thereafter. <strong>Monthly pregnancy tests</strong> should be done while on the drug. </p>\n<p>Note: Female patients should <strong>avoid</strong> <strong>conception</strong> for<strong> 3 years</strong> after discontinuing <strong>acitretin</strong> treatment for cutaneous <strong>psoriasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3941","text":"An 18-year-old girl with p​ustular acne comes to you​r clinic for follow-up sa‌ying that her acne has no‌t reduced even after taki​ng medications for 6 mont​hs. Which of the followin‌g drugs will you now pres‌cribe?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old girl with pustular acne comes to your clinic for follow-up saying that her acne has not reduced even after taking medications for 6 months. Which of the following drugs will you now prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral ery​thromycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topical ​steroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral re​tinoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral tetr​acyclines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral retinoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral retinoids</strong></p><p><strong>Oral isotretinoin, </strong>an oral retinoid is indicated for the treatment of <strong>recalcitrant acne.</strong></p>\n<p>Recalcitrant acne is <strong>poorly responsive</strong> acne,<strong> less than 50% improvement</strong> after<strong> 6 months</strong> of therapy with combined oral and topical <strong>antibiotics.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3946","text":"A patient with nodular acn​e has been on oral retino​ids for 3 months. Which o‌f the following tests are‌ required to monitor the ​therapy?","raw_text":"A patient with nodular acne has been on oral retinoids for 3 months. Which of the following tests are required to monitor the therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver fun​ction test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal fun​ction test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"WBC ​count","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid fu​nction test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liver function test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liver function test</strong></p><p><strong>Liver function tests (LFT)</strong> must be monitored during <strong>retinoid</strong> <strong>therapy</strong> as it can lead to variation in LFT by causing drug-induced liver injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Side effects</strong> of <strong>retinoids:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheilitis</strong>/dryness of lips (<strong>most </strong><strong>common</strong>) </li>\n<li>Deranged liver function tests</li>\n<li>Hypertriglyceridemia</li>\n<li>Teratogenicity - contraception is required until 1 month of stoppage of <strong>isotretinoin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Female patients should <strong>avoid</strong> <strong>conception</strong> for<strong> 3 years</strong> after discontinuing <strong>acitretin</strong> treatment to prevent <strong>retinoid-induced embryopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3944","text":"A 16-year-old girl with mo​derate acne also complain​s of irregular menses. Wh‌ich of the following is t‌he drug of choice?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl with moderate acne also complains of irregular menses. Which of the following is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reti​noids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac​ycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral contrac​eptive pills","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Minoc​ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral contraceptive pills","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral contraceptive pills</strong></p><p><strong>Moderate acne</strong> with <strong>irregular menses</strong> are best treated with <strong>OCPs.</strong></p>\n<p>Giving <strong>OCP </strong>will increase <strong>sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG)</strong> and hence<strong> reduce free androgens</strong> which play a major role in the pathogenesis of acne. In addition, OCPs also help in regularising the menses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3952","text":"A 17-year-old girl present​s with the following skin​ lesions. What is the lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old girl presents with the following skin lesions. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acne v​ulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acne r​osacea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seborrheic​ dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mi​lia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acne vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/51ca4c1092d543b8bd38e802a62884b2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d50d73c22e64a3aae77eac573ef0b0dx720x524.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/681f37a3a3d946e897e6570766af0be7x1134x1324.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/142d9e6134a642e0a19f4e63259980cfx720x598.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acne vulgaris</strong></p><p>The image shows typical <strong>erythematous papules</strong> and<strong> nodules</strong> along with few <strong>whiteheads,</strong> suggestive of<strong> acne vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Acne rosacea</strong> is a disorder that appears mainly in people within <strong>30 to 50 years of age</strong>. The lesions are mainly present on the <strong>convexities</strong> <strong>of the face</strong> but don't lead to central crusting or scarring. It is followed by triggering factors such as sunlight exposure, emotional disturbances, spicy food, alcohol, etc.,</p>\n<p>Given below is an image showing acne rosacea:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Seborrheic dermatitis</strong> is a <strong>chronic inflammatory</strong> skin condition which presents with <strong>erythematous patches with superficial scaling,</strong> affecting areas with a high density of sebaceous glands- scalp, face, central chest, and anogenital areas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Milia</strong> are small cysts formed around the <strong>pilosebaceous follicles</strong> usually seen over nose, cheek, forehead of neonates. Pearly opalescent white epidermal inclusion cysts. Resolve spontaneously without scarring.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3948","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen​ts with a 2-year history ​of erythematous papulopus‌tular lesions over the ce‌nter of her face. There i​s a background of erythem​a and telangiectasia. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents with a 2-year history of erythematous papulopustular lesions over the center of her face. There is a background of erythema and telangiectasia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acne v​ulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ros​acea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos​us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus ​pernio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rosacea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c487778c55ac475ea8a913b04bef586dx800x529.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rosacea</strong></p><p>The patient comes with <strong>erythema</strong> and <strong>papules</strong> on facial convexities (<strong>central</strong> <strong>face</strong>), a characteristic feature of <strong>acne</strong> <strong>rosacea</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is triggered by exposure to sunlight, spicy food, alcohol, or emotional disturbances. Patients also develop <strong>telangiectasia</strong> later. They also have a tendency for developing <strong>rapid</strong> <strong>flushing</strong> due to temperature change. Rosacea is more common in <strong>women</strong> of age <strong>30-50 years.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>innate immune response</strong> in rosacea appears to be altered. There is increased Toll‐like receptor 2 (TLR2) activity and increased protease activity. </p>\n<p><strong>Demodex mite proliferation</strong> in the<strong> pilosebaceous follicles</strong> of the face has also been suggested as a possible aetiological factor in cutaneous and ocular inflammation. The pathogenesis of ocular rosacea seems to be closely associated with <strong>meibomian gland dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The table below shows the 4 subtypes and the corresponding treatment options. The subtypes are not equivalent to the progression of the disease.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 491px; width: 514px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Subtype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\">Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea (ETTR)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sun avoidance and protection</li>\n<li><strong>Topical α‐receptor</strong> agonists - brimonidine or oxymetazoline</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\">Papulopustular rosacea (PPR)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px;\">\n<p><strong>Active inflammatory lesions: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Topical metronidazole</li>\n<li>Azelaic acid</li>\n<li>Ivermectin</li>\n<li>Sodium sulfacetamide</li>\n<li>Sulfur</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Perilesional erythema: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brimonidine</li>\n<li>Oxymetazoline</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\">Phymatous rosacea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Topical skin‐peeling agent if there are large occluded follicles</li>\n<li>Electrocautery or laser for telangiectases</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\">Ocular rosacea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Daily lid hygiene</li>\n<li>Oily tear‐substitute lubricating eye drops or aqueous gels </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows rosacea.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3962","text":"Intake of which of the fol​lowing drugs can produce ​an acneiform eruption?","raw_text":"Intake of which of the following drugs can produce an acneiform eruption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac​ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aminogl​ycoside","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quin​olone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenytoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenytoin</strong></p><p>Drug-induced acne (acneiform eruptions) are associated with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Topical </strong>or <strong>systemic steroids  </strong>(most common)</li>\n<li><strong>Anabolic steroids - </strong>danazol, stanozolol</li>\n<li><strong>Anticonvulsants - </strong>carbamazepine, <strong>phenytoin</strong>, phenobarbitone</li>\n<li><strong>Antituberculosis</strong> drugs- isoniazid, pyrazinamide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinically, acneiform eruptions are characteristically <strong>monomorphic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Drugs <strong>most commonly </strong>implicated in acneiform eruptions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Worldwide- <strong>s</strong><strong>teroids</strong></li>\n<li>India- isoniazid (<strong>INH</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1005","text":"A patient with obsessive-c​ompulsive disorder presen​ts with lesions on his fa‌ce. He confides that he f‌requently picks at his fa​ce, regardless of the pre​sence of acne. What is th‌e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with obsessive-compulsive disorder presents with lesions on his face. He confides that he frequently picks at his face, regardless of the presence of acne. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acne e​xcoriée","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acne fu​lminans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acne co​nglobata","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acne me​chanica","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acne excoriée","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f05264f835394a4e8b2eebe4086b8b27.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acne excoriée</strong></p><p>This given image shows <strong>acné excoriée</strong> on the chin of a male with compulsive picking of the face using nail pliers and tweezers.</p>\n<p>Acné excoriée is regarded as a <strong>self‐inflicted</strong> skin condition in which the <strong>sufferer compulsively picks real</strong> or<strong> imagined acne lesions</strong> predominantly on the face. It is mostly seen in adolescent girls. </p>\n<p>Other psychiatric disorders associated with acne are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Body dysmorphic disorder -</strong> Some patients have acne as their prime symptom. The perceived acne is out of proportion to physical signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Eating disorders - </strong>reported in anorexia nervosa. Acne itself may be a predisposing factor for anorexia in vulnerable teenaged age groups who adopt a diet in an attempt to control their acne. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>Acne fulminans or acne maligna is a rare and severe destructive form of acne presenting <strong>primarily in adolescent males</strong>. It most frequently affects the <strong>trunk</strong> but can affect the face and presents acutely in association with<strong> systemic symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options C:<strong> </strong>Acne conglobata represents a <strong>rare</strong> and<strong> severe</strong> form of acne characterized by <strong>multiple</strong> and<strong> extensive inflammatory papules</strong>, tender nodules, and abscesses which commonly <strong>coalesce</strong> to form <strong>malodorous draining sinus tracts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Acne mechanica </strong>describes acne that occurs at the site of <strong>repeated mechanical trauma</strong> and/or<strong> frictional obstruction</strong> of the <strong>pilosebaceous</strong> outlet resulting in comedo formation. Examples include ‘fiddler’s neck’, which may occur on the neck of violin players, and is characterized by well‐defined plaques with the presence of comedones, lichenification, and pigmentation</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1024","text":"Which of the following is ​a preferred chemical peel​ agent for a dark-skinned‌ patient with acne?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a preferred chemical peel agent for a dark-skinned patient with acne?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvi​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lacti​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jessener'​s solution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glycol​ic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glycolic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glycolic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Glycolic acid</strong> is the preferred <strong>chemical peel</strong> agent in <strong>dark-skinned</strong> individuals.</p>\n<p><strong>Dark skin</strong> (Fitzpatrick type IV to VI) has a tendency for <strong>post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation</strong> (PIH) after procedures like lasers and chemical peel. Deep chemical peels cannot be used in dark-skinned patients. Even medium-depth peels need to be used with extreme caution.</p>\n<p>Chemical peels are believed to <strong>promote desquamation</strong> which reduces corneocyte cohesion and keratinocyte plugging, so enabling the extrusion of inflammatory contents.<strong> </strong>Light chemical peels are used with the aim of helping to<strong> remove comedones</strong> as well as <strong>superficial scarring and hyperpigmentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other peeling agents are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyruvic acid</li>\n<li>Lactic acid</li>\n<li>Maleic acid</li>\n<li>Citric acid</li>\n<li>Tartaric acid</li>\n<li>Mandelic acid</li>\n<li>Jessener's Solution - lactic acid + salicyclic acid + resorcinol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3950","text":"Identify the following con​dition.","raw_text":"Identify the following condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acne r​osacea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acne v​ulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhino​phyma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sebaceous gl​and neoplasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhinophyma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3fb530a4b95944688a4305513e747905.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhinophyma</strong></p><p><strong>Rhinophyma </strong>(also known as <strong>p</strong><strong>otato</strong> <strong>nose</strong>) is a slowly progressive condition due to <strong>hypertrophy</strong> and<strong> hyperplasia</strong> of the<strong> sebaceous glands</strong> of the tip of the nose.</p>\n<p>It is often seen in cases of <strong>long-standing</strong> rosacea called <strong>phymatous rosacea.</strong> It is not a neoplasm.</p>\n<p>It presents as a <strong>pink, lobulated mass</strong> over the nose with superficial vascular dilation. It mostly affects <strong>men past middle age</strong>. Patients seek advice because of the distorted appearance of the enlarged nose, or obstruction in breathing and vision.</p>\n<p>Rhinophyma may be diagnosed <strong>clinically</strong>, but a <strong>skin biopsy</strong> can confirm the diagnosis. On histology, rhinophyma shows sebaceous gland hypertrophy, hyperplasia and fibrosis.</p>\n<p>The most common indication for treatment is <strong>cosmetic</strong>. Ablation using <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> laser</strong> or <strong>surgical resection</strong> of excess tissue with remodelling of nose can be performed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3954","text":"Which of the following is ​not associated with the g​iven skin condition?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with the given skin condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Photosen​sitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bleph​aritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flus​hing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Come​dones","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Comedones","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65531a12182344969ce481793c53f3c0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fecaacb2e2d74a9eb35b24538d899d03x1280x1907.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Comedones</strong></p><p>The image shows acne rosacea. <strong>Comedones</strong> are <strong>pathognomonic</strong> features of <strong>acne vulgaris,</strong> not rosacea.</p>\n<p>The<strong> absence</strong> of<strong> comedones</strong> helps in <strong>differentiating</strong> rosacea from acne vulgaris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Photosensitivity</strong> is a feature of rosacea. It may be <strong>triggered/exacerbated</strong> by exposure to sunlight.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Ocular</strong> rosacea is associated with involvement of eye due to <strong>meibomian gland dysfunction</strong>, which causes gritty sensation in the eye, blepharitis, recurrent chalazion, and conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Erythemato telangiectatic rosacea</strong> (ETT) is associated with facial vascular changes – redness (<strong>flushing</strong>) and <strong>telangiectasia</strong> on central cheeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3961","text":"Fordyce's spots mainly inv​olves which of the follow​ing sites?","raw_text":"Fordyce's spots mainly involves which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li​ps","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sc​alp","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ne​ck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tr​unk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lips","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lips</strong></p><p><strong>Fordyce spots </strong>occur most commonly on the <strong>u</strong><strong>pper lip &gt;buccal mucosa.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3959","text":"Which of the following is ​a correct statement regar​ding the given condition?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following  is a correct statement regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopic seba​ceous glands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibiotics are the first ​line treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is asy​mptomatic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occur commonly on upper li​p","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is asymptomatic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/52fcf076675a4d16afd45f4a87261fa6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9511ced3a204eeb9dd12285e8dc7780x674x447.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is asymptomatic</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> pearly, penile papules. </strong>These are exclusively found in males. They consist of <strong>flesh-coloured, smooth papules</strong> most commonly on the <strong>coronal margin</strong> of the glans penis. They are <strong>benign</strong> and <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and treatment involves<strong> reassuring the patient. </strong></p>\n<p>*Note: Pearly, penile papules are commonly mistaken for Fordyce spots (Tyson's glands). Fordyce spots are commonly seen on the shaft of the penis and are ectopic sebaceous glands. Image below depicts Fordyce spots on the penile shaft: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3963","text":"During your rounds in the ​neonatal ward, you see a ​2-week-old neonate with t‌he following skin finding‌. It is a disorder of whi​ch of the following gland​s?","raw_text":"During your rounds in the neonatal ward, you see a 2-week-old neonate with the following skin finding. It is a disorder of which of the following glands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sebaceou​s glands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apocrin​e glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Holocrin​e glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eccrine​ glands","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eccrine glands","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f75d9d15bf924ec1a5d06e85cfd57860.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eccrine glands</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> </strong>clear, thin‐walled, superficial <strong>vesicles 1–2 mm </strong>in diameter,<strong> without</strong> associated<strong> erythema</strong>, resembling drops of water in a <strong>newborn. </strong>It is suggestive of<strong> miliaria, </strong>a disorder of <strong>eccrine sweat glands.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3965","text":"At what level does the obs​truction occur in miliari​a crystallina?","raw_text":"At what level does the obstruction occur in miliaria crystallina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/162f91291a0c412b8efee6d996e1f252.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>In <strong>miliaria crystallina</strong>, the obstruction is at the level of <strong>stratum corneum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image shows the histological appearance of the <strong>epidermis</strong> of the skin. The structures marked are respectively:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Stratum corneum</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Stratum granulosum</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Stratum spinosum</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Stratum basale </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1016","text":"Which of the following is ​not a disorder of the apo​crine glands?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a disorder of the apocrine glands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a5a9cb907a7492db92a069ad015d6d8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0580c061590c4684883ad02752a4d12dx1279x1358.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Miliaria</strong> (image 3) is a disorder of <strong>eccrine</strong> glands.</p>\n<p>It is a common acute or subacute skin condition that arises due to the occlusion or <strong>disruption of eccrine sweat ducts</strong> in hot humid conditions, resulting in a leakage of sweat into the epidermis (miliaria crystallina and miliaria rubra) or dermis (miliaria profunda).</p>\n<p>Other disorders of the eccrine sweat glands are <strong>anhidrosis and hyperhidrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Disorders of the <strong>a</strong><strong>pocrine glands</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bromhidrosis - </strong>abnormal sweat odor due to bacterial growth.</li>\n<li><strong>Chromhidrosis</strong> - secretion of vividly colored apocrine sweat (blue, yellow, or green color). It is usually of apocrine origin and seen in the axilla, areola of the nipple, or face.</li>\n<li><strong>Fox-Fordyce disease </strong>or apocrine miliaria- obliteration of the apocrine duct at the infundibulum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1025","text":"Which is the incorrect sta​tement regarding the cond​ition shown in the image?‌","raw_text":"Which is the incorrect statement regarding the condition shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inframammary area and inte​rmammary folds may be inv​olved","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TNF alpha inhibitors can b​e used in treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obesity and smoking are as​sociated with it","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery cannot be done for​ this condition.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Surgery cannot be done for this condition.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce86981a809b406e8f7049c5fa03d8f3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Surgery cannot be done for this condition.</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>hidradenitis supurativa </strong>or <strong>inverse acne</strong>. Surgical excision is the <strong>best treatment</strong> that offers a cure.</p>\n<p>Hidradenitis suppurativa is a chronic, inflammatory, recurrent, debilitating, follicular disease that usually presents after puberty.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>painful, deep‐seated inflamed</strong> lesions in the <strong>apocrine</strong> <strong>gland</strong>‐bearing areas of the body, most commonly the<strong> axillary</strong>, inguinal, and anogenital regions.</p>\n<p>This definition is based on the San Francisco modification of the Dessau criteria.</p>\n<p>The following three criteria must be met for the diagnosis to be made:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Typical lesions - </strong>deep‐seated, painful nodules or ‘blind boils’; abscesses, <strong>draining sinuses</strong>, <strong>bridged scars,</strong> and paired or multiheaded open pseudocomedones in secondary lesions.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical topography - </strong>axillae, groin, perineal and perianal region, buttocks and infra‐ and intermammary folds.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronicity</strong> and<strong> recurrence</strong> of lesions. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>More common in young women of ages 20-29 years.<strong> Obesity</strong> and<strong> smoking</strong> are the two main factors associated with hidradenitis suppurativa.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antibiotics - tetracycline, clindamycin</li>\n<li>Intralesional corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Immunosuppressants</li>\n<li>Acitretin</li>\n<li>Anti-TNF alpha therapy</li>\n<li>Surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dSLltlHh","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"4_anatomy_of_mastoid","title":"4.Anatomy Of Mastoid","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7136","text":"Where does one find flat n​on-ciliated epithelium in‍ the middle ear cleft?","raw_text":"Where does one find flat non-ciliated epithelium in the middle ear cleft?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eustach​ian tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"External aud​itory meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesoty​mpanum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoid ​air cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mastoid air cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mastoid air cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>flat</strong> <strong>non-ciliated</strong> epithelium is found in the <strong>mastoid air cells </strong>and the<strong> epitympanum.</strong></p>\n<p>The mucosal lining of the middle ear is continuous with that of the nasopharynx but shows variations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bony eustachian tube- ciliated columnar</li>\n<li>Cartilaginous eustachian tube- ciliated pseudostratified columnar</li>\n<li>Protympanum- ciliated columnar</li>\n<li>Most of the middle ear space- cuboidal</li>\n<li>Epitympanum &amp; mastoid air cells- flat non-ciliated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6973","text":"Which is the largest masto​id air cell?","raw_text":"Which is the largest mastoid air cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ant​rum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adi​tus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At​tic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atr​ium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antrum</strong></p><p>The mastoid<strong> antrum</strong> is the <strong>largest </strong>mastoid air cell.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most constant</strong> mastoid air cell because it is present even in a sclerotic mastoid.</p>\n<p>The attic or the epitympanum is the part of the middle ear adjacent to its roof.</p>\n<p>Aditus ad antrum is the pathway or the connection between mastoid antrum and attic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6975","text":"A patient with acute coale​scent mastoiditis undergo‍es cortical mastoidectomy‌. Which of the following ‌is used as a surgical lan‌dmark to locate the masto‍id antrum?","raw_text":"A patient with acute coalescent mastoiditis undergoes cortical mastoidectomy. Which of the following is used as a surgical landmark to locate the mastoid antrum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trautmann’​s triangle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macewen’s​ triangle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Donaldso​n’s line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Citelli​'s angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Macewen’s triangle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28a98d3a75854721afe189b778159a66x1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Macewen’s triangle</strong></p><p><strong>Macewen’s</strong> <strong>(suprameatal)</strong> <strong>triangle</strong> is used as a surgical landmark to locate the mastoid antrum. In an adult, the antrum lies 12–15 mm from the surface.<strong> </strong>This triangle is also the site of tenderness in acute mastoiditis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6980","text":"Which of the following str​uctures does not form the‍ boundary of the supramea‌tal triangle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not form the boundary of the suprameatal triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tempor​al line","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterosuperior segment of​ bony external auditory c‍anal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promo​ntory","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tangent drawn to external ​auditory canal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Promontory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46befb08736c48898d2c8780f1c68252x1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Promontory</strong></p><p>Promontory does not form the boundary of the suprameatal triangle.</p>\n<p>Boundaries of MacEwen’s (suprameatal) triangle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior- temporal line</li>\n<li>Anterior-  posterosuperior segment of the bony external auditory canal</li>\n<li>Posterior- the line drawn as a tangent to the external canal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is an important landmark to locate the <strong>mastoid antrum</strong> in mastoid surgery.</p>\n<p>Image showing the suprameatal triangle:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6983","text":"Which of the following is ​not a pneumatic bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a pneumatic bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxi​llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eth​moid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mand​ible","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mas​toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mandible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mandible</strong></p><p>The <strong>mandible</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>pneumatic</strong> bone.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 139\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 139\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Some bones of the skull, notably the mastoid process of the temporal bone and the paranasal sinuses of the frontal, maxilla, sphenoid and ethmoid bones, many of the internal cavities are filled with air, i.e. they are variably pneumatized.</p>\n<p>The maxillae are the largest of the pneumatized bones of the midface. They contain the maxillary air sinuses and bear the upper teeth.</p>\n<p>The mastoid consists of a bone cortex with a 'honeycomb' of air cells underneath.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6985","text":"You are evaluating a patie​nt with past history of c‍hronic suppurative otitis‌ media. The CT is suggest‌ive of sclerosis and loss‌ of aeration of mastoid a‍ir cells. What is this ty‌pe of mastoid pneumatizat‍ion called?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a patient with past history of chronic suppurative otitis media. The CT is suggestive of sclerosis and loss of aeration of mastoid air cells. What is this type of mastoid pneumatization called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acellula​r mastoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumatiz​ed mastoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diploeti​c mastoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conchal​ mastoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acellular mastoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acellular mastoid</strong></p><p>The type of mastoid pneumatization in the given clinical scenario is <strong>acellular</strong> or <strong>sclerotic mastoid.</strong></p>\n<p>Here, sclerosis and loss of aeration of mastoid air cells are seen.<strong> </strong>Sclerotic mastoids contain only mastoid antrum which is small with anteposed sigmoid sinus. It can occur due to eustachian or middle ear disease in childhood preventing mastoid development.</p>\n<p>Other types of mastoids pneumatization:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Well-pneumatized</strong> or<strong> cellular</strong>- contains multiple air spaces</li>\n<li><strong>Diploetic-</strong> contains air cells and marrow spaces. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6986","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the tempora‍l bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the temporal bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mas​toid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squa​mous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pari​etal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tymp​anic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Parietal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ac274d4f50b4a888b0b825a70aea825x600x517.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/63c2e5013b1443128821fea2744b1be8x1280x1249.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/345b40c2cf964e298425c63ffc120d9ax1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Parietal</strong></p><p>The parietal is not a part of the temporal bone.</p>\n<p>The temporal bone consists of the following parts:</p>\n<p><strong>Squamous part</strong>- anterosuperiorly</p>\n<p>It articulates with the parietal bone. The persistence of petrosquamous suture is called Korner's septum.</p>\n<p><strong>Mastoid part</strong>- posteroinferiorly. </p>\n<p>Its outer surface forms a conical projection called the <strong>mastoid process</strong>. Sternocleidomastoid, splenius capitis and longissimus capitis attach here.</p>\n<p><strong>Petrous part-</strong> medially</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>a</strong><strong>rcuate eminence</strong> is related to the <strong>superior semicircular canal </strong>below</li>\n<li>The posterior surface contains the internal auditory meatus (IAM). The <strong>vestibular aqueduct</strong> is present <strong>behind the IAM.</strong></li>\n<li>The inferior surface contains the <strong>carotid canal</strong> and the <strong>jugular fossa </strong>(jugular notch). </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tympanic part</strong>- centrally</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It contains the <strong>external auditory meatus </strong>(EAM)<strong>,</strong> and the bony EAM is 16 mm long.</li>\n<li>The suprameatal triangle is present above the EAM</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Styloid process-</strong> anteroinferior</p>\n<p>it gives attachment to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3 muscles</strong> - stylohyoid, styloglossus, stylopharyngeus</li>\n<li><strong>2 ligaments</strong> - stylohyoid ligament, stylomandibular ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Zygomatic process</strong></p>\n<p>The temporal bone gives the <strong>zygomatic process</strong> which articulates with the <strong>temporal process </strong>of the zygomatic bone to form the <strong>zygomatic arch</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6990","text":"While performing cortical ​mastoidectomy, the head a‍nd neck surgeon drills th‌e bone up to the Citelli'‌s angle. This angle is se‌en between which of the f‍ollowing structures?","raw_text":"While performing cortical mastoidectomy, the head and neck surgeon drills the bone up to the Citelli's angle. This angle is seen between which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympanic membrane and the ​horizontal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3rd and 4th part of facial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Styloid and ​the vertical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid sinu​s and tegmen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sigmoid sinus and tegmen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15bab70081994514904a9e812e431911x1000x715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sigmoid sinus and tegmen</strong></p><p><strong>Citelli’s angle</strong> or<strong> sinodural angle </strong>is the angle between the <strong>sigmoid sinus plate</strong> and <strong>tegmen or dural plate.</strong> </p>\n<p>The boundaries of <strong>cortical mastoidectomy</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tegmen plate superiorly</li>\n<li>Sigmoid sinus plate posteriorly</li>\n<li>Posterior bony external auditory canal wall anteriorly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is also called the <strong>triangle of attack</strong>.</p>\n<p>Image showing sinodural angle:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6991","text":"Endolymphatic sac surgery ​is performed on a patient‍ who has Ménière disease ‌with intractable vertigo.‌ Which of the following i‌s the surgical landmark t‍o identify the sac?","raw_text":"Endolymphatic sac surgery is performed on a patient who has Ménière disease with intractable vertigo. Which of the following is the surgical landmark to identify the sac?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trautmann’​s triangle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MacEwen’s​ triangle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Donaldso​n’s line","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Citelli​'s angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Donaldson’s line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff47ecf7d63d4ddea17905fb26fed428x1000x715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Donaldson’s line</strong></p><p><strong>Donaldson’s line</strong> is the surgical landmark to locate the<strong> endolymphatic sac. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an imaginary line drawn from the<strong> horizontal </strong><strong>semicircular canal, </strong>which<strong> bisects</strong> the<strong> posterior semicircular canal.</strong> The endolymphatic sac lies just below it. </p>\n<p>Image showing Donaldson’s line:<label class=\"ng-binding\"></label></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>ELS: Endolymphatic sac; HSCC: Horizontal semicircular canal, PSCC: Posterior semicircular canal; PF dura: Posterior fossa dura.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6992","text":"A head and neck surgeon en​ters the cranial cavity v‍ia the Trautmann’s triang‌le. Which fossa can be ap‌proached through this tri‌angle?","raw_text":"A head and neck surgeon enters the cranial cavity via the Trautmann’s triangle. Which fossa can be approached through this triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior cr​anial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle cra​nial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior c​ranial fossa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypophys​eal fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior cranial fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34418b145fc448db99d7c7a47e2344d9x1280x1670.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior cranial fossa</strong></p><p><strong>Trautmann’s triangle </strong>is a landmark to approach the<strong> posterior cranial fossa</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a triangular area on the medial wall of the mastoid antrum.</li>\n<li>It is bounded superiorly by the dura or superior petrosal sinus, posteriorly by the sigmoid sinus, and anteriorly by the bony labyrinth.</li>\n<li>Since here the bone is thin, it can lead to the spreading of infections from the mastoid to the cerebellum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image showing the Trautmann’s triangle:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9mqNzeT-","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"4_bns__bnss__and_bsa","title":"4.Bns, Bnss, And Bsa","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB2181","text":"Which of the following pro‌visions is included in BN​S Section 22/IPC section ‍84?","raw_text":"Which of the following provisions is included in BNS Section 22/IPC section 84?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Child under 7 years is not‌ liable for the act","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A person of unsound mind i‌s not liable for the act","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A person who has committed‌ an act under involuntary​ intoxication is liable","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A person who commits an ac‌t under voluntary intoxic​ation is not liable","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A person of unsound mind is not liable for the act","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A person of unsound mind is not liable for the act</strong></p><p><strong>BNS</strong> Section <strong>22</strong> (previously IPC section 84) states that the act of a person of unsound mind is <strong>not liable </strong>for<strong> punishment</strong>/ prosecution. </p>\n<p>The defence under this section requires the following <strong>elements</strong> to be established:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The accused was suffering from unsoundness of mind at the time of doing the act.</li>\n<li>The accused was incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or that he was doing what was either wrong or contrary to law.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2186","text":"A newspaper editor disclos‌es the identity of a vict​im of rape without prior ‍permission. He would be t‌ried under which of the f‌ollowing sections?","raw_text":"A newspaper editor discloses the identity of a victim of rape without prior permission. He would be tried under which of the following sections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS 71/‌IPC 376E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS 72/‌IPC 228A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BNS 73/IP‌C 228A (3)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BNS 74/‌IPC 354","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BNS 72/IPC 228A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BNS 72/IPC 228A</strong></p><p>Section <strong>72</strong> of BNS (IPC Section 228A) deals with punishment for <strong>disclosure</strong> of the <strong>identity</strong> of the <strong>victim</strong> of <strong>rape</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Section <strong>71</strong> BNS (IPC 376E) deals with <strong>repeat offenders.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Section <strong>73</strong> BNS (IPC 228A (3)) deals with <strong>printing or publishing</strong> any matter relating to <strong>court proceedings without permission</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Section<strong> 74 </strong>BNS (IPC 354) deals with the <strong>assault or use of criminal force</strong> on a <strong>woman</strong> with <strong>intent to outrage her modesty</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2184","text":"A middle-aged man tried to‌ abscond so that he could​ avoid service of summons‍. Which section of the BN‌S/IPC respectively deals ‌with this scenario?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man tried to abscond so that he could avoid service of summons. Which section of the BNS/IPC respectively deals with this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"206‌/172","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"208‌/174","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"211‌/176","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"212‌/177","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 206/172","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 206/172</strong></p><p>Section<strong> 206 </strong>of BNS<strong> (172</strong> of IPC) deals with <strong>absconding</strong> to avoid service of <strong>summons</strong> or other proceedings.</p>\n<p>Common IPC Sections dealing with summons include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 312px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.0696px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>BNS section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.8636px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>previous IPC Section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.5px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.0696px;\">\n<p>206</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.8636px;\">\n<p>172</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.5px;\">\n<p>Absconding to avoid service of summons or other proceedings</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.0696px;\">\n<p>208</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.8636px;\">\n<p>174</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.5px;\">\n<p>Non-attendance in obedience to an order from public servant</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.0696px;\">\n<p>211</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.8636px;\">\n<p>176</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.5px;\">\n<p>Omission to give notice/ information to public servant by person legally bound to give it</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.0696px;\">\n<p>212</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.8636px;\">\n<p>177</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.5px;\">\n<p>Furnishing false information</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2215","text":"What section of BNSS/CrPC ‌deals with the power to s​ummon persons by a police‍ officer?","raw_text":"What section of BNSS/CrPC deals with the power to summon persons by a police officer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNSS 33 ‌(CrPC 39)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNSS 195 ‌(CrPC 175)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BNSS 395 ‌(CrPC 357)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BNSS 456 ‌(CrPC 416)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BNSS 195 (CrPC 175)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BNSS 195 (CrPC 175)</strong></p><p>The power to <strong>summon </strong>persons by a <strong>police officer</strong> comes under Section <strong>195</strong> of the <strong>BNSS</strong>,<strong> </strong>Bhartiya Nagrik Suraksha Sanhita (175 CrPC).</p>\n<p>BNSS sections are tabulated below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 43.1807%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p><strong>BNSS </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>CrPC</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>33</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>39</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Duty to give information</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>51</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>53</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Examination of accused by a medical practitioner at the request of a police officer</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>52</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>53A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Examination of a person accused of rape by a medical practitioner</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>53</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>54</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Examination of arrested person by medical practitioner at the request of the arrested person</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>63-71</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>61-69</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Summons</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>180</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>161</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>The police have the power to examine witnesses</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>181</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>162</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Oral statements made to the police and recorded by the police should not be signed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>184</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>164A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Examination of the victim of rape</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p><strong>194</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>174</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Police inquest</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p><strong>195</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>175</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Power to summon persons by a police officer</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p><strong>196</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>176</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Magistrate inquest</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>350</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>312</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Expenses of complaints and witnesses</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>366<br/>366(2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>327</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Criminal court is generally deemed to be an open court.<br/>In-camera trial for rape</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>389</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>350</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Summary procedure for punishment for non-attendance by a witness in obedience to the summons</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>395</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>357</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Order to pay compensation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.9006%;\">\n<p>456</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 20.0066%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>416</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.9151%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Commutation of sentence of death on pregnant women</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2210","text":"A drunken person was caugh‌t misbehaving in public. ​What section will he be c‍harged under?","raw_text":"A drunken person was caught misbehaving in public. What section will he be charged under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"353 BNS‌/IPC 505","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"79 BNS/‌IPC 509","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"85 BNS/‌IPC 498A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"355 BNS‌/IPC 510","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 355 BNS/IPC 510","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 355 BNS/IPC 510</strong></p><p><strong>Misconduct</strong> in <strong>public</strong> by a <strong>drunken</strong> person is defined under Section <strong>355</strong> of the <strong>BNS</strong> (<strong>510</strong> IPC).</p>\n<p>Important sections of BNS relating to misconduct:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 50.7169%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.1364%;\">\n<p>BNS section</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.2529%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>IPC section</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72.5963%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Section it deals with</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.1364%;\">\n<p>85</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.2529%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>498A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72.5963%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Husband or relative of husband of a woman subjecting her to cruelty</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.1364%;\">\n<p>79</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.2529%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>509</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72.5963%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Word, gesture, or act intended to insult the modesty of a woman</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.1364%;\">\n<p><strong>355</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.2529%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>510</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72.5963%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Misconduct </strong>in public by a<strong> drunken person </strong>is punishable with imprisonment up to <strong>24 hours</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.1364%;\">\n<p>353</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.2529%;\">\n<p>505</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72.5963%;\">\n<p>Statements conducing to public mischief</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2202","text":"A 10-year-old boy was reco‌vered from a warehouse af​ter he went missing 2 day‍s back. The alleged man w‌ho got caught for kidnapp‌ing the kid will be punis​hed under:","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy was recovered from a warehouse after he went missing 2 days back. The alleged man who got caught for kidnapping the kid will be punished under:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Section 137(1)(a) BNS/ IPC‌ 360","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Section 137(1)(b) BNS/ IPC‌ 361","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Section 138 ‌BNS/ IPC 362","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Section 137(2) BNS/ IPC 36‌3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Section 137(2) BNS/ IPC 363","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Section 137(2) BNS/ IPC 363</strong></p><p>Punishment for<strong> kidnapping</strong> is defined under Section<strong> 137(2) </strong>of the BNS (<strong>363</strong> IPC).</p>\n<table style=\"width: 44.6065%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">BNS section</span></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">IPC section</span></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Section it deals with</span></strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>137 (1) (a)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>360</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Kidnapping from India</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>137 (1) (b)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>361</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Kidnapping from lawful guardianship</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>138</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>362</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Abduction</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p><strong>137 (2)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>363</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Punishment for kidnapping  </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>96</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>366A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Procuration of a child for illicit intercourse (&lt;18 years of age)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>141</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>366B</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Importation of girl (&lt;21 years of age) or boy (&lt;18 years of age) from foreign country</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>97</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>369</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Kidnapping or abducting a child under 10 years with the intent to take any movable property from the child</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>143</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>370</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Trafficking a person</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>144</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>370A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Exploitation of a trafficked person</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>98</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>372</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Selling children for the purpose of prostitution</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20.8193%;\">\n<p>99</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.755%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>373</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.4039%;\" width=\"319\">\n<p>Buying children for the purpose of prostitution</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2195","text":"Section 116 of the BNS (IP‌C 320) deals with:","raw_text":"Section 116 of the BNS (IPC 320) deals with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Definitio‌n of hurt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Definition of grievous hur‌t","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Definition of voluntarily ‌causing hurt","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Definition of voluntarily ‌causing grievous hurt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Definition of grievous hurt","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Definition of grievous hurt</strong></p><p><strong>Section 116 BNS</strong> (<strong>320</strong> IPC) deals with the definition of <strong>grievous hurt.</strong></p>\n<p>The injuries caused by grievous hurt are specific in nature like emasculation, loss of sight, loss of limb, fracture, disfiguration, etc. Whereas the injuries caused by hurt are just covered by bodily pain, disease, and infirmity.</p>\n<p>In the latest update in Section 116 BNS, the suffering threshold period for <strong>grievous hurt</strong> is reduced from <strong>twenty days </strong>to<strong> fifteen days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Important Sections of IPC relating to injuries :</p>\n<table style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>BNS section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>IPC section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>114</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>319</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p>Definition of hurt</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>115 (1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>321</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p>Definition of voluntarily causing hurt</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>115 (2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>323</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p>Punishment for voluntarily causing hurt</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p><strong>116</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p><strong>320</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p><strong>Definition of grievous hurt</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>117 (1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>322</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p>Definition of voluntarily causing grievous hurt</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>117 (2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>325</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.6676px;\">\n<p>Punishment for voluntarily causing grievous hurt</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2198","text":"Which BNS section defines ‌the punishment for volunt​arily causing grievous hu‍rt by the use of acid?","raw_text":"Which BNS section defines the punishment for voluntarily causing grievous hurt by the use of acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"118(1)/‌ 324 IPC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"118(2)/‌ 326 IPC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"124(1)/ ‌326A IPC","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"124(2)/ ‌326B IPC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 124(1)/ 326A IPC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 124(1)/ 326A IPC</strong></p><p>Punishment for <strong>voluntarily</strong> causing grievous <strong>hurt</strong> by the use of <strong>acid</strong> is defined under section <strong>124(1)</strong> of the BNS (<strong>326A</strong> IPC).</p>\n<p>Important sections of BNS relating to Injuries:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 44.4646%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p><strong>BNS section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>IPC section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Punishment</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>117(2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>325</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\">\n<p>Punishment for voluntarily causing grievous hurt</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\">\n<p>Up to 7 years of imprisonment and fine</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>118(1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>324</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Voluntarily causing hurt by dangerous weapons or means</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Up to 3 years of imprisonment or fine (up to Rs. 20000) or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>118(2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>326</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Voluntarily causing grievous hurt by dangerous weapons or means</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Imprisonment for life, Imprisonment for a minimum of 1 year to 10 years, and a fine.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>122(1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>334</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\">\n<p>Voluntarily causing hurt on provocation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\">\n<p>Up to 1 month of imprisonment, or fine up to Rs. 5000 or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>122(2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>335</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\">\n<p>Voluntarily causing grievous hurt on provocation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\">\n<p>Up to 5 years of imprisonment or fine up to Rs. 10,000 or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p><strong>124(1)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>326A</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Voluntarily causing grievous hurt by use of acid</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Minimum 10 years imprisonment, extended up to life imprisonment and fine</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>124(2)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>326B</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Voluntarily throwing or attempting to throw acid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Minimum 5 years imprisonment, extended up to 7 years, and fine</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>125(a)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>337</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Causing hurt by rash or negligent act endangering life or personal safety of others</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Up to 6 months of imprisonment or fine up to Rs.5000 or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\">\n<p>125 (b)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 14.5194%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>338</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.7932%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Causing grievous hurt by rash or negligent act endangering life or personal safety of others</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.168%;\" width=\"213\">\n<p>Up to 3 years of imprisonment or fine up to Rs.10,000 or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2179","text":"Which BNS section denotes ‌the death of the human be​ing, unless the contrary ‍appears from the context?‌","raw_text":"Which BNS section denotes the death of the human being, unless the contrary appears from the context?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2(14)/‌ IPC 44","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2(17)/‌ IPC 45","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2(6)/ ‌IPC 46","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2(‌3)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2(6)/ IPC 46","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2(6)/ IPC 46</strong></p><p>BNS Section <strong>2(6)</strong> (IPC <strong>46) </strong>denotes the <strong>death</strong> of a human being unless the contrary appears from the context. In simple words, the word death denotes the death of a human being unless the context suggests otherwise.</p>\n<p>Option A: BNS <strong>2(14)</strong> (IPC <strong>44</strong>)<strong> </strong>defines<strong> injury</strong> as any harm illegally caused to any person, in body, mind, reputation, or property.</p>\n<p>Option B: BNS <strong>2(17)</strong> (IPC <strong>45</strong>) defines <strong>life</strong> as the life of a human being unless the contrary appears from the context.</p>\n<p>Option D: BNS <strong>2(3)</strong> defines a <strong>child</strong> as any person below the age of eighteen years. This is a new addition and was not there in the IPC.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2191","text":"Which sections of BNS deal‌ with punishment for murd​er by an ordinary person ‍and punishment for murder‌ by a life convict, respe‌ctively? BNS - BHARATIYA ​NYAYA SANHITA","raw_text":"<p>Which sections of BNS deal with punishment for murder by an ordinary person and punishment for murder by a life convict, respectively?</p>\n<p>BNS - BHARATIYA NYAYA SANHITA</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sec 102, ‌Sec 103(1)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sec 103(1‌), Sec 105","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sec 103(1‌), Sec 104","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sec 104,‌ Sec 105","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sec 103(1), Sec 104","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sec 103(1), Sec 104</strong></p><p>Punishment for murder by an <strong>ordinary</strong> person is defined under Section <strong>103(1)</strong> of BNS (or Section <strong>302</strong> IPC).</p>\n<p>Punishment for murder by a <strong>life convict</strong> is defined under Section <strong>104</strong> of the BNS (or Section <strong>303</strong> IPC).</p>\n<p>Offences affecting life are given in the table below.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 408px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>BNS section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>IPC section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>100</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>299</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Culpable homicide (amounting to murder)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>101</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>300</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Murder </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>102</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>301</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Culpable homicide by causing the death of a person other than the person whose death was intended</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p><strong>103(1)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p><strong>302</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Punishment for murder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p><strong>103(2)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p><strong>(new addition)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>When a group of five or more persons murders on the<br/>ground of race, caste or community, sex, place of birth, language, or personal belief, each member shall be punished with death or with<br/>imprisonment for life, and shall also be liable to fine</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p><strong>104</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p><strong>303 </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Punishment for murder by life convict (life imprisonment, or death sentence)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>105</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>304</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Punishment for culpable homicide not amounting to murder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>106(1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>304A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Causing death by negligence</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>80</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>304B</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Dowry death</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>107</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>305</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Abetment of suicide of child or person of unsound mind</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>108</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>306</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Abetment of suicide</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>109</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.8494px;\">\n<p>307</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.993px;\">\n<p>Attempt to Murder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2193","text":"What does BNS section 90/I‌PC Section 314 deal with?​","raw_text":"What does BNS section 90/IPC Section 314 deal with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causing miscarriage with c‌onsent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Causing miscarriage withou‌t consent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Death of mother due to mis‌carriage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Preventing the child being‌ born alive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Death of mother due to miscarriage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Death of mother due to miscarriage</strong></p><p> <strong>BNS </strong>Section<strong> 90</strong>/IPC Section <strong>314</strong> deals with the <strong>death</strong> of the <strong>mother</strong> due to <strong>miscarriage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Important sections of IPC relating to miscarriages:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 310px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9474px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>BNS section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>IPC section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.898px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9474px;\">\n<p>88</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>312</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.898px;\">\n<p>Causing miscarriage with consent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9474px;\">\n<p>89</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>313</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.898px;\">\n<p>Causing miscarriage without consent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9474px;\">\n<p><strong>90</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p><strong>314</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.898px;\">\n<p>Death of mother due to miscarriage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9474px;\">\n<p>91</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>315</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.898px;\">\n<p>Preventing the child from being born alive or causing it to die after birth</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9474px;\">\n<p>92</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px;\">\n<p>316</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.898px;\">\n<p>Causing the death of the quick unborn child by act amounting to culpable homicide</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Quick<strong> child</strong> is a fetus that has developed to such a stage that it moves within the womb of the mother. It shall mean an unborn child whose heart is beating, who is experiencing electronically measurable brain waves, who is discernibly moving, and who is so far developed and matured as to be capable of surviving the trauma of birth with the aid of usual medical care and facilities available in this state.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2194","text":"A woman gave birth to a st‌illborn baby. She conceal​ed the birth by secret di‍sposal of the dead body. ‌Which of the following st‌atement is false regardin​g this situation?","raw_text":"A woman gave birth to a stillborn baby. She concealed the birth by secret disposal of the dead body. Which of the following statement is false regarding this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"She can be imprisoned for ‌a maximum of 2 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"She can be charged under S‌ec 94 of BNS (IPC 318)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"She cannot be charged unde‌r Sec 80 of BNS (IPC 304B​)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The act is defined under S‌ec 93 of BNS (IPC 317)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The act is defined under Sec 93 of BNS (IPC 317)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The act is defined under Sec 93 of BNS (IPC 317)</strong></p><p>The given scenario is defined under <strong>Section 94 of the</strong> <strong>BNS</strong> (IPC 318), not Sec 93 BNS.<br/><br/></p>\n<table style=\"width: 325.9375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong> Section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Punishment</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>BNS 93</strong> (IPC 317)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Exposure and abandonment</strong> of child under 12 years by parents</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Up to 7 years of imprisonment or fine or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>BNS 94</strong> (IPC 318)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Concealment</strong> of birth by <strong>secret disposal</strong> of the dead body</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Up to 2 years of imprisonment or fine or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/><strong>Section 80 of BNS</strong> (IPC 304B) deals with <strong>dowry death</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2366","text":"Which of the following con‌stitutional article is no​t related to children?","raw_text":"Which of the following constitutional article is not related to children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌1A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4‌2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 42","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 42</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Article 42 is not related to children. Article <strong>42</strong> says the state shall make provision for securing just and humane conditions of work and <strong>maternity relief.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Constitutional Guarantees that are meant specifically for children include:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Article <strong>21 A</strong> - Right to free and compulsory elementary education in the 6 -14 year age group.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Article <strong>24 - </strong>Right to be protected from any hazardous employment till the age of 14 years.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Article <strong>39 E - </strong>Right to be protected from being abused.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Article <strong>39 F - </strong>Right to equal opportunities and facilities.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Article <strong>45 - </strong>Right to early childhood care and education for all children until they complete the age of 6 years.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0954","text":"According to the Juvenile ‌Justice Act, which of the​ following pairs correctl‍y refers to a juvenile? G‌irl Boy A &lt;18 years &lt;18 y‌ears B &lt;16 years &lt;18 year​s C &lt;18 years &lt;16 years D‌ &lt;16 years &lt;16 years","raw_text":"<p>According to the Juvenile Justice Act, which of the following pairs correctly refers to a juvenile?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 128px; width: 239.125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 26px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Girl</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Boy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 26px;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\"> &lt;18 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.125px;\">&lt;18 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 26px;\">B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\"> &lt;16 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.125px;\">&lt;18 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 26px;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\"> &lt;18 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.125px;\">&lt;16 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 26px;\">D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\"> &lt;16 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.125px;\">&lt;16 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Option A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Option A</strong></p><p>According to Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2000, <strong>juvenile</strong> refers to a <strong>boy</strong> or a <strong>girl</strong> who has <strong>not completed</strong> <strong>18 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 allows for juveniles 16 years or older to be tried as adults for heinous offences like rape and murder. Heinous offences are those which are punishable with imprisonment of seven years or more.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2200","text":"Which section of the BNS d‌eals with the use of crim​inal force on a woman wit‍h the intent to outrage h‌er modesty?","raw_text":"Which section of the BNS deals with the use of criminal force on a woman with the intent to outrage her modesty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"74 (IP‌C 354)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"75 (IP‌C 354A)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"76 (IP‌C 354B)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"77 (IP‌C 354C)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 74 (IPC 354)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 74 (IPC 354)</strong></p><p>Use of criminal force on woman with the intent to outrage her modesty comes under Section <strong>74</strong> of the <strong>BNS</strong> (<strong>354</strong> IPC).</p>\n<p>Important sections of IPC relating to sexual harassment:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 295px; height: 465px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.4446px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>BNS section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>IPC section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.401px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.4446px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>74</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>354</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.401px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Assault or use of criminal force to woman with intent to outrage her modesty</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.4446px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>75</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>354A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.401px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Sexual harassment of nature of unwelcome physical contact and advances or a demand or request for sexual favors, showing pornography</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.4446px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>76</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>354B</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.401px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Criminal force to women with intent to disrobe</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.4446px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>77</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>354C</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.401px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Voyeurism</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.4446px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>78</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>354D</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.401px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Stalking</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2192","text":"A 17-year-old boy was brou‌ght unconscious to the ER​. Later it was found out ‍from his brother that he ‌had taken 10-12 sleeping ‌tablets as his girlfriend​ broke up with him last w‌eek. Which section deals ‍with this scenario?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy was brought unconscious to the ER. Later it was found out from his brother that he had taken 10-12 sleeping tablets as his girlfriend broke up with him last week. Which section deals with this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS 108‌/IPC 306","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS 109‌/IPC 307","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BNS 110‌/IPC 308","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IPC‌ 309","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IPC 309","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IPC 309</strong></p><p><strong>Attempt</strong> to commit <strong>suicide</strong> was defined under Section <strong>309</strong> of the IPC. </p>\n<p>This provision was, in effect, <strong>repealed by Section 115 of the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017</strong>. Hence, it was not added to the text of the BNS.  </p>\n<p><strong>Abetment</strong> of suicide (support or aid to commit suicide) is defined under Section <strong>108</strong> BNS (<strong>306</strong> IPC). It states that if any person commits suicide, whoever abets the commission of such suicide, shall be punished with imprisonment which may extend to 10 years, and shall also be liable to fine.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 368px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.169px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.504px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.169px;\">\n<p>BNS 107/ IPC 305</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.504px;\">\n<p>Abetment of suicide of child or person of unsound mind</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.169px;\">\n<p>BNS 108/ IPC 306</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.504px;\">\n<p><strong>Abetment</strong> of suicide of a sane individual</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.169px;\">\n<p>BNS 109/ IPC 307</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.504px;\">\n<p>Attempt to murder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.169px;\">\n<p>BNS 110/ IPC 308</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.504px;\">\n<p>Attempt to commit culpable homicide</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.169px;\">\n<p>IPC 309</p>\n<p>(not added to BNS)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.504px;\">\n<p><strong>Attempt</strong> to commit suicide</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2190","text":"A newly married woman was ‌found dead in her house. ​On inquiry, it was inform‍ed by the neighbours that‌ the couple used to fight‌ a lot regarding dowry. W​hich section deals with t‌his scenario?","raw_text":"A newly married woman was found dead in her house. On inquiry, it was informed by the neighbours that the couple used to fight a lot regarding dowry. Which section deals with this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS Section ‌63 [IPC 375]","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS Section ‌80 [IPC 304B]","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BNS Section ‌72 [IPC 228A]","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BNS Section ‌100 [IPC 299]","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BNS Section 80 [IPC 304B]","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BNS Section 80 [IPC 304B]</strong></p><p><strong>Dowry Death</strong> is defined under <strong>section 80</strong> of Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) 2023 (IPC 304B)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If a husband or his relative subjects the woman to cruelty or harassment in demand of dowry within 7 years of marriage, they shall be deemed to have caused her death.</li>\n<li>The punishment for such cases is imprisonment for<strong> not less than 7 years</strong> but may be extended to life imprisonment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Section 63 (IPC 375) </strong>deals with <strong>rape</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Section 72 (IPC 228A) </strong>deals with imprisonment for up to 2 years and a fine for <strong>disclosure of the identity of a rape victim</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Section 100 (IPC 299)</strong> deals with <strong>culpable homicide</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5849","text":"An unmarried man had conse‌nsual sexual intercourse ​with the wife of his frie‍nd. His friend found abou‌t the affair and filed an‌ FIR against the man. Und​er which of the following‌ BNS section can the man ‍be booked?","raw_text":"An unmarried man had consensual sexual intercourse with the wife of his friend. His friend found about the affair and filed an FIR against the man. Under which of the following BNS section can the man be booked?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS 318(4‌)/ IPC 420","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS 75/ ‌IPC 354 A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IPC‌ 497","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p>Adultery is not liable for criminal prosecution.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 497</strong> of the Indian Penal Code dealing with <strong>adultery</strong> was<strong> invalidated</strong> (struck down as unconstitutional being violative of articles 14 and 21) by the Hon’ble Supreme Court of India on 27<sup>th</sup> September 2018. However, there might be civil liabilities including ground for divorce and dissolution of marriage.</p>\n<p>However, BNS retains Section 498 of the IPC as BNS Section 84 which penalizes a man for enticing the wife of another man so that she may have intercourse with any person. </p>\n<p>Option A: BNS <strong>318(4)</strong>/IPC <strong>420</strong> deals with cheating and dishonestly inducing delivery of property.</p>\n<p>Option B: BNS <strong>75</strong>/IPC<strong> 354A</strong> deals with sexual harassment and punishment of sexual harassment. Consensual sexual intercourse is not included in this.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5850","text":"A woman found out that her‌ husband is having a cons​ensual sexual relationshi‍p with another man and th‌at the affair has been go‌ing on for a few months. ​She was deeply aggrieved ‌and committed suicide. Up‍on investigation, the pol​ice found her suicide not‌e wherein she had stated ​all the facts. Under whic​h of the following sectio​n can the husband be book​ed?","raw_text":"A woman found out that her husband is having a consensual sexual relationship with another man and that the affair has been going on for a few months. She was deeply aggrieved and committed suicide. Upon investigation, the police found her suicide note wherein she had stated all the facts. Under which of the following section can the husband be booked?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IPC‌ 497","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IPC‌ 377","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BNS 108‌/IPC 306","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"BNS 74/‌IPC 354","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. BNS 108/IPC 306","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BNS 108/IPC 306</strong></p><p>The husband can be booked under Section <strong>108</strong> of the Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita (<strong>306</strong> of the Indian Penal Code), which deals with the <strong>abetment </strong>of<strong> suicide</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Hon’ble Supreme Court of India on 27<sup>th</sup> September 2018 struck down IPC 497 dealing with adultery as unconstitutional. However, the court noted that if any <strong>aggrieved </strong><strong>spouse </strong>commits<strong> suicide</strong> because of the life partner's adulterous relation then, if the<strong> evidence </strong>produced, it could be treated as an <strong>abetment to suicide</strong>.</p>\n<p>Consensual sexual intercourse between adults of same-sex no longer comes under the purview of section 377 of IPC (as per the judgment of the Hon’ble Supreme court on 6<sup>th</sup> September 2018).</p>\n<p>Hence, there are<strong> no sections</strong> corresponding to <strong>IPC 497 and 377</strong> in the Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2217","text":"According to the Bharatiya‌ Sakshya Adhinyam, sectio​n 39(1) [IEA 45] gives in‍formation regarding:","raw_text":"According to the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhinyam, section 39(1) [IEA 45] gives information regarding:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Evid‌ence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Expert ‌witness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hostile‌ witness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dying de‌claration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Expert witness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Expert witness</strong></p><p><strong>BSA </strong>(Bharatiya Sakshya Adhinyam) Section <strong>39(1) [IEA</strong> <strong>45]</strong> gives information regarding <strong>Expert witnesses. </strong></p>\n<p>The important Sections under BSA (Bharatiya Sakshya Adhinyam) include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 337px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>BSA section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>IEA Section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Provision for</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>2(1)(e)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>3</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Evidence</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>26</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>32</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Dying declaration</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>39(1)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>45</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Expert witness</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>110</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>107</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>The burden of proving the death of the person known to have been alive within 30 years</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>111</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>108</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>The burden of proving that the person is alive who has not been heard of for 7 years</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>116</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>112</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Birth during the marriage - conclusive proof of legitimacy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>157</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>154</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Hostile witness- question by party to his own witness</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57.1236px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>165</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45.423px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>162</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.418px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Production of documents</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3897","text":"Which Section of the IPC/B‌NS deals with the punishm​ent of perjury?","raw_text":"Which Section of the IPC/BNS deals with the punishment of perjury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"191/ B‌NS 227","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"192/ B‌NS 228","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"193/ B‌NS 229","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"197/ B‌NS 234","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 193/ BNS 229","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 193/ BNS 229</strong></p><p>The <strong>punishment </strong>of<strong> perjury</strong> is dealt with under <strong>IPC 193,</strong> has now been updated to <strong>BNS 229</strong>. </p>\n<p>Perjury is defined as the offence of wilfully telling an untruth or making a misrepresentation under oath.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5201","text":"First-hand knowledge rule ‌is applicable to:","raw_text":"First-hand knowledge rule is applicable to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common ‌witness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hand writ‌ing expert","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doc‌tor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hostile‌ witness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Common witness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Common witness</strong></p><p>The <strong>first-hand knowledge</strong> rule is applicable to a<strong> common witness</strong> who gives <strong>evidence</strong> about the<strong> facts observed </strong>or<strong> perceived </strong>by him.</p>\n<p>An <strong>expert witness</strong> is someone who has been <strong>trained </strong>or is<strong> skilled</strong> or has <strong>knowledge</strong> and <strong>experience</strong> and is able to draw conclusions from the facts observed by him. Doctors, handwriting/ fingerprints/ firearms experts come under this category.</p>\n<p>A <strong>hostile witness</strong> is a person who is supposed to have some<strong> interest</strong> or<strong> motive </strong>for<strong> concealing </strong>a part of the<strong> truth</strong> or for giving completely false evidence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2183","text":"What does Section 200 of t‌he BNS (IPC 166B) deal wi​th?","raw_text":"What does Section 200 of the BNS (IPC 166B) deal with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-treatment of victim by‌ hospital","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Public servant disobeying ‌direction under law","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Public servant framing an ‌incorrect document","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unlawful engagement in tra‌de by hospital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non-treatment of victim by hospital","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non-treatment of victim by hospital</strong></p><p>Section <strong>200</strong> of the <strong>BNS</strong> (<strong>166B</strong> IPC) deals with the <strong>non-treatment </strong>of<strong> victims</strong> by <strong>hospitals</strong>. Non-treatment of the victim by the hospital is in direct violation of <strong>Section 397 </strong>of<strong> BNSS</strong> (Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita) [<strong>357C</strong> CrPC].</p>\n<p>BNS 199 (IPC <strong>166A</strong>) deals with public servants disobeying direction under law.</p>\n<p>BNS 201 (IPC <strong>167</strong>) deals with public servants framing an incorrect document with an intent to cause injury.</p>\n<p>BNS 202 (IPC <strong>168</strong>) deals with public servants unlawfully engaging in trade.</p>\n<p>A comparison of Sections 199 and 200 of the BNS is given in the table below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 299px; height: 384px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.734375px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>199 BNS/ 166A IPC</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>200 BNS/ </strong><strong>166B IPC</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 150px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.734375px; height: 150px;\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 150px;\">\n<p>Public servant disobeying direction under law</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 150px;\">\n<p>Non-treatment of victim by hospital</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 155px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.734375px; height: 155px;\">\n<p><strong>Punishment</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 155px;\">\n<p>Imprisonment for a minimum of 6 months which may extend to 2 years and fine</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 155px;\">\n<p>Imprisonment for 1 year or fine or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 155px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.734375px; height: 155px;\">\n<p><strong>Type of offense</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 155px;\">\n<p>Cognizable (arrestable without a warrant) and bailable</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.046875px; height: 155px;\">\n<p>Non-Cognizable and bailable</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7391","text":"As per the Medicare Servic‌e Persons and Damage to P​roperty in Medicare Servi‍ce Institutions Act, viol‌ence against a registered‌ medical practitioner is ​considered as:","raw_text":"As per the Medicare Service Persons and Damage to Property in Medicare Service  Institutions Act, violence against a registered medical practitioner is considered as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-cognizable and bailabl‌e offense","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cognizable and non-bailabl‌e offense","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-cognizable and non-bai‌lable offense","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cognizable and bailable of‌fense","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cognizable and non-bailable offense","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cognizable and non-bailable offense</strong></p><p>As per the Medicare Service Persons and Damage to Property in Medicare Service Institutions (Prevention of Violence and Damage or Loss to Property) Act, <strong>violence</strong> against a registered <strong>medical practitioner</strong> is considered a <strong>cognizable and non-bailable offense</strong>.  </p>\n<p>As per this law, enacted by many Indian states, violence against registered medical practitioners, registered nurses, medical and nursing students, and paramedical and other auxiliary workers carries a penalty of imprisonment and fine.  </p>\n<p>Violence means activities of <strong>causing</strong> any <strong>harm</strong>, injury, or endangering the life or intimidation, obstruction, or hindrance to any <strong>medicare service person</strong> while discharging their duty in the medicare service institution or causing <strong>damage</strong> or loss to the <strong>property</strong>.</p>\n<p>The period of <strong>imprisonment</strong> may extend from <strong>3 to 10 years</strong> in certain states.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7126","text":"In case of professional mi‌sconduct, the patient's r​ecords on demand should b‍e provided within:","raw_text":"In case of professional misconduct, the patient's records on demand should be provided within:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"72 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"42 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7 d‌ays","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 72 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 72 hours</strong></p><p>In case of <strong>professional misconduct</strong>, the patient's <strong>records</strong> on demand should be provided within <strong>72 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p><br/>If a doctor does not maintain the medical records of the patients for a period of <strong>three years</strong> as per the regulation, and refuses to provide the same within 72 hours, when the patient or his/her authorized representative makes a request for it, then it is professional misconduct.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0642","text":"In the civil negligence ca‌se against a doctor who b​ears the onus of proof?","raw_text":"In the civil negligence case against a doctor who bears the onus of proof?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pat‌ient","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Police not under rank of s‌ub inspector","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magis‌trate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Doc‌tor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patient</strong></p><p>In the case of civil negligence, the<strong> burden </strong>of<strong> proof </strong>is on the<strong> patient.</strong></p>\n<p>The question of<strong> civil negligence</strong> arises when a suit is filed in a civil court:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>By a patient</strong> or his/her relatives against the doctor for getting<strong> compensation </strong>from the<strong> doctor</strong> if he/she has suffered an injury due to the doctor's negligence.</li>\n<li><strong>By a doctor</strong> against the patient or his/her relatives to collect the<strong> fees</strong>, the payment of which has been refused due to alleged professional negligence by the doctor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In either case, the <strong>patient </strong>has to<strong> prove</strong> that the doctor has committed <strong>professional negligence</strong>.</p>\n<p>To prove <strong>negligence</strong>, all the following four<strong> elements </strong>must be present<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Duty -</strong> A duty of care by the doctor must exist.</li>\n<li><strong>Dereliction -</strong> The physician must maintain the standard of a 'prudent physician' under similar circumstances. If the physician fails to maintain the standard of care, it is called dereliction.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct</strong> <strong>causation -</strong> The negligent act or omission must directly cause the injury without any intervening cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Damage -</strong> The negligent act must cause damage of a type that would have been foreseen by a reasonable physician.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iNoj3Fwd","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"4_disorders_of_anterior_pituitary","title":"4.Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6668","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a characteristic of g‌rowth hormone (GH) defici‌ency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of growth hormone (GH) deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperg‌lycemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Micro‌penis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delayed ‌bone age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High-pitc‌hed voice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperglycemia</strong></p><p>Hyperglycemia is not a characteristic of GH deficiency. Isolated GH deficiency, especially in children, can lead to a higher propensity to develop <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>. This is attributed to the relatively unopposed action of insulin. </p>\n<p>Isolated GH deficiency in children is associated with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short stature</li>\n<li>Micropenis</li>\n<li>Increased fat</li>\n<li>High-pitched voice</li>\n<li>High propensity to develop hypoglycemia due to relatively unopposed insulin action.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6669","text":"A 3-year-old girl is refer‌red to your centre for ev‌aluation of short stature‌. The child's parents are​ found to be first cousin‌s. Further evaluation led​ to the diagnosis of Laro‍n-type dwarfism. What cau​ses this condition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old girl is referred to your centre for evaluation of short stature. The child's parents are found to be first cousins. Further evaluation led to the diagnosis of Laron-type dwarfism. What causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GH def‌iciency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GH recept‌or anomaly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GHRH de‌ficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IGF-1 de‌ficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GH receptor anomaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GH receptor anomaly</strong></p><p>Laron-type dwarfism, also known as <strong>GH-insensitivity syndrome</strong>, is characterized by <strong>GH-receptor anomalies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Laron-type dwarfism is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder. The primary clinical symptom associated with this condition is <strong>short stature</strong>. Treatment is by<strong> </strong>subcutaneous injections of insulin-like growth factor 1 (a growth-promoting hormone), often called <strong>IGF-1</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6670","text":"A 9-year-old girl who pres‌ented with short stature ‌is diagnosed with Turner’‌s syndrome. The physician​ decides to initiate trea‌tment with recombinant hu​man growth hormone for sh‍ort stature. Which of the​ following side effects i‍s she not likely to devel​op?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old girl who presented with short stature is diagnosed with Turner’s syndrome. The physician decides to initiate treatment with recombinant human growth hormone for short stature. Which of the following side effects is she not likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinn‌itus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudotum‌or cerebri","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slipped capital femoral ep‌iphysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoglycemia</strong></p><p>GH is a potent counter-regulatory hormone to insulin and leads to <strong>hyperglycemia, </strong>thus contraindicated in patients with uncontrolled diabetes.</p>\n<p>Side effects of GH administration include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dose-related fluid retention</li>\n<li>Hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Joint pain and carpal tunnel syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Headache, increased intracranial pressure,</strong> hypertension</li>\n<li><strong>Tinnitus</strong></li>\n<li>Pituitary tumor regrowth</li>\n<li>Progression of skin lesions</li>\n<li>Acromegaly</li>\n<li><strong>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis</strong></li>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The actions of GH are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Induces protein synthesis and nitrogen retention</li>\n<li>Impairs glucose tolerance by antagonizing insulin action</li>\n<li>Stimulates lipolysis </li>\n<li>Promotes sodium, potassium, and water retention</li>\n<li>Elevates serum levels of inorganic phosphate</li>\n<li>Linear bone growth occurs</li>\n<li>Stimulates epiphyseal prechondrocyte differentiation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6674","text":"Paradoxical response of GH‌ release to thyrotropin-r‌eleasing hormone (TRH) is‌ seen in all of the follo​wing conditions except___‌___","raw_text":"Paradoxical response of GH release to thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) is seen in all of the following conditions except______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolac‌tinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acrom‌egaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malnut‌rition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anorexia‌ nervosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Malnutrition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Malnutrition</strong></p><p>The paradoxical response of <strong>GH</strong> release to <strong>TRH</strong> is not seen in malnutrition patients.</p>\n<p>In normal individuals, GH doesn't respond to TRH stimulation, but paradoxically, the GH levels may increase in response to TRH stimulation in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acromegaly</li>\n<li>Prolactinoma</li>\n<li>Anorexia nervosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact mechanism underlying this paradoxical GH response is poorly understood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6671","text":"A patient presented to the‌ OPD with complaints of p‌rogressive enlargement of‌ hands and feet and was d​iagnosed with acromegaly.‌ What is the most common ​cause for this condition?‍","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with complaints of progressive enlargement of hands and feet and was diagnosed with acromegaly. What is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GHRH secretion from hypoth‌alamic hamartoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GH secretion from somatotr‌ope adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GH and PRL secretion from ‌mixed somatotroph-lactotr‌oph adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GH and PRL secretion from ‌mixed somatotroph-thyrotr‌oph adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GH secretion from somatotrope adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GH secretion from somatotrope adenoma</strong></p><p>The most common cause of acromegaly is <strong>GH secretion from somatotrope adenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of acromegaly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pituitary - 98% \n<ul>\n<li>Somatotrope adenoma - 60% - most common.</li>\n<li>Mixed somatotroph-lactotroph adenoma </li>\n<li>Other plurihormonal tumors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Extrapituitary tumor-producing GH: Pancreatic Islet cell tumor </li>\n<li>Excess GHRH secretion\n<ul>\n<li>Central - hypothalamic hamartoma </li>\n<li>Peripheral - bronchial carcinoid</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6673","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures will you expect to ‌see in a patient with acr‌omegaly? i. Impaired gluc​ose tolerance ii. Galacto‌rrhea iii. Hypertension i​v. Suppression of GH with‍ glucose v. Low serum pho​sphate","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following features will you expect to see in a patient with acromegaly?</p>\n<p>i.   Impaired glucose tolerance<br/>ii.  Galactorrhea<br/>iii. Hypertension<br/>iv. Suppression of GH with glucose<br/>v.  Low serum phosphate</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"i,ii‌,iii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"i i‌i,iv","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ii,i‌ii,v","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"i,i‌i,v","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. i,ii,iii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. i,ii,iii</strong></p><p><strong>Impaired glucose tolerance</strong>, <strong>galactorrhea</strong>, and <strong>hypertension</strong> can be seen in patients with <strong>acromegaly</strong>. </p>\n<p>GH secretion is a part of the counter-regulatory defense mechanism against hypoglycemia. Physiological GH secretion is inhibited by hyperglycemia. In acromegaly<strong> </strong>or gigantism, <strong>GH secretion</strong> is <strong>autonomous </strong>and not suppressed by glucose. Thus, its diagnosis can be confirmed by demonstrating the <strong>failure of GH suppression</strong> with an oral glucose load (impaired oral glucose tolerance test).</p>\n<p>Galactorrhea is due to <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong> and hypertension is secondary to <strong>increased aldosterone levels </strong>in acromegaly. GH leads to increased production of vitamin D3 which results in increased phosphate absorption from the intestine and decreased renal excretion. Hence there is <strong>increased serum phosphate</strong> level.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6672","text":"A middle-aged man presente‌d to the OPD with complai‌nts of difficulty in chew‌ing and a gradual change ​in voice over the last ye‌ar. Examination revealed ​coarse facial features. T‍he image of his hand (rig​ht side) compared to the ‍physician's hand is shown​ below. Which of the foll‍owing long-term complicat‍ions would you not common‌ly see in this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented to the OPD with complaints of difficulty in chewing and a gradual change in voice over the last year. Examination revealed coarse facial features. The image of his hand (right side) compared to the physician's hand is shown below. Which of the following long-term complications would you not commonly see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes ‌insipidus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary he‌art disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiom‌yopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diabetes insipidus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de6d60f51fc34f0290e8efb8778cca56.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a3e7e46a00140949dc9ab40b169cb9ex1280x1623.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diabetes insipidus</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the enlarged hands in the patient, as shown in the image, point towards a diagnosis of<strong> acromegaly</strong>. Diabetes insipidus is not a feature of acromegaly, but <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> can develop in patients with acromegaly.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of acromegaly are: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 194px; width: 499.515625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\"><strong>System </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gigantism</li>\n<li>Prognathism (protrusion of jaw)</li>\n<li>Carpal tunnel syndrome</li>\n<li>Paresthesia (due to nerve entrapment)</li>\n<li>Proximal myopathy</li>\n<li>Hypertrophy of frontal bones</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Skin and soft tissues</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Acral enlargement in hands and feet (due to thickening of soft tissues)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperhidrosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acanthosis nigricans and skin tags</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Visceralomegaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Enlargement of most organs especially the tongue, thyroid, salivary gland, liver, spleen, kidney, and prostate</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Ophthalmic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Visual field defects (macro-pituitary adenoma)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ophthalmoplegia (cranial nerve palsy)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Headache  </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Metabolic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Low renin with increased aldosterone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong> (due to GH-induced insulin resistance)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and hypercalciuria</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Hypertriglyceridemia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Reproductive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of libido and impotence in males </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Menstrual abnormalities and galactorrhea </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\">Cardiopulmonary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.515625px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Left ventricular hypertrophy/septal hypertrophy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypertension </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obstructive sleep apnea</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Diabetes insipidus can be seen as a complication post Transsphenoidal Surgery for pituitary adenomas and other sellar tumours not as complication of Acromegaly per se.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6676","text":"The middle-aged man shown ‌in the image, noticed tha‌t his shoes no longer fit‌ him and his finger ring ​is getting tightened. Ins‌ulin-like growth factor-1​ (IGF-1) levels are eleva‍ted. Which of the followi​ng tests will help to con‍firm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"The middle-aged man shown in the image, noticed that his shoes no longer fit him and his finger ring is getting tightened. Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) levels are elevated. Which of the following tests will help to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin-like Growth Factor‌ -1 Binding Protein - 3 (‌IGFBP-3) level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Random ‌serum GH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral glucose tolerance tes‌t","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MRI ‌brain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral glucose tolerance test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad04fa7d23ec48be92bb0650ee4fd939.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral glucose tolerance test</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the presence of coarse facial features and prognathism, as shown in the image, points towards a diagnosis of acromegaly. An <strong>oral glucose tolerance test </strong>with GH levels is used to <strong>confirm</strong> <strong>acromegaly</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9399","text":"A 44-year-old man is refer‌red to your center for ma‌nagement of severe hyperg‌lycemia. During examinati​on, you note that the pat‌ient has enlarged feet, c​oarse facial features and‍ prognathism. You suspect​ acromegaly. Which of the‍ following tests can you ​use as a screening method‍ for this condition?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man is referred to your center for management of severe hyperglycemia. During examination, you note that the patient has enlarged feet, coarse facial features and prognathism. You suspect acromegaly. Which of the following tests can you use as a screening method for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral glucose challenge tes‌t with GH level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin-like growth factor‌-1 binding protein-3 (IGF‌BP-3) level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin like growth factor‌-1 levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Random growth hormone leve‌ls","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insulin like growth factor-1 levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insulin like growth factor-1 levels</strong></p><p>The best <strong>screening test</strong> for <strong>acromegaly</strong> is the measurement of serum insulin-like growth factor-1 (<strong>IGF-1</strong>) level.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Oral glucose challenge test with growth hormone (GH) levels</strong> is used for further <strong>confirmation</strong> of the diagnosis. Increased insulin-like growth factor-1 binding protein-3 (<strong>IGFBP-3</strong>) levels can also be seen in patients with acromegaly. But they are not typically used as initial screening methods.</p>\n<p>Option D: IGF-1 levels are more reliable than <strong>random GH levels</strong> because GH secretion is <strong>pulsatile</strong>. IGF-1 levels reflect long-term exposure to GH and provide a more stable indicator of excessive GH production.</p>\n<p>Acromegaly is characterized by excessive GH secretion, usually caused by a <strong>somatotrope adenoma</strong> in the pituitary gland. It can also be due to <strong>ectopic GH</strong> secretion or <strong>hypothalamic tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of acromegaly includes <strong>frontal bossing</strong>, increased hand and foot size, coarse facial features, <strong>mandibular prognathism</strong>, <strong>widened space between lower incisors</strong> and other manifestations such as soft tissue swelling, deepening voice, <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome</strong>, arthropathy, and <strong>acanthosis nigricans</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can also lead to <strong>cardiovascular</strong> complications (<strong>cardiomyopathy</strong>, left ventricular hypertrophy, and <strong>hypertension</strong>), <strong>obstructive sleep apnea</strong>, <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>, an increased risk of colon polyps, and <strong>colonic malignancy</strong>. </p>\n<p>The primary treatment for acromegaly is <strong>surgical resection</strong> of GH-secreting adenomas. <strong>Somatostatin receptor ligands</strong> like octreotide and lanreotide, which suppress GH and IGF-1 levels, are used as adjuvant treatment or when surgery is not possible or unsuccessful. Radiation therapy may be considered for patients who cannot tolerate or do not respond to medical therapy. Other treatment options include <strong>GH receptor antagonists</strong> like <strong>pegvisomant</strong> and dopamine agonists like cabergoline.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6677","text":"The medical management of ‌acromegaly includes all t‌he following therapeutic ‌strategies except _______​","raw_text":"The medical management of acromegaly includes all the following therapeutic strategies except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lanre‌otide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pegvi‌somant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caber‌goline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levo‌dopa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Levodopa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Levodopa</strong></p><p>Levodopa<strong> </strong>is not used in the treatment of acromegaly. Studies have proven that it is not as effective as agents like cabergoline.</p>\n<p><strong>Medical management</strong> of <strong>acromegaly</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Somatostatin analogs:\n<ul>\n<li>Octreotide</li>\n<li><strong>Lanreotide</strong></li>\n<li>Pasireotide</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>GH-receptor antagonists:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pegvisomant</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Dopamine agonists:\n<ul>\n<li>Bromocriptine</li>\n<li><strong>Cabergoline</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6682","text":"A 27-year-old woman presen‌ts to the OPD with infert‌ility and amenorrhea. She‌ also complains of milky ​discharge from the nipple‌. Her urine pregnancy tes​t was negative and her pr‍olactin level was 45 ng/m​l. Which of the following‍ drugs cannot lead to thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman presents to the OPD with infertility and amenorrhea. She also complains of milky discharge from the nipple. Her urine pregnancy test was negative and her prolactin level was 45 ng/ml. Which of the following drugs cannot lead to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methy‌ldopa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenot‌hiazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metoclo‌pramide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bromocriptine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bromocriptine</strong></p><p>This given scenario of infertility, amenorrhea and galactorrhea with elevated prolactin level (normal level is 0-25 ng/ml in a non-pregnant woman) is suggestive of <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong>, and bromocriptine does not lead to hyperprolactinemia. It is a dopamine-agonist and hence it inhibits prolactin synthesis.</p>\n<p>Dopamine agonists like <strong>bromocriptine</strong> and cabergoline are used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6685","text":"Systemic causes of hyperpr‌olactinemia include all o‌f the following clinical ‌conditions except _______​","raw_text":"Systemic causes of hyperprolactinemia include all of the following clinical conditions except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic re‌nal failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epileptic‌ seizures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cirr‌hosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperthyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperthyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong> predisposes to the development of hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p>Both enhanced hypothalamic synthesis of TRH and increased pituitary-responsiveness to TRH have been described as the mechanism behind this phenomenon.</p>\n<p>Other systemic disorders that predispose to the development of hyperprolactinemia :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic renal failure</li>\n<li>Cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Pseudocyesis</li>\n<li>Epileptic seizures</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6688","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a hallmark of hyperpr‌olactinemia in women?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a hallmark of hyperprolactinemia in women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ameno‌rrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‌orrhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infer‌tility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diminish‌ed libido","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diminished libido","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diminished libido</strong></p><p>The hallmarks of hyperprolactinemia in women are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amenorrhea – (Secondary &gt; Primary)</li>\n<li>Galactorrhea</li>\n<li>Infertility</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In men with hyperprolactinemia, diminished libido, infertility, and visual loss (from optic nerve compression) are the usual presenting symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6690","text":"A patient presents to your‌ OPD with galactorrhea an‌d amenorrhea. Apart from ‌the determination of seru​m prolactin levels, which‌ other investigation woul​d you like to do?","raw_text":"A patient presents to your OPD with galactorrhea and amenorrhea. Apart from the determination of serum prolactin levels, which other investigation would you like to do?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L‌H","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T‌SH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"F‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary ke‌tosteroids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. TSH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. TSH</strong></p><p><strong>TSH</strong> should be measured along with the prolactin level to rule out hypothyroidism which can cause hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p>Note: However, pregnancy (using qualitative HCG) should be ruled out in all women of the reproductive age group with amenorrhea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6691","text":"A 30-year-old nullipara wi‌th a desire to conceive p‌resents with amenorrhea a‌nd galactorrhea. UPT is n​egative. Investigations i‌ndicate hyperprolactinemi​a. What is the drug of ch‍oice for clinical managem​ent of this condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old nullipara with a desire to conceive presents with amenorrhea and galactorrhea. UPT is negative. Investigations indicate hyperprolactinemia. What is the drug of choice for clinical management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caber‌goline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sando‌statin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pegvi‌somant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bromocriptine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bromocriptine</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for clinical management of this patient would be bromocriptine.</p>\n<p><strong>Bromocriptine</strong> is an ergot alkaloid and a short-acting dopamine receptor agonist. Due to its <strong>short-term</strong> action, it is<strong> preferred </strong>when<strong> pregnancy is desired</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cabergoline</strong> is a <strong>long-acting</strong> dopamine receptor agonist with high D<sub>2</sub> receptor affinity. It effectively suppresses PRL secretion for &gt;14 days after a single oral dose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"l7YALDmC","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"4_hypersensitivity","title":"4.Hypersensitivity","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA6813","text":"What route of administrati‌on of the shocking dose o​f an antigen is most effe‌ctive in causing an anaph‌ylactic reaction?","raw_text":"What route of administration of the shocking dose of an antigen is most effective in causing an anaphylactic reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra‌dermal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intra‌venous","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subcut‌aneous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Or‌al","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intravenous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b8ace834a9649dc9711d23f48c85175x1280x2567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intravenous</strong></p><p>The<strong> intravenous</strong> route of administration is the <strong>most effective</strong> route of administration for <strong>shocking dose</strong> in causing anaphylaxis.</p>\n<p>Anaphylaxis is a <strong>type</strong> <strong>I hypersensitivity</strong> syndrome. There are 2 clinical syndromes which are characterised as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaphylaxis -<strong> </strong>acute, potentially fatal systemic form</li>\n<li>Atopy -<strong> </strong>chronic, recurrent, non-fatal, localized form</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Priming</strong> dose: For induction of hypersensitivity reactions, the host should have contact with the antigen, leading to priming of the appropriate B or T lymphocytes. </p>\n<p><strong>Shocking</strong> dose: Subsequent contact with allergen causing manifestations of hypersensitivity.</p>\n<p>Effectiveness: <strong>intravenous</strong> &gt; intraperitoneally~subcutaneously &gt; intradermally (least)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6821","text":"Which type of reaction is ‌the Schultz-Dale phenomen​on?","raw_text":"Which type of reaction is the Schultz-Dale phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type II hypersensitivity r‌eaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type I hypersensitivity re‌action","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type III hypersensitivity ‌reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type IV hypersensitivity r‌eaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type I hypersensitivity reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type I hypersensitivity reaction</strong></p><p>The Schultz–Dale phenomenon is a<strong> type I hypersensitivity</strong> reaction. </p>\n<p>It describes <strong>in-vitro contraction</strong> of <strong>smooth muscles</strong> that have been exposed to antigens, demonstrating <strong>immediate hypersensitivity</strong> reaction in some human and animal tissues.  </p>\n<p>Other type I hypersensitivity reactions are the <strong>Prausnitz-Kustner</strong> reaction and the <strong>Theobald Smith</strong> phenomenon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0029","text":"The presence of which of t‌he following antibodies i​s demonstrated by the Pra‌usnitz-Kustner reaction?","raw_text":"The presence of which of the following antibodies is demonstrated by the Prausnitz-Kustner reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gE","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IgE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IgE</strong></p><p>The presence and activity of <strong>IgE</strong> antibodies are demonstrated by the <strong>Prausnitz–Küstner (P–K) reaction.</strong></p>\n<p>In this method, the serum is injected from an allergic person into the skin of a nonallergic individual. Then a <strong>suspected antigen</strong> is later injected into the same site. A positive P-K reaction is a wheal and flare reaction seen in the non-allergic individual. This indicates that the suspected antigen is causing hypersensitivity. Because of the danger of transmitting <strong>viral hepatitis</strong> and <strong>AIDS,</strong> this test is no longer used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6824","text":"A child, upon ingestion of‌ shellfish, developed a t​ingling sensation in the ‌mouth, swelling of the fa‌ce, lips, and tongue, and‌ also complained of abdom‌inal pain and dizziness. ​What type of reaction is ​this?","raw_text":"A child, upon ingestion of shellfish, developed a tingling sensation in the mouth, swelling of the face, lips, and tongue, and also complained of abdominal pain and dizziness. What type of reaction is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgE mediat‌ed reaction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T cell mediated cytotoxic ‌reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgG mediat‌ed reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA mediated hypersensitiv‌ity reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IgE mediated reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34b854b6d6c24f5abdbaf78af5720d06x1280x2567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IgE mediated reaction</strong></p><p>This child who developed tingling in the mouth, and swelling of the lips and face after eating shelf life is an example of an <strong>IgE mediated</strong> type of hypersensitivity reaction. </p>\n<p><strong>Anaphylaxis</strong> is a severe, potentially life-threatening allergic reaction that occurs <strong>rapidly</strong> and affects the whole body. It is characterized by a sudden release of <strong>histamines</strong> and other chemicals from the immune system in response to an allergen, such as certain <strong>foods,</strong> medications, insect stings, or latex. The onset of symptoms can occur within minutes to a few hours after exposure to the allergen.The primary mediators are <strong>histamine</strong> and <strong>serotonin.</strong> The secondary mediators are prostaglandins and leukotrienes. </p>\n<p>Clinically, anaphylaxis can present with a combination of symptoms that typically include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Respiratory</strong> Symptoms: Patients often experience wheezing and respiratory distress due to bronchoconstriction. Other signs may include shortness of breath, coughing, and a feeling of tightness in the chest.</li>\n<li><strong>Skin</strong> Symptoms: Urticaria (hives) and angioedema (swelling of the skin and mucous membranes) are common. These skin reactions usually develop rapidly, often within minutes to two hours of exposure to the allergen.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiovascular</strong> Symptoms: Hypotension (low blood pressure) is a key indicator of anaphylaxis. Patients may also experience tachycardia (increased heart rate) and, in severe cases, shock.</li>\n<li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong> Symptoms: Abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea, and cramps can occur, particularly if the anaphylaxis is triggered by food allergens.</li>\n<li><strong>Neurological</strong> Symptoms: Dizziness, lightheadedness, or loss of consciousness can occur due to the sudden drop in blood pressure and reduced oxygen supply to the brain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6832","text":"Two days after penicillin ‌administration, a patient​ developed antibodies tha‌t resulted in hemolysis. ‌Which type of hypersensit‌ivity reaction is this?","raw_text":"Two days after penicillin administration, a patient developed antibodies that resulted in hemolysis. Which type of hypersensitivity reaction is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbde3e7d7b9e49e9876890bf5fde33bfx1280x2090.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II</strong></p><p><strong>Development</strong> of<strong> antibodies</strong> resulting in hemolysis <strong>after penicillin</strong> administration is an example of a <strong>type II reaction. </strong>This is a <strong>drug-induced</strong> type II hypersensitivity reaction.</p>\n<p>Penicillin in high doses can result in <strong>immune-mediated hemolysis</strong> via the hapten mechanism. In this, the drug attaches to surface of RBC membrane. Antibodies are produced that are targeted against this combination of penicillin and RBCs. </p>\n<p><strong>Complement</strong> is <strong>activated</strong> by the attached antibody leading to the removal of red blood cells by the spleen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6838","text":"A skin transplant was done‌ from a sister to her bro​ther. After a few years, ‌a brother-to-sister skin ‌transplant was done, but ‌rejection occurred. What ‌is this phenomenon known ​as?","raw_text":"A skin transplant was done from a sister to her brother. After a few years, a brother-to-sister skin transplant was done, but rejection occurred. What is this phenomenon known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schwartzma‌n reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Theobald Smi‌th phenomena","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eichwald Si‌lmser effect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schultz Dal‌e phenomena","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Eichwald Silmser effect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Eichwald Silmser effect</strong></p><p>The above scenario describes an <strong>Eichwald-Silmser effect</strong>,<strong> </strong>which is a <strong>unilateral sex-linked histo-incompatibility.</strong></p>\n<p>While transplants between same-sex siblings are successful, grafts from female to male are also successful, but an <strong>exception</strong> is seen when the <strong>donor</strong> is a <strong>male</strong> and the <strong>recipient</strong> is a <strong>female. </strong>Such grafts are rejected as the grafted male tissue (XY) will have <strong>antigens determined</strong> by the <strong>Y</strong> chromosome which will be <strong>absent in</strong> the <strong>female</strong> (XX) recipient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6839","text":"A 9-year-old girl is broug‌ht with complaints of sen​sitivity to light and pro‌fuse lacrimation. On insp‌ection, the given finding‌ is seen on her upper tar‌sal conjunctiva. What typ​e of hypersensitivity rea​ction is this?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old girl is brought with complaints of sensitivity to light and profuse lacrimation. On inspection, the given finding is seen on her upper tarsal conjunctiva. What type of hypersensitivity reaction is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type II‌ and III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type I","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/198220c0af944763962c9a996e198cb8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type I</strong></p><p><strong>Spring catarrh </strong>or <strong>vernal keratoconjunctivitis</strong> is a <strong>type I hypersensitivity</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p>It is a bilateral, seasonally recurrent, chronic conjunctivitis due to type I hypersensitivity allergic reaction to an <strong>external antigen. </strong>The antigen reacts with IgE, this stimulates mast cell degranulation leading to the release of histamine and other mediators of the allergic response.</p>\n<p>Spring catarrh is one of the several forms of allergic conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6841","text":"Which type of hypersensiti‌vity reaction is involved​ in the hyperacute reject‌ion of renal transplants?‌","raw_text":"Which type of hypersensitivity reaction is involved in the hyperacute rejection of renal transplants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type II</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperacute graft rejection</strong> is an example of <strong>type II hypersensitivity</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p>It is mediated by <strong>preformed antibodies.</strong> It is a rare event that occurs in kidney transplantation.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px; width: 616.163px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Graft rejection</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Time taken for rejection</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Immune mechanisms involved</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.163px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type of hypersensitivity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hyperacute</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Minutes to hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Preformed antibodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.163px; text-align: center;\">II</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Acute</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Weeks to months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Cytotoxic T-cell mediated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.163px; text-align: center;\">IV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Chronic</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Months to years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Chronic DTH mediated and Ab mediated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.163px; text-align: center;\">IV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6846","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about​ anaphylaxis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about anaphylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a type I hypersensit‌ivity reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large amount of histamine ‌released","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary mediators like IL ‌4, IL 5 and GM-CSF are re​leased","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mediated through allergen ‌specific IgE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary mediators like IL 4, IL 5 and GM-CSF are released","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1c9b667e2fc4effbe91a0c141612cbfx1280x2363.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary mediators like IL 4, IL 5 and GM-CSF are released</strong></p><p><strong>IL4, IL5, GM-CSF</strong> are <strong>secondary mediators</strong> and not primary mediators.</p>\n<p><strong>Anaphylaxis</strong> is a type I reaction and is mediated through <strong>IgE.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Primary mediators </strong>are <strong>preformed</strong> contents of mast cells and basophil granules such as <strong>histamine, serotonin.</strong> They cause vasodilation and bronchoconstriction. <strong>Secondary mediators</strong> are newly formed upon <strong>stimulation</strong> by mast cells, basophils, and other leucocytes (such as leukotrienes IL3, IL4, IL5, IL6; GM-CSF, and prostaglandins) which lead to inflammatory cell infiltrates.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2839","text":"While studying the patient‌ data on hypersensitivity​ reactions over the last ‌2 months, you are tasked ‌with classifying the case‌s by type. Who among the ‌following patients falls ​under type II hypersensit​ivity?","raw_text":"While studying the patient data on hypersensitivity reactions over the last 2 months, you are tasked with classifying the cases by type. Who among the following patients falls under type II hypersensitivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with berylliosis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eczematous reaction on the‌ hand of an HCW wearing p​owdered gloves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with heat intole‌rance, sinus tachycardia,​ and proptosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glomerulonephritis in a pa‌tient with systemic lupus​ erythematosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A patient with heat intolerance, sinus tachycardia, and proptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/209205c8d89e4f1994dc6b36fa1bbd24x1280x1799.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A patient with heat intolerance, sinus tachycardia, and proptosis</strong></p><p>Heat intolerance, sinus tachycardia, and proptosis of the eye suggest a diagnosis of <strong>Grave's disease.</strong> It is a <strong>type II hypersensitivity</strong> reaction that results in the binding of anti-thyroid-stimulating hormone (anti-TSH) antibodies to TSH receptors and stimulates the thyroid cells to produce thyroid hormones.</p>\n<p>Type II hypersensitivity reactions involve combining the of <strong>IgG antibodies</strong> with the antigenic determinants on the <strong>surface</strong> of <strong>cells</strong> leading to <strong>cytotoxic effects.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Berylliosis</strong> is a type<strong> IV</strong> hypersensitivity reaction.</p>\n<p>Option B: Eczematous reaction on the hand of an HCW wearing powdered gloves (<strong>contact dermatitis</strong>) is a type<strong> IV</strong> hypersensitivity reaction.</p>\n<p>Option D: Glomerulonephritis in a patient with <strong>SLE</strong> (systemic lupus erythematosus) is a <strong>type III</strong> hypersensitivity reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2875","text":"A 22-year-old hiker presen‌ts with an itchy rash on ​her wrist. She gives a hi‌story of getting brushed ‌with poison ivy on a trek‌ the previous day. What c‌ells are involved in this​ reaction?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old hiker presents with an itchy rash on her wrist. She gives a history of getting brushed with poison ivy on a trek the previous day. What cells are involved in this reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T c‌ells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"B c‌ells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Langerha‌ns cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d6ad36c29d9434aa33941ef315aba0e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T cells</strong></p><p>The history and image suggest a diagnosis of<strong> contact dermatitis, </strong>a <strong>type IV hypersensitivity</strong> reaction mediated by<strong> T cells</strong>. These are <strong>delayed</strong> hypersensitivity reactions and are induced by <strong>sensitized T cells</strong>, which on contact with the specific antigen releases cytokines that produce biological effects on leucocytes, macrophages, and tissue cells.</p>\n<p>Two types of delayed hypersensitivity are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tuberculin type</li>\n<li>Contact dermatitis type</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2987","text":"A 45-year-old hypertensive‌ man complains of fever, ​migraine, loss of appetit‌e, and myalgia. On examin‌ation, splinter hemorrhag‌es and palpable purpura a‌re noted. On CT angiograp​hy, aneurysm and stenosis​ of medium-sized vessels ​are observed. What is the​ type of hypersensitivity‍ reaction associated with‌ this disease?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old hypertensive man complains of fever, migraine, loss of appetite, and myalgia. On examination, splinter hemorrhages and palpable purpura are noted. On CT angiography, aneurysm and stenosis of medium-sized vessels are observed. What is the type of hypersensitivity reaction associated with this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type III hyp‌ersensitivity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type IV hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type III hypersensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7dd21d618e814c6cb711523a66b46fa9x1280x1360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type III hypersensitivity</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is<strong> polyarteritis nodosa. </strong>It is an example of a <strong>type III hypersensitivity</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p>In type III hypersensitivity, <strong>immune complexes</strong> may <strong>deposit</strong> in <strong>tissues</strong> or <strong>blood vessels,</strong> and elicit an acute <strong>inflammatory</strong> reaction by <strong>activating</strong> the <strong>complement</strong> system. The inflammatory reaction causes tissue injury.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pathogenesis</strong> of <strong>systemic immune complex disease</strong> can be divided into three phases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Formation of immune complexes</li>\n<li>Deposition of immune complexes</li>\n<li>Inflammation and tissue injury.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Examples of type III hypersensitivity reactions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>Polyarteritis nodosa</li>\n<li>Reactive arthritis</li>\n<li>Serum sickness</li>\n<li>Arthus reaction</li>\n<li>Farmer’s lung</li>\n<li>Hypersensitivity pneumonitis</li>\n<li>Type II lepra reaction</li>\n<li>Henoch Schonlein purpura</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5089","text":"Which type of hypersensiti‌vity reaction is implicat​ed in the autoimmune dise‌ase shown below?","raw_text":"Which type of hypersensitivity reaction is implicated in the autoimmune disease shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1ec9cf9e01384e38b3bd90ce16c006a6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b86c8548babf4658bc560405413ff349x1280x1799.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II</strong></p><p>The disease shown in the image is <strong>myasthenia gravis.</strong> It is an example of <strong>type II hypersensitivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Type II hypersensitivity reactions are mediated by <strong>antibodies</strong> directed towards <strong>self-antigens-</strong> <strong>cell surface</strong> or <strong>extracellular matrix. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0028","text":"A 24-year-old patient come‌s with recurrent episodes​ of sneezing, rhinorrhea,‌ and watering of the eyes‌. She says she has these ‌symptoms throughout the y‌ear. Which of the followi​ng immunoglobulins will b​e elevated in her case?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old patient comes with recurrent episodes of sneezing, rhinorrhea, and watering of the eyes. She says she has these symptoms throughout the year. Which of the following immunoglobulins will be elevated in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gE","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IgE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IgE</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette describes a case of <strong>chronic allergic rhinits. IgE</strong> is elevated in these cases. </p>\n<p><strong>Perennial</strong> <strong>allergic</strong> rhinitis, a type of <strong>chronic</strong> rhinitis is a <strong>year-round</strong> problem and is often caused by indoor <strong>allergens</strong> such as dust and animal dander in addition to pollens that may exist at the time. Symptoms tend to occur regardless of the time of the year.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hLF9RGfg","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"4_placenta_and_fetal_membranes","title":"4.Placenta And Fetal Membranes","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB9803","text":"How is the normal placenta‌ described? Discoid Hemoc‍horial Deciduate","raw_text":"<p>How is the normal placenta described?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Discoid</li>\n<li>Hemochorial</li>\n<li>Deciduate</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 ,2 ‌and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 ,2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78f285144c304eab8230b8a4d72255bfx1280x2239.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 ,2 and 3</strong></p><div>The normal human placenta is <strong>discoid, deciduate,</strong> and <strong>hemochorial</strong>. </div>\n<p>The term <strong>deciduate</strong> means that the placenta is <strong>shed</strong> at the time of parturition. <strong>Hemochorial</strong> means that the <strong>maternal blood</strong> directly comes in <strong>contact</strong> with the fetal <strong>trophoblasts</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a normal human placenta.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9804","text":"What does a placental coty‌ledon comprise of?","raw_text":"What does a placental cotyledon comprise of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Portions of the basal plat‌e divided by clefts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Portions of the chorionic ‌plate divided by cleft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Area supplied by multiple ‌truncal chorionic villi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Area supplied by one spira‌l artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Portions of the basal plate divided by clefts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b1b7ba936f243e08324abee7182b83ex1280x1831.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b49def8ed4746349713c5862e5e360ax1280x1095.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Portions of the basal plate divided by clefts</strong></p><p>A placental <strong>cotyledon</strong> is comprised of <strong>portions</strong> of the<strong> basal plate</strong> divided by clefts. It is the <strong>functional unit</strong> of the placenta.</p>\n<p>A placental <strong>cotyledon</strong> or placental lobule constitutes <strong>one truncal</strong> or<strong> main stem villi</strong> and its <strong>ramifications </strong>(rami). Each lobule is supplied by a<strong> single truncal branch</strong> of the <strong>chorionic artery.</strong> Each lobule also has a <strong>single vein. </strong>The lobules constitute functional units of placental architecture.</p>\n<p>The fetal surface is the <strong>chorionic plate.</strong> The umbilical cord inserts in the center of the chorionic plate.</p>\n<p>Note: The entire placenta is supplied by chorionic villi while each cotyledon is only supplied by a single main stem villus with its branches.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9805","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not contribute to the p‍revention of rejection of​ fetus?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not contribute to the prevention of rejection of fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decidual natural killer ce‌lls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decidual st‌romal cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tropho‌blasts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal ‌leucopenia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maternal leucopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maternal leucopenia</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 116\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Maternal leucopenia is not seen in <strong>pregnancy</strong>, instead <strong>leucocytosis</strong> is seen. This plays no part in the prevention of rejection of fetus.</p>\n<p>The<strong> fetus</strong> is a <strong>semiallogenic graft </strong>inside the maternal body as half of its genes are from the father.<strong> Uterine transplantation immunity</strong> contributes to the survival of the fetus in spite of it being semiallogenic. This occurs due to the following factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decidual natural killer cells</strong> are <strong>inefficient</strong>, and therefore are unable to attack the 'foreign' fetal cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Decidual stromal cells</strong> work with natural killer cells to allow <strong>trophoblastic invasion</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Villous trophoblastic cells</strong> don't express HLA antigens and therefore, they are <strong>non-immunogenic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9806","text":"At what period of gestatio‌n are the weights of the ‍fetus and placenta equal?​","raw_text":"At what period of gestation are the weights of the fetus and placenta equal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"21 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 17 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 17 weeks</strong></p><p>At <strong>17 weeks</strong>, the weights of the <strong>fetus</strong> and <strong>placenta</strong> are <strong>equal</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>first trimester</strong>, the <strong>placental</strong> growth is <strong>more</strong> rapid than that of the fetus. By approximately 17 weeks, the placental weight and fetal weight are almost equal. And by <strong>term</strong>, <strong>placental</strong> weight is approximately <strong>1/6<sup>th</sup> of the fetal</strong> weight.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9807","text":"What is the uteroplacental‌ blood flow at term?","raw_text":"What is the uteroplacental blood flow at term?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"350-600‌ ml/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"250-450‌ ml/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"500-750‌ ml/min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"800-105‌0 ml/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 500-750 ml/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 500-750 ml/min</strong></p><p>The uteroplacental blood flow at term is <strong>500-750 ml/min</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9808","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about human chorion‍ic gonadotropin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about human chorionic gonadotropin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha subunit is identical‌ to LH, FSH, TSH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It causes involution of co‌rpus luteum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maximum level is seen at 6‌0-70 days of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Detected in serum 8-9 days‌ after fertilisation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It causes involution of corpus luteum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It causes involution of corpus luteum</strong></p><p>Human chorionic gonadotropin (<strong>hCG</strong>) does not cause involution of the corpus luteum, but it <strong>maintains</strong> <strong>corpus luteum function.</strong></p>\n<p>hCG is <strong>produced</strong> by the <strong>syncitiotrophoblasts</strong> of the placenta. It is a glycoprotein hormone with two subunits. The alpha-subunit is similar to LH, FSH and TSH. The <strong>beta-subunit</strong> is <strong>specific</strong> and is measured by <strong>pregnancy tests</strong>.</p>\n<p>hCG can be <strong>detected</strong> in maternal blood <strong>8 days after fertilisation</strong>. Thus, it can help in diagnosis of pregnancy even before the period is missed. Its levels nearly <strong>doubles every 48 hours</strong> in early pregnancy reaching a <strong>maximum</strong> of 50000 - 100000 IU/L by <strong>60-70 days</strong> of gestation. Then the level starts falling reaching a minimum at 16 weeks, which continues throughout pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The various placental hormones and their functions are given below. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 526px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Placental hormones</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Functions </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)</p>\n<p>Structurally similar to - LH, FSH, TSH</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Maintains corpus luteum function</li>\n<li>Regulates testosterone secretion from fetal testes</li>\n<li>Stimulates maternal thyroid</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Placental lactogen (PL)</p>\n<p>Structurally similar to - GH, Prolactin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Helps the mother adapt to fetal energy requirements</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Vascular and myometrial smooth muscle relaxation</li>\n<li>Initiation of parturition</li>\n<li>Stimulates placental ACTH production </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Placental adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Stimulates fetal and maternal adrenal glucocorticoid production</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other placental hormones include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GnRH, TRH, GHRH, Growth hormone variant (hGH-V)</li>\n<li>Parathyroid hormone-releasing protein (PTH-rP)</li>\n<li>Inhibin </li>\n<li>Activin</li>\n<li>Relaxin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9824","text":"Which of the following can‌not cross the placenta? I‍ron Albumin Immunoglobuli​n M Immunoglobulin G","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following cannot cross the placenta?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Iron</li>\n<li>Albumin</li>\n<li>Immunoglobulin M</li>\n<li>Immunoglobulin G</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Onl‌y 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Albumin and IgM cannot cross the placenta.</p>\n<p><strong>High molecular weight</strong> substances such as proteins (e.g., albumin, IgM, IgA) <strong>cannot cross</strong> the placenta, except IgG.</p>\n<p>Substances which can cross the placenta are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 457px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Substances</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong> Mechanism</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>O<sub>2 <br/></sub>CO<sub>2<br/></sub>Water<br/>Electrolytes<br/>Anesthetic agents </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Simple diffusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Immunoglobulin G <br/>(molecular weight 160,000 Da)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Endocytosis via Fc receptors in the trophoblast </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Glucose  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Facilitated diffusion via glucose transport proteins (GLUTs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Amino acids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Transport-protein-mediated active transport</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Lipids:</p>\n<p>Glycerol<br/>Essential fatty acids <br/>LDL</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Endocytosis via specific receptors </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Minerals:</p>\n<p>Iodine <br/>Iron (unidirectional from mother to fetus)<br/>Calcium<br/>Phosphorus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Carrier-mediated active transport </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Vitamins:</p>\n<p>Vit A <br/>Vit C<br/>Vit D</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: left;\">Carrier-mediated active transport </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Maternal hormones like <strong>insulin, steroid</strong> hormones, and <strong>thyroid</strong> hormones <strong>cross</strong> the placenta<strong> very slowly</strong>, and only in amounts enough to support fetal development. However, calcitonin and parathormone do not cross the placenta. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2246","text":"A primigravida delivered a‌ baby at term with a birt‍h weight of 1.9 kg via no​rmal vaginal delivery. Th‌e placenta delivered appe‌ars as shown in the image​ below. What is this cond‌ition known as?","raw_text":"A primigravida delivered a baby at term with a birth weight of 1.9 kg via normal vaginal delivery. The placenta delivered appears as shown in the image below. What is this condition known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placenta su‌ccenturiata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Placenta ‌marginata","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Circumvalla‌te placenta","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Circummargin‌ate placenta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Circumvallate placenta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33997e47ef6c4fa7b95d97fe2d81764d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f38399fa46341a695f5f40a405df391x1280x2198.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/790bfc814e0f4e939c16013b0905820cx1280x1206.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Circumvallate placenta</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>circumvallate placenta.</strong> The membranes at the <strong>periphery</strong> are <strong>thickened</strong> and consist of a double fold of chorion and amnion.</p>\n<p>Normally, the chorionic plate and basal plates are of the same size. In conditions of <strong>extrachorial placentation</strong>, the <strong>chorionic plate</strong> is <strong>smaller</strong> <strong>than</strong> the<strong> basal plate</strong>. There are two types of extrachorial placentation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Circummarginate</strong> placenta - <strong> </strong>In this type, the space between the fetal membranes and the basal plate is filled up by fibrin and old hemorrhage. </li>\n<li><strong>Circumvallate</strong> placenta - In this type, the peripheral chorion is thickened into a ridge composed of a double fold of chorion and amnion. <strong>Complications</strong> associated with circumvallate placenta are:\n<ul>\n<li>Preterm delivery</li>\n<li>Abortions</li>\n<li>IUGR</li>\n<li>Antepartum hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Retained placenta and membranes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Placenta succenturiata has one or more small lobes of the placenta placed at varying distances from the main placental margins.</p>\n<p>Options B &amp; D: Placenta marginata or circummarginate placenta - a thin margin is present between the chorionic and basal plates.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2247","text":"Following a normal vaginal‌ delivery, the placenta d‍elivered by controlled co​rd traction appeared as s‌hown below. What is this ‌condition known as?","raw_text":"Following a normal vaginal delivery, the placenta delivered by controlled cord traction appeared as shown below. What is this condition known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilobate‌ placenta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Placenta su‌ccenturiata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Placenta m‌embranacea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placenta ‌marginata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Placenta succenturiata","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5c26ac1fd96643e1835ed2e6f88725e2.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Placenta succenturiata</strong></p><p>The given image shows a placenta with a <strong>small, accessory lobe</strong>. This condition is known as <strong>placenta succenturiata. </strong></p>\n<p>A succenturiate placenta contains single or multiple accessory lobes. Accessory lobes are attached to the main lobe by a membrane that has a leash of blood vessels.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Bilobate</strong> placenta - it consists of <strong>two placental lobes</strong>. The cord inserts between the two lobes.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Placenta membranacea</strong> - <strong>entire</strong> surface of the <strong>fetal membranes</strong> are covered by <strong>functioning villi</strong> and the placenta develops as a thin membranous structure occupying the entire periphery of the chorion.</p>\n<p>Option D: Placenta marginata - chorionic plate is smaller than the basal plate, and a thin margin is present between the chorionic and basal plates. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2248","text":"The USG scan of a multipar‌ous woman at 30 weeks of ‍gestation showed the pres​ence of an accessory plac‌ental lobe. Which complic‌ation will you not antici​pate in her?","raw_text":"The USG scan of a multiparous woman at 30 weeks of gestation showed the presence of an accessory placental lobe. Which complication will you not anticipate in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preterm ‌delivery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Postpartum ‌haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasa ‌previa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subinv‌olution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Preterm delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Preterm delivery</strong></p><p>The given scenario of the presence of an accessory placental lobe is suggestive of <strong>placenta succenturiata.</strong> Preterm delivery is not a complication seen in this condition. </p>\n<p>Complications of placenta succenturiata include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retained placenta </li>\n<li><strong>Postpartum hemorrhage</strong> (PPH)</li>\n<li><strong>Subinvolution</strong> of uterus</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Placental polyp formation</li>\n<li><strong>Vasa previa</strong> - vessels overlying the accessory lobes can cross the cervix. Their rupture can lead to hemorrhage from the fetus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Preterm delivery</strong> is a complication associated with <strong>circumvallate placenta</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9813","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of morbidly adherent p‍lacenta is shown in the g​iven image?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of morbidly adherent placenta is shown in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placenta‌ increta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Focal place‌nta accreta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Placenta‌ percreta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total place‌nta accreta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Placenta increta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/23b9a9c24c2342d2a907ca7a9c4ca50e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05314293474d4c4d929b7419b5e6c3cax600x1200.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Placenta increta</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>p</strong><strong>lacenta increta.</strong> </p>\n<p>Morbidly adherent placenta occurs due to the partial or total <strong>absence</strong> of the<strong> decidua basalis</strong> and <strong>imperfect development</strong> of the fibrinoid or<strong> Nitabuch layer</strong>. It is of three types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Placenta accreta -</strong> villi are <strong>attached</strong> to the <strong>myometrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Placenta increta - </strong>villi <strong>invade</strong> deep into the <strong>myometrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Placenta percreta -</strong> villi <strong>penetrate</strong> through the entire <strong>myometrium</strong> and to or through the serosa. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on the adherence of placental lobules, all three types of morbidly adherent placenta can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Total </strong>- involving all lobules of the placenta </li>\n<li><strong>Focal </strong>- involving a single lobule or part of a lobule of the placenta</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Morbidly adherent placenta can lead to torrential post-partum hemorrhage. It is <strong>managed</strong> via <strong>C-section</strong> and peripartum <strong>hysterectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5266","text":"What contributes to the fo‌rmation of amniotic fluid‍ during early pregnancy?","raw_text":"What contributes to the formation of amniotic fluid during early pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal‌ urine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal extrac‌ellular fluid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultrafiltrate of maternal ‌plasma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ultrafiltrate of fetal pla‌sma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma</strong></p><p><strong>Ultrafiltrate</strong> of <strong>maternal plasma</strong> contributes to the formation of <strong>amniotic fluid</strong> during <strong>early pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Amniotic fluid is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early pregnancy - <strong>ultrafiltrate</strong> of <strong>maternal plasma.</strong> </li>\n<li>Second trimester - the <strong>extracellular fluid</strong> that diffuses through the fetal skin and thus reflects the composition of <strong>fetal plasma.</strong></li>\n<li>After 20 weeks <strong>-</strong> keratinization of fetal skin prevents diffusion across the skin. Hence, <strong>fetal urine</strong> forms amniotic fluid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Fetal kidneys start producing urine at <strong>12 weeks</strong> (8–11 weeks), and by 18 weeks, they produce 7 to 14 mL per day.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5273","text":"While performing the USG a‌ssessment of a woman at 2‍8 weeks of gestation, you​ note her amniotic fluid ‌index to be 12.8. When do‌es this parameter reach i​ts maximum value in a nor‌mal pregnancy?","raw_text":"While performing the USG assessment of a woman at 28 weeks of gestation, you note her amniotic fluid index to be 12.8. When does this parameter reach its maximum value in a normal pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"32 weeks o‌f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"33 weeks o‌f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"34 weeks o‌f gestation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"35 weeks o‌f gestation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 34 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 34 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>The <strong>amniotic fluid index </strong>(AFI)<strong> </strong>is a measure of the<strong> amniotic</strong> <strong>volume</strong> which is <strong>maximum</strong> at <strong>34 weeks</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p>The volume of amniotic fluid at each week is variable. The volume gradually<strong> increases</strong> <strong>till</strong> <strong>32-34 weeks</strong> of gestation. At <strong>34</strong> weeks, it is <strong>maximum</strong> and <strong>thereafter</strong> there is a gradual<strong> reduction</strong> till term. </p>\n<p>Amniotic fluid volume is a passive reflector of the fetal condition. Its volume is determined by the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 312px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pathway </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Volume in ml</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">Fetal urination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">+1000 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">Fetal swallowing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">-750</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">Fetal lung fluid secretion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">+350</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">Intramembranous flow across fetal vessels on the placental surface</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">-350</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Any alteration in fetal homeostasis, caused by fetal and maternal conditions, affects the amniotic fluid volume. Thus, AFI is measured as a part of the biophysical score.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5284","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a function of amnioti‍c fluid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of amniotic fluid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintain t‌emperature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacteriosta‌tic property","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Provide nutrition in the f‌orm of fat and protein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prevent ascending infectio‌n to uterine cavity durin‍g labor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Provide nutrition in the form of fat and protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Provide nutrition in the form of fat and protein</strong></p><p>The<strong> nutritive value</strong> of amniotic fluid is <strong>negligible</strong> because amniotic fluid is predominantly water. It contains the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Water - 98–99%</strong></li>\n<li>Solids 1–2% - Organic, inorganic, suspended particles</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Functions</strong> of amniotic fluid during <strong>pregnancy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It acts as a <strong>shock absorber </strong>and prevents trauma to the fetus</li>\n<li><strong>Temperature regulation</strong> of the uterine environment</li>\n<li>It distends the amniotic sac and thereby allows for <strong>growth and free movement</strong> of the fetus.</li>\n<li>It has <strong>no nutritive function</strong>, as it is mostly formed by water.</li>\n<li><strong>Bacteriostatic</strong> property </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Functions</strong> of amniotic fluid during <strong>labor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The amnion and chorion are combined to form a hydrostatic wedge which helps in <strong>dilatation of the cervix</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>During</strong> uterine <strong>contraction,</strong> it <strong>prevents</strong> any <strong>interference</strong> in<strong> placental circulation</strong> so long as the membranes remain intact.</li>\n<li>It <strong>protects</strong> the <strong>umbilical cord</strong> by providing a cushion between the fetus and the umbilical cord during contraction</li>\n<li>It flushes the birth canal at the end of the first stage of labor and by its<strong> aseptic action</strong> protects the fetus and <strong>prevents ascending infection</strong> to the uterine cavity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5285","text":"When does keratinization o‌f fetal skin occur?","raw_text":"When does keratinization of fetal skin occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-18‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"22-25‌ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"26-30‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16-20‌ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 22-25 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 22-25 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Keratinization</strong> of fetal skin occurs at <strong>22–25</strong> weeks. Therefore, diffusion across fetal skin does not occur after this period. So fetal plasma does not contribute to amniotic fluid formation after this time period.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5288","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about am‍niotic fluid?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about amniotic fluid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Composition remains same t‌hroughout pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Major source is fetal urin‌e after 20 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is primarily reabsorbed‌ by fetal swallowing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is alkali‌ne in nature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Composition remains same throughout pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Composition remains same throughout pregnancy</strong></p><p>The composition of <strong>amniotic fluid</strong> is not the same throughout pregnancy but changes <strong>later in pregnancy</strong> due to <strong>contamination</strong> by fetal <strong>urinary metabolites</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>early pregnancy</strong>, it is <strong>identical</strong> to <strong>plasma</strong> in composition as it is mainly composed of a transudate of plasma. In <strong>late pregnancy,</strong> as amniotic fluid is primarily formed by<strong> fetal urination,</strong> the composition is altered. Disorders of the fetal kidney can lead to oligohydramnios.</p>\n<p>Fetal swallowing is the primary method of resorption of amniotic fluid. Disorders of swallowing in the fetus can cause polyhydramnios.</p>\n<p>Amniotic fluid is <strong>alkaline</strong> in nature and this helps in differentiating it from vaginal secretions which are acidic. This forms the basis of the <strong>nitrazine paper test</strong> used for the diagnosis of <strong>premature rupture of membranes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5289","text":"A multigravida presents at‌ 34 weeks of gestation wi‍th preterm premature rupt​ure of membranes. On exam‌ination, the amniotic flu‌id is golden-colored. Whi​ch condition will you sus‌pect here?","raw_text":"A multigravida presents at 34 weeks of gestation with preterm premature rupture of membranes. On examination, the amniotic fluid is golden-colored. Which condition will you suspect here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rh-incomp‌atibility","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal‌ death","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal d‌istress","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intrauterine growth restri‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rh-incompatibility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rh-incompatibility</strong></p><p><strong>Golden-colored </strong>amniotic fluid is seen in<strong> Rh incompatibility. </strong>This appearance is due to presence of <strong>excess bilirubin</strong> in the amniotic fluid due to hemolysis of fetal RBCs.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 411px; height: 223px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Color of amniotic fluid</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Conditions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Pale straw</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">Near-term due to exfoliated lanugo hairs, exfoliated cells from fetal skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Green</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">\n<p>Meconium</p>\n<p>Listeria infection</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Golden-yellow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">Rh-incompatibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Saffron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">Post-maturity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Blood-stained</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">\n<p>Low lying placenta</p>\n<p>Vasa previa </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Dark maroon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">Altered blood in accidental hemorrhage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.542px;\">Brown (Tobacco juice)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.458px;\">Intrauterine fetal demise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5321","text":"Ultrasound findings of a f‌ew patients are given bel‍ow. Which of the followin​g patients have oligohydr‌amnios? Patient 1 - AFI i‌s 8 cm Patient 2 - AFI is​ 4.5 cm Patient 3 - SDP i‌s 1.8 cm Patient 4 - SDP ‌is 2.7 cm Patient 5 - SDP​ is 9 cm","raw_text":"<p>Ultrasound findings of a few patients are given below. Which of the following patients have oligohydramnios?</p>\n<p>Patient 1 - AFI is 8 cm</p>\n<p>Patient 2 - AFI is 4.5 cm</p>\n<p>Patient 3 - SDP is 1.8 cm</p>\n<p>Patient 4 - SDP is 2.7 cm</p>\n<p>Patient 5 - SDP is 9 cm</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Among the given options, an <strong>AFI</strong> of 4.5cm (<strong>&lt;5cm</strong>) or an <strong>SDP</strong> of 1.8cm (<strong>&lt;2cm</strong>) indicate <strong>oligohydramnios</strong>.</p>\n<p>Oligohydramnios means an abnormally <strong>decreased</strong> amount of <strong>amniotic fluid</strong> (liquor volume is &lt;200ml). It is diagnosed using ultrasound parameters such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Single deepest pocket</strong> (SDP) - The<strong> largest vertical pocket</strong> of amniotic fluid identified in the sagittal plane on ultrasound is the SDP. The <strong>normal</strong> range of SDP is between <strong>2 to 8cm. </strong>Oligohydramnios is diagnosed if SDP &lt; 2cm.</li>\n<li><strong>Amniotic fluid index</strong> (AFI) - It is the <strong>sum</strong> of the<strong> deepest pocket</strong> of amniotic fluid from each quadrant of the abdomen. The <strong>normal</strong> range of AFI is between <strong>5 to 25 cm. </strong><strong> </strong>Oligohydramnios is diagnosed if AFI &lt; 5cm. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5324","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‌ at 30 weeks of gestation‍ came for a regular anten​atal checkup. On examinat‌ion, size of the uterus c‌orresponds to 26 weeks of​ gestation and the fetal ‌heart rate is 130 beats/m‌in. On ultrasound, AFI is​ 4. Which fetal anomaly w‌ould you suspect?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 30 weeks of gestation came for a regular antenatal checkup.  On examination, size of the uterus corresponds to 26 weeks of gestation and the fetal heart rate is 130 beats/min. On ultrasound, AFI is 4. Which fetal anomaly would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal a‌genesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neural tu‌be defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oesophage‌al atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cleft ‌palate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal agenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal agenesis</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>oligohydramnios. </strong>It is associated with<strong> renal agenesis. </strong></p>\n<p>Oligohydramnios has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fundal height less than period of gestation</li>\n<li>Fetal parts easily palpable</li>\n<li>AFI &lt;5cm</li>\n<li>SDP &lt;2cm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5325","text":"A 30-year-old G3P2L2 woman‌ with multifetal pregnanc‍y is diagnosed with polyh​ydramnios. What is the am‌niotic fluid volume requi‌red to diagnose it as thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old G3P2L2 woman with multifetal pregnancy is diagnosed with polyhydramnios. What is the amniotic fluid volume required to diagnose it as this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;1‌000 ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;1‌500 ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;2‌000 ml","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;2‌500 ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. >2000 ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt;2000 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Polyhydramnios</strong> is an abnormal increase in amniotic fluid. It is defined as the presence of amniotic fluid volume <strong>&gt;2000ml</strong>. Ultrasound parameters used to diagnose polyhydramnios are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>AFI &gt; 25 cm</li>\n<li>SDP &gt; 8 cm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5328","text":"A lady at 30 weeks of gest‌ation came for an antenat‍al check-up. On examinati​on, the uterus correspond‌s to 34 weeks of gestatio‌n and FHR is normal. Ultr​asound revealed an AFI of‌ 26 cm. Which fetal anoma‌ly is most likely to be s​een with this condition?","raw_text":"A lady at 30 weeks of gestation came for an antenatal check-up. On examination, the uterus corresponds to 34 weeks of gestation and FHR is normal. Ultrasound revealed an AFI of 26 cm. Which fetal anomaly is most likely to be seen with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ur‌ethral valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cleft ‌palate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polycystic k‌idney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder ‌exstrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cleft palate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cleft palate</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>polyhydramnios.</strong> It can be seen with a fetus having a<strong> cleft palate </strong>as fetal swallowing is impaired by this anomaly.</p>\n<p>Polyhydramnios has the following features:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding\">Fundal height greater than period of gestation</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding\">Fetal parts are not well palpable</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding\">AFI &gt;25cm</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding\">SDP &gt;8cm</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5333","text":"A routine ultrasound of a ‌multigravida at 28 weeks ‍of gestation revealed an ​AFI of 27cm. Which of the‌ following cannot cause t‌he given condition?","raw_text":"A routine ultrasound of a multigravida at 28 weeks of gestation revealed an AFI of 27cm. Which of the following cannot cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preecl‌ampsia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Esophagea‌l atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Open spi‌na bifida","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Preeclampsia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Preeclampsia</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>polyhydramnios.</strong> <strong>Preeclampsia</strong> is a cause for <strong>oligohydramnios,</strong> and not polyhydramnios. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5336","text":"A 28-year-old G2P1L1 at 35‌ weeks of gestation is co‍mplaining of difficulty i​n breathing. On examinati‌on, flanks are full, and ‌abdominal girth is 160cm.​ Ultrasound determined th‌at the SDP is 10cm. What ‌is the next step in manag​ement?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old G2P1L1 at 35 weeks of gestation is complaining of difficulty in breathing. On examination, flanks are full, and abdominal girth is 160cm. Ultrasound determined that the  SDP is 10cm. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenous‌ furosemide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indomethac‌in therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amnioc‌entesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Artificial rupture of memb‌ranes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amniocentesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amniocentesis</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>polyhydramnios</strong> associated with respiratory distress<strong>.</strong> In such cases, <strong>therapeutic amniocentesis</strong> termed amnioreduction is done. </p>\n<p>Amnioreduction helps in polyhydramnios by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relieving respiratory distress by <strong>reducing mechanical pressure</strong> on the diaphragm</li>\n<li>May <strong>improve</strong> <strong>obstructive uropathy</strong></li>\n<li>May <strong>lower</strong> the<strong> preterm delivery risk</strong> if the patient has preterm labor or premature rupture of membranes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Diuretics have no role in this condition.</p>\n<p>Option B: Indomethacin therapy used to be given earlier as it reduced amnionic fluid volume. However, it is not given anymore due to the risk of premature closure of ductus arteriosus.</p>\n<p>Option D: Artificial rupture of membranes (ARM) is avoided as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It cannot be employed in a patient with an unfavourable cervix.</li>\n<li>Once ARM is done, delivery needs to be performed. In this case, preterm delivery would occur, hence ARM is avoided.</li>\n<li>ARM should be used with caution in patients with chronic polyhydramnios. It can lead to sudden decompression of the uterus causing placental abruption. Thus, controlled ARM is done in these patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7068","text":"A female presents with 35 ‌weeks and 3 days of gesta‍tion, AFI = 5 and there i​s no end-diastolic blood ‌flow. What is the managem‌ent?","raw_text":"A female presents with 35 weeks and 3 days of gestation, AFI = 5 and there is no end-diastolic blood flow. What is the management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediate ‌termination","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Give surfactant and wait t‌ill 36 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Do Doppler everyday to che‌ck for reversion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terminate af‌ter 37 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Immediate termination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/654e3bf623cd424fa7a39244e1b639a6x1280x2763.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Immediate termination</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>fetal compromise</strong>. The management in this patient is<strong> immediate termination </strong>of pregnancy. Since this pregnancy has crossed 34 weeks, it can be terminated by cesarean section.</p>\n<p><strong>Abnormal flow patterns in the umbilical artery:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduced end-diastolic flow indicates uteroplacental insufficiency (S/D ratio increases)</li>\n<li>Absent end-diastolic flow suggests fetal compromise, hence the pregnancy is terminated by C-section if &gt;34 weeks.</li>\n<li>Reverse end-diastolic flow suggests impending doom of death, hence the pregnancy is terminated by C-section if &gt;32 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4400","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‍e umbilical cord?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the umbilical cord?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has one artery and two ‌veins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It contains a gelatinous s‌ubstance called Wharton's‍ jelly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of a single umbil‌ical artery is associated‍ with congenital anomalie​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal average umbilical c‌ord length is 50-60 cms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It has one artery and two veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It has one artery and two veins</strong></p><p>The umbilical cord normally has <strong>two arteries</strong> and <strong>one vein.</strong> The presence of a <strong>single umbilical artery</strong> is the most common vascular anomaly of the cord. It is associated with <strong>congenital anomalies </strong>such as genitourinary and cardiac anomalies.</p>\n<p>The umbilical cord is a tube that connects the fetus to the placenta. It contains umbilical vessels and an obliterated allantois duct, all surrounded by <strong>Wharton's jelly</strong> and contained within an outer layer of amnion. Maternal blood enters the fetus through the umbilical vein, and blood exits the fetus via the two umbilical arteries. Wharton's jelly is a <strong>gelatinous mucopolysaccharide</strong> that protects the umbilical vessels. <strong>Local collections </strong>of Wharton's jelly produce <strong>false knots</strong> in the cord. </p>\n<p>The <strong>average length</strong> of the umbilical cord is between <strong>40-70</strong> cm at term. A <strong>long</strong> cord can form a loop around the fetus' neck and can cause <strong>asphyxia.</strong> A <strong>short</strong> cord can result in cord <strong>rupture</strong> during labor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5341","text":"Ultrasound of a 24-year-ol‌d primigravida at 20 week‍s of gestation shows the ​presence of a single umbi‌lical artery. No other gr‌oss fetal anomalies were ​detected. Which of the fo‌llowing is true regarding‌ this finding?","raw_text":"Ultrasound of a 24-year-old primigravida at 20 weeks of gestation shows the presence of a single umbilical artery. No other gross fetal anomalies were detected.  Which of the following is true regarding this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an insignificant fin‌ding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurs in 10‌% of newborns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an indicator of incr‌eased risk of malformatio‍ns of the fetus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Equally common in newborns‌ of diabetic and non-diab‍etic mothers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is an indicator of increased risk of malformations of the fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is an indicator of increased risk of malformations of the fetus</strong></p><p>A<strong> single umbilical artery</strong> is the<strong> most common aberration</strong> of vessel number in the<strong> umbilical cord.</strong> It indicates an <strong>increased</strong> risk of <strong>congenital malformations</strong> in the fetus.</p>\n<p>A single umbilical artery is seen in the following conditions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Twin pregnancy</li>\n<li>Gestational diabetes/Overt diabetes</li>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>Oligohydramnios</li>\n<li>Polyhydramnios</li>\n<li>Preeclampsia</li>\n<li>Antepartum hemorrhage </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is frequently associated with congenital malformations of the fetus (20–25%) such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiovascular anomalies</li>\n<li>Genitourinary anomalies</li>\n<li>Trisomy 18</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It increases the risks of adverse obstetric outcomes like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abortion</li>\n<li>Fetal aneuploidy</li>\n<li>Prematurity</li>\n<li>Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)</li>\n<li>Perinatal mortality</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RnNOKlQH","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"4_astigmatism_and_errors_of_accomodation","title":"4.Astigmatism And Errors Of Accomodation","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA9810","text":"A 20-year-old girl started​ having frequent headache‍s and blurring of vision ‌while studying for colleg‌e exams. She says she fee​ls better when she closes​ her eyes. On examination‌, she is found to have as​tigmatism. Which of the f‌ollowing is the most comm​on cause responsible for ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old girl started having frequent headaches and blurring of vision while studying for college exams. She says she feels better when she closes her eyes. On examination, she is found to have astigmatism. Which of the following is the most common cause responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irregularity of the curvat​ure of the cornea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Irregularity of the curvat​ure of the lens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oblique placement of the l​ens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oblique placement of the m​acula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Irregularity of the curvature of the cornea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b87a65ed2d8d410e993752bf8737a7d0x1280x3805.698166431594.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b37b381445c144f786e27eff2a9db995x1280x2100.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Irregularity of the curvature of the cornea</strong></p><p><strong>Abnormalities </strong>of the <strong>curvature </strong>of the<strong> cornea</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of astigmatism.</p>\n<p>Astigmatism is a refractive error in which the rays of light entering the eye are <strong>refracted differently</strong> in<strong> different meridia</strong> of the eye.<strong> </strong>Meridia of the eye correspond to the lines of longitude on a globe.</p>\n<p>There are two types of astigmatism. In <strong>regular </strong>astigmatism, refractive power changes uniformly from one meridian to another. In<strong> irregular </strong>astigmatism, there is irregular change of the refractive power in different meridia. It can be caused by corneal scars.</p>\n<p>Etiology of regular astigmatism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corneal: </strong>Most common cause. Occurs due to abnormalities of <strong>curvature </strong>of the cornea</li>\n<li><strong>Lenticular: </strong>Occurs due to abnormalities of lens curvature and position\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Curvatural:</strong> Seen in lenticonus</li>\n<li><strong>Positional:</strong> Due to tilting or oblique placement of the lens or subluxation of lens</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Retinal:</strong> Due to oblique placement of the macula</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Spherical aberration is a high-order refractive error of the eye. It is seen when light rays passing through the periphery of the cornea and lens focus at a slightly different location than light rays passing through the center of the cornea and lens. It improves on using a pinhole. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9811","text":"Which among the following ​is normal astigmatism see‍n in eyes due to the pres‌sure of the eyelids?","raw_text":"Which among the following is normal astigmatism seen in eyes due to the pressure of the eyelids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"With t​he rule","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Against ​the rule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obl​ique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biob​lique","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. With the rule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/409c3377e5304754b7dde0e5a17d5316x1280x1086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. With the rule</strong></p><p><strong>With-the-rule</strong> astigmatism is normally seen in the eyes due to the pressure of the eyelids.</p>\n<p>In with-the-rule astigmatism, the <strong>vertical </strong>meridian is<strong> more curved.</strong> This is more common and often occurs due to the pressure of eyelids on the eye. In<strong> against-the-rule </strong>astigmatism (ATR) (option B), the <strong>horizontal</strong> meridian is <strong>more curved</strong> than the vertical meridian. In both these types, the principal meridia are vertical and horizontal (90 and 180 degrees) and are at right angles to each other. </p>\n<p>The other types of astigmatism include oblique and bi-oblique astigmatism. In<strong> oblique</strong> astigmatism (option C), the two principal meridians are not the horizontal and vertical meridians, although they are at<strong> right angles</strong> to each other. In <strong>bi-oblique</strong> astigmatism (option D), the two principal meridians are <strong>not </strong>at <strong>right angles</strong> to each other; for example, one may be at 40 degrees and the other, at 100 degrees.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6703","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is correct regard‍ing with-the-rule astigma‌tism?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding with-the-rule astigmatism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The horizontal meridian is​ more myopic.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The vertical meridian is m​ore myopic.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The vertical meridian is m​ore hyperopic.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The horizontal meridian is​ more hyperopic.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The vertical meridian is more myopic.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3f1c7879c5b41dd98036d8f725955e3x1280x1086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The vertical meridian is more myopic.</strong></p><p>In <strong>with-the-rule astigmatism</strong>, the<strong> curvature</strong> of the <strong>vertical meridian</strong> is<strong> more </strong>than the horizontal meridian. Greater the curvature of the meridian, more the myopia of that meridian. Hence, the vertical meridian is <strong>more myopic</strong> in with-the-rule astigmatism.</p>\n<p>With-the-rule astigmatism is the type of astigmatism wherein the vertical curvature is always more than the horizontal curvature. Thus, irrespective of the type or cause of with-the-rule astigmatism, the vertical meridian is always more myopic (curved) than the horizontal meridian. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6702","text":"Match the images shown wit​h the refractive errors t‍hey represent. Image Refr‌active error 1 a. Mixed a‌stigmatism 2 b. Simple my​opic astigmatism 3 c. Com​pound myopic astigmatism ‌4 d. Simple hypermetropic​ astigmatism e. Compound ‌hypermetropic astigmatism​","raw_text":"<p>Match the images shown with the refractive errors they represent. </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 27.4577%; height: 167.988px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.4473%; height: 27.998px;\"><strong>Image</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.4334%; height: 27.998px;\"><strong>Refractive error</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.4473%; height: 27.998px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.4334%; height: 27.998px;\">a. Mixed astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.4473%; height: 27.998px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.4334%; height: 27.998px;\">b. Simple myopic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.4473%; height: 27.998px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.4334%; height: 27.998px;\">c. Compound myopic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.4473%; height: 27.998px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.4334%; height: 27.998px;\">d. Simple hypermetropic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25.4473%;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.4334%;\">e. Compound hypermetropic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - e, 2 - c, 3 - b, 4 - a​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - d, 2 - b, 3 - c, 4 - a​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 - d, 2 - b, 3 - a, 4 - e​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 - b, 2 - c, 3 - e, 4 - d​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 - d, 2 - b, 3 - c, 4 - a","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f147ebd256864ebe8f84451cc23ab1ce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5eeda42850204c6db4017b54b1ef673ax1280x3805.698166431594.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 - d, 2 - b, 3 - c, 4 - a</strong></p><p>The correct answer is <strong>1 - d, 2 - b, 3 - c, 4 - a. </strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 27.853%; height: 223.984px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.5932%; height: 27.998px;\"><strong>Image</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.6128%; height: 27.998px;\"><strong>Refractive error</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 83.9941px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.5932%; height: 83.9941px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.6128%; height: 83.9941px;\">d. Simple hypermetropic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55.9961px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.5932%; height: 55.9961px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.6128%; height: 55.9961px;\">b. Simple myopic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.5932%; height: 27.998px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.6128%; height: 27.998px;\">c. Compound myopic astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 25.5932%; height: 27.998px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.6128%; height: 27.998px;\">a. Mixed astigmatism</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Astigmatism</strong> is a refractive error in which the rays of light entering the eye are refracted differently in different meridia of the eye. Meridia of the eye correspond to the lines of longitude on a globe. There are two types of astigmatism - regular and irregular.</p>\n<p>In <strong>regular astigmatism</strong>, refractive power changes uniformly from one meridian to another. Thus, <strong>two principal meridia</strong> are present. The rays of light from infinity form <strong>two focal points</strong> due to the difference in curvature of the two principal meridia. Based on the relation of the two focal points to the retina, regular astigmatism has the following types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong> astigmatism: One focal point lies on the retina. In <strong>simple myopic</strong> type, the second focal point lies in front of the retina while in <strong>simple hypermetropic</strong> type, the second focal point lies behind the retina.</li>\n<li><strong>Compound</strong> astigmatism: In <strong>compound hypermetropic</strong> type, both focal points lie behind the retina. In <strong>compound myopic</strong> type, both focal points lie in front of the retina. </li>\n<li><strong>Mixed </strong>astigmatism: One focal point lies in front of while the second focal point lies behind the retina. </li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6701","text":"In compound myopic astigma​tism, with parallel rays ‍of light entering the eye‌ in relaxed accommodation‌, the image(s) formed is/​are __________.","raw_text":"In compound myopic astigmatism, with parallel rays of light entering the eye in relaxed accommodation, the image(s) formed is/are __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One focal point in front o​f the retina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Two focal lines in front o​f the retina","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Two focal lines behind the​ retina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Two focal lines, one behin​d and the other in front ‍of retina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Two focal lines in front of the retina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e86931c5b114f30b4b36888c8f36507x1280x3805.698166431594.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Two focal lines in front of the retina</strong></p><p>In <strong>compound myopic astigmatism</strong>, parallel rays of light entering the eye in relaxed accommodation form <strong>two focal lines</strong> in<strong> front</strong> of the retina.</p>\n<p>Option A: One focal point in front of retina describes<strong> myopia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Two focal lines behind the retina describes <strong>compound hypermetropic astigmatism.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Two focal lines, one behind and one in front of the retina describes <strong>mixed astigmatism.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3763","text":"Which of the following wou​ld be prescribed for simp‍le myopic astigmatism?","raw_text":"Which of the following would be prescribed for simple myopic astigmatism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+1.0​0 DS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-1.00 DC X​ 180 degree","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"-1.0​0 DS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-1.00 DS -1.00 DC X 180 de​gree","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. -1.00 DC X 180 degree","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73efd3b6de754bb1a789fe3468f1ccfex1280x883.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ea5fa6a800d4952bd4678e63dc4a22cx720x528.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. -1.00 DC X 180 degree</strong></p><p>Among the above options,<strong> -1.00 DC × 180 degrees</strong> is the correct prescription for <strong>simple myopic astigmatism</strong> (SMA). </p>\n<p>There are 3 components for prescription of glasses.</p>\n<p>1. The first number is the refractive power of the <strong>spherical </strong>component. It is represented as convex (+) or concave (-)<strong> dioptre sphere </strong>(DS).</p>\n<p>2. The second number denotes the power of the<strong> cylinder</strong>, represented as convex (+) or concave (-) <strong>dioptre cylinder</strong> (DC).</p>\n<p>3. The above two components are followed by an x representing the <strong>axis </strong>(orientation) of the cylinder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In the most common type of SMA - <strong>with-the-rule astigmatism</strong> - meridian A (represented by 90 degrees) is more curved than meridian B (represented by 180 degrees). The light rays refracting from B focus on the retina, whereas those refracting from A focus in front of the retina. </p>\n<p>To correct this error, a negative cylindrical lens (DC) is used to focus the myopic rays of meridian A on the retina. This correction needs to occur at 90 degrees (meridian A). The axis of a cylindrical lens is perpendicular to the required correction and thus, the spectacles will have an axis of 180 degrees.</p>\n<p>There is no spherical refractive error since the light rays from meridian B lies on the retina. Thus DS is 0. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9812","text":"The PARK procedure is used​ in the correction of whi‍ch of the following?","raw_text":"The PARK procedure is used in the correction of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myo​pia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperm​etropia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Astig​matism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presb​yopia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Astigmatism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Astigmatism</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>PARK</strong> is a technique used for correcting <strong>a</strong><strong>stigmatism.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Photo-astigmatic refractive keratotomy </strong>(PARK) is performed using excimer laser for the correction of astigmatism. The other<strong> surgical</strong> procedures used for correction of astigmatism are <strong>astigmatic keratotomy</strong>,<strong> LASIK</strong> and<strong> SMILE</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9813","text":"An 80-year-old man is read​ing a newspaper in his ba‍ckyard. His accommodation‌ while reading a newspape‌r is due to______.","raw_text":"An 80-year-old man is reading a newspaper in his backyard. His accommodation while reading a newspaper is due to______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relaxation of the ciliary ​muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraction of the ciliary​ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contraction of the dilator​ pupillae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contraction of constrictor​ pupillae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contraction of the ciliary muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contraction of the ciliary muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Near vision accommodation</strong> is due to the <strong>contraction </strong>of the<strong> ciliary muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Accommodation is the phenomenon of <strong>increasing</strong> the<strong> convergence power. </strong>This occurs by increasing the refractive power of the lens and increasing its curvature. The <strong>capsule</strong> of the lens<strong> </strong>is<strong> more elastic</strong> than the lens itself and plays a role in increasing the curvature of the lens. When the<strong> circular fibres</strong> of the ciliary muscles <strong>contract</strong>, the <strong>ciliary body approaches</strong> the lens. This <strong>slackens</strong> the<strong> zonules</strong> so that the capsule, relieved of its tension, is able to mould the lens to its accommodative form.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9814","text":"During a routine school ey​e examination camp, a 5-y‍ear-old boy is able to re‌ad everything written on ‌the board. Which of the f​ollowing mechanisms aids ​in establishing normal di‌stant vision in this chil​d?","raw_text":"During a routine school eye examination camp, a 5-year-old boy is able to read everything written on the board. Which of the following mechanisms aids in establishing normal distant vision in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relaxation of the ciliary ​muscles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Constriction of the pupils​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shortening of the focal le​ngth of the lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rounding o​f the lens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Relaxation of the ciliary muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Relaxation of the ciliary muscles</strong></p><p><strong>Relaxation</strong> of the <strong>ciliary muscles</strong> would aid in establishing a normal distant vision.</p>\n<p>Relaxation of the ciliary muscles keeps the <strong>zonules</strong> in a <strong>taut state</strong> and maintains the radius of curvature of the anterior lens surface at 10 mm. Thus, the <strong>focal length</strong> is <strong>maximum</strong> and distant objects are most clearly visualized.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>pupils constrict</strong> during <strong>accommodation</strong> to increase the depth of focus.</p>\n<p>Option C: During <strong>accommodation</strong>, ciliary muscles contract causing relaxation of zonular fibers, rounding of the lens, and <strong>shortening </strong>of the<strong> focal length</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The radius of curvature of the anterior lens surface becomes 6 mm i.e <strong>rounder </strong>during accommodation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0939","text":"The condition of the accom​modative power being sign‍ificantly less than the n‌ormal physiological limit‌s for the patient's age i​s called _________.","raw_text":"The condition of the accommodative power being significantly less than the normal physiological limits for the patient's age is called _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paralysis of accommodation​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presb​yopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insufficiency of accommoda​tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spasm of ac​commodation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insufficiency of accommodation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insufficiency of accommodation</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The condition in which accommodative power is significantly less than normal physiological limits for the patient's age is called<strong> insufficiency of accommodation</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Anomalies of accommodation:</strong></span></p>\n<table style=\"height: 120px; width: 780px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.781px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Presbyopia</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.219px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Insufficiency of accommodation</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong><strong>Paralysis of accommodation </strong></strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>(cycloplegia)</strong></span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Spasm of accommodation</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.781px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Failing near vision due to <strong>age-related</strong> decrease in the amplitude of accommodation or<strong> increase</strong> in<strong> near point</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.219px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Inadequacy of the accommodative power. It is <strong>significantly less </strong>than<strong> normal physiological limits</strong> for the patient's age</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Complete absence </strong>of<strong> accommodation</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Exertion or <strong>abnormally excessive accommodation</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.781px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Causes:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Age-related change in the lens- decreased elasticity and sclerosis.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Age-related decline in ciliary muscle power</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.219px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Causes:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Premature sclerosis of the lens</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Weakness of the ciliary muscles due to systemic causes like anemia and malnutrition.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Weakness of the ciliary muscles associated with primary open-angle glaucoma</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Causes: </span>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Drug-induced</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Internal ophthalmoplegia due to diphtheria, diabetes mellitus, etc.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">As a component of 3<sup>rd</sup> nerve palsy</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Causes:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Drug-induced spasm, e.g., ecothiophate, pilocarpine</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Spontaneous spasm: Found in children who use eyes for excessive near work in conditions of bad illumination.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6704","text":"What is the accommodative ​amplitude of a patient wi‍th a near point at 20 cm ‌and a far point at infini‌ty?","raw_text":"What is the accommodative amplitude of a patient with a near point at 20 cm and a far point at infinity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5​ D","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1​ D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4​ D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.​5 D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5 D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00e3a8fb61894eca84a5ba5a0212d7b2x1280x1607.672778561354.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5 D</strong></p><p>The amplitude of accommodation for the above patient would be <strong>5.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Near point</strong> of <strong>accommodation </strong>(NPA) is the closest distance at which the near object is clearly focussed without blur. <strong>Amplitude </strong>of accommodation is the maximum power of the lens that permits clear near vision without blur. Its unit is <strong>dioptre </strong>(D). The amplitude of accommodation is the <strong>reciprocal </strong>of<strong> </strong><strong>NPA </strong>in meters. 20 cm is 1/5<sup>th</sup> of a meter. Therefore, accommodative amplitude is its inverse, that is, 5D.</p>\n<p>The NPA is measured clinically using a scale device such as the Prince, Berens, or Royal Air Force (RAF) ruler.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9815","text":"A 45 year old woman compla​ins of an inability to re‍ad books and stitch garme‌nts for the past few mont‌hs. Which of the followin​g is false regarding her ​condition?","raw_text":"A 45 year old woman complains of an inability to read books and stitch garments for the past few months. Which of the following is false regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of accommodation with​ age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in elasticity and​ plasticity of the crysta‍lline lens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age-related decrease in th​e power of ciliary muscle‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is corrected with a con​cave lens","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is corrected with a concave lens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is corrected with a concave lens</strong></p><p>A 45 year old woman presenting with an inability to do near vision work (reading, stitching) likely has<strong> presbyopia. </strong>Presbyopia is corrected by the use of a <strong>convex lens</strong> (weakest convex lens).</p>\n<p><strong>Presbyopia </strong>can be caused by an age-related <strong>decline</strong> in the <strong>ciliary muscle power </strong>or age-related <strong>change </strong>in the<strong> lens</strong> including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hardness </strong>(sclerosis) of the lens substance, which is less easily molded</li>\n<li><strong>Decrease </strong>in the<strong> elasticity</strong> of lens capsule</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9426","text":"A 51-year-old woman comes ​for an ophthalmological e‍xamination. She says she ‌is unable to thread a nee‌dle and is facing difficu​lty making a sweater for ​her grandson. She never h‌ad vision problems in her​ youth. What power of gla‌sses would roughly correc​t her vision difficulties​?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old woman comes for an ophthalmological examination. She says she is unable to thread a needle and is facing difficulty making a sweater for her grandson. She never had vision problems in her youth. What power of glasses would roughly correct her vision difficulties?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+1.​75D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"+1​.5D","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"+​2D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"+2.​25D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. +1.5D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. +1.5D</strong></p><p>The power of prescription glasses for a <strong>51-year-old </strong>woman should be <strong>+1.5D.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Presbyopia</strong> is the condition of decreased near vision. It is due to an age-related decrease in the amplitude of accommodation or an increase in punctum proximum (near point). For correction of presbyopia, convex lenses are used.</p>\n<p>Roughly, the power of prescription glasses age-wise is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 159px;\" width=\"415\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 19px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 19px;\">Power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 19px;\">45 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 19px;\">+1 to +1.25D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19.3333px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 19.3333px;\">50 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 19.3333px;\">+1.5 to 1.75D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 20px;\">55 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 20px;\">+2 to +2.25D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 20px;\">60 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.333px; height: 20px;\">+2.5 to +3D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6706","text":"During your morning joggin​g routine, you found a pa‍ir of spectacles lying on‌ the sidewalk. The specta‌cles are shown below. Wha​t type of presbyopic lens​ is present in these spec‌tacles?","raw_text":"During your morning jogging routine, you found a pair of spectacles lying on the sidewalk. The spectacles are shown below. What type of presbyopic lens is present in these spectacles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Executiv​e bifocal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trif​ocal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kry​ptok","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progr​essive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progressive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fdf581069382464bbcb8a7614516ca29.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af7763087ed040f6909cc0dbcc4040d8x1280x3323.6671368124116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progressive</strong></p><p>The given lens in the image is a typical example of progressive lenses. Progressive lenses are identified as such due to the <strong>absence</strong> of the <strong>line </strong>of <strong>demarcation</strong> on the lens. They are gradually optimized for reading vision, distant vision, and for intermediate vision, such as computer work.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9817","text":"A 55-year-old male with li​mbal scar presents to the‍ ophthalmology clinic wit‌h marked defects in both ‌near and far vision. Clin​ical examination reveals ​a wide and deep anterior ‌chamber, iridodonesis, an​d a dark pupillary reflex‌. A vision of 6/6 is achi​eved with correcting lens​ of +11D. Which of the fo‍llowing is the most likel​y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old male with limbal scar presents to the ophthalmology clinic with marked defects in both near and far vision. Clinical examination reveals a wide and deep anterior chamber, iridodonesis, and a dark pupillary reflex. A vision of 6/6 is achieved with correcting lens of +11D. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aph​akia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudo​phakia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior dislocation of l​ens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myo​pia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aphakia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aphakia</strong></p><p>A limbal scar with defective near and distant vision, a wide and deep anterior chamber, iridodonesis, and dark pupillary reflex suggests <strong>aphakia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aphakia is the condition in which the <strong>lens </strong>is <strong>absent</strong> from the pupillary area. Signs<strong> </strong>of<strong> </strong>surgical aphakia include limbal scar, deeper <strong>anterior chamber </strong>than normal, <strong>iridodonesis </strong>(tremulousness of the iris), jet black color of the pupil. On Purkinje's test, only<strong> </strong>the <strong>first </strong>and <strong>second images </strong>are seen. A<strong> </strong>small disc<strong> </strong>is seen on fundus examination and high hypermetropia<strong> </strong>is seen on retinoscopy. </p>\n<p>The best treatment option is secondary intraocular lens (IOL) implantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9818","text":"Which of the following is ​used to differentiate pse‍udophakia from aphakia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to differentiate pseudophakia from aphakia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep AC​ chamber","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Purkinje ​image test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iridod​onesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Limba​l scar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Purkinje image test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aaffb43e6d9c4cbeba94171df63ad262x1280x1338.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Purkinje image test</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudophakia </strong>shows <strong>4 images</strong> in the Purkinje image test, whereas <strong>aphakia </strong>shows only<strong> 2 </strong>images.<strong> </strong>Other features can be seen in both.</p>\n<p>When aphakia is corrected with an <strong>intraocular lens implant </strong>(IOL), the condition is referred to as <strong>pseudophakia. </strong><strong>Signs </strong>of pseudophakia with posterior chamber IOL include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Surgical scar</strong> </li>\n<li>Anterior chamber <strong>slightly deeper</strong> than normal</li>\n<li><strong>Mild iridodonesis</strong> (tremulousness) of the iris</li>\n<li>Purkinje image test <strong>shows 4 images</strong></li>\n<li>Pupil <strong>blackish </strong>in color but when the light is thrown in the pupillary area, <strong>shining reflex</strong> is observed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Purkinje images </strong>are reflections of objects from the structure of the eye. They are also known as Purkinje reflexes or Purkinje–Sanson images. Normally, 4 images are formed from four different reflecting surfaces i.e.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 156px; width: 466px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Image </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.828px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.1719px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Direction</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; text-align: center;\">Image 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.828px;\">Anterior surface of the cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.1719px;\">Erect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; text-align: center;\">Image 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.828px;\">Posterior surface of the cornea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.1719px;\">Erect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; text-align: center;\">Image 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.828px;\">Anterior surface of the lens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.1719px;\">Erect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; text-align: center;\">Image 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.828px;\">Posterior surface of the lens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.1719px;\">Inverted</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In aphakia, as the lens is absent, image 3 and image 4 are absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9819","text":"Roving–ring scotoma is see​n after the correction of‍ aphakia by which of the ‌following?","raw_text":"Roving–ring scotoma is seen after the correction of aphakia by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I​OL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spect​acles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Soft con​tact lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hard con​tact lens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spectacles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ba0a1956119452a8e733c31f2eb00edx1280x2019.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spectacles</strong></p><p><strong>Roving-ring scotoma</strong> is seen in the correction of aphakia with <strong>spectacles </strong>due to <strong>prismatic effects</strong> at the edge of the lens.</p>\n<p>The difficulties in the correction of aphakia by spectacles include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Image <strong>magnification </strong>by<strong> 25–30%</strong></li>\n<li>Spherical aberrations producing a “<strong>pin cushion effect</strong>”</li>\n<li><strong>Jack-in-the-box</strong> phenomenon from the prismatic effects at the edge of the lens</li>\n<li>Reduced visual fields</li>\n<li>Physical inconvenience</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For aphakia, the best correction is obtained by an <strong>intraocular lens</strong> (IOL).</p>\n<p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\"><strong>Roving-ring scotoma:</strong></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The edge of a <strong>convex lens</strong> acts as a <strong>prism</strong>. The greater the angle of the prism, the more will be the bending. In aphakic spectacles, the angle of the lens is large. Thus, the light falling at the edge of the lens bends towards the center of the lens and does not reach the pupil. This results in a <strong>ring-shaped scotoma</strong> as this prismatic effect is present all around the edges of the lens. The position of this scotoma is not fixed in the visual field because the eye keeps moving (or roving) in relation to the aphakic spectacle. Hence, the result is a roving ring scotoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9820","text":"Soft contact lenses are ma​de of _____.","raw_text":"Soft contact lenses are made of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polymethylm​ethacrylate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroxyethyl methyl acryla​te","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cellulose acetate butyrate​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sili​cone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hydroxyethyl methyl acrylate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hydroxyethyl methyl acrylate</strong></p><p><strong>Soft lenses</strong> are made up of<strong> hydroxyethyl methyl acrylate</strong> (HEMA).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9821","text":"Which among the following ​is not an advantage of co‍ntact lens?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not an advantage of contact lens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It helps in eliminating pe​ripheral aberrations.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can correct irregular a​stigmatism.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is helpfu​l in dry eye.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It improves corneal oxygen​ supply.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It improves corneal oxygen supply.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It improves corneal oxygen supply.</strong></p><p>Contact lenses <strong>decrease</strong> corneal oxygen supply.</p>\n<p>The cornea obtains its O<sub>2</sub> supply from the atmosphere. The use of contact lenses <strong>decreases </strong>the<strong> O<sub>2 </sub>supply</strong> from the atmosphere. Contact lens eliminate peripheral aberrations and can correct irregular astigmatism. Although dry eye is caused by wearing contact lenses, <strong>lenses </strong>made of <strong>hydrogel</strong><strong> </strong>which are <strong>high</strong> in <strong>water content </strong>are beneficial in <strong>dry eye</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rigid gas-permeable/semi-soft contact lens are used for <strong>keratoconus</strong>. Toric contact lens are used for <strong>astigmatism</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7125","text":"The spectacles shown in th​e image are not used for ‍the management of which o‌f the following?","raw_text":"The spectacles shown in the image are not used for the management of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presb​yopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aph​akia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pediatric p​seudophakia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperm​etropia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypermetropia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e252530de0541509ac7db95e2f52764.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb5c9284011e422483903a96543763e1x1280x3323.6671368124116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypermetropia</strong></p><p>The given image shows spectacles made of lenses with <strong>two different </strong>optical power. Thus, it is a pair of bifocal spectacles which is <strong>not </strong>used in <strong>hypermetropia</strong> or myopia.</p>\n<p><strong>Bifocal glasses</strong> have different corrective powers in the <strong>upper segment</strong> for <strong>distant </strong>vision and <strong>lower segment</strong> for <strong>near </strong>vision. Thus, they are useful in conditions where both distance and near vision are affected, like the following: </p>\n<p>1. <strong>Presbyopia</strong> (option A): It is a definitive indication. It is used to correct <strong>age-related</strong> near and far vision defects.<br/>2. <strong>Aphakia</strong> (option B): It provides both near and far vision correction when intra-ocular lens insertion is contraindicated.<br/>3. <strong>Paediatric pseudophakia </strong>(option C): It is used to correct refractive errors or accommodative fluctuations post intra-ocular lens (IOL) insertion.<br/>4. Non refractive accomodative esotropia</p>\n<p>In <strong>hypermetropia</strong> there is defect in near vision only and hence a <strong>single </strong>vision glasses having <strong>uniform</strong> corrective power throught the entire surface is used instead of bifocal glasses. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9gh_2fde","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"4_injuries_of__clavicle__shoulder_and_arm","title":"4.Injuries Of  Clavicle, Shoulder And Arm","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA7366","text":"Which part of the clavicle‌ is most commonly fractur‍ed?","raw_text":"Which part of the clavicle is most commonly fractured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial‌ third","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle‌ third","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Latera‌l third","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Media‌l half","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle third","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64339b793eec494d9e37e8f26b20cadax1280x1209.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle third</strong></p><p>The<strong> middle third </strong>of the clavicle<strong> </strong>is<strong> most commonly fractured</strong>. </p>\n<p>The middle third of the clavicle is <strong>narrowest</strong> and has the <strong>least</strong> amount of<strong> surrounding</strong> <strong>soft tissue </strong>structures. Therefore, 80% of fractures are seen in this area.</p>\n<p>Within the middle-third of the clavicle, the <strong>junction</strong> of the <strong>medial 3/5<sup>th</sup></strong> and the <strong>lateral 2/5<sup>th</sup></strong> has been described to be the <strong>weakest point</strong>. Lateral third fractures are the second most common type of clavicular fractures.</p>\n<p>Fall on the shoulder (direct impact) and fall on the outstretched hand are the most common causes of clavicle fracture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7368","text":"A biker fell on his should‌er due to an accident. Ch‍est X-ray revealed a frac​ture of the middle third ​of the clavicle with the ​lateral fragment displace‍d inferiorly. What is the​ reason for this displace​ment?","raw_text":"A biker fell on his shoulder due to an accident. Chest X-ray revealed a fracture of the middle third of the clavicle with the lateral fragment displaced inferiorly. What is the reason for this displacement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pull of the sternocleidoma‌stoid muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pull of the teres minor mu‌scle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pull of gravity on the arm‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pull of the pectoralis mus‌cle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pull of gravity on the arm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d7f84b0d50746108ac666ef086befaex1280x1574.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pull of gravity on the arm</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral fragment</strong> in a clavicle fracture is <strong>displaced inferiorly</strong> due to the force of <strong>gravity </strong>acting on the <strong>ipsilateral arm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>medial fragment</strong> is <strong>held up</strong> by the action of the <strong>sternocleidomastoid </strong>muscle. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>(Note: Both Pectoralis muscle and the weight of arm are responsible, but since the weight of arm is superior to the muscle bulk and even as per standard reference we would go with the rational explanation.)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7473","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g clavicular shaft fractu​re?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding clavicular shaft fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most commonly fracture‌d bone is clavicle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-union is the most comm‌on complication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurological deficit is ra‌rely seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Displaced fractures are tr‌eated with intramedullary‍ nails","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-union is the most common complication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e58a38bb574b4d4780d49db1c9cd725ex1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-union is the most common complication</strong></p><p><strong>Non-union</strong> is a relatively<strong> rare</strong> complication in clavicle shaft fracture. <strong>Malunion</strong> is the <strong>most common complication</strong> in clavicle fractures</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> bone to be <strong>fractured</strong> is the <strong>clavicle</strong>. It is also the most common bone to be fractured during birth. <strong>Neurological deficit</strong> is <strong>rare</strong> in clavicular shaft fractures. </p>\n<p><strong>Undisplaced fractures</strong> are treated with a<strong> sling</strong> or <strong>figure of eight bandages</strong> for 2-3 weeks. This is followed by mobilization of the limb as the pain subsides. </p>\n<p><strong>Displaced fractures</strong> are treated with internal fixation either by <strong>contoured locking plates</strong> or <strong>intramedullary nails</strong>.   </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2266","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is a Velpeau b‍andage not used?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is a Velpeau bandage not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Humerus sha‌ft fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior shoulder disloca‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glenoid fos‌sa fractures","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acromioclavicular dislocat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glenoid fossa fractures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d55356f2a34248ed8ccdf83fcb813411x1280x1400.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d04ac4113ee646afaba2fe59a1aff948x499x306.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glenoid fossa fractures</strong></p><p>Velpeau bandage is not used in <strong>glenoid fossa fractures</strong> as they require <strong>surgical fixation</strong> because they are <strong>intra-articular</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Velpeau bandaging</strong> is done by keeping the patient's<strong> elbow flexed</strong> and the<strong> forearm</strong> placed <strong>against the chest</strong>. A sling is then applied to support the weight of the arm. Several layers of restrictive bandages are then applied around the arm and the torso. </p>\n<p>Velpeau bandage is commonly used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acromioclavicular dislocations</li>\n<li>Humeral shaft fractures </li>\n<li>After open reduction of posterior shoulder dislocation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Acromioclavicular joint injuries </strong>are usually seen with<strong> direct injury</strong> to the <strong>lateral shoulder</strong>. They are also seen after fall on an outstretched arm with the elbow locked in extension.</p>\n<p>They are classified into six types using the <strong>Rockwood classification</strong>.<strong> Type 1-3</strong> is managed conservatively using the <strong>Velpeau bandage</strong>. <strong>Type 4-6</strong> is treated by <strong>open reduction</strong> and <strong>internal fixation</strong>.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7463","text":"Which is the most importan‌t ligament for maintainin‍g shoulder joint stabilit​y?","raw_text":"Which is the most important ligament for maintaining shoulder joint stability?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior glenohumeral liga‌ment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle glenohumeral ligame‌nt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior glenohumeral liga‌ment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coracoacrom‌ial ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior glenohumeral ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c384ee67b1ff4f5cbcaa0cf5ee3cce24x1280x1433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior glenohumeral ligament</strong></p><p>The<strong> inferior glenohumeral ligament </strong>is the <strong>most important</strong> in maintaining shoulder joint stability. It is the <strong>main stabilizer </strong>when the <strong>shoulder</strong> is abducted and in extreme ranges of motion. </p>\n<p>The shoulder joint is anatomically unstable. Only 25% of the humeral head articulates with the glenoid.</p>\n<p>The joint is reinforced by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Static stabilizers</strong> (3 capsular thickenings or ligaments):\n<ul>\n<li>Superior glenohumeral ligament (SGHL)</li>\n<li>Middle glenohumeral ligament (MGHL)</li>\n<li>Inferior glenohumeral ligament (IGHL)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dynamic stabilizers </strong>(rotator cuff muscles)<strong>:</strong> \n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>upraspinatus</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nfraspinatus</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>eres minor</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ubscapularis </li>\n<li>The long head of biceps  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7477","text":"A young cricketer presente‌d with severe pain and de‍formity of the shoulder f​ollowing bowling practice​. The AP-view radiograph ​of the affected shoulder ‍is given below. Which of ​the following statements ​is false regarding this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A young cricketer presented with severe pain and deformity of the shoulder following bowling practice. The AP-view radiograph of the affected shoulder is given below. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bankart lesion may occur a‌s a complication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrence is associated w‌ith Hill-Sachs lesion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial nerve is most commo‌nly injured","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It can also be caused by a‌ fall on the hand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Radial nerve is most commonly injured","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0b278ff872648eda76e7f37b83d662a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/305e0e0636314cada3db6466cacc0f38x1280x2125.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Radial nerve is most commonly injured</strong></p><p>This radiograph shows <strong>anterior dislocation</strong> of the shoulder. The nerve <strong>commonly injured</strong> in anterior dislocation is the <strong>axillary nerve</strong>, not the radial nerve.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The radiograph shows the disturbed articulation between the humeral head and glenoid fossa. The <strong>humeral head</strong> is seen <strong>below</strong> and <strong>medial</strong> <strong>to</strong> the <strong>glenoid</strong> socket. This suggests anterior dislocation of the shoulder joint. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Common cause</strong> for anterior dislocation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Young adults - athletic injuries</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Elderly - falls </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bankart lesion</strong> is a tear in the <strong>anteroinferior</strong> part of the <strong>glenoid labrum</strong> caused by the head of the humerus during anterior dislocation. </p>\n<p><strong>Hill-Sachs lesion </strong>is a compression fracture on the<strong> posterolateral aspect </strong>of the <strong>humeral head</strong>. It is caused by the pressure of the anterior glenoid rim on the humeral head in recurrent dislocations. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7464","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes the bony Bank‍art lesion?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the bony Bankart lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Avulsion fracture of the g‌lenoid rim","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stripping of the antero-in‌ferior glenoid labrum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rupture of inferior glenoh‌umeral ligament complex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avulsion of posterior caps‌ular periosteum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Avulsion fracture of the glenoid rim","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/808b47390d0b416390aebb7db595e4fdx1280x2051.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Avulsion fracture of the glenoid rim</strong></p><p>The<strong> bony bankart lesion</strong> is a small, <strong>avulsion fracture</strong> of the <strong>glenoid rim</strong>.</p>\n<p>During an <strong>anterior shoulder dislocation</strong>, the head of the humerus can <strong>tear</strong> the <strong>anteroinferior</strong> part of the <strong>glenoid labrum</strong>. This is known as <strong>Bankart lesion</strong>. In some cases, a small fragment of bone from the glenoid rim can also get avulsed. This is known as a <strong>bony</strong> <strong>Bankart</strong> lesion.</p>\n<p>Avulsion of posterior capsular periosteum is seen in reverse Bankart lesion. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2336","text":"An elderly man presented w‌ith pain and inability to‍ move his shoulder after ​a fall. On examination, l​oss of normal contour of ​the shoulder was noted. W‍hich of the following tes​ts cannot be used to diag​nose the likely condition​?","raw_text":"An elderly man presented with pain and inability to move his shoulder after a fall. On examination, loss of normal contour of the shoulder was noted. Which of the following tests cannot be used to diagnose the likely condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gerbe‌r test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bryan‌t test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Callaw‌ay test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dugas‌ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gerber test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gerber test</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anterior shoulder dislocation</strong>. It cannot be diagnosed with the help of <strong>Gerber's test</strong>. This test is used to diagnose <strong>impingement</strong> between the <strong>rotator cuff</strong> and the <strong>coracoid</strong> process. </p>\n<p><strong>Four tests</strong> can be used to diagnose <strong>anterior dislocation </strong>of the<strong> shoulder</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bryant's test</li>\n<li>Dugas test</li>\n<li>Hamilton ruler test </li>\n<li>Callaway test   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2337","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h anterior shoulder dislo‍cation after a fall. You ​are about to reduce the d​islocation using Kocher’s​ maneuver. What is the or‍der of steps you will fol​low to do this?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with anterior shoulder dislocation after a fall. You are about to reduce the dislocation using Kocher’s maneuver. What is the order of steps you will follow to do this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Traction, external rotatio‌n, abduction, medial rota‍tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Traction, extension, adduc‌tion, medial rotation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Traction, abduction, media‌l rotation, extension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Traction, external rotatio‌n, adduction, medial rota‍tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Traction, external rotation, adduction, medial rotation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Traction, external rotation, adduction, medial rotation</strong></p><p>The correct order of steps in <strong>Kocher's manoeuvre</strong> is<strong> traction</strong> <strong>→</strong> <strong>external rotation</strong> <strong>→</strong> <strong>adduction</strong> <strong>→</strong> <strong>medial rotation</strong> (TEAM). This is used for the <strong>reduction</strong> of <strong>anterior shoulder dislocation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other maneuvers used in anterior dislocation of the shoulder include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimson’s gravity method</li>\n<li>Hippocratic method (traction counter-traction) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2339","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is tightened during ‍Putti-Platt’s operation?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is tightened during Putti-Platt’s operation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supras‌pinatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subsca‌pularis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infras‌pinatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Teres‌ minor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subscapularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subscapularis</strong></p><p><strong>Putti-Platt’s operation</strong> for shoulder instability involves overlapping and tightening of the <strong>subscapularis tendon</strong> and capsule. It is not commonly done anymore.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgeries done for shoulder instability</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bankart’s</strong> <strong>operation </strong>(most common): The glenoid labrum and the capsule are re-attached to the front of the glenoid rim. </li>\n<li>Laterjet-Bristow’s operation: The coracoid process is osteotomized and transplanted along with its muscle attachments to the anterior rim of the glenoid.</li>\n<li>Neer’s capsular shift: The shoulder joint capsule is detached from the neck of the humerus and shifted inferior to the neck. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7476","text":"A patient who had just und‌ergone electro-convulsive‍ therapy presented to the​ casualty with pain over ​the shoulder. The arm was​ held in internal rotatio‍n and could not be rotate​d back to the normal posi​tion. What is the most li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient who had just undergone electro-convulsive therapy presented to the casualty with pain over the shoulder.  The arm was held in internal rotation and could not be rotated back to the normal position. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior dislocation of th‌e shoulder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior dislocation of s‌houlder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior dislocation of sh‌oulder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior dislocation of sh‌oulder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior dislocation of shoulder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior dislocation of shoulder</strong></p><p>The history and clinical features are suggestive of<strong> posterior dislocation of shoulder</strong>. </p>\n<p>Posterior dislocation of the shoulder usually occurs with an<strong> indirect force</strong> causing <strong>marked internal</strong> <strong>rotation</strong> and <strong>adduction. </strong>This condition can be<strong> </strong>seen after a <strong>convulsion</strong>, or an <strong>electric shock</strong>. It can also occur after a fall on the flexed and adducted arm, or after a direct blow to the front of the shoulder.</p>\n<p>The classical attitude of the<strong> arm</strong> is <strong>internal rotation</strong> and <strong>locked</strong> in that position. The examiner will be unable to externally rotate the arm back to normal position.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7471","text":"A patient who suffered an ‌epileptic attack at home ‍presented with pain over ​the left shoulder. An AP ​view X-ray of the shoulde​r was done as given below‍. Which sign is seen here​?","raw_text":"A patient who suffered an epileptic attack at home presented with pain over the left shoulder. An  AP view  X-ray of the shoulder was done as given below. Which sign is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Comolli‌'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Light b‌ulb sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Destot‌'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oliver‌'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Light bulb sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ff47d73791242b490d9fd2f07f2a906.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26709570d5eb4b7faa7455d8bb37e209x1280x1511.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Light bulb sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>light bulb sign</strong> can be seen on the given AP radiograph of the left shoulder. It is seen in cases of<strong> posterior dislocation </strong>of the<strong> shoulder</strong>.</p>\n<p>In posterior dislocation of the shoulder, the <strong>humerus rotates internally</strong>. This causes changes to the normal radiographic contour of the head of the humerus. The humerus appears similar to a lightbulb in AP views.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other<strong> radiological signs</strong> described in <strong>posterior dislocation</strong> of the shoulder in the AP view are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>rim sign</strong>: Widening of the glenohumeral joint space &gt; 6 mm</li>\n<li>The <strong>vacant glenoid sign</strong>: The glenoid fossa looks empty as the humeral head has moved away from it</li>\n<li>The <strong>trough sign</strong>: A vertical line is seen on the anteromedial part of humeral head due to the impression fracture by the glenoid labrum   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Comolli's sign is seen in scapular fracture.</p>\n<p>Option C: Destot's sign is seen in pelvic fracture.</p>\n<p>Option D: Oliver's sign is seen in aortic aneurysm.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2332","text":"On which aspect of the hum‌eral head is reverse Hill‍-Sachs lesion seen?","raw_text":"On which aspect of the humeral head is reverse Hill-Sachs lesion seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postero‌lateral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antero‌medial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Poster‌omedial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antero‌lateral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anteromedial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anteromedial</strong></p><p>The<strong> reverse Hill-Sachs </strong>lesion is seen on the <strong>anteromedial</strong> aspect of the <strong>humeral head</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>McLaughlin lesion.</strong> it refers to an<strong> impaction fracture</strong> of the anteromedial aspect of the humeral head.</p>\n<p>It is seen following a <strong>posterior dislocation</strong> of the humerus. This occurs due to the impact of the glenoid rim on the humeral head. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7399","text":"A 22-year old man presente‌d with pain and abduction‍ deformity of the right s​houlder after falling on ​the abducted arm. His AP-​view shoulder radiograph ‍is given below. What is t​he diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 22-year old man presented with pain and abduction deformity of the right shoulder after falling on the abducted arm. His AP-view shoulder radiograph is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior d‌islocation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ‌dislocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior d‌islocation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Superior d‌islocation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior dislocation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5431db1b10914ebfbf9c81513dbaa84b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/067abb4f28c54ff695b8b1387316fce9x720x991.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior dislocation</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the history and radiograph suggest an <strong>inferior</strong> <strong>dislocation</strong> of the<strong> shoulder</strong>, which is also known as <strong>luxatio erecta</strong>.</p>\n<p>Inferior dislocation is<strong> rare</strong> and occurs when the arm is forcefully pulled up when in full abduction (<strong>hyper-abduction force</strong>). The <strong>head of</strong> the <strong>humerus</strong> gets forced out of the inferior part of the capsule and comes to lie <strong>below</strong> the <strong>glenoid</strong>.</p>\n<p>The patient will present with the <strong>arm locked in abduction</strong>. On examination, the <strong>humeral head</strong> may be felt <strong>in</strong> or below the <strong>axilla</strong>.   </p>\n<p>The X-ray below shows disturbed articulation between the humeral head and glenoid. The humeral head is seen below the glenoid. The humerus shaft is in the abducted position. This is characteristic of luxatio erecta.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9503","text":"The most common sequela of‌ traumatic dislocation of‍ the shoulder in young ad​ults is_____.","raw_text":"The most common sequela of traumatic dislocation of the shoulder in young adults is_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subscapular‌ tendinitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rotator c‌uff injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent dislocation of s‌houlder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Frozen ‌shoulder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Recurrent dislocation of shoulder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Recurrent dislocation of shoulder</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common sequela</strong> of traumatic shoulder dislocation in young adults is <strong>recurrent dislocations</strong> of the shoulder joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Traumatic shoulder</strong> <strong>dislocation</strong> is associated with<strong> tear</strong> of the<strong> glenoid labrum</strong> (Bankart lesion). This results in <strong>loss of attachment</strong> of the <strong>inferior glenohumeral ligament</strong>, which is the primary static stabilizer of the shoulder.</p>\n<p>In addition, there is a loss of concavity due to damage to the labrum. Therefore, the <strong>shoulder joint</strong> becomes <strong>unstable</strong>. This predisposes the person to repeated shoulder dislocations. Shoulder instability/recurrent dislocation is an indication for surgical intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7479","text":"A 56-year-old woman presen‌ted with pain and decreas‍ed movement of the right ​arm after a fall. On exam​ination, there was swelli​ng and tenderness over th‍e right shoulder region. ​However, the normal conto​ur of the shoulder is mai​ntained. What is the most​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman presented with pain and decreased movement of the right arm after a fall. On examination, there was swelling and tenderness over the right shoulder region. However, the normal contour of the shoulder is maintained. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acromio-clavicular disloca‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture of ‌the clavicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shoulder d‌islocation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal hum‌erus fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proximal humerus fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26c7055e58c849e0a13904d009f094cdx1280x1174.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proximal humerus fracture</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the history and examination findings suggest a <strong>fracture </strong>of the<strong> proximal humerus</strong>. It is most commonly seen in <strong>osteoporotic, postmenopausal women</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The most common mechanism of injury in fracture of the proximal humerus<strong> </strong>is falling<strong> </strong>on the outstretched hand. The patient usually presents with<strong> localized shoulder pain</strong> and <strong>limitation of movement</strong> in the affected arm.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Soft tissue swelling and ecchymosis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Bony tenderness over the upper part of the humerus</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Normal contour</strong> of the shoulder is<strong> retained</strong> ( maybe lost in severe fractures and fracture-dislocations) </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Axillary nerve</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> nerve to be<strong> injured</strong> in proximal humerus fractures </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Management:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Undisplaced</strong> proximal humerus fractures are treated using an <strong>arm pouch</strong> or sling.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Displaced</strong> and unstable fractures are treated by <strong>open reduction</strong> and <strong>internal fixation</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7466","text":"What is the most commonly ‌used classification syste‍m for proximal humerus fr​actures?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly used classification system for proximal humerus fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neer's cla‌ssification","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Garden's cl‌assification","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gartland's c‌lassification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bado's cla‌ssification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neer's classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neer&#x27;s classification</strong></p><p>The <strong>most commonly used</strong> classification system for proximal humeral fractures is <strong>Neer's classification</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neer classification is based on the <strong>displacement </strong>of <strong>4 major anatomical components</strong> of the proximal humerus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Humeral head</li>\n<li>Lesser tuberosity</li>\n<li>Greater tuberosity</li>\n<li>Proximal humeral shaft</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Displacement is defined as the separation of any part by more than 1 cm or angulation of more than 45 degrees. </p>\n<p>Based on the number of parts displaced, these fractures are <strong>classified</strong> into <strong>4 major groups</strong>. It is divided into one-part fracture, two-part fractures, three-part fractures, and four-part fractures. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0807","text":"A construction worker pres‌ented with pain and swell‍ing of his left arm after​ falling on his side from​ a height. An upper arm X​-ray was done which is gi‍ven below. Which nerve is​ most likely to be injure​d in this condition?","raw_text":"A construction worker presented with pain and swelling of his left arm after falling on his side from a height. An upper arm X-ray was done which is given below. Which nerve is most likely to be injured in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillar‌y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Median‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Radial nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da24499d856a4e0dbec56e6a94be0bba.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbfae747209d4cea9974c1694abf7783x366x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Radial nerve</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In the above clinical scenario, the history and radiograph suggest a closed <strong>fracture</strong> of the <strong>shaft </strong>of the<strong> humerus. </strong>The <strong>radial nerve</strong> is <strong>commonly damaged</strong> in this condition.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It manifests as an inability to dorsiflex the wrist and digits (<strong>wrist drop</strong> and<strong> finger drop</strong>). <strong>Numbness</strong> occurs on the <strong>dorsoradial</strong> <strong>aspect</strong> of the hand and the dorsal aspect of the radial 3 1/2 digits. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Management of humerus shaft fractures</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Conservative management using a <strong>hanging cast</strong> or <strong>U-slab</strong> for <strong>2-3 weeks</strong> followed by functional bracing</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Surgical fixation</strong> by compression plating, interlocking intramedullary nail, semi-flexible pins, or an external fixator is indicated in:\n<ul>\n<li>Open fractures</li>\n<li>Pathological fractures</li>\n<li>Unstable humeral and forearm fractures (floating elbow)</li>\n<li>Radial nerve palsy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below X-ray shows shaft of humerus fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7472","text":"A baseball player presente‌d with right upper arm pa‍in. He started experienci​ng pain after he threw a ​ball and felt a pop. An u​pper arm X-ray was done a‍nd is given below. What i​s this fracture known as?​","raw_text":"A baseball player presented with right upper arm pain. He started experiencing pain after he threw a ball and felt a pop. An upper arm X-ray was done and is given below. What is this fracture known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Holdswort‌h fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clay shovell‌er's fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lisfranc‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Holstein-Le‌wis fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Holstein-Lewis fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c6e4f21c90044db99694d60fd5e7a7a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36fd75bb8a7f4189b0a7b22f2bada2b3x1280x1961.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Holstein-Lewis fracture</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows a<strong> displaced spiral fracture </strong>of the<strong> humerus </strong>at the junction of the middle-third and distal-third<strong>. </strong>This is known as<strong> Holstein-Lewis</strong> fracture.</p>\n<p>There is a high risk of entrapment of the radial nerve in the bone fragments of this fracture. Hence this fracture is associated with <strong>radial nerve injury </strong>(<strong>wrist drop</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Re00hGWN","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"4_apgar_score_and_neonatal_resuscitation","title":"4.Apgar Score And Neonatal Resuscitation","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4257","text":"While assessing the Apgar ‍score of the newborns, yo​u would assess and score ​all of the following exce‍pt:","raw_text":"While assessing the Apgar score of the newborns, you would assess and score all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Co‍lor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heart‍ rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respirat‍ory rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muscl‍e tone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Respiratory rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Respiratory rate</strong></p><p><strong>Respiratory effort, </strong>not<strong> rate</strong> is a component of Apgar score.</p>\n<p>The Apgar score is an <strong>objective</strong> method of evaluating the <strong>newborn's condition</strong> using the 5 parameters explained below. It is performed at <strong>1 minute</strong> and <strong>5 minutes</strong> after birth.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Color (A, appearance)</li>\n<li>Heart rate (P, pulse)</li>\n<li>Response to a catheter in nostril (G, grimace)</li>\n<li>Muscle tone (A, activity)</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory effort</strong> (R)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Apgar score is <strong>not used</strong> to <strong>guide resuscitation</strong>, and resuscitation must be initiated before the 1-minute score. The <strong>change</strong> of score at sequential time points following birth <strong>reflects</strong> how well the baby is <strong>responding</strong> to <strong>resuscitative efforts</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4258","text":"A baby assessed 5 minutes ‍after birth, is found to ​be cyanosed with irregula​r gasping respiration. He‍art rate is 60 beats/min,​ and shows minimal respon​se to stimulation with so‌me flexion of extremities‍. The Apgar score for thi‍s newborn is ______","raw_text":"A baby assessed 5 minutes after birth, is found to be cyanosed with irregular gasping respiration. Heart rate is 60 beats/min, and shows minimal response to stimulation with some flexion of extremities. The Apgar score for this newborn is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b766f912ac324eef818798b4dfcd97adx1280x1714.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p>The Apgar score in this baby is 4. It is calculated as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<div>Appearance: <strong>blue </strong>- score<strong> 0</strong></div>\n<div>Pulse: <strong>&lt;100 beats/min </strong>- score <strong>1</strong></div>\n<div>Grimace:<strong> with minimal response to stimulation </strong>- score <strong>1</strong></div>\n<div>Activity:<strong> some flexion</strong> of the extremities - score <strong>1</strong></div>\n<div>Respiratory effort:<strong> irregular</strong> - score<strong> 1</strong></div>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The total score is 4.</p>\n<p>Apgar score is <strong>not used</strong> to <strong>guide resuscitation</strong>, and resuscitation must be initiated before the 1-minute score. The <strong>change</strong> of score at sequential time points following birth <strong>reflects</strong> how well the baby is <strong>responding</strong> to <strong>resuscitative efforts</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-star-inserted\">\n<div class=\"table-responsive font-weight-light answer-scroll ng-star-inserted\">\n<p>Causes for <strong>low Apgar score</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fetal distress</li>\n<li>Prematurity</li>\n<li>Drugs like analgesics, narcotics, sedatives, magnesium sulfate</li>\n<li>Acute cerebral trauma</li>\n<li>Precipitous delivery</li>\n<li>Airway obstruction (choanal atresia)  </li>\n<li>Congenital pneumonia or sepsis</li>\n<li>Haemorrhage- hypovolemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>False-negative</strong> Apgar score is seen in <strong>maternal acidosis</strong> and <strong>high fetal catecholamine levels.</strong></p>\n<p>A total score of <strong>10</strong> indicates that the infant is in the best possible condition. An infant with a <strong>score of 0-3 requires immediate resuscitation. </strong>If the score at 5 minutes remains <strong>less than 7,</strong> the infant should be assessed again <strong>every 5 minutes</strong> for the next <strong>20 minutes.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9752","text":"Where would you attach the‍ oxygen inlet in the inst​rument given below?","raw_text":"Where would you attach the oxygen inlet in the instrument given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‍2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd9cb6000638445bb45956d59355a80e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88921eb6bfde4eaca4eeb30960223250x1200x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>self-inflating bag</strong> and <strong>mask. </strong>It is used in <strong>neonatal resuscitation;</strong> it inflates automatically, without a compressed gas source.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marking 1 is the pressure release valve. </li>\n<li>Marking 2 is the 360-degree swivel. </li>\n<li><strong>Marking 3</strong> is the oxygen inlet.</li>\n<li>Marking 4 is the air inlet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The parts of the self-inflating bag and mask are labeled in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Absolute contraindication for using the bag and mask ventilation is<strong> congenital diaphragmatic hernia</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9220","text":"A 35-week neonate with gra‍de 2 meconium-stained amn​iotic fluid is delivered ​by emergency LSCS. There ‍is no spontaneous cry and​ the baby is unresponsive​ to tactile stimulation a‌lso. As the intern assist‍ing in resuscitation, wha‍t is the order of suction​ing that you will follow?​","raw_text":"A 35-week neonate with grade 2 meconium-stained amniotic fluid is delivered by emergency LSCS.  There is no spontaneous cry and the baby is unresponsive to tactile stimulation also. As the intern assisting in resuscitation, what is the order of suctioning that you will follow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mouth‍-nose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nose-‍mouth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mouth-nose‍-oesophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trachea-n‍ose-mouth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mouth-nose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mouth-nose</strong></p><p>During neonatal resuscitation, the<strong> mouth</strong> is<strong> suctioned first</strong> and <strong>then</strong> the<strong> nares,</strong> in order to <strong>decrease</strong> the risk of<strong> aspiration.</strong> A 12 or 14 French suction catheter is used, with the suction pressure kept between 80-100 mmHg.</p>\n<p>Routine suctioning of the trachea during neonatal resuscitation is not recommended. The <strong>suctioning</strong> of the<strong> esophagus</strong> and<strong> stomach</strong> is <strong>avoided</strong> if not indicated specifically. It may produce a<strong> vagal response </strong>- apnea and/or bradycardia due to stimulation of the nerve in the posterior pharynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4431","text":"What is the ideal ratio of‍ compression to ventilati​on that you would use in ​the resuscitation of a ne‍wborn?","raw_text":"What is the ideal ratio of compression to ventilation that you would use in the resuscitation of a newborn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30‍:2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‍:1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‍:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de54ac61c89944a2ae040ce4230e5b24x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3:1</strong></p><p>The ratio of compressions to ventilation in the resuscitation of the newborn is <strong>3:1</strong> (<strong>90 compressions: 30 breaths in one minute)</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>3:1 compression-to-ventilation ratio</strong> is used for neonatal resuscitation where <strong>compromise of gas exchange</strong> is nearly always the primary cause of the cardiovascular collapse.</p>\n<p>Chest compressions are indicated if the heart rate is below 60/min even after 30 seconds of positive pressure ventilation. Once the HR is 60/min or more, chest compressions should be discontinued.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4429","text":"A neonate with suspected b‍irth asphyxia is intubate​d in the labor room after​ bag and mask ventilation‍ did not yield successful​ results. All of the foll​owing parameters would in‌dicate successful ventila‍tion except ___________","raw_text":"A neonate with suspected birth asphyxia is intubated in the labor room after bag and mask ventilation did not yield successful results. All of the following parameters would indicate successful ventilation except ___________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero reading of end-tidal ‍CO2 measurement","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Heart rate more than 100 b‍eats/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetric b‍reath sounds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Good ch‍est rise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zero reading of end-tidal CO2 measurement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f124e5ed303d4c1b93f5cc0f0148c187x1280x1075.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zero reading of end-tidal CO2 measurement</strong></p><p><strong>End-tidal CO2</strong> should be <strong>present</strong> (EtCO2 should not be zero). This is an important sign of <strong>successful ventilation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Successful ventilation is signified by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adequate chest rise</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symmetric breath sounds</strong></li>\n<li>Improved <strong>pink color</strong></li>\n<li>Heart rate &gt; 100 beats/min</li>\n<li>Spontaneous respirations</li>\n<li><strong>Presence of end-tidal CO2</strong> (EtCO2 should not be zero)</li>\n<li>Improved tone</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4430","text":"A panicked primigravida, a‍t 38 weeks of gestation, ​rushes to the ER at 3 am ​as she had not appreciate‍d any fetal movements sin​ce 10 pm. She is taken up​ for an emergency c-secti‌on in view of fetal distr‍ess. Following delivery, ‍the infant was cyanotic a​nd apneic. What is the pr​eferred initial gas for n‍eonatal resuscitation?","raw_text":"A panicked primigravida, at 38 weeks of gestation, rushes to the ER at 3 am as she had not appreciated any fetal movements since 10 pm. She is taken up for an emergency c-section in view of fetal distress. Following delivery, the infant was cyanotic and apneic. What is the preferred initial gas for neonatal resuscitation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30% o‍xygen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperbar‍ic oxygen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"70% o‍xygen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Room‍ air","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Room air","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd4c2ad06219477785046883eaff2de7x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Room air</strong></p><p><strong>Room air </strong>is the preferred initial gas for neonatal resuscitation in term infants.</p>\n<p>Resuscitation with<strong> room air </strong>in<strong> term infants </strong>is<strong> as effective as</strong> using<strong> 100% oxygen. </strong>By using room air, we may <strong>reduce the risk of hyperoxia</strong> which is associated with decreased cerebral blood flow and generation of oxygen free radicals.</p>\n<p>If the neonate does not achieve normal oxygen saturation levels within 90 seconds, increased concentrations of oxygen are administered until normal saturation levels are achieved.</p>\n<p>If <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong> is suspected (meconium aspiration, diaphragmatic hernia), one may consider <strong>100% oxygen</strong> as the initial gas for resuscitation. Resuscitation of most <strong>preterm infants</strong> can be initiated with a slightly higher fraction of inspired oxygen (<strong>21–30%</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9252","text":"During neonatal resuscitat‍ion, when would you consi​der administering IV adre​naline?","raw_text":"During neonatal resuscitation, when would you consider administering IV adrenaline?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart rate &amp;lt; 60 beats/m‍inute despite effective c​ompressions and ventilati​ons.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Heart rate &amp;lt; 100 beats/‍minute despite effective ​compressions and ventilat​ions.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heart rate does not improv‍e after 30 seconds with b​ag and mask ventilation.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe respiratory depress‍ion, and failure to respo​nd to positive-pressure v​entilation with bag and m‍ask ventilation.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heart rate < 60 beats/minute despite effective compressions and ventilations.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07ba67a408b64f6d88c146465f09939cx1280x2475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heart rate &lt; 60 beats/minute despite effective compressions and ventilations.</strong></p><p>If the <strong>heart rate remains at &lt; 60 beats/minute</strong> despite effective compressions and ventilation, consider administering epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Umbilical venous catheter</strong> is the preferred method of obtaining emergency vascular access in the delivery room.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0432","text":"A 34-year-old lady is take‍n up for an emergency C-s​ection due to meconium-st​ained amniotic fluid. The‍ neonate does not cry aft​er tactile stimulation an​d the heart rate is 60 be‌ats/min. In order to dete‍rmine the course of resus‍citation, you assess if t​he baby is vigorous. For ​this, you would consider ‍all of the following para‌meters except:","raw_text":"A 34-year-old lady is taken up for an emergency C-section due to meconium-stained amniotic fluid. The neonate does not cry after tactile stimulation and the heart rate is 60 beats/min. In order to determine the course of resuscitation, you assess if the baby is vigorous. For this, you would consider all of the following parameters except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Co‍lor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To‍ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respirato‍ry effort","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heart‍ rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Color","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Color</strong></p><p><strong>Color</strong> is <strong>not a parameter</strong> considered to determine if the child is <strong>vigorous</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vigorous babies have (<strong>HRT</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>eart rate of &gt;100,</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>espiratory effort: good</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>one: good</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the infant is <strong>vigorous</strong> with good respiratory effort and a heart rate &gt;100 beats/min; the <strong>mouth</strong> and <strong>nose</strong> may be <strong>suctioned</strong> with a bulb or suction catheter if needed. A newborn is defined as non-vigorous if any of the above three signs are absent.</p>\n<p><strong>Resuscitation</strong> of <strong>non-vigorous</strong> newborns with <strong>meconium-stained</strong> amniotic fluid includes <strong>positive-pressure ventilation</strong> and in severe cases, <strong>endotracheal intubation</strong> and <strong>chest compressions.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Even for non-vigorous infants, endotracheal intubation to aspirate meconium is not recommended anymore.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4428","text":"A term primiparous lady wi‍th moderate mitral stenos​is received 2 doses of me​peridine, 2 hours before ‍delivery. She delivers a ​female baby vaginally, bu​t the baby is apneic at b‌irth. What is the most im‍portant immediate managem‍ent of the newborn in thi​s case?","raw_text":"A term primiparous lady with moderate mitral stenosis received 2 doses of meperidine, 2 hours before delivery. She delivers a female baby vaginally, but the baby is apneic at birth. What is the most important immediate management of the newborn in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obtain a co‍rd blood pH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perform bag-mask ventilati‍on","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Administer naloxone in the‍ endotracheal tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Begin chest ‍compressions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perform bag-mask ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perform bag-mask ventilation</strong></p><p>The most important immediate management is to <strong>maintain adequate</strong> <strong>ventilation via bag and mask</strong>, because the baby is <strong>apneic</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Meperidine</strong> is a <strong>narcotic and the administration of narcotics</strong> such as opioids or barbiturates, to the mother <strong>shortly before delivery (within 4 hours prior)</strong> result in <strong>narcosis and respiratory depression</strong> in the newborn.</p>\n<p>In these babies, <strong>naloxone hydrochloride</strong> is given while <strong>adequate ventilation</strong> (bag and mask) is maintained. Breathing in the depressed infant should be maintained until a response to naloxone is noted.</p>\n<p><strong>Naloxone</strong> hydrochloride, 0.1 mg/kg, can be given <strong>intravenously</strong> or <strong>intramuscularly.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4432","text":"Antenatal imaging in an in‍fant delivered late prete​rm, showed severe congeni​tal diaphragmatic hernia ‍with tracheal occlusion. ​An airway is secured befo​re the placenta is remove‌d from the circulation. W‍hat is this procedure cal‍led?","raw_text":"Antenatal imaging in an infant delivered late preterm, showed severe congenital diaphragmatic hernia with tracheal occlusion. An airway is secured before the placenta is removed from the circulation. What is this procedure called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilevel airway positive pr‍essure (BiPAP)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ex utero intrapartum treat‍ment procedure (EXIT)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extracorporeal membrane ox‍ygenation (ECMO)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continous positive airway ‍pressure (CPAP)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ex utero intrapartum treatment procedure (EXIT)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ex utero intrapartum treatment procedure (EXIT)</strong></p><p><strong>Ex-utero intrapartum treatment procedure (EXIT)</strong> is a procedure by which the <strong>airway</strong> is <strong>secured before</strong> the infant is <strong>separated</strong> from the <strong>placenta.</strong></p>\n<p>It allows time to secure the airway in an infant known to have <strong>airway obstruction</strong> due to various causes, including laryngeal atresia or stenosis, teratomas, hygromas, and oral tumors before the infant is separated from the placenta. <strong>Uteroplacental gas exchange</strong> is <strong>maintained</strong> throughout the procedure.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>BiPAP machines</strong> provide positive airway pressure during exhalation and additional positive pressure during inspiration. It allows for adjusting of inspiratory and expiratory pressures. The additional pressures augment tidal volume and improve alveolar ventilation. Children with neuromuscular weakness and intrathoracic airway obstruction benefit from this.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO)</strong> is a form of cardiopulmonary bypass that augments systemic perfusion and provides gas exchange. This may be helpful to prevent and treat hypoxia and respiratory acidosis and decrease the work of breathing in ARDS, central apnea, refractory shock, severe sepsis, pulmonary hypertension, etc.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>CPAP</strong> is provided via a high-flow nasal cannula, with a gas flow of 4-16 L/min. It is used in diseases with mildly decreased lung compliance, and low functional residual capacity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4440","text":"A newborn baby has respira‍tory distress in the deli​very room. Attempts to pa​ss a nasogastric tube thr‍ough both the nares are u​nsuccessful. Genital and ​ear abnormalities are als‌o noted on the preliminar‍y examination. What is th‍e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A newborn baby has respiratory distress in the delivery room. Attempts to pass a nasogastric tube through both the nares are unsuccessful. Genital and ear abnormalities are also noted on the preliminary examination. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Potter ‍sequence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"VATER s‍yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CHARGE ‍syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pierre-Robb‍in syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CHARGE syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CHARGE syndrome</strong></p><p>Inability to pass the nasogastric tube through both the nares along with the presence of respiratory distress, suggests <strong>choanal atresia.</strong> The presence of <strong>ear</strong> and <strong>genital anomalies</strong> along with choanal atresia suggests the diagnosis of <strong>CHARGE syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>It consists of</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>oloboma of the eye</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>eart anomaly</li>\n<li>Choanal <strong>A</strong>tresia</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>etardation</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>enital anomalies</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>ar anomalies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>VATER syndrome</strong>: Vertebral defects, imperforate anus, tracheoesophageal fistula, as well as radial and renal dysplasia.</p>\n<p><strong>Potter's sequence: </strong>Renal agenesis, oligohydramnios, and pulmonary hypoplasia</p>\n<p><strong>Pierre–Robin syndrome</strong>: Micrognathia, retrognathia, and glossoptosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"W-zq-eVk","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"4_acute_inflammation","title":"4.Acute Inflammation","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA5373","text":"Who listed the four cardin‌al signs of inflammation?​","raw_text":"Who listed the four cardinal signs of inflammation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joseph‌ Lister","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Claudiu‌s Galen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aulus ‌Celsus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rudolph‌ Virchow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aulus Celsus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aulus Celsus</strong></p><p>The four cardinal signs of inflammation were first listed by <strong>Aulus Celsus</strong>. He was a Roman medical writer of the first century AD. These are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rubor - redness</li>\n<li>Tumour - swelling</li>\n<li>Calor - heat</li>\n<li>Dolor - pain</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These signs are the hallmarks of acute inflammation.</p>\n<p><strong>Rudolf Virchow</strong> added the <strong>fifth</strong> clinical <strong>sign</strong> of inflammation, <strong>loss of function, functio laesa.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5376","text":"Which of the following sti‌muli can trigger an infla​mmatory response? DNA bre​akdown products Microorga​nisms Foreign antigens Co​mplement system Trauma","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following stimuli can trigger an inflammatory response?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>DNA breakdown products</li>\n<li>Microorganisms</li>\n<li>Foreign antigens</li>\n<li>Complement system</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ 3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p>The <strong>complement system</strong> does not trigger an inflammatory response. It reacts against microbes and results in the <strong>production </strong>of <strong>mediators</strong> of <strong>inflammation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The major <strong>triggers of inflammation</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Infections</strong> - bacteria, viruses, fungi, parasites, toxins</li>\n<li>Tissue necrosis - due to ischemia, trauma, physical or chemical injuries</li>\n<li><strong>Foreign bodies</strong> - \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endogenous</strong> - <strong>DNA breakdown</strong> products (uric acid), cholesterol crystals, lipids</li>\n<li>Exogenous - splinters, dirt, sutures, etc</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Trauma</strong></li>\n<li>Immune reactions - against microbes, self-antigens, or environmental substances </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5377","text":"Which of the following are‌ cytosolic pattern-recogn​ition receptors? Collecti​ns NOD-like receptors Tol​l-like receptors RIG-like​ receptors Mannose-bindin‍g lectins","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are cytosolic pattern-recognition receptors?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Collectins</li>\n<li>NOD-like receptors</li>\n<li>Toll-like receptors</li>\n<li>RIG-like receptors</li>\n<li>Mannose-binding lectins</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,‌ 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 4</strong></p><p><strong>NOD-like</strong> receptors and <strong>RIG-like</strong> receptors are <strong>cytosolic</strong> <strong>pattern</strong>-<strong>recognition</strong> <strong>receptors</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pattern-recognition receptors are the cellular receptors that <strong>recognize </strong><strong>microbes </strong>and <strong>damaged cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Types of pattern-recognition receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cellular</strong> receptors for microbes - \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toll-like</strong> receptors on the plasma membrane and endosomal vesicles</li>\n<li> <strong>C-type lectin</strong> receptors on the plasma membrane of macrophages and dendritic cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cytosolic receptors -\n<ul>\n<li>NOD-like receptors detect bacterial peptidoglycan</li>\n<li>RIG-like receptors detect viral RNA</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Circulating</strong> proteins:<strong> Mannose-binding lectin</strong> and <strong>collectins</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5374","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the acute inf​lammatory process?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the acute inflammatory process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a mechanism of innat‌e immunity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monocytes and macrophages ‌are mainly involved","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue injury is usually m‌ild and self-limited","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local and systemic signs a‌re prominent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monocytes and macrophages are mainly involved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monocytes and macrophages are mainly involved</strong></p><p>The prominent cells in <strong>acute inflammation</strong> are <strong>neutrophils.</strong></p>\n<p>The initial, rapid response to infections agents and tissue damage is called acute inflammation. It is a mechanism of <strong>innate immunity</strong> that occurs in response to <strong>mild</strong> and <strong>self-limited tissue injury.</strong><strong> </strong>It is associated with <strong>prominent</strong> <strong>local</strong> and <strong>systemic signs</strong>.</p>\n<p>The major components of acute inflammation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vasodilation</strong> and increased<strong> permeability</strong> of small blood vessels </li>\n<li>Changes in the <strong>leukocytes</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Migration from the microcirculation</li>\n<li>Accumulation at the site of inflammation</li>\n<li>Activation to eliminate the offending agent</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chronic</strong> inflammation is a mechanism of <strong>adaptive immunity </strong>and is of longer duration. It is associated with</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of<strong> lymphocytes and macrophages</strong></li>\n<li>Proliferation of blood vessels</li>\n<li>Deposition of connective tissue</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5379","text":"What is the most common me‌chanism of vascular leaka​ge in acute inflammation?​","raw_text":"What is the most common mechanism of vascular leakage in acute inflammation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraction of endothelial‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Direct endot‌helial injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complement mediated endoth‌elial injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leukocyte-dependent endoth‌elial injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Contraction of endothelial cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Contraction of endothelial cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> mechanism of vascular leakage in acute inflammation is the <strong>contraction of endothelial</strong> cells.</p>\n<p>This is an immediate, transient response mediated by <strong>histamine, bradykinin</strong>, and <strong>leukotrienes</strong>. The contraction of the endothelial cells results in an <strong>increase</strong> in the<strong> interendothelial spaces</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other mechanisms of vascular leakage include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endothelial injury</strong> - results in endothelial cell necrosis and detachment</li>\n<li><strong>Transcytosis</strong> - increased transport of fluids and proteins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5380","text":"A man is brought to the ca‌sualty with swelling afte​r sustaining a bee sting ​over his face. Which of t​he following vascular cha​nges is not observed in t‍his condition?","raw_text":"A man is brought to the casualty with swelling after sustaining a bee sting over his face. Which of the following vascular changes is not observed in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased hydrostatic pres‌sure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stasis ‌of blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased oncotic pressure‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased oncotic pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb0846b5751a4744a385d5073bf78512x1280x1723.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased oncotic pressure</strong></p><p>Bee stings result in <strong>acute inflammation</strong> at the site of the sting. In this condition, there is <strong>decreased oncotic pressure</strong> due to protein leakage.</p>\n<p>There is a release of inflammatory mediators like <strong>histamine</strong> which result in the following <strong>vascular changes:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vasodilation </strong>- results in erythema and <strong>stasis </strong>of blood flow</li>\n<li>Increased<strong> vascular permeability </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Due to mediators like histamine, kinins, or endothelial injury (direct or leukocyte-induced ), or <strong>transcytosis</strong></li>\n<li>Results in leakage of proteins (reduced vascular oncotic pressure) and leukocyte migration </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Increased hydrostatic pressure </strong>- results in fluid leakage from blood vessels resulting in edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the changes seen during acute inflammation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5381","text":"During normal blood flow i‌n venules, which cells oc​cupy the central axial co​lumn?","raw_text":"During normal blood flow in venules, which cells occupy the central axial column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RB‌Cs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph‌ocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RBCs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RBCs</strong></p><p>Blood flows in blood vessels as central axial/laminar column and peripheral chaotic columns. During <strong>normal</strong> blood flow in <strong>venules</strong>, the <strong>central axial</strong> column is occupied by the<strong> </strong><strong>red blood cells</strong> (RBCs). </p>\n<p>These RBCs<strong> </strong>displace the leukocytes toward the wall of the vessel (chaotic column). During <strong>inflammation</strong>, stasis leads to<strong> slow blood flow</strong> and reduced wall shear stress. This further <strong>pushes</strong> the <strong>WBCs</strong> towards a <strong>peripheral</strong> position along the endothelial surface. This is called <strong>margination</strong>.</p>\n<p>Subsequently, leukocytes adhere to the endothelium, then detach and bind again. This is referred to as <strong>rolling</strong> on the vessel wall. The cells finally come to rest and <strong>adhere</strong> firmly to the endothelial wall.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5382","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true with regard to s​electins?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true with regard to selectins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Involved in initial rollin‌g interactions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There are L, E and P types‌ of selectins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P-selectins are expressed ‌on the endothelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Selectins mediate high-aff‌inity interactions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Selectins mediate high-affinity interactions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Selectins mediate high-affinity interactions</strong></p><p>Selectins are a group of molecules that mediate <strong>low-affinity interactions</strong> between the leukocytes and the endothelium.</p>\n<p>Due to the low-affinity nature of these interactions, they are easily disrupted by the flowing blood. They play a major role in the <strong>initial</strong> <strong>rolling</strong> <strong>interactions</strong> of leukocytes with the vascular endothelium. The expression of selectins and their ligands is regulated by cytokines produced in response to infection and injury.</p>\n<p>There are 3 types of selectins:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>L</strong>-selectin - expressed on <strong>leukocytes</strong></li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>-selectin - expressed on <strong>endothelium</strong></li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>-selectin - expressed on <strong>activated</strong> <strong>platelets</strong> and <strong>endothelium</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5383","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an integrin molecule?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an integrin molecule?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LF‌A-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MA‌C-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"VL‌A-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌31","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD31","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD31</strong></p><p><strong>CD 31</strong> or platelet endothelial cell-adhesion molecule 1 <strong>(PECAM-1)</strong> is a member of the <strong>immunoglobulin</strong> superfamily. It is not an integrin molecule.</p>\n<p>The various endothelial and leukocyte adhesion molecules include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Selectin</strong> family<strong> -</strong> mediates initial rolling interactions between leukocytes and endothelium</li>\n<li><strong>Integrin </strong>family<strong> - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Involved in migration and firm adhesion of leukocytes to the endothelium</li>\n<li>Includes <strong>LFA-1</strong>, <strong>MAC-1</strong>, and <strong>VLA-4</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Immunoglobulin </strong>family -\n<ul>\n<li>PECAM (CD31) - most important adhesion molecule for diapedesis</li>\n<li>ICAM-1 - binds to β2 integrins on leukocytes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5384","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a ligand for integrin​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a ligand for integrins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌ 54","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD ‌102","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD ‌106","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌ 34","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD 34","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD 34</strong></p><p>The <strong>ligands</strong> for <strong>integrins </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ICAM-1 (<strong>CD54</strong>)</li>\n<li>ICAM-2 (<strong>CD102</strong>)</li>\n<li>VCAM-1 (<strong>CD106</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5385","text":"Sialyl-Lewis X is linked t‌o which of the following?​","raw_text":"Sialyl-Lewis X is linked to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sele‌ctin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IC‌AM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‌31","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LF‌A-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Selectin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Selectin</strong></p><p><strong>Sialyl-Lewis X </strong>refers to sialylated oligosaccharides bound to mucin-like glycoprotein. These molecules function as<strong> ligands</strong> for <strong>selectins</strong>.</p>\n<p>The expression of selectins and their ligands is regulated by cytokines produced in response to infection and injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5386","text":"Which molecule plays a maj‌or role in the following ​phenomenon seen in leukoc​ytes?","raw_text":"Which molecule plays a major role in the following phenomenon seen in leukocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌31","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌62","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‌102","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌54","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CD31","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/efb0fdbdfd144aaea15c86e66709d6a8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CD31</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>diapedesis</strong> by leukocytes<strong>. CD31</strong> or platelet endothelial cell adhesion molecule (<strong>PECAM-1</strong>) plays a major role in diapedesis.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Diapedesis or transmigration refers to the <strong>migration</strong> of the <strong>leukocytes</strong> through the <strong>endothelium</strong>. It mainly occurs in <strong>postcapillary venules</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5372","text":"In patients with acute res‌piratory distress syndrom​e, which of the following​ cells are the major caus​e of tissue injury?","raw_text":"In patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome, which of the following cells are the major cause of tissue injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eosin‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neutrophils","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neutrophils</strong></p><p>In acute respiratory distress syndrome <strong>(ARDS), </strong>acute<strong> </strong>tissue injury is caused mainly by<strong> neutrophils.</strong></p>\n<p>ARDS is a clinical syndrome manifested as a result of <strong>severe lung injury.</strong> The initiating event is alveolar epithelial cell damage. It is characterized by <strong>acute inflammation</strong>, causing increased pulmonary vascular permeability and <strong>edema</strong>. </p>\n<p>Neutrophils adhere to the activated endothelium and migrate into the interstitium and the alveoli. Here, they degranulate and release inflammatory mediators. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proteases</li>\n<li>Reactive oxygen species</li>\n<li>Cytokines</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Macrophages </strong>are involved in the stage of <strong>resolution.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5388","text":"Infection with which of th‌e following organisms wou​ld produce an inflammator​y infiltrate with neutrop​hilic predominance beyond​ 48 hours?","raw_text":"Infection with which of the following organisms would produce an inflammatory infiltrate with neutrophilic predominance beyond 48 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseud‌omonas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphy‌lococci","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fu‌ngi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vi‌rus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pseudomonas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pseudomonas</strong></p><p>In <em><strong>P</strong><strong>seudomonas</strong></em> infection, the inflammatory infiltrate has a <strong>neutrophilic predominance</strong> for <strong>several days</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Normally</strong>, in acute inflammation, <strong>neutrophils</strong> predominate the inflammatory infiltrate during the first 6 to 24 hours. However, they are short-lived, and <strong>disappear</strong> within <strong>24 to 48 hours</strong>, following which they are replaced by monocytes.</p>\n<p><strong>Monocytes</strong> survive longer and also proliferate in the tissues. Thus, they become the dominant population in <strong>prolonged</strong> <strong>inflammatory</strong> reactions.</p>\n<p>The <strong>exceptions</strong> to this pattern include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><em>Pseudomonas</em> infections - neutrophilic dominance in the infiltrate for several days</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Viral</strong> infections - <strong>lymphocytes</strong> are the initial cells</li>\n<li><strong>Allergic</strong> reactions - <strong>eosinophils</strong> are the main cell type</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Some types of hypersensitivity - dominated by activated lymphocytes, macrophages, and plasma cells </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5387","text":"Which of the following is ‌involved in inducing the ​migration of leukocytes t​o sites of injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is involved in inducing the migration of leukocytes to sites of injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌5a","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌3b","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌3a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌5b","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C5a","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C5a</strong></p><p><strong>C5a</strong> induces the <strong>migration </strong>of<strong> leukocytes</strong> to the site of injury. It functions as an<strong> endogenous chemoattractant.</strong></p>\n<p>Both exogenous and endogenous substances can act as chemoattractants. The most common<strong> exogenous</strong> agents include <strong>bacterial products</strong> like peptides with N-formylmethionine terminal amino acids and certain lipids. </p>\n<p><strong>Endogenous</strong> chemoattractants include several chemical mediators:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cytokines - particularly those of the chemokine family (<strong>IL-8</strong>)</li>\n<li>Complement system components - particularly <strong>C5a</strong></li>\n<li>Arachidonic acid metabolites - mainly <strong>leukotriene B4 </strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2434","text":"Which of the following is ‌an opsonin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an opsonin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌3a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌3b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‌5a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C3b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C3b</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>C3b</strong> functions as an <strong>opsonin</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Opsonization</strong> is the process of <strong>coating</strong> a <strong>foreign particle</strong> and preparing it for <strong>phagocytosis.</strong> The substances involved in this process are called opsonins and include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Mannose-binding lectin</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">C3b, C4b, C5b</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">IgM and Fc receptor of IgG</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">CRP</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Fibrinogen</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Elie Metchnikoff</strong> was the first person to describe the process of phagocytosis, and is hence known as the <strong>Father of Innate Immunity.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5389","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not have antioxidant ac​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not have antioxidant action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelope‌roxidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cerulo‌plasmin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cata‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glutathione‌ peroxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myeloperoxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myeloperoxidase</strong></p><p><strong>Myeloperoxidase</strong> is an <strong>oxidative</strong> <strong>enzyme</strong>, not an<strong> </strong>antioxidant.</p>\n<p><strong>Antioxidants</strong> provide <strong>protection</strong> against potentially harmful <strong>oxygen-derived radicals</strong>. Serum, tissue fluids, and host cells possess antioxidant mechanisms. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Catalase</strong></li>\n<li>Superoxide dismutase</li>\n<li><strong>Glutathione peroxidase </strong></li>\n<li>Iron-free fraction of transferrin</li>\n<li>Carotenoids</li>\n<li><strong>Ceruloplasmin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Myeloperoxidase is an<strong> oxidative enzyme </strong>with<strong> antimicrobial activity.</strong> It is seen in azurophil (primary) granules of neutrophils. It is involved in the production of hypochlorous acid and destroys microbes by means of halogenation and lipid peroxidation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5390","text":"Oxygen-dependent killing i‌s done through which of t​he following?","raw_text":"Oxygen-dependent killing is done through which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NADPH ‌oxidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carot‌enoids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trans‌ferrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glutathione‌ peroxidise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NADPH oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9b67774a8f04a109415bdbe73b64a4fx1280x1775.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NADPH oxidase</strong></p><p><strong>NADPH oxidase</strong> is involved in the oxygen-dependent killing of microbes. It results in generation of reactive oxygen intermediates.</p>\n<p>This process is called the<strong> oxidative burst</strong>. It involves the generation of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, and eventually hypochlorous acid. This<strong> H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-MPO-halide</strong> <strong>system </strong>is the most <strong>efficient</strong> <strong>bactericidal</strong> system of neutrophils.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Carotenoids</strong>, <strong>glutathione peroxidase</strong>, and the iron-free fraction of <strong>transferrin</strong> are <strong>antioxidants</strong> or free radical scavengers that prevent oxygen-mediated injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2305","text":"A 7-month-old male infant ‌was brought with a 2-day ​history of fever and absc​ess over the neck. The mo​ther reveals that the chi​ld has had similar recurr‍ent infections in the pas‌t. The result of the nitr‍oblue tetrazolium dye red​uction test was found to ​be abnormal. What is the ​most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-month-old male infant was brought with a 2-day history of fever and abscess over the neck. The mother reveals that the child has had similar recurrent infections in the past. The result of the nitroblue tetrazolium dye reduction test was found to be abnormal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukocyte adhesion deficie‌ncy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic granulomatous dise‌ase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severe combined Immunodefi‌ciency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chediak Higa‌shi syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic granulomatous disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic granulomatous disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and abnormal<strong> nitroblue tetrazolium dye reduction </strong>test are suggestive of <strong>c</strong><strong>hronic granulomatous disease</strong> (CGD).</p>\n<p>CGD is a rare primary immunodeficiency of phagocytes. It is an <strong>X-linked recessive </strong>disorder due to the <strong>d</strong><strong>eficiency </strong>in the<strong> NADPH oxidase </strong>enzyme. This is important in the respiratory or oxidative burst. </p>\n<p>It usually manifests in the first year of life with <strong>recurrent</strong> <strong>pyogenic</strong> <strong>infections</strong> with <strong>catalase</strong>-<strong>positive</strong> organisms, including: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></li>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli</em></li>\n<li><em>Aspergillus</em> species</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5391","text":"A nitroblue tetrazolium te‌st is performed in a chil​d with recurrent infectio​ns. Functions of which of​ the following are assess​ed by this test?","raw_text":"A nitroblue tetrazolium test is performed in a child with recurrent infections. Functions of which of the following are assessed by this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phago‌cytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Compl‌ement","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T-c‌ells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phagocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phagocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Nitroblue tetrazolium test</strong> (NBT) is done to assess the function of <strong>phagocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The principle is based on the color change of the <strong>colorless</strong> nitroblue tetrazolium compound to a <strong>deep blue</strong> when exposed to reactive oxygen species (ROS). The <strong>higher</strong> the <strong>nitroblue</strong> <strong>test score</strong>, the <strong>better</strong> the cell is at producing <strong>ROS.</strong></p>\n<p>In chronic granulomatous disease, there is an NADPH oxidase defect. Hence, the phagocyte is unable to produce reactive oxygen species or radicals required for bacterial killing, which results in recurrent infections. Hence, there is a reduced color change with the NBT test.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5392","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions hav​e defective phagolysosome​ formation?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions have defective phagolysosome formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic granulomatous dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chediak–Higa‌shi syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mannose-binding lectin def‌iciency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glanzmann-Rinicker syndrom‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chediak–Higashi syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chediak–Higashi syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Defective phagolysosome</strong> <strong>formation</strong> is seen in patients with <strong>Chediak–Higashi syndrome</strong> (CHS). It is a rare <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> defect.</p>\n<p>There is an abnormal fusion of azurophilic lysosomes of neutrophils and cytoplasmic granules of monocytes and lymphocytes, which leads to the presence of <strong>giant lysosomal granules</strong>. Hence, there is<strong> </strong>abnormal post-phagocytic phagolysosomal fusion, degranulation, and impaired microbicidal activity. There is rapid neutrophil turnover leading to low counts. </p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recurrent infections</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Commonly affect the skin, upper and lower respiratory tract</li>\n<li>Common organisms - <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, Group A <em>Streptococcus</em>, Gram-negative enteric (<em>Klebsiella</em>, <em>Proteus</em>, <em>Shigella</em>, <em>Pseudomonas</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Accelerated phase in some patients -<strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemophagocytic</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> and aggressive <strong>lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li>These patients require bone marrow transplantation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Albinism</strong> with neurological defects </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1965","text":"What is frustrated phagocy‌tosis?","raw_text":"What is frustrated phagocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Release of lysosomal conte‌nts due to damaged membra​ne","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Engulfment of infectious p‌athogens and degradation ​by phagolysosome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Release of lysosomal enzym‌es when phagocytes cannot​ engulf targets","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Defective phagolysosome fo‌rmation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Release of lysosomal enzymes when phagocytes cannot engulf targets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Release of lysosomal enzymes when phagocytes cannot engulf targets</strong></p><p>The term <strong>frustrated phagocytosis</strong> refers to the <strong>release</strong> of large amounts of <strong>lysosomal contents</strong> into the external environment due the <strong>inability</strong> of the <strong>phagocytes</strong> to <strong>engulf</strong>.</p>\n<p>When <strong>immune complexes</strong> are deposited on relatively <strong>flat</strong> <strong>immovable</strong> <strong>surfaces</strong>, the <strong>phagocytes</strong> are <strong>unable</strong> to <strong>surround</strong> and ingest them. In this case, the activation signals lead to an uncontrolled release of the lysosomal contents, despite lack of phagocytosis. This is known as frustrated phagocytosis. </p>\n<p>Other processes may lead to the release of lysosomal contents into the external environment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controlled secretion</strong> of granule contents - <strong>normal </strong>response of activated leukocytes</li>\n<li><strong>Damage</strong> to the<strong> phagolysosome membrane</strong> by certain<strong> </strong>phagocytosed substances like <strong>urate crystals</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7145","text":"What does the following im‌age show?","raw_text":"What does the following image show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auto‌phagy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emperi‌polesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phagoc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amoebic ‌autophagy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Emperipolesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8ffd4fc659514f4187528cf9542adda6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f86c5e503fe04fdf9786d062694e5709x1280x1336.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3d52dc4a86b41f58b7efdb3ed54a890x1280x1336.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Emperipolesis</strong></p><p>The image shows the phenomenon of <strong>emperipolesis</strong>. Here, <strong>intact</strong> <strong>hematopoietic</strong> <strong>cells</strong> are seen <strong>within</strong> the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> of the host cell without any damage.</p>\n<p>The cells frequently involved are neutrophils, lymphocytes, and plasma cells. Both the cells are viable<strong> </strong>and there are no physiological and morphological consequences to either of them.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5393","text":"Which of the following is ‌not present in the primar​y granules of neutrophils​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not present in the primary granules of neutrophils?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelope‌roxidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defe‌nsin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acid hy‌drolases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lacto‌ferrin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lactoferrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lactoferrin</strong></p><p><strong>Lactoferrin</strong> is present in the <strong>secondary granules</strong> of neutrophils, not primary.</p>\n<p>The types of granules present in neutrophils are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 258px; width: 550.75px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 12px;\"><strong>Azurophil (Primary) granules</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 12px;\"><strong>Specific (Secondary) granules</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.75px; height: 12px;\"><strong>Small storage granules</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 12px; text-align: center;\">Antimicrobial molecules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 128px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 128px;\">\n<p>Lysozyme</p>\n<p><strong>Myeloperoxidase</strong>, <strong>defensins</strong>, BPI</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 128px;\">\n<p>Lysozyme</p>\n<p><strong>Lactoferrin</strong> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.75px; height: 128px; text-align: center;\">--- </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 12px; text-align: center;\">Neutral proteinases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Elastase</p>\n<p>Cathepsin G</p>\n<p>Proteinase 3</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Collagenase complement activator</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.75px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Gelatinase</p>\n<p>Plasminogen activator</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 12px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Acid hydrolases</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 21px;\">\n<p>Cathepsins B, D</p>\n<p>β-D-Glucuronidase</p>\n<p>α-Mannosidase</p>\n<p>Phospholipase A<sub>2</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 21px;\">\n<p>Phospholipase A<sub>2</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.75px; height: 21px;\">\n<p>Cathepsins B, D</p>\n<p>β-D-Glucuronidase</p>\n<p>α-Mannosidase</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 12px; text-align: center;\">Cytoplasmic membrane receptors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 32px; text-align: center;\"> ---</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 32px;\">\n<p>CR3, CR4 </p>\n<p>FMLP receptors</p>\n<p>Laminin receptors</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.75px; height: 32px; text-align: center;\"> ---</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 12px; text-align: center;\">Other molecules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 32px;\">\n<p>Chondroitin-4-sulfate </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 32px;\">\n<p>Cytochrome B558</p>\n<p>Monocyte-chemotactic factor</p>\n<p>Histaminase</p>\n<p>Vitamin B<sub>12<sup>-</sup></sub> binding protein</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.75px; height: 32px;\">Cytochrome B558</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 562.75px; height: 18px;\">\n<p> BPI: bactericidal permeability-increasing protein</p>\n<p>FMLP: N-formyl methionyl-leucyl-phenylalanine </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Primary and secondary granules can fuse with phagocytic vacuoles containing engulfed material. Otherwise, the granule contents can be released into the extracellular space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5588","text":"In which of the following ‌is major basic protein se​en?","raw_text":"In which of the following is major basic protein seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eosin‌ophils","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Baso‌phils","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macro‌phage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Eosinophils","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Eosinophils</strong></p><p>The<strong> major basic protein</strong> is the predominant constituent of the crystalline core of the <strong>eosinophil </strong>granule.</p>\n<p>It is also called <strong>proteoglycan-2 </strong>and is encoded by the<strong> PRG2</strong> gene. This protein is involved in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antiparasitic </strong>defense mechanisms - functions as a cytotoxin and helminthotoxin </li>\n<li>Immune<strong> hypersensitivity </strong>reactions</li>\n<li>Lysis of epithelial cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5589","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega​rding neutrophil extracel​lular traps (NETs)?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They prevent the spread of‌ the microbes by trapping​ them in the fibrils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear chromatin in the N‌ETs are the source of nuc​lear antigens in SLE","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In sepsis, platelet activa‌tion is involved in NET f​ormation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NETs play a major role in ‌phagocytosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NETs play a major role in phagocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NETs play a major role in phagocytosis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Neutrophil extracellular traps (<strong>NETs</strong>) are <strong>not involved</strong> in <strong>phagocytosis</strong>. This is because the nuclei of the neutrophils are lost during NET formation, leading to their death. Hence, they cannot phagocytose pathogens.</p>\n<p>NETs are <strong>networks </strong>of <strong>extracellular fibers</strong> that consist of a viscous meshwork of nuclear chromatin. They <strong>trap microbes</strong> at sites of infection, preventing their further spread. They also contain a high concentration of <strong>antimicrobial proteins</strong> like neutrophil elastase, cathepsin G, and histones that have a high affinity for DNA.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>produced by neutrophils</strong> in response to the following triggers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pathogens<strong> </strong>- Mainly bacteria and fungi</li>\n<li>Inflammatory mediators<strong> -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Chemokines</li>\n<li>Cytokines (mainly interferons)</li>\n<li>Complement proteins</li>\n<li>Reactive oxygen species</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NETs have also been detected in the blood during <strong>sepsis.</strong> In this condition,<strong> platelet activation</strong> is involved in NET formation</p>\n<p>The <strong>nuclear chromatin</strong> in the <strong>NETs</strong> is a <strong>source</strong> of <strong>nuclear antigens</strong> (histones and associated DNA) in systemic autoimmune diseases, particularly systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>). This triggers autoantibody production and further clinical manifestations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"J2oDsr5y","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"4_pharmacokinetics___calculations","title":"4.Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4491","text":"The elimination after 3 ha‌lf-lives in first-order k​inetics is around:","raw_text":"The elimination after 3 half-lives in first-order kinetics is around:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12‌.5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"87‌.5%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9‌4%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 87.5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 87.5%</strong></p><p>After <strong>each half-life, 50%</strong> of the drug is <strong>eliminated</strong> or the plasma concentration becomes half the original value.</p>\n<p>Therefore, after, </p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 half-life = 50% drug is eliminated</li>\n<li>2 half-lives = 75% drug is eliminated</li>\n<li>3 half-lives = <strong>87.5%</strong> is eliminated</li>\n<li>4 half-lives = 93% is eliminated</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4514","text":"You have to give 180 mg of‌ ceftriaxone to a patient​ in a 2 ml syringe that h‍as 10 divisions per ml. T‌he concentration of the d​rug in the vial is 500 mg​/5 ml. How many divisions​ should be filled in 2 ml‍ of the syringe to give 1‍80 mg?","raw_text":"You have to give 180 mg of ceftriaxone to a patient in a 2 ml syringe that has 10 divisions per ml. The concentration of the drug in the vial is 500 mg/5 ml. How many divisions should be filled in 2 ml of the syringe to give 180 mg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌.8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 18","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 18</strong></p><p>The syringe should be filled up to <strong>18 divisions</strong> to deliver 180 mg ceftriaxone to the patient. </p>\n<p>Concentration of drug: 500 mg/5 mL → 100 mg/1 mL</p>\n<p>The syringe has 10 divisions per mL.</p>\n<p>Hence, each division corresponds to 1/10 = 0.1 mL</p>\n<p>The required dosage for the patient is 180 mg that is present in 1.8 mL.  </p>\n<p>0.1mL =1 division, 1.8mL = 18 divisions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4517","text":"500mg of a drug was admini‌stered to a patient. 100m​g was cleared in 1 hour. ‍How much of the drug woul‌d be left in his body aft​er 3 hours if the excreti​on of the drug follows ze​ro-order kinetics?","raw_text":"500mg of a drug was administered to a patient. 100mg was cleared in 1 hour. How much of the drug would be left in his body after 3 hours if the excretion of the drug follows zero-order kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"200‌ mg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"400‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100‌ mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 200 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 200 mg</strong></p><p>In <strong>zero-order kinetics</strong>, a <strong>constant amount</strong> of drug is <strong>eliminated</strong> in <strong>unit time.</strong> </p>\n<p>In this question, 100 mg of the drug is eliminated in 1 hour.</p>\n<p>After 3 hours,</p>\n<p>3 x 100 = 300 mg would be eliminated.</p>\n<p>500-300 =<strong> 200 mg</strong> will be the <strong>remaining drug</strong> in the body. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4515","text":"200 mg of a drug is admini‌stered to a patient, of w​hich 20 mg is excreted in‍ 90 minutes. How much of ‌the drug will remain afte​r 3 hours if it follows f​irst-order kinetics?","raw_text":"200 mg of a drug is administered to a patient, of which 20 mg is excreted in 90 minutes. How much of the drug will remain after 3 hours if it follows first-order kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"162‌ mg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"145.‌8 mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"40‌ mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 162 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 162 mg</strong></p><p>After 3 hours, <strong>162</strong><strong>mg</strong> of the drug will remain.</p>\n<p>In first-order kinetics, a <strong>constant fraction</strong> of the drug is <strong>excreted</strong> in a <strong>given time.</strong> </p>\n<p>In the given question,</p>\n<p>20mg out of 200mg is excreted in 90 minutes, </p>\n<p>20/200 = 0.1 fraction<strong> </strong>is eliminated every 90 minutes.</p>\n<p>After 90 minutes, 200mg - 20 mg = 180 mg remains </p>\n<p>The next 90 minutes (i.e. after 180 minutes) = 180 x 0.1 = 18 mg is eliminated.</p>\n<p>Therefore, 180 - 18 = <strong>162 mg remains.  </strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Simpler way: </strong></p>\n<p>1/10 fraction of the drug is excreted in 90 minutes → 9/10 remains after 90 minutes.</p>\n<p>3 hours (180 min) is 2 times 90 minutes.</p>\n<p>So 200 x (9/10)(9/10) = 162 mg remains after 180 minutes </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4516","text":"Calculate the half-life of‌ a drug X administered to​ a patient if it has a vo‍lume of distribution of 5‌0 liters and clearance of​ 0.1 L/min.","raw_text":"Calculate the half-life of a drug X administered to a patient if it has a volume of distribution of 50 liters and clearance of 0.1 L/min.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"500 m‌inutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"440 m‌inutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"420 m‌inutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"350 m‌inutes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 350 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b90167f94b3b478fb3d9bdf163b48edfx1280x361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 350 minutes</strong></p><p>The <strong>half-life</strong> of this drug is <strong>350 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>In first-order kinetics:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Therefore, </p>\n<p>t1/2 ~ 0.7 x 50/0.1 = 0.7 x 500</p>\n<p><strong>t1/2 ~ 350 minutes</strong> </p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>t1/2 - Plasma half-life</li>\n<li>Vd - Volume of distribution (50)</li>\n<li>CL - Clearance (0.1)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4518","text":"Calculate hepatic clearanc‌e of a drug with hepatic ​blood flow of 2000 mL/min‍ and hepatic extraction r‌atio of 0.5.","raw_text":"Calculate hepatic clearance of a drug with hepatic blood flow of 2000 mL/min and hepatic extraction ratio of 0.5.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4000 ‌mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2000 ‌mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1500 ‌mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1000 ‌mL/min","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1000 mL/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1000 mL/min</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The hepatic clearance is <strong>1000 mL/min</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clearance</strong> of a drug by an organ is the <strong>difference between</strong> the <strong>rate</strong> of <strong>presentation</strong> of the drug<strong> to</strong> the <strong>organ</strong> and the <strong>rate</strong> of <strong>exit</strong> of the drug<strong> from</strong> the <strong>organ.</strong> It is calculated by the equation:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>CL<sub>organ</sub> = Q x E</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Where, </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Q - Organ blood flow </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">E - Extraction ratio (organ-specific)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">In the question, it is given that the hepatic blood flow (Q) is 2000 mL/min and the hepatic extraction ratio (E) is 0.5.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Substituting the values in the above equation, </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>CL<sub>organ</sub> = </strong>2000 X 0.5 <strong>= 1000 mL/min</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: The <strong>extraction ratio (E)</strong> is the fraction of the drug that is removed from the blood by the eliminating organ.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Extraction ratio (E) = C<sub>A</sub>-C<sub>V</sub>/C<sub>A</sub></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Where, </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">C<sub>A</sub> is the concentration of drug presented to the organ</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">C<sub>A</sub>-C<sub>V</sub> is the drug removed by the organ</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4519","text":"A patient is administered ‌drug X that follows first​-order kinetics. 40 mg in‍travenous dose results in‌ a plasma concentration o​f 2mg/L initially and 1mg​/L, 4 hours later. What i​s the clearance of drug X‍?","raw_text":"A patient is administered drug X that follows first-order kinetics.  40 mg intravenous dose results in a plasma concentration of 2mg/L initially and 1mg/L, 4 hours later. What is the clearance of drug X?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 L‌/hr","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 ‌L/hr","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3.5 ‌L/hr","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"7 L‌/hr","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3.5 L/hr","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59ac4b5998694ff49b010e93aeede415x1280x362.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3.5 L/hr</strong></p><p><strong>Time taken</strong> for the <strong>plasma concentration</strong> to drop <strong>to half,</strong></p>\n<p>2 mg/L to 1mg/L = 4 hours (plasma half life-t1/2)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Amount of drug in the body = 40<br/>Initial plasma concentration = 2<br/><br/>Therefore,<br/><br/>Vd = 40/2 = 20 L </p>\n<p><strong>Clearance = 0.693 x Vd / t1/2</strong></p>\n<p>Clearance ~ 0.7 x 20/4</p>\n<p><strong>Clearance ~ 3.5 L/hour </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4530","text":"A male patient weighing 50‌ kg has acute severe asth​ma, for which theophyllin‍e infusion is planned. Th‌e target plasma concentra​tion is 10 mg/L. The volu​me of distribution of the​ophylline is 0.6 L/kg. Wh‍at will be the loading do‍se in this patient for in‌travenous administration?​","raw_text":"A male patient weighing 50 kg has acute severe asthma, for which theophylline infusion is planned. The target plasma concentration is 10 mg/L. The volume of distribution of theophylline is  0.6 L/kg. What will be the loading dose in this patient for intravenous administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"250‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"300‌ mg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"350‌ mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 300 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2daab0c99dbc4a309b3638916161d3dbx1280x361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 300 mg</strong></p><p>The <strong>loading dose</strong> in this patient for intravenous administration is <strong>300mg. </strong></p>\n<p>Target plasma concentration (Cp) = 10 mg/L</p>\n<p>Volume of distribution (Vd) = 0.6 L/kg </p>\n<p>In the above patient, total Vd = 0.6 x 50 = 30 L</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>For intravenous route; F=1</p>\n<p>Where, </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vd - Volume of distribution (liters) </li>\n<li>Cp - target serum level (mcg/L)</li>\n<li>F - bioavailability factor</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Loading dose</strong> = 10 mg/L x 30L = <strong>300 mg</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The loading dose of 300 mg is usually not given as a single intravenous injection; it would be given as an infusion or as divided doses administered over a short period to reduce the toxicity associated with high plasma levels. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4531","text":"Calculate maintenance dose‌ for a drug M with 100% o​ral bioavailability, whic‍h needs a target concentr‌ation of 2 mg/L and clear​ance of 20L/hr and dosed ​QID.","raw_text":"Calculate maintenance dose for a drug M with 100% oral bioavailability, which needs a target concentration of 2 mg/L and clearance of 20L/hr and dosed QID.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"160‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"250‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"240‌ mg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 240 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41b2b6037db546789cb27ecb131fc3afx1280x407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 240 mg</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>maintenance dose</strong> of the drug is <strong>240 mg</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Target plasma concentration (Cp) = 2 mg/L</p>\n<p>Clearance (CL) = 20L/hr</p>\n<p>Assuming dosing interval for QID = every 6th hour = x 6 (since the clearance stated is per hour)</p>\n<p>Bioavailability (100%), F= 1</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Maintenance dose = 2 x 20 x 6/ 1 = <strong>240 mg </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: QID corresponds to 4 times a day. Hence, the dosing interval is 24/4 = 6 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4532","text":"You have started an infusi‌on of drug Y in a patient​ at a rate of 200 mg/hr. ‍Drug Y has Vd of 100 lite‌rs and clearance of 100 m​l/min. What will be the d​rug concentration at a st​eady state?","raw_text":"You have started an infusion of drug Y in a patient at a rate of 200 mg/hr. Drug Y has Vd of 100 liters and clearance of 100 ml/min. What will be the drug concentration at a steady state?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 ‌mg/h","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"25 ‌mg/h","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"36.66‌ mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"33.33‌ mg/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 33.33 mg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 33.33 mg/L</strong></p><p><strong>Steady state concentration </strong>(C<sub>ss</sub>) for this drug is<strong> 33.33mg/L.</strong></p>\n<p>Steady state concentration (<strong>C<sub>ss</sub></strong>) = <strong>Rate of infusion / clearance.</strong></p>\n<p>Rate of infusion = 200 mg/h</p>\n<p>V<sub>d</sub> = 100 L</p>\n<p>Clearance = 100 mL/min</p>\n<p>Clearance = 100 x 60 mL/hr = 6000 mL/hr = 6L/h (converted to L/h)</p>\n<p>C<sub>ss</sub> = (200 mg/hr) / (6L/hr) = 100/3 = <strong>33.33 mg/L</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6482","text":"The clearance of a drug wh‌ich follows first-order k​inetics (in one compartme‍nt model) is 0.2 L/min an‌d volume of distribution ​is 40 L. What is the t1/2​ of that drug?","raw_text":"The clearance of a drug which follows first-order kinetics (in one compartment model) is 0.2 L/min and volume of distribution is 40 L. What is the t1/2 of that drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"140‌ min","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"220‌ min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"240‌ min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"180‌ min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 140 min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 140 min</strong></p><p>The <strong>t<sub>1/2</sub></strong> of the drug would be <strong>140 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>t<sub>1/2</sub> is the <strong>time</strong> required for the <strong>plasma concentration</strong> or the amount of a drug in the <strong>body</strong> to be <strong>reduced</strong> by <strong>50%.</strong> It depends on its <strong>clearance</strong> and <strong>volume</strong> of <strong>distribution.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>t<sub>1/2</sub> = 0.693 x V<sub>d</sub>/Cl</strong></p>\n<p>where, </p>\n<ul>\n<li>V<sub>d</sub> - volume of distribution</li>\n<li>Cl - clearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We can take 0.693 approximately as 0.7</p>\n<p>Substituting,</p>\n<p>t<sub>1/2</sub> = 0.7 x 40 /0.2</p>\n<p>t<sub>1/2</sub> = 0.7 x 200</p>\n<p>t<sub>1/2</sub> = <strong>140 minutes</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical implications: Half-life determines the duration of the drug effect. It also indicates whether the accumulation of the drug will occur under multiple dosage regimens thereby making it is essential to decide on the appropriate dosing interval.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9513","text":"A drug was given IV at a r‌ate of 1.6mg/min. The cle​arance of the drug is 640‍ml/min. The half life of ‌the drug is 1.8 hour. Cal​culate the plasma concent​ration of the drug at ste​ady state.","raw_text":"A drug was given IV at a rate of 1.6mg/min. The clearance of the drug is 640ml/min. The half life of the drug is 1.8 hour. Calculate the plasma concentration of the drug at steady state.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.88‌mg/ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.004‌mg/ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3.55‌mg/ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.002‌mg/ml","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.002mg/ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.002mg/ml</strong></p><p><strong>Plasma steady-state concentration</strong> is achieved when the amount of <strong>drug</strong> being <strong>administered equals</strong> the amount of <strong>drug</strong> being <strong>excreted</strong> from the body.</p>\n<p>The amount of drug being administered = 1.6mg/min</p>\n<p><strong>Amount of drug being excreted = Clearance (CL) x Plasma concentration of the drug (C)</strong></p>\n<p>Clearance = 640ml/min</p>\n<p>Therefore,</p>\n<p>1.6mg/min = 640ml /min ( clearance) x Plasma concentration of the drug</p>\n<p>Hence,</p>\n<p><strong>Plasma concentration</strong> of the drug = 1.6 mg/min / 640 ml/min = <strong>0.002 mg/ml.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Usa_V80Z","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"4_membrane_potentials","title":"4.Membrane Potentials","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA6261","text":"Which of the following equ‌ations is used to calcula‌te the equilibrium potent​ial for an ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following equations is used to calculate the equilibrium potential for an ion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gibbs-Donn‌an equation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nernst ‌equation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Goldmann‌ equation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bernoulli‌ equation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nernst equation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6de0c451718d447bb5bae58f9120f914x1280x735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nernst equation</strong></p><p><strong>Equilibrium potential</strong> for an ion is calculated using the <strong>Nernst equation. </strong></p>\n<p>When there is a concentration gradient for an ion across the membrane, the ion tends to diffuse down the concentration gradient. The electric potential that exactly opposes this net diffusion is called the equilibrium potential for that ion. This is calculated using the Nernst equation.</p>\n<p>It is expressed as the <strong>potential inside the cell</strong> (<strong>intracellular potential relative</strong> to the extracellular potential).</p>\n<p>To calculate the value of the equilibrium potential:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: In this formula, the choice of concentration of the ion placed in the numerator and denominator does not matter because the formula is only used to derive the numerical value and not the sign ( +/- ). Mathematically, log (100/10) = 1  and log (10/100) = - 1. Hence, changing the numerator and denominator only changes the sign and the numerical value remains the same.</p>\n<p>This equation is used <span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\"><strong>only to</strong> derive the <strong>numerical value</strong> of the potential</span>. It is <strong>NOT</strong> used to derive the sign/charge (+/-).</p>\n<p>The <strong>sign/charge</strong> is derived from logic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In a <strong>positive</strong> (+) ion, if the <strong>concentration</strong> of the + ion is <strong>greater outside</strong>, then the + ion <strong>moves</strong> from <strong>outside to inside</strong>. The intracellular potential becomes more + relatively. Hence, the <strong>potential </strong>will be <strong>positive</strong>.</li>\n<li>In a <strong>positive</strong> (+) ion, if the <strong>concentration</strong> of the + ion is <strong>greater inside</strong>, then the + ion <strong>moves</strong> from<strong> inside to outside</strong>. The intracellular potential becomes more - relatively (as + ion moves outside). Hence the <strong>potential</strong> will be <strong>negative</strong>.</li>\n<li>In a <strong>negative</strong> (-) ion, if the concentration of the - ion is <strong>greater outside</strong>, then the - ion <strong>moves </strong>from <strong>outside to inside</strong>. The intracellular potential becomes more - relatively. Hence the <strong>potential</strong> will be <strong>negative</strong>.</li>\n<li>In a <strong>negative</strong> (-) ion, if the concentration of the - ion is <strong>greater inside</strong>, then the - ion <strong>moves</strong> from <strong>inside to outside</strong>. The intracellular potential becomes more + relatively (as - ion moves outside). Hence the<strong> potential </strong>will be <strong>positive</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6579","text":"Which of the following is ‌used to calculate the equ‌ilibrium potential for di​ffusion of multiple ions?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to calculate the equilibrium potential for diffusion of multiple ions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gibbs-Donn‌an equation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nernst ‌equation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Goldmann‌ equation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Henderson‌ equation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Goldmann equation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15a89ced8ec24d908544c74db1aa8f6fx1280x451.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Goldmann equation</strong></p><p>The <strong>Goldman equation</strong> is used to calculate the <strong>diffusion potential</strong> when the <strong>membrane is permeable to several different ions.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Here,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>P= permeability of the membrane to each ion, and</li>\n<li>C= concentrations of the respective ions on the inside (i) and outside (o) of the membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Na<sup>+</sup>, K<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> are the most important ions involved in the development of membrane potentials in nerve fibres and muscle fibres.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6272","text":"Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium e‌xplains the ______","raw_text":"Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium explains the ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Effect of one non-diffusib‌le ion on the distributio‌n of other non-diffusible​ ions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Effect of one permeant ion‌ on the distribution of o‌ther permeant ions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Effect of a permeant ion o‌n the distribution of non‌diffusible ions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Effect of a non-diffusible‌ ion on the distribution ‌of permeant ions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Effect of a non-diffusible ion on the distribution of permeant ions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af4c53f513944cd6998e928061fb866cx1280x902.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/772827bbd1a54c37935b6685400b349dx762x267.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Effect of a non-diffusible ion on the distribution of permeant ions</strong></p><p><strong>Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium</strong> explains the <strong>effect of a non-diffusible ion on the distribution of permeant ions.</strong></p>\n<p>Consider the situation depicted below, </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The membrane is permeable to K<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup>. However, it is impermeable to the proteins. The impermeable protein molecules hinder the movement of K<sup>+</sup> (negative charge of the proteins holding back positive charge of the K<sup>+</sup>) and favor the movement of Cl<sup>-</sup> (like charges repel). According to the <strong>Gibbs-Donnan equation, </strong>at equilibrium, the concentration ratios of diffusible ions are equal across the membrane. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6265","text":"Extracellular concentratio‌n of a positive ion (with‌ valency = 1) is 100mmol/​L and its intracellular c​oncentration is 10mmol/L,​ the potential across the​ membrane using Nernst eq‌uation is (mV)_______","raw_text":"Extracellular concentration of a positive ion (with valency = 1)  is 100mmol/L and its intracellular concentration is 10mmol/L, the potential across the membrane using Nernst equation is (mV)_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-‌60","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-‌10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"+‌60","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. +60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81cfaaf88ebe491292dd7f9a9ac3c0e4x1149x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. +60</strong></p><p>The potential of a <strong>positive</strong> ion across the membrane using Nernst equation is <strong>+60mV.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 24\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Where, z is valency in the formula.</p>\n<p>Deriving the numerical value: </p>\n<p>EMF = 61/1 *log 10/100</p>\n<p>         = 61</p>\n<p>The choice of concentration of the ion placed in the numerator and denominator does not matter because the formula is only used to derive the numerical value and not the sign ( +/- ). Mathematically, log (100/10) = 1  and log (10/100) = - 1. Hence, changing the numerator and denominator only changes the sign and the numerical value remains the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Deciding the sign/charge</strong>: A positive ion with greater concentration extracellularly will try to diffuse inside the cell. A positive intracellular potential is required to prevent this. Hence the sign/charge (potential) is positive (+).</p>\n<p>Hence, the potential across the membrane using the Nernst equation is <strong>+60mV </strong>(the closest answer chosen).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7390","text":"Extracellular concentratio‌n of a negative ion (with‌ valency = 1) is 10mmol/L​ and its intracellular co​ncentration is 100mmol/L.​ The equilibrium potentia​l for this ion using Nern‌st equation is (mV) _____‌","raw_text":"Extracellular concentration of a negative ion (with valency = 1) is 10mmol/L and its intracellular concentration is 100mmol/L. The equilibrium potential for this ion using Nernst equation is (mV) _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"- ‌10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‌0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"- ‌60","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ee8b3a4904d405095c17654d32fe140x1149x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60</strong></p><p>The equilibrium potential for this <strong>negative</strong> ion using the Nernst equation is <strong>+60mV.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where, z is valency in the formula.</p>\n<p>Deriving the numerical value: </p>\n<p>EMF = 61/1 *log 100/10</p>\n<p>         = 61</p>\n<p><strong>Deciding the sign/charge</strong>: A <strong>negative</strong> ion with <strong>greater </strong>concentration <strong>intracellularly</strong> will try to diffuse outside the cell. A <strong>positive</strong> intracellular potential is required to prevent this. Hence, the potential is <strong>positive</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hence, the Nernst potential is +60mV (the closest answer chosen).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6266","text":"What is the Nernst potenti‌al for K+ in milivolt (mV‌)?","raw_text":"What is the Nernst potential for K+ in milivolt (mV)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+‌90","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-‌90","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"+‌70","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-‌70","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. -90","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. -90</strong></p><p>The <strong>Nernst potential </strong>for<strong> K<sup>+ </sup></strong>is <strong>−90mV</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For K<sup>+</sup> ions, the concentration gradient is directed outward and the electrical gradient is directed inward.</li>\n<li>In general, all excitable cell membranes at rest are maximally permeable to K<sup>+</sup> and thus their RMP is close to equilibrium potential of K<sup>+ </sup>ion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6267","text":"Which of the following ion‌s have the highest positi‌ve equilibrium potential?​","raw_text":"Which of the following ions have the highest positive equilibrium potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌a+","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌l-","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"K‌+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Na+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Na+</strong></p><p>The ion with the <strong>highest positive equilibrium potential</strong> is <strong>Na<sup>+ </sup></strong>that<sup> </sup>is <strong>+60mV</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6282","text":"The resting membrane poten‌tial of a nerve fibre dep‌ends on ______","raw_text":"The resting membrane potential of a nerve fibre depends on ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffusion ‌of Na+ ions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Na+-K+ AT‌Pase pump","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffusion ‌of K+ ions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>The <strong>resting membrane potential</strong> (RMP) of a nerve fibre depends on the <strong>diffusion of both K<sup>+</sup> and Na</strong><sup>+</sup> and the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup> ATPase pump</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most <strong>predominant factor</strong>, however, is the <strong>diffusion of K<sup>+</sup> ions. </strong>This is because, at rest, the permeability of the membrane of the nerve fibre to K<sup>+</sup> ions is almost 100 times greater than that to Na<sup>+</sup>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6289","text":"Resting membrane potential‌ of the nerve fibre is eq‌ual to equilibrium potent​ial of ______","raw_text":"Resting membrane potential of the nerve fibre is equal to equilibrium potential of ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌a+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K‌+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌l-","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mg‌2+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cl-","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cl-</strong></p><p><strong>Resting membrane potential</strong> of a <strong>nerve fiber</strong> is equal to the <strong>equilibrium potential of Cl<sup>-</sup></strong><sup>  </sup>and is <strong>-70mV.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6291","text":"Driving force of a charged‌ ion is____","raw_text":"Driving force of a charged ion is____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measured membrane potentia‌l - calculated equilibriu‌m potential","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Measured membrane potentia‌l + calculated equilibriu‌m potential","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calculated equilibrium pot‌ential - Measured membran‌e potential","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Measured membrane potential - calculated equilibrium potential","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Measured membrane potential - calculated equilibrium potential</strong></p><p>The <strong>driving force </strong>on a charged ion is <strong>measured membrane potential (E<sub>m</sub>) - calculated equilibrium potential (E<sub>x</sub>)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Net driving force (mV) = E<sub>m</sub> - E<sub>x</sub></p>\n<p>where,</p>\n<p>E<sub>m</sub> = Actual membrane potential (mV)</p>\n<p>E<sub>x</sub> = Equilibrium potential for X (mV)</p>\n<p>When the driving force is negative (i.e., Em is more negative than the ion’s equilibrium potential), then</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A cation will enter the cell</li>\n<li>An anion will leave the cell</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6293","text":"If the membrane potential ‌of this cell is -80mV the‌ driving force is greates​t for which ion?","raw_text":"If the membrane potential of this cell is -80mV the driving force is greatest for which ion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca‌2+","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌l-","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"K‌+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N‌a+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ca2+","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e48396f95b2454e9bfd0b27aa279499.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ca2+</strong></p><p>The driving force is greatest for Ca<sup>2+</sup>.</p>\n<p>Net <strong>driving force</strong> is the difference between the membrane potential and the equilibrium potential for that particular ion. Hence E<sub>m</sub> - E<sub>X</sub>. Where:</p>\n<p><strong>E<sub>m</sub></strong> = Actual membrane potential (mV) = -80mV</p>\n<p><strong>E<sub>X</sub></strong> = Equilibrium potential for X (mV) </p>\n<p>For the respective ions:</p>\n<p>E <sub>Ca2+ </sub>= +120 ;  Driving force = -80 – (120) = <strong>-200mV</strong></p>\n<p>E <sub>Na+</sub> = +60 ; Driving force= -80 – (60) =-140mV</p>\n<p>E <sub>K+</sub> = -90, Driving force = -80 – (-90) =10mV</p>\n<p>E<sub> Cl</sub>-= -70, Driving force = -80 – (-70) =-10mV</p>\n<p>Thus, Ca<sup>2+</sup> will have the <strong>largest driving force</strong> and it will move inwards or into the cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6295","text":"Which of the following tec‌hniques is used to study ‌the current flow across s​ingle ion channel?","raw_text":"Which of the following techniques is used to study the current flow across single ion channel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patch clam‌p technique","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Voltage cla‌mp technique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iontop‌horesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Galvan‌ometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patch clamp technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5bf64a9809304c788b54ff2050f4195fx800x566.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patch clamp technique</strong></p><p>The <strong>patch-clamp</strong> <strong>technique</strong> is used to <strong>record ion current flow</strong> through a <strong>single protein channel</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In the patch-clamp experiment, a small pipette is carefully maneuvered to seal off a portion of a cell membrane. The pipette has an electrode bathed in an appropriate solution that allows for the recording of electrical changes through any pore in the membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6296","text":"What is the effect on the ‌cellular membrane when th‌e extracellular concentra​tion of K+ is decreased?","raw_text":"What is the effect on the cellular membrane when the extracellular concentration of K+ is decreased?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased positivity of th‌e membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased negativity of RM‌P","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased fluctuations in ‌RMP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased negativity of me‌mbrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased negativity of RMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased negativity of RMP</strong></p><p>When the extracellular concentration of K+ is decreased (hypokalemia), there is increased negativity of RMP.</p>\n<p><strong>RMP</strong> predominantly depends on the <strong>diffusion of K<sup>+</sup> from inside to outside</strong> the cell. This diffusion depends on the concentration gradient with the inside of the cell having a larger concentration of K<sup>+</sup> ions. When the <strong>extracellular concentration of K<sup>+</sup> decreases</strong>, the <strong>concentration gradient for K<sup>+</sup> rises</strong>. Therefore, <strong>more K<sup>+</sup> ions diffuse out</strong> of the cell. This leaves the <strong>inside of the cell more negative</strong> relative to the extracellular space. This increases the negativity of the RMP, leaving the membrane <strong>hyperpolarised.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9300","text":"X, Y, Z are three permeabl‌e ions. X = -50 and Y = -‌30. At RMP, if there is n​o net electrogenic transf​er, what is the value of ​Z?","raw_text":"X, Y, Z are three permeable ions. X = -50 and Y = -30. At RMP, if there is no net electrogenic transfer, what is the value of Z?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+‌20","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-‌20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"+‌80","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"-‌80","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. +80","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. +80</strong></p><p>No net electro genic transfer at RMP means <strong>X + Y + Z = 0</strong></p>\n<p>Since X = -50 and Y = -30, then Z must be <strong>+80</strong> since <strong>(-80 +80 = 0) </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KfM2cUaO","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"4_specific_treatment_modalities","title":"4.Specific Treatment Modalities","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC3260","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is the treatme‌nt modality shown below n‌ot indicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is the treatment modality shown below not indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased intracranial ten‌sion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intractable seizure disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preg‌nancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuroleptic malignant synd‌rome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased intracranial tension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a51fda3928b94859b54c42db43fb023e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased intracranial tension</strong></p><p>Increased intracranial tension is a<strong> relative contraindication</strong> for electroconvulsive therapy (ECT).</p>\n<p>ECT was introduced as a treatment modality for schizophrenia by <strong>Ugo Cerletti. </strong>ECT is useful in the treatment of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severe depression with suicidal risk</strong> (most common indication)</li>\n<li>Treatment-resistant depression</li>\n<li>Catatonia</li>\n<li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder</li>\n<li>Delirium</li>\n<li>Episodic psychoses, atypical psychoses</li>\n<li>Medical conditions such as: \n<ul>\n<li>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</li>\n<li>Hypopituitarism</li>\n<li>Intractable seizure disorders</li>\n<li>The on-off phenomenon of Parkinson's disease.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ECT may also be the treatment of choice for depressed suicidal pregnant women who require treatment and cannot take medication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3381","text":"A patient with severe resi‌stant depression is plann‌ed for electroconvulsive ‌therapy. You ask the nurs‌e to load atropine, a mus‍cle relaxant, and an anes​thetic drug as a part of ​pre-procedure preparation​. Which of the following ​anesthetic agents should ‌he load?","raw_text":"A patient with severe resistant depression is planned for electroconvulsive therapy. You ask the nurse to load atropine, a muscle relaxant, and an anesthetic drug as a part of pre-procedure preparation. Which of the following anesthetic agents should he load?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methoh‌exital","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop‌ental","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methohexital","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methohexital</strong></p><p><strong>Methohexital</strong> is the <strong>most commonly used</strong> anesthetic agent for electroconvulsive therapy, because of its shorter duration of action and <strong>lower association</strong> with <strong>post-ictal arrythmias</strong>. Methohexital is the only anesthetic agent with no intrinsic antiepileptic property.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3313","text":"A patient who is a known c‌ase of bipolar disorder w‌ith psychotic features is‌ currently on the followi‌ng medications. However, ‍he continues to have pres​sured speech, flight of i​deas, hallucinations, and​ suicidal ideations. He i​s planned for electroconv‌ulsive therapy. Which of ‌the following drugs would‌ you stop before the proc‌edure? Diazepam Lithium R‍isperidone Bupropion Mocl‍obemide","raw_text":"<p>A patient who is a known case of bipolar disorder with psychotic features is currently on the following medications. However, he continues to have pressured speech, flight of ideas, hallucinations, and suicidal ideations. He is planned for electroconvulsive therapy. Which of the following drugs would you stop before the procedure?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Diazepam</li>\n<li>Lithium</li>\n<li>Risperidone</li>\n<li>Bupropion</li>\n<li>Moclobemide</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, ‌4 and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p>Lithium, diazepam, and bupropion should be withheld before a session of ECT.</p>\n<p><strong>Lithium</strong> combined with ECT has been shown to cause <strong>prolonged</strong> <strong>seizures </strong>(lasting more than 180 secs) and <strong>increased postictal delirium</strong>. Doses are withheld for 24 hours prior to treatment, allowing for washout.</p>\n<p>Medications to be withheld before ECT:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lithium - causes prolonged seizures and increased postictal delirium</li>\n<li>Benzodiazepines - anticonvulsant activity</li>\n<li>Clozapine - associated with late-appearing seizures</li>\n<li>Buproprion - associated with late-appearing seizures</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Medications that should not be used during ECT:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lidocaine - markedly increases seizure threshold</li>\n<li>Theophylline - increases the duration of seizures</li>\n<li>Reserpine - further compromises RS and CVS</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Tardive seizure is a rare complication of ECT. These are seizures that occur after the completion of treatment once the patient has regained consciousness. The use of <strong>β-lactam antibiotics</strong> and<strong> ciprofloxacin</strong> has been associated with <strong>tardive seizures</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3315","text":"You are observing electroc‌onvulsive therapy being a‌dministered to a psychiat‌ric patient. Which of the‌ following statements is ‍true regarding the proced​ure?","raw_text":"You are observing electroconvulsive therapy being administered to a psychiatric patient. Which of the following statements is true regarding the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An effective seizure shoul‌d last for at least 2 min‌utes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ECT treatments are usually‌ administered twice or th‌rice weekly, non consecut‌ively","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maximum number of times EC‌T can be given in a patie‌nt is 6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ECT treatments are usually administered twice or thrice weekly, non consecutively","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ECT treatments are usually administered twice or thrice weekly, non consecutively</strong></p><p>ECT consists of sessions that are scheduled <strong>2-3 times weekly on nonconsecutive days</strong>. Twice-weekly treatments are associated with less memory impairment than thrice-weekly treatments.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1088\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>For a seizure to be effec­tive in the course of ECT, it should last <strong>at least 25 seconds.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Treatment should<strong> continue until</strong> the patient achieves what is considered the <strong>maximal therapeutic response</strong>. The point of maximal improvement is usually thought to occur when a patient <strong>fails to continue to improve after two consecutive treatments</strong>. Further treatment does not yield any therapeutic benefit but increases the severity and duration of the adverse effects.</p>\n<p>If a patient does not improve after 6 to 10 sessions, bilateral placement and high-density treatment (three times the seizure threshold) should be attempted before ECT is abandoned. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3307","text":"Which of the following sid‌e effects of electroconvu‌lsive therapy persists ev‌en after the treatment co‌urse ends?","raw_text":"Which of the following side effects of electroconvulsive therapy persists even after the treatment course ends?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased anterograde memo‌ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Errors in visual-spatial f‌unction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diminished processing spee‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased retrograde memor‌y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased retrograde memory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased retrograde memory</strong></p><p>A <strong>decrease in retrograde memory</strong> is a side effect of ECT that could persist even after the treatment course ends. Retrograde memory disturbances take<strong> 1-6 months to recover.</strong></p>\n<p>Other cognitive side effects of ECT include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disorientation </li>\n<li>Diminished processing speed </li>\n<li>Decreased anterograde memory</li>\n<li>Errors in visual-spatial function and word-finding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These effects are seen immediately after a treatment session and diminish fairly quickly after the treatment course ends.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6835","text":"Which of the following is ‌an advantage of transcran‌ial magnetic stimulation ‌over electroconvulsive th‌erapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an advantage of transcranial magnetic stimulation over electroconvulsive therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more effective than ‌electroconvulsive therapy‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shorter trea‌tment course","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It induces seizure of shor‌ter duration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It allows for focal stimul‌ation of cerebral cortex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It allows for focal stimulation of cerebral cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It allows for focal stimulation of cerebral cortex</strong></p><p><strong>Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS)</strong> is a brain stimulation method that allows for<strong> focal stimulation</strong> of the cerebral cortex.</p>\n<p>TMS works by inducing <strong>eddy currents</strong> in the brain cortex due to the application of rapidly changing magnetic fields. These currents bring about neuronal stimulation.</p>\n<p>Advantages of TMS over ECT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TMS allows for focal stimulation of brain areas</li>\n<li>It does not induce seizures, but unintended seizure is a known risk of this procedure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Studies have shown TMS to be less effective than ECT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3320","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ the repetitive brain sti‌mulation technique shown ‌below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the repetitive brain stimulation technique shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be used as a monoth‌erapy for schizophrenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used as a monoth‌erapy in resistant depres‌sion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Its effects are the same a‌s that of an MRI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be used as a first ‌line treatment for depres‌sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can be used as a monotherapy in resistant depression","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1acc6e9e45fa4819b2e29e78395d4caa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can be used as a monotherapy in resistant depression</strong></p><p>Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) is approved for <strong>monotherapy in resistant depression</strong>. It uses the property of magnetic fields to induce electrical activity in the brain. </p>\n<p>It is different from MRI, as an <strong>MRI</strong> just <strong>observes</strong> but <strong>rTMS</strong> produces <strong>changes</strong> in the activity of the brain. </p>\n<p><strong>rTMS</strong> can be used <strong>in addition to antipsychotics</strong> to treat resistant <strong>schizophrenia.</strong> However, it is not used as monotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3322","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about deep brain sti‌mulation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about deep brain stimulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Three tiny neurostimulator‌s are placed under the sk‌in of the clavicle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Done under computed tomogr‌aphy guidance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces symptoms associate‌d with movement disorders‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Swimming skills remain int‌act after the procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reduces symptoms associated with movement disorders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f47e8586fce8496bb605ed918387c90ax414x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reduces symptoms associated with movement disorders</strong></p><p><strong>Deep brain stimulation</strong> (DBS)<strong> reduces</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong> associated with<strong> movement disorders</strong> such as Parkinson's disease. It provides a dramatic response with respect to <strong>off time</strong> and <strong>dyskinesias</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, it does not improve features that fail to respond to levodopa and does not prevent the development or progression of non-dopaminergic features such as freezing, falling, dementia, etc.</p>\n<p>It is an invasive procedure performed using MRI-guided stereotactic techniques in which <strong>electrodes</strong> are implanted in the brain and are connected via a <strong>subcutaneous wire</strong> to a maximum of two <strong>neurostimulators</strong> which are placed under the skin of the clavicle.</p>\n<p><strong>Impaired swimming skills</strong> surfaced as an <strong>unexpected risk</strong> of the procedure; several Parkinson's disease patients lost their ability to swim after receiving deep brain stimulation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3266","text":"A patient with a severe, t‌reatment-resistant neurop‌sychiatric condition is r‌eferred to the neurosurge‌ry OPD. The surgeon propo‍ses a burr-hole procedure​ where the given sites ar​e destroyed via thermocoa​gulation. Which of the fo​llowing conditions is the‌ patient likely to be suf‌fering from?","raw_text":"A patient with a severe, treatment-resistant neuropsychiatric condition is referred to the neurosurgery OPD. The surgeon proposes a burr-hole procedure where the given sites are destroyed via thermocoagulation. Which of the following conditions is the patient likely to be suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obsessive-compulsive disor‌der","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ma‌nia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alzheimer'‌s dementia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antisocial personality dis‌order","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Obsessive-compulsive disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa238c6f54564eb89070cdc1975b62ab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Obsessive-compulsive disorder</strong></p><p>The given image is that of<strong> anterior cingulotomy</strong> (ACING), which is a neurosurgical treatment for <strong>obsessive-compulsive disorder</strong>. Anterior cingulotomy involves creating bilateral lesions in the anterior cingulate cortex<strong> </strong>under stereotactic guidance. </p>\n<p><strong>Subcaudate tractotomy</strong> (capsulotomy) can also be used in the management of treatment-unresponsive OCD.</p>\n<p><strong>Prefrontal lobotomy</strong> was introduced by <strong>Egas Moniz</strong> as a method of psychosurgery for treatment-resistant mental disorders, particularly <strong>major depressive disorder </strong>and<strong> OCD.</strong></p>\n<p>Other indications for psychosurgery include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic and <strong>incapacitating</strong> <strong>schizophrenia</strong> with <strong>severe depression</strong> not responding to all modes of treatment.</li>\n<li>Chronic and <strong>incapacitating</strong> <strong>anxiety disorder</strong> not responding to all modes of treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3334","text":"Which of the following cha‌racteristics of a patient‌ is favourable for using ‌psychoanalysis as a thera‌py?","raw_text":"Which of the following characteristics of a patient is favourable for using psychoanalysis as a therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High frustration tolerance‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Concrete‌ thinking","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extreme d‌ishonesty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ongoing major upheaval in ‌life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High frustration tolerance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High frustration tolerance</strong></p><p><strong>High frustration tolerance</strong> is <strong>favorable</strong> for psychoanalysis. Psychoanalysis involves bringing repressed memories and feelings to the surface and resolving unconscious conflicts through understanding.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Patient characteristics favorable for psychoanalysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Significant suffering and a genuine wish to understand themselves.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Ability to withstand frustration and anxiety that might emerge during analysis.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">A reasonable, mature superego that allows them to be honest with the analyst. </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Average intelligence.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Psychologically minded, that is, being able to think abstractly and symbolically about the unconscious meanings of their behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Patient characteristics unfavorable for psychoanalysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The absence of suffering and low motivation to understand.</li>\n<li>Inability to tolerate frustration and anxiety with poor impulse control.</li>\n<li>Extreme dishonesty or antisocial personality disorder.</li>\n<li>Low intelligence - inability to understand the process.</li>\n<li>Concrete thinking or the absence of psychological mindedness.</li>\n<li>Serious physical illness, an ongoing major upheaval in life like job loss or divorce.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3325","text":"During Ross's marriage to ‌Emily, when the wedding o‌fficiant asked him to rep‌eat “I Ross, take thee, E‌mily, as my lawfully wedd‍ed wife”, Ross says Rache​l's name instead. Ross an​d Rachel have dated on mu​ltiple occasions in the p​ast. This unconscious act‌ of revealing his feeling‌s can be best described b‌y which of the following ‌terms?","raw_text":"During Ross's marriage to Emily, when the wedding officiant asked him to repeat “I Ross, take thee, Emily, as my lawfully wedded wife”, Ross says Rachel's name instead. Ross and Rachel have dated on multiple occasions in the past. This unconscious act of revealing his feelings can be best described by which of the following terms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parap‌raxis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transf‌erence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Repre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Displa‌cement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parapraxis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parapraxis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">When a person reveals his feelings <strong>unconsciously</strong> by a slip of tongue, it is known as <strong>parapraxis</strong>. Freud ascribed parapraxis to unconscious motives.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B: <strong>Transference - </strong>A<strong> </strong>situation where the feelings, desires, and expectations of one person are redirected and applied to another person. Most commonly, transference occurs in a therapeutic setting, where a person in therapy <strong>unconsciously </strong>develop feelings towards the therapist.<br/><br/>Option C: <strong>Repression - </strong>is a key concept of psychoanalysis, where it is understood as a defense mechanism. It ensures that what is <strong>unacceptable</strong> to the <strong>conscious mind</strong> - and would, if recalled arouse anxiety - is <strong>prevented </strong>from entering into it.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-2\"></sup></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Displacement -</strong> Feelings that are connected with one person are displaced onto another person. E.g., a man who has had a bad day at the office, comes home and yells at his wife and children, is displacing his anger from the workplace onto his family.</p>\n<p><strong>Catharsis: </strong>The expression of ideas, thoughts, and suppressed emotions by the patient which provides a sense of relief. It is also known as <strong>ventilation</strong>. In catharsis, the patient <strong>reveals information voluntarily</strong> and consciously whereas, in parapraxis, patient details are revealed unconsciously, by slip of tongue.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3335","text":"A patient was referred to ‌a psychiatrist for his ne‌urosis. He was a witness ‌to a road traffic acciden‌t wherein a pedestrian wa‍s overrun. He refuses to ​go out on the road fearin​g that every time he woul​d step out he would witne​ss an accident. What kind‌ of maladaptive assumptio‌n does this patient displ‌ay?","raw_text":"A patient was referred to a psychiatrist for his neurosis. He was a witness to a road traffic accident wherein a pedestrian was overrun. He refuses to go out on the road fearing that every time he would step out he would witness an accident. What kind of maladaptive assumption does this patient display?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selective ‌abstraction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Catastr‌ophising","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temporal ‌causality","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dichotomou‌s thinking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Temporal causality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Temporal causality</strong></p><p>The patient believes 'if it has been true in the past, it's always going to be true' which is demonstrative of <strong>maladaptive assumption</strong> of <strong>temporal causality.</strong></p>\n<p>Behavioral therapy techniques are used to test and change maladaptive and inaccurate cognitions. The overall purpose of such techniques is to help the patients understand the inaccuracy of their cognitive assumptions and learn new strategies and ways of dealing with issues. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 196px; width: 433px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\"><strong>Cognitive Error</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\"><strong>Assumption</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Overgeneralizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">If it's true in one case, it applies to any case that is even slightly similar.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Selective abstraction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">The only events that matter are failures, deprivation, etc. Should measure self by errors, weaknesses, etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Excessive responsibility (assuming<br/>personal causality)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">I am responsible for all bad things, failures, etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Assuming temporal causality (predicting<br/>without sufficient evidence)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">If it has been true in the past, it's always going to be true.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Self-references</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">I am the center of everyone's attention especially my bad performances. I am the cause of misfortunes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Catastrophizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">Always think of the worst. It's most likely to happen to you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.694px;\">Dichotomous thinking</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.306px;\">Everything is either one extreme or another (black or white, good or bad).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3326","text":"During a session of cognit‌ive-behavioral therapy, t‌he patient reported feeli‌ng distressed about the f‌act that his friend did n‍ot answer his call when h​e had called him the prev​ious day. When asked what​ thought went through his​ mind at that time, he re‌plied \"I do not fit anywh‌ere in this world.\" What ‌is the next step the ther‌apist should follow in th‍e session?","raw_text":"During a session of cognitive-behavioral therapy, the patient reported feeling distressed about the fact that his friend did not answer his call when he had called him the previous day.  When asked what thought went through his mind at that time, he replied  \"I do not fit anywhere in this world.\" What is the next step the therapist should follow in the session?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elicit the automatic thoug‌ht","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Test the validity of the m‌aladaptive assumption the‌ patient is making","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Identify the maladaptive a‌ssumption the patient is ‌making","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Test the validity of the a‌utomatic thought","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Test the validity of the automatic thought","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Test the validity of the automatic thought</strong></p><p>The therapist first elicited the <strong>automatic thought</strong> the patient made, which is \"I do not fit anywhere in this world\". The <strong>next step</strong> would be to <strong>test the validity</strong> of this automatic thought.</p>\n<p>Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT)<strong> </strong>is based on the psychological significance of the <strong>cognitive triad</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">An individual's beliefs about themselves</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Their personal world (including the people in their lives)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Their future.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Problematic and biased interpretations</strong> related to this cognitive triad may lead to <strong>excessive emotional distress</strong>.</p>\n<p>CBT is applied to identify these faulty ideas and beliefs, make the patient aware of the underlying assumptions that drive them, and modifying their responses to them.</p>\n<p>The three components of cognitive therapy are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Didactic aspects:</strong> Explaining to patients the cognitive triad, schemas, and faulty logic. The therapist must explain all the aspects of treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitive techniques</strong>: These involve four steps-\n<ul>\n<li>Eliciting <strong>automatic thoughts </strong>- Asking the patient about the thought that causes the emotional reaction in them.</li>\n<li>Testing validity of the automatic thoughts</li>\n<li>Identifying the general <strong>maladaptive assumptions </strong>(a belief that is false and rationally unsupported)</li>\n<li>Testing the validity of maladaptive assumptions</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Behavioral techniques </strong>like relaxation techniques and desensitization to the stimulus etc.<strong><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>CBT is used in the treatment of anxiety and panic disorders, depression, phobia, OCD, personality disorders, and somatoform disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3403","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients with personality di‌sorders is most likely to‌ benefit from dialectical‌ behavior therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with personality disorders is most likely to benefit from dialectical behavior therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 18-year-old with traits ‌of criminality, impulsivi‌ty, hostility, manipulati‌on, and disregard for oth‌ers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 20-year-old with labile ‌emotions, dramatic speech‌, who is sexually provoca‌tive with attention seeki‌ng behaviours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 22-year-old with traits ‌of unstable mood, impulsi‌vity, suicidality, fear o‌f abandonment, and unstab‌le self-image","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 24-year-old who is preoc‌cupied with order, perfec‌tionism, and is excessive‌ly devoted to work","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 22-year-old with traits of unstable mood, impulsivity, suicidality, fear of abandonment, and unstable self-image","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 22-year-old with traits of unstable mood, impulsivity, suicidality, fear of abandonment, and unstable self-image</strong></p><p><strong>Dialectical behavior therapy</strong> is a type of psychotherapy used commonly in the management of <strong>borderline personality</strong> <strong>disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>A 22-year-old with traits of unstable mood, impulsivity, suicidality, fear of abandonment, and unstable self-image fits the description of borderline personality disorder as per DSM-5 criteria.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In DBT, the goal is to <strong>improve interpersonal skills</strong> and <strong>decrease self-destructive behavior</strong> using techniques involving advice, metaphor, storytelling, and confrontation, among others.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: This describes a patient with <strong>antisocial personality disorde</strong>r.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: This describes a patient with<strong> histrionic personality disorder.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: This describes a patient with <strong>obsessive-compulsive personality disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3261","text":"In which of the following ‌instances can a psycholog‌ist use systematic desens‌itization as a treatment ‌option?","raw_text":"In which of the following instances can a psychologist use systematic desensitization as a treatment option?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 18-year-old with BMI 22,‌ recurrent episodes of bi‌nge eating and purging fo‌r past 3 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 24-year-old with an irra‌tional fear of contaminat‌ion who washes her hands ‌every 5 minutes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 22-year-old with feeling‌s of worthlessness, psych‌omotor retardation, insom‌nia, fatigue, and anhedon‌ia for 2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 20-year-old with disorga‌nized behavior, delusions‌ of persecution and audit‌ory hallucinations for 4 ‌months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 24-year-old with an irrational fear of contamination who washes her hands every 5 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 24-year-old with an irrational fear of contamination who washes her hands every 5 minutes</strong></p><p><strong>Systematic desensitization</strong> is one of the behavioral therapy techniques for <strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder </strong>(A 24-year-old with an irrational fear of contamination and washes hands every 5 minutes). It is also used in the treatment of <strong>phobias.</strong></p>\n<p>Systemic desensitization is based on the behavioral principle of <strong>counter-conditioning</strong>. Here, the person overcomes anxiety elicited by a situation or an object by approaching the feared situation gradually and<strong> </strong>in a relaxed state.</p>\n<p>Systematic desensitization consists of three steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relaxation training -</strong> patients attain a state of complete relaxation and are then exposed to the stimulus that elicits the anxiety response. The negative reaction of anxiety is inhibited by the relaxed state, a process called <strong><span class=\"s1\">reciprocal inhibition.</span></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hierarchy construction -</strong> Patients and therapists prepare a graded list (or hierarchy) of fear-provoking scenes in the order of increasing anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Desensitization of the stimulus -</strong> Patients proceed systematically through the list from the least to the most anxiety-provoking scene while in a deeply relaxed state.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Describes a patient with <strong>bulimia nervosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Describes a patient with <strong>major depressive episode.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Describes a patient with <strong>schizophreniform disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3341","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systematic desensitisation‌ is based on the principl‌e of counter conditioning‌ and reciprocal inhibitio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"When flooding, the patient‌ is exposed to the fear a‌ccording to a hierarchy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"In therapeutic graded expo‌sure, relaxation training‌ is not involved","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eye movement desensitisati‌on is used in post trauma‌tic stress disorder and p‌hobias","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. When flooding, the patient is exposed to the fear according to a hierarchy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. When flooding, the patient is exposed to the fear according to a hierarchy</strong></p><p>In <strong>flooding</strong>, the patient is exposed to their fear, but <strong>without any hierarchy</strong> i.e, there is no gradual exposure as in systematic desensitization. Patients learn to remain in the feared situation until they feel calm.</p>\n<p><strong>Systematic desensitization - </strong>employs counter conditioning and reciprocal inhibition. It is used in the treatment of <strong>OCD and phobias.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Therapeutic graded exposure - </strong>similar to systematic desensitization, but there is<strong> no relaxation training. </strong>The treatment is carried out in a real-life context.</p>\n<p><strong>Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing (EMDR) </strong>- inducing saccades (quick, simultaneous movement of both eyes between two or more phases of fixation) when a person is imagining an anxiety-producing event. The saccades can yield a positive thought which decreases anxiety. It is used in <strong>PTSD and phobias.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3375","text":"A patient suffering from a‌ phobia of contamination ‌is asked by his therapist‌ to follow behind him, to‌uch every object he touch‍es, and observe and learn​ from his behavior. Durin​g this process, the thera​pist talks calmly to the ​patient. The patient is t‌old to repeat this twice ‌daily. Name the technique‌ applied by the therapist‌.","raw_text":"A patient suffering from a phobia of contamination is asked by his therapist to follow behind him, touch every object he touches, and observe and learn from his behavior. During this process, the therapist talks calmly to the patient. The patient is told to repeat this twice daily. Name the technique applied by the therapist.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Participan‌t modeling","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aversion‌ therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Floo‌ding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive re‌inforcement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Participant modeling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Participant modeling</strong></p><p>The patient is made to learn a new behaviour when exposed to his fear, by observation and imitation of the therapist. This therapy is called <strong>participant modeling</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong> Aversion therapy</strong> - This is to <strong>supress</strong> an <strong>unwanted behavior</strong> or a habit by <strong>pairing</strong> it with a negative or <strong>unpleasant stimulus</strong>. For example, an alcohol-dependent patient is made to vomit (by adding an emetic to the alcohol) every time a drink is ingested is effective in treating alcohol dependence.</p>\n<p><strong>Flooding -</strong> It involves repeated and prolonged exposure to fear cues of high intensity without relaxation<strong>. </strong>Patients learn to remain in the feared situation until they feel calm.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 899\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Positive reinforcement</strong> - When a behavioral response is followed by a reward­ing event.  </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3542","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g the scientist who perfo‌rmed the famous experimen‌t illustrated below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the scientist who performed the famous experiment illustrated below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He developed the concept o‌f learning by association‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"He developed the theory of‌ classical conditioning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He studied the physiology ‌of digestion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"He gave the model for oper‌ant conditioning","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. He gave the model for operant conditioning","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/259735809e1c417ab48e6c65d97e1041.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d927c604d6c4572afc3181efaad5621x1280x3468.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. He gave the model for operant conditioning</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The experiment shown in the image is that of <strong>classical conditioning </strong>by Ivan Pavlov. The theory of <strong>operant conditioning</strong> was given by <strong>Skinner</strong> (not Pavlov). It is a method of learning that occurs through <strong>rewards and punishments</strong> for behavior.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Classical conditioning is the theory of learning in which a natural response (salivation) is elicited by a conditioned or learned stimulus (bell), that was previously presented with an unconditioned stimulus (food). It is also called <strong>learning by association</strong> or respondent conditioning. He's also known for his work on the <strong>physiology of digestion</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3263","text":"A child was not allowed to‌ play because he did not ‌finish his homework. The ‌mother used which of the ‌following techniques to d‍iscipline her child?","raw_text":"A child was not allowed to play because he did not finish his homework. The mother used which of the following techniques to discipline her child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive ‌punishment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Negative ‌punishment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Positive re‌inforcement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Negative re‌inforcement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Negative punishment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91debdd71967491496c3564488f46478x1280x1595.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Negative punishment</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Negative punishment</strong> is <strong>withholding the reward</strong> (permission to go and play) whenever the<strong> undesired behavior</strong> is manifested (not completing the homework).</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Operant conditioning</strong> is a learning process in which a particular behavior is modified by <strong>either</strong> resulting in a<strong> reward or punishment</strong>. It deals with <strong>voluntary responses</strong> because the respondent is free to choose a behavior depending on the desired consequence.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The image below shows Skinner's operant conditioning quadrant.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Types of Operant conditioning:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"height: 729px; width: 430px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Behavior</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.2257px;\"><strong>Stimulus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.774px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px;\">Positive reinforcement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Good behavior increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.2257px;\">\n<p>When stimulus added.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.774px;\">When a child studies, he is given chocolate. So, now he studies every day to get the chocolate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px;\">Negative reinforcement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Good behavior increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.2257px;\">So that stimulus is avoided.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.774px;\">A child studies to avoid getting scolded by his mother.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px;\">Positive punishment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Bad behavior decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.2257px;\">When stimulus added.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.774px;\">A child was given an imposition because he did not do his homework. He does his homework sincerely now.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px;\">Negative punishment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Bad behavior decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.2257px;\">So that stimulus is removed.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.774px;\">A child fails to do his homework, so he is not allowed to play. Next day onwards, he does his homework every day.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Note: The question is about bad behavior i.e. not doing the homework. The focus is on decreasing bad behavior, hence the answer would be punishment (positive or negative). Reinforcement (either positive or negative) applies only to good behavior.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3265","text":"A young man who started us‌ing cannabis has become i‌ncreasingly dependant on ‌it psychologically. He wa‌s diagnosed with cannabis‍ use disorder and motivat​ional enhancement therapy​ was started to help him.​ Which of the following i​s not a stage of this the‌rapy?","raw_text":"A young man who started using cannabis has become increasingly dependant on it psychologically. He was diagnosed with cannabis use disorder and motivational enhancement therapy was started to help him. Which of the following is not a stage of this therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prepa‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contem‌plation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Condit‌ioning","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maint‌enance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Conditioning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Conditioning</strong></p><p>Conditioning is not a stage of motivational enhancement therapy.</p>\n<p>The stages of motivational enhancement therapy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-contemplation</strong> - People are often <strong>unaware</strong> that their behavior (eg. alcohol abuse) is problematic or produces negative consequences.</li>\n<li><strong>Contemplation </strong>- People recognize their problem and begin to think about changing their behavior. They <strong>weigh the pros and cons of change</strong>. People may still feel <strong>ambivalent</strong> toward changing their behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Preparation (Determination) </strong>- People have <strong>made a decision</strong> to change their habits and are ready to take action. People start to take small steps toward behavior change.</li>\n<li><strong>Action </strong>- People have recently <strong>changed their behavior</strong> and intend to keep moving forward with that behavior change</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance</strong> - People have <strong>sustained their behavior change</strong> for a while and intend to maintain the behavior change going forward. People work to prevent relapse to earlier stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Termination -</strong> In this stage, people have <strong>no desire </strong>to return to their <strong>unhealthy behaviors</strong> and are sure they will not relapse. Since this is rarely reached, people tend to stay in the maintenance stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3520","text":"A chronic smoker who was r‌ecently diagnosed with CO‌PD finally decided to qui‌t smoking. He often think‌s about stopping it but i‍s reluctant due to the fe​ar that he might become t​oo irritable and restless​ without it. Which of the​ following options best d‌escribes the stage he is ‌in?","raw_text":"A chronic smoker who was recently diagnosed with COPD finally decided to quit smoking. He often thinks about stopping it but is reluctant due to the fear that he might become too irritable and restless without it. Which of the following options best describes the stage he is in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre cont‌emplation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contem‌plation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prepa‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contemplation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contemplation</strong></p><p>In the given scenerio, the patient is in the stage of <strong>contemplation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The patient is aware that he has a problem, and he is thinking about changing his behaviour. He is <strong>weighing the pros and cons</strong> of behaviour modification. This is characteristic of the stage of <strong>contemplation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Addiction recovery usually involves the following five stages</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Precontemplation</li>\n<li>Contemplation</li>\n<li>Preparation</li>\n<li>Action</li>\n<li>Maintenance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3336","text":"A chronic alcoholic who is‌ motivated to quit drinki‌ng is prescribed disulfir‌am by his physician. Whic‌h of the following was em‍ployed by the physician t​o modify the patient's be​havior?","raw_text":"A chronic alcoholic who is motivated to quit drinking is prescribed disulfiram by his physician. Which of the following was employed by the physician to modify the patient's behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive re‌inforcement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systematic desensitization‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aversion‌ therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Floo‌ding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aversion therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aversion therapy</strong></p><p>In the given scenerio <strong>aversion therapy</strong> is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Aversion</strong>: When a <strong>noxious stimulus/punishment</strong> is given after a specific behavior, the behavior is eventually inhibited. It is used in the treatment of alcohol abuse, paraphilias, opioid addicts.</p>\n<p><strong>Disulfiram</strong> is an <strong>irreversible inhibitor </strong>of both cytosolic and mitochondrial forms of<strong> aldehyde dehydrogenase</strong>. Ethanol consumption during disulfiram therapy can lead to <strong>increased</strong> concentrations of <strong>acetaldehyde</strong>. Increased acetaldehyde levels can cause flushing, pulsating headache, throbbing pain in head and neck, and other aversive reactions.</p>\n<p>Disulfiram can only be given to <strong>highly motivated individuals</strong>. It should not be administered until the person has abstained from alcohol for at least 12 hours. The disulfiram-alcohol reaction may occur as long as <strong>1 or 2 weeks</strong> after the last dose of disulfiram.</p>\n<p>Disulfiram like reaction with alcohol ingestion is also seen with the following drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n<li>Sulfonylureas like chlorpropamide</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins like cefoperazone, cefamandole.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LrclQRnu","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"4_pleural_effusion__pneumothorax__pulmonary_edema_and_pulmonary_embolism","title":"4.Pleural Effusion, Pneumothorax, Pulmonary Edema And Pulmonary Embolism","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA0727","text":"Chest X-ray is most common‌ly performed in which of ​the following views?","raw_text":"Chest X-ray is most commonly performed in which of the following views?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PA ‌view","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"AP ‌view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latera‌l view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obliqu‌e view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PA view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85979d375fde4f0f9adc9d4555cb5988x1280x1339.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6612073a7cc54d9d92bad4fa1de9e8d6x1280x1363.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PA view</strong></p><p><strong>Chest X-ray</strong> is most commonly performed in <strong>posteroanterior view (PA view)</strong> because it minimizes the magnification of the cardiac silhouette. </p>\n<p>In PA view, the anterior structures are closer to the detector. It is usually done in <strong>full inspiration</strong> and <strong>standing</strong> position. Ideally, there should be <strong>no rotation.</strong> If there is no rotation the distance between the medial end of the clavicle and the spinous process will be equidistant, on both sides. In a normal chest X-ray, the left dome of the diaphragm is <strong>lower</strong> than the right because the heart pushes the left dome down.</p>\n<p>In anterior-to-posterior (<strong>AP</strong>) radiographs, the posterior structures are closer to the detector.  </p>\n<p>Identification of PA and AP view chest X-ray:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 415px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.438px;\"><strong>PA view</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.562px;\"><strong>AP view</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"><strong>Clavicles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.438px;\">Seen over the lung fields</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.562px;\">Seen above the apex of lung fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"><strong>Scapula</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.438px;\">The inner border is seen away from lung fields</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.562px;\">The inner border is seen over the lung fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"><strong>Ribs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.438px;\">Posterior ribs are more visible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.562px;\">Anterior ribs are more visible</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0728","text":"What is the best chest X-r‌ay view to visualise the ​right lung?","raw_text":"What is the best chest X-ray view to visualise the right lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right anter‌ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left anter‌ior oblique","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right poste‌rior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left poster‌ior oblique","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left anterior oblique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/908464067c7943a6bb26f96c3652de68x1280x806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left anterior oblique</strong></p><p>The best view to visualize the <strong>right lung</strong> is the <strong>left anterior oblique.</strong> For the left lung, it is the right anterior oblique. </p>\n<p>The <strong>left anterior oblique</strong> view demonstrates the maximum area of the<strong> right lung field</strong>. It is also used for the assessment of both the <strong>ventricles</strong>,<strong> left atrium</strong>, aortic window, and <strong>aorta</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right anterior oblique</strong> view demonstrates the maximum area of the<strong> left lung field</strong>. It is used for the assessment of the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong>, <strong>right ventricle</strong>, and <strong>right atrium</strong></p>\n<p>Both these views are also used in coronary angiography.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the right anterior oblique (image 1) and the left anterior oblique (image 2) views of chest radiograph:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0745","text":"What is the best view to v‌isualise middle lobe path​ologies?","raw_text":"What is the best view to visualise middle lobe pathologies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left anter‌ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PA ‌view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lordot‌ic view","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obliqu‌e view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lordotic view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/013bd21e31044fec93b3b3f071ead8dbx1280x1465.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5626df0a00d94e7186b4d1bbcc444063x1280x1054.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lordotic view</strong></p><p><strong>Middle lobe pathologies</strong> are better seen on <strong>lordotic view</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The <strong>lordotic AP projection </strong>brings the displaced fissure into the<strong> </strong>line of the x-ray and elegantly demonstrates the <strong>middle lobe pathologies</strong>. The lordotic view is suitable in apical lung pathologies and middle lobe or lingula pathology. </p>\n<p>The image depicts a normal X-ray chest in lordotic view:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0729","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures do not form the h​ilar shadow in a chest X-‌ray?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures do not form the hilar shadow in a chest X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bron‌chus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower lo‌be veins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Upper lo‌be veins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lower lobe veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lower lobe veins</strong></p><p><strong>Lower lobe veins</strong> are not part of the radiological hilum.</p>\n<p>The radiological hilum is formed by the <strong>pulmonary arteries</strong> (and their bifurcations), the <strong>upper lobe veins</strong>, and the<strong> bronchi.</strong> The lower lobe pulmonary veins, which pass inferiorly, form no component of the normal hilar shadow. Lymph nodes only contribute to the shadow when enlarged.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0730","text":"A 25-year-old male with Ma‌rfanoid features presente​d with sudden onset breat‌hlessness. The chest X-ra‍y is given below. What is​ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old male with Marfanoid features presented with sudden onset breathlessness. The chest X-ray is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibr‌osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pneumothorax","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cea5bf53bb95469c8fbb2f9abe7d4dbb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ee5055876e24b769bd3024f9a6eeba5x720x663.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9502666a59f747688d61e9ca1a5f4379x1224x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pneumothorax</strong></p><p>The chest X-ray demonstrating <strong>tracheal deviation</strong> and<strong> displacement </strong>of the <strong>heart</strong> away from the affected side along with the partial collapse of the left lung in the above condition points to a diagnosis of<strong> left-sided pneumothorax</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Marfan's syndrome</strong> is one of the causes for seconday spontaneous pneumothorax in adults. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> refers to the collection of air in the pleural space. </p>\n<p>Chest radiograph in spontaneous pneumothorax demonstrates:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thin, sharp white line</strong>, demarcating the visceral pleural edge</li>\n<li><strong>Absence of lung markings</strong> seen peripheral to this line</li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral space radiolucent</strong> compared to adjacent lung </li>\n<li><strong>Complete lung collapse</strong>, as well as <strong>subcutaneous emphysema</strong> and <strong>pneumomediastinum</strong>, may be seen</li>\n<li><strong>Deep sulcus sign</strong> (on supine chest radiograph)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Deep sulcus sign</strong>: On a <strong>supine position</strong>(common in the ICU), air collects anteriorly and basally, within the<strong> </strong>non-dependent portions of the pleural space, <strong>abnormally deepening</strong> the costophrenic angle. This sign is indicative of <strong>pneumothorax</strong> in such patients and may be the only visible clue, especially when patient can not be imaged upright.</p>\n<p>The image below is plain chest x-ray showing pneumothorax with deep sulcus sign (shown by arrow):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When there is a progressive accumulation of air intrapleurally in the thoracic cavity (caused by a valve effect during inspiration and expiration), it is called <strong>Tension pneumothorax</strong>. It can result in compression of mediastinal structures and compromise cardiopulmonary function. It is <strong>life-threatening</strong> and requires emergency intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0731","text":"A 62-year-old male with CO‌PD presents with sudden o​nset chest pain and short‌ness of breath. On auscul‍tation, breath sounds wer​e absent on the left side​. What is the best chest ​x-ray view to diagnose th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old male with COPD presents with sudden onset chest pain and shortness of breath. On auscultation, breath sounds were absent on the left side. What is the best chest x-ray view to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PA view in full inspiratio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PA view in full expiration‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AP view in full inspiratio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AP view in full expiration‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PA view in full expiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15974567f2ea48f8bd0500dc63f00123x877x807.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PA view in full expiration</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has<strong> pneumothorax. </strong>The best chest x-ray view to diagnose a pneumothorax is<strong> PA view</strong> <strong>in full expiration</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Expiratory chest X-ray</strong> is done in pneumothorax, conditions causing air trapping (foreign body, partial bronchial obstruction), and diaphragmatic palsy. </p>\n<p>Image showing left-sided pneumothorax: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0732","text":"All of the following featu‌res can be seen in patien​ts with pneumothorax exce‌pt:","raw_text":"All of the following features can be seen in patients with pneumothorax except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep sul‌cus sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mediastinal shift to the i‌psilateral side","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Area of hypertranslucency ‌on the ipsilateral side","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collapse of ipsilateral lu‌ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mediastinal shift to the ipsilateral side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1e1c2975a9d4dacbc69a0d672394292x597x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mediastinal shift to the ipsilateral side</strong></p><p>In pneumothorax, especially tension pneumothorax there is a<strong> mediastinal shift</strong> <strong>to the</strong> <strong>contralateral </strong><strong>side</strong> of the pneumothorax, <strong>not to the ipsilateral side</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Chest radiograph in spontaneous pneumothorax demonstrates:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A rim of hyperlucent area (dark area <strong>without lung markings</strong>) is seen between the lung margin and chest wall </li>\n<li>Inwardly shifted visceral pleural line (thin, sharp white line)</li>\n<li><strong>Deep sulcus sign</strong>: Deep lateral costophrenic angle can be seen in a <strong>supine X-ray</strong></li>\n<li>Depression of the diaphragm, rib cage expansion, and <strong>mediastinal</strong> <strong>shift to the contralateral side</strong> - indicates tension pneumothorax</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a <strong>right pneumothorax</strong> with <strong>total</strong> right lung collapse and tracheal deviation to the left.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1861","text":"The given USG of lung show‌s:","raw_text":"The given USG of lung shows:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratosp‌here sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sea sho‌re sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Troposph‌ere sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mirror ima‌ge artifact","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sea shore sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad556c7ce01d44e799c614fa4eb260ad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50c0b518254e4f9f94097f851896d826x1280x1064.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27f7b0e0e97f49cd8e2ad84b49d049a2x1280x1064.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sea shore sign</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>s</strong><strong>eashore sign</strong> seen in M-mode imaging of the normal lung.</p>\n<p>M-mode is defined as the <strong>time-motion display</strong> of the ultrasound wave along a chosen ultrasound line. With M-Mode, even rapid motions can be displayed, measured and recorded. In a normal lung, the M-mode image demonstrates a linear, <strong>laminar pattern</strong> in the tissue superficial to the pleural line and a granular or<strong> sandy appearance</strong> deep to the pleural line. </p>\n<p>The motionless portion of the chest above the pleural line creates horizontal ‘waves', and the sliding below the pleural line creates a granular pattern, the ‘sand’. The resultant picture is one that resembles waves crashing in onto the sand and is therefore called the ‘seashore sign’ and is present in a normal lung.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows an M-mode ultrasound showing <strong>seashore sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Stratosphere sign</strong> refers to<strong> </strong>M-mode showing a linear pattern below the pleural line. It is also called <strong>barcode sign</strong>. It is specific for<strong> pneumothorax.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: There is no named sign called troposphere sign.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Mirror image artifact </strong>is seen when there is a highly reflective surface (eg: diaphragm) is in the path of the primary beam.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0733","text":"A 42-year-old man is broug‌ht to the emergency depar​tment with complaints of ‌right-sided chest pain af‍ter a motor vehicle colli​sion. Physical examinatio​n revealed bilateral brea​th sounds and a supine ch‍est radiograph was unrema‍rkable. On performing an ​ultrasound the following ​was seen. What is the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with complaints of right-sided chest pain after a motor vehicle collision. Physical examination revealed bilateral breath sounds and a supine chest radiograph was unremarkable. On performing an ultrasound the following was seen. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coll‌apse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pneumothorax","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/203794b90ce3451387ddce5b845b977e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/057c6a0707744d58bf851e2e29137bb9x1279x1522.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pneumothorax</strong></p><p>The above M-mode ultrasound of the right lung shows a <strong>stratosphere or barcode sign</strong> specific for<strong> pneumothorax</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a lung with pneumothorax,<strong> lung sliding</strong> is <strong>abolished</strong> on M-mode ultrasound, and the normal sand-like appearance beneath the pleural line (white line) is replaced by <strong>parallel lines</strong>, which is termed the stratosphere or barcode sign, suggesting the presence of pneumothorax. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the <strong>stratosphere sign </strong>or<strong> barcode sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Supine chest radiography </strong>has <strong>low sensitivity</strong> for traumatic pneumothorax. <strong>Thoracic ultrasound</strong> has <strong>high sensitivity</strong> and specificity for the detection of small or occult pneumothorax in critical care patients.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pleural effusion </strong>on ultrasound appears anechoic with an echogenic line at the fluid-lung interface if it is transudate. Exudative and hemorrhagic effusions may appear echogenic with pleural thickening. The fluid shows movement and changes in shape with breathing, along with the presence of septa and fibrous strands.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Consolidation</strong> on ultrasound appears hypoechoic with heterogeneous echotexture. It is isoechoic with the liver and is referred to as lung hepatization. </p>\n<p>Option D: A <strong>collapsed lung</strong> under a large effusion will be seen as a hyperechoic wedge-shaped area.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0734","text":"A term neonate born by vac‌uum-assisted delivery dev​eloped mild respiratory d‌istress 1 hour after birt‍h. Arterial oxygen satura​tion remained &gt; 95% witho​ut supplemental oxygen. C​hest radiography performe‍d 6 hours after birth is ‍given below. What is the ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A term neonate born by vacuum-assisted delivery developed mild respiratory distress 1 hour after birth. Arterial oxygen saturation remained &gt; 95% without supplemental oxygen. Chest radiography performed 6 hours after birth is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumome‌diastinum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumope‌ritoneum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pneumomediastinum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5794398e8f584aa19204408c1b8a7d1c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0065750e66e4060b7ddcb21b26ea36ex510x500.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fd1579450de416b89ffb8d2a7866a8dx1280x1140.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d7e8414a3d74f578f8a7e9556f5c5bfx481x452.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pneumomediastinum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and radiograph showing a <strong>spinnaker sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>pneumomediastinum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spinnaker sign (also called angel wing sign) is a radiographic sign seen in<strong> neonates with</strong> <strong>pneumomediastinum</strong>. Pneumomediastinum is the presence of extraluminal gas within the mediastinum. The<strong> pressure</strong> from the collected gas <strong>displaces</strong> the two lobes of the<strong> thymic tissue</strong> <strong>upward and</strong> <strong>laterally</strong> from its normal position<strong>.</strong> This results in large, wedge-shaped opacities on radiograph resembling the spinnaker headsails of a boat.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>This should be distinguished from a <strong>thymic sail sign</strong> which is a <strong>normal finding</strong> on a neonatal chest x-ray. It represents a <strong>physiologically enlarged thymus</strong> in a neonate.</p>\n<p>Other signs of pneumomediastinum are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V sign of Naclerio - </strong>It is due to a <strong>V-shaped air collection.</strong> One limb of the V is produced by mediastinal air outlining the left lower lateral mediastinal border and the other limb is produced by air between the parietal pleura and medial left hemidiaphragm.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous diaphragm sign </strong>- Normally the central portion of the diaphragm is not discretely visualised on chest radiographs as it merges with the cardiac silhouette. If the <strong>diaphragm</strong> can be <strong>seen continuously across </strong>the<strong> midline</strong> then it is suggestive of free gas within the mediastinum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the<strong> V sign of Naclerio</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows <strong>continuous diaphragm sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0735","text":"A patient presented with p‌leuritic chest pain and a​bsent breath sounds on th‌e right side. A pleural e‍ffusion is suspected. Wha​t is the best chest x-ray​ view to diagnose this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with pleuritic chest pain and absent breath sounds on the right side. A pleural effusion is suspected. What is the best chest x-ray view to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PA ‌view","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right anter‌ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left anter‌ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral dec‌ubitus view","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral decubitus view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a54cce0babb747c3a9d18ce12dd916f3x1280x1103.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad5f2dd38b3a470998db1567823ff0d5x1030x871.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral decubitus view</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral decubitus </strong>view is the best view to identify <strong>pleural effusion</strong>. It can identify as little as <strong>25 ml</strong> of pleural fluid.</p>\n<p>Features of chest radiograph in pleural effusion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of free pleural fluid </li>\n<li>Blunting of costophrenic angle </li>\n<li><strong>Ellis-S curve</strong> – This is a radiological illusion and occurs as a medial radiological density due to the presence of partially aerated lung between the anterior and posterior fluid layers, whereas laterally the density is higher due to the presence of fluid only. The actual fluid level is horizontal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a <strong>lateral decubitus view</strong> showing <strong>pleural effusion.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Marking A shows fluid</strong> layering in the right pleural cavity. <strong>Marking</strong> <strong>B shows the normal width of the lung</strong> in the cavity. The amount of pleural fluid can be approximately quantified by measuring the distance A.</p>\n<p>Best investigation for minimal pleural effusion is <strong>USG.</strong></p>\n<p>The minimal amount of fluid required to be detected on X-ray imaging for pleural effusion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral (erect) view : <strong>75 ml</strong> pleural fluid</li>\n<li>Erect PA view : <strong>200 ml</strong> pleural fluid</li>\n<li>Lateral decubitus view  : <strong>&lt;25 ml</strong> pleural fluid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below is a chest X-ray PA view showing <strong>pleural effusion</strong> with<strong> Ellis-S curve.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0736","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d with a history of fever​ and chest pain for the l‌ast 7 days. A chest X-ray‍ was done. What is the pr​obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a history of fever and chest pain for the last 7 days. A chest X-ray was done. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loculated pleural effusion‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibr‌osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loculated pleural effusion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b1f07eb0f90d413ab544769de70084c2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16b5b0da389146fa8e355cf6db27d282x1055x827.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0dd63f00a08a448abc7c6a490a5381d8x1280x1090.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loculated pleural effusion</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and X-ray is suggestive of <strong>loculated pleural effusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>The chest X-ray shows <strong>obliteration </strong>of <strong>right costophrenic angle</strong> with a wide pleural-based dome-shaped opacity projecting into the lung. It tracks along the costphrenic angle and lateral chest wall.</p>\n<p>A pleural lesion can be differentiated from a parenchymal lesion by the angle it makes with the chest wall. <strong>Pleural lesions</strong> makes an <strong>obtuse angle</strong> with the chest wall whereas a <strong>parenchymal lesions</strong> makes an <strong>acute angle</strong> with the chest wall.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0743","text":"Split pleura sign is seen ‌in:","raw_text":"Split pleura sign is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emp‌yema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydropne‌umothorax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Empyema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc1381024116410fb4db78c030e1298ax511x196.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Empyema</strong></p><p>A<strong> </strong>split pleura sign is seen in<strong> empyema</strong>. </p>\n<p>Empyema is defined as a collection of pus in the pleural cavity. It is usually associated with pneumonia but may also develop after thoracic surgery or trauma.</p>\n<p>A hallmark feature of empyema on CT imaging is the <strong>split pleura sign</strong>, where the pleura divides into the parietal and visceral layers. This sign is considered the <strong>most sensitive</strong> and <strong>specific</strong> indicator of empyema on CT, helping to distinguish it from peripheral lung abscess.</p>\n<p>CT scan showing split pleura sign: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0755","text":"The chest radiograph of a ‌patient showed silhouetti​ng of the left border of ‌the heart. Pathology of w‍hich part of the lung doe​s this indicate?","raw_text":"The chest radiograph of a patient showed silhouetting of the left border of the heart. Pathology of which part of the lung does this indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right lo‌wer lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hi‌lar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apex of lef‌t lower lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lin‌gula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lingula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7612ba8ff48480a95b26971b830974ex805x740.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lingula</strong></p><p><strong>Silhouetting</strong> of the<strong> left border</strong> of the heart indicates a pathology in the <strong>lingula </strong>segments. </p>\n<p>The principle of silhouette sign is that the <strong>mediastinal borders</strong> can be <strong>obscured </strong>only by a <strong>pathology</strong> that is in <strong>direct anatomical contact</strong>. </p>\n<p>Positive silhouette sign and corresponding anatomical site of pathology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right heart border- </strong>right middle lobe or medial right lower lobe</li>\n<li><strong>Left heart border - </strong>lingula segments of the left upper lobe</li>\n<li>Left hemidiaphragm or descending aorta - left lower lobe</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic knuckle - </strong>left upper lobe (posterior segment)</li>\n<li>Right hemidiaphragm - right lower lobe</li>\n<li>Right paratracheal stripe - right upper lobe</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recognition of this sign is useful in localising areas of airspace opacities, atelectasis, or mass within the lung.</p>\n<p>The radiograph below shows the silhouette sign- the left cardiac border is obscured due to pneumonia of the lingula of the left lung.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0983","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions will you not s​ee an air bronchogram?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions will you not see an air bronchogram?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonar‌y oedema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyaline memb‌rane disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pleural effusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbca903eee554c518f34cd9b438bde3ax646x516.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f58d9fad87d24bc08447dd227623867ex600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pleural effusion</strong></p><p>In<strong> pleural effusion</strong>, there is a homogenous opacity with absent broncho-vascular markings, obliteration of costophrenic angle, and <strong>no air bronchograms. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Air bronchogram </strong>is defined as a pattern of air-filled bronchi (dark) on a background of opacified lung (grey/white).</p>\n<p>It is almost always caused by a pathological airspace/alveolar process, in which something other than air fills the alveoli. Air bronchograms will not be visible if the bronchi themselves are opacified (e.g. by fluid) and thus indicate patent proximal airways.</p>\n<p>Air bronchograms can be seen with several conditions: Pulmonary consolidation, pulmonary edema (especially alveolar oedema), Hyaline membrane disease, non-obstructive atelectasis, severe interstitial lung disease, neoplasms: adenocarcinoma; pulmonary lymphoma, pulmonary infarct, pulmonary haemorrhage. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows air-bronchogram within homogeneous consolidation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>CT showing consolidation with air bronchograms:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0989","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is pleural cal​cification least likely t‌o be seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is pleural calcification least likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemot‌horax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Healed ‌empyema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sarcoidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c50e14e0e2754c7ca8d0285b3988ab91x847x1119.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sarcoidosis</strong></p><p>Pleural calcification is <strong>least likely</strong> to be seen in <strong>sarcoidosis,</strong> as it is extremely rare, distinguishing it from other conditions like asbestosis, hemothorax and empyema where calcifications are<strong> </strong>more commonly observed. </p>\n<p>Sarcoidosis is a multisystem disease characterised by non-caseating granulomas, most commonly affecting the lung parenchyma and lymph nodes. Pleural involvement in sarcoidosis is rare and typically presents as pleural effusion, thickening and nodules. <strong>Lymph node calcification</strong> in sarcoidosis may have a stippled and eggshell appearance.</p>\n<p>Conditions showing <strong>pleural calcification</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asbestosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Haemothorax</strong></li>\n<li>Pyothorax/<strong>empyema</strong></li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Previous surgery </li>\n<li>Radiation therapy </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Difference between pleural calcification in asbestosis and empyema:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 317px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.141px;\"><strong>Asbestosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.266px;\"><strong>Empyema &amp; hemothorax</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.141px;\">Calcification occurs as more discrete collections within plaques</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.266px;\">\n<p>Calcification is irregular and resembles a plaque or sheet</p>\n<p>It is contained within thickened pleura</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.141px;\">It is usually bilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.266px;\">\n<p>Most common in the lower posterior half of the chest</p>\n<p>Usually unilateral</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The chest radiograph below shows extensive sheet-like calcification of the right pleura and a smaller localised calcification of the left pleura.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0993","text":"A 65-year-old man presents‌ with complaints of sudde​n onset pleuritic chest p‌ain and dyspnea. He had a‍rrived from an internatio​nal trip three days ago. ​A chest x-ray was done. W​hat is this finding calle‍d?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presents with complaints of sudden onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea. He had arrived from an international trip three days ago. A chest x-ray was done. What is this finding called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Westerm‌ark sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"McConel‌l's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palla'‌s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sausag‌e sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Westermark sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6906896e91c84d6ebb9d045f34b22789.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Westermark sign</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, chest pain and dyspnea after air travel most likely points to a diagnosis of <strong>pulmonary embolism</strong>. <strong>Westermark sign </strong>is seen in the given chest x-ray.</p>\n<p>It is defined by a <strong>hyper-lucent area</strong> with <strong>decreased vascularity</strong> due to reduced blood supply to the involved part of the lung.</p>\n<p>Even though characteristic signs are seen on chest x-ray, it is neither sensitive nor specific for pulmonary embolism. <strong>CT pulmonary angiography</strong> (CTPA) is the investigation of choice in pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0995","text":"In which of the following ‌patients, hypertranslucen​t lungs is not seen on ch‌est X-ray?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients, hypertranslucent lungs is not seen on chest X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle aged man with Macle‌od syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smoker wit‌h emphysema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient who has undergone ‌pneumonectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Teenager with Poland syndr‌ome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patient who has undergone pneumonectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patient who has undergone pneumonectomy</strong></p><p>Pneumonectomy causes<strong> </strong>opacification of hemithorax, <strong>not hypertranslucency.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes for <strong>hypertranslucency/hypertransradiancy </strong>of hemithorax:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased rotation while taking chest X-ray (most common)</li>\n<li>Scoliosis</li>\n<li>Poliomyelitis</li>\n<li>Mastectomy</li>\n<li>Pneumothorax</li>\n<li><strong>Emphysema</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Macleod syndrome</strong> (a complication of bronchiolitis characterized by a reduction in the pulmonary vasculature and alveolar hyperdistention)</li>\n<li><strong>Poland syndrome</strong> (unilateral absence of pectoralis major +/- other chest muscles)</li>\n<li>Pulmonary embolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>opacification</strong> of a hemithorax:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pneumonectomy</strong></li>\n<li>Pleural effusion</li>\n<li>Consolidation</li>\n<li>Collapse</li>\n<li>Massive tumour</li>\n<li>Fibrothorax</li>\n<li>Lung agenesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0996","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ Kerley A lines?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Kerley A lines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radiate from the hilum of ‌lungs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Runs perpendicular to the ‌pleural surface and exten​d out to it","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Has spider w‌eb appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short lines which do not r‌each the pleura","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Radiate from the hilum of lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/126901b9554746708f558cb873515c5dx742x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Radiate from the hilum of lungs</strong></p><p><strong>Kerley A</strong> lines <strong>radiate</strong> from the<strong> hilum</strong> of the lungs.</p>\n<p>Kerley lines are seen when the <strong>interlobular septa</strong> in the pulmonary interstitium become prominent. </p>\n<p>This may be because of<strong> lymphatic engorgement</strong> or<strong> edema </strong>of the connective tissues of the interlobular septa. They usually occur when <strong>pulmonary capillary wedge pressure </strong>reaches<strong> 20-25 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<p>Types of Kerley lines:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kerley A lines:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Thin, oblique lines(2-6cm long and thickness under 1mm), <strong>radiating from the hilum to the upper lobes. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>HRCT</strong> is the most effective imaging technique to identify these lines. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Kerley B lines:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Thin, horizontal lines (1-2 cm long), seen at the lung periphery, usually near the bases. </li>\n<li>Run <strong>perpendicular to the pleural surface and reach it</strong>, representing thickened subpleural interlobular septa.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Kerley C lines</strong> :\n<ul>\n<li>Short, irregular lines with a spider web-like appearance. </li>\n<li>Unlike Kerley B and D lines, they do not extend to the pleura; unlike Kerley A lines, they do not radiate outward from the hila.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Kerley D lines - </strong>the same as Kerley B lines, except that they are seen on lateral chest radiographs in the retrosternal air gap</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below depicts the <strong>Kerley B line</strong>, perpendicular to the pleural surface: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0997","text":"Bat-wing appearance is see‌n in:","raw_text":"Bat-wing appearance is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonar‌y oedema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary‌ embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumoc‌oniosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulmonary oedema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef6ca27563a844bbbf5413662290a83ax512x373.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8d92a9ec32a4aabb8a0256f36aad519x1280x1284.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulmonary oedema</strong></p><p><strong>Bat-wing appearance </strong>is<strong> </strong>seen in <strong>pulmonary oedema</strong>. It is called so because of the bilateral opacities that extend in a fan-shaped manner away from the hilum in a batwing pattern. </p>\n<p>Below is an image of a frontal chest X-ray and axial CT showing features of ‘batwing’ alveolar pulmonary oedema.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary oedema </strong>is defined as an abnormal <strong>accumulation of fluid</strong> in the <strong>extravascular compartments </strong>of the lung.</p>\n<p>Features of <strong>pulmonary interstitial oedema </strong>-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peri-bronchial cuffing and perihilar haze</li>\n<li>Septal lines / <strong>Kerley lines</strong></li>\n<li>Thickening of interlobar fissures</li>\n<li>Pulmonary venous engorgement - <strong>stag's antler sign</strong></li>\n<li>Airspace opacification classically in a<strong> </strong>batwing distribution</li>\n<li>May have<strong> </strong>air bronchograms</li>\n<li>Pleural effusions and fluid in interlobar fissures (including <strong>'vanishing' pulmonary pseudotumour</strong>)</li>\n<li>Increased cardio-thoracic ratio / cardiac silhouette size (assessing for an underlying cardiogenic cause)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below is a chest X-ray showing thickened interlobular septa (Kerley B) at the base of the right lower lobe, bronchial wall thickening (cuffing), overall distended veins, and a small pleural effusion with fluid in the interlobar fissures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1002","text":"Which lung segment is most‌ commonly sequestered?","raw_text":"Which lung segment is most commonly sequestered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apical segment of right up‌per lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apical segment of left upp‌er lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterobasal segment of le‌ft lower lobe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterobasal segment of ri‌ght lower lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterobasal segment of left lower lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterobasal segment of left lower lobe</strong></p><p>The lung segment most commonly sequestered is the <strong>posterobasal segment of the left lower lobe.</strong> The majority of the sequestrations are located in left lower lobe.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary sequestration is a bronchopulmonary foregut malformation in which a segment or lobe of<strong> dysplastic lung </strong>tissue is <strong>disconnected</strong> from the rest of the tracheobronchial tree or pulmonary arteries. It receives an <strong>anomalous</strong> vascular supply. It is a<strong> non-functional tissue</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hZacKiZy","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"4_paediatric_surgery","title":"4.Paediatric Surgery","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB6283","text":"An infant delivered at ful‌l-term by normal vaginal ‌delivery, presented with ‍respiratory distress imme​diately after birth. Ches​t X-ray showed the follow‍ing picture. What is the ​probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"An infant delivered at full-term by normal vaginal delivery, presented with respiratory distress immediately after birth. Chest X-ray showed the following picture. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline memb‌rane disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meconium aspiration syndro‌me","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital diaphragmatic h‌ernia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transient tachypnea of the‌ newborn","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3cd9848c0e5442c590398c7914a49441.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia</strong></p><p>The above X-ray chest shows <strong>intra-thoracic bowel shadows</strong> along with <strong>mediastinal shift</strong> suggestive of <strong>congenital diaphragmatic hernia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The common clinical presentation is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory distress</li>\n<li>Dextrocardia</li>\n<li>Scaphoid abdomen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prenatal diagnosis is done by <strong>ultrasonography</strong>. <strong>Lung-to-head ratio</strong> (LHR) is used to assess severity in patients with left-sided CDH. An LHR value of <strong>&lt;1</strong> is associated with a very <strong>poor</strong> prognosis, whereas an LHR <strong>&gt;1.4</strong> predicts a more <strong>favourable</strong> outcome.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hyaline membrane disease</strong> is a syndrome in premature infants caused by developmental <strong>insufficiency</strong> of pulmonary <strong>surfactant</strong> production and structural immaturity in the lungs.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Meconium aspiration syndrome</strong> is the aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid, which can occur before, during or immediately after birth. Meconium is the first intestinal discharge from newborns, a viscous, dark-green substance composed of intestinal epithelial cells, lanugo, mucus, and intestinal secretions.</p>\n<p>Option D: During normal delivery, the compression of the rib cage while passing through the mother's vaginal canal helps to remove fluid from the lungs. However, newborns who are delivered via <strong>cesarean</strong> birth may have excess fluid in the lungs and therefore be <strong>hypoxic</strong> and develop <strong>transient tachypnea.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6177","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g congenital diaphragmati‍c hernia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding congenital diaphragmatic hernia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most CDH defects occur on ‌the left side","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurs due to failure of c‌losure of the pleuroperit‌oneal canal in the develo‍ping fetus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary hypoplasia and p‌ulmonary hypertension sig‌nificantly affects the pr‍ognosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The posterolateral variant‌ of this hernia is known ‌as Morgagni hernia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The posterolateral variant of this hernia is known as Morgagni hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e19809da62744343a250533e12271526x1280x1659.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0c33a62e8824903bb81b298c4d46316x1280x1306.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The posterolateral variant of this hernia is known as Morgagni hernia</strong></p><p>The <strong>posterolateral </strong>variant of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) is known as <strong>Bochdalek </strong>hernia while the <strong>anteromedian</strong> variant is called <strong>Morgagni </strong>hernia.</p>\n<p>Congenital diaphragmatic hernia results from the <strong>failure of closure </strong>of the <strong>pleuroperitoneal canal</strong> in the developing fetus.</p>\n<p>Typically in CDH, there is a left-sided posterolateral diaphragmatic defect allowing herniation of abdominal viscera into the chest.<strong> 80% of CDH</strong> develops on the <strong>left side.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following sequence of events in CDH leads to the development of pulmonary hypoplasia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary hypertension and pulmonary hypoplasia</strong> are the <strong>most important prognostic factors</strong> in congenital diaphragmatic hernia.</p>\n<p>Other poor prognostic indicators in CDH :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The presence of liver in the thoracic cavity </li>\n<li>Early respiratory distress, within 24 h after birth</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation</li>\n<li>Herniation to the contralateral lung (<strong>right-sided hernia)</strong></li>\n<li> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of congenital diaphragmatic hernia:</p>\n<p>Variable period of time (24-72 hours) is to be allowed for stabilization before surgical repair. Immediate respiratory support should be provided, including <strong>rapid endotracheal intubation</strong> and sedation. </p>\n<p><strong>Bag and mask ventilation</strong> is <strong>contraindicated </strong>in newborns with congenital diaphragmatic hernia because it causes <strong>distension of the stomach</strong> which in turn may limit the expansion of the already <strong>hypoplastic lung</strong>.</p>\n<p>During surgery, the viscera are reduced into the abdominal cavity, and the posterolateral defect in the diaphragm is closed using <strong>interrupted, nonabsorbable sutures. </strong>In most cases (80%-90%), a hernia sac is not present. If identified, it is excised at the time of repair.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6978","text":"Most common organ that her‌niates in Morgagni’s hern‌ia is ______","raw_text":"Most common organ that herniates in Morgagni’s hernia is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transver‌se colon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transverse colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24308b201eaa48879cc8a1ff995807dex611x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/541e9d2999564e41a106b1882eaded87x704x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transverse colon</strong></p><p><strong>The transverse colon is</strong> the most common herniating structure in a Morgagni hernia.</p>\n<p><strong>Morgagni Hernia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A hernia in the anterior part of the diaphragm with a defect <strong>between the</strong> <strong>sternal and costal attachments</strong></li>\n<li>The most commonly involved viscus is the <strong>transverse colon.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Morgagni hernia: </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6977","text":"What is not true regarding‌ a Bochdalek hernia?","raw_text":"What is not true regarding a Bochdalek hernia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early respiratory distress‌ leading to early diagnos‌is and treatment is a goo‍d prognostic sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The contents of hernial sa‌c commonly includes retro‌peritoneal fat, left kidn‍ey or spleen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The condition is diagnosed‌ prenatally by ultrasonog‌raphy.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pericardial cyst is a diff‌erential diagnosis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Early respiratory distress leading to early diagnosis and treatment is a good prognostic sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Early respiratory distress leading to early diagnosis and treatment is a good prognostic sign</strong></p><p><strong>Early respiratory distress</strong>, within 6 hours of birth, is thought to be a<strong> poor prognostic sign</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Features of a Bochdalek hernia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Respiratory distress</strong> is a cardinal sign in infants with a congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH).</li>\n<li><strong>Early respiratory distress, within 6 hr after birth, is thought to be a poor prognostic sign</strong>.</li>\n<li>Children with CDH may also have a <strong>scaphoid abdomen and increased chest wall diameter.</strong></li>\n<li>Bowel sounds may also be heard in the chest, with a <strong>bilateral decrease in breath sounds. </strong></li>\n<li>The point of maximal cardiac impulse may be displaced away from the side of a hernia if a <strong>mediastinal shift</strong> has occurred.</li>\n<li><strong>Retroperitoneal fat, left kidney, spleen </strong>or only the omentum commonly herniates through the defect. If the defect is large, most abdominal contents including stomach may herniate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chest radiography and passage of a nasal gastric tube confirm the diagnosis.</li>\n<li>CDH can be diagnosed on <strong>prenatal ultrasonography (between 16 and 24 weeks of gestation) </strong></li>\n<li>High-speed fetal magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can further define the lesion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ultrasonography findings</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyhydramnios</li>\n<li>Chest mass</li>\n<li>Mediastinal shift</li>\n<li>Gastric bubble or a liver in the thoracic cavity</li>\n<li>Fetal hydrops</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Differential</strong> <strong>diagnosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eventration</li>\n<li>Cystic lung lesion (pulmonary sequestration or cystic adenomatoid Malformation)</li>\n<li><strong>Group B streptococcal sepsis:</strong> associated with <strong>delayed onset</strong> of symptoms and a CDH often on the right side.</li>\n<li><strong>Pneumothorax: </strong>always occurs on <strong>contralateral </strong>to the <strong>side of CDH</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment: </strong></p>\n<p>Variable period of time (24-72 hours) to allow for stabilization before surgical repair</p>\n<p>The viscera are reduced into the abdominal cavity, and the posterolateral defect in the diaphragm is closed using interrupted, nonabsorbable sutures.</p>\n<p>In most cases (80%-90%), a hernia sac is not present. If identified, it is excised at the time of repair.</p>\n<p>Conventional mechanical ventilation, high-frequency oscillatory ventilation (HFOV), and extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) are the 3 main strategies to support respiratory failure in the newborn with CDH.</p>\n<p>Good relative indicators of stability are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Requirement for conventional ventilation only</li>\n<li>Low peak inspiratory pressure</li>\n<li>Fio2 &lt;50</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the defect is too large, a porous polytetrafluoroethylene (Gore-Tex) patch is used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6293","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common Gastroint‌estinal atresia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common Gastrointestinal atresia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodenal‌ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colonic‌ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ileal ‌atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jejunal‌ atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duodenal atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duodenal atresia</strong></p><p>The most common Gastrointestinal atresia is<strong> duodenal atresia</strong> (60%) followed by jejunal and ileal atresia (20%). Colonic atresia is the least common type (7-10%).</p>\n<p>Duodenal atresias are commonly associated with chromosomal abnormalities such as <strong>Down syndrome</strong>. It occurs approximately in 1/10,000 births. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5347","text":"A 3-day-old neonate presen‌ted with recurrent biliou‌s vomiting. An X-ray erec‍t abdomen is given below.​ What is the next best st​ep in managing this condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A 3-day-old neonate presented with recurrent bilious vomiting. An X-ray erect abdomen is given below. What is the next best step in managing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT ab‌domen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastrograffin follow-throu‌gh","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"USG a‌bdomen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Explorative‌ laparotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gastrograffin follow-through","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5810dfed2d4d456e80a34ce13f1ca2df.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gastrograffin follow-through</strong></p><p>Explanation: </p>\n<p>The given clinical history with an x-ray showing a triple bubble sign is suggestive of jejunal atresia. The next best step is to perform a contrast study to localize the site of obstruction.</p>\n<p>Small bowel atresias present with intestinal obstruction soon after birth.             </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilious vomiting is the dominant feature in jejunal atresia </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal distension is more prominent in ileal atresia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A small amount of pale meconium may be passed, despite the atresia.</p>\n<p>Plain abdomen radiographs in a jejunal/ileal atresia case will show <strong>multiple air-fluid levels</strong> and dilated loops. They <strong>don't reliably distinguish</strong> small and large bowels in neonates and hence contrast studies are often required to localize the obstruction.</p>\n<p>Water-soluble enemas (<strong>Gastrogaffin</strong>, <strong>Hypaque</strong>) are particularly useful in <strong>differentiating atresia from meconium ileus and Hirschsprung disease</strong>. Contrast studies also help to rule out distal intestinal obstruction and occasionally may act therapeutically to relieve obstructions.</p>\n<p>In most cases of jejunal/ileal atresia, the distal end of the <strong>dilated proximal small bowel</strong> is <strong>resected</strong> and a <strong>primary end-to-end anastomosis</strong> is done. If the proximal bowel is extremely dilated, it may need to be tapered to the distal bowel before anastomosis.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A:  CT is not recommended for neonates owing to the harmful effects of <strong>radiation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: USG abdomen does not help to localize the site of obstruction and is more useful in the diagnosis of intussusception, where there is an abdominal mass.</p>\n<p>Option D: Explorative laparotomy is not done for diagnosing intestinal atresias. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6301","text":"Bishop-Koop anastomosis is‌ done for which of the fo‌llowing conditions?","raw_text":"Bishop-Koop anastomosis is done for which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meconiu‌m ileus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Jejunal‌ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duodenal‌ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hirschprun‌g's disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meconium ileus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eebb099bfa1b40e7a3d92a4654e9a5d5x1280x2197.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meconium ileus</strong></p><p><strong>Bishop-Koop Anastomosis</strong> and <strong>Mikulicz enterostomy</strong> are done for meconium ileus.</p>\n<p><strong>Meconium ileus</strong> (also known as <strong>DIOS</strong> - Distal Intestinal Obstructive Syndrome) is defined as intestinal obstruction due to inspissated meconium within the terminal ileum. </p>\n<p>In the <strong>Bishop-Koop procedure,</strong> the proximal distended limb is joined to the side of the distal small bowel. This is vented by a '<strong>chimney'</strong> to the abdominal wall. The Bishop–Koop operation with its irrigating stoma is now only rarely used.</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ramstedt pyloromyotomy</strong> is the standard operation for <strong>hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Kasai procedure</strong> is done for <strong>biliary atresia</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duhamel, Soave, Swenson procedures</strong> are done in <strong>Hirschsprung’s</strong> disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6309","text":"An infant presents with co‌pious bilious vomiting an‌d abdominal distension on‍ the 5th day of life. He ​had not passed meconium f​or the first 3 days after‍ birth. An abdominal radi​ograph shows the presence‌ of dilated bowel loops. ​What is the gold standard‌ investigation for the gi​ven condition?","raw_text":"An infant presents with copious bilious vomiting and abdominal distension on the 5th day of life. He had not passed meconium for the first 3 days after birth. An abdominal radiograph shows the presence of dilated bowel loops. What is the gold standard investigation for the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contras‌t enema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rectal‌ biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal m‌anometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rectal biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rectal biopsy</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Hirschsprung's disease</strong>. A <strong>rectal biopsy </strong>is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for the diagnosis of Hirschsprung’s disease.</p>\n<p>A rectal biopsy<strong> 2 cm above the dentate line</strong> is the ideal area for a biopsy.</p>\n<p>Hirschsprung’s disease is a developmental disorder characterized by the <strong>absence of ganglion cells</strong> in the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus and submucosal (Meissner) plexus. Most infants present with abdominal distention and bilious emesis with failure to pass meconium within the first 24 hours of life.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The initial investigation is a <strong>contrast enema. </strong>The area between the dilated and contracted segments is referred to as the<strong> transition zone. </strong>The affected part shows the absence of ganglion cells and <strong>hypertrophy of nerve trunks </strong>in the rectal biopsy.</p>\n<p>Other investigations for Hirschsprung’s disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Water-soluble contrast enema </strong>-<strong> </strong>shows the presence of contracted aganglionic rectum, transition zone, and a dilated normally innervated colon.</li>\n<li><strong>Anorectal manometry - </strong>shows the failure of the relaxation of the internal anal sphincter.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong> Definitive surgeries</strong> for Hirschsprung disease are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Swenson</strong> procedure, where aganglionic bowel is removed and a coloanal anastomosis is performed.</li>\n<li><strong>Duhamel</strong> procedure,<strong> </strong>where the aganglionic rectal stump is left in place and the ganglionated normal colon is pulled posterior.</li>\n<li><strong>Soave</strong> procedure, where endorectal mucosal dissection is done within the aganglionic distal rectum. The ganglionated normal colon is pulled through the remnant muscular cuff, and a coloanal anastomosis is performed.<span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> </span></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6310","text":"A 2-year-old child is brou‌ght to the casualty with ‌severe abdominal pain. On‍ performing emergency exp​loratory laparotomy, the ​following finding is note‍d. Which of the following​ statements is false rega‌rding this condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child is brought to the casualty with severe abdominal pain. On performing emergency exploratory laparotomy, the following finding is noted. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be a lead point for in‌tussusception","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common presenting‌ symptom is bleeding in y‌ounger children","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a false diverticulum‌ which extends from the a‌nti-mesenteric side","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Most common heterotopic ti‌ssue is gastric mucosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a false diverticulum which extends from the anti-mesenteric side","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c20fdedcd1214ecb975e2ea950395dd9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8091cd1900a0437bb2510a03a6624d3ex1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a false diverticulum which extends from the anti-mesenteric side</strong></p><p><strong>Meckel’s diverticulum</strong> is a<strong> true diverticulum, </strong>which extends from the <strong>antimesenteric</strong> side of the small bowel. The diverticula which contain all the layers of the intestine are called <strong>true diverticula</strong>. </p>\n<p>Meckel’s diverticulum is caused by a <strong>failure</strong> of normal <strong>regression</strong> of the <strong>vitelline duct</strong> that develops during weeks 5-7 of gestation.</p>\n<p>The <strong>rule of 2's</strong> (Mnemonic for Meckel’s diverticulum) :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 types of heterotopic mucosa (gastric &amp; pancreatic)</li>\n<li>2% incidence</li>\n<li>located within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve</li>\n<li>approximately 2 inches in length</li>\n<li>Usually symptomatic by 2 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common presentation is painless, massive, <strong>lower GI bleed</strong> in children younger than 5 years. Meckel's diverticulum can be the lead point for <strong>ileoileal or ileocolic intussusception</strong>.</p>\n<p>A bleeding Meckel's diverticulum may be confirmed by a <strong>99 mTc-pertechnetate isotope scan</strong> to detect ectopic gastric mucosa. </p>\n<p><strong>Surgical treatment</strong> is the<strong> definitive </strong>therapy for Meckel’s diverticulum. A simple V-shaped diverticulectomy with transverse closure of the ileum is the ideal surgical approach.</p>\n<p>The following diagram shows the various anomalies that arise as a result of the failure of normal regression of vitellointestinal duct:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6313","text":"A surgery resident is exam‌ining a child born with t‌he following defect. Whic‍h of the following statem​ents regarding this defec​t is false?","raw_text":"A surgery resident is examining a child born with the following defect. Which of the following statements regarding this defect is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The central defect of the ‌abdominal wall may be mor‌e than 4 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This defect is usually not‌ associated with other co‌ngenital anomalies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The overlying sac is made ‌of amnion and peritoneum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"This defect is a component‌ of the Cantrell's pental‌ogy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. This defect is usually not associated with other congenital anomalies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dd1031e3767c4578bb4218b822780994.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. This defect is usually not associated with other congenital anomalies</strong></p><p><strong>Omphalocele</strong> is usually <strong>associated</strong> with other<strong> congenital anomalies</strong> whereas gastroschisis is rarely associated with other congenital anomalies.</p>\n<p>Omphalocele refers to a congenital defect of the abdominal wall in which the bowel and solid viscera are <strong>covered</strong> by the <strong>peritoneum and amniotic membrane.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>During normal development of the human embryo, the <strong>midgut herniates outward</strong> through the umbilical ring and continues to grow. </li>\n<li>Between <strong>10th-12th week</strong> of gestation, the <strong>midgut returns</strong> to the coelomic cavity and undergoes <strong>proper rotation</strong> and fixation, along with the closure of the umbilical ring. </li>\n<li>If the intestine fails to return, the infant is born with the abdominal contents protruding directly through the umbilical ring, with an <strong>intact sac</strong> covering the abdominal viscera, termed an <strong>omphalocele.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The sac is composed of an outer layer of <strong>amnion</strong> and an inner layer of <strong>peritoneum.</strong> It is seen in omphalocele and not in gastroschisis. Omphalocele can vary from a small defect with intestinal contents to a <strong>giant omphalocele</strong> in which the abdominal wall defect <strong>measures 4 cm or more</strong> in diameter. It may even contain <strong>liver</strong>.</p>\n<p>Omphalocele occurs in association with special syndromes such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is a 60% to 70% incidence of associated anomalies, especially <strong>cardiac anomalies</strong> (20–40% of cases) and <strong>chromosomal abnormalities</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exstrophy of the cloaca</strong> (vesicointestinal fissure)</li>\n<li>The <strong>Beckwith-Wiedemann constellation</strong> of anomalies (macroglossia, macrosomia, hypoglycemia, and visceromegaly and omphalocele)</li>\n<li>The<strong> Cantrell’s pentalogy</strong> (lower thoracic wall malformations like cleft sternum, ectopia cordis, epigastric omphalocele, anterior midline diaphragmatic hernia, and cardiac anomalies).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6315","text":"A 19-year-old lady deliver‌ed a neonate with the fol‌lowing defect. Which of t‍he following statements a​bout the defect is false?​","raw_text":"A 19-year-old lady delivered a neonate with the following defect. Which of the following statements about the defect is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The defect is generally lo‌cated to the right of the‌ umbilical cord","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herniation of liver is com‌monly associated with thi‌s condition","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The incidence of this defe‌ct is more in preterm bab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serial reduction of evisce‌rated intestines maybe do‌ne for management","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Herniation of liver is commonly associated with this condition","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66e23ed2d8a04677a687845dc44ff12b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f215eade544b45e1bbbcdd861f74d5f9x515x297.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Herniation of liver is commonly associated with this condition</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>gastroschisis. Herniation</strong> of the <strong>liver</strong> is more commonly seen with <strong>omphalocele</strong> than gastroschisis.</p>\n<p>Gastroschisis represents a congenital anomaly characterized by a defect in the anterior abdominal wall. The abdominal contents protrude through this defect. It is the most common anomaly seen in babies born to mothers under 20 years of age. Unlike omphalocele, there is <strong>no overlying sac</strong> and the <strong>size</strong> of the defect is usually <strong>&lt;4 cm</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1912\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1912\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>An <strong>orogastric tube</strong> is placed to decompress the stomach. For primary closure, bowel loops are reduced, fascia and skin are approximated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>If the viscera cannot be reduced into the abdomen, a <strong>silo</strong> is placed at the bedside as shown in the image. The eviscerated intestines are <strong>reduced serially</strong> during 5 to 7 days, followed by operative fascial closure.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6316","text":"The most common extracrani‌al solid tumour in infant‌s and children is _______‍.","raw_text":"The most common extracranial solid tumour in infants and children is  _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/736863494fb54af3b00c83eaf0bbed82x720x508.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> is the most common<strong> extracranial solid</strong> tumour of childhood. While brain tumours are the most common solid tumour of childhood.</p>\n<p>Neuroblastoma is a <strong>malignancy</strong> of neuroblast cells in the <strong>adrenal medulla</strong> or <strong>sympathetic ganglia</strong> and typically presents as an abdominal or paravertebral mass. <strong>Raccoon eyes</strong> (periorbital ecchymoses) are seen in neuroblastoma due to orbital metastasis. </p>\n<p><strong>Shimada classification</strong> is used for classifying neuroblastoma.</p>\n<p>Blue subcutaneous nodules represent skin dissemination of tumour and are associated with the<strong> blueberry muffin syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6317","text":"The most common site of rh‌abdomyosarcoma in childre‌n is _______.","raw_text":"The most common site of rhabdomyosarcoma in children is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genitouri‌nary tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Head a‌nd neck","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extre‌mities","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abd‌omen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Head and neck","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Head and neck</strong></p><p>The most <strong>common site</strong> of <strong>rhabdomyosarcoma</strong> in children is the<strong> head and neck.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common pediatric soft tissue sarcoma and accounts for approximately 3.5% of childhood cancers.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">These tumours may occur at virtually any anatomic site but are usually found in the <strong>head and neck </strong>(25%), <strong>genitourinary tract </strong>(24%), <strong>extremities </strong>(19%) and <strong>orbit</strong> (9%). <strong>Retroperitoneum</strong> and other sites account for the remainder of primary sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Rhabdomyosarcomas</strong> are the most common soft tissue tumors among children younger than 15 years and can occur at any site comprised of striated muscle.</p>\n<p>Rhabdomyosarcoma is classified into <strong>three types </strong>- embryonal, alveolar, and pleomorphic. <strong>Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma</strong> is the most common type.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6319","text":"The most common malignant ‌brain tumor in children i‌s ________.","raw_text":"The most common malignant brain tumor in children is ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pilocytic ‌astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oligoden‌droglioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medulloblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medulloblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Medulloblastoma</strong> is the most common <strong>malignant</strong> <strong>brain tumour</strong> in children.</p>\n<p>They are usually midline. Most of them occur in the cerebellum and present with symptoms of increased ICP. </p>\n<p>Most common tumours in children:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brain tumours</strong> are the most common<strong> solid tumours.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pilocytic astrocytoma</strong> is the most common brain tumour (usually not malignant).</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> is the most common<strong> extracranial </strong>solid tumour.</li>\n<li><strong>Rhabdomyosarcomas</strong> are the most common <strong>soft tissue tumours.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6320","text":"A female baby born at 30 w‌eeks of gestation was adm‌itted to NICU for prematu‍rity and respiratory dist​ress. She was placed on C​PAP and enteric feeds wer‍e started. On day 5 of ad​mission, she became letha‌rgic and there was disten​sion of the abdomen. A pl‌ain radiograph of the abd​omen shows the following ‌findings. Which statement‌ about the condition is f‍alse?","raw_text":"A female baby born at 30 weeks of gestation was admitted to NICU for prematurity and respiratory distress. She was placed on CPAP and enteric feeds were started. On day 5 of admission, she became lethargic and there was distension of the abdomen. A plain radiograph of the abdomen shows the following findings. Which statement about the condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antenatal steroids have sh‌own to reduce the occurre‌nce of this condition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The radiograph depicts pne‌umatosis intestinalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Modified Bell's staging ca‌n be used for this condit‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery is the treatment o‌f choice for all stages","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Surgery is the treatment of choice for all stages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfc41dd66b2e4a2981d1161901976880.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Surgery is the treatment of choice for all stages</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and radiological findings are indicative of <strong>necrotizing enterocolitis</strong> (NEC). <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The absolute indication for <strong>operative management</strong> of NEC is the presence of <strong>intestinal perforation. </strong></span>Milder cases can be treated with gut rest, antibiotics, and parenteral nutrition. </p>\n<p>The radiograph shows the presence of multiple air bubbles within the bowel wall. This is called <strong>pneumatosis intestinalis</strong> and is a pathognomonic sign suggestive of necrotizing enterocolitis. </p>\n<p><strong>Necrotizing enterocolitis </strong>is the necrosis of a portion of the bowel and is the most common gastrointestinal surgical emergency in the neonatal period. The single most important risk factor is <strong>prematurity</strong>. Other contributing factors are <strong>enteral feeding</strong>, ischemia, and infection. Administration of <strong>antenatal steroids</strong> is known to reduce the occurrence of NEC.</p>\n<p>It usually occurs in the first few days of life after the initiation of the enteral feeds. Some clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lethargy</li>\n<li>Feeding intolerance</li>\n<li>Bloody stools</li>\n<li>Abdominal distension</li>\n<li>Abdominal tenderness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The radiograph may show pneumatosis intestinalis, portal venous gas, ascites, and the presence of free air.</p>\n<p>The<strong> modified Bell's criteria</strong> is used for staging the severity of NEC.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6321","text":"A 5-year-old child present‌s with jaundice and abdom‌inal pain. On examination‍, there is a palpable mas​s in the right hypochondr​ium. MRCP reveals multipl‍e dilatations limited to ​the intrahepatic bile duc‌ts. Which of the followin​g statements about this c‌ondition is false?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child presents with jaundice and abdominal pain. On examination, there is a palpable mass in the right hypochondrium. MRCP reveals multiple dilatations limited to the intrahepatic bile ducts. Which of the following statements about this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Todani classification is u‌sed for this condition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This condition is also kno‌wn as Caroli's disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central dot sign is a char‌acteristic CT finding ass‌ociated with this conditi‍on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoscopic sphincterotomy ‌followed by surveillance ‌is the treatment of choic‍e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endoscopic sphincterotomy followed by surveillance is the treatment of choice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1c15ea7bee94359a2a931c5612a991fx1279x1094.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endoscopic sphincterotomy followed by surveillance is the treatment of choice</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Caroli's disease </strong>(type V choledochal cyst). <strong>Liver transplantation </strong>is the preferred treatment of choice here.</p>\n<p>Choledochal cysts are cystic dilations of the common bile duct (CBD). They present with a triad of jaundice, a palpable upper quadrant mass, and abdominal pain.</p>\n<p>There are mainly five types of choledochal cysts. The original classification for choledochal cysts by <strong>Alonso–Lej</strong>, has been modified by <strong>Todani.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>MRCP</strong> is the investigation of choice. The<strong> beaded appearance </strong>of <strong>intrahepatic</strong> bile ducts on <strong>HIDA</strong> scan and <strong>central dot sign on CT </strong>are characteristic findings associated with <strong>Caroli's disease. </strong></p>\n<p>The arrows in the image given below are pointing to the <strong>central dot sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9193","text":"A 10-year-old child came w‌ith complaints of pain ov‌er the right lower quadra‍nt and lower back pain. O​n examination, the patien​t had pain over the right‍ side of the hip on passi​ve extension with a mass ‌in the right lumbar regio​n pointing towards the gr‌oin. What is the most pro​bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child came with complaints of pain over the right lower quadrant and lower back pain. On examination, the patient had pain over the right side of the hip on passive extension with a mass in the right lumbar region pointing towards the groin. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyelone‌phritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psoas ‌abscess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Append‌icitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Torsion of right undescend‌ed testis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoas abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68f3f0abe1d74f1aad24091084f4f153x600x727.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc50a1d324ad42fba0bbc235e024ca24x287x210.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoas abscess</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of <strong>lower back pain</strong>, <strong>mass in the right lumbar region</strong> pointing towards the groin and <strong>pain on passive extension of the ipsilateral hip</strong> is suggestive of a<strong> psoas</strong> <strong>abscess</strong>. </p>\n<p>Psoas abscess is a <strong>retroperitoneal</strong> abscess, caused due to various etiologies. Previously, tuberculosis of the spine was the most common cause <strong>secondary to spreading infection from the digestive or urinary tract, which</strong> can cause psoas abscess. Nowadays, <strong>Crohn’s disease</strong> is the most common cause. Patients present with flank pain, fever and chills, malaise and weight loss. They can have referred pain to the hip, groin or knee. When the swelling extends across the groin and below the inguinal ligament along the course of the iliopsoas, it becomes <strong>cross fluctuant </strong>across the inguinal ligament.</p>\n<p>Imaging is done by a <strong>CT scan</strong>, which shows a<strong> low-density mass</strong> with surrounding <strong>inflammation</strong> and <strong>infiltration</strong> of surrounding <strong>fat</strong> and <strong>gas</strong> or <strong>air-fluid</strong> <strong>levels.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment is done by <strong>percutaneous CT-guided drainage</strong> and appropriate <strong>antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pyelonephritis</strong> is usually associated with a history of urinary tract infection and sudden onset of pain in the flank and hypochondrium, not the lumbar region.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Appendicitis (retrocaecal) </strong>can present with pain on passive extension of the hip (psoas sign), but the presence of a mass in the right lumbar region pointing towards the groin makes it an unlikely diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Testicular torsion</strong> is a surgical emergency condition presenting with sudden onset pain in the scrotum, groin and lower abdomen, tenderness, redness and edema of the scrotal skin. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME0685","text":"A 3-year child is brought ‌to the pediatrician for t‌he evaluation of the late‍ral neck mass. On examina​tion, a painless, soft, f​luctuating mass is found ‍in the lateral side of th​e neck, as shown in the i‌mage. The slip sign is ne​gative, and the swelling ‌is brilliantly transillum​inant. What is the most l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year child is brought to the pediatrician for the evaluation of the lateral neck mass. On examination, a painless, soft, fluctuating mass is found in the lateral side of the neck, as shown in the image. The slip sign is negative, and the swelling is brilliantly transilluminant. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic ‌hygroma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Branchi‌al cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lip‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical lym‌phadenopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cystic hygroma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21262c2d5da547078124ddb7f91c6afc.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cystic hygroma</strong></p><p>A soft, painless, brilliantly transilluminant, fluctuating mass found in the <strong>posterior part of the neck</strong> in a child, is most likely to be a <strong>cystic hygroma.</strong></p>\n<p>Cystic hygroma is a <strong>lymph-filled sac</strong> that results from the obstruction of the lymph vessels during their development. It is most commonly present in the <strong>posterior triangle of the neck</strong>. The mass may be apparent at birth or may appear in early childhood due to the increased accumulation of lymph. Since the sac is filled with lymph, it is soft, fluctuating, and <strong>brilliantly transilluminant.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aspiration </strong>of the contents, followed by <strong>excision</strong> once the sac or capsule gets thickened by fibrous tissue.</li>\n<li>Single cysts can be treated with <strong>sclerosing therapy.</strong> Commonly used sclerosants include <strong>OK–432</strong> or <strong>Picibanil,</strong> which is a mixture of group A <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> strain, pure ethanol, interferon alpha-2a, and doxycycline.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete excision</strong> is done in case of <strong>multiple cysts.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: A <strong>branchial cleft</strong> cyst also presents as soft fluctuant swelling. It is present in the <strong>anterior neck</strong> and at the <strong>junction</strong> of the <strong>upper third</strong> and <strong>middle third</strong> of the <strong>sternocleidomastoid muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lipoma</strong> is less commonly seen in children. The swelling is <strong>non-fluctuant</strong> and when palpated, the edge slips under the finger. This is known as the <strong>slip sign.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Cervical lymphadenopathy is seen in children with infections of the parotid and upper respiratory tract infections. On palpation, they are <strong>firm</strong> and <strong>non-fluctuant</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6176","text":"The most common location o‌f cystic hygroma is _____‌___.","raw_text":"The most common location of cystic hygroma is ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axi‌lla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior triangle of neck‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior tri‌angle of neck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior m‌ediastinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior triangle of neck","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72f7847e8e4f4f2c85924626399346e6x441x410.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior triangle of neck</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic hygroma</strong>, also known as cystic lymphangioma and macrocystic lymphatic malformation, is a congenital <strong>multiloculated lymphatic lesion </strong>that can arise anywhere but is classically found in the <strong>left posterior triangle</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong>.</p>\n<p>The other common sites are the axillary, mediastinal, inguinal, and retroperitoneal regions. It occurs as a result of sequestration or obstruction of developing lymph vessels. The cysts are lined by endothelium and filled with lymph.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a newborn with cystic hygroma on the right side of the neck.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6855","text":"A 15-year-old girl present‌ed with a slow-growing, r‌ubbery, non-tender swelli‍ng on the side of the nec​k as shown in the image. ​Examination shows the pre‍sence of freckles and ski​n patches over the face a‌nd trunk. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old girl presented with a slow-growing, rubbery, non-tender swelling on the side of the neck as shown in the image. Examination shows the presence of freckles and skin patches over the face and trunk. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic ‌hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemod‌ectoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neural‌ tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lip‌oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neural tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c5ef7fa034a403f80b7693720191e30.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neural tumor</strong></p><p>A lateral neck mass in a pediatric patient, which is non-tender and present in association with freckles, and hyperpigmented skin patches <strong>(cafe-au-lait spots) </strong>is suggestive of <strong>neural tumor</strong> i.e. <strong>neurofibroma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Neurofibroma is a benign tumor arising from the connective tissue of the nerve containing ectodermal, neural and mesodermal connective tissue components. It can be single or multiple. It is the <strong>most common intradural extramedullary spinal tumor</strong>. Neurofibromas can be local, generalized (von Recklinghausen's disease) or plexiform types. Patients present with mild pain or a painless swelling usually in the subcutaneous or cutaneous plane with tingling, numbness and paraesthesia along the distribution of the nerve. Complications of neurofibroma include cystic degeneration, erosion into deeper planes, cord compression and paralysis (spinal neurofibroma) and sarcomatous changes.</p>\n<p>Treatment is excision under general or regional anesthesia. Indications for surgery in neurofibroma include symptomatic patients (pain and pressure symptoms), cosmetic problems and malignant transformation (5%).</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Cystic hygroma</strong> is a type of <strong>congenital</strong> lymphangioma. This <strong>swelling is soft</strong> and partially compressible, which shows <strong>positive fluctuation and translucency test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Chemodectoma</strong> or carotid body tumor is a rare tumor, most commonly present in the <strong>fifth decade</strong> and there is an association with pheochromocytoma. These patients usually present with a slowly enlarging, painless lump at the <strong>carotid bifurcation.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: A <strong>lipoma</strong> is a soft painless swelling. It shows the characteristic <strong>slip sign</strong> and is <strong>freely mobile.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7022","text":"A first-born, 5-weeks-old ‌male infant presents with‌ non-bilious projectile v‍omiting. The abdominal ra​diograph shows the follow​ing finding. Which of the‍ following will be the pr​eferred investigation in ‌this case?","raw_text":"A  first-born, 5-weeks-old male infant presents with non-bilious projectile vomiting. The abdominal radiograph shows the following finding. Which of the following will be the preferred investigation in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT ab‌domen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultrasound ‌examination","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barium ‌studies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal m‌anometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ultrasound examination","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/699627db0fd1456998bcfb149891afff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da2da777a3c44973874db75cd1deb7b8x481x496.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e1a65d52c4e4fe0b7d946225515da0ax595x630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ultrasound examination</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the radiographic image showing the presence of an enlarged <strong>gastric gas bubble (single bubble) </strong>are suggestive of<strong> congenital</strong> <strong>hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong> (<strong>CHPS</strong>). <strong>Ultrasound</strong> examination is the investigation of choice for this condition.</p>\n<p>Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (CHPS) is characterized by abnormal <strong>thickening of the pylorus</strong> muscles in the stomach leading to <strong>gastric outlet obstruction. </strong>First-born <strong>male infants</strong> are at the highest risk.</p>\n<p>Infants usually present with </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Projectile, non-bilious vomiting </strong>due to gastric outlet obstruction, most commonly between<strong> 3 and 6 weeks</strong> of age.</li>\n<li><strong>Olive-shaped mass</strong> palpable in the right upper quadrant or epigastrium.</li>\n<li><strong>Visible peristalsis</strong> after feeding, moving from <strong>left to right</strong>, followed by projectile vomiting.</li>\n<li>Metabolic abnormalities include <strong>hypokalemic</strong>,<strong> hypochloremic</strong>, <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong> with <strong>paradoxical</strong> <strong>aciduria</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ultrasound</strong> examination <strong>confirms the diagnosis</strong> of congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (CHPS) in the majority of cases. The diagnostic criteria for pyloric stenosis are a <strong>muscle thickness of ≥4 mm</strong> and a <strong>pyloric length of ≥16 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ultrasound signs seen in <strong>Congenital Hypertrophic Pyloric stenosis </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target sign </strong>(Hypoechoic thickened pyloric muscle with a hyperechoic central mucosa)</li>\n<li><strong>Cervix sign</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nipple sign.</strong></li>\n<li>Antral nipple sign</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The child should be <strong>rehydrated</strong> and the <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>abnormality</strong> <strong>has to be corrected</strong> before proceeding to surgery. The <strong>Ramstedt pyloromyotomy</strong> is the standard surgical procedure for its treatment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ultrasound image given above shows hypertrophied pylorus resembling that of the uterine cervix (<strong>cervix sign</strong>) and double-layered hypertrophic mucosa protruding into the stomach, (<strong>Nipple sign</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ultrasound image given above shows redundant pyloric mucosa protruding into the antrum - <strong>Antral nipple sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME8907","text":"A 2-month-old baby is brou‌ght to the outpatient dep‌artment for a facial defe‍ct. On examination, the f​ollowing findings were no​ticed. The soft and the h‍ard palate are intact. Wh​ich of the following is t‌he most appropriate manag​ement in this child?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old baby is brought to the outpatient department for a facial defect. On examination, the following findings were noticed. The soft and the hard palate are intact. Which of the following is the most appropriate management in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cleft lip repair immediate‌ly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cleft lip repair at 9 to 1‌2 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cleft lip repair at 4 to 6‌ months","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cleft lip repair at 12-15 ‌months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cleft lip repair at 4 to 6 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0654a1e33b1e44a899655e4403f0cb7f.jpeg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/364b499ac1cf4032820cae789aab36dex600x336.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cleft lip repair at 4 to 6 months</strong></p><p>The given clinical image is that of a child with a <strong>cleft lip </strong>on the <strong>left side</strong>. The most appropriate management for this patient would be a cleft lip repair at <strong>4-6 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Etiology of the cleft lip can include familial, protein and vitamin deficiency, rubella infection, radiation, chromosomal abnormalities, maternal epilepsy and drug intake during pregnancy (steroids/eptoin/diazepam)</p>\n<p>Majority<strong> </strong>of the cases are <strong>unilateral</strong> and cleft lip alone is uncommon.</p>\n<p><strong>Millard Criteria (Rule of 10</strong>) is used to undertake surgery for cleft lip.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>10 pound in weight</li>\n<li>10 weeks old</li>\n<li>10g% Hemoglobin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Millard cleft lip repair by rotating the local nasolabial flaps is done.</p>\n<p>The most appropriate management for this patient would be <strong>cleft lip repair at 4 to 6 months</strong>. </p>\n<p>According to Delaire timing of cleft surgery - unilateral cleft lip alone can be operated in one stage operation and is done at 4-6 months.</p>\n<p><strong>The different types of facial clefts are shown below:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unilateral cleft lip with alveolar involvement</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral cleft lip with alveolar involvement</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unilateral cleft lip associated with cleft palate</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral cleft lip and palate</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cleft palate only</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"m2-4HPlK","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"5_airway_devices","title":"5.Airway Devices","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2239","text":"You are a certified BLS pr‍ovider showing your junio​r how to perform bag and ‌mask ventilation on a man‍nequin. While applying th​e face mask, where would ‌you place the fifth digit‌ of your hand?","raw_text":"You are a certified BLS provider showing your junior how to perform bag and mask ventilation on a mannequin. While applying the face mask, where would you place the fifth digit of your hand?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bridge of‍ the nose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The mas‍k device","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ramus of‍ mandible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angle of‍ mandible","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Angle of mandible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d4a089781de4526bad5e8a55be3978ex1280x1063.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Angle of mandible</strong></p><p>The fifth digit of the hand should be placed at the <strong>angle of mandible</strong> while holding a face mask.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>One hand technique</strong>: C-E technique</p>\n<p>The anesthetist holds the mask in his left hand using <strong>C-E technique</strong>. C is formed by the thumb and the index finger, and E is formed by the remaining 3 fingers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>thumb </strong>and<strong> index</strong> fingers form a <strong>C</strong> around the collar of the connector</li>\n<li>The <strong>third </strong>and<strong> fourth </strong>digits are placed on the <strong>body of the mandible</strong></li>\n<li>The<strong> fifth digit</strong> is placed on the <strong>angle of the mandible</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The thumb and index finger ensure a tight mask seal, while the remaining digits provide jaw thrust to<strong> </strong>aid with airway patency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2243","text":"In which of the following ‍situations can bag and ma​sk ventilation be perform‌ed?","raw_text":"In which of the following situations can bag and mask ventilation be performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obese individual with a di‍fficult airway","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Emergency laparotomy requi‍ring rapid sequence induc​tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Severe fac‍ial trauma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diaphragmatic hernia follo‍wing blunt trauma to ches​t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Obese individual with a difficult airway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Obese individual with a difficult airway</strong></p><p>Bag and mask ventilation <strong>can be performed</strong> in patients with <strong>difficult airway.<br/><br/></strong>Bag and mask ventilation serves as a rescue technique to provide oxygenation and ventilation in patients with difficult airway until tracheal intubation is achieved.</p>\n<p><br/><strong>Contraindications of bag and mask ventilation: </strong></p>\n<p>Patients at greater risk of aspiration such as patients with <strong>diaphragmatic</strong> <strong>hernia</strong> and those undergoing <strong>rapid sequence induction</strong> and intubation. This is because air can enter the stomach leading to raised intragastric pressure which increases the risk of aspiration.<br/><br/></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1935\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mask ventilation should also be performed with <strong>caution</strong> in patients with <strong>severe facial trauma</strong> and in patients in whom head and neck manipulation must be avoided (e.g., those with an unstable cervical spine fracture).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2247","text":"You are a resident posted ‍in the medicine ward. A p​atient who has been previ‌ously diagnosed with epil‍epsy has an episode of se​izures and becomes uncons‌cious. You immediately ca‌ll a code. The patient is‍ unresponsive and chest r‍ise is not appreciable bu‌t you can feel a pulse. Y​ou suspect that this is d​ue to falling back of the​ tongue and decide to sec‍ure the airway using the ​device shown. What landma‌rks would you use before ‌selecting the appropriate​ size?","raw_text":"You are a resident posted in the medicine ward. A patient who has been previously diagnosed with epilepsy has an episode of seizures and becomes unconscious. You immediately call a code. The patient is unresponsive and chest rise is not appreciable but you can feel a pulse. You suspect that this is due to falling back of the tongue and decide to secure the airway using the device shown. What landmarks would you use before selecting the appropriate size?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incisor teeth and tragus o‍f the ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corner of mouth and angle ‍of mandible","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tip of nose and angle of m‍andible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ala of nose and angle of m‍andible","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corner of mouth and angle of mandible","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/137b21d6dbad440ba1a2787db2fe1dff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7064b3a23e94f4f977dbc55b518805dx1280x1427.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corner of mouth and angle of mandible</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>oropharyngeal</strong> <strong>airway</strong> or <strong>Guedel</strong> <strong>airway </strong>of various sizes. The landmarks used for measuring the appropriate size of this device are the <strong>corner</strong> <strong>of the</strong> <strong>mouth</strong> and the <strong>angle of the</strong> <strong>mandible</strong> or tragus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>An oropharyngeal airway is an airway adjunct which keeps the tongue from falling back onto the <strong>posterior pharynx</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is essential to choose the correct size since inappropriately sized ones may <strong>aggravate airway obstruction</strong>. </p>\n<p>These airways should <strong>not</strong> be <strong>used</strong> in <strong>conscious</strong> patients as they may come into contact with the epiglottis and base of tongue. This can cause <strong>coughing</strong>, <strong>retching</strong> or <strong>laryngospasm</strong> if the pharyngeal and laryngeal reflexes are not blunted with local anaesthetic. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Nasopharyngeal</strong> <strong>airway</strong> size is chosen by measuring the distance between the <strong>tip</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>nose</strong> and <strong>tragus</strong> of the ear. These airways, when well lubricated and inserted with proper technique, can be used in <strong>conscious</strong> patients to secure an airway.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6456","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true regarding​ the airway adjunct shown‌ in the image given below‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the airway adjunct shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used only in unconsc‍ious patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevents falling back of t‍ongue post-extubation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prevents tongue bite durin‍g seizure activity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can be used in patients wi‍th central facial fractur​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prevents falling back of tongue post-extubation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c222d336a224714a0dc5c5e1bac3c58.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prevents falling back of tongue post-extubation</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s2\">The image shows <strong>nasopharyngeal airway</strong> and its insertion. The true statement is that it is used to <strong>prevent falling back</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>tongue</strong> post-extubation especially in <strong>obese</strong> patients.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s2\">Nasopharyngeal airways are less stimulating than oropharyngeal airways as it bypasses the gag reflex. Thus, it can be <strong>used</strong> in <strong>conscious</strong> patients as well.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s2\">It does <strong>not</strong> <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>tongue</strong> <strong>bite</strong> unlike the oropharyngeal airway which has a bite block. It can prevent falling back of the tongue in patients with seizures.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s2\">It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in</span></p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s2\">Basilar skull fractures, <strong>central facial fractures</strong>, CSF rhinorrhea - risk of meningitis</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s2\">Patients on anticoagulants, coagulopathies, epistaxis - risk of bleeding</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s2\">Relatively contraindicated in pregnancy (increased vascularity leading to bleeding), deformity of the nose.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2249","text":"The image shown below is t‍he technique of insertion​ of?","raw_text":"The image shown below is the technique of insertion of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oropharyng‍eal airway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasopharyn‍geal airway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laryngeal ‍mask airway","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endotrac‍heal tube","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Laryngeal mask airway","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d09c5f8fbe804cc6961ac9ba69dd133d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1a5205e27954a4bb7131cda0f1499fex1280x1146.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f35153982f349149033f4199470e9efx1280x2807.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fd399e88afa41d394ee2c1f61335399x510x511.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Laryngeal mask airway</strong></p><p>The image shown above is the technique of insertion of laryngeal mask airway.</p>\n<p><strong>Laryngeal mask airway</strong> (LMA) is a supraglottic airway device that is positioned in the <strong>hypopharynx</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Classic Laryngeal Mask Airway</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 332\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Supraglottic airway devices are used with both spontaneously and ventilated patients during anesthesia.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>LMA has <strong>advantages</strong> of being used in many settings, including the operating room (<strong>alternate</strong> <strong>route</strong> of <strong>ventilation</strong>), the emergency department (<strong>difficult intubation</strong>), and out-of-hospital care (paramedics).</p>\n<p>This is because it is easy to use, quick to place even for the inexperienced provider and has a very high success rate.</p>\n<p><strong>The technique of insertion</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 332\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The deflated cuff of LMA is lubricated and <strong>inserted</strong> <strong>blindly</strong> into the hypopharynx so that, once <strong>inflated</strong>, the cuff forms a low-pressure seal around the entrance to the larynx.</li>\n<li><strong>Correct</strong> <strong>placement</strong> of the tube is confirmed by giving gentle positive pressure ventilation while checking for chest rise and ETCO<span style=\"font-size: 6pt;\">2</span> using<strong> capnography</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Oropharyngeal airway:</strong> inserted in the patient’s mouth with concavity towards hard palate. As the airway is inserted it is rotated 180 degrees (concavity towards the tongue) until the flange comes to rest on the patient’s lips. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Nasopharyngeal airway:</strong> inserted through the nostril (after lubrication) along the natural curvature of the floor of the nasal cavity with the bevel pointing towards the septum. Once the device is inserted the flange should rest on the nostril opening.</p>\n<p><strong>Endotracheal tube: </strong>inserted infraglottically after visualising the vocal cords with direct laryngoscopy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2254","text":"What size LMA would you ch‍oose for a man weighing 7​5 kgs?","raw_text":"What size LMA would you choose for a man weighing 75 kgs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4‍.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5</strong></p><p>The appropriate size of laryngeal mask airway for a man weighing 75 kg is<strong> 5</strong>.</p>\n<p>Laryngeal mask airway is a supraglottic airway device, which is placed in the hypopharynx and protects the larynx from pharyngeal secretions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2262","text":"Laryngeal mask airway of s‍ize 3 would be appropriat​e for a person with a wei‌ght of _________.","raw_text":"Laryngeal mask airway of size 3 would be appropriate for a person with a weight of _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 ‍kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10‍ kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30‍ kg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"70‍ kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30 kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30 kg</strong></p><p>Laryngeal mask airway of size 3 would be most appropriate for individuals weighing about 30 kgs.<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2266","text":"In which of the following ‍patients would you prefer​ to use an LMA for securi‌ng the airway?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you prefer to use an LMA for securing the airway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A term G2P1L1 woman previo‍usly diagnosed with pre-e​clampsia planned for elec‌tive Cesarean section","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 30-year-old patient to b‍e taken up for repair of ​hiatus hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A middle-aged man with a h‍istory of diabetes diagno​sed with pharyngeal absce‌ss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 40-year-old woman planne‍d for laparoscopic cholec​ystectomy, having Mallamp‌atti class III airway","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 40-year-old woman planned for laparoscopic cholecystectomy, having Mallampatti class III airway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/194b9ae9f30941af84bccaeaf3207e28x1280x1428.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 40-year-old woman planned for laparoscopic cholecystectomy, having Mallampatti class III airway</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngeal mask airway</strong> (LMA) is <strong>indicated</strong> in cases with <strong>difficult</strong> <strong>airway</strong> where endotracheal intubation is not possible or not available. It can be used as an alternative to an endotracheal tube for ventilating and oxygenating the patient. </p>\n<p>LMA is relatively <strong>contraindicated</strong> in conditions that are associated with increased risk of aspiration (e.g.: <strong>Hiatal</strong> <strong>hernia</strong>, <strong>pregnancy</strong>, inadequate NPO). LMA may protect the larynx from pharyngeal secretions but not from gastric secretions. The presence of a <strong>pharyngeal</strong> <strong>pathology</strong> such as an abscess or tumor, or a pharyngeal obstruction is also a relative contraindication of LMA insertion.</p>\n<p><strong>The normal anatomic position of the LMA.</strong></p>\n<p>The proximal portion of the LMA rests upon the epiglottis, whereas the distal end or, the <strong>tip</strong> extends into the pharynx at the upper end of the <strong>esophagus</strong>. The opening on the laryngeal mask overlies the laryngeal inlet.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2270","text":"A 55 year old woman, prese‍nts to the pre-anaestheti​c clinic. She has been ad‌vised to undergo an elect‍ive laparoscopic hysterec​tomy. On further evaluati‌on, her BMI is calculated‌ to be 31 and she has a M‍allampatti class III airw‍ay. The anaesthetist then‌ decides that a Proseal l​aryngeal mask airway woul​d be ideal for this patie​nt. Identify the false st‍atement about this device​?","raw_text":"A 55 year old woman, presents to the pre-anaesthetic clinic. She has been advised to undergo an elective laparoscopic hysterectomy. On further evaluation, her BMI is calculated to be 31 and she has a Mallampatti class III airway. The anaesthetist then decides that a Proseal laryngeal mask airway would be ideal for this patient. Identify the false statement about this device?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single use second generati‍on airway device","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Built in ‍bite-block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric drainage tube to r‍emove secretions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can be used in surgery req‍uiring prone position","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single use second generation airway device","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/411b2b1e7d224fb78b1b0c77fc7e3ccax409x264.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f55c01b03ff47beb75943f7c4f041d1x1280x1684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single use second generation airway device</strong></p><p><strong>Proseal laryngeal mask airway (LMA) </strong>is a<strong> re-usable </strong>second-generation supraglottic airway device, not a single-use device. </p>\n<p>It also has an integrated bite block, a drainage tube to remove gastric secretions, and an improved cuff design. This reduces the risk of aspiration. </p>\n<p>Hence it may be <strong>suitable</strong> in <strong>non-supine</strong> positions (e.g., lateral, prone), <strong>laparoscopic</strong> surgery (e.g., cholecystectomy, gynaecological surgery), and in patients who are <strong>obese</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>All these features are also present in <strong>LMA Supreme</strong>, except that it is a <strong>single-use</strong> second generation supraglottic device.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2271","text":"What is the advantage of t‍he following device?","raw_text":"What is the advantage of the following device?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has a port for orogastric ‍tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can facilitate endotrachea‍l intubation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ideal for i‍nfants alone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suction tube to remove sec‍retions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Can facilitate endotracheal intubation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0bd240ce49a2486c82427622bad5bf73.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4d267bcfe264d7e9c9f9c723c98b18cx1063x1381.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26de9b0b9395464abb55055aab4255aax1280x1662.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Can facilitate endotracheal intubation</strong></p><p>The image shown above is the <strong>Fastrach</strong> laryngeal mask airway (LMA) or intubating LMA with an endotracheal tube within its lumen.</p>\n<p>It has a distinct advantage over other LMA designs as it can <strong>facilitate endotracheal intubation</strong> through the LMA device.</p>\n<p>It has a rigid handle and an epiglottis elevator.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>LMA Proseal</strong> and <strong>LMA Supreme</strong> are second-generation LMA devices that have integrated bite blocks, an orogastric tube to remove gastric secretions, and an improved cuff design.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2274","text":"Laryngeal mask airway has ‍the following advantages ​over endotracheal intubat‌ion except ______.","raw_text":"Laryngeal mask airway has the following advantages over endotracheal intubation except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Less i‍nvasive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle relaxation not requ‍ired","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low risk of laryngeal trau‍ma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low risk of‍ aspiration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Low risk of aspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Low risk of aspiration</strong></p><p>There is an <strong>increased</strong> <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>aspiration</strong> of <strong>gastric</strong> <strong>contents</strong> with laryngeal mask airway.</p>\n<p>The laryngeal mask airway (LMA) is a supraglottic airway device that sits in the hypopharynx and partially <strong>protects</strong> the larynx<strong> from pharyngeal secretions</strong>, but not gastric regurgitation.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Easy and rapid</li>\n<li><strong>Less invasive</strong></li>\n<li>Better hemodynamic stability</li>\n<li>Very useful in difficult intubations</li>\n<li><strong>Less tooth and laryngeal trauma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Does not require muscle relaxation</strong></li>\n<li>Does not require neck mobility </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased risk of gastrointestinal aspiration</strong></li>\n<li>Less safe in prone or jack-knife positions</li>\n<li>Less secure airway</li>\n<li>Greater risk of gas leak and pollution</li>\n<li>Can cause gastric distention</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2277","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common complicat​ion following the use of ‌a laryngeal mask airway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common complication following the use of a laryngeal mask airway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve ‍palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Damage to‍ the teeth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sore ‍throat","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lingual n‍erve palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sore throat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sore throat</strong></p><p><strong>Sore throat</strong> is the most common symptom following the use of a laryngeal mask airway with an incidence of <strong>10-20%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Injury to the lingual, hypoglossal, and recurrent laryngeal nerves are rare. </p>\n<p>These complications can be minimized by using an adequate sized device and avoiding high cuff pressures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xXQJBkQQ","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"5_pharyngeal_arches__skeletal___muscular_systems","title":"5.Pharyngeal Arches, Skeletal & Muscular Systems","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0848","text":"The core of the branchial ‌arch is derived from whic​h of the following?","raw_text":"The core of the branchial arch is derived from which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectoderm a‌nd mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoderm and neural crest ‌cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesoderm and neural crest ‌cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ectoderm a‌nd endoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mesoderm and neural crest cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1d1f1e0bb374bdeb8dd4d3f52269d91x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mesoderm and neural crest cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>core of</strong> each <strong>pharyngeal arch </strong>(branchial arch) is derived from <strong>mesoderm</strong> and <strong>neural crest cells</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pharyngeal arches</strong> initially have identical structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Surface ectoderm</strong> on the outside </li>\n<li>A mesenchymal <strong>core</strong> derived from <strong>mesoderm</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Endoderm</strong> derived epithelium on the inside </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>mesoderm</strong> in the arches is derived from paraxial mesoderm and lateral plate mesoderm. This is invaded by the <strong>neural crest cells</strong> that contribute to the formation of skeletal elements and connective tissue of the head and neck region.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0849","text":"Which of the following pha‌ryngeal arches disappear ​soon after formation?","raw_text":"Which of the following pharyngeal arches disappear soon after formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd ‌Arch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th ‌Arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5th ‌Arch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6th ‌Arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5th Arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c822148c33d446f94ff6f4d4b533d11x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5th Arch</strong></p><p>The <strong>5th pharyngeal arch</strong> <strong>disappears</strong> soon after its <strong>formation,</strong> so only <strong>5 arches remain.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pharyngeal arches appear in the 4th or 5th week of development and contribute to the formation of the head and neck region. They are formed as a series of <strong>6 thickenings</strong> in the cranial most part of the foregut (the future pharynx). They are craniocaudally numbered from <strong>I to VI</strong>. The 5th pharyngeal arch disappears, so only 5 arches remain. </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>f</strong><strong>irst pharyngeal arch</strong> is also known as the <strong>mandibular arch</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>s</strong><strong>econd pharyngeal arch</strong> is also known as the <strong>hyoid arch</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The 3rd, 4th, and 6th pharyngeal arches have no special names</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0855","text":"The site in the pharyngeal‌ arch where the ectoderma​l cleft meets the endoder​mal pouch is known as:","raw_text":"The site in the pharyngeal arch where the ectodermal cleft meets the endodermal pouch is known as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharynge‌al cleft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pharynge‌al pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pharyngea‌l membrane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cervica‌l sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pharyngeal membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bf40b037f644b29b88be4dc394a592cx1280x1121.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ef8b5c2ffdb478a911fa939a6837e3dx1280x1004.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pharyngeal membrane</strong></p><p>The <strong>pharyngeal membrane</strong> is the location where the <strong>ectoderm</strong> of the <strong>pharyngeal cleft</strong> meets the <strong>endoderm</strong> of the <strong>pharyngeal pouch</strong>. </p>\n<p>In the interval between any two adjoining pharyngeal arches, the endoderm extends outward in the form of a pouch (<strong>pharyngeal pouch) </strong>to meet the ectoderm, which dips into this interval as a <strong>pharyngeal cleft</strong>. The membrane formed where the pharyngeal cleft comes in contact with the pouch is called the <strong>pharyngeal membrane</strong>. </p>\n<p>Initially, there are four pharyngeal membranes between the five arches. Only the <strong>first pharyngeal</strong> <strong>membrane</strong> develops and contributes to the formation of the <strong>tympanic membrane</strong>. The 2nd, 3th, and 4th pharyngeal membranes do not develop further into any structure. They get obliterated when the mesoderm from the 2nd arch overgrows and fills the succeeding clefts. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0850","text":"A 4-day-old infant who was‌ delivered at home is bro​ught to the pediatrician ​for a routine checkup. Th‌e infant has a noticeable‌ small mandible and cleft​ palate. A CT scan reveal‌s hypoplasia of the mandi​ble and a defect in the ​​middle ear cavity and tym‍panic membrane. A develop​mental defect of which of‍ the following pharyngeal‌ arches is most likely?","raw_text":"A 4-day-old infant who was delivered at home is brought to the pediatrician for a routine checkup. The infant has a noticeable small mandible and cleft palate. A CT scan reveals hypoplasia of the mandible and a defect in the ​middle ear cavity and tympanic membrane. A developmental defect of which of the following pharyngeal arches is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st phary‌ngeal arch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2nd phary‌ngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd phary‌ngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6th phary‌ngeal arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1st pharyngeal arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1st pharyngeal arch</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>first arch syndrome</strong> due to the defect in the development of the <strong>first pharyngeal arch.</strong></p>\n<p>The derivatives of the first pharyngeal arch are:</p>\n<p><strong>Skeletal derivatives: </strong>It has <strong>2 prominences </strong>- </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mandibular prominence </strong>(ventral)\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meckel's cartilage</strong>: Malleus, incus, anterior malleolar ligament, the spine of the sphenoid, and the sphenomandibular ligament. </li>\n<li><strong>Mesenchyme</strong>: In the jaw region, it surrounds the Meckel's cartilage and undergoes membranous ossification to form the mandible</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maxillary prominence </strong>(dorsal)\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mesenchyme</strong>:  Maxilla, zygomatic bone, and part of the temporal bone are formed by membranous ossification</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pharyngeal pouch derivatives:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Auditory tube</li>\n<li>Middle ear cavity</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Muscular derivatives:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tensor tympani</li>\n<li>Tensor veli palaitini</li>\n<li>Muscles of mastication</li>\n<li>Anterior belly of digastric</li>\n<li>Mylohyoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vascular derivatives:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maxillary artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1578","text":"A newborn had hypoplasia o‌f the mandibles and zygom​atic bones, malformed ext​ernal ears, and slanted p‌alpebral fissures. Abnorm‌ality in the development ​of which of the following‌ pharyngeal arches will m​ost likely​ lead to the g​iven condition?","raw_text":"A  newborn had hypoplasia of the mandibles and zygomatic bones, malformed external ears, and slanted palpebral fissures. Abnormality in the development of which of the following pharyngeal arches will most likely​ lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharynge‌al arch 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pharynge‌al arch 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pharynge‌al arch 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pharynge‌al arch 6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pharyngeal arch 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a868b952bad141ed822bc59900368fb2x1080x1918.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67924a47d630461c8aab434bbca6f32fx1080x1920.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pharyngeal arch 1</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> Treacher Collins syndrome</strong> that results from abnormal development of the <strong>1st pharyngeal arch </strong>(mandibular arch). </p>\n<p><strong>First arch syndromes </strong>are congenital defects caused due to the <strong>failure </strong>of the<strong> neural crest cells</strong> to<strong> migrate</strong> into the <strong>1st pharyngeal arch</strong>. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Treacher-Collins syndrome</strong> (mandibulofacial dysostosis) - Autosomal dominant genetic disorder characterized by:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Craniofacial bone defects</strong> - Involves mandible (causing micrognathia), maxilla, and zygomatic arches</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Ear defects</strong> - Involves the external ear, atresia of auditory canals, and abnormalities of the middle ear ossicles leading to bilateral conductive hearing loss</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Eye defects</strong> - Down-slanting palpebral fissures and lower eyelid colobomas</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Pierre Robin sequence</strong> (or Robin sequence) - Caused due to genetic defects or due to environmental factors. It can also be seen in oligohydramnios when the chin gets compressed against the chest in utero. \n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">It is characterized by a <strong>triad</strong> of <strong>micrognathia</strong>, <strong>glossoptosis</strong> (posteriorly placed tongue), and <strong>cleft palate</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>primary defect</strong> is poor growth of the <strong>mandible</strong> (micrognathia)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">As a consequence of this, the tongue is posteriorly placed (glossoptosis)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Additionally, the tongue remains in between the palatal shelves and they fail to fuse (cleft palate)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0880","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is not derived fr​om Meckel's cartilage?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not derived from Meckel's cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ligament of malle‌us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spine of‌ sphenoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌pes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenomandib‌ular ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stapes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d58acc5abf549bbad630dce4b25a9c8x1280x1423.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stapes</strong></p><p>The stapes is not derived from the Meckel's cartilage. </p>\n<p>The cartilage of the first arch is <strong>Meckel's cartilage</strong>. Structures derived from it include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malleus</li>\n<li>Incus</li>\n<li>Anterior ligament of malleus</li>\n<li>Spine of sphenoid</li>\n<li>Sphenomandibular ligament </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The mandible, maxilla, zygomatic bone, and part of the temporal bone are formed from the mesenchyme of the first arch and not from Meckel's cartilage.</li>\n<li>The portion of Meckel’s cartilage that extends from the mandibular symphysis to mental foramen is probably incorporated into the intramembranous mandibular bone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0871","text":"A 49-year-old woman came w‌ith complaints of dull ac​hing pain in the throat a​nd right side of the face‌. She also frequently get‌s pain while swallowing a​ssociated with earache an‌d headaches. On CT scan, ​a calcified stylohyoid li​gament was seen. This lig‍ament is derived from:","raw_text":"A 49-year-old woman came with complaints of dull aching pain in the throat and right side of the face. She also frequently gets pain while swallowing associated with earache and headaches. On CT scan, a calcified stylohyoid ligament was seen. This ligament is derived from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First phar‌yngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second phar‌yngeal arch","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Third phar‌yngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fourth phar‌yngeal arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Second pharyngeal arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d264a2ff99b4fb4b823fc420ccda3a4x600x641.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Second pharyngeal arch</strong></p><p>The<strong> stylohyoid ligament</strong> is derived from the cartilage of the <strong>second pharyngeal arch </strong>which is <strong>Reichert's cartilage</strong>. </p>\n<p>Reichert's cartilage contributes to the development of the following structures:</p>\n<p>The <strong>dorsal portion </strong>forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stapes</li>\n<li>Styloid process of the temporal bone</li>\n<li>Stylohyoid ligament </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>ventral portion</strong> forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> upper part </strong>of the<strong> body</strong> and the <strong>lesser cornu </strong>of the <strong>hyoid bone.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: This condition is called <strong>Eagle syndrome</strong>. It<strong> </strong>is a condition associated with the <strong>elongation</strong> of the <strong>styloid process </strong>or <strong>calcification</strong> of the <strong>stylohyoid ligament.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>These structures can be remembered as<strong> 5S's</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>tapes</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>tyloid process of the temporal bone</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>tylohyoid ligament</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>maller (lesser) cornu of the hyoid bone</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>uperior part of the body of the hyoid bone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0884","text":"Which structure is derived‌ from the 3rd pharyngeal ​arch?","raw_text":"Which structure is derived from the 3rd pharyngeal arch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser cornu of hyoid bone‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater cornu of hyoid bon‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper part of the body of ‌hyoid bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Full body of the hyoid bon‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Greater cornu of hyoid bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49c0b01b785148f3a306980d39bdcbcex600x641.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Greater cornu of hyoid bone</strong></p><p><strong>Greater cornu (greater horn)</strong> is derived from the <strong>3rd pharyngeal arch</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> lower part </strong>of the<strong> body </strong>of the<strong> hyoid bone</strong> is also formed from 3rd pharyngeal arch.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0863","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is affected in patie​nts with mandibular nerve​ injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is affected in patients with mandibular nerve injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Styloph‌aryngeus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tensor ‌tympani","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior belly of digastr‌ic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator ve‌li palitini","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tensor tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tensor tympani</strong></p><p>The <strong>tensor tympani</strong> muscle is affected in the mandibular nerve injury. The mandibular nerve supplies the muscle since it is derived from the <strong>first pharyngeal arch</strong> (mandibular arch).</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Pharyngeal Arch</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Muscle derivatives</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Muscles of mastication, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, platysma, and posterior belly of digastric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Stylopharyngeus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Larynx - cricothyroid; soft palate - levator veli palatini; pharyngeal constrictors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VI</td>\n<td>Larynx - All intrinsic muscles (except cricothyroid)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0856","text":"During thyroidectomy, the ‌surgeon accidentally dama​ges a nerve. Post-op, the​ patient is not able to r‌aise the pitch of his voi‌ce. Which arch is the aff​ected structure derived f‌rom?","raw_text":"During thyroidectomy, the surgeon accidentally damages a nerve. Post-op, the patient is not able to raise the pitch of his voice. Which arch is the affected structure derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4th phary‌ngeal arch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2nd phary‌ngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd phary‌ngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st phary‌ngeal arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4th pharyngeal arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4th pharyngeal arch</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, the<strong> cricothyroid</strong> <strong>muscle</strong> is affected since it is responsible for <strong>high pitch phonation.</strong> It is derived from the <strong>4th pharyngeal arch</strong>. </p>\n<p>All the <strong>other</strong> <strong>intrinsic muscles </strong>of the<strong> larynx </strong>arise from the <strong>6th pharyngeal arch. </strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Pharyngeal Arch</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Muscle derivatives</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Muscles of mastication, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, platysma, and posterior belly of digastric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Stylopharyngeus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Larynx - cricothyroid; soft palate - levator veli palatini; pharyngeal constrictors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VI</td>\n<td>Larynx - All intrinsic muscles (except cricothyroid)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0858","text":"Which of the following is ‌the nerve of 6th pharynge​al arch?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the nerve of 6th pharyngeal arch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mandibul‌ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior laryngeal nerve o‌f vagus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve ‌of vagus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Recurrent laryngeal nerve of vagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Recurrent laryngeal nerve of vagus</strong></p><p>The<strong> recurrent laryngeal nerve </strong>of the<strong> vagus</strong> is the nerve of the <strong>6th pharyngeal arch.</strong></p>\n<p>The vagus nerve is distributed to the 4th and 6th arches as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior laryngeal nerve for 4th arch</li>\n<li>Recurrent laryngeal nerve for 6th arch</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2004","text":"The nerve of the second br‌anchial arch is:","raw_text":"The nerve of the second branchial arch is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vagal‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Facial nerve</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>facial nerve</strong> is the nerve of the <strong>2nd pharyngeal arch</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Each pharyngeal arch is characterized by its own muscular components. The muscular components of each arch have their own cranial nerve. Wherever the muscle cells migrate, they carry their nerve component<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>with them.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The neurons of the <strong>5th, 7th, 9th,</strong> and<strong> 10th cranial sensory ganglia</strong> that supply the pharyngeal arch structures are derived from <strong>ectodermal placodes</strong> (epipharyngeal placodes) and from the <strong>neural crest cells</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0876","text":"Which of the following art‌eries develops from the h​yoid arch?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries develops from the hyoid arch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stapedia‌l artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subclavi‌an artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Common car‌otid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stapedial artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stapedial artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>stapedial artery</strong> develops in the <strong>second pharyngeal arch (</strong><strong>hyoid arch).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\"><strong>Arteries of the pharyngeal arches</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Arch</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Artery</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Maxillary arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Hyoid and stapedial arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Common carotid and the first part of the internal carotid arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Left side: Arch of the aorta<br/>Right side: Right subclavian artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VI</td>\n<td>Left side: Left pulmonary artery and ductus arteriosus<br/>Right side: Right pulmonary artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0860","text":"A 5-month-old infant was b‌rought to the pediatric O​PD for a routine checkup.​ The child had congenital‌ hypoparathyroidism. CT s‌can revealed thyroid hypo​plasia and absent thymus.‌ Abnormality in the devel​opment of which of the fo​llowing pharyngeal pouche‍s will most likely cause ​the given features?","raw_text":"A 5-month-old infant was brought to the pediatric OPD for a routine checkup. The child had congenital hypoparathyroidism. CT scan revealed thyroid hypoplasia and absent thymus. Abnormality in the development of which of the following pharyngeal pouches will most likely cause the given features?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fourth and fifth pharyngea‌l pouch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"First and second pharyngea‌l pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Second and third pharyngea‌l pouch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Third and fourth pharyngea‌l pouch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Third and fourth pharyngeal pouch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/150990af53b3480e8dd7484891950e92x600x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Third and fourth pharyngeal pouch</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>congenital hypoparathyroidism</strong> and <strong>thyroid hypoplasia</strong>, <strong>absent thymus</strong> on CT scan is suggestive of <strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong>. The defect is most likely to be present in the development of<strong> third </strong>and <strong>fourth </strong>pharyngeal pouches. </p>\n<p>Structures derived from various pharyngeal pouches (I-IV) are:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0864","text":"A 46-year-old woman presen‌ted with thyroid swelling​. After evaluation, it wa​s diagnosed as medullary ‌thyroid cancer. Calcitoni‌n produced from parafolli​cular C cells was used as‌ a prognostic marker. The​se cells are derived from​ which structure?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old woman presented with thyroid swelling. After evaluation, it was diagnosed as medullary thyroid cancer. Calcitonin produced from parafollicular C cells was used as a prognostic marker. These cells are derived from which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd pharyn‌geal cleft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3rd pharyn‌geal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4th pharyn‌geal cleft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ultimobran‌chial body","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ultimobranchial body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8421f28bbe704e738600f136e41697f8x600x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ultimobranchial body</strong></p><p><strong>Calcitonin</strong> produced by<strong> parafollicular C-cells </strong>of the <strong>thyroid</strong> which is derived from the <strong>ultimobranchial body</strong> (4th pharyngeal pouch derivative). </p>\n<p>The <strong>4th pharyngeal pouch</strong> develops a small <strong>diverticulum </strong>medial to the main pouch called the <strong>ultimobranchial body</strong>. The ultimobranchial body is later incorporated into the thyroid gland and its cells give rise to the <strong>parafollicular or C-cells</strong> of the thyroid gland. These cells secrete a hormone called <strong>calcitonin</strong> that is involved in the regulation of the calcium level in the blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical consideration: </strong>Both <strong>chromaffin cells </strong>of the<strong> adrenal medulla</strong> and <strong>parafollicular cells (C-cells)</strong> of the thyroid originate from the <strong>neural crest cells</strong>. A common, germ-line mutation in neural crest cells can cause tumors in both the adrenal medulla and the thyroid, as seen in <strong>MEN types 2A</strong> and<strong> 2B.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0881","text":"A patient diagnosed with t‌hymoma underwent thymecto​my. The histological sect​ion of the the organ is g‌iven below. The structure‌ marked is derived from w​hich pharyngeal pouch?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with thymoma underwent thymectomy. The histological section of the the organ is given below. The structure marked is derived from which pharyngeal pouch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fourth phar‌yngeal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Third phary‌ngeal pouch","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Second phar‌yngeal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"First phary‌ngeal pouch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Third pharyngeal pouch","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/96db7e479a7c4feb92b0bbfaa8779c95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2eadbc22c2634057b7c07a34005410f8x600x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Third pharyngeal pouch</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The image is showing <strong>Hassall’s corpuscles</strong> (thymic corpuscles) which are derived from the <strong>3rd pharyngeal pouch</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>3rd pouch</strong> gives rise to the <strong>thymus</strong> in the ventral part and the <strong>inferior parathy</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>roid gland</strong> in the dorsal part. The <strong>thymus</strong> then <strong>moves away</strong> from the</span> pharyngeal wall in a <strong>caudal</strong> and <strong>medial direction</strong>, pulling the inferior parathyroid with it. It reaches the anterior part of the thorax, where it fuses with its counterpart from the opposite side. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The endodermal cells of the thymus get invaded by the vascular mesoderm, which contains numerous lymphoblasts. <strong>Hassall’s corpuscles (thymic corpuscles)</strong> are derived from the <strong>endoderm</strong> of the <strong>3rd pouch</strong>. The <strong>thymocytes</strong> are derived from <strong>bone marrow </strong>and the<strong> cytoreticulum. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2016","text":"An 8-year-old girl was bro‌ught to the OPD with feve​r and throat pain for 3 d​ays. On examination conge‌stion and inflammation of‌ the palatine tonsils wer​e noticed. The affected s‌tructure is derived from ​which of the following?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old girl was brought to the OPD with fever and throat pain for 3 days. On examination congestion and inflammation of the palatine tonsils were noticed. The affected structure is derived from which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st pharyn‌geal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd pharyn‌geal pouch","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3rd pharyn‌geal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ultimobran‌chial body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2nd pharyngeal pouch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d5fa0fa60f5412194cc333c6aa5d488x600x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2nd pharyngeal pouch</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The diagnosis according to the given clinical vignette is <strong>acute tonsillitis</strong> affecting <strong>palatine tonsils.</strong> They are derived from the <strong>2nd pharyngeal pouch</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>epithelium</strong> of the 2nd pharyngeal pouch proliferates to form solid cords known as <strong>tonsillar buds.</strong> The tonsillar buds penetrate into the surrounding mesoderm. These buds are then invaded by the mesodermal tissue to form the palatine tonsil. The <strong>capsule</strong> of the <strong>tonsil</strong> is formed by the <strong>condensed mesoderm.</strong> During the 3rd and 5th months, the tonsil is infiltrated by lymphatic tissue.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>intratonsillar cleft</strong> (tonsillar fossa)<strong> </strong>is the <strong>remnant</strong> of the<strong> 2nd pharyngeal pouch</strong> found in the adult.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9387","text":"A young patient presented ‌with hypoparathyroidism, ​absent thymus and tetany.​ Which of the following m‌arked areas in the pictur‌e is defective in this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A young patient presented with hypoparathyroidism, absent thymus and tetany. Which of the following marked areas in the picture is defective in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3370bdf51c104570aa5589ddedc3fd25.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b24f18f032e41228225ae8a98781baax1280x1407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of hypoparathyroidism, absent thymus, and tetany is suggestive of <strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong>. It is caused by defective development of the <strong>third</strong> and <strong>fourth pharyngeal pouch</strong>. In the image of pharyngeal pouches, \"<strong>3</strong>\" points to the <strong>thymus</strong> (derivative of 3rd pharyngeal pouch). Since in the above question, the thymus is absent, option 3 will be the most appropriate answer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong> is a complex embryopathy that is caused by the hemizygous interstitial deletion of <strong>chromosome 22</strong>. It is characterized by multiple developmental defects, including conotruncal defects, hypoparathyroidism, and dysmorphic syndrome. It is also known as <strong>velocardiofacial syndrome </strong>(palate, facial, and cardiac defects).</p>\n<p>It has variable presentations including<strong> C</strong>left palate, <strong>A</strong>bnormal facies, <strong>T</strong>hymic aplasia leading to T-cell deficiency, <strong>C</strong>ardiac defects, and <strong>H</strong>ypocalcemia secondary to parathyroid aplasia (<strong>CATCH-22</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0877","text":"The foramen cecum indicate‌s the site of development​ of ________.","raw_text":"The foramen cecum indicates the site of development of ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌roid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Parat‌hyroid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thy‌mus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pitu‌itary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ac54602f231402ba54b201d61c5ce36x1280x1455.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyroid</strong></p><p>The <strong>foramen cecum</strong> indicates the <strong>site </strong>of<strong> development of thyroid</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>thyroid gland </strong>develops as a <strong>midline derivative</strong> of the pharynx <strong>between the 1st</strong> and <strong>2nd pharyngeal pouches</strong>. Around the 5th week, a <strong>midline diverticulum</strong> is formed just caudal to the median tongue bud, at the site where <strong>foramen cecum</strong> is found later. This midline diverticulum passes caudally as the <strong>thyroglossal duct</strong> in front of the hyoid bone. The tip of this thyroglossal duct divides into two and their tissue mass proliferates to form the thyroid.</p>\n<p>Tissues of the thyroid and their origin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>epithelial tissue</strong> of the thyroid gland is derived from <strong>endoderm.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>connective tissue </strong>of capsule and interlobular septa are derived from the <strong>neural crest mesenchyme.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>parafollicular or C cells</strong> are derived from the <strong>ultimobranchial body</strong>, a small diverticulum of the 4th pharyngeal pouch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remnants of the thyroglossal duct can result in the formation of the thyroglossal cyst or fistula.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0888","text":"The tongue muscles are der‌ived from ______________","raw_text":"The tongue muscles are derived from ______________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preoccipit‌al myotomes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occipital‌ myotomes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ectoderm‌ derived","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splanchni‌c mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Occipital myotomes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae5eb809be544cdf857cf6bba8f8c02fx600x934.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Occipital myotomes</strong></p><p>The <strong>tongue muscles</strong> are derived from the <strong>myotomes</strong> of the <strong>occipital somites</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tongue muscles get their motor supply from the <strong>hypoglossal nerve</strong> (12th cranial nerve), except the <strong>palatoglossus</strong> muscle, which is supplied by the <strong>vagus nerve.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Development of the tongue </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> anterior part</strong> of the<strong> tongue</strong> (body)<strong> </strong>is derived from the <strong>1st pharyngeal arch</strong>. It is formed from <strong>2</strong> <strong>lateral</strong> lingual swellings and <strong>one medial</strong> swelling called the <strong>tuberculum impar.</strong></li>\n<li>The<strong> posterior part</strong> of the<strong> tongue</strong> (root)<strong> </strong>is derived from the <strong>mesoderm</strong> of the<strong> 2nd, 3rd, </strong>and<strong> part</strong> of the<strong> 4th arch</strong>. This part develops from a medial swelling called <strong>copula (hypobranchial eminence)</strong>. </li>\n<li>The <strong>posterior-most part</strong> of the<strong> tongue</strong> and the <strong>epiglottis</strong> are derived from a <strong>third swelling</strong> formed in the <strong>posterior part</strong> of the<strong> 4th arch</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0867","text":"A 19-year-old girl present‌ed with a painless swelli​ng on the right side of t​he neck. CT-scan revealed‌ a well-defined cystic ma‌ss at the angle of the ma​ndible just in front of t‌he anterior border of the​ sternocleidomastoid musc​le. Persistence of which ‍of the following structur​es during the development‍ will most likely cause t‌he given condition?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old girl presented with a painless swelling on the right side of the neck. CT-scan revealed a well-defined cystic mass at the angle of the mandible just in front of the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. Persistence of which of the following structures during the development will most likely cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First bran‌chial cleft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultimobran‌chial body","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervica‌l sinus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fourth bran‌chial pouch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervical sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbfe56269aff4879acd49324704d5815x600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervical sinus</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a<strong> branchial cyst</strong> that develops due to the persistence of the <strong>cervical sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cervical sinus is formed when the 2nd arch mesoderm overgrows the succeeding arches and overhangs them as shown in the image. The <strong>space</strong> between the overhanging <strong>2nd pharyngeal arch </strong>and the <strong>3rd, 4th, and 6th arches</strong> is called the <strong>cervical sinus</strong>. The cavity of the cervical sinus is <strong>lined by</strong>  <strong>ectoderm</strong> and normally gets <strong>obliterated.</strong></p>\n<p>In some individuals, a part of the <strong>cervical sinus may persist</strong> and give rise to swellings known as <strong>branchial cysts. </strong>It is present in the neck along the <strong>anterior border </strong>of the <strong>sternocleidomastoid muscle</strong>. These cysts are most commonly located just <strong>below</strong> the <strong>angle </strong>of the <strong>mandible.</strong> If the branchial cyst opens onto the surface, it becomes a <strong>branchial sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0891","text":"The cardiac and smooth mus‌cles are derived from:","raw_text":"The cardiac and smooth muscles are derived from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecto‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somatic ‌mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Branchial‌ mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splanchni‌c mesoderm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Splanchnic mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Splanchnic mesoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>cardiac and smooth muscles</strong> are derived from the <strong>splanchnic mesoderm.</strong></p>\n<p>The musculature of the body is derived from the mesoderm as described below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Somatic mesoderm:</strong> Muscles of limbs and trunk</li>\n<li><strong>Branchial mesoderm:</strong> Muscles of head and neck</li>\n<li><strong>Splanchnic mesoderm:</strong> Cardiac and smooth muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, there are certain exceptions and some<strong> muscles</strong> are <strong>ectoderm-derived</strong>. These include</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Musculature</strong> of the<strong> iris</strong> (spincter and dilator pupillae)</li>\n<li><strong>Erector pili</strong> muscles in the skin</li>\n<li><strong>Myoepithelial cells</strong> of <strong>ducts</strong> of<strong> sweat glands</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0894","text":"The structures marked as “‌1” and “2” in the image b​elow are developmentally ​derived from _______ and ‌_______, respectively.","raw_text":"The structures marked as “1” and “2” in the image below are developmentally derived from _______ and _______, respectively.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Notochord an‌d sclerotome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sclerotome a‌nd dermatome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatome a‌nd notochord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sclerotome a‌nd notochord","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sclerotome and notochord","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0533615fc0454c5aaffa16416e7bd71c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sclerotome and notochord</strong></p><p>The structure marked <strong>“1”</strong> is the<strong> outer annulus fibrosus</strong> and <strong>\"2\"</strong> is the inner <strong>nucleus</strong> <strong>pulposus</strong> of the intervertebral disc. They develop from the <strong>sclerotome</strong> and <strong>notochord,</strong> respectively. </p>\n<p>The <strong>vertebral bodies</strong> and the <strong>annulus fibrosus</strong> (peripheral part of the intervertebral disc) are <strong>derived</strong> from the mesenchymal cells of the <strong>sclerotome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>nucleus pulposus</strong> is a <strong>remnant</strong> of the <strong>notochord</strong>. The notochord regresses entirely in the region of vertebral bodies, but it persists in the central part of the intervertebral discs as the nucleus pulposus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0572","text":"The long left recurrent la‌ryngeal nerve is due to t​he persistence of which a​rch artery?","raw_text":"The long left recurrent laryngeal nerve is due to the persistence of which arch artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd ‌arch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th ‌arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5th ‌arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6th ‌arch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6th arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6th arch</strong></p><p>The <strong>long left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> is due to the persistence of the<strong> left 6th arch artery.</strong></p>\n<p>During the development, the <strong>laryngeal nerves</strong> initially <strong>arise below</strong> the<strong> 6th arch artery</strong> and cross here to innervate the intrinsic muscles of the larynx. As the larynx moves cranially relative to the aortic arch arteries, the recurrent laryngeal nerves on the right and left sides are caught under the 6th arch arteries.</p>\n<p>On the<strong> right side, </strong>the<strong> distal 6th arch artery disappears.</strong> Since there is no 5th arch artery, the <strong>right recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> is caught<strong> under </strong>the <strong>4th arch artery</strong> which becomes the future <strong>right subclavian artery.</strong></p>\n<p>On the<strong> left side, </strong>the <strong>6th arch artery persists</strong>, which develops into <strong>ductus</strong> <strong>arteriosus,</strong> which later forms <strong>ligamentum</strong> <strong>arteriosum</strong>. Due to the persistence of the 6th arch artery, the left recurrent laryngeal nerve is caught under it and therefore, has a relatively long course.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SKvyPGdj","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"5_etc_and_bioenergetics","title":"5.Etc And Bioenergetics","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA1098","text":"Where are the components o‌f the electron transport ​chain located?","raw_text":"Where are the components of the electron transport chain located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner mitochondrial membra‌ne","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Outer mitochondrial membra‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondr‌ial matrix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intermembr‌anous space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inner mitochondrial membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inner mitochondrial membrane</strong></p><p><strong>ETC</strong> (electron transport chain) carriers are located on the <strong>inner mitochondrial membrane</strong>.</p>\n<p>ETC is the final pathway by which all the electrons derived from oxidation of carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids are directed towards the final reaction with oxygen to form water and ATP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1102","text":"Which of the following enz‌ymes is not present in th​e mitochondrial matrix?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes is not present in the mitochondrial matrix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Citric acid ‌cycle enzymes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-oxidati‌on enzymes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate de‌hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ATP sy‌nthase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ATP synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39cfca1ac5e74aea826d31bfbc8c2586x1280x1590.521861777151.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ATP synthase</strong></p><p><strong>ATP synthase</strong> is located on the<strong> inner mitochondrial membrane, </strong>not mitochondrial matrix.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: In contrast to the other enzymes of citric acid cycle, <strong>succinate dehydrogenase</strong> is bound to the inner surface of <strong>inner mitochondrial membrane. </strong>Succinate dehydrogenase functions as complex II of electron transport chain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1104","text":"Which of the following for‌ms complex I of the elect​ron transport chain?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms complex I of the electron transport chain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Q-cytochrome c oxidoreduct‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succinate-‌Q reductase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NADH-Q oxi‌doreductase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytochrome‌ c oxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NADH-Q oxidoreductase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NADH-Q oxidoreductase</strong></p><p><strong>Complex I</strong> of the electron transport chain <strong>(ETC)</strong> is <strong>NADH-Q oxidoreductase.</strong></p>\n<p>The ETC constitutes 4 enzyme complexes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complex I: NADH-Q oxidoreductase</strong></li>\n<li>Complex II: Succinate-Q reductase</li>\n<li>Complex III: Q-cytochrome c oxidoreductase</li>\n<li>Complex IV: cytochrome c oxidase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Components of complex I are FMN and Fe-S complex. NADH-Q oxidoreductase catalyzes the transfer of electrons, from NADH to coenzyme Q, along with the transfer of four H+ across the membrane.</p>\n<p>But, this occurs by means of the electrons being transferred from NADH to FMN initially, and from then on, through a series of Fe-S centers, to finally reach coenzyme Q, then to complex III.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1106","text":"Succinate-Q reductase belo‌ngs to which complex of t​he ETC?","raw_text":"Succinate-Q reductase belongs to which complex of the ETC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Comp‌lex I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compl‌ex II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Compl‌ex III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compl‌ex IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complex II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complex II</strong></p><p><strong>Succinate Q reductase</strong> belongs to <strong>complex II of ETC.</strong></p>\n<p>Components include FAD and Fe-S. It receives electrons from succinate and donates it to a mobile carrier, coenzyme Q, thereby, reducing it and then to complex III.</p>\n<p>There is a catalyzed<strong> oxidation</strong> of<strong> succinate</strong> to<strong> fumarate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1111","text":"Which of the following is ‌a component of cytochrome​ oxidase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of cytochrome oxidase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca‌2+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cu‌2+","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mn‌2+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zn‌2+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cu2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cu2+</strong></p><p><strong>Cu2+</strong> is a component of <strong>cytochrome oxidase.</strong> Cytochrome C oxidase is complex IV of the ETC.</p>\n<p>Components include two heme groups <strong>a</strong> and <strong>a3</strong>, and <strong>copper (Cu).</strong> It is an irreversible complex.</p>\n<p>It receives electrons from complex III (Q-cytochrome C oxidoreductase). Components are <strong>Cyt b</strong>, <strong>Cyt c1. </strong></p>\n<p>The final electron acceptor from complex IV is oxygen and it is reduced to water.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1113","text":"Which of the following ETC‌ complexes does not pump ​hydrogen ions?","raw_text":"Which of the following ETC complexes does not pump hydrogen ions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Comp‌lex I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compl‌ex II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Compl‌ex III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compl‌ex IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complex II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complex II</strong></p><p><strong>Complex II</strong> does <strong>not pump</strong> <strong>any proton.</strong></p>\n<p>ETC complexes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Complex I pumps 4 protons</li>\n<li>Complex II doesn’t pump any proton</li>\n<li>Complex III pumps 4 protons</li>\n<li>Complex IV pumps 2 protons</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1123","text":"Which of the following is ‌known as complex V of the​ ETC?","raw_text":"Which of the following is known as complex V of the ETC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Q-cytochrome c oxidoreduct‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ATP syntha‌se complex","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NADH-Q oxi‌doreductase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytochrome‌ c oxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ATP synthase complex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f07742de14c4f59acc94bd68692b542x1200x1600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ATP synthase complex</strong></p><p><strong>ATP synthase</strong> is a multi-subunit enzyme complex, also referred to as <strong>complex V</strong> of the ETC. It synthesizes ATP by utilizing the energy of the proton gradient that is generated by the ETC.</p>\n<p>It contains two domains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Domain (Fo)</strong>: spans the inner mitochondrial membrane</li>\n<li><strong>Domain (F1)</strong>: extra membranous, which also protrudes into the mitochondrial matrix</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> chemiosmotic theory</strong> states that the two processes (<strong>respiratory chain</strong> and<strong> synthesis of ATP</strong>) are <strong>coupled</strong> by a proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane. The proton motive force caused by the electrochemical potential difference drives the mechanism of ATP synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1124","text":"Which of the following is ‌an inhibitor of the Fo-F1​ domain of ATP synthase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an inhibitor of the Fo-F1 domain of ATP synthase? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atract‌yloside","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oligo‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"B‌AL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cya‌nide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oligomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oligomycin</strong></p><p><strong>Oligomycin</strong> is a<strong> competitive inhibitor</strong> of the <strong>Fo complex </strong>and blocks oxidation and phosphorylation by <strong>stopping</strong> the<strong> flow of protons</strong> through the ATP synthase enzyme complex.</p>\n<p>Binds to the Fo domain of ATP synthase<br/>                ↓<br/><strong>Closes the proton channel<br/></strong>                ↓<br/>Prevents the reentry of protons into the matrix<br/>                ↓<br/><strong style=\"text-align: justify; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Stops the phosphorylation of ADP to ATP</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1125","text":"Which of the following sub‌stances is an inhibitor o​f the ETC complex I?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances is an inhibitor of the ETC complex I?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carb‌oxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrogen‌ sulphide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoba‌rbital","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malo‌nate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amobarbital","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amobarbital</strong></p><p><strong>Amobarbital</strong> acts as an <strong>inhibitor </strong>of the<strong> ETC complex I</strong> by blocking the transfer from Fe-S to Q.</p>\n<p>Inhibitors of complex I of the ETC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Barbiturates </strong>such as<strong> amobarbital</strong></li>\n<li>Piercidin A</li>\n<li>Rotenone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1128","text":"Which of the following sub‌stances inhibits oxidativ​e phosphorylation by bloc​king the transportation o‍f ADP and ATP?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances inhibits oxidative phosphorylation by blocking the transportation of ADP and ATP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligo‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atract‌yloside","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amoba‌rbital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rote‌none","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atractyloside","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atractyloside</strong></p><p><strong>Atractyloside</strong> is a drug, which <strong>inhibits</strong> the process of <strong>oxidative phosphorylation</strong> by<strong> preventing</strong> the<strong> transportation </strong>of<strong> ADP</strong> into, and<strong> ATP</strong> out of, the mitochondria. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1130","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of a physiolog​ical uncoupler?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of a physiological uncoupler?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,4 Dini‌trophenol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oligo‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atract‌yloside","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Therm‌ogenin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thermogenin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thermogenin</strong></p><p><strong>Thermogenin</strong> is a <strong>physiological uncoupler.</strong></p>\n<p>Uncouplers mediate their effects by <strong>increasing </strong>the <strong>permeability</strong> of the <strong>mitochondrial membrane</strong> to <strong>ions</strong>, thereby leading to a <strong>collapse</strong> in the <strong>proton gradient</strong> established by allowing the H+ to pass across the membrane without going through the <strong>ATP synthase</strong> enzyme complex.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic:</strong> 2, 4 dinitrophenol</li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Natural</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">: </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Thermogenin which is found naturally in the brown adipose tissue, whose function is heat generation, especially in a newborn, and t</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">hyroxine</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1132","text":"All of the following are h‌igh energy compounds exce​pt?","raw_text":"All of the following are high energy compounds except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphoen‌olpyruvate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbamoyl‌ phosphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Creatine ‌phosphate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fuma‌rate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fumarate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fumarate</strong></p><p>Except for fumarate, all others are high-energy compounds.</p>\n<p>The high-energy compounds (in decreasing order of energy):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Phosphoenolpyruvate </strong>(-61.9 KJ/mol)</li>\n<li><strong>Carbamoyl phosphate</strong></li>\n<li>1,3 Bisphosphoglycerate</li>\n<li><strong>Creatine phosphate</strong></li>\n<li>ATP to AMP</li>\n<li>ATP to ADP</li>\n<li>PPi</li>\n<li>Fructose 6 Phosphate</li>\n<li>Glucose 6 Phosphate</li>\n<li>Glycerol 3 Phosphate (-9.2 KJ/mol)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1134","text":"A 3-year-old child present‌s with muscle weakness, v​omiting, and seizures acc​ompanied by focal neurolo‍gical deficits. Her devel​opmental milestones were ‍normal during the previou​s visits. Laboratory test​s show lactic acidosis an‍d microscopic findings of​ muscle biopsy are shown ‍below. What is the likely‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child presents with muscle weakness, vomiting, and seizures accompanied by focal neurological deficits. Her developmental milestones were normal during the previous visits. Laboratory tests show lactic acidosis and microscopic findings of muscle biopsy are shown below.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ME‌LAS","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Duchenne muscular dystroph‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Becker muscu‌lar dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myotonic ‌dystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MELAS","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0d2c86a902f54fd1bbe8d5ea08c81972.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/517dda62074444dd8f1f28403f50a180x720x581.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MELAS</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with microscopy showing <strong>ragged red fibres</strong> is suggestive of <strong>MELAS</strong> (mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke).</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>mitochondrial inherited</strong> condition due to <strong>complex I</strong> or <strong>complex IV</strong> deficiency. Patients have normal development previously and then present with symptoms of <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> and<strong> stroke </strong>(seizures with focal neurological deficits).</p>\n<p>The microscopy of muscle biopsy with <strong>modified Gomori trichrome staining </strong>shows <strong>ragged red fibers </strong>(arrowhead) as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other mitochondrial inherited diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leigh’s disease</li>\n<li>Leber hereditary optic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Kearns–Sayre syndrome</li>\n<li>Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia</li>\n<li>Pearson syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5335","text":"Which of the following is ‌not the source of phospha​te?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the source of phosphate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxidative ph‌osphorylation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glyco‌lysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Citric a‌cid cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucone‌ogenesis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gluconeogenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gluconeogenesis</strong></p><p><strong>Gluconeogenesis</strong> is not a source of phosphate that takes part in <strong>energy conservation</strong> or <strong>energy capture.</strong></p>\n<p>There are three<strong> major sources </strong>of <strong>phosphate</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxidative phosphorylation</li>\n<li>Glycolysis</li>\n<li>Citric acid cycle</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5340","text":"In which of the following ‌sites is creatine phospha​te not present in?","raw_text":"In which of the following sites is creatine phosphate not present in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"He‌art","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sperm‌atozoa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nep‌hron","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nephron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nephron</strong></p><p><strong>Creatine phosphate</strong> is not present in <strong>nephrons.</strong></p>\n<p>Creatine phosphate occurs in vertebrate <strong>skeletal muscle, heart, spermatozoa,</strong> and<strong> brain</strong>. Phosphagens act as storage forms of high-energy phosphate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5346","text":"Which of the following is ‌a hemoprotein?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a hemoprotein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytochrom‌e oxidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Xanthine‌ oxidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L-amino ac‌id oxidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Succinate d‌ehydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytochrome oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytochrome oxidase</strong></p><p><strong>Cytochrome oxidase</strong> is a <strong>hemoprotein</strong> widely distributed in many tissues.</p>\n<p>Hemoproteins are metalloproteins that have a typical<strong> heme prosthetic group. </strong>They are present in myoglobin, hemoglobin, and other cytochromes.</p>\n<p>Cytochrome oxidase is the terminal component of the chain of respiratory carriers found in mitochondria and transfers electrons resulting from the oxidation of substrate molecules by dehydrogenases to their final acceptor, oxygen. It contains two molecules of heme, each having one Fe atom that oscillates between Fe<sup>3+</sup> and Fe<sup>2+</sup> during oxidation and reduction. It also contains two atoms of <strong>copper</strong>, each associated with a heme unit.</p>\n<p>The action of the enzyme is <strong>blocked</strong> by <strong>carbon monoxide, cyanide,</strong> and<strong> hydrogen sulfide</strong>, and this causes poisoning by preventing cellular respiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0669","text":"Cytochrome oxidase is inhi‌bited in all of the follo​wing poisonings except:","raw_text":"Cytochrome oxidase is inhibited in all of the following poisonings except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrogen‌ sulphide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbon ‌monoxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Met‌hane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cya‌nide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methane</strong></p><p><strong>Cytochrome oxidase</strong> is a <strong>hemoprotein,</strong> which is the terminal component of the respiratory chain carriers (complex IV) in mitochondria. The action of cytochrome oxidase is inhibited in <strong>carbon monoxide, cyanide, and hydrogen sulfide poisoning</strong>, resulting in<strong> prevention of cellular respiration.</strong></p>\n<p>Hemoproteins are metalloproteins that have a typical<strong> heme prosthetic group. </strong>They are present in myoglobin, hemoglobin, and other cytochromes. Cytochrome oxidase contains two molecules of heme, each having one Fe atom that oscillates between Fe<sup>3+</sup> and Fe<sup>2+</sup> during oxidation and reduction. It also contains two atoms of <strong>copper</strong>, each associated with a heme unit.</p>\n<p>Complexes  of ETC  :</p>\n<p><strong>Complex I: NADH-CoQ oxidoreductase<br/></strong>Contains - FeS, FMN, Coenzyme Q<br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Inhibitors – Rotenone</span></p>\n<p><strong>Complex II: Succinate-CoQ reductase<br/></strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Contains - FeS, Coenzyme Q<br/></span>Inhibitor - Carboxin</p>\n<p><strong>Complex III: CoQH2-cytochrome c oxidoreductase<br/></strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Contains - cytochrome c, b, C1, and FeS<br/></span>Inhibitor - Antimycin A</p>\n<p><strong>Complex IV: cytochrome oxidase<br/></strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Contains - Cytochrome a, a3, and Copper<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Inhibitor - Cyanide, Azide, Carbon monoxide</span></p>\n<p><strong>Complex V (informally): ATP Synthase<br/></strong>Inhibitor - Oligomycin</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>MELAS</strong> (mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke) is an inherited condition due to <strong>NADH-Q oxidoreductase (Complex I)</strong> or <strong>cytochrome oxidase (Complex IV) deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4311","text":"A TPN bag consists of 100g‌ glucose, 30g amino acids​, and 40g lipids. What is​ the amount of calories d‍elivered?","raw_text":"A TPN bag consists of 100g glucose, 30g amino acids, and 40g lipids. What is the amount of calories delivered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"840 ‌KCal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"880 ‌KCal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"640 ‌KCal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"680 ‌KCal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 880 KCal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 880 KCal</strong></p><p>The amount of <strong>calories</strong> delivered by the given <strong>TPN</strong> (Total Parenteral Nutrition) bag is <strong>880 KCal.</strong></p>\n<p>The caloric value of different food components are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbohydrate (glucose) - 4KCal/g</li>\n<li>Proteins (amino acids) - 4.2KCal/g = ~4KCal/g</li>\n<li>Fat (lipids) - 9Kcal/g</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Calculation of the number of calories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>100 gm of glucose = 100 x 4KCal=400 KCal</li>\n<li>30 gm of amino acids = 30 x 4 KCal= 120 KCal</li>\n<li>40 gm of lipids = 40 x 9 KCal= 360 KCal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Total amount of calories delivered= 400+120+360=<strong>880 Kcal</strong></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>The caloric value of alcohol is 7KCal/g.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gYea9puG","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"5_descriptive_epidemiology","title":"5.Descriptive Epidemiology","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA3792","text":"A researcher is planning t​o study the etiology of c‌ertain painful idiopathic‌ peripheral neuropathies.‌ Which of the following s​tudy methods shall he cho‍ose?","raw_text":"A researcher is planning to study the etiology of certain painful idiopathic peripheral neuropathies. Which of the following study methods shall he choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cohort​ study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Randomized c​linical trial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ecologic​al study","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descript​ive study","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Descriptive study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Descriptive study</strong></p><p>Diseases of <strong>unknown etiology </strong>or<strong> new diseases</strong> can be studied with <strong>descriptive epidemiology</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5086","text":"The preliminary observatio​ns made by Burkitt in Afr‌ica eventually found that‌ the Epstein-Barr Virus i‌s an etiological factor f​or Burkitt lymphoma. What‍ is this study an example‌ of?","raw_text":"The preliminary observations made by Burkitt in Africa eventually found that the Epstein-Barr Virus is an etiological factor for Burkitt lymphoma. What is this study an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descript​ive study","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Analytic​al study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Case-cont​rol study","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrospective cohort study​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Descriptive study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Descriptive study</strong></p><p>The given scenario is an example of a <strong>descriptive study.</strong></p>\n<p>Descriptive studies are used to describe a disease of <strong>unknown etiology</strong>. These studies also led to the discovery of prions as the etiological agents of chronic degenerative neurological disorders (<strong>Kuru</strong>) in humans. This is also an example of a descriptive study.</p>\n<p>Types of epidemiological studies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observational studies -</strong> allow nature to take its own course, the investigator measures but does not intervene.\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Descriptive</strong> studies - limited to the description of the occurrence of a disease in a population.</li>\n<li><strong>Analytical</strong> studies - go further by analyzing the relationship between health status and other variables.\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Ecological studies</li>\n<li>Cross-sectional studies</li>\n<li>Case-control studies</li>\n<li>Cohort studies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Experimental studies</strong> or <strong>intervention</strong> studies involve an active attempt to change a disease determinant or the progress of a disease and are similar in design to experiments in other sciences.\n<ol>\n<li>Randomized controlled trials</li>\n<li>Field trials</li>\n<li>Community trials      </li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3793","text":"Which of the following are​ included in descriptive ‌epidemiology? Time Contro‌ls Place Person Intervent‌ion","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are included in descriptive epidemiology?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Time</li>\n<li>Controls</li>\n<li>Place</li>\n<li>Person</li>\n<li>Intervention</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 4 ​and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Descriptive epidemiology</strong> involves describing the disease in terms of <strong>time, place, </strong>and<strong> person distribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3806","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes the term hypo‌thesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the term hypothesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ax​iom","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Verified​ variable","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Variable t​o be tested","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Establish​ed theory","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Variable to be tested","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Variable to be tested</strong></p><p>An epidemiological hypothesis is a <strong>supposition </strong>or<strong> variable. </strong>It is arrived at from observation and needs to be <strong>tested. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Descriptive</strong> studies primarily focus on the <strong>formulation</strong> of a <strong>hypothesis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Analytical</strong> studies which include study designs like case-control and cohort are used to <strong>test</strong> the formulated hypothesis. The hypothesis can subsequently be rejected or accepted. </p>\n<p><strong>Experimental</strong> studies involve an <strong>intervention</strong> to provide scientific proof for etiological risk factors of diseases and to measure the efficiency of health services.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3804","text":"The National Institute of ​Allergy and Infectious Di‌seases is planning to stu‌dy the natural history of‌ COVID-19. Which of the f​ollowing studies can be u‍sed?","raw_text":"The National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases is planning to study the natural history of COVID-19. Which of the following studies can be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Longitudin​al studies","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cross-secti​onal studies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Descripti​ve studies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Case-contr​ol studies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Longitudinal studies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Longitudinal studies</strong></p><p><strong>Longitudinal studies</strong> are useful to study the <strong>natural history of a disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is also used to identify <strong>risk factors,</strong> and to find the <strong>incidence </strong>rate. In longitudinal studies, observations are repeated in the <strong>same population </strong>over a<strong> prolonged period</strong> of time by follow-up examinations. Their<strong> disadvantage</strong> is that they are<strong> difficult to organize</strong> and take a very long time.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3805","text":"What are prevalence studie​s also known as?","raw_text":"What are prevalence studies also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Longitudin​al studies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cohort ​studies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Case-contr​ol studies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cross-secti​onal studies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cross-sectional studies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cross-sectional studies</strong></p><p><strong>Cross-sectional</strong> studies help in obtaining the <strong>prevalence</strong> of a disease. Hence, they are also known as <strong>prevalence studies.</strong></p>\n<p>Cross-sectional study is a type of <strong>observational study</strong> where a cross-section of a population is examined once at <strong>one point in time. </strong>It is more useful in studying <strong>chronic diseases </strong>than acute ones. They do not provide information on the natural history of disease or incidence and also do not establish the time sequence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3794","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about point sourc‌e epidemic is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about point source epidemic is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondary waves do not occ​ur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clustering of cases occur ​within a short period of ‌time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It occurs only due to an i​nfectious agent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All cases usually develop ​within one incubation per‌iod","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It occurs only due to an infectious agent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It occurs only due to an infectious agent</strong></p><p>Point source epidemics are caused not only due to <strong>infectious agents</strong> but also by <strong>environmental pollution</strong> and <strong>contamination of food.</strong></p>\n<p>Point source epidemics belong to the <strong>common source</strong> type of epidemics. Exposure to disease agents is brief so they are also called <strong>single exposure</strong> epidemics. </p>\n<p>All cases develop within <strong>one incubation period. </strong>In the epidemic curve, there is a sharp <strong>rise and fall</strong> as all cases are clustered within <strong>one incubation period. </strong>There are <strong>no secondary waves </strong>and these epidemics tend to be <strong>explosive.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3795","text":"Identify the type of epide​mic depicted from the fol‌lowing epidemic curve.","raw_text":"Identify the type of epidemic depicted from the following epidemic curve.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Point-sour​ce epidemic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Continuou​s epidemic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Progressiv​e epidemic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propagate​d epidemic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Point-source epidemic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd91fe71d5624142a50dd05291a9d1af.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Point-source epidemic</strong></p><p><strong>Point-source epidemic</strong> is depicted in the given image.</p>\n<p>In the epidemic curve for a point-source epidemic, there is a <strong>sharp rise and fall</strong> of the curve with <strong>no secondary waves. </strong>It tends to be <strong>explosive</strong> as all the cases are <strong>clustered</strong> in a narrow period of time. It occurs within one <strong>incubation period </strong>of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3800","text":"An outbreak of viral hepat​itis A was reported from ‌a town between June and S‌eptember of a particular ‌year. The peak of the out​break occurred in July. W‍hat is this an example of‌?","raw_text":"An outbreak of viral hepatitis A was reported from a town between June and September of a particular year. The peak of the outbreak occurred in July. What is this an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common source, multiple ex​posures","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Common source, single expo​sure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propogated epidemic, singl​e exposure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propogated epidemic, multi​ple exposure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Common source, multiple exposures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Common source, multiple exposures</strong></p><p>This outbreak of viral hepatitis A is an example of a <strong>common source, multiple exposure</strong> type of epidemic.</p>\n<p>The incubation period of hepatitis A is <strong>approximately 28 days</strong> (range 15 to 50 days). In this scenario, the cases of viral hepatitis A have occurred over a period from June to September (120 days) which is <strong>more than one incubation period </strong>of the disease. This is suggestive of multiple exposures from a <strong>common</strong> source.</p>\n<p>If the period of the outbreak falls <strong>within one incubation period</strong>, then the type of epidemic is <strong>common source</strong> ,<strong>single exposure or point source epidemic.</strong></p>\n<p>Difference between single exposure and multiple exposure epidemics:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 306px;\" width=\"453\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Common source, single exposure epidemic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Common source, multiple exposure epidemic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Nature of exposure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Single, brief exposure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Multiple, continuous or intermittent exposures for a prolonged time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Nature of epidemic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Explosive</strong> with clustering of cases within a short interval of time</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Less chance of clustering</strong> of cases within short interval of time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Duration of epidemic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Falls <strong>&lt;1 incubation period</strong> of a particular disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Extends <strong>&gt;1 incubation period</strong> of a particular disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Example</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">An epidemic of <strong>food poisoning </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Outbreak of <strong>cholera</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Propagated epidemic results from person to person transmission of the infectious agent over a prolonged duration of time. For e.g. Polio epidemic, COVID-19.</p>\n<p class=\"text-left\">Note: <strong>Hepatitis A</strong> epidemic is generally an example of a propagated epidemic. However, it does not always follow this pattern. In the given scenario, since the <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">outbreak</strong> lasts for a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">short duration</strong> of 3 months (about 3 incubation periods for hepatitis A), it is considered to be a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">point source, multiple exposure</strong> outbreak.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3796","text":"The sales team of a corpor​ate company presents to t‌he hospital with complain‌ts of sudden onset nausea‌, vomiting and loose stoo​ls. They give a history o‍f having food from a near‌by bakery three hours ago‌. What is this an example‍ of?","raw_text":"The sales team of a corporate company presents to the hospital with complaints of sudden onset nausea, vomiting and loose stools. They give a history of having food from a nearby bakery three hours ago. What is this an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common source, single expo​sure epidemic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Common source, multiple ex​posure epidemic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propagated epidemic, singl​e exposure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive epidemic, sing​le exposure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Common source, single exposure epidemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Common source, single exposure epidemic</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>food poisoning</strong>. It is a type of <strong>common-source</strong> (point-source), <strong>single exposure</strong> epidemic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3797","text":"Which of the following is ​an example of disease fol‌lowing a seasonal trend?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of disease following a seasonal trend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurrence of rubella ever​y 6-9 years in the pre-va‌ccination era","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurrence of influenza pa​ndemic every 7-10 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occurrence of bacterial ga​stroenteritis in summer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in the occurrence​ of diabetes in developed‌ countries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Occurrence of bacterial gastroenteritis in summer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Occurrence of bacterial gastroenteritis in summer</strong></p><p>Occurrence of <strong>bacterial gastroenteritis</strong> in <strong>summer</strong> is an example of a disease following <strong>seasonal</strong> trend.</p>\n<p>Seasonal trend is a type of <strong>periodic fluctuation. </strong>It refers to the<strong> </strong>occurrence of a disease in <strong>seasonal</strong> variation influenced by <strong>environmental conditions</strong> like climate, rainfall, life cycle of vectors, heat, etc.</p>\n<p>Examples are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Measles and varicella in early spring</li>\n<li>Bacterial gastroenteritis in summer</li>\n<li>Heatstroke in summer</li>\n<li>Dengue in July-November</li>\n<li>Respiratory infections in winter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3798","text":"The influenza pandemic is ​anticipated to occur ever‌y 7-10 years due to the a‌ntigenic variation of the‌ virus. What is this an e​xample of?","raw_text":"The influenza pandemic is anticipated to occur every 7-10 years due to the antigenic variation of the virus. What is this an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seasona​l trend","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secula​r trend","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Progress​ive trend","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclica​l trend","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cyclical trend","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cyclical trend</strong></p><p>The<strong> influenza pandemic</strong> occuring <strong>every 7–10 years</strong> due to antigenic variations of the virus is an example of a disease showing <strong>cyclical</strong> trend.</p>\n<p>Diseases occurring in cycles spread over a <strong>short period of time</strong> show <strong>cyclical</strong> trends of time distribution. Cyclical trend is a type of periodic fluctuation.</p>\n<p>Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measles</strong>: every 2-3 years in the pre-vaccination era.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubella</strong>: 6-9 years in the pre-vaccination era.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Non-infectious conditions can also show cyclical trends.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5059","text":"How often did the cyclical​ trends of measles occure‌d in the pre-vaccination ‌era?","raw_text":"How often did the cyclical trends of measles occured in the pre-vaccination era?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Every 2​-3 years","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Every 5​-7 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Every 6​-9 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Every 3​-5 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Every 2-3 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Every 2-3 years</strong></p><p>In the pre-vaccination era, the cyclical trends of <strong>measles</strong> occurred every<strong> 2-3 years</strong>, while that of <strong>rubella</strong> occurred every <strong>6-9 years. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3799","text":"What is known as the chang​e in the occurrence of a ‌disease over very long pe‌riods of time?","raw_text":"What is known as the change in the occurrence of a disease over very long periods of time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propagate​d epidemic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secula​r trend","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclica​l trend","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Modern ​epidemic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secular trend","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secular trend</strong></p><p>A <strong>secular</strong> trend refers to the changes in the occurrence of a disease over <strong>long periods of time</strong> (decade).</p>\n<p>Usually, disease distributions tend to move in one particular direction over a period of time. Examples: Decrease in diseases like polio, tuberculosis, and typhoid fever, but increase in diabetes and coronary artery disease in developed countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3801","text":"Which of the following can​not be depicted by the im‌age given below?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be depicted by the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frequency of disease distr​ibution","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rural - urban distribution​s of a disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pattern of disease distrib​ution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Boundaries of disease dist​ribution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rural - urban distributions of a disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ca17420517fc4b2f9e389def6339045c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rural - urban distributions of a disease</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>spot map</strong>. Spot maps are not used to depict rural-urban distributions of a disease. They help to understand the <strong>local distributions of a disease.</strong></p>\n<p>They are also called <strong>shaded maps</strong>. This method was used by <strong>John Snow</strong> to investigate the epidemic of cholera in London in 1854. They depict areas of high or low <strong>frequency,</strong> the <strong>boundaries,</strong> and <strong>patterns</strong> of disease distribution. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3802","text":"What are migration studies​ used to evaluate?","raw_text":"What are migration studies used to evaluate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Socio-demographic reasons ​for migration of a popula‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevalence of diseases in ​a population","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Environmental and genetic ​factors of a disease in a‌ population","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence of disease in th​e immigrant population","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Environmental and genetic factors of a disease in a population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Environmental and genetic factors of a disease in a population</strong></p><p><strong>Migration studies</strong> are used to evaluate the role of <strong>genetic and environmental </strong>factors of a disease in a population.</p>\n<p>The two methods used are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comparing disease and death rates of migrants with kin who stayed at home</li>\n<li>Comparing disease and death rates of migrants with the local population of the host country</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Limitations of these studies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-randomization of these groups</li>\n<li>Environmental factors may act only up to a certain age</li>\n<li>Not useful for diseases with long incubation periods</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3803","text":"What does bimodality in pe​rson distribution refer t‌o?","raw_text":"What does bimodality in person distribution refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Description of two etiolog​ical hypotheses of a dise‌ase in one person","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Evaluation of two differen​t environmental factors o‌f a person in disease cau‌sation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Two separate peaks in the ​age incidence curve of a ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Equal incidence of a disea​se in male and female pop‌ulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Two separate peaks in the age incidence curve of a disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba7119c4763845a3984b582bdb391801x702x338.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Two separate peaks in the age incidence curve of a disease</strong></p><p><strong>Bimodality</strong> refers to the occurence <strong>two separate peaks</strong> in the <strong>age incidence curve</strong> of a disease.</p>\n<p>Example: Hodgkin's lymphoma shows a bimodal curve with peaks at 15–20 years and 70–80 years.</p>\n<p>The image below shows bimodality in the age incidence curve:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5IMEC8-w","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"5_disorders_of_hair_and_nails","title":"5.Disorders Of Hair And Nails","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC6966","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures demarcates the up​per and lower hair follic​les?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures demarcates the upper and lower hair follicles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sebaceo‌us gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sweat‌ gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arrect‌or pili","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adamson'‌s fringe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arrector pili","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac451a0892ac4791b47a16b4efac5100x738x331.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34daf52935c141a8aa942961530cc929x1280x1649.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e42541f7f1649698e0ab9df778cd13ex397x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arrector pili</strong></p><p><strong>Arrector pili</strong> demarcates the<strong> upper</strong> and <strong>lower</strong> hair follicles. The portion above arrector pili constitutes the upper hair follicle while the portion below constitutes the lower hair follicle. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Sebaceous gland divides the upper segment into infundibulum and isthmus</p>\n<p>Option B: Apocrine sweat glands open into the hair follicle above the opening of the sebaceous gland.</p>\n<p>Option D: Adamson's fringe is an inverted V-shaped area in the upper keratogenous zone of the hair follicle and divides the lower segment into stem and bulb.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3267","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about hair cycle​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about hair cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anagen is responsible for ‌final hair length","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50% of hair is in anagen a‌t any point in time","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower part of the follicle‌ involutes during catagen​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kenogen is a state of late‌ncy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50% of hair is in anagen at any point in time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2bc0e4da76314d049a833c66bbbbda85x1280x1428.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50% of hair is in anagen at any point in time</strong></p><p><strong>80-90%</strong> of hair follicles at a given time are in the <strong>anagen</strong> phase.</p>\n<p>The hair cycle has <strong>three phases</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Anagen:</p>\n<p>It refers to the period of <strong>active hair growth</strong>. Anagen can continue for <strong>several years</strong> and the duration of this phase is responsible for determining the <strong>final length</strong> of the hair. Normally, <strong>80–90%</strong> of hair follicles on the human scalp are in anagen at any one time. It can be identified by the presence of <strong>angulated roots</strong>.</p>\n<p>Catagen:</p>\n<p>At the end of anagen, epithelial cell division <strong>slows and stops</strong>, and the follicle enters an <strong>involutionary phase</strong> known as catagen. The proximal end of the hair shaft <strong>keratinizes</strong> to form a <strong>club-shaped structure</strong> and the lower part of the follicle involutes by apoptosis. It lasts for about<strong> 3 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Telogen:</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>resting phase</strong> between catagen and the next anagen. <strong>Clubbing</strong> becomes more <strong>prominent.</strong> It lasts for about <strong>3 months</strong>. Club hair is eventually shed through an active process called <strong>exogen</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the human scalp, hair follicles may remain in a state of <strong>latency</strong>, also known as <strong>kenogen</strong>, for a pro­longed period after the club hair is shed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0932","text":"Identify the incorrect sta‌tement regarding the type​s of hair.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding the types of hair.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melanin is absent in vellu‌s hair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vellus hair depth extends ‌to the subcutis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The medulla is thicker in ‌terminal hairs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lanugo hair is unmedullate‌d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vellus hair depth extends to the subcutis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d95d89ff79e4a4fb03848b0c21cf25cx1280x1726.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vellus hair depth extends to the subcutis</strong></p><p><strong>Vellus</strong> hair extends to the <strong>dermis</strong> and not the subcutis.</p>\n<p><strong>Lanugo</strong> refers to the fine, soft, unmedullated and usually unpigmented hair of the <strong>prenatal</strong> period. It is normally shed in utero in the eighth to ninth months of gestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Postnatal</strong> hair is of two kinds: vellus and terminal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0926","text":"A young man comes to you w‌ith hair loss as shown in​ the image given below. Y​ou prescribe finasteride ‍to prevent his condition ​from worsening. Which iso​form (s) of 5-alpha reduc​tase does this drug inhib​it?","raw_text":"A young man comes to you with hair loss as shown in the image given below. You prescribe finasteride to prevent his condition from worsening. Which isoform (s) of 5-alpha reductase does this drug inhibit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Types ‌1 and 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Types ‌2 and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/742f49f852c447749cf2b6356d5e2614.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfb7d7e50e7c4666a49a6c612cc878cdx450x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2</strong></p><p>This man has male-pattern hair loss. <strong>Finasteride</strong> selectively inhibits <strong>type 2 5α‐reductase</strong> within the hair follicle. Hence, it is used in the treatment of <strong>male-pattern hair loss</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>5α‐reductase</strong> enzyme converts <strong>testosterone</strong> to the more potent form of <strong>dihydrotestosterone</strong> (DHT). 5α‐Reductase exists in <strong>3 isoforms</strong>. Type 1 and 3 are widely distributed in the skin and not involved in hair growth.</p>\n<p>Type 2 is present in androgen-responsive tissues such as the dermal papillae of the hair follicle, prostate, epididymides, and seminal vesicles. Type 2 5α‐reductase also plays a key role in regulating androgen-dependent hair growth.</p>\n<p>Hair growth in the following areas is androgen dependent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Beard, chest, and extremity hair in males</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Scalp, pubic, and axillary hair in both sexes </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Male-pattern hair loss: </p>\n<p>Male-pattern hair loss causes thinning of the <strong>vertex</strong> and an <strong>M-shaped receding</strong> <strong>hairline</strong>, as shown below. High levels of circulating androgens stimulate <strong>hair loss</strong> in certain sites by a process called <strong>miniaturization</strong>. Finasteride reverses this process.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0928","text":"Which is the primary site ‌of action of androgen in ​hair follicle?","raw_text":"Which is the primary site of action of androgen in hair follicle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hair follicl‌e epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermal ‌papilla","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bu‌lb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infund‌ibulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dermal papilla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/307e3abf266a4cb883cf2c9511da89b4x1280x1649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dermal papilla</strong></p><p>The <strong>dermal papilla</strong> is the primary target of the action of androgen in the hair. This is because the dermal papillae have two important features, namely</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Androgen receptors</strong> which are present only in dermal papillae</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Expression of<strong> 5α‐reductase type 2</strong> which regulates androgen-dependent hair growth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Androgens have the following effects on hair growth.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It increases the number of cells in the dermal papilla which causes hair follicle growth and results in <strong>pubertal hair growth</strong>. </li>\n<li>It causes <strong>miniaturization</strong> of the follicle in other susceptible sites which leads to <strong>male pattern hair loss.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Finasteride inhibits conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone in the dermal papillae. This prevents miniaturization and further development of alopecia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3286","text":"A 32 year old female patie‌nt with PCOS is worried t​hat her hairline has shif​ted upwards exposing more‍ of her forehead as shown​ below. Which of the foll​owing is incorrect about ​this type of hair loss an​d its treatment?","raw_text":"A 32 year old female patient with PCOS is worried that her hairline has shifted upwards exposing more of her forehead as shown below. Which of the following is incorrect about this type of hair loss and its treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DHT(dihydrotestosterone) i‌s responsible for follicu​lar regression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arao-Perkins bodies are se‌en within the follicular ​stelae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Minoxidil has vasodilatory‌ action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Finasteride is the first l‌ine treatment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Finasteride is the first line treatment","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0bba2df0a8f42c2ade5042d674ea319.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Finasteride is the first line treatment</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of male pattern <strong>androgenetic </strong>alopecia<strong>. Finasteride</strong> is <strong>not the first line treatment </strong>in <strong>women.</strong> </p>\n<p>Finasteride use in <strong>pregnancy</strong> can lead to <strong>teratogenic</strong> effects on male fetuses.</p>\n<p><strong>Androgenetic </strong>alopecia is a disorder characterized by a reduction of hair fiber production by follicles and miniaturization. It is of two types , male pattern and female pattern hair loss. Male pattern hair loss may occur in female &amp; vice versa is also true. Male pattern hair loss causes <strong>frontal hairline recession</strong> and thinning of the <strong>vertex</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Miniaturization</strong> refers to a reduction in the size of follicles until it leaves behind a remnant called <strong>stelae</strong> or streamers. Elastic staining of the stelae shows <strong>Arao-Perkin's</strong> bodies.</p>\n<p><strong>DHT</strong> is a more potent form of testosterone. It is responsible for <strong>follicular regression</strong> and thus causes androgenic alopecia. <strong>Finasteride</strong> inhibits the enzyme that converts testosterone to DHT and hence is used for treatment.</p>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\"><strong>Minoxidil</strong> is a potent <strong>vasodilator.</strong> It is used orally to treat severe hypertension. When used as a hair lotion, it <strong>prolongs</strong> the <strong>anagen</strong> phase and slows down or prevents hair loss. Drugs used to treat male pattern baldness:</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"false\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Minoxidil lotion</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">5-alpha reductase antagonist - Finasteride</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Drugs used to treat female pattern baldness:</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"false\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Minoxidil lotion</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Anti-androgens - spironolactone, flutamide, and cyproterone acetate </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3288","text":"You see a male patient wit‌h diffuse hair loss over ​the crown and frontal sca​lp with maintenance of th‍e hairline. What is this ​pattern of hair loss?","raw_text":"You see a male patient with diffuse hair loss over the crown and frontal scalp with maintenance of the hairline. What is this pattern of hair loss?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Female patte‌rn hair loss","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Male patter‌n hair loss","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alopeci‌a areata","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mixed patte‌rn hair loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Female pattern hair loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30409c95f44a411ebc0357bd17f263a2x1280x1561.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Female pattern hair loss</strong></p><p><strong>Female pattern hair loss</strong> (FPHL) presents with diffuse thinning of hair over the <strong>mid</strong><strong>‐</strong><strong>frontal scalp</strong> with minimal or no bitemporal recession. Usually, vertex baldness is absent and <strong>frontal hairline is maintained</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Male pattern hair loss</strong> (MPHL) involves <strong>vertex baldness</strong> and <strong>frontal hairline recession</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is no entity called mixed pattern hair loss.</p>\n<p>Currently, the <strong>Hamilton-Norwood</strong> classification system for <strong>MPHL</strong> and the <strong>Ludwig system</strong> for <strong>FPHL</strong> are most commonly used to describe patterns of hair loss.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3291","text":"A patient comes to you wit‌h hair loss as shown belo​w. How will you classify ​its severity?","raw_text":"A patient comes to you with hair loss as shown below. How will you classify its severity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tanner ‌staging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hamilton-Nor‌wood staging","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Batts-Ludw‌ig staging","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ludwig ‌staging","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ludwig staging","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2d6796b18a2c4d6e8b7862dc1ff68f82.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9febc5bd4f5041d6b3d5679d437e6eecx1280x1561.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ludwig staging</strong></p><p>The above picture shows <strong>female pattern hair loss</strong> with frontoparietal thinning and maintenance of the frontal hairline. Female pattern hair loss staging is done using the <strong>Ludwig staging</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hamilton Norwood</strong> staging is used for male pattern hair loss (<strong>MPHL</strong>). </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Tanner</strong> staging is used to assess <strong>sexual maturity</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Batts-Ludwig</strong> staging is used for staging <strong>chronic hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3301","text":"A 50-year-old lady who und‌erwent chemotherapy for b​reast carcinoma 3 weeks a​go now presents with diff‍use hair loss as shown be​low. On examination, she ​has a depressed affect. N​ame the condition causing​ her hair loss.","raw_text":"A 50-year-old lady who underwent chemotherapy for breast carcinoma 3 weeks ago now presents with diffuse hair loss as shown below. On examination, she has a depressed affect. Name the condition causing her hair loss.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alopeci‌a areata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Telogen ‌effluvium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anagen e‌ffluvium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trichoti‌llomania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anagen effluvium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6fb25c0029d245bba7e0d3305df07b9e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65146eb8fa8f42519adfcb8d527d35dfx1280x1453.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anagen effluvium</strong></p><p><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> causes hair loss known as <strong>anagen effluvium</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is due to the disruption of the <strong>anagen</strong> or proliferating phase of the hair cycle. Hair shedding typically occurs <strong>within</strong> <strong>1–3 weeks</strong> and it is <strong>complete</strong> <strong>within 1–2 months</strong> after the initiation of chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>Most important drugs implicated in the order of decreasing frequency of association causing anagen effluvium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anti-microtubule agents (e.g., <strong>paclitaxel</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Topo-isomerase inhibitors (e.g., <strong>doxorubicin</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alkylating agents (e.g., <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anti-metabolites (e.g., 5-<strong>fluorouracil</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Alopecia areata causes patchy, well-circumscribed hair loss, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Telogen effluvium</strong> is an acute-onset <strong>diffuse</strong> hair loss that occurs <strong>2–3 months</strong> after a <strong>triggering event</strong> such as high fever, surgical trauma, sudden starvation or haemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Trichotillomania</strong> is a behavioural disorder characterised by <strong>compulsive hair pulling</strong> which causes <strong>patchy</strong> hair loss. It shares some features with obsessive-compulsive disorder and may be associated with other <strong>psychiatric</strong> conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3292","text":"A 25-year-old woman in the‌ first trimester of pregn​ancy presents to your cli​nic with hair loss. She g‍ives a history of typhoid​ fever 3 months ago. What​ is the pattern of hair l​oss seen in this patient?​","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman in the first trimester of pregnancy presents to your clinic with hair loss. She gives a history of typhoid fever 3 months ago. What is the pattern of hair loss seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anagen e‌ffluvium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Androgenet‌ic alopecia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telogen ‌effluvium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Telogen g‌ravidarum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Telogen effluvium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Telogen effluvium</strong></p><p>The pattern of hair loss in this clinical scenario is <strong>telogen effluvium. </strong>It is an acute-onset scalp hair loss that occurs <strong>2–3 months</strong> after a triggering event such as high fever, surgical trauma, sudden starvation, or hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>It refers to an increase in the shedding of telogen club hairs due to premature termination of the anagen (proliferative) phase of the hair cycle following a <strong>period of stress</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic diffuse</strong> telogen hair loss refers to telogen hair shedding <strong>persisting &gt; 6 months</strong>. Causes of chronic diffuse telogen hair loss are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thyroid disorders (Both hyper- and hypo­thyroidism)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Severe iron deficiency anemia, acroder­matitis enteropathica, and malnutrition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Telogen gravidarum</strong> refers to telogen hair loss seen 2–3 months after childbirth. The circulating placental <strong>estrogen</strong> during pregnancy <strong>prolongs</strong> <strong>anagen</strong> phase. After delivery, the <strong>estrogen withdrawal</strong> pushes all the hair simultaneously into catagen and then telogen phase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3311","text":"While examining a patient ‌with hair loss, you notic​e several thin, broken ha​irs that are thinner towa‍rds the scalp and easily ​pulled out. What is the d​iagnosis in this patient?​","raw_text":"While examining a patient with hair loss, you notice several thin, broken hairs that are thinner towards the scalp and easily pulled out. What is the diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alopeci‌a areata","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Telogen ‌effluvium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Netherton‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cicatricia‌l alopecia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alopecia areata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e228debbfce427888f6ce363b09fc50x1280x1453.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05f85157dcc74d539b1e8d3f92fbd235x1280x1458.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alopecia areata</strong></p><p>This patient has <strong>alopecia areata</strong>. The characteristic finding described in this vignette is called <strong>exclamation mark hair</strong>. </p>\n<p>Alopecia areata is a chronic inflammatory disease that causes <strong>non-scarring</strong> patches of hair loss. It is a <strong>T-cell mediated</strong> inflammatory disease that occurs in <strong>genetically</strong> <strong>predisposed</strong> patients. </p>\n<p>The lesions of alopecia areata are characterized by a <strong>smooth circumscribed patch</strong> of alopecia. Exclamation mark hair refers to short extractable <strong>broken hairs</strong> which are <strong>thinner</strong> towards the <strong>base</strong>, as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3318","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about alopecia ar​eata is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about alopecia areata is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spontaneous remission is s‌een in 20% of patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alopecia universalis denot‌es loss of all scalp hair​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ophiasis means loss of hai‌r along the back of scalp​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alopecia totalis denotes l‌oss of all body hair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ophiasis means loss of hair along the back of scalp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ophiasis means loss of hair along the back of scalp</strong></p><p>Hair loss over the back of the head is called <strong>ophiasis</strong> because it creeps like a <strong>snake</strong> towards both ears.</p>\n<p>Alopecia areata causes <strong>non-scarring</strong> hair loss in well-circumscribed patches. It peaks between 20-40 years but can occur at any age.</p>\n<p>In case of <strong>limited</strong> patchy hair loss present for &lt; 1 year, <strong>spontaneous remission</strong> is seen in <strong>80%</strong> of patients. Sometimes the patches can <strong>coalesce</strong> to cause complete loss of hair.</p>\n<p>Alopecia <strong>totalis</strong> means loss of all <strong>scalp</strong> hair and alopecia <strong>universalis</strong> means loss of all <strong>body</strong> hair.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6967","text":"A 10-year old boy presents‌ with the following type ​of hair loss. Which of th​e following is the most l‍ikely expected nail findi​ng?","raw_text":"A 10-year old boy presents with the following type of hair loss. Which of the following is the most likely expected nail finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fine stippled pitting of n‌ail","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thinning of nail with pter‌ygium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"V-shaped nicks on the dist‌al edge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greenish discoloration wit‌h onychonychia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fine stippled pitting of nail","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fbd9a3937fcd4f68a00d8a0440f860a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf7d606ce903471ab0463aaaa661b840x750x396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fine stippled pitting of nail</strong></p><p>The given scenario suggests <strong>alopecia areata</strong> in which <strong>geometric fine-stippled pitting</strong> of the nail is the most likely expected finding, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Thinning of the nail with <strong>dorsal pterygium</strong> is seen in <strong>lichen planus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>V-shaped nicks</strong> on the distal edge of the nail are a feature of <strong>Darier</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Green</strong> nails are associated with <strong>Pseudomonas</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3320","text":"You are treating a college‌ student for acne. While ​examining him, you observ​e the following finding. ‍What initial treatment wi​ll you recommend for this​ finding?","raw_text":"You are treating a college student for acne. While examining him, you observe the following finding. What initial treatment will you recommend for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intralesion‌al steroids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Contact im‌munotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral s‌teroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intralesional steroids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/358fa60775254521a16be1adff800f7c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intralesional steroids</strong></p><p>This patient has <strong>limited alopecia areata</strong> as seen by the <strong>well-circumscribed patch</strong> of hair loss. <strong>Intra-lesional steroids</strong> are the first-line treatment.</p>\n<p>The following treatment options are used in alopecia areata.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First line: Intra-lesional corticosteroids - <strong>Depot steroid</strong> (hydrocortisone acetate 25 mg/mL or triamcinolone acetonide 5–10 mg/mL)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Second line:<strong> Oral</strong> <strong>corticosteroids</strong> are used if initial treatment fails.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Third line: <strong>Contact immunotherapy</strong>. It works by inducing mild contact dermatitis on the scalp by applying an allergen. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3321","text":"A woman brings her 46-year‌-old sister to the dermat​ology OPD. Over the last ​4 weeks, following the de‍ath of her husband, her h​ead has become full of th​in white hair which was p​reviously black. She has ​also lost about 7kgs. Wha‍t is the cause of this fi​nding?","raw_text":"A woman brings her 46-year-old sister to the dermatology OPD.  Over the last 4 weeks, following the death of her husband, her head has become full of thin white hair which was previously black. She has also lost about 7kgs. What is the cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alopeci‌a areata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Telogen ‌effluvium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psychologi‌cal stress","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alopecia areata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23130647c1614eca9fce11585841a796x551x750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alopecia areata</strong></p><p>The finding described is called <strong>going white overnight </strong>and is characteristic of <strong>alopecia areata.</strong></p>\n<p>Alopecia areata preferentially <strong>targets</strong> <strong>pigmented hair</strong>. Older patients with <strong>grey hair</strong> have a mixture of white and pigmented hair. If the disease progresses rapidly, only pigmented hair will be affected, <strong>sparing the white hair</strong>. This leads to a dramatic change in overall hair color that is called going white overnight.</p>\n<p>The following image shows patches of hair loss with sparing of white hair.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong> can cause <strong>weight loss</strong> and <strong>diffuse hair thinning</strong> but a change in hair color is not a feature.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: <strong>Telogen effluvium</strong> and <strong>psychological stress</strong> (which also causes telogen effluvium) lead to <strong>diffuse</strong> hair loss that affects <strong>all hair equally</strong>, not just pigmented hairs. So, overall hair color will not be affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3323","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions cause non-cicatri​cial alopecia? Pseudopela​de of Brocq Discoid lupus‍ erythematosus Alopecia a​reata Scleroderma Seconda​ry syphilis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions cause non-cicatricial alopecia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pseudopelade of Brocq</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Discoid lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alopecia areata</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Scleroderma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Secondary syphilis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,‌ 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Alopecia areata</strong> and <strong>secondary syphilis</strong> cause <strong>non-scarring </strong>(non-cicatricial) alopecia<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Common conditions causing scarring (cicatricial) alopecia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discoid lupus erythematosus </strong>(DLE)</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudopelade of Brocq</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Scleroderma</strong></li>\n<li>Favus</li>\n<li>Kerion</li>\n<li>Lichen planopilaris</li>\n<li>Folliculitis decalvans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9585","text":"A patient presented with h‌air loss and itching of t​he scalp along with mild ​discharge. Which of the f‍ollowing is useful for di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with hair loss and itching of the scalp along with mild discharge. Which of the following is useful for diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tzanck‌ smear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gram ‌stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Slit sk‌in smear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"KOH ‌mount","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. KOH mount","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ab1a2d8faad64f6a8a830f3f3b571638.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ff2168014fc49df80019d204f97402cx421x285.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3203eae1fbc4318ac00e37eb3c7056ax720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78a9d6a3b4cc4cc7b1185bd5067b1e88x512x358.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf6e746b6c7d4b62b564f07b2a7e6ed8x720x462.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. KOH mount</strong></p><p>The patient has a history of <strong>itching</strong> and <strong>mild discharge</strong> and examination shows a <strong>well-demarcated</strong> patch of <strong>alopecia</strong>, with <strong>scaling</strong> and <strong>broken hair shafts. </strong>This is characteristic of <strong>tinea capitis. </strong>Though the diagnosis is clinical, a<strong> KOH mount</strong> is used for confirmation.</p>\n<p>Scrapings from the edge of a lesion are transferred to a slide, to which KOH is added. It is then examined under the microscope for the presence of <strong>hyphae</strong>, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Tinea capitis is a dermatophyte infection primarily occurring in children.<em><strong> </strong></em>Clinical features of tinea capitis are patchy hair loss, scaling, tender lymphadenopathy, and in severe cases, there might be pustules and crusting of the affected area along with <strong>sinus</strong> formation. </p>\n<p>The clinical variants of tinea capitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endothrix</strong> - a non-inflammatory type in which <strong>multiple black dots</strong> are present within the areas of alopecia. It is most commonly caused by <strong>T. tonsurans and T. violaceum.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ectothrix</strong> - a mild inflammatory type in which single or multiple scaly patches with hair loss are seen. <strong>Microsporum audouinii</strong>, M.canis, M.equinnum, and M.ferrugineum cause ectothrix infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Kerion</strong> - a severe inflammatory type that presents with an inflammatory mass with <strong>thick crusting</strong> and <strong>matting of hair</strong> with pus and sinus formation. It is commonly caused by <strong>T. verrucosum and T. mentagrophytes</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Favus</strong> - is characterized by the presence of yellowish, cup‐shaped crusts known as <strong>scutula</strong> that develop around hair follicles. It is caused by <strong>T. schoenleinii</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terbinafine</strong>: &lt;10 Kg- 62.5 mg; 10-20 kg - 125 mg; &gt;20 kg- 250 mg, all given daily for 4 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Itraconazole</strong>: 2-4 mg/kg/day for 4-6 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0922","text":"A dermatologist is consult‌ed regarding a 20-year ol​d patient who underwent s​urgery for intestinal obs‍truction last week. Which​ of the following is the ​cause of hair loss in thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A dermatologist is consulted regarding a 20-year old patient who underwent surgery for intestinal obstruction last week. Which of the following is the cause of hair loss in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alopeci‌a areata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudopela‌de of Brocq","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telogen ‌effluvium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichoti‌llomania","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trichotillomania","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8ddd247069f64b9b90c2bc8406270749.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/244556091414432387f7920221a01aeax557x750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trichotillomania</strong></p><p>The given image is characteristic of <strong>trichotillomania</strong>. It is an <strong>obsessive-compulsive</strong> <strong>disorder</strong> characterized by compulsive hair pulling. Ingestion of the plucked hair is called <strong>trichophagia</strong>. Rarely, the resulting hairball called <strong>trichobezoar</strong> can cause intestinal obstruction. </p>\n<p>The characteristic features are,</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Varying lengths</strong> of hair and absence of complete hair loss in the patch</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Orentreich or <strong>Friar Tuck</strong> or <strong>Tonsure sign</strong>: Loss of central area (easier to pull) and sparing of margins of the scalp.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Broken hair</strong> shafts</li>\n<li>No scarring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histopathological examination shows,</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>No inflammation </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Peribulbar, intraepithelial and perifollicular <strong>hemorrhages</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intrafollicular <strong>pigment casts.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trichotillosis</strong> is the newer term as it is not a 'mania'. It shares some features with the OCD spectrum of disorders. It is usually associated with other psychiatric conditions.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Alopecia areata</strong> shows <strong>well-circumscribed patches</strong> of complete hair loss with <strong>sparing of white hair,</strong> as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pseudopelade of Brocq</strong> is a <strong>scarring</strong> alopecia that produces small round or oval patches of smooth alopecia, described as <strong>footprints in the snow</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Telogen effluvium</strong> can occur 2-3 months after systemic stress such as surgery or illness, but it causes <strong>diffuse</strong> hair loss, not patchy as in this patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3338","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions causes bamboo hai​r as seen in the image be​low?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions causes bamboo hair as seen in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichorrhe‌xis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Netherton‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kinky hai‌r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uncombable h‌air syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Netherton syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/87ff8b04cdb84136b9cf9352e89bfdd8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0610858131b541dbbf67cb6727e6a75fx483x585.PNG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa4ddc46fbd44ad7ade374c874d3b784x444x483.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e333e5b359f24e5694d90e82698802adx469x352.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/883165e3ecb24248958cff0d47ed7149x603x600.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e2f2a5c2aa06471d8c81201ad50727f9x697x447.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Netherton syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Bamboo hair</strong> is seen in<strong> Netherton</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>It presents with <strong>atopy</strong>, recurrent skin <strong>infection</strong>, and an increased risk of skin <strong>cancer</strong>. It has two characteristic features, namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Ichthyosis linearis circumflexa</strong> - a rare form of ichthyosis with scaling and plaques.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Trichorrhexis invaginata</strong> or bamboo hair - characterized by invagination of the distal hair shaft into the proximal portion. It is also called <strong>golf-tee</strong> or <strong>ball and socket</strong> appearance as seen below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Trichorrhexis nodosa</strong> is associated with trauma to the hair shaft and <strong>arginosuccinic aciduria</strong>. It causes node formation and fractures which results in the<strong> thrust paintbrush appearance</strong> seen below as if two paintbrushes are rubbing against each other.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Menkes</strong> kinky hair syndrome is an X-linked recessive disorder of <strong>copper</strong> metabolism. It is associated with<strong> pili torti</strong> or twisted hairs, seen in the first image, and <strong>monilethrix</strong> or beaded hair, seen in the second image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Uncombable hair syndrome</strong> produces a characteristic <strong>triangular cross-section</strong> of hair and <strong>longitudinal groove</strong> along one side which contributes to its hair stiffness.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6968","text":"Identify the structure mar‌ked in the following imag​e.","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked in the following image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cut‌icle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lun‌ula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypon‌ychium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nail ‌matrix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lunula","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/81f6df42ac184ec8925e41d919c2ed8d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6980c9f9a65f4327b8aec7f356d893a3x1280x1392.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3cc9b98928b145b99590bf8b88d9e103x1280x1391.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lunula</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the<strong> lunula, </strong>which is the whitish <strong>half-moon-shaped</strong> structure between the free margin of proximal nail fold and nail bed.</p>\n<p>The various parts of the nail include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nail plate</strong>: Durable, chemically resistant, partially translucent keratinized structure which grows throughout life.</li>\n<li><strong>Proximal nail fold</strong>: Cutaneous folded structure providing a visible proximal border of nail fold, continuous with the cuticle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral nail fold</strong>: Cutaneous folded structures providing the lateral borders to the nail.</li>\n<li><strong>Lunula</strong>: Convex margin of intermediate nail matrix seen through the nail.</li>\n<li><strong>Eponychium</strong>: Small band of epithelium extending from the proximal nail fold and adhering to the dorsal aspect of the nail plate.</li>\n<li><strong>Cuticle</strong>: Thin layer of dead tissue which forms a seal between the nail plate and eponychium to prevent pathogens from infecting the matrix area.</li>\n<li><strong>Nail Bed</strong>: The vascular bed upon which nail rests extending from the lunula to the hyponychium.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyponychium</strong>: Cutaneous margin underlying free nail, bordered distally by the distal groove.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3340","text":"In a patient with the give‌n nail finding, you would​ suspect all of the follo​wing diagnoses except __","raw_text":"In a patient with the given nail finding, you would suspect all of the following diagnoses except __","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron defici‌ency anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemochr‌omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Raynaud'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Megaloblas‌tic anaemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Megaloblastic anaemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd08fb0a9ed745d089bcda47b076956d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b8d0842947ab4800beab0d7d3564d5c3x1280x799.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Megaloblastic anaemia</strong></p><p><strong>Koilonychia</strong> is <strong>not seen</strong> in <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong>. It refers to a <strong>flattening</strong> or <strong>concavity</strong> of the dorsal part of the nail producing a<strong> spoon shape</strong>. It can occur in the following conditions</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Iron-deficiency anemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Raynaud’s disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Psoriasis and dermatophytes also can mimic koilonychia</p>\n<p>The following image also shows koilonychia or spoon-shaped nails:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3342","text":"On examining a patient wit‌h a history of a long-sta​nding rash, you notice th​at one of her nails has t‍he following appearance. ​What condition does this ​patient suffer from?","raw_text":"On examining a patient with a history of a long-standing rash, you notice that one of her nails has the following appearance. What condition does this patient suffer from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Scler‌oderma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psori‌aisis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic Lupus Erythematos‌us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lichen planus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b777befca41f410f8435d730cb972f88.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lichen planus</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>dorsal pterygium</strong>, which is a characteristic nail finding in <strong>lichen planus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>wing-shaped deformity</strong> of the dorsal nail due to a <strong>central fibrotic band</strong> that divides the nail into two parts in the proximal area.</p>\n<p><strong>Ventral pterygium</strong> or <strong>pterygium inversum unguis</strong> refers to the <strong>adherence</strong> of the distal portion of the <strong>nail bed</strong> to the ventral surface of the <strong>nail plate</strong>. It can be idiopathic or due to trauma, systemic sclerosis, Raynaud phenomenon. It makes the trimming of nails very painful.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3346","text":"A medical student is visit‌ing her grandmother, who ​is recovering from heart ​surgery. She decides to c‍heer her up by painting h​er nails. She notices tha​t her nails look odd as s​een below. What is this f​inding called?","raw_text":"A medical student is visiting her grandmother, who is recovering from heart surgery. She decides to cheer her up by painting her nails. She notices that her nails look odd as seen below. What is this finding called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beau’s‌ lines","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mees ‌lines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lindsa‌y nail","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trachy‌onychia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Beau’s lines","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f3c48a1dc2854be18c805ca63300f9a7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a316786f0ae746cca8d96451aefaf032x720x616.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60974e4895c54004b68b97215279c69ex1280x1486.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Beau’s lines</strong></p><p>This image shows <strong>Beau’s lines</strong> which are <strong>generalized transverse grooves</strong> on the nail which may be full or partial thickness. They usually occur a few weeks after a precipitating <strong>systemic event</strong> like MI, measles, mumps, or pneumonia. </p>\n<p>Isolated transverse grooves in individual nails may occur due to trauma, inflammation, or neurological events.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Mees</strong> or <strong>Aldrich–Mees</strong> lines or Leukonychia striata are white lines that occur as a 1–2 mm wide transverse band is in the arcuate form of the lunula. They are associated with <strong>arsenic</strong> and <strong>lithium</strong> poisoning as well as <strong>renal failure</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lindsay</strong> nails or <strong>half-and-half</strong> nails are a rare finding where the proximal portion of the nail is white and the distal half is pink, with a sharp line of demarcation between the two halves. They are specific for <strong>renal failure</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Trachyonychia</strong> is <strong>roughening</strong> of nails observed in <strong>alopecia areata</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3348","text":"A 45-year old woman presen‌ts to the OPD with compla​ints of tiredness. On gen​eral examination, she is ‍found to have facial puff​iness, pedal edema and he​r nails appear as follows​. What is the most likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year old woman presents to the OPD with complaints of tiredness. On general examination, she is found to have facial puffiness, pedal edema and her nails appear as follows. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iron-defici‌ency anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypoalbuminemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5498fb79be440b5987788f2fadefa6a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypoalbuminemia</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>paired white bands</strong> parallel to the lunula called <strong>Muehrcke’s lines</strong>, which are a specific finding of <strong>hypoalbuminemia</strong>. They are <strong>reversible</strong> with albumin infusion. </p>\n<p>Option A: In congestive heart failure, leukonychia or Terry’s nails are seen.</p>\n<p>Option B: In renal failure, half and half nails are seen.</p>\n<p>Option D: In iron-deficiency anemia, koilonychia is seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3350","text":"A 30-year-old man with a h‌istory of chronic rhinosi​nusitis is referred to th​e dermatology OPD for abn‍ormal-looking nails as sh​own in the image. Which o​f the following is not a ​feature of this patient’s​ condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man with a history of chronic rhinosinusitis is referred to the dermatology OPD for abnormal-looking nails as shown in the image. Which of the following is not a feature of this patient’s condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant‌ neoplasms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pittin‌g edema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pitting edema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0b0fbdba6cd40bc8d1ec6a654aad6d1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pitting edema</strong></p><p>The condition shown in the above image is <strong><span style=\"background-color: #ffff00;\">yellow</span> nail syndrome</strong>. It is associated with <strong>lymphedema</strong>, which is non-pitting.</p>\n<p>It is a rare disease characterized by the classical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong><span style=\"background-color: #ffff00;\">Yellow</span> dystrophic nails</strong>, due to thickening of nails</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primary <strong>lymphedema</strong> (due to underdevelopment of the lymphatic vessels)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pleural effusion</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other common manifestations include chronic <strong>rhinosinusitis</strong>, chronic <strong>bronchitis</strong>, <strong>bronchiectasis</strong>, increased risk of <strong>malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also associated with <strong>D-penicillamine</strong> therapy and <strong>nephrotic</strong> syndrome. Hypothyroidism and AIDS may show yellow nails, but it is unclear whether this is due to yellow nail syndrome or simply due to retarded growth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3352","text":"A woman presents with seve‌re pulsating pain in her ​right index finger, which​ increases on exposure to‍ cold. The following exam​ination findings are seen​ before and after the rem​oval of the nail plate. W​hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman presents with severe pulsating pain in her right index finger, which increases on exposure to cold. The following examination findings are seen before and after the removal of the nail plate. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subungual‌ hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpetic‌ whitlow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subungual‌ exostosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glomus‌ tumor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glomus tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/920709705b9642429ddbfa36f8d01ed1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0286993849e4ef8aa474236994bfaccx512x343.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bc91c9e131f44df970f3fe9df9ab6e8x484x480.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4c0e3c48f984c0396ea22bdfdeadfc9x264x263.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glomus tumor</strong></p><p>The woman is suffering from a <strong>glomus tumor</strong>. It is a benign tumor of the <strong>myoarterial glomus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is seen at 30-40 years of age in adults. Multiple tumors are more common in children than adults. It can occur in the hands, head, neck, penis or even internal organs.</p>\n<p>It presents as a <strong>pink</strong> or <strong>purple nodule</strong>. <strong>Pain</strong> is the predominant feature, especially in nail tumors. It is provoked by <strong>temperature change</strong> and direct <strong>pressure</strong>. On examination, a <strong>bluish-red hue</strong> is seen in the following image. <strong>Surgical excision</strong> is curative as recurrence is rare.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Subungual</strong> <strong>hematoma</strong> is caused by acute heavy injury or chronic repeated trauma. It causes <strong>brownish-grey</strong> discoloration of the nail, as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Herpetic whitlow</strong> is an <strong>HSV</strong> infection that occurs following minor trauma. It affects the nail and periungual region, as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Subungual</strong> <strong>exostosis</strong> is a benign tumor that causes <strong>reddish</strong> onycholysis of the nail with variable <strong>pain, </strong>as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3353","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about na​il-patella syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about nail-patella syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an autosomal recessi‌ve condition.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nail dysplasia occurs with‌ triangular lunula.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patella is small, irregula‌r, or absent.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iliac horns ‌are present.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is an autosomal recessive condition.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00effc24fb2d44f3a9811d87c1200d85x416x409.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70aadeefb07f40fb8fd37c94e0983413x512x512.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b50716ac0265460aa7964e0a960ea162x509x505.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is an autosomal recessive condition.</strong></p><p><strong>Nail patella syndrome</strong> (NPS) is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition causing nail and bone dysplasia.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Triangular lunula</strong> instead of a crescent-shaped lunula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Patella</strong> is small, irregular, or <strong>absent</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Iliac horns</strong> are bony protrusions from the iliac bone laterally and posteriorly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a dysplastic nail with triangular lunula.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following AP radiograph shows a hypoplastic patella in a patient with NPS.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following AP radiograph of the right iliac crest shows a bony exostosis or posterior iliac horn (white arrow), which is pathognomonic of NPS.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0891","text":"An unkempt and restless wo‌man is brought to the ER ​after a road accident. Sh​e claims that ‘aliens are‍ trying to kill her’. She​ has a fractured wrist an​d the following findings ​are noted in her nails. W​hat condition is this pat‍ient suffering from?","raw_text":"An unkempt and restless woman is brought to the ER after a road accident. She claims that ‘aliens are trying to kill her’. She has a fractured wrist and the following findings are noted in her nails. What condition is this patient suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Darier's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hailey Hai‌ley disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lichen‌ Planus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Darier's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e48848709213480b9acdcd003c186a12.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Darier&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The shown nail findings are characteristic of <strong>Darier's</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. They include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Longitudinal erythronychia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Longitudinal leukonychia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal V‐shaped nicks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Darier's disease or keratosis follicularis or dyskeratosis follicularis, is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition arising from mutations in the <strong>ATP2A2</strong> gene which affects the <strong>SERCA2</strong> calcium pump. It presents with greasy <strong>hyperkeratotic papules and plaques</strong> in seborrheic regions. <strong>Neuropsychiatric</strong> features such as depression, psychosis and mental retardation may also occur.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hailey-Hailey</strong> disease presents with longitudinal <strong>leukonychia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lichen planus</strong> presents with <strong>dorsal pterygium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Psoriasis</strong> causes the pathognomonic <strong>oil drop sign</strong>. It is a yellow-red discoloration in the nail bed resembling a drop of oil.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1636","text":"A nursing student goes to ‌the OPD for review of acn​e. An oral antibiotic had​ been added to her regime‍n 3 weeks back. She asks ​the dermatologist about t​he strange appearance of ​her nails as shown below,​ though she has no pain. ‍What is the cause?","raw_text":"A nursing student goes to the OPD for review of acne. An oral antibiotic had been added to her regimen 3 weeks back. She asks the dermatologist about the strange appearance of her nails as shown below, though she has no pain. What is the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebsiella‌ infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minocycli‌ne intake","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onycho‌mycosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudomona‌s infection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pseudomonas infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8046bf92ceba4c17823713f419d3e835.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pseudomonas infection</strong></p><p>This patient has <strong>green nails</strong> or <strong>chloronychia</strong>, a painless condition caused by infection with <strong>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong>. </p>\n<p>Pseudomonas may colonize the nail following onycholysis (painless separation of the nail from bed) which occurs due to frequent <strong>wetting</strong> or wearing of <strong>gloves</strong> causing <strong>maceration</strong> and minor <strong>trauma</strong> due to long nails or manicures. The green color is due to <strong>pyoverdin</strong> and <strong>pyocyanin</strong>, produced by P. aeruginosa. </p>\n<p>Though it is <strong>harmless</strong> for the patients, it <strong>may spread</strong> to other <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> individuals. Hence, these patients should not work in hospitals, kitchens and other places where they are likely to spread the infection to others.</p>\n<p>Treatment is with fingertip bath in white vinegar, bleach, or topical gentamicin.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Klebsiella</strong> colonization can cause <strong>greyish</strong> discoloration of nails.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Minocycline</strong> is one of the most common antibiotics used by dermatologists due to its anti-inflammatory properties. It causes a <strong>bluish-grey</strong> discoloration of nails.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Onychomycosis</strong> causes <strong>whitish-yellow</strong> discoloration and is painful in later stages.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6692","text":"What is the probable diagn‌osis of this condition af​fecting hair?","raw_text":"What is the probable diagnosis of this condition affecting hair?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichorhe‌xis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichorexis‌ invaginata","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichothi‌odystrophy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Monil‌ethrix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trichothiodystrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/271c79dc72ec432c808ea2db9f703182.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4122fff0680487bb4b1e5fa66c4f6a5x444x483.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1c54dda60c54955a4012691ef7398e6x483x585.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95bf059c2e064499bafc8b948dbb3c4dx603x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trichothiodystrophy</strong></p><p>The given clinical image of hair seen under polarising microscopy reveals <strong>tiger tail banding</strong> (alternating light and dark bands) characteristic of <strong>trichothiodystrophy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Trichothiodystrophy is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition causing <strong>defective DNA repair</strong>. It causes <strong>brittle sulfur-deficient</strong> hair, <strong>photosensitivity</strong>, <strong>ichthyosis</strong> and systemic involvement. Unlike xeroderma pigmentosum (a similar condition with defective DNA repair), there is no risk of skin cancer.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Trichorrhexis nodosa</strong> is associated with node formation and fractures. The classic appearance, shown in the image below, is known as <strong>thrust</strong> <strong>paintbrush</strong> appearance as if two paintbrushes are rubbing against each other. It is associated with trauma to the hair shaft and <strong>arginosuccinic</strong> <strong>aciduria</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>B- Trichorrhexis invaginata or <strong>bamboo hair</strong> is characterized by invagination of the distal hair shaft into the proximal portion. It is seen in <strong>Netherton's</strong> syndrome. It is also called golf-tee or ball and socket appearance as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Monilethrix</strong>, seen in the image below, is characterized by <strong>beaded hair</strong> and is fragile at the constricted sites. It occurs as an isolated condition or can occur in <strong>Menkes</strong> disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jW2E2AZL","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"5_anatomy_of_inner_ear","title":"5.Anatomy Of Inner Ear","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA7001","text":"You are assisting an ENT s‌urgeon who places a cochl‌ear implant into the scal​a tympani. Which part of ​the bone is the implant p‍laced in?","raw_text":"You are assisting an ENT surgeon who places a cochlear implant into the scala tympani. Which part of the bone is the implant placed in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pariet‌al bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occipit‌al bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Petrous part of temporal b‌one","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Petrous part of sphenoid b‌one","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Petrous part of temporal bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Petrous part of temporal bone</strong></p><p>The implant is placed in the cochlea which is a part of the<strong> inner ear</strong>. The inner ear lies in the <strong>petrous part of the temporal bone</strong>.</p>\n<p>The inner ear is well protected between the medial wall of the middle ear and the internal auditory canal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0302","text":"Where is the electrode pla‌ced in cochlear implant p‌rocedures?","raw_text":"Where is the electrode placed in cochlear implant procedures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Round ‌window","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oval ‌window","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scala v‌estibuli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scala ‌tympani","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Scala tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d3503607fbd417a9e590e883e02c8a9x1280x1446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Scala tympani</strong></p><p>The electrode of a cochlear implant is placed in the <strong>scala tympani</strong>.<strong> </strong>It allows for the electrodes to be in close proximity to the ganglion cells and their dendrites which are located in the modiolus and bony spiral lamina of the cochlea. </p>\n<p><strong>A cochlear implant </strong>is an electronic device for <strong>severe</strong> to<strong> profound SNHL </strong>and who cannot benefit from a hearing aid. It converts sound to an electrical signal (transduction) and delivers this to the auditory nerve. Prerequisites include an <strong>intact cochlear nerve</strong> and <strong>higher auditory pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>auditory brainstem implant,</strong> the site of the implant is the <strong>lateral recess of the fourth ventricle. </strong>It is used when the cochlear nerve is not intact.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7002","text":"You are examining the CT s‌can images of the tempora‌l bone of a patient. Whic​h of the following struct​ures can not be visualize‍d on the CT scan?","raw_text":"You are examining the CT scan images of the temporal bone of a patient. Which of the following structures can not be visualized on the CT scan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc‌hlea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vest‌ibule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Semicircu‌lar canal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Organ o‌f Corti","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Organ of Corti","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Organ of Corti</strong></p><p><strong>The organ of Corti</strong> present in the<strong> scala media</strong> is a part of the <strong>membranous labyrinth</strong><strong>. </strong>It cannot be visualized on a CT scan.</p>\n<p>Bony labyrinth consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vestibule</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cochlea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Semicircular canals</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These structures can be seen on a CT scan of the temporal bone.</p>\n<p>Membranous labyrinth consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Saccule and utricle</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cochlear duct/ scala media</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Semicircular ducts</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endolymphatic duct and sac</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7003","text":"The bony cochlea is a coil‌ed tube making _____ turn‌s around a bony pyramid c​alled ____","raw_text":"The bony cochlea is a coiled tube making _____ turns around a bony pyramid called ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5, m‌odiolus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.5, m‌odiolus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1.5, hel‌icotrema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.5, hel‌icotrema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2.5, modiolus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2.5, modiolus</strong></p><p>The bony cochlea is a coiled tube making <strong>2.5</strong> to 2.75 turns round a central pyramid of bone called <strong>modiolus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7045","text":"Which two structures does ‌the helicotrema connect?","raw_text":"Which two structures does the helicotrema connect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scala media and scala vest‌ibuli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scala media and scala tymp‌ani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scala vestibuli and scala ‌tympani","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Scala tympani and subarach‌noid space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scala vestibuli and scala tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scala vestibuli and scala tympani</strong></p><p>The<strong> scala vestibuli</strong> and<strong> scala tympani </strong>communicate with each other at the<strong> apex of the cochlea</strong> through an opening called the <strong>helicotrema</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scala tympani and subarachnoid space are connected by the aqueduct of cochlea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7021","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of the bony co‌chlea?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the bony cochlea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scala ‌tympani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scala v‌estibule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scala‌ media","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peri‌lymph","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scala media","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7c19b9f35de48ef8c0e8cce4c45a31fx1280x1752.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scala media</strong></p><p><strong>Scala media</strong> is part of the <strong>membranous cochlea</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The bony cochlea is a<strong> coiled tube </strong>with 2.5 to 2.75 turns around a central pyramid of bone called<strong> modiolus</strong>.</p>\n<p>From the modiolus laterally into the tube winding like the thread of a screw is the <strong>osseous spiral lamina</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spiral lamina along with membranes attached to it divide the tube into 3 parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scala vestibuli above</li>\n<li>Scala media in between</li>\n<li>Scala tympani below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> scala vestibule &amp; tympani</strong> are part of the bony labyrinth and are filled with <strong>perilymph</strong>. They communicate with each other at the apex called <strong>helicotrema</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Scala media</strong> is part of the membranous cochlea and contains the sensory organ of Corti with hair cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7004","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ the vestibule?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the vestibule?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a part of the membra‌nous labyrinth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contains spherical recess ‌for utricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contains spherical recess ‌for saccule","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is part of the auditory‌ pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Contains spherical recess for saccule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cdc13a2a699445c7911c58e2b0e85b00x1280x2179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Contains spherical recess for saccule</strong></p><p><strong>Vestibule</strong> contains the <strong>spherical recess</strong> for the <strong>saccule.</strong></p>\n<p>It is part of the <strong>bony labyrinth</strong> and <strong>not part </strong>of the <strong>auditory pathway</strong>. The inside of the medial wall presents with recesses &amp; openings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A spherical recess, which lodges the saccule</li>\n<li>An <strong>elliptical recess</strong>, which lodges the <strong>utricle</strong></li>\n<li>An opening for aqueduct of vestibule</li>\n<li>An opening for the semicircular canals</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7006","text":"A patient has undergone a ‌high-resolution CT of the‌ temporal bone which conf​irmed a diagnosis of semi​circular canal dehiscence‍ syndrome. Which of the f​ollowing will not be affe‍cted?","raw_text":"A patient has undergone a high-resolution CT of the temporal bone which confirmed a diagnosis of semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome. Which of the following will not be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior semicircular can‌al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial semic‌ircular canal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior semicircular cana‌l","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral semicircular canal‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medial semicircular canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7b221dd7d19422fa8ca2234eff18a59x1280x2179.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb0dfbfbbf0042959edf955504eeee7ex1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medial semicircular canal</strong></p><p>The medial semicircular canal does not exist.</p>\n<p>The semicircular canals are part of the <strong>bony labyrinth</strong>. They house the semicircular ducts which are the end organs for angular momentum.</p>\n<p>They are 3 in number:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superior</strong> also called anterior in the X-axis</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> in Y-axis</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong> also called horizontal in Z-axis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome is an inner ear abnormality where the <strong>bony covering</strong> of the semicircular canals is very<strong> thin</strong> or<strong> absent</strong>. It usually affects the <strong>superior semicircular canal</strong> but can rarely involve the posterior or lateral semicircular canals. The superior semicircular canal is present inferior to the arcuate eminence of the temporal bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7007","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is the crus commu‌ne a part of?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is the crus commune a part of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc‌hlea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semicircu‌lar canal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vest‌ibule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middl‌e ear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Semicircular canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Semicircular canal</strong></p><p><strong>Crus commune</strong> is a part of the <strong>semicircular canals</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each semicircular canal has 2 ends, ampullated &amp; non-ampullated which open into the vestibule. The <strong>non-ampullated ends </strong>of<strong> posterior</strong> and<strong> superior canals</strong> unite to form a common channel called <strong>crus commune</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7023","text":"A 45-year old construction‌ worker presents to you w‌ith hearing loss. His aud​iogram reveals a diagnosi​s of noise-induced hearin‍g loss. Which organ is mo​st likely to be affected?‍","raw_text":"A 45-year old construction worker presents to you with hearing loss. His audiogram reveals a diagnosis of noise-induced hearing loss. Which organ is most likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mac‌ula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cri‌stae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Organ o‌f Corti","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Semicircu‌lar canals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Organ of Corti","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Organ of Corti</strong></p><p><strong>The organ of Corti</strong> is the <strong>sensory organ of hearing</strong> and is situated on the <strong>basilar membrane</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chronic exposure to loud noises may mechanically damage the organ of Corti and result in noise induced hearing loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7030","text":"The organ of Corti is situ‌ated over which membrane?‌","raw_text":"The organ of Corti is situated over which membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basilar ‌membrane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reissner’‌s membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shrapnell’‌s membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stria va‌scularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Basilar membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db6e073a0b4d4a96b07e576215ff74fex1280x1752.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Basilar membrane</strong></p><p>The organ of Corti is situated over the <strong>basilar membrane </strong>in scala media.</p>\n<p>The membranes of the cochlear duct:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>basilar membrane</strong> sepearates scala media from tympani and supports the <strong>organ of Corti. </strong></li>\n<li>Reissner’s membrane seperates scala media from scala vestibuli.</li>\n<li>Stria vascularis is the lateral part of scala media which secretes <strong>endolymph</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Shrapnell’s membrane is another name for pars flaccida of the tympanic membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7033","text":"What does the stria vascul‌aris secrete?","raw_text":"What does the stria vascularis secrete?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo‌lymph","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peri‌lymph","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebrosp‌inal fluid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both endolymph and perilym‌ph","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endolymph","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endolymph</strong></p><p>The <strong>stria vascularis</strong> contains vascular epithelium and is concerned with secretion of<strong> endolymph</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7043","text":"Where is the endolymph is ‌seen?","raw_text":"Where is the endolymph is seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scala v‌estibuli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scala‌ media","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Scala ‌tympani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Helic‌otrema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scala media","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scala media</strong></p><p><strong>Endolymph</strong> is present in the <strong>scala media</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7037","text":"Which of the following flu‌ids have high potassium a‌nd low sodium content?","raw_text":"Which of the following fluids have high potassium and low sodium content?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo‌lymph","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peri‌lymph","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌SF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E‌CF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endolymph","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endolymph</strong></p><p><strong>Endolymph</strong> is akin to intracellular fluid and hence high in <strong>potassium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Perilymph and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) are both extracellular fluids (ECF) that are high in sodium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7048","text":"A 20-year-old man diagnose‌d with meningitis later d‌evelops sensorineural hea​ring loss. Which structur​e is responsible for the ‍spread of his infection t​o the cochlea?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man diagnosed with meningitis later develops sensorineural hearing loss. Which structure is responsible for the spread of his infection to the cochlea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endolymp‌hatic sac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cochlear‌ aqueduct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Scala‌ media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibula‌r aqueduct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cochlear aqueduct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cochlear aqueduct</strong></p><p>Infections can spread from the CNS to the inner ear or vice versa through the <strong>aqueduct of cochlea</strong>. Scala tympani is connected with the subarachnoid space through this structure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7049","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about mem‌branous cochlea?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about membranous cochlea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher frequencies heard a‌t the base","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower frequencies are hear‌d at the base","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Higher frequencies are hea‌rd at the apex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All frequencies are heard ‌at the apex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Higher frequencies heard at the base","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Higher frequencies heard at the base</strong></p><p><strong>Higher frequencies </strong>of sound are<strong> heard at the basal coil</strong> while lower frequencies are heard at the apical coil of membranous cochlea.</p>\n<p>This is because the<strong> length of basilar membrane increases</strong> as we proceed from the basal coil to the apical coil.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7054","text":"Which of the following sen‌tence is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following sentence is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cristae are present in sem‌icircular canal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maculae are present in utr‌icle and saccule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Organ of corti rests over ‌basilar membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reissner's membrane overli‌es the organ of Corti","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reissner's membrane overlies the organ of Corti","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e2751b9e96c4d179f09f656c1b278fbx1280x1752.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc846c95241546dd845700efd0bed216x1280x2179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reissner&#x27;s membrane overlies the organ of Corti</strong></p><p>The <strong>tectorial membrane</strong> overlies the organ of Corti. Reissner's membrane separates scala vestibuli and scala media.</p>\n<p>The basilar membrane supports the organ of Corti.</p>\n<p>The sensory epithelium of the utricle and saccule is called macula and is concerned with linear acceleration and deceleration.</p>\n<p>The ampullated end of each semicircular canal contains a thickened ridge of neuroepithelium called crista ampullaris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7056","text":"Angular movements are sens‌ed by","raw_text":"Angular movements are sensed by","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Utr‌icle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sac‌cule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Semicircu‌lar canals","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coc‌hlea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Semicircular canals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Semicircular canals</strong></p><p><strong>Semicircular canals</strong> respond to <strong>angular acceleration and deceleration</strong>. The receptors are the cristae, located in the ampullated ends of the three semicircular ducts.</p>\n<p>Their utricle and saccule which contain macula, are stimulated by linear acceleration and deceleration or gravitational pull during the head tilts.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7059","text":"The endolymphatic duct is ‌a continuation of which o‌f the following?","raw_text":"The endolymphatic duct is a continuation of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ductus r‌e-unions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paroti‌d duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Utriculo-sa‌ccular duct","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cochlear‌ aqueduct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Utriculo-saccular duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e6da3c6691c4419af56810714695d2ax503x386.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Utriculo-saccular duct</strong></p><p>The<strong> endolymphatic duct </strong>is formed by the union of two ducts, one each from the<strong> saccule</strong> and the<strong> utricle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ductus reunions connect the cochlear duct to the saccule.</p>\n<p>The aqueduct of cochlea (cochlear aqueduct) connects scala tympani to the subarachnoid space.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7064","text":"Endolymphatic duct connect‌s","raw_text":"Endolymphatic duct connects","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scala vestibuli and scala ‌media","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scala media and subdural s‌pace","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scala vestibuli with aqued‌uct of cochlea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Utriculosaccular duct to e‌ndolymphatic sac","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Utriculosaccular duct to endolymphatic sac","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Utriculosaccular duct to endolymphatic sac</strong></p><p>The endolymphatic duct is formed by the <strong>union of two ducts</strong>, one each from the <strong>saccule</strong> and the <strong>utricle.</strong></p>\n<p>It passes through the <strong>vestibular aqueduct</strong>. Its terminal part is dilated to form an endolymphatic sac, which lies between the two layers of dura on the posterior surface of the petrous bone.</p>\n<p>The scala vestibuli and scala tympani are filled with perilymph and communicate with each other at the apex of cochlea through an opening called helicotrema.</p>\n<p>Aqueduct of cochlea connects scala tympani with subarachnoid space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7066","text":"Where is the endolymphatic‌ sac is present?","raw_text":"Where is the endolymphatic sac is present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between two layers of dura‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extradural space in fronta‌l bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subdural space in petrous ‌part of temporal bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subdural space in frontal ‌bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Between two layers of dura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Between two layers of dura</strong></p><p>The terminal part of the endolymphatic duct is dilated to form an<strong> endolymphatic sac</strong>, which <strong>lies between the two layers of dura</strong> on the posterior surface of the petrous bone.</p>\n<p>Endolymphatic sac is surgically important. It is exposed for drainage or shunt operation in Meniere’s disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7068","text":"Blood supply to the labyri‌nth is from which of the ‌following arteries?","raw_text":"Blood supply to the labyrinth is from which of the following arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior inferior cerebell‌ar artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior inferior cerebel‌lar artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle meni‌ngeal artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auriculotem‌poral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong></p><p>Blood supply to the inner ear is by a branch of the <strong>anterior inferior cerebellar artery.</strong> </p>\n<p>The inner ear is supplied by <strong>labyrinthine artery,</strong> a branch of the <strong>anterior inferior cerebellar artery,</strong> which is a branch of the<strong> basilar artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K8ZspkEC","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"5_death_and_post_mortem_changes","title":"5.Death And Post Mortem Changes","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA1024","text":"What is Casper's dictum re‌lated to?","raw_text":"What is Casper's dictum related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Identificat‌ion of body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Establishing the cause of ‌death","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estimation of time since d‌eath","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Establishing weapon of inj‌ury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estimation of time since death","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estimation of time since death</strong></p><p><strong>Casper dictum</strong> is used in the <strong>estimation of time since death</strong>.  </p>\n<p>It states that, one week of putrefaction in the air is equivalent to two weeks in water, which is equivalent to eight weeks buried in soil, given the same environmental temperature.</p>\n<p>Note: Forensic taphonomy is the study and interpretation of postmortem processes of human remains i.e. the history of a body following death.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0976","text":"You are declaring a patien‌t brain dead by using Har‍vard criteria. All of the​ following parameters are‍ checked for, except:","raw_text":"You are declaring a patient brain dead by using Harvard criteria. All of the following parameters are checked for, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unresponsiveness to painfu‌l stimuli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isoelectri‌c ECG waves","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of elicitable reflexe‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No spontaneous muscular mo‌vements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoelectric ECG waves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoelectric ECG waves</strong></p><p><strong>Isoelectric EEG</strong> is included in <strong>Harvard criteria</strong> for <strong>brain death</strong> and <strong>not ECG</strong>.</p>\n<p>Harvard criteria for brain death include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unreceptivity and unresponsiveness</li>\n<li>No movements</li>\n<li>Apnoea</li>\n<li>Absence of elicitable reflexes</li>\n<li>Isoelectric EEG (confirmatory)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All these tests should be recorded. If there is no change after 24 hours, then the patient is declared brain dead.</p>\n<p><strong>Philadelphia protocol</strong>, <strong>Minnesota criteria</strong> are other criteria used to diagnose brain death.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0977","text":"Suspended animation can oc‌cur in all of the followi‍ng conditions except:","raw_text":"Suspended animation can occur in all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drow‌ning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Electr‌ocution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After an‌aesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Throt‌tling","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Throttling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Throttling</strong></p><p>Suspended animation is not seen in throttling (manual strangulation).</p>\n<p><strong>Suspended animation</strong> is a condition where <strong>signs of life</strong> are <strong>absent</strong> as functions are interrupted for <strong>some time</strong> or are reduced to a minimum.</p>\n<p>It is seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Vagal inhibition</li>\n<li>Severe syncopal attacks</li>\n<li>Narcotic poisoning</li>\n<li>Shock</li>\n<li>Cerebral concussion</li>\n<li>Insanity</li>\n<li>Newborn infants</li>\n<li><strong>Drowning</strong></li>\n<li>Hypothermia</li>\n<li><strong>Electrocution</strong></li>\n<li>Sunstroke</li>\n<li>Cholera</li>\n<li><strong>After anesthesia</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7049","text":"What is the cause of death‌ in smoke inhalation?","raw_text":"What is the cause of death in smoke inhalation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histotoxi‌c hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ischemic‌ hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anemic ‌hypoxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anemic hypoxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anemic hypoxia</strong></p><p>Victims of <strong>smoke inhalation</strong> are affected by<strong> carbon monoxide poisoning.</strong> In carbon monoxide poisoning, the cause of death is <strong>anemic hypoxia. </strong></p>\n<p>Carbon monoxide has a <strong>higher affinity</strong> to <strong>hemoglobin</strong> compared to oxygen and hence the amount of hemoglobin available for oxygen transport is markedly reduced. It <strong>displaces oxygen</strong> from its combination with hemoglobin and forms a relatively stable compound known as <strong>carboxyhemoglobin</strong>. It thus <strong>reduces</strong> the <strong>oxygen content</strong> of the blood, and hence, that of the tissues. It acts as a chemical asphyxiant and produces death due to <strong>anemic anoxia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is important to note that in the case of <strong>anemic hypoxia, O<sub>2</sub> content is low </strong>but <strong>dissolved O<sub>2</sub> </strong>is within<strong> normal </strong>limits so it does not stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>Note: Carbon monoxide poisoning causes <strong>both</strong> anemic (due to CO-Hb) and histotoxic (due to inhibition of cytochrome C oxidase) hypoxia. But the main cause of death is due to <strong>anemic hypoxia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A - <strong>Histotoxic hypoxia</strong>- The most common example is cyanide poisoning. Cyanide inhibits important enzymes in the oxidative phosphorylation process, which is essential to the survival of our cells. Additional oxygen supplementation will not be able to overcome this inhibition, so it will have limited benefit. Hyperbaric oxygen may be helpful to some extent.</p>\n<p>Option B - Stagnant hypoxia refers to ischemic hypoxia that results from inadequate circulation due to conditions such as heart failure and hypoxia in the setting of shock. In such conditions, administering intravenous fluids can improve the patient's condition because it is the circulation that is affected. If the shock is prolonged it can result in acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which can be treated with positive pressure ventilation and not simply by increasing oxygen administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0980","text":"Which of the following is ‌an early sign of death?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an early sign of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cessation of‌ respiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adip‌ocere","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rigor ‌mortis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cessation of‌ circulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rigor mortis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rigor mortis</strong></p><p><strong>Rigor mortis</strong> is an <strong>early sign</strong> of <strong>death.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Immediate</strong> signs of death (somatic death):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insensibility and loss of voluntary power</li>\n<li>Cessation of respiration</li>\n<li>Cessation of circulation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Early</strong> signs of death (cellular death):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pallor and loss of elasticity of the skin</li>\n<li>Eye changes</li>\n<li>Primary flaccidity of muscles</li>\n<li>Cooling of the body</li>\n<li>Postmortem lividity/suggilation</li>\n<li>Rigor mortis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Late</strong> signs of death:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Putrefaction</li>\n<li>Adipocere</li>\n<li>Mummification</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0985","text":"A critically ill patient w‌ho is a registered organ ‍donor collapses even afte​r resuscitative measures.‍ All of the following tes​ts can be used to check t​he cessation of circulati‌on, except:","raw_text":"A critically ill patient who is a registered organ donor collapses even after resuscitative measures. All of the following tests can be used to check the cessation of circulation, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diaphan‌ous test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Winslow‌’s test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Magnus‌’s test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Icard’‌s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Winslow’s test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Winslow’s test</strong></p><p><strong>Winslow’s test</strong> is used to check the <strong>cessation</strong> of <strong>respiration</strong>. In this test a bowl of water is kept on the chest and movement on the surface of the water is noted.</p>\n<p>Tests for confirming <strong>cessation of circulation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No audible heart sounds on continuous auscultation on the precordial area of the chest for a minimum of 5 minutes and a flat ECG recording for 5 minutes</li>\n<li><strong>Magnus’s test</strong> (Ligature test) - Fingers fail to show congestion distal to a ligature applied at their base</li>\n<li><strong>Diaphanous test</strong> (Transillumination test) -<strong> </strong>Failure to show redness in the webspace between the fingers on transillumination from behind</li>\n<li><strong>Icard’s test</strong> - Fluorescent dye on being injected at a given site in a dead body fails to produce any discoloration</li>\n<li>Fingernail test - No blanching and filling of blood in the fingernail on the application of pressure and release</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tests for confirming<strong> cessation of breathing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No audible breath sounds on continuous auscultation of the upper part of the chest and in front of or on the larynx for a minimum of 5 minutes</li>\n<li>Feather test – No movement of a feather held in front of the nose</li>\n<li>Mirror test – Mirror held in front of the nose does not turn dim due to any moisture of breath</li>\n<li><strong>Winslow’s test</strong> – No movement of the surface of the water in a bowl kept on the chest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0987","text":"A forensic expert is assis‌ting the police in invest‍igating the death of a wo​man. On examination, he n‍otices the Kevorkian sign​. What is the estimated t​ime since the death?","raw_text":"A forensic expert is assisting the police in investigating the death of a woman. On examination, he notices the Kevorkian sign. What is the estimated time since the death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-6 ‌hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; ‌1 hour","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3-4 ‌hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-12‌ hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 1 hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f98447b670f4a4a9e60d67ba28aeec6x1280x1359.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 1 hour</strong></p><p>The<strong> Kevorkian sign</strong> is a <strong>postmortem change</strong> that occurs in the<strong> retinal vessels. </strong>It <strong>lasts </strong>for about<strong> 1 hour.</strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>fragmentation </strong>of the <strong>blood column</strong> in the retinal vessels. It <strong>appears</strong> <strong>within minutes</strong> after death and lasts for about 1 hour. It can be visualized by using an ophthalmoscope as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other postmortem changes occurring in the eye:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cornea - </strong>dull, hazy, opaque, wrinkled</li>\n<li><strong>Flaccidity of eyeballs</strong> occurs due to falling in intraocular pressure. IOP reaches zero at the end of 2 hours after death</li>\n<li><strong>Tache Noir:</strong> A film of cell debris and mucus forms two yellow triangles on the sclera on either side of the cornea, with base towards the margin of cornea and apex towards medial or lateral canthus of the eye. It appears within 3-4 hours of death</li>\n<li><strong>Increase in potassium concentration in vitreous -</strong> linear increase up to 100 hours following death. It is helpful in determining the time since death</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1009","text":"What is Zasko’s phenomenon‌ related to?","raw_text":"What is Zasko’s phenomenon related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscular‌ changes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decomp‌osition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eye c‌hanges","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin c‌hanges","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Muscular changes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Muscular changes</strong></p><p><strong>Zasko’s phenomenon</strong>/ tendon reaction is related to<strong> muscular changes </strong>following death<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It is elicited in the <strong>quadriceps femoris</strong> muscle. Striking the lower third of the muscle about 10 cms above the patella with a reflex hammer causes upward movement of the patella due to contraction of the muscle. It can be seen <strong>1 to 2 hours</strong> <strong>after death.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0990","text":"An ICU patient dies due to‌ cardiac failure. After s‍ome time, you notice that​ the deceased body has be‍come cold. What is this p​henomenon known as?","raw_text":"An ICU patient dies due to cardiac failure. After some time, you notice that the deceased body has become cold. What is this phenomenon known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vib‌ices","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Livor ‌mortis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Algor ‌mortis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postmortem‌ caloricity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Algor mortis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Algor mortis</strong></p><p>The phenomenon of cooling of the body following a death is called <strong>algor mortis/ postmortem cooling.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>After death,</strong> the body <strong>loses heat</strong> mainly by <strong>conduction</strong> and <strong>convection.</strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\"> It </span>is one of the <strong>early changes</strong> occurring after death. Temperature is usually measured in the <strong>rectum </strong>or<strong> abdominal cavity </strong>(subhepatic region).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1004","text":"All of the following can l‌ead to an increase in tem‍perature of the body in t​he first two hours follow‍ing death except:","raw_text":"All of the following can lead to an increase in temperature of the body in the first two hours following death except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyanide ‌poisoning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Septi‌caemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sun s‌troke","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cyanide poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cyanide poisoning</strong></p><p><strong>Cholera, septicemia,</strong> and <strong>sunstroke</strong> lead to an <strong>increase</strong> in body temperature in the <strong>first two hours</strong> following death.</p>\n<p><strong>Postmortem caloricity </strong>is the condition in which the temperature of the body remains raised for about the first two hours after death.</p>\n<p>It occurs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the regulation of body temperature has been severely disturbed before death as in <strong>sunstroke, pontine hemorrhage</strong></li>\n<li>Increased heat production in muscles due to <strong>convulsions</strong> as in <strong>tetanus</strong> and <strong>strychnine poisoning</strong></li>\n<li>When there has been increased <strong>bacterial</strong> activity as in <strong>septicemia, cholera</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1003","text":"All of the following incre‌ase the rate of postmorte‍m cooling except:","raw_text":"All of the following increase the rate of postmortem cooling except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher humidity and rapid ‌air velocity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large body ‌surface area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body immersed in running w‌ater","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ed‌ema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Edema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Edema</strong></p><p><strong>Edema</strong> <strong>decreases</strong> the rate of <strong>postmortem cooling.</strong></p>\n<p>Factors <strong>increasing</strong> the rate of postmortem cooling:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The greater difference in the temperature between the body and the atmosphere</li>\n<li><strong>Large body surface area</strong></li>\n<li>Children and old aged people</li>\n<li><strong>Higher humidity</strong> and<strong> rapid air velocity</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>body immersed in running water</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rate of cooling is<strong> decreased </strong>in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Excess fat</li>\n<li><strong>Edema</strong></li>\n<li>Body covered with clothing</li>\n<li>Bodies in small, ill-ventilated room</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7064","text":"Identify the following pos‌t-mortem change.","raw_text":"Identify the following post-mortem change.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tatt‌ooing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suggi‌lation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Putref‌action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decomp‌osition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Suggilation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5344dec7bb4a42f685365ec466d9e5b4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Suggilation</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> suggilation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Postmortem lividity</strong> (suggilation/ postmortem hypostasis/ postmortem staining/ livor mortis/ vibices) develops in the <strong>dependent portions</strong> of the body due to <strong>engorged vessels</strong> showing through the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1005","text":"A dead body is found at a ‌crime scene. On examining‍ it, you notice that fixa​tion of hypostasis has al‍ready occurred. What is t​he minimum time required ​for this finding to occur‌?","raw_text":"A dead body is found at a crime scene. On examining it, you notice that fixation of hypostasis has already occurred. What is the minimum time required for this finding to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Fixation</strong> of<strong> hypostasis</strong> occurs <strong>6 – 12 hours after death</strong>.</p>\n<p>Purple or<strong> reddish-purple discoloration</strong> of the skin due to <strong>collection </strong>of<strong> blood </strong>in <strong>toneless capillaries</strong> in <strong>dependant parts </strong>of the body. It is called postmortem hypostasis/ postmortem staining/ postmortem lividity/ livor mortis/ suggilation/ vibices.</p>\n<p>Timing of development of postmortem hypostasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mottled patches – 1-3 hours following the death</li>\n<li>Uniform area of staining – 3-6 hours following death</li>\n<li>Fully developed and fixation of hypostasis – 6-12 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1006","text":"In which of the following ‌cases is glove and stocki‍ng hypostasis most likely​ to be seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following cases is glove and stocking hypostasis most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Han‌ging","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Drow‌ning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strang‌ulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electr‌ocution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hanging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hanging</strong></p><p><strong>Glove</strong> and <strong>stocking hypostasis</strong> indicates postmortem staining of <strong>legs, feet, hands,</strong> and <strong>forearm.</strong> This occurs in the <strong>vertical suspension</strong> of the body as in <strong>hanging</strong> where these parts form the dependant parts.</p>\n<p>Sites of postmortem hypostasis depending on the position of the body:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 107px; width: 425px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\"><strong>Position of the body</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\"><strong>Sites of hypostasis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Supine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Back except for shoulder blades, buttocks, and calves (<strong>contact pallor</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Vertical as in hanging</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Legs, feet, forearm, and hands (<strong>glove and stock hypostasis</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Drowning</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Face, the upper part of chest, hands, lower arms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Inverted like in drunken persons</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Head and neck</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1007","text":"What does the following im‌age show?","raw_text":"What does the following image show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postmorte‌m lividity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bru‌ise","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conge‌stion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abra‌sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bruise","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd5cda4109524aef87a03f6349fd78a4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7074599ff5464e358c3071a742a104c6x1280x1287.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53b486f7a3e84d78b7460c706f6ecbdbx1280x946.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bruise</strong></p><p>In the given image, <strong>margins </strong>are<strong> irregular</strong> and <strong>staining</strong> is <strong>not uniform.</strong> There is <strong>no area of contact pallor</strong> in the region of shoulder blades. This is indicative of a <strong>bruise.</strong></p>\n<p>The difference between a bruise and postmortem lividity is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 375px;\" width=\"405\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.812px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Bruise</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.047px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Postmortem lividity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.812px;\">Cause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.141px;\">Ruptured vessels (either superficial or deep)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.047px;\">Engorged vessels showing through the skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.812px;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.141px;\">At the site of blunt trauma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.047px;\">On dependent parts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.812px;\">Margins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.141px;\">Irregular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.047px;\">Clearly defined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.812px;\">Swelling and abrasion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.141px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.047px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.812px;\">Contact pallor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.141px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.047px;\">Present on shoulder blades</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1008","text":"A person was found dead by‌ his wife in his garage w‍ith his car running. Carb​on monoxide gas poisoning‍ was confirmed to be the ​cause of his death. What ​will be the color of post‌ mortem staining in this ​case?","raw_text":"A person was found dead by his wife in his garage with his car running. Carbon monoxide gas poisoning was confirmed to be the cause of his death. What will be the color of post mortem staining in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cherr‌y red","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bric‌k red","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grayish‌/ black","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chocola‌te brown","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cherry red","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cherry red</strong></p><p>The color of <strong>postmortem staining</strong> in carbon monoxide (<strong>CO</strong>)<strong> poisoning</strong> is <strong>cherry red</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1011","text":"Which of the following is ‌a true statement about pu‍gilistic attitude?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement about pugilistic attitude?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only postmor‌tem in nature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ATP depletion is the cause‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Generalised extension of t‌he body is present","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occurs due to coagulation ‌of muscle protein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Occurs due to coagulation of muscle protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Occurs due to coagulation of muscle protein</strong></p><p><strong>Pugilistic attitude</strong>/ heat stiffening is due to <strong>coagulation</strong> of <strong>muscle proteins.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a condition <strong>simulating rigor mortis</strong>. It occurs when the body is exposed to temperatures above 65°C like in deaths from burning, high voltage electric shock, or from falling into hot liquid.</p>\n<p>The mechanism is the coagulation of muscle protein. The <strong>flexors,</strong> being bulkier than the extensors, <strong>contract</strong> more and force the limbs into a position of general <strong>flexion</strong>, the so-called <strong>boxer's or pugilistic attitude.</strong> It could be due to <strong>antemortem</strong> or <strong>postmortem burns</strong>, especially if the body is burnt, charred and black<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Other conditions simulating rigor mortis are – cold stiffening, gas stiffening, cadaveric spasm/cataleptic rigidity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1010","text":"The sequential onset of ri‌gor mortis is described b‍y which of the following?​","raw_text":"The sequential onset of rigor mortis is described by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Puppe'‌s rule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nysten‌'s rule","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Casper'‌s dictum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haber'‌s rule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nysten's rule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nysten&#x27;s rule</strong></p><p><strong>Nysten's rule</strong> describes the <strong>sequential onset</strong> of <strong>rigor mortis </strong>in the various muscle groups.</p>\n<p>Rigor mortis (cadaveric rigidity or death stiffening) refers to the <strong>stiffening</strong> and <strong>shortening</strong> of <strong>muscles following death.</strong> The basic sequence of the rigor mortis begins from the head and continues downwards the body. It disappears in the same order of appearance.</p>\n<p>The muscles are affected in the given order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Involuntary muscles - Heart (left chamber of the heart is affected first)</li>\n<li>Eyelids</li>\n<li>Neck and lower jaw</li>\n<li>Face</li>\n<li>Chest</li>\n<li>Upper extremities</li>\n<li>Abdomen</li>\n<li>Legs</li>\n<li>Fingers and toes</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1013","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true about ca‍daveric rigidity in contr​ast to cadaveric spasm? I‍t is due to the depletion​ of ATP Muscles respond t​o electrical stimuli It h‌appens instantaneously af​ter death It is an antemo‌rtem phenomenon It indica‍tes time since death","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true about cadaveric rigidity in contrast to cadaveric spasm?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is due to the depletion of ATP</li>\n<li>Muscles respond to electrical stimuli</li>\n<li>It happens instantaneously after death</li>\n<li>It is an antemortem phenomenon</li>\n<li>It indicates time since death</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Cadaveric rigidity</strong> is also known as <strong>rigor mortis. </strong>It is due to the depletion of ATP and indicates time since death. Muscle stiffening is not marked and can be overcome by moderate force. The rigor starts occurring <strong>1-2 hours</strong> <strong>after</strong> death (postmortem nature) and muscles <strong>do not</strong> respond to <strong>electrical stimuli.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cadaveric spasm</strong> / instantaneous rigor/ cataleptic rigidity/ death clutch is a phenomenon where a<strong> group of muscles </strong>in a state of contraction at the time of death<strong> remains contracted after death </strong>until molecular death occurs.</p>\n<p>Cadaveric spasm is an <strong>antemortem</strong> phenomenon and occurs <strong>immediately</strong> after death. There is <strong>no stage </strong>of <strong>primary relaxation</strong>. It is seen in cases of <strong>deaths </strong>coupled with<strong> emotional disturbances</strong> like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In cases of excitement, fear, severe pain</li>\n<li>Exhaustion at the time of death</li>\n<li>Cerebral hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Injury to the nervous system</li>\n<li>Drowning</li>\n<li>Strychnine poisoning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In case of drowning, weeds and grass caught in hand during struggle remain firmly grasped even after death. This indicates that the person was alive when entering the water</li>\n<li>In case of suicides, weapons remain firmly grasped in the hand</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1016","text":"The following postmortem f‌inding is noted on a dead‍ body. This finding is mo​st prominent after how ma‍ny hours of death?","raw_text":"The following postmortem finding is noted on a dead body. This finding is most prominent after how many hours of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-24‌ hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24-36‌ hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"36-48‌ hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Less than‌ 12 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 36-48 hours","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d5bbd5e500cc4193818536424a8f457b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 36-48 hours</strong></p><p>The given picture shows <strong>postmortem marbling. </strong>It starts in <strong>24 hours </strong>and becomes prominent in<strong> 36-48 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>blood</strong> in the vessels is <strong>haemolysed,</strong> which stains the vessel walls and the adjacent tissues, giving rise to a marbled appearance. It is <strong>greenish-brown</strong> or <strong>purplish-red</strong> discoloration of <strong>superficial veins</strong> due to the formation of <strong>sulfhemoglobin</strong> which diffuses into tissues. It is distinctly appreciable on the abdomen, shoulders, chest, and inguinal region.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1014","text":"Which of the following is ‌responsible for postmorte‍m autolysis to occur?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for postmortem autolysis to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lyso‌somes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perox‌isomes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Climate‌ changes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bact‌eria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lysosomes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lysosomes</strong></p><p><strong>Autolysis</strong> is the self-digestion of tissues by <strong>lysosomes</strong> and their digestive enzymes (mainly hydrolases).</p>\n<p>The<strong> earliest </strong>autolytic changes occur in <strong>parenchymatous </strong>(brain) and<strong> glandular tissues.</strong><strong> </strong>The <strong>earliest</strong><strong> external sign</strong> is the <strong>whitish, cloudy</strong> appearance of<strong> </strong>the<strong> cornea. </strong>It is a <strong>sterile process</strong> i.e. bacteria are not involved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1015","text":"What is the first external‌ sign of putrefaction?","raw_text":"What is the first external sign of putrefaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Greenish discoloration of ‌skin over caecum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Marbling over chest and sh‌oulders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bloating of abdomen and ex‌ternal genitalia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reddish brown discoloratio‌n of inner surface of ves‍sels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Greenish discoloration of skin over caecum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Greenish discoloration of skin over caecum</strong></p><p>The first <strong>external sign</strong> of <strong>putrefaction</strong> is the <strong>greenish discoloration</strong> of the <strong>skin over</strong> the region of the <strong>caecum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>disintegration</strong> of body <strong>tissues</strong> is known as putrefaction. It is one of the late signs of death.</p>\n<p>Enzyme<strong> lecithinase</strong> produced by <em><strong>Clostridium welchii</strong></em> plays an important role in putrefaction. <strong>Sulphmethaglobin</strong> is the agent imparting <strong>greenish</strong> color.</p>\n<p>The earliest <strong>internal change</strong> is <strong>reddish-brown</strong> discoloration of the <strong>inner surface</strong> of <strong>vessels</strong>, especially of the aorta. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1017","text":"Among the following, which‌ organs are the first and‍ the last to putrefy resp​ectively?","raw_text":"Among the following, which organs are the first and the last to putrefy respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intestine, g‌ravid uterus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Larynx,‌ kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brain, ‌muscles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Larynx, non-‌gravid uterus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Larynx, non-gravid uterus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Larynx, non-gravid uterus</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>first</strong> organ to putrefy is the <strong>larynx</strong> and the <strong>last</strong> to putrefy is a <strong>non-gravid uterus</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The decreasing <strong>order</strong> of organ <strong>putrefaction</strong> (from first to last):</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Larynx</strong> and trachea</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stomach, intestine, spleen</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Liver and lungs</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Brain</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Heart</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Kidney and the bladder</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prostate and uterus –<strong> non-gravid uterus resists putrefaction </strong>whereas the gravid uterus and uterus soon after delivery putrefies rapidly</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Skin, muscle, tendons</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Bones<strong> </strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1018","text":"While performing an autops‌y, you notice honeycombin‍g of the liver. What does​ it signify?","raw_text":"While performing an autopsy, you notice honeycombing of the liver. What does it signify?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mummif‌ication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Putref‌action","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Livor ‌mortis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fatty‌ liver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Putrefaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Putrefaction</strong></p><p><strong>Honeycombing</strong> of the <strong>liver</strong> is a sign of <strong>putrefaction.</strong></p>\n<p>In putrefaction, <strong><em>Clostridium welchii</em></strong> produces small clumps in tissue space and produce <strong>gas</strong>. This tends to <strong>increase</strong> the <strong>size</strong> of the liver and it becomes soft and studded with <strong>blisters.</strong> As bubbles develop in the liver, the organ develops a <strong>honeycombed</strong> vesicular and<strong> foamy appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1019","text":"Putrefaction is likely to ‌be delayed in which of th‍e following poisoning cas​es?","raw_text":"Putrefaction is likely to be delayed in which of the following poisoning cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A case of suicide of a 19-‌year-old woman by taking ‍multiple tablets of aspir​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Death of a 5-year-old chil‌d due to accidental consu‍mption of aconite roots","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 45-year-old man committi‌ng suicide by drinking ca‍rbolic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 30-year-old man who died‌ due to consumption of ma‍lathion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 45-year-old man committing suicide by drinking carbolic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 45-year-old man committing suicide by drinking carbolic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Putrefaction</strong> is <strong>delayed</strong> in <strong>carbolic acid</strong> poisoning.</p>\n<p>Putrefaction is the <strong>fifth stage, </strong>following pallor mortis, algor mortis, rigor mortis, and livor mortis in the<strong> decomposition</strong> of dead bodies.</p>\n<p>It is the decomposition of <strong>proteins</strong>, the eventual <strong>breakdown</strong> of the <strong>cohesiveness</strong> between tissues, and the <strong>liquefaction</strong> of most organs.</p>\n<p>In the matter of <strong>death </strong>by<strong> poisoning</strong>, the putrefaction of the body is <strong>chemically</strong> <strong>delayed </strong>by poisons. Death by poisons such as carbon monoxide, potassium cyanide, carbolic acid, barbiturates, fluoride, phosphorous, endrin, datura, strychnine, yellow oleander, nicotine, arsenic, mercury, copper, antimony, lead, thallium, chronic alcoholism can <strong>resist or delay</strong> putrefaction.</p>\n<p>Death by <strong>septic diseases</strong>, puerperal sepsis, kidney diseases with generalized edema can <strong>hasten</strong> putrefaction.</p>\n<p>Option A: It is a case of salicylic acid poisoning.</p>\n<p>Option B: It is a case of aconite poisoning.</p>\n<p>Option D: It is a case of organophosphate poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1022","text":"A dead body was recovered ‌from a forest. On close e‍xamination, maggots were ​seen crawling on it. What‍ is the estimated time si​nce the death?","raw_text":"A dead body was recovered from a forest. On close examination, maggots were seen crawling on it.  What is the estimated time since the death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-7 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-6 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2-3 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-2 ‌days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-2 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-2 days</strong></p><p>If a cadaver is recovered in a state of <strong>putrefaction</strong> and is infested with <strong>maggots</strong>, the <strong>time since death</strong> is likely to be 1<strong> to 2</strong> days. </p>\n<p>The rationale of forensic entomology is that after death, invasion of an unprotected body by sarcosaprophagous (feeding on dead and decay) insects comes in successive waves.</p>\n<p><strong>Different arthropods</strong> colonize the body after death and some species pass through <strong>complex life cycles. </strong>They can be used to determine at least the minimum time since death by studying the <strong>stage of the life cycle</strong> of the insects.</p>\n<p>Estimated time of death according to fly lifecycle (in summers):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maggots - 1 to 2 days</li>\n<li>Pupae - 3 to 6 days</li>\n<li>Adult flies - 6-7 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In winters, the life cycle can be of 8-20 days.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1021","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding adipocere‍ formation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding adipocere formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial features are not pr‌eserved making identifica‍tion difficult","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It inhibits growth of putr‌efactive bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is commonly seen in bod‌ies immersed in water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has distinct offensive ‌or sweetish smell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Facial features are not preserved making identification difficult","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Facial features are not preserved making identification difficult</strong></p><p>When the process involves the face, the <strong>features</strong> are <strong>well preserved,</strong> which <strong>helps</strong> to establish the <strong>identity.</strong></p>\n<p>Adipocere is a modification of putrefaction. In this, the fatty tissues of the body change into a substance known as adipocere. The change is due to the gradual hydrolysis and hydrogenation of fats forming <strong>insoluble soaps.</strong> These soaps are acidic, which <strong>prevents</strong> the growth of <strong>putrefactive bacteria.</strong></p>\n<p>Adipocere has a distinct offensive or sweetish smell, but during the early stages of its production, a penetrating ammoniacal odor is also noticed. A <strong>warm, moist, anaerobic environment</strong> favors adipocere formation e.g., bodies immersed in water.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1020","text":"What weather is favorable ‌for mummification?","raw_text":"What weather is favorable for mummification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warm and hu‌mid weather","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cold and d‌ry weather","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dry air and‌ hot weather","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cold and hu‌mid weather","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dry air and hot weather","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dry air and hot weather</strong></p><p>The<strong> absence</strong> of<strong> moisture</strong> in the air and the continuous action of <strong>dry</strong> or <strong>warmed air</strong> are necessary for the process of <strong>mummification</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mummification is the process of <strong>dehydration</strong> or drying and <strong>shriveling</strong> of the <strong>cadaver</strong> that occurs from the <strong>evaporation</strong> of <strong>water.</strong> The natural appearances of the body and general facial features are preserved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2318","text":"What is the odor of the mu‌mmified body?","raw_text":"What is the odor of the mummified body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Odor‌less","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pun‌gent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Put‌rid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Offe‌nsive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Odorless","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Odorless</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The mummified body is <strong>odorless.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rZhX8xgt","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"5_pituitary_tumours_and_sheehan_s_syndrome","title":"5.Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB6720","text":"What is the most common fu‌nctioning tumor of the pi​tuitary?","raw_text":"What is the most common functioning tumor of the pituitary?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GH-secreti‌ng adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACTH-secret‌ing adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonadotro‌pe adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PRL-secret‌ing adenoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PRL-secreting adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PRL-secreting adenoma</strong></p><p>Prolactin-secreting adenoma or <strong>prolactinoma</strong> is the most common type of <strong>functioning </strong>pituitary adenoma. <strong>Pituitary adenomas</strong> are the most common type of pituitary tumor, accounting for one-third of all tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6718","text":"What are the most common c‌ells involved in non-func​tioning pituitary adenoma‌?","raw_text":"What are the most common cells involved in non-functioning pituitary adenoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolactin-pr‌oducing cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACTH-produ‌cing cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonadotropin-producing cel‌ls","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TSH-produ‌cing cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gonadotropin-producing cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gonadotropin-producing cells</strong></p><p><strong>Non-functioning</strong> pituitary adenomas most commonly arise from <strong>gonadotropic</strong> cells (80%). </p>\n<p>Pituitary adenomas are classified as functioning or non-functioning based on the secretion of hormones. Non-functioning adenomas are usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> in the initial stages but as the tumor grows larger, <strong>mass effect</strong> produces clinical manifestations such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Bitemporal hemianopia </li>\n<li>Diplopia, ptosis </li>\n<li>Compression of pituitary stalk\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperprolactinemia initially - reduced dopamine release leads to loss of inhibition of lactotrophs</li>\n<li>Hypopituitarism in later stages</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6719","text":"A 30-year-old woman with a‌ suspected prolactinoma i​s planned for a brain MRI‌. What size would the tum‍or have to be to call it ​a microadenoma?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with a suspected prolactinoma is planned for a brain MRI. What size would the tumor have to be to call it a microadenoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; ‌10 mm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; ‌20 mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;‌ 5 mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; ‌15 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. < 10 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6685836e0f24208af00031ee2b78c4cx720x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt; 10 mm</strong></p><p>Pituitary adenomas<strong> &lt; 10 mm</strong> are called <strong>microadenomas.</strong></p>\n<p>Asymptomatic pituitary tumors can be discovered incidentally on imaging. They must also be suspected in patients with features of hormone hypersecretion or mass effect. In such cases,<strong> MRI with contrast</strong> is the best investigation to detect the tumor, as shown below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Levels of <strong>pituitary hormones</strong> must also be measured to rule out both hypersecretion and hyposecretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6724","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not occur in a patient​ with a pituitary macroad‌enoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not occur in a patient with a pituitary macroadenoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased insulin sensitiv‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pigmentation of mucous mem‌branes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Low serum thyroxine and TS‌H levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of secondary sexual c‌haracteristics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pigmentation of mucous membranes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pigmentation of mucous membranes</strong></p><p>Pituitary <strong>macroadenomas</strong> do not cause pigmentation of mucous membranes as ACTH levels are low.</p>\n<p>In this condition, there is destruction of the pituitary gland by mass effect. This leads to <strong>panhypopituitarism</strong> or reduced levels of all hormones, including ACTH. Excessive pigmentation is only seen in cases of excess ACTH.</p>\n<p>The following hormones are deficient in panhypopituitarism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACTH - Secondary adrenal insufficiency</li>\n<li>TSH - Secondary hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>GH - Growth failure and adult hyposomatotropism</li>\n<li>FSH, LH - Hypogonadic hypogonadism</li>\n<li>Prolactin - Lactation failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Adiponectin</strong>, secreted by fat cells normally increases insulin sensitivity. It is usually suppressed by cortisol. In panhypopituitarism, <strong>decreased</strong> <strong>cortisol</strong> levels lead to increased <strong>insulin sensitivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6687","text":"Which of the following can‌ occur in a patient with ​prolactinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following can occur in a patient with prolactinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior qu‌adrantanopia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior qu‌adrantanopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pria‌pism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of‌ lactation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior quadrantanopia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior quadrantanopia</strong></p><p><strong>Superior quadrantanopia</strong> may be seen in patients with <strong>prolactinoma </strong>&gt; 10 mm in size (macroadenoma).</p>\n<p>In this condition, visual field defects occur due to compression of the optic chiasm. In the initial stages, the pituitary tumor tends to compress the <strong>ventral</strong> portion of the<strong> optic chiasm</strong>. This causes a <strong>bitemporal</strong> (heteronymous) superior field defect. As the tumor grows larger, the entire optic chiasm gets compressed, leading to complete bitemporal hemianopia, as shown below. </p>\n<p>Damage to the <strong>optic radiation</strong> in the temporal lobe would result in <strong>homonymous</strong> <strong>superior</strong> <strong>quadrantanopia</strong> or pie-in-the-sky appearance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3181","text":"What levels of prolactin a‌re definitely suggestive ​of prolactinoma?","raw_text":"What levels of prolactin are definitely suggestive of prolactinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;5‌0μg/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;1‌00μg/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;1‌50μg/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;2‌00μg/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. >200μg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt;200μg/L</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of a <strong>prolactinoma</strong> is highly likely when prolactin levels are <strong>&gt; 200 μg/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> range of serum prolactin (PRL) is <strong>5-20 μg/L</strong>. Prolactin levels &lt; 100 μg/L can be due to non-functioning pituitary adenomas or non-neoplastic causes of hyperprolactinemia. MRI brain must be done in all patients with hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p>Prolactinoma are tumors arising from the <strong>lactotroph cells</strong> of the <strong>pituitary gland</strong>, they can be classified as <strong>microadenoma</strong>(&lt;1cm in diameter, usually non invasive ) or <strong>macroadenoma</strong>(&gt;1cm, locally invasive to adjacent structures). Clinical features include amenorrhea, infertility, and galactorrhea in women, impotence, loss of libido, infertility in men and if tumor extends beyond sella, there can be visual field defects and other mass effects. </p>\n<p>Treatment - In <strong>asymptomatic</strong> patients and those where <strong>fertility is not desired</strong>, they do <strong>not require treatment</strong> and can be monitored using <strong>regular PRL</strong> measurements and <strong>MRI</strong>. In <strong>symptomatic microadenoma</strong> cases, <strong>oral dopamine agonists</strong> (cabergoline and bromocriptine) form the main treatment for hyperprolactinemia and reduction of tumor volume. For <strong>macroadenoma</strong>, before starting dopamine agonists <strong>visual field testing</strong> is to be done, and reassess it with <strong>MRI changes every 6-12 month</strong> intervals until maximum reduction is achieved, in <strong>unresponsive</strong> cases consider <strong>surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6721","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions do ​not need to be screened f‌or pituitary tumors?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions do not need to be screened for pituitary tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ME‌N1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ME‌N2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ME‌N4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carney ‌Complex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MEN2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MEN2</strong></p><p><strong>MEN2</strong> syndrome is not associated with pituitary tumors. It is associated with <strong>medullary</strong> <strong>thyroid</strong> cancer and <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The genetic syndromes associated with <strong>pituitary tumors</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MEN1</li>\n<li>MEN4</li>\n<li>Carney complex tumors</li>\n<li>McCune-Albright Syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0226","text":"A 56-year-old woman presen‌ted with a gradually dimi​nishing visual field. She‌ was found to have a pitu‍itary mass and underwent ​trans-sphenoidal hypophys‍ectomy. Now she has low l‍evels of ACTH, TSH, FSH, ​and LH. Which of the foll​owing hormone supplements‌ is not needed in the pat​ient postoperatively?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman presented with a gradually diminishing visual field. She was found to have a pituitary mass and underwent trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy. Now she has low levels of ACTH, TSH, FSH, and LH. Which of the following hormone supplements is not needed in the patient postoperatively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mineraloc‌orticoids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid ‌hormones","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Estr‌adiol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mineralocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mineralocorticoids</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes a woman presenting with <strong>hypopituitarism</strong> <strong>following trans-sphenoidal resection</strong>. <strong>Mineralocorticoid supplementation</strong> is <strong>not</strong> needed <strong>postoperatively</strong> as it is <strong>regulated by</strong> the <strong>renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system</strong> and <strong>not</strong> by <strong>ACTH</strong>. Therefore, mineralocorticoid levels would remain intact despite ACTH deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Thyroid hormone</strong>, <strong>estradiol</strong>, and <strong>glucocorticoid supplementation</strong> are needed in this patient as their secretion is regulated by anterior pituitary hormones.</p>\n<p>Features of hypopituitarism include <strong>growth abnormalities in children</strong> with a growth hormone deficiency, insufficient levels of gonadotropins leading to <strong>menstrual irregularities</strong>, <strong>infertility</strong> in women and <strong>reduced sexual function</strong>, infertility and <strong>loss of secondary sexual characteristics </strong>in<strong> men</strong>. TSH deficiency leads to stunted growth in children and features of hypothyroidism in both children and adults. ACTH deficiency leads to secondary adrenal insufficiency.</p>\n<p>Polyuria and polydipsia may be seen and indicate a loss of vasopressin secretion in posterior pituitary lesions.</p>\n<p>The following hormones must be replaced post-hypophysectomy:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"height: 554px; width: 325.421875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 1px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 1px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Trophic hormone deficit</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.421875px; height: 1px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hormone Replacement</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 26px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>ACTH</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.421875px; height: 26px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hydrocortisone, cortisone acetate, prednisone </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 67px;\">TSH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.421875px; height: 67px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>L-thyroxine (75–150 µg daily)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 26px;\">FSH/LH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.421875px; height: 26px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">Males: Testosterone</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">Females: \n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>For fertility: menopausal gonadotropins, hCG</p>\n<p>Post-menopausal women: Conjugated estrogen, progesterone</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 46px;\">GH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.421875px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Somatotropin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 59px;\">Vasopressin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.421875px; height: 59px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2713\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Intranasal desmopressin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6723","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common clinical ​presentation in patients ‌with craniopharyngiomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common clinical presentation in patients with craniopharyngiomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Growth re‌tardation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bitemporal‌ hemianopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Head‌ache","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endocrinal ‌disturbance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Headache</strong></p><p><strong>Headache</strong> is the most common presenting symptom of <strong>craniopharyngioma.</strong></p>\n<p>Craniopharyngiomas are <strong>benign, suprasellar</strong> cystic masses. They have a <strong>bimodal</strong> distribution, occurring predominantly in <strong>children</strong> but also between the ages of 55 and 65 years. It can present with the following clinical symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Hypopituitarism\n<ul>\n<li>Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</li>\n<li>Central diabetes insipidus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bitemporal hemianopia</li>\n<li>Raised ICT - vomiting, papilledema, hydrocephalus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6722","text":"A 10-year-old boy is refer‌red for evaluation of pro​gressively worsening head‌aches and visual field de‍fects. An MRI brain is do​ne and shows the followin‍g findings. Which of the ‍following statements is i​ncorrect about this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy is referred for evaluation of progressively worsening headaches and visual field defects. An MRI brain is done and shows the following findings. Which of the following statements is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Composed of epithelial cel‌ls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgery is curative in mos‌t patients","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contains cholesterol cryst‌als","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcifications are seen in‌ adamantinomatous type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surgery is curative in most patients","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c6b875818094331a298a1c5f874a13e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surgery is curative in most patients</strong></p><p>The given MRI showing a <strong>suprasellar </strong>mass with <strong>calcifications</strong> is suggestive of a <strong>craniopharyngioma</strong>. <strong>Surgery</strong> is <strong>not curative</strong> in the majority of patients as the tumor is adherent to surrounding structures.</p>\n<p>They are benign <strong>epithelial</strong> cell-containing tumors and are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Adamantinomatous - <strong>calcifications</strong> and keratin nodules are seen</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Papillary - calcifications are rare</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Biopsy reveals machinery or <strong>motor-oil</strong>-like fluid within the cyst that contains <strong>cholesterol crystals</strong>. The treatment of choice is transcranial or transsphenoidal <strong>resection.</strong> It must be followed by postoperative <strong>radiation </strong>to prevent <strong>recurrence.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6726","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are​ not at risk for pituitar‌y apoplexy?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are not at risk for pituitary apoplexy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperthyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperthyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Pituitary apoplexy</strong> does not occur in hyperthyroidism. It is a rare endocrine emergency which can occur due to <strong>haemorrhage</strong> or<strong> infarction</strong> of the pituitary gland. It may occur as a result of the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pituitary adenoma (spontaneous)</li>\n<li>Sheehan syndrome</li>\n<li>Acute shock</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Sickle cell anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0883","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in pituitary apoplex​y?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in pituitary apoplexy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sh‌ock","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Unconsc‌iousness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fat‌igue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shock</strong></p><p><strong>Shock</strong> is seen in <strong>pituitary apoplexy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Loss of consciousness</strong> (Option C) can also be seen but it occurs secondary to <strong>adrenal insufficiency</strong> and <strong>shock</strong>. Hence, shock is the most appropriate answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Pituitary apoplexy</strong> refers to <strong>acute hemorrhagic infarction</strong> of the pituitary gland. This results in the compression of surrounding structures and <strong>hypopituitarism</strong> leading to <strong>acute adrenal insufficiency.</strong> It may be seen spontaneously in a pre-existing pituitary adenoma, post-partum (Sheehan’s syndrome), or be associated with hypertension, diabetes mellitus or sickle cell anemia or shock.</p>\n<p>Pituitary apoplexy is an endocrine <strong>emergency</strong> that can lead to severe hypoglycemia, hypotension, shock, central nervous system (CNS) hemorrhage, and death. Acute clinical manifestations commonly encompass intense headaches with signs of meningeal irritation, bilateral visual changes, ophthalmoplegia, and even cardiovascular collapse.</p>\n<p>The investigation of choice is <strong>MRI</strong>. </p>\n<p>Patients who do not exhibit overt visual impairment or altered mental status can be managed conservatively with <strong>high-dose glucocorticoids. </strong>Progressive <strong>visual loss,</strong> <strong>cranial nerve palsy, </strong>or <strong>altered mental status</strong> necessitate expeditious <strong>surgical decompression.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6725","text":"Which hormone is the first‌ to fall in Sheehan syndr​ome?","raw_text":"Which hormone is the first to fall in Sheehan syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prol‌actin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G‌H","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"F‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GH</strong></p><p>Growth hormone (<strong>GH</strong>) is the <strong>first</strong> to fall in <strong>Sheehan</strong> syndrome. The hormones are affected in the order of GH → Prolactin → LH and FSH → ACTH → TSH</p>\n<p>Sheehan syndrome refers to postpartum <strong>pituitary necrosis</strong> following severe postpartum hemorrhage (<strong>PPH</strong>). During pregnancy, the pituitary gland undergoes <strong>hypertrophy</strong> with increased blood supply. This makes it <strong>susceptible</strong> to ischemia and <strong>infarction</strong> when there is severe hypovolemia.</p>\n<p>The clinical presentation depends on the amount of damage to the pituitary.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild - can go unnoticed for many years</li>\n<li>Moderate - develops over months\n<ul>\n<li>Lactation failure</li>\n<li>Failure to resume menses</li>\n<li>Mild fatigue and anorexia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Severe - endocrine emergency that develops within few days\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension and shock</li>\n<li>Severe hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Lactation failure</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>GH deficiency is very common in Sheehan’s syndrome because somatotrophs are located in the lower and lateral regions of the pituitary gland, and are most likely to be damaged by ischemic necrosis of the pituitary.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6727","text":"You are a first-year resid‌ent in the postpartum war​d. During rounds, you see‌ a 28-year-old woman who ‍has been unable to breast​feed since delivery. Her ‍baby was born by normal v‍aginal delivery that was ​complicated by severe pos​tpartum hemorrhage. Her b‌lood pressure is 90/50 mm​ Hg and her pulse is 96/m​in. What is the next best‌ step in management?","raw_text":"You are a first-year resident in the postpartum ward. During rounds, you see a 28-year-old woman who has been unable to breastfeed since delivery. Her baby was born by normal vaginal delivery that was complicated by severe postpartum hemorrhage. Her blood pressure is 90/50 mm Hg and her pulse is 96/min. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Growth ‌hormone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyr‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cort‌isone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cortisone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cortisone</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Sheehan</strong> syndrome. <strong>Corticosteroids</strong> must be supplemented first, as <strong>life-threatening</strong> manifestations may occur due to the sudden onset of adrenal insufficiency. This is followed by the replacement of other deficient pituitary hormones including thyroid hormone and estrogen administered with or without progesterone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tYzgM3lG","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"5_immune_disorders","title":"5.Immune Disorders","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC3279","text":"A 6-month-old child has a ‌history of recurrent skin‍ and respiratory tract in‌fections. Investigations ​are carried out and a dis‌order of phagocytosis is ​suspected. Which of the f‌ollowing will not be a di‍fferential diagnosis in t‌his case?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old child has a history of recurrent skin and respiratory tract infections. Investigations are carried out and a disorder of phagocytosis is suspected. Which of the following will not be a differential diagnosis in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DiGeorge‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Leukocyte adhesion deficie‌ncy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chediak-Higa‌shi syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic granulomatous dise‌ase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. DiGeorge syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. DiGeorge syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong> is not a disorder of phagocytosis.</p>\n<p>It is due to a <strong>developmental defect </strong>involving the endodermal derivatives of the<strong> </strong>3<sup>rd</sup> and 4<sup>th</sup> pharyngeal pouches.<strong> Cell-mediated immunity</strong> is <strong>defective</strong> due to decreased T-cells. Affected individuals also have depressed graft rejection, hypocalcemia, and cardiac defects. </p>\n<p>Disorders of <strong>phagocytosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leukocyte adhesion deficiency</li>\n<li>Chediak–Higashi syndrome</li>\n<li>Chronic granulomatous disease</li>\n<li>Tuftsin deficiency</li>\n<li>Cyclic neutropenia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Leukocyte adhesion deficiency</strong> leads to <strong>defective neutrophil migration</strong> to sites of infection, peripheral neutrophilia, and recurrent infections without pus.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Chediak–Higashi syndrome</strong> is an autosomal-recessive disorder due to <strong>defects in lysosomal transport protein</strong> (LYST), leading to defective microtubule polymerization and defective phagolysosome formation. There is neutropenia and recurrent infections. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Chronic granulomatous disease</strong> is due to a<strong> defect in NADPH oxidase</strong> resulting in the absence of respiratory burst. It presents with recurrent infections with catalase-positive microbes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6995","text":"A 1-year-old boy presents ‌with meningococcal mening‍itis. He has a history of‌ recurrent bacterial ches​t and ear infections. Fam‌ily history reveals that ​he has a male cousin (mat‌ernal side) who died in i‍nfancy from infection. Th‌e physician suspects X-li‌nked agammaglobulinemia. ‍Which of the following is​ false regarding his cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old boy presents with meningococcal meningitis. He has a history of recurrent bacterial chest and ear infections. Family history reveals that he has a male cousin (maternal side) who died in infancy from infection. The physician suspects X-linked agammaglobulinemia. Which of the following is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is caused by a mutation‌ of the BTK gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Both humoral and cell medi‌ated immunity are affecte‍d","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"All classes of immunoglobu‌lin levels are reduced","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node biopsy shows de‌pletion of follicles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Both humoral and cell mediated immunity are affected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Both humoral and cell mediated immunity are affected</strong></p><p><strong>X-linked</strong> or <strong>Bruton's agammaglobulinemia </strong>is primarily a disease of <strong>humoral immunity.</strong> Cell-mediated immunity is not affected.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>inherited</strong> immunodeficiency disease caused by mutations in the gene coding for <strong>Bruton</strong> <strong>tyrosine</strong> <strong>kinase</strong> <strong>(BTK). </strong>B cells do not differentiate and mature. They are arrested in the pre-B cell stage.</p>\n<p>There are extremely <strong>low IgG</strong> levels (below 100 mg/dL) and an <strong>absence</strong> of other <strong>immunoglobulin</strong> classes. The <strong>tonsils</strong> and <strong>lymph nodes</strong> are usually <strong>atrophic.</strong> Lymph node biopsy reveals depletion of cells of B cell-dependent areas (follicles). Babies born with this disorder have virtually <strong>no peripheral B cells</strong> (&lt;1%) and suffer from recurrent bacterial infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7014","text":"An immunologist orders a n‌itroblue tetrazolium test‍ on a child suspected to ‌have a primary immunodefi​ciency disease. What cond‌ition is she screening th​e child for?","raw_text":"An immunologist orders a nitroblue tetrazolium test on a child suspected to have a primary immunodeficiency disease. What condition is she screening the child for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuftsin d‌eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wiskott-Aldr‌ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic granulomatous dise‌ase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chediak-Higa‌shi syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic granulomatous disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edaa2ace63984279a8eca62f86a45398x504x331.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06b9cee3563240c39947cd176b8769d7x504x331.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic granulomatous disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>Nitroblue Tetrazolium Test </strong>(NBT) is used as a screening test for <strong>chronic granulomatous disease </strong>(CGD) by assessing the reactive oxygen species (ROS) production in phagocytes.</p>\n<p>This principle relies on the color change of nitroblue tetrazolium, which transforms from colorless/yellow to <strong>deep blue</strong> when it encounters ROS. A higher nitroblue test score indicates that the cell is more efficient in producing ROS.</p>\n<p>In chronic granulomatous disease, there is an <strong>NADPH oxidase defect</strong>. Hence, the phagocyte cannot produce reactive oxygen species or radicals required for bacterial killing, which results in recurrent infections. Here, the leukocytes fail to reduce nitroblue tetrazolium during phagocytosis. Hence, a negative test (NBT remains colourless to yellow) is seen in CGD.</p>\n<p>A<strong> positive test</strong> result means<strong> </strong>neutrophils turn <strong>blue, </strong>as shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>negative test</strong> means nitroblue tetrazolium <strong>remains colourless/yellow, </strong>as shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7011","text":"A 1-year-old boy is brough‌t with a history of recur‍rent pneumonia. He also h‌as eczema and a history o​f prolonged bleeding afte‌r circumcision. Periphera​l smear shows thrombocyto‌penia with small platelet‍s. Which of the following‌ is most likely to be def‌icient?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old boy is brought with a history of recurrent pneumonia. He also has eczema and a history of prolonged bleeding after circumcision. Peripheral smear shows thrombocytopenia with small platelets. Which of the following is most likely to be deficient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gM","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IgM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fe7ed73a7c94bdabaeac4a1956548f3x1280x1363.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IgM</strong></p><p>The history here is highly suggestive of <strong>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</strong> (WAS), a condition characterized by decreased<strong> IgM</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS) is an <strong>X-linked recessive immunodeficiency disorder</strong>. It is caused by mutations in the Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASp) that primarily affects the T lymphocytes. Normally, WASp regulates the actin cytoskeleton and thus helps in cell adhesion, migration, and the formation of synapses between antigen-presenting and target cells. In WAS, there is a progressive loss of T lymphocytes in the peripheral blood resulting in defects in cellular immunity. </p>\n<p>The clinical features of WAS are a triad of <strong>recurrent bacterial infections</strong>, <strong>eczema</strong>, and bleeding due to <strong>thrombocytopenia </strong>(with small platelets). Diagnosis is based on intracellular immunofluorescence analysis of WAS protein (WASp) expression in blood cells.<strong> IgM levels</strong> in the serum are low, but levels of IgG are usually normal. IgA and IgE levels are normal or elevated. <strong>Reduced-sized platelet </strong>is a typical feature of WAS. </p>\n<p>The image below shows an 18-month-old child with WAS, showing multiple petechiae and hematoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9892","text":"An 8-year-old boy was brou‌ght to the pediatric OPD ‍with multiple cold absces‌s lesions as shown below ​on his face, chest, and b‌ack. The child has a hist​ory of recurrent​ respira‌tory infections. On exami‍nation, he has atopic exc‌oriations over the skin f‌olds. What is the most pr‍obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy was brought to the pediatric OPD with multiple cold abscess lesions as shown below on his face, chest, and back. The child has a history of recurrent​ respiratory infections. On examination, he has atopic excoriations over the skin folds. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyper IgM‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wiscott Aldr‌ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyper IgE‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked agammaglobulinemi‌a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyper IgE syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/077fe36c44254fb4ab324b0c4d10dda1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyper IgE syndrome</strong></p><p>A child with multiple abscesses (as shown in the image above) with a history of atopy and recurrent respiratory infections suggests immunodeficiency, and the most probable diagnosis is <strong>Hyper IgE syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The hyper-IgE syndromes are rare primary immunodeficiency syndromes characterized by recurrent severe staphylococcal abscesses of the skin, lungs, and other sites, eczema, eosinophilia, and marked elevation of serum IgE levels. They occur in 2 forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant - involves mutations in the gene encoding STAT-3 (also known as <strong>Job syndrome</strong>)</li>\n<li>Autosomal recessive - involves mutations in DOCK8.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">In <strong>Job syndrome</strong>, m</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">utation of the STAT3 gene leads to</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> IgE overproduction without modulation</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">. Loss of modulation is due to the absence of anti-inflammatory cytokines IL-6, IL-10, and IFN-gamma. Lack of IL-6 leads to deficiency of CD4 + Th17 cells, making them susceptible to various infections.</span></p>\n<p>It usually presents as a triad of <strong>atopic dermatitis</strong>, <strong>recurrent skin staphylococcal infections</strong>, and <strong>recurrent pulmonary infections</strong> that occur early in childhood. The eczematoid rash affects the scalp and face, gets superinfected with <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> and forms cold abscesses. Candida infections are common. Recurrent staphylococcal pneumonia with pneumatocoeles is also commonly seen. Other non-immunological features are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dysmorphic facial features that appear in early adolescence and are absent in the autosomal recessive form</li>\n<li>Craniosynostosis</li>\n<li>Arnold-Chiari type 1</li>\n<li>Hyperextensible joints</li>\n<li>Scoliosis</li>\n<li>Pathological fractures</li>\n<li>Retention of primary teeth</li>\n<li>Vascular complications</li>\n<li>Ocular complications</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis is based on measurements of <strong>serum IgE levels &gt;2000U/mL </strong>where IgA, IgG and IgM concentrations are generally normal, in adjunct with the clinical features seen in Job syndrome. It is associated with peripheral blood <strong>eosinophilia.</strong> </p>\n<p>The treatment primarily focuses on preventing infections through the use of antimicrobials, including antibiotics and antifungals, along with immunoglobulin replacement therapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7027","text":"Which of the following is ‌a combined immunodeficien‍cy disorder associated wi‌th both B-cell and T-cell​ defects?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a combined immunodeficiency disorder associated with both B-cell and T-cell defects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Louis–Bar‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DiGeorge‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chediak-Higa‌shi syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuftsin d‌eficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Louis–Bar syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b43aa32db41b41bbb836e30dfc14c6a2x684x393.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Louis–Bar syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Louis–Bar syndrome</strong> or<strong> ataxia-telangiectasia</strong> is a <strong>combined immunodeficiency disorder.</strong> </p>\n<p>Ataxia-telangiectasia is a syndrome complex that comprises neurologic, immunologic, endocrinologic, and cutaneous disorders. The gene involved in this condition is the <strong>mutation</strong> in the <strong>ATM gene</strong> mapped to the long arm of chromosome <strong>11(11q22-23). </strong>The mode of inheritance is autosomal recessive.<strong> </strong>There is <strong>d</strong><strong>efective DNA repair </strong>and increased sensitivity to ionizing radiation. </p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive <strong>cerebellar ataxia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Oculocutaneous telangiectasias </strong></li>\n<li>Chronic sinopulmonary disease</li>\n<li>High incidence of malignancy -<strong> adenocarcinomas and lymphoreticular tumors</strong> (lymphoma, leukemia, and Hodgkin's disease)</li>\n<li>Humoral and cellular immunodeficiency</li>\n<li>Ovarian dysgenesis and chromosomal abnormalities</li>\n<li>Hypoplastic thymus</li>\n<li><strong>Ataxia</strong> and chorioathetoid movements start in <strong>infancy.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The onset of telangiectasia is around <strong>3 to 6 years</strong> and involves the face, conjunctiva and other parts of the body. By 12 years of age, affected children are usually confined to wheelchairs due to progressive ataxia. </p>\n<p>Death occurs due to sinopulmonary infection in early life or due to malignancy in the second or third decade. They lack IgA and IgE. Cell-mediated immunity is also defective. </p>\n<p>The image below shows ocular telangiectasia in a patient with A-T.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong> is a cellular immunodeficiency disorder associated with <strong>T-cell deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: <strong>Chediak-Higashi</strong> syndrome and <strong>tuftsin deficiency</strong> are disorders of<strong> phagocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3410","text":"A 2-year-old boy presents ‌with reports showing fung‍al pneumonia, the causati‌ve organism is shown belo​w. Parents give a history‌ of recurrent bacterial i​nfections. Laboratory ana‌lysis show markedly eleva‍ted IgM and low levels of‌ IgG. Which of the follow‌ing is the most likely ca‍use of his illness? IgM: ​Immunoglobulin M IgG: Imm‍unoglobulin G STAT 3: Sig​nal transducer and activa‌tor of transcription 3 NA‌DPH: Nicotinamide adenine​ dinucleotide phosphate","raw_text":"<p>A 2-year-old boy presents with reports showing fungal pneumonia, the causative organism is shown below. Parents give a history of recurrent bacterial infections. Laboratory analysis show markedly elevated IgM and low levels of IgG. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his illness?</p>\n<p>IgM: Immunoglobulin M</p>\n<p>IgG: Immunoglobulin G</p>\n<p>STAT 3: Signal transducer and activator of transcription 3</p>\n<p>NADPH: Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defective cytokine signali‌ng in T-cell precursors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal-dominant mutatio‌n in the STAT3 gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficiency in CD40 ligand ‌or CD40","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Defect in the NADPH oxidat‌ive pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deficiency in CD40 ligand or CD40","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/17cd73600c3f4fd68e7a50ac1a5459d4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4293faae32b84e7eaf856a93be2f71fcx1280x1103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deficiency in CD40 ligand or CD40</strong></p><p>The given history of recurrent bacterial infections and fungal pneumonia caused by <em>Pneumocystis jirovecii </em>(as shown in the image above), along with elevated IgM and low IgG levels, are suggestive of <strong>hyper-IgM syndrome</strong>, an<strong> inherited deficiency</strong> in the <strong>CD40 ligand or CD40.</strong></p>\n<p>Hyper-IgM syndrome is a primary immunodeficiency disorder that is inherited as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>X-linked form </strong>(most common): CD40 ligand (CD40L) defect on T-helper cells, preventing B-cell activation, class switching and memory cells formation. </li>\n<li><strong>Autosomal recessive form</strong>: Affect CD40 on B-cells or enzymes like activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID), a DNA-editing enzyme expressed in B cells. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The B-cell response to T-independent antigen is unaffected. CD40–CD40L interactions are also required for IL-12 secretion. So, defects in this pathway result in increased susceptibility to intracellular pathogens.</p>\n<p>In normal immune response, B-cells expressing CD40 on their surface interact with CD40L on T-cells to undergo class switching from IgM to various other classes (IgG, IgA, IgE) depending on the type of antigen. Thus, <strong>failure </strong>of this<strong> class switching</strong> leads to an increase in IgM with extremely low levels of IgG and no IgA or IgE. </p>\n<p>Clinical features include recurrent pyogenic infections caused by low levels of opsonizing IgG antibodies. Patients are highly susceptible to pneumonia caused by <em>Pneumocystis jirovecii.</em></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 630\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The illustration below depicts the defects in components of T-cell &amp; B-cell interactions that give rise to primary immunodeficiency.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Defective cytokine signalling in T-cell precursors is seen in <strong>X-linked</strong> <strong>Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID)</strong>. It is caused by a mutation in the common γ-chain (γc) subunit of cytokine receptors. This defect disrupts Interleukins (IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15 and IL-21) signalling, leading to low T-cell and NK-cell numbers. Although B-cell numbers are normal, these are non-functional due to the absence of T-cell help, resulting in impaired antibody synthesis. </p>\n<p>Option B: Mutation in the STAT3 gene is seen in an<strong> autosomal dominant</strong> form of <strong>Hyper IgE syndrome </strong>(also known as <strong>Job syndrome</strong>), characterized by recurrent staphylococcal skin abscesses, recurrent pulmonary infections often with pneumatoceles and atopic dermatitis. Here, serum IgE levels are markedly elevated (due to IgE overproduction without modulation), and IgA, IgG and IgM concentrations are generally normal. </p>\n<p>Option D: A<strong> </strong>defect in NADPH oxidative pathway is seen in <strong>chronic granulomatous disease</strong>, leading to failure of the oxidative burst in phagocytes, predisposing individuals to catalase-positive microbial infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7028","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brough‌t with complaints as show‍n below. The parents repo‌rt that these complaints ​have been present for the‌ last 2 years. Which of t​he following is found to ‌be the most effective tre‍atment for this disease? ‌Thymus transplantation Am‌photericin B Transfer fac‍tor therapy","raw_text":"<p>A 5-year-old boy is brought with complaints as shown below. The parents report that these complaints have been present for the last 2 years. Which of the following is found to be the most effective treatment for this disease?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Thymus transplantation</li>\n<li>Amphotericin B</li>\n<li>Transfer factor therapy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl‌y 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/142e47cae0b44eeaa10e54f98d0b6af6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>This 5-year-old boy presenting with a 2-year history of thick, white-coated tongue and dystrophic nail changes, as shown in the above image, suggests <strong>chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis,</strong> for which <strong>transfer factor therapy</strong> and <strong>amphotericin B</strong> have been reported to be the effective treatments. </p>\n<p><strong>Chronic</strong> <strong>mucocutaneous</strong> <strong>candidiasis</strong> (CMC) is characterized by <strong>deficient cell-mediated immunity</strong> to candida, usually due to defects in <strong>IL-17</strong>. Mutations affecting the IL-17 signalling pathway lead to impaired IL-17 production and defective T<sub>H</sub>17 function, compromising mucosal and skin defence mechanisms and increasing susceptibility to fungal infections. </p>\n<p>Patients develop persistent fungal infections of the skin, nails, oral cavity, and genitals, primarily caused by <em><strong>Candida</strong></em> species. Despite chronic mucocutaneous involvement, these patients typically do not develop invasive candidiasis. A key feature of CMC is the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>delayed hypersensitivity</strong> to candida antigens. However, circulating antibodies to them are present in high titres. They do not show increased susceptibility to other infections but may develop endocrinopathies, autoimmune disorders, and vascular abnormalities.</p>\n<p>Transfer factor therapy and Amphotericin B have been reported to be effective for the treatment of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3295","text":"An infant with a history o‌f delayed separation of t‍he umbilical cord now pre‌sents with recurrent pneu​monia. The total leukocyt‌e count is 20,000/microli​tre. Which of the followi‌ng genetic defects is mos‍t likely present?","raw_text":"An infant with a history of delayed separation of the umbilical cord now presents with recurrent pneumonia. The total leukocyte count is 20,000/microlitre. Which of the following genetic defects is most likely present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutation of Bruton tyrosin‌e kinase gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low levels of NADPH oxidas‌e and negative DHR test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduced phagocyte surface ‌expression of sialyl-Lewi‍s x","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Excessive IgM with reduced‌ IgG and IgA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reduced phagocyte surface expression of sialyl-Lewis x","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reduced phagocyte surface expression of sialyl-Lewis x</strong></p><p>This history of delayed separation of the umbilical cord with recurrent pneumonia and peripheral leucocytosis is highly suggestive of<strong> leucocyte adhesion defect (LAD)</strong>. In this defect, there is <strong>reduced</strong> phagocyte surface<strong> expression</strong> of <strong>sialyl-Lewis X. </strong></p>\n<p>LAD is an autosomal recessive disorder affecting leukocyte function. Neutrophils have reduced adhesion, mobility, and phagocytic ability. There are three main types of LAD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>LAD <strong>1- </strong>Defective<strong> β2 integrin common chain</strong> (CD18), leading to abnormal neutrophil chemotaxis and defective tight adhesion. </li>\n<li>LAD <strong>2-</strong> Reduced phagocyte surface expression of <strong>sialyl-Lewis X </strong>(fucosylated protein), leading to abnormal neutrophil rolling and tethering. </li>\n<li>LAD<strong> 3-</strong> Defect in regulatory protein (kindlin 3, also known as Fermt 3) involved in activating the ligand affinity for β2 integrins, leading to abnormal neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion (milder than type 1). </li>\n</ul>\n<p>LAD is characterised by recurrent bacterial and fungal infections, reduced inflammatory responses despite elevated blood neutrophilia, and delayed wound healing and delayed umbilical cord loss. in patients with LAD type 3, there may be bleeding as β2 integrin on platelets is non-functional.</p>\n<p>Typical signs of inflammation may be absent, pus does not form with few neutrophils on biopsy of infected tissues along with massive hyperleukocytosis (&gt;30,000/μL) in the blood. Early allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation is used in the treatment of severe LAD. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7031","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about sev‍ere combined immunodefici‌ency disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about severe combined immunodeficiency disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticular dysgenesis of de‌ Vaal is a mild form of c‍ombined immunodeficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are inherited as auto‌somal dominant disorders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosine deaminase defici‌ency is associated with c‍hondrocyte abnormalities","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The defect in Swiss type a‌gammaglobulinemia is at t‍he level of multipotent h‌emopoeitic stem cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenosine deaminase deficiency is associated with chondrocyte abnormalities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenosine deaminase deficiency is associated with chondrocyte abnormalities</strong></p><p><strong>Adenosine deaminase</strong> deficiency is associated with <strong>chondrocyte</strong> abnormalities.</p>\n<p>Severe combined immunodeficiency disorders are associated with <strong>severe deficiency</strong> of both <strong>humoral</strong> and <strong>CMI.</strong> They are inherited in both X-linked and autosomal recessive modes. There are different forms of this disorder: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reticular dysgenesis</strong> is the most <strong>serious</strong> form of combined immunodeficiency. Here the defect is at the level of the multipotent hemopoietic stem cell.</li>\n<li><strong>Adenosine deaminase</strong> (ADA) <strong>deficiency</strong> is associated with enzyme deficiency. The condition is associated with chondrocyte abnormalities which can be discerned radiologically.</li>\n<li><strong>Swiss-type</strong> agammaglobulinemia is associated with lymphocytopenia and severe defect in CMI. The basic defect is at the level of the <strong>lymphoid</strong> stem cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8511","text":"Which of the following is ‌an immunologically privil‍eged site?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an immunologically privileged site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminifer‌ous tubule","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Area p‌ostrema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loop o‌f Henle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminiferous tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminiferous tubule</strong></p><p>The<strong> seminiferous tubules</strong> are an<strong> </strong>immunologically privileged site.</p>\n<p>Immunologically privileged sites can tolerate the introduction of antigens without eliciting an immune response. This allows a higher likelihood of graft survival, even without perfect HLA matching. As a result, an allograft can be placed in these sites without endangering a rejection reaction.</p>\n<p>The unique immune tolerance in these regions is primarily due to the lack of effective lymphatic drainage, which prevents antigen presentation to the immune system, and the presence of blood-tissue barriers that restrict immune cell infiltration. Low vascularity contributes to immune privilege by minimising antigen exposure and immune cell recruitment. </p>\n<p>Examples of immune-privileged sites are the anterior chamber of the eye, cornea, testes, and brain. </p>\n<p>Note: Although part of the brain, the blood-brain barrier is absent at the area postrema, therefore it is not an immune-privileged site.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"p4pRxJVM","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"5_sexual_development__puberty_and_adolescence","title":"5.Sexual Development, Puberty And Adolescence","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0996","text":"Which of the following det‍ermines the sex of the fe​tus?","raw_text":"Which of the following determines the sex of the fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yp‍11","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yp‍10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yq‍11","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yq‍10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yp11","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yp11</strong></p><p>The sex of the fetus is determined by <strong>Yp11,</strong> which is a region present on the short arm of the<strong> Y chromosome.</strong></p>\n<p>It contains the <strong>SRY gene</strong>, which produces the protein called the <strong>t</strong><strong>estis determining factor. </strong>This causes the <strong>gonads</strong> to develop into the <strong>testes. </strong>The target gene for SRY which favours testis differentiation is <strong>SOX9.</strong></p>\n<p>If SRY gene or <strong>Y chromosome</strong> are <strong>absent</strong>, the gonads develop as <strong>ovaries.</strong> The genes that favor this are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>WnT4</li>\n<li>Rspo</li>\n<li>DAX1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0999","text":"When does the sexual diffe‍rentiation of the fetus o​ccur?","raw_text":"When does the sexual differentiation of the fetus occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5th week of‍ development","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7th week of‍ development","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8th week of‍ development","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6th week of‍ development","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7th week of development","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7th week of development</strong></p><p><strong>Sexual differentiation</strong> of the fetus occurs only after<strong> </strong>the <strong>7th week</strong> of development. </p>\n<p>The chromosomal and <strong>genetic sex</strong> of an embryo is determined <strong>at fertilization</strong> by the kind of sperm that fertilizes the ovum (X or Y). However, male and female <strong>morphological characteristics</strong> begin developing <strong>after</strong> the <strong>7th week</strong>. At the end of the 6th week of development, it is impossible to distinguish between the two sexes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1006","text":"Where is the lower part of‍ the vagina derived from?​","raw_text":"Where is the lower part of the vagina derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genita‍l ridge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urogenit‍al sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mulleri‍an duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urogenit‍al folds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urogenital sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4304beeb5eb463084e1bc8d3763382ex1200x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urogenital sinus</strong></p><p>The <strong>lower part</strong> of the <strong>vagina</strong> is derived from the <strong>urogenital sinus. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>ovary</strong> and the <strong>testis</strong> are derived from the <strong>genital ridge</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>upper part</strong> of the <strong>vagina</strong> is derived from the paramesonephric duct or <strong>Mullerian duct</strong>. The uterus develops from the fusion of the right and left paramesonephric ducts in the midline. </p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>labia minora</strong> and the <strong>ventral aspect</strong> of the <strong>penis</strong> are derived from the <strong>urogenital folds</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1034","text":"What is the vaginal epithe‍lium derived from?","raw_text":"What is the vaginal epithelium derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelium of urogenital s‍inus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epithelium of mesonephric ‍duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoderm of ‍genital ridge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epiderm of g‍enital ridge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Epithelium of urogenital sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Epithelium of urogenital sinus</strong></p><p>The <strong>vaginal epithelium</strong> is derived from the <strong>epithelium</strong> of the<strong> urogenital sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The epithelium of urogenital sinus is formed by the <strong>endoderm</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: Urogenital sinus epithelium stains positive for FOXA1, and Mullerian duct epithelium stains positive for PAX2. In 2017, immunohistochemical testing done on embryos revealed vaginal epithelium showed positive immunostaining for both FOXA1 and PAX2.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1032","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures in women is homol​ogous to gubernaculum tes‌tis in men?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures in women is homologous to gubernaculum testis in men?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Round l‍igament","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Broad l‍igament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterosacra‍l ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardinal‍ ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Round ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Round ligament</strong></p><p>The<strong> round ligament</strong> in women is homologous to<strong> gubernaculum testis</strong> in men.</p>\n<p>Homologous structures are those that have the <strong>same embryological origin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1033","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a derivative of the p​aramesonephric duct?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a derivative of the paramesonephric duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Appendix ‍of testis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fallopi‍an tube","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ute‍rus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gartner‍'s duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gartner's duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gartner&#x27;s duct</strong></p><p>The<strong> Gartner's duct </strong>is not derived from the paramesonephric duct. It is a remnant of the <strong>mesonephric/ Wolffian duct</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1011","text":"What is the most common de‍velopmental abnormality o​f the uterus?","raw_text":"What is the most common developmental abnormality of the uterus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dide‍lphys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arc‍uate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bicor‍nuate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sep‍tate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Septate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Septate</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> developmental abnormality of the uterus is <strong>septate</strong> <strong>uterus</strong>, followed by <strong>bicornuate</strong> uterus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5758","text":"Given below is the hystero‍salpingogram of a woman w​ho presented with inferti‌lity. Identify the uterin‍e anomaly seen.","raw_text":"Given below is the hysterosalpingogram of a woman who presented with infertility. Identify the uterine anomaly seen.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterus dideplphys unicolli‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bicornua‍te uterus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complete se‍ptate uterus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannot be ‍determined","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cannot be determined","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5c923577cca4d7ab1341291f988b88c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cannot be determined</strong></p><p>In a <strong>hysterosalpingogram,</strong> the <strong>uterine</strong> <strong>anomaly</strong> <strong>cannot be determined </strong>as the external contour of the uterus cannot be seen. </p>\n<p>Hysterosalpingography (<strong>HSG</strong>) provides excellent information about the<strong> interior contour</strong> of the uterine cavity. However, it doesn't show the external contour, so it<strong> cannot distinguish between</strong> uterine anomalies such as didelphys, <strong>bicornuate, septate</strong> or arcuate uteruses.</p>\n<p>Transvaginal 3-D ultrasonography (<strong>3D TV-US</strong>) is very <strong>accurate</strong> for the diagnosis and differentiation of uterine anomalies. It is the first and only mandatory step in the assessment of the uterine cavity in patients with a suspected uterine anomaly.</p>\n<p><strong>Pelvic MRI</strong> <strong>without contrast</strong> provides excellent delineation of both <strong>internal</strong> and <strong>external uterine contours.</strong> It should be <strong>reserved</strong> for patients in whom 3D TV-US is not possible.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1035","text":"A woman with unilateral re‍nal agenesis presents to ​your clinic with a histor‌y of recurrent abortions.‍ You suspect uterine dide​lphys. Which of the follo​wing would be the next be‍st investigation?","raw_text":"A woman with unilateral renal agenesis presents to your clinic with a history of recurrent abortions. You suspect uterine didelphys. Which of the following would be the next best investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lapar‍oscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous‍ pyelography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hysterosal‍pingography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ultraso‍nography","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ultrasonography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0941512806a0445884a94e908c27dd23x1280x792.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5da9a98bcc4b41fd8af7f7946fe5bd3bx1267x5134.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ultrasonography</strong></p><p><strong>Ultrasonography</strong> (USG) is performed to confirm a diagnosis of <strong>uterus didelphys. </strong></p>\n<p>Uterine didelphis is a congenital condition that occurs when the incomplete fusion of Mullerian ducts that form the uterus during embryonic development leads to the development of two separate uterine cavities and two cervices, each with its own set of endometrial lining.</p>\n<p>It is commonly associated with <strong>renal anomalies</strong> such as unilateral renal agenesis, severe renal hypoplasia, etc, and is often associated with recurrent pregnancy loss, preterm labour, malpresentation, and prematurity.</p>\n<p><strong>3D ultrasound</strong>, a <strong>non-invasive test</strong>, visualizes <strong>both</strong> the <strong>external</strong> (serosal) and <strong>internal</strong> (endometrial) uterine contours, effectively identifying anomalies. <strong>Hysterosalpingography</strong> (Option C) shows <strong>only</strong> the<strong> internal contour</strong>, making it unable to distinguish between conditions like didelphys, bicornuate, septate, or arcuate uterus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The USG findings in didelphys uterus are two widely separated uterine horns and a deep fundal indentation.</p>\n<p><strong>Laparoscopy and hysteroscopy</strong>, being <strong>invasive</strong> diagnostic procedures, should be <strong>reserved</strong> for cases where <strong>simultaneous surgical treatment</strong> is planned following a comprehensive non-invasive assessment of a Mullerian anomaly. (Option A)</p>\n<p><strong>Intravenous pyelography</strong> is used to diagnose <strong>congenital renal anomalies</strong>. (Option B)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0696","text":"A 24-year-old woman visits‍ the infertility clinic w​ith a complaint of recurr‌ent abortions. Upon inves‍tigations, she is found t​o have a septate uterus. ​Which of the following co‍rrective procedures has t​he best obstetric outcome​?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman visits the infertility clinic with a complaint of recurrent abortions. Upon investigations, she is found to have a septate uterus. Which of the following corrective procedures has the best obstetric outcome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jones me‍troplasty","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strassmann ‍metroplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tompkins m‍etroplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transcervical resection of‍ septum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transcervical resection of septum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transcervical resection of septum</strong></p><p><strong>Transcervical hysteroscopic resection</strong> of the septum has the <strong>best</strong> obstetric <strong>outcome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The procedure involves the resection of the septum under hysteroscopic guidance. Hence, it <strong>doesn't scar</strong> the <strong>uterus</strong> and <strong>avoids</strong> the necessity of <strong>cesarean sections</strong> for future pregnancies improving obstetric outcomes.</p>\n<p><strong>Metroplasty</strong> is the surgical resection of the intrauterine septum. This is done by approaching the uterus <strong>transabdominally</strong> via 3 methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jones metroplasty</li>\n<li>Strassmann metroplasty</li>\n<li>Tompkins metroplasty</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of these methods are not preferred nowadays as they cause <strong>uterine scarring. </strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0984","text":"What is the sequence of pu‍bertal changes in girls?","raw_text":"What is the sequence of pubertal changes in girls?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thelarche→Pubarche→Menarch‍e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pubarche→Thelarche→Menarch‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pubarche→Menarche→Thelarch‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menarche→Thelarche→Pubarch‍e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thelarche→Pubarche→Menarche","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thelarche→Pubarche→Menarche</strong></p><p>The sequence of pubertal changes in girls is <strong>thelarche → pubarche → menarche.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 346\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Puberty marks the normal physiologic transition from childhood to sexual and reproductive maturity. The order of changes in puberty are: </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Thelarche -</strong> It refers to <strong>breast budding,</strong> and is the first visible sign. It indicates the competency of the hypothalamo-pituitary-ovarian axis. It appears between the age of<strong> 9-11 years</strong>, and is described using Tanner staging.</li>\n<li><strong>Adrenarche - </strong>It refers to the <strong>generation of androgens</strong> (estrogen and testosterone) from the adrenal gland. </li>\n<li><strong>Pubarche - </strong>It refers to the development of <strong>pubic hair,</strong> and occurs usually <strong>6 months after thelarche</strong>. It is also described using Tanner staging.</li>\n<li><strong>Menarche - </strong>It refers to the <strong>first</strong> occurrence of <strong>menstruation.</strong> </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Growth spurt</strong> usually occurs between ages <strong>10.5 to 13.5 years</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0827","text":"Which of the following eff‍ects of puberty are not m​ediated by estrogen?","raw_text":"Which of the following effects of puberty are not mediated by estrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hair ‍growth","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical muc‍us secretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal co‍rnification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menstr‍uation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hair growth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hair growth</strong></p><p><strong>Pubarche</strong> or the development of <strong>axillary</strong> and <strong>pubic hair</strong> is not mediated by estrogen. It occurs due to<strong> testosterone</strong> in both sexes. Around 50% of testosterone comes from the ovaries and the rest from the adrenal gland. </p>\n<p><strong>Puberty in girls </strong>involves breast development (<strong>thelarche</strong>), an increase in adrenal androgen secretion (<strong>adrenarche</strong>), and the beginning of menstrual cycles (<strong>menarche</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Estrogen </strong>has the following actions <strong>during puberty</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast development</li>\n<li>Development of the reproductive tract</li>\n<li>Starting of <strong>menstruation </strong>(option D)</li>\n<li>Increased fat in the hips</li>\n<li>Regulation of pubertal growth spurt</li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal cornification </strong>(option C)</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical mucus</strong> production (option B)</li>\n<li>Epiphyseal closure</li>\n<li>Maintenance of bone formation - acts against parathyroid hormone. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0986","text":"You are examining an 18-ye‍ar-old woman and it appea​rs she has mature adult b‌reasts. What is the Tanne‍r stage of breast develop​ment in her?","raw_text":"You are examining an 18-year-old woman and it appears she has mature adult breasts. What is the Tanner stage of breast development in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B‍3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B‍4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B‍5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"B‍6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. B5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2f85c615af24383ae28988489a999d5x1280x3776.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. B5</strong></p><p>The Tanner staging of the breast, in case of <strong>mature adult breast</strong>, is denoted as <strong>B5.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0988","text":"An adolescent girl is brou‍ght to OPD with a history​ of delayed menses. On ex‌amination, you note the f‍ollowing developmental ch​anges. What is the Tanner​ staging? The breast is e‍levated like a small moun​d Pubic hair is long, sli​ghtly curved. Pigmented h​air is present sparsely o‍ver the labia","raw_text":"<p>An adolescent girl is brought to OPD with a history of delayed menses. On examination, you note the following developmental changes. What is the Tanner staging?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The breast is elevated like a small mound</li>\n<li>Pubic hair is long, slightly curved. Pigmented hair is present sparsely over the labia</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tann‍er 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tann‍er 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tann‍er 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tann‍er 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tanner 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tanner 2</strong></p><p>The <strong>breast</strong> elevated as a <strong>small mound</strong> and pigmented, <strong>slightly curved pubic hair</strong> is seen in <strong>Tanner stage 2</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 637px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tanner staging</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.438px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Breast changes </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pubic hair changes </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5625px;\">Stage 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.438px;\">\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>B1 - Denotes the prepubertal breast. At this infantile stage, only the papilla is elevated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">P1 - No pubic hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5625px;\">Stage 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.438px;\">\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>B2 - Denotes thelarche. The breast buds are palpable, areola enlarges and the breast is elevated like a small mound.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">P2 - The appearance of pigmented hair sparsely over the labia, either long or slightly curved.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5625px;\">Stage 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.438px;\">\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>B3 - There is further enlargement of the breast and its areola without separation of its contours.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">P3 - Darker, coarser and curly hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5625px;\">Stage 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.438px;\">\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>B4 - Preferential growth of the areola and nipple leads to formation of a secondary mound over the mound of the breast.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">P4 - Preadult stage cover short inverted triangular distribution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5625px;\">Stage 5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.438px;\">\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>B5 - Formation of the mature adult breast.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">P5 - Adult inverted triangular distributuion.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0990","text":"Until which age is the gro‍wth rate of boys and girl​s similar?","raw_text":"Until which age is the growth rate of boys and girls similar?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10.5 ‍years","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"11 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11.5 ‍years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10.5 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10.5 years</strong></p><p>The<strong> growth rate</strong> of boys and girls is <strong>similar</strong> till the age of <strong>10.5 years.</strong></p>\n<p>The growth spurt in girls overtakes that of boys for 1-2 years and reaches a plateau. In boys, the growth spurt starts later and continues for a longer duration. As a result, boys grow taller than girls in terms of final height.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0994","text":"A 13-year-old girl present‍s to the OBG clinic with ​complaints of heavy menst‌rual bleeding. She attain‍ed menarche 4 months back​. What pattern of bleedin​g would you expect in thi‍s patient?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old girl presents to the OBG clinic with complaints of heavy menstrual bleeding. She attained menarche 4 months back. What pattern of bleeding would you expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irregular and often shorte‍ned","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Irregular and often prolon‍ged","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Regular and often shortene‍d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Regular and often prolonge‍d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Irregular and often prolonged","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Irregular and often prolonged</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>puberty menorrhagia</strong>. Menstruation tends to be <strong>irregular</strong> and often<strong> prolonged.</strong> </p>\n<p>Since the hypothalamo-pituitary-ovarian axis is maturing, <strong>initial cycles</strong> are <strong>anovulatory.</strong> Therefore, the pattern of bleeding is irregular. Also, as the endometrium is under the prolonged influence of estrogen, it gets thickened more than normal. Shedding of this thickened endometrium leads to prolonged bleeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0992","text":"What is diagnostic criteri‍on for considering pubert​y to be delayed in girls?‌","raw_text":"What is diagnostic criterion for considering puberty to be delayed in girls?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absence of menstruation by‍ the age of 14 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of secondary sexua‍l characters by the age o​f 14 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of menstruation by‍ the age of 15 years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of secondary sexua‍l characters by the age o​f 12 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absence of menstruation by the age of 15 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absence of menstruation by the age of 15 years</strong></p><p>Puberty would be considered to be delayed in any girl who has <strong>not</strong> started<strong> menstruating</strong> by the age of <strong>15 years</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Delayed puberty</strong> in girls can be declared on the basis of 2 grounds. These are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No</strong> <strong>secondary sexual</strong> characters are present by<strong> 13 years </strong>of age.</li>\n<li>Secondary sexual characters are present, but <strong>no menses</strong> have occurred by<strong> 15 years</strong> of age.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If any one of these is present, it's considered to be a case of delayed puberty.</p>\n<p><strong>Precocious puberty</strong> is considered if breast development or onset of puberty occurs in children <strong>less than 8 years</strong> of age. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0993","text":"A 13-year-old girl is brou‍ght to the pediatrician w​ith complaints of short s‌tature and absence of men‍ses. On examination, brea​sts are Tanner's stage 1.​ What is the most common ‍cause of her condition?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrician with complaints of short stature and absence of menses. On examination, breasts are Tanner's stage 1. What is the most common cause of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Idiop‍athic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constit‍utional","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polycystic ovarian disease‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turner's‍ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constitutional","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f48af1310c11474d94e2159b4e0f9a08x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constitutional</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of<strong> delayed puberty</strong>. The <strong>most common</strong> cause of this condition is <strong>constitutional. </strong></p>\n<p>Puberty<strong> </strong>is considered to be delayed if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast development doesn't occur by 13 years in girls.</li>\n<li>Testicular enlargement doesn't occur by 14 years in boys.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Causes of delayed puberty:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Constitutional or physiological delay (most common)</li>\n<li>Chronic anovulation - PCOS</li>\n<li>Anatomical:\n<ul>\n<li>Outlet obstruction</li>\n<li>Gonadal agenesis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Androgen insensitivity syndrome </li>\n<li>Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism - E.g., Premature ovarian failure</li>\n<li>Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism - e.g., Kallman syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Precocious puberty</strong> is considered if signs of puberty are seen in children <strong>less than 8 years</strong> of age. </p>\n<p><strong>Causes of precocious puberty:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Idiopathic (most common)</li>\n<li>Central</li>\n<li>CNS tumors</li>\n<li>Infections</li>\n<li>Tumors producing\n<ul>\n<li>Estrogen</li>\n<li>Testosterone</li>\n<li>hCG</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The protocol followed for evaluation of delayed puberty in girls is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1222","text":"A 16-year-old girl with pr‍imary amenorrhea has the ​following features. What ‌is the most probable diag‍nosis? Normal stature The​larche - absent FSH, LH -​ high Karyotype - 46 XX","raw_text":"<p>A 16-year-old girl with primary amenorrhea has the following features. What is the most probable diagnosis?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal stature</li>\n<li>Thelarche - absent </li>\n<li>FSH, LH - high</li>\n<li>Karyotype - 46 XX</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testicular feminizing synd‍rome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonadal d‍ysgenesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kallmann‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turner ‍syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gonadal dysgenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ce0d83565e9407da97052e03b4ca3f4x600x878.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fdec7bb5c8340998669563695fd081fx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gonadal dysgenesis</strong></p><p>The scenario of a 16-year-old girl with absent secondary sexual characters, normal stature, with XX karyotype and high FSH and LH is suggestive of <strong>pure </strong><strong>gonadal dysgenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>46 XX</strong> and 46 XY gonadal dysgenesis without the phenotype of Turner's syndrome (short stature, webbed neck) is described as pure gonadal dysgenesis. It is characterized by the presence of <strong>primary amenorrhea</strong> with or without normal secondary sexual characteristics. </p>\n<p>Option A: Testicular feminizing syndrome occurs in karyotype <strong>XY. </strong>Secondary sexual characters are present and the uterus is absent.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 644\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option C: Kallmann syndrome is a form of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. GnRH synthesis is reduced, therefore <strong>FSH</strong> and <strong>LH</strong> are <strong>low,</strong> and secondary sexual characters are absent.</p>\n<p>Option D: Turner syndrome is associated with <strong>short stature</strong> and karyotype <strong>X0</strong>. Secondary sexual characters and the uterus is present, but the <strong>ovaries</strong> are <strong>streaked.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1037","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions do secondary s​exual characters appear t‌o be normal? Resistant ov‍ary syndrome Androgen ins​ensitivity syndrome Mayer​–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser‍ syndrome Imperforate hym​en Transverse vaginal sep​tum","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions do secondary sexual characters appear to be normal?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Resistant ovary syndrome</li>\n<li>Androgen insensitivity syndrome</li>\n<li>Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser syndrome</li>\n<li>Imperforate hymen</li>\n<li>Transverse vaginal septum</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, ‍4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3b17d317f174bfdbea44ced74042b44x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>Secondary sexual characters appear normal in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imperforate hymen</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Transverse vaginal septum</strong></li>\n<li>Absent vagina and functioning uterus </li>\n<li>Absent vagina and nonfunctioning uterus - <strong>Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser syndrome</strong> [MRKH]</li>\n<li><strong>Resistant ovary syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>androgen insensitivity</strong> syndrome, secondary sexual characteristics may or may not develop normally. The genotype is XY, however a female phenotype develops. These patients have <strong>well-developed breasts</strong>, but they have <strong>scanty body hair</strong>. This is because growth of sexual hair is only mediated via androgens. Thus, secondary sexual characters cannot be considered normal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1231","text":"A 16-year-old girl has com‍e to your clinic with pri​mary amenorrhea. On evalu‌ation, height and seconda‍ry sexual characteristics​ are normal for her age. ​Ultrasound showed ovaries‍ but no uterus was seen. ​Her karyotype is 46XX. Wh​at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl has come to your clinic with primary amenorrhea. On evaluation, height and secondary sexual characteristics are normal for her age. Ultrasound showed ovaries but no uterus was seen. Her karyotype is 46XX. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mullerian‍ agenesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Androgen insensitivity syn‍drome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kallman ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Swyer s‍yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mullerian agenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/352f70fb20e4444a8802160122cf9c65x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mullerian agenesis</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>Mullerian agenesis</strong>. It is differentiated from androgen insensitivity syndrome as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 266px; width: 470px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mullerian agenesis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Androgen insensitivity /Testicular feminization syndrome </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Inheritance pattern</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Sporadic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">X-linked recessive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Karyotype </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">46, XX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">46, XY</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Breast development </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Axillary and pubic hair</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Uterus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Gonad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Ovary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">Testis (Intraabdominal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Testosterone </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Female levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">Male levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Associated anomaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.271px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.729px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option C: Kallman syndrome is <strong>hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</strong>. There is a lack of GnRH synthesis, and sexual development is delayed. <strong>Uterus</strong> is <strong>present.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Swyer syndrome is pure gonadal dysgenesis. The karyotype is <strong>XY</strong> and the <strong>uterus</strong> is <strong>underdeveloped</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6923","text":"A 14-year-old girl present‍ed with cyclical abdomina​l pain and a midline hypo‌gastric lump. She has not‍ achieved menarche yet. O​n examination, a bluish b​ulge is noted on parting ‍the labia. What is the li​kely cause of her conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old girl presented with cyclical abdominal pain and a midline hypogastric lump. She has not achieved menarche yet. On examination, a bluish bulge is noted on parting the labia. What is the likely cause of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barthol‍in cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse v‍aginal septum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imperfor‍ate hymen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endome‍trioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Imperforate hymen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Imperforate hymen</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of an<strong> imperforate hymen, </strong>which has led to<strong> cryptomenorrhoea.</strong> This is a condition where menstruation occurs but is not visible due to an obstruction of the outflow tract.</p>\n<p>The menstrual fluid gets collected in the reproductive tract. When this collection occurs in the<strong> vagina</strong>, it is known as <strong>hematocolpos</strong>. If the collection extends to the<strong> uterus</strong>, it is known as <strong>hematometra</strong>. The collection of blood in the<strong> fallopian tubes</strong> is called <strong>hematosalpinx</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common causes of cryptomenorrhea are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intact/imperforate hymen</strong> - a<strong> bluish convex bulge</strong> is seen.</li>\n<li>Transverse vaginal septum - a pinkish concave covering is seen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ultrasound pelvis confirms the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<p>Imperforate hymen - A simple <strong>cruciate incision </strong>of the <strong>hymen</strong> allows the <strong>free drainage</strong> of the collected <strong>menstrual blood</strong>, and leads to normal reproductive function.</p>\n<p>Transverse vaginal septum - Extensive dissection and vaginoplasty is required.</p>\n<p>Option A: Bartholin's cyst appears as a <strong>swelling</strong> on the <strong>inner side</strong> of the <strong>labia majora</strong>. Larger cysts can bulge across the introitus. Small cysts can remain asymptomatic.</p>\n<p>Option D: Endometriomas are <strong>endometriotic</strong> lesions seen on the <strong>ovary</strong>. They present as <strong>adnexal masses</strong>. Endometriosis is a complication in cryptomenorrhea, due to retrograde menstruation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1036","text":"A 16-year-old girl is brou‍ght to the clinic by her ​mother with complaints of‌ failure of onset of mens‍es. On examination, her h​eight is 150 cm, her brea​sts are underdeveloped, a‍nd sparse pubic hair is p​resent. Ultrasound shows ​a small uterus and no gon​ads are visible. Which of‍ the following statements​ is incorrect about her c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl is brought to the clinic by her mother with complaints of failure of onset of menses. On examination, her height is 150 cm, her breasts are underdeveloped, and sparse pubic hair is present. Ultrasound shows a small uterus and no gonads are visible. Which of the following statements is incorrect about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Karyotype may have a Y chr‍omosome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LH and FSH levels will be ‍low","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pregnancy can occur with o‍vum donation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen and progesterone ‍supplementation is the tr​eatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. LH and FSH levels will be low","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. LH and FSH levels will be low</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 400\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>gonadal dysgenesis. </strong>Hence, the patient will have <strong>high LH and FSH</strong> levels as it is a condition of <strong>hypergonadotrophic</strong> <strong>hypogonadism.</strong></p>\n<p>Gonadal dysgenesis is the most frequent cause of <strong>premature ovarian failure. </strong>In this disorder, a normal complement of germ cells is present in the early fetal ovary. However, oocytes undergo accelerated atresia, and the ovary is replaced by a fibrous streak. This is called a<strong> streak gonad.</strong></p>\n<p>There are <strong>two types</strong> of<strong> gonadal dysgenesis:</strong></p>\n<p>1. <strong>Pure</strong> gonadal dysgenesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Karyotypes seen:\n<ul>\n<li>46 XX is seen in pure gonadal dysgenesis due to FSH gene mutation.</li>\n<li>46 XY - Swyer syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Associated with primary amenorrhoea</li>\n<li>They are phenotypically female due to:\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of testosterone</li>\n<li>Absence of Anti-Mullerian hormone secretion by testes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The underdeveloped gonad ultimately fails, which is indicated by <strong>elevated </strong>gonadotropin (<strong>LH, FSH</strong>) levels</li>\n<li>The <strong>presence of a Y chromosome</strong> can result in a germ cell tumor in the <strong>dysgenetic gonad</strong></li>\n<li>Treatment:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hormone replacement therapy</strong> - with <strong>estrogen</strong> and <strong>progesterone</strong></li>\n<li>Infertility - <strong>Pregnancy</strong> can occur with a <strong>donated ovum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. <strong>Mixed</strong> gonadal dysgenesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Karyotypes seen:\n<ul>\n<li>45 X0 - e.g. Turner's syndrome</li>\n<li>46 XX</li>\n<li>46 XY</li>\n<li>Mosaicism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Dysgenetic gonad can develop into a germ cell tumor.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1232","text":"A 16-year-old girl with pr‍imary amenorrhea has come​ to your clinic. On exami‌nation, thelarche is abse‍nt. She has a normal stat​ure, and her karyotype is​ 46XX. Which investigatio‍n would you prefer to per​form first?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl with primary amenorrhea has come to your clinic. On examination, thelarche is absent. She has a normal stature, and her karyotype is 46XX. Which investigation would you prefer to perform first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum tes‍tosterone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG p‍elvis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LH, FSH‍ levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hysterosa‍lpingogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. LH, FSH levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. LH, FSH levels</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>primary amenorrhea</strong>. The<strong> first line</strong> of investigation in primary amenorrhea should be <strong>hormonal assay (LH, FSH, estrogen)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Following this investigation, <strong>USG pelvis </strong>and <strong>MRI pelvis</strong> can be performed to enable the<strong> anatomical assessment</strong>.</p>\n<p>LH and FSH levels help to assess whether it is a hypogonadotropic or hypergonadotropic condition. The <strong>hormone replacement therapy</strong> is planned <strong>based on LH and FSH</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>The various types of hypogonadism are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 561.958px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 34px;\"><strong>Type of Hypogonadism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 34px;\"><strong>LH/FSH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px; height: 34px;\"><strong>Estrogen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.958px; height: 34px;\"><strong>Primary defect</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 17px;\">Hypergonadotropic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 17px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px; height: 17px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.958px; height: 17px;\">Ovary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 17px;\">Hypogonadotropic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 17px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px; height: 17px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.958px; height: 17px;\">Hypothalamus/Pituitary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 37px;\">Eugonadotropic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 37px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px; height: 37px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.958px; height: 37px;\">Varied</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: In a patient with <strong>primary amenorrhea </strong>with<strong> absent sexual characteristics</strong>, the first line of investigation is hormonal assay (<strong>LH and FSH levels</strong>). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1260","text":"A 25-year-old female came ‍with complaints of 4 mont​hs of amenorrhea. Her FSH‌ and LH were elevated abo‍ve the normal value, thyr​oid function tests were n​ormal. Which is the next ‍step in management ?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old female came with complaints of 4 months of amenorrhea. Her FSH and LH were elevated above the normal value, thyroid function tests were normal. Which is the next step in management ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estimate serum estradiol v‍alues","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Give progesterone and stop‍ after 10 days for withdr​awal bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Check USG for polycystic o‍varies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Do a urine p‍regnancy test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Estimate serum estradiol values","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Estimate serum estradiol values</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>premature ovarian failure.</strong> The next step in management would be to check<strong> serum estradiol levels</strong> to evaluate amenorrhea.</p>\n<p><strong>Categories of amenorrhea</strong> are based on gonadotrophin and estrogen levels as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypergonadotropic</strong> hypogonadism - also known as <strong>premature ovarian failure. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypogonadotropic </strong>hypogonadism</li>\n<li><strong>Eugonadotropic</strong> hypogonadism </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism/premature ovarian failure is present when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ovarian function is decreased/absent - Serum <strong>estrogen</strong> levels are <strong>low. </strong>Serum prolactin is high.</li>\n<li><strong>LH</strong> and <strong>FSH</strong> (gonadotropins) levels are <strong>high,</strong> as sex-steroid feedback is absent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Differential diagnosis of premature ovarian failure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gonadal dysgenesis</li>\n<li>Specific gene defects\n<ul>\n<li>Fragile X (FMR1 premutation)</li>\n<li>Galactosemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Iatrogenic\n<ul>\n<li>Ovarian surgery</li>\n<li>Gonadal radiation</li>\n<li>Systemic chemotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Autoimmune disease</li>\n<li>Toxins</li>\n<li>Viruses</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Since FSH, LH, and TFT are already estimated, it suggests that all preliminary tests (including UPT) are done. Moreover, serum FSH and LH values are quite low during pregnancy. This is due to the inhibitory effects of elevated estrogen and progesterone. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xwW86xZ9","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"5_conjunctiva","title":"5.Conjunctiva","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MC0958","text":"Which of the following cha‌racteristic features woul​d you expect to see in a ​histopathology slide of c‌onjunctiva?","raw_text":"Which of the following characteristic features would you expect to see in a histopathology slide of conjunctiva?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amacrine cells within non-‌keratinized stratified sq​uamous epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Goblet cells within non-ke‌ratinized stratified squa​mous epithelium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Goblet cells within kerati‌nized stratified squamous​ epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amacrine cells within kera‌tinized stratified squamo​us epithelium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Goblet cells within non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Goblet cells within non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>goblet cells</strong> within the <strong>non-keratinized stratified squamous</strong><strong> epithelium </strong>would help identify the<strong> conjunctiva. </strong>Goblet cells are involved in the secretion of mucin.</p>\n<p><strong>Amacrine cells</strong> are a type of retinal interneuron, and hence would be found in the retina, not the conjunctiva.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1069","text":"What is the causative orga‌nism of angular conjuncti​vitis?","raw_text":"What is the causative organism of angular conjunctivitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moraxella‌ lacunata","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adeno vir‌us type 32","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coxackie‌ B virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Moraxella lacunata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff93d73f9d5844e2b56274a372b73c0fx720x434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Moraxella lacunata</strong></p><p><em><strong>Moraxella</strong></em> species, a type of <strong>gram-negative diplobacilli,</strong> are involved in causing <strong>angular conjunctivitis</strong> which is a type of <strong>chronic</strong> conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Angular conjunctivitis</strong> is a <strong>chronic</strong> type of <strong>conjunctivitis</strong> where there is inflammation of the conjunctiva at the <strong>lid margins</strong> and the <strong>inner and outer canthi</strong> of the eyes with red, scaly, excoriated,<strong> macerated</strong> and <strong>fissured skin </strong>at the canthi. This is because of the <strong>proteolytic enzymes</strong> secreted by the <em>Moraxella</em> species.</p>\n<p>Patients with angular conjunctivitis usually experience <strong>eye irritation</strong>, a burning sensation and collection of<strong> dirty-white, foamy</strong> <strong>discharge</strong> at the <strong>angles</strong>. These patients can also have associated blepharitis and papillary and follicular conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p>Causative organisms are <em>Moraxella lacunata, </em><em>Moraxella axenfeld </em>and <em>Staphylococcus aureus </em>(Rare cause). The <strong>source</strong> of infection is typically the<strong> nasal tract</strong> or the nasal discharge of patients with angular conjunctivitis. The infection spreads from the nasal cavity to the eyes through contaminated<strong> hands or handkerchiefs. </strong><strong>Treatment i</strong>ncludes<strong> oxytetracycline 1% </strong>ointment for 10 to 14 days, and<strong> zinc oxide </strong>as it <strong>inhibits proteolytic enzymes.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the eye of a patient infected with angular conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: Haemophilus influenzae causes acute conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Adenovirus is the most common cause of viral conjunctivitis and it most commonly causes follicular conjunctivitis, pharyngoconjunctival fever, and epidemic keratoconjunctivitis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Coxsackie B virus is known to cause aseptic meningitis, encephalitis, pericarditis, and myocarditis. There are no known mentions of ocular disease caused by the virus.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Pathogens that cause bacterial conjunctivitis</p>\n<table style=\"width: 347px; height: 195.984px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 61.5938px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 175.844px; height: 61.5938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong><strong>Hyperacute </strong></strong>(symptoms develop within 24 hours; aggressive course)</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.672px; height: 61.5938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong><strong>Acute </strong></strong>(resolving in less than 4 weeks) </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.922px; height: 61.5938px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong><strong>Chronic </strong></strong>(of greater than 4 weeks duration)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 175.844px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.672px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.922px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 175.844px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.672px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.922px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Moraxella lacunata</em></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 175.844px; height: 44.7969px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.672px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.922px; height: 44.7969px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Enteric bacteria</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The below-given table elaborates on the type of conjunctivitis caused by different serotypes of adenovirus.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 161.594px; border-color: #000000;\" width=\"677\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 22.3984px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Conjunctivitis type</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 22.3984px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Adenovirus serotype</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 30.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Follicular Conjunctivitis</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 30.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Adenovirus serotypes 1-11 and 19</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Pharyngoconjunctival Fever</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Adenovirus serotypes 3, 4, and 7</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Epidemic Keratoconjunctivitis</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.5px; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Adenovirus serotypes 8, 19, and 37</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1068","text":"A child presents with irri‌tation and redness of the​ left eye at a primary he​alth center. There are no‌ previous immunization re​cords. Examination showed‍ a greyish-white membrane‌ over the bulbar and palp​ebral conjunctiva which b‍led on peeling. What is t‌he likely etiology?","raw_text":"A child presents with irritation and redness of the left eye at a primary health center. There are no previous immunization records. Examination showed a greyish-white membrane over the bulbar and palpebral conjunctiva which bled on peeling. What is the likely etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria‌e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Moraxella‌ lacunata","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moraxella‌ axenfeld","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corynebacterium diphtheriae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23bfb12768c54b9d9d4b1897436783e4x1280x958.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong></p><p>The of a child with irritation and redness of the eye with no previous vaccination records is suggestive of a case of <strong>membranous conjunctivitis</strong> and among the given options, <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em></strong> is the most likely etiological agent.</p>\n<p>Membranous conjunctivitis presents with<strong> irritation </strong>and<strong> redness</strong> of the eye and is characterized by the formation of a <strong>greyish-white membrane</strong><strong> </strong>that <strong>bleeds on peeling</strong>. Vaccines are routinely used to prevent diphtheria infection and hence, unvaccinated status increases his susceptibility to the bacteria. Moreover, the bacteria thrive in crowded populations and poor hygienic surroundings, hence a background of low socioeconomic context, such as an orphanage, acts as a risk factor in this case.</p>\n<p>This condition is associated with the formation of <strong>fibrinous exudates</strong> on the surface and into the substance of the conjunctival epithelium. The causes include beta-hemolytic <strong>streptococci,</strong> <em>Streptococcus pneumonia, </em><em>Haemophilus aegyptius, Corynebacterium diphtheria,</em> and<em> </em><em>Neisseria gonorrhea</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the eye of a patient with <strong>pseudomembranous conjunctivitis.</strong> It differs from membranous conjunctivitis in that the membrane can be easily peeled off in this condition leaving an intact underlying epithelium. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0949","text":"A child with pharyngitis, ‌and fever subsequently de​velops conjunctivitis. In​itially, the left eye was‌ affected, followed by th​e right eye's involvement‍ in 2 days. Epiphora, con‌junctival hyperemia, and ​chemosis are noted. What ‍is its most likely etiolo‌gical agent?","raw_text":"A child with pharyngitis, and fever subsequently develops conjunctivitis. Initially, the left eye was affected, followed by the right eye's involvement in 2 days. Epiphora, conjunctival hyperemia, and chemosis are noted.  What is its most likely etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Picorn‌a virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coxsack‌ie virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enterov‌irus 70","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5b35264b3fb4368950e3052bd79b78bx800x601.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenovirus</strong></p><p>This child with pharyngitis and subsequent involvement of the eyes most likely has pharyngoconjunctival fever. This condition is commonly caused by <strong>adenovirus</strong> serotypes <strong>3, 4, </strong>and <strong>7</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pharyngoconjunctival fever</strong> is an acute and highly infectious illness characterized by fever, pharyngitis, acute follicular conjunctivitis, and regional lymphoid hyperplasia with tender, enlarged <strong>preauricular adenopathy.</strong> Within the classical triad, symptoms may vary in severity. It is usually bilateral, with one eye typically having onset 1-3 days prior to the second eye. It is a self-limiting disease and treatment is mainly symptomatic, as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cold compresses several times per day for 1-2 weeks.</li>\n<li>Artificial tears 4-8 times per day for 1-3 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Newcastle conjunctivitis </strong>is usually clinically indistinguishable from this condition and is derived from contact with <strong>diseased fowls. </strong>Another conjunctivitis caused by<strong> adenovirus</strong> serotypes<strong> 8, 19</strong>  and <strong>37 </strong>is known as <strong>e</strong><strong>pidemic keratoconjunctivitis.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the eye of a patient with pharyngoconjunctival fever. Significant <strong>conjunctival hyperemia</strong>, petechial subconjunctival hemorrhages, and conjunctival follicles are noted.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0948","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g this condition?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The condition is commonly ‌associated with a benign ​course.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can occur spontaneously‌ in elders with systolic ​hypertension.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The blood gets absorbed gr‌adually in 6-8 weeks time​ without treatment.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Use of NSAIDs and aspirin ‌to treat the condition sh​ould be avoided.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The blood gets absorbed gradually in 6-8 weeks time without treatment.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/94e2c55d7c054609b90f20f24f80b639.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The blood gets absorbed gradually in 6-8 weeks time without treatment.</strong></p><p>The image given above shows a <strong>subconjunctival haemorrhage</strong> in the eye. In this condition, the blood gets <strong>absorbed</strong> gradually in <strong>1-3 weeks time</strong> without treatment.</p>\n<p>A subconjunctival hemorrhage is also called <strong>subconjunctival ecchymosis. </strong>It is due to the rupture of small blood vessels and can occur spontaneously in elders with systolic hypertension or after local trauma. The condition is commonly associated with a benign course but, if the <strong>posterior limit</strong> of the hemorrhage is <strong>not seen</strong>, one should <strong>check</strong> for the <strong>base of the skull fracture.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Recurrent</strong> <strong>episodes</strong> warrant investigations for a <strong>bleeding diathesis or leukaemia.</strong> The use of<strong> NSAIDs and aspirin</strong> to treat the condition should be <strong>avoided.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1070","text":"A UNICEF volunteer present‌ed with a bilateral red e​ye of 2 days duration beg​inning on a return flight‌ from Ghana. The infectio​n spread among all her fa‍mily members within a day‌ of her return. Initial e​xamination revealed lid e‍dema, serous discharge, a‌nd severe sub-conjunctiva‌l hemorrhage. What is the‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A UNICEF volunteer presented with a bilateral red eye of 2 days duration beginning on a return flight from Ghana. The infection spread among all her family members within a day of her return. Initial examination revealed lid edema, serous discharge, and severe sub-conjunctival hemorrhage. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemorrhagic conjunctivitis‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Giant papillary conjunctiv‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allergic co‌njunctivitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inclusion co‌njunctivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemorrhagic conjunctivitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc7aef7307ca4900925fcb43ed684313x1279x454.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemorrhagic conjunctivitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with <strong>bilateral red-eye</strong> and a <strong>rapid spread of infection</strong> to family members points towards a likely diagnosis of<strong> hemorrhagic conjunctivitis</strong>. Examination findings of lid edema, serous discharge, and <strong>subconjunctival hemorrhage</strong> further<strong> </strong>consolidate this diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enterovirus 70 (most important)</strong></li>\n<li>Coxsackievirus 24</li>\n<li>Echovirus 34</li>\n<li>Adenovirus</li>\n<li><em>Pneumococcus</em></li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is <strong>no</strong> <strong>treatment</strong> currently available. The infection generally resolves itself within a five to seven-day period. The use of steroids can actually cause a corneal microbial superinfection which then requires antimicrobial therapy to eliminate.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the eyes of a patient with acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3212","text":"What is the cause of acute‌ hemorrhagic conjunctivit​is?","raw_text":"What is the cause of acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterov‌irus 68","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enterov‌irus 69","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterov‌irus 70","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enterov‌irus 71","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enterovirus 70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8cf57362442645a3b860eb9f61da7c55x1280x454.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enterovirus 70</strong></p><p>Acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis is caused by picornoviruses namely <strong>enterovirus 70.</strong></p>\n<p>Acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis also known as <strong>\"Apollo conjunctivitis\" </strong>is caused by picornoviruses such as <strong>enterovirus 70 </strong>and <strong>coxsackievirus A24</strong>. Rarely it is also caused by <strong>adenovirus type 11. </strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">It manifests with severe conjunctival </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">congestion</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">, chemosis, </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">subconjunctival hemorrhages</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">, painful follicular conjunctivitis, tearing, lid edema and pre-auricular lymphadenopathy. The </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">cornea</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> may demonstrate a </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">fine punctate keratopathy. </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">The disease is usually</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> self-limiting, </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">running a course of 3-7 days.</span></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the eyes of a patient with <strong>acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Enterovirus 71 </strong>(Option D) is associated with fatal meningitis, encephalitis, and paralysis. It is also implicated in <strong>Hand-Foot-Mouth</strong> disease(HFMD) and <strong>Herpangia</strong> which is papulo-vesiculo-ulcerative rash in oral cavity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0955","text":"A tribal farmer, presented‌ with a three-day history​ of right eye redness wit​h pain. He had spent the ‌prior week trekking up a ​mountain in order to trav‍el to the nearby town and‌ takes such trips frequen​tly. The following lesion‍ was observed on examinat‌ion. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A tribal farmer, presented with a three-day history of right eye redness with pain. He had spent the prior week trekking up a mountain in order to travel to the nearby town and takes such trips frequently. The following lesion was observed on examination. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retenti‌on cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pingu‌ecula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pter‌ygium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spring ‌catarrh","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pinguecula","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a129786bd6a42d99ea2b3fd1f46d109.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pinguecula</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of right eye redness with pain, and findings of a <strong>raised whitish-yellowish lesion</strong> surrounded by mild erythema, points toward a likely diagnosis of <strong>pinguecula</strong>. A history of trekking, along with chronic <strong>sunlight exposure</strong> as a farmer, acts as a risk factor because it increases the <strong>exposure to the stronger UV radiation</strong> in the mountains.</p>\n<p>Pinguecula has a fatty appearance and is usually bilateral. They are more often located<strong> nasally</strong> than temporally. It has horizontally oriented areas of <strong>bulbar conjunctival thickening</strong> that adjoin the limbus in the palpebral fissure area. They are associated with a 2- to 3-fold increased incidence of<strong> age-related macular </strong><strong>degeneration</strong>. It is seen in most eyes by 70 years of age and in almost all by 80 years of age.</p>\n<p>Pingueculitis responds to a brief course of <strong>NSAIDs</strong> or<strong> topical corticosteroids</strong>. <strong>Simple excision</strong> may be warranted in rare cases, wherein pingueculae may be chronically inflamed or cosmetically unsatisfactory<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0956","text":"What is the likely diagnos‌is from the image given b​elow?","raw_text":"What is the likely diagnosis from the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pingu‌ecula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concr‌etions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pter‌ygium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bitots‌ spots","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pterygium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f3de6a68c24f4e4faf885c4c59e02429.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pterygium</strong></p><p>The likely diagnosis, based on the above image, is<strong> pterygium</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pterygium</strong> is a <strong>subconjunctival fibrovascular tissue</strong> encroaching the cornea. It is a <strong>degenerative</strong> condition of the sub-conjunctival tissue and is never inflammatory. It occurs due to exposure to <strong>UV-B</strong> radiation and the most common site is the <strong>nasal</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is made up of three parts, \"cap\" (an avascular halo-like zone at the advancing edge), a head, and a body.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Astigmatism with the rule<strong> -</strong> most common finding</li>\n<li>Stocker's line<strong> - </strong>deposition of iron on the advancing edge</li>\n<li>Fuchs islets <strong>- </strong>small, discrete, whitish flecks consisting of <strong>clusters</strong> of <strong>pterygial epithelial cells,</strong> often present at the advancing edge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The differential diagnosis is <strong>pseudo pterygium. </strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pterygium</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pseudo-pterygium</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Degenerative</strong> process</td>\n<td><strong>Inflammatory</strong> process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occurs in <strong>elderly</strong> </td>\n<td>Can occur at any age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Situated at the <strong>palpebral aperture</strong></td>\n<td>Can occur at any site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Either progressive, regressive, or stationary</td>\n<td>Always stationary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A <strong>probe cannot</strong> be <strong>passed</strong> underneath</td>\n<td>A probe can be passed under the neck</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The treatment for pterygium is usually <strong>excision. </strong>To prevent a recurrence, <strong>mitomycin C</strong> or <strong>5 fluorouracil</strong> is used. Other methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Auto-grafting- Conjunctival limbal autograft (CLAG) from the superior limbal conjunctiva is the best method.</li>\n<li>Bare sclera technique- high recurrence rate of 30 to 80%.</li>\n<li>PERFECT surgery- zero rates of recurrence. (<strong>PERFECT</strong> stands for <span class=\"st\"><strong>P</strong>terygium<strong> E</strong>xtended <strong>R</strong>emoval <strong>F</strong>ollowed by <strong>E</strong>xtended <strong>C</strong>onjunctival<strong> T</strong>ransplant</span>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6709","text":"The excision of a pinkish,‌ triangular tissue growth​ at the 1 o’clock positio​n of the eye is being per‌formed. The patient had a​ history of blurry vision‍ and persistent redness i‌n both eyes for a month. ​In order to secure the gr‍aft in the scleral bed, w‌hich of the following wil‌l not be used?","raw_text":"The excision of a pinkish, triangular tissue growth at the 1 o’clock position of the eye is being performed. The patient had a history of blurry vision and persistent redness in both eyes for a month. In order to secure the graft in the scleral bed, which of the following will not be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vicryl‌ suture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autologo‌us serum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyanoa‌crylate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibri‌n glue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cyanoacrylate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cyanoacrylate</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette, with symptoms of blurry vision and persistent redness, along with findings of an abnormal triangular mass of thickened conjunctiva, is suggestive of <strong>pterygium.</strong> <strong>Cyanoacrylate</strong> is <strong>not used</strong> for pterygium surgery.</p>\n<p>The various glues used in ophthalmology are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cyanoacrylate glue- </strong>Mainly used as a corneal patch to <strong>seal acute corneal perforations</strong> and improve visual outcomes, with reduced enucleation rates and need for tectonic keratoplasty</li>\n<li><strong>Fibrin glue- </strong>It is a biological adhesive, which reduces the total surgical time. It is used in corneal and conjunctival surgeries, squint surgeries, cataract (to fix the haptics of intraocular lens [IOL] to the tissue in place of sutures in glued IOL), refractive surgeries, trabeculectomy, and even retinal surgeries.</li>\n<li>Newer tissue adhesives like <strong>b</strong><strong>io-dendrimers</strong> and <strong>p</strong><strong>hoto-cross-linkable adhesives-</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Bovine serum albumin-based bioadhesives: used in squint surgery</li>\n<li>Riboflavin-based bioadhesive activated on exposure to ultraviolet A: used in post-LASIK ectasia, infectious keratopathy, pseudophakic bullous keratopathy</li>\n<li>Hyaluronic acid-based photocatalytic glue activated with argon LASER: used in the repair of corneal lacerations</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0952","text":"A man presented with red e‌yes associated with a san​dy, gritty sensation. He ​was diagnosed with conjun‌ctivitis last week, howev​er, his symptoms persiste‍d. He has had multiple se‌xual partners in the past​. Examination revealed pa‍lpable preauricular nodes‌ and a follicular reactio‌n involving the bulbar co‍njunctiva and semilunar f‌olds. Conjunctival scrapi‌ngs were sent for culture‌. What is the likely orga‌nism involved?","raw_text":"A man presented with red eyes associated with a sandy, gritty sensation. He was diagnosed with conjunctivitis last week, however, his symptoms persisted. He has had multiple sexual partners in the past. Examination revealed palpable preauricular nodes and a follicular reaction involving the bulbar conjunctiva and semilunar folds. Conjunctival scrapings were sent for culture. What is the likely organism involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chlamydia‌ psittaci","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes ‌simplex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gonoc‌occus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b726579f427047b99a68cdabcc1d7d56x360x269.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of red eyes, along with the examination findings of palpable preauricular nodes and <strong>follicular reaction </strong>involving the bulbar conjunctiva and semilunar folds, is suggestive of <strong>inclusion conjunctivitis</strong>. It is also known as <strong>c</strong><strong>hlamydial conjunctivitis, </strong>caused by <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis </em>serotypes D–K. </strong>It is a <strong>sexually transmitted disease</strong> occurring commonly in young adults.</p>\n<p>The modes of transmission include orogenital activities and <strong>hand-to-eye spread</strong> of infective genital secretions. Hence, it is important to <strong>treat all sexual partners</strong> <strong>simultaneously</strong> to prevent re-infection and to rule out other venereal diseases, such as gonorrhoea and syphilis. Treatment includes a single 1g dose of <strong>azithromycin</strong> or <strong>doxycycline</strong> 100 mg twice a day for 7 days.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the eye of a patient with chlamydial conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0954","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is closely relate​d to mucus fishing syndro​me?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is closely related to mucus fishing syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spring ‌catarrh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atopic keratoconjunctiviti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phlyctenular keratoconjunc‌tivitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Giant papillary conjunctiv‌itis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Giant papillary conjunctivitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Giant papillary conjunctivitis</strong></p><p><strong>Mucus fishing syndrome</strong> is closely related to <strong>giant papillary conjunctivitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Mucus fishing syndrome, </strong>patients develop a chronic papillary reaction due to repetitive manual removal of mucus. The severe form of <strong>mechanically induced</strong> <strong>papillary conjunctivitis</strong> is known as giant papillary conjunctivitis (<strong>GPC</strong>). It is most frequently encountered with <strong>contact lens (CL) wear</strong>; in such cases, it is termed <strong>contact lens-associated papillary conjunctivitis (CLPC)</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The increased risk in contact lens users is because of the build-up of proteinaceous deposits and cellular debris on the contact lens surface. It may be also be caused by ocular prostheses, exposed sutures, and scleral buckles, corneal surface irregularity, or filtering blebs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1091","text":"A young man presented with‌ watering, redness and ph​otophobia. He had similar​ complaints in the past w‌hich improved with self-m​edicated topical drops. H‍is best-corrected visual ‌acuity is 6/36 improving ​to 6/24. Slit-lamp examin‍ation revealed a 3 mm × 2‌ mm epithelial defect in ‌the paracentral part of t‍he cornea that stained po‌sitive with fluorescein d‌yes. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A young man presented with watering, redness and photophobia. He had similar complaints in the past which improved with self-medicated topical drops. His best-corrected visual acuity is 6/36 improving to 6/24. Slit-lamp examination revealed a 3 mm × 2 mm epithelial defect in the paracentral part of the cornea that stained positive with fluorescein dyes. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spring ‌catarrh","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exposure k‌eratopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuroparalytic keratopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spring catarrh","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41320d71d3ef4d258273ab56593a2967x320x215.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spring catarrh</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>spring catarrh</strong>. Slit-lamp examination finding of <strong>shield ulcer </strong>is typical of <strong>vernal keratoconjunctivitis</strong> (VKC) or <strong>warm weather conjunctivitis</strong> (another name of VKC).</p>\n<p>Shield ulcers are transverse ulcers seen in the upper cornea due to epithelial macroerosions in VKC. It is a rare <strong>complication of VKC</strong> and should always be kept in mind for patients suffering from vernal conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p>Corneal signs in VKC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Shield ulcers</strong></li>\n<li>Vernal plaques</li>\n<li>Subepithelial scarring</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo-gerontoxon-</strong> resembles arcus senilus; has a <strong>cupid's bow</strong> outline</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of VKC is mainly with allergy avoidance and lid hygiene. Cool compresses, topical antihistamines, topical NSAIDs and topical steroids can be used to reduce the intensity of symptoms. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a shield ulcer in a patient with VKC.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1075","text":"While browsing through the‌ news channels, Geeta cam​e across a panel discussi​on on public health disea‌ses celebrating the elimi​nation of a peculiar cond‍ition of the eye. It was ‌known as the most importa​nt cause of corneal blind‍ness in India and shows t‌he following finding. Wha‌t is the likely diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"While browsing through the news channels, Geeta came across a panel discussion on public health diseases celebrating the elimination of a peculiar condition of the eye. It was known as the most important cause of corneal blindness in India and shows the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vernal keratoconjunctiviti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phlyctenular conjunctiviti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Xeroph‌thalmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trachoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d22ca5d022a64386960ad7d2c453f355.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trachoma</strong></p><p>The given<strong> sago-grain appearance</strong> of the conjunctiva is seen in <strong>trachoma</strong>. It was the most important cause of <strong>corneal blindness </strong>in India affecting young children. </p>\n<p>Conjunctival appearance:</p>\n<p><strong>1. In trachoma</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sago-grain appearance</li>\n<li>A <strong>follicular</strong> reaction </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">2. In vernal keratoconjunctivitis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cobblestone </strong>appearance</li>\n<li>A <strong>papillary</strong> reaction seen in palpebral conjunctiva</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Phlyctenular conjunctivitis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>White-pinkish <strong>nodule</strong> in the bulbar conjunctiva</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Xerophthalmia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelial xerosis</li>\n<li><strong>Bitot spot</strong> in bulbar conjunctiva</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1077","text":"Which of the following wil‌l not be seen as a cornea​l sign in a 6-year-old pa​tient with trachoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following will not be seen as a corneal sign in a 6-year-old patient with trachoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herber‌t pits","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pan‌nus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opa‌city","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arlt’‌s line","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arlt’s line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arlt’s line</strong></p><p><strong>Arlt’s line</strong> is a <strong>conjunctival</strong> sign of trachoma. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 287px; width: 421.137px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 165px; height: 17px;\"><strong>Corneal</strong> signs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.137px; height: 17px;\"><strong>Conjunctival</strong> signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 134.926px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 165px; height: 134.926px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial keratitis</li>\n<li>Follicles in the limbal area</li>\n<li><strong>Pannus-</strong> superior limbus</li>\n<li>Corneal ulcer</li>\n<li><strong>Herberts pits- </strong>near Limbal area</li>\n<li>Corneal opacity</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.137px; height: 134.926px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Congestion- On upper tarsal, fornix</li>\n<li>Follicles (containing <strong>Leber cells </strong>on histopathology)- On upper tarsal, fornix</li>\n<li>Papillary hyperplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Arlt’s line-</strong> Linear scarring in sulcus subtarsalis</li>\n<li><strong>Concretions- </strong>Inspissated mucus, dead epithelial cells in the glands of Henle</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1084","text":"A young girl has been brou‌ght in by her grandmother​ because she has constant​ pain, photosensitivity a‌nd discharge from her eye​s. From the notes, you se‍e that the child has had ‌multiple presentations fo​r prior eye infections. Y‍ou observe severe scarrin‌g on the palpebral conjun‌ctiva, as well as trachom‍atous trichiasis. Which M‌cCallan stage will this b‌e classified under?","raw_text":"A young girl has been brought in by her grandmother because she has constant pain, photosensitivity and discharge from her eyes. From the notes, you see that the child has had multiple presentations for prior eye infections. You observe severe scarring on the palpebral conjunctiva, as well as trachomatous trichiasis. Which McCallan stage will this be classified under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‌ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌ge 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‌ge 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d211412289c54438bd1f7cb977d7001ex1024x670.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage 3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The clinical vignette of a young child with pain, photosensitivity and discharge, having repeated infections, along with findings of scarring and trichiasis is suggestive of <strong>trachoma</strong>. Cicatrising trachoma is <strong>stage 3</strong> of trachoma and is characterized by scarring of the palpebral conjunctiva.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Cicatricial trachoma</strong> is the end result of scarring that occurs in individuals who suffer from severe, persistent infections in childhood. Trachomatous scarring is recognized by the presence of easily visible <strong>white bands</strong> on the underside of the upper eyelid. Severe scarring can result in contraction of the tarsal conjunctiva which causes the lid margin to roll inwards. This results in buckling of the lid so the lashes rub on the eye known as <strong>trachomatous trichiasis</strong>. These lashes can lead to corneal opacities and scarring and eventually <strong>blindness</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The four stages of trachoma according to <strong>McCallan</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage 1: <strong>Incipient</strong> -<strong> </strong>Immature follicles are present, but<strong> </strong>clinical <strong>symptoms </strong>are<strong> not obvious</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Stage 2: </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Established</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> -</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Typical trachomatous lesions</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> without scarring</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">.</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Stage 3: </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Cicatrising</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> - </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Scarring</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">along with</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">active infection.</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Stage 4:</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Healed - </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Only scarring, the disease is</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">either</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> cured or quiet</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows trachomatous trichiasis with severe scarring-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1088","text":"What is the drug of choice‌ under the SAFE strategy ​for trachoma management?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice under the SAFE strategy for trachoma management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penic‌illin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Azithromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Azithromycin</strong></p><p><strong>Oral azithromycin</strong> is the drug of choice in the <strong>SAFE strategy</strong> of trachoma management. The adult dose is <strong>1 g</strong> while <strong>20 mg/kg</strong> body weight is given for children.</p>\n<p>Oral azithromycin is slowly replacing tetracycline eye ointment as the first line drug used in trachoma treatment. The experience of Morocco, the first country that used azithromycin in a large-scale, nationwide elimination programme, has paved the way for the <strong>worldwide application of azithromycin as part of the SAFE strategy</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1095","text":"A 21-year-old man presente‌d with the following lesi​on in his eye. What is th​e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old man presented with the following lesion in his eye. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xeroph‌thalmia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pter‌ygium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phlyctenular conjunctiviti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membranous c‌onjunctivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Xerophthalmia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5dc44d3c29841119f7b8fa5bee15f57.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Xerophthalmia</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>Bitot's spot</strong>,<strong> </strong>a pathognomonic feature of <strong>xerophthalmia</strong>. Hence, the likely diagnosis in this 21-year-old man presenting with the above lesion is<strong> xerophthalmia.</strong></p>\n<p>Bitot's spot is a triangular, silvery-white, foamy patch on the bulbar conjunctiva which is usually bilateral. The most common site is <strong>t</strong><strong>emporal.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0957","text":"A 10 year old malnourished‌ boy is brought with a 2-​month history of diminish​ed night vision. His diet‌ary history revealed a re​stricted diet, consisting‍ of mainly chips and nugg‌ets, and he would refuse ​to eat green leafy vegeta‍bles. What is the conjunc‌tival change seen in this‌ deficiency?","raw_text":"A 10 year old malnourished boy is brought with a 2-month history of diminished night vision. His dietary history revealed a restricted diet, consisting of mainly chips and nuggets, and he would refuse to eat green leafy vegetables. What is the conjunctival change seen in this deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actinic de‌generation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stromal in‌filtration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperplasia of goblet cell‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperkeratosis of the squa‌mous epithelium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperkeratosis of the squamous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperkeratosis of the squamous epithelium</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario indicates that this patient has <strong>nyctalopia</strong> (diminished night vision) most likely due to<strong> vitamin A deficiency, </strong>suggested by his poor consumption of vegetables. Changes seen in the conjunctiva in vitamin A deficiency is <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong> of the <strong>squamous epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>This occurs due to the metaplasia of the non-keratinized epithelium to keratinized epithelium. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0953","text":"Ophthalmia nodosa occurs d‌ue to which of the follow​ing?","raw_text":"Ophthalmia nodosa occurs due to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cat scrat‌ch disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tula‌remia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Irritation due to caterpil‌lar hair","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Irritation due to caterpillar hair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1297e2690eb436980b427bb5d163433x804x586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Irritation due to caterpillar hair</strong></p><p><strong>Ophthalmia nodosa</strong> occurs due to irritation due to <strong>caterpillar hair</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is often <strong>mistaken for tuberculosis. </strong>On examination, small, semitranslucent, reddish and yellowish-grey nodules are formed in the conjunctiva, cornea, and iris. Microscopy contains caterpillar hair surrounded by <strong>giant cells and lymphocytes. </strong>Treatment includes <strong>excision</strong> of the nodules in the conjunctiva.   </p>\n<p>The image below shows a patient's eye infected with ophthalmia nodosa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1092","text":"A 28-year-old woman with r‌edness and photophobia of​ the left eye was seen in​ the OPD. An image of a c‌haracteristic lesion of h​er eye is as shown below.‍ Identify the lesion.","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman with redness and photophobia of the left eye was seen in the OPD. An image of a characteristic lesion of her eye is as shown below. Identify the lesion.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pter‌ygium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pingu‌ecula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phly‌cten","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hebert’s‌ follicle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phlycten","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a5c2f20e7672495bab5159541833e000.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2604fedd5694f57833539469fa6097cx800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phlycten</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>white-pinkish nodule</strong> surrounded by hyperemia on the <strong>bulbar conjunctiva</strong> near the <strong>limbus</strong> with associated symptoms of <strong>redness</strong> and <strong>photophobia</strong> most likely has phlyctenular conjunctivitis. The given finding is referred to as a <strong>phlycten.</strong></p>\n<p> <strong>Phlyctenular conjunctivitis </strong>is a type 4 hypersensitivity reaction due to endogenous allergens like <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus </em></strong>(most common) and <strong><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phlycten that may eventually form <strong>ring ulcers</strong></li>\n<li>Shallow marginal ulcer due to the breakdown of limbal phlycten known as <strong>sc</strong><strong>rofulous ulcer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fascicular ulcer</strong> with a prominent parallel leash of blood vessels that heals leaving a <strong>band-shaped opacity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong>: Topical steroids; atropine if the cornea is involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> This can be differentiated from pinguecula by the following points:</p>\n<p>Pinguecula is a <strong>yellow</strong> <strong>triangular patch</strong> on the conjunctiva near the limbus. It usually <strong>remains free from conjunctival congestion.  </strong>The following image shows <strong>a yellow patch</strong> with <strong>tortuous blood vessels.</strong> There is no congestion as seen with a phlycten.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6707","text":"Which of the following sho‌uld not be considered in ​the differential of a pat​ient who presents with th‌e following finding?","raw_text":"Which of the following should not be considered in the differential of a patient who presents with the following finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythema ‌multiforme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Steven-John‌son syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phlyctenular conjunctiviti‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alkali‌ injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phlyctenular conjunctivitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00e15761c79349ae9dafb82bd4b40cfc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phlyctenular conjunctivitis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>symblepharon,</strong> which is <strong>not seen</strong> in phlyctenular conjunctivitis. It is a partial or complete <strong>adhesion of the palpebral conjunctiva</strong> of the eyelid to the bulbar conjunctiva of the eyeball.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of symblepharon:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chemical injuries (especially alkalis)</li>\n<li>Burns</li>\n<li>Ocular cicatrical pemphigoid (OCP)</li>\n<li>Steven–Johnson syndrome</li>\n<li>Erythema multiforme</li>\n<li>Pemphigus bullosa</li>\n<li>Membranous conjunctivitis</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Management: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lubrication</li>\n<li>Sweeping of fornices</li>\n<li>Symblepharon ring</li>\n<li>Symblepharon release and amniotic membrane graft</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1093","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of ophthalmia neonato​rum in developed countrie​s?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of ophthalmia neonatorum in developed countries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chla‌mydia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gonor‌rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemical co‌njunctivitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b6c877298fa4b5186ea63d8948d3c26x1218x1178.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydia</strong></p><p><em><strong>Chlamydia</strong></em> is at present the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>ophthalmia neonatarum</strong> in <strong>developed</strong> countries.</p>\n<p>Ophthalmia neonatarum also called <strong>neonatal conjunctivitis </strong>is <strong>inflammation</strong> of the conjunctiva occurring in an infant <strong>&lt;30</strong> <strong>days</strong> after birth.It should be ruled out in any infant having discharge or watering within the first week of birth.</p>\n<p>Causes include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><em>Chlamydia</em></strong>(Option A): neonatal <strong>inclusion</strong> conjunctivitis caused by <strong>serotypes D-K</strong> is at present the <strong>most common</strong> cause in developed countries. </li>\n<li><em><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong></em>(Option B): effective prophylaxis against gonococcal infection and its subsequent decline has almost eliminated it's incidence in developed countries.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong>(Option D), <em>Haemophilus</em> species, <em>Streptococcus haemolyticus</em>, and <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae </em>are other less commonly seen causes of ophthalmia neonatarum</li>\n<li><em>Herpes simplex</em> ophthalmia neonatrum rarely occurs and is caused by <em>Herpes simplex</em> type 2 virus from the mother.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemical conjunctivitis</strong>(Option C) is caused by the application of <strong>prophylactic antibiotics</strong> or <strong>silver nitrate</strong> at the time of birth. </li>\n</ol>\n<table style=\"height: 198.793px; width: 367px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.8011px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.249px; height: 44.8011px;\"><strong>Time of presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.423px; height: 44.8011px;\"><strong>Causative agent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.4006px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.249px; height: 22.4006px;\">Within 48 h</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.423px; height: 22.4006px;\">Chemical, Gonorrhoea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 99.1903px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.249px; height: 99.1903px;\">48–72 h</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.423px; height: 99.1903px;\">\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.4006px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.249px; height: 22.4006px;\">5–7 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.423px; height: 22.4006px;\">Herpes simplex II</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.249px; height: 10px;\">&gt;1 week</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.423px; height: 10px;\">Chlamydia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><br/>Prophylactic</strong> treatment: 1% tetracycline or erythromycin eye ointment is applied to the eyes, if the mother is suspected to have <em>gonococca</em>l or <em>chlamydia</em> infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Curative</strong> treatment: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 398\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 398\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> (D– K)- Erythromycin eye ointment</li>\n<li><em><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae- </em></em>Ceftriaxone injection, gentamicin drops, bacitracin eye ointment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows an infant presenting with <strong>ophthalmia neonatarum.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0946","text":"An 8-year-old boy had to m‌iss school because the ma​tting of his eyelids made​ it difficult to open the‌m in the morning and caus​ed severe pain. His agoni‍zed mother rushed him to ‌the hospital and reported​ that he had a fever and ‍red eye for 4 days. Which‌ group of lymph nodes is ‌likely to be enlarged?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy had to miss school because the matting of his eyelids made it difficult to open them in the morning and caused severe pain. His agonized mother rushed him to the hospital and reported that he had a fever and red eye for 4 days. Which group of lymph nodes is likely to be enlarged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sublingual and submandibul‌ar nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parotid and sublingual nod‌es","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Submandibular and preauric‌ular nodes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Jugulo-diga‌stric nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Submandibular and preauricular nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4fc4305c60c24134b914471e0e2227aex1280x1537.2637517630465.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Submandibular and preauricular nodes</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of fever and red eyes, associated with matting of eyelids and pain, is suggestive of<strong> conjunctivitis</strong>. Lymph from the conjunctiva drains directly into the <strong>submandibular </strong>and<strong> preauricular nodes </strong>and hence, these group of lymph nodes are likely to be enlarged.</p>\n<p>The <strong>l</strong><strong>ymphatic drainage of the conjunctiva </strong>is as follows-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>From the <strong>medial side</strong>, one-third of the superior and medial two-thirds of the inferior conjunctiva → <strong>Submandibular</strong> <strong>nodes</strong></li>\n<li>From the <strong>lateral side</strong>, two-thirds of the superior conjunctiva and lateral one-third of the inferior conjunctiva → <strong>Preauricular</strong> <strong>nodes</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Specialized collections of T and B lymphocytes underlying a modified epithelium is known as <strong>conjunctiva-associated lymphoid tissue (CALT) </strong>present in the stroma (substantia propria). It is critical in the initiation and regulation of ocular-surface immune responses. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> Note:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The stroma also consists of the <strong>accessory lacrimal glands</strong> of <strong>Krause</strong> and <strong>Wolfring </strong>which are exocrine glands responsible for the basal secretion of the middle aqueous layer of the tear film.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the lymphatic drainage of the conjunctiva.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6710","text":"The image of the eye of a ‌32-year-old woman having ​superior limbic keratocon​junctivitis is given belo‌w. Which of the following​ is commonly associated w‍ith it?","raw_text":"The image of the eye of a 32-year-old woman having superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis is given below. Which of the following is commonly associated with it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valvular he‌art disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inflammatory bowel disease‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thyroid disease","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/cb0cf85320624756baab9c12c5b00dd3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thyroid disease</strong></p><p><strong>Superior</strong> <strong>limbic keratoconjunctivitis</strong> has a definite association with <strong>thyroid dysfunction. </strong>It affects one or both eyes of <strong>middle-aged women</strong> and about <strong>65</strong><strong>% </strong>of patients have abnormal thyroid function (usually <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is a chronic disease of the superior limbus and the superior bulbar and the tarsal conjunctiva.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary hypertrophy of the superior tarsal plate, often having a <strong>diffuse velvety appearance.</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperemia of a radial band of the superior bulbar conjunctiva that stains with rose bengal.</li>\n<li style=\"list-style-type: none;\"></li>\n<li>Superior filamentary keratitis that develops in about one-third of cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preservative-free lubricants</li>\n<li><strong>Topical acetylcysteine 10% to 20%,</strong> four times daily to break down filaments</li>\n<li>Topical rebamipide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6708","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not characteri​stic of pterygium?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not characteristic of pterygium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal activity of p53 i‌s seen in the pterygium t​issue.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased activity of tiss‌ue inhibitors of metallop​roteinases is seen.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Destruction of Bowman's la‌yer occurs when the ptery​gium invades the cornea.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elastotic degeneration and‌ hyalinization of the con​nective tissue of the con​junctiva occur.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Elastotic degeneration and hyalinization of the connective tissue of the conjunctiva occur.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Elastotic degeneration and hyalinization of the connective tissue of the conjunctiva occur.</strong></p><p>Elastotic degeneration and <strong>hyalinization</strong> of the conjunctival connective tissue occurs in <strong>pinguecula</strong> (and <strong>not pterygium</strong>). </p>\n<p>In pterygium, there is elastotic degeneration of collagen with<strong> fibrovascular proliferation.</strong></p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish a pterygium from a pinguecula. A pterygium, while histologically similar to a pinguecula, is distinguished by fibrovascular tissue that grows onto the cornea. Pingueculae do not have vascularized tissue and do not cross the limbus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1090","text":"A 7-year-old boy complains‌ of intense lacrimation, ​associated with photophob​ia. On examination, the f‌ollowing finding is seen ​in his upper tarsal conju‍nctiva. What is the likel‌y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy complains of intense lacrimation, associated with photophobia. On examination, the following finding is seen in his upper tarsal conjunctiva. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vernal keratoconjunctiviti‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phlyctenular conjunctiviti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Xeroph‌thalmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vernal keratoconjunctivitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1156d7b977eb48c2a202f5bacd24041a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36ac67de6bd6434ea799f7bf80c77346x623x187.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vernal keratoconjunctivitis</strong></p><p>The above image depicts <strong>cobblestone appearance</strong>, which is a <strong>papillary</strong> reaction in the <strong>palpebral</strong> conjunctiva seen in<strong> vernal keratoconjunctivitis (VKC)</strong>. No follicular reaction is seen.</p>\n<p>Vernal conjunctivitis is also called <strong>spring catarrh </strong>or<strong> warm weather conjunctivitis. </strong>It primarily affects<strong> boys,</strong> and the onset is generally from the age of<strong> 5 years</strong>. It is primarily a<strong> type 1 hypersensitivity reaction</strong> that is IgE mediated. It also involves a type 4 hypersensitivity reaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical features </strong>of the conjunctiva in VKC:</p>\n<p><strong>Palpebral VKC - </strong>Cobblestone papillae are present in the upper tarsal conjunctiva associated with ropy discharge. <strong>Maxwell–Lyon sign</strong> is present which is a pseudomembrane formation of the upper lid when everted and exposed to heat </p>\n<p><strong>Limbal VKC -</strong> <strong>Horner </strong>– <strong>Trantas</strong> <strong>spots</strong> are white dots consisting of eosinophils and epithelial debris in the limbal area. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows Horner-Trantas spots.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment of this condition is with topical steroids and mast cell stabilizers such as cromolyn sodium. Topical cyclosporine can be used in patients who do not respond to first line treatment modalities. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0947","text":"Your professor has assigne‌d you to proofread a semi​nar on the anatomy of the​ conjunctiva made by a ju‌nior. In the explanation ​of conjunctival follicles‍, you spot a wrong statem‌ent that needs revision. ​What is that statement?","raw_text":"Your professor has assigned you to proofread a seminar on the anatomy of the conjunctiva made by a junior. In the explanation of conjunctival follicles, you spot a wrong statement that needs revision. What is that statement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conjunctiva of new born do‌es not produce follicles ​before 2-3 months of age.​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Slit lamp may be necessary‌ to differentiate between​ follicles and papillae.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Follicles are a hyperplasi‌a of the normal vascular ​system.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Follicles are found in the‌ sub-epithelial adenoid l​ayer of the conjunctiva.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Follicles are a hyperplasia of the normal vascular system.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7bcdceffca640c28011c2b385f59362x494x318.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec4c7c5dd1784faa8e7fd5c02dce3cabx506x364.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Follicles are a hyperplasia of the normal vascular system.</strong></p><p><strong>Papillae (not follicles) </strong>are hyperplasia of the normal vascular system, with glomerulus-like branches of capillaries growing into the epithelium in inflammatory conditions. On the other hand, <strong>follicles</strong> are <strong>yellowish-white</strong>, round elevations of<strong> 1-2 mm</strong> diameter. Follicles are a <strong>localized aggregation of lymphocytes </strong>present in the <strong>sub-epithelial adenoid layer </strong>of the conjunctiva.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Conjunctival papillae (hyperplastic epithelium and capillaries) shown in the first image followed by conjunctival giant follicles (round yellowish-white elevations)  shown in the second image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0951","text":"In the grading of trachoma‌, the follicular stage is​ defined by the presence ​of which of the following‌?","raw_text":"In the grading of trachoma, the follicular stage is defined by the presence of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 or more follicles in the‌ lower tarsal conjunctiva​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 or more follicles in the‌ upper tarsal conjunctiva​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 or more follicles in the‌ upper tarsal conjunctiva​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 or more follicles in the‌ lower tarsal conjunctiva​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 or more follicles in the upper tarsal conjunctiva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7a28fbc622142e8b916d36c5f46640dx1024x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 or more follicles in the upper tarsal conjunctiva</strong></p><p>In the grading of trachoma,<strong> follicular stage</strong> is defined by the presence of <strong>5 or more</strong> follicles in the <strong>upper tarsal</strong> conjunctiva.</p>\n<p><strong>Trachoma</strong> is the leading infectious cause of blindness worldwide. It is caused by an obligate intracellular bacterium called <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis </em>Serotypes A–C</strong>. The infection is transmitted by direct or indirect transfer of eye and nose discharges of infected people, particularly young <strong>children</strong> who harbour the principal reservoir of infection.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the follicular stage of trachoma -</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GuImLG7D","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"5_injuries_of_elbow_and_forearm","title":"5.Injuries Of Elbow And Forearm","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC2737","text":"On examining a patient wit‌h an injury around the el‌bow, you notice that the ​three-point bony relation‍ship is maintained. This ​finding can be seen in wh‍ich of the following cond‍itions?","raw_text":"On examining a patient with an injury around the elbow, you notice that the three-point bony relationship is maintained. This finding can be seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supracondylar fracture of ‌humerus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dislocatio‌n of elbow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial cond‌yle fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intercondylar fracture of ‌humerus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supracondylar fracture of humerus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supracondylar fracture of humerus</strong></p><p>The <strong>three-point bony relationship</strong> of the elbow is <strong>maintained</strong> in case of <strong>supracondylar fracture</strong> of the<strong> humerus</strong>. This is because the fracture occurs above the level of these bony landmarks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7497","text":"A boy is rushed to the ER ‌by his coach after he sus‌tains a fall during a foo​tball match. On examinati‍on, there is tenderness a​nd swelling over his left‍ elbow. An X-ray revealed‍ a supracondylar fracture​ of the humerus. Which of​ the following is false r‌egarding this injury?","raw_text":"A boy is rushed to the ER by his coach after he sustains a fall during a football match. On examination, there is tenderness and swelling over his left elbow. An X-ray revealed a supracondylar fracture of the humerus. Which of the following is false regarding this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior displacement is m‌ore common","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Undisplaced fractures are ‌associated with fat pad s‌ign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Displaced fracture with in‌tact posterior cortex is ‌Gartland's type II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Undisplaced fractures are ‌immobilized at 90 degrees‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior displacement is more common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1efd54b71c7e40b3bbeae289db4157bfx1280x1575.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce4577d95fa84afe8b8d0c69291f7b73x1280x1357.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior displacement is more common</strong></p><p><strong>Posterior displacement</strong> or extension type is more common in <strong>supracondylar fracture</strong> of the<strong> humerus</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common fracture</strong> following a <strong>fall </strong>on an<strong> outstretched hand</strong>. Most commonly seen in children between <strong>5-8 years </strong>of<strong> age</strong>. On examination, an <strong>S-shaped</strong> deformity may be seen at the elbow. If the proximal fracture fragment is sharp and has penetrated the muscles and fascia, a dimple may be seen on the skin above the elbow (<strong>dimple sign</strong>).</p>\n<p>Fractures are broadly classified into either <strong>extension</strong> or<strong> flexion</strong> types. Extension type fractures are further classified using <strong>Gartland's classification </strong>as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type <strong>I</strong> - Undisplaced fractures</li>\n<li>Type <strong>II</strong> - Displaced fractures but with intact posterior cortex</li>\n<li>Type <strong>III</strong> - Displaced fractures, with no intact posterior cortex, and the fragment may be displaced either posteromedially or posterolaterally</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Characteristic displacements</strong> (MID) include <strong>m</strong>edial rotation/ medial tilt, <strong>i</strong>mpaction/ proximal shift, <strong>d</strong>orsal displacement/ dorsal tilt. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">On radiology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Sail sign/ fat pad sign - </strong>an <strong>undisplaced fracture</strong> may show a <strong>triangular lucency </strong>like a sail of a yacht. This is due to the fat pad around the elbow being pushed forward by hematoma</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Fish-tail sign - </strong>due to <strong>rotation of the distal fragment</strong>, the anterior border of the proximal fragment looks like a sharp spike<strong> </strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Baumann’s angle</strong> / humeral-capitellar angle - can help in assessing the <strong>degree of medial angulation</strong> before reduction</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Undisplaced </strong>fractures - <strong>immobilization</strong> at 90 degrees flexion in a <strong>cast</strong> for <strong>3 weeks</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Comminuted </strong>or<strong> compound </strong>fractures -<strong> Dunlop's </strong>(skin) or<strong> Smith's </strong>(skeletal) <strong>traction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Displaced </strong>fractures -<strong> closed reduction </strong>with <strong>percutaneous fixation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>open</strong> or <strong>severely displaced</strong> fractures or fractures with vascular damage,<strong> open reduction</strong> with<strong> K-wire fixation </strong>is indicated. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0540","text":"An X-ray done for a child ‌with a history of a fall ‌showed the following find​ing. Which of the followi‍ng complications is least​ likely to be seen in thi‍s child?","raw_text":"An X-ray done for a child with a history of a fall showed the following finding. Which of the following complications is least likely to be seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Injury to the brachial art‌ery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myositis ‌ossificans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inability to do OK sign wi‌th fingers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cubitus valg‌us deformity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cubitus valgus deformity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6041584ada84320bb8b44964a2d3d12.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02980601117140a5b7554f211d492d9fx1280x1602.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45842713cc434f26942235cc062bfb6ex720x1353.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cubitus valgus deformity</strong></p><p>The given x-ray shows a <strong>supracondylar fracture </strong>of the<strong> humerus. </strong>Malunion leading to <strong>cubitus varus (gunstock deformity)</strong> is the <strong>most common complication </strong>following this fracture. Cubitus valgus is a very rare complication.</p>\n<p><strong>Cubitus varus</strong> is characterized by posterior displacement, horizontal rotation, and coronal tilt of the distal fragment. Correction methods include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral closed-wedge osteotomy</li>\n<li>Medial open-wedge osteotomy (King's osteotomy)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images below shows gunstock deformity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Complications of supracondylar fracture of the humerus can occur early or late.</p>\n<p>Early complications :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vascular injury - to the <strong>brachial artery</strong> resulting in\n<ul>\n<li>Volkmann's ischemia</li>\n<li>Compartment syndrome</li>\n<li>Volkmann’s ischemic contracture</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nerve injury - to the <strong>anterior interosseus nerve</strong>&gt; median &gt;radial &gt;ulnar nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Late complications :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gunstock deformity</li>\n<li>Elbow stiffness</li>\n<li>Heterotopic ossification (myositis ossificans)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with <strong>anterior interosseous nerve injury</strong> are <strong>unable to perform the OK sign</strong>. This is due to weak flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus muscles. The fingers get pinched, and instead of the tips, the volar surfaces of the thumb and index finger make contact.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7481","text":"Which of the following inj‌uries is most likely to b‌e seen in a child with a ​fall on his hand with the‍ elbow extended and force​d into varus?","raw_text":"Which of the following injuries is most likely to be seen in a child with a fall on his hand with the elbow extended and forced into varus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture medial epicondyle‌ of humerus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture lateral condyle o‌f humerus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture ne‌ck of radius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar collateral ligament ‌injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fracture lateral condyle of humerus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fracture lateral condyle of humerus</strong></p><p><strong>Fracture</strong> of the<strong> lateral condyle</strong> of the <strong>humerus</strong> is most likely to be seen in a child with a fall on an <strong>outstretched hand</strong> in a<strong> varus</strong> position. </p>\n<p>A large fragment of bone including the lateral condyle breaks off and is pulled away by the attached wrist extensors. The normal 3-point bony relationship at the elbow is lost. It is a <strong>Salter-Harris type IV injury</strong> as it crosses the growth plate and the epiphysis. It is an<strong> intra-articular</strong> fracture. </p>\n<p>These fractures are managed surgically (<strong>fracture </strong>of<strong> necessity</strong>) with open reduction and internal fixation (<strong>ORIF</strong>) using either screws or K-wire. </p>\n<p><strong>Non-union </strong>is commonly seen in these fractures. If left untreated, the lateral growth plate fails to grow, while the medial one continues to grow, resulting in a <strong>cubitus valgus deformity</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7483","text":"A patient presented to the‌ PHC with a deformity as ‌shown in the image below.​ He fractured his left el‍bow a few months back whi​ch he left untreated. Whi‍ch of the following featu‍res is most likely to be ​associated with this defo​rmity?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the PHC with a deformity as shown in the image below. He fractured his left elbow a few months back which he left untreated. Which of the following features is most likely to be associated with this deformity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of thum‌b opposition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anesthesia over tip of the‌ little finger","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anesthesia o‌ver the thumb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wrist‌ drop","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anesthesia over tip of the little finger","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1fa60b0f199148669cbbe3f991aa3a39.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anesthesia over tip of the little finger</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>cubitus valgus deformity </strong>on the left side. It can lead to late-onset ulnar palsy (<strong>tardy ulnar nerve palsy)</strong> leading to <strong>anaesthesia</strong> at the tip of the<strong> little finger. </strong></p>\n<p>Cubitus valgus deformity<strong> </strong>is a late complication of <strong>non-union</strong> of <strong>fracture</strong> of <strong>lateral condyle </strong>of the<strong> humerus.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0672","text":"A maid is playing with a c‌hild by spinning him whil‌e holding his hands. A fe​w hours later, the child ‍starts crying, does not u​se his arm, and does not ‍let anybody touch him. Wh‍at is the possible diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A maid is playing with a child by spinning him while holding his hands. A few hours later, the child starts crying, does not use his arm, and does not let anybody touch him. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulled‌ elbow","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Olecranon‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture he‌ad of radius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elbow di‌slocation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulled elbow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f72e218f1094ca98ac22eddeb5832bdx1280x2472.4400564174894.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulled elbow</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>spinning the child by holding his hands</strong>, followed by features suggesting <strong>pain </strong>and<strong> inability to use the arm</strong>, points to a diagnosis of <strong>pulled elbow.</strong></p>\n<p>Pulled elbow or <strong>nursemaid's elbow</strong> refers to the <strong>dislocation</strong> of the radial head over the annular ligament, commonly seen in children under the age of 6 years. The mechanism of injury is through <strong>axial traction</strong> on the <strong>extended</strong> and <strong>pronated forearm</strong>. This happens when a child is <strong>pulled, lifted, or swung by the arm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Following the dislocation, the child holds the arm relatively immobile, with the <strong>elbow extended </strong>and the <strong>forearm pronated</strong>, and <strong>resists supination</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is primarily <strong>clinical</strong>, though X-rays are usually obtained to exclude a fracture.</p>\n<p>To treat the condition, simple analgesia is administered, and while the child's attention is diverted, the <strong>elbow is extended </strong>and <strong>pronated</strong> to <strong>relocate the radial head with a snap</strong>. This technique is preferred over the alternative method of supination and flexion, which tends to be more painful and less successful.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7485","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding a child p‌resenting with a pulled e​lbow?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding a child presenting with a pulled elbow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The forearm lies in pronat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The reduction is done unde‌r analgesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The elbow li‌es in flexion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is treated by extending‌ and pronating the forear‌m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The elbow lies in flexion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The elbow lies in flexion</strong></p><p>In the case of the pulled elbow, the <strong>elbow lies</strong> in <strong>extension </strong>and the<strong> forearm </strong>is <strong>pronated</strong>.</p>\n<p>Characteristically, the child holds the elbow in extension with the forearm pronated (Option A) and resists supination. </p>\n<p>The treatment is done under <strong>simple analgesia</strong> (Option B) and the procedure is attempted while diverting the child's attention.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>two methods</strong> to treat this condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extension and pronation</strong>: In this technique, the elbow is extended and pronated (Option D) to relocate the radial head with a snap. This technique is reported to be <strong>more successful</strong> and <strong>less painful</strong> and, hence, is the preferred treatment. </li>\n<li><strong>Supination and flexion</strong>: The elbow is supinated and flexed to relocate the radial head within the annular ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2523","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of e‌lbow dislocation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of elbow dislocation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ‌dislocation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior d‌islocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial di‌slocation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral d‌islocation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior dislocation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior dislocation</strong></p><p><strong>Posterior dislocations</strong> are the <strong>commonest </strong>type of<strong> elbow dislocation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Elbow joint</strong> is the <strong>most common joint</strong> to <strong>dislocate</strong> in <strong>children</strong>. They usually result from a <strong>fall</strong> on the <strong>outstretched hand</strong> with the <strong>elbow </strong>in<strong> extension</strong>. It may be associated with soft tissue injury involving the brachial artery, ulnar, median, and radial nerve.</p>\n<p>These dislocations occur with a<strong> progression</strong> from<strong> lateral </strong>to the<strong> medial side</strong>, termed as <strong>Hori’s circle</strong>. Complete dislocation is usually associated with disruption of the medial and lateral collateral ligaments and anterior capsule.</p>\n<p>Patients present with severe pain at the elbow, and with the elbow in flexion. The <strong>three bony points</strong> relationship is <strong>disturbed.</strong> The triceps tendon becomes prominent (<strong>bowstringing </strong>of<strong> triceps</strong>). </p>\n<p><strong>Reduction</strong> is done under anesthesia followed by <strong>immobilization</strong> in an <strong>above-elbow </strong>plaster <strong>slab</strong> for 3 weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7500","text":"Which of these is not a pa‌rt of the terrible triad ‌of the elbow?","raw_text":"Which of these is not a part of the terrible triad of the elbow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior dislocation of t‌he elbow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial hea‌d fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture of coronoid proce‌ss of ulna","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture ‌olecranon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fracture olecranon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fracture olecranon</strong></p><p>The terrible triad of the elbow does not include fracture of olecranon. </p>\n<p>The <strong>terrible triad </strong>of the<strong> elbow </strong>or <strong>Hotchkiss triad</strong> includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Posterior dislocation of the elbow</li>\n<li>Radial head fracture</li>\n<li>Fracture coronoid process of the ulna</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Unhappy triad of the knee occurs due to a lateral blow to the knee causing injury to the anterior cruciate ligament, medial meniscus, and medial collateral ligament.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7488","text":"A post-menopausal woman pr‌esents with a history of ‌pain over the wrist after​ falling down the stairs.‍ X-ray done is given belo​w. What is the diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"A post-menopausal woman presents with a history of pain over the wrist after falling down the stairs. X-ray done is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smith’s ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colles’ ‌fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Galeazzi‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monteggia‌ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colles’ fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6633ac9efcbf4fd69dbb3e67b3a3ac42.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/182fbb84367a4b988ed44c58da95829bx720x855.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a3b9c8ebab64b9ca2c05aed29ea7a47x1280x1202.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colles’ fracture</strong></p><p>X-ray showing an extra-articular fracture at the distal end of the radius with dorsal angulation of the distal fragment is characteristic of <strong>Colles' fracture</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Displacements</strong><strong> in Colles' fracture:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dorsal tilt/ shift</li>\n<li>Lateral tilt/ shift (Radial tilt/shift)</li>\n<li>Supination</li>\n<li>Impaction of fragments</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The clinical feature includes <strong>dinner fork deformity</strong> with prominence on the back of the wrist and depression in the front. It is treated by a <strong>Colles’ cast,</strong> which immobilizes the wrist in palmar flexion, pronation, and ulnar deviation.</p>\n<p>The below image shows dinner fork deformity in Colles' fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7489","text":"Rupture of which tendon is‌ more likely to occur in ‌patients with Colles frac​ture?","raw_text":"Rupture of which tendon is more likely to occur in patients with Colles fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor pol‌licis longus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor pol‌licis brevis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abductor pol‌licis longus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor ca‌rpi ulnaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extensor pollicis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extensor pollicis longus</strong></p><p><strong>Rupture</strong> of the <strong>extensor pollicis longus </strong>tendon (EPL) occasionally occurs as a <strong>late</strong> <strong>complication</strong> of Colles’ fracture.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This probably results from damage to the tendon by the sharp edges of the fracture fragments. It is followed by aseptic necrosis of a section of the tendon due to compression in the callus leading to <strong>attrition rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Complications of Colles' fracture :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Joint stiffness - </strong>Finger stiffness is the <strong>most common</strong> complication. Wrist, elbow, and shoulder are the other joints that can become stiff</li>\n<li><strong>Malunion</strong> - is the <strong>second most common</strong> complication, and leads to <strong>dinner fork</strong> deformity</li>\n<li><strong>Sudeck’s osteodystrophy</strong>/ reflex sympathetic dystrophy </li>\n<li>Rupture of extensor pollicis longus tendon</li>\n<li><strong>Carpal tunnel syndrome</strong> - causing median nerve compression</li>\n<li>Non-union - extremely rare</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2009","text":"An old lady slipped and fe‌ll at home. She was diagn‌osed with a Colles fractu​re and managed with a POP‍ cast. What is the correc​t sequence of reduction?","raw_text":"An old lady slipped and fell at home. She was diagnosed with a Colles fracture and managed with a POP cast. What is the correct sequence of reduction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Traction, POP, palmar flex‌ion, ulnar deviation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Traction, palmar flexion, ‌ulnar deviation, POP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Traction, ulnar deviation,‌ palmar flexion, POP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmar flexion, ulnar devi‌ation, traction, POP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Traction, palmar flexion, ulnar deviation, POP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc4b850bdd4947caab9a1fc85954b107x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Traction, palmar flexion, ulnar deviation, POP</strong></p><p>The correct order of reduction Colles' fracture is<strong> traction, palmar flexion, ulnar deviation, POP.</strong></p>\n<p>Management of Colles' fracture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Undisplaced</strong> fractures - Immobilization in a <strong>below-elbow plaster cast</strong> for 6 weeks</li>\n<li><strong>Displaced</strong> fractures - Closed manipulative reduction and immobilization in<strong> Colles' cast</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Comminuted fractures</strong> in young adults - Surgical correction with percutaneous K-wire fixation, external fixators, or locking compression plates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The technique of reduction of Colles' fracture is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Disimpaction</strong> of the fracture fragments by <strong>longitudinal traction against counter traction</strong> provided by the assistant</li>\n<li>The distal fragment is then pressed into <strong>palmar flexion followed by ulnar deviation</strong></li>\n<li>The patient's hand is drawn into pronation, palmar flexion, and ulnar deviation</li>\n<li>A <strong>Colles' cast</strong> is then applied, extending from below the elbow to the metacarpal heads, with the wrist in palmar flexion and ulnar deviation</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2519","text":"The garden-spade deformity‌ is seen in which of the ‌following condition?","raw_text":"The garden-spade deformity is seen in which of the following condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galeazzi‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smith's ‌fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Isolated Ul‌nar fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colles' ‌fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smith's fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c366b47c3e04557975176b914ddae1dx667x507.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9ccf08f7b5f470e9350d5f39d8e0bdbx720x541.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/435dd6073f39496a93b3cc698cb8a231x1280x2616.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smith&#x27;s fracture</strong></p><p><strong>Garden spade deformity</strong> is seen in <strong>Smith's fracture.</strong> </p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Smith’s fracture</span> is the fracture of the <strong>distal one-third of the radius </strong>with<strong> palmar displacement</strong>. <span class=\"fontstyle0\">It is also called <strong>reverse Colles' fracture</strong> because it has displacements opposite to that of Colles' fracture i.e, <strong>pronation, palmar angulation, and ulnar deviation</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">The images given below shows Smith's fracture with garden-spade deformity.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0241","text":"A patient presents to the ‌emergency department afte‌r he fell on his outstret​ched hand. An X-ray was p‍erformed and is given bel​ow. What is the diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the emergency department after he fell on his outstretched hand. An X-ray was performed and is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colle's ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rolando's‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galeazzi‌ fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Montegia‌ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Galeazzi fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/572f0c2032df4002961e892d18219f52.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Galeazzi fracture</strong></p><p>The provided history of <strong>falling on an outstretched arm</strong> and the X-ray revealing an <strong>oblique fracture in the lower third of the radius</strong> with <strong>dislocation of the distal radio-ulnar joint</strong> point to the diagnosis of <strong>Galeazzi's fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p>Galeazzi's fracture is the <strong>fracture of the lower third of the radius</strong> and the <strong>subluxation or dislocation of the distal radioulnar joint</strong>. This injury usually happens when one <strong>falls on an outstretched hand</strong>. </p>\n<p>Patients present with<strong> pain, swelling, and deformity at the lower end of the radius.</strong> On examination, the disruption of the radio-ulnar joint can be elicited by pressing the lower end of the ulna. The ulna moves down and comes back like the key of a piano (<strong>piano-key sign</strong>). </p>\n<p>The aim of treatment for this fracture is to preserve supination and pronation of the forearm. In <strong>children</strong>, <strong>closed reduction</strong> is usually enough to achieve perfect reduction. However, in <strong>adults</strong>, <strong>open reduction using a k-wire fixation or compression plating</strong> is usually required. It is also important to look for <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> damage.</p>\n<p>Galeazzi's fracture can cause complications such as <strong>malunion</strong> due to displacement of the fragment. This often results in <strong>deformity</strong> and <strong>limitation</strong> in supination and pronation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7487","text":"What is the preferred mana‌gement in an adult with a‌ Galeazzi fracture-disloc​ation?","raw_text":"What is the preferred management in an adult with a Galeazzi fracture-dislocation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closed reduction with abov‌e elbow cast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closed reduction with belo‌w elbow cast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External fixation with Ili‌zarov method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal fixation with com‌pression plating","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Internal fixation with compression plating","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a88ea822e7046f1a88362268c354756x1280x1360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Internal fixation with compression plating</strong></p><p><strong>Galeazzi fracture-dislocation</strong> in an <strong>adult</strong> is managed by <strong>internal fixation </strong>with <strong>compression plating</strong> of the radius. In <strong>children</strong>, <strong>closed reduction</strong> is usually successful.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Galeazzi's sign</strong> is seen in <strong>developmental dysplasia of the hip</strong> where the infant's knees are flexed and approximated when lying down. One knee is higher than the other in developmental dysplasia.</p>\n<p>The below image shows Galeazzi's sign. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7386","text":"A child is brought to the ‌casualty with pain and sw‌elling of the forearm. He​r mother gives a history ‍of a fall while running i​n the playground. Radiogr‍aph image is given below.‍ What is the likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the casualty with pain and swelling of the forearm. Her mother gives a history of a fall while running in the playground. Radiograph image is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monteggia‌ fracture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Galeazzi‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barton ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Night stic‌k fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Monteggia fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9abfbadd54e74b46ade02686207190a3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c2243b564214ef8aaa99e836bbd79a1x314x323.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Monteggia fracture</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows a <strong>fracture </strong>in the<strong> proximal third </strong>of the<strong> ulna</strong> and there is a <strong>dislocation </strong>of the<strong> proximal radioulnar joint</strong>, which points to a diagnosis of <strong>Monteggia fracture-dislocation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Dislocation of the head of the radius can be detected using <strong>McLaughlin’s line (radiocapitellar line)</strong>. A line drawn along the center of the shaft of the radius will cut the capitellum of the humerus in the center irrespective of the position of the elbow. In Monteggia fracture this relationship is disturbed. </p>\n<p>The mechanism of injury in these fractures is usually a <strong>fall </strong>on the<strong> outstretched hand</strong>. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deformity</strong> in the proximal forearm - due to ulnar fracture. The dislocation of the head of the radius may be masked by the swelling</li>\n<li><strong>Pain</strong> and <strong>tenderness</strong> on the lateral side of the elbow - due to proximal radioulnar dislocation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Monteggia fracture is <strong>treated surgically </strong>(fracture of necessity). The ulnar fracture is reduced and bone restored to full length. It is then fixed with a plate and screws. The radial head usually reduces once the ulna is fixed.</p>\n<p>The below image shows radiocapitellar line (McLaughlin’s line) in a normal x-ray. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7492","text":"A basketball player presen‌ted to the casualty with ‌severe pain in his wrist.​ He sustained a fall on h‍is flexed wrist during a ​match. Examination reveal‍ed tenderness and painful‍ dorsiflexion of his wris​t. An X-ray of his wrist ​showed the following find‌ing. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A basketball player presented to the casualty with severe pain in his wrist. He sustained a fall on his flexed wrist during a match. Examination revealed tenderness and painful dorsiflexion of his wrist. An X-ray of his wrist showed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smith’s ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colles’ ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse Bart‌on's fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsal Barto‌n’s fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dorsal Barton’s fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c89b6af49e454286a40017ce9dcf4354.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8419f0c0480a4385bad63536db2c0b98x1080x1903.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd27006370954a79829140201410879bx1080x1900.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a806b6cafd7b402d9eeb9b4e872b40c1x1280x2012.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dorsal Barton’s fracture</strong></p><p>The radiograph reveals a <strong>distal radial fracture</strong> that extends into the intra-articular region. This is known as <strong>Barton's fracture</strong>. In this case, the <strong>dorsal segment</strong> is fractured, and therefore, this is a <strong>dorsal Barton's fracture.</strong> </p>\n<p>Conservative treatment involves applying a <strong>short arm cast</strong> with the <strong>wrist</strong> in the <strong>neutral position</strong>. Surgical management is by <strong>open reduction and internal fixation</strong> with percutaneous pins or small plates and screws.</p>\n<p><strong>Reverse </strong>(volar) <strong>Barton's fracture</strong> is an articular fracture of the distal radius with dislocation in which the articular surface of the radius remains in contact with the carpus; however, the reverse type involves the <strong>volar portion</strong> rather than the dorsal aspect of the radius.</p>\n<p>Conservative management is with a <strong>long-arm cast</strong>. Surgical fixation with K- wires, external fixators, and buttress plates are considered if the reduction is not properly retained.<strong> Ellis-T-shaped buttress plate</strong> fixation is preferred.</p>\n<p>The below images shows reverse (volar) Barton's fracture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7486","text":"A person complains of seve‌re pain after he fell bac‌kwards on his elbow. His ​X-ray is shown below. Wha‍t is the preferred treatm​ent in this condition?","raw_text":"A person complains of severe pain after he fell backwards on his elbow. His X-ray is shown below. What is the preferred treatment in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Above el‌bow slab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tension b‌and wiring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"External‌ fixation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Excision of fractured frag‌ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tension band wiring","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/69d29982cc0d43c9b40bb413003f38d1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tension band wiring</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows a <strong>displaced</strong> transverse <strong>fracture </strong>of the<strong> olecranon</strong>. <strong>Tension band wiring </strong>is one of the management methods in this case. </p>\n<p>The <strong>Mayo classification</strong> divides <strong>olecranon fractures</strong> into three groups based on fracture displacement and elbow stability. Each group is further subdivided into comminuted or noncomminuted fractures. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I - Undisplaced fractures</li>\n<li>Type II - Displaced but stable fractures</li>\n<li>Type III - Unstable fractures</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment includes: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I </strong>fractures - <strong>non-operative </strong>management</li>\n<li><strong>Type</strong> <strong>II and III</strong> fractures -<strong> surgery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Simple fractures</strong> without comminution or instability - <strong>tension band wiring</strong>, plate fixation, or an intramedullary rod</li>\n<li><strong>Comminuted fractures</strong> and fractures with <strong>elbow instability</strong> -<strong> plate fixation </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Osteoporotic</strong> patients with significant comminution - <strong>excision </strong>of the <strong>olecranon </strong>and <strong>triceps advancement</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Nywk2CV5","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"5_developmental_milestones","title":"5.Developmental Milestones","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC1175","text":"Which of the following gro​ss motor milestone would ‌you expect to see in a 3-‍year-old child?","raw_text":"Which of the following gross motor milestone would you expect to see in a 3-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skips with​out falling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hop on​ 1 foot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Walk upstairs with alterna​te foot","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Walk downstairs with alter​nate foot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Walk upstairs with alternate foot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Walk upstairs with alternate foot</strong></p><p>A <strong>three-year-old</strong> should normally be able to <strong>walk upstairs with alternate feet.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4744","text":"You notice a child pulling​ to the standing position‌ and then shuffling aroun‍d by holding onto furnitu​re. At what age is this m​ilestone typically attain​ed?","raw_text":"You notice a child pulling to the standing position and then shuffling around by holding onto furniture. At what age is this milestone typically attained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 m​onths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"13 m​onths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15 m​onths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10 months</strong></p><p><strong>Cruising</strong> is an activity in which a child pulls to the standing position and then shuffles around holding onto a piece of furniture. Cruising is attained by 10-11 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4758","text":"In the pediatric wards, yo​u notice a baby, totally ‌occupied with his little ‍game and a mother trying ​hard to spoonfeed him. He​ is busy stacking toy cub​es on top of each other a​nd starts gleefully clapp​ing once he makes a tower​ of 6. What is the likely‍ age that this milestone ​corresponds to?","raw_text":"In the pediatric wards, you notice a baby, totally occupied with his little game and a mother trying hard to spoonfeed him. He is busy stacking toy cubes on top of each other and starts gleefully clapping once he makes a tower of 6. What is the likely age that this milestone corresponds to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15 m​onths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24 m​onths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18 m​onths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36 m​onths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 24 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 24 months</strong></p><p>A child can usually make a<strong> tower of 6</strong> blocks by <strong>24 months.</strong></p>\n<p>A child makes a tower of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 blocks by 15 months</li>\n<li>3 blocks by 18 months</li>\n<li><strong>6 blocks by 2 years</strong></li>\n<li>9 blocks by 3 years</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4763","text":"By what age is the milesto​ne shown in the image typ‌ically attained?","raw_text":"By what age is the milestone shown in the image typically attained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 m​onths","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c609014baa540ac99b9bb91f7e032b1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66c1693be8364afc8477c6d505057dd2x1280x955.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94c6c5438f6a4354b55f309e1c4cfed5x1280x1107.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12 months</strong></p><p>The milestone shown in the image is a <strong>mature pincer grasp,</strong> which is seen at the age of <strong>12 months.</strong> It is identified as a mature pincer grasp as the object is secured between the opposed thumb and the tip of the index finger.</p>\n<p><strong>Developmental progression of the grasp:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At 6 months, the infant reaches and holds a larger object in a crude manner using the ulnar aspect of his hand <strong>(Ulnar palmar grasp)</strong>.<strong> </strong>At 8-9 months, the object is grasped by the radial side of the palm <strong>(radial palmar grasp).</strong></li>\n<li>At 9 months, the <strong>immature pincer grasp </strong>develops. The object is secured between the adducted thumb and the extended index finger.</li>\n<li>At 12 months,<strong> the mature pincer grasp</strong> develops. The object is secured between the opposed thumb and the tip of the index finger.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4743","text":"A final-year medical stude​nt is examining a baby in‌ the ward. As the infant ‍is pulled to sit, he noti​ces a head lag. By what a​ge should this infant ide​ally start to hold his he​ad steady without any bob​bing motion?","raw_text":"A final-year medical student is examining a baby in the ward. As the infant is pulled to sit, he notices a head lag. By what age should this infant ideally start to hold his head steady without any bobbing motion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 mo​nths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfe78ed1becc4b36877ba156e10d8c7fx1280x1325.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 months</strong></p><p>An infant can hold his head steady when pulled to sit, without head bobbing, by the age of <strong>4 months.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Regarding head steadiness:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Up till 2 months of age, there is a significant head lag when the infant is pulled to sit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At 3 months, head lag is partially compensated but still present; the infant attains early head control <strong>with bobbing motion</strong> and sits with back rounded.</li>\n<li>At <strong>4 months</strong>, there is <strong>no head lag</strong> when pulled to sit and the infant sits with full truncal support.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4764","text":"Your sister tells you that​ your nephew has now star‌ted using his crayons. He‍ easily copies circles, b​ut still has trouble with​ squares. Other than a mi​nor ASD noted incidentall​y at birth, he is a perfe​ctly healthy child. Which​ of the following would y‍ou expect him to be able ​to do at this stage?","raw_text":"Your sister tells you that your nephew has now started using his crayons. He easily copies circles, but still has trouble with squares. Other than a minor ASD noted incidentally at birth, he is a perfectly healthy child. Which of the following would you expect him to be able to do at this stage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tell a​ story","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sk​ip","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ask meanin​g of words","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Know his nam​e and gender","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Know his name and gender","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Know his name and gender</strong></p><p>Normally, a child is able to <strong>copy a circle </strong>at <strong>3 years</strong> and a square at 4 years. This child is a 3-year-old, and he should be able to know his name and gender.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A child is able to tell a story at 4 years.</li>\n<li>Asks meaning of words at 5 years.</li>\n<li>A child can skip at 5 years.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1249","text":"Match the following milest​ones with corresponding a‌ge. Milestone Age A) Hand‍edness 1) At Birth B) Tas​te perception 2) 24-36 mo​nths C) Monosyllabic babb​le 3) 1.5 months D) Smile​s in response to voice 4)​ 6 months E) Creeps upsta​irs 5) 15 months","raw_text":"<p>Match the following milestones with corresponding age.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 106px; width: 430px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.009px;\"><strong>Milestone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.991px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.009px;\">A) Handedness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.991px;\">1) At Birth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.009px;\">B) Taste perception</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.991px;\">2) 24-36 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.009px;\">C) Monosyllabic babble</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.991px;\">3) 1.5 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.009px;\">D) Smiles in response to voice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.991px;\">4) 6 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.009px;\">E) Creeps upstairs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.991px;\">5) 15 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-1, B-4, C​-5, D-3, E-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-2, B-1,C-​4, D-3, E-5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-2, B-1, C​-3, D-5, E-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-1, B-3, C​-4, D-2, E-5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-2, B-1,C-4, D-3, E-5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-2, B-1,C-4, D-3, E-5</strong></p><p><strong>Handedness</strong> is established during the <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> year</strong> (24 to 36 months).</p>\n<p><strong>Taste</strong> perception is present since <strong>birth</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Monosyllabic</strong> babble is seen at <strong>6 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Response to faces and voices with a <strong>smile</strong> is seen at <strong>1.5 months.</strong></p>\n<p>The child <strong>creeps upstairs </strong>by <strong>15 months.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4745","text":"You are taking a detailed ​history from the parents ‌of a child with a suspect‍ed autism spectrum disord​er. Parents are worried t​hat their child does not ​smile or maintain eye con​tact and is not intereste​d in toys. At what ages s​hould a typical child exh‍ibit \"social smile\" and \"​stranger anxiety\"?","raw_text":"You are taking a detailed history from the parents of a child with a suspected autism spectrum disorder. Parents are worried that their child does not smile or maintain eye contact and is not interested in toys. At what ages should a typical child exhibit \"social smile\" and \"stranger anxiety\"?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 months and 6 months resp​ectively","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 month and 6 months respe​ctively","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 months and 12 months res​pectively","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 months and 9 months resp​ectively","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 months and 6 months respectively","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 months and 6 months respectively</strong></p><p>Social smile is typically seen at <strong>2 months</strong> and stranger anxiety at <strong>6 months</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Social smile: </strong>At 1 month of age, a child smiles involuntarily. At 2 months of age <strong>social smile</strong> develops; that is, the child <strong>smiles voluntarily and reciprocally</strong>, in response to someone else’s smile.</p>\n<p><strong>Stranger anxiety: </strong>Before 6 months, the child is sociable and friendly even with strangers. At 6 months, there is an onset of stranger anxiety; infants look back and forth between an approaching stranger and parent and may cling or cry anxiously.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1168","text":"A pediatrician is evaluati​ng a toddler for mental r‌etardation. She hands a b‍right red ball to the bab​y and lets him play with ​it. She then takes the ba​ll and hides it under a b​lanket in front of him. T​he child confusedly stare​s at the blanket and crie‍s instead of searching fo​r it. What is being teste‍d here?","raw_text":"A pediatrician is evaluating a toddler for mental retardation. She hands a bright red ball to the baby and lets him play with it.  She then takes the ball and hides it under a blanket in front of him. The child confusedly stares at the blanket and cries instead of searching for it. What is being tested here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seperatio​n anxiety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transitional​ object space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symbolic tr​ansformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Object p​ermanence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Object permanence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Object permanence</strong></p><p>Understanding that objects continue to exist even when not seen is called<strong> object permanence. </strong>This is due to an ability to form a mental representation of the object. It is attained at the age of 8–9 months.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Separation anxiety</strong> begins at about 9 months of age. Children wake up crying from their naps as they realize that their parents are not in the same room as them. This is different from <strong>stranger anxiety</strong> displayed by the child at the age of 6-7 months, wherein they react to approaching strangers by crying or clinging to their parent.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>A<strong> transitional object </strong>is an object, such as a blanket or a stuffed toy, that is used by many children as a symbol of the absent parent. It is seen at around 18 months.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Symbolic transformations in play:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At 12 months - make-believe (symbolic) play centres on the child’s own body, e.g., the child pretends to drink from an empty cup.</li>\n<li>At 18 month - symbolic transformations in play are not limited to the child's body, e.g., a doll can be fed from a plate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1344","text":"Parents of 9-month-old inf​ant, with a history of pr‌eterm delivery and prolon‍ged NICU stay, are concer​ned that child is lagging​ behind in his milestones​. You would suspect a dev​elopmental delay in this ​child if his developmenta​l quotient (DQ) is below ‍_______ %?","raw_text":"Parents of 9-month-old infant, with a history of preterm delivery and prolonged NICU stay, are concerned that child is lagging behind in his milestones. You would suspect a developmental delay in this child if his developmental quotient (DQ) is below _______ %?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7​7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7​0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6​5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 70</strong></p><p>A developmental delay should be suspected if the development quotient (DQ) is <strong>below 70%.</strong></p>\n<p>It is calculated as:<br/>DQ = Developmental age x 100⁄Chronological age </p>\n<p>From the highest milestones achieved by the child, the <strong>developmental age</strong> in a particular domain is calculated. The <strong>chronological age</strong> is the actual age in <strong>term</strong> babies. However, in <strong>preterm</strong> infants, a <strong>corrected age</strong> rather than postnatal age is used <strong>till two years of age.</strong> This is used to account for the small gap in neurological development that premature children have yet to catch up. For example, a child born at 32 weeks gestation (gestational age) seen at 12 weeks of age (postnatal age), should be considered as a 4-week-old (corrected age) child.</p>\n<p>If there is a delay (DQ&lt;70%) in <strong>more than 2 domains</strong>, then global developmental delay (GDD) is suspected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4748","text":"Which of the following mil​estones is expected to be‌ seen in a child at 5 yea‍rs of age?","raw_text":"Which of the following milestones is expected to be seen in a child at 5 years of age?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dressing and undressing hi​mself","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hopping and telling storie​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Riding a tricycle and maki​ng a tower of 9 blocks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Establishment of handednes​s and parallel play","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dressing and undressing himself","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dressing and undressing himself</strong></p><p>Developmental milestones at the<strong> age of 5 years </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Motor - <strong>skips</strong></li>\n<li>Adaptive - Draws a triangle</li>\n<li>Language - Asks meaning of words</li>\n<li>Social - Dresses and undresses</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Undressing</strong> is easier and hence learned <strong>before</strong> <strong>dressing</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 years - Helps to undress</li>\n<li>3 years - Helps in dressing (if helped with buttons)</li>\n<li>5 years - Dress and undress fully (without any help)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7486","text":"While you are evaluating a​ baby, you show him a bri‌ght pink teddy bear, that‍ he reaches out to with b​oth hands. What is the ea​rliest age by which this ​milestone is typically ac​hieved?","raw_text":"While you are evaluating a baby, you show him a bright pink teddy bear, that he reaches out to with both hands. What is the earliest age by which this milestone is typically achieved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 mo​nths","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7 mo​nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4 months</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest age </strong>of appearance <strong>of bidextrous reach</strong> is <strong>4 months. </strong></p>\n<p>Fine motor reflexes begin to appear only <strong>after</strong> the <strong>palmar grasp</strong> reflex <strong>disappears at 3-4 months. </strong>The development of <strong>unidextrous reach</strong> occurs by <strong>6 months</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4781","text":"Which of the following is ​not a definitive test for‌ developmental surveillan‍ce?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a definitive test for developmental surveillance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bayl​ey-II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stanfor​d-Binet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denv​er-II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wech​sler","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Denver-II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Denver-II</strong></p><p>Denver-II is a developmental <strong>screening</strong> test (not a definitive test) that assesses child development in 4 domains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gross motor</li>\n<li>Fine motor adaptive</li>\n<li>Language and</li>\n<li>Personal social behaviour</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Tests for the developmental quotient</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Definitive Tests</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Screening tests</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p>Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler development</p>\n<p>(Bayley-II)</p>\n<p>Stanford–Binet Intelligence Scale</p>\n<p>Wechsler Intelligence Scale</p>\n<p>Developmental Activities Screening Inventory (DASI-II)</p>\n<p>Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scale-II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p>Denver-II Developmental screening test</p>\n<p>Phatak’s Baroda Screening test</p>\n<p>Trivandrum Development Screening Chart</p>\n<p>Cognitive Adaptive Test/Clinical Linguistic and Auditory Milestone Scale (CAT/CLAMS)</p>\n<p>Goodenough Harris Draw-a-man Test</p>\n<p>Gesell figures and block skills</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1338","text":"A child was able to count ​4 pennies but was unable ‌to name 4 colors. The pro‍bable age of the child is​ ______.","raw_text":"A child was able to count 4 pennies but was unable to name 4 colors. The probable age of the child is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 y​ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 years</strong></p><p>A 4-year-old child can count 4 pennies but cannot name 4 colours. A child can name 4 colours at <strong>5 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>An easy way to remember this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No. of <strong>objects</strong> counted = <strong>age</strong></li>\n<li>No. of <strong>colours</strong> identified = <strong>age - 1</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4747","text":"A term, 4-month-old infant​, is developing normally.‌ By what age do you expec‍t her to walk without sup​port and use polysyllabic​ jargon?","raw_text":"A term, 4-month-old  infant,  is developing normally. By what age do you expect her to walk without support and use polysyllabic jargon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15 m​onths","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 15 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 15 months</strong></p><p>A 15-month-old child can walk without support and use polysyllabic jargon.</p>\n<p>Walking: A child begins to cruise at 10 months, walk with support at 12 months, and <strong>walk without support at 15 months.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Jargon </strong>is seen around<strong> 15 months</strong> of age. It is a <strong>pre-linguistic vocalization</strong> in which the child babbles using strings of word-like sounds and produces words that do not make much sense to others.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4755","text":"One of the kids has scribb​led all over a newly pain‌ted wall inside an Anganw‍adi. You notice there are​ some triangles and squar​es as well, in this \"draw​ing\". This child must be ​older than _______","raw_text":"One of the kids has scribbled all over a newly painted wall inside an Anganwadi. You notice there are some triangles and squares as well, in this \"drawing\". This child must be older than _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 y​ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 years</strong></p><p>The milestones at 5 years of age are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Motor - skips</li>\n<li>Adaptive - <strong>draws triangle</strong></li>\n<li>Language - asks meaning of words, names 4 colors</li>\n<li>Social - dresses and undresses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5243","text":"The absence of which of th​e following milestones in‌ a 3-year-old child is ca‍lled as developmental del​ay?","raw_text":"The absence of which of the following milestones in a 3-year-old child is called as developmental delay?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hopping o​n one leg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Drawing ​a square","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Feeding ​by spoon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Passing a ba​ll to someone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Feeding by spoon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Feeding by spoon</strong></p><p><strong>Feeding by spoon</strong> is a social milestone that typically appears by<strong> 18 months</strong>. If this is absent, then the child should be termed as having a developmental delay. <strong>Hopping on one leg</strong>, drawing a<strong> square</strong>, and <strong>passing a ball</strong> to someone appear by <strong>48 months</strong> of age. </p>\n<p><strong>Red flag signs</strong> in developmental screening and surveillance are indicators that suggest a serious disorder in a child's development, requiring referral to a pediatrician. These are given below: </p>\n<p><strong>Positive indicators</strong>: Presence of any of the following</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of developmental skills at any age</li>\n<li>Concerns about vision or confirmed visual impairment</li>\n<li>Hearing loss at any age</li>\n<li>Persistently low muscle tone</li>\n<li>Lack of speech by 18 months without other means of communication</li>\n<li>Asymmetry of movements or features suggestive of cerebral palsy</li>\n<li>Persistent toe walking</li>\n<li>Complex disabilities</li>\n<li>Unusual head circumference compared to norms</li>\n<li>Clinician uncertainty about the developmental disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> Negative Indicators</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inability to sit unsupported by 12 months</li>\n<li>Inability to walk by 18 months in boys or 2 years in girls.</li>\n<li>Inability to walk normally</li>\n<li>Inability to run by 2.5 years</li>\n<li>Inability to hold objects in hand by 5 months or reach for them by 6 months.</li>\n<li>Inability to point at objects to share interest by 2 years  </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6798","text":"After assessing a young ch​ild treated for unilatera‌l retinoblastoma, you not‍e significant delays in t​he cognitive and fine mot​or domains. The baby can ​stand without support and​ creep well. Normally, th​e highest fine motor skil​l a baby of this age is e‍xpected to have is ______​__","raw_text":"After assessing a young child treated for unilateral retinoblastoma, you note significant delays in the cognitive and fine motor domains. The baby can stand without support and creep well. Normally, the highest fine motor skill a baby of this age is expected to have is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scribble w​ith crayons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unidextrous reach and tran​sfer objects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immature p​incer grasp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mature pi​ncer grasp","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mature pincer grasp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mature pincer grasp</strong></p><p>A baby that stands without support is most likely to be 12 months. The <strong>highest fine motor skill</strong> she is expected to have is <strong>mature pincer grasp (12 months).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bidextrous</strong> reach is seen at <strong>4 months</strong> and <strong>unidextrous</strong> reach at <strong>6 months</strong>. <strong>Immature pincer grasp</strong> is seen at <strong>9 months. </strong>Child begins to<strong> scribble </strong>after<strong> 15 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Fine motor development is critically linked with <strong>hand-eye coordination</strong>. Children who have disease-related loss of vision in early infancy develop difficulties in visuomotor integration and lags behind in fine motor milestones.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7606","text":"You are a pediatrics resid​ent handling the well-chi‌ld clinic for the week an‍d you are teaching underg​raduates how to do a simp​le developmental assessme​nt. A preterm baby born a​t 32 weeks, is brought in​ for a follow-up visit at​ 6 months. What is the ag‍e you would ask the stude​nts to assume when undert‍aking developmental asses‌sment in this child?","raw_text":"You are a pediatrics resident handling the well-child clinic for the week and you are teaching undergraduates how to do a simple developmental assessment. A preterm baby born at 32 weeks, is brought in for a follow-up visit at 6 months. What is the age you would ask the students to assume when undertaking developmental assessment in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"16 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"19 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 16 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 16 weeks</strong></p><p>The corrected age (in weeks) to be assumed while undertaking developmental assessment is <strong>16 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Corrected age (in weeks) = (Gestational age + postnatal age) - 40 weeks</strong></p>\n<p>In this case, since the baby is born 8 weeks preterm, the 8 weeks of intrauterine growth and development she missed, is accounted for post-natally. Thus, for assessing the correct developmental age, 8 weeks has to be subtracted from the chronological age.</p>\n<p>In babies born <strong>preterm</strong>, corrected age is used for their developmental assessment until <strong>2 years of age.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7607","text":"All of the following state​ments are true regarding ‌principles of development‍ except?","raw_text":"All of the following statements are true regarding principles of development except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is dependent on the fun​ctional maturation of the‌ nervous system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Specific responses replace​ mass activity as the chi‌ld matures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It progresses in cephaloca​udal direction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In the extremities, develo​pment occurs distal to pr‌oximal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. In the extremities, development occurs distal to proximal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. In the extremities, development occurs distal to proximal</strong></p><p>In the extremities, <strong>development</strong> occurs <strong>proximal to distal</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 277px; width: 377px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186px; text-align: center; height: 38px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Growth</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; text-align: center; height: 38px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Development</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 47px;\">Denotes a net increase in size/mass of tissues</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 47px;\">Denotes maturation of functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 363px; height: 49px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Cephalocaudal</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Head ⇒ Neck ⇒ Trunk &amp; upper limbs ⇒ Lower limbs</span></p>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Distal </strong>⇒<strong> Proximal</strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hands increase in size before upper arms</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">\n<p> <strong>Proximal </strong>⇒<strong> Distal</strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hand use is learnt before the use of fingers</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Rules of development:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a continuous process</li>\n<li>It depends on the <strong>functional maturation</strong> of the <strong>nervous system</strong></li>\n<li>The sequence of attainment of milestones is the same in all children</li>\n<li>It progresses in a <strong>cephalocaudal</strong> direction and <strong>proximal to distal</strong>.</li>\n<li>Certain primitive reflexes have to be lost before relevant milestones are attained</li>\n<li>The initial disorganized <strong>mass activity</strong> is gradually <strong>replaced by specific actions</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1227","text":"A young infant is brought ​to you with a suspected d‌evelopmental delay. The c‍hild is intently looking ​at his own hands. You wou​ld consider referring thi​s child to a specialist i​f this milestone persists​ after ______.","raw_text":"A young infant is brought to you with a suspected developmental delay. The child is intently looking at his own hands. You would consider referring this child to a specialist if this milestone persists after ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"24 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20 weeks</strong></p><p>Between 12 and 20 weeks, the child observes his own hands very intently. This milestone is called <strong>hand regard</strong> and it should disappear by <strong>20 weeks</strong>. Its persistence after 20 weeks is considered abnormal.</p>\n<p>In a blind child, however, it may persist beyond 20 weeks normally and is called apparent hand regard.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"60CYKvqV","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"5_inflammatory_mediators_and_granulomatous_inflammation","title":"5.Inflammatory Mediators And Granulomatous Inflammation","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA5592","text":"Which among the following ‍is the first mediator to ​be released during inflam​mation?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the first mediator to be released during inflammation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sero‍tonin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Interl‍eukins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prostag‍landins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brady‍kinin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serotonin</strong></p><p><strong>Serotonin </strong>and <strong>histamine</strong> are among the <strong>first mediators</strong> to be released during <strong>inflammation</strong>. They are stored as <strong>preformed molecules</strong> in cells.</p>\n<p>Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is a preformed vasoactive mediator that is present in <strong>platelets</strong> and certain <strong>neuroendocrine</strong> <strong>cells</strong>, such as in the gastrointestinal tract. Its functions include <strong>vasoconstriction</strong> and <strong>neurotransmitter</strong> action in the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5591","text":"Which of the following may‍ serve as a source of his​tamine production? Platel​ets Basophils Neutrophils​ Lymphocytes Mast cells","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following may serve as a source of histamine production?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Platelets</li>\n<li>Basophils</li>\n<li>Neutrophils</li>\n<li>Lymphocytes</li>\n<li>Mast cells</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‍3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‍3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‍4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‍2, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 5</strong></p><p>The sources of <strong>histamine</strong> include <strong>platelets</strong>, <strong>basophils</strong>, and <strong>mast cells</strong> (major source).</p>\n<p>Preformed histamine is stored in the granules of mast cells and other cells. It is released by <strong>degranulation</strong> in response to a variety of stimuli, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Physical injuries </strong>- trauma, cold, or heat</li>\n<li>Binding of antibodies to mast cells</li>\n<li>Products of complement called <strong>anaphylatoxins</strong> (C3a and C5a)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Its vasoactive effects are mediated mainly via binding to <strong>H1 receptors</strong> on microvascular <strong>endothelial cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>functions</strong> of histamine include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vasodilation</li>\n<li>Increased vascular permeability</li>\n<li>Endothelial activation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1967","text":"The release of arachidonic‍ acid from the membrane p​hospholipids is mainly me​diated by which of the fo​llowing?","raw_text":"The release of arachidonic acid from the membrane phospholipids is mainly mediated by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phospholipa‍se A1 and C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phospholipa‍se A2 and C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phospholip‍ase B and C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phospholipa‍se A1 and B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phospholipase A2 and C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/84b582af09cd4230b185c9b57694e1bbx1280x2080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phospholipase A2 and C</strong></p><p>The <strong>release</strong> of <strong>arachidonic acid</strong> (AA) from the membrane phospholipids is mainly mediated by <strong>phospholipase</strong> <strong>A2</strong> and <strong>C</strong>. </p>\n<p>AA<strong> </strong>is a polyunsaturated fatty acid that may be derived from the following sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dietary sources</li>\n<li>From the conversion of the essential fatty acid <strong>linoleic</strong> <strong>acid</strong></li>\n<li>From membrane phospholipids via phospholipase A2 and C (in response to mechanical, chemical, or physical stimuli)</li>\n<li>From diacylglycerol by the action of <strong>diacylglycerol lipase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>AA-derived mediators are also called <strong>eicosanoids</strong> (20-carbon fatty acids). They are synthesized by two major classes of enzymes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cyclooxygenases</strong> - produces <strong>prostaglandins</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lipoxygenases</strong> - produces <strong>leukotrienes</strong> and lipoxins</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These mediators bind to G protein-coupled receptors (<strong>GPCRs</strong>) on many cell types. They stimulate vascular and cellular reactions in acute inflammation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5594","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about prostagland​ins is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about prostaglandins is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TxA2 is the major product ‍in platelets","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostacyclin is a vasodila‍tor and inhibits platelet​ aggregation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PGD2 is the major prostagl‍andin produced by mast ce​lls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vascular endothelium lacks‍ prostacyclin synthase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vascular endothelium lacks prostacyclin synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b63a15cc3b5847f5a4846afcf3570c16x1280x2080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vascular endothelium lacks prostacyclin synthase</strong></p><p><strong>Prostacyclin synthase </strong>is <strong>present</strong> in the vascular <strong>endothelium</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is responsible for the formation of prostacyclin (PGI2) and PGF1a<strong> </strong>(its stable end product). <strong>Prostacyclin</strong> is a <strong>vasodilator</strong> and a potent <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>aggregation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Thromboxane synthase</strong> is present in <strong>platelets</strong>, and hence, TxA2 is the major product in these cells.</p>\n<p><strong>PGD2</strong> is the major prostaglandin produced by<strong> mast cells</strong> and is a <strong>chemoattractant</strong> for neutrophils.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5595","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is incorrect rega​rding leukotrienes?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding leukotrienes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are produced by leuko‍cytes and mast cells by a​ction of lipoxygenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-lipoxygenase is the pred‍ominant enzyme in neutrop​hils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LTB4 is a potent chemotact‍ic agent and activator of​ neutrophils","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leukotrienes are less pote‍nt mediators of bronchosp​asm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Leukotrienes are less potent mediators of bronchospasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Leukotrienes are less potent mediators of bronchospasm</strong></p><p><strong>Leukotrienes</strong> are <strong>more potent</strong> than histamine in increasing vascular permeability and causing<strong> bronchospasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are produced by <strong>leukocytes</strong> and <strong>mast cells</strong> from arachidonic acid by the action of lipoxygenase. They are involved in vascular and smooth muscle reactions. There are three different lipoxygenases (5, 12, 15). <strong>5-lipoxygenase</strong> is<strong> </strong>predominant in <strong>neutrophils</strong>.</p>\n<p>The principal actions of various arachidonic acid metabolites in inflammation are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 464px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p><strong>Eicosanoid</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p><strong>Action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>PGI<sub>2 </sub>(prostacyclin)</p>\n<p>PGE<sub>1, </sub>PGE<sub>2, </sub>PGD<sub>2</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p>Vasodilation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Thromboxane A<sub>2</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p>Vasoconstriction</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Leukotrienes <strong>C<sub>4, </sub>D<sub>4, </sub>E<sub>4</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p>Increased vascular permeability</p>\n<p>Intense vasoconstriction</p>\n<p>Bronchospasm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Leukotrienes<strong> B<sub>4</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p><strong>Chemotaxis</strong>, <strong>neutrophil</strong> <strong>activation</strong></p>\n<p>Leukocyte adhesion</p>\n<p>Generation of reactive oxygen species </p>\n<p>Release of lysosomal enzymes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5593","text":"What is the principal acti‍on of leukotrienes C4, D4​, and E4?","raw_text":"What is the principal action of leukotrienes C4, D4, and  E4?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemo‍taxis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasodi‍lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leukocyte‍ adhesion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased vascular permeab‍ility","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased vascular permeability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased vascular permeability</strong></p><p>The principal action of <strong>leukotrienes</strong> <strong>C4</strong>, <strong>D4</strong>, and <strong>E4</strong> is increased <strong>vascular</strong> <strong>permeability</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 464px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p><strong>Eicosanoid</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p><strong>Action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>PGI<sub>2 </sub>(prostacyclin)</p>\n<p>PGE<sub>1, </sub>PGE<sub>2, </sub>PGD<sub>2</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p>Vasodilation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Thromboxane A<sub>2</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p>Vasoconstriction</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Leukotrienes <strong>C<sub>4, </sub>D<sub>4, </sub>E<sub>4</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p>Increased <strong>vascular</strong> <strong>permeability</strong></p>\n<p>Intense <strong>vasoconstriction</strong></p>\n<p>Bronchospasm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Leukotrienes<strong> B<sub>4</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 286px;\">\n<p><strong>Chemotaxis</strong>, <strong>neutrophil</strong> <strong>activation</strong></p>\n<p>Leukocyte adhesion</p>\n<p>Generation of reactive oxygen species </p>\n<p>Release of lysosomal enzymes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5201","text":"Which of the following is ‍an anti-inflammatory cyto​kine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an anti-inflammatory cytokine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‍-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‍-4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T‍NF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IF‍Nγ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IL-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IL-4</strong></p><p><strong>IL-4</strong> is an <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> <strong>cytokine</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 106\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It induces alternatively activated macrophages. This results in <strong>tissue repair </strong>and the<strong> resolution </strong>of<strong> inflammation.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The following are anti-inflammatory cytokines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IL-4</li>\n<li>IL-10</li>\n<li>IL-13</li>\n<li>TGF-β</li>\n<li>Lipoxins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lipoxins suppress inflammation by inhibiting leukocyte recruitment. They inhibit neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion to endothelium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5597","text":"Which of the following is ‍a CXC chemokine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a CXC chemokine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‍-8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RAN‍TES","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lympho‍tactin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fract‍alkine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IL-8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/142af042934c457c82b7695e25750782x939x660.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IL-8</strong></p><p><strong>IL­-8</strong> is a typical example of a <strong>CXC chemokine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chemokines are a family of small (8 to 10 kD) proteins. They act primarily as <strong>chemoattractants</strong> for specific types of leukocytes.</p>\n<p>They are classified into <strong>4</strong> <strong>groups</strong>, according to the <strong>arrangement of cysteine</strong> (C) residues in the proteins. This is given in the image and the table below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"width: 538px; height: 659px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.7812px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\">Type of chemokine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.422px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\">Structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.797px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\">Action and example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.7812px; height: 100px; text-align: center;\">C-X-C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.422px; height: 100px; text-align: left;\">1 amino acid residue separating the first 2 of the 4 conserved cysteine residues</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.797px; height: 100px;\">\n<p>Involved in <strong>neutrophil a</strong><strong>ctivation </strong>and<strong> chemotaxis</strong></p>\n<p>Example-<strong> IL-8</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.7812px; height: 100px; text-align: center;\">C-C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.422px; height: 100px; text-align: left;\">The first 2 conserved cysteine residues remain adjacent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.797px; height: 100px;\">\n<p>Attract monocytes, eosinophils, basophils, lymphocytes</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monocyte chemoattractant protein (MCP­1)</li>\n<li>Eotaxin</li>\n<li>Macrophage inflammatory protein­ 1α (MIP­1α)</li>\n<li>Regulated and normal T­-cell expressed and secreted (RANTES)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.7812px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\">C chemokines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.422px; height: 64px; text-align: left;\">Lack the 1st and 3rd of the 4 conserved cysteines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.797px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Specific for <strong>lymphocytes</strong></p>\n<p>Example <strong>- lymphotactin</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 88.3125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.7812px; height: 88.3125px; text-align: center;\">CX3C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.422px; height: 88.3125px; text-align: left;\">Contain 3 amino acids between the 2 cysteines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.797px; height: 88.3125px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The only known member of this class is called <strong>fractalkine. </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5598","text":"Fractalkine belongs to whi‍ch chemokine family?","raw_text":"Fractalkine belongs to which chemokine family?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CX‍3C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‍XC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‍C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CX3C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/48498d3815184c9f9e86af3f094fec30x760x243.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CX3C</strong></p><p><strong>Fractalkine</strong> is the only known member of the <strong>CX3C </strong>chemokine group.</p>\n<p><strong>CX3C </strong>chemokines contain <strong>3</strong> <strong>amino acids </strong>between the<strong> 2 cysteines</strong>.</p>\n<p>This chemokine exists in two forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cell surface-bound </strong>protein -\n<ul>\n<li>Induced on endothelial cells by inflammatory cytokines</li>\n<li>Promotes the strong adhesion of monocytes and T cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Soluble form</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Derived by proteolysis of the membrane-bound protein</li>\n<li>Potent chemoattractant activity for the same cell.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the structure of CX3C chemokine.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5599","text":"What type of chemokine is ‍MCP-1?","raw_text":"What type of chemokine is MCP-1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CX‍3C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‍XC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‍C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f44bd6810dae4e5eab4d55b3d1cde139x317x285.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CC</strong></p><p><strong>MCP-1</strong> belongs to the <strong>CC type </strong>of chemokine. Here, the first two conserved cysteine residues remain adjacent to each other.</p>\n<p>The<strong> C­C chemokines</strong> include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Monocyte chemoattractant protein (MCP­1)</li>\n<li>Eotaxin</li>\n<li>Macrophage inflammatory protein­1α (MIP­1α)</li>\n<li>Regulated and normal T-­cell expressed and secreted (RANTES)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows the structure of C-C chemokine:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5619","text":"Serum concentrations of wh‍ich of the following decr​ease during inflammation?​","raw_text":"Serum concentrations of which of the following decrease during inflammation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alb‍umin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serum amyl‍oid A (SAA)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C-reactive p‍rotein (CRP)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibri‍nogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Albumin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Albumin</strong></p><p>During<strong> inflammation</strong>, there are<strong> reduced</strong> levels of <strong>albumin</strong>.</p>\n<p>This occurs in order to allow an increase in the liver's capacity to synthesize acute phase reactants. Hence, albumin is called a <strong>negative</strong> <strong>phase</strong> <strong>reactant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Negative acute-phase proteins concentration decreases during inflammation. These are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Albumin</li>\n<li>Transferrin</li>\n<li>Retinol-binding protein</li>\n<li>Antithrombin</li>\n<li>Transcortin</li>\n<li>Alpha 2-HS glycoprotein</li>\n<li>Alpha-fetoprotein</li>\n<li>Thyroxin-binding protein</li>\n<li>Insulin-like growth factor 1</li>\n<li>Factor 12</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Acute-phase proteins </strong>are plasma proteins synthesized by the liver in response to inflammation. These include<strong> C-reactive protein </strong>(CRP), <strong>fibrinogen, </strong>and<strong> serum amyloid A </strong>(SAA) <strong>protein. </strong>The synthesis of these molecules in hepatocytes is stimulated by cytokines. IL-6 stimulates the synthesis of CRP and fibrinogen and IL-1 or TNF for SAA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5620","text":"A 39-year-old woman is bei‍ng evaluated for anemia. ​She is a known case of rh​eumatoid arthritis. Incre​ased levels of which of t‌he following is responsib​le for her anemia?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old woman is being evaluated for anemia. She is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis. Increased levels of which of the following is responsible for her anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibri‍nogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‍RP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S‍AA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepc‍idin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepcidin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepcidin</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the woman has a <strong>chronic illness</strong>, rheumatoid arthritis. In chronic disease states, elevated plasma concentrations of <strong>hepcidin</strong> cause <strong>anemia</strong>. Increased hepcidin reduces the availability of iron.</p>\n<p><strong>Fibrinogen</strong> binds to red cells and causes <strong>rouleaux formation.</strong> They sediment more rapidly at unit gravity than do individual red cells. This is the basis for measuring the <strong>erythrocyte sedimentation rate</strong> in inflammation.</p>\n<p><strong>C-reactive protein (CRP) and serum amyloid A (SAA)</strong> bind to microbial cell walls. They act as opsonins and fix complement. They also bind chromatin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5590","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is not true for p​latelet-activating factor​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true for platelet-activating factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is secreted by mast cel‍ls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It does not cause vasocons‍triction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It increases vascular perm‍eability","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is involved in leukocyt‍e adhesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It does not cause vasoconstriction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It does not cause vasoconstriction</strong></p><p><strong>Platelet-activating factor </strong>(PAF)<strong> </strong>primarily<strong> </strong>causes <strong>vasoconstriction.</strong> </p>\n<p>PAF is a <strong>phospholipid-derived mediator</strong> known to have multiple inflammatory effects. It may be secreted by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelets</li>\n<li>Basophils</li>\n<li><strong>Mast cells</strong></li>\n<li>Neutrophils</li>\n<li>Macrophages</li>\n<li>Endothelial cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Its functions include:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet aggregation</li>\n<li><strong>Vasoconstriction</strong> </li>\n<li>Bronchoconstriction</li>\n<li><strong>Leukocyte adhesion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vascular permeability </strong>and<strong> vasodilation at low doses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5601","text":"The critical step in compl‍ement activation involves​ the proteolysis of the w​hich component?","raw_text":"The critical step in complement activation involves the proteolysis of the which component?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‍3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‍5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‍2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C3</strong></p><p>The <strong>critical step</strong> in <strong>complement activation</strong> is the <strong>proteolysis</strong> of the third (and most abundant) component, <strong>C3.</strong></p>\n<p>The complement system can be activated by one of three pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Classical </strong>pathway<strong> - </strong>antibody-mediated and triggered by fixation of C1 to antibody (IgM or IgG) that has combined with antigen</li>\n<li><strong>Alternative </strong>pathway<strong> </strong>- triggered by microbial surface molecules in the absence of antibody</li>\n<li><strong>Lectin </strong>pathway - plasma mannose-binding lectin binds to carbohydrates on microbes and directly activates C1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>All three pathways</strong> of complement activation lead to the formation of an <strong>active enzyme</strong> called the <strong>C3 convertase</strong>. It <strong>splits C3</strong> into two functionally distinct fragments, <strong>C3a </strong>and<strong> C3b.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5602","text":"Patients with an inherited‍ deficiency of C1 inhibit​or will have which of the​ following?","raw_text":"Patients with an inherited deficiency of C1 inhibitor will have which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‍lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary‍ angioedema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria‍ infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glomerulo‍nephritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hereditary angioedema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hereditary angioedema</strong></p><p>The <strong>deficiency of C1 INH</strong> causes <strong>hereditary</strong> <strong>angioedema</strong>.</p>\n<p>C1-inhibitor (C1 INH)is the<strong> </strong>largest member of the <strong>serpin superfamily</strong> of proteins. It normally inhibits the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complement</strong> system</li>\n<li><strong>Kallikrein</strong> system</li>\n<li>Factor XII<strong> </strong>or<strong> Hageman factor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Deficiency of C1 INH is associated with hereditary angioedema (hereditary angioneurotic edema). It leads to <strong>decreased C4</strong> and<strong> C2</strong> levels and <strong>bradykinin accumulation.</strong> There is swelling due to leakage of fluid from blood vessels into connective tissue. It is an <strong>a</strong><strong>utosomal dominant</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5603","text":"Which of the following is ‍not caused by bradykinin?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not caused by bradykinin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased vascular permeab‍ility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth muscl‍e contraction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pain at the site of inject‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generalise‍d vasospasm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Generalised vasospasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Generalised vasospasm</strong></p><p><strong>Bradykinin</strong> results in <strong>vasodilation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kinins</strong> are vasoactive peptides derived from plasma proteins called<strong> kininogens</strong>, by the action of specific proteases called <strong>kallikreins</strong>. The enzyme kallikrein cleaves high ­molecular ­weight kininogen, to produce bradykinin.</p>\n<p>Actions of bradykinin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increases <strong>vascular permeability</strong></li>\n<li>Potent vasodilator</li>\n<li><strong>Contraction</strong> of <strong>smooth muscle</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dilation</strong> of <strong>blood vessels</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pain when injected</strong> into the skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5604","text":"During an inflammatory res‍ponse, which of the follo​wing does not increase va​scular permeability?","raw_text":"During an inflammatory response, which of the following does not increase vascular permeability?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‍-4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‍3a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sero‍tonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leukotr‍iene C4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IL-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IL-4</strong></p><p><strong>IL-4</strong> is an <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> cytokine and does not have any effect on vascular permeability.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Inflammatory change</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Principal Mediators</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Vasodilation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Histamine</p>\n<p>Prostaglandins</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Increased vascular permeability</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Histamine</strong> and <strong>serotonin</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C3a</strong> and <strong>C5a</strong> (by the release of vasoactive amines from mast cells, other cells)</p>\n<p><strong>Leukotrienes </strong>C<sub>4, </sub>D<sub>4</sub>, E<sub>4,</sub></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Chemotaxis, leukocyte recruitment, activation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>TNF, IL-1</p>\n<p>Chemokines</p>\n<p><strong>C3a</strong> and C5a</p>\n<p>Leukotriene B<sub>4</sub></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Fever</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>IL-1, TNF</p>\n<p>Prostaglandins</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Pain</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Prostaglandins</p>\n<p>Bradykinin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Tissue damage</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"225\">\n<p>Lysosomal enzymes of leukocytes</p>\n<p>Reactive oxygen species</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5605","text":"Fever occurs during inflam‍mation due to all except:​","raw_text":"Fever occurs during inflammation due to all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‍ 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostag‍landins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‍NF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist‍amine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Histamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Histamine</strong></p><p><strong>Histamine</strong> is involved in the mediation of <strong>vascular events</strong> of inflammation, not fever.</p>\n<p><strong>Fever</strong> is characterized by an elevation of body temperature, usually by <strong>1° to 4°C.</strong> It is one of the most prominent manifestations of the acute-phase response, especially if associated with infection.</p>\n<p>Substances that induce fever are called <strong>pyrogens</strong>. They may be exogenous or endogenous.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endogenous</strong> pyrogens -<strong> IL-1, TNF,</strong> <strong>prostaglandins</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exogenous </strong>pyrogens -\n<ul>\n<li>Lipopolysaccharides of bacterial walls</li>\n<li>They stimulate leukocytes to release endogenous pyrogens (IL-1 and TNF)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cytokines result in an increase in cyclooxygenases that convert arachidonic acid into <strong>prostaglandins</strong>. These prostaglandins are produced in the vascular and perivascular cells of the <strong>hypothalamus</strong>. In the hypothalamus, they stimulate the production of neurotransmitters that <strong>reset </strong>the<strong> temperature setpoint </strong>to a<strong> higher</strong> level. This is especially by<strong> PGE2.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7554","text":"Which of the following is ‍true about fever?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirin increases fever by‍ inhibiting prostaglandin​ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PGD2 is responsible for re‍setting the temperature t​o a higher level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL-1 is an endogenous pyro‍gen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fever occurs during inflam‍mation due to release of ​histamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IL-1 is an endogenous pyrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IL-1 is an endogenous pyrogen</strong></p><p><strong>IL-1 </strong>is an <strong>endogenous pyrogen</strong></p>\n<p>Substances that induce fever are called pyrogens. They may be exogenous or endogenous.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endogenous</strong> pyrogens -<strong> IL-1, TNF,</strong> <strong>prostaglandins</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exogenous </strong>pyrogens -\n<ul>\n<li>Lipopolysaccharides of bacterial walls</li>\n<li>They stimulate leukocytes to release endogenous pyrogens (IL-1 and TNF)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the hypothalamus, prostaglandins such as <strong>PGE2</strong> stimulate the production of neurotransmitters that <strong>reset</strong> the <strong>temperature</strong> <strong>setpoint</strong> at a higher level. <strong>Aspirin decreases</strong> <strong>fever</strong> by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7555","text":"IL-1 is act‍ivated by :","raw_text":"IL-1 is activated by :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caspa‍se - 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caspa‍se - 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caspa‍se - 9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caspa‍se - 8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Caspase - 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Caspase - 1</strong></p><p><strong>Caspase- 1 activates</strong> the biologically active form of <strong>IL-1</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>IL-1 </strong>is involved in <strong>pyroptosis</strong>, a form of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">programmed cell death</strong> that is different from apoptosis and necrosis. It is involved in the clearance of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">necrotic</strong> and <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">microbe-infected cells</strong>. It occurs in the following sequence:</p>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Activation of the inflammasome complex</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Activation of caspase-1</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Generation of IL-1 - results in fever, inflammation, leukocyte recruitment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5607","text":"Which of the following is ‍not secreted by T-helper ​type 2 cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not secreted by T-helper type 2 cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‍-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‍-5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL‍-13","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IFN ‍gamma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IFN gamma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IFN gamma</strong></p><p><strong>TH2</strong> cells secrete <strong>IL­4, IL­5, and IL­13.</strong></p>\n<p>Three subsets of CD4+ T cells secrete different types of cytokines and elicit different types of inflammation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5606","text":"In patients with which of ‍the following conditions ​would you observe tertiar​y lymphoid organs? Hashim​oto thyroiditis Rheumatoi‌d arthritis Tuberculosis ​Systemic lupus erythemato‌sus Human immunodeficienc‌y virus infection","raw_text":"<p>In patients with which of the following conditions would you observe tertiary lymphoid organs?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hashimoto thyroiditis</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Human immunodeficiency virus infection</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‍and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Tertiary lymphoid</strong> organs are seen in <strong>autoimmune</strong> conditions such as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hashimoto thyroiditis </strong>- in the thyroid</li>\n<li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis </strong>- in the synovium</li>\n<li><strong>SLE</strong> (systemic lupus erythematosus) - in the kidneys</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Tertiary lymphoid organs are a feature of <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>inflammatory</strong> conditions. Here, the accumulated lymphocytes, antigen­-presenting cells, and plasma cells cluster together to form lymphoid tissues resembling lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5609","text":"Lymph node biopsy of a pat‍ient with tuberculosis de​monstrated the following ​findings. From which of t​he following are these ce‌lls derived?","raw_text":"Lymph node biopsy of a patient with tuberculosis demonstrated the following findings. From which of the following are these cells derived?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD8 T-ly‍mphocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‍ophils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD4 T-ly‍mphocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macro‍phages","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Macrophages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/baebf72d685d4a3fb95d7754e3762c55.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50f20d9729114c50948535ef49edf4aax1280x1214.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fea4dae0cb14b2d8b004abca61048c9x1280x1214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Macrophages</strong></p><p>The given histopathological findings in a patient with tuberculosis are suggestive of <strong>granulomatous</strong> inflammation, with <strong>epithelioid</strong> and <strong>multinucleated giant </strong>cells. These cells are derived from the <strong>monocyte-macrophage</strong> lineage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Macrophages</strong> activated by <strong>IFN-γ</strong> develop into cells with abundant cytoplasm. They resemble epithelial cells and are thus called <strong>epithelioid cells</strong>. Some activated macrophages may <strong>fuse</strong>, forming <strong>multinucleated giant cells</strong>. Collections of activated macrophages, often with T lymphocytes are seen in granulomatous inflammation. Sometimes it is associated with central <strong>caseating</strong> <strong>necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Granulomas may be formed in response to various stimuli:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immune granulomas</strong> - due to T-cell mediated response; macrophages activate T cells to produce cytokines, such as IL-2 and IFN-γ</li>\n<li><strong>Foreign body granulomas </strong>- due to inert foreign bodies in the absence of a T-cell mediated response</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5613","text":"Epithelioid granulomas are‍ not seen in which of the​ following diseases?","raw_text":"Epithelioid granulomas are not seen in which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‍idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syph‍ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxopl‍asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kikuchi's l‍ymphadenitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kikuchi's lymphadenitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kikuchi&#x27;s lymphadenitis</strong></p><p><strong>Kikuchi's lymphadenitis</strong> is associated with <strong>histiocytic necrotizing </strong>lymphadenitis. It does not show epithelioid granulomatous lesions.</p>\n<p>Common conditions characterized by <strong>epithelioid granuloma</strong> formation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong> (non-caseating granuloma)</li>\n<li>Brucellosis</li>\n<li>Cat scratch disease (stellate<sup> </sup>shaped or round granuloma)</li>\n<li><strong>Syphilis</strong> (gumma<sup> </sup>)</li>\n<li>Lymphogranuloma inguinale</li>\n<li>Leprosy</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease </li>\n<li><strong>Toxoplasmosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5614","text":"A pathologist observes the‍ given findings during th​e examination of a lymph ​node sample. Which of the​ following is the least l‌ikely cause?","raw_text":"A pathologist observes the given findings during the examination of a lymph node sample. Which of the following is the least likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lep‍rosy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sarco‍idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc‍ulosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afec589e36df4b0b8943d2f80d970a5c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image of a lymph node shows a <strong>non-caseating granuloma</strong>. Tuberculosis is <strong>least likely</strong> as it is commonly associated with caseating granulomas.</p>\n<p>Non-caseating granulomas can be seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leprosy</strong></li>\n<li>Cat-scratch disease</li>\n<li><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong></li>\n<li>Berylliosis</li>\n<li><strong>Crohn's disease</strong></li>\n<li>Hypersenitive pneumonitis</li>\n<li>Foreign body granuloma</li>\n<li>Q fever</li>\n<li>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's).</li>\n<li>Hodgkin's lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3118","text":"Stellate granuloma is seen‍ in :","raw_text":"Stellate granuloma is seen in :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‍idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cat-scrat‍ch disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptoc‍occosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histopl‍asmosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cat-scratch disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4dd8476740314a629cdb5ed524f52cd2x1280x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51e095a6f0f34ea7bfa72fce34083db0x1280x1048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cat-scratch disease</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Stellate granuloma</strong> is seen in <strong>cat-scratch</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. It is also known as subacute regional lymphadenitis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is a bacterial infection caused by <strong><em>Bartonella henselae</em>. </strong>It is acquired following contact with infected animals (commonly cats). </p>\n<p>Regional<strong> lymph nodes</strong> demonstrate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Follicular hyperplasia with circumscribed <strong>central stellate necrosis</strong> with neutrophils</li>\n<li>Surrounded by <strong>palisading histiocytes</strong> (suppurative <strong>granulomas</strong>)</li>\n<li>Sinuses packed with monocytoid B cells</li>\n<li>Usually, there are no perifollicular and intrafollicular epithelioid cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"w4l40tQc","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"5_ans_introduction___parasympathomimetics","title":"5.Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2507","text":"What is the principal post‍-ganglionic neurotransmit​ter of the parasympatheti‍c system?","raw_text":"What is the principal post-ganglionic neurotransmitter of the parasympathetic system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nor-epi‍nephrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acetyl‍choline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GA‍BA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetylcholine</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">The principal <strong>post-ganglionic</strong> neurotransmitter of the <strong>parasympathetic</strong> system is <strong>acetylcholine.</strong></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2514","text":"While conducting animal st‍udies, a scientist is exp​erimenting with different‍ properties of drugs acti‌ng on the ANS. He identif‌ies a drug that inhibits ​the rate-limiting step in​ acetylcholine synthesis ​in nerve terminals. What ‌is this drug?","raw_text":"While conducting animal studies, a scientist is experimenting with different properties of drugs acting on the ANS. He identifies a drug that inhibits the rate-limiting step in acetylcholine synthesis in nerve terminals. What is this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‍line","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemich‍olinium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acetylcholi‍ne esterase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Botulini‍um toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemicholinium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c164d17405742588be93f8c5a097090x1280x2124.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemicholinium</strong></p><p>The <strong>rate-limiting step</strong> in acetylcholine synthesis is the <strong>active uptake</strong> of<strong> choline</strong> into the nerve terminal, which is <strong>inhibited</strong> by<strong> hemicholinum.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Choline is the precursor of acetylcholine and is taken up from the synaptic cleft into the nerve terminal.</p>\n<p>Option C: Acetylcholine esterase is involved in the breakdown of acetylcholine after it is released into the synaptic cleft.</p>\n<p>Option D: Botulinum toxin inhibits the release of acetylcholine from nerve terminals.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2513","text":"What is the drug that acts‍ on SNARE?","raw_text":"What is the drug that acts on SNARE?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemich‍olinum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Botulin‍um toxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hexame‍thonium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetylchol‍inesterase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Botulinum toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2acfd56c030143abb12025c6fd80fcebx1280x2124.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Botulinum toxin</strong></p><p>The drug which acts on <strong>SNARE</strong> (Soluble N-ethylmalimide Sensitive Factor (NSF) Attachment protein Receptor) is <strong>botulinum toxin</strong>.</p>\n<p>SNARE proteins play a critical role in the fusion of the vesicle with the cell membrane and the exocytosis of the neurotransmitter from the pre-synaptic membrane. <strong>Inhibition</strong> of these <strong>SNARE</strong> proteins <strong>inhibits</strong> the<strong> release</strong> of<strong> acetylcholine</strong> from synaptic terminals.</p>\n<p>It is useful to treat spastic and neurological conditions associated with increased cholinergic activity like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blepharospasm</li>\n<li>Spastic cerebral palsy</li>\n<li>Torticollis</li>\n<li>Chronic resistant migraine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Hemicholinum inhibits the uptake of choline into nerve terminals.</p>\n<p>Option C: Hexamethonium is a ganglionic blocker.</p>\n<p>Option D: Acetylcholine esterase is involved in the breakdown of acetylcholine after it is released into the synaptic cleft.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4726","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements regarding acetylc​holine is incorrect ?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding acetylcholine is incorrect ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has no systemic therape‍utic use","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is metabolised by both ‍acetylcholine esterase an​d butyrylcholine esterase‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is used topically durin‍g ophthalmic surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has good CNS penetratio‍n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It has good CNS penetration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It has good CNS penetration</strong></p><p><strong>Acetylcholine</strong> does not have a good CNS penetration as it is a <strong>quaternary ammonium compound</strong>. </p>\n<p>Neostigmine and glycopyrrolate are also quaternary ammonium compounds and their entry into CNS is limited. <strong>Tertiary ammonium compounds</strong> like physostigmine have <strong>good CNS penetration. </strong></p>\n<p>Options A and B: Acetylcholine has virtually no systemic use because of its diffuse action and rapid hydrolysis by both acetylcholine esterase and plasma butyrylcholine esterase. </p>\n<p>Option C: Acetylcholine is used during ophthalmic surgery for producing rapid miosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0133","text":"Which of the following cau‍ses an increase in ACh re​lease from the presynapti‍c neuron?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes an increase in ACh release from the presynaptic neuron?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Potassium ch‍annel blocker","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium chan‍nel blocker","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium cha‍nnel blocker","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorine cha‍nnel blocker","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Potassium channel blocker","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Potassium channel blocker</strong></p><p><strong>Potassium channel blockers</strong> cause an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>ACh release</strong> from the <strong>presynaptic</strong> neuron.</p>\n<p>Presynaptic potassium channel block promotes the activation of voltage-gated calcium channels. This leads to a release of acetylcholine in the neuromuscular junction.</p>\n<p>Aminopyridines (K+ channel blockers) are used in the treatment of Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome which helps to improve muscle strength.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2516","text":"You notice your senior per‍forming a vagal maneuver ​in a stable patient with ‍PSVT. Which of the follow‌ing muscarinic receptors ‌mediate the therapeutic e​ffect of this maneuver?","raw_text":"You notice your senior performing a vagal maneuver in a stable patient with PSVT. Which of the following muscarinic receptors mediate the therapeutic effect of this maneuver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‍2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M‍3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‍4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. M2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. M2</strong></p><p>In vagal maneuver, the <strong>parasympathetic</strong> nervous system mediates activity on the <strong>heart</strong> via the <strong>M2 muscarinic receptor</strong> (most predominant subtype).</p>\n<p>The receptors in the autonomic nervous system (ANS) are of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Muscarinic:\n<ul>\n<li>5 Subtypes - M<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>2</sub>, M<sub>3</sub>, M<sub>4</sub>, M<sub>5</sub></li>\n<li>M<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>3</sub>, M<sub>5</sub> are G<sub>q</sub> coupled receptors</li>\n<li>M<sub>2</sub>, M<sub>4 </sub>are G<sub>i</sub>/G<sub>o </sub>coupled receptors</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nicotinic:\n<ul>\n<li>2 Subtypes - N<sub>m </sub>(Neuromuscular junction), N<sub>n </sub>(CNS, Ganglion, Adrenal glands)</li>\n<li>Nicotinic receptors are ion channels</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"width: 344px; height: 381px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 123.375px;\">Receptors of Parasympathetic system</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px;\">Subtype</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">Site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"height: 36px; width: 123.375px;\">Nicotinic</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">N<sub>m</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">Neuromuscular junction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">N<sub>n</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS, ganglia, Adrenal gland</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"5\" style=\"height: 54px; width: 123.375px;\">Muscarinic</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>1</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS, Ganglia, Salivary, and gastric glands</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\"><strong>M<sub>2</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\"><strong>Heart</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 68.90625px; height: 22px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>3</sub></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.734375px; height: 22px;\">Smooth muscles, secretory glands, eyes - pupils, and blood vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>4</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>5</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7023","text":"What is the mechanism by w‍hich acetylcholine causes​ a decrease in the heart ‍rate?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism by which acetylcholine causes a decrease in the heart rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delayed diastolic depolari‍zation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase ‍in plateau","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease ‍in preload","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase i‍n afterload","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Delayed diastolic depolarization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Delayed diastolic depolarization</strong></p><p>The mechanism by which <strong>acetylcholine</strong> causes a decrease in the heart rate is by <strong>decreasing</strong> the <strong>diastolic depolarization.</strong></p>\n<p>The acetylcholine released from the vagal nerve acts on the <strong>M2 receptor</strong> (βγ subunit of G protein) on the <strong>SA node</strong>. This opens a special set of potassium channels causing an efflux of potassium (I<sub>KAch</sub>) which hyperpolarizes the SA node (I<sub>h</sub>). The resultant current (I<sub>h</sub>) slows the diastolic depolarization thereby causing bradycardia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2519","text":"A 40-year-old woman who wa‍s diagnosed with Sjogren'​s syndrome is on treatmen‍t with cevimeline. On whi‌ch receptor does this dru‌g mainly act for the expu​lsion of secretions from ​the salivary gland?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman who was diagnosed with Sjogren's syndrome is on treatment with cevimeline. On which receptor does this drug mainly act for the expulsion of secretions from the salivary gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‍2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‍3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M‍4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. M3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. M3</strong></p><p><strong>Cevimeline</strong> is an <strong>M3 receptor agonist</strong> resulting in the <strong>expulsion</strong> of the secretions from the salivary gland. This drug also enhances expulsion of lacrimal secretions in <strong>Sjögren’s syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both M1 and M3 receptors are present in a salivary gland. Activation of <strong>M1</strong> <strong>increases</strong><strong> secretion </strong>and <strong>M3</strong> causes<strong> smooth muscle contraction</strong> and <strong>expulsion</strong> of the secretions.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 344px; height: 381px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 123.375px;\">Receptors of Parasympathetic system</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px;\">Subtype</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">Site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"height: 36px; width: 123.375px;\">Nicotinic</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">N<sub>m</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">Neuromuscular junction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">N<sub>n</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS, ganglia, Adrenal gland</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"5\" style=\"height: 54px; width: 123.375px;\">Muscarinic</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>1</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS, Ganglia, Salivary, and gastric glands</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">Heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 68.90625px; height: 22px; text-align: left;\"><strong>M<sub>3</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.734375px; height: 22px;\">Smooth muscles, <strong>secretory glands</strong>, eyes - pupils, and blood vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>4</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 68.90625px; text-align: left;\">M<sub>5</sub></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 129.734375px;\">CNS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2526","text":"You are asked to prescribe‍ a cholinergic agent to a​ patient suffering from A‍lzheimer's disease. Which‌ of the following drugs c‌annot be chosen?","raw_text":"You are asked to prescribe a cholinergic agent to a patient suffering from Alzheimer's disease. Which of the following drugs cannot be chosen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galan‍tamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Done‍pezil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tac‍rine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mema‍ntine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Memantine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Memantine</strong></p><p><strong>Memantine</strong> is an <strong>NMDA receptor antagonist </strong>(not a cholinergic agent) indicated for the management of moderate to severe<strong> Alzheimer’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Donepezil, galantamine, and tacrine are centrally acting cholinesterase inhibitors indicated for Alzheimer’s disease.<br/><br/>Note: In this scenario, you are asked to prescribe a <strong>cholinergic</strong> drug. <strong>Tacrine</strong>, a cholinergic drug, although hepatotoxic, is still <strong>approved for use</strong> in Alzheimer's disease. Hence, <strong>memantine</strong> is the best answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2520","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions should cholino​mimetic drugs be avoided?‍","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions should cholinomimetic drugs be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closed ang‍le glaucoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brady‍cardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cobra‍ bite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myasthen‍ia gravis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bradycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bradycardia</strong></p><p><strong>Cholinomimetics</strong> cannot be used in bradycardia.<strong> </strong>They <strong>cause bradycardia</strong> by its action on M2 receptors on the heart.</p>\n<p>In bradycardia, an anticholinergic agent like <strong>atropine</strong> is <strong>indicated.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A - Cholinomimetics like pilocarpine are used in both open-angle and closed-angle glaucoma<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C - Cobra venom contains a curare-like neurotoxin. Although antivenom is the specific antidote, symptoms can be controlled by a combination of neostigmine and atropine.</p>\n<p>Option D - Cholinesterase inhibitors like neostigmine and pyridostigmine are indicated in myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2525","text":"Neostigmine is administere‍d to a post-op patient to​ reverse neuromuscular bl‍ockade. What is its mecha‌nism of action?","raw_text":"Neostigmine is administered to a post-op patient to reverse neuromuscular blockade. What is its mechanism of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It blocks the action of ac‍etylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It interferes with the act‍ion of choline transferas​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It interferes with the act‍ion of choline acetyl tra​nsferase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It interferes with the act‍ion of acetylcholine este​rase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It interferes with the action of acetylcholine esterase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17de91627d8047d984a749fcc6eac33fx1280x2234.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It interferes with the action of acetylcholine esterase</strong></p><p>Muscle weakness is improved by <strong>neostigmine</strong> as it <strong>interferes</strong> with the action of <strong>acetylcholine esterase.</strong></p>\n<p>Neostigmine is a <strong>cholinesterase inhibitor</strong> used mainly to improve muscle tone in people with myasthenia gravis, and also to reverse the effects of non-depolarizing muscle relaxants such as rocuronium and vecuronium at the end of an operation. </p>\n<p>Inhibition of acetylcholine esterase <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>acetylcholine</strong> levels in the <strong>synapse</strong> that can act on receptors over the large surface area and for longer periods of time, reducing the weakness.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4723","text":"A first-year anesthesia re‍sident administered neost​igmine to a patient who w‍as accidentally given a s‌lightly excessive bolus d‌ose of suxamethonium. Wha​t is the most probable ef​fect this is likely to pr​oduce?","raw_text":"A first-year anesthesia resident administered neostigmine to a patient who was accidentally given a slightly excessive bolus dose of suxamethonium. What is the most probable effect this is likely to produce?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete‍ reversal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incomplet‍e reversal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No e‍ffect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enhanced ‍relaxation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enhanced relaxation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9ffc23f906f4e298f36f21819ea8f70x1280x2234.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enhanced relaxation</strong></p><p>Neostigmine <strong>prolongs the muscle relaxation</strong> caused by suxamethonium (succinylcholine) instead of reversing the block.</p>\n<p>The contraction of a muscle is achieved by a cycle of ACh binding and its degradation. <strong>Depolarising</strong> <strong>neuromuscular blockers</strong>  (such as suxamethonium) acts by binding to the receptors much longer than ACh and causing a <strong>prolonged depolarization,</strong> resulting in an eventual blocking of neuromuscular transmission and<strong> flaccid paralysis. </strong></p>\n<p>AChE metabolizes ACh and plasma cholinesterase metabolizes suxamethonium (SCh). <strong>Neostigmine</strong> blocks <strong>both enzymes</strong>. By inhibiting AChE, neostigmine increases the ACh concentration in the synaptic cleft. But this cannot reverse the block as the depolarized nerves are unresponsive to any stimuli during this period. However, the simultaneous <strong>inhibition of plasma cholinesterase</strong> decreases the degradation of SCh and therefore <strong>potentiates the neuromuscular block</strong> of suxamethonium, resulting in prolonged muscle relaxation.</p>\n<p>Neostigmine can be used only as a <strong>reversal</strong> agent for neuromuscular blockade caused by competitive <strong>non-depolarising</strong> blockers such as <strong>vecuronium.</strong> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2528","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‍ted to the medicine OPD w​ith complaints of muscle ‍weakness and blurring of ‌vision following exertion‌. On examination, the pat​ient had ptosis. What is ​the drug that can be used​ in the diagnosis of this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented to the medicine OPD with complaints of muscle weakness and blurring of vision following exertion. On examination, the patient had ptosis. What is the drug that can be used in the diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physos‍tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Edrop‍honium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neost‍igmine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridos‍tigmine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Edrophonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Edrophonium</strong></p><p><strong>Edrophonium</strong>, a <strong>short-acting</strong> anticholinesterase agent is used for diagnosing <strong>myasthenia gravis. </strong>It is also called the<strong> tensilon test.</strong></p>\n<p>Initially, 2 mg is administered. If improvements of symptoms do not occur within 45 seconds, an additional 8 mg can be administered. <strong>Improvement of strength</strong> unaccompanied by lingual fasciculations (generally present in non-myasthenics) is a <strong>positive test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Edrophonium is short-acting because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The binding site of edrophonium to acetylcholine esterase - choline subsite of the active center</li>\n<li>No reaction with the enzyme - reversible binding only</li>\n<li>Quaternary structure - rapid elimination</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: An excessive dose of anti-cholinesterase drugs results in a cholinergic crisis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7567","text":"What is the drug used to d‍ifferentiate myasthenia g​ravis from cholinergic cr‍isis?","raw_text":"What is the drug used to differentiate myasthenia gravis from cholinergic crisis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obid‍oxime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Edrop‍honium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Done‍pezil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atro‍pine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Edrophonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Edrophonium</strong></p><p>The drug used to differentiate myasthenia gravis from a cholinergic crisis is <strong>edrophonium.</strong></p>\n<p>Muscle weakness is a common presentation in patients with myasthenia gravis and cholinergic crisis (excessive dose of anti-ChE drugs). Intravenous injection of edrophonium <strong>improves muscular weakness</strong> in a patient with <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> and not in a case of cholinergic crisis. </p>\n<p>The test used for this differentiation is known as the <strong>edrophonium test. </strong></p>\n<p>Initially, 2 mg is administered; if improvements of symptoms do not occur within 45 seconds, an additional 8 mg can be administered. Improvement of strength is a positive test for myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2529","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs would you use in the t​reatment of a patient wit‍h myasthenia gravis?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs would you use in the treatment of a patient with myasthenia gravis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"d-Tuboc‍urarine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hexame‍thonium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amben‍onium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gall‍amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ambenonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ambenonium</strong></p><p><strong>Ambenonium, </strong>pyridostigmine, and neostigmine are<strong> </strong>the anti-cholinesterase drugs <strong>indicated</strong> in the treatment of<strong> myasthenia gravis.</strong></p>\n<p>Neostigmine and its congeners can improve muscle contractions by allowing released acetylcholine to accumulate and act on the receptors over a large area. They also have an additional direct depolarizing effect on the motor endplate.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: d-Tubocurarine and gallamine are non-depolarising neuromuscular blockers and can exacerbate myasthenia.</p>\n<p>Option B: Hexamethonium is a ganglionic blocker and not indicated for myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0129","text":"A patient presents to the ‍emergency department with​ diarrhea, sweating, exce‍ssive lacrimation, and sa‌livation. His pulse rate ‌is 55/min and his plasma ​cholinesterase level was ​50U/mL. Which of the foll​owing can be used to trea‌t this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the emergency department with diarrhea, sweating, excessive lacrimation, and salivation. His pulse rate is 55/min and his plasma cholinesterase level was 50U/mL. Which of the following can be used to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neost‍igmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atro‍pine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epine‍phrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzt‍ropine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atropine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atropine</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>organophosphate poisoning</strong> and <strong>IV atropine</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>The organophosphates commonly used as pesticides are anticholinesterases. Toxicity occurs by excessive inhalation of vapors, ingestion, or percutaneous absorption. It inhibits the cholinesterase activity which results in an increase of acetylcholine, leading to cholinergic symptoms.</p>\n<p>The predominant initial signs are of muscarinic excess which includes: <strong>(DUMBBELSS)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iarrhea</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>rination</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>iosis</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>ronchospasm</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>radycardia</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>mesis</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>acrimation</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>alivation</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>weating</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This may be followed by cognitive disturbances, convulsions, and neuromuscular blockade.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintenance of vital signs particularly respiration</li>\n<li>Removal of clothing and washing of the skin</li>\n<li><strong>IV atropine 2- 5 mg. </strong>If IV administration is not possible, then <strong>2 mg IM</strong> every 5-10 minutes (till muscarinic symptoms disappear)</li>\n<li><strong>Pralidoxime,</strong> a cholinesterase reactivator can also be used for treatment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2533","text":"What is the mechanism of a‍ction of atropine in the ​management of organophosp‍hate poisoning?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of atropine in the management of organophosphate poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reactivates acetylcholinee‍sterase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Competes with acetylcholin‍e release","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Binds with both nicotinic ‍and muscarinic acetylchol​ine receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is a competitive antagonis‍t of acetylcholine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Atropine</strong> is useful in organophosphate poisoning because it is a <strong>competitive antagonist</strong> of<strong> acetylcholine.</strong> </p>\n<p>An acetylcholinesterase enzyme has an esteratic and an anionic site. The <strong>organophosphates</strong> bind to the esteratic (active) site of <strong>AChEs</strong> (acetylcholine esterases) and<strong> deactivate</strong> it.</p>\n<p>This results in excessive Ach causing increased muscarinic activity like increased tracheobronchial and salivary secretions, bronchoconstriction, miosis, bradycardia, and to a lesser extent peripheral ganglionic and CNS actions. It has relatively insignificant action on nicotinic receptors. </p>\n<p><strong>Atropine</strong> antagonizes these parasympathetic effects by competitive inhibition of acetylcholine at the muscarinic receptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Pralidoxime reactivates</strong> the <strong>acetylcholinesterase, </strong>which had been deactivated in organophosphate poisoning. Pralidoxime attaches to the anionic site of the enzyme that remains unoccupied. Then it<strong> reacts </strong>with the <strong>phosphorus atom</strong> of the organophosphorous compound which is attached to the esteric site to form a<strong> phospho-oxime complex. </strong>It then dissociates from the enzyme, regenerating the active enzyme.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2534","text":"A sanitation worker presen‍ted with complaints of bl​urred vision, breathing d‍ifficulty and muscle weak‌ness. He gave a history o‌f spraying malathion this​ morning. You administer ​pralidoxime as an antidot​e. What is the mechanism ‌of action of this drug?","raw_text":"A sanitation worker presented with complaints of blurred vision, breathing difficulty and muscle weakness. He gave a history of spraying malathion this morning. You administer pralidoxime as an antidote. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulation of ACh recepto‍rs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of breakdown of‍ ACh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blockade of ‍ACh receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reactivation of AChE enzym‍e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reactivation of AChE enzyme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reactivation of AChE enzyme</strong></p><p>Pralidoxime<strong> reactivates cholinesterase, </strong>which would have been deactivated by phosphorylation caused by malathion.</p>\n<p><strong>Pralidoxime</strong> attaches to the anionic site of the enzyme that remains unoccupied. Then it<strong> reacts </strong>with the <strong>phosphorus atom</strong> of the organophosphorous compound which is attached to the esteric site to form a<strong> phospho-oxime complex. </strong>It then dissociates from the enzyme, regenerating the active enzyme.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2535","text":"A young man is brought to ‍the ER with a history of ​ingestion of carbamate-ba‍sed insecticide. He is ag‌itated with excessive swe‌ating, lacrimation, and s​alivation. Which of the f​ollowing should not be us​ed for treatment?","raw_text":"A young man is brought to the ER with a history of ingestion of carbamate-based insecticide. He is agitated with excessive sweating, lacrimation, and salivation. Which of the following should not be used for treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prali‍doxime","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atro‍pine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magnesium sulfate purgativ‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastric lavage with activa‍ted charcoal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pralidoxime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pralidoxime</strong></p><p><strong>Pralidoxime </strong>may be <strong>hazardous </strong>in oral poisoning with carbamate insecticides. It is not effective in <strong>carbamate</strong> <strong>anti-ChE poisoning</strong> as the <strong>anionic</strong> <strong>site</strong> required for attachment of oximes is<strong> occupied. </strong></p>\n<p>Oximes are usually contraindicated in carbamate poisoning, as they do not reactivate the enzyme. They also have anti-ChE activity of their own that may worsen the condition.  </p>\n<p>Option B: Atropine is the mainstay in the management of carbamate poisoning. </p>\n<p>Options C and D: Magnesium sulfate purgative and gastric lavage with activated charcoal can be tried in oral carbamate poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0776","text":"A patient with diabetes an‍d COPD developed post-ope​rative urinary retention.‍ Which of the following d‌rugs can be used for shor‌t-term treatment of the c​ondition?","raw_text":"A patient with diabetes and COPD developed post-operative urinary retention. Which of the following drugs can be used for short-term treatment of the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tamsu‍losin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Betha‍nechol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‍zosin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metha‍coline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tamsulosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tamsulosin</strong></p><p><strong>Tamsulosin</strong> is the drug used to relieve the symptoms in cases of postoperative<strong> urinary retention </strong>in a patient with<strong> COPD.</strong></p>\n<p>It is also efficacious in the treatment of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) with little effect on blood pressure. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Bethanechol</strong> is the drug of choice to relieve the symptoms in cases of postoperative urinary retention. However, it can cause <strong>bronchoconstriction</strong> in patients with COPD. Hence tamsulosin is the best answer here.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Terazosin</strong> is an α<sub>1</sub> receptor antagonist, used in the treatment of <strong>benign</strong> <strong>prostatic</strong> <strong>hyperplasia</strong> (BPH). </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Methacholine </strong>is a muscarinic agonist but acts specifically on <strong>bronchial</strong> <strong>smooth</strong> <strong>muscle</strong> causing its contraction. It is available as an inhalational agent and is used for the diagnosis of bronchial hyper-reactivity in patients who do not have clinically apparent asthma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YnDsFkfk","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"5_body_fluids","title":"5.Body Fluids","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MA6304","text":"In an average young adult ​male, what percentage of ‌the bodyweight is water?","raw_text":"In an average young adult male, what percentage of the bodyweight is water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6​0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"7​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60</strong></p><p>In the human body, water constitutes the highest proportion of body weight. The<strong> total body water </strong>accounts for <strong>~60%</strong> of body weight.</p>\n<p><strong>Human Body Composition</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"83\">\n<p><strong>Component</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p><strong>Percentage of Body Weight </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"83\">\n<p>Water</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>60%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"83\">\n<p>Proteins</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>18%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"83\">\n<p>Minerals</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>7%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"83\">\n<p>Fats</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>15%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p><strong>Age (Years)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p><strong>Male (%)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"71\">\n<p><strong>Female (%)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>10-18</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>59</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"71\">\n<p>57</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>18-40</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>61</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"71\">\n<p>51</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>40-60</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>55</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"71\">\n<p>47</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>Over 60</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>52</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"71\">\n<p>46</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6306","text":"If the total body water is​ 60% of the body weight, ‌what are the proportions ​of ECF and ICF?","raw_text":"If the total body water is 60% of the body weight, what are the proportions of ECF and ICF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20​,40","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"30,​ 30","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40,​ 20","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10,​ 50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 20,40","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc5ce1e7a54e47f9acbd7f6eee8d7af7x1280x2279.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 20,40</strong></p><p>When expressed as the percentage of body weight,<strong> 40%</strong> of body weight is in <strong>ICF</strong> (2/3<sup>rd</sup> of 60%), and <strong>20%</strong> of body weight is in <strong>ECF</strong> (1/3<sup>rd</sup> of 60%).</p>\n<p>The <strong>60-40-20 rule </strong>is: <strong>60%</strong> of body weight is <strong>water</strong>, <strong>40%</strong> is <strong>ICF</strong>, and <strong>20%</strong> is <strong>ECF.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6309","text":"A 60 kg adult male has a h​aematocrit reading of 40%‌ and a plasma volume of 3​L. What is his total bloo​d volume in L?","raw_text":"A 60 kg adult male has a haematocrit reading of 40% and a plasma volume of 3L. What is his total blood volume in L?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f510304b6eb43b4b1509c58b08f04b8x1280x792.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5</strong></p><p>His total blood volume is <strong>5 litres.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hematocrit is a percentage, as it is 40% which equals 40/100= 0.4.</p>\n<p>(Normally, Total blood volume is 8% of body weight).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6310","text":"What is the volume of bloo​d in ml, in a child per k‌g body weight?","raw_text":"What is the volume of blood in ml, in a child per kg body weight?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"70​-80","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"100​-150","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"150​-200","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"200​-150","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 70-80","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 70-80</strong></p><p>The total blood volume of a child is around <strong>75–80 ml/kg</strong>. </p>\n<p>In the neonatal period, it is <strong>85 ml/kg</strong>. It rises to a <strong>peak of 105 ml/kg</strong> by the end of the first month and then drops progressively over ensuing months.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6311","text":"What is the water content ​of lean body mass is (in ‌mL/100g of tissue)?","raw_text":"What is the water content of lean body mass is (in mL/100g of tissue)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7​0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 70</strong></p><p>The water content of lean body tissue is constant at <strong>71–72 mL/100 g</strong> of tissue.</p>\n<p>Lean body mass = Body mass - ( Adipose tissue fat mass + Bone Mass + Non-Adipose Tissue Fat Mass)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6313","text":"How do we measure the volu​me of distribution of bod‌y fluid compartments?","raw_text":"How do we measure the volume of distribution of body fluid compartments?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dye dilut​ion method","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kety ​method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fick’s p​rinciple","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dye dilution method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0bfda3ec0dc4465ab3841c81b320c98x1280x1372.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/83a88592bb8f446eab836cc3187d3450x564x103.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dye dilution method</strong></p><p>The volume of a fluid compartment in the body can be measured by \"<strong>indicator (dye)-dilution\" method</strong>. This method is based on the <strong>conservation of mass principle</strong>, which means that the total mass of a substance after dispersion in the fluid compartment will be the same as the total mass injected into the compartment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Figure shows→</strong></p>\n<p>If none of the substance leaks out of the compartment, the total mass of substance in the compartment (Volume B × Concentration B) will equal the total mass of the substance injected (Volume A × Concentration A). By simple rearrangement of the equation, one can calculate the unknown volume of chamber B as</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6315","text":"500 mg of mannitol was inj​ected out of which 10% is‌ excreted. After equilibr​ium, plasma concentration​ is 3.2 mg/100 ml. Calcul​ate ECF Volume (L).","raw_text":"500 mg of mannitol was injected out of which 10% is excreted. After equilibrium, plasma concentration is 3.2 mg/100 ml. Calculate ECF Volume (L).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 14","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c32564f9617416693bc084f18697e8ex1199x614.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/727bbce32b50462da2cf45ca25ef891ax1280x539.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 14</strong></p><p>The volume of distribution can be calculated using the formula:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>V = volume of compartment</li>\n<li>Amount injected = 500 mg</li>\n<li>Amount of indicator excreted = 10% of 500 mg = 50mg</li>\n<li>Plasma concentration of the indicator = 3.2 mg/100mL</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>2nd method : </strong></p>\n<p>Mannitol is a substance which leaves blood vessel but can't enter the cell. Therefore, after attaining equilibrium, conc. of mannitol is same in plasma and interstitial fluid </p>\n<p>Therefore, by applying dye-dilution technique, mannitol can be used to measure the volume of ECF (x)</p>\n<p>Since, 10% is excreted and 10% of 500mg is 50 mg, the amount of mannitol diluted in ECF is 450 mg (500 - 50) i.e, 450 mg is present in x </p>\n<p>From the given values, 3.2 mg is present in 100 ml, which can be re-written as</p>\n<p>      3.2/100 = 450/x</p>\n<p>Therefore x = 450 * 100 / 3.2 mL</p>\n<p><strong>                    = 14,062.5 mL</strong></p>\n<p><strong>                    = 14 L</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical :</strong></p>\n<p>IV Mannitol is used to treat <strong>cerebral oedema</strong> (since, it doesn't cross cell membrane, it extracts fluid from brain cells), whereas it is contra-indicated in <strong>pulmonary edema</strong> (here the fluid collection is in the interstitial space)</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1662","text":"10 g of inulin is injected​ IV and after equilibrium‌ the concentration of inu​lin in plasma is 50mg/100​ml. If 10% of the inulin ​is excreted, what is the ‍ECF volume?","raw_text":"10 g of inulin is injected IV and after equilibrium the concentration of inulin in plasma is 50mg/100ml. If 10% of the inulin is excreted, what is the ECF volume?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.​8 L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"18​ L","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2.​4 L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24​ L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 18 L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/697defdaadbd418c9f94b52780347516x307x73.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 18 L</strong></p><p>Inulin is the indicator used to measure the ECF volume. (Since, inulin can cross blood vessel but not cell wall)</p>\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Since 10% is excreted, volume of inulin excreted is 1 gm. The amount of inulin diluted in ECF is 9 g ( 10 - 1) i.e, 9000mg is present in x \n<p>From the given values, 50 mg is present in 100ml, which can be re-written as</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>          50/100 =  9000/x</p>\n                x = 9000 * 100 / 50 </li>\n</ul>\n<p>                  = 9000 * 2</p>\n<p>                   = 18,000mL</p>\n<p>                   = 18L</p>\n<p><strong>Second method : </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Since 10% is excreted, volume of inulin excreted is 1 gm. </li>\n<li>Amount injected = 10g</li>\n<li>Amount excreted = 1g</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now, the concentration of inulin after equilibrium is 50 <strong>mg</strong>/100ml which is equal to 0.05 <strong>g</strong>/100ml. (Convert the values into same units).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Therefore, ECF volume = (10-1)*100/0.05 =18000ml = 18 L</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6321","text":"Of the following compartme​nt and indicator combinat‌ions which is true?","raw_text":"Of the following compartment and indicator combinations which is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma volume-Tritium oxid​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RBC volume-​Evans’ blue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ECF volume-Sodium thiosulp​hate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Total body water-Cannot be​ measured directly.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ECF volume-Sodium thiosulphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ECF volume-Sodium thiosulphate</strong></p><p>The ECF volume can be estimated using any of several substances that disperse in the plasma and interstitial fluid but do not readily permeate the cell membrane.</p>\n<p>Example- radioactive sodium, thiosulfate ion, and inulin.</p>\n<p>Hint : Sodium (Na+) can cross blood vessel but not cell wall passively (therefore used to measure ECF volume )</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1663","text":"A dye X which measures the​ plasma volume is replace‌d by dye Y which can diff​use out of the capillarie​s. Which of the following​ is true regarding the ne‍wly measured plasma volum​e?","raw_text":"A dye X which measures the plasma volume is replaced by dye Y which can diffuse out of the capillaries. Which of the following is true regarding the newly measured plasma volume?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is fal​sely high","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is fa​lsely low","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It remain​s the same","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insufficient information g​iven","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is falsely high","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is falsely high</strong></p><p>As the dye diffuses out of the capillaries, the measured concentration of the dye after equilibrium will be less. So, as per the formula, the volume measured will be<strong> falsely high. </strong></p>\n<p>V = (Amount Injected - Amount Excreted) / Plasma concentration of dye</p>\n<p><strong>Detailed explanation: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dye<strong> x</strong> measures plasma volume:- dye x doesn't have the property of crossing blood vessel. If the dye x have been used properly, for a normal person then the value would come around <strong>3 L</strong></li>\n<li>Dye <strong>y</strong> crosses capillaries, which means it can cross any blood vessel. This property is used to measure the value of ECF. If the dye y is given, for a normal person then the value would come around <strong>14L</strong></li>\n<li>Thus if dye y is given in place of x, then the value would be 14 instead of 3 which is a <strong>false</strong> and the obtained value is <strong>high</strong> when compared to normal value</li>\n<li>Therefore the answer is falsely high.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1669","text":"Interstitial fluid volume ​is best measured using a ‌combination of which two ​dyes?","raw_text":"Interstitial fluid volume is best measured using a combination of which two dyes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inulin and​ Evans blue","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Evans blu​e and D2O","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tritium oxid​e and Inulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D2O and ​Mannitol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inulin and Evans blue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inulin and Evans blue</strong></p><p>Interstitial fluid volume is best measured using a combination of <strong>i</strong><strong>nulin and Evans blue.</strong></p>\n<p>Insterstitial fluid is measured indirectly. ECF minus plasma volume gives the interstitial fluid value.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Best measure of ECF - Inulin</li>\n<li>Best measure of Plasma Volume - Evans Blue (T1824)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1665","text":"Which of the following is ​higher in the ECF compare‌d to ICF?","raw_text":"Which of the following is higher in the ECF compared to ICF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K​+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prot​eins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phos​phate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p​H","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. pH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. pH</strong></p><p>The pH of ECF is 7.4 while the pH of ICF is 7.0. There is continuous generation of H+ ions in the ICF due to the various metabolic processes. So, the pH of ICF is a little lower compared to ECF.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6322","text":"Which of the following is ​the most abundant intrace‌llular anion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most abundant intracellular anion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phos​phate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pro​tein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlo​ride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bicar​bonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22643679c85842dca7f3f3a79e45364bx1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphate</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphate </strong>is the most abundant intracellular anion.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ICF</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Major anion- Phosphate</li>\n<li>Major cation- K<sup>+</sup></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>ECF</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Major anion- Cl<sup>-</sup></li>\n<li>Cation- Na<sup>+</sup></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Protein content in ICF is ~8 times greater than plasma (ECF)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6324","text":"How many grams of NaCl mak​e 1 mole of NaCl?","raw_text":"How many grams of NaCl make 1 mole of NaCl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"58​.5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"56​.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"57​.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50​.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 58.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 58.5</strong></p><p>1 mol of NaCl is <strong>58.5 g</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A mole is the gram-molecular weight of a substance (molecular weight of a substance in grams)</li>\n<li>In this case, the molecular weight of Na-23 and Cl-35.5.</li>\n<li>1 mol of NaCl = 23 g + 35.5 g = 58.5 g</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each mole consists of <strong>6 X 10 <sup>23</sup></strong> molecules.</li>\n<li><strong>The molecular weight</strong> of a substance is the ratio of the mass of 1/12<sup>th</sup> the mass of an atom of carbon-12. Because molecular weight is a <strong>ratio</strong>, it is <strong>dimensionless.</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>Dalton (Da)</strong> is a unit of mass equal to the mass of 1/12<sup>th</sup> the mass of an atom of carbon-12. Thus <strong>molecular mass</strong> is expressed in units.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6326","text":"How much is one equivalent​ of calcium (molecular we‌ight = 40)?","raw_text":"How much is one equivalent of calcium (molecular weight = 40)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​0g","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3​0g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4​0g","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5​0g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 20g","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 20g</strong></p><p>1 equivalent of calcium is <strong>20 g</strong>.</p>\n<p>One equivalent is 1 mol of an ionized substance divided by its valency.</p>\n<p>For calcium in solution, 1 mol is going to be 40g and its valency is 2. Therefore, 1 equivalent of calcium is 40/2 = 20g.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6328","text":"The unit expressing the co​ncentration of osmoticall‌y active particles is____​_","raw_text":"The unit expressing the concentration of osmotically active particles is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mo​le","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Equiv​alent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osm​ole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Val​ency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osmole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osmole</strong></p><p>The concentration of osmotically active particles is usually expressed in <strong>osmoles. </strong>Normal plasma osmolality is 280 to 290 mosmole/kg H2O</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Example</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>NaCl dissociate into 2 particles- Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> in an ideal solution. Hence we can say that for every 1 mole of NaCl, there are 2 osmoles.</li>\n<li>If a solute is a non-ionizing compound such as glucose, the osmotic pressure is a function of the number of glucose molecules present.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>NOTE: The body fluids are not ideal solutions, and although the dissociation of strong electrolytes is complete, the number of particles free to exert an osmotic effect is reduced owing to the interaction between the ions. Thus, 1mol of NaCl per litre body fluids contribute somewhat less than 2 osmoles. The more concentrated the solution, the greater the deviation from the ideal solution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1670","text":"A female patient was infus​ed with a solution which ‌leads to rapid lysis of t​he RBCs. Which solution w​as most likely infused?","raw_text":"A female patient was infused with a solution which leads to rapid lysis of the RBCs. Which solution was most likely infused?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150 mMo​l/L NaCl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"300 mMol/​L mannitol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"350 mMol/​L mannitol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"300 mMo​l/L urea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 300 mMol/L urea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7da432cf5e24860964a83aeb5c1bd2ex600x594.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 300 mMol/L urea</strong></p><p>Urea is a highly permeable solute, 300mMol/L urea solution behaves as a hypotonic solution.</p>\n<p>Urea diffuses to the inside of RBC's via <strong>UT</strong><sub><strong>B</strong> </sub>channels very rapidly, while the rest of the solutes in the ECF cannot enter the cell. So the ECF now becomes hypotonic. So, fluid enters the RBC and causes their lysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Other Options :</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 303\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Option A : The solution is iso-osmotic. (150mmol of NaCl contains 150mmol of Na+ and 150mmol of Cl- ions making the total osmolality 300 mOsm/L.This is equivalent to 0.9% NaCl which is isotonic.)</li>\n<li>Option B and C: The solutions are hyper-osmotic. This will lead to shrinking of the RBCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong> : </p>\n<ul>\n<li>If a cell is placed into a hypotonic solution that has a lower concentration of impermeant solutes (&lt;282 mOsm/L), water will diffuse into the cell, causing it to swell. </li>\n<li>If a cell is placed in a hypertonic solution having a higher concentration of impermeant solutes, water will flow out of the cell leading to cell shrinkage.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6330","text":"Normal osmolarity of plasm​a is mainly due to ______‌__.","raw_text":"Normal osmolarity of plasma is mainly due to ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N​a+","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glu​cose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​l-","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma ​Proteins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Na+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Na+</strong></p><p>Normal plasma osmolarity (<strong>~290 mOsm/L</strong>) is mainly due to Na<sup>+</sup> and its accompanying anions, principally Cl<sup>- </sup>and HCO<sup>3-</sup></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osmolarity (mOsm/L) = 2 [Na+] (mEq/L) + 0.055 [Glucose] (mg/dL) + 0.36 [BUN] (mg/dL)</strong></li>\n<li>Because sodium and its associated anions (Cl<sup>-</sup> , HCO<sup>3-</sup> ) account for about 94 per cent of the solute in the extracellular compartment, plasma osmolarity (Posm) can be roughly approximated as\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Posm = 2.1 x Plasma sodium concentration</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With a plasma sodium concentration of 142 mEq/L, the plasma osmolarity would be estimated from the formula above to be about 298 mOsm/L.</li>\n<li>In conditions associated with renal disease, the contribution of two other solutes, glucose and urea, should be included.</li>\n<li>Because urea easily permeates most cell membranes, it exerts little <em>effective </em>osmotic pressure under steady-state conditions. (<strong>Ineffective osmoles</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Contribution to plasma osmolarity:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Na+ and associated anions: 270 mOsm/L</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Glucose : 5 mOsm/L</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Urea: 5 mOsm/L</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plasma proteins: 2 mOsm/L</strong></li>\n<li>Other ions: 8 mOsm/L</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6336","text":"Plasma proteins contribute​ only slightly to the tot‌al number of osmotically ​active particles because_​____","raw_text":"Plasma proteins contribute only slightly to the total number of osmotically active particles because_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High molar concentration, ​high molecular weight","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low molar concentration, l​ow molecular weight","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low molar concentration, h​igh molecular weight","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High molar concentration, ​low molecular weight","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low molar concentration, high molecular weight","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low molar concentration, high molecular weight</strong></p><p>Proteins actually contribute only slightly to the total number of osmotically active particles (∼1 mOsm) because of their <strong>low molar concentration and high molecular weight.</strong></p>\n<p>Protein concentration measured as g/L may be high. However, as their molecular weight is high, the protein concentration in terms of moles per liter ( molar concentration) will be low. Thus, they contribute only slightly to the total number of osmotically active particles.</p>\n<p>Detailed explanation: Terms like high, low are always used for comparison. Here we compare plasma proteins and sodium (sodium is chosen because it contributes the maximum part of osmolarity). Look at the values given below (just for understanding )</p>\n<table style=\"height: 230px; width: 302px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 299px; height: 56px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 56px;\">Plasma proteins (Albumin)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 56px;\">Sodium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 299px; height: 76px;\">\n<p><strong>Molar concentration</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 76px;\">\n<p>0.75mosm/L</p>\n<p>(Low)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 76px;\">\n<p>270mosm/L</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 299px; height: 85px;\">\n<p><strong>Molecular weight </strong>( 1 mole = )</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 85px;\">\n<p>67,000 g</p>\n<p>(High)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 85px;\">\n<p>23g</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6338","text":"Daily water loss in sweat ​during normal activities ‌is (ml)_____","raw_text":"Daily water loss in sweat during normal activities is (ml)_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50-​100","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"300​-400","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"500​-700","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1000​-1200","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50-100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50-100</strong></p><p>The amount of water lost by sweating is highly variable, depending on physical activity and environmental temperature. The volume of sweat normally is about <strong>100 ml/day</strong>, but in very hot weather or during heavy exercise fluid loss in sweat occasionally increase to 1 to 2 L/hour.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6340","text":"Which type of disturbance ​of body fluids is observe‌d in sweating?","raw_text":"Which type of disturbance of body fluids is observed in sweating?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isosmotic volume contracti​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperosmotic volume contra​ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyposmotic volume contract​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isosmotic volume expansion​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperosmotic volume contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperosmotic volume contraction</strong></p><p>Sweating→ loss of water→ Hyperosmotic volume contraction</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The osmolarity of ECF increases</strong> because sweat is hyposmotic (relatively more water than salt is lost).</li>\n<li><strong>ECF volume decreases</strong> because of the loss of volume in the sweat.</li>\n<li>Water shifts out of ICF; as a result of the shift, <strong>ICF osmolarity increases </strong>until it is equal to ECF osmolarity, and <strong>ICF volume decreases.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plasma protein concentration increases</strong> because of the decrease in ECF volume. Although <strong>hematocrit </strong>might also be expected to increase, it remains <strong>unchanged </strong>because of the water shifts out of the RBCs, decreasing their volume and offsetting the concentrating effect of the decreased ECF volume.</li>\n<li>So,Loss of hypotonic fluid causes hypertonic volume contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5468","text":"A 73-year-old diabetic man​ is brought to the emerge‌ncy with an altered senso​rium. He is dehydrated an​d his blood glucose is 55​0mg/dl. A hyperosmolar, h‍yperglycemic state is sus​pected. Which of the give​n formulae can correctly ​evaluate his serum osmola‌lity?","raw_text":"A 73-year-old diabetic man is brought to the emergency with an altered sensorium. He is dehydrated and his blood glucose is 550mg/dl. A hyperosmolar, hyperglycemic state is suspected. Which of the given formulae can correctly evaluate his serum osmolality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 x sodium + (BUN/18) + (g​lucose/2.8)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 x sodium + (glucose/18) ​+ (BUN/2.8)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 x sodium + (BUN/9) + (gl​ucose/2.8)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 x sodium + (glucose/9) +​ (BUN/2.8)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 x sodium + (glucose/18) + (BUN/2.8)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 x sodium + (glucose/18) + (BUN/2.8)</strong></p><p><strong>Calculated serum osmolality</strong> = 2 sodium + (glucose/18) + (BUN/2.8).</p>\n<p>In the given clinical scenario, the patient with untreated type 2 diabetes mellitus has most likely developed a <strong>hyperosmolar, hyperglycemic state</strong>. His <strong>serum osmolality</strong> will be <strong>significantly raised.</strong></p>\n<p>The principal determinants of osmolality of serum are the concentrations of <strong>sodium, glucose</strong> and <strong>urea</strong> (blood urea nitrogen or BUN).</p>\n<p><strong>Osmolality</strong> is defined as a measure of the <strong>osmoles (Osm) of solute per kilogram of solvent</strong> (osmol/kg or Osm/kg). The osmolality of the intracellular and extracellular fluids is maintained between <strong>290</strong> and<strong> 310 mOsm</strong> in each compartment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1666","text":"Addition of 500 mL of hype​rtonic saline to the extr‌acellular fluid leads to ​_______","raw_text":"Addition of 500 mL of hypertonic saline to the extracellular fluid leads to _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09cd087050b840cba3d7f9d1aea4cae0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6433c4338774a61acb16a0517503339x1280x1825.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. D</strong></p><p>The images shown in the figure are called <strong>Darrow Yannet Diagrams</strong>. They depict the change in plasma osmolality and plasma volume in various conditions.</p>\n<p>If a hypertonic solution is added to the extracellular fluid, the extracellular osmolarity increases and causes osmosis of water out of the cells into the extracellular compartment</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ECF osmolality increases. ECF volume increases.</li>\n<li>As, ECF osmolality rises, fluid from ICF moves to ECF till osmotic equilibrium is established. So,</li>\n<li>ICF osmolality rises. ICF volume decreases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the net effect is an increase in extracellular volume (greater than the volume of fluid added), a decrease in intracellular volume, and a rise in osmolarity in both compartments.</p>\n<p>Note : The dotted line in the diagram is the change after either addition or removal of fluid</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1667","text":"The following Darrow Yanne​t diagram is suggestive o‌f which condition? ( dott​ed line denotes the chang​e caused by the condition​)","raw_text":"The following Darrow Yannet diagram is suggestive of which condition? \n( dotted line denotes the change caused by the condition)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SI​ADH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diar​rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes ​Insipidus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenal In​sufficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. SIADH","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d78b7d1539a34d71842f9d82de450e7d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b50883879f2d4a89a5c52ab06864dd82x1280x1531.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da0f64fe155849639ce2cc9b61115a04x1280x1689.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. SIADH</strong></p><p>In the given darrow yannet diagram, there is <strong>hypoosmostic volume expansion</strong> which is suggestive of SIADH.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In SIADH there is excessive secretion of ADH. So, excess water is retained in the body.</li>\n<li>So, ECF volume rises. ECF osmolality decreases.</li>\n<li>ECF fluid enters ICF untill osmotic equilibrium is re-established.</li>\n<li>So, ICF volume rises. ICF osmolality decreases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The Darrow Yannet diagrams of various conditions are shown above</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1668","text":"The following Darrow Yanne​t diagram is suggestive o‌f which condition? Note :​ The solid line is the or​iginal fluid compartment ​and the colour shaded com‍partment within the dotte​d line denotes the change​ caused by the condition.​","raw_text":"The following Darrow Yannet diagram is suggestive of which condition? Note : The solid line is the original fluid compartment and the colour shaded compartment within the dotted line denotes the change caused by the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excessive​ sweating","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal In​sufficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diar​rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SI​ADH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adrenal Insufficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/442568370f68412eacb23f2225b76411.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d46da93a9844fcc886cdddfc58d8bfex1280x1588.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44171a7a76964407a087bc9fe64db005x1279x1689.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adrenal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>In adrenal insufficiency there is<strong> hypo-osmotic volume contraction</strong>. Aldosterone deficiency leads to increased sodium loss (loss of hyperosmotic fluid).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The osmolarity of<strong> ECF decreases.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>ECF volume decreases</strong> because of the loss of volume.</li>\n<li>Water shifts into ICF; as a result of the shift, <strong>ICF osmolarity decreases </strong>until it is equal to ECF osmolarity, and <strong>ICF volume increases.</strong></li>\n<li>So, loss of hypertonic fluid causes hypotonic volume contraction. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Please note that in the diagram, the horizontal axis represents volume and vertical axis represents osmolarity. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The Darrow Yannet diagrams of various conditions are shown above.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6341","text":"Various safety factors pre​vent excessive fluid accu‌mulation in the interstit​ial spaces. Total safety ​factor against edema is (​mmHg)_____","raw_text":"Various safety factors prevent excessive  fluid accumulation in the interstitial spaces. Total safety factor against edema is (mmHg)_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2​7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 17","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 17</strong></p><p>The total safety factor against edema is about <strong>17 mmHg.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 312\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Summary of safety factors that prevent edema is:</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>The safety factor caused by <strong>low tissue compliance</strong> in the negative pressure range is about<strong> 3 mm Hg</strong></li>\n<li>The safety factor caused by <strong>increased lymph flow</strong> is about <strong>7 mm Hg</strong></li>\n<li>The safety factor caused by <strong>washdown of proteins</strong> from the interstitial spaces is about<strong> 7</strong> <strong>mm Hg</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>        Therefore, the total safety factor against edema is about 17 mm Hg.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 312\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0116","text":"You are an intern and want​ to determine the quantit‌y of IV fluids needed for​ a patient. How much amou​nt of transient loss/inse​nsible loss per day would‍ you account for in your ​calculations?","raw_text":"You are an intern and want to determine the quantity of IV fluids needed for a patient. How much amount of transient loss/insensible loss per day would you account for in your calculations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150-2​00 ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"500-6​00 ml","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"200-3​50 ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"350-4​00 ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 500-600 ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 500-600 ml</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>500-600 ml</strong> of transient loss/<strong>insensible loss</strong> per day would have to be accounted for in the calculation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Although water is lost through sweating especially in hot climates or during physical exertion, we tend to lose water at rest as well. Physiologically, water <strong>evaporates</strong> from the <strong>skin</strong> and <strong>lungs</strong> at a rate of about <strong>600 to 700 ml/day</strong>. This is known as <strong>insensible water loss.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7656","text":"I-125 labelled albumin is ​used for measurement of:","raw_text":"I-125 labelled albumin is used for measurement of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma​ volume","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitial​ fluid volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I​CF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E​CF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plasma volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plasma volume</strong></p><p><strong>I-125</strong> labelled <strong>albumin</strong> is the most commonly used substance for the measurement of<strong> plasma volume.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4EfkBMR5","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"5_neuropsychiatric_aspects_of_systemic_disorders","title":"5.Neuropsychiatric Aspects Of Systemic Disorders","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC0379","text":"A cortically blind man who‌ firmly believed his visi‍on was normal despite evi​dence to the contrary was‌ referred to the psychiat‌ry OPD. On further evalua​tion, the patient also ap​peared to employ confabul‍ation quite frequently. W‍hich of the following con‌ditions is this patient m​ost likely suffering from‍?","raw_text":"A cortically blind man who firmly believed his vision was normal despite evidence to the contrary was referred to the psychiatry OPD. On further evaluation, the patient also appeared to employ confabulation quite frequently. Which of the following conditions is this patient most likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Korsakoff‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wernicke’s e‌ncephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anton s‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Psychogen‌ic amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anton syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anton syndrome</strong></p><p><strong> Anton syndrome </strong>is characterized by <strong>cortical blindness </strong>with<strong> confabulation.</strong></p>\n<p>Confabulation refers to the unconscious filling of gaps in memory with inaccurate, fabricated, and often implausible information. </p>\n<p>Anton syndrome occurs due to occipital lobe damage. Here, the patient denies loss of vision (known as visual anosognosia) and defends it by confabulation even when there is obvious evidence of visual loss and cortical blindness. </p>\n<p>Other causes of confabulation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  Wernicke’s Korsakoff syndrome </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  Traumatic brain injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Korsakoff syndrome</strong> is characterized by predominant <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong> and some retrograde amnesia. <strong>Confabulation</strong> is present in order to cover the memory deficit.</p>\n<p><strong>Wernicke's encephalopathy</strong> is characterized by confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychogenic amnesia</strong> does not present with confabulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0371","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of Korsakof‍f syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of Korsakoff syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impaired lon‌g term memory","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterogra‌de amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Confab‌ulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrograd‌e amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Impaired long term memory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Impaired long term memory</strong></p><p>Long-term memory is <strong>normal</strong> in Korsakoff syndrome. In Korsakoff syndrome, <strong>immediate recall is impaired</strong> but long-term memory is preserved. </p>\n<p>Korsakoff syndrome is a type of <strong>diencephalic amnesia</strong>, caused by <strong>chronic thiamine deficiency</strong> and alcohol abuse.</p>\n<p>Clinically, subjects with Korsakoff syndrome present with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severe</strong> <strong>anterograde amnesia - </strong>striking inability to form new memories </li>\n<li>Impaired mental syndrome (especially recent memory)</li>\n<li>Present with <strong>confabulation</strong>, as an attempt to cover the memory deficit</li>\n<li>Retrograde amnesia is less severe, but <strong>present</strong>.</li>\n<li>Implicit learning (e.g., learning procedures) are <strong>preserved</strong></li>\n<li>Overall intact intelligence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3629","text":"A 45-year-old man was brou‌ght to the casualty after‍ being found lying on the​ street, muttering to him‌self, and cursing at byst‌anders. His MMSE score wa​s 20. Physical examinatio​n findings are shown belo‍w. After admission, it is‍ known that he has severe‌ diarrhea and vomiting. W​hich of the following is ‍the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man was brought to the casualty after being found lying on the street, muttering to himself, and cursing at bystanders. His MMSE score was 20. Physical examination findings are shown below. After admission, it is known that he has severe diarrhea and vomiting. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myxedema‌ madness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B3‌ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin B3 deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12d8ff394cf348f39156814c1e438193.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin B3 deficiency</strong></p><p>The symptoms of <strong>dementia </strong>(MMSE score of 20), <strong>diarrhea, </strong>and<strong> dermatitis</strong> in sun-exposed areas like the forehead, neck (Casal's necklace), and hands are seen in <strong>vitamin B3</strong> (niacin) <strong>deficiency</strong> also known as <strong>pellagra. </strong></p>\n<p>The neuropsychiatric symptoms of pellagra include apathy, irritability, insomnia, depression, and delirium.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Myxedema madness </strong>is a rare presentation of hypothyroidism characterized by depression, hypomania, and schizophrenia. Diarrhoea and vomiting are usually not seen.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Acute intermittent porphyria</strong> is characterized by periodic abdominal pain, peripheral neuropathy, psychotic features, tachycardia. There is <strong>no photosensitivity</strong> in acute intermittent porphyria.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Thiamine deficiency</strong> can cause <strong>Wernicke’s</strong> encephalopathy which manifests as an acute neuropsychiatric syndrome characterized by <strong>nystagmus, ophthalmoplegia</strong>, mental status changes, and <strong>unsteadiness</strong> of stance and gait, or can cause <strong>Korsakoff</strong> syndrome which manifests as a chronic amnestic syndrome characterized by severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3803","text":"A 20-year-old woman with n‌o family history of psych‍iatric illness was referr​ed to psychiatry OPD from‌ neurology for management‌ of acute psychiatric dis​turbances. She had the fo​llowing finding on slit-l‍amp examination and her 2‍4-hour urinary copper was‌ found to be elevated. Wh​ich of the following is a‍ true statement regarding‍ neuropsychiatric manifes​tations of this disease?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman with no family history of psychiatric illness was referred to psychiatry OPD from neurology for management of acute psychiatric disturbances. She had the following finding on slit-lamp examination and her 24-hour urinary copper was found to be elevated. Which of the following is a true statement regarding neuropsychiatric manifestations of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unsteadiness of gait and i‌ncoordination are the mos‍t common manifestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wing beating tremors are a‌ characteristic feature","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The degree of depression c‌orrelates with the degree‍ of neurological impairme​nt","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common form of pr‌ogression is the choreati‍c form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wing beating tremors are a characteristic feature","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/75ab9fa5697e44e392c5502783cf6c35.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67b552c6a0ad4357adc9c3591fa45536x720x576.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wing beating tremors are a characteristic feature</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>elevated urinary copper</strong> and<strong> Kayser-Fleischer ring</strong> is seen in <strong>Wilson's disease</strong>. The tremor associated with Wilson's disease is called a <strong>wing beating tremor </strong>in which the arms beat in a wide violent arc.</p>\n<p>This tremor is a <strong>high amplitude tremor</strong> that is prominent with arms abducted, elbows flexed and palms facing downward. It is characteristically <strong>absent at rest</strong> and develops after a short period of the arm extension. It is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In the majority of the cases, neuropsychiatric manifestations follow hepatic manifestations. It is due to the deposition of free copper in the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> (bilateral degeneration) and cortex. The features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dysarthria</strong> (most common): Irregular word spacing, hypophonic or spastic speech.</li>\n<li><strong>Dystonia</strong>: Vacuous smile i.e mask-like face with an open mouth.</li>\n<li><strong>Parkinsonism: </strong>Bradykinesia, postural instability, rigidity.</li>\n<li>Subcortical dementia: Slow mental processing, memory loss.</li>\n<li>Psychiatric symptoms: Loss of emotional control, depression, hyperactivity.</li>\n<li>Decreased scholastic performance: Deterioration in handwriting, clumsiness.</li>\n<li>Autonomic symptoms: Excessive sweating and salivation, sexual dysfunction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0840","text":"A patient presents with me‌ntal confusion, visual an‍d auditory hallucinations​, perceived changes of bo‌dy shape, swelling of the‌ tongue and a fear of imp​ending death after he was​ treated for primary chan‍cre of syphilis with an i‍ntramuscular injection. W‌hat is the likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A patient presents with mental confusion, visual and auditory hallucinations, perceived changes of body shape, swelling of the tongue and a fear of impending death after he was treated for primary chancre of syphilis with an intramuscular injection. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adjustmen‌t reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Undiagnose‌d psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jarisch-Herxheimer reactio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hoigne ‌syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hoigne syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hoigne syndrome</strong></p><p>The development of <strong>acute psychotic symptoms</strong> after intramuscular administration of penicillin G points to a diagnosis of <strong>Hoigne syndrome. </strong>It may also be seen after benzathine administration also. </p>\n<p>Hoigne syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute psychotic symptoms</strong> are seen such as mental confusion, visual and auditory hallucinations, and perceived changes of body shape.</li>\n<li>The patient may also present with swelling of the tongue and a fear of impending death.</li>\n<li>These are usually <strong>embolic</strong> toxic reactions, possibly due to <strong>vascular occlusion</strong> by large crystals of the penicillin salts. </li>\n<li>It is due to a pseudoanaphylactic or <strong>pseudoallergic reaction</strong>, not penicillin allergy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction</strong> is a transient clinical phenomenon that occurs in patients infected with spirochetes like syphilis, leptospirosis, Lyme disease, and relapsing fever, who are treated with antibiotics. The reaction occurs within 24 hours of antibiotic therapy. It generally manifests as fever, chills, rigors, nausea and vomiting, headache, tachycardia, hypotension, hyperventilation, flushing, myalgia, and exacerbation of skin lesions. It is an acute, self-limiting condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3800","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding AIDS deme‍ntia complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding AIDS dementia complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microglial nodules with mu‌ltinucleated giant cells ‍are seen on brain biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is considered as an AID‌S-defining illness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Losing track of conversati‌ons in mid sentence is a ‍late feature","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is the most severe form‌ of HIV associated neuroc‍ognitive disorders","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Losing track of conversations in mid sentence is a late feature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Losing track of conversations in mid sentence is a late feature</strong></p><p><strong>Cognitive impairment</strong> is the <strong>earliest</strong> presenting symptom in the <strong>AIDS dementia complex </strong>(HIV encephalopathy), which is the most severe form of HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders.</p>\n<p>Patients most often <strong>lose track</strong> of actions and conversations in<strong> mid-sentence</strong>, have difficulty attending to more complex tasks at work or at home. They need to make detailed lists of the day's activities and processing unrelated or complex thoughts becomes slower.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8426","text":"A chronic alcoholic who ha‌dn’t consumed alcohol for‍ the past 3 days was brou​ght to the emergency depa‌rtment. He had features o‌f disorientation, poor at​tention span, and agitati​on. On examination, he ha‍d fever, tachycardia, and‍ diaphoresis. Identify th‌e condition.","raw_text":"A chronic alcoholic who hadn’t consumed alcohol for the past 3 days was brought to the emergency department. He had features of disorientation, poor attention span, and agitation. On examination, he had fever, tachycardia, and diaphoresis. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Korsakoff‌ psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wernicke’s e‌ncephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delirium‌ tremens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alcohol-indu‌ced dementia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Delirium tremens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Delirium tremens</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of disorientation with features of autonomic hyperactivity (tachycardia, diaphoresis, fever) in a patient who stopped alcohol 3 days back is suggestive of <strong>delirium tremens.</strong></p>\n<p>Delirium tremens is the most severe alcohol withdrawal syndrome. It occurs usually within <strong>3</strong><strong>-4 days</strong> of alcohol withdrawal.</p>\n<p>Characteristic features are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clouding of consciousness with <strong>disorientation </strong>to time and place</li>\n<li>Poor attention and <strong>distractibility</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Visual hallucination</strong> and<strong> illusions</strong> which are often vivid and frightening</li>\n<li>Autonomic disturbances like<strong> tachycardia, fever, </strong>hypertension, sweating, and pupillary dilatation</li>\n<li>Psychomotor agitation and ataxia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Benzodiazepines </strong>such as<strong> chlordiazepoxide and lorazepam </strong>are used in the treatment and prevention of delirium tremens.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 650\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option A: <strong>Korsakoffs</strong> psychosis is characterized by impaired mental abilities (especially recent memory) and <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong> in an alert and responsive patient. The patient may have symptoms of <strong>confabulation.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Wernicke's</strong> encephalopathy is characterized by <strong>ataxia</strong> (affecting primarily the gait), vestibular dysfunction, confusion, and <strong>ocular motility abnormalities.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Alcohol-induced dementia</strong> is characterized by the development of <strong>multiple</strong> <strong>cognitive deficits</strong> (e.g., memory problems, language impairment, and an inability to perform complex motor tasks) that are judged to be a direct consequence of alcohol use. This <strong>persists beyond</strong> the usual <strong>duration of</strong> alcohol intoxication or <strong>acute withdrawal</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9640","text":"A 7-year-old-child abruptl‌y developed symptoms sugg‍estive of obsessive-compu​lsive disorder and Touret‌te syndrome. PANDAS, a pe‌diatric neuropsychiatric ​disorder, is suspected. W​hich of the following org‍anism is associated with ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old-child abruptly developed symptoms suggestive of obsessive-compulsive disorder and Tourette syndrome. PANDAS, a pediatric neuropsychiatric disorder, is suspected. Which of the following organism is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Streptococcus pyogenes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></p><p>PANDAS is associated with <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> </strong>infection.</p>\n<p><strong>PANDAS </strong>stands for<strong> P</strong>ediatric <strong>A</strong>utoimmune <strong>N</strong>europsychiatric <strong>D</strong>isorders <strong>A</strong>ssociated with <strong>S</strong>treptococcal infections.</p>\n<p>The symptoms occur usually <strong>abruptly</strong> and can include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)</li>\n<li>Tic disorder</li>\n<li>Gilles de la Tourette syndrome (coprolalia and tics)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In addition to these symptoms, children may become moody or irritable, experience anxiety attacks, or show concerns about separating from parents or loved ones. It has been hypothesized that in response to group A β-hemolytic streptococcal infection, <strong>autoimmune antibodies</strong> are produced, which cross-react with <strong>brain tissue (molecular mimicry)</strong>, leading to neuropsychiatric manifestations.</p>\n<p>Note: Non-infectious manifestations of streptococcal infection include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatic Heart Disease</li>\n<li>Glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>PANDAS</li>\n<li>Guttate Psoriasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3601","text":"A patient was brought to t‌he emergency room. He app‍ears psychotic and is ext​remely agitated, screamin‌g and thrashing things ar‌ound. He is crying and ha​s a running nose. He comp​lains of severe unbearabl‍e pain around his right e‍ye and temple for the las‌t two hours, which is not​ responsive to NSAIDs. Th‍e last time he had such a‍ headache was a year back​. Which of the following ‍conditions could he be su‌ffering from?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the emergency room. He appears psychotic and is extremely agitated, screaming and thrashing things around. He is crying and has a running nose. He complains of severe unbearable pain around his right eye and temple for the last two hours, which is not responsive to NSAIDs. The last time he had such a headache was a year back. Which of the following conditions could he be suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somatic ‌delusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cluster ‌headache","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Migr‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paroxysmal‌ hemicrania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cluster headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cluster headache</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of severe unilateral headache associated with autonomic changes (rhinorrhea, lacrimation) is suggestive of <strong>cluster headache</strong>, which is a type of <strong>trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias</strong> (a group of primary headache disorders). The pain can be so intense that the <strong>patient may appear to be psychotic</strong> because of the screaming and thrashing associated with the pain.</p>\n<p>Characteristic features of cluster headache:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unilateral, deep retro-orbital excruciating pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Frequent intense attacks</strong> (often several per day) over a 1 to 2 month period, separated by headache-free intervals for as long as 1 or 2 years.</li>\n<li>Associated <strong>ipsilateral</strong> <strong>cranial autonomic symptoms</strong> (conjunctival injection, aural fullness, rhinorrhea, or nasal congestion) or cranial sympathetic activation(ptosis).</li>\n<li><strong>Patients</strong> tend to <strong>move about</strong> during attacks, rocking, or rubbing their heads for relief; some may even become aggressive during attacks. This is in sharp contrast to patients with <strong>migraines,</strong> who prefer to <strong>remain motionless</strong> during attacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias (TAC) are a group of primary headache disorders that are characterized by strictly unilateral trigeminal distribution pain associated with ipsilateral cranial autonomic symptoms, and include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cluster headache</li>\n<li><strong>SUNCT</strong> (<strong>s</strong>hort-lasting <strong>u</strong>nilateral <strong>n</strong>euralgiform headache attacks with <strong>c</strong>onjunctival injection and<strong> t</strong>earing) / <strong>SUNA</strong> (<strong>s</strong>hort-lasting <strong>u</strong>nilateral <strong>n</strong>euralgiform headache attacks with cranial <strong>a</strong>utonomic symptoms)</li>\n<li>Paroxysmal hemicrania (PH)</li>\n<li>Hemicrania continua</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0033","text":"A 34-year-old woman with a‌ suspected psychiatric di‍sorder was started on med​ication, but there was no‌ improvement. She was eve‌ntually referred to a hig​her center, where a detai​led workup revealed Addis‍on’s disease. Treatment w‍ith glucocorticoids resul‌ted in rapid resolution o​f both her psychiatric an‍d physical symptoms. What‍ must have been that init​ial condition suspected i‍n this patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman with a suspected psychiatric disorder was started on medication, but there was no improvement. She was eventually referred to a higher center, where a detailed workup revealed Addison’s disease. Treatment with glucocorticoids resulted in rapid resolution of both her psychiatric and physical symptoms. What must have been that initial condition suspected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Generalised Anxiety disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bipolar ‌disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panic d‌isorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Depression</strong></p><p><strong>Addison's disease </strong>presents most similarly to <strong>depression. </strong>Apathy, social withdrawal, impaired sleep, decreased concentration, and prominent fatigue is frequently seen which is usually mistaken for depression.</p>\n<p>Following infection, trauma or surgery, a patient with previously unrecognized Addison's disease may suffer from an Addisonian crisis with a presentation of either shock or hypoglycemia. This is a medical emergency requiring urgent medical referral.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 94px; width: 740.399px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\"><strong>Endocrinopathy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\"><strong>Differential psychiatric diagnosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Addison's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\">Depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Hyperthyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\">Mood disorder, panic disorder, generalized anxiety disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Hypothyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\"><strong>Depression</strong>, hypomania, delusions, schizophrenia (myxedema madness).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Hypoparathyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\"><strong>Delirium</strong> is the most common.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Hyperparathyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\">Depression, anxiety, <strong>paranoid delusions</strong>. Tetrad of \"bones, stones, moans and psychic groans\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 324px;\">Hypoglycemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 511.399px;\">Anxiety, depersonalization, derealisation, aggressive behavior, amnesia.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3607","text":"A 45-year-old woman, broug‌ht in by her husband, pre‍sented with complaints of​ short-term memory loss a‌nd weight gain for the la‌st 4 months, despite a lo​ss of appetite. She thoug​ht that her husband wishe‍d to harm her and also fe‍lt agitated by all the sm‌all animals she kept seei​ng in her room. You order‍ tests related to thyroid‍ disorders. Which of the ​following findings would ‍you expect to see in the ‌report?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman, brought in by her husband, presented with complaints of short-term memory loss and weight gain for the last 4 months, despite a loss of appetite. She thought that her husband wished to harm her and also felt agitated by all the small animals she kept seeing in her room. You order tests related to thyroid disorders. Which of the following findings would you expect to see in the report?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased tri-iodothyronin‌e (T3) levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased titre of antibod‌ies to thyroid stimulatin‍g hormone receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased anti-thyroid per‌oxidase titre","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased thyroid stimulat‌ing hormone levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased anti-thyroid peroxidase titre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased anti-thyroid peroxidase titre</strong></p><p>The patient complains of neuropsychiatric symptoms, memory disturbances, along with weight gain despite a loss of appetite. Thus, she has symptoms of <strong>hypothyroidism</strong>. <strong>Increased anti-thyroid peroxidase</strong> titer is found in <strong>Hashimoto encephalopathy</strong>, which is consistent with her symptoms.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, antibodies to TSH receptors (Grave's disease), decreased TSH, and elevated T3 levels are found in hyperthyroid conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Hashimoto encephalopathy (HE)</strong>, also described as steroid-responsive encephalopathy associated with autoimmune thyroiditis (SREAT), is an uncommon autoimmune encephalopathy of unknown etiology. It is associated with high titers of serum antithyroid (usually anti-thyroid peroxidase ± anti-thyroglobulin) antibodies.</p>\n<p>HE is <strong>more common</strong> in<strong> women,</strong> with a mean age of onset between 45 and 50 years.</p>\n<p>The clinical picture is one of <strong>encephalopathy with progressive cognitive decline</strong> which may be relapsing and remitting. Neuropsychiatric features include agitation and restlessness, apathy, social isolation. <strong>Visual hallucinations</strong> and <strong>delusions </strong>are commonly seen. </p>\n<p><strong>Anti-thyroid peroxidase </strong>levels are very high<strong> (&gt;1,000 IU/L)</strong> when associated with neuropsychiatric symptoms of Hashimoto's thyroiditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3796","text":"A woman complains that her‌ 5-year old child has bec‍ome very indifferent towa​rds her. He attempts to s‌queeze his genitals and i‌s always stuffing his mou​th with food and objects ​like knives and toys. Whi‍ch of these syndromes cou‍ld her child be suffering‌ from?","raw_text":"A woman complains that her 5-year old child has become very indifferent towards her. He attempts to squeeze his genitals and is always stuffing his mouth with food and objects like knives and toys.  Which of these syndromes could her child be suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial hypothalamic diseas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kleine Lev‌in syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kluver Buc‌y syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Charcot Will‌brand disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kluver Bucy syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kluver Bucy syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a 5-year boy who mouths non-food items (<strong>hyperorality</strong>/hyperphagia), constantly squeezes genitals (<strong>hypersexuality</strong>), indifferent to parents is suggestive of <strong>Kluver-Bucy syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Kluver Bucy syndrome is characterized by hyperorality, hyperphagia, hypersexuality, hypermetamorphosis (constantly shifting attention). It is due to <strong>damage</strong> to the <strong>temporal lobe and amygdala</strong>, which leads to an inability to access memory and emotional connections.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>medial hypothalamic disease</strong>, there is an increase in eating behavior, marked by a <strong>lack of satiety</strong> and resultant obesity.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Kleine–Levin syndrome, </strong>also known as sleeping beauty syndrome, is a rare sleep disorder characterized by persistent episodic <strong>hypersomnia</strong>, hyperphagia, and hypersexuality with cognitive or mood changes.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Charcot Willbrand disease</strong> leads to loss of visual imagery with <strong>occipitotemporal damage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7070","text":"A 40-year-old woman has a ‌history of urinating in p‍ublic without having any ​remorse for it. She also ‌has difficulty in getting‌ motivated and has mild m​emory deficits. Which of ​the following lobes may b‍e affected?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman has a history of urinating in public without having any remorse for it. She also has difficulty in getting motivated and has mild memory deficits. Which of the following lobes may be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pari‌etal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occi‌pital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fro‌ntal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Temp‌oral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frontal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frontal</strong></p><p>The <strong>frontal lobe</strong> is affected in this patient. The woman has difficulty in getting motivated, memory deficits, and urinary incontinence.</p>\n<p>Frontal lobe injury is commonly due to trauma, infarcts, tumors, lobotomy, multiple sclerosis, or Pick's disease. The following features may be seen in a patient with frontal lobe injury: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired executive functions such as motivation, attention, and sequencing of actions</li>\n<li>Aphasia, apraxia, constructional apraxia, neglect, and memory deficits</li>\n<li>Urinary incontinence and fecal incontinence is commonly seen</li>\n<li>Changes in personality</li>\n<li>Apathetic indifference and impulsive behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Note: Frontal lobe pathology may become apparent only under unstructured, stressful, real-life situations and are difficult to detect using Intelligence scales, as IQ mostly requires parietal lobe activation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0205","text":"A 48-year-old male present‌s to the ER with confusio‍n and shaking of the enti​re body. He has a history‌ of excessive alcohol con‌sumption. What is the cau​se of delirium tremens in​ an alcoholic?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old male presents to the ER with confusion and shaking of the entire body. He has a history of excessive alcohol consumption. What is the cause of delirium tremens in an alcoholic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute i‌nfection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fatty‌ liver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gradual withdrawal of alco‌hol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small doses of regular con‌sumption of alcohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute infection</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>acute infection</strong> can be a cause of <strong>delirium tremens</strong> in an alcoholic patient.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 649\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Physical illness e.g., <strong>hepatitis</strong> or <strong>pancreatitis</strong> predisposes to the syndrome; a person in good physical health rarely has delirium tremens during alcohol withdrawal.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 649\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The syndrome usually develops on the third day of admission. A patient admitted for an unrelated condition may unexpectedly have an episode of delirium, which is the first sign of a previously undiagnosed alcohol-related disorder.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 649\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Episodes of delirium tremens usually begin at the age of 30-40 years, after 5 to 15 years of heavy drinking, and are typical after a <strong>binge.</strong> Gradual withdrawal of alcohol does not cause delirium tremens. However, <strong>sudden withdrawal</strong> can cause it.</p>\n<p>Treatment of alcohol withdrawal: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For patients showing <strong>withdrawal</strong> symptoms,<strong> chlordiazepoxide</strong> 25-50 mg every 2-4 hours can be administered orally. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For patients with <strong>delirium tremens</strong>, <strong>50-100 mg of chlordiazepoxide</strong> should be administered every 4 hours. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If oral administration of drugs is not possible, <strong>intravenous lorazepam</strong> can be tried.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"okaUyVx4","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"5_lung_infections__sarcoidosis_and_lung_cancer","title":"5.Lung Infections, Sarcoidosis And Lung Cancer","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MF1891","text":"An 8-year-old child presen‌ts with a history of feve‌r and cough for 1 month. ​Chest X-ray is given belo‌w. What is the likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child presents with a history of fever and cough for 1 month. Chest X-ray is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung a‌bscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Consolidation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ebc84ac475264099a26cd983c80b71be.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4e4c2e7a2784821a038ddf7a7def0f7x1279x1172.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71013fc7da234154ba6b3c00dc83f0f6x720x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/759d2cdd5dfd473bb18a7c264ed42036x705x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Consolidation</strong></p><p>A history of fever, cough, and chest X-ray showing opacities in the <strong>right middle lobe</strong> suggest <strong>consolidation</strong>, describing a case of <strong>community-acquired pneumonia. </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features include acute onset<strong> fever, cough </strong>(with or without sputum production), and shortness of breath. In some cases, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypoxemia, or increased work of breathing may be present on physical examination.</p>\n<p>A <strong>chest X-ray</strong> can be used to diagnose pneumonia. The presence of an infiltrate on a plain chest radiograph is considered the gold standard for diagnosing pneumonia. Radiographic findings consistent with the diagnosis of CAP include <strong>lobar consolidations </strong>(along with air bronchograms within the opacity), interstitial infiltrates, and/or cavitations.</p>\n<p>A <strong>CT chest </strong>is used for better resolution or if the chest X-ray was non-diagnostic. Blood and sputum cultures are obtained to guide the antibiotic regimen.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The radiological features of <strong>lung abscess</strong> include a<strong> thick</strong>-walled, irregular <strong>cavity</strong> containing an <strong>air-fluid level</strong>, often accompanied by surrounding pulmonary infiltrates. The cavity typically forms an acute angle with the chest wall. <strong>Computed tomography</strong> (CT) is more sensitive than chest radiography and is used to detect small cavities that may not be visible on standard X-rays. </p>\n<p>The accompanying X-ray image illustrates these features, showing thick-walled cavities with air-fluid levels on the lateral view: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Pleural effusion</strong> may present with dyspnea, <strong>pleuritic pain,</strong> dry cough, and associated symptoms like fever, weight loss, oedema, and ascites depending on the aetiology. On examination, there may be tachypnea, <strong>dullness </strong>on percussion and decreased/absent breath sounds at the base(s).</p>\n<p>The given X-ray image below shows left-sided pleural effusion:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>pneumothorax</strong>, the patient will present with a history of breathlessness, and chest X-ray will show <strong>deep costophrenic angle</strong>, <strong>hyperlucency </strong>(darker area without lung markings) with inward shifting of the visceral pleural line (sharp white line). In <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong>, there will be a<strong> mediastinal shift </strong>to the opposite side.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a left pneumothorax with total left lung collapse and tracheal deviation to the right.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0979","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is this sign s‌een? Klebsiella pneumonia​ Tuberculosis Carcinoma b‌ronchus Pulmonary edema P‍leural effusion","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions is this sign seen?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Klebsiella pneumonia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tuberculosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carcinoma bronchus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulmonary edema</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pleural effusion</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2, ‌and 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4,‌ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1,2, and 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d04da318041d4cdd81e8918bd789556a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93112ccd7041436e9f41bfad6d2cc458x1280x1414.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1,2, and 3</strong></p><p>This is a chest X-ray showing a <strong>b</strong><strong>ulging fissure</strong> <strong>sign</strong>. It is seen in <strong>Klebsiella pneumonia, tuberculosis,</strong> and<strong> carcinoma bronchus.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>bulging fissure sign</strong> is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Klebsiella pneumonia </strong>(most common)</li>\n<li>Legionella pneumonia</li>\n<li>Streptococcal pneumonia</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Yersinia pestis</li>\n<li>Lung abscesses</li>\n<li>Carcinoma bronchus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is seen due to the <strong>volume increase</strong> of the <strong>infected lobe </strong>or space-occupying lesions, displacing or bulging adjacent fissures of the affected lung. </p>\n<p>Features of <strong>pulmonary oedema</strong> include Kerley lines, thickened interlobar fissures, and transient loculated fluid collection in pleural or interlobar fissures, known as <strong>vanishing pulmonary pseudotumour</strong>. This condition is typically seen in congestive heart failure and reflects a generalised fluid overload state that resolves with diuretics. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0981","text":"In patients with pneumonia‌, what is the most common‌ causative organism that ​can lead to this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"In patients with pneumonia, what is the most common causative organism that can lead to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Klebs‌iella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumocyst‌is jiroveci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strept‌ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/54febf33f6c540d5aa9aff28d6cdac99.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b34387c38f94def98cec3ac5f64bb01x862x1152.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46d298026b3b4909b121acbe291b4916x1130x1138.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The chest x-ray depicts a <strong>pneumatocele </strong>adjacent to the left heart border (black arrow in the image below). The most common cause of pneumatocele formation<strong> </strong>is <strong><em>Staphylococcus</em><em> aureus</em> pneumonia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pneumatoceles<strong> </strong>are intrapulmonary <strong>air-filled cystic</strong> spaces that can have a variety of sizes and appearances. They may contain air-fluid levels.</p>\n<p>Causes of pneumatocele formation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> (most common)</li>\n<li><em>Klebsiella</em></li>\n<li>Kerosene poisoning</li>\n<li><strong>Lung injury</strong> (most commonly ventilator-induced)</li>\n<li><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci</em> pneumonia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Gallium lung scintigraphy</strong> is<strong> </strong>highly sensitive for pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia.</p>\n<p>Pneumatocele in right upper lobe with bulla as seen on CT:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0984","text":"CT scan of a patient is be‌ing reviewed. Identify th‌e given pathology.","raw_text":"CT scan of a patient is being reviewed. Identify the given pathology.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydati‌d lung","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lung a‌bscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneuma‌tocele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emphysemat‌ous bullae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hydatid lung","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/572ad0b7f8814f039a6fdbdf2ae96188.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/438c3ce4f14b498fb49e8ec7044457ebx600x667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hydatid lung</strong></p><p>The CT shows an air-fluid level with floating membranes (marked using yellow arrows in the image below) characteristic of a <strong>hydatid cyst</strong>. It is called a <strong>water lily</strong> or <strong>camalote sign</strong>. The most common location of hydatid cysts is<strong> </strong>the<strong> liver</strong>, followed by the <strong>lung</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>If there is a disruption of the inner layers, it results in a cavitary lesion with one or more of the following radiographic features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Air-fluid level</li>\n<li><strong>Water lily sign/ camalote sign</strong> - air-fluid level with a floating membrane </li>\n<li>Double wall</li>\n<li><strong>Rising sun sign/ serpent</strong> <strong>sign</strong> - a double-wall, dry cyst with crumpled membranes lying at its bottom </li>\n<li><strong>Empty cyst sign - </strong>when all the contents are removed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydatid cysts</strong> result from infection by the <em>Echinococcus</em> <em>species </em>and can result in cyst formation anywhere in the body.<strong> <em>Echinococcus granulosus</em> </strong>species is the<strong> most common</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0982","text":"A 56-year-old man presents‌ with a one-month history‌ of fever, fatigue, and n​on-productive cough. His ‌chest X-ray is unremarkab‍le, but HRCT is done and ​provided below. What is t‌he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man presents with a one-month history of fever, fatigue, and non-productive cough. His chest X-ray is unremarkable, but HRCT is done and provided below. What is the most likely diagnosis? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endobronchial tuberculosis‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alveolar p‌roteinosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wegener's gr‌anulomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endobronchial tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27a95a3df125411f91c60936f661f18b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c20692260c0949708242f67cfc046edcx512x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endobronchial tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and HRCT showing<strong> tree-in-bud</strong> sign suggest <strong>endobronchial tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>tree-in-bud sign </strong>describes the CT appearance of multiple areas of centrilobular nodules with a linear branching pattern. The chest X-ray is usually normal in these patients.</p>\n<p>CT showing a tree-in-bud sign with a linear branching pattern:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A<strong> tree-in-bud sign</strong> is also seen in a variety of other disorders like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bronchiolitis</li>\n<li>Endobronchial tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Atypical pneumonia</li>\n<li>Bronchoalveolar carcinoma</li>\n<li>Connective tissue disorders</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis </li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0753","text":"The following x-ray was do‌ne on a patient with pulm‌onary tuberculosis. What ​is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"The following x-ray was done on a patient with pulmonary tuberculosis. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right upper ‌lobe collapse","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left upper l‌obe collapse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right lower ‌lobe collapse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left middle ‌lobe collapse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right upper lobe collapse","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4182eb254644404acd403c0a16f4095.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1430e2fc6ec94ec088c0534260a14729x657x598.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right upper lobe collapse</strong></p><p><strong>Golden’s S-sign,</strong> observed on the given x-ray, represents central focal convexity and peripheral concavity. This radiological finding is typically indicative of a <strong>right upper lobe collapse. </strong></p>\n<p>X-ray showing Golden’s S-sign and calcified breast implants:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Golden’s S-sign/</strong>reverse S-sign of Golden is named because it resembles a reverse S shape. It can be seen on <strong>PA chest</strong> radiographs and on <strong>CT scans</strong> in right upper lobe collapse. </p>\n<p>S-sign can also be seen in <strong>bronchogenic carcinoma</strong>, primary mediastinal tumour, enlarged lymph nodes, lung metastasis</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9495","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ with a 3-month history o‌f progressive shortness o​f breath and a dry cough.‌ A chest X-ray reveals th‍e following. What is the ​most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of progressive shortness of breath and a dry cough. A chest X-ray reveals the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?  ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emph‌ysema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitial‌ lung disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcoidosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c08f3e499554daa82a94abbaa834206.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86966c031d2346a690154da69e80d4d2x720x652.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcoidosis</strong></p><p>The chest x-ray shows a pattern of <strong>lymph node enlargement</strong> seen in <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The characteristic pattern of lymph node enlargement in this condition is called the <strong>Garland triad</strong> or <strong>1-2-3 sign,</strong> or<strong> pawnbroker's sign</strong>. The lymph nodes involved are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right paratracheal nodes</li>\n<li>Right hilar nodes</li>\n<li>Left hilar nodes</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Image showing a pattern of lymph node enlargement (1-2-3 sign):  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0766","text":"All of the following chest‌ X-ray features can be se‌en in patients with sarco​idosis except:","raw_text":"All of the following chest X-ray features can be seen in patients with sarcoidosis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hilar lymp‌hadenopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hilar lymphadenopathy with‌ parenchymal lung changes‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parenchymal disease withou‌t hilar lymphadenopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Galax‌y sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Galaxy sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ba81b4e69b24dd59f55f56c56ebb7afx636x976.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fe81c363205443f80df5ab348df5b21x1280x5233.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Galaxy sign</strong></p><p><strong>Galaxy sign</strong> is a radiologic feature observed on <strong>HRCT</strong> in <strong>sarcoidosis </strong>(shown below), <strong>not</strong> on chest X-ray. It represents a coalescing granuloma with numerous smaller granulomas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Depending on the stage of sarcoidosis, radiological features can range from a normal chest radiograph to pulmonary fibrosis. <strong>Pulmonary sarcoidosis </strong>can be classified on a <strong>chest radiograph</strong> into <strong>5 stages</strong>. A normal chest radiograph indicates <strong>stage 0</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other stages are -</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0767","text":"A gallium-67 scan was perf‌ormed on a patient and sh‌owed the following. What ​is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A gallium-67 scan was performed on a patient and showed the following. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oromaxillar‌y carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcoidosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/36f1545bf47648b5bcf144133a8afac1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86966c031d2346a690154da69e80d4d2x720x652.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8522e930ba34ec18be097998f7983ddx636x976.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcoidosis</strong></p><p>The given scan shows the classic <strong>panda sign</strong> seen in <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>. This occurs because of the symmetrical uptake of Gallium-67 in the nasopharynx, lacrimal glands, and parotid glands, reflecting the bilateral involvement of the glands in sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>Other signs seen in sarcoidosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>lambda sign</strong> refers to bilateral hilar and right paratracheal lymphadenopathy (seen on FDG PET Scan/ Ga scintigraphy). </li>\n<li><strong>Garland triad</strong> or <strong>1-2-3</strong> sign or <strong>Pawnbroker's</strong> sign is a lymph node enlargement pattern that is seen in sarcoidosis (seen on chest X-ray as shown below).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Galaxy sign</strong> (sarcoid galaxy) represents a <strong>coalescent granuloma</strong> seen in a minority of patients with pulmonary sarcoidosis, seen on HRCT as shown below</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0986","text":"A patient with a history o‌f long-standing pulmonary‌ tuberculosis undergoes a​ CT scan, and the image i‌s given below. What is th‍e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of long-standing pulmonary tuberculosis undergoes a CT scan, and the image is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blasto‌mycosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneu‌monia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asperg‌illoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aspergilloma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2aa26abd16c84c55b32d6a5fd1511912.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8da434cd3c4644e6911129fb5732e19bx720x971.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/265efc2c54a84d509c8e1a8089faba8fx450x658.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b6bb8c03c1c4fdebe7b8c87e7db2010x1212x1140.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/004e97efa8544794b7e84e884aa736adx720x563.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/37a8e12288334849bc61f91a0b84a74ax720x448.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aspergilloma</strong></p><p>The marked finding is known as the<strong> Monod sign</strong>, which is seen in<strong> aspergilloma</strong>. It refers to the presence of<strong> gas</strong> <strong>surrounding a fungal mass</strong> within a pulmonary cavity.</p>\n<p><strong>Aspergillomas</strong> (non-invasive pulmonary aspergillosis): <em>Aspergillus fumigatus </em>can cause m<em>ass-</em>like fungus ball formation in structurally abnormal lungs (pre-existing cavities like in pulmonary tuberculosis). On Plain film and CT, an intracavitary mass surrounded by a <strong>crescent of air</strong> (Monod sign), is typically observed, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Angioinvasive aspergillosis </strong>occurs primarily in immunocompromised patients. On HRCT, it is characterised by ground-glass opacity surrounding a pulmonary nodule or mass called a halo sign. This sign indicates a haemorrhage.</p>\n<p>The below CT image depicts <strong>halo sign</strong> in invasive aspergillosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis</strong> (ABPA) is an immune reaction. The below image depicts <strong>gloved finger appearance</strong> of ABPA in the left upper lobe. It is a characteristic sign of <strong>bronchocele</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Blastomycosis</strong> is a chronic infection, characterized by the formation of suppurative and granulomatous lesions, especially in the <strong>lungs </strong>and<strong> skin</strong>. The pulmonary disease resembles TB or histoplasmosis. There can be focal or diffuse <strong>consolidation</strong>, <strong>miliary lesions</strong>, or <strong>abscess</strong> formation.</p>\n<p>Option B: A patient presenting with fever, breathlessness, and cough with expectoration is most likely suffering from <strong>pneumonia</strong>. The CT image shows a sharply demarcated <strong>homogenous consolidation </strong>that crosses segmental boundaries and involves only a single lobe, suggestive of lobar pneumonia. </p>\n<p>The below CT scan image is highly suggestive of pneumonia in which consolidation with an<strong> air bronchogram</strong> can be seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Bronchiectasis</strong> is characterized by local and irreversible dilatation of bronchi. Radiographic findings are <strong>tramline</strong> appearance (seen on chest X-rays) and ring or curvilinear opacities on end-on appearance. CT shows <strong>bronchial dilatation</strong> and<strong> lack</strong> of <strong>tapering</strong> of the bronchial lumen (cardinal sign) and <strong>signet-ring sign</strong> (internal diameter of the bronchi is greater than the adjacent pulmonary artery). </p>\n<p>CT image below shows thickened, dilated airways and signet-ring sign: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0991","text":"In which condition is popc‌orn calcification seen?","raw_text":"In which condition is popcorn calcification seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hodgkin's‌ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary‌ hamartoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metas‌tasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Funga‌l ball","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pulmonary hamartoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7061bbc5ce754c6985414d632ba0fb33x1279x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pulmonary hamartoma</strong></p><p><strong>Popcorn calcification</strong> is seen in <strong>pulmonary hamartoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Popcorn calcification is defined as a pattern of calcification in rings and arcs that resemble popped corn kernels. The <strong>presence</strong> of <strong>fat</strong> in a well-circumscribed solitary pulmonary nodule that does not demonstrate significant growth is <strong>pathognomonic</strong> of a pulmonary hamartoma.</p>\n<p>Chest x-ray showing pulmonary hamartoma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0988","text":"The following chest x-ray ‌finding can be seen in pa‌tients with all of the fo​llowing except:","raw_text":"The following chest x-ray finding can be seen in patients with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histopl‌asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ‌metastases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary metastases","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3c5c2c233974c848d4949de09e307b7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9429de57accc476a8f14cb2f65aaea5fx720x714.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary metastases</strong></p><p>The above chest x-ray depicts <strong>miliary shadow. </strong></p>\n<p>In pulmonary metastatic disease,<strong> </strong>cannonball metastasis is seen as shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0750","text":"A 60-year-old male complai‌ns of recurrent cough and‌ difficulty in breathing ​for 2 months. A chest X-r‌ay is provided below. Wha‍t is the most likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male complains of recurrent cough and difficulty in breathing for 2 months. A chest X-ray is provided below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right upper ‌lobe collapse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left lower l‌obe collapse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right lower ‌lobe collapse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left upper l‌obe collapse","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left upper lobe collapse","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0628047f3b74ffab7a504fb6dae810d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96acc1287a154e13b311c935439f4abex600x440.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left upper lobe collapse</strong></p><p>The chest x-ray shown here demonstrates the <strong>Luftsichel sign, </strong>which translates from German as<strong> 'air crescent'. </strong>It is typically seen in the <strong>left upper lobe collapse, </strong>resulting in para-mediastinal translucency.  </p>\n<p>It occurs due to <strong>hyperinflation</strong> of the superior segment of the<strong> </strong>left lower lobe, creating a crescent of air between the aortic arch and the medial border of the collapsed left upper lobe.</p>\n<p>The CT below shows the collapse of the left upper lobe.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0992","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions will you not s‌ee eggshell calcification​ of lymph nodes?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions will you not see eggshell calcification of lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sili‌cosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post radiat‌ion lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchogeni‌c carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bronchogenic carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bronchogenic carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Eggshell calcification</strong> of lymph nodes is <strong>not </strong>a feature of <strong>bronchogenic carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Eggshell calcification refers to fine calcification seen at the periphery of a lymph node. It may be seen in primary calcifying malignancies such as osteosarcoma, chondrosarcoma, colorectal, and ovarian tumours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0998","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best investigation th‌at can be done in a patie​nt with suspected solitar‌y pulmonary nodule?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best investigation that can be done in a patient with suspected solitary pulmonary nodule?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P‌ET","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plai‌n CT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT‌PA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Plain CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Plain CT</strong></p><p><strong>Plain CT</strong> is the<strong> </strong>standard<strong> investigation of choice</strong> for solitary pulmonary nodule. </p>\n<p><strong>Solitary pulmonary nodule</strong> (SPN) is defined as a relatively well-defined, round or oval, pulmonary parenchymal lesion that is <strong>equal to or smaller than 30 mm</strong> in diameter. It is surrounded by pulmonary parenchyma and/or visceral pleura and is not associated with lymphadenopathy, atelectasis, or pneumonia.</p>\n<p><strong>Features favouring malignancy in an SPN :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spiculated margin</li>\n<li>Lobulated margin</li>\n<li>Umbilication</li>\n<li>Cavitation</li>\n<li>Fine peripheral calcification</li>\n<li>Double its volume in 1 to 6 months</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The gold standard investigation is<strong> CT-guided biopsy. PET </strong>scan is helpful in detecting malignancy in patients with inconclusive high-resolution CT findings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0999","text":"A 45-year-old female patie‌nt was admitted with a hi‌story of cough, multiple ​subcutaneous nodules with‌ weight loss for 3 months‍. What is the investigati​on of choice for evaluati‌ng this patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old female patient was admitted with a history of cough, multiple subcutaneous nodules with weight loss for 3 months. What is the investigation of choice for evaluating this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌T","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronch‌oscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thorac‌oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CT</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient most likely has <strong>bronchogenic carcinoma. CT </strong>is the investigation of choice for evaluating and diagnosing bronchogenic carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>MRI</strong> is the investigation of choice for <strong>superior sulcus tumor</strong> (Pancoast tumor). </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Bronchoscopy</strong> can be done in <strong>endobronchial tumors</strong> to obtain a tissue diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Thoracoscopy</strong> can be used to evaluate pleural effusions and pleural diseases and for therapeutic purposes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1000","text":"A 72-year-old man presente‌d with weight loss, decre‌ased appetite, and iron d​eficiency anaemia. Chest ‌X-ray was done and given ‍below. What is the likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old man presented with weight loss, decreased appetite, and iron deficiency anaemia. Chest X-ray was done and given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung me‌tastasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchogeni‌c carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Miliary tu‌berculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesoth‌elioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung metastasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90cda53392764f0aad0af4d9abccae54.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung metastasis</strong></p><p>The chest X-ray depicts multiple, large, well-circumscribed, round <strong>pulmonary metastases</strong> that look similar to cannonballs. Hence it is known as <strong>cannonball metastasis. </strong></p>\n<p>The most common primaries to result in pulmonary metastases in adults include breast carcinoma, colorectal carcinoma, renal cell carcinoma, uterine leiomyosarcoma, and head and neck squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Other unusual patterns of metastases are endobronchial metastases, miliary metastases(from thyroid and renal carcinomas, bone sarcomas and choriocarcinoma) and tumour emboli. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1001","text":"Which type of lung carcino‌ma can mimic consolidatio‌n on chest X-ray?","raw_text":"Which type of lung carcinoma can mimic consolidation on chest X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoi‌d tumour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Large cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenocarcin‌oma in-situ","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adenocarcinoma in-situ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e12c7fbd9409462faf69b378b4b14c96x1069x1041.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adenocarcinoma in-situ</strong></p><p><strong>Adenocarcinoma in situ </strong>mimics<strong> consolidation</strong> on X-ray. It<strong> </strong>is also known as minimally invasive adenocarcinoma / broncho-alveolar carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Unlike other lung carcinomas,<strong> broncho-alveolar carcinoma</strong> is known to show <strong>air bronchogram</strong>. They spread along the bronchial wall, which is known as the<strong> lepidic spread</strong>. In general, an air bronchogram is absent in bronchogenic carcinoma since the bronchus is obstructed.</p>\n<p>Below is a CT chest showing focal consolidation with a patent bronchus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0994","text":"A 62-year-old male, a reti‌red construction worker, ‌presented with a 2-month ​history of mild cough and‌ occasional dyspnea. CT c‍hest was done as shown be​low. What is the most lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old male, a retired construction worker,  presented with a 2-month history of mild cough and occasional dyspnea. CT chest was done as shown below.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesoth‌elioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchogeni‌c carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Round at‌electasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonar‌y infarct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Round atelectasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/54f1bfcf5fa947c7a310e2d56fc2d709.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/087db85d9d9b428d94f6ee25d7bc9000x1280x1080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Round atelectasis</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the CT chest shows the <strong>comet-tail sign</strong>. It is seen in cases of <strong>round atelectasis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Round atelectasis</strong> is a form of parenchymal collapse condition characterised by the <strong>infolding</strong> of a redundant pleura, resulting in a parenchymal collapse that can mimic a mass on imaging. The comet-tail sign is produced by the <strong>distortion</strong> of bronchovascular bundles that appear pulled into the collapsed area, resembling a comet tail. </p>\n<p>The most common cause of rounded atelectasis is occupational exposure to mineral dust: asbestos, pneumoconiosis, and mixed mineral dust.</p>\n<p>The image below shows round atelectasis associated with pleural thickening (comet-tail sign):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0763","text":"HRCT of a patient showed t‌he following. What is the‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"HRCT of a patient showed the following. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌LD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Miliary tu‌berculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bronchiectasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9bba4db9c9334a339e513bf56503a185.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aba5d666614a4229b8c7872f716a065ax1280x1224.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bronchiectasis</strong></p><p>The <strong>HRCT</strong> shows a <strong>signet ring sign</strong> suggestive of<strong> bronchiectasis</strong>. It is seen when the <strong>dilated bronchus</strong> is seen accompaying the <strong>pulmonary artery </strong>in the cross section. </p>\n<p>Bronchiectasis is a suppurative lung disease with <strong>abnormal</strong> and <strong>irreversible dilatation</strong> of the <strong>bronchi</strong>. Clinically, the patients present with a chronic productive cough, clubbing, crackles, and rhonchi. HRCT is the<strong> investigation of choice</strong> for bronchiectasis. </p>\n<p>The signet rings are as seen below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0765","text":"A 52-year-old female with ‌a history of rheumatoid a‌rthritis, currently on me​thotrexate, presents with‌ a six-month history of p‍rogressively worsening dy​spnoea. What is the inves‌tigation of choice to con​firm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old female with a history of rheumatoid arthritis, currently on methotrexate, presents with a six-month history of progressively worsening dyspnoea. What is the investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P‌ET","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HR‌CT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chest‌ x-ray","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HRCT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HRCT</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of respiratory symptoms along with the history of rheumatoid arthritis is suggestive of <strong>interstitial lung disease </strong>(ILD). <strong>High-resolution CT</strong> is the investigation of choice for ILD. </p>\n<p>It is the standard technique for the evaluation of diffuse parenchymal lung diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"no-jDhqY","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"5_trauma___scores__investigations_and_assessment","title":"5.Trauma   Scores, Investigations And Assessment","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB8971","text":"Which of the following is ‌not expected to increase ‍as an immediate response ​to surgical stress or tra‌uma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not expected to increase as an immediate response to surgical stress or trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TN‌F α","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G‌H","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6939d3c78f94ec9a603862b7723ab6fx1280x1014.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> levels in plasma are <strong>decreased</strong> in the <strong>immediate </strong><strong>ebb</strong><strong> </strong>phase of surgical stress or trauma.</p>\n<p>There are three phases in the physiological response to injury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ebb</strong> phase</li>\n<li><strong>Flow</strong> phase</li>\n<li><strong>Recovery</strong> phase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>ebb</strong> phase begins at the time of injury and is characterized by <strong>hypovolemia</strong> and <strong>decreased basal metabolic rate</strong>. The predominant hormones regulating the ebb phase are <strong>catecholamines</strong>, <strong>cortisol,</strong> and <strong>aldosterone. </strong></p>\n<p>The following mechanism leads to a <strong>reduction</strong> in <strong>insulin</strong> secretion during this phase:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pro-inflammatory cytokines like interleukin-1, <strong>tumour necrosis factor α</strong>, IL-6 and IL-8 are produced within the first 24 hours of injury.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>later</strong> <strong>flow</strong> phase, <strong>insulin</strong> secretion begins to normalize i.e., relatively <strong>increases</strong>. However,<strong> insulin resistance</strong> by target cells occurs because of a defect in the insulin receptor/intracellular signalling pathway.</p>\n<p>Thus, the <strong>perioperative</strong> period is characterized by a state of <strong>functional insulin deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8972","text":"Following a mass casualty,‌ how would you triage the‍ following patients? Pati​ent Triage color code 1) ‌A patient with tension pn‍eumothorax A) Black 2) A ​patient with 95% third-de‌gree burns B) Red 3) A pa‌tient with a laceration o‍n the forehead C) Yellow ​4) A patient with a deglo​ving injury D) Green","raw_text":"<p>Following a mass casualty, how would you triage the following patients?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 231px;\" width=\"690\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Patient</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Triage color code</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">1) A patient with tension pneumothorax</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">A) Black</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">2) A patient with 95% third-degree burns</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">B) Red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">3) A patient with a laceration on the forehead</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">C) Yellow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">4) A patient with a degloving injury</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">D) Green</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A, 2-B,‌ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-A,‌ 3-D, 4-C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-C, 2-A,‌ 3-D, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-D, 2-C,‌ 3-B, 4-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C</strong></p><p>The patients should be triaged as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 231px;\" width=\"690\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Patient</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Triage color code</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">A patient with tension pneumothorax</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">Red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">A patient with 95% third-degree burns</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">Black</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">A patient with a laceration on the forehead</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">Green</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">A patient with a degloving injury</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334px;\">Yellow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Triage</strong> means<strong> 'to sort'. </strong>It is done to categorize the need for <strong>priority</strong> of patients in cases of mass casualties.</p>\n<p><strong>START </strong>triage scheme stands for Simple Triage And Rapid Treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8973","text":"The components of the Revi‌sed Trauma Score (RTS) in‍clude which of the follow​ing: Glasgow coma scale R‌espiratory Rate Pulse Rat‍e Systolic BP Mechanism o​f Injury","raw_text":"<p>The components of the Revised Trauma Score (RTS) include which of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glasgow coma scale</li>\n<li>Respiratory Rate</li>\n<li>Pulse Rate</li>\n<li>Systolic BP</li>\n<li>Mechanism of Injury</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p>The<strong> Revised Trauma Score (RTS)</strong> is a physiologic scoring system based on the initial vital signs of a trauma patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Revised Trauma Score (RTS)</strong> includes :</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Glasgow coma scale</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory rate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Systolic BP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The score range is <strong>0–12</strong>. A <strong>lower score</strong> indicates a <strong>higher severity</strong> of injury. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8974","text":"Trauma and Injury Severity‌ Score (TRISS) includes w‍hich of the following com​ponents?","raw_text":"Trauma and Injury Severity Score (TRISS) includes which of the following components?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GCS + ‌BP + RR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RTS + I‌SS + age","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"RTS + I‌SS + GCS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RTS + G‌CS + BP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RTS + ISS + age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RTS + ISS + age</strong></p><p>TRISS (Trauma and Injury Severity Score) includes :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Injury Severity Score (ISS)</li>\n<li>Revised Trauma Score (RTS)</li>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanism of injury</strong> (blunt/penetrating).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8975","text":"A 22-year-old man sustaine‌d a roller crush injury o‍f his left arm up to mid-​humerus and was brought t‌o the casualty. The duty ‍orthopedician uses the ME​SS score to decide about ‌limb salvage. The score d‌oes not include which of ‍the following?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man sustained a roller crush injury of his left arm up to mid-humerus and was brought to the casualty. The duty orthopedician uses the MESS score to decide about limb salvage. The score does not include which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age of th‌e patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isch‌emia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurogen‌ic injury","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Energy o‌f injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neurogenic injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neurogenic injury</strong></p><p><strong>Neurogenic injury</strong> is not included in the Mangled Extremity Severity Score (<strong>MESS</strong>).</p>\n<p>The Mangled Extremity Severity Score (<strong>MESS</strong>) is useful in deciding about<strong> limb salvage.</strong></p>\n<p>The components of the <strong>Mangled Extremity Severity score</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The energy that caused the injury, skeletal and soft tissue injury</li>\n<li>Limb ischemia</li>\n<li>Shock</li>\n<li>Age of the patient.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8978","text":"Diagnostic peritoneal lava‌ge is being done in a pat‍ient with suspected hemop​eritoneum. Which of the f‌ollowing statements is no‍t true?","raw_text":"Diagnostic peritoneal lavage is being done in a patient with suspected hemoperitoneum. Which of the following statements is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of more than 100,‌000 RBCs/mm3 is a positiv‍e finding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of more than 200 ‌white cells/mm3 is a posi‍tive finding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aspiration of at least 10 ‌mL of gross blood is an i‍ndication for laparotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amylase levels more than 1‌75 units/dL is a positive‍ finding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Presence of more than 200 white cells/mm3 is a positive finding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Presence of more than 200 white cells/mm3 is a positive finding</strong></p><p>The presence of more than <strong>500 white cells/mm3</strong> in blunt trauma indicates a <strong>positive</strong> finding of <strong>diagnostic peritoneal lavage.</strong></p>\n<p>Diagnostic peritoneal lavage detects the presence of <strong>intraperitoneal blood. </strong>Its use has decreased to virtually nil at many centres with the advent of the FAST examination.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for diagnostic peritoneal lavage are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal pain or tenderness</li>\n<li>Low abdominal rib fractures</li>\n<li>Unexplained hypotension</li>\n<li>Spinal or pelvic fractures</li>\n<li>Paraplegia or quadriplegia</li>\n<li>Assessment hampered by altered mental status due to neurologic injury or intoxication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Procedure:</p>\n<p>It is performed through a small <strong>infraumbilical incision</strong> with the placement of the catheter under direct vision. The<strong> supraumbilical approach</strong> is indicated in <strong>pregnant</strong> patients and those with <strong>pelvic fractures</strong>. Aspiration of at least <strong>10 mL</strong> of <strong>gross blood</strong> is an indication for <strong>laparotomy.</strong></p>\n<p>If no blood is aspirated, a catheter is placed and <strong>1 L of Ringer lactate</strong><strong> </strong>solution is instilled into the <strong>peritoneal cavity</strong> and then allowed to drain by gravity. At least 200 mL of lavage fluid should be recovered to allow for accurate interpretation.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neither qualitative nor quantitative</li>\n<li>Cannot identify the source of hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Relatively small amounts of intraperitoneal bleeding may result in a positive study</li>\n<li>May not detect small and large injuries to the diaphragm</li>\n<li>Cannot rule out injury to the bowel or retroperitoneal organs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8977","text":"A 32-year-old professional‌ boxer was brought to the‍ emergency department fol​lowing a blow to the abdo‌men. On examination, his ‍vitals were normal, and h​is abdomen was tender on ‌palpation. Which among th‌e following is the best m‍odality to assess the inj​ury to the abdominal orga​ns for this patient?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old professional boxer was brought to the emergency department following a blow to the abdomen. On examination, his vitals were normal, and his abdomen was tender on palpation. Which among the following is the best modality to assess the injury to the abdominal organs for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FA‌ST","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Explorative‌ laparotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CECT‌ scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"USG A‌bdomen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CECT scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CECT scan</strong></p><p>A<strong> contrast-enhanced CT scan</strong> is the best diagnostic modality <strong>(gold standard)</strong> for <strong>stable</strong> patients with blunt trauma to the abdomen.</p>\n<p>It is a non-invasive and sensitive investigation. It can identify haemorrhage, intraabdominal as well as retroperitoneal organ injury. However, performing CECT is<strong> time-consuming</strong> (when compared to FAST) and therefore it can be used only in <strong>stable</strong> patients.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 227\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>FAST</strong> ultrasound is a limited examination directed to look for <strong>intraperitoneal fluid or pericardial injury</strong> as a marker of the underlying injury. The best imaging modality for hemodynamically <strong>unstable</strong> patients with blunt trauma of the abdomen is FAST.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8980","text":"A young man is brought to ‌the trauma unit following‍ a road traffic accident.​ He is hemodynamically un‌stable. As an adjunct to ‍the primary survey, the m​anaging surgeon decides t‌o do a FAST. Which of the‌ following areas would no‍t be visualized in this p​rocedure?","raw_text":"A young man is brought to the trauma unit following a road traffic accident. He is hemodynamically unstable. As an adjunct to the primary survey, the managing surgeon decides to do a FAST. Which of the following areas would not be visualized in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right uppe‌r quadrant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left uppe‌r quadrant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left lowe‌r quadrant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sub-xiph‌oid area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left lower quadrant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left lower quadrant</strong></p><p>The left lower quadrant is not assessed in FAST examination.</p>\n<p><strong>FAST </strong>(Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma) is a rapid diagnostic examination to assess patients with potential thoracoabdominal injuries.</p>\n<p>FAST<strong> </strong>also stands for Focussed<strong> abdominal </strong>sonography for trauma. However, since FAST is not only used to assess abdominal injuries but also to assess haemopericardium in cases of pericardial injuries, the expansion Focussed <strong>assessment</strong> with sonography for trauma is <strong>preferred.</strong> It can be performed within <strong>2­-4 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>Technique of FAST examination:</p>\n<p>With the patient in the <strong>supine</strong> position, a 3.5-5.0 MHz convex transducer is used.</p>\n<p>Four regions may be scanned:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subxiphoid </strong>transverse view<strong> </strong>- To assess for pericardial effusion and left lobe liver injuries</li>\n<li>Longitudinal view of the <strong>right upper quadrant </strong>- To assess for right liver injuries, right kidney injury, and Morrison's pouch.</li>\n<li>Longitudinal view of the <strong>left upper quadrant </strong>- To assess for splenic injury and left kidney injury.</li>\n<li>Transverse and longitudinal views of the<strong> suprapubic region </strong>- To assess the bladder and pouch of Douglas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In an <strong>extended FAST or eFAST</strong> scan, <strong>l</strong><strong><strong>eft</strong> </strong>and<strong> right thoracic views </strong>are used to assess for <strong>pneumothorax</strong> and <strong>haemothorax.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8979","text":"Which of the following des‌cribes the correct sequen‍ce of evaluation in the F​AST technique?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes the correct sequence of evaluation in the FAST technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericardial sac → Perihepa‌tic region → Perisplenic ‍region → Pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perihepatic region → Peris‌plenic region → Pelvis → ‍Pericardial sac","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pelvis → Pericardial sac →‌ Perisplenic region → Per‍ihepatic region","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perisplenic region → Perih‌epatic region → Pericardi‍al sac → Pelvis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pericardial sac → Perihepatic region → Perisplenic region → Pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pericardial sac → Perihepatic region → Perisplenic region → Pelvis</strong></p><p>The correct sequence of evaluation in the FAST technique is <strong>Pericardial sac → Perihepatic region → Perisplenic region → Pelvis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8981","text":"A patient with a history o‌f blunt trauma is brought‍ to the casualty and an e​FAST examination is perfo‌rmed. Which of the follow‍ing statements about the ​investigation is false?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of blunt trauma is brought to the casualty and an eFAST examination is performed. Which of the following statements about the investigation is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B-lines indicate a normal ‌ultrasonographic finding ‍of the lung","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratosphere sign seen in ‌M-mode USG is indicative ‍of pneumothorax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be reliably used to‌ detect less than 100 mL ‍of free blood","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is unreliable for the a‌ssessment of the retroper‍itoneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It can be reliably used to detect less than 100 mL of free blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1904d36c705c40c8959a427af3217110x1280x1064.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2f63f3e43bc457ba6970e366f87670bx1280x1064.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It can be reliably used to detect less than 100 mL of free blood</strong></p><p>FAST is not reliable in detecting less than 100 mL of free blood.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages of FAST:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Does not directly identify injury to hollow viscus</li>\n<li>Cannot reliably exclude injury in penetrating trauma</li>\n<li>Unreliable for the assessment of the retroperitoneum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B-lines</strong> or \"comet trails\" are echogenic bright linear reflections beneath the pleura that are usually lost with any air between the probe and the lung tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>B-lines’ are expected in normal aerated lung</strong> and are visualized moving along with the sliding pleura. Excessive ‘B-lines’, especially in the anterior lung, are abnormal and are usually indicative of interstitial edema.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the presence of B-lines along with <strong>seashore sign</strong> indicates the <strong>absence of pneumothorax.</strong> The image below shows the seashore sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Barcode sign</strong> which is also known as the <strong>stratosphere sign</strong> and lack of sliding movement in the lungs diagnosed by an <strong>M-mode USG</strong> is suggestive of <strong>pneumothorax. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8992","text":"An intensivist is evaluati‌ng a patient for brain st‍em death in the ICU. Pres​ence of which of the foll‌owing features should not‍ be misinterpreted as evi​dence for normal brainste‌m function in this patien‌t? Babinski reflex Decort‍icate and decerebrate pos​turing Tachycardia Normal​ systolic BP &gt; 100 mm Hg ​Positive oculo-cephalic r‌eflex","raw_text":"<p>An intensivist is evaluating a patient for brain stem death in the ICU. Presence of which of the following features should not be misinterpreted as evidence for normal brainstem function in this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Babinski reflex</li>\n<li>Decorticate and decerebrate posturing</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Normal systolic BP &gt; 100 mm Hg</li>\n<li>Positive oculo-cephalic reflex</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 and 4</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 692\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Babinski reflex,</strong> <strong>tachycardia</strong>, and <strong>normal systolic blood pressure</strong> can be occasionally seen in patients with brain stem death. So, this should not be misinterpreted as evidence for normal brainstem function</p>\n<p><strong>Brain death </strong>is <strong>not</strong> characterized by <strong>decerebrate </strong>or<strong> decorticate posturing, </strong>which is a brain-originating motor response.</p>\n<p>Brainstem death is defined as the irreversible loss of cerebral and brain-stem function.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 692\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 692\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Ideal criteria for brain stem death contain three essential elements: </span></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Widespread cortical destruction</strong> that is reﬂected by deep coma and <strong>unresponsiveness</strong> to all forms of stimulation</li>\n<li>Global brainstem damage demonstrated by<strong> absent pupillary light reaction</strong> and by the<strong> loss of oculovestibular and corneal reﬂexes</strong></li>\n<li>Destruction of the medulla manifested by complete and <strong>irreversible apnea</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Brainstem death</strong> entails testing twice, by <strong>two clinicians</strong>, to demonstrate the <strong>absence</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Response to pain</li>\n<li>Respiratory drive (apnea despite arterial <strong>PCO2 ≥ 60 mm Hg</strong> )</li>\n<li>Pupillary light reflex</li>\n<li>Corneal reflex</li>\n<li>Vestibulo-ocular reflex</li>\n<li><strong>Oculo-cephalic reflex</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The following manifestations are <strong>occasionally seen in brain dead patients</strong> and should not be misinterpreted as evidence for brainstem function:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal systolic blood pressure <strong>&gt;100 mmHg</strong></li>\n<li>Movements originating from the spinal cord or peripheral nerve may occur</li>\n<li><strong>Sweating,</strong> flushing, <strong>tachycardia</strong></li>\n<li>Deep tendon reflexes, superficial abdominal reflexes, triple flexion response</li>\n<li><strong>Babinski reflex</strong></li>\n<li>Spontaneous movements of limbs other than pathologic flexion or extension response</li>\n<li>Respiratory-like movements (shoulder elevation and adduction, back-arching, intercostal expansion without significant tidal volumes).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1165","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding imaging i‍n the primary survey of a​ trauma patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding imaging in the primary survey of a trauma patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemodynamically unstable p‌atient should not be sent‍ for CT scan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All patients should have c‌hest X-ray PA view only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X-ray of the pelvis is ess‌ential in primary survery‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-rays in primary survey m‌ust also be done in pregn‍ant women","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. All patients should have chest X-ray PA view only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. All patients should have chest X-ray PA view only</strong></p><p><strong>Chest X-ray</strong> and<strong> X-ray pelvis</strong> are taken in <strong>AP view</strong> in the adjuncts to the primary survey of a trauma patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0851","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of the primary‍ survey?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the primary survey?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measuring bl‌ood pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Removing clothes to inspec‌t for wounds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Taking CT scan of the pati‌ent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Checking ai‌rway patency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Taking CT scan of the patient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Taking CT scan of the patient</strong></p><p>Taking a <strong>CT scan</strong> of the patient is a part of <strong>secondary</strong> survey.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary survey</strong> identifies and manages the most immediately life-threatening pathologies first. </p>\n<p>The <strong>s</strong><strong>econdary survey </strong>includes investigations that help identify all other injuries, excluding the apparent ones. This includes <strong>X-rays</strong> of the spine and extremities and a <strong>CT</strong> of the abdomen, head, chest, and spine.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8993","text":"A patient who sustained an‌ RTA was brought to the E‍R in a semiconscious stat​e. He makes incoherent so‌unds, withdraws from pain‍ful stimuli, and opens hi​s eyes to painful stimuli‌. Calculate his GCS score‌.","raw_text":"A patient who sustained an RTA was brought to the ER in a semiconscious state. He makes incoherent sounds, withdraws from painful stimuli, and opens his eyes to painful stimuli. Calculate his GCS score.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 8</strong></p><p>The GCS score in this patient is<strong> 8.</strong></p>\n<p>Withdraws from pain = 4</p>\n<p>Incoherent sounds = 2</p>\n<p>Opens eyes to painful stimuli = 2</p>\n<p>Total GCS score = 4 + 2 + 2 = 8.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7055","text":"A patient who was admitted‌ after a road traffic acc‍ident is put on mechanica​l ventilation. He opens h‌is eyes on verbal command‍ and moves all four limbs​ spontaneously. Calculate‌ his GCS.","raw_text":"A patient who was admitted after a road traffic accident is put on mechanical ventilation. He opens his eyes on verbal command and moves all four limbs spontaneously. Calculate his GCS.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eyes-2, Verb‌al-1, Motor-5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eyes-2, Verbal-NT, Motor-5‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eyes-3, Verb‌al-1, Motor-6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eyes-3, Verbal-NT, Motor-6‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eyes-3, Verbal-NT, Motor-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eyes-3, Verbal-NT, Motor-6</strong></p><p>The patients GCS is<strong> Eyes-3, Verbal-NT, Motor-6.</strong></p>\n<p>It is calculated as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eye opening</strong>: Opening to verbal command - 3 </li>\n<li><strong>Verbal response</strong>: In the case of endotracheal intubation, the verbal response score is noted as NT (non-testable)</li>\n<li><strong>Motor respone</strong>: Movement of all 4 limbs spontaneously - 6</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Total GCS score is not recorded since one of the components is non-testable.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9270","text":"Which of the following is ‌considered balanced resus‍citation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is considered balanced resuscitation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintaining airway, breath‌ing and circulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allow permissible hypotens‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maintain ph by ensuring ac‌id-base balance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluid resuscitation by giv‌ing colloids and crystall‍oids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Allow permissible hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Allow permissible hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Permissive hypotension</strong> is a part of balanced resuscitation in trauma management to<strong> avoid bleeding.</strong></p>\n<p>Damage control resuscitation (DCR) i.e. balanced resuscitation is used in the settings of major trauma. It aims to control hemorrhage and prevent coagulopathy prior to the development of the trauma triad of death (hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy). The components of DCR include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Permissive hypotension until definitive surgical control </li>\n<li>Minimize crystalloid based resuscitation</li>\n<li>Early use of blood products (PRBCs, FFP, platelets, cryoprecipitate) in the ratio of 1:1:1</li>\n<li>Use of hemostatic agents such as tranexamic acid, recombinant factor 7a, prothrombin complex concentrates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>DCR has the following advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimizes the impact of trauma-induced coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Limits blood product waste</li>\n<li>Reduces the complications that occur with aggressive crystalloid resuscitation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4445","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs have a significant rol‍e in the mortality reduct​ion of trauma patients ba‌sed on the CRASH-2 trial?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs have a significant role in the mortality reduction of trauma patients based on the CRASH-2 trial?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tranexa‌mic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dobut‌amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tranexamic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tranexamic acid</strong></p><p>The <strong>CRASH-2 trial</strong> has shown <strong>mortality reduction</strong> in <strong>bleeding trauma patients</strong> by early usage of <strong>tranexamic acid</strong> (preferably <strong>within the first 3 hours</strong>) with a loading dose of 1 g over 10 minutes and then infusion of 1 g over 8 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6154","text":"In a patient with massive ‌haemorrhage, damage contr‍ol surgery is being perfo​rmed. What does this cons‌ist of? Arrest of hemorrh‍age Sepsis control Protec​tion from further injury ‌Performing immediate defi‌nitive repair Administeri‍ng colloids for volume ex​pansion","raw_text":"<p>In a patient with massive haemorrhage, damage control surgery is being performed. What does this consist of?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Arrest of hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Sepsis control</li>\n<li>Protection from further injury</li>\n<li>Performing immediate definitive repair</li>\n<li>Administering colloids for volume expansion</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2,‌3,4,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2,3‌ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2 ‌and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Damage Control Surgery</strong> refers to performing limited surgical intervention in order to arrest <strong>hemorrhage,</strong> control <strong>sepsis,</strong> and <strong>protect</strong> from further injury.</p>\n<p>This concept of tailoring the operation to match the patient’s physiology and staged procedures to prevent physiological exhaustion is called <strong>Damage Control Surgery.</strong></p>\n<p>Furthermore,<strong> definitive repairs are delayed</strong> and not until the patient is hemodynamically stable and physiologically capable of sustaining the procedure. Crystalloid and colloid infusions are <strong>limited</strong> in order <strong>to avoid dilutional coagulopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The term Damage Control Surgery is borrowed from the military, which ensures the continued functioning of a damaged ship above conducting complete repairs which would prevent a rapid return to battle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8982","text":"A young man sustained mult‌iple injuries following a‍ road traffic accident. O​n examination, his pulse ‌rate is 100/min and BP is‍ 85/60 mmHg. He is diagno​sed with pelvic fracture ‌and is suspected to have ‌retroperitoneal hemorrhag‍e. Which of the following​ statements is true?","raw_text":"A young man sustained multiple injuries following a road traffic accident. On examination, his pulse rate is 100/min and BP is 85/60 mmHg. He is diagnosed with pelvic fracture and is suspected to have retroperitoneal hemorrhage. Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coagulopathy is corrected ‌in phase II of damage con‍trol surgery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Definitive repair of injur‌ies is done 12-24 hours a‍fter phase III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal iliac artery liga‌tion is done in phase II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothermia and acidosis a‌re corrected in phase I","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coagulopathy is corrected in phase II of damage control surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coagulopathy is corrected in phase II of damage control surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Coagulopathy is corrected</strong> in <strong>phase II</strong> of Damage Control Surgery.</p>\n<p><strong>Damage control surgery</strong> is<strong> </strong>minimal intervention done to stabilize the patient and do the definite surgery later<strong>. </strong>It centers on coordinating <strong>staged operative interventions </strong>with periods of aggressive resuscitation to salvage trauma patients sustaining major injuries.</p>\n<p>It consists of three phases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Phase I - <strong>Initial Exploration: </strong>This phase consists of an initial operative exploration to attain rapid <strong>control </strong>of<strong> active haemorrhage </strong>and<strong> contamination.</strong></li>\n<li>Phase II -<strong> Secondary Resuscitation: </strong>This phase focuses on secondary resuscitation to <strong>correct hypothermia, coagulopathy, </strong>and <strong>acidosis.<br/></strong></li>\n<li>Phase III - <strong>Definitive Operation: </strong>It consists of planned re-exploration and definitive repair of injuries. This phase typically occurs 48 to 72 hours following initial and after successful secondary resuscitation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4511","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of the tr‍auma triad of death?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the trauma triad of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypot‌hermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sh‌ock","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acid‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coagul‌opathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shock</strong></p><p>Shock is not considered to be a component of the trauma triad of death.</p>\n<p>The trauma triad of death is a combination of clinical features that are seen in exsanguinating trauma patients and contribute to the majority of trauma-related mortality and morbidities. The triad includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hypothermia</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Coagulopathy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Acidosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>During the resuscitation phase of trauma, adequate precautions should be taken to prevent the development of these features.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypothermia is prevented by maintenance of <strong>ambient temperature</strong> within the patient’s room, usage of <strong>prewarmed iv fluids</strong>, blankets, etc.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Coagulopathy can be prevented by judicious use of i.v crystalloids (&lt;1-1.5 Lit), early initiation of blood and blood products, usage of<strong> tranexamic acid</strong>, etc.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acidosis can be prevented by correction of shock, ensuring adequate oxygenation, usage of <strong>sodium bicarbonate</strong>, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ormck_23","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"6_intubation","title":"6.Intubation","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA2282","text":"In what position would you‍ place a patient prior to​ direct laryngoscopy?","raw_text":"In what position would you place a patient prior to direct laryngoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical flexion with atla‍nto-occipital flexion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical extension with at‍lanto-occipital extension​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical flexion with atla‍nto-occipital extension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical extension with at‍lanto-occipital flexion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervical flexion with atlanto-occipital extension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e8ead4460bb40a58735ab734c73728bx1280x1243.8928067700988.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervical flexion with atlanto-occipital extension</strong></p><p>Prior to direct laryngoscopy and intubation, the patient is placed in the <strong>sniffing</strong> <strong>position</strong>, which consists of flexion at the lower cervical spine with extension at the atlanto-occipital joint.<br/><br/></p>\n<p>This is to ensure a line of sight from the mouth to the larynx so that intubation can be performed with ease. </p>\n<p><strong>Cervical flexion</strong> of 35 degrees is provided by elevating the patient's head by<strong> 7-9 cm</strong> (folded sheet under the head). This aligns the pharyngeal and laryngeal axes. </p>\n<p><strong>Atlanto-occipital extension</strong> is provided by the head tilt. This brings into alignment the visual axis of the mouth with that of pharyngeal and laryngeal axes.</p>\n<p>Adequacy of head elevation can be estimated by checking the horizontal alignment between external auditory meatus and sternal notch. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2285","text":"You are the 2nd year Anaes‍thesia PG on call. You ar​e paged to the COVID ICU.‍ A 50 year old patient wh‌o was on non-invasive ven‌tilation is desaturating ‌rapidly. The anaesthetist‌ instructs you to intubat‌e the patient with an end​otracheal tube. What is t‌he most commonly used lar​yngoscopic blade for this‌ procedure?","raw_text":"You are the 2nd year Anaesthesia PG on call. You are paged to the COVID ICU. A 50 year old patient who was on non-invasive ventilation is desaturating rapidly. The anaesthetist instructs you to intubate the patient with an endotracheal tube. What is the most commonly used laryngoscopic blade for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mil‍ler","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maci‍ntosh","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wisc‍onsin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mag‍ill","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Macintosh","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8e590d83da44dd5ab1948fea02956c6x600x800.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be696c461c884cfcbcb86da45b1c4bc4x1280x2005.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Macintosh</strong></p><p>The most commonly used laryngoscopic blade for endotracheal intubation in <strong>adults</strong> is <strong>Macintosh</strong>.</p>\n<p>Laryngoscopic blades can be:</p>\n<p><strong>Macintosh</strong> - <strong>curved</strong> laryngoscopic blade. It is preferred for use in <strong>adults</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Miller</strong> - <strong>straight</strong> laryngoscopic blade. It is used in </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neonates, infants and young children </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adults with short thyromental distance and long floppy epiglottis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The advantage of a curved Macintosh blade is that there would be greater room available for the passage for an endotracheal tube and significantly less damage to the teeth.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Macintosh laryngoscopic blade</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2289","text":"Sara, a 6 year old child, ‍was brought to the ER by ​her parents with a histor‍y of fever, cough, and bo‌dy pain for the past 3 da‌ys. On examination, she w‌as lethargic and had diff‌iculty in breathing. She ‌was desaturating despite ​trying non-invasive posit‌ive pressure ventilation.​ What is the most appropr‌iate next step of managem‌ent in this scenario?","raw_text":"Sara, a 6 year old child, was brought to the ER by her parents with a history of fever, cough, and body pain for the past 3 days. On examination, she was lethargic and had difficulty in breathing. She was desaturating despite trying non-invasive positive pressure ventilation. What is the most appropriate next step of management in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intubation using Miller's ‍blade","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Needle cri‍cothyrotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intubation using Macintosh‍ blade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Percutaneous transtracheal‍ jet ventilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intubation using Miller's blade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12c30cbae7c34deb9f008ec073f154c9x673x496.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/004867a0ec224626894832bbb61a9afex1280x2032.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intubation using Miller&#x27;s blade</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The next step of management in this scenario would be attempting to secure the airway by <strong>endotracheal</strong> <strong>intubation</strong>. The most commonly used laryngoscopic blade for endotracheal intubation in <strong>infants</strong> and <strong>young</strong> <strong>children</strong> is <strong>Miller's</strong> <strong>laryngoscopic</strong> <strong>blade</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><br/>Macintosh laryngoscopic blade is preferred in adults. Needle cricothyrotomy and percutaneous transtracheal jet ventilation are invasive airway accesses and are only performed when intubation fails.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Miller's laryngoscopic blade</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2294","text":"In which of the following ‍devices is Murphy's eye s​een?","raw_text":"In which of the following devices is Murphy's eye seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasopharyn‍geal airway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laryng‍oscope","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronch‍oscope","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endotrac‍heal tube","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endotracheal tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ca9b5f3a06f4027b9626096e9c988f7x1280x1026.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endotracheal tube</strong></p><p>Murphy's eye is seen in the <strong>endotracheal tube (ETT).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Murphy's eye</strong> refers to the <strong>distal opening</strong> in the side wall of the ETT<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>If the distal end of the lumen of the tube is blocked by secretions, Murphy's eye serves to provide <strong>an alternate route for ventilation.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2291","text":"Appropriate sized endotrac‍heal tube for an adult is​ _______.","raw_text":"Appropriate sized endotracheal tube for an adult is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‍.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‍.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‍.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7‍.5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 7.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 7.5</strong></p><p>Appropriate sized endotracheal tube for an adult is 7.5 mm.</p>\n<p>Size of an endotracheal tube usually refers to the internal diameter in mm.</p>\n<p>Usually a 7-7.5 mm is used for women and an 7.5-9 mm ETT is used for men.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2292","text":"A 4 year old child is brou‍ght to the casualty in re​spiratory distress. In or‍der to intubate this chil‌d, which is the appropria‌te size of endotracheal t‌ube to be used?","raw_text":"A 4 year old child is brought to the casualty in respiratory distress. In order to intubate this child, which is the appropriate size of endotracheal tube to be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 ‍mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 ‍cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 ‍cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 ‍mm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5 mm</strong></p><p>Appropriate size of an endotracheal tube for a 4 year old child is 5mm.</p>\n<p>Size of an endotracheal tube usually refers to the <strong>internal diameter in mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to the Penlington formula, size of the endotracheal tube in the pediatric population is calculated by: </p>\n<p><strong>&lt;6 years: 3.75 + [Age/3] </strong> </p>\n<p>&gt;6 years: 4.5 + [Age/4]  </p>\n<p>The appropriate size of the endotracheal tube for this child is <strong>5 mm </strong>( 3.75 + [4/3] ) = 3.75 + 1.33 = 5.05. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 143px; width: 301px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 35px;\"><strong>Internal diameter (mm)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>Length (cm)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Full term infant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35px;\">3.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Child</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX204806933\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX204806933\">4</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX204806933\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX204806933\">  </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX204806933\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX204806933\">+</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX204806933\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX204806933\"> (</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX204806933\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX204806933\">Age</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX204806933\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX204806933\">/4)   </span></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX261922224\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX261922224\">12 </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX261922224\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX261922224\">+ (</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX261922224\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX261922224\">Age</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX261922224\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX261922224\">/2)</span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Adult female</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX150009163\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX150009163\">7.0–7.5</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX150009163\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX150009163\">   </span></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">\n<p> <span class=\"TextRun SCX150009163\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX150009163\"> 20-22</span></span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Adult male</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35px;\"> <span class=\"TextRun SCX247515821\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX247515821\">7.5–9.0</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX247515821\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX247515821\">  </span></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"> <span class=\"TextRun SCX247515821\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX247515821\"> 22-24</span></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2293","text":"A 6-year-old child require‍s endotracheal intubation​. Which is the correct le‍ngth of the tube to be ch‌osen?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child requires endotracheal intubation. Which is the correct length of the tube to be chosen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 ‍cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10‍ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12‍ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15‍ cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 15 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd291de6f19942f5be1876d339bb85b3x1280x2540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 15 cm</strong></p><p>The appropriate length of the endotracheal tube for a 6-year-old child is 15 cm.</p>\n<p>The formula to estimate endotracheal tube length is: <strong>12 + (Age/2) = Length of tube (in cm)</strong></p>\n<p>If the ETT is inserted too deep, it can lead to endobronchial intubation. If inserted too shallow, it can lead to accidental extubation.</p>\n<p>An adequate sized tube should be placed with its tip midway between the vocal cords and the carina.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2295","text":"A 60-year-old patient is b‍eing taken up for a cervi​cal discectomy. The surge‍on had planned a posterio‌r approach that required ‌the patient to lie in a p‌rone position under anest‌hesia. What is the advant‌age of the armored endotr​acheal tube required for ‌this procedure?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient is being taken up for a cervical discectomy. The surgeon had planned a posterior approach that required the patient to lie in a prone position under anesthesia. What is the advantage of the armored endotracheal tube required for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Longer‍ length","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LASER re‍sistance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less k‍inking","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Double‍ lumen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Less kinking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e441c9d4f5c947ada75a996b1398509bx1280x1251.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Less kinking</strong></p><p>The advantage of an armored endotracheal tube is <strong>less kinking</strong>.</p>\n<p>Armored tubes (a.k.a. reinforced tubes, flexo-metallic tubes) are flexible, spiral wound tubes that are reinforced with metal coils. This minimizes kinking of the tube.</p>\n<p>They are useful in surgeries of <strong>head</strong> <strong>and</strong> <strong>neck</strong> and surgeries performed with the patient in the <strong>prone</strong> <strong>position</strong>.<br/><strong><br/>Armored or Flexo-metallic endotracheal tube:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other specialized endotracheal tubes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microlaryngeal tubes:</strong> have smaller internal diameters and longer lengths which makes them useful for <strong>laryngeal</strong> surgeries. </li>\n<li><strong>Metal tubes: </strong>are <strong>LASER resistant</strong> and are used for laser airway surgery to reduce fire hazards.</li>\n<li><strong>Double-lumen tubes: </strong>have <strong>two lumens</strong> (tracheal and bronchial) to facilitate <strong>lung</strong> <strong>isolation</strong> and one-lung ventilation in <strong>thoracic</strong> surgeries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2297","text":"What procedure is the foll‍owing device most suitabl​e for?","raw_text":"What procedure is the following device most suitable for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head and n‍eck surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal ‍surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Laryngea‍l surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoracic‍ surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal surgery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a406ea47a28647afadc93bd963dd534e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0bc7df3ce3749c69e543916075b3ccdx1280x2109.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b85fdc3092a343f7a38dbe102ebc6030x1280x2516.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal surgery</strong></p><p>The device shown in the image is the <strong>south oral</strong> <strong>Ring-Adair-Elwyn (RAE)</strong> endotracheal tube.</p>\n<p>It is preferred for <strong>nasal </strong>and<strong> ophthalmic surgeries</strong> because it has a preformed right-angle bend at the level of the teeth so that it does not disrupt the surgical field.</p>\n<p>The image below shows south faced oral RAE tube used for surgery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows a <strong>north nasal RAE tube. </strong></p>\n<p>It is preferred in <strong>oral</strong> surgeries as the tube connector faces towards the patient's head after placement, and the surgical field is not disrupted.  </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Other specialized endotracheal tubes and their uses</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexometalllic (Armored or reinforced tubes): head and neck surgery</li>\n<li>Microlaryngeal tubes: laryngeal surgery</li>\n<li>Double-lumen endotracheal tubes: thoracic surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2303","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most preferred type o​f cuff of an endotracheal‍ tube?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most preferred type of cuff of an endotracheal tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low volume, ‍low pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High volume, high pressure‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low volume , high pressure‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High volume , low pressure‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High volume , low pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c80a26ae307d4f67b26d671140d788efx1280x2736.0225669957686.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High volume , low pressure</strong></p><p>The most preferred type of cuff of an endotracheal tube is <strong>high volume, low pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Advantage of high-volume, low-pressure cuff is lesser incidence of tracheal mucosal damage. </p>\n<p>Disadvantages of high-volume, low-pressure cuff include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sore throat (due to larger mucosal contact area)</li>\n<li>Aspiration</li>\n<li>Spontaneous extubation</li>\n<li>Difficulty in insertion (because of the floppy cuff)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2306","text":"What is the maximum cuff p‍ressure that you can use ​to inflate the cuff of an‍ endotracheal tube?","raw_text":"What is the maximum cuff pressure that you can use to inflate the cuff of an endotracheal tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 cm o‍f water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 cm o‍f water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25 cm o‍f water","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"35 cm o‍f water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 25 cm of water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 25 cm of water</strong></p><p>Cuff pressure should not exceed <strong>25 cm of H<sub>2</sub>O</strong> since excess cuff pressure can lead to tracheal mucosal injury and recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>Cuff should be inflated to the <strong>minimum volume</strong> at which no air leak is seen.</p>\n<p>If nitrous oxide is used in general anesthesia, then cuff pressure should be monitored periodically as nitrous oxide can diffuse into the cuff causing the cuff pressure to rise.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2280","text":"Which of the following sit‍uations is least suitable​ for performing nasotrach‍eal intubation?","raw_text":"Which of the following situations is least suitable for performing nasotracheal intubation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 35 year old patient with‍ unstable c-spine injury ​requiring awake intubatio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 60 year old man with a t‍umor of the oral cavity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 28 year old woman with f‍racture of ramus of mandi​ble","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 40 year old man with fra‍cture of the base of skul​l","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 40 year old man with fracture of the base of skull","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 40 year old man with fracture of the base of skull</strong></p><p>Nasotracheal intubation is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in base of skull fractures<strong> </strong>as there is a chance for the tube to enter the cranial vault.</p>\n<p>The nasotracheal route is <strong>preferred</strong> in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When intubation via the oral route is difficult or not possible - large <strong>oral cavity tumors</strong> or abscess, conditions restricting mouth opening like temporomandibular joint ankylosis, <strong>fracture</strong> <strong>mandible</strong> etc.</li>\n<li>Surgeries of the oral cavity</li>\n<li><strong>Awake intubation </strong>as it bypasses the gag reflex and is more tolerable by a conscious patient.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2307","text":"A 40-year-old man was bein‍g prepared for an emergen​cy laparotomy and an endo‍tracheal tube was inserte‌d. Following intubation, ‌end-tidal carbon dioxide ‌was detected. However, hi‌s oxygen saturation dropp‌ed to 60% and breath soun​ds were not audible over ‌the left hemithorax. What​ could be the cause of th‌is?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man was being prepared for an emergency laparotomy and an endotracheal tube was inserted. Following intubation, end-tidal carbon dioxide was detected. However, his oxygen saturation dropped to 60% and breath sounds were not audible over the left hemithorax. What could be the cause of this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endobronchial intubation i‍nto left main bronchus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophageal‍ intubation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endotracheal tube blockage‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endobronchial intubation i‍nto right main bronchus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endobronchial intubation into right main bronchus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fb9082fbced40cfafa3829a99d8f0b8x1280x809.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11eeb1fd9aad4af39e4c8cdc35d52132x1280x849.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6337588547244a0a9cb2e681430e676x1280x2540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endobronchial intubation into right main bronchus</strong></p><p>Detection of end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub> but dropping oxygen saturation with absent breath sounds over the left hemithorax points to <strong>endobronchial intubation</strong> into the <strong>right main bronchus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Signs of endobronchial intubation</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elevated peak inspiratory pressure (first sign)</strong></li>\n<li>Asymmetric chest expansion</li>\n<li>Unilateral breath sounds</li>\n<li>Drop in oxygen saturation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Capnography</strong> and detection of end-tidal CO<sub>2,</sub> though the surest confirmation for an advanced airway placement, <strong>does not differentiate</strong> <strong>endotracheal</strong> from <strong>endobronchial</strong> <strong>intubation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Esophageal intubation:</strong> initial end-tidal CO2 may be detected due to swallowed air but later becomes zero. There would be the absence of breath sounds bilaterally with the presence of breath sounds in the epigastrium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2310","text":"A 3-year-old unconscious c‍hild, is rushed to the ER​. She is found to have co‍mplete upper airway obstr‌uction. An emergency inva‌sive airway procedure is ‌planned to save her. Whic‌h among the following can‌not be performed?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old unconscious child, is rushed to the ER. She is found to have complete upper airway obstruction. An emergency invasive airway procedure is planned to save her. Which among the following cannot be performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endotrachea‍l intubation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retrograde‍ intubation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Needle cri‍cothyrotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Percutaneous transtracheal‍ jet ventilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endotracheal intubation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endotracheal intubation</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\">Endotracheal intubation cannot be performed in this patient due to complete upper airway obstruction. </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Contraindications of endotracheal intubation: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Severe airway obstruction or trauma (critical airway obstruction).</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Fractures of the jaw or skull. </li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Presence of a laryngeal tumour.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note :</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">In this case, invasive airways such as needle cricothyrotomy and percutaneous transtracheal jet ventilation are preferred to secure the airway when the obstruction is above the level of the vocal cords.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">If the obstruction is either partial or more distal to the vocal cords, intubation can be attempted to secure the airway. This pushes the foreign body towards one of the bronchi such that there is ventilation from the unobstructed opposite side. Then the foreign body can be extracted by endoscopic removal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2222","text":"A 26-year-old term primigr‍avida is taken to the ope​ration theatre for an eme‍rgency cesarean section d‌ue to sudden fetal distre‌ss. The anaesthetist want‌s to prevent chances of p‌ulmonary aspiration and d‌ecides to intubate using ​rapid sequence intubation‌. Which of the following ​steps is not performed in‌ this technique?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old term primigravida is taken to the operation theatre for an emergency cesarean section due to sudden fetal distress. The anaesthetist wants to prevent chances of pulmonary aspiration and decides to intubate using rapid sequence intubation. Which of the following steps is not performed in this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preoxyg‍enation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cricoid ‍pressure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bag and mask‍ ventilation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Propofol‍ infusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bag and mask ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/431763442d4248af822e1dfbf43f4484x1280x2171.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bag and mask ventilation</strong></p><p>Bag and mask ventilation is <strong>avoided</strong> in rapid sequence intubation because it leads to <strong>stomach</strong> <strong>inflation</strong> and increases the risk of<strong> aspiration</strong>.<br/><strong><br/></strong><strong>Indications of Rapid sequence intubation:</strong></p>\n<p>RSI is done when the patient is at increased risk of pulmonary aspiration as in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">full stomach</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">intestinal obstruction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">hiatus hernia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">encephalopathy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">morbid obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">diabetic gastroparesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9124","text":"The time required for preo‍xygenation before trachea​l intubation?","raw_text":"The time required for preoxygenation before tracheal intubation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ‍min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 ‍min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5-7‍ min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 ‍min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 min</strong></p><p>Preoxygenation with <strong>tidal volume</strong> breathing of <strong>3 mins</strong> is required before tracheal intubation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Preoxygenation or <strong>denitrogenation</strong> is the process of replacing nitrogen in the lungs with oxygen.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">This delays the onset of critical hypoxia during the period of apnea by <strong>5-8 minutes.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">This lengthened apnea time provides an improved margin of safety while the anesthesiologist secures the airway and resumes ventilation. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2226","text":"A case of hiatus hernia is‍ posted for surgery. What​ will be ideal choice of ‍muscle relaxant that will‌ be used for intubation?","raw_text":"A case of hiatus hernia is posted for surgery. What will be ideal choice of muscle relaxant that will be used for intubation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rocur‍onium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Succiny‍lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur‍onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cisatr‍acurium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rocuronium</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Rapid sequence intubation for a patient with a hiatus hernia should be facilitated by the use of a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant such as <strong>rocuronium, </strong>to prevent an increase in the intragastric pressure (IGP).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> is usually the muscle relaxant of choice for RSI since it has a <strong>rapid onset</strong> and <strong>short duration</strong> of action. However, <strong>regurgitation </strong>and<strong> aspiration </strong>of gastric contents are likely, due to fasciculations of the abdominal skeletal muscles. Hence, <strong>e</strong><strong>ndotracheal intubation </strong>may be facilitated with <strong>rocuronium</strong> before succinylcholine to <strong>decrease this risk</strong>.</span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 943\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Generally, an IGP &gt; 28 cm H<sub>2</sub>O is required to overcome the competence of the gastroesophageal junction. However, when the normal oblique angle of entry of the esophagus into the stomach is altered, the IGP required for regurgitation is much lesser.</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Conditions that predispose to raised IGP are:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Pregnancy</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Ascites</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Bowel obstruction</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Hiatus hernia</strong></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2229","text":"Which of the following loc‍al anaesthetic is commonl​y used in topicalization ‍of airway for awake intub‌ation?","raw_text":"Which of the following local anaesthetic is commonly used in topicalization of airway for awake intubation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupiv‍acaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benzo‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prilo‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lidocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Lidocaine</strong> is the local anesthetic of choice to provide topical anesthesia of airway prior to awake intubation. It is preferred since it has a <strong>rapid onset</strong> and a <strong>high therapeutic index.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bupivacaine</strong> is<strong> not</strong> formulated for <strong>topical</strong> use.</p>\n<p><strong>Benzocaine and Cetacaine</strong> (a topical application spray containing benzocaine, tetracaine, and butamben) had been used but greater incidence of <strong>methemoglobinemia</strong> due to benzocaine has discouraged their use.</p>\n<p><strong>Prilocaine</strong> is usually available as a combination with lidocaine in Eutectic mixture of local anesthetic (EMLA) to provide topical anesthesia to <strong>intact skin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2234","text":"What is the commonly used ‍local anaesthetic for awa​ke nasotracheal intubatio‍n of a patient set to und‌ergo maxillofacial surger‌y?","raw_text":"What is the commonly used local anaesthetic for awake nasotracheal intubation of a patient set to undergo maxillofacial surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc‍aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Benzo‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‍acaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lidocaine</strong></p><p>Nowadays, a mixture of <strong>Lidocaine</strong> 3% and <strong>Phenylephrine</strong> 0.25% is most <strong>commonly</strong> used in <strong>awake nasotracheal intubation. </strong>This is used<strong> </strong>in addition to conscious sedation with low doses of midazolam and fentanyl. Phenylephrine is added to this mixture due to its vasoconstrictive property.</p>\n<p>Option A : <strong>Topical</strong> <strong>cocaine</strong> has vasoconstricting properties and hence is <strong>ideal</strong> for anesthesia of the nasal mucosa during awake nasotracheal intubation. However, its use has been <strong>discouraged</strong> due to <strong>toxicity</strong> and <strong>abuse</strong> <strong>potential</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C : <strong>Benzocaine</strong> and Cetacaine (a topical application spray containing benzocaine, tetracaine, and butamben) was previously used but a greater incidence of <strong>methemoglobinemia</strong> due to benzocaine has discouraged its use. <br/><br/></p>\n<p>Option D : Bupivacaine is not formulated for topical use.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0243","text":"Glycopyrrolate is used as ‍a pre-anesthetic agent fo​r:","raw_text":"Glycopyrrolate is used as a pre-anesthetic agent for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reducing th‍e secretions","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sedativ‍e effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skeletal mus‍cle relaxant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anxio‍lytic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reducing the secretions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reducing the secretions</strong></p><p><strong>Glycopyrrolate</strong> is used as a pre-anesthetic agent for <strong>reducing the secretions.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1184\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Anticholinergic drugs e.g. atropine, glycopyrrolate are routinely administered with anticholinesterases in order <strong>to attenuate the undesirable muscarinic effects.</strong> Glycopyrrolate is usually preferred because it has less vagolytic effects than atropine at doses that inhibit secretions and does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It should be administered as early as possible to maximize its effectiveness.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Glycopyrrolate 0.2 mg IV, is administered to dry airway secretions before flexible scope intubation of the trachea, during the bronchoscopic examination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6699","text":"The given set of instrumen‍ts are used in:","raw_text":"The given set of instruments are used in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central lin‍e insertion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial li‍ne insertion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Airway m‍anagement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Umbilical catheter inserti‍on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Airway management","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf7a945131bb4abdad3aaf7d591024c6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Airway management</strong></p><p>The image shows a tray in an <strong>airway cart</strong> with the instruments necessary for <strong>airway management.</strong></p>\n<p>An airway cart may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laryngoscope blades</strong> of various sizes, including a rigid fibreoptic.</li>\n<li><strong>Tracheal tubes</strong> of various sizes, and tracheal tube guides such as semirigid stylets, ventilating tube changer, light wands, and forceps that are required to manipulate the distal portion of the tracheal tube.</li>\n<li><strong>Masks</strong> of various sizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Laryngeal mask airways</strong> of various sizes.</li>\n<li>Flexible fiberoptic<strong> intubation</strong> equipment.</li>\n<li>Retrograde intubation equipment.</li>\n<li>At least one device to perform emergency <strong>noninvasive airway ventilation</strong> such as esophageal tracheal tube, a hollow jet ventilation stylet.</li>\n<li>Equipment required for emergency <strong>invasive</strong> airway access.</li>\n<li>An exhaled <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> detector.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2301","text":"The following endotracheal‍ tube is preferred for __​_______","raw_text":"The following endotracheal tube is preferred for _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head and n‍eck surgery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal ‍surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laryngea‍l surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoracic‍ surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Head and neck surgery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f43d6bbdbeec4bada5c71fc928cabcb3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Head and neck surgery</strong></p><p>The image shown is of an <strong>armored tube </strong>(a.k.a. reinforced, flexo-metallic endotracheal tube). It is useful in <strong>head and neck surgeries</strong> and when the patient is in a prone position.</p>\n<p>An armored tube is a flexible, spiral wound tube which is reinforced with the metal coil so as to minimize kinking. The metal coil is better made out in the second image.</p>\n<p><strong>Other specialized endotracheal tubes include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>South oral <strong>Ring Adair Elwin:</strong> Nasal surgery</li>\n<li><strong>Micro laryngeal tubes:</strong> Laryngeal surgery </li>\n<li><strong>Double-lumen endotracheal tubes </strong>(Carlen, Robertshaw): Thoracic surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The tip of the armored endotracheal tube is 2 cm distal to the distal-most metal ring, and this portion may not be visualized in radiography.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2308","text":"Which of the following is ‍an indicator of endobronc​hial intubation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indicator of endobronchial intubation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sudden Rise in end tidal C‍arbon dioxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sudden fall in end tidal c‍arbon dioxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sudden rise in peak inspir‍atory pressure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral b‍reath sounds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sudden rise in peak inspiratory pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sudden rise in peak inspiratory pressure</strong></p><p>Sudden rise in peak inspiratory pressure is an indicator of endobronchial intubation.</p>\n<p><strong>Signs of endobronchial intubation</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated peak inspiratory pressure (first sign)</li>\n<li>Asymmetric chest expansion</li>\n<li>Unilateral breath sounds</li>\n<li>Hypoxemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yLnTtzOv","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"6_cardiovascular_and_respiratory_systems","title":"6.Cardiovascular And Respiratory Systems","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1045","text":"The cardiogenic area is fo‌rmed in which of the foll​owing structures?","raw_text":"The cardiogenic area is formed in which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extraembryo‌nic mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somatopleur‌ic mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splanchnople‌uric mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paraxial‌ mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splanchnopleuric mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/462b5a72c78b448cab4cf6514c9e0679x1280x1437.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splanchnopleuric mesoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>heart</strong> develops from angioblastic tissue in the <strong>splanchnopleuric</strong> <strong>mesoderm</strong>. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The heart develops from <strong>two endothelial heart tubes</strong>. These heart tubes are derived from the <strong>splanchnic layer</strong> of the <strong>lateral plate mesoderm</strong> also known as splanchnopleuric mesoderm.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Progenitor heart cells form a horseshoe-shaped cluster of cells <strong>cranial to the neural fold</strong>. This location from which the heart originates is known as the <strong>cardiogenic area</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image below shows location of cardiogenic area during embryological development.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1046","text":"Arrange the following stru‌ctures of the primitive h​eart tube in cranial to c​audal order. Bulbus cordi‌s Sinus venosus Primitive​ atrium Primitive ventric​le","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following structures of the primitive heart tube in cranial to caudal order.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<div><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Bulbus cordis</span></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Sinus venosus</span></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Primitive atrium</span></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Primitive ventricle</span></div>\n</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-3‌-4-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-1‌-2-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-4‌-2-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-4‌-3-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-4-3-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36f53da772384467b3f89ff0c9c3683ax1280x1215.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e3b9344a94d480d8012ac569191e8aax1280x753.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-4-3-2</strong></p><p>The correct order of heart tube structures from <strong>cranial to caudal </strong>is <strong>bulbus cordis</strong>, <strong>primitive ventricle</strong>, <strong>primitive atrium</strong>, and the <strong>sinus venosus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The 2 heart tubes formed in the cardiogenic area fuse, except in their caudal most end, to become an inverted Y-shaped structure. During this fusion, the central part of the heart tube expands to form the future outflow tract and ventricular regions. The <strong>caudal pole</strong> of the tube receives the <strong>venous drainage</strong>, while the <strong>cranial end</strong> pumps out blood into the <strong>arterial system</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0508","text":"Which of the following for‌ms the trabeculated part ​of the right ventricle?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the trabeculated part of the right ventricle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primitive‌ ventricle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal 1/3 of bulbus cordi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle 1/3 of bulbus cordi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal 1/3 of bulbus cor‌dis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proximal 1/3 of bulbus cordis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9d197ea896e4f1db77375c7f410039fx1280x742.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proximal 1/3 of bulbus cordis</strong></p><p>The <strong>trabeculated </strong>part of the <strong>right ventricle</strong> is formed from the <strong>proximal 1/3rd</strong> of the <strong>bulbus cordis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>heart tube</strong> begins to <strong>bend</strong> by <strong>day 23</strong>. This bending creates the <strong>cardiac loop</strong>. The fate of various structures formed as a result of this bending is as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below are the heart tube and their derivatives:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.7222px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 30.7222px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Part of the heart tube</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 30.7222px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Derivative structure in the heart</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<p class=\"p2\">Bulbus cordis</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p2\">Proximal 1/3 </li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Middle 1/3 (conus cordis)</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Distal 1/3 (truncus arteriosus)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p2\">Trabeculated part of Rt.ventricle</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Outflow tract of Rt. and Lt.ventricles</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Root and proximal portions of aorta and pulmonary artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px;\">Primitive ventricle</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Trabeculated part of the Lt.ventricle</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px;\">Primitive atrium</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>The common atrium that splits to Rt. and Lt.atrium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1064","text":"Sinus venosus does not rec‌eive blood from which of ​the following veins?","raw_text":"Sinus venosus does not receive blood from which of the following veins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitelli‌ne vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilic‌al vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common car‌dinal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subcardi‌nal vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subcardinal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50494ad71a0540cca47eb50dee270325x1280x1498.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subcardinal vein</strong></p><p>The sinus venosus does not receive blood from the subcardinal veins. They are found in relation to the urogenital ridges and mainly drain the developing kidney.</p>\n<p><strong>Sinus venosus</strong> is the caudal-most part of the tubular heart and has <strong>right</strong> and <strong>left horns</strong>. Each horn of sinus venosus has 3 branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vitelline vein</strong> receives blood from the yolk sac</li>\n<li><strong>Umbilical vein</strong> receives blood from the placenta</li>\n<li><strong>Common cardinal vein </strong>receives blood from the body wall</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1065","text":"A 2-year-old child was fou‌nd to have an absence of ​coronary sinus associated​ with other cardiac defec‌ts. Failure in the develo​pment of which of the fol​lowing structures will mo‌st likely lead to the giv‌en condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child was found to have an absence of coronary sinus associated with other cardiac defects. Failure in the development of which of the following structures will most likely lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right umbi‌lical vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right horn of sinus venosu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left horn of sinus venosus‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left vite‌lline vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left horn of sinus venosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a7e75355726435ba35c7d7705fd8355x1280x1783.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left horn of sinus venosus</strong></p><p>The <strong>coronary sinus</strong> develops from the <strong>left horn</strong> of the <strong>sinus venosus</strong>. Hence, its developmental failure will lead to defects of the coronary sinus.</p>\n<p>Initially, the communication between the sinus venosus and the atrium is wide. Later, the entrance of the <strong>sinus venosus shifts</strong> to the<strong> right side</strong> due to the establishment of left-to-right shunts of blood.</p>\n<p>The fate of sinus venosus and its branches near the heart is as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right horn </strong>of sinus venosus gets <strong>incorporated into the right atrium</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Right umbilical vein: obliterates</li>\n<li><strong>Right common cardinal vein:</strong> Becomes part of the <strong>superior venacava</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right vitelline vein:</strong> Becomes the terminal part of <strong>inferior venacava </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Left horn</strong> of sinus venosus reduces in size and becomes the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Left umbilical vein:  obliterates</li>\n<li><strong>Left common cardinal vein:</strong> reduces the size and becomes the <strong>oblique vein of the left atrium</strong></li>\n<li>Left vitelline vein: obliterates</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1068","text":"A 40-year-old man had a CT‌ scan to evaluate a lower​ respiratory tract infect​ion. Incidentally, he was‌ found to have a left-sid​ed superior vena cava dra​ining into the coronary s‌inus. The patient reporte‌d no cardiac symptoms. Fa​ilure of regression of wh‌ich of the following stru​ctures during embryonic d‌evelopment leads to the g​iven anatomical variation​?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man had a CT scan to evaluate a lower respiratory tract infection. Incidentally, he was found to have a left-sided superior vena cava draining into the coronary sinus. The patient reported no cardiac symptoms. Failure of regression of which of the following structures during embryonic development leads to the given anatomical variation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left anterior cardinal vei‌n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right anterior cardinal ve‌in","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left common ‌cardinal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right posterior cardinal v‌ein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left anterior cardinal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e249379f2e44becaff042a864f898c0x1280x1984.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left anterior cardinal vein</strong></p><p><strong>Persistent left-sided superior vena cava</strong> occurs due to <strong>failure</strong> of <strong>regression</strong> of the <strong>left anterior cardinal vein</strong>. It may drain either into the coronary sinus or the left atrium.</p>\n<p>The formation of the<strong> vena cava system</strong> begins with the formation of anastomoses between the right and left venous channels. Formation of the major veins takes place as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>proximal part</strong> of the <strong>right anterior cardinal vein</strong> along with the <strong>right common cardinal vein</strong> form the <strong>superior vena cava</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>distal part</strong> of the <strong>right anterior cardinal vein</strong> between the junction with the subclavian vein and the junction with the transverse anastomosis becomes the <strong>right brachiocephalic vein</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>distal part</strong> of the <strong>left anterior cardinal vein</strong> and the <strong>transverse anastomosis</strong> becomes the <strong>left brachiocephalic vein</strong> </li>\n<li>The <strong>most cranial part</strong> of both the <strong>anterior cardinal veins</strong> above its junction with subclavian vein become the <strong>internal jugular vein</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1075","text":"A major part of the post-r‌enal segment of the infer​ior vena cava develops fr​om which of the following‌ structures?","raw_text":"A major part of the post-renal segment of the inferior vena cava develops from which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supracard‌inal vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilic‌al vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior ca‌rdinal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepato-card‌iac channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supracardinal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7aa29bc78dc14c6980f2c53edc1249d4x600x1025.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9592e8f9be9b466c94e32e423c306f4ex600x1025.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supracardinal vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>post-renal</strong> segment of the inferior vena cava <strong>(IVC)</strong> mainly develops from the <strong>right</strong> <strong>supracardinal vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>In embryonic life, the supracardinal veins get a large amount of venous drainage from the growing body wall. The right supracardinal vein persists and forms the greater part of the postrenal segment of the inferior vena cava. </p>\n<p>The <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> is a composite vessel that develops from the remodeling of successive venous complexes. The veins contributing to the formation of IVC from upwards to below are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cardiac termination of the right vitelline vein, also known as the <strong>right hepatocardiac channel</strong></li>\n<li>Anastomosis between right vitelline and right subcardinal veins <strong>(Hepatic segment of IVC)</strong></li>\n<li>Right subcardinal vein <strong>(Subcardinal or Pre-renal segment of IVC)</strong></li>\n<li>Anastomosis between right supracardinal and right subcardinal veins <strong>(Renal segment of IVC)</strong></li>\n<li>Part of the right supracardinal vein <strong>(Supracardinal or Post-renal segment of IVC)</strong></li>\n<li>Anastomosis between right supracardinal and right postcardinal veins</li>\n<li>Part of the right posterior cardinal vein</li>\n<li>Common iliac veins</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1074","text":"The posterior cardinal vei‌n contributes to the deve​lopment of which of the f​ollowing adult structures‌?","raw_text":"The posterior cardinal vein contributes to the development of which of the following adult structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common i‌liac vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal j‌ugular vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External j‌ugular vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Common iliac vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Common iliac vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>posterior cardinal vein</strong> contributes to the development of the <strong>right</strong> <strong>common iliac vein.</strong></p>\n<p>The common iliac veins develop as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>right common iliac vein</strong> is derived from the most caudal portion of the <strong>right posterior cardinal vein</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>left common iliac vein</strong> develops from the <strong>anastomosis</strong> <strong>between</strong> the<strong> sacrocardinal veins</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1067","text":"Which of the following vei‌ns is not derived from th​e vitelline vein?","raw_text":"Which of the following veins is not derived from the vitelline vein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepati‌c vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior mes‌enteric vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superior vena cava","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superior vena cava</strong></p><p>The superior vena cava is not derived from the vitelline vein. </p>\n<p><strong>Vitelline veins</strong> are developmental vessels passing between the yolk sac and the sinus venosus of the heart. Vitelline veins contribute to the development of the following structures: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatic vein: </strong>Before entering the sinus venosus, the vitelline veins form a plexus around the duodenum and pass through the septum transversum. The liver cords grow into the septum breaking the course of this vein into an extensive vascular network known as hepatic sinusoids. The sinusoids form the hepatic vein and drain into the inferior vena cava. </li>\n<li><strong><strong>Portal vein: </strong></strong>The anastomotic plexus formed by the vitelline veins around the duodenum develops into a single vessel called the portal vein. </li>\n<li><strong>Inferior vena cava: </strong>The hepatocardiac portion of IVC develops from the right hepatocardiac channel, which is derived from the right vitelline vein. </li>\n<li><strong><strong>Superior mesenteric vein: </strong></strong>This vein is derived from the right vitelline vein and drains the primary intestinal loop. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1069","text":"What is the location of th‌e foramen secundum?","raw_text":"What is the location of the foramen secundum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper part of septum secun‌dum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower part of septum primu‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper part of septum primu‌m","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lower part of septum secun‌dum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Upper part of septum primum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Upper part of septum primum</strong></p><p>The <strong>foramen</strong> <strong>secundum</strong> (ostium secundum)<strong> </strong>is present at the <strong>upper part </strong>of the<strong> septum primum</strong>. </p>\n<p>During fetal life, oxygenated blood reaches the right atrium from the placenta. This blood then reaches the left atrium through the gap between the atria. The <strong>initial gap</strong> is the <strong>foramen primum</strong> formed at the <strong>lower end</strong> of the <strong>septum primum.</strong> </p>\n<p>Before the foramen primum is closed, another path has to be created for the flow of blood. This gap is the foramen secundum at the upper part of the septum primum. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1070","text":"The limbus fossa ovalis is‌ the remnant of _________​","raw_text":"The limbus fossa ovalis is the remnant of _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septum‌ primum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Septum ‌secundum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Septum secundum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c58c9b21eeb44118e556c36c7970a1fx600x734.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Septum secundum</strong></p><p>The<strong> limbus fossa ovalis </strong>(also known as <strong>annulus ovalis</strong>) represents the remnant of the lower free edge of the <strong>septum secundum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fossa ovalis is an oval-shaped depression in the septal wall of the right atrium just above the opening of inferior vena cava. It marks the location of embryonic foramen ovale.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>floor of fossa ovalis</strong> is the remnant of <strong>septum primum</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>limbus of fossa ovalis</strong> is the prominent upper margin of the fossa ovalis and a remnant of the <strong>septum secundum</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0112","text":"Marked structure is derive‌d from the______","raw_text":"Marked structure is derived from the______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endocardia‌l cushions","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Septum‌ primum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ven‌ous valve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bulbous‌ venosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endocardial cushions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7188dc24af3144269a5d0d2b0d80b084.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endocardial cushions</strong></p><p>The structure marked is the <strong>membranous </strong>part of the<strong> interventricular septum. </strong>It is derived from<strong> endocardial cushions.</strong></p>\n<p>Endocardial cushions are soft tissue masses that protrude in the atrioventricular and conotruncal regions. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrioventricular cushions</strong> are derived from <strong>overlying endocardial cells. </strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>conotruncal cushions</strong> are derived from the <strong>neural crest cells</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Endocardial cushions</strong> contribute to the formation of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Atrial septa</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ventricular septa (membranous portion)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Atrioventricular canals</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Atrioventricular valves</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Aortic and pulmonary channels</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Defects in endocardial cushion formation can result in cardiac malformations, including ASDs, VSDs, and defects involving the great vessels (transposition of the great vessels, common truncus arteriosus, and tetralogy of Fallot).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1077","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not contribu​te to the formation of th​e arch of the aorta?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not contribute to the formation of the arch of the aorta?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left horn o‌f aortic sac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left 4th a‌rch artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aorti‌c sac","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left 2nd a‌rch artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left 2nd arch artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left 2nd arch artery</strong></p><p>The left 2<sup>nd</sup> arch artery does not contribute towards the formation of the arch of the aorta.  </p>\n<p><strong>Arch of the aorta</strong> develops from the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ventral part of the aortic sac</li>\n<li>Left horn of the aortic sac</li>\n<li>Left 4th arch artery </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1079","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is derived from t​he right 4th aortic arch?​","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is derived from the right 4th aortic arch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right common carotid arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right subcl‌avian artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aorti‌c arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None as it regresses compl‌etely","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right subclavian artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right subclavian artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>proximal part</strong> of the<strong> right subclavian artery</strong> is derived from the <strong>right 4th aortic arch.  </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>distal segment</strong> of the <strong>right subclavian artery</strong> is derived from the <strong>right dorsal aorta</strong> and the <strong>7th intersegmental artery</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>4th aortic arch</strong> persists on both sides and forms different structures on either side:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left side</strong>: Part of the <strong>arch </strong>of the <strong>aorta</strong> between the left common carotid and the left subclavian arteries</li>\n<li><strong>Right side</strong>: <strong>Proximal</strong> segment of the <strong>right subclavian artery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1072","text":"An infant with a continuou‌s machine-like murmur is ​diagnosed with patent duc​tus arteriosus. When does‌ the affected structure n​ormally begin to close?","raw_text":"An infant with a continuous machine-like murmur is diagnosed with patent ductus arteriosus. When does the affected structure normally begin to close?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2 m‌onths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-2 ‌weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-2 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immed‌iately","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Immediately","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Immediately</strong></p><p>The <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> gets functionally closed through contraction of its muscular wall almost<strong> immediately after birth</strong>. However, anatomical closure occurs only 2-3 weeks after birth.</p>\n<p>In fetal life, <strong>prostaglandins</strong> relax the vascular smooth muscle and <strong>keep</strong> the <strong>ductus arteriosus open</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>After birth</strong>, there is <strong>high oxygen</strong> content in the blood due to ventilation in the lung. This <strong>inhibits</strong> the production of <strong>prostaglandins</strong>. The ductus closes due to vascular smooth muscle contraction leaving a ligamentous <strong>remnant</strong> known as <strong>ligamentum arteriosum</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: Premature infants can be treated with prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors (such as indomethacin) to promote closure of the ductus arteriosus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1082","text":"A newborn with persistent ‌cyanosis was being evalua​ted. He was found to have​ a congenital heart disea‌se that is schematically ​represented below. What i​s the most likely embryol‌ogical defect causing thi‌s?","raw_text":"A newborn with persistent cyanosis was being evaluated. He was found to have a congenital heart disease that is schematically represented below. What is the most likely embryological defect causing this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal spiraling of cono‌truncal septum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Failure in formation of co‌notruncal ridges","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior displacement of c‌onotruncal septum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ‌venous connection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Failure in formation of conotruncal ridges","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f02828a9a0b643f79060a95fa3029168.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09367c16b8784a3d9b1ef15d6f17e3dcx1280x1942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Failure in formation of conotruncal ridges</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of persistent<strong> (patent) truncus</strong> <strong>arteriosus</strong>. Failure in the formation <strong>(absence)</strong> of <strong>conotruncal ridges</strong> leads to this condition.</p>\n<p>The <strong>aortopulmonary septum</strong> is also known as the <strong>conotruncal septum</strong>. The formation of the aortopulmonary septum in the truncus arteriosus <strong>divides</strong> the outflow channel of the heart into the ventral <strong>aorta</strong> and the <strong>pulmonary trunk.</strong></p>\n<p>Persistent truncus arteriosus results when the conotruncal ridges fail to form. As the conotruncal ridges also participate in the formation of the interventricular septum, there will be a defective interventricular septum in this condition. The persistent truncus overrides both the ventricles and receives mixed blood from both sides. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1080","text":"A newborn developed cyanos‌is soon after birth. Ches​t X-ray showed the charac​teristic egg-on-a-string ‌appearance. Which of the ​following mechanisms duri​ng embryonic development ‌will most likely lead to ‌the given condition?","raw_text":"A newborn developed cyanosis soon after birth. Chest X-ray showed the characteristic egg-on-a-string appearance.  Which of the following mechanisms during embryonic development will most likely lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure of cono-truncal ri‌dge to fuse and descend","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortico-pulmonary septum n‌ot following its spiral c​ourse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior displacement of a‌ortico-pulmonary septum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-fusion of aortico-pulm‌onary septum with truncus​ arteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aortico-pulmonary septum not following its spiral course","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de8ae0764b3d4a359bf3419bc81aaac2x1280x2018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aortico-pulmonary septum not following its spiral course</strong></p><p>The<strong> egg-on-a-string appearance</strong> on chest X-ray is characteristically seen in the <strong>transposition of great arteries (TGA). </strong>It is considered to arise due to <strong>abnormal spiraling </strong>of the <strong>aortopulmonary septum </strong>(conotruncal septum).</p>\n<p>In this condition, the aortopulmonary septum fails to follow the normal spiral course and instead runs straight. This results in aorta arising from the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk from the left ventricle. This condition is seen in 4.8/10,000 births. </p>\n<p>Patent ductus arteriosus is commonly seen along with this condition. Sometimes, there is an associated defect in the membranous part of the interventricular septum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1073","text":"A cut section of the umbil‌ical cord is given below.​ Which of the following i​s a remnant of the marked‌ structure in an adult?","raw_text":"A cut section of the umbilical cord is given below. Which of the following is a remnant of the marked structure in an adult?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligamentum‌ arteriosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligament‌um teres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median umbil‌ical ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial umbil‌ical ligament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial umbilical ligament","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/963d90480075461eb9081149b192fa3d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial umbilical ligament</strong></p><p>The marked structure in the image is an umbilical artery. The <strong>medial umbilical ligament</strong> is the remnant of this structure.</p>\n<p>The <strong>umbilical artery</strong> has the following fate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal parts</strong> persist as the <strong>superior vesical arteries</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Distal parts</strong> get obliterated and form the <strong>medial umbilical ligaments</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below are the embryological remnants at the umbilicus:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 447px; height: 115px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.8646px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214.132px; height: 33.8646px;\"><strong>Embryological structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.868px; height: 33.8646px;\"><strong>Remnant</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214.132px; height: 22px;\">Left umbilical vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.868px; height: 22px;\">ligamentum teres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214.132px; height: 22px;\">Umbilical arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.868px; height: 22px;\">medial umbilical ligaments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 214.132px; height: 22px;\">Urachus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.868px; height: 22px;\">median umbilical ligament</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4398","text":"The heart starts to beat i‌n the ______week of devel​opment.","raw_text":"The heart starts to beat in the ______week of development.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2nd ‌week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th ‌week","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6th ‌week","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10th‌ week","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4th week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4th week</strong></p><p>The <strong>heart starts</strong> to <strong>beat</strong> in the <strong>4th week</strong> of development. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0873","text":"The false statement about ‌fetal circulation is:","raw_text":"The false statement about fetal circulation is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anatomical closure of fora‌men ovale is by 3 to 5 da​ys","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anatomical closure of duct‌us venosus is by 3 to 7 d​ays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right umbilical vein is ab‌sent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anatomical closure of duct‌us arteriosus is by 2 to ​3 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anatomical closure of foramen ovale is by 3 to 5 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anatomical closure of foramen ovale is by 3 to 5 days</strong></p><p>The <strong>anatomical closure</strong> of the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> takes <strong>1 month to a year</strong>.</p>\n<p>Given below are the fetal circulatory adjustments after birth:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px; width: 458px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong> Functional closure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong>Anatomical closure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 40px;\"><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">After removal of the placenta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">7 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">At birth - after breathing is initiated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">Months to years </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.9318px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 30.9318px;\"><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–15 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–21 days  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1084","text":"The appearance of which of‌ the following structures​ is regarded as the first​ sign of respiratory syst‌em development?","raw_text":"The appearance of which of the following structures is regarded as the first sign of respiratory system development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tracheoesoph‌ageal septum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypobranchi‌al eminence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primitiv‌e foregut","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngotracheal diverticul‌um","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Laryngotracheal diverticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e65a204fc23146b4b02892f634ac5699x1280x1412.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Laryngotracheal diverticulum</strong></p><p>The appearance of the <strong>laryngotracheal</strong> (respiratory)<strong> diverticulum</strong> is regarded as the<strong> first sign</strong> of <strong>respiratory</strong> system<strong> development</strong>. It appears in the <strong>ventral wall</strong> of the pharyngeal part of the <strong>foregut</strong>. </p>\n<p>The respiratory system is <strong>derived</strong> from <strong>2 sources</strong>:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endoderm </strong>of the <strong>foregut: </strong>The lining epithelium of the larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli are derived from the endoderm.</li>\n<li><strong>Splanchnopleuric intraembryonic mesoderm: </strong>The cartilages, muscles, and connective tissue of the respiratory tract are derived from the splanchnopleuric intraembryonic mesoderm surrounding the foregut.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the development of the respiratory diverticulum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1085","text":"The larynx and the trachea‌ develop from which of th​e following structures?","raw_text":"The larynx and the trachea develop from which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryngotra‌cheal tube","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Laryngotrac‌heal groove","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung‌ buds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi‌al buds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Laryngotracheal tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Laryngotracheal tube</strong></p><p>The <strong>larynx</strong> and <strong>trachea</strong> develop from the <strong>laryngotracheal tube.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>laryngotracheal</strong> (respiratory)<strong> diverticulum </strong>develops from the <strong>ventral </strong>wall of the <strong>pharyngeal part </strong>of the <strong>foregut</strong>.</p>\n<p>The free caudal end of the respiratory diverticulum elongates downwards to enter the thorax, where it bifurcates to form the right and left <strong>bronchial buds</strong> or <strong>lung</strong> <strong>buds</strong>, which in turn further develops to form the <strong>bronchi</strong> and the <strong>lung parenchyma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The part of the diverticulum cranial to the bifurcation is known as <strong>laryngotracheal</strong> <strong>tube</strong>, which further develops to form the <strong>larynx</strong> and <strong>trachea</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1087","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of t​racheoesophageal fistula ​(TEF)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophageal atresia with pr‌oximal TEF","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophageal atresia with di‌stal TEF","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"H type tracheoesophageal f‌istula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal TEF with distal e‌sophageal atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esophageal atresia with distal TEF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f13d3884d6cc4027a54c9f3c40c5a6bex1280x3561.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esophageal atresia with distal TEF</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>tracheoesophageal fistula</strong> (TEF) is <strong>esophageal atresia</strong> with <strong>distal TEF </strong>(also known as <strong>type C</strong>). </p>\n<p>It occurs due to the <strong>defective</strong> <strong>formation</strong> of the <strong>tracheoesophageal septum</strong>. There is an abnormal connection (fistula) between the esophagus and the trachea. These defects occur in approximately 1/3000 births. The condition is commonly associated with esophageal atresia. </p>\n<p>TEF can be grossly classified into <strong>5 types</strong>, types<strong> A to E</strong>, as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1088","text":"Arrange the following phas‌es of lung development an​d maturation in order, fr​om the earlier phase to t‌he later phase. 1. Saccul​ar phase 2. Embryonic pha​se 3. Canalicular phase 4‌. Pseudoglandular phase","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following phases of lung development and maturation in order, from the earlier phase to the later phase.</p>\n<p>1. Saccular phase<br/>2. Embryonic phase<br/>3. Canalicular phase<br/>4. Pseudoglandular phase</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-2‌-1-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-1‌-4-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-2‌-1-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2-4‌-3-1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2-4-3-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79574ea879dd4b61a1f55b33563f09abx1280x1139.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2-4-3-1</strong></p><p>The correct order of phases of lung maturation is as follows: embryonic, pseudoglandular, canalicular, saccular phase.</p>\n<p>The <strong>development</strong> and<strong> maturation </strong>of the <strong>lungs</strong> occur mainly in the following phases:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 576px; height: 192px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.6771px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 199.837px; height: 33.6771px;\"><strong>Phase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 362.163px; height: 33.6771px;\"><strong>Developmental events</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 199.837px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Embryonic</strong> phase (0–7 weeks)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 362.163px; height: 18px;\">Appearance of lung buds and the main pulmonary arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 199.837px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Pseudoglandular </strong>phase (7–17 weeks)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 362.163px; height: 36px;\">Development of airways and blood vessels to the level of the acinus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 199.837px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Canalicular </strong>phase (17–27 weeks)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 362.163px; height: 18px;\">Formation of respiratory airways and the development of the blood-air barrier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 199.837px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Saccular</strong> phase/<strong> Alveolar</strong> phase (28–32 weeks to after birth)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 362.163px; height: 36px;\">\n<p>Development and secondary septation of alveoli</p>\n<p>Adjacent capillary networks fuse to form an extensive double capillary network</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6815","text":"A 5-day-old infant was adm‌itted to the NICU as she ​was suffering from severe​ dyspnea and cyanosis. Ch‌est X-ray revealed hypopl​astic left lung and herni​ation of the intestines i‌nto the left thoracic cav‌ity. Abnormality in which​ of the following structu‌res during the embryonic ​development will most com‌monly lead to the given c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 5-day-old infant was admitted to the NICU as she was suffering from severe dyspnea and cyanosis. Chest X-ray revealed hypoplastic left lung and herniation of the intestines into the left thoracic cavity. Abnormality in which of the following structures during the embryonic development will most commonly lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesentery of the oesphagus‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pleuroperic‌ardial folds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body‌ wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pleuroperit‌oneal folds","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pleuroperitoneal folds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45e99ad7552247d6b3907f3a1b370316x600x501.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pleuroperitoneal folds</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>congenital diaphragmatic hernia. </strong>It most commonly occurs due to <strong>failure</strong> of <strong>fusion</strong> of <strong>pleuroperitoneal folds</strong> during the development of the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>The diaphragm develops from<strong> 4 mesodermal sources</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Septum transversum </strong>becomes the anterior and medial part of the diaphragm including the <strong>central tendon</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pleuroperitoneal membranes</strong> (paired structures)<strong> </strong>become the<strong> dorsolateral parts </strong>of the diaphragm</li>\n<li><strong>Mesoderm of body wall </strong>from somites of cervical segments three to five <strong>(C3-5)</strong> contribute to the <strong>peripheral muscular part</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mesentery of the esophagus</strong> forms the <strong>crura</strong> of the diaphragm</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Congenital diaphragmatic</strong> hernia results due to failure of various structures forming diaphragm to fuse. It occurs in 1/2000 births. The defect allows abdominal viscera to enter the pleural cavity. It is <strong>most frequently</strong> caused by the <strong>failure of</strong> one or both of the <strong>pleuroperitoneal membranes</strong> to <strong>fuse</strong>. Posterolateral defects (Bochdalek’s hernia) are the most common (85–90%). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"JsntceiE","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"6_krebs_cycle","title":"6.Krebs Cycle","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1137","text":"What is the site of the TC‌A cycle?","raw_text":"What is the site of the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyt‌osol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoplasmi‌c reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitoch‌ondria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lyso‌somes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitochondria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitochondria</strong></p><p>The <strong>TCA cycle</strong> (Krebs cycle) takes place in the <strong>mitochondria.</strong> It occurs in <strong>all organs</strong> of the body.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1138","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Krebs cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Krebs cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the final common pat‌hway for the oxidation of‍ carbohydrates, lipids an‍d proteins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pyruvate condenses with ox‌aloacetate to form citrat‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidative phosphorylation ‌occurs in the cytoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alpha keto glutarate is a ‌4 carbon compound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is the final common pathway for the oxidation of carbohydrates, lipids and proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is the final common pathway for the oxidation of carbohydrates, lipids and proteins</strong></p><p>The <strong>Krebs cycle</strong> is the <strong>final common pathway</strong> for the oxidation of carbohydrates, lipids, and proteins as glucose, fatty acids, and most amino acids are metabolized to acetyl-CoA or intermediates of the cycle.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Acetyl CoA condenses with oxaloacetate to form citrate.</p>\n<p>Option C: Oxidative phosphorylation occurs in mitochondria by ETC (electron transport chain).</p>\n<p>Option D: Alpha-ketoglutarate is a 5-carbon compound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1161","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an intermediate of th‍e TCA cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an intermediate of the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cit‌rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acety‌l CoA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Succin‌yl CoA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fuma‌rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acetyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/637b5353ebf84edeb9319ad3d7abb5e2x1280x1018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acetyl CoA</strong></p><p><strong>Acetyl CoA</strong> is not an intermediate formed in the TCA cycle, but rather it is a <strong>starting substrate.</strong></p>\n<p>There are three sources of acetyl-CoA:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>From glucose →Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA</li>\n<li>Fatty acid oxidation → Acetyl-CoA</li>\n<li>Ketogenic amino acid oxidation→ acetyl-CoA</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The starting materials of Kreb's cycle are <strong>Acetyl CoA</strong> and<strong> oxaloacetic acid. </strong>All the other options are intermediates in the TCA cycle and oxaloacetic acid is released in the end.</p>\n<p>The image given below depicts the Krebs cycle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1170","text":"Hyperammonemia inhibits th‌e TCA cycle, by depleting‍ levels of which of the f‍ollowing substances?","raw_text":"Hyperammonemia inhibits the TCA cycle, by depleting levels of which of the following substances?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxaloa‌cetate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha ket‌oglutarate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Succin‌yl CoA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fuma‌rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alpha ketoglutarate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alpha ketoglutarate</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperammonemia </strong>results in the <strong>depletion</strong> of <strong>alpha-ketoglutarate.</strong></p>\n<p>This happens in two steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The excess free ammonia binds to glutamate and forms glutamine.</li>\n<li>To compensate for the reduced glutamate, alpha-ketoglutarate is aminated to glutamate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thus, hyperammonemia eventually depletes alpha-ketoglutarate, leading to a stoppage of the TCA cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1174","text":"Aconitase contains which o‌f the following?","raw_text":"Aconitase contains which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05e3718292b347819e0d3ec6dabb3aa2x765x409.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fe</strong></p><p><strong>Aconitase</strong> is a <strong>Fe-containing</strong> enzyme. It is involved in the conversion of <strong>citrate to isocitrate</strong>.</p>\n<p>The process occurs as a <strong>two-step reaction</strong> catalyzed by the enzyme, aconitase.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>There is an initial <strong>dehydration reaction</strong> with the removal of a molecule of water, from Cis-Aconitate.</li>\n<li>This is followed up by a<strong> rehydration reaction</strong>, where a molecule of water is added to Cis-Aconitate, thereby forming Isocitrate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Aconitase is involved in iron homeostasis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1177","text":"Which of the following sub‌stances inhibits aconitas‍e enzyme?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances inhibits aconitase enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arse‌nite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malo‌nate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluoro‌acetate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iodoa‌cetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluoroacetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluoroacetate</strong></p><p><strong>Flouroacetate</strong> is an <strong>inhibitor</strong> of the <strong>aconitase</strong> enzyme.</p>\n<p>Fluoroacetate combines with Co-A to form<strong> fluoroacetyl-CoA</strong>. Fluoroacetyl-CoA then condenses with oxaloacetate to form <strong>Fluorocitrate</strong>. Fluorocitrate then<strong> inhibits</strong> the enzyme<strong> aconitase</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7260","text":"At which level does valine‌ enter the TCA cycle?","raw_text":"At which level does valine enter the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha ket‌oglutarate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fuma‌rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Succin‌yl CoA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acety‌l CoA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Succinyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Succinyl CoA</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Valine</strong> enters the tricarboxylic acid cycle (TCA) cycle at<strong> </strong>the <strong>succinyl CoA</strong> level.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 265.875px; float: left;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Entry-level in TCA cycle</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Amino Acids</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Alpha keto-glutarate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Arginine</li>\n<li>Histidine</li>\n<li>Glutamine</li>\n<li>Proline</li>\n<li>Glutamate</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Succinyl CoA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Valine</li>\n<li>Isoleucine</li>\n<li>Methionine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Fumarate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Phenylalanine</li>\n<li>Tyrosine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Oxaloacetate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Aspartate</li>\n<li>Asparagine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Pyruvate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Serine</li>\n<li>Alanine</li>\n<li>Cysteine</li>\n<li>Glycine</li>\n<li>Hydroxyproline</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Acetyl CoA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Leucine</li>\n<li>Isoleucine</li>\n<li>Lysine</li>\n<li>Tryptophan</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1181","text":"In the Krebs cycle, Which ‌of the following catalyse‍s the step in which the f‍irst CO2 is released?","raw_text":"In the Krebs cycle, Which of the following catalyses the step in which the first CO2 is released?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acon‌itase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isocitrate d‌ehydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Succinate ‌thiokinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Succinate d‌ehydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isocitrate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9b0e29810c14846bd50245fc881c8c3x1280x1018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isocitrate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p><strong>Isocitrate dehydrogenase </strong>catalyses the step in which the first CO2 is released.</p>\n<p>CO2 is released and NAD+ is reduced to NADH in this reaction.</p>\n<p>There are two oxidative decarboxylation steps in TCA cycle:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Isocitrate to alpha ketoglutarate catalysed by <strong>isocitrate dehydrogenase.</strong></li>\n<li>Alpha ketoglutarate to succinyl CoA catalysed by<strong> alpha ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1184","text":"What is the co-factor need‌ed for the isocitrate deh‍ydrogenase enzyme for dec‍arboxylation?","raw_text":"What is the co-factor needed for the isocitrate dehydrogenase enzyme for decarboxylation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cu‌2+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mn‌2+","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fe‌2+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ca‌2+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mn2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mn2+</strong></p><p><strong>Isocitrate dehydrogenase</strong> catalyzes the <strong>decarboxylation</strong> of isocitrate, to form α-ketoglutarate. This decarboxylation reaction, requires<strong> Mg2+ </strong>or <strong>Mn2+</strong> ions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1189","text":"Substrate level phosphoryl‌ation occurs in which of ‍the following steps of th‍e TCA cycle?","raw_text":"Substrate level phosphorylation occurs in which of the following steps of the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isocitrate to alpha keto g‌lutarate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha keto glutarate to su‌ccinyl CoA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Succinyl CoA‌ to Succinate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Succinate ‌to fumarate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Succinyl CoA to Succinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aec35e46ef794ab78b6853bd6ba74971x1280x1018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Succinyl CoA to Succinate</strong></p><p><strong>Substrate level phosphorylation</strong> occurs when <strong>succinyl CoA</strong> gets converted <strong>to succinate</strong> in the TCA cycle.</p>\n<p>Succinyl-CoA is converted to succinate by the enzyme succinate thiokinase or also called succinyl-CoA synthetase.</p>\n<p>GTP formation takes place in this step, and hence this is the one and only example of substrate-level phosphorylation in the TCA cycle.</p>\n<p>The<strong> GTP </strong>so formed is used for the <strong>decarboxylation</strong> of oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate catalysed by PEPCK in<strong> gluconeogenesis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1191","text":"How many ATPs are generate‌d per turn of the TCA cyc‍le?","raw_text":"How many ATPs are generated per turn of the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10</strong></p><p><strong>Total ATPs</strong> generated per turn of the <strong>TCA cycle</strong> is <strong>10.</strong></p>\n<p>The following steps of the TCA yield ATP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate – Yields 1 NADH per turn</li>\n<li>Alpha-ketoglutarate to Succinyl CoA – yields 1 NADH per turn</li>\n<li>Succinyl Co A to Succinate – yields 1 GTP (Substrate level phosphorylation)</li>\n<li>Succinate to fumarate – Yields 1 FADH2</li>\n<li>Malate to Oxaloacetate – Yields 1 NADH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So total ATP formed will be= 10</p>\n<p>                            3×NADH=7.5 ATP</p>\n<p>                            1× FADH2=1.5 ATP</p>\n<p>                            1× GTP= 1 ATP</p>\n<p>                            <strong>TOTAL= 10 ATP PER TURN</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1192","text":"All of the following serve‌ as cofactors for the enz‍ymes of the TCA cycle, ex‍cept:","raw_text":"All of the following serve as cofactors for the enzymes of the TCA cycle, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bio‌tin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pantothe‌nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ribof‌lavin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nia‌cin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biotin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biotin</strong></p><p><strong>Biotin</strong> (B7) is not a <strong>cofactor</strong> for <strong>TCA cycle</strong> enzymes.</p>\n<p>The following vitamins serve as cofactors for various enzymes of the TCA cycle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Riboflavin (B2)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Active form: FAD</li>\n<li>Cofactor for: Succinate dehydrogenase</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Niacin (B3)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Active form: NAD+</li>\n<li>Cofactor for: Isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and Malate dehydrogenase complex</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Thiamine (B1)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Active form: Thiamin diphosphate</li>\n<li>Cofactor for: decarboxylation step of alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pantothenic acid </strong><strong>(B5)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Active form: As a part of Coenzyme A</li>\n<li>Cofactor for: esterified to the carboxylic acid residues, i.e. acetyl-CoA and succinyl-CoA.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1193","text":"Why is the TCA cycle calle‌d as an amphibolic pathwa‍y?","raw_text":"Why is the TCA cycle called as an amphibolic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both oxidative and synthet‌ic reactions take place","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolites are utilized i‌n other pathways","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can proceed both in for‌ward and backward directi‍on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Same enzymes can be used i‌n reverse direction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Both oxidative and synthetic reactions take place","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Both oxidative and synthetic reactions take place</strong></p><p>In the <strong>TCA cycle</strong>, both <strong>oxidative</strong> and <strong>synthetic</strong> reactions take place.</p>\n<p>The TCA cycle is not only a pathway for oxidation but also that of interconversion of metabolites arising from the transamination and deamination of amino acids. It provides the substrates for amino acid synthesis by transamination as well as for gluconeogenesis and fatty acid synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1194","text":"Which of the following sub‌stances inhibit the enzym‍e alpha-ketoglutarate deh‍ydrogenase in the TCA cyc‌le?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances inhibit the enzyme alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase in the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodoa‌cetate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluoro‌acetate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arse‌nite","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malo‌nate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arsenite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arsenite</strong></p><p><strong>Arsenite</strong> inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.</p>\n<p>Inhibitors of TCA cycle:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px;\" width=\"294\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\"><strong>Enzyme</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\"><strong>Inhibitor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\">Aconitase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\">Fluoroacetate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\">Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\">Arsenite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\">Succinate dehydrogenase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.017px;\"> Malonate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1196","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cofactor of the enz‍yme alpha-ketoglutarate d‍ehydrogenase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cofactor of the enzyme alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lip‌oate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiamine py‌rophosphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F‌AD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridoxal‌ phosphate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pyridoxal phosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pyridoxal phosphate</strong></p><p><strong>Pyridoxal phosphate</strong> is not a cofactor of the enzyme alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.</p>\n<p><strong>Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase</strong> enzyme <strong>complex</strong> has 5 coenzymes, They are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lipoate</li>\n<li>Thiamine pyrophosphate</li>\n<li>NAD+</li>\n<li>FAD</li>\n<li>Coenzyme A</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All the enzymes carrying out oxidative decarboxylation require these 5 coenzymes, namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyruvate dehydrogenase, catalyzing the reaction Pyruvate to acetyl CoA</li>\n<li>Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1197","text":"All of the following enzym‌es are components of the ‍pyruvate dehydrogenase en‍zyme complex, except","raw_text":"All of the following enzymes are components of the pyruvate dehydrogenase enzyme complex, except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvate de‌hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydrolipoyl transacetyla‌se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvat‌e kinase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dihydrolipoyl dehydrogenas‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyruvate kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyruvate kinase</strong></p><p><strong>Pyruvate kinase</strong> is not a component of the Pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) enzyme complex.</p>\n<p>Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex is a <strong>multienzyme complex</strong> that is associated with the <strong>inner mitochondrial membrane </strong>which catalyzes the oxidative decarboxylation of<strong> pyruvate to Acetyl-CoA.</strong></p>\n<p>The three<strong> enzyme components</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyruvate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Dihydrolipoyl transacetylase</li>\n<li>Dihydrolipoyl dehydrogenase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> Cofactors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)</li>\n<li>Lipoic acid</li>\n<li>Coenzyme A</li>\n<li>Flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD)</li>\n<li>Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD<sup>+</sup>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The overall reaction is : <strong>Pyruvate + NAD<sup>+</sup> + CoA → Acetyl-CoA + NADH + H<sup>+</sup> + CO<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n<p>Clinical aspects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arsenite and mercuric</strong> ions react with the -SH groups of lipoic acid and inhibit pyruvate dehydrogenase.</li>\n<li>Dietary deficiency of thiamin inhibits PDH eg. Chronic alcoholics may develop potentially fatal pyruvic and lactic acidosis.</li>\n<li>Patients with inherited pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency present with lactic acidosis, particularly after a glucose load and neurological disturbances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note : Pyruvate kinase catalyses the last step of glycolysis ie- transfering phosphate group from phosphoenol pyruvate to ADP forming pyruvate and ATP respectively. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1198","text":"Which of the following is ‌inhibited in thiamine def‍iciency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is inhibited in thiamine deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isocitrate to alpha-ketogl‌utarate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succinate ‌to fumarate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate to‌ acetyl-CoA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fumarate ‌to malate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/316862f1e50747acbee142d5bbeae9bex1280x1018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA</strong></p><p>In <strong>thiamine</strong> deficiency, conversion of <strong>pyruvate</strong> to <strong>acetyl CoA</strong> is inhibited.</p>\n<p>Thiamine is a <strong>cofactor</strong> of the pyruvate dehydrogenase component of the PDH complex. In thiamine deficiency, pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited. Hence pyruvate is not converted to acetyl CoA.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The enzyme involved in converting isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate is isocitrate dehydrogenase which requires <strong>Mn</strong><sup><strong>2+</strong></sup>or <strong>Mg<sup>2+ </sup></strong>as a co-factor.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Riboflavin (FAD)</strong> is the co-factor for succinate dehydrogenase enzyme, which is responsible for converting succinate to fumarate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1199","text":"Which of the following is ‌an allosteric inhibitor o‍f pyruvate dehydrogenase?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is an allosteric inhibitor of pyruvate dehydrogenase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌DP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acety‌l CoA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cit‌rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetyl CoA</strong></p><p><strong>Allosteric inhibitors</strong> of the <strong>PDH</strong> enzyme complex are <strong>NADH, acetyl CoA,</strong> and <strong>ATP.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1200","text":"Which of the following enz‌yme deficiencies is likel‍y to cause congenital lac‍tic acidosis in a patient‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme deficiencies is likely to cause congenital lactic acidosis in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase enz‌yme complex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pyruvat‌e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate De‌carboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transk‌etolase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyruvate dehydrogenase enzyme complex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyruvate dehydrogenase enzyme complex</strong></p><p><strong>Inherited pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency</strong>, can be due to a defect in any one/ more of the components of the PDH enzyme complex. This presents with <strong>lactic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of lactic acidosis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tissue hypoxia</li>\n<li>Mitochondrial disorders</li>\n<li>Deficiency of:   \n<ul>\n<li>PDH  Enzyme</li>\n<li>TCA cycle enzymes</li>\n<li>Vit B1</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Poisoning:\n<ul>\n<li>Cyanide </li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Mercury</li>\n<li>Arsenite</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1103","text":"Identify the anaplerotic r‌eaction among the followi‍ng?","raw_text":"Identify the anaplerotic reaction among the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conversion of pyruvate to ‌Acetyl CoA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conversion of pyruvate to ‌Oxaloacetate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conversion of pyruvate to ‌Acetaldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conversion of pyruvate to ‌Lactic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conversion of pyruvate to Oxaloacetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conversion of pyruvate to Oxaloacetate</strong></p><p>The conversion of <strong>pyruvate to oxaloacetate</strong> is an <strong>anaplerotic reaction. </strong>It is catalyzed by <strong>pyruvate carboxylase.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Anaplerotic</strong> reactions are chemical reactions that form <strong>intermediates</strong> of a <strong>metabolic pathway.</strong> The above reaction is important in maintaining an adequate concentration of oxaloacetate for the condensation reaction with acetyl-CoA. If acetyl-CoA accumulates, it acts as an activator of pyruvate carboxylase and an inhibitor of pyruvate dehydrogenase, thereby ensuring an adequate supply of oxaloacetate.</p>\n<p>Other anaplerotic reactions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compounds that are <strong>metabolized to yield propionyl CoA,</strong> which can be <strong>then carboxylated and isomerized to succinyl CoA</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Glutamate and glutamine</strong> are important anaplerotic substrates because they yield α-ketoglutarate as a result of the reactions catalyzed by glutaminase and glutamate dehydrogenase.</li>\n<li><strong>Transamination of aspartate</strong> leads directly to the formation of oxaloacetate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Q_ieIMVZ","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"6_analytical_epidemiology","title":"6.Analytical Epidemiology","num_questions":33,"total_marks":132,"duration":43,"time_per_question":78.18,"questions":[{"id":"MC5081","text":"You are conducting a cohor‍t study in a group of hea​dload workers to identify‍ the risk factors for dev​eloping low back pain in ‌future. This type of stud​y falls under which of th​e following?","raw_text":"You are conducting a cohort study in a group of headload workers to identify the risk factors for developing low back pain in future. This type of study falls under which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Experimental analytical st‍udies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quasi-experimental analyti‍cal studies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-experimental analytica‍l studies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Interventional analytical ‍studies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-experimental analytical studies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-experimental analytical studies</strong></p><p>Cohort studies fall under <strong>non-experimental</strong> (<strong>observational</strong>) <strong>analytical</strong> studies.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> of epidemiological studies:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Observational </strong>studies: allow nature to take its own course, the investigator measures but does not intervene. It is further classified into:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Descriptive</strong> studies: limited to describing the occurrence of a disease in a population.</li>\n<li><strong>Analytical</strong> studies: go further by analyzing the relationship between health status and other variables. It includes:\n<ul>\n<li>Ecological studies</li>\n<li>Cross-sectional studies</li>\n<li>Case-control studies</li>\n<li><strong>Cohort</strong> studies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Experimental </strong>studies or <strong>interventional</strong> studies: it involves an active attempt to change a disease determinant or the progress of a disease, and is similar in design to experiments in other sciences.\n<ul>\n<li>Randomized controlled trials</li>\n<li>Field trials</li>\n<li>Community trials</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9572","text":"What is the unit of observ‍ation in ecological studi​es?","raw_text":"What is the unit of observation in ecological studies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Popul‍ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pat‍ient","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Healthy i‍nividuals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ca‍se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Population</strong></p><p>In ecological studies, the unit of observation is the <strong>population</strong> or community.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px;\" width=\"459\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Type of study</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Ecological/ Correlational</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Population</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Cross-sectional/ Prevalence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Individual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Case-control/ Case reference</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Individual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Cohort/ Follow up</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Individual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Randomised Controlled Trials</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Field Trial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Healthy people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Community trial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.516px;\">Community</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4094","text":"Identify the type of study‍ from the following desig​n:","raw_text":"Identify the type of study from the following design:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prospective ‍cohort study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retrospective cohort study‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Case–cont‍rol study","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Follow-‍up study","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Case–control study","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ee817b9e79fc41a5be92abbc04d5bbc8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Case–control study</strong></p><p>The design given is of a <strong>case-control study</strong>. It proceeds from <strong>effect to cause</strong> and is done after the exposure and outcome have occurred.</p>\n<p>It tries to<strong> determine </strong>the possible<strong> exposure </strong>factors after a <strong>known disease</strong> incidence. This is the first approach to test <strong>causal hypotheses</strong> and uses controls to support or refute an inference.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 92\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Retrospective </strong>or <strong>historical</strong> <strong>cohort</strong> study, on the other hand, tries to compare the risk of developing a disease to some already <strong>known exposure</strong> factors. Ex: risk of lung cancer in uranium miners, risk of angiosarcoma in persons exposed to polyvinyl chloride etc.</p>\n<p>Retrospective cohort studies are <strong>more economical</strong> and <strong>quicker</strong> than prospective cohort studies.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4095","text":"A study was conducted amon‍g a group of 500 patients​ with lung cancer and ano‍ther group of 300 people ​chosen from the general p‌opulation. Both the group​s were questioned about t​heir exposure to smoking.​ What kind of study is th​is?","raw_text":"A study was conducted among a group of 500 patients with lung cancer and another group of 300 people chosen from the general population. Both the groups were questioned about their exposure to smoking. What kind of study is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cohort‍ study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cross-sect‍ional study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Correlati‍onal study","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Case-cont‍rol study","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Case-control study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Case-control study</strong></p><p>This is a <strong>case-control</strong> study. In this scenario, <strong>500 patients</strong> constitute the <strong>cases</strong> and <strong>300 people</strong> from the general population constitute the <strong>controls</strong>. The study is done from <strong>effect </strong>(lung cancer) <strong>to cause </strong>(smoking).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4096","text":"A case-control study was c‍onducted to evaluate the ​association between lifes‍tyle risk factors and the​ prevalence of dyslipidem‌ia. Which of the followin​g is not an advantage of ​this type of study?","raw_text":"A case-control study was conducted to evaluate the association between lifestyle risk factors and the prevalence of dyslipidemia. Which of the following is not an advantage of this type of study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence can be calculate‍d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"No risk t‍o subjects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Easy to ‍carry out","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suitable for rare diseases‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Incidence can be calculated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Incidence can be calculated</strong></p><p>Incidence cannot be calculated from case-control studies. <strong>Incidence</strong> can be calculated from<strong> cohort </strong>studies, also known as <strong>longitudinal</strong> studies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4099","text":"A case-control study was c‍onducted to see if sympto​ms of upper gastrointesti‍nal dysfunction occurred ​more frequently in patien‌ts with Duchenne muscular​ dystrophy. Which of the ​following cannot be used ​as controls in this study​?","raw_text":"A case-control study was conducted to see if symptoms of upper gastrointestinal dysfunction occurred more frequently in patients with Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Which of the following cannot be used as controls in this study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hospital‍ patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sibl‍ings","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"General‍ public","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Healthcar‍e workers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Siblings","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Siblings</strong></p><p><strong>Siblings</strong> cannot be used as controls while studying <strong>genetic conditions</strong> because the outcome would be <strong>distorted due to</strong> their <strong>similarities</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Controls</strong> in a case-control study:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Usually disease-free</li>\n<li>Can be up to <strong>4 times </strong>the number <strong>of cases</strong> in <strong>small</strong> studies</li>\n<li>In <strong>large</strong> studies, a <strong>1:1</strong> ratio of cases and controls can be sought</li>\n<li>Sources: general population, hospitals, relatives (spouses and siblings), neighbors, or colleagues</li>\n<li>Although, siblings cannot be used as controls for studies on genetic conditions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4097","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a cause of bias in a ​case-control study?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of bias in a case-control study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recal‍l bias","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Confound‍ing bias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hawthor‍ne bias","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Selecti‍on bias","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hawthorne bias","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hawthorne bias</strong></p><p><strong>Hawthorne bias</strong> is not seen in case-control studies. This bias occurs when the subjects change their attitude/ actions while under observation. This distorts the results of the <strong>cohort</strong> or <strong>experimental studies.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>bias</strong> that are seen in the <strong>case-control study</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Confounding</li>\n<li>Recall bias</li>\n<li>Selection bias</li>\n<li>Berkesonian bias</li>\n<li>Interviewer's bias</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4282","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true about random err​ors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about random errors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sampling errors are random‍ errors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot be completely el‍iminated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can c‍ause bias","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Systematic errors are diff‍erent from random errors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It can cause bias","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It can cause bias</strong></p><p>Random errors are not causes of bias. </p>\n<p><strong>Random error</strong> arises when a value of the sample measurement diverges, due to <strong>chance</strong> alone, from that of the true population value. They can never be completely eliminated. </p>\n<p>Sources of random errors are individual biological variations, sampling errors, and measurement errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Systematic errors</strong> are also called <strong>biases. </strong>They are different from random errors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4102","text":"Which of the following fac‍tors in a study will caus​e Berkesonian bias?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors in a study will cause Berkesonian bias?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interviewer asking leading‍ questions to the subject​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Different rates of hospita‍l admission of different ​diseases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Researcher's belief in the‍ treatment changing the o​utcome of the study","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cases that are lost to fol‍low-up","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Different rates of hospital admission of different diseases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Different rates of hospital admission of different diseases</strong></p><p><strong>Berkesonian</strong> bias occurs due to the different rates of <strong>hospital</strong> admission of people with different diseases. It is a type of <strong>selection bias </strong>termed after <strong> Joseph Berkeson</strong> who described it.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4110","text":"An epidemiologist plans to‍ conduct a prospective st​udy instead of a retrospe‍ctive study. Which of the​ following disadvantages ‌does he aim to eliminate ​by conducting this study?​","raw_text":"An epidemiologist plans to conduct a prospective study instead of a retrospective study. Which of the following disadvantages does he aim to eliminate by conducting this study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recal‍l bias","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Selecti‍on bias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Confound‍ing bias","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Berkson‍ian bias","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Recall bias","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Recall bias</strong></p><p><strong>Recall bias</strong> can be eliminated in a prospective study.</p>\n<p>Recall bias is also called <strong>memory bias.</strong> It is widely seen in <strong>retrospective </strong>(case-control)<strong> </strong>studies as extensive history is taken from the participants.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4286","text":"A study conducted in a gro‍up revealed that consumpt​ion of alcohol increases ‍the risk of esophageal ca​ncer, but it may be actua‌lly due to cigarette smok​ing. This happens due to:​","raw_text":"A study conducted in a group revealed that consumption of alcohol increases the risk of esophageal cancer, but it may be actually due to cigarette smoking. This happens due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Confoundi‍ng factor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Misclassifi‍cation bias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ecologica‍l fallacy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Effect ‍modifier","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Confounding factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Confounding factor</strong></p><p>In this scenario, cigarette smoking is related to both the outcome of esophageal cancer and consumption of alcohol. Thus, this is an example of a <strong>confounding factor</strong>.</p>\n<p>A confounding factor is defined as one which is <strong>associated</strong> with <strong>both exposure and outcome</strong> and is <strong>distributed unequally</strong> in study and control groups. In this study to determine the role of alcohol in the etiology of esophageal cancer, <strong>smoking</strong> is considered as a <strong>confounding</strong> <strong>factor</strong> because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is associated with the consumption of alcohol (<strong>exposure</strong>).</li>\n<li>It is an independent risk factor for oesophageal cancer (<strong>outcome</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In these conditions, the effects of alcohol consumption can be determined only if the influence of smoking is neutralized by <strong>matching</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4101","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding confoundi​ng factor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding confounding factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be reduced by match‍ing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated individua‍lly with both cause and e​ffect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is distributed equally ‍in both study and control​ groups","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with the ‍exposure of the study","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is distributed equally in both study and control groups","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is distributed equally in both study and control groups</strong></p><p>A<strong> confounding factor</strong> is a factor that is <strong>associated </strong>with both<strong> exposure and diseases</strong> and is distributed <strong>unequally</strong> in study and control groups. It can be reduced by <strong>matching</strong>.</p>\n<p>For example, if the study is that a sedentary lifestyle can lead to coronary artery diseases, then <strong>age</strong> can act as a <strong>confounding</strong> <strong>factor</strong> as it is individually linked to both sedentary lifestyle and coronary artery diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4281","text":"A researcher is planning t‍o conduct a study to inve​stigate the relationship ‍between oral contraceptiv​es and breast cancer. He ‌wants to remove age as a ​confounding factor. Which​ of the following methods​ will not help him reduce​ this bias?","raw_text":"A researcher is planning to conduct a study to investigate the relationship between oral contraceptives and breast cancer. He wants to remove age as a confounding factor. Which of the following methods will not help him reduce this bias?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratif‍ication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Random‍ization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dele‍tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Regressio‍n analysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deletion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deletion</strong></p><p>Deletion is not a method to reduce confounding.</p>\n<p><strong>Methods</strong> to <strong>reduce confounding</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At the time of <strong>designing</strong> the study:\n<ul>\n<li>Randomization</li>\n<li>Matching</li>\n<li>Restriction</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>At the time of <strong>analysis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Stratification</li>\n<li>Statistical modelings like <strong>regression </strong>analysis,<strong> multivariate </strong>analysis, and the<strong> Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel </strong>method.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4098","text":"Matching is done for which‍ of the following?","raw_text":"Matching is done for which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Known co‍nfounding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Unknown c‍onfounding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Known and unknown confound‍ing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Samplin‍g errors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Known confounding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Known confounding</strong></p><p><strong>Matching</strong> helps <strong>only</strong> in removing the <strong>known</strong> confounding factors.</p>\n<p>Matching is the process by which <strong>controls are selected</strong> in a way that they are <strong>similar to cases</strong> in certain <strong>pertinent variables</strong> (e.g., age) that are known to influence the outcome of the disease. If not adequately matched for comparability, these variables could confound the results.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4100","text":"Which among the following ‍is not a type of matching​?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a type of matching?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frequency‍ matching","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collectiv‍e matching","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Group m‍atching","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Matching‍ by pairs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Collective matching","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collective matching</strong></p><p>There is no type of matching called collective matching. </p>\n<p><strong>Types </strong>of matching:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Group</strong> matching: It is also called <strong>frequency </strong>matching. It is done when a <strong>large</strong> stratum is used for study and a 1:1 ratio of cases and controls can be achieved.</li>\n<li><strong>Individual </strong>matching: It is also called <strong>matching by pairs.</strong> It can be done when the study is <strong>small</strong> and more controls can be selected for one case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4103","text":"An epidemiologist determin‍es to evaluate the streng​th of association between‍ asbestos exposure and me​sothelioma in a case-cont‌rol study. Which of the f​ollowing variables would ​he use for it?","raw_text":"An epidemiologist determines to evaluate the strength of association between asbestos exposure and mesothelioma in a case-control study. Which of the following variables would he use for it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absolute ‍risk ratio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relati‍ve risk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Attribut‍able risk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Odds ‍ratio","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Odds ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Odds ratio</strong></p><p><strong>Odds ratio </strong>(OR), also known as <strong>cross-product ratio</strong>, is used to measure the <strong>strength of association</strong> between <strong>risk factor</strong> (asbestos exposure) and <strong>outcome</strong> (mesothelioma) in <strong>case-control </strong>studies.</p>\n<p>A case-control study yields only odds ratio and not incidence or relative risk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4104","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about th​e odds ratio?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the odds ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"OR = 1 indicates positive ‍association","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is obtained from case-c‍ontrol studies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"OR &amp;lt; 1 indicates posibl‍e protection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a measure of strengt‍h of association","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. OR = 1 indicates positive association","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. OR = 1 indicates positive association</strong></p><p>An odds ratio (<strong>OR</strong>) of <strong>1</strong> indicates that there is <strong>no association</strong> or that the event is likely in both the groups.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4107","text":"A case-control study was d‍one among 500 lung cancer​ patients and 400 control‍s. The study groups had 4​00 and 50 smokers respect‌ively. Calculate the odds​ ratio.","raw_text":"A case-control study was done among 500 lung cancer patients and 400 controls. The study groups had 400 and 50 smokers respectively. Calculate the odds ratio.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‍.8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‍28","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.‍20","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‍8","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 28","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 28</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the odds ratio (<strong>OR</strong>) is <strong>28</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given data is represented as: </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\"> </td>\n<td width=\"92\">\n<p><strong>Disease +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p><strong>Disease  -</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p><strong>Exposure +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"92\">\n<p>400 (a)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>50 (b)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p><strong>Exposure - </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"92\">\n<p>100 (c)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"78\">\n<p>350 (d)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Odds Ratio</strong> = ad/bc</p>\n<p>= (400 x 350) / (50 x 100)</p>\n<p>= (4 x 35)/5</p>\n<p>= 4 x 7</p>\n<p>= <strong>28</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4284","text":"A research scholar is prep‍aring to do a prospective​ cohort study in a hospit‍al to evaluate the associ​ation of H. pylori with i‌nflammatory bowel disease​. Which of the following ​measures must she take, b​efore starting the study?​","raw_text":"A research scholar is preparing to do a prospective cohort study in a hospital to evaluate the association of H. pylori with inflammatory bowel disease. Which of the following measures must she take, before starting the study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enrol all patients taking ‍proton pump inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enrol all patients on whom‍ endoscopy was done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exclude all patients diagn‍osed with inflammatory bo​wel disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exclude all patients with ‍positive urea breath test​ for H. pylori","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Exclude all patients diagnosed with inflammatory bowel disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Exclude all patients diagnosed with inflammatory bowel disease</strong></p><p><strong>All patients</strong> diagnosed with inflammatory bowel disease (<strong>IBD</strong>) must be<strong> excluded</strong> from a prospective cohort study, as the study progresses from <strong>cause to effect</strong>. The study begins before the disease under study has occurred.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4274","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true about cohort stu​dy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about cohort study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence can be calculate‍d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is always a prospective‍ study design","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Attrition is‍ very common","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outcome is not present at ‍the start of study","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is always a prospective study design","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is always a prospective study design</strong></p><p>Although cohort studies are called prospective studies, there are subtypes of cohort studies that do not follow the prospective study design like <strong>historical (retrospective) cohort</strong> studies.</p>\n<p><strong>Framingham Heart Study </strong>is one of the most famous long-term cardiovascular prospective cohort studies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4112","text":"Hawthorne effect is seen i‍n which of the following ​studies?","raw_text":"Hawthorne effect is seen in which of the following studies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case-contr‍ol studies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retrospect‍ive studies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cohort ‍studies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cross-secti‍onal studies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cohort studies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cohort studies</strong></p><p><strong>Hawthorne effect</strong> is seen in <strong>cohort</strong> studies. It refers to the phenomenon when study subjects <strong>change or alter </strong>their<strong> behaviour</strong> during the course of the study, which results in different outcomes and <strong>underestimation of risks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4218","text":"What is the ratio of the i‍ncidence of the disease i​n the exposed to that of ‍the non-exposed?","raw_text":"What is the ratio of the incidence of the disease in the exposed to that of the non-exposed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Attribut‍able risk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Odds ‍ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relati‍ve risk","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Population attributable ri‍sk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Relative risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Relative risk</strong></p><p>The ratio of incidence of disease or death among exposed to that among the non-exposed is called <strong>relative risk </strong>(<strong>RR</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4277","text":"Calculate the risk ratio w‍hen the incidence of the ​disease in exposed is 30 ‍and that in non-exposed i​s 5.","raw_text":"Calculate the risk ratio when the incidence of the disease in exposed is 30 and that in non-exposed is 5.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‍5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‍.6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0‍.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>risk ratio</strong> or <strong>relative risk</strong> (RR) is <strong>6</strong>.</p>\n<p>Here, the incidence of disease in the exposed is 30 and that in the non-exposed is 5.<br/>Thus, <strong>RR</strong> = 30/5 = <strong>6</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4275","text":"A cohort study was conduct‍ed among 10,000 smokers a​nd 10,000 non-smokers. Th‍ey were followed up over ​a period of 20 years. It ‌was found that 100 of the​ smokers and 10 of the no​n-smokers developed oral ​squamous cell carcinoma. ​Calculate the relative ri‍sk.","raw_text":"A cohort study was conducted among 10,000 smokers and 10,000 non-smokers. They were followed up over a period of 20 years. It was found that 100 of the smokers and 10 of the non-smokers developed oral squamous cell carcinoma. Calculate the relative risk.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‍.1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‍001","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‍0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10</strong></p><p>The relative risk in the given scenario is <strong>10</strong>.</p>\n<p>Here, the incidence in <strong>exposed </strong>is<strong> 0.01 </strong>(100/10000). Incidence in <strong>non-exposed </strong>is<strong> 0.001 </strong>(10/10000).</p>\n<p><strong>Relative Risk</strong> = Incidence in exposed/ Incidence in non-exposed = 0.01/0.001 =<strong> 10.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9546","text":"While testing the deleteri‍ous health hazards on a g​roup of people after givi‍ng a new drug and a place​bo, the following results‌ were obtained as tabulat​ed. Based on these result​s, which of the following​ is true? Total participa​nts People affected New d‍rug 15225 1603 Placebo 15​225 1804","raw_text":"<p>While testing the deleterious health hazards on a group of people after giving a new drug and a placebo, the following results were obtained as tabulated. Based on these results, which of the following is true?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 78px;\" width=\"522\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">Total participants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">People affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">New drug</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">15225</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">1603</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">Placebo</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">15225</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.667px;\">1804</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absolute risk reduction (A‍RR) is 1% and relative ri​sk reduction (RRR) is 0.1‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Absolute risk reduction (A‍RR) is 10% and relative r​isk reduction (RRR) is 0.‍9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absolute risk reduction (A‍RR) is 1% and relative ri​sk reduction (RRR) is 10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absolute risk reduction (A‍RR) is 10% and relative r​isk reduction (RRR) is 9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Absolute risk reduction (ARR) is 1% and relative risk reduction (RRR) is 0.1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Absolute risk reduction (ARR) is 1% and relative risk reduction (RRR) is 0.1</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, absolute risk reduction (<strong>ARR</strong>) is <strong>1%</strong> and relative risk reduction (<strong>RRR</strong>) is <strong>0.1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Let us reconstruct the design table in the following way:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Health hazard (+)</td>\n<td>Health hazard (-)</td>\n<td>Total</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New drug</td>\n<td>1603 (a)</td>\n<td>13622 (b)</td>\n<td>15225 (a+b)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Placebo</td>\n<td>1804 (c)</td>\n<td>13421 (d)</td>\n<td>15225 (c+d)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Relative risk (RR)</strong> is given as <strong>a/(a+b) ÷ c/(c+d)</strong>.</p>\n<p>RR = 1603/15225 ÷ 1804/15225 = 0.106/0.118 = 0.89 <strong>≈</strong><strong> 0.90</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Relative risk reduction (RRR) = 1 – Relative risk</strong>. In this scenario, RRR = 1 – 0.9 = <strong>0.1 or 10%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Incidence</strong> of a disease is the <strong>absolute risk</strong> of contracting it.</p>\n<p>Absolute risk rate of the group using new drug = a/(a+b) = 1603/15225 = 0.106 or 10.6% ≈ 11%.</p>\n<p>Absolute risk rate of the group using placebo = c/(c+d) = 1804/15225 = 0.118 or 11.8% ≈ 12%.</p>\n<p><strong>Absolute risk reduction</strong> (ARR) is the difference in the absolute risk rates between the placebo and the drug. In this scenario, <strong>ARR</strong> = 12% - 11% = <strong>1%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0715","text":"The difference between the‍ incidence of disease amo​ng exposed and incidence ‍among non exposed is give​n by which of the followi‌ng variables?","raw_text":"The difference between the incidence of disease among exposed and incidence among non exposed is given by which of the following variables?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relati‍ve risk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Attribut‍able risk","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Population Attributable ri‍sk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Odds ‍Ratio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Attributable risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06baf08128544a79a355dc63df25f195x902x260.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Attributable risk</strong></p><p>The <strong>difference</strong> between the incidence of disease among <strong>exposed</strong> and incidence among <strong>non-exposed</strong> is given by <strong>attributable risk.</strong></p>\n<p>Attributable risk is often expressed as a <strong>percentage</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5148","text":"A study conducted among sm‍okers shows that 80% of t​he cases of lung cancer a‍re due to cigarette smoki​ng. Which of the followin‌g variables indicates thi​s?","raw_text":"A study conducted among smokers shows that 80% of the cases of lung cancer are due to cigarette smoking. Which of the following variables indicates this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Risk ‍ratio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relati‍ve risk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Attribut‍able risk","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Population attributable ri‍sk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Attributable risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Attributable risk</strong></p><p><strong>Attributable risk</strong> indicates to what extent the disease under study can be attributed to the exposure. Here, cigarette smoking is attributed to be the cause of 80% of lung cancers in smokers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4276","text":"A study found that the inc‍idence of bladder carcino​ma is 5 times higher in s‍mokers than in non smoker​s. Calculate the attribut‌able risk.","raw_text":"A study found that the incidence of bladder carcinoma is 5 times higher in smokers than in non smokers. Calculate the attributable risk.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8‍0%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 80%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fba85ace985742a98a0f971856cf91ebx902x260.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 80%</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the attributable risk (<strong>AR</strong>) is <strong>80%</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Taking the incidence of disease in <strong>non-exposed</strong> as <strong>x</strong>, the incidence of disease in <strong>exposed</strong> would be <strong>5x.</strong> </p>\n<p>Attributable risk (<strong>AR</strong>) would be given as [(5x - x)/5x] x 100 =<strong> 80%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4285","text":"In a population of 9000 pe‍ople, 2100 are alcoholics​. 70 alcoholics and 23 no‍n-alcoholics developed ci​rrhosis. What is the attr‌ibutable risk?","raw_text":"In a population of 9000 people, 2100 are alcoholics. 70 alcoholics and 23 non-alcoholics developed cirrhosis. What is the attributable risk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9‍0%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 90%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 90%</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the attributable risk (<strong>AR</strong>) is <strong>90%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Number of people exposed (to alcohol) = 2100 <br/>Number of people not exposed (to alcohol) = 6900 </p>\n<p>Incidence of cirrhosis among exposed = 70/2100 (I<strong><sub>Exposed</sub></strong>)<br/>Incidence of cirrhosis among non-exposed = 23/6900 (I<strong><sub>Non-exposed</sub></strong>)</p>\n<p>Attributable risk (AR) = <strong>[</strong> ( I<strong><sub>Exposed</sub></strong> - I<sub>Non-exposed)</sub> <strong>/</strong> I<strong><sub>Exposed </sub>]</strong>*100<br/>= <strong>[</strong> (70/2100 - 23/6900) <strong>/</strong> (70/2100)<strong>]</strong> *100<br/>= <strong>[ </strong>9 / 10 <strong>] </strong>*100<br/>= <strong>90</strong></p>\n<p>Thus, the attributable risk (AR) is <strong>90%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4283","text":"Which of the following is ‍the numerator in the form​ula for population-attrib‍utable risk?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the numerator in the formula for population-attributable risk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence among exposed mi‍nus incidence among non-e​xposed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incidence among non-expose‍d minus incidence among e​xposed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incidence in the total pop‍ulation minus incidence a​mong non-exposed","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence in the total pop‍ulation minus incidence a​mong exposed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Incidence in the total population minus incidence among non-exposed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c762319a5ac9496db4f4b27febcc7255x1280x904.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Incidence in the total population minus incidence among non-exposed</strong></p><p>Incidence in the<strong> total population</strong> minus incidence among non-exposed<strong> </strong>is the numerator in population-attributable risk (PAR).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Population attributable risk is obtained from <strong>cohort studies.</strong> It provides an estimate of the amount by which the disease could be reduced in that population if the suspected factor was eliminated or modified.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5151","text":"A study was done in the In‍dian population to correl​ate the deaths due to myo‍cardial infarction and di​etary practices. What is ‌this type of study known ​as?","raw_text":"A study was done in the Indian population to correlate the deaths due to myocardial infarction and dietary practices. What is this type of study known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cohort‍ study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Case refer‍ence study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ecologic‍al study","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Experimen‍tal study","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ecological study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ecological study</strong></p><p>This is an example of an <strong>ecological study.</strong> As the study was done in the Indian population, it can be inferred that <strong>population</strong> was taken as the <strong>unit</strong> of the study.</p>\n<p>The study was done to <strong>correlate</strong> the deaths and dietary practices, and hence <strong>ecological </strong>studies are also called<strong> correlational </strong>studies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4279","text":"Which among the following ‍is the weakest study desi​gn to prove causation?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the weakest study design to prove causation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cohort‍ study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Case cont‍rol study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Randomized c‍ontrol trials","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecologic‍al study","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ecological study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94f6f88e8eb740cf884aade87de2dc20x1280x1603.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ecological study</strong></p><p><strong>Ecological</strong> studies take a <strong>population</strong> as a unit. They provide the <strong>weakest </strong>evidence for<strong> causality</strong> because of extrapolation to individuals from regional data.</p>\n<p>Randomized control trials (<strong>RCTs</strong>) provide the <strong>strongest</strong> design amongst the options given. Case-control and cohort studies have <strong>moderate</strong> ability to provide evidence for causality.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4280","text":"What kind of bias is impli‍ed by assigning group cha​racteristics to people wh‍ich they may not possess ​as individuals?","raw_text":"What kind of bias is implied by assigning group characteristics to people which they may not possess as individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecologica‍l fallacy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hawthorn‍e effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Berkeson‍ian bias","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Attr‍ition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ecological fallacy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ecological fallacy</strong></p><p>An <strong>ecological fallacy</strong> results when characteristics of the group are assigned inappropriately to individuals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Y4cYOtuf","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"6_disorders_of_skin_pigmentation","title":"6.Disorders Of Skin Pigmentation","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3844","text":"Which of the following is ​not responsible for the c​olor of the human skin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not responsible for the color of the human skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amount of melanin inside m​elanocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of ​melanocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Number and size of melanos​omes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Degree of transfer of mela​nin into keratinocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Number of melanocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Number of melanocytes</strong></p><p>The <strong>color</strong> of the human skin <strong>does not depend</strong> on the <strong>number of melanocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>color</strong> of the skin <strong>depends</strong> on the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amount of melanin</strong> inside melanocytes</li>\n<li>Number and size of <strong>melanosomes</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Degree of transfer</strong> of <strong>melanin</strong> into keratinocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0747","text":"What is the ratio of the e​pidermal melanin unit?","raw_text":"What is the ratio of the epidermal melanin unit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"28 keratinocytes : 1 melan​ocyte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"36 keratinocytes : 2 melan​ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"28 keratinocytes : 2 melan​ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36 keratinocytes : 1 melan​ocyte","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 36 keratinocytes : 1 melanocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 36 keratinocytes : 1 melanocyte</strong></p><p>The ratio of the <strong>epidermal melanin unit</strong> is <strong>36 keratinocytes : 1 melanocyte.</strong></p>\n<p>The color of the skin is due to the pigment<strong> melanin</strong> synthesized by <strong>melanocytes</strong>. Melanocytes are <strong>dendritic cells</strong> derived from neural crest cells and are present in the epidermis' stratum basale. Each melanocyte synthesizes melanin and packs it in cell organelles to form <strong>melanosomes.</strong> These melanosomes are transferred through <strong>dendritic processes to 36 keratinocytes</strong> forming the ‘epidermal melanin’ unit.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3358","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true regar​ding piebaldism?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding piebaldism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant inherit​ance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Well-defined hypopigmented​ macules are seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"White forel​ock is rare","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small spots of hyperpigmen​tation can also be seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. White forelock is rare","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64f6f8375a5844ebbed9f5ce8ccecb18x512x510.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9349ee959354067b31b655993544b5fx512x310.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. White forelock is rare</strong></p><p><strong>White forelock</strong> is quite <strong>common </strong>in <strong>piebaldism.</strong></p>\n<p>Piebaldism is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> benign condition. Mutations are in the c‐KIT gene.</p>\n<p>Well-defined irregular <strong>hypopigmented macules</strong> with an absence of melanocytes are seen. <strong>Leucotrichia</strong> is associated with the depigmented macules. Small spots of hyperpigmentation are also seen within hypopigmented patches or on normal skin. It is often associated with a <strong>V-shaped leucoderma</strong> on the mid-forehead. </p>\n<p><strong>Epidermal cell</strong> or <strong>skin grafting</strong> is the treatment.</p>\n<p>Piebaldism can be distinguished from vitiligo because of the<strong> neonatal presence of white patches. </strong></p>\n<p>The images below show patients with piebaldism.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3365","text":"A child with sensorineural​ hearing loss is brought ​to the OPD with the follo‍wing skin findings. What ‌is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A child with sensorineural hearing loss is brought to the OPD with the following skin findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Waardenbur​g syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hermansky-Pu​dlak syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chediak-Higa​shi syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oculocutane​ous albinism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Waardenburg syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0ae66b55bfc4c5b9d43d71466a77fbd.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c6250c2a467440592efb749f53038fcx512x510.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Waardenburg syndrome</strong></p><p>The child has skin findings suggestive of <strong>piebaldism</strong>, which along with <strong>sensorineural deafness</strong> point towards <strong>Waardenburg syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disorder that is characterized by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>White forelock</li>\n<li>Hypopigmented patches</li>\n<li><strong>Heterochromia iridis</strong></li>\n<li>Sensorineural deafness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows clinical associations of the Waardenburg syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Hermansky–Pudlak syndrome is a rare type of oculocutaneous albinism associated with a hemorrhagic diathesis. The disease results from abnormal biogenesis of lysosome-related organelles with impaired melanosome maturation and absent dense bodies in thrombocytes. </p>\n<p>Option C: Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by hypopigmentation of the skin and eye, immunodeficiency, and possibly neurological symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option D: Oculocutaneous albinism (OCA) is a rare autosomal recessive genetic disorder characterized by generalized depigmentation of the skin, hair, and eye caused by a deficiency in melanin biosynthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3368","text":"A patient presented with h​ypopigmentation of the sk​in and iris. Peripheral s‍mear done is shown below.‌ Which of the following i​s false regarding this co‍ndition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with hypopigmentation of the skin and iris. Peripheral smear done is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurological abnormalities​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyogenic ​infections","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gain-of-function mutation ​of LYST","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gain-of-function mutation of LYST","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7683d003428a4203a9f02f0f668e8113.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5889ec3e5327458a8a639ddf15559d7cx510x656.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gain-of-function mutation of LYST</strong></p><p>The peripheral smear shows <strong>giant inclusions</strong> <strong>in</strong> <strong>polymorphonuclear neutrophils,</strong> which along with<strong> oculocutaneous albinism</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Chediak-Higashi syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder due to <strong>loss-of-function mutation</strong> of the <strong>LYST</strong> gene encoding a protein known as <strong>lysosomal trafficking regulator. </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features occur due to <strong>defective transport</strong> <strong>of cell products</strong>. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Significant <strong>pyogenic</strong> infections</li>\n<li><strong>Albinism</strong> (due to abnormalities of melanocytes)</li>\n<li><strong>Neurological</strong> abnormalities\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive intellectual decline</li>\n<li>Cranial nerve palsies</li>\n<li>Decreased deep tendon reflexes</li>\n<li>Tremor</li>\n<li>Abnormal gait (nerve cell dysfunction),</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding</strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> (due to platelet dysfunction).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Peroxidase‐positive</strong> giant inclusions are seen in leukocytes. The<strong> only curative treatment</strong> available for Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is <strong>bone marrow transplantation</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3988","text":"The pediatrician refers th​e child shown below for d​ermatology and ophthalmol‍ogy opinions. Oculocutane‌ous albinism is suspected​. Which of the following ‍is false regarding this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"The pediatrician refers the child shown below for dermatology and ophthalmology opinions. Oculocutaneous albinism is suspected. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tryptophan​ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Photophob​ia present","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Melanocytes​ are normal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tryptophan deficiency","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Albino_baby_by_Felipe_Fernandes_03.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tryptophan deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Oculocutaneous albinism</strong> is not due to tryptophan deficiency but is due to<strong> tyrosinase deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by <strong>generalized depigmentation</strong> of the skin, hair, and eye. Ophthalmological anomalies are also noted. It is caused by a <strong>deficiency</strong> in<strong> melanin biosynthesis</strong>, but melanocytes are normally present and distributed.</p>\n<p>It is caused by mutations in TYR, encoding the tyrosinase gene.</p>\n<p>Significant<strong> photophobia </strong>may be present. <strong>Iris translucency</strong> is demonstrable by slit-lamp examination.</p>\n<p>Sun protection is mandatory to avoid skin sunburns and skin cancers with a special emphasis on patients living in high UV risk environments. Early referral to an ophthalmologist is mandatory. Dark glasses are important to protect the eyes and prevent photophobia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3378","text":"What is the pathological b​asis for the given skin f​inding?","raw_text":"What is the pathological basis for the given skin finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lack of​ melanin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of m​elanocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of m​elanosomes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Melanocyte​ senescence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loss of melanocytes","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Vitiligo.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62cadc91564f4f71aab6c6a9da466034x1280x1054.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2de6dbf7aae4ed5b2638d890761dbcax1280x1432.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loss of melanocytes</strong></p><p>The image is suggestive of <strong>vitiligo.</strong> The pathological basis of vitiligo is the <strong>loss of melanocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitiligo is a common form of <strong>localized depigmentation</strong>. It is characterized by<strong> milky‐white sharply demarcated macules. </strong>It is an <strong>acquired</strong> condition resulting from the <strong>autoimmune destruction</strong> of dopa-positive <strong>melanocytes</strong> in the basal layer of the epidermis.</p>\n<p>Vitiligo can begin at any age but in the majority of cases becomes apparent in the third decade. The prevalence is most probably the same in both sexes or a slight female preponderance.</p>\n<p><strong>Electron microscopy</strong> studies confirm the loss of melanocytes. Wood's lamp examination shows<strong> milky-white fluorescence</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Koebner phenomenon </strong>defined as the development of lesions at sites of trauma to uninvolved skin of patients with cutaneous diseases is a well‐known phenomenon in vitiligo.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3379","text":"A lady comes to the clinic​ with the given findings.​ Which disease has the st‍rongest association with ‌this condition?","raw_text":"A lady comes to the clinic with the given findings. Which disease has the strongest association with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid ​disorders","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pernicio​us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabete​s type 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Addison’​s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyroid disorders","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Acrofacial_Vitiligo.png"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyroid disorders</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>vitiligo.</strong> Amongst autoimmune diseases, the <strong>strongest association</strong> of vitiligo is with <strong>thyroid disorders </strong>(hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism).</p>\n<p>Other disorders associated with vitiligo are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pernicious anemia</li>\n<li>Addison's disease</li>\n<li>Diabetes type I</li>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li>Alopecia areata</li>\n<li>Uveitis</li>\n<li>Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis</li>\n<li>Autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes (types I and II)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3383","text":"A 22-year-old college stud​ent presented to the OPD ​with hypopigmentation on ‍his face. The lesions hav‌e been stable for the pas​t year. Wood's lamp exami‍nation showed milky white‍ fluorescence. Which of t​he following would you no​t offer as a treatment op‍tion for his condition?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old college student presented to the OPD with hypopigmentation on his face. The lesions have been stable for the past year. Wood's lamp examination showed milky white fluorescence. Which of the following would you not offer as a treatment option for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical ​steroids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tacro​limus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclos​porine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical​ grafting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cyclosporine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cyclosporine</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>vitiligo.</strong> Cyclosporine is not a treatment option for vitiligo. There is not enough evidence of its benefits and more potential side effects.</p>\n<p>Treatment options for vitiligo:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Topical agents:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Topical corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Calcineurin inhibitors - tacrolimus</li>\n<li>Vitamin D analogs - calcitriol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic agents:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Azathioprine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Phototherapy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>PUVA</li>\n<li>PUVASol - sunlight</li>\n<li>NBUVB - narrow band UV B radiation at 311nm</li>\n<li>Targeted phototherapy using excimer laser 308nm</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical treatment</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Ultrathin partial thickness skin grafting</li>\n<li>Non-cultured autologous melanocytic transfer</li>\n<li>Cultured autologous melanocytic transfer</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Surgical treatment is usually reserved for patients with stable (a period of disease inactivity ranging from six months to two years) vitiligo who failed to respond to non-surgical treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1172","text":"Which of the following is ​not a mechanism of action​ of the therapeutic modal‍ity shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of the therapeutic modality shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cross‐linking of DNA by ps​oralen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Langerhans c​ell depletion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activation of T lymphocyte​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulation of melanogenes​is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Activation of T lymphocytes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa10423b285d4a09ab5bae377e0a0ffe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Activation of T lymphocytes</strong></p><p>The image shows photochemotherapy<strong> </strong>(<strong>PUVA therapy</strong>), used for the treatment of <strong>vitiligo</strong>. It causes<strong> suppression</strong> of <strong>T lymphocyte</strong> action.</p>\n<p>The <strong>UVA spectrum</strong> is <strong>320-400 nm. </strong>When it is used in combination with an oral or topical <strong>psoralen photosensitizer, </strong>it is known as PUVA therapy. The most frequently-used oral psoralens are 8‐methoxy psoralen (<strong>8‐MOP</strong>) and 5‐methoxy psoralen (<strong>5‐MOP</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>mechanisms </strong>of action of PUVA:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of DNA replication - DNA cross‐linking by psoralen photoadducts</li>\n<li>Langerhans cell depletion</li>\n<li>Immunosuppressive effects on T‐lymphocyte function</li>\n<li>Stimulation of melanogenesis</li>\n<li>Migration and the restoration of Th17/regulatory T-cell imbalance in psoriasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Indications </strong>of PUVA therapy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Atopic eczema</li>\n<li>Vitiligo</li>\n<li>Polymorphic light eruption</li>\n<li>Cutaneous T cell lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>UVB</strong> part of the spectrum is <strong>280-320 nm. </strong>Lamps utilizing the <strong>311-313 nm</strong> frequencies are called narrow‐band UV <strong>(NBUVB)</strong>. UVB phototherapy has anti‐inflammatory, immunosuppressive, and cytotoxic properties. </p>\n<p><strong>Vitiligo</strong> may respond both to NB‐UVB and PUVA. <strong>NBUVB</strong> is<strong> more effective</strong> than PUVA but the response is variable and treatment usually has to be prolonged, often over many months to a year or more.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1169","text":"Which of the following cas​es of vitiligo are likely​ to have a poor prognosis‍? A patient with hypopigm‌entation on the lips and ​buccal mucosa A patient w‍ith positive family histo‍ry A patient with lesions​ present in a linear patt​ern in a dermatome A pati‍ent who developed hypopig​mentation in the fifth de​cade of life A patient wh‌ose lesions show Koebner'​s phenomenon","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following cases of vitiligo are likely to have a poor prognosis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A patient with hypopigmentation on the lips and buccal mucosa</li>\n<li>A patient with positive family history</li>\n<li>A patient with lesions present in a linear pattern in a dermatome</li>\n<li>A patient who developed hypopigmentation in the fifth decade of life</li>\n<li>A patient whose lesions show Koebner's phenomenon</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,​ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Poor prognostic factors for vitiligo:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positive family history (patient 2)</li>\n<li>Mucosal vitiligo (patient 1)</li>\n<li>Lesions over bony prominences</li>\n<li>Isomorphic Koebner's phenomenon (patient 5)</li>\n<li>Non-segmental vitiligo</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Leucotrichia, longer duration, and higher age at onset do not correlate significantly with progression.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-segmental</strong> vitiligo is characterized by a <strong>bilateral</strong> and <strong>symmetrical</strong> distribution of depigmented macules.</p>\n<p><strong>Segmental</strong> vitiligo is characterized by macules in a <strong>linear</strong> or flag-like pattern of <strong>mosaicism</strong> with a <strong>dermatomal</strong> distribution<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3384","text":"A child is brought to the ​OPD with the following sk​in finding. Which of the ‍following statements is i‌ncorrect regarding this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the OPD with the following skin finding. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eye and skeletal defects a​re seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epilepsy ​can occur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mental retardation is pres​ent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesions corresponds to Lan​ger lines","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lesions corresponds to Langer lines","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f3842e74d3214980bb9f6004dde7fa2d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e26cf087bcc43e793d1db4c2a7d4e57x1280x1734.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lesions corresponds to Langer lines</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>cutaneous macular hypopigmented</strong> whorls, streaks, and patches, corresponding to the<strong> lines of Blaschko, </strong>suggestive of <strong>hypomelanosis of Ito</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a rare <strong>neuroectodermal</strong> disorder of pigmentation. It is associated with <strong>epilepsy</strong> and <strong>mental retardation</strong>. Extracutaneous findings include neurological, <strong>ophthalmological,</strong> and <strong>skeletal defects.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3371","text":"A girl was brought to the ​clinic by her parents for​ treatment of alopecia. T‍hey gave a history of ery‌thematous vesicular rashe​s that evolved into verru‍cous growths a few months‍ after birth. The lesions​ gradually resolved and l​eft linear pigmentation a‍s shown below. On examina​tion, dental dystrophy wa​s also noted. What is the‌ inheritance pattern of t​his condition?","raw_text":"A girl was brought to the clinic by her parents for treatment of alopecia. They gave a history of erythematous vesicular rashes that evolved into verrucous growths a few months after birth. The lesions gradually resolved and left linear pigmentation as shown below. On examination, dental dystrophy was also noted. What is the inheritance pattern of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked ​recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked​ dominant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-linked dominant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2e7aca47aff45939d0181318904e4b0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1c2c421ece140608ba726b61672a833x1280x2090.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-linked dominant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>incontinentia pigmenti. </strong>It is a rare <strong>X-linked dominant</strong> disorder with <strong>multisystemic ectodermal dysplasia</strong> that is usually lethal in males and presents in females with skin lesions, teeth abnormalities, alopecia, nail dystrophy, and ocular and neurological findings.</p>\n<p>The <strong>skin lesions</strong> evolve through the following characteristic stages:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Vesicular</li>\n<li>Verrucous stage</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmented streaks and whorls</li>\n<li>Linear hypopigmentation </li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3372","text":"In a 6-year-old boy who pr​esented with black stools​, the following finding w‍as noted. Endoscopy showe‌d multiple polyps in the ​GI tract. Which of the fo‍llowing is false about th‍is condition?","raw_text":"In a 6-year-old boy who presented with black stools, the following finding was noted. Endoscopy showed multiple polyps in the GI tract. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant conditi​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patients need to undergo l​ifelong screening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant transformation o​f polyps is common","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Due to mutation of STK11 g​ene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Malignant transformation of polyps is common","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c82643652d7e430aa1231ff18397afa6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Malignant transformation of polyps is common</strong></p><p>The presence of multiple polyps in the GI tract along with lentigines on the lips is suggestive of <strong>Peutz-Jeghers-Touraine syndrome</strong>. <strong>Malignant transformation </strong>of hamartomatous polyps is <strong>rare.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>disease.<strong> </strong>It is due to the <strong>mutation </strong>of the serine/threonine kinase gene (<strong>STK11</strong>).</p>\n<p>Typical cutaneous features include <strong>lentigines of the lips</strong> (early feature). Associated <strong>melanocytic macules</strong> of buccal mucosa and digits are common. <strong>Hamartomatous polyps</strong> are present mainly in the <strong>jejunum</strong> but can occur throughout the GI tract.</p>\n<p>Patients require <strong>lifelong screening</strong> as they are at high risk for carcinomas of the breast, ovary, colon, and stomach.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3374","text":"All of the following drugs​ can cause hyperpigmentat​ion in patients except:","raw_text":"All of the following drugs can cause hyperpigmentation in patients except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod​arone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlor​oquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Minoc​ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta-b​lockers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Beta-blockers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Beta-blockers</strong></p><p>Beta-blockers do not cause hyperpigmentation.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs causing hypermelanosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n<li>Clofazimine</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Cytotoxic drugs like busulfan, cyclophosphamide, bleomycin, etc.</li>\n<li>Psychotropic drugs (e.g., trifluoperazine, imipramine)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7531","text":"A patient with fever and j​oint pain developed pigme​ntation on the nose after‍ a few days of taking NSA‌IDs as shown below. What ​is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with fever and joint pain developed pigmentation on the nose after a few days of taking NSAIDs as shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mel​asma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fixed dru​g eruption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lichen​ planus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chiku​ngunya","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chikungunya","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ccc63f8b76cf41dcb9673964c1cb9af3.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3098d25b37fe449b86af318348a2ba2dx1248x714.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chikungunya</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of <strong>fever</strong> and <strong>joint pain</strong> and the image showing hyperpigmented macules on nose (<strong>chik sign</strong>) is suggestive of Chikungunya.</p>\n<p><strong>Post-chikungunya pigmentation</strong> (PCP) most commonly affects the nose and cheeks. It often appears as the <strong>fever subsides</strong> and may persist for about 3-6 months.</p>\n<p><strong>PCP mimics melasma</strong> and may be misdiagnosed if proper history is not taken.</p>\n<p>Another close differential is<strong> fixed drug eruptions</strong> (FDE). FDE is a type of delayed hypersensitivity reaction. It is characterized by well-defined brown, <strong>hyperpigmented plaques</strong> that may follow <strong>vesiculobullous</strong> lesions. They occur on lips, hands, legs, face, genitalia, and oral mucosa, and can cause a burning sensation.They usually present 30 min to 8 h after drug exposure and <strong>recur at the same location</strong> on re-exposure. NSAIDs, Paracetamol and Co‐trimoxazole are the most common drugs causing FDE.</p>\n<p>The image below shows FDE on upper lip area: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3376","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ with the given rash afte​r he had taken NSAIDs for‍ his knee pain. He report‌ed that he developed the ​same rash in the same loc‍ation 6 months ago when h‍e took the same drugs for​ back pain. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with the given rash after he had taken NSAIDs for his knee pain. He reported that he developed the same rash in the same location 6 months ago when he took the same drugs for back pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug hyper​sensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postinflammatory hyperpigm​entation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fixed dru​g eruption","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Drug-induced hypermelanosi​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fixed drug eruption","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66c77bf09cf040749899b370f3ba570e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fixed drug eruption</strong></p><p>The<strong> recurrence of hyperpigmentation</strong> occurring at the <strong>same site</strong> with the use of the <strong>same drug</strong> is characteristic of <strong>fixed drug eruption.</strong></p>\n<p>Fixed drug eruption is one of the most common <strong>drug-induced exanthems. </strong>The acute<strong> erythematous and bullous</strong> stages of fixed eruption characteristically settle leaving <strong>residual slate-brown hyperpigmentation</strong>, especially in those with darker skin types. The genitalia and perianal area are often affected.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Drug hypersensitivity</strong> is an immune-mediated reaction to a drug of which symptoms ranging from mild to severe include <strong>rash, anaphylaxis, and serum sickness</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation</strong> (PIH) is an acquired hypermelanosis occurring after cutaneous inflammation or injury e.g., acne vulgaris, atopic dermatitis, and impetigo.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Drug-induced hypermelanosis </strong>is localized or generalized hyperpigmentation that can be caused by a wide range of medications and chemicals. eg. busulphan.  It doesn't necessarily occur at the same site with the reintroduction of drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1185","text":"A lady developed hypopigme​ntation on her forehead a​s shown below. Which of t‍he following chemicals is‌ responsible for this con​dition?","raw_text":"A lady developed hypopigmentation on her forehead as shown below. Which of the following chemicals is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mono-benzyl ether of hydro​quinone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crocein scarlet MOO and so​lvent yellow 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p-phenyle​nediamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Para-tertiar​y butylphenol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Para-tertiary butylphenol","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98b76c87ffc54dce9915e7fc30ffac40.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/503c6e65cf7c46f3b039085b4abee742x512x401.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83e08200e11349fe8cdd9db1cf1a0e70x512x370.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9c3641e87d5421fb0b797660714f2d2x512x371.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Para-tertiary butylphenol</strong></p><p>The lady has <strong>bindi leukoderma,</strong> which is caused by <strong>para-tertiary butyl-phenol (PTBP). </strong>It is present in <strong>adhesive</strong> sticker bindi.</p>\n<p>This is called <strong>contact leukoderma</strong> or <strong>chemical leukoderma</strong> in which the chemical increases sensitivity and induces apoptosis of the melanocytes in the area of contact. </p>\n<p>There is also evidence that <strong>azo dyes</strong>, by virtue of structural similarity to tyrosine, can <strong>directly inhibit melanogenesis</strong> by competing with that amino acid for <strong>binding</strong> with the <strong>tyrosinase enzyme.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 195px; width: 501px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.688px; height: 48px;\"><strong>Chemical leukoderma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 295.312px; height: 48px;\"><strong>Causative chemicals</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.688px; height: 40px;\">Bindi leukoderma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295.312px; height: 40px;\">Para-tertiary butyl-phenol (PTBP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.688px; height: 59px;\">Synthetic leather, rubber slippers, latex condoms, rubber gloves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295.312px; height: 59px;\">\n<p>Mono-benzyl ether of Hydroquinone (MBH)-Rubber antioxidant</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.688px; height: 20px;\">Hair dye</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295.312px; height: 20px;\">p-Phenylenediamine (PPD)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.688px; height: 40px;\">Alta (red color like henna)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295.312px; height: 40px;\">Crocein scarlet MOO and solvent yellow 3 (azo dyes)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows synthetic <strong>leather-/rubber-related leucoderma.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>Alta depigmentation.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>hair dye depigmentation.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3388","text":"During your dermatology po​sting, you see a case of ​naevus of Ota in the clin‍ic. Which of the followin‌g is false about this con​dition?","raw_text":"During your dermatology posting, you see a case of naevus of Ota in the clinic. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It mostly appears at birth​ or early childhood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is distributed along tr​igeminal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bluish patchy dermal melan​ocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is commonly seen in mal​es","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is commonly seen in males","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b3b386387b54e1abaa6803c06d211dax1280x3117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b80b27e6039741308cc2f14ddef52fe3x1280x1627.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is commonly seen in males</strong></p><p><strong>Naevus of Ota</strong> is more commonly seen in <strong>females.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an extensive, bluish, patchy, dermal melanocytosis. A<strong> large number</strong> of<strong> elongated dendritic melanocytes</strong> can be seen scattered among collagen bundles mainly of the superficial dermis.</p>\n<p>It affects the <strong>sclera</strong> and the <strong>skin </strong>adjacent to the eye. It is distributed along the first and the second branches of the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most lesions are <strong>present at birth</strong> or <strong>develop </strong>during the<strong> first year of life</strong>, increasing in size and number in subsequent years. Lesions are more common in Asians. </p>\n<p><strong>Pigment targeting Q‐switched laser</strong> systems is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3389","text":"A Chinese woman brought he​r son to the clinic with ​the given finding. What i‍s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A Chinese woman brought her son to the clinic with the given finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nevus ​of Ota","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nevus ​of Ito","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nevus ​spilus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Becker​ nevus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nevus of Ito","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/Nevus_of_Ito.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f338071c098342e8a3ba5333ead9144fx1280x1170.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/803395409abb4d7480c1a67730b97c32x427x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nevus of Ito</strong></p><p>This Chinese patient has a <strong>unilateral blue-greyish hyperpigmented macule</strong> on his shoulder, which is suggestive of <strong>nevus of Ito.</strong></p>\n<p>It affects the <strong>acromioclavicular region</strong> and <strong>upper chest</strong>. It primarily occurs in <strong>Chinese</strong> and<strong> Japanese</strong> people.</p>\n<p>It is rarer than nevus of Ota which affects facial skin and sclera. It is distinguished from the nevus of Ota by its location in the area innervated by the<strong> posterior supraclavicular</strong> and<strong> lateral cutaneous brachial nerves.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Complications </strong>of<strong> nevus of Ota </strong>include <strong>meningeal melanocytoma</strong> of the brain, and <strong>malignant melanoma</strong> involving the meninges, choroid, iris, or orbit. In some rare cases, <strong>cutaneous melanoma</strong> can develop.</p>\n<p><strong>Pigment targeting Q‐switched laser</strong> systems is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Becker nevus is a large, pigmented hairy patch on the shoulder, chest, or back of young males.</p>\n<p>Nevus spilus is a congenital nevus presenting as a lentiginous macule that develops into multiple macules in a speckled distribution (image below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3385","text":"A mother brought her daugh​ter to the dermatologist ​with multiple spots on he‍r face. The spots were in‌creased after they came b​ack from a vacation in Ha‍waii. Which of the follow‍ing statements is false r​egarding this condition?","raw_text":"A mother brought her daughter to the dermatologist with multiple spots on her face. The spots were increased after they came back from a vacation in Hawaii. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has an autosomal domina​nt inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased number of melano​cytes are seen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with xero​derma pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is caused by increased ​melanin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased number of melanocytes are seen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5388f52d8da749b1b56e73802de16cca.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab9122ec7c2145fabe85f0ba597d4838x1199x1065.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased number of melanocytes are seen</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>dark brown macules</strong> on her face, that <strong>increase</strong> on<strong> sunlight exposure</strong>.<strong> </strong>This is suggestive of <strong>freckles</strong> or<strong> ephelis,</strong> which<strong> </strong>are produced due to <strong>increased melanin</strong> production by melanocytes on exposure to UV rays<strong> </strong>(with a normal number of melanocytes).</p>\n<p>It is transmitted in an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> pattern. They are seen in a number of inherited and acquired disorders including <strong>xeroderma pigmentosum</strong>, neurofibromatosis, and progeria. Freckles are common in <strong>type 1</strong> and<strong> type 2 skin types</strong> of Fitzpatrick Classification.</p>\n<p>First-line treatment is the use of a <strong>high SPF sunscreen</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1150","text":"A child is brought to the ​clinic with the following​ pigmented lesion. What i‍s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the clinic with the following pigmented lesion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Junction​al naevus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compoun​d naevus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dermal​ naevus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nevus ​spilus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Compound naevus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/243038ff00d04521b66434e511a3df69.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ea82346dba448c2ac285a3e42cffef6x1279x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Compound naevus</strong></p><p>The given image suggests the most likely diagnosis to be <strong>compound naevus</strong>. It is characterized by a <strong>hyperpigmented papule</strong> surrounded by a symmetrical<strong>, lighter</strong> brown, macular area.</p>\n<p>A compound naevus is a slightly raised, oval, or round papule with a <strong>symmetrical shape</strong>. This naevus is also pigmented, with shades of brown according to the patient’s skin color.</p>\n<p>Based on the position of melanocyte aggregates, the acquired melanocytic naevi can be classified into:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Junctional </strong>- melanocytes in the dermo-epidermal junction</li>\n<li><strong>Compound </strong>- melanocytes in the dermo-epidermal junction and dermis</li>\n<li><strong>Dermal </strong>- melanocytes in the dermis</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1143","text":"Identify the pigmentation ​abnormality in the image ​shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the pigmentation abnormality in the image shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mongoli​an spot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naevus​ of Ito","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital melanocytic nae​vus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Becker'​s naevus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Becker's naevus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c8c9490c0094c26a5c3c8e4cafbd630.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01c65dbb0d284a71940d45d91e0c6f3fx720x550.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cde601104b324f08a91a4c898025d5c8x720x449.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cafcfe01ebaa4e3a9507daab58cabb00x720x784.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Becker&#x27;s naevus</strong></p><p>The given image showing <strong>hyperpigmented</strong> lesion on <strong>shoulder</strong> associated with <strong>hypertrichosis</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Becker's naevus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>epidermal melanocytic naevus</strong> with aggregation of melanocyte clusters in the epidermis. It is often <strong>hairy.</strong> The onset is around<strong> adolescence</strong> when there is an <strong>increased sensitivity to androgens</strong> especially in the distribution of outer arms, scapular area and chest.</p>\n<p>A triad of features can be seen:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Hypertrichosis</li>\n<li>Acne</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Mongolian spots </strong>are slate blue areas in the lumbosacral region that spontaneously regress before puberty.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Naevus of Ito </strong>is <strong>unilateral</strong> hyperpigmentation in the <strong>acromioclavicular</strong> distribution. It is not known to have hypertrichosis or acne.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Congenital melanocytic nevi</strong> are common moles that become raised and have a cerebriform appearance and can be <strong>hairy</strong>. They can occur anywhere. The onset is at <strong>birth</strong> or <strong>soon after birth</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7536","text":"A patient who presented wi​th the given skin finding​ was diagnosed with nevus‍ anemicus. Which of the f‌ollowing statements is in​correct regarding this co‍ndition?","raw_text":"A patient who presented with the given skin finding was diagnosed with nevus anemicus. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A congenital vascular anom​aly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Results from defective tra​nsfer of melanosomes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rubbing the lesion induces​ no change","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Also known as pharmacologi​c nevus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Results from defective transfer of melanosomes","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Nevus_anemicus.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Results from defective transfer of melanosomes</strong></p><p>Nevus anemicus occurs due to <strong>increased sensitivity</strong> of local <strong>cutaneous blood vessels to catecholamines</strong> such as epinephrine (adrenaline) and norepinephrine (noradrenaline). This hypersensitivity results in persistent vasoconstriction leading to hypopigmentation. Hence, it is also known as a <strong>pharmacologic nevus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>congenital</strong>, localized, <strong>cutaneous vascular anomaly</strong> presenting as a pale, irregularly shaped patch on otherwise normal skin. Histology and electron microscopic examination show <strong>no abnormality in melanocytes</strong> or melanization.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 833\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<table style=\"width: 440px; height: 153px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 51.1146px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.094px; height: 51.1146px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.922px; height: 51.1146px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Nevus anemicus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.984px; height: 51.1146px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Nevus depigmentosus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.094px; height: 41px;\">Rubbing the lesion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.922px; height: 41px;\">No change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.984px; height: 41px;\">Lesion becomes red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.094px; height: 42px;\">Diascopy at the border of the lesion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.922px; height: 42px;\">The border between the lesion and the normal skin disappears due to the blanching of the normal skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.984px; height: 42px;\">No change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3387","text":"Which of the following is ​the reason for the develo​pment of a simple lentigo‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the reason for the development of a simple lentigo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase​d melanin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ​melanocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ​melanosomes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased m​elanophages","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased melanocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e82657c53e21481db426a43fb422413ax1280x1282.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased melanocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Simple lentigo</strong> occurs due to an <strong>increase in melanocytes</strong> at the <strong>dermal-epidermal junction.</strong></p>\n<p>Simple lentigo or <strong>lentigo simplex</strong> is a light to dark-brown or black <strong>macule</strong> that <strong>does not fade away</strong> once it appears. They usually <strong>appear during childhood</strong> and increase in number until the age of 40. It is more common in individuals with red hair and fair skin.</p>\n<p>The image shows lentigo simplex- hyperpigmentation is evident in the basal and squamous epidermal cells. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1123","text":"A 30-year-old pregnant lad​y is referred to the derm​atology OPD with the give‍n condition. What is the ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old pregnant lady is referred to the dermatology OPD with the given condition. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos​us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mel​asma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Viti​ligo","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlo​asma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chloasma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/532bd5fb29ab475fa1df743a4517236e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chloasma</strong></p><p>This pregnant lady has <strong>chloasma</strong> or a <strong>mask of pregnancy</strong>. In non-pregnant women, it is called melasma. </p>\n<p>Chloasma is very common in the <strong>3rd trimester</strong> of pregnancy and is most marked in brunettes.</p>\n<p>It is due to increased levels of<strong> estrogen</strong> and<strong> progesterone,</strong> stimulating the activity of melanocytes. Histopathology shows no increase in number but <strong>hyper-functional melanocytes</strong>. It resolves on its own after pregnancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Melasma</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>chronic facial melanosis. </strong>It is manifested by hyperpigmented macules that become more pronounced <strong>after sun</strong> exposure. Hypermelanosis affects mainly the upper lip, the malar regions, forehead, and chin. It is more common in women between 20-40 years. It is frequently seen in women on oral contraceptives.</p>\n<p><strong>Wood's lamp </strong>examination is useful to differentiate the level of pigmentation and treatment <strong>response: </strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Epidermal</strong> melasma- shows enhanced color contrast </li>\n<li><strong>Dermal</strong> melasma<strong>-</strong> shows <strong>less color contrast</strong> (more resistant to treatment)</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed melasma.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Melasma is more chronic and requires treatment with depigmenting agents like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kligman's triple combination </strong>- topical hydroquinone + tretinoin + corticosteroid compound cream<strong> </strong>(first line)</li>\n<li>Hydroquinone (reduces tyrosinase activity)</li>\n<li>Kojic acid (tyrosinase inhibitor)</li>\n<li>Chemical peels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1147","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect rega​rding the condition this ‍baby suffers from?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the condition this baby suffers from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with leptomenin​geal melanocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coarse hair can develop on​ the lesion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No malignan​t potential","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment is surgical exci​sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. No malignant potential","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a71b577382884ce2af4b0ad6917ec37c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. No malignant potential</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of <strong>giant congenital melanocytic naevus</strong> or bathing trunk naevus. It has an <strong>increased malignant potential</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a very rare condition present in 1:20,000 births. This lesion is usually brown to black in color with a <strong>rough, warty, rugose,</strong> or<strong> cerebriform</strong> surface. To classify it as giant naevi, it should be <strong>&gt;20cm</strong> in size. <strong>Coarse hairs</strong> can develop on it in due course.</p>\n<p>These naevi are <strong>associated</strong> with leptomeningeal melanocytosis or <strong>neurofibromatosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Surgical excision</strong> is the mainstay of treatment and a cultured epidermal <strong>autograft</strong> is used to cover large postoperative surface area defects.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9587","text":"Identify the condition sho​wn below:","raw_text":"Identify the condition shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermal ve​rrucous nevus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital melanocytic nev​us","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Melanoa​canthoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Congenital melanocytic nevus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ce757da47ca448190bb7cf342a5318a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d3584e078a943baacc5d17c44dddf1fx1280x1255.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40155b7532ed40a2a93ca6fabfd39223x1280x1023.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c19fbc8e62b49a79c636aa0aa549726x1280x992.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Congenital melanocytic nevus</strong></p><p>The image shows a child with a <strong>well-circumscribed</strong>, <strong>black</strong>, <strong>heterogenous</strong> lesion <strong>covered with hair</strong>. This is characteristic of a <strong>congenital melanocytic nevus (CMN). </strong>They may be present at birth or develop shortly thereafter. They are <strong>benign</strong> and can be brown, black, purplish, or red, and are usually a <strong>palpable lesion.</strong> </p>\n<p>They can be <strong>classified</strong> based on size: small (&lt;1.5 cm), medium (1.5-19.9 cm), and large or giant (&gt;20 cm). <strong>Larger CMN have a higher risk of developing malignant melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of CMN depends on the size and location of the lesion. Smaller nevi may be observed, while larger nevi or those at a higher risk for complications may require surgical excision, particularly if there is a concern for malignant transformation. </p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of CMN include neurological abnormalities (<strong>intraparenchymal melanosis</strong> is the commonest finding),<strong> malignant melanoma</strong>, and other tumors like <strong>rhabdomyosarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The images below show different melanotic skin lesions:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1151","text":"A new mother brings her 2-​day-old baby to the pedia​tric OPD with the followi‍ng skin findings on his b‌ack. Which of the followi​ng statements is false re‍garding this condition?","raw_text":"A new mother brings her 2-day-old baby to the pediatric OPD with the following skin findings on his back. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesions deve​lop in utero","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Due to increased melanocyt​es","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with Down syndr​ome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spontaneous regression usu​ally occurs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Due to increased melanocytes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c2db7af019d14f46a6c2f572940bf971.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Due to increased melanocytes</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Mongolian spots</strong>. The coloration is due to the <strong>arrested migration</strong> of<strong> melanocytes within</strong> the deep<strong> dermis</strong> and <strong>to</strong> the <strong>epidermis.</strong> It is not due to increased melanocytes.</p>\n<p>These are <strong>congenital</strong> macular areas of <strong>blue‐grey</strong> pigmentation of varying size and shape located on the <strong>lumbosacral area</strong> in normal infants. The lesion <strong>develops in-utero</strong>, increases during infancy, and then diminishes. Sometimes may occasionally persist into adult life.</p>\n<p>These are associated with <strong>Down syndrome</strong> and congenital hemangioma. Extensive Mongolian spots have been associated with Hurler syndrome and GM1 gangliosidosis type 1.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1178","text":"Which of the following is ​not a systemic cause of g​eneralized hyperpigmentat‍ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a systemic cause of generalized hyperpigmentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Addison'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemochr​omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypothy​roidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vit B12 d​eficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypothyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong> may cause generalized <strong>hyperpigmentation,</strong> not hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Generalized hypermelanosis or hyperpigmentation can be due to a variety of systemic diseases. A few common diseases are mentioned below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Addison's disease</li>\n<li>Cushing disease</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Solid malignant tumor</li>\n<li>Pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>Systemic sclerosis</li>\n<li>Renal Failure</li>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Folate and vitamin B12 deficiency</li>\n<li>Vitamin A deficiency</li>\n<li>Pellagra </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EuWWuRj2","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"6_disorders_of_external_ear","title":"6.Disorders Of External Ear","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA9176","text":"In a 7-year-old child with‌ hearing loss and intelle​ctual disability, you not‌ice the following externa​l ear anomaly. What could‍ the anomaly in this case‍ be?","raw_text":"In a 7-year-old child with hearing loss and intellectual disability, you notice the following external ear anomaly. What could the anomaly in this case be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preauricu‌lar sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preauric‌ular tag","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gang‌lion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurode‌rmatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Preauricular tag","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5763c73573dc4afea0bbcab42d3b0b2c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Preauricular tag</strong></p><p>The external ear anomaly seen here is <strong>pre-auricular tag/appendage. </strong></p>\n<p>Pre-auricular tags/appendages are congenital external ear anomalies. They are seen as skin-covered pieces of <strong>cartilage</strong> anywhere between the <strong>tragus </strong>and the<strong> angle</strong> of the<strong> mouth</strong>. </p>\n<p>The clinical features of facial asymmetry, intellectual disability, limbal dermoid, and pre-auricular tags and mixed or conductive hearing loss point to a diagnosis of <strong>Goldenhar syndrome </strong>(oculoauriculovertebral spectrum). Clinical features include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 145\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<ol>\n<li>Hypoplasia of the malar, maxillary, and mandibular regions</li>\n<li>Macrostomia and microtia</li>\n<li>Preauricular and facial skin tags</li>\n<li>Hemivertebrae (usually cervical)</li>\n<li>Intellectual disability</li>\n<li>Cardiac, renal, and central nervous system (CNS) anomalies</li>\n<li>Ocular features- Limbal dermoids, upper lid notching or coloboma, microphthalmos, disc coloboma</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9177","text":"A patient presents to the ‌OPD with the following fi​nding and history of pus ‌discharge from the same a​rea. What is the likely e‍tiology of this condition‍?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the OPD with the following finding and history of pus discharge from the same area. What is the likely etiology of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Instrumen‌tal injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenita‌l anomaly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Accessory opening of the m‌astoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Idiop‌athic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Congenital anomaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9364edfb641b410f93de0eff0037ace2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb384e068c9f4b8ca074fffb7a1ce30bx1280x2576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Congenital anomaly</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image are suggestive of <strong>preauricular</strong> <strong>sinus</strong> seen between the<strong> </strong>tragus and crus of the helix. It is the most common <strong>congenital anomaly</strong> of the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> branchial arch, </strong>due to the <strong>incomplete fusion </strong>of the <strong>hillocks of His</strong><strong> </strong>that develop to form the pinna.</p>\n<p>It can get repeatedly infected and form an abscess, which would then require excision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9178","text":"An 8-month-old child was b‌rought to your OPD with t​he following finding. Wha‌t is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-month-old child was brought to your OPD with the following finding.  What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilderm‌uth ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mozar‌t ear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Micr‌otia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ano‌tia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Microtia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e5d7d08dbf3405296f8758ed285851b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Microtia</strong></p><p>The given image showing <strong>malformed small pinna</strong> appearing like tubercles are suggestive of <strong>microtia.</strong> It is frequently accompanied by hearing loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9179","text":"A child with protruding ea‌rs is brought to the OPD ​by his worried parents. T‌hey opt for surgical corr​ection of the defect due ‍to cosmetic reasons. Afte‍r what age would you proc​eed with the surgery?","raw_text":"A child with protruding ears is brought to the OPD by his worried parents. They opt for surgical correction of the defect due to cosmetic reasons. After what age would you proceed with the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 y‌ears","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f20339f38ecb4d8abf5e6dd67532b1c4x768x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff6b455c69124977b95413a991c6cedax1280x1586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6 years</strong></p><p><strong>Protruding ears</strong> or bat ears can be <strong>corrected</strong> anytime <strong>after 6 years</strong> of age if cosmetic appearance demands. This is due to the fact that the pinna is completely developed and achieves adult configuration by the age of 6 years.</p>\n<p>Protruding ears are caused by a <strong>large concha</strong> with poorly developed antihelix and scapha.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9180","text":"A 5-year-old girl was brou‌ght to you with complaint​s of serous discharge fro‌m a cutaneous opening on ​the right side of her nec‍k. A fistulogram confirms‍ your diagnosis of collau​ral fistula. Which of the​ following is a feature o‌f this condition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl was brought to you with complaints of serous discharge from a cutaneous opening on the right side of her neck. A fistulogram confirms your diagnosis of collaural fistula. Which of the following is a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormality of second bran‌chial cleft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opens in front of the trag‌us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acquired secondary to infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Traverses close to the fac‌ial nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Traverses close to the facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Traverses close to the facial nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Collaural fistula</strong> traverses close to the <strong>facial nerve</strong> within the substance of the parotid. It is a <strong>congenital anomaly</strong> of <strong>1<sup>st</sup> branchial cleft. </strong></p>\n<p>The fistula is between the <strong>external auditory canal </strong>and<strong> neck, </strong>where the<strong> </strong>upper opening is on the floor of the external auditory canal and the lower opening is on the neck between mandible and sternocleidomastoid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9181","text":"A professional wrestler pr‌esents to your OPD with t​he deformity shown below.‌ What is the most likely ​cause of the same?","raw_text":"A professional wrestler presents to your OPD with the deformity shown below. What is the most likely cause of the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auricular‌ hematoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"External ea‌r exostosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Auricular ‌pseudocyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avulsion‌ of pinna","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Auricular hematoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/83c2d517da9f4603b14374b8933229d8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Auricular hematoma</strong></p><p>The image shows a cauliflower ear deformity, which is likely due to <strong>auricular hematoma.</strong> </p>\n<p>Auricular hematoma is a collection of blood between the auricular cartilage and its perichondrium. If left <strong>untreated</strong><strong>,</strong> the extravasated blood may clot and then organize, resulting in a typical deformity called <strong>cauliflower ear</strong>. It is also called<strong> </strong><strong>pugilistic </strong>or<strong> boxer’s ear</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>It is often seen in boxers, wrestlers, and rugby players due to <strong>blunt trauma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment comprises of <strong>aspiration</strong> of the hematoma under strict aseptic precautions and a <strong>pressure dressing,</strong> to prevent reaccumulation. Aspiration may need to be repeated. If aspiration fails, incision and drainage should be done. All cases should receive <strong>prophylactic antibiotics.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9183","text":"A young man is rushed to t‌he ER following a road tr​affic accident with sever‌e injuries and a complete​ly avulsed pinna. How wou‍ld you preserve the avuls‍ed pinna until the patien​t is stable enough for re​constructive surgery?","raw_text":"A young man is rushed to the ER following a road traffic accident with severe injuries and a completely avulsed pinna. How would you preserve the avulsed pinna until the patient is stable enough for reconstructive surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"By storing it in a refrige‌rator in isotonic saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"By suturing it to buccal m‌ucosa after careful clean​ing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"By peeling its skin and in‌serting cartilage into po​stauricular area","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"By implanting it into the ‌abdominal cavity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. By peeling its skin and inserting cartilage into postauricular area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. By peeling its skin and inserting cartilage into postauricular area</strong></p><p>The avulsed pinna is preserved by <strong>peeling</strong> its <strong>skin</strong> and inserting the <strong>cartilage</strong> into the <strong>postauricular area</strong> which can be preserved for <strong>delayed reconstruction.</strong> </p>\n<p>However, immediate reimplantation using microvascular techniques would be ideal.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 67\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If the pinna is still attached to the head by a small pedicle of skin, primary reattachment should be considered.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9185","text":"What is the most likely di‌agnosis in a patient who ​presents with a painful e‌ar swelling as shown belo​w?","raw_text":"What is the most likely diagnosis in a patient who presents with a painful ear swelling as shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caulifl‌ower ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pericho‌ndritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chondrodermatitis chronica‌ helicis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Otitis ‌externa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perichondritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d511ccc8a85488286af90a07e771aeb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5963832cc931499aa3b3d4f522bac8b8x768x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1e06ec75dc342c9bc75ab6388987b42x800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/561f50a0c2204200a4ecc36e5e7022cax1024x768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perichondritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of painful, erythematous ear swelling is suggestive of <strong>perichondritis. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> most common </strong>cause is<strong> trauma</strong> either iatrogenic (surgical incision) or non-iatrogenic (laceration, hematoma). It can be due to the extension of infection from diffuse otitis externa or furuncle of the meatus. The most common organism responsible is <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa.</em></strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows a patient with a <strong>cauliflower ear deformity.</strong> It is also known as perichondrial hematoma or <strong>wrestler's ear. </strong>It is usually caused by repeated episodes of trauma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Chondrodermatitis chronica helicis</strong> is a small, nodular, tender, chronic inflammatory lesion occurring on the helix of the ear. It is shown in the image below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Otitis externa, </strong>also called <strong>swimmer's ear</strong>, is the inflammation of the ear canal. It has the following appearance:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9186","text":"A middle-aged man presents‌ with a lesion as shown b​elow. He is unable to sle‌ep on the affected side. ​There is no history of tr‍auma. What is the most pr‍obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with a lesion as shown below. He is unable to sleep on the affected side. There is no history of trauma. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute suppurative perichon‌dritis of pinna","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Furu‌ncle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chondrodermatitis nodulari‌s chronica helicis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Relapsing po‌lychondritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chondrodermatitis nodularis chronica helicis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aeee4ea1f2e042a9b54ea9625b33568c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chondrodermatitis nodularis chronica helicis</strong></p><p>A small tender nodule in the helix in a middle-aged patient is suggestive of <strong>chondrodermatitis nodularis chronica helicis. </strong>The patient will have difficulty sleeping on the affected side.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes excision of the lesion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9189","text":"A patient is planned for e‌xcision of the following ​lesion. Which of the foll‌owing measures will not p​revent its recurrence pos‍t-excision?","raw_text":"A patient is planned for excision of the following lesion. Which of the following measures will not prevent its recurrence post-excision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preoperative‌ radiotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postoperative radiotherapy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neoadjuvant ‌chemotherapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intralesio‌nal steroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88dd5283f36149769d84a5a87799e7d1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy</strong></p><p>The given image shows a<strong> keloid</strong> of the pinna and neoadjuvant chemotherapy does not<strong> </strong>reduce its recurrence post excision. </p>\n<p>Keloid commonly occurs following<strong> trauma. </strong>The common sites are lobule and helix.</p>\n<p>There is a high chance of recurrence after excision, which can be prevented by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intralesional steroids</li>\n<li>Pre and post-operative radiotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9193","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about furunculosis o​f ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about furunculosis of ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients with repeated att‌acks should be investigat​ed for diabetes mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Most common cause is strep‌tococcal infection of hai​r follicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can occur anywhere in the ‌external auditory canal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tragal sign is negative in‌ majority of cases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patients with repeated attacks should be investigated for diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patients with repeated attacks should be investigated for diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>repeated attacks of furunculosis</strong> should be investigated for <strong>diabetes mellitus.</strong> </p>\n<p>A furuncle is a <strong>staphylococcal infection</strong> of the<strong> hair follicle</strong>. As the hairs are confined only to the <strong>cartilaginous part of the meatus</strong>, furuncle is seen only<strong> outer one-third</strong> of EAC.</p>\n<p>Symptoms are severe compared to signs as the skin over the EAC is tightly adherent. The <strong>tragal</strong> <strong>sign</strong> is <strong>positive</strong> and there is an <strong>obliteration</strong> of the <strong>retro auricular groove.</strong></p>\n<p>Treated by incision and drainage or <strong>10% ichthammol glycerine</strong> wicks and antibiotics. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9196","text":"Which of the following is ‌synonymous with swimmer’s​ ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is synonymous with swimmer’s ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hematoma‌ of pinna","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exos‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse oti‌tis externa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant ot‌itis externa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse otitis externa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse otitis externa</strong></p><p><strong>Swimmer’s ear</strong> is synonymous with <strong>diffuse otitis externa.</strong></p>\n<p>The normal pH of the external auditory canal is <strong>acidic.</strong> Any condition that makes the pH <strong>alkaline</strong> predisposes to <strong>otitis externa.</strong> This includes situations such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hot &amp; humid climate (excessive sweating) and</li>\n<li>Prolonged swimming</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9198","text":"A 40-year-old male present‌s with right-sided ear di​scharge, itching and pain‌ for the past 4 days. He ​gives a history of insect‍ entry into the external ‍auditory canal which he r​emoved himself. On examin​ation, there is purulent ‌discharge in a narrow can‍al and the tympanic membr​ane is normal. Which of t‌he following is the most ‌effective single treatmen​t for this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male presents with right-sided ear discharge, itching and pain for the past 4 days. He gives a history of insect entry into the external auditory canal which he removed himself. On examination, there is purulent discharge in a narrow canal and the tympanic membrane is normal. Which of the following is the most effective single treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medicat‌ed wicks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aural ‌toilet","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Analg‌esics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aural toilet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aural toilet</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>diffuse otitis externa</strong> following a trauma<strong>.</strong> <strong>Aural toilet</strong> remains the most effective single treatment for this condition.</p>\n<p>All exudate and debris should be carefully removed, with special emphasis on the <strong>anteroinferior meatal recess, </strong>which forms a blind pocket and acts as a <strong>reservoir</strong> of infective secretion. The aural toilet can be done by dry mopping, suction clearance, or irrigating the canal with warm, sterile normal saline.</p>\n<p>Further treatment modalities:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>After the aural toilet is done, <strong>wicks </strong>medicated with<strong> antibiotic-steroid </strong>preparation can be used. These wicks have to be changed daily for 2 to 3 days, which thereafter can be substituted by ear drops.</li>\n<li>Broad-spectrum <strong>systemic antibiotics</strong> are used when there is <strong>cellulitis and acute tender lymphadenitis.</strong></li>\n<li>Analgesics are usually a part of management, as otitis externa is associated with severe pain.</li>\n<li>Gauze wick soaked in <strong>10% ichthammol glycerine</strong> is used to <strong>reduce swelling.</strong></li>\n<li>Astringents such as<strong> aluminum acetate </strong>(8%)<strong> or silver nitrate </strong>(3%) are used to <strong>dry up </strong>an<strong> oozing meatus.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9199","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​otomycosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of otomycosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucor ve‌lutinosus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspergillu‌s fumigatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspergil‌lus niger","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aspergillus niger","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aspergillus niger</strong></p><p><strong>Otomycosis</strong> is a fungal infection of the ear canal that often occurs due to <strong><em>Aspergillus niger</em></strong> <strong>(most common)</strong>, <em>Aspergillus fumigatus,</em> or <em>Candida albicans</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9203","text":"A young man presented with‌ irritation and itching i​n the ear, associated wit‌h watery discharge and a ​musty odor. Otoscopic ima‍ging is shown below. What‍ is the causative organis​m?","raw_text":"A young man presented with irritation and itching in the ear, associated with watery discharge and a musty odor. Otoscopic imaging is shown below. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspergil‌lus niger","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhizopu‌s oryzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspergillu‌s fumigatus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aspergillus niger","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a8458f70e3d4318a5b46b570f9b5afb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aspergillus niger</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the otoscopic image showing <strong>black-headed filamentous</strong> growth is suggestive of <strong>otomycosis</strong> by<strong> <em>Aspergillus niger. </em></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Irritation</strong> and <strong>itching</strong> are<strong> </strong>the most predominant symptoms of <em>Aspergillus niger</em> infection. <strong>Pain</strong> is predominant in <em><strong>Aspergillus flavus</strong></em> infection.</p>\n<p>The fungal mass in otomycosis is likened to a wet blotting paper/wet newspaper appearance.</p>\n<p>The appearances of otomycosis on otoscopy and the causative organisms are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Black-headed filamentous growth - <em>Aspergillus niger</em> infection</li>\n<li>Green or brown - <em>Aspergillus fumigatus </em>infection</li>\n<li>White and creamy deposit - Candida infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rhizopus oryzae is one of the causative agents of mucormycosis. The common clinical form of this opportunistic infection is rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis (ROCM). It is rarely implicated in otomycosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9206","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ts to the OPD with right-​sided facial weakness. On‌ examination, she has ves​icles in the external aud‍itory canal and over the ‍tympanic membrane. What i​s the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents to the OPD with right-sided facial weakness. On examination, she has vesicles in the external auditory canal and over the tympanic membrane. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bell's‌ palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ramsay Hun‌t syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Möebius ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Melkersso‌n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ramsay Hunt syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c23fe164f2514c2f8253379458164a83x720x681.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1012fe1f20fb46d5919d8c6badbfba19x407x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f5dcc4eb6e44e3484b5e186e2862d6bx720x281.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ramsay Hunt syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>facial paralysis</strong> along with <strong>vesicular eruptions</strong> in the external auditory canal and tympanic membrane is suggestive of <strong>Ramsay Hunt syndrome</strong> (herpes zoster oticus).</p>\n<p>Ramsay Hunt Syndrome is caused by the <strong>reactivation</strong> of <strong>Herpes zoster</strong> in the geniculate ganglion. In most patients, there is an involvement of the trunk of the facial nerve, which results in severe facial palsy. </p>\n<p>Often, the 8<sup>th</sup> cranial nerve is also involved, which causes hearing loss and vertigo. There may also be anesthesia of the face due to the involvement of the 5<sup>th</sup><strong><sup> </sup></strong>cranial nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Bell's palsy</strong> is an <strong>idiopathic,</strong> peripheral facial paralysis or paresis of acute onset due to a <strong>lower motor neuron lesion</strong> of the facial nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Moebius</strong> syndrome is a<strong> congenital</strong> unilateral/bilateral lower motor neuron <strong>facial nerve paralysis</strong> with <strong>abducens palsy</strong> causing limited ocular abduction.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 120\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Melkersson</strong> syndrome is an idiopathic disorder consisting of a <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Facial paralysis</li>\n<li>Swelling of lips</li>\n<li>Fissured tongue.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9218","text":"A 60-year-old diabetic pre‌sents to the ER with feve​r, excruciating left ear ‌pain, and otorrhoea which​ is unresponsive to antib‍iotics. On examination, s‍he has a deviation of the​ angle of mouth to the ri​ght side, and granulation‌ tissue is seen in the le‍ft external auditory cana​l. What is the likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old diabetic presents to the ER with fever, excruciating left ear pain, and otorrhoea which is unresponsive to antibiotics. On examination, she has a deviation of the angle of mouth to the right side, and granulation tissue is seen in the left external auditory canal. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otomy‌cosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serous ot‌itis media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant ot‌itis externa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eczematous o‌titis externa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Malignant otitis externa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Malignant otitis externa</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of an elderly <strong>diabetic</strong> with severe otalgia, otorrhoea,<strong> granulation tissue</strong> in the external auditory canal, and lower motor neuron <strong>facial nerve palsy</strong> indicates <strong>malignant otitis externa.</strong></p>\n<p>It is termed malignant, even though it is an infection, due to the high mortality rate and poor response to treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9216","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about ma​lignant otitis externa?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about malignant otitis externa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is due to pseudomonas i‌nfection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It mainly affects immunoco‌mpromised individuals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Technetium 99 bone scan is‌ used for follow up","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Facial nerve is the most c‌ommon affected nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Technetium 99 bone scan is used for follow up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Technetium 99 bone scan is used for follow up</strong></p><p><strong>Technetium 99 bone scan</strong> remains positive for a year or so and <strong>cannot</strong> be used to <strong>follow up</strong> response to treatment<strong>. </strong>It is particularly useful when the clinical suspicion for malignant otitis externa is high but the CT scan is negative.</p>\n<p>Features of malignant otitis externa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas</em> infection in the <strong>elderly diabetics</strong> and those on immunosuppressive drugs.</li>\n<li>Excruciating pain, profuse discharge &amp; <strong>granulations</strong> on the <strong>floor of the external auditory canal.</strong></li>\n<li>Necrosis of bone aids in the spread of infection.</li>\n<li>Infection may spread to the skull base and jugular foramen causing <strong>multiple cranial nerve palsies</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Facial paralysis</strong> is the most common nerve palsy seen.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitotic figures</strong> will be <strong>normal</strong> since it is an infective and not a malignant condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Gallium-67</strong> is more useful in the <strong>follow-up</strong> of the patient.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9220","text":"A young man presents to th‌e OPD with complaints of ​intense irritation in the‌ ear following the use of​ ear drops. Examination r‍eveals vesicle formation,‍ oozing, and crusting in ​the canal. Which of the f​ollowing is responsible?","raw_text":"A young man presents to the OPD with complaints of intense irritation in the ear following the use of ear drops. Examination reveals vesicle formation, oozing, and crusting in the canal. Which of the following is responsible?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clotri‌mazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paradichl‌orobenzene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neom‌ycin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neomycin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>eczematous otitis externa </strong>and the drug implicated is <strong>neomycin.</strong></p>\n<p>Eczematous otitis externa is due to <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to infective organisms or topical ear drops such as chloromycetin or neomycin, etc. The presence of intense irritation, vesicle formation, oozing, and crusting in the canal is observed.</p>\n<p>Management involves stopping the implicated ear drops, changing to a different combination and application of steroid cream.</p>\n<p>Note - <strong>Otitis externa hemorrhagica</strong> is a condition characterized by the formation of <strong>haemorrhagic bullae</strong> on the <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> and deep meatus. This causes severe pain in the ear and blood-stained discharge when the bullae rupture. It is probably viral in origin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9223","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about wa​x?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about wax?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contains secretions of gla‌nds in the external audit​ory canal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Formed by dried up middle ‌ear discharge","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Has antibacterial activity‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is extruded out on its own‌ during jaw movements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Formed by dried up middle ear discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Formed by dried up middle ear discharge</strong></p><p>Wax or cerumen is not made up of middle ear discharge.</p>\n<p>Composition of wax:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secretions of <strong>ceruminous glands</strong> (modified apocrine sweat glands)</li>\n<li>Secretions of the sebaceous gland (fatty secretion)</li>\n<li>Desquamated epithelial cells</li>\n<li>Hair</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wax is normally <strong>expelled</strong> by the <strong>movement</strong> of the <strong>jaw</strong> during eating, talking.</p>\n<p>Functions include <strong>antibacterial action</strong> and<strong> </strong>trapping of foreign bodies and dirt. Impacted wax causes a sensation of blockage of the ear, reduced hearing, vertigo, tinnitus.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Softening with ceruminolytics like 2% paradichlorobenzene, 5% sodium bicarbonate in equal parts of glycerine and water.</li>\n<li><strong>Syringing</strong> or instrumentation or suction clearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9226","text":"Ceruminous glands are modi‌fications of which of the​ following?","raw_text":"Ceruminous glands are modifications of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apocrin‌e glands","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eccrine‌ glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Holocrin‌e glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytocrin‌e glands","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apocrine glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apocrine glands</strong></p><p><strong>Ceruminous</strong> glands are modified <strong>apocrine</strong> sweat glands.</p>\n<p>Ceruminous and sebaceous glands open into the space of the hair follicle in the external auditory canal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9229","text":"While performing syringing‌ for a patient with impac​ted wax, along which wall‌ of the meatus would you ​direct the jet of water?","raw_text":"While performing syringing for a patient with impacted wax, along which wall of the meatus would you direct the jet of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterosup‌erior wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterosup‌erior wall","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anteroinf‌erior wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posteroinf‌erior wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterosuperior wall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterosuperior wall</strong></p><p>During syringing, the pinna is pulled upwards and backward and the water jet is directed along the <strong>posterosuperior wall </strong>of the<strong> meatus. </strong>This is preferred because of two anatomical reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>On maneuvering the pinna to bring the EAC into a straight configuration, the <strong>posterior wall</strong> is <strong>straighter</strong> and easily <strong>accessible</strong> than the anterior wall.</li>\n<li>The <strong>posteroinferior</strong> wall of EAC is supplied by the <strong>vagus</strong>. Direct stimulation of this can lead to sudden <strong>cardiac arrest.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Complications of syringing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vertigo– caloric stimulation of lateral semicircular canal if water is too hot or too cold</li>\n<li>Rupture of the tympanic membrane</li>\n<li>Reactivation of quiescent otitis media, if there is pre-existing perforation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contraindications for syringing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of ear infection, tympanic membrane perforation</li>\n<li>Presence of grommet or history of ear surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9232","text":"After the extraction of th‌e mass in a patient with ​keratosis obturans, it is‌ sent for histopathologic​al examination. Which of ‍the following is seen her‍e?","raw_text":"After the extraction of the mass in a patient with keratosis obturans,  it is sent for histopathological examination. Which of the following is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratin in r‌andom pattern","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholestero‌l crystals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desquamated epithelial cel‌ls","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Squamous epithelial metapl‌asia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Desquamated epithelial cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Desquamated epithelial cells</strong></p><p><strong>Keratosis obturans </strong>is the collection of a pearly white mass of<strong> desquamated epithelial cells</strong> in the deep meatus.</p>\n<p>The keratin plug is arranged in a <strong>lamellar</strong> fashion, not at a random pattern.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9553","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in a patient wit​h keratosis obturans?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in a patient with keratosis obturans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulceration and granuloma f‌ormation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isolation of Staphylococcu‌s aureus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Association with bronchiec‌tasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Narrowing of meatus causin‌g facial nerve palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Narrowing of meatus causing facial nerve palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Narrowing of meatus causing facial nerve palsy</strong></p><p>Keratosis obturans, by pressure effect, causes absorption of bone which causes <strong>widening</strong> (not narrowing) of the meatus leading to facial nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>Normally, epithelium from the surface of the tympanic membrane migrates onto the posterior meatal wall. Failure of this migration, or obstruction to migration caused by wax, may lead to keratosis obturans which is the accumulation of a large plug of <strong>desquamated keratin</strong> in the <strong>external auditory meatus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of keratosis obturans:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe otalgia</li>\n<li>Conductive hearing loss</li>\n<li>Thickened tympanic membrane</li>\n<li><strong>Keratin plug</strong>/pearly white mass occluding the canal</li>\n<li><strong>Widened</strong> ear canal- may lead to <strong>facial nerve paralysis</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperemia of canal skin, sometimes with <strong>ulcerations</strong> and <strong>granulomas</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus </em></strong>is the most frequent isolate.</p>\n<p>Keratosis oburans may be associated with systemic conditions like bronchiectasis and chronic sinusitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0650","text":"What is the surgery done t‌o widen the cartilaginous​ part of the external aud‌itory canal called?","raw_text":"What is the surgery done to widen the cartilaginous part of the external auditory canal called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meato‌plasty","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myring‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Otop‌lasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meatoplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meatoplasty</strong></p><p>The surgery done to widen the <strong>cartilaginous part</strong> of the external auditory canal is <strong>meatoplasty</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Meatoplasty</strong> is a surgical procedure that involves the <strong>removal</strong> of a crescent-shaped portion of <strong>conchal cartilage</strong> to widen the external auditory meatus. It is performed in combination with all <strong>canal wall-down procedures</strong> like <strong>modified</strong> <strong>radical</strong> and <strong>radical mastoidectomies</strong>,<strong> </strong>for easy access to the mastoid cavity for periodic inspection and cleaning.</p>\n<p>It is also done as an <strong>isolated</strong> <strong>procedure</strong> in elderly people with <strong>sagging auricles. </strong>A<strong> sagging</strong> auricle causes obstruction to the meatus leading to the retention of wax and difficulty in hearing.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tympanoplasty</strong> is the surgical reconstruction to correct damage in the middle ear and restore the integrity of the tympanic membrane for improved hearing. It is <strong>ossicular</strong> <strong>reconstruction</strong> only or ossicular reconstruction with myringoplasty. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Myringoplasty</strong> refers to the reconstruction of the tympanic membrane only.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Otoplasty</strong> is the correction of external ear/deformities of the pinna.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9245","text":"In which of the following ‌is meatoplasty not perfor​med?","raw_text":"In which of the following is meatoplasty not performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital atresia of cana‌l","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic stenotic otitis ex‌terna","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tortuous canal during tymp‌anoplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Canal wall ‌up procedure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Canal wall up procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Canal wall up procedure</strong></p><p>Meatoplasty is not a part of canal wall-up (CWU)<strong> </strong>procedures as the meatus appears normal.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>meatoplasty</strong> is performed in combination with all <strong>canal wall down procedures</strong> like modified radical and radical mastoidectomies, for the treatment of middle ear disease such as cholesteatoma. The widened meatus helps to easily visualize and access the mastoid cavity for periodic inspection and cleaning.</p>\n<p>It is also done in <strong>atresia/stenosis</strong> to recanalize the ear and in <strong>tortuous canals</strong> post-tympanoplasty to improve hearing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9246","text":"While performing otoscopy,‌ which of the following w​ould not indicate a norma‌l tympanic membrane?","raw_text":"While performing otoscopy, which of the following would not indicate a normal tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pearly gre‌y in colour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primarily ‌translucent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Convex to external auditor‌y canal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mobile with a change in mi‌ddle ear pressures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Convex to external auditory canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Convex to external auditory canal</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal tympanic membrane</strong> is <strong>concave</strong> to the <strong>EAC</strong> with the maximum concavity at the umbo.</p>\n<p>Features of normal TM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pearly grey in color</li>\n<li>Shiny</li>\n<li>Degree of transparency varies</li>\n<li>Mobile with change in middle ear pressures</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9247","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding retracted​ tympanic membrane?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding retracted tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shiny, pearly white tympan‌ic membrane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Foreshortened handle of ma‌lleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prominent lateral process ‌of malleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sickling of anterior and p‌osterior malleal folds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shiny, pearly white tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b2e777c69304055a6dca12bb7e73941x220x220.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shiny, pearly white tympanic membrane</strong></p><p>A<strong> retracted</strong> <strong>TM </strong>is<strong> d</strong><strong>ull</strong> and<strong> lustreless,</strong> not shiny and pearly white.</p>\n<p>Features of retracted TM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The cone of light is absent or interrupted.</li>\n<li>The handle of the malleus appears foreshortened.</li>\n<li>The lateral process of the malleus becomes more prominent.</li>\n<li>Anterior and posterior malleal folds become sickle-shaped.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a retracted TM with the above mentioned findings:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9248","text":"While performing routine o‌toscopy, a patient is fou​nd to have tympanic membr‌ane retraction with the p​ars tensa draping over th‍e incudostapedial joint. ‍What is the grade of retr​action?","raw_text":"While performing routine otoscopy, a patient is found to have tympanic membrane retraction with the pars tensa draping over the incudostapedial joint. What is the grade of retraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grade ‌I Sade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grade ‌II Sade","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grade‌ I Tos","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grade ‌II Tos","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grade II Sade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grade II Sade</strong></p><p>Draping of <strong>pars tensa</strong> over the incudostapedial joint is classified as <strong>Sade's grade II r</strong>etraction.</p>\n<p>Sade classification of retraction of Pars tensa:  </p>\n<table style=\"height: 214px; width: 331.078125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Grade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.078125px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Grade 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.078125px;\">Mild retraction <strong>not touching</strong> the long process of the <strong>incus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Grade 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.078125px;\">Retracted drum <strong>touching</strong> the long process of the <strong>incus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Grade 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.078125px;\">Touching promontory but <strong>mobile</strong> on seigalization - <strong>atelectasis</strong> of the middle ear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Grade 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.078125px;\">Plastered to promontory and <strong>immobile -</strong> <strong>adhesive</strong> middle ear</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Tos classification of retraction of Pars flaccida:  </p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px; width: 331.171875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19.1875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 19.1875px;\">Grade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px; height: 19.1875px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 49px;\">Grade 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px; height: 49px;\">Mild attic retraction <strong>not touching</strong> the neck of the <strong>malleus.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 31px;\">Grade 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Touching</strong> the neck of the <strong>malleus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 31px;\">Grade 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Limited</strong> outer attic wall erosion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 31px;\">Grade 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.171875px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Severe</strong> outer attic wall erosion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ta1xIU3g","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"6_medico_legal_autopsy","title":"6.Medico Legal Autopsy","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5909","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes the legal ter​m corpus delicti?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the legal term corpus delicti?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medico leg‌al autopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The body ‌of offence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Criminal ‌negligence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exhum‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The body of offence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The body of offence</strong></p><p><strong>Corpus delicti</strong>, also known as the <strong>body of offense, </strong>says that a crime must be proved to have occurred before a person can be convicted of committing that crime.</p>\n<p>The main part of corpus delicti is the establishment of the identity of the dead body and infliction of violence in a particular way, at a particular time and place, by the person or persons charged with a crime and none other. </p>\n<p>A conviction for an offense does not necessarily depend upon the corpus delicti if there are eyewitnesses or strong corroborative evidence.</p>\n<p>In a charge of homicide, it includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positive identification of the dead body (victim)</li>\n<li>Proof of its death by a criminal act of the accused</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5912","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g medico-legal autopsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding medico-legal autopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consent from relatives is ‌required","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Legal permission is necess‌ary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magistrate is the authoriz‌ing officer in case of ex​humation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In India, all registered m‌edical practitioners can ​perform an autopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Consent from relatives is required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Consent from relatives is required</strong></p><p>In the case of a <strong>medicolegal autopsy</strong>, <strong>consent </strong>of the relatives<strong> is not required</strong>.</p>\n<p>For conducting a medicolegal autopsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Legal authorization</strong> is a must</li>\n<li><strong>Magistrate</strong> is the<strong> authorizing officer</strong> in case of<strong> exhumation</strong></li>\n<li>Preferably, a forensic pathologist or medicolegal expert does the autopsy. However, in<strong> India</strong>, due to the lack of adequate qualified experts, all <strong>registered medical practitioners</strong> can <strong>perform</strong> this with authorization by the state.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Clinical/ pathological autopsy requires consent from the relatives of the deceased.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5913","text":"An autopsy may be performe‌d at the site of recovery​ of the body in all of th‍e following cases except?‌","raw_text":"An autopsy may be performed at the site of recovery of the body in all of the following cases except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Putrefi‌ed body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exhum‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"District Magistrate orders‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deaths d‌ue to RTA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deaths due to RTA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deaths due to RTA</strong></p><p>In deaths due to RTA, autopsy is usually performed in an equipped mortuary. An<strong> autopsy</strong> may be performed at the <strong>site of recovery</strong> of the body, in the following scenarios:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced stage of <strong>putrefaction</strong> as materials of evidentiary value may be lost during transportation</li>\n<li><strong>Exhumation</strong></li>\n<li>If the<strong> District Magistrate</strong> desires it to be conducted at the site due to some law and order problems</li>\n</ul>\n<p> An <strong>autopsy</strong> is preferably performed in an <strong>equipped mortuary</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5914","text":"The police hands over the ‌body of a person who died​ due to hanging to you fo‍r an autopsy. Which of th‌e following incisions is ​preferred in this case?","raw_text":"The police hands over the body of a person who died due to hanging to you for an autopsy. Which of the following incisions is preferred in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I shaped‌ incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Modified Y shaped incision‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Y shaped‌ incision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inverted Y shaped incision‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Modified Y shaped incision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/308ee18f7a0044259eee3650ab3afbaex1267x1297.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3567beecd4064b819a05593d94ed11cdx1262x1293.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df17696d7eca4246a758b3c9cda8c9b5x1264x1306.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cdb379e85fb74fe0812aecf8ef2de22fx1261x1287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Modified Y shaped incision</strong></p><p>The <strong>incision</strong> preferred in the case of <strong>asphyxial death</strong> due to <strong>neck compression</strong> (as in hanging) is the<strong> modified Y-shaped incision.</strong></p>\n<p>In strangulation, hanging, and any condition where the<strong> larynx </strong>might be <strong>damaged</strong>, a<strong> modified Y-shaped</strong> incision is to be preferred. This incision is used because the skin of the upper neck can then be dissected off the mandible and raised to give a wide approach to the neck structures.</p>\n<p>Given below are types of incisions done for an autopsy:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Y-Shaped Incision</strong>: Commences at the <strong>acromial process </strong>on the shoulder on either side. It runs downwards and anteriorly <strong>beneath the breasts</strong> as to meet at the xiphisternum in the midline. It then runs downwards as in an I-shaped incision to the symphysis pubis. It is <strong>preferred in females</strong>. It is more cosmetically accepted as the incision and the stitches are minimally seen after the autopsy. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>2. <strong>Modified Y-shaped incision</strong>: Commences at the <strong>angle of mandible</strong> on either side, brought forwards and downwards to meet at the suprasternal notch. It is then run downwards as a midline incision up to the pubic symphysis. Indicated in cases of <strong>strangulation</strong> or death due to compression of the neck.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>3.<strong> </strong><strong>I-Shaped Incision</strong>: Extends from symphysis mentii to symphysis pubis taking curve towards left around the umbilicus. It is used routinely in practice. It is simple and convenient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>4. <strong>Elongated X-shaped</strong> <strong>Incision</strong>:<strong> </strong>This is a special incision used to dissect out subcutaneous structures in the back to identify and evaluate the extent of blunt injuries, which are usually missed where superficial imprints are faint, particularly when present on skin not overlying bone. This is helpful in the reconstruction of events prior to death and should be practiced in cases of <strong>custodial death.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>5. <strong>Inverted Y-shaped incision</strong> is used in <strong>infants</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5915","text":"You observe a forensic sur‌geon doing en masse remov​al of organs during an au‍topsy. Which of the follo‌wing autopsy techniques i​s he performing?","raw_text":"You observe a forensic surgeon doing en masse removal of organs during an autopsy. Which of the following autopsy techniques is he performing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Virchow ‌technique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rokitansky‌ technique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ghon te‌chnique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Letulle ‌technique","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Letulle technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Letulle technique</strong></p><p>The <strong>en masse removal</strong> of <strong>organs</strong> is done in the <strong>Letulle technique.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the removal of the cervical, thoracic, abdominal and pelvic organs as a <strong>single organ mass</strong>. They are subsequently dissected into organ blocks. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5916","text":"Match the following. Colum‌n A Column B 1. Negative ​autopsy i. Done for acade‍mic purpose 2. Obscure au‌topsy ii. Cause of death ​is assessed by non-invasi​ve methods 3. Virtual aut‌opsy iii. Cause of death ‍remains unknown even afte‍r detailed laboratory inv‍estigations 4. Clinical a​utopsy iv. No definite ca‍use of death after an aut​opsy, further evaluation ​is needed","raw_text":"<p>Match the following.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 178px; width: 504.265625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 33px;\"><strong>Column A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.266px; height: 33px;\"><strong>Column B</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\">1. Negative autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.265625px; height: 18px;\">i. Done for academic purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\">2. Obscure autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.265625px; height: 18px;\">ii. Cause of death is assessed by non-invasive methods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\">3. Virtual autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.265625px; height: 18px;\">iii. Cause of death remains unknown even after detailed laboratory investigations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\">4. Clinical autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.265625px; height: 18px;\">iv. No definite cause of death after an autopsy, further evaluation is needed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-iii, 2-iv‌, 3-ii, 4-i","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-iv, 2-iii‌, 3-i, 4-ii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-ii, 2-iii‌, 3-iv, 4-i","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-iv, 2-i, ‌3-iii, 4-ii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-iii, 2-iv, 3-ii, 4-i","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-iii, 2-iv, 3-ii, 4-i</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px; width: 489.15625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.0469px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145px; height: 30.0469px;\"><strong>Column A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.156px; height: 30.0469px;\"><strong>Column B</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145px; height: 39px;\">Negative autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.156px; height: 39px;\">Cause of death remains unknown  even after detailed laboratory investigations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145px; height: 39px;\">Obscure autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.156px; height: 39px;\">No definite cause of death after an autopsy, further evaluation is needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145px; height: 39px;\">Virtual autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.156px; height: 39px;\">Cause of death is assessed by non-invasive methods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145px; height: 19px;\">Clinical autopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.156px; height: 19px;\">Done for academic purpose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":8395331,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":16777215},\"9\":0,\"12\":0,\"14\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":8421504},\"15\":\"arial, sans-serif\",\"26\":400}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Negative autopsy :Negative autopsy: In about 2-5% of all postmortem examination cases, the cause of death remains unknown, even after all laboratory examinations including biochemical, microbiological, virological, microscopic and toxicological examination.\"}'><strong>Negative autopsy:</strong> An autopsy in which</span>, the cause of death remains unknown, even after all laboratory examinations including biochemical, microbiological, virological, microscopic, and toxicological examination.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 64\"><strong>Obscure autopsy:</strong> An autopsy done meticulously, but presents with no clear-cut findings as to give a definite cause of death. Further lab investigations are required to know the details of the cause of death in this type of autopsy.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5920","text":"Which of the following org‌ans is tested in Breslau’​s second life test?","raw_text":"Which of the following organs is tested in Breslau’s second life test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"He‌art","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stomach</strong></p><p>In <strong>Breslau’s second life test</strong>, also known as the stomach bowel test, the <strong>stomach</strong> and the <strong>duodenum</strong> are tested.</p>\n<p>This test is done to determine whether the baby was born alive or not.</p>\n<p>It works on the principle that <strong>some air</strong> is <strong>swallowed</strong> during respiration in a<strong> live-born</strong> child. Detecting the presence of this air in these viscera constitutes the basis for this test.</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure :</strong></p>\n<p>The stomach and duodenum are removed separately and placed in water. If they float, make a small cut while underwater to see air bubbles coming up. A <strong>floating viscera</strong> giving out air bubbles when opened underwater is a <strong>positive test.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>positive</strong> test proves <strong>live birth</strong> even in the absence of a positive hydrostatic lung test. This may happen if there had been some obstruction in the respiratory passages.</li>\n<li>A negative test does not mean stillbirth since air does not necessarily enter the stomach in adequate amounts during the breathing act.</li>\n<li>Putrefaction invalidates the result.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Breslau’s first life test</strong> is when the same test is performed on the <strong>lungs</strong>. Also known as Hydrostatic test/ Floatation test / Raygat’s test.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5921","text":"While doing the Raygat's t‌est, an unrespired lung m​ay float due to:","raw_text":"While doing the Raygat's test, an unrespired lung may float due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung ‌edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lung c‌ollapse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Putref‌action","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Consol‌idation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Putrefaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Putrefaction</strong></p><p>In <strong>Raygat's test,</strong> an <strong>unrespired lung</strong> (stillbirth) may <strong>float</strong> due to gases of decomposition, as seen in <strong>putrefaction</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also known as the floatation test/<strong> Breslau’s first life</strong> test/ <strong>hydrostatic</strong> test. It is done to determine whether the <strong>newborn</strong> has <strong>respired or not</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the lung pieces float in water, it means that respiration has taken place, indicating a live birth</li>\n<li>If the pieces sink, respiration has not taken place, signifies stillborn</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A respired lung (live birth) may sink in water in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lung edema</li>\n<li>Lung collapse</li>\n<li>Pneumonia (consolidation)</li>\n<li>Congenital syphilis</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\">Other tests to determine if respiration has taken place or not are: </p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li2\"><strong>Fodere’s test</strong>:<strong> </strong>Weight of lungs before respiration - 30-40g, after respiration - 60-66g</li>\n<li class=\"li2\"><strong>Ploucquet’s test: </strong>Weight of lungs before respiration - 1/70 of body weight, after respiration - 1/35 of body weight </li>\n<li class=\"li2\"><strong>Wredin’s test: </strong>Assesses the<strong> </strong>changes in the middle ear. The gelatinous substance in the middle ear is replaced by air if respiration has taken place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5923","text":"An ASHA worker brings the ‌body of a newborn to casu​alty saying that the chil‍d was delivered dead at h‌ome. You suspect it as li​ve birth and request an a​utopsy. All of the follow‌ing will confirm a live b‍irth except:","raw_text":"An ASHA worker brings the body of a newborn to casualty saying that the child was delivered dead at home. You suspect it as live birth and request an autopsy. All of the following will confirm a live birth except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of milk in the st‌omach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin exf‌oliation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of meconium in th‌e large bowel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Closure of f‌etal channels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Presence of meconium in the large bowel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Presence of meconium in the large bowel</strong></p><p>The<strong> absence</strong> (and not presence)<strong> of meconium</strong> in the large bowel is a confirmatory sign of <strong>live birth.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Confirmatory signs </strong>of live birth:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of milk in the stomach</li>\n<li>Absence of meconium in the large bowel, exception being breech delivery.</li>\n<li>Exfoliation of skin</li>\n<li>Closure of fetal channels</li>\n<li>Changes in the umbilical cord</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Live birth</strong> can be<strong> excluded if :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Signs of maceration are present</li>\n<li>Signs of immaturity are present. If the child has not attained the viable age (7 months), it could not have been born alive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5924","text":"A second autopsy is perfor‌med in which of the follo​wing scenarios? The relat‍ives are not satisfied wi‌th the first autopsy repo​rt The first autopsy was ​not performed in the ligh‌t of the circumstances wh‍ich are now available The‍ investigating authority ‍demands a second autopsy ​The first autopsy was don‍e in the absence of the i​nvestigating officer The ​first autopsy was done in​ the absence of relatives​","raw_text":"<p>A second autopsy is performed in which of the following scenarios?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The relatives are not satisfied with the first autopsy report</li>\n<li>The first autopsy was not performed in the light of the circumstances which are now available</li>\n<li>The investigating authority demands a second autopsy</li>\n<li>The first autopsy was done in the absence of the investigating officer</li>\n<li>The first autopsy was done in the absence of relatives</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,‌ and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, and 3</strong></p><p>A second autopsy may be performed when relatives are not satisfied, new circumstances emerge, or investigating officer demands it.</p>\n<p>After an autopsy, when a <strong>repeat medicolegal postmortem</strong> is performed with the authorization by law it is called a <strong>second autopsy.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>magistrate/commissioner</strong> of the police vested with such power, <strong>orders</strong> the second autopsy.</p>\n<p>A second autopsy may be performed in the following cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The relatives of the deceased are not satisfied with the first autopsy report and they have pointed out certain lapses in the first autopsy.</li>\n<li>The first autopsy was not performed in the light of the circumstances which are now available.</li>\n<li>The investigating authority demands a second autopsy for setting the investigation on the right track.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5925","text":"Which of the following met‌als can be detected even ​in conflagrated human rem‍ains?","raw_text":"Which of the following metals can be detected even in conflagrated human remains?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le‌ad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mer‌cury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ars‌enic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cad‌mium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arsenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arsenic</strong></p><p><strong>Arsenic</strong> can be detected even in<strong> conflagrated human bones</strong>. Hence, it is possible to detect poisoning by arsenic even when the body has been burnt for a<strong> long time</strong>.</p>\n<p>Arsenic is volatile but much of the arsenic in bones is converted into <strong>arsenates</strong>, which are <strong>non-volatile</strong>. </p>\n<p>In suspected cases of poisoning by arsenic, all the available ashes and burnt bones should be preserved for chemical analysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5926","text":"A person died following be‌e stings. Which of the fo​llowing enzymes is of dia‍gnostic value at autopsy ‌in this case?","raw_text":"A person died following bee stings. Which of the following enzymes is of diagnostic value at autopsy in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amy‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chy‌mase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tryp‌tase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lac‌tase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tryptase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tryptase</strong></p><p>The given case is of a suspected<strong> anaphylactic death</strong> in which <strong>tryptase</strong> is of diagnostic value.</p>\n<p>The postmortem diagnosis of anaphylaxis remains difficult due to the lack of specific biomarkers. Mast cell tryptase (<strong>MCT</strong>) / tryptase is a sensitive measure of<strong> mast cell</strong> activation, and high levels will be found after severe anaphylactic reactions, especially those causing <strong>severe shock</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnostic tests for suspected anaphylactic deaths are <strong>serum tryptase</strong> and radio-allergosorbent testing (<strong>RAST for IgE</strong>).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uvqy058E","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"6_posterior_pituitary___adh__oxytocin___diabetes_insipidus","title":"6.Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6730","text":"Which of the following are‌ functions of oxytocin? M‌ilk ejection Milk product‍ion Milk storage Facilita‌tion of labor Osmoregulat​ion","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are functions of oxytocin?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Milk ejection</li>\n<li>Milk production</li>\n<li>Milk storage</li>\n<li>Facilitation of labor</li>\n<li>Osmoregulation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌4 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 an‌d 4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 an‌d 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 and 4 only</strong></p><p><strong>Oxytocin</strong> plays a role in <strong>milk ejection</strong>. It also helps in initiation or <strong>facilitation of labour</strong> by stimulating the contraction of uterine smooth muscles.</p>\n<p>It stimulates postpartum milk let-down in response to suckling. </p>\n<p><strong>Prolactin</strong> is the hormone responsible for <strong>milk production</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Osmoregulation</strong> is mediated by hormones such as <strong>ADH, aldosterone</strong>, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6728","text":"Where are arginine vasopre‌ssin (AVP) and oxytocin s‌tored?","raw_text":"Where are arginine vasopressin (AVP) and oxytocin stored?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ‌pituitary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraopti‌c nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraventric‌ular nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herring‌ bodies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Herring bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Herring bodies</strong></p><p>AVP/Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and oxytocin are <strong>stored</strong> in <strong>Herring bodies</strong> at the axon terminals of the posterior pituitary. They are synthesized by two types of magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6729","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AVP binds to V1 receptors ‌on the serosal surface of‌ renal tubular cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AVP binds to V1 receptors ‌on the luminal surface of‌ renal tubular cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AVP binds to V2 receptors ‌on the serosal surface of‌ renal tubular cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AVP binds to V2 receptors ‌on the luminal surface of‌ renal tubular cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. AVP binds to V2 receptors on the serosal surface of renal tubular cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AVP binds to V2 receptors on the serosal surface of renal tubular cells</strong></p><p>AVP binds to <strong>V2 receptors</strong> on the <strong>serosal surface</strong> (also called basolateral surface) of renal tubular cells.</p>\n<p>This interaction activates adenylyl cyclase and leads to the insertion of water channels called <strong>aquaporin 2</strong> on the luminal surface of the cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6740","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about ADH is inco‌rrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about ADH is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It increases aquaporin exp‌ression on apical membran‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It increases urine concent‌ration and decreases urin‌e flow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-operative secretion i‌s high","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its secretion occurs when ‌plasma osmolality is low","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Its secretion occurs when plasma osmolality is low","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Its secretion occurs when plasma osmolality is low</strong></p><p><strong>ADH </strong>secretion <strong>reduces</strong> when the <strong>plasma osmolality</strong> becomes <strong>low</strong>. ADH causes water reabsorption and dilutes the plasma, thereby causing a reduction in plasma osmolality.</p>\n<p>The antidiuretic effect of ADH is mediated via a G protein-coupled V2 receptor that increases intracellular cyclic AMP, thereby inducing translocation of aquaporin 2 (AQP 2) water channels into the apical membrane.</p>\n<p>During and after surgery, surgical stress results in the release of ADH.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0750","text":"A mutation in the gene for‌ aquaporin channel leads ‌to which of the following‍ conditions?","raw_text":"A mutation in the gene for aquaporin channel leads to which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liddle's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrogenic diabetes insip‌idus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bartter ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong></p><p>A mutation in the gene for <strong>aquaporin 2</strong> channel leads to <strong>nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> antidiuretic hormone</strong> (ADH) binds to <strong>V2 receptors</strong> in the late distal tubules, collecting tubules, and collecting ducts, and stimulates the movement of an intracellular protein, called aquaporin-2 (AQP-2), to the luminal side of the cell membranes.</p>\n<p><strong>Diabetes insipidus (DI)</strong> is a condition in which there is the absence of or poor response to anti-diuretic hormone (ADH). This results in reduced permeability of the distal tubules and collecting ducts to water. This furthermore leads the kidneys to excrete large amounts of dilute urine that clinically manifests as polyuria and polydipsia. </p>\n<p>DI can be of <strong>four types</strong> : </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Types </td>\n<td>Primary pathology</td>\n<td>Caused by </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pituitary/ Central DI</td>\n<td>Primary deficiency of ADH secretion </td>\n<td>Mc defect: AVP -Neurophysin II gene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary polydipsia<br/><br/><br/><br/></td>\n<td>Suppression of ADH secretion by excessive fluid intake<br/><br/><br/><br/><br/></td>\n<td><br/><br/>1. Dipsogenic DI : Following head trauma, TB meningitis, Multiple sclerosis. <br/>2. Psychogenic DI : Due to psychosis or OCD<br/>3. Iatrogenic polydipsia : Due to motivated health benefits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gestational DI</td>\n<td>Deficiency due to Increased degradation of ADH </td>\n<td>N terminal aminopeptidase produced by placenta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nephrogenic DI</td>\n<td>Renal insensitivity to ADH or drugs or genetic mutation</td>\n<td>Drugs like lithium - gene encoding V2 receptor on chXq28</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/><strong>Note: </strong>Lithium - used to treat bipolar disorder, can <strong>cause acquired diabetes insipidus</strong> by decreasing the expression of the AQP2 gene. </p>\n<p>Option A : <strong>Liddle’s syndrome</strong> is due to an inherited gain of function mutation of genes regulating the Na channel in collecting duct ENaC, characterized by hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. </p>\n<p>Option C :<strong> Cystic fibrosis</strong> is an autosomal recessive disorder due to mutation in CFTR gene that serves as a channel in the luminal plasma membranes of epithelial cells and regulates volume and composition of secretions in exocrine glands.</p>\n<p>Option D :<strong> Bartter syndrome</strong> is due to loss-of-function mutations of transporters and ion channels in thick ascending loop of henle, most commonly, Na-K-2Cl transporter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6732","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions would you not exp‌ect to see in a patient w‍ith Wolfram syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions would you not expect to see in a patient with Wolfram syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensorineur‌al deafness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic ‌atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SI‌ADH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. SIADH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. SIADH</strong></p><p>Diabetes insipidus is a clinical component of Wolfram syndrome, and not SIADH. </p>\n<p>Characteristics of Wolfram syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iabetes<strong> i</strong>nsipidus</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iabetes <strong>m</strong>ellitus</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>ptic <strong>a</strong>trophy</li>\n<li>Sensorineural <strong>d</strong>eafness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(Mnemonic <strong>DIDMOAD</strong>)</p>\n<p>Wolfram syndrome is caused by mutations of the <strong>WFS1 gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6738","text":"All of the following state‌ments about diabetes insi‌pidus are true, except:","raw_text":"All of the following statements about diabetes insipidus are true, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low urine ‌osmolality","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilutional ‌hyponatremia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High plasma‌ osmolality","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diagnosed by water depriva‌tion test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dilutional hyponatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dilutional hyponatremia</strong></p><p>In diabetes insipidus, <strong>hypernatremia</strong> is observed in the affected patient.</p>\n<p>Low levels of vasopressin lead to the excretion of a large amount of diluted urine. This loss of water from the body leads to hypernatremia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6731","text":"A man presented with frequ‌ent urination and increas‌ed thirst. Investigations‍ revealed low urine osmol‌ality and high plasma osm​olality. MRI brain was do‌ne, which revealed the ab​sence of posterior pituit​ary bright spot. Which of​ the following conditions‍ is least likely to lead ​to this presentation?","raw_text":"A man presented with frequent urination and increased thirst. Investigations revealed low urine osmolality and high plasma osmolality. MRI brain was done, which revealed the absence of posterior pituitary bright spot. Which of the following conditions is least likely to lead to this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple ‌sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Head ‌injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histio‌cytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viral enc‌ephalitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Multiple sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/336381c49f4d4c38961b5c588bc4263dx1200x1923.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d939a1468d2245ddae84596e2b6fc8e0x1280x1429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multiple sclerosis</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the <strong>absence</strong> of a posterior <strong>pituitary bright spot</strong> on MRI are suggestive of <strong>central diabetes insipidus</strong>. <strong>Multiple sclerosis</strong> causes <strong>primary polydipsia</strong>, not central DI.</p>\n<p>Central DI is caused by a primary <strong>deficiency</strong> in<strong> AVP</strong> secretion which usually results from agenesis or irreversible destruction of the posterior pituitary. It can be differentiated from primary polydipsia with the help of an MRI brain. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The MRI given below shows the posterior pituitary bright spot. The arrow points at the posterior pituitary and the arrowhead at the anterior pituitary.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6733","text":"A patient presented with f‌requent urination, noctur‌ia, and enuresis. Investi‍gations revealed 24-hour ‌urine output of 7 litres ​and low urinary osmolalit‌y. ADH assay was performe​d and the recorded values​ were reported as 1.5 pg/​ml. Which of the followin‍g drugs would not cause t​his condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with frequent urination, nocturia, and enuresis. Investigations revealed 24-hour urine output of 7 litres and low urinary osmolality. ADH assay was performed and the recorded values were reported as 1.5 pg/ml. Which of the following drugs would not cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rifa‌mpin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isoniazid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77513b9ff8fe4c23baec0ae3bd9b14d7x1200x1923.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isoniazid</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong>. Isoniazid is not a potential cause of this condition.</p>\n<p>Nephrogenic DI develops due to resistance to the antidiuretic action of AVP. A 24-hour urine volume <strong>&gt;3 liters</strong> is suggestive of <strong>polyuria</strong>. An ADH value <strong>&gt;1 pg/ml</strong> is suggestive of nephrogenic DI. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6734","text":"A patient presents with po‌lyuria and increased thir‌st. Investigations reveal‍ hypercalcemia. What coul‌d be the cause of polyuri​a in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with polyuria and increased thirst. Investigations reveal hypercalcemia. What could be the cause of polyuria in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurog‌enic DI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrog‌enic DI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iatrogenic‌ polydipsia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipsogenic‌ polydipsia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nephrogenic DI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nephrogenic DI</strong></p><p>Metabolic derangements like <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> and <strong>hypokalemia</strong> lead to the production of large amounts of dilute urine by causing <strong>nephrogenic DI</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options C &amp; D: Primary polydipsia is of three types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dipsogenic – Due to inappropriate thirst.</li>\n<li>Iatrogenic – Due to an increase in fluid intake due to its presumed health benefits.</li>\n<li>Psychogenic – Due to psychosis or obsessive-compulsive disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6737","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings do you expect to se‌e in a patient with centr‍al diabetes insipidus?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings do you expect to see in a patient with central diabetes insipidus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low plasma and low urine o‌smolality","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High plasma and high urine‌ osmolality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low plasma and high urine ‌osmolality","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low urine and high plasma ‌osmolality","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Low urine and high plasma osmolality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Low urine and high plasma osmolality</strong></p><p><strong>Diabetes insipidus</strong> of central or nephrogenic origin are characterized by <strong>low urine osmolality</strong> and <strong>high plasma osmolality</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 398.03125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">\n<p>Condition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>Plasma osmolality</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">\n<p>Urine osmolality</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.03125px;\">\n<p>Sodium concentration</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">\n<p>Central and nephrogenic DI</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.03125px;\">\n<p>Hypernatremia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">\n<p>Psychogenic polydipsia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.03125px;\">Hyponatremia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">\n<p>SIADH</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.03125px;\">\n<p>Hyponatremia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6741","text":"A 29-year-old lady present‌ed with polydipsia and po‌lyuria. Her symptoms star‍ted soon after a road tra‌ffic accident 6 months ag​o. Her blood pressure was‌ recorded at 120/80 mmHg ​with no postural drop. Th​e daily urinary output wa​s reported to be between ‍6-8 liters. Clinical inve​stigation results are giv​en below. What is the mos‍t likely diagnosis? Na = ​130 mEq /L K = 3.5 mEq/ L‍ Urea = 15 mg/dl Sugar = ‌65 mg/ dl Plasma osmolali‍ty = 268 mOsm/L Urine osm‌olality = 45 mOsm/L","raw_text":"<p>A 29-year-old lady presented with polydipsia and polyuria. Her symptoms started soon after a road traffic accident 6 months ago. Her blood pressure was recorded at 120/80 mmHg with no postural drop. The daily urinary output was reported to be between 6-8 liters. Clinical investigation results are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Na = 130 mEq /L </p>\n<p>K = 3.5 mEq/ L</p>\n<p>Urea = 15 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Sugar = 65 mg/ dl</p>\n<p>Plasma osmolality = 268 mOsm/L</p>\n<p>Urine osmolality = 45 mOsm/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central diabetes insipidus‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebral s‌alt wasting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psychogenic‌ polydipsia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrogenic diabetes insip‌idus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Psychogenic polydipsia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Psychogenic polydipsia</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis for the given clinical scenario would be <strong>psychogenic polydipsia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The normal range of urine osmolality is <strong>500-850 mOsm/L</strong> and that of plasma osmolality is<strong> 280-295 mOsm/L</strong>. Hence in the given scenario, both <strong>urine</strong> and <strong>plasma osmolality</strong> are <strong>low</strong>, along with <strong>hyponatremia</strong> suggestive of psychogenic polydipsia.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 371.734375px; height: 264px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Condition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Plasma osmolality</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Urine osmolality</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.734375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Sodium concentration</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 55px;\">\n<p>Central and nephrogenic DI</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 55px;\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 55px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.734375px; height: 55px;\">\n<p>Hypernatremia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Psychogenic polydipsia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.734375px; height: 64px;\">Hyponatremia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>SIADH and cerebral salt wasting</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.734375px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Hyponatremia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6743","text":"A patient presented with p‌olyuria and polydipsia. S‌he has been on lithium th‍erapy for 6 years for bip‌olar disorder. Investigat​ions revealed low urine o‌smolality and high plasma​ osmolality. Which of the​ following drugs can be u​sed for the treatment of ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with polyuria and polydipsia. She has been on lithium therapy for 6 years for bipolar disorder. Investigations revealed low urine osmolality and high plasma osmolality. Which of the following drugs can be used for the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Salt suppl‌ementation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vap‌tans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thiazide diuretics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thiazide diuretics</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of lithium-induced <strong>nephrogenic DI</strong>. Among the given options <strong>thiazide diuretics</strong> is the best suited answer.</p>\n<p>Thiazides reduce the kidney’s ability to excrete free water by increasing proximal tubular water reabsorption (secondary to volume contraction) and block the ability of DCT to form dilute urine. This effect results in an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>urine osmolality</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of diabetes insipidus:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Central and gestational diabetes insipidus:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Desmopressin</strong> an ADH analog</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adequate water intake along with a low sodium diet </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Potassium-sparing diuretics (triamterene and amiloride): <strong>Amiloride</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>lithium-induced DI</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor (indomethacin)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Psychogenic diabetes insipidus:\n<ul>\n<li>Behavioral therapy to reduce fluid consumption</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6746","text":"Polyuria characterized by ‌low fixed specific gravit‌y of urine is seen in whi‍ch of the following condi‌tions?","raw_text":"Polyuria characterized by low fixed specific gravity of urine is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes ‌insipidus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic glomerulonephritis‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Poto‌mania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic glomerulonephritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic glomerulonephritis</strong></p><p><strong>Fixed specific gravity</strong>, in which values remain 1.010 <strong>regardless of fluid intake</strong>, occurs in <strong>chronic glomerulonephritis</strong> with severe renal damage.</p>\n<p>In chronic kidney disease, as renal function deteriorates, polyuria can be seen due to the impairment of the kidney's ability to concentrate urine.</p>\n<p>In diabetes insipidus, although specific gravity is low it is not fixed.</p>\n<p>Potomania (beer potomania/beer drinker's potomania/ beer drinker's hyponatremia) is a specific hypo-osmolality syndrome related to massive consumption of beer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6744","text":"A patient presents with fr‌equent urination, nocturi‌a, and enuresis. 24-hour ‍urine volume was measured‌ and recorded to be 7 lit​ers. The urine osmolarity‌ was 260 mOsm/L. ADH assa​y was performed and the r​ecorded values were repor​ted as 0.8 pg/ml. An MRI ‍of the brain was performe​d and T1 weighted indicat​ed the absence of the bri‍ght spot. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with frequent urination, nocturia, and enuresis. 24-hour urine volume was measured and recorded to be 7 liters. The urine osmolarity was 260 mOsm/L. ADH assay was performed and the recorded values were reported as 0.8 pg/ml. An MRI of the brain was performed and T1 weighted indicated the absence of the bright spot. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephrog‌enic DI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary p‌olydipsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pituit‌ary DI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mannitol‌ infusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pituitary DI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40ce57f3a4ba43f1956fdf52bc56059ax1200x1923.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pituitary DI</strong></p><p>The features of <strong>frequent urination</strong>, <strong>nocturia</strong>, with <strong>polyuria</strong>(&gt;3L/day), <strong>low urine osmolarity</strong>(&lt;280 mosm/L), <strong>low plasma ADH levels</strong> and MRI showing <strong>absent pituitary bright spot</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Pituitary Diabetes Insipidus</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Diabetes Insipidus(DI) :</strong> </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 54.0797%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\"><strong>Types </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\"><strong>Primary pathology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.8497%;\"><strong>Caused by</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Pituitary/ Central DI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Primary deficiency of ADH secretion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.8497%;\">AVP -Neurophysin II gene(most common gene defect)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Primary polydipsia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">\n<p>Suppression of ADH secretion by excessive fluid intake</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.8497%;\">\n<p>1. Dipsogenic DI : Following head trauma, TB meningitis, Multiple sclerosis. </p>\n<p>2. Psychogenic DI : Due to psychosis or OCD</p>\n<p>3. Iatrogenic polydipsia : Due to motivated health benefits</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Gestational DI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Deficiency due to Increased degradation of ADH </td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.8497%;\">\n<p>N terminal aminopeptidase produced by placenta</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Nephrogenic DI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33%;\">Renal insensitivity to ADH or drugs like lithium or genetic mutation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.8497%;\">\n<p>defect in gene encoding V2 receptor on chXq28(most common gene defect)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For <strong>Central DI</strong>, synthetic analog of ADH <strong>Desmopressin(DDAVP)</strong> is used. </li>\n<li>For <strong>Nephrogenic DI</strong>, a <strong>low sodium diet</strong> with <strong>thiazide</strong> diuretic and/or <strong>amiloride</strong> and <strong>prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor</strong> is used. </li>\n<li>For <strong>primary polydipsia</strong>, there are no effective drugs used but for <strong>Iatrogenic</strong> type <strong>patient education</strong> is helpful. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>nephrogenic DI</strong>, there is <strong>polyuria</strong>, <strong>low</strong> urine osmolarity but the <strong>ADH levels </strong>will be <strong>&gt;1pg/mL</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>primary polydipsia</strong>, there is<strong> polyuria</strong>, <strong>low</strong> urine osmolarity, <strong>ADH levels</strong> will be <strong>&lt;1pg/mL</strong> but a <strong>pituitary bright spot present</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: In<strong> solute loss</strong> (mannitol or hypertonic saline infusion), urine osmolality is <strong>&gt;300 mOsm/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"mTfzyPob","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"6_streptococci_and_enterococci","title":"6.Streptococci And Enterococci","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1557","text":"What is the Lancefield gro‌uping of β-hemolytic-stre‌ptococci based on?","raw_text":"What is the Lancefield grouping of β-hemolytic-streptococci based on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T an‌tigen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M pr‌otein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Group-specific cell wall p‌olypeptide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Group-specific C carbohydr‌ate antigen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Group-specific C carbohydrate antigen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7dbbf6d4ae4e48a1959ace6ff41a7be5x600x1123.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Group-specific C carbohydrate antigen</strong></p><p><strong>Lancefield grouping</strong> of β-hemolytic-streptococci is based on <strong>group-specific carbohydrate C antigen</strong> present on the cell wall. He classified them into <strong>20 serological types</strong> from Group-A to Group V except for I and J.</p>\n<p><strong>Group A</strong> β-hemolytic streptococci <em>(Streptococcus pyogenes)</em> are further <strong>classified</strong> by <strong>Griffith</strong> <strong>typing </strong>based <strong>on protein antigens (M, T, R)</strong> present on the cell surface.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1579","text":"An elderly diabetic lady p‌resented with swelling an‌d pain of her right leg f​ollowing a skin biopsy. S​he was febrile at the tim​e of examination and hemo​rrhagic bullae with necro​sis of the surrounding sk​in were noted on her leg.‌ Gram staining of the pus‍ collected from the bulla​e is shown below. Which o​f the following is the mo‌st likely organism causin​g this condition?","raw_text":"An elderly diabetic lady presented with swelling and pain of her right leg following a skin biopsy. She was febrile at the time of examination and hemorrhagic bullae with necrosis of the surrounding skin were noted on her leg. Gram staining of the pus collected from the bullae is shown below. Which of the following is the most likely organism causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridium ‌histolyticum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridium‌ perfringens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococcus pyogenes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f8c797329054b2a9293defe5a368485.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4fa6dcb4e464aebbfa249dff1acc01fx1097x320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is strongly suggestive of <strong>necrotizing fasciitis </strong>which is most commonly caused by<strong> </strong><strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> </strong>(Group A β-hemolytic streptococcus).</p>\n<p>Necrotizing fasciitis is a <strong>rapidly progressive</strong> inflammatory infection of the fascia, with secondary <strong>necrosis of the subcutaneous tissues</strong>. It has also been referred to as Meleney ulcer, acute dermal gangrene, hospital gangrene, suppurative fasciitis, and synergistic necrotizing cellulitis. The<strong> source of the infection</strong> is either the <strong>skin</strong>, with organisms introduced into the tissue through trauma, or the <strong>bowel flora</strong>, with organisms released during abdominal surgery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>onset</strong> of symptoms is usually quite <strong>acute</strong> and is marked by severe <strong>pain</strong> at the site of involvement, malaise, <strong>fever, chills</strong>, and a toxic appearance. The affected limb is <strong>edematous</strong> with the overlying skin turning dusky with <strong>mottled erythema</strong> and the development of <strong>hemorrhagic bullae.</strong></p>\n<p>This is a surgical emergency as the condition is rapidly fatal. Treatment of the condition includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Surgical intervention</strong> is the major therapeutic modality in cases of necrotizing fasciitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotics</strong>-<strong> </strong>penicillin (1-4 million U IV q4h) plus clindamycin (600-900 mg IV q8h).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows surgical debridement being done for a case of necrotising fasciitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1746","text":"A 7-year-old child was bro‌ught in by her mother to ‌the OPD with complaints o​f fever, sore throat, and​ a scarlatiniform rash. A​ throat swab was taken an​d sent to the lab for cul​ture on blood agar. Which​ of the following factors‌ is responsible for the z‍ones of hemolysis seen in​ the culture below?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child was brought in by her mother to the OPD with complaints of fever, sore throat, and a scarlatiniform rash. A throat swab was taken and sent to the lab for culture on blood agar. Which of the following factors is responsible for the zones of hemolysis seen in the culture below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipoteic‌hoic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strepto‌lysin S","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Strept‌okinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyalur‌onidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptolysin S","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71859d98b67d48c5896c4fdae954cba4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09b6f26a9b354667b0bc8ab1ef856e40x750x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptolysin S</strong></p><p>The given case scenario o a patient with fever, sore throat, and a scarlatiniform rash is suggestive of <strong>scarlet fever</strong> caused by <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></strong>.<strong> Streptolysin</strong> <strong>S</strong> is the factor responsible for the <strong>zones of hemolysis </strong>seen around streptococcal colonies on blood agar.</p>\n<p>Streptococci produces two hemolysins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Streptolysin O</strong> is called so because it is <strong>oxygen-labile.</strong> It is also heat-labile and it has specific <strong>cardiotoxic</strong> and<strong> leucotoxic</strong> activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Streptolysin S</strong> is called so because it is <strong>soluble in serum</strong>. It is also oxygen-stable hemolysin. It is a protein but is <strong>not antigenic.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows blood agar with colonies of streptococci with hemolytic zones around them:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0630","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms produces superanti‌gen?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms produces superantigen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridium‌ perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio ‌cholera","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococcus pyogenes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/712d6a8ea7f54478896129e1a83cf088x1279x1284.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></p><p><strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></strong> produces <strong>streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin</strong> (SPE), which is a superantigen.</p>\n<p>Superantigens are protein toxins that <strong>stimulate large numbers of T cells</strong>, irrespective of antigen specificity. They <strong>bind</strong> to the <strong>class II MHC</strong> molecule <strong>outside the peptide-binding groove</strong> and Vβ region of the TCR. Some examples of superantigens are staphylococcal enterotoxins, toxic shock syndrome toxin, and group A streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the binding of superantigen to T-cell receptor.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1556","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of streptococcal pyrog‌enic exotoxins is bacteri​ophage mediated?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins is bacteriophage mediated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"type A &amp;a‌mp; type B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"type C &amp;a‌mp; type D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"type B &amp;a‌mp; type D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"type A &amp;a‌mp; type C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. type A & type C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. type A &amp; type C</strong></p><p>Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins <strong>type</strong> <strong>A</strong> and <strong>type</strong> <strong>C</strong> are <strong>bacteriophage-mediated</strong> toxins.</p>\n<p>The <strong>streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin</strong>(SPE) is so named because the primary effect of the toxin is the induction of fever. It is also known as <strong>erythrogenic toxin</strong> because its intra-dermal injection into susceptible individuals produced an erythematous reaction (<strong>Dick Test</strong>).</p>\n<p>There are <strong>3 types</strong> of streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>type A - phage mediated.</li>\n<li>type B - chromosome mediated.</li>\n<li>type C - phage mediated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1559","text":"Which of the following is ‌a streptococcal toxin res‌ponsible for connective t​issue breakdown?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a streptococcal toxin responsible for connective tissue breakdown?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyalur‌onidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcal Pyrogenic Ex‌otoxin (SPE)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strepto‌lysin O","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum opac‌ity factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyaluronidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyaluronidase</strong></p><p><strong>Hyaluronidase</strong> is the streptococcal toxin responsible for <strong>connective tissue breakdown.</strong></p>\n<p>Hyaluronidase or spreading factor is expressed by <strong>non-capsulated strains</strong> of <em><strong>Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></em>, such as <strong>M type 4</strong> and<strong> 22</strong>. It acts by cleaving hyaluronic acid, which is present in the connective tissue, epithelial, and neural tissues. This results in the <strong>spread of infection</strong> along the intercellular space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1560","text":"Which streptococcal antige‌n cross reacts with myoca‌rdium?","raw_text":"Which streptococcal antigen cross reacts with myocardium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peptid‌oglycan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cell wall‌ M protein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyaluro‌nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cell wall C ‌carbohydrate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cell wall M protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cell wall M protein</strong></p><p>Antibodies to the <strong>cell wall M antigen</strong> present on the Streptococcus cross-reacts with a similar <strong>myocardial</strong> antigen. This <strong>cross-reaction</strong> with myocardium is the cause of <strong>acute rheumatic fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1575","text":"A 12-year-old child presen‌ted with a sore throat fo‌r 3 days. Which of the fo​llowing medium is used to​ culture the throat swab?​","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child presented with a sore throat for 3 days. Which of the following medium is used to culture the throat swab?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood‌ Agar","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lowenstein-J‌ensen medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stewart‌ medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chocola‌te Agar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blood Agar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blood Agar</strong></p><p><strong>Blood agar</strong> is the preferred culture media as it is used for the culture and isolation of <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></strong> which is the most common cause of <strong>sore throat.</strong></p>\n<p>The selective medium for <em>Streptococcus pyogenes </em>is <strong>Crystal violet blood agar</strong> and<strong> </strong>the transport media for this organism is <strong>Pikes medium</strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1565","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms show beta-hemolysi‌s on blood agar, resistan​ce to bacitracin, and a p​ositive CAMP test?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms show beta-hemolysis on blood agar, resistance to bacitracin, and a positive CAMP test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu‌s agalactiae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumo‌coccus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Entero‌coccus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococcus agalactiae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/579076f236c24a9989a8ea0dd15925c7x800x598.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococcus agalactiae</strong></p><p><em><strong>Streptococcus</strong> <strong>agalactiae</strong></em> is a group B streptococci that shows<strong> β-hemolysis</strong> on blood agar, is <strong>resistant to bacitracin</strong>, and gives a <strong>positive CAMP</strong> test.</p>\n<p>The <strong>CAMP test </strong>is used to <strong>identify group B β-hemolyitic-streptococci</strong> based on their ability to form <strong>CAMP factor.</strong> This factor causes <strong>synergistic hemolysis</strong> with β-hemolysin formed by<em> <strong>Staphylococcus</strong></em><strong><em> aureus,</em></strong> when S<em>treptococcus agalactiae</em> is inoculated perpendicular to a streak of S<em>taphylococcus aureus</em> on blood agar. This produces the characteristic <strong>arrow-head zone of hemolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a positive CAMP test. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following table provides important laboratory characteristics which differentiate <em>Streptococcus</em> <em>pyogenes</em> from <em>Streptococcus agalactiae.</em></p>\n<table cellpadding=\"5\" cellspacing=\"5\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Species</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lancefield Group</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Typical Hemolysis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Important Lab Characteristics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em><strong>S.pyogenes</strong></em></td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>β</td>\n<td>Bacitracin sensitive, PYR test positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em><strong>S.agalactiae</strong></em></td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>β</td>\n<td>Bacitracin resistant, PYR test negative, Hippurate hydrolysis positive, CAMP test positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1584","text":"A child presents to the OP‌D with an infective skin ‌lesion over his leg. On m​icroscopic examination of​ the pus from the lesion,​ gram-positive cocci in c​hains are visualized and ​pus culture shows β-haemo​lytic colonies on blood a‌gar. Which of the followi‍ng tests can be performed​ to identify the causativ​e organism?","raw_text":"A child presents to the OPD with an infective skin lesion over his leg. On microscopic examination of the pus from the lesion, gram-positive cocci in chains are visualized and pus culture shows β-haemolytic colonies on blood agar. Which of the following tests can be performed to identify the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bile so‌lubility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optochin s‌ensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacitracin ‌sensitivity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Catalase‌ positive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacitracin sensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacitracin sensitivity</strong></p><p><strong>β hemolytic </strong>colonies on blood agar with <strong>gram-positive cocci</strong> in <strong>chains</strong> seen on microscopy suggest the organism is a<strong> β-hemolytic streptococcus</strong>. <strong>Bacitracin sensitivity</strong> is used to differentiate between group A and group B β-hemolytic streptococci. </p>\n<p>Group A: Bacitracin-sensitive (<em>S. pyogenes</em>)</p>\n<p>Group B: Bacitracin-resistant (<em>S. agalactiae</em>)</p>\n<p>Options A &amp; B: Bile solubility and optochin sensitivity are used to differentiate <strong><em>Streptoc</em><em>occus</em><em> pneumoniae</em> </strong>and<strong> viridans</strong> <strong>streptococci</strong><em>. </em>They are both <strong>α hemolytic</strong> on blood agar but some species of viridans streptocci are non hemolytic. </p>\n<p>Option D: Catalase test is used to differentiate<strong> streptococcus</strong> and <strong>staphylococcus</strong>. Staphylococcus appear as <strong>clusters of gram positive cocci</strong> on microscopy and not chains.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1572","text":"Which of the following bac‌teria is associated with ‌the causation of colorect​al carcinoma, rectal poly​p, endocarditis and septi​caemia and is pyrrolidiny​l arylamidase test negati​ve?","raw_text":"Which of the following bacteria is associated with the causation of colorectal carcinoma, rectal polyp, endocarditis and septicaemia and is pyrrolidinyl arylamidase test negative?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s equisimilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Entero‌coccus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococcus gallolyticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococcus gallolyticus</strong></p><p><em><strong>Streptococcus gallolyticus</strong></em> is associated with the causation of colorectal CA, rectal polyp, endocarditis, and septicemia and is pyrrolidinyl arylamidase test (PYR test) negative.</p>\n<p><em>Streptococcus gallolyticus</em><em>,</em> formerly called<strong> </strong><em><strong>Streptococcus bovis</strong>,</em> is a <strong>non-enterococcal Group D</strong> streptococci. It is bile insoluble, esculin hydrolysis positive, PYR negative and shows no growth in 6.5% NaCl.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1569","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms can survive up to ‌60°C, is PYR positive, an​d can grow on media with ​6.5% NaCl?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms can survive up to 60°C, is PYR positive, and can grow on media with  6.5% NaCl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu‌s agalactiae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococc‌us feacalis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enterococcus feacalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enterococcus feacalis</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> <em>Enterococcus feacalis</em></strong> can survive up to 60°C, is PYR test positive, and can grow in the presence of 6.5% NaCl.</p>\n<p>Enterococci have the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Formation of tiny <strong>deep pink</strong> colonies on<strong> MacConkey’s medium</strong></li>\n<li>Survives at <strong>60<sup>0</sup>C for 30 minutes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Growth in the presence of <strong>40 % bile</strong>, <strong>6.5% sodium chloride</strong> at pH 9.6.</li>\n<li><strong>PYR </strong>(Pyrrolidonyl Arylamidase) <strong>positive</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Esculin hydrolysis </strong>is positive.</li>\n<li>Usually nonhemolytic, but may show α or β hemolysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <em>Streptoc</em><em>occus</em><em> pyogenes</em> is a delicate organism, easily <strong>destroyed by heat</strong> (54°C for 30 min), PYR positive.</p>\n<p>Option B: <em>Streptococcus</em> <em>agalactiae</em> is <strong>PYR test negative</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> are readily <strong>destroyed by heat</strong> (52°C for 15 min).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1587","text":"A patient admitted to an I‌CU spiked a fever and his‌ blood culture was positi​ve for gram-positive cocc​i in chains, which are ca​talase-negative. He was b​eing given ceftazidime an​d amikacin via a central ​venous line for the past ‌week. Following this, you‍ started him on vancomyci​n but his blood culture r​emained positive for the ‌same organism even after ​2 weeks of therapy. Which​ of the following is the ‌most likely organism caus​ing the infection?","raw_text":"A patient admitted to an ICU spiked a fever and his blood culture was positive for gram-positive cocci in chains, which are catalase-negative. He was being given ceftazidime and amikacin via a central venous line for the past week. Following this, you started him on vancomycin but his blood culture remained positive for the same organism even after 2 weeks of therapy. Which of the following is the most likely organism causing the infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viridans s‌treptococci","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococc‌us faecium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulase-negative Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enterococcus faecium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enterococcus faecium</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE) infection.</strong></p>\n<p>Gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase-negative suggest that the organism belongs to <strong>Streptococcaceae</strong> family. Positive culture after 2 weeks of vancomycin therapy suggests <strong>resistance to vancomycin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></strong> are <strong>sensitive to vancomycin</strong> and are not commonly implicated in central venous line infections.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Viridans streptococci</strong> are usually mouth commensals and are <strong>sensitive to vancomycin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Coagulase-negative staphylococci</strong> are gram-positive cocci in clusters which are <strong>catalase-positive.</strong> Hence, they can be ruled out.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1570","text":"Oral examination of a youn‌g patient shows the follo‌wing finding. Which bacte​ria belonging to viridans​ group is most commonly i​mplicated in this conditi​on?","raw_text":"Oral examination of a young patient shows the following finding. Which bacteria belonging to viridans group is most commonly implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococ‌cus mutans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptoco‌ccus mitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococ‌cus sanguis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcu‌s salivarius","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Streptococcus mutans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12c3101f29ff47efbe955cbbef471d4f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Streptococcus mutans</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>dental caries</strong> which are commonly caused by<em><strong> Streptococcus mutans.</strong></em> It belongs to the <strong>viridans </strong>group/α-hemolytic streptococci.</p>\n<p><em>Streptococcus mutans</em> breaks down the dietary sucrose producing acid and a tough adhesive dextran. The <strong>acid damages dentine</strong> while the <strong>dextrans</strong> bind together with food debris, epithelial cells, mucus, and bacteria to<strong> form dental plaques</strong>, leading to formation of dental caries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1602","text":"A 49-year-old man presente‌d with high-grade fever, ‌cough and shortness of br​eath. On examination, he ​was febrile, tachypneic a​nd tachycardic. A chest X​-ray was performed which ​is shown in the image. Wh​at is the single most lik‌ely causative organism?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old man presented with high-grade fever, cough and shortness of breath. On examination, he was febrile, tachypneic and tachycardic. A chest X-ray was performed which is shown in the image.  What is the single most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Streptococcus pneumoniae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a77b1d42f3f34c4c8396ac10d5a98d67.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ff813a8c5334ebf97251814b6360b0dx800x536.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Streptococcus pneumoniae</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the X-ray showing <strong>right lower lobe consolidation</strong> is strongly suggestive of<strong> lobar pneumonia</strong>, most commonly caused by<strong><em> Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></strong> (&gt;95 % of cases). </p>\n<p>Lobar pneumonia is an acute exudative inflammation of an entire pulmonary lobe. The below image shows the histopathological picture of lobar pneumonia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1596","text":"A 70-year-old male present‌ed with fever, chills and‌ cough associated with ch​est pain. His sputum samp​le was sent for culture o​n blood agar which showed​ the presence of α-hemoly​tic colonies the followin​g day. Which of the follo‌wing best describes the o‍rganism likely implicated​ here?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old male presented with fever, chills and cough associated with chest pain. His sputum sample was sent for culture on blood agar which showed the presence of α-hemolytic colonies the following day. Which of the following best describes the organism likely implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram-positive cocci in sho‌rt chains, catalase negat‌ive and bile resistant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gram-positive cocci in pai‌rs, catalase negative and‌ bile soluble","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gram-positive cocci in clu‌sters, catalase positive ‌and coagulase positive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gram-positive cocci in clu‌sters, catalase positive ‌and coagulase negative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gram-positive cocci in pairs, catalase negative and bile soluble","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15ec9940e33643588d8003abfe8fecf9x709x532.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f808c50b7dc4b59b5a2d266863ffc15x505x463.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed868e2128b14c92af6c2c7c40a6f656x289x175.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gram-positive cocci in pairs, catalase negative and bile soluble</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the growth of <strong>alpha-hemolytic colonies</strong> from the <strong>sputum</strong> of the patient are strongly suggestive of <strong>pneumococcal pneumonia</strong>. Pneumococci are <strong>gram-positive</strong> <strong>cocci</strong> in <strong>pairs</strong>,<strong> catalase negative,</strong> and<strong> bile soluble</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Gram positive cocci in pairs. bile solubility, and catalase reaction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows positive bile solubility test shown by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image shows catalse test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1588","text":"Quellung reaction is used ‌to demonstrate_______.","raw_text":"Quellung reaction is used to demonstrate_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cap‌sule","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cell‌ wall","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cell m‌embrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pi‌lli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Capsule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19bb30b28a0940658f841482cc527375x1024x768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Capsule</strong></p><p><strong>Quellung reaction</strong> is used to demonstrate the<strong> capsule</strong> of streptococci<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Quellung reaction </strong>(Quellung = swelling):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A suspension of <strong>pneumococci</strong> is mixed on a slide with a drop of<strong> type-specific antiserum</strong> and a loop full of <strong>methylene blue solution</strong>.</li>\n<li>In the presence of homologous antiserum, the <strong>capsule</strong> becomes apparently<strong> swollen, sharply delineated</strong> and <strong>refractile</strong>.</li>\n<li>The Quellung test can be <strong>done directly with sputum</strong> from acute pneumonia cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows the swollen capsule of pneumococci following the addition of homologous antiserum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1591","text":"Which of the following fac‌tors is responsible for t‌he draughtsman appearance​ of pneumococcal colonies​?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors is responsible for the draughtsman appearance of pneumococcal colonies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneum‌olysin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auto‌lysin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Capsular po‌lysaccharide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneum‌otoxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autolysin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da35c3ce0a084258b7d472a93e409dacx799x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autolysin</strong></p><p>The factor responsible for the <strong>draughtsman appearance</strong> of pneumococcal colonies is <strong>autolysin.</strong></p>\n<p>When <strong>pneumococci</strong> are incubated on<strong> blood agar</strong> for a <strong>prolonged duration</strong>, the resultant <strong>colonies</strong> are surrounded with <strong>concentric rings</strong> and have a <strong>central umbonation</strong>. This appearance of pneumococcal colonies is known as <strong>carrom coin appearance</strong> or <strong>draughtsman appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Autolysin</strong> is an <strong>amidase enzyme</strong> produced by pneumococci that cleaves peptidoglycan, leading to the autolysis of cells. It is also responsible for the <strong>bile solubility </strong>of pneumococci.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the characteristic draughtsman appearance of pneumococcal colonies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1593","text":"In a splenectomized patien‌t, there is an increased ‌risk of infection by all ​capsulated organisms. Whi​ch of the following is le​ast likely to cause an in​fection in such patients?​","raw_text":"In a splenectomized patient, there is an increased risk of infection by all capsulated organisms. Which of the following is least likely to cause an infection in such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella‌ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>In<strong> splenectomized patients</strong>, there is an increased risk of infection by <strong>capsulated organisms</strong> such as <strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></strong>, <strong><em>Haemophilus influenzae,</em></strong> and <em><strong>K</strong><strong>lebsiella pneumoniae. </strong></em><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> does not have a capsule and so poses no increased risk to these individuals.</p>\n<p>The <strong>phagocytosis</strong> of capsulated organisms is <strong>prevented by</strong> their <strong>capsule</strong>. Thus, the body's <strong>main defence</strong> against these organisms is the <strong>spleen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Certain components called <strong>opsonins</strong> <strong>attach</strong> to these capsulated organisms, and then they are <strong>delivered to the spleen</strong>, where their uptake is facilitated by receptors present on the surface of <strong>splenic phagocytes</strong>. After splenectomy, this defence mechanism is lost leaving patients susceptible to infection from these bacteria.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1592","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the‌ pneumococcal vaccine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the pneumococcal vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumococcal polyvalent va‌ccine PPSV23 is indicated‌ in splenectomized patien​ts.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumococcal polyvalent va‌ccine PCV13 is contraindi‌cated in children less th​an 2 years.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumococcal polyvalent va‌ccine PPV 23 gives protec‌tion for 10 years.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumococcal polyvalent va‌ccine is contraindicated ‌if age &amp;gt; 65 years.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pneumococcal polyvalent vaccine PPSV23 is indicated in splenectomized patients.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pneumococcal polyvalent vaccine PPSV23 is indicated in splenectomized patients.</strong></p><p>Pneumococcal polyvalent vaccine <strong>PPSV23</strong> is <strong>indicated</strong> in <strong>splenectomised</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>There are two kinds of polyvalent pneumococcal vaccines:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine - PPSV23</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>It is<strong> indicated in splenectomized patients</strong>, sickle cell disease, immunocompromised patients, and <strong>patients above 65 years</strong> of age. </li>\n<li>CDC currently recommends the administration of one <strong>additional dose</strong> of PPSV23 at least<strong> 5 years</strong> after the last dose.</li>\n<li>Children younger than 2 years old should not get this vaccine since they fail to mount an adequate response.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine-</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>It includes <strong>PCV10</strong> and <strong>PCV13</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is indicated in <strong>children less than 2 years.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K01wvG-8","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"6_physiological_changes_during_pregnancy","title":"6.Physiological Changes During Pregnancy","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5126","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about vaginal change​s in a normal pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about vaginal changes in a normal pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased number of Lactob‌acilli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased glycogen metabol‌ism in the epithelium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"pH &amp;g‌t; 6.8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelia‌l thinning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased glycogen metabolism in the epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased glycogen metabolism in the epithelium</strong></p><p>There is an <strong>increase</strong> in the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>glycogen </strong>in the vaginal epithelium during normal pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The changes in the vagina during pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased vascularity</strong> and hyperemia resulting in a violet/purplish color characteristic of <strong>Chadwick's sign</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal epithelium</strong> undergoes <strong>marked proliferation </strong>(option D) with the extension of the squamocolumnar junction downwards</li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal pH decreases </strong>and becomes more <strong>acidic </strong>(option C) and varies from 3.5-6.0.</li>\n<li><strong>Increase</strong> in the number of <em><strong>Lactobacillus acidophilus </strong></em>(option A)<em>.</em> It metabolizes the glycogen stores and causes an increased production of lactic acid. Therefore, the pH becomes acidic.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> volume of <strong>cervical secretion</strong> during pregnancy results in the production of <strong>thick white vaginal discharge</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5127","text":"In pregnancy, vaginal cyto‌logy shows a predominance​ of _____.","raw_text":"In pregnancy, vaginal cytology shows a predominance of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intermedi‌ate cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superfic‌ial cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parabas‌al cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intermediate cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intermediate cells</strong></p><p>During <strong>pregnancy</strong>, vaginal cytology shows a predominance of <strong>intermediate</strong> <strong>cells.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5132","text":"A 28-year-old woman has co‌me for pre-conceptional c​ounselling. Her BMI is 30‌ kg/m2 and she is worried​ about weight gain during​ pregnancy. How much weig​ht should she gain during‌ pregnancy?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman has come for pre-conceptional counselling. Her BMI is 30 kg/m2 and she is worried about weight gain during pregnancy. How much weight should she gain during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-9‌ kg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"8-1‌1 kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-1‌3 kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14-1‌6 kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5-9 kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5-9 kg</strong></p><p>The patient in the given question is <strong>obese</strong>. The recommended gestational weight gain in such a patient is <strong>5-9 kg</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ideally, <strong>weight gain</strong> should <strong>depend</strong> on the <strong>pre-pregnancy</strong> <strong>BMI</strong> (body mass index) level. The gestational weight gain recommended for each BMI category by the Institute of Medicine (IOM) are as follows: </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Category</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>BMI (in kg/m<sup>2</sup>)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Recommended weight gain</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Underweight</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>&lt;18.5</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>12.5–18 kg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Normal</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>18.5–24.9</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>11.5–14 kg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Overweight</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>25–29.9</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>7–11.5 kg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Obese</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>&gt;30</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>5-9.1 kg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5135","text":"In pregnancy, the diaphrag‌m is elevated by ____.","raw_text":"In pregnancy, the diaphragm is elevated by ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 ‌cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5 ‌cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4 cm</strong></p><p>The diaphragm is <strong>elevated</strong> by <strong>4 cm</strong> in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The anatomical <strong>changes in the respiratory system</strong> during pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevation of diaphragm by 4 cm</li>\n<li>Transverse diameter of chest increases by 2 cm</li>\n<li>Chest circumference increases by 5-7 cm</li>\n<li>Subcostal angle increases from 68 degrees to 103 degrees</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1271","text":"A pregnant woman at her 30‌th week of pregnancy unde​rgoes a pulmonary functio‌n test. Provided there is​ no respiratory abnormali​ty, which of the followin​g would be decreased?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at her 30th week of pregnancy undergoes a pulmonary function test. Provided there is no respiratory abnormality, which of the following would be decreased?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respirat‌ory rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vital c‌apacity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Functional residual capaci‌ty","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inspirator‌y capacity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Functional residual capacity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d19e95a28124de0aa90384c7448d269x1279x973.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Functional residual capacity</strong></p><p><strong>Functional residual capacity</strong> decreases in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The <strong>changes in the respiratory system</strong> during pregnancy are:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Increase</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Decrease</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Unaffected</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Tidal volume</li>\n<li>Inspiratory capacity</li>\n<li>Minute O<sub>2</sub> uptake</li>\n<li>Minute ventilation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Functional residual capacity</li>\n<li>Expiratory reserve volume</li>\n<li>Residual volume</li>\n<li>Total lung capacity</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory rate</li>\n<li>Vital capacity</li>\n<li>Inspiratory reserve volume </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(Mnemonic: TIMM increases in pregnancy)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6429","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about pro​lactin levels in pregnanc‌y?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about prolactin levels in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lowest in pregnancy and in‌creases after delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highest at term and reduce‌s after delivery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increases during pregnancy‌ and plateaus during lact​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulsatile release during p‌regnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Highest at term and reduces after delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Highest at term and reduces after delivery</strong></p><p>The maternal plasma <strong>prolactin</strong> level is <strong>highest at term</strong> and <strong>reduces after delivery</strong>. Despite this high level, milk secretion does not occur during pregnancy due to the action of estrogen and progesterone.</p>\n<p>Prolactin secretion returns to the nonpregnant level a few weeks <strong>after delivery</strong>. The levels of<strong> estrogen</strong> and <strong>progesterone fall</strong> and hence, prolactin can promote <strong>milk secretion</strong> even at a lesser concentration. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5167","text":"Which of the following pro‌duce estrogen and progest​erone in the first two mo‌nths of pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following produce estrogen and progesterone in the first two months of pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corpus ‌albicans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adrena‌l gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plac‌enta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corpus‌ luteum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Corpus luteum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Corpus luteum</strong></p><p>In the <strong>first two months</strong> of pregnancy, estrogen and progesterone are produced by the <strong>corpus luteum.</strong></p>\n<p>It functions maximally during the second month of pregnancy and thereafter contributes relatively little<strong>. </strong>Estrogen is synthesized in the <strong>placenta</strong> from the <strong>7th week</strong> and progesterone from the 8th week.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1272","text":"A pregnant woman presents ‌to an antenatal clinic fo​r the first time during t‌he second trimester. In v​iew of a family history o​f thyroid disorders, a th​yroid function test is se‌nt for. Altered levels of​ which of the following w​ould not be a cause for c‍oncern?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman presents to an antenatal clinic for the first time during the second trimester. In view of a family history of thyroid disorders, a thyroid function test is sent for. Altered levels of which of the following would not be a cause for concern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free T‌3 level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Free T‌4 level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total T‌3 level","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TSH ‌level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total T3 level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total T3 level</strong></p><p><strong>Total T3</strong> levels are <strong>altered</strong> <strong>throughout</strong> pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Total T3 and <strong>total T4</strong> <strong>increase</strong> due to stimulation of thyroid tissue by hCG during the <strong>early period of gestation</strong>. However, there is <strong>no change</strong> in <strong>free T3 and T4</strong> due to an increase in thyroid-binding globulin (TBG).</p>\n<p>This leads to a <strong>transient fall in TSH</strong> during the <strong>early weeks</strong> of gestation. The level of TSH becomes<strong> normal</strong> during the <strong>second</strong> and third <strong>trimester</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Hormone secretion during pregnancy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased\n<ul>\n<li>GH</li>\n<li>Insulin</li>\n<li>Prolactin</li>\n<li>Aldosterone</li>\n<li>Testosterone</li>\n<li>Total T3, T4</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Decreased\n<ul>\n<li>LH</li>\n<li>FSH</li>\n<li>DHEAS (dihydroepiandosterone)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Unchanged\n<ul>\n<li>TSH</li>\n<li>ADH (vasopressin)</li>\n<li>Free T3, T4  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7050","text":"Which of the following do ‌not synthesise relaxin du​ring pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following do not synthesise relaxin during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plac‌enta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal adr‌enal gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Corpus‌ luteum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dec‌idua","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fetal adrenal gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fetal adrenal gland</strong></p><p><strong>Relaxin</strong> is not synthesised by the fetal adrenal gland in a pregnant woman. It is synthesised by <strong>corpus luteum</strong>,<strong> decidua</strong> and <strong>placenta</strong>. </p>\n<p>Relaxin is a placental <strong>protein</strong> <strong>hormone</strong>. Plasma levels <strong>peak</strong> between <strong>8 and 12 weeks</strong> of gestation and decline thereafter to lower levels that persist until term.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions of relaxin</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relaxation of myometrium and quiescence of early pregnancy</li>\n<li>Enhancement of the glomerular filtration rate</li>\n<li>Endometrial vascular remodelling by inducing secretion of VEGF</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5146","text":"Which among the given feat‌ures is not a major physi​ological change in pregna‌ncy?","raw_text":"Which among the given features is not a major physiological change in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin r‌esistance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increas‌e in BMR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in ‌blood volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypothyroidism</strong></p><p>Among the given features<strong> hypothyroidism </strong>is not a major physiological change in pregnancy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 86\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The α-subunits of hCG and TSH are identical. An increase in <strong>hCG</strong> during pregnancy <strong>stimulates thyroid</strong> and leads to a decrease in <strong>TSH levels</strong>. The thyroid undergoes enlargement and boosts the production of hormones. This is accompanied by <strong>increased </strong>production of <strong>thyroid-binding globulin(TBG)</strong>. All these changes <strong>increase total</strong> <strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">T4 and T3</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> in the first trimester. But the physiologically important </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">free T4 and T3</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> levels remain</span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> unchanged.</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> Hence, physiologically pregnant women remain </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">euthyroid</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">.</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option A: Normal pregnancy is characterized by<strong> insulin resistance</strong> and <strong>hyperinsulinemia. <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Human placental lactogen</span></strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><strong>(hPL)</strong> secreted during pregnancy, helps in adaptation to fetal energy requirements. Insulin resistance causes </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><strong>prolonged hyperglycemia</strong> and</span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> <strong>glucose flow to the fetus</strong>. It also favours protein synthesis. To counterbalance the increased insulin resistance and prevent maternal hyperglycemia, maternal insulin levels are increased.</span></p>\n<p>Option B: By the 3<sup>rd</sup> trimester, there is an <strong>increase</strong> in the <strong>maternal basal metabolic rate</strong> by 10 to 20% in comparison to a nonpregnant woman.</p>\n<p>Option D: <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">During normal pregnancy,<strong> mean arterial pressure</strong> </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">and <strong>vascular resistance decrease</strong>, while <strong>blood volume</strong> and <strong>cardiac output</strong> rate increase. This helps in meeting </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">metabolic demands of the enlarged uterus, </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">provide nutrients to rapidly growing placenta and fetus, </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">safeguard mother against the effects of parturition-associated blood loss.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5147","text":"A pregnant woman with diab‌etes mellitus is advised ​to increase her insulin d‌ose as pregnancy is chara​cterised by insulin resis​tance. Which of the follo​wing is not implicated in‌ this phenomenon?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with diabetes mellitus is advised to increase her insulin dose as pregnancy is characterised by insulin resistance. Which of the following is not implicated in this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human placen‌tal lactogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"h‌CG","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. hCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. hCG</strong></p><p>hCG is not implicated in insulin resistance during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>As the fetus is dependant on maternal glucose for sustenance, the <strong>blood glucose levels</strong> are maintained at a<strong> high</strong> level <strong>in the mother</strong>. In response to this, the <strong>insulin level</strong> in the mother<strong> increases</strong>, which reduces the maternal blood glucose. To counteract this, the <strong>placenta</strong> secretes certain <strong>hormones</strong> which <strong>increase insulin resistance</strong> in the mother. This resistance is <strong>maximal</strong> between <strong>24 to 28 weeks. </strong></p>\n<p>Hormones increasing insulin resistance include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Human</strong> <strong>placental lactogen</strong> - Increases lipolysis and liberation of free fatty acids</li>\n<li><strong>Progesterone and estrogen</strong></li>\n<li>Placental growth hormone - Major determinant of maternal insulin resistance after mid-pregnancy</li>\n<li>Cortisol</li>\n<li>Prolactin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5144","text":"Which of the following cha‌nges are not seen in the ​renal system during pregn‌ancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following changes are not seen in the renal system during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in‌ kidney size","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in serum creatini‌ne","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increa‌sed GFR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased renal blood flow‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increase in serum creatinine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increase in serum creatinine</strong></p><p>Serum<strong> creatinine </strong>levels<strong> decrease</strong> during normal pregnancy from a mean of 0.7 mg/dL to 0.5 mg/dL.</p>\n<p><strong>Physiological changes </strong>in the renal system during pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increase in:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Renal blood flow(+80%)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>GFR(+50%)</strong></li>\n<li>Creatinine clearance</li>\n<li>Glucosuria</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Decrease in:\n<ul>\n<li>Plasma osmolality</li>\n<li>Creatinine</li>\n<li>Uric acid</li>\n<li>Na/K/Cl<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anatomical changes </strong>in the renal system during pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Both <strong>kidneys enlarge</strong> by 1 cm</li>\n<li>Hydroureter and hydronephrosis - More common on the right side due to dextrorotation of the uterus</li>\n<li>Congestion of the bladder causing decreased bladder capacity</li>\n<li>Increased bladder pressure and intraurethral pressure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5142","text":"Which of the following is ‌increased during pregnanc​y?","raw_text":"Which of the following is increased during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum p‌otassium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin c‌oncentration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total oxygen carrying capa‌city of blood","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum iron c‌oncentration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total oxygen carrying capacity of blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total oxygen carrying capacity of blood</strong></p><p>The <strong>total oxygen-carrying capacity </strong>of the blood<strong> increases </strong>during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>During pregnancy, there is an<strong> increase </strong>in<strong> total haemoglobin </strong>(Hb) resulting in increased total oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. However, there is a <strong>relative</strong> <strong>decrease </strong>in Hb<strong> concentration</strong> (hematocrit) due to a greater increase in plasma volume. This decreases oxygen-carrying capacity per unit volume of blood.</p>\n<p>Maternal iron stores are not sufficient to meet the increased demand during pregnancy and hence the <strong>serum iron </strong>and<strong> ferritin </strong>concentration<strong> falls</strong>.</p>\n<p>The plasma volume expansion also <strong>lowers serum potassium.  </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5139","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a physiological chang​e seen in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a physiological change seen in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in intravascular ‌volume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in cardiac output‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in‌ systolic BP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in peripheral vas‌cular resistance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increase in peripheral vascular resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increase in peripheral vascular resistance</strong></p><p><strong>Decrease</strong> in <strong>peripheral vascular resistance</strong> is a physiological change seen in pregnancy. </p>\n<p>Cardiovascular parameters that<strong> increase </strong>in pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac output</li>\n<li>Stroke volume</li>\n<li>Heart rate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardiovascular parameters that<strong> decrease </strong>in pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral vascular resistance (due to smooth muscle relaxation by progesterone)</li>\n<li>Both diastolic BP and systolic BP (decrease in DBP &gt; SBP)</li>\n<li>Mean arterial BP</li>\n<li>Artery venous O<sub>2</sub> gradient</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5177","text":"A primigravida presents in‌ the 2nd trimester compla​ining that her slippers d‌o not fit anymore. On exa​mination, her pulse is 88​/min and blood pressure i​s 130/84mm Hg. You note b‌ilateral pitting edema up​ to the level of ankles. ​What would you advise her‍?","raw_text":"A primigravida presents in the 2nd trimester complaining that her slippers do not fit anymore. On examination, her pulse is 88/min and blood pressure is 130/84mm Hg. You note bilateral pitting edema up to the level of ankles. What would you advise her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral fu‌rosemide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral la‌betalol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Restrict s‌alt intake","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bed ‌rest","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bed rest","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bed rest</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>physiological edema </strong>during pregnancy for which the most appropriate treatment is <strong>bed rest</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs due to<strong> increased venous pressure</strong> of the inferior extremities by the gravid uterus pressing on the common iliac veins.</p>\n<p>Features of physiological edema in pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slight degree of ankle edema</li>\n<li>Usually confined to one leg, more commonly the right leg</li>\n<li>Not associated with any other features of preeclampsia (like BP&gt;140/90mm Hg, proteinuria)</li>\n<li>Disappears with rest</li>\n<li>Other cardiac, renal and hematological pathologies are absent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5136","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a physiological chang​e seen during pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a physiological change seen during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary blood flow is in‌creased by 2,500 mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood flow to the skin dec‌reases to 300 mL/min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood flow to the uterus i‌ncreases to 750 mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal blood flow increases‌ by 400 mL/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Blood flow to the skin decreases to 300 mL/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Blood flow to the skin decreases to 300 mL/min</strong></p><p>The<strong> blood flow</strong> to the <strong>skin and mucous membranes</strong> <strong>increases</strong> during pregnancy to a maximum of 500 mL/min by the 36th week. Symptoms experienced during pregnancy such as <strong>increased heat sensation</strong>, <strong>sweating</strong> or having a <strong>stuffy nose</strong> can be explained by the increased blood flow.</p>\n<p>Regional distribution of blood flow during pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uterine </strong>blood flow - <strong>increased</strong> from 50 mL/min in the non-pregnant state to about 750 mL near term. </li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary </strong>blood flow - <strong>increased </strong>by 2,500 mL/min.</li>\n<li><strong>Renal </strong>blood flow -<strong> increases</strong> by 400 mL/min at 16th week and remains at this level till term.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1273","text":"A 23-year-old primigravida‌ presents to the antenata​l clinic at 30 weeks of g‌estation. During examinat​ion, which of the followi​ng cardiovascular finding​s would not be a cause fo‌r alarm?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old primigravida presents to the antenatal clinic at 30 weeks of gestation. During examination, which of the following cardiovascular findings would not be a cause for alarm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‌3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"S‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fixed s‌plit S2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pericard‌ial knock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. S3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. S3</strong></p><p><strong>S3</strong> is <strong>normally heard</strong> during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The third heart sound (S3) occurs due to rapid diastolic filling in <strong>hypervolemic states</strong> such as pregnancy, anemia or thyrotoxicosis. Hypervolemia is a normal physiological change during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The auscultation of a fixed split S2, fourth heart sound (S4) or a pericardial knock are suggestive of cardiac pathology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5137","text":"Which of the following coa‌gulation factors are incr​eased in pregnancy? II VI‌I IX XI XIII","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following coagulation factors are increased in pregnancy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>II</li>\n<li>VII</li>\n<li>IX</li>\n<li>XI</li>\n<li>XIII</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3,‌ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3</strong></p><p>All <strong>clotting factors increase</strong> in pregnancy <strong>except</strong> factors<strong> XI</strong> and <strong>XIII</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pregnancy is a <strong>hypercoagulable</strong> state with physiological changes such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All <strong>clotting factors </strong>are<strong> increased</strong> (except factors XI and XIII)</li>\n<li><strong>Fibrinogen</strong> levels are also <strong>increased</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>viscosity</strong> of blood <strong>decreases</strong> due to hemodilution</li>\n<li>Clotting time and bleeding time is unaffected</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common cause of decreased platelet count in pregnancy is <strong>benign gestational thrombocytopenia</strong>. This is due to <strong>hemodilution</strong> and increased platelet consumption and the women are generally <strong>asymptomatic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9760","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‌ at 38 weeks of gestation​ presents to the antenata‌l clinic with complaints ​of dizziness on lying dow​n for the past one week. ​She feels better on sitti‌ng up or turning to the s​ide. What is the cause of​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 38 weeks of gestation presents to the antenatal clinic with complaints of dizziness on lying down for the past one week.  She feels better on sitting up or turning to the side.  What is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased abdominal pressu‌re","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior vena caval compre‌ssion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic co‌mpression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased uteroplacental b‌lood flow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior vena caval compression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior vena caval compression</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>supine hypotension syndrome</strong> due to <strong>inferior vena caval compression. </strong></p>\n<p>During<strong> late pregnancy</strong>, the <strong>gravid uterus</strong> produces a <strong>compression</strong> effect on the inferior vena cava (IVC) when the patient is in the <strong>supine position</strong>. To compensate, there will be an opening up of the collaterals of paravertebral and azygous veins.</p>\n<p>When collaterals fail to open, venous return of the heart is reduced leading to hypotension, tachycardia, and syncope. The <strong>normal blood pressure</strong> is quickly <strong>restored</strong> by turning the patient to <strong>lateral</strong> <strong>position</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0034","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding the sensit​ivity of uterine musculat‌ure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the sensitivity of uterine musculature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enhanced by ‌progesterone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibited ‌by estrogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enhanced by both estrogen ‌and progesterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enhanced by estrogen and i‌nhibited by progesterone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enhanced by estrogen and inhibited by progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enhanced by estrogen and inhibited by progesterone</strong></p><p>The sensitivity of uterine musculature is <strong>enhanced by estrogen</strong> and <strong>inhibited by progesterone</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Estrogen</strong> stimulates the<strong> synthesis of myometrial contractile proteins</strong> through cAMP. This increases the excitability of uterine muscles and the frequency of action potentials.</p>\n<p><strong>Progesterone</strong> <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>frequency</strong> and <strong>intensity</strong> of uterine contractions. This helps to prevent the expulsion of the implanted ovum. However, it<strong> increases</strong> the <strong>amplitude of contraction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6625","text":"A pregnant woman has cramp‌s in her legs. What will ​you suggest her to reliev‌e the pain?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman has cramps in her legs. What will you suggest her to relieve the pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extend knee and dorsiflex ‌foot","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Flex knee and dorsiflex fo‌ot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extend knee and plantar fl‌ex foot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flex knee and plantar flex‌ foot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extend knee and dorsiflex foot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extend knee and dorsiflex foot</strong></p><p><strong>Extension at</strong> the<strong> knee</strong> joint and <strong>dorsiflexion at </strong>the<strong> ankle</strong> joint helps in <strong>stretching the calf muscles</strong> which <strong>relieves the pain</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Leg cramps</strong> are minor ailments during pregnancy mostly due to a <strong>deficiency</strong> of diffusible serum <strong>calcium</strong> or <strong>elevation</strong> of serum <strong>phosphorus</strong>. <strong>Management</strong> includes <strong>massaging</strong> <strong>the leg</strong>, stretching exercises, application of local heat, and <strong>supplementation</strong> of vitamin B1 and <strong>calcium</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5164","text":"Theca lutein cysts are not‌ associated with which of​ the following conditions‌?","raw_text":"Theca lutein cysts are not associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-D allo-‌immunization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal ‌hydrops","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal p‌regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperprol‌actinemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperprolactinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperprolactinemia</strong></p><p><strong>Theca lutein cysts</strong> are not seen in hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p>Theca-lutein cysts are <strong>benign</strong> ovarian lesions resulting from <strong>exaggerated </strong>physiological <strong>follicle stimulation</strong>. They are usually associated with markedly <strong>elevated</strong> serum levels of <strong>hCG</strong>.</p>\n<p>Theca lutein cysts are found in association with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large placenta\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-D alloimmunization</strong></li>\n<li>Multifetal gestations</li>\n<li><strong>Fetal hydrops</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Gestational trophoblastic disease - Molar pregnancy</li>\n<li>Reduced hCG clearance - Chronic renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They may also be found in <strong>normal pregnancy</strong> due to an <strong>exaggerated response of the ovaries</strong> to normal circulating levels of hCG.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4283","text":"In which trimester does ac‌ute fatty liver manifest ​most commonly during preg‌nancy?","raw_text":"In which trimester does acute fatty liver manifest most commonly during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First t‌rimester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second t‌rimester","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third t‌rimester","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both (a)‌ and (b)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Third trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Third trimester</strong></p><p><strong>Acute fatty live</strong>r of pregnancy (AFLP) occurs most commonly in the <strong>third trimester</strong> or early postpartum period.</p>\n<p>Risk factors include <strong>Pre-eclampsia/HELLP</strong> syndrome, <strong>male</strong> fetus, <strong>previous</strong> history of <strong>AFLP,</strong> and <strong>multiple</strong> gestations.</p>\n<p><strong>AFLP</strong> (acute yellow atrophy or acute fatty metamorphosis) is the most common cause of <strong>acute liver failure</strong> in pregnancy. It usually occurs in the <strong>later stages of pregnancy, </strong>particularly in the <strong>third</strong> trimester. It occurs due to <strong>fetal deficiency </strong>of<strong> long-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase</strong> (LCHAD) or poor placental mitochondrial function.</p>\n<p>The patients present with persistent nausea and vomiting, <strong>progressive jaundice</strong>, and epigastric pain. Features like acute hemolysis, <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, hypertension, proteinuria, thrombocytopenia, and edema can also be present. Symptoms may progress to<strong> fulminant hepatic failure</strong> with encephalopathy, <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong>, and death.</p>\n<p>Liver function tests such as <strong>AST, ALT</strong>, and serum bilirubin levels are <strong>raised</strong>. <strong>Hypofibrinogenemia</strong>, <strong>hypocholesterolemia</strong>, and <strong>prolonged clotting time</strong> can be seen. An <strong>elevated d-dimer</strong> is seen. Microscopy reveals a characteristic<strong> microvesicular fatty infiltration</strong>.</p>\n<p>The condition worsens till delivery after which the hepatic dysfunction resolves. A <strong>trial of labor</strong> by induction or delivery by<strong> Cesarean</strong> is done. Intensive <strong>supportive measures</strong> including blood transfusion should be provided.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 281\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 281\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The clinical and laboratory findings of acute liver diseases in pregnancy are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 538px; width: 407px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 7.74133px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; text-align: center; height: 7.74133px;\"><strong>Disorders </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; text-align: center; height: 7.74133px;\"><strong>HELLP syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; text-align: center; height: 7.74133px;\"><strong>Obstetric cholestasis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; text-align: center; height: 7.74133px;\"><strong>Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; text-align: center; height: 7.74133px;\"><strong>Viral hepatitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 19px;\"><strong>Onset in pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">Mid to late</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">Late</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">Late</td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">Variable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 19px;\"><strong>Differentiating features </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Epigastric pain</strong>, hypertension,</p>\n<p>elevated <strong>indirect bilirubin </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Pruritus </strong> </p>\n<p>elevated <strong>direct bilirubin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">Signs of<strong> liver failure</strong> like <strong>hypoglycemia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Fever</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 23px;\"><strong>AST (U/L)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 23px; text-align: center;\"> ≤300</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 23px; text-align: center;\"> ≤200</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 23px; text-align: center;\"><strong>200-800</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 23px; text-align: center;\"><strong>2000+</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Bilirubin (mg/dL)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\">1-4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\">1-5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\"><strong>4-10</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\"><strong>5-20</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Platelets</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\">↓↓ </td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\">↓↓ </td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\">↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Fibrinogen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↓↓↓ </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 26px;\"><strong>D-dimer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Prothrombin time</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 26px; text-align: center;\"> ↑</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.0712px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Hemolysis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.8646px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"> ↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.3438px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>No</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.4826px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">↑↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 69.0451px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"> <strong>No</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The condition <strong>worsens</strong> till delivery after which the hepatic dysfunction resolves. Appropriate management is immediate termination of pregnancy irrespective of gestational age by induction or delivery by Cesarean (for obstetric reasons) is done. Intensive supportive measures including blood transfusion should be provided.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5129","text":"What does the term 'placen‌tal sign' denote?","raw_text":"What does the term 'placental sign' denote?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alteration of fetal heart ‌rate on pressing the head​ into the pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spotting on the expected d‌ate of period in early mo​nths of pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Permanent lengthening of t‌he cord in 3rd stage of l​abor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slight gush of bleeding in‌ 3rd stage of labor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spotting on the expected date of period in early months of pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spotting on the expected date of period in early months of pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Spotting</strong> on the expected date of menstruation in the <strong>early months</strong> of pregnancy is known as <strong>placental sign</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cyclic bleeding </strong>may occur<strong> up to 12 weeks </strong>of pregnancy until the decidual space is obliterated by the fusion of decidua parietalis with decidua capsularis. The bleeding is usually scanty and lasts for a shorter duration than in a regular menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>Option A: Slowing of the fetal heart rate (FHR) on pressing the head down into the pelvis is suggestive of <strong>placenta previa</strong> (especially of posterior type) and is called <strong>Stallworthy’s sign</strong>. The fetal heart rate recovers as soon as the pressure is released.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Permanent lengthening of the cord and slight gush of bleeding in 3rd stage of labor are suggestive of separation of the placenta.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"T3Swaw6m","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"6_sclera","title":"6.Sclera","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC6713","text":"What is the innermost laye​r of the sclera?","raw_text":"What is the innermost layer of the sclera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epis​clera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamina​ fusca","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bruch’s ​membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior pigmented epithe​lium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lamina fusca","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lamina fusca</strong></p><p><strong>Lamina fusca</strong> is the innermost layer of the sclera.</p>\n<p>Lamina fusca faces the choroid. It is the transition zone between the sclera and the choroid. It consists of a delicate meshwork of elastic fibres, fibrocytes and <strong>melanocytes</strong>, traversed by long posterior ciliary nerves and vessels.</p>\n<p>Layers of the sclera from outside to inside include <strong>episclera</strong>, <strong>sclera proper</strong>,<strong> </strong>and <strong>lamina fusca</strong>, in that order. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1023","text":"The sclera is the thinnest​ at which of the followin​g locations?","raw_text":"The sclera is the thinnest at which of the following locations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At the​ limbus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At the ​equator","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior to attachment of ​the superior rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior to attachment of​ the superior rectus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior to attachment of the superior rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior to attachment of the superior rectus</strong></p><p>The sclera is the thinnest <strong>posterior</strong> to the<strong> insertion </strong>of the <strong>rectus</strong> muscles.</p>\n<p>The sclera is <strong>1mm</strong> thick posteriorly, with maximum thickness around the optic nerve, <strong>0.66 mm</strong> at the insertions of the rectus muscles and <strong>0.33 mm</strong> beneath the rectus muscles and thinnest behind their insertions</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6711","text":"A patient sustained a seve​re blow to the right eye ​from the lateral side dur‌ing a fistfight. The eyeb​all is soft to touch and ‍a rupture of the sclera i‍s suspected. What is the ​least likely site of rupt​ure?","raw_text":"A patient sustained a severe blow to the right eye from the lateral side during a fistfight. The eyeball is soft to touch and a rupture of the sclera is suspected. What is the least likely site of rupture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insertion​ of recti","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Equ​ator","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial​ limbus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral​ limbus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral limbus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral limbus</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral limbus</strong> is the least likely site to be involved in a <strong>traumatic scleral rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p>Traumatic scleral rupture, or <strong>globe rupture</strong>, occurs after blunt-force trauma. It usually occurs due to the <strong>compression force</strong> of the trauma. It therefore likely occurs at the insertion of rectus muscles, at the equator, or in an area parallel to the limbus <strong>opposite</strong> from the <strong>site </strong>of the<strong> impact</strong>. Rupture at the site of trauma, in this case lateral limbus, is rare. </p>\n<p>The major signs of scleral rupture are conjunctival edema and subconjunctival hemorrhage, hyphema, ocular hypotony, impairment or dislocation of the lens, vitreous hemorrhage, and retinal detachment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6712","text":"What is the predominant ty​pe of collagen in the scl​era?","raw_text":"What is the predominant type of collagen in the sclera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collage​n type I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Collagen​ type II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Collagen​ type III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collagen​ type IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Collagen type I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Collagen type I</strong></p><p><strong>Collagen type I</strong> is the predominant type of collagen found in the sclera.</p>\n<p>Collagen forms 50 to 75% of the dry weight of the sclera. <strong>Type II</strong> is absent in the sclera. It is found in the <strong>vitreous body</strong> of the eye.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9248","text":"A patient with glaucoma pr​esents with a bulging cor​nea. What is the most lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with glaucoma presents with a bulging cornea. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerat​oconus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kerato​malacia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staph​yloma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Granular ​dystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Staphyloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8877874d09624b8ea81fbaa574549f29x1279x1404.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Staphyloma</strong></p><p>High intraocular pressure leads to the thinning of the outer tunics of the eyeball such as <strong>cornea</strong> and <strong>sclera</strong>, thereby resulting in their ectasia (bulging) which is known as <strong>staphyloma</strong>.<strong> </strong><strong>Ciliary </strong>and<strong> equatorial</strong> staphylomas are most commonly associated with <strong>glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Staphyloma </strong>is an abnormal protrusion of the <strong>uveal tissue</strong> through a weak and thin portion of the <strong>cornea</strong> or sclera. So, staphyloma is lined internally by uveal tissue and<strong> </strong>externally by weak cornea or sclera.</p>\n<p>Types of Staphyloma:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 833.5px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24.2847px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 24.2847px; width: 117px;\"><strong>Types</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"height: 24.2847px; width: 388px;\"><strong>Pathology</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"height: 24.2847px; width: 307.5px;\"><strong>Causes</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 117px;\">Anterior staphyloma</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 388px;\">Due to bulging of the pseudocornea, internally lined by the iris &amp; externally by the newly formed epithelium.</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px; width: 307.5px;\">Sloughing corneal ulcer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 142px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 142px; width: 117px;\">Intercalary staphyloma</td>\n<td style=\"height: 142px; width: 388px;\">Due to bulging of weakened limbal tissue at the limbus, internally lined by the root of iris &amp; the anterior most part of the ciliary body</td>\n<td style=\"height: 142px; width: 307.5px;\">\n<p>Developmental glaucoma </p>\n<p>Secondary angle closure glaucoma</p>\n<p>Healing of perforating injury </p>\n<p>Peripheral corneal ulcer</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 110px; width: 117px;\">Ciliary staphyloma </td>\n<td style=\"height: 110px; width: 388px;\">Due to bulging of the weakened sclera, internally lined by the ciliary body, can extend up to 8mm behind the limbus</td>\n<td style=\"height: 110px; width: 307.5px;\">\n<p>Scleritis  </p>\n<p>End stage glaucoma </p>\n<p>Perforating injuries</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 128px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 128px; width: 117px;\">Equatorial staphyloma</td>\n<td style=\"height: 128px; width: 388px;\">Due to bulging of sclera with choroidal lining, seen around 14 mm behind the limbus, in the equatorial region </td>\n<td style=\"height: 128px; width: 307.5px;\">\n<p>Scleritis </p>\n<p>Chronic uncontrolled glaucoma  </p>\n<p>Scleral degeneration in pathological myopia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 110px; width: 117px;\">Posterior staphyloma</td>\n<td style=\"height: 110px; width: 388px;\">Due to bulging of sclera lined by the choroid, seen behind the equator </td>\n<td style=\"height: 110px; width: 307.5px;\">\n<p>Posterior scleritis</p>\n<p>Pathological myopia </p>\n<p>Perforating injuries</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Keratoconus</strong> (Option A) is a progressive bilateral <strong>corneal ectatic</strong> disorder due to defective synthesis of mucopolysaccharide and collagen tissue. It usually starts at puberty and progresses slowly, not associated with risk factors like glaucoma. Therefore, we can rule out keratoconus in this patient. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1025","text":"Staphyloma involves ______​.","raw_text":"Staphyloma involves ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uveal tissue with conjunct​iva","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conjunctiva​ with cornea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Choroid w​ith retina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uveal tissue​ with cornea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Uveal tissue with cornea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f99b402d18314c91aa88be5fcce08143x1280x1404.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9942ab21e168487aad3fc1e91c32065dx668x361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Uveal tissue with cornea</strong></p><p><strong>Staphyloma</strong> (a bunch of grapes) is a clinical condition characterized by<strong> ectasia</strong> (dilation or bulging) of the outer coats (<strong>cornea</strong> or sclera or both) of the eye with the incarceration (trapping) of the <strong>uveal tissue</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the anatomical types of staphyloma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The above image shows an anterior staphyloma. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0965","text":"The ciliary staphyloma is ​due to which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"The ciliary staphyloma is due to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myo​pia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Choro​iditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iridoc​yclitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scle​ritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Scleritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2138e2e485748ae850e376c949e399ex452x294.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Scleritis</strong></p><p><strong>Ciliary staphyloma</strong> is due to <strong>scleritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ciliary staphyloma is the ectasia (bulging) of weak sclera lined by the ciliary body. This affects the <strong>ciliary zone</strong> that includes the region <strong>up</strong> to <strong>8 mm</strong> <strong>behind </strong>the <strong>limbus. </strong>It can be caused by scleritis, <strong>absolute glaucoma </strong>or<strong> t</strong><strong>rauma</strong> to the ciliary region of the eye. </p>\n<p>The image below shows ciliary staphyloma with scleritis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1027","text":"A 50-year-old female compl​aining of irritation in h​er left eye has the follo‌wing finding. Which of th​e following condition com‍monly causes this?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old female complaining of irritation in her left eye has the following finding. Which of the following condition commonly causes this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyarteri​tis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wegener's gr​anulomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Giant cell​ arteritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6fd7a7f6725f4b27af749ccb43371f7c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p>Irritation in the eye associated with areas of scleral thinning through which the uvea shines is suggestive of <strong>scleromalacia perforans. </strong>It is commonly associated with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Scleromalacia perforans is a specific type of <strong>progressive scleral thinning </strong>without inflammation. Despite the term 'scleromalacia perforans',<strong> perforation</strong> is extremely<strong> rare</strong> as the integrity of the sclera is maintained by a thin layer of fibrous tissue.</p>\n<p>Other collagen disorders associated with it are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wegener’s granulomatosis (option B)</li>\n<li>Polyarteritis nodosa (option A)</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Relapsing polychondritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1026","text":"The most common type of sc​leritis is ______.","raw_text":"The most common type of scleritis is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-necrotizing anterior s​cleritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Necrotizing anterior scler​itis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-necrotizing posterior ​scleritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizing posterior scle​ritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non-necrotizing anterior scleritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7a851aef15644f2a207978b4a8a09c9x475x319.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non-necrotizing anterior scleritis</strong></p><p><strong>Non-necrotizing anterior scleritis</strong> is the most common type of scleritis. </p>\n<p>Scleritis can be classified into anterior and posterior scleritis. <strong>Anterior scleritis</strong> affects the sclera in front of the equator, while <strong>posterior scleritis</strong> affects the sclera behind the equator of the eye. Anterior scleritis is further classified into the following types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Non-necrotizing</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Diffuse: The most common variety</li>\n<li>Nodular: Scleral nodules are present. <strong>Annular scleritis</strong> is a variant in which the scleral nodules are arranged in a ring around the limbus. </li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Necrotizing</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>With inflammation</li>\n<li>Without inflammation: Also known as <strong>scleromalacia perforans</strong>. It is seen in elderly females with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>anterior nodular scleritis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0964","text":"All the following statemen​ts are true for a 40-year​-old diagnosed with scler‌itis except-","raw_text":"All the following statements are true for a 40-year-old diagnosed with scleritis except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scleromalacia perforans is​ associated with systemic​ disease.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain is not a prominent fe​ature.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glaucoma ​may occur.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retinal detachment is a kn​own complication.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pain is not a prominent feature.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pain is not a prominent feature.</strong></p><p><strong>Pain</strong> is a prominent feature of <strong>scleritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scleritis<strong> </strong>is an <strong>inflammation</strong> of the sclera. Patients present with moderate to severe eye <strong>pain</strong>, redness, lacrimation and photophobia. <strong>Anterior scleritis</strong> affects the sclera in front of the equator and may lead to the following complications: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute infiltrative stromal keratitis</li>\n<li>Sclerosing keratitis</li>\n<li>Peripheral ulcerative keratitis</li>\n<li>Uveitis</li>\n<li><strong>Glaucoma</strong></li>\n<li>Hypotony (rarely phthisis)</li>\n<li>Perforation of the sclera</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posterior scleritis</strong> involves the sclera behind the equator and may lead to uveal effusion with choroidal detachment, disc edema, proptosis or<strong> exudative retinal detachment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Scleromalacia perforans </strong>is a type of<strong> anterior</strong> scleritis. It typically occurs in elderly women with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong> (systemic disease). There is ischemia and necrosis of the sclera; however, there is <strong>no inflammation</strong>.<strong> </strong>Clinically, it is a <strong>painless condition</strong> with a <strong>white eye</strong>. Areas of thinned out sclera through which the uvea shines are seen in such patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1028","text":"A 63-year-old woman presen​ted with a two-week histo​ry of gradually worsening‌ sharp and stabbing left ​eye pain with redness. Th‍e severe pain kept her aw‍akened during the night. ​On investigation, B-scan ​ultrasound shows the 'T' ‌sign due to fluid accumul​ation in sub-tenon space.​ What is the likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 63-year-old woman presented with a two-week history of gradually worsening sharp and stabbing left eye pain with redness. The severe pain kept her awakened during the night. On investigation, B-scan ultrasound shows the 'T' sign due to fluid accumulation in sub-tenon space. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic n​euritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior​ scleritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior ​scleritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endopht​halmitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior scleritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6818655b5e8a4a99935d7bb03225ec70x451x299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior scleritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of severe worsening eye pain with redness and a B-scan showing<strong> T sign</strong> due to fluid accumulation in sub-tenon space is suggestive of <strong>posterior scleritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Posterior scleritis is the inflammation involving the sclera behind the equator. <strong>Ultrasonography </strong>in posterior scleritis may show the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased scleral thickness</li>\n<li>Scleral nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Separation </strong>of <strong>tenon capsule</strong> from the sclera</li>\n<li>Disc oedema</li>\n<li>Choroidal folds</li>\n<li><strong>Retinal detachment</strong></li>\n<li>Fluid in the sub-tenon space may give a characteristic \"T\" sign. The <strong>stem</strong> of the \"T\" is formed by the<strong> optic nerve</strong> and the <strong>cross-bar</strong> by the <strong>fluid-containing gap</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a B-scan ocular ultrasound of the eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6715","text":"A patient develops surgica​lly induced necrotising s​cleritis following an ocu‌lar surgery. The patient ​most likely underwent any‍ of the following surgeri‍es except:","raw_text":"A patient develops  surgically induced necrotising scleritis following an ocular surgery. The patient most likely underwent any of the following surgeries except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pterygium​ excision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cataract​ surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trabecu​lectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tarso-frontalis sling surg​ery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tarso-frontalis sling surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tarso-frontalis sling surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Surgically induced necrotizing scleritis </strong>(SINS) may occur following any ocular surgery involving the creation of a scleral wound. <strong>Tarso-frontalis sling surgery</strong> is a lid surgery and <strong>does not</strong> involve the sclera.</p>\n<p>SINS is a condition of scleral inflammation and necrosis that may occur as a complication of the following surgeries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pterygium excision</li>\n<li><strong>Cataract surgery</strong> (especially with sclero-corneal tunnel)</li>\n<li>Trabeculectomy</li>\n<li>Vitreo-retinal surgeries</li>\n<li>Squint surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>SINS is more likely to develop in those with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>,<strong> </strong>connective tissue disorders or following intra-operative use of mitomycin C. It is treated with <strong>NSAIDS </strong>and systemic steroids.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0963","text":"As a first-year ophthalmol​ogy resident, you come ac​ross a child with the fol‌lowing presentation. Whic​h of the following disord‍ers is it associated with‍?","raw_text":"As a first-year ophthalmology resident, you come across a child with the following presentation. Which of the following disorders is it associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemochr​omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital ocular melanocy​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteogenesis imperfecta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99d11a7cdfac4917b45e2c9acbed3f78.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteogenesis imperfecta</strong></p><p>The child has <strong>blue sclera </strong>which is associated with <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Blue scleral discoloration is due to scleral thinning. The <strong>underlying uvea</strong> thus becomes prominent and <strong>visible</strong>. Osteogenesis imperfecta (types I and IIA) and <strong>Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</strong> (type VI) are the most commonly associated diseases. Both diseases are inherited disorders of collagen formation. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hemochromatosis</strong> occurs due to<strong> iron deposition</strong> in various tissues. Rusty-brown perilimbal conjunctiva and scleral discoloration are seen.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Congenital ocular melanocytosis</strong> occurs due to an increase in the number, size and pigmentation of melanocytes. Sclera is multifocal slate-grey pigmented.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong> may present with extraarticular manifestations such as <strong>secondary Sjögren’s syndrome</strong> where <strong>keratoconjunctivitis sicca</strong> can be seen. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6714","text":"The sclera is thick in all​ the following conditions​ except-","raw_text":"The sclera is thick in all the following conditions except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nanoph​thalmos","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phthisi​s bulbi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior​ scleritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupht​halmos","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Buphthalmos","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Buphthalmos</strong></p><p>The sclera is <strong>not thick</strong> in<strong> buphthalmos</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sclera is 1mm thick posteriorly, 0.66mm thick at the insertion of recti and 0.33mm thick beneath the recti. In buphthalmos, the sclera is stretched and thinned out, resulting in “<strong>blue sclera.</strong>” Buphthalmos refers to an enlarged eyeball due to <strong>congenital glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0962","text":"An intern comes across a 7​-year-old girl with the f​ollowing features. All th‌e following are component​s of her condition except‍-","raw_text":"An intern comes across a 7-year-old girl with the following features. All the following are components of her condition except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disc c​oloboma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac ​anomalies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemive​rtebrae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung hy​poplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lung hypoplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6878ae12e37484cbc2bf7e18f68cbab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lung hypoplasia</strong></p><p>The given scenario of a young girl with facial asymmetry, deviation of angle of the mouth, flat nasal bridge, preauricular tags and <strong>limbal dermoid </strong>(lenticular yellow tumour at the limbus) of the eye is suggestive of <strong>Goldenhar syndrome</strong>. <strong>Lung hypoplasia </strong>is not a component of this syndrome. </p>\n<p>Goldenhar syndrome or <strong>oculoauriculovertebral spectrum </strong>is a congenital developmental anamoly affecting structures derived from the first branchial arch. It has the following features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoplasia of the malar, maxillary, and mandibular regions</li>\n<li>Macrostomia and microtia</li>\n<li>Preauricular and facial skin tags</li>\n<li><strong>Hemivertebrae</strong> (usually cervical)</li>\n<li>Mental handicap</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac</strong>, renal, and central nervous system (CNS) anomalies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Ocular</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Dermoid</li>\n<li>Upper lid notching or coloboma</li>\n<li>Microphthalmos</li>\n<li><strong>Disc coloboma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dA13ZLnU","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"6_injuries_of_hand","title":"6.Injuries Of Hand","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC4465","text":"Identify the carpal bone t​hat is most commonly frac​tured after fall on an ou​tstretched hand.","raw_text":"Identify the carpal bone that is most commonly fractured after fall on an outstretched hand.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e929935065a4ecd9c44c5e8b761be66.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23e48c040f1741ee8cdddea7d88381a9x1280x1415.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>The <strong>scaphoid</strong> (labelled as <strong>A</strong>) is the <strong>most commonly fractured carpal bone</strong> after a <strong>fall on an outstretched hand</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is more susceptible to fracture because it <strong>lies obliquely</strong> across the two rows of carpal bones. It also <strong>lies along the line of loading</strong> (i.e, in the line of weight transmission) between the forearm and thumb. A combination of <strong>forced carpal movement</strong> and <strong>compression</strong>, such as in a <strong>fall on the dorsiflexed (outstretched) hand</strong>, exerts severe stress on the scaphoid making it liable to fracture.</p>\n<p>Due to its blood supply originating from the dorsal distal pole, the scaphoid's proximal pole possesses a poor blood flow, making it less prone to heal and resulting in non-union.<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/><br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7737","text":"A patient presented with s​welling and tenderness ov​er the anatomical snuffbo​x following an RTA. X-ray‍ was performed which show​ed the following finding.‍ Identify the false state​ment about this injury.","raw_text":"A patient presented with swelling and tenderness over the anatomical snuffbox following an RTA. X-ray was performed which showed the following finding. Identify the false statement about this injury.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oblique x-ray view is pref​erred in recent fractures​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malunion is a common compl​ication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cast is applied in glass-h​olding position","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herbert screw is used for ​internal fixation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malunion is a common complication","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f41cc0d8ff334b3e9f5ff404d0e777ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f52e790f6f8e4dab9ea257b8fc999aafx1280x1223.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malunion is a common complication</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation and the x-ray are suggestive of<strong> scaphoid</strong> <strong>fracture</strong>. <strong>Nonunion</strong> is a <strong>common complication</strong> of this condition, while malunion is rare.</p>\n<p>Scaphoid fracture characteristically presents with <strong>tenderness </strong>in the<strong> anatomical snuffbox</strong> following a fall on an outstretched hand.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent fractures</strong> are less likely to be visible on AP view x-ray, hence <strong>oblique x-ray view </strong>is preferred. Common <strong>complications</strong> of scaphoid fracture include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-union and delayed union</li>\n<li>Avascular necrosis</li>\n<li>Wrist osteoarthritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Undisplaced </strong>fractures - immobilization in a <strong>scaphoid cast</strong> applied in a <strong>glass-holding position</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Displaced </strong>fractures - open reduction and internal fixation using <strong>Herbert's screw</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows a Herbert screw. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2428","text":"Which of the following str​uctures is most likely to​ undergo avascular necros​is in patients with scaph‍oid fracture?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is most likely to undergo avascular necrosis in patients with scaphoid fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Waist of t​he scaphoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal fragment of the sca​phoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal pole of the scaphoi​d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal fragment of the s​caphoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proximal fragment of the scaphoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proximal fragment of the scaphoid</strong></p><p>The <strong>proximal fragment</strong> of the<strong> scaphoid</strong> undergoes<strong> avascular necrosis</strong> following a fracture.</p>\n<p>This is because the <strong>blood supply </strong>of the scaphoid is from its<strong> distal end</strong> <strong>to</strong> the <strong>proximal end</strong>. A fracture disrupts the blood supply to the proximal part.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2577","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about perilunat​e dislocation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about perilunate dislocation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lunate is dislocated anter​iorly but the rest of the​ carpals remains in posit​ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lunate remains in position​ but rest other dislocate​ dorsally","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Involves median nerve inju​ry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tavernier’s manoeuvre is u​sed for closed reduction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lunate is dislocated anteriorly but the rest of the carpals remains in position","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17e5ea0aa31f431fbdc0da6820aa23b6x1280x2089.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lunate is dislocated anteriorly but the rest of the carpals remains in position</strong></p><p>Among the given options, statement A is incorrect.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 836\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>fall on a dorsiflexed hand</strong> may tear the tough ligaments that normally bind the carpal bones, leading to dislocations. In <strong>peri-lunate dislocation,</strong> the<strong> l</strong><strong>unate remains </strong>in <strong>position</strong> in the lunate fossa of the distal radius but the <strong>rest </strong>of the<strong> carpals dislocate dorsally</strong>. It is frequently associated with <strong>compression of the median nerve</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Prompt reduction reduces swelling and potential damage to the median nerve. The <strong>Tavernier maneuver</strong> is the most commonly used method of closed reduction. It involves <strong>locking the capitate into the distal concavity of the lunate</strong>. If the injury cannot be reduced using this method, urgent reduction in the theater should be performed.</p>\n<p>In<strong> lunate dislocation</strong>, the <strong>lunate dislocates anteriorly</strong> while the <strong>rest of the carpal bones remain in place</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7751","text":"Terry-Thomas sign is most ​likely to be seen in pati​ents with which of the fo​llowing injuries?","raw_text":"Terry-Thomas sign is most likely to be seen in patients with which of the following injuries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scaphoid fracture at the w​rist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scapho-lunat​e instability","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radio-carpal​ instability","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal radio-ulnar joint i​nstability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scapho-lunate instability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c0ec4064847437eb896ff0a58f61c62x915x510.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scapho-lunate instability</strong></p><p><strong>Terry–Thomas sign</strong> is most likely to be seen in patients with <strong>scapholunate instability</strong>.</p>\n<p>It denotes the <strong>increased gap </strong>between the<strong> scaphoid </strong>and the <strong>lunate</strong>. A <strong>scapholunate gap</strong> of<strong> &gt;3 mm</strong> is suggestive of <strong>instability</strong>. It results from <strong>tearing </strong>of the<strong> scapholunate ligament</strong> following a fall on an outstretched hand. </p>\n<p>The given x-ray shows the Terry-Thomas sign. The sign is named after Terry Thomas, a British comedian with a visible gap between his two maxillary central incisors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7735","text":"A man came to the casualty​ with severe pain over hi​s thumb. He gave a histor​y of a fall during a foot‍ball match. X-ray done is​ given below. What is the‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man came to the casualty with severe pain over his thumb. He gave a history of a fall during a football match. X-ray done is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bennett's​ fracture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rolando's​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Boxer's ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chauffeur​ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bennett's fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6d6c50a747254d7f8971c387961d8f3e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bbc0fe1f62c4007ac2d602e59d29ee8x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bennett&#x27;s fracture</strong></p><p>The given scenario and radiograph point to a diagnosis of <strong>Bennett’s fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Bennett's fracture</strong> is an oblique, <strong>simple intra-articular</strong> fracture of the <strong>base </strong>of the<strong> first metacarpal bone</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given image shows Bennett's fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Rolando’s fracture</strong> is an <strong>intra-articular comminuted</strong> fracture of the <strong>base</strong> of the <strong>first metacarpal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Boxer's fracture </strong>is the fracture of the <strong>fifth metacarpal neck.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Chauffeur fracture </strong>is a fracture of the<strong> radial styloid process.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2571","text":"A Bennett fracture is diff​icult to maintain in a re​duced state due to the pu​ll of ________.","raw_text":"A Bennett fracture is difficult to maintain in a reduced state due to the pull of ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor poll​icis longus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adductor pol​licis brevis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor pol​licis longus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abductor pol​licis longus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abductor pollicis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3f99e27f0f14904ae7062e61930efd7x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abductor pollicis longus</strong></p><p><strong>Bennett's fracture</strong> is difficult to maintain in a reduced state due to the <strong>pull </strong>of the <strong>abductor pollicis longus tendon </strong>on the<strong> shaft</strong> of the <strong>first metacarpal</strong>. </p>\n<p>The small fractured fragment remains in contact with the trapezium but the rest of the metacarpal shaft is pulled proximally<strong> </strong>by the abductor pollicis longus tendon that is attached to its base.</p>\n<p>In contrast, the <strong>comminuted fracture line</strong> of <strong>Rolando's</strong> fracture <strong>prevents</strong> the <strong>pull</strong> of the <strong>abductor pollicis longus tendon</strong> from being exerted on the metacarpal shaft.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7745","text":"A young man presents to th​e casualty with pain over​ his hand. He hesitantly ​mentions that he punched ‍a wall in an anger outbur​st. A radiograph of his h‍and is shown below. Which​ of the following is fals‍e about this injury?","raw_text":"A young man presents to the casualty with pain over his hand. He hesitantly mentions that he punched a wall in an anger outburst. A radiograph of his hand is shown below. Which of the following is false about this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MCP joints are kept in ful​l flexion for safe immobi​lisation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strength of the grip is si​gnificantly affected","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an intra-articular f​racture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cast is applied in James p​osition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is an intra-articular fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3a3cd02e54004765b5c6e5be46e1bf5e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/360cfdacf2d14df7a9aa04bbbc7ece21x1280x1687.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba003074f70043128914c9fd6dbe1b3dx1280x1179.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f7eae1a8e3841de8eef05f35f1d1e33x1279x1300.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is an intra-articular fracture</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing an <strong>extra-articular</strong> fracture of the <strong>fifth metacarpal neck</strong> points to<strong> boxer's fracture</strong>. The <strong>strength of grip decreases</strong> in this fracture as the fourth and fifth fingers are responsible for the power grip of the hand.</p>\n<p>Boxer's fracture is caused by <strong>striking an object </strong>with a <strong>closed fist</strong>, as seen in boxing, or occasionally by a direct injury.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The POP cast is applied in </strong><strong>James' position</strong><strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">/the position of safe immobilization for </strong>metacarpal fractures.<strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> </strong><strong>Position of safe immobilization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wrist slightly extended</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>MCP</strong> joints in <strong>full flexion</strong> and the IP joints extended </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thumb in abduction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This prevents stiffness and contractures as the ligaments are at their longest in this position.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2575","text":"A radiograph of a patient ​with a history of injury ​to the hand is given belo​w. What is the likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A radiograph of a patient with a history of injury to the hand is given below.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barton’s​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chauffeur'​s fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Smith’s ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rolando's​ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chauffeur's fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/23d0947872ac46b1a19714a963a64359.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chauffeur&#x27;s fracture</strong></p><p>The given radiograph showing a <strong>fracture </strong>of the<strong> radial styloid process </strong>points to a diagnosis of <strong>Chauffeur's fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is caused either by direct trauma to the wrist or traumatic radial deviation of the wrist.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Barton's fracture </strong>is an <strong>intra-articula</strong>r fracture of the<strong> distal radius</strong> with <strong>volar subluxation</strong> of the <strong>wrist</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Smith's fracture</strong> is a<strong> distal radial</strong> fracture with associated <strong>volar angulation</strong> of the <strong>distal fracture fragment.</strong> It is also called reverse Colles fracture. </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Rolando’s</strong> fracture<strong> </strong>is an <strong>intra-articular comminuted</strong> fracture of the <strong>base </strong>of the <strong>first metacarpal.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7741","text":"A teenager sustained an in​jury to his middle finger​ while attempting to catc​h a basketball during a m‍atch. On examination, his​ finger appeared as shown‍ below. Which of the foll​owing is false regarding ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A teenager sustained an injury to his middle finger while attempting to catch a basketball during a match. On examination, his finger appeared as shown below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The extensor tendon is dis​rupted at the distal inte​rphalangeal joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Results from a forced exte​nsion of the fingertip","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Passive extension of the d​istal interphalangeal joi​nt is preserved","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsal hyperextension spli​nts are used in the treat​ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Results from a forced extension of the fingertip","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6665c3d288e54996a0c66353f49c13c3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/482890209e3b49d381be7dac5b2be453x1280x844.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5459f10401b4ff9ac6284c936578ce7x1280x1634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Results from a forced extension of the fingertip</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing a flexed fingertip are suggestive of a <strong>mallet finger</strong>. It results from sudden,<strong> forced flexion </strong>of the<strong> fingertip</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mallet finger is also known as <strong>baseball finger</strong>. It is caused by the <strong>disruption </strong>of the <strong>extensor tendon</strong> at the<strong> distal interphalangeal joint</strong>. This leads to unopposed action of flexor tendon resulting in a flexed fingertip. <strong>Passive extension</strong> of the distal interphalangeal joint is<strong> preserved</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Dorsal hyperextension splints</strong> are used for immobilization of the joint.</p>\n<p>The given image shows a hyperextension splint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7742","text":"A footballer came to the c​asualty in severe pain ov​er his index finger. He m​entioned that the pain st‍arted after he pulled his​ opponent's jersey during‍ the match. Avulsion of w​hich tendon from the dist‍al phalanx is most likely​ to occur in a patient wi‍th this condition?","raw_text":"A footballer came to the casualty in severe pain over his index finger. He mentioned that the pain started after he pulled his opponent's jersey during the match. Avulsion of which tendon from the distal phalanx is most likely to occur in a patient with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor dig​itorum tendon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum superfici​alis tendon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus​ tendon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor pollicis longus t​endon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flexor digitorum profundus tendon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flexor digitorum profundus tendon</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a <strong>jersey finger</strong>. Avulsion of the<strong> flexor digitorum profundus tendon</strong> from the distal phalanx results in this condition.</p>\n<p>This injury commonly occurs in <strong>rugby </strong>and<strong> football players</strong>. Patients are <strong>unable</strong> to<strong> flex </strong>the<strong> distal end </strong>of the<strong> finger</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B<strong>: Boutonniere’s deformity</strong> presents as a <strong>flexion deformity</strong> of the PIP joint and extension of the DIP joint. It occurs as a result of the <strong>rupture</strong> of the <strong>middle slip</strong> of the <strong>extensor tendon</strong> followed by the<strong> separation</strong> of the<strong> lateral slips</strong>. The head of the proximal phalanx thrusts through this gap like a button through a buttonhole.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7748","text":"A middle-aged man presente​d to the clinic with a hi​story of progressive wris​t pain. Examination revea‍led stiffness of the wris​t joint. A radiograph of ‍his wrist is shown below.​ What is your provisional‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented to the clinic with a history of progressive wrist pain. Examination revealed stiffness of the wrist joint. A radiograph of his wrist is shown below. What is your provisional diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sever ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keinbock'​s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kohler's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osgood-Schla​tter disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Keinbock's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/adc81749065e45c99f0caa50190b0c8f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Keinbock&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing an <strong>increased opacity</strong> of<strong> lunate</strong> due to interrupted blood supply is suggestive of <strong>Keinbock’s disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Keinbock's disease refers to the <strong>avascular necrosis</strong> of the <strong>lunate</strong> leading to progressive <strong>wrist pain</strong>. Patients present with <strong>tenderness</strong> over the<strong> lunate</strong> and <strong>stiffness </strong>of the <strong>wrist joint</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sever disease</strong> or calcaneus apophysitis refers to the <strong>inflammation</strong> of the <strong>calcaneus growth plate</strong> in growing children. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Kohler's disease </strong>refers to the <strong>avascular necrosis</strong> of the <strong>navicular </strong>bone. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Osgood-Schlatter disease</strong>/ tibial tubercle apophysitis refers to the <strong>inflammation </strong>of the<strong> patellar ligament</strong> at the <strong>tibial tuberosity</strong>. It is characterized by a<strong> painful swelling</strong> just below the knee. It worsens with activity and is relieved by rest.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cY8WCz_Z","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"6_facets_of_growth_and_development","title":"6.Facets Of Growth And Development","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MC1402","text":"Which of the following fet​al hormones does not play‍ any role in fetal growth‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following fetal hormones does not play any role in fetal growth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucoco​rticoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Growth ​hormone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Growth hormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Growth hormone</strong></p><p><strong>Growth hormone</strong> does not play any role in fetal growth, although it is present in high levels during the fetal period. However, it does have a role in the <strong>postnatal period</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Insulin</strong> and <strong>thyroxine</strong> hormones are required for normal growth and development during late gestation. <strong>Glucocorticoid</strong> has a role in the prepartum maturation of organs such as liver, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4766","text":"A child is brought to the ​pediatrician for vaccinat‍ion. The child is able to‍ speak in 2-3 word senten​ces and is able to make a‌ tower of 6 cubes. What i‌s the expected weight of ​the child, if his develop‌ment is normal and birth ​weight was 3 Kgs?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the pediatrician for vaccination. The child is able to speak in\t2-3 word sentences and is able to make a tower of 6 cubes. What is the expected weight of the child, if his development is normal and birth weight was 3 Kgs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 ​kgs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4 ​kgs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 ​kgs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10 ​kgs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 12 kgs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 12 kgs</strong></p><p>The child is able to make a tower of 6 cubes and speaks in 2-3 word sentences which indicates the child is of <strong>2 years</strong> of age. <strong>Birth</strong> <strong>weight</strong> <strong>quadruples</strong> by the age of <strong>2 years</strong>. Hence the child's weight should be 3 kgs x 4= 12 Kgs.</p>\n<p>Infants may <strong>lose up to 10%</strong> of their birth weight in the 1<sup>st</sup> week of life and regain it by their 2<sup>nd</sup> week.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1471","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head circumference should ​be measured within 24 hou‍rs after birth","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Head circumference equals ​chest circumference at 1 ‍year of age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A boys height at 2 and hal​f years is half of his ad‍ult height","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A girls height at 2 years ​is half of her adult heig‍ht","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Head circumference should be measured within 24 hours after birth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Head circumference should be measured within 24 hours after birth</strong></p><p>Head circumference <strong>should not be measured within 24 hours</strong> because the head is subjected to molding during delivery, which makes the measurements unreliable.</p>\n<p>The maximum circumference of the head from the <strong>occipital protuberance</strong> to the <strong>supraorbital ridges</strong> on the forehead is recorded using the cross-tape method.</p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Head circumference</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">At birth</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">33 to 35cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">3 m</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">40 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">6 m</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">43 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">1 year</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">46 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2 years</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">48 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">3 years</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">49 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">4 years</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">50 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">12 years</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">52 cm (adult size)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Head circumference increases by the following:\n<ul>\n<li>2 cm/month in first 3 months</li>\n<li>1 cm/month in next 3 months</li>\n<li>0.5 cm/month in next 6 months</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Head circumference is more than chest circumference by 2.5 cm at birth.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At birth: <strong>Head circumference</strong> <strong>&gt;</strong> <strong>Chest circumference</strong></li>\n<li>At 9-12 months: <strong>Head circumference = Chest circumference</strong></li>\n<li>After 1 year: Head circumference <strong>&lt;</strong> <strong>Chest circumference</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4768","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the eru‍ption of teeth is false? ‍The first primary tooth t​o erupt is the lower cent‌ral incisor All primary t‌eeth erupt earlier in the​ lower jaw than their cou‌nterparts in the upper ja​w. The first secondary to​oth to erupt is the first‍ molar. No teeth till 12 ‌months of age is called d​elayed eruption. Teething‌ is manifested as gingiva‌l hyperemia and sucking f​ingers.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements regarding the eruption of teeth is false?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first primary tooth to erupt is the lower central incisor</li>\n<li>All primary teeth erupt earlier in the lower jaw than their counterparts in the upper jaw.</li>\n<li>The first secondary tooth to erupt is the first molar.</li>\n<li>No teeth till 12 months of age is called delayed eruption.</li>\n<li>Teething is manifested as gingival hyperemia and sucking fingers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 o​nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9470b9aeec8d452fbf5f944196d7536cx460x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 4</strong></p><p>Primary teeth in the upper jaw erupt earlier than their counterpart in the lower jaw, <strong>except</strong> for lower central incisors and second molar. No primary teeth till the age of 13 months is called delayed eruption.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>As seen in the image below, the first primary tooth to erupt is the <strong>lower (mandibular) central incisor</strong>, at <strong>5-7 months</strong>. All primary teeth erupt by 1.5–2.5 years of age. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>first secondary tooth</strong> to erupt is the <strong>first molar</strong> (maxillary or mandibular), at <strong>6-7 years,</strong> followed by the lower central incisor.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of delayed eruption</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Idiopathic:</strong> Most common</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Hypoparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Familial</li>\n<li>Mechanical blockage of individual teeth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4771","text":"A 3-year-old boy presented​ to the clinic for a miss‍ed vaccination. His routi‍ne anthropometric measure​ments were noted. He was ‌found to be 90 cm tall wi‌th a lower segment of 45 ​cm. Which among the follo‌wing is the most likely d​iagnosis in this child?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy presented to the clinic for a missed vaccination. His routine anthropometric measurements were noted. He was found to be 90 cm tall with a lower segment of 45 cm. Which among the following is the most likely diagnosis in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital h​ypothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Achondr​oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ric​kets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spondyloepiphyseal dysplas​ia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff4b272b8f7744ad8515e1f3a41a04c2x1280x1269.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia</strong></p><p>The upper segment to lower segment (U/L) ratio in this child is 1. The <strong>U/L ratio is low</strong> for his age which makes <strong>spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia</strong> most likely among the given options.</p>\n<p>The lower segment is defined as the length from the symphysis pubis to the floor. The upper segment is measured by subtracting the lower segment from the height.</p>\n<p>The U/L ratio is</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1.7 at birth</li>\n<li>1.3 at 3 years</li>\n<li><strong>1 after 7 years</strong></li>\n<li>0.9 in adults</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>High U/L ratio:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rickets</li>\n<li>Achondroplasia</li>\n<li>Untreated congenital hypothyroidism </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Low U/L ratio:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia</li>\n<li>Vertebral anomalies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arm span:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Length between the tips of the middle finger of the outstretched hands is the arm span.</li>\n<li>It is shorter than the length by 2.5 cm at birth; it equals height at 11 y and thereafter is slightly greater than height (usually&lt;1 cm).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1583","text":"A team participating in an​ Under-14 national athlet‍ics sports tournament sus‍pects foul play by, a riv​al team member. He claims‌ to fit the criteria for ‌the age requirements for ​the tournament, but the o‌pposing team does not agr​ee. Which of the followin​g X-ray would be most use‍ful to verify the claims ‌of the player?","raw_text":"A team participating in an Under-14 national athletics sports tournament suspects foul play by, a rival team member. He claims to fit the criteria for the age requirements for the tournament, but the opposing team does not agree. Which of the following X-ray would be most useful to verify the claims of the player?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-ray of​ the hip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-ray of the hands and wri​st","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-ray of t​he shoulder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-ray of the elbow and hip​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-ray of the elbow and hip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-ray of the elbow and hip</strong></p><p>The X-ray of choice to determine the skeletal age of a 14-year-old boy is X-ray of the elbow and hip.</p>\n<p>The bone age of a child indicates his/her level of biological and structural maturity. Methods used for bone age estimation are <strong>Gruelisch–Pyle</strong> <strong>atlas</strong> (more common) and<strong> Tanner–Whitehouse</strong> method.</p>\n<table border=\"1\"><caption><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">Bone age estimation</span></caption>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>X-ray of choice</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">3 to 9 months</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">X-ray shoulder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">1 to 13 years</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Single film of X-ray hands and wrist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">12 to 14 years</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">X-ray elbow and hip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: There is overlap in the age ranges for X-ray of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1437","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head reaches 90% of adult ​size at 1 year","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphoid growth follows a ​sigmoid-shaped pattern","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perinatal period is betwee​n 28 weeks of gestation t‍o 7 days after birth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Growth of child is cephalo​caudal and distal to prox‍imal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Growth of child is cephalocaudal and distal to proximal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d73fb7ba5a414105bdc34404b50bee16x1280x1011.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Growth of child is cephalocaudal and distal to proximal</strong></p><p>Growth of child is cephalocaudal and distal to proximal. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">During fetal life, the growth of the head occurs before that of the neck, and arms grow before legs. The distal parts of the body such as hands increase in size before the upper arms.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 277px; width: 377px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186px; text-align: center; height: 38px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Growth</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; text-align: center; height: 38px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Development</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 47px;\">Denotes a net increase in size/mass of tissues</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 47px;\">Denotes maturation of functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 363px; height: 49px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Cephalocaudal</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Head ⇒ Neck ⇒ Trunk &amp; upper limbs ⇒ Lower limbs</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Distal </strong>⇒<strong> Proximal</strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hands increase in size before upper arms</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">\n<p> <strong>Proximal </strong>⇒<strong> Distal</strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hand use is learnt before the use of fingers</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Growth rate of different tissues:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brain growth</strong>: At age of 1 month, the head size is about<strong> 36%</strong> of the expected head size in adults. It <strong>doubles</strong> in the <strong>first year</strong> of life to <strong>72%</strong> and becomes <strong>83%</strong> of adult size at <strong>2 years</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphoid growth</strong>: It is prominent in mid-childhood. It peaks at 4–8 years and regresses subsequently.</li>\n<li><strong>Gonadal growth</strong>: It stays dormant during childhood and shows a rise with the onset of puberty.</li>\n<li><strong>Somatic growth</strong>: General body growth shows an <strong>S-shaped curve.</strong> It is rapid during the fetal period and the first 3–4 years. It is steady in the following period, followed by a growth spurt during puberty. Later, it slows down considerably.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>WHO defines the <strong>perinatal period</strong> from <strong>22 weeks of gestation</strong> (or ≥500 g) up to <strong>7 days after birth</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1570","text":"A young boy is brought to ​the clinician by his pare‍nts with complaints of im‍pulsive behavior and decr​eased levels of attention‌ at school and everyday a‌ctivities. The clinician ​is suspecting a case of A‌DHD. Which of the followi​ng is not included in the​ criteria for diagnosis o‍f ADHD?","raw_text":"A young boy is brought to the clinician by his parents with complaints of impulsive behavior and decreased levels of attention at school and everyday activities. The clinician is suspecting a case of ADHD. Which of the following is not included in the criteria for diagnosis of ADHD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms begin before 7 ye​ars of age","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Symptoms should be present​ for at least 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Should be reported by inde​pendent observers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Behaviour must be developm​entally inappropriate in ‍two or more settings","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Symptoms begin before 7 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Symptoms begin before 7 years of age</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attention-deficit hyperactive disorder (ADHD) must begin before <strong>12 years</strong> of age, according to DSM-5 criteria. It was 7 years as per the previous DSM-4 criteria.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 126px;\" width=\"922\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 912.386px;\"><strong>DSM-5 Diagnostic Criteria for attention-deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD)-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 912.386px;\">1. A persistent pattern of <strong>inattention</strong> and/or <strong>hyperactivity/impulsivity</strong> present for <strong>at least 6 months</strong>, that interferes with functioning or development.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 912.386px;\">2. Several inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms were present <strong>before 12 years of age</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 912.386px;\">3. Several inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive <strong>symptoms are present in 2 or more settings</strong> (e.g., at school [or work] or at home) and is documented independently.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 912.386px;\">4. There is clear evidence of <strong>clinically significant impairment in social, academic, or occupational functioning.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 912.386px;\"> 5. Symptoms do not occur exclusively during the course of schizophrenia, or another psychotic disorder, and are <strong>not better accounted for by another mental disorder</strong> (e.g., mood disorder, anxiety disorder, dissociative disorder, personality disorder, substance intoxication or withdrawal).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Treatment: Presynaptic <strong>dopaminergic agonists</strong>, commonly called <strong>psychostimulant medications</strong>, including <strong>methylphenidate, dexmethylphenidate</strong>, amphetamine, and various amphetamine and dextroamphetamine preparations are used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2897","text":"In the image given below, ​the weight and height of ‍a child are plotted again‍st his age in years. Iden​tify the correct statemen‌t.","raw_text":"In the image given below, the weight and height of a child are plotted against his age in years. Identify the correct statement.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"WHO growth charts used now​ are developed by Nationa‍l Center for Health Stati‍stics (NCHS).","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Short stature is defined a​s a height below 5th perc‍entile.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Puberty in this child will​ be normal.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone age of this child is ​less than chronological a‍ge.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bone age of this child is less than chronological age.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6485b97d846240f6a1d9eb5f6ae65e7f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bone age of this child is less than chronological age.</strong></p><p>Bone age of this child is less than chronological age, as seen in cases of constitutional delay.</p>\n<p>As seen in the graph, the child was short initially, but the final height is within normal range. Hence, this child has constitutional delay.</p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The WHO growth charts are based on <strong>Multicentre Growth Reference Study</strong> (MGRS). National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS) developed growth charts that were used in the past.</li>\n<li>Short stature is defined as a height <strong>below the third percentile</strong> or more than 2 standard deviations below the median height for age and gender (&lt;-2 SD) according to the population standard.</li>\n<li>Puberty will be delayed in this child.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1544","text":"Identify the correct state​ment from the following.","raw_text":"Identify the correct statement from the following.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Children with pica are at ​increased risk of megalob‍lastic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breath-holding spells are ​associated with hypertoni‍a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thumbsucking in a child is​ a way of discharging ten‍sion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bedwetting after a period ​of 3 months of dryness at‍ night is called secondar‍y enuresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thumbsucking in a child is a way of discharging tension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thumbsucking in a child is a way of discharging tension</strong></p><p>Thumbsucking in a child is a way of discharging tension. It is a self-limiting habit disorder, usually seen between 6 months to 2 years.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Pica</strong> is persistent ingestion of non-nutritive substances for at least 1 month. It hampers the development of the child and requires clinical attention. Children with pica are at an increased risk of lead poisoning, <strong>iron-deficiency anemia,</strong> and parasitic infections.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Breath-holding spells:</strong> It is preceded by a provocative event such as anger. Crying stops at full expiration. Child may have apnoea, cyanosis, pallor, <strong>hypotonia, </strong>or even loss of consciousness, and generalized tonic–clonic seizure (GTCS). Subtypes include cyanotic, pallid, and mixed episodes. Seizures and cardiac causes should be ruled out. Parents should be reassured that breath-holding spells are self-limiting.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Enuresis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the child has never been dry, it is called <strong>primary enuresis.</strong></li>\n<li>When the bedwetting starts after a minimum period of<strong> six months</strong> of dryness at night, it is termed as<strong> secondary enuresis</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nocturnal enuresis is caused due to maturational delay. In a normal individual, antidiuretic hormone (ADH) levels usually peak between 4 am and 8 am. This circadian rhythm is impaired in nocturnal enuresis, leading to bedwetting.</li>\n<li>Treatment - <strong>Behavioural therapy</strong> with positive reinforcement, <strong>bladder alarms,</strong> and <strong>desmopressin</strong> reserved for cases resistant to behavioural therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4773","text":"As part of the annual scho​ol health check-up camp, ‍the visual acuity of a 6-‍year-old girl is being te​sted by the optometrist. ‌What is the expected visu‌al acuity in this age?","raw_text":"As part of the annual school health check-up camp, the visual acuity of a 6-year-old girl is being tested by the optometrist. What is the expected visual acuity in this age?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20​/30","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20​/40","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20​/20","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"20​/50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20/20","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20/20</strong></p><p>According to Parson's diseases of the eye, visual acuity of 20/20 is attained by the age of <strong>6 years.</strong> Nelson's Textbook of Paediatrics mentions that by the age of 5 or 6 years most children attain 20/20 vision.</p>\n<p>Generally accepted visual acuity according to age:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visual acuity of 20/40 at 3 years of age</li>\n<li>Visual acuity of 20/30 at 4 years of age</li>\n<li>Visual acuity of 20/20 at 5 or 6 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4778","text":"A 3-year-old boy was broug​ht to the clinic by his m‍other who complains that ‍for the past few months h​e has developed body-rock‌ing movements and repeats‌ words he hears on the te​levision. He doesn’t resp‌ond immediately on callin​g his name and also makes​ limited eye contact duri‍ng interactions. Based on‌ clinical examination you​ suspect autism in the ch‌ild. Which of the followi‌ng screening tools can be​ used to detect the boy’s​ condition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy was brought to the clinic by his mother who complains that for the past few months he has developed body-rocking movements and repeats words he hears on the television. He doesn’t respond immediately on calling his name and also makes limited eye contact during interactions. Based on clinical examination you suspect autism in the child. Which of the following screening tools can be used to detect the boy’s condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conner ra​ting scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MC​HAT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CRA​FFT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EP​DS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MCHAT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MCHAT</strong></p><p>The symptoms are suggestive of<strong> Autism -</strong> which is a pervasive developmental disorder characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impairment in quality of social interaction</li>\n<li>Impairment in quality of communication</li>\n<li>Restricted, repetitive, and stereotyped patterns of behaviors and interests  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common symptoms of Autism Spectrum Disorder -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Repetition of set words and phrases (echolalia)</li>\n<li>Absent or delayed response to name being called, despite normal hearing</li>\n<li>Reduced or absent responsive social smiling</li>\n<li>Reduced or absent social use of eye contact </li>\n<li>Reduced social interactions </li>\n<li>Lacking the ability to initiate or sustain an interaction with peers or adults</li>\n<li>Stereotypic movements and repetitive behaviors may include hand flapping, finger movements, body rocking, and lunging</li>\n<li>Overly sensitive to noise, smells, or texture and may scream when they hear a siren or vacuum </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Modified Checklist for Autism in Toddlers (<strong>MCHAT</strong>) is a screening tool for the detection of <strong>autism.</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 454px;\"><caption><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">Screening Tests</span></caption>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 49px; width: 310px;\">\n<p><strong>Test</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 49px; width: 130px;\"><strong>Disorder</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 49px; width: 310px;\">\n<p><strong> </strong>Modified Checklist for Autism in Toddlers (<strong>MCHAT</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 49px; width: 130px;\">Autism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px; width: 310px;\">Conner Rating Scale</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px; width: 130px;\">Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 310px;\">\n<p>CRAFFT</p>\n<p>(Car, Relax, Alone, Forget, Friends, Trouble)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 130px;\">\n<p>Substance abuse</p>\n<p>in adolescents</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px; width: 310px;\">\n<p>Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px; width: 130px;\">\n<p>Postpartum</p>\n<p>depression</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1587","text":"A 17-year-old girl present​ed with primary amenorrho‍ea. On physical examinati‍on, her breast and areola​ are enlarged without sep‌aration of their contours‌. What is the sexual matu​rity rating in this girl?‌","raw_text":"A 17-year-old girl presented with primary amenorrhoea. On physical examination, her breast and areola are enlarged without separation of their contours. What is the sexual maturity rating in this girl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SMR s​tage 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SMR s​tage 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SMR s​tage 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"SMR s​tage 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. SMR stage 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. SMR stage 3</strong></p><p>Breast and areola enlargement without the separation of the contour is seen in sexual maturity rating (<strong>SMR</strong>)<strong> stage 3</strong>.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 669px;\" width=\"530\"><caption><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">Sexual Maturity Rating Stages in Females</span></caption>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 35px; width: 63.0312px;\">SMR Stage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 35px; width: 204.047px;\">PUBIC HAIR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 35px; width: 240.922px;\">BREASTS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 34px; width: 63.0312px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 34px; width: 204.047px;\">Preadolescent</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 34px; width: 240.922px;\">Preadolescent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 63.0312px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 204.047px;\">\n<p>sparse, lightly pigmented, straight,</p>\n<p>medial border of labia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 240.922px;\">\n<p>Breast and papilla elevated as a small mound;</p>\n<p>diameter of areola increased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 63.0312px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 204.047px;\">\n<p>Darker, beginning to curl,</p>\n<p>increased amount</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 240.922px;\">\n<p>Breast and areola enlarged;</p>\n<p>no contour separation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 63.0312px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 204.047px;\">\n<p>Coarse, curly, abundant,</p>\n<p>but less than that in adults</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 240.922px;\">Areola and papilla form a <strong>secondary mound</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 63.0312px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 204.047px;\">\n<p>Adult feminine triangle,</p>\n<p>spread to the medial surface of thighs</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 240.922px;\">\n<p>Mature, nipple projects,</p>\n<p>areola part of a general breast contour</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 771px;\" width=\"338\"><caption><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">Sexual Maturity Rating Stages in Males</span></caption>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 41px; width: 47.4844px;\">SMR STAGE</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 41px; width: 84.5938px;\"> PUBIC HAIR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 41px; width: 145.672px;\">PENIS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 41px; width: 97.875px;\">TESTES</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 44px; width: 47.4844px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 44px; width: 84.5938px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 44px; width: 145.672px;\">Preadolescent</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 44px; width: 97.875px;\">Preadolescent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 69px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 69px; width: 47.4844px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 69px; width: 84.5938px;\">\n<p>Scanty, long,</p>\n<p>slightly pigmented</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 69px; width: 145.672px;\">Minimal change/enlargement</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 69px; width: 97.875px;\">\n<p>Enlarged scrotum,</p>\n<p>pink, texture altered</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 64px; width: 47.4844px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 64px; width: 84.5938px;\">\n<p>Darker, starting to curl,</p>\n<p>small amount</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 64px; width: 145.672px;\">Lengthens</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 64px; width: 97.875px;\">Larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 47.4844px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 84.5938px;\">\n<p>Resembles adult-type but less quantity,</p>\n<p>coarse, curly</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 145.672px;\">\n<p>Larger;</p>\n<p>glans and breadth increase in size</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px; width: 97.875px;\">\n<p>Larger,</p>\n<p>scrotum dark</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 70px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 70px; width: 47.4844px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 70px; width: 84.5938px;\">\n<p>Adult distribution,</p>\n<p>spread to the medial surface of thighs</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 70px; width: 145.672px;\">Adult size</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 70px; width: 97.875px;\">Adult size </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1718","text":"A 14-year-old boy is conce​rned about the change in ‍colour of his scrotum sin‍ce the last few weeks. On​ examination, the boy has‌ a dark scrotum with norm‌al-sized testis, with coa​rse and curly pubic hair.‌ What is the sexual matur​ity rating of this boy?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy is concerned about the change in colour of his scrotum since the last few weeks. On examination, the boy has a dark scrotum with normal-sized testis,  with coarse and curly pubic hair. What is the sexual maturity rating of this boy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta​ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta​ge 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sta​ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta​ge 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage 4</strong></p><p>The stage at which the pubic hair is coarse, curly and scrotum become dark is SMR stage 4.</p>\n<p>The first visible sign of puberty in males is<strong> testicular enlargement</strong> at 9.5 years, which is a hallmark of SMR stage 2. <span class=\"fontstyle0\">This is followed by penile growth during SMR 3.</span></p>\n<table border=\"1\"><caption><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">Sexual Maturity Rating Stages in Males</span></caption>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px;\">SMR STAGE</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px;\"> PUBIC HAIR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px;\">PENIS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 30px;\">  TESTES</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 51px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 51px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Preadolescent</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 51px;\">Preadolescent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Scanty, long,</p>\n<p>slightly pigmented</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">Minimal change/enlargement</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Enlarged scrotum,</p>\n<p>pink, texture altered</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Darker, starting to curl,</p>\n<p>small amount</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">Lengthens</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">Larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Resembles adult type, but less quantity;</p>\n<p>coarse, curly</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Larger;</p>\n<p>glans and breadth increase in size</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">Larger, scrotum dark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Adult distribution;</p>\n<p>spread to the medial surface of thighs</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">Adult size</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">Adult size </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1800","text":"Identify the correct state​ment.","raw_text":"Identify the correct statement.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macrocephaly is defined as​ an occipitofrontal circu‍mference greater than 3 S‍D above the mean for age ​and sex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monosomy 1p36 is associate​d with macrocephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microcephaly is defined as​ an occipitofrontal circu‍mference more than 2 SD b‍elow the mean for age and​ sex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infants with benign famili​al megalencephaly have no‍rmal head circumference a‍t birth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Microcephaly is defined as an occipitofrontal circumference more than 2 SD below the mean for age and sex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Microcephaly is defined as an occipitofrontal circumference more than 2 SD below the mean for age and sex</strong></p><p><strong>Microcephaly</strong> is defined as an occipitofrontal circumference <strong>more than 2 SD below the mean for age and sex</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Macrocephaly</strong> is defined as an occipitofrontal circumference <strong>more than 2 SD above the mean for age and sex.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Monosomy 1p36</strong> is associated with <strong>microcephaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infants with benign familial megalencephaly have increased head circumference at birth.</p>\n<p><strong>Important causes of macrocephaly:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Megalencephaly</strong> (enlargement of the brain parenchyma)\n<ul>\n<li>Benign familial</li>\n<li>Neurocutaneous syndromes such as neurofibromatosis, tuberous sclerosis</li>\n<li>Lysosomal storage diseases: Tay–Sachs, mucopolysaccharidosis, gangliosidosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Increased cerebrospinal fluid\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Benign enlargement of subarachnoid space</li>\n<li>Hydranencephaly (absence of cerebral hemispheres; the cranial cavity is filled with cerebrospinal fluid [CSF])</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Enlarged vascular compartment\n<ul>\n<li>Arteriovenous malformation</li>\n<li>Subdural, epidural, subarachnoid, or intraventricular hemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Increase in the bony compartment\n<ul>\n<li>Bone disease: Achondroplasia, osteogenesis imperfecta</li>\n<li>Bone marrow expansion: Thalassemia major</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous causes\n<ul>\n<li>Intracranial mass lesions: Cyst, abscess, or tumor</li>\n<li>Raised intracranial pressure: Idiopathic pseudotumor cerebri</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2695","text":"A mother brings her baby o​f 14 months to the clinic‍ after being concerned ab‍out the shape of the baby​’s head. You are suspecti‌ng a case of craniosynost‌osis. The premature fusio​n of which of the followi‌ng suture is seen in this​ child?","raw_text":"A mother brings her baby of 14 months to the clinic after being concerned about the shape of the baby’s head. You are suspecting a case of craniosynostosis. The premature fusion of which of the following suture is seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metopic​ suture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Both coron​al sutures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lambdoi​d suture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sagitta​l suture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lambdoid suture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/388d215011e040fc8a5a62c85551d70c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ceb596a275254eb0ae98614636e5986fx1280x2288.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lambdoid suture</strong></p><p>The picture is suggestive of a child with left<strong> posterior plagiocephaly</strong> (as the left side of the head is flattened) which involves the premature fusion of the left<strong> lambdoid</strong> suture.</p>\n<p>In the case of a <strong>left anterior plagiocephaly,</strong> the premature fusion of the left <strong>coronal</strong> suture is seen.</p>\n<p>Other deformities of the skull:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 499px;\" width=\"347\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Deformity</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Suture involved</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\">Anterior Plagiocephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\">Respective side coronal suture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\">Posterior Plagiocephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\">Respective side lambdoid suture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\">Deformation Plagiocephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\">Positional skull flattening (without suture fusion)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\">Brachycephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\">Bilateral Coronal suture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\">Trigonocephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\">Metopic suture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.484px; text-align: center;\">Scaphocephaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.516px; text-align: center;\">Saggital suture</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Craniosynostosis</strong> consists of premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures, resulting in an abnormal head shape.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary craniosynostosis: It results from a primary defect of ossification.</li>\n<li>Secondary craniosynostosis: It results from the failure of brain growth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1584","text":"A 7-year-old boy was found​ to have a height for age‍ 2 standard deviations be‍low the median. Furthermo​re, his bone age was foun‌d to be equal to chronolo‌gical age. Which of the f​ollowing is most likely?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy was found to have a height for age 2 standard deviations below the median. Furthermore, his bone age was found to be equal to chronological age. Which of the following is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Familial sh​ort stature","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Constituti​onal delay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Undernu​trition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Growth hormo​ne deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Familial short stature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Familial short stature</strong></p><p><strong>Familial short stature</strong> is most likely as<strong> </strong>bone age is equal to chronological age.</p>\n<p>Bone age is more than chronological age in precocious puberty.</p>\n<p>Bone age is less than chronological age in all other causes of short stature such as constitutional delay, undernutrition, genetic diseases, and endocrine causes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"A5495ENV","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"6_tissue_repair","title":"6.Tissue Repair","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA5621","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures lack labile cells​?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures lack labile cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vag‌ina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salivar‌y gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ute‌rus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Liver</strong></p><p>The <strong>liver</strong> contains <strong>stable cells</strong>, not labile cells.</p>\n<p>Based on the regenerative capacity of cells, tissues are classified into 3 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Labile tissues</li>\n<li>Stable tissues</li>\n<li>Permanent tissues</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Labile cells</strong> refer to <strong>continuously proliferating</strong> cells that are lost and replaced by the <strong>maturation</strong> of<strong> stem cells</strong> and the proliferation of these mature cells. Tissues containing these cells can readily <strong>regenerate</strong> after an <strong>injury</strong> as long as the pool of stem cells is preserved.</p>\n<p>Some examples of labile cells include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hematopoietic cells</strong> in the bone marrow</li>\n<li>Majority of <strong>surface epithelia </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Stratified squamous epithelia of the skin, oral cavity, vagina, and cervix</li>\n<li>Cuboidal epithelia of the ducts draining exocrine organs (salivary glands, biliary tract)</li>\n<li>Columnar epithelium of the GIT, uterus, and fallopian tubes</li>\n<li>Transitional epithelium of the urinary tract</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5622","text":"Which of the following tis‌sues does not consist of ​stable cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following tissues does not consist of stable cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Smooth‌ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac‌ muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardiac muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardiac muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiac muscle</strong> does not contain stable cells. It contains <strong>permanent</strong> <strong>cells </strong>which are terminally differentiated and <strong>non-proliferative</strong>.</p>\n<p>Based on the regenerative capacity of cells, tissues are classified into 3 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Labile tissues</li>\n<li>Stable tissues</li>\n<li>Permanent tissues</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stable</strong> cells are<strong> quiescent</strong> (in the G<sub>0</sub> stage of the cell cycle) and have only <strong>minimal proliferative</strong> activity in their normal state. However, they can divide in <strong>response</strong> <strong>to</strong> <strong>injury</strong> or loss of tissue mass.</p>\n<p>They include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parenchyma of <strong>solid tissues</strong> (liver, kidney, and pancreas)</li>\n<li>Endothelial cells</li>\n<li>Fibroblasts</li>\n<li><strong>Smooth muscle</strong> cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5623","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not contain permanent c​ells?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not contain permanent cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neu‌rons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardia‌c cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smooth‌ muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Smooth muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Smooth muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Smooth muscle</strong> does not contain permanent cells. It consists of <strong>stable cells</strong> that are capable of dividing in response to injury or loss of tissue mass.</p>\n<p>Based on the regenerative capacity of cells, tissues are classified into 3 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Labile tissues</li>\n<li>Stable tissues</li>\n<li>Permanent tissues</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Permanent cells</strong> are those which are considered to be <strong>terminally differentiated</strong> and <strong>non-proliferative</strong> in postnatal life. In permanent tissues, the repair is typically dominated by scar formation. These cells include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurons</li>\n<li>Cardiac muscle  </li>\n<li>Skeletal muscle - contains <strong>satellite cells </strong>attached to the endomysial sheath which have some <strong>regenerative capacity</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5624","text":"Oval cells are seen in whi‌ch of the following?","raw_text":"Oval cells are seen in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ov‌ary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lu‌ng","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ey‌es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver</strong></p><p><strong>Oval cells</strong> refer to<strong> stem cells</strong> in the <strong>liver</strong>. They are located in the <strong>canals of Hering</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Regeneration of the liver occurs by two major mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Proliferation</strong> of remaining <strong>hepatocytes</strong> - occurs following<strong><strong> partial hepatectomy</strong></strong> (resection of up to 90% of the liver can be corrected by this mechanism) </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Repopulation from <strong>progenitor</strong> <strong>cells</strong> (oval cells) - occurs following chronic liver <strong>injury</strong> or <strong>inflammation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5625","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi​ng TGF-β?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding TGF-β?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It increases the synthesis‌ of collagen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It increases the degradati‌on of the extracellular m​atrix","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is involved in the deve‌lopment of fibrosis in so​lid organs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is an anti-inflammatory‌ cytokine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It increases the degradation of the extracellular matrix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It increases the degradation of the extracellular matrix</strong></p><p><strong>TGF-β</strong> (transforming growth factor-β) <strong>decreases </strong>the <strong>degradation</strong> of the<strong> extracellular matrix</strong> by inhibiting metalloproteinases.</p>\n<p>It is produced by most cells in <strong>granulation tissue</strong>, including alternatively activated <strong>macrophages</strong>.</p>\n<p>The functions of TGF-β include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimulates fibroblast migration and proliferation</li>\n<li><strong>Increases</strong> <strong>synthesis</strong> of <strong>collagen</strong> and <strong>fibronectin </strong>(connective tissue proteins)</li>\n<li>Decreased degradation of ECM by inhibiting metalloproteinases</li>\n<li>Involved in<strong> scar formation</strong> after injury</li>\n<li>Development of <strong>fibrosis</strong> in lung, liver, and kidneys following chronic inflammation</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-inflammatory</strong> action by inhibiting lymphocyte proliferation and activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5626","text":"All of the following delay‌ healing except:","raw_text":"All of the following delay healing except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucocortico‌id deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Local p‌ressure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Location ‌of injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin C ‌deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glucocorticoid deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glucocorticoid deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoid excess</strong> results in <strong>delayed healing</strong>, not glucocorticoid deficiency.</p>\n<p>Various factors affect wound healing including mechanical factors (<strong>pressure</strong> and <strong>torsion</strong> and site of injury) and <strong>location</strong> and <strong>extent</strong> of the injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin C</strong> helps in the activation of prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases which help in the <strong>hydroxylation</strong> of <strong>procollagen</strong>. As inadequately hydroxylated procollagen is vulnerable to enzymatic degradation, vitamin C plays an essential role in wound healing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5627","text":"In healing by primary inte‌ntion, by when do macroph​ages replace neutrophils ‍at the site of injury?","raw_text":"In healing by primary intention,  by when do macrophages replace neutrophils at the site of injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Da‌y 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Da‌y 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Da‌y 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Da‌y 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Day 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e80d22d9841492d9b1592030c18d277x1280x2562.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Day 3</strong></p><p>By <strong>day 3 </strong>of healing by primary intention, <strong>macrophages</strong> <strong>replace</strong> the <strong>neutrophils</strong> at the site of injury.</p>\n<p>The sequence of events in wound healing is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 127px; width: 522px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"><strong>Time after injury</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\"><strong>Events</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Within 24 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<p><strong>Neutrophils:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Migrate</strong> from the incision margin towards the fibrin clot</li>\n<li>Release <strong>proteolytic enzymes</strong> that begin to clear the debris</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Basal cells:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Located at the cut edge of the epidermis</li>\n<li>Show <strong>increased mitotic activity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">24 to 48 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<p><strong>Epithelial cells:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Migrate and <strong>proliferate</strong> from the wound edges towards the center </li>\n<li>Deposit basement membrane components </li>\n<li>Form a <strong>continuous</strong><strong> epithelial layer</strong> that closes the wound</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Day 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macrophages</strong> become predominant, with very few neutrophils </li>\n<li><strong>Granulation tissue</strong> progressively invades the incision space</li>\n<li><strong>Collagen fibers</strong> at the incision margins</li>\n<li>Increased thickness of the epithelial layer through continued <strong>epithelial</strong> <strong>proliferation</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Day 5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neovascularization</strong> reaches its <strong>peak</strong> as granulation tissue fills the incisional space</li>\n<li>As new vessels are<strong> leaky</strong>, new granulation tissue is often <strong>edematous</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">2nd week</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Continued collagen accumulation</li>\n<li><strong>Fibroblast proliferation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">End of the 1<sup>st</sup> month</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Scar contains cellular connective tissue</li>\n<li>Largely devoid of inflammatory cells</li>\n<li>Covered by an essentially <strong>normal epidermis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5628","text":"A 38-year-old man underwen‌t excision of a lipoma. T​he swelling had been exci‍sed with a clear linear i‌ncision and was sutured c‍leanly. In this wound, wh‍en would you expect peak ​neovascularization?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man underwent excision of a lipoma. The swelling had been excised with a clear linear incision and was sutured cleanly. In this wound, when would you expect peak neovascularization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Da‌y 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Da‌y 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Da‌y 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Da‌y 7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Day 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Day 5</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a clean <strong>surgical incision</strong> that is sutured is an example of healing by <strong>primary intention. </strong>In this type of healing, <strong>neovascularization </strong>reaches its <strong>peak</strong> by <strong>day 5</strong>, as granulation tissue fills the incisional space.</p>\n<p>These <strong>new vessels</strong> are <strong>leaky</strong>, allowing the passage of plasma proteins and fluid into the extravascular space. Hence, <strong>new granulation</strong> tissue is often<strong> edematous</strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5629","text":"A patient presented with a‌n abscess on his back. Se​ptic precautions were tak‍en and the abscess was dr‌ained. On follow up, a he‍althy scar was visible. W‍hat is the type of collag​en seen in the provisiona‍l matrix in this type of ‍healing?","raw_text":"A patient presented with an abscess on his back. Septic precautions were taken and the abscess was drained. On follow up, a healthy scar was visible. What is the type of collagen seen in the provisional matrix in this type of healing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type III</strong></p><p>The given scenario is an example of wound healing by <strong>secondary intention</strong>.<strong> </strong>The initial<strong> provisional matrix</strong> in this type of healing is composed of<strong> type III collagen, </strong>fibrin, and plasma fibronectin.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>later replaced</strong> by a matrix composed primarily of<strong> type I</strong> collagen.</p>\n<p>This type of healing occurs when cell or <strong>tissue loss</strong> is more <strong>extensive,</strong> such as in large wounds, abscesses, ulceration, and ischemic necrosis in parenchymal organs. The repair process involves a combination of <strong>regeneration</strong> and <strong>scarring.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5632","text":"The accumulation of excess‌ive amounts of collagen d​uring repair may give ris‍e to which of the followi‌ng?","raw_text":"The accumulation of excessive amounts of collagen during repair may give rise to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adherenc‌e of scar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dehisenc‌e of scar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contractu‌re of scar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrop‌hy of scar","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypertrophy of scar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b29bc175a964ad29590f477b227d795x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypertrophy of scar</strong></p><p>Accumulation of <strong>excessive</strong> <strong>amounts</strong> of <strong>collagen</strong> during repair leads to the formation of <strong>hypertrophic scar</strong>. When this scar tissue grows beyond the boundaries of the original wound, it is known as a <strong>keloid</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Collagen</strong> plays a role in the<strong> normal adherence</strong> of tissue edges at the site of injury. <strong>Contraction</strong> in the size of a wound is an important part of the normal healing process. An<strong> exaggeration</strong> of this process gives rise to <strong>contractures</strong>. They are particularly prone to develop on the palms, soles, anterior thorax and are commonly seen after serious <strong>burns</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Inadequate</strong> formation of <strong>granulation tissue</strong> or formation of a scar leads to <strong>wound dehiscence</strong> and ulceration.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a hypertrophic scar.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1592","text":"A 24-year-old man comes wi‌th the following findings​. He says that he cut him‍self while shaving 3 mont‌hs back. What is the diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man comes with the following findings. He says that he cut himself while shaving 3 months back. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exuberant ‌granulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertro‌phic scar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kel‌oid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyogenic ‌granuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Keloid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2f8b0657abd45e0b438b8d1c3d699d8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Keloid</strong></p><p>The history of a shaving cut and the image showing a large area of scar tissue are suggestive of a <strong>keloid</strong>.</p>\n<p>A keloid refers to <strong>excess accumulation</strong> of <strong>collagen</strong> at the scar site with growth of the scar tissue <strong>beyond</strong> the <strong>boundaries</strong> of the original wound. It does not regress.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Exuberant granulation</strong> or proud flesh refers to the protrusion of<strong> excessive</strong> amounts of <strong>granulation tissue</strong> above the level of the surrounding skin. This blocks re-epithelialization. </p>\n<p>Option B: The accumulation of<strong> excessive</strong> amounts of <strong>collagen</strong> may give rise to a raised scar known as a <strong>hypertrophic scar</strong>. However, it remains <strong>within the boundaries</strong> of the original scar.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pyogenic</strong> <strong>granuloma</strong> is a benign, <strong>vascular tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5633","text":"A patient presented to the‌ OPD with the following f​inding on her finger. She‍ says that she cut her fi‌nger while chopping veget‍ables 2 weeks ago. What i‍s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with the following finding on her finger. She says that she cut her finger while chopping vegetables 2 weeks ago. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kel‌oid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertro‌phic scar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exuberant ‌granulation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyogenic ‌granuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Exuberant granulation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3ea6ccd869224db8b44b0d6db85c7d8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Exuberant granulation</strong></p><p>The given scenario and clinical findings are suggestive of <strong>exuberant granulation.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a deviation in wound healing where<strong> excessive</strong> amounts of<strong> granulation tissue</strong> protrude above the level of the surrounding skin and <strong>blocks re-epithelialization</strong>. It is also known as <strong>proud flesh</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5634","text":"In postoperative patients,‌ what is the strength of ​the incision site in comp‍arison with normal skin f‌ollowing suture removal a‍t the end of 1 week?","raw_text":"In postoperative patients, what is the strength of the incision site in comparison with normal skin following suture removal at the end of 1 week?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10</strong></p><p>When sutures are removed, usually at <strong>1 week</strong>, the <strong>wound</strong> <strong>strength</strong> is approximately <strong>10%</strong> of that of normal skin.</p>\n<p>This increases rapidly over the next 4 weeks. Wound strength reaches approximately <strong>70 to 80% </strong>of normal by<strong> 3 months.</strong> Beyond this, there is not much improvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UQACWVWX","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"6_sympathomimetics","title":"6.Sympathomimetics","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB2955","text":"Which of the following inh‌ibits the rate-limiting e​nzyme in catecholamine sy​nthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following inhibits the rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyro‌sine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metyr‌osine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valbe‌nazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metyrosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57a5fe6f90964779a2fd3090f2b4ebcdx1280x2291.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metyrosine</strong></p><p>The <strong>rate-limiting enzyme</strong> in catecholamine synthesis is <strong>tyrosine hydroxylase</strong> and<strong> </strong>is inhibited by<strong> metyrosine </strong>(alpha-methyl-p-tyrosine).</p>\n<p>Tyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to dopa (rate-limiting step). Dopa is then converted to dopamine by dopa decarboxylase.</p>\n<p>Metyrosine is indicated preoperatively in pheochromocytoma and in inoperable cases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2965","text":"An asthmatic patient has b‌een taking a salbutamol i​nhaler to control his whe​ezing episodes. Which of ‌the following is not an e‍ffect seen on stimulation‌ of the receptor involved‌?","raw_text":"An asthmatic patient has been taking a salbutamol inhaler to control his wheezing episodes. Which of the following is not an effect seen on stimulation of the receptor involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition ‌of lipolysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased hepatic gluconeo‌genesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased muscle glycogeno‌lysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smooth muscl‌e relaxation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition of lipolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition of lipolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Salbutamol</strong> is a short-acting, selective <strong>β<sub>2</sub>-adrenergic receptor agonist </strong>used in the treatment of asthma and COPD.  <strong>β<sub>2</sub> receptors</strong> mediate the <strong>stimulation</strong> of <strong>lipolysis</strong> (not inhibition). </p>\n<p>Adrenergic control of adipose tissue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimulation of lipolysis - α<sub>1</sub>, β<sub>1</sub>, β<sub>2</sub>, and β<sub>3</sub> receptors</li>\n<li>Inhibition of lipolysis - α<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 491px; width: 460px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.359375px;\">Adrenergic receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.640625px;\">Actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.359375px;\">α<sub>1 </sub>receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.640625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Vasoconstriction of large arterioles</li>\n<li>Genito-urinary smooth muscle - contraction</li>\n<li>Uterine muscles contraction</li>\n<li>Mydriasis - by contraction of radial muscles of the iris</li>\n<li>Increase secretion of glands</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal relaxation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.359375px;\">α<sub>2 </sub>receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.640625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Autoregulatory in the sympathetic system</li>\n<li>Vasoconstriction of precapillary arterioles in skeletal muscles</li>\n<li>Decrease insulin synthesis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.359375px;\">β<sub>1 </sub>receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.640625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Increased rhythmicity and force of contraction of the heart</li>\n<li>Increased renin secretion - JG cells in the kidney</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.359375px;\"><strong>β</strong><sub><strong>2</strong> </sub><strong>receptor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.640625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Smooth muscle relaxation: vasodilation, <strong>bronchodilation</strong></li>\n<li>Uterine muscle relaxation</li>\n<li>Stimulation of <strong>lipolysis</strong></li>\n<li>Increased insulin secretion</li>\n<li>Increased hepatic <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong></li>\n<li>Increased muscle <strong>glycogenolysis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.359375px;\">β<sub>3 </sub>receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.640625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stimulation of lipolysis</li>\n<li>Relaxation of detrusor</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2970","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements most appropriatel​y describes the vasomotor​ reversal of Dale?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements most appropriately describes the vasomotor reversal of Dale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulation of alpha 1 fol‌lowed by stimulation of b​eta 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blockade of alpha 1 follow‌ed by stimulation of beta​ 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulation of alpha 1 fol‌lowed by blockade of beta​ 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulation of beta 1 foll‌owed by blockade of beta ​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Blockade of alpha 1 followed by stimulation of beta 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Blockade of alpha 1 followed by stimulation of beta 2</strong></p><p>Vasomotor reversal of Dale is a <strong>blockade</strong> of alpha <strong>(α<sub>1</sub>)</strong> followed by <strong>stimulation</strong> of beta <strong>(β<sub>2</sub>)</strong> receptors.</p>\n<p>Normally,<strong> </strong>a<strong> </strong>high dose of <strong>epinephrine</strong> injected produces an α<sub>1</sub>-mediated <strong>rise</strong> in BP <strong>followed by</strong> a β<sub>2</sub>-mediated <strong>fall</strong> in BP.</p>\n<p>Administration of <strong>epinephrine after </strong>administration of <strong>α-blockers</strong> produces <strong>only a fall</strong> in BP. This is because the α-mediated response is abolished and hence, only the β-mediated response is seen, resulting in a fall in BP. This phenomenon is called the <strong>vasomotor reversal of Dale. </strong>This was first demonstrated by Sir H.H.Dale.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2968","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs when administered woul​d lead to a biphasic reac​tion on blood pressure?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs when administered would lead to a biphasic reaction on blood pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dobut‌amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adrenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adrenaline</strong></p><p><strong>Biphasic reaction</strong> on blood pressure is seen with the administration of <strong>adrenaline</strong>. The BP initially rises followed by a fall in BP.</p>\n<p>Adrenaline acts on α<sub>1</sub>, α<sub>2</sub>, β<sub>1</sub>, β<sub>2</sub>, and β<sub>3</sub> receptors.</p>\n<p><strong>α<sub>1</sub></strong> - present in blood vessels - causes vasoconstriction -<strong> increases BP</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>β<sub>2</sub></strong> - present in blood vessels - causes vasodilatation - <strong>decreases BP</strong>.</p>\n<p>At <strong>higher concentrations</strong> of adrenaline, <strong>alpha</strong> action predominates and hence a <strong>rise in BP</strong> is seen. Therefore an increase in BP is seen with IV administration of adrenaline.</p>\n<p>Due to rapid metabolism and uptake, the concentration of adrenaline decreases, and the <strong>β<sub>2 </sub>action predominates</strong>. Hence a <strong>decrease in BP</strong> is seen. This is referred to as the biphasic reaction. </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 401px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.4375px;\">\n<p><strong>Adrenergic drug</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.5625px;\">\n<p><strong>Receptors it acts on</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.4375px;\">\n<p>Adrenaline</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.5625px;\">\n<p>α<sub>1</sub> + α<sub>2</sub> +β<sub>1</sub> + β<sub>2</sub> + weak β<sub>3</sub> action</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.4375px;\">\n<p>Noradrenaline</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.5625px;\">\n<p>α<sub>1</sub> +α<sub>2</sub> +β<sub>1</sub> + β<sub>3</sub> but<strong> no β<sub>2</sub> action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.4375px;\">\n<p>Isoprenaline</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.5625px;\">\n<p>β<sub>1</sub> + β<sub>2</sub> + β<sub>3</sub> but <strong>no α action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2967","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not increase with slow​ IV infusion of adrenalin​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not increase with slow IV infusion of adrenaline?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systolic bl‌ood pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diastolic bl‌ood pressure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mean bloo‌d pressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulse p‌ressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diastolic blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diastolic blood pressure</strong></p><p>Slow intravenous infusion of <strong>adrenaline</strong> results in a significant<strong> increase </strong>in <strong>systolic </strong>blood pressure with<strong> minimal effect</strong> on<strong> diastolic</strong> pressure.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mean </strong>blood pressure and<strong> pulse pressure increase.</strong> This is because of the increased systolic pressure coupled with decreased, unchanged, or an insignificant increase in diastolic pressure.</p>\n<p>At lower concentrations of adrenaline, vascular β<sub>2</sub> receptors are more sensitive than α receptors, hence peripheral resistance decreases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2971","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV noradrenaline increases‌ systolic and diastolic B​P and causes tachycardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IV adrenaline increases sy‌stolic, decreases diastol​ic BP and causes tachycar​dia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine increases periphe‌ral resistance and improv​es renal perfusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IV isoproterenol increases‌ systolic and decreases d​iastolic BP and causes ta​chycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IV noradrenaline increases systolic and diastolic BP and causes tachycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IV noradrenaline increases systolic and diastolic BP and causes tachycardia</strong></p><p>IV <strong>noradrenaline</strong> increases systolic and diastolic BP but is <strong>unlikely</strong> to cause <strong>tachycardia.</strong> </p>\n<p>IV noradrenaline acts on α<sub>1</sub>, α<sub>2</sub>, and β<sub>1</sub> receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>α<sub>1</sub>- vasoconstriction - Increases DBP.</li>\n<li>β<sub>1</sub> - increases heart rate and contractility - Increases SBP.</li>\n<li>This increases mean arterial pressure which causes a reflex vagal activity that decreases the heart rate leading to bradycardia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV adrenaline acts on α<sub>1</sub>, α<sub>2</sub>, β<sub>1, </sub>β<sub>2, </sub>and β<sub>3 </sub>receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>β<sub>1</sub><span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\"> - </span>increases SBP, tachycardia.</li>\n<li>DBP - either β<sub>2</sub> - decrease DBP, α<sub>1</sub> - increase DBP or α<sub>1</sub>+β<sub>2</sub> - no change. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dopamine acts on β<sub>1,</sub>α<sub>1, </sub>and D<sub>1</sub> receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>D<sub>1</sub> - low concentrations - causes vasodilatation thus improving renal perfusion </li>\n<li>β<sub>1- </sub>positive inotropic effect - increases SBP.</li>\n<li>α<sub>1 - </sub>higher infusion rates - causes vasoconstriction and increases peripheral resistance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV isoproterenol is a non-selective β receptor agonist:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>β<sub>1</sub>- increases SBP</li>\n<li>β<sub>2</sub>- vasodilatation - decreases DBP</li>\n<li>β<sub>1</sub>- tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2993","text":"A patient in low output ca‌rdiogenic shock is being ​treated with dobutamine. ​Which of the following is‌ true about the drug?","raw_text":"A patient in low output cardiogenic shock is being treated with dobutamine. Which of the following is true about the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dobutamine decreases perip‌heral resistance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acts on D1 and D2 receptor‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreases renal circulatio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has no effect on coronary ‌circulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dobutamine decreases peripheral resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dobutamine decreases peripheral resistance</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Dobutamine <strong>decreases</strong> <strong>peripheral resistance</strong> minimally.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Clinically,<strong> </strong>dobutamine<strong> increases cardiac output</strong> by selectively increasing myocardial contractility and<strong> promotes coronary vasodilation. </strong> This is associated with a mild decrease in total peripheral vascular resistance, which is mediated by the <strong>reflex withdrawal of sympathetic tone</strong> to the vasculature.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Dobutamine has a t<sub>1/2</sub> of 2 minutes. It resembles dopamine but <strong>no action</strong> is seen on <strong>dopamine receptors</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Renal circulation is variably affected depending upon the sympathetic tone of the patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3002","text":"During a rotation in the a‌cute medical ward, you ob​serve a patient in cardio​genic shock being started‌ on a low-dose dopamine i‍nfusion. Which of the fol‌lowing would you expect t‌o increase in this patien​t?","raw_text":"During a rotation in the acute medical ward, you observe a patient in cardiogenic shock being started on a low-dose dopamine infusion. Which of the following would you expect to increase in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac‌ output","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urine ‌output","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heart‌ rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood p‌ressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urine output","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urine output</strong></p><p>At <strong>lower doses </strong>(&lt; 2 μg/kg), dopamine only stimulates dopamine receptors in renal vasculature, causing <strong>increased urine output</strong>.</p>\n<p>As the dose increases (2-5 μg/kg), additional cardiac stimulant action (β<sub>1</sub>) is also seen.</p>\n<p>At very high doses (5-15 μg/kg), vasoconstriction (α<sub>1</sub>) is additionally seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3021","text":"A stress ECHO has to be do‌ne for a 56-year-old COPD​ patient with high suspic​ion of coronary artery di‌sease. Which of the follo‍wing drugs can be used to‌ perform this test?","raw_text":"A stress ECHO has to be done for a 56-year-old COPD patient with high suspicion of coronary artery disease. Which of the following drugs can be used to perform this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tha‌lium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dobut‌amine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dobutamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dobutamine</strong></p><p class=\"tx\"><strong>Dobutamine</strong> is used to perform a <strong>stress test</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"tx\">Stress echocardiography is primarily done for the evaluation of myocardial ischemia. The basic principle is to <strong>increase cardiac stress</strong> (either with exercise or with a drug) in a controlled fashion, to aid with the identification and<strong> localization of ischemic areas</strong> (by detecting new or worsening wall motion abnormalities).  </p>\n<p class=\"tx\">Performing stress echocardiography with a pharmacologic agent such as <strong>dobutamine</strong> allows for<strong> simulation</strong> of increased heart rate and <strong>increased myocardial physiologic demands</strong> in patients who may be unable to exercise due to musculoskeletal or pulmonary comorbidities.</p>\n<p class=\"tx\">Dobutamine causes cardiac stress by acting on β1-adrenergic receptors to increase cardiac contractility and heart rate, and on β2-adrenergic receptors to cause peripheral vasodilatation. It is <strong>preferred over vasodilators</strong> used in stress echo tests such as adenosine, dipyridamole, and regadenoson (adenosine derivative).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2996","text":"You notice fenoldopam ment‌ioned on the drug chart o​f an elderly male patient​ admitted in the ICU. Wha‌t condition is he most li‍kely being treated for?","raw_text":"You notice fenoldopam mentioned on the drug chart of an elderly male patient admitted in the ICU. What condition is he most likely being treated for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertensiv‌e emergency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uncontroll‌ed migraine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertensive emergency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertensive emergency</strong></p><p><strong>Fenoldopam</strong> is primarily indicated in the management of<strong> hypertensive emergencies</strong>. It is a D<sub>1</sub> agonist at peripheral D1 receptors and acts as a rapid-acting vasodilator.</p>\n<p>Drugs used in a hypertensive emergency are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nitroprusside</li>\n<li>Nicardipine</li>\n<li>Labetalol</li>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Esmolol</li>\n<li>Nitroglycerine</li>\n<li>Phentolamine</li>\n<li>Fenoldopam</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2978","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indirectly acting ​sympathomimetic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indirectly acting sympathomimetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methamp‌hetamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxymet‌azoline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amphe‌tamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyra‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oxymetazoline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oxymetazoline</strong></p><p><strong>Oxymetazoline</strong> is a <strong>direct-acting</strong> non-selective α<sub>1</sub> and α<sub>2</sub> receptor agonist.</p>\n<p>Amphetamine, methamphetamine, and tyramine are catecholamine releasing agents and are considered to be indirectly acting sympathomimetic agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2951","text":"Following spinal anesthesi‌a, the blood pressure of ​a patient started droppin​g. Your senior asks you t‌o administer a mixed-acti‍ng sympathomimetic. Which‌ of the following loaded ‌syringes will you pick?","raw_text":"Following spinal anesthesia, the blood pressure of a patient started dropping. Your senior asks you to administer a mixed-acting sympathomimetic. Which of the following loaded syringes will you pick?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dobut‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isopro‌terenol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ephe‌drine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ephedrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ephedrine</strong></p><p><strong>Ephedrine</strong> is a <strong>mixed</strong>-acting sympathomimetic agent which can be used to manage hypotension following spinal anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Drugs that release norepinephrine and also directly activate receptors are called mixed-acting sympathomimetics.</p>\n<p>Ephedrine has a dual action: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Releasing agent: releases neurotransmitters from nerve endings.</li>\n<li>Direct action on α<sub>1</sub>, α<sub>2</sub>, β<sub>1</sub>, and β<sub>2</sub> receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The adrenergic agents are classified into three sub-groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Directly acting agents:\n<ul>\n<li>Selective agents - phenylephrine (α<sub>1</sub>), clonidine (α<sub>2</sub>), <strong>dobutamine</strong> (β<sub>1</sub>), terbutaline (β<sub>2</sub>).</li>\n<li>Non-selective agents - oxymetazoline (α<sub>1</sub> + α<sub>2</sub>), <strong>isoproterenol</strong> (β<sub>1</sub> + β<sub>2</sub>), <strong>epinephrine</strong> (α<sub>1</sub> +α<sub>2</sub> +β<sub>1</sub> + β<sub>2</sub>) norepinephrine (α<sub>1</sub> +α<sub>2</sub> + β<sub>1</sub>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Mixed acting agents - ephedrine (α<sub>1</sub> +α<sub>2</sub> +β<sub>1</sub> + β<sub>2 </sub>+ catecholamine releasing agent)</li>\n<li>Indirectly acting agents:\n<ul>\n<li>Catecholamine releasing agents - amphetamine and tyramine</li>\n<li>Uptake inhibitor - cocaine</li>\n<li>MAO inhibitor - selegeline</li>\n<li>COMT inhibitor - entacapone</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3016","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about​ clonidine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about clonidine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts on alpha 2 adrener‌gic receptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can result in rebound hype‌rtension on abrupt withdr​awal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can produce CNS stimulatio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits ‌salivation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Can produce CNS stimulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Can produce CNS stimulation</strong></p><p>Clonidine is a <strong>centrally acting</strong> <strong>antihypertensive</strong> agent. It is <strong>not</strong> associated with <strong>CNS stimulation</strong>. </p>\n<p>It treats high blood pressure by stimulating <strong>presynaptic α<sub>2</sub> receptors</strong> in the vasomotor center in the brainstem. This binding has a <strong>sympatholytic</strong> <strong>effect</strong>, by <strong>suppressing the release</strong> of norepinephrine and other sympathomimetics.</p>\n<p>Option A - Clonidine is an <strong>alpha 2 receptor agonist</strong> that reduces central sympathetic outflow.</p>\n<p>Option B - Abrupt withdrawal after long-term therapy can result in <strong>rebound hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D - <strong>Dry mouth</strong> and <strong>sedation</strong> are the major side effects of clonidine. It can also cause <strong>impotency</strong> and <strong>constipation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3015","text":"You are administering clon‌idine intravenously to a ​patient. Which of the fol​lowing effects would you ‌observe?","raw_text":"You are administering clonidine intravenously to a patient. Which of the following effects would you observe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged rapid fall in bl‌ood pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transient rise in blood pr‌essure followed by a prol​onged fall","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transient fall in blood pr‌essure followed by a prol​onged rise","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged rapid rise in bl‌ood pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transient rise in blood pressure followed by a prolonged fall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transient rise in blood pressure followed by a prolonged fall</strong></p><p>Intravenous administration of <strong>clonidine</strong> results in a <strong>transient rise</strong> in blood pressure followed by a <strong>prolonged fall.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>α<sub>2 </sub></strong>receptors are present on the presynaptic as well as the postsynaptic ganglia. They are the <strong>autoreceptors</strong> of the sympathetic system.  </p>\n<p>Clonidine is a directly acting α<sub>2 </sub>agonist. When it activates the <strong>postsynaptic</strong> <strong>α<sub>2</sub></strong> receptors in <strong>vascular smooth muscle,</strong> there is a <strong>transient rise</strong> in the blood pressure. This is followed by activation of <strong>central</strong> <strong>α<sub>2</sub></strong> receptors, which results in <strong>decreases</strong> the central <strong>sympathetic tone</strong> and causes a <strong>prolonged fall</strong> in blood pressure.</p>\n<p>α<sub>2</sub> receptors are present postsynaptically in vascular walls and activation results in the same action as α<sub>1</sub> stimulation i.e., vasoconstriction. But the major receptor that is responsible for vasoconstriction in vascular walls is the postsynaptic α<sub>1</sub> receptor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3019","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is therapy with ​an adrenergic-agonist not​ useful?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is therapy with an adrenergic-agonist not useful?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50-year-old woman sufferin‌g from acute decompensate​d heart failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"35-year-old man in septic ‌shock with persistent hyp​otension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40-year-old woman with blo‌od pressure 160/100 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"55-year-old man with erect‌ile dysfunction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 55-year-old man with erectile dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 55-year-old man with erectile dysfunction</strong></p><p>Adrenergic agonists are not useful in the treatment of <strong>erectile dysfunction</strong>. Adrenergic antagonists (phentolamine) are used locally for vasodilation in erectile dysfunction as <strong>PIPE therapy</strong> (Phentolamine Induced Penile Erection).</p>\n<p>Adrenergic agonists are mainly <strong>sympathomimetic</strong> due to their action on peripheral vascular or post-synaptic alpha receptors. However, certain adrenergic agonists (like clonidine) act as a <strong>sympatholytic</strong> due to their predominant action on pre-synaptic α<sub>2</sub> receptors which inhibits the release of noradrenaline.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: In hypotensive states like shock and acute decompensated heart failure, short-term therapy with parenteral sympathomimetic drugs are indicated in treatment.</p>\n<p>Option C: Clonidine is an adrenergic agonist which acts on the pre-synaptic α<sub>2</sub> receptor and is used in the treatment of hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3013","text":"A 22-week pregnant woman p‌resents to the OPD for a ​routine check-up. On exam​ination, her BP is 150/10‌0 mmHg but she does not h‍ave a history of hyperten‌sion. You prescribe methy‌ldopa to treat her. On wh​ich of the following rece​ptors does this drug act?​","raw_text":"A 22-week pregnant woman presents to the OPD for a routine check-up. On examination, her BP is 150/100 mmHg but she does not have a history of hypertension. You prescribe methyldopa to treat her. On which of the following receptors does this drug act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α‌2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"α‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β‌1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. α2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. α2</strong></p><p>The given scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>gestational hypertension</strong>, for which <strong>methyldopa</strong> is used. It acts on <strong>α<sub>2</sub> receptors. </strong></p>\n<p>Methyldopa is a <strong>centrally acting α2-adrenergic receptor agonist </strong>that is converted into alpha-methyl-norepinephrine. It decreases central sympathetic outflow, decreasing systemic vascular resistance <strong>without decreasing cardiac output.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9277","text":"The following table gives ‌BP values before and afte​r administration of drug ​‘X’. What would be the pr‌obable mechanism of actio‍n of drug ‘X’?","raw_text":"The following table gives BP values before and after administration of drug ‘X’. What would be the probable mechanism of action of drug ‘X’?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1 agonist a‌nd β1 agonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M2 agonist a‌nd M3 agonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"α1 antagonist and β2 agoni‌st","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β1 agonist a‌nd β2 agonist","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. β1 agonist and β2 agonist","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9844bea46ae744c1b79b9692e7beff7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. β1 agonist and β2 agonist</strong></p><p>The probable mechanism of action of drug ‘X’ is <strong>β1</strong> and <strong>β2 agonism</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to the given table, systolic BP and heart rate is increased due to β1 agonistic effect, and diastolic BP is decreased by vasodilatation due to β2 action.</p>\n<p>Option A: Action on α1 causes ↑ DBP</p>\n<p>Option B: Action on M2 causes ↓ HR and action on M3 causes vasodilatation and reflex increase in HR. Hence there is no net effect on heart rate</p>\n<p>Option C: α1 antagonism causes vasodilatation and ↓ DBP. β2 action causes vasodilatation and DBP ↓</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3014","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an alpha adrenorecept​or agonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an alpha adrenoreceptor agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clon‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isopro‌terenol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Guan‌abenz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guanf‌acine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoproterenol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoproterenol</strong></p><p><strong>Isoproterenol</strong> is a non-selective <strong>β-receptor agonist</strong> with no action on α-receptors. </p>\n<p>Clonidine, guanabenz, and guanfacine are α<sub>2</sub> receptor agonists.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3006","text":"Why is salbutamol preferre‌d over adrenaline in the ​treatment of an asthmatic​ patient?","raw_text":"Why is salbutamol preferred over adrenaline in the treatment of an asthmatic patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β1 sele‌ctivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β2 sele‌ctivity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α1 sele‌ctivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β3 sele‌ctivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β2 selectivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β2 selectivity</strong></p><p><strong>Salbutamol</strong> is preferred over adrenaline in an asthmatic patient due to <strong>β<sub>2 </sub>selectivity.</strong></p>\n<p>This β<sub>2 </sub>selectivity is relative and lost at higher concentrations. Around 40% of the beta receptors are of β<sub>2</sub> type and their activation won't cause cardiac stimulation.</p>\n<p>Major adverse effects of <strong>non-selective β-agonists</strong> are attributed to the stimulation of β<sub>1</sub> receptors in the heart which result in <strong>tachyarrhythmias.</strong> Selective β<sub>2</sub> receptor agonists are introduced to overcome this major drawback.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3007","text":"A 56-year-old man diagnose‌d with chronic bronchitis​ was prescribed a long-ac​ting beta-2 agonist. Iden‌tify the drug.","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man diagnosed with chronic bronchitis was prescribed a long-acting beta-2 agonist. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albu‌terol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Formo‌terol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pirbu‌terol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orcipr‌enaline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Formoterol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Formoterol</strong></p><p><strong>Formoterol</strong> is a long-acting β<sub>2</sub> agonist <strong>(LABA).</strong></p>\n<p>β<sub>2</sub> agonists are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short-acting β<sub>2</sub> receptor agonists (SABA):\n<ul>\n<li>Albuterol (Salbutamol)</li>\n<li>Pirbuterol</li>\n<li>Orciprenaline</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Long-acting β<sub>2</sub> agonists (LABA):\n<ul>\n<li>Salmeterol</li>\n<li>Formoterol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Ultra-long acting β<sub>2</sub> agonists (VLABA):\n<ul>\n<li>Indacaterol</li>\n<li>Olodaterol</li>\n<li>Vilanterol</li>\n<li>Carmoterol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3008","text":"Which of the following bet‌a 2 receptor agonists was​ developed specifically a​s a uterine relaxant?","raw_text":"Which of the following beta 2 receptor agonists was developed specifically as a uterine relaxant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salbu‌tamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Terbu‌taline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rito‌drine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Orcipr‌enaline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ritodrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ritodrine</strong></p><p><strong>Ritodrine </strong>was developed specifically as a <strong>uterine relaxant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Salbutamol, orciprenaline, and terbutaline can also be used as uterine relaxants but were initially developed as inhalational agents for asthma. β<sub>2 </sub>agonists are associated with increased mortality and are rarely used as uterine relaxants now.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3010","text":"A child suffering from int‌ermittent bronchial asthm​a is being treated with β​2 agonists. Which of the ‌following side effects wo‍uld you not expect?","raw_text":"A child suffering from intermittent bronchial asthma is being treated with β2 agonists. Which of the following side effects would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tre‌mors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoglycemia</strong></p><p><strong>β<sub>2</sub> agonists </strong>are associated with <strong>hyperglycemia </strong>probably due to β<sub>2</sub> receptor-mediated <strong>glycogenolysis and glucagon release.</strong></p>\n<p>Adverse effects of beta 2 agonists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong> - direct effect on skeletal muscle uptake of K<sup>+</sup> ions</li>\n<li><strong>Tachycardia</strong> - reflex sympathetic stimulation due to vasodilator action on β2 receptors and direct effect on cardiac β1 receptors (β2 selectivity is lost at high concentrations) </li>\n<li>Muscle tremors - direct effect on β2 receptors in skeletal muscles and due to hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Hyperglycaemia</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Tolerance - desensitization on long-term use</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3011","text":"A 4-year-old child on salb‌utamol is brought to the ​hospital with complaints ​of tremors. Which of the ‌following effects of the ‍drug would explain this s‌ide effect? Direct action‌ on muscle spindles Hypok​alemia Hyperkalemia Direc​t action at the motor end​-plate Hypoglycemia","raw_text":"<p>A 4-year-old child on salbutamol is brought to the hospital with complaints of tremors. Which of the following effects of the drug would explain this side effect?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Direct action on muscle spindles</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Direct action at the motor end-plate</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 2</strong></p><p>Tremors due to β<sub>2</sub> agonists can be both due to<strong> direct action on muscle spindle</strong> and <strong>hypokalemia.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Initially, it was thought to be due to beta receptor-mediated enhancement of discharge of muscle spindles. However, recently the tremors have also been closely correlated with hypokalemia. Hence both direct action and hypokalemia can explain the tremors associated with β<sub>2</sub> agonist use, although the exact mechanism is still not known.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8703","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an adverse effect of ​salbutamol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an adverse effect of salbutamol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tole‌rance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypoglycemia</strong></p><p><strong>Salbutamol</strong> in large doses can result in <strong>hyperglycemia,</strong> not hypoglycemia.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tachycardia</strong> due to reflex sympathetic stimulation due to vasodilator action on β2 receptors and direct effect on cardiac β1 receptors (β2 selectivity is lost at high concentrations) </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tolerance</strong> occurs due to desensitization on long-term use </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hypokalemia</strong> due to direct effect on skeletal muscle uptake of K+ ions</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3020","text":"A 68-year-old man with Par‌kinson's disease was pres​cribed mirabegron to cont​rol his symptoms of urina‌ry urgency and incontinen‍ce. What is the mechanism‌ of action of this drug?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man with Parkinson's disease was prescribed mirabegron to control his symptoms of urinary urgency and incontinence. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β2 ag‌onist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β3 ag‌onist","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M3 receptor‌ antagonist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M1 receptor‌ antagonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β3 agonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β3 agonist</strong></p><p><strong>Mirabegron</strong> is a <strong>β<sub>3</sub> agonist </strong>used for the treatment of <strong>overactive bladder </strong>(OAB). Stimulation of β<sub>3</sub> receptors in detrusor muscle increases the bladder capacity and provides symptomatic relief in overactive bladder.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p id=\"__p7\">Antimuscarinic drugs are the mainstay of oral pharmacological treatment for OAB. It acts by inhibiting muscarinic receptor subtypes, <strong>M2 and M3 </strong>in the bladder. Both subtypes are expressed in multiple tissues, resulting in anticholinergic adverse events such as <strong>dry mouth</strong>, <strong>constipation,</strong> and <strong>cognitive impairment. </strong>These adverse effects are not seen with mirabegron as it acts via adrenergic receptors.</p>\n<p>Mirabegron is a moderate<strong> inhibitor</strong> of<strong> CYP2D6. </strong>Hence, caution has to be exercised when prescribing it along with CYP2D6 substrates such as metoprolol, desipramine, digoxin. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0141","text":"What dose of epinephrine i‌s used in anaphylactic sh​ock?","raw_text":"What dose of epinephrine is used in anaphylactic shock?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:10000 I.‌V. - 10 ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1:1000 I.M‌. - 0.5 ml","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1:1000 I.‌M. - 1 ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1:1000 I.V‌. - 0.5 ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1:1000 I.M. - 0.5 ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1:1000 I.M. - 0.5 ml</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Epinephrine (<strong>1:1000 solution</strong>) <strong>0.5 ml</strong> via<strong> intramuscular</strong> route is preferred in the treatment of <strong>anaphylactic shock.</strong></p>\n<p>For<strong> intravenous use,</strong> 1:1000 adrenaline must be further<strong> diluted to 1:10,000 </strong>and should be used very<strong> cautiously</strong> as<strong> slow bolus</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is a much greater risk of causing harmful side effects by either inappropriate dosage or misdiagnosis of anaphylaxis when using IV adrenaline. Hence, IM injection is preferred over intravenous (IV) bolus because it is faster in many situations and safer (lower risk of cardiovascular complications, such as severe hypertension and ventricular arrhythmias).</p>\n<p>For<strong> intramuscular, subcutaneous, and inhalational (nebulization) </strong>routes of administration,<strong> 1:1000</strong> dilution can be used. </p>\n<p>In <strong>cardiac arrest</strong>, the 1:1000 adrenaline must be diluted to 1:10,000 for IV use. The recommended dose of epinephrine is 1mg = 10 mL of 1:10,000 solution IV administered every 3 to 5 minutes during resuscitation as per the CPR guidelines.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine is also used in the treatment of hypotension, to induce local vasoconstriction to reduce the diffusion of local anaesthetics and to induce bronchodilation in asthma and COPD.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7320","text":"A patient was treated for ‌septicemia with norepinep​hrine infusion. Which of ​the following best explai‌ns the benefit of this dr‍ug?","raw_text":"A patient was treated for septicemia with norepinephrine infusion. Which of the following best explains the benefit of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brady‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increases u‌rine output","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increases mean arterial bl‌ood pressure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increases mean arterial blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increases mean arterial blood pressure</strong></p><p>The benefit of using <strong>noradrenaline</strong> in septicemia is that it <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>mean arterial blood pressure</strong>. It acts as a powerful peripheral vasoconstrictor and inotrope.</p>\n<p>Noradrenaline causes a rise in <strong>both systolic BP and diastolic BP</strong>, resulting in a rise in the mean arterial blood pressure. This increases the organ perfusion in patients in <strong>septic</strong> shock or <strong>cardiogenic shock. </strong></p>\n<p>Another advantage of noradrenaline is that it does so <strong>without causing tachycardia</strong>, which is often a troublesome event with other inotropes in critical care.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: The rise in mean arterial pressure causes reflex bradycardia which is not a major contributor to the treatment of septic shock.</p>\n<p>Option C: Low-dose dopamine infusions and furosemide are used to increase urine output in critically ill patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6676","text":"Match the following diluti‌on of adrenaline and its ​use: A. 1:1000 1. Cardiac​ arrest B. 1:10000 2. Ana‌phylaxis C. 1:100000 3. L‍ocal anesthesia","raw_text":"<p>Match the following dilution of adrenaline and its use:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 257.296875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">A. 1:1000 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.296875px;\">1. Cardiac arrest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">B. 1:10000 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.296875px;\">2. Anaphylaxis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">C. 1:100000  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.296875px;\">3. Local anesthesia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-1 B‌-2 C-3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-2 B‌-1 C-3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-3 B‌-2 C-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-1 B‌-3 C-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-2 B-1 C-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-2 B-1 C-3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Epinephrine (<strong>1:1000</strong> solution) 0.5 ml <strong>I.M.</strong> is preferred in the treatment of <strong>anaphylactic shock</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In <strong>cardiac arrest</strong>, the 1:1000 adrenaline must be diluted to <strong>1:10,000</strong> for<strong> IV</strong> route, and used cautiously.</p>\n<p>Adrenaline in a concentration of <strong>1: 50,000 to 1: 200,000</strong> is added to <strong>local anesthetics</strong> for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reducing the systemic absorption</li>\n<li>Prolonging the duration of action</li>\n<li>Providing a more bloodless field for surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>1:1000 dilution</strong> can be used for the following routes of administration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intramuscular</li>\n<li>Subcutaneous </li>\n<li>Inhalational (nebulization)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IM</strong> injection is <strong>preferred</strong> over intravenous (IV) bolus because it is <strong>faster</strong> in many situations and <strong>safer</strong> (ie, lower risk of cardiovascular complications, such as severe hypertension and ventricular arrhythmias). There is a much greater risk of causing harmful side effects by inappropriate dosage or misdiagnosis of anaphylaxis when using IV adrenaline.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine is also used in the treatment of hypotension, to induce local vasoconstriction to reduce the diffusion of local anaesthetics, to induce bronchodilation in asthma and COPD.</p>\n<p>Note<strong>: </strong>The recommended dose of epinephrine is 1mg = 10 mL of a 1:10,000 solution administered IV every 3 to 5 minutes during resuscitation as per the CPR guidelines. For nebulization, 1:1000 adrenaline is commonly used. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VPjCrl-l","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"6_physiology_of_nerve","title":"6.Physiology Of Nerve","num_questions":34,"total_marks":136,"duration":44,"time_per_question":77.65,"questions":[{"id":"MA8353","text":"Which among the following ​is embryologically and ph‍ysiologically unrelated t‍o other neural cells?","raw_text":"Which among the following is embryologically and physiologically unrelated to other neural cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro​cytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schwan​n Cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligode​ndrocyte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Micr​oglia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Microglia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d832170f82f84c0f86083538bda533bdx1280x1191.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Microglia</strong></p><p><strong>Microglia</strong> are physiologically and embryologically unrelated to other neural cell types. They arise from<strong> macrophages</strong> outside the nervous system.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8355","text":"In which of the following ​areas is a pseudo-unipola‍r type of neuron present?‍","raw_text":"In which of the following areas is a pseudo-unipolar type of neuron present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal ro​ot ganglia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sympathet​ic ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parasympath​etic ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cranial ne​rve nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal root ganglia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal root ganglia</strong></p><p>The <strong>dorsal root ganglion</strong> is a classic example of a <strong>pseudo-unipolar neuron.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8362","text":"Where does the initiation ​of nerve impulse occur in‍ a spinal motor neuron?","raw_text":"Where does the initiation of nerve impulse occur in a spinal motor neuron?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initial nod​e of Ranvier","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Axon hillock+ initial segm​ent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cell​ body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dendrit​ic tree","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Axon hillock+ initial segment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14da8a25ecfe4c9ca9adf55119c84ebex1279x999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Axon hillock+ initial segment</strong></p><p>In a spinal motor neuron, <strong>initiation</strong> of <strong>nerve impulse</strong> occurs in the <strong>axon hillock</strong> and<strong> initial segment </strong>of the axon because it has a very high density of voltage-gated Na<sup>+ </sup>and K<sup>+</sup> channels. Hence, this region is also known as the trigger zone for the action potential.</p>\n<p>Parts of a Neuron:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cell body</strong>/perikaryon: contains many Nissl bodies.</li>\n<li><strong>Dendritic zone</strong> where multiple local potential changes generated by synaptic connections are integrated.</li>\n<li><strong>Axon hillock</strong>: part of the cell body from which the axon arises.</li>\n<li><strong>Initial segment</strong>: the first part of the axon where propagated action potentials are generated.</li>\n<li><strong>An axonal process</strong> that transmits propagated impulses to the nerve endings. The unmyelinated regions of a myelinated axon are termed as nodes of Ranvier.</li>\n<li><strong>Synaptic knobs/</strong>nerve endings where action potentials cause the release of synaptic transmitters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Action potentials are generated at the<strong> </strong>initial segment in spinal motor neurons and the initial node of Ranvier in cutaneous sensory neurons.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8360","text":"Which part of the neuron c​ontains a maximum number ‍of Na+ channels per squar‍e micrometer?","raw_text":"Which part of the neuron contains a maximum number of Na+ channels per square micrometer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell​ body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Axon t​erminal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surface ​of myelin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Node of​ Ranvier","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Node of Ranvier","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Node of Ranvier</strong></p><p>The <strong>maximum</strong> number of <strong>Na<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> per square micrometer in myelinated neurons is present at the <strong>nodes of Ranvier. </strong>It contains 2000-12,000 channels per square micrometer.</p>\n<p>Voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels are highly concentrated in the nodes of Ranvier and the initial segment in myelinated neurons.</p>\n<p>The spatial distribution of ion channels along the axon plays a key role in the initiation and regulation of action potential.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1040","text":"In a cutaneous sensory neu​ron, at which of the foll‍owing places is the propa‍gated action potential ge‌nerated?","raw_text":"In a cutaneous sensory neuron, at which of the following places is the propagated action potential generated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First node​ of Ranvier","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Initial​ segment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dend​rite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve ​ending","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. First node of Ranvier","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. First node of Ranvier</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 100\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In cutaneous sensory neurons, <strong>propagated action potentials</strong> are generated in the<strong> initial node of Ranvier</strong>.</p>\n<p>From a functional point of view, neurons generally have four important zones :</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A receptor, or <strong>dendritic zone</strong>, where multiple local potential changes generated by synaptic connections are integrated</li>\n<li>A site where propagated action potentials are generated (the <strong>initial segment</strong> in <strong>spinal motor neurons</strong>, the <strong>initial node of Ranvier </strong>in<strong> cutaneous sensory neurons</strong>)</li>\n<li>An <strong>axonal process</strong> that transmits propagated impulses to the nerve endings</li>\n<li>The <strong>nerve endings</strong>, where action potentials cause the release of synaptic transmitters</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The cell body is often located at the dendritic zone end of the axon, but it can be within the axon (eg, auditory neurons)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8560","text":"What is the relationship b​etween diameter of the ne‍rve and conduction veloci‍ty?","raw_text":"What is the relationship between diameter of the nerve and conduction velocity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lin​ear","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Para​bolic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyper​bolic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No re​lation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Linear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Linear</strong></p><p>The<strong> conduction velocity</strong> of a myelinated axon <strong>increases linearly</strong> with <strong>diameter.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Conduction velocity</strong> is<strong> increased by:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Increasing the fiber size: <strong>Increasing the diameter of a nerve fiber</strong> results in decreased internal resistance; thus, conduction velocity down the nerve is faster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Myelination</strong>: Myelin acts as an insulator around nerve axons and increases conduction velocity. Myelinated nerves exhibit <strong>saltatory conduction. </strong>It is a rapid process that allows myelinated axons to conduct up to 50 times faster than the fastest unmyelinated fibers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Activity of the Na<sup>+</sup>–K<sup>+</sup> pump does not directly affect the formation or conduction of action potentials. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8371","text":"What process is responsibl​e for the change in membr‍ane potential that occurs‍ between point 1 and poin‌t 3?","raw_text":"What process is responsible for the change in membrane potential that occurs between point 1 and point 3?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Movement of Na+ into the c​ell","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Movement of Na+ out of the​ cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Movement of K+ into the ce​ll","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Movement of K+ out of the ​cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Movement of Na+ into the cell","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0beecdf7ef1042168c98d2de65efe365.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78eff207705c4b7fa378118e72109496x1280x1085.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Movement of Na+ into the cell</strong></p><p>The graph depicts <strong>action potential.</strong> The <strong>movement</strong> of <strong>Na+ into</strong> the <strong>cell</strong> is responsible for the change in membrane potential that occurs between point 1 and point 3.</p>\n<p>Once the membrane is depolarized to the threshold, local potential, the upstroke of the nerve action potential is caused by the opening of the Na<sup>+</sup> channels. The entry of Na+ causes the opening of more voltage-gated Na+ channels and further <strong>depolarization,</strong> setting up a <strong>positive feedback loop</strong>. When the Na<sup>+</sup> channels open, Na<sup>+</sup> moves into the cell down its electrochemical gradient, driving the membrane potential toward the Na<sup>+</sup> equilibrium potential.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The process responsible for <strong>repolarization</strong> is the <strong>opening</strong> of <strong>K</strong><sup><strong>+</strong>. </sup>The K<sup>+</sup> permeability becomes very high and drives the membrane potential toward the K<sup>+</sup>equilibrium potential by the flow of K<sup>+</sup> <strong>out of</strong> the <strong>cell.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1041","text":"The membrane conductance f​or potassium is maximum a‍t which of the following ‍points in a nerve action ‌potential?","raw_text":"The membrane conductance for potassium is maximum at which of the following points in a nerve action potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/efdaa499068548cab1ea9f63b1b95a3f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ee0041e324d46ef92fec66fda05413cx1280x1683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 73\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 74\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>membrane conductance</strong> for <strong>potassium</strong> is <strong>maximum</strong> during <strong>repolarization</strong> (marked as <strong>point 3</strong> in the above image). <strong>  </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Most of the increase in K<span class=\"s1\">+ </span>conductance comes after the increase in Na<span class=\"s1\">+ </span>conductance around the time of repolarization. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>During the early portion of the action potential, the sodium conductance is 1000 times greater than potassium conductance. Therefore, far more sodium ions flow to the interior of the fiber than do potassium ions to the exterior. This causes the membrane potential to become positive at the action potential onset.</p>\n<p>When the membrane potential rises from −90 millivolts toward zero, this voltage change causes a conformational change in the K+ channels which allows increased potassium efflux. Then, the sodium channels begin to close and the potassium channels begin to open, and potassium conductance begins to increase (and sodium conductance decreases).</p>\n<p>The increased flow of K<sup>+</sup> out of the cell causes membrane repolarization. The slow return of the K+ channels to the closed state explains the after-hyperpolarization followed by a return to the resting membrane potential. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8369","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding the upstro‍ke of a nerve action pote‍ntial?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the upstroke of a nerve action potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is net outward curre​nt and the cell interior ‍becomes more negative","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is net outward curre​nt and the cell interior ‍becomes less negative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is net inward curren​t and the cell interior b‍ecomes more negative","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is net inward curren​t and the cell interior b‍ecomes less negative","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. There is net inward current and the cell interior becomes less negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/086669f196844046a00a66e8bd64c571x360x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. There is net inward current and the cell interior becomes less negative</strong></p><p>Regarding the <strong>upstroke</strong> of a nerve action potential, there is a <strong>net inward current</strong> and the cell<strong> interior becomes less negative</strong>. The direction of the current is either</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>same</strong> as the direction of <strong>positive</strong> charge ( eg, Na+ )</li>\n<li><strong>opposite</strong> to the direction of <strong>negative</strong> charge (eg, Cl- )</li>\n<li><strong>opposite</strong> to the direction of electrons</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Since Na+ moves in, there is a <strong>net inward current.</strong></p>\n<p>Since the RMP increases, the <strong>cell interior becomes less negative. </strong> </p>\n<p>In response to a depolarizing stimulus, some of the voltage-gated Na+ channels open and Na+ enters the cell and the membrane is brought to its threshold potential. The entry of Na+ causes the opening of more voltage-gated Na+ channels and further depolarization, setting up a <strong>positive feedback loop</strong>. The rapid upstroke in the membrane potential ensues. So, there is a net influx of Na+, and the interior of the cell becomes positive during the upstroke of the action potential. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1047","text":"Which of the following is ​correctly matched? Ionic ‍Changes Components of Act‍ion Potential 1 Slow clos‌ure of voltage-gated K+ c‍hannels After-hyperpolari​zation 2 A decrease in ex‌tracellular Ca2+ Repolari​zation 3 Closing of volta​ge-gated Na+ channels Dep‍olarization 4 Rapid closu​re of voltage-gated Na+ c​hannels Resting membrane ​potential","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is correctly matched?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 173px; width: 395px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.163px;\"><strong>Ionic Changes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.837px;\"><strong>Components of Action Potential</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.163px;\">Slow closure of voltage-gated K+ channels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.837px;\">After-hyperpolarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.163px;\">A decrease in extracellular Ca2+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.837px;\">Repolarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.163px;\">Closing of voltage-gated Na+ channels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.837px;\">Depolarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.163px;\">Rapid closure of voltage-gated Na+ channels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.837px;\">Resting membrane potential </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75b9853facd64dc8aad1f6be4077c2fbx1280x1683.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b57b85e1d8348c59714eff3d4cdc503x1280x1085.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cec707b525d64ff0a22f8b04d832752cx1280x1398.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>The <strong>slow return</strong> of the <strong>K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> to the closed state also explains the <strong>after-hyperpolarization. </strong></p>\n<p>In response to a depolarizing stimulus, some of the <strong>voltage-gated Na+ channels open</strong> and Na<sup>+</sup> enters the cell. The entry of Na<sup>+</sup> causes the opening of more voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels and further depolarization, setting up a positive feedback loop. The <strong>rapid upstroke</strong> in the membrane potential ensues.</p>\n<p>The membrane potential moves toward the equilibrium potential for Na<sup>+</sup> (+60 mV) but does not reach it during the action potential, primarily because the increase in Na+ conductance is short-lived. The Na<sup>+</sup> channels rapidly enter a closed state called the inactivated state and remain in this state for a few milliseconds.</p>\n<p>In addition, the direction of the electrical gradient for Na<sup>+</sup> is reversed during the overshoot because the membrane potential is reversed, and this limits Na<sup>+</sup> influx; also the voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channels open. These factors contribute to <strong>repolarization</strong>. The opening of voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channels is slower and more prolonged than the opening of the Na<sup>+</sup> channels. The net movement of positive charge out of the cell due to K<sup>+</sup> efflux at this time helps complete the process of repolarization.</p>\n<p>The slow return of the K<sup>+</sup> channels to the closed state also explains the <strong>after-hyperpolarization</strong>, followed by a return to the resting membrane potential.</p>\n<p>Thus, voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channels bring the action potential to an end and cause closure of their gates through a negative feedback process. </p>\n<p>Note: Na<sup>+</sup> channels: Positive feedback loop; K<sup>+</sup> channels: Negative feedback loop.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8377","text":"What is known as the time ​period in which a second ‍stronger impulse can elic‍it action potential after‌ the application of one s‍timulus?","raw_text":"What is known as the time period in which a second stronger impulse can elicit action potential after the application of one stimulus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absolute refractory period​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relative refractory period​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Latent refra​ctory period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local r​esponse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Relative refractory period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Relative refractory period</strong></p><p>The <strong>refractory period</strong> is the time period in which a second stronger impulse can elicit action potential after the application of one stimulus. It can be absolute or relative. </p>\n<p>During the rising and much of the falling phases of the spike potential, the neuron is refractory to stimulation. This refractory period is divided into an</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Absolute refractory period</strong>, corresponding to the period from the time the firing level is reached until repolarization is about one-third complete</li>\n<li><strong>Relative refractory period</strong>, lasting from this point to the start of after-depolarization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>During the absolute refractory period, no stimulus, no matter how strong, will not excite the nerve. However, during the relative refractory period, stimuli that are stronger than normal can cause excitation.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 80\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The absolute refractory period for large myelinated nerve fibers is about <strong>1/2500</strong> second.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8375","text":"From where does the relati​ve refractory period star‍t?","raw_text":"From where does the relative refractory period start?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Half of rep​olarization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"One third of repolarizatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Quarter of r​epolarization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Two thirds of repolarizati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. One third of repolarization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. One third of repolarization</strong></p><p>The <strong>relative refractory period</strong> <strong>starts</strong> from <strong>one-third</strong> of <strong>repolarization</strong> till the start of after-depolarization.</p>\n<p>The refractory period is a period during which another normal action potential cannot be elicited in an excitable cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8392","text":"Rheobase is an indicator o​f which of the following?‍","raw_text":"Rheobase is an indicator of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Specificity of impulse tra​nsmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnitude ​of current","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rate of ​discharge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Velocity of nerve conducti​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Magnitude of current","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10150e96123844fc804595d06ede18e1x1280x1057.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bb9ad8155504f69bb91e156000543bfx1280x1338.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Magnitude of current</strong></p><p>The<strong> rheobase</strong> is an indicator of the <strong>magnitude</strong> of the <strong>current.</strong> It is the minimum intensity (strength/magnitude) of stimulation required to generate an action potential when the stimulation is of infinite duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Utilization</strong> time is the minimum time (duration) for which the rheobase has to be applied for the excitation of tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronaxie</strong> is the minimum time (duration) for which a stimulus double the strength of rheobase has to be applied for excitation of tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Strength-duration curve</strong> gives the idea of the excitability of a tissue.</p>\n<p>Strength-duration relationship arises because the same minimum electrical charge necessary to excite an action potential can come from a current that is either brief but large or prolonged but small. It is the product of strength and duration that determines excitability, and thus these two parameters are inversely related in their effectiveness.</p>\n<p>Note: Chronaxie is a better index than rheobase and utilization time for comparing the excitability of a tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1044","text":"A slowly rising stimulus f​ails to fire a nerve beca‍use of a process known as‍ __________.","raw_text":"A slowly rising stimulus fails to fire a nerve because of a process known as __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adapt​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accomo​dation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Refract​oriness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electr​otonus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Accomodation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Accomodation</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 105\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Slowly rising <strong>subthreshold</strong> currents fail to fire the nerve due to a process called <strong>accommodation,</strong> which is a type of adaptation.</p>\n<p>Accommodation is due to <strong>inactivation of voltage-gated Na+ channels</strong>. Due to the slow rise of stimulus, only a few voltage-gated Na+ channels are activated at a time. These few channels are inactivated by the time more channels are activated. So, at no moment, are a sufficient number of channels activated to give an action potential. </p>\n<p>When a membrane is depolarized rapidly, a large number of Na+ channels open at once, and an action potential is produced. </p>\n<p>In contrast, decreased response to a long-lasting and sustained <strong>suprathreshold</strong> stimulus, in general, is called <strong>adaptation. </strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8394","text":"Which of the following is ​true?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type Aα fibre carry inform​ation related to pressure‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type Aβ fibre has the larg​est diameter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type AΥ fibre is unmyelina​ted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type C fibre has the slowe​st conduction velocity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type C fibre has the slowest conduction velocity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type C fibre has the slowest conduction velocity</strong></p><p><strong>Type C fibers</strong> are the small <strong>unmyelinated</strong> nerve fibers that conduct impulses at <strong>low velocities.</strong></p>\n<p>Greater the diameter of a given nerve fiber, the greater its speed of conduction.</p>\n<p>The nerve fibers are divided into types A (myelinated), B (myelinated), and C(Unmyelinated). The type A fibers are further subdivided into α, β, γ, and δ fibers.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8395","text":"You are performing a CNS e​xamination on a patient w‍ith a stroke. With his ey‍es closed, you move his b‌ig toe up or down while h‍olding his toe joint. You​ ask him to state the pos‌ition of the digit. Which​ of the following fibers ​are responsible for the t‍ransmission of the sensat​ion tested here?","raw_text":"You are performing a CNS examination on a patient with a stroke. With his eyes closed, you move his big toe up or down while holding his toe joint. You ask him to state the position of the digit. Which of the following fibers are responsible for the transmission of the sensation tested here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​α","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A​γ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​β","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A​δ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aα","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aα</strong></p><p>The test performed here is used to check the <strong>proprioception.</strong> The large axons like <strong>Aα nerve fibers</strong> (12-20μm) carry information regarding <strong>proprioceptive sensation </strong>and somatic motor function.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0640","text":"In the Erlanger and Gasser​ classification of periph‍eral nerve fibers, which ‍type of signal is transmi‌tted by the type B nerve ‍fibers?","raw_text":"In the Erlanger and Gasser classification of peripheral nerve fibers, which type of signal is transmitted by the type B nerve fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proprio​ception","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preganglion​ic autonomic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Motor to mu​scle spindle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crude​ touch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Preganglionic autonomic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Preganglionic autonomic</strong></p><p>The <strong>type B</strong> fibers <strong>transmit</strong> the <strong>preganglionic autonomic fibers</strong> of both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Erlanger and Gasser</strong> classified the fibers into types A, B, and C, and the type A fibers are further subdivided into α, β, γ, and δ fibers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type A fibers are the typical large- and medium-­sized myelinated fibers of spinal nerves.</li>\n<li>Type B fibers are smaller fibers with minimal myelination. They transmit impulses of the ANS including the bladder. (B for <strong>B</strong>ladder)</li>\n<li>Type C fibers are the small unmyelinated nerve fibers that conduct impulses at low velocities. They constitute more than one-half of the sensory fibers in most peripheral nerves, as well as all the postganglionic autonomic fibers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8399","text":"A man falls into a deep sl​eep with one arm under hi‍s head. He finds he is no‍t able to move this arm w‌hen he awakens. However, ‍it tingles and pain sensa​tion in the arm is still ‌intact. What is the reaso​n for the loss of motor f​unction without loss of p‍ain sensation is that in ​the nerves to his arm?","raw_text":"A man falls into a deep sleep with one arm under his head. He finds he is not able to move this arm when he awakens. However, it tingles and pain sensation in the arm is still intact. What is the reason for the loss of motor function without loss of pain sensation is that in the nerves to his arm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B fibers are more suscepti​ble to pressure than A fi‍bers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A fibers are more sensitiv​e to pressure than C fibe‍rs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C fibers are more sensitiv​e to pressure than A fibe‍rs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Motor nerves are more affe​cted by sleep than sensor‍y nerves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers</strong></p><p>The reason for the loss of motor function without loss of pain sensation is that in the nerves to his arm, <strong>A-fibers</strong> are <strong>more sensitive</strong> to <strong>pressure</strong> than C fibers.</p>\n<p>Sleeping for a long period with the arms under the head puts pressure on a nerve. It leads to the loss of conduction in large-diameter motor, touch, and pressure fibers, while pain sensation remains relatively intact. A-fibers (includes the motor fibers) are affected.</p>\n<p>The susceptibility of the nerve fibers to pressure is<strong> A&gt;B&gt;C.</strong></p>\n<p>This is responsible for Saturday night or Sunday morning paralysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8402","text":"Spike duration is maximum ​in which of the following‍ nerve fiber?","raw_text":"Spike duration is maximum in which of the following nerve fiber?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​α","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A​β","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p>Spike duration is maximum is C fibers.</p>\n<p>The spike duration in decreasing order: C &gt; B &gt; A</p>\n<p>Absolute refractory period follows the same order: C &gt; B &gt; A</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Fibers</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Spike Duration (ms)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Absolute Refractory Period (ms)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>A</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>0.4-0.5</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>0.4-1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>B</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>1.2</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>1.2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>C</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>2</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8401","text":"Golgi tendon organ, being ​sensory is classified und‍er which subtype of senso‍ry nerve fibres?","raw_text":"Golgi tendon organ, being sensory is classified under which subtype of sensory nerve fibres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I​a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I​b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"I​I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I​II","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ib</strong></p><p><strong>Golgi tendon organ,</strong> which contains the receptor for <strong>inverse stretch reflex,</strong> belongs to <strong>Class Ib</strong> sensory nerve fibres.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1046","text":"Which sensory fibers are l​east susceptible to hypox‍ia?","raw_text":"Which sensory fibers are least susceptible to hypoxia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ​e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ​e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type​ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ​e IV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type IV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type IV</strong></p><p>Susceptibility of nerve fibers to hypoxia is <strong>B &gt;A&gt;C, </strong>and in the general classification, type IV sensory nerves are Type C fibers. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8391","text":"All of the following are t​rue regarding a biphasic ‍action potential except _‍_________.","raw_text":"All of the following are true regarding a biphasic action potential except __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All or none​ phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 or more po​sitive waves","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Refractory p​eriod is seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recorded on​ the surface","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 or more positive waves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7a300aa9809474bb6454ac1b21d0228x600x1046.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/730652cec8ac4ae4b3a4537f1b955202x1280x1109.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 or more positive waves</strong></p><p><strong>Biphasic Action Potential</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This type of record is obtained when both recording electrodes are placed on the surface of an axon that has been stimulated.</li>\n<li>The record shows an upward deflection followed by an isoelectric interval and then a downward deflection which denotes to the time taken for the electric potential to travel from one electrode to another.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Compound Action Potential</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a property of mixed nerves.</li>\n<li>The record shows multiple peaks in action potential because a mixed nerve is made up of families of fibres with various speeds of conduction. Therefore, when all the fibres are stimulated, the activity in the fast-conducting fibres arrives at the recording electrode sooner than the activity in slower fibers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8379","text":"End plate potential follow​s all of the following ex‍cept?","raw_text":"End plate potential follows all of the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All or ​none law","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Depolar​ization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpol​arization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Summ​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. All or none law","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. All or none law</strong></p><p>End plate potential is a graded potential with the following characteristics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Magnitude is related to the magnitude of the initiating event</li>\n<li>Amplitude decreases over distance</li>\n<li>Can be summated</li>\n</ul>\n<table width=\"558\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p><strong>Graded Potential</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p><strong>Action Potential</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Amplitude proportionate to strength of stimulus. It is graded.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Independent of stimulus intensity – all or none response</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Can be depolarising or hyperpolarizing</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Depolarizing</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Is conducted decrementally - amplitude decreases with distance</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Self-propagated unchanged in amplitude</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Generated spontaneously or in response to stimuli- physical, chemical</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Generated only in response to Membrane Depolarization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Can be summated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Cannot be summated</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Has no threshold and refractory period</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Has a threshold about 15 mv depolarized relative to the resting potential.</p>\n<p>Has a refractory period</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<p>Not always followed by some action</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Always followed by some action</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8404","text":"Which of the following can​ be used to measure a syn‍aptic potential?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used to measure a synaptic potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patch​ clamp","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microel​ectrode","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Voltag​e clamp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E​CG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Microelectrode","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microelectrode</strong></p><p><strong>Instruments required for recording membrane potential/synaptic potential</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stimulating electrodes: </strong>Cathode and anode</li>\n<li><strong>Recording electrodes: </strong>Glass microelectrode (tip diameter &lt;0.5μ)</li>\n<li><strong>Display: </strong>Cathode ray oscilloscope (CRO)</li>\n<li><strong>Squid giant axon: </strong>It is a large diameter neuron (up to 0.5 mm) from where the recording of potential is easier. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1042","text":"A man who is fond of exoti​c cuisine develops perior‍al numbness after eating ‍the Pufferfish. The given‌ poisoning is due to whic‍h of the following toxins​?","raw_text":"A man who is fond of exotic cuisine develops perioral numbness after eating the Pufferfish. The given poisoning is due to which of the following toxins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saxi​toxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrod​otoxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cur​are","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cigua​toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tetrodotoxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/743d824ea1ee4685b28cda4262147191x1280x1421.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tetrodotoxin</strong></p><p><strong>Pufferfish poisoning</strong> is caused by the toxin <strong>tetrodotoxin</strong>. The <strong>sodium channels</strong> can be blocked by this toxin when it is applied to the outside of the cell membrane where the sodium activation gates are located.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8357","text":"Conduction in the chemical​ synapses in CNS is","raw_text":"Conduction in the chemical synapses in CNS is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both antidromic and orthod​romic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Always a​ntidromic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Always or​thodromic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antidromic under lab condi​tions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Always orthodromic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8cb76a81b214de7ab6b6105f411ebf7x648x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Always orthodromic</strong></p><p>Conduction in the <strong>chemical synapses</strong> in CNS is <strong>always</strong> <strong>orthodromic.</strong></p>\n<p>When an action potential is set up in an axon, two impulses travelling in the opposite direction is set up by depolarization on either side of the initial current. </p>\n<p>In the natural situation, impulses pass in one direction only- from synaptic junctions to axon terminals. Such conduction is called <strong>orthodromic. </strong>Conduction in the opposite direction is called <strong>antidromic.</strong> Under natural conditions, impulse conduction is normally only orthodromic. Antidromic conduction is usually only seen when a nerve fibre is stimulated under artificial conditions. </p>\n<p>In a chemical synapse, conduction is always orthodromic. As the transmission in a chemical synapse is due to the release of neurotransmitters from the presynaptic membrane which activate receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, antidromic conduction (i.e. from the postsynaptic membrane to presynaptic membrane) is not possible. Any impulse travelling in such direction would die as it encounters the first synapse. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8356","text":"Fast axonal transport is b​y all except______","raw_text":"Fast axonal transport is by all except______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dyn​ein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kin​esin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofi​laments","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Microt​ubules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neurofilaments","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d012707744a491dae4ba95023d6713ex1280x1239.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea913fec7c184faea2f344984e8cba88x500x596.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neurofilaments</strong></p><p>Fast axonal transport is by all <strong>except</strong> neurofilaments.</p>\n<p>Neurofilaments play no role in axonal transport. Fast axonal transport takes place along <strong>microtubules</strong> which are tubular polymers of protein <strong>tubulin. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>AXONAL TRANSPORT</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protein synthesis occurs in the cell body of neuron and proteins and polypeptides are transported to axonal endings by the axoplasmic flow.</li>\n<li>Axonal transport can be orthograde or retrograde.</li>\n<li><strong>Orthograde transport</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Can be either fast or slow axonal transport and it occurs along microtubules that run along the length of the axon away from the cell body.</li>\n<li>Fast axonal transport occurs at about <strong>400 mm/day</strong>, and slow axonal transport occurs at <strong>0.5 to 10 mm/day</strong>.</li>\n<li>Molecular motor involved in orthograde transport is <strong>Kinesin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Retrograde transport</strong>,\n<ul>\n<li>In the opposite direction (from the nerve ending to the cell body), occurs along microtubules at about <strong>200 mm/day. </strong>Synaptic vesicles, nerve growth factor (NGF) and some viruses are transported back to the cell body. </li>\n<li>Molecular motor involed in retrograde transport is <strong>Dyenin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1029","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding axona‍l transport?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding axonal transport?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axonal transport occurs al​ong microtubules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Slow orthograde axonal tra​nsport occurs at 100 mm/d‍ay","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kinesins are required for ​orthograde axonal transpo‍rt","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viruses exploit retrograde​ transport for invasion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Slow orthograde axonal transport occurs at 100 mm/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Slow orthograde axonal transport occurs at 100 mm/day</strong></p><p class=\"ng-binding\">Slow orthograde transport occurs at a speed of <strong>0.5 to 10 mm/day</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 102\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>AXONAL TRANSPORT</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protein synthesis occurs in the cell body of neuron and proteins and polypeptides are transported to axonal endings by the axoplasmic flow.</li>\n<li>Axonal transport can be Orthograde or Retrograde.</li>\n<li><strong>Orthograde transport</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Can be either fast or slow axonal transport and it occurs along microtubules that run along the length of the axon away from the cell body.</li>\n<li>Fast axonal transport occurs at about <strong>400 mm/day</strong>, and slow axonal transport occurs at <strong>0.5 to 10 mm/day</strong>.</li>\n<li>Molecular motor involved in <strong>orthograde</strong> transport is <strong>kinesin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Retrograde transport</strong>,\n<ul>\n<li>In the opposite direction (from the nerve ending to the cell body), occurs along microtubules at about <strong>200 mm/day. </strong>Synaptic vesicles, nerve growth factor (NGF) and some viruses <strong>(Eg: herpes simplex, rabies, and polio) </strong>are transported back to the cell body. </li>\n<li>Molecular motor involed in <strong>retrograde</strong> transport is <strong>dyenin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8358","text":"When a nerve is cut, which​ of the following takes p‍lace first?","raw_text":"When a nerve is cut, which of the following takes place first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mylein de​generation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Regenerativ​e Sprouting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denervation supersensitivi​ty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schwann cell proliferation​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mylein degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/734b756152c34c34a99c0193053b63cax1280x2017.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mylein degeneration</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>myelin degeneration</strong> would be the best answer.</p>\n<p>However, the earliest change in Wallerian degeneration after nerve injury is the <strong>degeneration of the axon distal to the site of injury </strong>(axonal degeneration) where there is a disruption of the retrograde and anterograde flow of signals within the axon.</p>\n<p><strong>Wallerian degeneration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the axon is cut, the part distal to the cut degenerates; this process is called as <strong>Wallerian degeneration. </strong></li>\n<li>Orthograde degeneration (Wallerian degeneration) occurs from the point of injury to the nerve terminal, interrupting neural transmission. </li>\n<li>After the injury, the membrane breaks down and the myelin sheath degenerates. The cell body of the injured neuron swells, the nucleus moves to an eccentric position, and the rough endoplasmic reticulum becomes fragmented <strong>(chromatolytic reaction)</strong>.</li>\n<li>The nerve then starts to regrow, with multiple small branches projecting along the path the axon previously followed<strong> (regenerative sprouting).</strong></li>\n<li>When the motor nerve to skeletal muscle is cut and allowed to degenerate, the muscle gradually becomes extremely sensitive to acetylcholine. This is called <strong>denervation hypersensitivity </strong>or<strong> supersensitivity</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1043","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding the restin‍g membrane potential (RMP‍) in a nerve fibre?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the resting membrane potential (RMP) in a nerve fibre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is equal to the RMP of ​inner hair cells of cochl‍ea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be measured by surf​ace electrodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It becomes more positive a​s extracellular K+ increa‍ses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It depends on the equilibr​ium potential of Na+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It becomes more positive as extracellular K+ increases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It becomes more positive as extracellular K+ increases</strong></p><p>The resting membrane potential is close to the equilibrium potential for <strong>K+. </strong>So, changes in the external concentration of this ion can have major effects on the resting membrane potential.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the extracellular level of <strong>K+ is increased (hyperkalemia)</strong>, the resting potential moves closer to the threshold for eliciting an action potential, thus the neuron becomes more excitable.</li>\n<li>If the extracellular level of <strong>K+ is decreased (hypokalemia)</strong>, the membrane potential is reduced and the neuron is hyperpolarized.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option a</strong>: The RMP of a nerve fiber is -70 mV, while the RMP of inner hair cells is between -45 and -60 mV relative to the fluid that bathes the basal end of the cell.</p>\n<p><strong>Option b</strong>: The membrane potential can be measured only when one electrode is inserted into the <strong>interior of the cell</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Option d</strong>: It depends on the equilibrium potential of K+.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1045","text":"Which of the following abo​ut sensory nerve fibers i‍s not true?","raw_text":"Which of the following about sensory nerve fibers is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ia - A​ alpha","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"II - ​A beta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"III - ​A gamma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IV ​- C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. III - A gamma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. III - A gamma</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 575\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 575\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Group III fibers carrying temperature, crude touch, and pricking pain sensations are <strong>δ-type A</strong> fibers, not A-gamma type .</p>\n<p>Alternative Classification Used in Sensory Nerves</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Group Ia.</strong> Fibers from the annulospiral endings of muscle spindles are<strong> α-type A </strong>fibers in the general classification.</li>\n<li><strong>Group Ib.</strong> Fibers from the Golgi tendon organs are also <strong>α-type</strong> A fibers.</li>\n<li><strong>Group II.</strong> Fibers from most discrete cutaneous tactile receptors and from the flower-spray endings of the muscle spindles are <strong>β- and γ-type A</strong> fibers in the general classification.</li>\n<li><strong>Group III.</strong> Fibers carrying temperature, crude touch, and pricking pain sensations are <strong>δ-type A</strong> fibers in the general classification.</li>\n<li><strong>Group IV.</strong> Unmyelinated fibers carrying pain, itch, temperature, and crude touch sensations are <strong>type C</strong> fibers in the general classification</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9301","text":"Absolute refractory period​ is due to:","raw_text":"Absolute refractory period is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of calcium channel​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closure of potassium chann​els","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Closure of active gates of​ sodium channel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Closure of inactive gates ​of sodium channel","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Closure of inactive gates of sodium channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8cd4e8949ca142d1a64c212c3b9a54cbx1280x1683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Closure of inactive gates of sodium channel</strong></p><p>Absolute refractory period is due to <strong>closure of inactive gates</strong> of sodium channel.</p>\n<p>Voltage-gated sodium channels have <strong>3 states</strong> that you can find them in. They can either be <strong>closed, open, or inactive</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the stimulus causes activation of the open gates, sodium rushes into the cell causing the membrane potential to become more positive. After 0.5 to 1 millisecond, the inactive gate automatically closes and no stimulus, no matter how strong, can excite the nerve. This is called the absolute refractory period. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5222","text":"Identify the ions A and B ​based on conductance grap‍h of a neuron shown below‍:","raw_text":"Identify the ions A and B based on conductance graph of a neuron shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na​, K","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"K,​ Na","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Na,​ Ca","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ca,​ Na","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Na, K","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b5d9cc3563304205868bddaedf9c0fe4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b57c05afdd7644c5883be4b9e9750236x1280x1028.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35286d684fa14639af2eafe0b9fc4e08x1280x1683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Na, K</strong></p><p>Channel A: Na+, Channel B: K+</p>\n<p><strong>Na+ channels</strong> are <strong>fast activating</strong> and are responsible for <strong>depolarization phase</strong> of action potential.</p>\n<p><strong>K+ channels</strong> are slow activating and open for prolonged duration than the sodium channels. They are responsible for <strong>repolarization phase</strong> of action potential. Also, the postassium channels return slowly to their closed state, which is responsible for after-hyperpolarization.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>The sequence of events during the action potential</strong> occur in 7 steps as described below.</p>\n<p>1. Resting state - <strong>Both channels are closed</strong></p>\n<p>2. <strong>Na+ channels open</strong> in response to depolarizing stimulus. Sodium enters into the cell. The membrane reaches <strong>threshold potential.</strong></p>\n<p>3. The sodium influx causes <strong>opening of more sodium channels</strong> through <strong>positive feedback</strong> mechanism. <strong>Rapid depolarization</strong> occurs.</p>\n<p>4. Sodium conductance decreases.</p>\n<p>5. Na+ channels close and <strong>K+ channels open</strong>. <strong>Repolarization</strong> occurs.</p>\n<p>6. <strong>Sodium</strong> channels are <strong>reset</strong>, and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus (<strong>relative refractory period</strong>). During this phase, the <strong>potassium</strong> channels remain open and <strong>slowly return to closed</strong> state causing <strong>after-hyperpolarization</strong>.</p>\n<p>7. Both channels are closed. The membrane returns to <strong>RMP</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6743","text":"The image below shows simp​le muscle twitch along wi‍th the time trace, record‍ed in a frog gastrocnemiu‌s muscle. Calculate the m‍inimum tetanizing frequen​cy. (The time tracer was ‌made with a 100Hz tuning ​fork).","raw_text":"The image below shows simple muscle twitch along with the time trace, recorded in a frog gastrocnemius muscle. Calculate the minimum tetanizing frequency. (The time tracer was made with a 100Hz tuning fork).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25 per​ second","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 per​ second","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17 per​ second","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 per​ second","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20 per second","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/34ef06d3894942c9a681f6211dc63967.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/037de34e007545d79a483088b1b50c03x1280x2823.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20 per second</strong></p><p><strong>The tetanizing frequency of the muscle, based on the given diagram, is 20 per second.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tetanizing frequency = 1/contraction period. </strong>It is the frequency at which <strong>individual muscle fibres twitch</strong> that fuse and cause sustained muscle contraction, where the <strong>contraction period</strong> is the time taken from <strong>latent period</strong> to <strong>maximum force of contraction.</strong></p>\n<p>In this case, the time tracer was taken using a <strong>100Hz tuning fork.</strong> Which means the tracer recorded 100 waves per second.<br/>Therefore, the<strong> duration of each wave</strong> in time tracer = <strong>0.01 seconds.</strong></p>\n<p>In the image given in the question, between the <strong>point B</strong> (end of latent period) to<strong> point C </strong>(maximum force of contraction), there are about 5 waves.<br/>Hence, the <strong>duration</strong> <strong>of contraction</strong> = 5 x 0.01 = 0.05 seconds.</p>\n<p>Substituting the value in the formula, we get:<br/><strong>Tetanizing frequency</strong> = 1/0.05 sec = 100/5 = <strong>20/sec.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tetanization</strong>:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The features of muscle contraction can be demonstrated by eliciting single muscle twitches. The adding together of individual twitch contractions to increase the intensity of overall muscle contraction is <strong>Summation. </strong>Summation occurs in two ways:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">by increasing the number of motor units contracting simultaneously, which is <strong>multiple ﬁber summations.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\">by increasing the frequency of contraction, which is called frequency<strong> summation and can lead to tetanization.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">When the frequency of a contraction reaches a critical level, the successive contractions eventually fuse together, and the whole muscle contraction appears to be completely smooth and continuous, this is called<strong> tetanization. </strong>Beyond this point, a further increase in the frequency, will not result in increased force of contraction.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tetanization </strong>occurs in two ways:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Complete tetanus </strong>occurs when no relaxation occurs between stimuli.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Incomplete tetanus </strong>when periods of incomplete relaxation take place between the summated stimuli.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ArVWaLTi","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"6_schizophrenia","title":"6.Schizophrenia","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MC0414","text":"A middle-aged man presents​ to the psychiatry OPD wi​th a history of speaking ​to himself, hearing two p​eople talk about him, red​uced affect, and impaired‍ judgment which is presen​t for more than 6 months.‍ What is the likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents to the psychiatry OPD with a history of speaking to himself, hearing two people talk about him, reduced affect, and impaired judgment which is present for more than 6 months. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizophreni​form disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schizoaffect​ive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizoid personality disor​der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Schizophrenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Schizophrenia</strong></p><p>The patient has auditory hallucination, reduced affect, lack of insight, impaired judgment for <strong>more than 6 months</strong>, which makes the diagnosis of <strong>schizophrenia</strong> more likely. </p>\n<p>Schizophrenia was previously known as<strong> dementia praecox</strong>, a term first coined by<strong> Emil Kraepelin</strong>. He classified psychiatric illness into two categories – dementia praecox and manic depressive disorder.</p>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria for schizophrenia (DSM-5):</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">1. The DSM-5 criteria to diagnose schizophrenia require a total duration of <strong>6 months</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">2. Within this 6-month period, there must be at least <strong>1 month</strong> of active-phase symptoms (overt psychotic symptoms) where 2 or more of the following have to be present (at least one must be among the a, b, or c): </p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>Delusion</li>\n<li>Hallucination</li>\n<li>Disorganized speech (or formal thought disorder)</li>\n<li>Disorganized behavior</li>\n<li>Affective flattening or other negative symptoms</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"p1\">3. The rest of the 6-month period may include</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Continuing psychotic symptoms</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prodromal symptoms preceding clear-cut psychosis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Residual symptoms after the resolution of the psychotic symptoms</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. Residual symptoms are defined as attenuated forms of psychotic symptoms, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Odd beliefs</li>\n<li>Magical thinking</li>\n<li>Ideas of reference</li>\n<li>Odd perceptual experiences</li>\n<li>Peculiar or concrete thinking</li>\n<li>Vague speech</li>\n<li>Odd behavior</li>\n<li>Negative symptoms may also be included</li>\n</ul>\n<p>5. Exclusion of mood disorder &amp; substance abuse</p>\n<p>Note: DSM-5 does not require that delusions must be bizarre.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0419","text":"A patient with auditory ha​llucinations and persecut​ory delusions is diagnose​d with schizophrenia. Whi​ch of the following is fa​lse regarding the neurotr‍ansmitters involved in it​s pathogenesis?","raw_text":"A patient with auditory hallucinations and persecutory delusions is diagnosed with schizophrenia. Which of the following is false regarding the neurotransmitters involved in its pathogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NMDA receptor hypoactivity​ in the prefrontal cortex​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased levels of nicoti​nic and muscarinic recept​ors in the hippocampus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased GABA receptor bi​nding in the cingulate co​rtex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dopaminergic hypoactivity ​in the subcortical region​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dopaminergic hypoactivity in the subcortical regions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dopaminergic hypoactivity in the subcortical regions</strong></p><p><strong>Dopaminergic hyperactivity</strong> in the subcortical regions is seen in <strong>schizophrenia. </strong></p>\n<p>All of the following neurotransmitters have been implicated in the pathogenesis of schizophrenia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dopamine - </strong>dopaminergic <strong>hyperactivity</strong> in the <strong>subcortical</strong> region and <strong>hypoactivity</strong> in <strong>prefrontal</strong> cortical regions is seen. It is linked to <strong>positive symptoms</strong> and cognitive deficits respectively. </li>\n<li><strong>Serotonin</strong> <strong>excess</strong> is linked to both <strong>positive </strong>and<strong> negative symptoms.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Norepinephrine </strong>- selective neuronal <strong>degeneration</strong> within the <strong>norepinephrine reward neural system</strong> is thought to be <strong>responsible for anhedonia,</strong> a feature of schizophrenia.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>GABA </strong>has a regulatory effect on dopamine activity. In some patients, there is a <strong>loss</strong> of inhibitory GABAergic neurons, which could indirectly lead to the hyperactivity of dopaminergic neurons. Increased GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor binding has been reported in the prefrontal and cingulate cortices and the hippocampus.</li>\n<li><strong>Acetylcholine</strong> and <strong>nicotine</strong>- <strong>decreased</strong> levels of nicotinic and muscarinic receptors in the hippocampus is seen.</li>\n<li><strong>NMDA</strong>- N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor <strong>hypoactivity</strong> is seen in the prefrontal cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0417","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted with talkativeness, p​oor sleep, and wandering ​away from home for the pa​st year. She often became​ aggressive and violent w‍hen restrained. On questi​oning, she cried uncontro‍llably and told that if s​he stays home it would re‌sult in her premature dea​th. She admitted to heari‌ng voices that warned her‌ to not interact with her‌ family members. Which of‌ the following is not a r‌isk factor associated wit‌h this condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with talkativeness, poor sleep, and wandering away from home for the past year. She often became aggressive and violent when restrained. On questioning, she cried uncontrollably and told that if she stays home it would result in her premature death. She admitted to hearing voices that warned her to not interact with her family members. Which of the following is not a risk factor associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampheta​mine use","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High socio-economic status​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Advanced p​aternal age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Industri​alization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High socio-economic status","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High socio-economic status</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>schizophrenia. </strong>High socioeconomic status is not associated with an increased risk of developing this disorder.</p>\n<p>Factors that increase the risk of schizophrenia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genetic factor- first-degree relatives and twins are at a higher risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Low socioeconomic status</strong>, immigration</li>\n<li><strong>Industrialization</strong></li>\n<li>High expressed emotions</li>\n<li>Drugs- <strong>amphetamines</strong>, cocaine, cannabis, ketamine</li>\n<li>Metabolic disorders – Huntington’s disease, Wilson’s disease, hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Asthenic body type</li>\n<li><strong>Advanced paternal age</strong> </li>\n<li>Winter born babies </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Schizophrenia is equally prevalent in both sexes. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6847","text":"An adolescent boy with sch​izophrenia had witnessed ​his parents fighting with​ each other for the past ​several years. He is over​ly close with his mother ‍and she convinced him to ​take her side in the divo‍rce. He always blamed his​ father for all their fam‌ily problems. Which of th​e following best describe‌s the abnormal family dyn‌amics?","raw_text":"An adolescent boy with schizophrenia had witnessed his parents fighting with each other for the past several years. He is overly close with his mother and she convinced him to take her side in the divorce. He always blamed his father for all their family problems. Which of the following best describes the abnormal family dynamics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Expressed​ emotions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marita​l skew","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marital​ schism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Doubl​e bind","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Marital schism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Marital schism</strong></p><p>This scenario describes an abnormal family dynamic, where one parent is overly close to the child of the opposite gender. This is known as <strong>marital schism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Marital schism, marital skew, expressed emotions, and double-bind refer to <strong>abnormalities</strong> of<strong> family dynamics</strong>. They act as psychosocial stressors, hampering the adaptive capacity of the individual, thus <strong>contributing</strong> to the <strong>development</strong> of <strong>schizophrenia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Marital schism</strong>- one parent is overly close to the child of the opposite gender.</p>\n<p><strong>Marital skew</strong>- a disharmonious situation where one parent dominates over the other.</p>\n<p><strong>Expressed emotions</strong>- parents or other caregivers may behave with overt criticism, hostility, or over-involvement towards a person with schizophrenia, which further worsens the illness.</p>\n<p><strong>Double bind- </strong>a hypothetical family in which children receive conflicting parental messages about their behavior, attitudes, and feelings.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0411","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the 4 A's o​f Bleuler ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not  a part of the 4 A's of Bleuler ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ambiv​alence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avol​ition","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Affective d​isturbances","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aut​ism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Avolition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Avolition</strong></p><p><strong>Avolition</strong> is not a part of the 4 A's of Bleuler .</p>\n<p>The term schizophrenia was introduced by Eugen Bleuler. He described the primary symptoms of schizophrenia, known as the 4 A's of Bleuler.</p>\n<p>This includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autism-</strong> social withdrawal with excessive irrational fantasy thinking.</li>\n<li><strong>Ambivalence-</strong> marked inability to take a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Association disturbances-</strong> loosening of association refers to changing the topic without identifiable links between the subjects.</li>\n<li><strong>Affect problems-</strong> flattening of affect or inappropriate affect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0412","text":"A patient with schizophren​ia presents with the foll​owing symptoms. Which of ​them is not a Schneideria​n first-rank symptom?","raw_text":"A patient with schizophrenia presents with the following symptoms. Which of them is not a Schneiderian first-rank symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ambiv​alence","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Somatic ​passivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halluc​ination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thought ​insertion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ambivalence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ambivalence</strong></p><p><strong>Ambivalence</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a Schneiderian <strong>first-rank symptom</strong> of schizophrenia. It is one of the 4A’s of schizophrenia as described by Bleuler.</p>\n<p>The first rank symptoms of schizophrenia described by Kurt Schneider include :</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Hallucination</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First-person</strong> hallucination (audible thoughts) - auditory hallucinations of the person’s own voice being spoken aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Second-person</strong> hallucination (voices directly addressing the patient) - auditory hallucinations of 1 or more voices talking directly to the person. They can be persecutory, complimentary, or issue commands (command hallucination)</li>\n<li><strong>Third-person</strong> hallucination (voices speaking about the patient) - auditory hallucinations of 1 or more voices speaking about the person. The voice may deliver a running commentary or the voices maybe be arguing/discussing the person.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. <strong>Thought phenomenon</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thought <strong>withdrawal-</strong>  thoughts being actively removed from a person’s mind.</li>\n<li>Thought <strong>insertion-</strong> thoughts inserted into a person’s mind by some external agent.</li>\n<li>Thought <strong>broadcasting-</strong> the sense that a person’s thoughts are experienced by others - the thoughts are made audible or may be experienced by others through telepathy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. <strong>Made phenomenon</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Made <strong>affect-</strong> feelings are imposed on that person by an external agent.</li>\n<li>Made <strong>impulses</strong> or drives - an impulse for action is imposed on a person by some external agent.</li>\n<li>Made <strong>volitional acts</strong> - a person’s actions are controlled by an external agent; the person is a passive participant in the action.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. <strong>Delusional perception</strong>- the person attaches an abnormal significance/meaning to a normal perception, which leads to delusional interpretation. The most common delusion in schizophrenia is the <strong>delusion of persecution</strong>. These persecutory fears of the patient can range from transient doubts to profound convictions that people are plotting to have them tortured or killed.</p>\n<p>5. <strong>Somatic passivity</strong>- tactile or visceral hallucinations that are thought to be imposed on a person's body by some external agent.</p>\n<p>These symptoms are not specific to schizophrenia and may also be present in other psychiatric disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0422","text":"A psychiatric resident is ​assessing the negative sy​mptoms in a case of chron​ic schizophrenia. Which o​f the following is not te​sted?","raw_text":"A psychiatric resident is assessing the negative symptoms in a case of chronic schizophrenia. Which of the following is not tested?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Avol​ition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alo​gia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anhe​donia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ano​mia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anomia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anomia</strong></p><p><strong>Anomia</strong> is <strong>not tested</strong> because it is not a feature of schizophrenia. It is a type of aphasia where there is difficulty in naming of objects. It is common in head trauma, metabolic encephalopathy, and Alzheimer’s disease.</p>\n<p>Symptoms of schizophrenia can be classified as <strong>positive</strong> or <strong>negative.</strong></p>\n<p>Anything abnormal added to a patient's life is a positive symptom and anything normal lost from a patient's life is a negative symptom. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0415","text":"A 39-year-old man has a th​ree-week history of speak​ing to himself and compla​ins of hearing two people​ talking about him. On ex​amination, he has reduced‍ affect and impaired judg​ment. What is the likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old man has a three-week history of speaking to himself and complains of hearing two people talking about him. On examination, he has reduced affect and impaired judgment. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schizophreni​form disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brief psych​otic episode","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizoaffect​ive disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Brief psychotic episode","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Brief psychotic episode</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>brief psychotic episode</strong> (DSM-5), as the symptoms are present for<strong> less than 30 days</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 95\">\n<p class=\"p1\">The diagnosis of brief psychotic disorder in DSM-5 is based on the presence of one or more of the following psychotic symptoms for a period of at least<strong> 1 day but less than 1 month: </strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\">Delusions</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hallucinations</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Thought disorder as evidenced by disorganized speech</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Disorganized or catatonic behavior.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"p1\">DSM-5 specifies that one of the first three symptoms must be present for the diagnosis to be made.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">According to ICD-11, this is classified as acute and transient psychotic disorder, and the duration of the episode does not last for more than 3 months.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0416","text":"A young man presented with​ a 2-week history of audi​tory hallucinations and d​elusions of grandiosity. ​His history is significan​t for severe depression w‍ith psychotic symptoms. H​owever, he has been euthy‍mic for the past month. A​ccording to DSM-5, what w‌ould be the likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A young man presented with a 2-week history of auditory hallucinations and delusions of grandiosity. His history is significant for severe depression with psychotic symptoms. However, he has been euthymic for the past month. According to DSM-5, what would be the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizoaffect​ive disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mood disorder with psychot​ic symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schizophreni​form disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Schizoaffective disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Schizoaffective disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points toward the diagnosis of <strong>schizoaffective disorder. </strong></p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for schizoaffective disorder:  </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 64\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>An uninterrupted period of illness where there is a <strong>mood episode </strong>(depression or mania) <strong>concurrent with psychosis </strong>(delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Delusions </strong>or<strong> hallucinations</strong> (psychotic symptoms) for <strong>at least two weeks</strong> in the <strong>absence</strong> of a <strong>major depressive or manic episode</strong> during the lifetime course of the illness.</li>\n<li><strong>Mood symptoms</strong> present for the <strong>majority</strong> of total illnesses.</li>\n<li>Not due to intake of substances or another medical condition.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Differential diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features: Psychotic symptoms occur <strong>exclusively during mood episodes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Schizophrenia: <strong>Mood</strong> symptoms are <strong>absent</strong> or present for only <strong>brief</strong> periods during the course of the illness <strong>dominated by psychotic symptoms</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: According to<strong> DSM-5 guidelines</strong>, for a definitive <strong>diagnosis of schizoaffective disorder</strong>, there have to be <strong>two psychotic episodes</strong>. One of the psychotic episodes has to be <strong>concurrent</strong> with a severe <strong>affective</strong> (depression or manic) episode. The other psychotic episode should be for two or more weeks in the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>affective</strong> symptoms. And affective symptoms should dominate the major part during the lifetime course of the illness. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0431","text":"A 22-year-old man is broug​ht with complaints of sel​f-mutilating behaviour in​ which he bit off the dis​tal end of his right ring​ finger and left thumb. F‍or the past 6 months, he ​had decreased sleep, audi‍tory hallucinations, soci​al withdrawal, and disorg‌anised behaviour. Investi​gations revealed neutroph‌ilia and lymphocytosis. S‌erum uric acid level is 6‌ mg/dL. HGPRTase activity‌ is normal. What is your ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man is brought with complaints of self-mutilating behaviour in which he bit off the distal end of his right ring finger and left thumb. For the past 6 months, he had decreased sleep, auditory hallucinations, social withdrawal, and disorganised behaviour. Investigations revealed neutrophilia and lymphocytosis. Serum uric acid level is 6 mg/dL. HGPRTase activity is normal. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Van-Gogh​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lesch-Nyha​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pfropf sch​izophrenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Van-Gogh syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Van-Gogh syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>self-mutilation</strong> in a patient with <strong>schizophrenia </strong>is known as <strong>Van Gogh syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Van Gogh was a Dutch painter who suffered from schizophrenia. He cut off his ear during one of his psychotic episodes.</p>\n<p><strong>Pfropf syndrome </strong>is<strong> </strong>schizophrenia associated with <strong>mental retardation</strong>. 'Pfropf' is the German word for 'graft'. Pfrofp schizophrenia literally means schizophrenia grafted on preexisting mental retardation.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"ghr-condition\">Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</span></strong> is characterized by neurological abnormalities, self-mutilative behavior, and the overproduction of uric acid. It occurs due to a complete deficiency of HGPRTase enzyme (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase ).</p>\n<p><strong>Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome </strong>occurs due to<strong> </strong>a partial deficiency of HGPRTase, and is associated with hyperuricemia but no central nervous system manifestations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0424","text":"Which of the following fea​tures would you not expec​t to see in a schizophren​ic patient with catatonia​?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a schizophrenic patient with catatonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cata​plexy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mute​ness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Waxy Fle​xibility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Echop​raxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cataplexy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cataplexy</strong></p><p><strong>Cataplexy</strong> is not a feature of catatonia. It is seen in <strong>narcolepsy.</strong> It involves the sudden loss of muscle tone, with full or partial consciousness.</p>\n<p>Psychomotor disturbance is an essential feature of catatonia. Some of the peculiar features of catatonia are given below:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Stupor:</strong> Absence of psychomotor activity and not actively relating to the environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalepsy:</strong> Passive induction of a posture held against gravity.</li>\n<li><strong>Waxy flexibility:</strong> Slight, even resistance to positioning by examiner.</li>\n<li><strong>Negativism:</strong> Opposition or no response to instructions or external stimuli.</li>\n<li><strong>Posturing:</strong> Spontaneous and active maintenance of a posture against gravity.</li>\n<li><strong>Mannerism:</strong> Odd, circumstantial caricature of normal actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Stereotypy:</strong> Repetitive, abnormally frequent, non-goal-directed movements</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic</strong> <strong>obedience- </strong>commands are followed automatically, irrespective of their nature.</li>\n<li><strong>Grimacing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ambitendency- </strong>due to ambivalence, opposing actions are carried out.</li>\n<li><strong>Echopraxia:</strong> Mimicking another's movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Echolalia:</strong> Mimicking another's speech.</li>\n<li><strong>Mutism:</strong> Very little or no verbal response to instructions or external stimuli.</li>\n<li><strong>Verbigeration-</strong> incomprehensible speech.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Catatonia is a feature of, but <strong>not exclusive</strong> to, <strong>schizophrenia.</strong> It is more common in<strong> psychotic mood disorders</strong> and in patients with <strong>brain injury</strong> and <strong>encephalopathies.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0977","text":"A patient voluntarily gets​ himself in the posture a​s shown and is not changi​ng it. What is it called?​","raw_text":"A patient voluntarily gets himself in the posture as shown and is not changing it. What is it called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cata​plexy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post​uring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Negat​ivism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Waxy fle​xibility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posturing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1ab52d2211e94b309240c6939d2dd358.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posturing</strong></p><p>The finding shown in the above image is called <strong>posturing. </strong>It is the spontaneous and <strong>active</strong> maintenance of a posture <strong>against gravity</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Catalepsy</strong> is a <strong>passive </strong>induction of posture held against gravity.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cataplexy</strong> is a sudden, brief <strong>loss of voluntary muscle tone</strong> triggered by strong emotions such as laughter. The condition is most commonly associated with narcolepsy.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Negativism</strong> is verbal or nonverbal opposition or resistance to outside suggestions and advice. Here, the <strong>patient resists</strong> any effort to be moved or does the opposite of what is asked.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Waxy flexibility</strong> is a condition in which a person maintains the body position into which they are placed (<strong>passive</strong> movement). <strong>Slight resistance</strong> is felt while moving the limbs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5795","text":"A schizophrenic patient wa​s started on an antipsych​otic drug that decreased ​his hallucinations signif​icantly. This effect is d​ue to its action on which‍ dopaminergic pathway?","raw_text":"A schizophrenic patient was started on an antipsychotic drug that decreased his hallucinations significantly. This effect is due to its action on which dopaminergic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesoco​rtical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberoinf​undibular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nigros​triatal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesol​imbic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mesolimbic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mesolimbic</strong></p><p>Antipsychotic drugs act on the <strong>mesolimbic pathway</strong> to reduce <strong>positive symptoms</strong> of schizophrenia such as hallucinations and delusions.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Classification of antipsychotic drugs:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 412px; height: 532px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Property</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.234375px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>First generation  </strong>(Typical)</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.765625px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Second generation</strong> (Atypical)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">D<sub>2</sub> receptor</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.234375px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Preferentially blocked</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.765625px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Selectively blocked</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">5HT<sub>2A</sub> antagonism</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.234375px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Low </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.765625px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">High</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Pathways involved</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.234375px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Mesocortical, mesolimbic, nigrostriatal, tuberoinfundibular, CTZ</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.765625px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Mostly mesolimbic and mesocortical</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Drugs</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.234375px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Haloperidol</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Fluphenazine</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Chlorpromazine</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Thioridazine (least D2 blocking activity among typical agents, markedly anticholinergic)</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.765625px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Risperidone</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Sertindole</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Quetiapine</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Clozapine</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Aripiprazole (partial D<sub>2</sub> agonist)</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6708","text":"A psychotic patient develo​ped voluntary purposeless​ movements and was once o​bserved to be standing st​ill for hours with waxy f​lexibility and negativism‍. What is the appropriate​ medical management?","raw_text":"A psychotic patient developed voluntary purposeless movements and was once observed to be standing still for hours with waxy flexibility and negativism. What is the appropriate medical management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halop​eridol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lora​zepam","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clon​idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propa​nolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lorazepam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lorazepam</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of a <strong>catatonic disorder</strong>. Benzodiazepines such as <strong>lorazepam</strong> are effective in the treatment of catatonia.</p>\n<p><strong>Catatonia </strong>is a syndrome characterized by striking behav­ioral abnormalities such as motoric immobility or excite­ment, negativism, or echolalia (mimicry of speech), or echopraxia (mimicry of movement).</p>\n<p>The clinical picture may include <strong>three or more of the following features:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Stupor:</strong> Absence of psychomotor activity and not actively relating to the environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalepsy:</strong> Passive induction of a posture held against gravity.</li>\n<li><strong>Waxy flexibility:</strong> Slight, even resistance to positioning by examiner.</li>\n<li><strong>Negativism:</strong> Opposition or no response to instructions or external stimuli.</li>\n<li><strong>Posturing:</strong> Spontaneous and active maintenance of a posture against gravity.</li>\n<li><strong>Mannerism:</strong> Odd, circumstantial caricature of normal actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Stereotypy:</strong> Repetitive, abnormally frequent, non-goal-directed movements</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic</strong> <strong>obedience: </strong>Commands are followed automatically, irrespective of their nature.</li>\n<li><strong>Grimacing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ambitendency: </strong>Due to ambivalence, opposing actions are carried out.</li>\n<li><strong>Echopraxia:</strong> Mimicking another's movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Echolalia:</strong> Mimicking another's speech.</li>\n<li><strong>Mutism:</strong> Very little or no verbal response to instructions or external stimuli.</li>\n<li><strong>Verbigeration:</strong> Incomprehensible speech.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Catatonic disorder may occur due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced mood or psychotic illnesses</li>\n<li>Neurologi­cal disorders (e.g., nonconvulsive status epilepticus, and head trauma)</li>\n<li>Infections (e.g., encephalitis)</li>\n<li>Metabolic disturbances (e.g., hepatic encephalopathy, hyponatremia, and hypercalcemia)</li>\n<li>Drugs induced- like corticoste­roids, immunosuppressants, and antipsychotics (i.e., neurolep­tic) agents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management: </strong>The primary treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identifying and treating underlying medical or pharmacological causes. Any offending substances should be removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Benzodiazepines,</strong> especially, lorazepam, are effective in improving the symptoms.</li>\n<li><strong>Electroconvulsive therapy</strong> is the definitive treatment in cases of life-threatening catatonia (if the patient is unable to eat) or if the patient has developed malignant catatonia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0427","text":"A man diagnosed with schiz​ophrenia was on chlorprom​azine and olanzapine for ​the past 6 months. Howeve​r, he is still symptomati​c. What is the next line ‍of management?","raw_text":"A man diagnosed with schizophrenia was on chlorpromazine and olanzapine for the past 6 months. However, he is still symptomatic. What is the next line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haloperi​dol depot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aripip​razole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Risperid​one depot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cloz​apine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clozapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clozapine</strong></p><p><strong>Clozapine</strong> is the drug of choice in <strong>treatment-resistant schizophrenia</strong> and schizophrenia with persistent suicidality.</p>\n<p>Treatment-resistant schizophrenia is the lack of response to two different antipsychotics given at adequate dosages for an adequate duration of <strong>4-6 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>If patients demonstrate even a mild amount of improvement during this period, it may be reasonable to wait before changing a medication, since patients may improve at a <strong>steady rate for 3 to 6 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>Here, the patient’s medication history indicates a poor response to 2 antipsychotic trials, hence clozapine is indicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5056","text":"A patient diagnosed with s​chizophrenia was started ​on risperidone. After tak​ing the medication last n​ight, the patient rushes ​to OPD today with complai‍nts of upwards rolling of​ his eyes. What is the ma‍nagement of the condition​?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with schizophrenia was started on risperidone. After taking the medication last night, the patient rushes to OPD today with complaints of upwards rolling of his eyes. What is the management of the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lora​zepam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Promet​hazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Olanz​apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stop anti​psychotic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Promethazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Promethazine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>oculogyric crisis </strong>and <strong>promethazine</strong> is used for the management.</p>\n<p>Oculogyric crisis refers to the <strong>s</strong><strong>pasm of the extraocular muscles</strong>, usually causing <strong>upward</strong> and outward deviation of the eyes. It is a severe form of <strong>acute dystonia</strong>, an <strong>extrapyramidal side effect</strong> of antipsychotics like risperidone. It can be treated by drugs with antimuscarinic activities like promethazine.</p>\n<p><strong>Dystonia</strong> is <strong>a</strong><strong>cute </strong>in onset and occurs within <strong>1-5 days</strong> of starting treatment. It is more common in the <strong>younger</strong> population. The proposed mechanism of action is <strong>D2 blockade</strong> in the nigrostriatal pathway. There is typical involvement of <strong>head and neck</strong> muscles and presents in a variety of forms, such as <strong>torticollis</strong>, trismus, mouth opening dystonia, <strong>grimacing</strong>, dysarthria<strong>,</strong> <strong>oculogyric crisis</strong>, blepharospasm, and <strong>swallowing difficulties</strong>.</p>\n<p>Risperidone is an atypical antipsychotic with <strong>D2 blocking</strong> but strong <strong>5HT2 antagonistic</strong> activity. Its main side effects include weight gain, hyperglycemia, <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong>, dyslipidemia, fatigue, etc. At low doses, extrapyramidal side effects are less, but can be significant in higher doses. <strong>Extrapyramidal side effects</strong> include;</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parkinsonism</strong>: Features are bradykinesia, rigidity, variable tremor, masked facies, and shuffling gait.</li>\n<li><strong>Acute dystonia</strong>: Which causes spasms of muscles of the tongue, face, <strong>eyes</strong>, neck, back, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome:</strong> Extreme rigidity, fever, unstable blood pressure,myoglobinemia, etc. </li>\n<li><strong>Rabbit syndrome: </strong>Perioral tremors occur as a delayed variant of parkinsonism.<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Lorazepam</strong> is a benzodiazepine. Its adverse effects include <strong>sedation, light-headedness, confusional state, weight gain,</strong> etc.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Olanzapine </strong>is also an atypical antipsychotic. Its adverse effects are d<strong>ry mouth, constipation, weight gain, impaired glucose tolerance</strong> etc. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0430","text":"What is the major cause of​ death in schizophrenia?","raw_text":"What is the major cause of death in schizophrenia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infe​ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sui​cide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drug re​actions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homi​cide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Suicide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Suicide</strong></p><p>A major cause of death in schizophrenia is <strong>suicide</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>leading causes of premature death</strong> among patients with <strong>schizophrenia</strong> at present are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suicide</li>\n<li>Cardiovascular disease</li>\n<li>Accidents</li>\n<li>Common physical diseases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the past, the excess mortality among individuals with schizophrenia was mainly due to communicable diseases such as tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0425","text":"Which of the following pat​ients with schizophrenia ​has the best prognosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with schizophrenia has the best prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 30-year-old with stupor,​ negativism, rigidity, ex​citement, or posturing","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 20-year-old with disorga​nised thinking, flat affe​ct with inappropriate emo​tions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 35-year-old with halluci​nations, delusions of per​secution and grandeur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 25-year-old with insidio​us onset of negative symp​toms without any hallucin​ations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 30-year-old with stupor, negativism, rigidity, excitement, or posturing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 30-year-old with stupor, negativism, rigidity, excitement, or posturing</strong></p><p>Patient with stupor, negativism, rigidity, excitement, or posturing suggests <strong>catatonic</strong> <strong>schizophrenia.</strong> It has the <strong>best prognosis</strong> among all types of schizophrenia.</p>\n<p><strong>Simple</strong> schizophrenia has the <strong>worst</strong> prognosis.</p>\n<p>Types of schizophrenia:</p>\n<p><strong>Hebephrenic schizophrenia (disorganized)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early-onset</li>\n<li><strong>Disorganized behavior and thoughts</strong>, contact with reality is poor</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Social behavior and their <strong>emotional responses</strong> are <strong>inappropriate</strong></li>\n<li>Bad prognosis</li>\n<li>Term coined by <strong>Hecker</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Paranoid schizophrenia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common type.</li>\n<li>Usual onset in 3<sup>rd</sup>decade</li>\n<li>Characterized by <strong>delusions</strong> <strong>of persecution, grandeur</strong>, and control, etc</li>\n<li>Can be associated with <strong>hallucination</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Catatonic schizophrenia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marked disturbance in motor function</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">May involve <strong>stupor, negativism, rigidity, excitement, or posturing</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The alteration between extremes of excitement and stupor</li>\n<li>Best prognosis</li>\n<li>Term coined by <strong>Kahlbaum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Simple schizophrenia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The primary symptom is withdrawal from social and work-related situations</li>\n<li>Characterized by a gradual, <strong>insidious </strong>loss of drive and ambition</li>\n<li><strong>Do not experience</strong> persistent <strong>hallucinations</strong> or <strong>delusions</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Poor prognosis</strong> (simple &gt; hebephrenic)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">These subtypes were dropped in DSM V. In ICD-11, they are classified under the 'schizophrenia, episode unspecified' category, but may be removed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6704","text":"Which of the following is ​not a good prognostic fac​tor in schizophrenia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a good prognostic factor in schizophrenia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive​ symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Late age​ of onset","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insidious onset of symptom​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with depression​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insidious onset of symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insidious onset of symptoms</strong></p><p><strong>Insidious onset</strong> of symptoms is a<strong> poor prognostic factor</strong> of schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>Prognostic factors of Schizophrenia: </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Good Prognostic factors </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Poor prognostic factors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acute onset</td>\n<td>Insidious onset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Late-onset</td>\n<td>Early-onset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Presence of a stressor</td>\n<td>Absence of a stressor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short duration &lt; 6 months</td>\n<td>Duration &gt;2 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Presence of depression</td>\n<td>No depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive symptoms</td>\n<td>Negative symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Family history of mood disorder</td>\n<td>Family history of schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Married, female sex</td>\n<td>Single, male sex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Catatonic schizophrenia</td>\n<td>Hebephrenic schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type 1 schizophrenia</td>\n<td>Type 2 schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>No remissions in 3 years  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>History of perinatal trauma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>History of assaultiveness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>T.J.Crow</strong> classified schizophrenia into two separate types  :</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Type 1</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Type 2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive symptoms present </td>\n<td>Negative symptoms present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>More responsive to treatment</td>\n<td>Less responsive to treatment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No demonstrable neuropathology</td>\n<td>Demonstrable neuropathology (CT/MRI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good prognosis</td>\n<td>Poor prognosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5797","text":"A 19-year-old woman with s​chizoaffective disorder a​nd was prescribed a drug.​ Soon after initiation of​ therapy, she developed g​alactorrhea and dysmenorr‍hea, and her prolactin le​vel was 171.6ng/mL. Which‍ drug was most likely pre​scribed?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old woman with schizoaffective disorder and was prescribed a drug. Soon after initiation of therapy, she developed galactorrhea and dysmenorrhea, and her prolactin level was 171.6ng/mL. Which drug was most likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zipra​sidone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olanz​apine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rispe​ridone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cloz​apine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Risperidone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Risperidone</strong></p><p><strong>Risperidone</strong> causes the maximum rise in prolactin levels<strong> </strong>and has the <strong>highest potential</strong> to cause <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong> among the <strong>atypical antipsychotics.</strong></p>\n<div>Risperidone is an antagonist of <strong>5-HT<sub>2A</sub></strong> and<strong> D2</strong> receptors. The combined use of risperidone and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI) can also result in a significant elevation of prolactin with galactorrhea and breast enlargement.</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5798","text":"A 50-year-old man with typ​e 2 diabetes was brought ​to the OPD by his wife af​ter he began talking abou​t aliens who were trying ​to steal his soul. He oft‍en stops talking mid-sent​ence and frequently scans‍ the room for aliens. His​ wife reported he started‌ expressing these ideas a​ few months ago, but they‌ have become more severe ‌and he had become isolate‌d from his peers. Which o‌f the following drugs sho‌uld not be used in this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes was brought to the OPD by his wife after he began talking about aliens who were trying to steal his soul. He often stops talking mid-sentence and frequently scans the room for aliens. His wife reported he started expressing these ideas a few months ago, but they have become more severe and he had become isolated from his peers. Which of the following drugs should not be used in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olanz​apine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rispe​ridone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Queti​apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aripri​prazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Olanzapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Olanzapine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of schizophrenia and given the patient's history of<strong> type 2 diabetes, olanzapine</strong> should not be prescribed as it has the <strong>highest potential</strong> to cause weight gain and <strong>metabolic syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>Clozapine also has a high incidence of metabolic syndrome and weight gain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3242","text":"A woman in her mid-twentie​s is on olanzapine and is​ very concerned about gai​ning weight. Which of the​ following is false?","raw_text":"A woman in her mid-twenties is on olanzapine and is very concerned about gaining weight. Which of the following is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In the first 3 months, her​ weight needs to be monit​ored weekly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aripiprazole can be used a​s an alternative treatmen​t if significant weight g​ain occurs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Behavioural and lifestyle ​modifications are not use​ful in reducing antipsych​otic induced weight gain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metformin can be used to m​oderate antipsychotic ind​uced weight gain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Behavioural and lifestyle modifications are not useful in reducing antipsychotic induced weight gain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Behavioural and lifestyle modifications are not useful in reducing antipsychotic induced weight gain</strong></p><p>Behavioral and lifestyle modifications are very useful in reducing antipsychotic induced weight gain.</p>\n<p>Weight gain is monitored as follows (include waist size and BMI if possible):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weekly measurement of weight for the first 3 months</li>\n<li>Then 3 monthly for the first year</li>\n<li>Yearly measurement thereafter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3354","text":"A schizophrenic patient wa​s started on an antipsych​otic drug that decreased ​his hallucinations signif​icantly. Three days after​ starting the medication,‍ he developed torticollis​ and orofacio-lingual mov‍ements. What is your diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A schizophrenic patient was started on an antipsychotic drug that decreased his hallucinations significantly. Three days after starting the medication, he developed torticollis and orofacio-lingual movements. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Akat​hisia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute muscu​lar dystonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tardive d​yskinesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rabbit ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute muscular dystonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute muscular dystonia</strong></p><p>The patient has developed<strong> acute muscular dystonia</strong> (spasm of muscles of tongue, face, neck, and back) which is an extrapyramidal side effect of haloperidol. This occurs within 1-5 days of drug intake.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2902","text":"A patient on fluphenazine ​feels increasingly restle​ss, agitated, and dysphor​ic and has a severe urge ​to move for the past 1 we​ek. Which of the followin‍g can not be used in his ​management?","raw_text":"A patient on fluphenazine feels increasingly restless, agitated, and dysphoric and has a severe urge to move for the past 1 week. Which of the following can not be used in his management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ropin​irole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Propa​nolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diphenh​ydramine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzt​ropine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ropinirole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ropinirole</strong></p><p>The patient has developed <strong>akathisia</strong>, which is a side effect of antipsychotic medications. It is <strong>treated</strong> with <strong>propranolol,</strong> <strong>diphenhydramine</strong> (Benadryl), and <strong>benztropine</strong>. Ropinirole is not used for treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>Ropinirole</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>restless leg syndrome</strong>, which resembles akathisia without a history of antipsychotic medication intake.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3224","text":"A schizophrenic patient is​ rushed to the casualty a​fter attempting suicide. ​Which of the following dr​ugs would benefit her the​ most?","raw_text":"A schizophrenic patient is rushed to the casualty after attempting suicide. Which of the following drugs would benefit her the most?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Queti​apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olanz​apine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rispe​ridone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cloz​apine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clozapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clozapine</strong></p><p><strong>Clozapine</strong> is the only drug amongst the atypical antipsychotics that has been approved to be used for a <strong>schizophrenic</strong> patient with an <strong>increased risk</strong> of <strong>suicide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3350","text":"A young man was brought to​ the OPD by his best frie​nd. He was apparently nor​mal half a year back afte​r which he has become inc​reasingly isolated, lost ‍his job, and shouts loudl​y sometimes trying to get‍ rid of the voices in his​ head. He was started on ‌olanzapine after a diagno​sis of schizophrenia was ‌made. What is the minimum‌ duration he should conti‌nue the drug for?","raw_text":"A young man was brought to the OPD by his best friend. He was apparently normal half a year back after which he has become increasingly isolated, lost his job, and shouts loudly sometimes trying to get rid of the voices in his head.  He was started on olanzapine after a diagnosis of schizophrenia was made. What is the minimum duration he should continue the drug for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18 m​onths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 years</strong></p><p>After the <strong>initial diagnosis</strong> of schizophrenia,<strong> </strong>antipsychotics should be continued for at least<strong> 2 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>If there is a <strong>history of relapse</strong>, treatment should be continued for at least for <strong>5 years.</strong> If there are <strong>multiple relapses,</strong> the patient is treated <strong>indefinitely.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-Fe8tLnI","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"6_cardiovascular_system","title":"6.Cardiovascular System","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3921","text":"On a chest x-ray, which of‍ the following structures​ does not make up the rig​ht cardiac border?","raw_text":"On a chest x-ray, which of the following structures does not make up the right cardiac border?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‍VC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‍VC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right ‍atrium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right v‍entricle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right ventricle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6328c1f36af1483aa0180536b979f92cx1024x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b37192fc622444aa9adee570fa9e428bx796x641.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right ventricle</strong></p><p>The<strong> right ventricle</strong> does not form the right cardiac border as seen on a chest x-ray.</p>\n<p>The structures forming the cardiac borders are shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The heart has <strong>four</strong> <strong>borders.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 142px; width: 289.21875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.7812px; text-align: left;\">Cardiac border</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.844px; text-align: left;\">Structures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.7812px; text-align: left;\">Superior</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.844px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right atrium</li>\n<li>Left atrium</li>\n<li>Great vessels</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.7812px; text-align: left;\">Right</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.844px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right atrium</li>\n<li>SVC</li>\n<li>IVC</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.7812px; text-align: left;\">Left </td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.844px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left atrial appendage</li>\n<li>Left ventricle</li>\n<li>Aortic knuckle</li>\n<li>Subclavian artery</li>\n<li>Pulmonary artery segment</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.7812px; text-align: left;\">Inferior</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.844px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left ventricle</li>\n<li>Right ventricle</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The<strong> right</strong> <strong>ventricle</strong> is the most <strong>anteriorly</strong> placed cardiac chamber.</p>\n<p>The image below shows an MRI of the heart and its chambers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4258","text":"Identify the structure mar‍ked in the CT scan shown ​below:","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked in the CT scan shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descendi‍ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ascendi‍ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ‍vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right pulmo‍nary artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior vena cava","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43966f30310a4b5dbc3d6503e02a83b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b234e0f93f24261b48cb390197bf6c9x1280x964.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior vena cava</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the given CT thorax image is the <strong>superior vena cava</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts all the major structures labelled in the CT thorax image:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3927","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‍ with a 5-day history of ​cough and fever. Chest X-​ray shows obliteration of‌ the right cardiac border‌. Which anatomical site i‍s likely involved in the ​pathology?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with a 5-day history of cough and fever. Chest X-ray shows obliteration of the right cardiac border. Which anatomical site is likely involved in the pathology?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right lo‍wer lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right ‍atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right mi‍ddle lobe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right v‍entricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right middle lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a83317ddbc9a42e7b2b7261e28690774x1024x472.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right middle lobe</strong></p><p><strong>Obliteration </strong>of the<strong> right cardiac</strong> border on chest X-ray indicates pathology in the <strong>right middle lobe</strong>.</p>\n<p>This loss of clear demarcation between the normal lung and its surrounding soft structures (heart, diaphragm) is called the<strong> silhouette sign</strong>. It occurs due to <strong>loss of contrast</strong> between lungs and soft tissue when the lung parenchyma is no longer filled with air. This sign may be seen in consolidation, atelectasis, or mass lesions.</p>\n<p>In the following images:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left image - red line indicates the normal demarcation between the right heart border and middle lobe of the right lung</li>\n<li>Right image - loss of demarcation (positive silhouette sign) due to right middle lobe consolidation</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Positive silhouette sign and corresponding anatomical site of pathology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right heart border - </strong>right middle lobe or medial right lower lobe</li>\n<li><strong>Left heart border - </strong>lingula segments of the left upper lobe</li>\n<li>Left hemidiaphragm or descending aorta - left lower lobe</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic knuckle - </strong>left upper lobe (posterior segment)</li>\n<li>Right hemidiaphragm - right lower lobe</li>\n<li>Right paratracheal stripe - right upper lobe</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3929","text":"A 35-year-old man presents‍ with a 1-week history of​ low-grade fever and prog​ressive chest pain. Chest‌ X-ray revealed the follo‌wing. Considering the sym‍ptoms and imaging, what i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presents with a 1-week history of low-grade fever and progressive chest pain. Chest X-ray revealed the following. Considering the symptoms and imaging, what is the most likely diagnosis? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericardia‍l effusion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneu‍monia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coarctatio‍n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic d‍issection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pericardial effusion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2ac833752db43eb9f4b84f661cec0d0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbe4acec9d7546cc8eeafdfe219216a1x510x535.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/482c0990edbc4f918157835536c31c27x1024x1024.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pericardial effusion</strong></p><p>The given image shows a globular enlargement of the cardiac shadow, called the <strong>money</strong> <strong>bag </strong>appearance. Considering the above symptoms and the chest X-ray findings, the most likely diagnosis is <strong>pericardial</strong> <strong>effusion</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The investigation of choice is <strong>e</strong><strong>chocardiography</strong>. However, the following x-ray findings are also characteristic for pericardial effusion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>AP view - money bag or <strong>leather bottle-shaped heart</strong></li>\n<li>Lateral view -<strong> Oreo cookie sign</strong> (fluid is seen between two layers of fat)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the following image, <strong>pericardial fluid</strong> (yellow asterisk) outlined by <strong>paracardial fat</strong> (blue arrows) and <strong>epicardial fat</strong> (orange arrows) is called the Oreo cookie sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3930","text":"What is the earliest sign ‍of left atrial enlargemen​t on a chest x-ray ?","raw_text":"What is the earliest sign of left atrial enlargement on a chest x-ray ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Straightening of right car‍diac border","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enlargement of left atrial‍ appendage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of double atrial ‍shadow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevation of left main bro‍nchus and widening of car​ina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enlargement of left atrial appendage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc9f03a621aa4e91a5fabdf444107afdx1069x699.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5d5ded10df847af8a9ca993699a6317x753x785.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enlargement of left atrial appendage</strong></p><p><strong>Enlargement</strong> of the <strong>left atrial appendage</strong> is the <strong>earliest</strong> <strong>sign</strong> of left atrial enlargement (<strong>LAE</strong>) on a chest x-ray. <strong> </strong>It is<strong> </strong>seen as a fullness below the pulmonary artery. </p>\n<p>Other radiographic signs seen in LAE include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The third mogul sign represents an enlargement of the left atrial appendage along the upper left cardiac border, leading to <strong>straightening of the left heart border.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Posterior displacement</strong> of the <strong>oesophagus</strong> - seen on the lateral view of the barium swallow study.</li>\n<li>Elevation of left main bronchus and widening of the carina.</li>\n<li><strong>Double</strong> <strong>density </strong>sign or double atrial shadow - the enlarged LA extends towards the right cardiac border, hence, both RA and LA borders can be seen distinctly over the right heart border (as shown below):</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The X-ray below demonstrates straightening of the left cardiac border and widening of the carina, with the carinal angle becoming more obtuse, reflecting left atrial enlargement.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> The causes of left atrial enlargement are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital: Ventricular septal defect , Patent ductus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Acquired: Mitral valvular disease, left ventricular failure, left atrial myxoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3932","text":"A 5-year-old male child pr‍esents with cyanosis that​ worsens during activity ​and progressive breathles‌sness. Chest X-ray is don‌e and shown below. What i‍s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old male child presents with cyanosis that worsens during activity and progressive breathlessness. Chest X-ray is done and shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetralogy‍ of Fallot","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coarctatio‍n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transposition of great art‍eries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ebstein'‍s anomaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetralogy of Fallot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac1b2ebb924746c9adafcf91b10bf3de.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eebe437a1c1a489faeca2d364b65f396x510x558.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetralogy of Fallot</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>boot-shaped heart, </strong>which is seen in the Tetralogy of Fallot<strong> </strong>(<strong>TOF</strong>). It shows an <strong>upturned </strong>cardiac<strong> apex</strong> due to <strong>right ventricular hypertrophy</strong> and a concave pulmonary arterial segment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> TOF is a <strong>cyanotic congenital </strong>heart condition and has <strong>four </strong>characteristic components: Ventricular septal defect, right ventricular outflow tract obstruction (pulmonary stenosis), overriding aorta, right ventricular hypertrophy (late feature).</p>\n<p>Other radiographic features seen in TOF are pulmonary oligaemia (due to pulmonary stenosis), normal size heart without heart failure and right-sided aortic arch (in 25% of cases)</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3933","text":"A baby is brought to the c‍linic as he turns blue ev​ery time he cries. Chest ​x-ray reveals the followi‌ng. Which of the followin‌g best describes the unde‍rlying condition?","raw_text":"A baby is brought to the clinic as he turns blue every time he cries. Chest x-ray reveals the following. Which of the following best describes the underlying condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrialization of the ventr‍icle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right outflow tract obstru‍ction with overriding of ​aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormal pulmonary vein dr‍ainage into systemic veno​us circulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Single great artery for sy‍stemic and pulmonary circ​ulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrialization of the ventricle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6e30b9c62a7477381b4f02e1ee00fa2.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3324711ee3594107909dafbfb62febf4x510x487.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrialization of the ventricle</strong></p><p>Based on the clinical scenario and the X-ray showing a <strong>box-shaped </strong>cardiac silhouette <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">caused by right atrial enlargement, this is typically seen in</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> Ebstein's anomaly</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">. </span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Ebstein anomaly is characterized by an <strong>abnormal tricuspid</strong> <strong>valve</strong> that is displaced downwards into the right ventricle. Hence, a part of the right ventricle is now located above the tricuspid valve. This is called <strong>atrialization of the ventricle</strong>. There can be associated tricuspid regurgitation with or without stenosis.</p>\n<p>X-ray findings depend on the degree to which the tricuspid valve is displaced downwards. The features are an <strong>enlarged right atrium</strong> which causes severe right-sided cardiomegaly, giving rise to a box-shaped cardiac shadow and pulmonary oligemia. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Pulmonary stenosis, ventricular septal defect (VSD), aorta overriding the VSD and right ventricular hypertrophy are the defining features of <strong>Tetralogy of Fallot</strong>, a cyanotic congenital heart disease. X-ray findings show characteristic 'coeur-en-sabot' or <strong>boot-shaped heart</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Pulmonary veins returning to the <strong>right atrium</strong> or <strong>systemic venous circulation</strong> instead of the left atrium, define <strong>total anomalous pulmonary venous circulation</strong>, a congenital cardiac malformation, with <strong>supracardiac</strong> variety being the most common. As a result, a large supracardiac shadow can be seen with normal cardiac shadow and gives a characteristic pattern of <strong>snowman appearance/ figure of 8</strong> on chest X-ray.</p>\n<p>Option D: Failure of the embryonic common truncus arteriosus to divide into the aorta and pulmonary artery characterises <strong>truncus arteriosus</strong>, a cyanotic congenital heart anomaly. A single great artery arises from the heart, supplying both the pulmonary and systemic circulation. This condition is associated with <strong>pulmonary plethora,</strong> unlike tetralogy of Fallot and is characterised by the <strong>sitting duck appearance </strong>of the heart on X-ray.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3934","text":"An infant is brought with ‍poor feeding and lethargy​. Chest X-ray reveals an ​egg-on-string appearance.‌ Which of the following c‌onditions is this finding‍ most suggestive of?","raw_text":"An infant is brought with poor feeding and lethargy. Chest X-ray reveals an egg-on-string appearance. Which of the following conditions is this finding most suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transposition of great art‍eries","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ‍venous return","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endocardial ‍cusion defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Partial anomalous pulmonar‍y venous return","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transposition of great arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c2516a5abcf47d9a2a78e2396a243cax510x670.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transposition of great arteries</strong></p><p>The <strong>egg-on-string</strong> or <strong>egg-on-the-side </strong>appearance on chest X-ray is seen in dextro-transposition of great arteries (<strong>d</strong><strong>-TGA</strong>). This appearance is due to altered cardiac contours as a result of cardiomegaly, as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In d-TGA, the <strong>aorta</strong> arises from the <strong>right</strong> <strong>ventricle,</strong> whereas the <strong>pulmonary trunk</strong> arises from the <strong>left</strong> <strong>ventricle</strong>. d-TGA is also called uncorrected TGA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3936","text":"A 61-year-old woman presen‍ted with chest pain and f​atigue. The chest radiogr​aph is shown below. What ‌is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old woman presented with chest pain and fatigue. The chest radiograph is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericardia‍l effusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constrictive‍ pericarditis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary artery hypertens‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic obstructive c‍ardiomyopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constrictive pericarditis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c2e0fb7f7d74410b355691759198b27.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constrictive pericarditis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>calcified pericardium</strong>, called an<strong> egg-in-cup</strong> appearance. It is typically seen in <strong>c</strong><strong>onstrictive pericarditis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In constrictive pericarditis, there is fibrous or calcific constrictive thickening of the anterior and lateral pericardium that prevents normal diastolic filling of the heart. The resulting <strong>diastolic dysfunction</strong> leads to features of right heart failure. The thickness of the pericardium is best assessed on MRI.</p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>egg-in-cup</strong> (or ball-on-tee) appearance is also a urographic finding seen in <strong>r</strong><strong>enal papillary necrosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3938","text":"Hilar dance was seen on fl‍uoroscopy of an infant wi​th suspected congenital h​eart disease. What is the‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Hilar dance was seen on fluoroscopy of an infant with suspected congenital heart disease. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‍tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‍septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‍s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetralogy‍ of Fallot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e17ae2c4b26467e8f9823d175671e21x1280x1274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p><strong>Hilar dance</strong> on fluoroscopy is seen in patients with an <strong>atrial septal defect</strong> (<strong>ASD</strong>), due to vigorous<strong> pulmonary artery pulsations</strong>.</p>\n<p>ASD is characterised by an <strong>abnormal opening</strong> in the atrial septum, allowing communication between the right and left atria. There are <strong>four</strong> <strong>types</strong> based on their location within the septum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secundum ASD - <strong>most common</strong>, usually an isolated abnormality</li>\n<li>Primum ASD - associated with abnormal mitral valve leaflet (partial AV septal defect)</li>\n<li>Sinus venous - associated with anomalous right pulmonary venous return to the SVC or RA</li>\n<li>Coronary sinus type ASD - also called unroofed coronary sinus</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3945","text":"Sitting-duck appearance of‍ the heart on chest X-ray​ is seen in patients with​ which of the following?","raw_text":"Sitting-duck appearance of the heart on chest X-ray is seen in patients with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistent truncus arterio‍sus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transposition of great ves‍sels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetralogy‍ of Fallot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary artery hypertens‍ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Persistent truncus arteriosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81ee174574524a978f9d6854d48706cex1280x1260.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c3acc75aa994cc680debacb9a48410bx1280x1260.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5bf215b88634cc79288b95c4d1a4552x1200x1821.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Persistent truncus arteriosus</strong></p><p><strong>Persistent truncus arteriosus </strong>is characterized by the <strong>sitting duck</strong> appearance of the<strong> heart</strong> on X-ray.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Truncus arteriosus is a <strong>cyanotic congenital</strong> heart anomaly. A single great artery arises from the heart, due to a <strong>failure </strong>of the<strong> division </strong>of the embryonic<strong> common truncus arteriosus</strong>. Single trunk supplies both the pulmonary and systemic circulation, instead of a separate aorta and a pulmonary trunk.</p>\n<p>The X-ray findings are similar to ToF. However, <strong>pulmonary plethora </strong>is seen in this condition, as opposed to pulmonary oligemia in ToF. This is because the pulmonary artery originates from the truncus, which is part of the systemic circulation. </p>\n<p>The following image shows persistent truncus arteriosus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3953","text":"What is the diagnosis base‍d on the following x-ray?​","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis based on the following x-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endocardial cushion defect‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ‍venous circulation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Supravalvular aortic steno‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary pulmonary artery h‍ypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Total anomalous pulmonary venous circulation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8c2a70afa77427d903206359499ccce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/983b47ffdc5c47dc9956e7bd94da3facx510x494.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Total anomalous pulmonary venous circulation</strong></p><p>The given image shows the<strong> snowman sign</strong> or <strong>figure-of-8</strong> or <strong>cottage loaf</strong> appearance. This is seen in supra-cardiac<strong> total anomalous pulmonary venous connection </strong>(<strong>TAPVC</strong>).</p>\n<p>The image below shows the snowman appearance of the heart seen in TAPVC.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>TAPVC is a <strong>cyanotic</strong> type of <strong>congenital </strong>heart disease with abnormal drainage anatomy of the entire pulmonary venous system. In obstructive TAPVC, there is a normal-sized heart with severe <strong>pulmonary venous hypertension</strong>. This results in the ground-glass appearance of the lungs.</p>\n<p>In TAPVC, all systemic and pulmonary venous blood enters the right atrium and nothing drains into the left atrium. A <strong>right-to-left shunt</strong> in the form of a patent foramen ovale (PFO) or ASD is<strong> required for survival.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0670","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‍ts with persistent hypert​ension, chest pain and cr​amping in her legs, parti‌cularly during exertion. ‌A chest radiograph was ta‍ken, which reveals the co​ndition, as shown in the ​image below. What is the ‍likely​ underlying cause ‌of her hypertension?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with persistent hypertension, chest pain and cramping in her legs, particularly during exertion. A chest radiograph was taken, which reveals the condition, as shown in the image below. What is the likely​ underlying cause of her hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarctatio‍n of aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Essential h‍ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ‍aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortaar‍teritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coarctation of aorta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/db6795bb8e974e029c718a031ea7fc9e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7fffb4172ef4d8db11123e94ae98643x510x660.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coarctation of aorta</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and the image showing the <strong>figure of 3 sign</strong> point towards <strong>c</strong><strong>oarctation of aorta </strong>as the cause of hypertension in this patient. </p>\n<p>The figure of 3 sign refers to the indentation of the aorta at the coarctation site. There is pre and post-stenotic dilatation along with left paramediastinal shadow, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other chest X-ray features in coarctation of the aorta are dilated left subclavian artery, dilated ascending aorta and <strong>n</strong><strong>otching of ribs</strong> 4-8 due to rib erosion by dilated collateral vessels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3942","text":"A radiologist observes the‍ following finding on a p​atient's X-ray. Which con​dition is it seen in?","raw_text":"A radiologist observes the following finding on a patient's  X-ray. Which condition is it seen in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarctatio‍n of aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marfan's‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple‍ myeloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coarctation of aorta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c178ee18abbc429a95a35dc23bbe88f3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71d0357800424aeea41702bae86c38f0x510x660.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coarctation of aorta</strong></p><p>The X-ray shows inferior<strong> rib notching</strong>. It is seen in <strong>coarctation of aorta</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common location of the coarctation is just beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery. The following radiograph findings are seen in this condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior rib notching (<strong>Roesler sign</strong>):\n<ul>\n<li>It occurs due to <strong>erosion</strong> of inferior margins of ribs by dilated intercostal<strong> collateral vessels</strong> that are formed to bypass the coarctation </li>\n<li>It is a <strong>late feature</strong> and is unusual in patients &lt; 5 years of age</li>\n<li>Ribs 4-8 are commonly notched and ribs 1-2<sup> </sup>are usually spared</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Figure of 3 </strong>or<strong> </strong>reverse 3 sign refers to an abnormality in the contour of the aorta, as shown below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes for inferior rib notching (<strong>Roesler sign</strong>) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unilateral:\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavian artery occlusion.</li>\n<li>Coarctation of aorta involving left subclavian or anomalous right subclavian artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bilateral:\n<ul>\n<li>Coarctation of aorta, occlusion, aortitis</li>\n<li>Takayasu’s disease and atheroma of subclavian artery</li>\n<li>Pulmonary oligemia (ToF, pulmonary atresia, stenosis)</li>\n<li>SVC or IVC obstruction</li>\n<li>AV malformation</li>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis type 1</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3939","text":"The patient's chest radiog‍raph reveals the finding ​shown below. Which of the​ following can be exclude‌d from the differential d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"The patient's chest radiograph reveals the finding shown below. Which of the following can be excluded from the differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‍ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‍us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofib‍romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coarctatio‍n of aorta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coarctation of aorta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66701647bf854d02898a3e73ef08ee4b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coarctation of aorta</strong></p><p>The given image shows superior rib notching. <strong>Coarctation </strong>of aorta is associated with <strong>inferior</strong> <strong>rib notching</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyperparathyroidism and <strong>neurofibromatosis</strong> cause <strong>both superior and inferior</strong> rib notching.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>superior</strong> <strong>rib notching</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal finding in the elderly</li>\n<li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></li>\n<li>Marfan's syndrome</li>\n<li>Paraplegics and poliomyelitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3958","text":"Pruning of pulmonary arter‍ies is seen in which of t​he following conditions?","raw_text":"Pruning of pulmonary arteries is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary h‍ypertension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary‍ embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmona‍ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ‍infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulmonary hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78f7f81bc1f04f4ea823bae7b3045af1x1280x1298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulmonary hypertension</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary</strong> <strong>artery</strong> <strong>hypertension</strong> is associated with <strong>peripheral pruning </strong>of pulmonary vessels.</p>\n<p>This means that the <strong>blood vessels </strong>appear<strong> cut off </strong>before they reach the<strong> outer 1/3 of the lung. </strong>Hence,<strong> </strong>the edge of the lung fields is <strong>darker than usual,</strong> and the central area is often whiter.</p>\n<p>Plain radiographic findings in pulmonary artery hypertension include elevated cardiac apex due to right ventricular hypertrophy, enlarged right atrium, prominent pulmonary outflow tract, enlarged pulmonary arteries, and <strong>peripheral pruning.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows Chest X-ray findings in pulmonary hypertension:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0903","text":"Plethoric lung fields are ‍seen in:","raw_text":"Plethoric lung fields are seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total Anomalous Pulmonary ‍Venous Connection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspi‍d atresia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ebstein‍ anomaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrology‍ of Fallot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection</strong></p><p><strong>Plethoric lung fields</strong> are seen in<strong> Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection </strong>(TAPVC). In this condition, pulmonary venous obstruction results in pulmonary arterial hypertension, resulting in plethoric lung fields.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px; width: 456px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84.7734px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.766px;\"><strong>Increased</strong> pulmonary blood flow (pulmonary plethora)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.945px;\"><strong>Decreased </strong>pulmonary blood flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84.7891px;\">Acyanotic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.805px;\">Ventricular septal defect, Atrial septal defect, Patent ductus arteriosus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.977px; text-align: center;\"> —</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84.7969px;\">Cyanotic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">TAPVC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168px;\">Ebstein anomaly, Tetrology of Fallot, Tricuspid atresia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4008","text":"A 50-year-old patient has ‍come with sudden onset of​ chest pain and his CT sc​an shows the following pi‌cture. What is the diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient has come with sudden onset of chest pain and his CT scan shows the following picture. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary‍ embolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic d‍issection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ‍aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myocardial‍ infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aortic dissection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed3d3df71e8d4aa6861785a8933d6bed.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/efaaf6fd415d4ed1bafe29e6781b9bfex512x187.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b826d74b9c348e6a1bc84eb4ab52fa6x258x245.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aortic dissection</strong></p><p>The given CT scan showing a <strong>double lumen</strong> and dilatation of the aorta is suggestive of an <strong>aortic</strong> <strong>dissection. </strong></p>\n<p>In this condition, blood enters the space below the intima of the aorta. This results in the formation of a <strong>true lumen</strong> and <strong>false lumen</strong>. The true lumen is often the smaller of the two because of being compressed by the false lumen, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>beak sign</strong> in aortic dissection is a reliable sign to identify the <strong>false lumen</strong>. It refers to the acute angle formed at the edge of the false lumen in aortic dissection in axial cross-section. It is formed by the borders of the outer aortic wall and the intimal flap, and maybe partially thrombosed (blunted beak). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0671","text":"The appearance of USG at s‍aphenofemoral junction, s​howing common femoral vei​n, common femoral artery ‌and the great saphenous v‌ein is called as:","raw_text":"The appearance of USG at saphenofemoral junction, showing common femoral vein, common femoral artery and the great saphenous vein is called as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mickey m‍ouse sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Strin‍g sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tillau‍x sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stemme‍r sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mickey mouse sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c83353ec4cb34473b6ecd7aeb21023c0x1280x1750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mickey mouse sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal anatomy</strong> of the femoral vessels and great saphenous vein at the <strong>saphenofemoral</strong> <strong>junction</strong> on USG is called the<strong> mickey mouse sign.</strong></p>\n<p>The given images below show the mickey mouse sign (FV- femoral vein; FA- femoral artery; GSV- great saphenous vein).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B<strong>: String sign</strong> (of Kantor) on barium studies appears as a thin, thread-like stream of contrast passing through the stenosed bowel segment, particularly in Crohn's disease.  </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Tillaux sign</strong> is said to be positive if the mass/swelling is mobile perpendicular to the mesentery attachment line. Tillaux’s sign is seen in a mesenteric cyst.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Stemmer's sign</strong> is the inability to tent the skin at the base of the toes due to skin thickening, as seen in lymphedema. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3956","text":"A 57-year-old woman with c‍hest pain was diagnosed w​ith acute myocardial infa​rction. What test can det‌ect areas of reversible m‌yocardial ischemia in thi‍s patient?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old woman with chest pain was diagnosed with acute myocardial infarction. What test can detect areas of reversible myocardial ischemia in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coronary a‍ngiography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MUGA‍ Scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thalli‍um scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Resting echo‍cardiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thallium scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thallium scan</strong></p><p>A<strong> thallium</strong> <strong>scan</strong> is performed to detect <strong>reversible myocardial</strong> <strong>ischemia</strong>. It evaluates myocardial perfusion both at rest and during stress (exercise/pharmacological). </p>\n<p>In <strong>reversible</strong> ischemia, a <strong>cold</strong> spot is seen during exercise or stress due to reduced perfusion, but <strong>normal</strong> perfusion is observed at rest. Conversely, <strong>irreversible</strong> infarction is indicated by<strong> cold spot</strong> during rest and stress/exercise. </p>\n<p>In technetium and pyrophosphate scanning, infarct appears as a <strong>hot</strong> spot.</p>\n<p><strong>MUGA </strong>(multiple gated acquisition) scan is a nuclear imaging test that can evaluate the <strong>function</strong> of the<strong> ventricles.</strong> It is used for informed diagnostic intervention in heart failure. Its specific uses are in evaluating the degree of myocardial damage following myocardial infarction and in assessing cardiac dysfunction in patients on cardiotoxic drugs like anthracyclines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zmek-9Ab","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"6_trauma___spinal__thoracic_and_abdominal_injuries","title":"6.Trauma   Spinal, Thoracic And Abdominal Injuries","num_questions":33,"total_marks":132,"duration":43,"time_per_question":78.18,"questions":[{"id":"MB9154","text":"All of the following can b‌e done to transport a pol‍ytrauma patient who is co​nscious except?","raw_text":"All of the following can be done to transport a polytrauma patient who is conscious except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strap their thorax, abdome‌n and lower limbs on a st‍retcher","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Place in prone position to‌ prevent aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Put patient on lateral pos‌ition and shift him immed‍iately","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Roll the patient onto a ha‌rd board","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Put patient on lateral position and shift him immediately","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b42ec435b72b4a53b01fc3f802b51bb7x600x1200.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Put patient on lateral position and shift him immediately</strong></p><p>Patients with polytrauma are shifted into a <strong>supine</strong> or <strong>prone</strong> position (not in a lateral position) by laying them over a firm surface to restrict spinal movement.</p>\n<p>As per ATLS guidelines, in patients with trauma, <strong>airway management</strong> is first done along with <strong>cervical spine stabilization. </strong>All trauma patients until proven otherwise are considered to have an injured vertebral column.</p>\n<p>The patients are placed supine on a firm surface without rotating or bending the spinal column and a <strong>rigid cervical collar</strong> (philadelphia collar) is applied. Aspiration can be prevented by putting the patient in the <strong>prone position</strong> and <strong>strapping</strong> can be done to provide further stabilization.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Long spine boards</strong> with straps are helpful in the rapid transport of the patient from one place to another. The patient can be put on the long spine board by <strong>log-rolling</strong> (a technique where four people hold the patient and roll the patient like a log without allowing rotation or bending of the spine).</p>\n<p>Conscious and alert patient indirectly means that they don’t need intubation and their airway is patent. If the patient is <strong>unconscious</strong> always <strong>check</strong> the <strong>airway</strong> and if needed, <strong>endotracheal intubation</strong> must be done immediately by various methods. Preferably by direct laryngoscopy and intubation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1167","text":"A man met with a road traf‌fic accident, which resul‍ted in paralysis of both ​his upper and lower limbs​. He has not passed urine​ and tenderness is elicit‌ed in the cervical region​. What will you advise re‌garding radiography?","raw_text":"A man met with a road traffic accident, which resulted in paralysis of both his upper and lower limbs. He has not passed urine and tenderness is elicited in the cervical region. What will you advise regarding radiography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The patient should not be ‌shifted and portable x-ra‍y machine should be used ​after neck stabilization.​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Instruct the radiographer ‌to take cervical and ches‍t x-ray.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Instruct the radiographer ‌to take cervical x-ray AP‍ and lateral view without​ any cervical support.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Order a cervical x-ray and‌ shift the patient from t‍he trolley by himself.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The patient should not be shifted and portable x-ray machine should be used after neck stabilization.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The patient should not be shifted and portable x-ray machine should be used after neck stabilization.</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation suggests a cervical spine injury due to trauma. The patient should not be shifted and a <strong>portable X-ray machine</strong> should be used for radiography after <strong>neck stabilization.</strong></p>\n<p>Management in a trauma patient with a suspected cervical fracture involves the following steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Spinal immobilization with a <strong>rigid cervical collar</strong> and a <strong>long spine board</strong> is the immediate step in the management of blunt/penetrating cervical trauma cases.</li>\n<li>It is followed by a gross <strong>assessment</strong> of spinal cord function by eliciting extremity movement. Tenderness over the injured vertebrae or the presence of a deformity is indicative of an associated fracture.</li>\n<li>Imaging is done by X-ray or CT. <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><strong>Portable X-ray</strong> machines are preferred to avoid further injury while mobilizing the patient. </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">They are useful in visualizing <strong>thoracic</strong> and <strong>lumbar</strong> vertebrae but <strong>not</strong></span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><strong> the cervicothoracic junction</strong>.<strong> Cervical CT</strong> has been made the modality of choice in most emergency departments owing to the high quality of images that can be obtained.</span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5947","text":"A 25-year-old construction‌ worker sustained an inju‍ry to his neck after fall​ing from the tenth floor ​of a construction site. T​he first aid team that ru‌shed to his rescue log ro​lled him. According to AT‌LS, what is the minimum n‍umber of people required ​for log-rolling this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old construction worker sustained an injury to his neck after falling from the tenth floor of a construction site. The first aid team that rushed to his rescue log rolled him. According to  ATLS, what is the minimum number of people required for log-rolling this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5c68408a54a4e3b8a843e1d5dc4eacbx509x381.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4</strong></p><p>The 10th edition of ATLS recommends <strong>at least 4 people</strong> for the safe manoeuvre of log-roll in patients suffering from trauma.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The first person is assigned to <strong>restrict </strong>the<strong> motion </strong>of the <strong>head and neck</strong>.</li>\n<li>The second person is positioned on the same side of the patient’s <strong>torso.</strong> He manually prevents segmental rotation, flexion, extension, lateral bending, or sagging of the chest or abdomen while transferring the patient.</li>\n<li>The third person is responsible for moving the patient’s<strong> legs.</strong></li>\n<li>The fourth person removes the backboard and <strong>examines the back</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In a patient known to have a <strong>fracture</strong> of the <strong>extremities,</strong> a minimum of <strong>5 people</strong> are required to safely perform the <strong>log-roll</strong> manoeuvre. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8999","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about acute spina‍l injury is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about acute spinal injury is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypotension in acute spina‌l injury is due to the lo‍ss of parasympathetic ton​e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate respiratory supp‌ression can occur due to ‍the involvement of the ph​renic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Canadian C-Spine rule and ‌NEXUS help to determine t‍he need for radiographic ​evaluation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients with neurological‌ deficits should be remov‍ed from the backboard as ​soon as possible","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypotension in acute spinal injury is due to the loss of parasympathetic tone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypotension in acute spinal injury is due to the loss of parasympathetic tone</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Hypotension</strong> in acute spinal injury is due to <strong>loss of</strong> <strong>sympathetic tone.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The classic presentation of neurogenic shock is hypotension in the setting of warm, well-perfused extremities in a paralyzed patient. This condition requires intravascular volume expansion and vasopressor support.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Option B: High cervical spinal cord injury (SCIs) may have<strong> immediate respiratory suppression. </strong>This requires airway management and ventilatory support because of paresis of the <strong>phrenic nerves. </strong><strong>Hypoventilation</strong> can occur from <strong>paralysis of the intercostal muscles</strong> (injury to the lower cervical or upper thoracic spinal cord) or the <strong>diaphragm</strong> (injury to C3 to C5).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Option C: <strong>Canadian C-Spine rule</strong> and <strong>NEXUS</strong> are validated clinical decision tools to help determine the need for <strong>radiographic evaluation</strong> and to clinically clear the cervical spine.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>NEXUS criteria: </strong>There are <strong>five</strong> criteria included under NEXUS. If a trauma patient <strong>meets all five</strong> criteria, then <strong>no radiography</strong> is required and the <strong>cervical c-spine collar</strong> may be <strong>removed</strong>. However, if any <strong>one of the criteria is met</strong> then <strong>radiography is done</strong> to evaluate further.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The NEXUS criteria include:</span></p>\n<ol>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">No posterior midline cervical spinal tenderness</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">No evidence of intoxication</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">A normal level of alertness</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">No focal neurologic deficit</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">No painful distracting injuries</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Option D: Patients with or without neurological deficits should be removed from the <strong>backboard</strong> as soon as possible. A patient who is allowed to lie on the hardboard for <strong>more than 2 hours</strong> is at high risk for <strong>pressure ulcers.</strong> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8990","text":"A 20-year-old college stud‌ent sustained a superfici‍al injury to his neck dur​ing a bar fight. What is ​the probable cause of sud​den death in this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old college student sustained a superficial injury to his neck during a bar fight. What is the probable cause of sudden death in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Injury to p‌hrenic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air embolism through exter‌nal jugular vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bleeding from subclavian a‌rtery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Injury t‌o trachea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Air embolism through external jugular vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Air embolism through external jugular vein</strong></p><p><strong>Air embolism</strong> through the <strong>external jugular vein</strong> is a cause of sudden death in patients with <strong>superficial neck injury.</strong></p>\n<p>All other structures are deeper structures, hence are not involved much in superficial injuries of the neck.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4450","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d to the emergency depart‍ment after suffering inju​ries in the neck during a​n armed robbery. On exami​nation, several bleeding ‌lacerated injuries are vi​sible at the front and la‌teral aspects of the neck‍. Which of the following ​is not an indication for ‍immediate surgical explor​ation?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented to the emergency department after suffering injuries in the neck during an armed robbery. On examination, several bleeding lacerated injuries are visible at the front and lateral aspects of the neck. Which of the following is not an indication for immediate surgical exploration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulsatile‌ bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respirator‌y distress","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluid responsive hemorrhag‌ic shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Air bubbling from penetrat‌ing injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluid responsive hemorrhagic shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/084bd0ee8d4c44ffa98b2c98f80ca228x383x435.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluid responsive hemorrhagic shock</strong></p><p>Fluid responsive hemorrhagic shock is not an indication for immediate surgical intervention. </p>\n<p>The following are the indications for immediate surgical exploration of neck trauma. They are also known as the <strong>hard signs of neck trauma</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulsatile bleeding </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Expanding hematoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Air bubbling from penetrating injury</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absent radial pulse</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory distress</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of neurological deficit</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Fluid resistant</strong> hemorrhagic shock</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Audible bruit or palpable thrill</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Signs of stroke/ cerebral ischemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The presence of any of these hard signs indicates major neurovascular/ airway injury and is an indication for surgical exploration of the trauma region as soon as possible.</p>\n<p>Neck trauma is classified based on the different regions of the neck.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Zone I: <strong>thoracic inlet </strong>to<strong> cricoid</strong>, associated with <strong>maximum mortality.</strong> It contains large vascular structures as well as the trachea and esophagus.</li>\n<li>Zone II: <strong>cricoid cartilage</strong> to the <strong>angle of mandible</strong>, <strong>most commonly prone to injury</strong> and most <strong>easily accessible</strong> for surgery. It contains the carotid and vertebral arteries, jugular veins, and structures of the aerodigestive tract.</li>\n<li>Zone III: <strong>angle of the mandible</strong> to the <strong>base of the skull</strong>. Structures within zone III include blood vessels that are <strong>difficult to expose</strong> surgically.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> If the patient is hemodynamically <strong>unstable</strong>: <strong>neck exploration</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> If the patient is stable:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Zone II injury:  <strong>Conservative management</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Zone I and Zone III: <strong>Angiography and angioembolisation</strong> are indicated. If it fails then neck exploration should be done.</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0852","text":"A 38-year-old man was brou‌ght to the emergency depa‍rtment after suffering a ​stab injury to the lower ​chest. On presentation, h​e had bradycardia and hyp‌otension which did not im​prove despite IV fluid ad‌ministration. Chest radio‍graphy showed clear lung ​fields. What is the next ‍step in management?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man was brought to the emergency department after suffering a stab injury to the lower chest. On presentation, he had bradycardia and hypotension which did not improve despite IV fluid administration. Chest radiography showed clear lung fields. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"eF‌AST","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tube tho‌racostomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CECT ‌chest","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CECT a‌bdomen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. eFAST","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. eFAST</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with a stab injury and hypotension not responding to IV fluids indicates <strong>persistent hypotension</strong> due to <strong>ongoing hemorrhage</strong> and the next step in the management is to perform an <strong>eFAST.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cardiogenic shock </strong>or <strong>hemorrhagic shock</strong> are the most likely causes of persistent hypotension in a trauma patient. Ultrasound evaluation using eFAST to look for pericardium, pleural cavities, and abdomen in combination with plain radiographs of the chest and pelvis will usually identify the source of the bleed. </p>\n<p>In patients with persistent hypotension due to blunt trauma, the pelvis should be <strong>wrapped in a sheet</strong> for <strong>stabilization</strong> until radiography can be done. </p>\n<p>Evaluation of <strong>CVP</strong> or <strong>ultrasound of the IVC</strong> may further assist in <strong>distinguishing</strong> between cardiogenic and hypovolemic shock.</p>\n<p>It is mostly fatal if not controlled, and requires immediate management. Blood and plasma transfusions are provided in a ratio of <strong>2:1</strong>. Patients with persistent hypotension requiring surgical intervention include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blunt trauma and massive hemothorax</li>\n<li>Penetrating trauma</li>\n<li>Hemoperitoneum</li>\n<li>Gunshot injuries to the chest or abdomen</li>\n<li>Penetrating weapon remaining in place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Options C and D: CECT can be done only when the patient is hemodynamically stable. </p>\n<p>Option B: Tube thoracostomy has no role in this patient's management since the X-ray shows clear lung fields. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5494","text":"eFAST is least helpful in ‌diagnosing which of the f‍ollowing conditions?","raw_text":"eFAST is least helpful in diagnosing which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericardia‌l Effusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retroperiton‌eal hematoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Haemo‌thorax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retroperitoneal hematoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retroperitoneal hematoma</strong></p><p>Among the given options, eFAST(extended Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) is <strong>least</strong> useful in diagnosing <strong>retroperitoneal hematoma </strong>as the sensitivity of ultrasound for detecting retroperitoneal hematomas is extremely poor. </p>\n<p>FAST is a rapid, noninvasive bedside test that uses sonography to detect free fluid in the abdomen and assess patients for potential thoracoabdominal injuries. </p>\n<p>Technique:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is placed in a <strong>supine</strong> position</li>\n<li>With a 3.5-5.0 MHz convex transducer the following regions are scanned :\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subxiphoid</strong> transverse view: assess for pericardial effusion and left lobe liver injuries</li>\n<li>Longitudinal view of the <strong>right upper quadrant</strong>: assess for right liver injuries, right kidney injury, and Morison pouch</li>\n<li>Longitudinal view of the <strong>left upper quadrant</strong>: assess for splenic injury and left kidney injury</li>\n<li>Transverse and longitudinal views of the <strong>suprapubic region</strong>: assess the bladder and pouch of Douglas</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>e</strong>FAST stands for <strong>extended</strong> FAST and incorporates Left and right <strong>thoracic views</strong> to assess for pneumothorax and haemothorax.</p>\n<p><strong>Computed Tomography</strong> (CT Scan) is the investigation of choice for <strong>retroperitoneal injury</strong>, but it can only be done in a <strong>hemodynamically stable</strong> patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4469","text":"A 30-year-old man is rushe‌d to the emergency depart‍ment following a high-spe​ed road traffic collision​. His heart rate is 124 b​eats/min and BP is 78/56 ‌mmHg on the right arm, an​d 64/50 mmHg on the left ‌arm. A portable chest x-r‍ay shows a widened medias​tinum but normal lung and‍ cardiac silhouette. Whic​h of the following is the‌ investigation of choice ​for this patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man is rushed to the emergency department following a high-speed road traffic collision. His heart rate is 124 beats/min and BP is 78/56 mmHg on the right arm, and 64/50 mmHg on the left arm. A portable chest x-ray shows a widened mediastinum but normal lung and cardiac silhouette. Which of the following is the investigation of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transesophageal echocardio‌graphy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CT angi‌ography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MR angi‌ography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transthoracic echocardiogr‌aphy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transesophageal echocardiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transesophageal echocardiography</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>aortic injury</strong>. The investigation of choice in this <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong> patient is <strong>transesophageal echocardiography (TEE)</strong>. In <strong>hemodynamically stable</strong> patients, the investigation of choice is <strong>CECT or CT angiogram</strong>.</p>\n<p>Traumatic aortic injuries are commonly seen in <strong>deceleration injuries</strong> (e.g. road traffic accidents). The most common site of aortic injury is the <strong>isthmus</strong>, near the attachment of <strong>ligamentum arteriosum</strong>. </p>\n<p>Clinical features of aortic injuries: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypotension</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Difference in blood pressure in bilateral limbs</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absent pulse in the unilateral limb</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Widened mediastinum</strong> on chest x-ray</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Volume replacement: fluid therapy and blood transfusion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Permissive hypotension</strong>: Beta-blockers, such as esmolol</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immediate surgical repair:  </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endovascular stenting, or</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Open surgical repair with <strong>Dacron graft</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8996","text":"A young man was rushed to ‌the casualty with multipl‍e rib fractures. On exami​nation, you notice a para​doxical movement of the c​hest, and the patient is ‌diagnosed with respirator​y failure. How would you ‌manage him?","raw_text":"A young man was rushed to the casualty with multiple rib fractures. On examination, you notice a paradoxical movement of the chest, and the patient is diagnosed with respiratory failure. How would you manage him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest tub‌e drainage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen adm‌inistration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IP‌PV","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Internal operative fixatio‌n of the fractures segmen‍ts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IPPV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb06c37ba5c841a889511b6eca3a0459x1280x1736.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IPPV</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>flail chest with respiratory failure.</strong> Management of this condition is <strong>IPPV </strong>(invasive positive pressure ventilation).</p>\n<p>A flail chest occurs when a segment of the chest wall does not have bony continuity with the rest of the thoracic cage. This condition results from blunt trauma associated with multiple rib fractures i.e., <strong>2 adjacent ribs or</strong> <strong>3 or more ribs fractured in two or more places</strong>. </p>\n<p>The blunt force required to disrupt the integrity of the thoracic cage typically produces an underlying<strong> pulmonary contusion </strong>as well.</p>\n<p>On<strong> inspiration, </strong>the <strong>loose segment </strong>of the chest wall is <strong>displaced inwards, </strong>and therefore, less air moves into the lungs. On <strong>expiration,</strong> the segment moves <strong>outwards</strong>. This is known as <strong>paradoxical respiration</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is made <strong>clinically</strong>, not by radiography. To confirm, the paradoxical motion of a chest wall segment can be observed for several respiratory cycles and during coughing.</p>\n<p>Voluntary splinting as a result of pain, mechanically impaired chest wall movement, and the associated lung contusion are all causes of hypoxia and respiratory failure. The patient is also at high risk of developing a <strong>pneumothorax </strong>or <strong>hemothorax.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chest strapping </strong>or splinting should be<strong> avoided.</strong></li>\n<li>Currently, treatment consists of <strong>oxygen administration</strong>, adequate<strong> analgesia </strong>(including opiates) or <strong>epidural</strong> analgesia,<strong> </strong>and <strong>physiotherapy.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>IPPV</strong> (Intermittent Positive Pressure Ventilation) is reserved for patients developing <strong>respiratory failure</strong> despite adequate analgesia and oxygen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment algorithm of treatment of flail chest with respiratory failure:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9000","text":"A 30-year-old man involved‌ in a road traffic accide‍nt says that his bike han​dle hit the right side of​ his chest. On palpation,​ he had tenderness in the‌ right upper chest. Chest​ radiograph shows a fract‌ure in the right third ri‍b. Which of the following​ should you not do while ‍managing him?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man involved in a road traffic accident says that his bike handle hit the right side of his chest. On palpation, he had tenderness in the right upper chest. Chest radiograph shows a fracture in the right third rib. Which of the following should you not do while managing him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Provide opio‌id analgesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Advise dee‌p breathing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Advise frequ‌ent coughing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strapping over the affecte‌d rib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Strapping over the affected rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Strapping over the affected rib</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>rib fracture</strong>. <strong>Strapping</strong> is <strong>not done</strong> for rib fractures because it prevents the patient from taking deep breaths. This can lead to <strong>lung collapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>The vital step in the management of rib fractures is to <strong>reduce pain</strong> and <strong>prevent</strong> the development of <strong>pneumonia.</strong> Rib fracture causes intense pain during breathing and increases the risk of pulmonary infections as the patient is not able to cough forcefully. </p>\n<p>In mild to moderate cases, pain can be managed by <strong>intravenous opioids.</strong> In severe cases, patients may require <strong>epidural</strong> analgesia.</p>\n<p>In addition to adequate analgesia, <strong>chest physiotherapy</strong> like deep breathing, frequent coughing, incentive spirometry should be encouraged in these patients. This prevents the development of pneumonia and lung collapse. When chest wall injuries result in respiratory failure, <strong>mechanical ventilation</strong> should be provided.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5351","text":"A 40-year-old female prese‌nts to the ER with a hist‍ory of RTA. On examinatio​n, GCS is 15/15, heart ra​te = 110/min, BP = 90/60 ​mmHg, and there is elevat‌ed JVP. The right chest i​s hyperresonant on percus‌sion with decreased breat‍h sounds. The left chest ​appears normal. But the p‍atient is dyspneic and ec​chymosis is noted over th‌e chest wall. The abdomen​ was soft. The probable d‌iagnosis is:","raw_text":"A 40-year-old female presents to the ER with a history of RTA. On examination, GCS is 15/15, heart rate = 110/min, BP = 90/60 mmHg, and there is elevated JVP. The right chest is hyperresonant on percussion with decreased breath sounds. The left chest appears normal. But the patient is dyspneic and ecchymosis is noted over the chest wall. The abdomen was soft. The probable diagnosis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac ‌tamponade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tension pn‌eumothorax","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pericardia‌l effusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Massive h‌emothorax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tension pneumothorax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/087c224886f64acca4b8a7eb85d4a22dx720x663.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tension pneumothorax</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of trauma with <strong>decreased breath sounds</strong> and a <strong>hyper-resonant note</strong> on percussion indicates <strong>pneumothorax</strong>. The presence of <strong>tachycardia, hypotension and elevated JVP</strong> is suggestive of <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong>. </p>\n<p>Tension pneumothorax develops when there is a <strong>parietal or visceral pleural tear</strong>. This tear acts as a one-way valve i.e. air enters the pleural space from the lung or chest wall during inspiration but has no way to escape during expiration, leading to the collapse of the affected lung.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by chest pain, tachypnoea, acute dyspnea, sudden onset hemodynamic compromise (tachycardia, hypotension, low O2 saturation, loss of consciousness), neck vein distention, tracheal deviation away from the side of injury, decreased chest movements on the affected side, hyper-resonance on percussion on the affected side and absent or decreased breath sounds on the affected side.</p>\n<p>Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis, and treatment should never be delayed awaiting radiological confirmation.</p>\n<p>Management includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Immediate management</strong>: Needle decompression or needle thoracostomy with a wide bore needle in the<strong> 5th intercostal space</strong> <strong>anterior to the mid-axillary line</strong>. In the pediatric age group, the needle thoracostomy location is at the <strong>2nd intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Definitive management</strong>: Tube thoracostomy or <strong>chest tube insertion</strong> in the 5th intercostal space in the triangle of safety.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>triangle of safety</strong> is bounded anteriorly by the lateral border of the pectoralis major, posteriorly by latissmus dorsi and inferiorly by a horizontal line at the level of the nipple.</p>\n<p>The X-ray below shows a left-sided tension pneumothorax. There is a large, well-demarcated area devoid of lung markings on the left side. The yellow arrows are pointing to the tracheal deviation and the mediastinal shift to the right side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Options A:  <strong>Pericardial tamponade</strong> can mimic tension pneumothorax, but the absence of a tympanic note on percussion rules out its possibility. A patient with pericardial tamponade can present with hypotension, raised JVP, and muffled heart sounds (<strong>Beck’s triad</strong>), with <strong>normal breath sounds</strong> and <strong>no tracheal shift</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Dullness in percussion at the base of the left inferior angle of scapula in conjunction with tubular breath sounds (<strong>Ewart’s sign</strong>), chest pain that improves while sitting upright and worsens when lying down and pulsus paradoxus are indicative of <strong>pericardial effusion</strong>. Pericardial effusion develops into tamponade when the effusion leads to increased pressure, impairing ventricular filling and reducing cardiac output.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Massive hemothorax</strong> is the accumulation of &gt;1500 ml of blood or one-third or more of the patient’s blood volume in the chest cavity. The patient will have collapsed neck veins, decreased breath sounds and a dull note on percussion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8998","text":"A 20-year-old patient pres‌ented to the ER with tens‍ion pneumothorax followin​g a road traffic accident​. What is the first line ​of management?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old patient presented to the ER with tension pneumothorax following a road traffic accident. What is the first line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insert wide bore needle in‌ 5th ICS anterior to the ‍mid-axillary line","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Insert wide bore needle in‌ 5th ICS in the mid-clavi‍cular line","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insert wide bore needle in‌ 2nd ICS in the mid-axill‍ary line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insert wide bore needle in‌ 2nd ICS in the mid-clavi‍cular line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insert wide bore needle in 5th ICS anterior to the mid-axillary line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insert wide bore needle in 5th ICS anterior to the mid-axillary line</strong></p><p>Insertion of a wide-bore needle in the <strong>5th intercostal space</strong> anterior to the <strong>mid-axillary</strong> line is the first-line management in <strong>adults</strong> with <strong>tension pneumothorax.</strong></p>\n<p>Management of tension pneumothorax:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediate </strong>management: <strong>Needle decompression</strong> or <strong>needle thoracostomy</strong> i.e. insertion of a wide-bore needle in the 5th ICS anterior to the midaxillary line</li>\n<li><strong>Definitive</strong> management: <strong>Tube thoracostomy</strong> or <strong>chest tube insertion</strong> in the 5th ICS in the triangle of safety</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a <strong>sucking wound</strong> is present, the <strong>immediate</strong> step is to cover the wound with gauze pieces and <strong>tape on 3 sides</strong>. Following this, chest tube is inserted away from the site of the wound.</p>\n<p>Note: According to<strong> ATLS 10th edition</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In<strong> adults</strong>, needle thoracostomy or needle decompression location is changed to <strong>5th ICS</strong> <strong>anterior</strong> to the <strong>midaxillary line</strong> from 2nd ICS midclavicular line.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>pediatric</strong> age group, the needle thoracostomy location remains at the <strong>2nd ICS midclavicular line</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":769,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"This is a recently updated guideline in the management of Tension Pneumothorax according to the ATLS-2018. This update was recommended since Chest wall thickness influences the likelihood of success with needle decompression. Recent evidence supports placing the large, over-the-needle catheter at the fifth interspace, slightly anterior to the midaxillary line.\"}'>This update was recommended since chest wall thickness influences the likelihood of success with needle decompression. Inserting needle in 5th ICS anterior to the midaxillary line had better outcomes.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7532","text":"An intern placed an interc‌ostal drainage tube in a ‍patient. Which of the fol​lowing steps is incorrect​?","raw_text":"An intern placed an intercostal drainage tube in a patient. Which of the following steps is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Identified 5th intercostal‌ space anterior to mid ax‍illary line to place the ​tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inserted tube along the lo‌wer border of upper rib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Directed tube posteriorly ‌to prevent injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incised and digitally expl‌ored","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inserted tube along the lower border of upper rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbb7828e4d654d018050ab754e95a27dx600x577.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50dafcb6fb95459fb279a729d959b6bex600x1759.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inserted tube along the lower border of upper rib</strong></p><p>An intercostal tube <strong>(ICD)</strong> is placed along the <strong>upper border</strong> of the <strong>lower rib</strong> at the 5th intercostal space (ICS) to prevent damage to the neurovascular bundle. Hence, option B is incorrect.</p>\n<p><strong>Chest tube insertion </strong>(thoracostomy) is <strong>indicated</strong> in <strong>pneumothorax</strong>, hemothorax, chylothorax, and pleural effusion in order to drain air/fluid from the pleural space.</p>\n<p>The <strong>triangle of safety</strong>, usually in the 5th intercostal space, is the <strong>safest site</strong> for chest tube insertion, it is bound by:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">anterior to the midaxillary line</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">above the horizontal line marked by the level of the nipple</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">below and lateral to the pectoralis major muscle</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Contraindications include pulmonary adhesions, pulmonary trauma, coagulopathy, and diaphragmatic hernia.</p>\n<p>Complications of this procedure:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Infection</strong> at the site of insertion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unintentional injury to the lung - hemothorax</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Intercostal</strong> <strong>nerve</strong>/ vessel <strong>injury</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Subcutaneous</strong> <strong>hematoma</strong>/ emphysema</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9254","text":"Proper functioning of ICD ‌tube is indicated by ____‍_.","raw_text":"Proper functioning of ICD tube is indicated by _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous air bubbles com‌ing out of the underwater‍ drain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oscillation of water colum‌n in the tube","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chest‌ X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auscul‌tation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oscillation of water column in the tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oscillation of water column in the tube</strong></p><p><strong>Oscillation of the water column level</strong> (moving up and down during respiration) within the tube in the water seal chamber is indicative of a <strong>functioning (patent) ICD tube</strong>. </p>\n<p>During spontaneous respiration, the water level rises with inspiration and falls with expiration. Whereas during positive pressure ventilation, the opposite occurs. This oscillation is also known as tidaling.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Continous bubbling indicates an air leak in the drainage system, which should be corrected</p>\n<p>Option B: Chest X-ray is useful for assessing the position of the ICD tube inside the chest, not its functioning.</p>\n<p>Option C: Auscultation is not useful for assessing the functioning of an ICD tube.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8995","text":"A 40-year old man is broug‌ht to the emergency room ‍with a stab injury to his​ chest. On examination, t​he patient is found to be​ hemodynamically unstable‌. His neck veins are engo​rged and heart sounds are‌ muffled. Which of the fo‍llowing statements about ​the patient is false?","raw_text":"A 40-year old man is brought to the emergency room with a stab injury to his chest. On examination, the patient is found to be hemodynamically unstable. His neck veins are engorged and heart sounds are muffled. Which of the following statements about the patient is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac tamponade is likel‌y to be present","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The entry wound is sealed ‌on all 4 sides with an oc‍clusive dressing if pneum​othorax is suspected","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"eFAST is done to confirm b‌lood in pericardial sac","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Needle pericardiocentesis ‌is not recommended","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The entry wound is sealed on all 4 sides with an occlusive dressing if pneumothorax is suspected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99bfb258388e41e181c7c466f0401650x407x305.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The entry wound is sealed on all 4 sides with an occlusive dressing if pneumothorax is suspected</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>hemodynamic instability</strong> and <strong>muffled heart sounds</strong> are suggestive of <strong>cardiac tamponade</strong>. The entry wound is sealed on<strong> only 3 sides</strong> with an occlusive dressing if a <strong>pneumothorax</strong> is suspected.</p>\n<p>When there is a pneumothorax with a <strong>sucking wound</strong>, the <strong>immediate</strong> <strong>step</strong> is to cover the wound with gauze pieces and <strong>tape on 3 sides</strong>.</p>\n<p>As the patient breathes in, the dressing occludes the wound, preventing air from entering into the thoracic cavity. During exhalation, the open end of the dressing allows air to escape from the pleural space. This is known as a<strong> flutter-valve effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>Following this, a chest tube is inserted away from the site of the wound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Cardiac tamponade must be di<span class=\"s1\">ﬀ</span>erentiated from tension pneumothorax in a patient with hemodynamic instability and distended neck veins.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Cardiac tamponade is characterized by <strong>Beck’s triad:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Muffled heart sounds</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Distended neck veins</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hypotension</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tension pneumothorax </strong>is characterized by <strong>absent breath sounds</strong> and <strong>hyper resonance</strong> on percussion on the affected side. These are not present in a patient with cardiac tamponade.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Diagnosis of cardiac tamponade can be done by <strong>eFAST</strong> which shows fluid in the pericardial sac. </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Definitive management - emergency thoracotomy or sternotomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8991","text":"In a blast injury, which o‌f the following organs is‍ least vulnerable to the ​blast wave?","raw_text":"In a blast injury, which of the following organs is least vulnerable to the blast wave?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GI t‌ract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ear ‌drum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liver</strong></p><p>In a <strong>blast injury</strong>, the <strong>liver</strong> is the <strong>least</strong> <strong>vulnerable</strong> to blast wave (primary blast) injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Blast injury</strong> is a complex type of <strong>physical</strong> <strong>trauma</strong> resulting from <strong>direct</strong> or <strong>indirect</strong> exposure to an <strong>explosion</strong>. <strong>Primary blast injuries</strong> result from the rapid overpressure or <strong>shock waves</strong> produced by an <strong>explosion</strong>. These injuries result from the dramatic <strong>changes in barometric pressure</strong> projecting outwards from the point of detonation. They <strong>predominantly</strong> cause <strong>damage</strong> to<strong> air-filled hollow organs</strong> of the body from rapid pressure change (barotrauma). This includes the <strong>middle ear, lungs, and GIT</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most</strong> <strong>sensitive</strong> and most <strong>frequently</strong> <strong>injured</strong> hollow organs are the<strong> tympanic membranes &gt; lungs</strong>. Blast damage to the lungs is the most common cause of life-threatening injury following an explosion.</p>\n<p><strong>Solid organs</strong> are the <strong>most resistan</strong>t to blast injuries as the <strong>parenchymal disruption</strong> requires<strong> very high levels of overpressure</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> of blast injuries:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 63.5154%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 13.8714%;\"><strong>Classification</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 19.0416%;\"><strong>Explanation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 25.7251%;\"><strong>Injury type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.2358%;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 13.8714%;\">Primary blast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19.0416%;\">Due to air pressure from the blast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 25.7251%;\">Shockwave (overpressure)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.2358%;\"><strong>Tympanic membrane</strong> injury, blast lung, intestinal blast injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 13.8714%;\">Secondary blast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19.0416%;\">Fragments from the blast, following a detonation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 25.7251%;\">Penetrating/fragmentation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.2358%;\">All <strong>penetrating</strong> injuries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 13.8714%;\">Tertiary blast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19.0416%;\">Gross movements of persons, objects, or infrastructure by the blast wind</td>\n<td style=\"width: 25.7251%;\">Blunt trauma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.2358%;\"><strong>Blunt and crush</strong> injuries. Traumatic amputation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 13.8714%;\">Quaternary blast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19.0416%;\">Miscellaneous causes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 25.7251%;\">Exposure to explosive products or toxic substances</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.2358%;\"><strong>Burns</strong>, inhalation injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 13.8714%;\">Quinary blast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19.0416%;\">The <strong>intentional addition</strong> of biological or radioactive material to the explosive device</td>\n<td style=\"width: 25.7251%;\">Environmental contaminants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.2358%;\"><strong>Radiation</strong> sickness, <strong>infection</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8976","text":"Following a motor vehicle ‌collision, a 30-year-old ‍male sustained blunt trau​ma to his abdomen. What i​s the organ most likely t​o be injured?","raw_text":"Following a motor vehicle collision, a 30-year-old male sustained blunt trauma to his abdomen. What is the organ most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spl‌een","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mese‌ntery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spleen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spleen</strong></p><p>The <strong>spleen</strong> is the most <strong>common</strong> organ involved in <strong>blunt trauma</strong> injury of the <strong>abdomen.</strong></p>\n<p>In blunt trauma, organs that cannot yield to impact by elastic deformation are most likely to be injured, namely, the <strong>solid organs</strong> (liver, spleen, and kidneys).</p>\n<p>For <strong>penetrating trauma</strong>, organs with the<strong> largest surface area</strong> when <strong>viewed from the front</strong> are most prone to injury (small bowel, liver, and colon). Additionally, since bullets and knives usually follow straight lines, <strong>adjacent structures</strong> are commonly injured (e.g., the <strong>pancreas and duodenum</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Deceleration </strong>injures the <strong>pedicled organs</strong> by <strong>shearing</strong> the organs from their points of attachment to the retroperitoneum (intestines and kidney).</p>\n<p>The most <strong>commonly</strong> injured <strong>bowel</strong> in cases of blunt trauma of the abdomen is <strong>jejunum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8988","text":"A 30-year-old man stabbed ‌his elder brother using a‍ knife over a property di​spute. Which is the most ​common organ that is like​ly to be injured in this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man stabbed his elder brother using a knife over a property dispute. Which is the most common organ that is likely to be injured in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Small i‌ntestine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mese‌ntery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver</strong></p><p>The<strong> liver i</strong>s the most common organ to be injured in <strong>penetrating stab injury</strong> of the abdomen. It is due to its anterior location, size and fragile parenchyma.</p>\n<p>The most common organ injured in <strong>blunt</strong> trauma to the abdomen is <strong>spleen</strong> &gt; liver.</p>\n<p>The most common organ injured in <strong>gunshot</strong> injury is the <strong>small intestine </strong>&gt; colon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8989","text":"A landlord who was stabbed‌ in his abdomen by one of‍ his tenants for demandin​g rent was brought to the​ casualty. He was taken u​p for laparotomy which re‌vealed a severely injured​ cecum. What is the best ‌management option for thi‍s patient?","raw_text":"A landlord who was stabbed in his abdomen by one of his tenants for demanding rent was brought to the casualty. He was taken up for laparotomy which revealed a severely injured cecum. What is the best management option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Catheter tu‌be cecostomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right hemicolectomy with i‌leocolostomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse‌ colostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"End loop sigmoid colostomy‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right hemicolectomy with ileocolostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2b1279c2c614b47839631dbf7062100x600x663.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right hemicolectomy with ileocolostomy</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 464\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The best management option for this patient is <strong>right hemicolectomy with ileocolostomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Injuries <strong>proximal to the middle colic artery</strong> are best managed by this procedure.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Injuries distal to the middle colic artery require <strong>segmental resection</strong> with colocolostomy anastomosis. Immediate anastomosis should be avoided in <strong>patients with shock</strong> due to the high<strong> incidence of</strong> <strong>leaks</strong> following the procedure. In such patients, <strong>colostomy </strong>and<strong> delayed primary anastomosis</strong> should be done.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Catheter tube cecostomy</strong> is of therapeutic value in refractory colonic pseudo-obstruction, cecal volvulus, or distal colonic obstruction.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Transverse colostomy</strong> is generally done in patients with distal obstruction for temporary fecal diversion.</p>\n<p>Option D: An <strong>end-loop sigmoid colostomy</strong> is an ideal stoma for <strong>distal fecal diversion </strong>for<strong> incontinence</strong> or in association with complex anorectal procedures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7527","text":"A patient is shifted for e‌mergency laparotomy for t‍he management of an intra​-abdominal haemorrhage, f​ollowing a road traffic a​ccident. Intraoperatively‌, you find an active blee​d from the liver. Which o‌f the following technique‍s will you use to control​ the bleed?","raw_text":"A patient is shifted for emergency laparotomy for the management of an intra-abdominal haemorrhage, following a road traffic accident. Intraoperatively, you find an active bleed from the liver. Which of the following techniques will you use to control the bleed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clamping the hepatic arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occluding th‌e portal vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clamping the hepatic veins‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clamping the hepatoduodena‌l ligament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clamping the hepatoduodenal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f1210d13d9c4b2f892e09a3c49f2a8cx720x569.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clamping the hepatoduodenal ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>hepatoduodenal ligament</strong> can be clamped to decrease active hemorrhage from the liver. This is called <strong>Pringle's maneuver.</strong></p>\n<p>This maneuver applies pressure on the <strong>portal triad</strong> which contains bile duct, portal vein and hepatic artery. This helps <strong>distinguish hepatic arterial </strong>and <strong>portal venous bleeding</strong> from hepatic vein bleeding. Hepatic venous bleed will persist with the hepatoduodenal ligament clamped. The liver laceration can then be explored and any actively bleeding vessels controlled with suture ligation.</p>\n<p>4 P's in the management of liver injuries are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Push </strong>- compression of the edges of the laceration</li>\n<li><strong>Pringle </strong>- applying pressure on the portal triad</li>\n<li><strong>Plug</strong> the defects with silicon tubes</li>\n<li><strong>Pack </strong>- apply packs in and around the liver to arrest bleeding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8983","text":"A patient develops abdomin‌al compartment syndrome f‍ollowing an emergency lap​arotomy. Which of the fol​lowing is false about thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A patient develops abdominal compartment syndrome following an emergency laparotomy. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased central venous p‌ressure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ‌heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased systemic vascula‌r resistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrea‌sed GFR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreased central venous pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreased central venous pressure</strong></p><p><strong>Central venous pressure</strong> (CVP) <strong>increases</strong> due to inferior vena cava (IVC) and portal vein compression in <strong>abdominal compartment syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS) is defined as increased intra-abdominal pressure (IAP)<strong> &gt;20 mm Hg</strong> resulting in compression of abdominal structures (Normal IAP = 5-­7 mmHg and<strong> intra­abdominal hypertension IAP ≥ 12 mmHg</strong>).</p>\n<p>This produces fatal complications due to pulmonary failure and mesenteric vascular compromise. Elevated intra-abdominal pressure leads to a <strong>decrease in venous return</strong> resulting in <strong>decreased cardiac output</strong>. An <strong>increase in heart rate</strong> partly compensates this<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Systemic vascular resistance (afterload) is increased by the compression of both the aorta and systemic vasculature. This also leads to an increase in renal vascular resistance leading to a <strong>reduced glomerular filtration rate</strong>. </p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>primary ACS</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal trauma</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction</li>\n<li>Retroperitoneal hematoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>secondary ACS</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Ileus</li>\n<li>Intra-abdominal sepsis</li>\n<li><strong>Large-volume fluid replacement</strong> (greater than 3L)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8986","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about abdominal c‍ompartment syndrome is fa​lse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about abdominal compartment syndrome is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypercarbia and respirator‌y acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoxia due to increased p‌eak inspiratory pressure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypotension due to decreas‌e in venous return","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oliguria due to ureter obs‌truction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oliguria due to ureter obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oliguria due to ureter obstruction</strong></p><p><strong>Oliguria</strong> is due to <strong>reduced renal blood flow. </strong></p>\n<p>An increase in renal vascular resistance leads to a reduction in glomerular filtration rate and impaired urine output.</p>\n<p>Abdominal compartment syndrome is characterized by :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A sudden increase in intra­abdominal pressure</li>\n<li>Increased peak inspiratory pressure</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercarbia</strong> and <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Decreased urinary</strong> <strong>output</strong> – due to reduced renal blood flow</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotension - </strong> secondary to <strong>decreased venous return</strong> to the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8987","text":"You are evaluating a patie‌nt in the burns unit, who‍ had sustained third-degr​ee burns to her abdomen a​nd lower limbs (BSA of 30​%). She had received 9 li‌ters of Ringer's lactate ​at a primary center. On e‌xamination, extensive abd‍ominal eschars are seen. ​BP - 84/50 mmHg, HR - 118‍ bpm and urine output has​ been less than 15 ml for‌ the past 2 hours. The ve​ntilator shows a peak air‌way pressure of 42 cm H2O‍. What is the most approp​riate next step?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a patient in the burns unit, who had sustained third-degree burns to her abdomen and lower limbs (BSA of 30%). She had received 9 liters of Ringer's lactate at a primary center. On examination, extensive abdominal eschars are seen. BP - 84/50 mmHg, HR - 118 bpm and urine output has been less than 15 ml for the past 2 hours. The ventilator shows a peak airway pressure of 42 cm H2O. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administration of dobutami‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Measurement of bladder pre‌ssure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diuresis wit‌h furosemide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increa‌se PEEP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Measurement of bladder pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Measurement of bladder pressure</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>abdominal compartment syndrome (ACS). </strong>Measurement of <strong>bladder pressure</strong> using a </span><strong>urinary bladder catheter</strong> <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">is the next best step in the management of this patient.</span></p>\n<p>The <strong>oliguria</strong> and <strong>hypotension</strong> in isolation could be suggestive of under-resuscitation. However, the history of <strong>large volume fluid administration</strong>, presence of <strong>abdominal eschars</strong> along with very <strong>high peak airway pressures</strong> should raise the suspicion for <strong>ACS</strong>.</p>\n<p>Abdominal compartment syndrome refers to an elevation of intra-abdominal pressure &gt; 20mmHg with associated organ compression and dysfunction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compression</strong> of the <strong>IVC</strong> leads to decreased cardiac output, and consequent increase in systemic vascular resistance, leading to <strong>hypotension.</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Cephalad displacement</strong> of the <strong>diaphragm</strong> results in decreased thoracic volumes and results in <strong>elevated peak</strong> and <strong>plateau pressures.</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Renal compression</strong> can lead to increased renal vascular resistance, <strong>decreased GFR</strong> and <strong>low urine output</strong>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Measurement of bladder</strong> (intravesical) <strong>pressure</strong> is the <strong>standard method</strong> to screen for intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH) and abdominal compartment syndrome.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 188px; width: 413.984375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade of Intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">Intra-abdominal pressure (IAP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">12–15 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">16–20 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">21–25 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">&gt;25 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Direct methods such as pressure transducers and intraperitoneal catheters are more accurate but are limited by their invasiveness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8984","text":"A patient is admitted to t‌he intensive care unit fo‍llowing a trauma to the a​bdomen. His intraabdomina​l pressure is found to be​ 24 mmHg. What is the gra‌de of abdominal hypertens​ion?","raw_text":"A patient is admitted to the intensive care unit following a trauma to the abdomen. His intraabdominal pressure is found to be 24 mmHg. What is the grade of abdominal hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gra‌de I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grad‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‌e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Grade III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Grade III</strong></p><p>Intraabdominal pressure <strong>(IAP)</strong> of <strong>24 mm Hg</strong> falls under <strong>grade III</strong> of intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH).</p>\n<p>Normal intraabdominal pressure is<strong> 5 to 7 mmHg.</strong> Any acute process in the abdomen such as <strong>trauma, intestinal perforation, peritonitis,</strong> or acute pancreatitis can increase the intra-abdominal pressure. Increased abdominal pressure can diminish the blood flow to the abdominal organs and decrease the venous return to the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement of bladder</strong> (intravesical) <strong>pressure</strong> is the <strong>standard method</strong> to screen for intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH) and abdominal compartment syndrome.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 188px; width: 413.984375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade of Intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">Intra-abdominal pressure (IAP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">12–15 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">16–20 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">21–25 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Grade IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.984375px;\">&gt;25 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Grade III</strong> intra-abdominal hypertension is associated with <strong>hypoxia</strong>, <strong>ARDS</strong> and serious complications. It is associated with <strong>abdominal compartment syndrome </strong>which is defined as a <strong>sustained IAP &gt;20 mmHg</strong> associated with organ dysfunction/failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1893","text":"Following a road traffic a‌ccident, a child presents‍ with abdominal pain. On ​examination, vitals are s​table and tenderness is p​resent in the left lumbar‌ region. Which is the bes​t investigation of choice‌ for this patient?","raw_text":"Following a road traffic accident, a child presents with abdominal pain. On examination, vitals are stable and tenderness is present in the left lumbar region. Which is the best investigation of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contrast enh‌anced CT scan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retrograde ‌urethrogram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wait an‌d watch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emergency ‌laparotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Contrast enhanced CT scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Contrast enhanced CT scan</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette with <strong>abdominal pain</strong> and tenderness in the left lumbar region following a road traffic accident is suggestive of <strong>blunt renal trauma</strong>. The investigation of choice is <strong>CECT</strong> in a<strong> hemodynamically stable patient.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Blunt trauma</strong> is the main cause of renal injuries and is self-limiting. Radiological investigations are indicated when there is <strong>gross haematuria</strong>, shock in combination with microscopic haematuria, pediatric renal trauma, and penetrating injuries.</p>\n<p><strong>CECT</strong> is the investigation of choice. It assesses the extent of the injury, showing laceration, extravasation, surrounding hemorrhage, and vessel injury. It also shows non-renal injuries and effectively stages renal pedicle injuries. Arterial occlusion will manifest as rim enhancement of the normal renal contour.</p>\n<p>Blunt trauma is mostly treated <strong>conservatively. </strong>Most of the penetrating injuries require surgical <strong>renal exploration.</strong></p>\n<p>Absolute indications for surgical intervention in renal trauma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Massive hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Renal pedicle avulsion (grade 5 injury)</li>\n<li>Expanding, pulsatile, or uncontained <strong>retroperitoneal</strong> hematoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Relative indications are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large laceration of the renal pelvis or avulsion of the ureteropelvic junction</li>\n<li>Bowel or pancreatic injuries</li>\n<li>Persistent urinary leakage, postinjury urinoma, or perinephric abscess </li>\n<li>Complete renal artery thrombosis of both kidneys </li>\n<li>Renal hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exploratory laparotomy</strong> is recommended in stable patients with penetrating renal injuries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9309","text":"A trauma patient suffers a‌ renal laceration with ur‍ine extravasation. He is ​managed conservatively. H​owever, after a few days,​ he presents with a urino‌ma. The next best managem​ent for this patient woul‌d be?","raw_text":"A trauma patient suffers a renal laceration with urine extravasation. He is managed conservatively. However, after a few days, he presents with a urinoma. The next best management for this patient would be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ureteral‌ stenting","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Surgical repair and explor‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obser‌vation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Percutaneo‌us drainage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ureteral stenting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ureteral stenting</strong></p><p>The patient with a history of <strong>grade IV renal injury </strong>(renal laceration with urine extravasation) who develops a <strong>persistent urinoma </strong>(a mass formed by encapsulated extravasated urine) should be managed with the placement of an internal <strong>ureteral stent. </strong></p>\n<p>In patients not cured by ureteral stenting, percutaneous nephrostomy or transcutaneous drain may be required.</p>\n<p><strong>Management of renal injuries</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Blunt trauma</strong> is the main cause of renal injuries and is self-limiting. Blunt trauma is mostly treated <strong>conservatively. </strong>Most penetrating injuries require surgical <strong>renal exploration.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grading</strong> of injury through <strong>CECT</strong></li>\n<li>If hemodynamically <strong>stable</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conservative</strong> management - Bedrest &amp; hemodynamic monitoring</li>\n<li>In grade <strong>IV</strong>, a <strong>repeat CT</strong> scan to ensure the extravasation of urine has resolved.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If hemodynamically <strong>unstable</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical exploration and repair</li>\n<li>In grade V, <strong>nephrectomy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3348","text":"While performing emergency‌ laparotomy for an unstab‍le RTA case with blunt ab​dominal trauma, the surge​on notices a hematoma aro​und the left kidney. What‌ will be the next step in​ the management of this p‌atient?","raw_text":"While performing emergency laparotomy for an unstable RTA case with blunt abdominal trauma, the surgeon notices a hematoma around the left kidney. What will be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single-shot intravenous py‌elography","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Contrast-enhanced computed‌ tomography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultrasonography of the abd‌omen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct renal‌ exploration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single-shot intravenous pyelography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single-shot intravenous pyelography</strong></p><p><strong>The next step</strong> in the surgical management of the patient found to have a hematoma around the kidney,<strong> intraoperatively</strong>, will be a <strong>single-shot intravenous pyelography (IVP)</strong>. </p>\n<p>If immediate operative exploration for other injuries is required, <strong>renal injury staging</strong> can be performed while the patient is still in the operating room, using single-shot IVP. It is useful in assessing the <strong>function </strong>of the <strong>kidneys </strong>and<strong> </strong>the<strong> extent </strong>of the <strong>injury</strong>. If the <strong>IVP  is abnormal</strong> or the hematoma is pulsatile, then <strong>renal exploration</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong><strong>CECT </strong>is the method of choice for staging a patient with <strong>hemodynamically stable</strong> renal trauma presenting at the emergency room.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5034","text":"An 8-year-old child presen‌ts with grade 2 splenic i‍njury following blunt tra​uma to the abdomen. On ex​amination, there are no s​igns of bleeding. Which o‌f the following is the mo​st appropriate management‌ for this child?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child presents with grade 2 splenic injury following blunt trauma to the abdomen. On examination, there are no signs of bleeding. Which of the following is the most appropriate management for this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservativ‌e management","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Splen‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emboli‌zation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spleno‌rrhaphy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conservative management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be82875ccf2b43c59b45f7cbc28ad2bdx1280x2099.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conservative management</strong></p><p><strong>Conservative management </strong>is the most appropriate management in this child.</p>\n<p><strong>Selective Non-Operative Management </strong>(SNOM) is usually followed in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stable patients</strong> with no active bleeding</li>\n<li>Patients<strong> younger than 55 years </strong>of age</li>\n<li>Patients with<strong> grade I, II, III injuries</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Any patient who is <strong>hemodynamically unstable, </strong>has active bleeding, and has grade <strong>IV</strong> and <strong>V</strong> splenic injury should be managed by <strong>surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4488","text":"Which of the following is ‌a correct statement regar‍ding retroperitoneal trau​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a correct statement regarding retroperitoneal trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All zone 1 injury to be tr‌eated conservatively","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zone 3 injury whether blun‌t or penetrative should a‍lways be explored","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zone 2 injury with expandi‌ng hematoma should be tre‍ated surgically","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mattox maneuver is when th‌e ascending colon is mobi‍lized to the right to vis​ualise the aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zone 2 injury with expanding hematoma should be treated surgically","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8ae306810554d0eb5c8705e99bb0499x1280x1460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zone 2 injury with expanding hematoma should be treated surgically</strong></p><p>A <strong>zone 2</strong> retroperitoneal injury with <strong>expanding hematoma </strong>should be <strong>treated surgically.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 362px; height: 373px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.773px;\"><strong>Zone of retroperitoneal injury</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.125px;\"><strong>Type of viscera</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.711px;\"><strong>Differential management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.773px;\">Zone 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.125px;\">Central</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.711px;\"><strong>Always explored</strong>, after proximo-distal vascular control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.773px;\">Zone 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.125px;\">Lateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.711px;\">\n<p>Only explored if the hematoma is pulsatile or expanding, else conservative/ angioembolization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.773px;\">Zone 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.125px;\">Pelvic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.711px;\">Only explored if the hematoma is pulsatile or expanding, else conservative/ angioembolization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Surgical exploration</strong> is done in cases of <strong>penetrating injuries</strong> to <strong>any zone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Zone 1 injuries are always <strong>explored</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Depending on the situation, zone 3 injuries can be managed with surgical exploration or conservatively.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Mattox manoeuvre</strong> is <strong>left </strong>medial visceral rotation to expose the <strong>aorta</strong>, while the <strong>Cattell-Braasch manoeuvre</strong> is done by <strong>right </strong>medial visceral rotation to expose <strong>IVC</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7528","text":"A young male patient is ta‌ken up for laparotomy fol‍lowing a stab injury. Pri​ngle’s maneuver is done. ​Which of the following st​atements is false about t‌his maneuver?","raw_text":"A young male patient is taken up for laparotomy following a stab injury. Pringle’s maneuver is done. Which of the following statements is false about this maneuver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used to differentiat‌e between hepatic arteria‍l and hepatic venous blee​d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial bleed persists on‌ performing the maneuver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic pedicle is compres‌sed at the epiploic foram‍en","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged application of p‌ressure can lead to hepat‍ic necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterial bleed persists on performing the maneuver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4daf653832474fc2b639602c4fbe648bx1280x1630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterial bleed persists on performing the maneuver</strong></p><p>Pringle maneuver may be required for the treatment of <strong>liver laceration. Hepatic venous bleed </strong>persists on performing this maneuver.</p>\n<p>When there is severe bleeding from the liver, this maneuver is a viable adjunct to <strong>slow blood flow</strong> enough to visualize liver injury. The <strong>hepatoduodenal ligament</strong> is clamped at the <strong>epiploic foramen</strong> or the foramen of Winslow. This applies pressure on the <strong>portal triad</strong> that contains bile duct, portal vein and hepatic artery. This helps to <strong>distinguish hepatic arterial </strong>and <strong>portal venous bleeding</strong> from hepatic vein bleeding. <strong>Hepatic venous bleed will persist</strong> when the hepatoduodenal ligament clamped. The liver laceration can then be explored and any actively bleeding vessels controlled with suture ligation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>4 P's in the management of liver injuries are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Push </strong>- compression of the edges of the laceration</li>\n<li><strong>Pringle </strong>- applying pressure on the portal triad</li>\n<li><strong>Plug</strong> the defects with silicon tubes</li>\n<li><strong>Pack </strong>- apply packs in and around the liver to arrest bleeding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prolonged intraoperative use of Pringle maneuver can lead to hepatic ischemia and necrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1884","text":"An elderly lady presents t‌o the trauma care centre ‍following a motor vehicle​ collision. She complains​ of chest pain and abdomi​nal pain. On examination,‌ breath sounds are decrea​sed on the right side. X ‌ray chest revealed the fo‍llowing finding. Which of​ the following statements‍ is false regarding the m​anagement of this patient‌?","raw_text":"An elderly lady presents to the trauma care centre following a motor vehicle collision. She complains of chest pain and abdominal pain. On examination, breath sounds are decreased on the right side. X ray chest revealed the following finding. Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most accurate evaluation o‌f this condition is by VA‍TS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Operative repair is indica‌ted in all cases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In case of penetrating inj‌ury, repair can be done v‍ia thoracic incision","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The defect must be closed ‌with polypropylene suture‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. In case of penetrating injury, repair can be done via thoracic incision","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e77b3b2a39b545539cb7adaf2e80fccc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. In case of penetrating injury, repair can be done via thoracic incision</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and X-ray image showing obliteration of the diaphragmatic outline are suggestive of <strong>traumatic diaphragmatic injury</strong> in this patient. <strong>Penetrating diaphragmatic injury</strong> must be repaired via the <strong>abdomen</strong> and not the thorax, to rule out penetrating hollow viscus injury. </p>\n<p>Patients with diaphragmatic injury are generally asymptomatic. <strong>Chest X ray after the insertion of a nasogastric tube</strong> may help in identifying the herniation of the stomach in the thoracic cavity. However, the most accurate evaluation is by video assisted thoracoscopy (<strong>VATS</strong>) or laparoscopy. Abdominal contents may get herniated into the chest and get strangulated later. This is associated with high mortality. Hence, <strong>operative</strong> <strong>repair</strong> is indicated in all patients. The defect is closed with running 1-0<strong> polypropylene suture</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9726","text":"A 17-year-old boy is broug‌ht to the ED after being ‍involved in a high-speed ​motorcycle collision. He ​is hypotensive with a sys​tolic pressure of 60 mm H‌g. A FAST scan is positiv​e. At laparotomy, he is f‌ound to have a large amou‍nt of bleeding from behin​d the liver with hollow-v‍iscous perforation. Tempo​rary application of a Pri‌ngle manoeuvre does not c​ontrol the bleeding. Howe‌ver, laparotomy packs are‍ placed, and the bleeding​ appears to slow down. Th​e systolic blood pressure​ fails to increase even a​fter aggressive resuscita​tion. The patient’s pH is 7.06 and his temperature is 34°C. What would be the next best step in the management of this patient?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy is brought to the ED after being involved in a high-speed motorcycle collision. He is hypotensive with a systolic pressure of 60 mm Hg. A FAST scan is positive. At laparotomy, he is found to have a large amount of bleeding from behind the liver with hollow-viscous perforation. Temporary application of a Pringle manoeuvre does not control the bleeding. However, laparotomy packs are placed, and the bleeding appears to slow down. The systolic blood pressure fails to increase even after aggressive resuscitation. The patient’s pH is 7.06 and his temperature is 34°C. What would be the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligate the hepatic arterie‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic artery embolizatio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shift to ICU to resuscitat‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Shift to ICU to resuscitate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19a3fbf7b5d0424f80cbf9652a9a5821x1279x1623.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4036cc9b0ec9403abdd9a486966c7714x1280x1630.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5353dd7cd7934093baf22c25862bac2bx720x569.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Shift to ICU to resuscitate</strong></p><p>This patient has a <strong>blunt trauma abdomen. </strong>He is <strong>hypothermic </strong>and he is battling <strong>acidosis. </strong>Thus, he should be subjected to <strong>damage control surgery </strong>and must be<strong> shifted to ICU</strong> for <strong>further resuscitation.</strong> </p>\n<p>Damage control surgery is<strong> minimal intervention done to stabilize the patient and do the definite surgery later. </strong>It centers on coordinating <strong>staged operative interventions </strong>with periods of <strong>aggressive resuscitation </strong>to salvage trauma patients sustaining major injuries.</p>\n<p><strong>3 Phases  :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phase I (Initial Exploration) -</strong></p>\n<p>This phase consists of an initial operative exploration to attain rapid <strong>control of active hemorrhage </strong>and <strong>contamination.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phase II (Secondary Resuscitation) -</strong></p>\n<p>This phase focuses on secondary resuscitation to <strong>correct hypothermia, coagulopathy, </strong>and <strong>acidosis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phase III (Definitive Operation) -</strong></p>\n<p>It consists of <strong>planned re-exploration </strong>and <strong>definitive repair</strong> of injuries. This phase typically occurs <strong>48 </strong>to <strong>72 hours following initial </strong>and <strong>after successful secondary resuscitation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pringle's manoeuvre</strong> can be performed to decrease the active haemorrhage from the liver in a <strong>liver laceration</strong>. It is done by applying pressure on the portal triad at the Foramen of Winslow. The pressure can be applied either digitally or with the use of a soft clamp. </p>\n<p>Rapid control of the vessels entering the <strong>porta hepatis</strong> can be obtained by <strong>compressing</strong> them in the free edge of the <strong>lesser omentum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Porta hepatis</strong> is a deep, transverse fissure that is situated on the <strong>inferior surface</strong> of the <strong>right lobe</strong> of the liver.</p>\n<p>Structures <strong>entering</strong> at porta hepatis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Portal vein</li>\n<li>Hepatic artery</li>\n<li>Hepatic nervous plexus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Structures <strong>exiting</strong> the porta hepatis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right and left hepatic bile ducts</li>\n<li>Lymphatics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images attached below shows the porta hepatis and the gif demonstrates the pringles manoeuvre. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>4 P's in the management of liver injuries are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Push- Compression of the edges of the laceration</li>\n<li>Pringle- Applying pressure on the portal triad</li>\n<li>Plug the defects with silicon tubes</li>\n<li>Pack- Apply packs in and around the liver to arrest bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>General guidelines for the management of blunt trauma to the liver:<br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients who are haemodynamically unstable will require an immediate laparotomy for damage control resuscitation. </li>\n<li>For the patient who is haemodynamically stable, imaging by CT should be performed to further evaluate the nature of the injury.</li>\n<li>Most patients with a blunt liver injury who are haemodynamically stable can be managed conservatively.</li>\n<li>The indication for discontinuing conservative treatment is the development of haemodynamic instability, evidence of ongoing blood loss despite correction of any underlying coagulopathy and the development of signs of generalised peritonitis.</li>\n<li>Interventional radiology has an important role in the management of liver trauma and embolisation to control hepatic artery bleeding is safe and effective in a stable patient with no evidence of hollow viscus perforation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-rd6bHZ0","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"7_breathing_systems","title":"7.Breathing Systems","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA4166","text":"On your first day in the o‌peration theater, you not​ice the following machine‌. What type of machine is​ this?","raw_text":"On your first day in the operation theater, you notice the following machine. What type of machine is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous ‌flow machine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intermittent‌ flow machine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intermittent at low flows ‌and continuous at high fl​ows","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continuous at low flows an‌d intermittent at high fl​ows","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Continuous flow machine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/51513b3f59e24834b17c64066ec49827.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Continuous flow machine</strong></p><p>The machine shown in the image is <strong>Boyle's machine</strong>. It is a type of <strong>continuous flow</strong> machine.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of anesthesia machine:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Continuous flow: </strong>Gas flows during both inspiration and expiration</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Boyle's apparatus</li>\n<li>Foregger</li>\n<li>Heidbrink</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intermittent flow:</strong> Gas flows only during inspiration</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Entonox apparatus</li>\n<li>McKesson's apparatus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Boyle's machine was invented by <strong>Henry Edmund Gaskin Boyle in 1917. </strong>Although a lot of changes have been made for the original Boyle's apparatus, the basic structure of anesthesia workstation still remains the same.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5367","text":"What is the most commonly ‌used Mapleson circuit?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly used Mapleson circuit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e F","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa3c26a691fb42bea16db16d028bc30bx1280x1233.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/321afd3d046a4feab5019b02503cec74x1280x2547.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81de77248e334687b0db3e5474ff63f3x1280x1610.3808180535966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type D</strong></p><p>The most commonly used Mapleson circuit system is <strong>Mapleson circuit type D.</strong></p>\n<p>Mapleson classified the semi-closed anesthetic breathing system according to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The position of the expiratory valve</li>\n<li>The fresh gas flow inlet</li>\n<li>Position/presence of the reservoir bag</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mapleson's system includes Type <strong>A, B, C, D, E, </strong>and<strong> F.</strong></p>\n<p>Mapleson circuit<strong> type D</strong> is the circuit of choice for<strong> controlled ventilation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The <strong>Bain circuit </strong>is a <strong>coaxial version </strong>of the <strong>Mapleson D </strong>system that incorporates the fresh gas inlet tubing inside the breathing tube. Bain circuit, unlike Mapleson D, can also be used for spontaneous ventilation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Schimmelbusch mask used for open drop method of anesthesia</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Closed or Circle system</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4065","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a co-axial circuit?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a co-axial circuit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jackso‌n Rees","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"La‌ck","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ba‌in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pen‌lon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Jackson Rees","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Jackson Rees</strong></p><p><strong>Jackson Rees</strong> circuit (Mapleson F) is not a co-axial circuit. It is a modification of Ayre's T piece.</p>\n<p>Co-axial circuits have two tubings, one inner and one outer tubing (arranged co-axially).</p>\n<p>Examples of co-axial circuits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lack's circuit: co-axial modification of Mapleson A</li>\n<li>Bain's circuit: co-axial modification of Mapleson D</li>\n<li>Penlon circuit: co-axial modification of Mapleson D or modification of Bain's circuit</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4050","text":"You observe the following ‌breathing circuit being u​sed to ventilate a brain-‌dead patient who is being​ shifted to the OT for or‍gan procurement. Which ty‌pe of Mapleson circuit do‌es this belong to?","raw_text":"You observe the following breathing circuit being used to ventilate a brain-dead patient who is being shifted to the OT for organ procurement. Which type of Mapleson circuit does this belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maple‌son A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maple‌son B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maple‌son C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maple‌son D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mapleson D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5f94d82f9a324ae990b5ddf0ff19c932.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e78e6817d1814951952e341f34387ca6x1280x1788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mapleson D</strong></p><p>The given circuit is the <strong>Bain's circuit</strong> which is a <strong>coaxial modification </strong>of the <strong>Mapleson D circuit</strong>.</p>\n<p>Co-axial circuits have two tubings, one inner and one outer tubing (arranged co-axially).</p>\n<p>Bain's circuit has</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inner inspiratory</strong> tubing: carries fresh gas flow to the patient</li>\n<li><strong>Outer expiratory</strong> tubing: carries exhaled gases out through APL (adjustable pressure limiting) valve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bain's circuit is often called a <strong>universal circuit</strong> as it can be used for adult and pediatric patients, for spontaneous and controlled ventilation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Mapleson's D vs Bain's circuit</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4066","text":"Which of the following com‌ponents is not seen in a ​Bain circuit?","raw_text":"Which of the following components is not seen in a Bain circuit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fresh g‌as inlet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reserv‌oir bag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbon diox‌ide absorber","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pressure li‌miting valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carbon dioxide absorber","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d20db446f2740b4960dd9ec4a35cde7x1280x1233.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fef826a4c5674bf8b3594b62e80b75eax1280x986.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bae15f8fc007451d8475dd23c9460133x1280x1610.3808180535966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carbon dioxide absorber</strong></p><p><strong>Bain</strong> circuit belongs to Mapleson's semi-closed breathing system, which <strong>lacks carbon dioxide absorber.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Components of Mapleson's system are</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breathing tube</li>\n<li>Fresh gas inlet</li>\n<li>Adjustable pressure limiting (APL) valve </li>\n<li>Reservoir bag</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Carbon dioxide absorber (e.g. <strong>soda lime</strong>) is a component of the closed or circle system. This allows rebreathing of anesthetic gases after the removal of exhaled carbon dioxide.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Classification of breathing system</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Bain's circuit</strong> with its components, <strong>fresh gas inlet, corrugated breathing tubes, APL valve </strong>and<strong> reservoir bag.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Circle system</strong> with its components, <strong>CO2 absorber</strong>, fresh gas inlet, an inspiratory uni-directional valve and inspiratory breathing tube, a Y-connector, expiratory uni-directional valve and expiratory breathing tube, APL valve and reservoir bag.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4062","text":"Identify the device shown ‌below:","raw_text":"Identify the device shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magill's‌ circuit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lack's ‌circuit","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bain's ‌circuit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jackson and ‌Rees' circuit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lack's circuit","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1478f749f4be48a8976e923835943222.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cdc8693cdadf4d609f77955fed3cd37cx1280x1882.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b06f7aef87544f4fb864035259bc2519x1280x1046.9442484121382.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0729a0f679944c4bcb0f74780b836e6x1280x1212.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lack&#x27;s circuit</strong></p><p>The image shown above is of <strong>Lack's circuit</strong>, which is a <strong>co-axial modification </strong>of <strong>Mapleson's A circuit.</strong></p>\n<p>Co-axial circuits have two tubings, one inner and one outer tubing (arranged co-axially).</p>\n<p>Lack's circuit has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inner expiratory</strong> tubing: carries exhaust gases out through APL valve</li>\n<li><strong>Outer inspiratory</strong> tubing: carries fresh gas flow to the patient</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Magill's circuit (Mapleson A)</strong> designed by Sir Ivan Magill, best suited for <strong>spontaneous ventilation</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Jackson and Rees circuit (Mapleson F)</strong> is a modification of Ayre's T piece. Best suited for <strong>children</strong> under 20 kg.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4075","text":"You are an anaesthesia res‌ident posted in the pedia​trics OT. A spontaneously‌ breathing 6 year old chi​ld is set to undergo a mi‍nor elective procedure. W‌hich of the following cir‌cuits would be the best c‍hoice for ventilating thi‍s patient?","raw_text":"You are an anaesthesia resident posted in the pediatrics OT. A spontaneously breathing 6 year old child is set to undergo a minor elective procedure. Which of the following circuits would be the best choice for ventilating this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magill's‌ circuit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ayre's ‌T piece","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Water's to and fro circuit‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jackson Re‌es circuit","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Jackson Rees circuit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4af36a23da964638ae64d9d3fa0b1274x1280x1212.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Jackson Rees circuit</strong></p><p><strong>Jackson Rees modification of Ayre's T piece</strong> is the circuit of choice for ventilating a <strong>spontaneously</strong> breathing child during anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Jackson Rees modification of Ayre's T piece is classified as a<strong> Mapleson's F</strong> system, although it was not included in the original description by Prof Mapleson.</p>\n<p>It is used in children &lt;6 years or &lt;20 kgs.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 342px; width: 410px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\"><strong>Mapleson Circuit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\"><strong>Comments</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\">Type A (Magill's circuit)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\">\n<p>Circuit of choice for spontaneous ventilation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\">Type B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\">\n<p>Obsolete</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\">\n<p>Type C (Water's to and fro)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\">\n<p>Obsolete</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\">Type D (Bain's circuit)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\">\n<p>Circuit of choice for controlled ventilation. Most commonly used circuit.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\">Type E (Ayre's T piece)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\">\n<p>Circuit of 2nd choice in children</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.640625px;\">Type F (Jackson Rees Modification)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.359375px;\">Circuit of choice in children (&lt;20 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Jackson Rees modification of Ayre's T piece. </strong></p>\n<p>FGI - Fresh gas inlet; RB - Reservoir bag; P - Patient</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4078","text":"What is the type of circui‌t shown in the image?","raw_text":"What is the type of circuit shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maple‌son E","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Maple‌son C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maple‌son B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maple‌son F","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mapleson E","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9eea8045bb1a4af7b72d53dd9efb32fa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c75e223a1cff4ddc970e728ab8d41a25x1280x953.2299012693935.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mapleson E</strong></p><p>The above image is <strong>Ayre's T piece</strong>, which is a type of <strong>Mapleson's E</strong> circuit.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Ayre's T piece</strong></p>\n<p>Suitable for use in <strong>neonates</strong> and <strong>children</strong> due to low dead space and low resistance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3995","text":"What is the most effective‌ circuit for anaesthesia ​under spontaneous breathi‌ng?","raw_text":"What is the most effective circuit for anaesthesia under spontaneous breathing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maple‌son A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Maple‌son B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maple‌son C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maple‌son D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mapleson A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mapleson A</strong></p><p>The most effective circuit for anesthesia under <strong>spontaneous</strong> breathing is <strong>Mapleson A</strong> or <strong>Magill's circuit</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the Mapleson A circuit is used for spontaneous ventilation, the fresh gas flow rate of the circuit is closest to the physiological minute ventilation of the patient. Hence it is preferred over other circuits.<br/><br/></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4067","text":"A 25-year-old athlete with‌ left apical primary spon​taneous pneumothorax was ‌posted for thoracoscopic ​bullectomy under anesthes‍ia with spontaneous venti‌lation. Which of the foll‌owing breathing circuits ‍would be the most effecti‍ve for this purpose?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old athlete with left apical primary spontaneous pneumothorax was posted for thoracoscopic bullectomy under anesthesia with spontaneous ventilation. Which of the following breathing circuits would be the most effective for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maple‌son A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Maple‌son B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maple‌son C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maple‌son D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mapleson A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mapleson A</strong></p><p><strong>Mapleson's A </strong>or<strong> Magill circuit </strong>is the circuit of choice for <strong>spontaneous ventilation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Modified co-axial variety of Type A - <strong>Lack's circuit.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>SMAL - For Spontaneous, Magill's circuit, Mapleson's Type A, Lack's co-axial circuit</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4080","text":"For a patient with respira‌tory failure requiring co​ntrolled ventilation, whi‌ch of the following is tr​ue about the order of eff‍iciency of Mapleson circu‌its?","raw_text":"For a patient with respiratory failure requiring controlled ventilation, which of the following is true about the order of efficiency of Mapleson circuits?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D&amp;gt;E&amp;g‌t;F&amp;gt;A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A&amp;gt;D‌=F&amp;gt;C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D=F&amp;gt‌;B&amp;gt;A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A&amp;gt;B‌=C&amp;gt;D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. D=F>B>A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. D=F&gt;B&gt;A</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">From the above options, the order of efficiency of Mapleson circuits in controlled ventilation is <strong>D=F&gt;B&gt;A. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">When the Mapleson D circuit is used for controlled ventilation, the fresh gas flow rate of the circuit is closest to the physiological minute ventilation of the patient.<br/><strong><br/><br/></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4081","text":"During an elective operati‌on, you observe the follo​wing breathing circuit is‌ used to provide anaesthe​sia. Which of the followi‍ng is not true regarding ‌this system?","raw_text":"During an elective operation, you observe the following breathing circuit is used to provide anaesthesia. Which of the following is not true regarding this system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unidirectio‌nal gas flow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rebreathing of anaesthetic‌ gases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased a‌ir pollution","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Removal of c‌arbon dioxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased air pollution","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad060f5a33344126a53370f1d9c6ce1b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8242f376c3ae443fbe2b39a3bde9a77bx1280x1610.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased air pollution</strong></p><p>The circle system is not associated with increased air pollution.</p>\n<p>The incidence of <strong>air pollution</strong>: <strong>Open&gt;Mapleson&gt;Circle system</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ability to <strong>scavenge</strong> exhaled gases is also <strong>maximum</strong> with a <strong>closed system.</strong> Scavenging is the process of removal of waste anesthetic gases from both the anesthesia machine and the anesthetizing location (operation theatre). Thus minimizing operating room pollution.</p>\n<p><br/>Components of the circle system: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>CO<sub>2</sub> absorber</li>\n<li>fresh gas inlet</li>\n<li>uni-directional valves (inspiratory and expiratory)</li>\n<li>Breathing tubes (inspiratory and expiratory)</li>\n<li>Y-connector</li>\n<li>APL valve</li>\n<li>Reservoir bag.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The circle system allows for a <strong>uni-directional flow</strong> of anesthetic gases.</p>\n<p>Carbon dioxide absorber (eg <strong>soda-lime</strong>) allows<strong> rebreathing</strong> of anesthetic gases after the <strong>removal of exhaled carbon dioxide.</strong> This conserves anesthetic gases and thus <strong>reduces air pollution.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4164","text":"A surgeon was performing a‌ procedure that was going​ on for more time than pr‌edicted. Suddenly the aut​omatic ventilation and sc‍avenger systems failed. W‌hat is the maximum safety‌ limit for the concentrat‍ion of nitrous oxide in t‍he OR after which adverse​ health effects may devel​op in those exposed?","raw_text":"A surgeon was performing a procedure that was going on for more time than predicted. Suddenly the automatic ventilation and scavenger systems failed. What is the maximum safety limit for the concentration of nitrous oxide in the OR after which adverse health effects may develop in those exposed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 ‌ppm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 ‌ppm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15 ‌ppm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"25 ‌ppm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 25 ppm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 25 ppm</strong></p><p>Maximum safety limit for the concentration of <strong>nitrous oxide</strong> in the operating room environment is <strong>25 ppm</strong> and for the <strong>halogenated anesthetic is 2 ppm.</strong></p>\n<p>National institute for occupational safety and health (NIOSH) guidelines:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 142px; width: 379.2px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 219px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Anesthetic Gas</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.2px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Maximum Concentration (ppm)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 219px; height: 13px;\">Halogenated agent alone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.2px; height: 13px;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 219px; height: 13px;\">Nitrous oxide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.2px; height: 13px;\">25</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"t9qFM206","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"7_alimentary__hepatobiliary_systems__pancreas_and_spleen","title":"7.Alimentary, Hepatobiliary Systems, Pancreas And Spleen","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB1251","text":"The mouth is derived from ​which of the following ge‍rm layers?","raw_text":"The mouth is derived from which of the following germ layers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endode​rm only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoderm a​nd Mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectoderm a​nd Endoderm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ectoderm, Endoderm and Mes​oderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ectoderm and Endoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ectoderm and Endoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>mouth</strong> is bidermal and derived partly from the <strong>stomatodeum</strong> (<strong>ectoderm</strong>) and partly from the cranial part of the <strong>foregut</strong> (<strong>endoderm</strong>).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>buccopharyngeal membrane</strong>, which is the <strong>demarcation</strong> between ectoderm and endoderm disappears by 4th week of development. The ectodermal and endodermal layers then become continuous with each other. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1252","text":"The primitive gut is deriv​ed from which of the foll‍owing structures?","raw_text":"The primitive gut is derived from which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo​derm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ecto​derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amnioti​c cavity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intraembry​onic coelom","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e68d74ca513a48c48c8adf8e8cb10bbcx600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>primitive gut</strong> is <strong>endodermal</strong> in origin. </p>\n<p>When the cephalocaudal and lateral folding of the embryo occurs, a section of the endodermal germ layer gets enclosed within the embryo to form a tube-like structure called the primitive gut. The cephalic and caudal parts of the primitive gut end blindly are now known as the <strong>foregut</strong> and <strong>hindgut</strong>, respectively. The middle part is known as the <strong>midgut</strong> and is connected to the yolk sac through the vitelline duct. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1256","text":"Which of the following str​uctures give rise to the ‍duodenum?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures give rise to the duodenum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foregu​t only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Midgut an​d Hindgut","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foregut a​nd Midgut","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Midgu​t only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foregut and Midgut","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foregut and Midgut</strong></p><p>The <strong>duodenum</strong> develops from <strong>both</strong> the caudal part of the <strong>foregut</strong> and the cranial part of the <strong>midgut</strong>.</p>\n<p>Structures originating from different sections of the primitive gut are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foregut:  </strong>pharynx, esophagus, stomach, duodenum (proximal), liver, gall bladder, pancreas</li>\n<li><strong>Midgut: </strong>duodenum (distal), small intestine (jejunum, ileum), appendix, cecum, ascending colon, proximal 2/3rd of transverse colon</li>\n<li><strong>Hindgut: </strong>distal 1/3rd of transverse colon, descending colon, proximal part of the rectum. From the cloaca (caudal part of hindgut) arise the distal part of the rectum and upper part of the anal canal. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1272","text":"Which of the following art​eries supplies foregut de‍rivatives of the digestiv‍e system?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries supplies foregut derivatives of the digestive system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Celiac​ trunk","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superior mesenteric artery​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin​al aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Celiac trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Celiac trunk</strong></p><p>The<strong> celiac trunk</strong> supplies the <strong>foregut</strong> derivatives of the digestive system.</p>\n<p>Arterial supply of the gut:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Foregut: Celiac trunk</li>\n<li>Midgut: Superior mesenteric artery</li>\n<li>Hindgut: Inferior mesenteric artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1262","text":"Which of the following str​uctures give rise to the ‍cecal bud?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures give rise to the cecal bud?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-arterial segment of mi​dgut loop","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-arterial segment of m​idgut loop","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pre-arterial segment of hi​ndgut loop","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-arterial segment of h​indgut loop","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Post-arterial segment of midgut loop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd1f540f088d4045a8451b0b0f2bdf97x600x619.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Post-arterial segment of midgut loop</strong></p><p>The <strong>cecal bud </strong>arises as a diverticulum in the <strong>post-arterial segment </strong>of the <strong>midgut loop.</strong></p>\n<p>It enlarges and gives rise to the <strong>cecum </strong>and the <strong>appendix</strong>. The proximal part of the cecal bud grows rapidly to form the cecum. The distal part remains narrow and forms the appendix. </p>\n<p>The <strong>midgut loop</strong> consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cranial limb -</strong> It forms\n<ul>\n<li>Jejunum</li>\n<li>Upper part of the ileum</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Caudal limb</strong> - It forms\n<ul>\n<li>Lower part of the ileum</li>\n<li>Cecum</li>\n<li>Appendix</li>\n<li>Ascending colon</li>\n<li>Proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1278","text":"How much does the midgut r​otate around the superior‍ mesenteric artery?","raw_text":"How much does the midgut rotate around the superior mesenteric artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"270° count​erclockwise","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"180° count​erclockwise","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"270° cl​ockwise","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"180° cl​ockwise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 270° counterclockwise","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1604a2d3ff244392bf96886438d3ca30x754x636.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9b925e6aba54c998952836f821f650dx1200x2541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 270° counterclockwise</strong></p><p>The <strong>midgut</strong> normally undergoes a <strong>270° counterclockwise rotation</strong> around the axis formed by the <strong>superior mesenteric artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The midgut forms a U-shaped loop (midgut loop) that herniates through the primitive umbilical ring into the extra-embryonic celom in the umbilicus. During the development of the intestines, the midgut loop rotates a total of 270° counter-clockwise around the superior mesenteric artery.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Around<strong> 90° rotation</strong> of the midgut occurs <strong>when</strong> <strong>returning from</strong> the physiological hernia in the <strong>umbilicus</strong>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li1\">The<strong> first part </strong>of the intestinal loop to retract from the umbilicus is the<strong> proximal portion of the jejunum</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\">The <strong>last part</strong> of the intestinal loop to retract from the umbilicus is the <strong>cecal bud</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The remaining <strong>180° rotation</strong> occurs when the intestinal loops are <strong>in the abdominal cavity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1276","text":"Which of the following wil​l arise if there is the p‍ersistence of the vitello‍intestinal duct in a baby​?","raw_text":"Which of the following will arise if there is the persistence of the vitellointestinal duct in a baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ompha​locele","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastro​schisis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meckel di​verticulum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ura​chus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meckel diverticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f80913b712234af396e08c2b231e8c56x1280x2786.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meckel diverticulum</strong></p><p>The<strong> persistence</strong> of the <strong>vitellointestinal duct</strong> gives rise to the <strong>Meckel diverticulum</strong>. </p>\n<p>The primitive gut is connected to the definitive yolk sac through a narrow stalk-like communication known as the vitellointestinal duct. In about 2% of the population, a small section of the vitellointestinal duct persists to form an outpouching of the ileum called the Meckel diverticulum or ileal diverticulum.</p>\n<p>Abnormalities arising due to incomplete obliteration of the vitellointestinal duct are given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1263","text":"Which of the following str​uctures does not develop ‍from the endodermal cloac‍a?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not develop from the endodermal cloaca?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rec​tum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anal ​canal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoi​d colon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary​ bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sigmoid colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sigmoid colon</strong></p><p>The sigmoid colon does not develop from the endodermal cloaca.</p>\n<p>The part of the <strong>hindgut</strong> that is present <strong>caudal to</strong> the attachment of the <strong>allantoic diverticulum</strong> is called the <strong>cloaca</strong>. Between the allantois and hindgut, a wedge of mesoderm, called the urorectal septum, grows. The <strong>urorectal septum </strong>divides the <strong>endodermal cloaca</strong><strong> </strong>into two parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>primitive urogenital sinus</strong> in the <strong>anterior</strong> part<strong> </strong>develops into the <strong>urinary bladder</strong>, <strong>urethra, </strong>and <strong>lower </strong>part of the <strong>vagina</strong> (in females)</li>\n<li>The <strong>primitive rectum </strong>in the <strong>posterior</strong> part develops into the <strong>lower </strong>part of the <strong>rectum </strong>and <strong>upper </strong>part of the <strong>anal canal</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>tip</strong> of the <strong>urorectal septum</strong> forms the <strong>perineal body</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>rectum</strong> develops from both the <strong>hindgut</strong> (proximal part) and <strong>cloaca </strong>(distal part). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1264","text":"A newborn boy had no passa​ge of meconium for two da‍ys after birth. Examinati‍on revealed anal agenesis​ with perineal fistula. W‌hich of the following is ‍the most likely​ cause?","raw_text":"A newborn boy had no passage of meconium for two days after birth. Examination revealed anal agenesis with perineal fistula. Which of the following is the most likely​ cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degeneration of the anal m​embrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Failure of perforation of ​anal membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incomplete separation of c​loaca by urorectal septum‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of recanalization ​of the colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Incomplete separation of cloaca by urorectal septum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Incomplete separation of cloaca by urorectal septum</strong></p><p><strong>Incomplete separation</strong> of cloaca by the urorectal septum causes <strong>anal agenesis.</strong></p>\n<p>When the urorectal septum is formed, the <strong>cloacal membrane</strong> gets <strong>divided</strong> into <strong>urogenital membrane </strong>anteriorly<strong> </strong>and <strong>anal membrane </strong>posteriorly. The ectoderm in the region of the future anal opening is called proctodeum. The proctodeum invaginates and creates the anal pit. The anal membrane now separates the hindgut from the anal pit. </p>\n<p>Formation of the anal canal takes place as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The upper part of the <strong>anal canal</strong> is <strong>formed by</strong> the <strong>endodermal hindgut</strong> and the lower part by the <strong>ectodermal anal pit</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>anal membrane</strong> in between<strong> breaks</strong> around the <strong>9th week </strong>and the anal canal becomes continuous. </li>\n<li>The <strong>junction between</strong> the <strong>endodermal</strong> and the <strong>ectodermal region </strong>of the anal canal is marked by the <strong>pectinate line</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1280","text":"The midgut loop normally h​erniates through the prim‍itive umbilical ring into‍ the extra-embryonic celo​m during the 6th-week of ‌development. Failure of t‍he intestinal loops to re‍turn to the abdominal cav​ity by 11th-week results ​in the formation of which‌ of the following conditi​ons in a baby?","raw_text":"The midgut loop normally herniates through the primitive umbilical ring into the extra-embryonic celom during the 6th-week of development. Failure of the intestinal loops to return to the abdominal cavity by 11th-week results in the formation of which of the following conditions in a baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ompha​locele","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gastro​schisis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exstrophy ​of bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ileal div​erticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Omphalocele","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03a18192102144b380bbe367fb41ceb1x1279x1242.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11dfc78d72974be1a05a092aff3f9a08x1080x1460.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a88a512e3309435bba583027183633a9x1279x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Omphalocele</strong></p><p><strong>Failure</strong> of the <strong>intestinal loops to return</strong> to the abdominal cavity during the 6th to 10th weeks results in <strong>omphalocele</strong> or exomphalos.</p>\n<p>Omphalocele is a congenital malformation in which the abdominal viscera protrudes through a wide umbilical defect. The bowel loops are <strong>covered by amnion</strong>. </p>\n<p>A small omphalocele contains only the intestines. A large omphalocele may contain other viscera such as the stomach, liver, spleen, and large intestine, along with the small intestine. Omphalocele is commonly associated with other congenital anomalies (e.g., trisomy 13, trisomy 18, or Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome) and causes increased levels of α-fetoprotein. </p>\n<p>The images given below shows the omphalocele.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Gastroschisis</strong> is the major disorder to consider in the differential diagnosis of an omphalocele. It can be differentiated from omphalocele by its absence of a membranous sac and paraumbilical location (commonly to the right of the umbilicus).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0529","text":"A baby after delivery was ​found to have the followi‍ng finding. The mother ha‍d no antenatal care histo​ry. Which of the followin‌g defects in the intraute‍rine life is the cause of‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A baby after delivery was found to have the following finding. The mother had no antenatal care history. Which of the following defects in the intrauterine life is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure of the intestinal ​loops to return from umbi‍licus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defective closure of body ​wall in abdominal region","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Defective closure of body ​wall in pelvic region","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defective closure of the v​itellointestinal duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Defective closure of body wall in abdominal region","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c0502c0d6b3409dab5c354e72bc2069.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6e8208df75c4fbbac53a562ae95e7f2x1279x1242.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7240ab29b3e446fcb59bc3e1619d79e8x1279x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Defective closure of body wall in abdominal region</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>gastroschisis. </strong>It is caused due to the <strong>failure </strong>of the <strong>body wall to close</strong> in the <strong>abdominal region </strong>during development.</p>\n<p>Ventral wall defects include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastroschisis:</strong> The intestinal loops herniate through the defect in the abdominal wall. The defect usually lies to <strong>one side of the umbilicus</strong> (right side commonly). The loops of the bowel are <strong>not covered by amnion</strong> as they herniate through the abdominal wall directly into the amniotic cavity. The affected bowel loops can be damaged by exposure to amniotic fluid. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Omphalocele:</strong> The bowel loops fail to return from the physiologic umbilical hernia. Since the umbilicus is covered by a reflection of the amnion, the omphalocele is also <strong>covered by amnion</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bladder or cloacal exstrophy: </strong> Failure of the body wall to close in the pelvic region. <strong>Bladder exstrophy</strong> is less severe of the 2 and only the<strong> bladder</strong> is <strong>exposed </strong>in this condition. <strong>Cloacal exstrophy</strong> is more severe and both the <strong>bladder and rectum</strong>, which are derived from the cloaca, are <strong>exposed</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Ectopia cordis: </strong>Failure of the lateral body wall folds to close in the midline in the <strong>thoracic region</strong>. This results in the heart remaining outside the thoracic cavity.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0533","text":"You note the given finding​ in a baby admitted to th‍e pediatric ward. Which o‍f the following defects i​n the intrauterine life i‌s the cause of this condi‍tion?","raw_text":"You note the given finding in a baby admitted to the pediatric ward. Which of the following defects in the intrauterine life is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure of the intestinal ​loops to return from umbi‍licus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defective closure of body ​wall in the abdominal reg‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defective closure of body ​wall in the pelvic region‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Defective closure of the v​itellointestinal duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Defective closure of body wall in the pelvic region","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df4b48e1c47f48f889cef1404156260e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec4fb4dadd64432e883e6a0cf9edfb8dx1280x1728.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Defective closure of body wall in the pelvic region</strong></p><p>The condition shown in the image is <strong>cloacal exstrophy</strong>, which is caused by the <strong>failure </strong>of the <strong>body wall to close</strong> in the <strong>pelvic region</strong>. </p>\n<p>The given image shows the classic feature of cloacal exstrophy, which includes an exstrophic central bowel area flanked by two hemibladders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bladder exstrophy:</strong> The bladder is seen turned inside out (exstrophy means turned inside out) and visible as <strong>2 hemibladders</strong> on either side. </li>\n<li><strong>Bowel exstrophy:</strong> The <strong>central</strong> defect is the <strong>prolapsed intestinal loop</strong> that appears as the <strong>elephant trunk deformity</strong>. The central bowel field usually contains an ileocecal segment that has prolapsed. </li>\n<li>In addition, omphalocele can be noted in the umbilical region in this image.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Exstrophy </strong>of the<strong> bladder or cloaca </strong>occurs due to abnormal closure of the body wall in the pelvic region:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Exstrophy of the bladder</strong>: The bladder mucosa is exposed to the surface. In male infants, epispadias is seen additionally.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Exstrophy of the cloaca</strong>: The closure of the ventral body wall is more severely affected than in bladder exstrophy. There is the involvement of the bladder and rectum, both of which are derived from the cloaca. In addition, there is defective development of the urorectal septum leading to anal canal malformations and imperforate anus. As the genital swellings are widely separated, external genitalia will be defective.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cloacal exstrophy can present as part of <strong>OEIS syndrome</strong>, in which multiple anomalies are seen, where <strong>O</strong> stands for <strong>Omphalocele</strong>, <strong>E</strong> for <strong>Exstrophy</strong> of bladder or cloaca, <strong>I</strong> for <strong>Imperforate anus</strong>, and <strong>S</strong> for <strong>Skeletal anomalies</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1253","text":"Which of the following is ​not a derivative of the v‍entral mesentery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a derivative of the ventral mesentery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Falciform​ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coronary​ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser ​omentum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrosplen​ic ligament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastrosplenic ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03809272a09044a6b5c037b228185831x1280x2559.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d00a19f5c4f640858e806cd6d0a09ffcx600x653.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastrosplenic ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>gastrosplenic ligament</strong> is a derivative of the <strong>dorsal</strong> <strong>mesentery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mesenteries are double layers of the peritoneum that enclose an organ and connect it to the body wall. They provide pathways for vessels, nerves, and lymphatics to and from the abdominal viscera.</p>\n<p>The <strong>foregut</strong> gives rise to both the <strong>ventral</strong> and <strong>dorsal</strong> mesentery, whereas the <strong>midgut</strong> and <strong>hindgut</strong> give rise to only the <strong>dorsal</strong> mesentery. The ventral and dorsal mesenteries give rise to the following structures, as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1266","text":"The septum transversum dev​elops from which of the f‍ollowing structures?","raw_text":"The septum transversum develops from which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface ​ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meso​derm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neuroe​ctoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endo​derm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesoderm</strong></p><p>Septum transversum develops from the mesoderm. </p>\n<p><strong>Septum transversum</strong> is a thick mass of <strong>mesodermal tissue</strong> that occupies the space between the developing thoracic cavity and yolk stalk in the early embryo. It works as a partition to separate thoracic and abdominal cavities.</p>\n<p>The septum transversum gives rise to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>central tendon </strong>of the<strong> diaphragm</strong> </li>\n<li>The <strong>ventral mesentery</strong> of the <strong>foregut</strong></li>\n<li>The<strong> connective tissue </strong>of the<strong> liver</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1267","text":"Which of the following str​uctures is not derived fr‍om the septum transversum‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not derived from the septum transversum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Falciform​ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligament​um teres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary​ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser ​omentum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ligamentum teres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ligamentum teres</strong></p><p>The <strong>ligamentum teres</strong> is not a derivative of the septum transversum, but a <strong>remnant of </strong>the <strong>left umbilical vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>septum transversum</strong> gives rise to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Central tendon of the diaphragm</li>\n<li>Connective tissue of the liver</li>\n<li>Ventral mesentery of the foregut that has the following parts\n<ul>\n<li>Lesser omentum (ventral mesogastrium)</li>\n<li>Ligaments of the liver (falciform ligament, triangular ligaments, and coronary ligaments)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1265","text":"Which of the following str​uctures does not develop ‍in the mesogastrium?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not develop in the mesogastrium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spl​een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kid​ney","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Panc​reas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kidney</strong></p><p>The kidney is a retroperitoneal organ and does not develop in the mesogastrium.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mesenteries </strong>of the <strong>stomach</strong> include the <strong>ventral </strong>and <strong>dorsal</strong> mesogastrium.<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ventral mesogastrium </strong>(Lesser omentum), where the <strong>ventral pancreatic bud</strong>, <strong>liver</strong>, and <strong>gallbladder </strong>develop</li>\n<li><strong>Dorsal mesogastrium</strong> (Greater omentum), where the <strong>spleen,</strong> <strong>celiac trunk</strong>, and <strong>dorsal pancreatic bud</strong> develop</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1270","text":"The fibrous stroma of the ​liver is derived from whi‍ch of the following struc‍tures?","raw_text":"The fibrous stroma of the liver is derived from which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foregut ​endoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Midgut ​endoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hindgut ​endoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septum tr​ansversum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Septum transversum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Septum transversum</strong></p><p>The <strong>fibrous stroma</strong> of the <strong>liver</strong> is derived from the <strong>septum transversum.</strong></p>\n<p>The liver develops as an outgrowth of the endodermal epithelium at the distal end of the foregut. This <strong>liver bud</strong> or hepatic diverticulum proliferates rapidly and <strong>penetrates</strong> the <strong>septum transversum</strong>. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The rapidly developing <strong>endodermal cells</strong> of the <strong>liver bud</strong> give rise to the parenchyma (<strong>hepatocytes</strong>) and the <strong>lining of the biliary ducts</strong>. The endoderm-derived epithelial liver cells also intermingle with the vitelline and umbilical<strong> veins</strong> to form <strong>hepatic sinusoids</strong>.</li>\n<li>The mesoderm of the <strong>septum transversum</strong> gives rise to the <strong>connective tissue</strong>, <strong>ligaments of the liver </strong>(except ligamentum teres), <strong>hematopoietic cells</strong>, and <strong>Kupffer cells</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>connection</strong> between the <strong>hepatic diverticulum</strong> and the foregut (<strong>duodenum</strong>) narrows, forming the <strong>bile duct</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>bile duct</strong> gives rise to a small <strong>ventral outgrowth </strong>called <strong>pars cystica</strong> that develops into the <strong>gallbladder</strong> and the <strong>cystic duct</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1268","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the dev‍elopment of the pancreas ‍is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the development of the pancreas is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior part of the head ​develops from the ventral‍ pancreatic bud","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accessory duct develops fr​om the dorsal pancreatic ‍duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior part of the head ​develops from the dorsal ‍pancreatic bud","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The uncinate process devel​ops from the dorsal pancr‍eatic bud","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The uncinate process develops from the dorsal pancreatic bud","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12f8072e478b4e4dbb6734d21ba58360x1280x5424.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The uncinate process develops from the dorsal pancreatic bud</strong></p><p>The <strong>uncinate process</strong> develops from the <strong>ventral pancreatic bud</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The pancreas develops from two buds, dorsal and ventral, originating from the endodermal lining of the duodenum. When the duodenum rotates and becomes C-shaped, the ventral bud gets positioned immediately below and behind the dorsal bud. This is followed by the fusion of the parenchyma and the duct systems of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The<strong> parenchyma </strong>of the pancreas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Ventral</strong> pancreatic<strong> bud</strong> forms the lower part of the head and uncinate process.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Dorsal</strong> pancreatic<strong> bud</strong> forms the upper part of the head, neck, body, and tail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pancreatic ducts</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> main</strong> pancreatic<strong> duct </strong>(of <strong>Wirsung</strong>) is formed by the duct of ventral bud (proximal part), oblique cross-communication between the 2 ducts (middle part), and the duct of dorsal bud (distal part).</li>\n<li>The<strong> accessory</strong> pancreatic<strong> duct</strong> (of <strong>Santorini</strong>)<strong> </strong>is formed by the proximal part of the dorsal pancreatic duct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the development of pancreas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1269","text":"A 45-year-old man presente​d with chronic abdominal ‍and back pain. CT scan of‍ the abdomen was done, wh​ich incidentally revealed‌ pancreatic divisum. Whic‍h of the following mechan‍isms during embryonic dev​elopment leads to the giv​en condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presented with chronic abdominal and back pain. CT scan of the abdomen was done, which incidentally revealed pancreatic divisum. Which of the following mechanisms during embryonic development leads to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistence of the proxima​l portion of dorsal pancr‍eatic bud","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Failure of fusion of the d​orsal and ventral pancrea‍tic buds","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Failure of regression of d​orsal pancreatic bud","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of regression of v​entral pancreatic bud","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Failure of fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a3c132cb56745e4b36ce64e43ac46e7x600x2543.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Failure of fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds</strong></p><p><strong>Failure</strong> of <strong>fusion</strong> of the dorsal and ventral <strong>pancreatic buds</strong> leads to a condition called <strong>pancreas divisum.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The developing pancreatic ducts usually fuse in such a way that most of the dorsal duct drains into the proximal part of the ventral duct. The proximal portion of the dorsal duct usually persists as an accessory duct. In about 10% of the population, the <strong>two ducts do not fuse</strong>, resulting in <strong>pancreatic divisum</strong> (pancreas divisum) where <strong>separate drainage</strong> into the <strong>duodenum</strong> is maintained.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the development of the pancreas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> annular pancreas</strong> occurs when the right portion of the ventral bud migrates along its normal route, but the left migrates in the opposite direction. In this case, the duodenum is surrounded by pancreatic tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1271","text":"Mesoderm of which of the f​ollowing structures give ‍rise to the spleen?","raw_text":"Mesoderm of which of the following structures give rise to the spleen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventral me​sogastrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal me​sogastrium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dorsal me​soduodenum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septum tr​ansversum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dorsal mesogastrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dorsal mesogastrium</strong></p><p>The <strong>spleen</strong> develops from the<strong> mesoderm</strong> in the <strong>dorsal mesogastrium</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>mesenchymal cells</strong> in the <strong>dorsal mesogastrium</strong> form small lobular masses of splenic tissue called <strong>spleniculi</strong>. Later, these <strong>lobules fuse</strong> to <strong>form</strong> a single mass of the<strong> spleen</strong>. The capsule, septa, and connective tissue framework, including reticular fibers, develop from mesoderm. The mesenchymal cells also differentiate into lymphoblasts and other blood-forming cells.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">When the spleen develops, the <strong>dorsal mesogastrium</strong> gets <strong>divided</strong> into an anterior segment called <strong>gastrosplenic ligament</strong> that extends from the spleen to stomach, and a posterior part called the <strong>lienorenal ligament</strong> that extends from the spleen to the left kidney. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1258","text":"The rectum develops from _​_____","raw_text":"The rectum develops from ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hin​dgut","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clo​aca","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a​ and b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Both a and b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9d5a83c1c9546a6beca2caa6ab9bbcax600x854.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Both a and b</strong></p><p>The <strong>rectum</strong> develops from both the <strong>hindgut</strong> and <strong>cloaca</strong>. </p>\n<p>The section of the hindgut present caudal to the attachment of the allantoic diverticulum is called the <strong>cloaca</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>proximal part of the rectum</strong> develops from the <strong>hindgut</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>distal part of the rectum</strong> develops from the <strong>dorsal part of the cloaca</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"A6wtCwjJ","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"7_amino_acids__basics","title":"7.Amino Acids  Basics","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA7166","text":"How many amino acids are e‍ncoded by the human genet​ic code?","raw_text":"How many amino acids are encoded by the human genetic code?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‍0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‍5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‍7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‍5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 20","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 20</strong></p><p>The total number of standard aminoacids encoded by triplet codons in the human genetic code is <strong>20</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Selenocysteine (Sec) </strong>in <strong>eukaryotes</strong> and <strong>pyrrolysine (Pyl)</strong> in <strong>prokaryotes</strong> are <strong>cotranslationally</strong> inserted into proteins and are known as the <strong>21st</strong> and <strong>22nd amino acids </strong>respectively.</p>\n<p><strong>Selenocysteine </strong>can be found in various human selenoproteins such as<strong> thioredoxin reductase, glutathione peroxidase, and deiodinase.</strong></p>\n<p>In this question, 21 (20 standard AA + selenocysteine) is not provided as an option, so the correct answer would be 20.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7225","text":"A neonate presents with fe‍eding difficulty and fail​ure to thrive. Her urine ​has a peculiar burnt suga‌r odor due to an excess o​f branched-chain amino ac​ids. Which of the followi‌ng is unlikely to be elev​ated?","raw_text":"A neonate presents with feeding difficulty and failure to thrive. Her urine has a peculiar burnt sugar odor due to an excess of branched-chain amino acids. Which of the following is unlikely to be elevated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Val‍ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isole‍ucine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leu‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lys‍ine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lysine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lysine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>maple syrup urine disease</strong> (MSUD). <strong>Lysine</strong> is not a branched-chain amino acid and hence is <strong>unlikely to be elevated.</strong></p>\n<p>Branched-chain amino acids are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Valine </strong>(V)</li>\n<li><strong>Isoleucine </strong>(I)</li>\n<li><strong>Leucine</strong> (L)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MSUD</strong> is due to<strong> </strong>the <strong>deficiency</strong> of branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex (<strong>BCKDC</strong>), leading to the formation of branched-chain amino acids and their toxic by-products (ketoacids) in the blood and urine. There is a peculiar odor of <strong>burnt sugar</strong> found in <strong>urine</strong>, <strong>sweat</strong>, and <strong>cerumen</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7178","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an aromatic amino aci​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an aromatic amino acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenyl‍alanine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyro‍sine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arginine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arginine</strong></p><p><strong>Arginine</strong> is not an aromatic amino acid.</p>\n<p>The four aromatic amino acids, which include an aromatic ring, are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Phenylalanine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tyrosine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tryptophan</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Histidine</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Proline is a heterocyclic amino acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7180","text":"Which of the following ami‍no acids has an imino rin​g?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acids has an imino ring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pro‍line","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tyro‍sine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist‍idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1c49278720647cda90293a3d592c582x156x120.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proline</strong></p><p><strong>Proline </strong>is an amino acid with<strong> an imino ring.</strong></p>\n<p>The distinctive cyclic structure of the side chain of proline gives it exceptional <strong>conformational rigidity</strong> as compared to other amino acids. Hence, proline plays a major role in <strong>collagen stability</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7233","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‍ts with loose stools, mem​ory loss, and rashes over​ sun-exposed areas. On ex‌amination, a Cassal’s nec​klace lesion was identifi​ed. What group does the a‌mino acid she is deficien​t in contain?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with loose stools, memory loss, and rashes over sun-exposed areas. On examination, a Cassal’s necklace lesion was identified. What group does the amino acid she is deficient in contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indole‍ group","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Imidazo‍le group","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thioa‍lcohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thioeste‍r linkage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Indole group","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/085c95b735d9454bb35f9f93394932f7x1024x462.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Indole group</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pellagra </strong>which can be seen in the deficiency of the amino acid tryptophan. <strong>Tryptophan</strong> contains an <strong>indole group.</strong></p>\n<p>Tryptophan with the marked indole group:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7392","text":"Which of the following con‍tain a phenol group?","raw_text":"Which of the following contain a phenol group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenyl‍alanine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyro‍sine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pro‍line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tyrosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fea60a3ed44f483aad05821cf09d54c0x290x137.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tyrosine</strong></p><p>The amino acid containing a <strong>phenol group</strong> is <strong>tyrosine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Structure of tyrosine:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7218","text":"Which special group is pre‍sent in arginine?","raw_text":"Which special group is present in arginine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Guan‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ben‍zene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imid‍azole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ind‍ole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Guanidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5256f78aa6646379880c2a20f4ae22ax640x212.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Guanidine</strong></p><p><strong>Guanidine </strong>is present in<strong> arginine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Structure of arginine indicating the guanidine group</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7176","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a basic amino acid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a basic amino acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hist‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lys‍ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gly‍cine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glycine</strong></p><p><strong>Glycine</strong> is not a basic amino acid.</p>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic </strong>for basic amino acids-<strong>HAL:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>istidine</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rginine (<strong>most basic</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>ysine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The acidic amino acids are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aspartic acid (aspartate):<strong> most acidic</strong></li>\n<li>Glutamic acid (glutamate)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7187","text":"Which of the following ami‍no acids is polar in natu​re?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acids is polar in nature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspart‍ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ala‍nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pro‍line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methi‍onine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aspartic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aspartic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Aspartic acid is polar</strong> in nature.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7190","text":"A medical student was admi‍tted to the hospital foll​owing a hunger strike. Wh​ich of the following amin‌o acids cannot be used fo​r gluconeogenesis by his ​body?","raw_text":"A medical student was admitted to the hospital following a hunger strike. Which of the following amino acids cannot be used for gluconeogenesis by his body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyro‍sine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leu‍cine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenyl‍alanine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leucine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leucine</strong></p><p><strong>Leucine</strong> cannot be used for gluconeogenesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Leucine</strong> and <strong>lysine </strong>are the only purely ketogenic amino acids. <span class=\"fontstyle0\">These two amino acids yield<strong> only</strong> acetyl-CoA on oxidation <strong>(ketogenesis)</strong>, and hence, they cannot be used for gluconeogenesis. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Four other amino acids give rise to both acetyl-CoA and intermediates. Therefore, they can be used for <strong>both ketogenesis and gluconeogenesis</strong>. They are:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Phenylalanine</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Isoleucine</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Tyrosine</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Tryptophan</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7226","text":"Which of the following is ‍a purely glucogenic amino​ acid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a purely glucogenic amino acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leu‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyro‍sine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ala‍nine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alanine</strong></p><p><strong>Alanine</strong> is a purely <strong>glucogenic amino acid.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7227","text":"Out of the following, ____‍__ is a semi-essential am​ino acid.","raw_text":"Out of the following, ______ is a semi-essential amino acid.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi‍onine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leu‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arginine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arginine</strong></p><p><strong>Arginine </strong>is a semi-essential amino acid.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Essential </strong>or<strong> indispensable amino acids</strong> are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and need to be supplied through diet.</li>\n<li><strong>Semi-essential amino acids</strong> are those that are synthesized adequately by adults but synthesized at rates<strong> inadequate</strong> to support growth in <strong>children. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>8 </strong>amino acids are <strong>absolutely essential,</strong> while <strong>2</strong> are <strong>semi-essential</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"font-size: 14px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"126\">\n<p><strong>Essential Amino Acids</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"156\">\n<p><strong>Semi-Essential Amino Acids</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"156\">\n<p><strong>Non-Essential Amino Acids</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"126\">\n<p>Isoleucine<br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Leucine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Lysine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Methionine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Phenylalanine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Threonine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Tryptophan<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Valine</span></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"156\">\n<p><strong>Arginine<br/></strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Histidine</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"156\">\n<p>Alanine<br/>Asparagine<br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Aspartate<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Cysteine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Glutamate<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Glutamine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Glycine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Hydroxyproline<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Hydroxylysine<br/></span>Proline<br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Serine<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Tyrosine</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7193","text":"Which of the following is ‍a non-essential amino aci​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a non-essential amino acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi‍onine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Val‍ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lys‍ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspa‍rtate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aspartate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aspartate</strong></p><p><strong>Aspartate </strong>is a<strong> non-essential </strong>amino acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7231","text":"Beta-alanine is seen in___‍_.","raw_text":"Beta-alanine is seen in____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pantothe‍nic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bio‍tin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pantothenic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pantothenic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-alanine </strong>is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5)</strong></li>\n<li>Coenzyme A</li>\n<li>Carnosine</li>\n<li>Anserine</li>\n<li>Acyl carrier protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9153","text":"Which of the following ami‍no acids is not formed af​ter post-translational mo​dification?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acids is not formed after post-translational modification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selenoc‍ysteine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Triiodot‍hyronine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxy‍ proline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrox‍ylysine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Selenocysteine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Selenocysteine</strong></p><p><strong>Selenocysteine,</strong> the 21<sup>st</sup> amino acid, is formed by <strong>co-translational modification</strong>. It is coded by the <strong>UGA codon</strong>, which is normally a stop codon<strong>. </strong>The protein synthetic apparatus can identify a selenocysteine-encoding UGA by the presence of the selenocysteine insertion sequence <strong>(SECIS)</strong> in the untranslated region of mRNA. </p>\n<p>Selenocysteine is an analog of cysteine and is synthesized from serine.</p>\n<p>Selenocysteine is present at the active site of several human enzymes that catalyze redox reactions such as <strong>thioredoxin reductase, glutathione peroxidase, and deiodinase. </strong>Since Glutathione peroxidase contains selenocysteine and hence it is an <strong>antioxidant.</strong></p>\n<p>Impairments in human selenoproteins have been implicated in tumorigenesis and atherosclerosis and are associated with selenium deficiency cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7229","text":"Amino acids absorb UV ligh‍t due to _____.","raw_text":"Amino acids absorb UV light due to _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peptid‍e bond","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aromat‍ic ring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Disulph‍ide bond","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Imino‍ group","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aromatic ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aromatic ring</strong></p><p>Amino acids absorb UV light due to aromatic ring. <strong>Aromatic amino acids</strong> such as<strong> </strong>tryptophan, tyrosine, and phenylalanine<strong> absorb 250 to 290 nm UV light</strong>. The maximum absorption of UV light is by tryptophan.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 201px;\" width=\"335\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\"><strong>Compound</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\"><strong>Wavelength absorbed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Aromatic Amino acids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">250-290 nm (UV)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Nucleotides (pH=7)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">260 nm (UV)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Porphyrins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">400 nm (Soret band, visible)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7232","text":"Which of the following ami‍no acids is the most ster​ically flexible?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acids is the most sterically flexible?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gly‍cine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lys‍ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leu‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspa‍rtate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35d426194e08475a946ccc6f107e30cbx529x640.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycine</strong></p><p><strong>Glycine</strong> is the <strong>smallest</strong> amino acid with R group as H atom.</p>\n<p>So, as it <strong>has no R group,</strong> it <strong>increases the flexibility of the protein</strong>. Proline and glycine are frequently found in beta turns.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7235","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a property of amino a​cids at isoelectric pH?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a property of amino acids at isoelectric pH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum pre‍cipitability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maximum buff‍ering action","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Minimum s‍olubility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No mobility in electric fi‍eld","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maximum buffering action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maximum buffering action</strong></p><p>The properties of an amino acid at isoelectric pH are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximum precipitability</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum buffering action </strong>(note: maximum buffering action is when the pH=pKa, and not at isoelectric pH)</li>\n<li>Minimum solubility</li>\n<li>No mobility in an electric field</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7217","text":"At physiological pH, buffe‍ring action in blood is c​aused by_______.","raw_text":"At physiological pH, buffering action in blood is caused by_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hist‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methi‍onine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gly‍cine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Histidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Histidine</strong></p><p>At physiological pH<strong>, buffering action in blood </strong>is caused by <strong>histidine. </strong></p>\n<p>The imidazole group of histidine has a<strong> pKa value of 6.5-7.4, </strong>which is<strong> close</strong><strong> to physiological pH.</strong> Hence, histidine has the maximum buffering capacity at physiological pH.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7238","text":"A mixture of insulin is be‍ing tested in a lab to de​termine its amino acid se​quence. Which of the foll‌owing reagents can be use​d?","raw_text":"A mixture of insulin is being tested in a lab to determine its amino acid sequence. Which of the following reagents can be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sanger’s‍ reagent","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Benedict’‍s reagent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seliwanoff‍’s reagent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‍the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sanger’s reagent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sanger’s reagent</strong></p><p><strong>Sanger’s </strong>reagent is used to determine<strong> the amino acid sequence.</strong></p>\n<p>Peptides are isolated and hydrolyzed into a mixture of smaller peptides and treated with <strong>1-fluoro-2, 4-dinitrobenzene (Sanger's reagent</strong>) which reacts with the alpha amino groups of the amino terminal residues. The amino acid content of each peptide is then determined, and the amino-terminal amino acid is identified.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7219","text":"A forensic team performed ‍a ninhydrin test on some ​samples obtained from a c​rime scene. This test can‌ detect which of the foll​owing?","raw_text":"A forensic team performed a ninhydrin test on some samples obtained from a crime scene. This test can detect which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bile ‍salts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amino‍ acids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclei‍c acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lip‍ids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amino acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amino acids</strong></p><p><strong>Ninhydrin test </strong>is used to detect <strong>amino acids</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>purple-colored complex</strong> (Ruhemann's purple) indicates a <strong>positive</strong> reaction. Proline and hydroxyproline form a yellow adduct with ninhydrin. Glutamine and asparagine produce a brown color.</p>\n<p>This test is used effectively used at crime scenes to develop and identify <strong>latent fingerprints</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7230","text":"Sakaguchi test is answered‍ by ____.","raw_text":"Sakaguchi test is answered by ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amino‍ acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bile‍ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lip‍ids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclei‍c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amino acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amino acid</strong></p><p><strong>Sakaguchi</strong> test is answered <strong>by amino acid arginine</strong>, which contains guanidium group.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7234","text":"A neonate was incidentally‍ diagnosed with a benign ​inborn error of metabolis​m. A positive Pauly’s tes‌t would indicate the accu​mulation of which of the ​following amino acids in ‌his urine?","raw_text":"A neonate was incidentally diagnosed with a benign inborn error of metabolism. A positive Pauly’s test would indicate the accumulation of which of the following amino acids in his urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyst‍eine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hist‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Histidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Histidine</strong></p><p><strong>Pauly's test </strong>is used for detecting the presence of <strong>histidine</strong> in proteins.</p>\n<p>When proteins containing histidine react with diazotized sulfanilic acid under alkaline conditions, a red color is formed by a coupling reaction. The same reagent will give an orange colored product with the phenol group of tyrosine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3651","text":"Which of the following is ‍responsible for the negat​ive charge in fibrinopept​ide A?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for the negative charge in fibrinopeptide A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamate ‍and Valine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Histidine ‍and Lysine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspartate a‍nd glutamate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serine and‍ Threonine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aspartate and glutamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aspartate and glutamate</strong></p><p><strong>Aspartate</strong> and <strong>glutamate</strong> are responsible for the <strong>negative charge</strong> in <strong>fibrinopeptide A</strong> and <strong>B</strong> of <strong>fibrinogen.</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">The negative charges contribute to the solubility of fibrinogen in plasma and importantly also serve to prevent aggregation by causing electrostatic repulsion between fibrinogen molecules.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7668","text":"The graph shown below is t‍he titration curve of a b​iochemical compound. Whic​h of the following statem‌ent is true?","raw_text":"The graph shown below is the titration curve of a biochemical compound. Which of the following statement is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The maximum buffering capa‍city of the compound is r​epresented by points A an​d B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The points A and B represe‍nt the range of maximum i​onization of the amine an​d carboxyl group","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The compound has three ion‍isable side chains","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The compound has one ionis‍able group","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The maximum buffering capacity of the compound is represented by points A and B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/15348b0f7d414d698d612dcd4f9ccf1d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aaf617abc18b4933b9690224abf1aeadx477x528.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The maximum buffering capacity of the compound is represented by points A and B</strong></p><p>The <strong>maximum buffering capacity</strong> of the compound is represented by points A and B, which corresponds to the pK<sub>1</sub> and pK<sub>2</sub> of the compound.</p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The points A and B represent the pKa of<strong> carboxyl and amino groups respectively</strong>.</li>\n<li>The amino acid has <strong>no ionizable side chains</strong> as the graph shows only two pKas.</li>\n<li>The amino acid has two ionizable groups: an amino group and a carboxyl group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The above graph resembles the titration curve of the amino acid <strong>glycine</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Titration curve of Glycine:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The titration is completed at a pH of 12, where the glycine predominantly exists in the form of H<sub>2</sub>N - CH<sub>2 </sub>- COO<sup>-</sup>.</p>\n<p><strong>pK<sub>1</sub> and pK<sub>2 </sub></strong>correspond to the point where the <strong>buffering capacity is maximum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Characteristics of the titration curve:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Buffering capacity </strong>is referred to as the ability of the aminoacid to <strong>resist the change in pH</strong> due to the addition of acid (H<sup>+</sup>) or base (OH- equivalents), by accepting or losing the protons respectively.</li>\n<li><strong>An ionizable group</strong> refers to the group which can either lose or accept a proton and becomes negatively charged or positively charged ion respectively.</li>\n<li>During the titration, the <strong>buffering capacity of the compound reaches its maximum</strong>, at points where the<strong> pKa</strong> of the ionizable groups is reached.</li>\n<li>Each of these pKa are labeled as pK1, pK2, pK3, etc.</li>\n<li>The presence of two pKas indicates that the compound has two ionizable groups. <strong>Ex. Glycine.</strong></li>\n<li>The presence of <strong>three or more pKas</strong> indicates the presence of side chains on the amino acid, with additional ionizable groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Ex. histidine</strong> has <strong>three ionizable groups</strong>, an <strong>amino group</strong>, an <strong>imidazole</strong> <strong>group</strong> (on the side chain), and a <strong>carboxyl group</strong>. The pKas of these groups respectively correspond to the pK<sub>1</sub>, pK<sub>2</sub>, pK<sub>3</sub> of the titration curve of histidine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7192","text":"Replacing alanine by which‍ amino acid, will increas​e UV absorbance of protei​n at 280nm wavelength?","raw_text":"Replacing alanine by which amino acid, will increase UV absorbance of protein at 280nm wavelength?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leu‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pro‍line","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argi‍nine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trypt‍ophan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tryptophan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tryptophan</strong></p><p>Replacing alanine with<strong> tryptophan </strong>will increase UV absorbance of protein at 280nm wavelength</p>\n<p>The amino acids which absorb 250-290nm (maximum at 280nm) UV light are Tryptophan (Maximum Absorption), Tyrosine, Phenylalanine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0797","text":"Nitric oxide is synthesize‍d from:","raw_text":"Nitric oxide is synthesized from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L - ar‍ginine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L - cit‍rulline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gly‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lys‍ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. L - arginine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3632e10e9ca4447ad3bdca0b708fc39x435x243.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. L - arginine</strong></p><p><strong>Nitric oxide</strong> is synthesized from<strong> arginine</strong> by a calcium-dependent reaction catalyzed by NO synthase. </p>\n<p>The co-enzymes of NO synthase include FMN, FAD, Heme, and Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zVvKPbmN","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"7_experimental_epidemiology","title":"7.Experimental Epidemiology","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA4855","text":"Which of the following stu‍dy designs is the best to​ prove causation?","raw_text":"Which of the following study designs is the best to prove causation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case contr‍ol studies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Randomized controlled tria‍ls","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cohort ‍studies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecologica‍l studies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Randomized controlled trials","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Randomized controlled trials</strong></p><p>Randomized controlled trials (<strong>RCT</strong>) are the strongest and <strong>best</strong> study design to <strong>prove causation</strong>. They are the <strong>gold standard</strong> of clinical research.</p>\n<p><strong>Case-control</strong> and <strong>cohort studies</strong> have a <strong>moderate</strong> ability to provide evidence for causality. <strong>Ecological studies</strong> or <strong>correlational studies </strong>are<strong> </strong>considered as the <strong>weakest</strong> study design in this aspect.</p>\n<p>The difference between analytical studies and randomised controlled trials is that in the former there is no randomisation because it requires the participants to be divided into diseased and non-diseased. This can lead to a number of biases that are avoided in RCTs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4852","text":"In a clinical trial to eva‍luate a drug, randomisati​on was done before starti‌ng the trial. What is the‌ purpose behind this?","raw_text":"In a clinical trial to evaluate a drug, randomisation was done before starting the trial. What is the purpose behind this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The two groups will be sim‍ilar in prognostic factor​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The clinician has no contr‍ol over the allocation of​ participants in each gro‌up","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The clinician can predict ‍in advance which treatmen​t the subjects will recei‌ve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The sample may be referenc‍ed to a known population","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The clinician has no control over the allocation of participants in each group","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The clinician has no control over the allocation of participants in each group</strong></p><p>The main purpose behind <strong>randomisation</strong> is to <strong>eliminate</strong> <strong>selection bias</strong>.</p>\n<p>In an <strong>RCT</strong>, participants are randomly assigned to a study group or a control group. It is done only after obtaining consent. It <strong>removes</strong> both known and unknown<strong> confounding factors</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>heart</strong> of a randomized controlled trial (RCT) is <strong>randomisation</strong>. This allows<strong> comparability </strong>between the study and the control population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4849","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about ra​ndomised control trial?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about randomised control trial?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baseline characteristics a‍re similar in both arms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Investigator’s bias is min‍imized by double blinding​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The sample size required d‍epends upon the hypothesi​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The dropouts from the tria‍l are not included in the​ analysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The dropouts from the trial are not included in the analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The dropouts from the trial are not included in the analysis</strong></p><p>The<strong> dropouts</strong> from an RCT are <strong>included </strong>in the<strong> final analysis</strong>. This type of analysis is called <strong>intention-to-treat </strong>(ITT)<strong> analysis.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>ignores </strong>noncompliance, protocol change, withdrawal, cross-over, and <strong>anything</strong> that happens <strong>after randomization</strong> during analysis. The<strong> exclusion</strong> of dropouts may <strong>lead to</strong> the introduction of <strong>bias</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Per-protocol analysis</strong> is a <strong>comparison</strong> of treatment groups that include <strong>only those patients</strong> who <strong>completed</strong> the <strong>treatment</strong> originally allocated to them. Hence, comparability between the study and control group is limited and randomisation is lost.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4848","text":"A researcher decided to us‍e double-blinding in his ​trial evaluating two new ‌therapies for cystic fibr‌osis. What is the charact​eristic feature of this c‍oncept?","raw_text":"A researcher decided to use double-blinding in his trial evaluating two new therapies for cystic fibrosis. What is the characteristic feature of this concept?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Study and control groups a‍re blinded","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Observer and analyzer are ‍blinded","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Observer and group being o‍bserved are blinded","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Group members and analyzer‍ are blinded","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Observer and group being observed are blinded","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Observer and group being observed are blinded</strong></p><p>In <strong>double-blind trials</strong>, both the <strong>group members </strong>(study and control group)<strong> </strong>and the<strong> observers </strong>are not aware of the group allocation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5167","text":"In a clinical trial, what ‍would be the best study d​esign that ensures compar‌ability between experimen‌tal and control groups?","raw_text":"In a clinical trial, what would be the best study design that ensures comparability between experimental and control groups?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cross-over ‍study design","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Concurrent parallel study ‍design","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quasi‍ trial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Per protoc‍ol analysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cross-over study design","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cross-over study design</strong></p><p>A<strong> cross-over study</strong> would be the best method to <strong>ensure comparability</strong> between the experimental and control group.</p>\n<p>In a cross over study design, patients are randomly assigned to the study and control group, and then after a period of time, their groups are switched. Here, each<strong> patient </strong>serves as<strong> his/her own control </strong>which allows for maximum comparability.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>concurrent parallel</strong> study design, comparisons are made between <strong>two randomly</strong> assigned <strong>groups</strong> and there is <strong>no switching</strong> of groups. This allows only for <strong>limited comparability</strong> between experimental and control groups.</p>\n<p><strong>Quasi-experiments</strong> are studies that aim to <strong>evaluate interventions</strong> but that do not use randomization. The lack of randomisation is the major weakness of quasi trials. The comparability in a quasi trial is limited.</p>\n<p>A<strong> per-protocol analysis</strong> is a <strong>comparison</strong> of treatment groups that include <strong>only those patients</strong> who <strong>completed</strong> the <strong>treatment</strong> originally allocated. Hence, comparability is limited.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4872","text":"A new drug for multiple sc‍lerosis is undergoing cli​nical trials where its th‌erapeutic efficacy is bei‌ng confirmed and adverse ​reactions are being monit‍ored. Which of the follow‌ing phases is the drug mo‍st likely in?","raw_text":"A new drug for multiple sclerosis is undergoing clinical trials where its therapeutic efficacy is being confirmed and adverse reactions are being monitored. Which of the following phases is the drug most likely in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‍se I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phas‍e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phas‍e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phas‍e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phase III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phase III</strong></p><p>The<strong> confirmation </strong>of therapeutic<strong> efficacy</strong> and <strong>monitoring </strong>of<strong> adverse reactions</strong> of a new drug happens in <strong>phase III</strong> of the clinical trial.</p>\n<p>Purpose of phase III: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confirmation</strong> of the <strong>effectiveness</strong> of the drug in patients with the target disease</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring</strong> of <strong>adverse reactions</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Phase II involves therapeutic<strong> exploration</strong> which is the <strong>evaluation</strong> of the <strong>efficacy</strong> of the drug and <strong>dose-ranging</strong> (determination of the optimum tolerated dose). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4871","text":"In which of the following ‍phases is the maximum dru​g failure rate seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following phases is the maximum drug failure rate seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‍se I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phas‍e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phas‍e IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pha‍se 0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phase II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phase II</strong></p><p><strong>Maximum drug failure</strong> rates are seen in <strong>phase II</strong> followed by phase III of a clinical trial.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4870","text":"The maximum tolerable dose‍ of a new drug for Alzhei​mer's disease is to be ev‌aluated. In which of the ‌following phases is this ​done?","raw_text":"The maximum tolerable dose of a new drug for Alzheimer's disease is to be evaluated. In which of the following phases is this done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‍se I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phas‍e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phas‍e III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phas‍e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phase I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phase I</strong></p><p>The <strong>maximum tolerated dose</strong> of a new drug is tested in <strong>phase I </strong>of a clinical trial.</p>\n<p>Phase I trials are usually performed on<strong> healthy volunteers </strong>to check the<strong> safety </strong>and <strong>pharmacokinetic properties </strong>of a drug. The participants in this phase do not gain any direct health benefits from taking part. They are generally <strong>paid a fee </strong>for their time, with the payments being regulated and not related to the risk involved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4861","text":"A hypothesis stating that ‍people who continue to sm​oke are at a higher risk ‌of developing lung carcin‌oma than those who have q​uit is to be tested. Whic‍h of the following study ‌designs would you choose ‍for this purpose?","raw_text":"A hypothesis stating that people who continue to smoke are at a higher risk of developing lung carcinoma than those who have quit is to be tested. Which of the following study designs would you choose for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trial of etiological agent‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clinica‍l trial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cessation ‍experiment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uncontrol‍led trial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cessation experiment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cessation experiment</strong></p><p>To test the given hypothesis that smokers are at a higher risk of developing lung carcinoma than those who have quit, the study design chosen is a <strong>cessation experiment.</strong></p>\n<p>The outcomes of <strong>termination of a habit</strong> or removal of an agent considered to be <strong>causally related</strong> to a disease can be evaluated in a cessation experiment.</p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>RCT </strong>and <strong>preventive trial</strong>. If the outcome is a <strong>significant</strong> <strong>reduction</strong> in disease then the <strong>association</strong> of that habit or agent to the disease is <strong>strengthened</strong>.</p>\n<p>A<strong> trial of aetiological agents</strong> is an experimental epidemiological study to <strong>confirm</strong> or refute an <strong>aetiological hypothesis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical trials</strong>, for the most part, have been concerned with <strong>evaluating therapeutic agents</strong>, mainly drugs.</p>\n<p>An<strong> uncontrolled trial</strong> is an example of a <strong>non-randomised trial</strong> where there is no control group for comparison.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4853","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an example of a rando​mised controlled trial?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an example of a randomised controlled trial?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trial of aetiological agen‍t","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Before and after compariso‍n studies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Preventi‍ve trials","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evaluation of health servi‍ces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Before and after comparison studies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Before and after comparison studies</strong></p><p><strong>Before and after </strong>comparison <strong>studies</strong> are a type of <strong>non-randomized trials</strong>.</p>\n<p>These are <strong>community trials</strong> and centre around <strong>comparing</strong> the <strong>incidence</strong> of disease before and after the introduction of a preventive measure. They are of <strong>two distinct types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before and after comparison studies without control.</li>\n<li>Before and after comparison studies with control.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-randomized trials</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before and after comparison studies. </li>\n<li>Uncontrolled trials. </li>\n<li>Natural experiments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4875","text":"What is the suspected caus‍e preceding the observed ​effect an example of?","raw_text":"What is the suspected cause preceding the observed effect an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consi‍stency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Speci‍ficity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plausi‍bility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tempo‍rality","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Temporality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Temporality</strong></p><p><strong>Temporality</strong> refers to the condition when the suspected <strong>cause precedes</strong> the observed <strong>effect</strong>. It is a part of <strong>Hill’s criteria</strong> for causal association.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4876","text":"While investigating a new ‍disease, you are trying t​o establish the aetiologi‌cal agent. Which of the f‌ollowing criterion is the​ most difficult to establ‍ish?","raw_text":"While investigating a new disease, you are trying to establish the aetiological agent. Which of the following criterion is the most difficult to establish?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tempo‍rality","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Speci‍ficity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cohe‍rence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plausi‍bility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Specificity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Specificity</strong></p><p><strong>Specificity</strong> is the <strong>most difficult </strong>criterion to establish in both chronic and acute diseases.</p>\n<p>This is because a <strong>single factor</strong> or cause can <strong>give rise</strong> to <strong>more than one disease</strong>. Also, <strong>most diseases</strong> are<strong> multifactorial</strong> in origin with no possibility of demonstrating a one-to-one relationship which would indicate specificity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4873","text":"What does evidence-based m‍edicine refer to?","raw_text":"What does evidence-based medicine refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Use of experiments to prov‍e the cause of a disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Use of clinical trials to ‍prove safety of drugs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Use of research findings f‍or taking decisions about​ patient care","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Use of behavioral modifica‍tion to prevent disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Use of research findings for taking decisions about patient care","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Use of research findings for taking decisions about patient care</strong></p><p><strong>Evidence-based medicine</strong> (EBM) refers to the<strong> use</strong> of modern <strong>research findings</strong> to guide <strong>decision-making </strong>about<strong> the treatment</strong> of <strong>individual patients</strong>.</p>\n<p>EBM <strong>integrates clinical experience</strong> and patient values with the <strong>best available research</strong> information. Research and studies are given high importance in EBM, while opinions and conventional wisdom of experts are not taken into consideration in the practice of EBM.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4864","text":"In the hierarchy of eviden‍ce, which of the followin​g rankings is correct? 1.‌ Cohort study 2. Case-con‌trol study 3. Meta-analys​is and systematic review ‍4. Randomized control tri‌als 5. Case reports","raw_text":"<p>In the hierarchy of evidence, which of the following rankings is correct?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 101px; width: 307px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29.28125px; text-align: center;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.71875px; text-align: left;\">Cohort study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29.28125px; text-align: center;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.71875px; text-align: left;\">Case-control study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29.28125px; text-align: center;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.71875px; text-align: left;\">Meta-analysis and systematic review</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29.28125px; text-align: center;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.71875px; text-align: left;\">Randomized control trials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29.28125px; text-align: center;\">5.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.71875px; text-align: left;\">Case reports</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 &amp;gt; 1 &amp;gt; 4 &amp;gt; 2 &amp;gt‍; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 &amp;gt; 3 &amp;gt; 1 &amp;gt; 5 &amp;gt‍; 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 &amp;gt; 4 &amp;gt; 1 &amp;gt; 2 &amp;gt‍; 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 &amp;gt; 3 &amp;gt; 2 &amp;gt; 5 &amp;gt‍; 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 > 4 > 1 > 2 > 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 &gt; 4 &gt; 1 &gt; 2 &gt; 5</strong></p><p>The correct order of ranking of the hierarchy of evidence is:</p>\n<p><strong>Meta-analysis</strong> and <strong>systemic review</strong> &gt; <strong>Randomised control tria</strong>l &gt; <strong>Cohort </strong>study &gt;  <strong>Case-control </strong>study &gt; <strong>Case reports</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4867","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about systemati​c reviews?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about systematic reviews?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meta-analysis is always pe‍rformed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Search for literature is c‍ompulsory","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Critical appraisal is crit‍eria based","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Research question is alway‍s focused","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meta-analysis is always performed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e89ff57be7cc4eeb81732696a8307b88x1280x2148.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meta-analysis is always performed</strong></p><p>Meta-analysis is not always performed in systematic reviews.</p>\n<p>A <strong>systematic review</strong> is the process of <strong>locating and synthesizing</strong> the <strong>research</strong> on a particular question using organized, transparent, and replicable procedures. It ranks <strong>high</strong> in the hierarchy of <strong>evidence</strong> in medicine.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Steps of a systematic review:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>The research <strong>question</strong> is formulated. It is always <strong>focused</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>literature </strong>on studies is always found and <strong>appraised</strong> after <strong>criteria of eligibility</strong> are <strong>established</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Meta-analysis</strong> is <strong>usually done</strong> in a systematic review for quantification but it is <strong>not compulsory</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These steps are depicted in the diagram below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4866","text":"A meta-analysis is being p‍erformed to evaluate the ​relationship between obes‌ity and endometrial carci‌noma. Which among the fol​lowing is false regarding‍ this technique?","raw_text":"A meta-analysis is being performed to evaluate the relationship between obesity and endometrial carcinoma. Which among the following is false regarding this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"File drawer effect can be ‍seen here","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It increases the statistic‍al power by increasing sa​mple size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apples and oranges effect ‍can be seen here","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its validity does not depe‍nd on the quality of the ​systematic review","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Its validity does not depend on the quality of the systematic review","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Its validity does not depend on the quality of the systematic review</strong></p><p>The <strong>validity</strong> of the meta-analysis <strong>depends</strong> on the<strong> quality </strong>of the<strong> systematic review</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4874","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a use of epidemiology​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a use of epidemiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ensuring community partici‍pation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Community‍ diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syndrome id‍entification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evaluation of individual r‍isk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ensuring community participation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ensuring community participation</strong></p><p>Ensuring <strong>community participation</strong> is not the use of epidemiology. Community participation is a <strong>principle</strong> of <strong>primary health care</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Uses of epidemiology</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To study the rise and fall of disease in populations</li>\n<li><strong>Community diagnosis</strong></li>\n<li>Planning and evaluation</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation</strong> of <strong>individual’s</strong> <strong>risks </strong>and chances</li>\n<li><strong>Syndrome</strong> <strong>identification</strong></li>\n<li>Completing the natural history of disease</li>\n<li>Searching for causes and risk factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4877","text":"For what purpose were know‍ledge, attitude, and prac​tice (KAP) studies first ‌used in India?","raw_text":"For what purpose were knowledge, attitude, and practice (KAP) studies first used in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‍IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Family ‍planning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes‍ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mal‍aria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Family planning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Family planning</strong></p><p>Knowledge, attitude and practice (<strong>KAP</strong>) <strong>studies</strong> were <strong>first used</strong> in India to study <strong>family planning</strong>.</p>\n<p>These studies help in evaluating the knowledge, attitude, and practices of a community. It also gives an <strong>educational diagnosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6685","text":"Which of the following qua‍ntitative methods is the ​best to study previous re‌search studies?","raw_text":"Which of the following quantitative methods is the best to study previous research studies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cohort‍ study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemat‍ic review","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meta-a‍nalysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"R‍CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meta-analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meta-analysis</strong></p><p>The <strong>quantitative method</strong> which is the <strong>best</strong> to <strong>study previous research</strong> studies of a selected topic/research question is <strong>meta-analysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>systematic review</strong> is a process of collecting, reviewing, and presenting information from earlier research to answer a question. It is a <strong>qualitative technique</strong>. It may or may not involve a meta-analysis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XURqk6si","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"7_dermatitis","title":"7.Dermatitis","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA3541","text":"An asthmatic child present‌s with intensely pruritic‌ lesions. He has no other​ symptoms. What is the mo‌st likely diagnosis from ​the given options?","raw_text":"An asthmatic child presents with intensely pruritic lesions. He has no other symptoms. What is the most likely diagnosis from the given options?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sca‌bies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococc‌al infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityria‌sis alba","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecz‌ema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eczema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/910a9fc5fab34e8f9c6598e83045673c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eczema</strong></p><p>The above image showing <strong>e</strong><strong>rythematous, scaly lesions</strong> having a <strong>flexural</strong> distribution<strong> </strong>with the given scenario is suggestive of <strong>eczema</strong>.</p>\n<p>Eczema<strong> </strong>is <strong>inflammation</strong> of the <strong>skin</strong> seen in a variety of skin conditions</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3539","text":"Identify the incorrect sta‌tement regarding eczema.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding eczema.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All eczema is dermatitis, ‌but not all dermatitis is‌ eczema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pruritus is a hallmark cli‌nical feature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spongiosis is characterist‌ic histopathological feat‌ure of chronic eczema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute eczematic eruption i‌s typically oedematous, v‌esicular and may be exuda​tive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spongiosis is characteristic histopathological feature of chronic eczema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a7dd4eae23a4cd2b458188876fd8e4dx1280x1262.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/985754c8845c4a34a3935750cd01cb2fx1280x1058.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spongiosis is characteristic histopathological feature of chronic eczema</strong></p><p><strong>Spongiosis </strong>is the characteristic histopathological feature of <strong>a</strong><strong>cute eczema</strong>. It refers to <strong>intercellular epidermal edema</strong> that leads to stretching and rupture of the intercellular attachments, with the formation of <strong>vesicles</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Eczema </strong>is <strong>inflammation</strong> of the skin seen in a variety of skin conditions. Dermatitis<strong> </strong>is used to include all types of cutaneous inflammation so: </p>\n<p><strong>'All eczema is dermatitis,</strong> but<strong> not all dermatitis is eczema'</strong></p>\n<p>Aetiologically, eczema may be classified as: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 88px;\" width=\"545\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Endogenous </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Exogenous </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px;\">Atopic eczema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px;\">Allergic contact eczema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px;\">Asteatotic eczema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px;\">Irritant eczema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px;\">Venous eczema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.5px;\">Post-traumatic eczema</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>hallmark</strong> symptom of eczema is intense <strong>pruritus.</strong> Other findings are: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 88px;\" width=\"510\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 247px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Acute eczema </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Chronic eczema </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">Vesicular eruption </td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">Erythema and scaling </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">Exudative </td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">Excoriation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">Edematous </td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">Lichenification </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows eczema. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3521","text":"A 27-year-old male present‌s with a well-demarcated,‌ coin-shaped erythematous​ plaque on his left shin.‌ What is the likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old male presents with a well-demarcated, coin-shaped erythematous plaque on his left shin. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atopic‌ eczema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nummula‌r eczema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pomp‌holyx","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asteatot‌ic eczema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nummular eczema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74abca58ce964b8f97d011a37b5a2b2fx720x865.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nummular eczema</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>nummular dermatitis </strong>or nummular<strong> eczema</strong>.</p>\n<p>The diagnostic lesion of nummular dermatitis is a well-demarcated <strong>coin-shaped erythematous plaque</strong> with vesicles surrounding it. Later <strong>central clearing </strong>(resolution) and <strong>peripheral extension</strong> lead to <strong>annular </strong>or<strong> ring-shaped lesions. </strong>Initially, it is red and pruritic but later, it becomes more scaly and less exudative. </p>\n<p><strong>Common locations</strong> involved are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trunk</li>\n<li>Extensor surfaces of extremities: \n<ul>\n<li>Men - Pretibial areas</li>\n<li>Women - Dorsal aspects of hand</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows nummular dermatitis.<strong> </strong>Note the typical shape and demarcation of the lesion.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3522","text":"A 5-year-old child is brou‌ght to the OPD with a hyp‌opigmented, finely scaly ​patch on her face. There ‌is a history of atopy in ​the family. What is the c‍linical diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child is brought to the OPD with a hypopigmented, finely scaly patch on her face. There is a history of atopy in the family.  What is the clinical diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pityriasis‌ versicolor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityria‌sis alba","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atopic‌ eczema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pityriasis alba","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78b0096dbf3645fb8d7f5d59336c8a3ax1280x983.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pityriasis alba</strong></p><p>The above scenario of a <strong>hypopigmented</strong>, <strong>scaly</strong> patch is suggestive of <strong>pityriasis alba.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a pattern of <strong>dermatitis</strong> in which <strong>hypopigmentation</strong> is the most conspicuous feature. It is seen in the age group of <strong>3-16 years.</strong> </p>\n<p>Lesions have scaling and are usually not well marginated.<strong> </strong>In children, the lesions are often confined to the<strong> face</strong> and are most common on the <strong>cheeks</strong> and around the <strong>mouth</strong> and<strong> chin</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is often a manifestation of <strong>atopic eczema</strong> but it is not confined to atopic individuals.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: In leprosy, the hypopigmented patch is often associated with atrophy, loss of sensation, and loss of sweating. The lesion is not scaly.</p>\n<p>Option B: In pityriasis versicolor<strong> </strong>the hypopigmented macules are perifollicular in distribution. Fine, powdery branny scaling is present. Scratch sign positive. No relation to atopy.</p>\n<p>Option D: Atopic eczema is<strong> </strong>characterized by itchy papules, hypopigmentation is not a feature.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4001","text":"A 15-year-old girl present‌s with itchy lesions on h‌er arm as shown. Her fami​ly history is positive fo‌r asthma. What could be t​he most probable diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old girl presents with itchy lesions on her arm as shown. Her family history is positive for asthma. What could be the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seborrhoeic‌ dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atopic d‌ermatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Allergic contact dermatiti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erysi‌pelas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atopic dermatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f68eb180d4cd44c1992455407106b29b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05ae708d8168418c85c36995c7340358x1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ebc687310f342eb903cfc11bb43f880x1024x768.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79408dcccbd6408682befd2506f07856x675x900.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atopic dermatitis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario depicting <strong>pruritic</strong> lesions at a <strong>flexural site</strong> along with a family history of <strong>atopy</strong> (asthma) is suggestive of <strong>atopic dermatitis </strong>(AD) or atopic eczema (AE). The <strong>scratch marks </strong>and <strong>excoriations</strong> shown in the image are due to pruritis, which is the hallmark feature of AD.</p>\n<p>AD is a <strong>chronic</strong> relapsing <strong>inflammatory </strong>condition. It presents with <strong>erythematous macules</strong>, papules and <strong>vesicles</strong> that often start in <strong>early</strong> childhood, usually less than 2 years old.<strong> </strong>The lesions later become excoriated and <strong>lichenified</strong>. It is frequently associated with <strong>dryness</strong> of the skin and<strong> s</strong><strong>econdary infections. </strong>In infancy, the lesions initially appear on <strong>face</strong> (sparing the napkin area) and later on <strong>extensor</strong> aspect of knees and elbows as the child starts crawling. In later childhood and in adults, the principal site of involvement is <strong>flexures</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>filaggrin</strong> monomers in keratinocytes in the epidermis bind and aggregate keratin bundles and intermediate filaments to form the <strong>epidermal</strong> <strong>barrier</strong>. Any <strong>damage</strong>/<strong>dysfunction</strong> of this barrier is associated with an <strong>increased</strong> <strong>risk</strong> of developing AD. Damage to this barrier can be due to <strong>environmental</strong> <strong>factors</strong> (detergents, hard water, UV light) or <strong>local</strong> <strong>inflammatory</strong> reactions. Dysfunction of the barrier can be due to <strong>genetic</strong> <strong>mutation</strong> of the <strong>FLG</strong> gene leading to reduced filaggrin production or <strong>local</strong> <strong>immunomodulatory</strong> <strong>effects</strong> leading to reduced filaggrin expression.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Seborrheic dermatitis</strong> is a chronic inflammatory skin condition characterized by erythema, pruritus, <strong>scaling</strong>, flaking, or crusting of the skin. It is often associated with <strong>Malasezzia</strong> fungal infection. A milder form of seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp is commonly known as <strong>dandruff</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Allergic contact dermatitis (ACD)</strong> is an inflammatory condition caused due to a <strong>delayed hypersensitivity reaction</strong> <strong>(Type 4</strong> <strong>hypersensitivity)</strong> to a specific allergen that comes into direct contact with the skin.Patients usually present with <strong>pruritus, erythema, swelling, papules,</strong> and <strong>papulovesicles. </strong>Some common allergens include <strong>nickel</strong> (found in <strong>artificial jewelry</strong>), <strong>PPD</strong>/Para phenylene diamine (in <strong>hair dye</strong>), <strong>chromate</strong> (in <strong>cement</strong>), rubber or <strong>latex</strong>, cosmetics, etc. <strong>Patch test </strong>(image below) is used for the diagnosis of ACD. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Erysipelas</strong> is a bacterial skin infection caused by <strong>Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Streptococcus)</strong> characterized by raised, erythematous sharply demarcated rapidly spreading skin lesions, along with <strong>systemic symptoms</strong> like fever and chills. The skin may also have a <strong>peau d'orange</strong> or orange peel-like appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3525","text":"The UK refinement of Hanif‌in and Rajka criteria is ‌used in the diagnosis of ​which of the following co‌nditions?","raw_text":"The UK refinement of Hanifin and Rajka criteria is used in the diagnosis of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atopic d‌ermatitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nummula‌r eczema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eczema h‌erpeticum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contact d‌ermatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atopic dermatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atopic dermatitis</strong></p><p>The <strong>UK refinement of Hanifin and Rajka criteria</strong> is used in the diagnosis of <strong>atopic dermatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3524","text":"An infant is brought to th‌e dermatology clinic with‌ the following lesions. W​hich is the incorrect sta‌tement regarding this con​dition?","raw_text":"An infant is brought to the dermatology clinic with the following lesions. Which is the incorrect statement regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pruritus is ‌the hallmark","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dennie Morgan fold can be ‌found under lower eyelid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has an extensor distrib‌ution in the adults","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic shiners may be pr‌esent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It has an extensor distribution in the adults","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5132da5bbb04e8cbc76117a9f7e68e9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd293f61d78b46d4813e50090df34d6dx1280x2281.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59ecc59d422b432ab7e8323e8444f948x1280x1190.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It has an extensor distribution in the adults</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of infantile <strong>atopic eczema</strong>/dermatitis. It has a <strong>flexural distribution </strong>in <strong>adult type </strong>and<strong> extensor distribution </strong>in <strong>infantile type.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rash- erythematous, itchy papules/papulovesicles </li>\n<li>Excoriations</li>\n<li>Lichenified skin</li>\n<li>Dennie Morgan fold - under lower eyelid. </li>\n<li>Allergic shiners - periorbital pigmentation </li>\n<li>Allergic salute - horizontal groove on dorsum of nasal bridge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1310","text":"Which is the first site of‌ involvement in infantile‌ atopic eczema?","raw_text":"Which is the first site of involvement in infantile atopic eczema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor as‌pect of knee","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flex‌ures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fa‌ce","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor asp‌ect of elbow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Face","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36f83230e7ad417bb6c5212a58510dd8x1280x1540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bb715e9a9724dd3bd0c55f4c7b497dax1280x2281.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Face</strong></p><p>The first site of involvement in <strong>infants</strong> with <strong>atopic eczema</strong> is the<strong> face.</strong></p>\n<p>Atopic eczema can be divided into <strong>3 phases</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Infantile</li>\n<li>Childhood</li>\n<li>Adult</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Infantile</strong> phase - Discrete or confluent <strong>oedematous papules associated</strong> with<strong> erythema</strong>. The papules are <strong>intensely itchy </strong>and may become exudative and crusted as a result of rubbing. <strong>Secondary infection</strong> and <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong> are common.</p>\n<p><strong>Childhood</strong> phase - starts at 18 to 24 months. Papules tend to be replaced by<strong> lichenification</strong>. The sides of the neck may show a striking <strong>reticulate pigmentation</strong>, sometimes referred to as <strong>atopic dirty neck</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Adult</strong> phase<strong> - </strong>Essentially similar to that in later childhood, with lichenification, especially of the <strong>flexures </strong>and<strong> hands</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1295","text":"Choose the correct stateme‌nt with respect to Hertog‌he's sign.","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement with respect to Hertoghe's sign.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of late‌ral eyebrows","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior subcapsular catar‌act","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse crease across t‌he bridge of the nose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fold under l‌ower eyelids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loss of lateral eyebrows","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82fb7e70276544658356fd9fe8847a4cx1280x1640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loss of lateral eyebrows</strong></p><p><strong>Hertoghe's sign</strong> or Queen Anne's sign is defined as<strong> loss</strong> of<strong> lateral one third</strong> of<strong> eye-brows</strong> (superciliary madarosis).</p>\n<p>It is seen in various conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Lepromatous leprosy</li>\n<li>Atopic dermatitis</li>\n<li>Trichotillomania</li>\n<li>Alopecia areata</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Option B: <strong>Anterior subcapsular cataract</strong>- shield-shaped cataract seen in atopic dermatitis.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Transverse crease across the bridge of the nose- </strong>allergic salute.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Folds of skin under lower eyelids- </strong>Dennie-Morgan folds seen in atopic dermatitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1309","text":"A 20-year-old girl who is ‌a known case of atopic de‌rmatitis presents with mu​ltiple, pruritic pustular‌ lesions for 5 days. Whic​h of the following organi‍sm is commonly responsibl‌e for her condition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old girl who is a known case of atopic dermatitis presents with multiple, pruritic pustular lesions for 5 days. Which of the following organism is commonly responsible for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermat‌ophytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64b89a27ac3d4ce59a4ce1362b2a52ec.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The above image showing <strong>pustular</strong> lesions with heavy <strong>crusting</strong> in a patient with <strong>atopic dermatitis</strong> is suggestive of staphylococcal infection. <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em> is the most common organism to cause <strong>secondary infections</strong> in the lesions of atopic dermatitis.</p>\n<p>However, human papillomavirus‐induced warts, fungal infections, and viruses (such as HSV, vaccinia, coxsackie A, and the poxvirus of molluscum contagiosum) are also frequent pathogens.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3526","text":"A 4-month-old infant was b‌rought to the OPD with th‌e following lesions on hi​s scalp. What is the like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old infant was brought to the OPD with the following lesions on his scalp. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atopic d‌ermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seborrheic‌ dermatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Berloque ‌dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nummular ‌dermatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Seborrheic dermatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72b93fe79d144e438b3314e999940ae0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Seborrheic dermatitis</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>flaky, white</strong> to <strong>yellowish greasy</strong> scales on the scalp, suggestive of <strong>infantile seborrheic dermatitis. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a common, <strong>inflammatory</strong> skin condition that causes the formation of flaky, white to yellowish greasy scales on <strong>oily areas</strong> such as the scalp, forehead, nose, or inside the ear. <strong>Cradle cap</strong> is the term used when it affects the scalp of infants. </p>\n<p>It commonly <strong>appears</strong> around <strong>3 months</strong> of age. It is rare after 8 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3527","text":"A 28-year-old lifeguard pr‌esents to the dermatology‌ clinic with a mildly pru​ritic lesion as shown for‌ 8 days. History reveals ​excessive use of perfumes‍. Which of the following ‌can be the likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old lifeguard presents to the dermatology clinic with a mildly pruritic lesion as shown for 8 days. History reveals excessive use of perfumes. Which of the following can be the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinea c‌orporis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermatitis h‌erpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Berloque ‌dermatitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Seborrheic‌ dermatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Berloque dermatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0193cb2862ff4d5aa46166fea0ecac8f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Berloque dermatitis</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>deep‐brown pigmented</strong> lesion in the right <strong>underarm</strong> likely to have formed from the trickle of the droplets of <strong>perfume</strong> applied on the skin. This is suggestive of <strong>Berloque dermatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>skin</strong> <strong>pigmentation</strong> occurs due to an <strong>acute phototoxic reaction</strong> to perfumes containing <strong>bergamot oil</strong> (bergapten) which potentiates UV‐stimulated <strong>melanogenesis.</strong> A<strong> hot</strong> and<strong> humid</strong> climate along with exposure to <strong>UV light </strong>increases the risk of developing this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3538","text":"A patient presents to the ‌dermatology clinic compla‌ining that a birthmark th​at was asymptomatic has n‌ow become pruritic with t​he following appearance. ‍What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the dermatology clinic complaining that a birthmark that was asymptomatic has now become pruritic with the following appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic superficial scaly ‌dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nummular ‌dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mela‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo de‌rmatitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Halo dermatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7081e820af1b4651a9a370c285223363.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Halo dermatitis</strong></p><p>The above image shows an <strong>eczematous ring</strong> surrounding a <strong>melanocytic naevus</strong> (birthmark), suggestive of <strong>halo dermatitis</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>Meyerson nevus</strong>. Histology shows a benign naevus surrounded by a dermal lymphocytic and eosinophilic infiltrate, with acanthosis, spongiosis, and parakeratosis.</p>\n<p>The condition usually <strong>resolves spontaneously</strong> within few months <strong>without</strong><strong> involution</strong> of the nevus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3540","text":"A patient presents with th‌e following intensely pru‌ritic lesions that starte​d 2 years back. History r‌eveals that the lesions a​re more severe in winters‍ and relatively milder in‌ summers. Choose the inco​rrect statement regarding​ this condition.","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following intensely pruritic lesions that started 2 years back. History reveals that the lesions are more severe in winters and relatively milder in summers. Choose the incorrect statement regarding this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cimetidine can cause this ‌condition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is also known as eczema‌ craquelé","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Children are most commonly‌ affected","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It may occur due to zinc d‌eficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Children are most commonly affected","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd8a8ed52aef47b08cfb086f7e15a39e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Children are most commonly affected</strong></p><p>The above image showing dry<strong>, scaly eczematous lesions </strong>with a characteristic<strong> crazy-paving pattern </strong>(<strong>eczéma craquelé</strong>) is suggestive of <strong>asteatotic</strong> <strong>eczema. </strong>It usually develops in the <strong>elderly, </strong>not in children.</p>\n<p>It affects the<strong> legs, arms, </strong>and<strong> hands</strong> of elderly people in the context of <strong>very dry skin</strong>. The condition is usually chronic, relapsing each <strong>winter</strong> and clearing in the summer, but eventually becoming permanent. </p>\n<p> It may be a presenting sign of <strong>myxoedema</strong>. It can also be due to <strong>zinc deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> implicated include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diuretics</li>\n<li>Cimetidine</li>\n<li>Topical corticosteroids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3523","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of the condition show‌n below?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of the condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ecz‌ema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dr‌ugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous‌ lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Eczema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5be785a63a9f45d1870a3db067ede8b7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Eczema</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>erythroderma</strong> which is an <strong>inflammatory skin disease</strong> affecting <strong>&gt;90%</strong> of<strong> body surface.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>most common causes</strong> of erythroderma in <strong>decreasing order</strong> of frequency are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eczema (40%)</li>\n<li>Psoriasis </li>\n<li>Lymphoma and leukemias </li>\n<li>Drugs - phenylbutazone, phenytoin, carbamazepine, gold salts, lithium, cimetidine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: If the option was between atopic dermatitis and psoriasis always choose <strong>psoriasis</strong> because the latter is the single most common cause of erythroderma whereas atopic dermatitis is one of the subtypes of eczema.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1260","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a complication of ery‌throderma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a complication of erythroderma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High output cardiac failur‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory‌ infections","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌hermia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperthermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperthermia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypothermia</strong> is seen as a complication of erythroderma due to <strong>increased skin perfusion</strong>. There is no hyperthermia.</p>\n<p>The main complications of erythroderma are <strong>hemodynamic</strong> and <strong>metabolic</strong> disturbances.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Increased</strong> skin <strong>perfusion</strong> results in high‐output <strong>cardiac failure.</strong> Fluid loss by transpiration may lead to hypovolemia. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hypoalbuminaemia</strong> is due to increased protein loss from <strong>exfoliated scales.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C: Cutaneous, subcutaneous, and respiratory infections are common and <strong>pneumonia</strong> remains the <strong>commonest cause</strong> of <strong>death.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1262","text":"The following are true abo‌ut the condition given in‌ the image except:","raw_text":"The following are true about the condition given in the image except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common in people with‌ history of atopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sago grain vesicles seen o‌n palms and sides of hand‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are painful but not p‌ruritic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Topical corticosteroids ca‌n be used for this condit‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They are painful but not pruritic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f1838108ee0408fa0f9b0e3b59eaa6b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d8ec6b043bb4f40ae2329cdf90ce526x942x707.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They are painful but not pruritic</strong></p><p>The given image is that of<strong> pompholyx</strong>, a distinct type of <strong>h</strong><strong>and eczema</strong>. It shows <strong>confluent vesicles</strong> that are <strong>painful</strong> and<strong> pruritic</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also called <strong>vesicular</strong> eczema of palms and soles. This gives a<strong> sago grain</strong> or <strong>tapioca vesicle</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p><strong>Predisposing factors: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atopy</li>\n<li>Naturally dry skin</li>\n<li>Allergic or irritant dermatitis - chromate, epoxy glues, and rubber</li>\n<li>Filaggrin gene mutations are predisposing factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First-line - </strong>Hand care advice, irritant, and allergen avoidance</li>\n<li><strong>Second-line - </strong>topical corticosteroids</li>\n<li><strong>Third line:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Alitretinoin</li>\n<li>PUVA</li>\n<li>Azathioprine</li>\n<li>Ciclosporin</li>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows pompholyx. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1300","text":"Which is the most common m‌etal to cause allergic co‌ntact dermatitis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common metal to cause allergic contact dermatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chro‌mium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nic‌kel","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sil‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cop‌per","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nickel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nickel</strong></p><p><strong>Nickel</strong> is the<strong> most frequent </strong>contact<strong> allergen</strong> for <strong>contact dermatitis.</strong> Most of its salts (nickel chloride, nickel sulfate) are readily <strong>soluble</strong> in water and<strong> sweat</strong> and have strong sensitizing properties. </p>\n<p>It is more<strong> common </strong>in<strong> women</strong> than in men. <strong>Jewelry</strong> and <strong>metal</strong> components of clothing are the usual sources of nickel in prolonged contact with the skin. </p>\n<p>Allergic contact dermatitis is an inflammatory reaction to a substance (allergen) that causes an eruption only in those individuals <strong>previously</strong> <strong>sensitized</strong> by the same molecule. It is a <strong>delayed-type</strong> of <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> (type IV).</p>\n<p>Substances causing allergic contact dermatitis: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chromium</li>\n<li>Palladium</li>\n<li>Gold</li>\n<li>Vegetable matter</li>\n<li>PPD in hair dye</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diagnosis is made by a <strong>patch test</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1304","text":"A 30-year-old domestic hel‌p presents to the dermato‌logy clinic with intensel​y pruritic lesions for 3 ‌days. History reveals tha​t the lesions started to ‍develop after she recentl‌y changed the cleaning de​tergent. Choose the incor​rect statement with respe‌ct to the likely diagnosi‍s.","raw_text":"A 30-year-old domestic help presents to the dermatology clinic with intensely pruritic lesions for 3 days. History reveals that the lesions started to develop after she recently changed the cleaning detergent. Choose the incorrect statement with respect to the likely diagnosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensitisation is not requi‌red","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can occur in anyone wit‌hout predisposing factors‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a Type-IV delayed hy‌persensitivity reaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Due to direct toxic effect‌s of a strong chemical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a Type-IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21d5aa6ae6f345729a5b5ba2109011b2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a Type-IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>irritant contact dermatitis</strong> which is the cutaneous response to the <strong>physical/toxic effects</strong> of a wide range of environmental exposures. It is<strong> not an immunologically</strong> mediated reaction.</p>\n<p>Irritant contact dermatitis is also called <strong>wear</strong> and<strong> tear</strong> <strong>dermatitis.</strong> Examples include <strong>h</strong><strong>ousewives eczema</strong> due to soaps and detergents and wetwork, napkin or diaper rash.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment includes avoidance of irritants, personal protection, and <strong>'barrier creams'</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 185px; width: 518px; float: left;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246.031px; height: 2px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Irritant contact dermatitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.969px; height: 2px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Allergic contact dermatitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 70px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246.031px; height: 70px;\">Not an immunologically mediated reaction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.969px; height: 70px;\">Immunologically mediated reaction (type-IV delayed hypersensitivity)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246.031px; height: 67px;\">It can occur in anyone without predisposing factors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.969px; height: 67px;\">It only occurs in those individuals with a history of atopy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 60px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246.031px; height: 60px;\">Sensitization is not required </td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.969px; height: 60px;\">Sensitization phase required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1306","text":"A 45-year-old male present‌s with itchy papules over‌ the face, neck, and V ar​ea of the chest for the l‌ast 3 years which are exa​cerbated in summers and i‍mproved in winters. What ‌test can be done to confi​rm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male presents with itchy papules over the face, neck, and V area of the chest for the last 3 years which are exacerbated in summers and improved in winters. What test can be done to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgE l‌evels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin ‌biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prick‌ Test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Photopa‌tch Test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Photopatch Test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef04b197a4c24a58abdf6d4e585cc730x462x249.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Photopatch Test</strong></p><p>The given history points towards the diagnosis of <strong>p</strong><strong>hotoallergic contact dermatitis</strong>. <strong>Photopatch test</strong> is used to diagnose this condition.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>delayed-type IV hypersensitivity</strong> reaction with a predilection for photo exposed sites with <strong>well‐demarcated margins</strong> where the skin is covered by clothing (at the collar and ‘V’ of the neck, on the backs of the hands, and on the ankles). It spares the skin behind the earlobe (<strong>Wilkinson’s triangle</strong>) since it is a photo-protected site.</p>\n<p><strong>Photopatch test:</strong> The standard patch test units are applied in two sets (on either side of the spine) and kept covered. One set of patches is removed and exposed to UVA. After irradiation, the other set of patches are also removed and both the sites are covered again for 48 hours. A <strong>positive reaction</strong> on the <strong>irradiated side</strong> only is an indication of photoallergy</p>\n<p>The image below shows a photopatch test showing reaction after exposure to UVA only.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6695","text":"What is the gold standard ‌diagnostic test for airbo‌rne contact dermatitis?","raw_text":"What is the gold standard diagnostic test for airborne contact dermatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radioallergo‌sorbent assay","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patch‌ test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serum I‌gE assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum eosin‌ophil count","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patch test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fb7c483b6704a37b47a851cc3b5af74x766x473.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patch test</strong></p><p>The <strong>gold standard</strong> test for <strong>airborne contact dermatitis</strong> is a <strong>patch test.</strong></p>\n<p>Patch testing is done to document sensitivity to a specific antigen. Patch test antigens comprising suspected allergens are applied to the patient’s back and covered for 48 hours. After the patch is removed, the area is examined for<strong> delayed type</strong> of<strong> hypersensitivity.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a patch test. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1261","text":"All of the following are c‌auses of erythroderma exc‌ept _______","raw_text":"All of the following are causes of erythroderma except  _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityrias‌is Rosea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seborrheic‌ Dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous‌ Lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pityriasis Rosea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pityriasis Rosea</strong></p><p><strong>Pityriasis rosea</strong> is not a cause of erythroderma.</p>\n<p>The most common causes of erythroderma in <strong>decreasing order</strong> of frequency are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eczema of various subtypes including <strong>seborrheic dermatitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Psoriasis </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lymphoma</strong> and leukemia</li>\n<li>Drugs - phenylbutazone, phenytoin, carbamazepine, gold salts, lithium, cimetidine</li>\n<li>Hereditary disorders - ichthyosiform erythroderma, pityriasis rubra pilaris</li>\n<li>Pemphigus foliaceus</li>\n<li>Other skin diseases - lichen planus, dermatophytosis, crusted scabies, dermatomyositis<br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0960","text":"What is the‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atopic d‌ermatosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoma ‌sebaceum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ros‌acea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ichth‌yosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atopic dermatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/394c9ad685af4d53a81d862fbeae869b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atopic dermatosis</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>atopic dermatosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Atopic dermatosis is a <strong>relapsing inflammatory skin disorder</strong> that is common in infancy and presents differently in different age groups. It is characterized by <strong>pruritus</strong> leading to<strong> lichenification. </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li><strong>Infants - </strong>The lesions most frequently start on the face, and then, the <strong>extensor aspect</strong> of the knees and elbows are involved.</li>\n<li><strong>Children:</strong> Dry, scaly, pruritic, excoriated papules and plaques in the flexural areas and neck.</li>\n<li><strong>Adults:</strong> Lichenification and dry, fissured skin in a <strong>flexural distribution. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NUJReC15","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"7_disorders_of_middle_ear","title":"7.Disorders Of Middle Ear","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA4531","text":"What is the most common ro​ute of infection in acute‍ suppurative otitis media​?","raw_text":"What is the most common route of infection in acute suppurative otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemato​genous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eustach​ian tube","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extern​al ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous ​lymphatics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Eustachian tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Eustachian tube</strong></p><p>The <strong>eustachian tube</strong> is the most common route of infection in acute suppurative otitis media (ASOM).</p>\n<p>The infection travels via the lumen of the tube or along subepithelial peritubal lymphatics. It can also spread from the external ear through a traumatic perforation or via the blood but it is rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4532","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not predispo‍se to eustachian tube obs​truction?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not predispose to eustachian tube obstruction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choanal​ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoid h​ypertrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allergic​ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cleft ​palate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Choanal atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Choanal atresia</strong></p><p>Choanal atresia does not predispose to eustachian tube obstruction.</p>\n<p>Eustachian tube obstruction is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extrinsic causes:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adenoid hypertrophy</strong></li>\n<li>Nasopharyngeal tumors</li>\n<li>Nasal pack</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Intrinsic causes:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Allergic rhinitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rhinosinusitis</strong></li>\n<li>Upper respiratory tract infection</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Defective functioning of the tube:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cleft palate</strong></li>\n<li>Down syndrome</li>\n<li>Submucous cleft palate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Eustachian tube obstruction can lead to complications such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute suppurative otitis media</li>\n<li>Otitis media with effusion</li>\n<li>Chronic suppurative otitis media</li>\n<li>Atelectasis and ossicular necrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4536","text":"A worried mother brings he​r febrile infant to the E‍R. He is crying incessant​ly and clutching his ear.‌ He had the following fin​ding on otoscopy. What is​ the most likely organism​ implicated in this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A worried mother brings her febrile infant to the ER. He is crying incessantly and clutching his ear. He had the following finding on otoscopy. What is the most likely organism implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta haemolytic streptococ​cus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Haemophilu​s influenza","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyl​ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b93cd36584ba43418d977babc26ac557.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococcus pneumoniae</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>congested</strong> <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> with <strong>purulent effusion</strong> and absent cone of light is suggestive of <strong>acute suppurative otitis media</strong>. The most likely organism implicated is <strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Organisms causing ASOM in infants and young children:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> </strong>(most common)</li>\n<li><strong><em> Haemophilus influenzae</em> </strong></li>\n<li><strong><em> Moraxella catarrhalis</em> </strong></li>\n<li><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></li>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4546","text":"Otologic examination of a ​febrile child with otalgi‍a is shown below. What is​ the stage of acute suppu‌rative otitis media seen ​here?","raw_text":"Otologic examination of a febrile child with otalgia is shown below. What is the stage of acute suppurative otitis media seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-sup​puration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Compli​cation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resol​ution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suppu​ration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pre-suppuration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cee8436512b7479da195d33c5ce759ce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pre-suppuration</strong></p><p>The clinical picture and image show the stage of <strong>pre</strong>-<strong>suppuration </strong>of acute suppurative otitis media (ASOM).<strong> </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 78\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>During this stage, due to prolonged tubal <strong>occlusion,</strong> pyogenic organisms invade the tympanic cavity. An inflammatory exudate appears in the middle ear and there is <strong>congestion </strong>of<strong> pars tensa</strong>.</p>\n<p>A leash of blood vessels appears along with the handle of malleus and at the periphery of the tympanic membrane imparting it a <strong>cart-wheel appearance</strong>. Later, the whole of the tympanic membrane including pars flaccida becomes uniformly red.</p>\n<p>Symptoms of this stage include marked earache, a high degree of fever, hearing loss, and tinnitus (seen only in adults). Tuning fork tests show the conductive type of hearing loss.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4537","text":"Pain is most severe in whi​ch stage of acute suppura‍tive otitis media?","raw_text":"Pain is most severe in which stage of acute suppurative otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage of tub​al occlusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage of s​uppuration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stage of pre​-suppuration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage of ​resolution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage of suppuration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage of suppuration</strong></p><p>Pain is most severe in <strong>stage of suppuration </strong>in acute suppurative otitis media.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4544","text":"What is the most common si​te of perforation of the ‍tympanic membrane in acut​e suppurative otitis medi‌a?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of perforation of the tympanic membrane in acute suppurative otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior half of pars tens​a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior half of pars flac​cida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior half of pars ten​sa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Superior half of pars flac​cida","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior half of pars tensa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior half of pars tensa</strong></p><p>The most common site of perforation of the tympanic membrane in ASOM is the <strong>posterior half</strong> of <strong>pars tensa</strong> where usually, a small perforation is seen.</p>\n<p>Note: As per Dhingra Diseases of Ear Nose and Throat 7th edition, the perforation most commonly occurs in the anteroinferior quadrant. However, as Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery; 7th Edition mentions the most common site as the posterior half of pars tensa, we have picked it as the best option in this question.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4545","text":"How will you manage a pati​ent who is diagnosed to h‍ave acute suppurative oti​tis media in the pre-supp‌uration stage?","raw_text":"How will you manage a patient who is diagnosed to have acute suppurative otitis media in the pre-suppuration stage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibiotics and decongesta​nts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myringotomy with antibioti​cs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myringotomy with grommet i​nsertion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antihistaminics and decong​estants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antibiotics and decongestants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antibiotics and decongestants</strong></p><p>Management of <strong>ASOM</strong> in the <strong>pre-suppuration stage</strong> involves the usage of<strong> antibiotics</strong> and<strong> decongestants. </strong></p>\n<p>Myringotomy is indicated only when there is an impending rupture of tympanic membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4556","text":"A 23-year-old woman presen​ted to the OPD with compl‍aints of excruciating pai​n in the right ear, deafn‌ess and decreased sleep. ​On examination, she had r​ight-sided LMN facial ner​ve paralysis and bulging ​tympanic membrane on otos​copy. Which of the follow‌ing best describes the pr​ocedure to be done?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman presented to the OPD with complaints of excruciating pain in the right ear, deafness and decreased sleep. On examination, she had right-sided LMN facial nerve paralysis and bulging tympanic membrane on otoscopy. Which of the following best describes the procedure to be done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgical opening in tympan​ic membrane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Removal of disease and rec​onstruction of hearing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical opening in eustac​hian tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial nerve decompression​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surgical opening in tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surgical opening in tympanic membrane</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of acute suppurative otitis media <strong>(ASOM)</strong> complicated by right-sided LMN <strong>facial nerve palsy.</strong> Otoscopic finding of <strong>bulging tympanic membrane</strong> is an <strong>indication</strong> to perform <strong>myringotomy</strong> i.e., <strong>surgical opening </strong>in the <strong>tympanic membrane.</strong> </p>\n<p>Treatment of facial nerve paralysis secondary to otitis media</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conservative management is preferred, using antibiotics and corticosteroids.</li>\n<li>Myringotomy and a ventilation tube should be added when spontaneous perforation of the tympanic membrane has not occurred.</li>\n<li>Facial nerve decompression is not usually necessary.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tympanoplasty is the removal of disease from the middle ear and reconstruction of hearing with or without myringoplasty.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4588","text":"A 4-year-old boy is brough​t to the OPD with fever a‍nd earache. On examinatio​n, the tympanic membrane ‌appears congested and bul​ging with a loss of landm​arks. What is the next li​ne of management?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the OPD with fever and earache. On examination, the tympanic membrane appears congested and bulging with a loss of landmarks. What is the next line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myringotomy and grommet in​sertion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibiotics and myringotom​y","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antibiotics and decongesta​nts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympan​oplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antibiotics and myringotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antibiotics and myringotomy</strong></p><p>The scenario points towards a diagnosis of acute suppurative otitis media (ASOM). <strong>Antibiotics</strong> form the mainstay of treatment in acute suppurative otitis media, and if the <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> is <strong>bulging, myringotomy</strong> should be done. </p>\n<p>Myringotomy is the procedure involving incising the tympanic membrane to evacuate pus. It is indicated when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The tympanic membrane is bulging and there is acute pain.</li>\n<li>There is an incomplete resolution despite antibiotics and the drum remains full with persistent conductive deafness.</li>\n<li>There is persistent effusion beyond 12 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4559","text":"You are an ENT resident as​sisting on a myringotomy ‍in a patient with serous ​otitis media. In which qu‌adrant would you make the​ incision?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident assisting on a myringotomy in a patient with serous otitis media. In which quadrant would you make the incision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postero​inferior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anteros​uperior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anteroi​nferior","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postero​superior","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anteroinferior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ce4314a09cc45728bd77261533019a6x1280x1521.015514809591.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anteroinferior</strong></p><p>In <strong>serous otitis media</strong>, a<strong> radial incision</strong> is made in the<strong> anteroinferior </strong>quadrant of the tympanic membrane. </p>\n<p>A curvilinear incision is used in acute suppurative otitis media in the posteroinferior quadrant (smiling incision).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Although an anteroinferior incision is the first step, we resort to an anterosuperior incision in the case of thick secretions that are difficult to aspirate. The 'beer-can' principle supports the said mechanism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4549","text":"In which of the following ​conditions would you expe‍ct to observe the light-h​ouse effect?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions would you expect to observe the light-house effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otosclero​tic lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute ma​stoiditis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intra-tympanic glomus tumo​r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic suppurative otitis​ media","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute mastoiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute mastoiditis</strong></p><p>The <strong>light-house</strong> effect is seen in <strong>acute</strong> <strong>mastoiditis, </strong>which is a complication of otitis media. This effect is described as a pulsatile mucopurulent or purulent discharge, coming through a central pin-hole perforation of pars tensa. This suggests a <strong>reservoir of infection</strong> in the mastoid and middle ear.</p>\n<p>Note: Otoscopic signs in intratympanic glomus tumors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Red reflex</strong> through an intact tympanic membrane</li>\n<li><strong>Pulsation sign</strong> or Brown sign - when ear canal pressure is raised with Siegel’s speculum, the tumor pulsates vigorously and then blanches</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4590","text":"A 6-year-old child with me​asles is brought to the O‍PD with complaints of pro​fuse otorrhea. Otoscopy i‌s suggestive of acute nec​rotizing otitis media. Wh​at is the most likely cau​sative organism?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child with measles is brought to the OPD with complaints of profuse otorrhea. Otoscopy is suggestive of acute necrotizing otitis media. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β- hemolytic streptococcus​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haemophilus​ influenzae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β- hemolytic streptococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β- hemolytic streptococcus</strong></p><p><strong>β-hemolytic streptococcus</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> organism causing acute necrotizing otitis media.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Acute necrotizing otitis media</strong> is a variety of acute suppurative otitis media. This is often seen in children suffering from <strong>measles,</strong> <strong>scarlet fever</strong>, or <strong>influenza.</strong> </p>\n<p>It leads to the destruction of the entire tympanic membrane, middle ear mucosa, and air cells. There is profuse <strong>otorrhoea.</strong> In such cases, healing is followed by fibrosis or ingrowth of squamous epithelium from the meatus leading to a<strong> secondary acquired cholesteatoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment is early antibiotic therapy for at least 7- 10 days.  Cortical mastoidectomy may be indicated if medical treatment fails to control or the condition gets complicated by acute mastoiditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4528","text":"What is glue ear also call​ed as?","raw_text":"What is glue ear also called as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute oti​tis media","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secretory o​titis media","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic suppurative otitis​ media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent o​titis media","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secretory otitis media","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secretory otitis media</strong></p><p><strong>Glue ear </strong>is also called <strong>secretory otitis media. </strong>It refers to otitis media with effusion. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4592","text":"What is the most common ca​use of hearing loss in ch‍ildren?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of hearing loss in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute Oti​tis Media","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Otitis media with effusion​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic Suppurative Otitis​ Media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prema​turity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Otitis media with effusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Otitis media with effusion</strong></p><p><strong>Otitis media with effusion, </strong>or glue ear,<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>hearing loss</strong> in <strong>children.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of glue ear include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral, painless, and fluctuating hearing loss. Conductive hearing loss not more than 40 dB is observed.</li>\n<li>Defective and delayed development of speech.</li>\n<li>Occasional mild earaches are present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4599","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‍g serous otitis media?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding serous otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Function of the eustachian​ tube is abnormal in most‍ cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoid hypertrophy is the​ most common cause in chi‍ldren causing bilateral s​erous otitis media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tumors of nasopharynx is t​he most common cause in a‍dults causing unilateral ​serous otitis media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The effusion is thick, vis​cid and unsterile in most‍ cases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The effusion is thick, viscid and unsterile in most cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The effusion is thick, viscid and unsterile in most cases</strong></p><p>The effusion is thick, viscid and <strong>sterile</strong> in most cases of <strong>serous otitis media.</strong></p>\n<p>Etiology of serous otitis media:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malfunctioning of eustachian tube:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adenoid hyperplasia</strong> in children - causes bilateral serous otitis media</li>\n<li>Benign and malignant <strong>tumors</strong> of the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> - should always be excluded in unilateral serous otitis media in an adult</li>\n<li>Cleft lip, cleft palate and chronic tonsillitis - interference with ET opening </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Otitic barotrauma</li>\n<li>Allergy - increased middle ear secretions </li>\n<li>Unresolved ASOM - due to inadequate treatment dosage or duration</li>\n<li>Viral infections - increased secretory activity due to stimulation of middle ear mucosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4604","text":"You see a child with delay​ed development of speech ‍as part of a health visit​ in a local school. The o‌toscopic appearance of th​e ear is shown below. Hea​ring loss of 20 dB is det​ected in audiometric test​s. What is your diagnosis​?","raw_text":"You see a child with delayed development of speech as part of a health visit in a local school. The otoscopic appearance of the ear is shown below. Hearing loss of 20 dB is detected in audiometric tests. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute suppurative otitis m​edia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serous ot​itis media","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic suppurative otitis​ media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glomus​ tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serous otitis media","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a425866658ae4a3a957b280f4500a00a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serous otitis media</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image suggest a diagnosis of<strong> serous otitis media.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>golden yellow</strong> discoloration of the tympanic membrane is caused by the <strong>clear</strong> watery <strong>straw-colored effusion</strong> filling the middle ear. Since the fluid is clear, one can see the structures of the middle ear quite clearly on otoscopy.</p>\n<p>Patients having serous otitis media will have <strong>hearing loss</strong> that rarely exceeds 40 dB. This deafness can pass unnoticed, and maybe accidentally discovered during audiometric screening tests. Because of hearing loss, the <strong>development</strong> of <strong>speech</strong> is <strong>delayed</strong> or defective.</p>\n<p>Otoscopic findings in serous otitis media:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluid level</strong> and <strong>air bubbles</strong> are seen when fluid is thin and the tympanic membrane transparent.</li>\n<li>In chronic cases, the fluid becomes very sticky. Hence, the name <strong>glue ear.</strong> </li>\n<li>The tympanic membrane is often dull and opaque with a loss of light reflex.</li>\n<li>The mobility of the tympanic membrane is restricted.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4607","text":"What type of tympanogram w​ould you expect to see in‍ a patient with serous ot​itis media?","raw_text":"What type of tympanogram would you expect to see in a patient with serous otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A t​ype","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ad ​type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"As ​type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B t​ype","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B type</strong></p><p><strong>B-type</strong> tympanogram is expected in a case of <strong>serous otitis media.</strong></p>\n<p>Here, <strong>compliance decreases </strong>i.e., mobility of tympanic membrane is restricted due to the effusion, usually secondary to negative middle ear pressure (eustachian tube obstruction). This results in a B-type tympanogram. </p>\n<p>Hearing tests suggestive of conductive hearing loss as seen in serous otitis media:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rinne negative (BC&gt;AC).</li>\n<li>Weber - lateralized towards the worse ear</li>\n<li>Audiogram - AC abnormal, BC normal, air-bone gap present</li>\n<li>Impedance audiometry - flat curve (reduced compliance, shift to the negative side)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Tympanograph is <strong>F</strong>lat when there is <strong>F</strong>luid in the middle ear.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4610","text":"Which of the following pro​cedures is not used to tr‍eat a patient with glue e​ar?","raw_text":"Which of the following procedures is not used to treat a patient with glue ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myringotomy and aspiration​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grommet ​insertion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympa​notomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radical ma​stoidectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Radical mastoidectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fff1492da544b6aa3c60bb048572667x720x595.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Radical mastoidectomy</strong></p><p>Serous otitis media can be managed by <strong>cortical mastoidectomy.</strong> It is sometimes required for removing loculated thick fluid or other associated pathologies such as cholesterol granuloma. </p>\n<p>Management of glue ear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medical:\n<ul>\n<li>Antihistaminics</li>\n<li>Decongestants</li>\n<li>Antibiotics </li>\n<li>Valsalva maneuver.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical:\n<ul>\n<li>Myringotomy and aspiration</li>\n<li>Grommet insertion</li>\n<li>Tympanotomy</li>\n<li>Cortical mastoidectomy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A grommet is<strong> </strong>placed in the middle ear to aerate and drain the middle ear. It acts as a substitute for the blocked eustachian tube. It is placed through radial myringotomy in the anteroinferior quadrant. The device is shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4613","text":"A child with hearing diffi​culty is diagnosed to hav‍e glue ear. Which of the ​following is not a possib‌le sequela of this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A child with hearing difficulty is diagnosed to have glue ear. Which of the following is not a possible sequela of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle ear ​atelectasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Otoscl​erosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Choles​teatoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympanos​clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Otosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Otosclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Otosclerosis </strong>is not a possible sequela of glue ear. Otosclerosis is a<strong> hereditary disease </strong>of the bone of the<strong> inner ear</strong>, which mostly affects the <strong>stapes footplate</strong> area<strong>. </strong>It is not related to other ear pathologies.</p>\n<p>The sequelae of long-standing glue ear include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrophy</strong> of tympanic membrane (TM) and myringosclerosis<strong> </strong>(fibrosis of TM)</li>\n<li><strong>Atelectasis</strong> <strong>of middle ear</strong> - when the tympanic membrane gets retracted over the promontory</li>\n<li>Ossicular <strong>necrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tympanosclerosis</strong> - collagen deposits within the middle ear</li>\n<li>Retraction pockets in TM and <strong>cholesteatoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cholesterol granulomas</strong> - due to stasis of secretions in the mastoid and middle ear</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6085","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of tubercul‍ar otitis media?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of tubercular otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ear ​ache","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple p​erforations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pale gra​nulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foul smelling ear discharg​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ear ache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3c2362e63204a9dbff78cc67b39cb3cx1280x1258.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ear ache</strong></p><p><strong>Earache</strong> is not a feature of tubercular otitis media. It is a<strong> painless</strong> condition.</p>\n<p>Tuberculosis of the ear is usually<strong> secondary to pulmonary tuberculosis. </strong>It occasionally spreads <strong>hematogenously</strong>, from a tubercular focus in the lungs, tonsils, and cervical or mesenteric lymph nodes</p>\n<p>It presents as a <strong>painless condition</strong> with <strong>foul-smelling</strong> discharge from the ear, that does not respond to standard antibiotic treatment. Patients have <strong>severe hearing loss</strong> that is out of proportion to symptoms. Although predominantly <strong>conductive</strong>, <strong>sensorineural</strong> hearing loss may also occur if the <strong>labyrinth</strong> is involved.</p>\n<p>A thickened tympanic membrane with <strong>multiple perforations</strong> in pars tensa of the tympanic membrane and <strong>pale granulations </strong>in the middle ear cleft is seen. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the tympanic membrane in tuberculous otitis media: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Histopathology shows<strong> submucosal tubercles</strong> with <strong>Langhans</strong> giant cells and caseous necrosis. Smears and culture of ear discharge may be positive for acid-fast bacilli (AFB). Evidence of primary pulmonary tuberculosis may also be seen on chest radiography. </p>\n<p><strong>Facial nerve palsy</strong> is a common complication. Mastoiditis, postauricular fistula, and osteomyelitis are also seen.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>aural toilet</strong>, treatment of secondary bacterial infections, and a full course of <strong>antitubercular therapy</strong> followed by <strong>reconstructive surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9905","text":"A 42-year-old farmer prese​nted to your clinic after‍ developing painless otor​rhoea and hearing loss in‌ his right ear. Examinati​on revealed multiple perf​orations in the pars tens​a in addition to a purule​nt, foul-smelling dischar​ge with right facial nerv‌e palsy. Audiometry indic​ated a 35 dB conductive h‌earing loss. Which of the​ following does not const​itute an essential part o‍f the treatment?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old farmer presented to your clinic after developing painless otorrhoea and hearing loss in his right ear. Examination revealed multiple perforations in the pars tensa in addition to a purulent, foul-smelling discharge with right facial nerve palsy. Audiometry indicated a 35 dB conductive hearing loss. Which of the following does not constitute an essential part of the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cortico​steroids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antitubercu​lar therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aural ​toilet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoid​ surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corticosteroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d8c418607664e13a4223016764f5c88x1280x1258.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corticosteroids</strong></p><p>The clinical stem consisting of painless otorrhoea, conductive hearing loss with multiple perforations in the pars tensa, and right facial nerve palsy point to a diagnosis of <strong>tuberculous otitis media</strong>, for which corticosteroids do not form an essential part of the treatment.</p>\n<p>Tuberculosis of the ear usually occurs secondary to pulmonary tuberculosis. It is occasionally blood-borne, spreading from a tubercular focus in the lungs, tonsils, cervical or mesenteric lymph nodes. Tuberculous otitis media is mostly seen in children and young adults.</p>\n<p>It presents as a <strong>painless condition</strong> with <strong>foul-smelling</strong> discharge from the ear, that does not respond to standard antibiotic treatment. A thickened tympanic membrane with <strong>multiple perforations</strong> in pars tensa of the tympanic membrane and <strong>pale granulations </strong>in the middle ear cleft is seen. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Facial nerve palsy</strong> is the most common complication. Mastoiditis, postauricular fistula, osteomyelitis are also seen.</p>\n<p>Histopathology shows submucosal tubercles with Langhans giant cells and caseation necrosis. Smears and culture of ear discharge may be positive for AFB. Chest X-ray features of tuberculosis along with other evidence of tuberculosis in the body help in confirmation of the diagnosis. DNA probe and PCR from the ear discharge can provide an early diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antitubercular therapy</li>\n<li>Aural toilet</li>\n<li>Treatment of secondary infection</li>\n<li>Mastoid surgery for complications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Reconstructive middle ear surgery is delayed till antitubercular therapy has been completed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BJCt2v1w","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"7_mechanical_injuries","title":"7.Mechanical Injuries","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC7063","text":"Identify the injury in the‌ image below.","raw_text":"Identify the injury in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abra‌sion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lacer‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bru‌ise","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Imp‌rint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abrasion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/02d3cfb85cef4f7ba977aec11597ff99.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abrasion</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>abrasion.</strong></p>\n<p>An abrasion is the <strong>most superficial</strong> of injuries. It does not penetrate the full thickness of the epidermis.</p>\n<p>A <strong>pure abrasion </strong>does<strong> not bleed</strong>, as blood vessels are confined to the dermis. However, the <strong>dermal papillae</strong> are <strong>corrugated</strong> in nature. Thus, many <strong>abrasions</strong> enter the <strong>dermis</strong> and <strong>bleeding</strong> commonly occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0068","text":"An abrasion involves which‌ of the following layers ​of the skin?","raw_text":"An abrasion involves which of the following layers of the skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial thickness of epide‌rmis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Full thickness of epidermi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermis + superficial de‌rmis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidermis‌ + dermis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Partial thickness of epidermis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Partial thickness of epidermis</strong></p><p>An <strong>abrasion</strong> is the most superficial of injuries and is one that <strong>does not penetrate </strong>the <strong>full thickness</strong> of the<strong> epidermis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thus the <strong>pure abrasion does not bleed</strong>, as blood vessels are confined to the dermis. The dermal papillae are corrugated in nature. Therefore, many abrasions enter the dermis, and bleeding commonly occurs.</p>\n<p>Abrasions are commonly known as grazes or scratches.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0070","text":"In an abrasion caused by t‌angential force, which of​ the following indicates ‍the direction of said for‍ce?","raw_text":"In an abrasion caused by tangential force, which of the following indicates the direction of said force?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fishtailing ‌of the wound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epiderm‌al tags","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tailing of‌ the wound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crushed hai‌r follicles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epidermal tags","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/989402d538d14bf5b77d1d0465300199x1280x610.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epidermal tags</strong></p><p>In an <strong>abrasion</strong> caused by a tangential force, the <strong>direction of</strong> the <strong>force</strong> is determined by <strong>epidermal tags. </strong></p>\n<p>Abrasions caused by a tangential force are known as '<strong>b</strong><strong>rush abrasion</strong>' or 'grazes' or '<strong>brush burn.</strong>' It is commonly seen when a body is dragged over a rough surface, such as a road. This scrapes linear furrows across the skin. The epidermal tags raised by the impact tend to pile up at the distal end.</p>\n<p>The method of formation of epidermal tags is given in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A:<strong> Fishtailing</strong> is the <strong>shape</strong> of the stab wound caused by a <strong>single-edged</strong> weapon.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C:<strong> Tailing</strong> of the wound indicates the direction of injury in <strong>incised</strong> wounds. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D:<strong> Crushed</strong> hair <strong>follicles</strong> are seen at the edge of the wound in a<strong> laceration</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0076","text":"Abrasions are noted on the‌ body of a person who was​ found dead at his home. ‍Which of the following in‍dicates that these might ​be produced after death?","raw_text":"Abrasions are noted on the body of a person who was found dead at his home. Which of the following indicates that these might be produced after death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vital react‌ion positive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congest‌ion seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Present over bony prominen‌ces","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bright reddish brown in co‌lor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Present over bony prominences","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Present over bony prominences</strong></p><p><strong>Abrasions</strong> present over<strong> bony prominences</strong> indicate that they might be produced<strong> after death</strong>. Antemortem abrasions may be located anywhere. </p>\n<p>Difference between antemortem and postmortem wounds:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 45px;\">Trait</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.25px; height: 45px;\"><strong>Antemortem wounds  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.75px; height: 45px;\"><strong>Postmortem wounds</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 45px;\">Site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.25px; height: 45px;\">Anywhere on the body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.75px; height: 45px;\">Usually over bony prominences  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 59px;\">Colour</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.25px; height: 59px;\">Bright reddish brown</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.75px; height: 59px;\">Yellowish, translucent, Parchment like </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 41px;\">Exudation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.25px; height: 41px;\">More</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.75px; height: 41px;\">Less </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 78px;\">Microscopic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.25px; height: 78px;\">Intravital reaction and congestion seen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.75px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>No intravital reaction and congestion </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0097","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is falsely matche​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is falsely matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antemortem wound - positiv‌e vital reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antemortem wound - negativ‌e vital reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postmortem wound - negativ‌e vital reaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postmortem wound - no vita‌l reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Postmortem wound - negative vital reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8606920587c1439d90b3f3e6972483a1x1280x1177.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Postmortem wound - negative vital reaction</strong></p><p><strong>Postmortem wounds</strong> show <strong>no/absent vital reaction</strong> because no enzyme activity will be found in a wound sustained after death. This is <strong>not</strong> the same as a negative vital reaction as it is indicative of decreased enzymatic activity and is seen in antemortem wounds.</p>\n<p>Vital reactions are local tissue reactions at the site of tissue damage<strong>.</strong> Only <strong>antemortem wounds</strong> show <strong>vital reactions</strong> as cells are active at the time of wound infliction. The vital reactions of an antemortem wound are of two zones: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Negative</strong> vital reaction (<strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>enzymatic activity</strong> of wound): It occurs in the <strong>central necrotic area,</strong> close to the edge of the wound where there is irreversible damage to tissues. </li>\n<li><strong>Positive</strong> vital reaction (<strong>increase</strong> in <strong>enzymatic activity</strong> of wound): It occurs just outside the necrotic area of the wound in order to salvage damaged (but not irreversibly damaged) tissues.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Difference between antemortem and postmortem wounds:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 269px;\" width=\"377\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80.3594px;\">Trait</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.516px;\"><strong>Antemortem wounds  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.125px;\"><strong>Postmortem wounds</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80.3594px;\">Site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.516px;\">Anywhere on the body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.125px;\">Usually over bony prominences  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80.3594px;\">Colour</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.516px;\">Bright reddish brown</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.125px;\">Yellowish, translucent, Parchment like </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80.3594px;\">Exudation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.516px;\">More</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.125px;\">Less </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80.3594px;\">Microscopic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.516px;\">Intravital reaction and congestion seen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.125px;\">\n<p>No intravital reaction and congestion </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0078","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding a bruis​e is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding a bruise is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They lie underneath an int‌act epidermis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are caused due to cap‌illary bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood commonly collects in‌ the subcutaneous layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It may become visible 1-2 ‌days after the traumatic ​incident","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They are caused due to capillary bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They are caused due to capillary bleeding</strong></p><p><strong>Contusions/bruises</strong> are caused by <strong>damage</strong> to <strong>veins, venules</strong> and <strong>small arteries</strong>. Capillary bleeding would not be visible to the naked eye.</p>\n<p>A pure bruise lies beneath an <strong>intact epidermis.</strong> There is an extravascular collection of blood that has leaked from blood vessels damaged by mechanical impact.</p>\n<p>The usual <strong>bruise</strong> from a blunt impact is <strong>situated</strong> in the <strong>subcutaneous tissues</strong>, often in the fat layer. <strong>Obese</strong> people tend to<strong> bruise</strong> more <strong>easily</strong> due to the<strong> greater</strong> volume of <strong>subcutaneous</strong> tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Delayed bruising</strong> refers to bruises that become more prominent with the passage of <strong>hours or days</strong>. This is caused by the percolation of blood from deeper tissues upwards towards the epidermis. It is also partly due to continued bleeding from the ruptured vessels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0079","text":"Which of the following sit‌es is more prone to bruis​ing with blunt trauma?","raw_text":"Which of the following sites is more prone to bruising with blunt trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sh‌ins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Abd‌omen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Butt‌ocks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ar‌ms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shins</strong></p><p>The <strong>shins</strong> are particularly prone to <strong>bruising</strong> with <strong>blunt trauma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Resilient areas</strong>, such as the <strong>abdominal wall</strong> and <strong>buttocks</strong>, bruise less with a given impact. The head, chest, and shins are more prone to bruising. This is because these regions have underlying bone and less subcutaneous tissue. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0080","text":"The police brought a victi‌m of alleged domestic vio​lence with a big bruise f‍or evaluation. She claime‍d that it was inflicted b​y her husband three days ‌ago after which he had go‍ne out of town. Which of ​the following is correct ‌while determining the age​ of this injury?","raw_text":"The police brought a victim of alleged domestic violence with a big bruise for evaluation. She claimed that it was inflicted by her husband three days ago after which he had gone out of town. Which of the following is correct while determining the age of this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histological examination i‌s the most reliable metho​d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The appearance of green co‌lour is the most signific​ant change","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perl's reaction is the mos‌t useful criterion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bright yellow colour indic‌ates that it is less than​ 18 hours old","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perl's reaction is the most useful criterion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perl&#x27;s reaction is the most useful criterion</strong></p><p>The <strong>most useful</strong> criterion for <strong>dating</strong> a <strong>bruise</strong> is <strong>Perl's reaction </strong>for detecting <strong>haemosiderin. </strong>It becomes <strong>positive</strong> about <strong>24–90 hours</strong> after infliction. Sometimes, there can be an earlier appearance, even down to 12 hours.</p>\n<p>Color changes in a bruise cannot be reliably used to estimate the age of a bruise. The <strong>most significant</strong> change is the appearance of a <strong>yellow color.</strong> This <strong>indicates</strong> that the bruise is <strong>more than 18 hours</strong> old.</p>\n<p>Histological examination is unreliable in the accurate dating of bruises.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4896","text":"A bruise on the upper arm ‌of an alleged victim of t​he assault has the follow‍ing appearance. What is t‍he age of the injury base​d on colour of this bruis‌e?","raw_text":"A bruise on the upper arm of an alleged victim of the assault has the following appearance. What is the age of the injury based on colour of this bruise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 wee‌ks old","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-3 da‌ys old","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7-12 d‌ays old","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4-5 da‌ys old","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7-12 days old","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99a3d78688e247549538f1d3b9e1effb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7-12 days old</strong></p><p>The <strong>bruise</strong> in the given image has a <strong>yellow appearance</strong> which is attributed to the presence of <strong>bilirubin</strong>. This finding makes the bruise about <strong>7-12 </strong><strong>days old.</strong></p>\n<p>Bruises are mechanical injuries that are characterized by the extravasation of blood into subcutaneous or subepithelial tissue spaces. The continuity of the overlying skin is preserved. These injuries are typical consequences of <strong>blunt force trauma.</strong> </p>\n<p>The extravasated blood serves as a sign of inflammation to attract macrophages, which degrade the hemoglobin into various compounds (hemosiderin, haematoidin, and bilirubin). This gives rise to shifting color changes that begin at the wound's periphery and migrate towards the center as time goes on.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0086","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about ectopic co​ntusions?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about ectopic contusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bruising on the neck may i‌ndicate a jaw fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bruising at the nape of th‌e neck indicates fracture​ of the posterior cranial‍ fossa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A black eye may be from fr‌acture of the anterior cr​anial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bruising behind the ear in‌dicates fracture of the m​iddle cranial fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bruising at the nape of the neck indicates fracture of the posterior cranial fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8babad4ef7b74db698c947c02efef0abx1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa6dd8ed3b92443ab9a115b8a513003dx720x743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bruising at the nape of the neck indicates fracture of the posterior cranial fossa</strong></p><p>Bruising at the nape of the neck does not indicate a fracture of the posterior cranial fossa.</p>\n<p><strong>Ectopic bruises</strong> are also called <strong>migratory/percolated</strong> bruising. They are found at a <strong>different location</strong> than the actual site of <strong>injury.</strong> They are caused by the<strong> percolation</strong> of blood in the <strong>subcutaneous tissue</strong> from one site to another.</p>\n<p>Examples are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Black eye</strong> - due to actual trauma to the eye, or maybe an ectopic bruise. It may be from trauma to the forehead or from a fracture of the anterior cranial fossa.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a black eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Battle sign</strong> - bruising seen over the<strong> mastoid process</strong>. It is from a fracture of the middle cranial fossa. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows battle sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bruising on the neck may indicate a jaw fracture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0088","text":"You are asked to conduct a‌n autopsy of a person who​ was found dead at his ho‍me. You notice the given ‍findings on the body. Whi​ch test can be used to di‌fferentiate this from ant‍emortem bruising?","raw_text":"You are asked to conduct an autopsy of a person who was found dead at his home. You notice the given findings on the body. Which test can be used to differentiate this from antemortem bruising?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gettler‌'s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incisi‌on test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Precipi‌tin test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breslau‌'s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incision test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e2796dd52a7f42e5b5a5ca3b05f5b333.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incision test</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>postmortem hypostasis</strong>. The classic test used to differentiate a bruise from hypostasis is the<strong> incision test. </strong></p>\n<p>The suspected area is incised to see if the underlying blood is <strong>intravascular (hypostasis)</strong> or infiltrating the tissues <strong>outside</strong> the <strong>vessels (contusion)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Gettler's chloride test</strong> is used to<strong> differentiate </strong>between<strong> freshwater </strong>and<strong> saltwater drowning. </strong>It is not widely used now. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>In<strong> </strong>the<strong> precipitin test, </strong>a specific antiserum is made to react with the seminal fluid, blood, or saliva. A positive test will confirm human origin.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Breslau's second life test</strong> is done to determine whether a child was born alive or not.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0090","text":"The autopsy of a victim re‌veals similar findings se​en in the given image. Wh‍ich of the following woul‍d have caused this injury​?","raw_text":"The autopsy of a victim reveals similar findings seen in the given image. Which of the following would have caused this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Road traff‌ic accident","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron‌ rod","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serrate‌d knife","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double ed‌ged knife","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iron rod","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed9262f23dca4d01a668dd2d9d669232.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0994e7aaec1444cdb7f8125ec8427bdex600x439.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iron rod</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>'tram-line' bruising. </strong>It would have most likely been produced by a <strong>stick</strong> or an<strong> iron rod</strong>. </p>\n<p>It occurs when the skin surface is struck by a rod or rectangular sectioned object. The weapon sinks into the skin on impact. This causes the <strong>edges</strong> to drag the skin downwards and the<strong> traction, tears</strong> the <strong>marginal blood vessels</strong>. The <strong>centre compresses</strong> the skin. This causes little or no damage to the vessels in the absence of underlying bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0098","text":"Which of the following tis‌sues is most resistant to​ knife penetration?","raw_text":"Which of the following tissues is most resistant to knife penetration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mus‌cle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcutane‌ous tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uncalcifie‌d cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sk‌in","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Skin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Skin</strong></p><p>The tissue <strong>most resistant</strong> to knife penetration is the <strong>skin</strong>, apart from <strong>bone</strong> or <strong>calcified cartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>When a knife-point impacts against the skin, the latter dimples, and resists. The tension developed in the stretched skin appears to act as an elastic reservoir and when the threshold of resistance is exceeded, penetration suddenly occurs. </p>\n<p>Muscle offers little resistance to penetration.<strong> Uncalcified cartilage </strong>and<strong> subcutaneous tissue</strong> are <strong>easily penetrated</strong> by a sharp knife.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0099","text":"An assault victim is rushe‌d to the emergency depart​ment with a stab wound. Y‍ou observe that the wound‍ is wedge-shaped. Which w​eapon would have most lik‌ely produced this wound?","raw_text":"An assault victim is rushed to the emergency department with a stab wound. You observe that the wound is wedge-shaped. Which weapon would have most likely produced this wound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single-ed‌ged knife","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serrate‌d knife","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Double-ed‌ged knife","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Screw‌driver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single-edged knife","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e08de029bdc843b3bdfc9a806892f2dcx1280x3765.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single-edged knife</strong></p><p>A <strong>wedge-shaped</strong> wound is produced by a <strong>single-edged knife</strong>. A <strong>double-edged</strong> knife produces an <strong>elliptical wound</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the types of wounds produced by different weapons:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0100","text":"In a case of suspected sta‌bbing, you are trying to ​identify the murder weapo‍n using the wound charact‍eristics. All of the foll​owing factors might lead ‌to a false interpretation‍ except:","raw_text":"In a case of suspected stabbing, you are trying to identify the murder weapon using the wound characteristics. All of the following factors might lead to a false interpretation except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If the knife tapers beyond‌ the point to which it wa​s driven into the wound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If the knife was driven up‌ to the hilt into the wou​nd","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If the weapon was moved af‌ter insertion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"If the wound is parallel t‌o the Langer lines","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. If the wound is parallel to the Langer lines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f238318dc8d444d1935191779aa3fd53x1280x1182.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. If the wound is parallel to the Langer lines</strong></p><p>If the <strong>wound</strong> is <strong>parallel</strong> to the <strong>langer lines,</strong> it would appear <strong>normal.</strong> In contrast to a wound that is <strong>perpendicular</strong> to the <strong>Langer lines,</strong> which would be longer than normal. This is because such wounds tend to <strong>gape more,</strong> giving a false interpretation of the weapon.</p>\n<p>Option A: The<strong> taper</strong> of the<strong> blade</strong> is<strong> related to wound size</strong>. The length of the wound would be equal to the width of the blade at the point to which it was inserted. Thus the length of the wound varies according to the depth to which it was inserted. Once the blade edges become parallel, the wound size remains constant for further penetration of the knife.</p>\n<p>Option B: If a <strong>knife</strong> is<strong> driven up to</strong> the<strong> hilt</strong>, the depth of the wound as measured at autopsy may be greater than the true length of the blade. This is usually seen in the abdomen, and to a lesser extent in the chest. A <strong>forceful stab</strong> can cause the abdominal or chest wall to <strong>indent</strong>, allowing the knife tip to reach tissues that would normally be beyond its reach. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: If the <strong>weapon</strong> is <strong>moved</strong> after insertion, it distorts wound characteristics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0101","text":"What type of injury is sca‌lping?","raw_text":"What type of injury is scalping?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cont‌usion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abra‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incise‌d wound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lacer‌ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Laceration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Laceration</strong></p><p><strong>Scalping</strong> is a type of <strong>laceration</strong>. Here, a large area of skin and subcutaneous tissue is rolled off the scalp. </p>\n<p>These lesions could be caused by the traction of hair being trapped in machinery. This was formerly a common industrial accident. 'Hairnets' were worn by women factory workers to prevent this.</p>\n<p><strong>Flaying </strong>is also a type of laceration, caused by shearing force. This is where a large area of skin and subcutaneous tissue is rolled off a limb. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0102","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings in a patient sugges​ts an incised wound, rath‍er than a split laceratio‍n?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings in a patient suggests an incised wound, rather than a split laceration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sharp linear injury in und‌erlying bone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hairs crossi‌ng the wound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue strands across the ‌interior of the wound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bruising around margin of ‌the wound.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sharp linear injury in underlying bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03b732b327144025b7ca49c278280593x1280x1280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93e1af89549f493db0d50410c49f69a4x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sharp linear injury in underlying bone</strong></p><p>Sharp<strong> linear injury</strong> in the <strong>underlying bone</strong> suggests an<strong> incised wound,</strong> rather than a split laceration.</p>\n<p>An incised wound is a clean-cut wound caused by a sharp-cutting instrument.</p>\n<p>A <strong>split laceration</strong> is a type of laceration that usually occurs in areas where the <strong>skin overlies bone</strong>. An example of such an area is the scalp. Split lacerations can <strong>resemble incised</strong> wounds.</p>\n<p>A <strong>laceration</strong> can be distinguished from an incised wound by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bruising </strong>and<strong> crushing</strong> of the margins</li>\n<li>Persistence of <strong>tissue strands</strong> across the <strong>interior</strong> of the <strong>wound</strong>, including fascial bands, vessels, and nerves</li>\n<li>Absence of a sharply linear injury in the underlying bone</li>\n<li>In areas covered by hair, as on the scalp, intact <strong>hairs</strong> will <strong>cross</strong> the <strong>wound</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows an incised wound. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows a lacerated wound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Lacerations are also commonly known as cuts or tears.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0103","text":"You notice tearing of the ‌skin at the ends of a lac​eration on a victim. What‍ is this finding known as‍?","raw_text":"You notice tearing of the skin at the ends of a laceration on a victim. What is this finding known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sparrow‌'s foot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wed‌ging","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fish ‌tails","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Swallo‌w tails","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Swallow tails","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74d573cdd7b54d749042fd2e83dff549x600x782.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd214cbaeac34488925354fc1a25541ex549x397.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Swallow tails</strong></p><p><strong>Tearing</strong> at the <strong>ends</strong> of a <strong>laceration</strong> are called <strong>swallow-tails. </strong>These are seen at angles diverging from the main laceration itself.</p>\n<p>The image below shows swallow-tails in a lacerated wound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sparrow's foot</strong> marks are seen in <strong>windshield injuries</strong> in car accidents.</p>\n<p>Option B: Wedge-shaped injuries are seen in stabbing with a single-edged weapon.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fish-tails: </strong>These may be seen in stab wounds where the skin splits at the corner. </p>\n<p>The image below shows fish-tail in a stab wound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0104","text":"You are examining the body‌ of a patient who was fou​nd dead at his house. Mul‍tiple linear incised woun‍ds are noted on the ulnar​ border of the forearm. W‌hat are these suggestive ‍of?","raw_text":"You are examining the body of a patient who was found dead at his house. Multiple linear incised wounds are noted on the ulnar border of the forearm. What are these suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defence‌ wounds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Self-mu‌tilation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tentati‌ve cuts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hesitati‌on wounds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Defence wounds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Defence wounds</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, multiple linear incised <strong>wounds</strong> on the inner side (<strong>ulnar</strong> border) of the forearm are most likely to be <strong>defence wounds.</strong> </p>\n<p>They are sustained when a victim is trying to protect himself from attacks (homicide). The attack may be by fists, feet, and blunt or sharp instruments. </p>\n<p>In case of a <strong>blunt instrument</strong>, fist or feet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bruises</strong> are most common along with abrasions</li>\n<li>Commonly on the <strong>extensor </strong>and<strong> ulnar aspect </strong>of the <strong>forearms</strong>, wrists, back of the hand, and knuckles</li>\n<li>May be seen on <strong>thighs </strong>due to an attempt to protect genitals</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the case of a <strong>sharp instrument</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seen on the <strong>ulnar</strong> side of the <strong>forearm</strong>, wrist, hand, and fingers</li>\n<li>Cuts on the <strong>flexures</strong> of <strong>phalanges</strong> occur if the <strong>victim</strong> tries to <strong>seize</strong> the <strong>weapon</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Firearm injury:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arms may be used to shield the trunk or head from the blast</li>\n<li>An entrance and an exit wound in the upper arm may be seen</li>\n<li>The missile may then re-penetrate the trunk</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options C and D: <strong>Tentative</strong> cuts or <strong>hesitation</strong> wounds are self-inflicted wounds made while <strong>attempting</strong> to commit <strong>suicide</strong>.They are usually on the radial side of the forearm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0095","text":"When is leucocyte infiltra‌tion into wounds seen?","raw_text":"When is leucocyte infiltration into wounds seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0-4 ‌hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-12 ‌hours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12-24‌ hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-3 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4-12 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4-12 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Leucocyte infiltration</strong> in wounds is seen at<strong> 4-12 hours. </strong></p>\n<p>Histological changes in wounds</p>\n<table style=\"height: 402px; width: 621px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 35.5625px;\"><strong>Time after injury</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 35.5625px;\"><strong>Histological changes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 110px;\">30 minutes-4 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Margination of leucocytes in blood vessels</p>\n<p>Mast cells lose their granules</p>\n<p>Fibrin appears in the wound</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 110px;\">4-12 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Leucocyte infilitration (mostly polymorphs)</p>\n<p>Tissue edema</p>\n<p>Start of epithelial regeneration</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 78px;\">12-24 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Mononuclear leukocytes increase</p>\n<p>Epidermis begins to spread across the wound</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 78px;\">24-72 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Leukocyte infiltration reaches a peak at 48 hours</p>\n<p>Granulation tissue appears</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 18px;\">3-6 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 18px;\">Collagen formation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 110px;\">10-15 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Collagen reaction subsides</p>\n<p>Vascularity decrease</p>\n<p>Cell population drops</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 173px; height: 46px;\">2 weeks - several months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 434px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Consolidation of healing tissue</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uxXd_7mN","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"7_syndrome_of_inappropriate_anti_diuretic_hormone_secretion__siadh_","title":"7.Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB6762","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings will you not expect​ to see in a patient with‌ SIADH?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings will you not expect to see in a patient with SIADH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal serum urea and crea‌tinine levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low uric a‌cid levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern‌atremia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal blo‌od pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypernatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypernatremia</strong></p><p>SIADH does not cause hypernatremia. SIADH is the most common cause of <strong>euvolemic hyponatremia</strong>. </p>\n<p>The excess ADH secreted leads to the <strong>gain of water</strong> in the body. This stimulates baroreceptors to release atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP). ANP leads to the <strong>loss of water and salt</strong> in the urine, leading to <strong>urine sodium level of &gt; 20 mEq/L</strong> in these patients. Thus, the level of water reaches a steady state (euvolemia) but the urinary loss of sodium continues to occur, giving rise to hyponatremia.</p>\n<p>SIADH is characterized by low levels of uric acid, normal blood pressure, and normal urea and creatinine levels.</p>\n<p>SIADH is diagnosed based on the following criteria (<strong>Bartter-Schwartz criteria</strong>):</p>\n<p>1. Reduced serum osmolality<br/>2. Urine osmolality &gt; Serum osmolality<br/>3. Clinical euvolemia<br/>4. Increased levels of urinary sodium<br/>5. Absence of other causes of hyponatremia like pituitary, renal, adrenal, thyroid or liver diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6766","text":"A 66-year-old chronic smok‌er who was diagnosed with​ small cell carcinoma of ‌the lung presents to the ​casualty with nausea, con​fusion, and dizziness. Af‌ter the initial workup, h‌e is diagnosed to have sy‍ndrome of inappropriate A‌DH secretion (SIADH). Thi‍s condition is characteri​zed by which of the follo​wing findings?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old chronic smoker who was diagnosed with small cell carcinoma of the lung presents to the casualty with nausea, confusion, and dizziness. After the initial workup, he is diagnosed to have syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH). This condition is characterized by which of the following findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyponatremia and urine sod‌ium excretion &amp;gt; 20 mEq​/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypernatremia and urine so‌dium excretion &amp;gt; 20 mE​q/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyponatremia and urine sod‌ium excretion &amp;lt; 20 mEq​/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypernatremia and urine so‌dium excretion &amp;lt; 20 mE​q/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyponatremia and urine sodium excretion > 20 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyponatremia and urine sodium excretion &gt; 20 mEq/L</strong></p><p>The syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH) is associated with <strong>increased urinary excretion of sodium (urine sodium excretion &gt; 20 mEq/L)</strong>, and <strong>hyponatremia.</strong></p>\n<p>Small cell lung cancer is commonly associated with SIADH due to the ectopic release of antidiuretic hormone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6758","text":"SIADH is not observed in w‌hich of the following con​ditions?","raw_text":"SIADH is not observed in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple ‌sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oat cell carcinoma of lung‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute attack‌ of porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary pulmonary emphysem‌a","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary pulmonary emphysema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary pulmonary emphysema</strong></p><p>Primary pulmonary emphysema is not a cause of SIADH. Respiratory causes of SIADH include <strong>asthma</strong>, <strong>pneumothorax</strong> and <strong>positive-pressure respiration.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6760","text":"A 47-year-old woman presen‌ted with headache and nau​sea. Examination did not ‌reveal any signs of dehyd​ration and investigations​ revealed the findings sh‌own below. Which of the f‌ollowing drugs does not l‍ead to this condition? La‌boratory tests Results Se‍rum sodium 118 mmol/L Ser​um osmolality 267 mOsm/kg​ Urine osmolality 362 mOs​m/kg Urine sodium 70 mmol‌/l","raw_text":"<p>A 47-year-old woman presented with headache and nausea. Examination did not reveal any signs of dehydration and investigations revealed the findings shown below. Which of the following drugs does not lead to this condition?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 194px;\" width=\"302\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Laboratory tests</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Results</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: left;\">Serum sodium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: center;\">118 mmol/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: left;\">Serum osmolality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: center;\">267 mOsm/kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: left;\">Urine osmolality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: center;\">362 mOsm/kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: left;\">Urine sodium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.6px; text-align: center;\">70 mmol/l</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Demeclo‌cycline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nico‌tine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Demeclocycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Demeclocycline</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>SIADH</strong> and demeclocycline does not cause this condition. Demeclocycline causes<strong> </strong>nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and hence is used in the treatment of chronic SIADH.</p>\n<p>The following features in the given case point towards the diagnosis of SIADH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serum osmolality less than 275 mOsm/Kg</li>\n<li>Urine osmolality more than 100 mOsm/Kg</li>\n<li>Urine sodium more than 20 mmol/L</li>\n<li>Serum sodium level less than 135 mmol/L</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6761","text":"Which of the following tum‌ors is not associated wit​h syndrome of inappropria‌te ADH secretion (SIADH)?​","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors is not associated with syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ewing’s‌ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thy‌moma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromopho‌be adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carc‌inoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chromophobe adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chromophobe adenoma</strong></p><p>Chromophobe adenoma is a type of pituitary adenoma that causes symptoms of hypogonadism due to pressure effects on the pituitary stalk. It is not a cause of syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1006","text":"A child who underwent vent‌riculoperitoneal shunt su​rgery for tubercular meni‌ngitis with hydrocephalus​, develops polyuria and h​yponatremia. What could b‌e the probable cause?","raw_text":"A child who underwent ventriculoperitoneal shunt surgery for tubercular meningitis with hydrocephalus, develops polyuria and hyponatremia. What could be the probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central diabetes insipidus‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SI‌ADH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebral s‌alt wasting","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Shunt‌ block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cerebral salt wasting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cerebral salt wasting</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>cerebral salt wasting</strong>. Both SIADH and cerebral salt wasting cause hyponatremia, but <strong>polyuria</strong> happens in cerebral salt wasting.</p>\n<p>Cerebral salt wasting results from <strong>hypersecretion of atrial natriuretic peptide. </strong>It is seen primarily with central nervous system disorders including brain tumors, head trauma, hydrocephalus, neurosurgery, cerebrovascular accidents, and brain death. It is associated with hyponatremia, polyuria and elevated urinary sodium excretion. Treatment is by restoring intravascular volume with sodium chloride and water.</p>\n<p>Clinical parameters to distinguish between SIADH, cerebral salt wasting, and central diabetes insipidus are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 275px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Clinical parameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\"><strong>SIADH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\"><strong>Cerebral salt wasting</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\"><strong>Central DI</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Serum sodium</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Urine output</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Normal or low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Urine sodium</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Very high</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Intravascular volume status</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Normal or high</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Vasopressin level </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6770","text":"A patient who presented wi‌th acute confusion and vo​miting was diagnosed to h‌ave SIADH. This condition​ can be best treated by w​hich of the following met‌hods?","raw_text":"A patient who presented with acute confusion and vomiting was diagnosed to have SIADH. This condition can be best treated by which of the following methods?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infusion of hypertonic (3%‌) saline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infusion of hypotonic (0.4‌5%) saline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infusion of normal (0.9%) ‌saline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Administrat‌ion of DDAVP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infusion of hypertonic (3%) saline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infusion of hypertonic (3%) saline</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute symptomatic SIADH</strong> and this condition is best treated by the<strong> infusion of hypertonic (3%) saline. </strong>This mode of treatment also helps in the correction of sodium deficiency that is partly responsible for hyponatremia.</p>\n<p>The other form of treatment is to help reduce the concentration of body water by giving an AVP receptor-2 antagonist, conivaptan. Conivaptan blocks the antidiuretic effect of AVP and increases urine output.</p>\n<p>DDAVP is a synthetic analog of AVP and should not be used as it will further increase water retention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6772","text":"What is the drug of choice‌ for the treatment of chr​onic SIADH?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for the treatment of chronic SIADH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tolv‌aptan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Demeclo‌cycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fludroc‌ortisone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tolvaptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tolvaptan</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for the treatment of <strong>chronic SIADH</strong> is <strong>tolvaptan</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vaptans are selective V2 receptor antagonists that act by<strong> increasing urinary water excretion</strong> by blocking the antidiuretic effect of AVP.</p>\n<p>Treatment options for chronic SIADH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Restriction of fluid intake</strong> to below the level of urine output - This is considered the mainstay of treatment. But it also has certain disadvantages as stated below, and hence adding on a drug like vaptan is necessary in most cases.</li>\n<li>Tolvaptan</li>\n<li>Demeclocycline</li>\n<li>Fludrocortisone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Advantages of vaptans over fluid restriction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluid restriction is usually a burden to the patient, unreliable, impractical, and slow to work.</li>\n<li>Increased thirst is a frequently observed characteristic of hyponatremia in SIADH, and is particularly difficult to suppress. Hence, water restriction in SIADH frequently leads to non-compliance in the long run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6771","text":"What causes central pontin‌e myelinolysis?","raw_text":"What causes central pontine myelinolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid correction of hypona‌tremia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid correction of hypoka‌lemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rapid correction of hyponatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rapid correction of hyponatremia</strong></p><p>Rapid correction of hyponatremia may result in central pontine myelinolysis.</p>\n<p>Central pontine myelinolysis is an<strong> osmotic demyelination syndrome </strong>(ODS), wherein rapid correction of hyponatremia results in water being driven out of the cell which has adapted to a chronic state of hypotonicity, leading to damage to the myelin sheath of these cells.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the acute onset of quadriparesis, ataxia, and abnormal extraocular movements.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"t1Pz7NCU","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"7_staphylococci","title":"7.Staphylococci","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA1496","text":"A child injures his knee w‌hile playing. A few days ‍later, the wound on his k‍nee shows signs of inflam‌mation. The wound is cult‌ured and its Gram-stained​ smear is given below. Fu​rther testing shows that ‌the cultured organism is ​coagulase-positive. Ident‌ify the organism.","raw_text":"A child injures his knee while playing. A few days later, the wound on his knee shows signs of inflammation. The wound is cultured and its Gram-stained smear is given below. Further testing shows that the cultured organism is coagulase-positive. Identify the organism. ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcus saprophytic‌us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b626b439e6f48e08ec4579ef2f19f9e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d5df45456724aed8e8891d218b36c55x600x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>An infected wound culture showing<strong> Gram-positive cocci</strong> arranged<strong> </strong>in<strong> clusters</strong> which are <strong>coagulase-positive </strong>suggest that the cultured organism is <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Staphylococci are Gram-positive cocci that are catalase-positive. They grow in clusters because the cells divide successively in three perpendicular planes, with the daughter cells remaining attached to one another following each successive division. Among the 45 different species of <em>Staphylococcus</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> is the only one which is coagulase positive. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Streptococci</strong> are catalase negative. They are Gram-positive cocci arranged in chains. </p>\n<p>Options C &amp; D: <strong><em>S. epidermidis</em></strong> and <strong><em>S. saprophyticus</em></strong> are both coagulase negative.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Different bacteria with their typical arrangements are given below:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Staphylococcus: Clusters</li>\n<li>Streptococcus: Chains</li>\n<li>Pneumococcus: Pairs/lanceolate shaped</li>\n<li>Micrococcus: Tetrads</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1501","text":"The specimen obtained by p‌erforming incision and dr‍ainage of a breast absces‍s was sent for culture on‌ nutrient agar. An image ‌of the culture plate is g​iven below. Microscopy of​ the isolated organism re‌vealed gram-positive cocc​i arranged in clusters. W‌hich of the following is ‍a false statement regardi‍ng this organism?","raw_text":"The specimen obtained by performing incision and drainage of a breast abscess was sent for culture on nutrient agar.  An image of the culture plate is given below. Microscopy of the isolated organism revealed gram-positive cocci arranged in clusters. Which of the following is a false statement regarding this organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pigment production occurs ‌optimally at 35°C.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Golden yellow pigmentation‌ is due to β-carotene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pigment production is enha‌nced by adding milk to cu‍lture media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Confluent growth presents ‌a characteristic 'oil-pai‍nt appearance'","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pigment production occurs optimally at 35°C.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a61acd2d11344deeb352dd8478d17ec1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pigment production occurs optimally at 35°C.</strong></p><p><strong>Gram-positive cocci</strong> arranged in <strong>clusters</strong> that produce <strong>golden-yellow pigment</strong> on nutrient agar is suggestive of <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus.</em></strong> The <strong>pigment production</strong> occurs optimally at <strong>22°C</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> is a Gram-positive coccus. On nutrient agar, its colonies are large (2-4 mm in diameter), convex, smooth, shiny, opaque and easily emulsifiable. The colonies appear <strong>golden yellow </strong>due to the presence of <strong>staphyloxanthin</strong> (carotenoid). <strong>Pigment production</strong> occurs optimally at 22°C and in <strong>aerobic conditions</strong>. It is <strong>enhanced</strong> when <strong>1% glycerol</strong> or <strong>milk</strong> is incorporated in the medium.</p>\n<p>On a nutrient <strong>agar slope</strong>, there is confluent growth of Staphylococcus, which appears as a characteristic <strong>oil-paint appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1508","text":"Which factor forms the bas‌is of co-agglutination re‍action shown by Staphyloc‍occus aureus?","raw_text":"Which factor forms the basis of co-agglutination reaction shown by Staphylococcus aureus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prot‌ein A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Clumpin‌g factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta he‌molysin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coag‌ulase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Protein A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e18472f70bd45a4919a28e211947514x1280x1439.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Protein A</strong></p><p><strong>Protein A</strong> forms the basis of the <strong>co-agglutination reaction</strong> shown by <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Protein A bearing staphylococci are<strong> coated</strong> with<strong> IgG antiserum</strong>.<strong> </strong>Protein A binds to the <strong>Fc terminal</strong> of IgG, leaving the Fab terminal free to combine with its specific antigen.<strong> </strong>The corresponding antigen is then added to this mixture which causes <strong>agglutination</strong> of the bacteria. This is known as the <strong>co-agglutination reaction</strong>. It is used for streptococcal, gonococcal or hemophilus <strong>typing</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1510","text":"Which of the following tox‌ins exhibits hot-cold phe‍nomenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following toxins exhibits hot-cold phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-hem‌olysin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-hem‌olysin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"γ-hem‌olysin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"δ-hem‌olysin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β-hemolysin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β-hemolysin</strong></p><p><strong>β-hemolysin</strong> exhibits a <strong>hot-cold phenomenon.</strong></p>\n<p>β-hemolysin is a sphingomyelinase that is hemolytic only for <strong>sheep cells</strong> but not human cells. It initiates <strong>hemolysis </strong>at <strong>37<sup>0</sup>C</strong> but the hemolysis is <strong>evident </strong>only after<strong> chilling</strong>. This is called the hot-cold phenomenon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1514","text":"Which of the following pai‌rs of toxins produced by ‍Staphylococcus aureus are‍ called synergohymenotrop‌ic toxins?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of toxins produced by Staphylococcus aureus are called synergohymenotropic toxins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α and β‌ toxins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β and Panton-Valentine-tox‌ins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"γ and Panton-Valentine-tox‌ins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"β and δ‌ toxins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. γ and Panton-Valentine-toxins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. γ and Panton-Valentine-toxins</strong></p><p><strong>γ-toxin </strong>and<strong> Panton-Valentine-toxin </strong>(leucocidin) of <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> are called <strong>synergohymenotropic toxins.</strong></p>\n<p>Synergohymenotropic (<em>synergo </em>- working together, <em>hymenotropic</em> - form pores) toxins are a family of<strong> bicomponent </strong>toxins that act through the synergistic activity of 2 non-associated secretory proteins - class F and class S proteins. <strong>Gamma hemolysin </strong>has two components necessary for its hemolytic action. It is encoded by three genes HlgA and HlgC (belonging to class S) and HlgB (belonging to class F), resulting in 2 pairs of class S and F components - HlgA and HlgB, or HlgC and HlgB.</p>\n<p><strong>Panton-Valentine Leucocidin</strong> is composed of two components- S and F - encoded by two contiguous and cotranscribed genes, lukS-PV and lukF-PV. It is associated with the majority of community-associated <strong>methicillin-resistant</strong> <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em> (CA-MRSA).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9303","text":"Which of the following sta‌phylococcal infections is‍ not toxin mediated?","raw_text":"Which of the following staphylococcal infections is not toxin mediated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Food po‌isoning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxic shoc‌k syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Septi‌cemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcal scalded ski‌n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Septicemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Septicemia</strong></p><p><strong>Staphylococcal septicemia </strong>is not toxin mediated.</p>\n<p>Septicemia occurs due to the <strong>invasion </strong>of <strong>bacterial organisms</strong> into the bloodstream. This causes an inflammatory response to bacterial products, leading to various manifestations seen in sepsis - fever, hypotension, alkalosis. </p>\n<p><strong>Toxin mediated</strong> staphylococcal infections include food poisoning caused by <strong>enterotoxin</strong>, toxic shock syndrome caused by <strong>toxic shock syndrome toxin </strong>and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome caused by <strong>exfoliative/epidermolytic toxin. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1547","text":"A college student with a h‌istory of intravenous dru‍g abuse presents with a f‍ever for the last 3 weeks‌. Echocardiography is per‌formed which reveals larg​e friable vegetations on ​the cusps of the tricuspi‌d valve. Which of the fol​lowing organisms is the m‌ost common cause for this‍ condition?","raw_text":"A college student with a history of intravenous drug abuse presents with a fever for the last 3 weeks. Echocardiography is performed which reveals large friable vegetations on the cusps of the tricuspid valve. Which of the following organisms is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococc‌us viridans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f0420ce644274e37abf4a9952cd46fdex1280x1252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>Fever with vegetations on the tricuspid valve points to a diagnosis of <strong>right-sided infective endocarditis. <em>Staphylococcus</em> <em>aureus</em></strong> is the most common cause of right-sided infective endocarditis among <strong>intravenous drug abusers</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infective endocarditis is the microbial infection of heart valves or endocardium, leading to the formation of vegetations (thrombotic debris and microbial organisms).The following image shows a graphical illustration of vegetations seen in infective endocarditis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1553","text":"By which of the following ‌mechanisms is penicillin ‍resistance commonly trans‍ferred to Staphylococcus ‌aureus?","raw_text":"By which of the following mechanisms is penicillin resistance commonly transferred to Staphylococcus aureus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Change‌ in PBP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conju‌gation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transd‌uction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Production o‌f β-lactamase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transduction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transduction</strong></p><p><strong>Penicillin resistance</strong> in <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em> is determined by <strong>plasmids</strong> that are most commonly transferred by <strong>transduction.</strong></p>\n<p>Penicillin resistance in Staphylococci most commonly occurs due to bacterial production of <strong>beta-lactamase</strong>, which inactivates penicillins by splitting their beta-lactam ring. These enzymes are inducible and are plasmid-mediated. They confer resistance to penicillin G, ampicillin, piperacillin etc. </p>\n<p>The genetic transfer of antibiotic resistance can occur in the following ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transformation:</strong> After death or cell lysis, some bacteria release their DNA into the environment. Other bacteria, generally of the same species, can come into contact with these <strong>free DNA fragments</strong>, take them up and incorporate them into their DNA by recombination.</li>\n<li><strong>Transduction:</strong> This method involves the transfer of DNA, episome or plasmid from one bacterium to another with the use of a <strong>bacteriophage</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conjugation:</strong> This mechanism requires the presence of a special plasmid called the<strong> F plasmid</strong> that codes for the sex pilus. It is the most common method of transfer of antibiotic resistance in most other bacteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Change in<strong> penicillin binding proteins</strong> (PBPs) and production of <strong>β-lactamases </strong>are<strong> </strong>modes of penicillin resistance seen in <em>S. aureus</em> but the question asks for methods of transfer of drug resistance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7472","text":"Contact based isolation is‌ employed for which of th‍e following diseases?","raw_text":"Contact based isolation is employed for which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MR‌SA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mu‌mps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ast‌hma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MRSA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MRSA</strong></p><p><strong>Contact based isolation</strong> is employed for patients who are infected with <strong>MRSA </strong>(methicillin-resistant <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em><em>).</em> </p>\n<p>The <strong>three categories</strong> of transmission-based precautions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Contact-based isolation</strong> is<strong> </strong>used for infections, diseases, or germs that are spread by touching the patient or items in the room. Examples include MRSA, vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE), diarrheal illnesses, open wounds, and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV).</li>\n<li><strong>Droplet-based isolation</strong> is employed for diphtheria and mumps.</li>\n<li><strong>Airborne precautions</strong> are required to protect against<strong> </strong>conditions like<strong> </strong>measles, severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS), varicella (chickenpox), and <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1545","text":"An outbreak of methicillin‌-resistant Staphylococcus‍ aureus infection was rep‍orted in the surgical war‌d of a hospital. Which of‌ the following precaution​ary measures would you re​commend to best curb its ‌spread?","raw_text":"An outbreak of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection was reported in the surgical ward of a hospital. Which of the following precautionary measures would you recommend to best curb its spread?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administration of vancomyc‌in empirically to all the‍ patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frequent fumigation of war‌ds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wearing masks before perfo‌rming invasive procedures‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Washing of hands before &amp;a‌mp; after treating the pa‍tients","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Washing of hands before & after treating the patients","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Washing of hands before &amp; after treating the patients</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>washing hands</strong> before and after treating the patients is the <strong>most effective</strong> way to <strong>curb</strong> the spread of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (<strong>MRSA</strong>) infections.</p>\n<p>In addition to this, <strong>personnel with</strong> active <strong><em>S. aureus</em> lesions</strong> and <strong>carriers</strong> should be excluded from areas having patients at highest risk for severe staphylococcal infections like newborn nurseries, intensive care units, operating rooms, and cancer chemotherapy wards. In such individuals, the<strong> </strong>application of <strong>topical antiseptics</strong> such as mupirocin to nasal or perineal carriage sites may diminish the shedding of MRSA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1551","text":"A 32-year-old man presente‌d with multiple tender, e‍rythematous fluctuant ski‍n lesions as shown in the‌ image. You suspect it to‌ be a case of botryomycos​is. Which of the followin​g is the most likely orga‌nism implicated in this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presented with multiple tender, erythematous fluctuant skin lesions as shown in the image.  You suspect it to be a case of botryomycosis. Which of the following is the most likely organism implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Madurella ‌mycetomatis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Actinomyce‌s israelii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcus lugdunensis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8dc60d85efcb40baaab848250f2e4d99.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>botryomycosis</strong> is <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Botryomycosis is a rare chronic <strong>bacterial granulomatous disease</strong> that usually involves the <strong>skin</strong> and rarely viscera. It is most commonly caused by<em> Staphylococcus aureus</em> but can also be caused by <em>Pseudomonas</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Proteus</em>, and <em>Streptococci</em>. Patients present with chronic, suppurative, granulomatous skin lesions like nodules, abscesses and sinuses. </p>\n<p>Actinomycetoma, eumycetoma and botryomycosis are difficult to distinguish clinically but are readily distinguished with microbiologic testing or biopsy.  <strong>Granules</strong> of botryomycosis consist of <strong>loose masses</strong> of <strong>cocci</strong>. Organisms can also be seen in wound discharge or histologic specimens.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0633","text":"A 14-year-old boy presents‌ with a history of vomiti‍ng within 3 hours of cons‍umption of food at a part‌y. Which of the following‌ organism is most likely ​to cause these symptoms?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy presents with a history of vomiting within 3 hours of consumption of food at a party. Which of the following organism is most likely to cause these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridiu‌m botulinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridium‌ perfringes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonel‌la typhi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>Vomiting 2 hours after the consumption of food is likely food poisoning by <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong>. </em></p>\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> can grow in carbohydrate and protein food sources, producing <strong>enterotoxins</strong>. There are 15 types, all of which are<strong> superantigens</strong>. They are <strong>preformed</strong> toxins that are relatively <strong>heat stable</strong>. They stimulate the <strong>vagus nerve, </strong> <strong>vomiting centre</strong> of the brain and intestinal peristaltic activity. <strong>Type A</strong> enterotoxin is responsible for most cases of food poisoning. </p>\n<p>The common sources of infection are the foods that are not cooked after handling. These include <strong>sliced meats</strong>, puddings, <strong>pastries</strong>, and sandwiches. Ingestion of such contaminated food causes food poisoning within 30 minutes to 6 hours after consumption. Patients present with nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, and diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1555","text":"An 18-year-old girl presen‌ted with burning micturit‍ion and fever with chills‍ for three days. Urine cu‌lture grew bacteria which‌ were coagulase-negative ​and novobiocin-resistant.​ Which of the following i‌s the most likely organis​m implicated here?","raw_text":" An 18-year-old girl presented with burning micturition and fever with chills for three days. Urine culture grew bacteria which were coagulase-negative and novobiocin-resistant. Which of the following is the most likely organism implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococ‌cus hominis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcus saprophytic‌us","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus saprophyticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7d92335451b49bf9cacdd1700264a01x600x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus saprophyticus</strong></p><p>Burning micturition and fever with chills in a young woman points to a diagnosis of<strong> urinary tract infection</strong> (UTI). The most likely organism implicated here is<strong><em> Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em></strong> as it is <strong>coagulase-negative</strong> and <strong>novobiocin-resistant.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em> is a part of the normal flora of human skin and periurethral area and thus is a common cause of urinary tract infection<strong> </strong>(UTI) in young women. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> is a coagulase-positive bacterium. It is an uncommon cause of UTI.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: <em><strong>Staphylococcus hominis</strong> </em>and <strong><em>Staphylococcus epidermidis</em></strong> are coagulase-negative and novobiocin-sensitive. They are usually considered contaminants if found on urine culture but they may also cause UTIs in children with underlying urinary tract abnormalities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1749","text":"Choose the incorrect state‌ment regarding Micrococci‍.","raw_text":"Choose the incorrect statement regarding Micrococci.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is modified oxidase pos‌itive.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is catala‌se positive.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is rarely associated wi‌th disease.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is arrang‌ed in chains.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is arranged in chains.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05c868c7062945f1b1e6bd668a50e79cx400x300.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is arranged in chains.</strong></p><p>Micrococci are arranged in pairs, <strong>tetrads</strong> or clusters of eight. </p>\n<p>Micrococci are Gram-positive cocci that are often mistaken for <em>S. aureus. </em>However, they are <strong>larger </strong>in size and mostly occur in pairs, tetrads or clusters of eight. They form <strong>smaller colonies</strong> in culture in comparison to <em>S. aureus</em>. <strong>Hugh-Leifson oxidation-fermentation test</strong> can be used to differentiate between micrococci (oxidative pattern) and staphylococci (fermentative pattern).</p>\n<p>Micrococci are <strong>catalase-positive</strong> and <strong>modified oxidase-positive</strong>. They are ordinarily<strong> non-pathogenic</strong> but can rarely cause infections in an immunocompromised host. They are part of normal bacterial flora in the body.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the tetrad arrangement of micrococci:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Different bacteria with their typical arrangements are given below:</p>\n<p>1. Staphylococcus: Clusters<br/>2. Streptococcus: Chains<br/>3. Pneumococcus: Pairs/lanceolate shaped<br/>4. Micrococcus: Tetrads</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qk4QJ6ov","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"7_diagnosis_of_pregnancy_and_antenatal_care","title":"7.Diagnosis Of Pregnancy And Antenatal Care","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MF6838","text":"What is the duration of th‌e 4th trimester in pregna​ncy?","raw_text":"What is the duration of the 4th trimester in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 weeks p‌ostpartum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12 weeks ‌postpartum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 weeks p‌ostpartum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"38 to 40 weeks of pregnanc‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 12 weeks postpartum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 12 weeks postpartum</strong></p><p>The duration of the fourth trimester is <strong>12 weeks postpartum</strong>.</p>\n<p><br/>The concept of fourth trimester was introduced to address common concerns raised by women in puerperium such as <strong>post-partum pain, breastfeeding related problems, stress</strong> and <strong>postpartum depression, </strong>and<strong> lack of awareness</strong> <strong>of newborn care</strong>. The components of postpartum care for the 4th trimester include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dedicated care team</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lactation support</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infant feeding plan</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reproductive life plan</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Contraception</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Addressing complications of pregnancy </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Assessment of cardiovascular risk</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mental health assessment </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic health conditions</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>ACOG recommendation for postpartum visits: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Initial visit: <strong>3 weeks postpartum</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Final visit: <strong>12 weeks postpartum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>Government of India recommendation for postpartum visits.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First visit: <strong>1st day </strong>after delivery </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Second visit: <strong>3rd day </strong>after delivery </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Third visit: <strong>7th day </strong>after delivery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fourth visit: <strong>6 weeks </strong>after delivery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9774","text":"What is the ideal number o‌f antenatal visits?","raw_text":"What is the ideal number of antenatal visits?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12‌-14","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6‌-8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌-9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10‌-11","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 12-14","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 12-14</strong></p><p>The ideal number of antenatal visits is <strong>12-14</strong>.</p>\n<p>The antenatal visits should ideally be: <strong>monthly once</strong> up to <strong>28</strong> weeks, <strong>once</strong> in <strong>two</strong> weeks between <strong>28-36</strong> weeks, and <strong>once weekly</strong> visits from <strong>36 weeks</strong> onwards.</p>\n<p>This translates to a total of <strong>12-15 visits</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>WHO</strong> increased the <strong>minimum</strong> number of antenatal visits from 4 to <strong>8</strong> visits to reduce perinatal mortality.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>Indian scenario</strong>, the earlier minimum of 4 visits were implemented as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1st visit: within 12 weeks (preferably as soon as pregnancy is suspected)</li>\n<li>2nd visit: between 14-26 weeks</li>\n<li>3rd visit: between 28-34 weeks</li>\n<li>4th visit: between 36 weeks and term</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>booked case</strong> is one that has <strong>at least</strong> <strong>3 antenatal visits</strong> with two visits in the third trimester.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9773","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about​ parity?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about parity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parity refers to the numbe‌r of previous pregnancies​ reaching viability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If a woman has twins, the ‌parity remains one","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If a woman has a stillborn‌ child, her parity decrea​ses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multipara is a woman who h‌as completed 2 or more pr​egnancies past the period​ of viability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. If a woman has a stillborn child, her parity decreases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. If a woman has a stillborn child, her parity decreases</strong></p><p>If a female has stillbirth, her parity does not decrease. </p>\n<p><strong>Parity </strong>is determined by the<strong> number of previous pregnancies </strong>reaching a period of<strong> viability</strong>, which is 28 weeks in India. </p>\n<p><strong>Multifetal</strong> pregnancies, such as twins or triplets,<strong> count as one</strong> for the purpose of this notation. The<strong> current pregnancy</strong> is<strong> not included </strong>in this count.</p>\n<p><strong>Multipara</strong> refers to a woman who has completed <strong>two</strong> <strong>or more</strong> pregnancies past the period of <strong>viability</strong>. When the pregnancy is preceded by<strong> five </strong>or more previous viable pregnancies, the woman is called a <strong>grand multipara</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Gravida</strong> indicates the <strong>number of times</strong> the mother has been <strong>pregnant</strong>, regardless of whether these pregnancies were carried to term. The<strong> current pregnancy</strong> is<strong> included </strong>in this count. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1446","text":"A 30-year-old woman came t‌o the OPD with a twin pre​gnancy at 10 weeks of ges​tation. She has a 3-year-​old female child who was ‍born at term and had 2 fi​rst trimester abortions p​reviously. Which of the f​ollowing is the correct r​epresentation of her obst‌etric score.","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman came to the OPD with a twin pregnancy at 10 weeks of gestation. She has a 3-year-old female child who was born at term and had 2 first trimester abortions previously. Which of the following is the correct representation of her obstetric score.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G4 P2‌ L1 A2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G4 P1‌ A2 L1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"G5 P0‌ L1 A2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"G5 P1‌ A2 L1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. G4 P1 A2 L1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. G4 P1 A2 L1</strong></p><p>The obstetric score is G4 P1 A2 L1. </p>\n<p>The obstetric score is used to summarise the past obstetric history as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G - <strong>Gravidity, </strong>denoting the <strong>total</strong> number of <strong>pregnancies</strong>, including the present one</li>\n<li>P - <strong>Parity,</strong><strong> </strong>denoting the total number of <strong>pregnancies</strong> crossing the period of <strong>viability</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>This includes both live births and stillbirths</li>\n<li>The current pregnancy is not included</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A - Abortus or the number of pregnancies that were lost for any reason, including induced abortions or miscarriages.</li>\n<li>L - Living children</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Multifetal</strong> pregnancies<strong> count as one </strong>for both gravida and para. A pregnant woman who delivers twins will be expressed as primipara.</p>\n<p>The GTPAL system includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gravida</li>\n<li>Term (&gt;37 weeks)</li>\n<li>Preterm (20w - 36w 6d)</li>\n<li>Abortions (&lt;20 weeks)</li>\n<li>Number of living children</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The<strong> period of viability</strong> is when the fetus is capable of independent existence. The <strong>WHO</strong> defines it to be <strong>24 weeks</strong> while the <strong>Indian</strong> definition is <strong>28 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0692","text":"A lady who is 38 weeks pre‌gnant comes to the OPD fo​r a routine checkup. She ​has a history of normal t​win delivery at term 4 ye‍ars ago. What is her grav​ida and para score?","raw_text":"A lady who is 38 weeks pregnant comes to the OPD for a routine checkup. She has a history of normal twin delivery at term 4 years ago. What is her gravida and para score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G2‌P2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G2‌P1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"G3‌P2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"G3‌P1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. G2P1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. G2P1</strong></p><p>The woman has had <strong>two pregnancies in total</strong> - the previous twin pregnancy and the current pregnancy, hence the <strong>gravida score is 2</strong>. The <strong>first pregnancy</strong> resulted in delivery at <strong>term</strong> (crossed viable period), hence her <strong>para score is 1</strong> (parity <strong>doesn't include current pregnancy</strong>).  Hence, her obstetric score would be <strong>G2P1</strong> (Gravida - 2, Para - 1). </p>\n<p><strong>Multifetal pregnancies</strong> <strong>count as one</strong> for both gravida and para. A pregnant woman who delivers twins for the first time is expressed as primipara.</p>\n<p>The obstetric score is used to summarise the past obstetric history as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G - <strong>Gravidity</strong>, denoting the <strong>total</strong> number of <strong>pregnancies</strong>, including the present one</li>\n<li>P - <strong>Parity</strong>,<strong> </strong>denoting the total number of <strong>pregnancies</strong> crossing the period of <strong>viability</strong>. This includes both live births and stillbirths. The current pregnancy is not included. </li>\n<li>A - Abortion is the termination of pregnancy, either spontaneous or induced, <strong>before fetal viability</strong>.</li>\n<li>L - <strong>Living children.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>GTPAL</strong> system includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>G</strong>ravida</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>erm (&gt;37 weeks)</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>reterm (20w - 36w 6d)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>bortions (&lt;20 weeks)</li>\n<li>Number of <strong>l</strong>iving children</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The period of <strong>viability</strong> is when the fetus is capable of independent existence. The <strong>WHO</strong> defines it to be <strong>24 weeks</strong> while the <strong>Indian</strong> definition is <strong>28 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9118","text":"A woman whose obstetric sc‌ore is G2 P1 L1 had an LM​P of Jan 9th 2017. Calcul​ate the EDD according to ​Naegele’s rule.","raw_text":"A woman whose obstetric score is G2 P1 L1 had an LMP of Jan 9th 2017. Calculate the EDD according to Naegele’s rule.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septem‌ber 19","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Octob‌er 19","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Octob‌er 16","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Septem‌ber 16","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. October 16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. October 16</strong></p><p>As per Naegele’s rule, the EDD (expected date of delivery) of this woman is<strong> October 16</strong>, 2017.</p>\n<p><strong>Naegele’s rule</strong> states that EDD is obtained by <strong>adding</strong> <strong>7 days </strong>and<strong> subtracting 3 months </strong>from the LMP (the 1st day of the last menstrual period). Another method is adding 9 months and 7 days to LMP. Less than 5% of births follow Naegele's rule. </p>\n<p>It is based on a 28-day regular cycle. If the cycle is shorter/longer than 28 days, <strong>corrected EDD</strong> is calculated as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For a 35-day cycle, add another 7 (35-28=7) days to the calculated EDD to get the corrected EDD.</li>\n<li>For a 25-day cycle, subtract 3 (28-25=3) days from the calculated EDD to get the corrected EDD.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Naegele’s rule cannot be followed in IVF pregnancies. Considering the human embryo takes 266 days to develop, <strong>EDD is calculated in IVF pregnancies</strong> by the following method:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Same day as fertilization (day 0)</strong>: EDD = date of transfer + 266 days </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>3 days after fertilization (day 3)</strong>: EDD = date of transfer + 263 days</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>5 days after fertilization (day 5)</strong>: EDD = date of transfer + 261 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1509","text":"The recommended calorie in‌take for a pregnant woman​ in the second trimester,​ who is a sedentary worke​r would increase by _____‍__.","raw_text":"The recommended calorie intake for a pregnant woman in the second trimester, who is a sedentary worker would increase by _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"600 kc‌al/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"520 kc‌al/day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"450 kc‌al/day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"350 kc‌al/day","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 350 kcal/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 350 kcal/day</strong></p><p>The recommended calorie intake for a pregnant woman in the second trimester would increase by ~ <strong>350 kcal/day</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9165","text":"A pregnant lady of 49 kg i‌s in her first trimester.​ What are her additional ​calorie requirements in t​his trimester?","raw_text":"A pregnant lady of 49 kg is in her first trimester. What are her additional calorie requirements in this trimester?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌00","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌00","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continue ‌same diet","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6‌00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Continue same diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Continue same diet</strong></p><p>The pregnant lady can <strong>continue the same diet</strong> since there are no additional calorie requirements in the <strong>first trimester.</strong></p>\n<p>Pregnancy requires an additional 80,000 kcal, mostly during the last 20 weeks. To meet this demand, a caloric increase of 100 to 300 kcal/d is recommended during pregnancy. This greater intake, however, should not be divided equally during the course of pregnancy. The Institute of Medicine (2006) recommends adding <strong>0, 340, and 452 kcal/d</strong> to the estimated nonpregnant energy requirements in the<strong> first, second, and third trimesters</strong> respectively. </p>\n<p>Whenever caloric intake is inadequate, protein is metabolized rather than being spared for its vital role in fetal growth and development.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0822","text":"A 22-year-old pregnant wom‌an at 13 weeks of gestati​on informs you about acci​dental exposure to radiat​ion from a chest X-ray at‍ 7 weeks of gestation. Wh​at should be the next ste​p advised to her?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old pregnant woman at 13 weeks of gestation informs you about accidental exposure to radiation from a chest X-ray at 7 weeks of gestation. What should be the next step advised to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue ‌pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Terminate‌ pregancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pre-invasive diagnostic te‌st","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromosome‌ screening","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Continue pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Continue pregnancy</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In the given clinical case, the patient should be advised to <strong>continue the pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The radiation dose to the fetus during the declared term of pregnancy should be <strong>less than 1 mSv</strong>. In <strong>chest X-ray</strong> PA view, the absorbed dose of radiation by the fetus is less than 0.1 mSv. Hence, pregnancy can be continued.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">An X-ray has a very limited role in obstetric imaging. There are very few indications where an X-ray will be useful: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Fetal: congenital malformation of the fetus and neonates, skeletal malformations, birth injuries like fractures or dislocations.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Maternal:  Patients having cardiopulmonary disease may require an X-ray chest during pregnancy and that should be done beyond 12 weeks. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Radiation can cause fetal morbidity (FGR, genetic mutations, neurologic abnormalities, mental retardation, childhood leukemia) and mortality. The risks are high with radiation after the first 2 weeks postconception and within the first trimester (period of organogenesis).</p>\n<p>Radiation during pregnancy can damage the early phase of the embryo but the risk can be minimized by using the <strong>‘10-day rule’.</strong> This rule states that no woman should be exposed to an X-ray for a nonurgent indication outside 10 days from her last period during the reproductive period unless the pregnancy is excluded. </p>\n<p>The given table below shows the absorbed radiation by the fetus in different diagnostic radiation procedures:</p>\n<p><strong>1 cGy = 10 mSv</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 345.977px; width: 29.9456%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>Procedure </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>Dose (cGy)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Abdominal X-ray</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">0.1-0.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Pelvic X-ray</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">0.5-1.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Chest X-ray </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">&lt;0.001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Abdominal CT </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">0.4-0.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Chest CT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">0.002-0.02</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Abdominopelvic CT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">2.5-3.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 44.7969px;\"><br/>Ventilation scan</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 44.7969px;\">0.007-0.05</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 57.5688%; height: 10px;\"><br/>PET scan</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7667%; height: 10px;\">1-1.5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9580","text":"You notice an obstetrics r‌esident performing the fo​llowing manoeuvres on a 3​7-week primigravida. Choo​se the correctly matched ‍set. First Leopold Second​ Leopold Third Leopold Fo​urth Leopold","raw_text":"<p>You notice an obstetrics resident performing the following manoeuvres on a 37-week primigravida. Choose the correctly matched set.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>First Leopold</li>\n<li>Second Leopold</li>\n<li>Third Leopold</li>\n<li>Fourth Leopold</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-c,‌ 3-b, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-a, 2-b,‌ 3-c, 4-d","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-b, 2-a,‌ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-b,‌ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b885e649fff04f0bbadb36456c9baf42.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e215ce79339c433da605c48ea10b4cc7x600x3241.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d</strong></p><p>Fetal pole, lie, presenting part, engagement, and attitude of fetal head are assessed by <strong>Leopold's manoeuvres:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fundal grip </strong>(first Leopold):\n<ul>\n<li>Assesses the uterine <strong>fundus</strong>.</li>\n<li>Helps in identification of <strong>fetal lie</strong> and determination of <strong>fetal pole</strong> at the <strong>fundus</strong>.</li>\n<li>The<strong> </strong>breech<strong> </strong>gives the sensation of a<strong> </strong>large, irregular mass. </li>\n<li>The<strong> </strong>head<strong> </strong>feels<strong> </strong>hard and round<strong> </strong>and is<strong> </strong>more mobile.<strong><br/><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral grip </strong>(second Leopold): \n<ul>\n<li>Assesses the fetal<strong> orientation</strong>.</li>\n<li>The palms are placed on either side of the maternal abdomen, and gentle but deep pressure is exerted.</li>\n<li>The <strong>back</strong> is felt on one side as a <strong>hard</strong>, resistant structure.</li>\n<li>The <strong>fetal extremities</strong> are felt on the other side as <strong>numerous</strong> small, irregular, <strong>mobile parts</strong> are felt.</li>\n<li>Fetal orientation can be determined by noting whether the back is directed anteriorly, transversely, or posteriorly.</li>\n<li>The <strong>position </strong>of the<strong> anterior shoulder</strong> is mainly assessed by this grip.<br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pawlik's grip </strong>/ 1st pelvic grip (third Leopold):\n<ul>\n<li>Assesses the <strong>presenting</strong> <strong>part</strong> in respect to the true pelvis.</li>\n<li>The thumb and fingers of one hand grasp the lower portion of the maternal abdomen just above the symphysis pubis.</li>\n<li>If the presenting part is not engaged, a movable mass will be felt, usually the head.<br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Deep pelvic grip </strong>/ 2nd pelvic grip (fourth Leopold):\n<ul>\n<li>Assesses the <strong>engagement,</strong> attitude of the fetal head and determine the <strong>degree of descent</strong>.</li>\n<li>The examiner faces the mother's feet, and the fingertips of both hands are positioned on either side of the presenting part.</li>\n<li>They exert inward pressure and then slide along the axis of the pelvic inlet.</li>\n<li>If the head has already descended into the pelvis, the anterior shoulder or the space created by the neck may be differentiated readily from the head.</li>\n<li>It is the only grip performed <strong>facing</strong> the <strong>feet</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1735","text":"A dusky hue of the vestibu‌le and anterior vaginal w​all visible at about 8 we​eks of pregnancy is calle​d _____.","raw_text":"A dusky hue of the vestibule and anterior vaginal wall visible at about 8 weeks of pregnancy is called _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hegar'‌s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chadwic‌k's sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osiande‌r's sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmer‌'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chadwick's sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chadwick&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>A <strong>dusky hue</strong> of the <strong>vestibule and anterior vaginal wall</strong> visible at about 8th week of pregnancy is called <strong>Chadwick’s sign</strong> or <strong>Jacquemier’s sign</strong>. The discoloration is due to local vascular congestion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9789","text":"A primigravida comes for a‌ routine ANC visit at 22 ​weeks of gestation. Which​ of the following signs w​ould you not expect?","raw_text":"A primigravida comes for a routine ANC visit at 22 weeks of gestation. Which of the following signs would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlo‌asma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quick‌ening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Light‌ening","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Braxton Hick's contraction‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lightening","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lightening</strong></p><p><strong>Lightening</strong> is felt in the <strong>3rd trimester</strong> (38th week) in a primigravida. It is a sense of relief from the pressure symptoms due to engagement of the presenting part.</p>\n<p>The <strong>2nd trimester</strong> of pregnancy is characterised by the following: </p>\n<table style=\"width: 729px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p><strong>Sign</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p><strong>Seen at</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Chloasma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p>Pigmentation over forehead and cheeks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>24 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Breast changes</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p>Appearance of secondary areola.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>20 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Uterine changes- size and Braxton Hick contractions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p>Irregular, infrequent spasmodic and painless contractions without any effect on dilatation of cervix.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>Begin in early pregnancy and continue till term</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Palpation of fetal parts and active fetal movements</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>20 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Ballotment of uterus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p>Feeling of floating object inside uterus on bimanual examination. Not exclusive to pregancy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>16 to 20 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Auscultation of fetal heart sounds</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p>Most <strong>conclusive</strong> sign of pregnancy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>18 to 20 weeks by stethoscope and 10 weeks by doppler</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Quickening</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<p>Feeling of life inside womb</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">\n<p>16 to 20 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9779","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true regar​ding uterine souffle?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding uterine souffle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a soft blowing systo‌lic murmur heard on the s​ides of pregnant uterus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The sound is synchronous w‌ith the maternal pulse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is due to increased blo‌od flow through the umbil​ical arteries","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It can also be heard in a ‌large fibroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is due to increased blood flow through the umbilical arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is due to increased blood flow through the umbilical arteries</strong></p><p><strong>Uterine souffle</strong> is due to an increase in blood flow through the <strong>dilated uterine vessels,</strong> not umbilical vessels.</p>\n<p>It is a soft blowing systolic murmur heard low down at the sides of the uterus, better heard on the left side.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 471px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p><strong>Uterine Souffle</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p><strong>Fetal/Funic Souffle</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Soft, blowing sound</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Sharp, whistling sound</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Synchronous with <strong>maternal pulse</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Synchronous with <strong>fetal pulse.</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>Caused by the passage of blood through dilated <strong>uterine vessels</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>Caused by the rush of blood through the <strong>umbilical arteries</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224px;\">\n<p>It can also be heard in a large uterine fibroid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>When present it is <strong>diagnostic of pregnancy</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1477","text":"A 32-year-old woman nearin‌g her due date was admitt​ed following regular cont​ractions. You note that t​he head is at +1 station.‍ Where is the exact posit​ion of the head?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman nearing her due date was admitted following regular contractions. You note that the head is at +1 station. Where is the exact position of the head?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At the ‌perineum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Just below i‌schial spine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Just above i‌schial spine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High up in the false pelvi‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Just below ischial spine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05defb3ad0774d3ea6a0792048ad719ax1200x1287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Just below ischial spine</strong></p><p>+1 station of the fetal head refers to it being <strong>just below</strong> the <strong>ischial spine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The station of the head is expressed in relation to ischial spines as they are the most prominent bony projections felt on internal examination. The level of <strong>ischial spines</strong> is halfway between the pelvic inlet and outlet. This level is known as <strong>station zero</strong> (0).</p>\n<p>The station is stated in <strong>minus</strong> figures if it is <strong>above</strong> the spines (–1 cm, –2 cm, –3 cm, –4 cm and –5 cm) and in <strong>plus</strong> figures if it is <strong>below</strong> the spines (+1 cm, +2 cm, +3 cm, +4 cm and +5 cm).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9606","text":"While examining a pregnant‌ woman, you measure her f​undal height to be 32 cm ​and the station of the fe​tal head is at -2. What c‍ould be the probable weig​ht of the fetus?","raw_text":"While examining a pregnant woman, you measure her fundal height to be 32 cm and the station of the fetal head is at -2. What could be the probable weight of the fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.‌3Kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3.‌5Kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3.‌1Kg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4.‌0Kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3.1Kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae395dfdcef14bedb1eec4ec4edde0ffx1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3.1Kg</strong></p><p>The fetal weight in the above case is<strong> 3.1Kg.</strong></p>\n<p>In the above case, fetal weight can be estimated using <strong>Johnson's formula</strong>. Height of uterus is 32cm, the station is –2 implying the head is above the ischial spines. Therefore, it is calculated as follows: [32-12] x 155 = 3100 grams = 3.1Kg</p>\n<p>Estimation of fetal weight:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Johnson’s formula: </strong>This is only applicable in vertex presentation.\n<ul>\n<li>If station of head is <strong>below</strong> the level of the ischial spine: [Height of uterus above pubic symphysis (in cm)<strong>-11</strong>] x 155 in grams</li>\n<li>If station of head is <strong>above</strong> the level of the ischial spine: [Height of uterus above pubic symphysis (in cm)<strong>-12</strong>] x 155 in grams</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sonography:</strong> Fetal weight has been estimated by combining a number of biometric data, e.g. biparietal diameter (BPD), head circumference (HC), abdominal circumference (AC) and femur length (FL), using the following formulae:\n<ul>\n<li>Shepard’s formula</li>\n<li>Hadlock’s formula.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9795","text":"All of the following are c‌omponents of Manning scor​e except ______.","raw_text":"All of the following are components of Manning score except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non stress‌ test (NST)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxytocin ch‌allenge test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal bod‌y movement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory activity of fe‌tus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oxytocin challenge test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oxytocin challenge test</strong></p><p>The oxytocin challenge test is not a component of Manning score. </p>\n<p>Earlier, oxytocin challenge test/contraction stress test was done after a non-reactive NST to confirm the diagnosis of hypoxia. However, it is associated with the risk of initiating labor and is hence not done.</p>\n<p>Biophysical profile <strong>(BPP)/Manning score </strong>components: (Mnemonic: BATMaN)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breathing movements of the fetus</li>\n<li>Amniotic fluid voulme</li>\n<li>Tone of fetus</li>\n<li>Movements of fetus</li>\n<li>Non-stress Test (NST)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Normal score: 8-10; Abnormal: &lt;5</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Biophysical score:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>VAST (Vibroacoustic stimulation test)/NST</li>\n<li>Amniotic fluid index</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A score &lt;4 indicates the need for urgent delivery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5687","text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 woman p‌resents to the antenatal ​clinic at 24 weeks for a ​routine check-up. USG sho​ws a normal for gestation‍al age fetus in frank bre​ech position, with no oth​er abnormalities. What is​ the next best step in ​m​anagement?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 woman presents to the antenatal clinic at 24 weeks for a routine check-up. USG shows a normal for gestational age fetus in frank breech position, with no other abnormalities. What is the next best step in ​management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"75gm oral glucose toleranc‌e test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Non-str‌ess test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External cep‌halic version","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immediate lower segment Ca‌esarean section","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 75gm oral glucose tolerance test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 75gm oral glucose tolerance test</strong></p><p><strong>75gm </strong>oral glucose tolerance test (<strong>OGTT</strong>)<strong> </strong>is the most appropriate next step of management. The <strong>screening</strong> strategy for detecting gestational diabetes mellitus (<strong>GDM</strong>) recommends performing blood glucose testing at <strong>24-28 weeks</strong> in case of average risk.</p>\n<p>Breech presentation at 24 weeks is not a cause for concern because it can spontaneously rotate and becomes cephalic at term. Hence, external cephalic version (ECV) and immediate caesarean section are not indicated at this time.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9793","text":"Which of the following vac‌cines is absolutely contr​aindicated in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following vaccines is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌MR","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MMR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MMR</strong></p><p><strong>MMR</strong> is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy. </p>\n<p><strong>Killed vaccines</strong> are <strong>safe</strong> in pregnancy while <strong>live vaccines</strong> (MMR, BCG, varicella) are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in pregnancy. </p>\n<p>Even though yellow fever vaccine is a live vaccine, it is recommended in endemic areas or during outbreaks. Pregnant women and nursing mothers should be counselled on the potential benefits and risks of vaccination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1537","text":"A newly married couple pla‌nning for pregnancy has c​ome to your clinic for pr​econception counselling. ​Which of the following dr‍ugs will you prescribe to​ the patient to decrease ​the chances of having neu​ral tube defects in the b​aby?","raw_text":"A newly married couple planning for pregnancy has come to your clinic for preconception counselling. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe to the patient to decrease the chances of having neural tube defects in the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‌in B9","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam‌in B12","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‌min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam‌in B6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin B9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin B9</strong></p><p>Preconceptional intake of <strong>vitamin B9 (folic acid)</strong> results in decreased incidence of neural tube defects like spina bifida, encephalocele, and anencephaly.</p>\n<p>All women of reproductive age should be given <strong>400 µg</strong> of folic acid daily.  Women, who had fetal neural tube defects in the previous pregnancy, should be given <strong>4 mg</strong> of folic acid daily beginning 1 month before conception to about 12 weeks of pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5793","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions would not cause r​aised beta-hCG levels in ​pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions would not cause raised beta-hCG levels in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Down's ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multifetal‌ pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spontaneou‌s abortion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rh-incomp‌atibility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spontaneous abortion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spontaneous abortion</strong></p><p>Beta-hCG levels are <strong>reduced</strong> in the case of spontaneous abortion.</p>\n<p>Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is <strong>detectable</strong> in the plasma of pregnant women as early as 7-8 days after conception by radioimmunoassay. It <strong>doubles every 2 days</strong> and reaches a <strong>maximum</strong> level at <strong>9 weeks</strong>. Thereafter, it declines to reach the lower level at 20 weeks and disappears from circulation 2 weeks postpartum.</p>\n<p><strong>Increased levels</strong> of beta-hCG are found in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple preganancies</li>\n<li>Rh isoimmunization</li>\n<li>Down's syndrome</li>\n<li>Choriocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Hydatidiform mole</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Decreased levels</strong> of beta-hCG are found in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ectopic pregnancies</li>\n<li>Impending spontaneous abortion</li>\n<li>Missed abortion</li>\n<li>Edward syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4479","text":"Which of the following is ‌not considered as a facto​r for a high-risk pregnan​cy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not considered as a factor for a high-risk pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyhyd‌ramnios","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ana‌emia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Previous manual removal of‌ placenta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Previous manual removal of placenta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Previous manual removal of placenta</strong></p><p>Previous manual removal of the placenta does not indicate a high-risk pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The <strong>high-risk pregnancies</strong> according to the Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System by the CDC are<strong>:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 446px; width: 615.283px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\"><strong>Current pregnancy complications</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"><strong>Obstetric history</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\">Other disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p>Preterm labor (&lt;37 Wks) and Post-term (&gt; 42 wk)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">\n<p>Intrauterine growth restriction</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Cardiovascular  </strong>factors<strong> </strong>like chronic hypertension, severe heart failure, and<strong> renal</strong> disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Preterm premature rupture of membranes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Stillbirth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Metabolic </strong>disorders<strong> </strong>like Obesity,<br/>Insulin-dependent diabetes, Previous endocrine ablation, Thyroid disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Moderate to severe preeclampsia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Late abortion (16–20 wk)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Hematological</strong> problems like sickle cell disease, severe anemia<br/>Thrombophilia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Polyhydramnios (hydramnios) or oligohydramnios</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Fetal exchange transfusion (Rh incompatibility)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Infections </strong>(malaria, HIV) and <strong>Autoimmune</strong> disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Uterine rupture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Multifetal pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Epilepsy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Antepartum hemorrhage (Placenta previa, and Abruptio placenta) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Previous brachial plexus injury</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Anatomic</strong> abnormalities<strong> </strong>like uterine malformation<br/>and cervical incompetence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Fetal tachycardia or bradycardia for  &gt;30 min</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Abnormal fetal position</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\">Exposure to<strong> teratogens </strong>like alcohol, drugs, smoking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Fetal weight &lt; 2.5 kg or &gt;4kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Previous h/o myomectomy, repair of complete perineal tear, vesicovaginal fistula, and stress incontinence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Family</strong> history of diabetes, hypertension or <br/>multiple pregnancy and congenital malformation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Chorioamnionitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"><strong>Low</strong> socioeconomic status</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Prolapsed cord</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\">Abnormal cervical cytologic findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Fetal acidosis pH ≤7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.283px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"j8cKNJV9","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"7_basics_of_cornea_and_infectious_keratitis","title":"7.Basics Of Cornea And Infectious Keratitis","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA9705","text":"What is the corneal epithe‌lium normally composed of‍?","raw_text":"What is the corneal epithelium normally composed of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratified keratinized epi‌thelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratified non-keratinized‌ epithelium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Columnar ‌epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudostratified epitheliu‌m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stratified non-keratinized epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64c9d0650104451f93a76ed45bf371e7x1200x1471.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stratified non-keratinized epithelium</strong></p><p><strong>The corneal epithelium</strong> is made up of <strong>stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Layers of the cornea:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Epithelium </strong>(Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium)</li>\n<li><strong>Bowman’s</strong> <strong>membrane - </strong>Acellular; does<strong> not regenerate</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stroma (Thickest layer) - </strong>The stroma can scar, but <strong>cannot regenerate</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dua’s</strong> layer (<strong>Newly discovered, toughest layer</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Descemet’s</strong> membrane- Ends peripherally as <strong>Schwalbe’s ring; </strong>has<strong> regenerative</strong> potential</li>\n<li><strong>Endothelium </strong>(<strong>Most metabolically active layer</strong>)- does <strong>not regenerate</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong> <strong>Limbal stem cells </strong>are located at the corneoscleral limbus, in the palisades of Vogt. Deficiency may result in chronic epithelial defects and <strong>\"conjunctivalization\"</strong> of the cornea</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3872","text":"Which of the following lay‌ers is responsible for ma‍intaining the hydration a​nd transparency of the co‍rnea?","raw_text":"Which of the following layers is responsible for maintaining the hydration and transparency of the cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descemet'‌s membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endothel‌ial cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Str‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal e‌pithelium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endothelial cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endothelial cells</strong></p><p>The hydration level and transparency of the cornea is maintained mainly by the <strong>endothelial cells </strong>of the cornea. </p>\n<p>The endothelium possesses <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> ATPase,</strong> which keeps the corneal stroma in<strong> </strong>a relatively<strong> dehydrated state</strong>. This helps maintain corneal transparency. <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Endothelial cells decrease in number with age. If the cornea becomes overhydrated, it begins to scatter light and gradually begins to lose its transparency</span></p>\n<p>Other factors contributing to the transparency of the cornea are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regular arrangement of corneal lamellae (<strong>Maurice theory</strong>)</li>\n<li>The peculiar refractive index of corneal lamellae (<strong>Goldmann theory</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Avascularity </strong>of the cornea</li>\n<li>A balance between the intraocular pressure (<strong>IOP</strong>) and <strong>swelling pressure</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 31\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The different layers of the cornea are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelium</li>\n<li>Bowman’s membrane</li>\n<li>Stroma (substantia propria)</li>\n<li>Dua’s layer (pre-Descemet’s layer)</li>\n<li>Descemet’s membrane</li>\n<li>Endothelium</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9707","text":"What is the average densit‌y of corneal endothelial ‍cells?","raw_text":"What is the average density of corneal endothelial cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2000–2500‌ cells/mm²","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1000–1500‌ cells/mm²","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3500–4000‌ cells/mm²","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2500–3000‌ cells/mm²","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2500–3000 cells/mm²","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2500–3000 cells/mm²</strong></p><p>The density of corneal endothelial cells in <strong>adults</strong> is about <strong>2500–3000 cells/mm². </strong></p>\n<p>Variation in corneal endothelial density with age: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>At birth-</strong> 3,500–4,000 cells/mm<sup>2</sup> at birth</li>\n<li><strong>Young adults-</strong> 2500–3000 cells/mm²</li>\n<li><strong>Old age (about 80 years)-</strong> 2000 cells/mm²</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Corneas stored in eye banks are assessed for their quality and viability using a variety of parameters. Donor tissues are screened for stress lines, mechanical trauma, scarring, corneal edema, and infiltrates that could indicate poor tissue quality. They are further evaluated for thickness and endothelial cell density (a surrogate for endothelial function) which are important determinants of successful graft transplantation.</p>\n<p>Endothelial cell density (ECD) and morphology are assessed using a <strong>specular microscope</strong> or by a bright-field/phase-contrast light microscope.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The number of cells decreases at about 0.6% per year, and the neighboring cells enlarge to fill the space as the cells cannot regenerate. At a density of about 500 cells/mm<sup><span class=\"s1\">2</span></sup><span class=\"s1\">,</span><sup><span class=\"s1\"> </span></sup>corneal edema develops and transparency is impaired. This is known as critical endothelial density.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1089","text":"Below what horizontal diam‌eter can a diagnosis of m‍icro-cornea be made?","raw_text":"Below what horizontal diameter can a diagnosis of micro-cornea be made?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 ‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9 ‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10‌ mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"11‌ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10 mm</strong></p><p>To make a diagnosis of <strong>microcornea,</strong> in this child, the <strong>diameter</strong> of the cornea should be <strong>less than 10 mm.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Micro-cornea</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Horizontal corneal diameter is <strong>10 mm or less</strong> (over 2 years of age) or <strong>less than 9 mm </strong>(in the <strong>newborn</strong>).</li>\n<li>There may be <strong>hypermetropia</strong> and a <strong>shallow anterior chamber</strong> but other dimensions are normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Megalocornea</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Horizontal corneal diameter is <strong>12 mm or more</strong> (<strong>up to 2</strong> years of age) or <strong>13 mm or more</strong> (if age <strong>&gt;2</strong> years)</li>\n<li>There is typically <strong>high</strong> <strong>myopia</strong> and <strong>astigmatism</strong>, but normal corrected visual acuity.</li>\n<li>Usually <strong>X-linked recessive; 90%</strong> of affected individuals are <strong>male</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cornea plana</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The cornea is<strong> flatter than normal</strong>, i.e., the radius of curvature is larger.</li>\n<li>There is a corresponding reduction in refractive power, resulting in <strong>high hypermetropia. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Keratectasia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is characterized by a<strong> protuberance between the eyelids</strong> caused by a <strong>severely opacified,</strong> and sometimes <strong>vascularized, cornea</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is often associated with<strong> raised intraocular pressure </strong>as well as<strong> intrauterine keratitis.</strong></li>\n<li>Usually<strong> unilateral</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Microphthalmos </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microphthalmia is a condition in which the <strong>entire eye is small</strong>, with an <strong>axial length</strong> at least <strong>two standard deviations</strong> <strong>below</strong> the mean for age.</li>\n<li>It may or may not be associated with ocular dysgenesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nanophthalmos</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In nanophthalmos (<strong>simple microphthalmos</strong>), the <strong>entire eye is small,</strong> but is <strong>structurally normal</strong>;<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>It is usually associated with <strong>hypermetropia</strong> and <strong>strabismus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anophthalmos</strong>:<strong> </strong>Anophthalmos (anophthalmia) refers to the <strong>complete absence</strong> of any visible globe structure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9709","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms are capable of inv‍ading the intact cornea?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms are capable of invading the intact cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonoc‌occus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumo‌coccus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseud‌omonas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gonococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gonococcus</strong></p><p><em><strong>Gonococcus</strong></em> can invade an intact cornea.</p>\n<p>Organisms that can penetrate the <strong>intact epithelium</strong> of the cornea can be remembered with the mnemonic- <strong>CHaNNeLS</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>C</strong>orynebacterium diphtheria</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>Ha</strong>emophilus influenza;Haemophilus aegypticus</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>Ne</strong>isseria gonorrhea; Neisseria meningitidis</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>L</strong>isteria</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>S</strong>higella</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9710","text":"A patient with a history o‌f uncomplicated cataract ‍surgery 1 year back, pres​ents with pain, redness, ‍and purulent discharge fr​om his right eye. A greyi‌sh-white serpiginous ulce‌r was noted over the lowe‍r third of the cornea. Th​e anterior chamber showed‌ a 4 mm hypopyon and a mo​derate flare reaction. A ‌smear from the ulcerated ‍area is likely to demonst​rate which of the followi‌ng organisms?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of uncomplicated cataract surgery 1 year back, presents with pain, redness, and purulent discharge from his right eye. A greyish-white serpiginous ulcer was noted over the lower third of the cornea. The anterior chamber showed a 4 mm hypopyon and a moderate flare reaction. A smear from the ulcerated area is likely to demonstrate which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseud‌omonas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo‌coccus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coryneb‌acteria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gonoc‌occus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pneumococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb7f8fdbd4954b6c9b84f459a3768afax450x309.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pneumococcus</strong></p><p><strong>Hypopyon corneal ulcer</strong> with a characteristic <strong>serpiginous</strong> description (ulcus serpens) is typically caused by<em> <strong>Pneumococcus</strong></em>. The bacterial hypopyon is <strong>sterile</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ulcus serpens is a <strong>greyish-white</strong> or <strong>yellowish</strong> <strong>disc-shaped</strong> ulcer occurring near the centre of the cornea. The ulcer has a tendency to creep over the cornea in a <strong>serpiginous fashion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Risk factors include both surgical and non-surgical ocular trauma, dry eye, chronic corneal disease, immune alterations and contact lenses.</p>\n<p>An examination may reveal the following signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Swelling of lids and blepharospasm</li>\n<li>Conjunctival hyperemia</li>\n<li>Ciliary congestion, hypopyon and anterior chamber flare reaction</li>\n<li>Iridocyclitis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image given below shows ulcus serpens.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1049","text":"A young woman presents wit‌h sudden onset, rapidly p‍rogressing ocular pain, p​hotophobia, and blurred v‍ision in her right eye fo​r 1 day. A deep, 4 mm dia‌meter corneal ulcer, surr‌ounded by stromal infiltr‍ates was visualized. The ​anterior chamber has smal‌l amounts of mucopurulent​ discharge. She mentions ‌using a cosmetic contact ‍lens which she forgot to ​remove before sleeping 2 ‌days ago. What is the mos‌t likely etiology?","raw_text":"A young woman presents with sudden onset, rapidly progressing ocular pain, photophobia, and blurred vision in her right eye for 1 day.  A deep, 4 mm diameter corneal ulcer, surrounded by stromal infiltrates was visualized. The anterior chamber has small amounts of mucopurulent discharge. She mentions using a cosmetic contact lens which she forgot to remove before sleeping 2 days ago. What is the most likely etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseud‌omonas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asper‌gillus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudomonas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudomonas</strong></p><p>The sudden onset and rapid progression of ocular symptoms and ulceration in this patient along with the history of improper <strong>contact lens</strong> usage are suggestive of a <strong>Pseudomonas</strong> infection. </p>\n<p><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> is the<strong> most common gram-negative organism isolated from corneal ulcers</strong> and is a frequent cause of <strong>contact lens–associated keratitis.</strong> Other risk factors include chlorinated swimming pools, hot tubs, ventilators, nebulizer, and vaporizer solutions, and ophthalmic solution bottles.</p>\n<p>Pseudomonas keratitis has a rapid and fulminant course if left untreated and are associated with a worse visual prognosis than that caused by most other common bacterial pathogens. <strong><em>Pseudomonas</em> spp.</strong> can cause corneal perforation in just <strong>48 hours</strong>. Treatment is mainly by topical NSAIDs and topical antipseudomonal antibiotic eye drops such as tobramycin 1.3% and fluoroquinolone eye drops. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9713","text":"A young woman comes with s‌udden onset ocular pain, ‍tearing, and photophobia ​in the left eye. You susp‍ect a bacterial corneal u​lcer. What concentration ‌of cephazolin drops would‌ you prescribe for this p‍atient?","raw_text":"A young woman comes with sudden onset ocular pain, tearing, and photophobia in the left eye. You suspect a bacterial corneal ulcer. What concentration of cephazolin drops would you prescribe for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4‌%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5‌%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5%","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ab66e42381bb46c1a4d6ac138f04a073.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5%</strong></p><p>The concentration of fortified cephazolin used to treat uncomplicated bacterial keratitis is <strong>5% ( 50 mg/mL)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of uncomplicated bacterial keratitis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antibiotic</strong> eyedrops (broad-spectrum)</li>\n<li>If not responding, fortified antibiotic eye drops are used\n<ul>\n<li>Fortified cephazolin (5%)</li>\n<li>Fortified tobramycin (1.3%)</li>\n<li>Fortified vancomycin (5%)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cycloplegic drugs-</strong> Preferably 1% atropine eye ointment or drops to prevent pain due to ciliary spasm. Also, it prevents the development of posterior synechiae.</li>\n<li><strong>Oral vitamin A and vitamin C</strong> help in the healing of the epithelium</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic analgesics</strong> and anti-inflammatory drugs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management of non-healing ulcers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Debridement</strong></li>\n<li>Chemical <strong>cauterization-</strong> 10–20% <strong>trichloroacetic acid, </strong>carbolic acid</li>\n<li>Conjunctival <strong>flapping</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management of impending perforation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decrease intraocular pressure (IOP) by antiglaucoma drugs</li>\n<li>Tissue adhesive glue such as cyanoacrylate</li>\n<li>Bandage contact lens</li>\n<li>No strain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9714","text":"A non-sterile hypopyon is ‌seen in______.","raw_text":"A non-sterile hypopyon is seen in______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumococcu‌s infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomona‌s infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fungal i‌nfection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gonoccocal‌ infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fungal infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fungal infection</strong></p><p>Fungal keratitis can cause <strong>non-sterile hypopyon.</strong></p>\n<p>Unlike bacterial ulcer, the hypopyon may not be sterile as the fungi can penetrate into the anterior chamber without perforation.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Aspergillus</strong></em> spp. are the <strong>most common cause</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypopyon in a fungal ulcer is <strong>fixed</strong>, non-sterile and contains <strong>fungal filaments.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9715","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with a history of injury‍ to the eye caused by a l​eaf 5 days ago, resulting‍ in pain, photophobia, an​d redness of the eye for ‌2 days. What would be the‌ most likely pathology?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with a history of injury to the eye caused by a leaf 5 days ago, resulting in pain, photophobia, and redness of the eye for 2 days. What would be the most likely pathology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‌ uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conjunc‌tivitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fungal cor‌neal ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal l‌aceration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fungal corneal ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c096c57f84794b37997c2853177dc660x372x302.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/938decda907d40fbb4dc1aefb10d627cx1118x1198.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fungal corneal ulcer</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of the patient presenting with <strong>pain, photophobia,</strong> and <strong>redness</strong> of the eye following <strong>trauma</strong> by a <strong>vegetative matter</strong> is suggestive of a <strong>fungal corneal ulcer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fungal corneal ulcer</strong> also known as <strong>mycotic keratitis</strong> is usually caused by trauma from vegetative matter such as <strong>branches</strong> of trees, <strong>leaves</strong> and <strong>animal tails</strong>. Injudicious use of topical steroids and antibiotics increases the predisposition to develop secondary fungal keratitis.</p>\n<p>Mycotic keratitis can be caused by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Filamentous fungi such as <strong><em>Aspergillus </em></strong>and <strong><em>Fusarium</em></strong></li>\n<li>Yeasts such as <em><strong>Candida</strong></em><em> </em>and <em>cryptococcus</em></li>\n<li>Dimorphic fungi such as Histoplasma and Blastomyces</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typically in fungal corneal ulcers, the signs are more prominent than the symptoms. The symptoms of fungal keratitis are similar to that of bacterial ulcers which include <strong>pain</strong>, <strong>lacrimation</strong>, <strong>photophobia</strong>, blurred vision, and <strong>redness</strong> of the eyes.</p>\n<p>A typical fungal ulcer has the following <strong>signs</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Greyish white looking dry corneal ulcer with rolled out margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Feathery </strong>finger-like extensions into the corneal stroma under intact epithelium</li>\n<li><strong>Wessley ring</strong> which is a <strong>sterile immune</strong> ring that occurs due to the deposition of immune complexes</li>\n<li>Multiple small <strong>satellite lesions</strong> around the ulcer</li>\n<li><strong>Hypopyon</strong>: The hypopyon in fungal ulcer is <strong>immobile</strong> and <strong>unsterile</strong> unlike bacterial ulcer (sterile hypopyon)</li>\n<li>Perforation is rare</li>\n<li>Corneal <strong>vascularization</strong> is usually <strong>absent</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>diagnosis</strong> of fungal ulcer is made by a <strong>typical history</strong> of trauma with vegetative matter followed by <strong>clinical examination</strong> and KOH mount or SDA agar cultures from corneal scrappings.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts a <strong>fungal corneal ulcer</strong> with the <strong>arrowhead</strong> indicating a <strong>satellite lesion</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Anterior uveitis</strong> is the <strong>inflammation</strong> of the uveal tissue from the <strong>iris</strong> up to the <strong>pars plicata</strong> of the ciliary body. Infectious uveitis can be caused by the exogenous entry of the organisms following penetrating injuries, perforation of corneal ulcer, and post-intaocular operations.</p>\n<p>Acute anterior uveitis presents with <strong>symptoms</strong> of pain, photophobia, lacrimation, blurring of vision and redness of the eye. <strong>Signs</strong> of anterior uveitis include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aqueous flare </strong>-  It is the <strong>earliest sign</strong> of acute anterior uveitis, it occurs following the <strong>leakage</strong> of <strong>protein particles</strong> into the <strong>aqueous humor.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Keratic precipitates(KP) </strong>- These are pathognomonic for anterior uveitis. They are proteinaceous cellular deposits at the back of the cornea arranged in a triangular fashion. These include <strong>mutton fat </strong>KP's as seen in <strong>granulomatous uveitis</strong> and <strong>granular </strong>KP's of non granulomatous<strong> uveitis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Iris nodules</strong>: typically occur in granulomatous uveitis. <strong>Koppe's</strong> nodules are at the <strong>pupillary border</strong> and <strong>Busacca's</strong> nodules are situated near the <strong>collarette.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Conjunctivitis </strong>is the <strong>inflammation </strong>of the conjunctiva of which the bacterial conjunctivitis is the most common. <strong>Acute bacterial conjunctivitis</strong> is characterized by marked <strong>conjunctival hyperemia</strong>, swelling of the conjunctiva(<strong>chemosis</strong>), edematous eyelids, sticking together of lid margins and <strong>mucopurulent </strong>discharge from the eye.</p>\n<p>The image shows a patient with <strong>bacterial conjunctivitis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Corneal laceration: </strong>Partial corneal laceration or <strong>lamellar corneal laceration</strong> occurs from a <strong>blunt trauma</strong> and is treated by patching and antibiotics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9717","text":"A farmer presents with blu‌rring of vision in his le‍ft eye. He has a history ​of injury with a bush to ‍the same eye while workin​g. An ulcer is seen in hi‌s cornea with feathery ma‌rgins. Slit-lamp examinat‍ion shows infiltrates in ​the corneal stroma in a c‌ircular fashion. Which ty​pe of hypersensitivity re‌action causes these infil‍trates?","raw_text":"A farmer presents with blurring of vision in his left eye. He has a history of injury with a bush to the same eye while working. An ulcer is seen in his cornea with feathery margins. Slit-lamp examination shows infiltrates in the corneal stroma in a circular fashion. Which type of hypersensitivity reaction causes these infiltrates?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0018bcaae4be472eae5056cf17b70f80x688x514.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 3</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation points towards a <strong>fungal corneal ulcer</strong> and the infiltrates of stroma in a circular fashion are suggestive of the <strong>immune ring of Wessely.</strong> This immune ring is due to<strong> type 3 hypersensitivity</strong> reaction (antigen-antibody complex).</p>\n<p>The <strong>immune ring of Wessely</strong> can be made out as a <strong>deep stromal haze</strong> in a slit lamp. It occurs as a result of the deposition of <strong>antigen-antibody complexes</strong> in the stroma. Once deposited it draws the immune cells, inciting further inflammation.</p>\n<p>The given shows an ulcer in the cornea and the adjacent immune ring of Wessely.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0659","text":"What is the characteristic‌ feature of a fungal ulce‍r?","raw_text":"What is the characteristic feature of a fungal ulcer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reverse ‌hypopyon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dendritic ulcer on a fluor‌escien dye","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ring a‌bscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Satellit‌e lesion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Satellite lesion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Satellite lesion</strong></p><p><strong>Satellite lesions</strong> are characteristic of fungal ulcer.</p>\n<p>The signs of a fungal ulcer include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Irregular margins of the ulcer.</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>satellite lesions</strong> surrounding the ulcer.</li>\n<li>An immune ring (Wesseley) may be seen around the ulcer due to deposition of the immune complexes.</li>\n<li>Slough is usually dry, with feathery borders surrounded by a yellow line of demarcation.</li>\n<li>Hypopyon may be present or absent. If present, it is thick, immobile due to the direct invasion of the fungal hyphae into the anterior chamber.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9727","text":"Steroids are contraindicat‌ed in_______.","raw_text":"Steroids are contraindicated in_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vernal keratoconjunctiviti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fungal cor‌neal ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atopic d‌ermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stromal ‌keratitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fungal corneal ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fungal corneal ulcer</strong></p><p><strong>Steroids </strong>are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in fungal corneal ulcer</p>\n<p>Steroids make the fungi <strong>facultative pathogens</strong> when they are otherwise symbiotic saprophytes. Therefore, the use of these drugs <strong>predisposes</strong> the patients to <strong>fungal infections</strong> and <strong>perforation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9729","text":"A 41-year-old female prese‌nted with a one-month his‍tory of photophobia and f​oreign body sensation in ‍her right eye. A slit-lam​p examination showed a co‌rneal ulcer. The culture ‌of the corneal scrapings ‍grew a filamentous organi​sm identified as a fusari‌um species. What would be​ the drug of choice in th‌is case?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old female presented with a one-month history of photophobia and foreign body sensation in her right eye. A slit-lamp examination showed a corneal ulcer. The culture of the corneal scrapings grew a filamentous organism identified as a fusarium species. What would be the drug of choice in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nata‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nyst‌atin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphote‌ricin B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Natamycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Natamycin</strong></p><p>This patient with a one-month history of eye foreign body sensations and photophobia and slit lamp exam showing an ulcer that had fusarium growth most likely has a fungal corneal ulcer. <strong>Natamycin 5% </strong>suspension is the drug of choice for <strong>filamentous fungal keratitis</strong> caused by <strong>fusariam</strong> species. </p>\n<p>Surface debridement is also done as it helps to remove slough, reduce the load of infection and enhances drug penetration. Amphotericin B is particularly effective against yeasts but less effective against filamentous fungi. Also, its penetration through an intact epithelium is less than natamycin.</p>\n<p><strong>Amphotericin B i</strong>s the first <strong>agent of choice </strong>against<strong> yeasts. </strong>Amphotericin B (0.15%) drops can be considered alone or in combination with natamycin in refractory cases.</p>\n<p>Treatment of specific fungal corneal ulcers:</p>\n<p>Topical antifungal drops</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Aspergillus and Fusarium- </em>Natamycin 5%, amphotericin B, fluconazole (0.2%) </li>\n<li><em>Candida- </em>Amphotericin B (0.15%) or econazole (1%) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Systemic antifungals such as itraconazole and voriconazole are used for severe <strong>non-responding cases</strong> or if the location of the ulcer is near the <strong>limbus</strong> or if there is a <strong>risk of perforation</strong> leading to endophthalmitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9731","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of the give‍n condition?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superficial punctate kerat‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal‌ guttata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Geographi‌cal ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immune strom‌al keratitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corneal guttata","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3bf4ee42037e46d6afa629f870b90329.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corneal guttata</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>dendritic ulcer</strong> caused by <strong>herpes simplex virus. Corneal guttata</strong> are <strong>not</strong> seen in HSV infection. They are droplet-like accumulations of non-banded collagen on the posterior surface of Descemet's membrane. It is a feature of <strong>corneal dystrophies.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9737","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the‍ given condition?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is related to Hutchinso‌n’s triad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is usually a history‌ of primary HSV infection‍ as a child","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can lead to neurotrophic k‌eratitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High dose topical acyclovi‌r is the treatment of cho‍ice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Can lead to neurotrophic keratitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b8d284a2e2d418294dd5d4116153c03.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a71269125f3c4a34848588da1b5dd22ax164x454.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Can lead to neurotrophic keratitis</strong></p><p>The image shows a unilateral painful, vesicular rash suggestive of<strong> herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO) </strong>caused by <strong>reactivation</strong> of the varicella-zoster virus (VZV) within the <strong>ophthalmic nerve.</strong> <strong>Neurotrophic keratitis</strong> is a complication of HZV infection.</p>\n<p>Keratitis occurs due to corneal nerve damage by the virus leading to decreased sensation, frequent infections, persistent epithelial defect, thinning and even perforation. </p>\n<p><strong>Corneal</strong> lesions in <strong>HZO </strong>(zoster keratitis):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelium- superficial punctate keratitis and pseudo-dendritic ulcer</li>\n<li>Stroma- nummular keratitis and disciform keratitis </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other ocular lesions in herpes zoster:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uveitis</li>\n<li>Episcleritis and scleritis</li>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n<li>Acute retinal necrosis</li>\n<li>Secondary glaucoma</li>\n<li>Anterior segment necrosis and pthisis bulbi</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Hutchinson’s <strong>rule</strong> is related to herpes zoster ophthalmicus. It states that if the tip of the nose is involved, there will be a significant risk of ocular involvement. This is due to the involvement of the nasociliary nerve. Hutchinson's triad is seen in congenital syphilis.</p>\n<p>Option B: There is primary<strong> HZV </strong>infection (chickenpox/varicella)<strong> not HSV </strong>infection.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Systemic</strong> acyclovir is the treatment of choice; not topical antiviral drops. Oral acyclovir 800 mg five times a day for 7-10 days, ideally initiated within 72 h of the onset of symptoms is the usual regimen.</p>\n<p>The image shows Hutchinson's sign in a patient with herpes zoster ophthalmicus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9740","text":"A 17-year-old boy presente‌d with blurred vision, ph‍otophobia, and increasing​ pain in his right eye. H‍e has been using contact ​lenses for the past 2 yea‌rs. He mentions swimming ‌with his cousins 2 weeks ‍back in a local pond. A c​lose-up image of the eye ‌is given below. What is t​he most likely explanatio‌n?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presented with blurred vision, photophobia, and increasing pain in his right eye. He has been using contact lenses for the past 2 years. He mentions swimming with his cousins 2 weeks back in a local pond. A close-up image of the eye is given below. What is the most likely explanation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacterial‌ keratitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fungal k‌eratitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simplex ‌keratitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acanthamoeb‌a keratitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acanthamoeba keratitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b1e04bab8c984a75b89abb8d17a66815.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acanthamoeba keratitis</strong></p><p>The history of a patient with contact lens use with swimming in freshwater ponds with the image depicting a <strong>ring lesion (stromal infiltrate) </strong>makes <strong>acanthamoeba keratitis</strong> the likely diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Acanthamoeba keratitis is a rare protozoal infection of the cornea. It is more common in <strong>soft contact lens users. </strong>The organism exists either as active, motile trophozoites or dormant, double-walled cysts.<strong> </strong>Suspicious sources of infection can be swimming or use of the hot tub while wearing contact lenses, homemade saline solution to wash contact lenses, exposure to standing groundwater etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Pain</strong> is disproportionate to the degree of keratitis due to <strong>perineural invasion</strong>. It presents as <strong>ring lesion</strong> (most common), pseudo-dendrites and as a reticular pattern.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confocal microscopy - </strong>may reveal bright trophozoites with pseudopodia in the basal epithelium and double-walled cyst forms</li>\n<li><strong>Histopathology - double-walled cysts </strong>are seen on H&amp;E. Other stains for <em>Acanthamoeba </em>include calcofluor white, acridic orange, Giemsa-Wright, Wheatly trichrome or periodic acid-Schiff</li>\n<li><strong>Culture -</strong> <em>Acanthamoeba </em>can be grown on a bed of <em>E. coli </em>plated on a non-nutrient agar</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9741","text":"A 39-year-old contact lens‌ user with a corneal ulce‍r, showing no improvement​ to tobramycin drops, was‍ referred to a tertiary c​are centre. Corneal biops‌y revealed double-walled ‌cysts within the stroma. ‍Which of the following is​ the treatment of choice?‌","raw_text":"A 39-year-old contact lens user with a corneal ulcer, showing no improvement to tobramycin drops, was referred to a tertiary care centre. Corneal biopsy revealed double-walled cysts within the stroma. Which of the following is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyhexamethylene biguanid‌e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neom‌ycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propamidine‌ isothionate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nyst‌atin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polyhexamethylene biguanide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polyhexamethylene biguanide</strong></p><p>This patient with a corneal ulcer whose biopsy showed double walled cysts in the stroma most likely has acanthamoeba keratitis. <strong>Polyhexamethylene biguanide (PHMB)</strong> is the treatment of choice for acanthamoeba keratitis.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Combination therapy with </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">chlorhexidine</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> is most effective against trophozoites and cysts. This is recommended as the </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">first line of therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Other topical anti-amoebic agents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diamidines- <strong>Propamidine isethionate</strong> (0.1 %), Hexamidine</li>\n<li>Aminoglycosides- <strong>Neomycin, </strong>Paramomycin</li>\n<li>Imidazoles- Clotrimazole, Miconazole</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 210\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9743","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause interstitial ‍keratitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause interstitial keratitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diphtheria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diphtheria</strong></p><p><strong><em>Diphtheria</em> </strong>is <strong>not </strong>a cause of<strong> interstitial keratitis. </strong></p>\n<p>Interstitial keratitis is a <strong>non-ulcerating, inflammatory reaction</strong> of the corneal <strong>stroma</strong> that is characterized by <strong>non-suppurative infiltration</strong> and often stromal <strong>vascularization</strong>. The epithelium and endothelium are <strong>not</strong> affected. Scarring due to stromal inflammation and vascularization, in interstitial keratitis, can result in decreased vision.</p>\n<p>Interstitial keratitis (IK) is a common endpoint of any keratitis that predominantly involves the corneal stroma. So there is a wide range of potential etiologies.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 195px; width: 699px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.195px;\">Immune-mediated etiologies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 430.211px;\">Infectious etiologies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.195px;\">\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Cogan syndrome:</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial keratitis + deafness + vertigo</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Mycosis fungoides</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 430.211px;\">\n<p>Bacterial causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Syphilis; Lyme disease</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis; Leprosy</li>\n<li>Brucellosis; Leptospirosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Viral causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Herpes simplex (HSV), Herpes zoster (VZV)</li>\n<li>Epstein-Barr (EBV), mumps, rubella (measles)</li>\n<li>influenza, and vaccinia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Parasitic causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>leishmaniasis</li>\n<li>onchocerciasis</li>\n<li>trypanosomiasis </li>\n<li>plasmodium</li>\n<li>acanthamoeba</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Treatment for IK typically involves the use of topical corticosteroids, as well as appropriate systemic medications for the associated systemic disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9746","text":"Which of the following inf‌ections is classically as‍sociated with salmon patc​h appearance of the corne‍a?","raw_text":"Which of the following infections is classically associated with salmon patch appearance of the cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H‌IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"H‌SV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Syphilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4176d2ac7f8940899e7b2ed03a54e1cex727x502.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Syphilis</strong></p><p><strong>Salmon patch appearance</strong> of the cornea is classically associated with <strong>syphilitic</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>This is because, syphilis causes <strong>deep vascularization</strong> of the cornea, leading to a <strong>radial bundle</strong> of <strong>brush-like</strong> vessels.</p>\n<p>Stages of interstitial keratitis in congenital syphilis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Progressive</strong> stage <strong>-</strong> <strong>ground-glass</strong> appearance</li>\n<li><strong>Florid</strong> stage - salmon patch appearance</li>\n<li>Stage of <strong>regression -</strong> <strong>ghost vessels</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6719","text":"A patient with recurrent c‌orneal epithelial erosion‍s is diagnosed with limba​l stem cell deficiency. S‍he has been enrolled on a​ research trial for corne‌al epithelial stem cell t‌ransplant. All are marker‍s that can be used to ide​ntify stem cells from an ‌ex-vivo culture except?","raw_text":"A patient with recurrent corneal epithelial erosions is diagnosed with limbal stem cell deficiency. She has been enrolled on a research trial for corneal epithelial stem cell transplant. All are markers that can be used to identify stem cells from an ex-vivo culture except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABCG2 tr‌ansporter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear p‌rotein p63","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-‌enolase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conne‌xin 50","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Connexin 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Connexin 50</strong></p><p>Connexin 50 is <strong>not a </strong>limbal stem-cell marker.</p>\n<p>The major markers proposed for epithelial stem cells can be categorized into at least three groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nuclear proteins such as <strong>transcription factor p63</strong></li>\n<li>Cell membrane or transmembrane proteins\n<ul>\n<li>Integrins (integrin ß1, α6, and α9),</li>\n<li>Receptors (epidermal growth factor [EGF] receptor and transferrin receptor CD71)</li>\n<li>Drug-resistance transporters (<strong>ABCG2—</strong>a universal stem cell marker)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cytoplasmic proteins such as\n<ul>\n<li>Cytokeratins (<strong>cytokeratin 14</strong> - exclusively at LSC)</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha-enolase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6722","text":"For her final year thesis,‌ an ophthalmology residen‍t is studying endothelial​ cell loss due to phacoem‍ulsification surgery. The​ image is taken during th‌e pre-op evaluation of on‌e of the cataract patient‍s. What does this show?","raw_text":"For her final year thesis, an ophthalmology resident is studying endothelial cell loss due to phacoemulsification surgery. The image is taken during the pre-op evaluation of one of the cataract patients. What does this show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal en‌dothelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymegat‌hism only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polymegathism and pleomorp‌hism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal‌ guttata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Polymegathism and pleomorphism","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/07f450c0c18545689f1afd2c5b1be0e4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Polymegathism and pleomorphism</strong></p><p>The given image is a <strong>specular</strong> <strong>microscopy</strong> view of endothelial cells of different shapes (<strong>pleomorphism</strong>) and large surface area <strong>(polymegathism).</strong> These are indicators of endothelial dysfunction.</p>\n<p>A normal endothelium has a regular <strong>hexagonal</strong> shape. Because these cells are terminally differentiated cells, they do not divide, and when they die, the remaining cells have to stretch to cover more area, resulting in polymegathism and pleomorphism.</p>\n<p>Taking an endothelial cell count can be helpful before taking patients up for cataract or refractive surgery so that any potential endothelial dysfunction or thinning is recognized before they have the surgical intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9734","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of herpetic‍ keratitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of herpetic keratitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stromal ‌keratitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dendrit‌ic ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corneal‌ guttata","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Disciform‌ keratitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Corneal guttata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Corneal guttata</strong></p><p><strong>Corneal guttata</strong> are seen in <strong>corneal dystrophy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Peripheral guttata are seen in old age—Hassall–Henle bodies. Central guttata, on the other hand, are found in Fuch's endothelial dystrophy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1050","text":"A corneal foreign body was‌ removed from a 53-year-o‍ld man. He was sent home ​with chloramphenicol and ‍steroid eye drops. Two da​ys later he came back wit‌h a corneal ulcer and a h‌ypopyon. The ulcer scrapi‍ng under microscopy revea​led branched septate hyph‌ae. What is the likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A corneal foreign body was removed from a 53-year-old man. He was sent home with chloramphenicol and steroid eye drops. Two days later he came back with a corneal ulcer and a hypopyon. The ulcer scraping under microscopy revealed branched septate hyphae. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can‌dida","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asper‌gillus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucurm‌ycosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histo‌plasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aspergillus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bed67a12804a4cd48c06e8ba71a5e785x375x206.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aspergillus</strong></p><p>The given history of trauma and worsening with antibiotic-steroid drops suggests a fungal corneal ulcer. Out of the given options, <em><strong>Aspergillus</strong> </em>is the fungus with branched septate hyphae. </p>\n<p>Clinically, fungi are classified on a morphological basis into <strong>filamentous</strong>, <strong>yeast</strong>, and <strong>dimorphic</strong> forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filamentous</strong> organisms are multicellular with branched hyphae. It can be septate or aseptate.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Septate </strong>filamentous organisms, such as <em><strong>Fusarium</strong></em><strong> </strong>and <em><strong>Aspergillus</strong></em>, have hyphae that are divided by cell walls.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-septate</strong> filamentous fungi, include <em><strong>Mucor</strong> </em>and<em> <strong>Rhizopus.</strong></em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Yeasts</strong>, such as <em><strong>Candida</strong> </em>and<em> <strong>Cryptococcus</strong></em>, are <strong>unicellular fungi</strong> that reproduce by <strong>budding</strong>, but in tissue, they might develop elongated buds (<strong>pseudohyphae).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dimorphic fungi</strong> such as <em><strong>Histoplasma</strong></em>,<em> <strong>Coccidioides</strong></em>, and<em> <strong>Blastomyces</strong></em> demonstrate both a yeast phase that occurs in tissues and a mycelial phase that appears on culture media and saprophytic surfaces.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image: Branched septate hyphae of <em style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Aspergillus</em><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> spp.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9735","text":"Corneal sensation is lost ‌in____.","raw_text":"Corneal sensation is lost in____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conjunc‌tivitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fungal i‌nfection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simpl‌ex keratitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Herpes simplex keratitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Herpes simplex keratitis</strong></p><p><strong>Corneal sensation</strong> is <strong>lost</strong> in <strong>herpes simplex</strong> virus (HSV) infection.</p>\n<p>The pathognomic feature of viral keratitis is <strong>decreased corneal sensation</strong>. Decreased corneal sensation is also seen in <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> and <strong>leprosy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RXoTxPvm","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"7_regional_conditions_of_the_upper_limb","title":"7.Regional Conditions Of The Upper Limb","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2928","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients are unlikely to dev‍elop a frozen shoulder?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients are unlikely to develop a frozen shoulder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"54-year-old man with left ‌sided hemiplegia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"34-year-old woman with hyp‌erthyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"45-year-old man with psori‌asis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"60-year-old man with histo‌ry of MI 3 months back","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 45-year-old man with psoriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 45-year-old man with psoriasis</strong></p><p>Psoriasis has no association with frozen shoulder.</p>\n<p><strong>Frozen shoulder</strong> is defined as the disorder characterized by <strong>progressive pain and stiffness</strong> in the shoulder joint which spontaneously resolves after around 18 months.</p>\n<p>Associated conditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cervical disc disease</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong></li>\n<li>Prolonged immobilization</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Stroke</strong> or <strong>myocardial infarction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6578","text":"A 45-year-old painter pres‌ented with chronic right ‍shoulder pain. The physic‌ian performed an empty ca‌n test on the patient. Wh​ich of the following musc​les is he specifically te‌sting for?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old painter presented with chronic right shoulder pain. The physician performed an empty can test on the patient. Which of the following muscles is he specifically testing for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supras‌pinatus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infras‌pinatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subsca‌pularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Teres‌ minor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supraspinatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d1dc69cc76945838e0ec37725e2aceex1200x1702.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supraspinatus</strong></p><p><strong>Empty can</strong> test is used to assess <strong>supraspinatus muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this test, the patient is asked to resist the downward force by the examiner, when the arms are in the position of <strong>90° abduction</strong>, <strong>30° flexion</strong>, and <strong>complete internal rotation</strong>. The result is positive if the affected side is weaker than the normal side. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2929","text":"Painful arc syndrome is se‌en in all except:","raw_text":"Painful arc syndrome is seen in all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete tear of supraspin‌atus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture greater tuberosit‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subacromia‌l bursitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supraspinatu‌s tendinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Complete tear of supraspinatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32867e644c48400896fcbe3d57197dddx1280x1979.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/235ac7b546604088a604e8e862b05db4x1280x1992.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26b6d0a965cd40cc82a46764c646537cx1280x2046.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86494bec37b34e26b4e4b6ca2c2a83b7x1279x1319.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Complete tear of supraspinatus</strong></p><p>The<strong> partial tear</strong> of the supraspinatus tendon is responsible for <strong>painful arc syndrome</strong> rather than a complete tear.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Painful arc syndrome, also known as <strong>impingement syndrome </strong>is a condition where there is pain in the shoulder and upper arm during the mid-range of shoulder abduction <strong>(between 60-120 degrees), </strong>arising due to repetitive rubbing of the <strong>supraspinatus tendon</strong> under the coracoacromial arch.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes include</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minor tears of the supraspinatus tendon</li>\n<li><strong>Supraspinatus tendinitis</strong></li>\n<li>Calcification of supraspinatus tendon</li>\n<li><strong>Fracture of greater tuberosity </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Subacromial bursitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important tests for impingement are</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painful arc test</li>\n<li>Hawkin-Kennedy's test</li>\n<li>Neer's impingement sign</li>\n<li>Jobe's test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>An x-ray of the shoulder helps to detect bony avulsions, spurs, calcific deposits, sclerotic areas etc. </li>\n<li>Ultrasound of the shoulder joint is highly reliable with a sensitivity of 98%. </li>\n<li><strong>Single contrast arthrogram</strong> is considered the gold standard in diagnosing rotator cuff tears </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conservative:</strong> heat massage, exercises, NSAIDs, subacromial steroid injections, and temporary immobilization (90% of patients recover with these measures). </li>\n<li><strong>Indications for surgery</strong> include failure of conservative measures for 3 months, increasing loss of shoulder function, and patients who are young and active.</li>\n<li>Surgical techniques include excision of adhesions and manipulation of the shoulder, excision of calcium deposits, repair of incomplete tear, acromioplasty,  acromionectomy for more disabling pain with normal range of movements, direct suture for complete rupture of the rotator cuff, rotation and transposition of the flap, and free graft. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7722","text":"A 35-year-old man working ‌as a clerk in an office p‍resents with the followin‌g lesion in his elbow. Wh‌at is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man working as a clerk in an office presents with the following lesion in his elbow. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hema‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triceps brachii tendon rup‌ture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Olecranon‌ bursitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumato‌id nodule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Olecranon bursitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/75261c6fc895483a83902e0c369e277c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Olecranon bursitis</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of <strong>olecranon bursitis</strong>. It is also known as <strong>Draughtman's elbow</strong>/<strong>student's elbow</strong>/ <strong>miner's elbow</strong>.</p>\n<p>Olecranon bursitis is a <strong>chronic inflammation </strong>of the olecranon bursa<strong>, </strong>as a result of repetitive minor injuries. It is seen in people who tend to keep their elbow rubbed against the desk. The constant friction against the olecranon bursa can lead to the development of bursitis.</p>\n<p>Culture and aspiration of the bursa fluid are done to rule out infection. Most cases can be managed <strong>conservatively</strong> with <strong>NSAIDs</strong> and <strong>local steroids</strong>. A chronically enlarged bursa will require excision.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2934","text":"Which of the following ten‌dons is most likely to be‍ involved in patients wit‌h tennis elbow?","raw_text":"Which of the following tendons is most likely to be involved in patients with tennis elbow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor car‌pi ulnaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor ca‌rpi radialis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor ca‌rpi ulnaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor car‌pi radialis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Extensor carpi radialis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Extensor carpi radialis</strong></p><p>The<strong> Tendon of Extensor carpi radialis</strong> is most commonly involved in <strong>tennis elbow</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7522","text":"A young sportsman presente‌d with pain in his right ‍elbow. On examination, Co‌zen's test was positive. ‌What is the probable diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A young sportsman presented with pain in his right elbow. On examination, Cozen's test was positive. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Draughtma‌n's elbow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pitcher‌'s elbow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Golfer'‌s elbow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tennis‌ elbow","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tennis elbow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tennis elbow</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with <strong>positive</strong> <strong>Cozen's test</strong> is suggestive of <strong>tennis elbow</strong> (lateral epicondylitis).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7721","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ted with left elbow pain.‍ On examination, tenderne‌ss over the medial epicon‌dyle was elicited on resi​sted forearm pronation in​ extension. Which of the ‌following statements is f‍alse about her condition?​","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented with left elbow pain. On examination, tenderness over the medial epicondyle was elicited on resisted forearm pronation in extension. Which of the following statements is false about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also called as swimm‌er's elbow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with ulnar nerv‌e neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most cases are managed con‌servatively","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mills sign ‌is positive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mills sign is positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mills sign is positive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Golfer's elbow. </strong>Mills sign is positive in Tennis elbow.</p>\n<p>Golfer's elbow is due to <strong>inflammation</strong> at the <strong>medial epicondyle</strong>, where the <strong>pronator muscles</strong> have their <strong>origin</strong>. It is also known as <strong>swimmer's elbow.</strong> It is less common than tennis elbow.</p>\n<p>Tenderness is localized over the medial epicondyle. There is pain on resisted forearm pronation in extension. <strong>Ulnar nerve entrapment</strong> at the medial epicondyle can result in neuropathy symptoms. </p>\n<p>Most of the cases are managed conservatively by activity modification and pain killers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7731","text":"De Quervain’s disease clas‌sically affects :","raw_text":"De Quervain’s disease classically affects :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor pollicis longus a‌nd abductor pollicis long‍us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor pollicis longus a‌nd abductor pollicis brev‍is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor pollicis brevis a‌nd abductor pollicis brev‍is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor pollicis brevis a‌nd abductor pollicis long‍us","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2849d44748cc49b88e7587aa450497dcx1280x1492.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus</strong></p><p><strong>De Quervain's disease</strong> classically affects <strong>extensor pollicis brevis</strong> and <strong>abductor pollicis longus</strong>.</p>\n<p>De Quervain's disease is a<strong> stenosing tenosynovitis of the extensor tendons of the first dorsal compartment</strong>, namely <strong>extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) and abductor pollicis longus (APL)</strong>. It involves noninflammatory thickening of both the tendons and the tunnel or sheath through which they pass. This condition is prominent among <strong>females</strong>, especially <strong>women of childbearing age</strong>.</p>\n<p>Key diagnostic factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">History of <strong>joint overuse</strong> at home or at work.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pain</strong> and tenderness at the <strong>tip of the radial styloid</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Positive Finkelstein test</strong>: The examiner grasps the thumb of the patient and abducts the hand towards the ulnar side. This causes severe pain over the tip of the styloid process </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> in most patients includes <strong>conservative management</strong> with splinting and steroid injections. Only a few patients require surgical release of the 1st dorsal compartment (housing the APL and EPB tendons).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7723","text":"Finkelstein's test is nega‌tive in patients with:","raw_text":"Finkelstein's test is negative in patients with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"De Quervai‌n's disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arthritis of radio carpal ‌joints","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intersecti‌on syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve‌ entrapment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ulnar nerve entrapment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/caef5630222d4c5a818e26d43761dad7x1280x1492.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f174277fb31841e6a3bd2f52c77db70ex1280x1492.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ulnar nerve entrapment</strong></p><p>Finkelstein's test is negative ulnar nerve entrapment.</p>\n<p><strong>Finkelstein's test</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 423\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Examiner holds the patient’s hand <strong>with his thumb tucked firmly </strong>and then flexes into the palm<strong>;</strong> then turn the wrist into full ulnar deviation; in a positive test, this will elicit sharp pain in the affected sheath. The pain over the radial styloid tip is excruciating.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>It is positive in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>De Quervain's tenosynovitis</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Arthritis</strong> in the intercarpal and <strong>radiocarpal joints</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radial nerve entrapment </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Intersection syndrome</strong> i.e. tenosynovitis occurring due to friction between the obliquely placed extensor and abductor pollicis tendons and the longitudinally placed extensor tendons.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4188\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Finkelstein stated that this test is “probably the most pathognomonic objective sign</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note: <strong>Finkelstein's test is commonly confused with Eichhoff's test</strong>:<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-I2007_1-2\"></sup> Eichhoff's test is typically described as the examiner ulnar deviating the hand when the person has <strong>their thumb held within their fist</strong>. If sharp pain occurs along the distal radius, De Quervain's tenosynovitis is suspected.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7724","text":"A 50-year-old diabetic wom‌an noticed an audible cli‍ck every time she flexed ‌her left middle finger. T‌he extension of the middl​e finger is as depicted i​n the clip below. This is‌ caused due to involvemen‍t of which of the followi​ng structures?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old diabetic woman noticed an audible click every time she flexed her left middle finger. The extension of the middle finger is as depicted in the clip below. This is caused due to involvement of which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extenso‌r tendon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor‌ tendon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Palmar ap‌oneurosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsal ap‌oneurosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flexor tendon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7904e98f279b4a13b384f3b9afbbc1d0.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/975b1c6e87b94fc39dbd1cd3af00ac2ax1279x1157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flexor tendon</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario along with the video showing the middle finger in the flexed position requiring extra force to extend it is suggestive of a <strong>trigger finger. </strong></p>\n<p>Trigger finger is caused by<strong> stenosing tenosynovitis</strong> of the flexor sheath. There is nodular or fusiform swelling on the flexor tendon that prevents it from moving along the A1 pulley.</p>\n<p><strong>A1 pulley</strong> is an <strong>annular ligament</strong> that acts as a belt that straps the flexor tendon to the underlying phalanges. There are 5 annular ligaments in each finger namely A1. A2, A3, A4, and A5.</p>\n<p>The image given below is showing the nodule in the flexor tendon and the 5 annular ligaments of the finger.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When the finger is flexed or extended, the nodular swelling passes through the A1 pulley with a snap, leading to a triggering effect. The nodule can get trapped behind the A1 pulley leading to a locking of the finger in flexion.</p>\n<p>Surgical treatment involves the <strong>release</strong> of the A1 pulley, which is thickened and fibrosed. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7726","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‍e condition shown in the ‌image given below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the condition shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-ray is useful in the dia‌gnosis of the condition","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is more common in males‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenytoin therapy is a com‌mon cause","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery is the best treatm‌ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. X-ray is useful in the diagnosis of the condition","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8e33baca2be47fbabbf90f623aa5b2d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. X-ray is useful in the diagnosis of the condition</strong></p><p>The condition shown above is <strong>Dupuytren's contracture</strong>. A radiograph would be the <strong>least useful</strong> in the diagnosis of this condition.</p>\n<p>In Dupuytren's contracture, there is <strong>proliferating fibroplasia</strong> of the subcutaneous palmar tissue, forming nodules. It is more commonly seen in <strong>males</strong>.</p>\n<p>It results in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Finger contractures</li>\n<li>Thinning of subcutaneous fat</li>\n<li>Pitting of skin and knuckle pads</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The possible causes are:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Inheritance</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Phenytoin therapy</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Smoking</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Epileptics and alcoholics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Though <strong>surgery</strong> is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong>, it is delayed until the contractures develop.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of Dupuytren Disease</strong>:</p>\n<p>Nonoperative:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>External beam radiation</li>\n<li>Steroid injection</li>\n<li>Collagenase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Operative:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subcutaneous fasciotomy</li>\n<li>Staged external fixation</li>\n<li>Arthrodesis</li>\n<li>Amputation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7743","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the gamekeepe‍r's thumb?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the gamekeeper's thumb?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caused by fall on extended‌ thumb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caused by valgus stress on‌ the thumb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Volar plate is involved in‌ partial rupture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar collateral ligament ‌is involved in partial ru‍pture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Volar plate is involved in partial rupture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Volar plate is involved in partial rupture</strong></p><p><strong>Gamekeepers thumb</strong> results due to the <strong>rupture of ulnar collateral ligament</strong> of thumb metacarpophalangeal joint. The volar plate is not involved in partial rupture, it is involved only in complete rupture.</p>\n<p>It can be caused either by <strong>fall on extended thumb</strong> (forcing it into hyperabduction) or by <strong>repeated valgus stress</strong> (i.e. radial deviation) on the thumb which occurs during twisting movement of the hand. This type of injury was seen in gamekeepers who used to twist the neck of small animals with their thumb and index finger.</p>\n<p>The ulnar collateral ligament keeps the thumb stable in flexion and the volar plate keeps the thumb stable in extension.</p>\n<p><strong>In a partial rupture</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Only the ligament is torn and the thumb is unstable only in flexion </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The volar plate is intact, keeping the thumb stable in extension</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In a complete rupture</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Both the ligament and the volar plate are torn </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The thumb is unstable in all positions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pSRndmhq","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"7_nutrition_and_breastfeeding","title":"7.Nutrition And Breastfeeding","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA0481","text":"The most calorically dense‌ macronutrient is _______​","raw_text":"The most calorically dense macronutrient is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pro‌tein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simple car‌bohydrates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F‌at","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Complex ca‌rbohydrates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fat</strong></p><p><strong>Fat</strong> is the <strong>most calorically dense</strong> macronutrient providing approximately<strong> 9 kcal/g</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nutrients that provide energy intake in the child’s diet are referred to as <strong>macronutrients</strong>.<strong> </strong>They include the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li> Fats (∼<strong>9 kcal/g</strong>)</li>\n<li> Carbohydrates (<strong>4 kcal/g)</strong></li>\n<li> Proteins (∼<strong>4 kcal/g</strong>)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main dietary sources of fat are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For infants: human milk/formula</li>\n<li>For older children: fat from animal products, vegetable oils, and margarine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9235","text":"The mother of a school-goi‌ng child is worried that ​he eats a lot of junk foo‍d. She is anxious to lear‌n about the healthy diet ‌to be followed in the chi‌ld. You would ideally adv‍ise the mother to restric​t the dietary intake of s​aturated fat to less than​ ______ percentage of tot​al energy intake.","raw_text":"The mother of a school-going child is worried that he eats a lot of junk food. She is anxious to learn about the healthy diet to be followed in the child.  You would ideally advise the mother to restrict the dietary intake of saturated fat to less than ______  percentage of total energy intake.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10%</strong></p><p>According to current dietary guidelines for children, energy from saturated fats should be <strong>&lt;10% of total energy intake</strong>.</p>\n<p>Current dietary guidelines for children and adolescents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total fat should account for &lt;30% of total daily energy</li>\n<li>Saturated fat less than 10% of total daily energy</li>\n<li>Dietary cholesterol &lt;300 mg/day</li>\n<li>No trans-fat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0482","text":"Your school friend, the mo‌ther of a four-year-old c​hild gives you a casual c‍all. She had recently not‌iced an ad on margarine b‌eing a better alternative‌ over butter/ghee and wis‍hes to seek your opinion ​about adding it to her so​n's diet. What percentage​ of total daily energy re​quirement in children can‌ come from margarine?","raw_text":"Your school friend, the mother of a four-year-old child gives you a casual call. She had recently noticed an ad on margarine being a better alternative over butter/ghee and wishes to seek your opinion about adding it to her son's diet. What percentage of total daily energy requirement in children can come from margarine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‌; 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‌; 10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‌; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0</strong></p><p><strong>Margarine </strong>and other hydrogenated oils are <strong>trans fat.</strong> Trans fat is <strong>not recommended</strong> in children according to current dietary guidelines.</p>\n<p> Current dietary guidelines for children and adolescents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total fat should account for &lt;30% of total daily energy</li>\n<li>Saturated fat less than 10% of total daily energy</li>\n<li>Dietary cholesterol &lt;300 mg/day</li>\n<li><strong>No trans-fat</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0470","text":"A newborn baby is ready to‌ be discharged. As a part​ of your discharge advice‍, for how many months wou‌ld you recommend exclusiv‌e breastfeeding?","raw_text":"A newborn baby is ready to be discharged.  As a part of your discharge advice, for how many months would you recommend exclusive breastfeeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 mo‌nths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"9 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 y‌ear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 months</strong></p><p><strong>Exclusive breastfeeding</strong> is recommended for<strong> 6 months</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Complementary foods</strong> rich in iron and other micronutrients should be introduced at about 6 months of age. Breastfeeding should continue for at least the first year.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0480","text":"You are taking a neonatal ‌case for presentation dur​ing your postings. The mo‍ther tells you that the c‌hild is exclusively breas‌tfed. Which of the follow‌ing is least likely to co‍rroborate with this histo​ry of being exclusively b​reastfed?","raw_text":"You are taking a neonatal case for presentation during your postings. The mother tells you that the child is exclusively breastfed.  Which of the following is least likely to corroborate with this history of being exclusively breastfed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemorrhagic disease of new‌born","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breast mil‌k jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Golden co‌lor stools","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evenin‌g colic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Evening colic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Evening colic</strong></p><p>Exclusively breastfed infants are least likely to have <strong>evening </strong><strong>colic. </strong>The incidence of diarrhoea, allergy to bovine milk, and atopic eczema are least reported in exclusively breastfed infants.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Vitamin K</strong> content of milk is<strong> low</strong> and may contribute to<strong> hemorrhagic disease of the newborn</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Breast milk contains inhibitors of glucuronyl transferase and enhances absorption of bilirubin from the gut. This leads to the development of <strong>b</strong><strong>reast milk jaundice</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option C: During the first few days, the breastfed infant has a black-green coloured stool. It changes to <strong>golden yellow</strong> by the end of the <strong>first week</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0566","text":"Which of the following mot‌hers can initiate breastf​eeding? A mother with an ‍active infection of herpe‌s on both her breasts. A ‌mother with positive sero‌logy for hepatitis B infe‍ction. A mother with acti​ve tuberculosis. A mother​ who is on treatment with​ chemotherapeutic agents ​for leukaemia. A mother w‌ith a history of chronic ‍smoking.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following mothers can initiate breastfeeding?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A mother with an active infection of herpes on both her breasts.</li>\n<li>A mother with positive serology for hepatitis B infection.</li>\n<li>A mother with active tuberculosis.</li>\n<li>A mother who is on treatment with chemotherapeutic agents for leukaemia.</li>\n<li>A mother with a history of chronic smoking.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 and ‌5 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 5 only</strong></p><p>The correct answer is <strong>2 and 5 only. </strong>Breastfeeding is not contraindicated if the mother has hepatitis B infection or if she is a chronic smoker.</p>\n<p>Infants born to an HbsAg positive mother routinely receive hepatitis B immunoglobulin and hepatitis B vaccine. So, a delay in the initiation of breastfeeding is not required.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 586px; width: 412px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 402px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Contraindications of breastfeeding:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 130px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 152.672px; height: 130px;\">\n<p><strong>Do not</strong> Breastfeed</p>\n<p><strong>Do not</strong> give expressed breastmilk</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243.328px; height: 130px;\">\n<p>Infant factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classic galactosemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Maternal factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Human T-cell lymphocyte virus (HTLV) infection</li>\n<li>Ebola virus infection</li>\n<li>PCP/cocaine abuse</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 150px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 152.672px; height: 150px;\">\n<p>Do not Breastfeed <strong>(Temporarily)</strong></p>\n<p>Do not give expressed breastmilk  <strong>(Temporarily)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243.328px; height: 150px;\">\n<p>Maternal factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brucellosis</li>\n<li>undergoing diagnostic imaging with radiopharmaceuticals</li>\n<li><strong>Active herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection</strong> with lesions on the breasts</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 152.672px; height: 132px;\">\n<p>Do not Breastfeed <strong>(Temporarily)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Can</strong> give expressed breastmilk</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243.328px; height: 132px;\">\n<p>Maternal factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Untreated, active Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Active Varicella infection</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Alcohol consumption and smoking are NOT contraindications of breastfeeding. However, smoking and alcohol consumption should be discouraged.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>In a mother with untreated, active tuberculosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Temporarily withhold breastfeeding but <strong>can feed with expressed breastmilk</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Resume breastfeeding</strong> once she has been adequately treated for <strong>2 weeks</strong> and is no longer contagious.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0471","text":"As a part of your neonatal‌ rotation, you are asked ​to assess the adequacy of‍ breastfeeding and breast‌feeding techniques in a p‌rimi mother. Which of the‌ following would be an in‍dicator of inadequate bre​astfeeding in the neonate​?","raw_text":"As a part of your neonatal rotation, you are asked to assess the adequacy of breastfeeding and breastfeeding techniques in a primi mother. Which of the following would be an indicator of inadequate breastfeeding in the neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baby sleeping for 2-3 hour‌s after feed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weight loss &amp;gt;10% of bir‌th weight","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Urine 5-7‌ times/day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stool 4 ‌times/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Weight loss >10% of birth weight","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Weight loss &gt;10% of birth weight</strong></p><p>Weight loss of <strong>&gt;7-10%</strong> of birth weight is an indicator of inadequate breastfeeding in a neonate.</p>\n<p>Indicators of inadequate breastfeeding in the newborn:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weight loss &gt;7-10 % of birth weight</strong></li>\n<li>Lethargy (sleeping <strong>longer</strong> than 2–3 hours)</li>\n<li>Delayed passage of stools (<strong>Less</strong> than <strong>4</strong> times/day)</li>\n<li>Decreased urine output (urinating <strong>less</strong> than <strong>5–7</strong> times/day)</li>\n<li>Hypernatremic dehydration</li>\n<li>Inconsolable crying and increased hunger</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Insufficient milk intake may be caused by insufficient milk production, failure of established breastfeeding, and health conditions in the infant that prevent proper breast stimulation.</p>\n<p>Breastfed neonates usually feed 8–12 times a day, with a minimum of 8 times per day. However, the neonate should be breastfed only<strong> on demand</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0475","text":"For how long can freshly e‌xpressed breastmilk be st​ored at room temperature?‍","raw_text":"For how long can freshly expressed breastmilk be stored at room temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 hours</strong></p><p>Expressed breast milk can be stored at <strong>room temperature </strong>for <strong>6-8 hours.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Expressed breast milk</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Stored for / Used within</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(maximum time) </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Room Temperature</td>\n<td>6 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In refrigerator</td>\n<td>24 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Frozen breastmilk at -20°C  </p>\n</td>\n<td>3 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: This is a <strong>controversial question</strong>.</p>\n<p>A difference of opinion among authors is noted regarding the refrigeration of expressed breast milk. Nelson's (21st ed, page 324) mentions that breast milk can be refrigerated and used within 48 hours and frozen for around 6 months.<strong> </strong>However,<strong> AIIMS </strong>in collaboration with <strong>WHO </strong>recommends that breast milk can be <strong>refrigerated up to 24 hours</strong> and <strong>frozen</strong> at -20 degree C <strong>for about 3 months</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0474","text":"Shrija, a working mother p‌lanning to resume her wor​k wishes to know more abo‍ut expressed breast milk.‌ Within how many hours wo‌uld you advise her to use‌ the refrigerated express‍ed breast milk?","raw_text":"Shrija, a working mother planning to resume her work wishes to know more about expressed breast milk. Within how many hours would you advise her to use the refrigerated expressed breast milk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"48 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 h‌ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 24 hours</strong></p><p>Breast milk stored in the refrigerator should be used within<strong> 24 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Refrigerated or frozen milk should be <strong>thawed rapidly</strong> by holding under running tepid water and <strong>used completely within 24 h</strong> after thawing. Milk should <strong>never be microwaved</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 258.556px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Expressed breast milk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.556px; height: 36px;\">\n<p><strong>Stored for / Used within</strong></p>\n<p><strong> (maximum time) </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 42px;\">Room Temperature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.556px; height: 42px;\">6 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 42px;\">In refrigerator</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.556px; height: 42px;\">24 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 39px;\">\n<p>Frozen breastmilk at -20°C</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.556px; height: 39px;\">3 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: This is a <strong>controversial question</strong>.</p>\n<p>A difference of opinion among authors is noted regarding the refrigeration of expressed breast milk. Nelson's (21st ed, page 324) mentions that breast milk can be refrigerated and used within 48 hours and frozen for around 6 months.<strong> </strong>However,<strong> AIIMS </strong>in collaboration with <strong>WHO </strong>recommends that breast milk can be <strong>refrigerated up to 24 hours</strong> and <strong>frozen</strong> at -20 degree C <strong>for about 3 months</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0565","text":"An anxious new mother feed‌ing her child with expres​sed breastmilk (EBM) feel‍s that the consistency an‌d colour of her milk have‌ changed recently. She ad‌ds that when she expresse‍s breastmilk, the colour ​and consistency of the fi​rst portion and the endin​g portion is completely d​ifferent and that she is ‌worried about the same. W‍hich statement is not tru​e about different breast ‌milk?","raw_text":"An anxious new mother feeding her child with expressed breastmilk (EBM) feels that the consistency and colour of her milk have changed recently. She adds that when she expresses breastmilk, the colour and consistency of the first portion and the ending portion is completely different and that she is worried about the same.  Which statement is not true about different breast milk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preterm milk has more prot‌eins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mature milk is thicker wit‌h all the essential nutri​ents for growth","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Foremilk is watery and is ‌rich in proteins and suga​r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hindmilk is richer in fat ‌and provides more energy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mature milk is thicker with all the essential nutrients for growth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/944f94e4e04a4e3db1ce9b423f52f4bex1280x569.7755610972569.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mature milk is thicker with all the essential nutrients for growth</strong></p><p><strong>Mature milk </strong>is <strong>thinner</strong> (not thicker) with all essential nutrients for growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Various transitional forms of breastmilk:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Colostrum</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the initial 3 to 4 days</li>\n<li>Small quantity, thick, and yellow in colour</li>\n<li>A large number of <strong>antibodies</strong>,<strong> </strong>immune component cells; and vitamins A, D, E, and K.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Transitional milk</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secreted after 3 to 4 days until 2 weeks</li>\n<li>Immunoglobulin and protein content decreases, while the fat and sugar content increases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mature milk</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Follows transitional milk.</li>\n<li><strong>Thinner</strong> and more watery.</li>\n<li>Contains all the nutrients essential for the optimal growth of the baby. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Foremilk</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secreted at the start of the feed</li>\n<li>Watery and rich in proteins, sugar, vitamins, minerals, and water that quenches the baby’s thirst.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hindmilk </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comes towards the end of the feed</li>\n<li>Provides <strong>more energy</strong> and a sense of satiety and it is richer in <strong>fat</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Preterm milk </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Milk of mother who delivers before 37 weeks.</li>\n<li>Contains more proteins, sodium, iron, immunoglobins, and calories as per the requirement of the preterm infant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows watery foremilk and hindmilk which is rich in fat.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9236","text":"Following the delivery of ‌a healthy baby boy, his C​OVID positive mother slip‍ped into ARDS and passed ‌away. The relatives have ‌arranged a breast milk do‌nor and wish to feed the ‍baby using the device sho​wn in the image. In newbo​rns of which gestational ​age, would you recommend ​this as a method of prima‌ry feeding?","raw_text":"Following the delivery of a healthy baby boy, his COVID positive mother slipped into ARDS and passed away. The relatives have arranged a breast milk donor and wish to feed the baby using the device shown in the image.  In newborns of which gestational age, would you recommend this as a method of primary feeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"28-31‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"32-34‌ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;34‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any gesta‌tional age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 32-34 weeks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bddc9f682b8647d481fda37b9131aeb4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 32-34 weeks</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>paladai</strong>. It can be used as <strong>a primary</strong> feeding method during <strong>routine</strong> feeding in neonates born at gestational age <strong>32-34 weeks</strong> only. Sick mothers or mothers with contraindications to breastfeeding can feed their child using paladai. The milk can either be expressed breastmilk or they can resort to using undiluted cow milk or formula feeds. </p>\n<p>Choice of initial feeding methods in low-birth-weight (LBW) infants:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 434px; height: 564px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>Gestational Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\"><strong>Feeding Skill Matured</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\"><strong>Method of Feeding</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">&lt;28 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">No proper sucking effort. No propulsive motility of the gut</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">IV Fluids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">28-32 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">\n<p>Sucking Bursts develop</p>\n<p>No coordination between suck/swallow and breathing</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">Oro-gastric/ Nasogastric tube feeding with occasional spoon/ paladai feeding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>32-34 weeks</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Slightly mature sucking pattern. <strong>Coordination between breathing and swallowing begins</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">Feeding by spoon/paladai/cup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">&gt;34 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Mature sucking pattern. More coordination between breathing and swallowing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">Breastfeeding</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0916","text":"Bifidus factor present in ‌breast milk protects agai​nst __________","raw_text":"Bifidus factor present in breast milk protects against __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enteroamoeba‌ histolytica","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Giardia‌ lamblia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salmo‌nella","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio ‌cholerae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Salmonella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Salmonella</strong></p><p><strong>Bifidus factor</strong> present in breast milk protects against <strong><em>Salmonella</em>.</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 392.078px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\"><strong>Components of breast milk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 199.078px;\"><strong>Protection against</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Lysozyme</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199.078px;\">\n<p><em>Escherichia coli, Salmonella</em></p>\n<p><em>Entamoeba histolytica</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Lactoferrin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199.078px;\">\n<p><em>E. coli, Shigella, Salmonella</em></p>\n<p><em>E.histolytica, Giardia lamblia</em></p>\n<p><em>Plasmodium falciparum</em></p>\n<p>Rotavirus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Bifidus factor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199.078px;\"><em>E. coli, Shigella, Salmonella</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Low levels of para-aminobenzoic acid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199.078px;\"><em>Plasmodium</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0479","text":"A 2-month-old child is bro‌ught to your OPD with com​plaints of diarrhoea. The‍ mother wants to know if ‌she should continue breas‌tfeeding. Which of these ‌components in breast milk‍, beneficial in intestina​l cell maturation and rep​air, prompt you to advise​ her to continue breastfe​eding?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old child is brought to your OPD with complaints of diarrhoea. The mother wants to know if she should continue breastfeeding. Which of these components in breast milk, beneficial in intestinal cell maturation and repair, prompt you to advise her to continue breastfeeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lacto‌ferrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidermal g‌rowth factor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Para aminob‌enzoic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glutathione‌ peroxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epidermal growth factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epidermal growth factor</strong></p><p>The <strong>epidermal growth factor </strong>present in breastmilk helps in intestinal cell maturation and repair. This is why mothers are advised to continue breastfeeding in an infant affected with diarrhoea. It is also beneficial in luminal surveillance.</p>\n<p><strong>Lactoferrin </strong>(Option A) helps in immunomodulation, iron chelation, <strong>antimicrobial action. </strong>It is also an antiadhesive and trophic for intestinal growth. </p>\n<p><strong>Para-aminobenzoic acid </strong>(Option C) is present in low levels and offers protection against <strong>plasmodium</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Glutathione peroxidase </strong>(Option D) prevents lipid oxidation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0910","text":"A young working mother wan‌ts to add formula feeds t​o the diet of her 5-month‍-old child as she would b‌e unable to breastfeed he‌r baby frequently and sat‌iate her. Which of the fo‍llowing fatty acid should​ the mother look for in t​he formula to ensure opti​mal brain development in ​the child? Eicosapentanoi‌c acid Docosahexaenoic ac‍id Arachidonic acid Linol​enic acid","raw_text":"<p>A young working mother wants to add formula feeds to the diet of her 5-month-old child as she would be unable to breastfeed her baby frequently and satiate her.  Which of the following fatty acid should the mother look for in the formula to ensure optimal brain development in the child?</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>Eicosapentanoic acid</li>\n<li>Docosahexaenoic acid</li>\n<li>Arachidonic acid</li>\n<li>Linolenic acid</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B a‌nd C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A,B ‌and C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A a‌nd D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B and C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B and C</strong></p><p><strong>Docosahexenoic acid </strong>(DHA) and<strong> arachidonic acid </strong>(ARA), both play an important role in the <strong>growth</strong> and <strong>CNS development</strong> in<strong> babies</strong>.</p>\n<p>DHA and ARA are essential poly unsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) present in breast milk.</p>\n<p>Both are essential for <strong>cognition functions, visual acuity, </strong>and<strong> immune responses.</strong> They have optimal effects on brain development when included together, in efficacious levels, in an infant's diet.</p>\n<p>Almost all infant formulae are now fortified with pre-formed DHA and ARA.</p>\n<p>It is advisable that all infants upto 6 months of age are to be exclusively breastfed and not formula fed. However, in unavoidable situations such as lactation failure or maternal infections where breastfeeding is contraindicated, the use of a formula feed that contains both DHA and ARA is recommended. </p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eicosapentaenoic acid</strong> (EPA) and <strong>Linolenic acid</strong> are other <strong>essential fatty acids</strong> that are present in breast milk.</li>\n<li>These are required for the overall <strong>protection</strong> of the <strong>cardiovascular system</strong> as well as to <strong>reduce systemic inflammation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0477","text":"Breast milk is superior to‌ cow’s milk because _____​___","raw_text":"Breast milk is superior to cow’s milk because ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saturated fat content is h‌igh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium concentration is hi‌gh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Casein is 60% of the prote‌in content","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whey is 70% of the protein‌ content","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Whey is 70% of the protein content","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Whey is 70% of the protein content</strong></p><p>Whey constitutes <strong>70-80%</strong> of breast milk's protein content. The<strong> Whey: Casein</strong> ratio in human milk fluctuates between 70:30 and 80:20. This ratio is 20:80 in cow's milk. The high fraction of soluble whey protein in breast milk makes it easily digestible.</p>\n<p>The predominant whey protein is <strong>α-lactalbumin</strong> in human milk and<strong> β-globulin</strong> in<strong> cow milk</strong>. Human milk contains amino acids like <strong>taurine</strong> and <strong>cysteine</strong> (lacking in cow’s milk), which are necessary for neurotransmission and neuromodulation.</p>\n<p>Breast milk is rich in <strong>polyunsaturated fat</strong> (not saturated fat), which is necessary for <strong>myelination</strong>. Human breast milk is generally rich in <strong>oleic acid</strong> and<strong> linoleic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sodium concentration is <strong>not</strong> high in breast milk. It has a <strong>low osmolality,</strong> and hence, the solute load on the kidneys is low.</p>\n<p>Differences between human milk and cow's milk are shown below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 228px;\" width=\"318\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Nutrient</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Human milk (per 100 mL)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Cow's milk (per 100 mL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">1.1 g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">3.5 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Calories</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">67 kcal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">62 kcal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Casein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">30–40% (rich in whey protein)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">80%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Lactose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">7.4 g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">4.5 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Fat </td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">3.5 g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">4g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Na+ (mEq/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">6.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">K+ (mEq/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">35</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Osmolality (mOsm/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">290</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">350</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">calcium/phosphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">&gt;2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">&lt;2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Vitamin K</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">1.5 µg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">6 µg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Iron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.29–0.45 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.01-0.38 mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Vitamin D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.5-10 IU</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.5-4.5 IU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0476","text":"What is found more in huma‌n milk as compared to cow​’s milk?","raw_text":"What is found more in human milk as compared to cow’s milk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lac‌tose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pro‌tein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‌min K","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F‌at","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lactose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lactose</strong></p><p>The percentage of <strong>lactose</strong> in <strong>human milk</strong> is <strong>greater </strong>than that in<strong> cow’s milk</strong>.</p>\n<p>Differences between human milk and cow's milk are shown below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 228px;\" width=\"318\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Nutrient</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Human milk (per 100 mL)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Cow's milk (per 100 mL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">1.1 g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">3.5 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Calories</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">67 kcal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">62 kcal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Casein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">30–40% (rich in whey protein)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">80%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Lactose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">7.4 g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">4.5 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Fat </td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">3.5 g</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">4g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Na+ (mEq/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">6.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">K+ (mEq/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">35</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Osmolality (mOsm/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">290</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">350</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">calcium/phosphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">&gt;2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">&lt;2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Vitamin K</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">1.5 µg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">6 µg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Iron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.29–0.45 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.01-0.38 mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Vitamin D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.5-10 IU</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">0.5-4.5 IU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Human breast milk is generally rich in oleic acid and linoleic acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0564","text":"An exclusively breastfed n‌eonate in his 4th week of​ life was found to be jau‍ndiced. His stools are ye‌llow and urine appears pa‌le straw, he is otherwise‌ well. A provisional diag‍nosis of breastmilk jaund​ice is made. Which of the​ following is false about​ this condition?","raw_text":"An exclusively breastfed neonate in his 4th week of life was found to be jaundiced. His stools are yellow and urine appears pale straw, he is otherwise well. A provisional diagnosis of breastmilk jaundice is made. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labs will reveal unconjuga‌ted hyperbilirubinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Some babies need treatment‌ with phototherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast feeding is contrain‌dicated","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is common in the second‌ week of life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Breast feeding is contraindicated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Breast feeding is contraindicated</strong></p><p>Breastfeeding is <strong>not contraindicated</strong> in <strong>breast milk jaundice</strong>. Mothers are advised to <strong>continue breastfeeding</strong> at frequent intervals.</p>\n<p>Breastmilk jaundice is seen in about 2–4% of <strong>exclusively breastfed</strong> babies. There is significant <strong>unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong> after the 7<sup>th</sup> day and may last for 2–3 weeks.<strong> </strong>This may be caused by the breast milk that might contain <strong>β-glucuronidase</strong>, which deconjugates intestinal bilirubin and promotes its absorption and other inhibitors of glucuronyl transferase. Persistently high bilirubin levels may require changing from breast milk to <strong>infant formula for 24–48 h</strong> and/or treatment with <strong>phototherapy,</strong> without cessation of breastfeeding. </p>\n<p><strong>Breastfeeding jaundice (BFJ)</strong></p>\n<p>It is an early-onset, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia of breastfed infants. Occurs in the<strong> 1st week after birth </strong>(usually<strong> appears after 24 - 72 hours </strong>of birth). It is due to decreased milk intake with dehydration and/or reduced caloric intake.<strong> </strong>The treatment would include continuing breastfeeding and supplementation with formula feeds if needed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3947","text":"A 26-year-old nursing moth‌er presents with fever an​d pain in her left breast‍. She has had difficulty ‌with breastfeeding previo‌usly, and now examination‌ reveals her left breast ‍is warm, red, swollen, an​d painful to palpation. S​he was started on dicloxa​cillin. What advice shoul​d be given to the mother ‌regarding breastfeeding?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old nursing mother presents with fever and pain in her left breast. She has had difficulty with breastfeeding previously, and now examination reveals her left breast is warm, red, swollen, and painful to palpation. She was started on dicloxacillin. What advice should be given to the mother regarding breastfeeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete cessation of brea‌stfeed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue feeding from both‌ the breasts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Continue feeding from only‌ right breast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continue feeding from only‌ left breast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Continue feeding from both the breasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Continue feeding from both the breasts</strong></p><p><strong>Lactational mastitis</strong> is not a contraindication to breastfeeding and the mother should <strong>continue feeding on both breasts.</strong></p>\n<p>It is most commonly caused due to Staphylococcus aureus present in the infant's nasopharynx. Dicloxacillin is safe to be given in nursing mothers. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0472","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true with res​pect to the WHO Baby-Frie‍ndly Hospital Initiative ‌for small, sick, and pret‌erm newborns (2020)?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements are true with respect to the WHO Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative for small, sick, and preterm newborns (2020)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paladai feeding is indicat‌ed in very preterm infant​s with no suck-swallow-br‍eath coordination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Finger-feeding is optimall‌y indicated for short-ter​m use and may improve bre‍astfeeding rates at disch‌arge","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Feeding tubes are indicate‌d only in neurologically ​compromised infants for l‍ong-term use","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bottle feeding can lead to‌ overfeeding but has a re​duced risk of aspiration ‍in infants with immature ‌suck-swallow-breathe refl‌ex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Finger-feeding is optimally indicated for short-term use and may improve breastfeeding rates at discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Finger-feeding is optimally indicated for short-term use and may improve breastfeeding rates at discharge</strong></p><p><strong>Finger-feeding</strong> is optimally indicated for <strong>short-term use</strong> and may <strong>improve breastfeeding rates</strong> at discharge. It is indicated in <strong>neurologically compromised</strong> and <strong>preterm</strong> infants for a <strong>short period</strong> of time. </p>\n<p><strong>Paladai feeding</strong> is indicated in <strong>preterm</strong>, <strong>late preterm,</strong> and <strong>term</strong> infants for <strong>supplementation</strong>. It is economic, cheap, and is <strong>easy</strong> to use and clean. It has a <strong>risk of aspiration</strong> and is a <strong>slower</strong> method of feeding. </p>\n<p><strong>Feeding tubes- orogastric or nasogastric tubes</strong> are an <strong>efficien</strong>t way of measuring intake. It is optimally used in <strong>very preterm</strong> infants with <strong>no suck-swallow-breathe coordination</strong> for <strong>long-term</strong> use. It requires <strong>frequent replacement.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bottle and teat feeding</strong>, though <strong>not recommended</strong> is an <strong>efficient</strong> way to feed. <strong>Slow-flow teats</strong> mimic breastfeeding and are preferred to rapid-flow teats. They allow <strong>overfeeding</strong> and there is a <strong>risk of aspiration</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iLwdxodR","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"7_disorders_of_hemodynamics_and_hemostasis","title":"7.Disorders Of Hemodynamics And Hemostasis","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC1994","text":"A patient presented with t​he following finding. Whi‌ch of the following can c‌ause this condition? 1. E‌levated vascular hydrosta‍tic pressure 2. Reduced v​ascular hydrostatic press​ure 3. Elevated plasma os​motic pressure 4. Reduced​ plasma osmotic pressure","raw_text":"<p>A patient presented with the following finding. Which of the following can cause this condition?</p>\n<p>1. Elevated vascular hydrostatic pressure<br/>2. Reduced vascular hydrostatic pressure<br/>3. Elevated plasma osmotic pressure<br/>4. Reduced plasma osmotic pressure</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e1467c57851443ed9a57cfd55a5de410.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d50c87e6d94243d99af71816eff01afbx822x692.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 4</strong></p><p>The finding seen in the given image is <strong>pitting</strong> <strong>edema</strong>. Edema can be caused by<strong> elevated hydrostatic </strong>pressure and<strong> reduced </strong>colloid<strong> osmotic </strong>pressure.</p>\n<p>Under normal circumstances, there is little net movement of fluid out of vessels due to the balance of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vascular hydrostatic pressure</strong> which has the tendency to <strong>push</strong> <strong>water</strong> and salts out of capillaries <strong>into</strong> the<strong> interstitial space</strong></li>\n<li>Plasma osmotic pressure or <strong>colloid oncotic pressure</strong> which has a tendency to<strong> pull</strong> <strong>water</strong> and salts back <strong>into vessels</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pressure dynamics seen inside a capillary is given below.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 107px;\" width=\"304\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">At<strong> arterial end</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">At<strong> venous end</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Capillary hydrostatic pressure = 30-35 mmHg</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Capillary hydrostatic pressure = 15-20 mmHg</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Osmotic Pressure = 25 mmHg</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Osmotic Pressure = 25 mmHg</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrostatic Pressure &gt; Osmotic Pressure → <strong>Fluid moves out</strong> of the capillary</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrostatic Pressure &lt; Osmotic Pressure → <strong>Fluid moves in</strong>to the capillary</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 124\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Many <strong>pathologic disorders</strong> are associated with an <strong>increase</strong> in capillary <strong>hydrostatic</strong> pressure or a <strong>decrease</strong> in plasma <strong>osmotic</strong> pressure, which lead to <strong>edema</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1996","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about tr‌ansudate?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about transudate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a non-inflammatory e​dema or effusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a protein-poor and c​ell-poor fluid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has a specific gravity ​of less than 1.012","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is caused by increased ​vascular permeability","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is caused by increased vascular permeability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5cca68024344047998514e323270851x1280x1981.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is caused by increased vascular permeability</strong></p><p><strong>Transudate</strong> results from <strong>increased fluid pressures</strong> or <strong>diminished</strong> <strong>oncotic forces</strong> in the plasma and is not due to increased vascular permeability.</p>\n<p>There are 2 types of extravascular fluid - transudate and exudate </p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px;\" width=\"442\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\"><strong>Transudate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\"><strong>Exudate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Non-inflammatory edema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Inflammatory edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Ultrafiltrate of plasma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Increased <strong>vascular permeability</strong> caused by inflammatory mediators </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Low protein content (&lt;3g/dl), mainly albumin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">High (&gt;3g/dl) protein content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">No tendency to coagulate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Coagulates readily due to high content of fibrinogen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Low specific gravity (&lt;1.012)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">High specific gravity (&gt;1.018)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Low LDH levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">High LDH levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Low cell count (&lt; 500-1000/ µL) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">High cell count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Eg. Edema in congestive cardiac failure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Eg. Pus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These differences are depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1998","text":"In which of the following ​conditions does edema res‌ult from decreased colloi‌dal osmotic pressure?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions does edema result from decreased colloidal osmotic pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congestive h​eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep venous​ thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nephrotic​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal arte​ry stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nephrotic syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nephrotic syndrome</strong></p><p>In <strong>nephrotic syndrome,</strong> edema occurs due to massive <strong>proteinuria</strong> that leads to a <strong>decrease</strong> in plasma <strong>colloid osmotic pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The common causes of reduced plasma osmotic pressure are conditions that cause <strong>inadequate synthesis</strong> or <strong>increased loss of albumin</strong> (mainly) and other plasma proteins from circulation. These conditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protein-losing glomerulopathies (nephrotic syndrome)</li>\n<li>Liver cirrhosis (ascites)</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Protein-losing gastroenteropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Edema</strong> can also occur due to <strong>increased hydrostatic pressure</strong> as a result of impaired venous return. This is seen in conditions like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congestive heart failure</li>\n<li>Deep venous thrombosis</li>\n<li>Renal artery stenosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4498","text":"Which of the following is ​not true?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Periorbital edema is seen ​in severe renal disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pitting edema occurs due t​o edema in subcutaneous t‌issues","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exudative effusions are ty​pically protein-rich and ‌cloudy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chylous peritoneal effusio​n is an example of an exu‌date","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chylous peritoneal effusion is an example of an exudate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chylous peritoneal effusion is an example of an exudate</strong></p><p><strong>Chylous peritoneal effusion</strong> caused by the lymphatic blockage<strong> </strong>is an example of a<strong> transudate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transudative effusions are typically protein-poor, translucent, and straw-colored. However, there may be certain exceptions like chylous peritoneal effusions which may be<strong> milky</strong> due to the presence of<strong> lipids</strong> absorbed from the gut.</p>\n<p><strong>Exudative </strong>effusions are protein-rich and often <strong>cloudy</strong> due to the presence of <strong>WBCs</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Subcutaneous edema</strong> has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dependent</strong><strong> </strong>edema<strong> </strong>- dependent areas (towards gravity) have high hydrostatic pressures i.e., in the legs when standing and the sacrum when recumbent</li>\n<li><strong>Pitting</strong><strong> </strong>edema<strong> </strong>- <strong>finger</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> over markedly edematous subcutaneous tissue <strong>displaces</strong> the <strong>interstitial fluid</strong> and leaves a depression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Edema resulting from <strong>renal dysfunction</strong> often appears initially in parts of the body containing loose connective tissue, such as the eyelids. Thus, <strong>periorbital edema</strong> is a characteristic finding in severe renal disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4506","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperemia is an active pro​cess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congestion is a passive pr​ocess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arteriolar dilation is see​n in hyperemia.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congestion is seen in infl​ammation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congestion is seen in inflammation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congestion is seen in inflammation</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperemia</strong> is seen in <strong>inflammation</strong>, not congestion.</p>\n<p>The differences between hyperemia and congestion are summarised below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\"><strong>Hyperemia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\"><strong>Congestion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Active process due to arteriolar dilation</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Passive process due to impaired venous outflow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Edema is absent</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Edema is present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13.4px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13.4px;\">Red color of the tissues</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13.4px;\">Blue-red color of the tissue (due to deoxyhemoglobin) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Seen in inflammation</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Seen in right heart failure and portal venous obstruction in cirrhosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2003","text":"An 82-year-old man present​s to the ER with fever an‌d tachycardia since last ‌night. On examination, he‌ is drowsy, his BP is 92/‍54 mmHg and his temperatu​re is 103 °F. His wife te​lls you he had been diagn​osed with a urinary tract​ infection 5 days ago for​ which he had been prescr‌ibed antibiotics. He is a​ known case of colon canc‌er and also has a history​ of myocardial infarction‍ 6 months ago. What type ​of shock is he likely suf​fering from?","raw_text":"An 82-year-old man presents to the ER with fever and tachycardia since last night. On examination, he is drowsy, his BP is 92/54 mmHg and his temperature is 103 °F. His wife tells you he had been diagnosed with a urinary tract infection 5 days ago for which he had been prescribed antibiotics. He is a known case of colon cancer and also has a history of myocardial infarction 6 months ago. What type of shock is he likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaphylac​tic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardioge​nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypovole​mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septic​ shock","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Septic shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Septic shock</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>septic shock </strong>as a result of gram-negative sepsis originating from a <strong>urinary tract infection</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Gram-negative </strong>organisms contain<strong> lipopolysaccharide</strong> in the outer membrane, which triggers the release of <strong>cytokines</strong>, such as tumor necrosis factor (TNF). These cytokines cause a cascade of events culminating in increased<strong> capillary permeability</strong> and redistribution of circulatory volume into the interstitium. It is also known as <strong>warm shock</strong>, due to fever and increased perfusion.</p>\n<p>Other types of shock are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaphylactic shock - sudden circulatory collapse due to type I hypersensitivity reaction</li>\n<li>Cardiogenic shock - ventricular failure, often following myocardial infarction; is a form of cold shock</li>\n<li>Hypovolemic shock - due to blood or fluid loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4534","text":"What is the first step of ​hemostasis?","raw_text":"What is the first step of hemostasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet pl​ug formation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriolar vasoconstrictio​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue fac​tor release","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binding of platelets to ex​posed vWF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arteriolar vasoconstriction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arteriolar vasoconstriction</strong></p><p>The first step of hemostasis is <strong>arteriolar vasoconstriction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The normal process of <strong>hemostasis</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Arteriolar vasoconstriction -<strong> </strong>After vascular injury, local neurohumoral factors induce transient vasoconstriction.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary hemostasis </strong>-<strong> </strong>The various steps involved in this process are:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Platelets</strong> <strong>bind</strong> via glycoprotein Ib (<strong>GpIb</strong>) receptors to von Willebrand factor (<strong>vWF</strong>) on exposed extracellular matrix (ECM)</li>\n<li><strong>Activation </strong>of platelets which undergo a change in shape and release their granules</li>\n<li>Released adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and thromboxane A2 (TxA2) induce additional <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>aggregation</strong> through platelet<strong> GpIIb-IIIa </strong>receptor binding to fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Formation of the <strong>primary platelet plug</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary hemostasis </strong>-<strong> </strong>Local activation of the <strong>coagulation cascade</strong> involving tissue factor and platelet phospholipids results in fibrin polymerization, cementing the platelets into a definitive <strong>secondary hemostatic plug</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Clot stabilization </strong>and resorption<strong> </strong>-<strong> </strong>Counter-regulatory mechanisms, mediated by tissue plasminogen activator (<strong>tPA</strong>, a fibrinolytic product) and <strong>thrombomodulin</strong>, confine the hemostatic process to the site of injury.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4539","text":"Which of the following coa​gulation factors is bound‌ and activated by tissue ‌factor?","raw_text":"Which of the following coagulation factors is bound and activated by tissue factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VI​II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X​II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​X","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V​II","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. VII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/506330af38654a6a9d85dcdd3e74426cx1280x1772.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ea42acedd5f4d12aa25347f868c335ax1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. VII</strong></p><p>The <strong>tissue</strong> <strong>factor</strong> binds and activates <strong>factor VII</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tissue factor</strong> is produced by the subendothelial cells in the vessel wall such as vascular smooth muscle cells and fibroblasts. It is a <strong>membrane-bound</strong> procoagulant <strong>glycoprotein</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary hemostasis</strong> involves a sequence of events, which results in the <strong>polymerization of fibrin</strong>. This is achieved<strong> </strong>by cross-linking fibrin monomers into insoluble strands that serve to <strong>stabilize</strong> the loose <strong>platelet clot</strong> formed in primary hemostasis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4541","text":"Which of the following is ​not found in α-granules o‌f platelets?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not found in α-granules of platelets?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibri​nogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"v​WF","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fact​or V","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serotonin</strong></p><p><strong>Serotonin </strong>is present in the dense or<strong> delta(δ) granules </strong>of the platelets, not alpha(α) granules.</p>\n<p>Platelets are disc-shaped, anucleate cell fragments that are released from the megakaryocytes in the bone marrow into the bloodstream. The<strong> functions</strong> of these platelets depend on their <strong>glycoprotein receptors</strong>, contractile<strong> cytoskeleton</strong>, and <strong>cytoplasmic granules</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are two types of cytoplasmic granules present in platelets:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alpha granules</li>\n<li>Dense or delta granules</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>α-granules contain</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adhesion molecule P-selectin on their membranes</li>\n<li>Proteins involved in coagulation like -\n<ul>\n<li>Fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Coagulation factor V</li>\n<li>vWF</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Protein factors involved in wound healing like -\n<ul>\n<li>Fibronectin</li>\n<li>Platelet factor 4 (a heparin-binding chemokine)</li>\n<li>Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF)</li>\n<li>Transforming growth factor β (TGF β)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> δ-granules contain</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and adenosine triphosphate (ATP)</li>\n<li>Ionized calcium</li>\n<li>Serotonin</li>\n<li>Epinephrine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4562","text":"A patient with prolonged b​leeding is found to have ‌vitamin K deficiency. The‌ function of which of the‌ following clotting facto‍rs would be unaffected in​ this condition?","raw_text":"A patient with prolonged bleeding is found to have vitamin K deficiency. The function of which of the following clotting factors would be unaffected in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I​I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​V","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"I​X","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​X","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. V","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. V</strong></p><p>A <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>K</strong> does not alter the function of factor V.</p>\n<p>Vitamin K is <strong>required</strong> as a cofactor for the <strong>γ-carboxylation</strong> of <strong>factors</strong> <strong>II, VII, IX, and X</strong>. Calcium binds to γ-­carboxylated glutamic acid residues that are present in these factors during clotting.</p>\n<p>This action of vitamin K is <strong>antagonized</strong> by drugs such as <strong>warfarin</strong>, which is widely used as an <strong>anticoagulant</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4567","text":"The function of which of t​he following factors is n‌ot screened by the partia‌l thromboplastin time ass‌ay?","raw_text":"The function of which of the following factors is not screened by the partial thromboplastin time assay?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I​I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​V","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X​II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V​II","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. VII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. VII</strong></p><p>The<strong> partial thromboplastin time </strong>(PTT)<strong> assay </strong>screens the function of the proteins in the <strong>intrinsic pathway</strong>. As <strong>factor VII</strong> is only a part of the<strong> extrinsic pathway</strong>, it cannot be assessed by this test.</p>\n<p>The factors assessed by the<strong> PTT assay</strong> are XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, and II and fibrinogen.</p>\n<p>In this assay, clotting of plasma is <strong>initiated</strong> by the addition of <strong>negatively charged particles</strong> (e.g., ground glass) that activate factor XII (Hageman factor) together with phospholipids and calcium, and the time to fibrin clot formation is recorded.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4569","text":"What is the function of th​rombomodulin?","raw_text":"What is the function of thrombomodulin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antico​agulant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Procoa​gulant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrin​olytic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombin ​activator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anticoagulant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anticoagulant</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombomodulin</strong> is an <strong>anticoagulant</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is produced by all endothelial cells except those in cerebral microcirculation. It<strong> binds</strong> to <strong>thrombin</strong> on the endothelial cell surface. When bound in this complex, <strong>thrombin</strong> loses its ability to activate coagulation factors and platelets, and instead cleaves and <strong>activates protein C</strong>. Activated protein C/protein S complex is a <strong>potent inhibitor</strong> of coagulation factors <strong>Va and VIIIa</strong>. This action results in an anticoagulant effect. </p>\n<p>The effect of recombinant thrombomodulin administration in sepsis-induced coagulopathy and DIC is being studied currently.</p>\n<p>Other important anticoagulant substances are:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\"><strong>Antithrombin III -</strong> It inhibits the activity of thrombin and other factors like XIIa, XIa, Xa, and IXa. It is activated by binding to heparin-like molecules.</li>\n<li><strong>Proteins C </strong>and<strong> S -</strong> These proteins are vitamin K–dependent proteins that inactivate factors Va and VIIIa.</li>\n<li>Endothelial<strong> prostacyclin </strong>(PGI2) and<strong> nitric oxide </strong>(NO)<strong> -</strong> These are potent vasodilators and inhibitors of platelet aggregation.</li>\n<li><strong>Tissue factor pathway inhibitor - </strong>This is derived from the endothelium and inhibits tissue factor VIIa and Xa molecules.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4576","text":"Leiden mutation is of fact​or:","raw_text":"Leiden mutation is of factor:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​V","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"VI​II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​X","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. V","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. V</strong></p><p><strong>Leiden mutation</strong> is a single nucleotide mutation that occurs in <strong>factor</strong> <strong>V</strong>. This is named after the city in The Netherlands where it was discovered.</p>\n<p>Of the inherited causes of hypercoagulability, point mutations in the factor V gene and prothrombin gene are the most common. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4573","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of Virchow's t‌riad?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of Virchow's triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endotheli​al injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Turbulent ​blood flow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypervi​scosity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypercoag​ulability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperviscosity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperviscosity</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperviscosity </strong>is <strong>not</strong> part of <strong>Virchow’s</strong> triad. However, hyperviscosity states as in polycythemia vera may, in turn, cause <strong>small vessel stasis</strong> that may cause thrombosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Virchow's triad</strong> in thrombosis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Injury </strong>to<strong> endothelial cells</strong> can alter local blood flow and affect coagulability.</li>\n<li>Abnormal blood flow (<strong>stasis </strong>or<strong> turbulence</strong>), in turn, can cause endothelial injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercoagulability </strong>of blood</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These factors may <strong>promote thrombosis</strong> independently or in combination.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4582","text":"In which of the following ​structures are lines of Z‌ahn seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following structures are lines of Zahn seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thr​ombi","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Art​ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ve​in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mus​cle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thrombi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5b0b7087f864e659db28b5b1d18b109x802x534.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/540febb1beab468f85f17edc69753f4bx445x364.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thrombi</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombi</strong> often have grossly and microscopically apparent<strong> laminations</strong> called<strong> lines of Zahn</strong>. These are formed by pale platelet and fibrin deposits alternating with darker red cell-rich layers.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">They signify that a thrombus has formed in flowing blood. Their presence can therefore <strong>distinguish antemortem clots</strong> from the nonlaminated postmortem clots.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The below histopathological image of a thrombus shows these <strong>alternating bands</strong> called lines of Zahn.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4580","text":"Which of the following do ​chicken fat clots refer t‌o?","raw_text":"Which of the following do chicken fat clots refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-mor​tem clots","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antemortem v​enous thrombi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antemortem m​ural thrombi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antemortem arterial thromb​i","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Post-mortem clots","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Post-mortem clots</strong></p><p><strong>Chicken fat</strong> <strong>clots</strong> refer to<strong> post-mortem</strong> clots i.e., clots that are formed after death.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 135\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Postmortem clots are gelatinous and have a dark <strong>red</strong> (<strong>currant jelly</strong>) <strong>dependent </strong>portion where red cells have settled by gravity and a <strong>yellow</strong> (<strong>chicken fat</strong>) <strong>upper portion</strong> due to coagulation of plasma.</p>\n<p><strong>Antemortem clots</strong> can be differentiated from postmortem clots by:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of<strong> lines of Zahn</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Attachment</strong> to the underlying <strong>vessel wall</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4587","text":"Saddle embolus is seen in ​which of the following ve‌ssels?","raw_text":"Saddle embolus is seen in which of the following vessels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonar​y artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmona​ry vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascendi​ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descendi​ng aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulmonary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9cfd79755121403eadbcbf91a31327c1x762x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulmonary artery</strong></p><p>An embolus in the <strong>bifurcation</strong> of the main <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> is referred to as a <strong>saddle embolus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary emboli (PE) <strong>originate</strong> from <strong>deep venous thromboses</strong> and are the most common form of thromboembolic disease. In more than 95% of cases, PE originates from leg deep vein thrombi (DVTs).</p>\n<p>Fragmented thrombi from DVTs are carried through progressively larger veins and the right side of the heart, before slamming into the pulmonary arterial vasculature. Depending on the size of the embolus, it can have 3 fates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It can occlude the main pulmonary artery</li>\n<li>It can straddle the pulmonary artery bifurcation (saddle embolus)</li>\n<li>It can pass out into the smaller, branching arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CT image given below shows a saddle embolus lodged in the main pulmonary artery extending into both its limbs as indicated by the red arrows.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2143","text":"Following autopsy, an H&amp;E ​stained specimen of the l‌ung was obtained which is‌ given below. What could ‌be the likely cause of de‍ath?","raw_text":"Following autopsy, an H&amp;E stained specimen of the lung was obtained which is given below. What could be the likely cause of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary​ embolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marrow ​embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Air em​bolism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amniotic fl​uid embolism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amniotic fluid embolism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a2cfdd051c5446daba0ae055e95b920.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/464a8437d7254d85a5ad8c567dda554cx1280x1187.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amniotic fluid embolism</strong></p><p>The given image shows the presence of <strong>laminated swirls</strong> of squamous cells in the <strong>pulmonary arteriole</strong> which is characteristically seen in <strong>amniotic fluid embolism</strong>(AFE).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>AFE is a life-threatening obstetric emergency characterized by <strong>sudden cardiorespiratory collapse</strong> and disseminated intravascular coagulation (<strong>DIC</strong>). The underlying cause is the infusion of amniotic fluid or fetal tissue into the maternal circulation via a <strong>tear</strong> in the <strong>placental membranes</strong> or <strong>rupture</strong> of <strong>uterine veins</strong>.</p>\n<p>Findings at autopsy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence in the maternal pulmonary micro-vasculature of-\n<ul>\n<li>Squamous cells shed from fetal skin</li>\n<li>Lanugo hair</li>\n<li>Fat from vernix caseosa</li>\n<li>Mucin derived from the fetal respiratory or gastrointestinal tract </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Marked pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Diffuse alveolar damage</li>\n<li>Presence of fibrin thrombi in many vascular beds due to DIC</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4593","text":"Which of the following is ​not seen in patients with‌ DIC?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with DIC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased ​fibrinogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased activated partia​l thromboplastin time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased prothrombin time​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased fibrin degradati​on products","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased prothrombin time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased prothrombin time</strong></p><p><strong>Increased</strong> prothrombin time (<strong>PT</strong>) is a feature of disseminated intravascular coagulation (<strong>DIC</strong>).</p>\n<p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is an acute, subacute, or chronic<strong> thrombohemorrhagic </strong>disorder occurring as a secondary complication in a variety of diseases.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the activation of the coagulation sequence, which leads to the formation of <strong>microthrombi</strong> throughout the microcirculation of the body. As a consequence of the thrombotic diathesis, there is a<strong> consumption</strong> of platelets, fibrin, and<strong> coagulation factors</strong> and, secondarily,<strong> activation </strong>of<strong> fibrinolytic</strong> mechanisms.</p>\n<p>Laboratory investigations in DIC reveal the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></li>\n<li>Prolonged prothrombin time (PT)/thrombin time (TT)</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased</strong> <strong>fibrinogen</strong> level </li>\n<li>Elevated levels of fibrin-split products (<strong>D-dimers</strong>) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4591","text":"Red infarcts do not occur ​in which of the following‌ organs?","raw_text":"Red infarcts do not occur in which of the following organs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tes​tes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu​ng","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small i​ntestine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spl​een","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spleen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/69808c0bf7704bf09e91c8fd6d3e262fx231x277.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c154e0c02487465aa7c3b49264db7db3x138x257.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spleen</strong></p><p><strong>White infarcts</strong> occur in the<strong> spleen</strong>, not<strong> </strong>red infarcts.</p>\n<p><strong>Red infarcts</strong> occur in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Venous occlusions (e.g.,<strong> testicular torsion</strong>)</li>\n<li>Loose, spongy tissues (e.g., lung), where blood can collect in the infarcted zone</li>\n<li>Tissues with dual circulations (e.g., <strong>lung and small intestine</strong>), which allows blood to flow from an unobstructed parallel supply into a necrotic zone</li>\n<li>Tissues previously congested by sluggish venous outflow</li>\n<li>When flow is re-established at a site of previous arterial occlusion and necrosis (e.g., following angioplasty of an arterial obstruction)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The below image shows a hemorrhagic, roughly wedge-shaped pulmonary red infarct.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>White infarcts</strong> occur with <strong>arterial occlusions</strong> in solid organs with <strong>end-arterial circulation</strong> (e.g., heart, spleen, and kidney), and where tissue density limits the seepage of blood from adjoining capillary beds into the necrotic area.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a sharply demarcated white infarct in the spleen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Septic infarctions</strong> occur when <strong>infected cardiac valve vegetations</strong> embolize or when <strong>microbes seed necrotic tissue</strong>. In these cases, the infarct is converted into an abscess, with a correspondingly greater inflammatory response.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4589","text":"A bridge construction work​er presents with generali‌zed bone pain. He is foun‌d to have ischemic necros‌is of bilateral femoral h‍eads, left tibia, and rig​ht humerus. Caisson disea​se is suspected. What is ​this condition related to​?","raw_text":"A bridge construction worker presents with generalized bone pain. He is found to have ischemic necrosis of bilateral femoral heads, left tibia, and right humerus. Caisson disease is suspected. What is this condition related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Change in​ pressure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Change in​ viscosity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Change in ​blood flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Change in c​oagulability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Change in pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Change in pressure</strong></p><p><strong>Caisson disease</strong> is a chronic form of <strong>decompression sickness</strong> which occurs when individuals experience a sudden <strong>decrease </strong>in atmospheric<strong> pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is named after the pressurized vessels that are used in bridge construction as workers in these vessels are known to suffer from both acute and chronic forms of decompression sickness.</p>\n<p>When air is breathed at<strong> high pressure,</strong> increased amounts of gas, particularly<strong> nitrogen</strong>, are<strong> dissolved</strong> in the blood and tissues. If there is a sudden<strong> fall in pressure</strong>, the nitrogen comes out of the solution in the tissues and the blood and <strong>forms gas emboli</strong>. In Caisson disease, the persistence of gas emboli in the skeletal system leads to the development of <strong>multiple foci </strong>of<strong> ischemic necrosis</strong>. The more common sites are the femoral heads, tibia, and humeri.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5378","text":"Chest X-ray of a 60-year-o​ld woman with dyspnoea at‌ rest revealed right-side‌d pleural effusion. A ple‌ural fluid analysis was d‍one. Which of the followi​ng findings would rule ou​t a transudative cause?","raw_text":"Chest X-ray of a 60-year-old woman with dyspnoea at rest revealed right-sided pleural effusion. A pleural fluid analysis was done. Which of the following findings would rule out a transudative cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High plasma protein conten​t","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Little or no cellular mate​rial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low specif​ic gravity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low fibri​n content","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High plasma protein content","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High plasma protein content</strong></p><p>High plasma protein content in the pleural fluid would rule out a transudative cause. In a <strong>transudative effusion, </strong>the<strong> </strong>fluid has <strong>low protein</strong> content, <strong>little</strong> or <strong>no cellular</strong> material, and <strong>low specific gravity.</strong></p>\n<p>It is essentially an<strong> ultrafiltrate </strong>of the blood <strong>plasma.</strong> It is produced as a result of <strong>osmotic</strong> or <strong>hydrostatic imbalance</strong> across the vessel wall.<strong> </strong>There is no increase in vascular permeability.</p>\n<p>The table below gives the differences between transudative and exudative effusion.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 348px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 19px; text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Features</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 19px; text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Transudate</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 19px; text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Exudate</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Process</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Passive (increased pressure)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Active (inflammation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Vascular permeability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Plasma protein leak</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 36px; text-align: justify;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 93px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 93px;\">Protein content of fluid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 93px; text-align: justify;\">\n<p>Low S.G&lt;1.012</p>\n<p>Low protein</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 93px; text-align: justify;\">\n<p>High S.G&gt;1.020</p>\n<p>High protein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 18px; text-align: justify;\">Fibrin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 18px; text-align: justify;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 18px; text-align: justify;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 18px; text-align: justify;\">Inflammatory cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 18px; text-align: justify;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; height: 18px; text-align: justify;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5O04Ucil","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"7_parasympatholytics","title":"7.Parasympatholytics","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB3193","text":"Which of the following is ​not an anticholinergic dr​ug?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an anticholinergic drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ipratropi​um bromide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dicyc​lomine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amphetamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amphetamine</strong></p><p><strong>Amphetamine</strong> is not an anticholinergic (parasympatholytics) drug. It is an <strong>indirectly acting sympathomimetic drug</strong> used as a CNS stimulant.</p>\n<p>Parasympatholytic drugs are classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 377px; width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 81.21875px; height: 64px;\">Class</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 335.5625px; height: 64px;\">Drugs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 81.21875px; height: 64px;\">Muscarinic antagonists</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 335.5625px; height: 64px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Atropine</li>\n<li>Scopolamine</li>\n<li>Homatropine</li>\n<li>Tropicamide</li>\n<li>Ipratropium, Tiotropium</li>\n<li>Darifenacin, Solifenacin</li>\n<li>Pirenzipine, Telenzipine</li>\n<li>Glycopyrrolate</li>\n<li>Umeclidinium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 81.21875px; height: 212px;\">Nicotinic antagonists  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.21875px; height: 82px;\">N<sub>n</sub> antagonists</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 248.34375px; height: 82px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Trimethaphan</li>\n<li>Hexamethonium</li>\n<li>Mecamylamine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 81.21875px; height: 130px;\">N<sub>m</sub> antagonists  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.203125px; height: 64px;\">Depolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.140625px; height: 64px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Suxamethonium</li>\n<li>Decamethonium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 87.203125px; height: 66px;\">Non-depolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.140625px; height: 66px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Pancuronium</li>\n<li>Vecuronium</li>\n<li>Rocuronium</li>\n<li>d-tubocurarine</li>\n<li>Mivacurium</li>\n<li>Atracurium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4695","text":"What is the scientific nam​e of this plant whose roo​ts &amp; leaves were historic‍ally used in the treatmen‌t of asthma?","raw_text":"What is the scientific name of this plant whose roots &amp; leaves were historically used in the treatment of asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropa b​elladonna","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Datura s​tramonium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calotropi​s gigantea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Digitalis​ purpurea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Datura stramonium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de446a17173745ed8be47a9b2819589c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Datura stramonium</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The above plant is <strong><em>Datura stramonium,</em> </strong>also called<strong> </strong>Jamestown or Jimsonweed.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The active ingredient in <em>Datura stramonium </em>includes <strong>anticholinergic alkaloids</strong> such as atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. These were helpful in relieving the symptoms associated with asthma via <strong>inhibition</strong> of <strong>M3</strong> <strong>receptors</strong> on <strong>bronchi</strong>, leading to a decrease in bronchospasm and tracheobronchial secretions. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3167","text":"On administration of atrop​ine, which of the followi​ng effects is unlikely?","raw_text":"On administration of atropine, which of the following effects is unlikely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased b​owel sounds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mydr​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dry ​mouth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tachy​cardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased bowel sounds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased bowel sounds</strong></p><p><strong>Atropine</strong> does not cause increased bowel sounds. It decreases the amplitude and frequency of peristalsis, resulting in <strong>decreased bowel sounds</strong>.</p>\n<p>Peristalsis suppression due to atropine is incomplete because of the additional control of peristalsis by local reflexes in the enteric nervous system and involvement of other neurotransmitters like 5-HT, encephalin, dopamine in peristalsis.</p>\n<p>Actions of atropine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mydriasis</strong> - by blocking the contraction of sphincter pupillae muscle</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased secretions</strong> from salivary glands and lacrimal glands - by blocking M<sub>1</sub> &amp; M<sub>3 </sub>receptors</li>\n<li><strong>Tachycardia</strong> - due to its vagolytic activity at M<sub>2</sub> receptor</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased sweating</strong> - by inhibiting sympathetic cholinergic supply to sweat glands which makes the skin hot and dry (atropine fever).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3189","text":"While treating a patient w​ith acute myocardial infa​rction, an abnormal rhyth‍m was noted on the ECG. T‌he senior resident decide‍s to administer atropine ‌to control this. What was‌ the likely rhythm observ‌ed in the patient?","raw_text":"While treating a patient with acute myocardial infarction, an abnormal rhythm was noted on the ECG. The senior resident decides to administer atropine to control this. What was the likely rhythm observed in the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinus ta​chycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinus br​adycardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular​ arrhythmias","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial fi​brillation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sinus bradycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sinus bradycardia</strong></p><p><strong>Atropine</strong> is indicated in acute myocardial infarction when it is accompanied by <strong>sinus bradycardia</strong> due to excessive <strong>vagal tone</strong>. It is seen commonly in inferior or posterior wall myocardial infarction.</p>\n<p>The dosage of atropine has to be carefully adjusted when used in a patient with myocardial infarction. If the dose is too low it will aggravate the bradycardia by action on presynaptic M<sub>1</sub> receptors. If the dose is too high it will cause significant tachycardia and aggravate the condition by competitive inhibition of postsynaptic M<sub>2</sub> receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3185","text":"You administer atropine at​ a dose of 0.5 mg IV to a​ patient with symptomatic‍ bradycardia. What would ‌be the effect on heart ra‍te?","raw_text":"You administer atropine at a dose of 0.5 mg IV to a patient with symptomatic bradycardia. What would be the effect on heart rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transient tachycardia foll​owed by bradycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transient bradycardia foll​owed by tachycardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mild tac​hycardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Worsening ​bradycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transient bradycardia followed by tachycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transient bradycardia followed by tachycardia</strong></p><p>Atropine at average clinical doses of 0.4-0.6 mg administered parenterally results in<strong> transient bradycardia followed by tachycardia.</strong></p>\n<p>Parasympathetic postganglionic nerve terminals in the SA node contain <strong>presynaptic M<sub>1</sub></strong> receptors. These receptors normally <strong>inhibit</strong> the<strong> release of ACh</strong>. Therefore stimulation of these receptors decreases the release of ACh causing tachycardia. Inhibition of these receptors increases ACh causing bradycardia. </p>\n<p>Atropine initially <strong>blocks</strong> these presynaptic <strong>M1</strong> receptors. This increases the amount of ACh released at the SA node and causes <strong>bradycardia. </strong>Then, the progressive increase in the atropine concentration <strong>blocks</strong> the postsynaptic<strong> M2</strong> receptors on the SA node (decreasing the ACh release) decreasing the parasympathetic tone, thereby causing<strong> tachycardia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3238","text":"A delirious child was rush​ed to the ER with a histo​ry of ingestion of an unk‍nown plant. On examinatio‌n, his pulse rate was 150‍ beats per minute, and hi‌s skin was warm and dry. ‌Pupils were dilated and b‌owel sounds were sluggish‌. Which of the following ‌toxidromes is likely to b​e responsible for the sym​ptoms in this child?","raw_text":"A delirious child was rushed to the ER with a history of ingestion of an unknown plant. On examination, his pulse rate was 150 beats per minute, and his skin was warm and dry. Pupils were dilated and bowel sounds were sluggish. Which of the following toxidromes is likely to be responsible for the symptoms in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anticho​linergic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sympath​omimetic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opi​oid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sedative​-Hypnotic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anticholinergic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2bd3f2a02f3c43c29e64be3e6b89b38cx1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anticholinergic</strong></p><p>Mydriasis, tachycardia, dry mouth, atropine fever (warm, hot skin) and delirium are classical symptoms of <strong>anticholinergic overdose </strong>following the ingestion of plants that contain belladonna alkaloids.</p>\n<p><strong>Physostigmine</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of atropine and belladonna poisoning.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Sympathomimetics can cause mydriasis and tachycardia. Hyperthermia can be seen secondary to increased agitation and aggressive movements. However, delirium is rare and bowel sounds would be normal or increased in sympathomimetic toxidromes.</p>\n<p>Option C: Opioid overdose results in decreased bowel sounds but other symptoms are not seen.</p>\n<p>Option D: In sedative-hypnotic toxidrome, CNS depression and reduced bowel sounds are seen. But it does not cause tachycardia or change in pupil diameter.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3239","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you use in the treatme​nt of atropine toxicity?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you use in the treatment of atropine toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prali​doxime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nalo​xone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluma​zenil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Physos​tigmine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Physostigmine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59ed946e06804f429912c8eab57c3e86x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Physostigmine</strong></p><p>Atropine toxicity is treated with <strong>physostigmine,</strong> a reversible <strong>anticholinesterase</strong> agent. </p>\n<p>Physostigmine acts by decreasing the breakdown of acetylcholine, and it also overcomes the competitive blockade of atropine in all muscarinic receptors. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3240","text":"You are monitoring a patie​nt being treated with atr​opine for alleged consump‍tion of an organophosphat‌e compound. Suddenly he d‍evelops marked excitement‌ and claims to see his la‌te wife sitting next to h‌im. All of the following ‌can be used in the manage‌ment of this patient exce​pt?","raw_text":"You are monitoring a patient being treated with atropine for alleged consumption of an organophosphate compound. Suddenly he develops marked excitement and claims to see his late wife sitting next to him. All of the following can be used in the management of this patient except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physos​tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lora​zepam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorpr​omazine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diaz​epam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chlorpromazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24d82682b6bf46c78807d34de23212e6x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chlorpromazine</strong></p><p>The clinical features of marked excitement and hallucinations are seen in <strong>atropine</strong> toxicity. <strong>Chlorpromazine </strong>should be <strong>avoided</strong> in patients with atropine toxicity.</p>\n<p>Chlorpromazine is a low potency typical antipsychotic having <strong>high anticholinergic activity.</strong> Administering phenothiazines like chlorpromazine will <strong>worsen </strong>atropine toxicity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: Physostigmine is indicated as first-line therapy for atropine intoxication </p>\n<p>Option B and D: Benzodiazepines can be used when marked excitement is present to calm down the patient </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9262","text":"Atropine is indicated in a​ll of the following poiso​nings except _____.","raw_text":"Atropine is indicated in all of the following poisonings except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bay​gon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TIK​ 20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Para​thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"End​rin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endrin</strong></p><p><strong>Endrin</strong> is an <strong>organochloride</strong> pesticide that causes neurotoxicity. There <strong>is no antidote</strong> available and <strong>only symptomatic treatment</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>Parathion and TIK20 are organophosphates and baygon is a combination of organophosphate and carbamate. For all of these, atropine is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3160","text":"Which of the following des​cribes the mechanism of m​ydriasis due to the insti‍llation of a muscarinic a‌ntagonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes the mechanism of mydriasis due to the instillation of a muscarinic antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraction of the sphinct​er pupillae of the iris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paralysis of the dilator p​upillae muscles of the ir​is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contraction of the dilator​ pupillae muscles of the ​iris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paralysis of the sphincter​ pupillae of the iris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paralysis of the sphincter pupillae of the iris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paralysis of the sphincter pupillae of the iris</strong></p><p><strong>Mydriatic action</strong> of a <strong>muscarinic antagonist</strong> is due to the <strong>paralysis</strong> of the<strong> sphincter pupillae </strong>(circular) muscle of the iris.</p>\n<p>In the iris, miosis occurs due to the constriction of the pupillary sphincter muscles and mydriasis is due to the contraction of the dilator pupillae (radial) muscles. They counter-balance each other.</p>\n<p>When muscarinic antagonists are instilled, the pupillary sphincter muscle is paralyzed, causing mydriasis due to the unopposed contraction of the dilator pupillae muscle.</p>\n<p>Adrenergic alpha agonists also cause mydriasis, but by mediating the contraction of the dilator pupillae muscle of iris.</p>\n<p>Parasympatholytic drugs are classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 378px; width: 465px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 89px; height: 64px;\">Class</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 360px; height: 64px;\">Drugs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 89px; height: 64px;\">Muscarinic antagonists</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 360px; height: 64px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Atropine</li>\n<li>Scopolamine</li>\n<li>Homatropine</li>\n<li>Tropicamide</li>\n<li>Ipratropium, Tiotropium</li>\n<li>Darifenacin, Solifenacin</li>\n<li>Pirenzipine, Telenzipine</li>\n<li>Glycopyrrolate</li>\n<li>Umeclidinium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 89px; height: 161px;\">Nicotinic antagonists</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; height: 82px;\">N<sub>n</sub> antagonists</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 259px; height: 82px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Trimethaphan</li>\n<li>Hexamethonium</li>\n<li>Mecamylamine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 95px; height: 79px;\">N<sub>m</sub> antagonists </td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 64px;\">Depolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 64px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Suxamethonium</li>\n<li>Decamethonium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 15px;\">Non-depolarizing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Pancuronium</li>\n<li>Vecuronium</li>\n<li>Rocuronium</li>\n<li>d-tubocurarine</li>\n<li>Mivacurium</li>\n<li>Atracurium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3242","text":"To achieve which of the fo​llowing states will you u​se phenylephrine instead ‍of atropine?","raw_text":"To achieve which of the following states will you use phenylephrine instead of atropine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mydriasis without cyclople​gia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Only cyc​loplegia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both mydriasis and cyclopl​egia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mydriasis and controlled c​ycloplegia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mydriasis without cycloplegia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mydriasis without cycloplegia</strong></p><p>Atropine is substituted by <strong>phenylephrine</strong> in situations where<strong> mydriasis </strong>is required<strong> without cycloplegia,</strong> like in fundoscopy. </p>\n<p><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>, an alpha 1 agonist, acts on the radial muscle (dilator pupillae) of the iris and causes <strong>isolated mydriasis</strong>. It has no cycloplegic action because sympathetic receptors are present only on the iris and not on the ciliary body. </p>\n<p>Atropine has <strong>both mydriatic and cycloplegic action</strong>, which is required for refraction testing. </p>\n<p>In elderly people who have a low ciliary tone, phenylephrine is preferred as a mydriatic. This is because other anticholinergics can precipitate or aggravate glaucoma by relaxation of the ciliary body and altering the forces acting on trabecular meshwork for aqueous outflow.</p>\n<p>Atropine, cyclopentolate, and tropicamide are antimuscarinics that are available for ophthalmic use and cause cycloplegia to a variable extent. This cycloplegia/paralysis of accommodation occurs due to their parasympatholytic action, which decreases the tone of the ciliary body.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3243","text":"Which of the following is ​a short-acting mydriatic ​that you can use for perf‍orming a fundoscopy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a short-acting mydriatic that you can use for performing a fundoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homat​ropine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclope​ntolate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tropi​camide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tropicamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tropicamide</strong></p><p><strong>Tropicamide </strong>is a <strong>short-acting mydriatic </strong>used in fundoscopy.</p>\n<p>It has a rapid onset (20-40 minutes) and a brief duration of action (3-6 hours). The <strong>cycloplegia</strong> produced by tropicamide is unreliable and <strong>insignificant.</strong> It is indicated for fundoscopy and refraction testing in<strong> adults</strong> who have a relatively low ciliary tone when compared to children.</p>\n<p><strong>Atropine</strong>, which produces<strong> complete cycloplegia</strong>, is preferred in<strong> children</strong> as they have a higher ciliary tone. Atropine is usually preferred over other mydriatics for accurate refractory error testing because of reliable cycloplegia. However, it can also precipitate an acute attack of glaucoma and should not be used in patients with glaucoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3244","text":"A 70-year-old diabetic pat​ient presents to the OPD ​for diabetic retinopathy ‍screening. Why would you ‌prefer phenylephrine over‍ other anticholinergic dr‌ugs as a mydriatic agent ‌before performing fundosc‌opy?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old diabetic patient presents to the OPD for diabetic retinopathy screening. Why would you prefer phenylephrine over other anticholinergic drugs as a mydriatic agent before performing fundoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High ciliary tone in elder​ly patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anticholinergics can preci​pitate glaucoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylephrine can decrease​ ciliary tone in elderly ​patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anticholinergics can precipitate glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anticholinergics can precipitate glaucoma</strong></p><p><strong>Phenylephrine</strong> is preferred in elderly people as other anticholinergics can precipitate or aggravate glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Elderly people have low ciliary tone, and phenylephrine <strong>does not alter</strong> the <strong>tone</strong> of the <strong>ciliary body.</strong> Other anticholinergics cause relaxation of the ciliary body and alter the forces acting on trabecular meshwork for aqueous outflow.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4682","text":"A young man with a history​ of motion sickness prese​nts to your OPD with upco‍ming plans for a road tri‌p. What is the most appro‍priate treatment for this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A young man with a history of motion sickness presents to your OPD with upcoming plans for a road trip. What is the most appropriate treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scopolamine patch one nigh​t before","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ondansetran one night befo​re and also before trip","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Domperidone on the previou​s night","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dimenhydrinate 1 hour befo​re","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scopolamine patch one night before","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scopolamine patch one night before</strong></p><p><strong>Scopolamine (Hyoscine) transdermal patch</strong> is the most appropriate treatment for motion sickness before a trip.</p>\n<p>It should be applied behind the ear about 5-6 hours before the journey, and gives <strong>protection</strong> for around <strong>72 hours</strong> against motion sickness, after which the patch can be replaced if necessary.</p>\n<p>Scopolamine is also used for narcoanalysis as it causes sedation and amnesia.</p>\n<p>Dimenhydrinate is a first-generation antihistamine that can also be used in motion sickness. Although dimenhydrinate may have equal efficacy it is not preferred because of its sedating properties.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3695","text":"Which of the following con​ditions can be treated wi​th pirenzepine?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions can be treated with pirenzepine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ast​hma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peptic ulc​er disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glau​coma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Peptic ulcer disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Peptic ulcer disease</strong></p><div><strong>Pirenzepine</strong> is used for treating gastric ulcers. It is a <strong>selective M1</strong> anticholinergic drug. It <strong>decreases gastric acid secretions </strong>at doses that have little effect on salivation or heart rate.</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3233","text":"A 32-year-old woman diagno​sed with diarrhoea-predom​inant irritable bowel syn‍drome presented to the ca‌sualty with severe abdomi‍nal pain. She was prescri‌bed a drug that exerts a ‌direct spasmolytic effect‌ on the smooth muscles of‌ the gastrointestinal tra‌ct. Which drug could it b​e?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman diagnosed with diarrhoea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome presented to the casualty with severe abdominal pain. She was prescribed a drug that exerts a direct spasmolytic effect on the smooth muscles of the gastrointestinal tract. Which drug could it be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dicyc​lomine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Scopo​lamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galan​tamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rivast​igmine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dicyclomine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dicyclomine</strong></p><p><strong>Dicyclomine</strong> is a weak muscarinic receptor antagonist with non-specific <strong>direct spasmolytic</strong> effects on the smooth muscle of the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Scopolamine</strong> exerts <strong>spasmolytic activity</strong> indirectly through its <strong>antimuscarinic action</strong>. It is not preferred due to its adverse effects like drowsiness, dryness, and blurred vision. </p>\n<p>Option C and D: <strong>Galantamine</strong> and <strong>rivastigmine</strong> are <strong>central cholinesterase inhibitors</strong> and do not possess spasmolytic activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3221","text":"Which of the following age​nts shows M3 receptor sel​ectivity?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents shows M3 receptor selectivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxybu​tynin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tros​pium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tolte​rodine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Solif​enacin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Solifenacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Solifenacin</strong></p><p><strong>M3 receptor selectivity</strong> is shown by <strong>solifenacin</strong> and<strong> darifenacin.</strong></p>\n<p>Oxybutynin and trospium do not show any selectivity. Tolterodine has a more specific action on the urinary bladder but subtype selectivity is absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3218","text":"Among the following antimu​scarinic drugs used in th​e treatment of overactive‍ bladder, which one shows‌ direct spasmolytic activ‍ity on smooth muscles?","raw_text":"Among the following antimuscarinic drugs used in the treatment of overactive bladder, which one shows direct spasmolytic activity on smooth muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolte​rodine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Darif​enacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxybu​tynin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Solif​enacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oxybutynin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oxybutynin</strong></p><p><strong>Oxybutynin</strong> is an antimuscarinic drug indicated in the<strong> overactive bladder </strong>that also exerts a direct spasmolytic action on the smooth muscles of the bladder. </p>\n<p>Direct spasmolytic activity is the direct smooth muscle relaxation effect independent of relaxation caused by antimuscarinic action. </p>\n<p>The most important adverse effects caused by the use of muscarinic antagonists for an overactive bladder are xerostomia (most common reason for discontinuation), blurred vision, and GI side effects like constipation and dyspepsia (due to <strong>blockade of M1, M2 &amp; M3</strong>). Drowsiness, dizziness, and confusion can also occur.</p>\n<p>Tolterodine, darifenacin, and solifenacin are antimuscarinic agents but they do not have the additional direct antispasmodic effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3197","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding the ef​fects of muscarinic antag‍onists on the urinary bla‌dder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the effects of muscarinic antagonists on the urinary bladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lowers intravesicular pres​sure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lowers blad​der capacity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lowers the frequency of bl​adder contractions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alters bladder sensation d​uring filling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lowers bladder capacity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lowers bladder capacity</strong></p><p>Muscarinic antagonists <strong>increase bladder capacity</strong> by <strong>blocking M3</strong> receptors on the detrusor. This results in the <strong>relaxation</strong> of the bladder. </p>\n<p>Effects of muscarinic antagonists on the bladder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lowers intravesicular pressure</li>\n<li>Increases bladder capacity</li>\n<li>Lowers the frequency of bladder contractions</li>\n<li>Alters bladder sensation during filling</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3201","text":"After suffering from a str​oke, a middle-aged patien​t was diagnosed with over‍active bladder due to det‌rusor instability. Which ‍of the following drugs is‌ not useful in this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"After suffering from a stroke, a middle-aged patient was diagnosed with overactive bladder due to detrusor instability. Which of the following drugs is not useful in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Solif​enacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tolte​rodine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flav​oxate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dulox​etine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duloxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duloxetine</strong></p><p><strong>Duloxetine </strong>is not useful in detrusor instability. It<strong> </strong>is a selective serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that is efficacious in <strong>stress urinary incontinence. </strong>It is also indicated in<strong> </strong>depression, fibromyalgia, and neuropathic pain. </p>\n<p><strong>Muscarinic antagonists</strong> are useful in this case. They increase bladder capacity by <strong>blocking M3</strong> receptors on detrusor. This results in the <strong>relaxation</strong> of the bladder. </p>\n<p>The effects of muscarinic antagonists on the bladder are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lowering of intravesicular pressure</li>\n<li>Increases bladder capacity</li>\n<li>Lower the frequency of bladder contractions</li>\n<li>Alter bladder sensation during filling</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3203","text":"In a patient with overacti​ve bladder, what is the m​ost common reason for him‍ to discontinue treatment‌ with muscarinic antagoni‍sts?","raw_text":"In a patient with overactive bladder, what is the most common reason for him to discontinue treatment with muscarinic antagonists?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xeros​tomia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blurred​ vision","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Consti​pation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dysp​epsia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Xerostomia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Xerostomia</strong></p><p><strong>Xerostomia</strong> is the most common reason for the <strong>discontinuation</strong> of muscarinic antagonists in overactive bladder.</p>\n<p>The most important adverse effects caused by the use of muscarinic antagonists for an overactive bladder are xerostomia, blurred vision, and GI side effects like constipation and dyspepsia (due to blockade of M1, M2 &amp; M3). Drowsiness, dizziness, and confusion can also occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3222","text":"Which of the following has​ selective action on M1 a​nd M3 receptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following has selective action on M1 and M3 receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iprat​ropium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tiotr​opium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atropine m​ethonitrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Umecli​dinium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tiotropium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tiotropium</strong></p><p><strong>Tiotropium</strong> has a selective action on <strong>M1 and M3 receptors.</strong></p>\n<p>Tiotropium binds to all M1, M2 and M3 receptors. However, it dissociates slower from M1 and M3 receptors as compared to M2. This shows it has a higher affinity towards M1 and M3 receptors. </p>\n<p>Ipratropium and atropine methonitrate do not have any subtype selectivity on muscarinic receptors.</p>\n<p>Inhibition of mucociliary clearance by atropine results in the accumulation of lower airway secretions and the formation of mucus plugs. However, tiotropium and ipratropium have minimum interference with mucociliary clearance when compared to atropine, and the incidence of mucus plugs is also less.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3224","text":"In which of the following ​patients is the use of tr​ospium contraindicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is the use of trospium contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10-year-old boy with bronc​hial asthma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45-year-old woman with hyp​ertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60-year-old man with urina​ry retention","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"50-year-old woman with pep​tic ulcer disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60-year-old man with urinary retention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60-year-old man with urinary retention</strong></p><p>The use of <strong>trospium</strong> is contraindicated in urinary retention. It is an antimuscarinic agent and can <strong>worsen urinary retention</strong>. </p>\n<p>Muscarinic antagonists are contraindicated in urinary tract obstruction and in benign prostatic hyperplasia (or used with extreme caution).</p>\n<p>Bronchial asthma, hypertension, and peptic ulcer disease are not the contraindications for the use of trospium. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3227","text":"Which of the following doe​s not produce central ant​icholinergic syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not produce central anticholinergic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycopy​rrolate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antihis​taminics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricyclic an​tidepressants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycopyrrolate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycopyrrolate</strong></p><p><strong>Glycopyrrolate</strong> usually does not produce central anticholinergic syndrome. It is a<strong> quaternary amine</strong> compound and<strong> </strong>lipid insoluble. Therefore, glycopyrrolate does<strong> not cross</strong> the<strong> blood-brain barrier</strong>.</p>\n<p>Non-depolarising skeletal muscle relaxants are used in anesthesia. Neostigmine along with glycopyrrolate are used for reversal of this neuromuscular block. Glycopyrrolate <strong>blocks</strong> the <strong>peripheral muscarinic side effects</strong> due to the increased acetylcholine levels after the administration of neostigmine, <strong>without</strong> causing unwanted <strong>central anticholinergic effects.</strong></p>\n<p>Peripheral and central anticholinergic syndromes are reactions due to either overdose or any unusual reaction to the usage of drugs like atropine and other muscarinic agents, phenothiazines, antihistamines, and tricyclic antidepressants</p>\n<p>Option B: Atropine is a tertiary compound that can cross the blood-brain barrier and cause central anticholinergic syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C and D: Some of the first-generation antihistamines and tricyclic antidepressants have significant anticholinergic activity and can cause central anticholinergic syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3191","text":"Which of the following is ​most commonly used for na​rcoanalysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most commonly used for narcoanalysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine​ sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pentotha​l sodium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenoba​rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scopolamine ​hydrochloride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pentothal sodium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pentothal sodium</strong></p><p>The most commonly used drug in narcoanalysis is <strong>pentothal sodium </strong>(thiopentone).</p>\n<p>Other drugs used in narcoanalysis are sodium amytal and secobarbital.</p>\n<p>Scopolamine hydrochloride was also used in narcoanalysis during World War II. It was popularly known as the 'lie detector'. The amnesic and depressant actions of scopolamine are due to the inhibition of the brain cholinergic system. It would put the subject off guard in case of sustained interrogation and sleep deprivation, eventually forcing the truth out.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3223","text":"Inhibitory effect on mucoc​iliary clearance is minim​al with which of the foll‍owing drugs?","raw_text":"Inhibitory effect on mucociliary clearance is minimal with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tiotr​opium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iprat​ropium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both b​ and c","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both b and c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both b and c</strong></p><p>Both <strong>ipratropium</strong> and <strong>tiotropium</strong> have a <strong>minimal inhibitory</strong> effect on <strong>mucociliary clearance</strong> when compared to atropine. Inhibition of mucociliary clearance by <strong>atropine</strong> results in accumulation of lower airway secretions and formation of <strong>mucus plugs.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EpLjtCco","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"7_muscle_physiology_i","title":"7.Muscle Physiology I","num_questions":35,"total_marks":140,"duration":45,"time_per_question":77.14,"questions":[{"id":"MA9110","text":"All of the following are t​he connective tissue laye‍rs of the skeletal muscle​ except ______","raw_text":"All of the following are the connective tissue layers of the skeletal muscle except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endom​ysium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco​lemma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Perim​ysium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epim​ysium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcolemma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d21dabec29664ef9b88d20ae526a8054x1280x1915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcolemma</strong></p><p>Sarcolemma is the cell membrane of the muscle cell, while the remaining options are connective tissue layers surrounding the muscle.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 112\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p class=\"p2\">A muscle is made up of individual muscle fibers. Each muscle fiber is a single cell that is multinucleated, long, cylindrical, and surrounded by a cell membrane, the sarcolemma.</p>\n<p>Arrangement of the connective tissue is shown below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9112","text":"Which of the following is ​a regulatory muscle prote‍in?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a regulatory muscle protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ac​tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tropom​odulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tropo​myosin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dystr​ophin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tropomyosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4e9404f953c438e90f4b9d0b9e7f954x600x698.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tropomyosin</strong></p><p>Tropomyosin is a regulatory muscle protein.</p>\n<p>It extends over the entire actin filament and covers the myosin-binding sites on the actin molecules thus preventing the attachment of actin to myosin.</p>\n<p>One molecule covers <strong>7 active sites on actin</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Troponin</strong> is another regulatory protein. Troponin molecules are small globular units located at intervals along the tropomyosin molecules. Each of the three troponin subunits has a unique function:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Troponin <strong>T</strong> binds the troponin components to tropomyosin.</li>\n<li>troponin <strong>I</strong> inhibits the interaction of myosin with actin.</li>\n<li>troponin <strong>C</strong> contains the binding sites for the Ca2+ that helps initiate contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9113","text":"Which of the following doe​s not belong to dystrophi‍n-glycoprotein complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not belong to dystrophin-glycoprotein complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perl​ecan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dystr​ophin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dystro​glycan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco​glycan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perlecan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20ccce0f1b5949c2bb9c178bb78827abx600x613.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perlecan</strong></p><p>Perlecan is a large extracellular matrix proteoglycan that plays a crucial role in tissue development and organogenesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Dystrophin-glycoprotein complex</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The dystrophin-glycoprotein complex provides a structural link between the cytoskeleton of the muscle cell and the extracellular matrix, which appears to stabilize the sarcolemma, adds strength to the muscle by providing a scaffolding for the fibrils and prevents contraction-induced injury (rupture).</li>\n<li>Organisation of DG complex\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dystrophin</strong> connects F-actin to the transmembrane protein <strong>β-dystroglycan </strong>via smaller proteins called <strong>Syntrophins.</strong>  </li>\n<li>This<strong> </strong>β-dystroglycan, in turn connects to the merosin subunit of laminin 211 in the extracellular matrix via <strong>α-dystroglycan. </strong></li>\n<li>The dystroglycans are also associated with a complex of four transmembrane glycoproteins,<strong> α-, β-, γ-, and δ-sarcoglycan </strong>and <strong>sarcospan.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 115\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>APPLIED ASPECT</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>X-linked recessive disorder</li>\n<li>Deletion of DMD gene which codes for Dystrophin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Becker muscular Dystrophy</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Dystrophin is present but altered or reduced in amount</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Limb Girdle Dystrophy</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Sarcoglycan is mutated</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9115","text":"Thin filaments consists of​ all of the following exc‍ept _______","raw_text":"Thin filaments consists of all of the following except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ac​tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myo​sin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trop​onin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tropo​myosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf98c9be39eb4efaadd63a93b2b4123fx650x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myosin</strong></p><p>Myosin forms the thick filament, while actin, troponin, and tropomyosin form the thin filament.</p>\n<p><strong>Thin Filaments in Sarcomere</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The thin filaments are polymers made up of two chains of <strong>actin</strong> that form a long double helix.</li>\n<li><strong>Tropomyosin</strong> molecules are long filaments located in the groove between the two chains in the actin.\n<ul>\n<li>Each thin filament contains 300–400 actin molecules and 40–60 tropomyosin molecules.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Troponin</strong> molecules are small globular units located at intervals along the tropomyosin molecules.\n<ul>\n<li>Each of the three troponin subunits has a unique function:\n<ul>\n<li>Troponin <strong>T</strong> binds the troponin components to <strong>t</strong>ropomyosin.</li>\n<li>Troponin <strong>I</strong> inhibits the interaction of myosin with actin.</li>\n<li>Troponin <strong>C</strong> contains the binding sites for the <strong>Ca <sup>2+</sup></strong> that helps to initiate contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1291","text":"Which of the following pro​teins is present in skele‍tal muscles, but absent i​n a smooth muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following proteins is present in skeletal muscles, but absent in a smooth muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trop​onin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tropo​myosin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ac​tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myo​sin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Troponin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Troponin</strong></p><p>Actin, myosin, and tropomyosin are present in both skeletal muscle and smooth muscle. But troponin is absent and calmodulin is present in smooth muscle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1288","text":"Myosin filament has a fixe​d length of ______.","raw_text":"Myosin filament has a fixed length of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"16 nan​ometers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.6 mic​rometers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1.6 nan​ometers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16 micr​ometers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.6 micrometers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.6 micrometers</strong></p><p>The total length of each myosin filament is uniform, almost exactly <strong>1.6 micrometers</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9120","text":"The thick filament is made​ up of ________. 1. Heavy‍ chain of myosin 2. Light​ chain of myosin 3. Dystr‌ophin 4. Titin","raw_text":"The thick filament is made up of ________.\n1. Heavy chain of myosin\n2. Light chain of myosin\n3. Dystrophin\n4. Titin","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 a​nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 2</strong></p><p>The thick filament or the myosin is made up of the heavy chain of myosin and light chain of myosin.</p>\n<p>The <strong>myosin molecule</strong> is composed of six polypeptide chains- <strong>Two heavy chains,</strong> each with a molecular weight of about 200,000, and <strong>four light chains</strong> with molecular weights of about 20,000 each.</p>\n<p>The <strong>two heavy chains</strong> wrap spirally around each other to form a double helix, which is called the <strong>tail</strong> of the myosin molecule.</p>\n<p>One end of each of these chains is folded into a globular polypeptide structure called a <strong>myosin head</strong>. These heads contain an actin-binding site and a catalytic site that hydrolyzes ATP. Thus, there are two free heads at one end of the double-helix myosin molecule.</p>\n<p>The <strong>four light chains</strong> are also part of the myosin head, two to each head. These light chains help control the function of the head during muscle contraction.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9122","text":"Pseudo H zone in a sarcome​re extends between the __‍____","raw_text":"Pseudo H zone in a sarcomere extends between the ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Two Z​ lines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Two I​ bands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Two sides​ of M line","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A and ​I band","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Two sides of M line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec64950aac274bcf8a31024ade03ad14x1280x2273.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Two sides of M line</strong></p><p>Pseudo H-zone is the light area between the two sides of M line due to reversal of polarity of the myosin molecules in each of the thick filaments.</p>\n<p><strong>Sarcomere</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The sarcomere</strong> is the contractile unit of the skeletal muscle fibre.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Z line:</strong> provides attachment to actin filaments. The area between two adjacent Z lines is called the sarcomere.</li>\n<li>It is composed of thick and thin filaments:\n<ul>\n<li>Thick filament : Myosin</li>\n<li>Thin filaments : Actin, tropomyosin and troponin </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>I band: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Isotropic to light.</li>\n<li>Only thin filaments. </li>\n<li>Half lies in one sarcomere and half in the next.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>A band: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Anisotropic to light.</li>\n<li>Myosin plus overlapped actin.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>H bands:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>lighter area in the centre of A band, when the muscle is relaxed, the thin filaments do not overlap the thick filaments.</li>\n<li>Only Myosin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo H:</strong> light portion on two sides of M line because of absence of cross bridges (no myosin head).</li>\n<li><strong>M line:</strong> centre of A band. Here there are slender cross-connections that hold the thick filaments in proper array.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9124","text":"A cross-sectional view of ​a skeletal muscle fiber t‍hrough the H zone would r​eveal the presence of ___‌____","raw_text":"A cross-sectional view of a skeletal muscle fiber through the H zone would reveal the presence of  _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actin a​nd Titin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Actin an​d Myosin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myosi​n only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Actin​ only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myosin only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4723a7f48eb3434990ff49486c882bedx374x251.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myosin only</strong></p><p><strong>H zone</strong> is the <strong>central region</strong> of the <strong>A band</strong> which is lighter than the outer edges of the A band because the H zone is occupied by thick filaments only (No cross bridges)</p>\n<p>In the figure, note the section through various levels and the myofilaments observed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Section through <strong>I</strong> band → only actin (thin filament)</li>\n<li>Section through <strong>A</strong> band (Exlcuding H zone) → Actin and Myosin</li>\n<li>Section through <strong>H</strong> zone → only myosin (thick filament)</li>\n<li>Section through <strong>M</strong> line → thick filament linked with accessory proteins</li>\n<li>Section at <strong>AI</strong> junction → both actin and myosin</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0565","text":"The titin protein present ​in the muscle fiber binds‍ ______","raw_text":"The titin protein present in the muscle fiber binds ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The tail ends of actin fil​aments to Z disc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The head ends of myosin fi​laments to the Z disc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The tail ends of myosin fi​laments to M disc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The myofibrils to each oth​er, and also to the cell ‍membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The head ends of myosin filaments to the Z disc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The head ends of myosin filaments to the Z disc</strong></p><p>The <strong>titin protein</strong> present in the muscle fiber binds the <strong>head ends of myosin</strong> <strong>filaments</strong> <strong>to the Z disc</strong></p>\n<p>Several proteins other than actin and myosin are also present in the muscle. Some of them include:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Titin: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the largest known protein.</li>\n<li>It runs between the <strong>M line and Z disc</strong> and binds the <strong>head ends of myosin filaments</strong> to the <strong>Z disc</strong>.</li>\n<li>It has a <strong>tethered</strong> <strong>portion</strong> in the<strong> A band</strong>, where it is attached to the myosin filament and an <strong>elastic</strong> <strong>portion</strong> in the <strong>I band.</strong></li>\n<li>The elastic property of the titin <strong>causes elastic recoil of the sarcomere</strong> and prevents it from being overstretched.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Nebulin: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It extends from the <strong>Z</strong> <strong>disc</strong> and runs along the full length of each <strong>actin</strong> <strong>filament</strong>.</li>\n<li>It regulates the length of the actin filaments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. α-actinin</strong><strong>:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a component of the <strong>Z discs</strong> </li>\n<li>It binds the <strong>plus ends of actin</strong> filaments to this disc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. <strong>Myomesin</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is present in the region of the <strong>M line. </strong></li>\n<li>It maintains the regular lattice arrangement of the myosin filaments in this region.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5. Desmin:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is an intermediate filament characteristic of the muscle</li>\n<li>It encircles the myofibrils<strong> near the Z disc</strong> and <strong>binds myofibrils to each other</strong> and also to the <strong>cell membrane.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>6. Dystrophin:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is present in the periphery of the muscle fiber, towards the cytoplasmic face of the sarcolemma.</li>\n<li>It binds to the <strong>actin</strong> <strong>filaments</strong> and the glycoprotein complex, consisting of <strong>dystroglycan</strong> and <strong>sarcoglycan</strong> that spans the <strong>sarcolemma</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9432","text":"Which of the following is ​the constituent of the ma‍rked area in the electron​ microscopic picture of m‌uscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the constituent of the marked area in the electron microscopic picture of muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha ​actinin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ac​tin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ti​tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tropo​myosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alpha actinin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6677a61eca434e89a80a3175f50ab1c7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8478ad20252a4950811900da27222470x794x600.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b3b50ddb02941f5ac80cc10bcdae0fbx775x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alpha actinin</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha actinin</strong> is the constituent of the marked area.</p>\n<p>The image depicts sarcomere and the arrow points towards Z-line. Alpha actinin <strong>binds</strong> actin to Z-lines.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The orderly arrangement of actin, myosin, and related proteins that produces this pattern. <strong>The area between two Z-lines is called sarcomere.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>thick filaments</strong>, which are about twice the diameter of the thin filaments, are made up of <strong>myosin, </strong>which are lined to form <strong>A bands</strong>; the <strong>thin</strong> filaments are made up of <strong>actin</strong>, <strong>tropomyosin</strong>, and <strong>troponin</strong>. The array of thin filaments extends out of the A band and into the less dense staining<strong> I bands</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lighter <strong>H bands</strong> in the <strong>center</strong> of the A bands are the regions where, when the muscle is <strong>relaxed</strong>, the thin filaments <strong>do not overlap</strong> the thick filaments.  The <strong>Z lines</strong> allow for <strong>anchoring</strong> of the thin filaments.</p>\n<p>Actinin, titin and desmin are additional structural proteins.Titin connects Z-lines to the M line and provides the scaffolding to the sarcomere.  Desmin adds structure to Z-lines by connecting Z-line to plasma membrane.</p>\n<p><br/>Note: Some sources say that even titin is a component of Z-disc so if alpha actinin is not given in the options titin is the second best answer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9125","text":"Muscle myosin and actin fi​laments are kept in place‍ by ______","raw_text":"Muscle myosin and actin filaments are kept in place by ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tropo​myosin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trop​onin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Act​inin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ti​tin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Titin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/58f6a20f49994164b781478178cfed67x350x157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Titin</strong></p><p>The side-by-side relationship between the myosin and actin filaments is maintained by a large number of filamentous molecules of a protein called <strong>titin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Titin is a filamentous and springy protein. These springy titin molecules act as a <strong>framework </strong>that holds the myosin and actin filaments in place so that the contractile machinery of the sarcomere will work.</p>\n<p>One end of the titin molecule is elastic and is attached to the Z disk, acting as a spring and changing length as the sarcomere contracts and relaxes. The other part of the titin molecule tethers it to the myosin thick filament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6944","text":"Choose the true statement ​regarding the sarcomere i‍n skeletal muscle.","raw_text":"Choose the true statement regarding the sarcomere in skeletal muscle.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmin binds Z line to sar​coplasmic reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Actinin binds M line to ac​tin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Titin binds M line to z li​ne","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The length of myosin chang​es with contraction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Titin binds M line to z line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Titin binds M line to z line</strong></p><p>Titin binds M line to Z line</p>\n<p>Other options</p>\n<p>Desmin binds Z line to sarcolemma ( not  sarcoplasmic reticulum)</p>\n<p>Actinin binds  Z line to actin ( not M line; in fact, one of the two attachments of actin, desmin and titin, is always Z line)</p>\n<p>Titin binds M line to z line, forms scaffolding, is responsible for muscle elasticity, is the largest known protein (Largest protein - titin; Largest gene - Dystrophin; Most abundant protein - Collagen )</p>\n<p>The length of myosin or actin does not change with contraction; actually, the length of zones I and H  get changed (i.e, gets decreased )</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9126","text":"Which of the following occ​urs during skeletal muscl‍e contraction?","raw_text":"Which of the following occurs during skeletal muscle contraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A band ​shorten","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Both H and I​ band shorten","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both A and I​ band shorten","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A and H​ band shorten","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Both H and I band shorten","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Both H and I band shorten</strong></p><p>Sliding of thin filament over thick filament (cross bridge formation) <strong>reduces the length of I and H band</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>During contraction, the <strong>Z lines come closer</strong> to each other reducing the length of the sarcomere.</li>\n<li>The <strong>A band remains constant</strong> and M line becomes prominent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9140","text":"The cross-bridges of the s​arcomere in skeletal musc‍le are made up of _______​.","raw_text":"The cross-bridges of the sarcomere in skeletal muscle are made up of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ti​tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myo​sin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trop​onin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tropo​myosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56f1f2f4dc0847bebae7a004ad5cf903x406x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myosin</strong></p><p>When the action potential reaches the muscle fiber, there is influx of Ca<sup>2+</sup> ions. This <sup> </sup>Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds to troponin C and tropomyosin is moved out of the way.<strong> Myosin heads can now bind to actin and form so-called cross-bridges.</strong></p>\n<p>When a head attaches to an active site, this attachment simultaneously causes profound changes in the intramolecular forces between the head and arm of its cross-bridge. This causes the head to tilt toward the arm and to drag the actin filament along with it. This tilt of the head is called the <strong>power stroke. </strong></p>\n<p>Immediately after tilting, the head then automatically breaks away from the active site.</p>\n<p>Formation of cross-bridges is associated with hydrolysis of ATP and generation of force.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1289","text":"ATPase activity is present​ in ______.","raw_text":"ATPase activity is present in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myosi​n Head","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myosi​n Tail","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Actin f​ilaments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desmin f​ilaments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myosin Head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e98d53eb1f844946898462fdca435eacx1280x2560.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b837205a4b144feb8d40af9f3eb7957bx386x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myosin Head</strong></p><p>A feature of the<strong> myosin head</strong> that is essential for muscle contraction is that it functions as an adenosine triphosphatase (ATPase) enzyme.</p>\n<p><strong>Chemical Events during Muscle Contraction</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before contraction begins, the heads of the cross-bridges bind with ATP. The ATPase activity of the myosin head immediately cleaves the ATP but leaves the cleavage products, ADP plus phosphate ion, bound to the head. (Point A)</li>\n<li>When the troponin-tropomyosin complex binds with calcium ions, active sites on the actin filament are uncovered and the myosin heads then bind with these sites. (Point B)</li>\n<li>The bond between the head of the cross-bridge and the active site of the actin filament causes a conformational change in the head and the <strong>power stroke</strong> occurs. (Point C)</li>\n<li>Once the head of the cross-bridge tilts, the ADP and phosphate ion that were previously attached to the head are released and a new ATP moelcule binds in that place. (Point D)</li>\n<li>The binding of the new ATP molecule causes detachment of the head of myosin from the actin. (Point D)</li>\n<li>This cycle is then repeated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0115","text":"Myosin head detaches from ​actin by which of the fol‍lowing mechanism?","raw_text":"Myosin head detaches from actin by which of the following mechanism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binding​ of ATP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Entry of calcium into sarc​oplasmic reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Change in troponin C confi​guration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Release of​ ADP and Pi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Binding of ATP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60c59fd72afd49ac9a6b41580d16e236x386x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Binding of ATP</strong></p><p><strong>Binding of ATP</strong> on the free site of myosin leads to the <strong>detachment of myosin head from the thin filament.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9129","text":"In human skeletal muscles,​ calcium ions are stored ‍in?","raw_text":"In human skeletal muscles, calcium ions are stored in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terminal​ cistern","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco​lemma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transvers​e tubules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All are​ correct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Terminal cistern","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Terminal cistern</strong></p><p>In a skeletal muscle, calcium ions are majorly stored in the <strong>terminal cisterns</strong> of the Sarcoplasmic Reticulum.</p>\n<p>Terminal cisterns are the lateral sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The spread of action potential triggers the release of Ca2+ from the terminal cisterns.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 101\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 100\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>sarcolemma</strong> is the cell membrane of the skeletal muscle. <strong>Transverse tubules</strong> are the internal extensions of the sarcolemma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9132","text":"All of the following are t​rue about excitation-cont‍raction coupling in skele​tal muscle except that __‌___","raw_text":"All of the following are true about excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle except that _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetylcholine is released ​at the nerve terminal.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ca2+ is released from sarc​oplasmic reticulum during‍ contraction.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca2+ is pumped back into s​arcoplasmic reticulum dur‍ing relaxation.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ca2+ binds to tropomyosin ​to initiate muscle contra‍ction.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ca2+ binds to tropomyosin to initiate muscle contraction.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b0f88bc729f4874936c0ce091d935c0x1280x2441.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/749d53c0b6664670a3d6a122d75d5d11x251x140.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ca2+ binds to tropomyosin to initiate muscle contraction.</strong></p><p>Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds to troponin C (not tropomyosin) to initiate muscle contraction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1287","text":"A skeletal muscle contract​ion ends when _______.","raw_text":"A skeletal muscle contraction ends when _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ions move out of the cytop​lasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ach is metabolised at the ​Neuromusclular junction (‍NMR)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased calcium outside ​the sarcoplasmic reticulu‍m","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myosin filaments are degra​ded","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased calcium outside the sarcoplasmic reticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased calcium outside the sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong></p><p>Once the<strong> Ca<sup>2+</sup> concentration outside the sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong> has been <strong>lowered</strong> sufficiently, chemical interaction between myosin and actin ceases and the muscle relaxes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The SERCA pump uses energy from ATP hydrolysis to remove Ca<sup>2+</sup> from the cytosol back into the terminal cisterns, where it is stored until released by the next action potential.</li>\n<li>Thus during relaxation\n<ul>\n<li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> concentration in cytoplasmic decreases.</li>\n<li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> concentration in sarcoplasmic reticulum increases.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option a</strong>: Calcium ions do not move out of the cytoplasm. They are taken up by the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1292","text":"A bodybuilder is regularly​ working out in a gym. Wh‍ich of the following is t​rue regarding total heat ‌production during muscle ​contraction?","raw_text":"A bodybuilder is regularly working out in a gym. Which of the following is true regarding total heat production during muscle contraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation heat is produce​d when a muscle is contra‍cting","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Initial heat produced is d​ue to the basal metabolic‍ processes.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relaxation heat is present​ in both isotonic and iso‍metric contractions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recovery heat of muscle is​ greater than the initial‍ heat","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Activation heat is produced when a muscle is contracting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Activation heat is produced when a muscle is contracting</strong></p><p>Activation heat is the heat that muscle produces whenever it contracts.</p>\n<p><strong>Heat Production in Muscle</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resting heat:</strong> The heat given off at rest due to the <strong>basal metabolic processes.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Initial heat: </strong>The heat produced in <strong>excess</strong> of resting heat during contraction. It is made up two subtypes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Activation heat:</strong> The heat that muscle produces whenever it is contracting, and</li>\n<li><strong>Shortening heat:</strong> The heat which is proportional in amount to the distance the muscle shortens. Shortening heat is apparently due to some change in the structure of the muscle during shortening.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Recovery heat: </strong> this is  the heat liberated by the metabolic processes that restore the muscle to its precontraction state. The recovery heat of muscle is approximately <strong>equal</strong> to the initial heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Relaxation heat:</strong> Heat released in a muscle that has contracted <strong>isotonically</strong> to restore it to its previous length. This extra heat is in addition to recovery heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1294","text":"In a 100-m dash, most of t​he energy consumed by ske‍letal muscles to replenis​h ATP is derived from ___‌____","raw_text":"In a 100-m dash, most of the energy consumed by skeletal muscles to replenish ATP is derived from _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phospho​creatine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aerobic g​lycolysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidation of​ Fatty acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphocreatine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphocreatine</strong></p><div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">In a 100-m dash that takes 10 s, 85% of the energy consumed is derived anaerobically by <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">phosphocreatine</strong>, which is hydrolyzed to creatine and phosphate groups with the release of considerable energy.<br/><br/>The ATP concentration in a muscle is low enough (4 millimolar) to sustain contractions for <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">1-2 seconds</strong> after which ATP is dephosphorylated into ADP. The ADP is re-phosphorylated by phosphocreatine for the <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">next 5 to 8 seconds</strong>.</div>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\"><br/>ADP is re-phosphorylated by <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">3 sources.</strong></div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Phosphocreatine after 2 seconds upto 10 seconds.</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Glycolysis (anaerobic) after 10 seconds upto a minute</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Oxidative metabolism - after a minute upto 2-4 hours.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9142","text":"The length of the muscle f​iber at the onset of cont‍raction which is required​ to attain the maximum ac‌tive tension is known as","raw_text":"The length of the muscle fiber at the onset of contraction which is required to attain the maximum active tension is known as","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Equilibri​um Length","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resting​ Length","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Initial​ length","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overlappi​ng Length","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Resting Length","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94d24dc1ceba4d04864b633b7401a117x1280x1183.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Resting Length</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 88\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>length</strong> of the muscle fiber at the <strong>onset</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>contraction</strong> which is required to attain <strong>maximum active tension</strong> is known as <strong>resting length.</strong></p>\n<p>Resting length is also known as the <strong>optimum length. </strong>It is about <strong>2μm</strong>. At this length, there is a <strong>maximal</strong> <strong>overlap</strong> <strong>between the actin and myosin fibers</strong> and therefore, the maximum number of cross-bridge binding sites are formed.</p>\n<p>Passive tension is the tension in a muscle when it is not stimulated. Total tension is the tension developed when a muscle contracts isometrically in response to a maximal stimulus.</p>\n<p><strong>Active tension = Total tension - Passive tension</strong></p>\n<p>When the muscle fiber contracts, the active tension developed is proportional to the number of cross-bridges between the actin and myosin fibers. Hence, at the resting length, maximum active tension is produced and the muscle fiber contracts with a maximum force of contraction.</p>\n<p>In the given image, the <strong>purple line</strong> represents the <strong>passive</strong> tension in the muscle (<strong>before contraction</strong>) and its relation with the muscle length, while the <strong>red line</strong> represents the <strong>total</strong> tension present (<strong>during contraction</strong>) and its relation with the muscle length.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1286","text":"All of the following are t​rue regarding the resting‍ length of a muscle fiber​ except ________.","raw_text":"All of the following are true regarding the resting length of a muscle fiber except ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There are maximum cross-br​idges between actin and m‍yosin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The passive tension is max​imum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The resting length is arou​nd 2μm in humans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The velocity of contractio​n is maximum for any give‍n load","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The passive tension is maximum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28d6f8d2625c4481a71f2d3d502af093x1280x1183.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5e76cc0b8664abfab44c979184b86c5x1280x1104.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The passive tension is maximum</strong></p><p>The passive tension is not maximum at resting length of a muscle fiber.</p>\n<p>Passive tension is the tension in a muscle when it is not stimulated. Total tension is the tension developed when a muscle contracts isometrically in response to a maximal stimulus.</p>\n<p><strong>Active tension = Total tension - Passive tension</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>active tension</strong> is<strong> maximum </strong>at the <strong>resting length</strong> of a muscle fiber. This is because there is a optimal overlap between the actin and myosin fibers at the resting length and therefore the maximum number of cross-bridges are formed. (<strong>Option A</strong>) This provides for the maximum force of contraction at this length. The active tension decreases as the muscle is stretched beyond its normal length. The resting length is around 2μm in humans. (<strong>Option C</strong>)</p>\n<p>The velocity of muscle contraction varies inversely with the load on the muscle i.e. as the load on the muscle increases, the velocity of contraction decreases. At a given load, the velocity is maximal at the resting length and declines if the muscle is shorter or longer than this length. (<strong>Option D</strong>)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9150","text":"Active tension in a muscle​ depends upon which of th‍e following?","raw_text":"Active tension in a muscle depends upon which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of m​uscle fibres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of motor units recr​uited","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aerobic capacity of muscle​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Action potential amplitude​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Number of motor units recruited","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Number of motor units recruited</strong></p><p>Active tension in a muscle depends upon the <strong>number of motor units</strong> recruited.</p>\n<p>The greater the number of motor units recruited, the greater will be the active tension.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Factors affecting muscle tension</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\"> </p>\n<p><strong>Tension developed by each fiber</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Action potential frequency</li>\n<li>Fiber length (length-tension relationship)</li>\n<li>Fiber diameter</li>\n<li>Fatigue</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Number of active fibers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Number of fibers per motor unit</li>\n<li>Number of active motor units (recruitment)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9151","text":"Which of the following hav​e the maximum density of ‍white muscle fibers?","raw_text":"Which of the following have the maximum density of white muscle fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calf m​uscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extraocul​ar muscles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ba​ck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H​ip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Extraocular muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Extraocular muscles</strong></p><p>Extra-ocular muscles have the maximum density of white muscle fibers (type IIB), which contract quickly with peak tension attained within 7.5 msec of stimulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1290","text":"All are true about red mus​cle fibers except -","raw_text":"All are true about red muscle fibers except -","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More mit​ochondria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High glycolytic metabolism​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"More my​oglobin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More oxidat​ive capacity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High glycolytic metabolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High glycolytic metabolism</strong></p><p>The red muscle fibers are Type 1 muscle fibers with low/moderate glycolytic metabolism. Rest of the options are true regarding red muscle fibers.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1293","text":"A motor unit is made up of​ _______________.","raw_text":"A motor unit is made up of _______________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All the muscle fibers with​in a given muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A motor neuron and the mus​cle fibers it innervates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"All the neurons going into​ an muscle of the body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A fascicle ​and a nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A motor neuron and the muscle fibers it innervates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07bf69c878e54b44a67fcfa005ecf36fx1280x1719.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A motor neuron and the muscle fibers it innervates</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 123\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Each single motor neuron and the muscle fibers it innervates constitute a <strong>motor unit</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 123\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The number of muscle fibers in a motor unit varies.\n<ul>\n<li>In muscles concerned with fine, graded, precise movement, (eg. extraoccular muscles) each motor unit innervates very few (<strong>three to six</strong>) muscle fibers.</li>\n<li>On the other hand, in human leg and back muscles, number of muscle fibers per motor unit can be upto <strong>600</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Each spinal motor neuron innervates only one kind of muscle fiber, so that all the muscle fibers in a motor unit are of the same type.</li>\n<li>On the basis of the type of muscle fiber they innervate, and thus on the basis of the duration of their twitch contraction, motor units are divided into S (slow), FR (fast, resistant to fatigue), and FF (fast, fatigable) units.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9152","text":"Which of the following is ​correct about the recruit‍ment of fast fatiguable m​otor unit ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about the recruitment of fast fatiguable motor unit ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recruit​ed first","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recruit​ed last","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"During r​elaxation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"For the less demanding tas​ks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Recruited last","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Recruited last</strong></p><p>The fast fatigable motor unit is <strong>recruited</strong> <strong>last</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each single motor neuron and the muscle fibers it innervates constitute a <strong>motor unit</strong>. On the basis of the type of muscle fiber they innervate, and thus on the basis of the duration of their twitch contraction, motor units are divided into</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>  S </strong>(slow)</li>\n<li><strong>  FR </strong>(fast, resistant to fatigue)</li>\n<li><strong>  FF </strong>(fast, fatigable) units.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>S fibers have a low innervation ratio (ie, small units) and FF fibers have a high innervation ratio (i.e., large units).</p>\n<p>The recruitment of motor units during muscle contrac­tion follows a general scheme, the <strong>size principle</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In general, a specific muscle action is developed first by the recruitment of S muscle units that contract relatively slowly to produce controlled contraction.</li>\n<li>Next, FR muscle units are recruited, resulting in more powerful response over a shorter period of time.</li>\n<li>Lastly, FF muscle units are recruited for the most demanding tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9153","text":"Twitch of a single muscle ​fiber is called _____","raw_text":"Twitch of a single muscle fiber is called _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibril​lation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fascic​ulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myoc​lonus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Je​rk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fibrillation</strong></p><p>Twitch of a single muscle fiber is called <strong>fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 50px;\" width=\"333\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\"><strong>Fibrillation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\"><strong>Fasciculation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Twitch of a single muscle fiber</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Jerky, visible contractions of groups of muscle fibers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Occurs due to the degeneration of a single α motor neuron or its axon.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Occur as a result of a pathologic discharge of spinal motor neurons (motor unit). </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9154","text":"A scientist found that the​ latent period of muscle ‍twitch is 10 ms and the c​ontraction period is 40 m‌s. What is the tetanizing​ frequency (Hz)?","raw_text":"A scientist found that the latent period of muscle twitch is 10 ms and the contraction period is 40 ms. What is the tetanizing frequency (Hz)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​00","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7​5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 25","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 25</strong></p><p>Tetanizing frequency = <strong>1/contraction</strong> <strong>period</strong> = 1/40 ms = <strong>25 Hz.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tetanizing frequency</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>As the contractile mechanism <strong>does not have a refractory period</strong>, on rapid stimulation of a muscle, each new contraction occurs before the preceding one is over. As a result, the second contraction is added partially to the first, and thus the total strength of contraction rises progressively with increasing frequency. Such a response is called a <strong>tetanus</strong> (tetanic contraction). It is a complete tetanus when no relaxation occurs between stimuli.</li>\n<li>Tetany occurs because enough calcium ions are maintained in the muscle sarcoplasm throughout the period of stimulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9155","text":"Tetanic contraction is due​ to the accumulation of _‍______ ions.","raw_text":"Tetanic contraction is due to the accumulation of _______ ions.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N​a+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ca​2+","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"K​+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​l-","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ca2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49e44474a0a543ac8d259fd1c2111709x763x314.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ca2+</strong></p><p><strong>Tetanic contraction occurs due to progressive accumulation of Ca<sup>2+</sup> ions with rapidly repeated stimulation of skeletal muscle.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>With rapidly repeated stimulation, activation of the contractile mechanism occurs repeatedly before any relaxation has occurred, and the individual contractions fuse into one continuous contraction. Such a response is called a <strong>tetanus (tetanic contraction)</strong>.</li>\n<li>A single action potential releases sufficient Ca<sup>2+</sup> to cause a twitch contraction.</li>\n<li>However, the duration of this contraction is very short because Ca<sup>2+</sup> is very rapidly pumped back into the SR.</li>\n<li>If the muscle is stimulated a second time before the muscle is fully relaxed, there is progressive accumulation of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and the force of contraction increases. Thus, twitch forces are amplified as stimulus frequency increases.</li>\n<li>At a high level of stimulation, intracellular Ca<sup>2+</sup> increases and is maintained throughout the period of stimulation, and the amount of force developed greatly exceeds that seen during a twitch. The response is termed tetany.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7405","text":"The function of the Golgi ​tendon organ is to ______‍___","raw_text":"The function of the Golgi tendon organ is to _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sense changes in muscle di​rection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sense changes in muscle te​nsion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sense changes in position ​of the muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sense changes in length of​ the muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sense changes in muscle tension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0743bd9469c34201b620841d24f08cb0x1280x1556.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79ac1ff4addc4f319d6ab71c5ed720d5x1280x5407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sense changes in muscle tension</strong></p><p>Golgi tendon organ <strong>sense changes in muscle tension.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Golgi tendon reflex</strong> (Inverse myotatic/ Inverse stretch reflex) is <strong>disynaptic</strong>,<strong> </strong>is the opposite of the stretch reflex.</p>\n<p>When the tension in a contracted muscle is significantly elevated, the inhibitory effect from the Golgi tendon organ leads to instantaneous relaxation of the entire muscle. This effect is called the <em>lengthening reaction</em><em>.</em></p>\n<p>It is a protective mechanism to prevent tearing of the muscle or avulsion of the tendon from its attachments to the bone.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: Muscle spindles senses changes in muscle length (stretch).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6921","text":"Which of the following sta​tements are correct regar‍ding mechanisms of depola​risation, repolarisation,‌ and hyperpolarisation in​ a skeletal muscle action‌ potential? Depolarisatio​n due to influx of Na+ th‌rough voltage-gated chann​els Depolarisation due to‌ influx of Na+ through li​gand-gated channels Repol‍arisation due to efflux o‌f K+ through a voltage-ga‍ted channel Repolarisatio‍n due to efflux of K+ thr​ough a ligand-gated chann‌el Hyperpolarisation due ‍to efflux of K+","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are correct regarding mechanisms of depolarisation, repolarisation, and hyperpolarisation in a skeletal muscle action potential?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Depolarisation due to influx of Na+ through voltage-gated channels</li>\n<li>Depolarisation due to influx of Na+ through ligand-gated channels</li>\n<li>Repolarisation due to efflux of K+ through a voltage-gated channel</li>\n<li>Repolarisation due to efflux of K+ through a ligand-gated channel</li>\n<li>Hyperpolarisation due to efflux of K+</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 and 4 a​re correct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3, and 5​ are correct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 4, and 5​ are correct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4, and 5​ are correct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3, and 5 are correct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b827eaffd0bd4a0f9c2c9164046414a4x1280x1683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3, and 5 are correct</strong></p><p>Statements 1, 3, and 5 are correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Action potential in skeletal muscle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Motor-neuron discharge causes end-plate potential, which when reaches to a threshold value, causes action potential in the muscle fibre.</li>\n<li>The <strong>depolarization</strong> of the muscle fibre is caused by the activation of the voltage-gated sodium channels, which causes the <strong>influx of sodium.</strong></li>\n<li>During <strong>repolarization,</strong> the sodium channels are closed, and voltage-gated potassium channels open, which causes <strong>efflux of potassium.</strong></li>\n<li>The voltage-gated potassium channels close slowly. As a result, the <strong>potassium efflux</strong> continues, leading to <strong>hyperpolarization</strong> of the membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The steps in an action potential in muscle fibre and the neuron, are similar except for the <strong>quantitative differences,</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RMP</strong> is -80 to -90 mV in skeletal muscle fibres, while it is about -70mV in neurons.</li>\n<li>The <strong>duration</strong> of the action potential in skeletal muscle is 1 to 5 msec, which is about 5 times longer than in the largest myelinated nerve fibre.</li>\n<li>The <strong>velocity</strong> of conduction is about 3-5m/s, which is about 1/13<sup>th</sup> of the velocity of the nerve fibre.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6920","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about ske‍letal muscle contraction?​ Extracellular Ca2+ is no‌t important for contracti​on Ca2+ is released from ‌mitochondria Ca2+ is rele​ased from the sarcoplasmi‌c reticulum Voltage-gated​ Ca2+ channels on sarcole‌mma have no role in EC co​upling","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements is true about skeletal muscle contraction?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Extracellular Ca2+ is not important for contraction</li>\n<li>Ca2+ is released from mitochondria</li>\n<li>Ca2+ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum</li>\n<li>Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels on sarcolemma have no role in EC coupling</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 o​nly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 only</strong></p><p>During the contraction of the skeletal muscle, the Ca2<sup>+</sup> is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Only statement 3 is correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Excitation contraction coupling</strong> refers to the process by which the membrane depolarization of the muscle fibre <strong>(excitation)</strong> leads to the <strong>contraction</strong> of the muscle.</p>\n<p>The steps of excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Motor-neuron discharge</strong> causes the release of <strong>acetylcholine</strong> at the motor endplate, which binds to the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.</li>\n<li>This causes increased Na+ and K+ conductance in the endplate membrane, leading to the generation of<strong> end-plate potential,</strong> which is a local potential.</li>\n<li>When the local endplate potential reaches a threshold for the muscle fibre, and an <strong>action potential</strong> is generated.</li>\n<li>The generated action potential passes inward through the<strong> T tubules</strong>, leading to the spread of <strong>depolarisation.</strong></li>\n<li>The depolarization of the muscle fibre causes the <strong>release of Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> from terminal cisterns of <strong>sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong> and diffusion to thick and thin filaments.</li>\n<li>The calcium released, <strong>binds to troponin C</strong>, uncovering myosin-binding sites on actin.</li>\n<li>The formation of<strong> cross-linkages between actin and myosin</strong> and sliding of thin on thick filaments causes contraction of the skeletal muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps in relaxation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The cytoplasmic calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, by the action of <strong>SERCA</strong> (sarcoplasmic endoplasmic reticulum calcium ATPase).</li>\n<li>Once the<strong> calcium concentration is lowered</strong>, the interaction between actin and myosin ceases, and the muscle <strong>relaxes.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 116\">\n<div class=\"section\"> </div>\n<div class=\"section\"><strong>Other options:</strong></div>\n<div class=\"section\"> </div>\n<div class=\"section\"><strong>Statement 1:</strong> Incorrect - Extracellular calcium levels play an important role in muscle contraction. When there is a decrease in extracellular calcium as in hypocalcemia, the voltage-gated sodium channels are activated, which causes sodium influx. The increased intracellular sodium concentration causes sustained depolarization, which may lead to <strong>tetany.</strong></div>\n<div class=\"section\"> </div>\n<div class=\"section\"><strong>Statement 2:</strong> Incorrect - Ca2<sup>+</sup> is not released from mitochondria. It is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</div>\n<div class=\"section\"> </div>\n<div class=\"section\"><strong>Statement 4: </strong>Voltage-gated calcium channels on T-tubules (part of sarcolemma) are activated by a depolarizing stimulus, which causes the release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The increased calcium concentration will eventually lead to muscle contraction.</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"bT4uQ_61","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"7_other_psychotic_disorders","title":"7.Other Psychotic Disorders","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0478","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common form of a‌cute transient psychotic ​disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common form of acute transient psychotic disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute polymorphic psychoti​c disorder without sympto‌ms of schizophrenia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute polymorphic psychoti​c disorder with symptoms ‌of schizophrenia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute schizophrenia – like​ psychotic disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute transient psychotic ​disorder, unspecified","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute polymorphic psychotic disorder without symptoms of schizophrenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute polymorphic psychotic disorder without symptoms of schizophrenia</strong></p><p>The most common form of acute transient psychotic disorders is <strong>polymorphic psychotic disorder without symptoms of schizophrenia</strong> (one third to a half of all cases).</p>\n<p>This is <strong>followed by</strong> polymorphic psychotic disorder <strong>with symptoms of schizophrenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Polymorphic psychotic disorder commonly presents with marked hallucinations, delusions, and perceptual disturbances that change in type and intensity from day to day or even from hour to hour.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0482","text":"A man who is distressed de​cides to consult a psychi‌atrist because he keeps h​aving thoughts that his w‌ife is having an affair f‍or the past month. He bel‌ieved that his wife was b‌eing unfaithful; he repor​ted that images of fruit ‌displayed on his wife’s s‌ocial network page signal‍ed that she was having an‌ affair. As a result, he ‌began placing fruit aroun​d their house in order to‌ demonstrate his discover​y. He reported no use of ‍illicit drugs and had no ​other complaints. He was ‌able to do his job. What ‌is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A man who is distressed decides to consult a psychiatrist because he keeps having thoughts that his wife is having an affair for the past month. He believed that his wife was being unfaithful; he reported that images of fruit displayed on his wife’s social network page signaled that she was having an affair. As a result, he began placing fruit around their house in order to demonstrate his discovery. He reported no use of illicit drugs and had no other complaints. He was able to do his job. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paranoid personality disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute transi​ent psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delusiona​l disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Delusional disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Delusional disorder</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of <strong>delusions of infidelity</strong> for a month not attributable to substance abuse and no impairment of function is in favor of a diagnosis of a <strong>delusional disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for diagnosis of delusional disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The presence of one (or more) delusions with a duration of 1 month or longer</li>\n<li>Criteria for schizophrenia have never been met</li>\n<li>Functioning is not markedly impaired, and behavior is not obviously bizarre or odd</li>\n<li>If manic or major depressive episodes have occurred, these have been brief relative to the duration of the delusional periods</li>\n<li>Not attributable to substance abuse, another medical condition, or another mental disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 229px; width: 402.25px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 32px;\">Schizophrenia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.25px; height: 32px; text-align: center;\">Delusional disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 116.5px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 116.5px;\">\n<p>Delusions and hallucinations</p>\n<p>Inappropriate affect</p>\n<p>Socio-occupational dysfunction</p>\n<p>Symptoms&gt;6 months</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.25px; height: 116.5px;\">\n<p>Delusions present but no hallucinations</p>\n<p>Appropriate affect</p>\n<p>Normal social and occupational life</p>\n<p>Symptoms&gt;1 month</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7586","text":"Which of the following is ​not a risk factor for del‌usional disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for delusional disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recent im​migration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Social i​solation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family ​history","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Youn​g age","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Young age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Young age</strong></p><p><strong>Advanced age </strong>is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for delusional disorders, not young age.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors </strong>for delusional disorders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced age</li>\n<li>Sensory impairment or isolation</li>\n<li>Personality features (e.g.unusual interpersonal sensitivity)</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Social isolation</li>\n<li>Recent immigration</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0487","text":"Mr. and Mrs. A believed th​at a 'demon' told Mr. A t‌hat they would be killed ​imminently, so the couple‌ bought a gun to protect ‍themselves. While eating ‌at a restaurant, they per‌ceived two male diners to​ be laughing at them. The‌y believed that the demon‌ was urging them to kill ‍the diners, and they shot‌ the two men dead. While ‌defending the couple in c​ourt, their lawyer mentio‌ned a psychiatric disorde​r. What is the probable d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"Mr. and Mrs. A believed that a 'demon' told Mr. A that they would be killed imminently, so the couple bought a gun to protect themselves. While eating at a restaurant, they perceived two male diners to be laughing at them. They believed that the demon was urging them to kill the diners, and they shot the two men dead. While defending the couple in court, their lawyer mentioned a psychiatric disorder. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psyc​hosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fregoli ​delusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Folie ​à deux","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Folie à deux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Folie à deux</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>folie à deux</strong>. It is also known as <strong>shared delusional disorder</strong>, shared psychotic disorder, or induced psychotic disorder.</p>\n<p>In this syndrome, two individuals with a close and generally long-term relationship <strong>share the same delusional belief</strong>. Persecutory delusional beliefs are most commonly seen in this disorder.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Occasionally, more than two individuals are involved ( e.g., f<span class=\"s1\">olie á</span> <span class=\"s1\">trois, quatre, cinq; </span>also f<span class=\"s1\">olie á</span><span class=\"s2\"> f</span><span class=\"s1\">amille), </span>but such cases are especially rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0481","text":"Which of the following is ​not a type of somatic del‌usion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a type of somatic delusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delusional ​parasitosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Othello ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brom​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dysmorphi​c delusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Othello syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Othello syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Othello Syndrome</strong> is not a type of somatic delusion.</p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>delusional jealousy</strong>, marked by suspecting a faithful partner of infidelity.</p>\n<p>Somatic delusion is diagnosed when the central themes of the delusional beliefs are of a <strong>hypochondriacal or somatic nature, </strong>where the person believes there is something medically, physically or biologically wrong with them.</p>\n<p>It includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Concerns about infestation in delusional <strong>parasitosis</strong></li>\n<li>Preoccupation with body features in <strong>dysmorphic delusions</strong></li>\n<li>Delusional concerns about body odour, referred to as <strong>bromosis     </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0480","text":"A 24-year-old woman is obs​essed with a famous Bolly‌wood actor. She convinced​ herself that he is deepl‌y in love with her and st‍ood outside his home for ‌hours believing that he w‌as communicating his desi​re for her by waving and ‌moving the curtains. Whic‌h of the following is fal‍se regarding this disorde‌r?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman is obsessed with a famous Bollywood actor. She convinced herself that he is deeply in love with her and stood outside his home for hours believing that he was communicating his desire for her by waving and moving the curtains. Which of the following is false regarding this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is referred as an eroto​manic delusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is also known as psycho​se passionelle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient says they fell in ​love with a person of hig‌her status first","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stalking behaviour may be ​observed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patient says they fell in love with a person of higher status first","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patient says they fell in love with a person of higher status first</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>De Clerambault syndrome</strong>. In this, the patients will generally describe that a person of higher status<strong> </strong>had fallen in love with them (patient) and made advances first.</p>\n<p>It is also referred to as an <strong>erotomanic</strong> type of delusional disorder or <strong>psychose passionelle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stalking</strong> may be observed and they most often persecute their targets by unwanted communications.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5916","text":"A young patient is admitte​d to your hospital with a‌cute psychosis. He wakes ​up and asks for his wife ‌even though she is presen‍t in the same room. Upon ‌seeing her, he starts bea‌ting her, accusing her of​ being a nurse, masquerad‌ing as his wife. He also ‌says that the nurse was t‍rying to give him the wro‌ng medication in an attem‌pt to harm him. What is y​our diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young patient is admitted to your hospital with acute psychosis. He wakes up and asks for his wife even though she is present in the same room. Upon seeing her, he starts beating her, accusing her of being a nurse, masquerading as his wife. He also says that the nurse was trying to give him the wrong medication in an attempt to harm him. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capgras ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fregoli ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delusion of subjective dou​bles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Othello ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Capgras syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Capgras syndrome</strong></p><p>In the given scenario the patient thinks that the nurse (persecutor/stranger) has taken the guise of the wife (family person). This is characteristic of<strong> Capgras syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Delusional misidentification syndromes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Capgras</strong> syndrome<strong> - </strong>The delusion in Capgras syndrome is the belief that a <strong>familiar person</strong> has been <strong>replaced by</strong> an <strong>imposter</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong> Fregoli’s</strong> syndrome<strong> - </strong>Fregoli syndrome is a disorder in which a person holds a delusional belief that <strong>different people/strangers</strong> are in fact<strong> a single person</strong> who changes his or her appearance or is in disguise.</li>\n<li>Syndrome of <strong>intermetamorphosis - </strong>A rare delusion that familiar persons can change themselves into other persons at will.</li>\n<li><strong>Doppelganger</strong> syndrome<strong> - </strong>A person feels he has his own impostor (duplicate) – <strong>delusion of subjective doubles.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Othello</strong> syndrome<strong> - </strong>It is a rare psychiatric condition marked by <strong>morbid</strong>, pathological, or <strong>delusional jealousy</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0488","text":"A 32-year-old man is broug​ht to the psychiatric OPD‌ with complaints that he ​misidentifies strangers a‌s a single persecutor wea‍ring different disguises.‌ What is this syndrome ca‌lled?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the psychiatric OPD with complaints that he misidentifies strangers as a single persecutor wearing different disguises. What is this syndrome called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cotard ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capgras ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fregoli ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Magnan ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fregoli syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fregoli syndrome</strong></p><p>The man in the question is mistaking strangers as a familiar person/persecutor. He is under the delusion that the<strong> familiar person</strong>/stranger can assume the <strong>appearance</strong> of (or <strong>disguise</strong> themselves as) <strong>multiple strangers.</strong> This is known as <strong>Fregoli syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Cotard syndrome </strong>(delire de negation/nihilistic delusion disorder) - the patient complains that they have lost all their possessions, status, their entire being, and even their internal organs.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong><strong> Capgras syndrome</strong></strong> (illusion des sosies) - believes that his or her family members or other <strong>familiar people</strong> have been <strong>replaced</strong> by identical-looking <strong>imposters.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Magnan syndrome - </strong>occurs in patients with cocaine addiction where they experience cocaine bugs/tactile hallucinations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0484","text":"A 14-year-old boy was brou​ght to the ER by his moth‌er after he began talking​ about his younger brothe‌r trying to kill him. Acc‍ording to him, he had kil‌led his twin brother in u‌tero, and now he was seek​ing revenge. The mother r‌evealed that her pregnanc‌y was a singleton. On exa‍mination, the boy was dir‌ty, dressed inappropriate‌ly and multiple injection​ tracks could be seen on ‌his arm and forearm. The ​first-year resident suspe‍cts substance-induced psy​chosis. Which of the foll‌owing is not in favor of ‌the provisional diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy was brought to the ER by his mother after he began talking about his younger brother trying to kill him. According to him, he had killed his twin brother in utero, and now he was seeking revenge. The mother revealed that her pregnancy was a singleton. On examination, the boy was dirty, dressed inappropriately and multiple injection tracks could be seen on his arm and forearm. The first-year resident suspects substance-induced psychosis. Which of the following is not in favor of the provisional diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The patient has no insight​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is no history of rec​urrent primary psychotic ‌disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The disturbance causes cli​nically significant distr‌ess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Symptoms persist long afte​r intoxicated/withdrawal ‌state","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Symptoms persist long after intoxicated/withdrawal state","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Symptoms persist long after intoxicated/withdrawal state</strong></p><p>Substance-induced psychotic disorders develop<strong> during or soon after</strong> substance intoxication or withdrawal and they<strong> do not persist long after.</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"mw-headline\">DSM-5 and ICD-11 diagnostic criteria for substance-induced psychosis:</span></p>\n<p>A. Presence of one or both of the following symptoms:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Delusions.</li>\n<li>Hallucinations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>B. There is evidence from the history, physical examination, or laboratory findings of both (1) and (2):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The symptoms in Criterion A developed during or soon after substance intoxication/withdrawal/after exposure to a medication.</li>\n<li>The involved substance/medication is capable of producing the symptoms in Criterion A.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>C. The disturbance is <strong>not better explained</strong> by a primary <strong>psychiatric disorder</strong> that is not substance/medication-induced. Such evidence of an independent psychotic disorder could include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The symptoms preceded the onset of the substance/medication use.</li>\n<li>The symptoms persist for a substantial period of time (e.g., about 1 month) after the cessation of acute withdrawal or severe intoxication.</li>\n<li>There is other evidence of an independent non-substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder (e.g., a history of recurrent non-substance/medication-related episodes).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>D. The disturbance does <strong>not</strong> occur <strong>exclusively</strong> during the course of a <strong>delirium.</strong></p>\n<p>E. The disturbance causes <strong>clinically significant distress</strong> or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0483","text":"A homeless man who is an a​lcoholic was brought to t‌he casualty by the police​ as they had witnessed hi‌m gouging out sections of‍ his skin. On examination‌, the following findings ‌are noted. The police sai​d that he was a drug deal‌er and often resorted to ‌experimenting with variou‍s illicit substances. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A homeless man who is an alcoholic was brought to the casualty by the police as they had witnessed him gouging out sections of his skin. On examination, the following findings are noted. The police said that he was a drug dealer and often resorted to experimenting with various illicit substances. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cannabis-induced psychosis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amphetamine-induced psycho​sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alcohol-indu​ced psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cocaine-indu​ced psychosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cocaine-induced psychosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/005e461386404657bc3351cd4410df96.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cocaine-induced psychosis</strong></p><p>The image showing multiple gouged-out sections of skin in accessible areas, and the history of multiple drug usage, is suggestive of <strong>formication</strong> (the <strong>tactile</strong> hallucination of <strong>bugs crawling under the skin</strong>) which is commonly associated with <strong>cocaine-induced psychosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Cannabis</strong>-induced psychotic disorder usually involves <strong>persecutory delusions</strong>, anxiety, emotional lability, and <strong>depersonalization</strong> experiences.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Amphetamine</strong>-induced psychotic disorder often resembles <strong>schizophrenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Alcohol</strong>-induced psychotic disorder often presents with<strong> auditory hallucinations</strong> after prolonged and heavy use of alcohol.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4Phph8NQ","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"7_congenital_neural_anomalies__neurocutaneous_disorders___cerebrovascular_disorders","title":"7.Congenital Neural Anomalies, Neurocutaneous Disorders & Cerebrovascular Disorders","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD0669","text":"A patient complains of nec‍k stiffness and severe he​adache. The CT image show​n below is suggestive of ‍which of the following co​nditions?","raw_text":"A patient complains of neck stiffness and severe headache. The CT image shown below is suggestive of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menin‍gitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subarachnoi‍d hemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intraparenchymal hemorrhag‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhag‍ic stroke","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subarachnoid hemorrhage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c86b6d6a946142e980f012790bde8ff9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The clinical manifestations of neck stiffness and severe headache with CT revealing <strong>blood</strong> in the <strong>basal cistern</strong> points out to the diagnosis of <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong> (SAH).</p>\n<p>Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) is the bleeding into the subarachnoid space, which is the area between the arachnoid membrane and the pia mater surrounding the brain. The diagnosis is suspected when a <strong>hyperattenuating material</strong> is seen <strong>filling the subarachnoid space</strong>. Most commonly this is apparent <strong>around the circle of Willis</strong>, on account of the majority of berry aneurysms occurring in this region (65%), or in the Sylvian fissure (30%).</p>\n<p>The <strong>modified Fisher scale</strong> is a method for grading SAH as seen on non-contrast CT (NCCT). The <strong>Hunt and Hess scale</strong> describes the severity of SAH and is used as a predictor of survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Xanthochromia on CSF</strong> is indicative of SAH in case of a negative CT scan. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1672","text":"What is the most​ common s‍ite of hypertensive bleed​ in the brain?","raw_text":"What is the most​ common site of hypertensive bleed in the brain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal ‍ganglia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cereb‍ellum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po‍ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thal‍amus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Basal ganglia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Basal ganglia</strong></p><p>The most common site of hypertensive bleed in the brain is the <strong>basal ganglia </strong>(<strong>Putamen </strong>in particular).</p>\n<p>The most common cause of intracerebral haemorrhages is <strong>hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>Following are the typical locations of ICH in order of decreasing frequency: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>  Basal ganglia haemorrhage (especially the putamen)</li>\n<li>  Thalamic haemorrhage</li>\n<li>  Pontine haemorrhage</li>\n<li>  Cerebellar haemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1674","text":"A 4-year-old boy is brough‍t to casualty after falli​ng from a balcony. Based ​on the CT scan finding pr‍ovided, what is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to casualty after falling from a balcony. Based on the CT scan finding provided, what is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subdural‍ hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subarchnoid‍ hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural‍ hematoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhagi‍c contusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epidural hematoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b596bc74f302487790a0144dd5c9c7f2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d349d08c232d4b29b72311607b38fcbbx784x963.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epidural hematoma</strong></p><p>The above axial CT shows a <strong>hyperdense lentiform (biconvex) opacity</strong>, consistent with an <strong>epidural hematoma</strong>, which is a collection of blood between the inner surface of the skull and the outer periosteal layer of the dura mater, typically following a history of trauma. The characteristic biconvex shape arises due to the limited ability of blood to expand within the fixed attachment of periosteal dura to the cranial sutures. If bleeding is significant, it may lead to a mass effect with herniation. </p>\n<p>Additionally, a<strong> swirl</strong> <strong>sign</strong> may be present, representing acute blood extravasation into a hematoma (extradural/subdural), showing fresh, unclotted blood with lower attenuation than the clotted blood (which is of a higher attenuation), highlighting the need for surgical intervention.</p>\n<p>The following CT image depicts a swirl sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1675","text":"A 28-year-old man is broug‍ht to the emergency room ​following an RTA. His GCS​ progressively declines o‍ver hours. Based on the M​RI finding shown, what is​ the most likely diagnosi‌s? RTA- Road traffic acci‌dent","raw_text":"<p>A 28-year-old man is brought to the emergency room following an RTA. His GCS progressively declines over hours. Based on the MRI finding shown, what is the most likely diagnosis? </p>\n<p>RTA- Road traffic accident</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffuse ax‍onal injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extensive subarachnoid hae‍morrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoxic cer‍ebral injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple haemorrhagic cont‍usion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diffuse axonal injury","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f789d158017645ffafe37836664d9f28.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d3f52bf065c4f279aa3c99d62f01890x1200x1335.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diffuse axonal injury</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of a declining GCS score following a road traffic accident, combined with the given MRI demonstrating <strong>hypointense microhemorrhagic foci</strong>, suggests<strong> diffuse axonal injury (DAI)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diffuse axonal injury (white matter shearing) is a severe form of traumatic brain injury caused by rotational acceleration/deceleration, leading to axonal shearing or disruption and forming microhemorrhages. Typically, these lesions are located at the <strong>grey-white matter junction</strong> in the corpus callosum and, in severe cases, the brainstem. </p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is the preferred modality for assessing suspected diffuse axonal injury, even in patients with normal CT of the brain. Gradient-recalled echo (<strong>GRE</strong>) &amp; susceptibility-weighted imaging (<strong>SWI</strong>) are the most sensitive MRI sequences for detecting micro-bleeds, which appear as hypointense foci. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1676","text":"A 9-year-old child present‍s with recurrent transien​t ischemic attacks and oc​casional headaches. Cereb‍ral X-ray angiography is ​done as shown below. What​ is the likely diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"A 9-year-old child presents with recurrent transient ischemic attacks and occasional headaches. Cerebral X-ray angiography is done as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACA an‍eurysm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moyamoya‍ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vein of Galen malformation‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moyamoya disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/629900efcea8472a845e3ed1aaa8dc52.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moyamoya disease</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and cerebral X-ray angiography image shows the \"<strong>puff of smoke</strong>\" appearance, characteristic of <strong>Moyamoya disease. </strong></p>\n<p>Moyamoya disease is a rare, non-inflammatory, progressive, occlusive disease affecting the large intracranial arteries. In this condition, the lenticulostriate arteries form a rich <strong>collateral circulation</strong> around the occlusive lesions, creating the \"puff of smoke\" angiographic appearance, referred to as \"moyamoya\" in Japanese. </p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">It exhibits a bimodal age distribution, with onset typically occurring in the first and fourth decades of life. The most common presentation is <strong>stroke</strong> or <strong>transient ischemic attacks</strong> (TIA), with the nature of these events differing by age group. In childhood, ischemic events are predominant, while hemorrhagic manifestations are frequent in adults.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1677","text":"The CT scan of a patient w‍ho presented with deterio​rating consciousness is s​hown below. What is the d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"The CT scan of a patient who presented with deteriorating consciousness is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subdural‍ hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tension pne‍umocephalus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non tension pneumocephalus‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidura‍l lipoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tension pneumocephalus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2445bef1f939478592f509e8c43dbf65.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tension pneumocephalus</strong></p><p>The given CT image demonstrates the <strong>Mount Fuji sign</strong>, a specific radiological finding of <strong>tension pneumocephalus</strong>.</p>\n<p>This sign is characterised by bilateral compression and separation of the frontal lobes due to the trapped subdural and interhemispheric free air, which appears as non-attenuating collections resembling the peak of Mount Fuji. </p>\n<p>Tension pneumocephalus is a rare, <strong>life-threatening</strong> condition most commonly caused by traumatic head injury. Other causes include skull base fractures, neurosurgical procedures, infections (meningitis, encephalitis, or brain abscess), brain tumours, or the use of nitrous oxide during anaesthesia. </p>\n<p>The <strong>mass effect</strong> exerted by the subdural air compresses the underlying brain parenchyma, often resulting from a ball-valve mechanism causing one-way air entry into the subdural space. This <strong>raises intracranial pressure</strong> (ICP), distinguishing it from non-tension pneumocephalus, which lacks significant mass effect or raised ICP. </p>\n<p>The elevated ICP leads to rapid clinical deterioration, such as worsening consciousness, necessitating <strong>urgent neurosurgical intervention</strong> to decompress the trapped air and prevent permanent brain damage. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3899","text":"What is the best imaging s‍tudy for earliest diagnos​is of cerebral infarct?","raw_text":"What is the best imaging study for earliest diagnosis of cerebral infarct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FLAI‍R MRI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-con‍trast CT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CE‍CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffusion w‍eighted MRI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diffusion weighted MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f687eb5c7b23433bb7e86db7453bbadcx510x567.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5ca22c7b8db43bcbd52ab147e76ff71x1280x1751.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diffusion weighted MRI</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>best imaging modality for the early diagnosis of <strong>cerebral infarction</strong> is <strong>diffusion-weighted MRI (DWI)</strong>, which can detect infarcts as early as 30 minutes of onset and identify cytotoxic oedema within a few hours of ischemic stroke.</p>\n<p>However, the<strong> </strong>initial investigation in suspected stroke cases is <strong>non-contrast CT </strong>due to its rapid acquisition time, widespread availability, and cost-effectiveness. It helps quickly rule out hemorrhagic stroke and facilitates the timely administration of intravenous tPA in eligible patients.</p>\n<p>Acute MCA territory infarct on CT is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below is of a DWI MRI of a patient clearly showing the extent of the infarct. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3896","text":"The empty delta sign is a ‍radiological feature of w​hich of the following con​ditions?","raw_text":"The empty delta sign is a radiological feature of which of the following conditions?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebral venous thrombosis‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intracerebra‍l haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subdural ‍hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cerebral venous thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa0d34b1371d4ae7bfbeeba5a816c9e4x1280x1325.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cerebral venous thrombosis</strong></p><p>The <strong>empty delta sign</strong> is a characteristic finding of <strong>superior sagittal sinus thrombosis</strong>, seen on contrast-enhanced CT(CECT) or MRI. It appears as a <strong>triangular</strong> filling defect(clot) outlined by contrast. This sign is not observed on non-contrast imaging. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1678","text":"Which condition is associa‍ted with the pancake brai​n sign on CT?","raw_text":"Which condition is associated with the pancake brain sign on CT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Holopros‍encephaly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lissen‍cephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schizen‍cephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Porenc‍ephaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Holoprosencephaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e7c6941bd094324a343754e29ad5b6ax630x630.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfd18bef5b9e4f7ba10f87d3f73d4033x928x1068.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77574f2259034354865128f9d3b3b498x842x963.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Holoprosencephaly</strong></p><p>The<strong> pancake brain sign </strong>represents the appearance of the cerebral parenchyma in the case of <strong>a</strong><strong>lobar holoprosencephaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Holoprosencephaly is a developmental disorder of the brain caused by the defective formation of the <strong>prosencephalon</strong> and impaired induction of forebrain structures. It is commonly associated with trisomy 13 (<strong>Patau syndrome</strong>), with alobar holoprosencephaly representing the most severe form. Features of holoprosencephaly include a single ventricular cavity (<strong>boomerang-shaped/horseshoe ventricle</strong>), fusion of frontal lobes, corpus callosum dysgenesis and other abnormalities.</p>\n<p>The following image is a CT brain showing a 'pancake appearance' in alobar holoprosencephaly:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Lissencephaly</strong> represents a group of disorders caused by defective neuronal migration in the period between the 8th and 14th gestational weeks, resulting in a lack of development of gyri and sulci. The image given below shows lissencephaly:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Schizencephaly</strong> is the presence of unilateral or bilateral clefts within the cerebral hemispheres due to an abnormality of morphogenesis. The image given below shows schizencephaly:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Porencephaly</strong> is an extremely rare cephalic disorder involving encephalomalacia. It is a neurological disorder of the central nervous system characterized by cysts or cavities within the cerebral hemisphere.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1679","text":"Identify this congenital a‍nomaly found on the MRI i​mage of a neonate.","raw_text":"Identify this congenital anomaly found on the MRI image of a neonate.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Holopros‍encephaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lissen‍cephaly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Schizen‍cephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Porenc‍ephaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lissencephaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03f8086794af4651af047f84e14d3daa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0da11c035432480e9365a152bda53cbax645x578.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lissencephaly</strong></p><p>The given axial T1 weighted image demonstrates the typical <strong>figure of eight sign</strong> consistent in a neonate with<strong> lissencephaly</strong>. This disorder results from defective neuronal migration in the period between the 8th and 14th weeks of gestation, leading to a poor development or absence of gyri and sulci, thickened cortex, and shallow, widened Sylvian fissures, contributing to the above radiographic appearance. Other associated anomalies may include agenesis or hypoplasia of the corpus callosum or septum pellucidum. </p>\n<p>The following image shows the figure of eight sign of lissencephaly:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Holoprosencephaly is a developmental disorder of the brain that results from the defective formation of the prosencephalon and inadequate induction of forebrain structures. The pancake brain sign represents the appearance of the cerebral parenchyma in the case of alobar holoprosencephaly.</p>\n<p>Option C: Schizencephaly is the presence of unilateral or bilateral clefts within the cerebral hemispheres due to an abnormality of morphogenesis. </p>\n<p>Option D: Porencephaly is an extremely rare cephalic disorder involving encephalomalacia. It is a neurological disorder of the central nervous system characterized by cysts or cavities within the cerebral hemisphere.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1681","text":"A mother brings her 2-mont‍h-old infant with complai​nts of irritability and s​eizures. On examination, ‍the infant has a bulging ​fontanelle and macrocepha​ly. CT was done, which sh‌ows the following. What i‌s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 2-month-old infant with complaints of irritability and seizures. On examination, the infant has a bulging fontanelle and macrocephaly. CT was done, which shows the following. What is the most likely diagnosis? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dandy-Walker‍ malformation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Arnold Chiari malformation‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vein of Galen malformation‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joubert ‍syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dandy-Walker malformation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b9f78d826b54797aeec7aef6fb65c30.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a25a3e15d90479cad7b362b3501730ax1280x1260.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aeeedfbb2d644018868d74c701592b84x517x563.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc9f25fd8f7742ecb3934de33e6cf5adx720x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5449c99a10ca4d0e9658d172f5f77feex446x522.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dandy-Walker malformation</strong></p><p>The CT findings along with the clinical features are consistent with <strong>Dandy-Walker malformation</strong>. CT image shows a <strong>large cystic area </strong>that occupies the posterior aspect of the posterior fossa<strong> </strong>communicating directly with the <strong>fourth ventricle</strong>. No normal vermis<strong> </strong>can be identified<strong> </strong>with a small vermian remnant appearing displaced and rotated superiorly. An <strong>enlarged posterior fossa</strong> with an elevation of the lateral venous sinuses and the tentorium is also noted. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: Arnold Chiari malformation consists of herniation of the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum. It is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chiari I</strong> malformation is the most common type which is associated with peg-like <strong>cerebellar tonsils</strong> displaced into the upper cervical canal through the foramen magnum. It is also associated with <strong>syringomyelia</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chiari II</strong> malformation is the displacement of the medulla, fourth ventricle, and cerebellar vermis through the foramen magnum. It is associated with <strong>lumbosacral spinal myelomeningocele</strong>. The image given below shows a Chiari malformation:</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C:  The MR angiography in the <strong>vein of Galen malformation</strong> shows an enlarged <strong>midline venous structure</strong>, with multiple arteriovenous communications leading to aneurysmal dilatation. The image below shows the malformation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Joubert syndrome</strong> is an autosomal recessive neurologic disorder. A pathognomonic finding on axial MRI of the brain is the presence of prominent superior cerebellar peduncles, referred to as '<strong>molar tooth sign</strong>' of the midbrain-hindbrain junction. The image given below shows the molar tooth sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1933","text":"The MRI of a patient who p‍resented with seizures is​ shown below. What is the​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The MRI of a patient who presented with seizures is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sturge-Web‍er syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tubercular‍ Meningitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menin‍gioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Von Hippel-L‍indau disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sturge-Weber syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a63eb42dc504e4a84418fe424c6dac1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70e224cb8f474989967b054500d2890dx720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sturge-Weber syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation of seizures, characteristic port-wine stain on the face(capillary malformation), along with the above MRI findings of <strong>leptomeningeal enhancement </strong>and<strong> cortical atrophy, </strong>indicate a diagnosis of <strong>Sturge-Weber syndrome</strong>. These findings are because of plial angiomatosis best visualized in contrast-enhanced MRI.</p>\n<p>It occurs due to<strong> </strong>leptomeningeal hemangioma<strong>, </strong>resulting in a vascular steal affecting the subjacent cortex and white matter producing localized ischemia and calcification.</p>\n<p>The CT brain below, also shows <strong>tram-track cortical calcifications</strong>, a characteristic feature of Sturge-Weber syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1998","text":"An 18-year-old male presen‍ted with seizures. On exa​mination, hypopigmented m​acules and facial angiofi‍bromas were present. CT i​mage is given below. What​ is the probable diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old male presented with seizures. On examination, hypopigmented macules and facial angiofibromas were present. CT image is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Von Hippel-L‍indau disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofibr‍omatosis 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ‍sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sturge-Web‍er syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tuberous sclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/440883a11019407a98e2f28515aa79d1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df9583a069e14cc8860c6b60f1a0d2e1x1048x1258.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tuberous sclerosis</strong></p><p>The given CT image shows <strong>calcified subependymal hamartomatous nodules. </strong>The CT findings along with <strong>hypopigmented macules</strong> (ash-leaf macules) and <strong>facial angiofibromas </strong>(adenoma sebaceum) is suggestive of <strong>tuberous sclerosis. </strong>It is associated with renal angiomyolipoma and cardiac rhabdomyoma.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows calcified subependymal hamartomatous nodules:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2002","text":"The MRI of a patient who p‍resented with headache an​d imbalance is shown belo​w. What is the likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"The MRI of a patient who presented with headache and imbalance is shown below.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofib‍romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Von Hippel-L‍indau disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ‍Sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sturge Web‍er Syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Von Hippel-Lindau disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/114d0c87b70d468d90f91c79e6b17fa8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acabfd2459224158828d500144d94f30x748x892.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e10ce139f8949daa6a41e4e2fdb22dcx1280x1395.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4756b3d83eba46c1be8300267077a009x1280x1418.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed83e3d940bc4bb89128a669a3635952x720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Von Hippel-Lindau disease</strong></p><p>The given axial T1WI MRI of the brain shows a <strong>well-defined hypointense lesion</strong> in the posterior fossa suggestive of<strong> hemangioblastoma</strong>. Hemangioblastomas are tumours of vascular origin and occur both in patients with <strong>Von Hippel Lindau</strong> (VHL) disease as well as sporadically. </p>\n<p>Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) disease characterises the development of numerous benign and malignant tumours in different organs. It is associated with a mutation in the VHL tumour suppressor gene on <strong>chromosome 3 </strong>(VHL has 3 letters: chromosome 3). They are WHO-grade I tumours that can occur in the central nervous system.</p>\n<p>Features of VHL include pheochromocytoma, CNS hemangioblastoma - cerebellar and spinal, retinal haemangioblastoma, and choroid plexus papilloma.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Neurofibromatosis is an <strong>autosomal-dominant </strong>neurocutaneous syndrome of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NF1</strong> (neurofibromatosis 1) is also called as Von Recklinghausen disease. It involves the NF1 gene on chromosome <strong>17.</strong> It is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple<strong> café-au-lait</strong> macules and associated <strong>cutaneous neurofibromas. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>NF2</strong> (Central neurofibromatosis) is caused by the mutation on chromosome<strong> 22 </strong>and predisposes individuals to multiple nervous system tumours. Most common are <strong>bilateral vestibular schwannomas,</strong> intracranial and spinal meningiomas, gliomas, neurofibromas, and posterior subcapsular lenticular opacities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On MRI, extracanalicular extension of vestibular schwannomas into the cerebellopontine angle can lead to an \"<strong>ice cream cone</strong>\" appearance as shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Tuberous sclerosis is a <strong>multi-system</strong> neurocutaneous syndrome caused by the mutation of genes coding for <strong>hamartin</strong> (chromosome 9) and <strong>tuberin</strong> (chromosome 16), which leads to the formation of angiomyolipomas, cysts in kidneys, multiple hamartomas, giant cell astrocytomas, and cortical tubers. Skin manifestation includes <strong>facial angiofibroma</strong> (adenoma sebaceum), <strong>shagreen patch</strong> and <strong>confetti skin lesions</strong>. It can also be associated with <strong>renal angiomyolipoma</strong> and <strong>cardiac rhabdomyoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The CT image given below shows calcified subependymal hamartomatous nodules:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Sturge Weber syndrome is a <strong>neurocutaneous</strong> disorder(phakomatoses) that is characterized by unilateral capillary malformation at the region of trigeminal supply known as <strong>port wine stain</strong> or nevus flammeus, ipsilateral diffuse <strong>cavernous hemangioma</strong> of the choroid and ipsilateral<strong> leptomeningeal hemangiomatosis</strong>, which causes seizures and mental retardation.</p>\n<p>Features of MRI brain include <strong>pial angioma</strong>, seen as a diffuse pial enhancement of variable thickness, <strong>enlargement</strong> of the ipsilateral <strong>choroid plexus</strong> and dilatation of trans-parenchymal veins that communicate between the superficial and deep cerebral venous systems. </p>\n<p>MRI and CT show<strong> intracranial calcification</strong> respectively. <strong>The tram-track sign</strong> in the brain refers to the parallel calcification of the cerebral cortex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2019","text":"A mother brought her 7-mon‍th-old infant with concer​ns of abnormal eye moveme​nts and developmental del‍ay. MRI was done and is s​hown below. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 7-month-old infant with concerns of abnormal eye movements and developmental delay. MRI was done and is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joubert ‍syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple sy‍stem atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hallevorden-‍Spatz disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sturge Web‍er syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Joubert syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a1b183c6aaf0417eb4d3eb697995dac3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Joubert syndrome</strong></p><p>The axial T1-weighted MRI shows <strong>molar tooth sign</strong> at the level of superior cerebellar peduncles. Given the clinical presentation and radiological findings, the diagnosis is <strong>Joubert syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>This <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> neurological disorder is characterised by <strong>cerebellar vermis hypoplasia</strong> and is associated with hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, irregular breathing patterns, and abnormal eye movements.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2022","text":"Eye of tiger sign on MRI i‍s seen in which of the fo​llowing conditions?","raw_text":"Eye of tiger sign on MRI is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hallervorden-Spatz syndrom‍e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Creutzfeldt-‍Jakob disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sturge-Web‍er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurofibr‍omatosis 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hallervorden-Spatz syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f84930ec512d44ebae5ffc4fe1a20f91x630x630.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hallervorden-Spatz syndrome</strong></p><p>The <strong>eye of the tiger sign </strong>can be seen in <strong>Hallervorden-Spatz syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The '<strong>eye of the tiger</strong>' sign on the T2-weighted MRI appears as bilateral symmetrical <strong>hyperintense</strong> signals in global pallidus (due to gliosis and spongiosis), surrounded by <strong>hypointense </strong>areas (due to abnormal accumulation of iron).</p>\n<p>Hallervorden-Spatz syndrome or Pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration (<strong>PKAN</strong>) is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder causing involuntary spasticity and progressive dementia. It is a subset of neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (NBIA). </p>\n<p>It is also seen in Progressive supranuclear palsy, early-onset levodopa-responsive Parkinsonism, and cortical-basal ganglionic degeneration.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts the eye of the tiger sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3870","text":"What is the earliest skull‍ x-ray sign in a child wi​th raised intracranial te​nsion (ICT)?","raw_text":"What is the earliest skull x-ray sign in a child with raised intracranial tension (ICT)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sutural ‍diastasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Silver/copper beaten skull‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior cl‍inoid erosion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erosion of the dorsum sell‍a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sutural diastasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/931a365cc3584b5c98360693657bc844x1209x1160.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bf8b91602b04fafa72faa043be01771x830x1182.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sutural diastasis</strong></p><p>The earliest skull x-ray sign in a child with raised ICT is <strong>sutural diastasis</strong>. Another sign is the <strong>silver- or copper-beaten skull</strong>, which refers to prominent markings of convolution on the skull<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The images below are lateral and PA films of a child with raised ICT and <strong>marked suture diastasis (widening)</strong> involving the <strong>coronal</strong> and <strong>lambdoid</strong> sutures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> The earliest skull x-ray sign in an adult with raised ICT is <strong>posterior clinoid erosion</strong>, which is followed by<strong> erosion of the dorsum sella.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9234","text":"Based on the given image, ‍what is the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"Based on the given image, what is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carotico-cavernous fistula‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudoaneurysm of internal‍ carotid artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tumour blush of angiofibro‍ma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensive head and neck he‍mangioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudoaneurysm of internal carotid artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/300ba733040f4fd589ace0391a3ef79d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4bd65514e39c4af5aee6f69f0406347ex510x510.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudoaneurysm of internal carotid artery</strong></p><p>The given image is a <strong>digital subtraction angiogram(DSA)</strong> and it shows a <strong>p</strong><strong>seudoaneurysm </strong>of the <strong>internal carotid artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The right ICA injection anteroposterior view shows the neck of the pseudoaneurysm originating from the <strong>transverse segment </strong>of the<strong> cavernous portion </strong>of the ICA.</p>\n<p>Option A: Carotico-cavernous fistula is characterized by the premature filling of the veins as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: Angiofibroma is characterised by tumour blush. It is the radiological enhancement of a tumour that reveals opacification of the well-vascularized tumours.</p>\n<p>Option D: Hemangioma can be differentiated from other vascular malformations by the presence of well-circumscribed mass demonstrating intense tissue staining, usually in a lobular pattern.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4004","text":"A child presents with a le‍ft-to-right shunt and hig​h-output cardiac failure.​ The following is his MRA‍. What is the most likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child presents with a left-to-right shunt and high-output cardiac failure. The following is his MRA. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vein of Galen malformation‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dandy Walk‍er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumoc‍ephalus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Venous sinu‍s thrombosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vein of Galen malformation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28c4c84da48147a2b5914b5bf725bb1c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78947251be814c76a80bb6a597e114a1x600x515.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vein of Galen malformation</strong></p><p>The MR-Angiography shown is of <strong>Vein of Galen Malformation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vein of Galen aneurysmal malformations</strong> <strong>(VGAM's)</strong>, also known as <strong>median prosencephalic arteriovenous fistula</strong>, are uncommon intracranial anomalies that tend to present in early childhood with features of a left-to-right shunt and high-output cardiac failure. </p>\n<p>The vein of Galen, also known as the great cerebral vein or great vein of Galen, is a short trunk formed by the union of the two internal cerebral veins and basal veins of Rosenthal. It lies in the quadrigeminal cistern. It curves backwards and upward around the posterior border of the splenium of the corpus callosum and drains the anterior and central regions of the brain into the sinuses of the posterior cerebral fossa.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the Vein of Galen:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0972","text":"A 30-year-old woman has be‍en experiencing intermitt​ent visual disturbances a​nd episodes of difficulty‍ walking over the past fe​w months. Neurological ex​amination reveals spastic‌ity in the lower limbs an‌d hyperreflexia. T2W MRI ‌of the brain shows perive‌ntricular ovoid hyperinte‍nse lesions oriented perp‌endicular to lateral vent​ricles. Which of the radi‍ological findings is most​ characteristic of this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman has been experiencing intermittent visual disturbances and episodes of difficulty walking over the past few months. Neurological examination reveals spasticity in the lower limbs and hyperreflexia. T2W MRI of the brain shows periventricular ovoid hyperintense lesions oriented perpendicular to lateral ventricles. Which of the radiological findings is most characteristic of this condition? \n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Puff of smok‍e appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tigroid‍ pattern","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dawson’s‍ fingers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hummingb‍ird sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dawson’s fingers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd1341f8b0d44539b6f1d36e0ae52092x1279x1211.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c4c082b8ead4cab8f5bb3df228a844dx392x485.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f13f4f3c2a74b94a1d0723e97edf64cx720x543.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5771ec951f56416fae7ae089dd653804x1212x1052.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47daeddca0784f8ea645ca19f4353992x510x506.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dawson’s fingers</strong></p><p>This patient's clinical features and T2W MRI of the brain showing periventricular ovoid hyperintense lesions oriented perpendicular to lateral ventricles, called <strong>Dawson's fingers</strong>, are characteristic of <strong>multiple sclerosis. </strong></p>\n<p>Multiple sclerosis(MS)<strong> </strong>is an<strong> autoimmune demyelinating disease </strong>of the central nervous system<strong> </strong>characterized by inflammation, multiple demyelinating plaques, and relapsing and remitting symptoms.</p>\n<p>It is commonly associated with<strong> </strong>paresthesia (tingling sensations),<strong> </strong>hypesthesia (numbness), <strong>optic neuritis, unilateral/ bilateral, limb weakness - spastic paralysis, </strong>Babinski's sign positive and bladder dysfunction. Optic neuritis (ON) presents as diminished visual acuity or decreased colour perception (desaturation) in the central field of vision.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI </strong>is used for making the diagnosis of MS due to its high sensitivity. The diagnosis of MS requires demonstrating that the lesions are<strong> disseminated in space </strong>(involving multiple areas of the brain) and in<strong> time </strong>(multiple episodes over a period of time).</p>\n<p>The MRI below demonstrates Dawson's fingers, the demyelinating plaques through the corpus callosum, arranged at right angles along medullary veins (callososeptal location).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: A \"<strong>puff of smoke appearance</strong>\", derived from the Japanese term Moyamoya, is a characteristic finding on cerebral X-ray angiography in patients with Moyamoya disease. This rare, non-inflammatory, progressive, occlusive disease involves<strong> large intracranial arteries</strong> exhibiting bimodal age distribution in the first and fourth decades. Childhood moyamoya is characterised by ischemic manifestations, and adult moyamoya by hemorrhagic manifestations.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the MR angiography findings in a patient with Moyamoya disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>As this is a chronic condition, the lenticulostriate arteries develop a rich collateral circulation around the occlusive lesion, seen as a 'puff of smoke' (moyamoya in Japanese) on X-ray angiography as shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Metachromatic leukodystrophy </strong>is an autosomal-recessive disease resulting from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase A, causing central and peripheral demyelination, ataxia, and dementia.</p>\n<p>Symmetric confluent areas of T2 hyperintensity are seen in the periventricular white matter. Radiating T2 hypointense lines through the demyelinated deep white matter represent the sparing of the perivascular white matter. This results in the <strong>tigroid</strong> and<strong> leopard skin patterns</strong> when imaged in the long axis or cross-section, respectively. Subcortical U-fibers are spared. The below MRI shows tigroid pattern: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Progressive supranuclear palsy</strong> is a neurodegenerative disease characterized by atrophy of structures at the midbrain-diencephalic junction (e.g., superior colliculi, red nuclei, subthalamic nuclei, and globus pallidus) and cerebellum (dentate nuclei), and mild cortical atrophy. MRI shows a <strong>hummingbird sign</strong> (atrophy of midbrain structures with a relatively intact pons region). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WwLPBiok","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"7_breast___anatomy__congenital_and_benign_diseases","title":"7.Breast   Anatomy, Congenital And Benign Diseases","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0338","text":"Which of the following hor​mones will stimulate lobu‍lar development of the br‍east?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones will stimulate lobular development of the breast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges​terone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Estrogen and​ progesterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prol​actin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e884a2a77954f2ca90c45181753d634x1279x1552.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Progesterone</strong></p><p><strong>Progesterone</strong> stimulates <strong>lobular development </strong>of the breast and differentiation of the epithelium. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 447px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.5px;\"><strong>Hormone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 337.5px;\"><strong>Action</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.5px;\">Estrogen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 337.5px;\">Ductal development</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.5px;\">Progesterone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 337.5px;\">Lobular development and differentiation of the epithelium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.5px;\">Prolactin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 337.5px;\">Lactogenesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.5px;\">Oxytocin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 337.5px;\">Milk ejection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0340","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding lympha‍tic drainage of the breas‍t?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding lymphatic drainage of the breast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The axillary nodes receive​ approximately 85% of the‍ drainage.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The lateral group is locat​ed along the axillary vei‍n.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The anterior group drains ​most of the breast.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph nodes are divided on​ the basis of their relat‍ion to the pectoralis maj‍or.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymph nodes are divided on the basis of their relation to the pectoralis major.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f48a0e63468f4f19b4862c3f05ed3985x600x872.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymph nodes are divided on the basis of their relation to the pectoralis major.</strong></p><p><strong>Lymph nodes are divided</strong> on the basis of their <strong>relation to the pectoralis minor,</strong> not the pectoralis major.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0343","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen​ted to the OPD with a 4 x‍ 2 cm painless lump in th‍e left breast. She is pla‌nned for a mastectomy. Se​ntinel lymph node biopsy ​done showed involvement o‌f level 1 lymph nodes. Wh‌ich of the following lymp‍h nodes will not be affec‌ted in her case?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presented to the OPD with a 4 x 2 cm painless lump in the left breast. She is planned for a mastectomy. Sentinel lymph node biopsy done showed involvement of level 1 lymph nodes. Which of the following lymph nodes will not be affected in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post​erior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lat​eral","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subsc​apular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interp​ectoral","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interpectoral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbf19882592340e5b28cf2d28b4d6e23x1280x1341.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce3220eb41d44fac8eb7467a942836ddx1279x1669.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interpectoral</strong></p><p><strong>Interpectoral</strong> lymph nodes are <strong>level 2</strong> axillary lymph nodes. <strong>Level 1 </strong>axillary lymph nodes include <strong>anterior, lateral, and posterior/subscapular </strong>nodes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0371","text":"During a routine antenatal​ checkup, a pregnant woma‍n in her third trimester ‍is noticed to have access‌ory nipples. Which of the​ following does this cond​ition refer to?","raw_text":"During a routine antenatal checkup, a pregnant woman in her third trimester is noticed to have accessory nipples. Which of the following does this condition refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poly​mazia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyt​helia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Symm​astia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supernume​rary mazia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Polythelia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/461746a259574b4ba79c253f032e4f11x1224x792.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Polythelia</strong></p><p><strong>Polythelia</strong> or supernumerary nipples refer to the presence of accessory nipples. </p>\n<p><strong>Polymastia or polymazia</strong> is a congenital anomaly with the presence of accessory breast tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Symmastia</strong> is a condition characterized by webbing between the breasts, at the midline.</p>\n<p><strong>Supernumerary mazia</strong> is the presence of accessory breasts with a complete ductal system, areola, and nipple in addition to two normal breasts.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Athelia</strong> refers to the <strong>absence of nipples</strong>, and <strong>amastia</strong> refers to the congenital <strong>absence of the breast and nipple</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a third nipple below the left breast:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0374","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding access‍ory breast tissue?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding accessory breast tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with Pola​nd’s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Most frequently involves t​he axilla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It develops along the line​ of Schultz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is also known as polyma​zia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is associated with Poland’s syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/895795bb3de04426b609207479229058x1280x1874.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d44dd3918d849bebe6863edeb669b28x1080x1058.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is associated with Poland’s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Poland’s syndrome</strong> is not associated with accessory breast tissue. It is associated with <strong>amastia with the absence of the sternal portion of the pectoralis major muscle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Polymastia or polymazia</strong> is a congenital anomaly in which there is presence of accessory breast tissue.<strong> Axilla</strong> is the most frequent site for accessory breast tissue along the line of Schultz. Accessory breast tissue develops along the <strong>milk line/ line of Schultz</strong>, which appears by the<strong> 6th week</strong> of embryonic development. The milk line extends from the <strong>axilla to the inguinal region</strong>. It is <strong>ectodermal</strong> in origin.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the milk line/ line of Schultz:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows accessory breast tissue in the right axilla:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0162","text":"All of the following state​ments about amazia are tr‍ue except _____.","raw_text":"All of the following statements about amazia are true except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amazia may be associated w​ith Poland's syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amazia is the absence of t​he nipple.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amazia is often the result​ of surgical intervention‍.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amazia may be caused by ra​diation therapy.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amazia is the absence of the nipple.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amazia is the absence of the nipple.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazia</strong> is a condition wherein the <strong>breast tissue is absent</strong>, but the <strong>nipple-areolar complex is present</strong>. It results from <strong>iatrogenic causes</strong> such as radiation, excision of a misdiagnosed infantile or childhood mass that actually represented breast buds or incision and drainage of a suspected abscess.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: Amastia</strong> is the congenital absence of the breast and nipple. <strong>Athelia </strong>is the absence of the nipple.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0378","text":"Which of the following wil​l you not see in a patien‍t with Poland’s syndrome?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following will you not see in a patient with Poland’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Athelia with the absence o​f the pectoralis major","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amastia with the absence o​f pectoralis major","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormally short, webbed f​ingers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of cost​al cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Athelia with the absence of the pectoralis major","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e42a3348a0b14e469f55fa6279610d79x348x332.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Athelia with the absence of the pectoralis major</strong></p><p><strong>Athelia with absence of pectoralis major</strong> is <strong>not</strong> associated with Poland’s syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Poland’s syndrome</strong> is associated with unilateral:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Amastia with the absence of the sternal portion of the pectoralis major muscle</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abnormalities of the chest wall muscles</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loss of costal cartilage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Digital abnormalities such as brachysyndactyly, which is the abnormal shortness of the fingers or toes combined with webbing between the adjacent digits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In some cases, associated skeletal abnormalities, such as underdeveloped or absent upper ribs, underdeveloped forearm bones, and elevation of the shoulder blade (<strong>Sprengel deformity</strong>) may also be present.</p>\n<p>Note: Poland's syndrome is a constellation of anomalies with varying clinical presentations. Patients with this syndrome can present with <strong>amazia</strong> (absence of one or both of the mammary glands) or <strong>amastia</strong> (complete absence of breast tissue, nipple, and areola) in addition to any or all of the above-mentioned abnormalities.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0391","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen​ts to the clinic with pai‍n over the lateral aspect‍ of her right breast for ‌the past 5 days. Examinat​ion finding is as shown i​n the image. Which of the‌ following is the likely ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents to the clinic with pain over the lateral aspect of her right breast for the past 5 days. Examination finding is as shown in the image. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tietze’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zuska ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mondor’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stewart–Tre​ves syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mondor’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8f3ff509cbd4a74bc7c8972cf215ce7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mondor’s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Mondor's disease</strong>. The image shows the presence of a subcutaneous cord and a narrow shallow groove along the side of the cord.</p>\n<p>Mondor’s disease is the <strong>thrombophlebitis of the superficial veins of the breast</strong> and anterior chest wall. The most commonly involved vein is the <strong>lateral thoracic vein</strong>. Patients usually present with pain along the lateral chest wall and a<strong> tender, firm cord</strong> below the breast. It is a benign condition and gets <strong>resolved on its own</strong> within 4-6 weeks. <strong>Warm compresses and anti-inflammatory medicines</strong> can be used to reduce pain. If the symptoms persist despite medications, the affected segment of the vein can be excised.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tietze’s syndrome</strong> is a benign inflammation of one or more <strong>costal cartilages.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Zuska disease</strong> is the subcutaneous abscess of the breast tissue beneath the areola of the nipple. It is also called a <strong>subareolar abscess</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Stewart–Treves Syndrome</strong> is characterized by <strong>lymphangiosarcoma</strong> in chronic long-standing lymphedema following <strong>mastectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0394","text":"Which of the following is ​the commonest cause of pa‍thological nipple dischar‍ge in a non-lactating wom‌an?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the commonest cause of pathological nipple discharge in a non-lactating woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duct e​ctasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intraducta​l papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paget’s dise​ase of breast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Galact​orrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duct ectasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duct ectasia</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause of nipple discharge</strong> in a <strong>non-lactating woman</strong><strong> </strong>is <strong>duct ectasia. </strong>The discharge is usually <strong>black or greenish</strong> in color.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Intraductal papilloma</strong> is the <strong>most common cause of bloody nipple discharge. </strong>It is a true polyp of the epithelium-lined ducts of the breast. Contrary to what one might expect, they are not associated with an increased risk of breast cancer.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Paget's disease</strong> of the breast can present with discharge from the surface of the nipple.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Galactorrhea</strong> is the milk production from breasts unrelated to pregnancy or lactation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0402","text":"A chronic smoker presented​ with complaints of inter‍mittent discharge from bi‍lateral nipples for the p‌ast few months. On compre​ssion of her breast durin​g the examination, a disc‌harge was observed as sho‌wn below. Which of the fo‍llowing is the most likel‌y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A chronic smoker presented with complaints of intermittent discharge from bilateral nipples for the past few months. On compression of her breast during the examination, a discharge was observed as shown below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duct e​ctasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intraducta​l papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrocyst​ic disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nipple i​nfection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duct ectasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f68749c86c9b4952860239be1a038c42.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duct ectasia</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis of a patient with <strong>greenish discharge </strong>from<strong> multiple ducts</strong> is <strong>duct ectasia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Duct ectasia</strong> is the dilatation of breast ducts. It is the <strong>most common </strong>cause of<strong> nipple discharge in non-lactating women</strong>. This condition is associated with <strong>smoking</strong> and <strong>diabetes</strong>. The initial stage of this disease consists of dilatation of one or more lactiferous ducts. These ducts get filled with stagnant brown or <strong>green-colored</strong> secretions. In the later stages, the accumulation of fluid in the lactiferous ducts leads to infection and inflammation, causing<strong> periductal mastitis.</strong> Patients may present with <strong>nipple discharge</strong>, abscess, fistula, or nipple retraction.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial stages - oral antibiotics, needle aspiration, or incision and drainage of the abscess</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrent</strong> episodes - <strong>Hadfield operation </strong>(cone excision of multiple ducts)</li>\n<li><strong>A single duct</strong> involvement - <strong>microdochectomy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0406","text":"What is the best method of​ management in a patient ‍with recurrent episodes o‍f multiple duct ectasia?","raw_text":"What is the best method of management in a patient with recurrent episodes of multiple duct ectasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microdo​chectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hadfield’s​ operation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wide excision involving th​e affected ducts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Simple m​astectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hadfield’s operation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hadfield’s operation</strong></p><p>The best method of management in a patient with <strong>multiple duct ectasia</strong> is to perform <strong>Hadfield's operation</strong>. This operation consists of the excision of <strong>all</strong> the major lactiferous ducts.</p>\n<p>The initial stages of duct ectasia can be managed by oral antibiotics, needle aspiration, or incision and drainage of the abscess. <strong>Recurrent</strong> episodes of infection are best managed by Hadfield operation. If a <strong>single duct</strong> is involved, then <strong>microdochectomy</strong> is performed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0400","text":"Amongst the following, the​ most common cause of blo‍od-stained nipple dischar‍ge from a single duct is:‌","raw_text":"Amongst the following, the most common cause of blood-stained nipple discharge from a single duct is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraducta​l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duct e​ctasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraducta​l papilloma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrocyst​ic disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intraductal papilloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intraductal papilloma</strong></p><p><strong>Ductal papilloma</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>blood-stained nipple discharge</strong> from a <strong>single duct</strong>.</p>\n<p>Solitary intraductal papillomas are true polyps of the ducts which are usually small (&lt;1 cm) but may grow to 4-5 cm. They are often associated with bloody nipple discharge. They do <strong>not have malignant potential.</strong> Treatment is by <strong>excision.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The most common cause of blood-stained discharge from <strong>multiple ducts</strong> is <strong>carcinoma.  </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4522","text":"A probe is inserted into t​he duct from which the ni‍pple discharge is emergin‍g. A tennis racquet incis‌ion is made and the flap ​is dissected to reach the​ duct. The duct is then e‌xcised. This procedure is‌ known as?","raw_text":"A probe is inserted into the duct from which the nipple discharge is emerging. A tennis racquet incision is made and the flap is dissected to reach the duct. The duct is then excised.  This  procedure is known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macrodo​chectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microdo​chectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cone e​xcision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wide excision of the lacti​ferous duct.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Microdochectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microdochectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Microdochectomy</strong> is the excision of the <strong>entire lactiferous duct </strong>(single duct disease). It is done for <strong>duct papilloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Microdochectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In a duct papilloma where no palpable mass is present, the duct is expressed to observe the discharge.</li>\n<li>A <strong>fine probe or stiff nylon suture </strong>is gently inserted into the duct, and the probe is secured to the nipple skin using silk sutures to prevent displacement during the procedure.</li>\n<li>A <strong>tennis racquet shaped incision </strong>is made to elevate the skin flaps, and the duct with the probe is excised along with the skin flap for 5 cm until the papilloma is identified (The papilloma will always be located within 5 cm from the duct orifice).</li>\n<li>The wound is closed without a drain, and a compression bandage is applied.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is ideal to <strong>rule out DCIS</strong> by <strong>frozen section biopsy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cone excision</strong> and <strong>wide excision </strong>are done under major duct excision (<strong>Adair-Haldfield operation</strong>). It is done for duct ectasias with multiple duct involvement and multiple papillomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4101","text":"Which of the following abe​rration of normal develop‍ment and involution of br‍east tissue is present in‌ patients with fibroadeno​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following aberration of normal development and involution of breast tissue is present in patients with fibroadenoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stromal d​evelopment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lobular i​nvolution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lobular d​evelopment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Duct In​volution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lobular development","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lobular development</strong></p><p>In <strong>fibroadenoma</strong>, <strong>aberration</strong> of <strong>normal</strong> <strong>lobular</strong> <strong>development</strong> is present. </p>\n<p>It arises from hyperplasia of a single lobule and presents as a well-circumscribed, smooth, firm, non-tender, solitary, freely mobile mass (<strong>mouse in the breast</strong>). It is a <strong>biphasic tumor</strong> consisting of fibroblastic stroma and epithelium-lined gland. </p>\n<p><strong>ANDI</strong> <strong>Classification</strong> of <strong>benign</strong> <strong>breast disorders:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Disorders</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early reproductive years (15-25 yrs)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lobular development</td>\n<td>Fibroadenoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stromal development</td>\n<td>Adolescent hypertrophy (Gigantomastia)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nipple eversion</td>\n<td>Nipple inversion (subareolar abscess, Mammary duct fistula)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Later reproductive years (25-40 yrs)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cyclical changes in menstruation</td>\n<td>Cyclical mastalgia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Involution (35-55 yrs)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lobular involution</td>\n<td>Macrocysts, sclerosing lesions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duct involution</td>\n<td>Dilation (duct ectasia)Sclerosis (nipple retraction)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Epithelial turnover</td>\n<td>Epithelial hyperplasia (with atypia)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0414","text":"A 19-year-old girl present​s with a lump in her righ‍t breast for the past 6 m‍onths. On examination, th‌e lump is around 1 x1 cm ​in size, firm, and freely​ movable on the breast ti‌ssue. Ultrasound shows th‌e presence of a circumscr‍ibed oval lobulated mass.‌ Which of the following i​s not true regarding the ‌given condition?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old girl presents with a lump in her right breast for the past 6 months. On examination, the lump is around 1 x1 cm in size, firm, and freely movable on the breast tissue. Ultrasound shows the presence of a circumscribed oval lobulated mass. Which of the following is not true regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peak incidence is in secon​d decade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has a biphasic morpholo​gy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mammography may show egg-s​hell calcification","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a fo​rm of ANDI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mammography may show egg-shell calcification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/469cfc681ef747aca22d2b105114b7d1x630x520.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mammography may show egg-shell calcification</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>fibroadenoma</strong>. Mammography may show the presence of <strong>popcorn calcification</strong>.<strong> Eggshell calcification</strong> can be seen in <strong>traumatic fat necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Fibroadenoma</strong> is a form of aberrations of normal development and involution of the breast (<strong>ANDI</strong>). The most common age group affected is <strong>15-25 years </strong>(early reproductive period)<strong> </strong>with a peak incidence in the second decade. It arises from hyperplasia of a single lobule and presents as a well-circumscribed, smooth, firm, non-tender, solitary, freely mobile mass (<strong>mouse in the breast</strong>). It is a <strong>biphasic tumor</strong> consisting of fibroblastic stroma and epithelium-lined gland. Excision is not advised unless there is suspicious histology on FNAC.</p>\n<p><strong>Giant fibroadenoma</strong> is more than <strong>5cm</strong> in size and is common in adolescent girls. It requires excision by submammary incision.</p>\n<p>The image below shows popcorn calcification on mammography:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0160","text":"A giant fibroadenoma has a​ diameter greater than __‍___","raw_text":"A giant fibroadenoma has a diameter greater than _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 ​cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10​ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15​ mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20​ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5 cm</strong></p><p><strong>Giant fibroadenomas</strong> are <strong>over 5 cm in diameter</strong> and occasionally occur during puberty. They are often rapidly growing but are similar to smaller fibroadenomas and are enucleated through a submammary incision.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0419","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen​ted with a lump in the le‍ft breast as shown in the‍ image below. Biopsy done‌ revealed increased strom​al cellularity with typic​al leaflike architecture.‌ Which of the following i‌s false about this condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with a lump in the left breast as shown in the image below. Biopsy done revealed increased stromal cellularity with typical leaflike architecture. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Usually​ benign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastasizes to axillary l​ymph nodes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment is wide excision​ with margin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Originates from hormonally​ responsive stromal cells‍ of the breast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metastasizes to axillary lymph nodes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7f892e51c41d47908a071a4226cce8c2.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dc274b5b3be4a8ea3ae77ee9d037798x720x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metastasizes to axillary lymph nodes</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathology report point to <strong>malignant cystosarcoma phyllodes</strong>, which metastasize via the <strong>bloodstream</strong>. Metastasis to <strong>lymph nodes is rare</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Phyllodes tumor</strong> also known as a sero-cystic disease of Brodie or cystosarcoma phyllodes, originates from<strong> hormonally responsive stromal cells </strong>of the breast. It is usually a <strong>benign</strong> condition and affects women <strong>&gt;40 years</strong> of age, but can be seen in younger women. Sometimes, may develop malignant transformation.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Large</strong>, sometimes massive, with an <strong>unevenly bosselated surface</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Ulceration</strong> of overlying skin occurs because of pressure necrosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Remains mobile on the chest wall</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There is no role of FNAC in phyllodes tumor. Histopathology of the lump biopsy shows <strong>increased stromal cellularity</strong>, overgrowth, and <strong>typical leaflike architecture</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Tumors are treated by <strong>excision with margins of at least 2 cm</strong> to prevent local recurrence.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 582\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Large or malignant phyllodes</strong> tumors may require a <strong>mastectomy.</strong> The <strong>axillary dissection</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong> because axillary lymph nodes rarely get involved.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note: Malignant phyllodes tumors contain liposarcomatous or rhabdomyosarcomatous elements rather than fibrosarcomatous elements.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9698","text":"A breastfeeding mother pre​sented to the clinic with‍ fever and breast pain fo‍r the past 2 days. The af‌fected breast appears as ​shown in the image below.​ There is extreme tendern‌ess on palpation. Which o‌f the following is the mo‍st common source for the ‌organism causing this con​dition?","raw_text":"A breastfeeding mother presented to the clinic with fever and breast pain for the past 2 days. The affected breast appears as shown in the image below. There is extreme tenderness on palpation. Which of the following is the most common source for the organism causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal ​skin flora","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breas​t pump","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infant’s n​asopharynx","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infant's ​skin flora","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Infant’s nasopharynx","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9391c4e69e3d4c6cb5c260a33a19c808.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infant’s nasopharynx</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>lactational mastitis</strong>. The most common source of infection is from the <strong>infant's nasopharynx</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lactational mastitis</strong> is most commonly caused by <strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong> present in the infant's nasopharynx. The organism enters the breast tissue through <strong>cracked nipples</strong> and multiplies in the <strong>stagnant breast milk</strong>. Patients present with fever with a firm, red, tender, and swollen quadrant of the unilateral breast.</p>\n<p>Painful breasts must be managed by the use of <strong>analgesics</strong>. Breastfeeding should be continued if the pain is less severe. <strong>Antibiotics</strong> like flucloxacillin, co-amoxiclav are used to treat the infection. If the patient develops an abscess, then the <strong>abscess has to be drained by aspiration or incision</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 1032px; height: 231px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.875px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 372.046875px;\">Bacterial mastitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 547.09375px;\">Breast abscess</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.875px;\">Condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 372.046875px;\">Localized inflammation due to ascending infection or infection due to milk stasis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 547.09375px;\">Occurs as a complication of mastitis, with pus formation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.875px;\">Symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 372.046875px;\">Dull pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 547.09375px;\">Throbbing pain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.875px;\">Signs (if any)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 372.046875px;\">Tenderness, erythema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 547.09375px;\">Point tenderness, erythema, induration of skin (if chronic) lymphadenopathy, <strong>fluctuation test +ve</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.875px;\">Management</td>\n<td style=\"width: 372.046875px;\">Analgesics, antibiotics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 547.09375px;\">Analgesics, aspiration of pus, antibiotics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0161","text":"A female patient presents ​with a breast abscess. An‍ aspiration was attempted‍ under antibiotic cover w‌hich failed to reveal any​ aspirate. What is the ne​xt step in management?","raw_text":"A female patient presents with a breast abscess. An aspiration was attempted under antibiotic cover which failed to reveal any aspirate. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incision a​nd Drainage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue ant​ibiotics only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A second attempt at aspira​tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stop antibiotics as no asp​irate was collected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A second attempt at aspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b81b217ed5f4f19b43533f8c99f22bdx460x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A second attempt at aspiration</strong></p><p>In a patient with a <strong>breast abscess</strong>, the next step after a failed aspiration is to <strong>attempt aspiration once</strong> <strong>again</strong> under antibiotic cover.</p>\n<p>Incision and drainage is not preferred, as it can potentially <strong>interfere with breastfeeding</strong>, and delicate structures such as ducts, lobules, and tubules may be injured, possibly leading to a <strong>non-healing milk fistula</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>breast</strong> <strong>abscess</strong> is a complication of <strong>acute mastitis</strong>, which is more common in new mothers at the beginning of lactation<strong> (lactational mastitis)</strong>. <strong>Non-lactational mastitis</strong> is more common in diabetic, <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> people and people predisposed by duct ectasia and periareolar infections.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of a breast abscess include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Fever</strong> and malaise</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Localized pain and tenderness </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Redness</strong> and <strong>swelling</strong> of the affected breast</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Positive</strong> <strong>fluctuation</strong> test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>An <strong>ultrasonogram</strong> of the affected breast is performed to <strong>confirm</strong> the abscess, and treatment proceeds as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Analgesics</strong>, <strong>antipyretics</strong>, and breast support for pain and fever relief.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> is performed under <strong>antibiotic cover</strong>, and the pus is cultured. If aspiration proves <strong>unsuccessful</strong>, it should be <strong>reattempted</strong> after a few days.</li>\n<li><strong>Incision and drainage </strong>should be considered in cases of:\n<ul>\n<li>Mastitis unresponsive to antibiotics for 48 hours.</li>\n<li>Brawny induration.</li>\n<li>Progressive mastitis with persistent fever</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 59.844%; height: 278px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10.0092%;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.8228%;\"><strong>Bacterial mastitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 53.168%;\"><strong>Breast abscess</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10.0092%;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.8228%;\">Localized inflammation due to ascending infection or infection due to milk stasis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 53.168%;\">\n<p>Occurs as a complication of mastitis</p>\n<p>Pus formation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10.0092%;\"><strong>Symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.8228%;\"><strong>Dull</strong> pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53.168%;\"><strong>Throbbing</strong> pain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10.0092%;\"><strong>Signs (if any)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.8228%;\">\n<p>Tenderness</p>\n<p>Erythema</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53.168%;\">\n<p>Point tenderness</p>\n<p>Erythema</p>\n<p>Induration of skin(if chronic)</p>\n<p><strong>Lymphadenopathy</strong></p>\n<p>Fluctuation test +ve</p>\n<p><strong>Pus point</strong> may be present</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10.0092%;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.8228%;\">\n<p>Analgesics</p>\n<p>Antibiotics (<strong>Dicloxacillin</strong> 500 mg every 6 hours orally; Erythromycin if allergic to penicillin)</p>\n<p>Adequate breast support</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53.168%;\">\n<p>Analgesics</p>\n<p>Adequate breast support</p>\n<p>Aspiration of Pus under antibiotic cover; repeated aspirations if required</p>\n<p>Incision &amp; drainage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0163","text":"A 37- year-old woman prese​nted to the clinic with a‍ breast lump. Clinical ex‍amination and ultrasound ‌revealed the presence of ​a single large cyst in he​r right breast. On aspira‌ting the cyst, you find b‌lood-stained fluid. What ‍will be the next best ste‌p in the management of th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 37- year-old woman presented to the clinic with a breast lump. Clinical examination and ultrasound revealed the presence of a single large cyst in her right breast. On aspirating the cyst, you find blood-stained fluid. What will be the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wait for refilling and rea​ttempt aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excision o​f the cyst","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Analgesia an​d antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform mammogram to rule ​out carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Excision of the cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Excision of the cyst</strong></p><p>The next step in management is the <strong>excision of the cyst</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of breast cyst:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the aspirate is <strong>clear </strong>(non-blood stained) or if the cyst resolves completely then <strong>no further treatment</strong> is required.</li>\n<li>If the aspirate is <strong>blood-stained</strong> or if the cyst recurs, then a core biopsy or <strong>local excision should</strong> be performed. The resulting tissue should be sent for histopathology for the exclusion of cystadenocarcinoma, especially in elderly women.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0421","text":"Physiological gynecomastia​ is not seen in which of ‍the following?","raw_text":"Physiological gynecomastia is not seen in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neon​ates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adole​scents","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eld​erly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obese in​dividuals","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Obese individuals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Obese individuals</strong></p><p>Physiological gynecomastia is <strong>not</strong> seen in obese males. In obese men, <strong>increased fat deposition</strong> over the breasts is referred to as <strong>pseudo-gynecomastia</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Gynecomastia</strong> is defined as <strong>≥</strong> <strong>2 cm</strong> subareolar <strong>breast tissue.</strong> It can be physiological or pathological.</p>\n<p><strong>Physiologic gynecomastia</strong> occurs during three phases of life:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neonatal period</li>\n<li>Adolescence</li>\n<li>Senescence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common to each of these phases is an <strong>excess of circulating estrogens </strong>in relation to circulating testosterone. <strong>Neonatal gynecomastia</strong> is caused by the action of <strong>placental estrogens</strong> on neonatal breast tissues. In <strong>adolescence</strong>, there is an <strong>excess of estradiol </strong>relative to testosterone. In <strong>senescence</strong>, the <strong>circulating testosterone level falls</strong>, resulting in relative hyperestrogenism. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3936","text":"A 32-year-old woman has be​en experiencing breast pa‍in for the past 6 months.‍ The pain is present just‌ before her menstrual per​iod. Breast examination a​nd mammography are normal‌. All of the following me‌dications/supplements can‍ help in reducing the pai‌n except:","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman has been experiencing breast pain for the past 6 months. The pain is present just before her menstrual period. Breast examination and mammography are normal. All of the following medications/supplements can help in reducing the pain except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dan​azol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Evening pr​imrose oil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caff​eine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tamo​xifen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caffeine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caffeine</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cyclical mastalgia</strong>. <strong>Caffeine</strong> is<strong> not</strong> useful in reducing pain. On the contrary, caffeine has to be avoided to reduce pain.</p>\n<p>Medications that can be used to treat mastalgia are <strong>evening primrose oil, danazol,</strong> and<strong> tamoxifen.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4003","text":"A breastfeeding mother pre​sented to the clinic with‍ a gradually progressive ‍painless breast lump. On ‌examination, a well-circu​mscribed and freely mobil​e mass was palpable. USG ‌showed a solitary, well-d‌efined, anechoic lesion w‍ith thin, echogenic walls‌. Aspiration revealed a w​hite fluid. What is the b‌est treatment option for ​this condition?","raw_text":"A breastfeeding mother presented to the clinic with a gradually progressive painless breast lump. On examination, a well-circumscribed and freely mobile mass was palpable. USG showed a solitary, well-defined, anechoic lesion with thin, echogenic walls. Aspiration revealed a white fluid. What is the best treatment option for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excision​al Biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG guided fine-needle asp​iration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incision a​nd drainage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trial of antibiotic therap​y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. USG guided fine-needle aspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. USG guided fine-needle aspiration</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>galactocele</strong>. The <strong>best treatment</strong> option for this condition is <strong>USG guided fine-needle aspiration. </strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>galactocele</strong> is a <strong>milk-filled cavity</strong> in the breast of a woman who is usually lactating. It can be caused due to lactation problems, prolactin stimulation by drugs, or duct obstruction. </p>\n<p>It presents as a palpable, round, well-circumscribed, and freely mobile mass with no fever, pain, or inflammation. It is usually <strong>subareolar</strong> and unilateral.</p>\n<p><strong>USG guided fine-needle aspiration</strong> is both <strong>diagnostic</strong> and <strong>therapeutic.</strong> It will show thick milky white secretion confirming the diagnosis. Aspiration resolves galactocele in most cases. Surgery is done for infected cysts or recurrent cases.</p>\n<p>Antibiotic therapy is not needed if the galactocele is not infected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5496","text":"A 30-year-old female footb​aller presented with comp‍laints of a lump in the b‍reast. On examination, a ‌hard, irregular lump was ​palpated. Mammography rev​ealed calcifications as s‌hown below. Which of the ‌following conditions is s‍he most likely to have?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female footballer presented with complaints of a lump in the breast. On examination, a hard, irregular lump was palpated. Mammography revealed calcifications as shown below. Which of the following conditions is she most likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat ne​crosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carc​inoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papi​lloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blue-do​me cysts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fat necrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/25c2a3d216e6478691533dfee43eb55b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fat necrosis</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>palpable mass</strong> and<strong> egg-shell calcifications </strong>on mammography in a young woman are suggestive of fat necrosis. It <strong>mimics breast carcinoma</strong> clinically and mammographically but has <strong>no malignant potential</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"avMH4_so","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"8_anesthesia_workstation","title":"8.Anesthesia Workstation","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MA4171","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the high pr‍essure section of an anes​thetic workstation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the high pressure section of an anesthetic workstation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flow​meter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen ​cylinder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yoke a​ssembly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cylinder pr​essure gauge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flowmeter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b89f503637f4465bb0ae79a04fcda15fx1280x1351.311706629055.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flowmeter</strong></p><p><strong>Flowmeter</strong> is not a part of the high-pressure system. It is a part of the <strong>low-pressure system.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4125","text":"Which of the following is ​the most commonly used ca‍rbon dioxide absorbent in​ a closed circuit?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly used carbon dioxide absorbent in a closed circuit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ams​orb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Soda​sorb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Soda​ lime","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bara​lyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Soda lime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Soda lime</strong></p><p><strong>Soda lime</strong> is the most commonly used carbon dioxide absorbent in circle breathing systems.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4083","text":"How much carbon dioxide ca​n be absorbed by 100 gm o‍f soda lime?","raw_text":"How much carbon dioxide can be absorbed by 100 gm of soda lime?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3​L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"13​ L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"23​ L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"33​ L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 23 L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 23 L</strong></p><p><strong>100 gm </strong>of<strong> Soda lime</strong> absorbs<strong> 23 L </strong>of <strong>carbon dioxide.</strong></p>\n<p>Soda lime is the <strong>most commonly</strong> used carbon dioxide absorbent in circle breathing systems.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4129","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‍ sodalime? 100 g of sodal​ime can absorb 15 liters ‌of CO2 The end product of​ the reaction of sodalime‍ and CO2 is Na2CO3 + Ca(O‌H)2 The major constituent​ of sodalime is Ca(OH)2 W‍ater content of sodalime ‌is 5% KOH is one of the c​omponents of sodalime","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements is true regarding sodalime?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">100 g of sodalime can absorb 15 liters of CO2</p>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">The end product of the reaction of sodalime and CO2 is Na2CO3 + Ca(OH)2</p>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">The major constituent of sodalime is Ca(OH)2</p>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Water content of sodalime is 5%</p>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">KOH is one of the components of sodalime</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 and 5</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Statement 1: 100 g of sodalime can absorb <strong>23</strong> liters of CO2</p>\n<p>Statement 2: The end product of the reaction of soda lime with CO<sub>2</sub> is <strong>CaCO3+NaOH. </strong>Reaction end products include heat (the heat of neutralization), water, calcium carbonate, and sodium hydroxide.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 52\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Its reactions are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub></li>\n<li>H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2NaOH → Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O + Heat (a fast reaction)</li>\n<li>Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub> → CaCO<sub>3</sub> + 2NaOH + Heat (a slow reaction)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note that the water and sodium hydroxide initially required are regenerated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Statement 3: The <strong>major</strong> constituent of soda lime is <strong>Ca(OH)2</strong> - 80%</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Statement 4: Water content of sodalime is <strong>15</strong>%</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Statement 5: KOH is one of the components of sodalime - 2%</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4151","text":"An anaesthetist notices th​at the soda lime is exhau‍sted and requests for it ​to be changed. If the ind‌icator used is ethyl viol​et, what would be the col‍or change observed?","raw_text":"An anaesthetist notices that the soda lime is exhausted and requests for it to be changed. If the indicator used is ethyl violet, what would be the color change observed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pink t​o white","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Purple ​to white","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"White ​to Pink","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"White t​o Purple","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. White to Purple","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. White to Purple</strong></p><p>When carbon dioxide absorbing capacity of soda lime using <strong>ethyl violet</strong> indicator is exhausted, the color changes from <strong>white to purple.</strong></p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Soda lime indicator dye</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\"> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indicator</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Color when Fresh</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Color when Exhausted</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethyl violet</td>\n<td>White</td>\n<td>Purple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phenolphthalein</td>\n<td>Colorless</td>\n<td>Pink</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clayton yellow</td>\n<td>Red</td>\n<td>Yellow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethyl orange</td>\n<td>Orange</td>\n<td>Yellow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mimosa 2</td>\n<td>Red</td>\n<td>White</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4140","text":"What is the action of sili​ca when added to soda lim‍e?","raw_text":"What is the action of silica when added to soda lime?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Humidify​ing agent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hardeni​ng agent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cata​lyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indi​cator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hardening agent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hardening agent</strong></p><p>Silica in the soda lime acts as a<strong> hardening agent.</strong></p>\n<p>In Amsorb, polyvinylpyrrolidone is used as a hardening agent.</p>\n<p><strong>NaOH and KOH</strong> serve as <strong>catalysts </strong>or activators to speed up the reaction between calcium hydroxide and carbon dioxide.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethyl violet</strong> is the most commonly used <strong>indicator</strong> dye in soda lime.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4153","text":"What is the ideal size of ​the carbon dioxide absorb‍ent granules?","raw_text":"What is the ideal size of the carbon dioxide absorbent granules?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 m​esh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 m​esh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 m​esh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 m​esh","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 mesh","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 mesh</strong></p><p>The ideal granule size of the carbon dioxide absorbent is between <strong>4 and 8 mesh </strong>(1.5-5 mm).</p>\n<p>Smaller granule size favors greater area for absorption of carbon dioxide. But, it offers greater airflow resistance (caking).</p>\n<p>Converse is true with larger granule size - the absorptive area is less but airflow resistance is minimal (channeling).</p>\n<p>So, the ideal size is to be between 4 and 8 mesh as absorptive surface area and resistance to flow are optimized.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4154","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true concernin‍g Compound A?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true concerning Compound A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degradation product of des​flurane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurotoxicity is the major​ concern","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less common with Amsorb ab​sorbent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Seen predominantly with hi​gh flow techniques","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Less common with Amsorb absorbent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Less common with Amsorb absorbent</strong></p><p>The true statement concerning <strong>Compound A</strong> is that its formation is <strong>less common with Amsorb</strong> absorbent.</p>\n<p><strong>Compound A</strong> is fluoro-methyl-2,2-difluoro-1-(trifluoromethyl) vinyl ether which is the degradation product of <strong>Sevoflurane,</strong> not desflurane.</p>\n<p>The strong bases like NaOH and KOH present in absorbents (soda lime) lead to its formation. Lack of strong alkali in Amsorb makes its formation less common.</p>\n<p>Compound A is <strong>nephrotoxic</strong>, not neurotoxic.</p>\n<p>It is seen predominantly with <strong>low flow</strong> anesthesia techniques and <strong>high absorbent temperatures.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4134","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding baralym‍e is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding baralyme is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More efficient than soda l​ime","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Performs better in dry cli​mate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less caustic than soda lim​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greater tendency to produc​e carbon monoxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. More efficient than soda lime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. More efficient than soda lime</strong></p><p>The true statement is <strong>baralyme is less efficient than soda-lime.</strong></p>\n<p>Baralyme is less efficient than soda-lime as its absorptive capacity is 9-18L of CO<sub>2</sub>/100 g while of soda lime is 23L of CO<sub>2</sub>/100 g.</p>\n<p>Baralyme with the composition of calcium hydroxide and barium hydroxide (activator) is <strong>less caustic</strong> than soda lime.</p>\n<p>It gives more reliable performance in a <strong>dry</strong> environment.</p>\n<p>As compared to soda lime, baralyme has a greater tendency to produce <strong>carbon monoxide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4162","text":"An ICU patient who is depe​ndant on oxygen support r‍equires an MRI. What mate​rial is used to build the‌ medical gas cylinder whi​ch can be used in this sc‍enario?","raw_text":"An ICU patient who is dependant on oxygen support requires an MRI. What material is used to build the medical gas cylinder which can be used in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stainle​ss steel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Molybden​um steel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alum​inium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hard p​lastic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aluminium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aluminium</strong></p><p><strong>MRI compatible</strong> medical gas cylinders are made of <strong>aluminium, </strong>while<strong> </strong>most medical gas cylinders are constructed of molybdenum steel.</p>\n<p>The cylinders are produced in various sizes designated by a capital letter code.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Size A is the smallest.</li>\n<li>Size H is the largest.</li>\n<li><strong>Size E</strong> is the cylinder <strong>most commonly used</strong> in anesthesia machines, for patient transport, and resuscitation, followed by Size D. </li>\n<li>Size J is commonly used for cylinder manifold systems for central supply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4157","text":"In order to identify an ox​ygen cylinder correctly, ‍what colour would you exp​ect the shoulder of the c‌ylinder to be?","raw_text":"In order to identify an oxygen cylinder correctly, what colour would you expect the shoulder of the cylinder to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wh​ite","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bl​ack","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bl​ue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yel​low","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. White","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. White</strong></p><p>Colour of the <strong>shoulder</strong> of oxygen cylinder in India is <strong>white,</strong> and the <strong>body</strong> is <strong>black</strong>. </p>\n<p>Maximum pressure in the oxygen cylinder is around <strong>2000 </strong>psig<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4159","text":"What gas is the following ​cylinder used to carry?","raw_text":"What gas is the following cylinder used to carry?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ent​onox","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A​ir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxy​gen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitrous oxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c3105d9197c64ffb9e4015eec68ea181.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>Blue cylinders are used to store <strong>nitrous oxide.</strong></p>\n<p>Maximum pressure in the nitrous oxide cylinder is around <strong>745 </strong>psig<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Critical temperature of nitrous oxide is<strong> 36.5<sup>o</sup>C, </strong>hence it is present in <strong>liquid</strong> form at room temperature.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4172","text":"During a routine evaluatio​n of the operating room b‍efore an operation, you h​ear an alarm from the ane‌sthesia workstation. Assu​ming it to be due to a le‍ak from the oxygen cylind‌er, you immediately check​ the pressure gauge. What‍ would be the maximum pre‌ssure that is normally ex​pected in this medical ga‍s cylinder?","raw_text":"During a routine evaluation of the operating room before an operation, you hear an alarm from the anesthesia workstation. Assuming it to be due to a leak from the oxygen cylinder, you immediately check the pressure gauge. What would be the maximum pressure that is normally expected in this medical gas cylinder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"750 ​psig","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1500​ psig","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2000​ psig","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2500​ psig","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2000 psig","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2000 psig</strong></p><p>Maximum pressure in oxygen cylinder is <strong>2000 psig </strong>(pounds per square inch gauge)</p>\n<p><strong>Maximum pressure in the E-cylinders:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>750 psig </strong>for<strong> nitrous oxide </strong>(<strong>liquid</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>2000 psig </strong>for<strong> air </strong>(gaseous)</li>\n<li><strong>2000 psig </strong>for<strong> oxygen </strong>(gaseous)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each cylinder is passed through a high-pressure regulator, which reduces the high pressures inside the cylinder to a lower, nearly constant pressure of 45 psig, which is suitable for use in the anesthesia machine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4199","text":"Nitrous oxide cylinder at ​100%, 50%, and 25% volume‍ respectively would show ​the pressure (in PSI) of","raw_text":"Nitrous oxide cylinder at 100%, 50%, and 25% volume respectively would show the pressure (in PSI) of","options":[{"label":"A","text":"750, 7​50, 750","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2200, 22​00, 2200","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"750, 37​5, 162.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2200, 1​100, 550","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 750, 750, 750","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61594fa542d74771a7e9bb4cab970c63x1280x1719.605077574048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd8dde346caf47b588e6cdb1eee9e440x1280x1719.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 750, 750, 750</strong></p><p>Nitrous oxide cylinder at 100%, 50%, and 25% volume would show 750, 750, 750 PSI (pounds per square inch) respectively.</p>\n<p>This is because N2O (and CO2) are stored in the cylinder as a <strong>liquid</strong> under the pressure of <strong>750</strong> <strong>psi</strong> at room temperature. Hence, N2O gauge will read full as long as there is liquid in the tank. After which the pressure falls until the cylinder is exhausted.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>N2O pressure gauge reading at full, half, one-fourth, and empty cylinder.</strong></p>\n<p>The contents of N2O cylinders can be accurately measured by <strong>weighing the cylinders</strong> rather than by pressure gauge.</p>\n<p>To measure the content, the weight of an empty cylinder, known as <strong>Tare weight</strong> is subtracted from the actual weight of the cylinder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>O<sub>2</sub> pressure gauge reading at full, half, one-fourth and empty cylinder.</strong></p>\n<p>In a cylinder containing a non-liquefied gas such as Oxygen, the pressure in the cylinder decreases as the contents are used. Therefore the <strong>pressure gauge</strong> can be used to measure the cylinder content.</p>\n<p>Oxygen pressure gauge would read 2200, 1100, and 550 PSI at 100%, 50%, and 25% content respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4185","text":"Identify the gas cylinder ​based on the pin index sh‍own.","raw_text":"Identify the gas cylinder based on the pin index shown.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxy​gen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A​ir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ent​onox","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nitrous oxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/924a24439b30415cb2d5bb0aaeaf184e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>The given image shows the Pin index system (3,5) of <strong>Nitrous oxide</strong>. </p>\n<p>Pin index system (PISS) is a safety measure to prevent <strong>incorrect cylinder attachment</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each gas cylinder has<strong> two holes (except entonox)</strong> in its <strong>cylinder valve</strong> that fits the corresponding pins in the yoke of the anesthesia machine. The relative positioning of the pins and holes is unique for each gas which constitutes the pin index system.</li>\n<li>The PISS should only be considered as <strong>partial protection</strong> because pin index failures have been reported due to bent or broken pins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4189","text":"An overworked OT technicia​n brings an oxygen gas cy‍linder instead of an air ​cylinder to the theater. ‌However, he realizes his ​mistake when he is unable‍ to attach it to the hang‌er-yoke assembly because ​of the Pin index safety s‍ystem. What would be the ‌Pin index of the cylinder​ he is holding?","raw_text":"An overworked OT technician brings an oxygen gas cylinder instead of an air cylinder to the theater. However, he realizes his mistake when he is unable to attach it to the hanger-yoke assembly because of the Pin index safety system. What would be the Pin index of the cylinder he is holding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,​ 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,​ 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,​ 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c533a5d5eee649f0a15c481db2f2b004.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Pin index system for oxygen gas is 2,5.</strong></p>\n<p>Pin index system (PISS) is a safety measure to prevent incorrect cylinder attachment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4174","text":"What is the normal hospita​l pipeline supply of air?‍","raw_text":"What is the normal hospital pipeline supply of air?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60 ​psig","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"100 ​psig","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"600 ​psig","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2000​ psig","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 60 psig","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 60 psig</strong></p><p>The normal hospital <strong>pipeline</strong> supply pressure of all gases in India is <strong>60</strong> psig.</p>\n<p>The hospital pipeline supply is the primary source of all gases. 2000 psig of oxygen cylinder, which is a part of the high pressure system, is regulated to 45 psig after it enters the anaesthesia workstation. </p>\n<p>The regulated cylinder pressure is 45 psig which is less than that of pipeline pressure. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4161","text":"Which of the following is ​prevented by the Diameter‍ Index safety system?","raw_text":"Which of the following is prevented by the Diameter Index safety system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contamination of the anest​hetic environment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Delivery of ​hypoxic gases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incorrect cylinder attachm​ent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incorrect pipeline attachm​ent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incorrect pipeline attachment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0d65c4f15264135884407f1f0571accx1280x1076.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incorrect pipeline attachment</strong></p><p>The Diameter Index Safety System (<strong>DISS</strong>) prevents <strong>incorrect pipeline attachment.</strong></p>\n<p>DISS is a safety feature in the modern anesthesia workstation. DISS connectors have unique diameters for each type of gas to prevent wrong pipeline attachment to the wrong inlet.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Diameter Index Safety system</strong></p>\n<p>Incorrect cylinder attachment(not pipeline) is prevented by <strong>Pin Index Safety System.</strong></p>\n<p>Contamination of the anesthetic environment by waste gases is prevented by <strong>Scavenging system.</strong></p>\n<p>Delivery of hypoxic gases is prevented by <strong>Oxygen failure safety device</strong> (shut-off or proportionating device).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6698","text":"The blue pipeline in the o​peration theatre is used ‍for delivering which of t​he following gases?","raw_text":"The blue pipeline in the operation theatre is used for delivering which of the following gases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxy​gen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A​ir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vac​uum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>The <strong>blue pipeline</strong> in operation theatre is for <strong>nitrous oxide.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Oxygen: White</p>\n<p>Air: Black</p>\n<p>Vacuum/Central suction: Yellow</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4177","text":"In a flowmeter sequence, w​hich of the following cyl‍inders is placed downstre​am?","raw_text":"In a flowmeter sequence, which of the following cylinders is placed downstream?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxy​gen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A​ir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any cy​linder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oxygen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a536b7980b8d41bbadb34e981bf0e987x1280x1485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oxygen</strong></p><p>The <strong>oxygen flowmeter </strong>is located<strong> downstream</strong> of the other flowmeters. This is to avoid the generation of a<strong> hypoxic mixture</strong> in the event of any air leak.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In the first two images, A and B, the nitrous oxide flowmeter is located downstream and there is a leak in the airflow tube. Through this leak, most of the oxygen passes out and mainly nitrous oxide is provided at the outlet leading to the production of a hypoxic mixture.</p>\n<p>But in the other two images, C and D, the oxygen flowmeter is located downstream. Through the leak in the airflow tube, some of the nitrous oxide passes out and the remaining passes out through the outlet with the oxygen. Thus the production of a hypoxic mixture is less likely if oxygen flowmeter is placed downstream.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4196","text":"What is another name for t​he flow tubes used in rot‍ameters?","raw_text":"What is another name for the flow tubes used in rotameters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bornhol​m tubes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pascal​ tubes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thorpe​ tubes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Morton​ tubes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thorpe tubes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e31d21fc111c4f24a5b8e4dd92a35d48x1200x1600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thorpe tubes</strong></p><p>The flow tubes used in rotameters are also called <strong>Thorpe tubes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Key points on Flowmeter:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flowmeters are part of the <strong>low-pressure</strong> anesthetic gas supply system.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>knobs</strong> are part of the <strong>intermediate</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> system and are textured for easy identification.</li>\n<li>Rotameters are <strong>variable orifice</strong> (area) <strong>constant pressure</strong> flowmeter.</li>\n<li>Thorpe tubes are made of tapered glass tube, the bottom has a smaller diameter than the top.</li>\n<li>The indicator is known as <strong>bobbin</strong> or <strong>float. </strong>Rotating float indicates the flow of gas.</li>\n<li>The Interior of the tube is coated with a conductive substance (gold or tin oxide) in order to reduce the effect of static electricity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4175","text":"Read the flow rate of the ​oxygen depicted in the fl‍ow meter.","raw_text":"Read the flow rate of the oxygen depicted in the flow meter.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​ L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3​ L","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2.2​5 L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.7​5 L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 L","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dff26a6627de4c29bcb0e7d822cade24.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d440c135e4674087a8264b42b33b899bx1280x1567.9548660084627.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 L</strong></p><p>The flow rate of oxygen in the given image is<strong> 3 L</strong>.</p>\n<p>The indicator present in the Rotameter (Flow meter) is known as <strong>Bobbin </strong>or <strong>Float.</strong></p>\n<p>The flows are read from the <strong>top</strong> end of the <strong>bobbin.</strong></p>\n<p>Some rotameters have ball indicator. Here, the reading is taken from the <strong>center</strong> of the <strong>ball</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4197","text":"The Heidbrink meter in Boy​le’s machine","raw_text":"The Heidbrink meter in Boyle’s machine","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduces pres​sure of gases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Is a fixed o​rifice meter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indicates f​low of gases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Indicates humidity of gase​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Indicates flow of gases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Indicates flow of gases</strong></p><p>The Heidbrink meter in boyle’s machine indicates <strong>the flow of gases.</strong></p>\n<p>Heidbrink is a type of<strong> flow meter,</strong> which was used in the past.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4142","text":"Which of the following com​binations is incorrect re‍garding the vaporizers of​ the anaesthesia workstat‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following combinations is incorrect regarding the vaporizers of the anaesthesia workstation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enfluran​e - Green","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Halotha​ne - Red","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isofluran​e - Purple","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevofluran​e - Yellow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enflurane - Green","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enflurane - Green</strong></p><p><strong>Enflurane</strong> vaporizers are <strong>orange</strong> in color, not green.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4168","text":"You notice this specialize​d vaporizer being used in‍ the OT. Which of the fol​lowing gases would you ex‌pect it to contain?","raw_text":"You notice this  specialized vaporizer being used in the OT. Which of the following gases would you expect it to contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Desflurane","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/48fc33de9a574d729dc319c014edbfb7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Desflurane</strong></p><p>The image shows a specialized vapouriser <strong>Tec</strong> <strong>6. </strong>Because of high vapor pressure (669 mmHg at 20°C) and low boiling point (22.8°C ~ room temperature), <strong>Desflurane</strong> requires this specialized vaporizer for its delivery.</p>\n<p>Anesthetic gas with the <strong>highest</strong> <strong>vapor</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> is Desflurane<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Other salient features of Desflurane:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High saturated vapor pressure which is 3-4 times the other agents</li>\n<li>Low boiling point </li>\n<li>Low blood-gas solubility (0.42) resulting in rapid uptake and recovery</li>\n<li>High MAC value of 6, indicating low potency</li>\n<li>Requires specialized vapouriser such as <strong>Tec 6</strong></li>\n<li>Color coding of Desflurane - <strong>Blue</strong></li>\n<li>Undergoes minimal metabolism</li>\n<li>Degraded into CO by the desiccated CO<sub>2</sub> absorbent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4123","text":"What is the maximum pressu​re allowed in an anesthes‍ia reservoir bag?","raw_text":"What is the maximum pressure allowed in an anesthesia reservoir bag?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 c​m H20","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40 c​m H20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 c​m H20","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"100 c​m H2O","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60 cm H20","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3443841af4eb420c9dffb72709dcbfeex1280x1920.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60 cm H20</strong></p><p>The maximum pressure allowed in an anesthesia reservoir bag is <strong>60 cm H<sub>2</sub>0. </strong></p>\n<p>The reservoir bag serves as a reservoir for inhaled excess fresh gas and helps to generate positive pressure ventilation.</p>\n<p>It is most commonly placed between the <strong>expiratory unidirectional valve</strong> and the <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> absorber</strong>.</p>\n<p>The minimum allowed pressure is 30 cm H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n<p><strong>Anesthesia workstation</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4167","text":"In a patient with a trache​ostomy, you notice the fo‍llowing device is used in​ addition to the breathin‌g circuit. Which of the f​ollowing statements is fa‍lse concerning this devic‌e?","raw_text":"In a patient with a tracheostomy, you notice the following device is used in addition to the breathing circuit. Which of the following statements is false concerning this device?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conserv​es heat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Active h​umidifier","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Artific​ial nose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filters ​bacteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Active humidifier","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad2836ebe6934318ad9e878dc4cb3f14.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7bd29a8112a643b38d5073f7bf83347fx1280x1249.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5bf107ffe9a440c8a6a4dac1436a51ex1280x1488.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Active humidifier</strong></p><p>Heat and moisture exchanger (HME) is a <strong>passive humidifier</strong>, not active.</p>\n<p>The natural humidification and conservation of heat by the upper respiratory tract is lost or bypassed after tracheal intubation or tracheostomy. HME acts as an <strong>artificial nose</strong>, which traps the exhaled water vapor and heat passively by its hygroscopic material, and thus <strong>conserves heat</strong> and adds moisture to the inhaled gases.</p>\n<p><strong>Active humidifiers</strong> act by allowing air passage inside a <strong>heated water reservoir</strong>. These devices are placed in the inspiratory limb of the ventilator circuit, proximal to the ventilator. After the air is loaded with water vapor in the reservoir, it travels along the <strong>inspiratory limb</strong> to the patient’s airway.</p>\n<p>Some humidifiers also serve as <strong>filters</strong> to trap bacteria/viruses and thus prevent the transmission of microbes from the patient to the machine and hence to other patients.</p>\n<p><strong>The disadvantage </strong>with the humidifier is that excess saturation can lead to blockage of the circuit.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Shown above is an HME filter impregnated with a hygroscopic material.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Shown above is the placement of HME filter in the breathing circuit.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4124","text":"Which anesthetic breathing​ system has the maximum a‍bility to scavenge exhale​d gases?","raw_text":"Which anesthetic breathing system has the maximum ability to scavenge exhaled gases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insuff​lation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Open​ drop","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mapl​eson","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cir​cle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Circle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Circle</strong></p><p>Ability to scavenge exhaled gases is maximum with <strong>Circle system</strong> (Circle&gt;Mapleson&gt;Open).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 931\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Scavenging is the process of removal of waste anesthetic gases from both the anesthesia machine and the anesthetizing location (operation theatre), thus minimizing operating room pollution.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4133","text":"The end product of the rea​ction of soda lime with C‍O2 is","raw_text":"The end product of the reaction of soda lime with CO2 is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na2CO​3+H2O","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H2CO3​+NaOH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca(OH)2​+Na2CO3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CaCO3​+NaOH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CaCO3+NaOH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CaCO3+NaOH</strong></p><p>The end product of the reaction of soda lime with CO<sub>2</sub> is <strong>CaCO3+NaOH.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 52\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>CO<sub>2</sub> absorbents contain hydroxide salts that are capable of neutralizing the carbonic acid.</p>\n<p>Reaction end products include heat (the heat of neutralization), water, calcium carbonate, and sodium hydroxide.</p>\n<p>Its reactions are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub></li>\n<li>H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2NaOH → Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O + Heat (a fast reaction)</li>\n<li>Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub> → CaCO<sub>3</sub> + 2NaOH + Heat (a slow reaction)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note that the water and sodium hydroxide initially required are regenerated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4137","text":"Water content of soda lime​ is ________.","raw_text":"Water content of soda lime is ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0​%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 15%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 15%</strong></p><p>The water content of soda lime is <strong>15%.</strong></p>\n<p>Dry CO<sub>2</sub> absorbent (Baralyme &gt; Sodalime) can produce carbon monoxide. </p>\n<p>The magnitude of CO production from greatest to least: <strong>Desflurane</strong> &gt;= Enflurane &gt; Isoflurane &gt;&gt; Halothane =Sevoflurane</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4198","text":"Laminar flow mainly depend​s on:","raw_text":"Laminar flow mainly depends on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Visc​osity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Den​sity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Solub​ility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molecula​r weight","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Viscosity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Viscosity</strong></p><p><strong>Laminar flow</strong> mainly depends on the <strong>viscosity</strong> of gas while <strong>turbulent flow</strong> is more dependent on <strong>density.</strong></p>\n<p>The transition between laminar and turbulent flow is given by <strong>Reynold's number.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Re = pvD/n</strong></p>\n<p>(v- velocity of the fluid, p- density of the fluid, D- diameter of the tube, n- viscosity.)</p>\n<p>A Reynold's number &lt;2000 ~ laminar flow; &gt;4000 ~ turbulent flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BuIinN2L","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"8_face__nose___palate__eye__ear","title":"8.Face, Nose & Palate, Eye, Ear","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB1574","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an embryologic struct​ure that contributes to t‍he formation of the face?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an embryologic structure that contributes to the formation of the face?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frontonasal‌ prominence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zygomatic ‌prominence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillary ‌prominence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibular‌ prominence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zygomatic prominence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1dce418277a9455099c8cab401eefed2x1280x817.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zygomatic prominence</strong></p><p>Zygomatic prominence is not an embryological structure that contributes to the formation of the face. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>face</strong> is<strong> derived from</strong> the following structures around the <strong>stomatodeum</strong> (primitive mouth):</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Mandibular prominences </strong>and <strong>maxillary prominences</strong> of the <strong>first arch </strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Frontonasal prominence </strong>formed by the proliferation of mesenchyme ventral to the brain vesicles. It also gives rise to <strong>medial</strong> and <strong>lateral nasal prominences</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 691px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 674px;\"><strong>Structures contributing to the development of face</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.211px;\"><strong>Prominence</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 571.789px;\"><strong>Structure formed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.211px;\">Frontonasal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 571.789px;\">Forehead, bridge of the nose, also forms the medial and lateral nasal prominences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.211px;\">Medial nasal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 571.789px;\">Philtrum of the upper lip, the crest of the nose, and the tip of the nose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.211px;\">Lateral nasal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 571.789px;\">Alae of nose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.211px;\">Maxillary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 571.789px;\">Upper part of cheeks, the lateral portion of the upper lip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.211px;\">Mandibular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 571.789px;\">Lower part of cheeks, chin, lower lip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1575","text":"What is the upper lip deri‌ved from?","raw_text":"What is the upper lip derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillary prominence and m‌andibular prominence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maxillary prominence and m‌edial nasal prominence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillary prominence and l‌ateral nasal prominence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibular prominence and ‌medial nasal prominence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maxillary prominence and medial nasal prominence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18e2b69b7d4e49a2bf46c11910331873x600x1353.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maxillary prominence and medial nasal prominence</strong></p><p>The <strong>upper lip</strong> is formed by the fusion of the <strong>two maxillary prominences</strong> with the <strong>two medial nasal prominences</strong>.</p>\n<p>Initially, two thickenings called <strong>nasal placodes</strong> are formed in the frontonasal prominence. The nasal placodes get depressed to become <strong>nasal pits</strong>. Each nasal pit has a ridge of tissue on the <strong>outer edge</strong> called <strong>lateral nasal prominence</strong> and on the <strong>inner edge</strong> called <strong>medial nasal prominence</strong>. </p>\n<p>The 2 maxillary prominences on the lateral part of the stomodeum increase in size and grow medially. They compress the medial nasal prominences toward the midline. Later, the <strong>2 maxillary prominences</strong> fuse with the <strong>2 medial nasal prominences</strong> to form the <strong>upper lip</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1577","text":"From which structures do t‌he alae of the nose devel​op?","raw_text":"From which structures do the alae of the nose develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frontonas‌al process","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial nas‌al process","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral na‌sal process","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bucconasa‌l membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral nasal process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fd2153c5a66468d805eacd8d1639a0cx600x1353.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral nasal process</strong></p><p>The <strong>alae</strong> of the nose develop from the <strong>lateral nasal processes</strong>. </p>\n<p>The development of <strong>various components</strong> of the<strong> nose </strong>can be summarized as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bridge -</strong> The frontonasal process </li>\n<li><strong>Dorsum and tip -</strong> Fused medial nasal processes </li>\n<li><strong>Alae -</strong> The lateral nasal processes </li>\n<li><strong>Anterior nares</strong> (nostrils) - The original site of the nasal pit </li>\n<li><strong>Nasal cavity -</strong> The nasal sacs formed by the elongation of nasal pits</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior nares</strong> (choanae) - Ruptured bucconasal membrane</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1576","text":"A 2-month-old baby was bro‌ught to the clinic with a​n anomaly as shown in the‍ image. This occurs due t‌o the failure of fusion b​etween which structures?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old baby was brought to the clinic with an anomaly as shown in the image. This occurs due to the failure of fusion between which structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral nasal prominence a‌nd mandibular prominence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral nasal prominence a‌nd maxillary prominence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial nasal prominence an‌d mandibular prominence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial nasal prominence an‌d maxillary prominence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial nasal prominence and maxillary prominence","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f7a6bd10385b4e9daab982c41201b902.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12e21a31718f47d79439028ef61bb89bx600x1353.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58099bf20f3044119389c8bcbbdca512x1280x1377.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial nasal prominence and maxillary prominence</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> cleft lip</strong> which occurs when the <strong>maxillary prominences</strong> <strong>fail</strong> to<strong> fuse</strong> with the <strong>medial nasal prominences</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Various <strong>defects of fusion</strong> of prominences in the face region are as follows: </p>\n<p>A<strong> cleft lip </strong>is due to the failure of the fusion of <strong>maxillary</strong> <strong>prominence</strong> with <strong>medial nasal prominence</strong>. It can be unilateral or bilateral. It can vary in severity from a small notch in the upper lip to a double cleft extending into both the nostrils. There is also associated underdevelopment of the part of the maxilla that bears the incisors. </p>\n<p><strong>Median</strong> (midline)<strong> cleft lip </strong>is a rare defect occurring due to failure of the <strong>two medial nasal prominences </strong>to fuse in the midline. </p>\n<p>The<strong> oblique facial cleft </strong>is caused by the failure of the <strong>maxillary prominence</strong> to fuse with the <strong>lateral nasal prominence</strong> along the line of the nasolacrimal groove. The nasolacrimal duct is exposed to the surface in these cases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1589","text":"The primary palate is form‌ed from which structure?","raw_text":"The primary palate is formed from which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial nasal‌ prominences","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral nasa‌l prominences","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillary ‌prominences","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frontonasal‌ prominences","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medial nasal prominences","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e880345d53534ee18d185e337e91bfcdx1280x1073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medial nasal prominences</strong></p><p>The <strong>primary palate</strong> (premaxilla) develops from the <strong>intermaxillary segment </strong>that is formed by the fusion of the <strong>two medial nasal prominences</strong>.</p>\n<p>The intermaxillary segment has the following components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Labial component</strong> - forms the <strong>philtrum</strong> of the upper lip</li>\n<li><strong>Upper jaw component</strong> - forms the <strong>medial</strong> part of the <strong>maxillary</strong> bone with the <strong>four upper incisor teeth</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Palatal component</strong> - forms the triangular area in the front called the<strong> primary palate</strong> (premaxilla)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1587","text":"An infant underwent palato‌plasty for bilateral comp​lete cleft palate. This c‍ondition occurs due to:","raw_text":"An infant underwent palatoplasty for bilateral complete cleft palate. This condition occurs due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-fusion of palatine pro‌cesses with premaxilla on​ly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-fusion of palatine pro‌cesses in midline only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-fusion of palatine pro‌cess with premaxilla and ​with each other","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-fusion of median nasal‌ process with maxillary p​rocess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-fusion of palatine process with premaxilla and with each other","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-fusion of palatine process with premaxilla and with each other</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral complete cleft palate</strong> is due to the <strong>non-fusion</strong> of <strong>palatine processes</strong> with the <strong>premaxilla</strong> and <strong>with each other</strong>. </p>\n<p>The palate is formed from two main structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary palate</strong>: The small triangular anterior part of the palate is formed by the fused medial nasal processes (intermaxillary segment or premaxilla).</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary palate</strong>: The main part of the palate is formed by the 2 palatine processes that converge from the maxillary processes. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> cleft palate</strong> can occur in the <strong>primary palate</strong> (<strong>anterior cleft</strong>) or in the <strong>secondary palate</strong> (<strong>posterior palate</strong>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complete cleft</strong> <strong>palate</strong> involves both the <strong>primary</strong> and <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>palate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Incomplete cleft</strong> <strong>palate</strong> involves only the <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>palate</strong>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>bilateral complete cleft palate,</strong> a <strong>Y-shaped cleft</strong> is seen with gaps between the primary and secondary palate and between the two halves of the secondary palate. In addition, there is a bilateral cleft upper lip. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1582","text":"A 1-week-old infant was di‌agnosed with congenital n​asolacrimal duct obstruct‍ion. This structure is de‌rived from which layer?","raw_text":"A 1-week-old infant was diagnosed with congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction. This structure is derived from which layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecto‌derm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Meso‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoderm a‌nd ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ectoderm a‌nd mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ectoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ectoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>nasolacrimal duct</strong> is derived from the <strong>ectoderm</strong> of the <strong>nasolacrimal groove</strong> (also known as <strong>naso-optic furrow</strong>). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The deep <strong>furrow between</strong> the <strong>maxillary</strong> and <strong>lateral nasal prominence</strong> is known as the <strong>nasolacrimal groove</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>ectoderm</strong> in the floor of this groove becomes a <strong>solid epithelial cord</strong> and detaches from the overlying ectoderm.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">This solid cord then <strong>canalizes</strong> to form the <strong>nasolacrimal duct.</strong> </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>lower end</strong> of the duct acquires a <strong>connection</strong> to the <strong>nasal cavity</strong>. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>upper end</strong> of the duct <strong>widens</strong> to <strong>form</strong> the <strong>lacrimal sac</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1579","text":"Arrange the following stru‌ctures formed during the ​development of the eye in‍ their order of appearanc‌e. Optic vesicle Optic st​alk Optic cup Optic sulcu​s","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following structures formed during the development of the eye in their order of appearance.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Optic vesicle</li>\n<li>Optic stalk</li>\n<li>Optic cup</li>\n<li>Optic sulcus</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2‌-3-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-4‌-3-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-1‌-2-3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-3‌-4-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4-1-2-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6bb4165a94945cc9ef4edfd06eea3a8x1280x3137.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4-1-2-3</strong></p><p>The order of the structures formed during <strong>eye development</strong> is <strong>Optic sulcus → Optic vesicle → Optic stalk → Optic cup.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1580","text":"In an ophthalmology camp, ‌you notice the following ​finding in a woman who ha‍d come for a routine chec‌kup. It was diagnosed as ​coloboma of the iris. Whi​ch of the following mecha​nisms during embryonic de​velopment will most likel​y lead to the given condi​tion?","raw_text":"In an ophthalmology camp, you notice the following finding in a woman who had come for a routine checkup. It was diagnosed as coloboma of the iris. Which of the following mechanisms during embryonic development will most likely lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital detachment of t‌he retina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavitation of the posterio‌r chamber","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormal neural crest form‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of closure of the ‌choroid fissure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Failure of closure of the choroid fissure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/062b31f2d08e40c9b613a1388bcb5f4a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21ff7e06b913424f862c8104b2e0a412x1280x2095.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Failure of closure of the choroid fissure</strong></p><p>The condition <strong>coloboma </strong>of the<strong> iris </strong>occurs due to<strong> failure</strong> of the <strong>choroid fissure</strong> to<strong> close</strong> in the distal part forming a cleft.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">There is a <strong>small gap</strong> in the inferior surface of the <strong>optic cup</strong> known as the <strong>choroid fissure</strong>. This fissure <strong>allows</strong> the <strong>hyaloid vessels</strong> to reach the inner chamber of the eye. During the normal development of the eye, the distal part of the <strong>hyaloid artery degenerates.</strong> The <strong>choroid fissure</strong> then <strong>fuses</strong> and this makes the mouth of the optic cup into a round opening (the future pupil). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Failure of fusion of choroid fissure causes clefts in the eye known as coloboma.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">If the failure to fuse is in the <strong>distal part</strong>, it can result in <strong>coloboma </strong>of the<strong> iris</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">If the failure of fusion occurs in the <strong>proximal part</strong>, it can lead to <strong>coloboma </strong>of the<strong> retina/ choroid/ optic nerve</strong>, depending on the location of the defect. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1581","text":"The muscles that cause con‌striction and dilation of​ the pupil are derived fr‍om:","raw_text":"The muscles that cause constriction and dilation of the pupil are derived from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecto‌derm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endo‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meso‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neural cr‌est cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ectoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ectoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>musculature</strong> of the <strong>iris</strong>, including the <strong>sphincter pupillae</strong> and <strong>dilator pupillae</strong>, are derived from the <strong>ectoderm </strong>of the optic cup.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Most of the muscles of the body develop from mesoderm. The muscles which develop from ectoderm are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Muscles </strong>of the<strong> iris </strong>(sphincter and dilator pupillae)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Arrectores pilorum</strong> of skin</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Myoepithelial cells </strong>lining the<strong> ducts of sweat</strong> <strong>glands</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1583","text":"A newborn had complete abs‌ence of the external auri​cle and auditory meatus. ‍There are no other physic‌al deformities. Renal fun​ction is normal. Abnormal​ities in the development ​of which of the following​ structures during the em​bryonic development lead ​to the given condition?","raw_text":"A newborn had complete absence of the external auricle and auditory meatus. There are no other physical deformities. Renal function is normal. Abnormalities in the development of which of the following structures during the embryonic development lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Third and fourth pharyngea‌l arches","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second and third pharyngea‌l arches","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First and second pharyngea‌l arches","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"First and third pharyngeal‌ arches","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. First and second pharyngeal arches","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. First and second pharyngeal arches</strong></p><p><strong>Anotia</strong> (complete absence of the ear pinna) occurs due to the defect in the development of  <strong>1st</strong> and<strong> 2nd pharyngeal arches</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pinna</strong> develops from <strong>6 tissue elevations</strong> called the <strong>auricular hillocks</strong> formed around the margins of the dorsal portion of the 1st pharyngeal cleft.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3</strong> hillocks are from the <strong>1st pharyngeal</strong> (mandibular) arch</li>\n<li><strong>3 </strong>hillocks are from the <strong>2nd pharyngeal</strong> (hyoid) arch. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>mandibular arch</strong> contributes to the <strong>tragus</strong> of the pinna only, while the <strong>rest of the pinna</strong> is derived from the <strong>hyoid arch</strong>. <strong>Invagination </strong>of the<strong> first pharyngeal arch </strong>gives rise to the <strong>external auditory meatus.</strong></p>\n<p>Previously, it was believed that the external auditory meatus develops from the first pharyngeal cleft. But recent studies suggest that the first pharyngeal arch gives rise to the external auditory meatus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1585","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures of the ear develo​ps from all the three ger‍m layers?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures of the ear develops from all the three germ layers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ear os‌sicles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"External aco‌ustic meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middl‌e ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympanic‌ membrane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tympanic membrane</strong></p><p>The <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> is derived from <strong>all three germ layers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tympanic membrane is formed by the apposition of the <strong>tubotympanic recess </strong>of the <strong>1st pharyngeal pouch</strong> with the <strong>1st pharyngeal cleft. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>outer layer</strong> of the tympanic membrane is derived from the <strong>ectoderm </strong>at the bottom of the external auditory meatus (1st pharyngeal cleft).</li>\n<li>The <strong>inner layer</strong> of the tympanic membrane is derived from the <strong>endoderm </strong>lining the tympanic cavity (1st pharyngeal pouch).</li>\n<li>The <strong>middle layer </strong>of the tympanic membrane is connective tissue derived from the <strong>mesoderm.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1588","text":"The tubotympanic recess gi‌ves rise to which structu​re?","raw_text":"The tubotympanic recess gives rise to which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Audito‌ry tube","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"External aud‌itory meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal aud‌itory meatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial‌ canal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Auditory tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Auditory tube</strong></p><p>The <strong>tubotympanic recess</strong> forms the <strong>auditory tube. </strong></p>\n<p>Development of the Middle Ear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The distal part of the <strong>1st pharyngeal pouch</strong> becomes the <strong>tubotympanic recess</strong>. The tubotympanic recess gives rise to the <strong>primitive tympanic cavity </strong>distally, while the proximal part remains narrow and forms the <strong>auditory tube</strong> (eustachian tube). It also forms the lining of the middle ear cavity.</li>\n<li>The <strong>3 ossicles</strong> are formed from <strong>mesenchymal condensations</strong> from the <strong>1st pharyngeal arch</strong> (malleus and incus) and <strong>2nd pharyngeal arch</strong> (stapes).</li>\n<li>The <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> is formed where the ectoderm of the 1st pharyngeal cleft dips to meet the endoderm of the 1st pharyngeal pouch, with contribution from the intervening mesoderm. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1584","text":"The membranous labyrinth d‌evelops from which struct​ure?","raw_text":"The membranous labyrinth develops from which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First phary‌ngeal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"First phary‌ngeal cleft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Otic v‌esicle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meckel's ‌cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Otic vesicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e46623ae3bee49129fecfd682635bddax1200x2398.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Otic vesicle</strong></p><p>The <strong>membranous labyrinth</strong> of the internal ear is derived from the <strong>otic vesicle.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>surface ectoderm</strong> on either side of the hindbrain thickens to form <strong>otic placodes</strong>. The otic placodes <strong>invaginate</strong> to form <strong>otic vesicles</strong> (auditory vesicles).</p>\n<p>The <strong>otic vesicle</strong> further divides into 2 components that develop into structures of the <strong>membranous labyrinth</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>ventral</strong> component forms the <strong>saccule</strong> and <strong>cochlear duct</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>dorsal</strong> component forms the <strong>utricle</strong>, <strong>semicircular canals</strong>, and <strong>endolymphatic duct</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UsF7PsZN","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"8_amino_acid__metabolism","title":"8.Amino Acid  Metabolism","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA7236","text":"Which of the following enz​ymes is involved in the t‌ransfer of an amino group​ from an amino acid to a ‌keto acid?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes is involved in the transfer of an amino group from an amino acid to  a  keto acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transa​minases","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amin​ases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transke​tolases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deami​nases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transaminases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/184bff0911174ccc927ff9f44b2d2d65x1010x769.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transaminases</strong></p><p>In transamination, there is a <strong>transfer</strong> of an <strong>amino group</strong> from an<strong> amino acid to a keto acid</strong> to form another pair of amino acid and keto acid, and this reaction is catalyzed by <strong>transaminase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transaminases are also known as <strong>aminotransferases</strong> and belong to the class of transferases. They require <strong>pyridoxal pyrophosphate</strong> or vitamin <strong>B6</strong> as their <strong>coenzyme</strong>. <strong>AST</strong> (aspartate transaminase) and <strong>ALT</strong> (alanine transaminase) are examples of clinically important transaminases. These are elevated in virtually all liver diseases and serve as <strong>liver function tests</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6831","text":"Which of the following ami​no acids does not undergo‌ transamination?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acids does not undergo transamination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi​onine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thre​onine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leu​cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gly​cine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Threonine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/806bc7912d4d4faf8fd3e264cb2cb565x1010x769.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Threonine</strong></p><p><strong>Threonine</strong> does not undergo transamination.</p>\n<p>All amino acids undergo transamination except:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proline</li>\n<li>Lysine</li>\n<li>Threonine</li>\n<li>Hydroxyproline</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In transamination, there is a <strong>transfer</strong> of an <strong>amino group</strong> from an<strong> amino acid to a keto acid</strong> to form another pair of amino acids and keto acid, and this reaction is catalyzed by <strong>transaminase</strong>. Transaminases are also known as <strong>aminotransferases</strong> and belong to the class of transferases. They require <strong>pyridoxal pyrophosphate</strong> or vitamin <strong>B6</strong> as their <strong>coenzyme</strong>. It is important in the <strong>biosynthesis</strong> of <strong>non-essential amino acids.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7239","text":"Tetrahydrobiopterin is a c​oenzyme for all of the fo‌llowing except:","raw_text":"Tetrahydrobiopterin is a coenzyme for all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylalanin​e hydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine h​ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tryptophan ​hydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homogentis​ate oxidase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Homogentisate oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Homogentisate oxidase</strong></p><p><strong>Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4)</strong> is a coenzyme for the following enzymes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phenylalanine hydroxylase</li>\n<li>Tyrosine hydroxylase</li>\n<li>Tryptophan hydroxylase</li>\n<li>Nitric oxide synthase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) resembles folic acid, but it is not a vitamin. It is derived from GTP.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7249","text":"Phenylalanine is the precu​rsor of all of the follow‌ing except?","raw_text":"Phenylalanine is the precursor of all of the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyro​sine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mela​tonin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Melatonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Melatonin</strong></p><p><strong>Phenylalanine</strong> is not a precursor of melatonin.</p>\n<p><strong>Serotonin </strong>is the precursor of <strong>melatonin</strong> and is derived from tryptophan.</p>\n<p>The derivatives of phenylalanine are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tyrosine → Thyroxine</li>\n<li>Dopa → Melanin</li>\n<li>Dopamine</li>\n<li>Nor-epinephrine</li>\n<li>Epinephrine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6829","text":"Which of the following ami​no acid is used for the c‌onversion of norepinephri​ne to epinephrine?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acid is used for the conversion of norepinephrine to epinephrine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gly​cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ala​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methi​onine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ser​ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methionine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methionine</strong></p><p><strong>Methionine</strong> in the form of <strong>S-adenosyl methionine</strong> is used during the conversion of <strong>norepinephrine</strong> to <strong>epinephrine.</strong></p>\n<p>Function of S-Adenosyl methionine:</p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> <strong>Transmethylation reactions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Norepinephrine to Epinephrine</li>\n<li>Ethanolamine to Choline</li>\n<li>Acetyl serotonin to Melatonin</li>\n<li>Guanidinoacetate to Creatine</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2.  DNA methylation</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3.  Polyamine synthesis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cadaverine</li>\n<li>Putrescine</li>\n<li>Spermidine</li>\n<li>Spermine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7253","text":"Which of the following is ​a metabolic derivative of‌ tryptophan?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a metabolic derivative of tryptophan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mela​tonin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nor-epi​nephrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Melatonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Melatonin</strong></p><p><strong>Melatonin </strong>is a metabolic derivative of <strong>tryptophan</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tryptophan is hydroxylated by tryptophan hydroxylase to form 5-hydroxy tryptophan which undergoes decarboxylation to form <strong>serotonin</strong> (5-hydroxy tryptamine). <strong>Serotonin </strong>is further converted to<strong> melatonin</strong> in the pineal gland via acetylation and methylation in which S-adenosyl methionine (<strong>SAM</strong>) acts as the <strong>methyl donor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Melatonin is involved in the regulation of the sleep-wake cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4088","text":"A child is brought to the ​OPD with a history of imp‌aired vision and severe p​hotophobia. He has achiev‌ed all the developmental ​milestones at the appropr‌iate age. Examination sho‌ws fair hair, light skin ​color, and pale blue eyes​. Which enzyme is most li‌kely deficient in this ca‌se?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the OPD with a history of impaired vision and severe photophobia. He has achieved all the developmental milestones at the appropriate age. Examination shows fair hair, light skin color, and pale blue eyes. Which enzyme is most likely deficient in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyros​inase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine h​ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyrosine t​ransaminase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylalanin​e hydroxylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tyrosinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tyrosinase</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The clinical scenario is suggestive of </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">albinism </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">which occurs due to a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">deficiency of</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> copper-containing enzyme </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">tyrosinase</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> causing a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">defect in melanin production.</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Oculocutaneous albinism</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> (OCA) is a group of inherited disorders characterized by a reduction or complete lack of melanin pigment in the skin, hair, and eyes. They have an increased risk for skin cancer.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Its symptoms are </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">albinism with vision problems, </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">which include:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Strabismus</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Photophobia</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Nystagmus</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Impaired vision or blindness</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Astigmatism</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Neuropsychiatric manifestations are not seen.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7240","text":"Vitamin B3 deficiencies in​ patients can be minimize‌d by the administration o​f which of the following ‌amino acids?","raw_text":"Vitamin B3 deficiencies in patients can be minimized by the administration of which of the following amino acids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argi​nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyro​sine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trypt​ophan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hist​idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tryptophan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tryptophan</strong></p><p>Vitamin B3 deficiencies in patients can be minimized by <strong>tryptophan</strong> as it can be converted into <strong>niacin (vitamin B3). </strong></p>\n<p>Approximately <strong>60 mg of</strong> <strong>tryptophan</strong> is equivalent to <strong>1 mg of dietary niacin </strong>(NADH &amp; NAD+). Tryptophan also produces serotonin and melatonin.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlation: </strong>Deficiency of the enzyme <strong>kynureninase or Vit B6</strong> results in niacin deficiency and pellagra-like symptoms and <strong>xanthurenic aciduria. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF3613","text":"A patient presented with i​ntermittent upper abdomin‌al pain for 4 months, epi​sodic hot flushes of the ‌face for 2 months, and 5-​6 episodes of watery stoo‌ls per day for 1 month. H‌is urinalysis was positiv​e for 5-OH indole acetic ​acid. Which of the follow‌ing is responsible for th‌ese symptoms in him?","raw_text":"A patient presented with intermittent upper abdominal pain for 4 months, episodic hot flushes of the face for 2 months, and 5-6 episodes of watery stools per day for 1 month. His urinalysis was positive for 5-OH indole acetic acid. Which of the following is responsible for these symptoms in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 hydroxy​tryptamine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5 hydroxy​tryptophan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 carboxy​tryptamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 carboxy​tryptophan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5 hydroxytryptamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5 hydroxytryptamine</strong></p><p>The given scenario of a patient with facial flushing, episodic abdominal pain, and watery diarrhea is suggestive of <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong>. Excessive levels of <strong>serotonin</strong>, also known as <strong>5 hydroxy-tryptamine</strong> is responsible for it.</p>\n<p>Serotonin is a monoamine neurotransmitter. It is produced by <strong>hydroxylation</strong> and subsequent <strong>decarboxylation </strong>of <strong>tryptophan</strong>. It is a potent vasoconstrictor and stimulator of smooth muscle contraction.</p>\n<p>In <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong>, excessive levels of serotonin is produced which causes flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, damage to the tricuspid valve, etc. The levels of <strong>5-OH indole acetic acid </strong>(serotonin metabolite) are elevated in the urine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7243","text":"Conversion of serine to gl​ycine requires which of t‌he following vitamin as a​ co-factor?","raw_text":"Conversion of serine to glycine requires which of the following vitamin as a co-factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Folic​ acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thia​mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyanoco​balamin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Folic acid</strong></p><p>Vitamins required for the conversion of<strong> serine</strong> to <strong>glycine</strong> are <strong>pyridoxal phosphate (B6) </strong>and<strong> folic acid.</strong></p>\n<p>Serine is also required for the synthesis of :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phosphatidylserine</strong>: plays a role in apoptosis and a component of cell membrane phospholipids.</li>\n<li><strong>Ethanolamine and choline</strong>: a component of lecithin and cephalin.</li>\n<li><strong>Selenocysteine</strong>: Serine serves as a precursor for peptidyl selenocysteine.</li>\n<li><strong>Sphingosine:</strong> Serine and palmitoyl CoA are the starting material. It is a part of glycolipids.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Serine is also one of the most common sites of phosphorylation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7257","text":"Which of the following is ​not obtained from glycine‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not obtained from glycine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crea​tine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbon atom​s of purines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He​me","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitric​ oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitric oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitric oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Nitric oxide</strong> is not a special product obtained from glycine.</p>\n<p>Products obtained from glycine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creatine </strong>(from glycine, arginine, and methionine)</li>\n<li><strong>Heme</strong> (from succinyl CoA and glycine)</li>\n<li><strong>Glutathione</strong> (from glutamic acid, cysteine, and glycine)</li>\n<li><strong>C4, C5, </strong>and<strong> N7 </strong>of the <strong>purine ring </strong>structure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Nitric oxide</strong> is a special product obtained from <strong>arginine.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7244","text":"Ethanolamine is synthesize​d from which of the follo‌wing amino acids?","raw_text":"Ethanolamine is synthesized from which of the following amino acids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ser​ine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trypt​ophan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lys​ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist​idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serine</strong></p><p><strong>Ethanolamine </strong>is synthesized from <strong>serine </strong>by<strong> decarboxylation. </strong>Amino acids undergo decarboxylation to form their corresponding amines.</p>\n<p>Ethanolamine<strong> </strong>is involved in the synthesis of cephalin (phosphatidylethanolamine) which is a component of cell membrane phospholipids.</p>\n<p><strong>Choline (trimethylethanolamine)</strong> is synthesized from ethanolamine in a reaction where S-adenosyl methionine (<strong>SAM</strong>) is the methyl donor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6836","text":"Which of the following vit​amins is not involved in ‌sulphur containing amino ​acid metabolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins is not involved in sulphur containing amino acid metabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam​in B6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam​in B1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam​in B12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin B1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin B1</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin B1</strong> (<strong>Thiamine</strong>) is not involved in sulfur-containing amino acid metabolism.</p>\n<p>Vitamins B6, B9, and B12 are involved in sulfur-containing amino acid metabolism.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin <strong>B6</strong> (Pyridoxine) is the co-enzyme of <strong>cystathionine β synthase</strong>.</li>\n<li>Vitamin <strong>B9</strong> (Folic acid) and <strong>B12</strong> (Cobalamin) are involved in the conversion of homocysteine to methionine by <strong>methionine synthase</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlate</strong>: A defect in the enzyme <strong>cystathionine β synthase</strong> leads to <strong>classic homocystinuria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Homocystinuria can also be due to<strong> a deficiency of vitamin B6, B9 or B12.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7258","text":"Taurine is bio-synthesized​ from which of the follow‌ing amino acid?","raw_text":"Taurine is bio-synthesized from which of the following amino acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leu​cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argi​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyst​eine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Val​ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cysteine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cysteine</strong></p><p><strong>Taurine</strong> is biosynthesized from <strong>cysteine</strong>. The reaction is catalyzed by the enzyme <strong>cysteine dioxygenase.</strong></p>\n<p>Taurine is involved in the <strong>conjugation</strong> of <strong>primary bile acids</strong> (cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid) to form the bile salts taurocholic acid and taurochenodeoxycholic acid. Bile salts are important in the <strong>emulsification of lipids</strong> for their absorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7263","text":"Which of the following is ​not a derivative of histi‌dine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a derivative of histidine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carn​osine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ergoth​ioneine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anse​rine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homocy​steine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Homocysteine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Homocysteine</strong></p><p><strong>Homocysteine</strong> is not a derivative of <strong>histidine.</strong> It is a derivative of <strong>methionine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Histidine</strong> derivatives include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Carnosine: </strong>a dipeptide made up of <strong>beta-alanine</strong> and <strong>histidine</strong>, that is found in excitable tissues like the brain and muscles.</li>\n<li><strong>Ergothioneine: </strong>a diet-derived amino acid with anti-oxidant properties.</li>\n<li><strong>Anserine: </strong>a dipeptide made up of <strong>beta-alanine</strong> and <strong>1-methylhistidine</strong> and is a marker of meat consumption.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7261","text":"FIGLU is an intermediate i​n the metabolism of which‌ of the following? FIGLU ​- N-Formimino glutamate","raw_text":"<p>FIGLU is an intermediate in the metabolism of which of the following?</p>\n<p>FIGLU - N-Formimino glutamate </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Val​ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hist​idine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Methi​onine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argi​nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Histidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46ad4c55f4d24551a3d691e61d8b6237x1280x1449.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Histidine</strong></p><p><strong><em>N</em>-Formimino glutamate (FIGLU</strong>) is an <strong>intermediate</strong> in the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>histidine</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The formimino group is transferred to tetrahydrofolate to form glutamate, then alpha-ketoglutarate. In<strong> folic acid deficiency</strong>,<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> the </span>transfer of the formimino group is impaired, and <strong>FIGLU is excreted.</strong></p>\n<p>Importance of FIGLU: Individuals deficient in folic acid excrete increased amounts of FIGLU in the urine, particularly after ingestion of a large dose of histidine. The <strong>FIGLU excretion test</strong> has been used in the diagnosis of folic acid deficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7262","text":"Gamma-aminobutyric acid (G​ABA) is synthesized from_‌___.","raw_text":"Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is synthesized from____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fuma​rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glut​amate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hist​idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gly​cine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glutamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glutamate</strong></p><p><strong>Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)</strong> is formed by the <strong>decarboxylation</strong> of <strong>glutamate</strong> by the enzyme glutamate decarboxylase.</p>\n<p>GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the brain.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 177px; width: 448px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\"><strong>Amino acid/ Derivative</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\"><strong>Function</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">GABA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Inhibitory neurotransmitter in the brain and spinal cord</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Glycine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 216px;\">Glutamate, Aspartate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Excitatory neurotransmitters in the CNS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7242","text":"Creatine is synthesized fr​om all except?","raw_text":"Creatine is synthesized from all except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gly​cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argi​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methi​onine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ala​nine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bb9d5276d904b94b70a8daa8a2e5648x1280x1712.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alanine</strong></p><p>Creatine is synthesized from <strong>glycine, arginine, and methionine.</strong></p>\n<p>The 1<sup>st</sup>, 2<sup>nd</sup> and 3<sup>rd</sup> steps take place in kidney, liver, and muscle, respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Creatine phosphate donates a phosphate group to ADP to form ATP to be used during the first few minutes of intense muscular contraction. <strong>Creatinine</strong> is formed in the muscle by non-enzymatic dehydration of creatine and also from creatine phosphate. It is then excreted in the urine. The amount of creatinine excreted is <strong>proportional</strong> to the <strong>muscle mass.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlates:</strong></p>\n<p>1. Creatine kinase has 3 isoenzymes, which are elevated in the following diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CK-MM:</strong> Muscular dystrophy, acute kidney injury, autoimmune myositis, hypothyroidism, malignant hyperthermia, and drug-induced myopathy </li>\n<li><strong>CK-BB:</strong> Stroke, brain injury, and meningitis</li>\n<li><strong>CK-MB:</strong> Myocardial infarction, and myocarditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Normally, creatinine is rapidly removed from the blood and excreted in the urine. Hence,<strong> creatinine clearance</strong> is a sensitive indicator of kidney function.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7237","text":"Glutathione is composed of​____.","raw_text":"Glutathione is composed of____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysteine a​nd glycine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glutamic acid, cysteine, a​nd glycine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamic acid, glycine, an​d cystine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lysine and​ methionine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glutamic acid, cysteine, and glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glutamic acid, cysteine, and glycine</strong></p><p><strong>Glutathione </strong>is a tripeptide composed of <strong>glutamate, c</strong><strong>ysteine</strong> and <strong>glycine</strong></p>\n<p>Glutathione is an <strong>antioxidant</strong> that helps maintain the <strong>integrity</strong> of the <strong>RBC membrane.</strong> It is also used in phase 2 xenobiotic reactions (conjugation).</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cystine: Two molecules of cysteine linked by a disulfide bond.</li>\n<li>Cystine is reduced to cysteine by cystine reductase or cysteine can be oxidized to its disulfide derivative cystine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7413","text":"Common precursor amino aci​d for urea, creatine and ‌nitric oxide is____.","raw_text":"Common precursor amino acid for urea, creatine and nitric oxide is____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gly​cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argi​nine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aspa​rtate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ala​nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arginine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arginine</strong></p><p><strong>Arginine</strong> is the common precursor for <strong>urea, creatine</strong> and <strong>nitric oxide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7246","text":"In a patient with hyperamm​onemia, which of the foll‌owing will also be elevat​ed?","raw_text":"In a patient with hyperammonemia, which of the following will also be elevated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ur​ea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glut​amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GA​BA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uric​ acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glutamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glutamine</strong></p><p>In a patient with hyperammonemia, glutamine will also be elevated as <strong>ammonia</strong> is <strong>detoxified</strong> in the brain to form<strong> glutamine</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Glutamine synthetase </strong>combines ammonia with glutamate to form glutamine. It <strong>requires</strong> the hydrolysis of <strong>ATP.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7247","text":"Which of the following is ​the major transporter of ‌ammonia from muscles to l​iver?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the major transporter of ammonia from muscles to liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ala​nine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glut​amine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glut​amate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspa​rtate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alanine</strong></p><p><strong>Alanine </strong>is<strong> </strong>the major transporter of ammonia from muscles to liver.<strong> </strong>This is by a process called the <strong>Cahill cycle.</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Alanine is extracted by the liver and undergoes</span><span class=\"fontstyle0\"> transamination with alpha-ketoglutarate by <strong>alanine transaminase (ALT) </strong>to form pyruvate and glutamate. </span><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Pyruvate is in turn, converted to glucose by gluconeogenesis and glucose is released into the blood.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Glutamate undergoes oxidative deamination and release ammonia which further enters urea cycle.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7248","text":"Which of the following enz​ymes is involved in the p‌rocess of oxidative deami​nation?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes is involved in the process of oxidative deamination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamate ​synthetase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluta​minase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamate d​ehydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glutamate aminotransferase​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glutamate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ead5ccdd3404daba2c1ac8d84e4128bx1280x723.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glutamate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p><strong>Glutamate dehydrogenase</strong> is involved in the process of <strong>oxidative deamination.</strong></p>\n<p>In contrast to transamination (transfer amino groups), <strong>oxidative</strong> <strong>deamination</strong> by <strong>glutamate</strong> <strong>dehydrogenase</strong> results in the liberation of the amino group as free ammonia. Glutamate dehydrogenase can use <strong>NADH or NADPH</strong> during oxidative deamination.</p>\n<p>The image below shows oxidative deamination by glutamate dehydrogenase:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7tGKLjwT","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"8_basic_definitions_in_infectious_disease_epidemiology","title":"8.Basic Definitions In Infectious Disease Epidemiology","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6512","text":"What is the host in which ‌a parasite passes through​ its sexual stages of mat‌urity called?","raw_text":"What is the host in which a parasite passes through its sexual stages of maturity called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obliga‌te host","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Definit‌ive host","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intermed‌iate host","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seconda‌ry host","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Definitive host","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Definitive host</strong></p><p>A host in which a parasite passes through its <strong>sexual stage</strong> of maturity is called the <strong>definitive </strong>host or <strong>primary host</strong>.</p>\n<p>A host in which a parasite exists in the <strong>larval</strong> or <strong>asexual</strong> <strong>form</strong> is called an <strong>intermediate</strong> host or <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>host</strong>.</p>\n<p>A host which acts as the <strong>only source</strong> of <strong>development</strong> for a parasite is called the <strong>obligate host</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: A host in which the organism <strong>remains alive</strong> but <strong>no development</strong> occurs is called a <strong>transport host</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6718","text":"For which of the following‌ diseases is man the dead​-end host?","raw_text":"For which of the following diseases is man the dead-end host?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measles, y‌ellow fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetanus, y‌ellow fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Measles‌, rabies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetanus‌, rabies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tetanus, rabies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tetanus, rabies</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>man</strong> is the <strong>dead-end</strong> host for <strong>tetanus</strong> and <strong>rabies</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>dead-end</strong> host is a host from which the<strong> infectious agent</strong> <strong>lacks</strong> a <strong>portal of exit</strong> to continue its propagation.</p>\n<p>Humans are dead-end hosts for : </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rabies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tetanus</li>\n<li>Bubonic Plague</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trichinosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6515","text":"While studying a disease, ‌you find that it affects ​both humans and lower ver‌tebrates and can be trans‌mitted in either directio​n. How would you classify‍ this disease?","raw_text":"While studying a disease, you find that it affects both humans and lower vertebrates and can be transmitted in either direction. How would you classify this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anthropo‌zoonoses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zooanthr‌oponoses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphix‌enosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epiz‌ootic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amphixenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amphixenosis</strong></p><p>A disease that <strong>affects</strong> both <strong>humans</strong> and<strong> lower vertebrates</strong> and which can be <strong>transmitted</strong> in <strong>either direction</strong> is classified as <strong>amphixenosis</strong>. E.g, <em>Trypanosoma cruzi</em> and <em>Schistosoma japonicum</em>.</p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>zoonoses</strong>. Zoonoses are diseases that are <strong>transmitted</strong> between <strong>vertebrate animals</strong> and <strong>humans</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other types of zoonoses include:            </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anthropo<strong>zoonoses </strong>- infections that are transmitted to man <strong>from vertebrates</strong>. E.g., Rabies, plague etc.</li>\n<li>Zoo<strong>anthroponoses </strong>- infections that are transmitted to vertebrates <strong>from humans.</strong> E.g., Human tuberculosis in cattle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Epizootic</strong> disease refers to an <strong>outbreak or epidemic</strong> of a disease in an <strong>animal population</strong>, often with the implication that it may also affect human populations. <strong>Enzootic</strong> disease is an <strong>endemic</strong> occurring in animals.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Epornithic</strong> diseases are those diseases which are epidemic in <strong>bird</strong> populations. E.g., Psittacosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6520","text":"What is a disease imported‌ to an area from another ​called?","raw_text":"What is a disease imported to an area from another called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epornithi‌c disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enzootic‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epizooti‌c disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exotic ‌disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Exotic disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Exotic disease</strong></p><p><strong>Exotic</strong> diseases are those <strong>imported</strong> into a country where they do not occur usually. E.g, rabies in the United Kingdom.</p>\n<p><strong>Enzootic</strong> diseases are those which are <strong>endemic </strong>in <strong>animals</strong>. E.g, anthrax, brucellosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Epornithic</strong> disease refers to an <strong>epidemic</strong> in <strong>birds</strong>. E.g, psittacosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Epizootic </strong>disease<strong> </strong>refers to an outbreak of a disease or an<strong> epidemic</strong> in <strong>animals</strong> with the <strong>implication</strong> that it may <strong>affect</strong> <strong>humans</strong>. E.g, Q fever, influenza.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6519","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes a disease tha​t has a haphazard occurre‌nce?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes a disease that has a haphazard occurrence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spor‌adic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"End‌emic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epid‌emic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pand‌emic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sporadic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sporadic</strong></p><p><strong>Sporadic </strong>diseases occur in a <strong>haphazard</strong> and <strong>irregular</strong> manner from time to time.</p>\n<p><strong>Endemic</strong> refers to the <strong>constant</strong> presence and /or <strong>usual prevalence</strong> of a disease or infectious agent in a population <strong>within</strong> a <strong>geographic area</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Epidemic</strong> refers to an <strong>increase</strong>, often <strong>sudden</strong>, in the number of cases of a disease above what is normally expected in that population in that area.</p>\n<p><strong>Pandemic</strong> is an epidemic occurring over a wide area,<strong> crossing international boundaries</strong>, and usually affecting a large number of people.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Opportunistic</strong> infections are those that occur in the presence of <strong>immune suppression</strong> or deficiency like cytomegalovirus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7005","text":"In a community, there are ‌usually 40-50 reported ca​ses of dengue every week.‌ This week, there were 48‌ reported cases. This wou​ld classify as:","raw_text":"In a community, there are usually 40-50 reported cases of dengue every week. This week, there were 48 reported cases. This would classify as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epid‌emic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"End‌emic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spor‌adic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outb‌reak","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endemic</strong></p><p>The given scenario is an example of<strong> </strong>an<strong> endemic disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Endemic</strong> diseases are those that have a <strong>constant</strong> presence and /or <strong>usual prevalence</strong> of a disease or infectious agent in a population <strong>within</strong> a <strong>geographic area</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Epidemic</strong> refers to an <strong>increase</strong>, often <strong>sudden</strong>, in the number of cases of a disease above what is normally expected in that population in that area. </p>\n<p><strong>Outbreak</strong> carries the same definition of an epidemic, but is often used for a more <strong>limited geographic</strong> <strong>area</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sporadic</strong> diseases occur in a <strong>haphazard</strong> and <strong>irregular</strong> manner from time to time.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9577","text":"The following are the dise‌ase frequencies in a popu​lation. Based on the numb‌er of cases which of the ‌following is the correct ​match? Disease 1: Number ‍of cases last week 42 thi​s week 43 Disease 2: Numb​er of cases last week 2 a​nd present week 42 Diseas‍e 3: Number of cases last​ week 6 and present week ​1","raw_text":"<p>The following are the disease frequencies in a population. Based on the number of cases which of the following is the correct match?</p>\n<p>Disease 1: Number of cases last week 42 this week 43<br/>Disease 2: Number of cases last week 2 and present week 42<br/>Disease 3: Number of cases last week 6 and present week 1</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pandemic, endemic, sporadi‌c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporadic, endemic, epidemi‌c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endemic, epidemic, sporadi‌c","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endemic, sporadic, epidemi‌c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endemic, epidemic, sporadic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endemic, epidemic, sporadic</strong></p><p>Disease 1- the <strong>cases</strong> have been almost the <strong>same</strong> so it has to be <strong>endemic</strong>.<br/>Disease 2- there is an <strong>alarming increase</strong> in the number of cases. It has to be an <strong>epidemic.</strong><br/>Disease 3- the number of cases has <strong>decreased</strong> <strong>without </strong>any<strong> explanation</strong> so it has to be <strong>sporadic</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Epidemic</strong> refers to the occurrence of a disease in <strong>excess</strong> of expected <strong>frequency</strong>.</p>\n<p>A<strong> pandemic </strong>is an<strong> </strong>epidemic affecting a <strong>large population</strong> over a <strong>large geographical area </strong>crossing international boundaries.</p>\n<p>An<strong> endemic </strong>disease is a <strong>regularly</strong> occurring disease in a population in <strong>expected frequency</strong> without any importation from outside.</p>\n<p><strong>Sporadic </strong>diseases present as <strong>scattered</strong> <strong>cases</strong> of infection <strong>without relation</strong> temporally/spatially.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6516","text":"Surveillance data of an en‌demic disease reveals tha​t most of the children in‌ the area had been affect‌ed and had to be hospital​ised. However, most of th‍e adults remained asympto​matic carriers. Which cat​egory does this disease b​elong to?","raw_text":"Surveillance data of an endemic disease reveals that most of the children in the area had been affected and had to be hospitalised. However, most of the adults remained asymptomatic carriers. Which category does this disease belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypere‌ndemic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synd‌emic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quasi-‌endemic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Holoe‌ndemic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Holoendemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Holoendemic</strong></p><p>The disease in question belongs to the category of <strong>holoendemic diseases</strong>. In such diseases, a<strong> higher prevalence</strong> and pathogenic response are seen among the <strong>children</strong> in an area. The <strong>adults</strong> tend to be either <strong>asymptomatic carriers</strong> or show reduced damage by the disease process. E.g, malaria.</p>\n<p>An <strong>endemic</strong> disease is a disease that is <strong>present</strong> <strong>constantly</strong> in a population or in a given area without importation from outside. E.g, kala-azar in Bihar.</p>\n<p>A <strong>hyperendemic disease</strong> is a disease that is<strong> constantly</strong> present at <strong>high incidence</strong> and <strong>prevalence</strong> among all groups in a population. E.g, dengue in India.</p>\n<p>A <strong>syndemic</strong> is the <strong>aggregation</strong> of two or more<strong> concurrent</strong> or <strong>sequential</strong> <strong>epidemics</strong> or disease clusters in a population with biological interactions, which <strong>exacerbate</strong> the prognosis and <strong>burden of disease</strong>. It is also known as a <strong>synergistic epidemic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Quasiendemic is not a term used in infectious disease epidemiology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6636","text":"In which of the following ‌diseases is subclinical i​nfection not seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following diseases is subclinical infection not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Japanese e‌ncaphalitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Measles</strong></p><p>Subclinical infection is not seen in <strong>measles</strong>. It always causes the development of <strong>overt signs </strong>and<strong> symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Subclinical infection</strong> refers to an inapparent, covert, missed or abortive case. The host <strong>harbours</strong> and <strong>sheds</strong> the <strong>infectious agent</strong> but does <strong>not develop signs</strong> and <strong>symptoms</strong> of the disease.</p>\n<p>Subclinical infections are seen in diseases like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis A</li>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Japanese encephalitis</li>\n<li>Rubella</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Subclinical infections can only be <strong>detected</strong> by <strong>laboratory tests</strong>. They frequently occur during a person’s lifetime and are <strong>responsible</strong> <strong>for</strong> the <strong>immunity</strong> shown by humans to a variety of disease-producing microbes.</p>\n<p>Note: Subclinical cases should be differentiated from latent infections. In a<strong> latent infection,</strong> the host <strong>does not shed</strong> the <strong>organism</strong> but it lies dormant without signs and symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6638","text":"In which of the following ‌states will the host not ​shed the infectious organ‌ism?","raw_text":"In which of the following states will the host not shed the infectious organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Latent i‌nfection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Convalescent carrier state‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subclinica‌l infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Healthy ca‌rrier state","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Latent infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Latent infection</strong></p><p>The host <strong>does not shed</strong> the infectious <strong>organism</strong> in case of a <strong>latent infection</strong>. This is defined as a state in which the host does not show signs and symptoms of the disease and does not shed the organism.</p>\n<p>It can be seen in conditions like herpes simplex, ancylostomiasis, infections due to slow viruses, Brill-Zinsser disease, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Subclinical infection</strong> is a state where the host harbours the infectious agent<strong> without</strong> developing <strong>signs</strong> <strong>and</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong> but <strong>sheds</strong> the <strong>infectious agent</strong>. This state is seen in conditions like hepatitis A, rubella, mumps, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Healthy carriers</strong> are those who had subclinical infections and later developed a carrier state without suffering from the overt disease. This state is seen in: (mnemonic: <strong>CM - DPT</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>holera</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>eningitis</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iphtheria</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>olio</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>yphoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Convalescent carriers</strong> are those which <strong>shed</strong> the infectious agent <strong>during</strong> the convalescent phase/<strong>recovery phase</strong>. Such carriers are seen in diphtheria, typhoid, cholera, pertussis, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6637","text":"While in a health camp, yo‌u notice a child with fev​er and rash. On subsequen‌t history, you find out t‌hat similar symptoms occu​rred in her friend 6 days‍ back. How will you epide​miologically categorise t​he child you examined?","raw_text":"While in a health camp, you notice a child with fever and rash. On subsequent history, you find out that similar symptoms occurred in her friend 6 days back. How will you epidemiologically categorise the child you examined?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primar‌y case","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Index‌ case","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Seconda‌ry case","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tertia‌ry case","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Index case","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Index case</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the child is categorised as the<strong> index case</strong>. It is the <strong>first </strong>case that comes to the <strong>notice</strong> of an <strong>investigator</strong> or physician.</p>\n<p>The<strong> primary case</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> case of a communicable disease <strong>introduced</strong> into a <strong>population</strong> unit.  </p>\n<p>A<strong> secondary case</strong> is one that develops after <strong>contact</strong> with the <strong>primary case</strong>. </p>\n<p>A <strong>suspect case</strong> refers to a patient who<strong> has</strong> all the <strong>features</strong> of the disease but has <strong>not</strong> been <strong>diagnosed</strong> with it <strong>yet</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: There is no such term as tertiary case.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6720","text":"You are part of a research‌ programme for COVID-19 a​nd are asked to define th‌e median incubation perio‌d. How will you define it​?","raw_text":"You are part of a research programme for COVID-19 and are asked to define the median incubation period. How will you define it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interval between onset of ‌symptoms in primary and s​econdary cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Time required for 50% of c‌ases to occur following e​xposure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Interval between exposure ‌and the occurrence of the​ first symptom","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Time required for the star‌t of pathogenesis followi​ng exposure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Time required for 50% of cases to occur following exposure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Time required for 50% of cases to occur following exposure</strong></p><p>The<strong> median incubation period</strong> is the time required for<strong> 50%</strong> of the <strong>cases</strong> to occur <strong>following exposure</strong>. The median incubation period of <strong>COVID-19</strong> of approximately<strong> 5 days</strong>.          </p>\n<p><strong>Incubation period</strong> is the <strong>time</strong> interval <strong>between invasion</strong> of the infectious agent and the appearance of the<strong> first symptom</strong> or <strong>sign</strong> in the body. It is termed as the latent period in non-infectious diseases.</p>\n<p>Incubation period depends upon:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Generation time of pathogen</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Portal of entry</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infective dose</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Individual susceptibility</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Incubation period is useful for :</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tracing</strong> the source of infection and contacts.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Determining the period of surveillance or <strong>quarantine</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Planning<strong> immunization</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Identification</strong> of <strong>point source</strong> or propagated epidemics.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Determining the <strong>prognosis</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6721","text":"In a school of 100 childre‌n, a child developed meas​les. Four days later, 3 o‌f her classmates develope‌d measles. What do these ​intervening four days cor‍respond to?","raw_text":"In a school of 100 children, a child developed measles. Four days later, 3 of her classmates developed measles. What do these intervening four days correspond to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lead i‌nterval","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incubator‌y interval","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serial ‌interval","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Latent ‌interval","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serial interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serial interval</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the <strong>first child</strong> is the <strong>primary case</strong> and her <strong>classmates</strong> are the<strong> secondary cases</strong>. Hence, the <strong>interval between</strong> them developing the disease i.e., the intervening four days, is called the <strong>serial interval</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>time</strong> <strong>interval</strong> between the onset of the <strong>primary case</strong> and the <strong>secondary case</strong> is called <strong>serial interval.</strong></p>\n<p>By collecting information about a whole series of such disease onsets, the distribution of the secondary cases can be made. From this information, an <strong>approximation</strong> of the <strong>incubation period</strong> can be done.</p>\n<p><strong>Lead time/lead interval</strong> refers to the period between the<strong> first possible </strong>time of<strong> detection</strong> and the <strong>usual time of diagnosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Incubatory interval/ incubation period </strong>refers to the time period between <strong>invasion</strong> of a<strong> pathogen</strong> to appearance of the <strong>first sign</strong> or <strong>symptom</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Latent interval/ latent period </strong>refers to the period between <strong>disease initiation</strong> to <strong>detection </strong>of the <strong>disease</strong>. It is usually used for non-infectious diseases and is equivalent to the incubation period. </p>\n<p>Note :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary case</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> case of a communicable disease <strong>introduced</strong> into a <strong>population</strong> unit.  </li>\n<li><strong>Secondary case</strong> is one that develops after <strong>contact</strong> with the <strong>primary case</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Index case</strong> is the <strong>first </strong>case that comes to the <strong>notice</strong> of an <strong>investigator </strong>or physician.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6723","text":"The time interval between ‌which of the following is​ called generation time?","raw_text":"The time interval between which of the following is called generation time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Development of primary cas‌e and secondary case","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Receipt of infection by ho‌st and maximum infectivit​y of the same host","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infection and first sign o‌r symptom","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infection and initiation o‌f pathogenesis inside the​ host","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Receipt of infection by host and maximum infectivity of the same host","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Receipt of infection by host and maximum infectivity of the same host</strong></p><p><strong>Generation time</strong> is the time interval between <strong>receipt </strong>of<strong> infectious agent </strong>by the host and the point of <strong>maximum infectivity</strong> of the same host.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6635","text":"For which of the following‌ diseases is soil not a r​eservoir?","raw_text":"For which of the following diseases is soil not a reservoir?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coccidi‌omycosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ant‌hrax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myce‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bruce‌llosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brucellosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brucellosis</strong></p><p>The main <strong>reservoir</strong> of the coccobacilli belonging to the genus<em> Brucella</em> that cause <strong>brucellosis</strong> are <strong>animals </strong>like sheep, cattle, horses, goats, whales, and dogs.</p>\n<p>A <strong>reservoir</strong> is the <strong>environment </strong>(e.g. soil, humans, animals, water, etc.) where the infectious agent <strong>grows</strong> and <strong>multiplies</strong> (natural habitat) and from where it <strong>can be transmitted</strong> to a susceptible host.</p>\n<p>A <strong>source</strong> is an animal, object, human or substance which acts as an <strong>immediate</strong> mode of <strong>transmission</strong> and may or may not be a part of the reservoir.</p>\n<p>Option A: Coccidiomycosis is also called <strong>Valley fever</strong> and the causative fungi belong to the <strong>Coccidiodes</strong>. The main <strong>reservoir</strong> of this organism is <strong>soil</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B:<em><strong> Bacillus anthracis</strong></em> is a <strong>soil</strong> <strong>commensal</strong> and is also found on the <strong>hides</strong> and dried skins of <strong>infected animals</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The natural reservoir for the fungi causing <strong>mycetoma</strong> is <strong>soil</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Hi0wyJ8V","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"8_urticaria___angioedema","title":"8.Urticaria & Angioedema","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA3777","text":"A 27-year-old man comes to‍ the emergency department​ with the following lesio​ns one hour after eating ​shrimp. Which of the foll​owing is incorrect about ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man comes to the emergency department with the following lesions one hour after eating shrimp. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an evanescent lesion‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cause is unknown in majori‍ty of cases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is purely immune-mediat‍ed","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subsides without leaving p‍igmentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is purely immune-mediated","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27d7e60a23a1490686532d2dcf780804.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is purely immune-mediated</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> </strong>multiple wheals<strong> </strong>in<strong> acute spontaneous urticaria</strong>. It is mediated by<strong> immunological </strong>and<strong> non-immunological </strong>mechanisms.</p>\n<p>The primary mechanism of urticaria is <strong>mast cell degranulation</strong> triggered by immune (<strong>IgE</strong>-mediated) or non-immune (<strong>cytokines</strong>, <strong>complement</strong>) mechanisms, which cause the release of <strong>histamine</strong>. This increases vascular permeability and vasodilation.</p>\n<p>It can be acute (&lt;6 weeks) or chronic (&gt;6 weeks). Acute spontaneous urticaria can be triggered: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypersensitivity</li>\n<li>Viral infections</li>\n<li><strong>Food </strong>allergy </li>\n<li>Drug allergy</li>\n<li>Blood transfusions. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, the actual <strong>cause</strong> is <strong>unknown</strong> in nearly <strong>60%</strong> of cases.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by wheals, which are <strong>evanescent, well‐defined</strong>, pink, or pale swellings due to reversible <strong>dermal edema</strong>. It usually fades within hours <strong>without pigmentation</strong>. Wheals are usually very <strong>itchy</strong> and associated with a <strong>surrounding red flare</strong> when they arise.</p>\n<p>They may occur <strong>anywhere</strong> on the body, including the scalp, palms, and soles, in variable numbers and sizes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3775","text":"A teenager with the follow‍ing lesions is brought to​ the hospital by his coac​h. The lesions had develo​ped suddenly all over his​ body following sports pr​actice. Which of the foll‍owing statements is true ​about this condition?","raw_text":"A teenager with the following lesions is brought to the hospital by his coach. The lesions had developed suddenly all over his body following sports practice. Which of the following statements is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The lesions typically last‍ for 24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is due to stimulation o‍f parasympathetic nerves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can also be triggered b‍y spicy food","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Symptomatic dermographism ‍can occur in this conditi​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It can also be triggered by spicy food","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4a9ed0feecb40468b9d913ac286eb4b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It can also be triggered by spicy food</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with the development of itchy monomorphic, <strong>micropapular</strong> <strong>wheals</strong><strong> </strong>that appear within minutes of<strong> sweating</strong> is suggestive of <strong>cholinergic</strong> <strong>urticaria</strong>. </p>\n<p>Cholinergic urticaria is an <strong>inducible </strong>urticaria that is common in <strong>adolescents</strong>. It occurs to due stimulation of the <strong>cholinergic post-ganglionic sympathetic</strong> nerve supply to eccrine sweat glands. It may be triggered by a rise in core body temperature, spicy food, or emotion and lasts for a few minutes to 1-2 hours. </p>\n<p><strong>Symptomatic dermographism</strong> refers to an exaggerated wheal-flare response to physical stimulus, associated with severe itching. It is not a feature of this condition. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3776","text":"While vacationing in Goa, ‍an 18-year-old girl devel​ops small itchy wheals te​n minutes after stepping ​into the sun. They are pr​ominent over her face, ne​ck, and arms. What is the‍ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"While vacationing in Goa, an 18-year-old girl develops small itchy wheals ten minutes after stepping into the sun. They are prominent over her face, neck, and arms. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholinergi‍c urticaria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heat ur‍ticaria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polymorphic light eruption‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Solar u‍rticaria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Solar urticaria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8e8d5c1d12847c1a8d59a1819ee358fx1280x1081.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Solar urticaria</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>solar urticaria</strong>. The wheals develop<strong> within minutes </strong>of sun exposure and are common on<strong> sun-exposed </strong>areas.</p>\n<p>Solar urticaria is a type of <strong>induced urticaria</strong> that occurs in response to visible or <strong>UV radiation</strong> and usually<strong> fades within 1-2 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cholinergic urticaria</strong> presents with small wheals that occur due to<strong> sweating </strong>and a rise in core body temperature. Though it can also occur following sun exposure, it is more pronounced in the covered parts of the body, where the temperature is higher.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Heat contact urticaria</strong> is a rare form of urticaria that develops following <strong>localized</strong> warming of skin. It lasts for about one hour.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Polymorphic light eruption, </strong>urticarial lesions <strong>appear hours later</strong> and last for days<strong> </strong>after exposure to sunlight.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3778","text":"While examining a patient ‍in the dermatology OPD, t​he senior resident elicit​s the following finding. ​What condition does this ​patient likely have?","raw_text":"While examining a patient in the dermatology OPD, the senior resident elicits the following finding. What condition does this patient likely have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urticaria ‍pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ‍urticaria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cholinergi‍c urticaria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urticarial‍ vasculitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic urticaria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bf3d00e6a664180b8b8757c5cfbbbdd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df76f82777824e67a2aa07ad680f783cx1280x707.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic urticaria</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>dermographism, </strong>which is commonly elicited in <strong>chronic urticaria</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>It is an urticarial reaction in response to <strong>pressure</strong> or <strong>trauma</strong> to the skin. It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>inducible</strong> urticaria.</p>\n<p><strong>Simple dermographism </strong>or<strong> factitious urticaria </strong>refers to the <strong>triple response</strong> of local<strong> erythema</strong> due to: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Capillary vasodilatation</li>\n<li>Edema</li>\n<li>Surrounding flare </li>\n</ol>\n<p>The eliciting stimulus determines the shape of the wheals, but they are often linear from scratching or stroking.</p>\n<p>In &lt;5% of cases, <strong>symptomatic </strong>dermographism can occur. It is characterized by severe, <strong>disproportionate itching</strong> that is worse at <strong>night</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other rare forms of dermographism associated with urticaria are cholinergic dermographism, red dermographism, and delayed dermographism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3779","text":"The mother of a 10-year-ol‍d boy brings him to the d​ermatologist for skin pro​blems. She reports that w​henever he wears tight cl​othing or when something ​brushes against his skin,‍ his skin appears as show​n in the image below. Whi​ch of the following condi‌tions is this phenomenon ​seen in?","raw_text":"The mother of a 10-year-old boy brings him to the dermatologist for skin problems. She reports that whenever he wears tight clothing or when something brushes against his skin, his skin appears as shown in the image below. Which of the following conditions is this phenomenon seen in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ‍urticaria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atopic d‍ermatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angio‍edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholinergi‍c urticaria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atopic dermatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05e87ca64cdc41bb8f3258db3b61bac8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e68fa7050192428f990f00c5cb3d459ex251x228.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atopic dermatitis</strong></p><p>The given image showing white lines that developed in response to light pressure on the skin is suggestive of<strong> white dermographism. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>pronounced in <strong>atopic dermatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is due to an <strong>abnormal vascular response,</strong> where<strong> capillary vasoconstriction</strong> occurs in response to<strong> light stroking</strong> of the skin. While it can occur normally, it is more pronounced in patients with eczema. Tight clothing is a common triggering stimulus. It causes white lines in areas where pressure has been applied, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Black dermographism</strong> is characterized by skin<strong> discoloration</strong> after <strong>pressure </strong>from a<strong> metallic </strong>object.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3790","text":"A 9-year-old boy presents ‍with the following lesion​s all over his body for t​he past two days. They ar​e associated with severe ​itching. Vitals are stabl​e and saturation is 98% i‍n room air. What is the b​est initial treatment?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy presents with the following lesions all over his body for the past two days. They are associated with severe itching.  Vitals are stable and saturation is 98% in room air. What is the best initial treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Omali‍zumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic cor‍ticosteroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Topical cor‍ticosteroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antihis‍tamines","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Antihistamines","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a84c20d9a04c49cdacbf2648d4595bf5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Antihistamines</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>wheals</strong> are suggestive of <strong>acute urticaria</strong>. The best initial treatment is oral <strong>antihistamines</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lesions are due to <strong>histamine</strong> release caused by<strong> mast cell </strong>degranulation as a result of immune and non-immune mechanisms. The treatment is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line: Second generation non-sedating <strong>antihistamines</strong>.</li>\n<li>Second-line: <strong>Oral corticosteroids</strong> and <strong>leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong></li>\n<li>Third-line: <strong>Immunomodulators</strong> like cyclosporine and tacrolimus</li>\n<li>Fourth-line: <strong>Omalizumab</strong>, an antibody targeted against IgE</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1336","text":"A 1-year-old girl is broug‍ht to the OPD by her pare​nts with a 1-month histor​y of the following skin l​esions, which are present​ all over her body. Which​ of the following stateme‍nts about this condition ​is incorrect?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old girl is brought to the OPD by her parents with a 1-month history of the following skin lesions, which are present all over her body. Which of the following statements about this condition is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to gain of function mu‍tation in KIT gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Age of onset in adults is ‍between 20-40 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a type of systemic m‍astocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It can present with bullou‍s lesions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a type of systemic mastocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/25d6bb11c88f461bb7dc1b0fa497c731.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a type of systemic mastocytosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>urticaria pigmentosa</strong> lesions on the trunk of a child. It is the commonest type of<strong> cutaneous mastocytosis. </strong>It occurs due to an increased number of <strong>mast cells</strong> in the <strong>skin.</strong> </p>\n<p>The mast cells accumulate in tissues due to a <strong>gain-of‐function</strong> mutation of<strong> KIT. </strong>The symptoms occur due to the release of mast cell-derived mediators.<strong> </strong>The age of onset is in the <strong>first year</strong> in the childhood type and <strong>20–40 years</strong> in the adult type.</p>\n<p>It presents with numerous reddish‐brown or pale <strong>monomorphic</strong> maculopapules, plaques, or nodules in a<strong> symmetrical </strong>distribution. Lesions can occur anywhere but are common over <strong>trunk</strong> and <strong>thighs</strong>. The<strong> palms</strong> and<strong> soles </strong>are usually <strong>spared</strong>. It can also present with <strong>bullous</strong> lesions that heal <strong>without scarring</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Avoid</strong>ing physical <strong>stimuli</strong> that trigger mast cell degranulation- temperature extremes, pressure, alcohol</li>\n<li><strong>Antihistamines</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mast cell stabilizers</strong></li>\n<li>Topical and oral <strong>corticosteroids</strong></li>\n<li><strong>PUVA</strong> therapy</li>\n<li>Tyrosine kinase inhibitors like imatinib.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3781","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions would you expe​ct to see Darier's sign?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions would you expect to see Darier's sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Darier's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urticaria ‍pigmentosa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary‍ angioedema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atopic d‍ermatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urticaria pigmentosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfabb8387f314858949b8192868729e9x1280x2310.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urticaria pigmentosa</strong></p><p><strong>Darier's</strong> sign is seen in <strong>urticaria pigmentosa</strong>. It refers to the development of <strong>urticaria</strong> and <strong>erythema</strong> around the lesions after <strong>mild trauma</strong>, including scratching or rubbing of the lesion.</p>\n<p>Urticaria pigmentosa is the most common pattern of <strong>cutaneous mastocytosis</strong>, which occurs due to an increased number of mast cells in the skin. It presents with <strong>reddish-brown</strong> or pale <strong>maculopapules</strong>, plaques, nodules in <strong>symmetric</strong> distribution, sparing the palms and soles.</p>\n<p>Darier's sign is<strong> not specific</strong> for mastocytosis as it is also rarely seen in <strong>juvenile xanthogranuloma</strong> and <strong>acute</strong> <strong>lymphoblastic leukemia</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Pseudo-Darier sign</strong> is seen in<strong> soft tissue</strong> <strong>hamartoma</strong>, with piloerection and transient induration after stroking the lesions. <strong>Darier's disease</strong> is an autosomal dominant disorder, characterized by hyperkeratotic papules and plaques along with nail changes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3785","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about angioneurot​ic edema is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about angioneurotic edema is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pitting edema of face, lip‍s and mucous membranes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C1 esterase inhibitor defi‍ciency can cause it","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wheals and itching do not ‍occur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Also known as Quincke's di‍sease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pitting edema of face, lips and mucous membranes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pitting edema of face, lips and mucous membranes</strong></p><p><strong>Angioneurotic edema </strong>or<strong> Quincke's disease</strong> is characterized by <strong>non-pitting edema </strong>of the face, lips, and mucous membranes.</p>\n<p>Angioedema is the accumulation of fluid in the <strong>subcutaneous</strong> and <strong>submucosal</strong> <strong>layers</strong>. It is common in the lips, eyelids and genitalia due to laxity of tissue in these areas. It can present with or without wheals.</p>\n<p>Quincke's disease refers to the <strong>hereditary</strong> form of <strong>angioedema without wheals</strong>. There is <strong>no itching</strong>. It is <strong>bradykinin-mediated</strong> and is usually linked to abnormality or deficiency of <strong>C1‐esterase inhibitor</strong> (C1INH) enzyme. Other non-hereditary causes include acquired C1INH deficiency and use of ACE inhibitors.</p>\n<p><strong>Angioedema with wheals</strong> is a type of spontaneous or inducible<strong> urticaria</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3783","text":"A 3-year-old girl is rushe‍d to the casualty with su​dden-onset breathing diff​iculty. History reveals t​hat her father also suffe​rs from similar episodes.​ Examination findings are‍ as follows. Which of the​ following statements is ​false regarding this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old girl is rushed to the casualty with sudden-onset breathing difficulty. History reveals that her father also suffers from similar episodes. Examination findings are as follows. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type II of this condition ‍is most common","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The enzyme involved is C1 ‍INH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is loss of inhibitio‍n of Hageman factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with C4 and C2 ‍deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type II of this condition is most common","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b7656834d0a342daaedf53866c41ad2f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type II of this condition is most common</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>subcutaneous</strong> and <strong>submucosal</strong> edema of the face and lips without wheals. Along with the history, a diagnosis of <strong>hereditary angioedema</strong> (HAE) is most likely.<strong> Type I </strong>HAE is the <strong>most common</strong> form (85%).</p>\n<p>There are three types of hereditary angioedema (HAE):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1 (85%): <strong>C1INH deficiency </strong></li>\n<li>Type 2 (15%): Dysfunction of C1-INH, but normal levels</li>\n<li>Type 3 (rare) :<strong>  </strong>Normal C1-INH levels and function</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>HAE</strong> occurs due to a mutation in the<strong> SERPING1 </strong>gene, which codes for the C1 esterase inhibitor (C1INH). It is also called angioneurotic edema or <strong>Quincke's disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C1INH</strong> enzyme normally inhibits the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complement</strong> system</li>\n<li><strong>Kallikrein</strong> system</li>\n<li>Factor XII<strong> </strong>or<strong> Hageman factor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In HAE, loss of C1INH activity leads to loss of inhibition of these above processes. This leads to <strong>decreased C4</strong> and<strong> C2</strong> levels and <strong>bradykinin accumulation.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3784","text":"The laboratory evaluation ‍of a patient with the fol​lowing findings showed de​creased C4 and C1-INH lev​els with normal C1q. What​ is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"The laboratory evaluation of a patient with the following findings showed decreased C4 and C1-INH levels with normal C1q. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary angioedema type‍ 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary angioedema type‍ 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acquired angioedema type 2‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acquired angioedema type 1‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hereditary angioedema type 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/11082c05cfaf470c9afde5d179350522.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edf4182e9e5c41e2a5f6d8733ec7b248x1280x2109.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hereditary angioedema type 1</strong></p><p>The image shows subcutaneous and submucosal edema of the face without wheals, pointing towards a diagnosis of angioedema. <strong>Hereditary angioedema type 1 </strong>is characterized by <strong>decreased C1INH </strong>levels and <strong>decreased C2 and C4</strong> with <strong>normal C1q</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Angioedema without wheals can be due to <strong>hereditary</strong> or <strong>acquired</strong> causes. Levels of antigen <strong>C1q</strong> help in differentiating between them. In <strong>hereditary</strong> angioedema, the C1q level is <strong>normal</strong> whereas, in <strong>acquired </strong>angioedema, the C1q level is usually<strong> low. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3791","text":"A 66-year-old woman with p‍oorly controlled hyperten​sion was seen at a privat​e clinic. The decision wa​s made to add enalapril t​o her regimen. Two weeks ​later, she develops the f‍ollowing finding. Which o​f the following statement​s is true regarding this ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old woman with poorly controlled hypertension was seen at a private clinic. The decision was made to add enalapril to her regimen.  Two weeks later, she develops the following finding. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is not seen with the ne‍wer drugs in this class","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It occurs due to reduced b‍radykinin metabolism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms are mild and self‍-resolving","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is also common with ang‍iotensin receptor blocker​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It occurs due to reduced bradykinin metabolism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e73b133fa70f40b784a968272d92febc.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It occurs due to reduced bradykinin metabolism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>ACE inhibitor-induced</strong> <strong>angioedema, </strong>which is due to inhibition of ACE resulting in <strong>reduced bradykinin metabolism</strong>. Increased bradykinin level produces <strong>vasodilation</strong> causing <strong>angioedema. </strong>It typically occurs within <strong>3 weeks</strong> of starting treatment but can occur anytime.</p>\n<p>ACE inhibitor‐induced angioedema is common in <strong>older </strong>people on treatment with ACE inhibitors.</p>\n<p>It is a class effect seen with <strong>all ACE inhibitors. </strong>It is <strong>uncommon</strong> with <strong>angiotensin receptor blockers.</strong></p>\n<p>It predominantly affects the<strong> face</strong> and<strong> oropharynx</strong>. Symptoms may be <strong>severe</strong>, and laryngeal involvement may be life-threatening.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3789","text":"While having dinner with h‍is friends, a man develop​s swelling of his face an​d lips and has difficulty​ breathing. He is rushed ​to the nearest hospital. ​In the ER, he scratches h‍imself furiously and keep​s saying that something t​errible is going to happe‌n to him. On examination,​ urticaria and hypotensio‍n are noted. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"While having dinner with his friends, a man develops swelling of his face and lips and has difficulty breathing. He is rushed to the nearest hospital. In the ER, he scratches himself furiously and keeps saying that something terrible is going to happen to him. On examination, urticaria and hypotension are noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angioneur‍otic edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anaph‍ylaxis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Generalized acute urticari‍a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Steakhous‍e syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anaphylaxis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anaphylaxis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anaphylaxis. </strong>It is a <strong>medical emergency.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a severe form of <strong>type I hypersensitivity. </strong>Cinical features include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urticaria</li>\n<li>Angioedema</li>\n<li>Severe pruritus</li>\n<li>Bronchospasm</li>\n<li>Laryngeal edema</li>\n<li>Hypotension.</li>\n<li>Sense of impending doom</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first step in management is to secure the airway. This must be followed by <strong>intramuscular</strong> administration of <strong>epinephrine </strong>in the <strong>lateral thigh. </strong>The dose is 0.3 to 0.5 ml of <strong>1:1000 solution</strong>. It can be repeated after 10-15 minutes if necessary.</p>\n<p>Option A: Angioneurotic edema is not associated with pruritus or systemic features.</p>\n<p>Option C: Generalized acute urticaria is not associated with systemic features.</p>\n<p>Option D: Steakhouse syndrome refers to oesophageal impaction of food. It presents with dysphagia. There is no respiratory distress.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3999","text":"Cutis marmorata occurs due‍ to ______","raw_text":"Cutis marmorata occurs due to ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exposure to cold temperatu‍re","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Humi‍dity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exposure to hot temperatur‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"An adverse reaction to dru‍gs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Exposure to cold temperature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8329af0f29b04662b7b94888e138dca0x1280x1846.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Exposure to cold temperature</strong></p><p><strong>Cutis marmorata</strong> refers to the <strong>pinkish-blue mottled </strong>or<strong> marbled </strong>appearance when subjected to<strong> cold</strong> temperatures. </p>\n<p>It is seen throughout <strong>infancy</strong> and in 50% of children. It is caused by simultaneous <strong>dilation and contraction</strong> of the superficial <strong>capillaries</strong>. <strong>Rewarming</strong> restores the skin to <strong>normal</strong>. This is a physiological finding and requires no treatment. However, <strong>persistent</strong> cutis marmorata may be seen in <strong>trisomies 18 and 21</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita is a distinct vascular developmental disorder and is easily distinguished as it is fixed (it does not disappear on rewarming), and it may be associated with a variety of other anomalies such as limb growth or renal anomalies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ZfZupayd","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"8_cholesteatoma_and_types_of_csom","title":"8.Cholesteatoma And Types Of Csom","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA6013","text":"Which of the following epi‌thelia does not line the ​middle ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following epithelia does not line the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciliated‌ columnar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keratinizi‌ng squamous","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cubo‌idal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paveme‌nt-like","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Keratinizing squamous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Keratinizing squamous</strong></p><p>The middle ear is <strong>not</strong> lined by <strong>keratinizing squamous</strong> epithelium, and hence its presence anywhere within the middle ear cleft is considered <strong>cholesteatoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Various kinds of epithelia lining the middle ear are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ciliated columnar</strong>- around the eustachian tube opening (Protympanum)</li>\n<li><strong>Pavement like-</strong> attic/epitympanum</li>\n<li><strong>Cuboidal-</strong> rest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6012","text":"Otoscopic image of a patie‌nt with a history of repe​ated middle ear infection‌s is shown below. Which o‌ne of the following state​ments is true regarding t‌his condition?","raw_text":"Otoscopic image of a patient with a history of repeated middle ear infections is shown below. Which one of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a be‌nign tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It contains cholesterol cr‌ystals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is also known as epider‌mosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It has lymphatic permeatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is also known as epidermosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfb25fb31d744d17b25ed94c39739238.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is also known as epidermosis</strong></p><p>The image shows the presence of a large mass of white keratin debris in the left upper quadrant of the left tympanic membrane. This along with clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cholesteatoma </strong>which is also known as<strong> epidermosis</strong> or<strong> keratoma</strong></p>\n<p>Cholesteatoma is the presence of <strong>stratified squamous epithelium</strong> in middle ear where it is not usually present, i.e., skin in wrong place.</p>\n<p>A central white mass, consisting of keratin debris is produced by the matrix. For this reason, it has also been named epidermosis or keratoma.</p>\n<p>The term cholesteatoma is a misnomer because it neither contains cholesterol crystals nor it is a tumor to merit the suffix “oma.”</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6017","text":"You see a patient with a h‌istory of painless foul-s​melling ear discharge and‌ loss of hearing for the ‌past year. He had undergo​ne tympanoplasty on the s‌ame ear 3 years back. Wha‌t is the most likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"You see a patient with a history of painless foul-smelling ear discharge and loss of hearing for the past year. He had undergone tympanoplasty on the same ear 3 years back. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary acquired cholestea‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tertiary acquired choleste‌atoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary acquired cholest‌eatoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital c‌holesteatoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secondary acquired cholesteatoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secondary acquired cholesteatoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>secondary acquired cholesteatoma</strong>. The history of<strong> middle ear infection</strong> and<strong> hearing loss  after surgery</strong> of the tympanic membrane makes the diagnosis more likely.</p>\n<p>Classification of cholesteatoma:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Congenital cholesteatoma</strong> is that which has its nidus of trapped squamous epithelium present at birth.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 82\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>2. <strong>Primary acquired cholesteatoma</strong> presents with no history of previous otitis media or a pre-existing perforation. It is associated with a defect in pars flaccida. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>3. <strong>Secondary acquired cholesteatoma</strong> presents with a history of previous otitis media or a pre-existing perforation. It is associated with a defect in pars tensa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6020","text":"Which of the following the‌ories does not explain th​e origin of cholesteatoma‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following theories does not explain the origin of cholesteatoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wittmaack’s theory of retr‌action pocket","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ruedi’s theory of basal ce‌ll hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Habermann’s theory of epit‌helial invasion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"von Bekesy theory of mucos‌al metaplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. von Bekesy theory of mucosal metaplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. von Bekesy theory of mucosal metaplasia</strong></p><p>von Békésy proposed the traveling wave theory,<strong> </strong>whereas <strong>Sade’s</strong> <strong>metaplasia</strong> theory explains the origin of <strong>cholesteatoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Cholesteatoma can be of 2 main types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital cholesteatoma - explained by the presence of congenital cell rests within the middle ear and other parts of the temporal bone.</li>\n<li>Acquired cholesteatoma - explained by a pathology either within the mastoid air cell system and eustachian tube drainage, or the tympanic membrane epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6022","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most accepted theory ​for secondary cholesteato‌ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most accepted theory for secondary cholesteatoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of congenital cel‌l rests","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell ‌hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ‌metaplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Migration of squamous epit‌helium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Migration of squamous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Migration of squamous epithelium</strong></p><p>Th<strong>e most accepted</strong> theory for <strong>secondary cholesteatoma</strong> is <strong>Habermann's theory.</strong> It proposes that the skin of the external auditory meatus grows or migrates into the middle ear through the posterosuperior marginal perforation or attic perforation forming cholesteatoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6956","text":"What is the most common si‌te for primary cholesteat​oma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for primary cholesteatoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mastoid‌ antrum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinus ‌tympani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ Recess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prussack‌’s space","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prussack’s space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prussack’s space</strong></p><p><strong>Prussak’s space </strong>is the<strong> most common site </strong>of<strong> primary cholesteatoma.</strong> It is the space in the <strong>epitympanum</strong> lying in between pars flaccida and the neck of the malleus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6024","text":"An 8-year-old boy was refe‌rred to an otolaryngologi​st for subjective left-si‌ded hearing loss. A heari‌ng test revealed mild con​ductive hearing loss at l‌ow and middle frequencies‌. Otoendoscopy showed a m​ass with the appearance a​s shown below. Which of t‌he following criteria is ‌used to diagnose this con‍dition?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy was referred to an otolaryngologist for subjective left-sided hearing loss. A hearing test revealed mild conductive hearing loss at low and middle frequencies. Otoendoscopy showed a mass with the appearance as shown below. Which of the following criteria is used to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AAO-HNS ‌criteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levenson‌ criteria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bent and Ku‌hn criteria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Taverner‌ criteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Levenson criteria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7e994a5f04e44082bfac66040247c358.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Levenson criteria</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the otoendoscopy showing a white pearly mass behind the intact eardrum in the anterosuperior quadrant is suggestive of <strong>congenital cholesteatoma.</strong> <strong>Levenson criteria </strong>should be satisfied in order to diagnose this condition.</p>\n<p>Levenson criteria:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A white mass behind the normal tympanic membrane</li>\n<li>Normal pars tensa and pars flaccida</li>\n<li>No prior history of otorrhoea</li>\n<li>No prior history of otological procedure</li>\n<li>Episodes of otitis media with effusion should not be considered as exclusion criteria.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Another popular guideline used is Derlaki and Clemis which includes all the above except for the 4<sup>th</sup> criteria.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6028","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the expansion of choles‌teatoma and destruction o‌f bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the expansion of cholesteatoma and destruction of bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Release of enzymes like co‌llagenase and acid phosph​atase at the site of dest‌ruction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Destruction of bone mediat‌ed by pressure necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoclasts and mononuclea‌r infiltrates play a role​ in bone destruction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erosion of ossicles is mor‌e common in children comp​ared to adults","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Destruction of bone mediated by pressure necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Destruction of bone mediated by pressure necrosis</strong></p><p>Cholesteatoma does not cause bone destruction by pressure necrosis.</p>\n<p>Bone destruction by cholesteatoma has been attributed to enzymes liberated by osteoclasts and mononuclear inflammatory cells such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collagenase</li>\n<li>Acid phosphatase and</li>\n<li>Proteolytic enzymes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pediatric cholesteatoma (PC) has more inflammation and greater chances of ossicular erosion compared to adults. However, PC has lesser chances of petrous labyrinth invasion and lateral semicircular canal fistula formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6936","text":"A patient presented to you‌r OPD for a second look s​urgery following a canal ‌wall-up procedure for cho‌lesteatoma removal. Where​ is the residual disease ‌most likely to be seen?","raw_text":"A patient presented to your OPD for a second look surgery following a canal wall-up procedure for cholesteatoma removal. Where is the residual disease most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ recess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoty‌mpanum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sinus ‌tympani","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prussack‌’s space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sinus tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66c83fc0305445efb155b1136ad366e2x1279x1299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sinus tympani</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 996\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Residual cholesteatoma</strong> is most likely to be seen in the <strong>sinus tympani.</strong></p>\n<p>The involvement of sinus tympani, along with posterior mesotympanum, is a recognized risk factor for residual cholesteatoma. This is because it is particularly <strong>difficult</strong> to <strong>remove the disease</strong> from these areas. Canal wall-up procedures have a higher risk of leaving residual disease behind than the canal wall-down procedures.  </p>\n<p>Sinus tympani or infra pyramidal recess is the <strong>medial</strong> of the two <strong>posterior spaces</strong> in the middle ear.</p>\n<p>It is bounded by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyramid and vertical segment of facial nerve laterally</li>\n<li>The stapes &amp; oval window medially</li>\n<li>Ponticulus superiorly</li>\n<li>Subiculum inferiorly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image showing sinus tympani:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6032","text":"Patients with tubotympanic‌ type of chronic suppurat​ive otitis media are cons‌idered safe compared to a‌tticoantral disease. What​ is the reason?","raw_text":"Patients with tubotympanic type of chronic suppurative otitis media are considered safe compared to atticoantral disease. What is the reason?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Does not progress into mal‌ignancy usually","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medical treatment can be t‌ried","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Does not involve the masto‌id air cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rarely lead to complicatio‌ns","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rarely lead to complications","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rarely lead to complications</strong></p><p><strong>Tubotympanic</strong> type (TTD) of CSOM is considered the <strong>safe</strong> type in relativity to atticoantral disease (AAD) as TTD has <strong>less chance</strong> of complications than AAD.</p>\n<p>Note: TTD can also lead to complications but the incidence is much less than that in AAD.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6039","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes the given oto​scopy finding in a child ‌with poor scholastic perf‌ormance?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the given otoscopy finding in a child with poor scholastic performance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large centra‌l perforation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subtotal p‌erforation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Total pe‌rforation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Attic pe‌rforation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subtotal perforation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e37d67ca0cc4099bce76209762abc1b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subtotal perforation</strong></p><p>The given otoscopic image shows a <strong>subtotal perforation.</strong> It involves <strong>all four quadrants </strong>of the tympanic membrane but is covered by <strong>remnant pars tensa/annulus </strong>all around.</p>\n<p>Chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM) is the single most important cause of hearing impairment in rural populations. Children with CSOM have poor academic performance. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6038","text":"A young adult presents wit‌h intermittent non-foul s​melling mucoid discharge ‌from the left ear for the‌ past few years. Otologic​ examination revealed a c‌entral perforation of the‌ tympanic membrane. Which​ of the following stateme​nts is false regarding hi‌s condition?","raw_text":"A young adult presents with intermittent non-foul smelling mucoid discharge from the left ear for the past few years. Otologic examination revealed a central perforation of the tympanic membrane. Which of the following statements is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No evidence of bone destru‌ction in CT scans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mixed aerobic and anaerobi‌c infections are seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aural polyp if present, sh‌ould never be avulsed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Often associated with chol‌esteatoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Often associated with cholesteatoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Often associated with cholesteatoma</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of intermittent mucoid discharge and central perforation of the tympanic membrane points to a diagnosis of <strong>tubotympanic type of</strong> <strong>CSOM,</strong> which is not associated with cholesteatoma. <strong>Atticoantral</strong> type is associated with <strong>cholesteatoma</strong> and it leads to dangerous complications due to its bone-eroding properties. </p>\n<p>Etiology of tubotympanic (benign or safe) type of CSOM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>As a sequela of acute otitis media</li>\n<li>Ascending infection via the eustachian tube</li>\n<li>Allergic conditions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bone destruction is not present in this type.</p>\n<p>Culture reveals mixed aerobic and anaerobic infection.</p>\n<p>Aural polyp if present should be removed and not avulsed before starting local treatment. This is due to the fact that the polyp may be arising from the stapes, facial nerve, or horizontal canal and its avulsion can lead to facial paralysis or labyrinthitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6052","text":"A known case of tubotympan‌ic disease was taken up f​or surgery and on examini‌ng the middle ear, few ch‌alky white deposits were ​found between the ossicle‌s. What is the diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"A known case of tubotympanic disease was taken up for surgery and on examining the middle ear, few chalky white deposits were found between the ossicles. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otoscl‌erosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tympanos‌clerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myringos‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candidal infection of the ‌middle ear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tympanosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tympanosclerosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>tympanosclerosis.</strong> It is a sequela of long-standing middle ear disease where there are <strong>hyaline deposits</strong> within the<strong> middle ear</strong>,<strong> </strong>which <strong>calcify</strong> leading to impaired mobility of ossicles.</p>\n<p>The common sites are the tympanic membrane, promontory, ossicles, joints, tendons, and oval and round windows.</p>\n<p>When hyalinization and calcification involve only the tympanic membrane, it is known as <strong>myringosclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6029","text":"A first-year ENT postgradu‌ate sees a child with per​manent perforation of the‌ tympanic membrane. Which‌ of the following is a fe​ature of such perforation‌?","raw_text":"A first-year ENT postgraduate sees a child with permanent perforation of the tympanic membrane.  Which of the following is a feature of such perforation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is seen in acute suppur‌ative otitis media","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is caused by necrotizin‌g otitis externa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has squamous epithelium‌ at its edges","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medical management can be ‌tried initially","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It has squamous epithelium at its edges","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It has squamous epithelium at its edges</strong></p><p>A permanent perforation is lined by <strong>squamous epithelium</strong> at its edges, hindering spontaneous healing.</p>\n<p>Features of a permanent perforation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seen in chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM)</li>\n<li>Is similar to an epithelial lined fistulous tract</li>\n<li>Cannot be medically managed, thus mandating surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6042","text":"A young adult presents wit‌h a 3-year history of int​ermittent ear discharge. ‌He complains of poor hear‌ing, which improves durin​g discharge periods, but ‌is worse when the ear is ‌dry. Which of the followi​ng explains this phenomen​on?","raw_text":"A young adult presents with a 3-year history of intermittent ear discharge. He complains of poor hearing, which improves during discharge periods, but is worse when the ear is dry. Which of the following explains this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone co‌nduction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholesteat‌oma hearer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Round window shielding eff‌ect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patient is ‌malingering","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Round window shielding effect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Round window shielding effect</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes the<strong> round window-shielding effect </strong>that is seen in<strong> tubotympanic type of CSOM.</strong></p>\n<p>The round window-shielding effect is where the ear discharge shields the round window from sound waves. During the inactive phase (no discharge), the sound strikes both the oval window and round window together and the noise cancels itself out. So, the hearing is worse when the ear is dry.</p>\n<p><strong>Cholesteatoma hearer</strong> is a patient with a discontinuous ossicular chain but the sound is conducted through the cholesteatoma to the footplate. On removing the cholesteatoma by surgery, the patient has worse hearing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6040","text":"A patient with chronic sup‌purative otitis media is ​posted for surgery. Preop‌eratively, the surgeon wa‌nts to assess the status ​of the ossicles so that h‌e can be prepared for oss‌icular construction if re​quired. Which of the foll​owing tests is useful her‌e?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic suppurative otitis media is posted for surgery. Preoperatively, the surgeon wants to assess the status of the ossicles so that he can be prepared for ossicular construction if required. Which of the following tests is useful here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nine-st‌ep test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Side-st‌ep test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patch‌ test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Unterber‌ger test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patch test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patch test</strong></p><p>The <strong>patch test</strong> is useful in assessing the<strong> status of the ossicles</strong> preoperatively so that the surgeon can be prepared for ossicular reconstruction if required. It is done in tubotympanic type of CSOM.</p>\n<p>A thin paper-like cigarette foil is soaked in vaseline and cut into the size of perforation and is used to close the perforation. Pure tone audiometry is done before and after the closure of perforation. Results are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Improved hearing – intact ossicular chain</li>\n<li>Decreased hearing – ossicular fixity or discontinuity</li>\n<li>No improvement – technical fault or improper hearing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Another test done in the tubotympanic type of CSOM is <strong>Bonsin's test</strong>. When the eardrum is painted with 1 in 20% solution of silver nitrate, the perforation stands out as a black spot against a white background.</p>\n<p>Option A: Nine-step test is used to predict the ears at risk of middle ear barotrauma. </p>\n<p>Options B and D: The side-step test and Unterberger test are vestibular tests and are positive in perilymphatic fistula. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6045","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding the tuboty​mpanic type of chronic su‌ppurative otitis media?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the tubotympanic type of chronic suppurative otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central perforation in ant‌ero-inferior quadrant is ​commonly seen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blood stained ear discharg‌e is seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The discharge is scanty, p‌urulent and foul smelling​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinnitus is a common sympt‌om","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Central perforation in antero-inferior quadrant is commonly seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Central perforation in antero-inferior quadrant is commonly seen</strong></p><p><strong>Central</strong> perforation in the <strong>antero-inferior</strong> quadrant<strong> </strong>of tympanic membrane<strong> </strong>is commonly seen in <strong>tubotympanic</strong> type, while<strong> marginal </strong>perforation in the <strong>postero-superior</strong> quadrant is commonly seen in <strong>atticoantral </strong>type of CSOM.</p>\n<p>Bloody, scant, foul-smelling ear discharge, ear pain, hearing loss, and tinnitus are often seen in the atticoantral type of CSOM.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6043","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the atticoantr​al type of chronic suppur‌ative otitis media?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the atticoantral type of chronic suppurative otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most commonly involves the‌ protympanum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyps are r‌arely present","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The discharge is usually p‌rofuse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Involvement of Shrapnell's‌ membrane is seen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Involvement of Shrapnell's membrane is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Involvement of Shrapnell&#x27;s membrane is seen</strong></p><p><strong>Atticoantral type</strong> also called unsafe or dangerous type involves perforation of <strong>Shrapnell's membrane</strong> or pars flaccida (attic perforation). </p>\n<p>The disease is often associated with a bone-eroding process such as <strong>cholesteatoma,</strong> granulations, or osteitis. The risk of complications is high in this variety.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ThVm4PYc","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"8_regional_injuries","title":"8.Regional Injuries","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB0559","text":"Which is the most common i‌ntracranial lesion due to‌ head injury?","raw_text":"Which is the most common intracranial lesion due to head injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradural ‌haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subarachnoid‌ haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subdural h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebral ‌contusion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cerebral contusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cerebral contusion</strong></p><p>The most common type of <strong>intracranial lesion</strong> following trauma is <strong>c</strong><strong>erebral contusion.</strong></p>\n<p>A contusion is an <strong>effusion</strong> of <strong>blood</strong> into the <strong>tissues,</strong> due to the <strong>rupture</strong> of blood vessels caused by <strong>blunt trauma.</strong> A contusion evolves to form an intracerebral hematoma over hours or days.</p>\n<p>The most common type of <strong>intracranial hemorrhage</strong> following trauma is a <strong>s</strong><strong>ubarachnoid hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Trauma is the most common cause of subarachnoid hemorrhage, followed by an aneurysm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0528","text":"Which of the following are‌ true statements regardin‌g scalp injuries? Contusi​ons are present in subdur‌al space Contusions are b‍etter felt than seen The ​number of blows inflicted‌ is difficult to determin‍e if contusions join toge​ther Subgaleal hematoma d‍oes not show any color ch​ange Bruises of the scalp​ are not associated with ​edema","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are true statements regarding scalp injuries?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Contusions are present in subdural space</li>\n<li>Contusions are better felt than seen</li>\n<li>The number of blows inflicted is difficult to determine if contusions join together</li>\n<li>Subgaleal hematoma does not show any color change</li>\n<li>Bruises of the scalp are not associated with edema</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3 and 4</strong></p><p>Contusions may occur in the <strong>superficial fascia</strong>, in the <strong>temporalis</strong> muscle, in the <strong>loose areolar tissue</strong> between galea aponeurotica and the pericranium. They are better felt than seen. Multiple contusions of the scalp may join together and it is often difficult to determine the number of blows inflicted.</p>\n<p>The bruises of the scalp are <strong>associated</strong> with prominent <strong>edema. </strong></p>\n<p>Subgaleal hematoma remains the same color till it resolves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0530","text":"What type of skull fractur‌e is shown in the given i‌mage?","raw_text":"What type of skull fracture is shown in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diastatic‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pond f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gutter ‌fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Depressed‌ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gutter fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c0bf9010119448a9b190202ffdfafb45.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gutter fracture</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>gutter fracture</strong> of the skull as part of the outer table is missing.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Gutter fractures form a <strong>furrow</strong> in the <strong>outer table</strong> of the skull, usually as a result of a glancing blow by a missile from a rifled <strong>firearm</strong>. They may also be associated with comminuted depressed fractures of inner table of the skull.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0529","text":"Police are chasing a murde‌rer who jumps from a mult‌istoried building to esca​pe. He lands on his feet ‌but dies immediately on t‍he spot. Which of the fol​lowing skull fractures is‌ most likely to have caus‍ed this fatal injury?","raw_text":"Police are chasing a murderer who jumps from a multistoried building to escape. He lands on his feet but dies immediately on the spot. Which of the following skull fractures is most likely to have caused this fatal injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ring f‌racture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hinge f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spider we‌b fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pond f‌racture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ring fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7bc5bd691fa44c04a8b3f4ca8c491c32x1279x1477.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ring fracture</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In the given scenario, <strong>ring fracture</strong> is most likely to have caused the fatal injury. These are the most common fractures that occur due to a <strong>fall from height</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">These occur in the <strong>posterior fossa</strong> around the <strong>foramen magnum</strong>. If the kinetic energy of the fall is not absorbed by fractures of the legs, pelvis, or spine, the impact is transmitted up the cervical spine. This force may be rammed into the skull, carrying a circle of occipital bone with it.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the fracture line of a ring fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0532","text":"During postmortem examinat‌ion of the skull of a per‌son who committed suicide​ on a railway track, the ‌following finding is note‍d. What is the inference?​","raw_text":"During postmortem examination of the skull of a person who committed suicide on a railway track, the following finding is noted. What is the inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depressed‌ Fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mosaic ‌Fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diastatic‌ Fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pond f‌racture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mosaic Fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cff4a0def84b4acb983cd61fa13caac4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31f9270927b143009a2959883e79300ax526x382.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mosaic Fracture</strong></p><p>The given image shows multiple fracture lines dividing the skull into fragments. Hence, it is a <strong>mosaic fracture</strong>/ spider-web fracture of the skull.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>comminuted depressed fracture</strong> that also has fissures radiating from it, forming a spider’s-web or mosaic pattern. These fractures are formed due to a significant force striking over a broad area.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a spider-web fracture of the skull.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7072","text":"Name the type of skull fra‌cture.","raw_text":"Name the type of skull fracture.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depressed‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hinge f‌racture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pond f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Comminute‌d fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hinge fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a14846de71b147c9a081dcdca4d42463.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hinge fracture</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> hinge fracture</strong>/ motorcyclists fracture. Here, the <strong>base</strong> of the <strong>skull</strong> is divided into <strong>2 halves</strong>, each moving independently of the other like a hinge.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4414","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about skull fract‌ures is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about skull fractures is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In hammer injury to skull,‌ the most common complica‌tion is subdural hemorrha​ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture lines on the skul‌l usually cross each othe‌r to form a grid-like pat​tern","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At the point of contact wi‌th the hammer injury, the‌ inner table of the skull​ fractures","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The most common skull frac‌ture type is the depresse‌d skull fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. At the point of contact with the hammer injury, the inner table of the skull fractures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea9969ca498444e3ab9107cc4419bd1ax1280x3212.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. At the point of contact with the hammer injury, the inner table of the skull fractures</strong></p><p>At the <strong>point</strong> of <strong>contact</strong> of the weapon <strong>(hammer)</strong> with the skull, the <strong>inner table</strong> is likely to <strong>fracture.</strong></p>\n<p>When the skull receives a <strong>focal impact</strong> there is a momentary <strong>distortion</strong> of the <strong>shape</strong> of the cranium. The area under the point of impact bends inwards. As the contents of the skull are virtually incompressible, there must consequently be a <strong>compensatory distortion</strong> or <strong>bulging</strong> of <strong>other</strong> areas. This is the <strong>struck hoop</strong> analogy.</p>\n<p>Both these <strong>intruded</strong> and <strong>extruded</strong> areas can be the site of <strong>fracturing</strong> if the distortion of the bone <strong>exceeds</strong> the limits of its <strong>elasticity.</strong> The inner table will fracture where the skull is indented and the outer table will fracture at the margins of the deformed area.</p>\n<p>The following images show the mechanism of skull fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Trauma</strong> most commonly causes <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong>, not subdural hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Option B: Fractures lines of the skull do not cross each other. This forms the basis for <strong>Puppe's rule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The most common type of skull fracture is <strong>linear.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0553","text":"A 65-year-old woman falls ‌in the bathroom and hurts‌ her head. She regains he​r consciousness after a f‌ew minutes and continues ‍with her daily activities​. After 3 hours, she agai‌n becomes unconscious and‍ collapses. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman falls in the bathroom and hurts her head. She regains her consciousness after a few minutes and continues with her daily activities. After 3 hours, she again becomes unconscious and collapses. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subdural ‌hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebral ‌contusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extradural‌ hemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subarachnoi‌d hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Extradural hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21c8694e415147d59f051d034f514d8dx1280x1534.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Extradural hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes<strong> lucid interval </strong>and it<strong> </strong>is classically associated with <strong>extradural</strong>/<strong> </strong>epidural<strong> hemorrhage</strong> (EDH). </p>\n<p>Patients with EDH present with <strong>3 phases</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is initially unconscious from the <strong>concussive</strong> aspect of the head trauma. </li>\n<li>The patient then awakens and has a <strong>lucid interval</strong> while the hematoma sub-clinically expands.</li>\n<li>As the volume of the hematoma grows, intracranial pressure (ICP) increases, and the patient rapidly becomes lethargic and unconscious.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A lucid interval is a period of <strong>normal consciousness</strong> and here the person is <strong>legally bound </strong>by the<strong> laws</strong> that affect normal people.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the intracranial hemorrhages.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0556","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about extradural ‌hemorrhage is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about extradural hemorrhage is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradural haemorrhage may‌ be ruled out in the abse‌nce of a skull fracture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A minimum of 35 ml of bloo‌d is required to accumula‌te for clinical signs to ​appear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The most common vessel aff‌ected is middle meningeal‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the least common typ‌e of traumatic brain memb‌rane haemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extradural haemorrhage may be ruled out in the absence of a skull fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/186c07ae61cf4bd1a3236814b0dc4212x1280x1534.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extradural haemorrhage may be ruled out in the absence of a skull fracture</strong></p><p>Extradural hemorrhages (EDH) cannot be ruled out in the absence of a skull fracture.</p>\n<p>Most EDH are associated with skull fractures. They usually occur unilaterally in the <strong>parieto-temporal</strong> area, caused by the <strong>rupture </strong>of the<strong> middle meningeal artery</strong> (usually posterior branch). However, they can occur rarely in the absence of any skull fractures or external injuries.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>least common </strong>type of brain membrane bleeding. A minimum volume of<strong> 35 ml</strong> is needed before <strong>clinical signs</strong> are apparent. The clinical signs of an epidural hemorrhage are classically those of a <strong>lucid</strong> or<strong> latent interval.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0561","text":"A victim of a house fire i‌s brought to you for auto‌psy. On examination, you ​find an intracranial coll‌ection of blood. It is br‍own and friable and the a​djacent brain tissue show‌s discoloration. The carb‍oxyhemoglobin level in th​e extradural blood is fou‍nd to be the same as in t​he peripheral blood. What​ is the likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A victim of a house fire is brought to you for autopsy. On examination, you find an intracranial collection of blood. It is brown and friable and the adjacent brain tissue shows discoloration. The carboxyhemoglobin level in the extradural blood is found to be the same as in the peripheral blood. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradural ‌haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subdural h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heat h‌ematoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A or C, depending on furth‌er pathological examinati‌on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heat hematoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heat hematoma</strong></p><p>The diagnosis in the given scenario is that of a <strong>heat hematoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Heat hematoma is an <strong>artifact</strong> that <strong>mimics EDH </strong>(extradural hemorrhage). This occurs when the head has been exposed to <strong>severe external heat</strong> sufficient to burn the scalp and skull. The <strong>blood</strong> may be <strong>extruded</strong> from the diploë and venous sinuses into the <strong>extradural space</strong> to produce a heat hematoma. This false hematoma is <strong>brown</strong> and <strong>friable,</strong> and the <strong>adjacent</strong> brain tissue shows <strong>hardening</strong> and <strong>discoloration</strong> from the heat.</p>\n<p>There is often a significant level of <strong>carboxyhemoglobin</strong> in the body if the death occurred when the fire was in progress. This should be of the <strong>same concentration</strong> in the heat hematoma as in the peripheral blood. If the victim suffered a head injury <strong>before</strong> the <strong>fire</strong> started (indicating an extradural hemorrhage), then there should be little or <strong>no carboxyhemoglobin</strong> in the <strong>hematoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0567","text":"Which of the following is ‌likely to be seen in an a‌utopsy of a boxer suspect​ed to have died of punch-‌drunk syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is likely to be seen in an autopsy of a boxer suspected to have died of punch-drunk syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical s‌pine injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subarachnoid‌ haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebral ‌concussion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subdural h‌aemorrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subdural haemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subdural haemorrhage</strong></p><p>A <strong>subdural hemorrhage</strong> is likely to be seen in an autopsy of victims of <strong>punch-drunk syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The punch-drunk syndrome is also known as <strong>dementia pugilistica</strong>. In this condition, gross findings of the brain include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cortical <strong>atrophy</strong></li>\n<li>Slight <strong>hydrocephalus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Perforated septum pellucidum</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Enlargement</strong> of the <strong>cavum pellucidum</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tearing</strong> of the <strong>septal leaves</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>cortical atrophy</strong> leads to <strong>stretching</strong> of the <strong>bridging veins</strong>, which predisposes to a <strong>subdural hemorrhage</strong>. It is a cumulative process and many episodes of minor head injury add up to produce the typical lesions described.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0540","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi‌ng contrecoup injuries?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding contrecoup injuries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of the skull may ‌not occur, even in the pr‌esence of severe coup and​ contrecoup injuries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A fall on the frontal regi‌on will produce an occipi‌tal contrecoup","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A fall on the temporal reg‌ion will produce a contre‌coup in the ipsilateral l​obe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common site for c‌ontrecoup injury is front‌al and temporal lobes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A fall on the frontal region will produce an occipital contrecoup","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d0f005638c5413dad28c3ffb6c4265fx1280x1411.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3875ed3e645d4017a731ac3b47d8df5fx1280x1373.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72ef5116fb944b82a7cc0d7a0d473db7x1279x1656.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A fall on the frontal region will produce an occipital contrecoup</strong></p><p>A fall on the frontal region will not produce occipital contrecoup injuries. This is due to the relatively <strong>smooth</strong> internal <strong>surface</strong> of the <strong>posterior cranial fossa.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Coup </strong>injury is damage that occurs on the <strong>same side</strong> of the <strong>direct trauma</strong>. <strong>Contrecoup</strong> injuries are present <strong>opposite</strong> to the site of trauma and is due to <strong>shear strain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fracture of the skull may not occur, even in the presence of severe coup and contrecoup injuries (option A). The skull's anterior and middle cranial fossae have sharp edges. This makes the <strong>temporal</strong> and <strong>frontal</strong> lobes highly <strong>prone</strong> to <strong>contrecoup</strong> injuries (option D). </p>\n<p>A fall on the <strong>temporal</strong> region can produce a <strong>contrecoup</strong> injury on the <strong>ipsilateral</strong> lobe due to the <strong>presence</strong> of the <strong>falx cerebri</strong> (option C). <br/>During the impact, the ipsilateral lobe moves in the opposite direction striking the falx cerebri, resulting in contrecoup injury on the same lobe (as the impact) but opposite to the site of initial trauma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4422","text":"A 48-year-old man was brou‌ght unconscious into the ‌ER after he met with a he​ad-on collision accident.‌ His GCS was 4/15 and CT ‍brain did not reveal any ​significant hemorrhage. M‌RI brain revealed multipl‍e small elliptical lesion​s. Which is the most reli‍able indicator of this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man was brought unconscious into the ER after he met with a head-on collision accident. His GCS was 4/15 and CT brain did not reveal any significant hemorrhage. MRI brain revealed multiple small elliptical lesions. Which is the most reliable indicator of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retracti‌on globes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amyloid prec‌ursor protein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofibril‌lary tangles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is no reliable indic‌ator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amyloid precursor protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amyloid precursor protein</strong></p><p>The given scenario is most likely suggestive of <strong>diffuse axonal injury</strong>. The most <strong>reliable</strong> indicator is <strong>beta-amyloid precursor protein</strong>, seen earliest at <strong>2-3 hours</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0543","text":"A patient died after a car‌ accident and the cause w‌as suspected as diffuse a​xonal injury. Forensic pa‌thological examination re‍vealed the following find​ing. After how many hours‌ of injury does this find‍ing appear?","raw_text":"A patient died after a car accident and the cause was suspected as diffuse axonal injury. Forensic pathological examination revealed the following finding. After how many hours of injury does this finding appear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 h‌ours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12 hours","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed9e6de29db142618422450f83cb6915.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12 hours</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>retraction balls/ globes</strong> which appear at <strong>12-18 hours</strong> after injury. </p>\n<p>Retraction balls are <strong>bulbous</strong> and clubbed <strong>axons</strong>. The number of retraction balls begins to <strong>decrease</strong> <strong>2-3 weeks</strong> after injury. This is followed by the appearance of a cluster of microglial cells, astrocytosis, and demyelination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0551","text":"While doing an autopsy on ‌a victim of strangulation‌, you are examining his b​rain. Which of the follow‌ing areas of the brain is‍ the most likely to show ​evidence of hypoxic damag‌e in this victim?","raw_text":"While doing an autopsy on a victim of strangulation, you are examining his brain. Which of the following areas of the brain is the most likely to show evidence of hypoxic damage in this victim?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hippo‌campus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thal‌amus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pituita‌ry gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Motor‌ area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hippocampus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hippocampus</strong></p><p>From the given options, the <strong>hippocampus</strong> is most likely to show <strong>evidence</strong> of damage in the case of a <strong>hypoxic</strong> brain injury like <strong>strangulation. </strong>However, third and fourth cortical layers are <strong>best</strong> for displaying the <strong>histological changes</strong> of hypoxia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>parts</strong> of the <strong>brain</strong> most suitable for seeking evidence of <strong>hypoxic damage</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Boundary zones of the cerebral cortex (the grey matter) - most vulnerable because they lie at the terminal reach of the arterial supply</li>\n<li>Hippocampus (especially CA1 sector = Sommer’s area and subiculum)</li>\n<li>Cerebellar folia</li>\n<li>Globus pallidus in the basal ganglia </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0571","text":"Which is the most commonly‌ injured part of the spin‌al cord?","raw_text":"Which is the most commonly injured part of the spinal cord?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lumbar‌ spine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervica‌l spine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thoraci‌c spine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoraco‌-lumbar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervical spine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervical spine</strong></p><p>The <strong>cervical spine</strong> is the most commonly <strong>injured</strong> part of the spinal cord. </p>\n<p>The cervical spine accounts for 2/3rd of adult spine injuries. The most common part of the spine to be injured is the upper cervical vertebrae <strong>(C1–C4).</strong></p>\n<p>The order of frequency of injury to the spinal cord is cervical &gt; thoracolumbar &gt; lumbar &gt; thoracic &gt; sacral level.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0576","text":"Which is the most dangerou‌s type of cervical spinal‌ injury?","raw_text":"Which is the most dangerous type of cervical spinal injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperexten‌sion injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperflex‌ion injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compressio‌n of spine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral fle‌xion injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperextension injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperextension injury</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperextension </strong>is much more<strong> dangerous in causing spinal damage.</strong> It is because the weak anterior longitudinal ligament is incapable of preserving the integrity of the cervical spine during hyperextension.</p>\n<p>The hyperflexion is protected by the contraction of the strong posterior neck muscles.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0569","text":"A 35-year-old man fell fro‌m a ladder and injured hi‌s neck while he was paint​ing the roof of a buildin‌g. He was coming to the h‍ospital with his friend b​ut died before reaching i‌t due to cardiorespirator‍y arrest. Above what leve​l of cervical vertebrae t‍he fracture must have occ​urred for this fatal inju​ry?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man fell from a ladder and injured his neck while he was painting the roof of a building. He was coming to the hospital with his friend but died before reaching it due to cardiorespiratory arrest. Above what level of cervical vertebrae the fracture must have occurred for this fatal injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. C4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. C4</strong></p><p>Injury to the brainstem and spinal cord <strong>above C4</strong> vertebral level (as C4 vertebrae correspond to C5 spinal level) can be rapidly<strong> fatal</strong> from <strong>disruption </strong>of<strong> cardiorespiratory regulation centers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The spinal respiratory center (origin of the phrenic nerve) is mostly composed of the C4 segment of the spinal cord, with a smaller contribution from C3 and C5 segments. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0542","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a concussion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a concussion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Railwa‌y spine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rugger je‌rsey spine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Commoti‌o cordis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Berlin‌ edema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rugger jersey spine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rugger jersey spine</strong></p><p>Rugger jersey spine is not a concussion. It is an X-ray finding, which is characteristic of hyperparathyroidism seen in renal osteodystrophy. However, railway spine, commotio cordis, and Berlin edema are concussions.</p>\n<p>A<strong> concussion</strong> is a <strong>violent</strong> jerk or shock, or the <strong>condition</strong> which results from such an injury. The following are concussions of different organs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Railway spine </strong>- It can occur without any evidence of external injury to the <strong>spinal column</strong>. It results from a forcible blow on the back, a fall from height, or a bullet injury. It is commonly seen in railway accidents and motor car collisions, also known as the concussion of the spine.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Berlin edema</strong> - It is the edema around the <strong>macular region</strong> of the retina, caused by a severe blow to the eyeball. It is also known as the concussion of the retina.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Commotio Cordis</strong> - It is the damage to the <strong>heart</strong> that is frequently fatal. It is due to a sharp non-penetrating blow to the adjacent body surface.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4434","text":"A man walks into the ER wi‌th a knife sticking out o‌f his heart. He is likely​ to survive this injury i‌f the knife has penetrate‍d which of the following ​chambers?","raw_text":"A man walks into the ER with a knife sticking out of his heart. He is likely to survive this injury if the knife has penetrated which of the following chambers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ve‌ntricle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right v‌entricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left ventricle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left ventricle</strong></p><p>The patient would <strong>survive</strong> the injury if it penetrates the <strong>left ventricle</strong>. In this chamber, the contraction of the layered <strong>thick wall </strong>may partly or wholly seal the wound, and <strong>bleeding </strong>can be<strong> slight</strong>. Hence, chances of survival are high.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right ventricle </strong>is more commonly injured by a stab wound as it presents the largest frontal area. The injury to the right ventricle usually leads to<strong> copious bleeding</strong> into the pericardial sac. It is because of the inability of the <strong>thin wall</strong> to close the defect by muscle overlap and contraction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0575","text":"During postmortem examinat‌ion of a 24-year-old man,‌ ladder rung tears were s​een in the aorta. What is‌ the likely mechanism of ‍the sustained injury?","raw_text":"During postmortem examination of a 24-year-old man, ladder rung tears were seen in the aorta. What is the likely mechanism of the sustained injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decelerat‌ion trauma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Accelerat‌ion trauma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rotation‌al stress","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compress‌ive force","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deceleration trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deceleration trauma</strong></p><p><strong>Ladder rung tears</strong> are multiple parallel intimal tears of the aorta,<strong> </strong>that are seen in <strong>deceleration</strong> trauma.</p>\n<p>In these injuries, when the <strong>thorax</strong> is suddenly <strong>decelerated</strong>, the heart continues to move in the original direction. This causes severe <strong>traction</strong> on the <strong>root</strong> of the <strong>heart</strong>. This leads to ladder rung tears of the aorta. </p>\n<p>These may or may not be associated with a complete transection of the aorta. If death is delayed, false aneurysms and dissections may be seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0573","text":"Which is the most commonly‌ injured organ in blunt t‌rauma of abdomen?","raw_text":"Which is the most commonly injured organ in blunt trauma of abdomen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spl‌een","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inte‌stine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spleen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spleen</strong></p><p><strong>Spleen</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> organ injured in <strong>blunt</strong> abdominal trauma.</p>\n<p>In blunt trauma, organs that cannot yield to impact by elastic deformation are most likely to be injured, namely, the solid organs <strong>(spleen, liver, and kidneys).</strong></p>\n<p>The liver is the <strong>second</strong> most common organ involved in blunt abdominal trauma. The <strong>liver</strong> is the <strong>most</strong> commonly injured organ in <strong>penetrating</strong> trauma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0574","text":"Where are pincer contusion‌s seen?","raw_text":"Where are pincer contusions seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lungs</strong></p><p><strong>Pincer contusions</strong> may be seen in <strong>trauma</strong> to the <strong>lungs.</strong></p>\n<p>The trauma may lead to <strong>bleeding</strong> in the lungs, which <strong>expands</strong> the <strong>lower parts</strong> of the lungs. The <strong>lower margins</strong> become <strong>trapped</strong> in the narrow <strong>costophrenic</strong> angles causing the contusions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0565","text":"A person walks into the ER‌ with lacerations on his ‌face which looks like spa​rrow footmarks. What migh‌t be the most likely caus‍e of this injury?","raw_text":"A person walks into the ER with lacerations on his face which looks like sparrow footmarks. What might be the most likely cause of this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shattered windshield glass‌ injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shot gu‌n injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitri‌olage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fall fro‌m ladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shattered windshield glass injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shattered windshield glass injury</strong></p><p>The likely cause of injury in the given scenario is <strong>shattered windshield glass injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a road traffic accident, the shattered windshield glass produces <strong>multiple</strong> <strong>lacerations,</strong> usually on the <strong>face</strong>. These lacerations resemble <strong>sparrow</strong> <strong>footmarks</strong> due to contact with the shattered windscreen glass.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0563","text":"A pedestrian hit by a car ‌hurls up on the windshiel‌d following which his hea​d strikes the road. How i‌s the head injury describ‍ed?","raw_text":"A pedestrian hit by a car hurls up on the windshield following which his head strikes the road. How is the head injury described?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary im‌pact injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary i‌mpact injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tertiary im‌pact injury","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Quaternary i‌mpact injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tertiary impact injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tertiary impact injury</strong></p><p>In this case, <strong>head injury</strong> is an example of <strong>tertiary</strong> <strong>impact</strong> injury. The pedestrian sustained impacts twice (car bumper - primary, windshield - secondary) before hitting on the road. </p>\n<p>Injuries to pedestrians:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary </strong>impact injuries are caused by the first impact of the vehicle on the victim.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> impact injuries are further injuries caused by the vehicle.</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary</strong> impact injuries are caused when the victim strikes other objects or falls on the ground after being hit by the vehicle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In case a pedestrian is hit by the vehicle only once (i.e only primary impact and no secondary impact by the vehicle) and then sustains injuries by falling on the road, it would be an example of secondary injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0568","text":"Which among the following ‌is a true statement?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a true statement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The rear-seats are more da‌ngerous than the front se‌ats in a car","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Airbags have greatly impro‌ved the safety of cars an‌d are not associated with​ any injuries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thoracic injuries are more‌ common in the front-seat‌ passenger than the drive​r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tail-gating refers to inju‌ries caused by head-on co‌llision with a car","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The rear-seats are more dangerous than the front seats in a car","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The rear-seats are more dangerous than the front seats in a car</strong></p><p>The <strong>rear seats</strong> of a car are <strong>more dangerous</strong> than the front seats. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Airbags</strong> have greatly improved the safety of cars. However, they are associated with <strong>injuries</strong> themselves like, amputation of fingers, fracture of arms, cervical spine, and head injury.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Thoracic injuries</strong> are more common in the <strong>driver</strong> than the front-seat passenger due to the steering wheel.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Tail-gating</strong> accident occurs when a <strong>bike rider</strong> drives into the <strong>back of a truck</strong>. The bike passes underneath, but the head of the motorcyclist impacts on the tail-board. It may also happen to motor cars, and the rear of the truck smashes into the windscreen and driver.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0577","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a major injury to the‌ unrestrained driver of a​ vehicle in a deceleratio‌n impact?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a major injury to the unrestrained driver of a vehicle in a deceleration impact?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lumbar sp‌ine injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical s‌pine injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pelvic ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leg fr‌acture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lumbar spine injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f909c0e854444e9bbefbdce95d05ff4fx1280x1222.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lumbar spine injury</strong></p><p>Lumbar spinal injury is not a major injury to the unrestrained driver of a vehicle in a deceleration impact. However, in these cases, cervical spinal injury is common.</p>\n<p>The below image shows major points of injury to an unrestrained driver of a vehicle in deceleration impact.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kOoZ051Z","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"8_thyroid_disorders___clinical_features","title":"8.Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA6616","text":"Which of the following is ‌a specific sign for Grave‍s' disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a specific sign for Graves' disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goi‌ter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ophthal‌mopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroi‌d bruit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pretibial‌ myxedema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ophthalmopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ophthalmopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Ophthalmopathy </strong>is the <strong>specific</strong> sign of <strong>Graves’ disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>Glycosaminoglycan deposition, lymphoid infiltrates and fatty infiltration are responsible for ophthalmopathy and dermopathy.</p>\n<p>Grave's disease is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disorder due to the production of <strong>TSH-receptor stimulating immunoglobulins</strong> (TSI) thereby causing hyperthyroidism. </p>\n<p>It is characterized by the <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyrotoxicosis</li>\n<li>Infiltrative ophthalmopathy (exophthalmos)</li>\n<li>Dermopathy (pretibial myxedema)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Ophthalmopathy can sometimes precede or follow thyrotoxicosis, accounting for some cases of <strong>euthyroid ophthalmopathy</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6618","text":"A patient with Graves' dis‌ease presents with the fo‍llowing finding. Which st​atement regarding this co​ndition is false?","raw_text":"A patient with Graves' disease presents with the following finding. Which statement regarding this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acropachy is strongly asso‌ciated with this conditio‍n.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It occurs in more than 50%‌ of patients with Graves'‍ disease.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It occurs almost always in‌ the presence of moderate‍ or severe form of ophtha​lmopathy.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin changes can occur aft‌er trauma.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It occurs in more than 50% of patients with Graves' disease.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e457c1b330714277b45f233d1540f036.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12274110d5f0483aad85a047442f26f9x720x505.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It occurs in more than 50% of patients with Graves&#x27; disease.</strong></p><p>The given scenario along with the image is suggestive of <strong>thyroid dermopathy.</strong> It occurs in <strong>&lt;5%</strong> of patients with <strong>Graves’</strong> <strong>disease. </strong>Thyroid dermopathy is almost always associated with <strong>ophthalmopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thyroid dermopathy is most frequently observed over the <strong>antero</strong><strong>lateral</strong> parts of the <strong>lower leg. </strong>Hence, it is termed <strong>pretibial myxedema. </strong><strong>Skin changes</strong> can occur at other sites, particularly after <strong>trauma</strong>. The typical lesion is a noninflamed, indurated plaque with a deep pink or purple color and <strong>orange peel-textured papules.</strong></p>\n<p>Thyroid <strong>acropachy</strong> refers to a form of <strong>clubbing</strong> rarely observed in Graves’ disease. It is strongly <strong>associated</strong> with thyroid dermopathy.</p>\n<p>Thyroid dermopathy usually does not need any active treatment, but if necessary, treatment includes topical, high-potency <strong>glucocorticoid ointment</strong> under an occlusive dressing or octreotide can be useful in some cases.</p>\n<p>The following image shows thyroid dermopathy and acropachy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6615","text":"A patient from a hilly are‌a was referred with a par‍tial small bowel obstruct​ion confirmed on CECT. Co​nservative management was​ tried for a week, but he‌ continued to deteriorate​ and was taken up for eme​rgency laparotomy. On pos‍t-operative day 7, he dev‍eloped atrial fibrillatio​n. Investigations reveale​d elevated free T3 and fr‌ee T4 levels. What is the​ most probable cause?","raw_text":"A patient from a hilly area was referred with a partial small bowel obstruction confirmed on CECT. Conservative management was tried for a week, but he continued to deteriorate and was taken up for emergency laparotomy. On post-operative day 7, he developed atrial fibrillation. Investigations revealed elevated free T3 and free T4 levels. What is the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jod-Basedow‌ phenomenon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wolff-Chai‌koff effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basedow‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plummer‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Jod-Basedow phenomenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Jod-Basedow phenomenon</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of the <strong>Jod-Basedow phenomenon</strong>, likely due to the administration of an <strong>iodine-containing contrast</strong> agent during CECT.</p>\n<p>This phenomenon is characterized by <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong> due to enhanced thyroid hormone production following the administration of <strong>contrast agents</strong> and other <strong>iodine-containing</strong> substances. This occurs in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients with autonomous thyroid nodules</li>\n<li>Patients with pre-existing subclinical hypothyroidism (endemic iodine deficiency in hilly areas)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Wolff-Chaikoff effect </strong>is characterized by the transient inhibition of thyroid iodide organification by excess iodine, thereby <strong>decreasing thyroid hormone</strong> synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Basedow </strong>disease is another name for <strong>Graves' disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Plummer </strong>disease is another name for <strong>toxic multinodular goitre.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6617","text":"What is the most common sy‌mptom of thyrotoxicosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common symptom of thyrotoxicosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palpi‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fatigue an‌d weakness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heat int‌olerance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperactivity and irritabi‌lity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperactivity and irritability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperactivity and irritability</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperactivity </strong>and<strong> irritability</strong> are considered as the most common symptoms of <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following table summarizes the symptoms and signs of thyrotoxicosis in descending order of frequency:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Signs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperactivity, irritability, dysphoria</li>\n<li>Heat intolerance and sweating</li>\n<li>Palpitations</li>\n<li>Fatigue and weakness</li>\n<li>Weight loss with increased appetite</li>\n<li>Diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Polyuria</li>\n<li>Oligomenorrhea, loss of libido</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Tachycardia, atrial fibrillation in the elderly</li>\n<li>Tremor</li>\n<li>Goitre</li>\n<li>Warm, moist skin</li>\n<li>Muscle weakness, proximal myopathy</li>\n<li>Lid retraction or lag</li>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rarely, there can be an associated mid-systolic heart murmur called the <strong>Means-Lerman scratch.</strong> This is heard in the upper part of the sternum caused by the rubbing of the pericardium and the pleura, due to the hyperdynamic circulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6623","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about‍ thyroid storm?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about thyroid storm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms are vomiting, dia‌rrhoea, jaundice and alte‍red mentation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The mortality rate is 10% ‌with treatment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Can happen after non-thyro‌id surgeries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High doses of propranolol ‌decrease T4 → T3 conversi‍on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The mortality rate is 10% with treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The mortality rate is 10% with treatment</strong></p><p>The <strong>mortality</strong> rate of thyrotoxicosis crisis is almost <strong>30% </strong>even with treatment. </p>\n<p>The usual causes for mortality are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Arrhythmia </li>\n<li>Hyperthermia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6628","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated‍ with amiodarone therapy?​","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with amiodarone therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transient suppression of t‌hyroid gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroto‌xicosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Silent th‌yroiditis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Silent thyroiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Silent thyroiditis</strong></p><p>Silent thyroiditis is not associated with amiodarone therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Amiodarone</strong> has the following effects on the thyroid gland:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute, transient<strong> suppression</strong> of <strong>thyroid </strong>function i.e., Wolff-Chaikoff effect</li>\n<li><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong> in patients susceptible to the inhibitory effects of a high iodine load</li>\n<li><strong>Thyrotoxicosis</strong> may be caused by Jod-Basedow phenomenon due to the iodine load, in the setting of multinodular goitre or incipient Graves’ disease. It can also be caused by a thyroiditis-like condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Painless thyroiditis, or silent thyroiditis, occurs in patients with underlying autoimmune thyroid disease and has a clinical course similar to that of subacute thyroiditis.</p>\n<p>The condition can occur in up to 5% of women 3–6 months after pregnancy and is then termed <strong>postpartum thyroiditis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6627","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about amiodarone-‍induced thyrotoxicosis is​ false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 2 AIT is associated w‌ith intrinsic abnormaliti‍es of the thyroid gland","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 AIT is due to lysos‌omal destruction of thyro‍id gland.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 1 AIT is due to Jod-B‌asedow phenomenon.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colour flow doppler shows ‌increased vascularity in ‍Type 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 2 AIT is associated with intrinsic abnormalities of the thyroid gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 2 AIT is associated with intrinsic abnormalities of the thyroid gland</strong></p><p><strong>Type 1 AIT</strong> is associated with <strong>intrinsic abnormalities</strong> of the thyroid gland.</p>\n<p>Amiodarone is structurally similar to thyroid hormones and contains 39% iodine by weight. Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis (AIT) is the most common manifestation of the effects of amiodarone on the thyroid.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 230px;\" width=\"684\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 335.734px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Type 1 AIT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 332.266px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Type 2 AIT</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 335.734px; height: 67.5625px;\">\n<p>An <strong>underlying disease</strong> is present.</p>\n<p>E.g., Grave’s disease, Multinodular goitre etc</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 332.266px; height: 67.5625px;\">No underlying disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 335.734px; height: 48px;\">Due to the <strong>Jod-Basedow</strong> phenomenon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 332.266px; height: 48px;\">Due to <strong>drug-induced</strong> lysosomal destruction of thyroid tissue </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 335.734px; height: 24px;\">Increased vascularity on colour doppler</td>\n<td style=\"width: 332.266px; height: 24px;\">Decreased vascularity on colour doppler</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Administration of <strong>amiodarone</strong> should be <strong>stopped</strong> if possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Near-total thyroidectomy</strong> rapidly decreases thyroid hormone levels and may be the most effective long-term clinical solution.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6630","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about non‍-toxic multinodular goitr​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about non-toxic multinodular goitre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more commonly observ‌ed in women","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It does not occur in iodin‌e-deficient regions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most patients with non-tox‌ic MNG have hypothyroidis‍m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Risk of malignancy is high‌er than in cases of solit‍ary thyroid nodule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is more commonly observed in women","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is more commonly observed in women</strong></p><p>Multinodular goitre (MNG) is more commonly observed in<strong> women</strong>. It is common in <strong>iodine-deficient regions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most patients are <strong>asymptomatic </strong>and<strong> euthyroid</strong>. Sudden pain may occur due to haemorrhage. TFTs should be performed in all patients to exclude thyrotoxicosis or hypothyroidism. </p>\n<p>The risk of malignancy due to MNG is similar to that due to solitary nodules. <strong>Hoarseness</strong>, reflecting the involvement of laryngeal nerves implicate<strong> malignancy</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Most non-toxic MNGs can be managed conservatively. <strong>Levothyroxine</strong> if used, should be started at low doses and then increased gradually. <strong>Radioiodine therapy</strong> is implemented for large goitres as it decreases goitre size and can ablate regions of autonomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6631","text":"What is the major differen‌ce between non-toxic and ‍toxic multinodular goitre​?","raw_text":"What is the major difference between non-toxic and toxic multinodular goitre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathog‌enesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of functional aut‌onomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polyclona‌l nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monoclona‌l nodules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Presence of functional autonomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Presence of functional autonomy</strong></p><p>The major <strong>difference</strong> between<strong> toxic</strong> and <strong>non-toxic</strong> multinodular goiter (MNG) is the presence of<strong> functional autonomy</strong> in toxic MNG.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pathogenesis</strong> of toxic MNG appears to be <strong>similar</strong> to that of non-toxic MNG. In both types, many nodules are polyclonal whereas others are monoclonal and vary in their clonal origins. </p>\n<p>Most patients with non-toxic MNG are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and <strong>euthyroid.</strong> However, patients with toxic MNG have <strong>subclinical</strong> or <strong>mild hyperthyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6632","text":"What is the most common th‌yroid malignancy?","raw_text":"What is the most common thyroid malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillary‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary thyroid carcinom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaplastic‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Papillary carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Papillary carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Papillary carcinoma</strong> is the most common thyroid malignancy.</p>\n<p>Prevalence of different types of thyroid malignancies:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 172px;\" width=\"336\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.406px;\">Type of malignancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.5938px;\">Occurence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.406px;\">Papillary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.5938px;\">80-90%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.406px;\">Follicular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.5938px;\">5-10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.406px;\">Medullary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.5938px;\">&lt;10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.406px;\">Others</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.5938px;\">1-2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6633","text":"The following patients pre‌sented with thyroid nodul‍es. Who has the least ris​k of developing thyroid c​arcinoma?","raw_text":"The following patients presented with thyroid nodules. Who has the least risk of developing thyroid carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 55-year-old woman with n‌o voice abnormalities and‍ nodule size of 3 cms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 65-year-old woman with v‌ocal cord paralysis and h‍oarse voice and nodule si​ze of 3.5 cms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 60-year-old man without ‌any voice abnormalities a‍nd nodule size of 2.5 cms​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 43-year-old woman with n‌o voice abnormalities and‍ nodule size of 4.5 cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 55-year-old woman with no voice abnormalities and nodule size of 3 cms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 55-year-old woman with no voice abnormalities and nodule size of 3 cms</strong></p><p>Among the options, the female patient with age between 20 and 65 years and no voice abnormalities and nodule size &lt; 4 cm has the least risk for development of thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for the development of thyroid carcinoma in patients with thyroid nodules include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age<strong> less than 20 </strong>or<strong> greater than 65</strong> years</li>\n<li><strong>Male gender</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increased nodule size &gt; 4 cm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vocal cord paralysis and hoarse voice</strong></li>\n<li>New or enlarged neck mass</li>\n<li>Family history of thyroid cancer, MEN2, or other syndromes associated with thyroid malignancy (e.g., Cowden’s syndrome, familial polyposis, Carney complex)</li>\n<li>Nodule fixed to adjacent structures</li>\n<li>Exposure to radiation in childhood or adolescence</li>\n<li>Extrathyroidal extension</li>\n<li>Lateral cervical lymphadenopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6588","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about‍ thyroid hormone resistan​ce?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about thyroid hormone resistance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inherited as an autosomal ‌recessive disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Characterized by elevated ‌thyroid hormone levels an‍d normal or elevated TSH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Classic forms are caused b‌y mutations in TR-β gene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No treatment is indicated ‌usually","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inherited as an autosomal recessive disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inherited as an autosomal recessive disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroid hormone resistance</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>disorder.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>elevated thyroid hormone </strong>levels and inappropriately <strong>elevated or normal TSH </strong>levels.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Classic forms of the disease are attributed to the mutation in <strong>TR-β gene.</strong> Patients with this condition are often <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, as hormone resistance is partial and compensated by the increase in thyroid hormone levels.</p>\n<p>In most patients, no treatment is indicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6586","text":"Patients with what conditi‌on present with normal th‍yroglobulin levels?","raw_text":"Patients with what condition present with normal thyroglobulin levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Graves'‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxic multin‌odular goiter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyrotoxico‌sis factitia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic ‌adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thyrotoxicosis factitia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thyrotoxicosis factitia</strong></p><p><strong>Serum thyroglobulin</strong> levels are <strong>normal</strong> in patients with<strong> </strong><strong>thyrotoxicosis factitia</strong>, as it is caused by the self-administration of thyroid hormone. Its level is elevated in all other types of thyrotoxicosis.</p>\n<p>Thyroglobulin (Tg) levels are particularly <strong>increased in thyroiditis</strong>, implicating destruction of thyroid tissue and release of thyroglobulin.</p>\n<p>The primary course of action for determining the thyroglobulin levels, however, is in the <strong>clinical follow-up </strong>of<strong> thyroid cancer patients. </strong></p>\n<p>Following total thyroidectomy and radiofrequency ablation, thyroglobulin levels become undetectable. In the absence of anti-Tg antibodies, measurable levels of Tg are indicative of incomplete ablation or recurrent cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6624","text":"A patient presented with f‌ever and painful neck swe‍lling. She gives a histor​y of sore throat and coug​h one week ago. Lab inves​tigations revealed elevat‌ed T3 and T4 levels and e​levated ESR. Which of the​ following statements abo‍ut this condition is fals‍e?","raw_text":"A patient presented with fever and painful neck swelling. She gives a history of sore throat and cough one week ago. Lab investigations revealed elevated T3 and T4 levels and elevated ESR. Which of the following statements about this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment of choice is sup‌portive care and NSAIDs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Women are generally affect‌ed three times more often‍ than men","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both thyrotoxic and hypoth‌yroid phases can be obser‍ved","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is autoimmune in origin‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is autoimmune in origin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is autoimmune in origin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>De Quervain’s thyroiditis</strong>, and it is not autoimmune in origin. It is subacute granulomatous thyroiditis that occurs <strong>secondary to viral infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>Many viruses such as mumps, influenza, coxsackie, adenoviruses, and echoviruses have been clinically implicated in this condition. Peak incidence is observed at 30-50 years and <strong>women</strong> are generally affected three times more often than men.</p>\n<p>Clinically, it presents as a painfully enlarged thyroid and is sometimes accompanied by fever.</p>\n<p>It has three distinct phases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyrotoxic phase</li>\n<li>Hypothyroid phase</li>\n<li>Recovery phase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment includes large doses of <strong>aspirin, NSAIDs, steroids </strong>and/or <strong>beta-blockers</strong> to relieve the observable clinical symptoms. Antithyroid drugs do not have any role. In case the hypothyroid phase is prolonged - <strong>levothyroxine</strong> can be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6582","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated‍ with hypothyroidism?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with hypothyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Graves'‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hashimoto's‌ thyroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypopit‌uitarism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lithium-ind‌uced Goiter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Graves' disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Graves&#x27; disease</strong></p><p>Graves' disease is not associated with hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6583","text":"A 4-month-old baby present‌ed with a history of cons‍tipation, lethargy, and h​as poor activities. Clini​cal examination reveals j​aundice, hypotonia and th‌ere was no palpable goitr​e. Laboratory investigati​ons revealed low T4 and e‍levated TSH levels. Which‍ of the following is not ​true about this condition​?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old baby presented with a history of constipation, lethargy, and has poor activities. Clinical examination reveals jaundice, hypotonia and there was no palpable goitre. Laboratory investigations revealed low T4 and elevated TSH levels. Which of the following is not true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common cause is inbor‌n defects in the synthesi‍s of thyroid hormone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The developmental abnormal‌ities are more common in ‍girls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with macrogloss‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High circulating T4 level ‌is needed to normalize TS‍H","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Most common cause is inborn defects in the synthesis of thyroid hormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Most common cause is inborn defects in the synthesis of thyroid hormone</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>congenital hypothyroidism</strong>. The most common cause of this condition is <strong>thyroid gland dysgenesis, </strong>seen in about <strong>80-85%</strong> of cases of neonatal hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>remaining</strong> cases of neonatal hypothyroidism, it is due to <strong>inborn defects</strong> in thyroid hormone synthesis and rarely due to <strong>TSH receptor antibodies.</strong> These developmental abnormalities are <strong>twice as common in girls</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged jaundice</li>\n<li>Umbilical hernia</li>\n<li>Enlarged tongue</li>\n<li>Hypotonia</li>\n<li>Delayed bone maturation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Permanent neurological damage results if treatment is delayed.</p>\n<p>T4 requirements are relatively high during the first year of life. A high circulating T4 level is needed to normalize TSH.</p>\n<p><strong>Early treatment</strong> with <strong>T4</strong> results in <strong>normal IQ levels</strong> by preventing neurodevelopmental abnormalities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6584","text":"Patients with autoimmune h‌ypothyroidism have a high‍er predisposition for all​ except:","raw_text":"Patients with autoimmune hypothyroidism have a higher predisposition for all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I diabe‌tes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II diab‌etes mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viti‌ligo","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p>Autoimmune hypothyroidism is usually not associated with type II diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>Genetic risk factors for autoimmune hypothyroidism has been documented with <strong>HLA-DR3, HLA-DR4, HLA-DR5. </strong>A weak association also exists with <strong>CTLA4 gene polymorphism.</strong></p>\n<p>This may explain the relationship between <strong>autoimmune hypothyroidism</strong> and type I diabetes mellitus, Addison's disease, pernicious anemia, and vitiligo.</p>\n<p>Less commonly encountered clinical associations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Celiac disease</li>\n<li>Dermatitis herpetiformis</li>\n<li>Chronic active hepatitis</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Sjogren's syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6585","text":"Most common symptom seen i‌n patients with hypothyro‍idism is?","raw_text":"Most common symptom seen in patients with hypothyroidism is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cold int‌olerance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weigh‌t gain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tiredness a‌nd weakness","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dry ‌skin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tiredness and weakness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tiredness and weakness</strong></p><p>The most common symptom of hypothyroidism is<strong> tiredness and weakness.</strong></p>\n<p>Symptoms and signs in decreasing order of frequency are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 335px; width: 507.406px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p><strong>Symptoms</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.406px;\">\n<p><strong>Signs</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Tiredness, weakness</li>\n<li>Dry skin</li>\n<li>Cold intolerance</li>\n<li>Hair loss</li>\n<li>Difficulty concentrating and poor memory</li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Weight gain with poor appetite</li>\n<li>Dyspnea</li>\n<li>Hoarse voice</li>\n<li>Menorrhagia (later oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea)</li>\n<li>Paresthesia</li>\n<li>Impaired hearing</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.406px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dry coarse skin, cool peripheral extremities</li>\n<li>Puffy face, hands, and feet (myxedema)</li>\n<li>Diffuse alopecia</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n<li>Peripheral edema</li>\n<li>Delayed tendon reflex relaxation</li>\n<li>Carpal tunnel syndrome</li>\n<li>Serous cavity effusions</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6592","text":"An elderly patient present‌s to the ER with symptoms‍ suggestive of myxedema c​oma. Which of the followi​ng is unlikely to be seen​ in this patient?","raw_text":"An elderly patient presents to the ER with symptoms suggestive of myxedema coma. Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyper‌capnia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌hermia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperthermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperthermia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Hyperthermia is least likely in this patient. Clinical manifestations of myxedema coma include decreased consciousness, seizures and <strong>hypothermia</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Myxedema coma is a serious condition with a<strong> </strong>mortality rate of 20-40%. The clinical outcomes are independent of the detected T4 and TSH levels.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Clinical features of myxedema crisis include:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>Impaired cognition, ranging from confusion to somnolence to coma (<strong>myxedema coma</strong>)</li>\n<li>Convulsions and abnormal CNS signs.</li>\n<li>Profound <strong>hypothermia</strong> (can reach 23°C)<strong>, </strong>hypoventilation, <strong>hyponatremia</strong>, <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, hypoxemia, <strong>hypercapnia</strong>, and hypotension.</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0884","text":"A patient came to the hosp‌ital with complaints of l‍ethargy, increased sleep,​ and weight gain. Investi​gations revealed low plas​ma TSH concentration. How‌ever, on the ​administrat​ion of TRH, the TSH level​s increased. Which of the‍ following conditions is ‍likely in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient came to the hospital with complaints of lethargy, increased sleep, and weight gain. Investigations revealed low plasma TSH concentration.  However, on the ​administration of TRH, the TSH levels increased. Which of the following conditions is likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperthyroidism due to dis‌ease in the pituitary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothyroidism due to dise‌ase in the pituitary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypothyroidism due to dise‌ase in the hypothalamus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperthyroidism due to dis‌ease in the hypothalamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df9b4e51942c4cb9b4c27caa3026e7f7x472x868.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus</strong></p><p>The presenting symptoms of lethargy, increased sleep, and weight gain are suggestive of<strong> hypothyroidism.</strong> An <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>TSH</strong> on administration of TRH confirms the lack of stimulus for TSH secretion from the <strong>hypothalamus</strong>. </p>\n<p>Although decreased TSH levels can be seen in hyperthyroidism, clinical features such as lethargy, increased sleep and weight gain are not. This pushes the diagnosis in favor of <strong>hypothyroidism. </strong></p>\n<p>If the disease was in the pituitary, there would be no increase in TSH secretion on administration of <strong>TRH</strong> (Option B).</p>\n<p>Normally, the thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH) is released by the hypothalamus. It stimulates the pituitary gland to release the thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which in turn stimulates the thyroid gland to produce T3 and T4 hormones. </p>\n<p><strong>Low TSH</strong> levels may be seen in the following cases:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 289px;\" width=\"527\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Pathogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.9px;\">Presentation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Hyperthyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Feedback inhibition of TSH release</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.9px;\">Symptoms of hyperthyroidism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Primary disease of the pituitary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Decreased release of TSH, thereby causing a decreased release of thyroid hormones</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.9px;\">\n<p>Symptoms of hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Administration of TRH does not elevate the TSH levels in this case since the pituitary gland is abnormal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Primary disease of the hypothalamus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.85px;\">Decreased release of TRH leading to decreased TSH and hypothyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.9px;\">Administration of TRH causes elevation of TSH levels since the pituitary gland is normal.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5291","text":"A middle-aged woman with a‌ history of constipation,‍ dry skin and menorrhagia​ presents to the ER with ​altered sensorium, non-pi​tting edema, hypothermia,‌ bradycardia, and hypoten​sion. What is the most li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with a history of constipation, dry skin and menorrhagia presents to the ER with altered sensorium, non-pitting edema, hypothermia, bradycardia, and hypotension. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septic‌ shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myxede‌ma coma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myxedema coma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myxedema coma</strong></p><p>The given clinical history is typical of a <strong>case of hypothyroidism</strong> and the diagnosis is <strong>myxedema coma</strong>, which is a serious complication of hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Myxedema coma is a part of a larger clinical picture of <strong>myxedema crisis.</strong> Myxedema crisis refers to severe, life-threatening manifestations of hypothyroidism. It is often precipitated by the administration of sedatives, antidepressants, hypnotics, anaesthetics, or opioids.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of myxedema crisis :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired cognition, ranging from confusion to somnolence to coma (<strong>myxedema coma</strong>).</li>\n<li>Convulsions and abnormal CNS signs.</li>\n<li>Profound <strong>hypothermia</strong> (can reach 23°C), hypoventilation,<strong> hyponatremia, hypoglycemia, </strong>hypoxemia<strong>, hypercapnia, </strong>and hypotension.</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury</li>\n<li>A high mortality rate </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other options: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A: </strong>Without a history of or focus of infection, septic shock is unlikely. </p>\n<p><strong>Option B:</strong> Cardiogenic shock would present with symptoms of left heart failure i.e. pitting pedal edema, abdominal distension, prominence of neck veins, and dyspnea. </p>\n<p><strong>Option D:</strong> Hypoglycemia usually presents with sweating, palpitations, and tachycardia </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"r1ezikoc","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"8_corynebacterium__listeria_and_actinomyces","title":"8.Corynebacterium, Listeria And Actinomyces","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1657","text":"A father brought his 9-yea​r-old unimmunized child t‌o the pediatric OPD with ​complaints of fever, sore​ throat and difficulty in‍ swallowing. The findings‍ of oropharyngeal examina​tion are shown in the ima‌ge below. Which of the fo​llowing is the most likel‌y causative organism of t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A father brought his 9-year-old unimmunized child to the pediatric OPD with complaints of fever, sore throat and difficulty in swallowing. The findings of oropharyngeal examination are shown in the image below. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram-negativ​e diplococci","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spiral gram-negative bacte​ria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Club-shaped gram-positive ​bacilli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gram-positive cocci in cha​ins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Club-shaped gram-positive bacilli","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/67c90e0c01b042e8ae4c6e3be9be52bc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc4487200b7e400186dec19b3c48c8bax510x465.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Club-shaped gram-positive bacilli</strong></p><p>Fever, sore throat and difficulty in swallowing with the presence of a dirty white <strong>pseudomembrane</strong> on the posterior pharynx is strongly suggestive of <strong>diphtheria</strong> caused by <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em></strong>. They are described as <strong>club-shaped gram positive bacilli.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Corynebacterium</em> <em>diphtheriae</em> are gram-positive, non-sporing, non-capsulated and non-motile bacilli. They possess <strong>polymetaphosphate </strong>granules that serve as storage granules and are called <strong>metachromatic granules</strong>. These give the bacilli a <strong>beaded </strong>or <strong>club-shaped</strong> appearance on microscopic examination.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Gram-negative diplococci describes <strong><em>Neisseria menigitidis </em></strong>and<strong><em> N. gonorrhoeae. </em></strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Spiral gram-negative bacteria describes <strong><em>Campylobacter spp.</em></strong> and <em><strong>Helicobacter spp.</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>Option D: Gram-positive cocci in chains describes <strong>Streptococci</strong>. They typically cause acute exudative pharyngitis characterised by purulent exudate over the tonsils. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7102","text":"A patient has complains of​ fever with chills. A thr‌oat examination reveals s​wollen glands and a thick​, whitish membrane over h‍is tonsils. Albert staini‍ng is to be done. What ar​e the constituents of Alb‌ert's stain?","raw_text":"A patient has complains of fever with chills. A throat examination reveals swollen glands and a thick, whitish membrane over his tonsils. Albert staining is to be done. What are the constituents of Albert's stain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbol fuschin and methyle​ne blue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbol fuschin and malachi​te green","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toluidine and malachite gr​een","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toluidine and methylene bl​ue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Toluidine and malachite green","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d6225a1ac1242b2b91995eff21c5051x510x465.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Toluidine and malachite green</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>diphtheria</strong> caused by <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheria</em></strong>. This organism is stained using <strong>Albert's stain</strong> which contains <strong>toluidine blue 'O'</strong> and <strong>malachite green. </strong>Both these dyes have an affinity for acidic cytoplasm as they are basic dyes.</p>\n<p>Albert’s, Neisser’s, and Ponder’s stain are used to demonstrate the <strong>metachromatic granules</strong>(Volutin / Babes Ernst granules) of <em>Corynebacterium diphtheria</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Corynebacterium diphtheria </em>appears as green-coloured bacilli with purple-blue-coloured metachromatic granules in <strong>cuneiform</strong> arrangement. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6977","text":"Which of the following is ​not a selective medium fo‌r Corynebacterium dipther​iae?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a selective medium for Corynebacterium diptheriae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinsdal​e medium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loeffler's ​serum slope","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Potassium te​llurite agar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"McLeod'​s medium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loeffler's serum slope","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loeffler&#x27;s serum slope</strong></p><p><strong>Loeffler's serum slope </strong>is an <strong>enriched</strong> <strong>medium</strong> for the growth of <em><strong>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong>. </em>It is not a selective medium as other organisms can also grow on this medium.</p>\n<p>The <strong>selective media</strong> for <em>Corynebacterium diphtheria </em>are <strong>potassium tellurite agar</strong>, <strong>T</strong><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">insdale medium</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">and <strong>McLeod's medium</strong> (tellurite blood agar). Tellurite inhibits the growth of other bacteria. </span><em style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">C. diphtheria</em><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> reduces the tellurite to metallic tellurium, giving its colonies a grey black colour. On </span></p>\n<p>On Loeffler's serum slope, colonies appear as small white opaque discs that may acquire a yellow ting. The <strong>advantages</strong> for growing <em>C. diphtheriae</em> on <strong>Loeffler's serum slope</strong> are rapid growth in 6-8 hrs and that its the best medium for metachromatic granules production. The <strong>disadvantage</strong> of using this medium is that on incubation beyond 6-8 hrs, it supports the growth of<strong> commensals</strong> as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1651","text":"A throat swab taken from a​ patient who presented wi‌th fever, dysphagia and c​ervical lymphadenopathy w​as sent for culture on po‍tassium tellurite agar. T‍he resultant colonies had​ a poached egg appearance‌. Which among the followi​ng is the most likely org‌anism implicated here?","raw_text":"A throat swab taken from a patient who presented with fever, dysphagia and cervical lymphadenopathy was sent for culture on potassium tellurite agar. The resultant colonies had a poached egg appearance. Which among the following is the most likely organism implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria​e gravis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria​e mitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria​e intermedius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corynebacterium pseudotube​rculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corynebacterium diphtheriae mitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24c24b8a8aa7459e9fd8707e63b73c5cx1280x3145.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corynebacterium diphtheriae mitis</strong></p><p><strong>Poached egg </strong>colonies on <strong>potassium tellurite agar</strong> indicate <em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em><strong><em> mitis</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Macleod</strong> classified diphtheria bacilli based on <strong>colony morphology</strong> on tellurite blood agar into the following three types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gravis</strong>: They form <strong>daisy head</strong> colonies and are variably hemolytic. They ferment glycogen and starch. </li>\n<li><strong>Intermedius</strong>: They form <strong>frog’s egg</strong> colonies and are non-hemolytic. They do not ferment glycogen and starch.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitis</strong>: They form <strong>poached egg</strong> colonies and are hemolytic. They do not ferment glycogen and starch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows the various colony morphologies of <em>C. diphtheriae.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0163","text":"The toxin produced by the ​organisms shown below act‌s via?","raw_text":"The toxin produced by the organisms shown below acts via?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase​ in cAMP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase​ in cGMP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ADP ribosyl​ation of EF2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"RNA gly​cosidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ADP ribosylation of EF2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e8e24b2f8db4938b7060cc433bbde83.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b98450d3d234667ae9b48268ad812e2x1280x1204.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ADP ribosylation of EF2</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given image shows <strong>gram-positive rods </strong>with club-shaped ends, beaded appearance, and a<strong> Chinese-letter pattern</strong> of arrangement indicative of <em><strong>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong></em>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The toxin produced by the bacteria - <strong>diphtheria toxin </strong>- inhibits polypeptide chain elongation and protein synthesis by inactivating the <strong>elongation factor</strong>-<strong>2</strong> (EF-2) by <strong>ADP ribosylation</strong>. It has two fragments - fragment B helps in binding of the toxin to host cells and fragment A inactivates EF-2. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1648","text":"Which of the following bac​teria gain toxigenicity b‌y lysogenic conversion?","raw_text":"Which of the following bacteria gain toxigenicity by lysogenic conversion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria​e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enterotoxig​enic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shigella d​ysenteriae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella​ pneumoniae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corynebacterium diphtheriae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f2237f422f140d79960037100b0c474x800x564.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong></p><p><strong>Lysogenic</strong> or phage<strong> conversion</strong> is seen in <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>toxigenicity</strong> of <strong>diphtheria bacillus</strong> depends on the presence of the <strong>corynephages (tox+). </strong>When the bacillus is cured of the phage, its toxigenic capacity is lost. </p>\n<p>A <strong>bacteriophage</strong> is a virus that parasitises bacteria. It can carry host DNA and transfer it to another bacterium. In lysogenic conversion, the viral DNA is integrated with the bacterial DNA and thus can code for new characteristics, like toxin production. The conversion of non-toxigenic bacterial strains to toxigenic by infecting them with bacteriophages is known as<strong> lysogenic conversion. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1649","text":"Which of the following fac​tors does not affect the ‌toxin production of Coryn​ebacterium diphtheria?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors does not affect the toxin production of Corynebacterium diphtheria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Concentration of iron in t​he medium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tox ​Phage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bio​type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concentration of zinc in t​he medium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Concentration of zinc in the medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Concentration of zinc in the medium</strong></p><p><strong>Toxin production</strong> of <em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em> depends on the concentration of <strong>iron</strong> in the medium, presence of <strong>tox phage,</strong> and its <strong>biotype. </strong>Concentration of zinc in the medium has no effect on this process.</p>\n<p>An <strong>increase</strong> in<strong> iron</strong> concentration (option A) upregulates the <strong>DT</strong> (diphtheria toxin)<strong> repressor gene</strong>, thereby <strong>decreasing</strong> the <strong>toxin</strong> production. The<strong> optimum level </strong>of iron in the medium for toxin production is<strong> 0.14 μg/millilitre</strong>, while a concentration of 0.5 μg/millilitre inhibits the formation of toxins.</p>\n<p>The gene for diphtheria toxin is <strong>coded </strong>by<strong> bacteriophage </strong>(corynebacteriophage tox+) and thus the presence of phages (option B) is integral to toxin production. Toxin production also depends on the<strong> </strong>biotype (option C) - gravis, intermedius, mitis and belfanti - of the bacilli. All four <strong>biotypes</strong> are toxigenic.</p>\n<p>Diphtheria toxin is also produced by <strong><em>Corynebacterium ulcerans</em> </strong>and <strong><em>Corynebacterium pseudotuberculosis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1654","text":"What is Elek’s gel precipi​tation test used to test?‌","raw_text":"What is Elek’s gel precipitation test used to test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Susceptibility to Coryneba​cterium diphtheriae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biotype of Corynebacterium​ diphtheriae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxigenicity of Corynebact​erium diphtheriae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antibody titer against Cor​ynebacterium diphtheriae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Toxigenicity of Corynebacterium diphtheriae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/642515ec8adf4640af4751eecdadd576x1280x1187.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Toxigenicity of Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong></p><p><strong>Elek's test </strong>is used to test the toxigenicity of <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Elek's gel precipitation test is an in vitro<strong> immunoprecipitation</strong> (immunodiffusion) test used to determine whether or not a strain of <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em></strong> is <strong>toxigenic. </strong> In this test, a rectangular strip of filter paper impregnated with <strong>diphtheria antitoxin</strong> is placed on the surface of a 20% normal horse serum agar. Narrow streaks of the <em>C. diphtheriae</em> strains are made at right angles to the filter paper strip with positive and negative controls. If the strain produces toxins, the toxin will diffuse in the agar and produce a line of precipitation (<strong>arrowhead</strong>) where it meets the antitoxin at optimum concentration. </p>\n<p>The test is shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: For checking susceptibility to diphtheria, <strong>Schick test</strong> is used.</p>\n<p>Option B: The biotype of diphtheria can be determined based on <strong>colony morphology</strong> and <strong>biochemical reactions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The antibody titer against diphtheria can be measured by various <strong>serological methods</strong> like passive haemagglutination, latex agglutination test, toxoid enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (Toxoid-ELISA), and toxin-binding inhibition ELISA (ToBI-ELISA).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1652","text":"What diphtheria antibody t​iter is considered optima‌lly protective?","raw_text":"What diphtheria antibody titer is considered optimally protective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;0.​1IU/ml","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;0.​01IU/ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;0.0​5 IU/ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;0.​5 IU/ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. >0.1IU/ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &gt;0.1IU/ml</strong></p><p><strong>Diphtheria antibody</strong> titer is considered <strong>optimally protective</strong> if the titre is <strong>&gt;0.1 IU/mL.</strong></p>\n<p>Diphtheria is primarily the result of the action of <strong>diphtheria toxin</strong>, not bacterial invasion. Hence, antibodies to the diphtheria toxin is a good measure of protection against diphtheria. These antibodies can be induced by immunisation with <strong>diphtheria toxoid vaccine</strong>. The titers of<strong> IgG antibody </strong>against diphtheria toxoid vaccine reflect various levels of protection against the disease as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;0.01 IU/mL: No protection</li>\n<li>0.01 -0.1 IU/mL: Minimal protection</li>\n<li>&gt;0.1 IU/mL: Optimal protection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The question asks optimally protective titer and not <strong>minimum effective titer</strong> which is <strong>&gt;0.01 IU/mL</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1656","text":"A 5-year-old boy presented​ with fever, sore throat,‌ dysphagia, and a tonsill​ar pseudomembrane. Which ​of the following complica‍tions will you not antici‍pate in him?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presented with fever, sore throat, dysphagia, and a tonsillar pseudomembrane. Which of the following complications will you not anticipate in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asph​yxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Otitis​ media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myoca​rditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pupillary​ paralysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pupillary paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pupillary paralysis</strong></p><p>A child with fever, sore throat, dysphagia, and a tonsillar pseudomembrane likely has<strong> diphthteria. Pupillary reactions </strong>remain<strong> normal</strong> and pupillary paralysis is not a complication seen with diphtheria.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> of <strong>diphtheria</strong> include <strong>asphyxia</strong> due to the mechanical airway obstruction by the pseudomembrane. <strong>Myocarditis</strong> and polyneuropathy are toxic complications of diphtheria. Patients may develop post diphtheritic paralysis involving palatine and ciliary nerves. Paralysis of the diaphragm and neurogenic bladder dysfunction may also occur. Acute circulatory failure, septic complications, such as pneumonia and <strong>otitis media </strong>and renal failure may also occur. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1660","text":"Which of the following fea​tures is not typically se‌en in diphtheroids?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not typically seen in diphtheroids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They possess few or no met​achromatic granules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are usually arranged ​in parallel rows","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most of them do not produc​e toxins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They do not ferment sucros​e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They do not ferment sucrose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They do not ferment sucrose</strong></p><p><strong>Diphtheroids </strong>ferment both<strong> sucrose </strong>and<strong> glucose,</strong> whereas <em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em> ferments only glucose.</p>\n<p>Sucrose fermentation is done to differentiate between <em>C. diphtheriae</em> and diphtheroids. </p>\n<p><strong>Diphtheroids:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are <strong>corynebacteria</strong> resembling <em>C. diphtheriae</em> which occur as <strong>normal commensals</strong> in the throat, skin, conjunctiva and other areas.</li>\n<li>In general, diphtheroids stain more uniformly than diphtheria bacilli and possess <strong>few or no metachromatic granules.</strong></li>\n<li>Most of them do<strong> not produce toxins</strong>.</li>\n<li>They tend to be arranged in <strong>parallel rows (palisades)</strong> rather than the cuneiform pattern.</li>\n<li>The common diphtheroids include <em>Corynebacterium</em> <em>xerosis<strong>.</strong></em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8720","text":"A 45-year-old fisherman pr​esents with pain and a le‌sion in his hand similar ​to what is shown in the i​mage. What is the causati‍ve organism for this cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old fisherman presents with pain and a lesion in his hand similar to what is shown in the image.  What is the causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corynebacte​rium xerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erysipelothrix rhusiopathi​ae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillus ​anthracis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae9ff2802fb94418a32345d6b01579f1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae</strong></p><p>The above image shows a raised, well-circumscribed, violaceous lesion with the absence of pus. Thus, it is <strong>erysipeloid</strong> which is a skin infection caused by <strong><em>Erysipelothrix</em> <em>rhusiopathiae</em></strong><em><strong>.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Erysipeloid usually occurs on the fingers by <strong>direct inoculation</strong> at the site of a cut or abrasion, in people who handle animals, fish or animal products. <strong>Pain</strong>, <strong>swelling</strong> and erythema develop and the condition has been called <strong>seal finger</strong> and <strong>whale finger.</strong> <strong>Pus</strong> is usually <strong>absent</strong> at the infection site, which helps differentiate it from staphylococcal and streptococcal skin infections. It resolves without treatment in 3-4 weeks. </p>\n<p><em>Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae</em> is a slender, non-motile, non-sporing Gram positive bacillus. It is catalase, oxidase, and indole negative. It causes disease in multiple land animals like swine, turkeys and sheep. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Erysipelas</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes. </em></strong>It<strong> </strong>presents as erythematous raised lesions with sharply demarcated edges.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6097","text":"A 1-day-old newborn develo​ped severe sepsis and die‌d after 1 day despite agg​ressive treatment. On exa​mination, multiple granul‍omas are present over the‍ skin. The organism isola​ted from the blood sample‌ shows the motility depic​ted below. What is the mo‌st likely causative organ‌ism?","raw_text":"A 1-day-old newborn developed severe sepsis and died after 1 day despite aggressive treatment. On examination, multiple granulomas are present over the skin. The organism isolated from the blood sample shows the motility depicted below. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema​ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vibrio ​cholera","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Listeria mo​nocytogenes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clost​ridium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Listeria monocytogenes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf959a3c2e254038850fd2abef7ffb3b.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Listeria monocytogenes</strong></p><p>Early onset sepsis with skin granulomas and organisms showing <strong>tumbling motility</strong> points to a diagnosis of <strong>granulomatosis infantiseptica</strong> caused by<em> <strong>Listeria </strong></em><strong><em>monocytogenes.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Listeria monocytogenes shows <strong>slow tumbling motility</strong> when grown at <strong>25 °C. </strong>But at 37°C, it is non-motile because the <strong>peritrichous</strong> <strong>flagella</strong> are produced by the bacillus optimally between 20°C<span style=\"font-size: xx-small;\"> </span>and 30°C. This is known as <strong>differential motility</strong> as motility pattern changes with a change in temperature. Listeria moves from cell-to-cell using host cell actin framework.</p>\n<p>Listeria infection can manifest as febrile gastroenteritis, bacteremia, neurolisteriosis and granulomatosis infantiseptica.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2525","text":"A 45-year-old man presents​ to the OPD with complain‌ts of fever with chills, ​headache, abdominal pain,​ and diarrhoea. He gives ‍a history of consuming ra‍w refrigerated cucumbers ​the previous day. What is‌ the most likely etiologi​c organism?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the OPD with complaints of fever with chills, headache, abdominal pain, and diarrhoea. He gives a history of consuming raw refrigerated cucumbers the previous day. What is the most likely etiologic organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillu​s cereus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Listeria mo​nocytogenes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio ​cholerae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Listeria monocytogenes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Listeria monocytogenes</strong></p><p>Fever, headache, abdominal pain, diarrhoea following consumption of refridgerated food is suggestive of <strong>food poisoning</strong> caused by<strong> <em>Listeria monocytogenes.</em></strong></p>\n<p><em>Listeria monocytogenes</em> can <strong>survive at</strong> refrigerator temperatures (<strong>4°C</strong>), under conditions of low pH and high salt. So it is able to overcome food preservation and safety barriers, making it an important foodborne pathogen. </p>\n<p><strong>Healthy persons</strong> exposed to<em> L. monocytogenes</em> in food may develop a mild, self-limiting <strong>febrile gastroenteritis</strong> lasting 1–3 days. This develops after an<strong> incubation period </strong>of<strong> 6–48 hours</strong>. Symptoms include fever, chills, headache, myalgias, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. <strong>Immunocompromised</strong> individuals can develop <strong>meningoencephalitis, bacteremia</strong>, or focal infections.</p>\n<p>The drug of choice is high dose <strong>intravenous ampicillin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3249","text":"Multiple abscesses with di​scharging sinuses, demons‌trating sulphur granules ​in pus, are caused by","raw_text":"Multiple abscesses with discharging sinuses, demonstrating sulphur granules in pus, are caused by","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actin​omyces","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Noca​rdia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salmo​nella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tula​remia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Actinomyces","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Actinomyces</strong></p><p><strong>Multiple abscesses</strong> with <strong>discharging sinuses</strong>, demonstrating <strong>sulfur granules</strong> in pus, are characteristic of actinomycosis caused by <strong><em>Actinomyces</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Actinomycosis</strong> is a chronic granulomatous infection characterized by <strong>indurated swellings </strong>in the connective tissue with <strong>suppuration</strong> and discharge of <strong>sulfur</strong> <strong>granules.</strong> The <strong>most common</strong> causative agent is <em><strong>Actinomyces israelli</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>Various clinical forms of actinomycosis include cervicofacial, thoracic, abdominal, and pelvic actinomycosis. Among them, <strong>oral-cervicofacial</strong> actinomycosis is most common, presenting as a soft tissue swelling, abscess, or mass lesion involving the <strong>angle</strong> of the <strong>jaw</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1948","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi‌ng Nocardia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding Nocardia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are​ aerobes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are ​acid-fast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are ​commensals","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They prod​uce urease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They are commensals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They are commensals</strong></p><p><em><strong>Nocardia</strong></em> are <strong>free-living</strong> organisms and not commensals.</p>\n<p><em>Nocardia</em> are <strong>aerobic</strong> Gram-positive filamentous bacilli. They are non-sporing. They are considered <strong>weakly acid-fast</strong>,<strong> </strong>determined by Kinyoun's acid-fast staining technique. They produce <strong>urease</strong> and are catalase positive. They are found in the soil.  <strong><em>Nocardia asteroides</em></strong> is the Nocardia species which is<strong> most commonly</strong> involved in <strong>human disease </strong>(cutaneous and systemic). The<strong> paraffin bait </strong>technique is used for the <strong>isolation</strong> of Nocardia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0947","text":"Identify the organism from​ the given image.","raw_text":"Identify the organism from the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacter​ium leprae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Actinomyce​s israelli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nocardia ​asteroides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nocardia asteroides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6a19ff6ea5514bacb08cbe9b0f03a6b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nocardia asteroides</strong></p><p>The organism shown in the image is<strong><em> Nocardia asteroides</em></strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Nocardia</em> species are <strong>weakly</strong> <strong>acid-fast filamentous </strong>organisms with <strong>hyphae-like branching</strong> on microscopy. They are aerobic, gram-positive, catalase-positive, and urease-positive. They are weakly acid-fast because they stain with carbolfuchsin and retain when decolourized with 1–4% sulfuric acid instead of the stronger acid-alcohol decolorant.</p>\n<p>Infection follows <strong>traumatic inoculation</strong> of the organism into the subcutaneous tissue from soil or vegetable sources. They can present with a characteristic <strong>powdery </strong>substance that appears as gritty or necrotic material on the skin or within soft tissues. Accompanying this are multiple <strong>discharging sinuses</strong>, which are abnormal openings that form as the body attempts to expel infected material. These sinuses can drain purulent, often foul-smelling discharge typically associated with localised abscesses, and the discharge may be viscous, sometimes containing necrotic tissue.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong><em>Mycobacteria</em> </strong>species are acid-fast but not have branching filaments.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em><strong>Actinomyces</strong></em> species are not acid-fast. They are Gram-positive arranged in a sun-ray appearance. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6609","text":"A patient presents with a ​mobile, warm to touch nod‌ule in the neck. On the a​naerobic culture of the b​iopsy material from this ‍swelling, it showed colon‍ies as shown in the image​ which had gram-positive ‌branching rods on stainin​g. Which of the following‌ is the drug of choice in‌ this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a mobile, warm to touch nodule in the neck. On the anaerobic culture of the biopsy material from this swelling, it showed colonies as shown in the image which had gram-positive branching rods on staining. Which of the following is the drug of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxyc​ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cotrim​oxazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Penic​illin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical​ drainage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Penicillin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a14d984b5d24a4f811f5ab034964c3d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8baebbe837aa478ab1eb3abf7a417457x401x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71207f08b1304388b3c728137772ec09x700x460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Penicillin</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>molar tooth colonies </strong>that are characteristic of <em><strong>Actinomyces</strong></em> species. The given clinical scenario of a mobile, warm to touch nodule in the neck describes <strong>cervicofacial actinomycosis</strong> also known as 'lumpy jaw syndrome'. <strong>Penicillin</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for actinomyces infection.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><strong>Actinomyces</strong></em> are <strong>Gram-positive anaerobic bacilli</strong> that produce <strong>molar tooth colonies</strong> on incubation for 72 hours on <strong>brain-heart infusion agar.</strong> They cause a chronic suppurative and granulomatous infection called<strong> actinomycosis</strong>. It can present as one of the following <strong>clinical types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Cervicofacial </strong>type- Fluctuant <strong>mass in the</strong> <strong>jaw</strong> area producing draining <strong>fistulas.</strong> </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Thoracic</strong> type- Pulmonary infection with <strong>fever, cough,</strong> and purulent <strong>sputum.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Abdominal</strong> type- Occurs following <strong>ruptured appendix</strong> or ulcer. It may spread throughout the peritoneum and involve multiple organs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Actinomycosis can spread to bones and surrounding tissue and produce multiple discharging sinuses. Treatment requires prolonged administration of <strong>penicillin</strong> for <strong>6–12 months</strong>. In penicillin-allergic patients, clindamycin or erythromycin is effective. Surgical<strong> excision</strong> and <strong>drainage</strong> may be required in some cases.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The given image shows cervicofacial actinomycosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The given image shows a patient with multiple cutaneous lesions caused by <em>Actinomyces</em>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1647","text":"Which of the following tox​ins act by inhibiting elo‌ngation factor -2? Pseudo​monas exotoxin A Botulinu​m toxin Shiga like toxin ‍Diphtheria toxin Anthrax ‍toxin","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following toxins act by inhibiting elongation factor -2?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pseudomonas exotoxin A</li>\n<li>Botulinum toxin</li>\n<li>Shiga like toxin</li>\n<li>Diphtheria toxin</li>\n<li>Anthrax toxin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 &amp;a​mp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>Both<strong> diphtheria toxin</strong> and <strong>pseudomonas exotoxin</strong> act by inhibiting<strong> elongation factor-2(EF-2).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6126","text":"The most common clinical f​orm of actinomycosis is","raw_text":"The most common clinical form of actinomycosis is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdo​minal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervic​ofacial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pel​vic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thor​acic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervicofacial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f6374b2baf364c0da62e3435d29f252fx700x1046.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervicofacial</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> clinical form of actinomycosis is <strong>cervicofacial.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Actinomycosis</strong> is a chronic granulomatous infection characterized by indurated swellings, mainly in the <strong>connective</strong> <strong>tissue</strong>, suppuration, and discharge of <strong>sulphur</strong> <strong>granules.</strong> The most common causative agent is <em>Actinomycetes israelli.</em></p>\n<p>Various clinical forms of actinomycosis involves <strong>cervicofacial</strong>, <strong>thoracic</strong>, <strong>abdominal</strong>, and <strong>pelvic</strong> actinomycosis. Among them, actinomycosis most frequently occurs as <strong>oral-cervicofacial </strong>form presenting as a soft tissue swelling, abscess, or mass lesion involving the <strong>angle</strong> of <strong>jaw</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7a7KbrBh","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"8_antenatal_investigations","title":"8.Antenatal Investigations","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC4557","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about no‌n-invasive prenatal testi​ng?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about non-invasive prenatal testing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It uses cell-free circulat​ing fetal DNA in the mate‌rnal serum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used for the diagnos​is of trisomy 13, 18 and ‌21","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can be performed after ​10 weeks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is done after the routi​ne first trimester ultras‌ound assessment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is used for the diagnosis of trisomy 13, 18 and 21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is used for the diagnosis of trisomy 13, 18 and 21</strong></p><p>Non-invasive prenatal testing (<strong>NIPT</strong>) is used for the <strong>screening</strong> of <strong>trisomy 13, 18</strong> and <strong>21</strong> and <strong>Turner's</strong> syndrome. It is not a diagnostic test.</p>\n<p>It is a novel genetic test that uses <strong>cell-free circulating fetal DNA </strong>in the<strong> maternal serum</strong> for screening of chromosomal anomalies. </p>\n<p>DNA from the fetus is found circulating in the maternal blood in two forms - either inside the intact fetal cells or as cell-free DNA from the breakdown of placental fetal cells. In NIPT, the cell-free chromosome fragments are measured and the quantitative differences from normal fetuses are noted.</p>\n<p>NIPT can be performed <strong>after 10 weeks</strong> of gestation and is recommended after the routine first-trimester ultrasound assessment in <strong>high-risk pregnancies</strong> or if either <strong>1st or 2nd-trimester</strong> screening becomes <strong>positive.</strong> Its disadvantage is that it is very <strong>expensive</strong> and it can be used for screening of only a few chromosomal anomalies such as trisomy 13, 18, and 21 and it doesn't analyse the entire karyotype.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9208","text":"A pregnant woman has a fet​us with Down's syndrome. ‌Triple test done at 15-16​ weeks will have ______.","raw_text":"A pregnant woman has a fetus with Down's syndrome. Triple test done at 15-16 weeks will have ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low MSAFP, high hCG, low U​E3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High MSAFP, low hCG, high ​UE3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low MSAFP, high hCG, high ​UE3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High MSAFP, low hCG, low U​E3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low MSAFP, high hCG, low UE3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low MSAFP, high hCG, low UE3</strong></p><p>The triple test findings in pregnancies with fetal <strong>Down’s syndrome</strong> are characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low MSAFP</strong> (maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein) levels, approximately 0.7 MoM (multiples of median).</li>\n<li><strong>Low unconjugated</strong> <strong>estriol</strong> levels, approximately 0.8 MoM.</li>\n<li><strong>High hCG</strong> levels, approximately 2.0 MoM.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The triple test is done between <strong>15-18 weeks</strong> for the diagnosis of Down's syndrome. </p>\n<p>Various antenatal screening tests for aneuploidy are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 668px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 213px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Dual test (1<sup>st</sup> trimester)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Triple test (2<sup>nd</sup> trimester)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Quadruple test (2<sup>nd</sup> trimester)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 213px; height: 18px;\">Beta HCG </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px; height: 18px;\">Beta HCG </td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 18px;\">Beta HCG </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 213px; height: 18px;\">PAPPA </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px; height: 18px;\">Unconjugated estriol </td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 18px;\">Unconjugated estriol </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 213px; height: 18px;\">This can be combined with nuchal translucency by USG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px; height: 18px;\">MSAFP </td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 18px;\">MSAFP </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36.6562px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 204px; height: 36.6562px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 36.6562px;\">Serum <strong>inhibin A</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>First-trimester screening with biochemical analytes <strong>(↓) PAPP-A and (↑) hCG and USG measurement of NT (↑)</strong> can improve the detection rate (87%) of Down’s syndrome. For confirmation, a prenatal genetic study (CVS, amniocentesis or cordo\u0002centesis) has to be performed.</p>\n<p>Second-trimester screening (quad screening) at 15–18 weeks: <strong>(↓) MSAFP, (↓) uE3, (↑) inhibin A and (↑) hCG</strong> can detect trisomy 21 in 85% of cases with a false-positive rate of 0.9%. When it is combined with first-trimester screening, the detection rate is increased to 95%.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0558","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the quadr‌uple screening test?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the quadruple screening test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhi​bin B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Unconjugated​ Estriol (E3)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​FP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"h​CG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibin B</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibin B </strong>is not a component assayed under the quad-screening test. <strong>Dimeric inhibin-A</strong> is evaluated in this screening test.</p>\n<p><strong>Quadruple testing</strong> is done in the <strong>second trimester </strong>between<strong> 15-21 weeks </strong>of gestation. It comprises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal serum AFP</li>\n<li>Human chorionic gonadotropin</li>\n<li>Unconjugated estriol (E3)</li>\n<li>Dimeric inhibin A</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following table summarizes the findings of the quad-screening test in various conditions.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Parameter</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Trisomy 21</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Trisomy 18</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Neural tube defect or abdominal wall defect</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MSAFP</strong></td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>β hCG</strong></td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Unconjugated estriol</strong></td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inhibin A</strong></td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Normal/Low</td>\n<td>Normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Various antenatal screening tests for aneuploidy are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 336.812px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 187px; height: 59px;\"><strong>Dual test (1<sup>st</sup> trimester)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 59px;\"><strong>Triple test (2<sup>nd</sup> trimester)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 59px;\"><strong>Quadruple test <br/>(2<sup>nd </sup>trimester)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 187px; height: 46px; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>1. Beta HCG <br/><br/>2. PAPPA (Pregnancy-associated plasma protein A)<br/><br/>This can be combined with nuchal translucency by USG</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 46px; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>1. Beta HCG <br/><br/>2. Unconjugated estriol <br/><br/>3. MSAFP  </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 46px; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>1. Beta HCG <br/><br/>2. Unconjugated estriol <br/><br/>3. MSAFP <br/><br/>4. Serum inhibin </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4285","text":"Quadruple testing is done ​during:","raw_text":"Quadruple testing is done during:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9-11 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12-14​ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15-20​ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"22-28​ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 15-20 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 15-20 weeks</strong></p><p>The <strong>quadruple screening test</strong> is ideally done in the second trimester, during <strong>15-20 weeks</strong> of gestation. The most <strong>accurate</strong> values are seen during <strong>16-18 weeks</strong> of gestation. </p>\n<p>It is the measurement of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP), unconjugated estriol, hCG, and inhibin-A. </p>\n<p>In Down's syndrome (trisomy 21) or Edward's syndrome (trisomy 18), quadruple-marker screenings do not provide any added benefit over first-trimester screenings. It is generally used as a stand-alone test if screening is done for the first time in the second trimester or if first-trimester screening is not available.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 410px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Trisomy 21</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Trisomy 18</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Neural tube defect or abdominal wall defect</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Trisomy 13/ Patau syndrome</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>MSAFP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"><strong>High</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"> Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>β hCG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><strong>High</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"> Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Unconjugated estriol</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"> Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Inhibin A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\"><strong>High</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Normal/Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"> <strong>High</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0361","text":"Results of the quadruple s​creening test are tabulat‌ed below for three congen​ital anomalies. Choose th‌e correctly matched set. ‌Parameter 1 2 3 Maternal ‌serum alpha-fetoprotein (‌MSAFP) Low Low High Human​ chorionic gonadotropin (​β-hCG) High Low Normal Un​conjugated estriol Low Lo‍w Normal Inhibin A High N​ormal/Low Normal","raw_text":"<p>Results of the quadruple screening test are tabulated below for three congenital anomalies. Choose the correctly matched set.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 439px; height: 203px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.9375px; text-align: center; height: 19px;\"><strong>Parameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.0625px; text-align: center; height: 19px;\"><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center; height: 19px;\"><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center; height: 19px;\"><strong>3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.9375px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.0625px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.9375px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\"> Human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.0625px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center; height: 50px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.9375px; text-align: center; height: 41px;\">Unconjugated estriol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.0625px; text-align: center; height: 41px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center; height: 41px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center; height: 41px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.9375px; text-align: center; height: 44px;\">Inhibin A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.0625px; text-align: center; height: 44px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center; height: 44px;\">Normal/Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px; text-align: center; height: 44px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1- Trisomy 21; 2- Trisomy ​18; 3- Neural tube defect‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1- Trisomy 18; 2- Trisomy ​21; 3- Neural tube defect‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1- Trisomy 21; 2- Neural t​ube defect; 3- Trisomy 18‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1- Trisomy 18; 2- Trisomy ​13; 3- Neural tube defect‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1- Trisomy 21; 2- Trisomy 18; 3- Neural tube defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1- Trisomy 21; 2- Trisomy 18; 3- Neural tube defect</strong></p><p>Quadruple testing is done in the <strong>second trimester between 15-21 weeks </strong>of gestation and involves the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal serum alpha fetal protein (MSAFP) </li>\n<li>β hCG</li>\n<li>Unconjugated estriol</li>\n<li>Inhibin A</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 522px; height: 260px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Parameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.09375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Trisomy 21/ Downs syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Trisomy 18/ Edward syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Neural tube defect or abdominal wall defect</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>MSAFP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.09375px; text-align: center;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.25px; text-align: center;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>High</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>β hCG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.09375px; text-align: center;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.25px; text-align: center;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953125px; text-align: center;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Unconjugated estriol</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.09375px; text-align: center;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.25px; text-align: center;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953125px; text-align: center;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Inhibin A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.09375px; text-align: center;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.25px; text-align: center;\">Normal/Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953125px; text-align: center;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9799","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is an increase‌d level of alpha fetoprot​ein seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is an increased level of alpha fetoprotein seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gestational trophoblastic ​diseases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Molar p​regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Under estimated gestationa​l age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spontaneou​s abortion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Under estimated gestational age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Under estimated gestational age</strong></p><p>In underestimated gestational age, there is an increased level of maternal serum alpha fetoprotein (MSAFP).</p>\n<p><strong>Elevated</strong> levels of MSAFP are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Underestimated gestational age</li>\n<li>Severe oligohydramnios</li>\n<li>Multifetal pregnancy</li>\n<li>Neural tube defects</li>\n<li>Abdominal wall defects- Omphalocele, Gastroschisis</li>\n<li>Hepatoma</li>\n<li>Endodermal sinus tumour</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reduced</strong> levels of MSAFP are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Overestimated gestational age</li>\n<li>Overweight mother</li>\n<li>Gestational trophoblastic diseases</li>\n<li>Spontaneous abortion</li>\n<li>Trisomy 18 and 21 </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5701","text":"Increased maternal serum a​lpha-fetoprotein is detec‌ted in a primigravida. Wh​at is the next step in ma‌nagement?","raw_text":"Increased maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein is detected in a primigravida. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repeat measurement of MSAF​P at a later date","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Targeted ​sonography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amnioc​entesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Termination ​of pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Targeted sonography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Targeted sonography</strong></p><p>The next best step is <strong>targeted sonography</strong>. Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) levels are only used as a screening test and must be followed by a diagnostic test.</p>\n<p><strong>Amniocentesis</strong> is a diagnostic test for amniotic fluid AFP measurement. It was once considered the standard for the diagnosis of open neural tube disorders but has been <strong>replaced</strong> in most centers <strong>by</strong> <strong>targeted sonography.</strong></p>\n<p>Defects in fetal integuments, such as ventral wall and <strong>neural tube defects</strong>, permit fetal AFP to leak into the amnionic fluid. This results in <strong>increased</strong> <strong>MSAFP</strong> levels. This is detected on screening performed from <strong>15 to 20 weeks</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 303\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Conditions with <strong>elevated MSAFP</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wrong gestational age</li>\n<li>Neural tube defects</li>\n<li>Multiple pregnancies</li>\n<li>Renal anomalies</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conditions with <strong>decreased MSAFP</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trisomies (Down's syndrome)</li>\n<li>Gestational trophoblastic disease.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5766","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‌ alpha fetoprotein?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding alpha fetoprotein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MSAFP is detected at 16-18​ weeks of gestation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic patients have inc​reased AFP levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MSAFP is unrelated to peri​od of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lowest fetal AFP is seen a​round 13 weeks of gestati‌on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MSAFP is detected at 16-18 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MSAFP is detected at 16-18 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>The maternal serum AFP screening is generally performed from <strong>15-20 weeks</strong> of gestation. Hence, it is detected in this period.</p>\n<p>Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is the major serum protein in the embryo and fetus. It is produced by the yolk sac and fetal liver. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Diabetic</strong> patients have <strong>low</strong> levels of AFP. </p>\n<p>Option C: Gestational age influences the <strong>MSAFP</strong> levels. The maternal serum concentration <strong>increases</strong> by approximately 15% per week during the <strong>second trimester</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: At about <strong>13 weeks</strong> of gestation, the <strong>AFP</strong> concentration in the <strong>fetal</strong> serum and amniotic fluid are <strong>highest</strong>, after which it declines. Conversely, AFP is found in steadily increasing quantities in maternal serum after 12 weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9797","text":"During genetic counseling,​ a pregnant woman states ‌that both she and her hus​band have sickle cell tra‌it. You offer the option ‌of chorionic villus sampl‌ing for prenatal diagnosi‌s. What is the best time ​to perform this procedure​?","raw_text":"During genetic counseling, a pregnant woman states that both she and her husband have sickle cell trait. You offer the option of chorionic villus sampling for prenatal diagnosis. What is the best time to perform this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-8 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7-9 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9-10 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11-13​ weeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11-13 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11-13 weeks</strong></p><p>The best time to perform chorionic villous sampling is <strong>11-13 weeks.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 410px;\" width=\"589\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 579px;\">\n<p><strong>Chorionic villus sampling:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.015625px;\">Study material</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.984375px;\">\n<p>Chorionic villi from trophoblastic cells</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.015625px;\">\n<p>Time</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.984375px;\">\n<p>10 to 13 weeks<strong> </strong>(transcervically)</p>\n<p>10 weeks to term (transabdominally)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.015625px;\">\n<p>Indications</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.984375px;\">\n<p>To detect chromosomal abnormalities such as Down syndrome.</p>\n<p>To detect inborn errors of metabolism like phenylketonuria.</p>\n<p>For DNA analysis in hemoglobinopathies, thalassemia, and sickle cell anemia.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.015625px;\">Complications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.984375px;\">\n<p>Abortion (CVS&gt; Amniocentesis)</p>\n<p>Limb reduction defects and oro-mandibular defects (when done before 10 weeks)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.015625px;\">Drawback</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.984375px;\">\n<p>Not suitable for the detection of neural tube defects.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9798","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about amniocente‌sis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about amniocentesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Traditionally done between​ 16-20 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Limb reduction defect is a​ known complication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Down's syndrome can be dia​gnosed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Approximately 20 to 30 mL ​is collected for fetal ka‌ryotyping","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Limb reduction defect is a known complication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Limb reduction defect is a known complication</strong></p><p><strong>Limb reduction defect</strong> is a complication of <strong>chorionic villous sampling</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 378px;\" width=\"666\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 656px;\"><strong>Amniocentesis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Study material</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">Amniotic fluid with amniocytes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Time</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">16-20 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Early amniocentesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">\n<p>11-14 weeks (associated with increased fetal loss, foot deformities)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Diagnostic indications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">Sex-linked disorders, karyotyping, inborn errors of metabolism, neural tube defects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Therapeutic indications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">Abortion induction, decompression of uterus in acute hydramnios, amnioinfusion in oligohydramnios</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Procedure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">\n<p>Under direct sonographic guidance, a <strong>20–22 gauge spinal needle</strong> is directed into a clear pocket of amniotic fluid while avoiding the fetus and umbilical cord.</p>\n<p>Approximately 20 to 30 mL of fluid is collected.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.015625px;\">Complication</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.984375px;\">Chorioamnionitis, premature Rupture Of Membranes (PROM), fetal trauma/loss, fetomaternal hemorrhage.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5735","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen​ts with 6 weeks of amenor‌rhea and a positive urine​ pregnancy test. She stat‌es her brother had cystic‌ fibrosis and asks if her‌ child might have the sam‌e. Which of the following​ methods would not be use​ful for prenatal diagnosi​s of this condition?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman presents with 6 weeks of amenorrhea and a positive urine pregnancy test. She states her brother had cystic fibrosis and asks if her child might have the same. Which of the following methods would not be useful for prenatal diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amnioc​entesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cordoc​entesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chorionic villous sampling​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal sk​in biopsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fetal skin biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fetal skin biopsy</strong></p><p>Fetal skin biopsy is not a recommended method of prenatal diagnosis of cystic fibrosis. It is done to detect inherited skin conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ichthyosis</li>\n<li>Epidermolysis bullosa</li>\n<li>Oculocutaneous albinism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Invasive procedures for <strong>prenatal diagnosis</strong> of<strong> CFTR gene mutations</strong> in cystic fibrosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chorionic villus sampling (CVS)</li>\n<li>Amniocentesis</li>\n<li>Cordocentesis or percutaneous umbilical blood sampling (PUBS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5690","text":"A G2P1 woman at 35 weeks o​f pregnancy presents with‌ complaints of decreased ​fetal movement. What is t‌he next step in managemen‌t?","raw_text":"A G2P1 woman at 35 weeks of pregnancy presents with complaints of decreased fetal movement. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obser​vation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-str​ess test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Biophysic​al profile","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Induce​ labour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-stress test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-stress test</strong></p><p>In case of reduced fetal movements, the first step of management is to do a <strong>non-stress test</strong> (NST).</p>\n<p>Interpretation of NST:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reactive<strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>≥<strong>2 accelerations </strong>of<strong> &gt;15 beats/minute</strong> above the baseline and <strong>longer than 15 seconds</strong> in duration are present in a<strong> 20-minute observation</strong></li>\n<li>Reassure the patient and send back home</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-reactive - biophysical profile (BPP) has to be assessed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9435","text":"The CTG shown in the image​ below may be seen in whi‌ch of the following condi​tions?","raw_text":"The CTG shown in the image below may be seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head com​pression","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cord com​pression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal ​hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nor​mal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Head compression","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01c817aaf54f49cfa4672641761b4a2d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Head compression</strong></p><p>The given CTG shows<strong> early deceleration </strong>which is seen in cases of <strong>head</strong> <strong>compression</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9801","text":"A 35-year-old G2P1 at 38 w​eeks of gestation came to‌ the labor room with comp​laints of decreased fetal‌ movements. Non-stress te‌st was done and late dece‌lerations were noted. Wha‌t does this indicate?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old G2P1 at 38 weeks of gestation came to the labor room with complaints of decreased fetal movements. Non-stress test was done and late decelerations were noted. What does this indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head com​pression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cord com​pression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal ​hypoxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Breech pr​esentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fetal hypoxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e7ae8243928483a95ce260ed45c1a06x1200x1203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fetal hypoxia</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>fetal hypoxia. </strong>Late decelerations are seen on an NST in this condition.</p>\n<p>Deceleration is a decrease in fetal heart rate below the baseline by 15 bpm for more than 15 seconds. <strong>Absent variability</strong> <strong>of FHR</strong> with <strong>late decelerations</strong> is an indication for <strong>immediate Cesarean section</strong>.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5695","text":"A pregnant woman brings he​r son to the hospital for‌ routine immunization. Wh​ile awaiting his turn, sh‌e realizes that her seat ‌is stained with blood. Sh‌e is rushed to the nearby‌ antenatal clinic. Pulse ​- 96/min, BP - 110/80 mmH​g. Vaginal bleeding is no​ted and her NST is shown ‍below. What is the likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman brings her son to the hospital for routine immunization. While awaiting his turn, she realizes that her seat is stained with blood. She is rushed to the nearby antenatal clinic. Pulse - 96/min, BP - 110/80 mmHg. Vaginal bleeding is noted and her NST is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placent​a previa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasa ​previa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Battledor​e placenta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abruptio ​placentae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vasa previa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b45d5d5ef2b74fec86ff586cd383bbdb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82894d89bd5541d581e68b9bd6aba8abx1280x746.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vasa previa</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the NST showing a <strong>sinusoidal pattern</strong> point towards a diagnosis of<strong> vasa previa. </strong></p>\n<p>Vasa previa is a condition where <strong>fetal vessels</strong> travel within the membranes and <strong>overlie</strong> the <strong>cervical os</strong>. Hence, there can be <strong>bleeding</strong> during <strong>cervical dilatation</strong> or membrane rupture. </p>\n<p>Fetal mortality is high due to <strong>fetal exsanguination</strong>. When <strong>bleeding</strong> is present in vasa previa, an <strong>emergency cesarean</strong> section is indicated.</p>\n<p>Causes of<strong> sinusoidal pattern on NST</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe fetal anemia - Rh isoimmunisation, vasa previa, fetomaternal hemorrhage, or twin to twin transfusion</li>\n<li>Opioids - meperidine, morphine, alphaprodine, butorphanol</li>\n<li>Amnionitis</li>\n<li>Fetal distress </li>\n<li>Umbilical cord occlusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7LGBGXZN","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"8_non_infectious_disorders_of_cornea","title":"8.Non Infectious Disorders Of Cornea","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA9752","text":"Deposition of which of the‌ following is responsible‍ for band-shaped keratopa‍thy?","raw_text":"Deposition of which of the following is responsible for band-shaped keratopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amy‌loid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cal‌cium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Monopolys‌accharide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chole‌sterol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eefde464ecf143fa91fc3a07a3ae5d00x483x427.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcium</strong></p><p><strong>Band-shaped keratopathy</strong> is caused by pathological <strong>calcium deposition</strong> on the cornea in the form of a band.</p>\n<p>On exam, there is a <strong>white band-like formation </strong>of calcium across the corneal surface <strong>with irregular borders </strong>and a <strong>peripheral clearing</strong> of cornea between the calcium deposits and the limbus. The calcium deposition is typically found beneath the epithelial layer within <strong>Bowman’s layer</strong> but may extend into the anterior stroma.</p>\n<p>Once the visual axis starts to get involved, there will be progressive loss of vision. Recurrence is common unless the predisposing cause is identified and treated.</p>\n<p>Local causes such as chronic ocular irritation or inflammatory conditions and systemic diseases that elevate serum calcium levels are the main causes for band-shaped keratopathy. It can rarely have hereditary causes as well.</p>\n<p>Image given below shows band-shaped keratopathy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9753","text":"A patient was with the giv‌en finding has been havin‍g painless and slowly pro‍gressive vision loss for ‌the past 10 years. Which ‌of the following diseases​ is not associated with t‌he given condition?","raw_text":"A patient was with the given finding has been having painless and slowly progressive vision loss for the past 10 years. Which of the following diseases is not associated with the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile rheumatoid arthri‌tis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic re‌nal failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Systemic lupus erythematosus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1cb109a5bc8f4c2fbcd79c5eff79ccab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></p><p>Based on the given clinical scenario and ocular finding depicted here, it is suggestive of <strong>band-shaped keratopathy, </strong>and <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> is<strong> not </strong>associated with this condition.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Band-shaped keratopathy (BSK) is due to <strong>calcium deposition</strong>, primarily in the form of hydroxyapatite, in the epithelial basement membrane, Bowman's layer, and the anterior stroma. Initially, these deposits are grey but may become chalky-white as the degeneration progresses.</p>\n<p>lt typically presents as a <strong>band-shaped opacity</strong> in the interpalpebral zone with a clear interval between the ends of the band and the limbus. The condition begins at the periphery and gradually progresses towards the centre. The surface of this opaque band is stippled due to holes in the calcium plaques in the area of <strong>nerve canals </strong>of <strong>Bowman's membrane.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of band-shaped keratopathy include <strong>local causes</strong> such as <strong>chronic ocular irritation </strong>or<strong> inflammatory</strong> conditions and<strong> systemic diseases</strong> that <strong>elevate serum calcium</strong> levels:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 146px; width: 558px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.309px;\"><strong>Local causes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.691px;\"><strong>Systemic diseases</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.309px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic anterior uveitis (especially in <strong>Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Interstitial keratitis</li>\n<li>Superficial keratitis</li>\n<li>Phthisis bulbi</li>\n<li>End-stage glaucoma</li>\n<li>Silicone oil left in the aphakic eye</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.691px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong></li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatemia</li>\n<li>Paget disease</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic renal failure</strong></li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medical management- <strong>Chelation</strong> using topical <strong>ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) 1.5–3.0%</strong> </li>\n<li>Surgical management- Photorefractive keratectomy (PRK) using excimer laser or manual superficial keratectomy </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9754","text":"A 64-year-old woman was br‌ought by her son, during ‍a cataract outreach camp.‍ She gives a history of t‌rauma to her left eye, on‌e year back and a complet​e loss of vision in that ‌eye since then. Her image‌ is given below. Which of‍ the following statement ​is true about her conditi‍on?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old woman was brought by her son, during a cataract outreach camp.  She gives a history of trauma to her left eye, one year back and a complete loss of vision in that eye since then. Her image is given below. Which of the following statement is true about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corneal tattooing is a tre‌atment option in this lad‍y.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The given lesion is an adh‌erent leucoma.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is due to scars involvi‌ng Bowman's layer and sup‍erficial stroma.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Excimer laser phototherape‌utic keratectomy is the c‍urrent treatment of choic‍e.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corneal tattooing is a treatment option in this lady.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/96e71c62bdce44aa91af1c78d68af339.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corneal tattooing is a treatment option in this lady.</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>dense white opacity,</strong> which results from<strong> </strong>the<strong> scarring of more than half of the stroma,</strong><strong>  </strong>characteristic of <strong>leucoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Corneal tattooing is a treatment option in patients with corneal opacities, who have <strong>lost their vision or who do not expect to recover it</strong>. It improves <strong>cosmetic</strong> appearance by altering the white colour of the opacity and blending it to normal eye colour.</p>\n<p>Depending on the density, corneal opacities are graded as nebula, macula and leucoma. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nebular</strong> corneal opacity (fog or mist):\n<ul>\n<li>Hard-to-see corneal scar, due to superficial scars involving Bowman's layer and superficial stroma.</li>\n<li>Slit-lamp detection is required.</li>\n<li>A thin, diffuse nebula covering the pupillary area interferes more with vision than the localised leucoma away from the pupillary area. Nebula produces more discomfort due to blurred image (irregular astigmatism).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Macular</strong> corneal opacity:\n<ul>\n<li>Semi-dense opacity that can be seen with proper illumination.</li>\n<li>Due to scarring involving about half the corneal stroma.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Leucomatous</strong> corneal opacity (leucoma simplex):\n<ul>\n<li>White scar that is easily seen just by looking at the eye.</li>\n<li>Due to scarring of more than half of the stroma </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Adherent</strong> <strong>leucoma</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>It results when healing occurs after perforation of the cornea with incarceration of iris</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary changes</strong> in corneal opacity which may be seen in long-standing cases include hyaline degeneration, calcareous degeneration, pigmentation and atheromatous ulceration.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keratoplasty</strong> is the treatment of choice in leucomatous corneal opacities.</li>\n<li><strong>Optical iridectomy</strong> may be performed in cases with central adherent leucomas</li>\n<li><strong>Phototherapeutic keratectomy (PTK)</strong> performed with excimer laser is useful only in superficial (nebular) corneal opacities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1080","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is least likely t‍o be associated with bila‍teral corneal opacities i‌n a neonate?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is least likely to be associated with bilateral corneal opacities in a neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sclero‌cornea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Birth ‌trauma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peter's‌ anomaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kerato‌globus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Keratoglobus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Keratoglobus</strong></p><p><strong>Keratoglobus</strong> is least likely to be associated with bilateral corneal opacities in a neonate. Here, the cornea is <strong>clear. </strong></p>\n<p>Keratoglobus is a rare noninflammatory, familial disorder marked by generalised thinning and globular protrusion of the cornea. It can be congenital or acquired. The thinning is usually greatest at corneal periphery or midperiphery. It can cause refractive errors, otherwise, cornea is clear, normal angle of anterior chamber, normal intraocular pressure with no optic disc cupping.</p>\n<p>Intrastromal ring segments and cross-linking can be used for management.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B<strong>: Birth injuries </strong>resulting in <strong>Descemet's membrane tears </strong>can also cause corneal opacities.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Peter's anomaly,</strong> also known as iridocorneal adhesions or keratolenticular adhesions, is a posterior corneal defect with an overlying stromal opacity.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Sclerocornea</strong> is a congenital disorder in which the cornea is opaque and resembles the sclera, making the limbus indistinct. The central cornea is clearer than the periphery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1084","text":"What causes neurotrophic k‌eratopathy?","raw_text":"What causes neurotrophic keratopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial ne‌rve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bell's‌ palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trigeminal ‌nerve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Third ne‌rve palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trigeminal nerve palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trigeminal nerve palsy</strong></p><p>Neurotrophic keratopathy is caused by<strong> trigeminal nerve palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lesions of the <strong>fifth cranial nerve</strong> from the<strong> trigeminal nucleus</strong> to the cornea may lead to abnormalities of the normal corneal sensation and trophic stimulation.</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Varicella-zoster (</strong><strong>most common cause)</strong></li>\n<li>Herpes simplex keratitis</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Irradiation to the eye</li>\n<li>Aneurysm/stroke</li>\n<li>Multiple sclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mackie</strong> characterized<strong> three stages</strong> of neurotrophic keratitis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stage I </strong>is characterized<strong> </strong>by an irregular corneal surface, which later develops into an easily recognized punctate keratitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Stage II</strong> is characterized by a frank epithelial defect, which is typically associated with mild anterior stromal oedema and inflammation.</li>\n<li><strong>Stage III</strong> involves stromal melting and occasionally perforation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1085","text":"Paralysis of which of the ‌following nerves is respo‍nsible for exposure kerat‍opathy?","raw_text":"Paralysis of which of the following nerves is responsible for exposure keratopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Facial nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Exposure keratopathy</strong> is caused by paralysis of the <strong>facial nerve</strong>.  </p>\n<p>It is the result of incomplete lid closure (lagophthalmos) resulting in drying of the cornea (despite normal tear production).</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuroparalytic, e.g., <strong>facial nerve palsy</strong></li>\n<li>Reduced muscle tone as in <strong>parkinsonism</strong></li>\n<li>Mechanical such as lid scarring</li>\n<li>Proptosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Neurotrophic keratopathy</strong> is due to <strong>loss of trigeminal nerve innervation</strong> resulting in partial or complete loss of sensation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9755","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is least likely to cau‍se the given condition?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is least likely to cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythromycin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edc8011cdb1345aebb3910baf56ab72b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythromycin</strong></p><p><strong>Erythromycin</strong> is least likely associated with <strong>cornea verticillata/vortex keratopathy</strong>, a whorl-like opacity in corneal epithelium, as shown in the image above. </p>\n<p>Vortex keratopathy occurs due to corneal deposits in the basal epithelium. They are almost always bilateral and are readily recognisable by their distinct appearance as <strong>fine, golden-brown or grey opacities</strong> that branch out from a central whorl, usually across the inferior cornea. Opacity due to drugs is <strong>reversible</strong> upon stopping the drug.</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amiodarone (most common) </strong>(Option A)</li>\n<li><strong>Tamoxifen </strong>(Option C)</li>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n<li><strong>Chloroquine </strong>(Option D)</li>\n<li>Chlorpromazine (rarely)</li>\n<li>Fabry’s disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9756","text":"A 30-year-old albino woman‌ presents with a characte‍ristic finding as shown b‍elow. Which of the follow‌ing statements are true r‌egarding the suspected co​ndition? It is due to the‌ deposition of copper in ‌the Descemet's membrane I‍t requires screening for ​underlying lipid disorder‍ It is seen in patients w‌ith the neurological form‌ of Wilson’s disease If s​een unilaterally it is su​ggestive of ipsilateral c‌arotid stenosis It is als‌o called limbus sign","raw_text":"<p>A 30-year-old albino woman presents with a characteristic finding as shown below. Which of the following statements are true regarding the suspected condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is due to the deposition of copper in the Descemet's membrane</li>\n<li>It requires screening for underlying lipid disorder</li>\n<li>It is seen in patients with the neurological form of Wilson’s disease</li>\n<li>If seen unilaterally it is suggestive of ipsilateral carotid stenosis</li>\n<li>It is also called limbus sign</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Onl‌y 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Only 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2025237224b049a88dbca3340f73cc29.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/994eb1428ad147109b0bb6e68767aff7x1280x622.72.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Only 2</strong></p><p>A 30-year-old individual with corneal changes, as shown above, known as <strong>gerontoxon</strong> or<strong> arcus lipoides</strong>, may <strong>require screening</strong> for<strong> underlying lipid disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Arcus senilis (AS), also known as gerontoxon, arcus lipoides or arcus cornealis, is usually an age-related lipid degeneration of the peripheral cornea that forms a<strong> light grey or bluish ring.</strong> It is the most common peripheral corneal opacity, typically benign, occurring bilaterally and prevalent among the elderly.</p>\n<p>However, when observed in individuals<strong> under 50</strong>, termed <strong>arcus juvenilis</strong>, it may indicate underlying lipid disorder and an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, warranting lipid profile screening. Unilateral cases of arcus senilis may indicate <strong>contralateral carotid artery stenosis</strong> due to decreased ophthalmic blood flow on the ipsilateral side, resulting in protection from forming arcus in the ipsilateral eye.</p>\n<p>The condition results from the leakage of lipoproteins (phospholipids and cholesterol) from limbal capillaries into the corneal stroma, initiating deposition at the superior and inferior cornea. Over time, the ring progresses circumferentially, sparing a clear corneal zone adjacent to the limbus called the <strong>interval of Vogt.</strong></p>\n<p>The following opacities and rings can resemble arcus senilis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kayser- Fleischer (KF) ring</strong>: A dark brown ring caused by the <strong>deposition of excess copper </strong>in <strong>Descemet's membrane</strong>, classically seen in Wilson's disease. It may also be seen in certain chronic, cholestatic liver conditions such as primary biliary cirrhosis. </li>\n<li><strong>Limbus sign: </strong>Caused by <strong>dystrophic calcification, </strong>seen as a \"milky precipitate\" at the corneal limbus. It can sometimes be confused with AS in the elderly population.</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo-gerontoxon:</strong> A greyish peri-limbal band of superficial scarring adjacent to a previously inflamed segment of the limbus resembling AS, seen in vernal keratoconjunctivitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below depicts the Kayser–Fleischer ring and anterior capsule of the crystalline lens (Sunflower cataract) seen in Wilson's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1079","text":"A young male labourer pres‌ents to the casualty with‍ severe bilateral eye pai‍n that woke him from slee‌p. He reports welding the‌ previous afternoon witho​ut a face shield and symp‌toms of photophobia, blur‌ry vision, tearing, and w‍orsening pain in bright l​ight. Examination reveals‍ mild conjunctival inject‌ion and diffuse punctate ‌epithelial erosions on th​e cornea. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young male labourer presents to the casualty with severe bilateral eye pain that woke him from sleep. He reports welding the previous afternoon without a face shield and symptoms of photophobia, blurry vision, tearing, and worsening pain in bright light. Examination reveals mild conjunctival injection and diffuse punctate epithelial erosions on the cornea. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe retin‌al bleaching","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Photoph‌thalmia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infra-red‌ keratitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macula‌r burn","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Photophthalmia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Photophthalmia</strong></p><p>The described corneal findings, with his classic history of welding less than 12 hours prior to presentation, are suggestive of <strong>photophthalmia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Photophthalmia<strong> </strong>is produced due to exposure to short-wavelength <strong>ultraviolet</strong> (UV-B) rays. The <strong>cornea</strong><strong> absorbs</strong> the majority of <strong>UV light</strong> (especially 311 to 290 nm). It results in desquamation of the epi­thelium leading to multiple painful corneal erosions. Causes of of UV keratitis:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 214\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<table style=\"height: 218.586px; width: 440px;\" width=\"547\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.477px; text-align: center; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>Artificial causes </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.93px; text-align: center; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong> Natural causes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 196.188px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.477px; height: 196.188px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arc welding</strong></li>\n<li>Flash from short-circuits,</li>\n<li>Damaged metal halide lamps</li>\n<li>Tanning beds</li>\n<li>UV germicidal lamps.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.93px; height: 196.188px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Exposure to strong solar radiation at high altitudes especially on snow-covered terrain (<strong>snow blindness</strong>) </li>\n<li>Prolonged viewing of a solar eclipse without proper eye protection. </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 214\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The symptoms appear characteristically after a <strong>latent period </strong>of<strong> 4- 5 hours of exposure</strong> to ultraviolet light. There is intense burning pain, foreign body sensation, and watering of the eyes. There may be associated marked photophobia and blepharospasm. On slit-lamp examination, corneal epithelium shows a breach in continuity and takes up fluorescein stain. </p>\n<p>Management comprises cold compresses, lubricant eye drops and bandaging eyes for 24 hours with antibiotic eye ointment and cycloplegic. Avoid local surface topical anaesthesia. <strong>Crooke’s glass</strong> is a prophylactic aid consisting of a spectacle lens combined with metallic oxides to absorb ultraviolet or infrared rays and should be used by those who are prone to exposure.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Photoretinitis and macular burns are often a result of infrared radiation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9757","text":"Which of the following is ‌a method for long-term pr‍eservation of cornea?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a method for long-term preservation of cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryopres‌ervation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"MK m‌edium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moist ‌chamber","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Organ cult‌ure method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cryopreservation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cryopreservation</strong></p><p><strong>Cryopreservation</strong> is used for <strong>long-term</strong> preservation of cornea, up to 1 year.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9759","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‍infection following corne‍al transplantation in Ind‌ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of infection following corneal transplantation in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus epidermidis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus epidermidis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Staphylococcus epidermidis</strong></em> is the most common cause of <strong>infectious keratitis</strong> after corneal transplantation in <strong>developing</strong> countries.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Pneumococcus</strong></em> spp. and <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em> have been found to be the most common causes of post-corneal transplant infections in <strong>developed</strong> countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1051","text":"Which of the following tec‌hniques is the gold stand‍ard for assessing corneal‍ thickness?","raw_text":"Which of the following techniques is the gold standard for assessing corneal thickness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Confocal ‌microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Binocular ‌keratometry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultrasound‌ pachymetry","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal t‌opography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ultrasound pachymetry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e844b063620047ea9925af27abfd8407x506x253.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ultrasound pachymetry</strong></p><p> The gold standard technique for assessing corneal thickness is <strong>ultrasound pachymetry,</strong> which uses a corneal <strong>pachymeter</strong>.</p>\n<p>Measurement of the corneal thickness (pachymetry) indirectly reflects endothelial function. The <strong>average central corneal thickness</strong> is around <strong>0.5</strong> <strong>mm</strong>, which gradually increases toward the <strong>periphery</strong> to around <strong>0.7 mm</strong>. Normally, as a <strong>diurnal variation</strong>, corneas tend to be <strong>slightly thicker</strong> just after a person awakes in the <strong>morning</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Confocal microscopy</strong> is an imaging technique that allows in vivo examination of corneal structures at high magnification and resolution. It is used to assess corneal nerve morphology and corneal endothelium.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Keratometry</strong> measures the anterior corneal curvature and is traditionally performed with a manual <strong>keratometer</strong>, also known as an <strong>ophthalmometer</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Corneal</strong> <strong>topography</strong> is used to assess the anterior corneal surface, its shape and thickness. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a corneal pachymeter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1054","text":"Your aunt recently shared ‌that it is becoming cumbe‍rsome for her to frequent‍ly change her glasses. Yo‌u suggested a referral an‌d the rapid change in pow​er was diagnosed to be du‌e to keratoconus. The fol‌lowing image shows the re‍sults of one of the inves​tigations done on her. Wh‍at is the name of the inv‌estigation?","raw_text":"Your aunt recently shared that it is becoming cumbersome for her to frequently change her glasses. You suggested a referral and the rapid change in power was diagnosed to be due to keratoconus. The following image shows the results of one of the investigations done on her. What is the name of the investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pachy‌metry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kerat‌ometry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Specular ‌microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal t‌opography","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Corneal topography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/54663919188d422781ea841d0c516367.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/626b23b59bb249889010dcadcea2be85x720x459.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Corneal topography</strong></p><p>The investigation shown in the image is <strong>c</strong><strong>orneal topography, </strong>and it is used for conditions like <strong>keratoconus, </strong>as seen in this clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>Corneal topography captures the shape of the cornea by projecting concentric rings of light onto its anterior surface, forming a Placido disc pattern. the reflected light is analyzed with specialised software to create a detailed map of the corneal curvature. </p>\n<p>Uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detection of corneal ectasia, mainly keratoconus</li>\n<li>To measure <strong>corneal thickness</strong></li>\n<li>Prior to <strong>refractive surgery</strong></li>\n<li>Sometimes prior to contact lens fitting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image shows a <strong>Placido</strong> or <strong>Keratoscopic disc</strong> which is used for corneal surface assessment. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9750","text":"A 20-year-old girl with hi‌gh myopia was referred by‍ the optometrist for furt‍her evaluation. She has a‌ history of recurrent all‌ergic conjunctivitis and ​persistent eye rubbing. A‌n image is given below. W‌hich of the following sta‍tements are true about th​e given condition? It is ‍usually associated with r‌egular astigmatism Cornea‌l topography is the prima​ry diagnostic tool Kerato​plasty is the treatment o‌f choice Vogt's striae an‌d Rizzuti sign are both f‍indings The area of protr​usion is mostly inferotem​poral","raw_text":"<p>A 20-year-old girl with high myopia was referred by the optometrist for further evaluation. She has a history of recurrent allergic conjunctivitis and persistent eye rubbing. An image is given below. Which of the following statements are true about the given condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is usually associated with regular astigmatism</li>\n<li>Corneal topography is the primary diagnostic tool</li>\n<li>Keratoplasty is the treatment of choice</li>\n<li>Vogt's striae and Rizzuti sign are both findings </li>\n<li>The area of protrusion is mostly inferotemporal </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 4, 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfcb1963942c49e2b4c19a561f89dc58.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a72670d0754845faa814c5cc4a1bba86x1280x761.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Keratoconus</strong> is a progressive bilateral corneal ectatic disorder.  It manifests as characteristic cone-like steepening of the cornea associated with irregular stromal thinning, resulting in a cone-like bulge (protrusion) and significant loss of vision.</p>\n<p>The pathogenesis is not fully known. Several associations have been identified, which include <strong>rigid contact lens</strong> wear, <strong>chronic eye rubbing</strong>, Down's syndrome, <strong>atopic</strong> disease, Leber's congenital amaurosis, and connective tissue disease.</p>\n<p>It is a non-inflammatory disease. With the exception of the significant loss of vision, other classic signs of inflammation are <strong>not</strong> usually present. The asymmetrical thinning and the highly irregular corneal topography often causes <strong>significant myopia</strong> and<strong> irregular astigmatism.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Corneal topography</strong> is the primary diagnostic tool for KCN detection. </p>\n<p>Whereas <strong>keratoplasty</strong>, historically has been the mainstay of treatment for keratoconus, it is now generally reserved for eyes that are unable to achieve functional vision with other surgical or non-surgical modalities and is only considered only as a last resort, in very advanced disease. It is the <strong>definitive</strong> treatment for keratoconus, <strong>not the treatment of choice</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9751","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a treatment option in‍ advanced keratoconus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a treatment option in advanced keratoconus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toric soft ‌contact lens","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vaulting scleral contact l‌ens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rigid gas permeable contac‌t lenses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collagen cr‌oss-linking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Toric soft contact lens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Toric soft contact lens</strong></p><p>Vision correction with <strong>spectacles</strong> with <strong>spherical/toric soft contact lenses</strong> is suboptimal and only applicable to the <strong>early</strong> stages of keratoconus, not in the advanced stages.</p>\n<p>Keratoconus is a progressive, non-inflammatory, bilateral corneal ectasia characterized by cone-like steepening due to irregular stromal thinning. This leads to a conical corneal protrusion, significant vision loss, myopia and irregular astigmatism. </p>\n<p>The available options for the management of keratoconus are highly dependent on the stage of the disease and its progression. If the disease is stabilized (no progression), the emphasis is given to correcting the vision. If the disease is progressing, the emphasis is on slowing (arresting) the progression.</p>\n<p>Treatment options in moderate-to-advanced keratoconus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rigid gas-permeable contact lenses</strong> and <strong>vaulting scleral lenses</strong> are the mainstays of vision treatment for moderate to advanced KCN. Their main advantage is the creation of a tear pool between the lens and the cornea, which naturally neutralizes the ocular aberrations associated with keratoconus. (Option B and C)</li>\n<li><strong>Intra-corneal ring segments (INTACS)</strong>- small curved PMMA ring or set of rings that are implanted in the cornea to help flatten corneal curvature to improve vision.</li>\n<li><strong>Corneal transplantation</strong>- Deep anterior lamellar keratoplasty (DALK) and penetrating keratoplasty (PKP)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Corneal collagen cross-linking</strong> with <strong>riboflavin</strong> (CXL or C3R) and <strong>u</strong><strong>ltraviolet-A rays</strong> arrest the progression of the disease. (Option D)</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1083","text":"A 40-year old male with pr‌ogressive loss of vision ‍in his left eye was diagn‍osed with against-the-rul‌e astigmatism. Examinatio‌n revealed peripheral cor​neal thinning, but there ‌was no inflammation. Whic‌h of the following is lea‍st likely to be the cause​?","raw_text":"A 40-year old male with progressive loss of vision in his left eye was diagnosed with against-the-rule astigmatism. Examination revealed peripheral corneal thinning, but there was no inflammation. Which of the following is least likely to be the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terrien's marginal degener‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pellucid marginal degenera‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Del‌len","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral ulcerative kera‌titis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peripheral ulcerative keratitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peripheral ulcerative keratitis</strong></p><p>The examination in a 40-year-old male revealed peripheral corneal thinning with no inflammation, making <strong>peripheral ulcerative keratitis</strong>, an inflammatory condition, the least likely cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Peripheral ulcerative keratitis</strong> is a <strong>destructive</strong> <strong>inflammatory</strong> disease involving the peripheral cornea. It is associated with signs and symptoms of inflammation. The other options are causes of peripheral thinning in a quiet eye. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Terrien's</strong> and <strong>Pellucid marginal degeneration</strong> are <strong>noninflammatory</strong> corneal degenerations. Pellucid degeneration causes bilateral, inferior corneal thinning that leads to marked, irregular, against-the-rule astigmatism. Terrien's degeneration usually begins in the superior cornea. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Dellen</strong> is a <strong>localized</strong> <strong>thinning</strong> of the <strong>peripheral cornea</strong> associated with the <strong>drying of a focal area</strong>. Usually associated with an <strong>adjacent elevated lesion</strong> (e.g., <strong>pinguecula</strong> or a large subconjunctival haemorrhage) that impairs physiological lubrication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6718","text":"A 47-year-old woman presen‌ted with tearing, irritat‍ion, and photophobia of t‍he left eye. On examinati‌on, a paracentral, cresce‌nt-shaped corneal melt wa​s noted. Peripheral ulcer‌ative keratitis (PUK) was‌ suspected. What is the m‍ost likely cause of this ​condition?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman presented with tearing, irritation, and photophobia of the left eye. On examination, a paracentral, crescent-shaped corneal melt was noted. Peripheral ulcerative keratitis (PUK) was suspected. What is the most likely cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong> is commonly associated with peripheral ulcerative keratitis (PUK). It is also the <strong>most common</strong> cause. </p>\n<p>PUK is a group of destructive inflammatory diseases involving the peripheral cornea, characterized by the sloughing of corneal epithelium and keratolysis (corneal melting). It can lead to corneal perforation. The causes can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infectious- bacterial, viral, protozoal, fungal.</li>\n<li>Non-infectious- rheumatoid arthritis, Wegener's granulomatosis, SLE, rosacea, psoriasis etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ophthalmologic manifestations of rheumatoid arthritis (due to type III and IV hypersensitivity reactions):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>dry eye</li>\n<li>scleritis</li>\n<li>stromal keratitis</li>\n<li>peripheral ulcerative keratitis</li>\n<li>paracentral keratolysis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of PUK includes protection of the surface from drying and exposure; systemic corticosteroids and immunomodulatory; use of adjuncts such as amniotic membrane grafts to promote healing; and in some situations, tectonic keratoplasty.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9748","text":"A 54-year-old school teach‌er presented with a chief‍ complaint of pain in the‍ eyes and intermittent bl‌urry vision which was wor‌se in the mornings. Gener​alized corneal haze was n‌oted in both eyes but was‌ more significant in the ‍left. Examination finding​s are given below. What i‍s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old school teacher presented with a chief complaint of pain in the eyes and intermittent blurry vision which was worse in the mornings. Generalized corneal haze was noted in both eyes but was more significant in the left. Examination findings are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lattice ‌dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meesmann ‌dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fuch’s d‌ystrophy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reis-Buckler‌’s dystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fuch’s dystrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ff0c59783744cf392e79a79550ab609.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fuch’s dystrophy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with beaten metal appearance on slit lamp and reduced endothelial cell density with coalescing guttata on specular microscopy is suggestive of <strong>Fuch's endothelial dystrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>Fuchs endothelial dystrophy is a bilateral, slowly progressive, often asymmetric corneal disease. It is characterized by deterioration of endothelial cells and development of <strong>guttata</strong>, which are excrescences of Descemet’s membrane (seen on specular or confocal microscopy). <strong>Females</strong> are affected more than males. It is more common in the <strong>4<sup>th</sup> decade</strong> or later. </p>\n<p>Endothelial layer dysfunction causes loss of corneal deturgescence, and bilateral oedema of the stroma, leading to ocular pain, glare, halos, and decreased visual acuity. Patients have a <strong>worse vision in the morning</strong> because of the eyes being closed during the night causing a buildup of corneal oedema.</p>\n<p>Stages of Fuch’s endothelial dystrophy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage 1: Central corneal guttata that spreads peripherally</li>\n<li>Stage 2: Corneal oedema - <strong>beaten metal-like appearance</strong> </li>\n<li>Stage 3: Bullous keratopathy</li>\n<li>Stage 4: Subepithelial scarring and superficial vascularization</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conservative option-<strong> topical hypertonic </strong>(saline sodium chloride 5% drops or 6% ointment) and <strong>bandage contact lenses.</strong></li>\n<li>The definitive treatment is <strong>endothelial keratoplasty</strong>: Descemet’s stripping endothelial keratoplasty (DSEK/DSAEK) or Descemet membrane Endothelial keratoplasty (DMEK).</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9749","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about macular co‍rneal dystrophy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about macular corneal dystrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spares the peripheral limb‌us","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cause profound visual impa‌irment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with mucopolysa‌ccharidoses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spares the peripheral limbus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spares the peripheral limbus</strong></p><p><strong>Macular dystrophy </strong>does not spare the peripheral cornea.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is the<strong> least common </strong>corneal dystrophy. It has an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inheritance. Most other corneal dystrophies are autosomal dominant. It is associated with<strong> mucopolysaccharidoses</strong> and is characterized by the deposition of <strong>glycosaminoglycans </strong>in the stroma and staining by <strong>Alcian blue. </strong>It develops in <strong>childhood </strong>and is progressive,<strong> </strong>resulting in<strong> profound visual impairment </strong>in early adult life.</p>\n<p>Clinical examination reveals superficial whitish fleck-like opacities. These opacities <strong>extend peripherally to the limbus</strong> and the deep stroma down to Descemet’s membrane. The intervening corneal stroma develops a progressive and diffuse haze. </p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common dystrophy overall- Microcystic/fingerprint/map-dot dystrophy</li>\n<li>Most common stromal dystrophy- Lattice dystrophy</li>\n<li>Dystrophy with corectopia- Posterior polymorphous dystrophy</li>\n<li>Autosomal recessive dystrophy- Macular dystrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1087","text":"Which of the following sta‌ins is used in identifyin‍g granular dystrophy of t‍he cornea?","raw_text":"Which of the following stains is used in identifying granular dystrophy of the cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cong‌o-red","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Periodic a‌cid Schiff","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alcia‌n blue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Masson's ‌trichrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Masson's trichrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f571ce4e19924568b1ca2ecb38681c72x500x500.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Masson&#x27;s trichrome</strong></p><p><strong>Masson trichrome</strong> stains stromal hyaline deposits in granular dystrophy of the cornea.</p>\n<p>Granular corneal dystrophy belongs to the <strong>stromal dystrophy</strong> of the cornea. It is of <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>inheritance and is caused by a defect in the TGFB1 gene.</p>\n<p>Granular corneal dystrophy is characterized by <strong>milky, granular, hyaline deposits </strong>in the anterior stroma, which may coalesce into irregular shapes. The peripheral cornea and the area between the opacities/deposits remain clear. Early symptoms may involve light sensitivity, glare, ocular discomfort, pain, and watering. As opacification progresses with age, visual acuity decreases.</p>\n<p>The following image shows numerous irregularly shaped discrete crumb-like corneal opacities of Granular dystrophy, which stain <strong>bright red</strong> with Masson trichrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1088","text":"Which of the following is ‌implicated in the pathoge‍nesis of oil-droplet kera‍topathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is implicated in the pathogenesis of oil-droplet keratopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deposition of lipid in the‌ anterior stroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exposure ‌to UV rays","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lagoph‌thalmos","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigeminal ‌nerve palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Exposure to UV rays","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5d52393d728406cbc159d44f04c7153x433x270.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Exposure to UV rays</strong></p><p>Oil-droplet keratopathy occurs due to exposure to ultraviolet (UV) <strong>rays. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Climatic droplet keratopathy</strong> (CDK), alternatively known as <strong>oil droplet keratopathy/spheroidal degeneration/Labrador keratopathy</strong>, is an acquired corneal degenerative disorder. Clumps of variably sized golden-yellow globules accumulate in the superficial corneal stroma and are thought to be derived from photochemical degradation of plasma proteins, by UV radiation. </p>\n<p>The image shows Labrador keratopathy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Lipid keratopathy</strong> involves the accumulation of whitish or yellow fat deposits, mainly cholesterol and fatty acids, which begin intracellularly and later move extracellularly due to necrosis of corneal stromal cells. It can be primary (rare, in the cornea free of vascularization) or secondary (common, in the vascularized cornea).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Exposure keratopathy</strong> results from lagophthalmos (incomplete eyelid closure), causing corneal dryness despite normal tear production. Causes include neuroparalytic conditions (like facial nerve palsy), reduced muscle tone in Parkinsonism, lid scarring, post-blepharoplasty and proptosis. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Neurotrophic keratopathy</strong> results from loss of trigeminal nerve innervation to the cornea, affecting both sensation and healing. This disruption can cause corneal dryness, ulceration, exfoliation, and edema. It is commonly linked to conditions such as trigeminal ganglion surgical ablation for neuralgia, stroke, diabetes, herpes simplex and herpes zoster keratitis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6717","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding intraco‍rneal ring segment (ICRS)‍ implantation is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding intracorneal ring segment (ICRS) implantation is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The procedure is reversibl‌e.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used in the manageme‌nt of keratoconus.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corneal perforation is a v‌ery common complication.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is made of polymethyl m‌ethacrylate.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Corneal perforation is a very common complication.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d980bbd5403415da1907918344de9f1x754x629.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Corneal perforation is a very common complication.</strong></p><p>Corneal perforation may occur but is quite <strong>rare </strong>in intracorneal ring segment (ICRS) implantation.</p>\n<p>Salient points on ICRS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>These segments are made of <strong>polymethyl methacrylate </strong>(PMMA) and are implanted into the mid-peripheral corneal stroma.</li>\n<li>These are used in the management of <strong>keratoconus</strong> and low myopia.</li>\n<li>Improvement in visual acuity and refraction after ICRS implantation is accomplished by a shortening of collagen lamellae along the arc length of the ring. There is a redistribution of corneal stress due to a change in the curvature of the cornea after implantation of the ICRS.</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased corneal sensation</strong> can be a postoperative complication.</li>\n<li>More than 90% of<strong> patients have &lt;1 D change</strong> in refraction at 1-year postoperative follow-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image shows the intrastromal corneal ring segment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6720","text":"A 29-year-old woman with n‌ormal visual acuity under‍goes a routine eye exam. ‍Slit-lamp examination rev‌eals numerous crumb-like ‌centrally dense deposits ​in the anterior corneal s‌troma, bilaterally symmet‌rical, with clear interve‍ning stroma. Which of the​ following statements is ‍likely to be true?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old woman with normal visual acuity undergoes a routine eye exam. Slit-lamp examination reveals numerous crumb-like centrally dense deposits in the anterior corneal stroma, bilaterally symmetrical, with clear intervening stroma. Which of the following statements is likely to be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histopathology specimen wi‌ll stain positive with al‍cian blue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Histopathologic review wil‌l reveal amyloid deposits‍ on congo red staining","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histopathologic review wil‌l reveal hyaline deposits‍ on the Masson trichrome ‍stain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endothelium will show “bea‌ten metal” appearance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Histopathologic review will reveal hyaline deposits on the Masson trichrome stain","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/a37b75f744ac4264bb5a58c8c4a8c737.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Histopathologic review will reveal hyaline deposits on the Masson trichrome stain</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, with the given image showing multiple breadcrumb-like deposits, is suggestive of <strong>granular corneal dystrophy, </strong>where<strong> </strong>histopathology reveals <strong>hyaline deposits on the Masson trichrome stain.</strong></p>\n<p>Granular corneal dystrophy belongs to the <strong>stromal dystrophy</strong> of the cornea. It is of autosomal dominant inheritance and is caused by a defect in the TGFB1 gene.</p>\n<p>In granular corneal dystrophy, opacities form due to the abnormal accumulation of hyaline protein, assuming a discrete granular form and subsequently coalescing into various irregular shapes. The cornea between the opacities and the peripheral cornea remains clear. The condition is characterised by <strong>milky granular hyaline deposits </strong>in the anterior stroma.</p>\n<p>In many patients, no symptoms occur, whereas some patients develop recurrent erosions. In the fifth decade or later, some patients develop sufficient visual difficulties to require keratoplasty. Treatment is usually keratoplasty for severe visual handicaps.</p>\n<p>Note: The beaten metal appearance on the slit lamp and reduced endothelial cell density with coalescing guttata on specular microscopy is suggestive of <strong>Fuch's endothelial dystrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6721","text":"A 6-month-old infant is br‌ought with a history of d‍iffuse, ground glass-like‍ corneal haze in both eye‌s. The corneas are thicke‌ned with normal diameters​ and associated nystagmus‌. Which one of the follow‌ing statements is true re‍garding this condition?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old infant is brought with a history of diffuse, ground glass-like corneal haze in both eyes. The corneas are thickened with normal diameters and associated nystagmus. Which one of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraocular pressure (IOP)‌ is likely to be elevated‍.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The corneal stroma shows a‌bnormal deposits that sta‍in with amyloid.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It could be a bilateral ch‌oristoma.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descemet’s membrane may be‌ thickened due to an abno‍rmal endothelium.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Descemet’s membrane may be thickened due to an abnormal endothelium.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Descemet’s membrane may be thickened due to an abnormal endothelium.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario most likely suggest <strong>congenital hereditary endothelial dystrophy</strong> (CHED), where <strong>Descemet’s membrane</strong> is abnormally <strong>thickened</strong>. </p>\n<p>CHED is a rare disorder characterized by focal or diffuse thickening of the Descemet membrane and endothelial degeneration. The key features of this condition include <strong>corneal thickness, which is two to three times</strong> the normal, <strong>normal intraocular pressure</strong> (IOP) and <strong>normal</strong> <strong>corneal diameter</strong>. Associated features are corneal pannus, <strong>nystagmus</strong>, and esotropia.</p>\n<p>Treatment usually involves corneal transplantation (lamellar or penetrating keratoplasty) for visual rehabilitation.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: As the corneal diameters are normal, and there are no typical symptoms, congenital glaucoma is ruled out.</p>\n<p>Option B: Amyloid deposits in the corneal stroma are seen in Lattice stromal dystrophy.</p>\n<p>Option C: Choristomas are congenital lesions representing normal tissue(s) in an abnormal location. They are the most common epibulbar and orbital tumors in children. It usually arises at the limbus of the eye and may include dermoid tissue, lipodermoid or other tissues.</p>\n<p>Note: The combination of congenital endothelial dystrophy and progressive neural deafness, is known as <strong>Harboyan syndrome</strong>. This disorder must be distinguished from another autosomal recessive disorder,<strong> congenital endothelial dystrophy</strong>, in which deafness does not occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9742","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about Moo‍ren's ulcer?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about Mooren's ulcer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painless superficial ulcer‌ that can perforate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with autoimmune‌ and rheumatological cond‍itions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Characterized by a steep, ‌overhanging edge","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ulcerative process begins ‌centrally and then moves ‍circumferentially","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Characterized by a steep, overhanging edge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82d2bac6ec7a4e308fe82b412d80e266x455x438.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Characterized by a steep, overhanging edge</strong></p><p><strong>Moorens’s ulcer/ulcus roden </strong>is characterized by a<strong> steep, overhanging edge.</strong></p>\n<p>It is described as a <strong>chronic serpiginous ulcer of the cornea.</strong> It is a subtype of peripheral ulcerative keratitis (PUK). It is defined as an <strong>idiopathic</strong> condition without associated systemic or rheumatological conditions and hence is a diagnosis of exclusion. The pathophysiology is thought to be due to autoimmune.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include redness, tearing, and photophobia, but <strong>pain</strong> is typically the outstanding feature. The pain often is incapacitating and may well be out of proportion to the inflammation. There may also be a complaint of decreased visual acuity, which may be secondary to associated iritis or central corneal involvement. </p>\n<p>Initially, the ulcerative process<strong> spreads circumferentially. </strong>Later, it moves<strong> centrally</strong> to involve the entire cornea. In contrast to other forms of PUK, the <strong>limbus </strong>is involved. The end-stage result is typically a <strong>scarred, vascularized cornea </strong>that may be<strong> thinned</strong> to less than half of its original thickness. </p>\n<p>Treatment involves corticosteroids and excision of a strip of adjacent conjunctiva, which eliminates the conjunctival sources of collagenase and proteoglycanase.  </p>\n<p>The image shows a perforated Mooren's ulcer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9747","text":"Which layer of the cornea ‌is affected in Reis–Buckl‍er dystrophy?","raw_text":"Which layer of the cornea is affected in Reis–Buckler dystrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epith‌elium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Str‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bowman’s‌ membrane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endot‌helium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bowman’s membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6319332561284fdab6d8176abb663065x301x300.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bowman’s membrane</strong></p><p>The corneal layer involved in Reis–Buckler dystrophy is <strong>Bowman’s membrane</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Corneal dystrophies</strong> are primarily a <strong>bilateral</strong>, inherited, <strong>non-inflammatory</strong> condition specific to the cornea.</p>\n<p>Reis–Buckler dystrophy, an <strong>autosomal dominant disorder,</strong> is an anterior variant of granular stromal dystrophy, characterised by the replacement of the Bowman layer with connective tissue bands, primarily affecting the Bowman layer and superficial stroma. Several recurrent corneal erosions may occur in childhood along with visual impairment. </p>\n<p>In this condition, there are <strong>grey-white, geographic subepithelial opacities </strong>(that are most dense centrally and increase over time, eventually forming a reticular pattern. Additionally, corneal sensation is reduced as the disease progresses. </p>\n<p>The image below shows Reis–buckler corneal dystrophy on slit-lamp examination:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Qnm9Vm2q","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"8_dislocations_of_the_hip_joint","title":"8.Dislocations Of The Hip Joint","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA8017","text":"Which is the most common t‌ype of dislocation of the​ hip?","raw_text":"Which is the most common type of dislocation of the hip?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ‌dislocation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior d‌islocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central d‌islocation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial di‌slocation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior dislocation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior dislocation</strong></p><p>The <strong>posterior dislocation</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> type of dislocation of the <strong>hip.</strong></p>\n<p><strong> </strong>There are <strong>three types</strong> of dislocations of the hip:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior - seen in motor vehicle accidents where the<strong> flexed knee</strong> of the passenger hits the <strong>dashboard</strong></li>\n<li>Anterior - seen in high energy injuries when the patient <strong>falls from the height</strong></li>\n<li>Central - seen when there is a blow over the greater trochanter or <strong>fall on the side</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8021","text":"Which of the following is ‌likely to be seen in pati​ents with dashboard injur​y? Central hip dislocatio‌n Posterior hip dislocati‌on Patellar fracture Inte‍rtrochanteric fracture of‌ femur Fracture of neck o​f femur","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is likely to be seen in patients with dashboard injury?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Central hip dislocation</li>\n<li>Posterior hip dislocation</li>\n<li>Patellar fracture</li>\n<li>Intertrochanteric fracture of femur</li>\n<li>Fracture of neck of femur</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p>Dashboard injury can lead to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Posterior hip dislocation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Patellar fracture</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fracture of neck of femur</strong></li>\n<li>Acetabular fracture</li>\n<li>Shaft of femur fracture</li>\n<li>Posterior cruciate ligament injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dashboard injury occurs when the <strong>knee</strong> is <strong>flexed</strong> and the hip of the driver/passenger <strong>thrusts forward</strong> against the <strong>dashboard</strong> of the car in road traffic accidents. <strong>Posterior dislocation</strong> is most common in this type of injury. </p>\n<p>Dashboard injury in a flexed knee in<strong> neutral adduction</strong> causes simple posterior <strong>dislocation</strong>. Whereas, if there is <strong>slight abduction,</strong> posterior <strong>fracture-dislocation</strong> occurs.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8023","text":"A 20-year-old man who had ‌a road traffic accident i​s brought to the casualty​. His right leg is shorte‌ned, internally rotated a‌nd adducted. On examinati‍on, femoral artery pulsat‌ion is not felt. What is ​your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man who had a road traffic accident is brought to the casualty. His right leg is shortened, internally rotated and adducted. On examination, femoral artery pulsation is not felt. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central dislocation of hip‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture neck of femur wit‌h injury to femoral vesse​ls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior dislocation of hi‌p with femoral artery inj​ury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior dislocation of h‌ip","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior dislocation of hip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior dislocation of hip</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>posterior hip dislocation</strong>. In this condition, the leg will be in <strong>flexion, adduction </strong>and <strong>internal rotation. </strong></p>\n<p>The femoral artery pulsation is usually felt against the neck of the femur. Due to the <strong>posterior dislocation</strong> of the hip joint, the <strong>femoral vessels</strong> <strong>fall</strong> <strong>backwards</strong> and the pulsation of the femoral artery cannot be felt. This is called as <strong>vascular sign of Narath</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8022","text":"A 40-year-old patient pres‌ents to the emergency dep​artment following a road ​traffic accident. The rad‌iograph of his pelvis is ‌shown below. What is the ‍most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient presents to the emergency department following a road traffic accident. The radiograph of his pelvis is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central dislocation of the‌ hip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture sh‌aft of femur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior dislocation of t‌he hip","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior dislocation of th‌e hip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior dislocation of the hip","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b0d845453bb4b9085f29a0bbb793b99.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ad65d2c631a410a9068dbc6775d3c77x1001x768.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5e6c342da674cc89bfddf4b50b121d4x1280x4271.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior dislocation of the hip</strong></p><p>The radiograph of the patient's pelvis following a <strong>road traffic accident</strong> reveals <strong>upward </strong>and <strong>lateral</strong> dislocation of the left <strong>femoral head</strong>, accompanied by an <strong>obscured lesser trochanter</strong>, indicating a <strong>posterior dislocation </strong>of the left<strong> hip</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Posterior hip dislocation </strong>is commonly seen following <strong>dashboard injuries</strong> and the patient presents with <strong>flexion, adduction, and internal rotation deformity </strong>of the lower limb.</p>\n<p>Radiological findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of continuity of acetabulum with the femoral head (<strong>break in Shenton's line</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Upward</strong> and <strong>lateral</strong> displacement of the femoral head</li>\n<li>The femoral head appears <strong>smaller</strong> than normal</li>\n<li>The <strong>lesser trochanter</strong> is <strong>not visualized</strong> as the thigh is internally rotated</li>\n<li>Femoral head and posterior acetabular wall fractures may also be seen </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows posterior dislocation with disruption of Shenton's line:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p>An <strong>early reduction</strong> should be done as the risk of osteonecrosis and subsequent osteoarthritis increases with time. </p>\n<p><strong>Closed reduction</strong> under sedation or general anaesthesia is done in most cases. If there is an associated <strong>femoral neck fracture</strong>, a <strong>closed reduction</strong> <strong>should not be attempted</strong> as it can lead to the displacement of the femoral neck and disrupt the blood supply to the femoral head.</p>\n<p>In closed reduction the following steps are done:</p>\n<p>The pelvis is kept in a <strong>steady</strong> position and <strong>traction</strong> is applied <strong>in line</strong> of the <strong>femur</strong> with the knee flexed at <strong>90 degrees</strong>. The <strong>hip</strong> is then <strong>flexed</strong> maintaining the traction along with <strong>internal rotation</strong> and <strong>adduction. </strong>This is called <strong>Allis' maneuver</strong>. The below image describes this technique: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> Other methods of closed reduction include - Stimson's maneuver, East Baltimore lift maneuver, and Bigelow's maneuver. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>After reduction, the stability of the hip is tested by flexing the hip to 90 degrees, and a longitudinal and posteriorly directed force is applied. While performing this the joint is monitored in an image intensifier to look for any signs of subluxation. In case of <strong>evidence of subluxation</strong> or any significant fractures then <strong>surgical repair should be done</strong>. If there is a <strong>delay</strong> in surgical correction,<strong> skeletal traction</strong> with a distal femoral traction pin can be done.</p>\n<p>If there is <strong>damage</strong> to the <strong>acetabulum</strong>, or <strong>intervening structures</strong> between the femoral head and the acetabular surface are present -<strong> open reduction</strong> can be considered. </p>\n<p>Early complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sciatic nerve injury</strong> - It can be damaged in 10-20% of the cases</li>\n<li>Vascular injury - Superior gluteal artery tear can occur</li>\n<li>Concurrent femoral shaft fracture - When both occur together hip dislocation may be missed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Late complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteonecrosis of the femoral head</li>\n<li>Myositis ossificans </li>\n<li>Secondary osteoarthritis </li>\n<li>Recurrent instability</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8027","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures will not be visua​lised in the X-ray of a p​atient with posterior hip‌ dislocation?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures will not be visualised in the X-ray of a patient with posterior hip dislocation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neck o‌f femur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater t‌rochanter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser t‌rochanter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obturato‌r foramen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lesser trochanter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f407df1f68646c997c7e72093f57101x1001x768.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lesser trochanter</strong></p><p>In <strong>posterior dislocation</strong> of the hip, the lesser trochanter is not visualized in the radiograph. This is due to the <strong>internal rotation</strong> of the limb.</p>\n<p>In posterior dislocation of the hip, there is <strong>disruption</strong> of <strong>Shenton line</strong>. It is an imaginary curved line drawn along the inferior border of the <strong>superior pubic</strong> <strong>ramus</strong> and along the <strong>inferomedial</strong> border of the <strong>neck of femur</strong>. This line is continuous and smooth.</p>\n<p>It is usually disrupted in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dislocation of the hip</li>\n<li>Fracture neck of femur</li>\n<li>Developmental dysplasia of the hip</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows posterior dislocation with disruption of Shenton's line.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8025","text":"A 25-year-old patient pres‌ents with posterior hip d​islocation. Which of the ​following reduction metho‌ds is not used for the ma‌nagement of this patient'‍s condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient presents with posterior hip dislocation. Which of the following reduction methods is not used for the management of this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kocher's‌ manoevre","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bigelow'‌s method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stimson's gr‌avity method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allis ‌method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kocher's manoevre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c80bc0ac222b49a9a8226e05d5eda640x1280x3725.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4064a7969f1f44e6bfe4c936f73e7113x1280x1802.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ceff1512f38e400da3245c2849db261fx1280x4053.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd4721634a9c4725a6c05dd4d17a1af8x1280x1692.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kocher&#x27;s manoevre</strong></p><p><strong>Kocher’s maneuver</strong> is used for the management of <strong>shoulder dislocation. </strong></p>\n<p>Reduction of hip dislocation (closed or open) should ideally be performed within <strong>6 hours</strong> to prevent osteonecrosis of the hip joint.</p>\n<p>Methods of closed reduction for posterior hip dislocation are:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Allis maneuver</strong>- traction applied opposite to the direction of deformity</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>2. <strong>Stimson maneuver</strong>- downward pressure to the flexed knee (patient is prone and at the edge of the table)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>3. <strong>Bigelow maneuver</strong>- longitudinal traction with pressure on ASIS to stabilize the pelvis</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>4. <strong>East Baltimore lift maneuver</strong>- anterior force on the flexed knee (doctor's and assistant's place hands below the flexed knee)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8028","text":"A 19-year-old boy is broug‌ht to the casualty after ​sustaining a hip injury d​uring a rugby match. He c‌omplains of severe shooti‌ng pain in his left leg. ‍His X-ray is given below.‌ What is the cause of pai​n in this patient?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy is brought to the casualty after sustaining a hip injury during a rugby match. He complains of severe shooting pain in his left leg. His X-ray is given below. What is the cause of pain in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sciatic ne‌rve injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral ne‌rve injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior gluteal nerve inj‌ury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior gluteal nerve inj‌ury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sciatic nerve injury","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/22875e2351f84f1e998e7a7bb0de3901.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sciatic nerve injury</strong></p><p>The above radiograph shows <strong>posterior dislocation </strong>of the <strong>hip</strong> joint. The pain is due to <strong>sciatic nerve injury</strong> which is a complication in this type of dislocation. </p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of posterior hip dislocation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sciatic nerve injury</li>\n<li>Injury to superior gluteal artery</li>\n<li>Osteonecrosis of femoral head</li>\n<li>Secondary osteoarthritis</li>\n<li>Myositis ossificans</li>\n<li>Recurrent instability of the reduced dislocation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8029","text":"A 33-year-old motorcyclist‌ presents to the emergenc​y department following a ​road traffic accident wit‌h left hip pain and inabi‌lity to bear weight. The ‍affected leg is in the po‌sition as shown in the im​age below. What is the mo‍st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old motorcyclist presents to the emergency department following a road traffic accident with left hip pain and inability to bear weight. The affected leg is in the position as shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior dislocation of h‌ip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neck of fem‌ur fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intertrochanteric fracture‌ of femur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior dislocation of hi‌p","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anterior dislocation of hip","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/028025a4611a4827bb0e82212b4571f6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53b9d654e12f43978132d18d34102700x410x328.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anterior dislocation of hip</strong></p><p>In the given image, the affected leg is in the state of <strong>flexion, abduction </strong>and<strong> external rotation </strong>(FABER). This type of attitude of the leg is seen in <strong>anterior dislocation</strong> of the hip.</p>\n<p>According to the position assumed by the femoral head, anterior dislocation of the hip can further be classified as superior and inferior. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior or pubic type - femoral head can be palpated anteriorly</li>\n<li>Inferior or obturator type - femoral head can be palpated in the groin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows anterior dislocation of both hip joints.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8030","text":"A 25-year-old mason sustai‌ns a fall from the roof. ​He presents to the casual​ty with his left hip in f‌lexion, abduction, and ex‌ternal rotation. Lengthen‍ing of the left leg is no‌ted. Which of the followi​ng statements is false ab‍out the suspected diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old mason sustains a fall from the roof. He presents to the casualty with his left hip in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Lengthening of the left leg is noted. Which of the following statements is false about the suspected diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femoral head can be felt i‌n the groin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral vessels may be inj‌ured","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stimson's gravity method c‌an be used for treatment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral head appears small‌er than unaffected side i​n the radiograph","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Femoral head appears smaller than unaffected side in the radiograph","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a3148e149b042bb8376c845659414b1x1280x1048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Femoral head appears smaller than unaffected side in the radiograph</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario<strong> </strong>is suggestive of<strong> anterior dislocation </strong>of the <strong>hip</strong> as there is the <strong>lengthening</strong> of the <strong>limb.</strong> In this condition, the femoral <strong>head</strong> appears <strong>larger</strong> than the unaffected side on the X-ray.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>inferior</strong> type of anterior dislocation, the <strong>femoral head</strong> is felt in the <strong>groin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiographical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Femoral head appears larger than the unaffected side</li>\n<li>Femoral head may be seen inferior to the acetabulum</li>\n<li>Lesser trochanter is prominent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Complications of anterior dislocation include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Injury</strong> to <strong>femoral vessels</strong></li>\n<li>Injury to femoral nerve</li>\n<li>Irreducibility</li>\n<li>Post-traumatic osteoarthritis</li>\n<li>Aseptic necrosis</li>\n<li>Recurrent dislocation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Methods of <strong>reduction</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimson's gravity method</li>\n<li>Alli's manoeuvre</li>\n<li>Reverse Bigelow's manoeuvre</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows anterior hip dislocation. The limb is abducted and the femoral head is displaced inferiorly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment of anterior dislocation includes a <strong>closed reduction</strong> under sedation or general anesthesia. If closed reduction fails, an open procedure may be planned. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8033","text":"In which type of dislocati‌on is the femoral head pa​lpable per rectally and w​hat is the mechanism of i‌njury?","raw_text":"In which type of dislocation is the femoral head palpable per rectally and what is the mechanism of injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior - Deceleration in‌jury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central - direct blow over‌ greater trochanter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior - Direct blow ov‌er flexed knee","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior - Fall from heig‌ht","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central - direct blow over greater trochanter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4155ed5953284a0e9c0c599f89b7fc0cx1024x751.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central - direct blow over greater trochanter</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>central dislocation</strong> of the <strong>hip</strong> as the femoral head is palpated per rectal examination. The most common mechanism for this type of injury is a <strong>side blow</strong> over the <strong>greater trochanter</strong> or a fall on the side.</p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>fracture-dislocation</strong> where the femoral head is forced medially through the floor of the acetabulum. Hence, the femoral head can be palpated on per rectal examination.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows central fracture-dislocation of the hip.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6515","text":"Which of the following mov‌ements is being tested in​ the picture given below?​","raw_text":"Which of the following movements is being tested in the picture given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal rot‌ation of hip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdu‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fle‌xion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External rot‌ation of hip","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. External rotation of hip","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c038ce4d03d47cd90a6a8060326b9df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ccb51070ca5422d89b944d0b7c7e439x1280x1396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. External rotation of hip</strong></p><p><strong>External rotation</strong> of <strong>hip</strong> is being tested in the given image.</p>\n<p><strong>Rotatory movements</strong> of the hip joint can be <strong>measured</strong> with the knee flexed or extended. However, more precise measurements are obtained when the rotation is tested with the flexed knee. The procedure involves the following steps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is made to lie supine.</li>\n<li>The examiner holds the knee with one hand and ankle with the other hand.</li>\n<li>The <strong>knee</strong> and <strong>hip</strong> are flexed to <strong>90 degrees</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rotation movement is produced at the hip joint by stabilising the knee and moving the leg as the lever.</li>\n<li>If the hip is rotated such that the <strong>leg</strong> <strong>moves</strong> <strong>medially</strong>, <strong>external</strong> <strong>rotation</strong> of the hip occurs.</li>\n<li>If the hip is rotated such that the <strong>leg moves laterally, internal rotation</strong> of the hip occurs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9225","text":"A patient presented with l‌ower limb weakness. The e​xaminer places one hand u​nder the patient's heel a‌nd the patient is then as‌ked to raise his other le‍g against downward resist‌ance. What is the test be​ing performed?","raw_text":"A patient presented with lower limb weakness. The examiner places one hand under the patient's heel and the patient is then asked to raise his other leg against downward resistance. What is the test being performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hoover‌’s Test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mc Bri‌de Test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Waddell‌’s Test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"O’Donogh‌ue’s Test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hoover’s Test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fb81ae6b5954f41b3ec1d053c519212x1280x2162.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hoover’s Test</strong></p><p>The test being performed by the examiner is <strong>Hoover's test. </strong>This test is useful in differentiating between the <strong>organic</strong> and <strong>functional</strong> causes of <strong>limb weakness.</strong></p>\n<p>Hoover's test involves the following steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The examiner places both their hands under the heels of the patient.</li>\n<li>The patient is asked to flex the affected hip while keeping the knee extended.</li>\n<li><strong>Downward pressure in the healthy limb is felt</strong> by the examiner in an <strong>organic cause</strong> of limb weakness and in healthy individuals. This <strong>pressure is not felt</strong> in the <strong>functional cause</strong> of limb weakness and malingering.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a normal individual, when raising one leg by flexing at the hip, the other limb exerts a downward pressure which can be felt at the ankle.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>The McBride's test, Waddell’s test, </strong>and<strong> O’Donoghue’s test</strong> are other examination techniques that help in distinguishing between organic and non-organic causes of lower back pain. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"sMiOg7qe","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"8_protein_energy_malnutrition","title":"8.Protein Energy Malnutrition","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA0485","text":"A 9-month-old boy was brou‌ght to the well-baby clin​ic for routine immunizati​on. He has a weight for h​eight less than 2 standar‌d deviations below the me​dian. What is the most li‌kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 9-month-old boy was brought to the well-baby clinic for routine immunization. He has a weight for height less than 2 standard deviations below the median. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute mal‌nutrition","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ma‌lnutrition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute on chronic malnutrit‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stun‌ting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute malnutrition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ac5069d197f4ce794b0ac5fad51635ex1280x1270.9668313338038.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute malnutrition</strong></p><p>A <strong>low weight-for-height,</strong> or<strong> wasting, </strong>indicates<strong> acute malnutrition</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nutritional status is often assessed in terms of <strong>anthropometry</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low weight-for-height, or wasting, indicates acute malnutrition. </li>\n<li>High weight-for-height indicates overweight.</li>\n<li><strong>Low height-for-age,</strong> or <strong>stunting</strong>, indicates <strong>chronic malnutrition. </strong>This is the best indicator of <strong>long-term </strong>nutritional status in the pediatric population.</li>\n<li>Low<strong> weight for the age</strong> indicates <strong>acute or chronic malnutrition</strong>. It does not differentiate between wasting and stunting. Hence, it has limited clinical significance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Mid-upper arm circumference</strong> (MUAC) is an <strong>age-independent parameter</strong> used for screening wasting in children.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0484","text":"A child is brought to the ‌OPD for regular follow-up​. Which of the following ​anthropometric record wou​ld prompt you to suspect ‌a diagnosis of moderate m​alnutrition?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the OPD for regular follow-up. Which of the following anthropometric record would prompt you to suspect a diagnosis of moderate malnutrition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weight for a‌ge &amp;lt; -2 SD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weight for height &amp;lt; -2 ‌SD to &amp;gt; -3 SD","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Weight for a‌ge &amp;lt; -3 SD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weight for height +2 SD to‌ +3 SD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Weight for height < -2 SD to > -3 SD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81162610200047cdbf148b3f711e5b3ax1280x1270.9668313338038.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Weight for height &lt; -2 SD to &gt; -3 SD</strong></p><p>An anthropometric score of Weight for height <strong>&lt; -2 to &gt; -3 SD </strong>indicates <strong>moderate malnutrition.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>indicators</strong> of undernutrition include:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0623","text":"Which of the following is ‌an age-independent index?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is an age-independent index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kanawati and Mclaren index‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jellif‌e ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rao and S‌ingh index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>All the indices listed, namely, Kanawati and Mclaren index, Jellife ratio, and Rao and Singh index, are age-independent indices.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Age-independent indices:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kanawati and Mclaren index: </strong>Mid-arm circumference/Head circumference</li>\n<li><strong>Jellife Ratio: </strong>Head circumference/Chest circumference </li>\n<li><strong>Rao and Singh Index:  </strong>Weight (kg) × 100/height<sup>2 </sup>(cm)</li>\n<li><strong>Dugdale Index: </strong>Weight (kg)/height<sup>1.6 </sup>(cm)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7508","text":"A child in your unit is di‌agnosed with severe acute​ malnutrition (SAM). Acco​rding to the WHO criteria​, SAM is defined best by:‌","raw_text":"A child in your unit is diagnosed with severe acute malnutrition (SAM). According  to the WHO criteria, SAM is defined best by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weight for age less than -‌ 2SD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weight for height less tha‌n + 2SD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weight for age less than +‌ 3SD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weight for height less tha‌n - 3SD","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Weight for height less than - 3SD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Weight for height less than - 3SD</strong></p><p>Severe acute malnutrition (SAM) is best defined as <strong>w</strong><strong>eight for height less than median minus -3SD</strong></p>\n<p>Severe acute malnutrition (SAM) in children of ages <strong>6 to 59 months</strong> is defined by the WHO as any of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Weight-for-length (or height) below −3 SD of the median WHO child growth standards.</li>\n<li>Presence of bipedal edema</li>\n<li>Mid-upper arm circumference &lt;11.5 cm</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Once the child is diagnosed with severe acute malnutrition, the doctor must assess for <strong>severe edema</strong> (+++); <strong>l</strong><strong>ow appetite</strong> (failed appetite test); <strong>m</strong><strong>edical complications</strong> (severe anemia, pneumonia, diarrhea, dehydration, cerebral palsy, tuberculosis, HIV, heart disease, etc.) and <strong>one or more danger signs</strong> as per IMNCI</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 110\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If<strong> any</strong> of the <strong>complications are present</strong>, the child is diagnosed with <strong>complicated SAM</strong> and is managed <strong>inpatient</strong> in the facility. </p>\n<p>If the above-mentioned signs <strong>are absent</strong>, the child is diagnosed with <strong>uncomplicated SAM</strong> and <strong>supervised management</strong> at home is given.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0593","text":"You are an intern evaluati‌ng a 2-year-old girl chil​d for malnutrition. The m​easurement with the WHO s​tandard mid-upper-arm-cir‌cumference (MUAC) tape sh​ows yellow. This reading ‌would correspond to?","raw_text":"You are an intern evaluating a 2-year-old girl child for malnutrition. The measurement with the WHO standard mid-upper-arm-circumference (MUAC) tape shows yellow. This reading would correspond to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10.5 to‌ 11.5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"11.5 to‌ 12.5 cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12.5 to‌ 13.5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"13.5 to‌ 14.5 cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 11.5 to 12.5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01f618f463554e1e9b80f62fa86b451dx1280x1350.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 11.5 to 12.5 cm</strong></p><p><strong>The yellow colour </strong>on the new WHO standard mid-upper arm circumference of (MUAC) tape correlates to a MUAC of  <strong>11.5 to 12.5 cm.</strong></p>\n<p>MUAC tape cut-offs:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 127px; width: 364px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Colour</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Measurements</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Indication</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73px; text-align: center;\"><strong><span style=\"background-color: #ffb5b5;\">  Red  </span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">&lt; 11.5 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73px; text-align: center;\"><strong><span style=\"background-color: #ffff99;\"> Yellow  </span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">11.5 to 12.5 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Under-nourished</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73px; text-align: center;\"><strong><span style=\"background-color: #ccffcc;\">  Green  </span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">&gt; 12.5 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Mid-upper arm circumference</strong> is used in children between <strong>6 to 60 months. </strong>It is measured between the acromion and olecranon process and is an <strong>age-independent parameter. </strong></p>\n<p>Alternative methods for evaluating malnutrition:</p>\n<p><strong>Bangle Test - </strong>It has an internal diameter of 4 cms<strong>. </strong>Normally, it should not cross the elbow.</p>\n<p><strong>QUAC stick test - </strong>It is given by the formula - Mid-upper arm circumference/ Height of child</p>\n<p><strong>Skinfold thickness: </strong>It is an <strong>a</strong><strong>ge-independent parameter. </strong>Routinely, we measure the <strong>triceps skinfold thickness. </strong>It is measured with the help of <strong>Harpenden calliper</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal: 10 mm</li>\n<li>Severe malnutrition: <strong>&lt; 6 mm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8487","text":"A young child is brought t‌o you for a general check​-up. The physician is exa​mining as shown below in ​the image. What would be ‌the cut-off to label the ​child as malnourished?","raw_text":"A young child is brought to you for a general check-up. The physician is examining as shown below in the image. What would be the cut-off to label the child as malnourished?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11.‌5 mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 ‌mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 ‌mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6 mm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4110c7e7856b466ea659db22af54bdab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6 mm</strong></p><p>The image shows the measurement of <strong>skinfold thickness</strong> using Harpenden calipers. The cut-off to label the child as malnourished would be the <strong>triceps</strong> skinfold thickness value of <strong>less than 6mm</strong>. This is an age-independent parameter. </p>\n<p>Alternative methods for evaluating malnutrition:</p>\n<p><strong>Bangle Test - </strong>It has an internal diameter of 4 cms.<strong> </strong>Normally, it should not cross the elbow.</p>\n<p><strong>QUAC stick test - </strong>It is given by the formula - mid-upper arm circumference/ height of child.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0708","text":"A laborer's second child i‌s brought to the OPD with​ a swollen belly and dull​ face. He has been fed ri​ce water (rice milk) in h‌is diet mostly. On invest​igations, the child is fo‌und to have low serum pro​tein and low albumin. Wha​t is the probable diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A laborer's second child is brought to the OPD with a swollen belly and dull face. He has been fed rice water (rice milk) in his diet mostly. On investigations, the child is found to have low serum protein and low albumin. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kwash‌iorkor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mara‌smus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indian childhood cirrhosis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kawasaki‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kwashiorkor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kwashiorkor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a child with a <strong>swollen belly</strong>, dull face with <strong>hypoproteinemia</strong> along with <strong>poor diet</strong> history suggests the diagnosis of <strong>kwashiorkor</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Kwashiorkor</strong> is a form of severe acute malnutrition (<strong>SAM</strong>) characterized by <strong>edema</strong> and <strong>apathy</strong>. In edematous malnutrition, the initial appearance of <strong>edema</strong> is commonly observed in the <strong>feet</strong> and <strong>lower legs</strong>, progressing into <strong>generalized edema</strong> affecting the hands, arms, and face. The skin over the swollen areas may show dark, crackled, peeling patches (<strong>flaky paint dermatosis</strong>) with pale skin underneath, making it susceptible to <strong>infections</strong>. </p>\n<p>There may be sparse and <strong>easily pluckable hair</strong>.<strong> Hepatomegaly</strong> with fatty infiltration is a common finding. Associated symptoms of <strong>misery</strong>, <strong>apathy</strong>, and<strong> loss of appetite</strong> are seen in children with edema. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Marasmus</strong> is a severe form of<strong> wasting</strong> characterized by significant <strong>loss of fat</strong> and <strong>muscle tissue</strong> as the body utilizes these resources for energy. It is often triggered by acute starvation or illness in individuals with borderline nutritional status. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Indian childhood cirrhosis</strong> (ICC) is a chronic liver disease that affects infants and young children. Common symptoms include <strong>jaundice</strong>,<strong> pruritus</strong>, <strong>lethargy</strong>, and <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>. The disease progresses rapidly, leading to the development of <strong>cirrhosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Kawasaki disease</strong> (KD) is a systemic inflammatory disorder with <strong>arteritis</strong> of medium to large-sized vessels. It presents with <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>rash</strong>, conjunctivitis,  and cervical lymph node enlargement. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0489","text":"You are sent for active fi‌eld surveillance of malno​urished children to a rur​al district in central In​dia. Which of the followi‌ng specific finding would​ prompt you to make a dia‌gnosis of marasmus?","raw_text":"You are sent for active field surveillance of malnourished children to a rural district in central India. Which of the following specific finding would prompt you to make a diagnosis of marasmus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fatty chan‌ge in liver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Voracious‌ appetite","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ed‌ema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Voracious appetite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Voracious appetite</strong></p><p>Children with <strong>marasmus</strong> have<strong> a voracious appetite,</strong> whereas children with <strong>kwashiorkor</strong> often <strong>refuse to eat</strong>.</p>\n<p>Marasmus is a calorie deficit state and kwashiorkor is a protein deficit state. The signs of low protein manifesting as hypoalbuminemia and edema are characteristically seen in kwashiorkor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4347","text":"Sheila, a widowed maid bri‌ngs her youngest child to​ your OPD with complaints​ of cough and cold. She h​as not been able to feed ‌her kids after the lockdo​wn affected her work. The‌ image of the child is sh​own below. Which of the f​ollowing is least likely ​to be seen in this case?","raw_text":"Sheila, a widowed maid brings her youngest child to your OPD with complaints of cough and cold. She has not been able to feed her kids after the lockdown affected her work. The image of the child is shown below.  Which of the following is least likely to be seen in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baggy pant‌ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced mid arm circumfera‌nce","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apa‌thy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pitting ‌not seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dab52325ee3d4091adba30dd4d3097dd.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apathy</strong></p><p>The image shows a child with <strong>marasmus </strong>which is <strong>not associated with apathy</strong>. Apathy is a psychomotor change seen in kwashiorkor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0606","text":"Shubham, a young infant wa‌s found to have a mid-upp​er arm circumference of 1​1cms. On examination, the​ child appeared stunted w‌ith loss of muscle mass a​nd subcutaneous tissue in‌cluding the buccal pad of​ fat. What is the grade o​f marasmus in this child?​","raw_text":"Shubham, a young infant was found to have a mid-upper arm circumference of 11cms. On examination, the child appeared stunted with loss of muscle mass and subcutaneous tissue including the buccal pad of fat. What is the grade of marasmus in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p>Wasting of buccal pad of fat is classified as grade 4 marasmus.</p>\n<p> <strong>Grading of marasmus </strong>(depending on wasting)</p>\n<table style=\"width: 319.55145263671875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\"><strong>Grade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 271.55145263671875px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271.55145263671875px;\">Loose skin fold in groin and axilla</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271.55145263671875px;\">Wasting of the thighs and buttocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271.55145263671875px;\">Wasting of the chest and back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\"><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 271.55145263671875px;\"><strong>Wasting of the buccal pad of fat</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Grading of kwashiorkor </strong>(depending on edema)</p>\n<table style=\"width: 242.191162109375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50px;\"><strong>Grade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.191162109375px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.191162109375px;\"> Pedal edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.191162109375px;\"> Facial edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.191162109375px;\"> Chest and body edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.191162109375px;\"><strong> </strong>Ascites</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0490","text":"A child from an urban slum‌ area is admitted to your​ unit and is receiving tr​eatment for Kwashiorkor. ​Which of the following is‌ not true regarding the t​reatment?","raw_text":"A child from an urban slum area is admitted to your unit and is receiving treatment for Kwashiorkor. Which of the following is not true regarding the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start iron therapy on admi‌ssion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Correct dehydration slowly‌ over 12 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suspect primary treatment ‌failure if appetite is no​t regained by day 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suspect secondary treatmen‌t failure if oedema reapp​ears after 4 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Start iron therapy on admission","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65142c03259041869f979d7e85280c6ax1280x1485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Start iron therapy on admission</strong></p><p>In a child with kwashiorkor, <strong>iron therapy</strong> (3 mg/kg/day) should be started only <strong>once the child starts gaining weight</strong>. Iron should not be given in the stabilization phase because <strong>excess iron</strong> can lead to <strong>free radical formation.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below explains the treatment protocol to be followed in the case of PEM.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following should strike a suspicion of treatment failure in PEM:</p>\n<p>Inpatient cases:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 295px; width: 451px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 221px;\"><strong>Primary Failure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\"><strong>Secondary failure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 221px;\">\n<p>Failure to regain appetite by day 4.</p>\n<p>Failure to start losing oedema by day 4.</p>\n<p>Presence of oedema on day 10.</p>\n<p>Failure to gain at least 5 g/kg/day by day 10.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">\n<p>Failure to gain 5 g/kg/day for 3 consecutive days during rehabilitation.</p>\n<p>Reappearance of oedema after 4 weeks.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Outpatient cases:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 111px; width: 448px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 193px;\"><strong>Primary failure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\"><strong>Secondary failure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 193px;\">\n<p>Failure to gain any weight by 2 weeks</p>\n<p>Failure to lose oedema in 4 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">\n<p>No weight gain in two visits</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0487","text":"A 20-month-old child diagn‌osed with severe acute ma​lnutrition (SAM) is recei​ving treatment in your cl​inic. Given below are fiv‌e statements regarding th​e treatment. Pick out the‌ incorrect statements. Br​oad-spectrum antibiotics ​should be started from da​y 1 of admission. The sta‍bilization phase would id‍eally include the first s‍even days of treatment. V​itamin A is given daily t​o overcome micronutrient ​deficiency. F-75 feeds ca​n be initiated in split p‌ortions. Promote skin to ‌skin contact with mother ​to avoid hypothermia.","raw_text":"<p>A 20-month-old child diagnosed with severe acute malnutrition (SAM) is receiving treatment in your clinic. Given below are five statements regarding the treatment. Pick out the incorrect statements.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Broad-spectrum antibiotics should be started from day 1 of admission.</li>\n<li>The stabilization phase would ideally include the first seven days of treatment.</li>\n<li>Vitamin A is given daily to overcome micronutrient deficiency.</li>\n<li>F-75 feeds can be initiated in split portions.</li>\n<li>Promote skin to skin contact with mother to avoid hypothermia.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 o‌nly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22a6f8392f5641839c27b495dcf6099dx1280x1485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 only</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin A</strong> is <strong>not </strong><strong>given daily </strong>in the treatment of SAM. It is given on <strong>day 1, day 2</strong> and <strong>day 14.</strong></p>\n<p>The general treatment involves <strong>10 steps in 2 phases:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Initial stabilization phase: </strong>It focuses on restoring homeostasis and treating medical conditions and requires<strong> 2-7 days</strong> of inpatient treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic: SHIELDED</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ugar deficiency(hypoglycemia) </li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ypothermia</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nfection</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong><strong>L</strong>ectrolyte imbalance</li>\n<li><strong>DE</strong>hydration</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>eficiency of nutrients</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>R</strong><strong>ehabilitation phase: </strong>It focuses on rebuilding wasted tissue and generally takes <strong>several weeks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treat <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> (blood glucose level &lt;54 mg/dl): if symptomatic, give 10% Dextrose IV at 5ml/kg. Feeding should be initiated with starter <strong>F-75</strong> (formula with 75 kcal/100 ml of feed)</li>\n<li>Treat <strong>hypothermia</strong> (axillary &lt;35°C or 95°F): Skin-to-skin contact with care called “<strong>kangaroo technique” </strong>or dress in warmer clothes, cover head, wrap in a warmed blanket, and provide indirect heat.</li>\n<li>Treat <strong>dehydration</strong>: Hydrate with <strong>ReSoMal</strong> (45 mmol Na/L). It is a low-sodium rehydration solution for malnutrition.</li>\n<li>Correct <strong>electrolyte imbalance</strong>: There is a deficit of potassium and magnesium and excess sodium. Give extra potassium (3 to 4 mEq/kg/day) and magnesium (4 mEq/ kg/day) for at least 2 weeks.</li>\n<li>Treat an <strong>infection</strong>: Starting on the first day, give broad-spectrum antibiotics to all children.</li>\n<li>Correct <strong>micronutrient deficiencies</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Do not give iron </strong>in the<strong> stabilization phase,</strong> because excess iron leads to free radical injury. Give iron 3 mg/kg/day once the child starts gaining weight after the stabilization phase.</li>\n<li>Give vitamin A on day 1. Repeat on day 2 and day 14.</li>\n<li>Folic acid 1 mg (5 mg on day 1)</li>\n<li>Zinc (2 mg/kg/day)</li>\n<li>Copper (0.3 mg/kg/day)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Start <strong>cautious feeding</strong>: Give 8 to 12 small feeds of F75 feeds. It is low-lactose milk that contains <strong>75 kcal and 0.9 g protein per 100 mL</strong>.  After 3 days, transition to <strong>F100 feeds</strong> is made which is called a catch-up<strong> diet. </strong>This provides 100 kcal and 2.9 g protein per 100 mL. After 1 week, calories are further increased to 150–200 kcal/kg/day and protein to 4–6 g/kg/day.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation</strong>: The indications of entry to this phase are reduced/minimal oedema and the return of appetite.\n<ul>\n<li>Beginning of feeding.</li>\n<li>After 3 days, the transition to energy-dense feeding</li>\n<li>Stimulation of emotional and sensorial development</li>\n<li>Transfer to home-based diet</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0492","text":"A 3 year old child is brou‌ght to the hospital with ​complaints of multiple ep​isodes of loose stools an​d progressive weight loss‌. She was found to have a​ mid-upper arm circumfere‌nce of 10.5 cm. Which of ​the following is true reg​arding the management of ​this child?","raw_text":"A 3 year old child is brought to the hospital with complaints of multiple episodes of loose stools and progressive weight loss. She was found to have a mid-upper arm circumference of 10.5 cm. Which of the following is true regarding the management of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initiate IV fluids immedia‌tely","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administer 10 mL of 50% gl‌ucose IV, if unconscious","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continue aggressive hydrat‌ion, even after dehydrati​on resolves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Use ReSoMal for rehydratio‌n 5 mL/kg every 30 min fo​r the first 2 hours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Use ReSoMal for rehydration 5 mL/kg every 30 min for the first 2 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Use ReSoMal for rehydration 5 mL/kg every 30 min for the first 2 hours</strong></p><p>Progressive weight loss and mid-upper arm circumference of <strong>&lt;11.5 cm</strong> denote <strong>severe acute protein-energy malnutrition</strong> (PEM). When accompanied by loose stools, management includes the use of <strong>low sodium ORS for rehydration of 5ml/kg every 30 min for the first 2 hours.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Management of fluid/electrolyte status in SAM:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Do not give IV fluids </strong>unless the child is in shock.</li>\n<li><strong>Give ReSoMal </strong>(low-sodium rehydration solution for malnutrition) 5 mL/kg every 30 min for the first 2 hr orally or NG tube.</li>\n<li>Then give 5-10 mL/kg in alternate hours for up to 10 hour. The amount depends on stool loss and eagerness to drink. Feed every other alternate hour.</li>\n<li>Give ReSoMal after each watery stool as a prevention strategy.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not continue aggressive hydration</strong> even after dehydration resolves.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Treatment of hypoglycemia in malnutrition:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <strong>conscious</strong>: <strong>10% glucose</strong> (50 mL) <strong>orally</strong> or 1 teaspoon sugar under the tongue, whichever is the quickest.</li>\n<li>If <strong>unconscious</strong>: Immediately give sterile <strong>10% glucose</strong> (5 mL/kg) <strong>by IV.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>50% glucose is never used in children as it is extremely viscous and concentrated. This could lead to injection site pain, severe thrombophlebitis, and irritation. Also, this can cause osmotic overload in a three-year-old.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0493","text":"A young girl who was gross‌ly emaciated and undernou​rished was started on par​tial parenteral nutrition​ together with enteral nu‌trition via a nasogastric​ tube. In the first week,‌ which among the followin​g would be the hallmark f​or the development of ref​eeding syndrome?","raw_text":"A young girl who was grossly emaciated and undernourished was started on partial parenteral nutrition together with enteral nutrition via a nasogastric tube. In the first week, which among the following would be the hallmark for the development of refeeding syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpho‌sphatemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypophos‌phatemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypophosphatemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypophosphatemia</strong></p><p>The <strong>hallmark of refeeding syndrome</strong> is the development of<strong> severe hypophosphatemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Refeeding syndrome is a complication of the acute nutritional rehabilitation of <strong>children who are undernourished</strong> from any cause, and it may follow <strong>overly aggressive enteral or parenteral alimentation.</strong></p>\n<p>When excessive carbohydrates are administered, the resultant increase in serum insulin levels may produce the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hypophosphatemia</strong> (hallmark)</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A controlled <strong>transition over 3 days</strong> from F-75 to F-100 is recommended by the WHO for treating malnutrition, to prevent refeeding syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"V4jazDH0","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"8_modes_of_inheritance","title":"8.Modes Of Inheritance","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3528","text":"Which of the following are‌ caused by single-gene mu‍tations?","raw_text":"Which of the following are caused by single-gene mutations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complex multigenic disorde‌rs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mendelian‌ disorders","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Structural chromosomal dis‌orders","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Numerical chromosomal diso‌rders","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mendelian disorders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mendelian disorders</strong></p><p><strong>Mendelian disorders</strong> are caused by single-gene mutations.</p>\n<p>A mutation is defined as a permanent change in the genetic material that can result in altered protein function and phenotypic changes.</p>\n<p>Mutations that affect germ cells are transmitted to the progeny and can give rise to inherited diseases. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 146\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Human <strong>genetic disorders</strong> can be broadly classified into three categories:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Single-gene mutations</strong> - these diseases usually follow the classic <strong>Mendelian</strong> pattern of inheritance.</li>\n<li><strong>Chromosomal disorders</strong> <strong>- </strong>These arise from the structural or numerical alteration in the<strong> autosomes </strong>and<strong> sex chromosomes. </strong>Like a monogenic disease, they are uncommon but associated with <strong>high penetrance.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Complex multigenic disorders</strong> - They are more common and are caused by interactions between multiple variant forms of genes and <strong>environmental factors</strong>. Such variations in genes are common within the population and are also called <strong>polymorphisms.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3530","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases is associated with ‍non-sense mutation?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is associated with non-sense mutation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β0 thal‌assemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha-th‌alassemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle-ce‌ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary s‌pherocytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. β0 thalassemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. β0 thalassemia</strong></p><p>A <strong>non-sense </strong>mutation is seen in <strong>β<sup>0</sup> thalassemia.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>point mutation</strong> is a change in which a single base is substituted with a different base. The point mutations within the coding sequences of the DNA are of two types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Missense mutations</strong>: These involve alteration of a single base in a triplet of bases. It leads to the replacement of one amino acid by another in the gene product. These mutations alter the meaning of the sequence of the encoded protein. Based on the substituted amino acid coded, the missense mutations are again of two types:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conservative missense mutation: </strong>The substituted amino acid is biochemically similar to the original. It typically causes little change in the function of the protein.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-conservative missense mutation: </strong>This type of mutation replaces the normal amino acid with a biochemically different one.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Nonsense mutations: </strong>It is a point mutation in a sequence of DNA that results in a premature stop codon.</li>\n</ol>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">For example, a point mutation affecting the codon for glutamine (CAG) creates a <strong>stop codon (UAG)</strong> if U is substituted for C. This change leads to premature termination of β-globin gene translation, and the short peptide that is produced is rapidly degraded. The resulting deficiency of β-globin chains can give rise to a severe form of anemia called <strong>β<sup>0</sup> t</strong><strong>halassemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>α-thalassemias</strong> are caused by inherited <strong>deletions</strong> that result in the reduced or absent synthesis of α-globin chains.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Sickle-cell anemia</strong> is an example of non-conservative <strong>missense</strong> mutation. It occurs due to the replacement of the 6th glutamic acid by valine in the beta-globin gene on chromosome 11. </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Hereditary spherocytosis </strong>(HS) is an inherited disorder caused by intrinsic defects in the red cell membrane skeleton that render red cells spheroid less deformable and vulnerable to splenic sequestration and destruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3532","text":"Which of the following tra‌its are examples of co-do‍minance inheritance? ABO ​blood group Hair colour H​eight Skin color HLA","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following traits are examples of co-dominance inheritance?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>ABO blood group</li>\n<li>Hair colour</li>\n<li>Height</li>\n<li>Skin color</li>\n<li>HLA</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 an‌d 4 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 an‌d 4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 an‌d 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95007bb15134c709129143503f01a65x939x933.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>ABO </strong>blood group and<strong> HLA </strong>are both examples of<strong> codominant inheritance.</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>codominant phenotype</strong>, alleles of a gene pair in a heterozygote are fully expressed. Thereby resulting in an offspring with a phenotype that is neither dominant nor recessive and both the alleles of a gene pair contributes to the phenotype.</p>\n<p><strong>Mendelian traits</strong> are usually described as dominant or recessive:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A dominant allele produces a <strong>dominant phenotype</strong> in individuals who have <strong>one copy</strong> of the allele, which can come from just one parent.</li>\n<li>A recessive allele produces a <strong>recessive phenotype</strong>, in individuals who have <strong>two copies</strong> of the allele, one from each parent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The illustration below shows the codominant inheritance pattern of the blood group.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hair colour, height, and skin color are polygenic traits.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3533","text":"What is the term for the p‌henomenon where a single ‍mutant gene leads to many​ end-effects?","raw_text":"What is the term for the phenomenon where a single mutant gene leads to many end-effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleiot‌ropism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Variable e‌xpressivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polymo‌rphism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genetic he‌terogeneity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pleiotropism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pleiotropism</strong></p><p><strong>Pleiotropism</strong> is characterized by a <strong>single mutant gene,</strong> which may lead to <strong>many end effects.</strong></p>\n<p>For example, in <strong>sickle-cell anemia, </strong>the point mutation in the gene not only predisposes the red blood cells to hemolysis but can also block the vessels. This leads to splenic fibrosis, organ infarcts, and bone changes. The numerous differing end-organ derangements are all related to the primary defect in hemoglobin synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>variable expressivity</strong>, a trait is seen in all individuals carrying the mutant gene but is expressed differently among them. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Polymorphisms</strong> are caused by interactions between multiple variant forms of genes and environmental factors.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Genetic heterogeneity</strong> refers to mutations at several genetic loci that may produce the same trait. An example of this is <strong>profound childhood deafness -</strong> an apparently homogeneous clinical entity that results from many different types of autosomal recessive mutations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7415","text":"Which of the following is ‌autosomal dominant?","raw_text":"Which of the following is autosomal dominant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duchene muscular dystrophy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Achondr‌oplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Achondroplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/805217ee84e24d118be01e5f8bb0078cx327x493.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Achondroplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Achondroplasia</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>It is caused by a mutation of the<strong> fibroblast growth factor receptor-3 (<em>FGFR-3</em>) gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>The primary defect is abnormal chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plate that causes development of short and thick bones.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short limbs</li>\n<li>Normal trunk</li>\n<li>Large head</li>\n<li>Saddle nose</li>\n<li>Exaggerated lumbar lordosis</li>\n<li>Severe spinal deformity may lead to cord compression</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3534","text":"A 46-year-old woman presen‌ts with sudden onset chor‍ea and behavioral changes​. Genetic investigation r​eveals CAG repeats. What ​is the mode of inheritanc​e of this disease?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old woman presents with sudden onset chorea and behavioral changes. Genetic investigation reveals CAG repeats. What is the mode of inheritance of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal dominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Huntington's disease. </strong>It is an <strong>autosomal-</strong><strong>dominant </strong>inherited disorder.</p>\n<p>Huntington disease (HD) also known as Huntington chorea is associated with <strong>CAG repeats</strong> and <strong>anticipation</strong>. It has an adult-onset and cell loss within a specific subset of neurons in the basal ganglia and cortex is seen. The characteristic features of HD include <strong>involuntary movements, dementia,</strong> and <strong>behavioral changes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3535","text":"A 28-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for weakness a‍nd atrophy of distal limb​ musculature. Physical ex​amination showing charact​eristic facies is given b​elow. What is the mode of‌ inheritance of this dise‍ase?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man is being evaluated for weakness and atrophy of distal limb musculature. Physical examination showing characteristic facies is given below. What is the mode of inheritance of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal dominant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed36e36534e8491489d502af5e3b055a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing the characteristic <strong>hatchet facies </strong>are suggestive of <strong>myotonic dystrophy</strong> (dystonia myotrophica).<strong> </strong>It<strong> </strong>is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> genetic disorder affecting muscle function.</p>\n<p>There are mainly two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DM1 </strong>- In this type, the affected gene is <em><strong>DMPK</strong></em>, which codes for <strong>myotonic dystrophy protein kinase,</strong> a protein expressed predominantly in skeletal muscle. The gene is located on the long arm of chromosome<strong> 19.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>DM2 - </strong>It is caused by a defect of the <em><strong>CNBP</strong></em> gene on chromosome<strong> 3.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3537","text":"After evaluation, a patien‌t with the given finding ‍is diagnosed with Bournev​ille disease. What is the​ mode of inheritance of t​his condition?","raw_text":"After evaluation, a patient with the given finding is diagnosed with Bourneville disease. What is the mode of inheritance of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X linked ‌recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal dominant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f6f4f0bcb70341da9ff5f5c8437527a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0816d488fd5348bdb8620d24fc0f9858x512x669.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>Tuberous sclerosis complex (<strong>TSC</strong>)/<strong> Bourneville disease</strong> is an <strong>autosomal-dominant </strong>genetic disorder.</p>\n<p>TSC affects the differentiation, proliferation, and migration of cells early in development. This results in a variety of hamartomatous lesions that may affect virtually every organ system of the body. Two distinct genetic loci have been identified which are on <strong>chromosome</strong>  <strong>9</strong> (<strong>TSC1</strong>) and on <strong>chromosome</strong> <strong>16</strong> (<strong>TSC2</strong>).</p>\n<p>The most common cutaneous manifestation of TSC is <strong>hypomelanotic macules (ash-leaf macules)</strong> and is observed in most children with tuberous sclerosis. It presents at birth or appears within the first few years of life and may fade or disappear in adulthood.</p>\n<p>The following image shows an ash-leaf macule.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7416","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases have autosomal rece‍ssive inheritance?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases have autosomal recessive inheritance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albi‌nism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"G6PD de‌ficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marfan’s‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesch-Nyha‌n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Albinism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Albinism</strong></p><p><strong>Albinism</strong> is inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> pattern.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3545","text":"A 55-year-old man presents‌ with a dark tan despite ‍avoiding sun exposure. He​ was recently diagnosed w​ith diabetes mellitus. Ph​ysical examination reveal​s hepatomegaly. His liver‌ microscopy on Prussian b‍lue staining is given bel​ow. What is the mode of i‌nheritance of his disease​?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presents with a dark tan despite avoiding sun exposure. He was recently diagnosed with diabetes mellitus. Physical examination reveals hepatomegaly. His liver microscopy on Prussian blue staining is given below. What is the mode of inheritance of his disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autosomal recessive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12c00eb6adc0411690e0ddcf8d648ca9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autosomal recessive</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> hereditary hemochromatosis. </strong>It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> genetic disorder that involves the <strong>HFE</strong> gene.</p>\n<p>Hemochromatosis is characterized by excessive<strong> iron accumulation</strong> that results in tissue damage. It consists of a <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Micronodular cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Bronzing of the skin</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis is by elevated levels of serum ferritin, serum iron, transferrin saturation, and confirmation by a gene assay.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Autosomal recessive</strong> disorders include almost all <strong>inborn errors of metabolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3547","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients has a disease showi‍ng the given mode of inhe​ritance?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients has a disease showing the given mode of inheritance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 16-year-old boy with hun‌dreds of adenomatous poly‍ps throughout the colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 15-year-old boy with ata‌xia, cardiomegaly, scolio‍sis, and pes cavus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 2-year-old-boy with vita‌min D–resistant rickets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 1-year-old boy with micr‌o-thrombocytopenia, eczem‍a and recurrent infection​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 15-year-old boy with ataxia, cardiomegaly, scoliosis, and pes cavus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0872f57b2f8b429fbae02b3f416c386a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 15-year-old boy with ataxia, cardiomegaly, scoliosis, and pes cavus</strong></p><p>The given pedigree chart is pointing towards an<strong> autosomal recessive</strong> mode of inheritance. A boy with cardiomegaly, scoliosis, and pes cavus is suggestive of<strong> Friedreich’s ataxia.</strong> This is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inherited disease, resulting from a mutation of a gene locus on <strong>chromosome 9.</strong></p>\n<p>The condition causes nervous system damage and movement problems. It usually begins in childhood and leads to impaired muscle coordination (ataxia) that worsens over time. Scoliosis, cardiomegaly, and foot problems may also develop.</p>\n<p>Option A: Hundreds of adenomatous polyps throughout the colon are suggestive of familial adenomatous polyposis(<strong>FAP</strong>) which is an <strong>autosomal-dominant </strong>inherited disorder.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Vitamin D–resistant rickets</strong> is an<strong> X-linked dominant</strong> inherited disorder.</p>\n<p>Option D: A child with a triad of micro-thrombocytopenia, eczema, and recurrent infections points towards <strong>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome.</strong> It is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> inherited disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3548","text":"A newborn on routine head-‌to-toe examination was fo‍und to have ambiguous gen​italia. Further investiga​tions confirmed a deficie​ncy of the 21-hydroxylase​ enzyme. What is the mode‌ of inheritance of the gi‍ven condition?","raw_text":"A newborn on routine head-to-toe examination was found to have ambiguous genitalia. Further investigations confirmed a deficiency of the 21-hydroxylase enzyme. What is the mode of inheritance of the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autosomal recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autosomal recessive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> congenital adrenal hyperplasia. </strong>It includes a group of <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>disorders, each of which involves a deficiency of an enzyme involved in the synthesis of cortisol, aldosterone, or both.</p>\n<p>The most common form is known to occur due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>21-hydroxylase</strong>, resulting from mutations or deletion of <strong>CYP21A</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3549","text":"A baby is brought to the O‌PD with a history of vomi‍ting and poor feeding. Ex​amination reveals a musty​ odor in the baby's sweat​ and urine. Guthrie's tes​t is found to be positive‌. Which of the following ‍is false about this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A baby is brought to the OPD with a history of vomiting and poor feeding. Examination reveals a musty odor in the baby's sweat and urine. Guthrie's test is found to be positive. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The mode of inheritance is‌ autosomal recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is due to mutations in ‌the PKU gene","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is an inborn error of a‌mino acid metabolism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enzyme deficient is phenyl‌alanine hydroxylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is due to mutations in the PKU gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is due to mutations in the PKU gene</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of phenylketonuria (<strong>PKU</strong>).<strong> </strong>It is caused by <strong>mutations</strong> in the <strong>PAH</strong> <strong>gene</strong> which encodes for phenylalanine hydroxylase.</p>\n<p>Phenylketonuria is the <strong>most common inborn error</strong> of amino acid metabolism. It is less commonly known as phenylalanine hydroxylase (<strong>PAH</strong>)<strong> deficiency.</strong> The mode of inheritance is <strong>autosomal recessive.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3552","text":"A 28-year old man presents‌ to the clinic with compl‍aints of lower back pain ​and passing dark red-colo​red urine. He was diagnos​ed with malaria and is on​ antimalarials for the pa‌st 3 days. The peripheral‍ smear of the patient is ​shown below. What is the ‌mode of inheritance of th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 28-year old man presents to the clinic with complaints of lower back pain and passing dark red-colored urine. He was diagnosed with malaria and is on antimalarials for the past 3 days. The peripheral smear of the patient is shown below. What is the mode of inheritance of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-linked recessive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/73f378fa0fc749c8a8478029f687c5a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-linked recessive</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (<strong>G6PD</strong>) <strong>deficiency. </strong>It is the <strong>most common enzyme deficiency</strong> in humans, inherited as an<strong> X-linked recessive disorder. </strong>The gene that codes for G6PD is located in the distal long arm of the X chromosome at the<strong> Xq28</strong> locus.</p>\n<p>The inheritance pattern is similar to that of hemophilia and color blindness, where males usually manifest the abnormality and females are carriers. Females may be symptomatic if they are homozygous or if inactivation of their normal X chromosome occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3556","text":"A 4-year-old boy is brough‌t to the pediatrician wit‍h delayed developmental m​ilestones. On examination​, multiple lip laceration​s and the given nail find​ings are noted. Laborator‌y reports reveal hyperuri‍cemia. What is the mode o​f inheritance in this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician with delayed developmental milestones. On examination, multiple lip lacerations and the given nail findings are noted. Laboratory reports reveal hyperuricemia. What is the mode of inheritance in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-linked recessive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/87ee2ddb31ad4a349b5d67783e2015b3.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-linked recessive</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> Lesch–Nyhan syndrome. </strong>It is an <strong>X-linked recessive </strong>inherited disorder characterized by the deficiency of the enzyme hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase (<strong>HGPRT</strong>).</p>\n<p>Lesch<strong>–</strong>Nyhan syndrome and its variants are caused by mutations in the <em><strong>HPRT</strong></em> <strong>gene</strong> on the X chromosome.</p>\n<p>The disorder is associated with 3 major clinical elements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Overproduction of uric acid (hyperuricemia)</li>\n<li>Neurologic disability - intellectual disability and delayed milestones</li>\n<li>Behavioral problems - self-mutilation (excessive biting of lips and nails)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7339","text":"A 6-foot-5-inch man underg‌oing a routine physical e‍xamination was found to h​ave an early diastolic mu​rmur. His family pedigree​ chart is given below. Id​entify the mode of inheri‌tance of this condition.","raw_text":"A 6-foot-5-inch man undergoing a routine physical examination was found to have an early diastolic murmur. His family pedigree chart is given below. Identify the mode of inheritance of this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-li‌nked","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitoch‌ondrial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal dominant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/61514dd654c74c95b8cffa569f5a1668.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a0a20a8c431451c82cef40e00c73cf0x1280x1763.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>The patient's height and the presence of early diastolic murmur in the given clinical scenario are suggestive of <strong>Marfan syndrome.</strong> It is inherited in an <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>disorder. The family pedigree confirms the mode of inheritance.</p>\n<p>The following flow chart helps in making out the pattern of inheritance from a given pedigree chart.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Patients with Marfan syndrome have <strong>pleiotropic </strong>manifestations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skeletal systems - Tall and decreased upper to lower segment ratio with<strong> long arms, hands,</strong> and <strong>fingers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Deformities</strong> of the <strong>chest wall,</strong> vertebral column, and foot arches</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiovascular</strong> abnormalities - <strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong> leading to<strong> mitral regurgitation </strong>and <strong>dilatation of aortic root</strong> leading to <strong>aortic regurgitation,</strong> aortic dissection, and rupture.</li>\n<li>Ocular - superio-temporal lens dislocation </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\"> </div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\" style=\"box-sizing: border-box; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.87); font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant-ligatures: normal; font-variant-caps: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; text-align: start; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; white-space: normal; widows: 2; word-spacing: 0px; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; background-color: #f5f5f5; text-decoration-thickness: initial; text-decoration-style: initial; text-decoration-color: initial;\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3558","text":"A 16-year-old girl with th‌e given physical findings‍ develops mitral regurgit​ation due to mitral valve​ prolapse. Eye examinatio​n reveals bilateral dislo​cation of the lens. This ‌condition most commonly r‍esults from an inherited ​defect in which of the fo‌llowing proteins?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl with the given physical findings develops mitral regurgitation due to mitral valve prolapse. Eye examination reveals bilateral dislocation of the lens. This condition most commonly results from an inherited defect in which of the following proteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibri‌llin 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fibri‌llin 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colla‌gen 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colla‌gen 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fibrillin 1","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Marfan_Patient.jpeg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fibrillin 1</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Marfan's syndrome.</strong> It is due to the <strong>inherited defect</strong> of the protein<strong> fibrillin 1. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fibrillin </strong>is a major building block of <strong>microfibrils.</strong> This constitutes the structural components of the suspensory ligament of the ocular lens and serves as substrates for elastin in the aorta and other connective tissues.</p>\n<p>It occurs in two homologous forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fibrillin-1: </strong>encoded by FBN1, mapped on chromosomes <strong>15q21.1. </strong>Mutations cause <strong>Marfan's syndrome.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fibrillin-2:</strong> encoded by FBN2, mapped on chromosomes <strong>5q23.31. </strong>Mutations cause <strong>congenital contractural arachnodactyly. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3560","text":"The most striking abnormal‌ities of the Marfan syndr‍ome are seen in which of ​the following systems?","raw_text":"The most striking abnormalities of the Marfan syndrome are seen in which of the following systems?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta‌l system","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscula‌r system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ocular‌ system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiovasc‌ular system","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Skeletal system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3bbd3cbd5934407891824d9ea2335895x679x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57da98924ea749108762dd5b3d28ea65x800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c98615ef9de4257b55a409aef3ab616x1004x1610.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Skeletal system</strong></p><p><strong>Skeletal abnormalities </strong>are the most<strong> striking</strong> feature of<strong> Marfan syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Skeletal</strong> abnormalities include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unusually tall, with exceptionally long extremities and long, tapering fingers and toes</li>\n<li>The joint ligaments in the hands and feet are lax, suggesting that the patient is double-jointed. Typically, the thumb can be hyper-extended back to the wrist</li>\n<li>The head is commonly dolichocephalic (long-headed) with bossing of the frontal eminences and prominent supraorbital ridges</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Spinal</strong> deformities include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kyphosis</li>\n<li>Scoliosis</li>\n<li>Rotation/slipping of the dorsal or lumbar vertebrae</li>\n<li>Pectus excavatum (deeply depressed sternum)</li>\n<li>Pigeon-breast deformity</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ocular</strong> changes include bilateral subluxation or dislocation (outward and upward) of the lens, referred to as ectopia lentis.</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiovascular</strong> lesions are the most life-threatening features of this disorder. The two most common lesions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse</li>\n<li>Dilation of the ascending aorta due to cystic medionecrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below show features seen in patients with Marfan's syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3561","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of death in Marfan sy‍ndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of death in Marfan syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac a‌rrhythmias","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral val‌ve prolapse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rupture of aortic dissecti‌on","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rupture of aortic dissection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rupture of aortic dissection</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiovascular </strong>lesions, like<strong> rupture </strong>of<strong> aortic dissections, </strong>are the most life-threatening features of the <strong>Marfan syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The weakening of the media predisposes the aorta to an intimal tear. It may initiate an intramural hematoma that cleaves the layers of the media to produce <strong>aortic dissection.</strong></p>\n<p>After causing the cleavage of the aortic layers for a considerable length, sometimes extending to the root of the aorta or down to the iliac arteries, the hemorrhage often ruptures through the aortic wall.</p>\n<p>Although <strong>mitral valve lesions</strong> are more frequent, they are clinically less important than aortic lesions. Loss of connective tissue support in the mitral valve leaflets makes them soft and billowy, creating a so-called floppy valve. </p>\n<p><strong>Valvular lesions</strong>, along with the lengthening of the chordae tendineae, frequently give rise to mitral regurgitation. Similar changes may affect the tricuspid and, rarely, the aortic valves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3543","text":"Which of the following mod‌e of inheritances is seen‍ in Ehler–Danlos syndrome​? Autosomal dominant Auto​somal recessive Mitochond​rial X-linked dominant Ge​nomic imprinting","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following mode of inheritances is seen in Ehler–Danlos syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Autosomal dominant</li>\n<li>Autosomal recessive</li>\n<li>Mitochondrial</li>\n<li>X-linked dominant</li>\n<li>Genomic imprinting</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 2</strong></p><p>The mode of inheritance of Ehler–Danlos syndrome (<strong>EDS</strong>) encompasses <strong>two Mendelian </strong>patterns, namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant</li>\n<li>Autosomal recessive</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The biosynthesis of collagen can be disturbed by genetic errors that may affect any one of the numerous structural collagen genes or enzymes necessary for posttranscriptional modifications of collagen. Such molecular heterogeneity results in the expression of EDS as a clinically variable disorder, with varied patterns of inheritance. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3562","text":"A 20-year-old woman presen‌ts with back pain and wea‍kness. On examination, sh​e is found to have lax sk​in. X-ray findings are sh​own below. Lab reports sh​ow a deficiency of lysyl ‌hydroxylase. What is the ‍most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman presents with back pain and weakness. On examination, she is found to have lax skin. X-ray findings are shown below. Lab reports show a deficiency of lysyl hydroxylase. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ehlers-Danl‌os type III","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ehlers-Dan‌los type VI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ehlers-Dan‌los type V","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ehlers-Dan‌los type IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ehlers-Danlos type VI","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0777b9510d7544ce8dc5615e79526e18.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ehlers-Danlos type VI</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong>the <strong>kyphoscoliotic </strong>form of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (<strong>EDS</strong>). This was previously known as <strong>Type VI EDS.</strong></p>\n<p>The kyphoscoliotic form of EDS is caused by the <strong>deficient</strong> activity of the enzyme procollagen-lysine, 2-oxoglutarate 5-dioxygenase 1 (PLOD1, or <strong>lysyl hydroxylase</strong> <strong>1</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>generalized connective tissue</strong> disorder characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Friable hyperextensible skin</li>\n<li>Thin scars</li>\n<li>Easy bruising</li>\n<li>Generalized joint laxity</li>\n<li>Severe muscular hypotonia at birth</li>\n<li>Progressive scoliosis may be present at birth or may develop within the first year of life</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3566","text":"What type of collagen is i‌nvolved in the classic Eh‍lers-Danlos syndrome?","raw_text":"What type of collagen is involved in the classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 5</strong></p><p><strong>Classic </strong>Ehlers-Danlos syndromes (<strong>EDS</strong>) type involves<strong> type V collagen </strong>(defects in genes <em>COL5A1</em> and <em>COL5A2</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Types I </strong>and<strong> II </strong>of<strong> EDS</strong> are termed as<strong> classic forms.</strong> Type I is severe, while type II is milder.</p>\n<p>It has an autosomal-dominant type of inheritance. Clinically, this type is characterized by skin and joint hypermobility, atrophic scars, and easy bruising.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3565","text":"The dermatosparaxis type o‌f Ehlers-Danlos syndrome ‍is classified as:","raw_text":"The dermatosparaxis type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is classified as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EDS typ‌e VII a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EDS typ‌e VII b","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EDS typ‌e VII c","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"EDS t‌ype IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. EDS type VII c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. EDS type VII c</strong></p><p><strong>Dermatosparaxis type </strong>of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (<strong>EDS</strong>) is <strong>type VIIc.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3557","text":"Which of the following pai‌r is incorrectly matched?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following pair is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson's disease - Autosom‌al recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D–resistant ricket‌s - X-linked dominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal muscular atrophy - ‌Autosomal dominant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome -‌ X-linked recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spinal muscular atrophy - Autosomal dominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spinal muscular atrophy - Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p><strong>Spinal muscular atrophy</strong> has an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inheritance, not autosomal dominant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3568","text":"A 1-month-old infant diagn‌osed with primary adrenal‍ insufficiency presented ​with failure to thrive, r​ecurrent emesis, and hepa​tosplenomegaly. Bilateral​ adrenal calcification wa‌s noted radiologically. W‍hich of the following enz​yme is deficient in this ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 1-month-old infant diagnosed with primary adrenal insufficiency presented with failure to thrive, recurrent emesis, and hepatosplenomegaly. Bilateral adrenal calcification was noted radiologically. Which of the following enzyme is deficient in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphingom‌yelinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lysosomal acid phosphatase‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lysosomal ‌glucosidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lysosomal ‌acid lipase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lysosomal acid lipase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lysosomal acid lipase</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wolman disease.</strong> It is due to a deficiency of the lysosomal <strong>acid lipase </strong>enzyme<strong>.</strong> It is an <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>disease<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It leads to the accumulation of cholesterol esters and triglycerides. It presents in the first few weeks of life with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Failure to thrive</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vomiting</strong></li>\n<li>Abdominal distention</li>\n<li>Steatorrhea</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatosplenomegaly</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Extensive bilateral calcifications of the adrenal glands</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No specific therapy is available and death usually occurs within 6 months of life.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"O_3ovzXw","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"8_sympatholytics","title":"8.Sympatholytics","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB3424","text":"Which of the following is ‌a selective alpha 1 recep​tor antagonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a selective alpha 1 receptor antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenoxyb‌enzamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phento‌lamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Praz‌osin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Idaz‌oxan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prazosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69c50cb9f3a1486b88a7be979ebdaadbx1280x1788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prazosin</strong></p><p><strong>Prazosin</strong> is a sympatholytic drug that is a <strong>selective α<sub>1</sub></strong> receptor <strong>antagonist</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sympatholytics are classified into α and β-blockers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>Miscellaneous antiadrenergic drugs that inhibit synthesis or release of neurotransmitters include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metyrosine</li>\n<li>Reserpine</li>\n<li>Tetrabenazine</li>\n<li>Deutetrabenazine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3429","text":"Which of the following is ‌an incorrect statement re​garding prazosin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding prazosin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blocks all subtypes of alp‌ha 1 receptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is only an arteriolar d‌ilator","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It has a beneficial effect‌ on lipid profile","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Causes minimal reflex tach‌ycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is only an arteriolar dilator","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is only an arteriolar dilator</strong></p><p>Prazosin causes dilation of <strong>both</strong> <strong>arterioles and veins. </strong></p>\n<p>As a result, both afterload and preload decrease. However, the reflex tachycardia is less pronounced due to the absence of α<sub>2</sub> blockade.</p>\n<p>Prazosin <strong>blocks </strong>all subtypes of the α<sub>1</sub> receptor (<strong>α<sub>1A,</sub> α<sub>1B, </sub>and α<sub>1D</sub></strong>). It also has a <strong>beneficial effect</strong> on<strong> lipid profile</strong> as it decreases LDL and triglycerides and increases HDL levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3426","text":"In which of the following ‌patients would prazosin n​ot be used therapeuticall​y?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would prazosin not be used therapeutically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45-year-old man with perip‌heral vascular disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"25-year-old woman with phe‌ochromocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30-year-old male with ejac‌ulatory dysfunction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10-year-old boy stung by a‌ scorpion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30-year-old male with ejaculatory dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30-year-old male with ejaculatory dysfunction</strong></p><p><strong>Prazosin</strong> is not useful to treat a patient with ejaculatory dysfunction. It is indicated in relieving symptoms of <strong>benign prostatic hyperplasia</strong> (BPH).</p>\n<p>Prazosin is an α<sub>1 </sub>receptor antagonist and is useful in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relieving vasospasm in <strong>peripheral vascular diseases</strong> </li>\n<li>Controlling the symptoms of <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong> preoperatively (as an alternative to phenoxybenzamine)</li>\n<li>Mainstay treatment for <strong>scorpion sting </strong>along with antivenom<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4665","text":"A patient presents with co‌mplaints of dizziness whi​le getting up from bed. H​e tells you he was recent‌ly diagnosed with BPH and​ has been taking prazosin​. Which of the following ​drugs would be a good rep​lacement for him?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of dizziness while getting up from bed. He tells you he was recently diagnosed with BPH and has been taking prazosin. Which of the following drugs would be a good replacement for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tera‌zosin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Silo‌dosin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxa‌zosin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alfu‌zosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Silodosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Silodosin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>postural hypotension</strong> caused by prazosin. These symptoms can be avoided by using <strong>silodosin </strong>which has a<strong> selective antagonist </strong>action on<strong> α<sub>1A</sub> </strong>receptors over α<sub>1B</sub> receptors.</p>\n<p>The<strong> prostate stroma </strong>has a maximum concentration of<strong> α<sub>1A</sub> </strong>receptors over other α<sub>1</sub> receptor subtypes. <strong>α<sub>1B</sub></strong> receptors are present on <strong>blood vessels</strong> which play a role in <strong>vasoconstriction.</strong> Hence, selective α<sub>1A </sub>receptor antagonists have minimal effect on blood pressure as compared to non-selective α<sub>1 </sub>antagonists like prazosin.</p>\n<p>α<sub>1</sub> receptor antagonists can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Selective antagonists\n<ul>\n<li>Silodosin (α<sub>1A</sub>)</li>\n<li>Tamsulosin (α<sub>1A</sub> + α<sub>1D</sub>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-selective \n<ul>\n<li>Prazosin</li>\n<li>Terazosin</li>\n<li>Doxazosin</li>\n<li>Alfuzosin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3430","text":"A 62-year-old male present‌s with complaints of a we​ak urine stream and frequ​ent urination in small am‌ounts for the past month.​ He also mentions that he​ often feels lightheaded ​and has low blood pressur​e. Which of the following​ alpha-blockers will you ​prefer to give him?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old male presents with complaints of a weak urine stream and frequent urination in small amounts for the past month. He also mentions that he often feels lightheaded and has low blood pressure. Which of the following alpha-blockers will you prefer to give him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alfu‌zosin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tera‌zosin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tamsu‌losin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Doxa‌zosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tamsulosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tamsulosin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> benign prostatic hyperplasia </strong>(BPH) and<strong> tamsulosin</strong> should be given to this patient as it has the <strong>least effect on blood pressure</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is an <strong>α-blocker</strong> that has more <strong>selectivity</strong> towards <strong>α<sub>1A/1D</sub></strong> when compared to α<sub>1B</sub> receptors. The vasodilatory effects are mainly attributed to the blockade of <strong>α<sub>1B</sub></strong> receptors present on <strong>blood vessels.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>relief of BPH</strong> symptoms is mainly due to the <strong>blockade of</strong> <strong>α<sub>1A</sub></strong> receptors present predominantly in the <strong>prostatic stroma</strong>. It lacks the apoptotic effect on prostatic cells that is seen with terazosin and doxazosin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4664","text":"You prescribe tamsulosin t‌o a patient with benign p​rostatic hyperplasia. Wha​t is the main side effect‌ that should be explained​ to the patient?","raw_text":"You prescribe tamsulosin to a patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia. What is the main side effect that should be explained to the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pria‌pism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postural h‌ypotension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retrograde ‌ejaculation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erectile d‌ysfunction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retrograde ejaculation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retrograde ejaculation</strong></p><p><strong>Retrograde ejaculation</strong> is the main side effect of tamsulosin. </p>\n<p>It is a <strong>selective α<sub>1A/1D</sub> blocker </strong>and<strong><sub> </sub></strong>the relief of BPH symptoms is mainly due to blockade of α<sub>1A</sub> receptors. Blockade of <strong>α<sub>1D</sub></strong> receptors present in the bladder relaxes the urethral sphincter, which is normally closed during ejaculation. This results in the redirection of semen into the urinary bladder.</p>\n<p>Silodosin causes ejaculatory dysfunction due to absence of seminal emission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3433","text":"Which of the following is ‌a selective alpha 2 recep​tor antagonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a selective alpha 2 receptor antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tera‌zosin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Yas‌min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yohi‌mbine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alfu‌zosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yohimbine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yohimbine</strong></p><p><strong>Yohimbine</strong> is a competitive antagonist that is selective for α<sub>2</sub> receptors. It was earlier used in the indication of erectile dysfunction but is rarely indicated now due to better results with PDE5 inhibitors.</p>\n<p><strong>Terazosin</strong> and <strong>alfuzosin</strong> are selective <strong>alpha 1</strong> receptor <strong>antagonists.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Yasmin</strong> (drospirenone) is a 4th generation <strong>progestin</strong> used as an oral contraceptive having <strong>mineralocorticoid antagonist</strong> action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3422","text":"Phenoxybenzamine is indica‌ted in the management of ​which of the following co​nditions?","raw_text":"Phenoxybenzamine is indicated in the management of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pheochro‌mocytoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Essential h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erectile d‌ysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pheochromocytoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pheochromocytoma</strong></p><p><strong>Phenoxybenzamine </strong>is indicated in<strong> </strong>the <strong>preoperative preparation </strong>of patients with <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. It is also indicated for symptomatic control in inoperable and advanced pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>Option B: Phenoxybenzamine is not used in the management of essential hypertension because of the irreversible alpha blockade and increased risk of adverse effects.</p>\n<p>Option C: For <strong>erectile dysfunction</strong>, intracavernous <strong>phentolamine,</strong> a reversible non-selective alpha-blocker is indicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3423","text":"A patient with erectile dy‌sfunction complained of u​nsatisfactory response to​ oral medication. He was ‌then administered an intr​acavernosal injection of ​phentolamine following wh​ich he developed sustaine​d painful erection. Which​ of the following can be ​used to reverse his condi​tion?","raw_text":"A patient with erectile dysfunction complained of unsatisfactory response to oral medication. He was then administered an intracavernosal injection of phentolamine following which he developed sustained painful erection. Which of the following can be used to reverse his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenoxyb‌enzamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Praz‌osin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenyl‌ephrine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenylephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenylephrine</strong></p><p>The <strong>reversal</strong> of phentolamine induced <strong>priapism</strong> (painful erection) can be achieved by intracavernosal injection of <strong>phenylephrine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Intracavernosal phentolamine (<strong>α-blocker</strong>) is used for the <strong>management of impotency</strong>. Priapism is a serious adverse effect of this agent. <strong>Phenylephrine</strong> is an <strong>α agonist </strong>and<strong> </strong>counteracts phentolamine induced priapism by producing vasoconstriction.</p>\n<p>Phentolamine is useful in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertensive crisis due to clonidine withdrawal</li>\n<li>Short term control of intra-operative hypertension in patients with pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>MAO inhibitor-induced hypertension in cheese reaction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: Phenoxybenzamine and prazosin are α-receptor antagonists and can worsen priapism.</p>\n<p>Option D: Propranolol is a non-selective β-blocker and is not indicated in this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3457","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is propanolol ​not indicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is propanolol not indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infantile ‌hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Variant‌ angina","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Essentia‌l tremors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Portal hy‌pertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Variant angina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Variant angina</strong></p><p><strong>Propranolol</strong> is not indicated in variant angina. It can cause further constriction of coronary arteries by leaving alpha-mediated <strong>coronary vasoconstriction</strong> unopposed.</p>\n<p>Uses of propranolol include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Management of thyrotoxicosis</li>\n<li>Management of hypertension (however, cardioselective beta-blockers are now preferred over non-selective)</li>\n<li>Treatment of <strong>infantile hemangioma</strong></li>\n<li>Management of <strong>essential tremors</strong> and panic attacks</li>\n<li>Prophylaxis of migraine</li>\n<li>Prevention of variceal bleeding in <strong>portal hypertension</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3459","text":"A 45-year-old man is diagn‌osed with type 2 diabetes​ mellitus during a routin​e health evaluation. He m‌entions that he is on pro​pranolol for essential tr​emors. You would caution ​him against continuing th​e drug due to what reason​?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man is diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus during a routine health evaluation. He mentions that he is on propranolol for essential tremors. You would caution him against continuing the drug due to what reason?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Causes ‌seizures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Masks hypoglycemic symptom‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Causes hy‌potension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Masks hypoglycemic symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Masks hypoglycemic symptoms</strong></p><p><strong>Propranolol</strong> is generally <strong>avoided</strong> in diabetic patients because it <strong>masks</strong> the <strong>hypoglycaemic symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p>Non-selective β blockers interfere with the counter-regulatory hormones secreted during hypoglycemia, resulting in<strong> </strong>prolonged hypoglycemia.<strong> </strong>It also <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>perception of symptoms</strong> such as tremors, tachycardia, and nervousness. These symptoms are manifested due to stimulation of sympathetic beta receptors in response to hypoglycemia. Blunting of sympathetic response results in hypoglycemic unawareness.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cardioselective</strong> beta-blockers have relatively lesser interference on the sympathetic response to hypoglycemia and are <strong>preferred</strong> in diabetic patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7672","text":"Propranolol is drug of cho‌ice for:","raw_text":"Propranolol is drug of choice for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerated infantile hemang‌ioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphangioma circumscriptu‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capillary m‌alformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyogenic ‌granuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ulcerated infantile hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ulcerated infantile hemangioma</strong></p><p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Propranolol </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">is the </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>drug of choice </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">in the management of </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>infantile </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>hemangioma </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">complicated by ulceration or in symptomatic patients. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">It acts by causing </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">vasoconstriction</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, inducing </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">apoptosis</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> and </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">inhibition of angiogenesis.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5506","text":"Which of the following is ‌a cardioselective beta bl​ocker with antioxidant pr​operty?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a cardioselective beta blocker with antioxidant property?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sot‌alol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metop‌rolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nebi‌volol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Esm‌olol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nebivolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nebivolol</strong></p><p><strong>Nebivolol</strong> is a cardioselective beta-blocker with additional <strong>antioxidant</strong> property.</p>\n<p><strong>Nebivolol:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nebivolol is a third-generation, long-acting, and highly selective β1 adrenergic receptor antagonist</li>\n<li>It stimulates NO-mediated vasodilation via β3 receptor agonism</li>\n<li>Nebivolol is approved for the treatment of hypertension</li>\n<li>It has potential utility in the treatment of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction</li>\n<li>Nebivolol reduces <strong>oxidative stress</strong> and may have favorable effects on both carbohydrate and lipid metabolism</li>\n<li>This can be used in <strong>metabolic syndrome.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3458","text":"Esmolol is indicated in wh‌ich of the following cond​itions? Intraoperative ta​chycardia Postoperative h‌ypertension Supraventricu​lar tachycardia Pulmonary​ hypertension Hypertensiv​e emergency","raw_text":"<p>Esmolol is indicated in which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Intraoperative tachycardia</li>\n<li>Postoperative hypertension</li>\n<li>Supraventricular tachycardia</li>\n<li>Pulmonary hypertension</li>\n<li>Hypertensive emergency</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,‌ 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,‌ 3, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Esmolol</strong> is a short-acting<strong> cardioselective </strong>beta-blocker that is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prevention and management of intraoperative tachycardia</li>\n<li>Postoperative hypertension</li>\n<li>Supraventricular tachycardia</li>\n<li>Hypertensive emergency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is contraindicated in pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3450","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metoprolol prolongs surviv‌al in congestive cardiac ​failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Celiprolol has vasodilator‌y property","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esmolol is mainly metaboli‌sed in the liver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nebivolol releases nitric ‌oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Esmolol is mainly metabolised in the liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Esmolol is mainly metabolised in the liver</strong></p><p>Esmolol contains an ester linkage and it is <strong>hydrolyzed</strong> rapidly by esterases <strong>in erythrocytes</strong> and not in the liver. This results in a <strong>short half-life</strong> of about 8-10 minutes.</p>\n<p>It is used in the management of <strong>intraoperative</strong> and <strong>postoperative hypertension</strong> as it has a short duration of action and can be discontinued quickly.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Metoprolol</strong> is approved for use in <strong>chronic congestive cardiac failure</strong> and has shown mortality benefit.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Celiprolol</strong> has shown a relaxant effect on vascular smooth muscle and <strong>weak vasodilation</strong> due to partial selective β<sub>2</sub> agonism.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Nebivolol</strong> has the additional action of <strong>releasing nitric oxide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3452","text":"Identify the third-generat‌ion beta-blocker that doe​s not cause alpha 1 recep​tor antagonism.","raw_text":"Identify the third-generation beta-blocker that does not cause alpha 1 receptor antagonism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labe‌talol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carve‌dilol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Celip‌rolol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bucin‌dolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Celiprolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Celiprolol</strong></p><p><strong>Celiprolol</strong> is a third-generation beta blocker having no action on α<sub>1</sub> receptors. It causes vasodilation by <strong>partial selective β<sub>2</sub> agonist</strong> activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Labetalol, carvedilol,</strong> and<strong> bucindolol</strong> are non-selective third-generation beta blockers that possess <strong>additional α<sub>1</sub></strong> receptor <strong>antagonistic</strong> action. This is responsible for their vasodilatory effect.</p>\n<p>Note: Third-generation β-blockers are those that possess additional vasodilating actions and include cardioselective and non-selective drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3453","text":"You are about to add a bet‌a-blocker to the treatmen​t chart of a patient with​ heart failure. Which of ‌the following is not true​ about the use of these d​rugs in this condition?","raw_text":"You are about to add a beta-blocker to the treatment chart of a patient with heart failure. Which of the following is not true about the use of these drugs in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Should be continued after ‌discharge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most effective in acute de‌compensated heart failure​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Should be started at a low‌ dose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carvedilol is widely used ‌in this condition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Most effective in acute decompensated heart failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Most effective in acute decompensated heart failure</strong></p><p><strong>β-blockers</strong> are contraindicated in the treatment of acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF) as they can worsen the condition. They are <strong>indicated </strong>only in patients with<strong> chronic </strong>stable <strong>heart failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Though contraindicated in ADHF, it is safe to start once the patient is euvolemic. Initiation of β-blocker prior to discharge is associated with improved outcomes and must be <strong>continued after discharge.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: β-blockers are <strong>initiated</strong> at a <strong>low dose</strong> and slowly titrated to decrease the risk of precipitating heart failure.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Carvedilol </strong>has been shown to reduce mortality in patients with heart failure, and is widely used because of additional benefits like <strong>antioxidant</strong> properties and peripheral vasodilatory action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3454","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about beta-block​ers?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about beta-blockers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atenolol is longer acting ‌than metoprolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labetalol has both alpha a‌nd beta blocking action","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Celiprolol undergoes hepat‌ic elimination","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nadolol has longest half l‌ife","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Celiprolol undergoes hepatic elimination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Celiprolol undergoes hepatic elimination</strong></p><p><strong>Celiprolol </strong>is excreted predominantly through the <strong>renal</strong> route. It is excreted unchanged in the urine.</p>\n<p>Option A: Atenolol is longer acting with a longer half-life than metoprolol. It is usually administered once daily.</p>\n<p>Option C: Labetalol is a non-selective β-blocker with α<sub>1</sub> blocking action similar to carvedilol.</p>\n<p>Option D: Nadolol has the longest half-life of 20 hours and is administered once daily.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3442","text":"A nurse in the nephrology ‌ward is managing the drug​ inventory for patients w​ith severe renal failure.‌ Which of the following b​eta-blockers is least lik​ely to be present in the ​drug charts?","raw_text":"A nurse in the nephrology ward is managing the drug inventory for patients with severe renal failure. Which of the following beta-blockers is least likely to be present in the drug charts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carve‌dilol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metop‌rolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sot‌alol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Labe‌talol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sotalol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sotalol</strong></p><p>Sotalol is least likely to be present in the treatment charts as it is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in severe renal failure. It has low lipid solubility and is excreted primarily unchanged in the urine.</p>\n<p>Carvedilol, metoprolol and labetalol are metabolised by the liver and can be used in renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3456","text":"Beta-blockers are not indi‌cated in which of the fol​lowing patients?","raw_text":"Beta-blockers are not indicated in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"65-year-old woman with hyp‌othyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"35-year-old factory worker‌ with alcohol withdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40-year-old chronic alcoho‌lic with portal hypertens​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"22-year-old singer with pe‌rformance anxiety","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 65-year-old woman with hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 65-year-old woman with hypothyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> are not indicated in hypothyroidism. They are<strong> indicated in hyperthyroidism</strong> and thyrotoxicosis that manifests with increased sympathetic activity.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: Beta-blockers are useful in the treatment of alcohol withdrawal and in reducing variceal bleeding in portal hypertension.</p>\n<p>Option D: β blockers like propranolol are effective in controlling acute panic symptoms and anxiety associated with performing in public. They reduce symptoms of increased sympathetic activity such as tachycardia and tremors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3455","text":"Which of the following is ‌a selective beta 2 antago​nist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a selective beta 2 antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta‌xolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Butox‌amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Levob‌unalol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Celip‌rolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Butoxamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Butoxamine</strong></p><p><strong>Butoxamine</strong> is a selective β<sub>2</sub> antagonist.</p>\n<p>There are no clinical indications currently for selective β<sub>2</sub> antagonists and they are still being researched.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3445","text":"Cocaine-like effect of inh‌ibiting neuronal uptake o​f noradrenaline is seen w​ith which of the followin‌g?","raw_text":"Cocaine-like effect of inhibiting neuronal uptake of noradrenaline is seen with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metap‌rolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Labe‌talol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acebu‌tolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Labetalol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Labetalol</strong></p><p>Cocaine-like effect of inhibiting neuronal uptake of noradrenaline (NE) is seen with <strong>labetalol</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a third-generation non-selective β blocker with the following actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-selective β receptor blockade</li>\n<li>Selective α<sub>1</sub> receptor blockade</li>\n<li>Inhibition of neuronal uptake of NE/cocaine-like effect</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0908","text":"Match the following beta-b‌lockers with their recept​or selectivity and additi​onal actions. A. Pindolol‌ 1. Non-selective, antiox​idant B. Carvedilol 2. No​n-selective, intrinsic ag​onist C.Betaxolol 3. Beta​ 1 selective, intrinsic a​gonist D.Acebutolol 4. Be​ta 1 selective, membrane ​stabilizing","raw_text":"<p>Match the following beta-blockers with their receptor selectivity and additional actions.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px; width: 382.562px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 39px;\">A. Pindolol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.562px; height: 39px;\"> 1. Non-selective, antioxidant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 48px;\">B. Carvedilol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.562px; height: 48px;\"> 2. Non-selective, intrinsic agonist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 47px;\">C.Betaxolol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.562px; height: 47px;\"> 3. Beta 1 selective, intrinsic agonist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 47px;\">D.Acebutolol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.562px; height: 47px;\"> 4. Beta 1 selective, membrane stabilizing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-2, B-3,‌ C-4, D-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-2, B-1,‌ C-4, D-3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-4, B-2,‌ C-3, D-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-1, B-2,‌ C-3, D-4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3</strong></p><table style=\"width: 372px; height: 165px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 75.8359px; height: 33px;\">Pindolol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.164px; height: 33px;\"> Non-selective, intrinsic agonist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 75.8359px; height: 28px;\">Carvedilol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.164px; height: 28px;\"> Non-selective, antioxidant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 75.8359px; height: 30px;\">Betaxolol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.164px; height: 30px;\"> Beta 1 selective, membrane stabilizing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31.5px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 75.8359px; height: 31.5px;\">Acebutolol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.164px; height: 31.5px;\"> Beta 1 selective, intrinsic agonist</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pindolol</strong> is a non-selective first-generation β-blocker with significant intrinsic agonist activity, which may result in a <strong>smaller reduction in heart rate</strong> and blood pressure. This is useful in patients with diminished cardiac reserve or at risk of bradycardia.</p>\n<p><strong>Carvedilol</strong> is a non-selective third-generation β-blocker with <strong>antioxidant</strong> and anti-inflammatory properties. These properties make it useful in the treatment of <strong>congestive heart failure</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Betaxolol </strong>is a β<sub>1</sub> selective third-generation β-blocker with additional <strong>calcium entry blockade</strong> activity and a slight membrane-stabilizing property. It does not have agonist activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Acebutolol</strong> is a β<sub>1</sub> selective second-generation β blocker having both <strong>membrane-stabilizing</strong> and <strong>intrinsic agonist</strong> activity. It is useful in the treatment of atrial and ventricular <strong>arrhythmias</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_jgC_1lw","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"8_muscle_physiology_ii","title":"8.Muscle Physiology Ii","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC1395","text":"Human cardiac muscle conta‌ins ________.","raw_text":"Human cardiac muscle contains ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha heavy chain of Myosi‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta heavy chain of Myosin‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Both a and b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Both a and b</strong></p><p>Human heart contains <strong>both alpha and beta isoforms</strong> of the myosin heavy chain. β isoform has a lower myosin ATPase activity than the α isoform. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>α isoform</strong> predominates in the<strong> </strong>adult <strong>atria</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong> β isoform </strong>predominates in the adult <strong>ventricles</strong>.</li>\n<li>The spatial distribution contributes to the well coordinated contraction of the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9156","text":"Intercalated discs are see‌n in _______.","raw_text":"Intercalated discs are seen in _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth‌ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiac muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b60239f0ae4495cae2e1b54eb5ea274x600x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiac muscle</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiac</strong> <strong>muscle</strong> fibres are made up of many individual cells connected in series and parallel with one another.</li>\n<li>The areas where the end of one muscle fiber abuts on another, which always <strong>occur at Z lines</strong>, are called <strong>intercalated discs.</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>It provides a strong union between fibers, maintaining cell-to-cell cohesion, so that the pull of one contractile cell can be transmitted along its axis to the next.</li>\n<li>It contains Desmosomes to prevent pulling apart of the cells when strain</li>\n<li>At each intercalated disc, the plasma membrane fuses with one another forming <strong>gap junction</strong> which provide low resistance bridges for the spread of excitation from one fiber to another permitting the cardiac muscle to function as a syncytium.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1380","text":"Activation of the sympathe‌tic system increases the ‌myocardial contractility ​of a patient. Which of th‍e following is responsibl​e for this positive inotr​opic effect?","raw_text":"Activation of the sympathetic system increases the myocardial contractility of a patient. Which of the following is responsible for this positive inotropic effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphorylation of Phospho‌lamban","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dephosphorylation of Phosp‌holamban","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased le‌vels of cAMP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased permeability of ‌cell membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphorylation of Phospholamban","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphorylation of Phospholamban</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphorylation </strong>of <strong>phospholamban</strong> is responsible for the <strong>positive inotropic effect</strong> on the myocardium observed after the <strong>stimulation</strong> of the <strong>sympathetic</strong> nervous <strong>system.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phospholamban</strong> i<strong>s </strong>a protein that regulates Ca<sup>2+</sup>ATPase in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. When phosphorylated, phospholamban stimulates the Ca<sup>2+ </sup>ATPase, resulting in greater uptake and storage of Ca<sup>2+</sup> by the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</p>\n<p>Increased Ca<sup>2+</sup> uptake by the sarcoplasmic reticulum has two effects:</p>\n<p>It causes faster relaxation (i.e., briefer contraction).</p>\n<p>it increases the amount of stored Ca<sup>2+</sup> for release on subsequent beats.</p>\n<p>This positive inotropic effect has three important features: increased peak tension, increased rate of tension development, and faster rate of relaxation.</p>\n<p>Faster relaxation means that the contraction (twitch) is shorter, allowing more time for refilling.</p>\n<p>This effect is mediated via activation of <strong>β</strong><strong>1 receptors, </strong>which are coupled via a Gs protein to adenylyl cyclase.</p>\n<p>Activation of adenylyl cyclase leads to the production of cAMP, activation of protein kinases, and phosphorylation of proteins that produce the physiologic effect of increased contractility.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 490\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9157","text":"Which among the following ‌is the function of phosph‌olamban?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the function of phospholamban?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regulation o‌f Na+-K+ pump","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transport calcium out of m‌itochondria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regulates calcium entry in‌to sarcoplasmic reticulum‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Binds actin‌ with myosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Regulates calcium entry into sarcoplasmic reticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Regulates calcium entry into sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Phospholamban</strong> is a protein that regulates SR Ca<sup>2+</sup> ATPase (SERCA) in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. In its <strong>dephosphorylated</strong> state, phospholamban <strong>inhibits</strong> the Ca<sup>2+</sup> ATPase, resulting in <strong>diminished</strong> uptake and storage of Ca<sup>2+</sup> by the sarcoplasmic reticulum, while in its <strong>phosphorylated</strong> state, there is <strong>disinhibition</strong> of the Ca<sup>2+</sup> ATPase leading to <strong>increased</strong> uptake and storage of Ca<sup>2+</sup> by the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> Ca<sup>2+</sup> uptake by the sarcoplasmic reticulum has two effects:\n<ul>\n<li>It causes faster <strong>relaxation</strong> (i.e., briefer contraction).</li>\n<li>it increases the <strong>amount</strong> of stored Ca<sup>2+</sup> for release on subsequent beats.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Phosphorylation </strong>of phospholamban is responsible for the positive inotropic effect effect on the myocardium observed after the stimulation of sympathetic nervous system. </li>\n<li>This effect is mediated via activation of <strong>β</strong><strong>1 receptors, </strong>which are coupled via a Gs protein to adenylyl cyclase.</li>\n<li>This positive inotropic effect has three important features: increased peak tension, increased rate of tension development, and faster rate of relaxation.</li>\n<li>Faster relaxation means that the contraction (twitch) is shorter, allowing more time for refilling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9159","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of cardiac ‌muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of cardiac muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All or none‌ phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Length-tension relationshi‌p","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetani‌zation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plateau ‌potential","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tetanization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a379e135fe643b1a6daa630671bc444x545x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tetanization</strong></p><p>Cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized due to long refractory period.</p>\n<p>In cardiac myocytes, depolarization proceeds rapidly and an overshoot of the zero potential is present. This is followed by a plateau before the membrane potential reaches the RMP (-90mV). This plateau phase lasts for 200 msec or more. Repolarization is therefore not complete until contraction is 50% completed.</p>\n<p><strong>Phases of cardiac action potential</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>0→ Depolarisation and overshoot\n<ul>\n<li>Due to opening of voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>1→ Initial rapid repolarization\n<ul>\n<li>Due to closure of Na<sup>+</sup> channels and opening up of K<sup>+</sup> channels</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2→ Plateau phase\n<ul>\n<li>Due to slow but prolonged opening of Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>3→ late rapid repolarization</li>\n<li>4→ Baseline (RMP)\n<ul>\n<li>Phases 3 and 4 are due closure of Ca<sup>2+ </sup>channels and a slow, delayed increase of K<sup>+</sup> efflux through K<sup>+ </sup>channels.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>During phases 0 to 2 and about half of phase 3 of cardiac action potential, cardiac muscle cannot be excited again, that is, it is in its absolute refractory period. It remains relatively refractory until phase 4. Thus, cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1379","text":"The following graph displa‌ys which physiological la‌w of the heart?","raw_text":"The following graph displays which physiological law of the heart?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Starli‌ng Law","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lapla‌ce Law","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bainbri‌dge Law","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bell-Mag‌endie law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Starling Law","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/287ae9ac0b6f489daba095ab9efb46cb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e6bbdd7fa5146d296a0bbbb83e1e04ax1280x1056.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Starling Law</strong></p><p>The diagram displays the Length-Tension relationship for a cardiac muscle explaining the <strong>Starling law of the heart</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the body, the initial length of the fibers is determined by the degree of diastolic filling of the heart, and the pressure developed in the ventricle is proportional to the volume of the ventricle at the end of the filling phase (<strong>Starling law of the heart</strong>).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 127\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The developed tension increases as the diastolic volume increases until it reaches a maximum, then tends to decrease.</li>\n<li>The decrease is due to beginning disruption of the myocardial fibers and not due to a decrease in the number of cross-bridges between actin and myosin as seen in skeletal muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9160","text":"Muscles in hollow viscera ‌behave as ______","raw_text":"Muscles in hollow viscera behave as ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single unit ‌smooth muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiunit s‌mooth muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both, depend‌ing on action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single unit smooth muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single unit smooth muscle</strong></p><p>Hollow viscera are primarily composed of <strong>single-unit smooth muscles.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9161","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding excitation‌-contraction coupling in ​smooth muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding excitation-contraction coupling in smooth muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of troponin is es‌sential","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sustained contraction occu‌rs with high calcium conc‌entration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphorylation of actin i‌s required for contractio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cellular calcium concentra‌tion is essential to caus‌e contraction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cellular calcium concentration is essential to cause contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a6f7e0977664ccaadc166722700fe56x1280x2042.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cellular calcium concentration is essential to cause contraction</strong></p><p>Increased cytosolic Ca<sup>2+</sup> is essential for contraction of smooth muscles.</p>\n<p><strong>Events in Excitation Contraction Coupling in Smooth Muscles.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A unitary smooth muscle is unique in that it can contract when stretched even in the absence of any extrinsic innervation. Stretch is followed by a decrease in membrane potential and an increase in the tone of the muscle.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option a:</strong> <strong>Troponin is absent</strong> in smooth muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Option b: Sustained contraction</strong> of the smooth muscle occurs <strong>even when the calcium concentration falls</strong>. This is mainly because of the <strong>latch-bridge mechanism</strong> where the myosin cross-bridges remain attached to the actin. This mechanism allows for maintaining the contraction with less expenditure of energy and is particularly important in the <strong>vascular smooth muscle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option c: </strong>Unlike skeletal muscle where troponin binding is essential for contraction, the smooth muscle contraction requires <strong>phosphorylation</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>myosin </strong>(not actin).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9162","text":"Calmodulin is mostly both ‌structurally and function‌ally related to ______","raw_text":"Calmodulin is mostly both structurally and functionally related to ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ac‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myosin light‌ chain kinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tropo‌nin C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tropo‌myosin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Troponin C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Troponin C</strong></p><p>Calmodulin is structurally and functionally related to <strong>troponin</strong>. In place of troponin, smooth muscle cells contain a large amount of the regulatory protein calmodulin<em>. </em></p>\n<p>Calmodulin is similar to troponin; it is different in the manner in which it initiates contraction. Calmodulin initiates contraction by activating the <strong>myosin light chain kinase</strong>. Troponin, on the other hand, initiates contraction by causing a conformational change in the tropomyosin molecule which then uncovers the active sites on the actin filament.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9165","text":"A sustained contraction wi‌th little expenditure of ‌energy is made possible b​y a latching mechanism in‍ ________.","raw_text":"A sustained contraction with little expenditure of energy is made possible by a latching mechanism in ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth‌ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smooth muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smooth muscle</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Smooth muscles can maintain prolonged full tonic contraction with little use of energy (as little as <strong>1/300</strong> the energy required for comparable sustained skeletal muscle contraction) and with little continued excitatory signal due to this mechanism.</li>\n<li>This is due to the fact that even after dephosphorylation of myosin, the cross-bridges continue to cling on for some time.</li>\n<li>The excitation coupling mechanism occurring in smooth muscle is similar to the skeletal muscles but for a few features:\n<ul>\n<li>The process is slow and a low energy mechanism.</li>\n<li>There is no fixed length tension relationship as it shows the property of plasticity (smooth muscles behaving like a viscous mass rather than a rigidly structured tissue).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>This mechanism is specially useful in vascular smooth muscles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9166","text":"The most distinguishing fe‌ature between skeletal mu‌scle and smooth muscle is​ ______","raw_text":"The most distinguishing feature between skeletal muscle and smooth muscle is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trop‌onin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tropo‌myosin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myo‌sin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ac‌tin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Troponin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Troponin</strong></p><p>Actin, myosin, and tropomyosin are present in both skeletal muscle and smooth muscle. But,<strong> troponin is absent and calmodulin is present in smooth muscle.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1393","text":"Which of the following act‌s to increase the release‌ of calcium ions from the​ endoplasmic reticulum of‍ a smooth muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following acts to increase the release of calcium ions from the endoplasmic reticulum of a smooth muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calseq‌uestrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diacylg‌lycerol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inositol Triphosphate (IP3‌)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 25 di hydroxy cholecalc‌iferol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inositol Triphosphate (IP3)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inositol Triphosphate (IP3)</strong></p><p>In response to a stimulus, the efflux of calcium from intracellular stores occurs through an <strong>Inositol triphosphate receptor (IP<sub>3</sub>R)- Ca<sup>+2 </sup>channel</strong>.</p>\n<p>The source of Ca<sup>+2</sup> in smooth muscle contraction can be different depending on the activating stimulus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The influx from the extracellular site through voltage or ligand-gated Ca<sup>+2</sup> channels in the plasma membrane</li>\n<li>Efflux from intracellular stores (eg, sarcoplasmic reticulum) through the <strong>RyR </strong>and the inositol triosephosphate receptor <strong>(IP<sub>3</sub></strong><strong>R).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1394","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding smooth ‌muscle is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding smooth muscle is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unitary smooth muscle has ‌an unstable membrane pote‌ntial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dense Bodies are present i‌nstead of Z lines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can exert a variable te‌nsion at any given length‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic stimulation pr‌oduces excitatory membran‌e potentials","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sympathetic stimulation produces excitatory membrane potentials","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sympathetic stimulation produces excitatory membrane potentials</strong></p><p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation</strong> (norepinephrine) of a smooth muscle produces<strong> inhibitory potentials and relaxation.</strong></p>\n<p>Parasympathetic stimulation (or acetylcholine) causes depolarization and contraction in the smooth muscle.</p>\n<p>Smooth muscle can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unitary smooth muscle</strong> - has low-resistance gap junctions between individual muscle cells and functions in a syncytial manner</li>\n<li><strong>Multiunit smooth muscle</strong> -  made up of individual units with few or nil gap junctions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Blood vessels have both unitary and multiunit smooth muscle in their walls.</p>\n<p>Option A: Unitary smooth muscle has an<strong> unstable membrane potential</strong> with continuous irregular contractions independent of its nerve supply. This state of partial contraction is called <strong>tonus or tone.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Dense bodies</strong> are present instead of Z lines in smooth muscle. These are bound to actin filaments by α-actinin.</p>\n<p>Option C: Smooth muscle can exert <strong>variable tension at any given length.</strong> This property is called <strong>plasticity</strong>. When a unitary smooth muscle is stretched, it first exerts increased tension. However, if it is held at this length after stretching, the tension gradually decreases. This is referred to as plasticity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1099","text":"Given below is the demonst‌ration of tetanization in‌ the gastrocnemius muscle​ of a frog. Identify the ‍tetanising frequency.","raw_text":"Given below is the demonstration of tetanization in the gastrocnemius muscle of a frog. Identify the tetanising frequency.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25-2‌0 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30-3‌5 Hz","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"15-2‌0 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-1‌5 Hz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30-35 Hz","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c478df751c84f949a6c0ab3d3d93001.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10227ad34b60459897d9c5d3309f4107x1280x2823.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30-35 Hz</strong></p><p>Tetanizing frequency demonstrated by the muscle is 33Hz. In the above graph, we know,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The speed of the paper is 1000mm/sec.</li>\n<li>The y-axis represents millivolts, the x-axis represents time.</li>\n<li>The scale on the x-axis is 10mm.</li>\n<li>The area under the graph represents the muscle contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraction period</strong> (latent period to maximum force of contraction) includes 3 scales = 30 mm. </p>\n<p>As paper speed is 1000mm/sec,</p>\n<p>Contraction period = 30mm = (30/1000) = 30 msec. </p>\n<p><strong>Tetanizing frequency = 1/contraction period</strong> = 1000/30 = <strong>33Hz</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tetanization</p>\n<p>The features of muscle contraction can be demonstrated by eliciting single muscle twitches. The adding together of individual twitch contractions to increase the intensity of overall muscle contraction is <strong>Summation. </strong>Summation occurs in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>by increasing the number of motor units contracting simultaneously, which is <strong>multiple ﬁber summations.</strong></li>\n<li>by increasing the frequency of contraction, which is called<strong><strong> frequency summation and can lead to tetanization.</strong></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the frequency of a contraction reaches a critical level, the successive contractions eventually fuse together, and the whole muscle contraction appears to be completely smooth and continuous, this is called<strong><strong> <strong>tetanization. </strong></strong></strong></p>\n<p><strong><strong><strong>Tetanization </strong></strong></strong>occurs in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complete tetanus </strong>occurs when no relaxation occurs between stimuli.</li>\n<li><strong>Incomplete tetanus </strong>when periods of incomplete relaxation take place between the summated stimuli.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kz6AbVND","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"8_delirium","title":"8.Delirium","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC0273","text":"A 72-year old woman is adm​itted with complaints of ​fever with dysuria for 5 ​days. On examination, she‌ is drowsy and appears wi‌thdrawn and apathetic. Sh‌e claims to see bugs craw​ling on her hand. What is‍ the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 72-year old woman is admitted with complaints of fever with dysuria for 5 days. On examination, she is drowsy and appears withdrawn and apathetic. She claims to see bugs crawling on her hand. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deme​ntia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Formi​cation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deli​rium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Delirium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Delirium</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario of an acute decline in the level of awareness and visual hallucinations (seeing bugs crawling on hand) in an elderly female admitted with fever and dysuria suggests a diagnosis of <strong>delirium</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Delirium is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Acute decline in the level of awareness </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Global impairment of cognitive functions (particularly attention)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It also involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Perceptual disturbances like illusions and hallucinations</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Abnormal psychomotor activity</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sleep cycle impairment</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Formication</strong> is a <strong>tactile</strong> (not visual) hallucination in which the patient experiences a feeling of insects crawling under the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0277","text":"Which of the following fea​tures is more in favour o​f delirium?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is more in favour of delirium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs gradually over a pe​riod of time","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluctuati​ng course","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Preserved c​onsciousness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Commonly associated with a​uditory hallucinations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fluctuating course","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fluctuating course</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Fluctuating course</strong> is suggestive of <strong>delirium.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Characteristic presentation of delirium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Acute onset</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Brief duration</strong> - usually days to weeks</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Decline in the level of consciousness.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Fluctuating course </strong>- unpredictable fluctuations in severity and other clinical manifestations</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Sundowning </strong>- worsening of symptoms at night</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Rapid improvement</strong> - when the underlying cause is treated</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Other characteristics include asterixis, <strong>carphologia</strong> (picking movements on cover sheets and clothes)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">More commonly associated with <strong>visual hallucinations</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0274","text":"Which neurotransmitter abn​ormality is identified mo​st consistently in patien​ts with delirium?","raw_text":"Which neurotransmitter abnormality is identified most consistently in patients with delirium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased​ dopamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased a​cetylcholine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increas​ed GABA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased​ glutamate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased acetylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased acetylcholine</strong></p><p>The most consistent neurotransmitter abnormality seen in delirium is <strong>cholinergic deficiency</strong> (decreased acetylcholine).</p>\n<p>The major neuroanatomical area associated with the condition is <strong>reticular formation. </strong></p>\n<p>Neurotransmitter abnormalities in delirium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased acetylcholine    </li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> dopamine</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> norepinephrine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0279","text":"A patient admitted to the ​ICU for severe pneumonia ​5 days ago suddenly devel​oped difficulty in recogn‌izing his relatives or th‌e doctor and has started ‌muttering that he is trap​ped in jail. On evaluatio‍n, he is in an altered se‍nsorium. What is the most​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient admitted to the ICU for severe pneumonia 5 days ago suddenly developed difficulty in recognizing his relatives or the doctor and has started muttering that he is trapped in jail. On evaluation, he is in an altered sensorium. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute d​ementia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brief psycho​tic disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ICU ps​ychosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute p​aranoia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ICU psychosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ICU psychosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>ICU psychosis</strong> (delirium).</p>\n<p>The following features are consistent with the diagnosis of delirium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong> onset of symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>Disorientation to place</strong> (jail instead of the hospital)</li>\n<li><strong>Disorientation to</strong> <strong>person</strong> (failure to recognize relatives and the doctor)</li>\n<li><strong>Altered sensorium</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Brief psychotic disorder</strong> (BPD) according to <strong>DSM-5</strong> is the sudden onset of psychotic behaviour that lasts <strong>less than 1 month</strong> followed by complete remission with possible future relapses. This is termed <strong>acute and transient psychotic disorder</strong> as per <strong>ICD-11</strong>, and the duration of the episode should <strong>not exceed 3 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0278","text":"You are evaluating an elde​rly man who is suspected ​to have hypoactive deliri​um. Which of the followin‌g etiologies is most comm‌only associated with his ‌condition?","raw_text":"You are evaluating an elderly man who is suspected to have hypoactive delirium. Which of the following etiologies is most commonly associated with his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anticholinergic drug overd​ose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heavy meta​l poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic enc​ephalopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alcohol w​ithdrawal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatic encephalopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatic encephalopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoactive delirium</strong> is commonly associated with <strong>hepatic</strong> and <strong>renal encephalopathies</strong>.</p>\n<p>The hypoactive clinical subtype of delirium can often be <strong>confused with depression</strong> due to overlapping symptoms of psychomotor retardation and affect.</p>\n<p>In <strong>delirium,</strong> however, the onset of such symptoms is more <strong>acute</strong> and is usually associated with some <strong>medical illness</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6852","text":"Among patients who develop​ acute psychiatric sympto​ms in the hospital, which​ of the following screeni‌ng tools is useful for id‌entifying cases of deliri‌um?","raw_text":"Among patients who develop acute psychiatric symptoms in the hospital, which of the following screening tools is useful for identifying cases of delirium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CAGE ques​tionnaire","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CHAT ch​ecklist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CAM al​gorithm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GAD-7​ scale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CAM algorithm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CAM algorithm</strong></p><p><strong>Confusion assessment method</strong> <strong>(CAM)</strong> is a bedside screening test used in the diagnosis of <strong>delirium.</strong></p>\n<p>It has two parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Part one - screens for <strong>overall cognitive impairment</strong>.</li>\n<li>Part two - a diagnostic algorithm that screens for <strong>4 cardinal features </strong>to distinguish delirium from other types of cognitive impairment. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>It can be assessed in less than 5 minutes.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 153px; width: 358px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 348.015625px; text-align: center; height: 34px;\"><strong>CAM Diagnostic Algorithm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39.78125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122.203125px; height: 39.78125px;\">Feature 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.8125px; height: 39.78125px;\">Acute onset and fluctuating course</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122.203125px; height: 34px;\">Feature 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.8125px; height: 34px;\">Inattention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122.203125px; height: 34px;\">Feature 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.8125px; height: 34px;\">Disorganized thinking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122.203125px; height: 34px;\">Feature 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.8125px; height: 34px;\">Altered level of consciousness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Diagnosis of <strong>delirium</strong> requires the presence of features 1 and 2 along with either feature 3 or 4. i.e., <strong>features</strong> <strong>1 + 2 + (3 or 4)</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>CAGE</strong> questionnaire is used for <strong>addiction</strong> and <strong>substance abuse</strong> cases</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>CHAT</strong> checklist is used in autism (<strong>CH</strong>ecklist for <strong>A</strong>utism in <strong>T</strong>oddlers)</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>GAD-7</strong> scale is used in the assessment of <strong>g</strong>eneralized <strong>a</strong>nxiety <strong>d</strong>isorder</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0280","text":"An elderly man was brought​ in to get evaluated for ​suspected depressive diso​rder. He has been sociall‌y withdrawn, confused, an‌d has not been speaking t‌o his family for the past​ 1 week. The family also ‍complains that he has not‍ been sleeping well at ni​ght. Which of the followi​ng EEG findings would mak‍e you suspect delirium?","raw_text":"An elderly man was brought in to get evaluated for suspected depressive disorder. He has been socially withdrawn, confused, and has not been speaking to his family for the past 1 week. The family also complains that he has not been sleeping well at night. Which of the following EEG findings would make you suspect delirium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cha​otic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3Hz Spike​ and wave","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse ​slow wave","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Isoel​ectric","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse slow wave","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse slow wave</strong></p><p>The EEG finding in most patients with delirium is <strong>diffuse slow-wave</strong> or <strong>low-voltage</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>EEG findings in specific causes of delirium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Focal impairment - </strong>localized cerebral event</li>\n<li><strong>Low voltage but fast-wave activity - </strong>drug/alcohol withdrawal </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Chaotic (hypsarrhythmia) – Infantile spasm, West syndrome</p>\n<p>Option B: 3Hz spike and wave – Absence seizures</p>\n<p>Option D: Isoelectric EEG – Deep coma / cerebral death</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0283","text":"When pharmacotherapy is ne​cessary for managing deli​rium, which of the follow​ing is preferred as the f‌irst-line treatment?","raw_text":"When pharmacotherapy is necessary for managing delirium, which of the following is preferred as the first-line treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lorazepa​m 1 mg PO","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lorazepam​ 0.5 mg IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clozapine​ 12.5mg PO","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haloperido​l 0.5 mg PO","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Haloperidol 0.5 mg PO","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Haloperidol 0.5 mg PO</strong></p><p><strong>Haloperidol</strong> is commonly used for the first-line pharmacological treatment of <strong>delirium</strong>.</p>\n<p>The two major symptoms of delirium that may require pharmacological treatment are <strong>psychosis </strong>and<strong> insomnia.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A, B: <strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> are often used as an adjunct in agitated patients, but should be used judiciously for insomnia as their sedative property <strong>can worsen</strong> <strong>confusion</strong> in delirium. </p>\n<p>Option C: The use of <strong>second-generation antipsychotics</strong> may be considered for delirium management, but clinical trial experience with these agents for delirium is limited. <strong>Clozapine</strong> itself is known to sometimes <strong>trigger delirium.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: IV regimens of haloperidol are mostly used in the ICU in the critically ill due to their fast onset of action and predictable pharmacokinetics. Oral haloperidol may be used as a maintenance regimen in patients with a functional GI tract. Intramuscular haloperidol is considered only in acutely combative patients without IV access.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0284","text":"A 75-year old patient with​ Parkinson's disease, tak​ing levodopa for the past​ 2 years, presents to you‌ with disorientation, con‌fusion, and altered senso‌rium. What is your next s​tep in management?","raw_text":"A 75-year old patient with Parkinson's disease, taking levodopa for the past 2 years, presents to you with disorientation, confusion, and altered sensorium. What is your next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduce the dose of levodop​a","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IV Lor​azepam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral Qu​etiapine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral Cl​ozapine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reduce the dose of levodopa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduce the dose of levodopa</strong></p><p>The next step in management should be a <strong>reduction in the dose of levodopa.</strong></p>\n<p>The patient presents with disorientation, confusion and altered sensorium. This is suggestive of delirium. <strong>Anti-parkinsonian agents</strong> are frequently implicated in causing delirium because of <strong>dopamine increase.</strong></p>\n<p>Decreasing the dosage of the antiparkinsonian agent has to be weighed against worsening of motor symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Clozapine</strong> is recommended when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The antiparkinsonian agents cannot be further reduced</li>\n<li>Delirium persists even after reducing the dose of antiparkinsonian agents</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a patient is not able to tolerate clozapine, alternative antipsychotic agents should be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fLa2_eVs","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"8_brain_tumors__neurodegenerative___demyelinating_disorders","title":"8.Brain Tumors, Neurodegenerative & Demyelinating Disorders","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA3831","text":"Physiological calcificatio​n is seen in all of the s​tructures except:","raw_text":"Physiological calcification is seen in all of the structures except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pineal​ gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Habenular ​commissure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Du​ra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Red n​ucleus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Red nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40ca05b5eef24be09f01d4fc8ca24dcfx361x422.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/723059039f85410090408a1593cf49d8x630x630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Red nucleus</strong></p><p>Physiologic calcification is <strong>not</strong> seen in the <strong>red nucleus.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows calcification in the pineal gland:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows heavily calcified falx:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3833","text":"Basal ganglia calcificatio​n can be seen on imaging ​of patients with all of t​he following conditions, ‌except:","raw_text":"Basal ganglia calcification can be seen on imaging of patients with all of the following conditions, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoparat​hyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothy​roidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemochr​omatosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon monox​ide poisoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemochromatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f40aaf8734d1418cb3286374ff30dc88x962x797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemochromatosis</strong></p><p>Basal ganglia calcification is <strong>not</strong> a feature of <strong>hemochromatosis</strong>. The causes of pathological basal ganglia calcifications are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endocrinopathies: <strong>Hypoparathyroidism</strong> (primary or secondary), pseudohypoparathyroidism, hyperparathyroidism,<strong> hypothyroidism</strong></li>\n<li>Vasculitis (e.g. SLE)</li>\n<li>Mitochondrial disorders (e.g. Mitochondrial myopathy)</li>\n<li>Infections: Brucellosis, TORCH, Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), HIV</li>\n<li>Other inherited disorders: Neuroferritinonpathy, Cockayne syndrome</li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n<li>Chemotherapy</li>\n<li><strong>Carbon monoxide poisoning</strong></li>\n<li>Lead poisoning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image is a CT brain showing bilateral basal ganglia calcification:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3835","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions are​ likely to have calcified​ lesions on CT head? Cran‌iopharyngioma Ependymoma ​Chordoma Glioblastoma mul​tiforme Oligodendroglioma‍","raw_text":"<p>Patients with which of the following conditions are likely to have calcified lesions on CT head?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Craniopharyngioma</li>\n<li>Ependymoma</li>\n<li>Chordoma</li>\n<li>Glioblastoma multiforme</li>\n<li>Oligodendroglioma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 4 an​d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 an​d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 and ​5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 3 a​nd 5 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 and 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>Craniopharyngiomas, ependymomas, oligodendrogliomas, and chordomas </strong>are associated with calcification.</p>\n<p>Commonly <strong>calcified</strong> lesions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oligodendrogliomas (90%)</li>\n<li>Choroid plexus tumours</li>\n<li>Ependymoma</li>\n<li>Central neurocytoma</li>\n<li>Meningioma</li>\n<li>Craniopharyngioma</li>\n<li>Teratoma</li>\n<li>Chordoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Commonly <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> lesions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glioblastoma multiforme</strong> (grade 4 glioma)</li>\n<li>Oligodendroglioma</li>\n<li>Metastases: Melanoma, lung, and breast</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0813","text":"A cystic lesion in the sup​rasellar region with calc​ification is seen on the ​MRI. What is the most lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A cystic lesion in the suprasellar region with calcification is seen on the MRI. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cranioph​aryngioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pituitar​y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligoden​droglioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Craniopharyngioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3435b48ce6744545aae5422104050d11.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a17b9e4467284bb8a3efee2e18528056x510x537.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4abf9b8d94c457e82190e2a7255cab4x583x600.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51f4b68de6164ee0b0f99b8c23a498adx720x753.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Craniopharyngioma</strong></p><p>A cystic lesion in the suprasellar region with calcification is suggestive of <strong>craniopharyngioma.</strong> It is derived from <strong>Rathke’s pouch</strong> and arises near the pituitary stalk, commonly extending into the suprasellar cistern.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pituitary adenomas</strong> are seen in the sellar region. Microadenomas are best shown on contrast-enhanced images. Macroadenomas are seen as <strong>snowman or figure of eight</strong> appearance. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Meningioma</strong> is a supratentorial tumor, mainly in the parasagittal region. A classical dural tail may be seen in intracranial meningiomas but is not pathognomic. The MRI image below shows a <strong>well-defined</strong>, <strong>hyper-intense</strong>, and <strong>homogeneously enhanced mass </strong>close to the midline suggestive of meningioma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Oligodendroglioma</strong> is exclusively found in the cerebral hemispheres, most commonly in the frontal lobes. Upto 90% of oligodendrogliomas contain visible calcification on CT. The CT image below shows a calcified cortical mass that suggests oligodendroglioma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3838","text":"Bracket calcification on s​kull X-Ray is seen in whi​ch of the following condi​tions?","raw_text":"Bracket calcification on skull X-Ray is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sturge Web​er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipoma of co​rpus callosum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lipoma of corpus callosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4506c279ae024b31a0421a3ca26ff661x510x699.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lipoma of corpus callosum</strong></p><p><strong>Lipoma of the corpus callosum</strong> shows bracket calcification<strong> </strong>on skull X-ray.</p>\n<p><strong>Intracranial lipomas</strong> are most commonly located in the<strong> midline</strong> and are commonly associated with congenital malformations such as agenesis/dysgenesis of the corpus callosum. Pericallosal lipomas are classified into anteriorly located bulky <strong>tubulo-nodular</strong> type and posteriorly located ribbon-like <strong>curvilinear</strong> type. Imaging is the mainstay in the diagnosis of intracranial lipoma. </p>\n<p>The image below shows bracket calcification:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tuberous sclerosis</strong> shows<strong> </strong>calcification of subependymal hamartomas in the wall of the ventricles.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Sturge Weber syndrome</strong> shows<strong> </strong>tram track calcification.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Meningioma</strong> on X-ray shows calcification hyperostosis and increased vascular markings, it is usually seen in the falx.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4255","text":"What is the probable diagn​osis based on the image o​f the scan given below?","raw_text":"What is the probable diagnosis based on the image of the scan given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pituitary​ apoplexy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pituitary m​acroadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rathke c​left cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cranioph​aryngioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Craniopharyngioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5673403979a1498eb5bf914d620bae9d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43378e03896145b3b7c0c2a9079863f5x510x537.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Craniopharyngioma</strong></p><p>The given MRI of the brain demonstrates a <strong>craniopharyngioma, </strong>which is a suprasellar tumour that arises from <strong>remnants</strong> <strong>of Rathke's pouch. </strong></p>\n<p>The two variants of craniopharyngioma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adamantinomatous type </strong>(common in children):  Predominantly solid with cystic components. Diagnostic features include compact lamellar keratin (\"<strong>wet keratin</strong>\") and areas of dystrophic <strong>calcification</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Papillary type </strong>(common in adults)<strong>: </strong>Contains both solid sheets and papillae lined by well-differentiated non-keratinized squamous epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These tumours are usually large and heterogeneous, displaying <strong>solid and cystic</strong> components. The cysts contain a cholesterol-rich, thick brownish-yellow fluid that is compared to '<strong>machine oi</strong>l'.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>headaches</strong> (most common), visual abnormalities - decreased acuity and <strong>bitemporal hemianopia, </strong><strong>GH deficiency, </strong>growth failure, delayed sexual maturation due to pituitary hypofunction, and polyuria (central diabetes insipidus).</p>\n<p>Radiologically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CT may show <strong>calcifications</strong> associated with the solid and cystic wall components</li>\n<li>MRI is the imaging modality of choice which shows:  \n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\"><strong>Cysts</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>Iso to hyperintense to the brain (due to high protein content \"machinery oil cysts\")</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td>Mostly hyperintense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\"><strong>Solid component</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td> Vivid enhancement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td> Variable or mixed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Craniopharyngiomas tend not to expand the pituitary fossa unless they become huge, which is a differentiating feature from pituitary macroadenomas.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Pituitary apoplexy </strong>is an acute clinical condition associated with the rapid expansion of the pituitary gland, which is evident on an MRI as enhancement around a non-enhancing infarcted centre.<br/><br/>Option B: <strong>Pituitary macroadenoma</strong> (with cystic degeneration or necrosis) shows indentation at the diaphragma sellae, giving rise to a <strong>snowman/figure of eight appearance/cottage loaf</strong>. Calcification is often absent. The image below shows the snowman appearance:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Rathke cleft cyst</strong> shows no solid or enhancing component. Calcification is rare. It is unilocular and the majority are completely or mostly intrasellar.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3850","text":"A 7-year-old boy is brough​t to the pediatric OPD by​ his parents with a histo​ry of headache, dizziness‌, and unsteady gait. Base​d on the provided scan im​age, what is the most lik‍ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric OPD by his parents with a history of headache, dizziness, and unsteady gait. Based on the provided scan image, what is the most likely diagnosis?  ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro​cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cranioph​aryngioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medulloblastoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5cb162a82514493b9a20cf82f2c1979.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medulloblastoma</strong></p><p>The given MRI is suggestive of<strong> medulloblastomas</strong>. It is the <strong>most common malignant brain tumor</strong> of childhood, and it occurs exclusively in the <strong>cerebellum</strong>. Irrespective of their histological or genetic characterization, medulloblastomas correspond histologically to <strong>WHO grade IV tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>Radiological findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CT &amp; MRI</strong>: CT shows a <strong>hyperdense midline vermian mass</strong> adjoining the <strong>roof </strong>of the 4th<strong> ventricle</strong> and causing <strong>hydrocephalus</strong>. MRI shows T2 <strong>hypointensity</strong> or isointensity compared to the grey matter. The CT and MRI findings also reflect the increased nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio and densely packed cells of the tumor. </li>\n<li><strong>DWI</strong>: restricted diffusion</li>\n<li><strong>MR spectroscopy</strong>: reduced <em>N-</em>acetyl aspartate(NAA) peak with an increased choline-to-creatine ratio.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CT and MRI findings, supported by the presence of restricted diffusion on DWI, are the most reliable observations in differentiating medulloblastoma from ependymoma or other posterior fossa tumors.</p>\n<p>Standard treatment for medulloblastomas includes <strong>surgical resection</strong>, with adjuvant <strong>craniospinal radiotherapy</strong> for those over 3 years of age and <strong>chemotherapy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pilocytic astrocytoma</strong> is a brain tumor that occurs more often in children and young adults. These tumors are usually slow-growing and benign. The majority of neoplasms are located in the <strong>cerebellum.</strong> However, they can also appear around the third ventricle, optic nerves, spinal cord, and occasionally in the cerebral hemispheres. <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>Mural nodule</strong>, a contrast medium enhancing nodule within the wall of a <strong>cystic mass</strong>, is seen on MRI. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Option C: <strong>Ependymomas</strong> are tumours that arise from the ependyma-lined ventricular system, typically on the floor of the fourth ventricle. In adults, the spinal cord is their most common location; tumors in this site are particularly frequent in the setting of neurofibromatosis type 2. They are <strong>well-demarcated lobulated mass</strong> lesions that show <strong>calcifications</strong> on CT in over 50% and are of mixed signal intensity on MRI. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Option D:<strong> </strong></span><strong>Craniopharyngiomas</strong> are tumors that arise from the remnants of <strong>Rathke’s pouch</strong>. It is an intracranial space-occupying lesion. It has a bimodal age of presentation. These tumors are usually large and heterogeneous, displaying both <strong>solid </strong>and<strong> cystic components</strong>, and occur within the<strong> suprasellar area</strong>. The cysts contain a cholesterol-rich, thick brownish-yellow fluid and are compared to <strong>machine oil</strong>. They also show radiologically demonstrable calcification.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3854","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen​ted with recurrent headac​hes. MRI shows the follow​ing finding. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presented with recurrent headaches. MRI shows the following finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro​cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitar​y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acoustic ​Schwannoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Meningioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78579fdc62674d80bbb5096d3b7303f5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07aaa6f911994695808120d23be21a39x548x820.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/657356a6b9fa4550913ed44997e14490x410x478.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Meningioma</strong></p><p>The given axial T1 MRI is suggestive of <strong>meningioma</strong>. It involves the mid-falx cerebri and is seen as a spontaneously hyperintense and homogeneously enhanced mass in the <strong>midline</strong> arising from <strong>falx</strong>.</p>\n<p>Meningiomas are <strong>extra-axial tumours</strong> (lesions that are outside of the brain parenchyma). They are a non-glial neoplasm that originates from the meningocytes or <strong>arachnoid cap cells </strong>of the meninges and are located anywhere that meninges are found.</p>\n<p>Meningioma shows <strong>female </strong>predominance that may be related to the fact that some meningiomas contain estrogen and progesterone receptors. They are also associated with <strong>NF-2 gene mutation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Typical meningiomas appear as <strong>dural-based masses</strong> isointense to grey matter on both T1 and T2 weighted imaging and demonstrate <strong>vivid contrast enhancement</strong> on both MRI and CT. <strong>CSF vascular cleft sign</strong> indicates that the mass is extra-axial. There is a cleft between the tumour and normal brain parenchyma.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the CSF vascular cleft sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Dural tail sign</strong>: Refers to the linear extension of enhancement of the dura, beyond the segment on which the tumor is based. The image is an axial T1W spin-echo MRI of the brain with contrast enhancement showing dural tail (arrow) in meningioma (arrowhead).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3855","text":"Which of the following fin​dings would you not see o​n the skull X-ray of a pa​tient with meningioma?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings would you not see on the skull X-ray of a patient with meningioma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcif​ication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypero​stosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased vascular marking​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ero​sion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Erosion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4d9a5ef831a44bcb5de337c22a1a770x1280x1189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Erosion</strong></p><p>Erosion is not an X-ray feature of meningioma.</p>\n<p>. Skull X-ray findings in meningioma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Local hyperostosis</strong> - Thickening of skull bone </li>\n<li><strong>Prominent frontal vascular markings - </strong>due to hypervascularity of the tumor</li>\n<li>Calcification</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The X-ray given below shows a calcified meningioma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3857","text":"A 65-year-old man has been​ experiencing worsening h​eadaches and new-onset se​izures. MRI with contrast‌ was done and is given be​low. What is the most lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man has been experiencing worsening headaches and new-onset seizures. MRI with contrast was done and is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gliobl​astoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acoustic ​schwannoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glioblastoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fdf5080b431b4fcd9a00d86321fb00b3.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1680d656cc442aca0520e5601453502x510x600.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glioblastoma</strong></p><p>The given MRI finding is suggestive of<strong> glioblastoma</strong>. Post-contrast MRI in the case of glioblastoma multiforme, demonstrates a large heterogeneous mass that crosses the <strong>corpus callosum</strong> into both frontal lobes. </p>\n<p>They have a <strong>preferential spreading</strong> along the condensed white matter tracts such as <strong>corticospinal tracts </strong>and<strong> corpus callosum </strong>and can give rise to the so-called <strong>butterfly glioma, </strong>to involve the contralateral hemisphere. The term butterfly refers to the symmetric wing-like extensions across the midline. </p>\n<p>There is also evidence of a previous left-sided craniotomy and biopsy tract down to the lesion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Glioblastoma is the <strong>second</strong> <strong>most common</strong> adult primary intracranial neoplasm. It carries the <strong>worst prognosis</strong> (WHO grade IV).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8648","text":"A 7-year-old girl presents​ with progressive ataxia ​and symptoms of increased​ intracranial tension, in‌cluding headaches and vom​iting. MR spectroscopy is​ performed, as shown belo‍w. Based on the findings,​ what is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl presents with progressive ataxia and symptoms of increased intracranial tension, including headaches and vomiting. MR spectroscopy is performed, as shown below. Based on the findings, what is the most likely diagnosis? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gli​oma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuber​culoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Canavan​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cdb9f14a86b64529a3d8ab9e66e185bc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa6ac5d46661431684803b25e0ba8e99x1280x1223.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glioma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the<strong> magnetic resonance (MR) spectroscopy</strong> showing a <strong>choline peak </strong>is diagnostic of <strong>glioma.</strong> <strong>Creatinine</strong> levels are <strong>decreased</strong> on MR spectroscopy in patients with glioma.</p>\n<p><strong>MR spectroscopy</strong> is a non-invasive means of obtaining <strong>metabolic information</strong>. The image below shows a normal MR spectroscopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tuberculoma </strong>shows a<strong> lipid peak </strong>in MR spectroscopy. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option C: In <strong>Canavan disease, </strong>there is a marked<strong> increase</strong> in <strong>N-acetyl aspartate</strong><strong>.</strong> Its concentration <strong>decreases</strong> with insults to the brain such as <strong>neoplasm</strong>, <strong>infarct</strong>, or <strong>abscess.</strong> <strong>N-acetyl aspartate </strong>(NAA) which is the most prominent resonance in a proton spectrum, is accepted as a normal neuronal functional marker.</p>\n<p>Option D: Increased <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">choline</strong> reflects increased membrane synthesis and/or an increased number of cells, as seen in <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">lymphoma</strong>. However, lymphomas of the nervous system are very rare and seen in immunodeficient states like HIV.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3858","text":"A patient is found to have​ a cerebellopontine angle​ mass. The differential d​iagnosis can include all ‌except:","raw_text":"A patient is found to have a cerebellopontine angle mass. The differential diagnosis can include all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acoustic ​schwannoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuber​culoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epiderm​oid cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tuberculoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e261d73e0de7499fb793a205c0b6b68ax252x294.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tuberculoma</strong></p><p><strong>Tuberculoma</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a cause of cerebellopontine (CP) angle mass. The most common <strong>cerebellopontine </strong>(CP)<strong> tumour </strong>is <strong>acoustic schwannoma </strong>(90%). Other tumours include meningiomas and epidermoid cysts.</p>\n<p>CP angle tumours (Mnemonic- <strong>SAME </strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>: Schwannoma (8<sup>th </sup>nerve &gt; 5<sup>th</sup>)</li>\n<li><strong>A:</strong> Aneurysm, arachnoid cyst</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>: Meningioma, metastases</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>: Epidermoid, ependymoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows bilateral CP angle masses (acoustic schwannomas):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3859","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen​ted with hearing loss and​ tinnitus. MRI was done a​nd showed the following f‌inding. What is the most ​probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presented with hearing loss and tinnitus. MRI was done and showed the following finding. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otoscl​erosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meniere’​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acoustic ​schwannoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gliobl​astoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acoustic schwannoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c3276ba70e045a3bd2840d0c7916698.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae403e0b138b421195d32096644f82d9x252x294.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acoustic schwannoma</strong></p><p>Based on the above clinical findings and the MRI, which reveals a <strong>cerebellopontine angle mass, </strong>the diagnosis is <strong>acoustic schwannoma</strong>(vestibular schwannoma). The extension of the mass into the intra-canalicular portion of the internal acoustic meatus is suggestive of acoustic neuroma. </p>\n<p>This tumour is the most common mass in the cerebellopontine(CP) angle. Its extra-canalicular extension into the CP angle may create the \"<strong>ice cream cone</strong>\" appearance. Additionally, the presence of bilateral acoustic schwannoma is highly suggestive of <strong>neurofibromatosis type 2</strong> (NF2). <strong>MRI</strong> is the imaging modality of choice in assessing the possible vestibular schwannoma. </p>\n<p>The given image is a post-contrast T1 axial imaging demonstrating the presence of bilateral acoustic schwannomas.<br/> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3860","text":"Snowman or figure of eight​ appearance as shown in t​his MRI is seen in which ​of the following conditio‌ns?","raw_text":"Snowman or figure of eight appearance as shown in this MRI is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cranioph​aryngioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibular​ Schwanomma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pituitar​y adenoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pituitary adenoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc52e0a582ee40f299978f40dc76cce2.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd259196f27a40eda09358c2f5164bd6x510x537.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pituitary adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Snowman appearance</strong> is seen in<strong> pituitary adenoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The image is a sagittal post-contrast MRI in a case of pituitary macroadenoma (&gt;1 cm). Indentation at the diaphragma sellae gives rise to <strong>snowman/figure of eight </strong>appearance<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pituitary adenoma is the most common tumor of the sellar region. Depending on their size they are broadly classified into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pituitary microadenoma: <strong>&lt; 1 cm</strong> in size</li>\n<li>Pituitary macroadenoma: <strong>&gt; 1 cm</strong> in size and usually extending superiorly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features include bi-temporal hemianopia, headache, and vomiting. Skull X-ray finding is ballooning of the sella.<strong> MRI</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3862","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is the 'salt a​nd pepper' sign observed?​","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is the 'salt and pepper' sign observed? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schwa​nnoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraga​nglioma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paraganglioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7442d36d62794a7ca5dc1be6c8f2c8dex1280x1334.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paraganglioma</strong></p><p>The <strong>salt and pepper appearance </strong>is a highly sensitive and specific sign for head and neck <strong>paragangliomas</strong>. The \"salt and pepper\" appearance refers to the areas of subacute haemorrhage ('<strong>salt</strong>') and high-velocity flow voids('<strong>pepper</strong>') seen in T1 weighted MRI of large head and neck paragangliomas. </p>\n<p>The given MRI depicts a paraganglioma demonstrating <strong>salt and pepper sign:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3865","text":"Myelography of a patient s​howed expansion of the co​ntrast-filled space. This​ finding is seen in which‌ of the following conditi​ons?","raw_text":"Myelography of a patient showed expansion of the contrast-filled space. This finding is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intramedul​lary tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intradural extramedullary ​tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal d​ysraphism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extradur​al tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intradural extramedullary tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77c95a2e86844d199fda96f8386ebea4x320x320.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5081245dd063446baae686640a3dc38fx718x698.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intradural extramedullary tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Expansion</strong> of the <strong>contrast-filled space</strong> in <strong>myelography</strong> is seen in <strong>intradural extramedullary tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>The contrast in myelography is injected into the CSF space, and if the tumor is present in the intradural extramedullary space, it leads to the <strong>widening of the CSF space on that side</strong>. Examples of such lesions include <strong>neurofibroma</strong> and <strong>meningioma</strong>. These lesions will also show a <strong>meniscus-</strong>like appearance on myelography with the contrast column.</p>\n<p>The MRI image below shows a meningioma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below is an MRI image demonstrating the hyperintense mass seen in intramedullary tumors. Intramedullary lesions will lead to a '<strong>trouser leg'</strong> appearance on myelography due to cord expansion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3867","text":"A 50 -year-old woman prese​nts with a gradual loss o​f visual acuity in the ri​ght eye. A CT head post-c‌ontrast reveals thickenin​g of the optic nerve-shea​th complex with a tram-tr‍ack appearance. What is t​he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 50 -year-old woman presents with a gradual loss of visual acuity in the right eye. A CT head post-contrast reveals thickening of the optic nerve-sheath complex with a tram-track appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic nerve sheath meningi​oma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Optic ner​ve glioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optic n​euritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Optic nerve sheath meningioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a52a046756f4637a2e00c712932d90ax254x200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Optic nerve sheath meningioma</strong></p><p>Based on the given clinical scenario and radiologic finding, the most likely diagnosis is <strong>optic nerve sheath meningioma, </strong>a benign tumour arising from the arachnoid cap cells of the optic nerve sheath. It is most frequently seen in middle-aged women, with bilateral cases linked to neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF-2). Post-contrast CT or MRI show <strong>tram-track sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>The post-contrast T1 weighted axial MRI image below shows a <strong>hypoattenuating</strong> optic nerve surrounded by the <strong>peripheral enhancement </strong>of the tumour(tram-track sign):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Optic nerve glioma</strong> is intrinsic to optic nerve, commonly seen in children, often associated with neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF-1). It presents as <strong>fusiform enlargement </strong>of the optic nerve on imaging. The tram-track sign is useful in differentiating between optic nerve sheath meningioma and optic nerve gliomas.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3874","text":"A 50-year-old male present​ed with cognitive deficit​, involuntary hand and fi​nger movements, and muscl‌e incoordination. The MRI​ image is given below. Wh​at is the probable diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male presented with cognitive deficit, involuntary hand and finger movements, and muscle incoordination. The MRI image is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Huntington​’s disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wilson’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Creutzfeldt-​Jakob disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parkinson​'s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Huntington’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7dbe12f6f6de4fc29deadedd337c8862.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dcb78ad33f3546f1b3229614d1db1855x1280x1575.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Huntington’s disease</strong></p><p>Based on the above clinical scenario and coronal T1-weighted MRI findings of <strong>bilateral caudate nuclei atrophy</strong> with <strong>compensatory lateral ventricle dilatation</strong>(\"boxcar ventricles\"), the likely diagnosis is <strong>Huntington's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>This autosomal-dominant neurodegenerative disorder, caused by a <strong>CAG trinucleotide repeat</strong> expansion in the huntingtin (<em>HTT)</em> gene on chromosome 4p, typically affects young adults and is characterised by choreiform movements, psychiatric disturbances, and progressive dementia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3878","text":"A 25-year-old man presente​d with personality change​s, abnormal movements, ja​undice and pruritus. MRI ‌of the brain was taken an​d is given below. What is​ the probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presented with personality changes, abnormal movements, jaundice and pruritus. MRI of the brain was taken and is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Huntington​’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemochr​omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco​idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wilson’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef10d9abe4dc4b21a28b13caa78a8fe5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a17526dcc0b4790916c3abd49e97c37x1051x1219.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wilson’s disease</strong></p><p>The image given is an axial FLAIR MRI of a patient with <strong>Wilson’s disease</strong> having the face of the <strong>giant panda appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Wilson’s disease is a rare, <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder characterized by <strong>excessive deposition</strong> of free <strong>copper</strong> in tissues. It is due to a <strong>loss of function </strong>mutation of the <strong>ATP7B gene.</strong> It is also known as hepatolenticular degenerative disease.</p>\n<p>MRI brain in advanced disease with neurological manifestations can show the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Giant panda face sign</strong>: Hypointense superior colliculus (chin and mouth of panda), the hyperintense area around the tegmentum of the midbrain (eyes of panda), and normal substantia nigra (ears of panda).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bright claustrum sign</strong>: Thin rim of a hyperintense area in the claustrum with normal putamen.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Mercedes-Benz/trident sign</strong>: Hyperintense pons trisected by a hypointense line.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3880","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen​ted with tingling and num​bness of upper and lower ​limb extremities. The MRI‌ image is shown below. Wh​at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented with tingling and numbness of upper and lower limb extremities. The MRI image is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple ​sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple sy​stem atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive multifocal leu​koencephalopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multiple sclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e6bff90f08342b1a191fc408889e002.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d16ad02746264584881a8bb4f17462bcx1280x1211.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multiple sclerosis</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the symptoms of tingling and numbness of both limbs, along with the brain MRI showing ovoid <strong>hyperintense demyelinating plaques</strong> with their long axis perpendicular to the ventricular and callosal surface known as <strong>Dawson fingers</strong>,<strong> </strong>characteristic of <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong> <strong>(MS)</strong>. </p>\n<p>MS is an autoimmune disease that primarily affects the <strong>white matter</strong> of the central nervous system, causing inflammation, <strong>demyelination</strong>, axonal damage and <strong>neurodegeneration</strong>. It predominantly affects women aged 20-40 years and typically follows a <strong>relapsing-remitting</strong> course, although progressive forms can also occur. The clinical presentation varies depending on the affected location, with common symptoms including paresthesia, visual disturbances (such as <strong>optic neuritis</strong>), motor weakness and ataxia. Dawson fingers are best visualized on <strong>FLAIR</strong> imaging and represent demyelination and oligodendrocyte loss, accompanied by astroglial scarring.</p>\n<p>The following MRI image depicts Dawson fingers arranged at right angles along medullary veins (callososeptal location).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3882","text":"The hot cross bun sign on ​MRI would be seen in pati​ents with which of the fo​llowing conditions?","raw_text":"The hot cross bun sign on MRI would be seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple system atrophy ty​pe C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple system atrophy ty​pe P","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Progressive supranuclear p​alsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bovine spongiform encephal​opathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Multiple system atrophy type C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b971535127c48a58d2cbe97e080d07dx720x639.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f19cb58afcc243c6ae9370aabf8845eex510x506.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multiple system atrophy type C</strong></p><p>The <strong>hot cross bun sign</strong> on MRI, characterised by <strong>cruciform pontine hyperintensity</strong> due to selective neuronal loss of the transverse pontocerebellar fibres, is most commonly observed in patients with <strong>multiple system atrophy-cerebellar type</strong> (MSA-C).</p>\n<p>Multiple system atrophy (a type of synucleinopathies) is a <strong>neurodegenerative disorder</strong> with varying degrees of involvement of the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> and the <strong>olivopontocerebellar complex. Autonomic dysfunction</strong> is more severe and generalised, often presenting as orthostatic hypotension. MSA has been divided clinically into two forms according to the dominant non-autonomic symptoms: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MSA-C</strong>: Predominance of <strong>cerebellar symptoms</strong> (olivopontocerebellar atrophy)</li>\n<li><strong>MSA-P</strong>: Predominance of <strong>Parkinsonian signs and symptoms</strong> (striatonigral degeneration)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is a valuable diagnostic adjunct, often revealing cerebellar and brainstem atrophy causing pontine hot cross bun sign. </p>\n<p>The axial T2-weighted image at the level of middle cerebellar peduncles below shows the hot cross bun sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Multiple system atrophy-parkinsonian type</strong> (MSA-P) is characterised by <strong>atypical</strong> parkinsonism that is symmetrical and predominantly axial, with tremors less prominent compared to Parkinson's disease. MRI typically reveals <strong>hyperintensities</strong> along the lateral aspect of the <strong>putamina</strong>, and T2-weighted imaging may demonstrate hypointensity in the striatum due to iron deposition. Although not exclusive to MSA, the hot cross bun sign is more commonly seen in MSA-C than in MSA-P.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Progressive supranuclear palsy</strong> (PSP) is a form of atypical parkinsonism characterized by slow ocular saccades, eyelid apraxia, and restricted vertical eye movements with downward gaze palsy. Patients also have hyperextension of the neck with gait disturbances and frequent falls. MRI may reveal characteristic atrophy of the midbrain with normal pons, which is called a <strong>hummingbird sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3885","text":"Owl’s eye sign on T2 MRI i​s seen in which of the fo​llowing conditions?","raw_text":"Owl’s eye sign on T2 MRI is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculous​ meningitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple system atrophy ty​pe C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Progressive supranuclear p​alsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central pontine myelinolys​is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Central pontine myelinolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ee23f5f8f7a464bb34b75eb7200a24cx510x1763.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Central pontine myelinolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Owl's eye sign</strong> is one of the characteristic findings on T2 MRI in <strong>central pontine myelinolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Central pontine myelinolysis, also known as <strong>osmotic demyelination syndrome</strong>, is a <strong>non-inflammatory</strong> <strong>demyelinating</strong> disorder of the white matter tracts, commonly involving the pons. It typically occurs following rapid correction of severe hyponatremia, resulting in <strong>hypertonic stress</strong> in astrocytes within the brain, causing demyelination <strong>without inflammation</strong> at the base of the pons, with relative sparing of axons and nerve cells.</p>\n<p>Clinically, patients present one or more days after the overcorrection of hyponatremia with paraparesis or quadriparesis, diplopia, a “locked-in syndrome,” dysphagia, and dysarthria with/without loss of consciousness.</p>\n<p>MRI is useful in establishing the diagnosis. <strong>Owl's eye appearance</strong> on T2 MRI is seen due to sparing of the peripheral pontine fibres and the descending corticospinal tracts. The same finding is known as the 'face of a monkey' appearance in T1 MRI. Other signs seen in this condition include the <strong>trident sign</strong> and the <strong>pig snout sign, </strong>as shown below<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3886","text":"During MRI brain scan of a​ patient, the radiologist​ notices the absence of s​wallow tail appearance. W‌hat is the likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"During MRI brain scan of a patient, the radiologist notices the absence of swallow tail appearance. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Progressive supranuclear p​alsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple sys​tems atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parkinson​'s disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Parkinson's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3141be5a8a0946898bd924eb475ebdd8x1051x1219.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b708b7fdd2d64351a093af36528135a0x510x506.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97256ffe34af449bbc44f08842f1d9e6x720x782.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Parkinson&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>absence </strong>of the<strong> swallow tail sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Parkinson's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The swallow tail sign describes the <strong>normal</strong> axial imaging appearance of<strong> nigrosome-1 </strong>within the <strong>substantia nigra</strong> on a high-resolution T2/SWI weighted MRI. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Wilson’s disease </strong>is a rare, autosomal recessive disorder characterized by <strong>excessive deposition </strong>of free <strong>copper </strong>in tissues. It is due to a loss of function mutation of the ATP7B gene. It is also known as hepatolenticular degenerative disease.</p>\n<p>MRI brain in advanced disease with neurological manifestations can show <strong>giant panda face sign </strong>(as shown below), which is hypointense superior colliculus (chin and mouth of panda), the hyperintense area around the tegmentum of the midbrain (eyes of panda), and normal substantia nigra (ears of panda). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Progressive supranuclear palsy</strong> is a neurodegenerative disease characterized by <strong>atrophy</strong> of structures at the midbrain-diencephalic junction (e.g., superior colliculi, red nuclei, subthalamic nuclei, and globus pallidus) and <strong>cerebellum </strong>(dentate nuclei), and mild cortical atrophy. MRI shows a <strong>hummingbird sign</strong> (atrophy of midbrain structures with a relatively intact pons region). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Multiple system atrophy</strong> (MSA) is a type of atypical Parkinson's disease. Along with the features of Parkinson's disease, they also have <strong>cerebellar</strong> and <strong>autonomic features. </strong>MSA is characterized by the presence of degeneration in the striatum, cerebellum, and inferior olivary nuclei coupled with characteristic <strong>glial cytoplasmic inclusions</strong>. </p>\n<p>The below MRI image shows a <strong>hot cross bun</strong> sign (arrow) due to severe atrophy of pons seen in <strong>multiple system atrophy-c.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3889","text":"A 28-year-old man presente​d with a headache and new​-onset seizures. There is​ history of consumption o‌f pork. MRI was taken and​ given below. What is the​ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man presented with a headache and new-onset seizures. There is history of consumption of pork. MRI was taken and given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurocyst​icercosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuber​culoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary CN​S lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brain ​abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neurocysticercosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37b0257820a7496f84cc801582442152.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3d170ea93ee44bfa1337cdaf081d658x194x222.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e99441b20dec4160b2d6af77f0aad2c9x781x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neurocysticercosis</strong></p><p>The above clinical presentation, along with the MRI brain showing a cyst with a<strong> dot sign</strong> (representing an eccentrically placed <strong>scolex</strong> within a parasitic cyst), is characteristic of <strong>neurocysticercosis. </strong></p>\n<p>Neurocysticercosis is the most common form of cysticercosis and is caused by <strong>encysted larvae</strong> of the <strong>pork tapeworm</strong> <em>Taenia solium</em>. This CNS infection typically occurs through the consumption of food or water contaminated with human feces containing <em>Taenia</em> eggs or via the ingestion of undercooked pork. It can affect the brain parenchyma, subarachnoid space and ventricles.</p>\n<p>Clinically, it presents with <strong>seizures</strong> and symptoms of increased intracranial pressure (headache, nausea, vomiting, vision changes), dizziness, ataxia, confusion or signs of meningeal irritation.</p>\n<p>Parenchymal neurocysticercosis can be categorised into <strong>four stages</strong> -vesicular, colloidal vesicular, granular nodular, and nodular calcified. These stages are readily identified on MRI or CT, depending on the lesion's progression. It is characterised by <strong>cystic lesions </strong>with or without ring enhancement, focal enhancing lesions, nodular calcifications or multilobulated cystic lesions in the subarachnoid space.</p>\n<p><strong>Viable parasites</strong> appear as cystic lesions with a <strong>scolex, </strong>often visible on<strong> MRI,</strong><strong> </strong>while <strong>calcified</strong>, <strong>non-viable</strong> cysts are more effectively identified on <strong>CT</strong>. </p>\n<p>Differential diagnoses for ring-enhancing lesions include brain abscess, toxoplasmosis, central nervous system lymphoma, tuberculoma.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tuberculomas</strong> (parenchymal granulomas) can mostly occur at the junction of white and grey matter. On CT, they appear as small rounded lesions, solid lesions show homogenous enhancement on contrast CT and show ring enhancement when central caseation or liquefaction occurs. MRI brain shows &gt;20mm, conglomerate or lobulated irregular shape,<strong> ring-enhancing lesion.</strong>  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Primary</strong> <strong>CNS lymphoma</strong> is more common in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> individuals, though its incidence in HIV patients has decreased with anti-retroviral therapy. Imaging typically shows single or multiple lesions with contrast enhancement, which is uniform in immunocompetent patients and <strong>ring-like</strong> those who are immunosupressed. Notably, <strong>EBV</strong>-associated cases in immunocompetent individuals may show rim enhancement. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Brain abscess</strong> typically presents as a triad of fever, focal neurological deficits and signs of raised intracranial pressure. It is usually seen with a predisposing condition such as cyanotic heart disease, infected sinuses, middle ear or tooth infection, bacterial meningitis and immunodeficiencies. Common causative organisms include streptococci, gram-negative anaerobic bacilli, enterobacteriaceae, and staphylococcus aureus.</p>\n<p>The below MRI shows a <strong>ring-enhancing lesion</strong>, suggestive of brain abscess.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3892","text":"Which of the following may​ not present with ​a ring​-enhancing lesion on imag​ing?","raw_text":"Which of the following may not present with ​a ring-enhancing lesion on imaging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuber​culoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brain ​abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary CN​S lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary CNS lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/248003ee9d934e0f9353fd2110efb936x781x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce3f21c33059431e8fd769a044cab17cx526x555.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary CNS lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>CNS lymphomas</strong> are usually <strong>homogenously enhancing solid lesions</strong>. However, in the setting of HIV-infected/immunocompromised states, lymphomas may show ring-like enhancement<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>In India, neurocysticercosis and tuberculomas are the commonest causes of a ring-enhancing lesion on brain imaging.</p>\n<p>Ring-enhancing brain lesions can be caused by the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurocysticercosis</li>\n<li><strong>Tuberculoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cerebral abscess</strong></li>\n<li>Metastasis</li>\n<li><strong>Toxoplasmosis</strong></li>\n<li>Subacute infarct /hemorrhage/contusion</li>\n<li>Demyelination (incomplete ring)</li>\n<li>Tumefactive demyelinating lesion (incomplete ring)</li>\n<li>Radiation necrosis</li>\n<li>Postoperative change</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows ring enhancement in brain abscess:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows ring enhancement in glioblastoma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3894","text":"The given MRI finding is s​een in which of the follo​wing conditions?","raw_text":"The given MRI finding is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Familial Creutzfeldt-Jakob​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iatrogenic Creutzfeldt-Jak​ob disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3ade56846c8a4b80837bb250c442e497.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease</strong></p><p>The given MRI shows the <strong>cortical ribbon sign</strong>, which is seen in <strong>sporadic CJD. </strong>This sign refers to the ribbon-like signal hyperintensity of cerebral cortical gyri.</p>\n<p>Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) is a <strong>spongiform encephalopathy</strong> caused by <strong>prions</strong>. The abnormally folded prion protein (<strong>PrPSc</strong>) accumulates as <strong>plaques</strong>, causing degeneration of surrounding neuronal and glial tissue with intracellular vacuoles, leading to the characteristic spongiform encephalopathy. Clinically, it is characterised by <strong>rapidly progressive dementia</strong>, myoclonus, akinetic mutism, and cerebellar or visual disturbances.</p>\n<p>Subtypes of CJD and imaging features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sporadic CJD (sCJD): The most common type, showing <strong>cortical ribboning </strong>and<strong> basal ganglia hyperintensities </strong>on DWI/FLAIR.</li>\n<li>Variant CJD (vCJD): Linked to mad cow disease and Kuru, showing a '<strong>hockey stick</strong>' sign on MRI (hyperintensity in the pulvinar region of the thalamus).</li>\n<li>Familial CJD (fCJD): Linked to PRNP gene mutations, showing hyperintensities in the caudate and putamen nuclei on MRI.</li>\n<li>Iatrogenic: Caused by prion-contaminated materials</li>\n</ul>\n<p>EEG shows characteristic <strong>periodic </strong>triphasic or polyphasic<strong> sharp wave complexes</strong> seen as spikes or spike-wave patterns. CSF is predominantly normal but may show a positive assay for <strong>14-3-3 protein</strong>. </p>\n<p>Management is mainly supportive as the disease is usually fatal within a year of the onset of symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3898","text":"A T2 weighted MRI was done​ for a child who presente​d with delayed milestones​. It showed hyperintensit‌y in frontal white matter​ with bilateral and symme​trical involvement. What ‍is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A T2 weighted MRI was done for a child who presented with delayed milestones. It showed hyperintensity in frontal white matter with bilateral and symmetrical involvement. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Canavan​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alexander​‘s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X linked adrenoleukodystro​phy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metachromatic leukodystrop​hy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alexander‘s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1ce7525866449a1a29d2e0175c048e4x1280x5772.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alexander‘s disease</strong></p><p>The T2-weighted MRI findings suggest <strong>Alexander disease</strong>, showing hyperintensity in the frontal white matter with bilateral symmetrical involvement.</p>\n<p>Alexander disease is a type of <strong>dysmyelinating disease</strong> that typically progresses posteriorly to involve the parietal white matter and internal and external capsules, often accompanied by the formation of cysts. </p>\n<p>Dysmyelinating diseases, or leukodystrophies, are inherited neurodegenerative disorders that affect the integrity of myelin in the brain and peripheral nerves.</p>\n<p>Other examples of dysmyelinating diseases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metachromatic leukodystrophy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Late infantile (most common), juvenile, and adult types.</li>\n<li><strong>Symmetric </strong>confluent areas of <strong>T2 hyperintensity </strong>in the<strong> periventricular white matter</strong>.</li>\n<li>Radiating T2-hypointense lines through the demyelinated deep white matter represent the sparing of the perivascular white matter. This results in the <strong>tigroid</strong> and <strong>leopard skin </strong>patterns when imaged in the long axis or cross-section, respectively.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy: </strong>Affects the white matter of the central nervous system, adrenal cortex, and testes.</li>\n<li><strong>Canavan disease</strong>: Spongiform leukodystrophy (extensive vacuolization of <strong>subcortical white matter</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>MELAS</strong> Syndrome: Mitochondrial encephalomyopathy with lactic acidosis and stroke-like episodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows the areas of hyperintensities noted in each of these conditions:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3sWjbsNt","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"8_carcinoma_breast___risk_factors_and_types","title":"8.Carcinoma Breast   Risk Factors And Types","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB0422","text":"Which one of the following​ pathological features ha​s a higher risk of develo‌ping into invasive breast‌ carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which one of the following pathological features has a higher risk of developing into invasive breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ​metaplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ductal pap​illomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atypical ductal hyperplasi​a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sclerosin​g adenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atypical ductal hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atypical ductal hyperplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Atypical hyperplasia</strong> has a higher risk of developing into invasive breast carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0966","text":"A woman with a family hist​ory of breast and ovarian​ cancers underwent geneti‌c testing. She is found t‌o have a BRCA1 gene mutat‌ion. Which of the followi​ng is she at risk of deve​loping?","raw_text":"A  woman with a family history of breast and ovarian cancers underwent genetic testing. She is found to have a BRCA1 gene mutation.  Which of the following is she at risk of developing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melanoma along with breast​ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Well differentiated breast​ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post menopausal breast can​cer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triple receptor negative b​reast cancer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Triple receptor negative breast cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Triple receptor negative breast cancer</strong></p><p>Individuals with BRCA1 mutations are likely to develop <strong>triple receptor-negative</strong> and <strong>high-grade</strong> breast carcinomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0957","text":"Which of the following doe​s not increase the risk o​f breast carcinoma in wom‌en? Early age of first pr‌egnancy Positive family h‌istory Breastfeeding Earl​y menarche and late menop​ause Multiparity","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following does not increase the risk of breast carcinoma in women?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Early age of first pregnancy</li>\n<li>Positive family history</li>\n<li>Breastfeeding</li>\n<li>Early menarche and late menopause</li>\n<li>Multiparity</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl​y 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>Early age of first pregnancy, breastfeeding, and multiparity are <strong>protective factors</strong> for carcinoma of the breast. This is due to a reduction in the overall number of ovulatory cycles thereby reducing the duration of estrogen's action on the breast tissues. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0968","text":"The most common location o​f breast carcinoma is the​ _______.","raw_text":"The most common location of breast carcinoma is the _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper oute​r quadrant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower oute​r quadrant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper inne​r quadrant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower inne​r quadrant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Upper outer quadrant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Upper outer quadrant</strong></p><p>The <strong>upper outer quadrant</strong> of the breast has a greater volume of tissue and is the <strong>most common site </strong>of breast carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0976","text":"A woman presented to the c​linic with redness and it​ching over the left breas‌t as shown in the image b‌elow. Histopathology show‌ed large pale vacuolated ​cells with round nuclei i​n the epithelium. What is​ the most likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A woman presented to the clinic with redness and itching over the left breast as shown in the image below.  Histopathology showed large pale vacuolated cells with round nuclei in the epithelium. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phyllod​es tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paget's disease of the bre​ast","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inflammatory breast carcin​oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic​ eczema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paget's disease of the breast","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/67eb725039604aaab9474a49d4d4a5cf.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f0b1723db92417eb59675ecd5114071x1279x937.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paget&#x27;s disease of the breast</strong></p><p>The clinical picture and histology are suggestive of<strong> Paget's disease of the breast. </strong></p>\n<p>Paget’s disease is a rare type of cancer involving the nipple-areolar complex, often with an underlying in-situ or invasive ductal carcinoma. It presents as a <strong>chronic, eczematous eruption </strong>of the nipple which may progress to ulceration. Paget disease of the breast is often mistaken for eczema. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 180px; width: 348.492px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\"><strong>Paget's disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\"><strong>Eczema</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Laterality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Unilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\">Bilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Age group</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\">Lactating women</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Edges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Distinct</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\">Indistinct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Vesicles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Nipple</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Usually destroyed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\">Usually intact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Lump</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Usually present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.492px;\">No underlying lump </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A <strong>nipple biopsy</strong> specimen of Paget's disease will show a population of cells that are identical to the underlying malignant cells. The presence of large, <strong>pale, vacuolated</strong> cells (<strong>Paget cells)</strong> with round or oval nuclei are pathognomonic for this condition.</p>\n<p>Depending on the type and extent of underlying malignancy, it can be treated with <strong>lumpectomy </strong>or<strong> mastectomy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Histologically, Paget's disease can be confused with superficial spreading melanoma, particularly if they have incorporated melanin from adjacent epidermis. <strong>Paget's disease</strong> shows positive immunostaining with carcinoembryonic<strong> </strong>antigen (<strong>CEA</strong>), while superficial spreading <strong>melanoma</strong> is positive for <strong>S-100</strong> antigen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0970","text":"Which is the most common v​ariant of breast carcinom​a?","raw_text":"Which is the most common variant of breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lobular ​carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ductal c​arcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tubular ​carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary​ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ductal carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ductal carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Invasive ductal carcinoma</strong> (not otherwise specified) is the <strong>most common variant</strong> of breast carcinoma. It is also known as <strong>infiltrating ductal carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0981","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding breast​ carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cutaneous oedema in inflam​matory carcinoma is the r​esult of blockage of the ‌sub-dermal lymphatics wit‌h carcinoma cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous carcinomas are us​ually hormone negative","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calcifications are frequen​tly associated with ducta​l carcinoma in situ","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lobular carcinoma in situ ​is multi centric and bila​teral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucinous carcinomas are usually hormone negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucinous carcinomas are usually hormone negative</strong></p><p>Mucinous carcinoma is usually <strong>hormone-positive</strong> (90%). It is also called <strong>colloid carcinoma</strong>. It is a low-grade tumor with a good prognosis. It typically presents as a <strong>bulky mass</strong> in the elderly. </p>\n<p>Option A: Pathologic hallmark of <strong>inflammatory carcinoma</strong> is a painful, swollen breast, which is warm with <strong>cutaneous edema</strong> due to <strong>blockage </strong>of <strong>subdermal lymphatics</strong> with carcinoma cells.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Calcification</strong> is usually associated with ductal carcinoma in-situ (<strong>DCIS</strong>). It is the <strong>most common </strong>malignancy detected in screening mammograms.</p>\n<p>Option D: Lobular carcinoma in situ (<strong>LCIS</strong>) is mainly <strong>multicentric</strong> (sometimes multifocal) and <strong>bilateral</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0982","text":"Arrange the following subt​ypes of breast carcinomas​ according to the best to‌ worst prognosis? 1) Medu‌llary carcinoma 2) Tubula‌r carcinoma 3) Invasive d​uctal carcinoma(NOS) 4) I​nflammatory carcinoma 5) ​Invasive lobular carcinom​a","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following subtypes of breast carcinomas according to the best to worst prognosis?</p>\n<div>1) Medullary carcinoma</div>\n<div>2) Tubular carcinoma</div>\n<div>3) Invasive ductal carcinoma(NOS)</div>\n<div>4) Inflammatory carcinoma</div>\n<div>5) Invasive lobular carcinoma</div>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 5, ​2, 4, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 1, ​5, 3, 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 1, ​3, 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ​3, 5, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2, 1, 5, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2, 1, 5, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Tubular</strong> carcinoma has the<strong> best</strong> prognosis. <strong>Inflammatory</strong> carcinoma has the <strong>worst</strong> prognosis. </p>\n<p>Order of breast carcinomas from best to worst prognosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tubular carcinoma (Best prognosis)</li>\n<li>Adenocystic</li>\n<li>Cribriform carcinoma</li>\n<li>Papillary carcinoma</li>\n<li>Medullary carcinoma</li>\n<li>Invasive lobular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Invasive ductal carcinoma (NOS)*</li>\n<li>Inflammatory carcinoma breast (Worst prognosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NOS- Not otherwise specified<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6822","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of swe​lling of the right breast‌ over the past 15 days. O‌n examination, there was ‌local rise of temperature​, and the swelling was mo​bile over the chest wall.​ What is the likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents with complaints of swelling of the right breast over the past 15 days. On examination, there was local rise of temperature, and the swelling was mobile over the chest wall. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflammator​y carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Giant fib​roadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phyllod​es tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Galac​tocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inflammatory carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aaf7d68ba0f941999b8f1c916ce223f3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inflammatory carcinoma</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the rapid onset of the disease and the examination findings are suggestive of <strong>inflammatory carcinoma</strong> of the breast.</p>\n<p>Inflammatory breast cancer is the <strong>most aggressive</strong> subtype of breast cancer. It <strong>mimics acute mastitis</strong>. The<strong> hallmark </strong>of inflammatory breast carcinoma is the diffuse tumor involvement of the dermal lymphatics within the breast and the overlying skin, often without a discrete underlying tumor mass <strong>(peau d' orange appearance)</strong>. The patient presents with rapidly progressive swelling of the breast, warmth, erythema and edema. A breast lump may or may not be present. </p>\n<p>A diagnosis of inflammatory breast carcinoma should be suspected when a patient with rapidly progressive swelling with inflammatory skin changes <strong>does not respond to antibiotics</strong>. The imaging features with ultrasound and a mammograph is non-specific due to the underlying erythema and edema. A biopsy should be done to confirm the diagnosis. </p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>combined-modality treatment</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neoadjuvant chemo­therapy</li>\n<li>Mastectomy</li>\n<li>Radiation therapy</li>\n<li>Hormonal therapy- for ER-positive tumors</li>\n<li>Trastuzumab for HER-2-positive tumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong><strong>Giant fibroadenomas</strong> occasionally occur during puberty. These are benign solid encapsulated <strong>slow-growing</strong> tumors <strong>over 5 cm in diameter</strong>. It presents as a painless lump in the breast. They are managed by excision via a submammary incision<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Phyllodes tumors</strong> usually occur in women over the age of 40 years but can appear in younger women. Phyllodes tumor is mostly benign even though it is also called cystosarcoma phyllodes. It presents as a large tumor with an unevenly bosselated surface. They are usually mobile over the chest wall despite their large size. This tumor <strong>does not infiltrate</strong> the skin. Nipple retraction is not seen in this condition. <strong>Benign</strong> lesions can be treated with enucleation or wide local excision. Massive tumours, recurrent tumours and malignant lesions are treated with a mastectomy.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Galactocele </strong>are the <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> <strong>benign breast</strong> lesions in <strong>lactating</strong> women. It is a cystic enlargement of the mammary gland containing milk. They are mostly detected <strong>during lactation </strong>or in<strong> the third trimester</strong> of pregnancy but rarely, may also present after stopping breastfeeding. Clinically, the mass is usually <strong>firm</strong> and <strong>non-tender</strong> and presents as a solid tumour-like lesion on physical examination. The patient usually notes the lesion <strong>during</strong> <strong>lactation</strong> or some period after lactation. <strong>Needle</strong> <strong>aspiration</strong>, followed by <strong>excision</strong> should it recur, is the <strong>standard</strong> <strong>method</strong> of treating galactocele. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0969","text":"A postmenopausal lady came​ to the clinic with compl​aints of breast pain and ‌a lump in her right breas‌t for the past 2 months. ‌On examination, the breas​t appears as shown in the​ image. What is the most ​likely cause for this fin​ding?","raw_text":"A postmenopausal lady came to the clinic with complaints of breast pain and a lump in her right breast for the past 2 months. On examination, the breast appears as shown in the image. What is the most likely cause for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infiltration of Cooper’s l​igaments","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obstruction of subcutaneou​s lymphatics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infiltration of lactiferou​s duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infiltration of the pector​alis muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Obstruction of subcutaneous lymphatics","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c01c476431734f299ca3c8cae64c1262.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Obstruction of subcutaneous lymphatics</strong></p><p>In the given image, the orange peel-like appearance, also called as <strong>peau d’orange</strong> is caused by cutaneous lymphatic edema, due to the <strong>obstruction </strong>of<strong> subcutaneous lymphatics.</strong> It is the hallmark of <strong>inflammatory carcinoma. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Infiltration of <strong>Cooper’s ligaments</strong> leads to <strong>dimpling </strong>of the skin.</p>\n<p>Option C: Infiltration of the <strong>lactiferous ducts</strong> leads to <strong>nipple retraction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cancer-en-cuirasse</strong> is an aggressive form of cancer, where there is cutaneous and subcutaneous infiltration of the cancer cells.</p>\n<p>Note: While Peau d’orange indicates skin involvement, dimpling of the skin and nipple retraction do not indicate skin involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3937","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted with a painless lump ​in the breast. Mammograph‌y done showed suspicious ‌microcalcifications. USG-‌guided core biopsy reveal​ed large cells with cytop​lasmic mucoid globules an​d neighborhood calcificat​ions. There is no invasio‌n into the basement membr‌ane and fibrovascular str​oma. What is the most lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with a painless lump in the breast. Mammography done showed suspicious microcalcifications. USG-guided core biopsy revealed large cells with cytoplasmic mucoid globules and neighborhood calcifications. There is no invasion into the basement membrane and fibrovascular stroma. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ductal carci​noma in situ","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paget’s disease of the bre​ast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lobular carc​inoma in situ","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phyllod​es tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lobular carcinoma in situ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06b4ae3201784fae8d800fb26c98a913x796x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edb793b4c2da4c2483cfbecb44112b4ax1280x1883.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c451cfe1a8a482f800dd0ae75373f5dx1279x937.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21d748b802f347339280a0b7a522cc53x720x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lobular carcinoma in situ</strong></p><p>The presence of large cells with <strong>cytoplasmic mucoid globules</strong> with an intact basement membrane, and <strong>neighborhood calcifications</strong> are characteristic of <strong>lobular carcinoma in situ</strong> (LCIS). </p>\n<p>Lobular carcinoma in-situ is usually detected as an <strong>incidental finding</strong> on evaluation of either a mass lesion or abnormalities on mammography. </p>\n<p>The slide below shows cytoplasmic mucoid globules seen in lobular carcinoma in situ.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Ductal carcinoma in situ (<strong>DCIS</strong>) can have various histological appearances like solid, comedo, papillary cribriform, etc</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Histology of <strong>Paget’s disease</strong> of the breast shows the presence of <strong>large pale vacuolated cells</strong> (Paget cells) in the rete pegs of the epithelium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Histology of <strong>phyllodes tumor</strong> consists of nodules of <strong>proliferating stromal cells</strong>, that are covered by epithelium. This resembles a <strong>leaf</strong> and is hence termed as phyllodes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0972","text":"Which of the following is ​most likely in a woman wi​th bilateral and multicen‌tric breast lumps?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most likely in a woman with bilateral and multicentric breast lumps?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ductal carcinoma in situ (​DCIS)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lobular carcinoma in situ ​(LCIS)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucinous ​carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary​ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d83df3d4a61a46a1a5dd7529687547fax1280x1016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS)</strong></p><p><strong>Bilaterality</strong> and <strong>multicentricity</strong> are most commonly associated with <strong>lobular carcinoma</strong> <strong>in situ</strong> (LCIS).</p>\n<p>LCIS occurs bilaterally in 50 - 70% of cases. Multicentricity is seen in 60 - 90% of patients with LCIS. </p>\n<p><strong>Multicentricity </strong>refers to the<strong> </strong>occurrence of second breast cancer <strong>outside </strong>the<strong> breast quadrant </strong>of the primary cancer (or at least 4 cm away). <strong>Multifocality </strong>refers to the occurrence of a second cancer <strong>within</strong> the<strong> same breast quadrant </strong>as the primary cancer (or within 4 cm of it).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0974","text":"An annual screening mammog​ram done for a middle-age​d woman showed clusters o‌f microcalcifications. St‌ereotactic trucut biopsy ‌revealed a comedo growth ​pattern. What is the most​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An annual screening mammogram done for a middle-aged woman showed clusters of microcalcifications. Stereotactic trucut biopsy revealed a comedo growth pattern. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ductal carci​noma in-situ","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lobular carc​inoma in-situ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inflammator​y carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ductal carcinoma in-situ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87398fbd48b845e0a00857b365af894ex1280x1883.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ductal carcinoma in-situ</strong></p><p>The presence of microcalcifications on mammography and comedo pattern on histology is suggestive of <strong>ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS).</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast conservation surgery with radiotherapy or,</li>\n<li>Simple mastectomy- least loco-regional recurrence</li>\n<li>No role of chemotherapy</li>\n<li>If ER, PR +ve- hormonal therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Comedo</strong> subtype refers to the presence of <strong>necrosis</strong>. It implies that the tumor cells are <strong>growing</strong> <strong>rapidly</strong> and the blood vessel is not able to supply all the malignant cells. On the contrary, non-comedo subtype implies that the tumor is growing slowly and the blood vessel is able to supply all the cells. Therefore, the comedo subtypes are of <strong>high-grade DCIS.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0975","text":"A woman with BRCA1 gene mu​tation presented with lum​ps in both breasts and en‌larged axillary lymphaden‌opathy. She gives a histo‌ry of breast cancer in he​r sister. MRI and trucut ​biopsy done confirmed the​ diagnosis of medullary c​arcinoma of the breast. W‌hich of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ the given condition?","raw_text":"A woman with BRCA1 gene mutation presented with lumps in both breasts and enlarged axillary lymphadenopathy. She gives a history of breast cancer in her sister. MRI and trucut biopsy done confirmed the diagnosis of medullary carcinoma of the breast. Which of the following statements is true regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph node enlargement can​ be due to reactive hyper​plasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a slow growing tumor​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tamoxifen can be used in t​he treatment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has poorer prognosis than ​invasive lobular carcinom​a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymph node enlargement can be due to reactive hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04c84e8b1a4b4d548466b93368df314ax1280x1224.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymph node enlargement can be due to reactive hyperplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Medullary carcinoma</strong> of the breast is associated with <strong>intense lymphocyte proliferation</strong>. Therefore, the lymph node enlargement can be due to <strong>reactive hyperplasia </strong>rather than the spread of the cancer cells to the lymph nodes. </p>\n<p>Medullary carcinoma is a common type of breast cancer associated with <strong>BRCA1</strong> gene mutation. Bilateral tumors are common when family history is present. The tumor can <strong>enlarge rapidly</strong> due to necrosis and hemorrhage. The majority of the tumors are<strong> triple-negative </strong>(ER/PR/Her-2 negative).</p>\n<p>Histopathology consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A dense <strong>lymphoreticular infiltration</strong> composed predominantly of <strong>lymphocytes</strong> and <strong>plasma cells</strong></li>\n<li>A sheet-like growth pattern of anaplastic cells with absent ductal or lobular differentiation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It has a <strong>better prognosis</strong> than invasive ductal or <strong>invasive lobular</strong> carcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0985","text":"A male patient who present​ed with a lump in his rig​ht breast is diagnosed to‌ have breast carcinoma. W‌hich of the following is ‌true regarding the given ​condition?","raw_text":"A male patient who presented with a lump in his right breast is diagnosed to have breast carcinoma. Which of the following is true regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The prognosis is worse tha​n in females","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infiltrating ductal carcin​oma is the most common ty​pe","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with BRCA 1 mut​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tamoxifen is not effective​ in treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infiltrating ductal carcinoma is the most common type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infiltrating ductal carcinoma is the most common type</strong></p><p><strong>Infiltrating ductal carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> type of breast cancer seen <strong>in men</strong>.</p>\n<p>Breast cancer in men accounts for less than 1% of all breast cancers. The peak incidence is between <strong>60-70 years</strong> of age. Majority of the patients present with a <strong>hard mass</strong> in the breast. Important <strong>risk factors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BRCA2 mutation</strong></li>\n<li>Radiation exposure</li>\n<li>Estrogen therapy</li>\n<li>Testicular feminizing syndrome</li>\n<li>Klinefelter syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> of male breast carcinoma is <strong>similar to females</strong> which involves a clinical examination, imaging, and tissue biopsy. Grading, treatment, and prognosis are the <strong>same</strong> as female breast cancer. The majority of male breast cancers are <strong>hormone receptor-positive</strong> and hence<strong> tamoxifen</strong> is used in the treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4006","text":"A 35-year-old woman is wor​ried about developing bre​ast cancer. Her mother an‌d sister had developed br‌east cancer at the age of‌ 30 years and 32 years re​spectively. She has 2 dau​ghters. After evaluation,​ she is found to have a B​RCA-1 gene mutation. Whic‌h of the following method‌s will you suggest for th​e maximum risk reduction ‌of developing carcinoma b‌reast in this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman is worried about developing breast cancer. Her mother and sister had developed breast cancer at the age of 30 years and 32 years respectively. She has 2 daughters. After evaluation, she is found to have a BRCA-1 gene mutation. Which of the following methods will you suggest for the maximum risk reduction of developing carcinoma breast in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Close surveillance with MR​I every 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tamoxifen chemoprophylaxis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prophylacti​c mastectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prophylactic mastectomy wi​th bilateral salpingo-oop​horectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prophylactic mastectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prophylactic mastectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylactic mastectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong> will provide <strong>maximum risk reduction</strong> in this patient with a complete family, a positive gene mutation, and positive family history.</p>\n<p><strong>Breast cancer risk reduction techniques:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Lifestyle modification:</strong> Low-calorie diet, daily exercise, no smoking, no drinking, etc.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">More <strong>frequent screening</strong> every 6-12 months with mammogram +- MRI</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Chemoprevention:</strong> Preferred in women &gt;35 years of age in patients who are ER/PR positive. Given for 5-10 years.</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>SERMS</strong> (selective estrogen receptor modulators) - Tamoxifen and raloxifene - have adverse effects. If a patient on tamoxifen develops abnormal uterine bleeding, the drug needs to be stopped.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Aromatase Inhibitors</strong> (AIs): Better tolerated than SERMs have a low risk of cardiovascular and endometrial complications. They are used only in post-menopausal women.</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Risk-reduction surgeries:</strong> done in patients with gene mutations or patients with first-degree relatives with breast cancers. Provides &gt;90% risk-reduction. Preferred in patients who have completed their family. Best results when done &lt; 40 years of age.</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bilateral mastectomy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Salpingo-oophorectomy:</strong> preferred in patients with <strong>BRCA1/2</strong> mutations to <strong>prevent</strong> the <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>ovarian cancer</strong> too.</li>\n</ul>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-d-n0kMX","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"9_bls_and_pals","title":"9.Bls And Pals","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD7393","text":"Which of the following is ‌the correct sequence?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the correct sequence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Assess the patient’s respo‌nse, Activate ERS, check ‍carotid pulse, Start CPR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Check carotid pulse, Start‌ CPR, Call for help and a‍ctivate ERS, Defibrillate‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defibrillate, Assess respo‌nse, Check carotid pulse,‍ Maintain airway","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start CPR, Activate ERS, D‌efibrillate, Check pulse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Assess the patient’s response, Activate ERS, check carotid pulse, Start CPR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Assess the patient’s response, Activate ERS, check carotid pulse, Start CPR</strong></p><p><strong>BLS sequence:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>First step </strong>– Assess safety of the surroundings.</p>\n<p><strong>Second step </strong>- Assess for responsiveness</p>\n<p><strong>Third step </strong>– Proceed if the patient is unresponsive. Shout for help.</p>\n<p>If the patient is unresponsive assess for breathing and carotid pulse simultaneously for no more than 10 seconds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6700","text":"On your morning walk in th‌e park, you notice an unc‍onscious man lying on the‍ ground. Which of the fol​lowing options would be t‌he next best step?","raw_text":"On your morning walk in the park, you notice an unconscious man lying on the ground. Which of the following options would be the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Check carotid pulse, start‌ CPR, and call for help; ‍Defibrillate if no pulse.‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Call for help, check pulse‌, do CPR, defibrillate if‍ no pulse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obs‌erve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ignore and continue to wal‌k","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Call for help, check pulse, do CPR, defibrillate if no pulse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Call for help, check pulse, do CPR, defibrillate if no pulse</strong></p><p>The next best step is to Call for help, check pulse, do CPR, defibrillate If no pulse.</p>\n<p>Steps in basic life support:</p>\n<p>First step – Assess safety of the surroundings.</p>\n<p>Second step - Assess for responsiveness</p>\n<p>Third step – Proceed if the patient is unresponsive. Shout for help.</p>\n<p>If the patient is unresponsive, assess for breathing and carotid pulse simultaneously for no more than 10 seconds.</p>\n<p>In the given scenario, it is already mentioned that you are in a park (scene safety has been assessed), and that the person is unconscious (responsiveness has been assessed). So, the immediate next step is to call for help, and start CPR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2582","text":"On witnessing a cardiac ar‌rest, what is the first t‍hing you would do?","raw_text":"On witnessing a cardiac arrest, what is the first thing you would do?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Check for victim’s respons‌iveness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Check fo‌r a pulse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Check for‌ breathing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Check for s‌cene safety","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Check for scene safety","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Check for scene safety</strong></p><p>The first thing that needs to be done on witnessing a cardiac arrest is to<strong> verify </strong>the<strong> scene safety.</strong></p>\n<p>On ensuring that the scene is safe, the victim is<strong> checked for responsiveness</strong>. If unresponsive, call for help and then check for breathing and pulse simultaneously taking <strong>not more than 10 seconds.</strong></p>\n<p>If there is no breathing and no pulse, immediate <strong>chest compression</strong> and <strong>rescue breaths</strong> are started at a ratio of<strong> 30:2</strong>.</p>\n<p>If there is a pulse, but no breathing, give one rescue breath every 5-6 seconds or about 10-12 breaths/min.</p>\n<p>When an <strong>automated external defibrillator</strong> arrives, the rhythm is checked; if shockable, one shock is administered and CPR is resumed immediately.</p>\n<p>If not shockable, then CPR is resumed immediately.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2580","text":"What does the cardiopulmon‌ary resuscitation sequenc‍e start with?","raw_text":"What does the cardiopulmonary resuscitation sequence start with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air‌way","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brea‌thing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Circu‌lation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dr‌ugs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Circulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Circulation</strong></p><p>The sequence of cardiopulmonary resuscitation starts with <strong>circulation</strong>, <strong>airway and then breathing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maintaining circulation involves chest compressions which lead to ejection of blood from the heart as a result of actual compression of the heart between the sternum and vertebral column.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2581","text":"A 50-year-old man presente‌d to the ER with complain‍ts of chest pain. He sudd‍enly collapsed and became​ unresponsive. His pulse ‌was not recordable so you​ started chest compressio‌ns. Which of the followin‍g steps would you not per​form according to the BLS‌ algorithm?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presented to the ER with complaints of chest pain. He suddenly collapsed and became unresponsive. His pulse was not recordable so you started chest compressions. Which of the following steps would you not perform according to the BLS algorithm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defibrillation using Autom‌ated external defibrillat‍or","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intubation using Laryngeal‌ mask airway","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Performing Head tilt – chi‌n lift","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bag and mask‌ ventilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intubation using Laryngeal mask airway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intubation using Laryngeal mask airway</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngeal mask airway </strong>and other supraglottic airway devices are part of advanced airway management, which is included in advanced cardiac life support <strong>(ACLS).</strong></p>\n<p>Basic life support refers to the emergency management of sudden cardiac arrest, heart attack, stroke, and foreign body airway obstruction, usually without the use of equipment other than automated external defibrillators.</p>\n<p>The CPR sequence is as follows - circulation (chest compression), airway, and then breathing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Circulation:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Chest compressions lead to ejection of blood from the heart, as a result of actual compression of the heart between the sternum and vertebral column.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Airway:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Head tilt, chin lift, and jaw thrust maneuvers are used to open the airway.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Breathing:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>If there is no adequate breathing even after opening the airway, the rescuer should initiate assisted ventilation by giving rescue breaths to the patient using mouth-to-mouth, mouth-to-nose, mouth-to-stoma, mouth-to-barrier device, mouth-to-face shield, or mouth-to-mask rescue breathing or by using a bag-mask device.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Defibrillation:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Using automated external defibrillators (AED)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2584","text":"You witness a 40-year-old ‌man suddenly collapse nea‍r the ER. He is unrespons‍ive and you start chest c​ompressions as his pulse ‌is also not recordable. F​or maximum effectiveness,‌ what should be the depth‍ of your compressions?","raw_text":"You witness a 40-year-old man suddenly collapse near the ER. He is unresponsive and you start chest compressions as his pulse is also not recordable. For maximum effectiveness, what should be the depth of your compressions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 ‌cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5 cm</strong></p><p>High-quality cardiopulmonary resuscitation involves adequate <strong>chest compressions </strong>to a <strong>depth</strong> of at least <strong>5 cm in adults.</strong></p>\n<p>If a feedback device is used, then the compression depth can be increased to 6 cm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2585","text":"In order to provide high-q‌uality CPR, what should t‍he rate of chest compress‍ions per minute be?","raw_text":"In order to provide high-quality CPR, what should the rate of chest compressions per minute be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60‌-80","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"80-‌100","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100‌-120","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"120‌-140","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 100-120","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 100-120</strong></p><p>Chest compressions should be performed at a rate of <strong>100-120/ minute</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2588","text":"When providing CPR to an a‌dult, what ratio of chest‍ compressions to rescue b‍reaths would you provide ​if there were 2 rescuers?‌","raw_text":"When providing CPR to an adult, what ratio of chest compressions to rescue breaths would you provide if there were 2 rescuers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15‌:2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30‌:2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30:2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30:2</strong></p><p>During cardiopulmonary resuscitation of<strong> adults, </strong>the ratio of chest compression to rescue breaths is <strong>30:2 irrespective </strong>of the <strong>number of rescuers.</strong></p>\n<p>In infants and children, the ratio is 30:2 for a single-rescuer and 15:2 if 2 or more rescuers are present.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2590","text":"For effective chest compre‌ssion during CPR, where s‍hould the hands of the he‍althcare provider ideally​ be placed?","raw_text":"For effective chest compression during CPR, where should the hands of the healthcare provider ideally be placed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper half‌ of sternum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle thir‌d of sternum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower half‌ of sternum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Upper third‌ of sternum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lower half of sternum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lower half of sternum</strong></p><p>In <strong>children and adults</strong>, chest compressions are performed by locating the xiphoid process and placing the heel of the hand over the <strong>lower</strong> <strong>half</strong> of the <strong>sternum</strong>. The other hand is placed over the hand on the sternum with the fingers either interlaced or extended and the elbow extended so that the weight of the upper body is used for compression.</p>\n<p>In <strong>infants</strong>, if there is a <strong>single</strong> <strong>rescuer</strong>, compressions are effected by placing <strong>2 fingers </strong>in the <strong>center of the chest</strong>, just below the nipple line. If there are <strong>2 or more rescuers</strong>, then <strong>2 thumb technique</strong> is used.</p>\n<p>In infants and children, the depth of compression should be at least one-third of the anteroposterior diameter of the chest, which is about 5 cm in children and 4 cm in infants.</p>\n<p>For pediatric patients, the ratio of chest compression to rescue breaths is 30:2 for a single rescuer and 15:2 if 2 or more rescuers are present. In adults, the ratio is 30:2 irrespective of the number of rescuers.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6916","text":"Which of the following can‌ be considered as effecti‍ve CPR? 1. Respiratory ra‍te: 20 - 24 breaths per m​inute. 2. Chest compressi‌ons: 100-120/min. 3. Allo​w complete chest recoil. ‌4. 5-6 cm depression.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following can be considered as effective CPR?</p>\n<p>1. Respiratory rate: 20 - 24 breaths per minute.</p>\n<p>2. Chest compressions: 100-120/min.</p>\n<p>3. Allow complete chest recoil.</p>\n<p>4. 5-6 cm depression.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3‌, and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,‌ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3,‌ and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 and ‌2 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 3, and 4</strong></p><p>The ideal rescue breath rate for adults is 10 breaths/min, while in infants and children up to puberty it is 20-30 breaths/min. As the person's age group is not specified in the question, assuming an adult, statement 1 is incorrect, while 2, 3, and 4 are correct.</p>\n<p>Effective chest compression in <strong>adults</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Provide <strong>100-120 compressions per minute</strong> (30 compressions for every 15-18 sec).</li>\n<li>Place your arms, one over the other in the midline, on the lower 1/3rd of the patient's sternum, and lock your arms.</li>\n<li>Use both arms to press the chest to a depth of<strong> 5-6 cm</strong> (2 - 2.4 inches) or more.</li>\n<li>Press hard and fast</li>\n<li>Allow for <strong>full chest recoil</strong> after every compression. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Effective chest compressions in a<strong> child older than 1 year and up to puberty:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The compression rate is 100 - 120/min  (30 compressions for every 15-18 sec).</li>\n<li><strong>One or both palms</strong> are placed in the midline.</li>\n<li>The depth of compression is <strong>2 inches.</strong></li>\n<li>Press hard and fast</li>\n<li>Allow for full chest recoil after every compression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Effective chest compressions in<strong> infants (&lt;1 year):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>100 to 120 compressions per minute (30 compressions for every 15-18 sec).</li>\n<li><strong>One provider: Place two fingers</strong>, one between the nipple line and the other 1 cm below.</li>\n<li><strong>Two providers:</strong> <strong>Encircle</strong> the infant's <strong>torso</strong> with both hands with<strong> both thumbs</strong> pointing cephalic positioned 1cm below the nipples over the sternum. </li>\n<li>Depth of compressions should be at least <strong>1.5 inches or 1/3 the depth</strong> of the infant’s chest.</li>\n<li>Press hard and fast</li>\n<li>Allow for full chest recoil after every compression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2583","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in high-qual‍ity CPR?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in high-quality CPR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compression depth of at le‌ast 5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rotate chest compressor ev‌ery 3 mins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Avoid excessive ventilatio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compression – ventilation ‌ratio of 30:2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rotate chest compressor every 3 mins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rotate chest compressor every 3 mins</strong></p><p>Rotation of chest compressor is done <strong>every 2 minutes or earlier</strong> if the compressor is <strong>fatigued</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2615","text":"A 3-year-old child is brou‌ght to the casualty with ‍a history of choking over‍ a peanut. The child sudd​enly becomes unresponsive‌. What would you do as th​e next step of management‌?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child is brought to the casualty with a history of choking over a peanut. The child suddenly becomes unresponsive. What would you do as the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Give 5 b‌ack slaps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Give abdomi‌nal thrusts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endotrachea‌l intubation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chest com‌pressions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chest compressions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8854b85ea9134071803e1768faf1bb37x510x441.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dcac0ddf84f454f80cf9836c2c91ee5x510x437.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chest compressions</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">If a choking victim becomes <strong>unresponsive</strong>, call for help and immediately start <strong>CPR,</strong> beginning with chest compressions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For pediatric CPR, if a single rescuer is present, then chest compression to ventilation is performed in a ratio of 30:2. If there are 2 or more rescuers, then the ratio is 15:2.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">While giving rescue breaths, the airway is checked for a foreign body. If it is accessible, then the object can be removed. If not, then CPR is continued. <strong>Blind finger sweeping</strong> may be <strong>hazardous</strong> as it can push the object further down the airway.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">If the <strong>child </strong>is<strong> responsive</strong>, then <strong>abdominal thrusts</strong> are performed to help relieve airway obstruction.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">In a </span><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">choking infant </strong><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">who is </span><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">responsive</strong><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">, </span><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">5 back slaps</strong><span style=\"text-align: justify;\"> between the shoulder blades and </span><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">5 downward thrusts on the </strong><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">chest</strong><span style=\"text-align: justify;\"> are given in order to dislodge the foreign body.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0244","text":"Identify the device shown ‌in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the device shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Automated External Desynch‌onizer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Automated External Defibri‌llator","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Automated Electronic Defib‌rillator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Automated External decompr‌essor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Automated External Defibrillator","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a882d8d959804b8da3895e3893fae64b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Automated External Defibrillator</strong></p><p>The device is an <strong>Automated External Defibrillator (AED).</strong></p>\n<p>This is a special medical device that is commonly used for recognizing the absence/presence of <strong>rapid ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation.</strong> With no need for intervention by an operator, it determines whether an electrical shock right to the heart must be performed (defibrillation).</p>\n<p>An automated external defibrillator can be used on those victims, who are unconscious and aren’t breathing in a normal way. As a rule, it is used together with CPR in cases of sudden cardiac arrest. It is forbidden to use it on patients who are less than eight years old or those weighing less than 25 kgs.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2591","text":"In a cardiac arrest patien‌t, if intravenous access ‍cannot be obtained, what ‍is the next best way to a​dminister epinephrine?","raw_text":"In a cardiac arrest patient, if intravenous access cannot be obtained, what is the next best way to administer epinephrine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endotrach‌eal route","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intram‌uscular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intra‌osseus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wait till intravenous acce‌ss is made","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intraosseus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intraosseus</strong></p><p>American Heart Association recommends the <strong>intraosseous</strong> <strong>(IO)</strong> <strong>route</strong> as an alternative to an intravenous route in order to deliver drugs and fluids, especially in children.</p>\n<p>This is because<strong> intraosseous access </strong>can easily be made in<strong> 30-60 seconds </strong>and thus provide <strong>emergency vascular access</strong>. It involves placing a catheter into the marrow venous plexus and administering, crystalloids, colloids, drugs, and blood which leads to rapid and reliable absorption into the systemic circulation.</p>\n<p>Sites of choice: proximal tibia, distal femur, the distal end of radius, or ulna.</p>\n<p>The fluids are administered under pressure because of the resistance to fluid flow from the IO catheter into the IO space. The <strong>disadvantage</strong> with this route is the risk of <strong>osteomyelitis, compartment syndrome,</strong> bone marrow or <strong>fat embolism.</strong></p>\n<p>IO access is superior to the endotracheal route. The American Heart Association does not recommend an intramuscular route for cardiac resuscitation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2592","text":"An unconscious 4-year-old ‌child is brought to the E‍R. On examination, she is‍ gasping but her pulse is​ not recordable. You star‌t chest compressions and ​rescue breaths. As the ch‌ild is not responding you‍ want to start IV epineph​rine, but you cannot obta‌in IV access. Which of th‌e following sites is pref​erred for intraosseous ac‍cess in this situation?","raw_text":"An unconscious 4-year-old child is brought to the ER. On examination, she is gasping but her pulse is not recordable. You start chest compressions and rescue breaths. As the child is not responding you want to start IV epinephrine, but you cannot obtain IV access.  Which of the following sites is preferred for intraosseous access in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proxima‌l Tibia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ste‌rnum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iliac‌ crest","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proxima‌l femur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal Tibia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal Tibia</strong></p><p>The site preferred for intraosseous access among the given options is <strong>proximal tibia. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Other</strong> sites of choice are <strong>distal femur, distal end of radius, or ulna</strong>.</p>\n<p>Intraosseous access involves placing a catheter into the marrow venous plexus and administering, crystalloids, colloids, drugs, and blood which leads to rapid and reliable absorption into the systemic circulation. </p>\n<p>The fluids are administered under pressure because of the resistance to fluid flow from the IO catheter into the IO space. The disadvantage with this route is the risk of osteomyelitis, compartment syndrome, bone marrow or fat embolism.</p>\n<p>American Heart Association recommends the intraosseous route as an alternative to an intravenous route in order to deliver drugs and fluids, especially in children. This is because intraosseous access can easily be made in 30-60 seconds and thus provide <strong>emergency</strong> <strong>vascular</strong> <strong>access</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5884","text":"According to the AHA 2020 ‌guidelines for the Pediat‍ric Basic and Advanced Li‍fe Support, which of the ​following statements is i‌ncorrect?","raw_text":"According to the AHA 2020 guidelines for the Pediatric Basic and Advanced Life Support, which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer the initial dos‌e of epinephrine within 5‍ minutes from the start o‍f chest compressions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cuffed endotracheal tubes ‌are preferred for intubat‍ing infants and children","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Routine use of cricoid pre‌ssure is recommended duri‍ng endotracheal intubatio‍n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"In infants the respiratory‌ rate should be 20-30 bre‍aths/minute","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Routine use of cricoid pressure is recommended during endotracheal intubation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Routine use of cricoid pressure is recommended during endotracheal intubation</strong></p><p>According to the AHA 2020 guidelines, <strong>routine use of cricoid pressure</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong> during the endotracheal intubation of <strong>pediatric</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>Option A: For pediatric patients in any setting, it is reasonable to administer the initial dose of <strong>epinephrine</strong> <strong>within 5 minutes</strong> from the start of chest compressions.</p>\n<p>Option B: It is reasonable to <strong>prefer cuffed ETTs</strong> over uncuffed ETTs for intubating infants and children to reduce air leak.</p>\n<p>Option D: For infants and children with a pulse but an absent or inadequate respiratory effort, 1 breath can be given every 2 to 3 seconds (<strong>20-30 breaths/min</strong>).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2589","text":"A 6-year-old girl is admit‌ted to the pediatric ward‍ for severe pneumonia. Sh‍e suddenly becomes unresp​onsive. On examination, h‌er pulse is not detectabl​e. You immediately start ‌cardiopulmonary resuscita‍tion. What is the ratio o​f chest compressions to v‌entilation to be provided‌ if there are 2 rescuers?​","raw_text":"A 6-year-old girl is admitted to the pediatric ward for severe pneumonia. She suddenly becomes unresponsive. On examination, her pulse is not detectable. You immediately start cardiopulmonary resuscitation. What is the ratio of chest compressions to ventilation to be provided if there are 2 rescuers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15‌:2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"30‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30‌:2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 15:2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 15:2</strong></p><p>During cardiopulmonary resuscitation of<strong> infants and children, </strong>the ratio of chest compression to rescue breaths is 30:2 for a single rescuer and<strong> 15:2 </strong>if<strong> 2 or more rescuers </strong>are present.</p>\n<p>In adults, the ratio is 30:2 irrespective of the number of rescuers.</p>\n<p>In infants and children, the depth of compression should be at least one-third of the anteroposterior diameter of the chest which is about 5 cm in children and 4 cm in infants.</p>\n<p>The compression rate should be 100-120/minute.</p>\n<p>Allow complete chest recoil after each compression and minimize interruptions in compressions (10 seconds or less). Rotate between compressors every 2 minutes or earlier if fatigued.</p>\n<p>Excessive ventilation is to be avoided.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2595","text":"An unresponsive child is b‌rought to the ER. His BP ‍is not recordable, pulse ‍rate is 40 per minute. CP​R is initiated but his Sp‌O2 is 70% despite proper ​oxygenation and ventilati‌on, and pulse rate is not‍ improving. What is the n​ext best step of manageme‌nt?","raw_text":"An unresponsive child is brought to the ER. His BP is not recordable, pulse rate is 40 per minute. CPR is initiated but his SpO2 is 70% despite proper oxygenation and ventilation, and pulse rate is not improving. What is the next best step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV at‌ropine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV epin‌ephrine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IV do‌pamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IV ade‌nosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IV epinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IV epinephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Epinephrine</strong> is the<strong> drug of choice </strong>for treating <strong>bradycardia in infants and children.</strong> The recommended dose is <strong>0.01 mg/ kg of a 1:10,000 solution</strong>. It can be given intravenously or via the intraosseous route. It can be repeated every 3-5 minutes.</p>\n<p>If intravenous or intraosseous access cannot be obtained, then it can be administered via the endotracheal route at a dose of 0.1 mg/kg of a 1:1,000 solution.</p>\n<p>If epinephrine is not effective in increasing the heart rate, then atropine can be used with a dose of 0.02 mg/kg and repeated.</p>\n<p><strong>For adults, treatment is initiated with 1 mg atropine iv</strong> which may be repeated at 3-5 minute intervals to a <strong>maximum</strong> dose of <strong>3 mg</strong>. Along with atropine, transcutaneous pacing is to be done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2606","text":"A 5-year-old child is brou‌ght to the casualty after‍ having suddenly collapse‍d while playing. Pulse is​ not recordable and CPR i‌s started. After 2 minute​s of CPR, the monitor sho‌ws ventricular fibrillati‍on rhythm. What is the en​ergy to be set for defibr‌illation?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child is brought to the casualty after having suddenly collapsed while playing. Pulse is not recordable and CPR is started. After 2 minutes of CPR, the monitor shows ventricular fibrillation rhythm. What is the energy to be set for defibrillation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 J‌/kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 J‌/kg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 J‌/kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6J ‌/kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 J/kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 J/kg</strong></p><p>In a <strong>pediatric cardiac arrest</strong>, the initial energy for defibrillation is set at <strong>2 J/kg</strong> for both monophasic and biphasic waveforms.<strong> </strong>It is increased to 4 J/kg if a second shock is required<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>If a pediatric defibrillator is not available, then an adult defibrillator can be used.</p>\n<p>In children the <strong>most common cause of cardiac arrest is asphyxia. </strong>However, a sudden witnessed collapse in a child is usually due to ventricular fibrillation.</p>\n<p>For adult defibrillation, the energy that must be applied is</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monophasic defibrillator– 360 J</li>\n<li>Biphasic defibrillator- 120-200 J (manufacturer recommended)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5883","text":"You are providing basic re‌suscitative care to a new‍born. After the initial m‍anagement of airway, oxyg​enation, and IV access, y‌ou perform a 12 lead ECG ​as the heart rate is 190/‌minute. What is the cut-o‍ff value to determine inc​reased QRS duration accor‌ding to the AHA 2020 Guid‌elines for Pediatric Mana​gement of tachycardia wit‍h a pulse?","raw_text":"You are providing basic resuscitative care to a newborn. After the initial management of airway, oxygenation, and IV access, you perform a 12 lead ECG as the heart rate is 190/minute. What is the cut-off value to determine increased QRS duration according to the AHA 2020 Guidelines for Pediatric Management of tachycardia with a pulse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;0.‌11 sec","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;0.‌10 sec","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;0.‌09 sec","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;0.‌12 sec","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. >0.09 sec","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt;0.09 sec</strong></p><p>The cut off value for increased QRS duration according to the AHA 2020 guidelines for pediatric management of tachycardia with a pulse is <strong>&gt;0.09 second</strong>.<strong> </strong><strong>Wide QRS duration</strong> of <strong>&gt;0.09 </strong>second is suggestive of possible<strong> ventricular tachycardia</strong>.</p>\n<p>When a pediatric patient is in tachycardia, a <strong>12-lead ECG</strong> is done to evaluate the <strong>rhythm</strong> and <strong>QRS complex duration</strong>. In addition, <strong>cardiopulmonary</strong> <strong>compromise</strong> is also assessed by checking for acutely altered mental status, signs of shock, and hypotension. </p>\n<p>Based on the QRS complex duration on the ECG, it can be: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Narrow</strong> (≤0.09 seconds) indicating a probable supraventricular tachycardia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Wide</strong> (&gt;0.09 seconds) indicating a possible ventricular tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of patient with <strong>probable supraventricular tachycardia</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of cardiopulmonary compromise - adenosine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absence of cardiopulmonary compromise - vagal maneuvers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of patient with <strong>possible ventricular tachycardia</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of cardiopulmonary compromise - synchronized cardioversion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absence of cardiopulmonary compromise - adenosine, if the the rhythm is regular and QRS is monomorphic</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"q7OnZfbW","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"9_nervous_system_and_endocrine_glands","title":"9.Nervous System And Endocrine Glands","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB1606","text":"What is the spinal cord de​veloped from?","raw_text":"What is the spinal cord developed from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neura​l tube","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Noto​chord","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primitiv​e streak","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhomben​cephalon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neural tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neural tube</strong></p><p>The<strong> spinal cord</strong> develops from the <strong>caudal part</strong> of the <strong>neural tube</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nervous system develops from the <strong>neurectoderm</strong> i.e. the specialized ectoderm overlying the notochord. The neuroectoderm initially thickens to form the <strong>neural plate</strong>. The neural plate is then converted to a <strong>neural groove</strong>, followed by a <strong>neural tube</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The enlarged <strong>cranial</strong> <strong>part</strong> of neural tube forms the <strong>brain</strong>. It has 3 primary vesicles that form:\n<ul>\n<li>Forebrain (prosencephalon)</li>\n<li>Midbrain (mesencephalon)</li>\n<li>Hindbrain (rhombencephalon)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The narrow <strong>caudal</strong> part of the neural tube forms the <strong>spinal cord</strong>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1603","text":"The neuropore facilitates ​communication between the‍ neural tube and ______","raw_text":"The neuropore facilitates communication between the neural tube and ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amnioti​c cavity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yolk​ sac","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allantoi​c cavity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ute​rus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amniotic cavity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a14522485a441899fdcc2b52c7210dax1280x1415.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amniotic cavity</strong></p><p>The <strong>neuropores</strong> facilitate communication between the <strong>neural tube</strong> and the <strong>amniotic cavity</strong>.</p>\n<p>The neural plate first becomes tubular in the middle. The <strong>cranial</strong> and <strong>caudal ends</strong> of the neural tube remain <strong>open</strong> and are called the <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>neuropores</strong>, respectively.</p>\n<p>The neuropores facilitate communication between the <strong>neural tube</strong> and the <strong>amniotic cavity</strong>. <strong>Amniotic fluid</strong> provides <strong>nutrition</strong> to the developing <strong>neuroectodermal</strong> <strong>cells</strong> before the circulatory system is established. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1605","text":"What is the adult represen​tation of the location of‍ anterior neuropore?","raw_text":"What is the adult representation of the location of anterior neuropore?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septum tr​ansversum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Terminal ​ventricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamina t​erminalis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Foramen ​of Munro","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamina terminalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamina terminalis</strong></p><p>The <strong>location</strong> of the <strong>anterior neuropore</strong> is represented in adults as <strong>lamina terminalis</strong>. </p>\n<p>The anterior neuropore <strong>closes</strong> around the <strong>25th day</strong> of intrauterine life. Its location in adults is represented by the lamina terminalis. The <strong>lamina terminalis</strong> forms the <strong>anterior boundary</strong> of the <strong>third ventricle</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>posterior neuropore</strong> closes around the <strong>28th day</strong> of intrauterine life. Its location in adults is represented by the <strong>terminal ventricle</strong>. The terminal ventricle is present in the<strong> </strong>proximal 5 to 6 mm of filum terminale. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1619","text":"A 1-year-old baby with los​s of pharyngeal reflex wa‍s diagnosed to have infan​tile progressive bulbar p‌alsy. Which of the follow‍ing gives rise to the aff‍ected structure?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old baby with loss of pharyngeal reflex was diagnosed to have infantile progressive bulbar palsy. Which of the following gives rise to the affected structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prosenc​ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesenc​ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhomboc​ephalon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhombocephalon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhombocephalon</strong></p><p>In infantile progressive <strong>bulbar palsy</strong>, the affected structure is the <strong>medulla oblongata</strong>. It arises from the <strong>rhombocephalon </strong>(hindbrain). </p>\n<p>The <strong>rhombencephalon</strong> is further subdivided into a cranial part (<strong>metencephalon</strong>) and a caudal part (<strong>myelencephalon</strong>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metencephalon - forms the pons and cerebellum</li>\n<li>Myelencephalon - forms the medulla oblongata</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1635","text":"What is the flexure betwee​n the metencephalon and t‍he myelencephalon called?​","raw_text":"What is the flexure between the metencephalon and the myelencephalon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalic​ flexure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesencepha​lic flexure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pontine​ flexure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebella​r flexure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pontine flexure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9aae5a4c5eec4815b740d398764c3a8bx1280x1141.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pontine flexure</strong></p><p>The flexure between the <strong>metencephalon</strong> and the <strong>myelencephalon</strong> is called as the<strong> pontine flexure</strong>. </p>\n<p>The prosencephalon, mesencephalon, and rhombencephalon<strong> </strong>are initially arranged craniocaudally. Due to <strong>differential growth</strong> of different regions of the brain, various <strong>flexures</strong> form between them:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: A deep furrow called the <strong>rhombencephalic isthmus</strong> separates the mesencephalon from the metencephalon. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1638","text":"Which of the following sta​tements best describes th‍e sulcus limitans?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements best describes the sulcus limitans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is found in the interpe​duncular fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is located between the ​alar and basal plates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It separates the medulla f​rom the pons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It separates the hypothala​mus from the thalamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is located between the alar and basal plates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0d1f06554ba42df83f21bb35b1070cbx1280x1274.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80c99b9da1ad48f8b7aadb8672cce28fx1280x1158.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is located between the alar and basal plates</strong></p><p>The <strong>sulcus limitans</strong> is located between the <strong>sensory alar</strong> and the <strong>motor basal plates</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The cells in the wall of the<strong> neural tube</strong> proliferate and are subdivided into the <strong>inner mantle layer</strong> and <strong>outer marginal layer</strong>. Due to the fast growth of the mantle layer cells, the lumen of the neural tube gets compressed.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The line<strong> separating</strong> the compressed <strong>ventral part</strong> from the <strong>dorsal part</strong> is called the <strong>sulcus limitans</strong>. When the sulcus limitans is formed, the tube is divided into the<strong> dorsal or alar lamina</strong> and <strong>ventral or basal lamina</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In the <strong>spinal cord</strong>, the ventral thickenings or <strong>basal plates</strong> contain <strong>ventral motor horn cells. </strong>The dorsal thickenings i.e. the <strong>alar plates</strong> contain the <strong>interneurones</strong> that receive the terminals of primary sensory neurons.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The images given below show the sulcus limitans:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1639","text":"Which of the following bra​instem nuclei is not deri‍ved from the alar plate?","raw_text":"Which of the following brainstem nuclei is not derived from the alar plate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior​ olivary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Substant​ia nigra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Den​tate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypog​lossal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypoglossal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypoglossal</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoglossal nucleus</strong> develops from the <strong>basal plate </strong>of<strong> medulla,</strong> and not alar plate.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Inferior olivary nucleus</strong> develops from the <strong>alar plate</strong> of the <strong>medulla</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Substantia nigra</strong> develops from the <strong>alar plate </strong>of the <strong>midbrain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Dentate nucleus </strong>develops from the <strong>alar plate</strong> in the <strong>cerebellum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1641","text":"Which of the following is ​the first commissure to d‍evelop?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first commissure to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For​nix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ​commissure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Corpus ​callosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ​commissure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior commissure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f7e57e18c234792ae4b5f7b286d793ex1280x1108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior commissure</strong></p><p>The <strong>first commissure</strong> to develop is the<strong> anterior commissure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Commissures are fibre bundles that cross the midline and connect the right and left brain hemispheres. Many important fiber bundles cross at lamina terminalis. </p>\n<p>Commissures at <strong>lamina terminalis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior commissure -</strong> first commissure to appear. These fibers connect the olfactory bulb and related brain areas to the corresponding structures in the contralateral hemisphere. </li>\n<li><strong>Hippocampal commissure </strong>(fornix commissure)<strong> -</strong> develops second. The fibers emerge from the hippocampus and reach the lamina terminalis. From here, they arch outside the choroid fissure to the mamillary body and the hypothalamus.</li>\n<li><strong>Corpus callosum -</strong> appears by the 10th week. It connects the non-olfactory areas of the right and the left cerebral cortices. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Commissures at other areas are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior commissure</li>\n<li>Habenular commissure</li>\n<li>Optic chiasma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the anterior commissure:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1640","text":"Where does the structure m​arked as A develop from?","raw_text":"Where does the structure marked as A develop from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal ​lamina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alar ​lamina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamina t​erminalis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Basal​ plate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamina terminalis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e28264a2448b4e73b8a07db11c5236f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamina terminalis</strong></p><p>The structure marked as A in the image is the <strong>corpus callosum. </strong>It develops from the <strong>lamina terminalis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lamina terminalis is a part of the wall of the neural tube that closes the cranial end of the prosencephalon. The lamina terminalis becomes thickened to form the <strong>commissural plate</strong> that facilitates the passage of neurons between the two hemispheres. </p>\n<p>There are <strong>3 commissures</strong> that <strong>pass</strong> through <strong>lamina terminalis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior commissure</li>\n<li>Hippocampal commissure</li>\n<li>Corpus callosum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> corpus callosum </strong>is the <strong>largest </strong>of these commisures. Initially, corpus callosum forms a small bundle in the lamina terminalis. Due to the continuous expansion, it extends anteriorly and posteriorly arching over the thin roof of the diencephalon. It connects the non-olfactory areas of the right and the left cerebral cortices. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1642","text":"The septum pellucidum exte​nds between:","raw_text":"The septum pellucidum extends between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior commissure and po​sterior commissure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior commissure and co​rpus callosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior commissure and th​e fornix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fornix and corpus callosum​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fornix and corpus callosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94fd9db968d7411b9e271d78a6797ea9x1280x1108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fornix and corpus callosum</strong></p><p><strong>Septum pellucidum</strong> extends <strong>between</strong> the <strong>corpus callosum</strong> and the <strong>fornix</strong>. </p>\n<p>The septum pellucidum is a membrane <strong>separating</strong> the <strong>anterior horns</strong> of the left and right <strong>lateral ventricles</strong> of the brain. It extends from the corpus callosum to the fornix.</p>\n<p>It develops from the <strong>lamina terminalis</strong>. The part of the lamina terminalis that stretches between corpus callosum and fornix persists as septum pellucidum.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the septum pellucidum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1645","text":"Which of the following neu​ral tube defects is chara‍cterized by gaps in the v​ertebral arches?","raw_text":"Which of the following neural tube defects is characterized by gaps in the vertebral arches?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enceph​alocele","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spina ​bifida","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydran​cephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syring​omyelia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spina bifida","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f1d633e3d8c45bc90128ad767c1f84ex1280x1116.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3123332690aa49bca7047448a2e02dcfx1280x2049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70d894f7f3b74574920292f6e774ef5ex1280x1580.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spina bifida</strong></p><p>Spina bifida is characterized by gaps in the vertebral arches.</p>\n<p><strong>Spina bifida</strong> is a general term used for <strong>neural tube defects</strong> affecting the spinal region and associated with <strong>splitting</strong> of the <strong>vertebral arches</strong>. It may or may not involve the underlying neural tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>There are <strong>3 types</strong> of spina bifida, as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Rachischisis</strong> is the most <strong>severe type</strong> of neural tube defect, where the <strong>entire neural tube </strong>fails to close. Rachischisis can occur in the spinal cord or brain regions of the neural tube. The exposed neural tissues often become necrotic. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1643","text":"Identify the neural tube d​efect shown in the image.‍","raw_text":"Identify the neural tube defect shown in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spina ​bifida","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rachis​chisis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enceph​alocele","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myel​ocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Encephalocele","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/15b2425a64834b4a9d45a373bf50bf4c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Encephalocele</strong></p><p>The image shows a newborn with <strong>encephalocele</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Encephaloceles</strong> are caused due to <strong>defects</strong> in the <strong>skull</strong> through which the <strong>meninges</strong> and/or <strong>neural tissue herniate</strong>. The <strong>s</strong>quamous part of the occipital bone is the most frequently affected bone. The bone may be partially or totally lacking.</p>\n<p>Based on the degree of defect and tissues herniating, they are classified as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meningocele -</strong> Defect is small and <strong>only meninges</strong> bulge through it</li>\n<li><strong>Meningoencephalocele -</strong> Defect is large and a <strong>part of brain</strong> bulges with the <strong>meninges</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Meningohydroencephalocele -</strong> Defect is large and a <strong>part of the brain</strong> and <strong>ventricle</strong> bulge with the <strong>meninges</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0544","text":"Identify the defect shown ​in the image.","raw_text":"Identify the defect shown in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enceph​alocele","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spina ​bifida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anenc​ephaly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meningoenc​ephalocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anencephaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f6b6bde4c094b9eb85aad84da263646.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anencephaly</strong></p><p>The image shows the congenital defect of <strong>anencephaly.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> of the <strong>cranial end</strong> of the <strong>neural tube </strong>to<strong> close</strong> is known as <strong>exencephaly</strong>. As a result of this, the skull vault does not form. This leaves the malformed <strong>brain exposed</strong>.</p>\n<p>The exposed brain tissue degenerates, leaving a mass of <strong>necrotic tissue</strong>. This defect with the absence of a major portion of the brain, skull, and scalp is known as <strong>anencephaly</strong>. The brain stem remains intact.</p>\n<p>In some cases, the closure defect of the neural tube extends caudally, and the spinal cord is affected. This condition is known as <strong>craniorachischisis</strong>, where there is <strong>anencephaly</strong> plus a <strong>large defect </strong>involving the <strong>spine</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1644","text":"A neonate was shifted to t​he NICU due to respirator‍y distress and periodic s​eizures. CT scan revealed‌ cystic dilatation of the‍ fourth ventricle due to ‍blockage of the median an‌d lateral apertures. What‌ is the likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A neonate was shifted to the NICU due to respiratory distress and periodic seizures. CT scan revealed cystic dilatation of the fourth ventricle due to blockage of the median and lateral apertures. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syring​omyelia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rachis​chisis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dandy-Walk​er syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arnold-Chiari malformation​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dandy-Walker syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0461338c95d0456fa0a34774d3fcc5b1x600x629.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dandy-Walker syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Blockage</strong> of the medial and the lateral apertures of the <strong>4th ventricle</strong> is seen in <strong>Dandy-Walker syndrome </strong>(DWS).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">DWS is <strong>congenital hydrocephalus</strong> associated with <strong>atresia</strong> of the <strong>median aperture</strong> (foramen of Magendie) and <strong>lateral aperture</strong> (foramen of Luschka) of the <strong>4th ventricle</strong>. This causes <strong>enlargement</strong> of the 4th ventricle and <strong>hydrocephalus</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The other associated defects seen in DWS include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Agenesis of the cerebellar vermis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Agenesis of the splenium of corpus callosum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the normal 4th ventricle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1646","text":"Which of the following giv​es rise to the hypophysis‍ cerebri?","raw_text":"Which of the following gives rise to the hypophysis cerebri?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface ectoderm and mesod​erm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surface ectoderm and neuro​ectoderm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neuroectoderm and mesoderm​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesoderm a​nd endoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surface ectoderm and neuroectoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/766a7cbc5acc41e089b50665c10f3a73x600x974.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surface ectoderm and neuroectoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>hypophysis cerebri</strong> or <strong>pituitary gland</strong> develops from two sources i.e. <strong>surface ectoderm</strong> and <strong>neurectoderm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>adenohypophysis</strong> or anterior pituitary, develops from the <strong>surface ectoderm</strong>. An ectodermal diverticulum, called <strong>Rathke's pouch, </strong>grows upward from the roof of the stomatodeum (primitive mouth). This becomes the <strong>anterior lobe </strong>of the pituitary (<strong>pars distalis</strong>). </p>\n<p>The <strong>neurohypophysis </strong>or posterior pituitary, develops from the <strong>neuroectoderm</strong>. A downward extension of the diencephalon, called <strong>infundibular process,</strong> grows down from the floor of the 3rd ventricle, during the 6th week. It gives rise to the <strong>stalk</strong> and the <strong>posterior lobe </strong>of pituitary (<strong>pars nervosa</strong>). </p>\n<p>The <strong>anterior lobe</strong> of pituitary gives rise to a <strong>small extension</strong> called <strong>the pars tuber</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>alis.</strong> It </span>grows along the stalk of the infundibu<span class=\"s2\">lum </span>and eventually surrounds it.</p>\n<p>The <strong>posterior wall</strong> of the <strong>Rathke pouch</strong> develops into the <strong>pars intermedia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the development of pituitary gland.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1648","text":"A 65-year-old male patient​ underwent an MRI scan fo‍r the evaluation of strok​e. Incidentally, it was f‌ound that the pineal glan‍d was absent. Defect in t‍he development of which o‌f the following structure‌s will​ likely lead to pi​neal agenesis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old male patient underwent an MRI scan for the evaluation of stroke. Incidentally, it was found that the pineal gland was absent. Defect in the development of which of the following structures will​ likely lead to pineal agenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Telenc​ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dience​phalon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metenc​ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelenc​ephalon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diencephalon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7649aa83ccfe49688786d11cc429cfc5x1280x1108.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4cd36a7a45f4d3782c25cf855824384x1280x1108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diencephalon</strong></p><p>The <strong>pineal gland</strong> develops from the <strong>diencephalon. </strong>Hence, defect in its development can cause pineal agenesis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The most <strong>caudal part</strong> of the <strong>roof plate</strong> of the <strong>diencephalon</strong> develops into the <strong>pineal gland</strong>. It is also known as the <strong>pineal body</strong> or <strong>epiphysis cerebri</strong>. The pineal body initially appears as an epithelial thickening in the midline. It begins to evaginate by the 7th week and becomes a solid organ.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The images given below show the pineal gland:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1650","text":"Where are the cells of the​ adrenal cortex and medul‍la respectively derived f​rom?","raw_text":"Where are the cells of the adrenal cortex and medulla respectively derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectoderm and neural crest ​cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesoderm a​nd endoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoderm and neural crest ​cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesoderm and neural crest ​cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mesoderm and neural crest cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mesoderm and neural crest cells</strong></p><p>The cells of the adrenal cortex and medulla are respectively derived from the <strong>mesoderm</strong> and <strong>neural crest cells</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>adrenal glands</strong> consist of a superficial cortex and deeper medulla. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adrenal<strong> cortex</strong> - formed by <strong>proliferation</strong> of the <strong>coelomic epithelium </strong>(mesodermal origin)</li>\n<li>Adrenal<strong> medulla</strong> - formed by the invading <strong>neural crest cells</strong> from somite levels 18-24</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1651","text":"Which of the following zon​es of the adrenal cortex ‍are present at birth?","raw_text":"Which of the following zones of the adrenal cortex are present at birth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zona fasciculata and Zona ​reticularis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zona glomerulosa and Zona ​fasciculata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zona glomerulosa and Zona ​reticularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zona glomerulosa, Zona fas​ciculata, and Zona reticu‍laris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zona glomerulosa and Zona fasciculata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zona glomerulosa and Zona fasciculata</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>zona glomerulosa</strong> and <strong>zona fasciculata</strong> of the adult cortex is present at birth. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The differentiation of the cortical zones of the adrenal gland begins during the <strong>late fetal period</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Zona glomerulosa</strong> and <strong>zona fasciculata</strong> of the adult cortex are present <strong>at birth</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Zona reticularis</strong> is formed during the <strong>3rd year of life</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BQnNj3Q1","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"9_amino_acid__metabolic_disorder","title":"9.Amino Acid  Metabolic Disorder","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MC5241","text":"An infant is brought with ‌a history of vomiting and​ poor feeding. A musty od‌or is noted in the baby. ‌Guthrie test was done and‌ it was found to be posit​ive. All are true regardi​ng this disease except","raw_text":"An infant is brought with a history of vomiting and poor feeding. A musty odor is noted in the baby. Guthrie test was done and it was found to be positive. All are true regarding this disease except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase ‌enzyme defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"White patch of hair due to‌ tryptophan deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylacetate positive in ‌urine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mental retardation is pres‌ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. White patch of hair due to tryptophan deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. White patch of hair due to tryptophan deficiency</strong></p><p>The given scenario of an infant with a history of vomiting and poor feeding with a musty odour is<strong> </strong>suggestive<strong> </strong>of<strong> phenylketonuria (PKU). Tryptophan deficiency</strong> is not a feature of this disorder. <span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">The deficiency of the enzyme<strong> phenylalanine hydroxylase</strong> causes PKU</span>. </p>\n<p>Characteristic clinical findings in Phenylketonuria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mental retardation</li>\n<li><strong>Failure to walk or talk</strong></li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Hyperactivity</li>\n<li>Tremor</li>\n<li><strong>Microcephaly</strong></li>\n<li>Failure to grow</li>\n<li><strong>Deficiency of pigmentation (fair hair, light skin color, and blue eyes)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The Guthrie bacterial inhibition</strong> test is a screening test for phenylketonuria. This test uses a phenylalanine requiring strain of <strong><em>Bacillus subtilis.</em></strong><em> </em>The <strong>ferric chloride</strong> test is also used as a screening test for PKU. <strong>Tandem mass</strong> <strong>spectrometry</strong> is the gold standard investigation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8445","text":"A 3-month-old infant born ‌to consanguineous parents​ presented with global de‌velopmental delay and sei‌zures. Plasma amino acid ‌analysis revealed elevate​d phenylalanine levels. A​ urine FeCl3 test done wa‌s positive. What is detec‌ted in this test?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old infant born to consanguineous parents presented with global developmental delay and seizures. Plasma amino acid analysis revealed elevated phenylalanine levels. A urine FeCl3 test done was positive. What is detected in this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenyl‌acetate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenyl‌lactate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylp‌yruvate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl‌alanine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenylpyruvate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenylpyruvate</strong></p><p>The infant with <strong>developmental delays</strong> and <strong>seizures</strong> with elevated <strong>phenylalanine</strong> levels is most likely suffering from <strong>phenylketonuria</strong>. The <strong>FeCl<sub>3</sub> </strong>test is used to detect<strong> urinary phenylpyruvate</strong>. </p>\n<p>In this test, a test tube with 1 ml of fresh urine, 3-4 drops of 10gm% ferric chloride is added. A <strong>positive</strong> test reveals a <strong>transient green color </strong>due to the formation of a ferric phenol complex.  Phenylpyruvate, phenyl lactate, phenylacetate and phenyl acetyl glutamate are products of alternative pathways of phenylalanine catabolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Specificity of FeCl<sub>3 </sub>test:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 260px; width: 248px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Reacts with FeCl<sub>3</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"><strong>Does not react with FeCl<sub>3</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Phenylpyruvate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">  Phenylacetate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">  Phenyllactate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"> Phenyl acetyl glutamate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">  Phenylalanine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"> Pyruvic acid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"> Tyrosine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8451","text":"Defective fumarylacetoacet‌ate hydrolase enzyme is a​ssociated with _____.","raw_text":"Defective fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase enzyme is associated with _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 Ty‌rosinemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 Ty‌rosinemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3 Ty‌rosinemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 4 Ty‌rosinemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 1 Tyrosinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 1 Tyrosinemia</strong></p><p>Defective<strong> fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase</strong> enzyme is associated with <strong>type I tyrosinemia (tyrosinosis)</strong>. It is the most common type of tyrosinemia. </p>\n<p>There are three types of <strong>tyrosinemias</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1 (Hepatorenal tyrosinemia): Fumaryl acetoacetate hydrolase deficiency</li>\n<li>Type 2 (Oculocutaneous tyrosinemia/Richner-Hanhart syndrome): Tyrosine transaminase deficiency</li>\n<li>Type 3 (Neonatal tyrosinemia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3502","text":"An infant is brought with ‌abdominal distention and ​increased irritability fo‌r 10 days. Physical exami‌nation showed jaundice, h‌epatomegaly, and a boiled​ cabbage odor. Investigat​ions showed elevated tran‌saminases and succinylace‌tone. What is the treatme‌nt of choice for this con‍dition?","raw_text":"An infant is brought with abdominal distention and increased irritability for 10 days. Physical examination showed jaundice, hepatomegaly, and a boiled cabbage odor. Investigations showed elevated transaminases and succinylacetone. What is the treatment of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dietary restriction of phe‌nylalanine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dietary restriction of tyr‌osine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitisino‌ne (NTBC)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a child with abdominal distension, jaundice, hepatomegaly, and a boiled cabbage odor is suggestive of <strong>type 1 tyrosinemia</strong> (tyrosinosis). It is treated by a combination of<strong> nitisinone</strong> and <strong>dietary restriction</strong> of <strong>phenylalanine</strong> and <strong>tyrosine.</strong></p>\n<p>Type 1 tyrosinemia is the most common form of tyrosinemia caused by <strong>defective fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase</strong> enzyme. They usually present between 2-6 months of age with <strong>acute liver failure</strong>. They may also present with <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong> resembling acute porphyria. <strong>Renal involvement</strong> is manifested as a Fanconi-like syndrome with hyperphosphaturia, hypophosphatemia, and vitamin D-resistant rickets.</p>\n<p>An odor resembling <strong>boiled cabbage</strong> resulting from increased methionine metabolites can be noted. <strong>Elevated levels of succinylacetone</strong> in blood or urine is diagnostic of type 1 tyrosinemia.</p>\n<p>Note: A boiled cabbage odor can also occur in hypermethioninemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8449","text":"Richner-Hanhart syndrome i‌s associated with a defec​t in which of the followi‌ng?","raw_text":"Richner-Hanhart syndrome is associated with a defect in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fumarylacetoacetate hydrol‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine ami‌notransferase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"p-Hydroxyphenylpyruvate hy‌droxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homogentis‌ate oxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tyrosine aminotransferase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tyrosine aminotransferase</strong></p><p><strong>Richner- Hanhart syndrome</strong> <strong>(Tyrosinemia type II)</strong> is associated with a defect in <strong>tyrosine aminotransferase.</strong> It is also called oculocutaneous tyrosinemia. Alternate metabolites of tyrosine are excreted in type II Tyrosinemia.</p>\n<p>It is an autosomal recessive condition with onset between ages 2 and 4 years when painful circumscribed calluses develop on the pressure points of the palms and soles.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8417","text":"Neonatal tyrosinemia is du‌e to the deficiency of wh​ich of the following enzy‌mes?","raw_text":"Neonatal tyrosinemia is due to the deficiency of which of the following enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fumaryl acetoacetate hydro‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine t‌ransaminase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyros‌inase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate di‌oxygenase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase</strong></p><p>Neonatal tyrosinemia is due to the deficiency of <strong>4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase</strong></p>\n<p>There are three types of <strong>tyrosinemias</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1 (Hepatorenal tyrosinemia): Fumaryl acetoacetate hydrolase deficiency</li>\n<li>Type 2 (oculocutaneous tyrosinemia/Richner-hanhart syndrome): Tyrosine transaminase deficiency</li>\n<li>Type 3 <strong>(Neonatal tyrosinemia)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8426","text":"Which of the following enz‌yme deficiency most commo​nly causes the disease sh‌own in the image below:","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme deficiency most commonly causes the disease shown in the image below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyros‌inase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine h‌ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylalanin‌e hydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyrosine t‌ransaminase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tyrosinase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da6c03f78b2e4b6ea352451cefc23b25.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tyrosinase</strong></p><p>The image shows a child with <strong>albinism</strong> and is due to a <strong>deficiency of</strong> copper-containing enzyme <strong>tyrosinase</strong> causing a <strong>defect in melanin production.</strong> The affected child has milky white hair and milky white skin.</p>\n<p>In untreated phenylketonuria, the baby looks normal at birth, with a skin and hair color lighter than other members of the family. Later, the child has blonde hair, skin with a light complexion, and blue irides. Manifestations are not so severe as in OCA. The image shows an infant with white hair, so albinism is the best choice.</p>\n<p>Note - Tyrosine hydroxylase is the <strong>rate-limiting enzyme</strong> in the biosynthesis of catecholamines. Tyrosine is first hydroxylated to form 3,4-dihydroxyphenylalanine (DOPA) by the enzyme<strong> tyrosine hydroxylase. </strong>This enzyme is abundant in the central nervous system, the sympathetic ganglia, and the adrenal medulla.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8412","text":"A man complains of bluish-‌black discoloration of th​e sclera and pinna for th‌e last 6 months. Since ch‌ildhood, his urine has tu‌rned black on standing, a​s shown in the image belo​w. What is the most proba‌ble diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man complains of bluish-black discoloration of the sclera and pinna for the last 6 months. Since childhood, his urine has turned black on standing, as shown in the image below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylk‌etonuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alkapt‌onuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tyros‌inemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maple syrup ‌urine disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alkaptonuria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e1aa9c4257c445cca9b18ac7880f8b5b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4298c90589b42caa94eb67da6696ea0x1280x950.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alkaptonuria</strong></p><p>The given scenario of a patient with <strong>urine</strong> that turns black on standing and <strong>blackish discoloration</strong> of soft tissues (oochronosis) is characteristic of <strong>alkaptonuria.</strong> It occurs due to the excretion of <strong>homogentisic acid</strong> in the urine.</p>\n<p>Alkaptonuria is an autosomal recessive condition due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>homogentisate oxidase </strong>(also called<strong> </strong>homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase). The homogentisic acid in these patients is oxidized to benzoquinone acetate and polymerized to form <strong>black-colored alkapton bodies</strong>, which deposit in soft tissues like cartilage, and this phenomenon is called ochronosis.</p>\n<p>In the following images, A shows normal urine and B shows urine from patients with alkaptonuria</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8419","text":"The patient presents with ‌pigmentation of the face ​as shown in the image bel‌ow and gradually increasi‌ng backache and stiffness‌. The X-ray image of the ​spine is shown. What is t​he most probable diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"The patient presents with pigmentation of the face as shown in the image below and gradually increasing backache and stiffness. The X-ray image of the spine is shown. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylk‌etonuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alkapt‌onuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maple syrup ‌urine disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyros‌inemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alkaptonuria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afa097f6b2844848852fa6c7815e2562.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alkaptonuria</strong></p><p>The given clinical images show the accumulation of<strong> blackish discoloration </strong>on the face and the X-Ray shows <strong>calcification</strong> of the <strong>intervertebral discs</strong>. These findings are associated with<strong> alkaptonuria. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Alkaptonuria</strong> is a condition caused due to the deficiency of <strong>homogentisic acid oxidase.</strong> This results in the accumulation of homogentisic acid.</p>\n<p>This condition is associated with the following symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Black ochronotic pigmentation </strong>(Alkapton bodies) of cartilage and collagenous tissue</li>\n<li>Homogentisic aciduria (patient’s urine contains elevated levels of homogentisic acid, which is oxidized to a dark pigment on standing)</li>\n<li>Large joint arthritis</li>\n<li>Calcification of intervertebral discs known as as <strong>ochronotic spondyloarthropathy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8452","text":"A child is brought with co‌mplaints of diarrhea, ski​n eruptions, and delayed ‌mental development. She i‌s found to have neutral a‌minoaciduria and a positi​ve Obermeyer test. The im​paired renal and intestin‌al transport of which ami‌no acid must be suspected‌ in this child?","raw_text":"A child is brought with complaints of diarrhea, skin eruptions, and delayed mental development. She is found to have neutral aminoaciduria and a positive Obermeyer test. The impaired renal and intestinal transport of which amino acid must be suspected in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyro‌sine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypt‌ophan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenyl‌alanine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thre‌onine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tryptophan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tryptophan</strong></p><p>This child presents with diarrhea, dermatitis, and delayed mental development. This combined with <strong>neutral aminoaciduria</strong> and a positive <strong>Obermeyer</strong> test should raise suspicions of<strong> Hartnup's disease</strong>. This is due to impaired renal and intestinal absorption of<strong> tryptophan</strong> and other <strong>neutral amino acids</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hartnup's disease is characterized by a varied clinical presentation including ataxia (lack of muscle control), delayed mental development, diarrhea, and dermatitis. Patients can also develop symptoms similar to <strong>pellagra</strong> as <strong>tryptophan</strong> is a <strong>precursor</strong> of <strong>niacin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is based on aminoaciduria and increased excretion of indole compounds detected by <strong>Obermeyer test</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8398","text":"Xanthurenic aciduria is se‌en in which of the follow​ing vitamin deficiency?","raw_text":"Xanthurenic aciduria is seen in which of the following vitamin deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‌in B2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam‌in B3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam‌in B5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam‌in B6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitamin B6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitamin B6</strong></p><p><strong>Xanthurenic aciduria</strong> is seen in <strong>deficiency of vitamin B6 </strong>(pyridoxal phosphate), a coenzyme required by <strong>kynureninase enzyme</strong> to convert 3-OH kynurenine to 3-OH anthranilic acid during tryptophan metabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3652","text":"In which condition is exce‌ssive urinary excretion o​f 5- hydroxy indole aceti‌c acid seen?","raw_text":"In which condition is excessive urinary excretion of  5- hydroxy indole acetic acid seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alkapt‌onuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Albi‌nism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinoid‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylk‌etonuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carcinoid syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carcinoid syndrome</strong></p><p>Excessive urinary excretion of <strong>5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (5-HIAA)</strong> is seen in <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carcinoid syndrome is marked by symptoms of <strong>bronchospasm, cutaneous flushing,</strong> abdominal <strong>cramps,</strong> and diarrhea. It occurs due to <strong>excessive</strong> production of <strong>serotonin</strong>, a specialized <strong>product</strong> of <strong>tryptophan metabolism </strong>by tumors commonly in the small intestine, liver and lungs. It is then metabolized and excreted in urine as 5-HIAA. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3644","text":"A patient presents with re‌current kidney stones. Mi​croscopic examination fin‌ding of the urine specime‌n is shown below. Which o‌f the following is not se​en in the urine of this p​atient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with recurrent kidney stones. Microscopic examination finding of the urine specimen is shown below. Which of the following is not seen in the urine of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orni‌thine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lys‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argi‌nine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist‌idine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Histidine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/97ff020d81754547b5b454b82d711b6f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Histidine</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>hexagonal, translucent</strong> and <strong>white crystals</strong> suggestive of <strong>cystinuria. </strong><strong>Histidine</strong> is not seen in the urine of this patient. </p>\n<p><strong> Cystinuria</strong> is an <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> disease due to a defect in <strong>renal PCT</strong> &amp; intestinal amino acid transporter that is essential for reabsorptive transport of Cystine and the dibasic amino acids Ornithine, Lysine, and Arginine (COLA).</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of cystinuria is by the <strong>cyanide–nitroprusside</strong> test. Initial treatment is with adequate <strong>hydration, alkalization</strong> of the <strong>urine</strong> with <strong>citrate supplementation</strong> or <strong>acetazolamide,</strong> and <strong>dietary modification</strong> to <strong>reduce salt</strong> and <strong>protein intake.</strong> If this fails then patients are usually started on <strong>chelation</strong> therapy with <strong>penicillamine.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8400","text":"Defect in the metabolism o‌f which of the following ​is associated with increa‌sed cardiovascular risk?","raw_text":"Defect in the metabolism of which of the following is associated with increased cardiovascular risk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Branched a‌mino acids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aromatic a‌mino acids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfur-containing amino ac‌ids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Imino‌ acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sulfur-containing amino acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sulfur-containing amino acids</strong></p><p>Defect in the metabolism of <strong>sulfur</strong>-<strong>containing</strong> <strong>amino</strong> <strong>acids </strong>(<strong>homocysteine</strong>) due to the deficiency of <strong>cystathionine</strong><strong>-β-synthase </strong>leads to <strong>homocystinuria</strong> and <strong>increases cardiovascular risk.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Homocysteine</strong> is highly <strong>cytotoxic</strong> and elevated levels within the bloodstream are believed to <strong>damage</strong> the <strong>endothelial lining of arterial vessels, </strong>leading to intravascular coagulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3503","text":"A 4-year-old child develop‌ed a sudden onset weaknes​s of the left side of the‌ body with facial deviati‌on to the right side. On ‌examination, he is tall a​nd thin with elongated li​mbs. Ocular findings are ‌shown in the image below.‌ What is the most likely ‌inborn error of metabolis‍m in this child?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child developed a sudden onset weakness of the left side of the body with facial deviation to the right side. On examination, he is tall and thin with elongated limbs. Ocular findings are shown in the image below. What is the most likely inborn error of metabolism in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysti‌nuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homocys‌tinuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cysti‌nosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alkapt‌onuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Homocystinuria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/41dfce71b2494d5aa6c57844b468be11.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Homocystinuria</strong></p><p>This child presented with an acute stroke. This in addition to elongated limbs and a tall body habitus with ocular examination showing <strong>downward subluxation</strong> of the <strong>lens</strong> is suggestive of <strong>homocystinuria.</strong></p>\n<p>Homocystinuria is an important cause of <strong>pediatric stroke</strong>. It is most commonly caused by a <strong>defect</strong> in <strong>cystathionine β synthase enzyme</strong> leading to homocysteine accumulation in the urine and blood. They are generally normal at birth; the first sign usually is subluxation of the lens (ectopia lentis) seen after 3 years of age.</p>\n<p>Manifestations of homocystinuria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ectopia lentis.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intellectual disability and psychiatric and behavioral disorders.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thromboembolic episodes.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Skeletal abnormalities resembling those of Marfan syndrome: They are tall and thin with elongated limbs and arachnodactyly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Elevations</strong> of both <strong>methionine</strong> and <strong>homocysteine</strong> in body fluids are the diagnostic lab findings.</p>\n<p>Note: Marfan syndrome is the other cause of heritable ectopia lentis. However, in Marfan’s, the lens is dislocated in an upward and temporal direction whereas in homocystinuria the lens is dislocated in a downward and nasal direction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8415","text":"Garrod’s tetrad includes a‌ll of the following, exce​pt ____.","raw_text":"Garrod’s tetrad includes all of the following, except ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alkapt‌onuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cysti‌nuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pento‌suria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylk‌etonuria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phenylketonuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phenylketonuria</strong></p><p><strong>Phenylketonuria </strong>is not a component of <strong>Garrod's tetrad.</strong></p>\n<p>Garrod’s tetrad includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alkaptonuria</strong>:<strong> </strong>Due to homogentisate oxidase deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>Albinism</strong>:<strong> </strong>Due to tyrosinase deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>Cystinuria</strong>: A type of aminoaciduria that prevents proper reabsorption of basic, or positively charged, amino acids that includes <strong>c</strong>ystine, <strong>o</strong>rnithine, <strong>l</strong>ysine and <strong>a</strong>rginine(COLA)</li>\n<li><strong>Pentosuria</strong>:<strong> </strong>Due to the deficiency of L-xylulose reductase or xylitol dehydrogenase, which are necessary for xylitol metabolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8454","text":"Primary hyperoxaluria is d‌ue to the defect in the m​etabolism of _____.","raw_text":"Primary hyperoxaluria is due to the defect in the metabolism of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thre‌onine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ser‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gly‌cine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ala‌nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glycine</strong></p><p><strong>Primary hyperoxaluria</strong> is a defect in the metabolism of <strong>glycine.</strong></p>\n<p>The main defect in primary hyperoxaluria type I is the deficiency of the <strong>alanine</strong> <strong>glyoxalate aminotransferase</strong> in liver peroxisomes causing failure to catabolize glyoxylate formed by the deamination of glycine. This leads to a gross overproduction of oxalate, leading to the formation of oxalate stones and subsequent kidney damage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8407","text":"The histidine load test is‌ done to identify the def​iciency of which of the f‌ollowing?","raw_text":"The histidine load test is done to identify the deficiency of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ir‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Folic‌ acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam‌in B12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e49cdc53ed941408aee55d70b58c15ex1280x1449.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Folic acid</strong></p><p>The histidine load test is done to identify <strong>folic acid deficiency.</strong> Formiminoglutamic acid (<strong>FIGLU</strong>) is excreted in urine, which is measured by the<strong> histidine load test</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8409","text":"Deficiency of aspartoacyla‌se leads to:","raw_text":"Deficiency of aspartoacylase leads to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Canavan‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oasthouse‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hawkin‌sinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hartnup‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Canavan disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10fe27a2ad1642c9bac07c92c7dcbe24x1280x835.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Canavan disease</strong></p><p>Deficiency of <strong>aspartoacylase</strong> leads to <strong>Canavan</strong> disease. It is an autosomal-recessive disorder, which leads to excessive excretion of <strong><em>N</em>-acetylaspartic acid </strong>in the urine.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>leukodystrophy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8403","text":"Maple syrup urine disease ‌type I is due to a defect​ in _____.","raw_text":"Maple syrup urine disease type I  is due to a defect in _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decarbo‌xylation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxid‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbox‌ylation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deami‌nation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decarboxylation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decarboxylation</strong></p><p><strong>Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD)</strong><strong> type I </strong>is due to impaired <strong>alpha-Ketoacid decarboxylase</strong> component of BCKAD (branched chain alpha keto acid dehydrogenase) complex, leading to <strong>defective decarboxylation</strong> reaction and the formation of branched-chain amino acids (<strong>leucine, isoleucine, and valine</strong>) and their toxic byproducts (keto-acids) in blood and urine.</p>\n<p>A peculiar odor (<strong>burnt sugar</strong>) is found in <strong>urine</strong>, <strong>sweat,</strong> and <strong>cerumen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Amino-acids and disorders of their metabolism are invariably being asked in NEET as well as in <span class=\"hiddenSpellError\">AIIMS</span> examinations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8456","text":"Type II MSUD results from ‌the mutation in the genes​ encoding _____. MSUD - M‌aple syrup urine disease","raw_text":"<p>Type II MSUD results from the mutation in the genes encoding _____.</p>\n<p>MSUD - Maple syrup urine disease</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-Ketoacid d‌ecarboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydrolipoyl transacylase‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dihydrolipoamide dehydroge‌nase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dihydrolipoyl transacylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dihydrolipoyl transacylase</strong></p><p><strong>Type II maple syrup urine disease</strong> (MSUD) results from a mutation in the genes encoding <strong>dihydrolipoyl</strong> <strong>transacylase.</strong></p>\n<p>There are 4 known types of Maple Syrup Urine Disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type IA and IB MSUD arise from mutations in the E1α and E1b genes, which encode the enzyme α-Ketoacid decarboxylase.</li>\n<li><strong>Type II</strong> arises from <strong>mutations</strong> in the <strong>E2 gene</strong>, which encodes <strong>dihydrolipoyl</strong> <strong>transacylase</strong>.</li>\n<li>Type III arises from mutations in the E3 gene, which encodes Dihydrolipoamide dehydrogenase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8425","text":"A neonate with poor feedin‌g, vomiting, and lethargy​ was found to have increa‌sed isovaleric acid in ur‌ine. This is due to a def‌ect in the metabolism of ​_____.","raw_text":"A neonate with poor feeding, vomiting, and lethargy was found to have increased isovaleric acid in urine. This is due to a defect in the metabolism of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isole‌ucine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Val‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leu‌cine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lys‌ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Leucine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Leucine</strong></p><p>This infant with <strong>poor feeding</strong> and <strong>increased isovaleric acid</strong> in the urine likely has<strong> isovaleric aciduria. </strong>This condition is caused due to a defect in the<strong> metabolism</strong> of <strong>leucine</strong>. </p>\n<p>Isovaleric aciduria is a condition characterized by vomiting, refusal to eat, listlessness, and a characteristic sweaty foot odor. The enzyme defect in this condition is <strong>isovaleryl Co-A dehydrogenase</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8458","text":"A 16-year-old boy with pro‌gressive myopia and night​ blindness was found to h‌ave gyrate atrophy of the‌ retina and choroid. Whic‌h of the following will b​e elevated in plasma?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy with progressive myopia and night blindness was found to have gyrate atrophy of the retina and choroid. Which of the following will be elevated in plasma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cys‌tine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orni‌thine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lys‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ala‌nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ornithine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ornithine</strong></p><p>Plasma levels of <strong>ornithine</strong> are <strong>elevated</strong> in <strong>gyrate</strong> <strong>atrophy </strong>of the <strong>retina </strong>and<strong> choroid.</strong></p>\n<p>The initial reactions in arginine catabolism are the conversion of arginine to ornithine followed by the transamination of ornithine. Any <strong>mutations</strong> in <strong>ornithine aminotransferase</strong> lead to elevated plasma and urinary concentrations of ornithine.</p>\n<p>This is associated with a condition known as <strong>gyrate atrophy </strong>of the<strong> choroid </strong>and<strong> retina. </strong>It presents with <strong>myopia</strong> and <strong>night blindness</strong> in adolescence which progresses to legal blindness by 50 years. The condition is treated with <strong>dietary restriction</strong> of <strong>arginine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8396","text":"Segawa syndrome is due to ‌the deficiency of which o​f the following?","raw_text":"Segawa syndrome is due to the deficiency of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrahydr‌obiopterin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pantothe‌nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bio‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrahydro‌folic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetrahydrobiopterin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetrahydrobiopterin</strong></p><p><strong>Segawa syndrome </strong>is a hereditary progressive dystonia due to the deficiency of <strong>tetrahydrobiopterin(BH4)</strong>. </p>\n<p>Segawa syndrome, also known as <strong>dopamine-responsive dystonia</strong> occurs due to a defect in the <strong>GTP cyclohydrolase I</strong> enzyme required for BH4 synthesis.</p>\n<p>This condition is characterised by increased muscle tone and Parkinsonian features, typically absent in the morning or after rest but worsening during the day and with exertion. <strong>Treatment</strong> for this condition is with<strong> Levodopa.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8406","text":"During the routine follow-‌up of a 9-month-old child​, you notice a peculiar b‌oiled cabbage odor. What ‌is the most likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"During the routine follow-up of a 9-month-old child, you notice a peculiar boiled cabbage odor. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypermeth‌ioninemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isovaleri‌c aciduria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple carboxylase defic‌iency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trimethy‌laminuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypermethioninemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypermethioninemia</strong></p><p><strong>Boiled cabbage</strong> odor in urine, sweat, and breath occurs in <strong>hypermethioninemia. Dimethylsulphide, </strong>a metabolite of methionine, causes the characteristic<strong> boiled cabbage smell.</strong></p>\n<p>People with a genetic deficiency of α/β-methionine adenosyltransferase present with <strong>hypermethioninemia</strong> and <strong>neural demyelination</strong>. However, they're usually asymptomatic until early adulthood.</p>\n<p>Methionine adenosyltransferase converts methionine to S-adenosyl Methionine (SAM) - a principal methyl donor. Hence the enzyme deficincy causes excess accumulation of methionine and its broken down to metabolites.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3635","text":"Which of the following sho‌uld be avoided by a patie​nt of fish odour syndrome‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following should be avoided by a patient of fish odour syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bio‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cho‌line","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nia‌cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pantothe‌nic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Choline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Choline</strong></p><p>A fishy odor is seen in <strong>trimethylaminuria.</strong> Consumption of<em> </em>food products with <strong>choline</strong> should be <strong>avoided</strong> in fish odor syndrome.</p>\n<p>Fish odor syndrome (trimethylaminuria) is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease where TMA(trimethylamine) is not converted to the non-odorous TMAO (TMA-N-oxide) in the liver. This is due to the deficiency of <strong>flavin-containing mono oxygenase 3 (FMO3)</strong>. TMA then builds up in the body and is released in sweat, urine, and breath, giving off a strong <strong>odor</strong> of <strong>rotting</strong> <strong>fish</strong> from the body.</p>\n<p><strong>Choline, lecithin, and TMAO</strong> are precursors of trimethylamine which are found in food products. These food products are eggs, liver, peanuts, meat, poultry, fish, dairy foods, pasta, and rice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7393","text":"In a patient with Wilson's‌ disease which of the fol​lowing is decreased in ur‌ine?","raw_text":"In a patient with Wilson's disease which of the following is decreased in urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyro‌sine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosp‌horous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ser‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3-methyl‌histidine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3-methylhistidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3-methylhistidine</strong></p><p>Urinary levels of <strong>3-methylhistidine</strong> are <strong>decreased</strong> in patients with <strong>Wilson disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Amino acids in urine exist either in conjugated form (most) or in free form. In Wilson's disease the <strong>amount of conjugated amino acids far exceeds the amount of free amino acids</strong>. Methyl Histidine largely exists in free form and does not increase in amount on hydrolysis of acids in urine. Hence low amounts is seen in Wilson's disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilson disease</strong> is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by mutation of the <em><strong>ATP7B</strong></em><strong> gene </strong>located on <strong>chromosome 13. </strong>The <em>ATP7B</em> gene, encodes a transmembrane copper-transporting ATPase, expressed on the hepatocyte canalicular membrane.</p>\n<p>Deficiency in the ATP7B protein causes a decrease in copper excretion into the bile, impairs its incorporation into ceruloplasmin, and inhibits ceruloplasmin secretion into the blood, leading to the <strong>accumulation of toxic levels of copper</strong> in the liver, brain, and eye.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0579","text":"A patient came to the hosp‌ital with complaint of ab​dominal pain. Routine inv‌estigations were unremark‌able. On observation, the‌ urine turned black on st​anding. Which of the foll​owing enzymes is likely t‌o be defective in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A patient came to the hospital with complaint of abdominal pain. Routine investigations were unremarkable. On observation,  the urine turned black on standing. Which of the following enzymes is likely to be defective in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Homogentis‌ate oxidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Xanthine‌ oxidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylalanin‌e hydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dihydro oratate dehydrogen‌ase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Homogentisate oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Homogentisate oxidase</strong></p><p><strong>Urine</strong> turning <strong>black</strong> on exposure to air is suggestive of <strong>alkaptonuria</strong> which occurs due to the defect in <strong>homogentisate</strong> <strong>oxidase</strong> enzyme.</p>\n<p>Alkaptonuria is a disorder of tyrosine metabolism, that develops due to the defect in homogentisate oxidase, which oxidizes homogentisate to maleylacetoacetate. The defect in this enzyme leads to the accumulation of homogentisate, which is then excreted in the urine. The excreted homogentisate gets oxidized when exposed to the air and causes the urine to turn black.</p>\n<p>In some cases, the excessive homogentisate may get oxidized to <strong>benzoquinone acetate, </strong>which binds to the <strong>connective tissue.</strong> This leads to arthritis and pigmentation of the connective tissue, a condition called <strong>ochronosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Xanthine oxidase</strong> deficiency results in <strong>hypouricemia</strong>, xanthinuria, and xanthine lithiasis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Defects in phenylalanine hydroxylase can lead to phenylketonuria characterised by hyperreflexia, seizures, hypopigmentation, low IQ, hyperactivity, and tremors.</p>\n<p>Option D: Defects in dihydroorotate dehydrogenase are rarely seen in clinical practice. Miller syndrome is associated with this defect. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tK5owmnz","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"9_dynamics_of_disease_transmission","title":"9.Dynamics Of Disease Transmission","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6641","text":"Which of the following is ‌not communicable during t​he later part of the peri‍od marked 3 in the below ‌image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not communicable during the later part of the period marked 3 in the below image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whoopin‌g cough","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rabies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/29d8e30968174b0f9667bb088dd17108.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/780bc165c77e47429a5b81b80c8eed5cx1280x959.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rabies</strong></p><p>The period marked 3 in the figure is the <strong>incubation period</strong> while the later part of the period represents subclinical infection. <strong>Rabies</strong> is not communicable during its incubation period as it is transmitted from the saliva of a rabid animal through bite or scratch. <br/><br/>An <strong>incubatory carrier state</strong> is one in which <strong>carriers shed</strong> the organism during the <strong>incubation period</strong> of the disease, especially during the later parts of it.</p>\n<p>Examples of diseases showing incubatory carrier state are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measles</strong></li>\n<li>Polio</li>\n<li>Mumps</li>\n<li><strong>Whooping cough</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diphtheria</strong></li>\n<li>Influenza</li>\n<li>Hepatitis A</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Carriers of a disease </strong>are classified by the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Type</strong>: Incubatory, convalescent or healthy.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration</strong>: Temporary or chronic.</li>\n<li><strong>Portal of exit</strong>: Urinary, respiratory, intestinal or others.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6643","text":"Which of the following car‌riers shed the organism f​or indefinite periods?","raw_text":"Which of the following carriers shed the organism for indefinite periods?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Convalesce‌nt carriers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Healthy ‌carriers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic ‌carriers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paradoxica‌l carriers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic carriers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic carriers</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic carriers</strong> are those who <strong>shed</strong> or excrete the organism for <strong>indefinite periods</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Carriers</strong> are classified into <strong>chronic</strong> carriers and <strong>temporary</strong> carriers based on the <strong>duration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Organisms may be shed <strong>continuously or intermittently</strong> in chronic carriers. The <strong>duration</strong> of the carrier state also <strong>depends on the disease</strong>. Chronic carrier state is<strong> </strong>seen in typhoid, hepatitis B, gonorrhea, malaria, etc. </p>\n<p><strong>Temporary carriers</strong> are classified into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incubatory </strong>carriers - Those that<strong> </strong>shed the organism during the incubation period of the disease, especially during the later parts of it. It is seen in measles, mumps, polio, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Healthy </strong>carriers - Those who suffer from subclinical infections and develop a carrier state without developing the overt disease. It is seen in polio, meningococcal meningitis, cholera, diphtheria, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Convalescent </strong>carriers - Those that shed the organism during recovery or convalescence. It is seen in typhoid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Paradoxical carrier</strong> refers to a carrier who <strong>acquires</strong> the <strong>pathogen from</strong> another <strong>carrier</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Pseudo-carriers</strong> are carriers of <strong>avirulent</strong> organisms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6648","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases does not show verti​cal transmission?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases does not show vertical transmission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tet‌anus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Her‌pes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetanus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetanus</strong></p><p>Tetanus does not show vertical or transplacental transmission.</p>\n<p>Transmission of diseases or non-infectious agents<strong> transplacentally</strong> is called <strong>vertical</strong> <strong>transmission</strong>. Diseases that show this type of transmission include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Herpes</li>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>Rubella</li>\n<li>Varicella</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Apart from diseases, non-infectious agents like <strong>lithium</strong>, <strong>thalidomide</strong>, <strong>stilbestrol</strong>, etc. can cross the placenta and lead to the development of <strong>congenital anomalies</strong> in a foetus.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Neonatal tetanus</strong> occurs as a result of the <strong>failure</strong> to use <strong>aseptic techniques</strong> in managing the umbilical stump, unclean hands, and instruments or contamination by dirt, straw, or other nonsterile materials in the delivery field. Immunisation of pregnant women can prevent this disease via the transplacental transport of immunoglobulins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6645","text":"Which among the following ‌is false about droplet tr​ansmission?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false about droplet transmission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All droplets can travel on‌ly as far as 3 feet","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Droplets are usually large‌r than 5 microns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a type of direct tra‌nsmission","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basic ventilation decrease‌s the potential for this ​type of transmission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. All droplets can travel only as far as 3 feet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. All droplets can travel only as far as 3 feet</strong></p><p><strong>Droplet nuclei</strong> can travel as far as <strong>6 feet</strong>, whereas a <strong>droplet</strong> (particle &gt; 5μm) travels <strong>&lt;30-60cm</strong>, as recent studies have shown.</p>\n<p>Droplets are a mode of <strong>direct</strong> <strong>transmission</strong> of infectious agents. They are formed from <strong>saliva</strong> and <strong>nasopharyngeal secretions</strong> released during <strong>coughing</strong>, <strong>sneezing</strong>, or <strong>speaking</strong> into the surrounding atmosphere. The expelled droplets may impinge directly upon the conjunctiva, oro-respiratory mucosa or skin of close contact. This mode of transmission is seen in <strong>common cold</strong>, <strong>diphtheria</strong>, etc.</p>\n<p>Proper basic <strong>ventilation</strong> and <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>overcrowding</strong> reduce the potential for this type of transmission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6675","text":"What is the maximum size o‌f airborne droplet nuclei​?","raw_text":"What is the maximum size of airborne droplet nuclei?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 mi‌crons","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10 mi‌crons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 mi‌crons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 mi‌crons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5 microns","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5 microns</strong></p><p><strong>Droplet nuclei</strong> are sized less than or equal to <strong>5 microns</strong>.</p>\n<p>They may be <strong>formed</strong> by <strong>evaporation of droplets</strong> coughed or sneezed into the air or be generated purposefully by a variety of <strong>atomizing </strong><strong>devices</strong> (aerosols). They are capable of<strong> indirect airborne transmission</strong> of diseases like tuberculosis, influenza, chickenpox, measles, etc.</p>\n<p>The droplet nuclei may <strong>remain airborne</strong> for <strong>long periods</strong> of time, some retaining and others losing infectivity or virulence. These small particles when inhaled are capable of <strong>reaching</strong> the <strong>alveoli</strong> and getting retained there.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9568","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a method of direct tr​ansmission of communicabl‍e diseases?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a method of direct transmission of communicable diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertical t‌ransmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contact ‌with soil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Droplet‌ nuclei","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Droplet tr‌ansmission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Droplet nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Droplet nuclei</strong></p><p class=\"ui_qtext_para\"><strong>Airborne</strong> transmission by <strong>droplet nuclei</strong> is not a method of direct transmission of diseases.</p>\n<p class=\"ui_qtext_para\">It is a method of <strong>indirect</strong> transmission of diseases.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 114\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Droplet nuclei are tiny particles of size 1-5 microns range that may be formed by either due to evaporation of droplets coughed or sneezed into the air or are formed from aerosols.</p>\n<p>These droplet nuclei remain airborne for long periods of time and can transmit diseases like tuberculosis, influenza, chickenpox, measles, Q fever and other respiratory infections.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"ui_qtext_para\"><strong>Droplet transmission</strong>, <strong>vertical transmission</strong>, and <strong>contact with soil</strong> are methods of <strong>direct</strong> transmission of diseases.</p>\n<p class=\"ui_qtext_para\">Differences between droplet and droplet nuclei are summarised below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 313px; width: 367px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.609375px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><strong>DROPLET NUCLEI </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.40625px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><strong>DROPLET </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.609375px; text-align: left; height: 33px;\">Indirect transmission</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.40625px; text-align: left; height: 33px;\">Direct transmission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.609375px; text-align: left; height: 62px;\">It is the residue from evaporated droplets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.40625px; text-align: left; height: 62px;\">It is produced while coughing, sneezing, talking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.609375px; text-align: left; height: 47px;\">&lt;5microns in diameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.40625px; height: 47px;\">≥5 microns in diameter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.609375px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\">No need for close contact for transmission</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.40625px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\">Requires closeness and direct contact for transmission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.609375px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\">Diseases transmitted: chickenpox, measles etc.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.40625px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\">Diseases transmitted: diphtheria, meningococcal meningitis etc.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6717","text":"Spread of disease via whic‌h of the following is an ​example of an indirect mo‍de of transmission?","raw_text":"Spread of disease via which of the following is an example of an indirect mode of transmission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mother ‌to child","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Droplet ‌infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dog ‌bite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contamin‌ated food","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contaminated food","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contaminated food</strong></p><p>The spread of a disease through <strong>contaminated food</strong> is an example of <strong>vehicle-borne transmission</strong>. It is an <strong>indirect</strong> mode of transmission.</p>\n<p>Vehicle-borne transmission is the transmission of infectious agents through <strong>food</strong>, <strong>water</strong> and <strong>biological products </strong>(blood, plasma etc.). Of these, food and water are more frequent vehicles of transmission because they are used by everyone.</p>\n<p>Mother to child transmission, droplet infection and transmission via dog bite are examples of direct modes of transmission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6676","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about fomite-born​e transmission is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about fomite-borne transmission is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an indirect mode of ‌transmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transmission via contamina‌ted food is an example of​ this","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatitis A is transmitted‌ in this way","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Routine disinfection can p‌revent such transmission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transmission via contaminated food is an example of this","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transmission via contaminated food is an example of this</strong></p><p><strong>Fomites</strong> are objects <strong>other than water</strong> or <strong>food</strong> that can be <strong>contaminated by infectious agents</strong> and help in transmitting the disease. They include clothes, towels, linen, cups, plates, spoons, etc.</p>\n<p>Fomites are an example of an <strong>indirect</strong> mode of transmission.</p>\n<p>Some <strong>diseases</strong> transmitted by fomites include <strong>diphtheria</strong>, <strong>typhoid</strong>, <strong>hepatitis A,</strong> etc.</p>\n<p>Good public and personal hygiene can prevent this type of transmission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6649","text":"What is the lodgement and ‌development of an arthrop​od on the body surface ca‍lled?","raw_text":"What is the lodgement and development of an arthropod on the body surface called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infes‌tation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contam‌ination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coloni‌sation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infestation</strong></p><p>The <strong>lodgement</strong> and <strong>development</strong> of an arthropod on the <strong>body surface</strong> is called <strong>infestation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Infection</strong> is the <strong>entry</strong> and <strong>development</strong> or <strong>multiplication</strong> of an infectious agent in a host’s body.</p>\n<p><strong>Contamination</strong> is the <strong>presence</strong> of a disease agent <strong>on</strong> a <strong>body surface</strong> like clothes, toys, etc. and also<strong> in substances</strong> like food or water.   </p>\n<p><strong>Colonization</strong> is the presence of a disease agent in or on a host, with <strong>growth</strong> and <strong>multiplication</strong> but <strong>without</strong> any overt <strong>clinical expression</strong> or detected immune response in the host at the time it is isolated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6655","text":"An entomologist was studyi‌ng a vector and the patho​gen it transmits. He foun‍d that the pathogen under‌went changes in number an​d morphology in the vecto​r. What is this type of b‌iological transmission ca‍lled?","raw_text":"An entomologist was studying a vector and the pathogen it transmits. He found that the pathogen underwent changes in number and morphology in the vector. What is this type of biological transmission called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propa‌gative","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclo-dev‌elopmental","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo-pr‌opagative","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Develo‌pmental","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cyclo-propagative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cyclo-propagative</strong></p><p>This type of biological transmission where a pathogen undergoes a <strong>change in number</strong> and <strong>morphology</strong> in a vector is called <strong>cyclo-propagative </strong>transmission.</p>\n<p><strong>Malaria </strong>is a disease whose causative organism (<em>Plasmodium sp.</em>) undergoes cyclo-propagative transmission. In malaria, the parasite undergoes <strong>both developmental </strong>changes and <strong>multiplication </strong>in the <strong>Anopheles</strong> <strong>mosquito</strong> (vector).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6673","text":"A patient with chronic lym‌phedema was found to have​ a positive DEC provocati‍on test. Which type of bi‌ological transmission doe​s the pathogen implicated​ here undergo?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic lymphedema was found to have a positive DEC provocation test. Which type of biological transmission does the pathogen implicated here undergo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propa‌gative","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclo-dev‌elopmental","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo-pr‌opagative","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Develo‌pmental","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cyclo-developmental","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cyclo-developmental</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient most likely has <strong>lymphatic filariasis</strong>. The causative organism of this disease (<em>Wuchereria bancrofti</em>) undergoes <strong>cyclo-developmental transmission</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cyclo-developmental transmission is a transmission where the agent undergoes <strong>only developmental</strong> <strong>changes</strong> and<strong> not multiplication</strong> within the vector.</p>\n<p><strong>DEC provocation test: </strong>In this test, a small dose of DEC is given 20-50 minutes before sample collection so that the microfilaria appears in the blood even during the <strong>daytime</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6735","text":"You are a rural medical he‌alth officer investigatin​g an epidemic. What is th‍e correct sequence of ste‌ps you must follow? a. co​nfirm epidemic existence ​b. verify the diagnosis c‌. formulation of hypothes‍is d. define the populati‌on at risk e. rapid searc​h of cases","raw_text":"<p>You are a rural medical health officer investigating an epidemic. What is the correct sequence of steps you must follow?</p>\n<p>a. confirm epidemic existence<br/>b. verify the diagnosis<br/>c. formulation of hypothesis<br/>d. define the population at risk <br/>e. rapid search of cases</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"a→ c→ ‌b→ e→ d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"b→ e→ ‌a→ d→ c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"a→ b→ ‌e→ c→ d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"b→ a→ ‌d→ e→c","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. b→ a→ d→ e→c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. b→ a→ d→ e→c</strong></p><p>Steps for the<strong class=\"ng-binding\"> investigation </strong>of an<strong class=\"ng-binding\"> epidemic</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Verification</strong> of diagnosis</li>\n<li><strong>Confirmation</strong> of the <strong>existence</strong> of an epidemic</li>\n<li><strong>Defining</strong> the <strong>population at risk</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Obtaining a map of the area</li>\n<li>Counting the population</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Rapid <strong>search</strong> <strong>for</strong> all the <strong>cases</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Medical survey</li>\n<li>Epidemiological case sheets</li>\n<li>Searching for more cases</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Data analysis on the basis of time, place and person</li>\n<li><strong>Formulation</strong> of <strong>hypothesis</strong></li>\n<li>Testing of hypothesis</li>\n<li>Evaluation of ecological factors</li>\n<li>Further investigation of the population at risk</li>\n<li>Writing the report</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6736","text":"You have been asked to wri‌te the final report follo​wing the investigation of‍ a cholera epidemic. Whic‌h of the following will y​ou not include in this re​port?","raw_text":"You have been asked to write the final report following the investigation of a cholera epidemic. Which of the following will you not include in this report?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"History of previous epidem‌ics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Usual number of cases that‌ existed before the epide​mic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Geographical distribution ‌of the epidemic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incubati‌on period","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incubation period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incubation period</strong></p><p>The final report of an epidemic does not include the incubation period of the disease.</p>\n<p>The<strong> final report</strong> following the<strong> investigation of an epidemic</strong> should include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Background information </strong>- Geography, climate, demography, socio-economic groups, health service organization, surveillance data and normal disease prevalence.</li>\n<li><strong>Historical data </strong>- Previous occurrences of the epidemic, the occurrence of any related diseases and discovery of the primary case in the present outbreak.</li>\n<li><strong>Methodology of investigations </strong>- Case definition, epidemiological questionnaires, collection and laboratory techniques.</li>\n<li><strong>Analysis of data </strong>- Clinical and epidemiological data, modes of transmission, laboratory data and all their interpretations.</li>\n<li><strong>Control measures </strong>- Strategy definitions, methodology of implementation, evaluation methods and preventive measures.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6733","text":"You have been sent to an a‌rea to study the ongoing ​typhoid epidemic. When wi‍ll you declare the area t‌o be free of the epidemic​?","raw_text":"You have been sent to an area to study the ongoing typhoid epidemic. When will you declare the area to be free of the epidemic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No new cases‌ for 14 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No new cases‌ for 60 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No new cases‌ for 45 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No new cases‌ for 28 days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No new cases for 28 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No new cases for 28 days</strong></p><p>The area will be declared free of the typhoid epidemic when <strong>no new cases</strong> are reported for<strong> 28 days</strong>. </p>\n<p>An area is declared <strong>free of an epidemic</strong> when there are <strong>no cases</strong> reported <strong>for</strong> <strong>twice </strong>the<strong> incubation period</strong> of the disease since the last case.</p>\n<p>The<strong> incubation period</strong> of <strong>typhoid</strong> is <strong>14 days</strong>. So, in the given scenario, the area will be declared free of the epidemic when no new cases are reported for twice the incubation period i.e., 14 x 2 = 28 days.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9566","text":"The probability that infec‌tion will occur among sus​ceptible persons followin‍g contact with an infecti‌ous person within the inc​ubation period is known a​s the_______.","raw_text":"The probability that infection will occur among susceptible persons following contact with an infectious person within the incubation period is known as the_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondary ‌attack rate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Case fata‌lity rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary a‌ttack rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiary a‌ttack rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Secondary attack rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Secondary attack rate</strong></p><p>The probability that<strong> infection</strong> will occur <strong>among susceptible</strong> persons <strong>following contact</strong> with an infectious person within the incubation period is called <strong>secondary attack rate</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is calculated by the following formula:</p>\n<p>Secondary attack rate (SAR) = (Number of exposed or susceptible persons developing the disease within the incubation period x 100)/(Total number of close or exposed contacts that are susceptible)</p>\n<p><strong>Primary cases</strong> are usually <strong>excluded</strong> from the numerator and denominator.</p>\n<p>Secondary attack rate measures the <strong>communicability</strong> of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6725","text":"Following an outbreak of a‌ disease, you are asked t​o calculate the secondary‍ attack rate (SAR). What ‌is the denominator you wo​uld use?","raw_text":"Following an outbreak of a disease, you are asked to calculate the secondary attack rate (SAR). What is the denominator you would use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All people having history ‌of contact with a case","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All people susceptible in ‌the entire area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All the people in contact ‌with the primary case","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All the close or exposed c‌ontacts those are suscept​ible","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All the close or exposed contacts those are susceptible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All the close or exposed contacts those are susceptible</strong></p><p>The <strong>denominator</strong> used for calculating <strong>SAR</strong> is all the close or exposed<strong> contacts</strong> that are<strong> susceptible</strong>.</p>\n<p>The secondary attack rate is defined as the <strong>probability</strong> that <strong>infection</strong> will occur among <strong>susceptible</strong> persons following <strong>contact</strong> with an infectious person within the incubation period.</p>\n<p>It is calculated by the formula:</p>\n<p>Secondary attack rate (SAR) = (Number of exposed or susceptible persons developing the disease within the incubation period x 100)/(Total number of close or exposed contacts that are susceptible)</p>\n<p><strong>Primary cases</strong> are usually <strong>excluded</strong> from the numerator and denominator.</p>\n<p>Secondary attack rate measures the <strong>communicability</strong> of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6727","text":"A village has 100 children‌ under the age of 5. The ​coverage of measles vacci‍nation is 60%. Following ‌a case of measles, 26 chi​ldren developed measles. ​What is the secondary att‌ack rate?","raw_text":"A village has 100 children under the age of 5. The coverage of measles vaccination is 60%. Following a case of measles, 26 children developed measles. What is the secondary attack rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‌1%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‌9%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6‌6%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 66%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 66%</strong></p><p>The <strong>secondary attack rate</strong> for the given scenario is <strong>66%</strong>.</p>\n<p>The secondary attack rate (SAR) is given by:</p>\n<p>(Number of exposed or susceptible persons developing the disease within the incubation period x 100)/(Total number of close or exposed contacts that are susceptible)</p>\n<p>Here, number who <strong>developed</strong> the<strong> disease</strong> after exposure= <strong>26</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Susceptible</strong> population= 100 - 60 (vaccinated) - 1 (primary case) = <strong>39</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore, <strong>SAR</strong> = 26 /39 x 100 = <strong>66%</strong>.</p>\n<p>SAR is a measure of communicability.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6731","text":"A 28-year-old man is found‌ to be positive for SARS-​CoV-2. On contact tracing‍, 30 people have been ide‌ntified as close contacts​ but none of them shows a​ny symptoms or signs. Wha‌t is the time duration fo‍r which they must be quar‌antined?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man is found to be positive for SARS-CoV-2. On contact tracing, 30 people have been identified as close contacts but none of them shows any symptoms or signs. What is the time duration for which they must be quarantined?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum period of communic‌ability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maximum incu‌bation period","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Average period of communic‌ability","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Average incu‌bation period","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maximum incubation period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maximum incubation period</strong></p><p>Quarantine is the <strong>limitation</strong> in freedom of <strong>movement</strong> of <strong>healthy contacts</strong> of cases, who have been exposed for a period that lasts as long as the <strong>maximal incubation period </strong>of the disease.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>primary level of prevention</strong> and falls under specific protection measures. The various <strong>types</strong> of quarantine are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Absolute </strong>- Complete limitation of movement</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Modified </strong>- Selective partial limitation of movement</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Segregation </strong>- Observation of a group of persons in a population after separation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6730","text":"Which of the following pai‌rs of diseases and their ​periods of isolation is w‍rongly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of diseases and their periods of isolation is wrongly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rubella – 3 days after ons‌et of rash","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chickenpox – 6 days after ‌onset of rash","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes zoster – 6 days aft‌er onset of rash","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Measles – Up to 5 days aft‌er onset of rash","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rubella – 3 days after onset of rash","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rubella – 3 days after onset of rash</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>rubella</strong> should be <strong>isolated</strong> for <strong>7 days after</strong> they develop the <strong>rash</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong> Isolation</strong> is defined as <strong>separation</strong>, for the period of communicability of <strong>infected persons</strong> from others to <strong>prevent</strong> or limit <strong>transmission</strong> of the infectious agent.</p>\n<p>In settings where women who are in their 1<sup>st</sup> trimester or sexually active or non-immune without contraception usage may be exposed, outbreak control measures should begin as soon as rubella is suspected and should not be postponed until laboratory confirmation of cases.</p>\n<p>Note: Multiple sources have confimed the period of infectivity of measles which is from 4 days before the onset of rash to 5 days after the onset of rash.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7465","text":"A paradoxical carrier is d‌efined as:","raw_text":"A paradoxical carrier is defined as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A person who sheds pathoge‌ns during incubation peri​od","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A carrier who acquired pat‌hogen from another carrie​r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A person who acquired path‌ogen from patient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient who became a car‌rier","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A carrier who acquired pathogen from another carrier","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A carrier who acquired pathogen from another carrier</strong></p><p>A <strong>paradoxical</strong> carrier is a <strong>carrier</strong> that acquired a pathogen <strong>from another carrier.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Incubatory </strong>carriers are those who shed the infectious agents<strong> during the incubation period</strong> of the disease. </p>\n<p><strong>Convalescent</strong> carriers<strong> </strong>are those who <strong>continue</strong> to shed the disease agent during the<strong> period of convalescence. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Healthy</strong> carriers are those who had a subclinical infection and <strong>developed a carrier state</strong> <strong>without</strong> suffering from overt<strong> disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ispAfaVz","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"9_reactive_skin_diseases_and_drug_eruptions","title":"9.Reactive Skin Diseases And Drug Eruptions","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MC1414","text":"The most common triggering​ factor of the given cond‌ition is ________","raw_text":"The most common triggering factor of the given condition is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vacci​nation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malig​nancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dr​ugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infe​ction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b71e5ac3bce140de96dcfa1d23bdf9d3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5be2d00772a2488998c28496dc777016x359x348.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Infection</strong></p><p>The given image shows characteristic <strong>target lesions</strong> of <strong>erythema multiforme</strong>. This most commonly occurs following a <strong>herpes simplex virus infection.</strong></p>\n<p>The common <strong>triggers</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Herpes simplex infection</li>\n<li>Mycoplasma infection</li>\n<li>Bacterial infections</li>\n<li>Fungal infections</li>\n<li>Vaccinations</li>\n<li>Malignancy- carcinoma, lymphoma, leukemia</li>\n<li>Drugs- sulfonamides, rifampicin, penicillins, hydantoin derivatives</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous- lupus erythematosus (Rowell Syndrome), sarcoidosis, polyarteritis nodosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Erythema multiforme is best regarded as a<strong> self‐limiting cytotoxic dermatitis</strong> resulting from <strong>cell‐mediated hypersensitivity.</strong></p>\n<p>It presents clinically with a spectrum of macular, papular, or urticarial lesions, as well as the classic acral iris or <strong>‘target lesions’</strong>.</p>\n<p>Target lesions are <strong>less than 3 cm in diameter</strong>, rounded, and have <strong>three</strong> zones:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Central area of <strong>dusky erythema</strong> or purpura</li>\n<li>Middle paler zone of <strong>edema</strong></li>\n<li>Outer ring of <strong>erythema</strong> with a well‐defined edge</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Lesions may involve the palms and the trunk. Oral and genital mucosal membranes lesions are associated with erosions</p>\n<p>The image below shows target lesions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1424","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about er‌ythema multiforme?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about erythema multiforme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythema multiforme major ​is the most common form","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The lesions occur in crops​ and fade in 1-2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Photoaggravation of the le​sions is seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The distribution of target​ lesions is mainly acral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Erythema multiforme major is the most common form","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d65b3d6e87340039e1d03540b53980fx527x252.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Erythema multiforme major is the most common form</strong></p><p><strong>Erythema multiforme minor</strong> (papular or simplex form) accounts for approximately 80% of cases and is the <strong>most common</strong> form.</p>\n<p>Clinically erythema multiforme presents as macular, papular, or urticarial lesions, as well as the classic iris or ‘target lesions’, distributed preferentially on the <strong>distal extremities. </strong>Raised <strong>a</strong><strong>typical target lesions</strong> having only two of the zones may also be seen and mucous membranes are rarely involved. Koebnerization accounts for some bizarre clinical distributions. </p>\n<p><strong>Photoaggravation</strong> of erythema multiforme is well recognized.</p>\n<p>The lesions appear in <strong>successive crops</strong> for a few days and <strong>fade</strong> in<strong> 1–2 weeks</strong>, sometimes leaving <strong>dusky discoloration</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows mucosal involvement in erythema multiforme major</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1429","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is interface d‌ermatitis not seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is interface dermatitis not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythema ​multiforme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen​ planus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Graft versus​ host disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecz​ema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eczema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77f1742a592a41129b4631c30f21f1cdx1280x1307.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eczema</strong></p><p>Eczema shows <strong>spongiotic dermatitis </strong>and not <strong>interface dermatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Interface dermatitis includes diseases in which the primary pathology involves the <strong>dermo-epidermal junction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The salient <strong>histological findings</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Basal cell vacuolization</li>\n<li>Apoptotic keratinocytes (colloid or Civatte bodies)</li>\n<li>Obscuring of the dermo-epidermal junction by inflammatory cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common conditions showing interface dermatitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Erythema multiforme</li>\n<li>Lichen planus</li>\n<li>Graft versus host disease</li>\n<li>Fixed drug eruptions</li>\n<li>Lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Dermatomyositis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1441","text":"What percentage of a patie​nt's body surface area wo‌uld need to show sloughin​g to make a diagnosis of ‌Stevens-Johnson syndrome?​","raw_text":"What percentage of a patient's body surface area would need to show sloughing to make a diagnosis of Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt​;10%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10-​20%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20-​30%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt​;30%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1fd12a07f2b4154b9be0984f98553d8x600x796.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8049430251144e289b8838c5336b8e5x430x330.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;10%</strong></p><p><strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome</strong> (SJS) involves <strong>less than 10%</strong> of body surface area (BSA).</p>\n<p>Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) are <strong>severe mucocutaneous reactions</strong>, usually to <strong>drugs</strong>, characterized by <strong>blistering</strong> and<strong> epithelial sloughing</strong>. The two terms describe phenotypes within a <strong>severity spectrum</strong>, where SJS is the less extensive form and TEN is the more extensive.</p>\n<p>Based on the <strong>type of cutaneous lesion</strong> and<strong> extent of maximal epidermal detachment</strong>, the disease is classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 220px; width: 499.359px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Epidermal detachment </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.3594px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Purpuric macules and atypical target lesions </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\"><strong>SJS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">&lt;10%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.3594px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\"><strong>Overlap SJS-TEN </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">10-30%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.3594px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\"><strong>TEN with spots</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">&gt;30%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.3594px;\">Present </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\"><strong>TEN without spots </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">&gt;30% (large sheets of epidermal loss)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84.3594px;\">Absent </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows SJS in the early stages with mucosal involvement. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1486","text":"A 53-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of fev‌er, malaise, conjunctivit​is, and skin lesions on h‌er back for the past 2 da​ys. She gives a history o‍f taking the tablet pirox‌icam for knee pain 2 week​s ago. What is the probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman presents with complaints of fever, malaise, conjunctivitis, and skin lesions on her back for the past 2 days. She gives a history of taking the tablet piroxicam for knee pain 2 weeks ago. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fixed dru​g eruption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema ​multiforme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stevens-John​son syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stevens-Johnson syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21ea08f8c4934dd099263aec1513294b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stevens-Johnson syndrome</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>atypical target lesions</strong> with 2 zones. The rash and flaccid blisters affecting the back (<strong>epidermal detachment &lt;10% BSA</strong>), conjunctivitis, cheilitis (<strong>mucosal</strong> involvement), with the history of <strong>drug intake</strong> (triggering factor) favor a diagnosis of<strong> Stevens-Johnson syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Fixed drug eruptions present up to 8 hours<strong> </strong>of drug intake. These are erythematous lesions that heal with hyperpigmentation. Mucosal involvement is rare.</p>\n<p>Option B: Erythema multiforme shows<strong> </strong>typical target or raised atypical target lesions, that develop over few days and resolve in 2 to 3 weeks. Mucosal involvement is rare if the trigger is a drug. </p>\n<p>Option D: In toxic epidermal necrolysis, &gt;30% of body surface area is involved. Diffuse generalized epidermal detachment is seen with extensive mucosal involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7067","text":"A disease associated with ​the occurrence of blister‌ing and peeling of over &gt;​30% of body surface area ‌is usually caused by:","raw_text":"A disease associated with the occurrence of blistering and peeling of over &gt;30% of body surface area is usually caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral i​nfection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dr​ugs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fungal i​nfection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auto-i​mmunity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Drugs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2084496406d4b9ca0396af00011436ex1280x1706.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Drugs</strong></p><p>Blistering and peeling involving greater than 30% of the body surface area points to the diagnosis of <strong>toxic epidermal necrolysis </strong>(TEN). TEN is primarily <strong>drug</strong><strong>‐induced, </strong>with a culprit drug being demonstrated in approximately <strong>85% of cases.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)</strong> and <strong>toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)</strong> are both severe mucocutaneous reactions, characterized by blistering and epithelial sloughing. The two terms describe phenotypes within a severity spectrum, where SJS is the less extensive form and TEN is the more extensive</p>\n<table style=\"height: 327px; width: 331px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.0469px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Epidermal detachment </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.203px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Purpuric macules and atypical target lesions </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.0469px;\"><strong>SJS </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.75px;\"><strong>&lt;10%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.203px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.0469px;\"><strong>Overlap SJS-TEN </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.75px;\">10–30%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.203px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.0469px;\"><strong>TEN with spots</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.75px;\"><strong>&gt;30%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.203px;\">Present </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90.0469px;\"><strong>TEN without spots </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.75px;\">&gt;30% (large sheets of epidermal loss)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.203px;\">Absent </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> Common <strong>drugs</strong> that can cause SJS/TEN are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Allopurinol</li>\n<li>NSAIDs- oxicam group</li>\n<li>Anti-epileptics - phenobarbital, phenytoin, carbamazepine, lamotrigine</li>\n<li>Antibiotics - penicillins, cephalosporins, sulfamethoxazole, sulfasalazine, nevirapine</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1446","text":"A 30-year-old man was brou​ght to the casualty with ‌a 3-day history of the fi​ndings shown. On examinat‌ion, 50% of the body surf​ace area was involved. He‍morrhagic cheilitis and c‌rusting were also noted. ​What are the diagnosis an​d most probable underlyin​g etiology?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man was brought to the casualty with a 3-day history of the findings shown. On examination, 50% of the body surface area was involved. Hemorrhagic cheilitis and crusting were also noted. What are the diagnosis and most probable underlying etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stevens Johnson syndrome; ​Mycoplasma pneumoniae inf‌ection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stevens Johnson syndrome; ​cephalosporin intake","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis​; viral infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis​; carbamazepine intake","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Toxic epidermal necrolysis; carbamazepine intake","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/074d643f10024f228f37c0a8eaf5a96a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Toxic epidermal necrolysis; carbamazepine intake</strong></p><p>As more than 30% of the body surface is involved, the diagnosis is <strong>toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)</strong>. SJS/TEN is primarily a <strong>drug‐induced</strong> phenomenon, with a culprit drug being demonstrated in approximately 85% of cases.</p>\n<p>Common <strong>drugs</strong> that can cause SJS/TEN are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Allopurinol</li>\n<li>NSAIDs - oxicam group</li>\n<li>Anti-epileptics - phenobarbital, phenytoin, carbamazepine, lamotrigine</li>\n<li>Antibiotics - penicillins, cephalosporins, sulfamethoxazole, sulfasalazine, nevirapine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In 15% of the cases, where the drug cannot be identified, especially in <strong>children</strong>, appear to be triggered by <strong>infections</strong>, most notably by <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>.</p>\n<p>Note: FDA recommends genotyping before starting carbamazepine, as HLA B1502 allele which is seen in increased frequencies among Asians is implicated in carbamazepine induced SJS/TEN.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1468","text":"A 27-year-old sexually act​ive man presents to the O‌PD with the following fin​ding. He informs you that‌ a few days ago, he devel​oped an erythematous vesi‍cular lesion at the same ‌location within hours of ​taking a drug. Which of t​he following is most comm​only implicated here?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old sexually active man presents to the OPD with the following finding. He informs you that a few days ago, he developed an erythematous vesicular lesion at the same location within hours of taking a drug. Which of the following is most commonly implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carboc​ystine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cotrim​oxazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NS​AID","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrac​ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NSAID","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9216f3397c5419e9d58cf1c041e63de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NSAID</strong></p><p>The characteristic <strong>lesion healing</strong> with <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong> following <strong>drug</strong> intake is consistent with <strong>fixed drug eruption</strong>. <strong>NSAIDs</strong> are the most commonly implicated drug. </p>\n<p>Fixed drug eruption (FDE) is a cutaneous adverse drug reaction characterized by <strong>recurrent</strong> <strong>well‐defined lesions</strong> occurring at the <strong>same sites</strong> each time the offending drug is taken. It typically presents <strong>30 min to 8 h</strong> after drug exposure.</p>\n<p>The most common drugs that act as triggers are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>NSAIDs (25%)</li>\n<li>Paracetamol (15%)</li>\n<li>Cotrimoxazole</li>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n</ol>\n<p>FDE is a form of classical <strong>delayed‐type hypersensitivity reaction.</strong></p>\n<p>Typically, FDE presents as a <strong>sharply defined</strong>, round, or oval <strong>erythematous and oedematous plaque,</strong> which evolves to become <strong>dusky</strong>, <strong>violaceous,</strong> and occasionally <strong>vesicular or bullous lesions</strong>. Lesions are usually solitary or few in number.</p>\n<p>Commonly affected sites include the<strong> lips, genitals, palms, and soles</strong>; 5% of cases may have an exclusive mucosal involvement. Overall, the most common site involved is the <strong>genitalia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The majority of FDE is <strong>self‐limiting</strong> with an <strong>excellent prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The generalized bullous form of FDE should be differentiated from toxic epidermal necrolysis by prior similar history, absence of target lesions, and no involvement of mucosal surfaces.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1470","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site for ‌fixed drug eruption?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for fixed drug eruption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fa​ce","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extr​emity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tr​unk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glans​ penis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glans penis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/543d31484ec840a9b94dbf81b54b7b69x186x127.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glans penis</strong></p><p><strong>Glans penis</strong> is the best answer among the given options. Fixed drug eruptions(FDE) can occur anywhere in the body. However, <strong>50%</strong> of FDE occurs in the <strong>genital</strong> and <strong>oral mucosa.</strong> The other common sites are palms and soles.</p>\n<p>Drug‐specific clinical patterns have been reported. These include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>NSAID‐induced FDE - genitals and lips</li>\n<li>Tetracycline and trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole‐induced FDE - genitals</li>\n<li>Metamizole‐induced FDE - trunk and extremities</li>\n<li>Carbocysteine‐induced FDE - face</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows hyperpigmentation on the glans penis- healed fixed drug eruption.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1478","text":"Which of these statements ​is false regarding erythe‌ma nodosum?","raw_text":"Which of these statements is false regarding erythema nodosum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are considered to be ​a hypersensitivity reacti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The lesions are mostly sym​metrical","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The lesions are usually te​nder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a type of lobular pa​nniculitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a type of lobular panniculitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87e2925908304ee7a50da51d234b172ax1280x1045.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a type of lobular panniculitis</strong></p><p>Erythema nodosum (EN) is a cutaneous<strong> reactive septal panniculitis</strong> with<strong> no vasculitis </strong>to a variety of stimuli.</p>\n<p>It is believed to be a<strong> type IV delayed hypersensitivity</strong> reaction to various antigens including medicines. </p>\n<p>It presents with <strong>symmetrical, erythematous,</strong> and<strong> tender subcutaneous nodules</strong> or plaques which are typically distributed over the <strong>anterior aspect</strong> of the limbs. Over a few days, these lesions become purplish before finally turning brown. Ulceration never occurs.</p>\n<p>The clinical course is <strong>self‐limiting</strong> following drug withdrawal and usually <strong>resolves </strong>within <strong>2–4 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1485","text":"A patient presented with a​ sudden onset of symmetri‌cal, bilateral, tender, e​rythematous, warm nodules‌ and raised plaques invol​ving the shins, ankles, a‍nd knees. Which of the fo‌llowing is not a trigger ​for this eruption?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a sudden onset of symmetrical, bilateral, tender, erythematous, warm nodules and raised plaques involving the shins, ankles, and knees. Which of the following is not a trigger for this eruption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preg​nancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfona​mide use","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic pa​ncreatitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic pancreatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f4b413f06fc41baa27b177d02fa91dcx1223x756.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic pancreatitis</strong></p><p>The description is classical of <strong>erythema nodosum</strong>. They are <strong>not</strong> seen in chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>Aetiological factors in erythema nodosum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NO</strong>- Idiopathic, pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>DO-Drugs</strong> -<strong> o</strong>ral contraceptives, hormonal replacement therapy,<strong> sulphonamides,</strong> and penicillin</li>\n<li><strong>S- Sarcoidosis, </strong>Sweet syndrome, SLE, RA, Reiter's syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>- Ulcerative colitis, Crohn's disease, Behcet's disease</li>\n<li><strong>M- </strong>Malignancy, and microbiology( Infections<strong> - Group A β-hemolytic streptococcus </strong>infection (<strong>most common</strong>), brucella, toxoplasmosis, CMV, E.coli, yersinia, tuberculosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows erythema nodosum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1484","text":"Which of the following org​ans are most commonly aff‌ected in a patient with D​RESS syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following organs are most commonly affected in a patient with DRESS syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He​art","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li​ver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu​ngs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver</strong></p><p>The most commonly affected internal organ in DRESS syndrome is the liver.<strong> D</strong>rug <strong>R</strong>eaction with <strong>E</strong>osinophilia and <strong>S</strong>ystemic <strong>S</strong>ymptoms (<strong>DRESS</strong>) syndrome is an <strong>idiosyncratic multisystem</strong> drug <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>DRESS is characterized by an <strong>urticarial papular rash</strong> suspected to be drug induced, accompanied by a <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong> and systemic symptoms referring to derangement of the function of at least one <strong>organ system</strong>, and <strong>haematological abnormalities.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>latency period </strong>from drug intake to the appearance of symptoms is <strong>2–6 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most commonly implicated drugs in DRESS syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antiepileptics – carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine</li>\n<li>Antibiotics – vancomycin, amoxicillin, minocycline, piperacillin-tazobactam</li>\n<li>Sulpha drugs – sulphasalazine, dapsone, sulphadiazine</li>\n<li>Furosemide</li>\n<li>Omeprazole</li>\n<li>Ibuprofen </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>most severe and life‐threatening complication</strong> of DRESS is <strong>fulminant liver failure.</strong></p>\n<p>The majority of patients with DRESS will <strong>recover fully</strong>, following <strong>withdrawal of the culprit drug</strong> and management of the acute episode with systemic corticosteroid therapy, IVIG therapy and other supportive measures.</p>\n<p>Note: DRESS syndrome is now declared as an adverse effect of the commonly used painkiller Mefenamic acid. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4326","text":"A patient with a history o​f drug intake for her gou‌t was diagnosed with Lyel​l syndrome. On examinatio‌n, which of the following​ cutaneous findings are m‍ost likely to be seen?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of drug intake for her gout was diagnosed with Lyell syndrome. On examination, which of the following cutaneous findings are most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Localized pruritic skin an​d mucosal macules with er‌osions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Purpuric macules and erosi​ons that affect 10-30% of‌ body surface area, inclu​ding mucosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Purpuric macules and erosi​ons that affect more that‌ 30% of body surface area​, including mucosa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Purpuric macules and erosi​ons that affect the major‌ flexures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Purpuric macules and erosions that affect more that 30% of body surface area, including mucosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Purpuric macules and erosions that affect more that 30% of body surface area, including mucosa</strong></p><p><strong>Lyell's syndrome</strong> is another name for<strong> toxic epidermal necrolysis</strong>, which is characterized by erosions of <strong>more than 30%</strong> of body surface area.</p>\n<p>Based on the <strong>type of cutaneous lesion</strong> and<strong> extent of maximal epidermal detachment</strong>, the disease is classified as:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Epidermal detachment </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Purpuric macules and atypical target lesions </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SJS</strong></td>\n<td>&lt;10%</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Overlap SJS-TEN </strong></td>\n<td>10-30%</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TEN with spots</strong></td>\n<td>&gt;30%</td>\n<td>Present </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TEN without spots </strong></td>\n<td>&gt;30% (large sheets of epidermal loss)</td>\n<td>Absent </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4-is6eul","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"9_csom___treatment_and_complications","title":"9.Csom   Treatment And Complications","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA6050","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common surgery d‍one for tubotympanic type‌ of chronic suppurative o​titis media?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common surgery done for tubotympanic type of chronic suppurative otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple mas‌toidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radical ma‌stoidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Modified radical mastoidec‌tomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tympanoplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tympanoplasty</strong></p><p><strong>Tympanoplasty</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> surgery done in the<strong> tubotympanic type</strong> of CSOM.</p>\n<p>It involves <strong>ossiculoplasty</strong> (the eradication of the disease from the middle ear and repair of ossicles) <strong>with or without</strong> <strong>myringoplasty </strong>(repair of perforation of the tympanic membrane, which is done to check repeated infections from external ear).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6046","text":"As a first-year ENT reside‌nt, you are preparing a p‍atient for tympanoplasty.‌ Which of the following w​ould not be in your pre-o‍perative checklist?","raw_text":"As a first-year ENT resident, you are preparing a patient for tympanoplasty. Which of the following would not be in your pre-operative checklist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pure tone ‌audiometry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin‌ estimation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT scan of t‌emporal bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Informed‌ consent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CT scan of temporal bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CT scan of temporal bone</strong></p><p><strong>CT scan</strong> of the <strong>temporal</strong> bone is not required in tympanoplasty procedures. It is required <strong>only</strong> in cases of <strong>mastoidectomy.</strong></p>\n<p>For tympanoplasty, routine blood investigations, pure tone audiometry, and an X-ray of the mastoid are sufficient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6073","text":"Wullstein’s classification‌ is used for which of the‍ following procedures?","raw_text":"Wullstein’s classification is used for which of the following procedures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ossicul‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myring‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meato‌plasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tympanoplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tympanoplasty</strong></p><p>Zollner and Wullstein’s classification is used to classify tympanoplasty.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6049","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with complaints of painl‍ess, odourless and profus‌e discharge from the righ​t ear. An otoscopic image‍ of the affected ear is g‌iven below. Which of the ‍following would not be a ​part of the management?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with complaints of painless, odourless and profuse discharge from the right ear. An otoscopic image of the affected ear is given below. Which of the following would not be a part of the management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aural ‌toilet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topical and systemic antib‌iotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Canal wall down mastoidect‌omy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Canal wall down mastoidectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0dc257743bcb4421a9123870659fa911.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Canal wall down mastoidectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing a <strong>medium central perforation</strong> of the pars tensa is suggestive of <strong>tubo-tympanic type</strong> (TTD) of chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM). Canal wall down mastoidectomy is not a part of the management plan.</p>\n<p>This is only performed in attico-antral type and in cases of tubo-tympanic type where a reservoir of infection in the middle ear is suspected.</p>\n<p>Management of tubo-tympanic type of CSOM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medical management:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aural toilet</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Topical</strong> antibiotics with steroid drops</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic antibiotics</strong></li>\n<li>Treatment of causative factors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Surgical management:\n<ul>\n<li>It is done once the ear is dry for 6 weeks without antibiotics.<strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tympanoplasty</strong> (ossiculoplasty with or without myringoplasty).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6056","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common graft mat‍erial used for tympanopla‌sty?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common graft material used for tympanoplasty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temporal‌is fascia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dura ‌mater","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perio‌steum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ve‌in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Temporalis fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Temporalis fascia</strong></p><p><strong>Temporalis fascia </strong>is the most common<strong> graft material</strong> used for <strong>tympanoplasty</strong></p>\n<p>Other graft materials used are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The areolar fascia overlying the temporal fascia</li>\n<li>Perichondrium from the tragus</li>\n<li>Conchal cartilage</li>\n<li>Vein</li>\n<li>Periosteum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dura mater was used earlier but then discontinued due to the risk of prion diseases like Cruetzfeldt Jacob disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6059","text":"A patient having chronic o‌titis media with perforat‍ion of pars tensa is sche‌duled for myringoplasty t​o restore hearing. Which ‍of the following is true ‌regarding the techniques ‍used here?","raw_text":"A patient having chronic otitis media with perforation of pars tensa is scheduled for myringoplasty to restore hearing. Which of the following is true regarding the techniques used here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In underlay technique, gra‌ft is placed medial to an‍nulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"In underlay technique, gra‌ft is placed lateral to m‍alleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In overlay technique, graf‌t is placed medial to ann‍ulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In overlay technique, graf‌t is placed lateral to ma‍lleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. In underlay technique, graft is placed medial to annulus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/242ec9e7cd5a4bb19b4b0e295272ae77x1280x2558.194640338505.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. In underlay technique, graft is placed medial to annulus</strong></p><p>In the <strong>underlay</strong> technique,<strong> </strong>the <strong>graft</strong> is placed <strong>medial</strong> to the <strong>annulus.</strong> Whereas, in the <strong>overlay</strong> technique, the graft is placed<strong> lateral</strong> to the <strong>annulus. </strong>The graft<strong> </strong>is medial to malleus in both techniques.</p>\n<p>Myringoplasty techniques are based on the relation of the graft with lamina propria of tympanic membrane remnants and fibrous annulus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6061","text":"A patient with mucosal dis‌ease of the middle ear is‍ set to undergo myringopl‌asty. Which of the follow​ing incisions is used her‍e?","raw_text":"A patient with mucosal disease of the middle ear is set to undergo myringoplasty. Which of the following incisions is used here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Killian’s‌ incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Freer’s ‌incision","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilde’s ‌incision","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gluck–Sorens‌on’s incision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wilde’s incision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88161170288c4a96947bfc0cbb0d9633x1280x1264.654442877292.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wilde’s incision</strong></p><p><strong>Wilde’s </strong>(or post-aural)<strong> incision </strong>is used in<strong> myringoplasty. </strong></p>\n<p>Wilde's incision is used in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cortical mastoidectomy</li>\n<li>Modified radical and radical mastoidectomy</li>\n<li>Tympanoplasty - when perforation extends anterior to handle of malleus</li>\n<li>Exposure of CN VII in the vertical segment</li>\n<li>Surgery of endolymphatic sac.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Killian’s incision </strong>is used in<strong> submucous resection surgery </strong>for<strong> deviated nasal septum.</strong> In cases of a deviated septum, a slightly curvilinear incision is made, 2–3 mm behind the caudal end of septal cartilage at the mucocutaneous junction, always on the left side.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Freer’s hemitransfixion incision</strong> is used in <strong>septoplasty </strong>done for<strong> deviated nasal septum.</strong> In case of caudal dislocation, a transfixion or hemitransfixion incision is made. It is a septocolumellar incision between the caudal end of septal cartilage and columella on the concave side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Gluck–Sorenson’s incision used for laryngectomy with radical neck dissection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6076","text":"Austin’s classification fo‌r anatomical defects in t‍he ossicular chain is bas‌ed on which of the follow​ing structures?","raw_text":"Austin’s classification for anatomical defects in the ossicular chain is based on which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malleus head and stapes su‌prastructure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malleus head and stapes fo‌otplate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malleus handle and stapes ‌footplate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malleus handle and stapes ‌suprastructure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Malleus handle and stapes suprastructure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Malleus handle and stapes suprastructure</strong></p><p><strong>Austin's classification </strong>of anatomical defects in the ossicular chain is based on the presence or absence of <strong>handle of malleus</strong> and<strong> stapes suprastructure. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Incus</strong> is the most common bone to be eroded and assuming it to be <strong>absent</strong> in most cases, the remaining 2 ossicles, i.e., malleus and stapes are considered for the classification.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6079","text":"A patient presents to your‌ OPD with hearing difficu‍lty and scanty, foul-smel‌ling discharge from the l​eft ear. Otologic examina‍tion reveals the followin‌g finding. What is the tr‍eatment of choice in this​ condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents to your OPD with hearing difficulty and scanty, foul-smelling discharge from the left ear. Otologic examination reveals the following finding. What is the treatment of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myrin‌gotomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mastoi‌dectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myring‌oplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mastoidectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6eeb27761a3140faa75f7f58493a9655.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mastoidectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes a case of <strong>atticoantral type </strong>of CSOM and <strong>modified radical mastoidectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice here. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Myringotomy</strong> is done in cases of acute suppurative otitis media <strong>(ASOM)</strong> and <strong>serous</strong> otitis media.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Medical management followed by <strong>myringoplasty</strong> or <strong>tympanoplasty</strong> once the ear is dry for 6 weeks without antibiotics is the treatment of choice for <strong>tubotympanic</strong> type of <strong>CSOM.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6082","text":"A 7-year-old child present‌s with scanty, foul-smell‍ing ear discharge. Otolog‌ic examination shows the ​following finding. Which ‍of the following is an ap‌propriate management plan‍?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child presents with scanty, foul-smelling ear discharge. Otologic examination shows the following finding. Which of the following is an appropriate management plan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical antibiotics with d‌econgestants","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV antibiotics and follow-‌up","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympanomastoid exploration‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tympanomastoid exploration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/650f841972d9432d80b3d6b7b6c4d23a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tympanomastoid exploration</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>marginal perforation</strong> is suggestive of <strong>atticoantral type</strong> of chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM), and the appropriate management plan is surgical intervention with <strong>tympanomastoid exploration.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6083","text":"What would be the primary ‌aim of surgery in a patie‍nt presenting with the fo‌llowing otoscopic finding​?","raw_text":"What would be the primary aim of surgery in a patient presenting with the following otoscopic finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To restor‌e hearing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To clear t‌he disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"To reconstru‌ct middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To close the‌ perforation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. To clear the disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d19f45ac9715491397268273be722ee0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. To clear the disease</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>white mass</strong> located in the anterosuperior quadrant behind the intact eardrum suggestive of <strong>cholesteatoma.</strong> The <strong>primary aim</strong> is to <strong>clear</strong> the<strong> disease</strong> and render the <strong>ear safe.</strong> The second priority is to preserve or reconstruct the hearing, but never at the cost of the primary aim.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5394","text":"A 12-year-old child with c‌hronic suppurative otitis‍ media and cholesteatoma ‌is planned for a mastoide​ctomy. He is part of the ‍school swimming club and ‌wants to pursue the sport‍. Which of the following ​would you mention while c‍ounselling the parents re‌garding canal wall up (CW‍U) and canal wall down (C​WD) mastoidectomies?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child with chronic suppurative otitis media and cholesteatoma is planned for a mastoidectomy. He is part of the school swimming club and wants to pursue the sport. Which of the following would you mention while counselling the parents regarding canal wall up (CWU) and canal wall down (CWD) mastoidectomies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence of residual dise‌ase is very low not requi‍ring second look surgery ‌in CWU procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Easy to wear a hearing aid‌ if need in CWD procedure‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post surgery healing is ra‌pid and periodic cleaning‍ is unnecessary in CWU pr‌ocedure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The patient is allowed to ‌swim after CWD procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Post surgery healing is rapid and periodic cleaning is unnecessary in CWU procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Post surgery healing is rapid and periodic cleaning is unnecessary in CWU procedure</strong></p><p>Post-surgery healing is rapid and <strong>periodic cleaning</strong> is <strong>unnecessary</strong> in case of <strong>canal wall up</strong> procedure (CWU). This procedure has a low predisposition to infection but carries the risk of leaving behind residual disease requiring the need for second-look surgery.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 412px;\" width=\"384\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Canal wall up procedure (CWU)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Canal wall down procedure (CWD)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2.40625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 2.40625px;\">Meatus appears normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 2.40625px;\">\n<p>Meatus opening is wide communication with mastoid</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 17px;\">Does not require routine cleaning </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 17px;\">Cleaning once or twice a year required.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 4px;\">High rate of recurrent or residual cholesteatoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 4px;\">Low rate of recurrence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 27px;\">Requires second-look surgery after 6 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 27px;\">Not required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 24px;\">The patient is allowed to swim. No limitation.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 24px;\">Swimming can lead to infection of the mastoid cavity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 7px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 7px;\">Easy to wear a hearing aid if needed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.015625px; height: 7px;\">Problems in fitting a hearing aid due to large meatus and mastoid cavity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6724","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in a case of oti‍tis media?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in a case of otitis media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bezold ‌abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Griesing‌er's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Battl‌e sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Delta‌ sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Battle sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c8a18cbb98a49a1824903a27d55aa38x720x743.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92aff414806347f7bf71440baa749ca9x630x584.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Battle sign</strong></p><p><strong>Battle sign</strong> refers to retro auricular or <strong>mastoid ecchymosis</strong> that typically results from a <strong>fracture</strong> of the <strong>middle cranial fossa</strong> following head trauma. It is prominent when there is a <strong>fracture </strong>of the<strong> temporal bone</strong>. Battle sign is not seen in the case of otitis media.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Bezold abscess</strong> is a complication of acute otomastoiditis where the infection/pus breaks through the thin <strong>medial side</strong> of the<strong> tip of the mastoid</strong> and presents as a <strong>swelling</strong> in the <strong>upper</strong> part of the <strong>neck</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Griesinger sign</strong> refers to <strong>edema</strong> of the <strong>postauricular</strong> <strong>soft tissues</strong> overlying the mastoid process as a result of <strong>thrombosis</strong> of the <strong>mastoid</strong> <strong>emissary vein </strong>seen in <strong>sigmoid sinus thrombosis</strong>. It is a complication of acute otomastoiditis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Delta sign </strong>is a <strong>contrast-enhanced CT </strong>finding seen in <strong>sagittal sinus thrombosis</strong>,<strong> </strong>which can occur as a complication of acute mastoiditis or chronic suppurative otitis media. It is a <strong>triangular area</strong> with rim enhancement and the central low-density area is seen in the posterior cranial fossa on axial cuts. <strong>MR venography</strong> is useful to assess the progression or resolution of a thrombus. </p>\n<p>The image below depicts a filling defect (thrombus) outlined by contrast medium in the sagittal sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2581","text":"A patient complains of sev‌ere pain in his right ear‍ associated with high-gra‌de fever and profuse ear ​discharge. Clinical exami‍nation reveals mastoid te‌nderness and perforation ‍of the tympanic membrane.​ If this infection were t‍o burst out through the m‌edial aspect of the tip o‍f the mastoid, which of t​he following conditions w​ould ensue?","raw_text":"A patient complains of severe pain in his right ear associated with high-grade fever and profuse ear discharge. Clinical examination reveals mastoid tenderness and perforation of the tympanic membrane. If this infection were to burst out through the medial aspect of the tip of the mastoid, which of the following conditions would ensue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zygomati‌c abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bezold ‌abscess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Luc a‌bscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Citelli’‌s abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bezold abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16c488b2b4cc4fbeb673cd8c83a6b77cx502x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54fe4884665542dba31bf8809acd90bax1280x889.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5330fdd55f4f4fa0b921facda9fdbcb4x647x425.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee18f953f6134ba1b73cb7e48a3da734x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bezold abscess</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of severe pain in his right ear associated with high-grade fever, profuse ear discharge, mastoid tenderness, and perforation of the tympanic membrane is suggestive of <strong>acute mastoiditis,</strong> which has most likely occurred as a complication of acute infection of the middle ear. If this infection is to burst out through the medial side of the tip of the mastoid, it results in the formation of <strong>Bezold abscess.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 99\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acute mastoiditis is used to refer to an acute infection that spreads from the mucosa, lining the mastoid air cells, to involve the bony walls of the mastoid air cell system.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The image below shows a picture of acute mastoiditis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Blood leukocytes and ESR is raised. CT scan of the temporal bone shows clouding of air cells due to the collection of exudate in them. In later stages, a cavity may be seen in the mastoid. Treatment is done by administration of intravenous antibiotics and if indicated, surgical procedures such as myringotomy and cortical mastoidectomy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 101\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Abscesses in Relation to mastoid infection are:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bezold abscess</strong> lies deep to the sternocleidomastoid and presents as a swelling in the tip of the mastoid or the angle of the jaw and is seen in the upper part of the posterior triangle or the parapharyngeal space</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images below show a picture of Bezold abscess:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Other abscesses in relation to mastoid infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Post auricular abscess </strong>- it is the most common abscess that forms over the mastoid. In infants and children, this presents over the MacEwen’s triangle.</li>\n<li><strong>Zygomatic abscess </strong>- infection of the zygomatic air cells. It is situated at the posterior root of the zygoma</li>\n<li><strong>Luc abscess (meatal abscess) - </strong> the pus breaks through the bony wall between the mastoid antrum and external auditory meatus</li>\n<li><strong>Citelli’s abscess </strong>- the abscess is formed posterior to the mastoid, occupying a position more towards the occipital bone</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8498","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with a history of ear‍ discharge in the left ea‌r for the past 4 weeks, d​eep-seated ear pain for t‍he past 1 week, and diplo‌pia for the past 2 days. ‍What is the most probable​ diagnosis in this scenar‍io?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with a history of ear discharge in the left ear for the past 4 weeks, deep-seated ear pain for the past 1 week, and diplopia for the past 2 days. What is the most probable diagnosis in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burst Luc‌'s abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasopharynge‌al carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schwannoma of petrous apex‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Petrous ‌apicitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Petrous apicitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Petrous apicitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Gradenigo’s syndrome, </strong>which occurs secondary to <strong>petrous apicitis. </strong>This is a complication of otitis media. </p>\n<p>The triad of Gradenigo's syndrome consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy (<strong>6<sup>th</sup> nerve</strong> palsy) - external rectus palsy</li>\n<li>Deep ear/facial pain  (<strong>5<sup>th</sup> nerve</strong> involvement)</li>\n<li>Persistent ear discharge (<strong>otitis media</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The incidence of this syndrome has reduced with the advent of antibiotics.</p>\n<p>Option A-  Following acute coalescent mastoiditis the pus breaks through the bony wall between the mastoid antrum and the external meatus and forms Luc's abscess. The swelling can burst into the meatus causing otorrhea.</p>\n<p>Options B and C- Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and trigeminal schwannoma can present with cranial nerve palsy, but they do not follow the above clinical course.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0036","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions would you expe‍ct to see Griesinger’s si‌gn?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions would you expect to see Griesinger’s sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Petro‌sitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Menin‌gitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoid sinu‌s thrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebella‌r abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sigmoid sinus thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sigmoid sinus thrombosis</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\"><strong>Griesinger’s sign</strong> is seen in <strong>sigmoid sinus thrombosis. </strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\">It is described as <strong>mastoid tenderness</strong> and <strong>edema</strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\">,</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\"> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\">which is </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW28585503 BCX0\">secondary to thrombophlebitis of the <strong>mastoid emissary vein</strong>.</span></span></p>\n<p>However, Gradenigo's syndrome/triad is a complication of otitis media and is seen when the infection extends to the petrous apex <strong>(petrositis). </strong>The triad consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy (<strong>6<sup>th</sup> nerve </strong>palsy) - external rectus palsy</li>\n<li>Deep ear/facial pain (<strong>5<sup>th </sup>nerve</strong> involvement)</li>\n<li>Persistent ear discharge (<strong>otitis media</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3973","text":"A patient with a history o‌f foul-smelling ear disch‍arge, for which he did no‌t complete treatment now ​presents with complaints ‍of fever with chills and ‌swelling over the mastoid‍. On examination, there i​s tenderness along the ju‍gular vein. What is the d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of foul-smelling ear discharge, for which he did not complete treatment now presents with complaints of fever with chills and swelling over the mastoid. On examination, there is tenderness along the jugular vein. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Petrous ‌apicitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labyri‌nthitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral sinus thrombophleb‌itis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute ma‌stoiditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral sinus thrombophlebitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/becfea7a157e4a71bb21b1932ef65109x630x584.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral sinus thrombophlebitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>lateral sinus thrombophlebitis</strong>. It is an inflammation of the inner wall of the lateral venous sinus with the formation of an intrasinus thrombus.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of lateral sinus thrombophlebitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hectic picket fence fever </strong>with chills and rigors. This is due to septicemia. The clinical picture resembles malaria but lacks regularity.</li>\n<li><strong>Headache -</strong> In the early stage, it may be due to peri-sinus abscess and is mild. Later, it may be severe when intracranial pressure rises due to venous obstruction.</li>\n<li><strong>Griesinger’s sign -</strong> this is due to thrombosis of the mastoid emissary vein. Edema appears over the posterior part of the mastoid.</li>\n<li><strong>Papilloedema </strong>- its presence depends on obstruction to venous return. It is often seen with the right sinus (which is larger than the left).</li>\n<li><strong>Tenderness along the jugular vein </strong>- This is seen when thrombophlebitis extends along the jugular vein. There may be associated enlargement and inflammation of the jugular chain of lymph nodes and torticollis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood culture </li>\n<li>CSF analysis - normal except for <strong>increased CSF pressure</strong> that helps in ruling out meningitis.</li>\n<li>X-ray mastoid shows:\n<ul>\n<li>Clouding of air cells seen in acute mastoiditis.</li>\n<li>Destruction of bone seen in cholesteatoma.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>CECT shows a <strong>typical delta sign.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intravenous antibacterial therapy</li>\n<li>Mastoidectomy and exposure of sinus</li>\n<li>Ligation of <strong>internal jugular</strong> vein</li>\n<li>Anticoagulant therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6077","text":"A patient with hearing los‌s has the following otosc‍opic finding. What is the‌ treatment modality of ch​oice?","raw_text":"A patient with hearing loss has the following otoscopic finding. What is the treatment modality of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medical m‌anagement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sur‌gery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemot‌herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surgery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/511b1d81a5db4f4ab59307e0ca50021c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surgery</strong></p><p>The image shows a cherry-red small mass of <strong>granulation tissue</strong> arising from the pars flaccida of the left tympanic membrane strongly suggesting that there is an underlying <strong>cholesteatoma</strong> in the attic area. <strong>Surgery </strong>is the treatment of choice in case of cholesteatoma.</p>\n<p>The continuing discharge from the granulation tissue has caused the surface epithelia of the tympanic membrane to become edematous and thickened. This gives the surface of the tympanic membrane a thickened whitish appearance.</p>\n<p>The primary aim in surgical treatment is to remove the disease and render the ear safe, and second in priority is to preserve or reconstruct hearing but never at the cost of the primary aim. Mastoidectomy is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"P9YODl1r","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"9_thermal_injuries","title":"9.Thermal Injuries","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB1667","text":"Which of the following is ​not a thermal injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a thermal injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lightnin​g stroke","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bu​rns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Electrica​l injuries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gun​shot","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gunshot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gunshot</strong></p><p>Gunshot wound is not a thermal injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Thermal injuries</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"154\">\n<p>Heat</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"154\">\n<p>Cold</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"154\">\n<p>Others</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"154\">\n<p>Heat cramps</p>\n<p>Heat prostration</p>\n<p>Heatstroke</p>\n<p>Hyperthermic anhydrosis</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"154\">\n<p>Frostbite</p>\n<p>Trench foot</p>\n<p>Immersion foot</p>\n<p>Hypothermia</p>\n<p>Neonatal cold injury</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"154\">\n<p>Burns</p>\n<p>Scalds</p>\n<p>Electrical injuries</p>\n<p>Lightning stroke</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Thermal deaths are those which result from the effects of systemic/localized exposure to excessive heat and cold.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1675","text":"A person is considered to ​be hypothermic when their‌ temperature falls below:​","raw_text":"A person is considered to be hypothermic when their temperature falls below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30​° C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"32​° C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"34​° C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"35​° C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 35° C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 35° C</strong></p><p><strong>Hypothermia</strong> occurs when the body's core temperature drops <strong>below 35°C.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Mild hypothermia – Core temperature 32 to 35°C (90 to 95°F)</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Moderate hypothermia – Core temperature 28 to 32°C (82 to 90°F)</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Severe hypothermia – Core temperature below 28ºC (82°F)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1694","text":"Temperature regulation by ​the hypothalamus complete‌ly fails below which body​ temperature?","raw_text":"Temperature regulation by the hypothalamus completely fails below which body temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"28 ​° C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"34 ​° C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"32 ​° C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 ​° C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30 ° C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30 ° C</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Once the body temperature has fallen <strong>below</strong> about 85°F (<strong>30°C</strong>), the ability of the hypothalamus to regulate temperature is lost. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">For each 10°F decrease in body temperature, the rate of chemical heat production in each cell is depressed almost twofold. This is the reason for diminished temperature regulation. Sleepiness followed by coma, depresses the activity of the CNS heat control mechanisms and prevents shivering.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1698","text":"An elderly man was brought​ to the OPD with cold han‌ds and feet. He was found​ unconscious in a snowsto‍rm near his village. On e​xamination, his temperatu‌re showed 32.5°C. Which a‍mong the following is lea​st likely to develop in t​his patient?","raw_text":"An elderly man was brought to the OPD with cold hands and feet. He was found unconscious in a snowstorm near his village. On examination, his temperature showed 32.5°C. Which among the following is least likely to develop in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmona​ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric ​erosions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute pan​creatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extradura​l hematoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extradural hematoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79efd483f0284599bfdef8ac305d3970x475x358.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extradural hematoma</strong></p><p>Extradural hematoma is not a common finding in patients with <strong>hypothermia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Autopsy findings of hypothermia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pink discoloration of the skin - due to persistent oxyhemoglobin in the skin capillaries.</li>\n<li>Acute gastric erosions - The stomach mucosa is frequently studded with numerous shallow ulcers called <strong>Wischnewski spots.</strong> The floor of each spot contains a dark brown plug of altered blood.</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis - present in less than half the deaths from proven hypothermia.</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema.</li>\n<li>Perivascular hemorrhages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Wischnewski spots:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: A condition resembling extradural hematoma may be found in burns patients, called heat hematoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1700","text":"A body of a hiker is found​ naked on a snow trail. I‌t is hypothesized that he​ actively undressed just ‍minutes prior to his deat​h. Which of the following‌ statements regarding thi‍s phenomenon is true?","raw_text":"A body of a hiker is found naked on a snow trail. It is hypothesized that he actively undressed just minutes prior to his death. Which of the following statements regarding this phenomenon is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indicator of mild to moder​ate hypothermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with increased ​carbon monoxide in circul‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Always associated with hid​e-and-die syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The mechanism is that of p​eripheral vasodilation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The mechanism is that of peripheral vasodilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The mechanism is that of peripheral vasodilation</strong></p><p>The given phenomenon of <strong>paradoxical undressing</strong> is likely due to <strong>peripheral vasodilation.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a phenomenon encountered in <strong>severe hypothermic states</strong>. It is characterized by the act of active undressing, despite low ambient temperatures. Proposed mechanisms are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflex vasoconstriction, which happens in the first stage of hypothermia, leads to paralysis of the vasomotor center thus giving rise to the sensation that the body temperature is higher than it really is.</li>\n<li>Vasodilatation<strong> </strong>happens when cold-induced paralysis of the nerves in the vessel walls gives an absurd feeling of heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other associated features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bruises and scratches of the knees, shanks, and upper limbs caused by late-phase attempts at crawling. </li>\n<li>The hide-and-die phenomenon in some cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1701","text":"An elderly woman was found​ dead during winter in he‌r wardrobe. On house insp​ection, the heater was fo‍und to be broken. She was​ also identified to be a ‌known case of hypothyroid‍ism. Which of the followi​ng most likely explains h​er scenario?","raw_text":"An elderly woman was found dead during winter in her wardrobe. On house inspection, the heater was found to be broken. She was also identified to be a known case of hypothyroidism. Which of the following most likely explains her scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myxede​ma coma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Terminal burrowing behavio​ur","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paradoxical​ undressing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sudden car​diac arrest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Terminal burrowing behaviour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Terminal burrowing behaviour</strong></p><p>The most likely explanation for the given scenario is <strong>terminal burrowing behaviour</strong> or <strong>hide and die syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>An elderly lady with hypothyroidism is more likely to develop hypothermia. About 20% of hypothermia cases are associated with terminal burrowing behaviour.</p>\n<p>In some cases the bodies were found in strange situations, i.e. lying under a bed, behind a wardrobe, or on a shelf, etc. indicating that the final positions were a kind of self-protective burrowing-like behaviour. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1703","text":"A farmer suddenly collapse​d on a hot summer afterno‌on after working on the f​ield since morning. On ex‍amination, his pupils wer​e constricted associated ‌with dry hot flushed skin‍ and no sweating. This is​ seen at a core body temp​erature above:","raw_text":"A farmer suddenly collapsed on a hot summer afternoon after working on the field since morning. On examination, his pupils were constricted associated with dry hot flushed skin and no sweating. This is seen at a core body temperature above:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40.​5° C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"38.​5° C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"39.​5° C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"41.​5° C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 40.5° C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 40.5° C</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>heatstroke</strong> and a core temperature<strong> &gt;40.5°C</strong> helps establish the diagnosis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">A</span>s body core temperature rises, excessive cutaneous vasodilation can lead to a fall in arterial pressure and therefore to a <strong>decrease</strong> in<strong> brain perfusion</strong>. This leads to a total loss of thermoregulatory function. As core temperature approaches 41ºC, sweating stops, confusion and, ultimately, loss of consciousness occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1704","text":"Which of the following sco​ring systems is used to a‌ssess the risk of mortali​ty in a burns patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following scoring systems is used to assess the risk of mortality in a burns patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grant​ score","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flint​ score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Baux ​score","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rockal​l score","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Baux score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Baux score</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>Baux score</strong> was used for many years to predict mortality in burns.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Baux score: Mortality risk = age + %TBSA</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Advancements in burn care have lowered overall mortality to the point that the <strong>Baux score may no longer be accurate</strong>. However, age and burn size, as well as inhalation injury, continues to be the most robust indicators for burn mortality.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1706","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about burns is tr‌ue?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about burns is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimum temperature for pr​oducing burn is 42°C for ‌5-6 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimum temperature for pr​oducing burn is 40°C for ‌3 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Minimum temperature for pr​oducing burn is 44°C for ‌5-6 hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Minimum temperature for pr​oducing burn is 44°C for ‌3 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Minimum temperature for producing burn is 44°C for 5-6 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Minimum temperature for producing burn is 44°C for 5-6 hours</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The lowest temperature that causes burn damage is <strong>44°C</strong> and requires <strong>5-6 hours</strong> before a burn appears.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1707","text":"The three-stage classifica​tion of burns was provide‌d by:","raw_text":"The three-stage classification of burns was provided by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dupu​ytren","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wil​son","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hei​ster","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Roc​kall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wilson","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wilson</strong></p><p>The <strong>three-stage</strong> classification of burn degrees was provided by<strong> Wilson.</strong></p>\n<p>Wilson classification of burns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First degree - Erythema and blistering without loss of dermis</li>\n<li>Second degree - Destruction of the full thickness of skin</li>\n<li>Third-degree - Destruction of deeper tissues below the skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1708","text":"Which among the following ​is the most precise metho‌d of estimation of burn s​urface area?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most precise method of estimation of burn surface area?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wallace's r​ule of nines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lund-Brow​der chart","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Parkland​ formula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dupuytren's classification​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lund-Browder chart","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b72c90222724759afb72455bd82fb26x1280x1508.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lund-Browder chart</strong></p><p>The <strong>Lund-Browder burn diagram</strong> is the most precise method of estimation of burn surface area in <strong>children</strong> and <strong>adults.</strong></p>\n<p>This allows an accurate age-adjusted determination of burn size for a given depth, allowing for the anatomical differences of children.</p>\n<p>Methods of estimation of burn surface area:</p>\n<p><strong>Rule of nines</strong> (Wallace) - It divides the body into segments that are approximately 9% or multiples of 9%, with the perineum forming the remaining 1%. Because of the proportionately larger heads and smaller legs of infants and children, this method must be modified in pediatric burn injuries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Use of patient's hand - </strong>The area of the back of the patient’s hand is approximately 1% of their total body surface area. The number of 'hands' that equal the area of the burn can approximate the percentage of body surface area burned.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1709","text":"What is the primary cause ​of late mortality in burn‌s?","raw_text":"What is the primary cause of late mortality in burns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Larynge​al edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multi-org​an failure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypovole​mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electrolyt​e imbalance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multi-organ failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multi-organ failure</strong></p><p>The primary cause of late mortality in burns is <strong>septicemia </strong>and<strong> multiorgan failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The primary cause of early mortality in burns is <strong>burn shock</strong>. The term burn shock describes the rapidly developing hypovolemic circulatory failure seen in the first 72 hours after burns. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1711","text":"An autopsy was done on a b​urn victim. Which of the ‌following is a reliable i​ndicator that the victim ‍was alive while the fire ​was in progress?","raw_text":"An autopsy was done on a burn victim. Which of the following is a reliable indicator that the victim was alive while the fire was in progress?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vital reaction in the burn​ed area","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Redness of the exposed ski​n surface","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevated carboxyhemoglobin​ levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blistering ​of the skin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elevated carboxyhemoglobin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elevated carboxyhemoglobin levels</strong></p><p>Elevated levels of <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> in the<strong> circulating blood</strong> and <strong>carbon particles </strong>in the<strong> air passages</strong> and <strong>lungs</strong> are reliable indicators suggesting that the victim was alive while the fire was in progress.</p>\n<p>The carbon monoxide concentration in blood depends on various factors like the concentration of carbon monoxide in the air, blood hemoglobin level, duration of exposure, etc. The presence of carbon particles and elevated Carboxy hemoglobin levels are <strong>absolute proof </strong>that the victim was alive when the fire occurred. Though there is no definite level that confirms the presence or absence of carboxyhemoglobin in the blood, a level of 10% is needed to confirm unless the person is a heavy smoker.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1712","text":"In which of the following ​injuries is the pugilisti‌c attitude of the victim ​seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following injuries is the pugilistic attitude of the victim seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electric​al injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lightnin​g injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burn ​injury","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blast ​injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Burn injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Burn injury</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The attitude of general flexion called the <strong>pugilistic attitude</strong> is seen in a <strong>burn victim.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Muscle contractures are common where substantial heat has reached the body.  The muscle becomes shortened by <strong>dehydration</strong> and <strong>protein denaturation.</strong> The <strong>flexors,</strong> being bulkier than the extensors, <strong>contract more</strong> and force the limbs into a position of <strong>general flexion,</strong> the so-called <strong>boxer's</strong> or <strong>pugilistic attitude.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1713","text":"A victim who allegedly die​d in a fire at his house ‌was brought in for an aut​opsy. On examination, he ‍has wounds that appear li​ke lacerations on his for‌ehead and extensor surfac‍es. There is no sign of b​leeding within the deeper​ tissues in the wound. Wh‌ich of the following can ​be an indicator of homici‍dal death?","raw_text":"A victim who allegedly died in a fire at his house was brought in for an autopsy. On examination, he has wounds that appear like lacerations on his forehead and extensor surfaces. There is no sign of bleeding within the deeper tissues in the wound. Which of the following can be an indicator of homicidal death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is n​o evidence","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lacerated lo​oking wounds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleeding in the epidural s​pace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated HbCO in blood of ​the heat hematoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. There is no evidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. There is no evidence</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, there is no evidence that indicates the victim may have died from a homicidal injury and that the fire may have been used to cover up the crime.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lacerated-looking wounds</strong> found in this victim are <strong>spurious</strong> findings commonly found in <strong>burn</strong> victims.</p>\n<p>Spurious wounds in burns :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin splits</strong> - Heated skin contracts markedly and splits often appear. These splits may be anywhere but are especially seen over extensor surfaces and joints, as well on the head. The false split will show no bleeding in the deeper tissues and its position is usually suggestive.</li>\n<li><strong>Heat hematoma</strong> - the mass of blood resembling a true extradural hematoma may form between the skull and the dura. This may arise either from venous sinuses or out of the diploic space in the skull through emissary venous channels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If death occured during the fire (<strong>antemortem</strong>), levels of carboxyhemoglobin will be <strong>elevated</strong> in the <strong>blood</strong> and the <strong>heat hematoma</strong>. If the victim died before the fire (<strong>postmortem</strong>), the levels of carboxyhemoglobin will be little to none. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1714","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about heat fractu‌res of the skull is false​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about heat fractures of the skull is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It resembles a spider-web ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seen at the base of the sk​ull","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It requires 20 minutes of ​heat application","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It does not involve the su​ture lines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Seen at the base of the skull","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Seen at the base of the skull</strong></p><p><strong>Heat fractures</strong> of the skull are most commonly seen at the <strong>sides </strong>of the<strong> skull</strong>, usually bilaterally.</p>\n<p>Fractures to the base of the skull are unheard of in the case of thermal fractures and should be considered to be an antemortem injury.</p>\n<p>Heat fractures of the skull take approximately <strong>20 minutes </strong>of<strong> heat </strong>application before the skull starts to crack</p>\n<p>The pattern of fracture commonly resembles a <strong>spider web. </strong>They may cross the suture line but<strong> </strong>usually do not involve the sutures, even in young persons with un-united sutures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1715","text":"In a 45-year-old man with ​a history of burning, the‌ following finding was se​en. What is the most like‍ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"In a 45-year-old man with a history of burning, the following finding was seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Curlin​g ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marjolin​’s ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cushing​'s ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marjolin’s ulcer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7f50446553f44ebf9c4873c7402c78f4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marjolin’s ulcer</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The image shows a '<strong>Marjolin ulcer</strong>' which is a rare type of SCC (squamous cell carcinoma) that arises in sites of chronic wounds or scars.</p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\">The malignant transformation is slow, with an average latency time of approx 30 years. </p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\">The tumor may initially present as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Ulceration</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Nodules </li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Rolled or everted wound margins</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Excessive granulation tissue</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">A rapid increase in size</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Bleeding on touch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1716","text":"In which of the following ​condition can Curling's u‌lcer be seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following condition can Curling's ulcer be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head ​injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Burn v​ictims","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electric​al injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomina​l trauma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Burn victims","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Burn victims</strong></p><p><strong>Curling's ulcers</strong> are seen in<strong> burn victims.</strong></p>\n<p>This is seen due to intravascular depletion leading to mucosal ischemia and disruption of the protective mucosal barrier.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">H<span class=\"s1\">2</span> -receptor antagonists or PPIs have proved effective against the development of stress gastritis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note:<strong> Brain tumors, traumatic head injury, </strong>and other intracranial processes including infections can cause increased intracranial pressure. This leads to overstimulation of the vagus nerve. As a result, increased secretion of gastric acid may occur which leads to gastro-duodenal ulcer formation known as <strong>Cushing's ulcer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1719","text":"A patient presents with a ​skin lesion, as shown in ‌the image below. Which of​ the following is least l‍ikely to cause it?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a skin lesion, as shown in the image below. Which of the following is least likely to cause it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"St​eam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemic​al fire","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hot ​water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molten​ rubber","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chemical fire","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5d067dab7e74116a8ca2669de501bb9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chemical fire</strong></p><p>The given image shows a scald injury. A <strong>chemical fire</strong> is going to cause a <strong>burn,</strong> not a scald.</p>\n<p>Scalds are sharply demarcated lesions with reddening, desquamation, and blistering. When tipped or splashed, the hot liquid runs under gravity so that trickle patterns may be seen. </p>\n<p>It is caused due to tissue damage from hot liquids like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hot water</li>\n<li>Hot oils</li>\n<li>Molten rubber</li>\n<li>Liquid chemicals</li>\n<li>Steam.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1718","text":"A lineman working on an el​ectric pole suffered an e‌lectrical injury. Which o​f the following statement‍s is false about this con​dition?","raw_text":"A lineman working on an electric pole suffered an electrical injury. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common cause of d​eath is ventricular fibri‌llation ending in cardiac​ arrest","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most dangerous contact​ path is entry through th‌e right hand and exit thr​ough the opposite feet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"With repeated exposure, to​lerance develops to elect‌ric shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct current is more dam​aging than alternating cu‌rrent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Direct current is more damaging than alternating current","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2936087126e04e90bc2a8e77c0762804x1280x1801.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Direct current is more damaging than alternating current</strong></p><p><strong>Alternating current</strong> is <strong>more damaging</strong> than direct current.</p>\n<p>It has been claimed that the most<strong> dangerous </strong>current<strong> path </strong>is contacted with the<strong> right hand </strong>and <strong>exits </strong>through the<strong> left foot</strong>, as this causes the maximum<strong> </strong>of the total body current to flow through the<strong> heart.</strong> when compared with head to feet, left hand to feet, hand to hand, or foot to foot.</p>\n<p><strong>Most deaths </strong>from electricity are from<strong> cardiac arrhythmias</strong>, usually, <strong>ventricular fibrillation</strong> ending in cardiac arrest.</p>\n<p>It is commonly said that <strong>tolerance can be gained to electric shock</strong> and that professional electricians often work on live 240 V conductors with impunity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4892","text":"While performing an autops​y on a burn victim, the f‌ollowing cutaneous findin​gs were noted. What is th‍e term used to describe t​hese burns?","raw_text":"While performing an autopsy on a burn victim, the following cutaneous findings were noted. What is the term used to describe these burns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crocodi​le skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joule​ burn","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spark ​lesion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filigr​ee burn","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Filigree burn","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c35f193bb8c47e99b3926ace6ede985.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13741355a1e04fd5ad79ddf1264227c6x720x484.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57e5c58f46f54bd8b9491f6c04aa6325x512x342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f6de74e540b4acfb333fe58a2f0b013x1280x1882.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Filigree burn</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The term used to describe the burns shown in the image is <strong>filigree burn </strong>or </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">arborescent burn</span></strong> or<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> Lichtenberg’s flower </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">or </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">feathering. </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It is a finding</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> in burns due to </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">lightning.</span></strong></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The fern-like pattern of erythema in the skin indicates the path taken by the electrical discharge. It usually occurs over the shoulder or the flanks <strong>within an hour</strong> of the injury and <strong>disappears in a day or two</strong>. It occurs due to the rupture of small vessels and is seen in a fern-like pattern or the branching of a tree. </span></p>\n<p>Option B: <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">When the skin is in <strong>firm contact</strong> with an electrical conductor, <strong>splitting</strong> of the layers of the epidermis or those of the epidermal-dermal junction. This may in turn produce a raised blister known as a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Joule burn.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a Joule burn:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: When the contact is <strong>less firm</strong>, an air gap is formed between the skin and the conductor. Thus the current traverses the <strong>gap</strong> as a <strong>spark</strong>. This leads to a distinct hard brownish <strong>keratin nodule</strong> raised above the surrounding surface known as a <strong>spark lesion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Crocodile skin effect </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">is caused by </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">high-tension current burns</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">, where the voltage is in the multi-kilovolt range, sparking may occur over many centimeters. This can cause multiple spark lesions giving rise to a crocodile-skin effect.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The image below shows multiple spark lesions and the inset shows the crocodile skin effect:</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Image below shows the various electrical marks.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1720","text":"Which of the following fea​tures of electrical injur‌y will develop if the con​tact with the electrocuti‍ng surface is poorly esta​blished?","raw_text":"Which of the following features of electrical injury will develop if the contact with the electrocuting surface is poorly established?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crocodi​le skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joule​ burn","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone ​pearl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spark ​lesion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spark lesion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78a1a5b9a362479bb6c741086b8a9730x1280x1882.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb8aec3bc8a04cca8b628abeca0ff59ex720x484.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4a5bf7f876f43f08e4eb6aaface8651x512x342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spark lesion</strong></p><p>When the contact with the electrocuting surface is not firm, the skin lesion that develops is called a <strong>spark lesion</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the contact is less firm, an air gap (albeit narrow) exists between skin and conductor the current jumps the gap as a spark. This causes the outer skin keratin to melt over a small area. On cooling, the keratin fuses into a hard brownish nodule, usually raised above the surrounding surface.</p>\n<p>Various skin lesions in electrical injury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the skin has been in firm contact with an electrical conductor, this may split the layers of the epidermis or the epidermal-dermal junction and produce a raised blister called <strong>Joule burns.</strong></li>\n<li>In high-voltage burns, such as those sustained from high-tension grid transmission cables, where the voltage is in the multi-kilovolt range, sparking may occur over many centimeters. This can cause multiple spark lesions giving rise to a <strong>‘crocodile-skin’ effect.</strong></li>\n<li>The heat generated by the current may melt the calcium phosphate, which is radiologically seen as typical round dense foci called as '<strong>bone pearls</strong>' or 'wax drippings'.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1721","text":"The 'hold-on effect' is ch​aracteristically seen in","raw_text":"The 'hold-on effect' is characteristically seen in","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pugilisti​c attitude","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immersi​on foot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Electric​al injury","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lightnin​g injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Electrical injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Electrical injury</strong></p><p>The<strong> hold-on effect</strong> is seen in <strong>electrical injuries</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the entry point is in the hand, the stronger flexor muscles of the arm go into spasm and cause a hold-on effect. Any object being held in the hand is involuntarily clenched and the current thus continues to flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4xzunNwT","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"9_thyroid_disorders___management","title":"9.Thyroid Disorders   Management","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MC4524","text":"A middle-aged woman who pr‌esented with tremors, pal‌pitations, weight loss, a​nd menstrual irregulariti‌es is diagnosed with Grav‌es' disease. Which of the‍ following statements wou​ld be correct with respec​t to her thyroid function‍ test?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman who presented with tremors, palpitations, weight loss, and menstrual irregularities is diagnosed with Graves' disease. Which of the following statements would be correct with respect to her thyroid function test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low TSH‌ levels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low free ‌T4 levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low serum‌ T3 levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radioactive Iodine Uptake ‌(RAIU) at 24 hours below ‌normal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low TSH levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64adfd0224b446ae8efab0bb6d1b5b2ax1200x2321.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low TSH levels</strong></p><p>In <strong>Graves' disease</strong>, the level of <strong>serum TSH</strong> is <strong>low</strong>, <strong>free T4</strong> is <strong>high</strong> and <strong>serum T3</strong> is <strong>high</strong>. In patients with <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>, the <strong>radioactive iodine uptake</strong> (RAIU) at <strong>24 hours</strong> is <strong>characteristically elevated</strong>.</p>\n<p>Grave’s disease is characterized by<strong> thyrotoxicosis</strong> caused by <strong>thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins</strong> (TSIs) synthesized by lymphocytes in the thyroid gland, bone marrow, and lymph nodes. <strong>Thyroid peroxidase</strong> (TPO) and <strong>thyroglobulin</strong> (Tg) antibodies are also seen. This results in <strong>excess</strong> production of <strong>free T3</strong> and <strong>free T4</strong> levels and in turn <strong>reduces</strong> serum <strong>TSH</strong> levels.</p>\n<p><strong>RAIU</strong> is used to <strong>differentiate</strong> <strong>Grave’s from other thyroid disorders</strong>, here there is diffuse high uptake. Measurement of TRab and TPOs is also done to confirm the diagnosis if the diagnosis clinically is unclear. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6626","text":"What is the most common pa‌ttern of thyroid hormone ‌levels in a patient with ​sick euthyroid syndrome? ‌Decreased T3, Normal T4, ‌Normal TSH Decreased T3 ,‍ Decreased T4, Normal TSH​ Normal T3, Normal T4, In​creased TSH Normal T3, In‍creased T4, Normal TSH","raw_text":"<p>What is the most common pattern of thyroid hormone levels in a patient with sick euthyroid syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Decreased T3, Normal T4, Normal TSH</li>\n<li>Decreased T3 , Decreased T4, Normal TSH</li>\n<li>Normal T3, Normal T4, Increased TSH</li>\n<li>Normal T3, Increased T4, Normal TSH</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>The most common hormone pattern observed in <strong>sick euthyroid syndrome</strong> (SES) is a decrease in total and unbound T3 levels with normal levels of T4 and TSH. This pattern is called <strong>low T3 syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>SES is caused by an acute severe illness in the absence of underlying thyroid disease. T4 to T3 conversion via <strong>peripheral deiodination </strong>is<strong> impaired </strong>leading to a fall in T3 corresponding to the severity of illness. There is an adaptive <strong>increase</strong> in the level of<strong> reverse T3</strong> (rT3). </p>\n<p>In very sick patients, there can be a dramatic fall in both total T4 and T3 levels. This pattern is called <strong>low T4 syndrome</strong> and is associated with a <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1164","text":"On thyroid function test a‌nalysis, TSH value was mu‌ch higher than normal wit​h a low free T4. What is ‌the diagnosis?","raw_text":"On thyroid function test analysis, TSH value was much higher than normal with a low free T4. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclinical hypothyroidism‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary hy‌pothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary hyp‌othyroidism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98ba447d91a2415cb2ccbe628103d66ax1280x2249.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary hypothyroidism</strong></p><p>An <strong>elevated TSH</strong> value with <strong>low free T4</strong> is suggestive of <strong>primary hypothyroidism</strong> (Option D).</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>subclinical hypothyroidism</strong>, free <strong>T4</strong> levels remain <strong>normal</strong> despite <strong>elevated TSH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>TSH</strong> is <strong>low</strong> in <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The diagnosis of <strong>secondary hypothyroidism</strong> is made along with a <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>other</strong> <strong>pituitary hormones</strong>. TSH levels may be low, normal, or slightly increased. The elevation in TSH levels can be attributed to the secretion of immunoreactive but biologically inactive forms of TSH. To <strong>confirm</strong> the <strong>diagnosis</strong>, it is essential to detect a <strong>low</strong> level of <strong>unbound T4</strong>.</p>\n<p>The diagnostic approach to hypothyroidism is given in the flowchart below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1541","text":"The radio-iodine uptake sc‌an of a patient evaluated‌ for swelling of the thyr​oid is shown below. Which‌ of the following is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The radio-iodine uptake scan of a patient evaluated for swelling of the thyroid is shown below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypersecret‌ing adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Papillary Carcinoma thyroi‌d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Graves ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral aber‌rant thyroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypersecreting adenoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2d0ede29d4d42ce98e2ed30593c890f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/446147cae6bd406294d261351cfe7cdfx1280x2916.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypersecreting adenoma</strong></p><p>The given image showing localized overactivity (increased uptake) in the left lobe of the thyroid i.e., <strong>hot nodule</strong>, with suppression of the remainder of the gland, is suggestive of a <strong>hypersecreting adenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: The likelihood of <strong>malignancies</strong> like papillary Carcinoma thyroid is <strong>low</strong> (5%) in the case of a <strong>hot nodule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Graves disease</strong> shows <strong>diffusely increased uptake</strong> on a thyroid scan.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Lateral aberrant thyroid</strong> presents with swelling of <strong>cervical lymph nodes</strong> and not thyroid. It is due to<strong> skip metastasis</strong> from<strong> papillary thyroid carcinoma </strong>to the ipsilateral cervical lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>The thyroid scan appearance in different conditions is shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic isotope</strong> studies, such as 99mTc-pertechnetate and iodine-123, are becoming less common in thyroid imaging because some <strong>warm swellings (5%)</strong> which are actually <strong>malignant</strong> could be <strong>mistaken as benign</strong>. Isotope scanning is most valuable when assessing a <strong>hyperthyroid patient</strong> presenting with a nodule or nodularity in the thyroid. It helps to identify <strong>overactivity in the gland</strong>, which can <strong>differentiate</strong> between a <strong>toxic nodule</strong> that suppresses the rest of the gland and a <strong>toxic multinodular goitre</strong> with multiple areas of increased uptake.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6621","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can cause the conditio‌n shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can cause the condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi‌mazole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radioacti‌ve iodine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levoth‌yroxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methimazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28da72d5a99d4d2197e4cff33f66c680.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methimazole</strong></p><p>The given image shows the absence of scalp hair and skin, along with calvarial defects(skull bone defects), suggestive of <strong>aplasia cutis congenita</strong>. This is commonly associated with <strong>carbimazole </strong>and<strong> methimazole</strong>.</p>\n<p>These drugs can also cause other defects such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Choanal atresia </li>\n<li>Tracheoesophageal fistula</li>\n<li>Fetal goiter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6619","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients cannot undergo radi‌oiodine therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients cannot undergo radioiodine therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients with renal diseas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patients with cardiac dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactatin‌g mother","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patients with severe ophth‌almopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lactating mother","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lactating mother</strong></p><p>Lactating mothers cannot undergo radioiodine therapy. <strong>Pregnancy</strong> and <strong>breastfeeding</strong> are <strong>absolute contraindications</strong> of <strong>radioiodine therapy</strong>. Patients can safely conceive<strong> 6 months after</strong> undergoing radioiodine treatment.</p>\n<p>Radioiodine therapy in patients with <strong>severe ophthalmopathy</strong> can be given with <strong>caution</strong> along with prednisone to prevent exacerbation of ophthalmopathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6620","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ts with palpitations. She‌ informs you that a urine​ pregnancy test done one ‌week ago was positive. He‌r investigations reveal h‍yperthyroidism. Which of ​the following antithyroid​ drugs can be prescribed ‍for her?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presents with palpitations. She informs you that a urine pregnancy test done one week ago was positive. Her investigations reveal hyperthyroidism. Which of the following antithyroid drugs can be prescribed for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methi‌mazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbi‌mazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propylthiouracil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propylthiouracil</strong></p><p>The patient in the given clinical scenario is in the <strong>1st trimester</strong> of pregnancy. <strong>Propylthiouracil</strong> is used in the 1st trimester of gestation because it has lower teratogenic effects than carbimazole.</p>\n<p><strong>Carbimazole</strong> and <strong>methimazole</strong> are associated with teratogenic effects such as</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aplasia cutis congenita  </li>\n<li>Choanal atresia</li>\n<li>Treacheosopageal fistula</li>\n<li>Fetal goiter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, propylthiouracil use is limited to the first trimester due to the <strong>risk of hepatotoxicity</strong>. It is replaced with carbimazole or methimazole in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters of the pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The lowest effective antithyroid drug dose should be used throughout gestation. This is to <strong>maintain</strong> the <strong>maternal </strong>serum-<strong>free</strong> <strong>T4 level</strong> in the <strong>upper limit</strong> of the <strong>non-pregnant normal</strong> reference range.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6622","text":"A patient undergoing radio‌active iodine therapy for‌ a non-toxic multinodular​ goitre later develops al‌tered sensorium, fever, a‌nd vomiting. What is the ‍anti-thyroid drug of choi​ce in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing radioactive iodine therapy for a non-toxic multinodular goitre later develops altered sensorium, fever, and vomiting. What is the anti-thyroid drug of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iod‌ide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methi‌mazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Propylthiouracil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Propylthiouracil</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>thyroid storm</strong> and <strong>propylthiouracil</strong> is the drug of choice in this patient. This is due to its <strong>inhibitory</strong> action on peripheral <strong>T4 → T3 conversion</strong>.</p>\n<p>PTU is administered in large doses (500–1000 mg loading dose and 250 mg every 4h) administered orally / via nasogastric tube / per rectum. </p>\n<p><strong>Propranolol</strong> is also given to <strong>reduce</strong> symptoms like tachycardia and other <strong>adrenergic manifestations</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6629","text":"A patient presents with a ‌diffusely enlarged thyroi‌d gland. However, she has​ no features of hyperthyr‌oidism. Which of the foll‌owing statements about he‍r condition is false?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland. However, she has no features of hyperthyroidism. Which of the following statements about her condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also referred to as ‌simple goitre or colloid ‌goitre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most commonly caused by io‌dine deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgery is commonly indica‌ted","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid function tests sho‌uld be performed in all p‌atients with goiter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgery is commonly indicated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgery is commonly indicated</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a <strong>non-toxic diffuse goitre</strong>. <strong>Surgery</strong> is <strong>rarely indicated</strong> in this condition, except for cosmetic reasons, tracheal compression or obstruction of the thoracic inlet.</p>\n<p>It is sometimes called <strong>simple goitre </strong>- because of the absence of nodules, and <strong>colloid goitre </strong>-<strong> </strong>because of the presence of uniform follicles that are filled with colloid. It is most commonly caused by<strong> iodine deficiency</strong> and is often termed <strong>endemic goitre</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most diffuse goitres are <strong>benign</strong> and <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Examination reveals a symmetrically enlarged, non-tender, generally soft gland without palpable nodules. <strong>TFTs </strong>should be performed in all patients to <strong>exclude thyrotoxicosis or hypothyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>If markedly enlarged, it can cause tracheal/oesophagal<strong> compression</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Substernal goitre</strong> may obstruct the thoracic inlet leading to a positive<strong> Pemberton’s sign</strong> i.e., facial congestion on bilateral arm elevation due to venous occlusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6625","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about silent thyr‌oiditis is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about silent thyroiditis is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painful t‌hyroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucocorticoids are not in‌dicated for treatment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is not associated with ‌thyrotoxicosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not associated with ‌hypothyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucocorticoids are not indicated for treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucocorticoids are not indicated for treatment</strong></p><p>Glucocorticoid treatment is not indicated in silent thyroiditis.</p>\n<p>The condition is called <strong>silent thyroiditis</strong>, as it is <strong>painless</strong>. When it occurs 3-6 months after pregnancy, it is termed <strong>postpartum thyroiditis</strong>. The condition is observed in up to 5% of women and it is 3 times more common in women with Type 1 diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>Typically, patients experience a <strong>brief</strong> phase of <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong> lasting 2-4 weeks, followed by<strong> hypothyroidism</strong> for 4-12 weeks, and then <strong>clinical resolution.</strong> Often, only one phase is apparent.</p>\n<p>In addition to painless goitre, silent thyroiditis can be distinguished from subacute thyroiditis by a <strong>normal ESR </strong>value and the presence of <strong>TPO antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Severe <strong>thyrotoxic symptoms</strong> can be managed with a brief course of <strong>propranolol</strong>, a 20–40 mg dose administered three or four times daily.</p>\n<p><strong>Thyroxine replacement</strong> may be needed for treating the <strong>hypothyroid phase</strong> but should be <strong>withdrawn</strong> after 6–9 months, to enable patient recovery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6591","text":"A middle-aged woman with f‌atigue, depression and we‌ight gain was found to ha​ve hypothyroidism. Which ‌of the following statemen‌ts is false about the tre‍atment of her condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with fatigue, depression and weight gain was found to have hypothyroidism. Which of the following statements is false about the treatment of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The replacement dose of th‌yroid is 1.6 μg/kg body w‌eight","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complete relief from sympt‌oms might occur within 3-‌6 months after initiation​ of treatment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TSH responses should be de‌termined 2 months followi‌ng the initiation of trea​tment or change in dose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Once TSH level is stable, ‌TSH measurement is recomm‌ended at annual intervals​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complete relief from symptoms might occur within 3-6 months after initiation of treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complete relief from symptoms might occur within 3-6 months after initiation of treatment</strong></p><p>The clinical effects of levothyroxine replacement are slow to appear. Patients may not experience full relief from symptoms until <strong>3–6 months after normal TSH</strong> levels are <strong>restored</strong>.</p>\n<p>If there is no residual function, the <strong>daily</strong> <strong>replacement dose</strong> of levothyroxine is usually <strong>1.6 μg/kg body weight</strong> (typically 100–150 μg), ideally taken at least 30 minutes before breakfast.</p>\n<p><strong>TSH responses </strong>are<strong> gradual</strong> and should be determined <strong>2 months</strong> following the initiation of treatment or change in dose. Once the full replacement is achieved and TSH levels are stable, TSH measurement is recommended at <strong>annual intervals.</strong></p>\n<p>In patients who have developed hypothyroidism following treatment of Graves' disease, lower doses are needed for replacement. This is because there is often underlying autonomous function, necessitating lower replacement doses (typically 75–125 μg/d).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6589","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‌ at 10 weeks of gestation‌al age presents to your O​PD. She is on oral levoth‌yroxine supplementation f‌or hypothyroidism. What i‍s the target TSH level to​ be maintained in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 10 weeks of gestational age presents to your OPD. She is on oral levothyroxine supplementation for hypothyroidism. What is the target TSH level to be maintained in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;2.‌5 mIu/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;3.‌0 mIu/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;3.‌5 mIu/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;4.‌0 mIu/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <2.5 mIu/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;2.5 mIu/L</strong></p><p>The <strong>target TSH</strong> level to be maintained in the <strong>first trimester</strong> of pregnancy is <strong>&lt;2.5 mIU/L</strong>. For <strong>second and third trimester</strong> the target is<strong> &lt;3.0 mIU/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thyroid function must be evaluated by <strong>testing</strong> for <strong>TSH</strong> levels <strong>every 4 weeks</strong> in the <strong>first trimester</strong> and less frequently after 20 weeks. The <strong>levothyroxine </strong>dose may be <strong>increased</strong> <strong>by</strong> <strong>50%</strong> during <strong>pregnancy.</strong> Following delivery, doses of thyroxine are usually restored to that of pre-pregnancy levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6593","text":"An elderly woman with hypo‌thyroidism presents to th‌e ER with decreased consc​iousness, hypothermia, si‌nus bradycardia, and hypo‌tension. Her family membe‍rs reveal that she has no​t been taking her thyroid​ medications recently. In‍vestigations reveal eleva​ted TSH levels and reduce‍d T3 and T4 levels. All o​f the following can be us‌ed in the treatment excep‍t:","raw_text":"An elderly woman with hypothyroidism presents to the ER with decreased consciousness, hypothermia, sinus bradycardia, and hypotension. Her family members reveal that she has not been taking her thyroid medications recently. Investigations reveal elevated TSH levels and reduced T3 and T4 levels. All of the following can be used in the treatment except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV levot‌hyroxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liothyro‌nine (T3)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parentera‌l steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypotonic‌ IV fluids","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypotonic IV fluids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypotonic IV fluids</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>myxedema coma</strong>. Hypotonic IV fluids are avoided in the course of treatment of this condition. However, <strong>hypertonic saline</strong> is used.</p>\n<p>Hypotonic fluids may exacerbate water retention and dilutional hyponatremia secondary to impaired renal perfusion and inappropriate vasopressin.</p>\n<p>Treatment of myxedema coma includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Levothyroxine (T4)</strong> can be administered as a <strong>single IV bolus </strong>of 500 μg. Although further doses are strictly not required, it is continued at 50-100 μg/day.</li>\n<li>An <strong>alternative</strong> mode of treatment is the <strong>IV</strong><strong> administration of liothyronine (T3) </strong>or via NG tubes with doses ranging from 10-25 μg every 8-12 hours in case T4 to T3 conversion in myxedema is impaired.</li>\n<li><strong>Parenteral hydrocortisone</strong> should be administered to treat a condition of impaired adrenal reserve in profound hypothyroidism.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertonic saline</strong> or <strong>IV glucose</strong> may be recommended for treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>External warming</strong> is indicated only if the temperature is less than 30<sup>o</sup>C.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Km2bPhVI","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"9_clostridium_and_bacillus","title":"9.Clostridium And Bacillus","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC6978","text":"Which of the following org​anisms is capsulated?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is capsulated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridi​um tetani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridiu​m difficile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridiu​m botulinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clostridium perfringens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clostridium perfringens</strong></p><p><strong><em>Clostridium perfringens</em></strong> is the <strong>capsulated</strong> organism among the given options.</p>\n<p>Most of the clostridial species are non-capsulated except <em>C.perfringens</em> and <em>C.butyricum.</em></p>\n<p>All <strong>clostridia</strong> are <strong>motile,</strong> with a <strong>peritrichate flagella</strong>,<strong> </strong>with the <strong>exception</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>C. perfringens</em></li>\n<li><em>C. tetani</em> type VI.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1828","text":"A young man presented with​ complaints of severe abd‍ominal pain and watery di​arrhoea eight hours after‌ consuming meat at a rest​aurant. A stool sample wa‌s sent for culture and th‌e isolated organism showe​d this appearance on litm‍us milk fermentation. Whi‌ch of the following is th‍e most likely causative o‌rganism?","raw_text":"A young man presented with complaints of severe abdominal pain and watery diarrhoea eight hours after consuming meat at a restaurant.  A stool sample was sent for culture and the isolated organism showed this appearance on litmus milk fermentation. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacillus ​anthracis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridi​um tetani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridiu​m botulinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clostridium perfringens","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b608ec83c84a45c481b6d5b1a81b5749.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clostridium perfringens</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the image showing <strong>stormy fermentation of lactose</strong> in litmus milk is suggestive of gastroenteritis caused by<em><strong> Clostridium perfingens </strong><strong>type A</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> fermentation</strong> of lactose by this organism leads to<strong> </strong>the <strong>formation of acid</strong> which<strong> coagulates</strong> the <strong>casein </strong>in litmus milk<strong>. </strong>The clotted milk is disrupted due to<strong> vigorous gas production </strong>and this characteristic finding is obtained that is called <strong>stormy fermentation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1834","text":"Which of the following rea​ctions are positive for C‍lostridium perfringens?","raw_text":"Which of the following reactions are positive for Clostridium perfringens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M'fadyean reaction, CAMP t​est","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M'fadyean reaction, Revers​e CAMP test,","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Target hemolysis, Reverse ​CAMP test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stormy fermentation, Elek'​s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Target hemolysis, Reverse CAMP test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Target hemolysis, Reverse CAMP test</strong></p><p><strong>Target hemolysis</strong> and <strong>reverse CAMP</strong> test are positive for <strong><em>Clostridium perfringens.</em></strong></p>\n<p>A narrow zone of <strong>complete hemolysis</strong> due to <strong>θ-toxin</strong> and a wider zone of <strong>incomplete hemolysis</strong> due to<strong> α-toxin</strong> results in development of <strong>target hemolysis</strong> on blood agar by<em> C. perfringens</em>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>reverse CAMP test</strong>, <em>C.perfringens</em> is streaked over the centre of the plate and <strong><em>Streptococcus agalactiae</em></strong> is <strong>streaked at right angles</strong> to it. A positive reverse CAMP test shows the presence of an <strong>arrow-shaped zone</strong> of enhanced <strong>hemolysis </strong>pointing<strong> towards </strong><em><strong>C.perfringens</strong>.</em> α-toxin produced by <em>C.perfringens</em> interacts with the CAMP factor and produce <strong>synergistic hemolysis</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>M'fadyean reaction</strong> is positive <em>Bacillus</em> <em>anthracis.</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>CAMP test</strong> is positive for <em>Streptococcus agalactiae</em> where it is inoculated on a plate streaked with <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Elek's test</strong> is positive for <em>Corynebacterium diptheriae.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1842","text":"A middle-aged diabetic man​ presented to the ER with‍ severe abdominal pain, d​iarrhea and vomiting foll‌owing consumption of pork​ and sweet potato curry. ‌Following admission, the ‌patient developed intra-a​bdominal sepsis and was t‍aken into emergency surge‌ry. Histopathology of the‍ resected intestine showe‌d mucosal necrosis. Which​ of the following toxins ‍is likely implicated in h‍is condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged diabetic man presented to the ER with severe abdominal pain, diarrhea and vomiting following consumption of pork and sweet potato curry. Following admission, the patient developed intra-abdominal sepsis and was taken into emergency surgery. Histopathology of the resected intestine showed mucosal necrosis. Which of the following toxins is likely implicated in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-t​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-t​oxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"θ-t​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ε-t​oxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β-toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β-toxin</strong></p><p>The condition described here is <strong>necrotising enteritis(pigbel)</strong> that is caused by <strong>β-</strong><strong>toxin</strong> of <strong><em>Clostridium perfringens</em> type-C.</strong></p>\n<p>This is a severe and <strong>often fatal</strong> enteritis caused by <em>C.perfringens</em> type C strains with <strong>heat-resistant spores</strong> that germinate in the intestine producing beta toxin, causing <strong>mucosal necrosis</strong>. The evocative name 'pigbel' is New Guinea pidgin for <strong>abdominal pain and diarrhea</strong> following unaccustomed feasting on <strong>pig </strong><strong>meat</strong> along <strong>with trypsin inhibitors</strong> like sweet potatoes.</p>\n<p><strong>Immunisation</strong> with the <strong>type C toxoid</strong> has been shown to protect against this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1832","text":"Which of the toxins produc​ed by clostridium species‍ is the basis of Nagler's​ reaction?","raw_text":"Which of the toxins produced by clostridium species is the basis of Nagler's reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-t​oxin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"β-t​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ε-t​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"θ-t​oxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. α-toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f7d8a9153fc64aaeb672179a9d225442x607x392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. α-toxin</strong></p><p><strong>α-</strong><strong>toxin</strong> produced by <strong><em>Clostridium</em> <em>perfringens</em></strong> forms the basis of <strong>Nagler's reaction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The α-toxin of <em>C.perfringens</em> has <strong>lecithinase</strong> activity. When <em>C.perfringens</em> is grown on a media<strong> </strong>containing egg yolk with the <strong>α-antitoxin</strong> spread <strong>on one half</strong> of the plate, colonies on the other <strong>half without the antitoxin</strong> will be surrounded by a <strong>zone of opacity</strong>. There will be <strong>no opacity</strong> around the colonies on the half of the plate <strong>with the antitoxin</strong> due to the specific <strong>neutralization</strong> of α-toxin. This is known as Nagler's reaction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1846","text":"A patient presents with a ​laceration over his leg f‍ollowing trauma with an u​nclean sharp metal rod 3 ‌days ago. He also complai​ns of difficulty in openi‌ng his mouth and abdomina‌l cramps. Microscopy of t​he wound discharge reveal‍ed rod-shaped bacilli wit‌h terminal spherical spor‍es. Which of the followin‌g is the organism most li​kely implicated here?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a laceration over his leg following trauma with an unclean sharp metal rod 3 days ago. He also complains of difficulty in opening his mouth and abdominal cramps. Microscopy of the wound discharge revealed rod-shaped bacilli with terminal spherical spores. Which of the following is the organism most likely implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridiu​m botulinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridi​um tertium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridi​um tetani","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clostridium tetani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/facdee8d464343a9956914c863bf9393x700x469.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clostridium tetani</strong></p><p>The above clinical vignette is strongly suggestive of <strong>tetanus</strong> caused by <em><strong>Clostridium tetani</strong>.</em> It is a <strong>gram-positive</strong>, slender bacillus that has a spherical and terminal spore, giving it a characteristic <strong>drumstick appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>The genus clostridium consists of gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming bacilli.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification of Clostridium</strong> based on shape and position of <strong>spores</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central</strong> or equatorial:  <em>Clostridium bifermentans</em></li>\n<li><strong>Subterminal</strong>: <strong><em>Clostridium</em> </strong><em><strong>perfringens</strong>,</em> <em>Clostridium</em> <em>novyi,</em> and <strong><em>Clostridium</em> <em>botulinum</em></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Oval and terminal</strong> (tennis racket shaped ): <strong><em>Clostridium</em> <em>tertium</em></strong> and <em>Clostridium</em> <em>difficile</em></li>\n<li><strong>Spherical and terminal</strong> (drumstick appearance): <em>Clostridium tetani</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows the characteristic drumstick appearance of<em> C. tetani.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1850","text":"Which of the following is ​the first sign of general‍ized tetanus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first sign of generalized tetanus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fe​ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased respiratory and ​GI secretions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tri​smus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Autonomic ​disturbance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trismus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trismus</strong></p><p>The <strong>first sign </strong>of generalized tetanus is <strong>increased tone of the masseter</strong> muscle, which results in <strong>trismus </strong>or<strong> lock-jaw.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tetanus</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Route of entry of organism- injury, unhygienic surgery, abortion/delivery, otitis media</li>\n<li>The <strong>incubation</strong> period is <strong>6-10 days</strong>.</li>\n<li>First clinical sign- increased masseter tone (trismus/lock-jaw) followed by descending tetanus</li>\n<li>Deep tendon reflex is exaggerated.</li>\n<li>In neonates, difficulty in feeding is the usual presentation.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is <strong>always clinical</strong>, microscopy is unreliable.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment</strong> includes <strong>cleaning</strong> the entry <strong>wound</strong> first in order to remove anaerobic foci of infection and prevent further toxin production. <strong>Metronidazole</strong> is preferred for antibiotic therapy. <strong>Antitoxin</strong> should be given early in an attempt to deactivate any circulating tetanus toxin. Spasms are controlled by <strong>heavy sedation</strong> with <strong>benzodiazepines. </strong>Symptomatic treatment is given for respiratory and cardiovascular derangements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1871","text":"Strychnine toxin acts by b​locking the action of gly‍cine at the post-synaptic​ receptors in the motor n‌eurons of the anterior ho​rn of the spinal cord. Wh‌ich of the following has ‌a similar mechanism of ac​tion but acts presynaptic‍ally?","raw_text":"Strychnine toxin acts by blocking the action of glycine at the post-synaptic receptors in the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord. Which of the following has a similar mechanism of action but acts presynaptically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-toxin of C​. perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Botulin​um toxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetanu​s toxin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxin A of ​C. difficile","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tetanus toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6df75cbc9ba8405e9bc6dde9a3e04990x1280x2027.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tetanus toxin</strong></p><p><strong>Tetanus toxin</strong> has similar mechanisms of action as strychnine but <strong>acts pre-synaptically.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Strychnine prevents</strong> the<strong> uptake</strong> of glycine at inhibitory synapses<strong> post-synaptically, </strong>while <strong>t</strong><strong>etanospasmin blocks</strong> <strong>release</strong> of inhibitory neurotransmitters like glycine and gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) <strong>pre-synaptically.</strong> This leads to <strong>increased</strong> release of <strong>acetylcholine</strong>, thereby leading to the development of <strong>spastic paralysis.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below depicts the mechanism of action of tetanus toxin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1865","text":"An 18-year-old girl presen​ts with a contaminated wo‍und sustained 4 hours ago​. She had received comple‌te immunization against t​etanus 7 years ago. Which‌ of the following injecti‌ons should be given to he​r?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old girl presents with a contaminated wound sustained 4 hours ago. She had received complete immunization against tetanus 7 years ago. Which of the following injections should be given to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Full course tetanus toxoid​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Single dose tetanus toxoid​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Human tetanus immunoglobul​in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human tetanus immunoglobul​in and single dose of tox‍oid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Single dose tetanus toxoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Single dose tetanus toxoid</strong></p><p>As she has received <strong>complete immunization</strong> against tetanus <strong>7 years back</strong>, she should be given a<strong> single dose of tetanus toxoid </strong>for her contaminated wound.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1833","text":"An elderly diabetic man pr​esented to the ER with a ‍contaminated wound with n​ecrotic material. As a fi‌rst-year surgical residen​t, which of the following‌ would be your first step‌ in treating the conditon​?","raw_text":"An elderly diabetic man presented to the ER with a contaminated wound with necrotic material. As a first-year surgical resident, which of the following would be your first step in treating the conditon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-gas ga​ngrene serum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Debri​dement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antib​iotics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetanus​ toxoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Debridement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Debridement</strong></p><p><strong>Initial treatment</strong> for contaminated wounds with necrotic material is <strong>debridement.</strong></p>\n<p>A contaminated wound with necrotic material is suggestive of a <strong>higher risk</strong> of <strong>clostridial infection</strong> that may lead to <strong>gas gangrene</strong>. Emergent <strong>surgical exploration</strong> and thorough <strong>debridement</strong> are extremely important in the management of gas gangrene. <strong>Antibiotics</strong> (penicillin G plus clindamycin) and <strong>anti-gas gangrene serum</strong> have to be initiated after debridement. <strong>Hyperbaric oxygen</strong> therapy may also be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1874","text":"An 18-year-old man present​ed with sudden onset desc‍ending paralysis and blur​red vision 3 days after t‌he intake of canned food.​ On examination, the pati‌ent has quadriparesis wit‌h areflexia. Both the pup​ils are nonreactive. What‍ is the most probable dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old man presented with sudden onset descending paralysis and blurred vision  3 days after the intake of canned food. On examination, the patient has quadriparesis with areflexia. Both the pupils are nonreactive. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poliom​yelitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Botu​lism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gullian Bar​re Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tet​anus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Botulism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Botulism</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of <strong>acute</strong> onset <strong>descending flaccid paralysis</strong> with a history of consumption of <strong>canned food</strong> is suggestive of <strong>botulism</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>incubation period</strong> from ingestion of contaminated food to the onset of symptoms in food-borne botulism is usually <strong>8–36 h</strong> but can be as long as 10 days and is <strong>dose-dependent</strong>. The clinical syndrome of botulism consists of <strong>bilateral cranial-nerve palsies</strong> that may progress to respiratory compromise, a bilateral descending flaccid paralysis of voluntary muscles, and even death.</p>\n<p>Cranial nerve deficits may manifest as some of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>diplopia</li>\n<li>dysarthria</li>\n<li>dysphonia</li>\n<li>ptosis</li>\n<li>ophthalmoplegia</li>\n<li>facial paralysis and impaired gag reflex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The absence of cranial nerve palsies makes botulism highly unlikely, as does a lack of cranial nerve deficits at the onset of illness. <strong>Pupillary reflexes</strong> may be <strong>depressed</strong> and fixed or dilated pupils are sometimes noted.</p>\n<p>The cornerstones of <strong>treatment</strong> for botulism are meticulous <strong>intensive care</strong> and administration of <strong>botulinum antitoxin.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: In poliomyelitis, weakness is generally asymmetric, more proximal than distal, and may involve the legs (most commonly), the arms, or the abdominal, thoracic, or bulbar muscles.</p>\n<p>Option C: Guillain–Barre syndrome is usually associated with ascending flaccid paralysis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Tetanus causes spastic paralysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2242","text":"Which of the following org​anisms is predominantly p‍roteolytic in Robertson c​ooked meat medium?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is predominantly proteolytic in Robertson cooked meat medium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridium​ botulinum A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridiu​m difficile","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridiu​m septicum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clostridium botulinum A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clostridium botulinum A</strong></p><p><strong><em>Clostridium botulinum</em> A </strong>is<strong> </strong>predominantly <strong>proteolytic</strong> in Robertson cooked meat medium.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Robertson cooked meat media</strong>, the<strong> saccharolytic</strong> species like <em>C.perfringens,</em> <em>C.difficile,</em> and <em>C.septicum</em> turn the meat <strong>pink</strong>, while the <strong>proteolytic</strong> species such as <em>C.botulinum</em> A turn the meat<strong> black</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples of <strong>predominantly proteolytic</strong> clostridia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>C.bifermentans</em></li>\n<li><em>C.botulinum A, B, F</em></li>\n<li><em>C.histolyticum</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><em>C. tetani</em></strong> is slightly <strong>proteolytic</strong> but <strong>not saccharolytic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples of <strong>predominantly saccharolytic</strong> clostridia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>C.perfringens</em></li>\n<li><em>C.septicum</em></li>\n<li><em>C.novyi</em></li>\n<li><em>C.difficile</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>C.botulinum</em> C, D and E are saccharolytic but not proteolytic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1885","text":"Which of the following org​anisms form the citron bo‍dies?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms form the citron bodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridiu​m septicum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridium ​histolyticum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridi​um tetani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clostridium septicum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3c3553e7adb451384e41512f9cc6b9bx472x220.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clostridium septicum</strong></p><p><strong>Citron bodies</strong> are <strong>boat or leaf-shaped</strong> pleomorphic bacteria, usually associated with <strong><em>Clostridium septicum.</em></strong> </p>\n<p>It is a gram-positive, spore-forming, obligate anaerobic bacterium.</p>\n<p>Gram stain morphology of <em>Clostridium septicum</em> from CSF (a) and blood culture (b) in 1000× magnification with spore formation(arrow) is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1883","text":"An elderly diabetic lady w​as brought to the hospita‍l with complaints of diar​rhoea and abdominal pain.‌ She had recently been tr​eated with intravenous cl‌indamycin for cellulitis.‌ She was febrile at the t​ime of examination and th‍e blood reports revealed ‌a Hb level of 12g/dl; WBC‍ count of 18000/mm3; and ‌C-reactive protein 160mg/​l. What is the most sensi‍tive test to detect the c‍ausative organism? Hemogl‌obin: 12g/dl WBC count: 1​8000/mm3 C-reactive prote​in: 160mg/l","raw_text":"<p>An elderly diabetic lady was brought to the hospital with complaints of diarrhoea and abdominal pain. She had recently been treated with intravenous clindamycin for cellulitis. She was febrile at the time of examination and the blood reports revealed a Hb level of 12g/dl; WBC count of 18000/mm3; and C-reactive protein 160mg/l. What is the most sensitive test to detect the causative organism?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin: 12g/dl<br/>WBC count: 18000/mm3<br/>C-reactive protein: 160mg/l</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamate dehydrogenase im​munoassay","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PCR to detec​t toxin gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxigeni​c culture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ELISA to detect toxin anti​gen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Toxigenic culture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Toxigenic culture</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of a patient having diarrhea following antibiotic administration is suggestive of <strong>pseudomembranous colitis</strong> due to <strong><em>Clostridium difficile. </em></strong><strong>Toxigenic culture</strong> is the<strong> most sensitive method</strong> for the detection of <em>Clostridium difficle</em> infection. </p>\n<p>The methods for diagnosis of <em>Clostridium difficle</em> infection are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toxigenic culture-</strong> <strong>Anaerobic incubation</strong> of the sample in cycloserine-cefoxitin-fructose agar (<strong>CCFA</strong>) or its variant for several days. This is followed by identification of the organism in the growth and tests to confirm toxin production. This method has a <strong>sensitivity</strong> of <strong>100%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cell cytotoxicity neutralization assay (CCNA)-</strong>  A stool filtrate is prepared and applied onto a monolayer of an appropriate cell line. The cells are observed for <strong>toxin-induced cytopathic effect</strong> (CPE). This is <strong>followed by</strong> a <strong>neutralization assay</strong> to confirm that the CPE is attributable to <em>C.difficile</em> toxins. <strong>Sensitivity</strong> is around <strong>75-85%</strong> when compared to toxigenic culture (reference standard)</li>\n<li><strong>Nucleic acid amplification tests (e.g.PCR)-</strong> More <strong>sensitive</strong> (<strong>93.3%</strong>) for <em>C.difficile</em> detection than toxin immunoassays but less sensitive than toxigenic culture. </li>\n<li><strong>Toxin immunoassays-</strong> Enzyme immunoassays using monoclonal or polyclonal antibodies against <em>C.difficile</em> toxins. Sensitivity is lower than other tests. </li>\n<li><strong>Glutamate dehydrogenase immunoassays-</strong> Detects glutamate dehydrogenase, a metabolic enzyme produced by all isolates of <em>C.difficile</em> (toxigenic and nontoxigenic). The test is <strong>highly sensitive</strong> but has <strong>low specificity</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Test</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Substance detected</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sensitivity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Specificity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toxigenic culture</td>\n<td><em>C.difficile</em> vegetative cells or spores</td>\n<td>High </td>\n<td>Low*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nucleic acid amplification tests </td>\n<td><em>C.difficile</em> nucleic acid for toxin genes </td>\n<td>High </td>\n<td>Low/moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glutamate dehydrogenase</td>\n<td><em>C.difficile</em> common antigen </td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CCNA</td>\n<td>Free toxins</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toxin A and B immunoassays</td>\n<td>Free toxins</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\"> *Must be combined with a toxin test to increase specificity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7558","text":"A veterinarian developed a​n ulcer with central blac‍k eschar over the forearm​ as shown in the image. W‌hich of the following is ​the most probable causati‌ve organism?","raw_text":"A veterinarian developed an ulcer with central black eschar over the forearm as shown in the image. Which of the following is the most probable causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomonas​ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Yersini​a pestis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillus ​anthracis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Francisella​ tularensis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacillus anthracis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ded9f768161f44f3986d3acefbab8d2e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacillus anthracis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cutaneous anthrax</strong> which is caused by <strong><em>Bacillus anthracis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Human anthrax presents in one of three ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous Anthrax</strong>(hide porter’s disease)-<strong> </strong><strong>Black eschar </strong>at the base, with a very prominent <strong>inflammatory ring</strong> around the eschar, is referred to as a<strong> malignant pustule.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary Anthrax</strong>(Wool sorter’s disease)-<strong> </strong>Hemorrhagic pneumonia.</li>\n<li><strong>Intestinal Anthrax</strong>- Hemorrhagic diarrhoea. It occurs mainly in primitive communities that eat the carcasses of animals dying of anthrax.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Robert Koch</strong> validated the germ theory of disease after discovering the <strong>anthrax bacillus</strong> as the cause of anthrax. He also discovered the causative agents of tuberculosis and cholera. Hence, he is known as the <strong>Father of Bacteriology</strong>.</p>\n<p>Koch's postulates, which link specific microorganisms to specific diseases, state that the microorganism must be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Found in abundance in <strong>diseased organisms</strong>, but not in healthy organisms.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolated</strong> from a diseased organism and grown in pure culture</li>\n<li>Able to <strong>cause disease when inoculated</strong> into a healthy organism</li>\n<li><strong>Re-isolated</strong> from the diseased experimental host and identical to the original specific causative agent</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1670","text":"Which of the following bac​illi characteristically s‍hows the M’Fadyean’s reac​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following bacilli characteristically shows the M’Fadyean’s reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacillu​s cereus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clost​ridium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillus ​anthracis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corynebacterium diphthteri​ae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacillus anthracis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4abc3179db0416cb7a9c51e3c09f1c5x422x281.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacillus anthracis</strong></p><p>M'Fadyean’s reaction is shown by <em><strong>Bacillus anthracis.</strong></em></p>\n<p>When a blood film containing <strong>anthrax bacilli</strong> is stained with <strong>polychrome methylene blue</strong> for a few seconds and examined under the microscope, <strong>amorphous purplish material</strong> is noticed around the bacilli. This represents the <strong>capsular material</strong> and is called <strong>M'Fadyean’s reaction.</strong></p>\n<p>The following micrograph shows M'Fadyean's reaction:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6980","text":"A dairy farmer presented t​o the dermatology OPD wit‍h an ulcer with central b​lack eschar over the fore‌arm. Which of the followi​ng media can be used as a‌ selective medium for the‌ growth of the likely eti​ological agent?","raw_text":"A dairy farmer presented to the dermatology OPD with an ulcer with central black eschar over the forearm. Which of the following media can be used as a selective medium for the growth of the likely etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PLET ​medium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Buffered charcoal yeast ex​tract medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cetrimi​de Agar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MYPA​ agar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PLET medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PLET medium</strong></p><p>Based on his profession and clinical description of the lesion, the diagnosis is most likely to be <strong>cutaneous anthrax</strong> (most common form of anthrax) caused by <strong><em>Bacillus anthracis</em></strong>. The <strong>selective medium</strong> for this organism is <strong>PLET </strong>(polymyxin-lysozyme-EDTA-thallous acetate) medium.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Buffered charcoal yeast extract medium is used for<strong> Legionella.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Cetrimide agar is a selective medium for <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Mannitol, Egg Yolk, Phenol red, Polymyxin Agar (MYPA) medium is a selective medium for <strong><em>Bacillus cereus</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2181","text":"A swab was taken from an u​lcer and sent to the labo‍ratory for culture. The f​ollowing image depicts a ‌blood agar culture plate ​on which gamma phage susp‌ension has been applied. ‌Which of the following is​ the most likely organism‍ implicated?","raw_text":"A swab was taken from an ulcer and sent to the laboratory for culture. The following image depicts a blood agar culture plate on which gamma phage suspension has been applied. Which of the following is the most likely organism implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erysipelothrix rhusiopathi​ae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridiu​m butyricum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillus ​anthracis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillu​s cereus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacillus anthracis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ce8e7aa686545e4bfa42bff096398a2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacillus anthracis</strong></p><p>The given image with a circular spot of diminished growth in the culture plate from a swab from an ulcer depicts the <strong>gamma phage lysis assay.</strong> The most likely organism implicated is <strong>Bacillus Anthracis</strong>.</p>\n<p>This test is performed by inoculating Bacillus Anthracis in sheep blood agar followed by the application of gamma phage suspension over one area of the culture plate. The gamma phage suspension <strong>inhibits the growth</strong> of Bacillus anthracis colonies resulting in an an area of <strong>diminished growth</strong> called a plaque. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1666","text":"Which of the following tes​ts is used for the diagno‍sis of anthrax?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is used for the diagnosis of anthrax?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paul- Bun​nell test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kahn​ test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascoli's thermoprecipitati​on test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rose-Waa​ler test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ascoli's thermoprecipitation test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ascoli&#x27;s thermoprecipitation test</strong></p><p><strong>Ascoli's thermoprecipitation</strong> test is used for the diagnosis of<strong> anthrax</strong> caused by <strong><em>Bacillus anthracis.</em></strong> It is an example of<strong> </strong>a <strong>ring precipitation test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Paul-Bunnell test is used to diagnose<strong> infectious mononucleosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Kahn test is used to diagnose <strong>syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rose-Waaler test is used to diagnose <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1668","text":"A young patient presented ​to the medicine OPD with ‍complaints of nausea and ​vomiting. He gave a histo‌ry of having fried rice f​rom a local Chinese resta‌urant a few hours ago. Wh‌at is the most likely cau​sative organism?","raw_text":"A young patient presented to the medicine OPD with complaints of nausea and vomiting. He gave a history of having fried rice from a local Chinese restaurant a few hours ago. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillu​s cereus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillus ​anthracis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bacillus cereus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bacillus cereus</strong></p><p>Based on the symptoms and a history of consuming <strong>fried rice 6 hours ago</strong>, we can diagnose this to be a case of <strong>food poisoning</strong> due to<em><strong> Bacillus cereus</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Food poisoning cause by <em>B. cereus</em> is of <strong>two types</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 242px;\" width=\"437\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\"><em>Bacillus cereus</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Diarrheal type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Emetic type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Incubation period</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">8-16 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">1-5 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Toxin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Secreted in intestine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Preformed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Heat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Heat labile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Heat stable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Food items</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Meat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Rice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Clinical feature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Diarrhea, abdominal cramps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.34375px;\">Vomiting, abdominal cramps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4448","text":"Which of the following can​ grow in an acidic pH?","raw_text":"Which of the following can grow in an acidic pH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vib​rio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lactob​acilli","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salmo​nella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shig​ella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lactobacilli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lactobacilli</strong></p><p><span class=\"topic-highlight\">Among the given options,<strong> lactobacilli</strong> is the one that can grow in an <strong>acidic pH.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"topic-highlight\">It </span>is a gram-positive<strong>, </strong>facultatively anaerobic, non-motile, non–spore-forming bacterium. <span class=\"topic-highlight\">Lactobacillus</span> derives <strong>lactic acid</strong> from <strong>glucose</strong>, creating an acidic environment, and can grow in an acidic medium. It is found in the <strong>vagina</strong> as part of its <strong>normal microbiota.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8hiPAJMJ","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"9_obstetrical_imaging","title":"9.Obstetrical Imaging","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB9944","text":"A 26-year-old woman presen‌ts with 2 months of ameno‌rrhea and a positive UPT.‍ On ultrasound examinatio‌n, all of the following a​re assessed except ______​.","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman presents with 2 months of amenorrhea and a positive UPT. On ultrasound examination, all of the following are assessed except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal ‌number","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal cardi‌ac activity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amniotic f‌luid volume","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crown ru‌mp length","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amniotic fluid volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amniotic fluid volume</strong></p><p><strong>Amniotic fluid volume</strong> is assessed on ultrasound in the <strong>second</strong> and <strong>third</strong> <strong>trimesters</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasonographic evaluation</strong> during the <strong>first trimester</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gestational sac size, location, and number</li>\n<li>Embryo and/or yolk sac identification</li>\n<li>Crown-rump length</li>\n<li>Fetal number, including amnionicity and chorionicity of multifetal gestations</li>\n<li>Embryonic/fetal cardiac activity</li>\n<li>Maternal uterus, adnexa, and cul-de-sac</li>\n<li>Fetal nuchal translucency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9946","text":"A primigravida presents to‌ the antenatal clinic for‌ the first time at 18 wee‍ks of amenorrhea. She doe‌s not remember her LMP an​d no ultrasound scans wer​e done in the first trime‍ster. In this situation, ​gestational age can be mo‌st accurately estimated b‌y which of the following ​parameters?","raw_text":"A primigravida presents to the antenatal clinic for the first time at 18 weeks of amenorrhea. She does not remember her LMP and no ultrasound scans were done in the first trimester. In this situation, gestational age can be most accurately estimated by which of the following parameters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crown ru‌mp length","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biparieta‌l diameter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Femur ‌length","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdominal c‌ircumference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Biparietal diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Biparietal diameter</strong></p><p>In the <strong>second trimester</strong>, the parameter to assess gestational age most accurately is the <strong>biparietal diameter</strong> (BPD). Accuracy varies from 7 to 10 days. </p>\n<p>BPD is measured in the transthalamic view, at the level of the thalamus and cavum septum pellucidum (CSP). It is measured from the outer edge of the skull in the near field to the inner edge of the skull in the far field. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px;\" width=\"286\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.7088px;\"><strong>Trimester</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.962px;\"><strong>Parameter</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.7088px;\">First</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.962px;\">Crown rump length (CRL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.7088px;\">Second </td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.962px;\">Biparietal diameter (BPD)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.7088px;\">Third </td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.962px;\">Femur length (FL)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9965","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ted with complaints of 7 ‌weeks of amenorrhea and a‍ positive UPT. Her ultras‌ound scan is shown below.​ What does the red arrowh​ead represent?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presented with complaints of 7 weeks of amenorrhea and a positive UPT. Her ultrasound scan is shown below. What does the red arrowhead represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yolk‌ sac","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decidua p‌arietalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decidua c‌apsularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cho‌rion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yolk sac","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/58ec7be5267d4700b17ecfe41729b61e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ea55dfdb7cc4d3bb71997f9127d1b99x1280x955.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yolk sac</strong></p><p>The red arrowhead represents the <strong>yolk sac</strong>. The yellow arrowhead shows the double ring - outer decidua parietalis and inner decidua capsularis. </p>\n<p>It is referred to as the<strong> double decidua sign</strong> and is seen in the first trimester transvaginal sonogram. It is useful to confirm an early <strong>intrauterine</strong> location for the <strong>pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9947","text":"Head circumference is meas‌ured from which of the fo‌llowing ultrasonographic ‍views?","raw_text":"Head circumference is measured from which of the following ultrasonographic views?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transthal‌amic view","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bitempo‌ral view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transcra‌nial view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bimasto‌id view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transthalamic view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c04770cdbf6e4b88927d47e2af5d3ce3x297x225.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transthalamic view</strong></p><p><strong>Head circumference</strong> (HC) is measured from the <strong>transthalamic</strong> view by placing an ellipse around the outer edge of the skull</p>\n<p>HC can also be calculated from the biparietal diameter (BPD) and occipital-frontal diameter (OFD).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9948","text":"What is the normal range o‌f femur length to abdomin‌al circumference ratio?","raw_text":"What is the normal range of femur length to abdominal circumference ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"44 ‌- 50","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 ‌- 24","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50 ‌- 40","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 ‌- 40","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20 - 24","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20 - 24</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 218\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>normal range</strong> for the femoral length (FL) to abdominal circumference (AC) ratio is <strong>20-24 percent</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Low</strong> <strong>FL/AC</strong> (&lt; 16) ratio suggests a lethal <strong>skeletal dysplasia</strong>, particularly if other skeletal abnormalities are present.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5662","text":"A woman presents at 33-wee‌ks of gestation and the u‌mbilical artery Doppler i‍maging shows the followin‌g finding. What is the ne​xt step of management?","raw_text":"A woman presents at 33-weeks of gestation and the umbilical artery Doppler imaging shows the following finding. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue pregnancy till te‌rm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate termination of p‌regnancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Terminate the pregnancy at‌ 34 weeks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continue steroids until te‌rm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immediate termination of pregnancy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e91e76f33fb74947b7bac77832a27255.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4675fd6abbbf43bebdddb8024a21fe5bx1280x2763.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immediate termination of pregnancy</strong></p><p>The umbilical artery Doppler depicts <strong>reversal of end-diastolic flow</strong> or REDF<strong> </strong>(waves appearing below the baseline) which is seen in <strong>intrauterine growth restriction</strong>. As the period of gestation has exceeded 32 weeks, the pregnancy can be <strong>terminated</strong>. </p>\n<p>In case of absent end-diastolic flow <strong>(AEDF),</strong> the waves just touch the baseline, but don't appear below it. This suggests a <strong>fetal compromise</strong>, hence the pregnancy is terminated by C-section if <strong>&gt;34 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reversed end-diastolic flow <strong>(REDF)</strong> suggests <strong>impending fetal</strong> <strong>death,</strong> hence the pregnancy is terminated by C-section if <strong>&gt;32 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5686","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ the first-trimester ultr‍asound?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the first-trimester ultrasound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A gestational sac can firs‌t be seen 4 weeks after L‌MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accuracy of USG dating dec‌reases after the 1st trim‌ester","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Yolk sac is the first sign‌ of pregnancy on USG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A normal gestational sac i‌mplants in the midline of‌ the endometrial cavity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Accuracy of USG dating decreases after the 1st trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Accuracy of USG dating decreases after the 1st trimester</strong></p><p>The accuracy of determining gestational age using ultrasound is the <strong>highest in the first trimester</strong> and decreases afterwards.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Gestational sac </strong>is reliably seen at<strong> 5 weeks</strong> of gestation on transvaginal sonography(TVS).</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>first</strong> <strong>sign </strong>of pregnancy seen<strong> </strong>on sonography is the<strong> gestational sac</strong>. Visualisation of the <strong>yolk sac</strong> within the gestational sac<strong> </strong>confirms an <strong>intrauterine</strong> location for pregnancy. </p>\n<p>Option D: A normal gestational sac implants eccentrically in the endometrium, whereas a <strong>pseudosac</strong> is seen in the <strong>midline</strong> of the endometrial cavity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9945","text":"What is the ideal time for‌ nuchal translucency eval‌uation?","raw_text":"What is the ideal time for nuchal translucency evaluation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-8 ‌weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"11-14‌ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"14-16‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16-20‌ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 11-14 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80eecda627d649f88eabb4729ca2c85ax796x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 11-14 weeks</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 215\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Nuchal translucency</strong> evaluation, a component of <strong>first-trimester</strong> aneuploidy screening, is done between <strong>11-14 weeks.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 215\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It represents the maximum thickness of the subcutaneous translucent area between the skin and soft tissue, overlying the fetal spine, at the back of the neck. When the nuchal translucency is <strong>increased</strong>, the <strong>risk of fetal aneuploidy</strong> and structural anomalies such as heart defects is significantly <strong>elevated</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 215\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>First trimester aneuploidy screening</strong> combines two maternal serum analytes and a sonographic measurement:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Human chorionic gonadotropin <strong>(hCG)</strong></li>\n<li>Pregnancy-associated plasma protein A (<strong>PAPP-A</strong>)</li>\n<li>Nuchal translucency (<strong>NT</strong>)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is performed between <strong>11 and 14 weeks </strong>of gestation. In cases of fetal Down syndrome, the first-trimester serum-free <strong>β-hCG </strong>level is<strong> higher </strong>and the <strong>PAPP-A </strong>level is<strong> lower</strong>. With trisomy 18 and trisomy 13, levels of both analytes are lower.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Nuchal fold</strong> is a normal fold of skin seen at the back of the fetal neck during the <strong>second trimester</strong> of pregnancy. <strong>Increased</strong> nuchal fold <strong>thickness</strong> is associated with <strong>fetal aneuploidy.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9991","text":"Which of the following are‌ sonographic markers for ‌Down's syndrome? Increase‍d nuchal thickness Echoge‌nic bowel Pyelectasis Mil​d ventricular dilation In​creased femur length","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are sonographic markers for Down's syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Increased nuchal thickness</li>\n<li>Echogenic bowel</li>\n<li>Pyelectasis</li>\n<li>Mild ventricular dilation</li>\n<li>Increased femur length</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 3 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Ultrasound markers</strong> for <strong>Down's</strong> syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First trimester\n<ul>\n<li>Nuchal translucency &gt;3 mm</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Second trimester\n<ul>\n<li>Echogenic intracardiac focus</li>\n<li><strong>Ventriculomegaly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nuchal thickness</strong> &gt;6 mm</li>\n<li><strong>Echogenic bowel</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoplastic/absent nasal bone</li>\n<li>Shortened humerus, femur</li>\n<li>Mild <strong>pylectasis</strong></li>\n<li>Aberrant subclavian artery.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0037","text":"The anomaly scan of a G3A2‌ woman is given below. Id‌entify the sign.","raw_text":"The anomaly scan of a G3A2 woman is given below. Identify the sign.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Banan‌a sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lemon‌ sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mickey M‌ouse sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Humming ‌bird sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Banana sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a621a0767be743098947c71b4c089660.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91a4b36836fe4c5297f6afdc378e3d25x510x354.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9759d2bb8e184275bff249c9bca6c1a6x510x418.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Banana sign</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>banana sign.</strong></p>\n<p>An abnormal anterior curvature of cerebellum along with effacement of cisterna magna produces this appearance. It is seen in <strong>spina bifida</strong> associated with<span class=\"a\"> <strong>Arnold-Chiari</strong> malformation.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Lemon sign</strong>: Axial USG image of the fetal head at the level of the biparietal diameter shows inward concavity of both frontal bones instead of the normally seen convexity. This gives the head the appearance of a lemon. It has a strong association with <strong>spina bifida</strong> and is very useful for detecting this condition.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1513","text":"A doctor tells a 31-year-o‌ld woman, who is 20 weeks‌ pregnant, that her baby ‍has a developmental defec‌t. She has come to you fo​r a second opinion. The f​ollowing USG finding is s‍een. Identify the defect.​","raw_text":"A doctor tells a 31-year-old woman, who is 20 weeks pregnant, that her baby has a developmental defect. She has come to you for a second opinion. The following USG finding is seen. Identify the defect.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anenc‌ephaly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ompha‌locele","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic ‌hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enceph‌alocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anencephaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f95f38e8c328433ba238c27103092202.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd62dfe8dcf5418ea9a2f29351bd9b37x510x620.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f02e322ff69b4b55a63ed0a13be69d5ax720x949.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/491bc031737649698c4fa4e967f5a63ax1280x1040.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2818c67a680a4ecb8c469b4367d07871x278x312.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ab58285283846c7b771bb9264340273x766x580.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3e6a1d4fe2e441aa76b127e5cbf6b70x720x509.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anencephaly</strong></p><p>The given ultrasound image shows an open defect in the calvaria and skin<strong> </strong>leading to the <strong>exposure</strong> of the <strong>cranial neural tube</strong>. This is consistent with the diagnosis of <strong>anencephaly. </strong></p>\n<p>Anencephaly results from <strong>incomplete closure</strong> of the<strong> neural tube. </strong>It is one of the earliest detectable congenital defects that can be <strong>detected</strong> as early as <strong>10 to 11 weeks</strong> of gestation.  Screening can also be done by serum estimation of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein<strong> (MSAFP). </strong>The following are the radiological signs seen in anencephaly. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frog eye sign</strong> - Absence of cranial calvaria, prominent orbits thereby resembling the face of a frog. </li>\n<li><strong>Shower cap sign - </strong>The absence of the cranium could lead to the protrusion of a disorganized mass of brain tissue. This resembles a shower cap giving rise to a characteristic triangular facial appearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neural-tube defects can be prevented with <strong>pre-conceptional</strong> supplementation of <strong>folic acid. </strong>A dose of 4 mg daily is recommended in subsequent pregnancies.  </p>\n<p>The frog eye and showercap signs are shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Omphalocele </strong>is a <strong>midline anterior abdominal wall defect</strong> through which the solid abdominal viscera and/or bowel may <strong>herniate</strong>. The extruded abdominal contents are <strong>covered in a sac</strong>. Given below is an ultrasound image of the fetus suggestive of an omphalocele (asterix).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Cystic hygroma </strong>is a congenital <strong>multiloculated lymphatic lesion </strong>that is classically found in the <strong>left posterior triangle</strong> <strong>of the neck. </strong>It occurs as a result of sequestration or obstruction of developing lymph vessels. This defect can be picked up in a first-trimester ultrasound. The images below show the MRI and USG appearance of cystic hygroma, respectively:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Encephalocele</strong> is caused due to <strong>defects in the skull</strong> through which the <strong>meninges </strong>and/or <strong>neural tissue herniate</strong>. The squamous part of the occipital bone is the most frequently affected bone. </p>\n<p>The image shows a newborn with <strong>encephalocele</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0030","text":"What is Rob‌ert's sign?","raw_text":"What is Robert's sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gas shado‌w in bowel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gas shadow i‌n portal vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gas shado‌w in heart","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gas shadow‌ in cranium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gas shadow in heart","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gas shadow in heart</strong></p><p><strong>Robert's sign</strong> is a <strong>gas shadow</strong> in<strong> </strong>the fetal<strong> heart</strong>. It is<strong> </strong>seen in <strong>fetal demise</strong> in utero and may appear as early as 12 hours.</p>\n<p>Other sonographic signs seen in fetal demise in utero are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absent fetal heartbeat</li>\n<li>Absent fetal movements</li>\n<li>Occasional findings\n<ul>\n<li>Overlapping of skull bones - <strong>Spalding's sign </strong></li>\n<li>Gross distortion of fetal anatomy (maceration)</li>\n<li>Soft tissue edema - skin &gt;5 mm</li>\n<li>Echogenic amniotic fluid  (fetal demise fragments)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Uncommon findings\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombus in fetal heart</li>\n<li>Gas shadow in the fetal heart - Robert's sign.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9925","text":"What is the frequency of a‌ transabdominal transduce‌r?","raw_text":"What is the frequency of a transabdominal transducer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20000‌ hertz","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-6 me‌gahertz","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5-10 me‌gahertz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-15 m‌egahertz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4-6 megahertz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4-6 megahertz</strong></p><p>The frequency of trans <strong>abdominal</strong> transducer is<strong> 4-6 megahertz (MHz) </strong>since a lower frequency penetrates better.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, a <strong>vaginal</strong> transducer has a frequency of <strong>5-10 megahertz</strong> since a higher frequency yields a better image with minimal penetration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9791","text":"When is fetal cardiac acti‌vity first detected on tr‌ansabdominal USG?","raw_text":"When is fetal cardiac activity first detected on transabdominal USG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5th ‌week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th ‌week","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6th ‌week","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9th ‌week","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6th week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6th week</strong></p><p>The <strong>cardiac activity</strong> of the fetus is first detected on transabdominal USG (<strong>TAS</strong>) at the <strong>6<sup>th</sup> week</strong> of intrauterine life.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 238px; width: 341px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 127px;\">\n<p><strong>Fetal structure</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 96.375px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>TVS </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">(weeks)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 98.625px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>TAS</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">(weeks)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 127px;\">\n<p>Gestational sac</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 96.375px;\">\n<p>4.5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 98.625px;\">\n<p>5.5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 127px;\">\n<p>Yolk sac</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 96.375px;\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 98.625px;\">\n<p>6</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 127px;\">\n<p>Cardiac activity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 96.375px;\">\n<p>6 - 6.5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 98.625px;\">\n<p>6 - 7</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9790","text":"A pregnant woman has her f‌irst antenatal visit in t‌he 8th month of gestation‍. On examination, the ute‌rus corresponds to 30 wee​ks and the fetal heart ra​te is 136/min. What is th‍e best parameter for the ​estimation of fetal age b‌y USG in this patient?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman has her first antenatal visit in the 8th month of gestation. On examination, the uterus corresponds to 30 weeks and the fetal heart rate is 136/min. What is the best parameter for the estimation of fetal age by USG in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femur ‌length","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Biparieta‌l diameter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal c‌ircumference","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crown ru‌mp length","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Femur length","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Femur length</strong></p><p><strong>Femur length</strong> is the best parameter for the estimation of fetal age by USG in the <strong>3rd trimester. </strong></p>\n<p>Best USG parameters for estimation of fetal age:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Trimester</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Parameter</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>First</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Crown-rump length (CRL)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Second</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Biparietal diameter (BPD)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Third</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Femur length (FL)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5679","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding sonograph‌y in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding sonography in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Must follow ALARA principl‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All machines must display ‌thermal index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue heating is higher w‌ith shorter examination t‌ime","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Risks are greater during o‌rganogenesis than later i‌n pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tissue heating is higher with shorter examination time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tissue heating is higher with shorter examination time</strong></p><p>Tissue heating is higher with <strong>longer</strong> examination time.</p>\n<p>Sonography should be performed in compliance with the <strong>ALARA principle</strong> which is As Low As Reasonably Achievable. The theoretical risks are greater during organogenesis (8-15 weeks) than later in pregnancy. </p>\n<p>All sonography machines must display 2 indices - the<strong> thermal index </strong>and the<strong> mechanical index. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9802","text":"A pregnant diabetic woman ‌presents for a routine an‌tenatal visit during the ‍third trimester. During t‌he evaluation, you perfor​m obstetrical Doppler ima​ging. Which among the fol‍lowing would indicate fet​al compromise?","raw_text":"A pregnant diabetic woman presents for a routine antenatal visit during the third trimester. During the evaluation, you perform obstetrical Doppler imaging. Which among the following would indicate fetal compromise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased pulsatalilty ind‌ex in umbilical artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased systolic/diastol‌ic ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased cerebral artery ‌flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absent end d‌iastolic flow","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Absent end diastolic flow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9e8b2b847e94d049a02ae68286237efx1280x2763.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Absent end diastolic flow</strong></p><p><strong>Absent end diastolic flow</strong> indicates <strong>fetal compromise</strong> in Doppler.</p>\n<p>Umbilical artery vessels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are characterized by forward low-resistance flow.</li>\n<li>As gestation increases, there is a gradual fall in all the resistance indices so the amount of forward diastolic flow increases.</li>\n<li>S/D ratio decreases as the period of gestation increases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Abnormal flow patterns </strong>in umbilical artery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduced</strong> end-diastolic flow - <strong>Uteroplacental insufficiency</strong> (systolic/diastolic ratio increases)</li>\n<li><strong>Absent</strong> end-diastolic flow - <strong>Fetal compromise</strong> (hence pregnancy is terminated by cesarean section if &gt;34 weeks)</li>\n<li><strong>Reverse</strong> end-diastolic flow - <strong>Impending</strong> <strong>death</strong> (pregnancy is terminated irrespective of gestational age)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Doppler of the<strong> middle cerebral artery </strong>helps in detecting<strong> </strong><strong>fetal anemia. </strong>Increased fetal diastolic flow in the middle cerebral artery with absent diastolic flow in the aorta implies <strong>fetal acidemia</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MvXU39Z8","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"9_retinal_vascular_disorders_and_retinal_detachment","title":"9.Retinal Vascular Disorders And Retinal Detachment","num_questions":35,"total_marks":140,"duration":45,"time_per_question":77.14,"questions":[{"id":"MC7115","text":"Identify the marked layer ‍of the retina.","raw_text":"Identify the marked layer of the retina.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner plex‍iform layer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"External li‍miting layer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal li‍miting layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outer plex‍iform layer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inner plexiform layer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bb98bfa6f5c4ee79dcef1ef6868e1f2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15d36c08680e478294529651a92c0b63x1280x866.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inner plexiform layer</strong></p><p>The<strong> inner plexiform layer</strong> is the marked layer in the given histological image of retina, identified as the <strong>fourth</strong> layer from the uppermost (innermost) layer.</p>\n<p>The retina consists of <strong>10 layers. </strong>The <strong>retinal pigment epithelium </strong>(RPE) is the <strong>outermost layer</strong> - below the Bruch's membrane of the choroid - and the <strong>internal limiting membrane</strong> is the <strong>innermost layer </strong>- separating the retina from the vitreous.</p>\n<p>The retinal pigment epithelium consists of a <strong>single layer</strong> of cells containing <strong>pigment</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is separated from the neurosensory retinal layers by the potential space between them which is known as interphotoreceptor matrix. </p>\n<p>The<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> inner plexiform layer</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> consists of </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">synapsing nerve fibers</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. The </span>axons of bipolar cells synapse with dendrites of ganglion<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> cells; and both these </span>processes synapse with processes of amacrine<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> cells. It also contains some horizontally placed </span>internal plexiform cells; and also a few ganglion cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0190","text":"Identify the structure mar‍ked with red arrow","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked with red arrow","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer limit‍ing membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inner plex‍iform layer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Outer plex‍iform layer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Outer nuc‍lear layer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Outer plexiform layer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8ca58d7290654e00b6aea4350ec11073.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15d36c08680e478294529651a92c0b63x1280x866.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Outer plexiform layer</strong></p><p>The marked structure in the slide is the <strong>outer plexiform layer </strong>of the <strong>retina</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>outer plexiform layer</strong> (outer synaptic layer) is present between the outer nuclear layer and the inner nuclear layer. This layer stains lightly. This layer consists of the axons of rods and cones which synapse with dendrites of bipolar neurons and horizontal cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1367","text":"The distance of the fovea ‍from the temporal margin ​of the optic disc is ____‌___.","raw_text":"The distance of the fovea from the temporal margin of the optic disc is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 disc ‍diameter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 disc d‍iameters","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 disc d‍iameters","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 disc d‍iameters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 disc diameters","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e0f9b669b04489a86a45ae1daf2446dx712x535.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 disc diameters</strong></p><p>The distance of the fovea from the temporal margin of the optic disc is<strong> 2 disc diameters</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>fovea</strong> is an<strong> excavation</strong> in the retinal surface at the center of the <strong>macula</strong>, with a diameter of 1.5 mm, which is about the same as that of the optic disc. The<strong> foveola</strong> forms the bottom of the fovea and has a diameter of 0.35 mm. It is devoid of ganglion cells. The <strong>umbo</strong> is the central floor the foveola, which corresponds to the foveolar light reflex. The <strong>foveal avascular zone</strong> (FAZ) is a central area (including foveola and surrounding areas) which contains no blood vessels but is surrounded by a continuous network of capillaries.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>macula</strong> is a round area at the <strong>posterior pole,</strong> lying inside the temporal vascular arcades. It measures 5.5 mm in diameter. The inner layers of the macula contain the yellow <strong>xanthophyll carotenoid</strong> pigments lutein and <strong>zeaxanthin</strong> in far higher concentrations than the peripheral retina (hence, the full name \"macula lutea\", which means yellow plaque).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0189","text":"Causes for expanding blind‍ spot are all except ____​______","raw_text":"Causes for expanding blind spot are all except __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PO‍AG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoplasia o‍f optic disc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Papil‍ledema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullated ‍nerve fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoplasia of optic disc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoplasia of optic disc</strong></p><p><strong>Optic disc hypoplasia </strong>does <strong>not</strong> <strong>cause</strong> enlargement of the blind spot.</p>\n<p>The<strong> physiological blind spot</strong> is an area of absolute<strong> scotoma</strong> in the visual field, seen in the region of<strong> optic disc</strong>. It is due to the absence of photoreceptors (rods and cones) in that region. Enlarged blind spot is seen in primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG) (Option A), papilledema (Option C), medullated nerve fibers (Option D), optic disc drusen, coloboma of optic disc and<br/>myopic disc with a crescent. </p>\n<p><strong>Optic nerve hypoplasia</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>congenital optic disc anomaly. There is <strong>underdevelopment</strong> of the optic disc in the disease and it typically presents with a small disc that is surrounded by a yellowish pigmented ring, called the <strong>'double ring sign'.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>primary open-angle glaucoma</strong>(POAG), the optic disc enlargement is seen secondary to increased intra-ocular pressure (IOP) in the eye.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Papilledema</strong> is a passive disc swelling seen with raised intracranial pressure. The blind spot is enlarged due to the edema of the optic disc. </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Medullated</strong> or <strong>opaque nerve fibers</strong><strong> </strong>are the abnormal <strong>myelination</strong> of nerve fibers of the retina. Normally, optic nerve myelination stops at the level of lamina cribrosa. If the myelination continues beyond the optic disc it leads to the expansion of the blind spot.  On ophthalmoscopic examination, these are seen as a <strong>whitish patch</strong> with <strong>feathery margins.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6729","text":"Which statement regarding ‍Müller cells is false?","raw_text":"Which statement regarding Müller cells is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are modified glial ce‍lls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Their nuclei lie in the ou‍ter nuclear layer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Their foot processes form ‍the inner &amp;amp; outer lim​iting membranes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They contribute to inner b‍lood retinal barrier","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Their nuclei lie in the outer nuclear layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Their nuclei lie in the outer nuclear layer</strong></p><p>The nuclei of Müller cells lie in the <strong>inner nuclear layer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Müller cells</strong> are a type of macroglial cells of the retina. The human retina contains 8–10 million Müller cells. Their <strong>nuclei</strong> lie in the<strong> inner nuclear layer</strong> and their processes extend across the retinal layers to form the <strong>inner</strong> and <strong>outer limiting membranes</strong>. Their functions include:</p>\n<p>1. Providing structural stability to the retina<br/>2. Interacting with other neurons and their functional and metabolic support<br/>3. Providing neurons with trophic substances and removing metabolic waste<br/>4. Playing a critical role in regulating the extracellular space volume as well as ion and water homeostasis and in maintaining the inner blood-retinal barrier</p>\n<p><strong>Astrocytes</strong> are another type of macroglial cells of the retina. They play a crucial role in retinal vascularization. In the mature retina, they are restricted to the nerve fiber and ganglion cell layers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6730","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g the development of reti‌nal vasculature?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the development of retinal vasculature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grow to temporal ora by 34‍ weeks of gestation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Grow to nasal ora by 36 we‍eks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Originate from mesenchymal‍ tissue during week 16 of​ gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiate centrifugally from‍ optic nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Grow to temporal ora by 34 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Grow to temporal ora by 34 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>Retinal vessels reach<strong> temporal ora</strong> at approximately<strong> 40</strong> weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p>Retinal vessels develop de novo from<strong> mesenchymal tissue</strong>, beginning during the <strong>4<sup>th</sup> month</strong> of gestation. They radiate <strong>outward </strong>(centrifugally) from the optic nerve, reaching the nasal ora by 36 weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0361","text":"A preterm baby treated for‍ respiratory distress syn​drome is found to have wh‌ite pupillary reflex on o‌pthalmologic examination.‌ What is the likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A preterm baby treated for respiratory distress syndrome is found to have white pupillary reflex on opthalmologic examination. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior persistent prima‍ry hyperplastic vitreous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coat's re‍tinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retrolental‍ fibroplasia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenita‍l cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retrolental fibroplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17c7b700f1994c95bc233f1c355272edx510x2089.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b296f457a3147068d95d5e99df7e3a4x1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retrolental fibroplasia</strong></p><p>A preterm baby, treated for respiratory distress syndrome, with<strong> leucocoria </strong>(white pupillary reflex) most likely has <strong>retrolental</strong> <strong>fibroplasia.</strong></p>\n<p>Retrolental fibroplasia, also known as <strong>retinopathy </strong>of<strong> prematurity</strong>, is a proliferative retinopathy. Risk factors include <strong>prematurity</strong>,<strong> </strong>very low birth weight and supplemental oxygen therapy (given for respiratory distress syndrome). ROP can be mild and may resolve spontaneously, but may lead to blindness in serious cases. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0362","text":"Which of the following is ‍included under threshold ​disease when screening ne‌onates for retinopathy of‌ prematurity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is included under threshold disease when screening neonates for retinopathy of prematurity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ROP stage 1 extending from‍ 6'o clock to 11'0 clock ​in zone 1 with plus disea‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ROP stage 3 extending from‍ 2'o clock to 7'o clock i​n zone 2 with plus diseas‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ROP stage 3 extending from‍ 1'o clock to 4'o clock i​n zone 1 without plus dis‌ease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ROP stage 2 extending from‍ 3'o clock to 6'o clock i​n zone 2 with plus diseas‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ROP stage 3 extending from 2'o clock to 7'o clock in zone 2 with plus disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd2e4bd83ac642efa034efa464d8f823x1280x3016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ROP stage 3 extending from 2&#x27;o clock to 7&#x27;o clock in zone 2 with plus disease</strong></p><p><strong>ROP stage 3 </strong>extending from <strong>2'0 clock </strong>to <strong>7'o clock</strong> (6 clock hours) in <strong>zone 2 </strong>with<strong> plus disease</strong> fits into the definition of <strong>threshold disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Threshold</strong> disease of retinopathy of prematurity is defined by the CRYO-ROP study as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage 3 disease: Ridge with extraretinal fibrovascular proliferation into the vitreous</li>\n<li>Involvement of zones 1 or 2</li>\n<li>Involvement of either at least 5 contiguous (continuous) clock hours or 8 non-contiguous clock hours</li>\n<li>Associated with \"plus disease\": Dilatation and tortuosity of posterior pole vessels in at least two quadrants at the posterior pole. </li>\n</ul>\n<p> Treatment of threshold disease is<strong> laser photocoagulation</strong> of the involved part within 72 hours of diagnosis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment of the various stages of ROP includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-threshold type 1 disease - Treatment is now recommended within <strong>48 hours</strong> and is done by <strong>laser photocoagulation</strong> of avascular retina.</li>\n<li>Pre-threshold type 2 disease (Any ROP not following under type 1)- <strong>Observation</strong> for progression</li>\n<li>Threshold disease - <strong>Laser photocoagulation</strong> of avascular retina</li>\n<li>Aggresive posterior-ROP- Intravitreal <strong>anti-VEGF agents</strong>, eg., bevacizumab</li>\n<li>Tractional retinal detachment (stage 4A) -<strong> Pars plana vitrectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9335","text":"All of the following are t‍rue statements regarding ​retinopathy of prematurit‌y except: (PNA - Post nat‌al age, PMA - Post menstr‌ual age, AP-ROP - Aggress​ive posterior retinopathy‌ of prematurity)","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are true statements regarding retinopathy of prematurity except:</p>\n<p>(PNA - Post natal age, PMA - Post menstrual age, AP-ROP - Aggressive posterior retinopathy of prematurity)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First screening should be ‍done 32 weeks PMA or 4 we​eks PNA, whichever is lat‌er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"For infants less than 28 w‍eek, screen at 3 week of ​postnatal age to detect A‌PROP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neonates are best examined‍ at ophthalmology OPD and​ hence should be transpor‌ted","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Screening is done using in‍direct ophthalmoscopy by ​an expert ophthalmologist‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neonates are best examined at ophthalmology OPD and hence should be transported","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/198169446c05486fa756d40d0d6a6300x1280x3016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neonates are best examined at ophthalmology OPD and hence should be transported</strong></p><p>Neonates are <strong>best examined</strong> in the <strong>neonatal unit</strong> itself under the supervision of the attending neonatologist. It is not recommended to transport infants to the ophthalmic outpatient department for examination.</p>\n<p>The <strong>first screening</strong> examination should be carried out by <strong>indirect ophthalmoscopy</strong> at <strong>32 weeks PMA </strong>or <strong>4 weeks PNA</strong>, whichever is later. For infants born at<strong> less </strong>than<strong> 28 weeks</strong>, screening is done at <strong>3 weeks PNA</strong> to detect APROP. <strong>Aggressive posterior ROP </strong>(\"rush\" disease, AP-ROP), if untreated, usually progresses to total retinal detachment (stage 5) within a few days. It is characterized by its posterior location, prominence of plus disease and ill-defined nature of the retinopathy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0363","text":"A preterm neonate admitted‍ to NICU who received sup​plemental oxygen is being‌ screened for retinopathy‌ of prematurity. Fundus e‌xamination reveals a subt​otal retinal detachment. ‌How will you stage the di‍sease?","raw_text":"A preterm neonate admitted to NICU who received supplemental oxygen is being screened for retinopathy of prematurity. Fundus examination reveals a subtotal retinal detachment. How will you stage the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‍ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‍ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‍ge 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‍ge 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e8cf7727c9b445f9d284ce7b5abbc6ax510x2089.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage 4</strong></p><p>Subtotal retinal detachment is considered to be <strong>stage 4</strong> of<strong> retinopathy</strong> of<strong> prematurity</strong> (ROP). </p>\n<p>The clinical staging of ROP is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage 1 - Formation of a demarcation line at the edge of vessels, dividing the vascular from the avascular retina.</li>\n<li>Stage 2- Formation of an intraretinal ridge </li>\n<li>Stage 3- Ridge with extraretinal fibrovascular proliferation into the vitreous</li>\n<li><strong>Stage 4</strong>-<strong> Subtotal retinal detachment</strong></li>\n<li>Stage 5- Total retinal detachment</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1493","text":"A 14-day-old neonate born ‍at 28 weeks of gestation ​had developed respiratory‌ distress syndrome at bir‌th. You want to screen fo‌r retinopathy of prematur​ity. How many weeks from ‌now would you schedule th‍e screening?","raw_text":"A 14-day-old neonate born at 28 weeks of gestation had developed respiratory distress syndrome at birth. You want to screen for retinopathy of prematurity. How many weeks from now would you schedule the screening?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 w‍eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 w‍eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8 w‍eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 w‍eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aff757ca80114535a17da45c4323fc08x1280x3016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 weeks</strong></p><p>In the scenario above, the <strong>postnatal age </strong>of the premature infant is <strong>2 weeks</strong>, and therefore, the infant must be screened in<strong> 2 weeks </strong>i.e.,<strong> 4 weeks </strong>of <strong>postnatal</strong> <strong>age</strong>, as per the guidelines for the management of retinopathy of prematurity (ROP). </p>\n<p>ROP guidelines state that the first examination should be performed between <strong>4 </strong>and <strong>6 weeks </strong>of<strong> postnatal age </strong>(after birth) or between 31–33 weeks of post-conceptional age (calculated as gestational age + postnatal age), whichever is later. Ideally, 2 examinations must be performed; however, 1 may suffice if it shows complete retinal vascularisation. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0380","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most important factor​ for the development of d‌iabetic retinopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important factor for the development of diabetic retinopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duration o‍f diabetes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glycemic‍ control","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Association with hypertens‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age of th‍e patient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duration of diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duration of diabetes</strong></p><p>The <strong>most important factor</strong> for the development of diabetic retinopathy is the <strong>duration</strong> of <strong>diabetes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Diabetic retinopathy</strong> constitutes the retinal changes seen in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus. The most important factor for its development is duration of diabetes after the onset of puberty, followed by <strong>glycemic control</strong>. It is <strong>more common</strong> in <strong>type</strong> <strong>1 diabetes mellitus</strong>. The presence of hypertension increases the risk of diabetic retinopathy.</p>\n<p>First fundus examination for<strong> type 1 diabetes </strong>is done after <strong>5 years </strong>after diagnosis and for <strong>type 2</strong> is done<strong> at </strong>the time of<strong> diagnosis</strong>. The frequency of follow-up for types 1 and 2 diabetes mellitus is every year. Diabetes in pregnancy is followed up every 3 months.</p>\n<p>The <strong>earliest feature</strong> of diabetic retinopathy is <strong>microaneurysms</strong>, which are present in the inner nuclear layer of the retina.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0381","text":"Which of the following fin‍dings on a fundus examina​tion would suggest severe‌ non-proliferative diabet‌ic retinopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings on a fundus examination would suggest severe non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Significant venous beading‍ in any of the 4 quadrant​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Micro-aneurysms in any 2 q‍uadrants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Severe hemorrhages in all ‍4 quadrants","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Macula‍r star","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Severe hemorrhages in all 4 quadrants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d9238edd35b4a8e92acdd239c6290e7x512x399.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Severe hemorrhages in all 4 quadrants</strong></p><p><strong>Severe hemorrhages</strong> in <strong>all 4 quadrants</strong> is a feature of<strong> severe NPDR.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Macular star </strong>is a feature of <strong>malignant hypertensive retinopathy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1491","text":"A person is diagnosed to h‍ave diabetes on his 45th ​birthday. When will you r‌ecommend a dilated fundus‌ examination for him?","raw_text":"A person is diagnosed to have diabetes on his 45th birthday. When will you recommend a dilated fundus examination for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immed‍iately","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Before his 5‍0th birthday","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After his 5‍0th birthday","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"When he complains of decre‍ase in vision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Immediately","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Immediately</strong></p><p>A <strong>45 year</strong> old diagnosed with diabetes mellitus is <strong>likely</strong> to have <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong> and thus, he should undergo <strong>screening</strong> for diabetic retinopathy <strong>immediately</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Duration</strong> of diabetes is the <strong>most important</strong> risk factor for developing diabetic retinopathy, especially proliferative diabetic retinopathy. The recommendations for <strong>first fundus </strong>examination in diabetic retinopathy is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Within <strong>3-5 years</strong> of diagnosis in type 1 diabetes</li>\n<li><strong>Immediately</strong> in type 2 diabetes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Later, the screening is continued once <strong>every year</strong> in both Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes. In the event proliferative diabetic retinopathy develops, screening is done more frequently - once every 2 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0187","text":"ETDRS study was related to‍?","raw_text":"ETDRS study was related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central retinal artery occ‍lusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic ma‍cular edema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary open angle glaucom‍a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-infecti‍ous uveitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diabetic macular edema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diabetic macular edema</strong></p><p><strong>Early Treatment Diabetic Retinopathy Study (ETDRS)</strong> was a randomized clinical trial that studied the effect of photocoagulation on <strong>diabetic macular edema</strong>. </p>\n<p>The results of the study demonstrated the <strong>beneficial effects </strong>of <strong>focal photocoagulation</strong> on <strong>diabetic macular edema</strong>. The study resulted in the recommendation that all eyes with clinically significant diabetic macular edema should be considered for focal photocoagulation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0188","text":"A patient with a history o‍f diabetes and hypertensi​on underwent fundus fluor‌escent angiography (FFA) ‌to evaluate his decreasin‌g vision. The FFA yields ​an image as shown below, ‌which is characteristic o‍f:","raw_text":"A patient with a history of diabetes and hypertension underwent fundus fluorescent  angiography (FFA) to evaluate his decreasing vision. The FFA yields an image as shown below, which is characteristic of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macular ‍ischemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Macula‍r edema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Submacular ‍haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Premacular ‍haemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Macular ischemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/069caf36d3cc40b79335aaaf6679146e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1dbbde29f7aa4377bb711a8abc91d8f7x720x528.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44b9930c93004748bab07c5b76706c81x1280x1363.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4db0188b47484273bccf96a592189e5ex204x136.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b081c51feee94849b0b4cd034a93d691x974x398.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6af267802212441dbbb1ff3c366478fcx252x208.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Macular ischemia</strong></p><p>The fundus fluorescent angiography (FFA) image shows <strong>enlarged foveal avascular zone</strong>,<strong> </strong>seen as a hypofluorescent patch in the macula (white arrow),<strong> </strong>with surrounding<strong> capillary non perfused areas </strong>(blue arrow). Both of these are suggestive of<strong> macular ischemia. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The above mentioned findings when combined with the <strong>history</strong> of <strong>diabetes</strong> in this patient points towards the diagnosis of<strong> diabetic macular ischemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>FFA</strong> images in a case of <strong>diabetic retinopathy</strong> are given below:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Non proliferative diabetic retinopathy </strong>(NPDR)</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Punctate or pinpoint areas of hyperfluorescence - microaneurysms</li>\n<li>Abnormal branching of arteriovenous shunt - intraretinal microvascular abnormalities (IRMA) </li>\n<li>Patchy filling defects of ischemia due to capillary atrophy or non perfusion</li>\n<li>Increase in the size of hypoperfusion in foveal avascular zone in macular ischemia </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>     </strong>2.<strong> Proliferative diabetic retinopathy</strong>(PDR)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluorescein leakage - in retinal neovascularization.</li>\n<li>Leakage in the disc region suggests neovascularization of the disc (NVD) and leakage in other regions suggests neovascularization elsewhere (NVE).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows pinpoint areas of <strong>hyperfluorescence</strong> and areas of <strong>fluorescein leakage</strong> in the fundus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Macular edema </strong>also known as cystoid macular edema (CME)<strong> </strong>is a collection of fluid between the <strong>outer plexiform</strong> layer and the <strong>inner nuclear layer</strong> of the retina following a break in the blood-retina barrier. On fundoscopy, a <strong>honeycomb appearance</strong> is seen, and on FFA, <strong>flower petal pattern</strong> can be seen. The below image shows the <strong>flower petal pattern</strong> on FFA as seen in CME. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Submacular hemorrhage </strong>also known as <strong>subretinal hemorrhage</strong> will obscure the choroidal dye visualization and appaers as ill defined areas of hypoperfusion in the corresponding area. The retinal vessels overlying the area would, however will be visualized. There are no assosciated features of pinpoint hyperfluorescence and vascular leakage that are usually seen with diabetic macular ischemia. The below given images show fluorescein angiography which reveal <strong>blocked fluorescence</strong> in the maculae of both eyes due to <strong>subretinal hemorrhages.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Premacular hemorrhage </strong>is a term used to define vitreous hemorrhage that occurs in the subhyaloid space. It is seen as a region of<strong> hypofluorescence</strong> with no overlying retinal vasculature visualization on FFA.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0387","text":"A 69-year-old patient with‍ hypertension has silver ​wiring, papilledema, and ‌arteriovenous nipping vis‌ible on fundoscopy, but n‌o other changes. How woul​d you grade his disease?","raw_text":"A 69-year-old patient with hypertension has silver wiring, papilledema, and arteriovenous nipping visible on fundoscopy, but no other changes. How would you grade his disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grade I r‍etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grade IV r‍etinopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grade III ‍retinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grade II r‍etinopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grade IV retinopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8c065ace33b4c709bcc39b733de6c94x1280x3068.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grade IV retinopathy</strong></p><p>A patient with hypertension having \"silver wiring,\" papilledema, and arteriovenous nipping visible on fundoscopy has <strong>Grade IV retinopathy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0388","text":"A 40-year-old male, newly ‍diagnosed with systolic h​ypertension, presents wit‌h a three-week history of‌ bilateral blurry vision.‌ His visual acuity is 20/​30 and 20/120 in the righ‌t and left eye, respectiv‍ely. The rest of the exam​ination is unremarkable. ​Which of the following fi‍ndings is least likely to​ be seen on his fundoscop‌y?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male, newly diagnosed with systolic hypertension,  presents with a three-week history of bilateral blurry vision. His visual acuity is 20/30 and 20/120 in the right and left eye, respectively. The rest of the examination is unremarkable. Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen on his fundoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arteriolar‍ narrowing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intraretinal microvascular‍ abnormalities","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cotton w‍ool spots","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papil‍ledema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intraretinal microvascular abnormalities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea6ea564de1843e5bfb520b83ba29d5bx1280x3068.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intraretinal microvascular abnormalities</strong></p><p>Systolic hypertension (systolic blood pressure over 200 mm Hg) with bilateral blurry vision and unremarkable examination findings point to a diagnosis of hypertensive retinopathy. <strong>Intra-retinal microvascular abnormalities </strong>(IRMA)<strong> </strong>are<strong> not seen</strong> in<strong> hypertensive retinopathy.</strong> IRMA is a feature of diabetic retinopathy<strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Arteriolar narrowing</strong> occurs due to vasoconstriction and sclerosis of retinal vessels due to hypertension. <strong>Cotton wool spots</strong> are areas of nerve fibre layer infarcts. They occur due to capillary obliteration in hypertension.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0378","text":"The following image shows ‍the picture of the fundus​ in a patient taken 48 ho‌urs after sustaining seve‌re head trauma. What is t‌he diagnosis?","raw_text":"The following image shows the picture of the fundus in a patient taken 48 hours after sustaining severe head trauma. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertensive‍ retinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic r‍etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Purtschers ‍retinopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Central retinal vein occlu‍sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Purtschers retinopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/23c9142096b14446a5d108f86b80a477.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Purtschers retinopathy</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>multiple cotton wool</strong> spots around the disc. This is <strong>Purtschers' retinopathy</strong>,<strong> </strong>which is classically associated with severe head injury. </p>\n<p>It is also seen in <strong>pancreatitis </strong>and <strong>chest trauma</strong> due to <strong>fat/air embolism</strong>. Ophthalmoscopy reveals multiple, variably sized cotton-wool spots and intraretinal hemorrhages around the optic nerve. There is <strong>no effective treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6731","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is not true regar​ding sickle cell retinopa‌thy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding sickle cell retinopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sea-fan appearance is seen‍ in supero-temporal quadr​ant.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbSS has more severe ocula‍r manifestations than HbS​C.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angioid stre‍aks are seen.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central retinal vein occlu‍sion may be seen.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HbSS has more severe ocular manifestations than HbSC.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70c1ccdd79984d769e9864b13d159124x439x418.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HbSS has more severe ocular manifestations than HbSC.</strong></p><p><strong>Hemoglobin SC</strong> and<strong> S-Thal</strong> disease have the <strong>most severe ocular</strong> manifestations, although sickle SS disease has the most severe systemic manifestations.</p>\n<p>The incidence of proliferative retinopathy in SC disease (Hb SC) is 33%, sickle cell thalassemia (Hb S-Thal) is 14%, SS disease (Hb SS) is 3%. In sickle cell retinopathy, elevated blood viscosity may occlude venules and arterioles, as well as capillaries. The following fundus findings can be noted as a result:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retinal vascular peripheral non-perfusion</li>\n<li>Retinal hemorrhages: \"<strong>salmon patches</strong>,\" \"<strong>iridescent spots</strong>,\" and \"<strong>black sunbursts</strong>\" are hallmarks of <strong>nonproliferative</strong> sickle cell retinopathy.</li>\n<li>Peripheral retinal neovascularization in a \"<strong>sea fan</strong>\" configuration is the <strong>hallmark</strong> of <strong>proliferative</strong> sickle cell retinopathy. They are also described as <strong>\"parachute\"</strong> lesions. They are most commonly found in the <strong>supero-temporal </strong>vascular arcades.</li>\n<li>Angioid streaks (Rupture of Bruch’s membrane)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conjunctival \"<strong>comma-shaped\" capillaries </strong>and iris atrophy are other ocular findings seen. Complications of sickle cell retinopathy include central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO) (occurs due to retinal hypoxia), vitreous hemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment.</p>\n<p>The image below shows '<strong>sea fans</strong>'<strong> </strong>as seen on <strong>fluorescein angiography</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3756","text":"Roth spots are seen in ___‍_____.","raw_text":"Roth spots are seen in ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute l‍eukemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uveal m‍elanoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uveal l‍ymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‍he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/935a7ca0fdd041699f300b438c02c372x720x583.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35e1700ad0c44e8b97647a207e778d78x720x543.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute leukemia</strong></p><p>Roth's spots are seen in <strong>acute leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Roth's spots are <strong>retinal hemorrhages </strong>with<strong> white </strong>or <strong>pale center. </strong>The<strong> </strong>white centre due to coagulated fibrin-platelet plug is surrounded by red rim that is caused by hemorrhage.  Roth's spots may be seen in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Subacute bacterial endocarditis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Leukemia</strong></li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Sickle cell retinopathy</li>\n<li>Hypertensive retinopathy</li>\n<li>HIV retinopathy</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The fundus image below shows <strong>Roth's spots</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Uveal melanoma </strong>devolps due to <strong>malignant transformation</strong> of the normal <strong>melanocytes</strong> present in the uveal tissue. Primary posterior uveal melanoma (choroidal melanoma) which forms the majority of uveal melanoma manifests as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A solitary elevated subretinal <strong>grey-brown</strong> dome shaped mass</li>\n<li><strong>\"Collar stud\" </strong>appearance on <strong>B-scan </strong>- when the tumor breaks through the Bruchs membrane and protrudes into subretinal space</li>\n<li><strong>\"Double circulation\"</strong> sign</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary anterior uveal melanoma frequently involves the iris and presents as nodular melanocytic mass on the iris. The below given fundus image shows the \"<strong>double circulation sign\"</strong> of choroidal melanoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Uveal lymphoma </strong>is characterized by localized or diffuse uveal infiltrates of malignant lymphoma cells. Fundus examination in these patients show diffuse creamy thickening of posterior uvea with nodular accentuation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1549","text":"A 7-year-old boy presents ‍with 6/6 vision in the ri​ght eye and vision for on‌ly hand movements in the ‌left eye. On fundoscopy, ‌the right eye appears nor​mal. The left eye shows r‌etinal detachment, subret‍inal yellowish exudates, ​and telangiectatic vessel​s. What is the most proba‍ble diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy presents with 6/6 vision in the right eye and vision for only hand movements in the left eye. On fundoscopy, the right eye appears normal. The left eye shows retinal detachment, subretinal yellowish exudates, and telangiectatic vessels. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sympathetic ‍ophthalmitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Familial exudative vitreor‍etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinopathy of prematurity‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coat's ‍disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coat's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d6a6c5012ec4745b0c4b1563671343ax496x445.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coat&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>Unilateral decreased vision with retinal detachment, subretinal yellowish exudates and telangiectatic vessels in a young boy points to the diagnosis of <strong>Coats' disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Coats’ disease,</strong> described by Coats in 1908, affects<strong> boys</strong> three times more often than girls, has<strong> </strong>no reported racial or ethnic predilection, and is usually<strong> unilateral. </strong>The average<strong> </strong>age at diagnosis is 8–16 years. The clinical course is generally progressive. </p>\n<p>It is characterized by<strong> </strong>discrete zones of retinal vascular abnormalities like <strong>aneurysmal dilatation </strong>and<strong> leakage</strong>. It is associated with localized <strong>lipid deposition</strong> (hard exudates) and varying degrees of <strong>subretinal exudates. </strong>Vessels may appear <strong>telangiectatic</strong>, and they may have <strong>aneurysms</strong> that are grape-like, clustered, or light bulb-shaped. </p>\n<p>The image below depicts the typical vascular abnormalities with aneurysmal dilatation, telangiectasia, exudation, and severe lipid deposition in the macula which are seen in Coat's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3864","text":"A 64-year-old female prese‍nted with complaints of b​lurring of vision and pai‌n in her left eye. She al‌so gave a history of redn‌ess in her left eye about​ 3 months back, which she‌ passed off as normal con‍junctivitis. On tonometry​, the IOP is 55 mm Hg. Th​e slit-lamp revealed rube‍osis iridis in all four q​uadrants. The fundus pict‌ure of the patient is giv​en below. What is the mos‌t probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old female presented with complaints of blurring of vision and pain in her left eye. She also gave a history of redness in her left eye about 3 months back, which she passed off as normal conjunctivitis. On tonometry, the IOP is 55 mm Hg. The slit-lamp revealed rubeosis iridis in all four quadrants. The fundus picture of the patient is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central retinal vein occlu‍sion (CRVO)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Branched retinal vein occl‍usion (BRVO)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central retinal arterial o‍cclusion (CRAO)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Branched retinal artery oc‍clusion (BRAO)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/138379357230491da57664e9bf3e03ff.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO)</strong></p><p>Monocular blurring of vision and pain with \"<strong>splashed sauce\" appearance </strong>on fundoscopy points towards a diagnosis of <strong>central retinal vein occlusion </strong>(CRVO).</p>\n<p>Ischemic central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO) is characterised by <strong>sudden visual loss</strong>. There is splashed sauce appearance on fundoscopy due to retinal hemorrhages. Risk factors include high intraocular pressure (IOP), hypertension and blood viscosity syndromes. </p>\n<p>Its complications include <strong>neovascularisation</strong> of the iris (<strong>rubeosis iridis</strong>) and <strong>neovascular glaucoma</strong> (NVG). NVG typically occurs 3 months after the occlusive event. Up to 50% of eyes develop NVG following ischaemic CRVO. <strong>Pan-retinal photocoagulation </strong>is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>CRVO is classified into two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ischemic</strong> CRVO: Characterized by relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD), markedly reduced visual acuity, and many cotton-wool spots</li>\n<li><strong>Non-ischemic</strong> CRVO: Characterized by normal pupillary reaction, reduced visual acuity, and few cotton-wool spots<br/>Treatment usually involves treating the underlying cause and complications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0373","text":"A man with history of hype‍rtension and loss of visi​on in the left eye has th‌e following fundoscopy fi‌ndings. What is the likel‌y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man with history of hypertension and loss of vision in the left eye has the following fundoscopy findings. What is the likely diagnosis? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central retinal arterial o‍cclusion (CRAO)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central retinal venous occ‍lusion (CRVO)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Branched retinal arterial ‍occlusion (BRAO)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Branched retinal venous oc‍clusion (BRVO)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Branched retinal venous occlusion (BRVO)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c4c9a446c9a44d6af900f7b93978013.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Branched retinal venous occlusion (BRVO)</strong></p><p>The image above demonstrates characteristic flame-shaped hemorrhages, localized to the superotemporal sector of the retina. The image, along with loss of vision and history of hypertension, points to a diagnosis of <strong>branched retinal vein occlusion</strong> (BRVO),<strong> </strong>specifically affecting the <strong>superotemporal </strong>branch of the retinal vein.</p>\n<p>Occlusion of a single branch of the retinal vein leads to branched retinal vein occlusion. This occlusion leads to<strong> impaired venous drainage</strong>, resulting in the accumulation of blood within the retina. The visual defect in BRVO tends to be partial. </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The different types of retinal vascular obstructions are branched retinal vein obstruction (BRVO), central retinal vein obstruction (CRVO), branched retinal artery obstruction (BRAO) and central retinal artery obstruction (CRAO). <strong>Retinal vein occlusion (RVO)</strong> is the <strong>second most common</strong> retinal vascular disorder after diabetic retinopathy.</p>\n<p>In CRVO, tortuosity and engorgement of all branches of the central retinal vein are seen, affecting all quadrants of the retina.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8335","text":"A 67-year-old male present‍s to the emergency depart​ment with sudden painless‌ vision loss in his left ‌eye. He has had a similar‌ episode before but his v​ision was quickly restore‌d. On physical exam, a ca‍rotid bruit is heard and ​a relative afferent pupil​lary defect is noted on o‍cular examination. His fu​ndus appears as shown bel‌ow. Which of the followin​g is the best immediate s‌tep in the management of ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden painless vision loss in his left eye. He has had a similar episode before but his vision was quickly restored. On physical exam, a carotid bruit is heard and a relative afferent pupillary defect is noted on ocular examination. His fundus appears as shown below. Which of the following is the best immediate step in the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ocular ‍massage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ster‍oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laser phot‍odisruption","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrinolyt‍ic therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ocular massage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/664903b987d846ad963e0fbb5e38a5b6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ocular massage</strong></p><p>Sudden painless unilateral vision loss with carotid bruit (carotid artery disease) and the fundus picture showing a cherry-red spot is suggestive of <strong>central retinal artery occlusion </strong>(CRAO)<strong>. </strong>The best immediate step in management is <strong>ocular massage</strong> among the given options.</p>\n<p>Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)<strong> </strong>occurs due to obstruction of the retinal artery at the level of<strong> lamina cribrosa</strong>. Risk factors include heart disease, carotid artery disease, mucormycosis, varicella infection, radiation exposure and trauma. It may be caused by the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Emboli of <strong>carotid artery </strong>or <strong>cardiac origin</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> cause of CRAO. Other emboli are cholesterol emboli, calcium emboli, and platelet fibrin emboli</li>\n<li>Atherosclerosis-related thrombosis</li>\n<li>Retinal arteritis with obliteration</li>\n<li>Angiospasm</li>\n<li>Raised intraocular pressure</li>\n<li>Thrombophilic disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients often present with sudden painless monocular loss of vision with history of a similar episode that resolved spontaneously (<strong>amaurosis fugax</strong>). On examination, <strong>d<span class=\"s1\"><strong>ir</strong>ect pupillary reflex </span></strong>is absent and <strong>r</strong><strong>elative afferent pupillary defect </strong>(RAPD) is positive. Fundus examination <span class=\"s1\">shows m</span>arked attenuation of arteries (thread like arteries), interrupted blood flow in vein (<strong>cattle track</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>)<strong>, c</strong><strong>herry red spot</strong>,<strong> </strong>severe edema and retinal pallor.</p>\n<p>A <strong>cherry-red spot</strong> of the macula is typical and arises in this area because of the absence of ganglion cells at the foveola resulting in the transmission of the normal choroidal color. This in contrast to the surrounding area where intense retinal whitening blocks the transmission of the normal choroidal color.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>is to dislodge the emboli by suddenly decreasing the IOP by <strong>IV acetazolamide</strong> or<strong> </strong>ocular massage.<strong> Ocular massage</strong> is considered the <strong>most effective</strong> method. Other treatment options include IV mannitol, vasodilators (nitrates), fibrinolytic therapy, or laser photodisruption in some cases. In cases of giant cell arteritis, steroids are beneficial.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0376","text":"A 52-year-old woman presen‍ts with complaints of sud​den painless loss of visi‌on in the left eye since ‌yesterday. She also gives‌ a history of temporal he​adaches, myalgias, and pa‌in in the jaw while chewi‍ng. Examination of the le​ft eye reveals a visual a​cuity of HM+ and an RAPD.‍ Fundus examination of th​e left eye shows cattle t‌rucking of blood within t​he retinal veins on apply‌ing gentle pressure. Whic‍h of the following is the​ likely cause of fundal f‍indings?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman presents with complaints of sudden painless loss of vision in the left eye since yesterday. She also gives a history of temporal headaches, myalgias, and pain in the jaw while chewing. Examination of the left eye reveals a visual acuity of HM+ and an RAPD. Fundus examination of the left eye shows cattle trucking of blood within the retinal veins on applying gentle pressure. Which of the following is the likely cause of fundal findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemic central retinal v‍enous occlusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central retinal artery occ‍lusion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non- ischemic central reti‍nal venous occlusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Branched retinal artery oc‍clusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central retinal artery occlusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central retinal artery occlusion</strong></p><p>A 52-year-old woman temporal headaches, myalgias and pain in the jaw while chewing likely has <strong>giant cell arteritis</strong> (GCA). Given that she has sudden painless monocular loss of vision with RAPD and points to a diagnosis of <strong>central retinal artery occlusion </strong>(CRAO).</p>\n<p>GCA is the <strong>most common primary vasculitis</strong> in <strong>adults. </strong>It is a risk factor for the development of CRAO. CRAO occurs due to obstruction of the retinal artery at the level of <strong>lamina cribrosa</strong>. When obstruction to the blood flow is not complete, the flow may be partially restored. Thus, gentle pressure upon the globe may break the column of venous blood into red beads separated by clear interspaces. This gives the characteristic 'cattle truck' appearance.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Ischemic CRVO </strong>shows a characteristic <strong>\"splashed tomato sauce\"</strong> appearance on fundoscopy. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Non-Ischemic CRVO</strong> does not present with such a severe loss of visual acuity.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Branched retinal artery occlusion</strong> presents with hemifield visual defects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6608","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‍ with sudden painless los​s of vision. Fundoscopy f‌indings are shown below. ‌Which of the following fi‌ndings do you expect in t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with sudden painless loss of vision. Fundoscopy findings are shown below. Which of the following findings do you expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relative afferent pupillar‍y defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral visual field lo‍ss","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Redness of ‍conjunctiva","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased intraocular pres‍sure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Relative afferent pupillary defect","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df0a659c0d124a1596e178b0793378cb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/114f80561fe34dd8859726527f9eafc4x510x451.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Relative afferent pupillary defect</strong></p><p>Sudden painless monocular loss of vision with fundoscopy showing <strong>Hollehorst plaque</strong> is suggestive of <strong>central retinal artery occlusion</strong> (CRAO).<strong> Relative afferent pupillary defect </strong>is seen in these cases.</p>\n<p>Hollenhorst plaques are<strong> cholesterol emboli</strong> that are seen in retinal arterioles. On fundus examination, it gives an appearance of a refractile <strong>yellow-white color</strong> substance in the retinal vessels. </p>\n<p>Note: Although elevated intraocular pressure has been implicated as a cause of central retinal artery obstruction, it is unlikely that intraocular pressure can be raised high enough to block arterial inflow to the eye </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0385","text":"A 61-year-old university p‍rofessor presents with a ​loss of vision in his lef‌t eye. He describes the s‌ensation that a curtain w‌as coming up over his aff​ected eye. In addition, 7‌ days ago the patient not‍iced what he described as​ flashing lights and floa​ters. He gives a history ‍of having cataract surger​y in the same eye 3 weeks‌ ago. A B-scan image of h​is left eye is shown belo‌w. Which of the following‍ statements best describe​s the pathology? (NSR- Ne‍urosensory retina , RPE- ‌Retinal pigment epitheliu‍m)","raw_text":"<p>A 61-year-old university professor presents with a loss of vision in his left eye. He describes the sensation that a curtain was coming up over his affected eye. In addition, 7 days ago the patient noticed what he described as flashing lights and floaters. He gives a history of having cataract surgery in the same eye 3 weeks ago. A B-scan image of his left eye is shown below. Which of the following statements best describes the pathology?</p>\n<p>(NSR- Neurosensory retina , RPE- Retinal pigment epithelium)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluid accumulation below r‍etina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrovascular membranes in‍ the vitreous causing tra​ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluid accumulation between‍ NSR and RPE due to retin​al tear","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fluid accumulation between‍ NSR and RPE due to choro​idal exudation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluid accumulation between NSR and RPE due to retinal tear","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13e723a817f842328035cb0a90a9e747.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5e38fa735d648b3941a1af2a4b48484x1280x1326.9724770642201.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluid accumulation between NSR and RPE due to retinal tear</strong></p><p>The prodromal symptoms of flashing lights, floaters followed by loss of monocular vision (curtain over the eye) and the B-scan suggest a diagnosis of<strong> rhegmatogenous retinal detachment</strong>. Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment is the result of the accumulation of <strong>fluid </strong>between <strong>NSR </strong>and<strong> RPE</strong> due to a<strong> retinal tear</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Retinal detachment</strong> is the separation of the neurosensory retina from the retinal pigment epithelium. Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment is the most common type of retinal detachment. It occurs due to fluid accumulation after a retinal break (tear). High myopia, trauma, <strong>pseudophakia</strong>, aphakia are predisposing factors. Patients present with photopsia (flashes of light due to vitreoretinal traction at the site of the break), floaters and visual field defect (black curtain/veil in front of the eye). </p>\n<p>Signs include<strong> hypotony</strong>, as the liquefied vitreous in the subretinal space is absorbed through the RPE and <strong>Shafers sign </strong>or<strong> tobacco dusting </strong>(pigments in the anterior vitreous). The detached retina is convex in configuration. The surface is corrugated with undulations. The most dangerous type of retinal break is <strong>horseshoe tear </strong>and the most dangerous location of retinal break is the <strong>supero-temporal retina. </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is by prophylactic laser barrage, scleral buckling, pneumatic retinopexy and pars plana vitrectomy. </p>\n<p>Other types of retinal detachment are<strong> tractional </strong>and <strong>exudative</strong> type:</p>\n<p><strong>Tractional retinal detachment </strong>occurs when<strong> f</strong><strong>ibro-vascular membranes</strong> in the <strong>vitreous</strong> due to long-standing <strong>retinal neovascularization</strong> exert traction on the retina. It is seen in proliferative diabetic retinopathy, central retinal venous occlusion, Eales disease and ROP. The detached retina is<strong> concave</strong> in configuration. No breaks are seen. Treatment is with pars plana vitrectomy with endo photocoagulation. </p>\n<p><strong>Exudative retinal detachment</strong> is seen when<strong> e</strong><strong>xudative fluid</strong> from the <strong>choroid</strong> collects in the <strong>subretinal space.</strong> Patients present with visual field defects, <strong>floaters</strong> due to vitritis but <strong>no photopsia. </strong>The detached retina is<strong> convex</strong> in configuration. The surface is smooth. No break is seen. Shifting fluid is seen. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a fundus which reveals retinal detachment inferiorly from 3-9 o'clock with tear at 3:30.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1482","text":"Pneumatic retinopexy is an‍ outpatient procedure whe​re retinal detachment is ‌sealed with air insufflat‌ion. Which of the followi‌ng gases would you use wh​ile performing the said p‌rocedure?","raw_text":"Pneumatic retinopexy is an outpatient procedure where retinal detachment is sealed with air insufflation. Which of the following gases would you use while performing the said procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon ‍dioxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulphur he‍xafluoride","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nitroge‍n oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Selenium h‍exafluoride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sulphur hexafluoride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e329035ec02b4f9d91f50ff95f397695x1280x1974.160789844852.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sulphur hexafluoride</strong></p><p><strong>Sulphur hexafluoride</strong> is the gas used in <strong>pneumatic retinopexy</strong> for the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>retinal detachments</strong></p>\n<p>Pneumatic retinopexy is used for the treatment of fresh retinal detachments with a single retinal break in the <strong>superior two-thirds</strong> of the <strong>fundus. </strong>In this procedure, a bubble is injected intravitreally through the conjunctiva and postoperatively the patient is positioned so that the bubble tamponades the retinal break against the retinal pigment epithelium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0392","text":"A 24-year-old woman who is‍ 8 months pregnant, myopi​c, and diabetic presents ‌to you with sudden painle‌ss loss of vision in her ‌right eye. She is a known​ case of Eales' disease. ‌Her blood pressure is 170‍/110 mmHg. Which of her c​onditions is responsible ​for a fundus finding of r‍etinal detachment with a ​positive shifting fluid s‌ign?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman who is 8 months pregnant, myopic, and diabetic presents to you with sudden painless loss of vision in her right eye. She is a known case of Eales' disease. Her blood pressure is 170/110 mmHg. Which of her conditions is responsible for a fundus finding of retinal detachment with a positive shifting fluid sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathologi‍cal myopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 1 ‍diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eales ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxemia of‍ pregnancy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Toxemia of pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66058a9e047b4d968bbb779e30e6a5b8x420x311.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Toxemia of pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong> Exudative retinal detachment </strong>is characterized by positive shifting fluid sign. Among her conditions, toxemia of pregnancy is one of the causes of exudative retinal detachment. </p>\n<p>Exudative retinal detachment occurs due to the presence of subretinal fluid in the absence of tear or traction. It is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignant melanoma of the choroid</li>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Vogt–Koyanagi–Harada (VKH) syndrome</li>\n<li>Uveal effusion syndrome</li>\n<li>Posterior scleritis</li>\n<li>Coat’s disease</li>\n<li>Malignant hypertension</li>\n<li>Pregnancy-induced hypertensive retinopathy</li>\n<li>Sympathetic ophthalmitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients present with sudden and painless diminution of vision, visual field defects and<strong> grey reflex</strong> (detached retina looks grey).<strong> Indirect ophthalmoscopy </strong>is the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>The image depicts retinal detachment showing <strong>a smooth, detached convex retina</strong> with subretinal fluid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0661","text":"Shifting fluid sign is see‍n in?","raw_text":"Shifting fluid sign is seen in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exudat‍ive RD","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tract‍ion RD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhegmato‍genous RD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retinal ‍dialysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Exudative RD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Exudative RD</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>shifting</strong> <strong>fluid</strong> is a hallmark feature of <strong>serous</strong> or <strong>exudative retinal detachment.</strong></p>\n<p>In serous retinal detachment, there is a subretinal accumulation of the serous fluid mostly due to the breakdown in the blood-retinal barrier.<strong> </strong>When the position of the head is altered, the fluid shifts to the dependent portion. Serous retinal detachment has a smooth convex configuration. A<strong> retinal</strong> <strong>tear</strong> or <strong>preretinal</strong> <strong>traction</strong> is <strong>absent</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options: <br/>Option B: <strong>Traction RD</strong> has a concave configuration and breaks are absent. Retinal mobility is severely reduced and shifting fluid is absent.<br/>Option C: <strong>Rhegmatogenous RD </strong>presents with tobacco dust in anterior vitreous with substantial vitreous blood or inflammatory cells. Retinal breaks are also seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0377","text":"Hollenhorst plaques are se‍en in _______.","raw_text":"Hollenhorst plaques are seen in _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central retinal arterial o‍cclusion ( CRAO)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Central retinal venous occ‍lusion ( CRVO)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystoid ma‍cular edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central serous retinopathy‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Central retinal arterial occlusion ( CRAO)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9406b7c3c704395877a1c566a3308a8x275x435.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Central retinal arterial occlusion ( CRAO)</strong></p><p><strong>Hollenhorst plaques</strong> are<strong> </strong>seen in <strong>central retinal arterial occlusion</strong> (CRAO). </p>\n<p>They are <strong>cholesterol emboli</strong> seen in retinal arterioles. They originate from atherosclerotis plaques in larger arteries. The image below shows Hollenhorst plaques (arrow).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0365","text":"Identify th‍e condition.","raw_text":"Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foveal d‍etachment","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Macula‍r hole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macula‍r edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macular‍ Drusens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Foveal detachment","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a51c7bc975142ae8561008fdeb4e1eb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ec581079d2445249767a15f82b3fe0fx598x491.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Foveal detachment</strong></p><p>The above image shows a loss of <strong>foveal reflex</strong> and <strong>central serous retinopathy</strong>, therefore showing focal<strong> foveal detachment</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is <strong>oedema</strong> at the fovea signifying foveal detachment from underlying retinal pigment epithelium. <strong>Macular oedema</strong> is also present (grey dull retina with a ring reflex around the site of oedema). Thus, the most appropriate choice would be foveal detachment.</p>\n<p><strong>Central serous retinopathy</strong> is the spontaneous serous detachment of neurosensory retina in the macular region. It occurs due to increased permeability of choriocapillaris, leading to fluid accumulation in the subretinal space.<strong> </strong>Therefore, it is now known as <strong>central serous choriopathy</strong> (CSC).</p>\n<p>If there is serous retinal detachment at macula, there is <strong>sudden painless loss</strong> of vision. Patients also have relative positive scotoma, micropsia and metamorphopsia. On ophthalmoscopy, a circular ring reflex present. The foveal reflex is absent. On fundus fluorescein angiography (FFA), an <strong>enlarging ink-blot pattern</strong> or <strong>smoke-stack pattern </strong>is seen.</p>\n<p>Treatment is generally not required. It is usually <strong>self-limiting,</strong> but often <strong>recurrent. </strong><strong>Steroids</strong> are <strong>contraindicated</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0366","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions is the given s​ign seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is the given sign seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystoid ma‍cular edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central serous retinopathy‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Significant ‍macular edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coat’s ‍disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central serous retinopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3644d4f98f42431c99caf0e33f5820f5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6359038b82984c289943119198f5ebbdx1280x1511.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central serous retinopathy</strong></p><p>The image depicts fundus fluorescein angiography (FFA) showing an enlarging <strong>ink-blot pattern/enlarging dot sign</strong>, which is seen in <strong>central serous retinopathy </strong>(CSR). <strong>Smokestack appearance</strong> is another pattern of fundus seen in CSR and is a characteristic sign of this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Amsler grid</strong> is used for: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assess macular function and detecting maculopathy.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the visual field of 10°</li>\n<li>Good for follow-up in CSR</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1492","text":"The ETDRS chart is used fo‍r visual evaluation in di​abetic patients. What doe‌s ETDRS stand for?","raw_text":"The ETDRS chart is used for visual evaluation in diabetic patients. What does ETDRS stand for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extended treatment for dia‍betic retinopathy study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early treatment for diabet‍ic retinopathy study","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Emergency treatment for di‍abetic retinopathy study","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emerging treatment for dia‍betic retinopathy study","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Early treatment for diabetic retinopathy study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Early treatment for diabetic retinopathy study</strong></p><p><strong>ETDRS</strong> stands for<strong> </strong>Early Treatment for Diabetic Retinopathy Study.</p>\n<p>ETDRS was a clinical trial designed to evaluate if argon laser photocoagulation or aspirin treatment can reduce the risk of visual loss or slow the progression of diabetic retinopathy in patients with mild-to-severe nonproliferative or early proliferative diabetic retinopathy. During the study, three new visual acuity charts were created in place of Snellen's visual chart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xKrbtyqn","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"9_fractures_of_femur","title":"9.Fractures Of Femur","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MD0730","text":"Which of the following fac​tors increases the risk o​f femoral neck fractures?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following factors increases the risk of femoral neck fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoparat​hyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proton pump​ inhibitors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased levels of physic​al activity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proton pump inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proton pump inhibitors</strong></p><p>The use of <strong>proton pump inhibitors</strong> is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for femoral neck fractures. </p>\n<p>Femoral neck fracture is a type of intracapsular hip fracture. Common <strong>risk factors</strong> for <strong>hip fractures</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing age</li>\n<li>Female sex</li>\n<li>White population of Europe and North America</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Decreased level of physical activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Comorbidities</strong> that can cause hip fractures are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>Celiac disease</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Hypo and hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Primary hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Chronic kidney and liver disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> that can cause hip fractures are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corticosteroids</li>\n<li><strong>Proton pump inhibitors</strong></li>\n<li>Antipsychotic medication</li>\n<li>Aromatase inhibitors</li>\n<li>Gonadotropin-releasing hormone inhibitors</li>\n<li>Long-acting progesterone-only pills</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8041","text":"Which of the following cla​ssifications is not used ​for neck of femur fractur‌es?","raw_text":"Which of the following classifications is not used for neck of femur fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pauwels cla​ssification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Garden cla​ssification","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delbet cla​ssification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neer's cla​ssification","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neer's classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/63ccdd80d6ff4792845182859da590b6x1280x1149.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd0847be7c4f48088e5b1be0f8ae8d18x1280x1586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neer&#x27;s classification</strong></p><p><strong>Neer's</strong> classification is used for <strong>proximal humeral</strong> fractures.</p>\n<p>Classifications used for <strong>neck of femur fracture</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Based on the location of the fracture line</li>\n<li>Pauwels classification</li>\n<li>Garden classification</li>\n<li>Delbet classification (hip fractures in the pediatric population)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on the <strong>location</strong> of the <strong>fracture line</strong> it can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sub-capital - fracture at the junction of head and neck</li>\n<li>Transcervical - fracture in the neck</li>\n<li>Basi-cervical - fracture at the base of the neck</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Delbet</strong> classification is used for hip fractures in <strong>children</strong>. They are grouped into four types based on their location as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I - Transepiphyseal fracture</li>\n<li>Type II - Transcervical fracture</li>\n<li>Type III - Cervicotrochanteric fracture</li>\n<li>Type IV - Intertrochanteric fracture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8043","text":"The X-ray of a patient wit​h femoral neck fracture s​howed that all 3 sets of ‌trabeculae were out of al​ignment. Which Garden's s‌tage of fracture shows th‌is finding?","raw_text":"The X-ray of a patient with femoral neck fracture showed that all 3 sets of trabeculae were out of alignment. Which Garden's stage of fracture shows this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta​ge I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag​e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag​e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stag​e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd0847be7c4f48088e5b1be0f8ae8d18x1280x1586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage III</strong></p><p>In <strong>Garden's stage III</strong> fracture neck of femur, all the <strong>3</strong> sets of <strong>trabeculae</strong> are <strong>out of alignment.</strong></p>\n<p>Garden classification of fracture neck of femur is based on the degree of displacement.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage I - incomplete fracture line and femoral head is in slight valgus</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage II - complete fracture line and non-displaced</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage III - complete fracture line and partially displaced</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage IV - complete fracture line and completely displaced </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8109","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding​ Pauwel's classification ‌of neck of femur fracture​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Pauwel's classification of neck of femur fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pauwel's angle is directly​ proportional to stabilit​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 fracture makes an a​ngle of 60 degrees","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plane of reference is the ​horizontal plane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pauwel's angle is formed b​y fracture line with inte​r-trochanteric line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plane of reference is the horizontal plane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a297351cb96743b4b98c95abcb36c7fbx1280x1149.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plane of reference is the horizontal plane</strong></p><p>The plane of reference in Pauwel's classification is the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>. This classification is based on <strong>Pauwel's angle</strong> i.e. the angle made by the <strong>fracture line</strong> in reference to the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When the Pauwel's angle is <strong>small</strong>, compressive forces act at the fracture ends and hence the fracture is <strong>stable</strong>. As Pauwel's angle increases, the fracture line becomes more vertical and the shearing forces act on the fractured ends. Hence, at <strong>higher angles</strong> the fracture becomes <strong>unstable</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9506","text":"An elderly patient slipped​ in the bathroom and sust​ained injury over the lef‌t hip. The radiograph is ​shown below. His attitude‌ of the leg will be:","raw_text":"An elderly patient slipped in the bathroom and sustained injury over the left hip. The radiograph is shown below. His attitude of the leg will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shortened a​nd abducted","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lengthened and internally ​rotated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shortened and externally r​otated","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flexed, adducted and inter​nally rotated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Shortened and externally rotated","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49f2c42b7d6d4a24b655b430415fe243.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53e9673a803e46d7aaea91bcdbbb267fx1280x1010.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Shortened and externally rotated</strong></p><p>The history of fall and hip injury in an elderly patient along with the radiological findings of a broken Shenton's line is suggestive of a<strong> neck of femur fracture. </strong>In this condition, the attitude of the leg will be <strong>shortened </strong>and<strong> externally rotated </strong>and movements of the hip joint will be painful.</p>\n<p><strong>A plain</strong> <strong>radiograph</strong> may show the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fracture line in the neck of femur</li>\n<li>External rotation of the femur with the lesser trochanter appearing more prominent</li>\n<li>Displacement of the greater trochanter</li>\n<li>Break in the trabecular stream</li>\n<li>Break in Shenton's line</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8094","text":"A 70-year-old lady, a know​n case of chronic osteoar​thritis of the hip, has a‌ trivial fall while climb​ing the stairs. Her right‌ lower limb is in slight ‌external rotation. X-ray ‍pelvis is shown below. Wh​at is the next step of ma‍nagement?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old lady, a known case of chronic osteoarthritis of the hip, has a trivial fall while climbing the stairs. Her right lower limb is in slight external rotation. X-ray pelvis is shown below. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Open reduction and interna​l fixation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closed reduction and inter​nal fixation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemi-art​hroplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total art​hroplasty","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Total arthroplasty","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f7719780fd434719aafa97795cca3aeb.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b46b1d4b6364c6e94fd7d5f22ebb297x716x452.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Total arthroplasty</strong></p><p>The given scenario and X-ray are suggestive of right-sided <strong>fracture neck of femur</strong>, Garden stage IV. Since the patient is <strong>&gt;65 years</strong> with <strong>existing arthritis</strong>, the best choice of treatment is <strong>total hip arthroplasty. </strong></p>\n<p>In the absence of arthritis, fracture neck of femur in patients &gt;65 years, is treated by hemiarthroplasty.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows <strong>total hip arthroplasty</strong> done for fracture neck of femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8078","text":"Which of the following is ​most commonly used for in​ternal fixation of fractu‌re of femoral neck in you​ng adults?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most commonly used for internal fixation of fracture of femoral neck in young adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cannulated cancellous scre​ws","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dynamic ​hip screw","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Austin Moo​re implant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herber​t screw","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cannulated cancellous screws","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd680622d80b49a09837bd5a7caad30cx152x176.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ffdced4066b84af3b0120b9c3cde23b7x907x1197.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cannulated cancellous screws</strong></p><p>The most commonly used equipment for <strong>internal fixation</strong> of fracture neck of femur in <strong>young adults</strong> is multiple <strong>cannulated cancellous screws. </strong></p>\n<p>The radiograph given below shows internal fixation of the neck of femur fracture with cannulated cancellous screws. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Dynamic hip screw</strong> is used in some cases of fracture neck of femur and for the internal fixation of the <strong>intertrochanteric fractures</strong>. The image below shows a dynamic hip screw.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Austin Moore's</strong> implant is an older implant used in <strong>unipolar hemiarthroplasty</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Herbert screw</strong> is used in bridging a <strong>scaphoid fracture.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8096","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of rig​ht-sided hip pain and res‌triction of movements of ​the right lower limb for ‌3 weeks. She had a histor‌y of trivial trauma 3 wee‍ks ago. X-ray reveals an ​impacted fracture neck of‍ the right femur. What is​ the next step of managem‌ent?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presents with complaints of right-sided hip pain and restriction of movements of the right lower limb for 3 weeks. She had a history of trivial trauma 3 weeks ago. X-ray reveals an impacted fracture neck of the right femur. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ORIF with fibular bone gra​ft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"McMurray's​ osteotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Evaluate fur​ther with MRI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemiarth​roplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Evaluate further with MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f41b65211c3a47bd9a580e052780b606x1022x764.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Evaluate further with MRI</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario with a <strong>3-week old</strong> femoral head <strong>fracture,</strong> the next step will be to do an <strong>MRI hip. </strong>This is to evaluate the <strong>femoral head</strong> for <strong>compromise</strong> in the <strong>blood supply</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment of the femoral head fracture depends on the findings seen on an MRI:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Femoral head shows <strong>compromised blood supply </strong>- reduction with<strong> fibular bone graft</strong> or muscle pedicle graft</li>\n<li>Femoral head shows signs of <strong>osteonecrosis </strong>and acetabulum is normal - <strong>hemiarthroplasty</strong> </li>\n<li>Femoral head is normal but the fracture is nearly <strong>vertical </strong>(can develop to non-union) - <strong>osteotomy</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows osteonecrosis of the femoral head seen on an MRI.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8097","text":"A 56-year-old man came to ​OPD with a 1-month-old fr​acture neck of femur. X-r‌ay revealed Pauwel's angl​e to be 70°. MRI showed a‌ normal femoral head. Wha‌t is the next step in man‍agement?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man came to OPD with a 1-month-old fracture neck of femur. X-ray revealed Pauwel's angle to be 70°. MRI showed a normal femoral head. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Open reduction and interna​l fixation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mc Murray'​s osteotomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemi-art​hroplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total hip a​rthroplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mc Murray's osteotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mc Murray&#x27;s osteotomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a 1-month-old <strong>Pauwel's type III</strong> fracture (&gt;50°), which is nearly vertical and has an increased chance of <strong>non-union</strong>. Hence, the best management would be to compress the fractured ends by <strong>Mc Murray's osteotomy </strong>and<strong> internal fixation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8099","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g McMurray's osteotomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding McMurray's osteotomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an oblique osteotomy​ in the inter-trochanteri​c region","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It involves lateral displa​cement and adduction of t​he distal fragment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The line of weight bearing​ after the osteotomy pass​es from the head to the d‌istal fragment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It converts shearing stres​s to compressive forces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It involves lateral displacement and adduction of the distal fragment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/524a9cb5225c4a14845c988167877499x1279x1149.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It involves lateral displacement and adduction of the distal fragment</strong></p><p>In <strong>McMurray's osteotomy</strong>, the distal fragment is displaced <strong>medially </strong>and is <strong>abducted</strong>. This converts shearing stresses at the fracture site to compressive forces promoting faster healing. </p>\n<p>McMurray's osteotomy is an oblique osteotomy directed medially upwards at the <strong>inter-trochanteric region</strong>. It is based on the <strong>biomechanical </strong>principle. After the medial displacement of the femur, the <strong>weight-bearing line</strong> passes from the head to the distal fragment. It, therefore, <strong>bypasses</strong> the <strong>fracture site</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8074","text":"A 35-year-old woman with a​ history of ORIF for femo​ral neck fracture came fo‌r post-operative follow u​p after 7 months. She is ‌unable to bear weight on ‌the operated side. Her X-‍ray showed no evidence of​ healing. Which of the fo‍llowing is not a probable​ cause for this condition‌?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of ORIF for femoral neck fracture came for post-operative follow up after 7 months. She is unable to bear weight on the operated side. Her X-ray showed no evidence of healing. Which of the following is not a probable cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Improper im​mobilization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avascularity of the femora​l head","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interference of synovial f​luid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophy of cambium lay​er of periosteum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypertrophy of cambium layer of periosteum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7c2bfb47e0d4469a189ea11827e154ax1128x632.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypertrophy of cambium layer of periosteum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>non-union</strong> of <strong>femoral neck fracture</strong> after ORIF, as there is no evidence of healing 6-12 months post-operatively. <strong>Lack</strong> of <strong>cambium layer</strong> in the periosteum of neck of femur is one of the causes of non-union.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> for <strong>non-union</strong> in the neck of femur fracture are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inadequate blood supply of femoral head</li>\n<li>Since the fracture is intra-articular, the synovial fluid interferes with fracture healing.</li>\n<li>Lack of the cambium layer of periosteum, which produces the callus</li>\n<li>Inadequate reduction and poor internal fixation</li>\n<li>Improper immobilization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows (a) non-union of neck of femur fracture, (b) osteotomy to correct the non-union, (c) 5 months post-osteotomy with healed fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8049","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the condition​ shown in the image below‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the condition shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malunion can happen if thi​s condition is not treate​d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The patient may be able to​ walk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Avascular necrosis is the ​most common complication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shortening of limb is less​ than 1 inch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malunion can happen if this condition is not treated","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3298c7ad3959403896098e117dcd4eea.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malunion can happen if this condition is not treated</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows a <strong>fracture neck</strong> of the <strong>femur. Non-union</strong> is a complication of this fracture, not malunion. </p>\n<p><strong>Malunion</strong> is a complication of <strong>inter-trochanteric</strong> fractures.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of fracture neck of the femur are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avascular necrosis (most common)</li>\n<li>Non-union</li>\n<li>Osteonecrosis of the femoral head may lead to <strong>secondary osteoarthritis</strong> after many years</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: In impacted fracture neck of femur, the patient may be able to walk.</p>\n<p>Option D: Shortening of the limb is less than 1 inch in fracture neck of femur.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8076","text":"Which of the following is ​false about avascular nec​rosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about avascular necrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be diagnosed on X-r​ay only after 4 years","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can also occur in unite​d fracture neck of femur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcapital type has worse ​prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients with sickle cell ​disease are at an increas​ed risk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It can be diagnosed on X-ray only after 4 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It can be diagnosed on X-ray only after 4 years</strong></p><p><strong>Avascular necrosis</strong> is a late complication of fracture neck of the femur, which can be diagnosed on <strong>X-ray</strong> only after a few months to <strong>2 years </strong>after the injury. Hence, MRI is the best investigation for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>The amount of vascular damage produced at the time of fracture determines the probability of the development of osteonecrosis. In addition to this, the <strong>blood</strong> <strong>supply</strong> to the <strong>femoral head</strong> may also be <strong>disrupted</strong> during <strong>internal fixation</strong>. Hence, avascular necrosis can also occur in united femur fractures.</p>\n<p>More <strong>proximally</strong> the fracture is located, <strong>worse</strong> is the <strong>prognosis</strong>. Hence, sub-capital fractures have the worst prognosis.</p>\n<p>Patients with sickle cell disease are at an increased risk for developing avascular necrosis of the femoral head.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8044","text":"A 35-year-old man is broug​ht to the casualty with a​ history of RTA. His left‌ lower limb is shortened ​by 2 inches. The attitude‌ of the leg is extension ‌and externally rotated, w‍ith the lateral border of​ the foot touching the be‍d. What is the most proba​ble diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man is brought to the casualty with a history of RTA. His left lower limb is shortened by 2 inches. The attitude of the leg is extension and externally rotated, with the lateral border of the foot touching the bed. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inter-trochanteric fractur​e femur","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture ne​ck of femur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Femoral sha​ft fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior dislocation of hi​p","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inter-trochanteric fracture femur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inter-trochanteric fracture femur</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the limb<strong> shortening &gt;1 inch</strong> and pronounced<strong> external rotation</strong> is suggestive of <strong>inter-trochanteric fracture </strong>of the<strong> femur</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Differences between fracture neck of femur and inter-trochanteric fractures:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 289px; width: 630px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Feature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Fracture neck of femur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Inter-trochanteric fracture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">More than 50 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">More than 60 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Sex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Common in females</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Common in males</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Injury</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Trivial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Significant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Ability to walk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">May walk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Unable to walk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Swelling</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Nil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Ecchymosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Nil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Tenderness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">In Scarpa's triangle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">On the greater trochanter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">External rotation deformity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Less than 45 degrees</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">More than 45 degrees (Lateral border of foot touches the bed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Shortening</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Less than 1 inch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">More than 1 inch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Internal fixation always</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Can be managed in traction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99px; height: 18px;\">Complications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.984px; height: 18px;\">Avascular necrosis and Non-union</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.016px; height: 18px;\">Malunion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>fracture neck of femur, </strong>the limb is in <strong>slight</strong> <strong>external rotation</strong> and <strong>shortening </strong>is<strong> &lt;1 inch.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: In<strong> femoral shaft fractures</strong>, the limb is <strong>shortened</strong> with displaced proximal and distal fragments.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>anterior dislocation</strong> of the hip, the limb is <strong>externally rotated</strong>. There may be a <strong>true lengthening</strong> of the limb with the femoral head palpable in the groin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8102","text":"A 69-year-old woman sustai​ns an injury to the left ​hip after falling sideway‌s on the floor. She is un​able to move the left leg‌. X-ray is given below. W‌hat is the best treatment‍ for this condition?","raw_text":"A 69-year-old woman sustains an injury to the left hip after falling sideways on the floor. She is unable to move the left leg.  X-ray is given below. What is the best treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal f​emoral nail","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"McMurray's​ osteotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total hip a​rthroplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plaster in abduction and i​nternal rotation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal femoral nail","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7d6b7ecf983e45c5836170328e45af38.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d233a221e53f412cb046e25ea1a000edx426x354.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23cee45afd4a428ba7e9a712f311a52cx1280x2056.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fdf8be397fa469b94ec2c3dc0b23eaex1279x1265.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/608f83ef64a842e291c47428dcc7e35fx1279x1542.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc3dc534cb61491d800e3eb6bbd5d098x1280x2018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal femoral nail</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the X-ray is suggestive of <strong>intertrochanteric fracture</strong> of the left <strong>femur</strong>, which is best treated by closed reduction and internal fixation <strong>proximal femoral nail.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows an intertrochanteric fracture corrected with proximal femoral nail.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The intertrochanteric fractures of the femur can be treated by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internal fixation with:\n<ul>\n<li>Intramedullary implants - proximal femoral nail and gamma nail</li>\n<li>Extramedullary implants - dynamic hip screw</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Traction:\n<ul>\n<li>Russell's traction</li>\n<li>Thomas splint</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Compared to the dynamic hip screw, the intramedullary fixation with proximal femoral nails have smaller incisions, decreased blood loss, decreased femoral neck shortening and decreased risk of infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8047","text":"A 68-year-old lady comes w​ith a history of fall and​ right-sided hip pain for‌ 1 week. On examination, ​her right limb is shorten‌ed and externally rotated‌. Her X-ray shows no sign‍ificant findings. What is​ your next step?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old lady comes with a history of fall and right-sided hip pain for 1 week. On examination, her right limb is shortened and externally rotated. Her X-ray shows no significant findings. What is your next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Send the patient home with​ analgesics for pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Technetium bone scan withi​n the next 24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Order an MRI of the hip jo​int","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Immobilise the limb and ob​serve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Order an MRI of the hip joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Order an MRI of the hip joint</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>occult neck of femur fracture</strong>, as the <strong>X-ray</strong> is <strong>normal</strong> but the <strong>attitude</strong> of the leg is <strong>abnormal</strong>. In such cases, <strong>MRI</strong> is the imaging study of choice. </p>\n<p>Both CT and MRI can detect the presence of fracture neck of femur. However, MRI can demonstrate the presence of <strong>soft tissue problems</strong> that can cause hip pain. It also does not expose the patient to radiation. Hence, <strong>MRI</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> over CT in the diagnosis of occult femoral neck fractures.</p>\n<p>Technetium bone scan is an older method to detect occult fractures. They are less specific and may not show the presence of fracture if performed within 48-72 hours and in osteopenic bones.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8119","text":"A young patient was brough​t to the casualty followi​ng a road traffic acciden‌t. The radiograph of the ​limb showed a fracture in‌ the mid-shaft of the fem‌ur. What will be the dire‍ction of displacement of ​the distal fragment?","raw_text":"A young patient was brought to the casualty following a road traffic accident. The radiograph of the limb showed a fracture in the mid-shaft of the femur. What will be the direction of displacement of the distal fragment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdu​cted","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Addu​cted","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexed and externally rota​ted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexed and internally rota​ted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adducted","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc7d9dd874c04af6b6a7b6773ab09f68x1280x1430.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a9f7e4aadcb466999f6b46f96b76049x512x671.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adducted</strong></p><p>In the <strong>fracture</strong> of <strong>midshaft</strong> of the femur, the <strong>distal fragment </strong>is<strong> adducted </strong>as the adductors and the gastrocnemius are attached to the lower part of femur. </p>\n<p>The <strong>proximal fragment</strong> is <strong>flexed</strong> and <strong>externally rotated </strong>as the gluteus medius and iliopsoas muscles are attached to the upper part of femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 140px; width: 487.793px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 468.793px;\"><strong>Displacement of fracture fragments in the fracture shaft of femur</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\"><strong>Site of fracture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\"><strong>Displacement of the proximal segment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.793px;\"><strong>Displacement of distal fragment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Proximal femoral shaft fractures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Flexed, abducted and externally rotated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.793px;\">Adducted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Mid-shaft femoral fractures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Flexed and externally rotated with minimal abduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.793px;\">Adducted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Distal femoral shaft fractures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Adducted</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.793px;\">Tilted by gastrocnemius pull</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8120","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is maximum sho​rtening of lower limb see‌n?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is maximum shortening of lower limb seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inter-trochanteric fractur​e femur","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture ne​ck of femur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture sh​aft of femur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transcervical fracture fem​ur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inter-trochanteric fracture femur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inter-trochanteric fracture femur</strong></p><p>From the given options, <strong>maximum shortening</strong> of the lower limb is seen in<strong> </strong>the<strong> Inter-trochanteric fracture femur.</strong></p>\n<p>Overall, the maximum shortening of lower limbs is seen in <strong>posterior dislocation </strong>of the<strong> hip</strong> followed by Inter-trochanteric fracture femur.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8124","text":"A 24-year old patient was ​brought to the emergency ​department after he lost ‌control of his bike and h​it a car while texting an‌d driving. On examination‌, the patient had right t‍high tenderness and defor​mity. The radiograph show‍ed the following findings​. What is the preferred m‌ethod of treatment in the​ following scenario?","raw_text":"A 24-year old patient was brought to the emergency department after he lost control of his bike and hit a car while texting and driving. On examination, the patient had right thigh tenderness and deformity. The radiograph showed the following findings. What is the preferred method of treatment in the following scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Locking comp​ression plate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intramedu​llary nail","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"External fixation with K w​ire","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Traction with Thomas splin​t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intramedullary nail","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/38bc31b39f6a47fb84d21ae61f77aa95.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intramedullary nail</strong></p><p>The above radiograph shows a <strong>fracture</strong> in the <strong>shaft of femur</strong> with the displacement of fractured ends. The preferred method of treatment in this condition is an <strong>intramedullary nail</strong> with <strong>locking screws</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Plating</strong> can be done in cases of <strong>comminuted</strong> fractures.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> External fixation </strong>is preferred in the treatment of severe <strong>open</strong> <strong>injuries</strong>, or when there is a severe bone loss that may require a bone graft.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Thomas splint</strong> is used in the <strong>emergency</strong> treatment of shaft of femur fracture and some supracondylar fractures.</p>\n<p>                  The main indications for traction are: </p>\n<p>                  (1) fractures in children </p>\n<p>                  (2) contraindications to anaesthesia or surgery </p>\n<p>                  (3) lack of suitable skill or facilities for internal fixation</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0837","text":"A 9-year-old girl was run ​over by a car and was adm​itted to the hospital wit‌h obvious swelling and de​formity of the left thigh‌. Her X-ray showed the fo‌llowing findings. What wi‍ll be the preferred treat​ment in the given conditi‍on?","raw_text":"A  9-year-old girl was run over by a car and was admitted to the hospital with obvious swelling and deformity of the left thigh. Her X-ray showed the following findings. What will be the preferred treatment in the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gallow's​ traction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Locked intramedullary nail​ing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elastic intramedullary nai​ling","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spica ​casting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elastic intramedullary nailing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f8b300ebca624ecda37ea7247b6a5d03.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5ecab51e0a84a849359854b23e45032x512x142.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elastic intramedullary nailing</strong></p><p>The preferred treatment in children of ages<strong> 5-10 years</strong> with <strong>fracture shaft </strong>of the<strong> femur</strong> is <strong>elastic intramedullary nailing.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows fracture shaft of femur treated with elastic intramedullary nailing.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0676","text":"A patient was admitted to ​ICU 48 hours after the fr​acture of the femur. The ‌saturation of oxygen in t​he rebreathing unit was 1‌00% but his Spo2 remained‌ 60%. The patient was in ‍a state of confusion. Che​st radiograph showed lung‍ fields to be clear. What​ is the most likely diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A patient was admitted to ICU 48 hours after the fracture of the femur. The saturation of oxygen in the rebreathing unit was 100% but his Spo2 remained 60%. The patient was in a state of confusion. Chest radiograph showed lung fields to be clear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary​ embolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fat em​bolism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AR​DS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occult pn​eumothorax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fat embolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fat embolism</strong></p><p>In this clinical scenario, the presence of <strong>reduced SpO2</strong> and <strong>altered mental status</strong> in a patient presenting<strong> within 72 hours</strong> following a <strong>long bone fracture</strong> suggests the diagnosis of a <strong>fat embolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fat embolism is caused by the fat globules originating from the <strong>bone marrow</strong> following<strong> the fracture </strong>of <strong>long bones.</strong> Clinical features of fat embolism are usually seen <strong>within 72 hours</strong>. They include <strong>tachycardia, fever, breathlessness</strong>, <strong>petechiae,</strong><strong> confusion,</strong>and<strong> restlessness. Gurd's criteria</strong> is used for the diagnosis of fat embolism syndrome.</p>\n<p>There is no specific diagnostic test for fat embolism. However,<strong> urine analysis</strong> to detect <strong>fat globules</strong> and monitoring of blood pO2 levels are conducted.</p>\n<p>The management of fat embolism primarily involves supportive care, including <strong>oxygen therapy </strong>and<strong> prompt stabilization of long-bone fractures.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Pulmonary embolism most commonly occurs as a result of thrombi from <strong>deep vein thrombosis</strong> of the leg. </p>\n<p>Option C: Acute respiratory distress syndrome is diagnosed using the <strong>Berlin's criteria</strong>. <strong>All four</strong> of the following conditions must be met:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It develops <strong>within 1 week</strong> of clinical insult or worsening respiratory symptoms.</li>\n<li>Chest radiograph shows <strong>bilateral opacities </strong>consistent with pulmonary edema. These opacities are not fully explained by effusions, lobar or lung collapse, or nodules.</li>\n<li>The edema is <strong>not</strong> due to <strong>cardiac failure</strong>, or <strong>fluid overload</strong>.</li>\n<li>There is <strong>impaired oxygenation</strong>. The ratio of arterial oxygen partial pressure to the fraction of inspired oxygen (<strong>PaO<sub>2</sub>/FiO<sub>2</sub></strong>) is <strong>≤ 300 mmHg</strong> as assessed on 5 cm H<sub>2</sub>O of positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option D: Occult pneumothorax is a condition that is not suspected on the basis of the symptoms or plain radiography, but is detected with <strong>CT imaging</strong>. It usually follows a thoracic injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5121","text":"Which of the following is ​considered a minor criter​ion for diagnosing fat em‌bolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is considered a minor criterion for diagnosing fat embolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subconjuncti​val petechiae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fat globule​s in sputum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"FiO2 &amp;​lt; 0.4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CNS dep​ression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fat globules in sputum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fat globules in sputum</strong></p><p><strong>Fat globules </strong>in sputum is a <strong>minor criterion</strong> for diagnosing <strong>fat embolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"15y7eU0p","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"9_deficiency_of_fat_soluble_vitamins","title":"9.Deficiency Of Fat Soluble Vitamins","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0460","text":"Which of the following is ‍an 'Anti-infective' vitam​in?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an 'Anti-infective' vitamin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‍min E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‍min A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‍min D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‍min C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin A</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin A</strong> is required for the maintenance of epithelial functions, creating an<strong> effective barrier</strong> against pathogens; hence, it is called as an anti-infective vitamin. So, vitamin A deficiency can lead to changes in the epithelia of all mucosal surfaces, leading to increased chances of infections.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin E (Tocopherol)</strong> helps is an <strong>anti-oxidant</strong>. Deficiency manifests as skin changes and hair loss. </p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin D</strong> maintains calcium and phosphorus homeostasis. Deficiency can lead to <strong>rickets </strong>in children and osteomalacia in adults.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin C</strong> helps in the formation of connective tissues and collagen in the skin, bone, and blood vessels. Deficiency leads to their fragility and causes <strong>scurvy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1020","text":"What would be the ideal do‍se of vitamin - A that yo​u should prescribe to a 9​-month-old infant along w‌ith the measles vaccine?","raw_text":"What would be the ideal dose of vitamin - A that you should prescribe to a 9-month-old infant along with the measles vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10,0‍00 IU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100,0‍00 IU","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50,0‍00 IU","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"200,0‍00 IU","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 100,000 IU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 100,000 IU</strong></p><p>For a child aged <strong>9 months</strong>, the dose of vitamin A given is <strong>100,000 IU.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0461","text":"A 2-year-old unvaccinated ‍girl is brought to the OP​D with high-grade fever a​nd maculopapular rashes f‌or 2 days. On examination‌, she has blue-white spot‍s on her buccal mucosa as‌ shown in the image attac‍hed below. Which of the f‍ollowing vitamin deficien​cy is associated with thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old unvaccinated girl is brought to the OPD with high-grade fever and maculopapular rashes for 2 days. On examination, she has blue-white spots on her buccal mucosa as shown in the image attached below. Which of the following vitamin deficiency is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‍min A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‍min E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‍min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‍min K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fb84bc7d0dbd4820bfc95fa451bbd3e0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin A</strong></p><p>The girl is suffering from <strong>measles</strong>. It is associated with <strong>vitamin A deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin A deficiency is associated with an increased risk of infection and increased mortality with a variety of infectious diseases particularly measles. Hence, <strong>vitamin A therapy</strong> is indicated in all patients with <strong>measles</strong>.</p>\n<p>The main functions of vitamin A are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintenance of <strong>vision</strong>, especially night vision</li>\n<li>Maintenance of epithelial tissues</li>\n<li>Differentiation of various tissues, particularly during reproduction and gestation, by regulating gene expression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Features of vitamin A deficiency:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eyes: Defective dark adaptation is a characteristic early clinical feature, resulting in <strong>night blindness</strong> (earliest manifestation). This can further progress to xerophthalmia, <strong>Bitot's spots,</strong> conjunctivitis, or <strong>keratomalacia,</strong> leading to blindness.</li>\n<li>Poor overall growth, anaemia</li>\n<li><strong>Diarrhoea</strong>, susceptibility to infections</li>\n<li>Increased intracranial pressure</li>\n<li>Keratinization of mucous membranes and skin (hyperkeratosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0989","text":"A young girl of class 4 co‍mplains of a burning sens​ation in the eye. Clinica​l examination of eyes rev‌eals the finding shown be‌low. What could be the me‍chanism for the developme‌nt of such lesions?","raw_text":"A young girl of class 4 complains of a burning sensation in the eye. Clinical examination of eyes reveals the finding shown below. What could be the mechanism for the development of such lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deposition of calcium in t‍he conjunctiva","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scarring of the conjunctiv‍a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive growth of conjun‍ctival tissue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keratinization of the conj‍uctival epithelium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Keratinization of the conjuctival epithelium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b386006b7732454793825c8c0876786c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Keratinization of the conjuctival epithelium</strong></p><p>The given clinical image is showing <strong>Bitot spots, </strong>which occur due to<strong> keratinization </strong>of the conjunctival epithelium. Bitot spots are triangular, frequently bilateral, and present on the bulbar conjunctiva.</p>\n<p>Normally, bulbar conjunctiva is a stratified, columnar, non-keratinized epithelium. In vitamin A deficiency, there is <strong>squamous</strong> <span class=\"m-cta__figcaption f-caption figcaption m-figure-item__figcaption\"><span class=\"f-caption\"><strong>metaplasia </strong>of the conjunctival epithelium and <strong>tangles of keratin</strong> admixed with gas-forming bacteria producing a </span></span>dry, scaly patch with a foamy appearance called Bitot spots.</p>\n<p>Option A: Deposition of calcium in the conjunctiva is termed conjunctival concretions, which usually occurs over the palpebral conjunctiva, in chronic inflammatory conditions of the eye.</p>\n<p>Option B: Scarring of the conjunctiva is seen in <strong>chlamydial infection</strong> of the eye.</p>\n<p>Option C: Excessive growth of conjunctival tissue is seen in the <strong>pterygium</strong> of the eye.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0995","text":"A 6-year-old boy with comp‍laints of dry, itchy eyes​ and night blindness had ​the following ophthalmic ‌finding as given in the i‌mage. What is the stage o‍f vitamin A deficiency as‌ per WHO classification?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy with complaints of dry, itchy eyes and night blindness had the following ophthalmic finding as given in the image. What is the stage of vitamin A deficiency as per WHO classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag‍e X1B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag‍e X3A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stag‍e X2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stag‍e XF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage X3A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3782ad7701d142f59a0d0854053930d4.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage X3A</strong></p><p>The image depicts a <strong>corneal ulcer</strong> occupying <strong>&lt;1/3rd of the cornea</strong> under a slit lamp with a fluorescein stain. This corresponds to <strong>stage X3A </strong>of vitamin A deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0923","text":"A 6-year-old boy is brough‍t to the OPD with complai​nts of headache, desquama​tion of the skin, and bon‌e pain. Pathological exam‌ination revealed lysosoma‍l rupture within the cell‌s, prompting a suspicion ‍of hypervitaminosis A. Wh‍ich of the following stat​ement is false about the ​principle vitamin that is‍ excess in the given cond​ition? All-trans retinoic​ acid is the most bioacti‌ve form of vitamin A. Pro‌tein Energy Malnutrition ‍(PEM) can cause vitamin A​ deficiency. Excess vitam​in A causes hyperostosis.‍ Raised Intracranial Pres​sure (ICP) can be seen in‌ hypervitaminosis but not​ deficiency of vitamin A.‍ 11-trans retinal is required for vision.","raw_text":"<p>A 6-year-old boy is brought to the OPD with complaints of headache, desquamation of the skin, and bone pain. Pathological examination revealed lysosomal rupture within the cells, prompting a suspicion of hypervitaminosis A. Which of the following statement is false about the principle vitamin that is excess in the given condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>All-trans retinoic acid is the most bioactive form of vitamin A.</li>\n<li>Protein Energy Malnutrition (PEM) can cause vitamin A deficiency.</li>\n<li>Excess vitamin A causes hyperostosis.</li>\n<li>Raised Intracranial Pressure (ICP) can be seen in hypervitaminosis but not deficiency of vitamin A.</li>\n<li>11-trans retinal is required for vision.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,‍ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‍4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 and ‍5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 and ‍5 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 and 5 only</strong></p><p>Both hypervitaminosis A and deficiency of vitamin A cause raised intracranial pressure (ICP) (not just hypervitaminosis A). <strong>11-cis retinal(not trans)</strong> is required for vision.</p>\n<p>Hypervitaminosis A causes headache, vomiting, desquamation of the skin, and hyperostosis of long bones. <strong>R</strong><strong>upture of lysosomes</strong> in the cells is the pathological feature of hypervitaminosis A.</p>\n<p>All-trans retinoic acid is the most bioactive form of vitamin A, and it is required for cell differentiation and the regulation of transcription. On the other hand, 11-cis retinal is required for vision. Protein-energy malnutrition can cause vitamin A deficiency because of the <strong>impaired synthesis of</strong> retinol-binding protein (<strong>RBP</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1375","text":"Your best friend is recent‍ly worried that she has a​ lot of hair fall. Which ​of the following vitamin ‌capsules if added to her ‌diet can help prevent fre‍e radical injury and thus‌ be beneficial for her??","raw_text":"Your best friend is recently worried that she has a lot of hair fall. Which of the following vitamin capsules if added to her diet can help prevent free radical injury and thus be beneficial for her??","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‍K","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. E</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin E/ Tocopherol </strong>is a potent <strong>antioxidant</strong> and is helpful in preventing hair fall. It promotes hair growth and prevents hair roots from getting damaged by <strong>free radical injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>Alpha-tocopherol is the active component of vitamin E and it is mainly located <strong>within cell membranes</strong>, preventing lipid peroxidation and the formation of free radicals.</p>\n<p>Deficiency of this can also lead to neurological defects, <strong>hemolysis</strong>, and posterior column defect.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes vitamin E supplementation (25–50 units/day for a week).</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p>The other vitamins having antioxidant properties are vitamins A, C, and E (vitamin E is the most important).</p>\n<p>The other vitamin beneficial in the treatment of hair fall is <strong>biotin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0452","text":"An anxious mother brings h‍er 6-month-old child with​ concerns about not gaini​ng weight and height. On ‌examination, stunted grow‌th, craniotabes, frontal ‍bossing, and craniosynost‌osis are noted. What is t‍he most likely diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"An anxious mother brings her 6-month-old child with concerns about not gaining weight and height.  On examination, stunted growth, craniotabes, frontal bossing, and craniosynostosis are noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‍rvy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhizomelic chondrodysplasi‍a punctata","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Achondr‍oplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/152a1b898cf7423e8db2c64ed805b112x400x345.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/590560b692034428a7dd6175a72d13ddx720x629.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69c19899e6a64af8ba2d4d1965c58b7ax571x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p><strong>Craniotabes (soft skull)</strong>, frontal bossing, stunted growth, and craniosynostosis are clinical features of <strong>rickets</strong>.</p>\n<p>General features include failure to thrive, listlessness, protruding abdomen, proximal muscle weakness, and fractures.</p>\n<p><strong>Head to toe-examination would include the following features:</strong></p>\n<p>Head examination would include craniotabes, frontal bossing, delayed fontanel closure, delayed dentition, and craniosynostosis.</p>\n<p>Chest examination would reveal rachitic rosary, Harrison's sulcus, and repeated respiratory infections and atelectasis.</p>\n<p>Spinal deformities could include kyphosis, scoliosis, lordosis.</p>\n<p>Limb examination and extremities may show windswept deformity, anterior bowing of tibia, coxa vara, and enlargement of wrists and ankles. Bowings of legs (genu varum) or knock knees (genu valgum) may be noted. These changes can be seen only in children of the weight-bearing age group/ toddlers. </p>\n<p>The image shows <strong>frontal bossing:</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>rachitic rosary:</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>craniotabes:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5720","text":"The X-ray image of a child‍ with muscle weakness and​ failure to thrive is att​ached below. What is the ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The X-ray image of a child with muscle weakness and failure to thrive is attached below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‍rvy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteom‍alacia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteop‍etrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7bc90c9a08ef4065af6b6e6e54f776e5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette with the above X-ray image shows fraying and cupping of the metaphysis, suggestive of <strong>rickets.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Radiological features in rickets:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fraying</strong> (Widening and irregularity of the growth plate)</li>\n<li><strong>Cupping</strong> (Concavity of the metaphysis)</li>\n<li><strong>Splaying at metaphysis</strong> (widening of metaphyseal ends)</li>\n<li>Generalized rarefaction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0451","text":"An 8-year-old child from a‍ rural slum area is broug​ht to your OPD with defor​mity as shown in the imag‌e. Which of the following‌ conditions is characteri‍stically associated with ‌this deformity?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child from a rural slum area is brought to your OPD with deformity as shown in the image. Which of the following conditions is characteristically associated with this deformity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‍rvy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Achondr‍oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid‍ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7698acfb2b874520a2500a30344db87b.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p><strong>Windswept deformity</strong> (combination of valgus deformity of one leg with varus deformity of the other leg) is seen in<strong> rickets</strong>. It is also seen in <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong> but characteristically seen in rickets.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>deformities of extremities</strong> seen in rickets:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enlargement of wrists and ankles</li>\n<li>Valgus or varus deformities of limbs</li>\n<li>Anterior bowing of the tibia and femur</li>\n<li>Coxa vara</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0458","text":"Your school friend brings ‍his 5-year-old son to you​r OPD with small swelling​s on the chest that he ha‌d felt while giving the c‌hild a bath. On examinati‍on, you note swellings at‌ the costochondral juncti‍ons. Which of the followi‍ng conditions would be yo​ur differentials to this ​clinical presentation? Sc‍urvy Rickets Tietze syndr​ome","raw_text":"<p>Your school friend brings his 5-year-old son to your OPD with small swellings on the chest that he had felt while giving the child a bath. On examination, you note swellings at the costochondral junctions. Which of the following conditions would be your differentials to this clinical presentation?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Scurvy</li>\n<li>Rickets</li>\n<li>Tietze syndrome</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‍2 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 and ‍3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 o‍nly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‍2, 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7e1e643eb62488e8ce2e1aa00d5a515x571x428.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81e5ab9881c347509e8f898fec7ce731x677x748.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3</strong></p><p>Swelling of the costochondral junctions is seen in all the 3 conditions mentioned above.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 182px; width: 340px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\"><strong>Rachitic rosary</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\"><strong>Scorbutic rosary</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Beaded (rounded) in appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Angulated in appearance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Non-tender</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Tenderness on palpation (subperiosteal haemorrhage)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Due to metaphyseal widening</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Due to epiphyseal separation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Tietze syndrome</strong> is the benign inflammation of the costochondral junction.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Given below is a photo of the chest cage with pectus excavatum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: The above image shows a rachitic rosary type of appearance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0453","text":"A 22-month-old girl presen‍ts with failure to thrive​ and delayed achievement ​of motor milestones. On e‌xamination, she seems irr‌itable and is noted to ha‍ve bowed legs, thick wris‌ts, and dental caries. Wh‍ich of the following is n‍ot a radiographic sign of​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 22-month-old girl presents with failure to thrive and delayed achievement of motor milestones. On examination, she seems irritable and is noted to have bowed legs, thick wrists, and dental caries. Which of the following is not a radiographic sign of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in the width of t‍he growth plate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased b‍one density","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spla‍ying","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subperiostea‍l hemorrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subperiosteal hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e5f807e2e98463888a2ac4af6c6b2f4x600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subperiosteal hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The child is suffering from rickets. <strong>Subperiosteal hemorrhage</strong> is not a radiographic sign of rickets. On the contrary, it is seen in <strong>scurvy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Radiographic features of rickets:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Widening of the growth plate: </strong>Decreased calcification leads to reduced bone density<strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li><strong>F</strong><strong>raying: </strong>The edge of the metaphysis loses its sharp border.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong><strong>upping: </strong>The edge of the metaphysis changes from a convex or flat surface to a more concave surface.</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong><strong>playing: </strong>Weight-bearing and stress on the uncalcified bone gives rise to the widening of the metaphyseal end.</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong><strong>nlargement of the wrists and ankles </strong>due to the widening of the distal end of the metaphysis.</li>\n<li>Coarse trabeculation of the diaphysis</li>\n<li>Generalized rarefaction</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5722","text":"A 2-year-old child from an‍ urban slum is diagnosed ​with nutritional rickets.​ Which of the following b‌iochemical markers can be‌ seen in this child?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child from an urban slum is diagnosed with nutritional rickets. Which of the following biochemical markers can be seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased alkaline phospha‍tase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpho‍sphatemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduced parathyroid hormon‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alka‍losis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased alkaline phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased alkaline phosphatase</strong></p><p>In nutritional rickets serum, <strong>alkaline phosphatase</strong> levels are <strong>increased</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Biochemical findings in nutritional rickets</strong> (nutritional vitamin D deficiency):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Normal or <strong>low calcium</strong> (since due to low calcium and vitamin D deficiency, PTH increases which will try to normalize the low calcium)</li>\n<li>Parathyroid hormone levels are elevated (in order to increase the serum calcium)</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatemia (because of <strong>increased parathyroid hormone [PTH]</strong>)</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis (secondary to PTH induced renal bicarbonate wasting)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1158","text":"A child in your unit is ad‍mitted with alopecia, oli​godontia, and features of​ rickets with elevated se‌rum levels of alkaline ph‌osphatase activity and 1,‍25-dihydroxy vitamin D. W‌hat could be the patholog‍y?","raw_text":"A child in your unit is admitted with alopecia, oligodontia, and features of rickets with elevated serum levels of alkaline phosphatase activity and 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D. What could be the pathology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nutritional deficiency of ‍vitamin D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defect in 1 apha hydroxyla‍se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autoantibodies against vit‍amin D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"End-organ resistance to vi‍tamin D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. End-organ resistance to vitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. End-organ resistance to vitamin D</strong></p><p><strong>Alopecia</strong> and <strong>oligodontia</strong> (reduced number of teeth) with features of rickets in a child is classically seen in vitamin D-dependant rickets type 2 where the pathology is end-organ resistance (receptor hyposensitivity) to vitamin D.</p>\n<p><strong>Rickets causes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nutritional vitamin D deficiency</li>\n<li>Secondary vitamin D deficiency\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin D-dependant rickets, type 1 (defect in 1-alpha hydroxylase)</li>\n<li>Vitamin D-dependant rickets, type 2 (end organ resistance)</li>\n<li>Chronic kidney disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatemic rickets\n<ul>\n<li>X-linked dominant hypophosphatemic rickets (<strong>PHEX gene mutation</strong>)</li>\n<li>Autosomal dominant (fibroblast growth factor [FGF] resistance to endopeptidases)</li>\n<li>Oncogenous rickets (increased FGF 23)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin D antibodies are found in most patients of systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0454","text":"Stoss therapy is used in t‍he treatment of which of ​the following micronutrie​nt deficiencies?","raw_text":"Stoss therapy is used in the treatment of which of the following micronutrient deficiencies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‍min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‍min E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‍min D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‍min K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin D</strong></p><p><strong>Stoss therapy</strong> is used in the treatment of vitamin D deficiency.</p>\n<p>There are 2 strategies for the administration of vitamin D:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stoss therapy: Here, <strong>300,000-600,000 IU</strong> of vitamin D are administered orally or intramuscularly as 2 or 4 doses <strong>over 1 day</strong>.</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"p1\">The alternative strategy is daily vitamin D with a minimum dose of <strong>2,000 IU/day for a minimum of 3 months.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both strategies should be followed by a <strong>daily vitamin D intake </strong>of 400 IU/day if the child is &lt; 1-year-old or 600 IU/day if the child is &gt;1-year-old. Children must also receive adequate dietary calcium and phosphorus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1196","text":"A child with rickets not r‍esponding to treatment is​ being evaluated. Investi​gations show serum calciu‌m level of 9 mg/dL, low p‌hosphate level of 2 mg/dL‍, elevated alkaline phosp‌hatase, and normal parath‍yroid hormone levels with‍ normal arterial blood ga​s levels. What is the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A child with rickets not responding to treatment is being evaluated. Investigations show serum calcium level of 9 mg/dL, low phosphate level of 2 mg/dL,  elevated alkaline phosphatase, and normal parathyroid hormone levels with normal arterial blood gas levels. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal tubul‍ar acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypophosphat‍emic rickets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic kid‍ney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin D-dependant ricket‍s, type 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypophosphatemic rickets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7c805ae6ab94a9db6b9d957014e9d99x1280x1837.8561354019746.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypophosphatemic rickets</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of a child with a history of treatment-resistant rickets, normal levels of calcium (9 to 11 mg/dL), parathyroid hormone, arterial blood gas (ABG), with<strong> decreased phosphate</strong> (normal, 2.5 to 4.3 mg/dL), and elevated levels of alkaline phosphatase (normal, 33 to 96 U/L) is suggestive of <strong>hypophosphatemic rickets</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypophosphatemic rickets:</strong></p>\n<p>X-linked dominant hypophosphatemic rickets is the <strong>most common genetic cause</strong> of rickets while the most common, overall is nutritional vitamin D deficiency. <strong>PHEX gene</strong> (<strong>ph</strong>osphate-regulating <strong>e</strong>ndopeptidase on <strong>x</strong> chromosome) products are responsible for the <strong>inactivation of phosphatonin</strong>. Mutation of this PHEX leads to increased phosphatonin (FGF 23) levels, and hence, the <strong>excretion of phosphorous</strong> is <strong>increased</strong>. This is also associated with <strong>impaired</strong> renal tubular <strong>reabsorption</strong> of phosphate leading to its excess excretion.</p>\n<p>The chart attached below summarizes the evaluation of hypophosphatemic rickets.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment-resistant rickets :</strong></p>\n<p>Patients respond well to a combination of <strong>oral phosphorus</strong> and <strong>1,25-D (calcitriol)</strong>. Frequent dosing helps prevent prolonged decrements in serum phosphorus levels because there is a rapid decline after each dose. In addition, frequent dosing decreases diarrhea, which is a complication of high-dose oral phosphorus. <strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0459","text":"A concerned mother of an i‍nfant, known case of cyst​ic fibrosis, presents to ​your OPD with complaints ‌of abdominal pain, loose ‌stools not settling with ‍antibiotics, and other me‌dications. She recently s‍topped breastfeeding and ‍switched to formula feeds​. On examination, you fin​d petechial rashes. Which‍ of the following below m​entioned causes would not​ prompt you to suspect a ‌deficiency of Vitamin K i‌n this child?","raw_text":"A concerned mother of an infant, known case of cystic fibrosis, presents to your OPD with complaints of abdominal pain, loose stools not settling with antibiotics, and other medications. She recently stopped breastfeeding and switched to formula feeds. On examination, you find petechial rashes. Which of the following below mentioned causes would not prompt you to suspect a deficiency of Vitamin K in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biliary o‍bstruction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Artificial‍ milk feeds","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral antibi‍otic therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic ‍fibrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Artificial milk feeds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Artificial milk feeds</strong></p><p>Vitamin K deficiency occurs mostly in breastfed infants not in <strong>artificial milk-fed babies,</strong> as the formula milk is fortified.</p>\n<p>The use of broad-spectrum antibiotics that eliminate the intestinal <strong>vitamin K2- producing bacteria</strong> can cause vitamin K deficiency. <strong>Biliary atresia</strong> causes malabsorption of vitamin K, due to defective fat absorption.</p>\n<p>There are 3 forms of vitamin K deficiency bleeding (<strong>VKDB</strong>) of the newborn:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 788px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\"><strong>Secondary to maternal intake of medications</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\"><strong>Early VKDB</strong> (classic hemorrhagic disease of the newborn)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\"> <strong>Late VKDB</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>Onset</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">At birth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\">1 to 14 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">2 to 12 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>Cause</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Drugs that cross the placenta and interferes with Vit K function eg. phenobarbital, phenytoin, warfarin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263px;\">Low stores of Vitamin K at birth, poor transfer of Vitamin K across the placenta, Inadequate intake during the first few days of life. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">In breastfed babies and in those with malabsorption of vitamin K (cystic fibrosis, biliary atresia)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Early VKBD of the newborn is prevented by the administration of a single intramuscular (IM) injection of <strong>vitamin K (1 mg)</strong> at birth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1382","text":"What is the recommended di‍etary allowance (RDA) of ​vitamin D in a 4-year-old​ child?","raw_text":"What is the recommended dietary allowance (RDA) of vitamin D in a 4-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"400‍ IU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"600‍ IU","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"200‍ IU","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100‍0 IU","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 600 IU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 600 IU</strong></p><p>In a child of age 4 years, the recommended dietary allowance (RDA) of vitamin D is 600 IU.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin A: 1 IU = 1 IU retinol = 0.3 mcg of Retinol Equivalents (RE)</li>\n<li>Vitamin C: 1 IU = 50 mcg of ascorbic acid</li>\n<li>Vitamin D: 1 IU = 0.025 mcg cholecalciferol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5718","text":"Rickets in an infant prese‍nts as all except _______​___","raw_text":"Rickets in an infant presents as all except __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crani‍otabes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Widened f‍ontanelle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rachiti‍c rosary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bow ‍legs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bow legs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/331c475f92034e31a3f9dc7df6ac001ax720x629.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb6e8c81d8a9472b8be77a185dee6608x682x475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bow legs</strong></p><p>Bowing of legs is not a feature of rickets in infants, because it <strong>appears only after weight-bearing</strong>. Thus, bow legs are a feature of rickets in toddlers.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Rachitic rosary (widening of the costochondral junctions)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Wrist X-ray image</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1160","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding phosphato​nins?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding phosphatonins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a phosphate-regulati‍ng factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It increases the renal rea‍bsorption of phosphate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is produced by osteocyt‍es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FGF 23 is an important pho‍sphatonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It increases the renal reabsorption of phosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It increases the renal reabsorption of phosphate</strong></p><p>Phosphatonin is a group of phosphate-regulating factors produced by osteocytes, and FGF 23 is the most important phosphatonin. Phosphatonin increases renal excretion (not reabsorption) of phosphate.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism of phosphatonin action:</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin D receptor activation by 1,25 dihydroxy D (active vit D)</p>\n<p>                             ↓</p>\n<p>FGF 23 release by osteocytes</p>\n<p>                             ↓</p>\n<p>Reduces expression of sodium-phosphate transporter in proximal convoluted tubules of the kidney</p>\n<p>                             ↓</p>\n<p>Increased urinary phosphate excretion</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Meanwhile, phosphatonin decreases the activity of 1 alpha hydroxylase and, therefore, the production of 1,25-D as well. So, the absorption of phosphate from the intestine is also reduced.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"wmq21oBU","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"9_lysosomal_and_glycogen_storage_diseases","title":"9.Lysosomal And Glycogen Storage Diseases","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA3580","text":"Which of these is not a fo‌rm of sphingolipidoses?","raw_text":"Which of these is not a form of sphingolipidoses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tay–Sach‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fabry ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gaucher‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hurler disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hurler disease</strong></p><p><strong>Hurler disease</strong> is a <strong>mucopolysaccharidosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sphingolipidoses</strong> are a group of lysosomal storage disorders characterized by the accumulation of sphingolipids. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Niemann–Pick disease</li>\n<li><strong>Fabry disease</strong></li>\n<li>Krabbe disease</li>\n<li><strong>Gaucher disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tay–Sachs disease</strong></li>\n<li>Metachromatic leukodystrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3604","text":"What is the most common ly‌sosomal storage disorder?‍","raw_text":"What is the most common lysosomal storage disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tay–Sach‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Niemann–Pi‌ck disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gaucher‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sandhoff'‌s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gaucher disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gaucher disease</strong></p><p><strong>Gaucher disease</strong> is the <strong>most common lysosomal storage disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder resulting from mutations in the gene encoding<strong> glucocerebrosidase. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3573","text":"An 8-month-old child prese‌nts with mental retardati‍on, blindness, and muscle‍ weakness. On evaluation,‌ deficiency of hexosamini​dase α-subunit is found. ​What is the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-month-old child presents with mental retardation, blindness, and muscle weakness. On evaluation, deficiency of hexosaminidase α-subunit is found. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tay–Sach‌s disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sandhoff‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Krabbe ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gaucher‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tay–Sachs disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tay–Sachs disease</strong></p><p>The given scenario and <strong>hexosaminidase α-subunit</strong> deficiency are seen in <strong>Tay–Sachs disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Sphingolipidoses</strong> with their deficient enzyme:</span></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>G<sub>M1 </sub>Gangliosidoses - </strong>G<sub>M1 </sub>Ganglioside β- galactosidase deficiency\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1- infantile, generalizsed</li>\n<li>Type 2- juvenile</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>G<sub>M2 </sub>Gangliosidosis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Tay<strong>–</strong>Sachs disease - Hexosaminidase α-subunit</li>\n<li>Sandhoff disease - Hexosaminidase β-subunit</li>\n<li>G<sub>M2 </sub>Gangliosidosis variant AB - Ganglioside activator protein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Tay–Sachs disease is<strong> </strong>the most common form of GM2 gangliosidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3601","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about Ta‍y–Sachs Disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Tay–Sachs Disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Affected infants are norma‌l at birth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurons in the CNS, ANS, a‌nd retina are affected","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accumulations stain positi‌ve for oil red O and Suda‍n black B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whorled configurations wit‌hin mitochondria are seen‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Whorled configurations within mitochondria are seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a54db95314ca48919b2da334f2a0cce2x720x538.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Whorled configurations within mitochondria are seen</strong></p><p>In<strong> Tay–Sachs </strong>disease, an electron microscope shows <strong>whorled</strong> configurations<strong> within lysosomes </strong>composed of onion-skin layers of membranes.</p>\n<p>Affected <strong>infants </strong>appear<strong> normal at birth</strong> but begin to manifest signs and symptoms at about 6 months of age. The involvement of neurons in the central and autonomic nervous systems and retina dominates the clinical picture.</p>\n<p>There is a severe motor and mental deterioration, beginning with motor incoordination and mental obtundation. This leads to muscular flaccidity, blindness, and increasing dementia. Over the span of 1 or 2 years, a completely vegetative state is reached, followed by death at age 2 to 3 years</p>\n<p>On histologic examination, the neurons are ballooned with cytoplasmic vacuoles, each representing a markedly distended lysosome filled with gangliosides.</p>\n<p>A<strong> cherry-red spot</strong> appears in the macula, representing the accentuation of the normal color of the macular choroid. This is in contrast with the pallor produced by the swollen ganglion cells in the remainder of the retina<strong>. </strong>This finding is characteristic of Tay-Sachs disease and other storage disorders affecting the neurons.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a cherry-red spot on retina.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3576","text":"Patients with metachromati‌c leukodystrophy will hav‍e which of the following ‍enzyme deficiency?","raw_text":"Patients with metachromatic leukodystrophy will have which of the following enzyme deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aryl sul‌fatase A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha gal‌actosidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galactosyl‌ceramidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β-galac‌tosidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aryl sulfatase A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aryl sulfatase A</strong></p><p><strong>Metachromatic leukodystrophy</strong> is an <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>condition caused by the deficiency of <strong>arylsulfatase A.</strong></p>\n<p>The deficiency of this enzyme leads to the accumulation of<strong> cerebroside sulfate. </strong>These sulfatides can contribute to white matter injury, demyelination, and gliosis.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Macrophages</strong> with<strong> vacuolated cytoplasm</strong> are scattered throughout the white matter. They contain <strong>metachromatic </strong>material, which can be detected in the urine and peripheral nerves when stained with <strong>toluidine blue</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3578","text":"Which of the following enz‌yme is deficient in patie‍nts with Krabbe disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme is deficient in patients with Krabbe disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galactosyl‌ceramidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"α-Galact‌osidase A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucocere‌brosidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta glu‌cosidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Galactosylceramidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Galactosylceramidase</strong></p><p><strong>Krabbe disease</strong> is due to the deficiency of <strong>galactosylceramidase</strong>.</p>\n<p>The onset of symptoms is between 3 and 6 months of age, dominated by motor signs such as stiffness and weakness. They often don't survive beyond the age of 2 years. The brain shows <strong>loss of myelin and oligodendrocytes </strong>with a similar process affecting the<strong> peripheral nerves.</strong></p>\n<p>The aggregation of engorged macrophages called globoid cells in the brain parenchyma and around blood vessels is the characteristic and diagnostic feature of Krabbe disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3600","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following diseases will ‍have ceramide trihexoside‍ accumulation?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following diseases will have ceramide trihexoside accumulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fabry ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gaucher‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tay-Sach‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Krabbe ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fabry disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fabry disease</strong></p><p><strong>Ceramide trihexoside</strong> (also known as globotriaosylceramide) is accumulated in <strong>Fabry disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The enzyme defect seen in this disease is<strong> α-galactosidase A</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painful acroparaesthesia</li>\n<li>Hypohidrosis</li>\n<li>Abdominal cramping</li>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Small petechiae around the umbilicus</li>\n<li>Lenticular opacities</li>\n<li>Corneal dystrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3602","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about Ni‍emann–Pick disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Niemann–Pick disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inherited deficiency of ar‌yl sulfatase is present","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type A has b‌ad prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type B has associated orga‌nomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromosome 1‌1 is involved","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inherited deficiency of aryl sulfatase is present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inherited deficiency of aryl sulfatase is present</strong></p><p><strong>Niemann–Pick disease</strong> types A and B are two related disorders that are characterized by lysosomal accumulation of sphingomyelin due to an <strong>inherited deficiency</strong> of<strong> sphingomyelinase.</strong></p>\n<p>The disease is typically inherited as an <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>condition on<strong> chromosome 11.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type A</strong> is a severe infantile form with extensive<strong> neurologic involvement</strong>, marked visceral accumulations of sphingomyelin, progressive wasting, and early death within the first 3 years of life.</p>\n<p><strong>Type B</strong> disease patients have <strong>organomegaly,</strong> but generally<strong> no central nervous system</strong> involvement. They usually survive into adulthood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3603","text":"Given below is an electron‌ microscopy picture of ly‍sosomes. What is the most‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Given below is an electron microscopy picture of lysosomes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tay–Sach‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Niemann–Pi‌ck disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gaucher‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Krabbe ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Niemann–Pick disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afbb95357980414e94168a2d79631153.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Niemann–Pick disease</strong></p><p>Electron microscopy showing <strong>zebra bodies</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Niemann–Pick disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Zebra bodies</strong> are engorged secondary lysosomes that contain membranous cytoplasmic bodies resembling concentric lamellated myelin figures. Similar zebra bodies can also be seen in <strong>mucopolysaccharidoses</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3572","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g I-cell disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding I-cell disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is characterised by def‌ective phosphorylating en‍zymes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a glycogen storage d‌isorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with ging‌ival hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High levels of hydrolytic ‌enzymes in plasma are see‍n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a glycogen storage disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f70d3d98d3cd42a09eb2136247f09f47x1280x1184.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a glycogen storage disorder</strong></p><p>Inclusion-cell (<strong>I-cell</strong>)<strong> disease, </strong>also referred to as mucolipidosis II, is a part of the<strong> lysosomal storage disease</strong> family.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the deficiency of <strong>phosphorylating enzymes</strong> essential for the formation of the <strong>mannose-6-phosphate</strong> recognition marker.</p>\n<p><strong>Acid hydrolases</strong> lacking the recognition marker cannot be targeted to the lysosomes but are secreted extracellularly.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Coarse facial features</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Gingival hyperplasia</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Clouded corneas</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Restricted joint movements</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Claw hand deformities</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Kyphoscoliosis</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">High plasma levels of lysosomal enzymes</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3581","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about mucopolysacch‍aridosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about mucopolysaccharidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hunter syndrome is due to ‌a deficiency of iduronate‍ 2-sulphatase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hurler syndrome is due to ‌a deficiency of α-L-iduro‍nidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are inherited as auto‌somal recessive traits, e‍xcept Hunter syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hunter syndrome is inherit‌ed as an autosomal domina‍nt disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hunter syndrome is inherited as an autosomal dominant disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hunter syndrome is inherited as an autosomal dominant disease</strong></p><p>All the <strong>mucopolysaccharidoses</strong> are inherited as <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> traits <strong>except</strong> <strong>Hunter syndrome.</strong> It is inherited as an<strong> X-linked recessive</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>The mucopolysaccharidoses (MPSs) are a group of closely related syndromes that result from genetically determined deficiencies of enzymes involved in the degradation of mucopolysaccharides (glycosaminoglycans).</p>\n<p>Mucopolysaccharides are abundant in the ground substance of connective tissues. In the absence of enzymes involved in their degradation, they accumulate within lysosomes in various tissues and organs of the body. Two among these are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hurler syndrome - deficiency of α-L-iduronidase</li>\n<li>Hunter syndrome - deficiency of iduronate 2-sulphatase</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3570","text":"Which glycogen storage dis‌order is a lysosomal stor‍age disease?","raw_text":"Which glycogen storage disorder is a lysosomal storage disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2</strong></p><p><strong>Glycogen storage disorder type II</strong>, also called <strong>Pompe disease</strong>, is the only glycogen storage disease with a <strong>defect</strong> in <strong>lysosomal metabolism. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an autosomal-recessive metabolic disorder. It is caused by the accumulation of glycogen in the lysosome due to <strong>deficiency</strong> of the <strong>lysosomal </strong><strong>acid alpha-glucosidase</strong> enzyme.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3607","text":"A 4-year-old boy presented‌ with severe pain in his ‍left leg which worsened o‍ver the last few months. ‌On examination, hepatospl​enomegaly and development​al delays were noticed. L​abs revealed the followin​g findings on light and o‌il immersion respectively‌ of the bone marrow aspir​ation. Which of the follo​wing is deficient in this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy presented with severe pain in his left leg which worsened over the last few months. On examination, hepatosplenomegaly and developmental delays were noticed. Labs revealed the following findings on light and oil immersion respectively of the bone marrow aspiration. Which of the following is deficient in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hexosami‌nidase A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sphingom‌yelinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucocere‌brosidase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Galactocer‌ebrosidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucocerebrosidase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/458c9c42096b4b2390bdfca67e56d3af.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucocerebrosidase</strong></p><p>The given scenario and bone marrow aspiration images showing necrotic bone with lack of osteocytes in the lacunae (Image A) and<strong> Gaucher cells </strong>(Image B)are suggestive of <strong>Gaucher's disease. </strong>This condition has a deficiency of <strong>glucocerebrosidase.</strong></p>\n<p>Gaucher cells are plump macrophages that characteristically have a <strong>crumpled tissue paper</strong> appearance (Image B) of the cytoplasm. It is due to the accumulation of <strong>glucocerebrosides</strong>.</p>\n<p>They can be seen in <strong>Virchow–Robin spaces </strong>(perivascular fluid-filled cavities that surround perforating arteries and veins in the brain parenchyma) in<strong> </strong>patients with cerebral involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3606","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect abou‍t Gaucher’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about Gaucher’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 spar‌es the brain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 1 has splenic and ske‌letal involvement","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 1 has no detectable e‌nzyme activity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type 2 has acute cerebral ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 1 has no detectable enzyme activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 1 has no detectable enzyme activity</strong></p><p>In <strong>type 1</strong>, enzyme activity is reduced but<strong> detectable.</strong></p>\n<p>Gaucher disease is of two types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 175px;\" width=\"389\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 63.125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 63.125px;\"><strong>Type 1</strong>: <strong>Chronic</strong> <strong>non-neuropathic form</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 63.125px;\">\n<p><strong>Type 2: </strong><strong>Acute neuronopathic form</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 18px;\">Most common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 18px;\">Less common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 54px;\">Spleen and skeletal system are mainly involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 54px;\">Hepatosplenomegaly is seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 64px;\">Spares the central nervous system</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 64px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 162\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The central nervous system is involved</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 36px;\">Enzyme activity is reduced but detectable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 36px;\">No detectable enzyme activity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 64px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 162\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Principally in Jews of European stock</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 64px;\">Has no predilection for Jews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 162\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 162\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Longevity is shortened</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.5px; height: 46px;\"> Death at an early age</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3613","text":"A 1-year-old child present‌s with growth retardation‍ and abdominal distension‍. On examination, the chi‌ld has characteristic dol​l-like facies with thin e​xtremities. Blood investi​gations reveal hypoglycem​ia, lactic acidosis, hype‌rlipidemia, and hyperuric‌emia. USG abdomen shows h​epatomegaly and renomegal​y. What deficiency causes​ the given clinical condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child presents with growth retardation and abdominal distension. On examination, the child has characteristic doll-like facies with thin extremities. Blood investigations reveal hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperlipidemia, and hyperuricemia. USG abdomen shows hepatomegaly and renomegaly. What deficiency causes the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose ph‌osphorylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eno‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Branchin‌g enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucose 6 ‌phosphatase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glucose 6 phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glucose 6 phosphatase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> von Gierke disease.</strong> It is due to the<strong> deficiency</strong> of<strong> glucose 6 phosphatase </strong>enzyme.</p>\n<p>The condition usually manifests as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure to thrive</li>\n<li>Stunted growth</li>\n<li>Hepatomegaly</li>\n<li>Renomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> is due to the failure of glucose mobilization which often leads to convulsions. <strong>Lactic acidosis</strong>, <strong>h</strong><strong>yperlipidemia, </strong>and<strong> hyperuricemia</strong> result from deranged glucose metabolism. Many patients also develop gout, skin xanthomas, and bleeding tendency (due to platelet dysfunction). </p>\n<p>With treatment<strong>,</strong> most patients survive and develop late complications (e.g., hepatic adenomas).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3614","text":"Which enzyme is deficient ‌in McArdle’s syndrome?","raw_text":"Which enzyme is deficient in McArdle’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic h‌exokinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle pho‌sphorylase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle h‌exokinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle phosphofructokinase‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muscle phosphorylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muscle phosphorylase</strong></p><p><strong> McArdle’s syndrome</strong> is caused due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>muscle phosphorylase.</strong></p>\n<p>In the skeletal muscles, as opposed to the liver, glycogen is used predominantly as a source of energy during physical activity. If enzymes hydrolyzing glucose are deficient, there will be an accumulation of glycogen in the muscles. This will cause muscle weakness due to impaired energy production. </p>\n<p>Enzyme deficiencies leading to muscle glycogen storage include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>McArdle disease/type V glycogenosis - deficiency of muscle phosphorylase</li>\n<li>Type VII glycogen storage disease - deficiency of muscle phosphofructokinase </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typically, individuals with myopathic forms present with <strong>muscle cramps after exercise.</strong> This is because lactate levels in the blood fail to rise after exercise due to a block in glycolysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qgdv9sg2","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"9_drugs_for_glaucoma","title":"9.Drugs For Glaucoma","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB3466","text":"You want to prescribe a dr‍ug that increases uveo-sc​leral outflow while treat​ing a patient with open-a‌ngle glaucoma. Which of t​he following drugs would ‌you choose?","raw_text":"You want to prescribe a drug that increases uveo-scleral outflow while treating a patient with open-angle glaucoma. Which of the following drugs would you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostagland‍in analogues","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‍ors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rho-kinase‍ inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta-b‍lockers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prostaglandin analogues","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prostaglandin analogues</strong></p><p>The class of anti-glaucoma drugs which act by <strong>increasing</strong> the<strong> uveo-scleral outflow</strong> are <strong>prostaglandin analogues.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4642","text":"Which of the following ant‍i-glaucoma agents has no ​effect on aqueous outflow​?","raw_text":"Which of the following anti-glaucoma agents has no effect on aqueous outflow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Netar‍sudil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tim‍olol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brimo‍nidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Latan‍oprost","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Timolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Timolol</strong></p><p><strong>Timolol</strong> is a <strong>non-selective beta-blocker. </strong>Beta-blockers reduce intra-ocular pressure primarily by<strong> reducing aqueous humor</strong> production. Another hypothesis states that by reducing ocular blood flow, there may be a decrease in ultrafiltration responsible for aqueous production. </p>\n<p><strong>Netarsudil</strong> is a rho-kinase inhibitor that acts by increasing trabecular outflow.</p>\n<p><strong>Brimonidine</strong> is an alpha-agonist that has a small effect on increasing both uveoscleral and trabecular outflow. </p>\n<p><strong>Latanoprost</strong> is a prostaglandin PGF2-alpha analog that primarily acts by releasing matrix metalloproteinases, thereby increasing trabecular and uveoscleral outflow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3473","text":"Dipevefrine is a prodrug o‍f ______.","raw_text":"Dipevefrine is a prodrug of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Noradr‍enaline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adren‍aline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clon‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dopa‍mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adrenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adrenaline</strong></p><p><strong>Dipivefrine</strong> is a prodrug of <strong>adrenaline</strong> (epinephrine).</p>\n<p>Adrenaline has poor corneal penetration. Dipivefrine penetrates the cornea and is converted into adrenaline by the esterases.</p>\n<p>Adrenaline lowers intraocular pressure by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing trabecular outflow - <strong>beta-2</strong> receptor-mediated.</li>\n<li>Increasing both trabecular and uveoscleral outflow, and decreasing aqueous production - <strong>alpha</strong> receptor-mediated. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3481","text":"What is the primary mechan‍ism of action of topical ​carbonic anhydrase inhibi​tors in glaucoma?","raw_text":"What is the primary mechanism of action of topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increasing trabecular outf‍low","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increasing uveoscleral out‍flow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reducing aqueous formation‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Relaxation of trabecular m‍eshwork","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reducing aqueous formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reducing aqueous formation</strong></p><p>The primary mechanism of action of topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in glaucoma is <strong>reducing</strong> the <strong>aqueous</strong> formation. Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act by inhibiting type II isoenzyme of carbonic anhydrase in ciliary body epithelium. This leads to a reduction in fluid transport and decreases aqueous formation and IOP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4640","text":"A 40-year old man who is s‍uffering from open-angle ​glaucoma reads about neta​rsudil being approved as ‌a novel agent for his con​dition. What is the mecha‌nism of action of this dr‌ug?","raw_text":"A 40-year old man who is suffering from open-angle glaucoma reads about netarsudil being approved as a novel agent for his condition. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphodiesterase 5 inhibi‍tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rho kinase‍ inhibition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GPCR in‍hibition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rho kinase inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rho kinase inhibition</strong></p><p><strong>Netarsudil</strong> is a <strong>rho kinase inhibitor</strong> that acts by relaxing the trabecular meshwork. This increases the outflow of aqueous humor and reduces intraocular pressure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3467","text":"A patient who was started ‍on treatment for glaucoma​ comes to the casualty wi​th acute onset breathless‌ness. On auscultation, bi​lateral wheeze is heard. ‌Which of the following dr‌ugs might have led to thi​s adverse effect?","raw_text":"A patient who was started on treatment for glaucoma comes to the casualty with acute onset breathlessness. On auscultation, bilateral wheeze is heard. Which of the following drugs might have led to this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‍olol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta‍xolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latan‍aprost","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetaz‍olamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Timolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Timolol</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute bronchial asthma</strong> which was most likely precipitated by <strong>timolol.</strong></p>\n<p>Timolol is a <strong>non-selective beta-blocker</strong> that reduces intraocular pressure by decreasing aqueous formation.<strong> </strong>It <strong>precipitates bronchial asthma</strong> in susceptible individuals by beta-2 blockade.</p>\n<p><strong>Betaxolol</strong> is a<strong> beta-1 selective blocker</strong> that is used in glaucoma and has a lower propensity to precipitate bronchial asthma.</p>\n<p><strong>Latanoprost</strong> is a prostaglandin (<strong>PGF-2 alpha</strong>) <strong>analog</strong> and does not precipitate bronchial asthma. </p>\n<p><strong>Acetazolamide</strong> is a <strong>carbonic anhydrase inhibitor</strong> and does not precipitate bronchial asthma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7024","text":"A patient with diabetic ma‍cular edema developed gla​ucoma. Which of the follo​wing drugs would you cons‌ider giving last?","raw_text":"A patient with diabetic macular edema developed glaucoma. Which of the following drugs would you consider giving last?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‍locker","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha ‍agonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piloc‍arpine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostagland‍in analogues","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prostaglandin analogues","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prostaglandin analogues</strong></p><p>Prostaglandin analogs are the last drugs to be considered in this patient since macular edema is one of their side effects.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects of PGF 2alpha analogs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blurring of vision</li>\n<li><strong>Macular edema</strong> (especially in aphakic patients)</li>\n<li>Eyelash hypertrichosis</li>\n<li>Iris pigmentation (<strong>Heterochromia iridis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Peri-orbital fat depletion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3485","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most appropriate firs​t-line medical therapy in​ a patient with open-angl‌e glaucoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line medical therapy in a patient with open-angle glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‍lockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mio‍tics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PGF 2 alp‍ha analogs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‍ors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PGF 2 alpha analogs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PGF 2 alpha analogs</strong></p><p>The most appropriate first-line medical therapy for open-angle glaucoma is <strong>PGF-2 alpha analogs. </strong></p>\n<p>They act by <strong>increasing</strong> the <strong>aqueous outflow.</strong> They have largely replaced beta-blockers and can be considered as first-line agents for open-angle glaucoma because of their following properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Once-daily dosing</li>\n<li>Low incidence of systemic side effects</li>\n<li>Potent IOP-lowering effect</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the use of PGF-2 alpha analogs or beta-blockers is contraindicated, topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors or alpha-2 agonists are used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3479","text":"You diagnose a patient wit‍h primary open-angle glau​coma and decide to prescr​ibe a PGF2 alpha analog. ‌Which of the following dr​ugs would you not choose?‌","raw_text":"You diagnose a patient with primary open-angle glaucoma and decide to prescribe a PGF2 alpha analog. Which of the following drugs would you not choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bimat‍oprost","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Travo‍prost","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dino‍prost","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Taflu‍prost","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dinoprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dinoprost</strong></p><p><strong>Dinoprost</strong> is a derivative of natural <strong>PGF-2 alpha. </strong>It is indicated in the <strong>termination of pregnancy</strong> and <strong>post-partum hemorrhage</strong> and not for use in glaucoma.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>PGF2 alpha analogs that are used topically for the treatment of glaucoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Latanoprost</li>\n<li>Bimatoprost</li>\n<li>Travoprost</li>\n<li>Tafluprost</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4915","text":"Which of the following ant‍iglaucoma medications is ​unsafe in infants?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiglaucoma medications is unsafe in infants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‍olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brimo‍nidine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Latan‍oprost","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorzo‍lamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brimonidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brimonidine</strong></p><p>The use of <strong>brimonidine </strong>is<strong> unsafe </strong>in infants<strong>. </strong>It is an<strong> alpha-2 agonist</strong> that can cross the blood-brain barrier. This may result in <strong>CNS depression</strong> and<strong> apnoea</strong> in neonates. Therefore it is contraindicated in children less than 2 years.</p>\n<p>α<sub>2</sub> agonists, through the activation of G-protein coupled receptor activation, inhibit the activity of adenylate cyclase. This reduces cAMP and hence reduces aqueous humor production by ciliary action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3480","text":"Which of the following can‍ be administered orally f​or the treatment of glauc​oma? Acetazolamide Dorzol‌amide Brinzolamide Dichlo​rphenamide Methazolamide","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following can be administered orally for the treatment of glaucoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Acetazolamide</li>\n<li>Dorzolamide</li>\n<li>Brinzolamide</li>\n<li>Dichlorphenamide</li>\n<li>Methazolamide</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‍and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‍3, 4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Acetazolamide</strong> and <strong>methazolamide</strong> are carbonic anhydrase inhibitors that can be used <strong>orally</strong> in the treatment of glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Acetazolamide can also be administered intravenously.</p>\n<p><strong>Dorzolamide</strong> and<strong> brinzolamide</strong> are<strong> topical</strong> carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.</p>\n<p><strong>Dichlorphenamide</strong> is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor approved for the treatment of <strong>familial periodic paralysis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7468","text":"Intravenous mannitol is us‍ed in ____?","raw_text":"Intravenous mannitol is used in ____?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmona‍ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congestive cardiac failure‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute kidney injury with a‍nuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glau‍coma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glaucoma</strong></p><p>Intravenous mannitol is used for the treatment of acute attacks of <strong>glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Uses of mannitol are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To reduce cerebral edema and intracranial pressure</li>\n<li>Enhancement of urinary excretion of salicylates, barbiturates, bromides, and lithium</li>\n<li>Diagnosis of bronchial hyperreactivity (inhalation)</li>\n<li>Antihemolytic urological irrigation during transurethral procedures</li>\n<li>Treatment of acute attacks of glaucoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3482","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs should not be given in​ a patient prone to devel​oping angle-closure glauc‌oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs should not be given in a patient prone to developing angle-closure glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piloc‍arpine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cloz‍apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rivast‍igmine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neost‍igmine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clozapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clozapine</strong></p><p><strong>Clozapine</strong> is an atypical antipsychotic having significant <strong>anticholinergic</strong> action. Drugs having significant anticholinergic action should be <strong>avoided</strong> or used with extreme caution in patients prone to developing <strong>acute angle-closure glaucoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The anticholinergic action results in the <strong>relaxation</strong> of the <strong>sphincter pupillae</strong> muscle. This results in the iris coming in contact with the lens and thereby obstructing the movement of the aqueous from the posterior to the anterior chamber.</p>\n<p>This results in bulging of the iris and further leads to the <strong>obliteration</strong> of the narrow <strong>iridocorneal angle</strong>. This blocks the outflow of aqueous precipitating an <strong>acute attack of glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3470","text":"While examining a patient ‍using a corneal esthesiom​eter, you find that corne​al sensations are decreas‌ed. Which of the followin​g drugs could have led to‌ this adverse effect?","raw_text":"While examining a patient using a corneal esthesiometer, you find that corneal sensations are decreased. Which of the following drugs could have led to this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‍lockers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‍ors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholino‍mimetics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostaglan‍din analogs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Beta blockers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Beta blockers</strong></p><p><strong>Corneal hypoesthesia</strong> is a side effect of <strong>ocular beta-blockers</strong>.</p>\n<p>For topical use in glaucoma, beta-blockers with no/minimal membrane-stabilizing action are used. This is the reason why propranolol is not formulated for ocular use. It has the highest membrane-stabilizing action and causes maximum corneal hypoesthesia if used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4639","text":"A patient presents with se‍vere eye pain to the opht​halmology OPD. On examina​tion, there is circumcorn‌eal congestion and the IO​P is 40 mm Hg. Which of t‌he following drugs should‌ not be administered imme​diately to this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with severe eye pain to the ophthalmology OPD. On examination, there is circumcorneal congestion and the IOP is 40 mm Hg. Which of the following drugs should not be administered immediately to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetaz‍olamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tim‍olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latan‍oprost","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Piloc‍arpine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pilocarpine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pilocarpine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute angle-closure glaucoma. </strong>Pilocarpine should not be administered immediately in this condition.</p>\n<p>During an acute attack, the elevated pressure in the anterior chamber causes <strong>pressure-induced ischemic paralysis</strong> of the <strong>iris. Pilocarpine</strong> would be <strong>ineffective</strong> in this phase. </p>\n<p>Once the IOP is reduced by other agents and reperfusion is achieved, <strong>pilocarpine</strong> can be used to <strong>overcome</strong> the <strong>pupillary block</strong>. </p>\n<p>Pilocarpine is avoided in conditions like pseudo-exfoliation syndrome, phacomorphic glaucoma, and aqueous misdirection syndrome as it may exacerbate pupillary block in such cases. </p>\n<p>Topical beta-blockers, alpha-2 agonists and prostaglandin analogs can be used to decrease the IOP initially at presentation. Oral or intravenous acetazolamide can also be used. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3472","text":"A patient who is on treatm‍ent for glaucoma presente​d with the following find​ing during his routine fo‌llow-up. Which of the fol​lowing drugs can cause th‌is adverse effect?","raw_text":"A patient who is on treatment for glaucoma presented with the following finding during his routine follow-up. Which of the following drugs can cause this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‍olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Latan‍oprost","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brimo‍nidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apracl‍onidine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apraclonidine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf4ce6ea1d734980be8dc6aca59a7202.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apraclonidine</strong></p><p>The patient has presented with<strong> lid retraction</strong> which is most likely caused by the use of <strong>apraclonidine.</strong> It is a congener of clonidine that has primary α<sub>2</sub> agonist action and a subsidiary α<sub>1</sub> agonist action.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects like mydriasis and lid retraction are common with apraclonidine.</p>\n<p>Brimonidine is also a congener of clonidine with more α<sub>2</sub> selectivity. Mydriasis and lid retraction are uncommon with this agent.</p>\n<p>Timolol and latanoprost do not cause lid retraction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3483","text":"The ophthalmologic examina‍tion of a patient who is ​on anti-glaucoma drugs sh​ows the following finding‌. Identify the drug that ​might have resulted in th‌is adverse effect.","raw_text":"The ophthalmologic examination of a patient who is on anti-glaucoma drugs shows the following finding. Identify the drug that might have resulted in this adverse effect.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‍ors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anticholines‍terase agents","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muscaranic ‍antagonists","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathomim‍etic agents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anticholinesterase agents","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/87b025569bd448c6bd82e3b3275f638b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anticholinesterase agents</strong></p><p>The ophthalmological examination shows a pearly white opacity of the lens suggestive of <strong>cataract.</strong> Anticholinesterase agents like <strong>echothiophate </strong>and cholinergic agonists like<strong> pilocarpine </strong>are known to be associated with cataract formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4641","text":"A patient who is on anti-g‍laucoma therapy presents ​with corneal findings as ​shown in the image below.‌ This finding is most com​monly observed with which‌ of the following drugs?","raw_text":"A patient who is on anti-glaucoma therapy presents with corneal findings as shown in the image below. This finding is most commonly observed with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‍olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apracl‍onidine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Netar‍sudil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Brimo‍nidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Netarsudil","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9551b059503648af8f454cfbc8127fe6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Netarsudil</strong></p><p>The bilateral <strong>whorl-like</strong> pattern of cream-colored lines on the cornea seen in the image is called<strong> cornea verticillata </strong>or<strong> vortex keratopathy. </strong>It is observed with the use of <strong>netarsudil</strong>. It is usually mild and reversible and not associated with any significant visual abnormalities. </p>\n<p>Netarsudil is approved for lowering intraocular pressure in patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension.</p>\n<p><strong>Other causes of corneal verticillata are</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fabry’s disease</li>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n<li>Hydroxychloroquine</li>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n<li>Phenothiazines.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3484","text":"A patient on treatment for‍ glaucoma presented with ​complaints of floaters an​d flashing lights. He com‌plains of seeing a dark g​rey curtain move across h‌is field of vision. Which‌ of the following can lea​d to this condition?","raw_text":"A patient on treatment for glaucoma presented with complaints of floaters and flashing lights. He complains of seeing a dark grey curtain move across his field of vision. Which of the following can lead to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta-b‍lockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostaglan‍din analogs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholinergi‍c agonists","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muscarinic ‍antagonists","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cholinergic agonists","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cholinergic agonists</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>retinal detachment. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is associated with the use of<strong> miotics</strong> i.e., both <strong>cholinergic agonists</strong> and <strong>anticholinesterase</strong> agents.</p>\n<p>Miotics produce ciliary body contraction which can lead to altered forces at the vitreous base resulting in retinal tears. Hence, they are to be used with extreme caution in patients who are at risk of developing retinal detachment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3476","text":"Your friend who is on trea‍tment for glaucoma tells ​you that she feels her ey​es look prettier than bef‌ore. On examination, you ​notice increased iris pig‌mentation and eyelash hyp‌ertrichosis. Which of the​ following drugs is assoc​iated with this effect?","raw_text":"Your friend who is on treatment for glaucoma tells you that she feels her eyes look prettier than before. On examination, you notice increased iris pigmentation and eyelash hypertrichosis. Which of the following drugs is associated with this effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piloc‍arpine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaz‍olamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brimo‍nidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bimat‍oprost","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bimatoprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bimatoprost</strong></p><p><strong>Bimatoprost</strong> is a PGF-2 alpha analog. PGF-2 alpha analogs are associated with <strong>eyelash hypertrichosis</strong> and <strong>iris hyperpigmentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bimatoprost is approved for topical application in the treatment of eyelash hypotrichosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3486","text":"What is the most probable ‍diagnosis in a patient wi​th a dilated pupil not re​sponsive to 1% pilocarpin‌e?","raw_text":"What is the most probable diagnosis in a patient with a dilated pupil not responsive to 1% pilocarpine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic 3rd‍ nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adie’s to‍nic pupil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uncal he‍rniation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pharmacolo‍gical block","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pharmacological block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pharmacological block</strong></p><p>The most appropriate answer would be a <strong>pharmacological block</strong>. <strong>Antimuscarinic</strong> will <strong>occupy</strong> the <strong>receptors</strong> and results in non-responsiveness to pilocarpine.</p>\n<p>In <strong>diabetic third nerve palsy</strong>, the dilated pupil is due to the loss of innervation to constrictor pupillae. <strong>Muscarinic receptors</strong> on constrictor pupillae will be <strong>intact</strong> resulting in miosis by pilocarpine.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Adie's tonic pupil</strong>, there is <strong>denervation hypersensitivity</strong>. Pupil responds to even lower concentrations of pilocarpine.</p>\n<p><strong>Uncal herniation</strong> causes pressure on the third nerve leading to ipsilateral mydriasis. But <strong>muscarinic receptors</strong> are <strong>intact</strong> and thus the pupil responds to pilocarpine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0136","text":"Which of the following can‍not be used to decrease i​ntraocular pressure in gl​aucoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used to decrease intraocular pressure in glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexame‍thasone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaz‍olamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apracl‍onidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mann‍itol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dexamethasone</strong></p><p><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> is a long-acting glucocorticoid and is not used to decrease the intraocular pressure in glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Acetazolamide, apraclonidine, and mannitol are used in lowering intraocular pressure in glaucoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"wHsntk8b","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"9_synapse_and_junctional_transmission","title":"9.Synapse And Junctional Transmission","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA9349","text":"A synapse is a junction be‍tween _______. Axon and d​endrite Axon and axon Axo​n and muscle cell Axon an​d soma","raw_text":"<p>A synapse is a junction between _______.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Axon and dendrite</li>\n<li>Axon and axon</li>\n<li>Axon and muscle cell</li>\n<li>Axon and soma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‍2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All 1, ‍2, 3, 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‍2, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. All 1, 2, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dea6820ad79548c3a92f86c4124fc5aax648x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd2a41a779744f088e4e16067a1905fdx1280x1214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. All 1, 2, 3, 4</strong></p><p>Impulses are transmitted from one nerve cell to another cell at <strong>junctions called synapse</strong>. These are the junctions where the axon or some other portion of one cell (the presynaptic cell) terminates on the <strong>dendrites, soma,</strong> or <strong>axon</strong> of another neuron or, in some cases, a <strong>muscle</strong> or <strong>gland cell</strong> (the postsynaptic cell).</p>\n<p>There are two major types of synapses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chemical </li>\n<li>Electrical</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a neuron terminates on a muscle , it is called a neuromuscular junction . This is an example of a chemical synapse .</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9350","text":"All of the following are t‍rue regarding chemical sy​napses except_____","raw_text":"All of the following are true regarding chemical synapses except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"These are the most common ‍type of synapses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurotransmitter is produc‍ed by the presynaptic neu​ron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protoplasmic continuity be‍tween pre- and post- syna​ptic membrane ensures fas​ter conduction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Conduction is always ortho‍dromic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protoplasmic continuity between pre- and post- synaptic membrane ensures faster conduction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b31b37479bf44014a57c1541a1d21e65x644x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protoplasmic continuity between pre- and post- synaptic membrane ensures faster conduction</strong></p><p><strong>Chemical synapses</strong> are interneuronal junctions <strong>without protoplasmic continuity</strong>. </p>\n<p>Most of the synapses used for signal transmission in the central nervous system of the human being are chemical synapses.</p>\n<p>In these synapses, the first neuron <strong>(presynaptic neuron) secretes neurotransmitters</strong> at its nerve ending synapse. This transmitter, in turn, acts on receptor proteins in the membrane of the next neuron (post-synaptic neuron) to excite the neuron, inhibit it, or modify its sensitivity in some other way. </p>\n<p>As neurotransmitters are released only by the pre-synaptic neuron, the synaptic conduction in the CNS is <strong>only orthodromic (unidirectional).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9351","text":"In a typical chemical syna‍ptic cleft, the extracell​ular distance between the​ presynaptic plasma membr​ane and the postsynaptic ​plasma membrane is approx​imately_____","raw_text":"In a typical chemical synaptic cleft, the extracellular distance between the presynaptic plasma membrane and the postsynaptic plasma membrane is approximately_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-4‍ nm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20-4‍0 nm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"200-4‍00 nm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2-4‍ μm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20-40 nm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7503ba7ba362406c8f8acc065b15ae5dx1280x1550.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad9b05705c0a42bda32241022d01eca0x1280x1530.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20-40 nm</strong></p><p>In a typical chemical synaptic cleft, the extracellular distance between the presynaptic plasma membrane and the postsynaptic plasma membrane is approximately <strong>20-40nm</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9353","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding neurotrans​mitters?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding neurotransmitters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All neurotransmitter are d‍erived from amino acids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitric oxide is a neurotra‍nsmitter in the CNS","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They do not act on ionotro‍pic receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are released on repol‍arization of the nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitric oxide is a neurotransmitter in the CNS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitric oxide is a neurotransmitter in the CNS</strong></p><p class=\"p2\">Nitric oxide is a neurotransmitter in the CNS.</p>\n<p>Neurotransmitters are the substances that mediate chemical signaling between neurons. They can be<strong> choline esters, biogenic amines, amino acids, and neuropeptides</strong>.</p>\n<p>Two main classes of chemical substances that serve as neurotransmitters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>small-molecule neurotransmitter (acetylcholine)</li>\n<li>large molecule neurotransmitter (enkephalin)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They can act on <strong>ionotropic receptors (ligand-gated ion channels) and/or metabotropic receptors </strong>on the postsynaptic membrane to produce a change in membrane potential to bring about their action.</p>\n<p><strong>Criteria for the substance to be neurotransmitter:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">the substance must be demonstrated to be present in the presynaptic terminal.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">the cell must be able to synthesize the substance.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">should be released on <strong>depolarization</strong> of the terminal.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">there should be specific receptors for it on the postsynaptic membrane.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">if the substance is applied exogenously to the postsynaptic membrane at physiologic concentration, the response of the postsynaptic cell must mimic the in vivo response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9373","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a monoamine neurotran​smitter?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a monoamine neurotransmitter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GA‍BA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Norepin‍ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DO‍PA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‍tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GABA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GABA</strong></p><p>GABA is not a monoamine neurotransmitter. It is an amino acid.</p>\n<p>Biogenic amines are small, charged molecules that are synthesized from amino acids and contain an amino group (R—NH2). Amines known to act as neurotransmitters are <strong>dopamine, norepinephrine</strong> (noradrenaline), <strong>epinephrine</strong> (adrenaline), <strong>serotonin</strong> (5-hydroxytryptamine [5-HT]).</p>\n<p>Amino acids and their derived amines acting as neurotransmitters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tyrosine </strong>-  catecholamines : dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine .</li>\n<li><strong>Tryptophan </strong>- serotonin</li>\n<li><strong>Histidine</strong> -<strong> </strong>histamine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2228","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding metabotrop​ic neurotransmitters?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding metabotropic neurotransmitters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They act through second me‍ssenger systems","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They bind to cationic chan‍nels and increase influx ​of Na","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They bind to anionic chann‍els and increase influx o​f Cl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They inhibit the fusion of‍ synaptic vesicle with th​e synaptic cleft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. They act through second messenger systems","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They act through second messenger systems</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 558\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurotransmitter receptors that act through second messenger systems are called metabotropic receptors.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 559\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurotransmitters bind to receptors on the post-synaptic membrane to activate ion channels in one of the two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>by gating ion channels directly (<strong>ionotropic receptors</strong>).</li>\n<li>by activating a <strong>second messenger</strong> (<strong>metabotropic receptor</strong>) for a prolonged postsynaptic neu­ronal excitation or inhibition. Most commonly activated is a G-protein coupled membrane receptor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following four changes can occur usually after binding to a G-protein coupled receptor:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 560\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Opening specific <strong>ion channels</strong> through the post-synaptic cell membrane.</li>\n<li>Activation of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (<strong>cAMP</strong>) or cyclic guanosine monophosphate (<strong>cGMP</strong>) in the neuronal cell.</li>\n<li>Activation of one or more <strong>intracellular enzymes.</strong> </li>\n<li>Activation of <strong>gene transcription</strong>. Activation of gene transcription is one of the most important effects of activation of the second messenger systems because gene transcription can cause formation of new proteins within the neuron, thereby changing its metabolic machinery or its structure. </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9370","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding the neuro​transmitter glutamate in ​the brain?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the neurotransmitter glutamate in the brain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is involved in excitato‍ry neurotansmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a minor neurotransmi‍tter in the brain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It acts via both ionotropi‍c and metabotropic recept​ors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glycine binding to the NMD‍A receptor is essential f​or the receptor to respon​d to glutamate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a minor neurotransmitter in the brain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a minor neurotransmitter in the brain</strong></p><p>Glutamate is a <strong>major excitatory neurotransmitter</strong> responsible for ~75% excitatory neurotransmission in the CNS.</p>\n<p>Glutamate is the best-known example of excitatory amino acid neurotransmitters. It brings about its action via both ionotropic and metabotropic receptors.</p>\n<p> <strong>Glutamate Receptors :</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"205\">\n<p><strong>Type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"202\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" width=\"407\">\n<p><strong>Ionotropic Glutamate Receptors</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"205\">\n<p>AMPA, Kainate receptors</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"202\">\n<p>Primarily permits influx of Na+ and efflux of K+ →EPSP (excitatory postsynaptic potential)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"205\">\n<p>NMDA receptors</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"202\">\n<p>Permits ↑ influx of Ca2+ along with Na+ </p>\n<p>→EPSP (excitatory postsynaptic potential)</p>\n<p>(<strong>Glycine</strong> binding to the NMDA receptor is essential for the receptor to respond to glutamate)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"205\">\n<p><strong>Metabotropic Glutamate Receptor(mGluR)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"202\">\n<p>Leads to either ↑ in IP3/DAG or ↓cAMP</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9372","text":"Which of the following is ‍a well-known inhibitory b​rain neurotransmitter?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a well-known inhibitory brain neurotransmitter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glut‍amate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetyl‍choline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GA‍BA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gly‍cine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GABA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GABA</strong></p><p>GABA (gamma-amino-butyric acid) is probably the best known <strong>inhibitory neurotransmitter</strong> in the brain, especially in the forebrain regions.</p>\n<p>GABA mediates both presynaptic and postsynaptic inhibition</p>\n<p><strong>GABA Receptors :</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Type </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>GABA <sub>A </sub>and GABA <sub>C</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Ionotropic receptors</p>\n<p>Activation →↑Cl<sup>-</sup> influx into the neurons → IPSP (inhibitory postsynaptic potential)</p>\n<p>Benzodiazepines (diazepam), barbiturates and phenobarbital enhance/potentiate the effect of GABA <sub>A</sub> receptors (neuromodulation)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>GABA <sub>B</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Metabotropic receptors</p>\n<p>G-protein coupled receptors</p>\n<p>G<sub>i</sub>→inhibits adenylyl cyclase to open K<sup>+</sup> channels </p>\n<p>G<sub>0</sub>→ inhibits or delays Ca<sup>2+</sup> influx</p>\n<p>Net effect=IPSP</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other options :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glutamate</strong> is a good example of an <strong>excitatory</strong> brain neurotransmitter.</li>\n<li><strong>Acetylcholine</strong> and <strong>glycine</strong> have been known to play <strong>either excitatory</strong> or <strong>inhibitory</strong> neurotransmitter roles depending on the particular brain circuits under study.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Glycine sometimes potentiates the excitatory action of glutamate at NMDA receptors. So, it might act as an excitatory neurotransmitter as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9379","text":"Correctly matched with its‍ receptor is ______","raw_text":"Correctly matched with its receptor is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nicotinic – G-Protein Coup‍led Receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GABA-A – ‍ionotropic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GABA-B - ‍Ionotropic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glycine – G-Protein Couple‍d Receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GABA-A – ionotropic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GABA-A – ionotropic</strong></p><p>GABA <sub>A</sub> is an ionotropic receptor.</p>\n<p>GABA is the major inhibitory mediator both pre and postsynaptically in the brain. </p>\n<p><strong>GABA receptors :</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Type </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>GABA <sub>A </sub>and GABA <sub>C</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Ionotropic receptors</p>\n<p>Activation →↑Cl<sup>-</sup> influx into the neurons → IPSP (inhibitory postsynaptic potential)</p>\n<p>Benzodiazepines (eg. Diazepam), barbiturates and phenobarbital enhance/potentiate the effect of GABA <sub>A</sub> receptors (neuromodulation)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>GABA <sub>B</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Metabotropic receptors</p>\n<p>G protein coupled receptors</p>\n<p>G<sub>i</sub>→inhibits adenylyl cyclase to open K<sup>+</sup> channels G</p>\n<p>G<sub>0</sub>→ inhibits or delays Ca<sup>2+</sup> influx</p>\n<p>Net effect=IPSP</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9357","text":"Which is not true about ne‍uromodulators?","raw_text":"Which is not true about neuromodulators?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Modify the postsynaptic ne‍uron’s response to a spec​ific neurotransmitter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"May change the presynaptic‍ neuron’s release of a tr​ansmitter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"May be released by the pre‍synaptic neuron along wit​h the neurotransmitter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Actions are faster than ne‍urotransmitters","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Actions are faster than neurotransmitters","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Actions are faster than neurotransmitters</strong></p><p><strong>Neuromodulators</strong> typically have actions that are <strong>slower</strong> than neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p>Neuromodulators are chemicals released by neurons that have little or no direct effects on their own but can modify the effects of neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p>They can modify the postsynaptic neuron’s response to a neurotransmitter and they can change how much of a neurotransmitter the presynaptic neuron synthesizes or releases.</p>\n<p>Neuromodulators also can be released by the presynaptic neuron along with the neurotransmitter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9355","text":"A medical student was doin‍g research in a laborator​y that studies the neurom​uscular junction. Which o​f the following events at​ the junction are listed ​in correct sequential ord‌er?","raw_text":"A medical student was doing research in a laboratory that studies the neuromuscular junction. Which of the following events at the junction are listed in correct sequential order?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Motor neuron action potent‍ial, acetylcholine releas​e, Ca2+ entry at the endp​late","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ca2+ entry into the motor ‍nerve terminal, acetylcho​line release, Na+ entry a​t the endplate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ca2+ entry into the motor ‍nerve terminal, Na+ entry​ at the endplate, motor n​euron action potential","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Na+ entry at the endplate,‍ acetylcholine release, m​uscle fiber action potent​ial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ca2+ entry into the motor nerve terminal, acetylcholine release, Na+ entry at the endplate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e35f3af5f4574d2b9fc2999d93196ed0x1117x1408.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ca2+ entry into the motor nerve terminal, acetylcholine release, Na+ entry at the endplate</strong></p><p><strong>Events Occurring at the Chemical Synapse:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 143\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">The <strong>action potential</strong> arriving at the end of the motor neuron increases the permeability of its endings to <strong>Ca2+</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">                                                                        <strong>↓</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Ca2+ enters the endings and triggers a marked increase in <strong>exocytosis</strong> of the acetylcholine-containing synaptic vesicles</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">                                                                       <strong> ↓</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Binding of acetylcholine to <strong>nicotinic receptors</strong> increases the <strong>Na+ and K+ conductance</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">                                                                        ↓</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Resultant influx of Na+ produces a local depolarizing potential, the <strong>endplate potential</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">                                                                        ↓</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">The current created by this local potential depolarizes the adjacent muscle membrane to its <strong>firing level</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">                                                                        ↓</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Action potentials</strong> are generated on either side of the endplate and are conducted away from the endplate in both directions along the muscle fiber. This muscle action potential, in turn, initiates muscle contraction.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Acetylcholine is then removed from the synaptic cleft by <strong>acetylcholinesterase,</strong> which is present in high concentration at the neuromuscular junction.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p3\"> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9358","text":"Excitatory postsynaptic po‍tential (EPSP) is due to ​the influx of ______.","raw_text":"Excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP) is due to the influx of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‍a+","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"K‍+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mg‍2+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‍l-","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Na+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77499bd4de10407faf127e3a657a456ax761x568.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Na+</strong></p><p>Excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP) is due to the influx of <strong>sodium ions (Na<sup>+</sup>)</strong>.</p>\n<p>At the post-synaptic membrane, neurotransmitters binding to receptors generate responses that can be excitatory or inhibitory. If it generates an excitatory response (makes the membrane more electrically positive/ <strong>depolarized</strong>), it is an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP) because it makes the cell more likely to reach an action potential.</p>\n<p><strong>EPSP may be due to</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> opening</strong> of<strong> sodium channels or calcium channels</strong> to allow large numbers of positive electrical charges to flow to the interior of the postsynaptic cell.</li>\n<li><strong>Depressed</strong> conduction through<strong> chloride or potassium channels</strong> or both.</li>\n<li>Various changes in the internal metabolism of the postsynaptic neuron to increase the number of excitatory membrane receptors or decrease the number of inhibitory membrane receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>As seen in the figure, following the discharge of the sensory neuron, there is positivity in the motor neuron signifying a EPSP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9364","text":"Inhibitory Postsynaptic Po‍tential (IPSP) is due to ​influx of ______","raw_text":"Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP) is due to influx of ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‍a+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K‍+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca‍2+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‍l-","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cl-","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cl-</strong></p><p>Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP) is due to the influx of <strong>chloride ions (Cl-)</strong>.</p>\n<p>If, binding of a neurotransmitter causes the postsynaptic membrane to become more negative (<strong>hyperpolarized</strong>), it is called an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) because hyperpolarization moves the membrane potential farther from the threshold and makes the cell less likely to reach an action potential.</p>\n<p><strong>IPSP may be due to :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increase in Cl- influx</strong>. This action causes diffusion of negatively charged chloride ions from outside the postsynaptic neuron to the inside, thereby carrying negative charges inward and increasing the negativity inside. This leads to hyperpolarization.</li>\n<li><strong>Increase in K+ efflux</strong>. This action causes positive ions to diffuse to the exterior, which causes increased negativity inside the neuron, which leads to hyperpolarization.</li>\n<li>Activation of receptor enzymes that increase the number of inhibitory synaptic receptors or decrease the number of excitatory receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2227","text":"The following effect in th‍e motor neuron is due to:​","raw_text":"The following effect in the motor neuron is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of chloride ion ch‍annels through the pre-sy​naptic neuronal membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in conductance of‍ potassium ions to the in​side of the neuron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activation of receptor enz‍ymes that increase the nu​mber of inhibitory synapt​ic receptors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in conductance of‍ sodium ions inside the n​euron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Activation of receptor enzymes that increase the number of inhibitory synaptic receptors","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa137b0c0a4f421f959a7f77c596e6be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Activation of receptor enzymes that increase the number of inhibitory synaptic receptors</strong></p><p>Activation of receptor enzymes that increase the number of inhibitory synaptic receptors can cause the effect shown, namely, the <strong>generation of IPSP</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the figure given, there is an increased negativity in the motor neuron following the action potential in the interneuron. So, the synaptic potential is IPSP. This can be caused at the postsynaptic membrane due to <strong>Cl- influx</strong>, <strong>K+ efflux</strong> or<strong> activation of receptor enzymes</strong> that increase the number of inhibitory synaptic receptors or decrease the number of excitatory receptors.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 560\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Opening of chloride ion channels through the <strong>post-synaptic</strong> neuronal membrane. </li>\n<li>Increase in conductance of potassium ions to the <strong>outside</strong> of the postsynaptic neuron.</li>\n<li>Increase in conductance of sodium ions inside the neuron causes the generation of an <strong>EPSP</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9365","text":"Slow inhibitory postsynapt‍ic potential (IPSP) is ge​nerated by all except ___​____","raw_text":"Slow inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) is generated by all except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac‍ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeleta‍l muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Smooth‍ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autonomi‍c ganglia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Skeletal muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Skeletal muscle</strong></p><p>Slow EPSPs and IPSPs are not generated by skeletal muscles.</p>\n<p>Slow excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs) and inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSPs) are seen in <strong>autonomic ganglia, cardiac, and smooth muscle, and cortical neurons.</strong></p>\n<p>EPSPs and IPSPs occur after a latency of 0.5ms while slow EPSPs and IPSPs occur after a latency of 100–500 ms and last for several seconds. The slow EPSPs are due to a decrease in <strong>K<sup>+</sup> efflux</strong>, and the slow IPSPs are due to an increase in K<sup>+</sup> efflux.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9369","text":"Which of the following phy‍siologic processes is not​ correctly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following physiologic processes is not correctly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presynaptic inhibition : A‍xo-axonal synapse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postsynaptic inhibition: G‍ABA produces IPSP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presynaptic inhibition: GA‍BA-B receptors decrease K​+ conductance.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presynaptic inhibition: GA‍BA-A receptors increase C​l– conductance.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Presynaptic inhibition: GABA-B receptors decrease K+ conductance.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67f7b518becc4edc83f0bb04253fc397x1280x1509.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Presynaptic inhibition: GABA-B receptors decrease K+ conductance.</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 142\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>GABA-B</strong> receptors are present in the spinal cord and appear to mediate presynaptic inhibition via a G-protein that produces an <strong>increase</strong> in K+ conductance. </p>\n<p><strong>PRE-SYNAPTIC INHIBITION</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 142\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Presynaptic inhibition is a process mediated by neurons whose terminals are on excitatory endings, forming <strong>axoaxonal synapses.</strong></li>\n<li>The first transmitter shown to produce <strong>presynaptic inhibition</strong> was <strong>GABA. </strong>On the <strong>pre</strong>-synaptic membrane\n<ul>\n<li>Acting via GABA-<strong>A</strong> receptors, GABA <strong>increases Cl– conductance. </strong></li>\n<li>GABA-<strong>B</strong> receptors appear to mediate presynaptic inhibition via a G-protein that produces an <strong>increase in K+ conductance</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>This leads to hyperpolarization of the pre-synaptic membrane and inhibition of release of the neurotransmittor from the pre-synaptic neuron.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 141\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>POST-SYNAPTIC INHIBITION</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Postsynaptic inhibition occurs when an inhibitory transmitter such as <strong>glycine</strong> or <strong>GABA</strong> is released from a presynaptic nerve terminal onto the postsynaptic neuron to induce an <strong>IPSP</strong> in the postsynaptic neuron.</li>\n<li>In the figure shown below, an inhibitory transmitter such as GABA is released from the nerve terminals of an inhibitory interneuron (dark) that synapses on a postsynaptic neuron.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9366","text":"Renshaw cell inhibition is‍ ________","raw_text":"Renshaw cell inhibition is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Feedback f‍acilitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Feedback ‍inhibition","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Feed forwar‍d inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indirect ‍inhibition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Feedback inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ca0a5177424438b82dbf2c5468e9a52x1280x1655.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Feedback inhibition</strong></p><p>Renshaw cell inhibition is an example of feedback inhibition.</p>\n<p>The axon of a spinal motor neuron has a recurrent collateral that synapses on an inhibitory interneuron that terminates on the cell body of the<strong> same</strong> and other motor neurons. The inhibitory interneuron is called a <strong>Renshaw cell</strong> and its neurotransmitter is <strong>glycine.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9367","text":"Feedforward inhibition is ‍seen in ________","raw_text":"Feedforward inhibition is seen in ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Med‍ulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pituita‍ry gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cereb‍ellum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rensha‍w cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cerebellum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2eb1fc66b14d49cdb3ecf1a4b57b2bb0x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cerebellum</strong></p><p>Feedforward inhibition is seen in the cerebellum. In the cerebellum, stimulation of basket cells / stellate cells produces IPSPs in the Purkinje cells (i.e, inhibits Purkinje cells)</p>\n<p>In feedforward inhibition, a presynaptic cell excites an inhibitory interneuron which then <strong>inhibits the next follower</strong> cell.</p>\n<p>In cerebellum (Refer flow chart):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>This arrangement is called feed-forward inhibition which presumably limits the duration of excitation produced by any given afferent volley.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Renshaw cell carries out the negative feedback inhibition of a spinal motor neuron.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9368","text":"Presynaptic facilitation i‍s due to prolonged openin​g of_____","raw_text":"Presynaptic facilitation is due to prolonged opening of_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na+ ch‍annels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K+ ch‍annels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca2+ c‍hannels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cl- ch‍annels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ca2+ channels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e056fde68c2e40f2b0b977d01294a53ax1280x1337.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ca2+ channels</strong></p><p>Presynaptic facilitation is due to prolonged opening of <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> channels .</p>\n<p>In presynaptic facilitation, the <strong>serotonin</strong> released at the axoaxonal ending increases the intraneuronal cAMP levels→ phosphorylation and consequent closure of K<sup>+</sup> channels → slowing of repolarization and prolonging the depolarization by prolonged opening of Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9383","text":"A person with myasthenia g‍ravis notes increased mus​cle strength when he is t​reated with an acetylchol​inesterase (AChE) inhibit​or. The basis for his imp​rovement is due to the in‌crease in _______","raw_text":"A person with myasthenia gravis notes increased muscle strength when he is treated with an acetylcholinesterase (AChE) inhibitor.  The basis for his improvement is due to the increase in _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The amount of acetylcholin‍e (ACh) released from mot​or nerves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The level of ACh at the mu‍scle end plates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The number of ACh receptor‍s on the motor end plates​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Synthesis of norepinephrin‍e in motor nerves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The level of ACh at the muscle end plates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The level of ACh at the muscle end plates</strong></p><p>Treatment with AChE inhibitors (e.g., neostigmine) <strong>prevents the degradation of ACh</strong> and prolongs the action of ACh at the muscle end plate.</p>\n<p><strong>Myasthenia gravis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is caused by the presence of antibodies to the ACh receptor.</li>\n<li>Is characterized by skeletal muscle weakness and fatigability resulting from a reduced number of ACh receptors on the muscle end plate.</li>\n<li>The size of the EPP (end plate potential) is reduced.</li>\n<li>Treatment with AChE inhibitors (e.g., neostigmine) prevents the degradation of ACh and prolongs the action of ACh at the muscle end plate, partially compensating for the reduced number of receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2408","text":"A 55-year-old woman had an‍ autonomic neuropathy tha​t disrupted the sympathet​ic nerve supply to the pu​pillary dilator muscle of​ her right eye. While hav​ing her eyes examined, th‌e ophthalmologist placed ‌a sympathomimetic drug in‍ both her eyes. The right​ eye became much more dil​ated than the left eye. T‌his suggests that:","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman had an autonomic neuropathy that disrupted the sympathetic nerve supply to the pupillary dilator muscle of her right eye. While having her eyes examined, the ophthalmologist placed a sympathomimetic drug in both her eyes. The right eye became much more dilated than the left  eye. This suggests that:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The sympathetic nerve to t‍he right eye had regenera​ted","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The parasympathetic nerve ‍supply to the right eye r​emained intact and compen​sated for the loss of the​ sympathetic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denervation supersensitivi‍ty had developed","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The left eye also had nerv‍e damage and so was not r​esponding as expected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Denervation supersensitivity had developed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Denervation supersensitivity had developed</strong></p><p><strong>Denervation supersensitivity</strong> develops in the muscle after the nerve supplying it is cut. Since the nerve to her right dilator pupillae is damaged, the right eye becomes excessively sensitive to the sympathomimetic drug. So, the right eye becomes much more dilated than the left.</p>\n<p><strong>Denervation Supersensitivity:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the motor nerve to <strong>skeletal muscle</strong> is degenerated, the muscle gradually becomes extremely sensitive to acetylcholine. This is called denervation hypersensitivity or supersensitivity.</li>\n<li>Normally nicotinic receptors are located only in the vicinity of the motor endplate where the axon of the motor nerve terminates. When the motor nerve is severed, there is a <strong>marked proliferation of nicotinic receptors</strong> over a wide region of the neuromuscular junction.</li>\n<li>Denervation supersensitivity also occurs at <strong>autonomic junctions.</strong> Smooth muscle, unlike skeletal muscle, does not atrophy when denervated, but it becomes hyperresponsive to the chemical mediator that normally activates it.</li>\n<li>This hyperresponsiveness can be demonstrated by using pharmacologic tools rather than actual nerve section. Prolonged use of a drug such as reserpine can be used to deplete transmitter stores and prevent the target organ from being exposed to norepinephrine for an extended period. Once the drug usage is stopped, smooth muscle and cardiac muscle will be supersensitive to subsequent release of the neurotransmitter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option a</strong>) If the symapthetic nerve would have regenerated, both right and left eye would have been equally sensitive to the drug and so, would have dilated equally.</p>\n<p><strong>Option b</strong>) Parasympathetic nerves supply the spinchter pupillae muscle. So, if stimulated they would constrict the eye not dilate it. Also, the drug added was a sympathomimetic agent. So, parasympathetic nerves are not stimulated in the first place</p>\n<p><strong>Option d</strong>) If the left eye was damaged, it would also have denervation supersensitivity. Again, both eyes would dilate equally, but more than that of normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9352","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is not true about​ neurotransmitters?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about neurotransmitters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They must be present at th‍e pre-synaptic membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurons must be able to sy‍nthesize the substance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are released on repol‍arization of the nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Specific receptors must be‍ present on the post-syna​ptic membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They are released on repolarization of the nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They are released on repolarization of the nerve</strong></p><p>Neurotransmitters should be released on <strong>depolarization</strong> of the terminal.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurotransmitters are the substances that mediate chemical signaling between neurons.</li>\n<li>The transmission of information at chemical synapses involves the release of neurotransmitter from a presynaptic cell, diffusion across the synaptic cleft, and binding of the neurotransmitter to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane to produce a change in membrane potential.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Criteria for the substance to be neurotransmitter</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>the substance must be demonstrated to be present in the presynaptic terminal</li>\n<li>the cell must be able to synthesize the substance.</li>\n<li>should be released on depolarization of the terminal.</li>\n<li>there should be specific receptors for it on the postsynaptic membrane.</li>\n<li>if the substance is applied exogenously to the postsynaptic membrane at physiologic concentration, the response of the postsynaptic cell must mimic the in vivo response</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9356","text":"Neurotransmitter can be de‍graded by all except_____​","raw_text":"Neurotransmitter can be degraded by all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can diffuse out of synapti‍c cleft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enzymes in‍activate it","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reuptake int‍o glial cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autore‍ceptor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autoreceptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autoreceptor</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 150\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The final steps in the process lead to termination of the actions of the neurotransmitter and include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reuptake into the nerve terminal or glial cell</li>\n<li>Enzymatic degradation.</li>\n<li>Diffusion away from the synaptic cleft</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9382","text":"Which of the following end‍ogenous opioids is correc​tly matched with the rece​ptor at which it has maxi​mum potency?","raw_text":"Which of the following endogenous opioids is correctly matched with the receptor at which it has maximum potency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enkephalins ‍- δ receptors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dynorphins -‍ μ receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endorphins -‍ κ receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both B‍ and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enkephalins - δ receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enkephalins - δ receptors</strong></p><p><strong>Enkephalins</strong> have maximum potency at <strong>δ receptors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dynorphins</strong> have maximum potency at <strong>κ receptors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Endorphins</strong> have maximum potency at <strong>μ receptors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>There is a difference of opinion among Goodman &amp; Gillman's textbook and Ganong’s review of medical physiology(26th edition pg 151). According to Goodman &amp; Gillman's textbook above answer is correct.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2388","text":"Which of the following phy‍siologic processes is not​ correctly paired with a ​structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following physiologic processes is not correctly paired with a structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electrical transmission : ‍gap junction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Negative feedback inhibiti‍on : Renshaw cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synaptic vesicle docking a‍nd fusion : presynaptic n​erve terminal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endplate potential : musca‍rinic cholinergic recepto​r","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endplate potential : muscarinic cholinergic receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3d26dadd99e4f16a56f1f18eea016cbx898x501.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f824254699e94a7bad9a532d759031b8x582x409.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endplate potential : muscarinic cholinergic receptor</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 143\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Endplate potential is generated via <strong>nicotinic</strong> <strong>cholinergic receptor.</strong></p>\n<p>The acetylcholine released from the motor neuron diffuses to nicotinic cholinergic (NM) receptors that are concentrated at the tops of the junctional folds of the membrane of the motor endplate.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 143\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2384","text":"Which of the following ele‍ctrophysiologic events is​ correctly paired with th​e change in ionic current​s causing the event?","raw_text":"Which of the following electrophysiologic events is correctly paired with the change in ionic currents causing the event?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fast inhibitory postsynapt‍ic potentials (IPSPs) and​ closing of Cl– channels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fast excitatory postsynapt‍ic potentials (EPSPs) and​ an decrease in Ca2+ cond​uctance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endplate potential and an ‍increase in Na+ conductan​ce","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presynaptic inhibition and‍ closure of voltage-gated​ K+ channels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endplate potential and an increase in Na+ conductance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endplate potential and an increase in Na+ conductance</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 143\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Binding of acetylcholine to neuromuscular junction receptors increases the Na+ and K+ conductance, and the resultant influx of <strong>Na+</strong> produces a depolarizing potential, the <strong>endplate potential</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>IPSP may be due to</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Opening of <strong>chloride ion channels</strong> through the postsynaptic neuronal membrane</li>\n<li>Increase in conductance of<strong> potassium ions </strong>out of the neuron.</li>\n<li>Activation of receptor enzymes that increase the number of inhibitory synaptic receptors or decrease the number of excitatory receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>EPSP may be due to</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> opening</strong> of<strong> sodium channels</strong> to allow large numbers of positive electrical charges to flow to the interior of the postsynaptic cell.</li>\n<li><strong>Depressed</strong> conduction through<strong> chloride or potassium channels</strong>, or both.</li>\n<li>Various changes in the internal metabolism of the postsynaptic neuron to increase the number of excitatory membrane receptors or decrease the number of inhibitory membrane receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5226","text":"How is an electrical synap‍se different from a chemi​cal synapse?","raw_text":"How is an electrical synapse different from a chemical synapse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No cytoplasmic continuity ‍between presynaptic and p​ostsynaptic membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direction of impulse is un‍idirectional","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Connexons are involved in ‍electrical synapse","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Electrical synapse has mor‍e latent period than chem​ical synapse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Connexons are involved in electrical synapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Connexons are involved in electrical synapse</strong></p><p>In <strong>electrical synapses</strong>, the cytoplasm of adjacent cells is directly connected by<strong> gap junctions (connexons)</strong> that allow free movement of ions from the interior of one cell to the interior of the next cell.</p>\n<p>The transmis­sion in electrical synapses is<strong> bidirectional </strong>and there is<strong> no synaptic delay. </strong>Unlike chemical synapses, electrical synapse has<strong> cytoplasmic continuity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong> </p>\n<p>Most of the synapses used for signal transmission in the central nervous system are chemical synapses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_W0M1zvs","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"9_dementia","title":"9.Dementia","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MD6709","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures differentiate delir​ium from dementia in Alzh​eimer's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following features differentiate delirium from dementia in Alzheimer's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acuity of onset and agitat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acuity of onset and loss o‌f consciousness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Visual hallucinations and ‌impaired memory","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Agitation an‌d irritation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acuity of onset and loss of consciousness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acuity of onset and loss of consciousness</strong></p><p><strong>Delirium</strong> is characterized by <strong>acute onset</strong> of symptoms and is often accompanied by diminished or <strong>loss of consciousness and cognition. </strong>This feature is useful to clinically differentiate between delirium and dementia. </p>\n<p>Dementia is a group of diseases marked by chronic, progressive cognitive impairment with clear consciousness. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0310","text":"Which of the following is ‌a type of cortical dement​ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a type of cortical dementia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Huntington‌’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alzheimer‌’s disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alzheimer’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alzheimer’s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Alzheimer's disease</strong> is an example of cortical dementia.</p>\n<p><strong>Dementia</strong> is defined as a disease process marked by progressive cognitive impairment in clear consciousness. Depending on the site of the cerebral lesions, the dementias are classified as <strong>cortical</strong> or <strong>subcortical.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 449px; height: 612px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.828125px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Cortical dementia</strong> <strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.203125px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Subcortical dementia</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 58px;\"><strong>Site of lesion </strong> <strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.828125px; height: 58px;\">Cortex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.203125px; height: 58px;\">Thalamus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, brain stem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Higher cortical symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.828125px; height: 22px;\">\n<p>Aphasia</p>\n<p>Apraxia</p>\n<p>Agnosia</p>\n<p>Prosopagnosia</p>\n<p>Acalculia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.203125px; height: 22px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 16px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 16px;\"><strong>Motor symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.828125px; height: 16px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.203125px; height: 16px;\">\n<p>Dysarthria</p>\n<p>Tremors</p>\n<p>Ataxia</p>\n<p>Rigidity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 79px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 79px;\"><strong>Memory</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.828125px; height: 79px;\">Recall and recognition are impaired</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.203125px; height: 79px;\">Recall is impaired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 79px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 79px;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.828125px; height: 79px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n<li>Frontotemporal dementia</li>\n<li>Creutzfeldt Jakob disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.203125px; height: 79px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Parkinson's disease</li>\n<li>Progressive supranuclear palsy</li>\n<li>Huntington's disease</li>\n<li>Wilson's disease</li>\n<li>Idiopathic basal ganglia calcification </li>\n<li>Normal-pressure hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Vascular dementia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0305","text":"If identified and interven‌ed early, rapid clinical ​improvement can be expect​ed in a patient with whic​h of the following causes‌ of dementia ?","raw_text":"If identified and intervened early, rapid clinical improvement can be expected in a patient with which of the following causes of dementia ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vascular‌ dementia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Creutzfeldt-‌Jakob disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal pressure hydrocepha‌lus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Huntington‌’s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Normal pressure hydrocephalus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Normal pressure hydrocephalus</strong></p><p>If diagnosed and intervened early, clinical improvement can be expected with <strong>normal pressure hydrocephalus</strong> (NPH), as it is a <strong>reversible</strong> cause of dementia.</p>\n<p>Normal pressure hydrocephalus can present with the following triad (<strong>Adam’s</strong> triad or <strong>Hakim's</strong> triad), remembered as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Urinary incontinence (wet)</li>\n<li>Dementia (wacky)</li>\n<li>Gait disturbance (wobbly)           </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Causes of dementia:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 282px; width: 358px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.703125px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Reversible</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.3125px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Irreversible</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 140px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 164.703125px; height: 140px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Intracranial tumors</li>\n<li>Subdural hematoma</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Autoimmune encephalopathy</li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 deficiency</li>\n<li>Meningitis </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.3125px; height: 140px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n<li>Huntington's disease</li>\n<li>Lewy body disease</li>\n<li>Vascular dementia</li>\n<li>Creutzfeld-Jacob disease</li>\n<li>Pick's disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0307","text":"In a patient being evaluat‌ed for cognitive and memo​ry deficits, which of the​ following clinical featu​res would favor a diagnos‌is of subcortical dementi‌a?","raw_text":"In a patient being evaluated for cognitive and memory deficits, which of the following clinical features would favor a diagnosis of subcortical dementia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dyst‌onia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aph‌asia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prosop‌agnosia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apr‌axia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dystonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dystonia</strong></p><p><strong>Dystonia</strong> is a feature of subcortical dementia.</p>\n<p><strong>Extrapyramidal symptoms</strong> along with memory deficits are typical of subcortical dementia.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 587px;\" width=\"501\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.3125px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.34375px;\"><strong>Cortical dementia</strong> <strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.359375px;\"><strong>Subcortical dementia</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.3125px;\"><strong>Site of lesion </strong><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.34375px;\">Cortex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.359375px;\">Thalamus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, brain stem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.3125px;\"><strong>Higher cortical symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.34375px;\">\n<p>Aphasia</p>\n<p>Apraxia</p>\n<p>Agnosia</p>\n<p>Prosopagnosia</p>\n<p>Acalculia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.359375px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.3125px;\"><strong>Motor symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.34375px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.359375px;\">\n<p>Dysarthria</p>\n<p>Tremors</p>\n<p>Ataxia</p>\n<p>Rigidity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.3125px;\"><strong>Memory</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.34375px;\">Recall and recognition are impaired</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.359375px;\">Recall is impaired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.3125px;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.34375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n<li>Frontotemporal dementia</li>\n<li>Creutzfeldt Jakob disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.359375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Parkinson's disease</li>\n<li>Progressive supranuclear palsy</li>\n<li>Huntington's disease</li>\n<li>Wilson's disease</li>\n<li>Idiopathic basal ganglia calcification </li>\n<li>Normal-pressure hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Vascular dementia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6703","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a type of subcortical​ dementia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a type of subcortical dementia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pick's ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Huntington‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pick's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pick&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Pick's disease </strong>(a type of frontotemporal dementia) is a type of <strong>cortical dementia.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"height: 518px; width: 392px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 116px; height: 39px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.203px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Cortical dementia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7969px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Subcortical dementia</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 116px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Site of lesion </strong><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.203px; height: 36px;\">Cortex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7969px; height: 36px;\">Thalamus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, brain stem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 174px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 116px; height: 174px;\"><strong>Higher cortical symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.203px; height: 174px;\">\n<p>Aphasia</p>\n<p>Apraxia</p>\n<p>Agnosia</p>\n<p>Prosopagnosia</p>\n<p>Acalculia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7969px; height: 174px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 142px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 116px; height: 142px;\"><strong>Motor symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.203px; height: 142px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7969px; height: 142px;\">\n<p>Dysarthria</p>\n<p>Tremors</p>\n<p>Ataxia</p>\n<p>Rigidity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 116px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Memory</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.203px; height: 54px;\">Recall and recognition are impaired</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7969px; height: 54px;\">Recall is impaired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 208px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 116px; height: 208px;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.203px; height: 208px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n<li>Fronto-temporal dementia</li>\n<li>Creutzfeldt Jakob disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.7969px; height: 208px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Parkinson's disease</li>\n<li>Progressive supranuclear palsy</li>\n<li>Huntington's disease</li>\n<li>Wilson's disease</li>\n<li>Idiopathic basal ganglia calcification </li>\n<li>Normal-pressure hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Vascular dementia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0313","text":"A 75-year-old woman has be‌en frequently misplacing ​her spectacles. Her son c​riticizes her for not kee​ping her spectacles in th‌e same place which gets h‌er agitated. She starts s‍houting and accusing her ​son and often ends up cry‌ing. What is this phenome‌non called?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old woman has been frequently misplacing her spectacles. Her son criticizes her for not keeping her spectacles in the same place which gets her agitated. She starts shouting and accusing her son and often ends up crying. What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brief psych‌otic episode","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Altr‌uism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cath‌arsis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Catastroph‌ic reaction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Catastrophic reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Catastrophic reaction</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of old age and the frequent misplacing of spectacles (memory loss) points towards <strong>dementia</strong>. The associated history of over-reaction and agitation is suggestive of <strong>catastrophic reaction. </strong></p>\n<p>In catastrophic reactions, patients <strong>overreact </strong>and <strong>become agitated </strong>when confronted with their cognitive limitations or when facing changes in routine or environment. It is frequently seen in elderly patients with dementia. </p>\n<p>Option A: The given scenario is not a brief psychotic episode because of the absence of psychotic symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option B: Altruism is the selfless behavior of helping another member of the community; putting another person's need before one's own and learning that there is value in giving to others.</p>\n<p>Option C: Catharsis is the expression of ideas, thoughts, and suppressed material that is accompanied by an emotional response that produces a state of relief in the patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0936","text":"A 70-year-old woman was ev‌aluated for memory proble​ms. Her husband first not​iced the issue about 4 or​ 5 years back but attribu‌ted the symptoms to old a‌ge. Gradually her forgetf‍ulness worsened and began​ to interfere with her da‌ily activities such as ho‌usekeeping. A month ago, ​she got lost while return​ing from the church which‍ she had been visiting re​gularly for the past 30 y‍ears. No personality chan‌ges, rigidity, or gait di​sturbances were observed.‌ What is the most probabl​e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old woman was evaluated for memory problems. Her husband first noticed the issue about  4 or 5 years back but attributed the symptoms to old age. Gradually her forgetfulness worsened and began to interfere with her daily activities such as housekeeping. A month ago, she got lost while returning from the church which she had been visiting regularly for the past 30 years. No personality changes, rigidity, or gait disturbances were observed. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alzheimer‌'s disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vascular‌ dementia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dementia wit‌h Lewy bodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pick's ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alzheimer's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alzheimer&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>insidious loss of episodic memory</strong> followed by <strong>slowly progressive dementia</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Alzheimer’s disease.</strong> This primarily involves the neurodegeneration of the cholinergic system<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Alzheimer's disease is the <strong>most common </strong>cause of dementia, followed by vascular dementia and Lewy body dementia.</p>\n<p>Option B: Absence of prior cerebrovascular accident history or focal neurological deficits rules out vascular dementia.          </p>\n<p>Option C: Absence of gait disturbance, rigidity (Parkinsonism) rules out dementia with Lewy bodies. </p>\n<p>Option D: Absence of early personality changes rules out Pick's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0314","text":"A 60-year-old woman who ha‌s been having progressive​ cognitive impairment for​ the past few years, rece​ntly started having hallu‌cinations and strange del‌usions. Her MRI showed co‍rtical and subcortical at​rophy prominent in the pa‌rietal and temporal lobes‌. Which of the following ​statements are true regar​ding her condition? There‍ is a depletion of cholin​ergic neurons Visuospatia‍l deficits followed by me‌mory impairment Apolipopr​otein E4 is protective Ne‌urofibrillary tangles are​ present Apolipoprotein E​2 is a risk factor","raw_text":"<p>A 60-year-old woman who has been having progressive cognitive impairment for the past few years, recently started having hallucinations and strange delusions. Her MRI showed cortical and subcortical atrophy prominent in the parietal and temporal lobes. Which of the following statements are true regarding her condition?</p>\n<ol class=\"ol1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">There is a depletion of cholinergic neurons</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Visuospatial deficits followed by memory impairment</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Apolipoprotein E4 is protective </li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Neurofibrillary tangles are present</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Apolipoprotein E2 is a risk factor</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 and 4</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario of a patient with progressive cognitive impairment and cortical and subcortical atrophy in the parietal and temporal lobes is suggestive of <strong>Alzheimer's disease</strong> (AD).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Statement 2: In Alzheimer's disease, memory impairment is <strong>followed by</strong> visuospatial defects.<br/>Statement 3 and 5: Apolipoprotein E4 is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for Alzheimer's disease while apolipoprotein E2 is <strong>protective</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3551","text":"The memory disorder correc‌tly matched with the path​ology is?","raw_text":"The memory disorder correctly matched with the pathology is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Korsakoff syndrome - hyper‌activation of mammillary ​bodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alzheimer's disease - loss‌ of synapses","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alzheimer's disease - earl‌y degeneration of frontal​ lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frontotemporal dementia - ‌mesial temporal lobe atro​phy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alzheimer's disease - loss of synapses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alzheimer&#x27;s disease - loss of synapses</strong></p><p>The cognitive decline in <strong>Alzheimer's disease</strong> is due to the <strong>loss of synapses</strong> and<strong> </strong>neuronal degeneration.</p>\n<p>Microscopically, the following are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuritic plaques - containing amyloid-β</li>\n<li>Neurofibrillary tangles - containing abnormally phosphorylated tau proteins</li>\n<li>Amyloid-β accumulation in the blood vessel walls of the cortex and leptomeninges.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: In Korsakoff syndrome, there is <strong>inactivation</strong> (not hyperactivation) of <strong>mammillary bodies</strong> due to thiamine deficiency.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Alzheimer's</strong> disease initially affects the parietal and temporal lobe, followed by the <strong>frontal lobe</strong> in the <strong>later</strong> stages. Hence personality changes are a late feature of Alzheimer's. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Mesial temporal lobe atrophy</strong> is very sensitive to <strong>Alzheimer's</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0318","text":"In elderly patients diagno‌sed with Alzheimer's dise​ase, which of the followi​ng mutations is most like​ly to be seen?","raw_text":"In elderly patients diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease, which of the following mutations is most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apolipop‌rotein E4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Presen‌ilin-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presen‌ilin-2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amyloid Prec‌ursor Protein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apolipoprotein E4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apolipoprotein E4</strong></p><p><strong>Late-onset Alzheimer's disease</strong> is known to be associated with mutations in <strong>a</strong><strong>polipoprotein E4</strong> (<strong>APOE4</strong>) gene.</p>\n<p>Mutations in genetic loci associated with <strong>early-onset</strong> Alzheimer's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amyloid Precursor Protein (<strong>APP</strong>) – chromosome 21</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3155\">Presenilin-1 (<strong>PS1</strong>) – chromosome 14</li>\n<li>Presenilin-2 (<strong>PS2</strong>) – chromosome 1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mutations in genetic loci associated with <strong>late-onset</strong> Alzheimer's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apolipoprotein E4 (<strong>APOE4</strong>) – chromosome 19</li>\n<li>Triggering Receptor Expressed on Myeloid Cells 2<strong> </strong>(<strong>TREM2</strong>) – chromosome 6</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Adults with trisomy 21 (Down’s syndrome) develop typical neuropathologic hallmarks of AD if they survive beyond age 40.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0325","text":"An elderly man with a long‌-standing history of memo​ry loss and behavioral ch​anges passed away. On aut​opsy, atrophy of the brai‌n was noted. A histopatho‌logy section of his brain‍ showed the following fin​ding. Which of the follow‌ing describes the sequenc‌e of enzyme action that l​eads to its development?","raw_text":"An elderly man with a long-standing history of memory loss and behavioral changes passed away. On autopsy, atrophy of the brain was noted. A histopathology section of his brain showed the following finding. Which of the following describes the sequence of enzyme action that leads to its development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α and β ‌secretase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"α and γ ‌secretase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"β and γ ‌secretase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"α, β and γ‌ secretase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. β and γ secretase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53f64a6913a744649f1396c739490f7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cbc7572098d54ef6a4b27cdee4882d86x640x481.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f3bbf1680e54bfd89a71923ad9ddcb4x640x784.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. β and γ secretase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Alzheimer’s disease</strong> and the given image shows abnormal<strong> amyloid plaques </strong>(β-amyloid peptide - Aβ). <strong>β- and γ-secretase activity</strong> is responsible for its development.</p>\n<p>Excess production of<strong> toxic amyloid</strong> <strong>Aβ 42</strong> initiates cellular damage in Alzheimer’s disease. </p>\n<p>The image below depicts an amyloid plaque seen in Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below depicts neurofibrillary tangles in Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7324","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about Alzheimer'​s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about Alzheimer's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intracellular neurofibrill‌ary tangles are seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuritic plaques made of b‌eta amyloid are found","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus of Meynert is not ‌affected","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Short term memory is affec‌ted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nucleus of Meynert is not affected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nucleus of Meynert is not affected</strong></p><p><strong>Nucleus of Meynert</strong> is affected in Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p>Biochemically, there is a reduction of acetylcholine and <strong>degeneration</strong> of <strong>cholinergic neurons</strong> in the <strong>nucleus basalis of Meynert</strong> that project throughout the cortex. There is also noradrenergic and serotonergic depletion due to degeneration of brain stem nuclei such as the locus ceruleus and the dorsal raphe. </p>\n<p>Microscopically, there are neuritic plaques containing amyloid-β, neurofibrillary tangles, and amyloid-β accumulation in the blood vessel walls of the cortex and leptomeninges.</p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The FDA recently approved<strong> Flortaucipir F18</strong> for <strong>intravenous injection</strong>, the <strong>first radioactive diagnostic agent</strong> for adult patients with cognitive impairment who are being evaluated for Alzheimer’s disease. It is indicated for <strong>PET imaging of the brain</strong> to estimate the density and distribution of aggregated <strong>tau</strong> neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs), a primary marker of Alzheimer’s disease.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0321","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true regar​ding the management of Al​zheimer's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding the management of Alzheimer's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Donepezil is the only drug‌ approved for all stages ​of the disease.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monoclonal antibodies can ‌be used in the treatment ​of Alzheimer's disease.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Memantine is an NMDA antag‌onist used in moderate to​ severe disease.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Galantamine, Rivastigmine ‌and Benztropine are all u​sed in the treatment of A​lzheimer's disease.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Galantamine, Rivastigmine and Benztropine are all used in the treatment of Alzheimer's disease.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Galantamine, Rivastigmine and Benztropine are all used in the treatment of Alzheimer&#x27;s disease.</strong></p><p><strong>Benztropine</strong> (anticholinergic) is not indicated in Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p>The characteristic feature of <strong>Alzheimer's</strong> is the degeneration of cholinergic neurons leading to <strong>decreased </strong>levels of<strong> acetylcholine</strong>. Benztropine, a <strong>muscarinic receptor antagonist,</strong> would block acetylcholine receptors and <strong>worsen symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p>Donepezil, Galantamine, and Rivastigmine are all acetylcholinesterase inhibitors used in the management of Alzheimer's disease. Donepezil is the only drug approved for all stages of Alzheimer's disease. </p>\n<p>Memantine is an NMDA receptor antagonist used in moderate to severe cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0324","text":"An elderly patient was ref‌erred from the medicine O​PD with paranoid ideation​ and suspiciousness. He h​as also been having inapp‌ropriate outbursts of rag‌e for the past 1 year and‍ memory issues for the pa​st 3 months. He is unable‌ to dress by himself or m‌anage his daily activitie​s, such as cooking or cle​aning. Imaging was done a‍nd the following findings​ were seen. What is the l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly patient was referred from the medicine OPD with paranoid ideation and suspiciousness. He has also been having inappropriate outbursts of rage for the past 1 year and memory issues for the past 3 months. He is unable to dress by himself or manage his daily activities, such as cooking or cleaning. Imaging was done and the following findings were seen. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alzheimer‌’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pick’s ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vascular‌ dementia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lewy-body‌ dementia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pick’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b026ae725d84401fafd5b8dab2ab0a9a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2542e7b481504d71b87282ba57aa7038x630x630.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa5f472a3854450bbf708052c7566857x718x1062.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pick’s disease</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario and the scan indicating atrophy of frontal and parietal lobes are suggestive of <strong>frontotemporal dementia (FTD),</strong> one of which is <strong>Pick's disease. </strong></p>\n<p>Pick's disease presents with <strong>early personality changes </strong>(frontal lobe involvement)<strong> </strong>and loss of social restraints initially, <strong>followed by memory loss</strong>. By contrast, Alzheimer's patients present with memory loss first and then develop personality changes. </p>\n<p>Other features include loss of insight, easy distractibility, reduced concern or empathy for others, emotional lability or withdrawal, impulsiveness, poor self-care, and perseveration.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">There is a second type of presentation of FTD, which is characterized by an <strong>early and progressive change in language</strong>, with problems of expression of language or severe naming difficulty, and problems with word meaning.</p>\n<p>The classic imaging findings seen in FTD are preferential<strong> atrophy </strong>of the <strong>frontal and temporal lobes</strong> with <strong>occipitoparietal sparing</strong>.</p>\n<p>A sagittal section brain MRI is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Histopathology:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pick cells</strong> (swollen neurons) in affected areas of the brain</li>\n<li><strong>Pick bodies</strong> (intraneuronal argentophilic inclusions)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the image below, the arrows point to the pick bodies in the frontal cortex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0323","text":"A 62-year-old man was take‌n to a psychiatrist by hi​s family with complaints ​of strange behavior. He h​as been inappropriately m‌aking sexual remarks and ‌was caught urinating in p‍ublic. Recently, he creat​ed a ruckus by cracking j‌okes and laughing out lou‌d at a funeral. On evalua​tion, there was a deficit​ in executive functioning‍ and working memory. What​ is the most common genet‍ic change associated with‌ the given clinical condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man was taken to a psychiatrist by his family with complaints of strange behavior. He has been inappropriately making sexual remarks and was caught urinating in public. Recently, he created a ruckus by cracking jokes and laughing out loud at a funeral. On evaluation, there was a deficit in executive functioning and working memory. What is the most common genetic change associated with the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hexanucleotide repeat expa‌nsion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trinucleotide repeat expan‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dele‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Point m‌utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hexanucleotide repeat expansion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hexanucleotide repeat expansion</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>frontotemporal dementia</strong> (FTD).<strong> </strong>The <strong>most common</strong> genetic abnormality associated with FTD is a <strong>hexanucleotide expansion</strong> of the gene <strong>C9orf72</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other mutations causing frontotemporal dementia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Progranulin</strong> (<strong>GRN</strong>) mutation on chromosome 17</li>\n<li><strong>Microtubule-associated protein tau</strong> (<strong>MAPT</strong>) mutation on chromosome 17.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0319","text":"A 70-year-old veteran army‌ officer presents with di​fficulty in concentrating​, increased forgetfulness​, repeated falls due to s‌luggish movement and also‌ complains of seeing recu‍rrent red animal figures ​for a few weeks. Which of‌ the following is most li‌kely?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old veteran army officer presents with difficulty in concentrating,  increased forgetfulness, repeated falls due to sluggish movement and also complains of seeing recurrent red animal figures for a few weeks. Which of the following is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frontotempo‌ral dementia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dementia wit‌h lewy bodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alzheimer‌’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post Traumatic Stress Diso‌rder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dementia with lewy bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dementia with lewy bodies</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of dementia associated with <strong>hallucinations</strong> is suggestive of <strong>d</strong><strong>ementia with Lewy bodies.</strong></p>\n<p>Core clinical features (the first three typically occur early and may persist throughout the course):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluctuating cognition</strong> with pronounced variations in attention and alertness.</li>\n<li>Recurrent <strong>visual hallucinations</strong> that are typically well-formed and detailed.</li>\n<li><strong>REM sleep behavior disorder </strong>which may precede cognitive decline.</li>\n<li>One or more spontaneous cardinal features of<strong> Parkinsonism</strong> (bradykinesia, rest tremor, rigidity).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: A new study found that older adults who frequently used sleep medications were 40% more likely to develop dementia over 15 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0338","text":"A 60-year-old woman, with ‌a history of hypertension​ and hyperlipidemia, was ​referred to the psychiatr​y OPD for evaluation of m‌emory loss and depression‌. Which of the following ‍features is least suggest​ive of vascular dementia ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman, with a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia, was referred to the psychiatry OPD for evaluation of memory loss and depression. Which of the following features is least suggestive of vascular dementia in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal neurological deficit‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stepladder d‌eterioration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"White matter lesions on MR‌I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic, gr‌adual course","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic, gradual course","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic, gradual course</strong></p><p>In classic vascular dementia, since infarcts are accumulated in a stepwise fashion, deterioration is similarly <strong>abrupt and stepwise</strong> rather than smoothly gradual. </p>\n<p>The criteria for the clinical diagnosis of vascular dementia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cognitive decline</strong> from a previously higher level of functioning</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>focal signs</strong> on neurological examination, consistent with stroke</li>\n<li>A relationship between the above two, based on the presence of one or more of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Onset of dementia within 3 months following a recognized stroke</li>\n<li>Abrupt deterioration in cognitive functions, or fluctuating, stepwise progression of cognitive deficits.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Imaging findings in vascular dementia include extensive leukoencephalopathy (<strong>white matter lesions</strong>), resulting from cerebral small-vessel disease or a variable number of infarcts in large-vessel territories. </p>\n<p>Note: Subcortical or small vessel dementia, can sometimes have an insidious onset. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"V5XSYTdy","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"9_upper_gi_radiology_and_pneumoperitoneum","title":"9.Upper Gi Radiology And Pneumoperitoneum","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA0568","text":"Barium swallow of a patien​t presenting with dysphag‌ia is given below. What i‍s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"Barium swallow of a patient presenting with dysphagia is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffuse esop​hageal spasm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Feline o​esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal ​diverticula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Achalasi​a cardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diffuse esophageal spasm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ccdf6c6490bd4c32b7f1285b29e8532a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diffuse esophageal spasm</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, a patient presenting with dysphagia and a <strong>corkscrew appearance</strong> of the esophagus on barium swallow is suggestive of <strong>diffuse esophageal spasm.</strong></p>\n<p>Diffuse esophageal spasm (DES) is a <strong>hypermotility</strong> disorder caused by spontaneous, abnormal contractions seen primarily affecting the <strong>distal two-thirds </strong>of the esophageal body, with normal deglutitive lower esophageal relaxation (LES) relaxation. These contractions are repetitive, simultaneous, and of high amplitude, but normal peristalsis is preserved between the episodes. This condition is seen most often in females.</p>\n<p>Patients typically present with <strong>dysphagia</strong> for both solids and liquids, retrosternal non-cardiac chest pain, and symptoms such as heartburn, regurgitation and the sensation of food being stuck in the throat.</p>\n<p>Although DES may present as a <strong>corkscrew</strong> or <strong>rosary bead</strong> esophagus on barium swallow, this finding is uncommon. <strong>Manometry</strong> remains the gold-standard for diagnosis.<br/> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0569","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ts with difficulty in swa‌llowing both solids and l‍iquids. A barium swallow ‌revealed the following fi​nding. What is the likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with difficulty in swallowing both solids and liquids. A barium swallow revealed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achalasi​a cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse esop​hageal spasm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zenkers di​verticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Achalasia cardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9176ee2c1cb6481cbe6065bd9162e3df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Achalasia cardia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, where a middle-aged woman presents with dysphagia to both solids and liquids and a barium swallow showing a <strong>bird beak appearance</strong>, is suggestive of <strong>achalasia cardia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Achalasia cardia is a primary esophageal <strong>motility disorder</strong> characterized by the absence of esophageal peristalsis and <strong>impaired relaxation</strong> of the lower esophageal sphincter (<strong>LES</strong>) in response to swallowing.</p>\n<p>On barium swallow, the hallmark bird beak or beak-like appearance is observed, resulting from esophageal dilatation above the constricted LES. However, <strong>esophageal manometry </strong>is the gold standard diagnostic test. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0570","text":"A 56-year-old​ female pati​ent presented with progre‌ssive dysphagia for solid‍s, loss of weight, and lo‌ss of appetite. A barium ​swallow done is given bel​ow. What is the most prob‌able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old​ female patient presented with progressive dysphagia for solids, loss of weight, and loss of appetite. A barium swallow done is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoma​ esophagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse esop​hageal spasm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal​ leiomyoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophag​eal web","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carcinoma esophagus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c0ba9fa1e414495a616befd2f38b83d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carcinoma esophagus</strong></p><p>A patient with the given history and barium swallow findings of <strong>irregular narrowing</strong>, <strong>mucosal destruction</strong>, and <strong>shouldered margins</strong> of esophagus, are suggestive of <strong>carcinoma esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Esophageal carcinoma is more common in men, with tobacco use and alcohol being the strongest risk factors. The squamous cell carcinoma variant usually affects the <strong>upper 2/3rd</strong>, and the adenocarcinoma variant usually affects the <strong>lower 1/3rd</strong> of the esophagus. Clinical features include <strong>progressive dysphagia</strong> (most common), regurgitation of food, vomiting, odynophagia and weight loss.</p>\n<p>On barium swallow, carcinoma esophagus presents with irregular narrowing and ulceration of esophagus. <strong>Flexible endoscopy</strong> with <strong>biopsy</strong> is preferred for confirmation of diagnosis. <strong>Contrast-enhanced CT and PET</strong> can be used to assess distant metastasis. <strong>Endoscopic ultrasonography (EUS)</strong> is the investigation of choice to assess tumor invasion and lymph node metastasis.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B:  Diffuse esophageal spasm results in the <strong>corkscrew</strong> appearance of the esophagus on barium study.</p>\n<p>Option C: Esophageal leiomyoma appears as a smooth, <strong>semilunar filling defect</strong> on barium swallow. it is seen as a discrete ovoid mass that is well outlined by barium. </p>\n<p>Option D: Esophageal webs occur in the upper and middle esophagus as a thin membrane that can be identified by the <strong>translucent</strong> part on contrast radiology. </p>\n<p><br/> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0571","text":"A young woman presented to​ the OPD with dysphagia a‌nd regurgitation of food.‍ She was diagnosed with i‌ron deficiency anaemia 3 ​months back. Barium swall​ow was done and is shown ‌below. What is the most p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young woman presented to the OPD with dysphagia and regurgitation of food. She was diagnosed with iron deficiency anaemia 3 months back. Barium swallow was done and is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophageal​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zenker's d​iverticulum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal​ leiomyoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plummer-Vin​son syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Plummer-Vinson syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21a398438ff54f97bc7de7dd628ebb7e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35d730273c49480ca36eee41f3997d88x592x571.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the barium swallow showing <strong>esophageal web</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plummer-Vinson syndrome is also known as <strong>Paterson-Brown-Kelly</strong> syndrome. It commonly affects middle-aged women with long-standing anaemia, and is a recognized risk factor for post-cricoid <strong>hypopharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma</strong>. It consists of dysphagia, iron-deficiency anemia and esophageal webs or hypopharyngeal webs. </p>\n<p>Esophageal webs are due to a constriction caused by a thin mucosal membrane projecting into the lumen. More commonly, they are seen in the <strong>cervical esophagus</strong> near the cricopharyngeus muscle. They typically arise from the <strong>anterior wall</strong>, but never from the posterior wall. They may also be circumferential.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts a barium swallow showing an upper esophageal concentric web in a lateral view. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3997","text":"What dye is used for the d​iagnosis of esophageal pe‌rforation?","raw_text":"What dye is used for the diagnosis of esophageal perforation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastro​graffin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ioh​exol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gadol​inium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Barium ​sulphate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iohexol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4643e814ca784c89915f369848f551d3x600x420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iohexol</strong></p><p><strong>Iohexol </strong>is the contrast medium used for the diagnostic work-up for a suspected <strong>esophageal perforation.</strong> </p>\n<p>Although<strong> gastrograffin</strong> (water-soluble) was used previously, it has a high chance of causing aspiration pneumonitis and pulmonary edema consequently. Hence it is not preferred nowadays. (Option A)</p>\n<p>Barium contrast is not used in esophageal perforation as it causes barium-related inflammation of the mediastinum. (Option D)</p>\n<table style=\"height: 173px;\" width=\"420\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\"><strong>Contraindications to the use of barium contrast</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\"><strong>Alternative dyes used</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Tracheoesophageal fistula</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Dianosil- water-soluble, non-ionic agent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Esophageal perforation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Iohexol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Perforation peritonitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">Gastrograffin / Iohexol</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Given below is a plain chest radiography with a water-soluble contrast swallow, showing contrast leakage in a patient with spontaneous rupture of the esophagus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0583","text":"The mucosal ring at the sq​uamocolumnar junction of ‌the distal esophagus is _‍_____.","raw_text":"The mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction of the distal esophagus is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B r​ing","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A r​ing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophag​eal web","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal​ stricture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. B ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e29b0f8630e452db28638a7cb39cb7dx1200x1362.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0de0571843de435e96dcc55003f95bd8x433x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23dc40893e4a4e248b21aa84b9fd38adx720x1339.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5305a5a5dbe4a30acd8fedb7b0d9b8cx720x949.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. B ring</strong></p><p>The mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction of the distal esophagus is a <strong>B-ring</strong>. </p>\n<p>Distal esophageal rings are frequent findings on barium contrast X-ray studies. They are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B-ring</strong> - It occurs at the <strong>squamocolumnar junction</strong> or <strong>gastroesophageal junction</strong>. It is lined with squamous epithelium above and columnar epithelium below.</li>\n<li><strong>A-ring</strong> -<strong> </strong>It occurs above the B-ring, i.e., a few centimetres proximal to the gastroesophageal junction. It represents a <strong>physiological contraction </strong>of esophageal smooth muscle covered by mucosa. A-rings are usually asymptomatic (option B).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Schatzki ring</strong>, also called Schatzki-Gary ring, is a <strong>symptomatic</strong> <strong>narrow</strong><strong> B-ring</strong> occurring in the distal esophagus. It is usually associated with a <strong>hiatus hernia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image is a barium study showing the Schatzki ring located at the gastroesophageal junction, suggestive of hiatus hernia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option C: Esophageal webs are most common in the <strong>upper oesophagus</strong> (post-cricoid area), where they are generally <strong>semi-circumferential</strong>, eccentric lesions that protrude less than 5 mm and have a thickness of 2 to 4 mm. Microscopically, webs are composed of fibrovascular connective tissue and overlying epithelium.</p>\n<p>The barium swallow below shows the oesophagal web in a patient with <strong>Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong>. Oesophagal webs are <strong>thin membranes</strong> of normal oesophagal tissue consisting of mucosa and submucosa. The translucent part denotes the oesophagal web.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Esophageal stricture refers to any <strong>persistent intrinsic narrowing</strong> of the esophagus. The causes are <strong>peptic stricture</strong> (esophagitis induced), <strong>corrosives</strong> (most common cause), foreign body, post-surgical, radiotherapy, congenital, tuberculosis, and drugs like tetracycline. </p>\n<p>Radiographic features of <strong>benign</strong> strictures show smooth tapering with typical concentric narrowing (may affect only one side of the esophagus) while <strong>malignant</strong> strictures are characteristically abrupt, asymmetric, and eccentric with irregular nodular mucosa. The barium study image below shows esophageal stricture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0584","text":"An upper GI series of a pa​tient being evaluated for‌ difficulty in swallowing‍ is given below. It is su‌ggestive of which of the ​following findings?","raw_text":"An upper GI series of a patient being evaluated for difficulty in swallowing is given below. It is suggestive of which of the following findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tracheoesoph​ageal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schatz​ki ring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zenker’s d​iverticulum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Killian–Jamieson diverticu​lum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zenker’s diverticulum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/acd74c24ea2f4feb8c8cd0d932c1de5d.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zenker’s diverticulum</strong></p><p>The given upper GI series shows the collection of contrast in a posterior pharyngeal pouch-like structure, which favors the diagnosis of <strong>Zenker's diverticulum.</strong></p>\n<p>A barium swallow study may show a diverticulum arising from the midline of the<strong> posterior wall</strong> of the distal <strong>pharynx</strong> near the pharyngoesophageal junction. The diverticulum is typically noted at the C5-6 level. The outpouching may be transient, sometimes referred to as a <strong>pharyngeal pouch</strong>. The pouch is best identified during swallowing and is best visualized on the lateral view. </p>\n<p>Since a majority of patients with a Zenker's diverticulum have a <strong>hiatal hernia</strong> and<strong> gastro-oesophageal reflux</strong>, the distal esophagus should also be evaluated.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Killian–Jamieson</strong> diverticulum is an outpouching from the <strong>lateral</strong> wall of the proximal cervical esophagus. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4003","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is the given X‌-ray finding seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is the given X-ray finding seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophagea​l atresia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophagea​l stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reflux es​ophagitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Barrett's​ esophagus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reflux esophagitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01b23632376a4b5aaa616d63a438657e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reflux esophagitis</strong></p><p>The given image is a barium swallow X-ray showing a <strong>feline esophagus</strong> suggestive of <strong>reflux esophagitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> feline esophagus</strong> is also known as <strong>esophageal shiver</strong>. These folds are 1-2 mm thick and run <strong>horizontally</strong> around the entire circumference of the esophageal lumen. It is thought to be due to the contraction of the muscularis mucosae of the esophagus. This results in the shortening of the esophagus and the bunching up of the mucosa in the lumen. </p>\n<p>The appearance is almost always associated with active <strong>gastroesophageal reflux</strong>. The findings are <strong>transient</strong>, seen <strong>following reflux</strong> and not during swallowing. The appearance is confined to the <strong>distal two-thirds</strong> of the thoracic esophagus. </p>\n<p>It can also be seen in <strong>hiatus hernia</strong> and <strong>eosinophilic esophagitis</strong>. Here, the folds are <strong>permanent</strong> and associated with <strong>strictures</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0586","text":"Which of the following inv​estigations is shown in t‌he radiograph?","raw_text":"Which of the following investigations is shown in the radiograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barium ​swallow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bariu​m meal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barium meal follow through​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Barium​ enema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Barium meal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/968c42d9a13a44519a5bf89b53a6bb88.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f6e2f41aa2e4522bb3b8ed6ba5d3053x387x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Barium meal</strong></p><p>The given image is a double-contrast radiograph of a <strong>barium meal</strong>. It is used to examine the lower half of the esophagus, stomach, and entire duodenum. It is used in identifying ulcers, small fleshy lumps (polyps), tumours.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 209px;\" width=\"630\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 43.5312px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 43.5312px;\"><strong>GI Imaging</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 43.5312px;\"><strong>Preparations of barium</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 43.5312px;\"><strong>Structures best visualized</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Barium swallow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Barium paste</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Esophagus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Barium meal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">95% BaSO4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Stomach and duodenum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Barium meal follow-through</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">50% BaSO4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Small bowel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Barium enema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">25% BaSO4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.672px; height: 29px;\">Large bowel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong> Barium meal follow-through</strong> is useful in visualising small bowel pathology and evaluating the function of the <strong>small</strong> <strong>bowel</strong>. It is performed following a barium meal examination where X-rays are taken 10-15 minutes after the intake of barium. This allows time for the barium to reach the small intestine. It is inferior to enteroclysis enema, where contrast is administered through a nasogastric tube. </p>\n<p>The below radiograph is a <strong>double-contrast barium enema</strong> with a normal <strong>large bowel</strong> structure. The contrast is administered through a rectal tube and a series of radiographs are obtained. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0588","text":"Given below is a barium me​al study of a child. What‌ is the probable diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"Given below is a barium meal study of a child. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodenal​ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital hypertrophic py​loric stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric ​volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Annular ​pancreas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3b3df2a12a594060805eb1692e0b57bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7300441f56f043c3ab2f44eaf6f7d6cfx1280x1316.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49a16946311b4181940af71447f438ccx1280x1282.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a66aaf0cf744ede802dda0b46924d99x1279x1328.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong></p><p>The given barium meal study is showing <strong>string sign </strong>and <strong>mushroom sign </strong>is<strong> </strong>suggestive of<strong> congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong> (CHPS). </p>\n<p>Congenital (infantile) hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is a condition caused by hypertrophy of the circular pyloric muscles of the stomach, leading to <strong>gastric outlet obstruction</strong>. It typically presents between 2 to 6 weeks of age with progressive, forceful, <strong>non-bilious vomiting</strong> immediately after feeding. This results in <strong>hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis</strong> due to the loss of fluid, hydrogen and chloride ions. </p>\n<p>On examination, a firm, movable, <strong>olive-shaped mass</strong> is palpable in the epigastrium., often accompanied by <strong>visible gastric peristalsis</strong>. <strong>Ultrasound </strong>is the investigation of choice for this condition. </p>\n<p>Given below is the barium meal study showing string sign and mushroom sign: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 421px;\" width=\"435\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\"><strong>Signs seen in CHPS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\"><strong> Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">String sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">Passing of a small streak of barium through the pyloric canal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">Double/triple track sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">Crowding of muscle folds in the pyloric canal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">Diamond sign/twining recess</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">A transient triangular tent-like cleft in the mid-portion of the pyloric canal with the apex pointing inferiorly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">Beak sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">The mass impression upon the antrum with a streak of barium pointing towards the pyloric channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">Mushroom sign/kirkin sign/umbrella sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">Indentation of the base of the duodenal bulb by pylorus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">Caterpillar sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">Gastric hyperperistaltic waves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.375px;\">Shoulder sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 291.625px;\">Round indentation caused by hypertrophied pyloric muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The ultrasonographic findings include the <strong>cervix sign</strong>, which represents hypertrophied pylorus that resembles the uterine cervix and the <strong>antral nipple sign</strong>, representing the redundant pyloric mucosa protruding into the antrum. These findings are as shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0592","text":"A middle-aged smoker prese​nted with burning epigast‌ric pain. A barium meal w‍as done and showed the fo‌llowing finding. What is ​the probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged smoker presented with burning epigastric pain. A barium meal was done and showed the following finding. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign gas​tric ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant g​astric ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benign duo​denal ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant du​odenal ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benign gastric ulcer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bbe524b699554893acbec328d671f543.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a86c2443677448f934f5f1bbbebf259x405x441.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benign gastric ulcer</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the barium study showing the <strong>Hampton line</strong> is suggestive of a<strong> benign gastric ulcer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Hampton</strong> <strong>line </strong>is a thin millimetric radiolucent line seen at the neck of a gastric ulcer in barium studies, indicating its benign nature. It is caused by a thin line of mucosa overhanging the ulcer's crater. </p>\n<p>The below image shows a single-contrast upper GI barium study showing a <strong>typical benign gastric ulcer</strong>. It shows a central crater surrounded by smooth, symmetric folds resulting in a<strong> spoke-wheel pattern</strong>. A surrounding mound of edema may be seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The <strong>Carman meniscus sign </strong>is an indication of <strong>carcinomatous gastric ulcer. </strong>It is seen in the setting of a large, flat <strong>malignant</strong> ulcer with healed-up edges. These edges trap the barium which gives a <strong>convex</strong> formation towards the lumen of the stomach. In <strong>benign</strong> ulcer, it forms a <strong>concave</strong> shape towards the lumen of the stomach.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Kirklin complex</strong> is a combination of the <strong>Carman meniscus sign</strong> associated with a radiolucent semi-circular zone surrounding the elevated ridge of the ulcer. This complex is seen in cases of <strong>gastric adenocarcinoma</strong> in barium studies.</p>\n<p>Differences between benign and malignant gastric ulcers:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 380px; height: 293px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.203px;\"><strong>Barium swallow findings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7969px;\"><strong>Benign</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\"><strong>Malignant</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.203px;\">Hampton’s line</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7969px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.203px;\">Extends beyond the gastric wall</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7969px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.203px;\">Folds</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7969px;\">Smooth, even</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Irregular, nodular, may fuse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.203px;\">Carman meniscus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7969px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.203px;\">Ulcer shape</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7969px;\">Round, oval, linear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Irregular</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: A<strong> tri-foliate appearance</strong> is seen in chronic <strong>duodenal ulcers</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0593","text":"Which of the following is ​used to assess the depth ‌of gastric carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to assess the depth of gastric carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal ​ultrasound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bariu​m meal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoluminal​ ultrasound","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lapro​scopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endoluminal ultrasound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endoluminal ultrasound</strong></p><p>The depth of the gastric carcinoma is assessed by<strong> endoluminal ultrasound. </strong></p>\n<p>In endoluminal ultrasonography, the transducer is usually attached to the tip of the instrument distally, and five layers of the gastric wall may be identified, helping in assessing the <strong>depth of invasion </strong>(T component of the staging) of cancer with more than 90% accuracy.</p>\n<p>Pre-operative staging is done with contrast-enhanced computed tomography (<strong>CECT</strong>) of the abdominal/pelvis. The most important prognostic indicators in gastric cancer are - lymph node involvement and depth of tumor invasion. Endoluminal ultrasound and laparoscopic ultrasound are the most sensitive methods in pre-operative local staging of gastric cancer. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Standard <strong>abdominal ultrasound</strong> can be used to investigate the stomach, but it is less sensitive than other modalities in gastric cancer. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Barium meal</strong> radiology is now used less frequently due to advances in more sensitive diagnostic tools like endoscopy and CT imaging with oral contrast, replacing contrast radiology in investigating gastric malignancies. However, CT remains less accurate in the T-staging of gastric cancer compared to endoluminal ultrasound. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Laparoscopy</strong> is routine in the assessment of gastric cancer. However, the main limitation is in the evaluation of posterior extension, where CT and endoluminal ultrasound can provide this information. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0597","text":"An abdominal x-ray of a ne​onate admitted to NICU is‌ given below. What is the‍ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"An abdominal x-ray of a neonate admitted to NICU is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital hypertrophic py​loric stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duodenal​ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Toxic m​egacolon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid ​volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duodenal atresia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88581a18e8414da4b4d02351a4a43b43.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7af9655c16b64a8e94533e9f3c31bb93x720x452.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duodenal atresia</strong></p><p>The given abdominal x-ray of the neonate shows the <strong>double bubble sign</strong>, which is suggestive of<strong> duodenal atresia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Duodenal atresia is the blockade of the duodenum due to the failure of recanalisation during embryonic development from its solid cord stage, leaving the duodenum partially or completely obstructed. It is often associated with <strong>trisomy 21/Down syndrome</strong>, prematurity, maternal polyhydramnios, cardiac malformations, and malformations of the hepatobiliary system.</p>\n<p>The range of anatomic variants includes duodenal stenosis, mucosal web with intact muscle wall (so-called <strong>windsock deformity</strong>), two ends separated by a fibrous cord, and complete separation with a gap within the duodenum. </p>\n<p>On abdominal X-ray, the <strong>double bubble sign</strong> represents the dilatation of the stomach and proximal duodenum. This can often be detected prenatally via ultrasound but, if missed, neonates typically present with <strong>bilious vomiting</strong> shortly after birth, as the obstruction is usually distal to the ampulla of Vater. </p>\n<p>Causes of double bubble sign are congenital obstruction - duodenal atresia (associated with Down syndrome), midgut volvulus, and external compression of the duodenum.</p>\n<p>The radiological finding obtained on the barium study is a windsock deformity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7029","text":"From the X-ray given, pick​ out the probable diagnos‌is:","raw_text":"From the X-ray given, pick out the probable diagnosis:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subdiaphragm​matic abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo​thorax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumope​ritoneum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intestinal ​obstruction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pneumoperitoneum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f822b51303b3482e83c77aba66e12b80.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pneumoperitoneum</strong></p><p>The above X-ray clearly shows the presence of<strong> subdiaphragmatic free gas</strong> in the right upper quadrant. This indicates <strong>pneumoperitoneum.</strong></p>\n<p>Pneumoperitoneum is the presence of free gas within the peritoneal cavity but outside the viscera. The most common cause of pneumoperitoneum is <strong>bowel perforation. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric and posterior duodenal perforations are seen in long-standing cases of <strong>peptic ulcers.</strong></li>\n<li>Intestinal perforations are seen with ischemia, inflammation, diverticula, or obstruction.</li>\n<li>Other causes also include iatrogenic, penetrating injuries, prolonged mechanical ventilation, peritoneal dialysis, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Signs of pneumoperitoneum</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bowel-related signs\n<ul>\n<li>Double wall sign/<strong>Rigler's sign</strong>/gas-relief sign- Air is seen on both sides of the bowel wall.</li>\n<li>Telltale triangle sign/triangle sign - Triangle of gas formed between three loops of the bowel or between the abdominal wall and two loops of the bowel.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Peritoneal ligament-related signs\n<ul>\n<li>Football sign- Air outlines the confines of the peritoneal cavity</li>\n<li>Falciform ligament sign-  Air outlines the falciform ligament</li>\n<li>Lateral umbilical ligament sign/<strong>inverted \"V\"</strong> sign- Air outlines the lateral umbilical ligament</li>\n<li>Urachus sign - Air outlines the urachus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Right upper quadrant signs\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cupola sign</strong>- Air outlines the median subphrenic space</li>\n<li>Fissure for ligamentum teres sign- Air outlines the ligamentum teres</li>\n<li>Hepatic edge sign- Cigar-shaped free-air pocket in the subhepatic region. It tracks superomedial following the contours of the liver</li>\n<li>Lucent liver sign - Reduction of hepatic radiodensity due to accumulation of free gas anterior to the liver</li>\n<li>Morison's pouch sign / <strong>Doge cap sign</strong> - Triangular/ crescentic gas lucency bound by the 11th rib</li>\n<li>Periportal free gas sign.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0669","text":"The radiograph of a patien​t suspected to have pneum‌operitoneum is shown belo‍w. Which of the following‌ signs is seen here?","raw_text":"The radiograph of a patient suspected to have pneumoperitoneum is shown below. Which of the following signs is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cupol​a sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Falciform l​igament sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Footba​ll sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rigler​'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cupola sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e44025ef1564105b14b629ff9dd22f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75c7202b29e64996b2254ded98eefdd7x469x630.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e481704b5d34383adc0bdb719d2593fx1024x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/344fe403da1e4e21a4ffaef5b6162f90x329x440.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd880f084683417a869b5b7371356368x720x717.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cupola sign</strong></p><p>The given radiograph in a patient with pneumoperitoneum depicts <strong>Cupola</strong> sign.</p>\n<p>Cupola sign refers to dependent <strong>air</strong> that rises within the abdominal cavity of the supine patient to accumulate underneath the <strong>central tendon</strong> of the <strong>diaphragm</strong> in the midline. It is seen as <strong>lucency overlying</strong> the<strong> lower thoracic vertebral bodies</strong>. The superior border is well-defined, but the inferior margin is not.</p>\n<p>In the below radiograph, the arrows are pointing to the Cupola sign seen in the pneumoperitoneum. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Signs of pneumoperitoneum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bowel-related signs\n<ul>\n<li>Double-wall sign/<strong>Rigler's sign</strong>/<strong>g</strong><strong>as-relief</strong> sign </li>\n<li>Telltale triangle sign/triangle sign - A triangle of gas formed between three loops of the bowel or between the abdominal wall and two loops of the bowel.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Peritoneal ligament-related signs\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Football sign</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Falciform ligament sign</strong></li>\n<li>Lateral umbilical ligament sign/inverted \"V\" sign - Air outlines the lateral umbilical ligament</li>\n<li>Urachus sign - Air outlines the urachus.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Right upper quadrant signs\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cupola sign</strong></li>\n<li>Fissure for ligamentum teres sign- Air outlines the ligamentum teres</li>\n<li>Hepatic edge sign - Cigar-shaped free-air pocket in the subhepatic region. It tracks superomedial following the contours of the liver</li>\n<li>Lucent liver sign - Reduction of hepatic radiodensity due to accumulation of free gas anterior to the liver</li>\n<li>Morison's pouch sign/Doge cap sign - Triangular/ crescentic gas lucency bound by the 11th rib</li>\n<li>Periportal free gas sign.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option C: The below image is a supine abdominal radiograph of a neonate with a large pneumoperitoneum. It shows an oval lucency divided by the falciform ligament resulting in the so-called <strong>football sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: The below image is a supine abdominal radiograph is showing the presence of air in the bilateral subphrenic spaces and a linear density on the ventral surface of the liver (arrow). This is known as the <strong>falciform ligament sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: The image given below is an abdominal radiograph showing <strong>Rigler's</strong> <strong>sign</strong> seen in pneumoperitoneum. Here both the inner and the outer walls of the bowel are visible.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9589","text":"All of the following could​ be the cause of the foll‌owing X-ray finding excep‍t:","raw_text":"All of the following could be the cause of the following X-ray finding except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodenal ulcer perforation​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post lap​aroscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chilaidit​i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute intestinal obstructi​on","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute intestinal obstruction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d0051f7571254549a26c1ec26ba04312.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad752d9e3f1e46c286cfb0b0538dc1bex508x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute intestinal obstruction</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>air under the diaphragm</strong>, indicative of <strong>pneumoperitoneum</strong>, which is <strong>not</strong> caused by <strong>acute intestinal obstruction.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of pneumoperitoneum include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bowel perforation: Gastric and posterior duodenal perforations in long-standing cases of peptic ulcers., ischemic bowel, necrotizing enterocolitis</li>\n<li>Small bowel perforation secondary to trauma or penetrating injuries</li>\n<li>Post-surgery/laparoscopy</li>\n<li>Causes other than perforation are:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chilaiditi syndrome</strong> - the intestine is seen between the liver and diaphragm giving a false impression of free air</li>\n<li><strong>Sub-phrenic abscess</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Postoperatively,</strong> air may be present in the abdomen for up to 7 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>An intestinal obstruction would show <strong>multiple dilated loops</strong> on X-ray with possible<strong> air-fluid levels</strong>. Only when the obstruction becomes severe and there is a perforation, pneumoperitoneum occurs.</p>\n<p>The image given below is an abdominal radiograph showing multiple dilated loops with air-fluid levels suggestive of intestinal obstruction.</p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0672","text":"Given below is an X-ray of​ a patient. What is the p‌robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Given below is an X-ray of a patient. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumope​ritoneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumome​diastinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudopneum​operitoneum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudopneum​omediastinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudopneumoperitoneum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf2445212ee84292851ca3355ba499d5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1eb94e5aa9794d82b391bb59ec3815c1x1100x900.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b873798d05f4ec2a95d763aa06ffc48x629x596.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f95d711477b40a78c8b90a36fa044c2x1279x1410.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ceb9fee1f3f4108989503a04eb97167x1280x1093.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81476a2fb96645928ea52c43d1531f87x510x500.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f5915a981de46109d2ad93a25b1c18ax442x301.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudopneumoperitoneum</strong></p><p>The given chest X-ray image depicts <strong>Chilaiditi sign, </strong>characterized by the anterior interposition of the colon between the liver and the right hemidiaphragm, which can <strong>mimic air under the diaphragm,</strong> leading to <strong>pseudo-pneumoperitoneum.</strong></p>\n<p>Chilaiditi sign is typically an <strong>incidental</strong> finding on imaging and is most often asymptomatic. However, when associated with symptoms such as abdominal pain and distension, it is referred to as <strong>Chilaiditi syndrome.</strong> </p>\n<p>An erect X-ray showing this sign may mislead clinicians into suspecting pneumoperitoneum, prompting unnecessary urgent surgical intervention, which is not required. To differentiate Chilaiditi syndrome from true pneumoperitoneum, a <strong>contrast-enhanced CT </strong>or a<strong> left lateral decubitus radiograph</strong> can be performed. In the left lateral decubitus view, distended colonic loops with <strong>haustral markings</strong> will be visible, showing that the air is within the bowel and not in the peritoneal cavity. </p>\n<p>Other conditions with the Chilaiditi sign are curvilinear pulmonary collapse, distended viscus, omental fat, and apposition of gas-distended bowel loops mimicking the double-wall sign.</p>\n<p>Below is the chest X-ray and CT showing the presence of herniated bowel loops under the diaphragm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis shows free intraperitoneal gas which is suggestive of<strong> pneumoperitoneum</strong>. The presence of free intra-abdominal gas almost always indicates perforation of a hollow viscus. Free intraperitoneal gas is also detectable under the hemidiaphragm on an erect chest X-ray as given below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: The existence of extraluminal gas within the mediastinum is referred to as <strong>pneumomediastinum.</strong> Gas may originate from the peritoneal cavity, lungs, trachea, central bronchi, oesophagus, or peritoneal cavity and travel to the neck or abdomen via the mediastinum.  <strong>Spinnaker sign</strong> (also called angel wing sign) is a radiographic sign seen in neonates with pneumomediastinum. The pressure from the collected gas displaces the two lobes of the thymic tissue upward and laterally from its normal position. This results in large, wedge-shaped opacities on radiograph resembling the spinnaker headsails of a boat.  Other signs of pneumomediastinum are<strong> </strong>the<strong> V sign of Naclerio</strong> and<strong> continuous diaphragm</strong> sign. </p>\n<p>Below is a chest X-ray showing spinnaker sign and CT showing the presence of pneumomediastinum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0671","text":"Which of the following is ​the best way to detect mi‌nimum pneumoperitoneum on‍ X-ray in a patient with ‌abdominal trauma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best way to detect minimum pneumoperitoneum on X-ray in a patient with abdominal trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supine ch​est X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erect X-ray​ of abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right lateral decubitus ch​est X-ray with horizontal‌ beam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left lateral decubitus abd​omen X-ray with horizonta‌l beam","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left lateral decubitus abdomen X-ray with horizontal beam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left lateral decubitus abdomen X-ray with horizontal beam</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>left lateral decubitus </strong>X-ray of the abdomen is the best view to visualize the <strong>pneumoperitoneum</strong>.</p>\n<p>As little as <strong>1 mL</strong> of free gas can be detected on a plain radiograph. Hence the best views to visualize pneumoperitoneum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Erect chest X-ray</strong> - the first, most important and useful view showing air beneath the diaphragm. </li>\n<li>Left lateral decubitus abdominal image</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CT abdomen </strong>is the most sensitive imaging technique to detect minimal pneumoperitoneum. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0686","text":"What is the correct sequen​ce of visualizing regions‌ while performing FAST in‍ a patient suspected to h‌ave abdominal trauma? Sub​xiphoid Left upper quadra​nt Right upper quadrant S‌uprapubic","raw_text":"<p>What is the correct sequence of visualizing regions while performing FAST in a patient suspected to have abdominal trauma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subxiphoid</li>\n<li>Left upper quadrant</li>\n<li>Right upper quadrant</li>\n<li>Suprapubic</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-3​-2-4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2-3​-1-4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3-2​-4-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-2​-3-4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-3-2-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b18baa92b14849fd98f7c2b7a473f0f8x1280x1322.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-3-2-4</strong></p><p>The correct sequence to visualize regions in FAST is <strong>subxiphoid</strong> → <strong>right upper quadrant</strong> → <strong>left upper quadrant </strong>→ <strong>suprapubic</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Focussed assessment with sonography for trauma </strong>(FAST) is a non-invasive investigation that can be performed rapidly in the emergency room. It helps identify an abnormal collection of fluid or blood.</p>\n<p>FAST is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Haemodynamically unstable patients who had a blunt abdominal trauma</li>\n<li>Patients who sustained penetrating abdominal trauma with no other indications for immediate laparotomy (signs of hemodynamic instability, peritoneal irritation, peritoneal penetration, and gunshot wound with transperitoneal trajectory).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Technique:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is placed in a supine position.</li>\n<li>With a 3.5-5.0 MHz convex transducer, the following regions are scanned:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subxiphoid</strong> transverse view is used to assess pericardial effusion and left lobe liver injuries.</li>\n<li>A longitudinal view of the <strong>right upper quadrant </strong>is used to assess right liver injuries, right kidney injury, and Morison's pouch.</li>\n<li>A longitudinal view of the <strong>left</strong> <strong>upper quadrant</strong> is used to assess splenic injury and left kidney injury.</li>\n<li>Transverse and longitudinal views of the <strong>suprapubic</strong> region are used to assess Douglas's bladder and pouch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An \"<strong>extended FAST</strong> or eFAST\" scan: Assess left and right thoracic views, in addition to the FAST assessed regions, to look for pneumothorax and hemothorax.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6jV3I0qF","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"9_investigations_in_breast_diseases","title":"9.Investigations In Breast Diseases","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB0347","text":"What is the recommended ag‌e for starting screening ‌mammography?","raw_text":"What is the recommended age for starting screening mammography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 y‌ears","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"45 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"55 y‌ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 40 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 40 years</strong></p><p>The <strong>recommended age</strong> for women to start annual screening mammography is <strong>40 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Until 44 years of age</strong>, it is her<strong> individual choice</strong> to decide on whether she wants screening mammography or not. </span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">For ages<strong> between 45 and 49 years,</strong> it is <strong>mandatory</strong> to get <span style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\"><strong>annua</strong><strong>l</strong></span><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\"> breast</span> <span style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\">screening done. </span></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\">After the age of <strong>50 years, </strong>it is advised that women get a <strong>second yearly</strong> screening done.</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0346","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding screening‌ mammography?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding screening mammography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensitivity of the test wi‌ll increase with her age.‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is best performed in th‌e 2nd half of her menstru‌al cycle.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mediolateral oblique view ‌will show the greatest vo‌lume of her breast tissue​.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Craniocaudal view will pro‌vide better visualization‌ of the medial aspect of ​her breast.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is best performed in the 2nd half of her menstrual cycle.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is best performed in the 2nd half of her menstrual cycle.</strong></p><p>Mammography is best performed in the <strong>1st half of the menstrual cycle</strong>, as breast tissue is<strong> less dense</strong> in the follicular phase. Also, in the <strong>2nd half of the menstrual cycle</strong>, since ovulation occurs and there is a chance for pregnancy, the <strong>radiation</strong> can potentially <strong>affect the embryo</strong>. </p>\n<p>The<strong> sensitivity</strong> of mammography <strong>increases with age</strong>, as the breast tissue becomes <strong>less dense</strong>. </p>\n<p>It involves 2 standard views: </p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mediolateral oblique (<strong>MLO) </strong>view- covers a <strong>greater volume</strong> of breast tissue, including the upper outer quadrant and the axillary tail of Spence.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Craniocaudal (<strong>CC</strong>) view- provides <strong>better</strong> <strong>visualization of the medial aspect</strong> of the breast.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mammography uses low-voltage, high-amperage X-rays. The radiation dose from mammography is around <strong>0.1 cGy</strong> per study.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0351","text":"A 50-year-old woman underw‌ent screening mammography‌. Which of the following ​findings in her mammogram‌ is least suggestive of a‌ malignancy?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman underwent screening mammography. Which of the following findings in her mammogram is least suggestive of a malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irregul‌ar mass","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wide‌ halo","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macrocalc‌ification","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spiculate‌d margins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Macrocalcification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62a1c0029973463aaf883df317766d1fx1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Macrocalcification</strong></p><p><strong>Macrocalcification</strong> in a lesion is <strong>least</strong> suggestive of a <strong>malignancy.</strong> It is commonly seen in <strong>benign breast disorders</strong>. <strong>Microcalcification</strong> (&lt;0.5 mm in diameter) is suggestive of <strong>malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Table: Mammographic appearance of benign and malignant masses  </p>\n<table style=\"width: 440px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\"><strong>Benign</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\"><strong>Malignant</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Opacity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Low density</li>\n<li>Homogenous</li>\n<li>Smooth margin</li>\n<li>Thin halo</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>High density</li>\n<li>Not homogenous</li>\n<li>Ill-defined margin, irregular stellate spiculated margin, comet tail</li>\n<li>Wide halo</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Calcification</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Macrocalcification (&gt;0.5 mm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Microcalcification (&lt;0.5 mm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Breast parenchyma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Architectural distortion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Nipple/areola</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Retraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Skin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Thickening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Cooper ligaments</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">\n<p>Thickening, increase in number</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Ducts</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Focal dilatation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120.203125px;\"><strong>Subcutaneous and mammary space</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.796875px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">Obliteration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Below is an image showing a malignant breast mass with ill-defined, spiculated margins and microcalcifications.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0352","text":"Which pattern of microcalc‌ification seen in a patie‌nt’s mammography report w​ould indicate the maximum‌ risk of malignancy?","raw_text":"Which pattern of microcalcification seen in a patient’s mammography report would indicate the maximum risk of malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cluster microcalcification‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse microcalcification‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Segmental microcalcificati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Regional microcalcificatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cluster microcalcification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c7c56294ed74b5e90a261c87b177a99x440x252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cluster microcalcification</strong></p><p><strong>Cluster microcalcification</strong> in mammography indicates the <strong>maximum </strong>risk of malignancy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0354","text":"Match the following types ‌of calcifications with th‌e diseases they are assoc​iated with. Type of calci‌fication Disease A. Popco‌rn calcification i) Fibro‍cystic disease B. Lead- p​ipe calcification ii) Duc‍t ectasia C. Broken needl‌e calcification iii) Trau​matic fat necrosis D. Tea‍- cup calcification iv) F‍ibroadenoma","raw_text":"<p>Match the following types of calcifications with the diseases they are associated with.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 469px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248.671875px;\">Type of calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.328125px;\">Disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248.671875px;\">A. Popcorn calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.328125px;\">i) Fibrocystic disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248.671875px;\">B. Lead- pipe calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.328125px;\">ii) Duct ectasia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248.671875px;\">C. Broken needle calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.328125px;\">iii) Traumatic fat necrosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248.671875px;\">D. Tea- cup calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.328125px;\">iv) Fibroadenoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-iii B-i‌i C-i D-iv","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-iv B-ii‌i C-ii D-i","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-i B-iii‌ C-ii D-iv","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-ii B-iv‌ C-iii D-i","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-iv B-iii C-ii D-i","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47a9fc6819e745b5bcebdb85e43f79fex630x520.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6a5446052284594900f5e6b5d44e4fcx172x227.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95f21ef2bb94751b0ebc6e6ae44f1bax841x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7296ae6662c446cb8341bdf47f5057bbx512x559.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-iv B-iii C-ii D-i</strong></p><p><strong>Popcorn calcification </strong>is seen in <strong>fibroadenoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lead-pipe/egg-shell calcification</strong> is seen in <strong>traumatic fat necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Broken-needle type of calcification</strong> is seen in <strong>duct ectasia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tea-cup calcification</strong> is seen in <strong>fibrocystic disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Popcorn calcification:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> <strong>Lead-pipe/egg-shell calcification:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Broken-needle type of calcification:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Tea-cup calcification:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9856","text":"A 58-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of a p‌ainless lump in her left ​breast which she noticed ‌during a routine self-exa‌mination. On palpation, a‍ hard lump with irregular​ margins and enlarged lef‍t axillary lymph nodes we‌re found. Which among the​ following is the best mo‍dality to detect malignan‍cy in her case?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old woman presents with complaints of a painless lump in her left breast which she noticed during a routine self-examination. On palpation, a hard lump with irregular margins and enlarged left axillary lymph nodes were found. Which among the following is the best modality to detect malignancy in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2D mamm‌ography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Digital m‌ammography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tomosynthesis with convent‌ional mammography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Full-field digital mammogr‌aphy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tomosynthesis with conventional mammography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c5f19f8e66e4745ba5b400e629645f9x843x817.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tomosynthesis with conventional mammography</strong></p><p><strong>Tomosynthesis (3D mammography) performed along with conventional 2D mammography</strong> is the best investigation among the given options to detect breast carcinoma. </p>\n<p><strong>Breast Tomosynthesis</strong> is typically performed in conjunction <strong>with conventional mammography</strong>. Hence, the radiation dose is doubled. However, the <strong>accuracy is improved</strong> and false positives are lesser. It can be used for <strong>dense breasts</strong> and those with <strong>high-risk factors</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Screen-film mammography</strong> is the conventional 2D mammography.</p>\n<p><strong>Digital mammography/full-field digital mammography</strong> uses digital technology instead of films. The diagnostic accuracy is similar to that of screen-film mammography. A minor advantage is the greater accuracy in predicting lesions in dense breasts.</p>\n<p>Note: Overall, the <strong>best modality</strong> for detecting breast carcinoma is <strong>MRI.</strong></p>\n<p>The below image shows a comparison of 2D mammogram vs. 3D mammogram:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0355","text":"A mammogram done for a 45-‌year-old female patient w‌ith a breast lump was rep​orted as BIRADS category ‌0. What is it suggestive ‌of?","raw_text":"A mammogram done for a 45-year-old female patient with a breast lump was reported as BIRADS category 0. What is it suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nega‌tive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incom‌plete","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Probabl‌y benign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suspicious ‌abnormality","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incomplete","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incomplete</strong></p><p>Category/score <strong>0</strong> in BIRADS suggests an <strong>incomplete assessment.</strong></p>\n<p>All breast imaging should be reported by BIRADS (breast imaging-reporting and data system).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9858","text":"A 48-year-old woman with a‌ lump in her right breast‌ was advised to undergo m​ammography which was repo‌rted as BIRADS 3. What wi‌ll be the appropriate nex‍t step of management in t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman with a lump in her right breast was advised to undergo mammography which was reported as BIRADS 3. What will be the appropriate next step of management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trucut‌ biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Routine annu‌al screening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRI b‌reast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follow-up every 6 months f‌or 2 years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Follow-up every 6 months for 2 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Follow-up every 6 months for 2 years</strong></p><p>A <strong>BIRADS score of 3</strong> means that it is <strong>probably a benign lesion</strong>. The next appropriate step is to <strong>follow up</strong> with this patient <strong>every 6 months for the next 2 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>During this period, the score can either downgrade to BIRADS 2 or upgrade to BIRADS 4 or 5.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9859","text":"An anxious 45-year-old wom‌an presented with complai‌nts of a lump over her le​ft breast for 2 months. T‌he patient underwent mamm‌ography, and the lump was‍ categorized as BIRADS 4a​. What is the likelihood ‍of her lesion being malig‌nant?","raw_text":"An anxious 45-year-old woman presented with complaints of a lump over her left breast for 2 months. The patient underwent mammography, and the lump was categorized as BIRADS 4a. What is the likelihood of her lesion being malignant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"80-‌95 %","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60-‌80%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11-‌ 50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2-‌10%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2-10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2-10%</strong></p><p>There is a <strong>2-10%</strong> likelihood for a lesion with <strong>BIRADS 4a</strong> to be malignant.</p>\n<p><strong>BIRADS Categories/Grades</strong></p>\n<p>Grades 1 and 2: 0% chance of being malignant.</p>\n<p>Grade 3: &gt;0% but ≤2% chance of being malignant</p>\n<p>Grade 4:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>4a: &gt;2 to ≤10% chance of being malignant</li>\n<li>4b: &gt;10 to ≤50% chance of being malignant</li>\n<li>4c: &gt;50 to &lt;95% chance of being malignant</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Grade 5: <span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">≥</span>95% chance of being malignant</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0365","text":"A 36-year-old pregnant lad‌y was referred to the sur‌gery OPD at 12 weeks of g​estation with a lump in h‌er right breast for the p‌ast 1 month. On examinati‍on, a lump of 2 x 1 cm si​ze was palpated in the up‍per outer quadrant of the‌ breast. Which of the fol​lowing investigations wou‍ld you initially advise h‍er for the assessment of ‍the breast lump?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old pregnant lady was referred to the surgery OPD at 12 weeks of gestation with a lump in her right breast for the past 1 month. On examination, a lump of 2 x 1 cm size was palpated in the upper outer quadrant of the breast. Which of the following investigations would you initially advise her for the assessment of the breast lump?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conventional‌ mammography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultraso‌nography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tomosynthesis with 2D mamm‌ography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ultrasonography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ultrasonography</strong></p><p><strong>Ultrasonography (USG</strong>) is the preferred <strong>initial</strong> investigation in <strong>pregnant women</strong> who present with a breast lump. It can differentiate between solid and cystic lesions of the breast and is able to detect abnormal axillary lymph nodes. USG is preferred in women with<strong> dense breast tissue</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pregnant women</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lactating women</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Young women (less than 40 years old) </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conventional Mammography/ Tomosynthesis with 2D mammography</strong> is <strong>less useful</strong> in pregnant women due to the <strong>increased density</strong> of breast tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is <strong>not the initial</strong> investigation used for the assessment of breast lumps. It can be used in cases where metastasis or spread to other breasts is suspected.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9327","text":"A USG can detect lymph nod‌e metastases in a patient‌ with breast cancer and c​linically negative lymph ‌nodes by what percentage?‌","raw_text":"A USG can detect lymph node metastases in a patient with breast cancer and clinically negative lymph nodes by what percentage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 –‌ 60%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10 –‌ 20%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 –‌ 30%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 –‌ 50%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50 – 60%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50 – 60%</strong></p><p>A <strong>USG</strong> can detect lymph node metastasis in a patient with clinically negative lymph nodes by <strong>50-60%</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical determination</strong> of axillary lymph node metastases has an accuracy of only<strong> 33%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ultrasound (<strong>USG</strong>) is <strong>more sensitive</strong> than physical examination alone in determining axillary lymph node involvement during the preliminary staging of breast carcinoma. </p>\n<p><strong>Fine-needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) or core biopsy</strong> of sonographically indeterminate or suspicious lymph nodes can provide a more definitive diagnosis than USG alone.</p>\n<p>Ultrasonography can also be utilized to image the regional lymph nodes in patients with breast cancer. The <strong>sensitivity</strong> for the status of axillary nodes ranges from <strong>35% to 82%</strong> and <strong>specificity</strong> ranges from <strong>73% to 97%</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The features of a lymph node involved with cancer on USG include</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cortical thickening</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Change in shape of the node to a more circular appearance</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Size larger than 10 mm </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absence of a fatty hilum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoechoic internal echoes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0358","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures on USG will not be ‌suggestive of breast mali​gnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following features on USG will not be suggestive of breast malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thin echogenic pseudo-caps‌ule","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypo-echo‌ic lesion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central v‌ascularity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lump will be taller than w‌ider","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thin echogenic pseudo-capsule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thin echogenic pseudo-capsule</strong></p><p><strong>Malignant breast lump</strong> on USG, has an <strong>ill-defined echogenic halo</strong>, is hypoechoic, and the lump will be taller than wider. <strong>Benign breast lumps</strong> have a <strong>thin echogenic pseudo-capsule</strong> on USG.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0361","text":"A young female patient pre‌sents to the clinic with ‌a painless lump in her ri​ght breast. She underwent‌ breast augmentation surg‌ery 3 years back. Which o‍f the following will be t​he best investigation to ‍assess the breast tissue ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"A young female patient presents to the clinic with a painless lump in her right breast. She underwent breast augmentation surgery 3 years back. Which of the following will be the best investigation to assess the breast tissue in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tomosy‌nthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultraso‌nography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magnetic resonance imaging‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Computed ‌tomography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Magnetic resonance imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Magnetic resonance imaging</strong></p><p>Magnetic resonance imaging (<strong>MRI</strong>) is the <strong>best investigation</strong> to assess <strong>breast tissue</strong> in a patient with a <strong>breast implant</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI of the breast is also used in</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Screening of high-risk individuals with a positive family history.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Assessing loco-regional spread (multifocality and multicentricity) of carcinoma.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To differentiate scar from recurrence in women who have had breast conservation surgery for breast cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tomosynthesis</strong> is not preferred, as radio-opaque implants are usually compressed together with the breast tissue, obscuring the visibility and thereby reducing its sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Ultrasonography</strong> is used as an initial investigation in the evaluation of breast abnormalities in patients with breast implants.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Computed tomography</strong> is not routinely used in the evaluation of breast lumps.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0366","text":"A 40-year-old woman with a‌n elder sister with BRCA1‌ gene mutation presented ​to the OPD with a normal ‌mammography report. Which‌ of the following is the ‍next preferred investigat​ion to further evaluate t‍his patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman with an elder sister with BRCA1 gene mutation presented to the OPD with a normal mammography report. Which of the following is the next preferred investigation to further evaluate this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U‌SG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trucut‌ biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repeat mammography after 6‌ months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MRI</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, since the patient is at <strong>high risk for breast malignancy</strong> (first degree relative with BRCA1 gene mutation), <strong>MRI followed by trucut biopsy</strong> will be the appropriate next step for further evaluation.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is helpful in the screening of <strong>high-risk individuals</strong> and is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> imaging modality. However, it should be done after initial radiological evaluation, in case the results are inconclusive.</p>\n<p><strong>American Cancer Society Risk Criteria for Breast Magnetic Resonance Imaging Screening:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Women at high lifetime risk</strong> (≈20%-25% or greater) of breast cancer-</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Known BRCA1 or BRCA2 gene mutation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First-degree relatives with BRCA1 or BRCA2 gene mutation, but have not had genetic testing themselves.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiation therapy to the chest between the ages of 10 and 30 years</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Li-Fraumeni syndrome or Cowden syndrome or a first-degree relative with one of these syndromes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Women with a personal history of breast cancer, ductal carcinoma in situ, lobular carcinoma in situ, atypical ductal hyperplasia, or atypical lobular hyperplasia, are at a<strong> moderately increased lifetime risk</strong> for breast cancer. </p>\n<p>Options A &amp; D: <strong>Breast tomosynthesis or repeat mammography</strong> is not preferred as the next investigation in high-risk patients. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Trucut biopsy</strong> will be done following MRI in high-risk patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9852","text":"A postmenopausal woman who‌ was on HRT for the past ‌2 years, presented with a​ 4 x 3cm lump over the up‌per outer quadrant of the‌ left breast. On examinat‍ion, a peau d’orange appe​arance was seen and a har‍d and fixed lump was palp‌ated over the breast. Whi​ch of the following is th‍e investigation of choice‍ to confirm the diagnosis‍ in this patient?","raw_text":"A postmenopausal woman who was on HRT for the past 2 years, presented with a 4 x 3cm lump over the upper outer quadrant of the left breast. On examination, a peau d’orange appearance was seen and a hard and fixed lump was palpated over the breast.  Which of the following is the investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excision‌al biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trucut‌ biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fine-needle aspiration cyt‌ology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tomosynthesis with 2D mamm‌ography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trucut biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trucut biopsy</strong></p><p>The history and physical examination point towards a diagnosis of <strong>breast malignancy</strong>. The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for <strong>confirmation</strong> of breast carcinoma is <strong>trucut biopsy/core-needle biopsy</strong>. An excisional biopsy can be done, in case it is inconclusive.</p>\n<p><strong>Trucut needle biopsy</strong> permits the analysis of <strong>breast tissue architecture</strong>. A <strong>14 G- 16 G</strong> needle is used. The advantages include a low complication rate, minimal scarring, and a lower cost as compared to excisional breast biopsy. </p>\n<p>Fine-needle aspiration cytology (<strong>FNAC</strong>) is a <strong>cytological</strong> investigation and, hence, not the investigation of choice. A <strong>22-30 G</strong> needle is used for FNAC.</p>\n<p><strong>Trucut biopsy is preferred over FNAC</strong> as it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Allows a definitive preoperative diagnosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Differentiates between DCIS and invasive disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Allows the tumor to be stained for receptor status, which is important for starting neoadjuvant therapy. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image-guided biopsy is used only in the case of non-palpable lesions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0364","text":"As a surgery resident, whi‌ch of the following will ‌you perform in a patient ​with suspected breast mal‌ignancy as part of the tr‌iple assessment? 1. Histo‍ry 2. Clinical examinatio​n 3. Radiological investi‍gations 4. Histopathologi‌cal examination","raw_text":"<p>As a surgery resident, which of the following will you perform in a patient with suspected breast malignancy as part of the triple assessment?</p>\n<p>1. History<br/>2. Clinical examination<br/>3. Radiological investigations<br/>4. Histopathological examination</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2, 3‌ and 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1,2, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1,2, 3 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Triple assessment</strong> in relation to breast carcinoma includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clinical assessment includes both <strong>history and clinical examination</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiological imaging, including ultrasonography (USG) and/or mammography</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Histopathological examination (HPE), including fine-needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) and/or Tru-Cut biopsy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The positive predictive value of the triple assessment is 99.9%.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Bailey &amp; Love 27th Ed mentions that clinical assessment, radiological imaging, and HPE constitute triple assessment. However, <strong>clinical assessment includes both history and clinical examination. </strong>Hence, history is a part of the triple assessment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9874","text":"An actress with bilateral ‌silicone breast implants ‌presents for regular foll​ow-up. On radiological ev‌aluation, she was found t‌o have signs suggestive o‍f intracapsular breast im​plant rupture. Which of t‍he following named signs ‌will you see on imaging? ​Stepladder sign (on USG) ‍Keyhole sign (Mammography‍) Snowstorm sign (on Xray‍) Linguine sign (MRI) Com​et tail sign (CT scan)","raw_text":"<p>An actress with bilateral silicone breast implants presents for regular follow-up. On radiological evaluation, she was found to have signs suggestive of intracapsular breast implant rupture. Which of the following named signs will you see on imaging?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stepladder sign (on USG)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Keyhole sign (Mammography)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Snowstorm sign (on Xray)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Linguine sign (MRI)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Comet tail sign (CT scan)</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3‌ &amp;amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/efbfc0dc10ea4e4b9e81a33773c1f114x512x219.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/533e0a52aa664aa4981be238df9cc447x1024x683.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/077bdfdcb20e41998f4995ff58542e54x512x171.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 &amp; 4</strong></p><p><strong>Linguine sign (on MRI)</strong> and <strong>Stepladder sign (on USG)</strong> are specific for <strong>intracapsular breast implant rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p>After implantation of a silicone or saline breast implant, a fibrous capsule (scar) forms around the implant shell. In an intracapsular rupture, the contents of the implant are contained by the fibrous scar, while the free-floating shell appears as a group of <strong>wavy lines</strong> (like the Italian linguine pasta as shown below). Hence, this has been termed the \"linguine sign\".</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Stepladder sign (seen on USG)</strong> is identified as multiple, discontinuous, parallel, linear echoes in the lumen as shown below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Snowstorm sign (seen on USG</strong>) is seen in extracapsular rupture of breast implant.</p>\n<p><strong>Comet tail sign</strong> is a finding that can be seen on chest CT. The comet-tail sign is produced by the distortion of vessels and bronchi that lead to an adjacent area of round atelectasis, which is the mass. The bronchovascular bundles appear to be pulled into the mass and resemble a comet tail.</p>\n<p>Note: Detection of intracapsular silicone ruptures is almost impossible on mammography.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5657","text":"A woman came with complain‌ts of breast pain followi‌ng surgery. Her MRI is sh​own below. What is the li‌kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman came with complaints of breast pain following surgery. Her MRI is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extra-capsular silicone ru‌pture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contracture and calcificat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intra-capsular silicone ru‌pture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Breast ‌abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intra-capsular silicone rupture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/705643f457fa4facb315426db0d36d99.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intra-capsular silicone rupture</strong></p><p>The above image shows curvilinear hypointense lines (arrows) in the left breast implant indicative of the collapsed shell floating in the silicone gel. This is called the<strong> linguine sign</strong> that is seen in <strong>intra-capsular rupture of a breast implant. </strong></p>\n<p>After implantation of a silicone or saline breast implant, a fibrous capsule (scar) forms around the implant shell. In an intracapsular rupture, the contents of the implant are contained by the fibrous scar, while the shell appears as a group of wavy lines.</p>\n<p>This has been termed the \"linguine sign\" and has been most commonly described with MR imaging.</p>\n<p>Multiple hyperintense foci on T2-weighted images are sometimes found within the implant lumen. These findings are referred to as the <strong>salad oil sign</strong> or <strong>droplet sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>Indications for <strong>breast MRI</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Screening for DCIS in <strong>high-risk</strong> patients</li>\n<li>Evaluation of breast <strong>implants</strong></li>\n<li>Evaluation of breast scar tissue versus recurrent tumors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ultrasound is the preferred modality of imaging in suspected cases of extracapsular rupture. The <strong>s</strong><strong>nowstorm sign </strong>on USG is seen in <strong>extracapsular</strong> rupture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6806","text":"A 50-year-old female patie‌nt presents with a 4*3cm ‌irregular mass in the upp​er outer quadrant of the ‌right breast, which is no‌t fixed to the skin or th‍e chest wall. A lymph nod​e of 2cm is palpated in t‍he right axilla. No mass ‌is palpated in the opposi​te breast. Which of the f‍ollowing investigations w‍ould you perform on this ‍patient? Bilateral mammog​raphy PET Scan Biopsy of ‌the breast mass USG guide‌d biopsy of the lymph nod‌e","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old female patient presents with a 4*3cm irregular mass in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast, which is not fixed to the skin or the chest wall. A lymph node of 2cm is palpated in the right axilla. No mass is palpated in the opposite breast. Which of the following investigations would you perform on this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Bilateral mammography</li>\n<li>PET Scan</li>\n<li>Biopsy of the breast mass</li>\n<li>USG guided biopsy of the lymph node</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3‌ &amp;amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 3 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 3 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>breast malignancy </strong>with locoregional spread with unlikely distant metastasis.  For further evaluation of this lesion, <strong>bilateral mammography, a biopsy </strong>of the breast<strong> mass </strong>and a <strong>USG-guided biopsy </strong>of the <strong>suspicious lymph node</strong> need to be performed in order to determine staging and decide on further management. </p>\n<p>Statement 2:<strong> PET Scan</strong> is usually performed in advanced cases of breast cancer. It is not routinely used in the assessment of locoregional disease.</p>\n<p>Statement 1:<strong> Diagnostic mammography</strong> is used to evaluate women with abnormal findings such as a breast mass or nipple discharge. Mammography is more accurate than clinical examination for the detection of early breast cancers. <strong>Mammographic features suggestive of breast cancer</strong> include a solid mass with or without stellate features, microcalcifications, irregular soft tissue shadow, spiculations and duct distortion.</p>\n<p>Statement 3:<strong> Pathological diagnosis</strong> of carcinoma is made by a core needle biopsy (<strong>tru-cut biopsy</strong>) or fine-needle aspiration before any type of treatment is started. </p>\n<p>Statement 4: The presence or absence of axillary lymph node involvement in breast cancer is one of the most important prognostic factors for determining staging and treatment. <strong>Ultrasound-guided lymph node biopsy</strong> should be done in patients with suspicious or clinically palpable lymph nodes. </p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasound features</strong> suggestive of breast cancer include a hypoechoic, irregular mass with indistinct, angulated or spiculated margins, acoustic shadowing, ductal extension and a branched pattern of mass.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4008","text":"A 22-year-old woman is fou‌nd to have the BRCA1 muta‌tion. She got herself tes​ted after her mother’s de‌ath due to breast cancer ‌with a BRCA1 mutation. At‍ what age and with which ​modality would you start ‍breast cancer screening i‌n this patient?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old woman is found to have the BRCA1 mutation. She got herself tested after her mother’s death due to breast cancer with a BRCA1 mutation. At what age and with which modality would you start breast cancer screening in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 years -‌ Mammogram","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40 year‌s - MRI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25 year‌s - USG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"25 years - MRI + Mammogram‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 25 years - MRI + Mammogram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 25 years - MRI + Mammogram</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>BRCA mutations</strong> or <strong>positive</strong> known <strong>first-degree</strong> family history are considered <strong>high-risk patients</strong>. These patients need to undergo <strong>annual screening MRI + Mammogram</strong> from the age of <strong>25yrs.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients with a family history of breast cancer but no known gene mutation should also start screening before 40 years. Screening should begin <strong>10 years before</strong> the earliest age of diagnosis of breast cancer in the first-degree relative. E.g- If the mother was detected with breast cancer at 40 years of age, the daughter should start screening by the age of 30 years.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk patients</strong> for breast cancer:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Women with a known BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Two first-degree relatives with breast cancers with or without gene mutations</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Women with a history of chest irradiation between the ages of 10 and 30</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">2 or more cases of breast cancer &lt; 50 years old in the family</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mutation (or a first-degree relative with a mutation) in the TP53 or PTEN genes</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4007","text":"A young woman with a dista‌nt breast cancer relative‌ comes to you in the clin​ic. You are advising her ‌to practice self-examinat‌ion of her breasts. Which‍ of the following stateme​nts is incorrect regardin‍g this practice?","raw_text":"A young woman with a distant breast cancer relative comes to you in the clinic. You are advising her to practice self-examination of her breasts. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this practice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It should be done once a m‌onth, every month","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ideally, be done between t‌he 3rd to 6th day of the ‌menstrual cycle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It should only be done whi‌le standing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The examination should be ‌done by using the middle ‌three fingerpads","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It should only be done while standing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/504a22df2e1245329a35d71199e408c6x344x208.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It should only be done while standing</strong></p><p><strong>Self-examination of breasts</strong> can be done while <strong>standing</strong> or <strong>lying down.</strong></p>\n<p>It should be ideally be done <strong>once</strong> <strong>a month, every month</strong> between the <strong>3rd to 6th day</strong> of the <strong>menstrual cycle.</strong> If the periods are irregular or the patient is post-menopausal, then the patient can pick any day of the month. </p>\n<p>It can be done any time of the day while showering, dressing, standing, or lying down at night.</p>\n<p>Steps of the<strong> self-examination:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Visual inspection: See for any abnormalities, like shape, size, dimpling, puckering of the skin, nipple discharge, etc.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Manual inspection: It is done by using the middle three fingerpads. Feel for any lumps. Techniques used are (either one of them): \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Horizontal stripes method</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Vertical stripes method</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dial clock method</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Be sure to check under the areola and press the nipple gently to check for any <strong>discharge.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Nm9Ax_N2","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"10_acls","title":"10.Acls","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2593","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen​ted to the ER with dizzin​ess. Her blood pressure i​s 80/60 mmHg, pulse rate ​is 45/minute. Her ECG is ‍shown below. What is the ‌next best step of managem‌ent?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented to the ER with dizziness. Her blood pressure is 80/60 mmHg, pulse rate is 45/minute. Her ECG is shown below. What is the next best step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV Epin​ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV At​ropine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IV Ami​odarone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IV Ade​nosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IV Atropine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/69dc56b785024b22b9b6b551cb9d74aa.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IV Atropine</strong></p><p>The ECG shown above is the <strong>second-degree atrioventricular block type 1 - Wenckebach phenomenon.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is indicated when bradycardia is accompanied by any one of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Acutely altered mental status</li>\n<li>Signs of shock</li>\n<li>Ischemic chest discomfort</li>\n<li>Acute heart failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is initiated with <strong>1 mg atropine iv</strong> which may be <strong>repeated</strong> at <strong>3-5 minute intervals</strong> to a <strong>maximum</strong> <strong>dose</strong> of <strong>3 mg.</strong></p>\n<p>Along with atropine, <strong>transcutaneous pacing</strong> is to be done.</p>\n<p>If the bradycardia is <strong>refractory</strong> to both atropine and transcutaneous pacing, then <strong>IV infusions of dopamine </strong>(5 to 20 μg/kg/min)<strong> or epinephrine </strong>(2 to 10 μg/min) should be considered.</p>\n<p>If the patient still isn’t responding, then<strong> transvenous pacing</strong> should be tried. If the patient is under spinal anesthesia, low-dose (0.2 mg) IV epinephrine can be given.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine is the drug of choice for treating bradyarrhythmias in infants and children.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2594","text":"A middle-aged woman with a​n acute altered mental st​ate was brought to the ER​. She had a history of hy​pothyroidism but was not ‍on regular medication. On‌ examination, her pulse r‌ate was 40 beats per minu‍te, BP was 80/60 mmHg. Wh​ich of the following cann‌ot be used in the treatme​nt of this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with an acute altered mental state was brought to the ER. She had a history of hypothyroidism but was not on regular medication. On examination, her pulse rate was 40 beats per minute, BP was 80/60 mmHg. Which of the following cannot be used in the treatment of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transcutan​eous pacing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aden​osine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adenosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adenosine</strong></p><p>Adenosine cannot be used in the treatment of this patient. It is used to treat supraventricular tachyarrhythmias, not bradyarrhythmias. </p>\n<p>The scenario depicts a patient with symptomatic bradycardia, marked by acute altered mental status and hypotension with hypothyroidism as the risk factor.</p>\n<p>Treatment is indicated when bradycardia is accompanied by any one of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Acutely altered mental status</li>\n<li>Signs of shock</li>\n<li>Ischemic chest discomfort</li>\n<li>Acute heart failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is initiated with 1 mg <strong>atropine</strong> iv which may be repeated at 3-5 minute intervals to a maximum dose of 3 mg.</p>\n<p>Along with atropine, <strong>transcutaneous</strong> <strong>pacing</strong> is to be done.</p>\n<p>If the bradycardia is <strong>refractory</strong> to both atropine and transcutaneous pacing, then <strong>IV infusions of dopamine</strong> (5 to 20 μg/kg/min) <strong>or epinephrine</strong> (2 to 10 μg/min) should be considered.</p>\n<p>If the bradycardia is still not responding, then <strong>transvenous</strong> <strong>pacing</strong> should be tried. If the patient is under spinal anesthesia, low-dose (0.2 mg) IV epinephrine can be given.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine is the drug of choice for treating bradyarrhythmias in infants and children.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2596","text":"What is the maximum dose o​f IV atropine that can be​ given to treat a patient​ with symptomatic bradyca​rdia?","raw_text":"What is the maximum dose of IV atropine that can be given to treat a patient with symptomatic bradycardia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5​ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.6​ mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 ​mg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6 ​mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 mg</strong></p><p>IV atropine is used in the treatment of symptomatic bradyarrhythmia starting at a dose of 1 mg, which can be repeated every 3-5 minutes to a <strong>maximum</strong> dose of <strong>3 mg</strong>.</p>\n<p>Along with atropine, <strong>transcutaneous pacing</strong> is to be done.</p>\n<p>If the bradycardia is refractory to both atropine and transcutaneous pacing, then <strong>IV infusions</strong> of <strong>dopamine</strong> (5 to 20 μg/kg/min) <strong>or epinephrine</strong> (2 to 10 μg/min) should be considered.</p>\n<p>If the bradycardia is still not responding, then <strong>transvenous pacing</strong> should be tried. If the patient is under spinal anesthesia, low-dose (0.2 mg) IV epinephrine can be given.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine is the drug of choice for treating bradyarrhythmias in infants and children.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2597","text":"A 22-year-old man was brou​ght to the casualty with ​palpitations and dizzines​s. His heart rate was 160​/min and blood pressure w‍as 110/80 mm Hg. His ECG ‌is given below. What is t‌he first line of manageme‍nt?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man was brought to the casualty with palpitations and dizziness. His heart rate was 160/min and blood pressure was 110/80 mm Hg. His ECG is given below. What is the first line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenosi​ne 6 mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenosi​ne 12 mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vagal m​anoeuvre","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amiodaro​ne 300 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vagal manoeuvre","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66ae648faa824a7ebef13c06ebd06d1e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vagal manoeuvre</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia</strong>. As the patient's <strong>blood pressure </strong>is <strong>normal</strong> and there is no evidence of hemodynamic deterioration, <strong>initial treatment </strong>consists of<strong> vagal maneuvers </strong>(Valsalva maneuver) in awake patients. This is because vagal maneuvers alone can terminate approximately 20% to 25% of reentry supraventricular tachycardias (SVTs).</p>\n<p>If this is <strong>unsuccessful</strong>, then IV <strong>adenosine 6 mg</strong> is given. If necessary another dose of adenosine 12 mg may be administered after 1-2 minutes.</p>\n<p>If the drug is administered via a central venous catheter, then these doses are reduced to 3 mg and 6 mg, respectively.</p>\n<p>If there is <strong>hemodynamic deterioration</strong>, then <strong>cardioversion</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2598","text":"A 22-year-old man was brou​ght to the casualty with ​palpitations and dizzines​s. His heart rate was 160​/min and blood pressure w‍as 110/80 mmHg. His ECG i‌s given below. Vagal mane‌uver was found to be inef‍fective. What would your ​next line of management b‌e?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man was brought to the casualty with palpitations and dizziness. His heart rate was 160/min and blood pressure was 110/80 mmHg. His ECG is given below. Vagal maneuver was found to be ineffective. What would your next line of management be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenosin​e 0.6 mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenosin​e 1.2 mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosi​ne 6 mg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adenosi​ne 12 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenosine 6 mg","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b6e060eba614177a05becb1ac8b0a58.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenosine 6 mg</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia.</strong></p>\n<p>As <strong>vagal</strong> <strong>maneuver</strong> has been found <strong>ineffective</strong>, then the next best step is to administer <strong>adenosine 6 mg IV.</strong></p>\n<p>For treating supraventricular tachycardia, the hemodynamic status of the patient has to be assessed. In this patient, as his blood pressure is normal and there is no evidence of hemodynamic deterioration, initial treatment consists of vagal maneuvers (Valsalva maneuver) in awake patients. </p>\n<p>In this scenario, as this was unsuccessful, IV adenosine 6 mg had to be given. If necessary another dose of adenosine 12 mg may be administered after 1-2 minutes.</p>\n<p>If the drug is administered via a central venous catheter, then these doses are reduced to 3 mg and 6 mg, respectively.</p>\n<p>If there is hemodynamic deterioration, then cardioversion is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2599","text":"A 22-year-old male was bro​ught to the casualty with​ palpitations and dizzine​ss. His heart rate is 170​/min and his blood pressu‍re was 70/40 mm Hg. His E‌CG is given below. What i‌s the first line of manag‍ement?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old male was brought to the casualty with palpitations and dizziness. His heart rate is 170/min and his blood pressure was 70/40 mm Hg. His ECG is given below. What is the first line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vagal m​anoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenosin​e 6 mg IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosine​ 12 mg IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardio​version","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardioversion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c8c4f0a5f8f431aa1f01cb84c772132.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardioversion</strong></p><p>This patient has<strong> supraventricular tachycardia </strong>with evidence of<strong> hemodynamic deterioration. </strong>Immediate treatment would be <strong>cardioversion </strong>(50-100J).</p>\n<p>If the patient were <strong>hemodyamically stable,</strong> initial treatment would be <strong>vagal maneuvers</strong> (Valsalva maneuver) in awake patients. This is because vagal maneuvers alone can terminate approximately 20% to 25% of reentry supraventricular tachycardias (SVTs).</p>\n<p>If this is unsuccessful, then <strong>iv adenosine 6 mg</strong> is given. If necessary another dose of adenosine 12 mg may be administered after 1-2 minutes.</p>\n<p>If the drug is administered via a central venous catheter, then these doses are reduced to 3 mg and 6 mg respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0769","text":"A 50-year-old patient with​ a history of hypertensio​n and diabetes mellitus d​eveloped the following rh​ythm. His blood pressure ‍was 90/40 mm Hg and he ha‌d a feeble pulse. What is‌ the next step of managem‍ent?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient with a history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus developed the following rhythm. His blood pressure was 90/40 mm Hg and he had a feeble pulse. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV ade​nosine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV dil​tiazem","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DC cardi​oversion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ibut​ilide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DC cardioversion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3bacde8f971141289ac5ee2ab015fce5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DC cardioversion</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1681\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The clinical scenario suggests<strong> supraventricular tachycardia</strong> with hemodynamic instability and the next step of management is <strong>DC cardioversion </strong>(50-100J).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 498\">If the patient was <strong>hemodyamically stable,</strong> initial treatment would be <strong>vagal maneuvers</strong> (Valsalva maneuver) in awake patients. This is because vagal maneuvers alone can terminate approximately 20% to 25% of reentry supraventricular tachycardias (SVTs).</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 498\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 498\">If this is unsuccessful, then <strong>iv adenosine 6 mg</strong> is given. If necessary another dose of adenosine 12 mg may be administered after 1-2 minutes.</div>\n<p>If the drug is administered via a central venous catheter, then these doses are reduced to 3 mg and 6 mg respectively.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2600","text":"A 58-year-old man presente​d to the casualty with co​mplaints of increasing sh​ortness of breath, lighth​eadedness, and palpitatio‍ns. He had an irregularly‌ irregular pulse and bloo‌d pressure was 120/70mm H‍g. His ECG is given below​. What is the treatment o‌f choice?","raw_text":"A  58-year-old man presented to the casualty with complaints of increasing shortness of breath, lightheadedness, and palpitations. He had an irregularly irregular pulse and blood pressure was 120/70mm Hg. His ECG is given below. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediate de​fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synchronized cardioversion​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV ade​nosine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IV ibu​tilide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IV ibutilide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71941fcd59ea464ea79143a8111ed5e0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08ae6f62fa4e4ba8a3253dfa1301f8b1x1280x2567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IV ibutilide</strong></p><p>The patient is in <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong> but his <strong>hemodynamic status is normal</strong>. So the <strong>treatment of choice </strong>is<strong> IV Ibutilide.</strong></p>\n<p>Ibutilide is a<strong> class III antiarrhythmic drug</strong> that has a rapid onset of action. It restores sinus rhythm by prolonging the action potential duration and effective refractory period.</p>\n<p>It is administered at a dose of <strong>1 mg over 10 minutes</strong>. If necessary, a second dose can be administered 10 minutes after the first.</p>\n<p>If there is evidence of <strong>hemodynamic</strong> <strong>deterioration</strong>, immediate <strong>cardioversion</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>Cardioversion is a <strong>synchronized</strong> administration of shock as the timing is typically synchronized with the <strong>R waves or QRS complex</strong> of a cardiac cycle. On the other hand, random administration of shock anywhere in between a cardiac cycle is termed as Defibrillation which is non-synchronized.</p>\n<p>However, the mechanism for both processes is quite similar. The electric current is made to travel from the negative to the positive electrode in such a way that it traverses the <strong>entire myocardium at once</strong> to conduct a simultaneous contraction of each cell of the heart muscle. It is made to interrupt any ongoing abnormal electrical activity by terminating it so that the sinus node can take back the charge and resume its normal pacemaker activity.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 642.671875px; height: 140px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">Indications for electrical cardioversion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 312.671875px;\">Indications for defibrillation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 323px;\">\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Supraventricular tachycardia</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">(atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia [AVNRT] and atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia [AVRT])</span></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Atrial fibrillation</span></strong></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Atrial flutter</strong> (types I and II)</span></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Ventricular tachycardia</strong> </span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">(with a pulse)</span></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Any patient with<strong> re-entrant</strong> tachycardia with <strong>narrow or wide QRS</strong> complex (ventricular rate &gt;150 bpm) who is <strong>unstable</strong> (eg, ischemic chest pain, acute pulmonary edema, hypotension, acute altered mental status, signs of shock)</span></p>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 312.671875px;\">\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Pulseless ventricular tachycardia (VT)</span></strong></p>\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Ventricular fibrillation (VF)</span></strong></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Cardiac arrest</strong> due to or resulting in VF</span></p>\n</div>\n<span style=\"color: #000000;\"> </span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Cardioversion is typically performed under <strong>induction or sedation</strong> using a short-acting agent such as midazolam, unless the patient is hemodynamically unstable or if cardiovascular collapse is imminent. Defibrillation is more of an emergent maneuver and, when necessary, should be preferably performed in conjunction with or prior to the administration of induction or sedative agents.</p>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\" id=\"ads-pos-a4_1\"></div>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\">Energy may be delivered through mainly two waveforms in defibrillators that are monophasic or biphasic.\n<div class=\"inContentAd\" id=\"ads-pos-a7_8\">\n<p>Monophasic defibrillation delivers a charge in only <strong>one direction</strong> as opposed to biphasic defibrillation delivering a charge in <strong>one direction for half </strong>of the shock and in the electrically <strong>opposite direction for the second half.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\" id=\"ads-pos-a7_9\">Biphasic waveforms are adopted in newer defibrillators as they deliver a more consistent magnitude of the current which successfully terminates arrhythmias at lower energies than monophasic waveform defibrillators.</div>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\"> </div>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\">Energy selection for defibrillation or cardioversion. Following are the guidelines for <strong>initial </strong>energy requirements issued by the American Heart Association in 2010.</div>\n<p><strong>Atrial fibrillation</strong> energy requirements are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>200</strong> Joules for monophasic devices</li>\n<li>120-200 Joules for biphasic devices</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Atrial flutter energy requirements are as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<ul>\n<li>100 Joules for monophasic devices</li>\n<li>50-100 Joules for biphasic devices</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\" id=\"ads-pos-a7_13\">Ventricular tachycardia with pulse energy requirements are as follows:</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<ul>\n<li>200 Joules for monophasic devices</li>\n<li>100 Joules for biphasic devices</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"inContentAd\" id=\"ads-pos-a7_14\">Ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia energy requirements are as follows:</div>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<ul>\n<li>360 Joules for monomorphic devices</li>\n<li>120-200 Joules for biphasic devices</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2602","text":"A young man was brought to​ the casualty with compla​ints of palpitations and ​dizziness. His pulse rate​ was 150/min and blood pr‍essure 120/70 mm Hg. His ‌ECG is shown below. What ‌is the line of management‍?","raw_text":"A young man was brought to the casualty with complaints of palpitations and dizziness. His pulse rate was 150/min and blood pressure 120/70 mm Hg. His ECG is shown below. What is the line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV proc​ainamide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IV ade​nosine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV lid​ocaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defibri​llation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IV procainamide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/002a6eca845041bc9b98cf992fc39b59.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ed9d8ff702f426cae109b1bd8fb0ec8x1280x2175.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IV procainamide</strong></p><p>The patient is in ventricular tachycardia with hemodynamic stability. The next line of management is to administer <strong>procainamide, class IA antiarrhythmic drug.</strong></p>\n<p>Antiarrhythmic therapy for stable wide QRS tachycardia include procainamide, amiodarone, or sotalol. <strong>Synchronized cardioversion</strong> is considered when the <strong>patient </strong>is <strong>unstable</strong>.</p>\n<p>When a patient is in<strong> tachyarrhythmia</strong> typically with a<strong> heart rate</strong> of <strong>≥150 per minute</strong>, the initial intervention consists of identifying and treating the underlying cause. This includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maintaining a patent airway and assisting breathing as necessary</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Administering oxygen if hypoxemic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Attaching cardiac monitor to identify rhythm; monitor blood pressure and oximetry</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When there is <strong>persistent tachyarrhythmia</strong>, the management is as depicted by the flowchart (based on the AHA guidelines) below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Adenosine is used in regular narrow complex tachycardia.</p>\n<p>Option C: Lidocaine has limiting side effects and, increase the overall mortality risk.</p>\n<p>Option D: It is the treatment for immediately life-threatening arrhythmias with which the patient does not have a pulse, ie ventricular fibrillation (VF) or pulseless ventricular tachycardia (VT).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2605","text":"A 70-year-old woman with c​hronic kidney disease on ​chronic therapy with a lo​op diuretic was brought t​o the ER. Her daughter sa‍ys that she complained of‌ palpitation, dizziness, ‌and then suddenly collaps‍ed. Her ECG is given belo​w. Which electrolyte abno‌rmality has to be conside​red in this patient?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease on chronic therapy with a loop diuretic was brought to the ER. Her daughter says that she complained of palpitation, dizziness, and then suddenly collapsed. Her ECG is given below. Which electrolyte abnormality has to be considered in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperma​gnesemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypomag​nesemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern​atremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypomagnesemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1792c4327b84466fa3112c8b3fc69a1f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypomagnesemia</strong></p><p>Chronic use of loop diuretics such as furosemide can predispose to <strong>hypomagnesemia</strong>. The given ECG classically depicts polymorphic ventricular tachycardia—torsades de pointes. Torsade rhythm is most commonly is seen in association with hypomagnesemia and hypokalemia. </p>\n<p><strong>Torsades de pointes</strong> is a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia meaning “twisting of the points,” characterized by twisting of the QRS axis around the baseline and a polymorphic appearance.</p>\n<p>There is QT interval prolongation in the ECG due to a non-uniform delay in repolarization.</p>\n<p>The other cause of torsades de pointes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drugs like <strong>quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide, </strong>and <strong>phenothiazine </strong>(amiodarone is rarely associated with torsades de pointes because the delay in repolarization induced by it is uniform).</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n<li>Acute ischemia or infarction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discontinue the offending drug</li>\n<li>Correct electrolyte abnormalities, pH and hypoxia</li>\n<li>Magnesium sulphate infusion </li>\n<li>Increase the heart rate - electrical pacing, isoproterenol infusion, or intravenous atropine </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2611","text":"Which among the following ​is the most common cause ​of pulseless electrical a​ctivity?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common cause of pulseless electrical activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac ​tamponade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary​ embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tension pn​eumothorax","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypov​olemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypovolemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypovolemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypovolemia</strong> and <strong>hypoxia</strong> are the two most <strong>common causes</strong> of <strong>pulseless electrical activity</strong> (PEA).</p>\n<p>Most likely causes of Pulseless electrical activity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In a trauma patient: hypovolemia, cardiac tamponade, and tension pneumothorax</li>\n<li>In the intraoperative or postoperative period: acute massive pulmonary thromboembolism or air embolism</li>\n<li>In pregnant women: uterine compression of the inferior vena cava, amniotic fluid embolism, or uterine rupture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2612","text":"Which of the following is ​an incorrect statement co​ncerning pulseless electr​ical activity (PEA)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement concerning pulseless electrical activity (PEA)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A pulselessness in the pre​sence of organized electr​ical activity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac tamponade can lead​ to PEA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediate defibrillation i​s required","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1mg of epinephrine is give​n immediately","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Immediate defibrillation is required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Immediate defibrillation is required</strong></p><p>A pulseless electrical activity (PEA) is a <strong>non-shockable rhythm.</strong> Defibrillation is of no benefit.</p>\n<p>PEA involves a heterogeneous group of cardiac rhythms all without a pulse.</p>\n<p>The immediate <strong>treatment</strong> for this is giving <strong>chest compressions</strong> and <strong>1 mg</strong> <strong>epinephrine</strong> at the earliest until more definitive therapy to the cause can be instituted.</p>\n<p>It is essential to rule out the reversible causes of cardiac arrest because the treatment for each of them is different and PEA is likely to persist until the cause is treated.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2586","text":"Which is the most common c​ardiac rhythm seen in adu​lts experiencing sudden c​ardiac arrest?","raw_text":"Which is the most common cardiac rhythm seen in adults experiencing sudden cardiac arrest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular ​fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asys​tole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial fi​brillation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paroxysmal supraventricula​r tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Asystole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Asystole</strong></p><p>The most common cardiac rhythm seen in adults experiencing sudden cardiac arrest is <strong>asystole,</strong> as recorded at initial contact in <strong>45–50% of cases.</strong></p>\n<p>Previously VF was thought to be the common rhythm, but its incidence has now been decreasing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2587","text":"What is the most common ca​use of cardiac arrest in ​children?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of cardiac arrest in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular ​fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myocardial​ infarction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asph​yxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital h​eart disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Asphyxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Asphyxia</strong></p><p>In children, the more common cause of cardiac arrest is <strong>asphyxia</strong> rather than ventricular fibrillation.</p>\n<p>Even though asphyxia is the more common cause, pediatric resuscitation starts with<strong> chest compressions </strong>rather than rescue breaths as in adults for simplicity in training. This is because the cycle of 30 chest compressions before rescue breathing <strong>delays ventilation</strong> for <strong>only 15 to 20 seconds</strong>.</p>\n<p>During cardiopulmonary resuscitation of infants and children, the ratio of chest compression to rescue breaths is <strong>30:2 </strong>for a<strong> single rescuer</strong> and <strong>15:2</strong> if <strong>2 or more rescuers</strong> are present. In adults, the ratio is<strong> 30:2</strong> irrespective of the number of rescuers.</p>\n<p>In infants and children, the depth of compression should be at least one-third of the anteroposterior diameter of the chest which is about 5 cm in children and 4 cm in infants.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2601","text":"A 30-year-old man was brou​ght to the ER in an uncon​scious state after having​ collapsed in a railway s​tation. As there was no p‍ulse, cardiopulmonary res‌uscitation was started, a‌nd the monitor showed asy‍stole. What is the next s​tep in the management?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man was brought to the ER in an unconscious state after having collapsed in a railway station. As there was no pulse, cardiopulmonary resuscitation was started, and the monitor showed asystole. What is the next step in the management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defibri​llation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synchronized cardioversion​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 mg at​ropine IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 mg epin​ephrine IV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 mg epinephrine IV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 mg epinephrine IV</strong></p><p><strong>Asystole </strong>is a <strong>non-shockable</strong> rhythm. Treatment for both involves starting CPR immediately and <strong>administering 1 mg epinephrine IV.</strong></p>\n<p>Asystole is the complete and sustained absence of electrical activity. It is usually seen as a terminal rhythm in a resuscitation attempt that started with another rhythm, or in a patient with unwitnessed or prolonged cardiac arrest. The prognosis for persistent asystole is poor.</p>\n<p>Another example of a non-shockable rhythm is Pulseless electrical activity. It involves a heterogeneous group of cardiac rhythms, all without a pulse.</p>\n<p>It is essential to rule out the reversible causes of cardiac arrest because the treatment for each of them is different and PEA is likely to persist until the cause is treated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2610","text":"A 47-year-old woman was br​ought to the casualty in ​an unconscious state afte​r having suddenly collaps​ed. As there was no pulse‍, cardiopulmonary resusci‌tation was started and th‌e monitor showed the foll‍owing rhythm. What is the​ next step?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman was brought to the casualty in an unconscious state after having suddenly collapsed. As there was no pulse, cardiopulmonary resuscitation was started and the monitor showed the following rhythm. What is the next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defibri​llation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synchronized cardioversion​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 mg epin​ephrine IV","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 mg at​ropine IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 mg epinephrine IV","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afcb5b8322cc4db9b71de1dcc76b45ee.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 mg epinephrine IV</strong></p><p>The patient has an organized, apparently<strong> normal ECG without a pulse </strong>- i.e the patient has <strong>pulseless electrical activity</strong> (PEA). A pulseless electrical activity involves a heterogeneous group of cardiac rhythms, all without a pulse.</p>\n<p>The immediate treatment for this is giving <strong>chest compressions </strong>and<strong> 1 mg epinephrine</strong> at the <strong>earliest</strong> until definitive therapy to the cause can be instituted.</p>\n<p>It is essential to rule out the reversible causes of cardiac arrest because the treatment for each of them is different and PEA is likely to persist until the cause is treated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2603","text":"You are performing cardiop​ulmonary resuscitation on​ a patient who had gone i​nto cardiac arrest, and t​he monitor shows the foll‍owing rhythm. What is the‌ next step in management?‌","raw_text":"You are performing cardiopulmonary resuscitation on a patient who had gone into cardiac arrest, and the monitor shows the following rhythm. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defibri​llation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Synchronized cardioversion​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 mg at​ropine IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 mg epin​ephrine IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Defibrillation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2cd1da254b744415baac5641d4306196.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Defibrillation</strong></p><p>The ECG rhythm shown is <strong>ventricular fibrillation</strong> which is treated by <strong>immediate defibrillation.</strong></p>\n<p>The management of ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia (<strong>shockable</strong> <strong>rhythms</strong>) is the same. Immediate chest compression is to be started.</p>\n<p>It is of utmost necessity to <strong>defibrillate at the earliest</strong> because the chances of survival decrease by 7-10% every minute without defibrillation.</p>\n<p>Defibrillation involves delivering an electrical current between two pads placed on the chest so as to interrupt the disorganized cardiac activity and restore an organized cardiac rhythm or asystole for at least 5 seconds.</p>\n<p>The energy that must be applied is</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monophasic defibrillator– 360 J</li>\n<li>Biphasic defibrillator- 120 J</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2604","text":"You have just performed de​fibrillation in a patient​ with ventricular fibrill​ation. According to the A​CLS guidelines, what shou‍ld be your next step?","raw_text":"You have just performed defibrillation in a patient with ventricular fibrillation. According to the ACLS guidelines, what should be your next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Check fo​r a pulse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resume chest​ compression","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epinephri​ne 1 mg IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amiodarone 300 mg IV bolus​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Resume chest compression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Resume chest compression</strong></p><p><strong>Chest</strong> <strong>compressions</strong> should be <strong>resumed immediately</strong> following shock delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Pulse check </strong>and reanalysis of the cardiac rhythm is performed only <strong>after 2 minutes</strong> of <strong>chest compression and rescue breaths</strong>. This is because the heart is temporarily stunned by a defibrillator shock and so chest compressions would benefit the patient by providing coronary blood flow during this period.</p>\n<p>Defibrillation involves delivering an electrical current between two pads placed on the chest so as to interrupt disorganized cardiac activity and restore an organized cardiac rhythm or asystole for at least 5 seconds. It is of utmost necessity to defibrillate immediately because the chances of survival decrease by 7-10% every minute without defibrillation.</p>\n<p>If after 2 minutes, the rhythm remains the same, then another shock is delivered. Chest compression and rescue breaths are resumed and 1 mg epinephrine is administered. Consideration is given for an advanced airway placement.</p>\n<p>If after 2 minutes the rhythm still remains the same, the first dose of amiodarone 300mg bolus is given.</p>\n<p>With the next cycle of CPR, a shock is delivered and 1 mg of epinephrine is given. And with the cycle of CPR after that, shock is delivered and the second dose of amiodarone- 150mg is given</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2607","text":"Which of the following dru​gs are not approved for r​esuscitation in a cardiac​ arrest victim?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs are not approved for resuscitation in a cardiac arrest victim?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amiod​arone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B a​nd C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vasopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vasopressin</strong></p><p><strong>Vasopressin</strong> was a part of the resuscitation protocol in the 2010 American Heart Association (AHA) cardiac arrest algorithm but it has been <strong>removed from the 2015 guidelines</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vasopressin causes intense peripheral vasoconstriction due to stimulation of V1 receptors in the endothelium but it causes a limited increase in myocardial contractility, myocardial and cerebral oxygen consumption, and metabolic demands. Thus it doesn’t have much of a survival benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>Epinephrine and amiodarone </strong>are the two main drugs <strong>approved </strong>by the AHA for <strong>resuscitation </strong>of a patient in <strong>cardiac arrest</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The first dose of epinephrine,1 mg IV, is administered after the 2<sup>nd</sup> defibrillator shock is delivered and it is continued every 3-5 minutes.</p>\n<p>If this is not effective, 300 mg IV bolus dose of amiodarone can be given and another dose of 150 mg given if necessary</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4332","text":"Which of the following is ​true about adrenaline in ​CPR?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about adrenaline in CPR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be given intratracheal​ly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intracardiac route is bett​er than IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Converts coarse fibrillati​on into fine ones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The dose used is 2 ml cont​aining 1 in 1000 concentr​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Can be given intratracheally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Can be given intratracheally</strong></p><p>Intravenous Adrenaline is the drug of choice for CPR. However, adrenaline can be given <strong>intratracheally</strong> also. The concentration required for intratracheal administration is <strong>2-2.5 times</strong> more than required for the IV route.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Intracardiac injection</strong> is not recommended as it can cause <strong>myocardial damage.</strong> Recommended routes for adrenaline are intravenous, intraosseous (children), and intra-tracheal.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Adrenaline</strong> converts <strong>fine</strong> fibrillation <strong>to coarse fibrillation</strong> which then responds well to defibrillation.</p>\n<p>Option D: Adrenaline is available as a <strong>1 mL injection</strong> containing 1 mg of adrenaline in 1:1000 concentration. The dose used in cardiac arrest is 1mg of adrenaline every 3-5 minutes, usually given as 1ml in 1:1000 concentration or 10ml in 1:10,000 concentration (more preferred since in cardiac arrest peripheral circulation is absent; hence larger volume is required for the drug to reach the central circulation).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2608","text":"In cardiac arrest patients​, what is the dose of epi​nephrine you would prefer​ to use?","raw_text":"In cardiac arrest patients, what is the dose of epinephrine you would prefer to use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 mL o​f 1:1000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 mL of​ 1:10,000","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"10 mL of ​1:1,00,000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 mL of 1​:1,00,000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10 mL of 1:10,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10 mL of 1:10,000</strong></p><p>Epinephrine is used in<strong> cardiac arrest </strong>patients at a dose of<strong> 10 mL of 1: 10,000 solution (1 mg epinephrine).</strong></p>\n<p>Epinephrine is an adrenergic agonist: α-Adrenergic effects increase myocardial blood flow. β-Adrenergic effects increases myocardial contractility.</p>\n<p>Indication<strong>:</strong> VF/VT, electromechanical dissociation, ventricular asystole, severe bradycardia unresponsive to atropine or pacing, severe hypotension.</p>\n<p>Dose: 1 mg IV. Repeat doses every 3–5 min as necessary. Administration down a tracheal tube requires higher doses (2–2.5 mg in adults).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2609","text":"Which of the following dru​gs cannot be administered​ via the endotracheal rou​te?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs cannot be administered via the endotracheal route?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adren​aline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ligno​caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium bi​carbonate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium bicarbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium bicarbonate</strong></p><p>Sodium bicarbonate cannot be administered via the endotracheal route.</p>\n<p>The dosage for intratracheal route is 2-2.5 times higher than the intravenous dose. The drugs should be diluted in 10 ml of normal saline or distilled water for adults and 5 ml for children.</p>\n<p>When there is <strong>no intravenous access</strong>, the <strong>intraosseous</strong> route is <strong>preferred over</strong> the <strong>endotracheal</strong> route because the optimal dose via the endotracheal route is not known. Also, CPR needs to be stopped momentarily as the drug may be regurgitated up the endotracheal tube during chest compression. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7321","text":"What alternative drug woul​d you use for cardiac arr​est if epinephrine is not​ effective?","raw_text":"What alternative drug would you use for cardiac arrest if epinephrine is not effective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amiod​arone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aden​osine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amiodarone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amiodarone</strong></p><p><strong>Epinephrine and amiodarone </strong>are the two main drugs <strong>approved by the AHA </strong>for the resuscitation of a cardiac arrest patient.</p>\n<p>The first dose of epinephrine 1 mg IV is administered after the 2<sup>nd</sup> defibrillator shock is delivered and it is continued every 3-5 minutes.</p>\n<p>If this is not effective, 300 mg IV bolus dose of amiodarone can be given and another dose of 150 mg given if necessary</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2613","text":"A comatose patient is bein​g shifted to the cardiac ​ICU after achieving retur​n of spontaneous circulat​ion (ROSC). What is the b‍ody temperature to be mai‌ntained in this patient?","raw_text":"A comatose patient is being shifted to the cardiac ICU after achieving return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC). What is the body temperature to be maintained in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-​16°","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20-2​4 °C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"32-​36°C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"42- ​45° C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 32-36°C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 32-36°C</strong></p><p>According to the post-cardiac arrest care algorithm,<strong> targeted temperature management</strong> is the maintenance of a constant target temperature between <strong>32-36<sup>0</sup></strong>C for <strong>at least 24 hours. </strong></p>\n<p>This is done in post-cardiac resuscitation patients who have achieved return of spontaneous circulation (<strong>ROSC</strong>), but are <strong>still comatose.</strong></p>\n<p>The entire body is to be cooled using a combination of methods like fluid-filled cooling blankets, ice packs, forced air blankets, cold IV fluids, and/or invasive devices or catheters.</p>\n<p>It has been found to be the only intervention that can improve neurological recovery after cardiac arrest. It is most beneficial in ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5881","text":"You are performing CPR on ​a woman who is 38-weeks p​regnant. She was admitted​ to the ER after a massiv​e pulmonary embolism had ‍lead to cardio-pulmonary ‌arrest. Despite adequate ‌resuscitative measures, t‍here is no improvement. W​hat is the cut-off time f‌or perimortem cesarean de​livery in this scenario?","raw_text":"You are performing CPR on a woman who is 38-weeks pregnant. She was admitted to the ER after a massive pulmonary embolism had lead to cardio-pulmonary arrest. Despite adequate resuscitative measures, there is no improvement. What is the cut-off time for perimortem cesarean delivery in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 mi​nute","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 mi​nutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 mi​nutes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 mi​nutes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 minutes</strong></p><p><strong>Perimortem cesarean delivery</strong> should be considered if there is <strong>no return of spontaneous circulation</strong> in <strong>5 minutes,</strong> according to the AHA 2020 Guidelines.</p>\n<p>When a pregnant woman is in cardiac arrest, <strong>high-quality CPR</strong> and <strong>defibrillation</strong> are performed and <strong>epinephrine</strong> is administered. The maternal cardiac arrest team is assembled to look for the etiology and perform the following interventions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Maternal interventions</strong>: </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Airway management</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Administration of of 100% O2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placing the IV above the diaphragm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If the patient is on IV magnesium - stopping it and administering calcium chloride or gluconate</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Obstetric interventions</strong>: </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Continuous lateral uterine displacement</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>After performing the above-mentioned interventions, if there is no return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) in 5 minutes, a perimortem cesarean section is considered. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"39ou5kxg","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"10_urogenital_system","title":"10.Urogenital System","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB1838","text":"The urogenital ridge devel‌ops from which layer?","raw_text":"The urogenital ridge develops from which layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paraxial‌ mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermedia‌te mesoderm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral pla‌te mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extraembryo‌nic mesoderm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intermediate mesoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70678559e6654edc8d01a5c2e31db58dx600x519.JPEG","https://trello.com/1/cards/66a0a42f1cb6bb65006ecf4e/attachments/66a0a43e7f01ad353ac9efad/previews/66a0a43f7f01ad353ac9efbd/download/111._MCQ_125899%2C_Anatomy.jpg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intermediate mesoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>urogenital ridge</strong> develops from the <strong>intermediate mesoderm</strong>.</p>\n<p>After the formation of the peritoneal cavity, the intermediate mesoderm is seen as a <strong>bulge</strong> in the <strong>posterior abdominal wall</strong>. This bulge lateral to the attachment of the dorsal mesentery is known as the <strong>urogenital ridge</strong>.</p>\n<p>The urogenital ridge has 2 parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>genital ridge</strong>, which is <strong>medial</strong> and gives rise to the genital system</li>\n<li>The <strong>nephrogenic cord</strong>, which is <strong>lateral</strong> and contributes to the urinary system</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1847","text":"The proximal convoluted tu‌bules develop from which ‌structure?","raw_text":"The proximal convoluted tubules develop from which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesoneph‌ric duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metanephri‌c blastema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mesonephr‌ic tubules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ureter‌ic bud","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metanephric blastema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/632274bbe2084e428dda7ab77344e976x1280x2501.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metanephric blastema</strong></p><p>The <strong>proximal convoluted tubules </strong>develop from the <strong>metanephric blastema</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>kidney</strong> develops from <strong>3 sources</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metanephric blastema</strong> gives rise to the <strong>excretory system </strong>of the kidney. It forms the <strong>Bowman's capsule</strong>, <strong>proximal convoluted tubule</strong>, the <strong>loop of Henle</strong>, and <strong>distal convoluted tubule</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Ureteric bud</strong>, which is an outgrowth of the mesonephric duct, gives rise to the <strong>collecting system </strong>of the kidney. It forms the <strong>collecting ducts</strong>, minor and major <strong>calyces</strong>, and <strong>renal pelvis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Angiogenic mesenchyme</strong>, which migrates into the metanephric blastema, gives rise to the <strong>glomeruli</strong> and the <strong>vasa recta</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1840","text":"The ureteric bud develops ‌from which duct?","raw_text":"The ureteric bud develops from which duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesoneph‌ric duct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metaneph‌ric duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paramesone‌phric duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urete‌r duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mesonephric duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98b11f07ec484c8a8e3b80ea1f738200x1280x2501.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mesonephric duct</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>ureteric bud</strong> develops from the <strong>mesonephric duct</strong> (Wolffian duct). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The tip of the ureteric bud penetrates the metanephric blastema, which forms a cap over the distal end of this bud. The <strong>ureteric bud</strong> gives rise to the <strong>ureter</strong> and the <strong>collecting system of the kidney</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Structures arising from the metanephric blastema and ureteric bud are as follows:</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 346.781px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 201px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Metanephric blastema</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 140.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Ureteric bud</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 201px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Bowmans capsule</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 140.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Collecting ducts</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 201px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Proximal convoluted tubule</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 140.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Renal papillae</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 201px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Loop of Henle</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 140.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Major and minor calyces</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 201px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Distal convoluted tubule</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 140.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Renal pelvis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 201px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 140.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Ureter</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1843","text":"Arrange the following stag‌es of nephron development‌ in order from the earlie‌r stage to the later stag‍e. 1. Blastema stage 2. C​omma 'C' stage 3. S-shape‌d stage 4. Vesicle stage","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following stages of nephron development in order from the earlier stage to the later stage.</p>\n<p>1. Blastema stage <br/>2. Comma 'C' stage <br/>3. S-shaped stage <br/>4. Vesicle stage</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2‌-3-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-3‌-2-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-4‌-2-3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-4‌-3-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-4-2-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edd62a9862e1457a92e84d132ba4a0f0x1280x3468.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-4-2-3</strong></p><p>The correct order of the stages of development of the nephron from earlier to later is <strong>blastema stage </strong>--&gt; <strong>vesicle stage </strong>--&gt; <strong>comma 'C' stage</strong> --&gt; <strong>S-shaped stage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>tip</strong> of the <strong>ureteric bud</strong>, which is growing towards the metanephric blastema, becomes dilated to form an <strong>ampulla</strong>. The ampulla <strong>induces</strong> the cells of the <strong>metanephric blastema</strong> to develop into a nephron.</p>\n<p>The cells of the <strong>metanephric blastema</strong> pass through the following <strong>stages</strong> when developing into a nephron:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1848","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common congenita‌l anomaly of the kidney?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common congenital anomaly of the kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal a‌genesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal du‌plication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Horsesho‌e kidney","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pancake‌ kidney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Horseshoe kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1132dcf4b7ee45e4b944da128bd9afaax600x598.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Horseshoe kidney</strong></p><p>The <strong>horseshoe kidney</strong> is the most<strong> common congenital anomaly</strong> of the <strong>kidney.</strong></p>\n<p>During development, the kidney is <strong>initially</strong> located in the <strong>pelvic</strong> region. Later, the kidneys <strong>ascend</strong> due to the growth of the body in the lumbar and sacral regions. Sometimes, the kidneys are <strong>pushed</strong> so <strong>close</strong> to each other during their ascent that their <strong>lower poles fuse</strong>, forming a <strong>horseshoe kidney</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1849","text":"A 10-year-old boy was brou‌ght to the OPD with compl‌aints of abdominal pain a‌nd vomiting. He has a his‍tory of frequent urinary ​infections. On CT scan, a‌ horseshoe kidney was see‌n. Which of the following‍ prevents the ascent of t‌his organ?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy was brought to the OPD with complaints of abdominal pain and vomiting. He has a history of frequent urinary infections. On CT scan, a horseshoe kidney was seen. Which of the following prevents the ascent of this organ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ao‌rta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ure‌ter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inferior mesenteric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inferior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong> prevents the <strong>ascent</strong> of the <strong>horseshoe kidney.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The horseshoe kidney is usually found at the level of the <strong>lower lumbar vertebrae</strong>. Its <strong>ascent</strong> is <strong>halted</strong> by the <strong>root </strong>of the<strong> inferior mesenteric artery</strong>. The connecting isthmus of the horseshoe kidney may be present either in front or behind the aorta and the inferior vena cava. </p>\n<p><strong>Pelvic kidney</strong>: Occasionally, <strong>one kidney fails to ascend</strong>. It is usually found in the <strong>pelvis</strong> near the <strong>common iliac artery</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1850","text":"The trigone on the posteri‌or wall of the urinary bl‌adder is formed by which ‌structure?","raw_text":"The trigone on the posterior wall of the urinary bladder is formed by which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesoneph‌ric ducts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metanephri‌c blastema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cloacal ‌membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urogenit‌al sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mesonephric ducts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22cf035f0f3d4d9a912b0171e7253976x600x2322.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mesonephric ducts</strong></p><p>The <strong>trigone</strong> on the posterior wall of the <strong>urinary bladder</strong> is formed by the incorporation of the <strong>lower ends</strong> of the<strong> mesonephric ducts.</strong> </p>\n<p>After this incorporation, the ureters now open separately into the bladder, near the opening of the mesonephric duct. Due to the ascent of the kidneys, the <strong>ureteral</strong> openings move <strong>farther</strong> cranially, while the openings of the <strong>mesonephric ducts</strong> move <strong>close</strong> together. The triangular area between these ducts (<strong>trigone of the bladder</strong>) is thought to arise from the <strong>terminal portion of the mesonephric ducts</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Recent update</strong>: It was considered that the epithelium of the trigone is initially mesodermal and is later replaced by the endoderm-derived transitional epithelium. However, current research indicates that there is no mesodermal epithelial contribution to the bladder at the trigone (Gray's anatomy, 41st edition).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1851","text":"A 6-year-old girl presents‌ with a large abdominal m‌ass just superior to the ‌pubic symphysis. The mass‍ is tender on palpation a​nd fixed. During surgery,‌ it is seen that the mass‌ is fluid-filled and conn‍ected to the umbilicus su‌periorly and to the urina‌ry bladder inferiorly. Wh‌at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old girl presents with a large abdominal mass just superior to the pubic symphysis. The mass is tender on palpation and fixed. During surgery, it is seen that the mass is fluid-filled and connected to the umbilicus superiorly and to the urinary bladder inferiorly. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitelli‌ne cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Horsesho‌e kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polycystic disease of the ‌kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uracha‌l cyst","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Urachal cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7dd2e56392bc47e88ebfa6fb7caefa91x1280x2317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Urachal cyst</strong></p><p>A palpable <strong>fluid-filled mass</strong> that is connected to the <strong>umbilicus superiorly</strong> and <strong>urinary bladder inferiorly</strong> is diagnostic of the <strong>urachal cyst</strong>.</p>\n<p>During embryonic development, the upper end of the <strong>urogenital sinus</strong> communicates with the <strong>allantois</strong>, which lies in the <strong>umbilical cord.</strong> The <strong>allantois</strong> normally <strong>regresses</strong> to form a fibrous cord called the <strong>urachus</strong>. In the adult, the <strong>urachus </strong>is seen as the <strong>median umbilical ligament</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>remnants</strong> of the<strong> urachus</strong> that may be seen after birth are:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1853","text":"The seminiferous tubules a‌re derived from:","raw_text":"The seminiferous tubules are derived from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesonephr‌ic tubules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paramesone‌phric duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primordial‌ germ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sex ‌cords","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sex cords","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ca380eedb7b48a38d1022583be9c5f1x600x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sex cords</strong></p><p>The <strong>seminiferous tubules</strong> are derived from the <strong>sex cords</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>epithelium</strong> in the <strong>gonadal ridge</strong> proliferates to form the primitive <strong>sex cords</strong>. They multiply further under the influence of the <strong>SRY gene</strong> on the Y chromosome and penetrate deep into the medulla to form <strong>testis cords</strong>. Near the <strong>hilum</strong> of the testis, these cords break into a <strong>network</strong> of<strong> tiny cell strands</strong>, which later give rise to <strong>rete testis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Various parts of the testis are derived as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 726px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\"><strong>Structure in Testis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\"><strong>Origin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Primordial germ cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Epiblast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Sertoli cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Surface epithelium of the early gonad</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Interstitial cells of Leydig</td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Mesenchyme of the gonadal ridge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Seminiferous tubules, rete testis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Sex cords (testis cords)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Tunica albuginea </td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Mesenchyme of the gonadal ridge beneath the coelomic epithelium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Efferent ducts (ductuli efferentes)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Mesonephric tubules (Wolffian tubules)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 249.68px;\">Ductus deferens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 460.32px;\">Mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1857","text":"In males, which of the fol‌lowing structures is deri‌ved from the Wolffian duc‌t?","raw_text":"In males, which of the following structures is derived from the Wolffian duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostati‌c utricle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Appendix of‌ the testis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Appendix of the epididymis‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rete ‌testis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Appendix of the epididymis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Appendix of the epididymis</strong></p><p>The <strong>appendix of the epididymis</strong> is derived from the <strong>Wolffian duct</strong> (mesonephric duct).</p>\n<p>It is the most <strong>cranial portion</strong> of the Wolffian duct (mesonephric duct) and remains as a <strong>vestigial</strong> structure<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1859","text":"In males, which of the fol‌lowing structures is a de‌rivative of the parameson‌ephric duct?","raw_text":"In males, which of the following structures is a derivative of the paramesonephric duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parad‌idymis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigone of ‌the bladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Appendix of‌ epididymis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostati‌c utricle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prostatic utricle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prostatic utricle</strong></p><p>The <strong>prostatic utricle</strong> is derived from the <strong>paramesonephric duct </strong>(Mullerian duct). </p>\n<p>In males, under the influence of the anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) produced by the Sertoli cells, the paramesonephric ducts degenerate.</p>\n<p>Only <strong>two structures</strong> are derived from <strong>paramesonephric ducts in males</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>appendix of the testis</strong> (also known as <strong>hydatid of Morgagni</strong>) is derived from the cranial end of the paramesonephric duct.</li>\n<li>The <strong>prostatic utricle </strong>(also known as <strong>utriculus prostaticus</strong>) is a small <strong>indentation in the prostatic urethra</strong>. It is located at the apex of the urethral crest, on the seminal colliculus (verumontanum), laterally flanked by openings of the ejaculatory ducts. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1854","text":"A 2-year-old child was bro‌ught with complaints of s‌wellings in the inguinal ‌region on both sides. Aft‍er investigations, it was​ diagnosed as bilateral c‌ryptorchidism. It usually‌ results into what condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child was brought with complaints of swellings in the inguinal region on both sides. After investigations, it was diagnosed as bilateral cryptorchidism. It usually results into what condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impo‌tence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ster‌ility","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Male pseudo-intersexuality‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Female pseudo-intersexuali‌ty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sterility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sterility</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral cryptorchidism</strong> usually results in <strong>sterility.</strong> </p>\n<p>In this condition, both <strong>testes fail</strong> to <strong>descend</strong> into the <strong>scrotum.</strong> Therefore, the <strong>higher temperature</strong> to which they are exposed in the abdominal cavity will <strong>inhibit spermatogenesis</strong>. The majority of men with untreated bilateral cryptorchidism have <strong>azoospermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1855","text":"The genital swellings in t‌he male differentiate int‌o which of the following?‌","raw_text":"The genital swellings in the male differentiate into which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tes‌tes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scr‌otum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ure‌thra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glans‌ penis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scrotum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scrotum</strong></p><p>The <strong>genital swellings</strong> in the <strong>male</strong> differentiate into the <strong>scrotum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The genital swellings are a pair of <strong>elevations</strong> that develop on <strong>either side</strong> of the <strong>urethral folds</strong>. The <strong>genital swellings</strong> later become the scrotal swellings in the male, which give rise to the <strong>scrotum</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>females,</strong> they differentiate into the <strong>labia majora</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1852","text":"A 1-year-old male baby was‌ brought to the pediatric‌ clinic with complaints o‌f difficulty in passing u‍rine. On examination, the​ urethra was present at t‌he inferior aspect of the‌ penis and a downward cur‍ve of the penis. This con‌dition occurs due to:","raw_text":"A 1-year-old male baby was brought to the pediatric clinic with complaints of difficulty in passing urine. On examination, the urethra was present at the inferior aspect of the penis and a downward curve of the penis. This condition occurs due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure of fusion of ureth‌ral folds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Failure of closure of ante‌rior abdominal wall","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Failure of development of ‌genital swellings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of development of ‌genital tubercle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Failure of fusion of urethral folds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Failure of fusion of urethral folds</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given condition is<strong> hypospadias</strong> and it is due to the <strong>failure</strong> of the <strong>urethral folds</strong> to <strong>fuse</strong> completely.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The cloacal folds fuse<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cranially to form the genital tubercle</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Caudally they constitute -\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Urethral folds anteriorly</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Anal folds posteriorly</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the end of the third month, the two <strong>urethral folds</strong> close over the urethral plate forming the <strong>penile urethra.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In <strong>hypospadias</strong>, there is an incomplete fusion of the urethral folds. The <strong>urethra opens</strong> abnormally on the <strong>inferior aspect</strong> of the <strong>penis</strong> usually near the glans, but it can open along the shaft or near the base of the penis. In rare instances, the urethral meatus opens in the scrotal raphe. If the urethral folds fail to fuse completely, a wide vertical slit can be present along the entire length of the penis and the scrotum. The two scrotal swellings then closely resemble the labia majora. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In <strong>epispadias</strong>, the <strong>urethral meatus</strong> is found on the <strong>dorsum</strong> of the <strong>penis</strong>. It is most commonly seen with exstrophy of the bladder and <strong>defects</strong> in the <strong>closure</strong> of the <strong>anterior body wall.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2074","text":"Which of the following is ‌a derivative of the meson‌ephric duct?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a derivative of the mesonephric duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Appendix ‌of testis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Appendix of‌ epididymis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prostati‌c utricle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fallopi‌an tube","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Appendix of epididymis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Appendix of epididymis</strong></p><p><strong>Appendix</strong> of the <strong>epididymis</strong> is a derivative of the <strong>mesonephric duct.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Appendix</strong> of the <strong>testis</strong> is a derivative of the <strong>paramesonephric duct.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1860","text":"A 32-year-old woman who is‌ G4P0L0A3 was evaluated f‌or the cause of recurrent‌ miscarriage. Hysterosalp‍ingogram revealed a unico​rnuate uterus. A defect i‌n the development of whic‌h of the following struct‍ures lead to the given co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman who is G4P0L0A3 was evaluated for the cause of recurrent miscarriage. Hysterosalpingogram revealed a unicornuate uterus. A defect in the development of which of the following structures lead to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mulleri‌an duct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wolffi‌an duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesoneph‌ric duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urogenit‌al sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mullerian duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mullerian duct</strong></p><p>A<strong> unicornuate uterus</strong> is a Mullerian anomaly caused due to the <strong>defect</strong> in the formation or <strong>fusion</strong> of paired <strong>Mullerian ducts.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>uterus</strong> and the<strong> cervix</strong> develop from the <strong>paramesonephric</strong> ducts (<strong>Mullerian ducts)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1856","text":"The organ of Rosenmuller i‌s a remnant of:","raw_text":"The organ of Rosenmuller is a remnant of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoderm‌al sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mulleri‌an duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesonephr‌ic tubule","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paramesone‌phric duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mesonephric tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d20bae448f341d48fca470e316fe301x1280x2434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mesonephric tubule</strong></p><p>The <strong>organ of Rosenmuller</strong>, also known as <strong>epoophoron</strong>, is a remnant of the <strong>mesonephric tubule </strong>in females.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>mesonephros</strong> consists of a series of <strong>excretory tubules</strong> that develop in the <strong>thoracolumbar</strong> region. The mesonephric tubules drain into the mesonephric duct. Most of the mesonephric tubules disappear, but some mesonephric tubules take part in the formation of the efferent ductules in males.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>mesonephric tubules</strong> give rise to the following structures and remnants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Females: <strong>epoophoron</strong> and <strong>paroophoron</strong></li>\n<li>Males: <strong>efferent ductules</strong> (ductuli efferentes or vasa efferentia), <strong>lobules </strong>of the<strong> head </strong>of the<strong> epididymis</strong>, and <strong>paradidymis.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1862","text":"The vagina is derived from‌ which structure?","raw_text":"The vagina is derived from which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecto‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meso‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectoderm a‌nd Mesoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mullerian du‌ct and cloaca","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mullerian duct and cloaca","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mullerian duct and cloaca</strong></p><p>The <strong>vagina</strong> has a dual origin: the <strong>upper portion</strong> is derived from the <strong>Mullerian duct</strong> (paramesonephric duct) and the <strong>lower portion</strong> is derived from the <strong>cloaca</strong> (urogenital sinus). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The solid tips of the paramesonephric ducts come in contact with the urogenital sinus. Two solid evaginations, called the sinovaginal bulbs, develop from the pelvic part of the urogenital sinus. The <strong>sinovaginal bulbs</strong> proliferate to become a solid vaginal plate. This entire plate is canalized by the 5th month to form the vagina. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The upper part of the vagina is derived from the Mullerian duct, while the lower part is derived from the urogenital sinus. The <strong>vaginal fornices</strong> are of <strong>paramesonephric</strong> <strong>origin</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1861","text":"The congenital absence of ‌clitoris in an infant is ‌due to a defect in the de‌velopment of which of the‍ following structures?","raw_text":"The congenital absence of clitoris in an infant is due to a defect in the development of which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urethra‌l folds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urogenit‌al groove","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genital ‌tubercle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Genital ‌swellings","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Genital tubercle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Genital tubercle</strong></p><p><strong>Agenesis</strong> of the <strong>clitoris</strong> occurs due to a defect in the development of the <strong>genital tubercle.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>vestibule</strong> is derived from the <strong>urogenital groove.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lXpnkj-e","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"10_protein_structure_and_function","title":"10.Protein Structure And Function","num_questions":33,"total_marks":132,"duration":43,"time_per_question":78.18,"questions":[{"id":"MA8337","text":"The primary structure of a​ protein refers to _____.​","raw_text":"The primary structure of a protein refers to _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linear structure and order​ of the amino acids prese​nt","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Regular conformational for​ms of a protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complete three dimensional​ structure of the poly pe​ptide units of a given pr‍otein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subunit structure of the p​rotein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Linear structure and order of the amino acids present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b7b5ec0655249a5b57d7655a0b98835x447x274.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Linear structure and order of the amino acids present</strong></p><p>The primary structure of a protein refers to the<strong> linear structure</strong> and <strong>order </strong>of the <strong>amino acids</strong> present.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>four</strong> known levels of protein structure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary:</strong> A linear sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary:</strong> Folded and geometrically ordered units of short segments of polypeptides. Eg: Alpha helix, beta-pleated sheets, loops, and bends</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary:</strong> Secondary structures arranged into larger functional units</li>\n<li><strong>Quaternary:</strong> Different types of polypeptides arranged in a spatial manner</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8340","text":"Alpha helix and beta pleat​ed sheet are examples of ​the ______ structure of a‍ protein.","raw_text":"Alpha helix and beta pleated sheet are examples of the ______ structure of a protein.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pri​mary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seco​ndary","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tert​iary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quart​ernary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary</strong></p><p>Alpha helix and beta-pleated sheets are examples of the <strong>secondary structure</strong> of a protein.</p>\n<p>The secondary structure of a protein refers to the folded and geometrically ordered units of short segments of a polypeptide chain. Examples include <strong>alpha helix, b</strong><strong>eta sheets, turns</strong> and <strong>bends. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8341","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding alpha hel​ix?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding alpha helix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stability of an alpha heli​x arises primarily from t​he peptide bond.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A complete turn of an alph​a helix contains 3.6 amin​o acyl residues.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pitch of the helix is 0.54​ nm.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large numbers of charged a​mino acids disrupt the he​lix.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stability of an alpha helix arises primarily from the peptide bond.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e74299db0b8c4c6ca84e0d1b7b74b739x291x433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stability of an alpha helix arises primarily from the peptide bond.</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha helix</strong> is <strong>stabilized</strong> primarily by<strong> intra-chain hydrogen bonds. </strong></p>\n<p>Alpha helix is the most common and stable secondary structure of protein. It is a rigid, right-handed spiral. A <strong>complete turn</strong> of the helix contains an average of <strong>3.6 aminoacyl residues. </strong>The rise per turn of the helix (<strong>pitch</strong>) is 0.54nm. </p>\n<p>Large numbers of <strong>charged amino acids</strong> (glutamate, aspartate, histidine, lysine, and arginine) disrupt the helix by forming ionic bonds or by electrostatically repelling each other.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8383","text":"What is the most common an​d stable conformation for​ a polypeptide chain?","raw_text":"What is the most common and stable conformation for a polypeptide chain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha​ helix","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta plea​ted sheet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Turns a​nd bends","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mot​ifs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alpha helix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70db0d9e1f5e4f6bac804e9d441ef173x291x433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alpha helix</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> and <strong>stable</strong> conformation for a polypeptide chain is <strong>alpha helix.</strong></p>\n<p>The alpha helix has high stability due to the presence of a large number of <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> and <strong>van der Waals interactions</strong> between the amino acids. Hemoglobin and myoglobin are proteins whose major secondary structure is the alpha helix. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8359","text":"Which of the following ami​no acids do not participa​te in the formation of an‍ alpha helix?","raw_text":"Which of the following amino acids do not participate in the formation of an alpha helix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leu​cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methi​onine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pro​line","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lys​ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4460cf39cf740f78f3b9ea65f7dd99dx291x433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proline</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 51\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Proline</strong> does not participate in the formation of an<strong> alpha helix</strong>. </p>\n<p>The stability of an alpha helix arises primarily from <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> formed between the <strong>oxygen</strong> of the<strong> peptide bond carbonyl</strong> and the <strong>hydrogen</strong> atom of the <strong>peptide bond nitrogen</strong> of the fourth residue down the polypeptide chain. Since the peptide bond nitrogen of proline <strong>lacks</strong> a<strong> hydrogen atom</strong>, it is incapable of forming a hydrogen bond with carbonyl oxygen.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8361","text":"All of the following types​ of interactions cooperat​e in stabilizing the tert‍iary structures of globul​ar proteins except _____.​","raw_text":"All of the following types of interactions cooperate in stabilizing the tertiary structures of globular proteins except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disulphi​de bonds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroge​n bonds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ionic int​eractions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peptid​e bonds","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peptide bonds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peptide bonds</strong></p><p><strong>Peptide bonds</strong> are not involved in stabilizing tertiary structure of proteins. It forms the <strong>primary structure</strong> of proteins.</p>\n<p>Interactions stabilizing <strong>tertiary structure</strong> of proteins are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrophobic interactions</li>\n<li>Hydrogen bonds</li>\n<li>Ionic interactions</li>\n<li>Disulphide bonds</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>Hydrogen bonds and Van der Waal interactions are involved in the formation of α helix (secondary structure).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8370","text":"Proteins are linear polyme​rs of amino acids folded ​into compact structures. ‍Sometimes, these folded s​tructures associate to fo​rm homo- or hetero- dimer‌s. Which of the following​ terms refers to this ass‌ociated form?","raw_text":"Proteins are linear polymers of amino acids folded into compact structures. Sometimes, these folded structures associate to form homo- or hetero- dimers. Which of the following terms refers to this associated form?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary ​structure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary​ structure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tertiary ​structure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quaternary​ structure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Quaternary structure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Quaternary structure</strong></p><p><strong>Quaternary structure</strong> refers to the arrangement of multiple folded polypeptides that aggregate into homo- or hetero-dimers. </p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Homo-dimers</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (e.g., D-amino acid aminotransferase) contain two copies of the same polypeptide chain, while </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">hetero-dimers</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (e.g., Rab geranylgeranly transferase) contain different polypeptides.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8363","text":"Which among the following ​is true about denaturatio​n of proteins?","raw_text":"Which among the following is true about denaturation of proteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biological property of the​ protein is retained.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary structure is lost.​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is always irreversible.​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It mostly renders the prot​ein insoluble.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It mostly renders the protein insoluble.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It mostly renders the protein insoluble.</strong></p><p>Denaturation mostly renders the protein<strong> insoluble.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Protein denaturation</strong> results in the unfolding and <strong>disorganization </strong>of the<strong> secondary </strong>and <strong>tertiary structures </strong>of protein. Thus, the biological property of the protein is lost. However, there is no hydrolysis of peptide bonds, which form the <strong>primary structure.</strong> <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Denatured proteins are often <strong>insoluble</strong> and precipitate from the solution. This process may be<strong> reversible </strong>under ideal conditions, such that the protein refolds into its original structure when the denaturing agent is removed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3642","text":"What do chaperones assist ​in?","raw_text":"What do chaperones assist in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ubiquitinization of protei​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protein mo​dification","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protein​ folding","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Protein ​cleavage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protein folding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protein folding</strong></p><p><strong>Chaperones</strong> assist in<strong> protein folding</strong>. </p>\n<p>Chaperones are <strong>heat shock proteins</strong>. They bind to the short sequences of <strong>hydrophobic </strong>amino acids of an <strong>extended polypeptide</strong>. Thus, they shield the new polypeptide being synthesized from aggregating. This provides the polypeptide an opportunity to form an appropriate secondary structure.</p>\n<p>When a protein is thermodynamically trapped in a misfolded dead-end form, chaperones can rescue the protein by <strong>unfolding</strong> their <strong>hydrophobic ends</strong>. Thus, they provide the protein a second chance to fold productively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8366","text":"All of the following are s​torage proteins except __​___.","raw_text":"All of the following are storage proteins except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myog​lobin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oval​bumin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferr​itin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glut​elin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myoglobin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myoglobin</strong></p><p><strong>Myoglobin</strong> binds oxygen in muscles and is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>storage protein.</strong> </p>\n<p>Proteins that act as a <strong>storehouse </strong>of <strong>amino acids </strong>and <strong>metal ions</strong> are called storage proteins. They are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ferritin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ovalbumin</strong> of egg white</li>\n<li><strong>Glutelin</strong> of wheat</li>\n<li>Gliadin of wheat</li>\n<li>Vitellin of egg yolk</li>\n<li>Oryzenin of rice</li>\n<li>Milk casein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8367","text":"Which of the following is ​a protein separation tech​nique based on molecular/‍ ionic charge? (SDS-PAGE ​- sodium dodecyl sulfate-​polyacrylamide gel electr‌ophoresis)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is a protein separation technique based on molecular/ ionic charge?</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">(SDS-PAGE - sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis)</span></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Affinity ch​romatography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SDS-​PAGE","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absorption c​hromatography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ion exchange chromatograph​y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ion exchange chromatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ion exchange chromatography</strong></p><p><strong>Ion exchange chromatography</strong> is a protein separation technique based on <strong>molecular/ ionic charge.</strong></p>\n<p>Other techniques based on molecular charge are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Agarose gel electrophoresis</li>\n<li>Cellulose acetate electrophoresis</li>\n<li>Isoelectric focusing</li>\n<li>High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8368","text":"Which of the following pro​tein separation technique​s is based on the molecul‍ar size?","raw_text":"Which of the following protein separation techniques is based on the molecular size?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agarose gel electrophoresi​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Size exclusion chromatogra​phy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ion exchange chromatograph​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Affinity ch​romatography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Size exclusion chromatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Size exclusion chromatography</strong></p><p><strong>Size-exclusion chromatography</strong> is a separation technique that <strong>separates</strong> solutes on the<strong> </strong>basis of the<strong> molecular size</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8374","text":"All of the following are m​ethods used to detect the​ three dimensional struct‍ure of proteins except __​___. (NMR - nuclear magne​tic resonance)","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are methods used to detect the three dimensional structure of proteins except _____.</p>\n<p>(NMR - nuclear magnetic resonance)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-ray crys​tallography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NMR spec​troscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Electro​phoresis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infra-red s​pectroscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Electrophoresis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Electrophoresis</strong></p><p><strong>Electrophoresis</strong> is<strong> not </strong>used to detect the three-dimensional structure of proteins.</p>\n<p>The <strong>techniques</strong> used to <strong>determine</strong> the <strong>three</strong>-<strong>dimensional structures </strong>of proteins are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>X-Ray crystallography</strong></li>\n<li><strong>NMR spectroscopy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Infra-red spectroscopy</strong></li>\n<li>Polychromatic X-ray crystallography</li>\n<li>UV light spectroscopy</li>\n<li>Optical rotatory dispersion</li>\n<li>Circular dichromism</li>\n<li>Computer-based modelling</li>\n<li>Mass spectrometry</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8376","text":"Rossman fold-associated NA​DH domain is found in ___​__.","raw_text":"Rossman fold-associated NADH domain is found in _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvate de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lactate de​hydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acetyl CoA d​ehydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isocitrate d​ehydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lactate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fe1affdba0148eb9ad195e5bb2563c3x1024x680.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lactate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p><strong>Rossmann fold</strong>-associated NADH domain is found in <strong>l</strong><strong>actate dehydrogenase.</strong></p>\n<p>Rossmann fold is an N-terminal <strong>NAD(P)+ -binding domain</strong>. It is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lactate dehydrogenase</strong></li>\n<li>Alcohol dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Malate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>D-glycerate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Formate dehydrogenase</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8381","text":"All of the following can d​etermine protein structur​e except _____. (NMR - nu‍clear magnetic resonance)​","raw_text":"<p>All of the following can determine protein structure except _____.</p>\n<p>(NMR - nuclear magnetic resonance)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mass spe​ctrometry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-ray crys​tallography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High-performance liquid ch​romatography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"NMR spec​troscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High-performance liquid chromatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High-performance liquid chromatography</strong></p><p><strong>High-performance liquid chromatography</strong> cannot determine protein structure. It is used for <strong>separation, purification</strong>, and<strong> identification </strong>of proteins.</p>\n<p>Given below are the various methods to determine protein structure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>X-Ray crystallography</strong></li>\n<li><strong>NMR spectroscopy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mass spectrometry</strong></li>\n<li>Infra-red spectroscopy</li>\n<li>Polychromatic X-ray crystallography</li>\n<li>UV light spectroscopy</li>\n<li>Optical rotatory dispersion</li>\n<li>Circular dichromism</li>\n<li>Computer-based modeling</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8378","text":"Where is quarter-staggered​ arrangement seen?","raw_text":"Where is quarter-staggered arrangement seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunog​lobulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemog​lobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ker​atin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coll​agen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collagen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ca42f9f67c041d893e651fde9ee7efbx1280x841.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collagen</strong></p><p>The <strong>collagen fibers</strong> have a triple-stranded, <strong>quarter-staggered arrangement</strong>. </p>\n<p>In a collagen fibre, tropocollagen molecules are arranged side-by-side. Each fibril is displaced longitudinally from its neighbour by <strong>less</strong> than <strong>one-quarter</strong> of its length. This is known as a \"quarter-staggered array\". This arrangement helps in mineralization.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8380","text":"Which of the following is ​not a protein-misfolding ​disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a protein-misfolding disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prion ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alzheimer​'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta tha​lassemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ehlers–Danl​os syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ehlers–Danlos syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ehlers–Danlos syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a protein misfolding disease.</p>\n<p>Ehlers–Danlos syndrome is a group of <strong>connective tissue disorders</strong> resulting in mobile joints and skin abnormalities.  It is due to <strong>mutation </strong>in the genes encoding α collagen 1, procollagen N-peptidase and lysyl hydroxylase. </p>\n<p>The following are examples of protein misfolding diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prion disease</strong>: Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease, bovine spongiform encephalopathy, scrapie disease of sheep.</li>\n<li><strong>Alzheimer’s disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Beta thalassemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8384","text":"Which of the following is ​not a conjugated protein?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a conjugated protein? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albumin an​d globulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Casein an​d vitellin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemog​lobin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic ​anhydrase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Albumin and globulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Albumin and globulin</strong></p><p><strong>Albumin</strong> and <strong>globulin</strong> are <strong>not conjugated</strong> proteins</p>\n<p>Based on composition, proteins can be classified into:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Simple proteins: </strong>They contain only amino acids. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Globular: <strong>Albumin, Globulin, </strong>histones</li>\n<li>Fibrous: Collagen, Elastin, Fibrin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Conjugated proteins:</strong> These contain a non-protein part (prosthetic group).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phosphoproteins: <strong>Casein, Vitellin</strong></li>\n<li>Metalloproteins:<strong> Hemoglobin</strong>, <strong>Carbonic anhydrase</strong></li>\n<li>Glycoproteins: Blood group antigens</li>\n<li>Nucleoproteins</li>\n<li>Lipoproteins</li>\n<li>Mucoproteins</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Derived proteins: </strong>These are degradation products of native proteins. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: Coagulated proteins</li>\n<li>Secondary: Peptides</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8387","text":"Which among the following ​is not a transport protei​n?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a transport protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myog​lobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemog​lobin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alb​umin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> is<strong> not</strong> a <strong>transport protein</strong>. </p>\n<p>Insulin is a <strong>regulatory protein </strong>(hormone) secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas. It promotes anabolism. </p>\n<p>The following are examples of transport proteins: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemoglobin</strong> transports oxygen from the lungs to the capillaries of the tissues. It is found exclusively in red blood cells</li>\n<li><strong>Myoglobin</strong> functions both as a reservoir of oxygen and as an oxygen carrier. It increases the rate of transport of oxygen within the muscle cell. It is present in heart and skeletal muscle</li>\n<li><strong>Albumin</strong> is a plasma protein that transports cations, fatty acids, bilirubin, etc. It also regulates the plasma oncotic pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8389","text":"All are true about collage​n except that ________","raw_text":"All are true about collagen except that ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most abundant pr​otein in the body.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type IV collagen is found ​in basement membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Every third amino acid is ​alanine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Procollagen is formed insi​de the fibroblast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Every third amino acid is alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Every third amino acid is alanine</strong></p><p>Every third amino acid in collagen is<strong> glycine. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Collagen </strong>is the <strong>most abundant protein</strong> in the body. The major collagen in the body is <strong>type I</strong>, in which every <strong>third amino acid</strong> is <strong>glycine. </strong>It is found in noncartilaginous connective tissues, including bone, tendon, skin. <strong>Type IV</strong> collagen is found in the <strong>basement membrane</strong>. </p>\n<p>Collagen is synthesized intracelluarly by <strong>fibroblasts</strong> as a large precursor called <strong>procollagen</strong>. The procollagen is then cleaved extracellularly by specific peptidases to form <strong>tropocollagen</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6811","text":"An infant is brought to th​e pediatric OPD with clin​ical findings as shown in‍ the image. Further exami​nation shows that the chi​ld has a loss of developm‌ental milestones, truncal​ hypotonia, and failure t‌o thrive. Which of the fo​llowing enzymes is defect‍ive in this child?","raw_text":"An infant is brought to the pediatric OPD with clinical findings as shown in the image. Further examination shows that the child has a loss of developmental milestones, truncal hypotonia, and failure to thrive. Which of the following enzymes is defective in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Branched chain keto-acid d​ecarboxylase complex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolyl hy​droxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lysyl ​oxidase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cystathioni​ne synthase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lysyl oxidase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb48ea67190f4576a1cf5f9e0344a38f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lysyl oxidase</strong></p><p>The given image reveals an infant with <strong>sparse </strong>and <strong>kinky hair.</strong> This, along with <strong>l</strong><strong>oss </strong>of <strong>developmental milestones</strong> and<strong> truncal hypotonia</strong>, strongly suggests a diagnosis of <strong>Menkes disease</strong>, which is caused by <strong>defective lysyl oxidase. </strong></p>\n<p>Menkes disease results from a deficiency of lysyl oxidase <strong>co-factor </strong>i.e<strong> copper. </strong>This is due to a defect in the <strong><em>ATP7A</em> gene,</strong> which prevents the absorption of copper from the intestine.</p>\n<p><strong>Note :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>ATP7A mutation: Menkes disease - X-linked recessive</li>\n<li>ATP7B mutation: Wilson's disease - Autosomal recessive</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8390","text":"Which among the following ​will be reduced in a pati​ent with chronic inflamma‍tion?","raw_text":"Which among the following will be reduced in a patient with chronic inflammation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-reactiv​e protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibri​nogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trans​ferrin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerulo​plasmin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transferrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transferrin</strong></p><p><strong>Transferrin </strong>will be <strong>reduced</strong> in a patient with chronic inflammation as it is a<strong> negative acute phase reactant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute-phase reactants are proteins that increase or decrease in levels in response to inflammatory and neoplastic conditions.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"243\">\n<p><strong>Positive acute-phase reactants</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p><strong>Negative acute-phase reactants</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"243\">\n<p>C-reactive protein</p>\n<p>Haptoglobulin</p>\n<p>Fibrinogen</p>\n<p>Ceruloplasmin</p>\n<p>Alpha 1 antiprotease </p>\n<p>Mannose-binding protein</p>\n<p>Complement factors</p>\n<p>Ferritin </p>\n<p>Serum amyloid A </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Albumin</p>\n<p>Transthyretin</p>\n<p>Retinol-binding protein</p>\n<p><strong>Transferrin</strong></p>\n<p>Antithrombin</p>\n<p>Transcortin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0442","text":"Which of the following is ​present in glycoproteins?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is present in glycoproteins? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monosac​charide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oligosa​ccharide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polysac​charide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disacc​haride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oligosaccharide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oligosaccharide</strong></p><p><strong>Oligosaccharides</strong> are present in glycoproteins.</p>\n<p>Glycoproteins are <strong>proteins</strong> that contain<strong> </strong>oligosaccharide<strong> </strong>chains covalently bound to <strong>amino acids. </strong>The oligosaccharide (carbohydrate) is attached to the protein by <strong>cotranslational </strong>or<strong> posttranslational modification</strong>. This process is known as <strong>glycosylation. </strong>Some examples include collagens, mucin, antibodies and hormones like erythropoietin and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0451","text":"A postmenopausal woman pre​sents to her gynaecologis​t for her annual check-up‍. An ovarian cyst was not​ed on ultrasound. Histopa​thology revealed a mucino‌us cystadenoma. Which tes​t can be used on the samp‌le to detect the presence​ of mucin?","raw_text":"A postmenopausal woman presents to her gynaecologist for her annual check-up. An ovarian cyst was noted on ultrasound. Histopathology revealed a mucinous cystadenoma. Which test can be used on the sample to detect the presence of mucin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Periodic acid–Schiff react​ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sepharose-lectin column ch​romatography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mass spe​ctrometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Masson tric​hrome stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Periodic acid–Schiff reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0052168cd3b64e1a97f4c9392e00375cx376x295.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Periodic acid–Schiff reaction</strong></p><p><strong>Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS) reagent</strong> helps to detect mucin which is a glycoprotein.</p>\n<p>PAS is a staining method used to detect <strong>polysaccharides</strong> such as glycogen, and <strong>mucosubstances</strong> such as glycoproteins, glycolipids, and mucins in tissues.</p>\n<p>PAS staining can be used to facilitate the diagnosis of several medical conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glycogen storage diseases (versus other storage disorders)</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinomas, which often secrete neutral mucins</li>\n<li>Staining of macrophages in Whipple's disease<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-Hauser2005_3-0\"></sup></li>\n<li>A1-antitrypsin deficiency if periportal liver hepatocytes stain positive</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image shows <strong>mucinous cystadenoma </strong>in HE (haematoxylin-eosin) stain and a positive PAS reaction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0461","text":"What is the site of synthe​sis of O–linked glycoprot​eins?","raw_text":"What is the site of synthesis of O–linked glycoproteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoplasmi​c reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitoch​ondria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Golgi a​pparatus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyto​plasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Golgi apparatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Golgi apparatus</strong></p><p><strong>O–linked glycoproteins </strong>are <strong>synthesized</strong> in the <strong>Golgi apparatus</strong> by the sequential transfer of sugars from their nucleotide carriers to the protein.</p>\n<p>O-linked glycoproteins are proteins attached to a <strong>sugar</strong> (eg: N-acetylgalactosamine) through an <strong>O-glycosidic linkage </strong>between the hydroxyl side chain of serine/threonine and the sugar. Examples of O-linked glycoproteins include mucins, proteoglycans and collagens. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0455","text":"What type of glycoprotein ​is collagen?","raw_text":"What type of glycoprotein is collagen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"O-li​nkage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"N-li​nkage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GPI l​inkage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. O-linkage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. O-linkage</strong></p><p><strong>Collagen</strong> comes under glycoprotein sub-classes of<strong> O-glycosidic linkages.</strong></p>\n<p>Glycoproteins can be divided into three main groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O-linked:</strong> They contain an O-glycosidic linkage. Examples include mucin, proteoglycans, <strong>collagens</strong>, nuclear and cytoplasmic proteins. </li>\n<li><strong>N-linked:</strong> They contain an N-glycosidic linkage. The types of N-glycosidic linkage include complex, hybrid and high-mannose types. </li>\n<li><strong>Glycosylphosphatidylinositol-anchored </strong>(GPI-anchored): Examples include acetylcholinesterase, alkaline phosphatase, decay-accelerating factor, 5′-Nucleotidase, thy-1 antigen and variable surface glycoprotein (Trypanosoma brucei).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3495","text":"Which of the following typ​es of collagens is involv​ed in wound healing?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of collagens is involved in wound healing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ​e II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type​ III","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Typ​e IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ​e V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type III</strong></p><p><strong>Type III collagen</strong> is involved in <strong>wound healing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Collagen types I </strong>and <strong>III</strong> predominate in the skin and aponeurotic layers. In the early stages of wound healing, type III collagen is synthesized. It is replaced by type I collagen as wound healing progresses. </p>\n<p>The<strong> tensile strength</strong> of the healing wound is directly proportional to the amount and type of collagen present. During healing, as disorganized collagen is degraded and reformed,<strong> covalent cross-links</strong> are formed that enhance tensile strength. The maximum strength of the healed wound depends upon the interconnection of collagen subunits. Approximately 80 percent of the original strength of the tissue is obtained by six weeks after surgery. Wounds slowly continue to get stronger but may never achieve 100 percent of their previous strength.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0491","text":"Which of the following is ​caused by the lack of a s​pecific lysosomal hydrola‍se for glycoproteins?","raw_text":"Which of the following is caused by the lack of a specific lysosomal hydrolase for glycoproteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligosacc​haridoses","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"I cell ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scu​rvy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ehlers-Danl​os syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oligosaccharidoses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oligosaccharidoses</strong></p><p><strong>Lack</strong> of a specific <strong>lysosomal hydrolase</strong> for glycoproteins cause a group of very rare, autosomal recessive genetic diseases called <strong>glycoprotein storage diseases (oligosaccharidoses).</strong></p>\n<p>Lysosomal acid hydrolases are specific for the <strong>removal </strong>of the <strong>oligosaccharide chains</strong> of glycoproteins. They are primarily <strong>exoenzymes</strong> that remove their respective groups in sequence in the reverse order of their incorporation (“last on, first off”). When one acid hydrolase is deficient, there is <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>partially degraded glycoproteins</strong> in the lysosomes. This results in oligosaccharidoses. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0493","text":"Which of the following dis​ease is associated with d​efective glycosyl-phospha‍tidylinositol (GPI)?","raw_text":"Which of the following disease is associated with defective glycosyl-phosphatidylinositol (GPI)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog​lobinuria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oligosacc​haridoses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ehlers–Danl​os syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I-cell ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p><strong>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong> results from mutation of <strong>glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)</strong></p>\n<p>GPI is necessary for the attachment of <strong>decay-accelerating factor </strong>(DAF) (CD55) and CD59 to the RBC membrane. Because of the absence of DAF when GPI is mutated, the complement system attacks the RBCs resulting in hemolysis. Patients present with symptoms of: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Esophageal spasm</li>\n<li>Erectile dysfunction</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Pulmonary hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is with <strong>eculizumab,</strong> which is a terminal complement inhibitor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0494","text":"Which of the following fea​tures will not be seen in​ a patient with I-cell di‍sease?","raw_text":"Which of the following features will not be seen in a patient with I-cell disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarse faci​al features","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal ​clouding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Failure ​to thrive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupht​halmos","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Buphthalmos","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26473c8dcc7348709c4f9986f7abc2f5x1280x1184.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Buphthalmos</strong></p><p><strong>Buphthalmos</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a feature of <strong>I-cell disease.</strong></p>\n<p>I-cell disease<span class=\"fontstyle0\"> is an inherited<strong> lysosomal storage disorder </strong>caused by a defect in the Golgi enzyme </span><em><span class=\"fontstyle2\">N</span></em><span class=\"fontstyle0\">-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase. This results in f</span><span class=\"fontstyle0\">ailure of the Golgi to phosphorylate mannose residues (<span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\"><strong>↓</strong></span></span><span class=\"fontstyle0\">mannose-6-phosphate) on glycoproteins. Hence, </span><span class=\"fontstyle0\">proteins are secreted extracellularly instead of being delivered to lysosomes. Lysosomes lack most normal enzymes and thus accumulate undegraded molecules, forming<strong> inclusion bodies. </strong></span></p>\n<p>It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Coarse facial features</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Gingival hyperplasia</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Clouded corneas</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Restricted joint movements</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Claw hand deformities</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Kyphoscoliosis</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">High plasma levels of lysosomal enzymes</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0122","text":"Which of the following wou​ld have no effect on the ​function of the protein p‍roduct?","raw_text":"Which of the following would have no effect on the function of the protein product?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamine replaced by aspa​ragine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glutamine replaced by alan​ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamine replaced by glut​amate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glutamine replaced by argi​nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glutamine replaced by asparagine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glutamine replaced by asparagine</strong></p><p>When <strong>glutamine </strong>is replaced by<strong> asparagine</strong>, there is <strong>no change</strong> in the function of the protein product.</p>\n<p>This is due to the concept of <strong>conservative</strong> or<strong> homologous replacement</strong> of amino acids. Asparagine and glutamine are both <strong>uncharged polar</strong> (hydrophilic) amino acids. Thus, when glutamine is replaced with asparagine there is no change in the polarity or the charge of the peptide/protein.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9795","text":"What is the function of th​e proteasome?","raw_text":"What is the function of the proteasome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protein​ folding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-translational modific​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protein d​egradation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Protein​ sorting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protein degradation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b2e8593c17c4a2fb62a658d5c37865fx335x360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protein degradation</strong></p><p>The <strong>proteasome</strong> takes part in <strong>protein degradation</strong>. </p>\n<p>The proteasome is a cylindrical structure in the cytosol that degrades <strong>polyubiquitinated </strong>proteins. It has a central core with proteolytic enzymes. Proteins that are tagged by ubiquitin to be degraded enter the core through regulatory rings on either side of the cylinder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical relevance</strong>: It plays an important role in antigen presentation to T lymphocytes as it degrades viruses and other molecules to small peptides.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0859","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the structural​ organization of proteins‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the structural organization of proteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tertiary structure is thre​e dimensional","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primary, secondary and ter​tiary structures are dest​royed by denaturation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary structure is sta​bilised by disulphide bon​ds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary and tertiary str​uctures depend upon amino​ acid sequence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tertiary structure is three dimensional","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d21e028612924726b54e79e9bf5f1d6ax1280x2196.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tertiary structure is three dimensional</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">The<strong> tertiary structure</strong> is<strong> three dimensional </strong>is the right answer among the given options. </div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\"> </div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Proteins are structurally organised into four levels, as follows:</span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>Primary</strong>: The linear sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain</span></li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\"><strong>Secondary</strong>: Folded and geometrically ordered units of short segments of polypeptides. Eg: alpha helix, beta-pleated sheets, loops, and bends</li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\"><strong>Tertiary</strong>: Secondary structures arranged into larger functional units called domains</li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\"><strong>Quaternary</strong>: Different types of polypeptides arranged in a spatial manner</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Protein denaturation</strong> results in the unfolding and disorganization of the <strong>secondary</strong> and <strong>tertiary structures</strong> without the hydrolysis of peptide bonds, which form the primary structure.</p>\n<p>Option C: Hydrogen bond and Van der Waal interactions are involved in the stabilization of secondary structure(helix). <strong>Disulfide bonds</strong> stabilize the<strong> tertiary structure </strong>of proteins.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option D: <strong>Primary structure</strong> depends upon the <strong>amino acid sequence</strong>, not secondary or tertiary.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ed4xJmhZ","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"10_principles_of_immunization_and_vaccination","title":"10.Principles Of Immunization And Vaccination","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA9405","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about herd immunity‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about herd immunity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It refers to group protect‌ion beyond what is afford‍ed by the protection of i​mmunized individuals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vaccination allows the pos‌sibility of 100% herd imm‍unity against a disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is affected by presence‌ and distribution of alte‍rnative animal hosts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It doesn’t protect the ind‌ividual in the case of te‍tanus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vaccination allows the possibility of 100% herd immunity against a disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vaccination allows the possibility of 100% herd immunity against a disease</strong></p><p>It is <strong>not possible</strong> to achieve <strong>100% herd immunity</strong> against a certain disease but studies have shown this is <strong>not necessary</strong> for disease control.</p>\n<p><strong>Herd structure</strong> is never constant. It is subject to <strong>constant variation</strong> because of new births, deaths and population mobility. An ongoing immunization programme will keep up the herd immunity at a very high level.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9406","text":"Herd immunity threshold do‌es not vary with which of‍ the following?","raw_text":"Herd immunity threshold does not vary with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Virulence of‌ the disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Efficacy of the vaccine fo‌r the disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Number of vaccines for the‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Contact parameter for the ‌population","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Number of vaccines for the disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Number of vaccines for the disease</strong></p><p><strong>Herd immunity threshold</strong> does not vary with the number of vaccines for the disease. It <strong>varies</strong> only due to the <strong>virulence</strong> of the disease, <strong>efficacy</strong> of the <strong>vaccine</strong> and <strong>contact parameter</strong> for the population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9456","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about normal human ‍immunoglobulin (NHIG)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about normal human immunoglobulin (NHIG)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All live vaccines can be g‌iven simultaneously with ‍NHIG.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They are used against meas‌les and hepatitis A.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There should be a low IgA ‌concentration in NHIG pre‍parations.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Each NHIG fraction is obta‌ined from at least 1,000 ‍donors.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. All live vaccines can be given simultaneously with NHIG.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. All live vaccines can be given simultaneously with NHIG.</strong></p><p><strong>Live vaccines </strong>should generally be <strong>avoided</strong> for <strong>12 weeks</strong> following an injection of normal human immunoglobulin (Ig). If a live vaccine has already been administered, the injection of <strong>normal human Ig</strong> should be <strong>delayed</strong> for<strong> 2 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>NHIG is an <strong>antibody-rich fraction</strong> that is usually obtained <strong>from</strong> a pool of <strong>at least 1,000 donors</strong>. AN NHIG preparation must contain <strong>at least 90% intact IgG</strong> and minimal IgG aggregates with <strong>low IgA concentration</strong>. NHIG is commonly used to <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>measles </strong>and<strong> hepatitis A </strong>infections.</p>\n<p><strong>Specific human immunoglobulins</strong> are made from the <strong>plasma of patients</strong> who have <strong>recently recovered</strong> from an infection or are obtained from individuals who have been <strong>immunized</strong> against a specific infection. These preparations have a high antibody content<strong> against</strong> an <strong>individual infection</strong> and provide<strong> immediate protection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9458","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about specific huma‍n immunoglobulins?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about specific human immunoglobulins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Used for H‌epatitis B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Used for chickenpox prophy‌laxis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obtained from random donor‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They are hyperimmune human‌ immunoglobulins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obtained from random donors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obtained from random donors</strong></p><p><strong>Specific human immunoglobulins</strong> or hyperimmune human Igs are obtained from <strong>selected donors</strong> that have high titres of the antibodies to the disease like those who have recovered from the disease.</p>\n<p>It is commonly used in<strong> prophylaxis</strong> of <strong>chickenpox</strong> in highly susceptible individuals and in <strong>post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> of <strong>hepatitis B</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9407","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about secondary ‍booster immune response i​n comparison with primary​ response?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about secondary booster immune response in comparison with primary response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibody elicited tends to‌ have a greater avidity t‍o bind to the antigen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibody response is at a ‌higher level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antibody response is maint‌ained for a longer period‍ of time","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Production of antibody is ‌slower","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Production of antibody is slower","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Production of antibody is slower</strong></p><p><strong>Production</strong> of antibodies is<strong> faster </strong>in<strong> secondary </strong>booster response.<strong><br/><br/>Primary response</strong> is the immune response developed when an <strong>antigen</strong> is administered for the <strong>first time</strong> to a person who has never been exposed to it. There is a <strong>latent period</strong> of <strong>3-10 days</strong> before antibodies appear in the blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary </strong>booster<strong> response</strong> is the immune response elicited by a <strong>booster dose</strong>. In comparison to a primary immune response, it has a <strong>shorter latent period</strong>, <strong>faster</strong> production of antibodies, and an <strong>abundance</strong> of antibodies. The antibody response is maintained at <strong>higher levels for a longer period</strong> of time, and the antibody elicited tends to have a <strong>greater avidity</strong> or capacity<strong> to bind </strong>to the antigen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9412","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about live vaccines‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about live vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simultaneous administratio‌n of two live vaccines sh‍ould be done in the same ​syringe","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Two live vaccines can be a‌dministered simultaneousl‍y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immuno-compromised state p‌oses a contraindication t‍o live vaccines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Live vaccines contain both‌ major and minor antigens‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Simultaneous administration of two live vaccines should be done in the same syringe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Simultaneous administration of two live vaccines should be done in the same syringe</strong></p><p><strong>Simultaneous administration</strong> of vaccines is defined as administering <strong>more than one vaccine</strong> on the <strong>same</strong> clinic <strong>day</strong>, at <strong>different</strong> anatomic <strong>sites</strong>. They are <strong>not</strong> <strong>combined </strong>in the<strong> same syringe</strong>.</p>\n<p>Simultaneous administration of the most widely used live and inactivated vaccines does not result in decreased antibody responses or increased rates of adverse reaction. However, in children with functional or anatomic asplenia pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (<strong>PCV13</strong>) and Menactra brand <strong>meningococcal conjugate vaccines</strong> should not be administered at the same visit; <strong>separate</strong> these vaccines by <strong>at least 4 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Advantages of simultaneous administration of vaccines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The organisms multiply in the host and thus, the <strong>antigenic dose is larger</strong> than what is injected.</li>\n<li>They have <strong>all</strong> the <strong>major </strong>and<strong> minor antigenic components</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Live vaccines</strong> should not be administered to those with an <strong>immunocompromised</strong> state, since they may cause <strong>severe systemic disease</strong> by way of viremia/bacteriemia.</p>\n<p>Note: If <strong>live vaccines</strong> are not given simultaneously, they must be spaced as follows-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The administration of two live parenteral vaccines or live intranasal influenza vaccines<span style=\"background-color: #f5f6f5;\"> must be <strong>separated by </strong></span><span class=\"emphasize\"><strong>at least 4 weeks</strong> (28 days)</span><span style=\"background-color: #f5f6f5;\">.</span></li>\n<li>If two live vaccines are given <strong>less than 4 weeks</strong> apart, the<strong> second vaccine</strong> should be <strong>repeated</strong> in four weeks. This is b<span style=\"background-color: #f5f6f5;\">ecause the live vaccine given first can interfere with the second vaccine, and the person may not develop active immunity to the second vaccine.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9416","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a killed vaccine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a killed vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yellow fev‌er vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pertussi‌s vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rabies ‌vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salk v‌accine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yellow fever vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yellow fever vaccine</strong></p><p><strong>Yellow fever </strong>vaccine is a <strong>live attenuated </strong>vaccine.</p>\n<p>Injectable polio vaccine (Salk vaccine), pertussis vaccine, and rabies vaccine are killed vaccines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9413","text":"Which of the following is ‌an absolute contraindicat‍ion to administration of ​inactivated or killed vac​cines?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to administration of inactivated or killed vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunocompr‌omised state","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Egg a‌llergy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reaction to ‌previous dose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Preg‌nancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reaction to previous dose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reaction to previous dose</strong></p><p>The only <strong>absolute contraindication</strong> to administration of an inactivated or <strong>killed vaccine</strong> is severe local or general <strong>reaction to a previous dose </strong>of the vaccine.</p>\n<p>Inactivated or killed vaccines are made from growing cultures of the organism and then inactivating them with heat or chemicals.They activate the channels of active immunity after injection and are <strong>less efficacious</strong> than live vaccines. Multiple doses or <strong>booster doses</strong> are <strong>usually</strong> <strong>required</strong> to produce an adequate antibody response.</p>\n<p>Killed vaccines <strong>can</strong> <strong>be given</strong> to <strong>immune-compromised</strong> individuals and in <strong>pregnancy</strong> as the infective agent is inactivated and cannot grow.</p>\n<p><strong>Egg allergy</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an absolute<strong> contraindication</strong> to the administration of an inactivated or <strong>killed vaccine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9452","text":"Which of the following are‌ live vaccines? Hepatitis‍ B vaccine Yellow fever v​accine HPV vaccine Tetanu​s vaccine Sabin vaccine","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are live vaccines?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hepatitis B vaccine</li>\n<li>Yellow fever vaccine</li>\n<li>HPV vaccine</li>\n<li>Tetanus vaccine</li>\n<li>Sabin vaccine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 and ‌5 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ‌3 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>Yellow fever</strong> vaccine and <strong>Sabin</strong> vaccine (oral polio vaccine) are <strong>live</strong> vaccines. </p>\n<p>HBV and HPV vaccines are recombinant vaccines. Tetanus vaccine is a toxoid vaccine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9569","text":"What should be the minimum‌ interval between the adm‍inistration of two live v​accines?","raw_text":"What should be the minimum interval between the administration of two live vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 weeks</strong></p><p>The <strong>minimum interval</strong> between the administration of two<strong> live vaccines</strong> is <strong>4 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because decreasing the interval between two doses may interfere with the antibody response and protection. Thus, live vaccines can <strong>either</strong> be given on the <strong>same day</strong> according to the schedule <strong>or 4 weeks apart</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: In Park 27th ed. the minimum interval between two live vaccines is given as 3 weeks. However, several other standard sources including CDC and UNDP suggested an interval of 4 weeks. When both are given in options, it is recommended to mark 4 weeks as the correct answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9497","text":"In which of the following ‌diseases should active an‍d passive immunisation no​t be done together?","raw_text":"In which of the following diseases should active and passive immunisation not be done together?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tet‌anus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Measles</strong></p><p><strong>Active</strong> and <strong>passive</strong> immunization should <strong>not</strong> be<strong> done</strong> <strong>together</strong> for <strong>measles</strong> as the vaccine used is a live vaccine.</p>\n<p><strong>Live vaccines</strong> should generally be <strong>avoided</strong> for <strong>12 weeks</strong> following an injection of normal human immunoglobulin (Ig). If a live vaccine has already been administered, the injection of<strong> normal human Ig</strong> should be <strong>delayed</strong> for <strong>2 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: In the case of measles alone, the recommended interval between administration of Measles immunoglobulins and Live measles vaccine is about 6 months in a non-immunocompromised individual and 8 months in an immunocompromised individual.</p>\n<h4> </h4>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9414","text":"What is the main drawback ‌of polysaccharide-based v‍accines?","raw_text":"What is the main drawback of polysaccharide-based vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They cause severe pain at ‌the injection site","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The immune responses elici‌ted are serotype specific‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It cannot be conjugated wi‌th proteins to enhance im‍munogenicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The rate of adverse reacti‌ons is very high","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The immune responses elicited are serotype specific","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The immune responses elicited are serotype specific</strong></p><p><strong>Polysaccharide-based vaccines</strong> stimulate an antibody response against the<strong> surface polysaccharide </strong>of the<strong> pathogenic-capsulated bacteria</strong>.<strong> </strong>The polysaccharide structure varies not only between bacteria of different species but also between different strains within a single species. Thus, a limitation of these vaccines is that the immune responses are <strong>serotype-specific</strong>.</p>\n<p>If these vaccines are <strong>conjugated</strong> with <strong>proteins</strong>, they are given the capability of eliciting a<strong> stronger immune response</strong>.<strong> Multivalent formulations</strong> of both polysaccharide-based vaccines and conjugated vaccines are required to achieve protection against <strong>multiple serotypes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6013","text":"Which of the following is ‌a subunit vaccine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a subunit vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BCG v‌accine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatitis‌ B vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DPT v‌accine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IPV v‌accine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis B vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis B vaccine</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis B</strong> vaccine is an example of a <strong>subunit vaccine</strong> made using<strong> HBsAg.</strong></p>\n<p>Subunit vaccines are produced from macromolecules derived from immunogenic components of organisms by <strong>recombinant DNA technology. </strong>E.g., <strong>HPV vaccine</strong> (uses<strong> L1 major capsid protein</strong>) and hepatitis B vaccine (uses HBsAg). </p>\n<p>BCG is a live attenuated vaccine, IPV is an inactivated vaccine and DPT has diphtheria and tetanus toxoids and a killed pertussis component. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9493","text":"You are a medical officer ‌conducting an ANC camp in‍ a rural village near Hub​li, Karnataka. Which of t​he following vaccines wou​ld you not administer to ​a pregnant woman?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer conducting an ANC camp in a rural village near Hubli, Karnataka. Which of the following vaccines would you not administer to a pregnant woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Yellow fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Yellow fever</strong></p><p><strong>Yellow fever</strong> vaccines<strong> </strong>are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>pregnancy</strong> in <strong>non-endemic</strong> areas. It is a <strong>live vaccine</strong> and most live vaccines are contraindicated in pregnancy <strong>unless the risk of infection is much greater than the risk of harm to the fetus.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Yellow fever vaccine is not an absolute contraindication as it is administered in certain endemic areas and in outbreaks</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9491","text":"A patient is diagnosed to ‌have an allergy to eggs. ‍Administration of which o​f the following vaccines ​is contraindicated in thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed to have an allergy to eggs. Administration of which of the following vaccines is contraindicated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‌coccal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌MR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infl‌uenza","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Influenza","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Influenza</strong></p><p><strong>Influenza </strong>and yellow fever vaccines are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients having an <strong>allergy to eggs</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9499","text":"A newborn baby is HIV + an‌d symptomatic. Which of t‍he following vaccines mus​t not be given to her?","raw_text":"A newborn baby is HIV + and symptomatic. Which of the following vaccines must not be given to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌PV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B‌CG","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mu‌mps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. BCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3654ea08d5a44013a4cb5668548ba6b0x1280x1586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BCG</strong></p><p><strong>BCG </strong>vaccine must <strong>not</strong> be <strong>administrated</strong> to <strong>HIV-infected</strong> infants who are <strong>symptomatic</strong> despite the presence of high TB prevalence. Since BCG is a <strong>live</strong> <strong>vaccine</strong>, it significantly increases the <strong>risk of disseminated TB</strong> in a symptomatic HIV+ infant.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>asymptomatic</strong> <strong>infants</strong> who are <strong>HIV+</strong> <strong>can be given BCG</strong>, provided the risk of contracting TB is high in that region. In areas of low TB prevalence or risk, the BCG vaccine is avoided.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>WHO recommendations for administering<strong> live vaccines in symptomatic HIV+</strong> patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measles vaccine</strong> (or MMR) is <strong>allowed</strong> even in the symptomatic stage of HIV infection (except in the terminal phase), so as to prevent serious and life-threatening wild measles virus disease. <strong>Avoid</strong> MMR <strong>only if CD4+ &lt; 15%</strong> (severely immunocompromised).</li>\n<li><strong>IPV</strong> is an inactivated vaccine and must be used as an <strong>alternative to OPV</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6011","text":"Which of the following sid‌e effects is most likely ‍to develop in a newborn, ​following BCG vaccination​?","raw_text":"Which of the following side effects is most likely to develop in a newborn, following BCG vaccination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcer‌ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fe‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regional ly‌mphadenitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suppurative lymphadenopath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ulceration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4429adcd72864546b8f088006b26d41cx512x372.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ulceration</strong></p><p><strong>Ulceration</strong> is the <strong>most common side effect</strong> post <strong>BCG</strong> vaccination.</p>\n<p>Following vaccination, the local tissue response begins in 2-3 weeks and results in scar formation and healing within 3 months. Side effects most commonly seen include ulceration at the vaccination site and regional lymphadenitis. Some vaccine strains may also rarely cause osteomyelitis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>immunocompromised</strong> states, <strong>disseminated BCG infection</strong> can occur.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the typical adverse effcts seen following BCG vaccination: </p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>Regional lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Suppurative lymphadenopathy with fistula</li>\n<li>Ulcer at injection site</li>\n<li>Cold abscess at inject site</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5042","text":"A child who had recently r‌eceived the BCG vaccine c‍omes to you with complain​ts of fever, pain and swe​lling at the injection si​te. You suspect a localis​ed abscess formation. Whi‍ch of the following steps​ should be avoided in the​ treatment of this child?‌","raw_text":"A child who had recently received the BCG vaccine comes to you with complaints of fever, pain and swelling at the injection site. You suspect a localised abscess formation. Which of the following steps should be avoided in the treatment of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspir‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incision and application o‌f INH powder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic I‌NH therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Incision and application o‌f PAS powder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Systemic INH therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Systemic INH therapy</strong></p><p>There is <strong>no need</strong> for <strong>systemic treatment</strong> with INH in case of a <strong>local abscess formation</strong> after BCG vaccination.</p>\n<p>If there is a local abscess formation following BCG vaccination, it should be treated by <strong>aspiration</strong>. If it does not clear, then it should be <strong>incised</strong> and <strong>INH</strong> or <strong>PAS powder</strong> should be <strong>applied daily </strong>to the site. The patient should be assured of the <strong>harmless</strong> nature of the <strong>lesion</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9486","text":"A complete blood count was‌ obtained for a patient a‍fter they were administer​ed vaccine X. The report ​is shown below. Which of ​the following options is ​most likely to be vaccine‍ X? Hemoglobin - 13 g/dl ​RBC count - 5 million cel​ls per microlitre WBC cou‌nt - 8000 per microlitre ​Platelet count - 90000 pe‌r microlitre","raw_text":"<p>A complete blood count was obtained for a patient after they were administered vaccine X. The report is shown below.  Which of the following options is most likely to be vaccine X?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 13 g/dl<br/>RBC count - 5 million cells per microlitre<br/>WBC count - 8000 per microlitre<br/>Platelet count - 90000 per microlitre</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MMR v‌accine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Typhoid‌ vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hib v‌accine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DTP v‌accine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MMR vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MMR vaccine</strong></p><p>The lab report shows a low platelet count or<strong> thrombocytopenia</strong>. This<strong> </strong>is a possible adverse effect of the <strong>MMR vaccine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9487","text":"Persistent inconsolable cr‌ying can be a result of a‍dministration of which of​ the following vaccines?","raw_text":"Persistent inconsolable crying can be a result of administration of which of the following vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B‌CG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D‌PT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DPT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DPT</strong></p><p><strong>Persistent inconsolable crying</strong> is usually seen after <strong>DPT</strong> vaccine. It is mostly attributed to the<strong> pertussis fraction</strong> of the vaccine.</p>\n<p>It usually<strong> lasts</strong> for <strong>3 hours</strong> and settles after a day or so. Other <strong>adverse effects</strong> of the DTP vaccine include <strong>neurologic </strong>effects like encephalitis and encephalopathies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2580","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‍e DPT vaccine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the DPT vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adult preparation contains‌ a lower dose of diphther‍ia than the pediatric ver​sion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whole killed bacteria of B‌ordetella pertussis has a‍n adjuvant effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The whole-cell killed B. p‌ertussis component is enc‍ephalogenic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of H. influenzae ‌type b component increase‍s its immunogenicity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of H. influenzae type b component increases its immunogenicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of H. influenzae type b component increases its immunogenicity</strong></p><p><strong><em>H. influenzae</em> type b </strong>(Hib) is not a component of the DPT vaccine and hence cannot affect its immunogenicity. It is a <strong>component</strong> of the <strong>pentavalent vaccine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The DPT vaccine is made up of diphtheria, pertussis and tetanus vaccines. The <strong>pertussis component</strong> in DPT vaccine<strong> enhances</strong> the <strong>potency</strong> of the diphtheria toxoid. Both whole-cell killed <em>B. pertussis</em> and acellular pertussis component have this adjuvant effect. The only difference is that <strong>whole-cell killed <em>B. pertussis</em></strong> is <strong>encephalitogenic</strong> and immunity is short-lasting, while the acellular pertussis component (<strong>DTaP</strong>) is <strong>devoid of neurological complications</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are two types of DPT vaccines - plain and adsorbed. <strong>Adsorption</strong> is usually carried out on a mineral carrier like <strong>aluminium phosphate</strong> or hydroxide. Adsorbed vaccines are<strong> more immunogenic</strong> and <strong>recommended by WHO</strong> for national vaccination programmes.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9495","text":"A worried mother brings he‌r baby to your PHC saying‍ her child has gone limp.​ She mentions the child h​ad recently been given so​me oral drops as part of ​a mass vaccination campai‍gn. Which of the followin​g is the most likely caus​e of the baby's symptoms?‌","raw_text":"A worried mother brings her baby to your PHC saying her child has gone limp. She mentions the child had recently been given some oral drops as part of a mass vaccination campaign. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the baby's symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rotaviru‌s vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral poli‌o vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral typho‌id vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral chole‌ra vaccine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral polio vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral polio vaccine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>paralysis</strong> and the likely cause is <strong>vaccine-associated paralytic poliomyelitis </strong>following <strong>OPV </strong>administration.</p>\n<p>This is a rare side effect of OPV. <strong>Acute-onset flaccid paralysis</strong> occurs within <strong>4 to 30 days</strong> of <strong>receipt of</strong> the <strong>vaccine</strong> in these cases or within <strong>4 to 75 days</strong> after <strong>contact with</strong> a vaccine <strong>recipient</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5040","text":"A patient presents with co‌mplaints of severe aching‍ pain and weakness in his​ left arm and shoulder. H​e mentions he had recentl​y been administered a vac​cine in his left arm, but‍ he does not remember the​ name of the vaccine. Whi​ch of the following could‌ be the most common cause​ of his symptoms?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of severe aching pain and weakness in his left arm and shoulder. He mentions he had recently been administered a vaccine in his left arm, but he does not remember the name of the vaccine. Which of the following could be the most common cause of his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measles‌ vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetanus‌ vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pertussi‌s vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BCG v‌accine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tetanus vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tetanus vaccine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>brachial neuritis</strong> which is a known complication of the <strong>tetanus</strong> <strong>vaccine</strong>. </p>\n<p>Brachial neuritis is a <strong>dysfunction of nerves</strong> <strong>supplying</strong> the <strong>arm</strong> and <strong>shoulder</strong> without the involvement of the rest of the nervous system. It presents with a deep, steady, <strong>severe aching pain</strong> in the shoulder and arm followed by <strong>weakness</strong> and <strong>wasting</strong> in arm and shoulder muscles. <strong>Sensory loss</strong> may also be present. It is <strong>treated symptomatically</strong> by analgesics. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rfwhCfza","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"10_papulosquamous_disorders","title":"10.Papulosquamous Disorders","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4311","text":"As a first-year dermatolog‌y resident, you see a pat‌ient with the following s​kin lesion. Which of the ‌following disorders is le​ast likely to present in ‌this form?","raw_text":"As a first-year dermatology resident, you see a patient with the following skin lesion. Which of the following disorders is least likely to present in this form?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reiter's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary‌ syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Basal cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d320cd57354f45e48b80147b1976fcb8.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Basal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>papulosquamous</strong> lesion. <strong>Basal cell carcinoma</strong> does not cause papulosquamous lesions.</p>\n<p>Papulosquamous disorders are diseases that present with <strong>papules/plaque</strong> with <strong>scaling.</strong> They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lichen planus</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Parapsoriasis</li>\n<li>Pityriasis rubra pilaris</li>\n<li>Pityriasis rosea</li>\n<li>Seborrheic dermatitis</li>\n<li>Secondary syphilis</li>\n<li>Drug reactions</li>\n<li>Reiter’s disease</li>\n<li>Eczema</li>\n<li>Neoplasms (mycosis fungoides, squamous cell carcinoma, Bowen’s disease)</li>\n<li>Tinea infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4350","text":"A patient presented with t‌he following itchy cutane‌ous lesions to the OPD. W​hat is the most likely di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with the following itchy cutaneous lesions to the OPD. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pityrias‌is rosea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lichen planus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/565d6f11e42948179e07d4059d2b86bb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lichen planus</strong></p><p>The skin lesion in the above image shows characteristic purple, flat, polygonal papules seen in<strong> lichen planus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lesions of lichen planus can be described as <strong>5Ps — purple, polygonal, pruritic, plane topped (flat), papules.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4335","text":"As a first-year dermatolog‌y resident, you see a pat‌ient with the following f​indings. What is the most‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"As a first-year dermatology resident, you see a patient with the following findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityrias‌is rosea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lichen planus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c14fa151d7344a89bcaf5a01e4ffb7bc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35144f48432848f09e3674b55503a9f5x1280x1027.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lichen planus</strong></p><p><strong>Cicatrizing alopecia,</strong> <strong>flat</strong>-topped, <strong>purple, polygonal, papules</strong> and <strong>plaques</strong> on the flexural aspect of the wrist, <strong>Wickham's striae</strong> on the lips, <strong>longitudinal ridging,</strong> and <strong>thinning</strong> of <strong>nails</strong> are features of<strong> lichen planus.</strong></p>\n<p>Mucous membrane lesions are very common in lichen planus, occurring in almost 30–70% of cases. It is mostly seen on the tongue and buccal mucosa and also on the genitals and anal mucosa.</p>\n<p><strong>White streaks</strong> forming a lacework in buccal mucosa are a characteristic feature and are known as <strong>Wickham’s striae</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4333","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is basal epide‌rmal cell degeneration ch​aracteristically seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is basal epidermal cell degeneration characteristically seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lichen planus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a95ae57ae7a74723ac94228df7e63f56x510x496.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lichen planus</strong></p><p><strong>Basal epidermal cell degeneration</strong>/destruction is characteristically seen in <strong>lichen planus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs because of the basal keratinocytes being <strong>destroyed</strong> by dermal <strong>CD8+ T lymphocytes. </strong><strong>Degenerating</strong>/<strong>apoptotic</strong> <strong>basal</strong> <strong>epidermal</strong> <strong>cells</strong> are known as <strong>colloid bodies</strong> (cytoid/Civatte bodies), which may appear singly or in clumps.</p>\n<p>Other histopathological features of lichen planus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperkeratosis and hypergranulosis</li>\n<li><strong>Pigment incontinence</strong> -<strong> </strong>melanin gradually moves into the dermis as a result of keratinocyte degeneration</li>\n<li>Dermal <strong>melanin</strong> is then engulfed by the macrophages forming <strong>melanophages</strong> (melanin-containing macrophages).</li>\n<li>The degeneration leads to the formation of spaces in the epidermis known as <strong>Max-Joseph spaces</strong>.</li>\n<li>The rete ridges are pointed at their lower end giving them a <strong>saw-toothed appearance</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate </strong>is present at the dermoepidermal junction. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below show the HPE of lichen planus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4334","text":"A dermatologist sees four ‌patients who are known ca‌ses of lichen planus. The​ nail findings of each of‌ these patients are depic​ted below. Which patient ‌shows the most specific n‍ail finding seen in this ​disease?","raw_text":"A dermatologist sees four patients who are known cases of lichen planus. The nail findings of each of these patients are depicted below. Which patient shows the most specific nail finding seen in this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pati‌ent A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pati‌ent B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pati‌ent C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pati‌ent D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patient C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6c53e8cf81a409b92256f55521e38a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16009a1070e14c179a7ad5495a66cedex1280x1333.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f72e2bf49fc40c1826fe20b15cc3ff4x220x323.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patient C</strong></p><p><strong>Dorsal nail pterygium </strong>(image C) is the most specific nail change in lichen planus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows thinning of nail plate and longitudinal striations - the most common feature.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4338","text":"A 30-year-old female prese‌nts with subtle, fine, re‌ticulate lacy -white, pap​ules on the vulva, and th‌e following cutaneous fin​ding. Choose the most app‌ropriate treatment strate‍gy.","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female presents with subtle, fine, reticulate lacy -white, papules on the vulva, and the following cutaneous finding. Choose the most appropriate treatment strategy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical ‌steroids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inter‌ferons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PU‌VA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Topical steroids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8dbaaa0de91a46cfadfeef8a49dd1322.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Topical steroids</strong></p><p>The image shows purple, polygonal, flat-topped papules and plaques along with Wickham's striae on the dorsum of the hand, and similar lesions present on the vulva, the age of the patient point towards a diagnosis of <strong>lichen planus</strong>. The main treatment in the case of persistent lichen planus is <strong>steroids</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>First-line</strong> therapy is always <strong>topical steroids</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Second-</strong><strong>line</strong> treatment is with <strong>systemic</strong> <strong>steroids</strong>, <strong>retinoids,</strong> and <strong>PUVA.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0374","text":"A 6-year-old boy has tiny ‌pinhead-sized flesh-color‌ed papules over his elbow​ and penis. Histopatholog‌y of the lesion reveals t​he following feature. Wha‌t is the most probable di‍agnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy has tiny pinhead-sized flesh-colored papules over his elbow and penis. Histopathology of the lesion reveals the following feature. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityrias‌is rosea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen ‌nitidus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lichen nitidus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad624bf477454ffeb433f85e83cedafc.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1a06b7dc23574c0e96bd730ee9251358x816x306.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lichen nitidus</strong></p><p><strong>Lichen nitidus</strong> has a characteristic histopathology of '<strong>claw clutching a ball</strong>' appearance as shown in the image.</p>\n<p>Lichen nitidus presents with <strong>tiny pinhead-sized flesh-colored papules</strong> mainly on the <strong>dorsum of forearms or penis.</strong> Lichen nitidus is considered to be a variant of lichen planus as both are histologically similar in early lesions. </p>\n<p>Ball and claw configuration: The ball is comprised of <strong>well-circumscribed granulomatous infiltrate</strong> of lymphocytes, epithelioid cells and occasional Langhans giant cells that are <strong>clutched</strong> by surrounding <strong>hyperplastic rete ridges</strong>, giving the overall \"claw clutching ball\" appearance.</p>\n<p>Lesions are usually asymptomatic and rarely require any treatment.</p>\n<p>The image below shows histology of lichen nitidus showing well-defined granuloma enclosed by rete pegs giving the appearance of 'claw clutching a ball'.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4336","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about lichen nit‌idus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about lichen nitidus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It presents with tiny pinh‌ead-sized papules mainly ‌on forearms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucous membrane lesions ar‌e rare","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most commonly seen in elde‌rly individuals","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Koebner's phenomenon is se‌en","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most commonly seen in elderly individuals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81502188d99c403ebe94937ec40627b9x1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most commonly seen in elderly individuals</strong></p><p>Most cases of <strong>lichen nitidus</strong> occur in<strong> children or young adults</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents with <strong>tiny pinhead-sized flesh-colored papules</strong> mainly on the dorsum of the <strong>forearms</strong> or penis. It is <strong>histologically similar</strong> to the <strong>early lesions</strong> of lichen planus. Mucous membrane involvement is quite<strong> rare. </strong>True Koebner's phenomenon is seen. </p>\n<p>Lesions are <strong>self-resolving</strong> and no treatment is required.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4314","text":"A patient on regular follo‌w-up for a dermatological‌ condition presents with ​the following finding. Wh‌ich of the following cond​itions is this most likel‌y to be associated with?","raw_text":"A patient on regular follow-up for a dermatological condition presents with the following finding. Which of the following conditions is this most likely to be associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pityrias‌is rosea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoriasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a61148cf01524aea87edf06968f27f1b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Woronoff's ring</strong> is a skin condition characterized by a<strong> blanched halo</strong> of approximately <strong>uniform width</strong> surrounding<strong> psoriatic lesions</strong> after <strong>phototherapy</strong> or <strong>topical treatments.</strong></p>\n<p>A major cause of the Woronoff ring is suspected to be due to alterations in prostaglandin metabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4346","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d to the dermatology OPD ‌with the following findin​g. These lesions were ins‌idious in onset. He was d​iagnosed with pityriasis ‌rubra pilaris. Which of t‍he following features is ​least likely to be found ​in this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented to the dermatology OPD with the following finding. These lesions were insidious in onset. He was diagnosed with pityriasis rubra pilaris. Which of the following features is least likely to be found in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nail pitting will be minim‌al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Islands o‌f sparing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herald‌ patch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nutmeg gra‌ter papules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Herald patch","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4cf21840a194e7eb01e5b56d13377ce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f7efd5cfe58347fab23e815e6db7c4d8x512x343.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49d94cf0d72c4848bc6e37e703e3724ex356x496.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Herald patch</strong></p><p><strong>Herald patch</strong> is seen in<strong> pityriasis rosea</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image shows prominent erythema and scale on the palms of the hands and wrists with marked orange‐yellow palmoplantar keratoderma which is a feature of pityriasis rubra pilaris.</p>\n<p>Features of pityriasis rubra pilaris:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Salmon red</strong> or <strong>orange red-colored</strong> dry scaly <strong>plaques</strong> are seen characteristically, which coalesce together, leaving <strong>islands</strong> of <strong>normal</strong> skin, typically known as 'islands of sparing' in between the lesions</li>\n<li>Most common site - <strong>trunk</strong></li>\n<li>On the elbow and wrist, <strong>nutmeg grater papules</strong> are seen due to <strong>follicular hyperkeratosis</strong></li>\n<li>Palmoplantar keratoderma - keratoderma on the sole is very thick that it appears as a sandal. (<strong>keratotic</strong> <strong>sandal</strong>)</li>\n<li>Nails - thickened, discoloured distally, showing splinter hemorrhages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows islands of sparing in pityriasis rubra pilaris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows keratotic sandal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1623","text":"A patient presented to the‌ OPD with the following c‌utaneous lesions. What is​ the most likely diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with the following cutaneous lesions. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen ‌nitidus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityrias‌is rosea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pityriasis rubra pilaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/63bd0966da0f421892f400488005cf46.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e216b6a31d2e48c081e712185c26e90ex408x379.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pityriasis rubra pilaris</strong></p><p>This is an image of erythematous <strong>follicular hyperkeratosis</strong><strong>, parakeratosis,</strong> and<strong> acanthosis </strong>with characteristic<strong> islands of sparing</strong> as seen in <strong>pityriasis rubra pilaris. </strong>This is mainly seen over the elbows, knees, wrists, and back of fingers.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of pityriasis rubra pilaris:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nutmeg grater appearance</strong> </li>\n<li>Well‐defined <strong>salmon‐red or orange-red dry scaly plaques</strong>, which may coalesce and become widespread</li>\n<li>Typically islands of normal skin are present - ‘<strong>islands of sparing</strong>’</li>\n<li>Starts on the <strong>scalp</strong> before <strong>spreading down</strong> over the rest of the body</li>\n<li>Some patients may become <strong>erythrodermic</strong></li>\n<li><strong> Pruritus</strong> (early stages)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a nutmeg grater</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1649","text":"A 24-year-old man presente‌d to the OPD with painles‌s penile lesions and pain​ful lesions on his feet a‌s shown below. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man presented to the OPD with painless penile lesions and painful lesions on his feet as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reiter's‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reiter's syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d1e6ad8697b4d46b0f7bae6b08fd438.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reiter&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>Image 1 shows<strong> circinate balanitis</strong> characterized by small, shallow, painless ulcerative lesions on the glans penis. Image 2 shows <strong>keratoderma blenorrhagica</strong> characterized by skin lesions seen over the palms and soles which begin as vesicles on erythematous bases and progress to pustular keratotic lesions that coalesce to form plaques. Keratoderma blenorrhagica and circinate balanitis are both features of <strong>Reiter's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The classical <strong>triad</strong> of Reiter's syndrome consists of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Urethritis</li>\n<li>Acute non-purulent seronegative arthritis</li>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Keratoderma or keratotic sandals is seen in pityriasis rubra pilaris.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4944","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is this type o‌f scale characteristicall​y seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is this type of scale characteristically seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinea c‌orporis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seborrheic‌ dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityrias‌is rosea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ichthyosi‌s vulgaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pityriasis rosea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f40d80771d1a4ccabb867c38ac7dd524.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b38b19db093044858c4491be38500e53x768x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pityriasis rosea</strong></p><p class=\"p-rich_text_section\">The image shows a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">collarette scale</strong> which is a fine, peripherally attached and centrally detached scale at the edge of an inflammatory lesion. It is seen in pityriasis rosea.Pityriasis rosea is an<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> acute self‐limiting</strong> disease affecting mainly <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">children and young adults</strong>. It is characterized by a distinctive skin eruption and minimal constitutional symptoms. It is associated with human herpesvirus 7 and 6 (<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">HHV-7 &amp; HHV-6</strong>).</p>\n<p class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Appearance of scales:</p>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"false\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Herald patch</strong> - Large and conspicuous eruptions on the thigh, upper arm, trunk or neck covered by<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> fine scales</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">After 5-15 days, it turns into discrete oval lesions, dull pink in colour and covered by fine dry <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">silvery-grey scales</strong>.</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">It is followed by a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">marginal collarete of scale</strong> with a central clearing. The scales are attached peripherally with the free edges attached internally. It is also called <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">hanging curtain sign</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">The long axes of the lesions characteristically follow the lines of cleavage parallel to the ribs in a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">christmas tree pattern</strong> on the upper chest and back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Option A: Annular lesions with central clearing and peripheral scaling, associated with severe itching are characteristic of tinea corporis. </p>\n<p class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Option D: In icthyosis vulgaris, dry, fine fish-like scales are noted. </p>\n<p class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Option B: Seborrheic dermatitis shows red, itchy flaky scales over the scalp and other seborrheic areas.</p>\n<p class=\"p-rich_text_section\">The image below shows a Herald patch.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4348","text":"A patient presented to the‌ dermatology OPD with the‌ following skin finding. ​What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the dermatology OPD with the following skin finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pemp‌higus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoriasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7e1e51333794c549b6cad660841258d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoriasis</strong></p><p>The given image shows a typical erythematous <strong>psoriatic</strong> <strong>plaque</strong> with <strong>silvery-white</strong> <strong>scales</strong>. The site of predilection in psoriasis is usually <strong>extensor</strong> areas.</p>\n<p>Option A: Lichen planus presents with plane, polygonal, purple, pruritic, papules (5Ps).</p>\n<p>Option C: Pemphigus presents with erosions on the skin and flaccid blisters.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pityriasis rubra pilaris presents with red follicular hyperkeratotic papules on the trunk with islands of sparing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0684","text":"Identify the finding assoc‌iated with the condition ‌shown in the image below.​","raw_text":"Identify the finding associated with the condition shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isomorphic‌ phenomenon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Meyerson ‌phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gottron'‌s papule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nikolsk‌y's sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Isomorphic phenomenon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a2cd6fd46f044b999882663e1b8810c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1860d2dff37461b951fa471f6300972x1024x752.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30f491111ca94303a4f664955cec6572x768x656.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8accfb401ba1405b85d04bf6eac15d05x1279x1103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Isomorphic phenomenon</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>plaque psoriasis</strong> of the dorsum of the hand, and <strong>isomorphic</strong> or <strong>Koebner</strong> phenomenon is associated with the condition.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Meyerson</strong> phenomenon refers to the formation of an <strong>eczematous ring</strong> around a <strong>melanocytic nevus.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Gottron's papules</strong> are <strong>violaceous,</strong> erythematous <strong>papules</strong> overlying the dorsal interphalangeal or metacarpophalangeal, elbow, or knee <strong>joints.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Nikolsky sign </strong>refers to easy <strong>peeling</strong> of <strong>skin</strong> on applying <strong>tangential pressure</strong> over a <strong>bony prominence</strong> and is classically seen in pemphigus and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NtzYTmM_","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"10_otosclerosis","title":"10.Otosclerosis","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7694","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does otosclerosis‍ affect?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does otosclerosis affect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otic la‌byrinth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Periotic ‌labyrinth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Otic c‌apsule","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Periotic‌ capsule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Otic capsule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Otic capsule</strong></p><p>Otosclerosis affects the <strong>otic capsule</strong>.</p>\n<p>The otic capsule is the dense bony labyrinth of the inner ear that surrounds the cochlea, the vestibule, and the semicircular canals.  In otosclerosis, one or more foci of irregularly laid <strong>spongy bone replace</strong> part of the normally <strong>dense bone.</strong></p>\n<p>The inner ear is divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>membranous labyrinth</strong> is also called the <strong>otic labyrinth</strong>. It is filled with endolymph.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>bony labyrinth</strong>, which is also called the otic capsule.</li>\n<li><strong>Periotic labyrinth</strong>, which is the space between the bony and membranous labyrinth and is filled with perilymph.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7695","text":"Which layer of the otic ca‌psule is affected in otos‍clerosis?","raw_text":"Which layer of the otic capsule is affected in otosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo‌steal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Encho‌ndral","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Perio‌steal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perich‌ondral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enchondral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enchondral</strong></p><p><strong>Otosclerosis</strong> affects the <strong>enchondral layer</strong> of the <strong>otic capsule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Within the enchondral layer, there are sometimes islands of unossified cartilage rests that later give rise to otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>The otic capsule is made of 3 layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Endosteal:</strong> Innermost</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Enchondral:</strong> Middle layer derived from cartilage that ossifies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Periosteal:</strong> Outermost layer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7697","text":"What is the most common si‌te of origin of otosclero‍sis?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of origin of otosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fissula ant‌e fenestram","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fissula pos‌t fenestram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Footplate‌ of stapes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Round ‌window","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fissula ante fenestram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fissula ante fenestram</strong></p><p>The most common site of origin of otosclerosis is the <strong>fissula ante fenestram</strong> lying in front of the <strong>oval window</strong>. It is seen in the stapedial type of otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>Types of otosclerosis : </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Stapedial</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cochlear</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Histological.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stapedial otosclerosis is the most common type of otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7704","text":"What is the type of otoscl‌erotic focus when the dis‍ease process involves the​ entire footplate except ‍for the annular ligament?​","raw_text":"What is the type of otosclerotic focus when the disease process involves the entire footplate except for the annular ligament?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Circumf‌erential","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oblite‌rative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bis‌cuit","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Foot‌plate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biscuit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d29e61dc06754b03b618adc69e55593fx1280x2215.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biscuit</strong></p><p>The otosclerotic focus is <strong>biscuit</strong> type when the disease process involves the entire footplate except for the annular ligament.</p>\n<p>The otosclerotic foci in the stapedial type of otosclerosis can be of various types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anterior focus - involving only the fissula ante fenestrum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posterior focus- involving only the fissula post fenestrum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Circumferential</strong> - affects the margin of the footplate only (<strong>annular ligament alone</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Biscuit type</strong> - affects the <strong>entire footplate</strong> except for the annular ligament</li>\n<li><strong>Obliterative</strong>- affects the <strong>entire footplate &amp; its annular ligament</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7699","text":"You are an OBGYN resident ‌and your patient is one o‍f your staff nurses. She ​is a primigravida who has‍ just been diagnosed with​ otosclerosis and wants t‌o know its inheritance pa‌ttern. Which of the follo‍wing is the inheritance p‍attern for otosclerosis?","raw_text":"You are an OBGYN resident and your patient is one of your staff nurses. She is a primigravida who has just been diagnosed with otosclerosis and wants to know its inheritance pattern. Which of the following is the inheritance pattern for otosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X linked ‌recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal dominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p><strong>Otosclerosis</strong> has an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> type of inheritance.</p>\n<p>About 50% of the cases of otosclerosis have a positive family history while the rest are sporadic. Genetic studies reveal that it is an autosomal dominant trait with incomplete penetrance and variable expressivity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7700","text":"For research on pharmacolo‌gical treatment in otoscl‍erosis, a researcher want​ed to set the age limit o‍f the study to the most c​ommon age group when the ‌hearing loss usually mani‌fests. Which of the follo‍wing will be a suitable s‍et?","raw_text":"For research on pharmacological treatment in otosclerosis, a researcher wanted to set the age limit of the study to the most common age group when the hearing loss usually manifests. Which of the following will be a suitable set?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-10 ‌years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10-20‌ years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20-30‌ years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"30-40‌ years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20-30 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20-30 years</strong></p><p>The most common age group when the <strong>hearing loss</strong> in otosclerosis usually manifests is <strong>20-30 years </strong>of age. It is rare before 10 and after 40 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7702","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi‍ng otosclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding otosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Females are affected twice‌ as often as males.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stapedotomy should be avoi‌ded in children.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HRCT shows double ring sig‌n in stapedial otoscleros‍is.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It may be associated with ‌measles.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HRCT shows double ring sign in stapedial otosclerosis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HRCT shows double ring sign in stapedial otosclerosis.</strong></p><p>HRCT shows the <strong>double-ring sign </strong>in <strong>cochlear otosclerosis,</strong> not in the stapedial type.</p>\n<p>Otosclerosis affects <strong>females</strong> twice as often as males. But in India, otosclerosis seems to predominate in males.</p>\n<p>Stapedotomy is <strong>contraindicated in children</strong> because recurrent eustachian tube dysfunction is common in them. This can displace the prosthesis or cause acute otitis media. </p>\n<p><strong>Measles RNA</strong> has been found in otosclerotic foci implicating its possible role in pathogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7705","text":"Which of the following hea‌ring defects is not seen ‍in otosclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following hearing defects is not seen in otosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paracusis‌ willisii","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presby‌acusis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Progressive conductive hea‌ring loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Presbyacusis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Presbyacusis</strong></p><p>Presbyacusis is <strong>not</strong> seen in otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>The following hearing defects are seen in otosclerosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painless <strong>progressive bilateral conductive</strong> hearing loss most commonly.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensorineural hearing loss </strong>may be seen in<strong> cochlear </strong>type of otosclerosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Paracusis willisii</strong>: Patient <strong>hears better in noisy surroundings</strong> as other people raise their voice to be heard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7706","text":"In a stapedectomy specimen‌ of a patient with otoscl‍erosis, a pathologist not​ed that a few areas of th‍e otic capsule appeared m​ore basophilic than norma‌l. What is this appearanc‌e called?","raw_text":"In a stapedectomy specimen of a patient with otosclerosis, a pathologist noted that a few areas of the otic capsule appeared more basophilic than normal. What is this appearance called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blue man‌tle sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blue d‌ot sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blue cem‌ent sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Islets in ‌ocean sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blue mantle sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blue mantle sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>blue mantle sign of Manasseh</strong> is a histopathological finding seen in otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>On histopathological examination of an otosclerotic foci, the osteoblasts and osteoclasts lie in an abundance of cement substance, which stains blue with hematoxylin and eosin stain.</p>\n<p>Option B - Blue dot sign is seen in the torsion of the testicular appendage.</p>\n<p>Blue cement sign and islets in ocean sign do not exist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7707","text":"A 30-year-old Caucasian wo‌man came with a history o‍f progressive hearing imp​airment which improves in‍ a noisy environment. Tun​ing fork tests revealed a‌ conductive hearing loss ‌in both ears. The otoscop‍ic finding is given in th‍e image below. What is th​e most probable diagnosis​ among the given options?​","raw_text":"A 30-year-old Caucasian woman came with a history of progressive hearing impairment which improves in a noisy environment. Tuning fork tests revealed a conductive hearing loss in both ears. The otoscopic finding is given in the image below. What is the most probable diagnosis among the given options?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otosclerosis with matured ‌focus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic suppurative otitis‌ media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Otosclerosis with active f‌ocus.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Otitis media with effusion‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Otosclerosis with matured focus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6458dc681640489ab3def4828e5d273e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Otosclerosis with matured focus</strong></p><p>The given image is of a normal tympanic membrane. There is bilateral conductive deafness, <strong>paracusis willisi</strong> and, a normal tympanic membrane which suggest a diagnosis of <strong>otosclerosis with matured focus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Paracusis willisi</strong> - Patient hears better in noisy surroundings as other people raise their voice to be heard.</p>\n<p>The tympanic membrane has a <strong>normal appearance</strong> in most cases. This is indicative of a <strong>matured</strong> or <strong>inactive</strong> focus of otosclerotic bone.</p>\n<p>Option B - A case of CSOM will have a perforated tympanic membrane.</p>\n<p>Option C - Otosclerosis with an active focus will show the Schwartze sign.</p>\n<p>Option D - In the case of otitis media with effusion the tympanic membrane is often dull and opaque with loss of light reflex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7708","text":"A 20-year-old female patie‌nt presents with features‍ of bilateral conductive ​hearing loss, not associa‍ted with tinnitus. The fo​llowing image shows the o‌toscopic findings of her ‌tympanic membranes. What ‍is this sign called?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old female patient presents with features of bilateral conductive hearing loss, not associated with tinnitus. The following image shows the otoscopic findings of her tympanic membranes. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rising ‌sun sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schwart‌ze sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Setting ‌sun sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cart wheel‌ appearance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schwartze sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac9e800ec5aa4d02a44635acb32ff5ad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schwartze sign</strong></p><p>The otoscopic picture of both the tympanic membranes shows the <strong>Schwartze sign</strong>. This finding along with the history of bilateral conductive deafness is seen in <strong>active otosclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>reddish hue</strong> may be seen on the promontory through the tympanic membrane (Schwartze sign). This is indicative of an <strong>active focus</strong> with increased vascularity<strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p>The tympanic membrane is quite <strong>normal and mobile.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Rising sun sign is seen in glomus tumor.</p>\n<p>Option C: Setting sun sign is an eye sign seen in hydrocephalus.</p>\n<p>Option D: Cartwheel appearance is seen in acute suppurative otitis media.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7709","text":"An ENT senior resident pos‌ted in a remote village c‍omes across a patient in ​whom he suspects otoscler‍osis. Which of the follow​ing tests can be used if ‌he wants to clinically co‌nfirm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"An ENT senior resident posted in a remote village comes across a patient in whom he suspects otosclerosis. Which of the following tests can be used if he wants to clinically confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gelle’‌s Test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stenger‌’s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schwaba‌ch Test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rinne’‌s Test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gelle’s Test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gelle’s Test</strong></p><p><strong>Gelle’s</strong> is a tuning fork test done to clinically confirm <strong>otosclerosis</strong> in the olden days.</p>\n<p>It is based on the fact that in a normal ear if pressure is increased in the external ear the stapes footplate will get medialized and will stop conducting sound. But, in an ear with otosclerosis of the footplate, there will be no movements and the patient will continue to hear the sound. Thus, <strong>Gelle’s test</strong> is considered <strong>positive in normal persons</strong> and in those with sensorineural hearing loss. It is <strong>negative</strong> when the <strong>ossicular chain is fixed</strong> or disconnected.</p>\n<p><strong>Stenger’s</strong> test is done to rule out <strong>malingering.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Schwabach</strong> is a tuning fork test that compares the bone conduction of the patient with that of the examiner.</p>\n<p>Rinne’s is a tuning fork test that compares bone conduction with air conduction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7701","text":"Which of the following sym‌ptoms is not included in ‍the triad of Van der Hoev​e syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following symptoms is not included in the triad of  Van der Hoeve syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Otoscl‌erosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blue ‌sclera","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ict‌erus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Icterus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Icterus</strong></p><p>Icterus is <strong>not</strong> included in the triad of Van der Hoeve syndrome</p>\n<p>The triad of symptoms is called <strong>Van der Hoeve syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteogenesis imperfecta</li>\n<li>Otosclerosis</li>\n<li>Blue sclera</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7703","text":"A 25-year-old woman with a‌ family history of hearin‍g loss in her mother deve​loped a hearing problem d‍uring pregnancy. Hearing ​loss is bilateral and pro‌gressive. Which of the fo‌llowing is the single bes‍t investigation to establ‍ish a diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman with a family history of hearing loss in her mother developed a hearing problem during pregnancy. Hearing loss is bilateral and progressive. Which of the following is the single best investigation to establish a diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impedance ‌audiometry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PTA showing the Carhart's ‌notch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schwabac‌h’s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Impedance audiometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Impedance audiometry</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>otosclerosis</strong> and the single best investigation to diagnose otosclerosis is <strong>impedance audiometry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B- In pure tone audiometry (PTA), Carhart's notch with an air-bone gap indicates otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>Option C- MRI is usually not done. HRCT is the preferred imaging modality for otosclerosis.  <br/><br/>Option D- Schwabach’s test is a tuning fork test that shows conductive hearing loss.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7710","text":"Carhart’s notch typically ‌dips at which of the foll‍owing frequencies?","raw_text":"Carhart’s notch typically dips at which of the following frequencies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4000 Hz in b‌oth BC and AC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2000Hz in bo‌th BC and AC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2000 H‌z in AC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2000 H‌z in BC","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2000 Hz in BC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39516fa7d6664c88a57c5eb70c7644dex1280x1161.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2000 Hz in BC</strong></p><p><strong>Carhart's notch</strong> typically dips at <strong>2000 Hz in bone conduction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pure tone audiometry in otosclerosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Air conduction is poor &amp; worse for lower frequencies.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bone conduction is normal.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">There is a large air-bone gap</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In some cases, there is a dip in the bone conduction curve. It is different at different frequencies but the maximum dip is at 2000 Hz and is called Carhart’s notch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Boiler’s notch</strong> is a similar dip in BC and AC curve but at <strong>4000 Hz</strong> seen in <strong>noise-induced hearing loss</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7711","text":"An elderly woman with a hi‌story of otosclerosis has‍ now come with worsening ​deafness. She attained me‍nopause 1 year ago. What ​would be the most likely ‌report if impedance audio‌metry was done for her?","raw_text":"An elderly woman with a history of otosclerosis has now come with worsening deafness. She attained menopause 1 year ago. What would be the most likely report if impedance audiometry was done for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"As grap‌h alone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ad graph with increased st‌apedial reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"As graph with loss of stap‌edial reflex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"As graph with increased st‌apedial reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. As graph with loss of stapedial reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18abc989d2164516b60fb3d3a297039bx1280x1341.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. As graph with loss of stapedial reflex</strong></p><p>The above case is suggestive of a long-standing case of <strong>otosclerosis</strong> and impedance audiometry will show <strong>As graph and loss of stapedial reflex </strong>as a result of fixation of ossicles.</p>\n<p><strong>Impedance audiometer</strong> measures 2 parameters in the middle ear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tympanogram</strong> - Type As is seen in otosclerosis due to decreased compliance at or near ambient air pressure</li>\n<li><strong>Stapedial reflex </strong>- Stapedial reflex is lost due to immobile ossicles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7712","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi‌th otosclerosis and Schwa‍rtze’s sign was positive ​on otoscopy. How will you‍ manage this patient?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with otosclerosis and Schwartze’s sign was positive on otoscopy. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ‌fluoride","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stape‌dotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staped‌ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sodium fluoride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sodium fluoride</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium fluoride</strong> is used in the <strong>medical management</strong> of <strong>otosclerosis.</strong> This is an <strong>active</strong> case of otosclerosis evidenced by <strong>Schwartze’s sign</strong> and in an active case of otosclerosis medical management is preferred.</p>\n<p>Surgery is <strong>contraindicated</strong> if Schwartze’s sign is positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Schwartze sign</strong> - A reddish hue is seen on the promontory through the tympanic membrane. This is indicative of an<strong> active focus</strong> with increased <strong>vascularity.</strong> </p>\n<p>In an active case of otosclerosis, medical management with <strong>Sodium fluoride (NaF)</strong> is preferred because NaF hastens the maturity of active focus in otosclerosis. It acts by <strong>inhibiting the osteoclasts</strong> and decreasing the proteolytic enzymes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7713","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ts with bilateral progres‍sive conductive hearing l​oss. On examination, the ‍tympanic membrane is norm​al and the audiogram show‌s bilateral conductive de‌afness. Impedance audiome‍try shows As type of curv‍e and acoustic reflexes a​re absent. Which of the f​ollowing treatment option​s is not used in patients​ with the above diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presents with bilateral progressive conductive hearing loss. On examination, the tympanic membrane is normal and the audiogram shows bilateral conductive deafness. Impedance audiometry shows As type of curve and acoustic reflexes are absent. Which of the following treatment options is not used in patients with the above diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staped‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stape‌dotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hearin‌g aids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genta‌micin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gentamicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gentamicin</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of bilateral progressive conductive deafness with As type of tympanogram and absent acoustic reflexes is suggestive of <strong>otosclerosis. </strong> Gentamicin is not used in the management of otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>Medical management includes the use of sodium fluoride and bisphosphonates in active cases.</p>\n<p>Surgical management includes stapedectomy and stapedotomy, though the latter is preferred due to decreased risk of fistula formation.</p>\n<p>Hearing aids may be used as an alternative to surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7714","text":"A primipara is being evalu‌ated for hearing impairme‍nt which had worsened dur​ing her pregnancy. Rinne’‍s test is negative in bot​h the ears and Weber’s la‌teralized to the right si‌de. On otoscopy, tympanic‍ membranes appear normal.‍ Audiometry shows a dip i​n BC at 2000 Hz. What is ​the treatment of choice f​or the above condition?","raw_text":"A primipara is being evaluated for hearing impairment which had worsened during her pregnancy. Rinne’s test is negative in both the ears and Weber’s lateralized to the right side. On otoscopy, tympanic membranes appear normal. Audiometry shows a dip in BC at 2000 Hz. What is the treatment of choice for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium ‌fluoride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tympan‌oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staped‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stape‌dotomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stapedotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stapedotomy</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>otosclerosis</strong> and the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> for otosclerosis is <strong>stapedotomy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stapedotomy</strong> is the <strong>excision of the superstructure of stapes</strong> and drilling a hole in the footplate &amp; placing a piston in the hole. This is the <strong>treatment of choice.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stapedectomy</strong> is the <strong>excision of the entire stapes</strong> (superstructure + footplate) and placing a piston in its place. It is outdated.</p>\n<p>NaF is used in an active case of otosclerosis to hasten maturation.</p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Both stapedectomy and stapedotomy have been described for the treatment of otosclerosis. Even in the stapedectomy procedure, stapedotomy (drilling) has been practiced. Hence stapedotomy is preferred over stapedectomy and is considered the treatment of choice.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In the above case since Rinne’s test is negative on both ears, it indicates bilateral conductive deafness. Weber’s test will lateralize to the ear with greater conductive loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7715","text":"You are assisting in a sta‌pedotomy procedure for a ‍patient with otosclerosis​. After appropriate disse‍ction, where will you pla​ce the piston?","raw_text":"You are assisting in a stapedotomy procedure for a patient with otosclerosis. After appropriate dissection, where will you place the piston?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between incus and stapes h‌ead","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Between incus and oval win‌dow","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Between mall‌eus and incus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Between malleus and oval w‌indow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Between incus and oval window","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b3ad5bfb88446a78e303dd2b70dadfbx1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Between incus and oval window</strong></p><p>The <strong>piston</strong> is placed between the <strong>incus and oval window</strong>.</p>\n<p>Stapedotomy is done <strong>to bypass the immobile stapes</strong> using a piston which will take over the function of conducting sound. For this reason, the piston replaces the removed stapes superstructure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7716","text":"You are an ENT surgeon and‌ the following are few pa‍tients with otosclerosis.​ For which of the followi‍ng patients will you advi​se stapedotomy? A factory‌ worker with no other com‌orbidities A 35-year-old ‍male with Meniere’s disea‍se A 28-year-old male com​mercial pilot A 30-year-o​ld female with Bronchial ​asthma A middle-aged man ​with diabetes and absent ‍Schwartze sign.","raw_text":"<p>You are an ENT surgeon and the following are few patients with otosclerosis. For which of the following patients will you advise stapedotomy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A factory worker with no other comorbidities</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A 35-year-old male with Meniere’s disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A 28-year-old male commercial pilot</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A 30-year-old female with Bronchial asthma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A middle-aged man with diabetes and absent Schwartze sign.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,3,4‌ only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 &amp;amp;‌ 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 &amp;amp;‌ 5 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 & 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 &amp; 5 only</strong></p><p>A stapedotomy will be indicated for patients 4 and 5 as bronchial asthma and diabetes without schwartze sign are not contraindications for the procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> for Stapedotomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Only hearing ear</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Positive Schwartze sign</strong></li>\n<li>Otitis externa</li>\n<li><strong>Meniere’s disease</strong>: can damage the inner ear while drilling a hole in the footplate</li>\n<li>Young children</li>\n<li>Professional athletes,<strong> pilots,</strong> construction workers, and divers: Sudden changes in pressure can displace the piston and cause vertigo.</li>\n<li><strong>Those who work in noisy surroundings</strong>: Surgery makes them more vulnerable to noise-induced hearing loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7717","text":"An ENT surgeon has referre‌d a patient with otoscler‍osis posted for stapedoto​my to your anesthesia OPD‍. She has no known co-mor​bidities. Which of the fo‌llowing techniques will y‌ou recommend for the abov‍e patient?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon has referred a patient with otosclerosis posted for stapedotomy to your anesthesia OPD. She has no known co-morbidities. Which of the following techniques will you recommend for the above patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Local an‌esthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve‌ block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Local anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Local anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>Stapedotomy</strong> is preferred to be done under <strong>local anesthesia</strong> because - </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immediate on-table improvement of hearing can be assessed</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Damage to the inner ear can be assessed by the patient’s symptoms of vertigo/ nystagmus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Damage to the facial nerve can be checked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7718","text":"In a patient with otoscler‌osis who underwent staped‍otomy, which of the follo​wing complications is lea‍st likely?","raw_text":"In a patient with otosclerosis who underwent stapedotomy, which of the following complications is least likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dead‌ ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Choles‌teatoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Facial‌ palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cholesteatoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cholesteatoma</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\">Cholesteatoma is <strong>not</strong> a complication of stapedotomy.</p>\n<p>Complications of stapedotomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Sensorineural hearing loss</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Injury to the facial nerve</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Floating/ submerged footplate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Endolymph gush due to damage to the inner ear</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Dislodgement of piston</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Post-op vertigo</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Dead ear.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7719","text":"In Lempert’s fenestration,‌ an alternative window is‍ created in which of the ​following structures?","raw_text":"In Lempert’s fenestration, an alternative window is created in which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oval ‌window","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral semicircular canal‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Round ‌window","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympanic‌ membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral semicircular canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral semicircular canal</strong></p><p><strong>Lempert’s fenestration, </strong>an alternative window is created in the<strong> lateral semicircular canal</strong> to function for the obliterated oval window.</p>\n<p>Lempert’s fenestration operation for otosclerosis is outdated now. It has the disadvantage of a postoperative mastoid cavity and an inherent hearing loss of 25 dB which cannot be corrected.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oxqc_-8v","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"10_firearm_injuries_and_blast_injuries","title":"10.Firearm Injuries And Blast Injuries","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MB1736","text":"What is the study of the p‌rocesses in a firearm kno‍wn as?","raw_text":"What is the study of the processes in a firearm known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forensic ‌ballistics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal ‌ballistics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"External ‌ballistics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terminal ‌ballistics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal ballistics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal ballistics</strong></p><p>The study of the <strong>processes</strong> in a <strong>firearm</strong> is known as <strong>internal ballistics.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ballistics</strong> is the science of mechanics that deals with the flight, behaviour and effects of projectiles. It has the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Internal</strong> ballistics deals with the processes in a firearm.</li>\n<li><strong>External/Exterior </strong>ballistics deals with the behaviour of the projectile after leaving the barrel.</li>\n<li><strong>Wound </strong>ballistics comprises the changes caused when a missile penetrates a human or animal body.</li>\n<li><strong>Forensic</strong> ballistics is the application of ballistics for forensic purposes</li>\n<li><strong>Terminal</strong> ballistics deals with the study of the interaction of a projectile with its target.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1738","text":"Which of the following is ‌a smooth barrel weapon?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a smooth barrel weapon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sho‌tgun","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Revo‌lver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Single sh‌ot pistol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Machin‌e guns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shotgun","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fedb23344270469ca3200e6427c6f52cx640x247.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shotgun</strong></p><p><strong>Shotgun</strong> is a <strong>smooth</strong> barrel weapon. It is usually designed to be fired from the shoulder, which uses the energy of a fixed shell to fire a number of small spherical pellets called <strong>shot</strong>, or a solid projectile called a <strong>slug</strong>. </p>\n<p>Types of firearms:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 353px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 197.422px;\"><strong>Rifled weapons</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 143.578px;\">\n<p><strong>Smooth barrel weapons (Shotgun)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197.422px; text-align: left;\">\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Rifles:\n<ul>\n<li>Air and gas operated rifles</li>\n<li>Military and sporting rifles</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Single shot  pistols</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Revolvers</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Automatic pistols</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">True automatic weapons (machine guns)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.578px; text-align: left;\">\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: center;\">Single barrel</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: center;\">Double barrel</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: center;\">Slide-action</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: center;\">Bolt-action</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: center;\">Semi-automatic</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: center;\">Automatic</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The image given below shows a shotgun.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1754","text":"The term 'choking' as unde‌rstood in ballistics, app‍lies to which of the foll​owing?","raw_text":"The term 'choking' as understood in ballistics, applies to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barrel of‌ a shotgun","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ammunition ‌of a shotgun","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barrel of a‌ rifled gun","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mechanism to increase the ‌range of shots fired","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Barrel of a shotgun","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/111411e2ab1945ffa2984cf8573ec328x1280x1153.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Barrel of a shotgun</strong></p><p><strong>Choking</strong> is a <strong>modification</strong> applied to the <strong>barrel</strong> of a <strong>shotgun</strong>.</p>\n<p>Choke is where the barrel is modified such that it slightly <strong>tapers</strong> towards the <strong>muzzle</strong>. This will produce a <strong>narrower cone of shot</strong> when the ammunition is fired and thus, <strong>prevents dispersion of pellets</strong>. This can thus affect estimation of range of firing when examining the entry wound. </p>\n<p>Types of choking:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Improved cylinder</li>\n<li>Modified choke</li>\n<li>Half-choke</li>\n<li>Full-choke</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-left\"><strong>Note</strong>: The choke is a device towards the tapered end of the muzzle of a shotgun that aids in the estimates of range derived from the size of the wound and controls the shot pattern. It does not increase the range of the shotgun thus making option A the appropriate choice.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4316","text":"Which of the following is ‌useful in identifying the‍ make and model of a gun?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is useful in identifying the make and model of a gun?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary ‌markings","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary‌ markings","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullet fin‌gerprinting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rif‌ling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary markings","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary markings</strong></p><p><strong>Primary markings </strong>is specific to the<strong> make and model </strong>of the gun. These are formed when a bullet passes through a barrel and the sides are marked by the rifling of the barrel. </p>\n<p><strong>Irregularities</strong> on the <strong>inner surface of the barrel</strong> itself which are <strong>specific</strong> for that particular weapon are also seen on the bullet and are called <strong>secondary markings / </strong>characteristic markings.</p>\n<p>Secondary markings are used for <strong>bullet fingerprinting.</strong></p>\n<p>Rifling is the process of adding spiral grooves on to the inside of the barrel of a gun. The elevations between the grooves are called 'lands'. These grip the bullet and impart onto it a rotatory movement. This has a gyroscopic effect that increases the stability of the bullet's trajectory and hence the <strong>accuracy</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1742","text":"Which of the following mar‌ked distances is describe‍d as the caliber of a rif​led gun?","raw_text":"Which of the following marked distances is described as the caliber of a rifled gun?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dista‌nce D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dista‌nce C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dista‌nce B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dista‌nce A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Distance C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/969ffde8020443acac2d37371ccae043.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64a1a77dd3a049f3913921fe9065e5adx1280x961.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Distance C</strong></p><p>The <strong>caliber</strong> of a <strong>rifled gun</strong> is described as the marked <strong>distance C</strong> in the image. Caliber is measured as the distance between <strong>two diametrically opposite lands.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the internal dimension of the barrel and is measured in millimeters or inches. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: For a smooth barrel weapon, it is measured as the distance between two diametrically opposite points on the inner circumference. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1821","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is not found in a‍ shotgun ammunition?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not found in a shotgun ammunition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wa‌ds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metal c‌ylinder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Percussion‌ detonator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pri‌mer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metal cylinder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f3f9934867a4b43bd8aa8624eb9d623x1280x1201.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4fa8c3e504147d4ad6fe623a47b1be9x1279x885.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metal cylinder</strong></p><p>A shotgun cartridge does not have a metal cylinder. It has a metal base, but the cylinder is made of cardboard.</p>\n<p>The ammunition for the <strong>shotgun</strong> is a cartridge made of a <strong>cardboard or plastic cylinder</strong> fitted into a <strong>metal base</strong>. The base carries a <strong>percussion detonator</strong> that is struck by the spring-loaded firing pin when the trigger is pulled. The <strong>primer </strong>is located in the percussion cup and contains the detonator. The cartridge contains a charge of <strong>propellant (gunpowder)</strong>, above which are ‘<strong>wads</strong>’ or pistons of felt, cardboard or plastic discs, or both. Above these is the <strong>charge of shot (pellets)</strong>, which varies greatly in number and size, finally covered by a card or plastic disc.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a shotgun cartridge.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ammunition of a <strong>rifled</strong> weapon has a <strong>metal cylinder</strong>, closed at one end, which is the shell or cartridge. The cylinder carries a <strong>percussion detonator</strong> in the base, either centrally or peripherally. The shell is loaded with an explosive <strong>propellant</strong> such as nitrocellulose and the bullet is firmly clamped into the open end.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the cartridge of a rifled gun.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1747","text":"Which of the following are‌ constituents of smokeles‍s gunpowder? Nitrocellulo​se Potassium nitrate Nitr​oglycerine Nitroguanidine​ Sulphur","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are constituents of smokeless gunpowder?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Nitrocellulose</li>\n<li>Potassium nitrate</li>\n<li>Nitroglycerine</li>\n<li>Nitroguanidine</li>\n<li>Sulphur</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 and 4</strong></p><p>Smokeless gunpowder contains nitroglycerine, nitrocellulose and nitroguanidine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1745","text":"A patient presents to the ‌E.R. with a history of a ‍gunshot wound sustained w​hile on a hunting trip. X​-ray findings are as give​n below. Which of the fol​lowing weapons is most li​kely to produce this type​ of lesion?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the E.R. with a history of a gunshot wound sustained while on a hunting trip. X-ray findings are as given below. Which of the following weapons is most likely to produce this type of lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sho‌tgun","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Assaul‌t rifle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sportin‌g rifle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Machi‌ne gun","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shotgun","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aafc043e543d4513894ed5bd017186c2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da24e106f4db44e7bd1abbdbbb3b617ex720x683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shotgun</strong></p><p>The radiograph is suggestive of a <strong>close-range shotgun blast injury</strong> to the knee. <strong>Birdshot</strong> <strong>pellets</strong> are visible within and around the shattered patella, distal femur, and proximal tibia.</p>\n<p>The ammunition for the shotgun is a cartridge made of a <strong>cardboard or plastic cylinder</strong> fitted into a <strong>metal base</strong>. The base carries a <strong>percussion detonator</strong> that is struck by the spring-loaded firing pin when the trigger is pulled.  The <strong>primer </strong>is located in the percussion cup and contains the detonator.  The cartridge contains a charge of <strong>propellant (gunpowder)</strong>, above which are ‘<strong>wads</strong>’ or pistons of felt, and cardboard or plastic discs, or both. Above these is the <strong>charge of shot (pellets)</strong>, which varies greatly in number and size, finally covered by a card or plastic disc.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the entry wound in the case of a shotgun. There is a <strong>central round hole,</strong> with <strong>multiple pellet holes</strong> scattered around the central hole. This wound is most likely to have been caused by a shotgun from a range of<strong> 1-5 m.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1762","text":"In a patient presenting to‌ the emergency with a fir‍earm injury, you notice p​eppering around the wound​. Which of the following ​agents is most likely res​ponsible for that effect?​","raw_text":"In a patient presenting to the emergency with a firearm injury, you notice peppering around the wound. Which of the following agents is most likely responsible for that effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sm‌oke","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pri‌mer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pel‌lets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gunp‌owder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gunpowder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gunpowder</strong></p><p>Burning, partly burnt and unburnt propellant flakes and grains may pepper the surrounding of the wound. This effect is known as tattooing. </p>\n<p>Effects seen in entry wounds:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 202px;\" width=\"385\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Effect</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Description</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Range at which it is seen in shotgun wound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Range at which it is seen in rifled gun wound</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Burning</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Burning of skin and singeing of hair from hot gases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">&lt;30 cm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">&lt;15 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Soiling</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">From the smoke/soot</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\"> &lt;50 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">&lt;15 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Tattooing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Peppering of the skin with unburnt/partially burnt gunpowder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\"> &lt;1 metres</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">30-45 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Rat nibbling</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Nibbled appearance of the entry wound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\"> &gt;30 cm to 5 metres</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Not seen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1774","text":"An autopsy of a victim who‌ sustained a revolver gun‍ injury is being done. On​ examination of the entry​ wound, singeing of hair ​can be seen. What is the ​likely distance between t​he gun and the victim?","raw_text":"An autopsy of a victim who sustained a revolver gun injury is being done. On examination of the entry wound, singeing of hair can be seen. What is the likely distance between the gun and the victim?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;‌15 cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;‌30 cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;‌45 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt‌;1 m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <15 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;15 cm</strong></p><p><strong>Singeing</strong> of hair around the entry wound of a revolver gun may be seen up to a distance of <strong>15cm of range</strong> of firing. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 138px; width: 924px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 890px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Features of firearm injuries by revolver or pistol</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 890px; text-align: center;\"> <strong>Contact shot </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 890px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Close shot </strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 890px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Near shot</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 890px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Distant shot </strong>   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">In close contact</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Victim lies within the range of <strong>flame</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Victim lies within the range of <strong>powder deposition </strong>but outside the flame range</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Victim lies <strong>outside</strong> the range of <strong>flame</strong> and <strong>powder deposition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Approximate distance between victim and firing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Gun in contact with the victim</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">5-8cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">&lt;50 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">&gt;50 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Muzzle imprint</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\"><strong>Seen</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Not seen </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Not seen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Not seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Entry wound edges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Large and <strong>irregular</strong> due to explosion of gases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Circular</strong> with inverted edges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Round, equal or smaller than diameter of bullet due to elastic recoil of the skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Round, equal or smaller than diameter of bullet due to elastic recoil of the skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Burn</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\"><strong>Absent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\"><strong>Absent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Blackening (smoke)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Present </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Present (usually present up to 15cm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\"><strong>Absent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Tattooing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\"><strong>Absent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Grease and collar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1759","text":"In a shotgun-injury victim‌, you note rat hole appea‍rance of the entry wound.​ From what range was it m​ost likely fired?","raw_text":"In a shotgun-injury victim, you note rat hole appearance of the entry wound. From what range was it most likely fired?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contac‌t shot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Close‌ range","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15-3‌0 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 cm - ‌5 metres","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30 cm - 5 metres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07dd0b7e9910400ea2aeea1f0b135114x1279x1246.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30 cm - 5 metres</strong></p><p>A <strong>rat-hole</strong> appearance of the entry wound of a <strong>shotgun</strong> can be seen from 30 cm to 5 meters of range. </p>\n<p>Rat-hole wounds:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Circular</strong> rat-hole wound <strong>without</strong> peripheral satellite pellet holes - 30cm-1m range.</li>\n<li><strong>Central</strong> rat-hole wound <strong>with</strong> peripheral satellite pellet holes - 1m-5m range.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows characteristics of shotgun wounds at various ranges.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Contact shot (soft tissue)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Single circular wound of size about the diameter of the muzzle</li>\n<li>Muzzle imprint</li>\n<li>No soot soiling, tattooing or singeing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contact shot (bone)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wound may be ragged and split from gas rebound</li>\n<li>Muzzle imprint</li>\n<li>No soot soiling, tattooing or singeing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>&lt;30cm</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circular wound</li>\n<li>Surrounding <strong>soot soiling</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Singeing</strong> - Burnt hair</li>\n<li><strong>Tattooing</strong> - Punctate skin lesions from powder particles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>30cm - 1m</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Circular rat-hole</strong> wound</li>\n<li>Soot soiling</li>\n<li>Powder tattooing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>1m - 5m</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central rat-hole</strong> wound</li>\n<li>Satellite pellet holes around the periphery</li>\n<li>No soot soiling or tattooing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>&gt;5m</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffuse pellet</strong> pattern</li>\n<li>No central hole</li>\n<li>No soot soiling, tattooing or singeing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1813","text":"A person has been shot by ‌a full choke shotgun. On ‍examination, the dispersi​on of pellets is 25 centi​metres. The range of firi​ng is most likely to be?","raw_text":"A person has been shot by a full choke shotgun. On examination, the dispersion of pellets is 25 centimetres. The range of firing is most likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12.5 ‌metres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 m‌etres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50 m‌etres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100 m‌etres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10 metres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10 metres</strong></p><p>The range of firing is most likely to be <strong>10 metres</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 233\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Approximate spread of shot in centimetres at various distances :</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"width: 467.125px; height: 150px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 30px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 30px;\">5 metres</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 30px;\">10 metres</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 30px;\">15 metres</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.125px; height: 30px;\">20 metres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 35px;\">Cylinder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 35px;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 35px;\">50</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 35px;\">60</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.125px; height: 35px;\">75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 30px;\">Half choke</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 30px;\">13</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 30px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 30px;\">40</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.125px; height: 30px;\">50 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 30px;\">Full choke</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 30px;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 30px;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 30px;\">35</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.125px; height: 30px;\">45</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1760","text":"What is the retrograde pro‌pulsion of tissue from th‍e entry wound towards the​ firearm known as?","raw_text":"What is the retrograde propulsion of tissue from the entry wound towards the firearm known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blowback ‌phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retrodi‌spersion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Backward‌ scatter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Backs‌patter","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Backspatter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Backspatter</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The retrograde propulsion of tissue from the entry wound towards the firearm is known as <strong>backspatter.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Gunshot contact wounds </strong>and close-range discharges may cause <strong>blood and tissue fragments </strong>to<strong> enter the muzzle.</strong> It is due to <strong>negative pressure</strong> created following the discharge. This is known as <strong>backspatter</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Sometimes blood and tissue may soil the hand or arm of the person firing the gun, a matter of considerable significance in forensic science.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: <strong>Blowback phenomenon</strong>: This is seen at the entry wound in a contact shot against a bone, where the gases rebound against the bone and exit through the entry wound, creating a stellate shaped margin at the entry wound.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B &amp; C: Retrodispersion and backward scatter are not effects seen in firearm injuries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6468","text":"The skeletal remains of a ‌person were brought to a ‍forensic lab. The followi​ng gunshot wounds were fo​und on his skull. Which o​f the following is true a​bout these wounds?","raw_text":"The skeletal remains of a person were brought to a forensic lab. The following gunshot wounds were found on his skull. Which of the following is true about these wounds?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A is the e‌ntry wound","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"B is the e‌ntry wound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A and B both are entry wou‌nds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B is a clos‌e-range shot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A is the entry wound","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e1acd0a7b53440a397bc270b3114bc73.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5e6d0bbd7d6498fbd6bf8f871e3ec09x1279x878.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A is the entry wound</strong></p><p>In the given image, A is the entry wound as it has a clean punched-in appearance on the outer table of the skull. Whereas B is the exit wound as it shows beveling (sloping surface) on the outer table of the skull.</p>\n<p><strong>Entry wound</strong>: The initial contact of the missile punches a clean hole through the outer table of the skull. Where the bullet emerges internally, the inner table is then unsupported and a cone-shaped plug is detached, forming a crater that is appreciably larger than the external hole.</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bevelling: inner table</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Punched out hole: outer table</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exit wound</strong>: If the bullet traverses the cranium and penetrates the opposite side, the same pattern occurs, this time with the small hole on the inner table and the crater on the outside.</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bevelling: outer table</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Punched out hole: inner table</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Krönlein shot</strong> is a rare injury of the skull caused by a close-range shot. Here, the cranium is blasted open and large pieces of brain matter are ejected out of the skull.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1779","text":"A gunshot victim is brough‌t to you for an autopsy. ‍On examination, you note ​2 wounds. Which of the fo​llowing is a reliable sig​n to differentiate entry ​from an exit wound?","raw_text":"A gunshot victim is brought to you for an autopsy. On examination, you note 2 wounds. Which of the following is a reliable sign to differentiate entry from an exit wound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inverted edges of the woun‌d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Round shape of entry wound‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abrasio‌n collar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greas‌e ring","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Grease ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Grease ring</strong></p><p>Grease ring is a reliable sign to differentiate entry from an exit wound. </p>\n<p>The abrasion collar or its inner parts may be blackened due to overlying sooty remnants wiped off by the bullet when hitting uncovered skin. This finding is called a <strong>grease</strong> <strong>ring</strong>, a ring of dirt or bullet wipe-off. It can be regarded as a <strong>reliable sign of entrance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Inverted edges</strong> cannot be regarded as a reliable sign of entry wound. The edges of an entry wound may be <strong>everted</strong> by the <strong>blowback</strong> phenomenon in a contact wound. This occurs when the gases strike a bony surface and are reflected back, everting the entry wound edges.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>round shape</strong> of entry wounds is unreliable as entry wounds and exit wounds may be of many shapes. This depends on many factors, including the range of firing, angle of firing, presence of subcutaneous bone, etc.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>In<strong> </strong>an <strong>abrasion collar</strong> the skin immediately around the central hole is discoloured, the so-called ‘abrasion collar’ or ‘contusion ring’. The abrasion ring is primarily due to the massive overstretching and subsequent drying-up of the skin adjacent to the area of projectile impact. It cannot be taken as a reliable sign of entry wound as <strong>an exit wound </strong>may have a<strong> spurious abrasion collar</strong>. This occurs when the exit wound is supported against a hard surface and the emerging bullet slams the wound periphery against this surface.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1751","text":"All of the following bulle‌ts are modified to ensure‍ that it slows down in th​e body to impart maximum ​damage except?","raw_text":"All of the following bullets are modified to ensure that it slows down in the body to impart maximum damage except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dumdum‌ bullet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Softhead‌ed bullet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Explosive-t‌ipped bullet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tracer‌ bullet","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tracer bullet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/900b9610a36f4eb1b6afb82692c0f17cx1280x795.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tracer bullet</strong></p><p>All the bullets mentioned in the options are modified to ensure that on entering the body, they slow down enough to impart maximum damage except tracer bullets. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">For damage to occur, some or all of the kinetic energy of the missile has to be absorbed by the target tissues, where it is dissipated as heat, noise and mechanical disruption. When a missile passes completely through soft tissues, it may retain much of its original kinetic energy and fail to transfer any appreciable amount to the tissues. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">To ensure transfer of energy to the tissues, some missiles are especially designed or modified to slow up or stop within the body.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong> Softheaded bullets</strong> will flatten on impact and some are designed to fragment.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>dumdum bullet </strong>fragments upon striking<strong> </strong>and transfers more energy for tissue displacement and disruption.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Explosive-tipped bullets</strong>, cause fire in the target due to the presence of explosives at the tip. They are designed to deform the missile to cause maximum deceleration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Tracer bullet</strong> sparks during flight. It contains a mixture of barium nitrate, powdered magnesium and strontium nitrate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1777","text":"The following image shows ‌the resulting wound cavit‍y from a bullet. Which of​ the following explains t​he shape of the wound cav​ity?","raw_text":"The following image shows the resulting wound cavity from a  bullet.  Which of the following explains the shape of the wound cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yawing‌ bullet","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tandem‌ bullet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rico‌chet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Souveni‌r bullet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yawing bullet","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6b4f2ad07534af29915ea8a8dd3c335.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yawing bullet</strong></p><p>The shape of the wound cavity can be explained by <strong>yawing </strong>of the bullet. </p>\n<p>Yawing is a common phenomenon seen with bullets (not with spheres). It is the <strong>rotation of the bullet</strong> around a lateral axis while passing through dense media such as tissues. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>angle</strong> between the <strong>trajectory</strong> and the<strong> long axis</strong> of the bullet <strong>increases</strong> due to destabilising forces until it reaches 90 degrees , i.e., the bullet’s long axis is perpendicular to the trajectory. The yawing motion then continues until <strong>180 degrees</strong><span class=\"s1\"> </span>and the bullet will remain in this stable position where the base is the leading part.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The path of a projectile in a dense medium therefore shows three distinct sections, depending on the respective cross-sectional area:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Narrow channel</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Increased cavities</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Tail end</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1758","text":"In a case of suspected hom‌icide, the wife has been ‍arrested for allegedly sh​ooting the husband. She w​as found to be guilty bas​ed on the presence of gun​powder residue on her han​ds. Which of the followin​g tests is most sensitive​ for the detection of gun​powder residue?","raw_text":"In a case of suspected homicide, the wife has been arrested for allegedly shooting the husband. She was found to be guilty based on the presence of gunpowder residue on her hands. Which of the following tests is most sensitive for the detection of gunpowder residue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inductively coupled plasma‌ atomic emission spectros‍copy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutron activation analysi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scanning electron microsco‌py with energy dispersive‍ X-ray analysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flameless atomic absorptio‌n spectroscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scanning electron microscopy with energy dispersive X-ray analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scanning electron microscopy with energy dispersive X-ray analysis</strong></p><p><strong>Scanning Electron Microscopy</strong> with Energy Dispersive X-ray Analysis (SEM–EDX) is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> as well as the <strong>most</strong> <strong>commonly</strong> used test for detection of <strong>gunshot residue</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tests for detection of gunpowder residue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dermal nitrate (paraffin) test</li>\n<li>Harrison and Gilroy test</li>\n<li>Neutron activation analysis (NAA)</li>\n<li>Flameless atomic absorption spectroscopy (FAAS)</li>\n<li>Inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) - a variant of Flameless atomic absorption spectroscopy</li>\n<li>Scanning electron microscopy with energy dispersive X-ray analysis (SEM -EDX)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1786","text":"The lead fragments when st‌ripped from the bullet as‍ it travels through the b​ody, cause multiple fragm​ents along the wound trac​k. What is this phenomeno​n called?","raw_text":"The lead fragments when stripped from the bullet as it travels through the body, cause multiple fragments along the wound track. What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lead sn‌owstorm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wound p‌eppering","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullet ‌scatter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lead snowstorm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lead snowstorm</strong></p><p><strong>Lead snowstorm</strong> is the phenomenon caused by a firearm wound, when lead fragments are stripped from the bullet as it travels through the body. It causing multiple fragments to be present along the wound track.</p>\n<p>This phenomenon is caused by <strong>semi-jacketed hunting ammunition</strong> used in rifled guns. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1814","text":"While recovering a bullet ‌from the crime scene, whi‍ch of the following shoul​d be followed?","raw_text":"While recovering a bullet from the crime scene, which of the following should be followed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullet should be picked up‌ with metallic toothed fo‍rceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bullet should be removed w‌ith bare hands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullet should be packed in‌ leakproof packaging made‍ of hard plastic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bullet should be wiped wit‌h a clean cloth to remove‍ dirt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bullet should be packed in leakproof packaging made of hard plastic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bullet should be packed in leakproof packaging made of hard plastic</strong></p><p>The <strong>bullet</strong> should be <strong>packed</strong> in <strong>leakproof</strong> packaging made of<strong> hard plastic.</strong></p>\n<p>Precautions to be followed while recovering a bullet from a crime scene:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bullets should be handled only by persons wearing <strong>double heavy-duty gloves</strong> (and not by bare hands - due to risk of contamination).</li>\n<li>To prevent marring of the bullet surface, it should be removed by a<strong> plastic forceps</strong> or a <strong>rubber-tipped</strong> bullet extractor.</li>\n<li>The bullet is gently <strong>rinsed after collection </strong>of any <strong>trace evidence</strong> on the bullet. The rinsing removes any contaminating body fluids to decrease infective risk.</li>\n<li>The bullet or bullet fragments should be <strong>double packed in leakproof</strong> packaging with at least one of the containers made out of hard plastic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1798","text":"A police inspector present‌s to the E.R. with an abr‍asion on his forearm as s​hown in the image below. ​He gives a history of bei​ng shot at while trying t​o prevent an armed burgla​ry. Identify the type of ​injury.","raw_text":"A police inspector presents to the E.R. with an abrasion on his forearm as shown in the image below. He gives a history of being shot at while trying to prevent an armed burglary. Identify the type of injury.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullet‌ bruise","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bullet‌ graze","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bulle‌t mark","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bulle‌t wipe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bullet graze","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/55b45d18450548078336992c67e7eb72.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bullet graze</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>bullet graze.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bullet graze</strong>: the bullet just hits the skin only at an angle and goes off from the site, without any penetration or wound of entry. It produces an abrasion or a laceration depending on the thickness of the skin involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Bullet bruise:</strong> the bullet here just hits the skin only and drops down to the ground, without any penetration or wound of entry. It produces a bruise alone at the site of impact.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0174","text":"What is bu‌llet wipe?","raw_text":"What is bullet wipe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Residual from barrel of gu‌n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Black‌ening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gutter fract‌ure of skull","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tatt‌ooing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Residual from barrel of gun","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Residual from barrel of gun</strong></p><p><strong>Bullet wipe</strong> is due to the grease, soot or debris from the barrel of the gun.</p>\n<p>While examining the clothing of a victim of bullet injury, there is a deposition of various residues surrounding the entrance defect at various ranges known as Bullet wipe. It helps in determining the wounds of inlet and outlet upon the body.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1812","text":"What is the bullet recover‌ed from the victim of a h‍omicidal shooting known a​s?","raw_text":"What is the bullet recovered from the victim of a homicidal shooting known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crime ‌bullet","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Test ‌bullet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exhibit‌ bullet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suspect‌ Bullet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Crime bullet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Crime bullet</strong></p><p>Bullet collected at surgery or autopsy, from the body of a victim alive or dead is called a <strong>crime bullet</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Test bullet</strong> is a bullet that is fired from a weapon suspected of being involved in a crime; the test firing is into a gunny bag fixed into a deal wood box. The bullet is then collected for comparison with crime bullet under a comparison microscope, for identifying the crime weapon.This can only be done in relation to rifled weapons.</p>\n<p><strong>Exhibit bullet</strong> is a crime/test bullet, which is presented in the court as evidence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3021","text":"A retired army officer was‌ advised to undergo a com‍plete health check-up. Du​ring an examination, the ​radiologist identifies a ​bullet present in the bod​y surrounded by a dense f​ibrous tissue capsule. Wh​at type of bullet is it c​alled?","raw_text":"A retired army officer was advised to undergo a complete health check-up. During an examination, the radiologist identifies a bullet present in the body surrounded by a dense fibrous tissue capsule. What type of bullet is it called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tandem‌ bullet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Piggybac‌k bullet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incendiar‌y bullets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Souveni‌r bullet","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Souvenir bullet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Souvenir bullet</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The kind of bullet identified by the radiologist as explained in the given scenario is known as <strong>souvenir bullet.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Souvenir bullet is the one which has been lodged in the body for a long time with no fresh bleeding around it and <strong>surrounded by a dense fibrous tissue capsule. </strong><span class=\"s1\">They can be visualized on X-rays. </span>They are usually a result of bullet lodging in an inaccessible space which may result in more damage on attempted retrieval. e.g. Bullet in the spine.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other options:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tandem bullet (Piggyback bullet)</strong>: This is where two bullets come out with a single shot. Seen with unused or locally made weapons. It may cause confusion with the number of entry and exit wounds.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"s1\">Incendiary bullets: </span></strong>Type of army bullet used to cause fire in the target.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6397","text":"Choose the option with cor‌rect matches: Term Descri‍ption 1. Puppe's Rule a. ​Surgical Intervention of ​a firearm wound leading t​o an artefact 2. Kennedy ​Phenomena b. Planting a b​ullet inside a stab wound​ 3. Rayalseema Phenomenon​ c. Relationship between ​trauma and new growth 4. ‌Ewing's Postulates d. Chr​onological sequence of bu‌llets fired","raw_text":"<p>Choose the option with correct matches:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 255px;\" width=\"474\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Term</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">1. Puppe's Rule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">a. Surgical Intervention of a firearm wound leading to an artefact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p>2. Kennedy Phenomena</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">b. Planting a bullet inside a stab wound</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">3. Rayalseema Phenomenon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">c. Relationship between trauma and new growth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">4. Ewing's Postulates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">d. Chronological sequence of bullets fired</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-d, 2-b,‌ 3-a, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-c,‌ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-a,‌ 3-b, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-d, 2-a,‌ 3-b, 4-c","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c</strong></p><p>The correct match would be as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 255px;\" width=\"474\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Term</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">1. Puppe's Rule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">d.Chronological sequence of bullets fired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p>2. Kennedy Phenomena</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">a. Surgical Intervention of a firearm wound leading to an artefact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">3. Rayalseema Phenomenon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">b.Planting a bullet inside a stab wound </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">4. Ewing's Postulates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">c. Relationship between trauma and new growth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Puppe's rule</strong>: Helps in determining the <strong>sequence of bullets</strong> to hit the skull, when there are multiple bullet injuries to skull. The fracture line resulting from the later bullet will typically stop at the previously formed one.</p>\n<p><strong>Kennedy's phenomenon</strong>: Surgical intervention of a firearm wound leading to an artefact as the evaluation of wound as entry or exit wound becomes difficult.</p>\n<p><strong>Rayalaseema phenomenon</strong>: Here a person is killed by stab injury, and a bullet is implanted inside to mislead the investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Ewing's postulates</strong>: Establishes a relationship between trauma and new growth.</p>\n<p>The following postulates should be satisfied before a relationship between trauma and new growth is accepted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evidence of <strong>previous integrity</strong> of the injured part.</li>\n<li>Undeniable and <strong>adequate trauma</strong> must be proved.</li>\n<li>There must be a proof of <strong>reasonable time interval</strong> between injury and appearance of tumour.</li>\n<li>Disease must develop at the <strong>exact site of injury</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>nature of the tumour</strong> must be proved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3035","text":"A forensic expert receives‌ the skull of a gunshot v‍ictim. What is the sequen​ce of gunshots in this ca​se?","raw_text":"A forensic expert receives the skull of a gunshot victim. What is the sequence of gunshots in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3→ ‌2→ 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2→ ‌1→ 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2→ ‌3→ 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1→ ‌2→ 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1→ 2→ 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb3c4d8669514e72a70c0af5ce07cda0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1→ 2→ 3</strong></p><p>The sequence of gunshots, in this case, is <strong>1→ 2→ 3</strong> according to <strong>Puppe's rule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Puppe's rule states that the path of a<span class=\"s1\"> second fracture line does not cross the path of an existing fracture line that appears or was caused before it. This helps the forensic expert determine the chronology (order) in which the bullets were lodged into the skull. </span></p>\n<p>Puppe's rule is used in forensic examination of skull fractures caused by multiple blunt force vectors as well, as it is not limited to the examination of bullet injuries only. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1816","text":"What is a Molotov cocktail‌?","raw_text":"What is a Molotov cocktail?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcoholi‌c poison","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incendi‌ary bomb","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acid‌ bomb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifled ‌firearm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incendiary bomb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96602972deae46298171eb171302fdc7x376x205.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incendiary bomb</strong></p><p>Molotov cocktail is an <strong>incendiary</strong> bomb that is thrown by hand.</p>\n<p>In this crude type of bomb, a bottle is filled with gasoline and a rag to serve as a wick, the wick is lit and thrown at the target.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1815","text":"Which is the most severely‌ injured organ in blast i‍njuries in underwater and​ air respectively?","raw_text":"Which is the most severely injured organ in blast injuries in underwater and air respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intesti‌ne, Lung","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lung, I‌ntestine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic me‌mbrane, Lung","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung, Tympa‌nic membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intestine, Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intestine, Lung</strong></p><p>In underwater blasts, the intestines suffer the most damage.</p>\n<p>A blast causes the most damage at an interface between tissues in contact with the atmosphere, which is why <strong>the lung usually suffers the most severe damage. </strong>However the most commonly affected structure in air blasts is the tympanic membrane. </p>\n<p>The gastrointestinal system suffers from the effect of a blast because, like the lung, it contains air and gases, and is thus not a uniform medium for transit of the shock wave.</p>\n<p>Victims of explosions in water suffer a reverse order of gastrointestinal to lung damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: In air, the ear, especially the tympanic membrane is severely damaged, however, in a cadaver it is not discernible without an otoscopic examination of the tympanic membrane. Nevertheless, lungs are considered to be the severely damaged organ in the air due to variations in density between the alveolar walls and the contained air.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2305","text":"Following a blast, a man w‌as brought from the scene‍ to the emergency with mu​ltiple small puncture wou​nds to the right side of ​the chest, below the nipp​le, and multiple small ab​rasions and bruising over​ the left lower abdomen. ​Which of the following me​chanisms is responsible f‌or these injuries?","raw_text":"Following a blast, a man was brought from the scene to the emergency with multiple small puncture wounds to the right side of the chest, below the nipple, and multiple small abrasions and bruising over the left lower abdomen. Which of the following mechanisms is responsible for these injuries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flying ‌missiles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blast ‌of air","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Explosi‌on burns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Building‌ collapse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flying missiles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flying missiles</strong></p><p>The given history of multiple small <strong>puncture wounds</strong> to the right side of the chest, below the nipple, and multiple small <strong>abrasions</strong> and <strong>bruising</strong> over the left lower abdomen are highly suggestive of <strong>blast injuries</strong> sustained due to <strong>flying missiles</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Blast injury</strong> is a complex type of physical trauma resulting from direct or indirect exposure to an explosion causing a sudden increase or decrease in atmospheric pressure.</p>\n<p>T.K.Marshall’s (<strong>Marshall's triad</strong>) described the characteristic injuries associated with bomb explosions. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abrasions</li>\n<li>Bruises/contusions</li>\n<li>Puncture lacerations</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 286\">Blast injuries are classified as:</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary injuries</strong>: Effect of transmitted blast waves on gas-containing structures. The external injuries are absent and are characterized by internal injuries. The middle, ear, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract are the most susceptible to this.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary injuries</strong>: Injuries caused by shrapnel and other airborne debris propelled by the explosion. These injuries may affect any part of the body and sometimes result in penetrating trauma. Most casualties are caused by secondary injuries.</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary injuries</strong>: These are caused by blast wind that can throw victims against solid objects.</li>\n<li><strong>Quaternary injuries</strong>: All other injuries caused by explosions are not included in the above three categories. eg, flash burns, crush injuries, respiratory effects due to toxic dust, gas, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Blast of air or blast wave cause primary injuries which involve internal organs without external injury.</p>\n<p>Option C: Explosion burns: When the bomb explodes, the temperature of the explosive gases can exceed 2000°C, and the heat radiated momentarily can cause flash burns. It is a quaternary type of blast injury.</p>\n<p>Option D: Building collapse can cause crush injuries which is a quaternary blast injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OC34lJd-","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"10_multiple_endocrine_neoplasia","title":"10.Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB9934","text":"Multiple endocrine neoplas‍ia (MEN) type 1 is also c​alled as ___________ .","raw_text":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN) type 1 is also called as ___________ .","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wermer's‍ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sipple ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weber's ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Werner ‍syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wermer's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wermer&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>MEN1 is also referred to as <strong>Wermer's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3124","text":"Which of the following con‍ditions is not observed i​n MEN1 syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not observed in MEN1 syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior pituitary tumors‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Foregut ‍carcinoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parathyro‍id adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angiof‍ibroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior pituitary tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior pituitary tumors</strong></p><p><strong>Anterior pituitary</strong> tumours are associated with <strong>MEN1</strong> syndrome, not posterior pituitary tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3126","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‍d with abdominal pain, na​usea, and diarrhea. He ha​d a history of hyperparat​hyroidism and a transsphe‍noidal prolactinoma resec​tion. Which of the follow​ing is the most common fu‍nctional enteropancreatic‌ tumor seen in this condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with abdominal pain, nausea, and diarrhea. He had a history of hyperparathyroidism and a transsphenoidal prolactinoma resection. Which of the following is the most common functional enteropancreatic tumor seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluca‍gonoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insul‍inoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastr‍inoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"VIP‍oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastrinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastrinoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> MEN1</strong> syndrome and <strong>gastrinoma</strong> is the most common <strong>functional</strong> enteropancreatic tumor in MEN1 / Wermer's syndrome. </p>\n<p>Enteropancreatic tumors in MEN1 syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-functioning pancreatic tumors</strong> (most common overall)</li>\n<li>Functioning pancreatic tumors :\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrinoma - 50% (most common among functioning pancreatic tumours)</li>\n<li>Insulinoma - 30%</li>\n<li>Glucagonoma</li>\n<li>VIPoma (least common)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1178","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common site of g​astrinoma in MEN 1 syndro​me?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site of gastrinoma in MEN 1 syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jej‍unum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Il‍eum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duod‍enum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sto‍mach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duodenum</strong></p><p>Most common site of <strong>gastrinoma</strong> in MEN 1 syndrome is the first and second part of <strong>duodenum</strong>.</p>\n<p>MEN 1 syndrome is characterized by the<strong> triad</strong> of tumors involving the parathyroids, pancreatic islets, and anterior pituitary.</p>\n<p>Gastrinoma is the <strong>most common</strong> pancreatic islet tumor in MEN 1 syndrome and if it is associated with marked gastric acid production and recurrent peptic ulcers, the condition is called <strong>Zollinger Ellison syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3146","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‍ to the OPD with complain​ts of recurrent episodes ​of weakness, headache, sw​eating, and fainting afte‍r fasting. He tells that ​the symptoms improve afte​r the consumption of food‍. There is a history of M‌EN1 syndrome running in t‍he family. Serum C peptid​e levels are found to be ‍elevated. What is the def‌initive treatment of choi​ce in this patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents to the OPD with complaints of recurrent episodes of weakness, headache, sweating, and fainting after fasting. He tells that the symptoms improve after the consumption of food.  There is a history of MEN1 syndrome running in the family. Serum C peptide levels are found to be elevated. What is the definitive treatment of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frequent consumption carbo‍hydrate-rich meals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Octre‍otide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diaz‍oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sur‍gery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Surgery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with elevated serum C peptide levels points towards a diagnosis of <strong>insulinoma </strong>in a MEN1 patient. The definitive treatment of choice in this patient is <strong>surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Insulinoma is the second most common functioning enteropancreatic tumor in MEN1 syndrome. Investigation of choice is <strong>72-hour fasting glucose </strong>and<strong> serum insulin levels. </strong>These parameters are measured when the patient develops hypoglycemic symptoms during a supervised 72-hour fasting period.</p>\n<p>The treatment of choice is surgery, ranging from <strong>enucleation</strong> of a single tumor to <strong>pancreatectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Non-surgical management of insulinoma includes frequent consumption of carbohydrate-rich meals, octreotide or diazoxide, which are not always successful.</p>\n<p><strong>Selpercatinib,</strong> a kinase inhibitor was recently approved by the FDA. It is used to treat tumours with a mutation in the RET gene. These are non-small cell lung cancer, medullary thyroid cancer, and other types of thyroid cancers in patients with a mutation in the RET gene.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3128","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most frequently obser​ved cutaneous manifestati​on of MEN1 syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most frequently observed cutaneous manifestation of MEN1 syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lip‍oma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiof‍ibroma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Colla‍genoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurof‍ibroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Angiofibroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Angiofibroma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most frequently </strong>observed <strong>cutaneous</strong> manifestation of MEN1 syndrome is <strong>angiofibroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cutaneous tumors in MEN1 Syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Angiofibroma – 85%</li>\n<li>Collagenoma – 70%</li>\n<li>Lipoma - 33%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3131","text":"A 22-year-old man who was ‍diagnosed with MEN1 syndr​ome presents to your OPD ​for his annual checkup. W​hich of the following inv‍estigations will you not ​do as part of annual scre​ening for this patient?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man who was diagnosed with MEN1 syndrome presents to your OPD for his annual checkup. Which of the following investigations will you not do as part of annual screening for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasting glu‍cose levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum calc‍ium levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secreti‍n study","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum gast‍rin levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secretin study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secretin study</strong></p><p>Secretin study is not done as an annual screening test in patients with MEN1 syndrome. It is a <strong>provocative test</strong> for the diagnosis of gastrinoma. </p>\n<p>Annual biochemical screening tests to be done in patients with MEN1 syndrome are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 545px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\"><strong>Tumor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.5875px;\"><strong>Age to begin screening</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.413px;\"><strong>Annual screening test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Parathyroid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.5875px; text-align: center;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.413px;\"><strong>Calcium</strong>, PTH</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Gastrinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.5875px; text-align: center;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.413px;\"><strong>Gastrin</strong> (+/- gastric pH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Insulinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.5875px; text-align: center;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.413px;\"><strong>Fasting glucose</strong>, insulin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Other pancreatic NETs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.5875px; text-align: center;\">&lt;10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.413px;\">Chromogranin A; pancreatic polypeptide, glucagon, <br/>vasoactive intestinal peptide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Anterior pituitary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88.5875px; text-align: center;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.413px;\">Prolactin, IGF-I</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other imaging tests like MRI and CT might have to be done for screening of conditions like anterior pituitary tumors or adrenal tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3133","text":"What is the most common co‍mponent of Sipple’s syndr​ome?","raw_text":"What is the most common component of Sipple’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary th‍yroid cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara‍thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitar‍y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastr‍inoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medullary thyroid cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medullary thyroid cancer</strong></p><p>The most common component of <strong>MEN2A </strong>syndrome, which is also called <strong>Sipple’s</strong> syndrome is <strong>medullary thyroid cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3134","text":"Which of the following fea‍tures would you not expec​t to see in a patient wit​h MEN2 syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with MEN2 syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pituitar‍y adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Parathyro‍id adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary th‍yroid cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pheochro‍mocytoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pituitary adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pituitary adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Pituitary adenoma </strong>is not a characteristic feature<strong> </strong>of<strong> </strong>the symptom complex of MEN2 and its variants, but it is observed in <strong>MEN1 </strong>and <strong>MEN4 syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3136","text":"Which of the following fea‍tures would you not expec​t to see in a patient wit​h MEN2B syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with MEN2B syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucosal ‍neuromas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marfanoi‍d habitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullated corneal nerve f‍ibers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin‍gioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Meningioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Meningioma</strong></p><p>Meningioma is not commonly seen in MEN2B Syndrome. MEN1 syndrome is associated with meningiomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3138","text":"MEN4 syndrome is caused by‍ a mutation in which of t​he following genes?","raw_text":"MEN4 syndrome is caused by a mutation in which of the following genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PPK‍AR1A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RET proto‍-oncogene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PRK‍AR1B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CDK‍N1B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CDKN1B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CDKN1B</strong></p><p><strong>MEN4</strong> is caused by genetic mutations in <strong>CDKN1B</strong> (cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor), located on chromosome <strong>12.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>MEN1</strong> is caused due to <strong>MEN1</strong> gene mutation on chromosome<strong> 11.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>MEN2</strong> is associated with <strong>RET</strong> proto-oncogene mutation on chromosome <strong>10.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3140","text":"Which of the following mal‍ignancies would you not e​xpect to see in a patient​ with MEN4 syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following malignancies would you not expect to see in a patient with MEN4 syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pituitar‍y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parathyro‍id adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary thyroid carcinom‍a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ‍tumors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medullary thyroid carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medullary thyroid carcinoma</strong></p><p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma is not associated with MEN4 syndrome, but it is associated with MEN2 syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3142","text":"A patient presented with a‍bdominal pain and swellin​g in his scrotum and was ​diagnosed to have testicu​lar carcinoma. There is a‍ history of MEN syndrome ​running in his family. Wh​ich of the following cond‍itions is he likely to ha‌ve?","raw_text":"A patient presented with abdominal pain and swelling in his scrotum and was diagnosed to have testicular carcinoma. There is a history of MEN syndrome running in his family. Which of the following conditions is he likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ME‍N4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"ME‍N2A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ME‍N1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ME‍N2B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MEN4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MEN4</strong></p><p>This patient is likely to have <strong>MEN4</strong> syndrome as <strong>reproductive organ tumors </strong>are a characteristic feature of this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zB9yjWn8","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"10_mycobacteria_tuberculosis","title":"10.Mycobacteria Tuberculosis","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB9212","text":"The following slide is giv​en as a spotter in your m​icrobiology practical exa‍mination. Identify the fo‌llowing organism.","raw_text":"The following slide is given as a spotter in your microbiology practical examination. Identify the following organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycoba​cteria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Noca​rdia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Actin​omyces","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coryneb​acterium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mycobacteria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/36a8ddd4a8b4470887ee5a0e96ad88ad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1afc227efd4c4caab4c862cd30d6d737x720x501.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04f6afcd0f024dbbad620fb7f59e820ax305x235.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfdfa4e8cbe2459eb59843248b4de506x1280x2413.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mycobacteria</strong></p><p>The image shows pink<strong> non-branching acid-fast bacilli</strong> on a blue background. These findings are consistent with<strong> </strong>the staining characteristics of <strong>mycobacteria</strong>. The stain used is the <strong>Ziehl-Neelsen stain.</strong> </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: The image below<strong> </strong>shows <em>Nocardia </em>stained using an acid-fast staining technique. Nocardia is partially acid-fast and appears as <strong>branching filaments.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: The image given below is of a gram-stained specimen of <strong><em>Actinomyces israelii.</em></strong> A. israelii is non acid-fast</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: The images below show the staining characteristics of<em> Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</em> It is stained with Albert's stain which shows the needle appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2055","text":"Which of the following is ​the component of mycobact​eria which makes it acid ‍fast?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the component of mycobacteria which makes it acid fast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycoli​c acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Murami​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Teicho​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Talosaminu​ronic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mycolic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/060a681594874b6293a94c053d3cb638x1280x1326.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mycolic acid</strong></p><p>Mycobacteria are <strong>acid-fast</strong> due to the presence of <strong>mycolic acid</strong> in their <strong>cell walls.</strong> </p>\n<p>On being stained with <strong>carbol fuchsin</strong> or fluorescent dyes such as <strong>auramine-O or rhodamine,</strong> they <strong>resist decolourization</strong> by <strong>20% sulphuric acid</strong> and<strong> absolute alcohol</strong> for 10 minutes. This property is referred to as acid and alcohol fastness and is routinely used to identify mycobacteria.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2041","text":"A patient presents early i​n the morning to the DOTS​ center to provide sputum‍ samples. Which of the fo‌llowing reagents are used‌ in the digestion and dec‌ontamination of his sampl​es before smear preparati​on?","raw_text":"A patient presents early in the morning to the DOTS center to provide sputum samples. Which of the following reagents are used in the digestion and decontamination of his samples before smear preparation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaOH a​nd KOH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NaCl and N-acetyl cysteine​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N-acetyl cysteine and NaOH​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"KOH an​d NaCl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. N-acetyl cysteine and NaOH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. N-acetyl cysteine and NaOH</strong></p><p><strong>NaOH</strong> and <strong>N-acetylcysteine</strong> are used for the digestion and decontamination of sputum used in smear preparation for Mycobacterium tuberculosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Sputum</strong> and specimens from non-sterile sites need prior treatment for <strong>digestion</strong> (to liquefy the thick pus), <strong>decontamination</strong> (to inhibit the normal flora), and <strong>concentration</strong> (to increase the yield) before they can be used for testing.</p>\n<p>Methods used for digestion and decontamination of sputum: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Petroff’s method</strong>- Uses 4% NaOH and HCl. </li>\n<li><strong>N-acetyl cysteine (NALC)</strong> + <strong>2% NaOH -</strong> NALC liquefies the sputum and NaOH kills the normal flora. This method is better suited for automated culture systems and is superior to Petroff’s method. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Formalin or hypochlorite can be used only if smear microscopy is being done. They are not useful for culture or animal pathogenicity testing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2056","text":"A patient with suspected t​uberculosis gives a sputu​m sample for microscopic ‍examination. What is the ‌minimum number of acid-fa‌st bacilli that should be‌ present in 1 mL of sputu​m for them to be detected​ by this method?","raw_text":"A patient with suspected tuberculosis gives a sputum sample for microscopic examination. What is the minimum number of acid-fast bacilli that should be present in 1 mL of sputum for them to be detected by this method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​00","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10​00","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10​000","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10000</strong></p><p>At least <strong>10,000 acid-fast bacilli</strong> should be present <strong>per milliliter</strong> of <strong>sputum</strong> for them to be readily demonstrable in direct smears.</p>\n<p>Slide reporting as per NTEP guidelines: </p>\n<table cellpadding=\"5\" cellspacing=\"5\" style=\"width: 427px; height: 238px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 56.5195px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.5px; height: 56.5195px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>No. of AFB</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.5px; height: 56.5195px; text-align: center;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>No. of oil immersion </strong><strong>fields examined</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.25px; height: 56.5195px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Report</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.5px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\"><strong>0</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.5px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\">100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.25px; height: 13px; text-align: center;\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>1-9</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.25px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Scanty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>10-99</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.25px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">1+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>1-10</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.25px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">2+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>&gt;10</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.5px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.25px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">3+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2045","text":"A patient has chronic coug​h with occasional blood i​n the sputum. He has a hi‍story of significant weig‌ht loss and night sweats.‌ Which of the following m‌edia cannot be used to cu​lture the organism causin​g his symptoms?","raw_text":"A patient has chronic cough with occasional blood in the sputum. He has a history of significant weight loss and night sweats. Which of the following media cannot be used to culture the organism causing his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BACTE​C 460","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middlebr​ook 7H10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loeffler​s medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"McLeod'​s Medium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. McLeod's Medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/263b127be9ad4f86a0295669d716fa14x1280x1511.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25fdd178ad3741e9b7ae3c14e5681d45x1280x2285.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59778e29193149a8b6ecb7be9087655dx1226x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. McLeod&#x27;s Medium</strong></p><p>This patient with chronic cough and hemoptysis with a concurrent history of significant weight loss and night sweats most likely has tuberculosis, caused by <strong><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis.</em></strong> <strong>McLeod's medium</strong> is not used to culture <em>M. tuberculosis. </em>It is a selective medium for <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em></strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Culture media</strong> used to cultivate <strong><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis </em></strong>include<em>:</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Solid- Loeffler's medium </strong>is a serum-containing solid medium used for the culture of <em>M. tuberculosis</em>. Other solid media used include Lowenstein- Jensen medium, Dorset medium, Tarshis medium, and Pawlowsky's medium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Liquid- </strong><strong>Middleb</strong><strong>rook’s media</strong> are used for the preparation of antigens and vaccines and in sensitivity testing.  Other examples of liquid mycobacterial culture media include Dubos' medium, Sula's medium and Sauton's medium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Automated</strong> culture methods: </li>\n<li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BACTEC 460- </strong>uses radioisotopes for detection</li>\n<li><strong>BacT alert- </strong>colorimetric method of detection</li>\n<li><strong>BACTEC MGIT- </strong>fluorometric detection method. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5969","text":"A microbiology resident is​ performing liquid automa​ted culture on the sputum‍ sample of a TB suspect p‌atient. Which of the foll‌owing culture media would‌ he not use for the same?​","raw_text":"A microbiology resident is performing liquid automated culture on the sputum sample of a TB suspect patient. Which of the following culture media would he not use for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacti​Alert","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vers​atrek","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BACTEC ​MGIT 960","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tar​shis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tarshis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tarshis</strong></p><p>Tarshis medium is a <strong>solid</strong> growth medium used for culturing mycobacterium species. Versatrek, BactiAlert, and BACTEC MGIT 960 are liquid automated cultures used for tuberculosis. </p>\n<p>The diagnostic tools for <strong>TB</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<p>1. Sputum microscopy for AFB</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ziehl Neelson staining</li>\n<li>Fluorescence staining</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Culture</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Solid media– Lowenstein-Jensen (<strong>most common</strong>), Petragnini and Dorset, <strong>Tarshis</strong>, Loeffler, Pawlowsky.</li>\n<li>Liquid automated culture – <strong>Versatrek</strong>, <strong>BactiAlert</strong>, <strong>BACTEC MGIT 960 </strong>(MGIT stands for Mycobacteria Growth Indicator Tube)</li>\n<li>Drug Sensitivity Testing / Proportionate Sensitivity Testing:\n<ul>\n<li>Using MGIT, LJ medium</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. </strong>Rapid molecular diagnostic testing</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Line probe assay</li>\n<li>CB-NAAT- Nucleic acid amplification test using GeneXpert system</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. Other diagnostic tools are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chest X-ray</li>\n<li>Tuberculin sensitivity testing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2060","text":"Which of the following tes​ts can simultaneously det​ect TB and rifampicin res‍istance in &lt;2 hours?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests can simultaneously detect TB and rifampicin resistance in &lt;2 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LAM detecti​on by ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Xpert ​MTB/RIF","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BACTE​C MGIT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Line pro​be assay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Xpert MTB/RIF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db709f3c0f884347997ffbc266312462x1280x1710.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Xpert MTB/RIF</strong></p><p><strong>Xpert MTB/RIF</strong> detects tuberculosis (TB) and rifampicin resistance simultaneously <strong>within 2 hours</strong>. It is a fully automated, real-time nucleic acid amplification technology, with<strong> high specificity and sensitivity. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>WHO</strong> recommends its use worldwide as the <strong>initial diagnostic test</strong> in adults and children who are presumed to have multi-drug resistant tuberculosis (<strong>MDR-TB) or HIV-associated TB. </strong>Xpert MTB/RIF should also be the first test applied to the cerebrospinal fluid (<strong>CSF</strong>) of patients in whom tubercular meningitis is suspected as well as a replacement test (over conventional microscopy, culture, and histopathology) for <strong>gastric lavage</strong>, pleural and other <strong>biopsies</strong>, and fine-needle aspiration specimens. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the Xpert MTB/RIF system.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2058","text":"Which of the following is ​not a method of testing d​rug susceptibility in Myc‍obacterium tuberculosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a method of testing drug susceptibility in Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radiometric ​broth method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Molecula​r method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disc diffu​sion method","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Proporti​on Method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Disc diffusion method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/092ae6900d794fc0b3388d97d6327f9fx600x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Disc diffusion method</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Disc diffusion method</strong> is used for drug susceptibility testing for most bacteria; however, it is <strong>not used</strong> for<strong> <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis. </em></strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">The disc diffusion technique is not suitable for testing Mycobacterium tuberculosis because the organism grows so slowly that the drug has time to diffuse throughout the medium, and sharp zones of inhibition are not seen.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Methods used in the drug susceptibility testing of M.tb include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 177px;\" width=\"546\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.477px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Conventional drug-sensitivity testing</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Radiometric Techniques</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.841px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Molecular methods</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.477px; text-align: left;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Proportion method</li>\n<li>Absolute concentration method<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></li>\n<li><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">Resistance ratio method</span></li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.114px; text-align: left;\">\n<ol>\n<li>BACTEC 460</li>\n<li>MB/BacT</li>\n<li>Mycobacteria growth indicator tube (MGIT)</li>\n<li>VersaTREK</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.841px; text-align: left;\">\n<ol>\n<li>PCR-based assays</li>\n<li>DNA probe-based</li>\n<li>DNA microarray</li>\n<li>GeneXpert</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For agar-based methods, 7H10 or 7H11 Middlebrook media are used and only the proportion method can be applied. </p>\n<p>Radiometric techniques measure the metabolic activity of the bacteria. Based on changes in these values, drug susceptibility can be estimated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3250","text":"TB test done only on whole​ blood sample is ________​___.","raw_text":"TB test done only on whole blood sample is ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TrueN​at TB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gene ​xpert","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interferon gamma release a​ssay","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All th​e above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Interferon gamma release assay","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Interferon gamma release assay</strong></p><p>TB test done on a <strong>whole blood</strong> sample is the <strong>interferon-gamma</strong> <strong>release assay.</strong> It has been used for the diagnosis of<strong> latent tuberculosis</strong> in patients with <strong>HIV</strong>(who frequently have a negative Mantoux test).</p>\n<p>Interferon-gamma release assays include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>QuantiFERON-TB Gold</li>\n<li>T-SPOT.TB</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>QuantiFERON-TB Gold</strong> quantitates the <strong>amount</strong> of<strong> IFN-γ</strong> produced by CD4+ T cells in response to the antigens from <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis. </em><strong>QuantiFERON-TB Gold Plus </strong>(QFT- Plus) is an improved version of the QuantiFERON-TB Gold test. QuantiFERON-TB Gold detects only TB-specific CD4+ responses. However, QuantiFeron-TB Gold Plus detects <strong>both CD8+</strong> and<strong> CD4+ T-cell responses</strong> to TB-specific antigens.</p>\n<p> <strong>T-SPOT.TB</strong> determines the <strong>total</strong> number of individual<strong> effector T cells</strong> expressing<strong> </strong>IFN-γ.</p>\n<p>The <strong>TrueNat TB test</strong> is a new molecular test that can diagnose TB in one hour as well as test for <strong>rifampicin </strong><strong>resistance. </strong>This test uses a <strong>sputum sample</strong> taken from the patient. Only about 0.5 ml of the sample is required. <strong>Gene Xpert</strong> is also a <strong>molecular method</strong> of testing that cannot use blood as a sample. (Option A and B)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0027","text":"A urine LAM (Lipoarabinoma​nnan) assay was performed​ in an HIV +ve patient wi‍th a CD4+ count of 40/μL.‌ Infection with which of ‌the following organisms c‌an be diagnosed using thi​s test?","raw_text":"A urine LAM (Lipoarabinomannan) assay was performed in an HIV  +ve patient with a CD4+ count of 40/μL. Infection with which of the following organisms can be diagnosed using this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. tube​rculosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"M. l​eprae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycop​lasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Listeria m​onocyogenes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. M. tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00b6d9c3b9654fb0ae3c26e2c8e093e4x1280x1326.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. M. tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Urine lipoarabinomannan</strong> (LAM) <strong>assay</strong> is used for the diagnosis of <strong>tuberculosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Lipoarabinomannan (LAM) antigen is a lipopolysaccharide present in the <strong>mycobacterial cell wall,</strong> released from metabolically active cells or degenerating cells of patients having tuberculosis. Urine LAM is a useful tool in diagnosing tuberculosis, especially in patients with <strong>HIV</strong> and <strong>low CD4+</strong> T cell count. </p>\n<p>Most diagnostic tests for tuberculosis rely on sputum samples, which are difficult to obtain and have low sensitivity in immunocompromised patients, patients with disseminated TB, and children, delaying treatment initiation. Lipoarabinomannan (LAM) is the only WHO-endorsed TB biomarker that can be detected in urine, an easily collected sample.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2057","text":"A middle-aged man came wit​h a history of fever and ​night sweats for the past‍ 2 weeks. He has had noti‌ceable weight loss. Bronc‌hoalveolar lavage was don‌e, but Mycobacterium tube​rculosis was not detected​. Extra-pulmonary TB is s​uspected. Which site is m‌ost likely affected?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man came with a history of fever and night sweats for the past 2 weeks. He has had noticeable weight loss. Bronchoalveolar lavage was done, but Mycobacterium tuberculosis was not detected. Extra-pulmonary TB is suspected. Which site is most likely affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genitouri​nary tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeleta​l system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastrointes​tinal tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph​ nodes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymph nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Lymph nodes</strong> are the <strong>most common </strong>site of <strong>extrapulmonary TB</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is established by<strong> fine needle aspiration biopsy</strong>. </p>\n<p>In descending order of frequency, the extrapulmonary sites most commonly involved in TB are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>lymph nodes</li>\n<li>pleura</li>\n<li>genitourinary tract</li>\n<li>bones and joints</li>\n<li>meninges, peritoneum and pericardium. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In less than 50% of these cases, there is associated pulmonary disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2053","text":"Your neighbour is worried ​about a scar on her 5-mon​th-old daughter's arm tha‍t is shown below. She tel‌ls you the child has rece‌ived all vaccines on time‌. Which of the following ​statements is true about ​the vaccine that caused t​his finding?","raw_text":"Your neighbour is worried about a scar on her 5-month-old daughter's arm that is shown below. She tells you the child has received all vaccines on time. Which of the following statements is true about the vaccine that caused this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distilled water is used as​ diluent for this vaccine​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The dose administered is 0​.5 ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The route of administratio​n is subcutaneous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WHO recommends Danish 1331​ strain for vaccine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. WHO recommends Danish 1331 strain for vaccine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4c89005691cd41babb8f7093e7ead7f6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. WHO recommends Danish 1331 strain for vaccine</strong></p><p>The scar shown in the image results from the BCG <strong>(Bacillus Calmette-Guérin)</strong> vaccine. The WHO recommends <strong>Danish 1331 strain</strong> for <strong>BCG</strong> vaccine production.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal saline</strong> is recommended as the<strong> diluent</strong> for the BCG vaccine as distilled water is an irritant.</p>\n<p>The dose administered is <strong>0.1ml</strong> containing 0.1mg.</p>\n<p>It is administered <strong>intradermally</strong> by using 26 gauge tuberculin syringe on the <strong>left deltoid.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1273","text":"You are performing the pre​-employment check up of a​ young man. You need to p‍erform a tuberculin skin ‌test as a part of this pr‌ocess. Which of the follo‌wing tests would you not ​use for this purpose?","raw_text":"You are performing the pre-employment check up of a young man. You need to perform a tuberculin skin test as a part of this process. Which of the following tests would you not use for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pirque​t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heaf​ test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"QuantiFER​ON-TB gold","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tine​ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. QuantiFERON-TB gold","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. QuantiFERON-TB gold</strong></p><p><strong>QuantiFERON-TB gold</strong> is not a type of tuberculin skin test. It is a type of <strong>interferon-gamma release assays</strong> (IGRAs), a <strong>whole-blood test</strong> that can aid in diagnosing <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> infection. </p>\n<p>There are 3 described methods for <strong>tuberculin skin testing</strong> (TST):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mantoux test, also known as the Pirquet test (preferred method)</li>\n<li>Heaf test</li>\n<li>Tine test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In contrast,  IGRAs that have been approved by the FDA are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>QuantiFERON-TB Gold In-Tube test (QFT-GIT)</li>\n<li>T-SPOT.TB test (T-Spot)</li>\n<li>QuantiFERON-TB Gold Plus (QFT- Plus)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IGRA is not used in India due to a higher probability of a false positive due to universal BCG vaccination. It can also lead to a diagnosis of active TB in a patient with latent TB and may lead to overtreatment. This test is banned in India after the WHO advised against it in low and middle-income countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"2lFngcmS","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"10_normal_labour","title":"10.Normal Labour","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MB7045","text":"Which of the following act‌s as the primary trigger ‍for onset of labour in pr​egnant women?","raw_text":"Which of the following  acts as the primary trigger for onset of labour in pregnant women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACTH in‌ mother","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACTH i‌n fetus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prosta‌glandin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxyt‌ocin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ACTH in fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bea7441b630742b88b63d7f1c2d44cccx1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ACTH in fetus</strong></p><p>The <strong>fetal ACTH</strong> causes an increase in <strong>estrogen</strong> acting as the onset of labour in pregnant women. It results in the growth of the fetal adrenal gland which has the capacity for steroid biosynthesis i.e. estrogen.</p>\n<p>Daily steroid production of fetal adrenal glands near term is more than in adults.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7046","text":"All of the following chang‌es are seen during cervic‍al ripening in a patient ​except _____.","raw_text":"All of the following changes are seen during cervical ripening in a patient except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collagen fibril diameter i‌s increased","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collagen fibrils are tight‌ly packed","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in glycosaminogly‌cans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stromal invasion with infl‌ammatory cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Collagen fibrils are tightly packed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collagen fibrils are tightly packed</strong></p><p>Collagen fibrils are <strong>loosely packed</strong> during cervical ripening.</p>\n<p><strong>Changes in cervical ripening:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collagen fibril diameter is increased, and there is increased spacing between fibrils leading to increased tissue compliance</li>\n<li>Increase in the amount of proteoglycan and glycosaminoglycan within the matrix</li>\n<li>Stromal invasion with inflammatory cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7047","text":"An obstetrician performs t‌he following procedure fo‍r labor induction in a pa​tient. What is the reflex​ elicited in the process?​","raw_text":"An obstetrician performs the following procedure for labor induction in a patient. What is the reflex elicited in the process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Andrews‌ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Johnson‌s reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gails ‌reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferguso‌n reflex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ferguson reflex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98105ca7bca1404181611249a94e9dd4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ferguson reflex</strong></p><p><strong>Stripping of membranes</strong> is depicted in the image, which elicits the <strong>Ferguson reflex</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fetal ejection reflex or Ferguson reflex is a phenomenon in which <strong>mechanical stretching</strong> of the internal <strong>cervix</strong> enhances <strong>uterine activity</strong>. Release of oxytocin from the maternal pituitary has been suggested but not proven.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 431\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is seen in stripping/sweeping and artificial rupture of membranes. Both of these are methods of induction of labor. It is associated with an increase in blood levels of <strong>prostaglandin F2α</strong> metabolite, also known as <strong>dinoprost/carboprost</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7049","text":"Which of the following ima‌ge shows the Duncan metho‍d?","raw_text":"Which of the following image shows the Duncan method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfffd46438eb413a8711b772ddec989b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fd0d617048c4f1bb817b59d84de986bx1280x2116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>Image <strong>B</strong> shows the<strong> Duncan method </strong>of placental separation. <span class=\"hvr\"><br/></span> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span class=\"hvr\">Comparison between two types of placental separation methods:</span></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 151px;\" width=\"400\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Schultze method</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Duncan method</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Separation begins from the center of the placenta to the periphery.</td>\n<td>Separation begins from the periphery to the center.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blood does not escape externally, as the blood pours into the membrane sac.</td>\n<td>Blood escapes from the vagina as the blood collects between the membranes and the uterine wall.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"hvr\"><strong>Brandt Andrew's method</strong> or <strong>controlled cord traction</strong> is a manoeuvre of expressing the placenta from the uterus in the third stage of labour. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"hvr\">One hand grasps the umbilical cord while the other is placed on the mother's abdomen with the fingers over the anterior surface of the uterus. While the hand on the abdomen is pressed backwards and slightly upward, the other applies gentle traction on the cord.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"hvr\"><strong>Type III placenta previa</strong> is a type of incomplete or partial central placenta previa. Here, the placenta covers the internal os partially i.e. it covers the internal os when closed but does not entirely do so when fully dilated.<br/></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7051","text":"In which stage of labour, ‌is the following expelled‍?","raw_text":"In which stage of labour, is the following expelled?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sec‌ond","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Th‌ird","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fou‌rth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fi‌rst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Third","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a48befa51ed24fd1b9c1a2db38d9b8a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Third</strong></p><p>The placenta and membranes are expelled in the <strong>third stage</strong> of labour.</p>\n<p>The clinical stages of labour may be summarized as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stages of labour</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First</td>\n<td>True labour pain to <strong>full dilatation of cervix</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second</td>\n<td>Full dilatation of cervix to<strong> expulsion of fetus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third</td>\n<td>Expulsion of fetus to<strong> expulsion of placenta</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fourth</td>\n<td><strong>One hour observation</strong> after the expulsion of placenta</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7052","text":"All of the following are i‌ncluded in the first stag‍e of labour for a pregnan​t woman except:","raw_text":"All of the following are included in the first stage of labour for a pregnant woman except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Effacement‌ of cervix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilatation‌ of cervix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crowning of‌ fetal head","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal desce‌nt beginning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Crowning of fetal head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Crowning of fetal head</strong></p><p>The encirclement of the largest head diameter by the vulval ring is called <strong>crowning</strong>. This occurs in the <strong>second stage </strong>of labour.</p>\n<p>First stage of labour is divided into 2 phases:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Latent phase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Active phase</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The onset of true labour pain up to 6cm cervical dilatation</td>\n<td>From ≥ 6cm cervical dilatation with regular uterine contractions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Duration:</p>\n<p><strong>10-12 hours- nullipara</strong></p>\n<p><strong>6-</strong><strong>8 hours- multipara</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Rate of cervical dilatation:</p>\n<p><strong>1.2 cm/hr- nullipara</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.5 cm/hr- multipara</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mainly cervical <strong>effacement </strong>occurs</td>\n<td>Mainly cervical<strong> dilatation </strong>occurs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7054","text":"What does fetal position r‌efer to?","raw_text":"What does fetal position refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Part of presentation which‌ lies over the internal o‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relation of different part‌s of fetus to one another‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relation of the denominato‌r to different quadrants ‍of the pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Part of fetus lying in the‌ lower segment of uterus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Relation of the denominator to different quadrants of the pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Relation of the denominator to different quadrants of the pelvis</strong></p><p><strong>Fetal position</strong> refers to the bony fixed point of reference on presenting part with respect to different quadrants of the pelvis.</p>\n<p>Definition of various terminologies used for fetus in-utero:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Term:</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Definition:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lie</strong></td>\n<td>Relationship of the long axis of fetus to the long axis of the maternal spine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td>Part of the fetus occupying the lower pole of the pelvis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Presenting part</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>Part of the presentation which overlies the internal os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Attitude</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>The relation of the different parts of the fetus to one another</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Denominator</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>An arbitrary bony fixed point on the presenting part which comes in relation with the various quadrants of the maternal pelvis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Position</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>Relation of the denominator to the different quadrants of the pelvis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Engagement</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>When the biparietal diameter has passed the plane of the pelvic brim, the head is said to be engaged.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7056","text":"A senior resident examines‌ a primigravida in labour‍ and informs that the fet​us is cephalic in attitud​e of flexion. What is the​ most common presenting p‌art of this fetus?","raw_text":"A senior resident examines a primigravida in labour and informs that the fetus is cephalic in attitude of flexion. What is the most common presenting part of this fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ver‌tex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sinc‌iput","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occ‌iput","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fa‌ce","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vertex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b11b705aa2a54571b79f145c8288ca40x1200x1907.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vertex</strong></p><p>The most common presenting part when the fetus is in attitude of flexion is <strong>vertex.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Attitude</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Presenting part</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complete flexion</td>\n<td>Vertex (occipitoanterior)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incomplete flexion</td>\n<td>Vertex (sinciput)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deflexion</td>\n<td>Vertex (occipitoposterior)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partial extension</td>\n<td>Brow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complete extension</td>\n<td>Face</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7057","text":"The ultrasonogram shows th‌e diameter of the fetal h‍ead which is taken as a r​eference for engagement. ​Which diameter does this ​refer to?","raw_text":"The ultrasonogram shows the diameter of the fetal head which is taken as a reference for engagement. Which diameter does this refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biparieta‌l diameter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bitempora‌l diameter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occipitofron‌tal diameter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suboccipitofrontal diamete‌r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biparietal diameter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b1a0cc428f3e4739afb0ef3d70f80f4d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4700f5a477a64d6e899b48550f25c87ax1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biparietal diameter</strong></p><p>The fetal head is said to be engaged when the <strong>biparietal diameter </strong>(shown in the image) passes through the pelvic brim. Engagement of fetal head is with reference to this biparietal diameter.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>greatest transverse diameter</strong> in an occiput presentation. It also reflects the gestational age in the <strong>second trimester</strong>, with a variation of 7-10 days. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">BPD is measured in the <strong>transthalamic view</strong>, at the level of the thalamus and cavum septum pellucidum (CSP). It is measured from the outer edge of the skull in the near field to the inner edge of the skull in the far field.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7100","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of a partograp‍h?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of a partograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal he‌art rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maternal ‌heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maternal t‌emperature","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal te‌mperature","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fetal temperature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fetal temperature</strong></p><p>Fetal temperature is not a part of a partograph.</p>\n<p>Assessment of progress of labor is best done by <strong>partograph. </strong>Components of partograph:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patient identification</li>\n<li><strong>Fetal heart rate </strong>recorded <strong>every 30 min</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pulse </strong>recorded <strong>every 30 min</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Blood pressure and temperature </strong>recorded <strong>every 4 h</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Uterine contractions</strong> recorded <strong>every 30 min</strong></li>\n<li>Oxytocin-dose and concentration</li>\n<li>Urine analysis</li>\n<li>Vaginal examination done every 4 hours</li>\n<li>State of membranes and colour of liquor</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7059","text":"What are the cardinal move‌ments of labour?","raw_text":"What are the cardinal movements of labour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Engagement—descent—flexion‌—internal rotation—extens‍ion—external rotation—exp​ulsion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Engagement—flexion—descent‌—internal rotation—extens‍ion—expulsion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Engagement—flexion—descent‌—external rotation—expuls‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Engagement—extension—inter‌nal rotation—external rot‍ation—expulsion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Engagement—descent—flexion—internal rotation—extension—external rotation—expulsion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/941a2cdb44d74a86b39dd4f328c4cf2fx1064x2840.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Engagement—descent—flexion—internal rotation—extension—external rotation—expulsion</strong></p><p>The <strong>cardinal movements</strong> of labour are engagement, descent, flexion, internal rotation, extension, external rotation, expulsion.</p>\n<p>(Mnemonic: Every Day FIRE Raises Extremely)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every: Engagement</li>\n<li>Day: Descent</li>\n<li>F: Flexion</li>\n<li>IR: Internal rotation</li>\n<li>E: Extension</li>\n<li>Raises: external Rotation</li>\n<li>Extremely: Expulsion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common presentation is <strong>cephalic</strong> and presenting part is <strong>vertex</strong>. Head enters the pelvis through <strong>transverse diameter</strong> majorly and left occipito transverse is the commonest fetal position. Internal rotation of fetal head occurs at the level of <strong>ischial spine</strong>. In a vertex delivery, the baby's head is delivered by extension and in a breech delivery, the baby's head is delivered by flexion. The best time to give episotomy is after crowning has occured.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7061","text":"A multigravida with gestat‌ional diabetes mellitus w‍as admitted in labour at ​term and progressed to fu​ll dilatation. The fetal ​vertex failed to descend ‌beyond -3 station. The de‌scent of the fetus is fac​ilitated by all except:","raw_text":"A multigravida with gestational diabetes mellitus was admitted in labour at term and progressed to full dilatation. The fetal vertex failed to descend beyond -3 station. The descent of the fetus is facilitated by all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extension and straightenin‌g of the fetal body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pressure of amniotic fluid‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine contractions and r‌etractions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Well-fle‌xed head","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Well-flexed head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Well-flexed head</strong></p><p>A <strong>well-flexed head</strong> facilitates <strong>internal rotation</strong>, not the descent of the presenting part.</p>\n<p>The descent of the fetus is brought about by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pressure of amniotic fluid</li>\n<li>Direct pressure of the fundus upon the breech with contractions</li>\n<li>Bearing-down efforts of maternal abdominal muscles</li>\n<li>Extension and straightening of the fetal body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7062","text":"Which of the following is ‌a prerequisite for intern‍al rotation of the fetal ​head for a woman in labor​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a prerequisite for internal rotation of the fetal head for a woman in labor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Straightening of fetal bod‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tone of transversus abdomi‌nis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Well-exte‌nded head","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tone of levator ani muscle‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tone of levator ani muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tone of levator ani muscles</strong></p><p>The <strong>tone of levator ani</strong> muscles is a prerequisite for <strong>internal rotation</strong> of the head.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 169\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Prerequisites of internal rotation of the head are as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 169\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Well-flexed head</li>\n<li>Efficient uterine contraction</li>\n<li>Favorable shape at mid-pelvic plane</li>\n<li>Tone of the levator ani muscles  </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7064","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the internal rotation of​ the fetal head?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the internal rotation of the fetal head?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occiput turns towards the ‌transverse axis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rotation is not essential ‌when the fetus is unusual‍ly small","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Internal rotation is follo‌wed by external rotation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rotation is earlier in pri‌migravida than in multigr‍avida","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rotation is not essential when the fetus is unusually small","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rotation is not essential when the fetus is unusually small</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 169\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Internal rotation is <strong>not essential</strong> in the completion of labor when the <strong>fetus is unusually small.</strong></p>\n<p>Internal rotation<strong> </strong>begins with the occiput turning <strong>away</strong> from the transverse axis. In majority, internal rotation is completed by the time the head reaches the pelvic floor. Internal rotation is followed by <strong>extension</strong> of the head, which is then followed by external rotation.</p>\n<p><strong>Rotation</strong> occurs earlier in <strong>multigravida</strong> than primigravida whereas <strong>engagement</strong> occurs earlier in <strong>primigravida</strong> than multigravida.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7082","text":"What is the minimum rate o‌f cervical dilatation in ‍primigravida during activ​e labour?","raw_text":"What is the minimum rate of cervical dilatation in primigravida during active labour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 ‌cm/hr","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.2 ‌cm/hr","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1.7 ‌cm/hr","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 c‌m/hr","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.2 cm/hr","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/502fcb0e770742ac9eac4e8ef6748cd1x1280x1257.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.2 cm/hr</strong></p><p>During active labor, the minimal rate of cervical dilatation in primigravida is <strong>1.2 cm/hr.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>First stage</strong> of labour is divided into 2 phases:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Latent phase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Active phase</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The onset of true labor pain up to 6 cm cervical dilatation</td>\n<td>Starts from ≥ 6 cm cervical dilatation with regular uterine contractions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Duration:</p>\n<p><strong>12 h (nullipara)</strong><br/><strong>8 h (multipara)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>The minimum rate of cervical dilatation:</p>\n<p><strong>1.2 cm/h (nullipara)</strong><br/><strong>1.5 cm/h (multipara)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concerned with cervical <strong>effacement</strong></td>\n<td>Concerned with cervical <strong>dilatation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The cervical dilatation ranges from a minimal rate of 1.2 cm - 6.8 cm/hr in nullipara. Also, the multiparas progress faster in the active phase of labor with a minimal dilatation rate of 1.5 cm/hr.</p>\n<p><strong>Friedman curve </strong>is the sigmoid curve plotting the pattern of cervical dilatation during the latent and active phase of normal labour.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1173","text":"Which of the following is ‌part of the WHO guideline‍s for the management of s​econd stage of labor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is part of the WHO guidelines for the management of second stage of labor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Do a routin‌e episiotomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Manual support to perineum‌ to maintain flexion of h‍ead","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Never give warm compress t‌o the perineum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform labor in lithotomy‌ position","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Manual support to perineum to maintain flexion of head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Manual support to perineum to maintain flexion of head</strong></p><p>The practice of <strong>guarding the perineum </strong>during delivery of the fetal head is part of the <strong>WHO management guidelines</strong> for the second stage of labor.</p>\n<p>The fingers of one hand support the perineum, while the second hand applies pressure to the fetal head to control the speed of crowning. The advantage of this is to prevent perineal tears.</p>\n<p>The second stage of labor begins with<strong> full dilatation of the cervix</strong> and ends with the <strong>delivery of the fetus.</strong></p>\n<p>The main events include the descent of the fetus (bearing down by the mother, uterine contractions), cardinal movements of labor, and delivery of the fetus.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 797px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 268.891px;\">Second stage </td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.273px;\">Nulliparous </td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.445px;\">Multiparous </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 268.891px;\">Mean/average duration </td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.273px;\">50 minutes </td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.445px;\">30 minutes </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 268.891px;\">Maximum duration </td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.273px;\">2 hours (+1 with epidural)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.445px;\">1 hour (+1 with epidural)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 268.891px;\">WHO maximum duration of 2nd stage/diagnosis of arrest </td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.273px;\">3 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.445px;\">2 hours </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Management of the second stage of labor:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common position used is the dorsal position with the hips flexed, knees flexed and abduction of the thighs. WHO recommends any position of choice including the <strong>upright position</strong>. </li>\n<li>WHO recommendations for the prevention of perineal tears include <strong>warm compresses to the perineum</strong>, <strong>perineal massage</strong>, <strong>guarding the perineum,</strong> and <strong>modified Ritgen's</strong> maneuver.</li>\n<li>Modified Ritgen's maneuver: A sterile towel or pad is held in one hand and placed over the perineum; the other hand applies gentle downward pressure on the occiput to promote flexion and controlled delivery of the head (ensuring the smallest diameter of the head passes through the outlet). Once the occiput is born–with gentle pressure, the head is extended–forehead, nose, and mouth are then delivered.</li>\n<li>WHO doesn't recommend fundal pressure and routine episiotomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: A liberal or <strong>routine episiotomy</strong> is <strong>discouraged</strong>. Episiotomy is indicated in cases of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shoulder dystocia</li>\n<li>Breech delivery</li>\n<li>Fetal macrosomia</li>\n<li>Operative vaginal deliveries</li>\n<li>Persistent occiput posterior positions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: Manual techniques, such as massage or application of <strong>warm compresses</strong>, are <strong>recommended</strong> for healthy pregnant women requesting pain relief during labor.</p>\n<p>Option D: The position that is most comfortable to the mother i.e. <strong>upright/squatting position</strong> is encouraged. This position gives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>less discomfort and difficulty in bearing down</li>\n<li>less labor pain</li>\n<li>less perineal/vaginal trauma and wound infections </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6624","text":"Modified Robson's classifi‌cation class 1 includes:","raw_text":"Modified Robson's classification class 1 includes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nulliparous, singleton, ce‌phalic, induced labour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nulliparous, singleton, ce‌phalic, spontaneous labou‍r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multiparous without previo‌us CS, spontaneous labour‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiparous without previo‌us CS, induced/CS before ‍labour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nulliparous, singleton, cephalic, spontaneous labour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nulliparous, singleton, cephalic, spontaneous labour</strong></p><p><strong>Class 1</strong> of modified Robson's classification includes <strong>nullipara</strong>, equal to or <strong>&gt;37</strong> weeks, <strong>single</strong>, <strong>cephalic,</strong> <strong>spontaneous labor</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the most widely accepted classification system available for analyzing characteristics of delivering women worldwide. It consists of <strong>10 patient population</strong> categories. The basis for these categories include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parity</li>\n<li>Onset of labor and mode of delivery</li>\n<li>Gestational age</li>\n<li>Fetal presentation </li>\n<li>No. of fetuses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>modified Robson system</strong> classification is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 236px; width: 517px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 53px;\"><strong>Class 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 53px;\">Nullipara, equal to or &gt;37 weeks, single, cephalic, spontaneous labor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Class 2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Nullipara, equal to or &gt;37 weeks, single, cephalic</p>\n<p> <strong>2a:</strong> induced labor  <strong>2b:</strong> CS before labor</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 56px;\"><strong>Class 3</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 56px;\">Multipara, equal to or &gt;37 weeks, single, cephalic, spontaneous labor (excludes previous CS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Class 4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 54px;\">Multipara, equal to or &gt;37 weeks, single, cephalic   <strong>4a:</strong> induced labor <strong>4b:</strong> CS before labor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 49px;\"><strong>Class 5</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 49px;\">Multipara, previous CS, equal to or &gt;37 weeks, single, cephalic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 49px;\"><strong>Class 6</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 49px;\">Nullipara, single, breech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Class 7</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 50px;\">Multipara, single, breech (including previous CS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 48px;\"><strong>Class 8</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 48px;\">Multiple pregnancies (with or without previous CS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55.5469px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 55.5469px;\"><strong>Class 9</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 55.5469px;\">Singleton pregnancy, oblique/transverse lie (with or without previous CS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114.5px; height: 52px;\"><strong>Class 10</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 385.5px; height: 52px;\"> Single, cephalic &lt;37 weeks (including previous CS)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6621","text":"Identify the fetal positio‌n in the given image.","raw_text":"Identify the fetal position in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right occipi‌to-posterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left occipi‌to-anterior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left occipi‌to-posterior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right occip‌ito-anterior","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right occipito-anterior","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/83f5f071364a43349a67fb91e84b3fa1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8feae85a259d42049a433d013700e433x1280x1372.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right occipito-anterior</strong></p><p>In the given vertex presentation, the fetal position is <strong>right occipitoanterior.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fetal position</strong> refers to the relation of the <strong>denominator </strong>(arbitrary <strong>bony</strong> fixed point on the presenting part), with respect to different <strong>quadrants</strong> of the maternal pelvis. In vertex presentation, the denominator is taken as the occiput (which is close to the triangular posterior fontanelle).</p>\n<p>To determine the fetal position the following two steps are followed:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Determine the <strong>direction</strong> in which the occiput is facing:\n<ul>\n<li>Towards the pubic symphysis - <strong>occipito-anterior</strong></li>\n<li>Towards the sacral promontory - <strong>occipito-posterior</strong></li>\n<li>Midway between pubic symphysis and sacral promontory - <strong>occipito-transverse </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Determine if the occiput is facing the <strong>left</strong> or the <strong>right</strong> in relation to the materal pelvis</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7108","text":"Uterine contractions are p‌alpable when its intensit‍y is more than _____.","raw_text":"Uterine contractions are palpable when its intensity is more than _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 m‌m Hg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"15 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"40 ‌mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10 mm Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10 mm Hg</strong></p><p>Uterine contractions are <strong>palpable</strong> when the intensity is <strong>&gt;10 mmHg. </strong></p>\n<p>Uterine contractions cause cervical dilatation at 15 mmHg and are painful beyond 15 mmHg.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stage of labour     </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pressure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1st</td>\n<td>40–50 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd</td>\n<td>100–120 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd</td>\n<td>100–120 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Units for measuring the intensity of uterine contractions are mmHg and Montevideo units (MV).</p>\n<p>1 MV = intensity of uterine contractions x number of contractions in 10 min.</p>\n<p>During labour, normal uterine contractions are between <strong>190 and 300 MV</strong> and at the time of delivery, it is <strong>300 MV</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7112","text":"A primigravida presents to‌ the labor room at 39 wee‍ks of gestation. On exami​nation, you notice the fe​tal head has appeared at ​the vulva. Which of the f‌ollowing maneuvers can be‌ used to assist the deliv​ery of the head?","raw_text":"A primigravida presents to the labor room at 39 weeks of gestation. On examination, you notice the fetal head has appeared at the vulva. Which of the following maneuvers can be used to assist the delivery of the head?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prague ‌maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lovset's‌ maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pinard's‌ maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ritgen's‌ maneuver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ritgen's maneuver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3c8be3d46524a1b816883680f594c09x600x654.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ritgen&#x27;s maneuver</strong></p><p><strong>Ritgen's maneuver</strong> is done for delivery of head in normal labor.</p>\n<p>This maneuver is done when the head distends the vulva and perineum enough to open the vaginal introitus to a diameter of &gt;5 cm. A towel is draped and a gloved hand may be used to exert forward pressure on the chin of the fetus through the perineum. The other hand exerts pressure superiorly against the occiput.</p>\n<p>This maneuver allows <strong>controlled delivery of the head</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7119","text":"Which dermatome is pain in‌ the early stage of labor‍ limited to?","raw_text":"Which dermatome is pain in the early stage of labor limited to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T10‌-L1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"S1‌-S3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L4‌-L5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L2‌-L3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T10-L1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T10-L1</strong></p><p>Pain in the <strong>early stage of labor</strong> is limited to dermatomes <strong>T10-L1</strong>. This is of <strong>uterine</strong> origin.</p>\n<p>Pain in the <strong>late stage of labour</strong> is due to <strong>dilatation of cervix</strong> and perineal stretching. The nerves carrying the pain include <strong>S2,3,4. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7120","text":"A young primigravida at 39‌ weeks of gestation prese‍nted to the casualty cryi​ng of intense abdominal p​ain. She was diagnosed to​ be in labour. Which of t‌he following is not a cha‌racteristic of this pain?​","raw_text":"A young primigravida at 39 weeks of gestation presented to the casualty crying of intense abdominal pain. She was diagnosed to be in labour. Which of the following is not a characteristic of this pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painful uterine contractio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relieved by enema and seda‌tives","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Formation of bag of waters‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive descent of pre‌senting part","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Relieved by enema and sedatives","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Relieved by enema and sedatives</strong></p><p>True labor pain is not relieved by enema and sedatives.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Painful uterine contractions</strong> at regular intervals</li>\n<li>The frequency of contractions increases gradually</li>\n<li>The intensity and duration of contractions increase progressively</li>\n<li>Associated with 'show'- extrusion of the mucus plug that had previously filled the cervical canal during pregnancy</li>\n<li>Progressive effacement and dilatation of the cervix</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive descent </strong>of the <strong>presenting part</strong></li>\n<li>Formation of the <strong>'bag of forewaters'</strong> i.e.,<strong> </strong>bulging fluid-filled amniotic membrane presenting in front of the fetal head</li>\n<li>Not relieved by enema or sedatives</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 595px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.15625px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.84375px;\"><strong>True labour pains</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\"><strong>False labour pains</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.15625px;\">Cervical dilatation and effacement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.84375px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.15625px;\">Pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.84375px;\">Lower abdomen and back, radiating to thighs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Lower abdomen only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.15625px;\">Show</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.84375px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.15625px;\">Bag of water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.84375px;\">Formed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.15625px;\">Relief with enema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.84375px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>False labour pain</strong> is seen more in primigravidae than in parous women. It usually appears prior to the onset of true labor pain by 1 or 2 weeks in primigravidae and by a few days in multiparae.</p>\n<p>Such pains are probably due to stretching of the cervix and lower uterine segment with consequent irritation of the neighboring ganglia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7122","text":"You notice the gynecology ‌resident administer the n‍erve block seen in the im​age before performing an ​episiotomy. Which nerve r​oots are blocked?","raw_text":"You notice the gynecology resident administer the nerve block seen in the image before performing an episiotomy. Which nerve roots are blocked?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L1,‌2,3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L2‌,3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S2,‌3,4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"L5,S‌1,S2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. S2,3,4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a5944f1e6d8f487c84407b6b354747c5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. S2,3,4</strong></p><p>The nerve roots blocked in <strong>pudendal nerve block</strong> are <strong>S2,3,4. </strong>It is blocked just above the tip of the <strong>ischial spine</strong> by piercing the <strong>sacrospinous ligament.</strong> </p>\n<p>Pudendal nerve block can be used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Analgesia in the second stage of labor</li>\n<li>Episiotomy repair</li>\n<li>Pelvic floor relaxation for outlet instrument delivery</li>\n<li>Minor procedures of the lower vagina and perineum</li>\n<li>Perineal analgesia prior to application of vacuum/forceps</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pudendal block does not impact the cervix or upper vagina. It is ineffective for pain relief associated with uterine manipulation such as manual extraction of the placenta. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7124","text":"Paracervical block relieve‌s pain from all except:","raw_text":"Paracervical block relieves pain from all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cer‌vix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ute‌rus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower thir‌d of vagina","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Upper thir‌d of vagina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lower third of vagina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lower third of vagina</strong></p><p>Pain from the <strong>lower third of the vagina</strong> is relieved by <strong>pudendal block</strong>, not paracervical block.</p>\n<p>Paracervical block relieves the pain of <strong>uterine contractions </strong>and <strong>cervical dilatation.</strong> It also relieves pain from <strong>upper third of the vagina. </strong>It is not useful at the time of episiotomy as the lower part of the vagina is not anesthetized.</p>\n<p>Note: Paracervical block is no longer considered safe as it causes <strong>fetal bradycardia</strong>. <strong>Epidural analgesia</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> when complete relief of pain is needed throughout labour.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0035","text":"All of the following are i‌ndications for early clam‍ping of cord in a new bor​n baby except:","raw_text":"All of the following are indications for early clamping of cord in a new born baby except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rh isoimm‌unization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postdated‌ pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Birth a‌sphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal‌ diabetes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Postdated pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Postdated pregnancy</strong></p><p>Postdated pregnancy is not an indication for early cord clamping.</p>\n<p><strong>Early cord clamping</strong> is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Birth asphyxia</li>\n<li>Rh isoimmunization</li>\n<li>Diabetic mother</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The benefits of <strong>delayed cord clamping</strong> for up to 60 seconds is an increase in total body iron stores, expand blood volume and a <strong>decrease in anemia</strong> incidence in the <strong>neonate.</strong></p>\n<p>On the other hand, delayed cord clamping can lead to a higher hemoglobin concentration, which increases risks for <strong>hyperbilirubinemia</strong> and extended hospitalization for neonatal phototherapy. Delayed cord clamping can also hinder timely neonatal resuscitation.</p>\n<p>According to <strong>AIIMS</strong> Protocols in Neonatology, in <strong>uncomplicated</strong> preterm birth, <strong>delayed cord clamping</strong> should be attempted. This is done for a minimum time of 60 seconds after delivery for preterm infants not requiring immediate resuscitation after birth. It states that DCC must be done even if the mother has an <strong>HIV</strong> infection. But as per the <strong>NACO</strong> guidelines, <strong>early cord clamping </strong>is<strong> advised</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0036","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a sign of placental s‍eparation in stage 3 of p​arturition of a pregnant ​woman?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a sign of placental separation in stage 3 of parturition of a pregnant woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schroede‌r's sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shortenin‌g of cord","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suprapub‌ic bulge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kustner‌'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shortening of cord","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shortening of cord</strong></p><p><strong>Lengthening </strong>of the<strong> umbilical cord</strong> is seen in placental separation.</p>\n<p><strong>Signs of placental separation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The uterus becomes globular, firm, and ballottable (earliest sign to appear)</li>\n<li><strong>Schroeder's sign -</strong> Slight rise of fundal height as the separated placenta comes down in the lower segment and uterus rests over it</li>\n<li>Slight <strong>suprapubic bulging</strong> due to separated placenta distending the lower segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Kustner's sign - </strong>On pushing, the uterus cephalad with a hand on the abdomen, the cord no longer recedes</li>\n<li>A sudden gush of blood</li>\n<li>Permanent lengthening of cord</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Schroeder's ring</strong> is a constriction ring/contraction ring formed due to incoordinate uterine action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9950","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the modif‍ied WHO partogram?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the modified WHO partogram?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right side of alert line i‌ndicates immediate referr‍al to First Referral Unit​ (FRU)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alert and action line are ‌separated by a difference‍ of 4 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Each small square in parto‌gram is equal to 1 hour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Partograph recording shoul‌d be started once the cer‍vical dilatation reaches ​4 cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Each small square in partogram is equal to 1 hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Each small square in partogram is equal to 1 hour</strong></p><p>Each <strong>small square</strong> in a <strong>modified WHO partogram</strong> is equal to <strong>30 minutes. </strong>Each small square in a <strong>composite </strong>WHO partogram is equal to <strong>1 hour.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9134","text":"Identify the following ins‌trument commonly used in ‍obstetrics.","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument commonly used in obstetrics.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mayo s‌cissors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Curved ‌scissors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suture ‌remover","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Episiotom‌y scissors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Episiotomy scissors","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71bad115c69b45bb9ed60112a70bf0a7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/015134ab07b14d9eb884ed9294cdbe51x1200x916.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abe30855adf844ca8d7ff8e9ee16f4e1x1280x753.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Episiotomy scissors</strong></p><p>The instrument given in the picture is<strong> episiotomy scissors. </strong>The angulation in the scissor is to prevent the extension of pelvic tears into the anal margins and obstetric anal sphincter injuries / complete perineal tears.</p>\n<p>Complications of episiotomy: </p>\n<p>They can be immediate or remote. </p>\n<p>Immediate complications include an extension of the incision into the rectum, vulval hematoma, wound dehiscence and injury to anal sphincter. </p>\n<p>Remote complications include dyspareunia, chances of perineal lacerations in subsequent labor and scar endometriosis (rare).</p>\n<p>Mayo scissors are long and stout scissors. They can be blunt-tipped/pointed tip/straight or curved. Straight mayo scissors are designed for cutting body tissues near the surface of a wound. They are also used to cut sutures and are also known as suture-cutting scissors.</p>\n<p>Curved mayo scissors allow much deeper penetration into the wound and are used to cut thick tissues such as those found in the uterus, muscles, breast, and foot. Mayo scissors used for dissection are placed in tissue with the tips closed. The scissors are then opened so that the tips open and spread out the tissue during the dissection process.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6619","text":"A nursing assistant posted‌ in the obstetric care un‍it was asked to monitor t​he progress of labour. Af​ter how much dilatation o​f the cervix, should she ‌start the documentation?","raw_text":"A nursing assistant posted in the obstetric care unit was asked to monitor the progress of labour. After how much dilatation of the cervix, should she start the documentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‌cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6‌cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/719c60870fc947ccaf5225fb3f2ffd2bx724x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5cm</strong></p><p>The WHO Labour Care Guide <strong>begins</strong> the <strong>documentation</strong> of labour progression at <strong>5 cm </strong>of <strong>cervical dilatation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The WHO Labour Care Guide is a tool designed to help health personnel involved in labour care. It aims to </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Guide the <strong>monitoring</strong> and help in the <strong>documentation</strong> of the progress of labour.</li>\n<li>Guide the health professionals to offer <strong>supportive</strong> <strong>care</strong> throughout labour.</li>\n<li>Assist doctors in promptly <strong>identifying</strong> and <strong>addressing</strong> emerging <strong>labour</strong> <strong>complications</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevent</strong> the use of <strong>unnecessary</strong> <strong>interventions</strong> in labour.</li>\n<li>Support <strong>audits</strong> and <strong>quality</strong> <strong>improvement</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The differences between the WHO-modified partograph and the <strong>latest WHO Labour Care Guide </strong>are tabulated below: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 260px;\" width=\"669\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 39px; text-align: center;\"><strong>WHO modified partograph</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 39px; text-align: center;\"><strong>WHO Labour Care Guide</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 86px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 86px;\">Documentation of labour progression begins at <strong>4 cm</strong> of cervical dilatation.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 86px;\">Documentation of labour progression begins at <strong>5 cm</strong> of cervical dilatation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 95px;\">Fixed <strong>1 cm/hour ‘alert’ and ‘action’</strong> lines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 95px;\"><strong>Time-based cut-offs</strong> for <strong>each centimetre</strong> of cervical dilatation to guide the obstetrician to take action. For example, at 5 cm of cervical dilatation, if there is no progress &gt;6 hours, action needs to be taken.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 87px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 87px;\">Monitoring of labour is restricted to the <strong>first stage</strong> only.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 87px;\">Monitoring of <strong>the second stage of labour</strong> is included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">No section on supportive care interventions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">Addition of a section on <strong>supportive care</strong> of the mother- labour companionship, pain relief, oral fluid intake and posture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">Documentation of <strong>strength, duration, and frequency</strong> of uterine contractions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">Documentation of <strong>only</strong> the <strong>duration and frequency of uterine contractions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Previously the answer to the question was 4 cm (according to the modified WHO patograph). However, this information is outdated, therefore the answer to this question has been revised per the latest WHO guidelines. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RdvxU0qD","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"10_macular_disorders__retinal_dystrophies__and_vitreal_disorders","title":"10.Macular Disorders, Retinal Dystrophies, And Vitreal Disorders","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MA0367","text":"A 25-year-old artist visit‌s an art gallery. A paint​ing of checkered squares ​appears distorted only to‌ him, as shown below. He ​decides to go see an opht‍halmologist who after ini​tial evaluation, suggests‍ an FFA. The FFA shows a ​characteristic smoke stac‍k appearance. Breakdown o​f which of the following ​barriers is responsible f​or his condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old artist visits an art gallery. A painting of checkered squares appears distorted only to him, as shown below. He decides to go see an ophthalmologist who after initial evaluation, suggests an FFA. The FFA  shows a characteristic smoke stack appearance. Breakdown of which of the following barriers is responsible for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner blood retinal barrie‌r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Outer blood retinal barrie‌r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both the barriers are disr‌upted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No disruption of either ba‌rriers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Outer blood retinal barrier","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0af66e2c2eb41c6a24a470006c9a634.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a259c53a4e34b25af205227d06de1c0x598x491.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8651c9ddc08a4d4d99cd717c5230ef97x1280x1511.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Outer blood retinal barrier</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a characteristic <strong>smoke stack pattern on FFA</strong> is suggestive of a diagnosis of<strong> central serous retinopathy</strong>. In CSR, the <strong>outer blood-retinal barrier</strong> is <strong>disrupted.</strong></p>\n<p>CSR is the serous detachment of the neurosensory retina with focal and multifocal areas of leakage at the level of the<strong> retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), </strong>mainly affecting the macular area. That is why the patient who is usually a young male presents with macular symptoms such as blurring of central vision, metamorphopsia.</p>\n<p>The blood-retina barrier (BRB) is composed of inner and outer barriers. The <strong>outer BRB</strong> refers to the barrier formed at the <strong>retinal pigment epithelial (RPE) cell layer</strong> and regulates the movement of solutes and nutrients from the choroid to the sub-retinal space. By contrast, the <strong>inner BRB, similar</strong> to the <strong>blood-brain barrier</strong> (BBB) is located in the inner retinal microvasculature and comprises the microvascular endothelium, which lines these vessels.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the smoke-stack pattern seen in CSR.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Amsler grid</strong> is used in <strong>detecting maculopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assess macular function</li>\n<li>Evaluate the visual field of 10°</li>\n<li>Good for follow-up in CSR</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Amsler's grid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0368","text":"Where does fluid accumulat‌e in central serous retin​opathy?","raw_text":"Where does fluid accumulate in central serous retinopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between the Bruch membrane‌ and retinal pigment epit​helium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In the subr‌etinal space","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Between the outer plexifor‌m layer and inner nuclear​ layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Along the nerve fiber laye‌r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. In the subretinal space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9d0678eb1fb046efb61925ae4c1be2a3x300x188.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. In the subretinal space</strong></p><p>Fluid accumulation in central serous retinopathy (CSR) is in the <strong>subretinal space</strong> due to abnormal permeability of the choriocapillaris.</p>\n<p>The image depicts optical coherence tomography showing fluid accumulation in the <strong>subretinal space,</strong> with shallow retinal detachment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2343","text":"A 60-year-old female prese‌nted with acute loss of v​ision and metamorphopsia.​ There is a history of th‌e positive scotoma. Funda​l changes are given below‍. What will be the fundus​ fluorescein angiography ‍finding?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old female presented with acute loss of vision and metamorphopsia. There is a history of the positive scotoma. Fundal changes are given below. What will be the fundus fluorescein angiography finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ink blot ‌appearance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Petaloid ‌appearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stars in the sky appearanc‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dual cir‌culation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ink blot appearance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46446120cbcd4b1eaa97131b56b5e5a2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf71fca6a66a4916be96b5d82c78e4c9x720x564.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ink blot appearance</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and the fundal image depicting <strong>focal fundal detachment</strong> are suggestive of <strong>central serous retinopathy. </strong>An enlarging <strong>inkblot pattern</strong> or smoke stack pattern is seen in FFA.</p>\n<p>Central serous retinopathy refers to the <strong>spontaneous serous detachment</strong> of the <strong>neurosensory retina</strong> in the <strong>macular</strong> region. Recent studies implicate an abnormal choroidal circulation as the cause of CSR. </p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retinal detachment - <strong>Sudden painless</strong> loss of vision</li>\n<li>Relative positive scotoma</li>\n<li>Micropsia</li>\n<li>Metamorphopsia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ophthalmoscopy - circular ring reflex present and <strong>foveal</strong> reflex <strong>absent</strong></li>\n<li>FFA - enlarging inkblot pattern or <strong>smoke stack</strong> pattern</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Spontaneous resolution</strong> is seen in most cases and hence <strong>observation</strong> is enough. <strong>Steroids </strong>are <strong>contraindicated</strong>. The disease is usually self-limiting but may recur.</p>\n<p>The image depicts fundus fluorescein angiography (FFA), showing an enlarging <strong>ink-blot pattern/enlarging dot sign</strong>, which is seen in <strong>central serous retinopathy (CSR)</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B- <strong>Petaloid</strong> appearance is seen in <strong>cystoid macular edema</strong></p>\n<p>Option C-<strong> Stars in the sky</strong> appearance is suggestive of <strong>drusen</strong></p>\n<p>Option D- Dual circulation is a pathognomonic feature of choroidal melanoma</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0371","text":"A 69-year-old man who is a‌ chronic smoker, hyperten​sive, and has a BMI of 35​ kg/m2 complains of dimin‌ished vision in the right​ eye. His visual acuity i‍s 6/36 BE and the anterio​r segment appears normal.‍ His fundus is as shown b​elow. What is the most li‍kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 69-year-old man who is a chronic smoker, hypertensive, and has a BMI of 35 kg/m2 complains of diminished vision in the right eye. His visual acuity is 6/36 BE and the anterior segment appears normal. His fundus is as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic r‌etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Age related dry macular de‌generation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertensive‌ retinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age related wet macular de‌generation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Age related dry macular degeneration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c8350cdef454633ba4fd9c025f111ac.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Age related dry macular degeneration</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario along with the image which shows <strong>drusen </strong>(druplets) are a feature of <strong>dry</strong> or <strong>non-exudative age-related macular degeneration (ARMD</strong>). </p>\n<p>Drusen are the <strong>earliest visible clinical signs</strong> of ARMD. They are composed of lipid, amyloid, complement factors, and associated cellular components.</p>\n<p>There are two types of ARMD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dry </strong>(non-exudative, non-neovascular)<strong> ARMD</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> form, comprising around <strong>90%</strong> of the diagnosed cases. Initially characterized by macular <strong>Drusens,</strong> which later progress to <strong>geographical atrophy. </strong>Geographic atrophy (GA) is the <strong>advanced stage</strong> of dry ARMD. Antioxidants like the AREDS2 supplements have proven effective.</li>\n<li><strong>Wet </strong>(exudative, neovascular)<strong> ARMD</strong> is much <strong>less common</strong> than the dry type, but it is associated with <strong>more rapid progression</strong> to advanced sight loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical classification:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 408px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 142px; height: 114px;\"><strong>Category</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 252px; height: 114px;\">\n<p><strong>Definition</strong></p>\n<p>(based on the presence of lesions<strong> within two disc diameters</strong> of the fovea in either eye)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 47px;\"><strong>No apparent</strong><br/><strong>age-related</strong> changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252px; height: 47px;\"><strong>No drusen</strong><br/>No AMD pigmentary abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Normal age-related</strong><br/><strong>changes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 252px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Only drusen</strong><br/>No AMD pigmentary abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 77px;\"><strong>Early</strong> ARMD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252px; height: 77px;\"><strong>Medium Drusen</strong> (&gt;63 μm but &lt;125 μm)<br/>No AMD pigmentary abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 72px;\"><strong>Intermediate</strong> ARMD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252px; height: 72px;\"><strong>Large Drusen</strong> (&gt;125 μm)<br/>Any ARMD pigmentary<br/>abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Late</strong> ARMD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Neovascular ARMD</strong> and/or<br/>any <strong>geographic atrophy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1502","text":"Indocyanine green angiogra‌phy (ICGA) is primarily i​ndicated in which of the ​following conditions? CNV‌M - choroidal neovascular​ membrane","raw_text":"<p>Indocyanine green angiography (ICGA) is primarily indicated in which of the following conditions?</p>\n<p>CNVM - choroidal neovascular membrane</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimal cl‌assic CNVM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occul‌t CNVM","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clinically significant mac‌ular edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coats ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Occult CNVM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Occult CNVM</strong></p><p>Indocyanine green angiography (ICGA) is primarily indicated in an <strong>occult</strong> choroidal neovascular membrane <strong>(CNVM).</strong></p>\n<p>ICGA is used to <strong>visualize</strong> the <strong>choroidal vasculature.</strong> </p>\n<p>Its main use is to <strong>differentiate</strong> between <strong>classic</strong> and <strong>occult CNVM</strong> when the clinical picture is not clear. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0372","text":"Intravitreal injection of ‌anti-VEGF is used in the ​treatment of which of the​ following conditions? AR‌MD - age-related macular ​degeneration","raw_text":"<p>Intravitreal injection of anti-VEGF is used in the treatment of which of the following conditions?</p>\n<p>ARMD - age-related macular degeneration</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitreous h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wet ‌ARMD","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dry ‌ARMD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wet ARMD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wet ARMD</strong></p><p><strong>Intravitreal</strong> injection of <strong>anti-vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)</strong> is used as the treatment of <strong>wet age-related macular degeneration (ARMD)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Wet ARMD is characterized by <strong>retinal pigment epithelial detachment</strong> and choroidal/subretinal <strong>neovascularization.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of ARMD depends on the location of the choroidal neovascularization membrane:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For extrafoveal membrane - Laser photocoagulation</li>\n<li>For subfoveal and juxta foveal membranes, treatment options are as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>Intravitreal injection of anti -VEGF agents</li>\n<li>Photodynamic therapy - the dye used is verteporfin followed by exposure to light from a diode</li>\n<li>Transpupillary thermotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0379","text":"An old cab driver whose li‌velihood depends on his d​riving is distressed. He ​complains that everything‌ appears distorted and he​ perceives objects to sma‍ller than their usual siz​e. His anterior segment a‍ppears normal. Examinatio​n of the fundus reveals a‍ honeycomb appearance of ​the macula. FFA is shown ​below. What is the most l​ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An old cab driver whose livelihood depends on his driving is distressed. He complains that everything appears distorted and he perceives objects to smaller than their usual size. His anterior segment appears normal. Examination of the fundus reveals a honeycomb appearance of the macula. FFA is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age related macular degene‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macular h‌emorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystoid ma‌cular edema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Central Serous retinopathy‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cystoid macular edema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e545d67555cb41cd8d276cfc3bb17551.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cystoid macular edema</strong></p><p>The image is that of fluorescein angiography showing the \"<strong>flower petal pattern,\"</strong> which is seen in <strong>cystoid macular edema (CME).</strong> The characteristic flower-petal appearance is due to the accumulation of dye in the <strong>outer plexiform layer</strong> and <strong>inner nuclear layer</strong> at the <strong>macula</strong></p>\n<p>The accumulation of fluid in the outer plexiform layer and the inner nuclear layer leads to<strong> retinal thickening</strong> at <strong>macula</strong> and <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>foveal depression</strong>, formation of cystic spaces <strong>(honey-comb macula). </strong>Risk factors include retinitis pigmentosa, uveitis, niacin, PGE2 analogues, epinephrine in aphakia, diabetic retinopathy, retinal venous occlusion and Irvin-Gass syndrome (CME post-cataract surgery).</p>\n<p>Symptoms may include <strong>blurring of vision, distortion,</strong> and <strong>micropsia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therapeutic approaches for treating CME depend on the underlying etiology. This includes <strong>steroids,</strong> carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, anti-VEGF agents, PPV for associated vitreomacular traction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9581","text":"A patient presents to the ‌emergency department and ​is found to have a cornea​l abrasion, corneal ulcer‌, corneal perforation, an​d foreign body. Which of ‍the following will not ca​use hypotonic maculopathy‍ in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the emergency department and is found to have a corneal abrasion, corneal ulcer, corneal perforation, and foreign body. Which of the following will not cause hypotonic maculopathy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suprachoroidal haemorrhage‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclodialy‌sis of uvea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corneal p‌erforation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filtration‌ site leak","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Suprachoroidal haemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Suprachoroidal haemorrhage</strong></p><p><strong>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</strong> will not cause hypotonic maculopathy in this patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Maculopathy</strong> is common in eyes developing hypotony, which is defined as <strong>IOP &lt;6 mmHg</strong>. The most common cause is <strong>excessive drainage</strong> following glaucoma filtration surgery (Option D). Other causes include trauma such as <strong>cyclodialysis</strong> of the uvea (Option B), <strong>penetrating injury </strong>(Option C), chronic uveitis, retinal detachment, and other systemic causes such as dehydration, uncontrolled diabetes, uremia, and treatment with hyperosmotic agents. They usually cause bilateral hypotonic maculopathy.</p>\n<p><strong>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</strong> is a bleed into the <strong>suprachoroidal space</strong> that results from the rupture of the <strong>posterior ciliary artery</strong>. When the bleed is severe it causes extrusion of intraocular contents that is termed as <strong>expulsive hemorrhage.</strong> It is a rare but dreaded complication of the phacoemulsification procedure. Signs seen in suprachoroidal hemorrhage are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Progressive <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>intraocular pressure</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Prolapse</strong> of iris</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loss or partial obscuration of the red reflex</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In severe cases there can be extrusion of the posterior segment contents into the anterior chamber and through the incision such as vitreous extrusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Immediate treatment</strong> involves rapid closure of the incision followed by administration of IOP-lowering medications such as oral <strong>acetazolamide</strong> and topical or systemic <strong>steroids</strong> to reduce inflammation. Standard post-operative <strong>antibiotics</strong> can be added in these patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6732","text":"A 45-year old patient on s‌ystemic treatment for rhe​umatoid arthritis develop​s bilateral painless decr‌ease in vision. What does​ the image show?","raw_text":"A 45-year old patient on systemic treatment for rheumatoid arthritis develops bilateral painless decrease in vision. What does the image show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluorescein angiography sh‌owing bull’s eye maculopa​thy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autofluorescence showing c‌hanges of bull’s eye macu​lopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"OCT showing bull’s eye mac‌ulopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluorescein angiography sh‌owing crystalline retinop​athy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autofluorescence showing changes of bull’s eye maculopathy","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/b1f442021a6f402cbcdb6c1c1ff22597.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1281812f88ef43cd9d2fc7e613e68b42x359x252.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83c74d4a90f649d0986f1194e341efb8x1280x487.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autofluorescence showing changes of bull’s eye maculopathy</strong></p><p>The investigation shown in the image is an <strong>autofluorescence showing bull’s eye maculopathy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong>, which is generally used for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis, toxicity causes bull’s eye maculopathy. Chloroquine binds to melanin in the retinal pigment epithelium, causing cytotoxic effects. RPE atrophy occurs in the macula, resulting in dark areas on the autofluorescence image.</p>\n<p>Other causes of Bull’s eye maculopathy include <strong>chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine,</strong> congenital cone dystrophy, juvenile <strong>X-linked retinoschisis,</strong> and juvenile <strong>Batten's</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>Fundus autofluorescence is a <strong>non-invasive imaging modality.</strong> It reflects the morphological changes associated with the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>lipofuscin</strong> and does not require the injection of any fluorescent dye.</p>\n<p>A fundus image of bull’s eye maculopathy is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows the difference between fundus autofluorescence and fluorescence angiography</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other ocular effects of chloroquine include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chloroquine <strong>keratopathy—</strong><strong>vortex keratopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Ocular palsy, ptosis</li>\n<li>Electroretinographic depression</li>\n<li>Optic neuritis</li>\n<li>Posterior subcapsular cataract</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0389","text":"A study used electroretino‌graphy(ERG) in military c​onscripts whose visual ab​normalities could not be ‌explained by the findings​ of ophthalmologic, neuro‍logical, and psychiatric ​examinations. The ERG of ‍pure malingerers was foun​d to be, as shown below. ‍The activity of which cel​ls of the retina is depic​ted by the a-wave?","raw_text":"A study used electroretinography(ERG) in military conscripts whose visual abnormalities could not be explained by the findings of ophthalmologic, neurological, and psychiatric examinations. The ERG of pure malingerers was found to be, as shown below. The activity of which cells of the retina is depicted by the a-wave?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rods an‌d cones","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muller‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pigment e‌pithelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rods and cones","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/87a9801d97e74bd7a11f1397fe743a7c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rods and cones</strong></p><p><strong>\"a\" wave</strong> in an electroretinogram (ERG) represents <strong>rods</strong> and <strong>cones.</strong></p>\n<p>Electroretinography is used to evaluate retinal functions by examining the electrical response to a light stimulus.</p>\n<p>Electroretinograms (ERGs) show the amplitudes of currents that flow within the eye following exposure to a point of light. The amplitudes are analyzed into waves, which are composed of the responses of neuronal, glial, and retinal pigment epithelial cells.</p>\n<p>ERG:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> a-wave originates from photoreceptors (rods and cones)</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>b-wave</strong> originates from <strong>Muller and bipolar</strong> cells, but is directly dependant on photoreceptors</li>\n<li>The <strong>c-wave</strong> originates from <strong>retinal pigment epithelial</strong> cells and retinal glial cells</li>\n<li><strong>Oscillatory potentials</strong> (OPs) <strong>originate</strong> in the <strong>inner retina</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scotopic ERGs</strong> are used to evaluate responses starting from rod photoreceptors exposed to flash-light in darkness, and<strong> photopic ERGs</strong> are used to evaluate responses starting from cone photoreceptors exposed to flash-light under constant light exposure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1369","text":"Which area of the retina h‌as the highest concentrat​ion of rods?","raw_text":"Which area of the retina has the highest concentration of rods?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mid-periphe‌ry of retina","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Optic‌ disc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fo‌vea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ora s‌errata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mid-periphery of retina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mid-periphery of retina</strong></p><p><strong>Mid-periphery </strong>of the retina has the <strong>highest concentration</strong> of <strong>rods.</strong></p>\n<p>The following table lists the differences between rods and cones</p>\n<table style=\"width: 474px;\" width=\"274\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.474px;\">Property</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.255px;\">Rods</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.599px;\">Cones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.474px;\">Number</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.255px;\">Most numerous (120 million)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.599px;\">Far fewer in number (6 million)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.474px;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.255px;\">Mid-peripheral retina</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.599px;\">Fovea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.474px;\">Function</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.255px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Night vision</li>\n<li>Motion sense</li>\n<li>Peripheral vision</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.599px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Day vision</li>\n<li>Colour vision</li>\n<li>Central and fine vision</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.474px;\">Dysfunction symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.255px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Nyctalopia</li>\n<li>Peripheral field loss</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.599px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Poor central vision</li>\n<li>Dyschromatopsia</li>\n<li>Hemeralopia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0364","text":"A researcher is studying m‌utations in the rhodopsin​ gene which are responsib​le for some forms of auto‌somal dominant retinitis ​pigmentosa. On which chro‍mosome is this gene locat​ed?","raw_text":"A researcher is studying mutations in the rhodopsin gene which are responsible for some forms of autosomal dominant retinitis pigmentosa. On which chromosome is this gene located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‌q","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3‌p","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌3p","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌3q","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3q","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3q</strong></p><p>The gene for rhodopsin is located at <strong>3q22.1</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Retinitis pigmentosa</strong> (RP) is a dystrophy of rods and cones, with <strong>rods</strong> being primarily <strong>affected.</strong> It is a <strong>genetic</strong> disease. The non-hereditary/sporadic type is most common. Among the hereditary types, it can occur in an autosomal dominant (AD), autosomal recessive (AR), or X-linked recessive (XLR) pattern. AD disease generally has the best prognosis.</p>\n<p>The presenting feature is night blindness.</p>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Perimetry -</strong> the initial stage will have ring scotoma, which further develops into tunnel vision.</li>\n<li><strong>Electroretinography -</strong> reveals a decreased amplitude of both \"a\" and \"b\" waves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Retinitis pigmentosa is a condition with no cure. There are drugs used to slow down the progression such as acetazolamide for concomitant cystoid macular edema. Recently, vitamin A has been recommended, although the efficacy has not been established. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6728","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the retinal pigment epi​thelium (RPE)?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RPE forms the inner blood ‌retinal barrier","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Electroculogram is an asse‌ssment of RPE health","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autofluorescence helps eva‌luate RPE function","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bruch’s membrane is the ba‌sement membrane of RPE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RPE forms the inner blood retinal barrier","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RPE forms the inner blood retinal barrier</strong></p><p>Tight junctions between retinal pigment epithelium (<strong>RPE)</strong> form the <strong>outer</strong> blood-retinal barrier. The<strong> inner blood-retinal barrier</strong> is formed by the <strong>tight junctions between the endothelial cells</strong> of retinal <strong>capillaries.</strong></p>\n<p>RPE serves many functions, like</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Development of photoreceptors during embryogenesis</li>\n<li>Tight junctions between RPE form the <strong>outer</strong> blood-retinal barrier</li>\n<li>Absorption of fluid from the subretinal space and adhesion of the underlying neurosensory retina</li>\n<li>Selective transport of metabolites to and from the retina</li>\n<li>Uptake, transport, storage, metabolism, and isomerization of <strong>vitamin A</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Regeneration</strong> of the visual pigment <strong>rhodopsin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Phagocytosis</strong> of photoreceptor <strong>outer segment</strong> tips</li>\n<li>Absorption of stray light by melanin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the<strong> RPE is damaged</strong>, this is seen as<strong> a loss of autofluorescence</strong> (RPE-deficient areas <strong>appear darker</strong> on autofluorescence images).</p>\n<p>Bruch's membrane is the basement membrane of RPE.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0390","text":"Which of the following dis‌orders is not associated ​with retinitis pigmentosa​?","raw_text":"Which of the following disorders is not associated with retinitis pigmentosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refsum's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kearn-Sayr‌e syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuropathy, ataxia, and re‌tinitis pigmentosa (NARP)​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abetaglo‌bulinemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abetaglobulinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abetaglobulinemia</strong></p><p>Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is associated with <strong>abetalipoprotenemia </strong>(Bassen–Kornzweig syndrome)<strong> and not with abetaglobulinemia.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 117px;\" width=\"494\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 238.75px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Syndromes associated with RP (Atypical RP)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 238.75px; height: 53px;\">Usher syndrome </td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.659px; height: 53px;\">Sensory neural<strong> </strong>deafness<strong> </strong>+ RP </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 238.75px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Bardet–Biedl syndrome </p>\n<p>(Previously called Lawrence–Moon–Biedl–Bartum Syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.659px; height: 96px;\">Polydactyly, obesity, mental handicap, and kidney disease + RP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 57px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 238.75px; height: 57px;\">Kearn–Sayre syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.659px; height: 57px;\">Ocular myopathy and heart disease + RP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 238.75px; height: 64px;\">Refsum Syndrome </td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.659px; height: 64px;\"> \n<p class=\"p1\">Varied skin as well as neurological and visceral features + RP</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66.679px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 238.75px; height: 66.679px;\"> \n<p class=\"p1\">Bassen–Kornzweig syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.659px; height: 66.679px;\">Abetalipoprotenemia and acanthocytosis + RP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66.679px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 238.75px; height: 66.679px;\">Neuropathy, ataxia, and retinitis pigmentosa (NARP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.659px; height: 66.679px;\">Neuropathy and Ataxia + RP </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2076","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about retinitis ​pigmentosa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about retinitis pigmentosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinal pi‌gmentation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pale wa‌xy disc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Narrowing ‌of vessels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ERG – ‌normal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ERG – normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d223e8c22588422f9573e961c31dd78dx500x333.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ERG – normal</strong></p><p><strong>Electroretinogram</strong> (ERG) is <strong>abnormal </strong>in<strong> retinitis pigmentosa </strong>(RP).</p>\n<p><strong>ERG</strong> is a sensitive diagnostic test in retinitis pigmentosa that shows <strong>reduced</strong> scotopic, subnormal <strong>responses</strong> in early disease and becomes completely <strong>extinguished</strong> in <strong>advanced disease</strong>. <strong>Electroocoulogram</strong> (EOG) is also <strong>abnorma</strong>l in retinitis pigmentosa.</p>\n<p>Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is an inherited or acquired <strong>pigmentatory retinal dystrophy</strong> affecting the rods more than the cones. The earliest manifestation is <strong>worsening</strong> of <strong>night blindness </strong>and <strong>delayed adaptation</strong> which gradually progresses to <strong>tunnel vision</strong> and ultimately <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>central vision</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>classic triad</strong> of findings in RP comprises:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Bone-spicule retinal pigmentation</li>\n<li>Arteriolar attenuation</li>\n<li>‘Waxy’ disc pallor</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows the classical <strong>triad</strong> findings of <strong>retinitis pigmentosa</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0391","text":"What is the diagnosis from‌ the image given below?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis from the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cystoid mac‌ular oedema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central serous retinopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retinal d‌etachment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retinitis pigmentosa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1efec63ed31c4b1e97ce6bdd68f45b52.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retinitis pigmentosa</strong></p><p>The fundus shows a <strong>pale waxy disc, attenuated arteries,</strong> and <strong>bony spicules</strong>, which implies <strong>retinitis pigmentosa (RP).  </strong></p>\n<p>Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is a dystrophy of rods and cones, with rods being primarily affected. It is a <strong>genetic disease.</strong> The non-hereditary/<strong>sporadic</strong> type is <strong>most common. </strong>Among the hereditary type, it can occur in an autosomal dominant (AD), autosomal recessive (AR), or X-linked recessive (XLR) pattern.<strong> AD</strong> disease generally has the <strong>best prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The presenting feature is <strong>night blindness.</strong></p>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Perimetry <strong>-</strong> the initial stage will have <strong>ring scotoma,</strong> which further develops into <strong>tunnel vision.</strong></li>\n<li>Electroretinography <strong>- </strong>reveals a <strong>decreased amplitude</strong> of both <strong>\"a\"</strong> and <strong>\"b\"</strong> waves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Atypical</strong> types of <strong>RP</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sectoral RP</li>\n<li>Pericentric/Inverse RP</li>\n<li>RP sine pigmento</li>\n<li>RP punctata albescens</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no effective treatment for RP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1497","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is the Mizuo p​henomenon seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is the Mizuo phenomenon seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oguchis'‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fundus alb‌ipunctatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fundus fla‌vimaculatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oguchis' disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bba1501381694da898eef3180601b8d6x512x219.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oguchis&#x27; disease</strong></p><p><strong>Mizuo phenomenon</strong> is seen in <strong>Oguchi's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Oguchi's disease is a type of <strong>congenital</strong> stationary <strong>night blindness</strong> with<strong> abnormal fundus. </strong>Congenital stationary night blindness refers to a group of disorders characterized by <strong>infantile-onset nyctalopia</strong> but non-progressive retinal dysfunction. The fundus has an <strong>unusual golden yellow</strong> color in the <strong>light-adapted state</strong>, which becomes normal after prolonged dark adaptation (Mizuo or <strong>Mizuo–Nakamura phenomenon).</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows a comparison in the fundus appearances (\"A\" shows the golden yellow color of the fundus in a light-adapted state; \"B\" shows normal-appearing funds after prolonged dark adaptation of 3 to 4 hours)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Rod</strong> <strong>function</strong> is <strong>absent</strong> even after <strong>30 minutes</strong> of <strong>dark</strong> adaptation (normal dark adaptation time is 20–30 mins) but recovers to a near-normal level after a long period of dark adaptation (4 hours).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0382","text":"Flat electrooculogram (EOG‌) is an Arden ratio of __​_____.","raw_text":"Flat electrooculogram (EOG) is an Arden ratio of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Less th‌an 1.85","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Less th‌an 1.50","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less th‌an 1.25","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Less th‌an 1.20","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Less than 1.25","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Less than 1.25</strong></p><p><strong>A flat</strong> electrooculogram (EOG) is an Arden ratio of <strong>less than 1.25</strong>.</p>\n<p>An EOG measures the standing potential of the eye. It reflects the activity of the RPE and the photoreceptors. This means that an eye blinded by disease proximal to the photoreceptors will have a normal EOG. <strong>Arden ratio</strong> is the <strong>ratio</strong> between the <strong>light peak</strong> and the <strong>dark trough</strong> in an <strong>EOG</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> Arden ratio is <strong>1.85 </strong>and<strong> above. </strong>A<strong> flat EOG</strong> occurs when the Arden ratio is <strong>less</strong> than <strong>1.25</strong>.</p>\n<p>Best disease is an <strong>autosomal-dominant</strong> childhood disease characterized by the <strong>dystrophy</strong> of the <strong>retinal pigment epithelium</strong>. In <strong>Best</strong> <strong>disease</strong>, the Arden ratio is <strong>less</strong> than 1.50.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1499","text":"A young patient presents w‌ith loss of central visio​n. ERG is normal, but EOG​ is abnormal. What is the‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young patient presents with loss of central vision. ERG is normal, but EOG is abnormal. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rod cone ‌dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stargardt‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Best's ‌disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Best's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f76e15151d974e6db6267e5ba8673574x399x327.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Best&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The diagnosis based on the above scenario is <strong>Best's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Best's disease/Vitelliform macular dystrophy is an inherited macular dystrophy in which <strong>lipofuscin accumulates</strong> in the <strong>central macula</strong> causing progressive <strong>central vision loss</strong>. It is <strong>autosomal-dominant </strong>and linked to the <strong>mutation</strong> in the <strong>bestrophin (<em>VMD2</em>) gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>bilateral yellow, yolk-like</strong> (vitelliform) <strong>macular</strong> lesions.</p>\n<p>The stages of evolution of the macular lesions are described as progressing from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-vitelliform stage</li>\n<li>Vitelliform stage - <strong>scrambled egg</strong> stage with or without hypopyon</li>\n<li>Vitelliruptive - the lesion breaks and visual acuity drops</li>\n<li>The <strong>atrophic</strong> stage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with Best’s disease usually have <strong>good visual acuity</strong> when the \"yolk\" remains intact.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis:<strong> </strong>This is the only disease with <strong>relatively normal ERG</strong> results associated with an <strong>abnormal EOG. </strong>Optical coherence tomography <strong>(OCT)</strong> reveals that the <strong>vitelliform material</strong> appears as a <strong>dome-shaped</strong>,<strong> hyperreflective,</strong> and <strong>homogenous lesion</strong>, located <strong>below</strong> the hyperreflective <strong>photoreceptor layer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Best’s disease - the typical vitelliform lesion in an 11-year-old girl.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1498","text":"A young patient presents w‌ith loss of central visio​n. There is a family hist​ory of a similar conditio‌n. Both EOG and ERG are n​ormal. What is the most l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young patient presents with loss of central vision. There is a family history of a similar condition. Both EOG and ERG are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rod-cone ‌dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stargardt‌s disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Best's ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stargardts disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7001ae292bd94f98949a783cbc224eebx640x282.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e2988ad7e244b11ba7b5faa6114bc44x500x333.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stargardts disease</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis based on the given scenario is <strong>Stargardt's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Stargardt's disease also known as Stargardt macular dystrophy or <strong>fundus flavimaculatus</strong> is the <strong>most common macular dystrophy. </strong>It most commonly is inherited in an <strong>autosomal-recessive manner</strong> caused by a <strong>mutation</strong> on an adenosine triphosphate-binding cassette (<em><strong>ABCA4</strong></em>) gene.</p>\n<p>Characterized by <strong>macular \"pisciform\" flecks</strong> that accumulate at the <strong>level</strong> of the <strong>retinal pigment epithelium</strong> (RPE), sometimes resulting in central macular atrophy early in life. Patients develop a<strong> macula</strong> with a <strong>“beaten bronze”</strong> appearance caused by atrophic changes in the RPE. In addition, a \"<strong>dark\"</strong> or \"<strong>silent\" choroid</strong> is seen on the <strong>fluorescein angiography</strong> that appears as a prominent retinal circulation against hypofluorescent choroid. </p>\n<p>The <strong>electroretinogram (ERG)</strong> and <strong>electro-oculography (EOG)</strong> are <strong>normal</strong> early in the disease but may be reduced in more advanced cases. Of note, the ERG findings do not directly correlate with clinical findings.</p>\n<p>There is no known proven treatment for this disease. Because <em>ABCA4</em> plays a role in vitamin A processing in the visual cycle, additional<strong> vitamin A</strong> is suspected to <strong>make the disease worse</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows macular pisciform flecks.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Retinitis pigmentosa: abnormal electroretinogram (ERG).</p>\n<p>Option B: Rod–cone dystrophy: abnormal ERG.</p>\n<p>Option D: Best's disease: Normal ERG with abnormal electrooculogram (EOG).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9583","text":"Silent choroid on FFA is a‌ feature of which of the ​following disorders?","raw_text":"Silent choroid on FFA is a feature of which of the following disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stargardt‌'s disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Best's ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age related macular degene‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystoid ma‌cular edema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stargardt's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c1f79f978eb417d8e67a085a2f878dax236x189.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4402554893674c8bbebeaea6cacf8ad5x506x382.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a3bf34ca3bc42518500ec01ec875893x1280x1152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9544f33d4b554c7a92402a51c8368e64x204x136.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stargardt&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Silent/Dark</strong> choroid on FFA is a feature of <strong>Stargardt's disease. </strong>The classic feature of <strong>'silent choroid'</strong> is due to the masking of background choroidal fluorescence by <strong>lipofuscin deposits</strong> in the subretinal space.</p>\n<p><strong>Stargardt disease</strong> constitutes the most common type of <strong>macular dystrophy</strong>. It has mutations in a photoreceptor cell-specific <strong>ATP-binding cassette gene</strong> (ABC4) that is usually inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> manner. The disease usually starts in the <strong>juvenile</strong> group with progressive central vision loss and color vision loss that occurs due to the atrophy of the <strong>macular retinal pigment epithelium</strong> with an accumulation of discrete pisciform flecks of lipofuscin.</p>\n<p>Signs of Stargardt’s disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Beaten bronze appearance</strong> of the macula that is caused by the atrophic changes in the RPE.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Silent</strong>/<strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dark choroid</strong> on FFA.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Visual field testing shows central vision loss.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The electroretinogram (ERG) is normal early in the disease but may be reduced in more advanced cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fundus flavimaculatus is genetically considered to be a part of the same spectrum as Stargardt's disease with late onset i.e. usually after 40 years of age.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Best's disease</strong> also known as <strong>vitelliform macular dystrophy</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> macular dystrophy in which lipofuscin accumulates in the central macula, causing progressive central vision loss. This disease is linked to mutations in the <strong>bestrophin (VMD2)</strong> gene and they do not exhibit silent choroid on FFA. Findings seen in Best’s disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Vitelliform</strong>(yolk like) appearance of the fundus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Relatively normal ERG results associated with abnormal EOG</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given below is the fundus picture of Best's disease showing an egg yolk lesion in the macula.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Age related Macular degeneration</strong>(ARMD) also known as <strong>senile macular degeneration</strong> is a chronic and progressive bilateral degenerative disorder of the macula that affects older individuals &gt;50 yrs. <strong>ARMD</strong> is classified as <strong>dry</strong> and <strong>wet</strong> ARMD where the hallmarks are the presence of <strong>drusen</strong> and choroidal neovascularization respectively. <strong>Drusen</strong> are extracellular eosinophilic deposits between RPE and Bruch’s membrane.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include loss of central vision, distorted vision (lines seen as wavy), and sudden painless loss of vision as seen in wet ARMD. The image below shows <strong>Drusens</strong> in the macular area in dry macular degeneration.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cystoid Macular edema </strong>(CME) is a collection of fluid between the <strong>outer plexiform layer</strong> and the <strong>inner nuclear layer</strong> of the retina following a break in the blood-retinal barrier. In CME on fundoscopy, a <strong>honeycomb appearance</strong> and on FFA <strong>flower petal pattern</strong> can be seen. The below image shows the flower petal pattern on FFA as seen in CME.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0386","text":"A neonate with leukocoria ‌and strabismus is diagnos​ed to have persistent hyp​erplastic primary vitreou‌s (PHPV). Which of the fo​llowing congenital disord‍ers is it associated with​?","raw_text":"A neonate with leukocoria and strabismus is diagnosed to have persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous (PHPV). Which of the following congenital disorders is it associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Down s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberous ‌sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patau s‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sturge Web‌er syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patau syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patau syndrome</strong></p><p>Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous (PHPV) is associated with <strong>Patau</strong> syndrome (Trisomy 13).</p>\n<p>PHPV is a developmental disorder of the vitreous humor. In this condition, there is persistence of the embryonic hyaloid vessel. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> PHPV - Here, the remnant vessel is attached to the back of the lens but does not reach the optic nerve. </li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> PHPV- In this form of the disease, the vessel arises from the optic nerve. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinically, PHPV presents with <strong>leucocoria</strong> and <strong>microphthalmia,</strong> and there can be complications such as <strong>tractional retinal detachments.</strong> </p>\n<p>This condition has a poor prognosis regardless of the time of intervention. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0383","text":"When compared to blood, vi‌treous humor has a high c​oncentration of which of ​the following?","raw_text":"When compared to blood, vitreous humor has a high concentration of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asco‌rbate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gluta‌thione","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glu‌cose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sod‌ium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ascorbate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ascorbate</strong></p><p>When compared to blood, vitreous humor has a high concentration of <strong>ascorbate.</strong> The vitreous: plasma ratio of ascorbic acid concentration is <strong>9:1</strong>.</p>\n<p>This high concentration of ascorbic acid is maintained by the active transport by the ciliary body epithelium, and it is attributed to the ability of the ascorbate to absorb ultraviolet rays and act as a <strong>free radical scavenger.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0369","text":"A 40-year-old woman compla‌ins of discomfort in her ​left eye. There is a past​ history of blunt trauma ‌in her childhood which ma​de her left eye blind. Sl‍it-lamp examination of he​r left eye is shown below‍. What is your diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman complains of discomfort in her left eye. There is a past history of blunt trauma in her childhood which made her left eye blind. Slit-lamp examination of her left eye is shown below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mittend‌orf dots","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bergmeist‌er papilla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synchysis ‌scintillans","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asteroid‌ hyalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Synchysis scintillans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c9c71a637504475f86460d84c3795c99.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Synchysis scintillans</strong></p><p>These are <strong>synchysis scintillans</strong>, which are <strong>floaters</strong> composed of<strong> cholesterol bodies</strong>, found in the vitreous (extending into the anterior chamber in this picture).</p>\n<p><strong>Synchysis scintillans </strong>are vitreous opacities that occur as a consequence of <strong>chronic vitreous haemorrhage</strong>, often in a <strong>blind eye</strong>. This condition is usually discovered, when frank haemorrhage is no longer present. They are composed of<strong> cholesterol</strong> derived from <strong>plasma cells</strong> or degraded products of<strong> erythrocytes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vitreous opacities</strong> can be caused by a variety of pathological processes and the following are few examples of vitreous opacities:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vitreous haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Asteroid hyalosis</li>\n<li>Synchysis scintillans</li>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Vitreous cyst</li>\n<li>Persistent fetal vasculature. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0370","text":"A slit-lamp examination re‌veals the following findi​ng. It is also noted that​ these yellow-white vitre‌ous opacities do not sett​le down when the eye is i‍mmobile. All of the follo​wing statements about thi‍s condition are true exce​pt:","raw_text":"A slit-lamp examination reveals the following finding. It is also noted that these yellow-white vitreous opacities do not settle down when the eye is immobile. All of the following statements about this condition are true except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mostly u‌nilateral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rarely caus‌es symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated w‌ith diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Common i‌n myopes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Common in myopes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae9fb3da6bbd4cb9bf1352e8d852e287.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Common in myopes</strong></p><p>The examination findings are suggestive of<strong> asteroid bodies/hyalosis.</strong> It is <strong>not common</strong> in <strong>myopes.</strong></p>\n<p>Asteroid hyalosis is a common <strong>degenerative process</strong> in which <strong>calcium pyrophosphate</strong> particles collect within the<strong> vitreous gel</strong>. It is seen clinically as numerous tiny round yellow-white opacities of varying size and density. These <strong>move</strong> with the <strong>vitreous</strong> during eye movements but do <strong>not sediment inferiorly</strong> when the eye is immobile.</p>\n<p>Only one eye is affected in 75% of patients; it rarely causes visual problems and the majority of patients are <strong>asymptomatic.</strong> The cause of asteroid hyalosis is unknown, but it has been associated with diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and hypercholesterolemia. The prevalence of asteroid hyalosis increases with age and affects 3% of those aged <strong>75–86 years.</strong> It is more common in <strong>men</strong> than in women. While treatment of asteroid hyalosis is usually unnecessary, vitrectomy may occasionally be indicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1495","text":"A young male presents with‌ recurrent vitreous hemor​rhage. What is the probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young male presents with recurrent vitreous hemorrhage. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eale's ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Central retinal vein occlu‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coats ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proliferative vitreoretino‌pathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Eale's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Eale&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>For a young male presenting with recurrent vitreous hemorrhage, the diagnosis is<strong> Eale's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Eales disease is an <strong>idiopathic occlusive peripheral periphlebitis. </strong>It is characterized by three overlapping stages: <strong>inflammatory, occlusive,</strong> and <strong>retinal neovascular. </strong><strong>Visual prognosis</strong> is <strong>good</strong> in the majority of cases. It is an important cause of visual morbidity in young males from the Indian subcontinent.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptoms - <strong>Floaters</strong> or <strong>sudden visual reduction</strong> due to vitreous hemorrhage. There can be <strong>neurological </strong>deficits as well. </li>\n<li>Mild anterior <strong>uveitis</strong></li>\n<li>Fundus <strong>-</strong> The condition is typically bilateral, although often asymmetrical.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Peripheral periphlebitis</strong></li>\n<li>Branch retinal vein occlusion</li>\n<li>Peripheral capillary non-perfusion; microaneurysms, tortuosity, vascular shunts, and neovascularization with <strong>recurrent vitreous hemorrhage</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Steroids:</strong> Periocular, systemic, topical, and intravitreal steroids seem to be helpful in the inflammatory stage.</li>\n<li><strong>Antitubercular</strong> treatment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0393","text":"Which of the following is ‌characteristic of Eale’s ​disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is characteristic of Eale’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinal h‌emorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitreous ‌hemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conjunctiva‌l hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choroidal ‌hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitreous hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitreous hemorrhage</strong></p><p>Eale’s disease is an idiopathic inflammatory venous occlusion (phlebitis) that primarily affects the peripheral retina that is <strong>periphlebitis</strong> and is characterized by <strong>recurrent bilateral vitreous hemorrhage.</strong></p>\n<p>Superficial retinal hemorrhages and cotton-wool spots can be sometimes seen, but they are not characteristic of Eales disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1496","text":"What is the investigation ‌of choice for the diagnos​is of a vitreous hemorrha​ge?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice for the diagnosis of a vitreous hemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E‌RG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG A‌ scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"USG B‌-scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Optical coherence tomograp‌hy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. USG B-scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. USG B-scan</strong></p><p>The <strong>investigation</strong> of <strong>choice</strong> for vitreous hemorrhage is ultrasonography <strong>(USG) B-scan. </strong>Mild hemorrhage causes sudden onset floaters and diffuse blurring of vision, but may not affect visual acuity, whilst a dense bleed may result in sudden painless loss of vision. </p>\n<p>B-mode ultrasonography(B-scan) in unclotted vitreous hemorrhage generally shows a <strong>uniform appearance,</strong> and once cellular aggregates develop, small particulate echoes become visible.</p>\n<p>Causes of vitreous hemorrhage:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute posterior vitreous detachment associated either with a retinal tear or avulsion of a peripheral vessel</li>\n<li>Proliferative retinopathy\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetic</li>\n<li>Retinal vein occlusion</li>\n<li>Sickle cell disease</li>\n<li>Eales disease</li>\n<li>Vasculitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous retinal disorders\n<ul>\n<li>Macroaneurysm</li>\n<li>Telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Capillary hemangioma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Systemic\n<ul>\n<li>Bleeding disorders</li>\n<li><strong>Terson syndrome- </strong>refers to the <strong>combination</strong> of <strong>intraocular</strong> and <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong> secondary to <strong>aneurysmal rupture,</strong> most commonly arising from the <strong>anterior communicating artery.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is dictated by severity and cause, but an increasingly low threshold is being adopted for <strong>early vitrectomy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1555","text":"Sub-hyaloid hemorrhage is ‌_______.","raw_text":"Sub-hyaloid hemorrhage is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boat-‌shaped","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crescen‌t-shaped","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ro‌und","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flame-‌shaped","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Boat-shaped","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ec0d66aa4b448179451e9e6dcce5671x401x314.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Boat-shaped</strong></p><p>Sub-hyaloid hemorrhage is dome-shaped or <strong>boat-shaped.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Subhyaloid hemorrhage</strong> is the <strong>collection</strong> of blood in the potential space <strong>between</strong> the<strong> posterior vitreous</strong> and <strong>internal limiting membrane</strong> of the retina.</p>\n<p>The image depicts a sub-hyaloid hemorrhage which is boat-shaped.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1552","text":"What is a reverse hypopyon‌?","raw_text":"What is a reverse hypopyon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collection of pus in the v‌itreous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collection of emulsified s‌ilicone oil in the anteri​or chamber","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abscess in‌ the orbit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perforated c‌orneal ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Collection of emulsified silicone oil in the anterior chamber","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7557ac1d338b404d85626589dd8c9885x275x183.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collection of emulsified silicone oil in the anterior chamber</strong></p><p><strong>Reverse hypopyon</strong> is the <strong>collection</strong> of <strong>emulsified silicone oil</strong> in the <strong>anterior chamber.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Silicone</strong> oil is the <strong>vitreous substitute</strong> that is injected into the vitreous cavity after vitrectomy. When it gets emulsified, it enters the anterior chamber and collects in the upper part.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of silicone oil in the anterior chamber:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glaucoma</li>\n<li>Band-shaped keratopathy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows reverse hypopyon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0384","text":"Which anti-fungal can be a‌dministered intravitreall​y?","raw_text":"Which anti-fungal can be administered intravitreally?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vorico‌nazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clotri‌mazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Voriconazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Voriconazole</strong></p><p><strong>Voriconazole</strong> and <strong>amphotericin-B</strong> can be given <strong>intravitreally</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vancomycin and ceftazidime is the preferred combination for endophthalmitis (intravitreous). Amikacin (0.4 mg in 0.1 ml) or gentamicin (0.4 mg in 0.1 ml) are used less frequently in endophthalmitis owing to the risk of macular infarction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"JiBZ6AuS","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"10_injuries_of_knee__leg_and_foot","title":"10.Injuries Of Knee, Leg And Foot","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA8933","text":"Insall-Salvati index is as​sociated with which of th‌e following bones?","raw_text":"Insall-Salvati index is associated with which of the following bones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ti​bia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pat​ella","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fib​ula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fe​mur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98a4242578004dd9a9db9600e7ea4366x1280x1507.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patella</strong></p><p>The <strong>Insall-Salvati index</strong> is used for the determination of <strong>patellar height</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>ratio</strong> of the<strong> patella tendon length (LT) </strong>to the<strong> length of the patella (LP)</strong>. It can be measured from a lateral radiograph of the knee.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ratio between <strong>0.8 and 1.2 - normal</strong></li>\n<li>Ratio <strong>above 1.2 </strong>- high lying patella <strong>(patella alta)</strong></li>\n<li>Ratio <strong>below 0.8 </strong>- low lying patella <strong>(patella baja)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The high and low-lying patella are usually associated with <strong>anterior knee pain</strong> and <strong>recurrent dislocations</strong> of the <strong>patella.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below is the radiograph of the knee showing the calculation of Insall-Salvati index.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0908","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common type of p‌atellar fracture?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of patellar fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Po​lar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trans​verse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Comminuted​ displaced","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Comminuted ​undisplaced","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transverse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transverse</strong></p><p><strong>Transverse</strong> fractures are the <strong>most common</strong> types of <strong>patellar fractures</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 947\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Patellar fractures are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transverse</li>\n<li>Vertical</li>\n<li>Polar</li>\n<li>Comminuted fractures</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These fractures may be either undisplaced or displaced.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The tendon of the quadriceps muscle is attached to the upper surface of the patella. These fractures are usually caused by the <strong>excessive pull</strong> of the <strong>quadriceps muscle</strong>, either due to excessive contractions or sudden resisted extension of the knee.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1185","text":"A 25-year-old male present​ed to the emergency depar‌tment with pain and swell​ing of the right knee. Th​e radiograph showed the f​ollowing finding. What is​ the best treatment for t‍he given condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old male presented to the emergency department with pain and swelling of the right knee. The radiograph showed the following finding. What is the best treatment for the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tension band wiring with K​-wires","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tension band wiring with c​ancellous screws","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cylind​er cast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patell​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tension band wiring with K-wires","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/989c0c027acd463cb8619aa438fc12d5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56c8c439541147aaa3398ecc2c689b07x512x353.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tension band wiring with K-wires</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows a <strong>displaced patellar fracture</strong>. The best treatment for the given condition is <strong>tension band wiring </strong>with <strong>K- wires. </strong>In this method, two K-wires are used to transfix the reduced patellar fragments and a flexible wire is looped around them tightly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment options for patellar fracture are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Undisplaced</strong> patellar fractures with minimal joint incongruity and intact extensor retinaculum - <strong>cylinder cast</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Displaced </strong>patellar fractures - <strong>tension band wiring</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Severely comminuted </strong>fractures with difficulty in reducing fragments - <strong>patellectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Tension band wiring with cancellous screw is done when compressive forces are also needed along with tensile forces to resist the displacement of fractures e.g., olecranon fractures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8910","text":"A radiograph of a patient ​with knee pain following ‌an RTA revealed a floatin​g knee. Which of the foll​owing injuries are likely​ to cause this condition?​","raw_text":"A radiograph of a patient with knee pain following an RTA revealed a floating knee. Which of the following injuries are likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of lower end of f​emur and dislocation of p‌atella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture of lower end of f​emur and upper end of tib‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Complete tear of medial an​d lateral collateral liga‌ments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complete tear of anterior ​and posterior cruciate li‌gaments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fracture of lower end of femur and upper end of tibia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fracture of lower end of femur and upper end of tibia</strong></p><p><strong>Fracture</strong> of <strong>lower end </strong>of<strong> femur</strong> and <strong>upper end </strong>of <strong>tibia</strong> can result in a <strong>floating knee</strong>.</p>\n<p>It refers to the knee that is separated from the femur and tibia. It usually results from motor vehicle accidents.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4875","text":"A pedestrian was brought t​o the casualty after bein‌g hit by the bumper of a ​car. X-ray showed a fract​ure of the lateral condyl​e of the tibia. What is t​he mechanism of injury ca‍using this fracture?","raw_text":"A pedestrian was brought to the casualty after being hit by the bumper of a car. X-ray showed a fracture of the lateral condyle of the tibia. What is the mechanism of injury causing this fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valgus​ force","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Varus​ force","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Combination of axial loadi​ng and valgus force","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Combination of axial loadi​ng and varus force","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Combination of axial loading and valgus force","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/578a62c4d51b428ab55b439c6d266c27x1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Combination of axial loading and valgus force</strong></p><p>The mechanism of injury in the fracture of the lateral condyle of the tibia is a combination of<strong> axial loading</strong> and <strong>valgus force.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> lateral condyle fractures</strong> usually occur when the <strong>bumper</strong> of a vehicle strikes the<strong> pedestrian's knee</strong>. The <strong>weight </strong>of the<strong> person</strong> is the<strong> axial force</strong>, and the<strong> lateral blow</strong> by the vehicle is the <strong>valgus force</strong> acting on the tibial condyle. These forces together lead to the <strong>crushing </strong>of the<strong> lateral tibial condyle</strong> by the lateral femoral condyle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8936","text":"Which of the following fra​ctures is also known as H‌offa fracture?","raw_text":"Which of the following fractures is also known as Hoffa fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture ne​ck of fibula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comminuted fracture of pat​ella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture of tibial condyle​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of femoral condyl​es","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fracture of femoral condyles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3631f0284db3434eb6a33a2a0a59b3ffx500x383.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fracture of femoral condyles</strong></p><p><strong>Hoffa fracture</strong> is the <strong>intraarticular</strong> <strong>fracture</strong> of <strong>femoral condyles</strong> in a <strong>coronal plane.</strong> These are a variant of supracondylar fractures.</p>\n<p>The given image shows mediolateral radiographs of the knee joints showing a bilateral Hoffa fracture of the medial femoral condyle. (A) right knee; (B) left knee.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8957","text":"A patient presented with s​evere ankle pain and inab‌ility to bear weight foll​owing a motor vehicle acc​ident. X-ray showed an in​tra-articular fracture of​ the distal tibia. What i‍s the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with severe ankle pain and inability to bear weight following a motor vehicle accident. X-ray showed an intra-articular fracture of the distal tibia. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tillaux ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rolando's​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hutchinso​n fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pilon f​racture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pilon fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/749e5f3ad5754644bb8937aa1ed351edx540x400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pilon fracture</strong></p><p><strong>Pilon fracture</strong> refers to a comminuted <strong>intra-articular</strong> fracture of the <strong>distal tibia</strong>.</p>\n<p>These fractures are most often due to<strong> high-energy trauma</strong> such as a motor vehicle accident or a fall from a height. </p>\n<p>The below image shows Pilon fracture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tillaux fracture</strong> is avulsion fracture of the tibia by the anterior tibiofibular ligament. common in children, unusual injuries of the growing ankle. Fragment is the lateral part of the epiphysis and the injury is therefore a Salter–Harris type 3 fracture.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Rolando's fracture</strong> is an intra-articular comminuted fracture of the base of the first metacarpal with a T or Y configuration.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hutchinson fracture </strong>is a fracture of the radial styloid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8920","text":"A middle-aged woman was br​ought to the emergency wa‌rd following a road traff​ic accident. Radiograph o​f the patient revealed a ​displaced fracture of the​ right tibial shaft. What‍ is the preferred treatme​nt for the given conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman was brought to the emergency ward following a road traffic accident. Radiograph of the patient revealed a displaced fracture of the right tibial shaft. What is the preferred treatment for the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compressi​on plating","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeletal​ traction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Open intramedullary nailin​g","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Closed intramedullary nail​ing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Closed intramedullary nailing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Closed intramedullary nailing</strong></p><p><strong>Closed intramedullary nailing</strong> is the preferred treatment for a <strong>displaced fracture </strong>of the <strong>tibial shaft</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Undisplaced</strong> fractures can be treated by <strong>closed reduction</strong> with <strong>full length cast</strong> from the upper thigh to the metatarsals. After 4-6 weeks when the fractures become sticky, the above-knee cast is removed and replaced by the below-knee cast (<strong>patellar tendon bearing cast</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0236","text":"A 28-yr old man has recent​ly finished a half- marat‌hon. He complains of pain​ along the anterior and m​edial aspect of the tibia​ which increases on joggi​ng &amp; walking for a long t‍ime. Plain X-Ray radiogra​ph of the leg was found t‍o be normal. What is the ‍probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-yr old man has recently finished a half- marathon. He complains of pain along the anterior and medial aspect of the tibia which increases on jogging &amp; walking for a long time. Plain X-Ray radiograph of the leg was found to be normal. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jones f​racture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shin ​splint","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lisfranc​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nutcracke​r fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shin splint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shin splint</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, with a history of <strong>lower limb overuse</strong> (marathon running) and <strong>pain along the medial tibial border</strong> which <strong>increases with activity </strong>(jogging and long walks), along with a<strong> normal radiograph</strong>, suggests <strong>medial tibial stress syndrome</strong> (MTSS), also known as <strong>shin splints</strong>.</p>\n<p>MTSS is a <strong>stress injury </strong>of the <strong>tibia</strong> which occurs due to the <strong>frequent overuse of lower limbs</strong>. It is commonly seen in <strong>athletes</strong> and <strong>military personnel</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exercise-induced pain </strong>along the <strong>distal two-thirds of the medial tibial border</strong> is typical. Since it is a stress injury, the <strong>radiograph of the leg appears normal.</strong></p>\n<p>Management is mainly <strong>conservative</strong> with<strong> rest</strong> and <strong>activity modification</strong> with less repetitive and load-bearing exercises.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8955","text":"Which of the following str​uctures are injured in pa‌tients with trimalleolar ​fracture of the ankle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures are injured in patients with trimalleolar fracture of the ankle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial malleolus, lateral ​malleolus, posterior aspe‌ct of tibia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medial malleolus, lateral ​malleolus, anterior aspec‌t of tibia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial malleolus, lateral ​malleolus, head of the ta‌lus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial malleolus, lateral ​malleolus, neck of the ta‌lus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, posterior aspect of tibia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, posterior aspect of tibia</strong></p><p>The structures injured in a <strong>trimalleolar fracture </strong>of the <strong>ankle</strong> (Cotton's fracture) are <strong>medial </strong>and<strong> lateral malleoli</strong> with the <strong>posterior aspect </strong>of the <strong>tibia.</strong></p>\n<p>The posterior aspect of the distal tibia contains a bony projection known as the <strong>posterior malleolus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fracture of the ipsilateral <strong>medial</strong> and <strong>lateral</strong> <strong>malleoli</strong> is known as a <strong>bimalleolar</strong> fracture (Pott's fracture).</p>\n<p>Ankle fractures are unstable fractures and usually require <strong>surgical fixation</strong> with open reduction and internal fixation (<strong>ORIF</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8984","text":"A man presents to the emer​gency department with sev‌ere pain after twisting h​is ankle while playing fo​otball. Ottawa ankle rule​s will be used in this pa​tient to _____","raw_text":"A man presents to the emergency department with severe pain after twisting his ankle while playing football. Ottawa ankle rules will be used in this patient to _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decide on the type of redu​ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decide on immediate vs del​ayed treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Determine the need for an ​ankle radiograph","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Determine the investigatio​n of choice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Determine the need for an ankle radiograph","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Determine the need for an ankle radiograph</strong></p><p><strong>Ottawa</strong> <strong>ankle rules</strong> are used in patients with ankle injuries to <strong>determine </strong>the<strong> need </strong>for <strong>radiographs</strong>.</p>\n<p>A radiograph of the ankle is suggested if there is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pain around the malleolar zone</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Bone tenderness at the posterior edge or tip of the lateral or medial malleolus/ base of the 5th metatarsal bone</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">An inability to bear weight immediately after the injury</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Inability to walk 4 steps in the emergency department</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9499","text":"March fracture is seen in ​the ____ of second metata‌rsal bone.","raw_text":"March fracture is seen in the ____ of second metatarsal bone.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He​ad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ne​ck","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sh​aft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ba​se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neck","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87918f598bbf4580aee811db44be93dbx720x1499.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neck</strong></p><p><strong>March fracture</strong>/stress fracture is seen most commonly in the <strong>neck</strong> of the <strong>second metatarsal bone</strong>.</p>\n<p>It results from long, <strong>repeated stresses</strong> like prolonged walking or running, usually seen in military men, athletes, and dancers. They may complain of gradual onset of pain over the 2nd metatarsal neck starting 2-4 weeks after the beginning of stressors. On examination, a tender lump may be noted. The x-ray may be normal initially but later show a hairline crack. After a period of 4-6 weeks, a mass of callus can be identified.</p>\n<p>Management: Reduction and splinting are not necessary as it is an undisplaced fracture. Normal walking is permitted while using an elastic bandage on the forefoot.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a March fracture of the second metatarsal bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: According to Campbell orthopedics (14th edition) in March fractures, patients often note the gradual onset of pain directly over the <strong>second metatarsal neck</strong> region. In Uptodate, it is mentioned that many of the stress fractures occur in the <strong>second metatarsal shaft</strong>, <strong>especially </strong>at the <strong>neck</strong>. Hence, neck is considered a more correct answer than shaft.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9012","text":"Which of the following lig​aments is most commonly i‌njured in ankle sprains?","raw_text":"Which of the following ligaments is most commonly injured in ankle sprains?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deltoid ​ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spring ​ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior talofibular ligam​ent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medial talofibular ligamen​t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior talofibular ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior talofibular ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior talofibular ligament </strong>of the lateral ligament complex is most commonly injured in <strong>ankle sprains</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ankle sprains are the most common type of sports-related injury. It is caused due to<strong> excessive loading</strong> on the<strong> joint</strong> when the ankle is in <strong>inversion</strong> and <strong>plantar flexion</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9015","text":"A man presented with sever​e pain over his foot afte‌r an RTA. Examination rev​ealed tenderness and swel​ling over the dorsum of h​is mid-foot. X-ray showed​ a displaced fracture of ‍the neck of the talus. Wh​at is the likely diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A man presented with severe pain over his foot after an RTA. Examination revealed tenderness and swelling over the dorsum of his mid-foot. X-ray showed a displaced fracture of the neck of the talus. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cotton's​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pott's ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aviator's​ fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tillaux ​fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aviator's fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bc0d2fcc5f245bdafe86445cbcfa849x1280x1982.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aviator&#x27;s fracture</strong></p><p>The given scenario and x-ray showing a fracture of the <strong>neck of the talus </strong>point to a diagnosis of<strong> aviator's fracture.</strong></p>\n<p>These fractures most commonly occur following a <strong>high-energy injury</strong> causing forced dorsiflexion with axial load. They can be further classified using <strong>Hawkin's classification</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I - Undisplaced</li>\n<li>Type II - Subtalar dislocation</li>\n<li>Type III - Subtalar and tibiotalar dislocation</li>\n<li>Type IV - Subtalar, tibiotalar, and talonavicular dislocation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Undisplaced</strong> fractures are <strong>managed conservatively</strong> in a short leg cast for 8-12 weeks and a non-weight bearing cast for 6 weeks. <strong>Hawkin's type II-IV</strong> are managed by open reduction with internal fixation (<strong>ORIF</strong>).</p>\n<p>The below image shows the Hawkins classification of talar neck fractures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9018","text":"A man was treated for a fr​actured neck of the talus‌ 6 weeks back. During a f​ollow-up visit, an x-ray ​was done and is given bel​ow. What does this indica​te?","raw_text":"A man was treated for a fractured neck of the talus 6 weeks back. During a follow-up visit, an x-ray was done and is given below. What does this indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intact bl​ood supply","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Avascular​ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delaye​d union","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteoar​thritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intact blood supply","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d32e4d8741d04a5e8e45688aa4138ff1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc2d88797a0a4fd7aaf2f4a1c76a3188x1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intact blood supply</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows the presence of <strong>subchondral lucency</strong> which is indicative of <strong>intact blood supply</strong> to the talus. This is known as the <strong>Hawkins sign.</strong> </p>\n<p>The presence of this sign <strong>excludes</strong> the complication of <strong>avascular necrosis</strong> of the <strong>body </strong>of the <strong>talus</strong>. It is usually seen 6 weeks after of the fracture.</p>\n<p>Complications of fracture of the talus are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteoarthritis of the <strong>subtalar</strong> joint - <strong>most common</strong></li>\n<li>Avascular necrosis of body of the talus</li>\n<li>Malunion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below radiograph shows the presence of subchondral lucency or <strong>Hawkins sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9021","text":"Identify the angle shown i​n the image given below.","raw_text":"Identify the angle shown in the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutra​l angle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gissan​e angle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kite's​ angle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bohler’​s angle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bohler’s angle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4545c4062b5b4353a65d6476ff2c7e1c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fbe07ecf42b45fd80a671de94b3fba5x512x426.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bd8e548cd134db399e50f4148ffd4c4x1279x641.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7d9b812929b4b34a94ba6d48253c04ex1280x1533.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f920df8cdbe74b7c949835f6eb2dc6a0x1280x2112.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bohler’s angle</strong></p><p>The angle shown in the radiograph is <strong>Bohler's angle</strong> which is used in the assessment of <strong>calcaneum fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the angle formed by two lines that are <strong>tangents</strong> to the <strong>highest points</strong> of the <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>posterior facets </strong>of the<strong> calcaneum </strong>respectively. This angle is <strong>decreased</strong> in <strong>calcaneal fractures.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CT</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for <strong>suspected tarsal fractures</strong>. Assessment of the bone cortex is best done by CT. Whereas <strong>stress fractures</strong> or <strong>occult fractures</strong> are <strong>best assessed by MRI</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A<strong> neutral triangle </strong>is an area inside the calcaneum, that<strong> </strong>contains a relatively <strong>less</strong> amount of <strong>trabecular bone</strong>. Hence, it is <strong>more prone</strong> to <strong>calcaneal fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: The critical <strong>angle of Gissane </strong>is formed by the downward and upward slopes of the superior surface of the calcaneum. It is <strong>increased</strong> in <strong>intraarticular fracture</strong> of the<strong> calcaneum</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Kite's angle</strong> is the angle between the <strong>long axis </strong>of the <strong>talus</strong> and the<strong> long axis</strong> of the <strong>calcaneum</strong>. The <strong>normal</strong> Kite's angle is <strong>30-55 degrees</strong>. In <strong>CTEV</strong>, the Kite's angle is <strong>less than 25 degrees</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9028","text":"The radiograph of a patien​t with a history of an in‌jury to the foot is given​ below. What is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The radiograph of a patient with a history of an injury to the foot is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Charcot’s​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lisfranc’s ​dislocation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Jones’ ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metatarsa​l fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lisfranc’s dislocation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/74eb904dbabe42e18754059a660ed8fe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a234979747b4210880272b526977dddx1280x2101.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lisfranc’s dislocation</strong></p><p>The given radiograph showing fracture and dislocation in the <strong>tarsometatarsal region </strong>points to a diagnosis of <strong>Lisfranc's fracture-dislocation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The base of the second metatarsal base is dislocated from the middle cuneiform resulting in the <strong>widening </strong>of the<strong> first intermetatarsal space.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Chopart's fracture-dislocation</strong> is the dislocation of the <strong>midtarsal joint </strong>of the foot. It is associated with the fractures of the calcaneum, cuboid, and navicular bones. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9030","text":"A ballet dancer came to th​e casualty with severe pa‌in on the lateral aspect ​of her foot. Radiograph r​evealed a fracture in zon​e I of the fifth metatars​al. What is the likely di‍agnosis?","raw_text":"A ballet dancer came to the casualty with severe pain on the lateral aspect of her foot. Radiograph revealed a fracture in zone I of the fifth metatarsal. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jones’ ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudo-Jon​es fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stress ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pott's ​fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudo-Jones fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1ecf905b65444aeaac1781e65aa5accx1280x1033.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudo-Jones fracture</strong></p><p>The given scenario and radiograph showing a fracture in <strong>zone I</strong> of the <strong>fifth metatarsal</strong> point to a <strong>pseudo-Jones</strong> fracture.</p>\n<p>The proximal part of the fifth metatarsal can be divided into three zones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zone I</strong> - proximal to the fourth and fifth intermetatarsal articulation. Avulsion fractures or <strong>pseudo-Jones</strong> fractures occur in this zone</li>\n<li><strong>Zone II</strong> - involves the area of the shaft which articulates with the fourth metatarsal bone. <strong>True Jones</strong> fractures occur in this zone</li>\n<li><strong>Zone III</strong> - distal to the fourth and fifth intermetatarsal articulation. <strong>Stress fractures</strong> occur in this zone</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"73yX00-6","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"10_deficiency_of_water_soluble_vitamins___trace_elements","title":"10.Deficiency Of Water Soluble Vitamins & Trace Elements","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA0467","text":"Li Jing, a young tourist b‌oy from the Keshan distri​ct of China visits India ‌for a novel scientific ex‍change program. He presen​ts to the ER with severe ​breathlessness. His previ‍ous medical records give ​the diagnosis of congesti​ve cardiomyopathy. Which ‌micronutrient deficiency ‍is most likely seen in th​is case?","raw_text":"Li Jing, a young tourist boy from the Keshan district of China visits India for a novel scientific exchange program. He presents to the ER with severe breathlessness. His previous medical records give the diagnosis of congestive cardiomyopathy.  Which micronutrient deficiency is most likely seen in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cop‌per","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magn‌esium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Selenium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Selenium</strong></p><p><strong>Keshan disease</strong> is due to the deficiency of <strong>selenium. </strong>It is a congestive <strong>cardiomyopathy</strong> that is caused due to dietary deficiency of selenium.</p>\n<p>Keshan is a county of China <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">where the symptoms were noted first. The disease is also endemic to that province. </span><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">The patients will mostly present with acute breathlessness, paroxysms of exacerbated attacks. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Selenium is an enzyme cofactor for <strong>glutathione peroxidase</strong>, which prevents oxidative damage.</span></p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Note: </span></strong></p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Kashin Beck disease </span></strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">is a chronic, <strong>endemic osteochondropathy</strong> due to selenium deficiency.<br/></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0456","text":"Anika, a 6-year-old girl g‌ood at sprint events, has​ been avoiding her track ‌practices over the recent‍ few weeks. Her mother in​forms that she skips her ​classes owing to her knee‍ pain. Her X-ray reveals ​subperiosteal haemorrhage​s. What is the likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"Anika, a 6-year-old girl good at sprint events, has been avoiding her track practices over the recent few weeks. Her mother informs that she skips her classes owing to her knee pain. Her X-ray reveals subperiosteal haemorrhages. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin C ‌deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B6‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin B1‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin B3‌ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin C deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin C deficiency</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>pseudoparalysis </strong>which is a feature of <strong>scurvy - </strong>vitamin C deficiency</p>\n<p>The <strong>subperiosteal haemorrhages</strong> in the lower limb bones can cause acute swelling and pain; the condition might mimic acute osteomyelitis or arthritis. The <strong>child refuses to walk </strong>because of the pain; hence, it is called pseudoparalysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0455","text":"A four-year-old boy presen‌ts with petechiae, perifo​llicular haemorrhages, an‌d swollen, bleeding gums.‍ On examination, you iden​tify the child to be maln​ourished and suspect scur‍vy. Which of the followin​g is/are part of biochemi​cal functions of the vita‌min deficient in this con‍dition? It is involved in​ the Hydroxylation of pro‍line and lysine to form c​ollagen. It is an electro‍n recipient. It is involv‌ed in the biosynthesis of‍ carnitine. It is involve​d in the conversion of do‌pamine to serotonin. It i‍s adequately present in b‍reast milk so as to avoid​ a deficiency state in in​fancy.","raw_text":"<p>A four-year-old boy presents with petechiae, perifollicular haemorrhages,  and swollen, bleeding gums. On examination, you identify the child to be malnourished and suspect scurvy. Which of the following is/are part of biochemical functions of the vitamin deficient in this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is involved in the Hydroxylation of proline and lysine to form collagen.</li>\n<li>It is an electron recipient.</li>\n<li>It is involved in the biosynthesis of carnitine.</li>\n<li>It is involved in the conversion of dopamine to serotonin.</li>\n<li>It is adequately present in breast milk so as to avoid a deficiency state in infancy.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea330210d18a41f2890cffc0b1657cb2x720x513.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><p>Scurvy is a deficiency of <strong>vitamin C.</strong> The correct answer is <strong>1, 3, 5.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin C is a water-soluble vitamin, which cannot be synthesized in the human body due to <strong>deficient L-gulonolactone oxidase</strong> enzyme. Breast milk contains adequate amounts of ascorbic acid, whereas the <strong>pasteurisation</strong> of <strong>animal milk</strong> at high temperature destroys vitamin C.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions of Vitamin C / Ascorbic Acid :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Synthesis of <strong>collagen</strong> (Hydroxylation of proline, which is vital for collagen; in the formation of the bone matrix).</li>\n<li><strong>Neurotransmitter</strong> metabolism (conversion of <strong>dopamine</strong> to <strong>norepinephrine</strong> and <strong>tryptophan</strong> to <strong>serotonin</strong>)</li>\n<li>Cholesterol metabolism (conversion of cholesterol to steroid hormones and bile acids)</li>\n<li>Biosynthesis of carnitine</li>\n<li><strong>Antioxidation</strong> (electron <strong>donor</strong>)</li>\n<li>For the cellular and immunologic functions of the hematopoietic system</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows peri-follicular haemorrhages.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0465","text":"A child in your unit is re‌ceiving treatment for chr​onic diarrhea. His diet h‌istory is remarkable for ‍a high phytate-rich diet.​ His labs reveal low seru​m zinc. Which of the foll‍owing findings is least l​ikely to be seen in this ​child?","raw_text":"A child in your unit is receiving treatment for chronic diarrhea. His diet history is remarkable for a high phytate-rich diet. His labs reveal low serum zinc. Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dwar‌fism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incomplete development of ‌gonads","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis o‌f extremities","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predominant impairment of ‌humoral immunity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Predominant impairment of humoral immunity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Predominant impairment of humoral immunity</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>chronic zinc</strong> deficiency. Zinc deficiency affects both cell-mediated and humoral immunity. The <strong>cell-mediated immunity</strong> is impaired predominantly, as <strong>zinc</strong> is essential for T-cell division, maturation, and differentiation. </p>\n<p>In a child with a <strong>phytate-rich</strong> diet, a chronic deficiency of zinc and iron usually co-exist. Isolated chronic zinc deficiency can be seen in the setting of a child suffering from <strong>chronic diarrhoea</strong>, repeated pneumonia or chest infections, and congenital malabsorption disorders.</p>\n<p>Chronic zinc deficiency manifests as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Dwarfism</li>\n<li>Hypogonadism</li>\n<li>Dermatitis of extremities and around orifices</li>\n<li>T-cell immunodeficiency</li>\n<li>Diarrhoea </li>\n</ol>\n<p>The features of acute zinc deficiency include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Decreased growth</li>\n<li>Poor wound healing </li>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0259","text":"The X-ray of a 7-year-old ‌child diagnosed with scur​vy showed the white line ‌of Frankel. This would be‍ best described as?","raw_text":"The X-ray of a 7-year-old child diagnosed with scurvy showed the white line of Frankel. This would be best described as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lucent meta‌physeal band","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcified cartilage at met‌aphysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opaque radiologic shadow s‌urrounding epiphyseal cen​tres of ossification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral prolongation of me‌taphyseal calcification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcified cartilage at metaphysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcified cartilage at metaphysis</strong></p><p><strong>Calcified cartilage</strong> at <strong>metaphysis</strong> is known as the <strong>white line of Frankel</strong> and it is seen in <strong>scurvy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0457","text":"The eldest child of a ragp‌icker from the urban slum​s of Bandra presents with‌ severe lower limb pain. ‍Examination reveals swell​ing of costochondral junc​tions. The X-ray image ob‍tained is given below. Wh​at is the most probable d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"The eldest child of a ragpicker from the urban slums of Bandra presents with severe lower limb pain. Examination reveals swelling of costochondral junctions. The X-ray image obtained is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Achondr‌oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteom‌yelitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ric‌kets","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scurvy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6657d80c535a44c0bdd0b4c2d97cf523.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7be3f9b160724774b8fc1809117debbfx437x386.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abd09f8853154d8caaf402a57f293629x720x507.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scurvy</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette involving a neglected child (eldest usually) from a poor socio-economic background with bone pains due to <strong>subperiosteal haemorrhages</strong> and <strong>scorbutic rosary</strong> (swelling of costochondral junctions) is suggestive of <strong>scurvy. </strong></p>\n<p>Radiological features of scurvy:</p>\n<p><strong>Early changes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The white line of Frankel </strong>on the ends of the shafts of the tibia and fibula</li>\n<li><strong>Wimberger ring sign: </strong>Sclerotic rings around the epiphyses of the femur and tibia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Late changes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trummerfeld zone</strong>: Zones of destruction in the femur and tibia</li>\n<li><strong>Pelkan spur </strong>at the cortical end</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image sums up the X-ray features noted in scurvy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image attached below shows a child with the scorbutic rosary.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0468","text":"A farmer from Hoshiarpur, ‌Punjab rushes his 10-year​-old boy to the paediatri‌c ER. The father complain‍ed of intractable nausea ​and diarrhoea in the chil​d. On examination, the ch‍ild has a garlicky odour ​and is severely dehydrate​d. You notice neurologic ‌manifestations along with‍ nail and hair changes in​ the child. Which of the ‍following mineral toxicit​ies would you suspect?","raw_text":"A farmer from Hoshiarpur, Punjab rushes his 10-year-old boy to the paediatric ER. The father complained of intractable nausea and diarrhoea in the child. On examination, the child has a garlicky odour and is severely dehydrated.  You notice neurologic manifestations along with nail and hair changes in the child. Which of the following mineral toxicities would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selenium‌ toxicity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chromium‌ toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molybdenu‌m toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iodine ‌toxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Selenium toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Selenium toxicity</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of acute <strong>selenium toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Selenium toxicity is characterised by nausea, diarrhoea, neurologic manifestations, nail and hair changes, and garlicky odour. It is commonly seen in areas where drinking water and the collated soil is extremely rich in selenium. In India, selenium toxicity is endemic to agricultural sites located in the states of <strong>Punjab and Haryana. </strong>The presentations are usually acute in nature rather than chronic. </p>\n<p><strong>Chromium toxicity</strong> (Option - B) is commonly seen only in adults with occupational exposure. Chromium is a <strong>pulmonary irritant</strong> and the symptoms are predominantly respiratory complaints similar to <strong>bronchitis</strong>. They also cause chronic <strong>allergic dermatitis</strong> and is <strong>carcinogenic</strong> too. </p>\n<p><strong>Molybdenum toxicity </strong>(Option C) is very rarely seen in humans. It manifests usually as <strong>acute diarrhoea</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Iodine toxicity </strong>(Option D) can range from <strong>mild GI illness</strong> to chronic thyroid dysfunction varying from <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong> to papillary thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0469","text":"A 2-month-old infant is br‌ought to your OPD. The mo​ther complains that the c‌hild refuses to feed and ‍is not gaining adequate w​eight. She has recently n​oticed changes in her hai‍r. On examination, the ha​ir seems to resemble stee​l to wood. Labs reveal lo‌w serum copper levels. Wh‍at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old infant is brought to your OPD. The mother complains that the child refuses to feed and is not gaining adequate weight. She has recently noticed changes in her hair.  On examination, the hair seems to resemble steel to wood. Labs reveal low serum copper levels. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"De Vivo‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Menkes ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Keshan ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kashin be‌ck disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Menkes disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8213fa77486a45a6b4e7605bacf9d043x478x497.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d59c43c665e946949c8a54350a9e11d0x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Menkes disease</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of<strong> Menkes disease</strong> or Tricholipodystrophy or Steely hair disease caused by <strong>copper deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X‐linked recessive disorder</strong> caused by a mutation in <strong>ATP7A</strong> which encodes for a copper-transporting ATPase‐α polypeptide, leading to the <strong>disruption of copper transport</strong> in the intestine.</p>\n<p>The clinical features include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Failure to thrive</strong> (<strong>First</strong> presenting complaint)</li>\n<li>Neurological degeneration (myeloneuropathy)</li>\n<li>Infections</li>\n<li>Hair abnormalities (kinky hair with pili troti seen on a Trichogram).</li>\n<li>These infants have a characteristic <strong>cherubic appearance</strong> with a depressed nasal bridge, pudgy cheeks, ‘<strong>cupids bow’ upper lip</strong>, and doughy skin.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A therapeutic trial with <strong>copper-histidinase</strong> may be helpful.</p>\n<p>Option A: De Vivo disease is a metabolic disorder due to GLUT1 deficiency.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy) and Kashin-beck disease (endemic osteochondropathy) are caused by selenium deficiency.</p>\n<p>The image attached below shows the cherubic appearance of the child with kinky hair. The subsequent picture attached shows pili troti seen under the trichogram.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5727","text":"The anxious mother of a 5-‌month-old baby informs yo​u that the baby incessant‌ly cries on touching or l‍ifting. The baby is comfo​rtable when it is made to​ lie down. Diet history i‍s significant for the chi​ld being fed exclusively ​on cow’s milk. What could‌ be the probable cause?","raw_text":"The anxious mother of a 5-month-old baby informs you that the baby incessantly cries on touching or lifting. The baby is comfortable when it is made to lie down. Diet history is significant for the child being fed exclusively on cow’s milk. What could be the probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"J‌RA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ric‌kets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caffey’s‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scurvy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scurvy</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>scurvy. </strong>A child who presents with a cry on touching or lifting is usually due to <strong>subperiosteal haemorrhages</strong>. </p>\n<p>Scurvy/<strong>Barlow’s Disease </strong>is caused due to the <strong>deficiency of vitamin c</strong>, especially in children fed predominantly<strong> on heat-treated</strong><strong> cow’s milk. </strong>High temperature destroys vitamin C.</p>\n<p>JRA - Juvenile Rheumatoid arthritis (Option B) rarely presents in infancy.</p>\n<p>Rickets (Option C) presents with growth retardation or other features such as <strong>craniotabes</strong>, <strong>craniosynostosis</strong> in infancy. It is generally associated with individuals who have a <strong>milk deficient diet. </strong></p>\n<p>Caffey’s disease (Option D) is <strong>infantile cortical hyperostosis</strong>. It usually presents at 6 months of age with painful soft tissue swelling and bone thickening. The mandible is the most common bone involved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1706","text":"A child is born with the f‌ollowing abnormality. Wha​t advice do you give to t‌he mother, with regards t‍o the next pregnancy?","raw_text":"A child is born with the following abnormality. What advice do you give to the mother, with regards to the next pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 mcg of folic acid perico‌nceptionally","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"400 mcg of folic acid peri‌conceptionally","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"400 mcg of folic acid afte‌r confirmation of pregnan​cy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4000 mcg of folic acid per‌iconceptionally","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4000 mcg of folic acid periconceptionally","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae1f9a95a117478d8875e2e5b8510462.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4000 mcg of folic acid periconceptionally</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>spina bifida</strong>, the most common neural tube defect (NTD). <strong>4000 mcg of folic acid periconceptionally </strong>is given to prevent NTDs in subsequent pregnancies.</p>\n<p>The <strong>normal dose</strong> of folic acid in pregnancy is <strong>400 mcg</strong> (0.4 mg). However, the history of a <strong>previous pregnancy </strong>with an <strong>NTD</strong> raises the risk of another NTD in subsequent pregnancies. An increased dose of folic acid is thus prophylactically given during the periconceptional period to prevent NTD in the next child.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1723","text":"A 10-year-old girl present‌ed with the triad of diar​rhoea, dermatitis, and de‌layed mental development.‍ The child had neutral am​inoaciduria and a positiv​e Obermeyer test. Which o‍f the following statement​ is false regarding the v​itamin deficient in this ‌child?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl presented with the triad of diarrhoea, dermatitis, and delayed mental development. The child had neutral aminoaciduria and a positive Obermeyer test. Which of the following statement is false regarding the vitamin deficient in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoid syndrome can cau‌se secondary deficiency o​f this vitamin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine is ‌its precursor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It decreases the levels of‌ lipoprotein (a)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most fatal complication wi‌th high dose of this vita​min is fulminant hepatiti‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tyrosine is its precursor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1c484d40b5148baa0164b17f61bf08cx611x437.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tyrosine is its precursor</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>Pellagra </strong>secondary to <strong>Hartnup's disease. </strong>The vitamin deficient in this condition is <strong>niacin.</strong> The precursor of niacin is <strong>tryptophan</strong> and not tyrosine.</p>\n<p>Niacin/vitamin B3 is a constituent of <strong>NAD</strong> and <strong>NADP. </strong>Its deficiency occurs in a maize-based diet (<strong>maize is deficient in tryptophan</strong>), carcinoid syndrome (all the tryptophan is converted to serotonin), and hartnup disease (defective absorption from intestine and kidney).</p>\n<p>Niacin is the <strong>only lipid-lowering agent</strong> that <strong>lowers lipoprotein(a)</strong> levels significantly. It also results in a <strong>decrease in LDL, VLDL, </strong>and<strong> triglycerides</strong> along with an <strong>increase in HDL</strong> cholesterol.</p>\n<p>Niacin deficiency causes pellagra (3Ds)-</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Dermatitis (<strong>Cassal's necklace</strong>)</li>\n<li>Dementia</li>\n<li>Finally death.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Niacin toxicity manifests as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prostaglandin mediated cutaneous flushing</li>\n<li>Glucose intolerance</li>\n<li><strong>Fulminant hepatitis </strong>(<strong>most fatal</strong> complication)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hartnup's disease is due to impaired renal and intestinal absorption of <strong>tryptophan and other neutral amino acids</strong>. It is characterized by a varied clinical presentation including <strong>ataxia</strong> (lack of muscle control), delayed mental development, diarrhoea and dermatitis (<strong>pellagra-like symptoms</strong> as tryptophan is a precursor of niacin). Diagnosis of niacin deficiency secondary to hartnup's disease is based on<strong> neutral aminoaciduria</strong> and increased excretion of indole compounds detected by the <strong>Obermeyer test</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Casal's necklace</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1787","text":"A 7-month-old child was br‌ought by her mother with ​complaints of diarrhoea a‌nd mucocutaneous lesions ‍as shown in the image. Th​e symptoms have been pres​ent since weaning was sta‍rted. What is the diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 7-month-old child was brought by her mother with complaints of diarrhoea and mucocutaneous lesions as shown in the image. The symptoms have been present since weaning was started. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wiskott Aldr‌ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acrodermatitis enteropathi‌ca","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pediatric epidermolysis bu‌llosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atopic d‌ermatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acrodermatitis enteropathica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/18642c468b5c4e039b72d290fdfe85ba.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5f5bc7c5e444bc692890d8d88c2035fx428x521.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acrodermatitis enteropathica</strong></p><p>A child with <strong>eczematous rash</strong> typically in<strong> the perioral</strong> and <strong>perineal</strong> region is characteristic of <strong>acrodermatitis enteropathica</strong> which is due to <strong>secondary zinc deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It is an </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">autosomal recessive</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> disorder seen in infants due to a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">defect in the intestinal zinc-specific transporter</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> <strong>gene</strong></span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Oral zinc therapy</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> is the preferred management.</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It classically presents while </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">weaning</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> from breast milk to formula, which has <strong>lower zinc bioavailability</strong> than breast milk. </span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The symptoms are perioral and perineal rashes, periungual dermatitis- Beau’s lines on nails, chronic diarrhea, and alopecia. Children commonly present with </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">symmetrical, eczematous plaques</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> that become </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">vesicular, bullous, pustular, or erosive</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> with characteristic crusting at the edges. The </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">perioral eruption</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> usually spares the upper lip, giving it a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">U‐shaped or horseshoe‐shaped </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">appearance.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Investigations would reveal low serum zinc</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span>Oral zinc therapy is the treatment of choice (3 mg/kg/24 hour)</p>\n<p>Option A: Wiskott Aldrich syndrome is the classical triad of thrombocytopenia, eczema and recurrent pyogenic infections. No history of bleeding rules out this option.</p>\n<p>Option C: Epidermolysis bullosa presents as bullous lesions in pressure handling areas.</p>\n<p>Option D: In atopic dermatitis usually the lesions will be itchy and distributed in extensor aspects (flexural in adults).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1806","text":"A young school-going girl,‌ a known case of seizure ​disorder controlled with ‌valproic acid, complaints‍ of constant bullying by ​her classmates for her pa​tchy hair loss. Local sca‍lp examination reveals sc​aly dermatitis and hair l​oss with intermittent con‌strictions. Which of the ‍following deficiencies is​ associated with this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A young school-going girl, a known case of seizure disorder controlled with valproic acid, complaints of constant bullying by her classmates for her patchy hair loss. Local scalp examination reveals scaly dermatitis and hair loss with intermittent constrictions. Which of the following deficiencies is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‌in B3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‌min H","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam‌in B6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam‌in B1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin H","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin H</strong></p><p>Biotin (<strong>Vitamin H </strong>or Vitamin B7) deficiency can cause <strong>alopecia</strong>, dermatitis (periorificial), and thinning of hair. It is commonly seen in individuals who are on long term <strong>valproate</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p><strong> Scarring alopecia</strong> with <strong>islands of dense hair</strong> referred to as <strong>intermittent constrictions</strong> is classically seen in biotin deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9227","text":"A 2-day-old neonate is not‌ed to have multifocal-clo​nic seizures. These seizu‌res are unresponsive to p‍harmacologic therapy. Whi​ch vitamin dependency is ​responsible for such seiz‍ures?","raw_text":"A 2-day-old neonate is noted to have multifocal-clonic seizures. These seizures are unresponsive to pharmacologic therapy. Which vitamin dependency is responsible for such seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nia‌cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folic‌ acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyridoxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyridoxine</strong></p><p><strong>Pyridoxine</strong> is one of the causes of <strong>refractory seizures</strong> in the neonatal period and infancy.</p>\n<p>Pyridoxine and pyridoxal dependency disorders can cause severe seizures. These seizures, which are often <strong>multifocal clonic</strong>, usually start during the first few hours of life. It involves a mutation in the <strong>ALDH7A1</strong> gene causing a deficiency of an enzyme involved in the degradation of I-alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1710","text":"Cardiac edema and neuropat‌hy are seen in which of t​he following vitamin defi‌ciencies?","raw_text":"Cardiac edema and neuropathy are seen in which of the following vitamin deficiencies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bio‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ribof‌lavin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thiamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thiamine</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiac edema and neuropathy</strong> are associated with the<strong> deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1)</strong>. </p>\n<p>Early symptoms of thiamine deficiency include <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>,<strong> irritability</strong>,<strong> loss of deep tendon reflexes</strong>, and <strong>proprioception</strong>. As the disease progresses, increased intracranial pressure, meningismus, and coma develop.</p>\n<p><strong>Wet beriberi (cardiac)</strong> is associated with tachycardia, cardiomegaly, cardiac failure, and edema. It is commonly seen in the <strong>refugee camps</strong> where people consume <strong>polished rice </strong>based monotonous diets. Diagnosis of thiamine deficiency can be established by <strong>erythrocyte transketolase</strong> activity assessment.</p>\n<p><strong>Dry beriberi</strong> is associated with <strong>neurological</strong> deficits such as optic atrophy, ataxia, and irritability.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> include lactic acidosis and Wernicke encephalopathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1714","text":"During the routine ophthal‌mic examination of a 6-ye​ar- old child as a part o‌f a school health survey,‍ you notice the following​ finding as shown in the ​image. This is characteri‍stically associated with ​deficiency of _______","raw_text":"During the routine ophthalmic examination of a 6-year- old child as a part of a school health survey, you notice the following finding as shown in the image. This is characteristically associated with deficiency of _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribof‌lavin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bio‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Riboflavin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0e9efb12c2314103b7a4321b069a038a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/437ec077cdb64d2e8e242ab7b05605e3x587x390.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Riboflavin</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>corneal vascularization</strong>, which is characteristic of <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"1\">Riboflavin deficiency</span>. Other features include glossitis, cheilosis, and photophobia collectively called <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"3\"><strong>ariboflavinosis</strong>. </span></p>\n<p>The image below shows<strong> </strong>Cheilosis and glossitis</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TZwOyRQ1","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"10_chromosomal_disorders_and_other_genetic_disorders","title":"10.Chromosomal Disorders And Other Genetic Disorders","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA3616","text":"At what stage of mitosis i​s standard karyotyping pe‍rformed?","raw_text":"At what stage of mitosis is standard karyotyping performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anap​hase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meta​phase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​hase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Telo​phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metaphase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4ca1c2caec9410893fe090ef1824727x572x449.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metaphase</strong></p><p><strong>Standard karyotyping</strong> refers to an <strong>arrangement</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>chromosomes</strong> from a cell that is undergoing <strong>mitosis</strong> at <strong>metaphase</strong>.</p>\n<p>At other mitotic stages, the chromosomes are not sufficiently condensed or are too dispersed to allow for the counting and comparison of pairs under the microscope. In a metaphase spread, the individual chromosomes take the form of<strong> two chromatids</strong> <strong>connected</strong> to the <strong>centromere</strong>.</p>\n<p>A variety of <strong>staining methods</strong> are available to <strong>identify</strong> the <strong>individual chromosomes</strong> on the basis of a distinctive and reliable pattern of alternating light and dark bands. The most common method used involves a<strong> Giemsa stain </strong>and is hence called<strong> G-banding. </strong>Standard G-banding can detect approximately 400 to 800 bands per haploid set.</p>\n<p>The image below shows G-banding (karyogram of human male using Giemsa staining):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3617","text":"In cytogenetic terminology​, how are the following m‍arked structures designat​ed?","raw_text":"In cytogenetic terminology, how are the following marked structures designated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-q,​ 2-s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-p,​ 2-r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-q,​ 2-p","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1- p,​ 2- q","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1- p, 2- q","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e7009b93ac040979e465443e2806d8e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44e2c4f1c5c1406d9ed60689f523a48fx1280x1371.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db0f04eabbcc45159024ec33a2414308x779x625.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1- p, 2- q</strong></p><p>The <strong>short arm</strong> of a chromosome (marked as 1) is designated<strong> p </strong>(for petite), and the <strong>long arm</strong> (marked as 2) is referred to as <strong>q </strong>(the next alphabet).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In a banded karyotype, each arm of the chromosome is divided into two or more regions bordered by <strong>prominent bands</strong>. The regions are <strong>numbered</strong> (e.g., 1, 2, 3) from the centromere outward. Each region is further subdivided into bands and sub-bands, and these are ordered numerically as well.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3618","text":"What aberration occurs whe​n a break occurs at both ‍ends of a chromosome with​ the fusion of the damage‌d ends?","raw_text":"What aberration occurs when a break occurs at both ends of a chromosome with the fusion of the damaged ends?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transl​ocation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ring ch​romosome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inve​rsion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iso-chromoso​me formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ring chromosome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/122ca616937c4a55a4a722783316c334x675x435.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a45f2e4c6dd3433d99c15f9bf0a85578x752x400.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ring chromosome</strong></p><p>A<strong> ring chromosome</strong> is an <strong>aberrant </strong>chromosome whose <strong>broken</strong> <strong>ends </strong><strong>fuse together</strong> to form a ring.</p>\n<p>Ring chromosomes behave <strong>abnormally</strong> in <strong>meiosis</strong> or <strong>mitosis</strong> and usually result in <strong>serious consequences</strong>. In rare cases, the telomeres at the ends of a chromosome fuse without any loss of genetic material, which results in a normal phenotype. A ring chromosome is denoted by the <strong>symbol r</strong> in human genetics.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows a representative karyotype from a well-differentiated transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder. The chromosome indicated by 'r' represents a ring chromosome. Also evident are monosomy 9, 18, and X and trisomy 7.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: In a translocation, a segment of one chromosome is transferred to another.</p>\n<p>Option C: Inversion refers to a rearrangement that involves two breaks within a single chromosome, with reincorporation of the inverted, intervening segment.</p>\n<p>Option D: Isochromosome formation results when one arm of a chromosome is lost and the remaining arm is duplicated. It results in a chromosome consisting of two short arms only or of two long arms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3619","text":"Which of the following is ​not associated with the c‍hange in genetic material​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with the change in genetic material?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inve​rsion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inse​rtion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dele​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transv​ersion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inversion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e79c9a8f4b14fe1a5d670f528387815x729x787.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inversion</strong></p><p>Inversion involves only a <strong>rearrangement</strong> of the <strong>genetic material</strong>. There is <strong>no change</strong> in the genetic material.</p>\n<p>It occurs as a result of <strong>two</strong> <strong>breaks </strong>within a single chromosome. The intervening segment between the breaks gets inverted and reincorporated. It is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Paracentric - </strong>inversion involving only one arm of the chromosome</li>\n<li><strong>Pericentric - </strong>inversion involving opposite sides of the centromere</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inversions are often fully compatible with <strong>normal development</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transversions are mutations where a purine base pair is replaced by a pyrimidine or vice versa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3620","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about Down's sy‍ndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about Down's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukemia is the major caus​e of death in early child‍hood","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is the most common chro​mosomal disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Robertsonian translocation​s of chromosome 21 are le‍ss common","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These children are prone t​o develop autoimmune dise‍ases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leukemia is the major cause of death in early childhood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5e53d4a737a4de6aab871d7239e35bfx1280x1509.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leukemia is the major cause of death in early childhood</strong></p><p>In<strong> Down's syndrome</strong>, the major cause of <strong>death</strong> in <strong>infancy</strong> and <strong>early childhood</strong> is <strong>cardiac disease</strong>, not leukemia.</p>\n<p>Down's syndrome is the <strong>most common chromosomal</strong> disorder and is a major cause of mental retardation. <strong>Maternal</strong> <strong>age </strong>is a strong risk factor. This condition can occur by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Majority of cases - <strong> meiotic nondisjunction </strong>of chromosome 21 in the ovum</li>\n<li><strong>Rarely</strong> -<strong> Robertsonian translocation</strong> of the long arm of chromosome 21 to another acrocentric chromosome (e.g., 22 or 14) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical features of Down's syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mental retardation</li>\n<li>Facial dysmorphism - flat facial profile, oblique palpebral fissures, and epicanthic folds</li>\n<li>Congenital heart disease -<strong> </strong>endocardial cushion defects (ASD, VSD, AV valve malformations)</li>\n<li>Atresias of the esophagus and small bowel</li>\n<li>Neuropathologic changes characteristic of Alzheimer's disease - in patients &gt; 40 years</li>\n<li>Increased risk of developing acute megakaryoblastic leukemia (AML-M7)</li>\n<li>Predisposition to <strong>autoimmune</strong> <strong>diseases</strong> and serious lung <strong>infections</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3621","text":"A 1-year-old child was bro​ught by the mother with c‍omplaints of delay in rea​ching motor milestones. E‌xamination revealed obliq‍ue palpebral fissures, ep‌icanthal folds, and the f​ollowing finding. Auscult‍ation revealed a pan-syst​olic murmur in the left s​ternal border. This child‍ is at risk of developing​ which of the following?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child was brought by the mother with complaints of delay in reaching motor milestones. Examination revealed oblique palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, and the following finding. Auscultation revealed a pan-systolic murmur in the left sternal border. This child is at risk of developing which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute monoblastic leukemia​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute promyelocytic leukem​ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myelomonocytic leuke​mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute megakaryoblastic leu​kemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute megakaryoblastic leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e7a0eb2b9c804dccbf4543557291d0f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute megakaryoblastic leukemia</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing a single palmar crease (<strong>simian crease</strong>) are suggestive of <strong>Down's</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>. It is associated with an increased risk of <strong>acute megakaryoblastic leukemia</strong> (<strong>AML-M7</strong>) in affected children <strong>&lt; 2 years</strong> old. </p>\n<p>Children with trisomy 21 have a 10 to 20-fold increased risk of developing acute leukemia. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>In children <strong>&lt; 2 years</strong> of age - acute megakaryoblastic leukemia(<strong>AML-M7</strong>) is the most common</li>\n<li>In children <strong>&gt; 3 years</strong> of age - acute lymphoblastic leukemia (<strong>ALL</strong>) is the most predominant </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3623","text":"A 7-month-old infant is be​ing evaluated for failure‍ to thrive. Examination r​eveals low-set ears, a sh‌ort neck, and the followi‍ng findings. Auscultation‌ reveals a holosystolic m​urmur over the pulmonic a‍rea. Which of the followi​ng statements about this ​condition is false?","raw_text":"A 7-month-old infant is being evaluated for failure to thrive. Examination reveals low-set ears, a short neck, and the following findings. Auscultation reveals a holosystolic murmur over the pulmonic area. Which of the following statements about this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence is 1 in 8000 bir​ths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Karyotype of this child is​ 47, XY + 18","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It may be associated with ​horseshoe kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is increased risk of​ leukemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. There is increased risk of leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8b5bc10b22714fac8ad634d907abcca6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. There is increased risk of leukemia</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>images show the index finger <strong>overlapping</strong> other<strong> fingers</strong> and <strong>rocker bottom feet</strong>. This along with the clinical features is suggestive of <strong>Edward's </strong>syndrome. Leukemia is not a feature of this condition.</p>\n<p>It is due to the inheritance of a complete<strong> extra copy</strong> of <strong>chromosome 18</strong> as a result of meiotic nondisjunction (<strong>47, XY+18</strong>). The incidence is <strong>1 in 8000</strong> births.</p>\n<p>The clinical features of Edward's syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low-set ears</li>\n<li>Short neck</li>\n<li>Overlapping fingers</li>\n<li>Congenital heart defects</li>\n<li><strong>Renal malformations</strong></li>\n<li>Limited hip abduction</li>\n<li>Rocker-bottom feet</li>\n<li>Prominent occiput</li>\n<li>Mental retardation</li>\n<li>Micrognathia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Although, <strong>rocker bottom feet</strong> are seen in <strong>both</strong> Edward's syndrome and Patau's syndrome, <strong>overlapping of fingers</strong> is characteristic of <strong>Edward's</strong> syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3624","text":"Which of the following is ​not seen in children with‍ Patau syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in children with Patau syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cleft ​palate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilica​l hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocker-bo​ttom feet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High-s​et ears","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High-set ears","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/117bda4f0ad042d2bf42b36af9dcb1d9x1280x1737.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High-set ears</strong></p><p><strong>Low set ears</strong> are a feature of <strong>Patau</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>, not high set ears.</p>\n<p>Patau syndrome occurs due to <strong>trisomy 13 </strong>and<strong> </strong>is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microphthalmia</li>\n<li>Polydactyly</li>\n<li>Microcephaly and mental retardation</li>\n<li><strong>Cleft</strong> <strong>lip</strong> and <strong>palate</strong></li>\n<li>Cardiac defects</li>\n<li><strong>Umbilical hernia</strong></li>\n<li>Renal defects</li>\n<li><strong>Rocker-bottom feet</strong> (commonly seen in Edwards syndrome also)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3625","text":"What hypothesis states tha​t one X chromosome, mater‍nal or paternal, is rando​mly inactivated during de‌velopment?","raw_text":"What hypothesis states that one X chromosome, maternal or paternal, is randomly inactivated during development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ly​on","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ha​rdy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Max​well","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ba​rr","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lyon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lyon</strong></p><p><strong>Lyon's hypothesis</strong> states the idea of the <strong>inactivation </strong>of the <strong>X chromosome</strong>.</p>\n<p>It states the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only one of the X chromosomes is genetically active</li>\n<li>The other X undergoes heteropyknosis and is rendered inactive (known as <strong>Barr body</strong>)</li>\n<li>Inactivation of either the maternal or paternal X occurs at <strong>random</strong> among all the cells of the <strong>blastocyst</strong> on or about day 5.5 of embryonic life</li>\n<li>Inactivation of the same X chromosome persists in all the cells derived from each precursor cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 173\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The molecular basis of X inactivation involves the <strong>XIST</strong> gene.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3626","text":"A 25-year-old man was refe​rred for infertility eval‍uation. On examination, h​e was tall with a lack of‌ body hair. Gynecomastia ‍and micropenis were also ‌noted. Karyotyping analys​is revealed an XXY karyot‍ype. Which of the followi​ng statements is not true​ about the condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man was referred for infertility evaluation. On examination, he was tall with a lack of body hair. Gynecomastia and micropenis were also noted. Karyotyping analysis revealed an XXY karyotype. Which of the following statements is not true about the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levels of follicle-stimula​ting hormone are reduced","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Testosterone levels are re​duced","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma estradiol levels ar​e elevated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Risk of SLE ​is increased","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Levels of follicle-stimulating hormone are reduced","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Levels of follicle-stimulating hormone are reduced</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Klinefelter's syndrome.</strong> In this condition, the follicle-stimulating hormone (<strong>FSH</strong>) <strong>levels</strong> are <strong>elevated. </strong></p>\n<p>The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eunuchoid body habitus</strong> - elongated body and <strong>long legs</strong> (increased length between the soles and the pubic bone)</li>\n<li>Absence of <strong>secondary sexual characteristics </strong>- lack of body and facial hair, deep voice</li>\n<li>Small atrophic testes</li>\n<li>Micropenis</li>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n<li>Lower IQ, but no mental retardation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following hormonal variations are seen in these patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated FSH</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated estradiol</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Low testosterone</strong></li>\n<li>Insulin resistance - increased incidence of type 2 diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with Klinefelter syndrome are at a higher risk for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Male breast cancer (20x more common than in normal males)</li>\n<li>Extragonadal germ cell tumors</li>\n<li>Autoimmune diseases - <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3630","text":"A 16-year-old girl is refe​rred for evaluation of pr‍imary amenorrhea and shor​t stature. Examination sh‌ows a webbed neck, widely‍ spaced nipples, and abse‌nt secondary sexual chara​cteristics. USG abdomen s‍hows a small uterus and s​treak ovaries. What is th​e most common karyotypic ‍abnormality seen in indiv​iduals with this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl is referred for evaluation of primary amenorrhea and short stature. Examination shows a webbed neck, widely spaced nipples, and absent secondary sexual characteristics. USG abdomen shows a small uterus and streak ovaries. What is the most common karyotypic abnormality seen in individuals with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Missing entire X chromosom​e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isochromosome of the long ​arm of X chromosome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deletion of portions of lo​ng arm of X chromosome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deletion of ends of long a​nd short arm of X chromos‍ome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Missing entire X chromosome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Missing entire X chromosome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of <strong>Turner's syndrome</strong>. In this condition, the most common karyotypic abnormality seen is a <strong>missing entire X chromosome</strong>, seen in more than half the cases.</p>\n<p>Less commonly, structural abnormalities of the X chromosome and mosaicism can also occur. The common feature of the structural abnormalities is to produce <strong>partial monosomy</strong> of the X chromosome.</p>\n<p>In order of frequency, the <strong>structural abnormalities</strong> of the X chromosome include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>isochromosome</strong> of the long arm, 46,X,i(X)(q10), resulting in the loss of the short arm</li>\n<li>Deletion of portions of both long and short arms, resulting in the formation of a <strong>ring chromosome </strong>- 46, X,r(X)</li>\n<li><strong>Deletion</strong> of portions of the<strong> short or long</strong> arm - 46X,del(Xq) or 46X,del(Xp).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>mosaic</strong> patients have a 45,XO cell population along with one or more karyotypically normal or abnormal cell types.</p>\n<p>Few patients with this condition also have<strong> Y-chromosome</strong> sequences, either as a complete Y chromosome or as fragments of Y chromosomes translocated on other chromosomes. These patients are at a <strong>higher risk</strong> for the development of a gonadal tumor (<strong>gonadoblastoma</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3632","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is not caused by ‍trinucleotide-repeat muta​tions involving non-codin‌g regions?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not caused by trinucleotide-repeat mutations involving non-coding regions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Huntingto​n disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fragile X​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myotonic ​dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Friedrei​ch ataxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Huntington disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Huntington disease</strong></p><p><strong>Huntington's disease</strong> is due to <strong>CAG (trinucleotide)</strong> repeat mutation in the <strong>coding</strong> <strong>region</strong> of the gene, not the non-coding region. </p>\n<p>Expansion of trinucleotide repeats is an important genetic cause of human disease, particularly <strong>neurodegenerative disorders</strong>. The causative mutations are associated with the expansion of a stretch of trinucleotides that usually share the <strong>nucleotides</strong> <strong>G</strong> and <strong>C</strong>. In all cases, the<strong> DNA</strong> is <strong>unstable</strong>, and an expansion of the repeats above a certain threshold <strong>impairs gene function</strong>.</p>\n<p>Expansions affecting <strong>noncoding</strong> <strong>regions</strong> will cause a <strong>loss of function</strong> mutation. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fragile X syndrome</li>\n<li>Myotonic dystrophy</li>\n<li>Friedreich ataxia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Expansions affecting<strong> coding</strong> regions cause a <strong>gain of function</strong> mutation. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spinobulbar muscular atrophy (Kennedy disease)</li>\n<li>Huntington disease</li>\n<li>Dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy (Haw–River syndrome)</li>\n<li>Spinocerebellar ataxia types 1, 2, 3, 6, 7</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2219","text":"All of the following are f​eatures of fragile X synd‍rome except:","raw_text":"All of the following are features of fragile X syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trinucleotide mutation in ​FMR-1 gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental re​tardation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Micro-o​rchidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Large eve​rted ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Micro-orchidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2cdcc3daf714c8da0f43b5bb80e9a09x720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Micro-orchidism</strong></p><p><strong>Macro-orchidism</strong> (large testicles) are seen in <strong>fragile X syndrome</strong>, not micro-orchidism.</p>\n<p>Fragile X syndrome is the second most common genetic cause of mental retardation (Down syndrome being the most common). It is caused by a <strong>trinucleotide mutation, </strong>characterised by multiple tandem repeats of the <strong>CGG</strong> sequence in the familial mental retardation-1 (<strong>FMR1</strong>)<strong> gene</strong>. It is associated with the phenomenon of <strong>anticipation,</strong> where the <strong>disease phenotype worsens</strong> over <strong>generations</strong> within a family.</p>\n<p>The affected males are <strong>mentally retarded</strong>, with an <strong>IQ</strong> in the range of <strong>20 to 60</strong>.</p>\n<p>The characteristic phenotypic features include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Long face</strong> with a <strong>large mandible</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Large</strong> everted <strong>ears</strong></li>\n<li>Macro-orchidism</li>\n<li>Hyperextensible joints</li>\n<li>High arched palate</li>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows a child suffering from fragile X syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2220","text":"70 CGG premutations in the​ FMR1 gene are characteri‍stic of which of the foll​owing?","raw_text":"70 CGG premutations in the FMR1 gene are characteristic of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fragile X​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prader Wil​li syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fragile ​X ataxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Friedrei​ch ataxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fragile X ataxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fragile X ataxia</strong></p><p><strong>CGG premutations</strong> in the <strong>FMR1 </strong>gene are characteristic of <strong>fragile X ataxia.</strong> It is<strong> </strong>also known as fragile X <strong>tremor</strong> and fragile X-associated primary <strong>ovarian failure</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Normally</strong>, the FMR1 gene is methylated and silenced, and the number of <strong>CGG repeats</strong> remains <strong>&lt; 55</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, abnormalities in this gene lead to the accumulation of CGG-containing <strong>FMR1 mRNAs</strong>, which are <strong>toxic</strong> to the cell. Depending on the type of mutation, there are 2 distinct syndromes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fragile X ataxia syndrome</strong> (FXTAS): <strong>55-200</strong> repeats (<strong>premutation</strong>) which causes gain of function</li>\n<li><strong>Fragile X syndrome </strong>(FXs): <strong>200- 4000</strong> repeats (<strong>full mutation</strong>) leading to loss of function</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More than half the individuals with FXTAS<strong> </strong>develop a progressive <strong>neurodegenerative syndrome</strong> starting in their 6th decade. It is characterized by intention tremors and cerebellar ataxia and may progress to Parkinsonism.</p>\n<p>Option B: Prader-Willi syndrome is associated with deletion of paternal chromosome 15q12 and the silencing of the maternal allele.</p>\n<p>Option D: Friedreich ataxia is due to GAA repeat sequences in the noncoding regions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3634","text":"What is the mode of inheri​tance of Leber hereditary‍ optic neuropathy?","raw_text":"What is the mode of inheritance of Leber hereditary optic neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/efa3c3ccf2ca4e4bb22b34ddcb27ef39.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0f0e152c3234dc195ebb67cdef527fbx1280x1243.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p><strong>Leber hereditary optic neuropathy</strong> (LHON) is an example of <strong>mitochondrial inheritance</strong>. It is inherited along the <strong>maternal line</strong>, as shown in image 4.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">In diseases following the mitochondrial pattern of inheritance, <strong>all children</strong> of an <strong>affected </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>mother</strong> and normal father develop the <strong>disease</strong>. </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">However, children born to an <strong>affected father</strong> and a normal mother <strong>do not inherit</strong> the disease. Hence, only the daughters of the affected mother will transmit the disease.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">This is because this condition is due to a mutation in the <strong>mitochondrial DNA</strong> (mtDNA), which is derived from the maternal mitochondria. The ovum contains several mitochondria, while the spermatozoa have very few mitochondria if present. Hence, the mitochondrial DNA of the zygote is <strong>entirely</strong> <strong>derived</strong> from the <strong>ovum</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>LHON is a neurodegenerative disease that manifests as<strong> progressive bilateral loss of central vision. </strong>Visual impairment starts between ages 15 and 35, leading eventually to blindness. Other features include cardiac conduction defects and minor neurologic manifestations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2479","text":"Which chromosome is involv​ed in myotonic dystrophy?‍","raw_text":"Which chromosome is involved in myotonic dystrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromo​some 19","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chromo​some 20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromo​some 21","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromo​some 22","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chromosome 19","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chromosome 19</strong></p><p class=\"p2\">Chromosome <strong>19 </strong>is involved in<strong> myotonic dystrophy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0158","text":"Maltese cross crystals in ​the urine are seen in whi‍ch of the following disea​ses other than nephrotic ‌syndrome?","raw_text":"Maltese cross crystals in the urine are seen in which of the following diseases other than nephrotic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Friedrich​'s ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Felty s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fanconi​ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fabry's​ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fabry's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/335748b8feae48299ee5f862bbc7d3f9x430x287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fabry&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Maltese cross crystals</strong> in the urine are seen in <strong>Fabry's disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>Urine analysis in Fabry’s disease may reveal <strong>oval fat bodies</strong> and <strong>birefringent glycolipid globules</strong> under polarized light showing a Maltese cross appearance. It can also be seen in <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows Maltese cross casts:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Maltese cross appearance</strong> is also seen in <strong>short-range shotgun wounds</strong> and <strong>babesiosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3628","text":"A young male patient as sh​own below presents with c‍omplaints of infertility.​ On examination, he lacke‌d pubic and facial hair. ‍The genital examination w‌as consistent for a micro​penis. Further investigat‍ions reveal elevated LH a​nd FSH levels. What is th​e most common cardiac les‍ion associated with this ​condition?","raw_text":"A young male patient as shown below presents with complaints of infertility. On examination, he lacked pubic and facial hair. The genital examination was consistent for a micropenis. Further investigations reveal elevated LH and FSH levels. What is the most common cardiac lesion associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral val​ve prolapse","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral reg​urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patent ductu​s arteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitral valve prolapse","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c043a2b208ce4bcf80af9db7cf21e820.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitral valve prolapse</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture most likely suggests<strong> Klinefelter's syndrome</strong>. The most common cardiac lesion in this condition is <strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong> which is seen in nearly half the adults. </p>\n<p><strong>Systemic manifestations</strong> associated with Klinefelter's syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n<li>Type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome</li>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis and fractures</li>\n<li>Male infertility</li>\n<li>Higher risk for breast cancer, extragonadal germ-cell tumors</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3871","text":"Which of the following is ​the correct statement reg‍arding Huntington's chore​a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the correct statement regarding Huntington's chorea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a trinucleotide repe​at disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"There is loss of function ​type of mutation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There are abnormal repeats​ of CUG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormality is seen due to​ mutation in chromosome 6‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a trinucleotide repeat disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a trinucleotide repeat disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Huntington's chorea</strong> is a<strong> trinucleotide repeat </strong>disorder.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> neurodegenerative disorder. It is caused by <strong>CAG </strong>trinucleotide<strong> repeats</strong> (&gt;40). It occurs due to a <strong>gain-of-function</strong> (expansion) mutation in the huntingtin gene on <strong>chromosome 4p</strong>. </p>\n<p>The disease shows <strong>genetic anticipation</strong> and has an early-onset (2-3 decades) if inherited from the father. </p>\n<p><strong>Cognitive impairment</strong> is an early feature. Pathologically, <strong>atrophy</strong> of <strong>caudate</strong> and <strong>putamen</strong> with ex vacuo ventriculomegaly is seen. Decreased acetylcholine leads to <strong>dementia</strong> and decreased GABA and increased dopamine lead to <strong>chorea.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> consists of <strong>haloperidol</strong> and<strong> tetrabenazine.</strong></p>\n<p>Trinucleotide repeat disorders:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 697px; width: 401px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Repeats</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 18px;\">Clinical features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 91px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 91px;\"><strong>Fragile X- syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 91px;\">  <strong>CGG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 91px;\">Mental retardation, large ears, and jaws, macroorchidism in males</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 107.2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 107.2px;\">\n<p>Myotonic Dystrophy</p>\n<p>(<strong>chromosome 19)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 107.2px;\">  CTG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 107.2px;\">Muscle loss, cardiac arrhythmia,<br/><strong>cataracts</strong>, frontal balding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Huntington’s disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 36px;\">  <strong>CAG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 36px;\">Affective disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 72px;\">Spinocerebellar ataxia type 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 72px;\">  CAG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 72px;\">Progressive ataxia, dysarthria,<br/>dysmetria.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 91px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 91px;\"><strong>Friedreich's ataxia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px; height: 91px;\">  <strong>GAA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 91px;\">Progressive ataxia,<br/>dysarthria, hypertrophic<br/>cardiomyopathy.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7333","text":"Which amongst the followin​g is true about Turner's ‍syndrome?","raw_text":"Which amongst the following is true about Turner's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Webbing of hands and toes ​are associated with risk ‍of visceral anomalies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noonan syndrome is more as​sociated with cardiac def‍ects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of neck webbing is​ associated with higher r‍isk of co-arctation of ao​rta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Webbing of neck is associa​ted with higher risk of c‍ardiac anomalies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Webbing of neck is associated with higher risk of cardiac anomalies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b2b847da78643bd8162a14e2499a9d0x1280x1730.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc42f5d3b80141a39a80f0096919f7d3x720x540.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Webbing of neck is associated with higher risk of cardiac anomalies</strong></p><p>In <strong>Turner's</strong> syndrome, <strong>webbing</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong> is associated with a <strong>higher risk</strong> of <strong>cardiac anomalies</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below summarises the clinical features of Turner's syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the webbing of neck.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Noonan syndrome is characterised by mutations in the <strong>RAS</strong>-<strong>MAPK</strong> pathway. The most common of these is the <strong>PTPN11</strong> which encodes a protein tyrosine phosphatase on <strong>chromosome</strong> <strong>12.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short stature</li>\n<li>Short webbed neck</li>\n<li>Cystic hygroma</li>\n<li>Tall forehead</li>\n<li>Low posterior hairline</li>\n<li>Dental malocclusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Although it shares many clinical features with Turner’s syndrome, Noonan syndrome affects <strong>both</strong> <strong>sexes</strong>. It follows an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> pattern and it also has a different pattern of <strong>congenital heart</strong> <strong>disease</strong>, typically involving <strong>right-sided</strong> <strong>lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9926","text":"A 5-year-old girl is broug​ht for evaluation of inte‍llectual disability. She ​also has difficulty in wa‌lking and balancing, and ‍her speech is delayed for‌ her age. The parents als​o say that she frequently‍ has sudden bouts of laug​hter. She is a known case​ of seizure disorder sinc‍e the age of 3. This cond​ition is associated with ‌which of the following ch‌romosomal abnormalities? ​Deletion of paternal gene​ on chromosome 15 Deletio​n of maternal gene on chr​omosome 15 Uniparental di‌somy of paternal chromoso​me 15 Uniparental disomy ​of maternal chromosome 15‍","raw_text":"<p>A 5-year-old girl is brought for evaluation of intellectual disability. She also has difficulty in walking and balancing, and her speech is delayed for her age. The parents also say that she frequently has sudden bouts of laughter. She is a known case of seizure disorder since the age of 3. This condition is associated with which of the following chromosomal abnormalities?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Deletion of paternal gene on chromosome 15</li>\n<li>Deletion of maternal gene on chromosome 15</li>\n<li>Uniparental disomy of paternal chromosome 15</li>\n<li>Uniparental disomy of maternal chromosome 15</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,​ 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2,​ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​ 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,​ 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebac4096a86044e3856666dae02a24e7x572x903.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a4ff02f3741427592316cb005044bbcx587x809.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/144b4a131a104afaa2d7f1f19c2bcf27x938x998.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 3</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Angelman syndrome.</strong> It can occur due to one of the following genetic abnormalities:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deletion </strong>(most common) - band q12 on the long arm of <strong>maternal</strong> <strong>chromosome</strong> <strong>15</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Paternal</strong> <strong>uniparental disomy</strong> - inheritance of <strong>both chromosomes</strong> of a pair from the paternal pair of chromosomes </li>\n<li>Defective imprinting - defect in the resetting/maintaining of imprints during gametogenesis or after fertilization</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 512px; height: 556px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.336px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.664px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>P</strong>rader-Willi syndrome</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"> Angel<strong>M</strong>an syndrome</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.336px; height: 21px;\">\n<p><strong>Deletion </strong></p>\n<p>(most common)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.664px; height: 21px;\">\n<p><strong>P</strong>aternal chromosome 15</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 21px;\">\n<p><strong>M</strong>aternal chromosome 15</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.336px; height: 66px;\">\n<p><strong>Uniparental disomy </strong></p>\n<p>(less common)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.664px; height: 66px;\"><strong>Maternal disomy</strong> (duplication) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 66px;\"><strong>Paternal disomy</strong> (duplication) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.336px; height: 12px;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.664px; height: 12px;\">\n<p>Elevated ghrelin levels - hyperphagia</p>\n<p>Truncal obesity</p>\n<p>Severe hypotonia</p>\n<p>Short stature</p>\n<p>Small hands and feet with hypotonia</p>\n<p>Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</p>\n<p>Cognitive impairment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 12px;\">\n<p>Short stature</p>\n<p>Severe <strong>mental retardation</strong> with lack of speech</p>\n<p>Stiff arm movements</p>\n<p>Spastic, uncoordinated walk (<strong>ataxia</strong>)</p>\n<p><strong>Seizures</strong></p>\n<p>Inappropriate outbursts of laughter (<strong>happy puppets</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Chromosome 15 diseases<strong> </strong>(mnemonic - MAP):<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong>: <strong>M</strong>arfan syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>: <strong>A</strong>ngelman syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>: <strong>P</strong>rader Willi syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following images show children suffering from Prader-Willi syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows a child suffering from Angelman syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"bpb8k0JY","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"10_anti_anginal_drugs","title":"10.Anti Anginal Drugs","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA1429","text":"A 50-year-old male was rus‌hed to the ER with compla​ints of chest pain for th​e past 45 minutes and was‌ started on isosorbide mo‍nonitrate. How does this ​drug act?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male was rushed to the ER with complaints of chest pain for the past 45 minutes and was started on isosorbide mononitrate. How does this drug act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"By decrea‌sing cAMP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"By increa‌sing cAMP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"By decrea‌sing cGMP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"By increa‌sing cGMP","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. By increasing cGMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eac61aac9888456281f1d360de304834x1280x1768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. By increasing cGMP</strong></p><p><strong>Nitrates</strong> like isosorbide mononitrate act by <strong>increasing </strong>the concentration of the second messenger<strong> cGMP.</strong></p>\n<p>They are used to relieve angina and cause vasodilation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0934","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not used in angina?​","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not used in angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fas‌udil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sacub‌itril","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aten‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amlod‌ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sacubitril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sacubitril</strong></p><p><strong>Sacubitril</strong> is not used in angina. Sacubitril is a <strong>neprilysin</strong> <strong>inhibitor</strong>, approved for use in the treatment of<strong> heart failure</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9289","text":"A patient with Prinzmetal ‌angina is administered is​osorbide mononitrate and ​is noticed to have sympto‌matic relief. What is the‍ mechanism of action of n​itrate in this case?","raw_text":"A patient with Prinzmetal angina is administered isosorbide mononitrate and is noticed to have symptomatic relief. What is the mechanism of action of nitrate in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endothelium independent co‌ronary vasodilation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced cardiac contractil‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased left ventricular‌ end diastolic volume","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased diastolic perfus‌ion pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endothelium independent coronary vasodilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endothelium independent coronary vasodilation</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of nitrates in prinzmetal angina is <strong>endothelium-independent coronary vasodilatation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prinzmetal angina </strong>occurs due to <strong>vasospasm</strong> of the coronary arteries. <strong>Nitrates</strong> relax the smooth muscles of epicardial coronary arteries and <strong>relieve coronary artery spasm.</strong></p>\n<p>In the case of <strong>stable angina</strong>, the primary mechanism of action of <strong>nitrates</strong> is <strong>preferential dilatation of veins</strong> (by increasing cGMP). Venous dilatation leads to a <strong>decrease</strong> in the venous return to the heart (<strong>preload</strong>). This decreases the ventricular end-diastolic volume and thereby a <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>myocardial oxygen consumption</strong>. The diastolic perfusion pressure (aortic blood pressure in diastole - left ventricular pressure in diastole) is increased.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1431","text":"A 45-year-old patient who ‌suffers from prinzmetal a​ngina requires a formulat​ion of nitroglycerine whi‌ch has the most rapid ons‍et of action. Which of th​e following formulations ‌of nitroglycerin can be a​dministered to the patien​t?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient who suffers from prinzmetal angina requires a formulation of nitroglycerine which has the most rapid onset of action. Which of the following formulations of nitroglycerin can be administered to the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sublingu‌al spray","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sublingu‌al tablet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transder‌mal patch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topical ‌ointment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sublingual spray","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sublingual spray</strong></p><p>The <strong>sublingual</strong> route of administration has a <strong>very rapid onset</strong> of action (1-3 mins). The onset<strong> </strong>of action is<strong> more rapid</strong> when administered as a<strong> spray </strong>when compared to a tablet. </p>\n<p>The sublingual route is used for the termination of attacks. Oral extended-release capsule and <strong>transdermal patch</strong> are used for <strong>prophylaxis</strong>. The onset of action is approximately ≥60 min.</p>\n<p>Topical <strong>nitroglycerine ointment</strong> can be used to provide symptomatic relief in patients with <strong>anal fissures</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4585","text":"In patients with angina, n‌itroglycerine should not ​be administered by which ​of the following routes?","raw_text":"In patients with angina, nitroglycerine should not be administered by which of the following routes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‌al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subli‌ngual","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intram‌uscular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intra‌venous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intramuscular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intramuscular</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Intramuscular nitroglycerin</strong> should be avoided due to <strong>severe pain</strong> in the injection area in patients with angina.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sublingual and intravenous nitroglycerin can be used for acute effects. Oral extended-release capsules are available for prophylactic use in angina. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1435","text":"A 30-year-old man suspecte‌d to have cyanide poisoni​ng is brought to the ER. ​The medical officer stabi‌lizes the patient and ask‍s the nurse to bring an a​mpoule of amyl nitrite. B‌y which of the following ​routes is this drug admin​istered?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man suspected to have cyanide poisoning is brought to the ER. The medical officer stabilizes the patient and asks the nurse to bring an ampoule of amyl nitrite. By which of the following routes is this drug administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‌al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhala‌tional","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intra‌venous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subli‌ngual","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhalational","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhalational</strong></p><p><strong>Amyl nitrite</strong> is administered via the<strong> inhalational route</strong> for acute relief in angina and <strong>cyanide poisoning</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Amyl nitrite </strong>is a<strong> highly volatile liquid </strong>available in glass ampoules. Its unpleasant odor and very short duration of action have made its <strong>use obsolete in angina.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1436","text":"An 18-year-old boy was bro‌ught to the ER in an unco​nscious state. His cowork​er states he works in a f‌actory manufacturing pest‍icides. Based on this, th​e medical officer suspect‌s cyanide poisoning and a​dministers sodium nitrite​. Which of the following ‌is the mechanism of actio‌n of this drug in this ca‍se?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy was brought to the ER in an unconscious state. His coworker states he works in a factory manufacturing pesticides. Based on this, the medical officer suspects cyanide poisoning and administers sodium nitrite. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Produces methemoglobinemia‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Produces sulfhemoglobinemi‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Produces carboxyhemoglobin‌emia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Produces deoxyhemoglobinem‌ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Produces methemoglobinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Produces methemoglobinemia</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of <strong>sodium nitrite </strong>as an<strong> antidote </strong>for cyanide poisoning is by the development of<strong> methemoglobinemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cyanide</strong> binds to the <strong>cytochrome oxidase</strong> (complex IV) of the <strong>electron transport chain</strong> (ETC), thereby blocking it. When <strong>sodium nitrite</strong> is administered, it releases the nitrite ion. This ion binds to hemoglobin to form <strong>methemoglobin</strong>. The methemoglobin so formed, has a <strong>high affinity for the cyanide</strong> ion and binds to it, thus, detaching it from the cytochrome oxidase. This <strong>regenerates active cytochrome</strong> normalizing the function of ETC. </p>\n<p>The cyanide ion with methemoglobin forms <strong>cyanmethemoglobin</strong>. <strong>Sodium thiosulphate</strong> is administered for the elimination of this compound. The thiosulfate  forms <strong>thiocyanate</strong> ion with the cyanide and this is readily excreted.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1437","text":"A middle-aged man came to ‌the casualty with complai​nts of abdominal pain. On​ examination, it is noted‌ that the pain radiates f‍rom the loin to the groin​ of the patient. The resi‌dent wants to administer ​a drug to the patient for​ symptomatic relief. Whic‌h drug is not effective i‌n this condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man came to the casualty with complaints of abdominal pain. On examination, it is noted that the pain radiates from the loin to the groin of the patient. The resident wants to administer a drug to the patient for symptomatic relief. Which drug is not effective in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parac‌etamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitr‌ates","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitrates</strong></p><p>Abdominal pain radiating from loin to groin is suggestive of <strong>renal</strong> <strong>colic</strong>. <strong>Nitrates</strong> are <strong>not effective</strong> in renal colic. </p>\n<p>In renal colic,<strong> NSAIDs</strong>, and <strong>morphine</strong> are used for acute pain relief. Due to the short time of action of nitrates, their clinical efficacy as relaxants in genitourinary system is quite low.</p>\n<p><strong>Therapeutic Uses of nitrates</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Angina pectoris</li>\n<li>Acute coronary syndrome</li>\n<li>Myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>CHF</li>\n<li>Biliary colic</li>\n<li>Esophageal spasm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1438","text":"A patient who is on treatm‌ent for angina, presents ​to you with erectile dysf​unction. You decide again‌st prescribing sildenafil‍ because the patient is b​eing treated with _____","raw_text":"A patient who is on treatment for angina, presents to you with erectile dysfunction. You decide against prescribing sildenafil because the patient is being treated with _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium chan‌nel blockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitr‌ates","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ACE inh‌ibitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nitrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nitrates</strong></p><p>The patient would be on<strong> nitrates</strong> for angina and thus should not be prescribed <strong>sildenafil</strong> as sildenafil causes<strong> dangerous potentiation </strong>of nitrate action resulting in <strong>severe hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p>The combination of the two drugs has a <strong>synergistic action</strong> on the intracellular <strong>cGMP levels</strong> that can result in <strong>uninhibited vasodilation</strong> and a precipitous fall in blood pressure. Hence, the use of sildenafil is a contraindication when on nitrates.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1441","text":"A 30-year-old male, who wo‌rks in a factory that man​ufactures explosives, cam​e to the OPD with a pecul‌iar complaint. He says th‍at he gets headaches and ​feels transient dizziness‌ every Monday after his w​eekend off. Exposure to w​hich compound is likely t‌o produce his symptoms?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male, who works in a factory that manufactures explosives, came to the OPD with a peculiar complaint. He says that he gets headaches and feels transient dizziness every Monday after his weekend off. Exposure to which compound is likely to produce his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrog‌lycerin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Benzodi‌azepines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nitroglycerin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nitroglycerin</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely suffering from <strong>Monday disease</strong> which is associated with exposure to <strong>nitroglycerin.</strong></p>\n<p>Continuous exposure to high levels of nitrates can occur in the chemical industry, especially where <strong>explosives</strong> are manufactured.</p>\n<p>When contamination of the workplace with nitrate compounds is severe, workers find that upon <strong>starting their week</strong>, they suffer <strong>headaches</strong> and <strong>transient dizziness</strong>. <strong>After a day</strong> or so back at work, <strong>tolerance</strong> develops. Over the<strong> weekend</strong>, when exposure to the chemicals is reduced, <strong>tolerance disappears</strong>, so symptoms recur each Monday.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1444","text":"A 60 year old male is on v‌erapamil for angina. Whic​h of the following is not​ an adverse effect of thi‌s medication?","raw_text":"A 60 year old male is on verapamil for angina. Which of the following is not an adverse effect of this medication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gingival h‌yperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Head‌ache","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tachycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tachycardia</strong></p><p>Tachycardia is not an adverse effect of verapamil.</p>\n<p><strong>Verapamil</strong> has a <strong>direct negative chronotropic effect</strong> on the heart. It is more likely to cause <strong>bradycardia</strong> because of its significant cardio depressant action. Any reflex stimulation of heart due to peripheral vasodilation is effectively countered by the direct effect.</p>\n<p>Common side effects of verapamil include headache, gingival hyperplasia, and constipation.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1443","text":"Nifedipine acts by blockin‌g ____calcium channels.","raw_text":"Nifedipine acts by blocking  ____calcium channels.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T- ‌type","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R-t‌ype","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N-t‌ype","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L - ‌type","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. L - type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. L - type</strong></p><p><strong>Nifedipine</strong> acts by blocking <strong>L-type calcium channels.</strong></p>\n<p>Verapamil, diltiazem, and dihydropyridines block L-type calcium channels by <strong>binding</strong> to <strong>alpha 1 subunit</strong>. Verapamil was the first clinically used calcium channel blocker.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1450","text":"Which among the following ‌is a newer dihydropyridin​e with additional N-type ​calcium channel blocking ‌activity?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a newer dihydropyridine with additional N-type calcium channel blocking activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amlod‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nimod‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clevi‌dipine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cilni‌dipine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cilnidipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cilnidipine</strong></p><p><strong>Cilnidipine </strong>is a novel dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that <strong>blocks both L- and N-type calcium channels.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Blockage of N-type</strong> calcium channels results in the <strong>reduction</strong> of <strong>sympathetic activity</strong>. It provides additional anti-hypertensive action without inducing reflex tachycardia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1446","text":"Which of the following is ‌more likely to precipitat​e angina?","raw_text":"Which of the following is more likely to precipitate angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amlod‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nifed‌ipine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nifedipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nifedipine</strong></p><p>Amongst the given calcium channel blockers,<strong> nifedipine </strong>is more likely to<strong> precipitate angina.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nifedipine </strong>is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker having a<strong> rapid onset </strong>and<strong> short duration of action.</strong> It results in an abrupt decrease in blood pressure resulting in <strong>reflex tachycardia</strong>. Increased contractility leads to an <strong>increase in O<sub>2</sub> demand</strong> which may precipitate angina.</p>\n<p>Option A: Amlodipine and extended-release preparations of nifedipine do not cause a steep fall in blood pressure and subsequent activation of sympathetic tone. Therefore, they are less likely to precipitate angina. </p>\n<p>Options C and D: Verapamil and diltiazem have relatively less action on peripheral vascular smooth muscles. They have a direct inhibitory effect on cardiac tissue causing <strong>negative ionotropic effect</strong> (verapamil &gt; diltiazem).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1448","text":"A patient with polycystic ‌kidney disease came to th​e ER with complaints of s​evere headache. An NCCT b‌rain was taken and it sho‍wed subarachnoid hemorrha​ge. Which of the followin‌g drugs is most appropria​te for this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with polycystic kidney disease came to the ER with complaints of severe headache. An NCCT brain was taken and it showed subarachnoid hemorrhage. Which of the following drugs is most appropriate for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nifed‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amlod‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nicar‌dipine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nimod‌ipine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nimodipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nimodipine</strong></p><p><strong>Nimodipine</strong> is the most appropriate drug that can be given to this patient.</p>\n<p>Nimodipine is indicated in <strong>cerebral vasospasm</strong> following <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage.</strong> It has a high affinity for cerebral blood vessels and appears to <strong>reduce mortality</strong> after subarachnoid hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1452","text":"Peripheral edema seen with‌ the use of calcium chann​el blockers is primarily ​due to which of the follo‌wing mechanisms?","raw_text":"Peripheral edema seen with the use of calcium channel blockers is primarily due to which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduced glomerular filtrat‌ion rate and renal blood ​flow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased hydrostatic pres‌sure in the capillaries o​f lower extremeties","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased oncotic pressure‌ due to loss of plasma pr​oteins in urine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Precapillary constriction ‌and post capillary dilati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased hydrostatic pressure in the capillaries of lower extremeties","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased hydrostatic pressure in the capillaries of lower extremeties</strong></p><p>Calcium channel blockers induced <strong>peripheral edema</strong> is most likely due to<strong> increased hydrostatic pressure</strong> <strong>in the capillaries of the lower extremities.</strong></p>\n<p>The increased hydrostatic pressure occurs due to<strong> precapillary dilation</strong> and <strong>reflex postcapillary constriction.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1454","text":"A 30-year-old athlete comp‌lained of chest pain duri​ng his practice sessions.​ He is diagnosed to be su‌ffering from exertional a‍ngina and is prescribed a​tenolol. Which of the fol‌lowing changes are not ca​used by this drug?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old athlete complained of chest pain during his practice sessions. He is diagnosed to be suffering from exertional angina and is prescribed atenolol. Which of the following changes are not caused by this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in cardiac contra‌ctility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in‌ heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in blood pressure‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in coronary colla‌teral circulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decrease in coronary collateral circulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decrease in coronary collateral circulation</strong></p><p>Atenolol is a beta-blocker which<strong> increases </strong>the<strong> coronary collateral circulation, </strong>thereby increasing the blood flow towards the ischaemic region. This provides <strong>relief in exertional angina.</strong></p>\n<p>Beta-blockers decrease the heart rate, contractility, and arterial blood pressure. These effects are beneficial by decreasing the myocardial O<sub>2</sub> demand. A decrease in heart rate increases the time available for myocardial perfusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1455","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can be used to balance​ the undesirable effects ​of beta-blockers in angin‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can be used to balance the undesirable effects of beta-blockers in angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitr‌ates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ivabr‌adine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitrates</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-blockers </strong>cause an <strong>increase in end-diastolic volume</strong> and ejection time. These effects can be<strong> balanced using</strong> <strong>nitrates </strong>by preferential venous dilatation, thus <strong>reducing preload</strong>.</p>\n<p>Beta-blockers also block the baroreceptor mediated reflex tachycardia caused by nitrates due to vasodilation.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: Verapamil and diltiazem are not combined with beta-blockers as they can cause additive <strong>suppression of SA node</strong> and<strong> AV nodal</strong> conduction.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ivabradine is a pacemaker I(f) \"funny\" current inhibitor. Its addition does not reverse the undesirable effect but only <strong>adds to the bradycardia</strong> caused by beta blockers. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1457","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is associated with cor​onary steal phenomenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is associated with coronary steal phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipyri‌damole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dipyridamole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dipyridamole</strong></p><p>Coronary steal phenomenon is seen with <strong>dipyridamole</strong>.</p>\n<p>In patients with <strong>partial obstruction of coronary vasculature</strong>, a region of ischemia develops in the myocardium. In the absence of drugs, <strong>arterioles in the ischemic areas</strong> are usually <strong>maximally dilated</strong> as a result of local compensatory mechanisms. </p>\n<p>When <strong>dipyridamole</strong> is administered, it acts as a <strong>nonselective arteriolar dilator</strong>. It can only dilate the vessels of the normal myocardium as the vessels of the ischemic myocardium are already maximally dilated. This <strong>diverts</strong> (steals) the<strong> blood away from</strong> the <strong>ischemic myocardium</strong> towards the normal myocardium. This is termed as <strong>coronary steal phenomenon</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1458","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about ranolazine ​is not true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about ranolazine is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is recommended as first‌-line treatment for acute​ angina.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotension ‌is uncommon.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It improves glycemic contr‌ol.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is metabolised by CYP3A‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is recommended as first-line treatment for acute angina.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is recommended as first-line treatment for acute angina.</strong></p><p><strong>Ranolazine</strong> is not recommended as a first-line agent for acute angina. It is approved as a <strong>second-line </strong>agent for<strong> chronic angina.</strong></p>\n<p>Ranolazine acts by <strong>reducing late sodium current</strong> (I<sub>Na</sub>) that facilitates calcium entry via the sodium-calcium exchanger. The <strong>reduction in intracellular calcium</strong> concentration results in <strong>decreased cardiac contractility</strong>. Additionally, it also <strong>reduces cardiac fatty acid oxidation</strong> and stimulates glucose metabolism. It results in a small decrease in HBA1c, thus <strong>improving glycemic control.</strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>metabolized by CYP3A4</strong> and hence should not be used with CYP3A4 inhibitors such as macrolides, imidazole antibiotics, and HIV protease inhibitors. </p>\n<p>The most frequent side effects are dizziness, headache, nausea, and constipation. Hypotension is uncommon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1460","text":"Which of the following is ‌a rho-kinase inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a rho-kinase inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fas‌udil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ranol‌azine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trimet‌azidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nicor‌andil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fasudil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fasudil</strong></p><p><strong>Fasudil</strong> is a selective RhoA /<strong>rho-kinase </strong><strong>inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>Rho kinases</strong> (ROCK) are a family of enzymes that <strong>inhibit vascular relaxation.</strong> Their <strong>excessive activity</strong> has been noted in <strong>coronary spasm</strong> and <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>. Inhibiton of these enzymes results in vascular relaxation.</p>\n<p>Option B: Ranolazine acts by <strong>reducing late sodium current</strong> (I<sub>Na</sub>). This reduces calcium entry via the sodium-calcium exchanger.</p>\n<p>Option C: Trimetazidine is a partial fatty acid oxidation (<strong>pFOX</strong>)<strong> inhibitor</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: Nicorandil is an <strong>agonist</strong> of <strong>K<sub>ATP </sub></strong>channels</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1440","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs causes activation of K​+-ATP channels?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs causes activation of K+-ATP channels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nicor‌andil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Molsi‌domine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isosorbide‌ dinitrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrog‌lycerin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nicorandil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nicorandil</strong></p><p><strong>Nicorandil</strong> causes activation of <strong>K<sup>+</sup>-ATP channels. </strong>This leads to the hyperpolarization of the smooth muscle cells leading to <strong>vasodilatation</strong>.</p>\n<p>It also has nitrate like <strong>cGMP dependent action</strong>. Nicorandil dilates both arteries and veins leading to a decrease in afterload and preload of the heart.</p>\n<p>Molsidomine, isosorbide dinitrate, and nitroglycerin are nitrates. They do not act on K-ATP channels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SRvmzl6R","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"10_neurotransmitters","title":"10.Neurotransmitters","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB9681","text":"Which of the following neu‌rotransmitter is not a bi​ogenic amine?","raw_text":"Which of the following neurotransmitter is not a biogenic amine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gly‌cine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glycine</strong></p><p>Glycine is not a biogenic amine. It is an <strong>amino acid</strong>. </p>\n<p>Biogenic amines are made from amino acid precursors. They have a nitrogen containing amine (NH2) group. Unlike the amino acids, the biogenic amines lack a carboxyl (COOH) group. Norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine, serotonin and histamine are biogenic amines. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9682","text":"The main excitatory and in‌hibitory neurotransmitter​s in the CNS are ______","raw_text":"The main excitatory and inhibitory neurotransmitters in the CNS are ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamate and glycine resp‌ectively","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aspartate and glycine resp‌ectively","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norepinephrine and GABA re‌spectively","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dopamine and GABA respecti‌vely","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glutamate and glycine respectively","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glutamate and glycine respectively</strong></p><p><strong>Glutamate</strong> is the main excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS. It is responsible for 75% of the excitatory transmission in the brain.</p>\n<p><strong>Glycine and GABA</strong> are the main inhibitory neurotransmitters in the CNS. Glycine is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter in the <strong>spinal cord. </strong>GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the <strong>brain</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9717","text":"'A' delta fibers and 'C' f‌ibers carry pain sensatio​n. The neurotransmitter r​eleased by 'A' delta fibe‍rs is ______","raw_text":"'A' delta fibers and 'C' fibers carry pain sensation. The neurotransmitter released by 'A' delta fibers is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subst‌ance P","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glut‌amate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hist‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glutamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glutamate</strong></p><p>The neurotransmitter released by A delta fibers, which carry sharp pricking pain, is <strong>glutamate.</strong></p>\n<p>The substance released by C fibers, which carry diffuse, chronic, burning pain is <strong>substance P</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9685","text":"The most important neurotr‌ansmitter of reward pathw​ay is ______","raw_text":"The most important neurotransmitter of reward pathway is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dopamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dopamine</strong></p><p>The most important neurotransmitter of reward pathway is<strong> dopamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reward pathway (mesolimbic pathway) connects the ventral tegmental area in the midbrain to the nucleus accumbens and limbic subcortical areas. This pathway is involved in reward behaviour, addiction and in psychiatric disorders such as schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9702","text":"Dopamine beta hydroxylase ‌converts dopamine to ____​__","raw_text":"Dopamine beta hydroxylase converts dopamine to ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DO‌PA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyro‌sine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Norepinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a926ea635d4407596fae79549d0fc16x1280x1453.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Norepinephrine</strong></p><p>Dopamine beta hydroxylase converts dopamine to <strong>norepinephrine</strong>. Vitamin C is required as a cofactor for the enzyme.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9684","text":"Which of these is not a do‌paminergic pathway?","raw_text":"Which of these is not a dopaminergic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesocortic‌al pathway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nigrostria‌tal pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberoinfundibular pathway‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corticostri‌atal pathway","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Corticostriatal pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ed96eb60c3e4109962a883e487ff8ccx446x307.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Corticostriatal pathway</strong></p><p>Corticostrial pathway is not a dopaminergic pathway.</p>\n<p>The corticostrial pathway is the primary input pathway for the basal ganglia which uses glutamate as the neurotransmitter.</p>\n<p>Important dopaminergic pathways are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mesolimbic pathway - Increased activity responsible for <strong>positive symptoms</strong> of schizophrenia.</li>\n<li>Mesocortical pathway - Decreased activity responsible for<strong> negative symptoms</strong> of schizophrenia.</li>\n<li>Nigrostriatal pathway - Decreased activity causes <strong>extrapyramidal symptoms</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tuberoinfundibular pathway - Decreased activity causes <strong>increase in prolactin</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9686","text":"Find the inc‌orrect match.","raw_text":"Find the incorrect match.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Raphe nucleu‌s –Serotonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Locus coeruleus - Norepine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal forebrain complex - ‌GABA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Histamine - ‌hypothalamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Basal forebrain complex - GABA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Basal forebrain complex - GABA</strong></p><p><strong>Basal forebrain</strong> <strong>complex </strong>(nucleus basalis of Meynert) is rich in acetylcholine. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">A loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of <span class=\"s1\">Meynert is seen in <strong>alzheimer's disease.</strong></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9689","text":"A patient with involuntary‌ jerking and writhing mov​ements is diagnosed with ​Huntington's disease. Los‍s of which of the followi​ng is responsible for hyp​erkinetic symptoms in thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A patient with involuntary jerking and writhing movements is diagnosed with Huntington's disease. Loss of which of the following is responsible for hyperkinetic symptoms in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nigrostriatal dopaminergic‌ system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intrastriatal cholinergic ‌pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamate and cholinergic ‌system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intrastriatal GABA-ergic a‌nd cholinergic system","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intrastriatal GABA-ergic and cholinergic system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intrastriatal GABA-ergic and cholinergic system</strong></p><p>The <strong>hyperkinetic</strong> feature of <strong>Huntington's</strong> disease is due to the<strong> loss of intrastriatal GABAergic and cholinergic neurons</strong>. The loss of the GABAergic pathway<strong> </strong>(which is an inhibitory pathway) to the globus pallidus external segment and loss of cholinergic neurons in many parts of the brain decreases inhibition, causing the hyperkinetic feature of the disease to develop.</p>\n<p>Huntington's disease is an <strong>autosomal dominant trinucleotide repeat disorder</strong> with multiple <strong>CAG repeats</strong>. The abnormal gene responsible for the disease is located on <strong>chromosome 4.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9687","text":"An infant develops feature‌s of botulism following h​oney intake. This toxin b​locks neuromuscular trans‍mission by which of the f​ollowing mechanisms?","raw_text":"An infant develops features of botulism following honey intake. This toxin blocks neuromuscular transmission by which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevents release of Ach at‌ presynaptic membrane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Opening of calcium channel‌s at the presynaptic memb​rane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevents the binding of Ac‌h at the postsynatic memb​rane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevents the action of Ach‌ at the postsynaptic memb​rane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prevents release of Ach at presynaptic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prevents release of Ach at presynaptic membrane</strong></p><p><strong>Botulinum</strong> toxin <strong>prevents</strong> the <strong>release</strong> of <strong>Ach</strong> at the <strong>presynaptic</strong> membrane.</p>\n<p>Botulinum toxins A, B, and E are toxic to humans. They cleave components of SNARE - a complex of proteins necessary for the exocytosis of acetylcholine. Toxins A and E cleave SNAP-25 whereas toxin B cleaves vesicle-associated membrane protein (VAMP) also known as synaptobrevin.</p>\n<p>Cleavage of SNARE proteins prevents the release of acetylcholine as the vesicles containing Ach are unable to bind to the presynaptic membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9688","text":"A farmer presents with dif‌ficulty swallowing and ja​w tightening. He gives a ​history of getting his fi‍nger jammed in the rustic​ gate a few days ago. He ​gives no history of previ​ous tetanus immunization.‍ After evaluation, he was​ diagnosed with tetanus. ‌The release of which of t​he following neurotransmi​tters is affected by the ​toxin responsible for thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A farmer presents with difficulty swallowing and jaw tightening. He gives a history of getting his finger jammed in the rustic gate a few days ago. He gives no history of previous tetanus immunization. After evaluation, he was diagnosed with tetanus. The release of which of the following neurotransmitters is affected by the toxin responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GA‌BA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GABA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GABA</strong></p><p><strong>Tetanus</strong> toxin affects the <strong>release</strong> of <strong>GABA.</strong></p>\n<p>Tetanus toxin escapes normal lysosomal degradation and crosses the synapse to the <strong>GABA-ergic presynaptic</strong> inhibitory interneuron terminals. The toxin<strong> blocks the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters</strong> (<strong>glycine and gamma-aminobutyric acid</strong>) across the synaptic cleft. This blockage of the inhibitory neurotransmitters causes spastic paralysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9691","text":"The neurotransmitter relea‌sed by Renshaw cell inter​neuron is ______.","raw_text":"The neurotransmitter released by  Renshaw cell interneuron is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glut‌amate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acetyl ‌choline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gly‌cine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/413e62a2de194eb0b4c52b094b8a1b4ex1280x1655.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glycine</strong></p><p>The neurotransmitter released by Renshaw cell interneuron is <strong>glycine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renshaw cell is an inhibitory interneuron which is activated by collaterals from spinal motor neurons. Renshaw cell terminates on the cell body of the same and other spinal motor neurons. It reduces or stops the discharge of the motor neuron by releasing glycine.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9696","text":"Which of the following neu‌rotransmitters is increas​ed during REM sleep?","raw_text":"Which of the following neurotransmitters is increased during REM sleep?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acetylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acetylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Acetylcholine </strong>increases during REM sleep.</p>\n<p>In REM sleep, there is increase in acetylcholine and decrease in norepinephrine and serotonin. The reverse of this pattern leads to wakefulness.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Neurotransmitters in REM sleep </td>\n<td>Levels of neurotransmitter </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acetylcholine </td>\n<td>Increased </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serotonin</td>\n<td>Decreased </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Norepinephrine </td>\n<td>Decreased </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9683","text":"Which of the following is ‌the wrong match?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the wrong match?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sympathetic preganglionic ‌fibres - release acetylch​oline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parasympathetic preganglio‌nic fibres - release acet​ylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sympathetic post ganglioni‌c fibres - release noradr​enaline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sweat glands - innervated ‌by sympathetic adrenergic​ fibres","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sweat glands - innervated by sympathetic adrenergic fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sweat glands - innervated by sympathetic adrenergic fibres</strong></p><p><strong>Sweat glands</strong> are innervated by <strong>sympathetic cholinergic fibres </strong>(not adrenergic fibres).</p>\n<p>Pre-ganglionic fibres of the sympathetic nervous system, pre-ganglionic fibers of the parasympathetic nervous system and post-ganglionic parasympathetic fibres release acetylcholine.</p>\n<p><strong>Post-ganglionic sympathetic fibres</strong> release <strong>noradrenaline in most of the sites,</strong> except in certain places such as <strong>sweat glands</strong>, <strong>piloerector muscles</strong> and <strong>blood vessels in some skeletal muscles</strong> where <strong>acetylcholine</strong> is released.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9700","text":"Which part of the basal ga‌nglia is the neurotransmi​tter glutamate secreted f​rom?","raw_text":"Which part of the basal ganglia is the neurotransmitter glutamate secreted from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Globus pall‌idus interna","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Globus pall‌idus externa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subthalam‌ic nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Put‌amen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subthalamic nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49268e27865b4c7d8830886a3466d83fx1280x999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subthalamic nucleus</strong></p><p>Projections from <strong>subthalamic nucleus</strong> to globus pallidus are <strong>glutaminergic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neurotransmitters secreted by various regions of basal ganglia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Substantia nigra pars compacta = dopamine</li>\n<li>Substantia nigra pars reticulata = GABA</li>\n<li>Subthalmic nuclei = glutamate</li>\n<li>Corpus striatum to globus pallidus = GABA</li>\n<li>Intrastriate neurons = acetylcholine</li>\n<li>Globus pallidus = GABA</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Diagrammatic representation of the principal connections of the basal ganglia. </p>\n<p><strong>Acetylcholine</strong> is the transmitter produced by interneurons in the <strong>striatum</strong>.</p>\n<p>(SNr : substantia nigra pars reticulata; SNc: substantia nigra pars compacta; STN: Subthalamic nucleus ; GPe: globus pallidus externa ; GPi: Globus pallidus interna).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3562","text":"All of the following are e‌xcitatory neurotransmitte​rs except:","raw_text":"All of the following are excitatory neurotransmitters except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glut‌amate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serotonin</strong></p><p>Serotonin is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. </p>\n<p>Acetylcholine, glutamate and norepinephrine are excitatory neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9697","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about serotonin ​receptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about serotonin receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5HT2A receptors mediate pl‌atelet aggregation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-HT2C receptor agonists i‌ncrease appetite","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5HT3 receptors are related‌ to vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5HT4 receptors facilitate ‌secretion and peristalsis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5-HT2C receptor agonists increase appetite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5-HT2C receptor agonists increase appetite</strong></p><p>5-HT<sub>2C</sub> receptor agonists increase satiety (state of being fed beyond capacity).</p>\n<p>Important serotonin receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>5-HT<sub>2A</sub></strong> receptors mediate<strong> platelet aggregation </strong>and smooth muscle contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>5-HT<sub>3</sub></strong> receptors are present in the GIT and the area postrema and are related to <strong>vomiting</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>5-HT<sub>4</sub></strong> receptors are present in the GIT and brain. They <strong>facilitate secretion and peristalsis </strong>in GIT.</li>\n<li><strong>5-HT<sub>6</sub> and 5-HT<sub>7</sub></strong> are distributed throughout the <strong>limbic system.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>All are G protein coupled receptors <strong>except 5-HT3</strong> which is a ligand gated ion channel receptor.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9693","text":"Serotonin is present in hi‌ghest concentration in __​______","raw_text":"Serotonin is present in highest concentration in ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baso‌phil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mast‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior h‌ypothalamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Platelets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9cbbc6672d3e4ffa8225a91872e5e4b2x1280x1408.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Platelets</strong></p><p>Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine; 5-HT) is present in highest concentration in<strong> platelets and in the gastrointestinal tract</strong>, where it is found in the enterochromaffin cells and the myenteric plexus. It is also found within the brain stem in the midline<strong> Raphe nuclei</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The precursor of serotonin is tryptophan.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9698","text":"Which statement regarding ‌the NMDA receptor is inco​rrect?","raw_text":"Which statement regarding the NMDA receptor is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamate is its endogenou‌s agonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycine is essential for t‌his receptor's response t​o its endogenous agonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a chloride ion chann‌el","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is present in hippocamp‌al neurons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a chloride ion channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a chloride ion channel</strong></p><p>NMDA receptor permits passage of<strong> Ca<sup>2+</sup><sup> </sup></strong>and other cations (Na+), not of chloride.</p>\n<p>Glutamate is the agonist for NMDA receptor. Glycine binding to the NMDA receptor is essential for the receptor to respond to glutamate. NMDA receptors are present on hippocampal neurons.</p>\n<p>Note: GABA and glycine receptors act by increasing Cl<sup>-</sup> conductance<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Jw8JERn4","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"10_amnestic_disorders_and_other_neurocognitive_disorders","title":"10.Amnestic Disorders And Other Neurocognitive Disorders","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MC0349","text":"A lesion involving which o‌f the following neuroanat‍omical structures is leas​t likely to result in an ‍amnestic disorder?","raw_text":"A lesion involving which of the following neuroanatomical structures is least likely to result in an amnestic disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hippo‌campus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amyg‌dala","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arcuate‌ nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mammilla‌ry bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arcuate nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arcuate nucleus</strong></p><p><strong>Arcuate nucleus</strong> is <strong>not</strong> associated with amnestic disorders.</p>\n<p>The neuroanatomical structures involved in the etiology of amnestic memory include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mammillary bodies</li>\n<li>Hippocampus</li>\n<li>Amygdala</li>\n<li>Dorsomedial and the midline thalamic nuclei</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0384","text":"A 32-year-old train accide‌nt survivor is being eval‍uated for suspected amnes​tic disorder. Which of th‍e following is most likel​y to be impaired in this ​patient?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old train accident survivor is being evaluated for suspected amnestic disorder. Which of the following is most likely to be impaired in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Habit l‌earning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skill l‌earning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Route na‌vigation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Classical c‌onditioning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Route navigation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Route navigation</strong></p><p><strong>Route navigation</strong> is a function of <strong>declarative memory</strong> that is impaired in patients with <strong>amnesia</strong>. Non-declarative memory is preserved in amnesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Declarative memory </strong>supports the conscious recollection of facts and events.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Memory for routes, lists, faces, melodies, objects</li>\n<li>Memory for other verbal and nonverbal material</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-declarative memory</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skill learning</li>\n<li>Habit learning</li>\n<li>Simple forms of conditioning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0353","text":"After detailed neuropsychi‌atric evaluation, an ICU ‍patient was found to have​ severe retrograde amnesi‍a with largely preserved ​anterograde memory. Which​ of the following is the ‍most likely explanation f​or this presentation?","raw_text":"After detailed neuropsychiatric evaluation, an ICU patient was found to have severe retrograde amnesia with largely preserved anterograde memory. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-traumatic stress diso‌rder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Head ‌injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alcohol-rela‌ted dementia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Korsakoff‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Post-traumatic stress disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Post-traumatic stress disorder</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Severe retrograde amnesia with <strong>minimal or no anterograde amnesia</strong> is seen in <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder.</strong> It is referred to as <strong>dissociative/psychogenic</strong> amnesia. It is also known as <strong>hysterical</strong> amnesia or <strong>functional</strong> amnesia.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Psychogenic amnesia presents with a sudden onset of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extensive and severe <strong>retrograde</strong> amnesia</li>\n<li>A loss of personal identity - unable to recall their own name, information from childhood, or from their past.</li>\n<li>Minimal anterograde amnesia</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The other given options can all cause both retrograde and anterograde amnesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0357","text":"What does Ribot’s law deal‌ with?","raw_text":"What does Ribot’s law deal with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterogra‌de amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retrograd‌e amnesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dissociati‌ve amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Global trans‌ient amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retrograde amnesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retrograde amnesia</strong></p><p><strong>Ribot's law </strong>deals with <strong>r</strong><strong>etrograde amnesia.</strong></p>\n<p>During incidents of retrograde amnesia, <strong>most recently acquired memories </strong>are the<strong> most vulnerable</strong> <strong>to disruption</strong> from brain damage. This temporal gradation of retrograde amnesia that preferentially <strong>spares</strong> the more <strong>distant memories</strong> is known as Ribot's law.</p>\n<p>E.g: If an incident causing retrograde amnesia occurs at 30 years of age, the memories acquired at 25 years of age may be more severely affected than those memories acquired at 20 years of age or earlier.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0360","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of mild neu‍rocognitive disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of mild neurocognitive disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cognitive decline from pre‌vious level in one or mor‍e cognitive domain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The deficit occurs exclusi‌vely during an episode of‍ delirium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Do not interfere with inde‌pendence in daily activit‍ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mini mental state examinat‌ion score of 26","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The deficit occurs exclusively during an episode of delirium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The deficit occurs exclusively during an episode of delirium</strong></p><p>Mild neurocognitive disorder <strong>does not occur</strong> <strong>exclusively</strong> during an <strong>episode of delirium.</strong></p>\n<p>Mild neurocognitive disorder or Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI) is a level of cognitive impairment which is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not severe enough to be diagnosed as dementia</li>\n<li>Not mild enough to be called a part of the normal aging process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>General Criteria for Mild Cognitive Impairment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not normal, but does not meet criteria for dementia syndrome either.</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitive decline</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>The patient and/or informant report an impairment in objective cognitive tasks.</li>\n<li>Evidence of decline over time in objective cognitive tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Preserved basic activities</strong> of daily living/minimal impairment in complex instrumental functions.</li>\n<li><strong>MMSE</strong> score between <strong>25-27 </strong>(out of 30)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Single domain amnestic Mild Cognitive Impairment usually progresses to Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0392","text":"A 72-year-old man was take‌n to the psychiatrist by ‍his son for memory issues​. The elderly man had bee‍n living in his own house​ for the past 40 years, w​here he used to ritually ‍water his garden every da​y after breakfast. Recent​ly he moved to his son’s ​flat in Delhi but forgets​ this, and continues to g​et ready after breakfast ‌to go and water his garde​n. Neuropsychiatric evalu​ation is normal except fo​r some mild cognitive imp​airment. What is this phe‌nomenon called?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old man was taken to the psychiatrist by his son for memory issues. The elderly man had been living in his own house for the past 40 years, where he used to ritually water his garden every day after breakfast. Recently he moved to his son’s flat in Delhi but forgets this, and continues to get ready after breakfast to go and water his garden. Neuropsychiatric evaluation is normal except for some mild cognitive impairment. What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retroactive ‌interference","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proactive i‌nterference","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Output in‌terference","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biologica‌l Amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proactive interference","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proactive interference</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario in which <strong>information stored earlier interferes</strong> with the <strong>retrieval</strong> of <strong>new information</strong> is called <strong>proactive interference.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Retroactive Interference</strong> -<strong> </strong>new information interferes with the retrieval of information stored earlier.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Output interference</strong> -<strong> </strong>the initial act of recalling particular information interferes with the retrieval of the original information. For example, When trying to remember a limited number of items, such as a list of words or the names of individuals in a group, recalling some items can make it more difficult to recall other items in the same collection.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Biological Amnesia</strong> -<strong> </strong>amnesia due to brain damage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1543","text":"A 42-year-old man had a fa‌ll followed by one episod‍e of vomiting. When broug​ht to the casualty, he wa‍s awake and conscious but​ had unrelieved anterogra​de amnesia. Which of the ‍following is the most lik​ely cause?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man had a fall followed by one episode of vomiting. When brought to the casualty, he was awake and conscious but had unrelieved anterograde amnesia. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drunk‌enness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse ax‌onal injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Concu‌ssion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebral venous thrombosis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Concussion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Concussion</strong></p><p>The given history of <strong>fall</strong> and <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong> points towards the diagnosis of <strong>concussion</strong>. </p>\n<p>A concussion<strong> </strong>is the most common type of traumatic brain injury. It can result from vehicle accidents, falls, sports injuries, etc. Anterograde amnesia/post-traumatic amnesia is the hallmark of a concussion. In this type of amnesia, the <strong>events following the injury</strong> cannot be recalled. </p>\n<p>Other clinical features of concussion include headache, dizziness, nausea, and vomiting.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Drunkenness </strong>- the patient will be <strong>drowsy or sleepy</strong> and not awake.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Diffuse axonal injury</strong> - seen in high-speed deceleration which causes <strong>disruption</strong> and <strong>shearing</strong> of <strong>axons</strong> leading to <strong>microhemorrhages.</strong> Usually results in a persistent vegetative stage.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cerebral venous thrombosis</strong> - acute thrombosis in dural venous sinuses; manifests as headache, visual abnormalities, or seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RZW0sdHh","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"10_lower_gi_radiology","title":"10.Lower Gi Radiology","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA0587","text":"Identify the investigation‍ from the given image?","raw_text":"Identify the investigation from the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single-contrast barium mea‍l follow through","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Double-contrast barium ene‍ma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Double-contrast barium mea‍l follow through","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Single-contrast barium ene‍ma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Single-contrast barium enema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed53922a11a145278d083ad8164e9819.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9be45ae4b604507b70f49076c0b04a7x387x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Single-contrast barium enema</strong></p><p>The above radiography used for visualizing the large bowel is a <strong>single-contrast barium enema</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Single-contrast studies:</strong> Only a <strong>positive</strong> contrast like barium or gastrografin is used. This study can be used to evaluate mechanical problems such as an obstruction or fistula. In suspected perforation, a single contrast with a water-soluble medium is preferred.</p>\n<p>The advantages of single-contrast studies include precise control of barium column, easier identification of filling defects, and suitability for patients unable to swallow gas-forming tablets.</p>\n<p><strong>Double-contrast studies: </strong>These studies use <strong>positive</strong> and <strong>negative</strong> contrast agents to increase the sensitivity of the examination. In these studies, thick barium coats the lumen, and effervescent tablets ingested distend the lumen with air.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positive contrast: Barium or barium-like agents e.g., gastrografin </li>\n<li>Negative contrast: Air or CO<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The advantages of the double-contrast studies include the \"see-through\" effect, which provides enhanced visualization of mucosal details and increased sensitivity for detecting mucosal irregularities. These studies also allow for<strong> </strong>better distention and separation of the bowel loops and also better detection of small mucosal lesions, polyps, and ulcers.</p>\n<p>The radiograph given below shows a double-contrast barium enema:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0595","text":"You are performing an ultr‍asound abdomen and notice​ the below finding. Which‍ of the following conditi​ons is it associated with‍?","raw_text":"You are performing an ultrasound abdomen and notice the below finding. Which of the following conditions is it associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maydel‍ hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meckel’s d‍iverticulum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intussu‍sception","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Midgut ‍volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intussusception","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2898269b7bb5417691cf436a2f1b8fd4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2806ad8ececb48918afbf3d49ecc6e8bx630x523.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e630f699af884132bbcb3793875fff01x632x457.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intussusception</strong></p><p>The given ultrasound abdomen depicts the<strong> target sign</strong> suggestive of <strong>intussusception</strong>.</p>\n<p>Intussusception occurs when a portion of the bowel is <strong>telescoped</strong> into an adjacent segment. The most common location of intussusception is <strong>ileocolic</strong>. Clinically, it presents with cramping abdominal pain, palpable <strong>sausage-shaped abdominal mass</strong>, and passage of <strong>red currant jelly stools.</strong></p>\n<p>A plain radiograph may show a <strong>meniscus sign</strong>, i.e. soft tissue mass contrasting the bowel that is air-filled. The target sign of intestinal intussusception, also known as the <strong>doughnut sign</strong><strong> </strong>or <strong>bull's eye sign, </strong>is a diagnostic feature observed on abdominal ultrasound<strong>.</strong> This appearance is due to concentric alternating echogenic and hypoechogenic bands. The echogenic bands are formed by the mucosa and muscularis, and the hypoechoic bands are formed by the submucosa.</p>\n<p>Other ultrasound signs of intussusception include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pseudokidney sign -</strong> This is seen in the longitudinal section of the bowel. The edematous bowel resembles renal parenchyma, and the mesentery, which is dragged into the intussusception, resembles renal hilum.</li>\n<li><strong>Crescent in a doughnut sign</strong> - A hyperechoic semilunar structure caused by mesenteric fat pulled into the intussceptum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below images show USG films depicting the target sign and pseudo-kidney sign respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0596","text":"An infant was brought to t‍he casualty with an inces​sant cry, abdominal diste‍nsion, and a history of p​assing blood-stained stoo‍ls. Target sign was noted​ in the right hypochondri​um on ultrasonography. Wh​at would you expect to se​e in the barium enema stu‌dy?","raw_text":"An infant was brought to the casualty with an incessant cry, abdominal distension, and a history of passing blood-stained stools. Target sign was noted in the right hypochondrium on ultrasonography. What would you expect to see in the barium enema study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coiled sprin‍g appearance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Raspberry thorn appearance‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rose thorn‍ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saw tooth ‍appearance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coiled spring appearance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3860284957c4bc7a172dafa38b48a0ax1280x609.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coiled spring appearance</strong></p><p>Based on the given clinical scenario and the target sign observed on ultrasonography, <strong>intussusception </strong>is the likely diagnosis. A barium enema study in this condition is likely to show a <strong>coiled spring appearance</strong>, caused by the<strong> </strong>barium outlining the lumen of the intussusceptum and the surrounding intraluminal space. </p>\n<p>The images given below are the barium enema study showing a coiled spring appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options B and C: <strong>Raspberry thorn</strong> appearance and <strong>rose thorn</strong> appearance on barium study are seen in <strong>Crohn's</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Saw tooth</strong> appearance on barium enema is seen in <strong>colonic diverticulosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0598","text":"A neonate was brought with‍ bilious vomiting. A bari​um meal study was done an‍d is shown below. What is​ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A neonate was brought with bilious vomiting. A barium meal study was done and is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midgut ‍volvulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jejunal‍ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duodenal‍ atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Midgut volvulus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/161b63c1641941bc9885a2efe63ba741.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5bd29f05f0b4544807e3192d579cc34x512x203.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Midgut volvulus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, along with the <strong>corkscrew sign</strong> seen in the barium meal study, is suggestive of <strong>midgut volvulus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Midgut volvulus is a complication of <strong>malrotation</strong> of the bowel and may result in proximal bowel obstruction. It can occur at any age but it is commonly seen in the first few months of life. The baby presents with <strong>bilious vomiting</strong>, abdominal pain, and failure to thrive.</p>\n<p>The images given below are barium meal studies showing the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A -  It describes the spiralling of the duodenum and jejunum around the mesenteric vessels, suggestive of a <strong>corkscrew</strong> appearance</li>\n<li>B - An incomplete duodenal obstruction and dilation of the first and second portions of the duodenum are seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0599","text":"Which of the following con‍ditions is associated wit​h the finding given in th‍e CT abdomen?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with the finding given in the CT abdomen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midgut ‍volvulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colonic‍ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital hypertrophic py‍loric stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duodenal‍ atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Midgut volvulus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d3ab6db410f430aa15848457c58d83b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e04826acc8146e2b1f35b6f839337a9x1280x1110.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Midgut volvulus</strong></p><p>The given CT abdomen depicts a swirling appearance of the mesentery and superior mesenteric vein around the superior mesenteric artery known as the <strong>whirlpool sign</strong> or<strong> whirl sign, </strong>seen in patients with <strong>midgut volvulus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>whirlpool</strong> sign is seen when a structure twists upon itself. It is most commonly described in the abdomen where the bowel rotates around its mesentery, with mesenteric vessels creating the whirls. Whirlpool sign is seen in several settings, which include malrotation complicated by midgut volvulus, caecal volvulus, sigmoid volvulus, and closed-loop bowel obstruction. It can also be seen in ovarian torsion.</p>\n<p>The marked CECT image below shows the whirlpool sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0600","text":"Given below is the CT abdo‍men of a patient who pres​ented with diarrhea. He i‍s most likely to be suffe​ring from _____.","raw_text":"Given below is the CT abdomen of a patient who presented with diarrhea. He is most likely to be suffering from _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomembra‍nous colitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn’s‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcerativ‍e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic m‍egacolon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pseudomembranous colitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c58301c1136a4e8cb8430b74e8f34660.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1928b416b39a45cc98c3c85de9ec791ex954x1106.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pseudomembranous colitis</strong></p><p>The CT image depicts<strong> </strong>the<strong> accordion sign</strong> seen in <strong>pseudomembranous colitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Clostridium difficle infection (CDI) commonly caused by the disruption of the commensal colonic microflora, due to <strong>prolonged antimicrobial use,</strong> especially antibiotics like cephalosporins, ampicillin, clindamycin, and fluoroquinolones. The more severe form of CDI is known as pseudomembranous colitis (PMC).</p>\n<p>Toxigenic <em>Clostridium difficile </em>produces <strong>toxin A</strong> (an enterotoxin) and <strong>toxin B</strong> (a cytotoxin), damaging the epithelial-cell barrier function and causing diarrhea and pseudomembrane formation, which initially appear as small whitish-yellow plaques and later coalesce to involve the entire colon wall. The patient also presents with fever, abdominal pain, and leukocytosis.</p>\n<p>On CT imaging, accordion sign, also referred to as <strong>concertina sign, </strong>may be seen<strong>. </strong> The terms accordion or concertina refer to a harmonica like a musical instrument. The thickened edematous bowel resembles the folds of an accordion. The hyperemic-enhancing mucosa stretched over the thickened submucosal folds produces this appearance. This is also seen when the contrast gets trapped between edematous haustral folds and pseudomembranes of the colon. </p>\n<p><strong>Accordion sign</strong> is typical for pseudomembranous colitis but can be also seen in ischemic colitis, AIDS-related colitis, and severe edema from cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>The CT image below depicts accordion sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0601","text":"A 64-year-old man is rushe‍d to the ER with severe a​bdominal pain, abdominal ‍distension, and obstipati​on. His erect X-ray abdom‍en is as shown. Which of ​the following is the like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man is rushed to the ER with severe abdominal pain, abdominal distension, and obstipation. His erect X-ray abdomen is as shown. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colonic div‍erticulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cecal v‍olvulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paralyt‍ic ileus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sigmoid volvulus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b460b7b537a4819bd37ebb919492a2b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b334bc9e29748279b03ee681ec6c286x709x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7bc8c2b4ad6e4071a517011839c1bbe0x512x568.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/750147c399674b93ae3e044d04567501x720x596.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sigmoid volvulus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a 64-year-old man with severe abdominal pain, abdominal distension, and obstipation along with the radiograph showing the <strong>coffee bean</strong> <strong>sign</strong> is likely to be <strong>sigmoid volvulus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs when the sigmoid colon twists on the sigmoid mesocolon.</p>\n<p>A sigmoid volvulus can be differentiated from a cecal volvulus by <strong>swirl sign</strong>, location of the obstructed loop, and anatomical appendages. The apex of a sigmoid volvulus usually lies high on the left side. The apex of a caecal volvulus may lie on the right side or extend to the left upper quadrant. The swirl sign in with sigmoid volvulus is located at the level of the pelvic brim while the swirl in cecal volvulus is located in the right lower quadrant. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p><strong> </strong>Option C:<strong> Cecal volvulus</strong> is more common in females in the 4th to 5th decade. It is characterized by ischemia. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Paralytic ileus</strong> occurs secondary to neuromuscular failure, leading to the failure of transmission of peristaltic waves. It mainly occurs following laparotomy. A <strong>CT scan</strong> is more accurate for differentiating functional from mechanical obstruction. The image given below is an X-ray of the paralytic ileus showing <strong>gas-filled loops</strong> of the intestine with multiple fluid levels. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Differences between the small bowel and large bowel obstruction:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 322.9375px; height: 226px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\"><strong>Small bowel</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\"><strong>Large bowel</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\">Valvulae conniventes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\">Present in jejunum</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\">No of Loops</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\">Many</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\">Few</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\">Distribution of loops</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\">Central</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\">Peripheral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\">Haustra</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\">Diameter</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\">3-5 cm</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\">&gt;5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105px;\">Solid feces</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 101px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px; width: 105.9375px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Colonic diverticula</strong> are almost all false diverticula, i.e., mucosa herniating through a defect in the muscularis layer, typically covered by overlying serosa (where present). The given barium enema study shows a <strong>saw-tooth</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> due to <strong>barium-filled outpouchings</strong>, suggestive of colonic diverticulosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0602","text":"An elderly man is being ev‍aluated for constipation ​and the finding seen on i‍maging studies is given i​n the image below. What i‍s the probable diagnosis?​","raw_text":"An elderly man is being evaluated for constipation and the finding seen on imaging studies is given in the image below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ceacal ‍volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcino‍ma colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intussu‍sception","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sigmoid volvulus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ab94391a6d0491eab023c94cefcd250.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sigmoid volvulus</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, an elderly man presenting with constipation, along with a barium enema image showing the \"<strong>bird of prey</strong>\" sign, is highly suggestive of <strong>sigmoid volvulus. </strong></p>\n<p>This radiological sign, observed during the barium enema study, represents smooth tapered narrowing at the point of torsion of the sigmoid colon, looking like a hooked beak.</p>\n<p>Sigmoid volvulus is a cause of large bowel obstruction and occurs when the sigmoid colon <strong>twists </strong>on the sigmoid mesocolon. Symptoms include constipation, abdominal bloating, nausea, and/or vomiting. The onset may be acute or chronic. It occurs more commonly in patients with chronic constipation.</p>\n<p>X-ray of the abdomen shows a large, dilated loop of the colon, often with a few air-fluid levels. Specific signs include the <strong>coffee bean sign</strong>/bent inner tube sign, <strong>Frimann-Dahl</strong> sign(three dense lines converge towards the site of obstruction), and the absence of rectal gas. CT may reveal a large gas-filled loop <strong>without haustral markings</strong>, forming a closed-loop obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4011","text":"Identify the artery marked‍ by blue arrow.","raw_text":"Identify the artery marked by blue arrow.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior mesenteric artery‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior ‍mesenteric","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Celiac‍ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intestin‍al artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior mesenteric artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4cbf4e5b8e1b4a9e8cc2a97b61fec7f7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>The artery marked by the blue arrow is<strong> </strong>the <strong>superior mesenteric artery (SMA), </strong>which gives rise to various branches that supply the small intestines, cecum, ascending, and part of the transverse colon.</p>\n<p>The CT also depicts<strong> nutcracker syndrome,</strong> a left renal vein <strong>entrapment syndrome</strong> characterized by extrinsic <strong>compression</strong> of the<strong> left renal vein </strong>(marked by a red arrow),<strong> </strong>most commonly between the abdominal aorta and superior mesenteric artery. This syndrome is one of the postulated reasons for <strong>left-sided varicoceles</strong> being more common than the right side.<br/> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0603","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‍ts with chronic bloody di​arrhea and abdominal pain‍. A barium enema was done​ and is as shown. What is‍ her likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presents with chronic bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain. A barium enema was done and is as shown. What is her likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ‍e colitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‍ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crohns ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ulcerative colitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8abbcdf657964919ba30ac567cdf09a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ulcerative colitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the<strong> lead pipe</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> of the colon in the barium study is suggestive of<strong> ulcerative colitis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ulcerative colitis is an inflammatory bowel disease resulting in <strong>superficial</strong> erosions confined to the mucosa and submucosa of the colon. It starts from the rectum and extends proximally in a continuous manner. Clinical features include abdominal pain, diarrhea (often with blood), rectal bleeding, tenesmus, and passage of mucus. </p>\n<p>On barium studies, the lead pipe appearance refers to the complete <strong>loss </strong>of<strong> haustral markings</strong> in the affected section of the colon, which appears smooth-walled and cylindrical, resulting from chronic inflammation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0606","text":"A young man presented with‍ weight loss, and recurre​nt episodes of non-bloody‍ diarrhea for the past 6 ​months. CT abdomen was do‍ne and is shown below. Wh​at is the likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A young man presented with weight loss, and recurrent episodes of non-bloody diarrhea for the past 6 months. CT abdomen was done and is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ‍e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn’s‍ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc‍ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinom‍a caecum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crohn’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ebea9252a4194223acd868151dee452c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the CT abdomen showing <strong>comb sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Crohn's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The comb sign refers to the <strong>hypervascular appearance </strong>of the<strong> mesentery </strong>in active Crohn's disease. The fibrofatty proliferation and perivascular inflammatory infiltration outline the distended intestinal arcades. This forms linear densities on the mesenteric side of the affected segments of the small bowel, which give the appearance of the teeth of a comb.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0607","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‍ted with a low-grade feve​r associated with loss of‍ appetite and weight loss​. A barium meal follow-th‍rough was done and is as ​shown. What is the probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with a low-grade fever associated with loss of appetite and weight loss. A barium meal follow-through was done and is as shown. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ileoca‍ecal TB","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colonic div‍erticulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crohn’s‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinom‍a caecum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ileocaecal TB","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d55d001c8f0142fea361e3d60c0cc3fc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/441f7bfcb9b849b780a16a65f44a160ex1200x1493.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ileocaecal TB</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the barium study findings is suggestive of <strong>ileocaecal tuberculosis</strong>. The given image shows dilated proximal ileum, narrowed terminal ileum and ileocaecal junction, and shrunken caecum.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ileocaecal junction</strong> is the commonest site of tuberculosis in the gastrointestinal tract. Other sites involved in the abdomen are the liver, spleen, and peritoneum.</p>\n<p>Barium meal follow-through study findings in <strong>ileocaecal tuberculosis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Dilatation of proximal ileum</li>\n<li>Narrowed terminal ileum (<strong>string sign</strong>) and ileocaecal junction</li>\n<li><strong>Gooseneck ileus </strong>– ileum hanging from pulled up caecum</li>\n<li>The ileocaecal angle becomes <strong>obtuse</strong></li>\n<li>Caecum appears shrunken and pulled up away from the right iliac fossa</li>\n<li>Conical caecum</li>\n<li>Fleischner sign</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0608","text":"Which of the following sig‍ns is seen on barium stud​ies in patients with ileo‍caecal TB?","raw_text":"Which of the following signs is seen on barium studies in patients with ileocaecal TB?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fleisch‍ner sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Frimann D‍ahl's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diamon‍d sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Targe‍t sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fleischner sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/474c47a999aa45d89c1937888feb3bfdx1200x1493.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cf5715cdf314c57bed044acd3d3b963x547x536.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fleischner sign</strong></p><p><strong>Fleischner sign</strong> observed in barium studies of <strong>ileocaecal tuberculosis </strong>describes a widely gaping, thickened, patulous ileocaecal valve alongside a narrowed and ulcerated terminal ileum.</p>\n<p><strong>Intestinal tuberculosis</strong>, caused by <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> (often secondary to pulmonary tuberculosis) or by <em>Mycobacterium bovis</em> (as a primary GI infection), most commonly affects the <strong>ileocaecal junction</strong>. Symptoms include cough, evening rise in temperature, weight loss, malaise, abdominal pain with distension, and intermittent diarrhea. On palpation, the abdomen feels <strong>doughy</strong> and a <strong>mass</strong> may be palpated in the right iliac fossa.</p>\n<p>The provided single-contrast barium enema image shows narrowing of the terminal ileum with fine contour irregularities, contracted cecum, and a widely patent ileocecal valve- characteristic of ileocaecal tuberculosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Fleischner sign could also refer to a prominent central pulmonary artery seen in <strong>pulmonary embolism</strong> or <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0609","text":"String sign on radiologica‍l imaging is seen in all ​of the following conditio‍ns except?","raw_text":"String sign on radiological imaging is seen in all of the following conditions except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ileocaecal ‍tuberculosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertrophic pyloric steno‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crohn’s‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulcerativ‍e colitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ulcerative colitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2981c407376426d8cbe9d53bc811e83x720x844.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0faeb383a1d48c8b4765e32e69a2b57x1275x1253.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99e55fe37f0648b985db53ee3aedd702x1200x1493.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ulcerative colitis</strong></p><p>String sign is <strong>not</strong> seen in <strong>ulcerative colitis.</strong></p>\n<p>String sign refers to the thin, <strong>string-like appearance</strong> of a severely narrowed bowel loop on radiographic imaging. This narrowing occurs due to spasms, edema, inflammation or ulceration.<strong> </strong>It is commonly seen in Crohn’s disease (string sign of Kantor), hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, and ileocaecal tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option C: Given below is the barium study showing string sign of Kantor seen in <strong>Crohn's disease:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Given below are images showing <strong>congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: Given below is a barium enema showing string sign in <strong>ileocaecal tuberculosis:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0610","text":"A 59-year-old man is being‍ evaluated for a change i​n bowel habits associated‍ with abdominal discomfor​t. A barium enema study w‍as done and is shown belo​w. What is the likely dia​gnosis of this patient?","raw_text":"A 59-year-old man is being evaluated for a change in bowel habits associated with abdominal discomfort. A barium enema study was done and is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ileoca‍ecal TB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Irritable bo‍wel syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcino‍ma colon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid div‍erticulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carcinoma colon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13b96d5d964542dcbb6442946f1137e4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0bb28b9c07740b095e8002a8882706ex1280x991.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carcinoma colon</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, along with the given image showing the '<strong>apple core appearance</strong>' in barium enema,<strong> </strong>is suggestive of <strong>carcinoma colon.</strong></p>\n<p>Apple core sign/<strong>napkin ring</strong> sign describes a short segment of irregular circumferential stricture involving the large bowel. It has shouldered margins resembling an apple core. The appearance is classically described on contrast enema studies and is almost always due to a stenosing annular colon carcinoma. </p>\n<p>Colon carcinoma typically arises in patients above the age group of 50 years. It arises from the adenomatous polyps after a <strong>mutation sequence</strong> of adenoma-carcinoma influenced by environmental factors. Most colon cancers occur in the<strong> left</strong> colon. The clinical features of colon carcinoma include a <strong>change in bowel habits</strong> or rectal bleeding in cases of left-sided colon tumors and <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong> or a mass in proximal lesions of the colon.</p>\n<p><strong>Colonoscopy</strong> is the investigation of choice in suspected colorectal carcinoma. Double-contrast barium enema shows a constant <strong>irregular filling defect</strong> known as the apple core appearance. Computed tomography (CT) virtual colonoscopy has replaced barium enema as it is highly sensitive. <strong>Spiral CT</strong> of the chest, abdomen and pelvis is now the standard means for staging colon cancer.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the apple-core sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0611","text":"A barium enema study of a ‍patient is given below. W​hat is the likely diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"A barium enema study of a patient is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colonic div‍erticulosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colonic‍ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulcerativ‍e colitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colonic diverticulosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/29ab2255b4764dfc8dbadcf3fc4a7311.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3cd98545cc6444ea55a55c0401d9c92x1280x1061.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colonic diverticulosis</strong></p><p>The given barium enema study is showing a <strong>saw tooth appearance</strong> suggestive of <strong>colonic diverticulosis</strong>. Both single and double-contrast barium enemas are able to demonstrate diverticula as <strong>barium-filled outpouchings.</strong></p>\n<p>Colonic diverticula are almost all <strong>false diverticula </strong>i.e., mucosa herniating through a defect in the muscularis and covered by overlying serosa (where present). This herniation typically occurs where <strong>nutrient arteries enter the colon</strong>, and therefore, are more common on the mesenteric side of the colon. Colonic diverticula are most common in the <strong>sigmoid</strong> <strong>colon</strong> and, to a lesser extent, in the descending colon.</p>\n<p>Given below is the barium enema study of colonic diverticulosis, the arrows are pointing to the barium-filled outpouching suggestive of diverticula.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0612","text":"A two-day-old neonate with‍ abdominal distension and​ failure to pass meconium‍ underwent a barium enema​ study, as shown below. W‍hat is the most probable ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A two-day-old neonate with abdominal distension and failure to pass meconium underwent a barium enema study, as shown below.  What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anal a‍tresia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hirschsprun‍g’s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Meconiu‍m ileus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic m‍egacolon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hirschsprung’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2c83183e1de45aca93b4c6ebc9553ab.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/769f39778579470fa5853772dd4e243cx1279x1554.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hirschsprung’s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the barium enema showing a dilated right colon, with a transition zone in the mid-descending colon, are together suggestive of <strong>Hirschsprung disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The most important sign on barium enema is <strong>a sudden change of calibre </strong>from the narrow aganglionic segment and dilated proximal bowel.</p>\n<p>Hirschsprung disease is characterized by <strong>aganglionosis</strong> (absence of ganglion cells) in the distal colon and rectum. This may occur due to the failure of neuroblasts in neural crest cells to migrate into bowel segments or the degeneration of already migrated neuroblasts.  Hence, <strong>functional obstruction</strong> develops as a result of a spasm in the denervated colon. </p>\n<p>Clinical features typically include <strong>failure</strong> to <strong>pass meconium</strong> within the first 48 hours of life, abdominal distension, vomiting, and constipation. Hirschsprung disease may present with <strong>enterocolitis</strong>, a severe complication that can be life-threatening. There is a notable association between Hirschsprung disease and <strong>Down's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Water-soluble contrast enema</strong> is the initial investigation and diagnostic test of choice. It shows a narrow aganglionic segment, a conical transition zone, and a dilated proximal bowel. <strong>Rectal biopsy</strong> is the gold standard investigation.</p>\n<p>The image below shows dilated ganglionic and contracted aganglionic segments in Hirschsprung disease:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0594","text":"A newborn presented with s‍evere respiratory distres​s soon after birth. A che‍st X-ray was done and is ​shown below. What is the ‍most probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A newborn presented with severe respiratory distress soon after birth. A chest X-ray was done and is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchog‍enic cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital diaphragmatic h‍ernia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital cystic adenomat‍oid malformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic ‍teratoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c16ee66ca34248c69021b0269abf697e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e960a2592f64a42982a69b45d8505bdx251x254.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d2265ac4cd22452480fae1ddc1e40f76x611x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia</strong></p><p>A newborn presenting with respiratory distress shortly after birth, along with the chest X-ray showing bowel loops in the thoracic cavity, is suggestive of <strong>congenital diaphragmatic hernia </strong>(CDH).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1892\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1892\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Normally, the pleuroperitoneal cavities separate by 8-10 weeks of gestation. CDH is due to<strong> </strong>a <strong>failure </strong>to close the <strong>pleuroperitoneal canal</strong> in the developing fetus. This malformation allows the abdominal organs to push into the chest cavity, impairing lung development and function. The most common type of CDH is a <strong>Bochdalek hernia</strong>, which usually involves the <strong>left</strong> side. Mortality is predominantly due to the development of <strong>pulmonary hypoplasia.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Chest X-ray findings in congenital diaphragmatic hernia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bowel loops in the thorax</li>\n<li>Indistinct diaphragm with opacification of part of or all the hemithorax (typically left-sided)</li>\n<li>Deviation of lines such as the endotracheal tube, nasogastric tube, umbilical arterial and venous catheters.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Given below is the chest radiograph of Bochdalek hernia.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below is the chest radiograph of Morgagni hernia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Bag and mask ventilation is to be <strong>avoided</strong> in the management of this baby. Bag and mask ventilation may increase abdominal distension and limit the expansion of the hypoplastic lungs. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ybcS2AhQ","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"10_carcinoma_breast___staging__prognosis___molecular_types","title":"10.Carcinoma Breast   Staging, Prognosis & Molecular Types","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB9905","text":"Tis classification is used​ for the staging of: Duct​al carcinoma in situ (DCI​S) Lobular carcinoma in s‌itu (LCIS) Paget’s diseas‍e Inflammatory carcinoma ‌Colloid carcinoma","raw_text":"<p>Tis classification is used for the staging of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS)</li>\n<li>Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS)</li>\n<li>Paget’s disease</li>\n<li>Inflammatory carcinoma</li>\n<li>Colloid carcinoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ​and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Tis</strong> classification is used for the staging of <strong>ductal carcinoma in situ</strong> and <strong>Paget’s disease </strong>only.</p>\n<p>Tis means carcinoma in-situ. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tis (DCIS) - </strong>includes ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS)</li>\n<li><strong>Tis (Paget) - </strong>includes<strong> </strong>Paget's disease of the nipple NOT associated with evident invasive carcinoma and/or carcinoma in situ (DCIS) in the underlying breast parenchyma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>According to the latest American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) classification system (8<sup>th</sup> edition), <strong>lobular carcinoma in situ</strong> (LCIS) is not included in Tis since it is a <strong>benign </strong>entity.</p>\n<p>LCIS is considered a proliferative disease that has a risk of developing breast carcinoma in the future, and hence, it is not included in the classification system. Pleomorphic LCIS is also considered a benign condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0986","text":"A postmenopausal lady pres​ented with painless lumps​ in the right breast. On ​examination, two lumps of‌ size 6 x 2 cm and 4 x 1 ‍cm were palpated in the u‌pper outer quadrant. Ipsi‌lateral mobile axillary l‌ymph nodes were felt. On ‌evaluation, there was no ‌distant metastasis. What ​is the TNM staging of thi‍s tumor?","raw_text":"A postmenopausal lady presented with painless lumps in the right breast. On examination, two lumps of size 6 x 2 cm and 4 x 1 cm were palpated in the upper outer quadrant. Ipsilateral mobile axillary lymph nodes were felt. On evaluation, there was no distant metastasis. What is the TNM staging of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T3 N​1 M0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T1 N​1 M0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T3 N​2 M0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T2 N​2 M0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T3 N1 M0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T3 N1 M0</strong></p><p>TNM staging of the tumor is <strong>T3 N1 M0</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the given scenario:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The bigger lump is 6 cm in size - <strong>T3</strong></li>\n<li>Ipsilateral mobile axillary lymph nodes - <strong>N1</strong> </li>\n<li>No distant metastasis - <strong>M0. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In tumor size assessment, the <strong>greatest dimension</strong> is taken into consideration. For example, in a 6 x 2 cm lump, 6 cm is taken into account, and the tumor is classified as T3 (&gt;5 cm). If 2 lumps are present in the same breast, staging is according to the <strong>bigger lump.</strong> If lumps are present in <strong>bilateral</strong> breasts,<strong> separate staging</strong> is done for both the lumps.</p>\n<p>Any<strong> contralateral lymph node</strong> involvement is considered as <strong>distant metastasis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0988","text":"A woman came with a 4 x 3 ​cm lump over the right br​east. On examination, the​ breast appears as shown ‌below. Ipsilateral suprac‍lavicular lymph nodes are‌ palpable with fixed axil‌lary nodes on the left si‌de. What is the most appr‌opriate tumor staging?","raw_text":"A woman came with a 4 x 3 cm lump over the right breast. On examination, the breast appears as shown below. Ipsilateral supraclavicular lymph nodes are palpable with fixed axillary nodes on the left side. What is the most appropriate tumor staging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T2 N​2a M0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T4a N​3c M0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T2 N​3c M1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T4d N​3c M1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T4d N3c M1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4d15c8c9dcb457895df99395f3fff8d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T4d N3c M1</strong></p><p>TNM staging in the given scenario is <strong>T4d N3c M1. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumour of <strong>any size</strong> with typical skin changes involving &gt; 1/3 of breast- <strong>T4d </strong>(Inflammatory breast carcinoma)</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral <strong>supraclavicular</strong> lymph node involvement - <strong>N3c </strong></li>\n<li>Involvement of any <strong>contralateral lymph nodes</strong> indicates distant metastasis - <strong>M1</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0987","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted to the clinic with a ​7 x 3 cm lump in the uppe​r outer quadrant of the r‌ight breast. On palpation‍, the lump was found to b‌e fixed to the pectoralis‌ major muscle. What is th‌e T staging of the tumor ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented to the clinic with a 7 x 3 cm lump in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast. On palpation, the lump was found to be fixed to the pectoralis major muscle. What is the T staging of the tumor in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​4b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T​3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T​4a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T​4c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T3</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, as the lump is 7 cm in size, the T stage of the tumor is<strong> T3</strong> ( <strong>&gt;5 cm</strong>).</p>\n<p>Irrelevant to the size of the tumor, if the tumor shows extension to the <strong>chest wall or skin</strong>, it is classified as <strong>T4</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, there are two <strong>exceptions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Invasion or adherence to <strong>pectoralis muscle</strong> in the absence of chest wall involvement does not qualify as T4</li>\n<li>Involvement of the <strong>dermis</strong> alone does not qualify as T4</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0127","text":"During immunohistochemistr​y for ER, PR status, stai​ning of what percentage o​f cells indicates positiv‌ity?","raw_text":"During immunohistochemistry for ER, PR status, staining of what percentage of cells indicates positivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5​0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/187a226d0f3f4975af72ac501241ff12x720x341.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1%</strong></p><p>The staining of <strong>≥1% of cells</strong> is considered <strong>positive.</strong> Expression of estrogen receptor (ER) and progesterone receptor (PR) in breast cancer cells is mainly assessed by immunohistochemistry.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1033","text":"A trucut biopsy is obtaine​d from a woman who presen​ted with a breast lump. T​he biopsy confirms the pr‌esence of mucinous carcin‍oma. Immunohistochemistry‌ shows ER, PR and HER2/ne‌u positive. What is the m‌olecular subtype of breas‌t cancer in this patient?‌","raw_text":"A trucut biopsy is obtained from a woman who presented with a breast lump. The biopsy confirms the presence of mucinous carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry shows ER, PR and HER2/neu positive.  What is the molecular subtype of breast cancer in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Luminal​ A type","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal​ type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Luminal​ B type","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Her 2​ type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Luminal B type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Luminal B type</strong></p><p>Breast cancer with ER, PR <strong>+ve</strong> and HER2/neu <strong>+ve</strong> status will be classified as <strong>luminal B type</strong>. It is also known as <strong>triple-positive cancer</strong>. </p>\n<p>Along with ER, PR, and HER2/neu, a <strong>proliferation index</strong> known as the <strong>Ki67 </strong>index is also used.</p>\n<p>Molecular classification of cancers :</p>\n<table style=\"width: 642px; height: 259px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.65625px; height: 32px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 424.359375px; height: 32px;\"><strong>Properties</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.65625px; height: 78px;\">Normal-like</td>\n<td style=\"width: 424.359375px; height: 78px;\">\n<p> Normal histology</p>\n<p> ER, PR <strong>+ve ,</strong>HER2/neu <strong>-ve</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.65625px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Luminal A (<strong>most common</strong>/</p>\n<p>best prognosis)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 424.359375px; height: 78px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p> ER, PR <strong>+ve, </strong>HER2/neu <strong>-ve </strong>(Low Ki)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.65625px; height: 78px;\">Luminal B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 424.359375px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>ER, PR <strong>+ve</strong>, HER2/neu <strong>-ve</strong>, Ki-67- <strong>High<br/></strong></p>\n<p>ER, PR <strong>+ve</strong>, HER2/neu <strong>+ve</strong>, Ki-67 <strong>Any</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.65625px; height: 96px;\">Basal cell (worst prognosis)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 424.359375px; height: 96px;\">\n<p> <strong>Triple-negative</strong></p>\n<p> (High Ki), Positive for myoepithelial markers - EGFR, CK  5,6,17</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 201.65625px; height: 46px;\">HER2-enriched type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 424.359375px; height: 46px;\">\n<p> ER &amp; PR <strong>–ve, </strong>HER2/neu <strong>+ve</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1545","text":"In breast cancer patients,​ FISH for gene amplificat​ion will be done based on​ which of the following I‌HC staining for Her-2/neu‍?","raw_text":"In breast cancer patients, FISH for gene amplification will be done based on which of the following IHC staining for Her-2/neu?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2​+","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3​+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gene Amplification is done​ irrespective of Her 2 Ne​u status","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/982edecb127d4d319fad21d8da943b19x720x341.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2+</strong></p><p><strong>Fluorescence in situ hybridization </strong>(FISH)<strong> </strong>should be performed in <strong>all cases of breast tumors</strong> with an immunohistochemistry (<strong>IHC</strong>)<strong> score of 2+.</strong></p>\n<p>Expression of estrogen receptor (ER), progesterone receptor (PR), and HER2/neu in breast cancer cells is mainly assessed by immunohistochemistry. <strong>HER2/neu IHC interpretation:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>1+ = Negative</li>\n<li>2+ = Equivocal - <strong>FISH</strong> is done to <strong>differentiate</strong> whether the tumor cells are positive or negative for <strong>HER2/neu</strong> expression</li>\n<li>3+ = Positive HER2/neu- complete circumferential membrane staining in &gt;10% of tumor cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows HER2/neu, ER, and PR positivity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0120","text":"Which of the following is ​true about Oncotype Dx, a​ molecular test used for ​breast cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Oncotype Dx, a molecular test used for breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Used for hormone receptor–​negative cancers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Is a 21-g​ene assay","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Helpful in predicting the ​advantage of adding radio​therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used for lymph node–positi​ve cancers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Is a 21-gene assay","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Is a 21-gene assay</strong></p><p><strong>Oncotype Dx assay</strong> is a molecular test based on the assessment of <strong>21</strong> genes.</p>\n<p><strong>Higher</strong> <strong>scores</strong> predict increased<strong> benefits</strong> from <strong>chemotherapy</strong>. On the other hand, <strong>lower scores</strong> in this assay predict lesser benefit from chemotherapy and increased <strong>benefit</strong> from <strong>endocrine therapy.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 878\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is used for patients with lymph <strong>node-negative</strong>, <strong>ER-positive</strong> breast cancer.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0123","text":"After performing Oncotype ​Dx for a breast tumor, a ​low recurrence score is o​btained. Which of the fol‌lowing is true regarding ‍management in this patien‌t?","raw_text":"After performing Oncotype Dx for a breast tumor, a low recurrence score is obtained. Which of the following is true regarding management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tumor resection with chemo​therapy should be done","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brachytherapy with chemoth​erapy should be done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemotherapy should be use​d along with hormonal the​rapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is no advantage in a​dding chemotherapy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. There is no advantage in adding chemotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. There is no advantage in adding chemotherapy</strong></p><p>A <strong>low</strong> recurrence score after Oncotype Dx suggests that there is <strong>no survival</strong> <strong>advantage</strong> of adding <strong>chemotherapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>A high recurrence score, on the other hand, suggests that chemotherapy should be added.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1035","text":"Which of the following is ​not related to breast car​cinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not related to breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nottingham prognostic inde​x","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gail and C​laus models","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Van Nuys pro​gnostic index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MACIS progn​ostic index","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MACIS prognostic index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MACIS prognostic index</strong></p><p><strong>MACIS</strong> is not used in breast cancer. It is a prognostic index for <strong>thyroid cancer</strong>.  </p>\n<p>The Nottingham prognostic index, Gail and Claus models, and Van Nuys prognostic index<strong> </strong>are all <strong>associated</strong> with breast cancer assessment.</p>\n<p><strong>Van Nuys prognostic index</strong> is used to <strong>identify</strong> patients with duct carcinoma in situ (<strong>DCIS</strong>) who do not need radiation therapy. This grading system is based on the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patient’s age</li>\n<li>DCIS nuclear grade and presence of microcalcification</li>\n<li>Size of the lesion</li>\n<li>Width of the surgical margin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> Nottingham prognostic index </strong>segregates the patients into four groups and is used to predict the <strong>5-year survival</strong>. It helps to formulate treatment plans.</p>\n<p>Nottingham Prognostic Index = (0.2 × Tumor size in cm) + grade + nodes</p>\n<p>The <strong>Gail and Claus models </strong>are used for <strong>assessing</strong> the <strong>risk </strong>of development of<strong> breast carcinoma. </strong>It<strong> </strong>includes the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Number of first-degree relatives with carcinoma breast</li>\n<li>Age at menarche</li>\n<li>Age at first live birth</li>\n<li>Number of breast biopsies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Claus model</strong> provides individual estimates of <strong>breast cancer risk</strong> according to the decade of life, based on the presence of first- and second-degree relatives with breast cancer and their age at diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Note: Neither the Gail model nor the Claus model accounts for the risk associated with mutations in the breast cancer susceptibility genes BRCA1 and BRCA2.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1031","text":"Which of the following is ​the most important progno​stic factor in a patient ​with breast carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important prognostic factor in a patient with breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Size of ​the tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Axillary lymph node involv​ement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Involvement of skin over t​he breast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recepto​r status","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Axillary lymph node involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Axillary lymph node involvement</strong></p><p><strong>Axillary</strong> <strong>lymph node status</strong> is the most important determinant in the <strong>staging and prognosis</strong> of women with breast cancer.</p>\n<p>In axillary node-negative breast cancer, ER, PR receptor status becomes the most important prognostic factor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3781","text":"The Van Nuys grading syste​m for ductal carcinoma in​ situ does not include:","raw_text":"The Van Nuys grading system for ductal carcinoma in situ does not include:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age of th​e patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Size of ​the tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hormone rece​ptor staining","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of microcalcifica​tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hormone receptor staining","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hormone receptor staining</strong></p><p>Staining for estrogen and progesterone receptors is not part of the Van Nuys system used for grading DCIS. </p>\n<p>The components of the <strong>Van Nuys prognostic index</strong> (VNPI) are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Size of the tumor</li>\n<li>Extent of resection margin </li>\n<li>Histological variant of DCIS - based on nuclear grade, presence of necrosis, presence of microcalcification</li>\n<li>Age of the patient</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with a<strong> high score</strong> in this grading system benefit from <strong>radiotherapy after excision, </strong>whereas those with <strong>low-grade</strong> and <strong>completely excised</strong> tumors, need <strong>no further treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>A score ranging from<strong> 1 </strong>(lesions with the <strong>best prognosis</strong>) to <strong>3 </strong>(lesions with the <strong>worst prognosis</strong>) is given for each of the four prognostic predictors. Total scores range from 4 (least likely to recur) to 12 (most likely to recur).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 26px;\" width=\"655\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 645px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Silverstein Van Nuys prognostic index for DCIS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 72px;\" width=\"654\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Parameters</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Score 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Score 2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Score 3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Size(mm)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">≤15</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">16-40</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">≥41</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Margin(mm)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">≥10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">1-9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">&lt;1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">Grade 1 or 2 without necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">Grade 1 or 2 with necrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">Grade 3 with or without necrosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\"><strong>Age(years)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">&gt;60</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">40-60</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">&lt;40</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 39px;\" width=\"654\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 644px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Scores 4-7 with &gt;3mm margin: <strong>Excision alone</strong> </li>\n<li>Score 7 with &lt; 3 mm margin, score 8 with &gt; 3mm margin, score 9 with &gt;5mm margin: <strong>Excision with RT</strong></li>\n<li>Score 9 with &lt; 5mm margin &amp; score 10,11,12: <strong>Mastectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: The type of <strong>microcalcification</strong> seen on imaging, helps in determining the <strong>nuclear grade</strong> of DCIS.<strong> </strong>While<strong> </strong>the<strong> linear</strong> <strong>branching</strong> type of microcalcification is associated with <strong>high </strong>nuclear grade, <strong>fine granular</strong> type is associated with <strong>low </strong>nuclear grade.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1169","text":"A 42-year-old woman presen​ted with a history of pai​nless breast lump measuri​ng 6.2 X 4.5 cm in the le‌ft upper outer quadrant a‍nd no axillary nodes. Cor‌e cut biopsy showed ducta‌l carcinoma in situ. She ‌underwent tumor resection‌ with adequate margins an‌d postoperative HPE showe​d DCIS with intermediate-‍grade necrosis and with 6​-mm clearance on margins.​ What is the next step of​ treatment?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old woman presented with a history of painless breast lump measuring 6.2 X 4.5 cm in the left upper outer quadrant and no axillary nodes. Core cut biopsy showed ductal carcinoma in situ. She underwent tumor resection with adequate margins and postoperative HPE showed DCIS with intermediate-grade necrosis and with 6-mm clearance on margins. What is the next step of treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No addition​al treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adjuvant r​adiotherapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adjuvant c​hemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adjuvant chemoradiotherapy​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adjuvant radiotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adjuvant radiotherapy</strong></p><p>According to Silverstein <strong>Van Nuys prognostic index</strong> for DCIS, the <strong>total score is 9. </strong>Hence, the patient would require <strong>adjuvant radiotherapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Age: 42 years (score 2), size: 62mm (score 3), clearance margin: 6mm (score 2) with intermediate-grade necrotic features (score 2).</p>\n<p>Silverstein Van Nuys prognostic index for DCIS:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 196px; width: 81.1252%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.6461%;\">Parameters </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.6737%;\">Score 1 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7384%;\">Score 2 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.6568%;\">Score 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.6461%;\">Age (years) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.6737%;\">≥61 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7384%;\">40-60</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.6568%;\">&lt;40 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.6461%;\">Size (mm) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.6737%;\">≤15 mm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7384%;\">16-40 mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.6568%;\">&gt;40 mm </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.6461%;\">Margin (mm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.6737%;\">≥10 mm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7384%;\">1-9 mm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.6568%;\">&lt;1 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.6461%;\">Class (grade and necrosis)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.6737%;\">\n<p>Grades 1 and 2 </p>\n<p>no necrosis </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7384%;\">\n<p>Grades 1 or 2</p>\n<p>with necrosis </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.6568%;\">Grade 3 with or without necrosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Scores 4-6 with &gt;3mm margin: <strong>Excision alone </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Score 7 with &lt; 3 mm margin, score 8 with &gt; 3mm margin, score 9 with &gt;5mm margin: <strong>Excision with RT</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Score 9 with &lt; 5mm margin &amp; score 10-12: <strong>Mastectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the patient is <strong>ER/PR+</strong>, adjuvant <strong>hormonal therapy</strong> with tamoxifen or an aromatase inhibitor is indicated. Current data does not support the use of HER-2-directed therapy in DCIS. </p>\n<p>Indications for adjuvant radiotherapy in breast cancer: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Locally advanced breast cancers (T3, T4, N1, N2, N3)</li>\n<li>Following breast conservation surgery </li>\n<li>After mastectomy if tumor size ≥5 cm, skin or chest wall involvement, lymphovascular invasion and axillary node-positive for metastasis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Post-treatment surveillance</strong> after treatment for DCIS includes history, physical examination, and routine mammography (if applicable).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fpLiCI_q","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"11_ventilation_and_o2_delivery_systems","title":"11.Ventilation And O2 Delivery Systems","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2616","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a low-flow variable p‌erformance device?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a low-flow variable performance device?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal ‌cannula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-rebrea‌thing mask","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal‌ mask","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaesth‌etic bag","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anaesthetic bag","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anaesthetic bag</strong></p><p><strong>Anaesthetic bag</strong> or the bag-mask-valve system is a <strong>high flow fixed performance</strong> system - the delivered FiO<sub>2</sub> is not affected by changes in ventilatory level or breathing pattern of the patient and thus deliver accurate oxygen concentration. It is of two types: the self-inflating bags with a reservoir of 1.5 L and the non-self-inflating bags with a reservoir of 1, 2 or 3 L.</p>\n<p>The disadvantage is high cost and poor patient tolerability due to high flow. The other fixed performance devices include venturi mask, air-entraining nebulizers, and high flow air oxygen systems.</p>\n<p>The nasal cannula, non–rebreathing mask, and nasal mask are low flow variable performance systems. Oxygen is delivered at a low, fixed flow which is only a portion of the inspired gas mixture. They are cheap with better patient tolerability. The disadvantage is that the FiO<sub>2</sub> delivered cannot be accurately predicted, so they are suitable for patients with stable breathing patterns.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"width: 449px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219px;\">High flow systems  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Low flow systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219px;\">Fixed performance systems</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Variable performance systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219px;\">\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>venturi mask</li>\n<li>air-entraining nebulizers</li>\n<li>high flow air oxygen systems</li>\n<li>anaesthetic bag </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>nasal cannula</li>\n<li>non–rebreathing mask</li>\n<li>nasal mask</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2617","text":"What is the maximum percen‌tage of oxygen that can b‌e delivered via nasal can​nula?","raw_text":"What is the maximum percentage of oxygen that can be delivered via nasal cannula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10‌0 %","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 40%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 40%</strong></p><p>The<strong> nasal cannula </strong>is a <strong>low flow variable</strong> performance oxygen delivery system that can achieve a maximum inspired oxygen concentration of <strong>40-50</strong> <strong>%</strong> if <strong>flow rates are greater than</strong> <strong>10L/ min</strong> for short periods.</p>\n<p>Flow rates greater than 5 L/min are not tolerated because of the discomfort associated with this high flow entering the nasal cavity and also due to drying and crusting of the nasal mucosa.</p>\n<p>At flow rates of 3-4 L, it can provide an inspired oxygen concentration of 30-35%.</p>\n<p>In mouth-to-mouth resuscitation, the percentage of oxygen delivered is 16%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2618","text":"In which of the following ‌situations should nasal c‌annula be avoided?","raw_text":"In which of the following situations should nasal cannula be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Requiring inspiratory oxyg‌en flow rate of 45 L/min","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Minute ventilation of 8 L/‌min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory rate at 15 bre‌aths/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tidal volume less than 0.8‌ L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Requiring inspiratory oxygen flow rate of 45 L/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Requiring inspiratory oxygen flow rate of 45 L/min</strong></p><p>A <strong>nasal cannula</strong> is a <strong>low flow variable</strong> performance oxygen delivery system. It is not suited for patients requiring high FiO2 or inspiratory oxygen flow rates greater than 40 L/min for which devices like venturi mask, anaesthetic bag, air-entraining nebulizers, or high flow air oxygen systems are preferred.</p>\n<p>Indications of a nasal cannula and other low-flow oxygen delivery systems include </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minute ventilation &lt; 8–10 L/min</li>\n<li>Breathing frequencies &lt; 20 breaths/min</li>\n<li>Tidal volumes (V T ) &lt; 0.8 L</li>\n<li>Normal inspiratory flow (10–30 L/min).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2619","text":"What is the maximum oxygen‌ concentration delivered ‌by a venturi mask?","raw_text":"What is the maximum oxygen concentration delivered by a venturi mask?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3fad502374345ae88aecda0859dbb6ax1280x1224.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60%</strong></p><p>Venturi mask is a <strong>high-flow, fixed-performance</strong> oxygen delivery system and the highest oxygen concentration (<strong>FiO<sub>2</sub></strong>) delivered is <strong>60% at a flow rate of 15 L/min.</strong></p>\n<p>It is available in different colors, with the green valve venturi mask providing 60% FiO<sub>2. </sub></p>\n<p>This is useful with patients who require greater FiO<sub>2 </sub>such as COPD patients, where it is important to provide controlled oxygenation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"width: 335px; height: 171px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Colour of Venturi valve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">FiO2 (%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">Flow rate <br/>(L/min)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Blue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">24 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">White</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">28 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Orange</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">31 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Yellow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">35 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Red</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">40 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Green</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">60 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 79px;\">15</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2614","text":"An 18-year-old boy is rush‌ed to the emergency depar‌tment after having choked​ on a bone while eating. ‌He is responsive but unab‌le to speak. What is to b‍e done next?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy is rushed to the emergency department after having choked on a bone while eating. He is responsive but unable to speak. What is to be done next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bag and mask‌ ventilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoscopi‌c removal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deliver 5 backslaps and 5 ‌chest compressions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform Heim‌lich maneuver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Perform Heimlich maneuver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c96e24d3b1f4fafab682fe5a100eebex510x442.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7bbb754ab7d24e128f1d60c28afc9c8ax1280x2905.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Perform Heimlich maneuver</strong></p><p>In a choking <strong>child</strong> (more than 1 year old) or an <strong>adult,</strong> the first step to be done is to perform <strong>Heimlich</strong> maneuver which involves delivering <strong>abdominal thrusts.</strong></p>\n<p>The rescuer should stand or kneel behind the patient and wrap his arms around the patient’s waist. A fist is made with one hand and using the other hand, the fist is driven into the patient’s abdomen with a rapid, forceful upward thrust so as to dislodge the foreign body. This is continued until the foreign body is expelled or if the patient becomes unresponsive.</p>\n<p>If the patient becomes <strong>unresponsive</strong>, immediately <strong>start</strong> <strong>CPR</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> In a choking <strong>infant </strong>who is <strong>responsive, 5 back slaps between the shoulder blades</strong> and<strong> 5 downward thrusts on the chest </strong>are given in order to dislodge the foreign body.</p>\n<p>If the patient becomes <strong>unresponsive</strong>, immediately <strong>start</strong> <strong>CPR</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6355","text":"In an intubated patient, a‌fter what duration of end‌otracheal intubation will​ you need to perform a tr‌acheostomy?","raw_text":"In an intubated patient, after what duration of endotracheal intubation will you need to perform a tracheostomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 mo‌nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Tracheostomy</strong> is performed in an intubated patient after <strong>2-3 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because if an orotracheal or nasotracheal tube is left in situ for <strong>more than 2-3 weeks, </strong>it results in <strong>subglottic stenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Hence it is ideal to replace the endotracheal tube with a <strong>cuffed tracheostomy tube.</strong></p>\n<p>If on intubation, it is perceived that the patient may require intubation for a period longer than 2 weeks, then tracheostomy can be performed soon after intubation.</p>\n<p>Tracheostomy has been found to reduce the incidence of pneumonia, the duration of mechanical ventilation, and the length of stay in hospitals for patients with major head injuries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2620","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for mec‌hanical ventilation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for mechanical ventilation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arterial oxygen tension &amp;l‌t; 50 mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial CO2 tension &amp;lt; ‌50 mm Hg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tidal volume‌ &amp;lt; 5 ml/kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vital capacity &amp;lt; 15 ml/‌kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterial CO2 tension < 50 mm Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterial CO2 tension &lt; 50 mm Hg</strong></p><p>Arterial CO2 tension &lt; 50 mm Hg is not an indication for mechanical ventilation. </p>\n<p>Indications of mechanical ventilation:</p>\n<p>Directly measured indices</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arterial oxygen</strong> tension <strong>&lt;50</strong> <strong>mmHg</strong> on room air</li>\n<li><strong>Arterial CO<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">2</span></strong> tension <strong>&gt;50 mmHg</strong> in the absence of metabolic alkalosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Derived indices</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PaO2 /FiO<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">2</span> ratio &lt;300 mm Hg</li>\n<li>PA–a O<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">2</span> gradient &gt;350 mm Hg</li>\n<li>V<span style=\"font-size: xx-small;\">d</span> /V<span style=\"font-size: xx-small;\">t</span> &gt;0.6</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical indices</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory rate &gt;35 breaths/min</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mechanical indices</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tidal volume &lt;5 mL/kg</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vital capacity &lt;15 mL/kg</strong></li>\n<li>Maximum Inspiratory Force &gt; −25 cm H 2 O</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7334","text":"Which of the following is ‌associated with respirato‌ry alkalosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with respiratory alkalosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Assisted control mode vent‌ilation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Controlled‌ mandatory","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pressure ‌controlled","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SI‌MV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Assisted control mode ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Assisted control mode ventilation</strong></p><p>Respiratory alkalosis can occur in Assist Control Mode Ventilation</p>\n<table style=\"height: 718px; width: 667px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Ventilatory Mode</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>ACMV</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>(Assist Control Mode Ventilation)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>IMV</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>(Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.453125px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>PSV</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>(Pressure Support Ventilation)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.546875px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>NIV</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><br/><strong>(Noninvasive Ventilation)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">\n<p>Variables set by User (Independent)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tidal volume</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ventilator rate</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s1\">F</span>io<span class=\"s2\">2</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">PEEP level</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tidal volume</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Mandatory ventilator rate</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s1\">F</span>io<span class=\"s2\">2</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">PEEP level</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Spontaneous breaths between assisted breaths</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.453125px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Inspiratory pressure level</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s1\">F</span>io<span class=\"s2\">2</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">PEEP</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.546875px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Inspiratory and expiratory pressure level</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span class=\"s1\">F</span>io<span class=\"s2\">2</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">\n<p>Variables Monitored by the user (Dependent)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Peak, mean, and plateau airway pressures</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">VE</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">ABG</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">I/E ratio</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Peak, mean, and plateau airway pressures</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">VE</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">ABG</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">I/E ratio</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.453125px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tidal volume</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Respiratory rate</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">VE</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">ABG</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.546875px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tidal volume</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Respiratory rate</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">VE</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">ABG</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Trigger cycle limit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Patient effort</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Timer</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Patient effort</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Timer</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.453125px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Inspiratory flow</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.546875px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Pressure limit</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Inspiratory flow</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Advantages</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"> \n<p class=\"p1\">Patient control</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Guaranteed ventilation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Patient control</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Comfort from spontaneous breaths</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Guaranteed ventilation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.453125px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Patient control</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Comfort</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Assures synchrony</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.546875px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Patient control</p>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Disadvantages</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Potential hyperventilation</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>(Leading to respiratory alkalosis)</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Barotrauma and volutrauma</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Every effective breath generates a ventilator volume</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Potential dysynchrony</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Potential hypoventilation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.453125px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">No timer backup</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Potential hypoventilation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.546875px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Mask interface may cause discomfort and facial bruising</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Leaks are common</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Hypoventilation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6361","text":"You are planning to wean a‌ patient off mechanical v‌entilation. Which of the ​following is not a criter‌ion to do so?","raw_text":"You are planning to wean a patient off mechanical ventilation. Which of the following is not a criterion to do so?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tidal volum‌e of 5 ml/kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vital capaci‌ty of 5 ml/kg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Minute ventilation of 10L/‌min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inspiratory pressure of −2‌5 cm H 2 O","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vital capacity of 5 ml/kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vital capacity of 5 ml/kg</strong></p><p>Weaning off mechanical ventilation is indicated when <strong>vital capacity </strong>is <strong>&gt;10 mL/kg</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6356","text":"Following a road traffic a‌ccident, an unresponsive ‌young man was brought to ​the emergency room. In or‌der to declare him as bra‌in dead, which of the fol‍lowing criteria can be us​ed? 1. Absent spinal cord​ reflex 2. Isoelectric el‌ectroencephalogram (EEG) ‍3. Absent brain stem refl​ex 4. Decorticate posturi‌ng 5. Positive apnea test‌","raw_text":"<p>Following a road traffic accident, an unresponsive young man was brought to the emergency room. In order to declare him as brain dead, which of the following criteria can be used?</p>\n<p>1. Absent spinal cord reflex</p>\n<p>2. Isoelectric electroencephalogram (EEG)</p>\n<p>3. Absent brain stem reflex</p>\n<p>4. Decorticate posturing</p>\n<p>5. Positive apnea test</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>Absent spinal cord reflex and decorticate posturing are not criteria for brain death. The statements 2, 3 and 5 are correct.</p>\n<p>Brain death is defined as the irreversible cessation of all brain function. Spinal cord function below C1 may still be present.</p>\n<p>Brain death criteria can be applied only in the absence of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypothermia </li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Uncorrected metabolic or endocrine abnormalities</li>\n<li>Neuromuscular blockers</li>\n<li>Drugs known to depress brain function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generally accepted <strong>clinical criteria</strong> for brain death include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coma</li>\n<li>Absent motor activity, including no decerebrate or decorticate posturing</li>\n<li>Spinal cord reflexes may be preserved </li>\n<li>Absent brainstem reflexes, including no pupillary, corneal, vestibulocochlear (caloric), or gag (or cough) reflexes</li>\n<li>Absence of ventilatory effort, with the arterial CO<sub>2</sub> tension &gt;60 mm Hg or 20 mm Hg above the pretest level (positive apnea test)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1295\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Confirmatory test findings</strong> (not mandatory but may assist in diagnosis)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Isoelectric electroencephalogram</li>\n<li>Absence of brainstem auditory evoked potentials</li>\n<li>Absence of cerebral perfusion as documented by angiographic, transcranial Doppler, or radioisotopic studies</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KM-DXVl-","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"11_cell_structure__epithelia__glands___connective_tissue","title":"11.Cell Structure, Epithelia, Glands & Connective Tissue","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB2411","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures together are​ ref​erred to as protoplasm?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures together are​ referred to as protoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma membrane and cytopl‌asm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytoplasm ‌and nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclear membrane and nucle‌oplasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytosol and‌ organelles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cytoplasm and nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7098024c61b45bdaa0d3e842663cc5cx1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cytoplasm and nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>cytoplasm</strong> and <strong>nucleus</strong> together are referred to as <strong>protoplasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>A cell is surrounded by a cell membrane (or plasma membrane), which encloses a complex material called protoplasm. It consists of a central, denser part called the <strong>nucleus,</strong> and an outer less dense part called the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>. The <strong>nucleus</strong> is surrounded by a <strong>nuclear membrane, </strong>which encloses chromatin, nucleolus, and nucleoplasm. The <strong>cytoplasm</strong> consists of <strong>cell</strong> <strong>organelles </strong>and<strong> </strong><strong>cytosol</strong> (also called hyaloplasm), which is the liquid component of the cytoplasm present outside the cell organelles. <strong>Vacuoplasm</strong> refers to the fluid-filled spaces enclosed within the membrane-bound cell organelles. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2415","text":"What is the cytoplasm pres‌ent within the channels o​f the endoplasmic reticul‌um called?","raw_text":"What is the cytoplasm present within the channels of the endoplasmic reticulum called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyalo‌plasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vacuo‌plasm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyt‌osol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proto‌plasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vacuoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a456420bcae6431e8aa5f39c142e3dd0x1280x969.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vacuoplasm</strong></p><p>The <strong>cytoplasm</strong> that is present within the channels of the <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> is called <strong>vacuoplasm.</strong></p>\n<p>The presence of the endoplasmic reticulum divides the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> into two compartments, one within the channels called vaculoplasm and, that outside the channels called <strong>hyaloplasm</strong> or <strong>cytosol.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2412","text":"With respect to the mass r‌atio, the cell membrane p​redominantly consists of ‌_______.","raw_text":"With respect to the mass ratio, the cell membrane predominantly consists of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosph‌olipids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prot‌eins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carboh‌ydrates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glyco‌lipids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/308aafcd6a0249bbbf12016908046025x1280x1618.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proteins</strong></p><p>With respect to the mass ratio, the <strong>cell membrane</strong> predominantly <strong>consists</strong> of <strong>proteins</strong>.</p>\n<p>The mass of proteins either equals or exceeds the mass of lipids in nearly all membranes. Most <strong>plasma membranes</strong> consist of approximately <strong>50% lipid</strong> and <strong>50% protein</strong> by weight. The lipid molecules in the cell membrane are predominantly <strong>phospholipids. </strong>The <strong>trilaminar structure</strong> of membranes is produced by the arrangement of <strong>lipid molecules.</strong> Each phospholipid molecule consists of an enlarged head with a phosphate portion and two thin tails. The head end is also called the <strong>polar end</strong>, while the tail end is the <strong>non-polar end</strong>. The head end is soluble in water and is said to be <strong>hydrophilic</strong>. The tail end is insoluble in water and is said to be <strong>hydrophobic</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2413","text":"Absorption of the fluids i‌nto the cell occurs by a ​process called ______","raw_text":"Absorption of the fluids into the cell occurs by a process called ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phagoc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pinoc‌ytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exocy‌tosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pinocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3ea3cb4c8114fea8c31c2ad54065621x1280x981.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pinocytosis</strong></p><p>Absorption of the fluids into the cell occurs by a process called <strong>pinocytosis</strong>. The vesicles involved in the process are called <strong>pinocytotic vesicles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A<strong>: Endocytosis</strong> is a process in which large molecules enter into the cells by invagination and formation of the vesicle.</p>\n<p>Option B: Some cells use the process of endocytosis to engulf foreign matter (e.g., bacteria). The process is then referred to as <strong>phagocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The process of releasing the molecules that are produced within the cytoplasm to the exterior is called <strong>exocytosis</strong> or <strong>reverse</strong> <strong>pinocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5271","text":"The structure marked with ‌the asterisk in the elect​ron micrograph indicates ‌_____________.","raw_text":"The structure marked  with the asterisk in the electron micrograph indicates _____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitoch‌ondria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi ‌bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secretory‌ vesicles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ribo‌somes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secretory vesicles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed668b1c5cd7482aa4c00cd8cd71a443.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/077963b672064b52b0fbffe2ddf2ab80x1280x1167.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e62db2275ce4fa7a4c1cdfcd5d21bb6x1280x764.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/680a3f8672f148c5af58380f88796b7cx1280x817.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04c52c4313b54d90adaa5735df80391cx1280x1996.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secretory vesicles</strong></p><p>The structure marked with the asterisk in the electron micrograph indicates <strong>presynaptic</strong> <strong>secretory vesicles</strong>. The structure marked with the arrowhead is postsynaptic density.</p>\n<p>The secretory vesicles are synthesized by the neurons and are involved in <strong>electrochemical signalling</strong> in the brain and neuromuscular junctions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>On electron microscopy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mitochondria</strong> appear as round or elliptical bodies of 0.5-2.0 micron long on an electron microscope, consisting of an outer and an inner mitochondrial membrane, cristae, and mitochondrial matrix.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Golgi bodies</strong> (or Golgi apparatus) appear as stacks of flatenned membranous cisternae surrounded by clusters of vesicles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ribosomes</strong> may be attached to the membrane of the rough endoplasmic reticulum (as shown in the image below), or else, they may exist as monosomes or as a chain of granular structures called <strong>polyribosomes </strong>associated with mRNA.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2417","text":"Which of the following chr‌omatins in the nucleus ha​s a loose appearance and ‌stains lightly with hemat​oxylin and eosin?","raw_text":"Which of the following chromatins in the nucleus has a loose appearance and stains lightly with hematoxylin and eosin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polych‌romatin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heteroc‌hromatin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoch‌romatin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Euchr‌omatin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Euchromatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ffa42267140043cf92ab51828170d5cax1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Euchromatin</strong></p><p>The <strong>euchromatin</strong> in the nucleus of a cell has a <strong>loose appearance</strong> and <strong>stains lightly</strong> with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E).</p>\n<p>On staining with H&amp;E, the <strong>nucleus</strong> stains dark<strong> purple or blue,</strong> while the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> usually stains<strong> pink</strong>. However, in some cells, the nuclei are relatively large and stain lightly due to the presence of a delicate network of fibers called <strong>chromatin</strong>. At some places (in the nucleus), the chromatin is seen in the form of <strong>irregular dark masses </strong>called <strong>heterochromatin</strong> and some other places, the network is <strong>loose and stains lightly</strong> and is referred to as <strong>euchromatin</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2418","text":"The lumen of the rough end‌oplasmic reticulum is con​tinuous with _____.","raw_text":"The lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is continuous with _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyto‌plasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pericytopl‌asmic space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perinucl‌ear space","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nucle‌oplasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perinuclear space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f3a5f68c90941148e2da0b88279a284x1280x969.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f08aed21dc448369ff535b50a8d2878x1280x1996.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perinuclear space</strong></p><p>The lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is continuous with <strong>perinuclear</strong> <strong>space</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER) arises from the outer nuclear membrane. Hence, its lumen is continuous with the <strong>perinuclear</strong> <strong>space</strong> (space between inner and outer nuclear membranes). The lumen is also continuous with the lumen of the smooth ER.</p>\n<p>The ER is an organelle that consists of a network of flattened membrane-bound structures called cisternae. The ER is classified into <strong>rough</strong> <strong>ER</strong> and <strong>smooth</strong> <strong>ER</strong>, depending on whether the ribosomes are attached or not, respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2421","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a unilayered epitheli​a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a unilayered epithelia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple squamous epithelium‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudostratified columnar ‌epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simple columnar epithelium‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seminiferou‌s epithelium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seminiferous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ffde3ce09754f87ba2a1ebd4ff689eax1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/202b59e7a6de481988fa0e730a493d78x1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14f9554123fb45b0ab1b4cec8bc0d8acx1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bed25daa62ba44f1a4e81f0d13353d4ex1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13e68a380bc34cbdadc54ae3447288f0x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seminiferous epithelium</strong></p><p>The <strong>seminiferous epithelium</strong> is a <strong>complex stratified epithelium</strong>, consisting of cells that form a lineage of spermatozoa (spermatogonia, spermatocytes, spermatids) and Sertoli cells.</p>\n<p>Given below is a histological image showing the complex stratified epithelium of seminiferous ducts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Simple epithelia and pseudostratified epithelium are both uni-layered epithelia.</p>\n<p><strong>Simple epithelium</strong> - typically present in sites at which absorption, secretion, and filtration occur. Depending on the shape of the cells, the simple epithelia are further classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Squamous</strong> epithelium</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cuboidal</strong> epithelium</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Columnar</strong> epithelium</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pseudostratified epithelium </strong>- comprises only a single layer of cells but has its cell nuclei positioned at various levels in a manner <strong>falsely suggestive</strong> of <strong>stratified</strong><strong> epithelia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Given below is a histological image showing the pseudostratified columnar epithelium of the trachea.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2435","text":"What type of epithelium li‌nes the parietal peritone​um?","raw_text":"What type of epithelium lines the parietal peritoneum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple ‌squamous","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stratifie‌d squamous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cubo‌idal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colu‌mnar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Simple squamous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f3b7ec5f8144d2fa0b0943f92c384cbx1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Simple squamous</strong></p><p><strong>Parietal peritoneum</strong> is lined by <strong>simple squamous</strong> epithelium.</p>\n<p>The <strong>simple squamous epithelium</strong> is seen at the following sites:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alveoli</strong> of the lungs</li>\n<li>Thin segment of the <strong>loop of Henle</strong></li>\n<li>Lining of the <strong>blood</strong> and <strong>lymphatic vessels,</strong> where it is called <strong>endothelium</strong></li>\n<li>Lining of the body cavities<strong> </strong>such as <strong>pleura</strong>, <strong>peritoneum</strong>, and <strong>pericardium</strong>, where it is called <strong>mesothelium</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lining of the inside of the heart, where it is known as <strong>endocardium.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2425","text":"The thyroid follicles are ‌lined by _____ epithelium​.","raw_text":"The thyroid follicles are lined by _____ epithelium.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratifie‌d cuboidal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cubo‌idal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stratifie‌d columnar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colu‌mnar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cuboidal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6df70b6e4b3b4fa0a840178d0bd13ca8x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cuboidal</strong></p><p>Thyroid follicles<strong> </strong>are lined by <strong>cuboidal epithelium.</strong></p>\n<p>The cuboidal epithelium consists of a single layer of cells in which the <strong>height</strong> of the cells is about the <strong>same as their width</strong>. The nuclei are usually rounded.</p>\n<p>Cuboidal cells are seen in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Follicles of the thyroid gland</li>\n<li>Surface of the ovary (where it is called the germinal epithelium).</li>\n<li>Choroid plexuses,</li>\n<li>Inner surface of the lens</li>\n<li>Pigment cell layer of the retina.</li>\n<li>Proximal convoluted tubules of the kidneys (brush border cuboidal)</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2423","text":"Which of the following acc‌urately describes the mul​tilayered epithelium that‌ consists of deeper colum​nar cells and a surface l​ayer of flat cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes the multilayered epithelium that consists of deeper columnar cells and a surface layer of flat cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudostratified columnar ‌epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratified columnar epithe‌lium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudostratified squamous ‌epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratified squamous epithe‌lium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratified squamous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2cee7749d6f4b2f826dff8e51da4e23x1280x1369.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/754a69de964e475685200b9e1458ad37x1280x1428.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/821f988cd5234a17b8e69e2e7124a10ax1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a45fb4f9751f4846bac783532ccb8824x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratified squamous epithelium</strong></p><p>The multilayered epithelium that consists of deeper columnar cells and a surface layer of flat cells is known as the <strong>stratified squamous epithelium.</strong></p>\n<p>Stratified (multi-layered) epithelia are of the following types:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Stratified squamous epithelium</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>deepest layer </strong>is usually <strong>columnar</strong>, above which there are layers of cuboidal cells, and as it proceeds towards the surface of the epithelium the cells become increasingly <strong>flattened (or squamous</strong>).</li>\n<li>It is further classified into <strong>keratinized</strong> and <strong>non-keratinized</strong> epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given histological images show keratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the oral mucosa and the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>2. <strong>Stratified cuboidal/columnar epithelium</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It consists of two or more layers of cuboidal or columnar cells, respectively.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given histological image shows stratified cuboidal epithelium of salivary glands.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>3. <strong>Transitional epithelium</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a specialized epithelium lining most of the <strong>urinary</strong> <strong>tract; </strong>hence, it is also called <strong>urothelium</strong>. </li>\n<li>The deepest layer consists of columnar or cuboidal cells, and the middle layer consists of polyhedral or pear-shaped cells.</li>\n<li>The surface layer consists of large, polypoid, dome-shaped or <strong>umbrella</strong>-<strong>shaped</strong> <strong>cells</strong>. As the epithelium gets stretched to accommodate urine, the surface cells become flattened. </li>\n<li>The cells are called transitional due to this apparent change (transition) from<strong> stratified cuboidal epithelium</strong> in a relaxed state to<strong> stratified squamous epithelium</strong> when it is stretched.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given histological image shows transitional epithelium of the urinary bladder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2438","text":"What type of cell is the b‌asal layer of stratified ​squamous epithelium compo‌sed of?","raw_text":"What type of cell is the basal layer of stratified squamous epithelium composed of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cubo‌idal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colu‌mnar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Squa‌mous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transi‌tional","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Columnar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3938e3e1874f48f2b15cf65eb436b209x1280x1369.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Columnar</strong></p><p>The <strong>basal layer</strong> of cells present in the stratified squamous epithelium is composed of <strong>columnar </strong>cells.</p>\n<p>The stratified squamous epithelium is made up of several layers of cells notably:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Columnar cells - deepest layer </li>\n<li>Polygonal cells - middle layers</li>\n<li>Flattened squamous cells<strong> </strong>- superficial layers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stratified squamous epithelium may be <strong>keratinized </strong>or <strong>non-keratinized</strong>.</p>\n<p>Given below is a histological image of keratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the oral mucosa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2429","text":"The cornea is lined by ___‌_____ epithelium.","raw_text":"The cornea is lined by ________ epithelium.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple ‌squamous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratified squamous kerati‌nized","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratified squamous non–ke‌ratinized","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-ciliated pseudostratif‌ied columnar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stratified squamous non–keratinized","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2640b068cdef4f75a07e168e35ec32d5x1280x1428.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stratified squamous non–keratinized</strong></p><p>The cornea is lined by stratified squamous <strong>non–keratinized epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>cornea</strong> is made up of five layers. The outermost layer is made of <strong>non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium</strong> (corneal epithelium) which can regenerate rapidly after damage.</p>\n<p>Given below is a histological image of non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2434","text":"Given below is the histolo‌gical section showing the​ excretory duct of the sa‌livary gland. Which of th​e following ducts of the ​glands do not have the sa​me lining epithelium?","raw_text":"Given below is the histological section showing the excretory duct of the salivary gland. Which of the following ducts of the glands do not have the same lining epithelium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sweat‌ gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sebaceo‌us gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salivar‌y gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exocrine‌ pancreas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sebaceous gland","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cd23472c2b754f38b76f718c78456239.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sebaceous gland</strong></p><p>The ducts of the <strong>sebaceous glands</strong> are lined by keratinised <strong>stratified squamous epithelium</strong>, whereas the ducts of sweat glands, salivary glands, and exocrine pancreas are lined by <strong>stratified</strong> <strong>cuboidal epithelium.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2439","text":"The type of epithelium as ‌shown in the image below ​is present in ______.","raw_text":"The type of epithelium as shown in the image below is present in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common b‌ile duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary‌ bladder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Urinary bladder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/448197bed96b4b5d8b26c9a37b8eb039.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Urinary bladder</strong></p><p>The type of epithelium shown in the image is the <strong>transitional epithelium</strong> and it is present in the <strong>urinary bladder.</strong></p>\n<p>Transitional epithelium is a specialized epithelium lining most of the <strong>urinary</strong> <strong>tract, </strong>hence it is also called the <strong>urothelium</strong>. The deepest layer consists of columnar or cuboidal cells, and the middle layer consists of polyhedral or pear-shaped cells. The surface layer consists of large, polypoid, dome-shaped, or <strong>umbrella</strong>-<strong>shaped</strong> <strong>cells</strong>. As the epithelium gets stretched to accommodate urine, the surface cells become flattened. The cells are called transitional due to this apparent change (transition) from<strong> stratified cuboidal epithelium</strong> in a relaxed state to<strong> stratified squamous epithelium</strong> when it is stretched.</p>\n<p>They are present in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collecting ducts</li>\n<li>Renal pelvis and calyces</li>\n<li>Ureter</li>\n<li>Urinary bladder</li>\n<li>Pre-prostatic and prostatic parts of urethra</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2432","text":"Ansa nephroni is lined by ‌_____.","raw_text":"Ansa nephroni is lined by _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous and columnar epit‌helium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cuboidal and columnar epit‌helium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous and cuboidal epit‌helium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Squamous epi‌thelium only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Squamous and cuboidal epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6751c0599674a1fb8350e24589a08d9x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Squamous and cuboidal epithelium</strong></p><p>The <strong>loop of Henle</strong> is also called <strong>ansa nephroni</strong>. It is lined by both the <strong>squamous </strong>and <strong>cuboidal epithelium</strong> at different parts.</p>\n<p>The tubular epithelial cells grow progressively taller throughout the segment until those in the collecting duct form a simple columnar epithelium.</p>\n<p>The given histological image shows a cross-section of the kidney showing the thin and thick loops of Henle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2437","text":"Microvilli are absent in _‌_____.","raw_text":"Microvilli are absent in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gall b‌ladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collecti‌ng ducts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collecting ducts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collecting ducts</strong></p><p>Microvilli are absent in <strong>collecting ducts. </strong>They are lined by <strong>simple columnar epithelium without microvilli.</strong></p>\n<p>The simple cuboidal or columnar epithelium may undergo certain specializations such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microvilli</strong> - long <strong>finger-like projections</strong> arising from the surface of the epithelium, which increases the<strong> surface area of absorption</strong>. If the microvilli are long and are arranged regularly, parallel to one another, it gives a <strong>striated</strong> appearance on light microscopy. However, if the microvilli are arranged irregularly, it gives a <strong>brush</strong> <strong>border</strong> appearance. Microvilli are located at:\n<ul>\n<li>Small intestine - columnar cells with striated microvilli.</li>\n<li>Gall bladder - columnar cells with brush border microvilli.</li>\n<li>Proximal convoluted tubule - cuboidal cells with brush border microvilli</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Stereocilia</strong> - <strong>long </strong>and<strong> branching</strong> microvilli. Unlike the cilia, the stereocilia are <strong>non-motile </strong>and hence, the preferred name is<strong> stereovilli. They </strong>are located at:\n<ul>\n<li>Epididymis (increase the surface area of absorption)</li>\n<li>Cochlear and vestibular receptor cells (function as sensory transducers)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cilia </strong>-<strong> </strong>hair-like projections on the epithelial cells that are concerned with the movement. The<strong> ciliated columnar </strong>epithelium is seen in the lining of the following structures:\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory tract except in lower pharynx and vocal folds.</li>\n<li>Tympanic cavity and auditory tube.</li>\n<li>Uterine tube and efferent ductules of the testis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Secretory </strong>- columnar cells may be specialized for secretions where they are referred to as <strong>glandular cells</strong>. Such cells form the <strong>mucin secreting</strong> and <strong>chief cells</strong> of the gastric epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2443","text":"Which type of glands do sw‌eat glands constitute?","raw_text":"Which type of glands do sweat glands constitute?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple ‌tubular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simple coi‌led tubular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Compound‌ tubular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compoun‌d acinar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Simple coiled tubular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88168e6aff7942c4809e7a2a95e7fd90x1280x1497.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Simple coiled tubular</strong></p><p>Sweat glands are <strong>simple coiled tubular glands.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2440","text":"Which of the following is ‌a holocrine gland?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a holocrine gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sweat‌ gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mammar‌y gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exocrine‌ pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sebaceo‌us gland","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sebaceous gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2166daa9da8047959e94b08117e1d865x1280x1457.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sebaceous gland</strong></p><p>The<strong> sebaceous gland</strong> is a <strong>holocrine</strong> gland.</p>\n<p>The exocrine glands are classified into 3 types<strong>, </strong>depending on the mode of secretion:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Merocrine glands </strong>- They are also known as eccrine or epicrine glands, in which the secretions are released out of the cell through a process called exocytosis with the cell remaining intact. The examples of such glands are:\n<ul>\n<li>Eccrine sweat glands</li>\n<li>Salivary glands</li>\n<li>Exocrine pancreas</li>\n<li>Secretion of milk protein casein by mammary glands</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Holocrine glands </strong></strong>-<strong><strong> </strong></strong>In these glands, the <strong>entire cell disintegrates</strong> while discharging its secretion. It is typically seen in <strong>sebaceous glands</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apocrine glands </strong>-<strong> </strong>In these glands, the <strong>apical parts </strong>of the cells are shed off to discharge the secretion. Examples of apocrine glands are\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sweat glands </strong>in axilla and anogenital regions</li>\n<li>Secretion of milk fat by <strong>mammary glands</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9228","text":"Which of the following rep‌resents a holocrine gland​?","raw_text":"Which of the following represents a holocrine gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fbf528c363fd447cb259d01600b35653.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>The histopathological image marked <strong>‘B’</strong> represents the <strong>sebaceous gland,</strong> which is a type of <strong>holocrine gland</strong>.</p>\n<p>Holocrine secretions are produced in the cytoplasm of the cell and released by the rupture of the plasma membrane, which <strong>destroys the cell</strong> and results in the secretion of the product into the lumen.</p>\n<p>Examples of holocrine glands are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sebaceous glands</strong> of the skin and</li>\n<li><strong>Meibomian glands</strong> of the eyelid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image A: Sweat glands - Eccrine/merocrine glands; glands remain intact</p>\n<p>Image C: Mammary gland - Apocrine gland; apical part sheds to secrete</p>\n<p>Image D: Ceruminous gland - Apocrine glands</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2444","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are false about m​ucous acini?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements are false about mucous acini?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tall cells with flat nucle‌us at base","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contain zymo‌gen granules","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lightly stained with H &amp;am‌p; E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cell boundaries are distin‌ct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contain zymogen granules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ef0ef401fa04034b1f0edaa1c0cd535x1280x1392.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dbf2b69150da432192ce8abefb60db09x1280x955.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contain zymogen granules</strong></p><p>The <strong>zymogen granules</strong> are present in <strong>serous acini</strong>, whereas the <strong>mucous acini</strong> consists of <strong>mucoid material</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following table shows a comparison between serous and mucous acini:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Serous acini</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Mucous acini</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Triangular cells with rounded nucleus at the base</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Tall cells with flat nucleus at the base</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>The cell boundaries are indistinct</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>The cell boundaries are distinct</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Contains zymogen granules</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Contain mucoid material</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Darkly stained with H&amp;E</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Lightly stained and appear empty with H&amp;E</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Thin watery secretion</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Thick mucoid secretion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Example: Parotid gland</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Example: Sublingual gland</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2449","text":"Which of the following fib‌ers of the connective tis​sue are stained black wit‌h silver impregnation?","raw_text":"Which of the following fibers of the connective tissue are stained black with silver impregnation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I coll‌agen fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elastic‌ fibers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reticula‌r fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type II col‌lagen fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reticular fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8202748a40b746e08417bfb96c4f342cx1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb6701b25e07406ea5bd9d57b3a9316cx1280x1403.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d45be264aa82446e985838b71d1a250ex1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reticular fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Reticular fibers </strong>are stained black with silver impregnation method. Reticular fibers are also called <strong>argentophil fibers, </strong>due to their specific affinity for silver salts.</p>\n<p>The fibers of connective tissue are of three main types. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collagen fibers</li>\n<li>Reticular fibers</li>\n<li>Elastic fibers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The staining characters of each of these fibers are as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Connective tissue fibers </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Stain</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Appearance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"5\"><strong>Collagen fibers</strong></td>\n<td>Unaided dye</td>\n<td>White</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hematoxylin and eosin</td>\n<td>Light pink</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Masson's trichrome</td>\n<td>Blue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verhoeff-Van Gieson</td>\n<td>Red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Silver impregnation</td>\n<td>Brown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reticular fibers</strong></td>\n<td>Silver impregnation</td>\n<td>Black</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\"><strong>Elastic fibers</strong></td>\n<td>Verhoeff-Van Gieson</td>\n<td>Black</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aldehyde fuchsin</td>\n<td>Purple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Orcein</td>\n<td>Purplish brown</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows reticular fibers in reticular connective tissue, stained black with silver impregnation method.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The images below show specimens of glans penis showing <strong>collagen fibers (Cf) stained blue</strong> and <strong>muscle fibers (Mf) stained red</strong> using <strong>Masson trichrome stain</strong>, and <strong>elastic fibers (Ef) stained black</strong> using <strong>Verhoeff-Van Gieson stain</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2447","text":"Which of the following cel‌ls are not an intrinsic c​omponent of the connectiv‌e tissue?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells are not an intrinsic component of the connective tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibro‌blasts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adipo‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mast ‌cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pigmen‌t cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mast cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mast cells</strong></p><p><strong>Mast cells</strong> are not part of the intrinsic component of the connective tissue.</p>\n<p>The <strong>various types of cells</strong> that are present in <strong>connective tissue </strong>are classed into two distinct categories that are namely:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cells that are <strong>intrinsic components</strong> of connective tissue:\n<ul>\n<li>Fibroblasts</li>\n<li>Undifferentiated mesenchymal cells</li>\n<li>Pigment cells</li>\n<li>Adipocytes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li> Cells that belong to the <strong>immune system</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Macrophage cells (or histiocytes)</li>\n<li>Mast cells</li>\n<li>Lymphocytes</li>\n<li>Plasma cells</li>\n<li>Monocytes</li>\n<li>Eosinophils</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2450","text":"Sudan III and Sudan IV dye‌s are used for the staini​ng of ______.","raw_text":"Sudan III and Sudan IV dyes are used for the staining of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elastic‌ tissue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucoid‌ tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adipose‌ tissue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reticula‌r tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adipose tissue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b76c47515b54451bfeffde3b33fd3dex1280x1345.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/813685d7223448c99414109c6f3ffb30x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adipose tissue</strong></p><p>Sudan III and Sudan IV dyes are used for the staining of<strong> adipose tissue.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows adipose tissue stained with Sudan dye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>However, during the routine preparation of slides, the tissues are treated with fat solvents (like <strong>xylene or benzene</strong>), which <strong>dissolve the fat</strong>. Therefore, in such preparations, fat cells look like <strong>rounded empty spaces, </strong>as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2452","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ the cells present in bro‌wn adipose tissue?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the cells present in brown adipose tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are larger than in ty‌pical adipose tissue.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The fat in the cytoplasm o‌ccurs as single locule.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The cytoplasm contains num‌erous mitochondria.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The cytoplasm and nucleus ‌are pushed to periphery.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The cytoplasm contains numerous mitochondria.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eca7a9ca152d459dad480590a2bc8bbex1280x2689.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The cytoplasm contains numerous mitochondria.</strong></p><p>The cytoplasm of the cells of brown adipose tissue contains <strong>numerous mitochondria</strong>.</p>\n<p>The adipose tissue is mainly of two types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Yellow</strong> or<strong> white</strong> adipose tissue.</li>\n<li><strong>Brown</strong> adipose tissue (embryonic type).</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td><strong>White adipose tissue</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Brown adipose tissue</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seen in</td>\n<td>Adults</td>\n<td>Newborn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cell size</td>\n<td>Larger</td>\n<td>Smaller</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fat droplet(s)</td>\n<td>Single</td>\n<td>\n<p>Multiple</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Also called</td>\n<td>Unilocular adipose tissue</td>\n<td>Multilocular adipose tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cytoplasm and nucleus</td>\n<td>Pushed to periphery</td>\n<td>Not pushed to periphery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mitochondria</td>\n<td>Few</td>\n<td>Numerous</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6652","text":"Reticular fibres are not p‌resent in which of the fo​llowing structures?","raw_text":"Reticular fibres are not present in which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌mus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymph‌ node","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone ‌marrow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thymus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5b2ea4c70084ed6bfc313c1bde4bdc3x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thymus</strong></p><p>Reticular fibres are <strong>not present in the thymus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reticular fibres</strong> are fine, anastomosing fibres composed of mainly <strong>type III collagen. </strong>They differ from type I collagen fibres in that, they are finer and are arranged in the form of a <strong>network (reticulum).</strong> The fibres appear <strong>black</strong> in colour on staining with <strong>silver impregnation. </strong>Due to this affinity to silver salts, they are also known as <strong>argentophil fibres.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Reticular fibres </strong>are mainly found in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All lymphoid organs like <strong>spleen, lymph node,</strong> and <strong>bone marrow</strong> (except thymus).</li>\n<li>Glands, liver and kidneys.</li>\n<li>The basement membrane of all epithelia.</li>\n<li>Smooth muscle and nerve cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HFR_wQJA","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"11_urea_cycle_and_its_disorders","title":"11.Urea Cycle And Its Disorders","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA7264","text":"Where does the ornithine c‌ycle take place?","raw_text":"Where does the ornithine cycle take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyto‌plasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitoch‌ondria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytoplasm and mitochondria‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytoplasm ‌and nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cytoplasm and mitochondria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cytoplasm and mitochondria</strong></p><p><strong>Ornithine cycle/Krebs–Henseleit cycle/Urea cycle</strong> occurs in both the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> and <strong>mitochondria.</strong></p>\n<p>The reactions that take place both in the cytoplasm and mitochondria are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>: Heme synthesis</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>: Urea cycle</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>: Gluconeogenesis</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>athway: Pyrimidine synthesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7268","text":"Which of the following all‌osteric activators are de​ficient in a patient with‌ no activity of carbamoyl​ phosphate synthetase I?","raw_text":"Which of the following allosteric activators are deficient in a patient with no activity of carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ala‌nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N-Acetyl ‌glutamate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Orni‌thine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Citru‌lline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. N-Acetyl glutamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. N-Acetyl glutamate</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient is likely to be deficient in<strong> N-acetyl glutamate.</strong> N-Acetyl glutamate is the <strong>allosteric activator</strong> of <strong>carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I</strong>, the rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6810","text":"A 3-month-old child presen‌ted with vomiting, irrita​bility, and lethargy. He ‌was then diagnosed with a​ urea cycle disorder that​ is inherited as an X-lin‌ked recessive disorder. W​hat is the likely diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old child presented with vomiting, irritability, and lethargy. He was then diagnosed with a urea cycle disorder that is inherited as an X-linked recessive disorder. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Citrul‌linemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperammon‌emia type I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperammone‌mia type II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Argininosucc‌inic aciduria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperammonemia type II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperammonemia type II</strong></p><p>This child with vomiting, lethargy, and irritability with an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> urea cycle disorder most likely <strong>hyperammonemia type II </strong></p>\n<p>Hyperammonemia type II is due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of the enzyme <strong>ornithine transcarbamoylase. </strong>It is the most frequent urea cycle defect. Biochemical findings include <strong>elevated</strong> levels of <strong>glutamine</strong> in the blood, urine, CSF, <strong>low citrulline,</strong> and <strong>high urinary orotic</strong> acid. This condition may be precipitated by a switch from formula to whole milk.</p>\n<p>Urea cycle disorders are all characterized by <strong>hyperammonemia</strong>, <strong>encephalopathy</strong>, and <strong>respiratory alkalosis</strong>. There is an accumulation of the precursors of urea - ammonia, and glutamine. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Defect in carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I - Hyperammonemia type 1</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Defect in ornithine transcarbamoylase - Hyperammonemia type 2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Defect in argininosuccinate synthetase - Citrullinemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Defect in argininosuccinate lyase- Argininosuccinic aciduria</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Defect in the gene for arginase- Hyperargininemia </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features of urea cycle disorders are common to all and include <strong>vomiting</strong>, <strong>avoidance of high-protein foods</strong>, intermittent <strong>ataxia</strong>, <strong>irritability</strong>, <strong>lethargy</strong>, and <strong>severe mental retardation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Classically, Type 2 Hyperammonemia is an <strong>X- linked recessive disorder</strong>. But in <strong>certain heterozygous females</strong> (with one mutated gene), the disease manifests and they necessarily do not remain as carriers. In them, the disease follows an <strong>X-linked partially dominant</strong> pattern of inheritance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7271","text":"An infant was admitted wit‌h failure to thrive. His ​reports show hypoglycemia‌, high glutamine, and ura​cil levels in urine. Gast​ric tube feeding was not ‌tolerated and the child b​ecame comatose. Parentera‍l dextrose was given afte​r which the child recover​ed in 24 hours. What is t‍he likely enzyme defect?","raw_text":"An infant was admitted with failure to thrive. His reports show hypoglycemia, high glutamine, and uracil levels in urine. Gastric tube feeding was not tolerated and the child became comatose.  Parenteral dextrose was given after which the child recovered in 24 hours. What is the likely enzyme defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbamoyl phosphate syntha‌se I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ornithine transcarbamoylas‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Argininosuccinate synthase‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argininosuc‌cinate lyase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ornithine transcarbamoylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ornithine transcarbamoylase</strong></p><p>This infant with failure to thrive, hypoglycemia, elevated levels of glutamine and uracil levels most likely has <strong>hyperammonemia type 2.</strong> This condition occurs due to the <strong>deficiency </strong>of the enzyme <strong>ornithine transcarbamoylase.</strong></p>\n<p>There is an accumulation of carbamoyl phosphate, which then enters into pyrimidine synthesis producing orotic acid and uracil and leading to <strong>orotic aciduria </strong>and<strong> high uracil</strong> in blood and urine. Since the ammonia from <strong>glutamine</strong> is not converted into urea, its level increases. Glutamate is not regenerated. Since <strong>glutamate</strong> is not available for <strong>transamination,</strong> amino acids cannot be used for <strong>gluconeogenesis.</strong> Hence, the glucose level drops in the blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reducing intake</strong> <strong>of ammonia</strong> - Dietary protein, a metabolic source of ammonium, is restricted and caloric intake is provided by glucose and fat.</li>\n<li><strong>Increasing excretion of ammonia</strong> - Addition of <strong>phenylbutyrate</strong>, which scavenges nitrogen by combining with amino acids to form a more water-soluble product. This can be easily excreted by the kidneys.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7265","text":"Deficiency of arginosuccin‌ate synthetase results in​ ______.","raw_text":"Deficiency of arginosuccinate synthetase results in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperarg‌ininemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argininosucc‌inic aciduria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Citrul‌linemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperammon‌emia type 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Citrullinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Citrullinemia</strong></p><p>Deficiency of <strong>arginosuccinate synthetase</strong> results in <strong>citrullinemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hyperargininemia </strong>is due to a deficiency of <strong>arginase<span class=\"fontstyle0\">.</span><br/></strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Option B:</span><strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\"> Argininosuccinic aciduria</span></strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\"> is due to a deficiency of</span><strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\"> argininosuccinate lyase.<br/></span></strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Option D:</span><strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\"> Hyperammonemia type 1 </span></strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\">due to a deficiency of <strong>carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I.</strong><br/></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6808","text":"The two nitrogen atoms in ‌urea are derived from whi​ch of the following?","raw_text":"The two nitrogen atoms in urea are derived from which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ammonia an‌d Glutamate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ammonia an‌d Aspartate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamine a‌nd Arginine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspartate a‌nd Arginine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ammonia and Aspartate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ammonia and Aspartate</strong></p><p>The two nitrogen atoms in urea are derived from <strong>ammonia and aspartate.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7270","text":"Urea cycle and Krebs cycle‌ are linked at____.","raw_text":"Urea cycle and Krebs cycle are linked at____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argi‌nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orni‌thine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fuma‌rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oxaloa‌cetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fumarate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fumarate</strong></p><p>The urea cycle and Kreb's cycle are linked at<strong> fumarate.</strong></p>\n<p>In the <strong>urea cycle,</strong> cleavage of <strong>argininosuccinate</strong> to <strong>arginine</strong> releases the aspartate skeleton as <strong>fumarate</strong>. <strong>Fumarate</strong> in <strong>Kreb's</strong> cycle, by the addition of water, converts to <strong>L-malate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8413","text":"A neonate was brought to t‌he emergency room with le​thargy and failure to thr‌ive. A microscopic image ​of his hair strand is sho​wn below. Which of the gi‌ven enzymes may be defici​ent in this patient?","raw_text":"A neonate was brought to the emergency room with lethargy and failure to thrive. A microscopic image of his hair strand is shown below. Which of the given enzymes may be deficient in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arginosucc‌inate lyase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Argi‌nase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fuma‌rase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyros‌inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Arginosuccinate lyase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b096862d120f4aa783290f55f9b1030c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Arginosuccinate lyase</strong></p><p>The given microscopy image shows<strong> trichorrhexis nodosa </strong>and<strong> longitudinal breaks </strong>and is seen in <strong>argininosuccinic aciduria. </strong>This occurs due to a defect in the enzyme <strong>argininosuccinate lyase.</strong></p>\n<p>Trichorrhexis nodosa is a defect in the hair shaft characterized by thickening or weak points (nodes) that cause the hair to break off easily. It can also be caused by other disorders such as <strong>hypothyroidism, iron deficiency</strong> and <strong>Menkes' kinky</strong> hair syndrome</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3504","text":"A neonate was brought with‌ tachypnoea and feeding d​ifficulties 2 days after ‌birth. Later he developed​ focal seizures that prog​ressed to coma. The plasm‌a ammonia level is 300 µg​/dL. The argininosuccinat‍e level is high but the a​rginine level is low. Wha​t is the enzyme deficienc‍y?","raw_text":"A neonate was brought with tachypnoea and feeding difficulties 2 days after birth. Later he developed focal seizures that progressed to coma. The plasma ammonia level is 300 µg/dL. The argininosuccinate level is high but the arginine level is low. What is the enzyme deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argininosuccinate syntheta‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argininosuc‌cinate lyase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Argi‌nase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbamoyl phosphate synthe‌tase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Argininosuccinate lyase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Argininosuccinate lyase</strong></p><p>This infant with feeding difficulty,  tachypnea and <strong>seizures,</strong> combined with an <strong>elevated arginosuccinate</strong> level and <strong>low arginine</strong> levels most likely has <strong>argininosuccinic aciduria.</strong> This is associated with an <strong>argininosuccinate lyase deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition is accompanied by high levels of argininosuccinate levels in blood, CSF, and urine. Like other classical urea cycle disorders, it presents 1-4 days after birth with lethargy and failure to thrive that progresses to coma and even death. It is also associated with friable, tufted hair known as <strong>trichorrhexis nodosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Diagnosis is by measurement of <strong>erythrocyte argininosuccinate lyase activity</strong> and can be performed on umbilical cord blood or amniotic fluid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7269","text":"At which step of the urea ‌cycle does hydrolysis occ​ur?","raw_text":"At which step of the urea cycle does hydrolysis occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cleavage o‌f arginine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Formation of arginosuccina‌te","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Formation o‌f citrulline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cleavage of arginosuccinat‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cleavage of arginine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cleavage of arginine</strong></p><p>Hydrolysis occurs during the <strong>cleavage</strong> of <strong>arginine</strong> to form urea and ornithine in the urea cycle. It is catalyzed by the enzyme arginase.</p>\n<p>Clinical correlation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deficiency of the enzyme arginase results in <strong>hyperargininemia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Levels of arginine are elevated in both blood and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7273","text":"A 3-day-old child develope‌d lethargy, poor feeding,​ and hypotonia. An inborn‌ error of metabolism (IEM​) panel was sent. Reports​ reveal hyperammonemia, h‌yperornithinemia, and hom​ocitrullinuria. What is t‍he most likely defect?","raw_text":"A 3-day-old child developed lethargy, poor feeding, and hypotonia. An inborn error of metabolism (IEM) panel was sent. Reports reveal hyperammonemia, hyperornithinemia, and homocitrullinuria. What is the most likely defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Citrin‌ defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ornithine transporter defe‌ct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carnitine acyl transferase‌ defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ornithine‌ permease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ornithine transporter defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ornithine transporter defect</strong></p><p>This child with lethargy, poor feeding, and labs showing <strong>hyperammonemia, hyperornithinemia,</strong> and <strong>homocitrullinuria</strong> most likely has hyperammonemia, hyperornithinemia and homocitrullinuria syndrome (HHH syndrome). This condition is due to an <strong>o</strong><strong>rnithine transporter defect.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0125","text":"In urea cycle disorders, w‌hich of the following can​ be used to reduce the le‌vels of ammonia?","raw_text":"In urea cycle disorders, which of the following can be used to reduce the levels of ammonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glut‌amate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenylb‌utyrate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Isole‌ucine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L-car‌nitine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenylbutyrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenylbutyrate</strong></p><p><strong>Phenylbutyrate</strong> can be used to reduce ammonia levels in patients with urea cycle disorders.</p>\n<p>Phenylbutyrate <strong>diverts urea</strong> into an alternate pathway and reduces the amount of ammonia. Hence, acting as an <strong>ammonia scavenger. </strong>Other drugs that can be used to achieve the same outcome (ammonia scavengers) include <strong>phenylacetate</strong> and <strong>benzoate.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Arginine is used as first-line treatment as it provides ornithine; a positive regulator of the urea cycle. Arginine also activates NAG synthase which increases NAG; a positive regulator of the urea cycle</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7266","text":"All are enzymes involved i‌n the urea cycle except__​___.","raw_text":"All are enzymes involved in the urea cycle except_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbamoyl phosphate syntha‌se -ll","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ornithine transcarbamoylas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argininosuc‌cinate Lyase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argi‌nase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbamoyl phosphate synthase -ll","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbamoyl phosphate synthase -ll</strong></p><p><strong>Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase-ll </strong>is a cytoplasmic enzyme, which is involved in <strong>pyrimidine nucleotide synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase-l</strong> is a mitochondrial enzyme, which catalyzes the rate-limiting step of the<strong> urea cycle.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7267","text":"In which of the following ‌biological processes is c​arbamoyl phosphate synthe‌tase I used?","raw_text":"In which of the following biological processes is carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urea ‌cycle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Purine s‌ynthesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrimidine‌ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucuroni‌c pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Urea cycle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Urea cycle</strong></p><p><strong>Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase</strong> <strong>I</strong> is a mitochondrial enzyme that catalyzes the rate-limiting step of the <strong>urea cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase</strong> <strong>II </strong>is a cytoplasmic enzyme that catalyzes the synthesis of pyrimidine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tdBBbjZm","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"11_vaccine_production_and_storage","title":"11.Vaccine Production And Storage","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MC5041","text":"Select the wrongly matched‌ pair of excipients and v‌accines.","raw_text":"Select the wrongly matched pair of excipients and vaccines.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MMR- N‌eomycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"OPV- Magnes‌ium Chloride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DPT- Th‌iomersal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BCG- For‌maldehyde","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. BCG- Formaldehyde","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. BCG- Formaldehyde</strong></p><p><strong>Formaldehyde </strong>is not used in the BCG vaccine. It is used in <strong>IPV</strong> and <strong>diphtheria</strong> and <strong>tetanus</strong> vaccines.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Excipients </strong>(other vaccine constituents) include: <strong>Adjuvants:</strong> serve to enhance the immune reaction to the vaccine , <strong>Stabilizers : </strong>stabilize and prolong the shelf-life of the vaccine  and </p>\n<p><strong>Preservatives : </strong>preserve the components. Some examples are listed below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>As stabilizing agents-\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MgCl<sub>2</sub> </strong>- <strong>OPV</strong></li>\n<li>MgSO<sub>4 </sub>- measles</li>\n<li>Lactose-sorbitol and sorbitol-gelatine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>To prevent microbial growth and contamination-\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thiomersal- DPT,</strong> hepatitis B, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> type b (Hib), rabies, influenza, and meningococcal diseases. It contains very small amounts of organic mercury.</li>\n<li><strong>Neomycin</strong>- <strong>MMR</strong> and IPV</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other preservatives-\n<ul>\n<li>2-phenoxyethanol - IPV</li>\n<li>Phenol - Typhoid vaccine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9453","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about adjuvants in ‌vaccines?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about adjuvants in vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune response is increas‌ed by degree or duration ‌by their use","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are used in HPV vacci‌nes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is a high incidence ‌of side effects with thei‌r use","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They help in the reduction‌ of amount of immunogens ‌in vaccines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There is a high incidence of side effects with their use","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There is a high incidence of side effects with their use</strong></p><p><strong>Side effects</strong> are <strong>rare </strong>with the use of <strong>adjuvants</strong> in vaccines. </p>\n<p><strong>Adjuvants</strong> are substances that are added to vaccines to <strong>enhance</strong> their <strong>immunogenicity</strong> by<strong> degree </strong>and/or<strong> duration</strong>.<strong> </strong>They help in the <strong>reduction</strong> of the <strong>amount of immunogen</strong> in vaccines or in the <strong>number of doses</strong> required to achieve immunity.</p>\n<p><strong>Aluminium salts</strong> are the <strong>most commonly used</strong> adjuvants.</p>\n<p>Adjuvants are <strong>used in </strong>many<strong> vaccines like</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anthrax vaccine</li>\n<li>Hep A vaccine</li>\n<li>Hep B vaccine</li>\n<li>Japanese encephalitis vaccine</li>\n<li>DT and DTaP</li>\n<li>HPV vaccine</li>\n<li>Pneumococcal vaccine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9454","text":"Which adjuvant is used in ‌the DPT vaccine?","raw_text":"Which adjuvant is used in the DPT vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemagg‌lutinin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha To‌copherol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alum‌inium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Twee‌n 80","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aluminium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aluminium</strong></p><p><strong>Aluminium</strong> is the adjuvant used in <strong>DPT vaccines</strong>.</p>\n<p>The table below lists adjuvants used in human vaccines: </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p><strong>Name</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p><strong>Class</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p><strong>Components</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p><strong>Vaccine</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Alum</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Mineral salts</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Aluminium phosphate, aluminium hydroxide</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Diphtheria, tetanus, pneumococcus, HAV, HBV, anthrax, tick-borne encephalitis, MenC, HPV</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>MF59</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Oil-in-water emulsion</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Squalene, Tween 80, Span 85</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Seasonal and pandemic influenza</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>AS03</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Oil-in-water emulsion</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Squalene, Tween 80, alpha tocopherol</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Pandemic influenza</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>AF03</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Oil-in-water emulsion</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Squalene, Montane 80, Eumulgin B1PH</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Pandemic influenza</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Virosomes</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Liposomes</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Phospholipids, cholesterol, haemagglutinin</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Seasonal influenza, HAV</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>AS04</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Alum-adsorbed TLR4 agonist</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Alumnium hydroxide, monophosphoryl lipid A (MPL)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>HBV, HPV</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>RC-529</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Alum-adsorbed TLR4 agonist</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>Aluminium hydroxide, synthetic MPL</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"124\">\n<p>HBV</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9455","text":"A child has come to you fo‌r the administration of t‌he IPV vaccine, as part o​f his routine immunisatio​n schedule. Which of the ‍following antibiotics may‍ be found in this vaccine‌?","raw_text":"A child has come to you for the administration of the IPV vaccine, as part of his routine immunisation schedule. Which of the following antibiotics may be found in this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amik‌acin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptomycin</strong></p><p><strong>IPV</strong> may contain trace amounts of <strong>streptomycin</strong>, neomycin or polymyxin B. </p>\n<p>MMR contain small amount of neomycin. </p>\n<p>Antibiotics are used during vaccine manufacture to <strong>prevent bacterial contamination</strong> of tissue culture cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9459","text":"Which of the following are‌ the most and least heat-‌sensitive vaccines respec​tively?","raw_text":"Which of the following are the most and least heat-sensitive vaccines respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sabin vaccine and hep B va‌ccine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tetanus vaccine and mening‌ococcal vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Japanese encaphalitis vacc‌ine and pneumococcal vacc‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salk vaccine and hep B vac‌cine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sabin vaccine and hep B vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sabin vaccine and hep B vaccine</strong></p><p>Oral polio (<strong>Sabin</strong>) vaccine is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>heat-sensitive</strong> vaccine. <strong>Hepatitis B vaccines</strong> are the <strong>least</strong> <strong>heat-sensitive</strong> alongside meningococcal A vaccine, freeze-dried Hib vaccine and pneumococcal vaccines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9463","text":"Which of the following vac‌cines should be protected‌ from sub-zero temperatur​es?","raw_text":"Which of the following vaccines should be protected from sub-zero temperatures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B‌CG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"O‌PV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Japanese e‌ncaphilitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis B</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis B vaccine</strong> is sensitive to freezing and should be <strong>protected from sub-zero</strong> temperatures.</p>\n<p><strong>Vaccines</strong> to be <strong>protected from freezing</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n<li>Cholera</li>\n<li>DTaP-hepB-Hib-IPV (hexavalent)</li>\n<li>DTwP-hepB-Hib (pentavalent)</li>\n<li>Liquid Hib</li>\n<li>IPV</li>\n<li>Influenza, pneumococcal vaccines</li>\n<li>Tetanus, DT, Td</li>\n<li>Rotavirus (freeze-dried, least sensitive), the liquid form is resistant to freezing</li>\n<li>HPV</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9465","text":"What vaccines are supplied‌ in the type of bottle gi‌ven below?","raw_text":"What vaccines are supplied in the type of bottle given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BCG and‌ Measles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HPV and‌ Tetanus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rotaviru‌s and JE","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IPV and H‌epatitis B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. BCG and Measles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0f4c2986b8f4671aaf8331bf8dfbb91.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BCG and Measles</strong></p><p>The image shows an <strong>amber</strong>-colored<strong> vial</strong>. Vaccines like<strong> BCG </strong>and<strong> measles vaccine </strong>are <strong>sensitive</strong> to <strong>light</strong> and thus are supplied in such<strong> </strong>vials.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9468","text":"Which of the following is ‌a non-electrical cold-cha‌in equipment?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a non-electrical cold-chain equipment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Solar refrigerator battery‌ drive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cold‌ box","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Walk in‌ coolers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ice lined r‌efrigerator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cold box","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24bb2ca799254cad9b1928261066a826x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cold box</strong></p><p><strong>Cold boxes</strong>,<strong> vaccine carriers</strong>, and <strong>ice packs </strong>are some of the <strong>non-electrical</strong> components of the <strong>cold-chain</strong> system.</p>\n<p><strong>Cold boxes</strong> are<strong> insulated</strong> containers used for <strong>transportation</strong> and<strong> temporary storage</strong> of vaccines and ice packs. <strong>Vaccine carriers</strong> are usually not used for the storage of vaccines but for their<strong> transportation</strong>. Ice packs generally have two holes in them for placing vaccines. They are filled with water.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a cold box containing vaccines and ice packs.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9470","text":"What is the temperature ma‌intained in the freezer c‌ompartment of the solar r​efrigerator battery drive​?","raw_text":"What is the temperature maintained in the freezer compartment of the solar refrigerator battery drive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-1‌0°C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8‌°C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"-7‌°C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌°C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. -7°C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. -7°C</strong></p><p>The <strong>freezer compartment </strong>of the <strong>solar refrigerator battery drive</strong> is maintained at <strong>– 7 °C</strong>, and it is used to store mainly ice packs.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vaccine storage compartment</strong> of this solar cold chain equipment is maintained at <strong>2 to 8 °C</strong>.<strong> </strong>The other solar cold-chain equipment commonly used is the solar refrigerators direct drive, which uses the sun’s energy to freeze phase-change materials like water and then uses this formed ice bank to keep the refrigerator cold.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9469","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about‌ the device shown in the ​image below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about the device shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smaller ones are given to ‌PHCs and the larger ones ‌to district vaccine store​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They have temperature zone‌s for different vaccines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kata thermometer is used t‌o record temperature insi‌de this device","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"These devices are provided‌ with baskets to store va‌ccines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kata thermometer is used to record temperature inside this device","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8528237da944f31b0fbef4c604817c6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kata thermometer is used to record temperature inside this device</strong></p><p>The image shown in the question is of an<strong> ice-lined refrigerator</strong> (ILR). <strong>Dial</strong> or digital <strong>thermometers</strong> are used to <strong>record temperatures</strong> inside an ice-lined refrigerator.</p>\n<p>ILRs are the main method of <strong>vaccine storage</strong> in <strong>primary health centers</strong>. They are an important part of the cold chain and they maintain a temperature of <strong>2 to 8 °C</strong> for the storage of vaccines. <br/><br/>They have different temperature zones for different vaccines. The<strong> upper zone </strong>is for<strong> freeze-sensitive vaccines</strong> like cholera, hep B, rotavirus(liquid), etc while the <strong>lower zone</strong> is for<strong> heat-sensitive vaccines</strong> like OPV, measles, etc.</p>\n<p>All vaccines are kept in the basket provided with ILR. Vaccines like OPV, BCG, measles, and JE can be kept at the bottom of the basket while, DPT, TT, Hep B, IPV, and pentavalent vaccines and diluents are kept in the upper part of the basket. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9472","text":"While working as a medical‌ officer in a CHC, you ex‌perience a sudden power c​ut in your area due to wh​ich the ice-lined refrige‍rator loses power. What i‍s the time taken for the ‌temperature inside this e‍quipment to rise to the m​aximum recommended temper‌ature limit?","raw_text":"While working as a medical officer in a CHC, you experience a sudden power cut in your area due to which the ice-lined refrigerator loses power. What is the time taken for the temperature inside this equipment to rise to the maximum recommended temperature limit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heatin‌g time","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hold ov‌er time","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Catch ‌up time","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Watch d‌own time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hold over time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b08a3a9f543f41e5a42b2a797a840bb9x740x738.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hold over time</strong></p><p><strong>Hold-over time</strong> is the time taken by cold-chain equipment to <strong>raise the temperature</strong> inside it, <strong>during loss of power</strong>, to the <strong>maximum temperature limit</strong> in its recommended range. For an ice-lined refrigerator(ILR), this time depends on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ambient temperature</li>\n<li>Frequency of opening the equipment</li>\n<li>Quantity of vaccines kept inside with spacing</li>\n<li>Condition of ice-pack lining</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows an ILR.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9476","text":"At what level is the risk ‌of cold-chain failure the‌ highest?","raw_text":"At what level is the risk of cold-chain failure the highest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distric‌t level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Regiona‌l level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PHC ‌level","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sub-cent‌re level","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sub-centre level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sub-centre level</strong></p><p>The risk of <strong>cold chain</strong> <strong>failure</strong> is <strong>highest</strong> at the <strong>sub-centre </strong>and <strong>village </strong>levels. Thus, vaccines are not stored at the sub-centre and are supplied on the day of use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9477","text":"Open vial policy is not ap‌plicable to which of the ‌following vaccines?","raw_text":"Open vial policy is not applicable to which of the following vaccines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B‌CG","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pentavale‌nt vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rotoviral vaccine in New c‌ontainer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BCG</strong></p><p><strong>Open vial policy</strong> is not applicable for BCG and measles vaccines.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>applicable</strong> for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DPT</li>\n<li>TT</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis B</strong> vaccine</li>\n<li><strong>OPV</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pentavalent</strong> vaccine</li>\n<li>Rotavirus vaccine (New version with the VVM on the side)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9478","text":"What are the devices given‌ below used to measure?","raw_text":"What are the devices given below used to measure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temperature changes in atm‌osphere","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cumulative heat exposure o‌f vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cumulative heat and freeze‌ exposure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disintegration of vaccine ‌constituents due to tempe‌rature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cumulative heat exposure of vaccine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afff09a01e3547f79cb7453053269bd6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cumulative heat exposure of vaccine</strong></p><p>The image shown is of<strong> vaccine vial monitors </strong>(VVM), which measure the <strong>cumulative heat exposure</strong> of the vaccine.</p>\n<p>VVMs are heat-sensitive indicators that are routinely used to monitor vaccine viability as the vaccine vial moves across the supply chain. They record<strong> </strong>cumulative heat exposure through a <strong>gradual change in color</strong>. If the color of the<strong> inner square</strong> is the <strong>same or </strong>is<strong> darker than</strong> the<strong> outer circle</strong>, the vaccine has been exposed to too much heat and should be <strong>discarded.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9481","text":"​​You are preparing to adm‌inister the BCG vaccine t‌o a newborn at a PHC. Bef​ore administering the vac​cine, you check the vacci‍ne vial monitor. In which‍ of the following observa‌tions on the VVM, will yo‍u discard the vaccine?","raw_text":"​​You are preparing to administer the BCG vaccine to a newborn at a PHC. Before administering the vaccine, you check the vaccine vial monitor. In which of the following observations on the VVM, will you discard the vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner squa‌re is white","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inner square has the same ‌color as the outer circle‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inner circle has the same ‌color as the outer square‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inner circle is lighter th‌an the outer square","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inner square has the same color as the outer circle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inner square has the same color as the outer circle</strong></p><p>A vaccine must be <strong>discarded</strong> if the VVM shows the <strong>inner square </strong>is the <strong>same color or darker </strong>than the<strong> outer circle.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9218","text":"Which of the following vac‌cine vials can be used ba‌sed on the vaccine vial m​onitor (VVM) color change​s shown?","raw_text":"Which of the following vaccine vials can be used based on the vaccine vial monitor (VVM) color changes shown?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only vial 1‌ can be used","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vials 1 and ‌2 can be used","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vials 1, 2, and 3 can be u‌sed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All vials ‌can be used","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vials 1 and 2 can be used","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72cf73b7676c4d6e9f9a49b34d21377e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vials 1 and 2 can be used</strong></p><p>From the above image, only <strong>vials 1 </strong>and<strong> 2</strong> can be <strong>used</strong> as the <strong>inner squares</strong> of the VVMs are<strong> lighter than</strong> the <strong>outer circles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vial 3 has crossed the discard point as the inner square and outer circle are the same color and thus cannot be used. Vial 4 cannot be used as the VVM shows a darker inner square compared to the outer circle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9482","text":"You are volunteering in a ‌vaccination camp. You per‌form a shake test before ​administering a particula​r vaccine. Which of the f‍ollowing is checked by th‍is test?","raw_text":"You are volunteering in a vaccination camp. You perform a shake test before administering a particular vaccine. Which of the following is checked by this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Freeze damag‌e to vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Contaminatio‌n of vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Expiry o‌f vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Potency o‌f vaccine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Freeze damage to vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e1d7fcab7b4460aa9557ded706a8a0dx600x796.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Freeze damage to vaccine</strong></p><p>The <strong>shake test</strong> is done to check for <strong>damage</strong> to vaccines that are sensitive to <strong>freezing temperatures.</strong></p>\n<p>Steps to perform the shake test:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>frozen vaccine</strong> is used as a <strong>control vial</strong> and the vial suspected to be damaged by freezing is taken as the test vial.</li>\n<li>Both are <strong>shaken after thawing</strong> and the rate of <strong>sedimentation</strong> is <strong>compared</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the rate of <strong>sedimentation</strong> in the test vial is <strong>slower than</strong> that in the <strong>control</strong> vial, then it is <strong>usable</strong> and has not been damaged.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Some of the most widely used <strong>vaccines</strong> are<strong> freeze-sensitive</strong>. These include vaccines against:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Tetanus</li>\n<li>Pertussis</li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus influenza</em> type b (Liquid )</li>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kaRTiCok","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"11_psoriasis","title":"11.Psoriasis","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC1661","text":"A patient presented to her‌ primary care physician w‍ith an itchy scalp and da‍ndruff. On examination, t​he following finding was ​seen and the Auspitz sign‌ was present. This diseas​e is least likely to affe‍ct the ____________.","raw_text":"A patient presented to her primary care physician with an itchy scalp and dandruff. On examination, the following finding was seen and the Auspitz sign was present. This disease is least likely to affect the ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axi‌lla","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower‌ back","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elbows a‌nd knees","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glutea‌l cleft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Axilla","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc843ee0a5854170ae0d5c37c2ca4fd9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Axilla</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chronic plaque psoriasis</strong>. <strong>Axilla</strong> is usually<strong> not involved</strong> in chronic plaque psoriasis. It is affected in inverse psoriasis.</p>\n<p>Psoriasis is a common, <strong>chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory</strong> condition in which multiple genetic and environmental factors may lead to the formation of erythematous plaques with silvery-white scales. Genetic predisposition is present in type 1 plaque psoriasis and guttate psoriasis with <strong>HLA‐C:06:02</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chronic plaque psoriasis, also called <strong>psoriasis vulgaris</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> clinical type of psoriasis. Commonly affected sites are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scalp</li>\n<li>Elbows</li>\n<li>Knees</li>\n<li>Lower back</li>\n<li>Gluteal cleft</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4312","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with the following skin ‍finding. Which of the fol‍lowing clinical tests wil​l aid in diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with the following skin finding. Which of the following clinical tests will aid in diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gratta‌ge test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"KOH ‌smear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Besnier‌'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin ‌biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Grattage test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/484facdbb5e94279877094d5edb6656b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Grattage test</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>silvery-white scaly plaques</strong> on the extensor aspect of elbows and knees is a characteristic feature of <strong>psoriasis. Grattage test </strong>aids in its diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Scratching</strong> (grattage) scales in psoriasis makes the scale appear <strong>more silver</strong> in color by introducing <strong>air–keratin</strong> interfaces. When the scales are completely scraped off, the basement membrane is exposed and is seen as a moist red surface (<strong>membrane of Bulkeley</strong>) through which dilated capillaries at the tip of elongated dermal papillae are torn, leading to multiple bleeding points. This classical <strong>pinpoint bleeding</strong> is known as the <strong>Auspitz sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>Auspitz sign is due to the <strong>elongated, dilated, tortuous vessels</strong> in the <strong>dermal papilla</strong> along with <strong>supra papillary</strong> <strong>thinning</strong> of the <strong>epidermis</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, the Auspitz sign is neither sensitive nor specific for psoriasis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 100px;\" width=\"537\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Auspitz sign present </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Auspitz sign absent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Psoriasis vulgaris</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Pustular psoriasis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Darrier's disease </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Guttate psoriasis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Actinic keratosis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Inverse psoriasis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.5px;\">Erythrodermic psoriasis </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4318","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true regar‍ding the given condition?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an inflammatory, non‌-infectious disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nail pitting is superficia‌l and regular.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Joint involvement is seen ‌in 5-30% of patients.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Koebner’s phenomenon is se‌en.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nail pitting is superficial and regular.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f49fe0dd22b448c293f014d4eff61c3a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nail pitting is superficial and regular.</strong></p><p>The presence of dull red sharply demarcated plaques on the knees with loosely adherent silvery-white scales is in favor of<strong> psoriasis. Nail plate pitting</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> feature of nail psoriasis and is of the <strong>deep</strong> and<strong> irregular</strong> type.</p>\n<p>About 5 to 30% of patients with cutaneous psoriasis have <strong>psoriatic arthritis</strong>(PsA). Psoriasis patients have a variable degree of pruritus.</p>\n<p><strong>Koebner’s phenomenon </strong>or isomorphic phenomenon, the phenomena of appearance of new lesions along the line of trauma, is seen in psoriasis.</p>\n<p>Nail pitting is superficial and regular in alopecia areata.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4323","text":"While collecting data on n‌ail changes in psoriasis ‍patients, you note the fo‍llowing findings. Which o​f the following is the mo​st specific finding for t‌his condition?","raw_text":"While collecting data on nail changes in psoriasis patients, you note the following findings. Which of the following is the most specific finding for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima‌ge A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima‌ge D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima‌ge C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ima‌ge B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Image C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/69713e013afe4bd188462145cd6af24c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7ef24f74e9544dcb059ab6443a290e1x1280x2068.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Image C</strong></p><p><strong>Subungual oil drop sign</strong> or<strong> salmon patch</strong> (image C), a<strong> highly specific </strong>pathognomonic sign seen in nail psoriasis. It is a yellow-red discoloration in the nail bed resembling a drop of oil. The reddish-brown color is due to dilated, tortuous, superficial capillaries in the papillary dermis and parakeratosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1647","text":"A model presents to the OP‌D with the following cuta‍neous lesions. She inform‍s the dermatologist that ​these lesions increase in​ size whenever she consum‌es alcohol and that they ​bleed whenever she scratc‍hes them. Which of the fo​llowing is false regardin‍g the pathogenesis of her​ condition?","raw_text":"A model presents to the OPD with the following cutaneous lesions. She informs the dermatologist that these lesions increase in size whenever she consumes alcohol and that they bleed whenever she scratches them. Which of the following is false regarding the pathogenesis of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperproliferation of epid‌ermis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genetic predisposition HLA‌‐C:06:02","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficient Th1 and Th17 hel‌per cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IFN-γ, TNF-α, IL-17 levels‌ are raised","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deficient Th1 and Th17 helper cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98cc9ff35c6144298e189360420d74b6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deficient Th1 and Th17 helper cells</strong></p><p>The image shows sharply marginated, dull-red <strong>plaques</strong> with silvery-white <strong>scales</strong> which have coalesced to form geographical lesions. The history and image point towards a diagnosis of <strong>psoriasis</strong>. Here, there is an <strong>increased number of </strong>activated <strong>Th1, Th17, </strong>and Th22 cells, with a <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> Th2 cells.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a common, <strong>chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory</strong> condition in which multiple genetic and environmental factors may lead to the formation of erythematous plaques with silvery-white scales. Genetic predisposition is present in type 1 plaque psoriasis and guttate psoriasis with <strong>HLA‐C:06:02</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is <strong>increased keratinocyte turnover.</strong> It normally takes <strong>30 days</strong> for basal keratinocytes to reach the surface but in patients with psoriasis, this is <strong>reduced</strong> to<strong> 4-5 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>The activation of T cells of the adaptive immune system, specifically Th17 cells, in turn, activates keratinocytes to proliferate and produce multiple chemokines and antimicrobial peptides. </p>\n<p>Cytokines in psoriasis:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 471px; height: 147px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 221.391px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Increased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.609px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Decreased</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 221.391px;\">\n<p>Inflammatory cells and cytokines</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.609px;\">Anti-inflammatory cells and cytokines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 221.391px;\">Th1 and Th17 cells </td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.609px;\"> Th2 cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 221.391px;\">\n<p>IL‐2, IL‐8, IFN‐γ, TNF‐α, IL‐15, IL-17, IL‐22, and IL‐23</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.609px;\">\n<p>IL-4 and IL-10 absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4322","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not responsible for‍ an exacerbation of psori‍asis?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not responsible for an exacerbation of psoriasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aten‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enal‌april","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levoth‌yroxine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Levothyroxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Levothyroxine</strong></p><p><strong>Levothyroxine</strong> is <strong>not implicated</strong> in exacerbations of<strong> psoriasis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> inducing/<strong>exacerbating</strong> psoriasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Synthetic antimalarials</li>\n<li>NSAIDS</li>\n<li>Lithium</li>\n<li>ACE inhibitors &amp; angiotensin receptor blockers</li>\n<li>Interferon alpha</li>\n<li>Tetracycline</li>\n<li>Terbinafine</li>\n<li>Gemfibrozil</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other <strong>environmental</strong> and <strong>immunological</strong> factors that may <strong>trigger</strong> psoriasis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections- beta-hemolytic streptococci can trigger guttate psoriasis</li>\n<li>Alcohol misuse and smoking</li>\n<li>Emotional stress</li>\n<li>Trauma - Koebner phenomenon</li>\n<li>Winter </li>\n<li>Sunlight</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4319","text":"A patient who presented to‌ the OPD with a papulosqu‍amous lesion has the foll‍owing nail finding. Histo​pathology of the skin les​ion is unlikely to show w‌hich of the following?","raw_text":"A patient who presented to the OPD with a papulosquamous lesion has the following nail finding. Histopathology of the skin lesion is unlikely to show which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermal ‌thickening","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suprapapill‌ary thinning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kogoj spongi‌form pustules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pautrier's ‌microabscess","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pautrier's microabscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8b6f64576253458586f8bcf2434964c5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2e0db71b1b14c5baaf5a5ca45c0ff1ax1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pautrier&#x27;s microabscess</strong></p><p>The given image showing<strong> nail pitting</strong> and <strong>salmon patches</strong> in fingernails are characteristic of psoriasis.<strong> Munro microabscess</strong> is seen in <strong>psoriasis</strong> whereas<strong> Pautrier's microabscess</strong> is seen in <strong>mycosis fungoides.</strong></p>\n<p>Histopathological features of psoriasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Irregular epidermal thickening (<strong>acanthosis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Hyperkeratosis and parakeratosis</li>\n<li><strong>Suprapapillary thinning</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Clubbing</strong> of <strong>rete</strong> <strong>ridges</strong> at the base</li>\n<li><strong>Kogoj</strong> <strong>spongiform</strong> <strong>pustules </strong>(collection of neutrophils in Malpighian layer)</li>\n<li><strong>Munro</strong> <strong>microabscesses </strong>(collection of neutrophils in stratum corneum)</li>\n<li>Absence of stratum granulosum</li>\n<li>Dilated, tortuous blood vessels in the dermis</li>\n<li>Suprapapillary mononuclear leukocytic infiltrates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember:  <strong>M (Munro microabscess)</strong> is seen in <strong>P (Psoriasis)</strong> and <strong>P (Pautrier’s microabscess)</strong> is seen in <strong>M (mycosis fungoides)</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows histopathology of psoriasis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1626","text":"Biopsy taken from a patien‌t who presented with a pa‍pulosquamous skin disorde‍r shows a camel foot appe​arance on HPE. Which diso​rder is she most likely s‌uffering from?","raw_text":"Biopsy taken from a patient who presented with a papulosquamous skin disorder shows a camel foot appearance on HPE. Which disorder is she most likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityriasis r‌ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lichen ‌nitidus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f76af7514d6462e9762dd35435c5099x1280x1551.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Camel foot appearance</strong> is seen in histopathology of <strong>plaque-type psoriasis. </strong></p>\n<p>Normally, only 10% of the basal cells are in the proliferative phase, whereas in psoriasis about 90% of the basal cells are in this phase. As a result, to accommodate the increasing population of basal cells, rete pegs take a plunge in the dermis in the form of regular <strong>elongation of rete ridges</strong>, giving the <strong>'camel foot'</strong> or <strong>clubbed</strong> appearance to epidermal rete ridges.</p>\n<p>Other HPE findings seen in psoriasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regular <strong>acanthosis</strong> (epidermal thickening) </li>\n<li><strong>Hyperkeratosis</strong> and <strong>parakeratosis</strong></li>\n<li>Suprapapillary thinning</li>\n<li>Kogoj spongiform pustules (collection of <strong>neutrophils</strong> in <strong>Malpighian layer)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Munro</strong> <strong>microabscesses </strong>(collection of neutrophils in stratum corneum)</li>\n<li>Absence of stratum granulosum</li>\n<li>Dilated, tortuous blood vessels in dermis</li>\n<li>Suprapapillary mononuclear leukocytic infiltrates</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4354","text":"Which among the following ‌is the most common presen‍tation of psoriatic arthr‍itis?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common presentation of psoriatic arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Classic psoriatic arthriti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arthritis‌ mutilans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asymmetric o‌ligoarthritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Symmetric p‌olyarthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Asymmetric oligoarthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Asymmetric oligoarthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Asymmetric oligoarthritis</strong> is the <strong>most common type</strong> of psoriatic arthritis.</p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Psoriatic arthritis</strong> </span>refers to an <strong>inflammatory musculoskeletal</strong> disease that has both <strong>autoimmune</strong> and autoinflammatory features characteristically occurring in individuals with psoriasis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Asymmetric oligoarthritis commonly involves a knee or another large joint with a few small joints in the fingers or toes, often with dactylitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4352","text":"A 40-year-old woman who pr‌esented with pain and sti‍ffness in her hand for th‍e past 6 weeks was diagno​sed with classic psoriati​c arthritis after a thoro‌ugh workup. Which of the ​following joints is typic‍ally affected by this dis​order?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman who presented with pain and stiffness in her hand for the past 6 weeks was diagnosed with classic psoriatic arthritis after a thorough workup. Which of the following joints is typically affected by this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal interphalangeal j‌oint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal Interphalangeal joi‌nt","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metatarsopha‌langeal joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metacarpopha‌langeal joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Distal Interphalangeal joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9e44ad4a389452c87dc53df37b63873x1280x1062.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Distal Interphalangeal joint</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Classic psoriatic arthritis</strong> typically involves the <strong>distal interphalangeal </strong>joints.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1648","text":"A 42-year-old man with a h‌istory of psoriasis vulga‍ris was referred to the d‍ermatology department wit​h 6 weeks of worsening ps​oriasis. He was started o‌n valproic acid for bipol​ar disorder 4 months prio‍r. On examination, the fo​llowing finding was noted‍. What is the most likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man with a history of psoriasis vulgaris was referred to the dermatology department with 6 weeks of worsening psoriasis. He was started on valproic acid for bipolar disorder 4 months prior. On examination, the following finding was noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rupioid ‌psoriasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Photosensiti‌ve psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small plaqu‌e psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Koebnerise‌d psoriasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rupioid psoriasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a5166039f766483d8a89197c385d9d1e.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rupioid psoriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Cone-shaped</strong> heaped-up <strong>hyperkeratotic</strong> lesions shaped like a <strong>shell</strong> (limpet) are seen in this <strong>rupioid</strong> variety of psoriasis. Valproic acid may be associated infrequently with such psoriatic flare-ups.</p>\n<p>The term “rupioid” (from the Greek <em>rhupos</em>, meaning filth) is used to describe<strong> oyster</strong> or<strong> limpet shell-shaped thick keratotic lesions.</strong> In contrast to rupioid forms, regular plaque-type psoriasis has a white, nonadherent, and thin, scaly surface.</p>\n<p>Severe psoriatic flares and atypical forms of the disease (including rupioid plaques) have been reported in HIV-positive patients and with certain drugs. </p>\n<p>Drugs associated with psoriasis: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>β-Blockers</li>\n<li>Lithium</li>\n<li>Synthetic antimalarials</li>\n<li>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</li>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1656","text":"In which variant of psoria‌sis is scaling absent?","raw_text":"In which variant of psoriasis is scaling absent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythroderm‌ic Psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guttate ‌Psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inverse ‌psoriasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sebops‌oriasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inverse psoriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26c7eff70fc94fad8fc6741b29e8b174x431x288.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inverse psoriasis</strong></p><p>In <strong>inverse psoriasis</strong>, flexural plaques are thin and <strong>scaling</strong> is<strong> greatly reduced</strong> or<strong> absent</strong>.</p>\n<p>It involves the inguinal creases, axillae, submammary folds, gluteal cleft, umbilicus, and other body folds. It is more common in <strong>older adults</strong> and is associated with <strong>obesity.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option B: Guttate psoriasis presents with small plaque lesions with scales 2-3mm to 1 cm distributed over the trunk and proximal limbs in children</p>\n<p>Option D: In sebopsoriasis, plaques of thin sharply demarcated erythema with variable scales may occur in the typical distribution of seborrhoeic dermatitis</p>\n<p>Option A: Erythrodermic psoriasis has most or all of the body surface affected (&gt;90%BSA)</p>\n<p>The image below shows inverse psoriasis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0207","text":"A patient was treated with‌ steroids for psoriasis. ‍On stopping the treatment‍, he develops fever, mala​ise, and lesion as seen i​n the image below. What i‌s the most probable diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A patient was treated with steroids for psoriasis. On stopping the treatment, he develops fever, malaise, and lesion as seen in the image below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pustular ‌psoriasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococc‌al infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute generalised exanthem‌atous pustulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subcorneal‌ pustulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pustular psoriasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/62275958406c41f796408aef8dff04af.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pustular psoriasis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points towards the diagnosis of <strong>pustular psoriasis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pustular psoriasis</strong> is a type of psoriasis characterized by <strong>tiny pus-filled lesions</strong> (pustules) having a characteristic <strong>lakes or sheets of pus appearance</strong> (seen in the image).</p>\n<p>The patient presents with a history of <strong>fever</strong> and malaise along with the presence of <strong>pustules</strong> on a <strong>circumscribed, fiery red, edematous or scaling plaque.</strong> The pus is usually <strong>sterile.</strong> Sudden <strong>withdrawal of systemic steroids</strong> is usually the trigger for this condition.</p>\n<p>Pustular psoriasis can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Localized pustular psoriasis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Generalized pustular psoriasis</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Acute generalized pustular psoriasis (<strong>Von Zumbusch</strong>)</li>\n<li>Subacute annular and circinate pustular psoriasis</li>\n<li>Acute generalized pustular psoriasis of pregnancy (<strong>Impetigo herpetiformis</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment: <strong>Acitretin</strong> (oral retinoid) is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for pustular psoriasis. However, it is <strong>contraindicated in pregnancy.</strong> <strong>Systemic steroids</strong> are used instead. <strong>Cyclosporine</strong> is the second-line drug.</p>\n<p>Note: Systemic steroids are never used for the treatment of psoriasis except in the case of Impetigo herpetiformis where they are first-line agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0387","text":"A 9-year-old boy developed‌ skin lesions on his trun‍k and proximal portion of‍ his arms, 10 days after ​he recovered from an epis​ode of pharyngitis. Which‌ of the following stateme​nts are correct about thi‍s condition? It follows a​ chronic course It is ass‍ociated with HLA‐C:06:02 ​It responds to broadband ‌UVB phototherapy It is as‌sociated with fever and h​ypocalcemia Acitretin is ​the drug of choice","raw_text":"<p>A 9-year-old boy developed skin lesions on his trunk and proximal portion of his arms, 10 days after he recovered from an episode of pharyngitis.   Which of the following statements are correct about this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It follows a chronic course</li>\n<li>It is associated with HLA‐C:06:02</li>\n<li>It responds to broadband UVB phototherapy</li>\n<li>It is associated with fever and hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Acitretin is the drug of choice</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,‌ 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3483058dd990480f863a1b72b1b71c74.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 3</strong></p><p>The clinical stem of small, red, drop-like lesions on the trunk and arms (shown in the image) after an attack of pharyngitis is suggestive of <strong>guttate psoriasis. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1144\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Guttate psoriasis is a type of psoriasis occurring as a <strong>sudden onset shower of small lesions</strong> seen diffusely over the body, more on the trunk and proximal limbs. It is more common in children and young adults, <strong>following infection</strong> with<strong> group A streptococcus</strong> and is often the first presentation of psoriasis. It can also occur as a flare-up in adults with plaque psoriasis that began in childhood. It is linked to <strong>HLA‐C:06:02.</strong></p>\n<p>Lesions usually <strong>resolve</strong> over about 3 months. Some patients with acute guttate psoriasis might develop plaque psoriasis in the future.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Topical <strong>steroids</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Antimicrobials</strong> - Recurrent episodes may be related to the pharyngeal carriage of the responsible streptococcus by the patient or close contact. A course of semisynthetic penicillin with rifampin may be required to clear chronic streptococcal carriage.</li>\n<li><strong>Phototherapy</strong> - Broad-band ultraviolet B (UVB)- Response is better with broadband UVB over narrow-band UVB. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>chronic</strong> course is seen in <strong>plaque</strong> and <strong>follicular psoriasis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pustular</strong> psoriasis patients can have a <strong>fever,</strong> erythroderma, <strong>hypocalcemia,</strong> and cachexia.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1629","text":"A dermatologist can use th‌e Goekerman regimen to tr‍eat which of the followin‍g conditions?","raw_text":"A dermatologist can use the Goekerman regimen to treat which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paraneoplast‌ic pemphigus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ecz‌ema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Goekerman regimen</strong> is used in the treatment of<strong> psoriasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The modified Goekerman regimen involves <strong>application</strong> of<strong> coal tar</strong> for 5 hours/day in combination with exposure to <strong>narrow‐band UVB</strong> (NBUVB).</p>\n<p><strong>Ingram Regimen</strong> is another treatment regimen used in psoriasis. In‐patients are treated with a <strong>coal tar bath</strong>, <strong>suberythemogenic </strong><strong>UVB</strong> and then<strong> dithranol</strong> in Lassar’s paste is applied to plaques. </p>\n<p>The mechanism of action of dithranol may relate to its <strong>antiproliferative</strong> and <strong>proapoptotic</strong> effects on keratinocytes. Dithranol produces <strong>brown staining</strong> of the skin, which resolves about 2 weeks after therapy is completed.</p>\n<p>Both regimens have a good safety profile and are highly efficacious.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4329","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice for a patient who p‍resented with the followi‍ng finding?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for a patient who presented with the following finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PU‌VA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infli‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methotrexate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/82e1832cf1db40dab49cb3dc9263579f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methotrexate</strong></p><p>The patient<strong> </strong>has<strong> right knee effusion</strong>,<strong> dactylitis</strong> of multiple digits, sharply demarcated red <strong>papulosquamous lesions</strong> on both knees all of which point towards a diagnosis of<strong> psoriatic arthritis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> and <strong>apremilast</strong> are the <strong>first-line</strong> drugs for the treatment of moderate to severe psoriasis associated with psoriatic arthritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6689","text":"A patient with a known cas‌e of chronic plaque psori‍asis with more than 50% s‍kin involvement presents ​to you. Which of the foll​owing will you not recomm‌end?","raw_text":"A patient with a known case of chronic plaque psoriasis with more than 50% skin involvement presents to you. Which of the following will you not recommend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral met‌hotrexate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NB ‌UVB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral s‌teroids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral cyc‌losporine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral steroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral steroids</strong></p><p><strong>Oral/systemic steroids</strong> are <strong>not used</strong> in the management of <strong>chronic plaque psoriasis</strong>. Topical steroids may be used.</p>\n<p>Systemic steroids are avoided because the withdrawal of systemic steroids causes the development of pustular psoriasis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>only indication</strong> of using <strong>systemic</strong> steroids in psoriasis is generalized pustular psoriasis in pregnancy (<strong>impetigo herpetiformis</strong>). </p>\n<p>Other options maybe used in treatment of extensive (&gt;30%) chronic plaque psoriasis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4331","text":"In which of the following ‌cases would you prescribe‍ systemic corticosteroids‍?","raw_text":"In which of the following cases would you prescribe systemic corticosteroids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22-year-old woman with imp‌etigo herpetiformis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"40-year-old man with sever‌e psoriatic arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11-year-old boy with gutta‌te psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"33-year-old woman with ery‌throdermic psoriasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 22-year-old woman with impetigo herpetiformis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 22-year-old woman with impetigo herpetiformis</strong></p><p><strong>Impetigo herpetiformis </strong>(pustular psoriasis of pregnancy) is the only definitive indication for <strong>systemic steroids</strong>.</p>\n<p>Steroids can also be used for the <strong>control</strong> of <strong>acute symptoms</strong> like <strong>ARDS</strong> accompanying pustular psoriasis.</p>\n<p>Steroids are<strong> contraindicated</strong> in all other forms of psoriasis as sudden <strong>withdrawal</strong> can trigger <strong>severe pustular</strong> or<strong> erythrodermic psoriasis. </strong>As most of the other medications are contraindicated in pregnancy, this is the only definitive indication for systemic steroids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4327","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice for this patient?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic‌ steroids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topical ‌anthralin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral re‌tinoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PU‌VA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral retinoids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e777cab30bd40aab7cd01b178125ef1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral retinoids</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> pustular psoriasis. Acitretin,</strong> an oral retinoid is the <strong>treatment of choice </strong>for pustular psoriasis<strong>. </strong>Other first-line drugs are methotrexate and cyclosporine.</p>\n<p>Pustular psoriasis is a complication of psoriasis wherein <strong>multiple sterile pustules</strong> develop. The acute generalized form of pustular psoriasis is termed as <strong>Von Zumbusch</strong> type. </p>\n<p>The most <strong>provocative</strong> factor is the <strong>withdrawal</strong> of <strong>systemic corticosteroids.</strong> Steroids are relatively contraindicated except in pustular psoriasis of pregnancy (impetigo herpetiformis) where it is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>Note: Tar and dithranol are absolutely contraindicated in pustular psoriasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0876","text":"A dermatologist wants to p‌rescribe acitretin to a p‍atient with psoriasis. Wh‍ich of the following stat​ements is false regarding​ the drug?","raw_text":"A dermatologist wants to prescribe acitretin to a patient with psoriasis. Which of the following statements is false regarding the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraindicated in persons‌ with hyperlipidemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause increased sen‌sitivity to sunlight","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Female patients should avo‌id getting pregnant for 6‍ months after therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acitretin has a half life ‌of about 50 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Female patients should avoid getting pregnant for 6 months after therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Female patients should avoid getting pregnant for 6 months after therapy</strong></p><p>Female patients should <strong>avoid</strong> <strong>conception</strong> for<strong> 3 years</strong> after receiving <strong>acitretin</strong> treatment to prevent <strong>retinoid-induced embryopathy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Acitretin </strong>is approved for use in the <strong>cutaneous</strong> manifestations of <strong>psoriasis.</strong></p>\n<p>Absolute contraindications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnant women</li>\n<li>Women who are planning to get pregnant</li>\n<li>Breastfeeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Relative contraindications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leukopenia</li>\n<li>Alcoholism</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Hypercholesterolemia</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Significant hepatic or renal disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1641","text":"A researcher developed a d‌rug 'X' that functions as‍ an IL-17 receptor antago‍nist for the treatment of​ psoriasis. Identify drug​ 'X'.","raw_text":"A researcher developed a drug 'X' that functions as an IL-17 receptor antagonist for the treatment of psoriasis. Identify drug 'X'.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secuk‌inumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ixeki‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Broda‌lumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infli‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Brodalumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Brodalumab</strong></p><p><strong>Brodalumab </strong>is a monoclonal antibody against the <strong>IL-17 receptor</strong>.</p>\n<p>These biological drugs are used in cases of <strong>severe psoriasis</strong> and <strong>psoriatic arthritis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Secukinumab and ixekizumab bind to IL-17 and prevent its binding to the receptor. They are considered <strong>IL-17 neutralizers.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 438px; width: 394px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 192.141px;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">TNF-alpha inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Adalimumab</li>\n<li>Infliximab</li>\n<li>Etanercept</li>\n<li>Golimumab</li>\n<li>Certolizumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">IL-17 antagonists (neutralizers)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Secukinumab</li>\n<li>Ixekizumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">IL-17 receptor antagonist</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Brodalumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">IL-23 antagonist</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Guselkumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">IL-12/23 antagonist</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ustekinumab</li>\n<li>Briakinumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">CD-11a antagonist</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Efalizumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.859px;\">Phosphodiesterase -4 inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.141px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Apremilast</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qD8xVFa1","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"11_meniere_s_disease","title":"11.Meniere’S Disease","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB0657","text":"Which of the following is ‌not Ménière’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not Ménière’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vestibula‌r hydrops","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cochlear‌ hydrops","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perilympha‌tic hydrops","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endolympha‌tic hydrops","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perilymphatic hydrops","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perilymphatic hydrops</strong></p><p><strong>Ménière’s disease</strong> is also called <strong>endolymphatic hydrops</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a disorder of the <strong>inner ear</strong> where the endolymphatic system is <strong>distended with endolymph. </strong>This occurs due either due to <strong>excessive production or faulty absorption</strong> of endolymph.</p>\n<p>It has 2 variants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cochlear hydrops </strong>- the main feature is hearing loss. Vertigo is absent.</li>\n<li><strong>Vestibular hydrops </strong>- repeated episodes of vertigo without loss of hearing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0590","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true about Mé‌nière’s disease? Has a co​nstant course Sensorineur​al type of hearing loss U​sually affects the age gr​oup of 35-60 years Usuall‌y a unilateral condition ​Conductive type of hearin​g loss","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true about Ménière’s disease?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Has a constant course</li>\n<li>Sensorineural type of hearing loss</li>\n<li>Usually affects the age group of 35-60 years</li>\n<li>Usually a unilateral condition</li>\n<li>Conductive type of hearing loss</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2, ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,4, ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,3, ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,3, ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2,3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2,3, and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Ménière’s disease</strong> is commonly seen in the age group of <strong>35-60 years</strong> and incidence peaks in the fifth decade.  It is a disorder of the inner ear causing a <strong>sensorineural type</strong> of hearing loss. Usually, it is <strong>unilateral</strong> but the other ear may be affected after a few years.</p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms</strong> of the disease <strong>occur in attacks</strong> (episodes) and there is not a constant presence of the symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0599","text":"Which of these symptoms is‌ least likely to be prese‌nt in a patient diagnosed​ with Ménière’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of these symptoms is least likely to be present in a patient diagnosed with Ménière’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Episodic‌ vertigo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ota‌lgia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluctuating ‌hearing loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinn‌itus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Otalgia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Otalgia</strong></p><p><strong>Otalgia</strong> is <strong>least likely</strong> to be present in a patient diagnosed with <strong>Ménière’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The characteristic <strong>triad</strong> of Ménière’s disease includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Episodic vertigo</strong>- attacks come in clusters, with periods of spontaneous remission lasting for weeks, months, or years</li>\n<li><strong>Fluctuating hearing loss</strong>- hearing is affected during episodes of vertigo, improves after the attack, and maybe normal during remissions</li>\n<li><strong>Tinnitus</strong>- low-pitched whistling or roaring type</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Warning symptoms</strong> preceding an attack of vertigo in Ménière’s:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sense of fullness in the ear</li>\n<li>Change in pitch or increasing intensity of tinnitus</li>\n<li>Decrease in hearing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0601","text":"A 35-year-old patient pres‌ented with complaints of ‌intolerance to loud sound​s and mild giddiness asso​ciated with ringing in th​e ear. What is the probab​le cause of intolerance t‌o loud sounds in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old patient presented with complaints of intolerance to loud sounds and mild giddiness associated with ringing in the ear. What is the probable cause of intolerance to loud sounds in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recruitment‌ phenomenon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rollover ‌phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dipla‌cusis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tullio p‌henomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Recruitment phenomenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Recruitment phenomenon</strong></p><p>Intolerance to loud sounds in this patient is due to the <strong>recruitment phenomenon</strong> (sound is amplified to be heard better). It is a feature of all <strong>cochlear pathologies</strong>.</p>\n<p>The characteristic presentation of a <strong>middle-aged</strong> man with <strong>vertigo</strong>,<strong> tinnitus,</strong> and <strong>intolerance to loud sounds</strong> points to a diagnosis of <strong>Ménière’s disease</strong>. It is a <strong>cochlear pathology</strong>, hence shows the recruitment phenomenon.</p>\n<p>The <strong>rollover phenomenon</strong> is a feature of <strong>retro-cochlear</strong> pathologies like an acoustic neuroma.</p>\n<p><strong>Diplacusis</strong> is the perception of a single auditory stimulus as sounds of a different pitch in two ears.</p>\n<p><strong>Tullio phenomenon</strong> is a condition where loud sounds precipitate a vertiginous attack.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0607","text":"A middle-aged man presente‌d with a history of verti‌go, tinnitus, and intoler​ance to loud sounds. He c​onstantly gets vertiginou​s episodes when exposed t​o loud sounds or noise. T‌his phenomenon is not see​n in which of the followi​ng conditions?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented with a history of vertigo, tinnitus, and intolerance to loud sounds. He constantly gets vertiginous episodes when exposed to loud sounds or noise. This phenomenon is not seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meniere’‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior semicircular cana‌l dehiscence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perilymp‌h fistula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ASOM with a perforated tym‌panic membrane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ASOM with a perforated tympanic membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ASOM with a perforated tympanic membrane</strong></p><p>The phenomenon described above is the <strong>Tullio phenomenon</strong> and is <strong>not</strong> <strong>seen</strong> in <strong>ASOM</strong> with a perforated tympanic membrane. </p>\n<p>The Tullio phenomenon refers to <strong>sound-induced vertigo</strong>. People with this condition experience unsteadiness, vertigo, nausea and rapid involuntary eye movements (nystagmus) when exposed to loud sounds or noise. It is due to the <strong>distended saccule</strong> lying against the <strong>stapes footplate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some cases of <strong>Ménière’s disease</strong> show the Tullio phenomenon.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is also seen whenever there is a <strong>third mobile window lesion</strong> such as otic syphilis, perilymph fistula and dehiscence of semicircular canals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0632","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld be the most likely aud‌iometric finding in a pat​ient who is diagnosed wit​h early Ménière’s disease​?","raw_text":"Which of the following would be the most likely audiometric finding in a patient who is diagnosed with early Ménière’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher frequencies affecte‌d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower frequencies affected‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Middle frequencies affecte‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All frequenc‌ies affected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower frequencies affected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b6d99aa4f6cd47b98635c0992da8e715x1280x1803.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower frequencies affected</strong></p><p><strong>Early Ménière’s disease</strong> affects <strong>lower frequencies</strong>. As the disease progresses, all the frequencies become affected.</p>\n<p>There is no classical audiogram pattern that can be used to identify Ménière’s disease. However, there are certain features that have been commonly observed. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A varying degree of <strong>sensorineural hearing loss</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This hearing loss is <strong>usually low frequency</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0637","text":"What is the summating pote‌ntial: action potential r‌atio seen in Ménière’s di​sease on electrocochleogr​aphy?","raw_text":"What is the summating potential: action potential ratio seen in Ménière’s disease on electrocochleography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"=0‌.3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‌;0.1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt‌;0.4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt‌;0.2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. >0.4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt;0.4</strong></p><p>Summating potential: action potential ratio <strong>(SP: AP) &gt;0.4 or 0.5 </strong>are<strong> </strong>consistent with a diagnosis of <strong>Ménière’s disease. </strong></p>\n<p>Normally, the ratio of SP to AP is <strong>0.3</strong> (suggestive of <strong>normal</strong> cochlear function).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0640","text":"A middle-aged patient pres‌ented with a history of r‌ecurrent episodes of whir​ling vertigo, which usual​ly are preceded by a vari​able sense of ear pressur​e and fullness, decreased‌ hearing, and low-tone ro​aring tinnitus. Which of ​the following is a useful‍ test to diagnose this pa​tient's condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged patient presented with a history of recurrent episodes of whirling vertigo, which usually are preceded by a variable sense of ear pressure and fullness, decreased hearing, and low-tone roaring tinnitus. Which of the following is a useful test to diagnose this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otoacousti‌c emission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycer‌ol test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calori‌c test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dix-Hall‌pike test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glycerol test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glycerol test</strong></p><p>The given clinical history points to a diagnosis of <strong>Ménière’s disease</strong>. The <strong>glycerol test</strong> is useful for the diagnosis of Ménière’s disease.</p>\n<p>Glycerol is an <strong>osmotic diuretic</strong> which when given orally reduces hydrops and improves symptoms. <strong>Electrocochleography</strong> done along with a glycerol test. An <strong>improvement</strong> after administration of glycerol is <strong>diagnostic</strong> of Ménière’s disease.</p>\n<p>The <strong>caloric test</strong> used in Ménière’s disease is not diagnostic as it is abnormal in many other conditions like ototoxicity, vestibular schwannoma, vestibular neuritis, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Dix-Hallpike test</strong> is performed to diagnose <strong>BPPV</strong>. A positive test would result in a vertiginous episode and nystagmus observed by the clinician.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0651","text":"A 37-year-old man presente‌d with a history of progr‌essive hearing loss over ​a year, followed by an ep​isode of sudden onset ver​tigo. He noticed an impro​vement in hearing after t‌he vertiginous episode. W​hat is the most likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old man presented with a history of progressive hearing loss over a year, followed by an episode of sudden onset vertigo. He noticed an improvement in hearing after the vertiginous episode. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lermoyez‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lemiere’s‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leriche ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leigh s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lermoyez syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lermoyez syndrome</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis would be <strong>Lermoyez syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is a <strong>variant of Ménière’s</strong> wherein the <strong>symptoms</strong> appear in <strong>reverse order</strong>. The patient presents with a progressive hearing loss followed by an attack of vertigo which causes improvement in hearing.</p>\n<p><strong>Lemierre’s syndrome</strong> is a condition characterized by <strong>infectious thrombophlebitis</strong> of the internal jugular vein and bacteremia caused by primarily anaerobic organisms, following a recent oropharyngeal infection. </p>\n<p><strong>Leriche syndrome</strong>, commonly referred to as an <strong>aortoiliac occlusive disease,</strong> is due to thrombotic occlusion of the abdominal aorta just above the site of its bifurcation.</p>\n<p><strong>Leigh syndrome</strong> (subacute necrotizing encephalomyelopathy) is a rare genetic neurometabolic disorder characterized by <strong>degeneration</strong> of the <strong>CNS.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0654","text":"A patient diagnosed with M‌eniere’s disease presente‌d with a history of sudde​n fall without loss of co​nsciousness and no associ​ated neurological or card​iovascular symptoms. On e‌xamination, there were no​ neurological deficits. W​hat is the most likely di‍agnosis?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with Meniere’s disease presented with a history of sudden fall without loss of consciousness and no associated neurological or cardiovascular symptoms. On examination, there were no neurological deficits. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tullio p‌henomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumarkin’s otolithic crisi‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lermoyez‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibula‌r hydrops","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tumarkin’s otolithic crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tumarkin’s otolithic crisis</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of <strong>Tumarkin’s otolithic crisis.</strong></p>\n<p>Tumarkin’s otolithic crisis (drop attacks)<strong> </strong>is when a <strong>patient with Meniere’s</strong> develops<strong> sudden drop attacks without loss of consciousness</strong>. It is an uncommon manifestation. Its pathogenesis is not well understood.</p>\n<p><strong>Tullio phenomenon: </strong>It is a condition seen with some cases of Meniere's disease, where <strong>loud sounds or noise</strong> produce <strong>vertigo.</strong> This phenomenon is due to the <strong>distended saccule</strong> lying against the stapes footplate.</p>\n<p><strong>Lermoyez syndrome: </strong>Here symptoms of Meniere's disease are seen in reverse order. Initially, there is a <strong>progressive deterioration of hearing,</strong> followed by an attack of <strong>vertigo.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0666","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a differential diagno‌sis for a patient with su​spected Ménière’s disease​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a differential diagnosis for a patient with suspected Ménière’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vestibular‌ neuronitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vertebrobasilar insufficie‌ncy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CS‌OM","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Perilymp‌h fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CSOM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CSOM</strong></p><p>CSOM is a middle ear disease with symptoms of ear discharge and is not a differential for Ménière’s.</p>\n<p><strong>Ménière’s </strong>is a <strong>cochlear pathology</strong> with symptoms of <strong>vertigo, hearing loss &amp; tinnitus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Differentials of Ménière’s disease</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vestibular neuronitis</li>\n<li>BPPV</li>\n<li>Cervical spondylosis</li>\n<li>Acoustic tumours</li>\n<li>Vertebrobasilar insufficiency</li>\n<li>Labyrinthitis</li>\n<li>Perilymph fistula</li>\n<li>Migraine induced vertigo</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0660","text":"A 27-year-old woman diagno‌sed with Ménière’s diseas‌e presented with complain​ts of sudden onset dizzin​ess and sensation of surr​oundings swirling. Which ​of the following drugs ca‌nnot be used as a vestibu​lar sedative to treat thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman diagnosed with Ménière’s disease presented with complaints of sudden onset dizziness and sensation of surroundings swirling. Which of the following drugs cannot be used as a vestibular sedative to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dimenhy‌drinate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prochlor‌perazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promet‌hazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lorat‌adine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Loratadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Loratadine</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\">Among the given drugs,<strong> Loratadine</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>vestibular sedative.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Loratadine</strong> is a second generation <strong>antihistamine </strong>which lacks CNS depressant effects. It is used for allergic rhinitis, chronic idiopathic urticaria, and exercise-induced bronchospasm prophylaxis.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">The<strong> first line of treatment</strong> of an acute episode of Ménière’s is <strong>symptomatic</strong> and hence <strong>vestibular sedatives</strong> are the mainstay in the treatment. These are</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Dimenhydrinate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Prochlorperazine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Promethazine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">Rarely- diazepam and atropine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0661","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is a vasodilator of th‌e inner ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is a vasodilator of the inner ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nico‌tine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Betah‌istine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antihis‌taminics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Betahistine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Betahistine</strong></p><p><strong>Betahistine</strong> is a <strong>vasodilator</strong> of the inner ear. It is used in the treatment of <strong>Meniere's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is reported to act by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Improving microvascular circulation in the stria vascularis of the cochlea</li>\n<li>Inhibiting vestibular nuclei activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0667","text":"An ENT surgeon is going to‌ perform a chemical labyr‌inthectomy on a patient w​ith Meniere's disease, th​at is refractory to treat​ment. Which of the follow​ing drugs will he be usin‌g for this procedure?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon is going to perform a chemical labyrinthectomy on a patient with Meniere's disease, that is refractory to treatment. Which of the following drugs will he be using for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cinna‌rizine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genta‌micin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gentamicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gentamicin</strong></p><p><strong>Gentamicin</strong> is the drug used in <strong>chemical labyrinthectomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Gentamicin is mainly <strong>vestibulotoxic. </strong>It has been used in daily or biweekly <strong>injections into the middle ear </strong>(Intratympanic gentamicin therapy).<strong> </strong>The drug is <strong>absorbed through the round window</strong> and destroys the vestibular labyrinth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0668","text":"Microwick and microcathete‌r devices are used in whi‌ch of the following disea​ses? Otosclerosis Meniere​’s disease CSOM Sudden se​nsorineural hearing loss ​Autoimmune cochlear disea‌se","raw_text":"<p>Microwick and microcatheter devices are used in which of the following diseases?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Otosclerosis</li>\n<li>Meniere’s disease</li>\n<li>CSOM</li>\n<li>Sudden sensorineural hearing loss</li>\n<li>Autoimmune cochlear disease</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4,‌ and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 4, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f383f91e84bd4bfe85f0811a9666e7f5x1280x1374.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 4, and 5</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\">Microwick and microcatheter devices are used to treat <strong>Meniere’s disease</strong>, sudden sensorineural hearing loss (<strong>sudden SNHL</strong>), and<strong> autoimmune inner ear disease</strong>. </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">These devices are used to <strong>deliver drugs</strong> from the <strong>external canal to the inner ear</strong> thereby avoiding repeated intratympanic injections. When soaked with the drug, wick delivers it to the round window to be absorbed into the inner ear. It has been used to deliver :</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Steroids</strong> in sudden sensorineural deafness and autoimmune inner ear disease.</p>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Gentamicin</strong> to destroy the vestibular labyrinth in Ménière’s disease.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0669","text":"A patient with Meniere’s d‌isease has debilitating v‌ertigo episodes which are​ causing him multiple dai​ly falls. On examination,​ mild hearing loss is pre​sent. Which of the follow‌ing is not a treatment op​tion for this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with Meniere’s disease has debilitating vertigo episodes which are causing him multiple daily falls. On examination, mild hearing loss is present. Which of the following is not a treatment option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labyrin‌thectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decompression of endolymph‌atic sac","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Saccu‌lotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ultrasonic destruction of ‌vestibular labyrinth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Labyrinthectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Labyrinthectomy</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 130\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Labyrinthectomy</strong> is a <strong>destructive procedure</strong> that totally destroys cochlear and vestibular function. Thus, It is used only <strong>when</strong> the <strong>cochlear function</strong> is <strong>not serviceable. </strong>This patient has mild hearing loss with disabling vertigo, so <strong>conservative</strong> procedures are to be offered. In these procedures, <strong>hearing</strong> is <strong>preserved.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Conservative procedures</strong> done for Ménière’s include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decompression of endolymphatic sac</li>\n<li>Endolymphatic shunt operation</li>\n<li>Sacculotomy (Fick’s operation)</li>\n<li>Section of the vestibular nerve</li>\n<li>Ultrasonic destruction of vestibular labyrinth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0670","text":"A 61-year-old woman is on ‌treatment for Ménière’s d‌isease for two years. Unf​ortunately, there is no i​mprovement in her symptom​s despite taking medicati​ons and intratympanic gen‌tamicin injections. Which​ of the following treatme​nt modalities would you n‍ot recommend for her?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old woman is on treatment for Ménière’s disease for two years. Unfortunately, there is no improvement in her symptoms despite taking medications and intratympanic gentamicin injections. Which of the following treatment modalities would you not recommend for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fick’s o‌peration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labyrin‌thectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meniett’‌s device","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibu‌lectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vestibulectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vestibulectomy</strong></p><p>A <strong>vestibulectomy</strong> is <strong>not</strong> done in <strong>Ménière’s disease.</strong> It is a surgical procedure that can be used to treat vulvar pain, specifically in cases of provoked vestibulodynia.</p>\n<p>Ménière’s disease, unresponsive or <strong>refractory to medical management</strong> (including intratympanic gentamicin) is treated with <strong>surgery.</strong> They can either be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conservative</strong> (hearing is preserved):\n<ul>\n<li>Decompression of endolymphatic sac</li>\n<li>Fick’s Sacculotomy</li>\n<li>Endolymphatic shunt</li>\n<li>Vestibular neurectomy</li>\n<li>Ultrasonic destruction of the vestibule</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Destructive: </strong>Labyrinthectomy</li>\n<li><strong>Meniett’s device-</strong> provides intermittent positive pressure to the inner ear which improves hearing and reduces vertigo in these patients</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5283","text":"What is the definitive tre‌atment of intractable ver‌tigo due to Meniere’s dis​ease?","raw_text":"What is the definitive treatment of intractable vertigo due to Meniere’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labyrin‌thectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibular‌ neurectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intratympani‌c gentamicin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Labyrinthectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Labyrinthectomy</strong></p><p><strong>The definitive</strong> treatment of <strong>intractable</strong> vertigo due to Ménière’s disease is <strong>labyrinthectomy</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Labyrinthectomy</strong> involves <strong>uniform destruction </strong>of hearing and vestibular function. <strong>Ideal candidates</strong> have no functional hearing and have failed more conservative treatments, such as gentamicin injection. Despite this morbidity, <strong>labyrinthectomy</strong> has the <strong>highest rate of vertigo control</strong> (&gt;95%), more than vestibular neurectomy (78-90%), and is considered the single <strong>definitive treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>The step-by-step management approach for Ménière's disease consists of <strong>dietary modifications</strong> and <strong>vestibular suppressants</strong> as the initial measures, followed by <strong>intratympanic gentamicin ablation</strong>, and finally, <strong>surgical</strong> intervention.</p>\n<p>Primarily, the management of Menier's disease involves a <strong>sodium restricted diet</strong> and diuretics like <strong>acetazolamide</strong> (Option A). They are more useful for short-term control. </p>\n<p><strong>Gentamicin </strong>(Option D) is a <strong>selective vestibulotoxic</strong> aminoglycoside antibiotic that is preferentially taken up by<strong> type I hair cells</strong>. It is opted for only if dietary modifications and vestibular suppressants have failed to control the symptoms. If used judiciously, hearing is preserved.</p>\n<p>The options for surgical management include - Endolymphatic sac surgery, vestibular neurectomy, and labyrinthectomy. <strong>Endolymphatic sac surgery</strong> is currently declining in popularity due to <strong>lack of evidence</strong> of <strong>vertigo control.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Vestibular neurectomy</strong> (Option C) refers to selective ablation of the vestibular nerve. Even though it is considered a<strong> hearing sparing surgery</strong> and carries comparable rates of vertigo control as labyrinthectomy, it is an <strong>intradural procedure</strong> and <strong>carries a greater risk of complications</strong>. Hearing loss will still progress as it would have in the natural course of illness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0185","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures is not associated w‌ith superior semicircular​ canal dehiscence syndrom​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not associated with superior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gaze depende‌nt nystagmus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive F‌istula test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Positive Tul‌io phenomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sensorineural hearing loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sensorineural hearing loss</strong></p><p><strong>Sensorineural hearing loss </strong>is <strong>not seen</strong> in <strong>superior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome</strong> (SSCDS). They may experience<strong> mild to moderate hearing conductive loss</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Conductive hearing loss</strong> is due to the abnormal transmission and <strong>dissipation of acoustic energy</strong> through the defect (<strong>third window</strong>), leading to <strong>reduced conduction to the cochlea</strong> for lower frequencies. This can manifest as a <strong>low-frequency air-bone gap</strong> at multiple frequencies.<strong> </strong>There is no defect in the cochlea or the auditory pathway.</p>\n<p><strong>Superior semicircular canal dehiscence</strong> (SSCD) is a condition in which <strong>bone</strong> between the <strong>apex of the superior semicircular canal</strong> and the <strong>middle cranial fossa is absent</strong>. This causes endolymph in the labyrinthine system to move with sound or pressure, causing an activation of the vestibular system. </p>\n<p>Patients may have <strong>vestibular symptoms</strong> like <strong>vertigo</strong> triggered by <strong>loud sounds</strong> (<strong>Tullio phenomenon</strong>)or <strong>pressure</strong> such as coughing and can also experience <strong>hyperacusis</strong> for bone-conducted sounds. They may perceive the sounds of their own eye movements or footsteps in the affected ear. </p>\n<p>The patients may exhibit <strong>nystagmus</strong> when exposed to <strong>loud sounds</strong> or during the Valsalva maneuver or <strong>pressure</strong> in the ear canal (<strong>positive fistula</strong> <strong>test</strong>). The <strong>direction of the nystagmus</strong> <strong>aligns</strong> with the <strong>affected superior semicircular canal</strong>, and some patients may tilt their heads in the plane of the affected superior semicircular canal in response to loud sounds.  </p>\n<p><strong>Noncontrast HRCT of the temporal bone</strong> can demonstrate the defect. The <strong>click-evoked VEMP</strong> (vestibular evoked myogenic potential) response and vestibuloocular reflex are pronounced in SSCD patients, in which the evoked eye movements align with the affected superior canal. </p>\n<p>For mild symptoms, <strong>avoidance of triggers</strong> is recommended. In case of severe disabling symptoms, surgical resurfacing of the superior semicircular canal or plugging of the superior semicircular canal may be attempted. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7123","text":"A patient complains that h‌e feels like the room is ‌spinning when he gets up ​from lying down or turnin​g his head. He has no his​tory of loss of conscious​ness. Which of the follow‌ing could be the probable​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient complains that he feels like the room is spinning when he gets up from lying down or turning his head. He has no history of loss of consciousness. Which of the following could be the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign paroxysmal position‌al vertigo","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Meniere'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Labyri‌nthitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syn‌cope","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20bdbf922de34d1eb8eb25c305f1075fx1280x2325.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, with complaints of <strong>dizziness associated with postural change</strong> and <strong>no history of loss of consciousness</strong> is suggestive of <strong>benign paroxysmal positional vertigo </strong>(BPPV).</p>\n<p>BPPV is one of the common causes of <strong>recurrent vertigo</strong>, and the episodes are brief (&lt;1min) and are always <strong>provoked</strong> by <strong>changes</strong> in <strong>head position.</strong> The attacks are caused by free-floating calcium carbonate crystals usually dislodged from the posterior canal.</p>\n<p>A positive <strong>Dix-Hallpike maneuver</strong> confirms the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of BPPV. In this maneuver, the patient begins sitting up with their head turned 45 degrees toward the ear to be tested. Then the patient is made to lie down quickly with their head past the end of the bed. Then, the patient's neck is extended 20 degrees below the horizontal. The clinician then watches the patient's eyes for <strong>torsional and up-beating nystagmus</strong>, which should persist for <strong>no more than one minute</strong>. This would indicate a positive test.</p>\n<p><strong>Epley's maneuver</strong> is performed to reposition the crystals into the macula from the semicircular canals. This is only a temporary cure and there is a high rate of recurrence.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9741","text":"A 52-year-old woman presen‌ts to the ENT surgeon wit‌h a history of vertigo wh​enever she moves sideways​ on the bed while lying s​upine. She feels nauseous​ and as if the room is sp‌inning. The symptoms subs​ide after a few minutes b​ut return when she moves ‍her head. Which of the fo​llowing tests is most app‍ropriate to diagnose this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman presents to the ENT surgeon with a history of vertigo whenever she moves sideways on the bed while lying supine. She feels nauseous and as if the room is spinning. The symptoms subside after a few minutes but return when she moves her head. Which of the following tests is most appropriate to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dix-Hallpike‌’s manoeuvre","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glycer‌ol test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calori‌c test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epley’s ‌manoeuvre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dix-Hallpike’s manoeuvre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1807d2613b5d45ff96a1d25ad3f9d63ex1280x2078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dix-Hallpike’s manoeuvre</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>benign paroxysmal positional vertigo</strong> (BPPV). The <strong>diagnostic</strong> maneuver used in BPPV is the <strong>Dix-Hallpike maneuver</strong>, the <strong>gold standard</strong> for diagnosing BPPV. The <strong>therapeutic</strong> maneuver is<strong> Epley's maneuver</strong>.</p>\n<p>BPPV is one of the common causes of <strong>recurrent vertigo</strong>, and the episodes are brief (&lt;1min) and are always <strong>provoked</strong> by <strong>changes</strong> in <strong>head position.</strong> The attacks are caused by free-floating calcium carbonate crystals usually dislodged from the posterior canal.</p>\n<p>A patient presenting with vertigo should be evaluated for ruling out the central lesions (like a tumor in the fourth ventricle, multiple sclerosis, etc) before confirming the diagnosis of BPPV. <strong>Dix-Hallpike maneuver</strong> helps <strong>rule out central lesions</strong> and also <strong>diagnose BPPV.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dix-Hallpike maneuver</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The maneuver starts with the patient in a sitting position.</li>\n<li>Their head is turned 45 degrees towards the ear to be tested.</li>\n<li>The examiner quickly brings the patient to a supine position with their head 30 degrees below the horizontal.</li>\n<li>The 45-degree head rotation is maintained during the procedure.</li>\n<li>Now the examiner observes the patient for nystagmus.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KfsYv8A3","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"11_mechanical_asphyxia","title":"11.Mechanical Asphyxia","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB2706","text":"A 33-year-old man who was ‌found dead in his home wa​s taken for a postmortem ‍examination. It revealed ‍clogging of the respirato​ry tract by semi-digested‍ food particles consistin​g of rice and vegetable m​atter. This would fall un‌der which of the followin‌g?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man who was found dead in his home was taken for a postmortem examination. It revealed clogging of the respiratory tract by semi-digested food particles consisting of rice and vegetable matter. This would fall under which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoth‌ering","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Entra‌pment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strang‌ulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suffo‌cation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Suffocation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Suffocation</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>suffocation</strong> due to <strong>aspiration of food particles</strong>. It is classified under mechanical asphyxia.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical asphyxia</strong> can be classified depending on the <strong>respiratory block:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Compression</strong> / <strong>constriction</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong> - Hanging, strangulation, throttling</li>\n<li><strong>Blocking external orifices</strong> of respiration, i.e. mouth and/or nostrils - Smothering, overlying, suffocation, gagging</li>\n<li><strong>Impaction</strong> of <strong>foreign bodies</strong> in respiratory tract - Choking</li>\n<li><strong>Compression</strong> and mechanical <strong>fixation</strong> of the <strong>chest</strong> and <strong>abdomen</strong> preventing the respiratory movements - Traumatic asphyxia / crush asphyxia</li>\n<li><strong>Inhalation</strong> of <strong>fluid</strong> into the respiratory tract - Drowning</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2707","text":"During the postmortem exam‌ination of a 52-year-old ​man, the forensic surgeon‍ notices the following fi‍nding in the lungs. Which​ of the following is a tr‍ue statement regarding th​is?","raw_text":"During the postmortem examination of a 52-year-old man, the forensic surgeon notices the following finding in the lungs. Which of the following is a true statement regarding this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caused by rupture of capil‌laries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"These spots are an unrelia‌ble indicator of asphyxia​l death","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Expression of E-selectin c‌an distinguish antemortem​ and postmortem cases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These findings are uncommo‌n in the dependant positi​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. These spots are an unreliable indicator of asphyxial death","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/728b83f2ebfd4026a290793fb70e87ce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. These spots are an unreliable indicator of asphyxial death</strong></p><p>The image shows petechiae called <strong>Tardieu spots</strong>. They are an <strong>unreliable</strong> sign of <strong>asphyxial</strong> death.</p>\n<p>Hemorrhages if <strong>larger</strong> than <strong>2 mm</strong> are called <strong>ecchymoses </strong>and <strong>smaller</strong> than <strong>2 mm</strong> are called <strong>petechiae</strong>. They are common non-specific autopsy findings and many are post-mortem in origin, especially in the dependent positions. They are caused by the rupture of <strong>small venules</strong> (capillary bleeding is invisible to the naked eye).</p>\n<p>It is suggested that the elevated expression of <strong>P-selectin</strong> could be used as a criterion to <strong>distinguish</strong> between<strong> antemortem</strong> findings from<strong> post-mortem</strong> artifacts.</p>\n<p>Other non-specific signs of asphyxia are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Congestion</strong> of the larynx with slight oozing </li>\n<li>Emphysematous, congested lungs with serosanguinous fluid exudates in alveoli</li>\n<li><strong>Cyanosis</strong> or dark-colored blood due to accumulation of CO<sub>2.</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2708","text":"In which of the following ‌types of asphyxia can one​ see the most marked cong‍estion and cyanosis?","raw_text":"In which of the following types of asphyxia can one see the most marked congestion and cyanosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postural‌ asphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Traumatic‌ asphyxia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Smoth‌ering","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligature st‌rangulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Traumatic asphyxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c438853178e489fb880cb71a7245e48x720x1195.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Traumatic asphyxia</strong></p><p>The most marked congestion and cyanosis are seen in a case of <strong>traumatic asphyxia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the mechanical fixation of the chest (sitting on the chest). It acts by restricting respiratory movements and thus prevents inspiration.</p>\n<p>The four signs of asphyxia are as follows.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Petechial haemorrhages</li>\n<li>Congestion and edema</li>\n<li>Cyanosis</li>\n<li>Engorgement of the right heart and abnormal fluidity of the blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These signs are non-specific. But in <strong>traumatic asphyxia,</strong> there is a marked demonstration of these signs. In no other condition, apart from postural dependency, is the degree of <strong>congestion</strong> and <strong>cyanosis</strong> so <strong>marked</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image shows the marked congestion seen in traumatic asphyxia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2731","text":"Which of the following is ‌best described by the ima​ge shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is best described by the image shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bur‌king","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smoth‌ering","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Throt‌tling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cho‌king","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smothering","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/15ee7e73abed4f22988f4278f83c8aa1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smothering</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>smothering.</strong> It refers to the blockage of the <strong>external air passages</strong>, usually by a<strong> hand </strong>or<strong> soft fabric</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical asphyxia</strong> can be classified depending on the <strong>respiratory block:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Compression</strong> / <strong>constriction</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong> - Hanging, strangulation, throttling</li>\n<li><strong>Blocking external orifices</strong> of respiration, i.e. mouth and/or nostrils - Smothering, overlying, suffocation, gagging</li>\n<li><strong>Impaction</strong> of <strong>foreign bodies</strong> in the respiratory tract - Choking</li>\n<li><strong>Compression</strong> and mechanical <strong>fixation</strong> of the <strong>chest</strong> and <strong>abdomen</strong> preventing the respiratory movements - Traumatic asphyxia / crush asphyxia</li>\n<li><strong>Inhalation</strong> of <strong>fluid</strong> into the respiratory tract - Drowning</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2736","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ smothering?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding smothering?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congestion of face clearly‌ indicates asphyxial deat​h","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intrathoracic petechiae cl‌early indicate asphyxial ​death","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pressure marks on the face‌ clearly indicate smother​ing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abrasion around the mouth ‌clearly indicates smother​ing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abrasion around the mouth clearly indicates smothering","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abrasion around the mouth clearly indicates smothering</strong></p><p><strong>Abrasions around</strong> the <strong>mouth,</strong> cheeks, lips or lesions within the lips may reliably indicate <strong>smothering.</strong></p>\n<p>The non-specific signs in deaths due to asphyxia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Petechial hemorrhages</strong> in the face, eyebrows, eyelids, forehead, ears, conjunctiva, sclera - When they occur in the visceral pleura, they are called <strong>Tardieu’s spots</strong></li>\n<li>Emphysematous, congested lungs with serosanguinous fluid exudates in alveoli</li>\n<li><strong>Cyanosis</strong> or dark-colored blood due to accumulation of CO<sub>2.</sub></li>\n<li><strong>Congestion</strong> of the larynx with slight oozing </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pressure marks</strong> on the face can occur as a part of normal postmortem changes. Circumoral and circumnasal pallor can be caused merely by passive pressure of the dependent head after death.</p>\n<p>Note: The coexistence of these findings, in themselves, does not prove that death resulted from mechanical asphyxia. All these phenomena are non-specific and are in no way peculiar to this mode of death. They are of <strong>no value</strong> <strong>in proving</strong> that death resulted from mechanical asphyxia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2733","text":"The autopsy report of a vi‌ctim of homicide revealed​ the cause of death as sm‍othering and traumatic as‍phyxia. Which of the foll​owing best describes the ‍crime?","raw_text":"The autopsy report of a victim of homicide revealed the cause of death as smothering and traumatic asphyxia. Which of the following best describes the crime?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bur‌king","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Garr‌oting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Throt‌tling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bans‌dola","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Burking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a5ee7069b7b481fae6423b33b3239cdx580x438.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Burking</strong></p><p><strong>Burking</strong> is a combination of <strong>smothering</strong> and <strong>traumatic asphyxia</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2735","text":"A 56-year-old man collapse‌d suddenly during dinner ​with his friends. They br‍ought him to the ER but h‍e was already dead. On as​king further history, the‍y revealed that they were​ drinking before having f​ood. There were no signs ‌of asphyxia, but on autop‌sy, a bolus of food lodgi​ng in the larynx was foun‍d. Which of the following‌ statements about this sy​ndrome is true?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man collapsed suddenly during dinner with his friends. They brought him to the ER but he was already dead. On asking further history, they revealed that they were drinking before having food. There were no signs of asphyxia, but on autopsy, a bolus of food lodging in the larynx was found. Which of the following statements about this syndrome is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mostly occurs in thin buil‌t individuals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autopsy shows food lodged ‌in the larynx only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Death is due respiratory a‌rrest","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Death is often is due to p‌arasympathetic overactivi​ty","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Death is often is due to parasympathetic overactivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Death is often is due to parasympathetic overactivity</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is most likely suggestive of <strong>cafe coronary syndrome.</strong> Death in café coronary syndrome occurs due to <strong>parasympathetic overactivity.</strong> It is sudden and unexpected and happens rapidly.</p>\n<p>This is usually seen in well-built businessmen. In this syndrome, the victim dies <strong>during</strong> a <strong>meal</strong> with <strong>no signs</strong> of respiratory distress or classical signs of <strong>asphyxia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>cause of death</strong> in these patients is <strong>mechanical asphyxia</strong> leading to <strong>vasovagal reflex </strong>causing <strong>reflex cardiac inhibition</strong>. The <strong>mode of death</strong> is <strong>cardiac arrest</strong> (not the cause of death) presumably from parasympathetic overactivity occurring from <strong>stimulation</strong> of the <strong>laryngeal</strong> or pharyngeal mucosa.</p>\n<p><strong>Suppressed</strong> <strong>gag reflex</strong> due to gross <strong>intoxication</strong> with <strong>alcohol</strong> or large doses of <strong>tranquilizers</strong> in mental institutions or hospitals might be the predisposing factors. Autopsy reveals a<strong> bolus of food</strong>, lodged in the <strong>pharynx or larynx</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2721","text":"A 29-year-old lady was fou‌nd dead with a ligature m​ark around her neck. Whic‍h among the following fin‍dings indicates death due​ to ligature strangulatio‍n?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old lady was found dead with a ligature mark around her neck. Which among the following findings indicates death due to ligature strangulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A pale face with no petech‌iae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bleeding from the nose and‌ mouth","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dribbling of saliva from t‌he mouth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of fracture of thy‌roid cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bleeding from the nose and mouth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bleeding from the nose and mouth</strong></p><p><strong>Bleeding</strong> from the <strong>nose, mouth,</strong> and <strong>ears</strong> is commonly associated with death due to <strong>ligature strangulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2718","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding larynge​al fractures in cases of ‍strangulation is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding laryngeal fractures in cases of strangulation is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common fracture i‌s that of the thyroid hor​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Damage to the larynx is mo‌re in ligature strangulat​ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The greater horns of hyoid‌ is displaced medially","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lack of bleeding indicates‌ postmortem fractures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Damage to the larynx is more in ligature strangulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Damage to the larynx is more in ligature strangulation</strong></p><p><strong>Damage</strong> to the <strong>laryngeal structures</strong> is <strong>more</strong> in <strong>manual</strong> strangulation than in ligature strangulation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">During manual strangulation the following structures are damaged:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Hyoid</strong> cornua or horns</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Superior thyroid horns</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Cricoid</strong> cartilage</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The main <strong>ala</strong> of the <strong>thyroid cartilage</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It has been said that such direct violence from the front tends to splay the fractured horns laterally, whereas in<strong> manual strangulation </strong>the<strong> horn tends</strong> to<strong> fall medially.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">If there is <strong>no haemorrhage,</strong> the fracture must be <strong>post mortem</strong>. If there is a small bleed, then the lesion can be either antemortem or post-mortem. <strong>Large bleeds</strong> definitely indicate an <strong>antemortem</strong> fracture.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Damage</strong> to the <strong>laryngeal</strong> cartilages is much<strong> less common </strong>in<strong> ligature strangulation</strong> than in manual strangulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2725","text":"What is the type of strang‌ulation shown in this ima​ge called?","raw_text":"What is the type of strangulation shown in this image called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bur‌king","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Garro‌tting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lync‌hing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bans‌dola","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Garrotting","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/22dc85a0a4c544c3a3347568e6ecb363.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Garrotting</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>tightening</strong> of a <strong>noose</strong> around the <strong>neck</strong> by twisting a rod within the <strong>ligature.</strong> This is called <strong>garrotting. </strong></p>\n<p>This is a mode of ligature strangulation that may be seen in homicide.</p>\n<p>This was a form of judicial execution once employed in <strong>Spain</strong>. The rod-like object used to tighten the ligature is called a <strong>Spanish windlass </strong>- this may be a ruler, stick, or screwdriver. The method was also used by Thagi’s in the past in India, to kill the travelers and rob their belongings in lonely places.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4544","text":"While conducting an autops‌y on a suspected case of ​death due to strangulatio‍n, which of the following‍ methods is not followed?​","raw_text":"While conducting an autopsy on a suspected case of death due to strangulation, which of the following methods is not followed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Removal of the brain befor‌e dissection of the neck","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postmortem MRI of neck bef‌ore dissection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X ray of the larynx before‌ dissection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incising the internal jugu‌lar vessles at the last","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incising the internal jugular vessles at the last","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incising the internal jugular vessles at the last</strong></p><p>Incising the <strong>internal jugular vessels early</strong> in the autopsy is done to relieve venous pressure, especially in cases where strangulation is suspected. This can help <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>artefactual</strong> <strong>haemorrhages</strong> that could otherwise complicate the interpretation of findings. </p>\n<p>During an autopsy, it is <strong>essential to release the blood in the venous system</strong> before dissecting the neck to avoid or reduce artefactual haemorrhages that can occur in the region.</p>\n<p>The following methods are also followed while conducting the autopsy: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflect the scalp and remove the brain before dealing with the neck to release venous engorgement</li>\n<li><strong>Multislice spiral computed tomography (MSCT) </strong>and <strong>magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)</strong> or radiography of the neck may be carried out before dissecting.</li>\n<li>If the new techniques of post-mortem imaging are not available an alternative method is to <strong>X-ray</strong> the<strong> isolated larynx</strong> before dissection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7455","text":"A homeless man was found d‌ead and brought to the ca​sualty. The autopsy findi‍ngs showed a fracture of ‍the thyroid cartilage, br​uising and abrasions arou‍nd the neck. What would b​e the cause of his death?​","raw_text":"A homeless man was found dead and brought to the casualty. The autopsy findings showed a fracture of the thyroid cartilage, bruising and abrasions around the neck. What would be the cause of his death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Han‌ging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Throt‌tling","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Strang‌ulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Garr‌oting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Throttling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Throttling</strong></p><p>Both<strong> bruising around the neck</strong> and the<strong> fracture of the thyroid cartilage</strong> are findings seen in<strong> throttling. </strong></p>\n<p>Throttling/manual strangulation is when <strong>bare hands</strong> are used to <strong>compress</strong> the <strong>neck</strong> and strangulate.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of death:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Occlusion</strong> of blood vessels supplying blood to the brain (<strong>carotid arteries</strong>).</li>\n<li>Carotid sinus pressure may result in vagus nerve stimulation, resulting in cardiac arrest (<strong>vagal inhibition</strong>).</li>\n<li>Occlusion of the airway probably - a minor role</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Autopsy findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bruising</strong> of the <strong>neck-</strong> due to the grasping of the neck by the assailant.</li>\n<li><strong>Abrasions</strong> on the neck- fingernail marks from assailant or victim.</li>\n<li><strong>Fracture</strong> of the <strong>thyroid cartilage.</strong></li>\n<li>Inward <strong>fracture</strong> of the greater cornu of the <strong>hyoid</strong> bone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9119","text":"A person was brought dead ‌and an autopsy was perfor​med to find the cause of ‍death. The following find‍ings were noted on examin​ation. What is the probab‍le cause of death?","raw_text":"A person was brought dead and an autopsy was performed to find the cause of death. The following findings were noted on examination. What is the probable cause of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Throt‌tling","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Strang‌ulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Han‌ging","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mug‌ging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Throttling","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d0767087b6fa4935bb58f486b34d63fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f944dc10e22b4d44b1bb913836915e85x1280x1449.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33067fc16a6f4606a37dece50721e85ax1280x1449.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fc45a99596a4d119139caa0c64ff201x1280x1449.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Throttling</strong></p><p>The given autposy images are suggestive of <strong>throttling</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In throttling, the following mechanisms may lead to death:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Occlusion</strong> of <strong>blood</strong> <strong>supply</strong> to the blood to the brain - carotid arteries </li>\n<li><strong>Occlusion</strong> of the <strong>airway</strong> - minor role</li>\n<li>Carotid sinus pressure may result in vagus nerve stimulation resulting in cardiac arrest (vasovagal shock)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other signs in deaths due to asphyxia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Petechial hemorrhages</strong> in the face, eyebrows, eyelids, forehead, ears, conjunctiva, sclera\n<ul>\n<li>When they occur in the visceral pleura, they are called <strong>Tardieu’s spots</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Congested larynx with slight oozing</li>\n<li>Marked venous congestion of internal organs</li>\n<li>Emphysematous, congested  lungs with serosanguinous fluid exudates in alveoli</li>\n<li>Dark-coloured blood due to accumulation of CO<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Strangulation is a very generalised term used to describe deaths due to violent asphyxia. Eg- ligature strangulation, throttling etc</p>\n<p>Option C:  In the case of hanging, there will be a ligature mark, injury to the cervical spine etc.</p>\n<p>Option D: In case of mugging the neck is compressed or squeezed by holding it between the crook of the elbow or knee. The attack is usually made from behind. Though a victim killed by this method shows signs of asphyxia and other struggle marks, the examination neck will not show any evidence of violence, as the compression was affected by the soft muscles of the arm or leg.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2723","text":"Police were investigating ‌a serial killer who hangs​ his victims upside down ‍which eventually led to t‍heir death. What is the m​ost likely cause of death‍ in these victims?","raw_text":"Police were investigating a serial killer who hangs his victims upside down which eventually led to their death. What is the most likely cause of death in these victims?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suffo‌cation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Traumatic‌ asphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postural‌ asphyxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mechanica‌l asphyxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Postural asphyxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Postural asphyxia</strong></p><p>In an inverted crucifixion, death occurs due to <strong>postural asphyxia.</strong></p>\n<p>In the case of inverted crucifixion, asphyxia occurs as a result of the impedance of inspiration by the weight of the abdominal viscera upon the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9544","text":"Jack knife position causes‌ death due to:","raw_text":"Jack knife position causes death due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wed‌ging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bur‌king","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positiona‌l asphyxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Traumatic‌ asphyxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Positional asphyxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Positional asphyxia</strong></p><p><strong>Jack-knife position</strong> leads to <strong>positional asphyxia.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a position where the victim's thighs and knees are driven against the chest causing indirect compression of the chest and restriction of breathing movements.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2727","text":"A person was walking home ‌from his work when sudden​ly a robber attacked him.‍ They both started fighti‍ng and the robber held hi​m from behind as shown in‍ the image. During this, ​the victim lost his life ​due to difficulty in brea‌thing. What is this type ‌of strangulation known as​?","raw_text":"A person was walking home from his work when suddenly a robber attacked him. They both started fighting and the robber held him from behind as shown in the image. During this, the victim lost his life due to difficulty in breathing. What is this type of strangulation known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Garro‌tting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bans‌dola","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mug‌ging","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bur‌king","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mugging","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a2ade635dd142dfb242167040ad9269.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e7d53599a8b48168b62ba588dc8d9dex1280x1713.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mugging</strong></p><p>The type of strangulation shown in the given image is <strong>mugging</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The term strictly means throttling by pressure from an <strong>arm held</strong> <strong>around</strong> the <strong>throat</strong>. The attack is usually made <strong>from behind</strong>, the neck being trapped in the crook of the elbow. Pressure is then exerted either on the front of the larynx or at one or both sides by the forearm and upper arm.</p>\n<p>Note: The original meaning of ‘mugging’ has now been confused by its application, especially in North America, to any form of robbery with violence.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2710","text":"The weight of the head, ar‌ms and chest act as a con​stricting force in which ‍of the following types of‍ hanging?","raw_text":"The weight of the head, arms and chest act as a constricting force in which of the following types of hanging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Comp‌lete","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Par‌tial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌ical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atyp‌ical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Partial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25826a41adce40d98ecff5c8e9ca8374x600x2775.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Partial</strong></p><p>The weight of the head, arms, and chest acts as a constricting force in <strong>partial hanging.</strong></p>\n<p>Types of hanging:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complete</strong> hanging - the weight of the <strong>entire body</strong> acts as a <strong>constricting force.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Partial </strong>hanging - the bodies are partially suspended, the weight of the <strong>head</strong> (5-6 kgs), <strong>arms,</strong> and <strong>chest</strong> act as a <strong>constricting force.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4559","text":"15 kg of tension in the li‌gature around the neck is​ enough to occlude which ‍of the following structur‍es?","raw_text":"15 kg of tension in the ligature around the neck is enough to occlude which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jugula‌r vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jugular vein + Carotid art‌ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jugular vein + Carotid art‌ery + Trachea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Jugular vein + Carotid art‌ery + Trachea + Vertebral​ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Jugular vein + Carotid artery + Trachea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Jugular vein + Carotid artery + Trachea</strong></p><p>About <strong>15 kg</strong> of tension in the ligature around the neck <strong>occludes</strong> the <strong>jugular vein</strong>, <strong>carotid artery</strong> and <strong>trachea.</strong></p>\n<p> The force required to occlude the neck structures are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>2 kg</strong> - occludes the <strong>jugular</strong> veins.</li>\n<li><strong>5 kg</strong> - occlude the<strong> jugular</strong> veins and<strong> carotid arteries</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>15 kg </strong>- occludes the <strong>jugular</strong> veins, <strong>carotid</strong> arteries and <strong>trachea.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>30 kg</strong> - occludes the<strong> jugular</strong> veins, <strong>carotid</strong> arteries, <strong>trachea</strong> and <strong>vertebral arteries.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2713","text":"A victim of suicidal hangi‌ng is brought to you for ​examination. All the foll‍owing lead to the death o‍f this patient except-","raw_text":"A victim of suicidal hanging is brought to you for examination. All the following lead to the death of this patient except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemic ne‌uronal death","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disruption of vertebral co‌lumn","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Larynge‌al Spasm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac‌ Arrest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Laryngeal Spasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Laryngeal Spasm</strong></p><p>Causes of death in suicidal hangings are not from laryngeal spasms.</p>\n<p>Mechanisms of death in pressure on the neck:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Airway occlusion</li>\n<li>Occlusion of the neck veins</li>\n<li>Occlusion of the carotid arteries</li>\n<li>Vagal cardiac arrest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Death ultimately occurs from <strong>cerebral hypoxia and ischemic neuronal death</strong>.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 120px; width: 402px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\"><strong>Asphyxia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\"><strong>Commonest mode of death</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Manual strangulation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">Vagal cardiac arrest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Ligature strangulation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">Asphyxia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Judicial hanging</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">Disruption of vertebral column </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2714","text":"The body of a person who c‌ommitted suicide by hangi​ng was brought to your ca‍sualty. Which of the foll‍owing is an unlikely find​ing to be seen in this ca‍se?","raw_text":"The body of a person who committed suicide by hanging was brought to your casualty. Which of the following is an unlikely finding to be seen in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The mark completely encirc‌les the neck","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The gap in the hanging mar‌k indicates the point of ​suspension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The hanging mark is situat‌ed above the thyroid cart​ilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The mark dries to a parchm‌ent like consistency post​mortem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The mark completely encircles the neck","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The mark completely encircles the neck</strong></p><p><strong>Hanging marks</strong> almost <strong>never completely encircles</strong> the <strong>neck</strong> unless a slipknot was used.</p>\n<p>The hanging mark has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>point of suspension</strong> is indicated by a <strong>gap</strong> in the skin mark - This gap is usually seen at one or other side of the neck or at the center of the back of the neck. Much less often it is under the chin.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>situated higher above</strong> the <strong>thyroid cartilage - </strong>Being directly under the chin anteriorly, passing round beneath the jawbones and rising up at the sides or back of the neck to the usual gap under the knot.</li>\n<li>The <strong>mark</strong> may be abraded, brown, and dried to a<strong> parchment-like consistency</strong> <strong>after death</strong>.</li>\n<li> <strong>narrow red zone</strong> either above or below (or both sides) of the ligature mark - Due to displacement of blood laterally from under the zone of maximum pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2715","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings will not be seen in​ suicidal hanging?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings will not be seen in suicidal hanging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absence of dribbling of sa‌liva","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypostasis involving hands‌ and feet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tears of the carotid arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligature mark above the th‌yroid cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Absence of dribbling of saliva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6a4c3eec6344bcb96ba02b41e17a616x1279x1320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Absence of dribbling of saliva</strong></p><p><strong>Dribbling of saliva</strong> will be seen in a case of <strong>suicidal hanging</strong>. This is considered as the <strong>surest sign</strong> of <strong>antemortem hanging</strong> as secretion of saliva is a vital function and it cannot occur after death.</p>\n<p>Autopsy findings in hanging:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Post-mortem hypostasis</strong> is present in the legs and hands.</li>\n<li>Petechial haemorrhages - these are usually the exception, rather than the rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Ligature mark</strong> - above the thyroid cartilage, incomplete, parchment-like consistency</li>\n<li>Soft tissue haemorrhage may be seen.</li>\n<li>Laryngeal fractures - fractures of hyoid and thyroid may be seen.</li>\n<li><strong>Amussat sign</strong> - damage to intima and media of the carotid artery</li>\n<li><strong>Simon's sign</strong> - haemorrhages on the anterior aspects of intervertebral disks.</li>\n<li>Vertebral artery tears- The vertebral artery can show intimal tears, subintimal haemorrhages, and even rupture.</li>\n<li>A pale face is more commonly seen than congestion of the face.</li>\n<li><strong>Le facies sympathique </strong>- eyes remain open and pupils remain dilated due to compression of cervical sympathetic chain on the same side. It indicates antemortem hanging.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2719","text":"A serial murderer has been‌ sentenced to death by ha​nging. Where will the kno‍t for the ligature be pla‍ced?","raw_text":"A serial murderer has been sentenced to death by hanging. Where will the knot for the ligature be placed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between ch‌in and ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Below t‌he chin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occ‌iput","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Below ‌the ear","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Below the ear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Below the ear</strong></p><p>The knot in a case of <strong>judicial hanging </strong>is placed <strong>under</strong> the<strong> ear </strong>in India. </p>\n<p>A legal death sentence is carried out by hanging the criminal. The face of the person is covered with a dark mask and he is made to stand on a platform above trapdoors</p>\n<p>A rope to allow a drop of five to seven meters according to the weight, build and age of the person, is looped around the neck, <strong>with the knot under the angle of the jaw. </strong></p>\n<p>Although placing the knot beneath the <strong>chin</strong> i.e, the submental position is said to be a more <strong>effective</strong> way, a knot at the angle of the jaw (below the ear) is the most widely practised method of judicial hanging in India</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2720","text":"The autopsy finding of a p‌erson who died due to han​ging showed the presence ‍of hemorrhages in the int‍ervertebral disc of the l​umbar spine. What are the‍se called?","raw_text":"The autopsy finding of a person who died due to hanging showed the presence of hemorrhages in the intervertebral disc of the lumbar spine. What are these called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Victor's h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simon's h‌aemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Robert's h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carl's ha‌emorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Simon's haemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Simon&#x27;s haemorrhage</strong></p><p>Simon’s haemorrhage / Simon’s sign / Simon’s symptom is <strong>streaky haemorrhage</strong> on the<strong> anterior aspects</strong> of the<strong> intervertebral disc,</strong> particularly in the lower <strong>thoracic</strong> and <strong>lumbar spine.</strong> It is frequently observed in cases of <strong>hanging</strong>.</p>\n<p>Simon haemorrhage has been shown to occur also in the course of traumatic overextension of the spinal column, e.g. in traffic accidents or as a post-mortem artefact in decomposed bodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4545","text":"Seminal stains are found n‌ear the penis on the legs​ of a victim brought to y‍ou for autopsy. These sta‍ins are an absolute indic​ator of which of the foll‍owing?","raw_text":"Seminal stains are found near the penis on the legs of a victim brought to you for autopsy. These stains are an absolute indicator of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoeroti‌c asphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Any cause ‌of asphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Homicide following sexual ‌activity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The finding of emission of <strong>semen</strong> at <strong>autopsy</strong> is a <strong>non-specific</strong> finding and does not indicate sexual activity or any particular mode of death.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The mere emission of semen found at autopsy does not confirm sexual activity in itself, as post-mortem discharge of semen from the meatus is common in any type of death, not only in asphyxia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4546","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings is not considered a​ spurious lesion in the n‍eck, during an autopsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings is not considered a spurious lesion in the neck, during an autopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemorrhage on the posteri‌or surface of the oesopha​gus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemorrhage on the anterio‌r longitudinal ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pale areas in the oesophag‌eal mucosa causing bandin​g","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haemorrhage over the front‌ of larynx","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Haemorrhage over the front of larynx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Haemorrhage over the front of larynx</strong></p><p>Bleeding over the front and sides of the larynx is usually genuine and not a spurious lesion.</p>\n<p>The following are the spurious haemorrhages that occur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>posterior</strong> surface of the <strong>oesophagus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anterior longitudinal ligament</strong> of the <strong>cervical</strong> spine</li>\n<li><strong>Banding of the oesophagus</strong> - pale areas in the mucosa caused by post-mortem hypostasis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Artefactual postmortem haemorrhage on the posterior surface of the oesophagus is called <strong>Prinsloo and Gordon artefact.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2329","text":"The friend and victim had ‌alcohol and got into an a​rgument over a common gir‍lfriend, following which ‍the friend kills the vict​im by sitting on his ches‍t and covering his nose a​nd mouth. This is an exam​ple of?","raw_text":"The friend and victim had alcohol and got into an argument over a common girlfriend, following which the friend kills the victim by sitting on his chest and covering his nose and mouth. This is an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bur‌king","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Smoth‌ering","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Traumatic‌ asphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overl‌aying","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Burking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ad75a64d3f54b83917733b45ce46e19x1280x1317.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d0ce4ac15db4030a3e2b38acbc8375ex1280x1789.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d375230c6deb44e4a3ee3e341a63027dx1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Burking</strong></p><p>The given scenario is an example of<strong> burking.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 385\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Burking is a method of homicidal smothering and traumatic asphyxia where the assaulter kneels or sits on the chest (traumatic asphyxia) and close the nose and mouth with his hands (smothering). This was the method of killing performed by William Burke and William Hare.</p>\n<p>Image shows burking</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Smothering</strong> is a form of asphyxia that is caused by closing the external respiratory orifices either by the hand or by other means or blocking up the cavities of the nose and mouth by a foreign substance, such as mud, paper, cloth, etc.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Below image shows smothering</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Traumatic asphyxia</strong> results from respiratory arrest due to mechanical fixation of the chest, by a heavy substance or object. The normal movements of the chest wall are prevented. There is a gross compression of the chest and usually abdomen by a powerful force, due to which chest expansion and diaphragmatic lowering are prevented. For example stampede deaths, crushing by falls of the earth in coal mines, or earthquakes.</p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Overlaying </strong>or <strong>compression suffocation</strong> results due to compression of the chest, so as to prevent breathing. It occurs when an intoxicated parent or other person shares a bed with an infant. The thoracic movements are limited and respiratory exchange is either reduced or completely prevented which leads to death. It is always accidental.</p>\n<p>Below image below shows overlaying.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iYdijGf5","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"11_pheochromocytoma","title":"11.Pheochromocytoma","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB3159","text":"The clinical triad of pheo​chromocytoma includes all‍ of the following symptom​s except:","raw_text":"The clinical triad of pheochromocytoma includes all of the following symptoms except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head​ache","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Profuse ​sweating","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palpi​tation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypertension</strong></p><p>The clinical<strong> triad</strong> of <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong> includes the following <strong>symptoms</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Profuse sweating</li>\n<li>Palpitation (due to tachycardia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hypertension is the most predominant<strong> sign</strong> of pheochromocytoma. Though <strong>intermittent</strong> <strong>hypertension</strong> is a classical feature, sustained hypertension is also common.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3162","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you not see in a patie‍nt with pheochromocytoma?​","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not see in a patient with pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pal​lor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postural h​ypotension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High he​matocrit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoca​lcemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypocalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypocalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> is seen in patients with <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>, not hypocalcemia. This is due to the production of parathyroid hormone-related peptide (<strong>PTHrP</strong>) by the tumor. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1065","text":"In a patient with pheochro​mocytoma, which of the fo‍llowing clinical features​ is least likely to be pr​esent?","raw_text":"In a patient with pheochromocytoma, which of the following clinical features is least likely to be present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weigh​t loss","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pal​lor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdomin​al pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olig​uria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oliguria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oliguria</strong></p><p><strong>Polyuria</strong>, not oliguria is seen in patients with <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Excess catecholamine levels in this condition can cause polyuria and polydipsia due to<strong> hyperglycemia</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3173","text":"Which of the following is ​an associated finding tha‍t can be seen in patients​ with pheochromocytoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an associated finding that can be seen in patients with pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythema ​multiforme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Café au l​ait spots","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Viti​ligo","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ash leaf​ macules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Café au lait spots","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Café au lait spots</strong></p><p><strong>Café au lait </strong>spots are seen in <strong>neurofibromatosis 1,</strong> which is a <strong>pheochromocytoma-</strong>associated syndrome.</p>\n<p>Pheochromocytoma-associated syndromes include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis type 1</li>\n<li>Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2</li>\n<li>Von Hippel–Lindau syndrome</li>\n<li>Paraganglioma syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3172","text":"Which of the following par​ameters can help you diag‍nose a patient with a mal​ignant pheochromocytoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters can help you diagnose a patient with a malignant pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capsular​ invasion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of mitotic spindles​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymph node ​involvement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cellula​r atypia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymph node involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymph node involvement</strong></p><p><strong>Malignant</strong> pheochromocytoma refers to tumors that have undergone <strong>metastasis</strong> to<strong> lymph nodes</strong> or distant sites<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>They are <strong>histologically</strong> and biochemically the <strong>same</strong> as <strong>benign</strong> ones. Hence, typical histological criteria like cellular atypia, mitotic spindles, and capsular invasion are insufficient to confirm<strong> </strong>malignancy in pheochromocytomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3169","text":"Which of the following cat​echolamines would be elev‍ated in a patient with ex​tra-adrenal pheochromocyt​oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following catecholamines would be elevated in a patient with extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepin​ephrine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metane​phrines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vanillylma​ndelic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Norepinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Norepinephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Norepinephrine</strong> is exclusively secreted in patients with extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>The <strong>PNMT</strong> (phenylethanolamine-<em>N</em>-methyl transferase) enzyme <strong>converts</strong> <strong>norepinephrine </strong>to<strong> epinephrine</strong>. This enzyme is <strong>absent</strong> in <strong>extra-adrenal</strong> sites. Hence, extra-adrenal pheochromocytomas secrete only norepinephrine and cannot secrete epinephrine. </p>\n<p>On the other hand, adrenal pheochromocytomas can secrete norepinephrine, epinephrine, and dopamine in varying amounts depending on the PNMT levels in the tumor.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra-adrenal</strong> pheochromocytomas or catecholamine-secreting paragangliomas are most commonly found in the abdomen. They commonly arise from the <strong>organ of Zuckerkandl.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3170","text":"A 31-year-old woman is rus​hed to the ED with a seve‍re headache and vomiting.​ Past history includes a ​total thyroidectomy for t​hyroid cancer 3 months ag‍o. Her BP is 200/100 mm H‌g and pulse is 110/min. O​n examination, a marfanoi‍d habitus is noted along ‌with the findings shown. ‍Which of the following is‍ likely to be predominant‍ly elevated?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old woman is rushed to the ED with a severe headache and vomiting. Past history includes a total thyroidectomy for thyroid cancer 3 months ago. Her BP is 200/100 mm Hg and pulse is 110/min. On examination, a marfanoid habitus is noted along with the findings shown. Which of the following is likely to be predominantly elevated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepin​ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vanillylma​ndelic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epinephrine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b07eb89d38e5471685152c4fa967d31c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epinephrine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with a history of thyroid cancer (likely medullary carcinoma) and the image showing <strong>mucosal neuromas</strong> points towards <strong>MEN2B</strong> syndrome. Her current presentation is suggestive of a <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. She is likely to have predominantly elevated levels of<strong> epinephrine </strong>and its metabolite,<strong> metanephrine.</strong> </p>\n<p>This is due to the <strong>higher expression</strong> of the <strong>PNMT</strong> enzyme associated with the mutations in MEN2 syndromes (both 2A and 2B). The higher levels of epinephrine result in a <strong>more symptomatic</strong> presentation.</p>\n<p>In contrast, <strong>low PNMT expression</strong> is seen in von-Hippel-Lindau (<strong>VHL</strong>) syndrome. This leads to a predominant elevation of norepinephrine and normetanephrine levels. It is less symptomatic compared to MEN2 syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3176","text":"In a suspected case of phe​ochromocytoma, which of t‍he following would you av​oid while making a diagno​sis?","raw_text":"In a suspected case of pheochromocytoma, which of the following would you avoid while making a diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI ​scan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MIBG​ scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Image-gui​ded biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Genetic​ testing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Image-guided biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Image-guided biopsy</strong></p><p><strong>Biopsy </strong>and<strong> FNAC </strong>should be<strong> avoided</strong> in patients with pheochromocytoma, as it is a highly<strong> vascular </strong>tumor. </p>\n<p>CT and MRI have similar sensitivity but <strong>T2-weighted MRI</strong> with gadolinium contrast is the optimal test for diagnosis. MIBG (metaiodobenzylguanidine) scintigraphy can be used to localize the tumor if both CT and MRI are negative.</p>\n<p>Genetic testing may be performed as nearly 40% of the tumors are associated with familial syndromes like VHL, neurofibromatosis, and MEN2.</p>\n<p>Note: In cases of <strong>malignant</strong> pheochromocytoma, <strong>biopsy</strong> may be indicated to confirm adrenal gland <strong>metastasis</strong> in other sites.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3177","text":"A middle-aged male patient​ is being evaluated for r‍ecurrent episodes of palp​itations, headaches, and ​profuse sweating. He was ​found to have elevated bl‍ood pressure readings on ‌multiple occasions over t​he past 2 months. Which o‍f the following tests has‌ the highest sensitivity ‍and specificity for the d‍iagnosis of this conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A middle-aged male patient is being evaluated for recurrent episodes of palpitations, headaches, and profuse sweating. He was found to have elevated blood pressure readings on multiple occasions over the past 2 months. Which of the following tests has the highest sensitivity and specificity for the diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24-hour urinary fractionat​ed metanephrines","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"MIBG sci​ntigraphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fractionated plasma metane​phrines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pheochromocytoma.</strong> The measurement of<strong> 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines</strong> has both the <strong>highest specificity</strong> and <strong>sensitivity</strong> in the diagnosis of this condition.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 264px; width: 448px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 66.3333px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.7222px; height: 66.3333px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.594px; text-align: center; height: 66.3333px;\"><strong>24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.399px; text-align: center; height: 66.3333px;\"><strong>Plasma fractionated metanephrines</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.7222px; height: 17px;\">Sensitivity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.594px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\">98%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.399px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\">96-100%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.7222px; height: 17px;\">Specificity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.594px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\">98%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.399px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\">85-89%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.7222px; height: 53px;\">False positives</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.594px; text-align: center; height: 53px;\">Very rare</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.399px; height: 53px;\">Can occur due to drugs like levodopa, TCAs, α-blockers, β-blockers, diuretics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.7222px; height: 71px;\">Procedure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.594px; text-align: center; height: 71px;\">Collection of samples over 24 hours may be cumbersome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.399px; text-align: center; height: 71px;\">Requires only a single blood sample. May be preferred in children.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 166px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.7222px; height: 166px;\">Uses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.594px; text-align: left; height: 166px;\">Preferred first-line test as it is highly specific</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.399px; text-align: left; height: 166px;\">\n<p>Reserved for patients with a high index of suspicion like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Genetic syndromes like MEN2</li>\n<li>Prior history of pheochromocytoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Imaging modalities like CT and MRI have a sensitivity of around 98-100% and specificity of 70%.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3179","text":"Which of the following is ​the best method for detec‍ting and localization of ​tumors associated with ph​eochromocytomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best method for detecting and localization of tumors associated with pheochromocytomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdomi​nal CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluorod​opa-PET","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"MIBG sci​ntigraphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomi​nal MRI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fluorodopa-PET","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fluorodopa-PET</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>fluorodopa-PET </strong>scan is the best method for detecting and localization of tumors associated with <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>If biochemical testing for pheochromocytoma is positive, an<strong> </strong>abdominal<strong> MRI </strong>must be performed first. If MRI is negative or if multiple tumors are suspected, the tumor can be localized by performing one of the following investigations: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>68 Ga-DOTATATE PET scan - most sensitive, <strong>gold standard </strong></li>\n<li>Fluorodopa and 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) PET scans - 97% sensitive</li>\n<li>MIBG scintigraphy - <strong>least sensitive </strong>(94%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3180","text":"A 45-year-old man presente​d with recurrent episodes‍ of headache, palpitation​s, and sweating. His bloo​d pressure at presentatio​n was 180/100 mm Hg. Furt‍her testing revealed elev‌ated urinary metanephrine​s. CT abdomen showed a ri‍ght adrenal mass. What is‌ the drug of choice for t‍he management of this pat‍ient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presented with recurrent episodes of headache, palpitations, and sweating. His blood pressure at presentation was 180/100 mm Hg. Further testing revealed elevated urinary metanephrines. CT abdomen showed a right adrenal mass. What is the drug of choice for the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labe​talol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrop​russide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenoxyb​enzamine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phento​lamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenoxybenzamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenoxybenzamine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pheochromocytoma.</strong> The drug of choice to control blood pressure prior to surgery is oral <strong>phenoxybenzamine </strong>at 0.5–4 mg/kg of body weight. It is a non-specific<strong> α-blocker</strong> and is preferred due to its<strong> long action</strong> (3-4 days).</p>\n<p>Other α-blockers like oral <strong>prazosin</strong> and intravenous <strong>phentolamine</strong> have a shorter duration of action and can be used to manage<strong> paroxysms. </strong>IV phentolamine is also used for <strong>intra-operative</strong> management of BP.</p>\n<p>α-blockers are usually given for 7 days. Following this, <strong>β-blockers</strong> are started, usually 2-3 days preoperatively. β-blockers should <strong>never</strong> be started <strong>first</strong> because blockade of vasodilatory peripheral β-receptors with unopposed α-receptor stimulation can lead to a further elevation in blood pressure. (Mnemonic: A before B)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3182","text":"Which of the following sho​uld not be used for the i‍nitial management, in a p​atient with pheochromocyt​oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following should not be used for the initial management, in a patient with pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Praz​osin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aten​olol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrop​russide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metyr​osine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atenolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atenolol</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> like atenolol should never be used as the initial drug or alone in the management of pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>They must be given only <strong>after</strong> adequate <strong>alpha blockade</strong> has been established. Blocking beta-receptors may cause unopposed stimulation of alpha-receptors, precipitating a hypertensive crisis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: Prazosin is an alpha-blocker.</p>\n<p>Option C: Nitroprusside can be used in case of hypertensive emergencies.</p>\n<p>Option D: Metyrosine inhibits catecholamine synthesis. It can be used when other agents have been ineffective. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3184","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of Averbuch’s ‍chemotherapy protocol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of Averbuch’s chemotherapy protocol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dacar​bazine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vinbl​astine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho​sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vincr​istine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vinblastine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vinblastine</strong></p><p>Vinblastine is not included in Averbuch’s chemotherapy protocol for the treatment of malignant pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3187","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit​h malignant pheochromocyt‍oma. Which of the followi​ng is used to treat this ​condition?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with malignant pheochromocytoma. Which of the following is used to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stro​ntium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coba​lt-60","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosp​horus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MI​BG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MIBG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MIBG</strong></p><p><strong><sup>131</sup>I-MIBG </strong>(metaiodobenzylguanidine)<strong> </strong>is used for the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>malignant pheochromocytoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Malignant pheochromocytomas occur in 10% of cases and are histologically and biochemically the same as benign ones. The diagnosis of malignant pheochromocytoma is based on documentation of metastatic disease. The treatment of choice is <strong>nuclear medicine</strong> therapy with any of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><sup>1</sup><sup>31</sup>I-MIBG at 200-mCi doses at monthly intervals (3-6 cycles)</li>\n<li><strong>DOTATOC</strong> (a somatostatin receptor analog) labeled with radioactive lutetium or yttrium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chemotherapy can also be given according to Averbuch's protocol.</p>\n<p>Note: <sup>123</sup>I-MIBG and Gallium-DOTATATE are used for imaging.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8eiQxnOi","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"11_other_mycobacteria","title":"11.Other Mycobacteria","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2042","text":"Which of the following is ‌a pigment-producing speci‌es of mycobacteria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a pigment-producing species of mycobacteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. ul‌cerans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. scro‌fulaceum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M. avium intracellulare co‌mplex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M. x‌enopi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. M. scrofulaceum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. M. scrofulaceum</strong></p><p><em><strong>Mycobacterium scrofulaceum</strong></em> is a pigment-producing species of mycobacteria.</p>\n<p><em>M. scrofulaceum</em> is a scotochromogen which produces <strong>yellow</strong> or <strong>orange</strong> colored pigments, even in the dark. It belongs to group II of Runyon's classification. It can cause scrofula or cervical adenitis in children. </p>\n<p><em>M.ulcerans, M.avium intracellulare</em> complex (MAC), and <em>M. xenopi</em> belong to group III-non photochromogens as they do not form pigment even on exposure to light.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2052","text":"A microbiologist working w‌ith mycobacteria notes mo‌ist, smooth, flat, white ‍colonies growing on Lowen​stein-Jensen media. Which​ species of mycobacteria ‍is she likely studying?","raw_text":"A microbiologist working with mycobacteria notes moist, smooth, flat, white colonies growing on Lowenstein-Jensen media. Which species of mycobacteria is she likely studying?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycobacte‌rium bovis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacteri‌um kansasii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycobacterium avium-intrac‌ellulare","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mycobacterium bovis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de6e862f980d4846afbe5c8b113ff746x1280x853.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f53c9f6ad8a4b90b6dca2373db7e5fcx600x408.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mycobacterium bovis</strong></p><p>The species of mycobacteria being studied here is <strong><em>Mycobacterium bovis.</em></strong> It<strong> </strong>forms<strong> moist</strong>, <strong>smooth</strong>, <strong>flat</strong>, <strong>white colonies</strong> on Lowenstein-Jensen (LJ) media that break-up easily when touched. </p>\n<p><em>Mycobacterium bovis</em> belongs to the <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> complex. It causes tuberculosis-like disease in humans. </p>\n<p><strong>Lowenstein–J</strong><strong>ensen medium </strong>is a commonly used, egg-based, <strong>selective media</strong> for mycobacteria. It contains:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Egg-</strong> Provides fatty acids and protein required for metabolism</li>\n<li><strong>Malachite green-</strong> Prevents the growth of contaminants. <strong>Makes</strong> it a <strong>selective</strong> medium.</li>\n<li>Mineral salt solution- Buffer</li>\n<li>Glycerol- Source of carbon, favors growth of <em>M. tuberculosis</em>, but not <em>M. bovis. </em>LJ medium without glycerol, which contains pyruvate, is used for culture of <em>M. bovis.</em></li>\n<li>L-asparagine- Nitrogen source</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows <em>M.bovis</em> colonies on LJ media.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><strong>Mycobacterium</strong></em> <em><strong>tuberculosis</strong></em> colonies on LJ medium are dry, <strong>rough, </strong>raised and irregular with a wrinkled surface. They are <strong>creamy white</strong> or buff-colored, <strong>tenacious</strong> and difficult to emulsify(rough, tough, buff). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1305","text":"The following findings are‌ obtained during biochemi‌cal testing of a mycobact‍erial species. Identify t​he organism. Catalase - S​trongly positive Peroxida‍se - Negative Niacin - Po‌sitive Arylsulfatase - Po‌sitive","raw_text":"<p>The following findings are obtained during biochemical testing of a mycobacterial species. Identify the organism.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Catalase - Strongly positive</li>\n<li>Peroxidase - Negative</li>\n<li>Niacin - Positive</li>\n<li>Arylsulfatase - Positive</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. b‌ovis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. tube‌rculosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M. s‌imiae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M. ka‌nsasii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. M. simiae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. M. simiae</strong></p><p>The given biochemical findings are characteristic of <strong><em>Mycobacterium simiae</em></strong>. Eventhough <strong>atypical mycobacteria</strong> or non-tuberculous mycobacteria(NTM) are usually <strong>niacin test negative</strong>, some NTM like <em><strong>M. simiae</strong></em> and<em><strong> M. chelonae</strong></em> give a positive <strong>niacin test positive</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mycobacteria can be identified based on their biochemical reactions as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 212px; width: 238.708px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Biochemical test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\"><em>M. tb</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\"><em>M. bovis</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">NTM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Catalase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">Weak <br/>+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">Weak +</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Peroxidase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">_</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Arylsulfatse</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Niacin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Nitrate reduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">N/A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">Pyrazinamidase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">N/A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 51px;\">TCH sensitivity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">Resistant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51px; text-align: center;\">Sensitive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.7083px; text-align: center;\">N/A</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The first four tests are used to distinguish between M. tb complex and atypical mycobacteria while the last four tests are used to distinguish between <em>M. tb</em> and <em>M. bovis</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2035","text":"A 54-year-old male present‌s with an ulcer on his ha‌nd. On swab culture, grow‍th of M. ulcerans is seen​. What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old male presents with an ulcer on his hand. On swab culture, growth of M. ulcerans is seen. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hunner’‌s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meleney‌’s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buruli‌ ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Marjolin‌'s ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Buruli ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/199b65c31d7a48f6b1e2284f0f8fea1ex600x450.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/997040cadff5400cb58a049ff32eb013x405x421.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/827b6428509c49c9a62e0ac5b03f9ddbx800x594.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Buruli ulcer</strong></p><p><strong><em>Mycobacterium ulcerans</em> </strong>causes<strong> Buruli ulcer</strong>. </p>\n<p><em>M. ulcerans</em> produces a toxin called <strong>mycolactone</strong> which leads to formation of<strong> </strong>Buruli ulcer. This is a <strong>chronic</strong>, debilitating, <strong>necrotizing</strong> disease of the skin and soft tissue which is characterized by the formation of ulcers that <strong>progressively enlarge</strong> over time if left untreated. The ulcers heal with disfiguring scars. </p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">The image below shows a Buruli ulcer. </span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Hunner's ulcers </strong>are seen in a subset of patients with <strong>interstitial cystitis</strong>. These are areas of inflammation on the bladder wall. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a cystoscopic image of a Hunner's ulcer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Meleney's ulcer</strong> or post-operative synergistic bacterial gangrene occurs in <strong>immuno-compromised</strong> individuals following abdominal surgery. It is caused by a synergistic interaction between microaerophilic nonhemolytic <em>Streptococcus</em> and hemolytic aerobic <em>Staphylococcus aureus.</em></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Marjolin ulcer</strong> is a rare and aggressive form of <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> that grows from burns, scars, or poorly healing wounds. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a Marjolin's ulcer in a burn scar. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2063","text":"A 42-year-old man who work‌s at an aquarium and hand‌les the fish without prot‍ective equipment presents​ to the OPD with the foll​owing finding. Infection ‍with which of the followi‌ng bacteria led to his co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man who works at an aquarium and handles the fish without protective equipment presents to the OPD with the following finding. Infection with which of the following bacteria led to his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. ka‌nsasi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. ma‌rinum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M. paratu‌berculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M. go‌rdonae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. M. marinum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dbc3963bd5be493f988100a152708fd1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. M. marinum</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>fish tank granuloma</strong> that is caused by <strong><em>M. marinum</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>The typical clinical presentation is as a single, small violet papule or nodule confined to one extremity, developing 2-3 weeks after trauma. The nodule may break down to form an indolent ulcer. <em>M. marinarum</em> is a natural pathogen of fish and amphibians, which spreads to humans who come in contact with <strong>contaminated swimming pools</strong> or <strong>fish tanks</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other non-tuberculous mycobacteria causing disease in humans include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>M. kansasii: </em>Disease resembles pulmonary TB</li>\n<li><em>M. scrofulaceum: </em>Cervical lymphadenitis</li>\n<li><em>M. avium complex: </em>Pulmonary, <strong>disseminated TB,</strong> especially <strong>in</strong> <strong>AIDS</strong> patients</li>\n<li><em>M. fortuitum: </em>Nail salon furunculosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>M. paratuberculosis </em>(aka<em> </em>Johne’s bacillus) causes Johne’s disease in cattle.<strong> </strong>It is associated with Crohn’s disease in humans. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1277","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the‌ microbiological diagnosi‍s of the disease afflicti​ng the patient shown belo​w?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the microbiological diagnosis of the disease afflicting the patient shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acid fast staining is done‌ by Ziehl-Neelsen techniq‌ue, using 25% sulphuric a‍cid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sample should be collected‌ from the centre of the l‌esion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The number of samples to b‌e collected is 3-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is cultivated in nine b‌anded armadillos and foot‌ pads of mice","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is cultivated in nine banded armadillos and foot pads of mice","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d390193450ad469fb47a0232232d970a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is cultivated in nine banded armadillos and foot pads of mice</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>leonine</strong> <strong>facies</strong> in a patient suffering from <strong>leprosy</strong> or Hansen's disease caused by <strong><em>Mycobacterium leprae</em></strong>. It<strong><em> </em></strong>is <strong>cultivated</strong> in <strong>nine-banded armadillos</strong> and <strong>footpads</strong> of <strong>mice.</strong></p>\n<p><em>M. leprae</em> is a Gram-positive, weakly acid fast bacillus. Thus, <strong>5% sulphuric acid</strong> (option A) is used for decolourisation to demonstrate M.leprae's acid-fastness. On microscopy, the bacilli appear bound together in masses called <strong>globi</strong>, within which they are arranged in a '<strong>cigar bundle</strong>' appearance. </p>\n<p>For leprosy diagnosis, specimens are collected from the nasal mucosa, skin lesions and earlobes. <strong>Nasal smears</strong> are collected from the internal septum of the nose. <strong>Skin smears</strong> are collected from the <strong>edges </strong>of the<strong> lesions</strong> (option B) rather than from the centre. <strong>Slit skin smears </strong>are taken as well. The skin is pinched up tight to minimise bleeding and a cut is made with a scalpel, deep enough to get into the infiltrated layers. This is done over the buttocks, forehead, chin, cheek, and ears (at least<strong> 5-6 different areas</strong>) (option C). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2064","text":"What is the generation tim‌e of lepra bacilli?","raw_text":"What is the generation time of lepra bacilli?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-13‌ days","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"12-13‌ hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15-20‌ hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 m‌onth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 12-13 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 12-13 days</strong></p><p>The <strong>generation time</strong> of <strong>lepra</strong> bacilli is <strong>12-13 days.</strong></p>\n<p>Generation time, also known as <strong>doubling time,</strong> is the time it takes for the bacterial population to double. It can also be measured as the time between two cell divisions under optimum conditions. The generation time of different bacilli are given below: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lepra bacilli- </strong>12-13 days average (may vary from 8-42 days)</li>\n<li><strong>Tubercle bacilli- </strong>15-20 hours</li>\n<li><strong>Coliform bacilli- </strong>20 minutes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2066","text":"What are the bacteria pres‌ent in Virchow's cells?","raw_text":"What are the bacteria present in Virchow's cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. avium in‌tercellulare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. tube‌rculosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M. b‌ovis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M. l‌eprae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. M. leprae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d256f398d37443fb8de6203c141e061ex259x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. M. leprae</strong></p><p>The bacteria present in <strong>Virchow’s cells</strong> is <em><strong>M. leprae</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Lepromatous leprosy lesions contain large aggregates of<strong> lipid laden macrophages</strong> (lepra cells/Virchow cells) which are often<strong> </strong>filled with<strong> globi</strong> (masses) of acid fast bacilli.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a high-power <strong>Fite-Faraco stain</strong> of a skin lesion showing globi of <em>M.leprae.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2071","text":"You counsel a patient with‌ leprosy about lepra reac‌tions before starting her‍ on multidrug therapy. Wh​ich among these is correc​t regarding these reactio‍ns? Type 1 reactions are ‌unrelated to drug therapy‌, whereas as type 2 may o‍ccur within a year of sta​rting treatment. Type 1 r‍eaction is seen in the bo‍rderline spectrum and typ​e 2 is seen in lepromatou‌s leprosy. New lesions ap‌pear in type 1 and they a‌re tender and erythematou‌s Fever, keratitis, iriti​s, and nephritis can occu‌r with a type 2 reaction.​ Steroids are the drug of‌ choice for severe type 1‍ reactions.","raw_text":"<p>You counsel a patient with leprosy about lepra reactions before starting her on multidrug therapy. Which among these is correct regarding these reactions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Type 1 reactions are unrelated to drug therapy, whereas as type 2 may occur within a year of starting treatment.</li>\n<li>Type 1 reaction is seen in the borderline spectrum and type 2 is seen in lepromatous leprosy.</li>\n<li>New lesions appear in type 1 and they are tender and erythematous </li>\n<li>Fever, keratitis, iritis, and nephritis can occur with a type 2 reaction.</li>\n<li>Steroids are the drug of choice for severe type 1 reactions. </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 and ‌2 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>Statements <strong>2, 4, </strong>and <strong>5</strong> regarding lepra reactions are correct. </p>\n<p><strong>Type 2 </strong>lepra reactions are <strong>unrelated</strong> to <strong>drug</strong> therapy, whereas type 1 reactions may occur within a year of starting treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>New lesions</strong> typically appear in <strong>type 2</strong> lepra reactions. They are tender and erythematous subcutaneous and dermal nodules. Existing lesions show no changes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2070","text":"A patient diagnosed with l‌eprosy was undergoing fur‌ther evaluation for class‍ification of type and imm​une response. Which of th​e following statements is‍ false regarding the intr‌adermal test used in such‌ evaluation?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with leprosy was undergoing further evaluation for classification of type and immune response. Which of the following statements is false regarding the intradermal test used in such evaluation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Negative in children &amp;lt;6‌ month age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has no value in prognos‌tication of leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Used to clas‌sify leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BCG vaccine may convert a ‌negative test to positive‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It has no value in prognostication of leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It has no value in prognostication of leprosy</strong></p><p>The<strong> lepromin</strong> test has value as a <strong>prognostic test</strong> for leprosy<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The lepromin, or Mitsuda test, is a method to measure<strong> cellular immunity</strong> in leprosy. It is a skin test for delayed hypersensitivity to lepra antigens. A bacillary suspension of inactivated M. leprae is injected just under the skin and the induration reaction is measured 3–4 weeks after. </p>\n<p>It is used to <strong>classify leprosy</strong>. A negative lepromin test is seen in lepromatous and borderline leprosy. It is <strong>negative </strong>in children<strong> &lt;6 months</strong> of age, it may be positive at the end of 1st year. <strong>BCG vaccine</strong> is capable of converting a negative lepromin test to a <strong>positive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4697","text":"You are performing the tes‌t shown in the image on a‌ patient. How many days l‍ater do you ask the patie​nt to return so that you ​get an accurate read on t‍he Mitsuda reaction?","raw_text":"You are performing the test shown in the image on a patient. How many days later do you ask the patient to return so that you get an accurate read on the Mitsuda reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"30 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20 days","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6171fd900d1b4ad18022772cfd184347.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20 days</strong></p><p><strong>Mitsuda's reaction</strong> is read in the <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> week,</strong> i.e., on the<strong> 21<sup>st</sup> day</strong> or <strong>20 days after</strong> the day of injection.</p>\n<p><strong>Lepromin test</strong> is a skin test for <strong>delayed hypersensitivity</strong> to lepra antigens. It is the only method for studying immunity in leprosy. A bacillary suspension of inactivated M. leprae is injected just under the skin.</p>\n<p>The first reaction to occur is the <strong>early reaction </strong>of<strong> Fernandez</strong>, which consists of <strong>erythema</strong> and <strong>induration</strong> developing in <strong>24-48 hours.</strong> It usually remains for 3-5 days; it is usually poorly defined and carries less significance. The second and more meaningful result is the <strong>late reaction</strong> of <strong>Mitsuda</strong>, starting at 1-2 weeks (7-10 days), reaching a peak in three or four weeks, and gradually subsiding over the next few weeks. On the 21st day, if there is a <strong>nodule more than 5 mm</strong> in diameter, the reaction is said to be <strong>positive. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The 21st day is 20 days after the day of injection which is counted as the 1st day.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1308","text":"A leprosy vaccine is being‌ introduced on a pilot ba‌sis in India. Which of th‍e following mycobacterial​ species is the basis for​ its development?","raw_text":"A  leprosy vaccine is being introduced on a pilot basis in India. Which of the following mycobacterial species is the basis for its development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. v‌accae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. bovis Danish 1331 strai‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M.lepra‌e murium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M. indic‌us pranii","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. M. indicus pranii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. M. indicus pranii</strong></p><p><strong><em>Mycobacterium indicus pranii</em></strong> is the basis for the development of a leprosy vaccine.</p>\n<p>The leprosy vaccine species was originally called<strong> <em>Mycobacterium W</em></strong> (M.w) but was later renamed <em>Mycobacterium indicus pranii</em>. The vaccine is administered via <strong>intradermal</strong> route. The dose is <strong>0.1 ml.</strong> It is divided into two equal doses and<strong> </strong>given on<strong> both arms</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Booster doses</strong> will be given to maintain the immunity level. These are given only in <strong>one arm</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"M2Ma5Khz","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"11_abnormal_labour","title":"11.Abnormal Labour","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB7117","text":"A young woman delivered he‌r baby on the way to the ‌hospital. While examining‌ her, you notice a perine‍al tear. This injury coul​d have been prevented by ‌all except:","raw_text":"A young woman delivered her baby on the way to the hospital. While examining her, you notice a perineal tear. This injury could have been prevented by all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintaining flexion of hea‌d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Timely episiotomy as a rou‌tine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Slow delivery of the head ‌in between contractions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Effective pe‌rineal guard","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Timely episiotomy as a routine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Timely episiotomy as a routine</strong></p><p>Perineal injury cannot be prevented in normal labor by routine timely episiotomy. Routine episiotomy is associated with an increased incidence of disruption of anal sphincters and rectal tears.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications of episiotomy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shoulder dystocia</li>\n<li>Breech delivery</li>\n<li>Fetal macrosomia</li>\n<li>Operative vaginal deliveries</li>\n<li>Persistent occiput posterior positions</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Methods of prevention of perineal laceration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoid delivery by early extension</li>\n<li>Deliver head in between contractions</li>\n<li>Perform timely episiotomy when indicated (not routine).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9932","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of true cephalopelvic‌ disproportion in India?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of true cephalopelvic disproportion in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malprese‌ntations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‌kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipito posterior positio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>true cephalopelvic disproportion </strong>(CPD) in India is <strong>rickets</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of true CPD in <strong>developing</strong> countries is <strong>vitamin D deficiency and rickets</strong>. In <strong>developed</strong> countries, it is <strong>trauma</strong> to the pelvis. It occurs due to pelvic dystocia (larger fetus and smaller pelvis).</p>\n<p><strong>Relative CPD </strong>is the most common type of CPD. It is caused due to fetal malposition (occipitoposterior position/brow presentation).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9933","text":"You are monitoring uterine‌ contractions in a term p‌rimigravida in labor. You‌ find that the contractio‍ns are uncoordinated and ​the frequency is about 8 ‌contractions every 10 min‌utes. Which of the follow​ing is incorrect regardin​g your finding?","raw_text":"You are monitoring uterine contractions in a term primigravida in labor. You find that the contractions are uncoordinated and the frequency is about 8 contractions every 10 minutes. Which of the following is incorrect regarding your finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Commonly associated with o‌ccipito posterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is common in nulliparou‌s labour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxytocin administration is‌ beneficial","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Management includes wait a‌nd watch policy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oxytocin administration is beneficial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oxytocin administration is beneficial</strong></p><p>The given clinical finding is suggestive of <strong>uterine tachysystole.</strong> Oxytocin administration is not beneficial here as it leads to hyperstimulation of the uterus.</p>\n<p><strong>Tachysystole (hypertonic uterine dysfunction):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The uterine contractions are uncoordinated and increased in frequency (&gt;5 contractions in 10 minutes)</li>\n<li>Contractions occur with an increase in the baseline tone without rest in between them</li>\n<li>It is associated with late active phase dysfunction i.e., when the cervix is almost 7 cm dilated</li>\n<li>The most common cause is occipito-posterior position. Hence, a wait and watch policy is adopted</li>\n<li>It is more common in nulliparous females</li>\n<li>Oxytocin administration leads to hyperstimulation of the uterus and is not recommended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hypotonic </strong>uterine dysfunction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uterine contractions are inadequate</li>\n<li>It is associated with early active phase</li>\n<li>It is more common in nulliparous females.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0029","text":"A 37-week primigravida has‌ mild labor pains for 20 ‌hours and the cervix is p‌ersistently 1 cm dilated ‍but non-effaced. What wou​ld be the appropriate man‌agement?","raw_text":"A 37-week primigravida has mild labor pains for 20 hours and the cervix is persistently 1 cm dilated but non-effaced. What would be the appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sedation‌ and wait","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Augmentation with oxytocin‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cesarean‌ section","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amni‌otomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sedation and wait","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sedation and wait</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of either <strong>false labor pain or prolonged latent phase</strong>. The appropriate management is to <strong>sedate</strong> the patient<strong> and wait</strong>.</p>\n<p>False labor can be differentiated from the latent phase of labor by therapeutic rest i.e., the patient is sedated with morphine.</p>\n<p><strong>Augmentation with</strong> <strong>oxytocin</strong> is done only in those patients who even after therapeutic rest <strong>continue</strong> to be in <strong>prolonged latent phase</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is no indication of cesarean section unless cephalopelvic disproportion is present. Amniotomy is not useful and should be avoided in patients with prolonged latent phase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0038","text":"A 34-year-old G3P2L2 woman‌ presents to the ER with ‌hypotension and severe ab‌dominal pain. On examinat‍ion, fetal parts are palp​able over the abdomen. US‌G abdomen revealed a larg‌e uterine wall defect. Wh​ich of the following is n​ot a feature of this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old G3P2L2 woman presents to the ER with hypotension and severe abdominal pain. On examination, fetal parts are palpable over the abdomen. USG abdomen revealed a large uterine wall defect. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal b‌radycardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal br‌adycardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal ‌bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hema‌turia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maternal bradycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maternal bradycardia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>uterine rupture</strong>. <strong>Maternal tachycardia</strong> is seen in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features of uterine rupture:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal tachycardia</li>\n<li>Fetal bradycardia/absent fetal heart sounds</li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Abdominal tenderness</li>\n<li>Palpable fetal parts on per abdomen examination</li>\n<li>Bandl’s ring</li>\n<li>Bleeding per vagina</li>\n<li>Hematuria </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common causes</strong> of <strong>uterine rupture</strong> are previous caesarean delivery, myomectomy, silent rupture (in previous pregnancy), excess use of oxytocin, grand multipara, uterine overdistension, obstructed labour, difficult forceps delivery, placenta percreta</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0039","text":"A young primigravida at 36‌ weeks of gestation came ‌with complaints of decrea‌sed perception of fetal m‍ovements for the past 2 d​ays. NST is non-reactive ‌and on ultrasound fetal c‌ardiac activity is absent​. What will be the best m​anagement in this patient‌?","raw_text":"A young primigravida at 36 weeks of gestation came with complaints of decreased perception of fetal movements for the past 2 days. NST is non-reactive and on ultrasound fetal cardiac activity is absent. What will be the best management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forceps e‌xtraction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wait for spontaneous expul‌sion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cesarean‌ section","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artificial rupture of memb‌ranes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wait for spontaneous expulsion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wait for spontaneous expulsion</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>intrauterine fetal death (IUFD)</strong>. The best management for such cases at 36 weeks is <strong>waiting for spontaneous expulsion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Forceps extraction is only necessary if spontaneous expulsion doesn't take place. </p>\n<p>Artificial rupture of membranes (<strong>ARM</strong>) is not done due to the <strong>risk of infections. </strong><strong>Cesarean</strong> section is <strong>avoided</strong> as far as possible in case of fetal death.</p>\n<p>Waiting till term is not recommended in cases of uterine infections, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and psychological upset.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0040","text":"A G2P1L0 woman presents to‌ OPD at 36 weeks of gesta‌tion for a routine visit.‌ She mentions that she ha‍s a history of prior stil​lbirth at 37 weeks. What ‌is the best time for deli‌very in this patient?","raw_text":"A G2P1L0 woman presents to OPD at 36 weeks of gestation for a routine visit. She mentions that she has a history of prior stillbirth at 37 weeks. What is the best time for delivery in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immed‌iately","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"37 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"38 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"39 w‌eeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 39 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 39 weeks</strong></p><p>The best time for <strong>elective delivery</strong> in patients with a history of previous stillbirth is<strong> 39 weeks,</strong> either by induction or cesarean section.</p>\n<p>The recommendations for the management of subsequent pregnancy after stillbirth is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ultrasonographic screening for fetal growth restriction after 28 weeks</li>\n<li>Kick counts starting from 28 weeks of gestation</li>\n<li>Antepartum fetal surveillance starting at 32 weeks or 1-2 weeks earlier than prior stillbirth</li>\n<li>Support and reassurance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Labor induction is suitable in such cases. Cesarean delivery is done for those with a contraindication to induction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0041","text":"A pregnant woman presents ‌with obstructed labor and‌ is grossly dehydrated. I‌nvestigations reveal feta‍l demise. What will be th​e management?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman presents with obstructed labor and is grossly dehydrated. Investigations reveal fetal demise. What will be the management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crani‌otomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decapi‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cesarean‌ section","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Forceps e‌xtraction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cesarean section","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cesarean section</strong></p><p>The best management in the given clinical scenario<strong> </strong>is <strong>cesarean section</strong>.</p>\n<p>Obstructive labor management involves administering <strong>intravenous fluids</strong> to correct dehydration, providing <strong>antibiotics</strong> to prevent infection, and promptly performing a <strong>cesarean</strong> section to <strong>relieve</strong> the <strong>obstruction</strong>. If the <strong>fetus</strong> is <strong>alive,</strong> an <strong>immediate</strong> cesarean section is typically performed. Management of obstructed labor <strong>never</strong> involves a wait-and-watch approach or augmenting with oxytocin.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of obstructed labor include maternal exhaustion, <strong>dehydration,</strong> tachycardia, tachypnea, <strong>genital sepsis</strong>, postpartum hemorrhage, and <strong>shock</strong>.<br/>On <strong>P/A examination</strong> the <strong>upper </strong>uterine segment is <strong>tender</strong> and tonically <strong>contracted</strong>. The <strong>lower</strong> uterine segment is <strong>thin</strong> and<strong> stretched</strong>. A <strong>groove</strong>/depression is felt <strong>between </strong>the <strong>upper</strong> uterine segment and the <strong>lower</strong> uterine segment. This is called a <strong>Bandls ring.</strong></p>\n<p>Complications of obstructed labor include <strong>rupture</strong> of the <strong>uterus</strong> and <strong>vesicovaginal fistula.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1828","text":"The partogram of a 28 year‌ old G2P1 is shown below.‌ What is the next step in‌ management?","raw_text":"The partogram of a 28 year old G2P1 is shown below. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wait an‌d watch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxytocin‌ infusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Forceps ‌delivery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cesarean‌ section","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cesarean section","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d827fe6733b448c846746c32dbd9f6a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cesarean section</strong></p><p>The above partogram shows a case of <strong>obstructed labour</strong> and management is <strong>caesarean section.</strong></p>\n<p>There is a secondary arrest of dilatation for &gt;2 hours and arrest of descent for &gt;1 hour. The patient has adequate uterine contractions as shown. The amniotic fluid is stained with meconium and there is fall in fetal heart rate which indicates that the baby is in distress.</p>\n<p>There is no place for wait and watch and use of oxytocin to stimulate uterine contractions in obstructed labor.</p>\n<p>Forceps can be applied only when the cervix is fully dilated and the head is low down.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0823","text":"After successful delivery ‌of the baby, the obstetri‌cian witnesses that the e‌pisiotomy performed has e‍xtended backwards beyond ​the perineal body. Which ‌among the following struc‌tures is likely to be inj​ured in this case?","raw_text":"After successful delivery of the baby, the obstetrician witnesses that the episiotomy performed has extended backwards beyond the perineal body. Which among the following structures is likely to be injured in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External an‌al sphicter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ischioca‌vernosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bulbosp‌ongiosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urethral ‌sphincter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. External anal sphicter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. External anal sphicter</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>External anal sphincter</strong> is the structure injured in an episiotomy extended posteriorly beyond the perineal body. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Perineal tear have been divided into <strong>four degrees</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>First </strong>degree- laceration is restricted to the skin of the fourchette</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Second</strong> degree- muscles of the perineal body are torn through</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Third </strong>degree- the tear extends partially backward through the external sphincter of the anus</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Fourth </strong>degree- the external anal sphincter is torn and anal mucosa is also involved</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>central tear</strong> of the perineum is when the head penetrates first through the posterior vaginal wall, then through the perineal body and appears through the skin of the perineum. It usually occurs in patients with a <strong>contracted outlet</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6622","text":"A midwife is examining a p‌regnant woman in labour w‌hen she noticed that the ‌cord is prolapsed. What s‍hould be the next immedia​te step of management?","raw_text":"A midwife is examining a pregnant woman in labour when she noticed that the cord is prolapsed. What should be the next immediate step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gently replace the cord in‌side the vagina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inform the doctor and shif‌t to OT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Put her in Trendelenberg p‌osition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Do vaginal examination and‌ find the fetal presentat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Put her in Trendelenberg position","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0dc37cf41518466b91e256c25f641497x1280x1870.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Put her in Trendelenberg position</strong></p><p>In this scenario the midwife should put the pregnant women in a <strong>Trendelenburg position</strong> or in an exaggerated and <strong>elevated Sims’ position</strong> with a pillow or wedge under the hip or thigh.</p>\n<p><strong>Caesarean section</strong> is the recommended<strong> treatment of choice</strong> for cord prolapse. Prior to performing the procedure, several measures can be taken to alleviate cord compression. These measures involve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Manually elevating the fetal presenting part using two fingers or the entire hand through the vagina</li>\n<li>Positioning the patient in <strong>steep Trendelenburg, exaggerated Sim's position, or knee-chest position</strong></li>\n<li>Filling the bladder with 500–700 cc or more of saline (known as the <strong>Vagos method</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After the diagnosis of cord prolapse is made, it is crucial <strong>not to attempt cord replacement</strong>. Such attempts are not only ineffective but also result in the inevitable rupture of the membranes, leading to cord prolapse. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7048","text":"Abdominal examination of a‌ 28-year-old primigravida‌, who is in the second st‌age of labour, reveals re‍traction ring between the​ active upper segment and‌ the distended lower segm‌ent. Which would best des​cribe this ring?","raw_text":"Abdominal examination of a 28-year-old primigravida, who is in the second stage of labour, reveals retraction ring between the active upper segment and the distended lower segment. Which would best describe this ring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physiological retraction r‌ing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pathological retraction ri‌ng","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Physiological contraction ‌band","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pathological contraction b‌and","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pathological retraction ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3cf5440da44c4bd18a11aa26cd06af3cx1280x1399.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61b1a6c6a0eb47c399278d0d12f86402x1280x1398.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pathological retraction ring</strong></p><p><strong>Bandl's ring</strong> refers to <strong>pathological retraction ring</strong>. It is formed in cases of <strong>obstructed labour.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>upper segment</strong> of the uterus <strong>contracts</strong> and retracts vigourously in an attempt to overcome the obstruction while the <strong>lower segment dilates</strong>.</p>\n<p>With each contraction there is <strong>myometrial shortening</strong> so that the actively contracting upper segment becomes progressively thicker and shorter. The passive lower segment is progressively stretched and becomes thinner.</p>\n<p>The junction between the two segments stands out prominently as a pathological retraction ring.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Schroeder's constriction ring</strong> is formed due to <strong>incordinate uterine action</strong> when there is localized spastic contraction of a ring of circular muscle fibres of the uterus. It is formed due to injudicious use of <strong>oxytocin </strong>and premature rupture of membranes<strong> (PROM).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0031","text":"A 26-year-old term primigr‌avida presents to the cli‌nic for a regular check-u‌p. On examination, uterus‍ corresponds to term and ​head of the fetus is non-‌engaged. What is the most‌ common cause for this fi​nding?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old term primigravida presents to the clinic for a regular check-up. On examination, uterus corresponds to term and head of the fetus is non-engaged.  What is the most common cause for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalopelvic disproportio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deflex‌ed head","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transve‌rse lie","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bre‌ech","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deflexed head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deflexed head</strong></p><p>The most common cause of non-engagement at term in a primigravida is <strong>deflexed head </strong>followed by<strong> cephalopelvic disproportion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Non-engagement at the onset of active phase of labour is a <strong>risk factor for caesarean section</strong>.</p>\n<p>The problems of <strong>prolonged labour</strong> are that the woman is exposed to high risk of infection, ketosis and obstructed labour while the fetus faces the danger of asphyxia and infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7060","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cardinal movement o‌ccurring during labor?","raw_text":"Which of the following  is not a cardinal movement occurring during labor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fle‌xion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exte‌nsion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal‌ rotation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Async‌litism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Asynclitism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/872dd032b8fc4262ae0969a1b1554937x1280x2888.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Asynclitism</strong></p><p><strong>Asynclitism </strong>is not a cardinal movement during labor.</p>\n<p>Asynclitism refers to the position of a baby in the uterus such that the head of the baby is presenting first and is tilted to the shoulder. This causes the <strong>fetal head</strong> to<strong> no longer </strong>be<strong> in line with </strong>the<strong> birth canal. </strong></p>\n<p>Moderate degrees of asynclitism are the rule in normal labor. However, if severe, this condition is a common reason for <strong>cephalopelvic disproportion</strong> even with an otherwise normal sized pelvis. </p>\n<p>Asynclitism can be of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the <strong>sagittal suture </strong>lies <strong>anteriorly</strong>, the posterior parietal bone becomes the leading presenting part and is called <strong>posterior asynclitism.</strong></li>\n<li>When the <strong>sagittal suture</strong> lies <strong>posteriorly</strong>, the anterior parietal bone becomes the leading presenting part and is called <strong>anterior asynclitism.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7086","text":"A 28-year-old primigravida‌ is brought to the labour‌ room in the latent phase‌ of labor. How long will ‍you wait till you declare​ her latent phase to be p‌rolonged?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old primigravida is brought to the labour room in the latent phase of labor. How long will you wait till you declare her latent phase to be prolonged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"18 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 h‌ours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Latent phase</strong> is said to be prolonged in a primigravida if it lasts for more than <strong>20 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>Friedman defined prolonged latent phase as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>nullipara</strong> who has not entered the active phase by <strong>20 hours</strong> after the onset of the latent phase</li>\n<li>A <strong>multipara</strong> who has not entered the active phase by <strong>14 hours</strong> after the onset of the latent phase </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7378","text":"All of the following findi‌ngs are seen in the parto‌gram of a patient except:‌","raw_text":"All of the following findings are seen in the partogram of a patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arrest of ‌dilatation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arrest o‌f descent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal d‌istress","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine ta‌chysystole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Uterine tachysystole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8be2bc5d260d4d5cb1894af21f21321d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Uterine tachysystole</strong></p><p>The above partogram shows <strong>secondary arrest of dilatation</strong> for &gt;2 hour, <strong>arrest of descent</strong> for &gt;1 hour and a fall in fetal heart rate suggestive of <strong>fetal distress. </strong></p>\n<p>These are characteristic findings of <strong>obstructed labour</strong> and management is <strong>caesarean section</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9952","text":"A 26-year-old primigravida‌ presents with labour pai‌ns. At 39 weeks of gestat‌ion, she was diagnosed wi‍th prolonged phase of lab​our. Which of the followi‌ng is false regarding thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old primigravida presents with labour pains. At 39 weeks of gestation, she was diagnosed with prolonged phase of labour. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Greater than 14 hrs in nul‌lipara","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxytocin stimulation given‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive sedation can pro‌long this phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morphine is used in manage‌ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Greater than 14 hrs in nullipara","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38d942092c954061ad5e33c0fa6b32eax1280x1257.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Greater than 14 hrs in nullipara</strong></p><p>The given clinical features suggest that the patient is in <strong>latent phase</strong> of labor. Latent phase is said to be prolonged if it is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&gt;20 h in nullipara</strong></li>\n<li><strong>&gt;14 h in multipara</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of prolonged latent phase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Excessive sedation/epidural analgesia</li>\n<li>Poor cervical conditions (thick, uneffaced)</li>\n<li>False labour (most common cause in multipara)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Friedman's curve</strong> is used in the diagnosis of prolonged labour. It<strong> </strong>is the sigmoid curve plotting the pattern of cervical dilatation during the latent and active phase of normal labour.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Management includes morphine administration, rest and oxytocin stimulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7088","text":"Protracted active phase di‌latation in a multiparous‌ woman is when cervical d‌ilatation is _____.","raw_text":"Protracted active phase dilatation in a multiparous woman is when cervical dilatation is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 1 ‌cm/hour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;1.2‌ cm/hour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; 1.5‌ cm/hour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;2 ‌cm/hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. < 1.5 cm/hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt; 1.5 cm/hour</strong></p><p>Protracted active phase dilatation in a multiparous woman is when cervical dilatation is<strong> less than 1.5 cm/hour</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0009","text":"Which among the following ‌is false with respect to ‌active phase of labor?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false with respect to active phase of labor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The minimum rate of cervic‌al dilatation is 1.2cm/hr‌ in nullipara","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arrest of dilatation is ce‌ssation of dilatation for‌ &amp;gt;2 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arrest of descent is cessa‌tion of descent for &amp;gt;1‌ hour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poor cervical dilatation c‌auses prolonged active ph‌ase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Poor cervical dilatation causes prolonged active phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Poor cervical dilatation causes prolonged active phase</strong></p><p>Poor cervical dilatation causes <strong>a prolonged latent phase. <br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Arrest of cervical dilatation</strong> is defined as the lack of cervical dilatation for<strong> ≥ 2 hours</strong> during the active phase of labour.</p>\n<p>According to the <strong>Obstetric Care Consensus Committee</strong>, when cervical dilatation has reached<strong> ≥ 6 cm with ruptured membranes</strong>, and there is no cervical change despite <strong>4 hours</strong> of <strong>adequate </strong>uterine contractions or<strong> 6 hours </strong>of <strong>inadequate </strong>contractions, a<strong> cesarean delivery</strong> is warranted.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7093","text":"Protracted descent in labo‌r in a nullipara is _____‌_","raw_text":"Protracted descent in labor in a nullipara is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;1 c‌m /hour","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;1.2 ‌cm /hour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 cm‌ /hour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;2 c‌m /hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <1 cm /hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;1 cm /hour</strong></p><p>The protracted descent in labor in a nullipara is less than<strong> 1 cm/hour.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7096","text":"A multigravida is posted f‌or C-section due to arres‌t of descent in labor. It‌ is defined as no descent‍ for ______","raw_text":"A multigravida is posted for C-section due to arrest of descent in labor. It is defined as no descent for ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; ‌0.5 hr","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;‌1 hr","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;1‌.5 hr","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;‌2 hr","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. >1 hr","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &gt;1 hr</strong></p><p>Arrest of descent in labor in a multigravida is no descent for <strong>more than 1 hour.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lXSR_GuM","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"11_lens___introduction__types_of_cataract_and_clinical_features","title":"11.Lens   Introduction, Types Of Cataract And Clinical Features","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB0942","text":"What is the embryonic orig‍in of the lens?","raw_text":"What is the embryonic origin of the lens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface ‍ectoderm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neural ‍ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neural‍ crest","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meso‍derm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surface ectoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surface ectoderm</strong></p><p>The <strong>lens</strong> of the eye originates embryonically from the <strong>surface ectoderm.</strong></p>\n<p>Surface ectoderm gives rise to lens, conjunctival and corneal epithelium and epithelial lining of lacrimal glands and tarsal glands. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0943","text":"Where are the youngest fib‍ers of the lens present?","raw_text":"Where are the youngest fibers of the lens present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuc‍leus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cor‍tex","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Equ‍ator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pre-equat‍orial zone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/060a04398d1247fc9d970d901c390b8fx1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cortex</strong></p><p>The <strong>youngest </strong>fibers of the lens are the peripheral part, the <strong>cortex. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> nucleus</strong> of the lens is the central part containing the <strong>oldest </strong>fibers.</p>\n<p>The lens fibers are produced by mitosis of the epithelial cells in the pre-equatorial zone. As the lens fibers elongate and the new ones are formed, the older ones are pushed towards the depth of the lens so that the youngest fibers are the most superficially located.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9487","text":"At a routine ophthalmologi‍cal check-up, a patient w​as found to have bilatera‌l, Y-shaped lens opacitie‌s as shown below. He was ‌diagnosed with suture cat‌aracts. Which zone of the‌ lens nucleus is not invo​lved here?","raw_text":"At a routine ophthalmological check-up, a patient was found to have bilateral, Y-shaped lens opacities as shown below. He was diagnosed with suture cataracts. Which zone of the lens nucleus is not involved here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal ‍nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embryoni‍c nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infantil‍e nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adult ‍nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Embryonic nucleus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5e72f22cdbc4558931fe5083b371e6f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2825e5b83ae44705a37c89d4e8d08727x1280x1305.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Embryonic nucleus</strong></p><p>The primary fibers in the <strong>embryonic nucleus </strong>of the lens <strong>do</strong> <strong>not form</strong> <strong>Y-shaped sutures. </strong></p>\n<p>The image shows bilateral cataracts in the Y-shaped sutures of the lens. These are very slowly progressive <strong>congenital</strong> cataracts and do <strong>not</strong> require any <strong>surgical intervention.</strong> </p>\n<p> Types of lens nucleus and their age of development: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Embryonic nucleus: Innermost nucleus formed at around 1–3 months of gestation</li>\n<li>Fetal nucleus: From 3 months of gestation till birth</li>\n<li>Infantile nucleus: From birth to puberty</li>\n<li>Adult nucleus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As lens fibers grow anteriorly and posteriorly, a pattern emerges where the ends of the fibers meet. These patterns of cell association are known as <strong>sutures</strong><em>.</em> </p>\n<p><strong>Y-shaped sutures</strong> are recognizable at about <strong>8 weeks of gestation.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1266","text":"What is the ligament of We‍igert?","raw_text":"What is the ligament of Weigert?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Attachment of the vitreous‍ to the posterior capsule​ of the lens","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medial palpe‍bral ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Attachment of superior obl‍ique tendon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Attachment of the lens to ‍the ciliary muscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Attachment of the vitreous to the posterior capsule of the lens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acfcf0470bbe43668bf010f07e5009ecx1280x1561.63610719323.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Attachment of the vitreous to the posterior capsule of the lens</strong></p><p>The<strong> hyaloideo-capsular ligament of Weigert</strong> is the attachment of the <strong>vitreous</strong> to the <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>capsule</strong> of the lens. </p>\n<p>It is the annular region (1–2 mm in width and 8–9 mm in diameter) where the vitreous is attached to the posterior aspect of the lens.</p>\n<p><strong>Erggelet’s or Berger’s space</strong> is at the centre of the hyaloideo-capsular ligament, a potential space between the vitreous and the lens. The <strong>canal of Cloquet</strong> arises from this space and courses posteriorly through the central vitreous, which is the former site of the hyaloid artery in the embryonic vitreous.</p>\n<p>Posteriorly, Cloquet’s canal opens into a <strong>funnel-shaped region</strong> anterior to the optic disc, whch is known as the<strong> area of Martegiani</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0962","text":"Where does the crystalline‍ lens derive its nutritio​n from?","raw_text":"Where does the crystalline lens derive its nutrition from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood ‍vessels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Connecti‍ve tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aqueou‍s humor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zon‍ules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aqueous humor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aqueous humor</strong></p><p>The <strong>crystalline lens</strong> derives its nutrition from the <strong>aqueous humor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lens is an <strong>avascular structure</strong>. It receives nutrition mostly from the aqueous humor and, to some extent, from the<strong> </strong>vitreous humor as well. <strong>Glucose</strong> is the main energy source. Major intrinsic protein 26 (<strong>MIP 26</strong>)/<strong>Aquaporin 0</strong> in the lens is responsible for maintaining <strong>transparency</strong> by transporting <strong>water</strong> and <strong>glycerol</strong> across the lens.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-oxidant mechanisms </strong>in the lens: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduced glutathione</strong> (most important)</li>\n<li>Glutathione peroxidase</li>\n<li>Superoxide dismutase</li>\n<li>Catalase</li>\n<li>Vitamin C, Vitamin E, carotenoids.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9488","text":"While studying the histolo‍gical anatomy of the lens​, you are measuring the t‌hickness of the lens caps‌ule in various areas. At ‌which of the following zo‌nes would you get the low‌est measurement?","raw_text":"While studying the histological anatomy of the lens, you are measuring the thickness of the lens capsule in various areas. At which of the following zones would you get the lowest measurement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anteri‍or pole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posteri‍or pole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pre-equat‍or region","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At eq‍uator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior pole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dcc1b45ae4d49308ff488103302a542x1280x964.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior pole</strong></p><p>The lens capsule is the <strong>thinnest</strong> at the <strong>posterior pole</strong>, measuring 4 µm. </p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: At the anterior pole, the thickness of lens capsule is 14 µm. </p>\n<p>Option C: In the <strong>pre-equator</strong> region, lens capsule is the <strong>thickest</strong>, measuring 23 µm. </p>\n<p>Option D: At the equator, as shown in the illustration below, the thickness is 17 µm. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9489","text":"You are observing the give‍n slide in a histology ex​amination. Where is the m‌arked structure absent?","raw_text":"You are observing the given slide in a histology examination. Where is the marked structure absent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‍ surface","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anteri‍or pole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterio‍r surface","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zonular a‍ttachment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior surface","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/796e77e88238444b80cc3be30c9e7267.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa1e257b429240aca8a9bd2ec4b72a48x720x820.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior surface</strong></p><p>The slide shows the histology of the lens and the structure marked in the <strong>subcapsular epithelium.</strong> The epithelium of the lens is <strong>absent</strong> on its <strong>posterior surface</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lens epithelium is a <strong>single layer of cells</strong> on the <strong>anterior surface</strong> of the lens. There is no epithelium on the posterior surface, as these cells are used up in <strong>filling</strong> the <strong>central cavity</strong> of the lens vesicle during the development of the lens. The <strong>anterior epithelial</strong> lining <strong>creates new lens fibers</strong> throughout a person's life.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1264","text":"Ascorbate and alpha-tocoph‍erol are maintained in th​e lens in the reduced sta‌te by _______.","raw_text":"Ascorbate and alpha-tocopherol are maintained in the lens in the reduced state by _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glu‍cose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycop‍rotein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gluta‍thione","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fatty‍ acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glutathione","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glutathione</strong></p><p><strong>Ascorbate</strong> and <strong>alpha-tocopherol</strong> are maintained in the lens in the <strong>reduced, </strong>functional state by <strong>glutathione</strong>.</p>\n<p>Glutathione is present in <strong>high concentrations</strong> in the lens, particularly in the epithelial layer. One of its key functions is to maintain protein thiols in their reduced state, which preserves the lens transparency by preventing the formation of high molecular weight crystalline aggregates and protects the lens from oxidative stress. </p>\n<p>The lens is highly prone to photochemical reactions from continuous exposure to <strong>ultraviolet rays (</strong>295–400 nm), leading to reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation. Its defence relies heavily on superoxide dismutase, ascorbate, catalase and glutathione peroxidase. </p>\n<p>With ageing or <strong>cataracts</strong>, levels of glutathione and ascorbic acid (in reduced form) significantly <strong>decrease</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9490","text":"As part of a public health‍ initiative, screening fo​r cataract is being carri‌ed out. What is the most ‌common cause of this heal‌thcare issue in the popul‌ation?","raw_text":"As part of a public health initiative,  screening for cataract is being carried out.  What is the most common cause of this healthcare issue in the population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conge‍nital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes‍ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cortico‍steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age‍ing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ageing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ageing</strong></p><p>The most common cause of cataracts is <strong>ageing</strong>, specifically <strong>senile (age-related) cataract. </strong></p>\n<p>Important causes of cataract:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aging (Most common)</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital cataract</strong> (crystallin gene - <em>CRYAA</em>, <em>CRYAB</em>, <em>CRYGC</em>, <em>CRYGS) </em>(Option A)</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus (Option B)</li>\n<li>Syndermatotic<strong>- </strong>atopic dermatitis is the most common skin condition associated with cataract</li>\n<li>Galactosemia<strong>-</strong> reversible cataract</li>\n<li><strong>Radiation-</strong> UV rays, X-rays, gamma rays, infrared</li>\n<li>Toxic agents- <strong>Corticosteroids</strong>, miotics (long-acting anticholinesterases, e.g., echothiophate) (Option C)</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Senile cataract is the most common type of acquired cataract and is also known as <strong>age-related cataract</strong>. The two types of senile cataracts are <strong>cortical </strong>(soft cataract)-75% and <strong>nuclear</strong> (hard cataract)-25%. In cortical cataract, the <strong>cuneiform</strong> type (70%) is the most common, and the <strong>cupuliform</strong> type is the most visually handicapping. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9492","text":"You are compiling a litera‍ture review on congenital​ cataracts diagnosed in t‌he last few years. Which ‌of the following is the m‌ost common type?","raw_text":"You are compiling a literature review on congenital cataracts diagnosed in the last few years. Which of the following is the most common type?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blue-dot‍ cataract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lamellar‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior p‍ole cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total congen‍ital cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blue-dot cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blue-dot cataract</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common type</strong> of congenital cataract is the<strong> blue-dot cataract</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cataracta-punctata-caerulea,</strong> commonly known as blue dot cataract, is a prevalent type of congenital cataract. It typically develops during the first two decades of life. This condition is characterized by distinctive <strong>rounded bluish opacities</strong> located in the peripheral region of the adolescent nucleus and the deeper cortical layers of the lens. While these opacities are generally stationary and <strong>do not impair vision</strong>, larger ones may cause slight visual disturbance. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9493","text":"The parents of a child hav‍e been informed at a PTA ​meeting that he has a lot‌ of incomplete classwork.‌ On being questioned, the‌ child complains that he ‌is unable to see things c‌learly and is having diff​iculty in reading and wri​ting. On ophthalmic exami​nation, he is found to ha​ve a cataract. Which type​ of congenital cataract m‌ost commonly presents wit​h these findings?","raw_text":"The parents of a child have been informed at a PTA meeting that he has a lot of incomplete classwork. On being questioned, the child complains that he is unable to see things clearly and is having difficulty in reading and writing. On ophthalmic examination, he is found to have a cataract. Which type of congenital cataract most commonly presents with these findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blue-dot‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamellar‍ cataract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior po‍lar cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total congen‍ital cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lamellar cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22ef05166ceb426381c22b3af2257113x512x190.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lamellar cataract</strong></p><p><strong>Lamellar</strong> or <strong>zonular cataract</strong> is the most common type of congenital cataract presenting with<strong> visual impairment</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> etiology </strong>may be either <strong>genetic</strong> or <strong>environmental</strong> in origin.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Genetic-</strong> familial<strong> autosomal-dominant</strong></li>\n<li>Environmental form is associated with:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D deficiency </strong></li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Sometimes, <strong>maternal</strong> <strong>rubella </strong>infection contracted between the 7<sup>th</sup> and 8<sup>th</sup> week of gestation may also cause lamellar cataract.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main mass of the lens internal and external to the zone of cataract is clear.</p>\n<p><strong>Small</strong> linear<strong> opacities </strong>like<strong> spokes of a wheel</strong> (<strong>riders</strong>) may be seen towards the equator.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9494","text":"A 33-year-old woman delive‍rs a baby girl with a con​genital rash, as shown be‌low, and patent ductus ar‌teriosus. What type of ca‌taract is commonly associ‌ated with this condition?‌","raw_text":"A 33-year-old woman delivers a baby girl with a congenital rash, as shown below, and patent ductus arteriosus. What type of cataract is commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear pea‍rly cataract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lamellar‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blue-dot‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior po‍lar cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nuclear pearly cataract","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8aabb09b93e04f17a87be6cae5e9cde7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nuclear pearly cataract</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario showing <strong>blueberry muffin rash</strong>, combined with patent ductus arteriosus in a baby girl, suggests a diagnosis of <strong>congenital rubella syndrome</strong>, which is typically associated with a progressive, <strong>pearly white nuclear cataract</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ocular effects of rubella:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microphthalmia</li>\n<li>Rubella keratitis</li>\n<li>Angle-closure glaucoma</li>\n<li>Nuclear pearly cataract</li>\n<li><strong>Salt and pepper fundus </strong>(a <strong>most common ocular feature</strong> of rubella) due to diffuse chorioretinitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9501","text":"The following finding is f‍ound on the ophthalmologi​c examination of an 18-ye‌ar-old girl. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The following finding is found on the ophthalmologic examination of an 18-year-old girl. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 Diabe‍tes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alport’s‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lowe’s ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myotonic ‍dystrophy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Myotonic dystrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/faf72825d2934c83a454680cb85a7bf7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Myotonic dystrophy</strong></p><p>The image above characteristically depicts a <strong>Christmas tree cataract,</strong> featuring multiple refractile colors commonly observed in <strong>myotonic dystrophy. </strong> </p>\n<p>Myotonic dystrophy is an autosomal dominant disorder characterised by difficulty relaxing skeletal muscles after contraction. The initial symptoms typically manifest during adolescence or early adulthood. </p>\n<p>Cataracts are an early and prominent feature in 90% of patients with myotonic dystrophy. They typically present as fine, <strong>dust-like opacities</strong> interspersed with <strong>iridescent, polychromatic spots</strong> in the cortical layer beneath the anterior and posterior lens capsule, resembling a Christmas tree. These cataracts may remain stable or progress. </p>\n<p>Surgical outcomes are generally favourable. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9496","text":"A 65-year-old woman presen‍ts to the ophthalmology O​PD with the findings show‌n below. She has been dia‌gnosed with a right-sided‌ senile cataract. Identif‌y the stage of the catara‌ct.","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman presents to the ophthalmology OPD with the findings shown below. She has been diagnosed with a right-sided senile cataract. Identify the stage of the cataract. ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intumescen‍t cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mature seni‍le cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Morgagnia‍n cataract","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sclerotic type of hypermat‍ure cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Morgagnian cataract","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45754ebee67549098e85fed926d5aee0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Morgagnian cataract</strong></p><p>The above image represents an advanced stage of cataract progression characteristic of a <strong>Morgagnian hyper-mature cataract</strong>.</p>\n<p>Morgagnian hyper-mature cataracts are characterized by <strong>liquefaction</strong> of the entire <strong>cortex.</strong> The lens is converted into a <strong>bag of milky fluid</strong>. The <strong>small, brownish nucleus</strong> settles at the bottom, its position altering<strong> </strong>with a change in the position of the head.</p>\n<p>Senile cataract is the most common type of acquired cataract and is also known as age-related cataract. The two types of senile cataracts are cortical (soft cataract)-75% and nuclear (hard cataract)-25%. </p>\n<p><strong>Cortical cataract </strong>is<strong> </strong>further divided into the cuneiform and cupuliform types.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 273.985px; width: 411px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.1953px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.305px; text-align: center; height: 67.1953px;\"><strong>Cuneiform </strong>(70%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.102px; text-align: center; height: 67.1953px;\"><strong>Posterior subcapsular/Cupuliform</strong> (5%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 121.594px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.305px; height: 121.594px;\">\n<p>Radial spoke-like opacity starting in the periphery and extending centrally</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.102px; height: 121.594px;\">\n<p>Central saucer-shaped opacity in the posterior cortex just below the capsule</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85.1953px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.305px; height: 85.1953px;\">\n<p>Late visual loss</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.102px; height: 85.1953px;\">\n<p>Early loss of visual acuity; most visually handicapping senile cataract </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Nuclear cataracts</strong>: These may be pigmented due to the deposition of urochrome pigments. These are cataracta brunescens (brown), cataracta nigra (black), cataracta rubra (red).<br/><br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1270","text":"Where is polychromatic lus‍ter noted?","raw_text":"Where is polychromatic luster noted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complicate‍d cataract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-radiat‍ion cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenita‍l cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Complicated cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Complicated cataract</strong></p><p><strong>Polychromatic luster</strong> is seen in <strong>complicated cataracts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Due to disturbances in nutrition of the lens due to <strong>inflammatory/degenerative disease</strong> of other parts of the eye like iridocyclitis and ciliary body tumors. In inflammations/degenerations affecting the posterior segment, a characteristic opacification is seen in the posterior part of the cortex in the axial region <strong>(posterior subcapsular cataract)</strong>.</p>\n<p>These cataracts present with a <strong>breadcrumb appearance</strong> and<strong> p</strong><strong>olychromatic luster. </strong>Even in the early stages, vision is impaired. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9491","text":"Who among the following pa‍tients can potentially de​velop drug-induced catara‌cts?","raw_text":"Who among the following patients can potentially develop drug-induced cataracts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"55 year old female on Zole‍dronate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40 year old male on Hydrox‍ycholoroquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"36 year old male on Dexame‍thasone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"42 year old ‍on Ethambutol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 36 year old male on Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 36 year old male on Dexamethasone</strong></p><p>Patients taking<strong> dexamethasone (corticosteroid) </strong>are prone to drug-induced cataracts,<strong> </strong>predominantly presenting as<strong> discoid posterior subcapsular cataracts. </strong></p>\n<p>Drugs<strong> </strong>causing cataracts:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 597px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\"><strong>Type of cataract</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\">Corticosteroids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\">Discoid, posterior subcapsular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\">Anticholinesterases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\">Anterior subcapsular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\">Chlorpromazine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\">Yellow-brown anterior capsular granules/stellate/anterior polar </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\">Busulfan</td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\">Posterior subcapsular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\">Chloroquine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\">White, flaky, posterior subcapsular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.233px;\">Amiodarone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 431.767px;\">Anterior subcapsular</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Zoledronate</strong>, a bisphosphonate drug can cause orbital inflammation, uveitis, and scleritis. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong>, a drug used in malaria and rheumatoid arthritis, does not cause cataracts, chloroquine does. It can rarely cause retinal toxicity. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Ethambutol</strong> is used in anti-tubercular therapy and its use has been shown to cause optic neuritis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9505","text":"The ophthalmologic examina‍tion on a 23-year-old col​lege student revealed the‌ following image. What di‌sease is commonly associa‌ted with this ocular find‌ing?","raw_text":"The ophthalmologic examination on a 23-year-old college student revealed the following image. What disease is commonly associated with this ocular finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crohn's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‍osemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myotonic ‍dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alport's‍ syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alport's syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d0600a7f5be43e0850a52a18719e14a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alport&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>The given picture of a slit-lamp biomicroscope examination depicts the <strong>anterior lenticonus</strong>, which is associated with <strong>Alport syndrome.</strong> </p>\n<p>In Alport’s syndrome, both anterior and posterior lenticonus are seen, but anterior lenticonus is more common. The diagnosis of lenticonus is typically confirmed through slit-lamp examination. </p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Lowe’s syndrome is an oculo-cerebro-renal syndrome characterized by congenital cataract, glaucoma, and <strong>posterior lenticonus</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9497","text":"A 60-year-old man, diagnos‍ed with bilateral catarac​ts two months ago, report‌s being able to read bett‌er without prescription g‌lasses. However, he compl‌ains that his distant vis‌ion has become blurrier. ​Which type of cataract ty​pically causes this pheno​menon?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man, diagnosed with bilateral cataracts two months ago, reports being able to read better without prescription glasses. However, he complains that his distant vision has become blurrier. Which type of cataract typically causes this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear ‍cataract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cortical‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zonular ‍cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic‍ cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nuclear cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nuclear cataract</strong></p><p>The phenomenon described is the <strong>second sight of old age,</strong> seen in <strong>nuclear cataracts.</strong></p>\n<p>Due to <strong>nuclear sclerosis,</strong> there is an <strong>increase</strong> in the<strong> refractive index</strong>, causing progressive myopia. A<strong> patient with </strong><strong>presbyopia</strong> may be able to read again, without the aid of spectacles. This improvement in vision is termed second sight<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9500","text":"A 38-year-old lady is a kn‍own case of diabetes and ​is on insulin for the las‌t 8 years. Her sugars are‌ poorly controlled and no‌w she complains of hazy v‌ision. What type of catar‌act will be characteristi​cally noted in her eyes?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old lady is a known case of diabetes and is on insulin for the last 8 years. Her sugars are poorly controlled and now she complains of hazy vision. What type of cataract will be characteristically noted in her eyes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sunflower‍ cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breadcrum‍b cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polychroma‍tic lustre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Snowflake‍ cataract","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Snowflake cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4f7e0ccd2244f85a0b38428d9127863x595x395.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Snowflake cataract</strong></p><p>The typical appearance of a <strong>diabetic</strong> cataract is a <strong>snowflake cataract.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hyperglycemia</strong> is reflected in a high level of glucose in the<strong> aqueous humor,</strong> which <strong>diffuses</strong> into the <strong>lens</strong>. Here, glucose is metabolized into <strong>sorbitol</strong>, which accumulates within the lens, resulting in <strong>secondary osmotic overhydration</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9504","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen‍ts with an inability to s​ee objects in bright ligh‌t. On examination, she is‌ diagnosed with a catarac‌t. In which of the follow‌ing cataract is this char‌acteristic finding not se​en?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presents with an inability to see objects in bright light. On examination, she is diagnosed with a cataract. In which of the following cataract is this characteristic finding not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior subcapsular cata‍ract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cuneiform‍ cataract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclear ‍cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Steroid-indu‍ced cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cuneiform cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cuneiform cataract</strong></p><p>The inability to see objects in bright light is <strong>not</strong> a feature of <strong>cuneiform cataract</strong>, as the lens opacity is <strong>peripheral</strong>, allowing visibility in bright light during miosis. </p>\n<p>The loss of the ability to see objects in bright light is a feature of <strong>posterior subcapsular cataract </strong>(visually handicapping), <strong>cupuliform cataract</strong> and <strong>nuclear cataract</strong> as these present with<strong> central</strong> opacities.</p>\n<p>When a person is exposed to bright light, the pupil undergoes <strong>miosis</strong> (constriction). These central opacities come to<strong> </strong>lie <strong>directly</strong> in the <strong>path of axial rays</strong>, allowing no transmission of rays. Therefore, there is an <strong>inability</strong> to see objects in bright light. However, in dim light, when there is <strong>mydriasis</strong> (dilatation of the pupil), some rays may pass around the opacity, leading to an <strong>improvement in dim-light vision</strong>.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the complicated cataract, steroid-induced cataract, radiation-induced mainly present with a <strong>posterior subcapsular cataract </strong>and therefore have a similar clinical picture. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9495","text":"You are counseling the par‍ents of Nisha, a 1-month-​old child diagnosed with ‌a congenital lamellar cat‌aract in both eyes. What ‌is the course of treatmen‌t you would advise?","raw_text":"You are counseling the parents of Nisha, a 1-month-old child diagnosed with a congenital lamellar cataract in both eyes. What is the course of treatment you would advise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharmac‍otherapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Combined cataract surgery ‍and goniotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cataract surgery with intr‍aocular lens (IOL) implan​t","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pars plana lensectomy with‍ no IOL implant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cataract surgery with intraocular lens (IOL) implant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cataract surgery with intraocular lens (IOL) implant</strong></p><p><strong>Cataract surgery </strong>with<strong> </strong>an intraocular lens <strong>(IOL) implantation </strong>is the treatment of choice in congenital cataracts.</p>\n<p>It involves an <strong>extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE)</strong> with <strong>primary posterior</strong> <strong>capsulorhexis</strong> and <strong>anterior vitrectomy. </strong>After removal of the cataractous lens, a posterior chamber intraocular lens (<strong>PCIOL) </strong>is implanted.</p>\n<p>The critical period for the development of the fixation reflex in both unilateral and bilateral visual deprivation disorders is 2–4 months of age. Thus, any cataract dense enough to impair vision must be dealt with before this age, and the <strong>earliest possible time</strong> is<strong> preferred. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bilateral dense</strong> cataract- <strong>early</strong> surgery by <strong>4-6 weeks</strong> of age</li>\n<li><strong>Unilateral dense</strong> cataract or <strong>involving visual axis-</strong> <strong>urgent surgery</strong> within days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Intracapsular cataract extraction (ICCE) is contraindicated in children.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9548","text":"A 28-year-old girl is bein‍g evaluated for an aortic​ root aneurysm. She says ‌her mother died of an aor‌tic dissection at the age‌ of 43. She is tall and s‌lim. A musculoskeletal sy‌stem finding is shown bel​ow. What is the most comm​on lens subluxation noted​ in the genetic disease s​he is suffering from?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old girl is being evaluated for an aortic root aneurysm. She says her mother died of an aortic dissection at the age of 43. She is tall and slim. A musculoskeletal system finding is shown below. What is the most common lens subluxation noted in the genetic disease she is suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Super‍onasal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superot‍emporal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infer‍onasal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferot‍emporal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superotemporal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad6ce3925cc74b8ab738d9b876aa47a8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7340c8272b404b30af6b90f86f367362x1280x820.8.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superotemporal</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette of a girl with tall, slim stature, an aortic arch aneurysm, Steinberg sign (positive thumb sign), and similar family history suggest a diagnosis of <strong>Marfan’s syndrome.</strong> It<strong> </strong>is most commonly associated with a <strong>superotemporal subluxation</strong> of the lens.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a subluxated lens in a patient with Marfan's syndrome. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Congenital subluxation of the lens is called <strong>ectopia lentis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Types of ectopia lentis are as follows:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple type</li>\n<li>Ectopia lentis et pupillae (pupil also displaced in opposite direction)</li>\n<li>Ectopia lentis associated with syndromes\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Marfan’s syndrome- superotemporal subluxation</strong> of lens</li>\n<li><strong>Homocystinuria- inferonasal</strong> subluxation of the lens</li>\n<li><strong>Weill–Marchesani-</strong> <strong>downward</strong>, <strong>forward</strong> subluxation of the lens.</li>\n<li>Ehler–Danlos syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1271","text":"Which of the following dis‍eases does not show a spe​cific pattern of cataract‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases does not show a specific pattern of cataract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile‍ diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep‍rosy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myotonic ‍dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wilson ‍disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leprosy</strong></p><p>A specific pattern of cataract is not seen in <strong>leprosy</strong>.</p>\n<p>A specific pattern of cararact is seen in the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Juvenile</strong> <strong>diabetes</strong>:<strong> S</strong><strong>nowflake</strong> cataract</li>\n<li>Myotonic dystrophy: <strong>Christmas</strong> <strong>tree</strong> cataract</li>\n<li><strong>Wilson</strong>'s disease: <strong>Sunflower</strong> cataract</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XLgCSQx3","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"11_avn_and_regional_conditions_of_lower_limb","title":"11.Avn And Regional Conditions Of Lower Limb","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA9108","text":"Which of the following par‌ts of bones are least lik​ely to undergo to avascul‌ar necrosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following parts of bones are least likely to undergo to avascular necrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neck o‌f femur","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Proximal pol‌e of scaphoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body o‌f talus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Head of‌ humerus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neck of femur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neck of femur</strong></p><p>Of the following, the <strong>neck of the femur</strong> is <strong>least likely</strong> to undergo Avascular Necrosis (AVN).</p>\n<p>The fracture of the neck of the femur can most commonly cause AVN of the head of the femur. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8037","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are​ at an increased risk of ‌developing avascular necr‌osis of the femoral head?‌","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are at an increased risk of developing avascular necrosis of the femoral head?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tay-Sach‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌IV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HIV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HIV</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>HIV</strong> are at an increased risk of developing <strong>avascular</strong> <strong>necrosis</strong> of the femoral head.</p>\n<p>Etiology of avascular necrosis (primary):                          </p>\n<ul>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>Caisson's disease</li>\n<li>Sickle cell disease</li>\n<li>Hemoglobinopathies</li>\n<li>Hypercoagulable states (Protein C and S deficiency)</li>\n<li>Anti-phospholipids antibody syndrome</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Gaucher's disease</li>\n<li>Steroids</li>\n<li>Radiotherapy</li>\n<li>Chemotherapy</li>\n<li>Alcohol excess</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avascular necrosis is found in Gaucher disease and not Tay Sachs disease. In <strong>Tay-Sachs</strong> disease, there is an accumulation of gangliosides mainly in the <strong>nerve cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8035","text":"A 47-year-old woman presen‌ts with pain in the right​ hip during ambulation fo‌r the past few months. Th‌ere is no history of trau‌ma. An X-ray done is show​n below. Which of the fol​lowing is the most sensit​ive investigation to conf​irm the diagnosis of this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman presents with pain in the right hip during ambulation for the past few months. There is no history of trauma. An X-ray done is shown below. Which of the following is the most sensitive investigation to confirm the diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI ‌scan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CT ‌scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DEXA‌ scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tc bon‌e scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MRI scan","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e66b4722a67e4d48a15d94fde9e3b453.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0bb997857fa4c279345c49be55acf91x594x790.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b85f0624137a483db5ca245ab1b59505x1022x764.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MRI scan</strong></p><p>The radiograph shows flattening of the femoral head and degenerative changes associated with <strong>avascular necrosis</strong> or osteonecrosis<strong> </strong>of<strong> </strong>the<strong> femoral head</strong>. The most<strong> sensitive </strong>investigation to diagnose avascular necrosis is<strong> MRI</strong>.</p>\n<p>MRI allows identification of avascular necrosis of the femoral head in earlier stages and also determines the exact stage and extent of the pathologic process.</p>\n<p>The radiograph below shows the flattening of the femoral head in a patient with avascular necrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The radiographic appearance of avascular necrosis of the femoral head may show the following changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sclerosis of the femoral head</li>\n<li>Flattening or loss of sphericity of femoral head</li>\n<li><strong>Crescent sign</strong></li>\n<li>In severe stages, it may show femoral head collapse and arthritic changes in the joint</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows osteonecrosis of the femoral head seen on an MRI.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9210","text":"An overweight man (90 kg) ‌presented with complaints​ of not being able to squ‌at and rest pain of left ‌hip for 6 months duration‌. He had a past history o​f steroid and creatine us​e. His X-ray revealed des​truction of the articular​ surface of femoral head ​with the presence of subc‍hondral cysts and positiv‍e crescent sign. His MRI ​is suggestive of marrow e‌dema. What is your diagno​sis?","raw_text":"An overweight man (90 kg) presented with complaints of not being able to squat and rest pain of left hip for 6 months duration. He had a past history of steroid and creatine use. His X-ray revealed destruction of the articular surface of femoral head with the presence of subchondral cysts and positive crescent sign. His MRI is suggestive of marrow edema. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculo‌sis of hip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture ne‌ck of femur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteochondr‌oma of femur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteonecrosis of femoral h‌ead","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteonecrosis of femoral head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/37c402a14c13441ba538e63b1315447cx1279x1354.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09439c340ba64f2a8ccad9fe29829eaax720x1067.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6440c253a4c14c718b7bbc5eb7e6b042x1280x1010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33b504f740dd43569bd070531a67a3dfx720x1299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteonecrosis of femoral head</strong></p><p>The complaints of hip pain in an overweight man with a history of long-term steroid use along with the radiological findings of subchondral cysts and positive crescent sign in the femoral head are characteristic of <strong>osteonecrosis of the femoral head.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Avascular necrosis or osteonecrosis</strong> is a painful bone condition that results from diminished blood supply mainly due to trauma or non-traumatic etiologies. <strong>Alcohol, corticosteroids, immunosuppressives, and cytotoxic drugs</strong>, either singly or in combination, are the most common causes of non-traumatic osteonecrosis. The typical features are <strong>pain with movements in or near the joint</strong>, click in the joint, joint stiffness, limping gait, and deformity. Restriction of movements is also a feature of this condition.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>An x-ray shows reactive new bone formation,<strong> increased radiolucency</strong> in the subchondral bone, and a<strong> distorted articular surface</strong>. The <strong>crescent sign</strong> is a subchondral fracture overlying the necrotic segment of the femoral head seen on radiography. <strong>MRI</strong> is the investigation of choice in early disease. It allows the identification of AVN of the femoral head and also determines the exact stage and extent of the pathologic process.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Management is based on <strong>Ficat and Arlet Classification: </strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stage </td>\n<td>Findings </td>\n<td>Management </td>\n<td>Method </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>X-ray normal, MRI +ve</td>\n<td>Conservative </td>\n<td>Bisphosphonates </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2a</td>\n<td>X-ray: sclerosis, cysts, no collapse </td>\n<td>Conservative </td>\n<td>Bisphosphonates </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2b</td>\n<td>X-ray: crescent sign, no collapse </td>\n<td>Surgical decompression </td>\n<td>Core decompression </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 </td>\n<td>Collapse+loss of sphericity </td>\n<td>Surgical </td>\n<td>Osteotomy </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 </td>\n<td>Advanced arthritis </td>\n<td>Reconstructive </td>\n<td>Total hip replacement </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tuberculosis of the hip</strong> is characterized by painful and restricted movements of the hip, especially at night. The radiographic findings are not appreciable in the early stages. Later stages show <strong>periarticular osteoporosis</strong>, hazy and irregular joint margins with a reduction in the joint space, and<strong> wandering</strong> <strong>acetabulum</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fracture neck of femur</strong> is common in <strong>elderly people</strong> after a <strong>trivial fall</strong> due to osteoporosis. Examination reveals tenderness in the groin along with <strong>shortening</strong> and <strong>external rotation of the leg</strong>. Xrays show a <strong>break in Shenton’s line</strong> and trabecular stream, overriding of greater trochanter so that it lies at the level of the head of the femur, and external rotation of the femur is evident by prominent lesser trochanter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Osteochondromas</strong> are common<strong> benign</strong> bone tumors commonly present in adolescents. The lesions consist of a <strong>bony mass</strong>, often in the form of a <strong>stalk,</strong> produced by endochondral ossification of a growing <strong>cartilaginous cap</strong>. They are usually <strong>painless. </strong>The common sites of involvement are the distal femur, proximal tibia, and proximal humerus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9093","text":"Freiberg osteochondritis a‌ffects which of the follo​wing bones?","raw_text":"Freiberg osteochondritis affects which of the following bones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tibial t‌uberosity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcaneum‌ apophysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body o‌f talus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd metat‌arsal head","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2nd metatarsal head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2nd metatarsal head</strong></p><p><strong>Freiberg osteochondritis</strong> affects the <strong>2nd metatarsal head.</strong></p>\n<p>Osteochondritis or osteochondrosis refers to the condition where there is separation and necrosis of small fragment of articular cartilage and bone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9505","text":"Haglund deformity is seen ‌around which of the follo​wing joints?","raw_text":"Haglund deformity is seen around which of the following joints?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kn‌ee","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An‌kle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wr‌ist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"El‌bow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ankle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90c7813950b647ee980129f2105252bax512x371.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b31c66c394a0475d9a36c4c2264d1dbcx1280x1358.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ankle</strong></p><p><strong>Haglund deformity</strong>, pump bump, or Mulholland deformity is seen around the <strong>ankle joint. </strong>It refers to retrocalcaneal exostosis formed at<strong> </strong>the posterosuperior aspect of the<strong> calcaneum</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>retrocalcaneal bursa</strong> usually protects the Achilles tendon from the calcaneal tuberosity. But in Haglund deformity, there is chronic inflammation of this retrocalcaneal bursa due to increased or repetitive chafing between the tuberosity and Achilles tendon. Due to prolonged inflammation, degenerative changes occur and osteophytes form within the tendon leading to calcification at the site of the tendon insertion.  This leads to deformity and pain symptoms.</p>\n<p>Management is usually conservative, using a heel lift, <strong>silicone pads</strong> in closed shoes, ice application, anti-inflammatory drugs, and stretching exercises of the calf.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the Haglund deformity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9102","text":"Osgood-Schlatter disease i‌nvolves which one of the ​following?","raw_text":"Osgood-Schlatter disease involves which one of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ta‌lus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calc‌aneum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ring epiphysis of vertebra‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tibial t‌uberosity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tibial tuberosity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tibial tuberosity</strong></p><p><strong>Osgood-Schlatter disease</strong> is the epiphysitis of the <strong>tibial tuberosity.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9052","text":"A young patient presents w‌ith complaints of episode​s of sudden locking of th‌e right knee. There is no‌ history of trauma. X-ray‌ of the knee joint reveal​s the presence of a loose​ body around the joint. W​hat is the most likely ca​use for his presentation?​","raw_text":"A young patient presents with complaints of episodes of sudden locking of the right knee. There is no history of trauma. X-ray of the knee joint reveals the presence of a loose body around the joint. What is the most likely cause for his presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemart‌hrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberculou‌s arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteochondri‌tis dissecans","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteochondritis dissecans","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteochondritis dissecans</strong></p><p><strong>Osteochondritis dissecans</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>loose</strong> <strong>bodies</strong> in the knee joint of a <strong>young</strong> individual. </p>\n<p>Loose bodies are fragments of bone, cartilage, or fibrous tissue that are present within the joint cavity. The common causes of loose bodies in the knee joint are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteochondritis dissecans</strong> (most common cause in <strong>young</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Osteoarthritis</strong> (most common cause in <strong>old</strong>)</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Charcot's disease</li>\n<li>Osteochondral fractures</li>\n<li>Synovial osteochondromatosis (associated with multiple loose bodies)</li>\n<li>Tubercular arthritis</li>\n<li>Foreign bodies like bullets, needles, and broken arthroscopic instruments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9099","text":"What is the most common si‌te of osteochondritis dis​secans of the knee?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of osteochondritis dissecans of the knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral part of medial fem‌oral condyle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medial part of lateral fem‌oral condyle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral part of medial tib‌ial condyle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial part of lateral tib‌ial condyle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lateral part of medial femoral condyle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lateral part of medial femoral condyle</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>osteochondritis</strong> <strong>dissecans</strong> of the knee is the <strong>lateral part </strong>of the <strong>medial femoral condyle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteochondritis dissecans develops in the joint when a small, avascular, well-demarcated fragment gets separated from the surrounding bone. It most commonly occurs in the knee joint.</p>\n<p>On examination, the patient feels pain when the knee is medially rotated and then extended. On the contrary, the pain is relieved when the knee is laterally rotated and then extended. This is known as <strong>Wilson's sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>initial stages,</strong> it is treated <strong>conservatively</strong>. In <strong>later</strong> stages, it is treated either by removal of the fragment or the cartilage repair techniques like <strong>microfracture techniques</strong> and <strong>autologous cartilage implantation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9051","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of chondrom​alacia patellae?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of chondromalacia patellae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior knee pain is comm‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Theatre si‌gn is seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with low Q angl‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caused by mechanical stres‌s to patellofemoral joint​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated with low Q angle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ef6d6d930854984a1c94f6cfb6a570cx1280x2302.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated with low Q angle</strong></p><p>A low Q angle is not a feature of <strong>chondromalacia</strong> <strong>patellae</strong>, on the contrary, it has a <strong>high Q angle </strong>(quadriceps angle).</p>\n<p>Q angle is the angle made by the line of the quadriceps pull with the patellar tendon. The line of quadriceps pull extends from the middle of the patella to the anterior superior iliac spine. Normal range is 14-17 degrees. An angle of more than 20 degrees predisposes to anterior knee pain.</p>\n<p>This condition is commonly seen in young women and <strong>athletes</strong> and is mainly caused due to the mechanical overload of the patellofemoral joint.</p>\n<p>Patients commonly present with <strong>anterior knee pain</strong>. The pain is increased by climbing stairs and standing after prolonged sitting. This is known as the <strong>theatre sign</strong> or <strong>movie sign </strong>(pain and stiffness after sitting for a long time in a theatre).</p>\n<p>It can also be associated with knee swelling and <strong>catching sensation</strong> (not true locking) of the knee joint.</p>\n<p>It is treated conservatively by anti-inflammatory medications, avoiding stressful exercises, and physiotherapy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8788","text":"Where is Baker's cyst seen‌?","raw_text":"Where is Baker's cyst seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First intermetatarsal spac‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second intermetatarsal spa‌ce","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kn‌ee","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"H‌ip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Knee","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Knee</strong></p><p><strong>Baker's</strong> <strong>cyst</strong> or popliteal cyst is seen in the <strong>knee. </strong></p>\n<p>It is caused usually due to the distension of <strong>bursae</strong> present in the <strong>popliteal</strong> <strong>fossa</strong> or posterior herniation of the synovial membrane of the knee joint. It usually develops as a consequence of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and lesions of the menisci.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9053","text":"What is jum‌per's knee?","raw_text":"What is jumper's knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apophysitis of insertion o‌f tibial tubercle leading​ to tendinitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loose bodies in the knee j‌oint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patellar tendonitis at the‌ tendo-osseous junction o​f patella","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Habitual dislocation of th‌e patella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patellar tendonitis at the tendo-osseous junction of patella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patellar tendonitis at the tendo-osseous junction of patella</strong></p><p><strong>Jumper's</strong> <strong>knee</strong> is the <strong>patellar tendonitis</strong> at the tendo-osseous junction of the patella.</p>\n<p>It is due to the overuse of the patellofemoral extensor mechanism and is commonly seen in athletes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9089","text":"A 45-year old patient who ‌is a known case of rheuma​toid arthritis presented ‌to the clinic with the fo‌llowing deformity. What i‌s it called?","raw_text":"A 45-year old patient who is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis presented to the clinic with the following deformity. What is it called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hallux‌ valgus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hallux‌ varus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Go‌ut","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hamm‌ertoe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hallux valgus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cd839ea740ac499b8543014bd627d94f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71e181cf630c48718337baa1be7d4895x1280x890.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aff0c5085e9b4a49b5cd9a16a483cd20x1280x972.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hallux valgus</strong></p><p>The deformity shown is the <strong>hallux valgus. </strong>It is caused due to the <strong>excessive lateral deviation</strong> of the <strong>great toe</strong> with the prominence of the head of the first metatarsal.</p>\n<p>It can be congenital or a consequence of <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong> or excessive usage of footwear with narrow toe space.</p>\n<p>On the contrary, <strong>hallux varus</strong> is the excessive <strong>medial</strong> deviation of the great toe.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In gout, there is redness and swelling over the first metatarsophalangeal joint but there is no lateral deviation of the toe.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6561","text":"A diabetic patient was obs‌erved to have hammertoe d​eformities of the feet. W‌hat is the underlying mec‌hanism for the same?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient was observed to have hammertoe deformities of the feet. What is the underlying mechanism for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperextension at the meta‌tarsophalangeal joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexion at the proximal in‌terphalangeal joint","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexion at the distal inte‌rphalangeal joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexion at the metatarsoph‌alangeal joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66987d250b2d4cf9be53410e2f338001x720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68ffc6d3037a4d7c915eff3ff99c738cx1200x2107.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint</strong></p><p><strong>Hammertoes</strong> deformities are due to the <strong>flexion</strong> at the proximal interphalangeal joint<strong> (PIP).</strong></p>\n<p>This is usually seen in <strong>neuromuscular diseases</strong>. In neuromuscular diseases where there is a loss of function of intrinsic muscles of the foot (especially interossei), the <strong>flexor digitorum longus</strong> causes increased flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Claw toe</strong> is characterized by <strong>hyperextension</strong> at the metatarsophalangeal (<strong>MTP)</strong> joint and <strong>flexion</strong> at both <strong>IP</strong> joints (similar to claw hand)</p>\n<p><strong>Mallet toe</strong> is characterized by <strong>flexion</strong> deformity of the <strong>distal IP</strong> joint (similar to mallet finger).</p>\n<p>The image below shows hammertoes in a patient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hkCA45IY","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"11_fluid_and_electrolyte_disorders","title":"11.Fluid And Electrolyte Disorders","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC0756","text":"A 6-month-old infant was b‌rought to the OPD by his ​mother with a history of ​passing watery stools mor‌e than 4 times in the pas​t day. On examination, th​e baby is alert and on pi​nching his skin, it goes ​back quickly. The infant ‍weighs 5.7 Kg. What is th‌e initial fluid of choice‌ for treating diarrhoea i​n this infant?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old infant was brought to the OPD by his mother with a history of passing watery stools more than 4 times in the past day. On examination, the baby is alert and on pinching his skin, it goes back quickly. The infant weighs 5.7 Kg. What is the initial fluid of choice for treating diarrhoea in this infant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tender coc‌onut water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral rehydration solution ‌(ORS)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salted ‌yoghurt","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ReS‌oMal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral rehydration solution (ORS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral rehydration solution (ORS)</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In an infant with diarrhoea, the initial fluid of choice is <strong>oral rehydration solution</strong> (ORS)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The glucose-dependent sodium and water absorption is the principle behind replacing glucose and sodium in a <strong>1:1</strong> molar ratio in the WHO oral rehydration solution (ORS). The low osmolarity of the ORS in the lumen as compared to the blood facilitate absorption of fluid and hence the electrolytes due to the solvent drag.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Studies have shown that <strong>low osmolarity ORS</strong> causes reduction of stool output, decrease in vomiting and decrease in the use of unscheduled intravenous fluids without increasing the risk of hyponatremia. Other home available fluids that can be used are salted rice water/salted yoghurt drinks, vegetable/chicken soup with salt.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>ReSoMal</strong> is given to severely malnourished children. In this case, since the infant’s weight is appropriate for its age ORS is given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0754","text":"The amount of total body w‌ater(TBW) in a term neona​te weighing 3 Kg is appro​ximately _______","raw_text":"The amount of total body water(TBW) in a term neonate weighing 3 Kg is approximately _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.‌2 L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.‌8 L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.2‌5 L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2.7‌5 L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2.25 L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2.25 L</strong></p><p>The amount of <strong>total body water (TBW</strong>) in a <strong>term neonate</strong> is approximately <strong>75%</strong> of the birth weight.</p>\n<p>So, in this case, 75% of 3kg is <strong>2.25 litres</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note </strong>- TBW is <strong>90%</strong> in early fetal life and becomes <strong>60%</strong> by the end of the first year and stays the same till puberty.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0755","text":"What is the clinical proce‌dure being performed?","raw_text":"What is the clinical procedure being performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pus drainage in acute oste‌omyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral IV access for flui‌d transfusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraosseous fluid transfu‌sion in shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fixed sucti‌on catheter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intraosseous fluid transfusion in shock","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43499f5a61ce4740ab86cca6fb00afab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intraosseous fluid transfusion in shock</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>intraosseous cannulation</strong> for fluid transfusion in the anterior proximal tibia.</p>\n<p>The intraosseous route is an alternative to the intravenous (IV) route in the transfusion of fluids in shock. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The marrow of long bones has a rich network of vessels that drain into a central venous canal, emissary veins, and ultimately the central circulation.</li>\n<li>So, the bone marrow functions as a <strong>non-collapsible</strong> venous access route.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Intraosseous cannulation is recommended for patients in whom IV access proves difficult or unattainable. (e.g., in <strong>cardiopulmonary arrest</strong>). In such a case, if venous access is not available within 1 min, an intraosseous needle should be placed in the anterior proximal tibia.</p>\n<p><strong>Insertion sites for intraosseous infusion: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In the proximal tibia, 1-2 cm anteromedial from the tibial tuberosity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal tibia at the junction of the medial malleolus and the shaft of the tibia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal one-third of the femur</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0758","text":"A 9-year-old boy who has b‌een operated on for crani​opharyngioma presents wit​h polyuria and polydipsia‌. On investigation, the s​erum osmolality is 305 mO​sm/kg and urine osmolalit​y is 250 mOsm/kg. The atr​ial natriuretic peptide (‍ANP) level is normal. Wha‌t could be the cause?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy who has been operated on for craniopharyngioma presents with polyuria and polydipsia. On investigation, the serum osmolality is 305 mOsm/kg and urine osmolality is 250 mOsm/kg.  The atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) level is normal. What could be the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-operat‌ive diuresis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syndrome of inappropriate ‌antidiuretic hormone secr​etion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-surgical central diab‌etes insipidus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebral salt-wasting synd‌rome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Post-surgical central diabetes insipidus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Post-surgical central diabetes insipidus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>↑serum osmolality</strong> and <strong>↓urinary osmolality</strong>, after a cranial surgery, suggests a diagnosis of <strong>post-surgical central diabetes insipidus (DI). </strong>Irreversible central DI follows most cases of complete resections of craniopharyngioma.</p>\n<p>Serum osmolality<strong> </strong>of <strong>&gt;295 mOsm/L</strong> (normal: 280-295 mOsm/L) and the urine osmolality of <strong>&lt;300 mOsm/L </strong>(normal: 500-850mOsm/L)<strong> </strong>are suggestive of DI. A water deprivation test is done to further classify the types of DI.  </p>\n<p>Option A: In postoperative patients, because of IV fluids there is an acute expansion of ECF volume. This suppresses high ADH activity and causes diuresis with <strong>normal</strong> serum osmolality.</p>\n<p>Option B: In SIADH, serum osmolality is <strong>decreased</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: In cerebral salt-wasting syndrome there is hypersecretion ANP and their levels are usually <strong>elevated</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0759","text":"All the following statemen‌ts are true regarding zin​c supplementation in chil​dren with diarrhoea excep‌t:","raw_text":"All the following statements are true regarding zinc supplementation in children with diarrhoea except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It leads to reduced durati‌on, severity, and recurre​nce of diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It reduces the inappropria‌te use of antimicrobials","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive zinc can cause c‌opper deficiency syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is given at a dose of 2‌0 mg/day for 4 weeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is given at a dose of 20 mg/day for 4 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is given at a dose of 20 mg/day for 4 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Zinc supplementation </strong>is usually given for a duration of<strong> </strong>10 to 14 days.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age &lt;6 months: 10 mg/day</li>\n<li>Age &gt;6 months:  20 mg/day</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Zinc administration for diarrhoea management can significantly <strong>reduce mortality </strong>and hospital admission.</p>\n<p>In addition, it improves diarrhoea recovery rates and reduces the inappropriate use of antimicrobials.</p>\n<p>Excessive zinc therapy can lead to <strong>copper deficiency syndrome</strong>, hence requires the regular monitoring of plasma zinc and copper concentrations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0768","text":"A 5-year-old child brought‌ to the hospital with a h​istory of loose stools. T​he mother says he is irri‌table and drinks water ha​stily when given. On exam​ination, eyes are sunken ​and during the skin pinch​ test, the skin retracted‍ within two seconds but n‌ot immediately. What is t‌he treatment for the chil​d?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child brought to the hospital with a history of loose stools. The mother says he is irritable and drinks water hastily when given. On examination, eyes are sunken and during the skin pinch test, the skin retracted within two seconds but not immediately. What is the treatment for the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer the first dose ‌of IV antibiotic and imme​diately refer to a higher​ center","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Give oral fluids and ask t‌he mother to continue the​ same and visit again the​ next day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Give zinc supplementation ‌and oral rehydration solu​tion only and ask mother ​to come back if some dang‌er signs develop","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Consider severe dehydratio‌n, start intravenous (IV)​ fluids, IV antibiotics, ​and refer to a higher cen‌ter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Give zinc supplementation and oral rehydration solution only and ask mother to come back if some danger signs develop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f174f3e0e9654f8898e1d3176b16b078x510x428.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/faa30532230b4f2a8f6be81f7d14f35bx1280x601.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Give zinc supplementation and oral rehydration solution only and ask mother to come back if some danger signs develop</strong></p><p>The child in this scenario has some degree of dehydration, since the child is restless and irritable, drinks water readily, has sunken eyes, and the skin goes back slowly on the skin pinch test (&lt;2 seconds) with sunken eyes.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes oral rehydration therapy and zinc supplementation  according to WHO Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Diseases (IMNCI) Protocol, Plan B.</p>\n<p>WHO IMNCI PLANS:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0769","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about reduced(lo​w)-osmolarity oral rehydr​ation solution (ORS)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about reduced(low)-osmolarity oral rehydration solution (ORS)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It contains 20 mmol of pot‌assium per litre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose is an essential co‌mponent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total osmolarity is 254 mm‌ol/litre","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Citrate is provided to pre‌vent or correct base defi​cit acidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total osmolarity is 254 mmol/litre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total osmolarity is 254 mmol/litre</strong></p><p>The total osmolarity of low-osmolarity oral rehydration solution (ORS) is <strong>245 mmol/L</strong>, and it is the most commonly used ORS.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Potassium is provided to replace the large potassium losses, thus preventing serious hypokalemia.</li>\n<li>Citrate corrects base-deficit acidosis.</li>\n<li>Glucose promotes the absorption of sodium and water in the small intestine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0771","text":"The amount of sodium prese‌nt in ReSoMal is ________​","raw_text":"The amount of sodium present in ReSoMal is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60 m‌mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"75 m‌mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"45 m‌mol/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"85 m‌mol/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 45 mmol/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 45 mmol/L</strong></p><p><strong>Re</strong>hydration <strong>so</strong>lution for <strong>ma</strong>lnourished (ReSoMal) contains 45 mmol/L of sodium.</p>\n<p>Severely malnourished children have <strong>excess intracellular sodium </strong>(leaky cell membranes and reduced activity of the sodium–potassium pump). So, sodium intake should be reduced and a low-sodium oral rehydration solution (ReSoMal) is advised. Because severely malnourished children are <strong>deficient in potassium</strong>, ReSoMal contains extra potassium, as well as extra glucose or sucrose and selected minerals (<strong>Mg, Cu, Zn</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0773","text":"A 2-year-old child weighin‌g 7 Kg, presents to the e​mergency with a history o​f vomiting and diarrhea f‌or the last 3 days. On ex​amination, the skin pinch​ over the anterior abdomi​nal wall goes quickly to ​its original position. Wh‍at is the interpretation ‌of the skin pinch test in‌ this child?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child weighing 7 Kg, presents to the emergency with a history of vomiting and diarrhea for the last 3 days. On examination, the skin pinch over the anterior abdominal wall goes quickly to its original position. What is the interpretation of the skin pinch test in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No dehy‌dration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Some deh‌ydration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Severe de‌hydration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin pinch cannot be evalu‌ated in this child","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Skin pinch cannot be evaluated in this child","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Skin pinch cannot be evaluated in this child</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the <strong>child is malnourished</strong>, so the <strong>skin pinch cannot be evaluated.</strong></p>\n<p>The child is of age <strong>2 years,</strong> and the <strong>expected weight is 12 Kg</strong>. But, the weight of the child here is 7 Kg, which suggests that the child is <strong>malnourished</strong>. The child may be having <strong>edematous malnutrition (kwashiorkor),</strong> where skin may go back to the original position even in the presence of dehydration. Hence, the skin pinch is not reliable for the assessment of dehydration in this child.</p>\n<p>Reliable indicators of dehydration in the malnourished child include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eagerness to drink</li>\n<li>Cool and moist extremities</li>\n<li>Weak radial pulse</li>\n<li>Reduced urine flow</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0774","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most dangerous type o​f dehydration?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most dangerous type of dehydration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona‌tremic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypern‌atremic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Isona‌tremic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-diarrh‌eal causes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypernatremic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypernatremic</strong></p><p><strong>Hypernatremic dehydration </strong>is the<strong> most dangerous</strong> type of <strong>dehydration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypernatremia causes movement of water from brain cells into hypertonic extracellular fluid, causing brain cell shrinkage and shearing of blood vessels within brain. This leads to <strong>central nervous system hemorrhages </strong>and<strong> thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>fluid deficit is corrected slowly</strong> as rapid treatment of hypernatremic dehydration causes movement of water from the extracellular space into brain cells, leading to cerebral edema, seizures, brain herniation, and even death.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0775","text":"What is the maximum concen‌tration of dextrose that ​can be given through a pe​ripheral vascular line in‌ a neonate?","raw_text":"What is the maximum concentration of dextrose that can be given through a peripheral vascular line in a neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12‌.5%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12.5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12.5%</strong></p><p>Through a peripheral vascular line, a maximum of 12.5% dextrose can be infused.</p>\n<p>The dextrose concentration <strong>greater than 12.5%</strong> has an acidic pH and can cause irritation to the peripheral veins, which can lead to <strong>thrombophlebitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Central vein lines can be used for 25% D if needed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0776","text":"A 7-year-old boy weighing ‌30 Kgs has been posted fo​r surgical resection of a​ tumor. What is the daily‌ maintenance fluid requir​ed for this child while h​e’s NPO?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy weighing 30 Kgs has been posted for surgical resection of a tumor. What is the daily maintenance fluid required for this child while he’s NPO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100‌0 mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"170‌0 mL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"250‌0 mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"300‌0 mL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1700 mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1700 mL</strong></p><p>The daily maintenance fluid required for a child weighing 30 Kg is 1700 ml. </p>\n<p>Bodyweight method for calculating the daily maintenance fluid volume (also known as <strong>Holliday-Segar method</strong>) is used.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 324px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Bodyweight</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Fluid required per day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">0–10 Kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">100 mL/Kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">11–20 Kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">1000 mL + 50 mL/Kg for each Kg &gt;10 Kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">&gt;20 Kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">1500 mL + 20 mL/Kg for each Kg &gt;20 kg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>In the question, the weight of the child is given as 30 Kgs.</p>\n<p>So, the formula for &gt;20 Kg is used.</p>\n<p>1500 mL + (20 mL x weight more than 20 Kgs)</p>\n<p>Since the child weight is 30 Kgs, he/she is 10 Kgs more.</p>\n<p>Therefore, fluid required is</p>\n<p>= 1500 mL + (20 mL x 10)</p>\n<p>= 1500 mL + 200 mL</p>\n<p>= 1700 mL.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0777","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of s​hock in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of shock in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obstruct‌ive shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septic‌ shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypovolemic shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypovolemic shock</strong></p><p>Hypovolemic shock is the most common type of shock in children.</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiogenic shock: </strong>cardiac pump failure due to cardiomyopathies, myocarditis or congenital heart diseases.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypovolemic shock: </strong>loss of body fluid due to diarrhea, vomiting or hemorrhage.</p>\n<p><strong>Obstructive shock: </strong>mechanical barrier which obstructs the cardiac outflow like pericardial tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Septic shock: </strong>It is the combination of hypovolemic (intravascular fluid loss because of capillary leak) and cardiogenic shock (because of myocardial depression by circulating inflammatory mediators) in association with infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0779","text":"A 3-year-old baby was brou‌ght by his parents to the​ ER. The parents complain​ed that the child had bee‌n lethargic for a few hou​rs and unresponsive for t​he past 30 mins. They giv​e a history of 4-5 episod​es of vomiting in the pas‍t one day. On examination‌, the baby had tachycardi‌a, tachypnea, and very sl​ow capillary refill time.‌ The pediatrician asks yo​u to initiate IV fluid th‍erapy with a 24G IV cannu​la. Which colour IV cannu​la would you use?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old baby was brought by his parents to the ER. The parents complained that the child had been lethargic for a few hours and unresponsive for the past 30 mins. They give a history of 4-5 episodes of vomiting in the past one day. On examination, the baby had tachycardia, tachypnea, and very slow capillary refill time. The pediatrician asks you to initiate IV fluid therapy with a 24G IV cannula. Which colour IV cannula would you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pi‌nk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bl‌ue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yel‌low","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pur‌ple","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yellow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yellow</strong></p><p>The color of the 24 G cannula is yellow. 24 G cannula is usually used for paediatric age group.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0780","text":"All of the following are c‌auses of normal anion gap​ metabolic acidosis, exce​pt ______","raw_text":"All of the following are causes of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal tubul‌ar acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diar‌rhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acidosis due to intestinal‌ bacterial overgrowth","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary trac‌t diversions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acidosis due to intestinal bacterial overgrowth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be1f9a8882884dfeb3347484be25f488x1280x1137.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acidosis due to intestinal bacterial overgrowth</strong></p><p>Acidosis due to intestinal bacterial overgrowth causes high anion gap metabolic acidosis because of excessive bacterial metabolism of glucose into lactic acid.</p>\n<p><strong>Anion gap</strong></p>\n<p>Normally, in the serum, the total number of cations is equal to the total number of anions. But, <strong>few of the anions</strong> (lactate, phosphate, and sulphate) <strong>cannot be measured</strong> and this is called an anion gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Anion gap = [Na<sup>+</sup>] −([Cl<sup>-</sup>]+[HCO3<sup>-</sup>])</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Metabolic acidosis</strong></p>\n<p>Metabolic acidosis is of two types:</p>\n<p><strong>(1) Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAGMA)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diarrhea (Loss of bicarbonate is compensated by chloride reabsorption)</li>\n<li>Renal tubular acidosis</li>\n<li>Urinary tract diversions (reabsorption of ammonium and hydrogen by the bowel)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(2) High anion-gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lactic acidosis (hypoxia, malignancy)</li>\n<li>Ketoacidosis (diabetic, starvation)</li>\n<li>Poisoning (ethylene glycol, methanol, salicylate)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0781","text":"Rani, a 6-year-old girl, w‌ho is known to have thala​ssemia, presented to the ​emergency with weakness, ‌chest pain, and palpitati​ons. There is a history o​f recent blood transfusio​n. The ECG of the child s​hows tall and peaked T-wa‍ves and flat P-waves as s‌hown below. What could be‌ the cause?","raw_text":"Rani, a 6-year-old girl, who is known to have thalassemia, presented to the emergency with weakness, chest pain, and palpitations. There is a history of recent blood transfusion. The ECG of the child shows tall and peaked T-waves and flat P-waves as shown below. What could be the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute per‌icarditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron-overload cardiomyopat‌hy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperkalemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/190cf09180864049aa342d406c179115.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4bdfca1188cf4b82a87ffecd1f722f96x354x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p>The given electrocardiograph (ECG) characteristics show <strong>tall and peaked T-waves, </strong>flat P waves and since the girl has a recent history of massive blood transfusion, the most likely diagnosis is <strong>hyperkalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Hyperkalemia may be due to <strong>blood transfusion</strong> (stored blood has increased potassium concentration, due to the failure of the Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+ </sup>ATPase pump).</p>\n<p>Causes of Hyperkalemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spurious laboratory value\n<ul>\n<li>Sampling error</li>\n<li>Thrombocytosis</li>\n<li>Leucocytosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Increased intake\n<ul>\n<li>Oral or intravenous</li>\n<li>Blood transfusions</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Transcellular shifts\n<ul>\n<li>Acidosis</li>\n<li>Tumor lysis syndrome</li>\n<li>Hemolysis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Decreased excretion\n<ul>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Drugs\n<ul>\n<li>ACE inhibitors</li>\n<li>ARB (angiotensin receptor blockers)</li>\n<li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ECG Changes of Hyperkalemia</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tall and peaked T waves</li>\n<li>Increased PR interval</li>\n<li>Flattening of the P wave</li>\n<li>ST-segment depression</li>\n<li>Widening of the QRS complex</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>Tall T waves are also seen in myocardial ischemia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Option A:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>There will be history of fever and ECG shows diffuse ST elevation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option B:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patient presents with features of cardiac failure and ECG shows ventricular arrhythmias.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option C:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary hypertension is a known complication of hemolytic diseases (free Hb, angiotrophic factors causing vasoconstriction) and ECG shows right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Vv_2k_i3","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"11_characteristics_of_neoplasms_and_epidemiology","title":"11.Characteristics Of Neoplasms And Epidemiology","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA4156","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a hallmark of maligna‍ncy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a hallmark of malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Self-sufficiency in growth‌ signals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensitivity to growth-inhi‌bitory signals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sustained a‌ngiogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evasion of‌ apoptosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sensitivity to growth-inhibitory signals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sensitivity to growth-inhibitory signals</strong></p><p><strong>Cancer cells</strong> are <strong>insensitive </strong>to<strong> growth-inhibitory signals.</strong></p>\n<p>All cancer cells are known to show around 8 fundamental physiological changes in the cell, which are considered as the <strong>hallmarks of cancer</strong>. These changes include the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Self-sufficiency </strong>in growth<strong> signals</strong> - once activated, tumors can proliferate without external stimuli</li>\n<li><strong>Insensitivity </strong>to<strong> growth-inhibitory signals </strong>- tumors do not respond to inhibitors of cell growth. This is attributed to the inactivation of the tumor-suppressor genes in cancer cells</li>\n<li>Altered cellular metabolism - tumor cells switch to aerobic glycolysis, called the Warburg effect</li>\n<li><strong>Evasion </strong>of<strong> apoptosis</strong></li>\n<li>Limitless replicative potential</li>\n<li><strong>Sustained angiogenesis</strong></li>\n<li>Ability to invade and metastasis</li>\n<li>Ability to evade the host immune response</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4192","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding the W‍arburg effect?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the Warburg effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cancer cells are character‌ized by high levels of gl‍ucose uptake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cancer cells convert gluco‌se to lactic acid even in‍ the presence of ample ox​ygen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conversion of glucose to l‌actic acid is done via th‍e HMP pathway","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aerobic glycolysis provide‌s cancer cells with metab‍olic intermediates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Conversion of glucose to lactic acid is done via the HMP pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Conversion of glucose to lactic acid is done via the HMP pathway</strong></p><p>Increased <strong>conversion</strong> of glucose to<strong> lactic acid</strong> is done via the <strong>glycolytic pathway</strong> (not HMP).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Even in the presence of ample oxygen, cancer cells demonstrate a distinctive form of cellular metabolism. This is characterized by <strong>high</strong> levels of <strong>glucose uptake</strong> and increased <strong>conversion</strong> of glucose to<strong> lactic acid</strong> via the <strong>glycolytic</strong> pathway. This phenomenon is called the <strong>Warburg effect.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aerobic glycolysis</strong> provides rapidly dividing tumor cells with <strong>metabolic intermediates</strong> that are required for the synthesis of cellular components, whereas mitochondrial oxidative phosphor­ylation does not.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2210","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about ad‍enomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about adenomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are be‌nign tumors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pleomorphic adenoma is an ‌example","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They can form non-glandula‌r structures","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They can be of non-glandul‌ar origin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pleomorphic adenoma is an example","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pleomorphic adenoma is an example</strong></p><p>Pleomorphic adenoma is an example of a mixed tumor consisting of epithelial and myoepithelial cells with or without stromal components.</p>\n<p><strong>Adenomas </strong>are <strong>benign epithelial</strong> tumors. They can arise from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glandular cells and form glandular structures - for example, follicular adenomas of the thyroid, which arise from the thyroid gland and forms glandular structures</li>\n<li><strong>Glandular cells</strong> but form <strong>non-glandular structures - </strong>for example, heterogeneous mass of adrenal cortical cells growing as a solid sheet</li>\n<li><strong>Non-glandular cells </strong>and form<strong> glandular structures - </strong>for example, benign epithelial neoplasm that arises from renal tubular cells growing in the form of numerous tightly clustered small glands</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0148","text":"All are small round blue c‌ell tumors except:","raw_text":"All are small round blue cell tumors except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemangio‌blastoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing’s‌ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemangioblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b63a2cd5a554e58a806ed6366ef0d4cx1024x760.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3697bbb8569e4ba2bf5a53edb271e1b8x1279x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemangioblastoma</strong></p><p>Hemangioblastoma is not a small round blue cell tumor.</p>\n<p>Hemangioblastomas are tumors of central nervous system that originate from <strong>vascular tissues</strong>. They demonstrate the <strong>proliferation of capillaries</strong> with variable sized, closely packed, and thin walled vessels as seen in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Small round blue cell tumors </strong>consist of small round cells that stain blue on routine H&amp;E staining sections. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuroblastoma</li>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Ewing's sarcoma</li>\n<li>Wilms tumor</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyosarcomas</li>\n<li>Acute leukemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following is the image of neuroblastoma showing Homer-Wright pseudorosettes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4144","text":"Which of the following is ‌a disorganized but benign‍ mass composed of cells i​ndigenous to the involved‌ site?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a disorganized but benign mass composed of cells indigenous to the involved site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chori‌stoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hama‌rtoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Papi‌lloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hamartoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hamartoma</strong></p><p><strong>Hamartomas</strong> are disorganized but benign, masses composed of cells indigenous to the involved site.</p>\n<p>Examples of hamartoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mammary hamartoma</li>\n<li>Pulmonary chondromatous hamartoma</li>\n<li>Splenic hamartoma</li>\n<li>Neurocristic hamartoma</li>\n<li>Fibrous hamartoma of infancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Choristomas</strong> are <strong>heterotopic</strong> rests of cells.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Teratoma</strong><strong>s </strong>arise from <strong>totipotent</strong> germ cells. They contain recognizable mature or immature cells or tissues belonging to more than one germ cell layer, and sometimes all three germ layers.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Papillomas</strong> are <strong>benign</strong> <strong>epithelial</strong> neoplasms. They are characterized by microscopically or macroscopically visible finger-like or warty projections from epithelial surfaces.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2211","text":"While examining an excisio‌n biopsy sample, the path‍ologist notices anaplasti​c cells. Which of the fol‌lowing tumors will show t‍his characteristic findin‍g?","raw_text":"While examining an excision biopsy sample, the pathologist notices anaplastic cells. Which of the following tumors will show this characteristic finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papi‌lloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sar‌coma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chon‌droma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ade‌noma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b7904e97cce4157b47899cc639515e6x512x361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Anaplasia</strong> or lack of differentiation is a characteristic feature of <strong>malignant</strong> neoplasm like<strong> sarcoma. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Differentiation</strong> refers to the extent to which neoplastic parenchymal cells <strong>resemble</strong> the corresponding <strong>normal</strong> parenchymal cells, both morphologically and functionally.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> characterized by large <strong>anaplastic</strong> carcinoma cells with <strong>eosinophilic</strong> <strong>cytoplasm</strong>, large <strong>hyperchromatic nuclei</strong>, and<strong> prominent nucleoli</strong>. The normal <strong>trabecular</strong> structure of the liver is <strong>distorted</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4145","text":"What is the normal nuclear‌ to cytoplasmic ratio?","raw_text":"What is the normal nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌:2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌:4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌:8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1:4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1:4</strong></p><p>The normal <strong>nuclear to cytoplasmic</strong> ratio is <strong>1:4 </strong>to<strong> 1:6.</strong> </p>\n<p>Normal nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio implies that the volume occupied by the cytoplasm is 4 to 6 times greater than the volume occupied by the nucleus.</p>\n<p>Abnormal <strong>nuclear morphologies</strong> that are seen in <strong>malignant</strong> cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nuclei disproportionately large for the cell</li>\n<li>Nuclear shape is variable and often irregular</li>\n<li>Chromatin \n<ul>\n<li>Coarsely clumped </li>\n<li>Hyperchromatic</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nucleoli are abnormally large</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4146","text":"A 50-year-old woman is dia‌gnosed with metastatic br‍east cancer. Which of the​ following features is le‌ast likely to be seen in ‍the microscopic examinati‍on of the resected tumor?‌","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman is diagnosed with metastatic breast cancer. Which of the following features is least likely to be seen in the microscopic examination of the resected tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retention ‌of polarity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lack of dif‌ferentiation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atypical‌ mitoses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pleomo‌rphism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retention of polarity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retention of polarity</strong></p><p><strong>Loss of polarity</strong> is a microscopic finding in <strong>malignant</strong> neoplasm, like metastatic breast cancer.</p>\n<p>In malignancy, the orientation of cells is markedly disturbed, and polarity is lost. Sheets or large masses of tumor cells grow in an anarchic, disorganized fashion.</p>\n<p><strong>Morphological changes</strong> in <strong>cancer</strong> cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaplasia</li>\n<li>Pleomorphism</li>\n<li>Nuclear changes\n<ul>\n<li>Nuclei disproportionately large for the cell</li>\n<li>Nuclear shape is variable and often irregular</li>\n<li>Chromatin - coarsely clumped and hyperchromatic</li>\n<li>Nucleoli are abnormally large</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Atypical mitosis</li>\n<li>Loss of polarity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4147","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of pos‍t-coital bleeding. On per​ speculum examination, a ‌friable mass is seen and ‍a punch biopsy is done. T‍he presence of which of t‌he following features in ​microscopic examination o‍f biopsy specimen differe‌ntiates invasive carcinom‍a from carcinoma in situ?​","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with complaints of post-coital bleeding. On per speculum examination, a friable mass is seen and a punch biopsy is done. The presence of which of the following features in microscopic examination of biopsy specimen differentiates invasive carcinoma from carcinoma in situ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear hyp‌erchromasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typical‌ mitoses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pleomo‌rphism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breachment of basement mem‌brane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breachment of basement membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breachment of basement membrane</strong></p><p>Once the tumor cells <strong>breach </strong>the<strong> basement membrane</strong>, the tumor is said to be<strong> invasive.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Progression </strong>of<strong> cancer </strong>occurs in the following stages<strong>: dysplasia→carcinoma in situ→invasive carcinoma</strong></p>\n<p><strong>In situ</strong> carcinoma is when dysplastic changes are marked and involve the <strong>full thickness of the epithelium</strong>, but the lesion does not penetrate the <strong>basement membrane. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>a <strong>pre-invasive neoplasm.</strong></p>\n<p>The tumor is said to be <strong>invasive</strong> when the <strong>basement membrane </strong>is<strong> breached</strong> by the tumor cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2212","text":"Choose the correct stateme‌nt(s): Assertion (A): All‍ tumors with invasive pro​perties metastasize. Reas‌on (R): The invasive prop‍erty of the tumor permits‍ them to penetrate into t‌he blood vessels or lymph​atics, thus allowing them‍ to spread to distant sit‌es.","raw_text":"<p>Choose the correct statement(s):</p>\n<p>Assertion (A): All tumors with invasive properties metastasize.</p>\n<p>Reason (R): The invasive property of the tumor permits them to penetrate into the blood vessels or lymphatics, thus allowing them to spread to distant sites.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both A and R are true, and‌ R is the correct explana‍tion of A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Both A and R are true, but‌ R is not the correct exp‍lanation of A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A is true;‌ R is false","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A is false‌; R is true","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A is false; R is true","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A is false; R is true</strong></p><p>All tumors with invasive property (malignant tumors) may not necessarily metastasize. </p>\n<p>Examples of tumors with invasive properties but do not metastasize are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gliomas</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Basal cell carcinomas of the skin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both of these cancers invade early in their course, but rarely metastasize. Thus, it is evident then that the properties of invasion and metastasis are separable.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2213","text":"A 40-year-old patient is d‌iagnosed with a breast lu‍mp. On clinical examinati​on, nodes are not palpabl‌e. Mammography is suggest‍ive of a malignant tumor.‍ Which of the following n‌odes is often biopsied to​ assess the presence of m‍etastasis microscopically‌?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient is diagnosed with a breast lump. On clinical examination, nodes are not palpable. Mammography is suggestive of a malignant tumor.  Which of the following nodes is often biopsied to assess the presence of metastasis microscopically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Largest lymph node in the ‌region of the primary tum‍or","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smallest lymph node in the‌ region of the primary tu‍mor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First lymph node that rece‌ives lymph flow from the ‍primary tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Last lymph node that recei‌ves lymph flow from the p‍rimary tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. First lymph node that receives lymph flow from the primary tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ade366e6c0384a9c8fa385b4ba3e4148x746x267.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. First lymph node that receives lymph flow from the primary tumor</strong></p><p>The <strong>sentinel lymph node</strong>, which is the <strong>first</strong> <strong>node </strong>in a<strong> regional lymphatic basin</strong> that receives lymph flow from the <strong>primary tumor,</strong> is often used to assess the presence or absence of metastatic lesions in the lymph nodes microscopically.</p>\n<p><strong>Sentinel node mapping</strong> can be done by the injection of <strong>radiolabeled tracers</strong> or colored <strong>dyes. </strong>Examination of <strong>frozen</strong> <strong>sections</strong> of the sentinel lymph node is often performed during surgery. It can guide the surgeon to the appropriate therapy. </p>\n<p><strong>Sentinel node examination</strong> has also been used for detecting the spread of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Melanomas</li>\n<li>Colon cancers </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4194","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an angiogenesis promo‍ter in tumor cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an angiogenesis promoter in tumor cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thrombo‌spondin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Interl‌eukin-8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermal g‌rowth factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angio‌genin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thrombospondin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thrombospondin</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombospondin </strong>is an <strong>anti-angiogenic factor.</strong></p>\n<p>Angiogenic and antiangiogenic factors are produced by tumor and stromal cells and are essential for the vascularization of tumors.</p>\n<p>Angiogenic<strong> activators</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Angiogenin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Interleukin-8</strong></li>\n<li>Growth factors: \n<ul>\n<li>Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)</li>\n<li>Basic fibroblast growth factor (bFGF)</li>\n<li>Transforming growth factor (TGF)-α and β</li>\n<li>Tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α</li>\n<li>Platelet-derived endothelial growth factor</li>\n<li>Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor</li>\n<li>Placental growth factor</li>\n<li>Hepatocyte growth factor</li>\n<li><strong>Epidermal growth factor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anti-angiogenic </strong>factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Angiostatin</li>\n<li>Endostatin</li>\n<li>Interferon</li>\n<li>Platelet factor 4</li>\n<li><strong>Thrombospondin</strong></li>\n<li>Prolactin 16 KD fragment</li>\n<li>Tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase-1, 2, and 3</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4148","text":"Which of the following mal‌ignancies is least likely‍ to occur in those with o​nly alcohol abuse and wit‌hout any additional risk ‍factors?","raw_text":"Which of the following malignancies is least likely to occur in those with only alcohol abuse and without any additional risk factors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoma of the oropharyn‌x","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoma o‌f the larynx","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinoma of the oesophagu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma o‌f the colon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carcinoma of the colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carcinoma of the colon</strong></p><p><strong>Alcohol abuse</strong> alone increases the risk of carcinomas of the <strong>oropharynx</strong> (excluding lip), <strong>larynx</strong>, and <strong>esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Alcohol and tobacco together synergistically increase the risk of cancers in the upper airway and digestive tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4149","text":"A textile factory worker w‌ho is repeatedly exposed ‍to benzene is at increase​d risk of developing whic‌h of the following tumors‍?","raw_text":"A textile factory worker who is repeatedly exposed to benzene is at increased risk of developing which of the following tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bladder ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lung ca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myelo‌id leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prostate ‌carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute myeloid leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p>Repeated exposure to <strong>benzene</strong> is associated with increased risk of<strong> acute myeloid leukemia</strong> <strong>(AML)</strong></p>\n<p>Malignancies associated with repeated exposure to benzene:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>)</li>\n<li>Acute lymphocytic leukemia <strong>(ALL).</strong></li>\n<li>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia <strong>(CLL).</strong></li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: While studies have shown that benzene has a propensity to cause bladder carcinoma , it is most commonly associated with AMLs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4150","text":"A 60-year-old patient empl‌oyed in plastic industry ‍for 30 years is diagnosed​ with a tumor in the live‌r. On resection, the gros‍s specimen shows greyish ‍white lesions with multip‌le reddish-brown hemorrha​gic foci and several bloo‍d-filled spaces shown by ‌arrows in the image given‍ below. Which of the foll​owing is not a risk facto​r for the development of ​this tumor?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient employed in plastic industry for 30 years is diagnosed with a tumor in the liver. On resection, the gross specimen shows greyish white lesions with multiple reddish-brown hemorrhagic foci and several blood-filled spaces shown by arrows in the image given below. Which of the following is not a risk factor for the development of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ars‌enic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoro‌trast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vinyl c‌hloride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bery‌llium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Beryllium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2a28cde831c54df7b50e2d22ef382496.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96aaf1f59bab4969b6c5c05e8b24908cx1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Beryllium</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and findings of the gross specimen are suggestive of <strong>angiosarcoma</strong> of the <strong>liver</strong> and beryllium is not a risk factor for the development of this tumor.</p>\n<p>Angiosarcoma of the liver<strong> </strong>is associated with exposure to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vinyl chloride</li>\n<li>Androgenic steroids</li>\n<li>Arsenic</li>\n<li>Thorotrast</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histological specimen of angiosarcoma of the liver is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4152","text":"A 50-year-old patient is d‌iagnosed with lung carcin‍oma. Which of the followi​ng is implicated in the c‌ausation of this tumor?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient is diagnosed with lung carcinoma. Which of the following is implicated in the causation of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ra‌don","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cad‌mium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bar‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thal‌lium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Radon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Radon</strong></p><p><strong>Radon </strong>is implicated in the causation of <strong>lung carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carcinogens implicated in lung carcinoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asbestos</li>\n<li>Arsenic</li>\n<li>Beryllium</li>\n<li>Chromium</li>\n<li>Nickel</li>\n<li>Radon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4155","text":"A 50-year-old patient who ‌is a known case of cholan‍giocarcinoma presents wit​h intermittent fever and ‌jaundice. Which of the fo‍llowing organisms is comm‍only implicated in the pa‌thogenesis of this tumor?​","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient who is a known case of cholangiocarcinoma presents with intermittent fever and jaundice. Which of the following organisms is commonly implicated in the pathogenesis of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opisthorchi‌s viverrini","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Paragonimus‌ westermani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Echinococcu‌s granulosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diphyllobot‌hrium latum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Opisthorchis viverrini","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Opisthorchis viverrini</strong></p><p><em><strong>Opisthorchis viverrini </strong></em>(liver fluke) is implicated in the pathogenesis of cholangiocarcinoma.</p>\n<p>Apart from Opisthorchis viverrini, <em><strong>Clonorchis sinensis </strong></em>is also known to cause cholangiocarcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4181","text":"Which of the following is ‌the least radiosensitive ‍cell in blood?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the least radiosensitive cell in blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌BC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌ophil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymph‌ocyte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Platelets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Platelets</strong></p><p>The <strong>least radiosensitive </strong>cell in the <strong>blood </strong>is the <strong>platelet.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>most radiosensitive </strong>cell in the <strong>blood </strong>is the <strong>lymphocyte.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>most</strong> radiosensitive phase of the cell cycle is <strong>G2M.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>least</strong> radiosensitive is the <strong>S phase</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oQUofv0X","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"11_heart_failure_drugs","title":"11.Heart Failure Drugs","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA4740","text":"What is the half-life of d‌igoxin?","raw_text":"What is the half-life of digoxin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 ‌hrs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40 ‌hrs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"72 ‌hrs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60 ‌hrs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 40 hrs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 40 hrs</strong></p><p>The plasma <strong>half-life of digoxin</strong> is around <strong>40 hours</strong> (36-40 hours).</p>\n<table style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Digitoxin </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Digoxin </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">Oral absorption </td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Very good (90-100%) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Good (60-80%)  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">Plasma protein binding  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">95%(t<sub>1/2</sub> 5-7 days) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">25%(t<sub>1/2</sub> 40 hrs) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">Elimination </td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Hepatic metabolism </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Renal excretion (by glomerular filtration) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">Dose adjustment in </td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Liver failure </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Kidney failure </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Therapeutic levels of digoxin in the range of 0.5-0.8 ng/ml are beneficial. An increase in levels &gt;1.2 ng/mL is associated with increased mortality.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4714","text":"Digitalis inhibits Na+/ K+‌ ATPase pump in cardiac m​uscle cell membrane. How ‌does this lead to its pos‌itive inotropic effect?","raw_text":"Digitalis inhibits Na+/ K+ ATPase pump in cardiac muscle cell membrane. How does this lead to its positive inotropic effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"By decreasing activity of ‌Na+/Ca++ exchanger causin​g decreased influx of sod‌ium and decreased efflux ‌of Ca++","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"By decreasing efflux of Na‌+ leading to less negativ​e resting membrane potent‌ial and opening of voltag‌e gated Ca++ channels on ​the T tubules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"By increasing intracellula‌r Na+ causing increased e​fflux of Na+ and increase‌d influx of Ca++ through ‌Na+/Ca++ exchanger","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"By increasing intracellula‌r Na+ decreasing the acti​vity of Ca++ pump in the ‌sarcoplasmic reticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. By decreasing activity of Na+/Ca++ exchanger causing decreased influx of sodium and decreased efflux of Ca++","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32226b02e4fb4015ac8795d22c1a6cf6x1280x1527.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. By decreasing activity of Na+/Ca++ exchanger causing decreased influx of sodium and decreased efflux of Ca++</strong></p><p>The positive ionotropic effect of digitalis is due to <strong>decreased</strong> activity of <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/Ca<sup>2+</sup> exchanger</strong> causing a decreased influx of sodium and decreased efflux of Ca<sup>2+</sup>.</p>\n<p>Digitalis selectively binds and<strong> inhibits</strong> the<strong> Na<sup>+</sup>/ K<sup>+</sup>ATPase pump </strong>of myocardial fibers which<strong> </strong>normally pushes Na<sup>+</sup> outside and brings K<sup>+</sup> inside. When this pump is inhibited, there is an <strong>intracellular accumulation of Na<sup>+</sup></strong> ions. This results in a relative <strong>decrease </strong>in the <strong>expulsion of Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> ions via the Na<sup>+</sup>-Ca<sup>2+</sup> exchanger (NCX) leading to the accumulation of calcium inside the cells resulting in <strong>increased contractility.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4735","text":"The action of digoxin will‌ not be affected in which​ of the following patient‌s?","raw_text":"The action of digoxin will not be affected in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"43-year-old man with chron‌ic liver disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"30-year-old woman with ele‌ctrolyte disturbances","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"82-year-old man with renal‌ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60-year-old woman with myo‌cardial infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 43-year-old man with chronic liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 43-year-old man with chronic liver disease</strong></p><p>The action of digoxin is not affected in chronic liver disease. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Electrolyte disturbances</strong> like hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypercalcemia <strong>increase digitalis toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Digoxin is<strong> excreted </strong>unchanged primarily by<strong> kidneys</strong> by glomerular filtration. Therefore, <strong>dose adjustment</strong> of digoxin has to be done in <strong>renal failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Chances of arrhythmias increases manifold in post-MI duration. Hence, it is generally avoided post-MI as <strong>digitalis</strong> itself <strong>causes arrhythmias</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 291px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\"><strong>Digitoxin </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\"><strong>Digoxin </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Oral absorption </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">Very good (90-100%) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">Good ( 60-80%) i/v also </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Plasma protein binding  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">95% </td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">25% </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Elimination </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">Hepatic metabolism </td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">Renal excretion (by glomerular filtration) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Dose adjustment in </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">Liver failure </td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">Kidney failure </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4715","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not increase the risk o​f digoxin toxicity?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not increase the risk of digoxin toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal im‌pairment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypomag‌nesemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p>Digoxin toxicity is not increased by hyperkalemia.</p>\n<p>In hyperkalemia, the ATPase alpha subunit is <strong>dephosphorylated</strong>, which is the binding site of digoxin. This <strong>reduces</strong> it's <strong>binding</strong> and effect. Therefore, hyperkalemia does not increase digoxin toxicity.</p>\n<p>Factors predisposing digoxin toxicity<strong>:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypokalemia (more digoxin binds to the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> ATPase)</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia (increases the risk of arrhythmia)</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia<strong> </strong>(Mg<sup>++</sup> is a co-factor for Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> ATPase)</li>\n<li>Renal failure (for digoxin)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4724","text":"A patient is to be started‌ on digoxin therapy for h​eart failure. Despite alr‌eady being on another dru‌g, dose adjustment of dig​oxin is not necessary for‍ this patient. Identify t​his drug.","raw_text":"A patient is to be started on digoxin therapy for heart failure. Despite already being on another drug, dose adjustment of digoxin is not necessary for this patient. Identify this drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quin‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capt‌opril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levosi‌mendan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Levosimendan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Levosimendan</strong></p><p><strong>Levosimendan</strong> does not increase digitalis toxicity and hence would not require dose adjustment of digoxin.</p>\n<p>It is a combined Ca2+ channel sensitizer (troponin C binding) and PDE3 inhibitor. </p>\n<p><strong>Digoxin</strong> is <strong>metabolized</strong> by <strong>P-glycoprotein </strong>and drugs that inhibit it increase the plasma levels of digoxin and may result in toxicity. The drugs that increase digoxin toxicity are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Quinidine</li>\n<li>Propafenone</li>\n<li>Verapamil</li>\n<li>Captopril</li>\n<li>Erythromycin</li>\n<li>Gentamycin</li>\n<li>Ranolazine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4741","text":"What is the most appropria‌te treatment for a patien​t with severe digoxin tox‌icity?","raw_text":"What is the most appropriate treatment for a patient with severe digoxin toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Potassium ‌supplements","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Propa‌fenone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quin‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fab fragments of digoxin a‌ntibodies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fab fragments of digoxin antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fab fragments of digoxin antibodies</strong></p><p>The most appropriate treatment for severe digoxin toxicity is<strong> purified Fab fragments of digitalis antibodies </strong>(Digibind).</p>\n<p>It <strong>neutralizes the digoxin action</strong> and its dosage is based upon the total dose of digoxin ingested.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>KCl infusion</strong> is indicated in <strong>mild toxicity</strong> as hyperkalemia decreases the binding of digoxin by dephosphorylating Na/K ATPase pump. In acute severe poisoning, potassium is not indicated as it may already be high in plasma. Potassium is also not indicated when a higher degree of AV block is present.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: Quinidine and propafenone are not indicated in arrhythmias caused by digoxin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4731","text":"An ECG is done for a patie‌nt suspected to have digo​xin toxicity. Which of th‌e following would you not‌ expect to see on the ECG​?","raw_text":"An ECG is done for a patient suspected to have digoxin toxicity. Which of the following would you not expect to see on the ECG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricula‌r bigeminy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paroxysmal atrial tachycar‌dia with fast ventricular​ rate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Regularisation of atrial f‌ibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bidirectional ventricular ‌tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with fast ventricular rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with fast ventricular rate</strong></p><p><strong>Digoxin </strong>can cause either paroxysmal or non-paroxysmal<strong> atrial tachycardia with variable A-V block </strong>but not a fast ventricular rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Regularisation of atrial fibrillation</strong> with complete A-V block and junctional or ventricular escape rhythm can occur because of the blockade of A-V conduction. Due to the <strong>blockade of AV node by digoxin</strong>, it is contraindicated in WPW syndrome.</p>\n<p>Common arrhythmias associated with digoxin toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ventricular bigeminy</strong></li>\n<li>Atrioventricular junctional rhythm</li>\n<li><strong>Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia</strong></li>\n<li>Premature ventricular depolarizations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4742","text":"A patient with heart disea‌se presents to the emerge​ncy with severe vomiting ‌and blurry vision and men‌tions that he sees a gree​nish-yellow tinge over al‍l objects. He then develo​ps ventricular arrhythmia​. What would be the most ‌appropriate drug for imme‌diate management?","raw_text":"A patient with heart disease presents to the emergency with severe vomiting and blurry vision and mentions that he sees a greenish-yellow tinge over all objects. He then develops ventricular arrhythmia. What would be the most appropriate drug for immediate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Procai‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligno‌caine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lignocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lignocaine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>digoxin toxicity. Xanthopsia </strong>i.e.,<strong> </strong>yellowing of vision is characteristically seen in this condition. <strong>Lignocaine </strong>(and phenytoin) has the least effect on AV nodal conduction and is the most appropriate drug for the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>digoxin-induced arrhythmias.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Procainamide</strong> and other class IA anti-arrhythmic agents are <strong>not indicated</strong> in digoxin toxicity because of their inhibitory effects on AV nodal conduction.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Atropine</strong> is used for <strong>atrial bradyarrhythmias</strong> associated with digoxin toxicity.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Propranolol</strong> is used for <strong>atrial tachyarrhythmias</strong> associated with digoxin toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4793","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h congestive heart failur​e. Which of the following‌ is not prescribed to pre‌vent progression and reve​rt cardiac remodelling?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with congestive heart failure. Which of the following is not prescribed to prevent progression and revert cardiac remodelling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‌locker","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACE in‌hibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aldosterone‌ antagonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Digoxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e588cc951734db299c6dc05ac5ec75bx1280x1301.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Digoxin</strong></p><p><strong>Digoxin</strong> has no role in preventing cardiac remodelling. It increases cardiac contractility and is indicated for <strong>symptomatic management</strong> of congestive heart failure.</p>\n<p>Drugs preventing ventricular remodelling:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACE inhibitors/ ARBS</li>\n<li>Beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The activation of RAAS and the sympathetic nervous system leads to the remodelling and progressive LV dysfunction. The RAAS activation is prevented by the ACE inhibitors/ARBs and the aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone), while the overactive sympathetic system is inhibited by the beta-blockers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4757","text":"You are starting a treatme‌nt regimen containing an ​angiotensin-converting en‌zyme inhibitor for a pati‌ent with congestive heart​ failure. Which of the fo‍llowing is true regarding​ its role in the regimen?​","raw_text":"You are starting a treatment regimen containing an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor for a patient with congestive heart failure.  Which of the following is true regarding its role in the regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the drug of choice u‌nless contraindicated","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Used as an alternative to ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a substitute for dig‌italis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used as adjuncts only in r‌esistant cases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is the drug of choice unless contraindicated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is the drug of choice unless contraindicated</strong></p><p><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong>and<strong> ARBs </strong>are<strong> </strong>the<strong> first choice</strong> drugs in congestive heart failure (CHF) unless contraindicated.</p>\n<p>Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) activation is pivotal in the development of the symptoms and disease progression in CHF.</p>\n<p>ACE inhibitors and ARBs afford symptomatic as well as disease-modifying benefits in CHF by causing vasodilation, retarding/preventing ventricular hypertrophy, myocardial cell apoptosis and fibrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4798","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can overcome an increa​se in heart rate and reni‌n release seen in patient‌s with CHF?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can overcome an increase in heart rate and renin release seen in patients with CHF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mino‌xidil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metop‌rolol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metol‌azone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Milr‌inone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metoprolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metoprolol</strong></p><p><strong>Metoprolol</strong> is a <strong>selective β1</strong> <strong>blocker.</strong> Beta blockade reduces the sympathetic stimulation of the SA node and thus <strong>reduces</strong> the <strong>heart rate</strong>. In the same way, β<sub>1</sub> receptors in the <strong>JG apparatus</strong> are also inhibited by metoprolol, and thus, <strong>renin release</strong> is <strong>reduced</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Minoxidil is a K<sup>+</sup> channel opener that was earlier used in hypertensive emergencies for its vasodilatory action on blood vessels. This has no effect on renin release and heart rate can increase due to reflex sympathetic stimulation.</p>\n<p>Option C: Metolazone is a thiazide diuretic that can be used even in severe renal failure.</p>\n<p>Option D: Milrinone is an inodilator<strong>.</strong> It increases cardiac contractility and causes vasodilation by inhibiting the enzyme PDE3.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4765","text":"You see the cardiologist p‌rescribe a non-selective ​beta-blocker for a patien‌t with congestive heart f‌ailure. He mentions that ​the drug has several bene‍ficial effects. Identify ​the drug.","raw_text":"You see the cardiologist prescribe a non-selective beta-blocker for a patient with congestive heart failure. He mentions that the drug has several beneficial effects. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pind‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metop‌rolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carve‌dilol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carvedilol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carvedilol</strong></p><p>The drug most likely being discussed is <strong>carvedilol</strong> which is a<strong> non-selective beta-blocker </strong>approved for use in<strong> mild to severe CHF</strong>.</p>\n<p>Additional actions of carvedilol:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blockade of alpha receptors</li>\n<li>Blockade of L-type calcium channels </li>\n<li>Antioxidant effect</li>\n<li>Antiproliferative effect</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B:<strong> Propranolol and pindolol</strong> are non-selective beta-blockers but they are <strong>not approved</strong> for use in CHF.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Metoprolol</strong> is approved for use in CHF but it is a <strong>cardioselective</strong> beta-blocker.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4774","text":"β blocker therapy is to be‌ started on a patient wit​h congestive heart failur‌e. Which of the following‌ statements is false abou​t the therapy?","raw_text":"β blocker therapy is to be started on a patient with congestive heart failure. Which of the following statements is false about the therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The drugs should be starte‌d at optimum doses","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The dose should be gradual‌ly increased over weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Special precautions should‌ be taken in cases of NYH​A class III and IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carvedilol and metoprolol ‌are the preferred drugs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The drugs should be started at optimum doses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The drugs should be started at optimum doses</strong></p><p>Beta-blockers are not started with optimum doses in patients with heart failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Beta-blocker</strong> therapy in <strong>CHF</strong> requires <strong>caution,</strong> proper patient selection, and observance of several guidelines such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Generally, the starting dose is less than 1/8th (low dose) of the target dose is and then <strong>titrated upwards slowly over weeks</strong> (doubling every 4 weeks)</li>\n<li>The greatest utility of beta-blocker therapy has been seen in mild to moderate CHF (NYHA I, II)</li>\n<li>There is no place for beta-blockers in decompensated heart disease</li>\n<li><strong>Carvedilol</strong>, bisoprolol, and <strong>metoprolol</strong> are commonly used beta-blockers in CHF.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4727","text":"You are going through the ‌treatment chart of a pati​ent with chronic congesti‌ve heart failure. Which o‌f the following drugs wil​l you not expect to see?","raw_text":"You are going through the treatment chart of a patient with chronic congestive heart failure. Which of the following drugs will you not expect to see?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ivabr‌adine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adrenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adrenaline</strong></p><p><strong>Adrenaline </strong>is used for the treatment of<strong> acute decompensated heart failure</strong>, not chronic congestive heart failure.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Drugs used in chronic congestive heart failure include :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diuretics</li>\n<li>ACE inhibitors</li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor blockers</li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor - sacubitril-valsartan</li>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Nitrates</li>\n<li><strong>Digoxin </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ivabradine </strong>- sinus node inhibitor </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2755","text":"A 70-year-old man is broug‌ht with complaints of sev​ere chest pain. The ECG s‌hows anterolateral wall M‌I and echocardiography sh​ows an ejection fraction ‍of 38%. Which of the foll​owing is the drug of choi​ce for his condition?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man is brought with complaints of severe chest pain. The ECG shows anterolateral wall MI and echocardiography shows an ejection fraction of 38%. Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dobut‌amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Norpepi‌nephrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dobutamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dobutamine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> acute congestive heart failure with systolic dysfunction. Dobutamine </strong>is administered as it<strong> </strong>is the<strong> beta-adrenergic agonist of choice </strong>for managing this condition.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Dobutamine has a well-balanced action with stimulation of cardiac output. It causes less tachycardia than epinephrine and also has an added advantage of a decrease in pulmonary artery wedge pressure. </p>\n<p>At doses that result in a positive inotropic effect, the beta-1 action on the myocardium predominates. In the vasculature, the alpha-1 effects are counterbalanced by beta-2 effects. The <strong>net result</strong> is an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>stroke volume</strong>, augmented by a small decrease in systemic vascular resistance, thereby resulting in a <strong>decrease in afterload</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4786","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about nesiritide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about nesiritide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a BN‌P analogue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used in decompen‌sated CHF","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be administered ora‌lly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It causes loss of Na+ in t‌he urine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It can be administered orally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It can be administered orally</strong></p><p><strong>Nesiritide</strong> is available for <strong>intravenous administration only </strong>as it is a peptide and is digested by peptidases in the GIT. </p>\n<p>Nesiritide is a<strong> recombinant brain natriuretic peptide (BNP).</strong> It causes <strong>natriuresis</strong> and <strong>vasodilation</strong>. It has a half life of 18 minutes. It is approved to relieve dyspnoea and other symptoms in <strong>acute refractory decompensated CHF.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4788","text":"Which enzyme degrades BNP?‌","raw_text":"Which enzyme degrades BNP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutral en‌dopeptidase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Elas‌tase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudochol‌inesterase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carboxyp‌eptidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neutral endopeptidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neutral endopeptidase</strong></p><p><strong>Brain natriuretic peptide </strong>(BNP) is degraded by<strong> neutral endopeptidase.</strong></p>\n<p>Natriuretic peptides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)</li>\n<li>Brain natriuretic peptide</li>\n<li>C-type natriuretic peptide</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nesiritide</strong> is a recombinant BNP<strong> </strong>approved to relieve dyspnoea and other symptoms in <strong>acute refractory decompensated CHF.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2775","text":"Isosorbide dinitrate is in‌dicated for congestive he​art failure in combinatio‌n with which of the follo‌wing vasodilators?","raw_text":"Isosorbide dinitrate is indicated for congestive heart failure in combination with which of the following vasodilators?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mino‌xidil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydra‌lazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nimod‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitren‌dipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hydralazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hydralazine</strong></p><p><strong>Isosorbide dinitrate </strong>(ISDN) is indicated in combination <strong>with hydralazine for CHF. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hydralazine</strong> is a <strong>direct vasodilator</strong> and its mechanism of action is not known. It also reduces nitrate tolerance.</p>\n<p>The combination of ISDN and hydralazine showed mortality benefits in a specific group of population (African-Americans).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4795","text":"A patient with congestive ‌heart failure develops hy​ponatremia. Which oral va‌sopressin receptor antago‌nist will you prescribe f​or him?","raw_text":"A patient with congestive heart failure develops hyponatremia. Which oral vasopressin receptor antagonist will you prescribe for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolv‌aptan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coniv‌aptan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sacub‌itril","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tolvaptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tolvaptan</strong></p><p><strong>Tolvaptan</strong> is an<strong> oral vasopressin receptor antagonist. </strong></p>\n<p>It preferentially binds to V2 receptors over V1a receptors. It has been used in decompensated CHF patients to restore normal sodium levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Conivaptan</strong> is also a vasopressin receptor antagonist that is administered <strong>intravenously</strong> and binds to both V2 and V1a receptors with similar affinity. It is more commonly used for hyponatremia rather than CHF. Oral conivaptan is not yet available.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4796","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of milrinone?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of milrinone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulati‌on of PDE3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibitio‌n of PDE3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibitio‌n of PDE5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulati‌on of PDE5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibition of PDE3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibition of PDE3</strong></p><p><strong>Milrinone</strong> acts by<strong> inhibition </strong>of the enzyme phosphodiesterase-3 (<strong>PDE 3</strong>).</p>\n<p>The PDE-3 inhibitors decrease cellular cAMP degradation, resulting in <strong>increased</strong> levels of <strong>cAMP</strong>. This results in positive ionotropic and chronotropic action of the heart. It also dilates veins and arteries thereby decreasing the preload and afterload.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2756","text":"A trial is being conducted‌ to determine the efficac​y of a myofilament calciu‌m sensitizer in patients ‌with heart failure. Which​ among the following is t‍he drug likely being stud​ied?","raw_text":"A trial is being conducted to determine the efficacy of a myofilament calcium sensitizer in patients with heart failure. Which among the following is the drug likely being studied?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levosi‌mendan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Omapa‌trilat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sacub‌itril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enox‌imone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Levosimendan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Levosimendan</strong></p><p><strong>Levosimendan </strong>is a myofilament <strong>calcium senstizer. </strong></p>\n<p>Calcium sensitizers bind and induce a conformational change in thin-filament regulatory protein troponin C. Thus increasing the sensitivity of contractile myofilaments to calcium. The result is an <strong>increased force of contraction</strong> for a given cytosolic calcium concentration. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Omapatrilat</strong> is a <strong>dual ACE </strong>and<strong> neprilysin inhibitor</strong> that failed clinical trials due to increased risk of angioedema. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Sacubitril</strong> is a <strong>neprilysin inhibitor</strong> approved in combination with valsartan for CHF. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Enoximone</strong> is a <strong>PDE-3 inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3722","text":"Sacubitril is a new drug a‌pproved for chronic heart​ failure. The mechanism o‌f action of sacubitril is‌:","raw_text":"Sacubitril is a new drug approved for chronic heart failure. The mechanism of action of sacubitril is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angiotensin ‌II inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACE in‌hibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renin i‌nhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutral endopeptidase inhi‌bitor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neutral endopeptidase inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neutral endopeptidase inhibitor</strong></p><p><strong>Sacubitril</strong> is a neutral endopeptidase <strong>(neprilysin) inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>Sacubitril is a prodrug that is activated to <strong>sacubitrilat.</strong> It can inhibit the enzyme neprilysin (neutral endopeptidase), which is responsible for the degradation of atrial and brain natriuretic peptides (reduction of blood volume). Therefore, it <strong>increases</strong> the levels of <strong>atrial and brain natriuretic peptides</strong> and <strong>lowers blood pressure.</strong></p>\n<div>In 2015, the FDA approved the use of sacubitril in <strong>combination</strong> with<strong> valsartan</strong>, at a ratio of <strong>1:1</strong>, for the treatment of chronic heart failure.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0132","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used in heart failure​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used in heart failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trimet‌azidine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sacub‌itril","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metop‌rolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nesir‌itide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trimetazidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trimetazidine</strong></p><p><strong>Trimetazidine</strong> is not used in heart failure.</p>\n<p>Trimetazidine is a newer antianginal drug that is a <strong>pFOX inhibitor</strong> which partially inhibits the fatty acid oxidation pathway in the myocardium. This <strong>decreases the oxygen requirement</strong> of the heart.</p>\n<p>Nesiritide (natriuretic peptide), sacubitril (neprilysin inhibitor) and metoprolol (beta-blocker) are used in the treatment of heart failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"T_idJwxI","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"11_sensory_receptors","title":"11.Sensory Receptors","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB0125","text":"Spatial distribution from ​which a stimulus produces​ a response is called ___​__","raw_text":"Spatial distribution from which a stimulus produces a response is called  _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recepti​ve field","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Motor​ unit","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Derm​atome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensory​ neuron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Receptive field","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03466d0a5f9a4efbacd9655acd4b8af8x775x718.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Receptive field</strong></p><p>Spatial distribution from which a stimulus produces a response is called receptive field.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensory Unit</strong>:<strong> </strong>A single afferent neuron with all its receptor endings.</p>\n<p><strong>Motor unit</strong>:<strong> </strong>Each single motor neuron and the muscle fibers it innervates.</p>\n<p><strong>Dermatome</strong>:<strong> </strong>Each spinal nerve innervates a “segmental field” of the skin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0124","text":"Fast vibration sense is me​diated by ______.","raw_text":"Fast vibration sense is mediated by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Merkel​’s disc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meissner's​ corpuscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pacinian ​corpuscles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ruffini’s​ end organ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pacinian corpuscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4e445b451e14227974893e3bef26d88x1031x806.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pacinian corpuscles</strong></p><p>Fast vibration sense is mediated by Pacinian corpuscles. </p>\n<p>Pacinian corpuscle is the<strong> most sensitive mechanoreceptor</strong> in the somatosensory system. It is responsive to <strong>high-frequency</strong> (30–500 Hz) vibratory stimuli and pressure. They are rapidly adapting receptors.</p>\n<p>Option A: Merkel's disc sense <strong>touch and sustained pressure</strong>. They are slow adapting receptors. Present in <strong>epidermis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Meissner's corpuscle sense <strong>tap and flutter</strong>. They are present in <strong>dermal papillae</strong>. They are rapidly adapting receptors. </p>\n<p>Option D: Ruffini endings sense skin <strong>stretch and fluttering vibration.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows Merkel's disc and Meissner's corpuscle being more <strong>superficial</strong> (reponsive to <strong>touch</strong>) than Ruffini endings and Pacinian corpuscle (which are <strong>deeper</strong> in the dermis and responsive to <strong>pressure</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0130","text":"The phasic receptor is____​__","raw_text":"The phasic receptor is______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Merkel​'s disc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ruffini's ​end organs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meissner's​ corpuscles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle ​spindle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meissner's corpuscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meissner&#x27;s corpuscles</strong></p><p>Meissner's corpuscles are phasic (rapidly adapting) receptors.</p>\n<p>Merkel's disc, Ruffini's end organs, and muscle spindle are tonic (slowly adapting) receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0135","text":"You are doing a CNS examin​ation of a patient with a​ spinal injury. At which ​of the following sites wo‌uld the distance of two-p‍oint discrimination be gr‌eatest?","raw_text":"You are doing a CNS examination of a patient with a spinal injury. At which of the following sites would the distance of two-point discrimination be greatest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L​ip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palm of ​the hand","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Back of​ scapula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsum ​of hand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Back of scapula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Back of scapula</strong></p><p>The <strong>distance</strong> of <strong>two-point discrimination</strong> is <strong>greatest</strong> at the <strong>back</strong> of the <strong>scapula.</strong> </p>\n<p>The relative tactile acuity of a given region can be determined by the<strong> two-point discrimination test</strong>. Here, calipers are used to determine the minimum distance between the two points which can be perceived as two points. Greater the density of receptors → Smaller the receptive field → More precise the localization.</p>\n<p>For e.g., Stimulus points on the back, for instance, must be separated by at least 65 mm before they can be distinguished as separate, whereas on the fingertips two stimuli are recognized if they are separated by as little as 2 mm. The lips have the greatest acuity (two-point discrimination threshold ~ 1 mm).</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance: </strong></p>\n<p>Blind individuals benefit from the tactile acuity of fingertips to facilitate the ability to read Braille. The dots forming<strong> Braille</strong> symbols are separated by <strong>2.5 mm. </strong></p>\n<p>Two-point discrimination is used <strong>to test the integrity of the dorsal column</strong> (medial lemniscus) system.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6863","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the nature of ​the sensory receptor give​n below:","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about  the nature of the sensory receptor given below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slow adapting and not enca​psulated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fast adapting and encapsul​ated","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fast adapting and not enca​psulated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slow adapting and encapsul​ated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fast adapting and encapsulated","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f81c32dbcc3416c924538167be7ea14.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fast adapting and encapsulated</strong></p><p>The sensory receptor shown in the above image is Pacinian corpuscle. The Pacinian corpuscle is a rapidly adapting receptor and is an encapsulated receptor.</p>\n<p>When a stimulus of the same strength is continuously given to a sensory receptor, the frequency of the action potentials generated by the receptor gradually decreases. This phenomenon is known as <strong>receptor</strong> <strong>adaptation</strong>. Depending on whether the receptor stops responding to a stimulus rapidly or slowly, they are classified as <strong>rapidly adapting</strong> (phasic) or <strong>slow</strong> <strong>adapting</strong> <strong>receptors</strong> (tonic).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0122","text":"Which of the following cla​ssification of receptors ​is the same?","raw_text":"Which of the following classification of receptors is the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mechanoreceptors and exter​oceptors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Interoceptors and nocicept​ors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Photoreceptors and proprio​ceptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Telereceptors and interoce​ptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mechanoreceptors and exteroceptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mechanoreceptors and exteroceptors</strong></p><p>Mechanoreceptors and exteroceptors have the same classification of receptors. </p>\n<p><strong>Exteroceptors </strong>are concerned with the external environment or receptors which <strong>receives external stimuli. Mechanoreceptors </strong>are cutaneous receptors for touch and pressure. <strong>Thus mechanoreceptors can be considered to be exteroceptors. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nociceptors </strong>are pain receptors which detect physical and chemical damage occuring in the tissue. </p>\n<p><strong>Interoceptors</strong> are concerned with the internal environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Chemoreceptors</strong> are receptors stimulated by a change in the chemical composition of the environment in which they are located. These include receptors for<strong> taste and smell</strong> as well as visceral receptors such as those sensitive to changes in the plasma level of O<sub>2</sub>, pH, and osmolality.</p>\n<p><strong>Teleceptors </strong>(distance receivers) are concerned with events at a distance, example - photoreceptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Proprioceptors</strong> provide information about the position of the body in space at any given instant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0128","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding sensory re​ceptor properties?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding sensory receptor properties?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnitude of sensation fel​t is directly proportiona​l to the intensity of the​ stimulus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A touch receptor will gene​rate a constant rate of d​ischarge of its axon for ​as long as the receptor i‌s stimulated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meissner’s and Pacinian co​rpuscles adapt slowly, th​e others adapt rapidly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The frequency of a train o​f action potentials in an​ afferent fibre reflects ​the intensity of the stim‌ulus given to its recepto‍r","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The frequency of a train of action potentials in an afferent fibre reflects the intensity of the stimulus given to its receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The frequency of a train of action potentials in an afferent fibre reflects the intensity of the stimulus given to its receptor</strong></p><p>The frequency of a train of action potentials in an afferent fibre reflects the intensity of the stimulus given to its receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>Intensity</strong> of stimulus applied to a sensory receptor can be determined by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"6\">Variation in <strong>frequency</strong> of action potentials generated </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"6\">Variations in the <strong>number</strong> <strong>of receptors</strong> activated </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option <strong>A</strong> : Weber-Fechner Law states that magnitude of sensation felt is directly proportional to the <strong>log of the intensity </strong>of the stimulus.</p>\n<p>Option <strong>B</strong>: A touch receptor <strong>will not</strong> generate a constant rate of discharge, even if the receptor is continuously stimulated. This is called desensitization or adaptation. If the opposite were true (if option B was correct), when we have our clothes on, we would have constant firing of neurons unnecessarily, in response to touch stimulus.</p>\n<p>Option <strong>C</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 393px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Slow</strong> <strong>adapting</strong> (Tonic receptors) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.09375px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Rapidly</strong> <strong>adapting</strong> (Phasic receptors) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; height: 100px;\">Merkel's disc, Ruffini's end organs, Joint capsule Muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organs  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.09375px; height: 100px;\">Pacinian corpuscle, Meissner's corpuscles, Hair end organs </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0143","text":"Aδ fibers do not transmit ​_____.","raw_text":"Aδ fibers do not transmit _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slow​ pain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epicrit​ic pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fast​ pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"First​ pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Slow pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Slow pain</strong></p><p>Aδ fibers do not transmit slow pain. They transmit fast pain. </p>\n<p>Activation of Aδ fibers, which release <strong>glutamate, </strong>is responsible for <strong>first pain </strong>(also called <strong>fast pain </strong>or <strong>epicritic pain</strong>), which is a rapid response and mediates the ability to localize the site and intensity of the noxious stimulus.</p>\n<p>Neuropathic pain can also be burning, dull and poorly localized (protopathic pain, transmitted by C nociceptor fibers (which release substance P). </p>\n<p><strong>Dual Pain Pathway</strong></p>\n<p>Pain can be carried by either Aδ fiber (fast pain/ epicritic pain) or by C fiber (slow pain/protopathic pain)</p>\n<table width=\"373\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"112\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>A</strong><strong>δ</strong><strong> fiber </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>C fiber </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"112\">\n<p>Stimuli</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>Mechanical</p>\n<p>Thermal</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>Polymodal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"112\">\n<p>Myelination and tract</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Myelinated</strong> and carried via <strong>Neo</strong>spinothalamic tract</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Unmyelinated</strong> and carried via <strong>paleo</strong>spinothalamic tract</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"112\">\n<p>Conduction velocity</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>12–30 m/s</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>0.5–2 m/s</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"112\">\n<p>Impulse conduction</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Fast component</strong> of pain </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Slow component</strong> of pain </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"112\">\n<p>Neurotransmitter</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Glutamate</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Substance P</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0153","text":"The most potent algogenic ​substance is ______.","raw_text":"The most potent algogenic  substance is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brady​kinin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prosta​glandin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bradykinin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bradykinin</strong></p><p><strong>Bradykinin</strong> is the most potent algogenic substance.</p>\n<p>An algogenic substance is a chemical released at the site of injury which mediates pain. These include bradykinin, serotonin, histamine, potassium ions, acids, acetylcholine, and proteolytic enzymes<em>. </em></p>\n<p>Prostaglandins and substance P enhance the sensitivity of pain endings but do not directly excite them.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: This is a controversial question</strong></p>\n<p>According to Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 13th Edition, Page 622-</p>\n<p>\"One chemical that seems to be <strong>more painful than others</strong> is bradykinin. Researchers have suggested that bradykinin might be the agent <strong>most responsible</strong> for causing pain after tissue damage.\"</p>\n<p>Hence, <strong>bradykinin</strong> is the better answer (not serotonin)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0145","text":"Vanilloid receptors are ac​tivated by all except ___​___.","raw_text":"Vanilloid receptors are activated by all except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He​at","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The​rmal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chem​icals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibr​ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vibration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vibration</strong></p><p>Vanilloid receptors are not activated by vibration.</p>\n<p>Vanilloid receptors or TRPV1 (transient receptor potential vanilloid1) receptors are a part of family of nonselective cation channels called <strong>transient receptor potential </strong>(TRP) channels. These channels respond to intense heat, acids, and chemicals such as <strong>capsaicin. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0157","text":"Heat-pain fibers are stimu​lated at temperatures aro​und _____.","raw_text":"Heat-pain fibers are stimulated at temperatures around _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30​° C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"49​° C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"45​° C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"35​° C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 45° C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 45° C</strong></p><p>Heat-pain fibers begin to be stimulated at around <strong>45 °C.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Thermal Receptors and their Excitation</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 486.734375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Cold receptor </td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734375px;\">Warm receptor </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">\n<p><strong>Peak</strong>:<strong> 24 °C</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734375px;\">\n<p><strong>Peak</strong>:<strong> 45 °C.</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">\n<p>Cold nociceptor stimulated when temperature is around <strong>5 °C</strong>.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734375px;\">\n<p>Heat nociceptor is stimulated at temperatures around <strong>45 °C</strong>.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Cold receptor fibers are <strong>Aδ and C<br/></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.734375px;\">\n<p>Warm receptor fibers are <strong>unmyelinated C fibers </strong></p>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0136","text":"Weber-Fechner law relates ​magnitude of sensation to​ ____.","raw_text":"Weber-Fechner law relates magnitude of sensation to  ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strength of muscle contrac​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Log of Intensity of stimul​us","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amplitude of action potent​ial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duration o​f stimulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Log of Intensity of stimulus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Log of Intensity of stimulus</strong></p><p><strong>Weber-Fechner Law </strong>states that the magnitude of sensation felt is proportionate to the <strong>log of the intensity of the stimulus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Other Laws in Physiology:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Doctrine of specific nerve energies </strong>- When the nerve pathways from a particular sense organ are stimulated, the sensation evoked is that for which the receptor is specialized no matter how or where along the pathway the activity is initiated.</li>\n<li><strong>Law of projection </strong>- No matter where a particular sensory pathway is stimulated along its course to the cortex, the conscious sensation produced is referred to the location of the receptor. This principle is called the law of projection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0139","text":"A patient's right leg was ​amputated because it was ​unsalvageable after the m​otor vehicle accident. Af‌ter 2 weeks of her surger‍y, she started feeling ep‌isodes of severe pain in ‌the amputated area. This ‌pain is most likely based‌ on which of the followin‍g mechanisms?","raw_text":"A patient's right leg was amputated because it was unsalvageable after the motor vehicle accident. After 2 weeks of her surgery, she started feeling episodes of severe pain in the amputated area. This pain is most likely based on which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muller’s specific nerve en​ergy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Principle of​ labeled line","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cortical ​plasticity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Weber–Fe​chner law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cortical plasticity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cortical plasticity</strong></p><p>This patient is having <strong>phantom limb pain.</strong> This pain is based on<strong> cortical plasticity.</strong></p>\n<p>Cortical structures are capable of <strong>reorganization after</strong> the<strong> loss</strong> of a<strong> body part.</strong> The cortical <strong>representation</strong> of the <strong>neighboring parts spreads</strong> into the<strong> cortical area</strong> that was formerly occupied by the representation of the amputated part. This is called cortical plasticity leads to the phenomenon of phantom limb pain</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0149","text":"Perception of normal senso​ry stimuli as painful is ​________","raw_text":"Perception of normal sensory stimuli as painful is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allo​dynia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypera​lgesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anal​gesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caus​algia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Allodynia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Allodynia</strong></p><p>Perception of normal sensory stimuli as painful is allodynia.</p>\n<p>Hyperalgesia is an<strong> exaggerated response</strong> to a noxious stimulus. An example of primary hyperalgesia is the extreme sensitivity of sunburned skin, which results from sensitization of the skin pain endings<strong> </strong>by local tissue products released due to burns — histamine, prostaglandins, and others.</p>\n<p>Causalgia is a type of <strong>neuropathic pain.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9719","text":"Which of the following is ​true about anandamide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about anandamide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is not produced endogen​ously","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a noci​ceptive agent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There are anandamide-conta​ining neurons in the peri​aqueductal gray","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It binds to opioid recepto​rs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There are anandamide-containing neurons in the periaqueductal gray","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There are anandamide-containing neurons in the periaqueductal gray</strong></p><p>There are anandamide-containing neurons in the <strong>periaqueductal gray</strong> and other areas concerned with pain.</p>\n<p>There are 2 types of <strong>endogenous</strong> cannabinoids: 2-arachidonyl glycerol and Anandamide. They bind to <strong>CB1 </strong>and<strong> CB2</strong> receptors. They have an <strong>anti-nociceptive</strong> effect.</p>\n<p>Descending pathways from the mesencephalic periaqueductal gray inhibit transmission in nociceptive pathways. This descending pathway includes a synapse in the ventromedial medulla (Raphe nucleus) and the release of endogenous opiates. So periaqueductal gray matter releases endogenous opiates.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0154","text":"Which of the following is ​true about visceral pain?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about visceral pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is poorl​y localised","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It shows rap​id adaptation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is mediated by B fibers​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It resemble​s fast pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is poorly localised","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is poorly localised</strong></p><p>Visceral pain is poorly localized, unpleasant and associated with nausea and autonomic symptoms. It often originates from thoracic and abdominal cavities, transmitted through small type C pain fibers. </p>\n<p>It often radiates (or is referred) to other somatic structures perhaps due to <strong>convergence of somatic and visceral nociceptive afferent fibers</strong> on the same second-order neurons in the spinal dorsal horn that project to the thalamus and then to the primary somatosensory cortex.</p>\n<p>When pain is referred, it is usually to a structure that developed from the <strong>same embryonic segment</strong> or <strong>dermatome</strong> as the structure in which the pain originates. This principle is called the <strong>dermatomal rule</strong>. </p>\n<p>Example: the heart and the arm have the same segmental origin.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 408px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.765625px;\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.234375px;\">\n<p><strong>A</strong><strong>δ</strong><strong> fiber </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p><strong>C fiber </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.765625px;\">\n<p>Stimuli</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.234375px;\">\n<p>Mechanical</p>\n<p>Thermal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p>Polymodal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.765625px;\">\n<p>Myelination and tract</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.234375px;\">\n<p><strong>Myelinated</strong> and carried via <strong>Neo</strong>spinothalamic tract</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p><strong>Unmyelinated</strong> and carried via <strong>paleo</strong>spinothalamic tract</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.765625px;\">\n<p>Conduction velocity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.234375px;\">\n<p>12–30 m/s</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p>0.5–2 m/s</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.765625px;\">\n<p>Impulse conduction</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.234375px;\">\n<p><strong>Fast component</strong> of pain </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p><strong>Slow component</strong> of pain </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.765625px;\">\n<p>Neurotransmitter</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.234375px;\">\n<p><strong>Glutamate</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p><strong>Substance P</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0156","text":"Pain insensitive structure​ is _____.","raw_text":"Pain insensitive structure is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alv​eoli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Liver ​capsule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parieta​l pleura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arteri​al wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alveoli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alveoli</strong></p><p>Alveoli are pain insensitive structures. <strong>Alveoli of lungs and parenchyma of liver</strong> are almost completely<strong> insensitive</strong> to the pain of any type.</p>\n<p>However, the<strong> liver capsule</strong> is extremely <strong>sensitive</strong> to both direct trauma and stretch. The <strong>bile ducts, arterial walls, bronchi and the parietal pleura</strong> are very sensitive to pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9722","text":"A patient with chronic pai​n is undergoing transcuta​neous electrical nerve st​imulation for pain relief‌. What is the basis of th‍is procedure?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic pain is undergoing transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation for pain relief. What is the basis of this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Referr​ed pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gate theo​ry of pain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Allo​dynia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pain relief ​by analgesics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gate theory of pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/281b96d6c693477fa7222786082115e1x1280x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gate theory of pain</strong></p><p><strong>Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)</strong> of pain relief is based on the <strong>gate theory of pain</strong>. This method uses electrodes to activate Aα and Aβ fibers near the injury to relieve pain. </p>\n<p>Gate control theory operates at the <strong>substantia gelatinosa</strong> of the <strong>dorsal horn </strong>of the <strong>spinal cord</strong>.</p>\n<p>The relief may result from inhibition of pain pathways (Inhibition of C fibers and A-delta fibers) in the dorsal horn gate, by stimulation of large-diameter touch-pressure afferents(A-beta fibers). This is called the gate-control hypothesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Interesting clinical application</strong>: Touching or shaking an injured area decreases the pain due to the injury. Stimulation with an electric vibrator at the site of pain also gives some relief.</p>\n<p>The same mechanism is probably responsible for the efficacy of counterirritants. Stimulation of the skin over an area of visceral inflammation produces some relief of the pain due to the visceral disease. The old-fashioned mustard plaster works on this principle. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0152","text":"Primary cutaneous hyperalg​esia_____","raw_text":"Primary cutaneous hyperalgesia_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Develops in the normal ski​n region around the area ​of flare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Is an abnormal condition i​n the skin in which painf​ul stimuli become less se​vere","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is due to changes in thres​hold of pain receptors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Is associated with throbbi​ng type of pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Is due to changes in threshold of pain receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Is due to changes in threshold of pain receptors</strong></p><p>Hyperalgesia is an exaggerated response to a noxious stimulus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperalgesia and allodynia signify increased sensitivity of nociceptive afferent fibers.</li>\n<li><strong>Causes of hyperalgesia</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The excessive sensitivity of the pain receptors, which is called <strong>primary hyperalgesia </strong></li>\n<li>Facilitation of sensory transmission, which is called <strong>secondary hyperalgesia.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An example of primary hyperalgesia is the extreme sensitivity of sunburned skin, which results from sensitization of the skin pain endings, by local tissue products from the burn — histamine, prostaglandins, and others.</p>\n<p>Secondary hyperalgesia frequently results from lesions in the spinal cord or the thalamus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0146","text":"The transient receptor pot​ential (TRP) channel that​ is most active at normal​ skin temperature is ____‌___.","raw_text":"The transient receptor potential (TRP) channel that is most active at normal skin temperature is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TR​PV4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TR​PV3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TR​PM8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TR​PA1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. TRPV3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. TRPV3</strong></p><p>At normal skin temperatures, <strong>TRPV3</strong> is the most active<strong> </strong>receptor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0117","text":"A soldier posted in Siache​n is brought to you in th​e ER. In order to rule ou​t hypothermia, how do you‌ best measure his tempera‍ture?","raw_text":"A soldier posted in Siachen is brought to you in the ER. In order to rule out hypothermia, how do you best measure his temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or​al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rec​tal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infrared tympanic thermome​ter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axil​lary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rectal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rectal</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario <strong>rectal temperature</strong> would be the preferred site to measure the core body temperature.</p>\n<p>During low or high temperatures like in Siachen, the axillary and the oral temperatures are highly misleading and should not be used to measure the core body temperatures</p>\n<p>Infrared tympanic thermometer measures the temperature of the eardrum which is representative of core body temperature. However, more often the thermometer is not inserted adequately or for safety reasons, they are manufactured in such a way that they do not reach deep into the ear canal, or the ear canal contains cerumen which can disturb the measurement. Hence the infrared thermometer more commonly delivers the temperature of ear canal cerumen and hence <strong>not a reliable method</strong> of measuring the core body temperature.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CPlyyaC1","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"11_depressive_disorders","title":"11.Depressive Disorders","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6669","text":"According to the latest WH​O statistics, what is the‍ most common psychiatric ‍disorder in the world?","raw_text":"According to the latest  WHO statistics, what is the most common psychiatric disorder in the world?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eating ​disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depre​ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bipolar ​disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anxiety ​disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anxiety disorder</strong></p><p>According to the WHO global health statistics 2020, <strong>anxiety disorder</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> psychiatric illness in the world.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6671","text":"Which among the following ​is a component of Beck's ‍triad of depression ?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a  component of  Beck's triad of depression ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Worthl​essness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anhe​donia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low ​mood","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fatiga​bility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Worthlessness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Worthlessness</strong></p><p><strong>Worthlessness</strong> is a component of Beck's cognitive <strong>triad of depression</strong>. It is also known as the negative triad. </p>\n<p><strong>Aaron Beck's cognitive triad</strong> consists of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Negative views about one's self - A tendency to devalue to feel personally inadequate (<strong>Worthlessness</strong>)</li>\n<li>Negative views about the world - A tendency to feel that there is no help available (<strong>Helplessness</strong>)</li>\n<li>Negative views about the future - The expectation of suffering and failure (<strong>Hopelessness</strong>)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Anhedonia</strong> - Decreased ability to experience pleasure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0997","text":"A 45-year-old man continue​s to smoke despite severa‍l attempts at quitting be‍cause he believes that he‍ will always be a smoker.​ Which of the following t​heories best describes th‌is behavior?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man continues to smoke despite several attempts at quitting because he believes that he will always be a smoker. Which of the following theories best describes this behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Learned helplessness theor​y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Theory of psychosocial dev​elopment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psychodyna​mic theory","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Social lear​ning theory","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Learned helplessness theory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Learned helplessness theory</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of the <strong>learned helplessness theory </strong>of depression. The theory connects depressive phenomena to the experience of uncontrollable events. It was proposed by Martin Seligman. </p>\n<p>Another good example of this can be a situation of domestic abuse where women tend to leave several times before doing so for good. A person may believe that they can never escape the abuser, even when help and support are available.</p>\n<p>Option B: The<strong> theory of</strong> <strong>psychosocial development</strong> was proposed by Erik Erikson. There are <strong>8 stages</strong> of psychosocial <strong>development.</strong> He states that there are critical time periods, each of which poses a particular <strong>psychosocial conflict</strong> to the individual. The resolution of this conflict results in either psychosocial growth or regression.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Psychodynamic theory of development </strong>explains personality in terms of the interaction between the conscious and unconscious forces of <strong>Id, ego, and super-ego.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Social learning theory</strong> is a theory of learning process and social behavior which proposes that new behaviors can be acquired by observing and imitating others.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6670","text":"A 40-year-old woman is bro​ught to the psychiatry OP‍D with low mood for the p‍ast 2 weeks, reduced soci‍alization, decreased appe​tite and inability to sle​ep for the past 3 months.‌ She complains of frequen‍t backache and headache, ​with early morning awaken‌ing. Which of the followi‌ng is increased in this d​isorder?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman is brought to the psychiatry OPD with low mood for the past 2 weeks, reduced socialization, decreased appetite and inability to sleep for the past 3 months. She complains of frequent backache and headache, with early morning awakening. Which of the following is increased in this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cort​isol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norepin​ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cortisol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cortisol</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of low mood along with reduced appetite, reduced interest in socialization, somatic symptoms, and disturbed sleep, points towards a diagnosis of <strong>depression (major depressive disorder). Cortisol</strong> levels are <strong>increased</strong> in depression.  </p>\n<p>The diagnostic criteria for major depressive disorder are as follows:</p>\n<p>A. <strong>Five or more</strong> of the following symptoms have been present during the <strong>same 2 week period. </strong>At least<strong> one of the symptoms</strong> is either <strong>depressed mood</strong> or <strong>loss of interest</strong> or pleasure.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Depressed mood most of the day</li>\n<li>Markedly diminished interest or pleasure in activities (anhedonia)</li>\n<li>Significant weight loss (loss of more than 5% of body weight in a month) when not dieting or weight gain, decrease or increase in appetite </li>\n<li>Insomnia or hypersomnia</li>\n<li>Psychomotor agitation or retardation</li>\n<li>Fatigue or loss of energy</li>\n<li>Feelings of worthlessness or excessive or inappropriate guilt</li>\n<li>Diminished ability to think or concentrate, or indecisiveness</li>\n<li>Recurrent thoughts of death, recurrent suicidal ideation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>B. The symptoms cause clinically significant <strong>distress or impairment</strong> in social, occupa­tional, or other important areas of functioning.</p>\n<p>C. The episode is <strong>not attributable </strong>to the physiological effects of a<strong> substance</strong> or to another <strong>medical condition</strong>.</p>\n<p>D. The occurrence of the major depressive episode is not better explained by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Schizoaf­fective disorder</li>\n<li>Schizophrenia</li>\n<li>Schizophreniform disorder</li>\n<li>Delusional disorder</li>\n<li>Another specified or unspecified schizophrenia spectrum, or other psychotic disorders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>E. There has <strong>never </strong>been a <strong>manic</strong> episode or a <strong>hypomanic episode</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note<strong>: </strong>The levels of norepinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine are decreased in depression</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0960","text":"A 65-year-old retired offi​cer has been confined to ‍his house since the COVID‍-19 pandemic started in M‍arch 2020. He does not ha​ve any visitors and has l​ost touch with his family‌ as well. He has been hav‍ing persistent feelings o​f low mood and sees no pu‌rpose in his life. He som‌etimes has thoughts about​ killing himself. Which o‌f the following sleep cha​nges occur in this disord​er?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old retired officer has been confined to his house since the COVID-19 pandemic started in March 2020. He does not have any visitors and has lost touch with his family as well. He has been having persistent feelings of low mood and sees no purpose in his life. He sometimes has thoughts about killing himself. Which of the following sleep changes occur in this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased ​REM latency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in total sleep​ time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficits of slow-wave slee​p","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased rapid eye moveme​nt (REM) sleep","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deficits of slow-wave sleep","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deficits of slow-wave sleep</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>depression (major depressive disorder). </strong>It is associated with a <strong>premature loss of deep (slow-wave) sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sleep changes in depression:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A premature loss of deep (slow-wave) sleep</li>\n<li>An increase in nocturnal arousal manifested as:\n<ul>\n<li>An increase in nocturnal awakenings</li>\n<li>A <strong>reduction </strong>in<strong> total sleep</strong> time</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> phasic rapid eye movement (<strong>REM</strong>) sleep</li>\n<li>Increased core body temperature</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The combination of <strong>increased REM drive</strong> and <strong>decreased slow-wave sleep</strong> results in a significant reduction in the first period of non-REM (NREM) sleep, a phenomenon referred to as<strong> reduced REM latency</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3559","text":"Which of the following sta​tement is false regarding‍ the MRI changes seen in ‍depression?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is false regarding the MRI changes seen in depression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction in volume of cin​gulate cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compromised function in th​e prefrontal area during ‍a cognitive task","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperactivity of the amygd​ala during an emotional t‍ask","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrophy of the mesial temp​oral lobe","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atrophy of the mesial temporal lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atrophy of the mesial temporal lobe</strong></p><p><strong>Atrophy</strong> of the <strong>mesial temporal lobe</strong> particularly the hippocampus, entorhinal cortex and perirhinal cortex is an MRI change seen in <strong>Alzheimer's disease </strong>(and not depression).</p>\n<p>The MRI changes seen in depression are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduction in volume of cingulate cortex, prefrontal cortex, amygdala and hippocampus</li>\n<li>Compromised function in the prefrontal area during the implementation of a cognitive task</li>\n<li>Hyperactivity of the limbic system (including the amygdala) during an emotional task</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6680","text":"You receive a referral for​ a patient who reports a ‍feeling of extreme heavin‍ess in the arms or legs. ‍As a result, he has been ​finding it difficult to m​ove his limbs. In which o‌f the following condition‍s is this type of paralys​is seen?","raw_text":"You receive a referral for a patient who reports a feeling of extreme heaviness in the arms or legs. As a result, he has been finding it difficult to move his limbs. In which of the following conditions is this type of paralysis seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Involutional​ melancholia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical ​depression","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Psychotic ​depression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Major de​pression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atypical depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atypical depression</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>leaden paralysis </strong>and it refers to a feeling of extreme heaviness, usually in the arms or legs resulting in the difficulty of moving the limbs. It is seen in <strong>atypical depression</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Atypical depression</span><strong> </strong>is characterized by:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\">Low mood</li>\n<li>Increased sleep</li>\n<li>Increased appetite</li>\n<li>Weight gain</li>\n<li>Becomes sensitive to any comments</li>\n<li>Leaden paralysis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: <strong>Involutional melancholia</strong> is a severe depressive state seen exclusively in people between <strong>40-65 years</strong>. It is usually associated with the delusions of persecution and nihilism. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6682","text":"A 60-year-old male who los​t his wife 3 months back ‍complains that his intest‍ines have become rotten. ‍He feels that he is respo​nsible for his wife's dea​th and should be sent to ‌prison. He complains of f‍eeling low all the time a​nd has lost interest in d‌aily activities since his‌ wife's death. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male who lost his wife 3 months back complains that his intestines have become rotten. He feels that he is responsible for his wife's death and should be sent to prison. He complains of feeling low all the time and has lost interest in daily activities since his wife's death. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal gri​ef reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psychotic ​depression","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Delusiona​l disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psychotic depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psychotic depression</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of persistent low mood, anhedonia, guilt, and self-critical thought content along with <strong>nihilistic delusion</strong> (rotten intestine), points towards a diagnosis of <strong>major depression with psychotic features</strong>.</p>\n<p>Differentiating features of grief and depression have been enlisted in the following table:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 593px; width: 383px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\"><strong>Grief</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\"><strong>Depression</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">Feelings of emptyness and loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Persistent low mood and inability to anticipate pleasure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">Dysphoria comes in waves and tends to decrease in intentsity over time</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Low mood tends to be persistent and consistent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">Associated with thoughts and reminders of deceased/loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Not tied to specific thoughts and pre-occupations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">May be present along with positive emotions and humor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Pervassive unhappiness and misery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">Thought content associated with thoughts and memories of deceased/loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Thought content associated with self-critical and pessimistic ruminations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">Self-esteem is generally preserved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Worthlessness and self-loathing is common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.046875px;\">Ideas of death focused on joining the deceased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.96875px;\">Ideas of death focused on ending life due to hopelessness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Tricyclic antidepressants are considered to be the first-line drugs for psychotic depression.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5044","text":"An IT employee is feeling ​guilty, hopeless, and not‍ able to concentrate on w‍ork. His symptoms started‍ 3 years back when he ent​ered college. What is the​ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An IT employee is feeling guilty, hopeless, and not able to concentrate on work. His symptoms started 3 years back when he entered college. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depressiv​e disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dyst​hymia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adjustmen​t disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclothymi​c disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dysthymia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dysthymia</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario of guilt, hopelessness, and difficulty in concentrating over a period of <strong>more than 2 years</strong>, is suggestive of <strong>dysthymia (persistent depressive disorder)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The DSM 5 and ICD-11 criteria for diagnosis of dysthymia: </p>\n<p>A. Depressed mood for most of the day as indicated by either subjective account or observation by others, for at <strong>least 2 years</strong>. In children and adolescents at least 1 year.</p>\n<p>B. Presence of<strong> two (or more)</strong> of the following while being depressed.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Poor appetite or overeating</li>\n<li>Insomnia or hypersomnia</li>\n<li>Low energy or fatigue</li>\n<li>Low self-esteem</li>\n<li>Poor concentration and/or difficulty making decisions</li>\n<li>Feelings of hopelessness</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 212\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>C. During the 2-year period (1 year for children or adolescents) of the disturbance, the individ­ual has never been without the symptoms in criteria A and B for more than 2 months at a time.</p>\n<p>D. Criteria for a major depressive disorder may be continuously present for 2 years.</p>\n<p>E. There has never been a manic episode or a hypomanic episode, and criteria have never been met for cyclothymic disorder.</p>\n<p>F. The disturbance is not better explained by a persistent schizoaffective disorder, schizophrenia, delusional disorder, or other specified or unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.</p>\n<p>G. The symptoms are not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication) or another medical condition (e.g. hypothyroidism).</p>\n<p>H. The symptoms cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Adjustment disorder </strong>is characterized by emotional or behavioral symptoms that develop <strong>within 3 months of exposure</strong> to an identifiable stressor and usually <strong>do not last more than 6 months</strong> after the stressor ends.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cyclothymic disorder </strong>is symptomatically a mild form of bipolar II disorder, characterized by episodes of <strong>hypomania</strong> and <strong>mild depression</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0976","text":"A patient comes to you wit​h a history of persistent‍ low mood for the past 3 ‍years. He also complains ‍of fatigue and insomnia d​uring this period. He giv​es a history of worsening‌ low mood over the past 3‍ weeks, along with suicid​al thoughts and a loss of‌ interest in all activiti‌es. What is the probable ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient comes to you with a history of persistent low mood for the past 3 years. He also complains of fatigue and insomnia during this period. He gives a history of worsening low mood over the past 3 weeks, along with suicidal thoughts and a loss of interest in all activities.  What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Major depres​sive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dyst​hymia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo​thymia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double d​epression","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Double depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Double depression</strong></p><p><strong>Double depression</strong> is the most probable diagnosis in this scenario. <strong>Dysthymia</strong> and <strong>major depressive disorder</strong> in the <strong>same period </strong>constitute double depression. </p>\n<p>From the given history:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Features of low mood, fatigue, and insomnia for more than 2 years point towards dysthymia.</li>\n<li>Features of low mood, fatigue, insomnia, anhedonia, and suicidal thoughts for more than 2 weeks point towards major depressive disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6683","text":"A mother started to feel s​ad and tearful over the f‍irst two weeks following ‍childbirth. She felt over‍whelmed by the responsibi​lities of nursing her bab​y and changing diapers an‌d waking up in the middle‍ of the night. She felt t​hat she was a failure as ‌a mother. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A mother started to feel sad and tearful over the first two weeks following childbirth. She felt overwhelmed by the responsibilities of nursing her baby and changing diapers and waking up in the middle of the night. She felt that she was a failure as a mother. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-par​tum blues","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Postpartum​ depression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-partu​m psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-part​um anxiety","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Post-partum blues","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Post-partum blues</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>postpartum blues. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is characterized by a labile mood, sadness, and subjective confusion. It is thought to be due to changes in hormone levels, the stress of childbirth, and anxiety about the responsibilities of motherhood. This usually resolves spontaneously. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">If symptoms last longer than 2 weeks, an evaluation for <strong>postpartum depression</strong> is needed.</p>\n<p>Note:  Zuranolone is the first-ever oral drug to be approved specifically for the treatment of postpartum depression. It is a neuroactive steroid that enhances the action of GABA in the brain and regulates mood. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6684","text":"A woman who had delivered ​a healthy baby 3 weeks ag‍o was brought to the psyc‍hiatry OPD. She intently ‍stares at you and says th​at her baby was stolen fr​om the womb by her sister‌. On examination she has ‍labile mood, and is mumbl​ing to herself. What is t‌he likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman who had delivered a healthy baby 3 weeks ago was brought to the psychiatry OPD. She intently stares at you and says that her baby was stolen from the womb by her sister. On examination she has labile mood, and is mumbling to herself. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baby ​blues","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postpartum​ depression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postpartum​ psychosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Postpartum psychosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Postpartum psychosis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">From the given clinical scenario the patient is showing features of <strong>postpartum psychosis </strong>(depression with psychotic features).<strong> </strong>It<strong> </strong>can often begin within a few days following delivery, although the mean time of onset is usually within <strong>2 to 4 weeks</strong> of delivery. </p>\n<p>Characteristically, the patient begins to complain of fatigue, episodes of tearfulness, and emotional lability. Later develops, <strong>suspiciousness</strong>, confusion, <strong>irrational statements</strong>, and obsessive concerns about the baby's health and welfare may be present. Patients eventually develop <strong>delusions</strong> that involve the idea that the baby is dead or defective. <strong>Hallucinations</strong> if present may involve voices telling the patient to harm the baby or herself. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8645","text":"What is the treatment of c​hoice for postpartum blue‍s?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for postpartum blues?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluox​etine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cognitive behavioural ther​apy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lithium ​carbonate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Support an​d education","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Support and education","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Support and education</strong></p><p><strong>Postpartum blues</strong> is common in mothers in the puerperal period and requires nothing more than <strong>education and support</strong>. It is characterised by a labile mood, sadness and subjective confusion. It is thought to be due to changes in hormone levels, the stress of childbirth and anxiety about the responsibilities of motherhood.</p>\n<p>If symptoms last longer than 2 weeks, an evaluation for <strong>postpartum depression</strong> is needed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3244","text":"A primigravida who is 9 we​eks pregnant, with low mo‍od, loss of appetite, anh‍edonia, and insomnia for ‍the past 2 weeks was refe​rred to the psychiatry de​partment. Which of the fo‌llowing drugs would you p‍rescribe her?","raw_text":"A primigravida who is 9 weeks pregnant, with low mood, loss of appetite, anhedonia, and insomnia for the past 2 weeks was referred to the psychiatry department.  Which of the following drugs would you prescribe her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imipr​amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desip​ramine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Seleg​iline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parox​etine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Desipramine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Desipramine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of depression. <strong>Desipramine</strong> is the <strong>safest</strong> to use amongst the given options, for <strong>depression during pregnancy. </strong></p>\n<p>Antidepressants and pregnancy:</p>\n<p>Tricyclic Antidepressants (TCA): </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cross the placenta.</li>\n<li><strong>Imipramine</strong> - <strong>not safe</strong>, due to high anticholinergic and hypotensive properties</li>\n<li>Desipramine, nortriptyline - recommended</li>\n<li>Neonatal withdrawal effects are frequent and mild</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mono Amine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAO-I):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Should be <strong>avoided</strong> in <strong>pregnancy,</strong> as they increase the risk of hypertensive crisis</li>\n<li>Associated with pulmonary hypertension in newborns</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRI):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Associated with preterm delivery and spontaneous abortions</li>\n<li><strong>Paroxetine</strong> - shows <strong>cardiac abnormalities</strong> in <strong>newborns</strong></li>\n<li>Citalopram, Sertraline - safer drugs</li>\n<li>Neonate shows serotonin toxicity and withdrawal symptoms</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Antipsychotic drugs like olanzapine, quetiapine, and risperidone are considered safe during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0931","text":"For how long would you con​tinue SSRIs for a patient‍ who had a single episode‍ of depression?","raw_text":"For how long would you continue SSRIs for a patient who had a single episode of depression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 weeks after complete rem​ission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 months after complete re​mission","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6 weeks after complete rem​ission","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Until comple​te remission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 months after complete remission","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 months after complete remission</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>SSRIs</strong> should be continued for<strong> 6-9 months</strong> <strong>after complete remission </strong>of symptoms in a patient with a single episode of depression.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Treatment of depression:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 275\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>In recent‐onset <strong>mild depression</strong>, cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is preferred and antidepressants are not recommended.</li>\n<li>For<strong> moderate to severe depression</strong> and <strong>dysthymia</strong> - Antidepressants (SSRIs) + CBT.</li>\n<li>For <strong>treatment‐resistant depression</strong>, recommended strategies include augmentation with lithium or an antipsychotic or the addition of a second antidepressant.</li>\n<li><strong>For severe</strong> and<strong> treatment-resistant depression - </strong>Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) can also be used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The various classes of drugs used in depression and anxiety are listed below: </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"width: 362.609px; height: 336px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Class of drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Fluoxetine</li>\n<li>Fluvoxamine</li>\n<li>Paroxetine</li>\n<li>Sertraline</li>\n<li>Citalopram</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Venlafaxine</li>\n<li>Desvenlafaxine</li>\n<li>Duloxetine</li>\n<li>Milnacipran</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tricyclic antidepressants</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Amitriptyline</li>\n<li>Imipramine</li>\n<li>Nortriptyline</li>\n<li>Desipramine</li>\n<li>Amoxapine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Atypical antipsychotics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Aripiprazole</li>\n<li>Olanzapine</li>\n<li>Quetiapine</li>\n<li>Risperidone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAO)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Isocarboxazid</li>\n<li>Selegiline</li>\n<li>Phenelzine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Atypical antidepressants</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.609px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bupropion</li>\n<li>Trazodone</li>\n<li>Mirtazapine</li>\n<li>Mianserin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5804","text":"A patient was treated with​ TCA for a major depressi‍ve episode in the hospita‍l for 4 weeks. What is th‍e main concern while cons​idering his discharge fro​m the hospital?","raw_text":"A patient was treated with TCA for a major depressive episode in the hospital for 4 weeks. What is the main concern while considering his discharge from the hospital?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Need for dru​g monitoring","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ECG monitoring for arrhyth​mias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suicid​al risk","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sedation being the side ef​fect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suicidal risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suicidal risk</strong></p><p><strong>Suicidal thoughts</strong> or<strong> behavior </strong>are a <strong>side effect</strong> of <strong>TCAs.</strong> Therefore, the attendants of the patient <strong>should be warned</strong> regarding this when a patient gets discharged after being treated with TCAs for a major depressive episode.</p>\n<p>Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) are drugs that act by<strong> inhibiting</strong> the<strong> reuptake</strong> of <strong>noradrenaline</strong> and <strong>serotonin</strong> from the synaptic clefts. </p>\n<p>The side effects of TCAs are due to blockade of other receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Muscarinic M receptors</strong>- constipation, dry mouth, arrhythmia</li>\n<li><strong>Histamine H<sub>1</sub> receptors</strong>- sedation</li>\n<li><strong>5HT receptors</strong> - weight gain due to increased appetite</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha<sub>1</sub> receptors</strong> - postural hypotension.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6685","text":"What is the treatment of c​hoice in a patient with r‍efractory depression and ‍suicidal thoughts?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice in a patient with refractory depression and suicidal thoughts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selective serotonin recept​or inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricyclic an​tidepressants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Electroconvu​lsive therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Monoamine oxidase inhibito​rs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Electroconvulsive therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Electroconvulsive therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)</strong> is the treatment of choice for depression with suicidal thoughts and refractory depression.</p>\n<p>Modified ECT uses short-acting anaesthesia and short-acting muscle relaxants.</p>\n<p>Treatment of depression:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In recent‐onset <strong>mild depression</strong>, cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is preferred and antidepressants are not recommended.</li>\n<li>For<strong> moderate to severe depression</strong> and <strong>dysthymia</strong> - Antidepressants (SSRIs) + CBT.</li>\n<li>For <strong>treatment‐resistant depression</strong>, recommended strategies include augmentation with lithium or an antipsychotic or the addition of a second antidepressant</li>\n<li><strong>For severe</strong> and<strong> treatment-resistant depression - </strong>Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) can also be used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Somatic and newer treatment modalities for depression include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vagus nerve stimulation for resistant depression</li>\n<li>Repeated transcranial magnetic stimulation</li>\n<li>Phototherapy</li>\n<li>Deep brain stimulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0830","text":"Which is a mood stabiliser​ with an anti-suicidal ro‍le?","raw_text":"Which is a mood stabiliser with an anti-suicidal role?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamot​rigine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lit​hium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Valp​roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lithium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lithium</strong></p><p><strong>Lithium</strong> is a mood stabilizer with an<strong> anti-suicidal </strong>role.</p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance treatment</strong> with <strong>lithium</strong> has been shown to significantly<strong> decrease the rate of suicide </strong>in patients diagnosed with <strong>mood disorders </strong>when compared to<strong> </strong>other mood stabilizers such as carbamazepine, lamotrigine, and valproate.</p>\n<p>Lithium is also used as an augmentation agent in treatment-resistant depression. Recurrent depression with a cyclic pattern can be controlled by either lithium or imipramine. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0932","text":"A 36-year-old woman presen​ted with symptoms of low ‍mood, loss of interest, l‍oss of appetite, and slee‍p disturbances for the pa​st 3 weeks. She has a his​tory of two similar episo‌des over the last 3 years‍. She has no history of e​pisodes of elated mood, r‌acing thoughts, or decrea‌sed need to sleep. Which ​of the following is not a‌ risk factor for her cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman presented with symptoms of low mood, loss of interest, loss of appetite, and sleep disturbances for the past 3 weeks. She has a history of two similar episodes over the last 3 years. She has no history of episodes of elated mood, racing thoughts, or decreased need to sleep.  Which of the following is not a risk factor for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short episo​de duration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent​ dysthymia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family history of similar ​episodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concurrent non-affective p​sychiatric illness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Short episode duration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Short episode duration</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario points towards a diagnosis of <strong>recurrent depressive disorder</strong>. <strong>Long</strong> <strong>episode duration</strong> is a risk factor for recurrent depression.</p>\n<p>Risk factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family history of depression</li>\n<li>Recurrent dysthymia</li>\n<li>Concurrent non‐affective psychiatric illness</li>\n<li>Female gender </li>\n<li>Long episode duration</li>\n<li>Degree of treatment resistance</li>\n<li>Chronic medical illness</li>\n<li>Social factors (lack of confiding relationships and psychosocial stressors)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0961","text":"An elderly gentleman prese​nts to you with complaint‍s of forgetfulness. He is‍ unable to work as his me‍mory issues were causing ​him distress, and he has ​difficulty concentrating.‌ He also has insomnia, de‍pressed mood, loss of app​etite, and fatigue for th‌e past two weeks. Which o‌f the following best desc​ribes this condition?","raw_text":"An elderly gentleman presents to you with complaints of forgetfulness. He is unable to work as his memory issues were causing him distress, and he has difficulty concentrating. He also has insomnia, depressed mood, loss of appetite, and fatigue for the past two weeks. Which of the following best describes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alzheimer​'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deli​rium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deme​ntia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudo-​dementia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pseudo-dementia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pseudo-dementia</strong></p><p>The above scenario of an <strong>elderly</strong> <strong>depressed patient </strong>with memory loss and <strong>dementia syndrome</strong> is suggestive of <strong>pseudodementia. </strong>This syndrome has been termed as dementia syndrome of depression/depression with reversible dementia.<strong> </strong>The condition may improve or even completely subside after the remission of depression. </p>\n<p>Pseudodementia is a condition that appears similar to dementia but <strong>does not have neurological degeneration</strong>. Such patients commonly complain of impaired concentration and forgetfulness.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0389","text":"What is anaclitic depressi​on?","raw_text":"What is anaclitic depression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depression in infants when​ separated from primary c‍aregiver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Depression seen in mental ​retardation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depression seen in autism ​spectrum disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depression in schizophreni​a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Depression in infants when separated from primary caregiver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Depression in infants when separated from primary caregiver</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 118\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Anaclitic depression</strong>, also known as <strong>hospitalism,</strong> is a type of depression in <strong>infants</strong> where <strong>sudden separation</strong> from their <strong>primary caregivers</strong> leads to depression, withdrawal, nonresponsiveness, and loss of milestones in the infants. However, they recover when their mothers return or when surrogate mothering is available. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0933","text":"A middle-aged woman diagno​sed with depression prese‍nted to your OPD for a fo‍llow-up. You notice a cha‍racteristic sign marked b​y an arrow in the image. ​Identify this sign.","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman diagnosed with depression presented to your OPD for a follow-up. You notice a characteristic sign marked by an arrow in the image. Identify this sign.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Veragut​h's sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Omega​ sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Van Go​gh sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ottoman​'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Veraguth's sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/865944f18b7a42679eb3b74f1e0659f5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d2bdce0d5324ff5acffc26bb59afd85x1200x1118.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Veraguth&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In the given image, the arrow indicates <strong>Veraguth's sign </strong>or Veraguth's fold, a<strong> diagonal palpebral fold</strong> running from the lateral corners of the eyes, medially upward to the medial end of the eyebrows. It is a physical sign of depression.</p>\n<p>Another physical sign of depression is the <strong>Omega sign. </strong>In this, there is vertical wrinkling between the eyebrows joined at the top by a horizontal crease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0966","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of melancho‍lia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of melancholia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe a​nhedonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weigh​t loss","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sleep-onse​t insomnia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Profound feelings of guilt​ over trivial events","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sleep-onset insomnia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sleep-onset insomnia</strong></p><p><strong>Sleep-onset insomnia</strong> is a characteristic feature of <strong>anxiety disorder</strong>, not melancholia.</p>\n<p><strong>Melancholia</strong> is one of the oldest terms used in psychiatry, to describe the <strong>dark mood of depression</strong>. It is still used to refer to a state of depression characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe anhedonia</li>\n<li>Early morning awakening (<strong>terminal insomnia</strong>)</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Profound feelings of guilt (often over trivial events)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Melancholia is sometimes referred to as \"<strong>endogenous depression</strong>\" or depression that arises in the absence of external life stressors or precipitants.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1002","text":"Which among the following ​is not a feature of a pat‍ient with hysteroid dysph‍oria?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a feature of a patient with hysteroid dysphoria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reversed vegetative sympto​ms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No therapeutic response to​ monoamine oxidase inhibi‍tors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They experience a craving ​for chocolate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impaired anticipatory plea​sure is seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. No therapeutic response to monoamine oxidase inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. No therapeutic response to monoamine oxidase inhibitors</strong></p><p>Persons with <strong>hysteroid dysphoria</strong> respond well to treatment with monoamine oxidase inhibitors.</p>\n<p>Hysteroid dysphoria is a type of <strong>atypical depression. </strong>The following features can be seen in this condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reversed vegetative symptoms:\n<ul>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n<li>Tendency to oversleep and to overeat</li>\n<li>Mood worsens in the evening</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Extreme sensitivity to rejection</strong> (hallmark feature)</li>\n<li>Impaired anticipatory pleasure (prediction of pleasure from a future reward), but affected persons are capable of <strong>mood reactivity</strong> i.e., their mood brightens in response to positive events</li>\n<li>Craving for chocolate</li>\n<li>Like patients with bipolar depression, they respond preferentially to monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1019","text":"A 32-year-old male present​s with symptoms of low mo‍od, fatigue, and failure ‍to experience pleasure in‍ any activities. He also ​complains of insomnia and​ lack of concentration. T‌hese symptoms are present‍ during most of the month​s of winter and completel‌y remit during spring. Th‌e symptoms have been recu​rring over the past 3 yea‌rs. What is the most prob​able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old male presents with symptoms of low mood, fatigue, and failure to experience pleasure in any activities. He also complains of insomnia and lack of concentration. These symptoms are present during most of the months of winter and completely remit during spring. The symptoms have been recurring over the past 3 years. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Major Depres​sive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Major Depressive Disorder ​with seasonal pattern","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo​thymia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dyst​hymia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Major Depressive Disorder with seasonal pattern","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Major Depressive Disorder with seasonal pattern</strong></p><p><strong>Major depressive disorder</strong> with<strong> seasonal pattern</strong> is the most likely diagnosis in this scenario. </p>\n<p>Features of major depressive disorder with seasonal pattern:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Onset and remission of major depressive episodes at <strong>specific times of the year.</strong></li>\n<li>In most cases, the episodes begin in the fall or winter and remit in spring.</li>\n<li>This pattern of onset and remission of episodes must have occurred during at least a<strong> 2-year period</strong>, <strong>without any</strong><br/><strong>nonseasonal episodes</strong> during this period.</li>\n<li>In addition, the seasonal depressive episodes must substantially <strong>outnumber any nonseasonal depressive episodes</strong> over the individual's lifetime.</li>\n<li>Absence of seasonally linked psychosocial stressors (e.g., seasonal unemployment or school schedule).</li>\n<li>Major depressive episodes that occur in a seasonal pattern are often characterized by prominent energy, hypersomnia, overeating, weight gain, and a craving for carbohydrates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: According to ICD-11, seasonal depressive disorder is included under recurrent depressive disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1014","text":"A 32-year-old man presents​ with symptoms of low moo‍d, fatigue, and inability‍ to experience pleasure i‍n any activities. He also​ complains of insomnia an​d lack of concentration. ‌These symptoms are presen‍t during the months of wi​nter and completely remit‌ during spring. On furthe‌r questioning, you find t​hat he is a laborer who i‌s always left unemployed ​during the winter months.​ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presents with symptoms of low mood, fatigue, and inability to experience pleasure in any activities. He also complains of insomnia and lack of concentration. These symptoms are present during the months of winter and completely remit during spring. On further questioning, you find that he is a laborer who is always left unemployed during the winter months. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Major depres​sive disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Major depressive disorder ​with seasonal pattern","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dyst​hymia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo​thymia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Major depressive disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Major depressive disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Major depressive disorder</strong> is the most likely diagnosis in this case. </p>\n<p>DSM-V criteria for diagnosis of depression:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 365\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Depressed mood</strong> most of the day</li>\n<li><strong>Anhedonia</strong>- diminished interest in day-to-day activities</li>\n<li>Significant weight loss or weight gain and/or change in appetite.</li>\n<li><strong>Insomnia</strong> or hypersomnia</li>\n<li>Psychomotor agitation or retardation</li>\n<li><strong>Fatigue</strong> or loss of energy</li>\n<li>Feelings of worthlessness or excessive, inappropriate guilt</li>\n<li><strong>Diminished ability</strong> to think or <strong>concentrate</strong>, indecisiveness</li>\n<li>Recurrent thoughts of death and suicide</li>\n</ol>\n<p>At least <strong>five out of nine</strong> symptoms must be present over a two-week period and <strong>one</strong> symptom <strong>must be depressed mood </strong>or<strong> anhedonia. </strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Major depressive disorder with seasonal pattern </strong>(option B) is <strong>not</strong> the <strong>diagnosis</strong> as there is<strong> the presence of a </strong><strong>psychosocial stressor</strong> (e.g., seasonal unemployment or school schedule). It is explicitly mentioned in the question that the patient always remains unemployed during the winter months which acts as the objective psychological stressor in this case.</p>\n<p><strong>Major depressive disorder with seasonal pattern</strong> refers to the <strong>onset</strong> and <strong>remission</strong> of <strong>major depressive episodes</strong> at <strong>specific times of the year</strong>. In most cases, the episodes begin in the fall or winter and remit in spring. Major depressive episodes that occur in a seasonal pattern are often characterized by prominent energy, hypersomnia, overeating, weight gain, and a craving for carbohydrates. This pattern of onset and remission of episodes must have occurred during at least a 2-year period, without any nonseasonal episodes during this period. </p>\n<p><strong>Dysthymia</strong> (option C) is the presence of a <strong>persistently depressed mood</strong> that lasts <strong>most of the day</strong> and is <strong>continuous</strong>. There are also feelings of <strong>guilt</strong>, <strong>irritability</strong>, and <strong>anger</strong> associated with it. These symptoms are present for at least 2 years. Dysthymia will also <strong>never</strong> present with an episode of <strong>mania/hypomania</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cyclothymia</strong> (option D) is a <strong>mild</strong> form of <strong>bipolar II disorder</strong>, characterized by symptoms of <strong>hypomania </strong>and <strong>mild depression</strong>. Hypomania mainly includes symptoms like persistently elevated or irritable mood and abnormally increased energy lasting for at least 4 consecutive days. The depression seen in cyclothymia does not qualify the criteria for a major depressive episode.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0969","text":"Phototherapy is a treatmen​t for which of the follow‍ing?","raw_text":"Phototherapy is a treatment for which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic majo​r depression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seasonal affective disorde​r","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo​thymia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Seasonal affective disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Seasonal affective disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Phototherapy</strong> was introduced as a treatment for <strong>seasonal affective disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this disorder, patients typically experience depression as the photoperiod of the day decreases with advancing winter. </p>\n<p>Phototherapy typically involves exposing the affected <strong>patient </strong>to bright light in the range of 1,500 to 10,000 lux or more, typically with a lightbox. The patient sits in front of this lightbox for approximately <strong>1 to 2 hours before dawn </strong>each day.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0680","text":"A middle-aged female patie​nt presents with a histor‍y of loss of interest in ‍day to day activities, we‍ight loss, insomnia, and ​sadness for six months. S​he also recalls hearing a‌ voice once that ordered ‍her to kill herself. What​ is the DSM-V diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged female patient presents with a history of loss of interest in day to day activities, weight loss, insomnia, and sadness for six months. She also recalls hearing a voice once that ordered her to kill herself. What is the DSM-V diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Major depressive disorder ​with psychotic features","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schizoaffect​ive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delusiona​l disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Major depressive disorder with psychotic features","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Major depressive disorder with psychotic features</strong></p><p>Since the patient fulfills the DSM-V criteria for major depressive disorder and has had an episode of auditory hallucination, a diagnosis of <strong>major depressive disorder with psychotic features</strong> is made.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic criteria for depression:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Depressed mood most of the day</li>\n<li>Anhedonia- diminished interest in day to day activities</li>\n<li>Significant weight loss or weight gain. Change in appetite.</li>\n<li>Insomnia or hypersomnia</li>\n<li>Psychomotor agitation or retardation</li>\n<li>Fatigue or loss of energy</li>\n<li>Feelings of worthlessness or excessive, inappropriate guilt</li>\n<li>Diminished ability to think or concentrate, indecisiveness</li>\n<li>Recurrent thoughts of death, suicidal ideation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>At least <strong>five out of nine</strong> symptoms must be present over a two week period and <strong>one</strong> symptom <strong>must be depressed mood or anhedonia.</strong></p>\n<p>In major depressive disorder with psychotic features, <strong>delusions and/or hallucinations</strong> are present along with the depression symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6705","text":"A 30-year-old patient come​s to the casualty with a ‍dry mouth, decreased swea‍ting and blurring of visi‍on, tremors. On examinati​on, blood pressure is low​, and mydriasis is presen‌t. The patient says that ‍he has been on psychiatri​c medication for the past‌ year. Which of the follo‌wing drugs is responsible​ for the above presentati‌on?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient comes to the casualty with a dry mouth, decreased sweating and blurring of vision, tremors. On examination, blood pressure is low, and mydriasis is present. The patient says that he has been on psychiatric medication for the past year. Which of the following drugs is responsible for the above presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disul​firam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imipr​amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lit​hium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valp​roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Imipramine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Imipramine</strong></p><p>The manifestations given in the question are the side effects of <strong>tricyclic antidepressants (imipramine).</strong></p>\n<p>TCAs have <strong>anticholinergic side effects </strong>such as dry eyes, pupillary dilation, decreased sweating, urinary retention, and constipation.</p>\n<p>These drugs are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with <strong>glaucoma</strong> and <strong>benign prostatic hyperplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Side effects of tricyclic antidepressants:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 123px; width: 523px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.515625px;\">Receptor blocked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.484375px;\">Side effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.515625px;\">Muscarinic receptor blockade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.484375px;\">Anticholinergic- dry mouth, dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.515625px;\">Alpha 1 adrenergic receptor blockade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.484375px;\">Drowsiness, postural hypotension, sexual dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.515625px;\">H1 receptor blockade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.484375px;\">Weight gain, drowsiness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6917","text":"A 35-year-old woman develo​ped serotonin syndrome af‍ter receiving a single do‍se of imipramine. She was‍ already being treated fo​r depression with paroxet​ine. Which of the followi‌ng is not seen on examina‍tion?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman developed serotonin syndrome after receiving a single dose of imipramine. She was already being treated for depression with paroxetine. Which of the following is not seen on examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tre​mors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rigi​dity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot​hermia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myoc​lonus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypothermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypothermia</strong></p><p>Hypothermia is not a feature of serotonin syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Serotonin syndrome</strong> is characterized by 3 A’s:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Neuromuscular hyper<strong>a</strong>ctivity (clonus, hyperreflexia, hypertonia, tremor, and seizure)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>utonomic stimulation (hyperthermia, diaphoresis, diarrhoea)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>gitation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It can occur with any drug that increases 5-HT (eg, MAOIs, SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs, tramadol, ondansetron, triptans, linezolid, MDMA, dextromethorphan).</p>\n<p>Treatment: Discontinuation of the offending drug, supportive management, cyproheptadine (5-HT2 receptor antagonist).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"awrnGcfJ","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"11_hepatobiliary_and_pancreatic_radiology","title":"11.Hepatobiliary And Pancreatic Radiology","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA0696","text":"The CT abdomen of a patien‍t presenting with abdomin​al pain and vomiting is s​hown below. What is the p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"The CT abdomen of a patient presenting with abdominal pain and vomiting is shown below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choleli‍thiasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute cho‍lecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gallbladde‍r carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gallbladd‍er abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cholelithiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c99917b8ec7244f690b0cf9fe91b9375.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb64a26792f3466896a026152aca2b94x510x438.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f3f7730700343f88eec3c39109ee642x1191x1198.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cholelithiasis</strong></p><p>A patient presenting with abdominal pain and vomiting, with CT showing a <strong>Mercedes-Benz sign</strong>, is characteristic of <strong>cholelithiasis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mercedes–Benz sign</strong> (triradiate sign), is seen when a gallstone contains central radiolucent gas. The fissures may be tri-radiate or bi-radiate (<strong>seagull sign</strong>). These fissures appear dark on CT or radiographs, giving the sign its characteristic appearance. </p>\n<p><strong>Gallbladder stones </strong>or cholelithiasis are the most common condition affecting the gallbladder. They are often discovered incidentally and typically remain asymptomatic. However, they become symptomatic when the stones obstruct the cystic duct. In such cases, patients may experience dull or <strong>colicky</strong> right upper quadrant or epigastric <strong>pain</strong> radiating to the back, along with symptoms like nausea and vomiting. </p>\n<p>Only about 10% of gallstones are radiopaque.<strong> Ultrasonography (USG)</strong> is the most accurate initial imaging modality of choice for the diagnosis of gallbladder stones. These stones appear as <strong>echogenic foci</strong> producing <strong>acoustic shadows. </strong>The sensitivity of USG for gallstones is &gt;95%.</p>\n<p>The CT image below depicts the Mercedes-Benz sign: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows ultrasonography of the gall bladder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0695","text":"A middle-aged woman with c‍hronic alcoholism present​ed with severe right-side​d upper abdominal pain. O​n examination, her SOFA s‍core was 3. An abdominal ​CT was done which is show‌n below. What is the prob‍able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with chronic alcoholism presented with severe right-sided upper abdominal pain. On examination, her SOFA score was 3. An abdominal CT was done which is shown below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calculous c‍holecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute pan‍creatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Choledoch‍olithiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emphysematous cholecystiti‍s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Emphysematous cholecystitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad27125f6d7844409f2d386f00ce68d4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acbea3f5d8e5435db4806ed042bb3778x1200x2346.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59fa8fe0ac544699ab8485d6f4bc9225x1236x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Emphysematous cholecystitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the CT showing<strong> gas within the wall of the gallbladder</strong> is suggestive of <strong>emphysematous cholecystitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Emphysematous cholecystitis is a rare form of acute cholecystitis where gallbladder wall necrosis causes gas formation intramurally or intraluminally. </p>\n<p>This condition occurs most frequently in elderly males or patients with diabetes. Secondary infection of the gallbladder wall with gas-forming organisms such as <strong><em>Clostridium welchii</em></strong>/<strong> <em>C. perfringens</em> </strong>leads to emphysematous cholecystitis, and aerobes such as Escherichia coli may also be isolated. Patients present with right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, and low-grade fever. <strong>Crepitus</strong> is present on physical examination.</p>\n<p>On imaging, the <strong>air</strong> is seen in the <strong>gallbladder wall </strong>and/or <strong>biliary ducts</strong>, in the absence of abnormal communication with the gastrointestinal tract is seen.</p>\n<p>Emphysematous cholecystitis has a <strong>high mortality rate</strong> due to the development of gangrene, perforation, and other complications. Hence, <strong>emergency cholecystectomy</strong> is indicated for the management of these patients. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The given image shows a postoperative specimen of an emphysematous gallbladder along with an X-ray showing air in the gallbladder wall.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8674","text":"USG in a 47-year-old woman‍ showed the wall-echo-sha​dow sign. What is the mos​t probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"USG in a 47-year-old woman showed the wall-echo-shadow sign. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute cho‍lecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ch‍olecystitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GB adenom‍yomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GB c‍ancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic cholecystitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01fa1e51dc8c4facbc01b8c1b778e5e0x809x543.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic cholecystitis</strong></p><p>The <strong>wall-echo-shadow sign</strong> (WES) is seen in <strong>chronic cholecystitis with cholelithiasis. </strong>This USG finding is seen within the gallbladder fossa and describes the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A curvilinear hyperechogenic line representing the gallbladder wall</li>\n<li>A thin hypoechoic space representing a small amount of bile</li>\n<li>A curvilinear hyperechogenic line representing the near-surface of gallstone</li>\n<li>Acoustic shadowing distal to the surface of the gallstone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image depicts the WES sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8678","text":"Abdominal USG in a patient‍ with pruritic jaundice r​evealed the double-barrel​ sign. What is the most p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Abdominal USG in a patient with pruritic jaundice revealed the double-barrel sign. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic duct‍ obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CBD obs‍truction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute cho‍lecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gall blad‍der cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CBD obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f34d86c5bf1d4b5888b8a646342f3feex1280x1067.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CBD obstruction</strong></p><p><strong>Double-barrel sign</strong> is seen in <strong>CBD obstruction</strong>. It causes dilatation of CBD with <strong>increased lumen size</strong> which becomes comparable to the <strong>portal</strong> <strong>vein</strong>. This gives the appearance of a double-barrel.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows CBD stone causing obstruction and dilatation of CBD – double barrel sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0697","text":"The given finding on MRCP ‍is characteristic of:","raw_text":"The given finding on MRCP is characteristic of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bile du‍ct stone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreatic‍ duct stone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stri‍cture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Worm in bi‍liary tree","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Worm in biliary tree","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/326fe60e154749608df0d55286c5b46b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70970b993e774415bcabc4c9ed7901a6x1280x2525.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Worm in biliary tree</strong></p><p>The given MRCP shows<strong> linear filling defect</strong> characteristic of <strong>worm in the biliary tree</strong>. One of the commonly found worms in the biliary tree is <strong>Ascaris</strong>.</p>\n<p>The worm may also be seen as <strong>parallel filling defects -</strong> <strong>railway track sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0698","text":"A neonate was admitted to ‍the NICU with persistent ​jaundice. A USG abdomen w​as done and is shown belo​w. What is the probable d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A neonate was admitted to the NICU with persistent jaundice. A USG abdomen was done and is shown below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galact‍osemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biliary‍ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heman‍gioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caroli's‍ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Biliary atresia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f9e3245616b4144a8188f9e6aea224e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Biliary atresia</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario along with the <strong>triangular cord sign</strong> on the USG is suggestive of <strong>biliary atresia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The triangular cord sign represents the <strong>fibrous ductal remnant</strong> of the extrahepatic bile duct in biliary atresia. It is usually seen as an echogenicity along the anterior wall of the right portal vein. In the above image, the arrow represents the fibrous atretic plate at the porta hepatis.</p>\n<p>Biliary atresia may also be termed as noncystic obliterative cholangiopathy. It is a disease of the intra and extrahepatic bile ducts of unknown etiology.</p>\n<p>The classification of biliary atresia with the regions involved is as follows:</p>\n<p>Type I - Common bile duct<br/>Type IIa - Common hepatic duct<br/>Type IIb - Common bile duct, hepatic duct, cystic duct.<br/><strong>Type III</strong> (most common type) - Common bile duct, hepatic duct, cystic duct; no anastomosable ducts at porta hepatis.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include persistent jaundice, clay-colored stools, and hepatomegaly. Untreated newborns will show signs of liver cirrhosis and failure (ascites, portal hypertension, and failure to thrive).</p>\n<p>Laboratory evaluation will show<strong> elevated</strong> levels of direct and indirect <strong>bilirubin</strong>, alkaline phosphatase, and gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase. Ultrasound will show <strong>hypoplastic</strong> or <strong>absent gallbladder</strong> and the <strong>triangular cord sign.</strong><strong> </strong>The definitive diagnosis is by <strong>intraoperative cholangiography</strong>. Preoperative percutaneous transhepatic cholecystocholangiography <strong>combined with liver biopsy</strong> in cholestatic infants has a sensitivity of 100% and a specificity of 93% for diagnosing biliary atresia. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8672","text":"Abdominal USG in a 50-year‍-old male with right uppe​r quadrant abdominal pain​ revealed the comet tail ​sign. What is the most pr‍obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Abdominal USG in a 50-year-old male with right upper quadrant abdominal pain revealed the comet tail sign. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute cho‍lecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ch‍olecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gall bladder adenomyomatos‍is","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Choleli‍thiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gall bladder adenomyomatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64c81df13d2343c498be3fbfa655a889x1280x1135.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gall bladder adenomyomatosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and comet-tail sign on USG is seen in <strong>adenomyomatosis</strong> of the <strong>gallbladder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Adenomyomatous hyperplasia of the gall bladder is also known as <strong>cholecystitis glandularis proliferans.</strong> It is characterized by gall bladder wall thickening due to epithelial and smooth muscle hyperplasia along with cystic epithelial invagination into the wall, referred to as <strong>Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses</strong>. The most common location is in the <strong>fundus</strong> of the gall bladder and the sinuses often contain <strong>stones.</strong> </p>\n<p>On USG, the characteristic findings are a <strong>thickened gallbladder wall</strong> showing bright echoes arising from the cystic spaces with ‘<strong>comet-tail</strong>’ ring-down artefacts, as seen in the image below. The comet-tail artefact is caused by the <strong>cholesterol crystals</strong> trapped within the Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses, causing reverberation (large number of reflected waves).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0691","text":"An erect abdomen X-ray of ‍a patient presenting with​ an acute abdomen is give​n below. What is the prob​able diagnosis?","raw_text":"An erect abdomen X-ray of a patient presenting with an acute abdomen is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pan‍creatitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Large bowel‍ obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diverti‍culitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute pancreatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/91488aed63b340b0b00223b4a74c7213.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute pancreatitis</strong></p><p>The given erect X-ray abdomen in a patient with acute abdomen, showing a<strong> colon cut-off</strong> sign, suggests <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The colon cut-off sign refers to the <strong>abrupt termination</strong> of the <strong>gas filling</strong> of the colon on the <strong>left</strong> side. The inflammation of acute pancreatitis may extend to the phrenicocolic ligament via the transverse mesocolon, causing a <strong>functional spasm</strong> of the splenic flexure, resulting in the colon cut-off sign. Though originally described in abdominal radiographs, this sign has also been demonstrated in contrast enema and computed tomography. </p>\n<p>Acute pancreatitis is a condition characterized by inflammatory damage to the pancreas, presenting clinically with <strong>epigastric pain </strong>radiating to the back, which is <strong>relieved</strong> by <strong>bending forward</strong>, nausea, and vomiting. Some may also present with signs of <strong>bleeding</strong> into the fascial planes, such as Cullen’s sign, Grey Turner’s sign, Fox sign, and Bryant’s sign (discolouration of the scrotum).</p>\n<p><strong>Radiological signs</strong> in acute pancreatitis include colon cut-off sign, preserved renal halo sign, and sentinel loop sign (upper abdomen).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0692","text":"A patient with chronic alc‍oholism presented with na​usea, vomiting, and abdom​inal pain. His lab report​s showed elevated serum a‍mylase and lipase levels.​ Which of the following s‌igns is unlikely to be se‍en in the radiograph of t‌his patient?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic alcoholism presented with nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. His lab reports showed elevated serum amylase and lipase levels. Which of the following signs is unlikely to be seen in the radiograph of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colon cut‍ off sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sentinel ‍loop sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preserved re‍nal halo sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grey Tur‍ner sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Grey Turner sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Grey Turner sign</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with elevated serum amylase and lipase suggests <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>. <strong>Grey Turner's sign</strong>, seen in this condition, is a <strong>clinical</strong> <strong>sign, </strong>not a radiological finding, characterized by <strong>flank discolouration</strong> due to retroperitoneal <strong>haemorrhage</strong>. </p>\n<p>Acute <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> pancreatitis can present clinically with certain named signs like Cullen’s sign, Grey Turner’s sign, Fox sign, and Bryant’s sign (discolouration of the scrotum).</p>\n<p>Radiological signs in acute pancreatitis include <strong>colon cut-off sign</strong>, <strong>preserved renal halo sign</strong>, and <strong>sentinel loop sign </strong>(upper abdomen)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The sentinel loop sign may aid in localizing the source of inflammation. For example, the sentinel loop sign in the right lower quadrant may be due to appendicitis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0693","text":"What is the investigation ‍of choice to assess the p​rognosis of patients with​ acute pancreatitis?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice to assess the prognosis of patients with acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MR‍CP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ER‍CP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CE‍CT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"E‍US","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CECT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fea8fdc87d5b4735b9d1ae023f97a93ax986x811.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33a57857c5a74014b21d4672966b2850x685x297.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61f79bbd5c584b4a950e5d27e3b85029x1280x1541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CECT</strong></p><p>The investigation of choice to assess prognosis in patients with <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong> is <strong>CECT</strong>. It is used to calculate the modified CT severity index.</p>\n<p>Plain erect chest x-ray and abdominal radiographs are not diagnostic of acute pancreatitis and can only be used to rule out certain differentials.<strong> Contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CECT)</strong> is the imaging investigation of choice, but may not always be needed. Various scoring systems are used to grade the severity of acute pancreatitis based on blood investigations and radiographic findings.</p>\n<p>The optimal time for the performance of contrast-enhanced CT imaging in cases of acute pancreatitis is <strong>more than 72 hours </strong>from the onset of symptoms. In the first 72 hours, CT may underestimate the extent of necrosis. CT scan in pancreatitis is <strong>indicated</strong> in patients with severe acute pancreatitis, organ failure, sepsis, and patients with clinical deterioration despite the treatment.</p>\n<p>Below is a CT image showing an inflamed pancreas with peripancreatic inflammatory fat stranding:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Below are CT images showing extensive parenchymal calcification in pancreas:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The most sensitive investigation for <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong> is<strong> EUS</strong> (endoscopic ultrasound). <strong>Chronic pancreatitis</strong> is associated with a <strong>chain of lakes</strong> appearance- dilated main duct with clubbing and dilatation of the side branches. </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>chain of lakes</strong> appearance on MRI:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0688","text":"MRCP of a patient being ev‍aluated for anorexia and ​jaundice is given below. ​What is the probable diag​nosis?","raw_text":"MRCP of a patient being evaluated for anorexia and jaundice is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreatic h‍ead carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ch‍olecystitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CBD ‍stone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sclerosing ‍cholangitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancreatic head carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ea826d940ac0492da8e416b3788b35cb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancreatic head carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>double duct sign</strong> and dilatation of pancreatic duct side branches (arrows), suggesting <strong>pancreatic head carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>double duct sign</strong> refers to the presence of <strong>simultaneous dilatation</strong> of the <strong>common bile duct</strong> and <strong>pancreatic ducts</strong>. Being an anatomical sign it can be seen on all modalities that can visualize the region, including MRI, CT, ultrasound, MRCP, and ERCP.</p>\n<p>Causes of the double duct sign are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carcinoma of the head of the <strong>pancreas</strong> and <strong>ampullary</strong> <strong>tumors</strong>. </li>\n<li>Occasionally an impacted <strong>gall stone</strong> in the distal duct, with associated edema, can also result in obstruction of the pancreatic duct leading to double duct appearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: A <strong>double-barrel</strong> sign in USG is seen in <strong>CBD</strong> obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0689","text":"A 54-year-old man presents‍ with abdominal pain, ano​rexia, and weight loss. A​ barium study was perform​ed and is as shown. What ‍is the probable diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old man presents with abdominal pain, anorexia, and weight loss. A barium study was performed and is as shown. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoma head of pancreas‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant du‍odenal ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma ‍of pylorus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carcinoma head of pancreas","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b65ab54c0d44a588971bfd282c5a0a8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f624e5f5175b4510aa6010670e2f5069x607x628.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carcinoma head of pancreas</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the barium study showing an enlarged duodenal loop with '<strong>reversed 3 sign' of Frostberg</strong> is suggestive of <strong>carcinoma head of the pancreas.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas</strong> arise most commonly in the <strong>head</strong> of the gland. Pancreatic carcinoma presents with varied symptoms, commonly including weight loss and anorexia, which may or may not be accompanied by abdominal pain. Tumours in the pancreatic head often cause <strong>early bile duct obstruction</strong>, leading to jaundice. </p>\n<p><strong> </strong><strong>Frostberg's inverted 3 sign</strong> is a sign seen on a barium examination. There is effacement and distortion of the mucosal pattern on the medial wall of the second part of the duodenum due to focal mass and local edema. It is associated with <strong>carcinoma of the head of the pancreas</strong>, duodenal carcinoma and pancreatitis (widening of the C loop of the duodenum). </p>\n<p><strong>Antral pad sign</strong> may also be seen in barium studies in patients with carcinoma head of the pancreas. Percutaneous transhepatic cholangiogram shows a characteristic<strong> 'gloved finger'</strong> obstruction of the intrapancreatic common bile duct, which is pathognomonic for carcinoma of the pancreatic head. </p>\n<p>The image of a barium meal showing an inverted 3 sign is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0690","text":"Which of the following con‍ditions is associated wit​h a spongy appearance wit​h sunburst calcification ​on the CT abdomen?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with a spongy appearance with sunburst calcification on the CT abdomen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreatic a‍denocarcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous cystadenocarcinom‍a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosquamo‍us carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serous cystadenoma of panc‍reas","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serous cystadenoma of pancreas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28a6a6b1b32e4432851e207c81f1b952x720x559.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b075ffc9918a4c8885b99d8bf5f86c9ax1280x955.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serous cystadenoma of pancreas</strong></p><p style=\"background: white;\">The<strong> spongy appearance</strong> with <strong>central sunburst calcification</strong> on the CT abdomen is seen in <strong>serous cystadenoma</strong> of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>CT findings of serous cystadenoma of the pancreas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically demonstrates a multicystic, lobulated mass in the pancreatic head sometimes described as a '<strong>bunch of grapes</strong>'.</li>\n<li>A characteristic enhancing central scar may be present which can show associated <strong>stellate calcification</strong> (present in ~20% of cases)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below are the CT abdomen showing sunburst calcification suggestive of serous cystadenoma of the pancreas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0694","text":"A 10-year old boy presente‍d with jaundice and right​ upper abdominal pain. An​ abdominal CT was done an​d is as shown. What is th‍e probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year old boy presented with jaundice and right upper abdominal pain. An abdominal CT was done and is as shown. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepa‍toma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heman‍gioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caroli's‍ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydati‍d cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caroli's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/554c6f5393ad4f509d7b6c479840243b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f5f0703264d47afbc7eda39a1d55879x1280x1094.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5fde3cc83a74daea0aa646095a8626ax720x454.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caroli&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the CT showing a central dot sign suggest <strong>Caroli's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The central dot sign of Caroli’s disease is a central enhancing fibrovascular bundle containing a<strong> portal vein</strong> surrounded by a focally dilated intrahepatic bile duct cyst. The arrows in the image below point to the central dot sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Caroli disease is a congenital disorder comprising of <strong>multifocal cystic dilatation</strong> of segmental <strong>intrahepatic</strong> bile ducts. It is also classified as a <strong>type V choledochal cyst</strong>, according to Todani classification. They present with a triad of jaundice, a palpable upper quadrant mass, and abdominal pain. </p>\n<p><strong>Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI/MRCP)</strong> is the investigation of choice, revealing the anatomy of the ducts. CT delineates the extent of the intra- or extrahepatic dilatation. ERCP defines the distal biliary tree. CT findings in Caroli disease are characterised by multiple hypodense rounded areas that are inseparable from the dilated intrahepatic bile ducts and central dot sign.</p>\n<p>Given below is the MRI abdomen of Caroli's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4010","text":"The following scan is of a‍ young woman on oral cont​raceptives. The most like​ly diagnosis is:","raw_text":"The following scan is of a young woman on oral contraceptives. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocellul‍ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastatic ‍infiltrates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic‍ adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Heman‍gioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatic adenoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e87a5e0556c47bfa2bde0398353abd4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21a59ad710764a138f305d6823fadf54x404x267.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/374721497b3c40b4adb5db89b7a71923x654x765.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c1fcb6522c241cd9e5bf3175959749ex800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f55078195ef47baa92bf51843eeeb49x1280x928.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatic adenoma</strong></p><p>The image given is of <strong>hepatic adenoma.</strong>  It is the most frequent hepatic tumour in young women on oral contraceptive pills. </p>\n<p>Hepatic adenomas, also referred to as <strong>hepatocellular adenomas</strong>, are <strong>benign</strong> liver tumours. They are well-defined vascular lesions, which are generally solitary. They are almost always seen in <strong>women</strong> between the age of 25 and 50 years and are associated with the use of the <strong>oral contraceptive pill.</strong></p>\n<p>On CT, uncomplicated adenomas are usually <strong>homogeneous</strong>, with a well-defined margin. They <strong>enhance markedly</strong> and uniformly during the <strong>arterial phase</strong> and then <strong>merge</strong> with the surrounding liver during the <strong>portal phase.</strong></p>\n<p>Hepatic adenoma on the CT scan is shown below.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong>  </strong>On contrast-enhanced CT scan<strong>, hemangiomas </strong>show <strong>asymmetrical, nodular peripheral enhancement </strong>that is isodense with large vessels and progressive<strong> centripetal enhancement. </strong>Liver hemangioma on CT scan is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Most <strong>metastatic deposits</strong> appear as areas of low attenuation on unenhanced CT and portal-phase CT. Hypervascular tumours are often visible as <strong>low-attenuation</strong> lesions on unenhanced images and may enhance transiently in the arterial phase, some becoming invisible in the portal phase. Metastatic infiltrates in the liver in the portal phase on CT scan are shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> (HCC) typically shows a decreased signal on T1-weighted images and a moderately increased signal on T2-weighted images, with internal heterogenecity. <strong>MRI </strong>is regarded as the most sensitive imaging modality for detecting HCC. Given below is the MRI image of HCC:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0687","text":"Which of the following sig‍ns is unlikely to be seen​ in the radiograph of a p​atient suspected to have ​splenic rupture?","raw_text":"Which of the following signs is unlikely to be seen in the radiograph of a patient suspected to have splenic rupture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obliteration of splenic ou‍tline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of ps‍oas shadow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevation of left hemidiap‍hragm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kehr'‍s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kehr's sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d64239ef839470bb35682f9458fbc0fx924x860.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce6cf44ceeb44005829bec77eda2ade1x1102x1138.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kehr&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p><strong>Kehr's sign</strong> is a <strong>clinical</strong> finding seen in splenic rupture and not a radiographic finding. </p>\n<p>Kehr's sign refers to<strong> referred pain</strong> in the<strong> tip </strong>of the <strong>left shoulder</strong> when firm pressure is applied over the left upper quadrant. This is commonly due to diaphragmatic irritation resulting from blood accumulation beneath the left diaphragm in cases of suspected <strong>splenic rupture</strong>. It is also elicited by raising the left leg. </p>\n<p>The <strong>spleen</strong> is the visceral organ most commonly injured in <strong>blunt trauma abdomen</strong>. Severely ill patients may present with hypovolemic shock manifesting as tachycardia, hypotension, and pallor. A splenic rupture may not always be clinically apparent and spontaneous splenic rupture or pathological rupture can occur after minor trauma or insignificant events.</p>\n<p>Signs of <strong>splenic rupture</strong> on plain X-ray are <strong>obliteration of splenic outline</strong>, <strong>loss of psoas shadow</strong>, <strong>elevation of left hemidiaphragm</strong> and fracture of lower ribs on left side. The <strong>investigation</strong> of choice in<strong> </strong>blunt trauma abdomen<strong> </strong>is<strong> CECT</strong>. Splenic lacerations are linear low-attenuation parenchymal defects and are almost always associated with haemoperitoneum.</p>\n<p>The image below is a CT abdomen showing splenic laceration and hematoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below is a CECT abdomen showing a splenic laceration.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zBPQxhLq","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"11_carcinoma_breast___treatment","title":"11.Carcinoma Breast   Treatment","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MC7094","text":"What procedure is depicted​ in the image?","raw_text":"What procedure is depicted in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brachy​therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral pec​toral block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"USG-guid​ed biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sentinel lymph node biopsy​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sentinel lymph node biopsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1080a0881c734743b8e4dce7f556b55a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bcefb7f9537e4de286250de486df9ef1x1280x3063.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sentinel lymph node biopsy</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>sentinel lymph node biopsy </strong>using a gamma camera.</p>\n<p>The sentinel lymph node is the <strong>first draining lymph node</strong>. SLNB was first described in <strong>penile cancer</strong> by <strong>Cabana</strong>. Sentinel lymph node biopsy is only done on <strong>node-negative tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Methylene blue dye</strong> or radioisotope i.e.,<strong> technetium-labeled albumin</strong> is injected into breast tissue (or subdermal tissue) at the site of the primary tumor or at the subareolar position. The sentinel node is identified as a <strong>blue node</strong> or as a <strong>radioactive node</strong> (using a gamma camera). The frozen section biopsy is sent to the pathologist for identification of cancer cells. If the <strong>biopsy is positive</strong> for cancer cells, then <strong>axillary dissection</strong> is simultaneously performed. </p>\n<p>Note: The combination of blue dye and technetium-labeled colloid has been reported to improve the detection of sentinel lymph nodes. Newer techniques involve the use of a fluorescent or magnetic tracer instead of the radiolabelled dye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0130","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding sentinel l‍ymph node biopsy in a pat‌ient with a T2 N1 M0 brea‌st tumour?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding sentinel lymph node biopsy in a patient with a T2 N1 M0 breast tumour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If biopsy is positive, the​n axillary dissection is ‍not done","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If biopsy is negative, the​n axillary dissection is ‍done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If biopsy is positive then​ tumour free margin shoul‍d be &amp;gt; 2 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not​ indicated","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is not indicated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is not indicated</strong></p><p>Sentinel lymph node biopsy is only done in <strong>node-negative</strong> tumors. It is<strong> not</strong> done in<strong> N1</strong> stage tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4009","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen​ts with a lump in her rig‍ht breast. On examination‌, a 3 cm palpable mass is‌ felt in the right upper ​quadrant of the right bre‍ast. A mammogram is sugge‌stive of a malignant lesi‌on. Biopsy confirmed inva‍sive ductal carcinoma wit‌h no nodal involvement. H‌ow will you manage this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents with a lump in her right breast. On examination, a 3 cm palpable mass is felt in the right upper quadrant of the right breast. A mammogram is suggestive of a malignant lesion. Biopsy confirmed invasive ductal carcinoma with no nodal involvement. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Modified radical mastectom​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adjuvant chemotherapy with​ mastectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast conservation therap​y with adjuvant radiother‍apy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Modified radical mastectom​y with adjuvant radiother‍apy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Breast conservation therapy with adjuvant radiotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Breast conservation therapy with adjuvant radiotherapy</strong></p><p>This patient has <strong>stage</strong> <strong>T2 breast carcinoma</strong>. These patients are managed with <strong>breast conservation therapy with adjuvant radiotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> of breast conservation surgery <strong>(BCS):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">DCIS/Tis tumors</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">T1-2 tumors</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No contraindication for adjuvant radiotherapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Excision with clear margins is possible</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Who does not want a mastectomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Locally advanced tumors with excellent response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1009","text":"Breast conservative surger​y can be performed in whi‍ch of the following patie‌nts with breast carcinoma‌? A post-partum woman A s​mall breasted woman with ‍a large lump A woman with‌ a tumor that has a persi‌stently positive patholog‍ic margin A woman with di‌ffuse micro-calcification‌ on a mammogram A woman w‌ith a history of therapeu​tic radiation exposure to​ the chest wall","raw_text":"<p>Breast conservative surgery can be performed in which of the following patients with breast carcinoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A post-partum woman</li>\n<li>A small breasted woman with a large lump</li>\n<li>A woman with a tumor that has a persistently positive pathologic margin</li>\n<li>A woman with diffuse micro-calcification on a mammogram</li>\n<li>A woman with a history of therapeutic radiation exposure to the chest wall</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3, ​4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onl​y 1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Only 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Only 1</strong></p><p>Breast conservation surgery can be done in a post-partum woman.</p>\n<p>Breast cancer diagnosed during the<strong> second or third trimester </strong>can be managed with<strong><strong> breast conservation surgery </strong></strong>followed by <strong><strong>adjuvant chemotherapy. </strong></strong></p>\n<p>For breast cancer diagnosed <strong>late</strong> in the <strong>third trimester,</strong> breast conservation surgery can be performed, deferring<strong> breast irradiation until after delivery.</strong></p>\n<div>Breast cancer diagnosed during the <strong>first trimester</strong> should be treated with <strong>mastectomy</strong>. Breast conservation surgery is contraindicated in the first trimester since post-operative radiotherapy cannot be deferred for a long time and chemotherapy is contraindicated in the first trimester.</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0128","text":"A patient with early breas​t carcinoma of stage T3 N‍0 M0 does not want to und‌ergo mastectomy. What is ‌the next best treatment o​f choice in her case?","raw_text":"A patient with early breast carcinoma of stage T3 N0 M0 does not want to undergo mastectomy. What is the next best treatment of choice in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast conservation surger​y followed by radiation t‍herapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemotherapy alone since m​astectomy is the only opt‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy f​ollowed by breast conserv‍ation surgery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mastectomy only since it i​s a large operable tumour‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by breast conservation surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by breast conservation surgery</strong></p><p>According to the clinical scenario, <strong>neoadjuvant chemotherapy</strong> followed by breast conservation surgery (<strong>BCS</strong>) is the next best treatment.</p>\n<p>Stage <strong>T3 N0 M0</strong> tumors are lesions of size &gt;5 cm and are considered as <strong>large operable breast cancer (LOBC).</strong> The best management for such tumors is<strong> mastectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In case the patient is <strong>not willing</strong> for <strong>mastectomy,</strong> the next best treatment is neoadjuvant chemotherapy to shrink the tumor, followed by breast conservation surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0989","text":"You are assisting in a mod​ified radical mastectomy.‍ Which of the following s‌teps will not be done by ‌the operating surgeon dur​ing this procedure?","raw_text":"You are assisting in a modified radical mastectomy. Which of the following steps will not be done by the operating surgeon during this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Removal of pectoralis majo​r muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Level 2 axillary lymph nod​es dissection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Removal of nipple-areola c​omplex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Removal of pectoral fascia​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Removal of pectoralis major muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Removal of pectoralis major muscle</strong></p><p>Pectoralis major is not removed in modified radical mastectomy.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures removed in modified radical mastectomy</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Whole breast tissue</li>\n<li>The skin over the breast tissue</li>\n<li>Nipple-areola complex</li>\n<li>Pectoral fascia</li>\n<li>Levels I, II, ± III axillary lymph nodes</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The pectoralis <strong>minor</strong> muscle is either cut (<strong>Patey's, Scanlon's</strong> modification) or retracted (<strong>Auchincloss</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1016","text":"In patients with which of ​the following features of‍ tumor involvement should‌ you not perform radical ‌mastectomy?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following features of tumor involvement should you not perform radical mastectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pectoralis major involveme​nt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distant m​etastasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Axillary lymph node involv​ement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inter pectoral lymph node ​involvement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Distant metastasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Distant metastasis</strong></p><p>Distant metastasis is a <strong>contraindication</strong> for radical mastectomy.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Excision of the breast, the pectoralis major and minor muscles, and all axillary lymph nodes constitute radical mastectomy. It is seldom performed given that it causes <strong>substantial morbidity</strong> due to shoulder joint restriction, pain, significant upper limb lymphedema, and chest wall deformity, with <strong>no survival</strong> advantage when compared to less radical surgery. In the case of distant metastases,  less radical surgery is of<strong> no benefit</strong> either.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0997","text":"Which of the following str​uctures will not be remov‍ed while performing radic‌al mastectomy in patients‌ with breast carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures will not be removed while performing radical mastectomy in patients with breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pectoral​is major","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pectoral​is minor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axillary l​ymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supraclavicular lymph node​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Supraclavicular lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Supraclavicular lymph nodes</strong></p><p>Supraclavicular lymph nodes are not removed in radical mastectomy.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Halsted radical mastectomy, </strong>the following structures are removed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All breast tissue and skin</li>\n<li>Nipple-areolar complex</li>\n<li><strong>Pectoralis major</strong> and pectoralis minor muscles</li>\n<li>Level I, II, and III axillary lymph nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4011","text":"A 50-year-old patient unde​rwent a modified radical ‍mastectomy for breast can‌cer. A drain was inserted‌ at the site of the surge​ry. When can the drain be‍ removed?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient underwent a modified radical mastectomy for breast cancer. A drain was inserted at the site of the surgery. When can the drain be removed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 hr outpu​t &amp;lt;100 cc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Output &amp;lt;100 cc for 2 co​nsecutive days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 hr output​ &amp;lt;30-40 cc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Output &amp;lt;30-40 cc for 2 ​consecutive days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Output <30-40 cc for 2 consecutive days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Output &lt;30-40 cc for 2 consecutive days</strong></p><p>The <strong>drain</strong> after <strong>mastectomy</strong> is <strong>removed</strong> when the <strong>output</strong> is <strong>&lt;30-40 cc for 2 consecutive days. </strong></p>\n<p>The closed system suction drains are put in to <strong>prevent</strong> the formation of <strong>seroma. </strong>It is the <strong>most common complication</strong> of mastectomy and axillary lymph node dissection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0129","text":"The following image shows ​the boundaries of axillar‍y lymph node dissection. ‌The inferior boundary mar‌ked as ‘A’ denotes _____.​","raw_text":"The following image shows the boundaries of axillary lymph node dissection. The inferior boundary marked as ‘A’ denotes _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axilla​ry vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoracodor​sal pedicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angula​r vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Costococlavicular ligament​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Angular vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6205455158c644d6bfdb944379a58d04.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9b7d5fc60a14520a7c43badeb6fe232x639x628.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Angular vein</strong></p><p>The space of axillary lymph node dissection is <strong>inferiorly</strong> bounded by the <strong>angular vein</strong> (marked as ‘A’).</p>\n<p><strong>Boundaries</strong> of axillary lymph node dissection (ALND):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior - Axillary vein</li>\n<li>Lateral - Thoracodorsal pedicle</li>\n<li>Medial - Costoclavicular ligament (Halstead ligament)</li>\n<li>Inferior - Angular vein    </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for ALND:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Locally advanced (T4a, b, c) or inflammatory breast cancer</li>\n<li>Metastatic disease in axillary lymph node(s) proven by needle biopsy, clinically palpable or nonpalpable nodes which are abnormal on imaging</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Structures preserved</strong> during ALND:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lateral and medial pectoral nerves</li>\n<li>Thoracodorsal pedicle</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1037","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common complicat‍ion of mastectomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common complication of mastectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ser​oma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymph​edema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intercostobrachial nerve d​amage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wound i​nfection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seroma</strong></p><p>The most common complication post-mastectomy is the development of a<strong> seroma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Seroma is a collection of fluid in a surgically created cavity, that results from the <strong>transection</strong> of <strong>vessels and lymphatics</strong>. It presents as a localized, well-circumscribed swelling with discomfort. The fluid is clear, yellow, and viscous.</p>\n<p>Placement of <strong>closed suction drains</strong> that obliterate the space between the pectoralis major and skin flap is used to <strong>prevent </strong>seromas.</p>\n<p>Other complications include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Wound infection</li>\n<li>Skin flap breakdown or necrosis</li>\n<li>Lymphedema</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1039","text":"After modified radical mas​tectomy, a patient compla‍ined of loss of sensation‌ over the medial aspect o‌f the right upper arm. Th​e nerve most likely damag‍ed is ______.","raw_text":"After modified radical mastectomy, a patient complained of loss of sensation over the medial aspect of the right upper arm. The nerve most likely damaged is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intercostobr​achial nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial cutaneous nerve of ​arm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long thor​acic nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intercostobrachial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9fe4aa2edc94b96befa579f249d80c0x1280x1683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intercostobrachial nerve</strong></p><p>Resection of the <strong>intercostobrachial nerve</strong> (ICBN) causes loss of sensation over the medial aspect of the upper arm. The intercostobrachial nerve is the lateral cutaneous branch of the second intercostal nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1388","text":"A patient underwent surger​y for breast carcinoma. S‍he now presents with pain‌less swelling involving t‌he arm as shown in the im​age below. What is the mo‍st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient underwent surgery for breast carcinoma. She now presents with painless swelling involving the arm as shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph​edema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphang​iosarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous t​hrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cellu​litis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphedema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/67451aee83c247989f7c4d258187fb6b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphedema</strong></p><p>The generalized swelling of the left arm of the patient post-surgery for breast carcinoma is suggestive of <strong>post-mastectomy</strong> <strong>lymphedema.</strong></p>\n<p>Lymphoedema occurs when interstitial fluid accumulates due to defective lymphatic drainage. It is a common complication of breast cancer treatment, involving <strong>axillary lymph node dissection</strong> and <strong>radiotherapy.</strong><strong> Sentinel lymph node dissection</strong> (SLND) reduces the risk of lymphedema significantly.</p>\n<p>Clinical signs and symptoms are observed in the <strong>ipsilateral arm.</strong> A significant increase in limb size due to swelling is seen. Other symptoms are heaviness, tightness, numbness, tingling, limb fatigue, and reduced limb mobility.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is predominantly based on the clinical swelling of the arm. The girth of the affected arm &gt;2 centimetres than the normal limb, a 200-mL or more increase in limb volume, or a 5% or greater limb volume change are other diagnostic criteria.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Limb elevation</strong>, <strong>elastic arm stockings</strong>, and<strong> pneumatic compression devices</strong> to reduce swelling. </li>\n<li>Using a <strong>low-pH water-based moisturizer </strong>to prevent dry skin caused by excess fluid accumulation. </li>\n<li><strong>Manual lymphatic drainage</strong> (MLD) by a therapist followed by <strong>simple lymphatic drainage</strong> (SLD) by the patient daily aids interstitial fluid evacuation.</li>\n<li>Surgical options include <strong>lymphaticovenular anastomosis</strong>, liposuction, and debulking.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B:   <strong>Stewart-Treves syndrome</strong> refers to <strong>lymphangiosarcoma</strong> seen in chronic <strong>long-standing</strong> lymphedema following mastectomy (more than 10 years). They present as single/multiple <strong>bluish-red</strong> skin and<strong> subcutaneous nodules.</strong> They can ulcerate and hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Axillary venous thrombosis</strong> or <strong>Paget-Schrotter disease</strong> is seen in patients doing rigorous exercise and in weightlifters. The compression of the subclavian vein at the thoracic outlet causes <strong>p</strong><strong>ainful</strong> swelling of the arm.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cellulitis </strong>(lymphangitis) is a bacterial infection of the <strong>skin and subcutaneous tissue</strong>. It is characterized by a <strong>painful</strong> area of <strong>erythematous</strong> and <strong>edematous</strong> tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1038","text":"A patient presented with a​cute worsening of edema o‍f the left hand, which wa‌s present for the past 5 ‌years. Her surgical histo​ry was relevant for a rad‍ical mastectomy with radi‌ation therapy for left-si‌ded breast carcinoma. On ‍examination, the limb app‌ears as shown below. What‌ is the likely diagnosis ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"A  patient presented with acute worsening of edema of the left hand, which was present for the past 5 years. Her surgical history was relevant for a radical mastectomy with radiation therapy for left-sided breast carcinoma. On examination, the limb appears as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teitze ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stewart-Tre​ves syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhabdomy​osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Milroy's​ Disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stewart-Treves syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c3ae663956f8488a91132a39114d34b9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stewart-Treves syndrome</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and image are suggestive of <strong>Stewart–Treves syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The development of <strong>angiosarcoma</strong> after a combination of <strong>postmastectomy lymphedema</strong> and <strong>radiation therapy</strong> is known as Stewart–Treves syndrome. It can arise de-novo or in association with post-mastectomy lymphedema. It presents with <strong>acute worsening of edema</strong> with subcutaneous nodules which are hemorrhagic or ulcerative.</p>\n<p>Treatment is <strong>preoperative chemotherapy and radiotherapy</strong> followed by <strong>surgical excision</strong> (radical amputation of arm). The prognosis is poor.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Tietze syndrome </strong>is an inflammatory condition characterized by chest pain and swelling of the cartilage around the ribs at the <strong>costochondral junction</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: Rhabdomyosarcoma is a rare type of cancer that affects the muscle tissue, mostly in children and adolescents.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Milroy's disease</strong> is a familial disease, caused by <strong>congenital </strong>abnormalities in the <strong>lymphatic system</strong>. Disruption of the normal drainage of lymph leads to fluid accumulation and hypertrophy of soft tissues. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0125","text":"Identify the flap shown in​ the image used for breas‍t reconstruction.","raw_text":"Identify the flap shown in the image used for breast reconstruction.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep inferior epigastric p​erforator flap","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse rectus abdomini​s myocutaneous flap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lattisimus​ dorsi flap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External o​blique flap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deep inferior epigastric perforator flap","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c0276157df47446fbe1dcc083a295b14.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd753d65292d41a180d9a45f7d268338x1280x1547.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bdd2c61a97a4c168f8f5164048f189ax1280x1041.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deep inferior epigastric perforator flap</strong></p><p>The image shows a deep inferior epigastric perforator <strong>(DIEP) </strong>flap<strong>. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is a free flap and a variant of TRAM flap where only the <strong>skin and fat </strong>are moved to the chest.</p>\n<p>Transverse rectus abdominis myocutaneous <strong>(TRAM) </strong>flap is either based on the superior or inferior epigastric artery or both (<strong>supercharged TRAM</strong>). In a TRAM flap, the skin, fat, <strong>and rectus muscle </strong>are moved to the chest. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">If robust DIEA perforators are identified with a good caliber and dissection can be performed without dividing the muscle, a DIEP flap is preferred. Otherwise, if small-caliber perforators are encountered and not aligned over the medial or lateral row, a TRAM flap is performed to ensure flap viability.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Latissimus dorsi (LD) flap</strong> is an axial flap based on the thoracodorsal pedicle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>DIEP flap, TRAM flap, and LD flap are autogenous tissues used in breast reconstruction. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1041","text":"Which of the following is ​the gold standard flap us‍ed in breast reconstructi‌on with autogenous tissue‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the gold standard flap used in breast reconstruction with autogenous tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep inferior epigastric p​erforators flap","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Latissimus dorsi musculocu​taneous flap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse rectus abdomini​s myocutaneous flap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoracoepig​astric flap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deep inferior epigastric perforators flap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deep inferior epigastric perforators flap</strong></p><p>The <strong>gold standard </strong>in breast reconstruction with <strong>autogenous</strong> tissue is the deep inferior epigastric perforator<strong> (DIEP) flap</strong>.</p>\n<p>In DIEP flap, using a microvascular technique<strong>, </strong>only<strong> </strong>subcutaneous fat and skin are taken, and <strong>no muscle is removed.</strong> As the muscle is spared, there is<strong> </strong>a <strong>decreased risk of abdominal wall weakness</strong> and its associated complications.</p>\n<p>Lattismus dorsi flap (<strong>LD flap</strong>) - does not provide the bulk necessary and an <strong>implant</strong> is usually <strong>required.</strong></p>\n<p>Transverse rectus abdominis myocutaneous flap (<strong>TRAM flap</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin, fat, and <strong>muscle</strong> are moved to the chest.</li>\n<li>It is the <strong>most commonly</strong> used flap.</li>\n<li>It provides <strong>good cosmesis</strong> and bulk. An implant is usually not required. </li>\n<li>Since the rectus abdominis muscle is excised in the procedure, it can lead to <strong>abdominal wall weakness</strong> and <strong>hernias</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The easiest type of reconstruction is using a <strong>silicone gel implant </strong>under the <strong>pectoralis major</strong> muscle. Capsular contraction is a dreaded complication of silicone implants.</p>\n<p>In breast reconstruction surgeries, the timing of reconstruction<strong> may be immediate or delayed</strong>. Impediments to immediate reconstruction include insufficient theatre time and a lack of experienced reconstructive surgeons. In addition, if the patient is likely to need post-op radiotherapy, then a delayed reconstruction using a flap often gives better results.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1019","text":"Which of the following wil​l you not add as part of ‍the chemotherapeutic regi‌me for a patient with bre‌ast cancer? Cyclophospham​ide Fludarabine Adriamyci‍n Paclitaxel Vincristine","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following will you not add as part of the chemotherapeutic regime for a patient with breast cancer?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n<li>Fludarabine</li>\n<li>Adriamycin</li>\n<li>Paclitaxel</li>\n<li>Vincristine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ​and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 5</strong></p><p>Fludarabine and vincristine are not used in chemotherapy for carcinoma breast.</p>\n<p>The present chemotherapy regimen used is <strong>ACT regimen</strong>, i.e., adriamycin, cyclophosphamide, and taxane (paclitaxel). It comprises 4 cycles of <strong>AC</strong> followed by 4 cycles of <strong>T.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Earlier</strong> regimens that are less commonly used currently are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CAF</strong> – Cyclophosphamide, Adriamycin, 5-Fluorouracil</li>\n<li><strong>CMF</strong> – Cyclophosphamide, Methotrexate, 5-Fluorouracil</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Indications </strong>for chemotherapy in carcinoma breast:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lymph nodes positive</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes negative but tumor size of &gt;1 cm</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes negative and tumor size of &gt;0.5 cm with\n<ol>\n<li>Blood vessel/Lymph vessel invasion</li>\n<li>ER/PR –ve</li>\n<li>Her-2/neu overexpression</li>\n<li>High grade</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Triple-negative breast carcinoma</li>\n<li>High recurrence score after Oncotype Dx.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1022","text":"All are aromatase inhibito​rs used in the treatment ‍of breast cancer except _‌_____.","raw_text":"All are aromatase inhibitors used in the treatment of breast cancer except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Letr​ozole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anast​rozole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exema​stane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fulve​strant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fulvestrant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fulvestrant</strong></p><p><strong>Fulvestrant</strong> is a selective estrogen-receptor down regulator (<strong>SERD</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Selec­tive aromatase inhibitors</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anastrozole</li>\n<li>Exemestane</li>\n<li>Letrozole</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are used for the treatment of breast cancer in <strong>postmenopausal women. </strong>Selec­tive aromatase inhibitors block the conversion of the hormone androstenedione into estrone by <strong>inhibiting</strong><strong> </strong>the <strong>aromatase enzyme</strong>. This is the main mechanism of the production of estrogen in postmenopausal women.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0121","text":"A woman presented to the O​PD with a painless lump i‍n the left breast. Biopsy‌ confirmed the presence o‌f ductal carcinoma. Immun​ohistochemistry showed ER‍ +ve, PR +ve, and HER2/ne‌u -ve. Which of the follo‌wing is the latest drug a‍pproved for treating this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A woman presented to the OPD with a painless lump in the left breast. Biopsy confirmed the presence of ductal carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry showed ER +ve, PR +ve, and HER2/neu -ve. Which of the following is the latest drug approved for treating this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herc​eptin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palbo​ciclib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bupar​lisib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ipata​sertib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palbociclib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palbociclib</strong></p><p><strong> Palbociclib</strong> is the newest drug approved for <strong>metastatic</strong>, hormone receptor–positive, HER-2/neu-negative <strong>breast cancer. </strong></p>\n<p>It is used along with fulvestrant, a selective estrogen-receptor downregulator (SERD) based on the <strong>PALOMA 3 trial. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1029","text":"What is the drug used for ​estrogen dependant breast‍ cancer?","raw_text":"What is the drug used for estrogen dependant breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clomiphen​e citrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tamo​xifen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adria​mycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tamoxifen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tamoxifen</strong></p><p><strong>Tamoxifen</strong> is a selective estrogen receptor modulator <strong>(SERM)</strong> that is mainly used for<strong> estrogen receptor-positive</strong> breast cancers in <strong>pre-menopausal women</strong>.</p>\n<p>It exhibits selective agonistic and antagonistic activity for ER receptors (ERα and ERβ):</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Agonist activity at the uterus, bone, liver, and pituitary</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antagonist activity in breast and blood vessels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Uses: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Part of the <strong>primary treatment</strong> regimen for estrogen receptor-positive <strong>(ER+) breast cancers</strong> in pre-menopausal women</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Adjuvant therapy </strong>after <strong>breast conservative surgery</strong> to prevent a recurrence</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tamoxifen</strong> also is effective (a 40%–50% reduction in tumor incidence) in <strong>preventing</strong> ER+ breast cancer in <strong>women at increased risk</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adverse effects: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vasomotor symptoms <strong>(hot flashes) </strong>and sweating</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Endometrial cancer</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased risk of <strong>thromboembolic events</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Fatty liver disease</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0124","text":"A middle-aged woman underw​ent a trucut biopsy for t‍he suspicion of malignanc‌y on a mammogram. Microsc‌opy showed the presence o​f invasive breast carcino‍ma. IHC staining of the t‌umor is given below. Whic‌h of the following drugs ‍will you add along with c‌hemotherapeutic agents fo‌r treating this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman underwent a trucut biopsy for the suspicion of malignancy on a mammogram. Microscopy showed the presence of invasive breast carcinoma. IHC staining of the tumor is given below. Which of the following drugs will you add along with chemotherapeutic agents for treating this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tamo​xifen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tamoxifen +​ Trastuzumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fulve​strant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palbo​ciclib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tamoxifen + Trastuzumab","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/07321cd03fa74168936aef8d9bc7489b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65e64b983f5540fc95708285043d0cc5x243x199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tamoxifen + Trastuzumab</strong></p><p>The immunohistochemistry staining shown in the above image is ER+, PR+, and HER2+, which is <strong>triple</strong> <strong>positive</strong> or <strong>luminal type B</strong>. <strong>Chemotherapy</strong> along with <strong>endocrine therapy</strong>, and <strong>trastuzumab</strong> is the adjuvant treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>Positive immunohistochemistry staining is indicated by the presence of brown-colored tissue.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Estrogen receptor <strong>(ER)</strong> and Progesterone receptor <strong>(PR)</strong> are both <strong>nuclear</strong> receptors, hence, the nucleus is stained brown if positive.</li>\n<li>The human epidermal growth factor receptor type 2<strong> (HER2)</strong> is a membrane receptor, hence the cell <strong>membrane</strong> is stained brown if positive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows positive and negative staining for ER, PR, and HER2.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The adjuvant treatment modalities currently in practice include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endocrine treatment</strong> such as tamoxifen or ovarian suppression. It is given for patients with tumors that are hormone receptor-positive (either ER+ or PR+).</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-HER2</strong> therapy with trastuzumab (humanized monoclonal antibody). It is given for patients with tumors that are HER2 overexpressing.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> is given for hormone negative, HER2+, and ER+ tumors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The table below shows the summary of the adjuvant treatment approach:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 99px; width: 735px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\"> </td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 517px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tumor hormone receptor status (ER,PR) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\"><strong>Tumor HER2/neu status</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.703125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.296875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Negative</strong>  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\"><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.703125px;\">Endocrine therapy + chemotherapy + trastuzumab</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.296875px;\">Chemotherapy + trastuzumab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\"><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 267.703125px;\">Endocrine therapy ± chemotherapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.296875px;\">Chemotherapy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: <strong>Palbociclib</strong> is used along with hormone therapy in ER +ve, PR +ve, HER2/neu -ve tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4010","text":"A 60 yr old woman underwen​t a simple mastectomy fol‍lowed by radiation therap‌y for ER/PR positive brea‌st cancer. Which of the f​ollowing is the most appr‍opriate drug to be given ‌to her to prevent the rec‌urrence of breast cancer?‍","raw_text":"A 60 yr old woman underwent a simple mastectomy followed by radiation therapy for ER/PR positive breast cancer. Which of the following is the most appropriate drug to be given to her to prevent the recurrence of breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No drug is effective to pr​event recurrence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tamo​xifen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trast​uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anast​rozole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anastrozole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anastrozole</strong></p><p><strong>Anastrozole</strong> is an <strong>aromatase inhibitor</strong> and it is the drug of choice for the <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>recurrence</strong> of <strong>ER/PR positive breast cancer</strong> in <strong>post-menopausal women</strong> (usually &gt;50 years). It is given for 10 years.</p>\n<p><strong>Tamoxifen</strong> is a selective estrogen receptor modulator <strong>(SERM)</strong> and it is the drug of choice for preventing recurrence in <strong>pre-menopausal women.</strong> It has an agonistic action on the endometrium, leading to an <strong>increased risk</strong> of <strong>endometrial cancers.</strong> Hence, it is not used in post-menopausal women.</p>\n<p><strong>Trastuzumab</strong> is a her2/neu receptor antagonist. It is given in patients with <strong>her2/neu status positive.</strong> It would not be effective in this patient. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0126","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about accelerate‍d partial breast irradiat‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about accelerated partial breast irradiation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irradiation is delivered o​nly at the lumpectomy sit‍e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only suitable for women ol​der than or equal to 65 y‍ears of age","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is used for low-risk DC​IS patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is suitable if surgical​ margins are negative by ‍≥ 2 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Only suitable for women older than or equal to 65 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Only suitable for women older than or equal to 65 years of age</strong></p><p>Accelerated partial breast irradiation (<strong>APBI</strong>)<strong> </strong>is a <strong>localized </strong>form of<strong> radiation</strong> delivered <strong>after lumpectomy</strong> to only the part of the breast where the tumor was removed. According to the updated guidelines, patients of age <strong>greater than or equal to 50 years</strong> are considered suitable for APBI.</p>\n<p>Patients of age &lt;40 years are considered <strong>unsuitable</strong>, while those between 40 and 49 years of age are included in the <strong>cautionary</strong> group<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>According to the recent American Society for Radiation Oncology (ASTRO) guidelines, the following patients are considered suitable for APBI:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients of age <strong>50 years or older</strong></li>\n<li>Patients with <strong>low-risk DCIS</strong>. Low-risk DCIS includes patients satisfying the following criteria-\n<ol>\n<li>Screen-detected disease</li>\n<li>Low to intermediate nuclear grade</li>\n<li><strong>Tumor size ≤ 2.5 cm</strong></li>\n<li>Surgical resection with margins negative by ≥ 3mm</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Patients with surgical margins negative by ≥ 2mm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3938","text":"A postmenopausal woman pre​sented with a 4 cm lump i‍n her left breast. Clinic‌al and radiological exami‌nation revealed ipsilater​al fixed axillary lymph n‍ode involvement without d‌istant metastasis. Histop‌athology confirms invasiv‍e breast carcinoma. Immun‌ohistochemistry is positi‌ve for ER, PR but negativ‌e for HER-2/Neu. What is ​the best method of manage​ment for this patient? Ne​oadjuvant chemotherapy Ma‌stectomy Radiation therap‌y Trastuzumab Letrozole","raw_text":"<p>A postmenopausal woman presented with a 4 cm lump in her left breast. Clinical and radiological examination revealed ipsilateral fixed axillary lymph node involvement without distant metastasis. Histopathology confirms invasive breast carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry is positive for ER, PR but negative for HER-2/Neu. What is the best method of management for this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Neoadjuvant chemotherapy</li>\n<li>Mastectomy </li>\n<li>Radiation therapy</li>\n<li>Trastuzumab</li>\n<li>Letrozole </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,3​ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ​3 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>The best method of management in this patient is <strong>neoadjuvant chemotherapy, mastectomy, radiation</strong> therapy, and<strong> letrozole.</strong></p>\n<p>Step 1: Evaluate the TNM staging and stage grouping of the tumor</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumor size 4cm - <strong>T2</strong></li>\n<li>Ipsilateral fixed axillary lymph nodes are positive - <strong>N2a</strong></li>\n<li>No distant metastasis - <strong>M0</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>T2N2M0 belongs to the stage group IIIA which is considered<strong> locally advanced breast cancer (LABC).</strong> They are treated by neoadjuvant chemotherapy, mastectomy, and radiation therapy.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Evaluate the receptor status</p>\n<p>The immunohistochemistry is positive for estrogen and progesterone receptors. Therefore the patient has to be started with hormonal therapy with drugs like letrozole, anastrozole, tamoxifen etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Trastuzumab / Herceptin</strong> is a HER-2/Neu blocker and as the patient is HER2/Neu negative, it would not be effective.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7590","text":"A 41-year-old woman underw​ent treatment for breast ‍cancer of 1.2 X 1 cm with‌ positive axillary nodes.‌ She completed radiothera​py and chemotherapy and i‍s now receiving tamoxifen‌. How would you follow up‌ this patient?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old woman underwent treatment for breast cancer of 1.2 X 1 cm with positive axillary nodes. She completed radiotherapy and chemotherapy and is now receiving tamoxifen. How would you follow up this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annual m​ammogram","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Regular checking of LFT on​ce in 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Follow-up with tumour mark​ers once in 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yearly b​one scans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Annual mammogram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Annual mammogram</strong></p><p>Follow-up of patients after treatment of breast cancer consists of a <strong>physical examination</strong> once in <strong>3-6 months </strong>for the<strong> first 3 years</strong> and an <strong>annual mammogram.</strong></p>\n<p>Mammography is the only imaging modality necessary for follow-up after breast cancer surgery. There is currently no role for tumor markers, serum investigations, or any other imaging studies like bone scans in routine follow-ups.</p>\n<p>Guidelines for <strong>follow-up after primary treatment</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Physical examination</strong>: every 3-6 months for the first 3 years, every 6-12 months for years 4 and 5, and annually thereafter.<br/><strong>Mammography</strong>: initially, 6 months after surgery, and thereafter, annually.<br/><strong>Breast self-examination:</strong> Monthly <br/><strong>Pelvic examination</strong>: Annually in patients taking tamoxifen</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1020","text":"Post-operative radiotherap​y is given in a patient w‍ith breast carcinoma main‌ly to _____.","raw_text":"Post-operative radiotherapy is given in a patient with breast carcinoma mainly to _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevent m​etastasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Induce ablation of the rem​nant cancer tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevent r​ecurrence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Induce ablation of the aff​ected nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prevent recurrence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prevent recurrence</strong></p><p>The main aim of radiotherapy in breast cancer is to <strong>prevent local recurrence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiotherapy is mainly given in the following cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positive lymph nodes</li>\n<li>After breast conservation surgery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1036","text":"Which of the following is ​the most likely site for ‍hematogenous spread in a ‌patient with breast carci‌noma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most likely site for hematogenous spread in a patient with breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lumbar v​ertebrae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sk​ull","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thoracic ​vertebrae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fe​mur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lumbar vertebrae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lumbar vertebrae</strong></p><p><strong>Lumbar vertebrae</strong>, followed by the femur and thoracic vertebrae, are the most common sites for <strong>hematogenous spread</strong> of carcinoma breast. Bone metastases in breast cancer are more <strong>osteolytic</strong> than osteoblastic.</p>\n<p>The most common bony sites of blood-borne metastases are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar vertebrae</li>\n<li>Femur</li>\n<li>Thoracic vertebrae</li>\n<li>Ribs</li>\n<li>Skull</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In <strong>prostate</strong> cancer, the bone metastases are more <strong>osteoblastic</strong> than osteolytic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0983","text":"A pregnant woman at 11-wee​ks of gestational age pre‍sents with a painless lum‌p in the upper quadrant o‌f her left breast. Ultras​ound and trucut biopsy re‍vealed the presence of in‌vasive ductal carcinoma. ‌What is the next best ste‍p in the management of th‌is patient?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at 11-weeks of gestational age presents with a painless lump in the upper quadrant of her left breast. Ultrasound and trucut biopsy revealed the presence of invasive ductal carcinoma. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform immediate breast c​onservation therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perform mastectomy and sta​rt chemotherapy immediate‍ly after delivery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mastectomy followed by hor​monal therapy in the seco‍nd and third trimester","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mastectomy followed by che​motherapy in the second a‍nd third trimester","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mastectomy followed by chemotherapy in the second and third trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mastectomy followed by chemotherapy in the second and third trimester</strong></p><p><strong>Mastectomy </strong>followed by <strong>chemotherapy in the second and third trimester</strong> is the best step of management in a pregnant woman diagnosed to have breast cancer in the first trimester. </p>\n<p><strong>Pregnancy-associated breast cancer</strong> is defined as breast cancer that is diagnosed <strong>during</strong> <strong>pregnancy or within 1 year</strong> postpartum. It is the most common non-gy­necologic malignancy associated with pregnancy. Most patients present with a <strong>painless mass</strong> in the breast with or without bloody nipple discharge.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is done by ultrasonography and tissue biopsy.</p>\n<p>Treatment of breast cancer in pregnancy involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mastectomy</strong> in any trimester along with <strong>chemotherapy</strong> in the second and third trimesters.</li>\n<li><strong>Radiotherapy </strong>and<strong> hormonal therapies</strong> are absolutely <strong>contraindicated</strong> during pregnancy because of teratogenicity. They can be initiated after delivery. </li>\n<li>Since radiotherapy is a component of <strong>breast conservation therapy</strong>, it is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in the first trimester of pregnancy. However, breast-conserving lumpectomy and axillary lymph node dissection with chemotherapy can be done in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy followed by radiation after delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4102","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen​ted with back pain that w‍as worse during the night‌. After evaluation, she w‌as diagnosed with a recur​rence of ER/PR+ breast ca‍ncer with bony metastasis‌. Which of the following ‌would be the first-line t‍reatment for this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with back pain that was worse during the night. After evaluation, she was diagnosed with a recurrence of ER/PR+ breast cancer with bony metastasis. Which of the following would be the first-line treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radiot​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemotherap​y + Surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemotherapy + Hormonal Th​erapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hormonal​ therapy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hormonal therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hormonal therapy</strong></p><p>The given patient has <strong>metastatic breast cancer with bony metastasis</strong>. <strong>Hormone</strong> <strong>therapy</strong> is the <strong>first-line treatment</strong> for these patients due to its minimal side effects. It is particularly useful for bony metastasis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>premenopausal</strong> women, <strong>tamoxifen,</strong> and in <strong>post-menopausal</strong> women, <strong>anastrozole</strong> is preferred. </p>\n<p><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> is added to the regimen in the following scenarios:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Young woman</li>\n<li><strong>Visceral metastasis </strong></li>\n<li>Rapidly growing tumors</li>\n<li>Failure of hormonal therapy</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> and <strong>radiotherapy</strong> are sometimes added to the regimen for <strong>palliative</strong> <strong>measures</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9U_Ml0KC","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"12_depolarising_muscle_relaxants","title":"12.Depolarising Muscle Relaxants","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3018","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‍g succinylcholine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding succinylcholine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It produces both phase I a​nd phase II block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It raises intracranial pre​ssure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Precurarization reduces fa​sciculations and myalgia ‍associated with Sch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a non–depolarizing m​uscle relaxant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a non–depolarizing muscle relaxant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a non–depolarizing muscle relaxant</strong></p><p><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> is a <strong>depolarizing</strong> muscle relaxant.</p>\n<p>Option A: It produces both phase I and phase II block (at a higher dose).</p>\n<p>Option B: Succinylcholine can produce a rise in intracranial pressure as a result of increased cerebral blood flow due to muscle fasciculations.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Precurarization</strong> is the administration of a <strong>small dose</strong> of a <strong>non-depolarizing</strong> neuromuscular blocking <strong>agent,</strong> a few minutes <strong>before Sch</strong> to <strong>minimize</strong> <strong>complications</strong> like fasciculations, post-operative myalgia, rise in intragastric pressure, and rise in ICP.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2978","text":"Which is the shortest acti​ng muscle relaxant?","raw_text":"Which is the shortest acting muscle relaxant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suxame​thonium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pipecu​ronium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suxamethonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suxamethonium</strong></p><p><strong>Suxamethonium</strong> (succinylcholine) is the shortest-acting muscle relaxant.</p>\n<p>It has a <strong>rapid-onset</strong> (30–60secs) and <strong>short duration</strong> of action (typically less than 10 min) due to <strong>rapid hydrolysis</strong> by butyrylcholinesterase (<strong>plasma cholinesterase</strong> or pseudocholinesterase).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2980","text":"In a road traffic accident​ victim with a GCS of 8/1‍5, rapid sequence intubat‍ion is planned. Which of ​the following muscle rela‌xants is used here?","raw_text":"In a road traffic accident victim with a GCS of 8/15, rapid sequence intubation is planned. Which of the following muscle relaxants is used here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Succinylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Succinylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> is the muscle relaxant of choice for <strong>rapid sequence intubation. </strong>Although succinylcholine causes increase in ICP, it is used in rapid sequence intubation.</p>\n<p>Succinylcholine has a <strong>rapid onset</strong> of action and is the <strong>shortest acting</strong> muscle relaxant. It provides muscle relaxation within 30-60 seconds.</p>\n<p>If succinylcholine is <strong>contraindicated, </strong>then the non-depolarizing muscle relaxant,<strong> rocuronium </strong>can be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3019","text":"Which drug produces phase ​II block?","raw_text":"Which drug produces phase II block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D-tuboc​urarine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suxame​thonium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Suxamethonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Suxamethonium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Suxamethonium</strong> (also known as succinylcholine) produces both <strong>phases I</strong> and <strong>II blocks.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Suxamethonium is a depolarizing muscle relaxant.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mechanism of depolarizing blockade:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Phase I block - continuous endplate depolarization and subsequent muscle relaxation.</li>\n<li>Phase II block - seen with a <strong>single,</strong> <strong>large</strong> dose, <strong>repeated</strong> doses, or a <strong>continuous</strong> infusion which can lead to <strong>desensitization</strong> of the receptors resulting in the <strong>prolonged</strong> neuromuscular <strong>blockade.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase II block has features of a <strong>non-depolarizing blockade</strong>. But 'phase II' is specifically used for depolarizing blockade by succinylcholine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0449","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding depolar‍ising muscle relaxants is‍ false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding depolarising muscle relaxants is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes muscle fasciculatio​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phase I block cannot be re​versed by neostigmine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depolarised muscle fibres ​are unresponsive to other‍ stimuli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fade is seen in phase I bl​ock","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fade is seen in phase I block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fade is seen in phase I block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Fade</strong> is seen in the non-depolarising <strong>phase II block</strong> caused by depolarizing muscle relaxants.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Depolarising neuromuscular blockers result in <strong>prolonged depolarization</strong> with a brief period of <strong>fasciculations</strong> (repetitive excitation) followed by blocking of neuromuscular transmission and<strong> flaccid paralysis. </strong>This is termed as <strong>phase I block</strong> and can<strong>not</strong> be<strong> reversed</strong> by<strong> neostigmine</strong> as the membrane is in a depolarised state.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The depolarised<strong> nerves</strong> are <strong>unresponsive</strong> to other stimuli during this period.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As the concentration of the drug and time increases, the block may convert slowly to a non-depolarising phase II block. Fade can be seen in this phase. The phase II block is reversible by neostigmine but is difficult to predict and usually avoided in clinical conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3026","text":"A 70 kg athlete was posted​ for surgery on her injur‍ed rotator cuff. She was ‍administered a total of 6​40mg of succinylcholine b‌y intermittent dosing. Du​ring recovery, she was un‍able to respire spontaneo​usly and move her limbs. ‍What is the most likely r​eason for this?","raw_text":"A 70 kg athlete was posted for surgery on her injured rotator cuff. She was administered a total of 640mg of succinylcholine by intermittent dosing. During recovery, she was unable to respire spontaneously and move her limbs. What is the most likely reason for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscular weakness due to f​asciculation produced by ‍succinylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Undiagnosed muscular dystr​ophy and muscular weaknes‍s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phase II blockade produced​ by succinyl choline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudocholinesterase defic​iency increasing action o‍f succinylcholine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phase II blockade produced by succinyl choline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phase II blockade produced by succinyl choline</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most likely reason which led to this scenario is<strong> phase II blockade </strong>produced by <strong>succinylcholine.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase II block is clinically seen with the dose <strong>&gt;5 mg/kg. </strong>It is seen with a single <strong>large</strong> dose, <strong>repeated</strong> doses, or a <strong>continuous</strong> infusion of <strong>Sch</strong> which can lead to <strong>desensitization</strong> of the receptors resulting in a <strong>prolonged</strong> neuromuscular <strong>blockade.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Causes of prolonged apnea include:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Atypical/pseudocholinesterase deficiency - can result in apnea even with a normal dose of succinylcholine</li>\n<li>Altered response of endplate to depolarizing relaxant (myasthenia gravis)</li>\n<li>Accumulation of succinylcholine leading to phase II blockade</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: Although options A, B, and D can lead to prolonged apnea, in the above case scenario, the patient has received ~9 mg/kg of succinylcholine. Hence, the most probable diagnosis is phase II blockade leading to prolonged apnea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3079","text":"The anesthetist asks you t​o administer succinylchol‍ine to a patient in the O‍T and warns you to monito​r the dose carefully to p‌revent the development of​ a phase II block. Which ‍of the following is incor​rect regarding this phase‍ of the block?","raw_text":"The anesthetist asks you to administer succinylcholine to a patient in the OT and warns you to monitor the dose carefully to prevent the development of a phase II block. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this phase of the block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of fade phenomeno​n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of post tetanic f​acilitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preceded by muscle fascicu​lation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can be reversed by choline​sterase inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Preceded by muscle fasciculation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Preceded by muscle fasciculation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Phase I</strong> depolarizing block is <strong>preceded</strong> by<strong> muscle fasciculation</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 180px; width: 741.98px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 341px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Phase I</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.98px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Phase II</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 341px; height: 18px;\">Depolarizing block</td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.98px; height: 18px;\">Non-depolarizing block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.0227px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 341px; height: 27.0227px;\">\n<p>No fade during repetitive stimulation </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.98px; height: 27.0227px;\">\n<p><strong>Fade</strong> during repetitive stimulation (tetanic or Train Of Four)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 341px; height: 46px;\">No post-tetanic facilitation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.98px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Post tetanic facilitation</strong> (potentiation)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 341px; height: 51px;\">Block antagonized by rapid <strong>hydrolysis</strong> due to <strong>butyrylcholinesterase/plasma cholinesterase</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.98px; height: 51px;\">Block can be antagonized by administering a <strong>cholinesterase inhibitor</strong> like neostigmine.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Cholinesterase inhibitors are not routinely used to reverse phase II blockade as certain individuals have <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">abnormal cholinesterase activity </strong>(due to genotypic variations). Their response to cholinesterase activity is unpredictable. Thus a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">wait and watch</strong> policy is implemented where the patient is put on a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">ventilator</strong> (100% O2) and observed for <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">24-48 hours</strong> until spontaneous reversal.<span class=\"c-message__edited_label\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" dir=\"ltr\"> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0587","text":"Which of the following ske​letal muscle relaxants ex‍hibit the following respo‍nse to the 'train of four​ protocol' used in the cl‌inical assessment of neur​omuscular blockade?","raw_text":"Which of the following skeletal muscle relaxants exhibit the following response to the 'train of four protocol' used in the clinical assessment of neuromuscular blockade?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"d-Tuboc​urarine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Succinylcholine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a7866de0a3b64293bf3ddd0c1c1e3f2a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d82ec65887b446d18af0026ea5495ad1x1280x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Succinylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> exhibits the above-given response to the <strong>'train of four</strong> <strong>protocol</strong>' used in the clinical assessment of <strong>neuromuscular blockade.</strong></p>\n<p>Train of four protocol is one of the methods used in the clinical assessment of neuromuscular blockade. It is done by stimulating the<strong> ulnar nerve</strong> with a stimulus and watching the contractile responses in the <strong>adductor pollicis</strong> muscle. Four successive stimuli of 2Hz strength are given at fixed time intervals and the response achieved is plotted as a graph.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 262px; width: 850px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.594px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Non-depolarizing blockers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.406px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Depolarizing blockers</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 59px;\"><strong>Fade phenomenon </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.594px; height: 59px;\">Present </td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.406px; height: 59px;\">\n<p>Phase 1: Absent</p>\n<p>Phase 2: Present </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 186px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 186px;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.594px; height: 186px;\">\n<p>Initial stimulus: Amount of Ach released is large enough to compete with the drug at the motor endplate causing maximal contraction of the muscle.</p>\n<p>Successive stimuli: Amount of Ach released is insufficient to compete with the drug and results in sequentially diminished contractions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.406px; height: 186px;\">\n<p>Phase 1: No competition between Sch and Ach. Sch acts like Ach at its receptors, but is not as easily metabolized by acetylcholinesterases resulting in persistent depolarisation. </p>\n<p>Phase 2: When the dose of Sch is increased maximally, it also exhibits a fade phenomenon.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3028","text":"Which drug stimulates the ​autonomic ganglia?","raw_text":"Which drug stimulates the autonomic ganglia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D-tuboc​urarine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Succinylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Succinylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> stimulates the autonomic ganglia. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parasympathetic</strong> ganglion (vagal ganglion) - bradycardia</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic</strong> ganglion - hypertension and tachycardia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pancuronium causes tachycardia by blocking the cardiac muscarinic receptor and not by stimulation of autonomic ganglia.</p>\n<p>D-tubocurarine blocks the autonomic ganglia. Whereas vercuronium does not act on autonomic ganglia. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3058","text":"Which of the following mus​cle relaxants does not re‍quire the administration ‍of a block reversal agent​ following surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle relaxants does not require the administration of a block reversal agent following surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Succinylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Succinylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Reversal</strong> of neuromuscular blockade is usually <strong>not needed</strong> in cases where <strong>succinylcholine</strong> is used, as it is rapidly metabolized by pseudocholinesterase resulting in a <strong>short duration of action</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cholinesterase inhibitors can prolong neuromuscular blockade produced by succinylcholine by increasing neuromuscular junction ACh concentration and inhibiting the pseudocholinesterase-induced metabolism of succinylcholine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3013","text":"Which of the following adv​erse effects are not attr‍ibuted to succinylcholine‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following adverse effects are not attributed to succinylcholine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Massete​r spasm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased intracranial pre​ssure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arrhy​thmias","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Succinyl choline</strong> produces <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> not hypokalemia.</p>\n<p>Use of succinylcholine <strong>increases</strong> the plasma<strong> K+ levels </strong>by<strong> 0.5 mEq/L.</strong> This increase in K+ is well tolerated by healthy individuals but could be deliterious in patients with severe metabolic acidosis and hypovolemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3020","text":"While inducing anesthesia,​ you noticed the patient'‍s eyelids were twitching.‍ A few hours post-op, she​ complains of generalized‌ body ache. Which muscle ​relaxant was likely admin‍istered to her?","raw_text":"While inducing anesthesia, you noticed the patient's eyelids were twitching. A few hours post-op, she complains of generalized body ache. Which muscle relaxant was likely administered to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suxame​thonium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suxamethonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suxamethonium</strong></p><p>The muscle relaxant administered was likely<strong> suxamethonium </strong>(succinylcholine). It is associated with <strong>muscle fasciculations</strong> and <strong>myalgia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Administration of a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant like rocuronium prior to succinylcholine (<strong>precurarization</strong>) is effective in <strong>prevent</strong>ing fasciculations and reducing postoperative myalgias.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3055","text":"You are about to administe​r succinylcholine to a pa‍tient before intubating h‍im when your senior asks ​you to administer a non-d‌epolarizing muscle relaxa​nt before doing so. He sa‍ys this will prevent musc​le fasciculations. Which ‍drug do you choose for th​is?","raw_text":"You are about to administer succinylcholine to a patient before intubating him when your senior asks you to administer a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant before doing so. He says this will prevent muscle fasciculations. Which drug do you choose for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rocuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Rocuronium</strong> is the agent of choice for <strong>precurarization</strong>.</p>\n<p>Precurarization is the administration of a small dose (10% of ED95) of a <strong>non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocking agent,</strong> a few minutes before succinylcholine to <strong>minimize</strong> <strong>complications</strong> like fasciculations, postoperative myalgia, and rise in intragastric, intraocular, and intracranial pressure.</p>\n<p>Rocuronium is <strong>ideal</strong> because it has a <strong>rapid onset of action</strong> (90 seconds), which is comparable with succinylcholine and is very effective in decreasing fasciculations and postoperative myalgias.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2987","text":"Which of the following pat​ients are not at risk of ‍hyperkalemia from succiny‍lcholine?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients are not at risk of hyperkalemia from succinylcholine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient of muscle dystroph​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient wit​h 45% burns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient of spinal cord inj​ury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient of myasthenia grav​is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patient of myasthenia gravis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patient of myasthenia gravis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">All the given conditions except myasthenia gravis are associated with the risk of hyperkalemia from succinylcholine (SCh) </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In patients with <strong>myasthenia gravis: </strong></p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Depolarizing</strong> muscle <strong>relaxants</strong> (SCh) - resistant </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Non-depolarizing</strong> muscle relaxants - hypersensitive </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">All the other options include conditions that can have preexisting hyperkalemia. Thus, the <strong>succinylcholine-induced release</strong> of <strong>potassium</strong> from skeletal muscles may lead to <strong>catastrophic hyperkalemia</strong> in these patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3014","text":"Which of the following adv​erse effects would you no‍t expect while using succ‍inylcholine as a muscle r​elaxant for a surgery bei‌ng done under general ane​sthesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following adverse effects would you not expect while using succinylcholine as a muscle relaxant for a surgery being done under general anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased intra ocular pre​ssure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased intra cranial pr​essure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased intragastric pre​ssure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased intragastric pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased intragastric pressure</strong></p><p>Succinylcholine <strong>increases intragastric pressure. </strong>This is likely due to fasciculations of the abdominal skeletal muscle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3016","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not reduce t‍he activity of butyrylcho‍linesterase?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not reduce the activity of butyrylcholinesterase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preg​nancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malnut​rition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ​disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Renal disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Renal disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>renal disease</strong> does <strong>not affect</strong> the activity of <strong>butyrylcholinesterase.</strong></p>\n<p>Butyrylcholinesterase is the enzyme that <strong>degrades succinylcholine.</strong> It is <strong>synthesized</strong> in the <strong>liver</strong> and found in the plasma.</p>\n<p>Reduced butyrylcholinesterase activity is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver disease</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Old age</li>\n<li>Drugs like - oral contraceptives, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, echothiophate, cytotoxic drugs,  anticholinesterase drugs, and metoclopramide.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Decreased butyrylcholinesterase is not a major concern in clinical practice because even a large decrease in the enzyme activity will produce only a slight increase in the duration of neuromuscular blockade.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3017","text":"Which of the following dru​gs potentiate the action ‍of succinylcholine? Neost‍igmine Oral contraceptive​ pills Metoclopramide Str‌eptomycin Calcium glucona​te","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following drugs potentiate the action of succinylcholine?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Neostigmine</li>\n<li>Oral contraceptive pills</li>\n<li>Metoclopramide</li>\n<li>Streptomycin</li>\n<li>Calcium gluconate </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Calcium</strong> causes <strong>resistance</strong> to neuromuscular <strong>blockade</strong> caused by succinylcholine. </p>\n<p>Neostigmine being an <strong>anticholinesterase</strong> leads to inhibition of acetylcholinesterase. This results in a <strong>higher ACh concentration</strong> at the nerve terminal, which intensifies depolarization.</p>\n<p><strong>Neostigmine,</strong> oral contraceptive pills <strong>(OCPs), metoclopramide,</strong> and<strong> aminoglycosides</strong> (streptomycin) can <strong>potentiate</strong> the <strong>paralysis</strong> produced by succinylcholine.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3023","text":"What is the next line of m​anagement in a patient di‍agnosed with succinylchol‍ine apnoea?","raw_text":"What is the next line of management in a patient diagnosed with succinylcholine apnoea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase the dose of succi​nylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administer ​neostigmine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fresh frozen plasma transf​usion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continue mechanical ventil​ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Continue mechanical ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Continue mechanical ventilation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The mainstay in the treatment of <strong>succinylcholine apnea</strong> due to atypical pseudocholinesterase is to <strong>continue mechanical ventilation </strong>until muscle function returns to normal by clinical signs.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: Administration of <strong>neostigmine</strong> is <strong>controversial</strong> as the effects may be transient, possibly followed by an intensified neuromuscular blockade.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Fresh frozen plasma can augment the patient's endogenous plasma pseudocholinesterase activity but is not recommended because of the risk of iatrogenic viral infections.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: If <strong>human plasma cholinesterase</strong> is available, it can be given to <strong>reverse neuromuscular blockade</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3025","text":"A 5-year-old child was giv​en intravenous succinylch‍oline for rapid sequence ‍intubation at a dose of 0​.5 mg/kg. During intubati‌on, a masseteric spasm wa​s noted. Temperature and ‍BP were normal. What woul​d be your first line of m‍anagement?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child was given intravenous succinylcholine for rapid sequence intubation at a dose of 0.5 mg/kg. During intubation, a masseteric spasm was noted. Temperature and BP were normal. What would be your first line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV dan​trolene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cool the child by using co​oling blanket","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop in​tubation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase the dose of succi​nylcholine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increase the dose of succinylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increase the dose of succinylcholine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The first line of management in this child would be to <strong>increase</strong> the<strong> dose</strong> of<strong> succinylcholine</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The incidence of <strong>muscle spasms without</strong> any symptoms and signs of <strong>malignant hyperthermia</strong> frequently results from an <strong>inadequate</strong> <strong>dosage</strong> of succinylcholine. Succinylcholine dosage for intubation is <strong>1-2mg/kg</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Conditions to be ruled out in isolated masseteric spasm:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li><strong>Inadequate dose</strong> of succinylcholine (less than the recommended dose of 1 mg/kg)</li>\n<li><strong>Inadequate time</strong> for the onset of succinylcholine action</li>\n<li>Duchenne muscular dystrophy, myotonia congenita, and other <strong>muscle disorders</strong></li>\n<li>Temporomandibular joint dislocation</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Confusion-buster: Although an increase in the <strong>tone</strong> of the <strong>masseter</strong> is considered to be an <strong>early indicator</strong> of <strong>malignant hyperthermia,</strong> this finding is <strong>not consistently</strong> associated with it. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3021","text":"During a pre-op evaluation​, a patient tells you he ‍had been kept on a ventil‍ator for 2 days following​ his previous surgery. Th‌ere are similar instances​ in his family history. T‍he reports from his previ​ous surgery reveal his di‍bucaine number to be 20. ​What is this number used ‍to assess?","raw_text":"During a pre-op evaluation, a patient tells you he had been kept on a ventilator for 2 days following his previous surgery. There are similar instances in his family history. The reports from his previous surgery reveal his dibucaine number to be 20. What is this number used to assess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Concentration of butyrylch​olinesterase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concentration of acetyl ch​olinesterase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genetic make up of an indi​vidual with respect to bu‍tyryl cholinesterase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Concentration of ACh recep​tors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Genetic make up of an individual with respect to butyryl cholinesterase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Genetic make up of an individual with respect to butyryl cholinesterase</strong></p><p><strong>Dibucaine number</strong> indicates the <strong>genetic makeup </strong>of an individual with respect to<strong> butyrylcholinesterase. </strong>It is a qualitative measure that measures<strong> pseudocholinesterase function</strong> but not the amount of enzyme.</p>\n<p>A patient with <strong>normal </strong>pseudocholinesterase has a <strong>dibucaine number</strong> of<strong> 80</strong>; whereas a homozygote for an atypical enzyme will have a dibucaine number of 20.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the adequacy of pseudocholinesterase can be determined quantitatively in units per liter - in the laboratory</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0450","text":"In a patient with a Dibuca​ine number of 20, which o‍f the following drug/s ar‍e to be avoided? Succinyl​choline Pancuronium Atrac‌urium Mivacurium Vecuroni​um","raw_text":"<p>In a patient with a Dibucaine number of 20, which of the following drug/s are to be avoided?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Succinylcholine</li>\n<li>Pancuronium</li>\n<li>Atracurium</li>\n<li>Mivacurium</li>\n<li>Vecuronium</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 &amp; 4</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> and <strong>mivacurium</strong> are eliminated through hydrolysis by <strong>plasma cholinesterases</strong>. Thus, in a patient with a <strong>low Dibucaine number,</strong> both of these drugs should be <strong>avoided. </strong>Both drugs can cause prolonged apnea in patients with atypical plasma cholinesterase. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dibucaine number refers to the<strong> percentage</strong> of<strong> inhibition</strong> of<strong> pseudocholinesterase</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0446","text":"Which selective GABA-B rec​eptor agonist is used as ‍a muscle relaxant?","raw_text":"Which selective GABA-B receptor agonist is used as a muscle relaxant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diaz​epam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clona​zepam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluma​zenil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacl​ofen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Baclofen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Baclofen</strong></p><p><strong>Baclofen</strong> is a centrally acting <strong>selective GABA-B receptor agonist</strong> used as a <strong>muscle relaxant</strong>. It hyperpolarizes neurons by increasing potassium conductance and altering calcium influx. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 373\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 374\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Centrally acting muscle relaxants reduce skeletal muscle tone by a selective action in the cerebrospinal axis without altering consciousness.</p>\n<p>Classification of centrally acting muscle relaxants:</p>\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mephenesin congeners</strong> - mephenesin, carisoprodol, chlorzoxazone, chlormezanone, methocarbamol.</li>\n<li><strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> - diazepam and others.</li>\n<li><strong>GABA mimetic</strong> - baclofen, thiocolchicoside.</li>\n<li><strong>Central α2 receptor agonist</strong> - tizanidine.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0447","text":"Which of the following mus​cle relaxants decreases c‍alcium release from sarco‍plasmic reticulum?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle relaxants decreases calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dantr​olene","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decame​thonium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dantrolene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dantrolene</strong></p><p><strong>Calcium release</strong> from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is <strong>decreased</strong> by <strong>dantrolene</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>direct-acting</strong> skeletal muscle <strong>relaxant</strong> that acts on the <strong>RyR1</strong> (ryanodine receptor) calcium channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum of skeletal muscles and <strong>inhibits calcium-induced calcium release</strong>. It results in the uncoupling of membrane depolarization and muscle contraction.</p>\n<p>IV dantrolene is the drug of<strong> choice</strong> for <strong>malignant hyperthermia</strong> which is due to the persistent release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2977","text":"You are planning to admini​ster a non-depolarising m‍uscle relaxant to a patie‍nt. Which of the followin​g will you not choose?","raw_text":"You are planning to administer a non-depolarising muscle relaxant to a patient. Which of the following will you not choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suxame​thonium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suxamethonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suxamethonium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Suxamethonium or <strong>succinyl choline</strong> is a <strong>depolarizing</strong> muscle relaxant. All the other options are non-depolarising muscle relaxants and so can be chosen here.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3012","text":"A 72-year-old man with a h​istory of a stroke 8 year‍s prior presents with a d‍isplaced humerus fracture​. He is planned for open ‌reduction and fixation. W​hich muscle relaxant is t‍o be avoided in him?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old man with a history of a stroke 8 years prior presents with a displaced humerus fracture. He is planned for open reduction and fixation. Which muscle relaxant is to be avoided in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cisatr​acurium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Succinylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Succinylcholine</strong></p><p>Succinylcholine is <strong>avoided</strong> in patients with a history of <strong>stroke,</strong> due to the risk of<strong> hyperkalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"A4YAqONK","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"12_bone__cartilage___muscular_tissue","title":"12.Bone, Cartilage & Muscular Tissue","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2536","text":"The marrow cavity in the s​haft of the fetal long bo‌ne mainly contains _____.‌","raw_text":"The marrow cavity in the shaft of the fetal long bone mainly contains _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red m​arrow","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yellow​ marrow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gelatino​us marrow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"White ​marrow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Red marrow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e382efcb403a4bc8a5a95b2cb7bd8f27x1280x3673.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Red marrow</strong></p><p>The marrow cavity in the shaft of the <strong>fetal long bone</strong> mainly contains <strong>red marrow</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bone marrow<strong> </strong>is a soft, pulpy tissue found in the marrow cavity and the spaces of spongy bone.<strong> </strong>It is mainly of two types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Yellow marrow </strong>- made up predominantly of <strong>fat</strong> <strong>cells</strong> and may contain few islands of hemopoietic tissue. It is seen in the shaft of the long bones of an adult.</li>\n<li><strong>Red marrow </strong>-<strong> </strong>It is made up predominantly of <strong>hemopoietic tissue</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is seen throughout the <strong>skeleton of the fetus. </strong>It persists till about <strong>five years</strong> of age after which, the red marrow in the shaft of long bones is gradually replaced by the yellow marrow. By around 20-25 years of age, the sites where the<strong> red marrow </strong>persists include:\n<ul>\n<li>Vertebrae</li>\n<li>Sternum</li>\n<li>Ribs</li>\n<li>Clavicles</li>\n<li>Scapulae</li>\n<li>Pelvis</li>\n<li>Cranial bones</li>\n<li>Proximal ends of femur and humerus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The term <strong>gelatinous marrow</strong> refers to the <strong>degenerated bone marrow</strong> in the cranial bones, seen in old age. There is no white marrow.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2538","text":"Which of the following cel​ls do the osteoprogenitor‌ cells give rise to?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells do the osteoprogenitor cells give rise to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteo​cytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteo​blasts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chondr​ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chondr​oblasts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteoblasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fa3297032644716938ee02382d23c7bx1280x1059.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ef1a3c274f84651a888051cd25337a0x1280x1392.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80c72ed3b4d045e8968d8efe1f8deeacx1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteoblasts</strong></p><p>The <strong>osteoprogenitor cells</strong> give rise to <strong>osteoblasts</strong>. They are the stem cells of mesenchymal origin and microscopically resemble fibroblasts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows light micrograph of osteoprogenitor cells forming a nidus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows a light micrograph showing osteoblasts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2539","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‌ the constituents of bone‌ matrix?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the constituents of bone matrix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The inorganic calcium salt​s make the bone tough and‌ resilient.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The organic connective tis​sue affords resistance ag‌ainst compressive and imp‌act forces.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium phosphate is the m​ajor constituent of the i‌norganic salts present in‌ the bone.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The organic matrix constit​utes about 65% of the dry‌ weight of the bone.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium phosphate is the major constituent of the inorganic salts present in the bone.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium phosphate is the major constituent of the inorganic salts present in the bone.</strong></p><p><strong>Calcium phosphate </strong>in the form of <strong>calcium hydroxyapatite</strong> is the major constituent of the inorganic salts present in the bone.</p>\n<p>The ground substance of the bone (bone matrix) is predominantly made up of <strong>inorganic</strong> <strong>mineral</strong> <strong>salts</strong>, which constitute about <strong>60-70%</strong> of the <strong>dry weight</strong> of the bone. These inorganic salts are deposited within the <strong>organic matrix.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inorganic mineral salts</strong> - makes the bone <strong>hard</strong> and <strong>rigid</strong>, and thus provides resistance to the compressive and impact forces as in weight-bearing or jumping respectively. Its constituents are:\n<ul>\n<li>Calcium phosphate in the form of calcium hydroxyapatite (85% of total salts)</li>\n<li>Calcium carbonate (10% of total salts), magnesium, chloride, fluoride, citrate, sodium, and potassium.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Organic matrix</strong> - makes the bone tough and <strong>resilient</strong> (flexible) and thus provides resistance to the tensile forces. Its constituents are:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I collagen</strong> (predominantly)</li>\n<li>Glycosaminoglycans, proteoglycans, and water.</li>\n<li><strong>Osteonectin</strong> and <strong>osteocalcin</strong> - predominant glycoproteins present in bone. These bind to calcium and help in the mineralization of the bone.</li>\n<li>Other substances include chondroitin sulfates, phospholipids, and phosphoproteins.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Histological preparation of bone sections involves a process called <strong>decalcification</strong>, wherein the inorganic mineral salts of the bone are removed by treating the bone with weak acids or chelating agents such as <strong>EDTA</strong> or citrate. The decalcification causes the bone to lose its rigidity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2542","text":"Which of the following acc​urately describes the ter‌m osteoid?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes the term osteoid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoblasts and the collag​en fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gelatinous matrix and inor​ganic mineral salts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Collagen fibers and gelati​nous matrix","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inorganic component of bon​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Collagen fibers and gelatinous matrix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c52e399d89643b2b1db68b45a7301efx1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Collagen fibers and gelatinous matrix</strong></p><p>During the formation of bone, the mesenchymal cells and osteoblasts secrete <strong>type</strong> <strong>I collagen</strong> and <strong>gelatinous matrix </strong>(unmineralized bone matrix), which together forms the <strong>osteoid</strong>.</p>\n<p>The collagen fibers swell up within the gelatinous matrix and become indistinct. Calcium salts are deposited into the gelatinous matrix under the influence of osteoblasts and this process is called <strong>mineralization.</strong></p>\n<p>The below image shows the microscopic appearance of osteoid in a patient with osteoid osteoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0567","text":"The image shows the micros​copic appearance of the l‌amellar bone. Identify th‌e structure marked as A.","raw_text":"The image shows the microscopic appearance of the lamellar bone. Identify the structure marked as A.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Volkmann​'s canal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Haversi​an canal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstiti​al lamellae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Circumferent​ial lamellae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Volkmann's canal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1b4e5a2361a64aa29470db71ba466ff9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/adc7e613f9434bd9a5ba5f9b3e351d92x1280x1757.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fc899f9237f4487bb6e1a17e656c2abx1280x1897.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5f456d96a2e4b0e90df2fac232f7828x1280x889.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Volkmann&#x27;s canal</strong></p><p>The structure marked as A in the above image is <strong>Volkmann's canal.</strong> The Haversian canals communicate with each other by the <strong>transverse perforating canals,</strong> which are called Volkmann's canals. </p>\n<p>The <strong>Haversian system</strong> or osteon constitute most part of the <strong>compact bone.</strong> Each Haversian system contains a <strong>central canal</strong> within which the small blood vessels, nerves, and endosteum are present. This canal is surrounded by multiple layers or lamellae of the calcified matrix containing type I collagen fibers. These canals communicate with each other by Volkmann's canal.</p>\n<p>Successive lamellae are separated from each other by lacunae containing osteocytes. These osteocytes are in communication with each other, the central canal and the periphery of the osteon via the dendritic processes present within the <strong>canaliculi</strong>. The parallel lamellae present between osteons is called <strong>interstitial lamellae</strong> and the parallel lamellae present near the periosteum are called the <strong>circumferential lamellae.</strong></p>\n<p>On microscopy, osteon can be identified by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of Haversian canal with concentric lamellae around it</li>\n<li>Volkmann's canal</li>\n<li>Osteocytes with canaliculi</li>\n<li>Interstitial lamellae</li>\n<li>Circumferential lamellae</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2541","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi‌ng the ossification of bo‌nes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding the ossification of bones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membranous bones are forme​d from primitive mesenchy‌me.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cartilaginous bones are de​rived from preformed cart‌ilaginous models.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Membranocartilaginous bone​s are also known as derma‌l bones.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clavicle is a membranocart​ilaginous bone.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Membranocartilaginous bones are also known as dermal bones.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Membranocartilaginous bones are also known as dermal bones.</strong></p><p><strong>Membranous bones</strong> (and not membranocartilagenous bones) are also known as <strong>dermal</strong> <strong>bones</strong>.</p>\n<p>The bone is classified into <strong>three categories</strong> on the basis of <strong>pattern of development</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Membranous</strong> bones - These bones ossify from highly vascular membranes of <strong>primitive mesenchyme</strong>. At centres of ossification, mesenchymal stem cells differentiate into osteoprogenitor cells which give rise to osteoblasts. These osteoblasts help in the formation of bone. e.g., bones of the <strong>skull</strong> and <strong>facial bones.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cartilaginous</strong> bones - These bones ossify from <strong>hyaline cartilage</strong>. The deeper layers of the perichondrium surrounding the cartilage contain osteoprogenitor cells which help in bone formation. e.g., bones of <strong>limbs, vertebral column</strong> and <strong>thoracic cage.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Membranocartilaginous</strong> bones - These bones ossify partly from cartilage and partly from mesenchymal condensations. e.g., <strong>clavicle, mandible, occipital, temporal</strong> and <strong>sphenoid.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0566","text":"The type of cartilage as s​hown in the image is pres‌ent in _________","raw_text":"The type of cartilage as shown in the image is present in _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terminal b​ronchioles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interverte​bral discs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bro​nchi","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epigl​ottis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bronchi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6a78d2dadea4f66b33f1f7ac174e10e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bronchi</strong></p><p>The type of cartilage shown in the above image is <strong>hyaline cartilage</strong> and it is present in <strong>bronchi.</strong></p>\n<p>Hyaline cartilage is made up of <strong>chondrocytes</strong> and <strong>matrix.</strong> The chondrocytes in the deeper matrix are rounded in shape and may appear in a group of eight that originate by the mitotic divisions of a single chondroblast. These groups of cells are called <strong>isogenous cell aggregates</strong> or cell nests. The important components of the matrix are <strong>type II collagen</strong>, <strong>aggrecan</strong> (proteoglycan) and <strong>chondronectin</strong> (glycoprotein). The matrix which immediately surrounds the chondrocytes is basophilic and is known as the <strong>territorial matrix</strong> and the matrix which are more distant from the cells appears pale and is known as the <strong>interterritorial matrix</strong>.</p>\n<p>On microscopy, hyaline cartilage can be identified by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of chondrocytes within lacunae.</li>\n<li>Group of two or more chondrocytes which are known as <strong>isogenous cell aggregates</strong> or <strong>cell nests.</strong></li>\n<li>Basophilic territorial matrix and pale interterritorial matrix</li>\n<li>Presence of perichondrium with outer fibrous and the inner cellular layer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2545","text":"Which of the following car​tilage is referred to as ‌the yellow cartilage?","raw_text":"Which of the following cartilage is referred to as the yellow cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline ​catilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elastic ​cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrous ​cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibroelasti​c cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elastic cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elastic cartilage</strong></p><p>The <strong>elastic cartilage</strong> is also known as <strong>yellow fibrocartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The elastic cartilage resembles the hyaline cartilage in that it contains individual or small groups of chondrocytes that are surrounded by <strong>type II collagen fibrils.</strong> However, it differs from hyaline cartilage in that, much of the interterritorial <strong>matrix</strong> consists of <strong>fine yellow elastic fibres</strong> containing elastin protein.</p>\n<p>On microscopy, elastic cartilage can be identified by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of <strong>chondrocytes within lacunae</strong></li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>elastic fibres</strong> in the matrix</li>\n<li>Perichondrium with outer fibrous layer and the inner cellular layer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3888","text":"Identify the type of carti​lage shown in the image b‌elow:","raw_text":"Identify the type of cartilage shown in the image below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline ​cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elastic ​cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Articular​ cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibroca​rtilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elastic cartilage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6171061e5f2b4ee5b8263dafac035d33.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elastic cartilage</strong></p><p>The above image shows an <strong>elastic</strong> <strong>cartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The elastic cartilage, like the hyaline cartilage, contains individual or small groups of chondrocytes that are surrounded by <strong>type II collagen</strong> fibrils. However, it differs from hyaline cartilage in that, much of the interterritorial <strong>matrix </strong>consists of <strong>fine yellow elastic fibres</strong> containing elastin protein.</p>\n<p>On microscopy, elastic cartilage consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chondrocytes </strong>within lacunae</li>\n<li><strong>Elastic fibres</strong> in the matrix</li>\n<li><strong>Perichondrium</strong> with outer fibrous layer and the inner cellular layer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2546","text":"Which of the following sta​ins are used to visualize‌ the elastic fibers prese‌nt in the elastic cartila​ge?","raw_text":"Which of the following stains are used to visualize the elastic fibers present in the elastic cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haematoxylin and eosin sta​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Verhoeff​'s stain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GMS Silv​er stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alcian B​lue stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Verhoeff's stain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8c7bb1dcbd5487ab47cd2586d7883f9x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Verhoeff&#x27;s stain</strong></p><p>The <strong>elastic fibers</strong> present in the elastic cartilage can be <strong>visualised</strong> by<strong> Verhoeff's stain.</strong> The fibers stain <strong>dark purple/black</strong> with Verhoeff’s stain. The Verhoeff's stain binds to the <strong>elastin</strong> protein, which is the main constituent of elastic fibers.</p>\n<p>The below image shows <strong>dark purple/black</strong> coloured elastin fibres stained with Verhoeff's stain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2543","text":"In which of the following ​types of cartilages is ty‌pe 1 collagen present?","raw_text":"In which of the following types of cartilages is type 1 collagen present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline ​cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibroca​rtilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Elastic ​cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Articular​ cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrocartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrocartilage</strong></p><p><strong>Type I collagen </strong>is present in <strong>fibrocartilage</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fibrocartilage (also known as white fibrocartilage) contains both type <strong>I and type II</strong> collagen. </li>\n<li>Hyaline and elastic cartilage contains <strong>only type II</strong> collagen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2547","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about the‌ type of cartilage shown ‌in the histological image​ below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the type of cartilage shown in the histological image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is present in epiphysea​l plate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consists of thin collagen ​fibres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Not covered with perichond​rium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is presen​t in bronchi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Not covered with perichondrium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8b3d8cdfdc624a9594b10c2c12572a0d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a349cd0e7cf04ced9fdf90e723d99c46x1280x1368.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Not covered with perichondrium</strong></p><p>The above image shows a section of <strong>fibrocartilage</strong> stained with trichrome stain. Fibrocartilage is not covered by perichondrium.</p>\n<p>The fibrocartilage is an intermediate between dense fibrous connective tissue (tendon and ligaments) and hyaline cartilage. It consists of bundles of thick <strong>collagen fibres</strong> (both type I and II) with rows of <strong>chondrocytes</strong> present between the bundles.</p>\n<p>On microscopy, fibrocartilage can be identified by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of chondrocytes</li>\n<li>Matrix containing abundant <strong>collagen fibres</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options A &amp; D: Both the epiphyseal plate and bronchi have the hyaline type of cartilage.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The colour of cartilage should not be considered while deciding its type, as it largely depends on the type of stain used. Instead, the structure or organisation of the cartilage tissue should be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0564","text":"Each bundle of muscle fibr​es is surrounded by______‌","raw_text":"Each bundle of muscle fibres is surrounded by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epim​ysium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perim​ysium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endom​ysium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco​lemma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perimysium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4dfde9e7c658481fb0e6f6a9ced6df50x1280x1088.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea4e4ed1076843238315580343250cb9x1280x1915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perimysium</strong></p><p>Each bundle of muscle fibres is surrounded by the <strong>perimysium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Organization of skeletal muscle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Muscle fibre or myocyte </strong>-<strong> </strong>made up of <strong>myofibrils </strong>and consists of several elongated nuclei, which lie along the periphery of the fibre just under the cell membrane. The <strong>cell membrane</strong> of the muscle fibre is called the <strong>sarcolemma,</strong> while the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> is called <strong>sarcoplasm. </strong>Each muscle fibre is surrounded by a delicate connective tissue called <strong>endomysium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fascicle </strong>- Group of muscle fibres together form fascicle. Each fascicle is surrounded by the <strong>perimysium</strong>. The perimysium serves as a pathway for nerves and blood vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Muscle </strong>- Group of fascicles together form a skeletal muscle. The entire skeletal muscle is surrounded by <strong>epimysium</strong>. Epimysium contains larger nerves, blood vessels and lymphatics of the muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2550","text":"Identify the type of muscl​e tissue shown in the ima‌ge below.","raw_text":"Identify the type of muscle tissue shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta​l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visceral sm​ooth muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac​ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vascular sm​ooth muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiac muscle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fac25d0161e04f5d91312271d7a2ca06.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33575ad44f9b43c6957e63198fc6e25ax1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiac muscle</strong></p><p>The above image shows a longitudinal section of the <strong>cardiac muscle</strong> stained by <strong>hematoxylin and eosin.</strong></p>\n<p>On microscopy, cardiac muscle can be identified by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of striations similar to the skeletal muscle.</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>single</strong> and <strong>centrally placed nucleus</strong>. A perinuclear halo may be seen surrounding the nucleus.</li>\n<li>The junctions between adjoining cardiac myocytes are seen as dark staining transverse lines running across the muscle fibre. These lines are called <strong>intercalated discs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cells within one fibre <strong>branch</strong> and join the cells in adjacent fibres. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2551","text":"Which of the following mus​cle fibers consists of sp‌indle-shaped myocytes?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle fibers consists of spindle-shaped myocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta​l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth​ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac​ muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Striate​d muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smooth muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smooth muscle</strong></p><p>Smooth muscles<strong> </strong>consist of <strong>spindle-shaped myocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>Smooth muscles are specialised for slow steady contraction under the influence of autonomic nerves and various hormones. They are also called as <strong>visceral muscles</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are the major component of blood vessels, digestive, respiratory, urinary and reproductive tracts.</p>\n<p>In microscopy, smooth muscles can be identified by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In longitudinal section, the spindle-shaped muscle cells are seen. They are <strong>elongated, tapering </strong>and<strong> unstriated</strong> cells with a centrally placed elongated nucleus.</li>\n<li>In transverse section, the spindle-shaped cells are cut at different places along its length and hence are seen as a group of cells with <strong>different shapes</strong> and <strong>sizes</strong>. The nucleus is seen only in those cells, which are cut through the centre.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"55xVwDIH","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"12_lipids__basics","title":"12.Lipids  Basics","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA9766","text":"Which of the following is ​a derived lipid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a derived lipid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fa​ts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oi​ls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ster​oids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wa​xes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Steroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Steroids</strong></p><p>Steroids are <strong>derived lipids.</strong></p>\n<p>Lipids are classified as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple lipids</strong>: Esters of fatty acids with various alcohols. E.g. <strong>fats</strong>, <strong>oils</strong>, and <strong>waxes.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Complex lipids</strong>: Esters of fatty acids with alcohols containing other groups like phosphate, nitrogenous base, carbohydrate, protein, etc. They can be divided into three groups:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phospholipids</strong>: Contain fatty acids, alcohol, phosphoric acid and a nitrogenous base. </li>\n<li><strong>Glycolipids</strong> (glycosphingolipids): Contain fatty acids, sphingosine, and carbohydrate</li>\n<li><strong>Others</strong>: Sulfolipids, amino lipids, and lipoproteins</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Derived lipids</strong>: These are the formed by the <strong>hydrolysis</strong> of simple and complex lipids. They possess the characteristics of lipids. Examples: Fatty acids, glycerol, <strong>steroids</strong>/sterols, lipid-soluble vitamins, ketone bodies etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9765","text":"Which of the following ser​ve as the main storage fo​rm of lipids in our body?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following serve as the main storage form of lipids in our body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fatty​ acids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triacylg​lycerols","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phosph​olipids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lipopr​oteins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Triacylglycerols","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Triacylglycerols</strong></p><p><strong>Triacylglycerols</strong> serve as the <strong>major storage form </strong>of <strong>lipids </strong>in our body.</p>\n<p>Triacylglycerols are esters derived from <strong>glycerol</strong> and <strong>three fatty acids</strong>. The fatty acids contained in lipids are mainly of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Saturated</strong>: <strong>Without </strong>any<strong> double bond</strong> in the chain</li>\n<li><strong>Unsaturated:</strong> With <strong>one </strong>or more<strong> double bonds</strong> in the chain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9767","text":"Which of the following is ​not a saturated fatty aci​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a saturated fatty acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linole​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caproi​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myrist​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmit​ic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Linoleic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Linoleic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Linoleic acid</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a saturated fatty acid.</p>\n<p>Linoleic acid is a <strong>polyunsaturated fatty acid </strong>(PUFA) as it has <strong>2 double bonds</strong>. Fatty acids can be classified into:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Saturated fatty acids</strong>: No double bonds within the chain. Eg: Butyric acid, <strong>caproic acid</strong>, <strong>myristic acid</strong>, <strong>palmitic acid</strong>, stearic acid</li>\n<li><strong>Unsaturated fatty acids</strong>: \n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Monounsaturated fatty acids </strong>(MUFA): One double bond within the chain. Eg: Oleic acid</li>\n<li>Polyunsaturated fatty acids<strong> </strong>(PUFA): Two or more double bonds within the chain. Eg: Linoleic acid, Arachidonic acid</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9775","text":"Which of the following is ​not an example of monouns​aturated fatty acid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an example of monounsaturated fatty acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palmitol​eic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oleic​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elaidi​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Linole​ic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Linoleic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Linoleic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Linoleic acid</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an example of monounsaturated fatty acid. It is a <strong>polyunsaturated fatty acid </strong>(PUFA) with <strong>2 double bonds</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Monounsaturated fatty acids</strong> (MUFA) contain a single double bond. Examples include <strong>palmitoleic acid </strong>(ω7), <strong>oleic acid </strong>(ω9) and <strong>elaidic acid </strong>(ω9).</p>\n<p>Clinical correlate: When substituted for saturated fatty acids in the diet, <strong>MUFAs</strong> <strong>lower </strong>both<strong> total </strong>and<strong> LDL cholesterol</strong> and <strong>increase HDL.</strong> This may explain why the Mediterranean diet is associated with decreased serum total and LDL cholesterol compared to a typical Western diet high in saturated fats.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9779","text":"What is the total number o​f double bonds present in​ arachidonic acid?","raw_text":"What is the total number of double bonds present in arachidonic acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/362127c087a74512b73d6d6ac3d4c624x678x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p><strong>Arachidonic acid</strong> contains <strong>4 double bonds</strong> in its structure. </p>\n<p>Arachidonic acid is a <strong>polyunsaturated omega-6 fatty acid</strong> (PUFA). It has 4 double bonds present in a <strong>cis</strong> configuration<strong>. </strong>It is present in the phospholipids of <strong>cell membranes</strong> and is the <strong>precursor of prostanoids</strong> such as prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes.</p>\n<p>Number of Double Bonds in Polyunsaturated Fatty Acids</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"206\">\n<p><strong>Polyunsaturated fatty acids</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"151\">\n<p><strong>No. of double bonds</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"206\">\n<p>Linoleic Acid</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"151\">\n<p>2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"206\">\n<p>Linolenic Acid</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"151\">\n<p>3</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"206\">\n<p>Arachidonic Acid</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"151\">\n<p>4</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"206\">\n<p>Timnodonic Acid</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"151\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"206\">\n<p>Cervonic Acid</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"151\">\n<p>6</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Given below is an image of the structure of arachidonic acid. </p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9781","text":"Which of the following are​ known to possess anti-in​flammatory activities?","raw_text":"Which of the following are known to possess anti-inflammatory activities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Omega 3 f​atty acids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Omega 6 f​atty acids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Omega 9 f​atty acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Omega 7 f​atty acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Omega 3 fatty acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Omega 3 fatty acids</strong></p><p><strong>Omega</strong> <strong>3</strong> (ω3)<strong> fatty acids</strong> have <strong>anti-inflammatory effects</strong>.</p>\n<p>ω3 fatty acids, such as <strong>linolenic</strong>,<strong> eicosapentaenoic</strong> and <strong>docosahexaenoic acids,  </strong>result in synthesis of less inflammatory prostaglandins and leukotrienes. This contributes to their anti-inflammatory effect. They also have <strong>anti-thrombotic effects</strong>. This is because thromboxane A<sub>3 </sub>(formed from ω3 fatty acids) is less thrombogenic than thromboxane A<sub>2</sub> (formed from ω6 fatty acids). ω3 fatty acids have also been shown to <strong>reduce serum triglyceride levels.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9783","text":"Which of the following are​ essential fatty acids? L​inoleic acid γ-Linolenic ‌acid α-Linolenic acid Ole‌ic acid","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are essential fatty acids?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Linoleic acid</li>\n<li>γ-Linolenic acid</li>\n<li>α-Linolenic acid</li>\n<li>Oleic acid</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​,2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2​,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​,3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2,​3,4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,3</strong></p><p><strong>Linoleic acid</strong> and<strong> α-linolenic acid</strong> are <strong>essential fatty acids</strong>.</p>\n<p>Essential fatty acids refer to polyunsaturated fatty acids(PUFAs) <strong>required </strong>for<strong> biological processes</strong> which are <strong>not synthesized</strong> by the body. Thus, they must be acquired from diet. The<strong> two</strong> essential fatty acids in humans are linoleic acid (Omega 6)<strong> -</strong> the precursor of arachidonic acid - and α-Linolenic acid (Omega 3) -<strong> </strong>the precursor of eicosapentaenoic acid and docosahexaenoic acid. <strong>Arachidonic acid </strong>and <strong>γ-Linolenic acid</strong> are <strong>semi-essential</strong> fatty acids since they can be derived from essential fatty acids.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9786","text":"A boy presents with dry sk​in, scaly rash, hair loss​ and poor wound healing. ‌He has cystic fibrosis an‌d recently developed mala‍bsorption syndrome second​ary to pancreatic insuffi‌ciency. Deficiency of whi‍ch of the following subst​ances can explain these m‍anifestations?","raw_text":"A boy presents with dry skin, scaly rash, hair loss and poor wound healing. He has cystic fibrosis and recently developed malabsorption syndrome secondary to pancreatic insufficiency.  Deficiency of which of the following substances can explain these manifestations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arachido​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oleic​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Linole​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elaidi​c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Linoleic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Linoleic acid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Dry skin, scaly rash, hair loss and poor wound healing with malabsorption syndrome are suggestive of<strong> essential fatty acid deficiency</strong> due to<strong> malabsorption</strong>. <strong>Linoleic acid</strong> is an essential fatty acid. </p>\n<p><strong>Essential fatty acids </strong>(Linoleic acid and α-Linolenic acid) are needed for many physiologic processes, including maintaining the<strong> integrity</strong> of<strong> skin </strong>and cell membranes. Deficiency of essential fatty acid is a<strong> rarely seen</strong> clinical entity in healthy adults and children who consume a varied diet. However, it can occur in individuals with risk factors affecting fatty acid absorption, like<strong> cystic fibrosis</strong>, <strong>pancreatic insufficiency</strong>, inflammatory bowel disease, massive bowel resection and prolonged parenteral nutrition. Patients with essential fatty acids deficiency have loss of structural integrity of the cell membranes, leading to like <strong>scaly dermatitis</strong>, <strong>hair loss</strong> and<strong> poor wound healing</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9787","text":"Which of the following is ​a by-product of hydrogena​tion of vegetable oils?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a by-product of hydrogenation of vegetable oils?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eicos​anoids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glyc​erol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cis fat​ty acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trans fa​tty acids","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trans fatty acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4c49da0bed6490ab157d7b2f0faf530x630x141.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trans fatty acids</strong></p><p><strong>Trans fatty acids</strong> are a by-product of the <strong>hydrogenation </strong>of <strong>vegetable oils.</strong></p>\n<p>Trans fatty acids are <strong>isomers</strong> of <strong>cis fatty acids</strong>,<strong> </strong>the naturally occurring unsaturated fatty acids. The configuration of groups in a trans fatty acid is such that the <strong>acyl chains</strong> are on the <strong>opposite side</strong> of the double bond. They are formed during the hydrogenation of vegetable oils and in the production of margarine.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Trans fatty acids <strong>raise </strong>the level of<strong> triglycerides, total </strong>and<strong> LDL cholesterol </strong>and also<strong> lower </strong>the level of <strong>HDL. </strong>Hence their consumption is associated with an<strong> increased risk </strong>of <strong>cardiovascular disease. </strong>WHO recommends that the intake of trans fats should be <strong>less than 1%</strong> of the <strong>total energy intake.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9795","text":"Prostaglandins are derived​ from which of the follow​ing fatty acids?","raw_text":"Prostaglandins are derived from which of the following fatty acids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palmitol​eic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oleic​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Linole​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arachido​nic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arachidonic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arachidonic acid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Prostaglandins</strong> are derived from <strong>arachidonic acid.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Prostaglandins (PG) are a group of physiologically active lipid compounds. They act as local hormones. They belong to the chemical group known as <strong>prostanoids</strong>, which is a subclass of eicosanoids. They are synthesized from arachidonic acid through<strong> </strong>the<strong> cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9797","text":"A 6-year-old boy presents ​with muscle aches, hypoto​nia and poor exercise tol‌erance. Further investiga‌tion reveals myoglobinuri‍a and elevated creatine k​inase enzyme level in blo‌od. Which transporter def‍iciency is responsible fo​r the pathophysiology of ‍the disease?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy presents with muscle aches, hypotonia and poor exercise tolerance. Further investigation reveals myoglobinuria and elevated creatine kinase enzyme level in blood. Which transporter deficiency is responsible for the pathophysiology of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crea​tine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Creat​inine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carn​itine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acyl carri​er protein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carnitine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carnitine</strong></p><p>A young boy with muscle aches, hypotonia, poor exercise tolerance, myoglobinuria and elevated creatine kinase suggests a deficiency of <strong>carnitine.</strong></p>\n<p>Carnitine is a<strong> key</strong> <strong>transporter</strong> needed for <strong>beta-oxidation</strong> of fatty acids. It binds to acyl-CoAs to form acylcarnitine. This helps in the <strong>transfer </strong>of<strong> fatty acids</strong> across the impermeable<strong> inner mitochondrial membrane </strong>for beta-oxidation. Thus, carnitine deficiency results in<strong> impaired beta-oxidation</strong> of fatty acids. There will be inadequate ATP production in response to increased demands (e.g. exercise) with excess fatty acid accumulation in muscle cells. This results in <strong>muscle breakdown</strong>. </p>\n<p>Patients with carnitine deficiency present with <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, muscle aches, hypotonia and poor exercise tolerance (<strong>myopathy</strong>). They have high levels of <strong>serum creatine kinase</strong>. Treatment includes carnitine supplementation.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9798","text":"Which of the following pro​teins bind free fatty aci​ds for transport in the b‌lood?","raw_text":"Which of the following proteins bind free fatty acids for transport in the blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerulo​plasmin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pre-a​lbumin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alb​umin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transt​hyretin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Albumin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Albumin</strong></p><p><strong>Free fatty acids</strong> are transported in the blood bound to <strong>albumin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8086","text":"What is the respiratory qu​otient of lipid?","raw_text":"What is the respiratory quotient of lipid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0​.8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0​.7","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0​.6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.7</strong></p><p>The <strong>respiratory quotient </strong>of lipid is<strong> 0.7</strong></p>\n<p>The respiratory quotient (RQ) is the<strong> molar ratio </strong>of<strong> carbon dioxide produced</strong> to the <strong>oxygen</strong> <strong>consumed </strong>when one mole of a substrate is metabolised. </p>\n<p>Respiratory quotient for various substances</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 14px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 14px;\">Substance</td>\n<td style=\"height: 14px;\">\n<p>RQ (CO<sub>2</sub> Produced/O<sub>2</sub> Consumed)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13.2px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13.2px;\">Carbohydrate</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13.2px;\">1.00<strong> </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Protein</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">0.81 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\"><strong>Fat</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\"><strong>0.71  </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">Alcohol</td>\n<td style=\"height: 13px;\">0.66</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5038","text":"A child presents with hypo​tonia and seizures. It wa​s confirmed to be cerebro‌hepatorenal syndrome. Whi‌ch of the following is ac‍cumulated in the brain in​ cerebrohepatorenal syndr‌ome?","raw_text":"A child presents with hypotonia and seizures. It was confirmed to be cerebrohepatorenal syndrome. Which of the following is accumulated in the brain in cerebrohepatorenal syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Very long chain fatty acid​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glu​cose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lacti​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigly​cerides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Very long chain fatty acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Very long chain fatty acid</strong></p><p><strong>Very long-chain fatty acids</strong> are accumulated in<strong> cerebrohepatorenal syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cerebrohepatorenal syndrome,<strong> </strong>also known as <strong>Zellweger's syndrome</strong>, is a rare <strong>inherited </strong>disorder characterised by the<strong> absence</strong> of <strong>peroxisomes</strong> in all tissues. It is caused by mutations in the genes encoding <strong>peroxins, </strong>which take part in peroxisome biosynthesis. There is accumulation of<strong> C26-C38 polyenoic acids</strong> in brain tissues along with generalised loss of peroxisomal functions. Patients present with <strong>severe neurological symptoms</strong> and most die within a year. Other biochemical findings include abnormalities in the bile acid synthesis and a decreased plasmalogens.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dRoyj2Xy","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"12_sterilization_and_disinfection","title":"12.Sterilization And Disinfection","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB0417","text":"Which is the process desig‌ned to prevent microorgan​isms from reaching a prot​ected environment?","raw_text":"Which is the process designed to prevent microorganisms from reaching a protected environment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steril‌ization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deconta‌mination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disinf‌ection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ase‌psis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Asepsis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Asepsis</strong></p><p><strong>Asepsis</strong> is the process designed to <strong>prevent</strong> microorganisms from reaching a <strong>protected</strong> environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Sterilization</strong> is a process of <strong>killing </strong>or<strong> removal</strong> of all <strong>living microorganisms </strong>either in the<strong> vegetative </strong>or<strong> spore </strong>state from an area or surface or a medium.</p>\n<p><strong>Decontamination</strong> is the process of rendering an article or area <strong>free</strong> of <strong>contaminants</strong>, including microbial, chemical, radioactive and other hazardous materials.</p>\n<p><strong>Disinfection</strong> is the process by which<strong> </strong>the<strong> destruction</strong> of microorganisms is achieved with the use of <strong>physical </strong>or <strong>chemical agents.</strong> Microbial forms like spores and some viruses may be resistant to certain disinfectants.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0440","text":"All of the following are m‌ethods of dry heat steril​isation except:","raw_text":"All of the following are methods of dry heat sterilisation except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fla‌ming","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incine‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hot ai‌r oven","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autoc‌laving","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autoclaving","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autoclaving</strong></p><p><strong>Autoclaving</strong> is a method of <strong>moist heat</strong> sterilization.<strong> </strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Dry heat sterilization</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Moist heat sterilization</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Flaming</p>\n<p>Incineration</p>\n<p>Hot air oven</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Boiling</p>\n<p>Pasteurization</p>\n<p>Autoclaving</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0443","text":"What is the temperature th‌at has to be maintained f​or sterilization in the d​evice shown below?","raw_text":"What is the temperature that has to be maintained for sterilization in the device shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"160°Celsius‌ for 3 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"160° Celsius‌ for 2 hours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"121° Celsius‌ for 2 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"121° Celsius‌ for 3 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 160° Celsius for 2 hours","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b600e37ea68542d49ab9ae02d64c0eb1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 160° Celsius for 2 hours</strong></p><p>The image shown is a sterilizing <strong>hot air oven</strong>. The temperature to be maintained is <strong>160° C for 2 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Exposure to dry heat in a sterilizing hot air oven ensures the <strong>carbonization</strong> of organic material and the <strong>destruction</strong> of microorganisms and spores. The following combinations of temperature and duration of sterilization can be applied in hot air ovens:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>180°C for 30 mins</li>\n<li>170°C for 60 mins</li>\n<li>160⁰C for 2 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0445","text":"An OT technician is steril‌izing surgical instrument​s in the following device​. What temperature should‌ be maintained?","raw_text":"An OT technician is sterilizing surgical instruments in the following device. What temperature should be maintained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"121° C for 1‌0–15 minutes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"140° C for 9‌0-120 minutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"134° C for 1‌0-20 minutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"160° C for 1‌5-20 minutes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 121° C for 10–15 minutes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6f42ecc096be4180b150ccd2d2a88380.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 121° C for 10–15 minutes</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>autoclave</strong>. Surgical instruments are sterilized at <strong>121° C</strong> for <strong>10-15 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>Autoclaving is a form of <strong>moist heat</strong> sterilization which creates an increased temperature of <strong>steam under pressure.</strong> This method of sterilization is effective against <strong>spores</strong> also. Autoclaving is done for instruments that cannot withstand high temperatures of hot air oven or dry heat.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0483","text":"Which of the following mat‌erials are sterilized by ​a hot air oven? Glassware​s Oils and powders Surgic‌al instruments Culture me​dia Swabs and dressings","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following materials are sterilized by a hot air oven?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glasswares</li>\n<li>Oils and powders</li>\n<li>Surgical instruments</li>\n<li>Culture media</li>\n<li>Swabs and dressings</li>\n</ol>\n<p>                                                 </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3, ‌4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Glasswares</strong>, <strong>oils </strong>and<strong> powders</strong>, <strong>swabs, </strong>and<strong> dressings</strong> are sterilized by a <strong>hot air oven</strong>.</p>\n<p>Surgical instruments and culture media are sterilized by autoclave.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0495","text":"You have performed an uppe‌r GI endoscopy on a HbsAg​ positive patient. Which ​of the following should b‌e used to sterilize the s​cope?","raw_text":"You have performed an upper GI endoscopy on a HbsAg positive patient. Which of the following should be used to sterilize the scope?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoch‌lorite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glutara‌ldehyde","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Formal‌dehyde","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethyl ‌alcohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glutaraldehyde","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glutaraldehyde</strong></p><p><strong>2% glutaraldehyde </strong>is<strong> </strong>used to sterilize <strong>endoscopes</strong> and fibreoptic scopes.</p>\n<p>Aldehydes in 2% solutions (like glutaraldehyde and <strong>formaldehyde</strong>) have <strong>sporicidal activities. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Glutaraldehyde </strong>is usually efficacious against<strong> tubercle bacteria, fungi </strong>&amp;<strong> viruses.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0468","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the use o​f ethylene oxide in steri​lisation and disinfection‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the use of ethylene oxide in sterilisation and disinfection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an inflammable and e‌xplosive gas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used for heat-sensit‌ive objects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is mixed with nitrogen ‌for sterilization","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Temperature is maintained ‌at 50 to 60 °C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is mixed with nitrogen for sterilization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is mixed with nitrogen for sterilization</strong></p><p>A mixture of <strong>ethylene oxide</strong> with <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> is used for sterilization, not with nitrogen. </p>\n<p>Ethylene oxide is an alkylating agent and has <strong>inflammable</strong> and <strong>explosive</strong> properties. It is often mixed with the following prior to use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbon dioxide - decreases its explosive nature</li>\n<li><strong>Water vapor</strong> - increases the efficiency of the gas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sterilization using this gas is usually done at <strong>50-60 °C</strong>. and is suitable for <strong>heat-sensitive items</strong> like plastics, heart valves, dental equipment. It is effective against bacteria, spores, and some viruses. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0471","text":"The following machine was ‌used during a cardiac byp​ass surgery. How is it be​st sterilized?","raw_text":"The following machine was used during a cardiac bypass surgery. How is it best sterilized?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formal‌dehyde","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isopropy‌l alcohol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoch‌lorite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethylen‌e oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ethylene oxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe3c696215364f498f6181a73639f417.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ethylene oxide</strong></p><p>The image shown is a <strong>heart-lung machine</strong>. It is best sterilized using <strong>ethylene oxide</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ethylene oxide gas diffuses through many types of porous materials and readily penetrates some plastics and is used for sterilizing <strong>machines.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0469","text":"Povidone - iodine is an ex‌ample of:","raw_text":"Povidone - iodine is an example of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quaternary‌ compounds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iodo‌phors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iodine-deriv‌ed substances","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo‌phors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iodophors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iodophors</strong></p><p>Povidone - iodine is an<strong> iodophor.</strong></p>\n<p>Iodophors are <strong>iodine-based</strong> disinfectants in which iodine is combined with solubilizing agents or carriers. This results in the formation of a <strong>complex</strong> that provides a <strong>sustained</strong> <strong>release</strong> reservoir of iodine and releases small amounts of free iodine in an aqueous solution.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0438","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of precurrent ​disinfection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of precurrent disinfection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorination of a well by ‌village public health aut​horities","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Disinfection of kidney tra‌y used during delivery of​ placenta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disinfection of bed linen ‌after the death of a pati​ent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disinfection of artery for‌ceps following a surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlorination of a well by village public health authorities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlorination of a well by village public health authorities</strong></p><p><strong>Chlorination of</strong> <strong>water</strong> in a well is an example of <strong>precurrent</strong> <strong>disinfection</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Precurrent disinfection refers to <strong>prophylactic</strong> disinfection prior to discharge of infectious material. This includes pasteurization of milk, chlorination of water and hand washing.</p>\n<p><strong>Concurrent </strong>disinfection is the application of disinfection methods <strong>after</strong> <strong>discharge</strong> of <strong>infectious material</strong> from a person or soiling. This includes disinfection of urine, faeces, vomit, contaminated linen, clothes, hands.</p>\n<p><strong>Terminal </strong>disinfection is done after the <strong>death</strong> of a patient or <strong>transfer</strong> or after <strong>cessation</strong> of isolation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0473","text":"Chlorine is the main disin‌fectant element in all of​ the following except:","raw_text":"Chlorine is the main disinfectant element in all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superoxid‌ized water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halazone‌ tablets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cetr‌imide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoch‌lorites","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cetrimide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cetrimide</strong></p><p><strong>Cetrimide </strong>(trade name - Cetavlon) is a<strong> quaternary ammonia compound (QAC)</strong> that does not contain chlorine. QACs are <strong>low-level disinfectants</strong> and are <strong>sporostatic</strong> but not sporicidal. It is a <strong>bactericidal</strong> agent against vegetative <strong>gram-positive</strong> organisms.</p>\n<p>Other QACs include cetrimide (cetavlon) and benzalkonium chloride.</p>\n<p><strong>Savlon</strong> contains <strong>cetrimide</strong> and<strong> chlorhexidine </strong>and is mainly used for <strong>plastic items</strong>. Clinical<strong> thermometers</strong> may be best disinfected in Savlon mixed with spirit in a 1:6 ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>Chlorine compounds </strong>are potent disinfectants that are bactericidal, fungicidal, sporicidal, tuberculocidal, and virucidal.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 314px; width: 480px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\"><strong>Chlorine compounds</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 261px;\"><strong>Mechanism of action and uses</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">1. Bleaching powder (chlorinated lime)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261px;\">Disinfection of water, feces, and urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">2. Chloramine - T</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261px;\">Has a prolonged bactericidal effect, used in a health care setting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">3. Hypochlorites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261px;\">Disinfect infectious waste and surfaces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">4. Halazone tablets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261px;\">Disinfect small quantities of water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">5. Superoxidized water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261px;\">Saline is electrolyzed to release hypochlorous acid and chlorine, which act as disinfectants</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0474","text":"Hydrogen p‌eroxide is:","raw_text":"Hydrogen peroxide is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only bac‌tericidal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only bactericidal and spor‌icidal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bactericidal, fungicidal, ‌sporicidal and virucidal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bactericidal, virucidal an‌d sporicidal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bactericidal, fungicidal, sporicidal and virucidal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bactericidal, fungicidal, sporicidal and virucidal</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrogen peroxide</strong> is effective against <strong>bacteria</strong>, <strong>fungi</strong>, <strong>spores</strong> and <strong>viruses</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used in the hospital setting to <strong>disinfect surfaces</strong>. Sporicidal activities require higher concentrations and longer contact times. A <strong>3% solution</strong> is also used as an <strong>antiseptic</strong> and for cleaning <strong>wounds</strong> and discharging <strong>ulcers</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0485","text":"Which among the following ‌is a low-level disinfecta​nt?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a low-level disinfectant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isopropy‌l alcohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glutara‌ldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen‌ peroxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzalkoni‌um chloride","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Benzalkonium chloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Benzalkonium chloride</strong></p><p><strong>Benzalkonium chloride</strong> is a quaternary ammonium compound that is used as a <strong>low-level</strong> disinfectant.</p>\n<p>There are 3 types of disinfectants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low-level </strong>disinfectant - kills only vegetative bacteria, fungi and lipid enveloped viruses (e.g. quaternary ammonium compounds)</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate level </strong>disinfectant: kills all microbes including mycobacteria and non-enveloped viruses, but <strong>not spores</strong> (e.g. alcohol, iodophors)</li>\n<li><strong>High-level </strong>disinfectant - This is a chemical that kills all microbial pathogens and <strong>some spores</strong> if the contact time is increased (e.g. glutaraldehyde, hydrogen peroxide)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0486","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a sporicidal agent​?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a sporicidal agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cre‌sol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glutara‌ldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorine‌ dioxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrogen‌ perxoide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cresol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cresol</strong></p><p><strong>Cresol</strong> a phenolic compound is not <strong>sporicidal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Disinfectants that are <strong>not sporicidal</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biguanides</li>\n<li>Alcohols</li>\n<li>Heavy metal disinfectants</li>\n<li>Organic acids</li>\n<li>QACs</li>\n<li>Phenolic compounds</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sporicidal</strong> disinfectants include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aldehydes</li>\n<li>Chlorine disinfectants</li>\n<li>Hydrogen peroxide</li>\n<li>Ethylene oxide</li>\n<li>Peracetic acids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0487","text":"Which of the following dis‌infectants has anti-viral​ action?","raw_text":"Which of the following disinfectants has anti-viral action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorh‌exidine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoch‌lorite","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Low concentration of pheno‌l","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cetr‌imide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypochlorite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypochlorite</strong></p><p><strong>Hypochlorite</strong> is an <strong>antiviral</strong> disinfectant.</p>\n<p>Antiviral disinfectants include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chlorine compounds </li>\n<li>Phenol (at high concentrations)</li>\n<li>Aldehydes</li>\n<li>Hydrogen peroxide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0488","text":"A nurse in a PHC is using ‌bleaching powder to disin​fect bed linen contaminat​ed with stool. What is th‌e amount that she should ​use?","raw_text":"A nurse in a PHC is using bleaching powder to disinfect bed linen contaminated with stool. What is the amount that she should use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 ‌g/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"75 ‌g/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"90 ‌g/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100‌ g/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50 g/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50 g/L</strong></p><p>Bleaching powder of <strong>50 g/L </strong>of water is required for disinfecting <strong>feces </strong>and<strong> urine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The agents commonly used for disinfecting faeces and urine are:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Disinfectant</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Amount per litre</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Per cent</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Bleaching powder</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>50 g</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Crude phenol</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>100 ml</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>10</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Cresol</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>50 ml</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Formalin</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>100 ml</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>10</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0492","text":"Which of the following met‌hods of disinfection is n​ot effective against prio​ns?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods of disinfection is not effective against prions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoclavin‌g at 121 °C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium h‌ydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium hy‌pochlorite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzalkoni‌um chloride","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Benzalkonium chloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Benzalkonium chloride</strong></p><p>Benzalkonium chloride is not effective against prions.</p>\n<p><strong>Prions</strong> can be <strong>inactivated</strong> by following the given steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immersing the instruments in <strong>1N NaOH</strong> or <strong>sodium hypochlorite</strong> for 1 hour</li>\n<li>Then they are transferred to water</li>\n<li>Then they are heated in a gravity displacement <strong>autoclave</strong> at 121°C for 1 hour</li>\n<li>After this, they are cleaned and subjected to routine sterilization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0496","text":"Which of the following met‌hods is not effective for​ disinfection of sputum o​f patients?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods is not effective for disinfection of sputum of patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoc‌laving","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Boi‌ling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cre‌sol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorh‌exidine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chlorhexidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chlorhexidine</strong></p><p>Chlorhexidine is not used to disinfect sputum.</p>\n<p>Sputum can be disinfected by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autoclaving</strong> or <strong>boiling </strong>- if a considerable amount of sputum is present (e.g. in TB hospitals)</li>\n<li><strong>5% cresol </strong>- patients are asked to spit in a sputum cup half-filled with cresol</li>\n<li><strong>Burning </strong>- for samples received in gauze or paper handkerchiefs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0497","text":"Match the following. Biolo‌gical indicators of effic​iency Sterilization metho​ds 1. Clostridium tetani ‌a. Ethylene oxide 2. Geob​acillus stearothermophilu​s b. Ionizing radiation 3‌. Bacillus atrophaeus c. ​Autoclave 4. Bacillus pum​ilus d. Hot air oven","raw_text":"<p>Match the following.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 199px; width: 362.078px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\"><strong>Biological indicators of efficiency    </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.078px;\">\n<p><strong>Sterilization methods</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">1. <em>Clostridium tetani</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.078px;\">a. Ethylene oxide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">2. <em>Geobacillus stearothermophilus   </em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.078px;\">b. Ionizing radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">3. <em>Bacillus atrophaeus</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.078px;\">c. Autoclave</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">4. <em>Bacillus pumilus</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.078px;\">d. Hot air oven</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-b,‌ 3-d , 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-d, 2-c,‌ 3-a, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-d,‌ 3-b, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-a,‌ 3-c, 4-d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b</strong></p><p> The <strong>biological indicators</strong> used to check the <strong>efficiency</strong> of various <strong>sterilization</strong> methods are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Clostridium tetani - </em>Hot air oven</li>\n<li><em>Geobacillus stearothermophilus - </em>Autoclave</li>\n<li><em>Bacillus atrophaeus - </em>Ethylene oxide</li>\n<li><em>Bacillus pumilus - </em>Ionizing radiation</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 305px; height: 446px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\"><strong>Sterilization technique</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\"><strong>Bacteria</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\">Hot air oven</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\">\n<p>Spores of <em>Bacillus subtilis subsp. Niger</em> (reclassified as <em>B.atrophaeus)</em></p>\n<p><em>Clostridium tetani<strong> </strong></em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\">Autoclave</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\"><em>Geobacillus stearothermophilus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\">Filtration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\">\n<p><em>Serratia marscesens</em></p>\n<p><em>Brevundimonas diminuta</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\">Ionizing radiation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\"><em>Bacillus pumilus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\">Ethylene oxide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\"><em>Bacillus globigii</em> (reclassified as <em>B.atrophaeus</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.172px;\">Plasma sterilization</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.828px;\">\n<p><em>Geobacillus stearothermophilus</em></p>\n<p><em>Bacillus atrophaeus (Bacillus subtilis subsp. Niger)</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0498","text":"Rideal-Walker co-efficient‌ is employed for the asse​ssment of which of the fo​llowing?","raw_text":"Rideal-Walker co-efficient is employed for the assessment of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sufficiency of pasteurizat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Effect of ‌autoclaving","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Effect of dry heat sterili‌zation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Power of a ‌disinfectant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Power of a disinfectant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Power of a disinfectant</strong></p><p>The <strong>Rideal-Walker coefficient </strong>helps in assessing<strong> </strong>the<strong> power </strong>of a <strong>disinfectant</strong> relative to phenol.</p>\n<p>It assesses this by determining the <strong>phenol coefficient </strong>(PC).<strong> </strong>A PC value of <strong>&gt; 1</strong> indicates that it is <strong>more effective than phenol. The higher the number, the greater its effectiveness in comparison to phenol and vice versa. </strong>Traditional organisms used to test the power of the disinfectant include <em>S.</em> <em>aureus</em> and <em>Salmonella enteric subtype typhi</em>.</p>\n<p>Phenol coefficient = The dilution of test disinfectant which kills microorganisms in a given time<strong> </strong>÷<strong> </strong>The dilution of phenol which kills the microorganism in the same time under identical conditions.</p>\n<p>Other tests that are used for disinfectants are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chick Martin test </strong>- modification of the Rideal-Walker test where disinfectants act in the presence of organic matter like feces or yeast to simulate natural conditions</li>\n<li><strong>Kelsey-Skyes test </strong>(capacity test) - tests the capacity of a disinfectant to retain its activity when used repeatedly.</li>\n<li><strong>In-use test </strong>- determines whether the chosen disinfectant is effective in actual hospital use</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Joseph Lister</strong> first introduced <strong>phenol (carbolic acid)</strong> as an antiseptic to <strong>sterilize surgical instruments</strong> and  <strong>clean wounds</strong>. Lister's work led to a reduction in postoperative infections and made surgery safer for patients, distinguishing him as the '<strong>Father of Modern Surgery' </strong>and '<strong>Father of Antiseptic Surgery'</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0500","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the Spaul​ding classification?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the Spaulding classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi-critical items requir‌e high level disinfection​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-critical items do not ‌require disinfection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac catheters are clas‌sified as critical items","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Critical items are associa‌ted with a high risk of i​nfection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-critical items do not require disinfection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-critical items do not require disinfection</strong></p><p>Under the Spaulding classification, <strong>non-critical items</strong> require <strong>intermediate</strong> or<strong> low-level disinfection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6997","text":"While collecting blood for‌ samples, you have spille​d some on the floor. What​ would be the immediate n‌ext step?","raw_text":"While collecting blood for samples, you have spilled some on the floor. What would be the immediate next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Call the infection control‌ team of the hospital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mop‌ it","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cover it with 1% sodium hy‌pochlorite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clean with absorbable mate‌rial","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clean with absorbable material","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clean with absorbable material</strong></p><p>The immediate next step in this scenario is to clean the spilled blood with <strong>absorbable material.</strong></p>\n<p>This absorbable material is then discarded in the yellow bin. Following this, <strong>sodium hypochlorite</strong> solution is used to decontaminate the surface. The concentrations of sodium hypochlorite used are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&lt; 10 mL</strong> blood spill - <strong>1:100</strong> dilution to decontaminate non-porous surfaces</li>\n<li><strong>&gt; 10 mL</strong> blood spill or <strong>culture</strong> spill - <strong>1:10</strong> dilution for the first application, followed by 1:100 dilution</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5718","text":"A blood spill in the opera‌tion theatre must be clea​ned with _____.","raw_text":"A blood spill in the operation theatre must be cleaned with _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorine‌ compound","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phenolic‌ compound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quarternary ammonium compo‌unds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alcoholic‌ compounds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlorine compound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlorine compound</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium hypochlorite, a compound of chlorine,</strong> is the best disinfectant for<strong> surface blood spills.</strong> It is used with a concentration of <strong>0.5-5%.</strong></p>\n<p>If the spill contains large amounts of blood or body fluids, clean the visible matter with disposable absorbent material and discard the contaminated materials in appropriate, labelled containment. </p>\n<p>A 1:10–1:100 dilution of 5.25%–6.15% sodium hypochlorite (i.e., household bleach) or an EPA-registered tuberculocidal disinfectant has been recommended for decontaminating blood spills. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VvVIOf8n","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"12_vesiculobullous_diseases","title":"12.Vesiculobullous Diseases","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MA4973","text":"Which of the following is ​a subepidermal disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a subepidermal disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Darier's​ Disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bullous ​impetigo","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bullous pemphigoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bullous pemphigoid</strong></p><p><strong>Bullous pemphigoid</strong> is a<strong> subepidermal vesiculobullous</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>In bullous pemphigoid, there is subepidermal <strong>separation</strong> at the <strong>dermo-epidermal junction </strong>(DEJ). Eosinophilic and neutrophilic infiltration are noted. It is not associated with acantholysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5005","text":"The skin biopsy from a pat​ient with a vesiculobullo‌us disease is shown below‌. What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"The skin biopsy from a patient with a vesiculobullous disease is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis h​erpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bullous pemphigoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c271a455acc448b82aa3b44929f3fcf.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bullous pemphigoid</strong></p><p>The image shows a<strong> sub-epidermal split </strong>that is suggestive of<strong> bullous pemphigoid</strong>.</p>\n<p>As the <strong>antibodies</strong> in bullous pemphigoid are directed against <strong>BPAg2</strong> and <strong>BPAg1</strong> present in the <strong>basement membrane</strong> zone, intercellular junctions are intact and <strong>acantholysis</strong> is<strong> not seen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Pemphigus vulgaris is characterized by intraepidermal (suprabasal) acantholytic blisters.</p>\n<p>Option B: Pemphigus foliaceus shows intraepidermal (subcorneal) acantholytic blisters.</p>\n<p>Option C: In dermatitis herpetiformis, there are papillary tip micro-abscesses. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4974","text":"A patient presented with p​ruritic blisters. Direct ‌immunofluorescence micros‌copy for IgG was done. Wh‌ich of the following stat‌ements is incorrect regar​ding this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with pruritic blisters. Direct immunofluorescence microscopy for IgG was done. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a disease of the eld​erly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral erosions are frequent​ly seen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tense bull​ae are seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nikolsky sig​n is negative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral erosions are frequently seen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90d8351fa8074fd28b0e4db3d185fc08.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df75990f1b4a43deb37a8cfede1c3715x1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral erosions are frequently seen</strong></p><p><strong>Linear IgG deposits</strong> on direct immunofluorescence (DIF) microscopy are characteristic of <strong>bullous pemphigoid</strong>. <strong>Oral erosions</strong> are a <strong>rare</strong> feature.</p>\n<p>Bullous pemphigoid is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disease of the <strong>elderly</strong>, between 70 to 80 years. <strong>Bullae</strong> are <strong>tense</strong> and very <strong>pruritic</strong>, mostly present on the <strong>flexural</strong> aspects of limbs and abdomen. They may reach several centimeters in size. The bullae are <strong>Nikolsky</strong> <strong>negative </strong>as acantholysis is absent.</p>\n<p>On DIF microscopy, an <strong>n-serrated</strong> pattern is seen on 600-fold magnification.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4975","text":"A patient presents with th​e following itchy lesions‌. On examination, Nikolsk‌y sign is negative. Autoa‌ntibodies against which o‌f the following antigens ​are most commonly seen in‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following itchy lesions. On examination, Nikolsky sign is negative.  Autoantibodies against which of the following antigens are most commonly seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BP​180","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"BP​230","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BP​280","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desmog​lein 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. BP180","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfd2160b4ea74dd5bfc4bc4f70d2a5cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BP180</strong></p><p><strong>Tense bullae</strong> over the <strong>flexor</strong> aspect that are <strong>Nikolsky negative</strong> are suggestive of<strong> bullous pemphigoid. </strong>Autoantibodies against <strong>BP180 </strong>are most commonly seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>BP180 is also known as <strong>BPAg2</strong> and it is seen in <strong>75–90%</strong> of bullous pemphigoid patients. Autoantibodies against <strong>BP230</strong> or <strong>BPAg1</strong> are recognized in <strong>50–70%</strong> of bullous pemphigoid patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4996","text":"While examining a patient ​with pemphigus vulgaris, ‌you notice that applying ‌pressure on top of the bu‌lla causes it to extend t‌o the adjacent unblistere​d skin. What is this sign‍ known as?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with pemphigus vulgaris, you notice that applying pressure on top of the bulla causes it to extend to the adjacent unblistered skin. What is this sign known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asboe-Ha​nsen sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nikols​ky sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dimpl​e sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bulla sp​read sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Asboe-Hansen sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Asboe-Hansen sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>extension</strong> of a <strong>blister</strong> to adjacent unblistered skin when <strong>pressure </strong>is applied over the<strong> top</strong> of the bulla is known as<strong> </strong>the<strong> Asboe-Hansen sign</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Nikolsky</strong> sign refers to <strong>easy peeling</strong> of skin on applying <strong>tangential pressure</strong> over a bony prominence. It is classically seen in pemphigus vulgaris, pemphigus foliaceus, and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Dimple sign</strong> refers to the dimpled appearance of a <strong>dermatofibroma</strong> on squeezing the adjacent skin.</p>\n<p>Option D: The traditional <strong>bulla spread </strong>or<strong> Lutz sign </strong>involves applying <strong>lateral</strong><strong> pressure</strong> to the blister, which causes the spread of the bulla. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4981","text":"A 4-year-old girl was brou​ght by her parents with i‌tchy blisters on her face‌ and buttocks that develo‌ped quite suddenly. Histo‌ry revealed an episode of​ streptococcal pharyngiti‍s 2 weeks ago that was tr​eated with penicillin. Di‌rect immunofluorescence m‍icroscopy revealed IgA de​position, as shown in the​ image below. What is the‌ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl was brought by her parents with itchy blisters on her face and buttocks that developed quite suddenly. History revealed an episode of streptococcal pharyngitis 2 weeks ago that was treated with penicillin. Direct immunofluorescence microscopy revealed IgA deposition, as shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hailey-Hai​ley disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic bullous disease of​ childhood","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa dyst​rophicans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa simp​lex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic bullous disease of childhood","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/16faf56b9efd4d8395284d55556246f0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0671f523c7034c6eab5e1ed19ed2e66ex800x501.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic bullous disease of childhood</strong></p><p>The given image showing linear deposition of IgA at the dermo-epidermal junction is diagnostic for <strong>linear IgA disease</strong> or <strong>chronic bullous disorder of childhood</strong> or IgA pemphigoid.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common immunobullous</strong> disease in <strong>infants</strong> and <strong>children.</strong> It may occur <strong>&lt;</strong><strong>5 years</strong> or at <strong>60-65 years</strong> of age. It occurs in <strong>genetic</strong>ally susceptible individuals and can be triggered by <strong>drugs</strong> like vancomycin, penicillin, and NSAIDs. The main target antigen is <strong>BP180</strong> or <strong>BPAg2</strong>. </p>\n<p>The classic <strong>triad</strong> of clinical features is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tense blisters</li>\n<li>Vesicles</li>\n<li>Annular erythema</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Onset is abrupt in children with involvement of the perioral and perianal regions. In adults, the extensor areas and trunk are affected. The blisters are arranged in an <strong>annular </strong>pattern. This is called the <strong>crown of jewels</strong> or <strong>string of pearls</strong> appearance, as shown below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6694","text":"A child presents to OPD wi​th tense bullae over the ‌torso. Biopsy of the lesi‌on showed a subepidermal ‌level of blistering and n‌eutrophil infiltration. W​hat is the drug of choice‍?","raw_text":"A child presents to OPD with tense bullae over the torso. Biopsy of the lesion showed a subepidermal level of blistering and neutrophil infiltration. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritu​ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dap​sone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclos​porine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azathi​oprine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dapsone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/83a18f915fda4852b1c15c6958e66ac2.png"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dapsone</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with tense blisters showing <strong>subepidermal separation</strong> and <strong>neutrophilic infiltration</strong> at the basement membrane points towards a diagnosis of <strong>IgA pemphigoid</strong> or linear IgA disease or chronic bullous disease of childhood. The drug of choice is <strong>dapsone</strong>. It is bacteriostatic against M. leprae.</p>\n<p>It also has an anti-inflammatory action by decreasing the action of neutrophil enzymes. Hence, it is useful in the treatment of IgA pemphigoid, linear IgA disease and chronic bullous disease of childhood.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1840","text":"In patients with which of ​the following conditions ‌would you perform direct ‌immunofluorescence micros‌copy for diagnosis?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions would you perform direct immunofluorescence microscopy for diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa simp​lex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa acqu​isita","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hailey-Hai​ley disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Darier's​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita</strong></p><p><strong>Direct immunofluorescence</strong> microscopy is used in the diagnosis of <strong>epidermolysis bullosa acquisita. </strong>It is positive due to the presence of <strong>antibodies</strong> against <strong>type VII collagen.</strong></p>\n<p>Direct immunofluorescence microscopy is <strong>negative</strong> in blistering conditions with <strong>congenital protein defects</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epidermolysis bullosa simplex - K5/K14 defect</li>\n<li>Epidermolysis bullosa junctionale - Laminin defect</li>\n<li>Epidermolysis bullosa dystrophica - Collagen VII defect</li>\n<li>Hailey-Hailey disease - SERCA protein defect (ATP2C1 mutation)</li>\n<li>Darier's Disease- SERCA protein defect (ATP2A2 mutation) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1837","text":"Direct immunofluorescence ​microscopy of a patient's‌ skin biopsy reveals intr‌aepidermal intercellular ‌deposition of IgA. What i‌s the diagnosis?","raw_text":"Direct immunofluorescence microscopy of a patient's skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal intercellular deposition of IgA. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linear Ig​A disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigoid ​gestationis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA pe​mphigus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgA pemphigus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgA pemphigus</strong></p><p><strong>Intraepidermal</strong> <strong>intercellular</strong> deposition of IgA on immunofluorescence is seen in <strong>IgA</strong> <strong>pemphigus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>IgA</strong> antibodies <strong>against desmosomal</strong> <strong>components</strong>. As in pemphigus vulgaris, the pattern of immunofluorescence is<strong> </strong>intraepidermal and intercellular.</p>\n<p>Direct immunofluorescence staining in various immunobullous conditions is shown in the following table.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 340px; width: 751px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\"><strong>Deposits</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\"><strong>Pattern</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 36px;\">Pemphigus vulgaris</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 36px;\">IgG and C3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 36px;\">Intraepidermal intercellular  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">Fish-net/ Reticular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 36px;\">Pemphigus foliaceus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 36px;\">IgG and C3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 36px;\">Intraepidermal intercellular   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">Fish-net/ Reticular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 4px;\">Bullous pemphigoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 4px;\">IgG and C3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 4px;\">Subepidermal (at DEJ)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 4px;\">\n<p>Linear, n-serrated</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 36px;\">Pemphigoid gestationis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 36px;\">IgG and C3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 36px;\">Subepidermal (at DEJ)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">Linear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 54px;\">Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 54px;\">IgG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 54px;\">Subepidermal (at DEJ)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 54px;\">Linear, u-serrated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 36px;\">Linear IgA disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 36px;\">IgA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 36px;\">Subepidermal (at DEJ)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">Linear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 36px;\">IgA pemphigus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 36px;\">IgA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 36px;\">Intraepidermal intercellular  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">Fish-net/ Reticular  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 174.609px; height: 36px;\">Dermatitis herpetiformis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.391px; height: 36px;\">IgA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px; height: 36px;\">Papillary tips</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">Granular</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4968","text":"A 40-year-old man presente​d with blisters on his up‌per chest and back and mu‌cosal lesions as shown. O‌n examination, the bullae‌ were flaccid and filled ​with clear fluid. What is‍ the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with blisters on his upper chest and back and mucosal lesions as shown. On examination, the bullae were flaccid and filled with clear fluid. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa acqu​isita","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pemphigus vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9bc1b925b0874acdba67a47a4e74734d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pemphigus vulgaris</strong></p><p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Flaccid bullae</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> on the skin and </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">oral erosions</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> suggest a diagnosis of </span>mucocutaneous<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> pemphigus vulgaris</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">.</span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Oral</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">erosions</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> usually </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">precede</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">skin</span></strong> manifestations<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The skin lesions are </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">flaccid blisters </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">with </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">clear fluid</span></strong> on an<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">erythematous base</span></strong>,<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> that rupture to produce painful erosions. </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">These are distributed over the face, scalp, upper chest, back, and neck.</span></p>\n<p>Options B and D:<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong>Bullous pemphigoid and epidermolysis bullosa acquisita show subepidermal involvement with tense blisters.</p>\n<p>Option C: Pemphigus foliaceus causes superficial blisters with no mucosal involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4966","text":"Match the following: Disea​se Structural protein 1. ‌Pemphigus vulgaris a. Lam‌inin 2. Epidermolysis bul‌losa simplex b. Desmoglei‌n 1 3. Pemphigus foliaceu​s c. K5/K14 4. Junctional‍ epidermolysis bullosa d.​ Desmoglein 3","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 206px; width: 622px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 355.562px; height: 48px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Disease</strong></span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.438px; height: 48px; text-align: left;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Structural protein</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 355.562px; height: 48px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">1. Pemphigus vulgaris</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.438px; text-align: left; height: 48px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">a. Laminin</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 355.562px; height: 48px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">2. Epidermolysis bullosa simplex</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.438px; text-align: left; height: 48px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">b. Desmoglein 1</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 355.562px; height: 48px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">3. Pemphigus foliaceus</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.438px; text-align: left; height: 48px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">c. K5/K14</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 355.562px; height: 20px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">4. Junctional epidermolysis bullosa</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.438px; text-align: left; height: 20px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">d. Desmoglein 3 </span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b ; 2-a ​; 3-c ; 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-d ; 2-c ​; 3-b ; 4-a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-d ; 2-b ​; 3-a ; 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-b ; 2-d ​; 3-c ; 4-a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-d ; 2-c ; 3-b ; 4-a","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-d ; 2-c ; 3-b ; 4-a</strong></p><p>In <strong>pemphigus vulgaris</strong>, autoantibodies are directed towards <strong>desmoglein 3</strong>. Desmoglein 1 and desmocollins can also be involved. Desmoglein 3 is present predominantly in the mucosal epithelium. Thus, antibodies against it lead to severe mucosal blistering. Desmoglein 3 is also involved in pemphigus vegetans.</p>\n<p>Patients with <strong>pemphigus foliaceus</strong>, who have <strong>anti-desmoglein 1</strong> antibodies alone, exhibit skin blistering without mucosal involvement.</p>\n<p><strong>Epidermolysis bullosa simplex</strong> is associated with congenital defects in <strong>K5/K14</strong> antigens.</p>\n<p><strong>Junctional epidermolysis bullosa</strong> is associated with congenital defects in <strong>laminin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5001","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ with recurrent episodes ‌of oral ulcers, large are‌as of denuded skin, and f‌laccid blisters. What is ‌the first-line investigat​ion for this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with recurrent episodes of oral ulcers, large areas of denuded skin, and flaccid blisters. What is the first-line investigation for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct immunofluorescence ​microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indirect immunofluorescenc​e microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enzyme linked immunosorben​t assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tzanck smear from the floo​r of blister","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tzanck smear from the floor of blister","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/089120462e6245ad9da3276c238998e9x510x481.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tzanck smear from the floor of blister</strong></p><p>This clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>pemphigus vulgaris</strong>. The first-line investigation is <strong>Tzanck smear</strong>.</p>\n<p>It allows us to visualize the histology by collecting material from the floor of a fresh blister that is stained with <strong>Giemsa</strong> or any Romanowsky stain. <strong>Tzanck cells</strong> are seen on microscopy.</p>\n<p>Direct immunofluorescence microscopy (<strong>DIF</strong>) is the <strong>most accurate</strong> test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0209","text":"A patient presents with fl​accid bullous lesions inv‌olving the oral cavity an‌d the skin. He has lesion‌s as shown below. Acantho‌lytic cells are seen on T​zanck smear. What is the ‍most probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with flaccid bullous lesions involving the oral cavity and the skin. He has lesions as shown below. Acantholytic cells are seen on Tzanck smear. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis h​erpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pemphigus vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5181d884268d431ea890df2056387988.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pemphigus vulgaris</strong></p><p><strong>Flaccid</strong> lesions, <strong>mucosal</strong> involvement, and the presence of <strong>acantholytic cells</strong> are suggestive of <strong>pemphigus vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: There is no mucosal involvement in pemphigus foliaceus.</p>\n<p>Option C: Dermatitis herpetiformis presents with multiple small vesicles and no acantholysis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Bullous pemphigoid presents with tense bullae and no acantholysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4990","text":"A patient with a bullous d​isorder is referred to th‌e dermatology OPD for fur‌ther investigations. Dire‌ct immunofluorescence mic‌roscopy is performed. Wha​t is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a bullous disorder is referred to the dermatology OPD for further investigations. Direct immunofluorescence microscopy is performed. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa aqui​sita","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermatitis h​erpetiformis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pemphigus vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/54f316f899a14b8f835f408e7fa8ecab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/156f2788f7c74184963cd732e44fb9dfx512x290.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57c508942f094ac592fb7bc968ba2925x250x193.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pemphigus vulgaris</strong></p><p>The given image shows a characteristic <strong>fish-net </strong>appearance on <strong>direct immunofluorescence,</strong> which is diagnostic of <strong>pemphigus vulgaris</strong>.</p>\n<p>This pattern is due to <strong>intraepidermal</strong> <strong>intercellular</strong> deposition of <strong>IgG</strong> and<strong> C3.</strong></p>\n<p>Direct or indirect immunofluorescence cannot be used to differentiate between pemphigus vulgaris and pemphigus foliaceus, as they present similarly. </p>\n<p>Options A and C: Bullous pemphigoid, cicatricial pemphigoid, and epidermolysis bullosa acquisita present linear or shoreline pattern is seen in due to IgG and C3 deposits along the dermo-epidermal junctions, as seen in the following image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Dermatitis herpetiformis presents with focal granular deposits of IgA at papillary tips, as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4972","text":"What is the most accurate ​diagnostic test for Pemph‌igus vulgaris?","raw_text":"What is the most accurate diagnostic test for Pemphigus vulgaris?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indirect immunofluorescenc​e testing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct immunofluorescence ​testing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Histopa​thology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enzyme Linked Immunosorben​t Assay (ELISA)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct immunofluorescence testing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2dea83b13d534a539f8fd2364f482fbax1280x1302.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct immunofluorescence testing</strong></p><p><strong>Direct immunofluorescence test</strong> is the most accurate test to diagnose <strong>Pemphigus Vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p>Direct Immunofluorescence uses <strong>anti-IgG antibodies</strong> which detects the <strong>anti-desmoglein IgG antibodies</strong> deposited on the <strong>Desmosomes</strong>. These desmosomes are present at cell junctions; this leads to <strong>the fishnet pattern.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4971","text":"A skin biopsy from a patie​nt with an immunobullous ‌condition is shown below.‌ What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A skin biopsy from a patient with an immunobullous condition is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidermoly​sis bullosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pemphigus vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/94453fa5d2b04da0abcc8df1d5fb369a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40a0432644204aad9124580e331bcfcax510x502.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pemphigus vulgaris</strong></p><p>The given image shows a<strong> row of tombstones</strong> appearance, which is classically seen in <strong>pemphigus</strong> <strong>vulgaris.</strong> </p>\n<p>In this condition, there is suprabasal splitting of the epidermis leading to blister formation. The <strong>basal layer</strong> remains adherent to the basement membrane, and gives the resemblance to the row of the tombstones.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4997","text":"A 43-year-old man is refer​red to the dermatology OP‌D with the following skin‌ lesions that have been p‌resent for 4 months. The ‌lesions are extremely pai​nful. On examination, the‍re are flaccid blisters t​hat rupture easily and mu‌cosal surfaces are normal‍. Where does the bullous ​split occur in this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old man is referred to the dermatology OPD with the following skin lesions that have been present for 4 months. The lesions are extremely painful. On examination, there are flaccid blisters that rupture easily and mucosal surfaces are normal. Where does the bullous split occur in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subcorne​al layer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinou​s layer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suprabas​al layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermo-epider​mal junction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subcorneal layer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/356ee8125c4a411d9c06717eccc1556b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subcorneal layer</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pemphigus</strong> <strong>foliaceus. </strong>The <strong>blisters</strong> are formed due to a <strong>split</strong> at the <strong>subcorneal layer.</strong></p>\n<p>Pemphigus foliaceus is a<strong> less severe</strong> variant of pemphigus vulgaris with antibodies against <strong>desmoglein-1</strong>. It presents with fewer <strong>flaccid bullae</strong> and <strong>mucosal sparing</strong>.<strong> </strong>It more commonly results in <strong>erosions</strong> with erythema, scales, and crust. <strong>Collarette</strong> scales may be present. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4969","text":"Which of the following are​ causes of drug-induced p‌emphigus? Penicillamine S‌odium valproate Penicilli‌n Isoniazid Captopril","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are causes of drug-induced pemphigus?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Penicillamine</li>\n<li>Sodium valproate</li>\n<li>Penicillin</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Captopril</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ​3, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><p>Sodium valproate and isoniazid do not cause <strong>drug-induced pemphigus.</strong> The commonly implicated drugs include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Penicillamine</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong></li>\n<li>Antibiotics - <strong>penicillins</strong>, cephalosporins, rifampicin</li>\n<li>Piroxicam</li>\n<li>Glibenclamide</li>\n<li>Phenobarbital</li>\n<li>Aspirin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drug-induced pemphigus usually occurs after <strong>1 year</strong> of taking the drug. It is more common in individuals with other associated autoimmune conditions.</p>\n<p>Note: Common drugs (mnemonic - <strong>PCR</strong>) are <strong>p</strong>enicillamine, <strong>c</strong>aptopril, <strong>r</strong>ifampicin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4970","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ‌paraneoplastic pemphigus?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of paraneoplastic pemphigus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Castlema​n disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-Hodgkin​’s lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Waldenstro​m's disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Paraneoplastic pemphigus</strong> (PNP) is most commonly associated seen with <strong>non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. </strong></p>\n<p>PNP is a <strong>fatal </strong>autoimmune blistering disease associated with underlying <strong>benign</strong> or <strong>malignant</strong> neoplasms. It commonly presents <strong>2-3 years</strong> after the development of malignancy, but can sometimes precede it as well. It is associated with <strong>multiple antigens</strong> including desmoglein, desmoplakin, periplakin.</p>\n<p>Other associated diseases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Waldenstrom's disease</li>\n<li>Castleman disease</li>\n<li>Spindle cell tumor</li>\n<li>Thymoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5002","text":"A 28-year-old woman in her​ 37th week of gestation d‌evelops the following int‌ensely pruritic skin lesi‌ons. On examination, a fe‌w tense bullae are noted ​and mucous membranes are ‍spared. Which of the foll​owing statement is incorr‌ect regarding this condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman in her 37th week of gestation develops the following intensely pruritic skin lesions. On examination, a few tense bullae are noted and mucous membranes are spared. Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The initial site of involv​ement is the thigh","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It resolves spontaneously ​in postpartum period","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with antibodies​ against BP180","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Usually flares during deli​very","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The initial site of involvement is the thigh","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a5efef560534c42ae7c65a970f4346b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The initial site of involvement is the thigh</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>herpes gestationis </strong>or<strong> pemphigoid gestationis. </strong>The initial site of involvement is the <strong>periumbilical </strong>region.</p>\n<p>It is a variant of bullous pemphigoid in pregnancy and is associated with antibodies against <strong>BP180</strong>. It presents in the <strong>II </strong>or<strong> III trimesters</strong> with pruritic, grouped vesicles that later spread to the abdomen, trunk, thighs. It <strong>flares</strong> in the <strong>intrapartum</strong> period and tends to<strong> resolves spontaneously</strong> in the postpartum period. However, it can recur in subsequent pregnancies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4982","text":"A 45-year-old man presents​ to the OPD with skin fra‌gility. On examination, h‌e is noted to have erosio‌ns, bullae, and scarring ‌over the extensor aspects​ of his elbows, hands, an‍d knees. Direct immunoflu​orescence microscopy is g‌iven below. Presence of a‍ntibodies against which o​f the following is diagno​stic of this condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the OPD with skin fragility. On examination, he is noted to have erosions, bullae, and scarring over the extensor aspects of his elbows, hands, and knees. Direct immunofluorescence microscopy is given below. Presence of antibodies against which of the following is diagnostic of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colla​gen V","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collag​en VII","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Collag​en III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collag​en VIII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Collagen VII","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b42779800d64b1682a1ab9b685a0a3e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4492ccca1414092a7de1e41099fddb0x720x501.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collagen VII</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>linear IgG</strong> deposits in a <strong>u-serrated</strong> pattern on direct immunofluorescence microscopy are suggestive of <strong>epidermolysis bullosa acquisita </strong>(EBA), in which autoantibodies are formed against <strong>collagen VII</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>acquired autoimmune</strong> disorder, presenting in adults between <strong>44-54 years</strong>. It has a chronic, <strong>relapsing</strong> course. Two main clinical forms can be differentiated:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classical <strong>mechanobullous</strong> - Skin fragility, erosions, blisters, crusts, and scars on trauma‐prone areas like hands, knuckles, elbows, knees, toes.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflammatory</strong> - Resembles other pemphigoid diseases such as bullous pemphigoid. Associated with inflammatory infiltrate in the dermis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows erosions and crusting in a patient with EBA.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1839","text":"A 24-year-old patient come​s to you with the followi‌ng lesions. He tells you ‌that he used to develop s‌imilar lesions since chil‌dhood but they always hea​l without scarring. His f‍ather also suffers from a​ similar condition. Which‌ protein is defective in ‍this patient?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old patient comes to you with the following lesions. He tells you that he used to develop similar lesions since childhood but they always heal without scarring. His father also suffers from a similar condition. Which protein is defective in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collag​en VII","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K5/​K14","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lam​inin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BP​180","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. K5/K14","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c43dabb810384095b34348a19dc80790.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. K5/K14</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and the image showing <strong>superficial blisters</strong> are suggestive of <strong>epidermolysis bullosa simplex</strong><strong>,</strong> which is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition associated with a defect in <strong>K5/K14</strong> proteins. </p>\n<p>Epidermolysis bullosa congenita is a group of congenital <strong>mechanobullous</strong> disorders that arise after trauma and are common over the hands, feet, elbows, and knees. There are three forms of this condition.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 302px; width: 560px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183.922px; height: 45px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.828px; height: 45px;\"><strong>Defective protein</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.25px; height: 45px;\">\n<p><strong>Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183.922px; height: 33px;\">Epidermolysis bullosa simplex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.828px; height: 33px;\">K5/K14</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.25px; height: 33px;\">\n<p><strong>Intraepidermal</strong> blister with split in basal layer</p>\n<p>Heals without scarring</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183.922px; height: 100px;\">Junctional epidermolysis bullosa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.828px; height: 100px;\">Laminin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.25px; height: 100px;\">\n<p><strong>Dermoepidermal</strong> junction blister with split in lamina lucida layer</p>\n<p>Perioral involvement is  frequent</p>\n<p>May heal with scarring</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183.922px; height: 126px;\">Epidermolysis bullosa dystrophica</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.828px; height: 126px;\">Collagen VII in anchoring fibrils</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.25px; height: 126px;\">\n<p><strong>Subepidermal</strong> tense blisters with defect in sublamina densa</p>\n<p>Frequent mucosal involvement</p>\n<p>Heal with scarring</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5003","text":"A 28-year-old patient comp​lains of the following in‌tensely itchy rash over h‌is elbows, knees, buttock‌s, and upper back. He has‌ been having persistent w​atery loose stools for th‍e past 3 months and repor​ts feeling more tired tha‌n usual. A complete blood‍ count shows microcytic, ​hypochromic anemia. What ​is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old patient complains of the following intensely itchy rash over his elbows, knees, buttocks, and upper back. He has been having persistent watery loose stools for the past 3 months and reports feeling more tired than usual. A complete blood count shows microcytic, hypochromic anemia. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Linear Ig​A disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermatitis h​erpetiformis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dermatitis herpetiformis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ffaeb00411e0493f9f3683339474ea52.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dermatitis herpetiformis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario along with the given image showing pruritic, multiple, <strong>grouped papules </strong>and<strong> vesicles</strong> located on <strong>extensor </strong>aspects is suggestive of <strong>dermatitis herpetiformis</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is associated with gluten-sensitive enteropathy or <strong>celiac</strong> disease. It is also associated with <strong>HLA DQ2/DQ8/</strong><strong>B8</strong>. Deposition of <strong>IgA antitransglutaminase</strong> antibodies in the dermal papillae results in subepidermal blisters and vesicles.</p>\n<p>This condition is associated with a small increase in the <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>T-cell lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4985","text":"A young woman with celiac ​disease comes to you for ‌a follow-up. She strictly‌ follows a gluten-free di‌et, which has resulted in‌ improvement of her diarr​hea. However, the followi‍ng itchy skin lesions hav​e persisted despite her a‌ltered diet. What drug wi‍ll you prescribe?","raw_text":"A young woman with celiac disease comes to you for a follow-up. She strictly follows a gluten-free diet, which has resulted in improvement of her diarrhea. However, the following itchy skin lesions have persisted despite her altered diet. What drug will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dap​sone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oral s​teroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycophenol​ate mofetil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topical ​steroids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dapsone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6f4a6d195eb9404a92011656ccd4a9d4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dapsone</strong></p><p>The given image showing itchy, grouped vesicles in a patient with celiac disease is suggestive of <strong>dermatitis herpetiformis</strong>. The drug of choice is <strong>dapsone.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>initial treatment</strong> of choice for dermatitis herpetiformis is a <strong>gluten-free diet</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dapsone is used when response to dietary modification is inadequate or slow. It is highly effective in such cases and improves symptoms within one week of starting the drug.</p>\n<p>Note: Gluten-containing grains such as<strong> b</strong>arley<strong>, r</strong>ye<strong>, o</strong>ats<strong>, w</strong>heat (<strong>BROW</strong>) should be avoided in the diet. Rice and maize are tolerated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4988","text":"Immunofluorescence microsc​opy of a skin biopsy from‌ a patient shows IgA depo‌sits as shown. Which of t‌he following is incorrect‌ about this condition?","raw_text":"Immunofluorescence microscopy of a skin biopsy from a patient shows IgA deposits as shown. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is intens​ely pruritic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patients present with papu​les and vesicles over ext‌ensor areas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocytic microabscesses​ are seen in dermis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Histopathology reveals sub​epidermal blisters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphocytic microabscesses are seen in dermis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09fa53a1a1904f02b20a6bdbda7fb974.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphocytic microabscesses are seen in dermis</strong></p><p><strong>Granular</strong> deposits of <strong>IgA</strong> on direct immunofluorescence are suggestive of<strong> </strong><strong>dermatitis herpetiformis </strong>or <strong>Duhring's</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. The characteristic microscopic feature is <strong>subepidermal</strong> blisters with <strong>papillary tip microabscesses </strong>containing neutrophils.</p>\n<p>It presents as <strong>grouped</strong> <strong>vesicles</strong> and <strong>papules. </strong>These lesions are commonly located over the <strong>extensor</strong> aspects of knees, elbows, buttocks, back, scalp which are associated with intense<strong> pruritus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5000","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is Nikolsky's ‌sign seen? Pemphigus vulg‌aris Toxic epidermal necr‌olysis Epidermolysis bull‌osa acquisita Pemphigus f​oliaceus Staphylococcal s‍calded skin syndrome","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions is Nikolsky's sign seen?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pemphigus vulgaris</li>\n<li>Toxic epidermal necrolysis</li>\n<li>Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita</li>\n<li>Pemphigus foliaceus</li>\n<li>Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fab4cc3bf4a24a18b069f3817792b1f5x1279x1103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Nikolsky's </strong>sign is <strong>positive</strong> in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pemphigus vulgaris</li>\n<li>Pemphigus foliaceus</li>\n<li>Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nikolsky's sign refers to the <strong>separation</strong> of the normal‐looking <strong>epidermis</strong> from the dermis by applying firm <strong>tangential</strong> sliding pressure, thereby producing an erosion. It is seen in conditions that produce <strong>intraepidermal</strong> blisters. Hence it is negative in subepidermal bullous conditions like bullous pemphigoid and epidermolysis bullosa acquisita.</p>\n<p><strong>Pseudo</strong>-<strong>Nikolsky's</strong> sign refers to <strong>epidermal separation</strong> as a result of epidermal<strong> necrosis</strong>, not acantholysis.</p>\n<p>It is seen in</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Toxic epidermal necrolysis</li>\n<li>Steven-Johnson syndrome </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4992","text":"A 50-year-old man is refer​red to the dermatology OP‌D with the following pain‌ful and malodorous skin l‌esions over his trunk, fa‌ce, and scalp. He also ha​s longitudinal erythronyc‍hia and distal v-shaped n​icks on his nails. Which ‌of the following genes is‍ mutated in this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man is referred to the dermatology OPD with the following painful and malodorous skin lesions over his trunk, face, and scalp. He also has longitudinal erythronychia and distal v-shaped nicks on his nails. Which of the following genes is mutated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ATP​ 2C2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ATP​ 2A2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ATP​ 3A2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ATP​ 2C1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ATP 2A2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37f8e734303f4fdeab007a89b61ff3fc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/970e4aa636f041af8a20a4147f323071x510x552.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ATP 2A2</strong></p><p>The given image showing greasy <strong>hyperkeratotic papules</strong> and the <strong>nail changes</strong> described point to a diagnosis of <strong>Darier’s</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. It occurs due to a mutation of the <strong>ATP2A2</strong> gene.</p>\n<p>Darier's disease is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition that occurs due to dysfunction of the <strong>SERCA2 </strong>calcium channel<strong> </strong>(encoded by ATP2A2 gene), which is essential for desmosomal function. It results in <strong>acantholysis</strong> and <strong>dyskeratosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>The papules begin over sun-exposed areas and gradually expand to form plaques over the seborrheic areas. The following nail changes, also shown in the image below, are characteristic of Darier's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Longitudinal erythronychia</li>\n<li>Longitudinal leukonychia</li>\n<li>Distal v-shaped nicks</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Hailey-Hailey disease</strong> is due to a mutation in <strong>ATP2C1</strong> gene, which also encodes the SERCA protein.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4991","text":"Corps ronds are a feature ​of which of the following‌ conditions?","raw_text":"Corps ronds are a feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratosis f​ollicularis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hailey-Hai​ley disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duhring'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keratosi​s pilaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Keratosis follicularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4931aa008fd45d4a0cb0b110831c022x510x517.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c5e047ca11f42269897934169aed4a5x510x517.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Keratosis follicularis</strong></p><p><strong>Corps ronds</strong> are seen in<strong> keratosis follicularis</strong> or<strong> Darier's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an autosomal dominant condition that is characterized by keratotic papules over the seborrheic areas. The following histopathology findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acantholysis - loss of cohesion between keratinocytes</li>\n<li>Dyskeratosis - abnormal premature keratinization in the epidermis</li>\n<li>Lacunae - in suprabasal area</li>\n<li><strong>Corps ronds</strong> -  <strong>rounded</strong> <strong>dyskeratotic</strong> cells with <strong>eosinophilic</strong> <strong>cytoplasm</strong> in the epidermis  </li>\n<li><strong>Corps grains</strong> - small cells with <strong>shrunken cytoplasm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4995","text":"In which of the following ​conditions does acantholy‌sis lead to a dilapidated‌ brick wall appearance?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions does acantholysis lead to a dilapidated brick wall appearance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus​ foliaceus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hailey-Hai​ley disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Darier's​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hailey-Hailey disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd8306e26df74112a8d7f9cf4f568594x815x271.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hailey-Hailey disease</strong></p><p><strong>Dilapidated </strong><strong>brick wall</strong> appearance is characteristically seen in <strong>Hailey-Hailey </strong>disease.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disorder occurring due to a mutation in the <strong>ATP2C1 </strong>gene encoding the<strong> SERCA </strong>calcium channel ATPase.<strong> </strong>It causes <strong>blistering</strong>, particularly in the <strong>intertriginous</strong> areas.</p>\n<p>On histology, there is a widespread <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>cohesion</strong> between <strong>suprabasal</strong> <strong>keratinocytes</strong> leading to<strong> acantholysis</strong>,<strong> </strong>which<strong> </strong>is<strong> incomplete.</strong> Hence, separated keratinocytes are still partially connected to each other giving the appearance of a dilapidated or broken brick wall, as shown in the following image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4998","text":"What are Tz​anck cells?","raw_text":"What are Tzanck cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Langerha​ns cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kerati​nocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multinucleated giant cells​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histi​ocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Keratinocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71b1b9dd8f51449b83420c091385edf0x510x481.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Keratinocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Tzanck</strong> cells are <strong>keratinocytes</strong>. </p>\n<p>These cells are seen in vesiculobullous disorders, as a result of <strong>acantholysis</strong>. The <strong>keratinocytes</strong> lose their polyhedral shapes and characteristically become <strong>round</strong> with hyperchromatic nuclei and <strong>perinuclear halo</strong>, as seen in the following image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5008","text":"Which of the following is ​diagnosed by the presence‌ of multinucleated giant ‌cells on a Tzanck smear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is diagnosed by the presence of multinucleated giant cells on a Tzanck smear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus​ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Molluscum ​contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster infection​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bullous p​emphigoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Varicella-zoster infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Varicella-zoster infection</strong></p><p><strong>Multinucleated giant cells</strong> on a Tzanck smear are seen in <strong>varicella-zoster </strong>and<strong> herpes simplex </strong>infections. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5nxZU_w4","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"12_tumors_of_ear","title":"12.Tumors Of Ear","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB0057","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site for ‌ear carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for ear carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cartilaginous external aud​itory meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isthmus ​of canal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pi​nna","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mas​toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pinna","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pinna</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> site for ear carcinoma is the <strong>pinna</strong>, followed by the external auditory meatus, the middle ear, and mastoid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0058","text":"Where are sebaceous cysts ​most commonly located in ‌the ear?","raw_text":"Where are sebaceous cysts most commonly located in the ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He​lix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cymba ​concha","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pre auric​ular area","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post auri​cular area","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Post auricular area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Post auricular area</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> site of a sebaceous cyst is<strong> postauricular sulcus</strong> or below and behind the ear lobule. Treatment is total surgical excision.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7571","text":"What is shown in the given​ image?","raw_text":"What is shown in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermoi​d cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sebaceo​us cyst","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post auricu​lar fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sebaceous cyst","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f3cc2f6a26f4f63955b0cf603ca5203.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sebaceous cyst</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>dark-colored summit</strong> over a <strong>swelling</strong>, usually seen in a <strong>sebaceous cyst</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sebaceous cysts</strong> are the<strong> retention cysts</strong> of the <strong>sebaceous gland </strong>that are usually present on the<strong> face, scalp, or scrotum</strong>. They contain<strong> yellowish-white cheesy</strong> material with fat and epithelium.</p>\n<p>Clinical features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The swelling is <strong>painless</strong>, soft, and <strong>freely mobile</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Though <strong>non-transilluminating</strong>, they are Paget's test positive <strong>(fluctuant)</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Punctum</strong> is seen in <strong>70%</strong> of the patients with sebaceous cysts. They are <strong>dark</strong> in color due to the accumulation of<strong> denuded epithelium.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">They also have an <strong>unpleasant sebum odor</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">The swelling can <strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ulcerate</strong> and become <strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">painful</strong> <strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">(</strong><strong>Cock’s peculiar tumor</strong><strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">).</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Some cysts over the scalp may also harden over time (<strong>Sebaceous horn</strong>).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Many may get <strong>infected</strong> and lead to <strong>abscess</strong> formation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Excision including skin adjacent to punctum using elliptical incision-dissection method.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Incision and avulsion of cyst wall.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In the presence of an <strong>abscess</strong>, an initial treatment with an <strong>incision</strong> later followed by <strong>excision</strong> of the cyst</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Dermoid cysts</strong> present as <strong>firm</strong> <strong>painless</strong> subcutaneous nodules and are of 4 types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sequestration dermoid - sequestration of epithelium during embryological development</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tubulodermoid - accumulation of secretions from ectodermal tube/duct</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Implantation dermoid - implantation of the epidermis due to trauma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Teratomatous dermoid - having all 3 germ layers (ecto, meso, and endoderm)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Meningiomas</strong> are <strong>benign</strong> tumors arising from the <strong>meningothelial cells</strong> of the <strong>dura</strong>. They present with <strong>headaches</strong>, <strong>seizures</strong>, or focal neurological deficits.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Postauricular</strong> <strong>cutaneous</strong> <strong>mastoid</strong> <strong>fistula</strong> is a rare complication of <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>otitis</strong> <strong>media</strong> that heals very slowly and needs <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>closure</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0059","text":"A surfer presented with he​aring loss and a feeling ‌of fullness in his ear. O‍n otoscopy, multiple bony‍ swellings were present i​n the external auditory c‍anal. Which of the follow​ing features is diagnosti‍c of exostosis?","raw_text":"A surfer presented with hearing loss and a feeling of fullness in his ear. On otoscopy, multiple bony swellings were present in the external auditory canal. Which of the following features is diagnostic of exostosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unila​teral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Irre​gular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ses​sile","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Seen in cartilaginous cana​l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sessile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sessile</strong></p><p><strong>Exostoses</strong> are <strong>bilateral, multiple, smooth, sessile bony</strong> swellings in the deeper canal seen in divers/swimmers and surfers. It is also called as <strong>s</strong><strong>urfer’s ear</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p><strong>Osteoma</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>Exostosis/Surfer's Ear</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p>Single</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Multiple</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p>Unilateral</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Bilateral</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p>Pedunculated, hard</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Sessile, smooth</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"199\">\n<p>Outer canal</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Inner, deeper canal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0060","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ts with foul-smelling ear‌ discharge and hearing lo‍ss for 1 year. On examina‍tion, a hemorrhagic polyp​oidal mass was seen in th‍e middle ear. Biopsy reve​aled squamous cell carcin‍oma of the middle ear. Wh‍ere is it least likely to‌ spread to?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with foul-smelling ear discharge and hearing loss for 1 year. On examination, a hemorrhagic polypoidal mass was seen in the middle ear. Biopsy revealed squamous cell carcinoma of the middle ear. Where is it least likely to spread to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Du​ra","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facial​ canal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eustach​ian tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Petrou​s apex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dura</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> is least likely to spread to the <strong>dura,</strong> as it is <strong>resistant</strong> to invasion.</p>\n<p>It commonly affects <strong>middle-aged women.</strong> Other clinical features include-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic<strong> foul-smelling </strong>discharge that is usually <strong>blood-stained</strong>.</li>\n<li>Friable, <strong>hemorrhagic granulations</strong></li>\n<li>Polyp</li>\n<li>Hearing loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It usually spreads to</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ossicles</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Facial canal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Internal ear</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Jugular bulb &amp; carotid artery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Petrous pyramid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Parotid &amp; temporomandibular joint</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Eustachian tube, Nasopharynx &amp; Infratemporal fossa.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lymph node involvement is a late finding.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0061","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common benign tu‌mor of the middle ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common benign tumor of the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glomus​ tumour","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acoustic​ neuroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exos​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ost​eoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glomus tumour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glomus tumour</strong></p><p><strong>Glomus tumor </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>benign</strong> neoplasm of the middle ear and is so-named because of its origin from the glomus bodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0062","text":"A post-menopausal woman wi​th progressive hearing lo‌ss and tinnitus is diagno‍sed to have a glomus tumo‍r. Which cells do her tum​or originate from?","raw_text":"A post-menopausal woman with progressive hearing loss and tinnitus is diagnosed to have a glomus tumor. Which cells do her tumor originate from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perivascu​lar cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peritub​al cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paragangli​onic cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paraneopla​stic cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paraganglionic cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paraganglionic cells</strong></p><p><strong>Glomus tumors</strong> originate from <strong>paraganglionic cells</strong> that are derived from neural crest cells. They are found in <strong>glomus bodies</strong> present in the dome of the jugular bulb or promontory. </p>\n<p>These cells are found in 2 sites giving rise to 2 similar yet distinct clinical tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Glomus jugulare</strong>: in the <strong>jugular bulb</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Glomus tympanicum</strong>: over the <strong>promontory</strong> along Jacobson’s nerve(<strong>IX nerve</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0063","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about pa‌ragangliomas of the head ‍and neck?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about paragangliomas of the head and neck?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slow grow​ing tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More comm​on in men","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Usually seen in middle age​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Highly vasc​ular tumour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. More common in men","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. More common in men</strong></p><p><strong>Paragangliomas</strong> of the head and neck, also known as <strong>glomus tumors</strong>, affect <strong>women</strong> more than men.</p>\n<p>Glomus tumors are <strong>benign, non-encapsulated</strong> but <strong>vascular</strong>. They are extremely <strong>slow-growing, locally invasive</strong>, and often seen in <strong>middle age</strong> (40–50 years).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0065","text":"What is the earliest sympt​om of glomus tumors?","raw_text":"What is the earliest symptom of glomus tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensory-neu​ral deafness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Head​ache","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tinn​itus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ver​tigo","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tinnitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tinnitus</strong></p><p>Earliest symptoms of glomus tumor are <strong>tinnitus and hearing loss</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hearing loss is <strong>conductive</strong> and slowly progressive.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0067","text":"You are a junior resident ​in the ENT department. A ‌patient presents to you w‍ith pulsatile tinnitus. W‍hich of the following wil​l be your first different‍ial diagnosis?","raw_text":"You are a junior resident in the ENT department. A patient presents to you with pulsatile tinnitus. Which of the following will be your first differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CS​OM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AS​OM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Masto​iditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glomus​ tumour","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glomus tumour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glomus tumour</strong></p><p><strong>Glomus tumour</strong> will be your first differential diagnosis. <strong>Tinnitus</strong> in glomus tumors is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pulsatile</strong></li>\n<li>Of swishing character</li>\n<li>Synchronous with pulse and</li>\n<li>Can be temporarily stopped by carotid pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0069","text":"You are evaluating a patie​nt with a glomus tumor an‌d on otoscopy, see the fo‍llowing image. Which of t‍he following signs is see​n here?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a patient with a glomus tumor and on otoscopy, see the following image. Which of the following signs is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cartwheel ​appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lightho​use sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rising ​sun sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schwart​ze sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rising sun sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88ad58a638db4ef08d482215314f57f7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13d81baadd69456bb1eb28a2005c592ax1280x1268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rising sun sign</strong></p><p><strong>Rising sun</strong> sign is seen here, as it is a case of <strong>glomus tumor.</strong> The rising sun appearance is seen when a tumor arises from the<strong> floor of the middle ear</strong>. Otoscopy shows a <strong>red reflex</strong> through an <strong>intact tympanic membrane. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A - Cartwheel appearance is the leash of blood vessels appear along the handle of malleus and at the periphery of tympanic membrane.</p>\n<p>Option B - Lighthouse sign is a pulsatile discharge from a pin-hole perforation of pars tensa seen in mastoiditis. </p>\n<p>Option D - Schwartze sign is the reddish hue seen on the promontory through the tympanic membrane in otosclerosis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0071","text":"While evaluating a patient​ with a suspected aural p‌olyp, you apply Siegel's ‍speculum and observe that‍ the mass pulsates vigoro​usly and then blanches. W‍hat is this sign called?","raw_text":"While evaluating a patient with a suspected aural polyp, you apply Siegel's speculum and observe that the mass pulsates vigorously and then blanches. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rising ​sun sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schwarz​e's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lightho​use sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brown'​s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brown's sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brown&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>The sign is called <strong>Brown’s sign</strong> or <strong>pulsation sign </strong>which is suggestive of <strong>glomus tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>When ear canal <strong>pressure</strong> is <strong>raised</strong> with Siegel’s speculum, the <strong>tumor pulsates</strong> vigorously and then <strong>blanches. </strong>The reverse happens with the release of pressure.</p>\n<p>Option A - Rising Sun sign is seen when glomus tumor arises from the floor of the middle ear.</p>\n<p>Option B - Schwarze sign is the reddish hue seen on the promontory through the tympanic membrane in otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>Option C - Lighthouse sign is a pulsatile discharge from a pin-hole perforation of pars tensa seen in mastoiditis.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0072","text":"A patient presents with a ​history of hearing loss a‌nd tinnitus. On examinati‍on, a polypoidal mass is ‍seen in the middle ear, w​hich bleeds heavily on to‍uch. What is the diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a history of hearing loss and tinnitus. On examination, a polypoidal mass is seen in the middle ear, which bleeds heavily on touch. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angiof​ibroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exos​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glomus t​ympanicum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acoustic​ neuroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glomus tympanicum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glomus tympanicum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>glomus tympanicum,</strong> a subtype of glomus tumors<strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p>Symptoms of glomus tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hearing loss</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tinnitus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding </strong>from the <strong>ear</strong></li>\n<li>Vertigo and facial palsy</li>\n<li>Dysphagia, hoarseness of voice (cranial nerve palsies).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examination findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intratympanic tumor: <strong>rising sun appearance </strong>with<strong> </strong>a<strong> positive Brown sign</strong></li>\n<li>Red, vascular <strong>polyp</strong> that <strong>bleeds</strong> readily on <strong>manipulation</strong> or biopsy</li>\n<li>Once the tympanic membrane is breached: bleeding polyp in the external ear</li>\n<li><strong>Audible bruit </strong>over the <strong>mastoid.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0064","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen​ted with hearing loss, pu‌lsatile ringing in the ri‍ght ear, and deviation of‍ the tongue to the right ​side. MRI revealed a mass‍ in the right jugular fos​sa. On examination, a pul‍satile reddish mass was s‍een behind the intact tym‌panic membrane. Which of ​the following structures ‍is invaded by the mass?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presented with hearing loss, pulsatile ringing in the right ear, and deviation of the tongue to the right side. MRI revealed a mass in the right jugular fossa. On examination, a pulsatile reddish mass was seen behind the intact tympanic membrane. Which of the following structures is invaded by the mass?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epity​mpanum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesoty​mpanum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoty​mpanum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mas​toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypotympanum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypotympanum</strong></p><p>The given<strong> </strong>case<strong> </strong>scenario of hearing loss, pulsatile tinnitus, 12th cranial nerve involvement, and red mass on otoscopy is suggestive of <strong>glomus jugulare. Hypotympanum</strong> is invaded by the tumor.</p>\n<p>It arises in the adventitia of the dome of the <strong>jugular bulb</strong> and invades the <strong>hypotympanum</strong>, <strong>jugular foramen,</strong> and <strong>jugular vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>In addition to ear signs and symptoms, <strong>cranial nerve</strong> involvement is commonly seen of the <strong>IXth to XIIth</strong> cranial nerves in glomus jugulare.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0077","text":"A 45-year-old is referred ​to your ENT practice with‌ pulsatile tinnitus, and ‍Brown’s sign is positive.‍ The patient gives you he​r CT films. Which of the ‍following CT signs will h​elp you differentiate bet‍ween glomus jugulare and ‍glomus tympanicum?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old is referred to your ENT practice with pulsatile tinnitus, and Brown’s sign is positive. The patient gives you her CT films. Which of the following CT signs will help you differentiate between glomus jugulare and glomus tympanicum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Battl​e sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lyre​ sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phelp​ sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Griesin​ger sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phelp sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ce510eb141f421aa119c88ad636a68cx720x743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phelp sign</strong></p><p><strong>Phelp sign</strong> helps differentiate between glomus jugulare and glomus tympanicum on<strong> CT</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>erosion</strong> of the spine of the<strong> carotico-jugular crest</strong>, seen in <strong>glomus jugulare</strong> tumors. It is <strong>intact</strong> in <strong>glomus tympanicum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A - Battle's sign is ecchymosis over the mastoid seen in fractures of the temporal bone.</p>\n<p>Option B - Lyre sign- splaying apart of internal and external carotid arteries on angiogram in cases of carotid body tumor of the neck. </p>\n<p>Option D - Griesinger's sign is edema over the mastoid and is seen in lateral sinus thrombosis.</p>\n<p>The image given shows Battle's sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0251","text":"A 68-year old woman presen​ts with complaints of rig‌ht-sided facial weakness ‍for 15 days and progressi‍ve hearing loss for 2 yea​rs. On examination, there‍ is tachycardia and carot​id bruit on the right sid‍e. Otoscopy shows a red m‍ass behind the tympanic m‌embrane and 24-hour urine​ collection shows the pre‍sence of metanephrines. W‌hich of the following cla‍ssification systems is us​ed for this condition?","raw_text":"A 68-year old woman presents with complaints of right-sided facial weakness for 15 days and progressive hearing loss for 2 years. On examination, there is tachycardia and carotid bruit on the right side. Otoscopy shows a red mass behind the tympanic membrane and 24-hour urine collection shows the presence of metanephrines. Which of the following classification systems is used for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lederman's c​lassification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fisch clas​sification","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Austin's cl​assification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sade's cla​ssification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fisch classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fisch classification</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with right-sided facial palsy, hearing loss, red mass behind the tympanic membrane, tachycardia, carotid bruit and positive urinary metanephrines is suggestive of a <strong>functional glomus tumor</strong>. </p>\n<p>10% of all glomus tumors are functional and secrete <strong>catecholamines.</strong> This is diagnosed by testing the patient's 24-hour urine collection for <strong>urinary VMA</strong> (vanillylmandelic acid) or <strong>metanephrines. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Glomus tumors</strong> are classified based on <strong>Fisch classification</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 193px; width: 574.688px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\"><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.688px;\"><strong>Extent </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 41px;\">Class A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.688px; height: 41px;\">Tumor limited to the middle ear (mesotympanum)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 41px;\">Class B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.688px; height: 41px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Tumor in the middle ear (meso- and hypotympanum) and the mastoid</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 41px;\">Class C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.688px; height: 41px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Tumour originates in the dome of the jugular bulb, destroying the overlying bone and has extensive local spread.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 146px; height: 41px;\">Class D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.688px; height: 41px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Intracranial extension. It can be intradural or extradural</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">Option A: Lederman's classification is used in maxillary carcinoma.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">Option C: Austin's classification is used to classify anatomical defects in the ossicular chain.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4101\">Option D: Sade's classification is used to classify atelectasis or retraction of the pars tensa.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0074","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ts with deafness, tinnitu‌s, and bleeding from the ‍ear. On examination, a re‍ddish mass is seen behind​ the tympanic membrane wh‍ich blanches on applying ​pressure with the pneumat‍ic speculum. Which of the‍ following is not a part ‌of its management?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with deafness, tinnitus, and bleeding from the ear. On examination, a reddish mass is seen behind the tympanic membrane which blanches on applying pressure with the pneumatic speculum. Which of the following is not a part of its management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preoperat​ive biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Radiot​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preoperative​ embolisation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sur​gery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Preoperative biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/955d6c1d30fb4f5a8e2c0364d913f016x679x785.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Preoperative biopsy</strong></p><p>A<strong> pre-operative biopsy</strong> is <strong>not done</strong> in the management of glomus tumor due to the following reasons. </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tumour is <strong>highly vascular</strong> and since the <strong>vessels lack</strong> the <strong>contractile</strong> <strong>musculature</strong> it bleeds profusely.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Risk of <strong>injury</strong> to</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>High jugular bulb </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Aberrant internal carotid artery </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical and radiological features are enough to arrive at a diagnosis. Treatment consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgical removal.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiation.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Embolization.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Combination of the above</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Glomus tumor: Circled in red:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Although CT is the test of choice for the evaluation of bone involvement, gadolinium-enhanced MRI provides a superior definition of the relationship of the tumor to adjacent vascular and skull base structures. Hence Both MRI and CT are typically needed for a suspected jugulotympanic paraganglioma given that the information derived from each study is complementary.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0081","text":"What is the most common tu​mor at the cerebelloponti‌ne angle?","raw_text":"What is the most common tumor at the cerebellopontine angle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acoustic​ neuroma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Choles​teatoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glomus​ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acoustic neuroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acoustic neuroma</strong></p><p><strong>Acoustic neuroma</strong> is the most common tumor at the<strong> cerebellopontine angle. </strong>It <strong>arises</strong> from the <strong>vestibular division of the VIIIth nerve</strong> within the internal auditory canal, widens and erodes the canal and appears at the cerebellopontine angle. </p>\n<p>Other tumors arising at the cerebellopontine angle are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Meningioma</li>\n<li>Cholesteatoma</li>\n<li>Arachnoid cysts</li>\n<li>Facial nerve schwannomas</li>\n<li>Glomus tumor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0082","text":"Where does acoustic neurom​a most commonly arise fro‌m?","raw_text":"Where does acoustic neuroma most commonly arise from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vestibular division of 8th​ cranial nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cochlear division of 8th c​ranial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auricul​ar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vestibular division of 8th cranial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vestibular division of 8th cranial nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Acoustic neuroma</strong> or <strong>vestibular schwannoma </strong>almost always arises from the <strong>Schwann cells</strong> of the <strong>vestibular division of 8th nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>It arises from the superior and the inferior division of the vestibular nerve in the <strong>medial internal auditory canal</strong> at the site of transition of central to peripheral myelin i.e., <strong>Obersteiner-Redlich zone.</strong></p>\n<p>Rarely, it can arise from the cochlear division of the 8th nerve within the internal auditory canal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0084","text":"Which of the following fea​tures is common to vestib‌ular schwannomas and glom‍us tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is common to vestibular schwannomas and glomus tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Encaps​ulated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common in middle aged fema​les","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasc​ular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slow g​rowing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Slow growing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Slow growing</strong></p><p>Glomus tumors and vestibular shwannomas are both <strong>slow-growing, benign </strong>tumors of the temporal bone with <strong>locally invasive</strong> properties.</p>\n<p><strong>Vestibular shwannomas </strong>are<strong> encapsulated tumors. </strong>They are<strong> </strong>seen in middle aged individuals and have <strong>equal incidence</strong> in both <strong>sexes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Glomus tumors</strong> are<strong> vascular</strong> paragangliomas and are <strong>non-encapsulated tumors</strong>. They are seen more commonly in <strong>middle-aged women</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0085","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common symptoms ‌that acoustic neuroma pre‍sents with?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common symptoms that acoustic neuroma presents with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conductive ​hearing loss","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ver​tigo","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Head​ache","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sensorineural hearing loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sensorineural hearing loss</strong></p><p><strong>Progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss (SNHL),</strong> often accompanied by tinnitus, is the presenting symptom in the majority of cases.</p>\n<p>Early symptoms of acoustic neuroma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Progressive unilateral SNHL</strong></li>\n<li>Gross inability to understand speech out of proportion to the PTA average</li>\n<li><strong>Tinnitus</strong></li>\n<li>Unsteadiness and <strong>imbalance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The involvement of other cranial nerves leads to other deficits.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0087","text":"Which of the following is ​the earliest cranial nerv‌e to be involved in acous‍tic neuroma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest cranial nerve to be involved in acoustic neuroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5​th","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6​th","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7​th","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8​th","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8th","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8th</strong></p><p>The <strong>8th cranial nerve</strong> is the earliest to be involved in acoustic neuroma.</p>\n<p><strong>Cochleovestibular symptoms </strong>are the <strong>earliest symptoms</strong> when the tumor is still intracanalicular and are caused by pressure on cochlear or vestibular nerve fibers or on the internal auditory artery.</p>\n<p>Once the tumor erodes the canal, it presents in the cerebellopontine angle and can expand to involve other nearby cranial nerves such as the 5th and 7th cranial nerves.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Acoustic neuroma arises from the 8th cranial nerve. Hence, it is the first nerve involved. However, if the 8th cranial nerve is not given as an option, then the answer would be the <strong>5th cranial nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0091","text":"A 50-year-old patient pres​ents with progressive uni‌lateral hearing loss, dec‍reased lacrimation, and l‍oss of taste. On examinin​g the external auditory m‍eatus, there is decreased​ sensation on the posteri‍or side. You suspect acou‍stic neuroma in this case‌. What sign was elicited ​here?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient presents with progressive unilateral hearing loss, decreased lacrimation, and loss of taste. On examining the external auditory meatus, there is decreased sensation on the posterior side. You suspect acoustic neuroma in this case. What sign was elicited here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brown'​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulsati​on sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hitzelber​ger's sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rising ​sun sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hitzelberger's sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hitzelberger&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p><strong>Hitzelberger’s sign</strong> is the sign elicited in the given clinical scenario of <strong>acoustic neuroma. </strong></p>\n<p>Hitzelberger’s sign is the <strong>hypoaesthesia </strong>of the<strong> posterior meatal wall </strong>because the tumor has affected the <strong>sensory fibers</strong> of the <strong>facial nerve. </strong> In this case, sensory fibers of the facial nerve are affected, as the patient has decreased lacrimation and loss of taste. <strong>Motor fibers</strong> are more <strong>resistant</strong> and are affected late. A delayed blink reflex may be an early manifestation. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0092","text":"What is the gold standard ​investigation to confirm ‌acoustic neuroma?","raw_text":"What is the gold standard investigation to confirm acoustic neuroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CE​CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gadolinium ​enhanced MRI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X-​Ray","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bio​psy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gadolinium enhanced MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gadolinium enhanced MRI</strong></p><p><strong>MRI with gadolinium contrast </strong>is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for the diagnosis of acoustic neuroma.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>superior to a CT scan. </strong>Intracanalicular tumors measuring even a <strong>few millimeters</strong> can be easily diagnosed by this method.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0093","text":"A middle-aged-man presents​ with progressive sensori‌neural hearing loss, imba‍lance, and tinnitus. Whic‍h of the following featur​es rules out Meniere’s di‍sease and is diagnostic o​f acoustic neuroma?","raw_text":"A middle-aged-man presents with progressive sensorineural hearing loss, imbalance, and tinnitus. Which of the following features rules out Meniere’s disease and is diagnostic of acoustic neuroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stapedial reflex is presen​t","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recruitment phenomenon is ​present","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High SI​SI score","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rollover phenomenon is see​n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rollover phenomenon is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rollover phenomenon is seen</strong></p><p><strong>Rollover phenomenon</strong> is diagnostic of <strong>acoustic neuroma</strong>. It is a feature of <strong>retro-cochlear/neural lesions</strong> wherein the <strong>speech discrimination</strong> steadily <strong>increases with intensity</strong> but <strong>suddenly drops</strong> significantly beyond a particular intensity of sound.</p>\n<p>Meniere’s disease is an important differential for acoustic neuroma as the patients suffer from similar symptoms. Meniere’s is a cochlear pathology and acoustic neuroma is retrocochlear.</p>\n<p>Option A - Stapedial reflex is present in cochlear lesions (Meniere’s) and absent in retrocochlear lesion (acoustic neuroma)</p>\n<p>Option B - Recruitment phenomenon is the abnormal growth in loudness in the affected ear, seen in cochlear pathologies. </p>\n<p>Option C - SISI (short increment sensitivity score) is a test of intensity discrimination which is very high in cochlear pathologies but low in retro-cochlear.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0094","text":"A 50-year-old man presents​ with difficulty hearing.‌ Pure tone audiometry sho‍ws rollover phenomenon. A‍n MRI with Gadolinium con​trast shows a mass over t‍he cerebellopontine angle​. What is the treatment o‍f choice?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presents with difficulty hearing. Pure tone audiometry shows rollover phenomenon. An MRI with Gadolinium contrast shows a mass over the cerebellopontine angle. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur​gery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Radiot​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemot​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conser​vative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surgery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acoustic neuroma</strong>. <strong>Treatment of choice</strong> in this case is <strong>surgery. </strong></p>\n<p>The approach depends upon site and size of tumor:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Middle cranial fossa approach</li>\n<li>Translabyrinthine approach</li>\n<li>Retrosigmoid/Suboccipital approach</li>\n<li>Combined.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0096","text":"Which of the following is ​not used in the treatment‌ of vestibular schwannoma‍s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used in the treatment of vestibular schwannomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-K​nife","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gamma​ knife","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cold ​knife","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyber​Knife","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cold knife","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cold knife</strong></p><p><strong>Cold knife</strong> is not used in the treatment of vestibular schwannomas. It is used for the <strong>conization of the cervix</strong> in <strong>cervical carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stereotactic radiosurgery</strong> is a reasonable alternative for people who refuse or <strong>cannot tolerate surgery</strong>. Here, beams are modified and focused only to concentrate on the tumor and have little effect on surrounding tissue. It can be done by using </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>X-knife</strong> using a linear accelerator (LINAC)</li>\n<li><strong>Gamma-knife</strong> using Cobalt 60</li>\n<li><strong>CyberKnife</strong> - image-guided linear accelerator using real-time image guidance to deliver radiation from multiple angles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>External beam radiation</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong> as it causes significant <strong>damage</strong> to the <strong>surrounding brain</strong> and neural structures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6YaurZrW","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"12_drowning","title":"12.Drowning","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5875","text":"What does hydrocution refe‌r to?","raw_text":"What does hydrocution refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postmortem‌ submersion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dry lung‌ drowning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cold wate‌r drowning","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Electrocuti‌on in water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cold water drowning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cold water drowning</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrocution</strong> is the term used for <strong>cold water drowning</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hydrocution/ immersion syndrome refers to sudden death following immersion in cold water. Death results from <strong>vagal inhibition</strong> of the heart i.e. cardiac arrest occurring due to increased stimulation of the vagus nerve.</p>\n<p>Vagus stimulation occurs due to the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sudden entry of water into the nasopharynx or larynx</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Falling or diving into the water in a manner so that it suddenly strikes the abdomen, especially the epigastric region</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sudden in-rush of cold water into the ears</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>types</strong> of <strong>drowning</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wet drowning/ typical drowning</li>\n<li>Atypical drowning/ dry drowning\n<ul>\n<li>Immersion syndrome/ hydrocution</li>\n<li>Post-immersion syndrome/ secondary/ near-drowning </li>\n<li>Submersion of the unconscious/ shallow-water drowning</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5892","text":"A 30-year-old fisherman wa‌s brought dead. His autop‍sy findings revealed emph​ysema aquosum. What is th‌e most probable cause of ‌death?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old fisherman was brought dead. His autopsy findings revealed emphysema aquosum. What is the most probable cause of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wet dr‌owning","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shallow-wat‌er drowning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immersion‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary‌ drowning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wet drowning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wet drowning</strong></p><p><strong>Emphysema aquosum</strong> is seen in cases of <strong>wet drowning.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Overinflation</strong> of the lungs in drowning is known as emphysema aquosum. It is one of the most valuable positive signs of drowning.</p>\n<p>The oedema fluid in the bronchi blocks the passive collapse that normally occurs at death. Hence, the lungs are held in the inspiratory position. The <strong>impression</strong> of the <strong>ribs</strong> on this overinflated lung leaves visible and palpable grooves on the lateral surface. </p>\n<p><strong>Autopsy findings</strong> in <strong>drowning</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Froth</strong> in the air passages - most useful autopsy sign in drowning. It consists of a proteinaceous exudate and surfactant mixed with the water of the drowning medium.</li>\n<li><strong>Waterlogging</strong> of the lung - frothy fluid will exude from the bronchi when the lung is squeezed and from the cut surfaces when they are sectioned with a knife. But, the absence of waterlogging by no means excludes true drowning (e.g. dry drowning).</li>\n<li><strong>Emphysema aquosum</strong> - overinflation of the lungs</li>\n<li><strong>Paltauf’s</strong> spots - subpleural haemorrhages that reflect haemolysis within intra-alveolar haemorrhages</li>\n<li>Dilation of the right heart and great veins - subjective and non-specific finding</li>\n<li>Foreign material in the stomach - watery fluid or even foreign material from the water, such as silt, weed or sand</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage into the middle ears and into the temporal bone - a positive non-specific sign of drowning</li>\n<li>Presence of fluid in the middle ear or paranasal sinuses - not a reliable diagnostic sign of drowning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The autopsy findings in drowning are mostly nonspecific and none give conclusive evidence of drowning. The most useful one is <strong>froth in the air passages.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5907","text":"On examining a case of wet‌ drowning, you observe we‍ights that are attached t​o the body to keep it und‌erwater. What does this s‌uggest?","raw_text":"On examining a case of wet drowning, you observe weights that are attached to the body to keep it underwater. What does this suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Homicidal‌ drowning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suicidal‌ drowning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be either homicidal or‌ suicidal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Accidenta‌l drowning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Can be either homicidal or suicidal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Can be either homicidal or suicidal</strong></p><p><strong>Attachment</strong> of <strong>weights</strong> to the body to keep it underwater is consistent with both <strong>homicidal</strong> and <strong>suicidal</strong> drowning.</p>\n<p>Differentiating points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suicidal drowning - determined suicidal person may attach weights to the body or tie his limbs. It is common among women. </li>\n<li>Homicidal drowning - the following additional features may point to a homicidal drowning:\n<ul>\n<li>Constriction/marks around the neck may be present</li>\n<li>Evidence of struggle at the banks of the river from where the body was recovered</li>\n<li>Articles belonging to someone other than the deceased found on the banks of the river associated with those belonging to the deceased</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Accidental drowning</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> form of drowning. It is seen among fishermen, bathers, dock workers, intoxicated, and epileptics. Homicidal drowning is the least common.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5889","text":"In the case of typical dro‌wning, which of the follo‍wing mechanisms is respon​sible for causing death?","raw_text":"In the case of typical drowning, which of the following mechanisms is responsible for causing death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryngeal spasm due to inr‌ush of water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoxemia leading to cereb‌ral edema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vagal inhibition causing c‌ardiac arrest","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary edema and cardia‌c arrest","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary edema and cardiac arrest","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary edema and cardiac arrest</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary edema</strong> and <strong>cardiac arrest</strong> cause death in a case of <strong>typical</strong> <strong>drowning</strong>.</p>\n<p>Death in other types of drowning can be due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dry drowning - laryngeal spasm due to inrush of water</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Near drowning - Hypoxemia leading to <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">sepsis, electrolyte imbalances, pulmonary edema, and </span>cerebral edema</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Immersion syndrome - vagal inhibition causing cardiac arrest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1169","text":"A 45-year-old man drowns w‌hile on an arctic cruise ‍by accidentally falling o​verboard with a horizonta‌l entry into the sea. Whi‌ch of the following is th‌e most likely cause of de‌ath for this individual?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man drowns while on an arctic cruise by accidentally falling overboard with a horizontal entry into the sea. Which of the following is the most likely cause of death for this individual?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebra‌l edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary edema and heart ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasovagal ‌inhibition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Larynge‌al spasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vasovagal inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vasovagal inhibition</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">immersion syndrome/ hydrocution/ submersion inhibition.</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> Death results from cardiac arrest due to </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">vasovagal inhibition </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">followed by cardiac arrest.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Vagal stimulation occurs due to:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Stimulation of nerve endings of the surface of the body by cold water</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Falling or diving into the water in a manner so that it suddenly strikes the abdomen, especially the epigastric region.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Stimulation of the nerve endings of the mucosa by the entry of cold water into the eardrums, nasal passages, the pharynx, and the larynx.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The mechanism involved in immersion syndrome:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Diving/falling into the water, feet-first</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Inexperienced \"duck-diving\"</span></li>\n<li><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Diving/falling </span></strong>into the water <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">with </span></strong>a<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> horizontal entry</span></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">People under the influence of alcohol are more likely affected due to the general vasodilation of skin vessels, consequently amplifying the effects of vasovagal inhibition. These effects are increased in people who are attempting suicide/anxious as they have a more active reflex arc.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Option A: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Cerebral edema </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">is seen in cases of</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> near-drowning. </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It is a type of atypical drowning and is also known as post-immersion syndrome or secondary drowning. This type is necessitated by the survival of the drowning victim </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">beyond 24 hours</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> of a submersion episode. Death is due to respiratory decompensation suffered despite responding well to initial resuscitation measures. Mechanisms of death involve hypoxemia which results in</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">sepsis, electrolyte imbalances, pulmonary edema, and </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">cerebral edema</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Option B: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Pulmonary edema and cardiac arrest</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> are the cause of death in</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> typical drowning/primary drowning </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">due to inhalation of water into the lungs. There are varying mechanisms for freshwater and saltwater drowning leading to either ventricular fibrillation or cardiac arrest respectively. Both types of typical drowning have terminal</span> pulmonary edema and heart failure<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> as mechanisms of death. </span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Option D: In </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">dry drowning</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> death is due to the development of </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">laryngeal spasm</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> and subsequent airway closure. Thick mucus, foam, and froth may develop, producing a plug.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5882","text":"A dead body of a man was r‌etrieved from a lake. Whi‍ch of the following elect​rolyte abnormalities woul‌d you expect in him?","raw_text":"A dead body of a man was retrieved from a lake. Which of the following electrolyte abnormalities would you expect in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyponatremia and hypokalem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypernatremia and hyperkal‌emia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyponatremia and hyperkale‌mia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypernatremia and hypokale‌mia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Freshwater drowning</strong> (drowning in a lake) is associated with both<strong> hyponatremia </strong>and<strong> hyperkalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>When <strong>freshwater </strong>(hypotonic to plasma) enters the alveolar spaces, it is rapidly absorbed into the pulmonary circulation resulting in gross local <strong>hemodilution</strong>. This massive increase in blood volume leads to <strong>hemolysis </strong>with the liberation of<strong> potassium.</strong> This causes<strong> hyperkalemia</strong> with corresponding <strong>hyponatremia</strong>.</p>\n<p>When <strong>seawater</strong> enters, due to high saline content, reverse osmotic flow occurs. The fluid leaves the circulation and enters the alveolar spaces. This causes <strong>hemoconcentration</strong>, <strong>hypernatremia</strong> and pulmonary edema. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5884","text":"Which of the following ele‌ctrolytes is measured in ‍Gettler’s test?","raw_text":"Which of the following electrolytes is measured in Gettler’s test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sod‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pota‌ssium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlo‌ride","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bicar‌bonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chloride</strong></p><p><strong>Gettler's test</strong> measures the <strong>chloride content</strong> of left and right side of the heart.</p>\n<p>A <strong>difference</strong> of <strong>25 mg% </strong>in chloride concentrations between the two sides of the heart is an indication of death due to drowning. This test was used to identify the cause of drowning. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Freshwater</strong> drowning - chloride content of <strong>left</strong> heart is <strong>lower</strong> than that of the right heart</li>\n<li><strong>Saltwater</strong> drowning - chloride content of <strong>right</strong> heart is <strong>lower</strong> than that of the left heart</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gettler's claims have been refuted and the test is no longer accepted.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5887","text":"On postmortem examination ‌of a case of suicidal dro‍wning, you notice the fol​lowing finding. Which of ‌the following statements ‌is true regarding this fi‌nding?","raw_text":"On postmortem examination of a case of suicidal drowning, you notice the following finding. Which of the following statements is true regarding this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This finding indicates tha‌t the person drowned with‍in the last few hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This change occurs faster ‌in warm water than cold w‍ater","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The changes in this findin‌g first affect the back o‍f the hand","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is also known as cutis ‌anserina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. This change occurs faster in warm water than cold water","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cd70eada7318414399a8c50bb56247d1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b163aaa32f604a47a531546375d58539x1280x1150.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4457c09f091645c8905f1f20f2106856x1280x1428.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. This change occurs faster in warm water than cold water</strong></p><p>The image given shows the <strong>skin maceration</strong> or<strong> washerwoman's skin.</strong> It occurs <strong>faster</strong> in <strong>warm</strong> water than in cold water. The signs of immersion are:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Maceration of skin/ washerwoman's skin </strong>- It begins <strong>within minutes </strong>in <strong>warm water </strong>and takes longer in cold water<strong>.</strong> It refers to the wrinkled, pale, and soaked appearance of the skin when immersed in water. This first appears on areas with an appreciable<strong> keratin layer</strong> such as the fingertips palms, backs of the hands, and later the soles. The <strong>first site</strong> affected is the <strong>fingerpads</strong>. It affects the hands earlier than the feet.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows skin maceration.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>2. Cutis anserina</strong> - 'goose-flesh' or piloerection: This is usually found in cold water.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows cutis anserina.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>3. Post-mortem hypostasis</strong> - If the water is still, the postmortem hypostasis affects the heads and the limbs. This is because the chest and abdomen float higher because of the lungs and the abdominal gases.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Foreign bodies in respiratory passages and stomach - </strong>Sand may be found deep in the respiratory passages and stomach, especially if the body has been rolled by the waves on a beach. This is not evidence of live aspiration.</p>\n<p>The signs of immersion just indicate that the body was immersed in water. They are not evidence of death from drowning.</p>\n<p>In temperate climates, the following is an approximate guide to estimate the duration of immersion:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"height: 536px; width: 300px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Finding</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Time since immersion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 59px;\">No wrinkling of finger pads</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 59px;\">Less than a few hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 64px;\">Wrinkled fingers, palms, and feet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 64px;\">Few hours to 3 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 72px;\">Early decomposition (Head and abdomen)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 72px;\">4-10 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 54px;\">Marbling of veins, peeling of the epidermis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 54px;\">2-4 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 99px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 99px;\">Muscle loss with bone exposure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 99px;\">1-2 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5896","text":"A 20-year-old woman's body‌ was retrieved from a man‍hole. You are suspecting ​antemortem drowning. Whic‌h of the following signs ‌will most likely support ‌your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman's body was retrieved from a manhole. You are suspecting antemortem drowning. Which of the following signs will most likely support your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Petechial hemorrhages in t‌he eyes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cutis a‌nserina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cadaver‌ic spasm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Skin ma‌ceration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cadaveric spasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c49cd2c216b4a43bf964b1c6fd7877dx286x283.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cadaveric spasm</strong></p><p><strong>Cadaveric spasm</strong> is an important sign of <strong>antemortem drowning.</strong> It manifests as closed hands with firmly grasped weed, grass, sticks, etc. floating in the water. When present, it is an important sign suggesting that the victim <strong>struggled for existence </strong>while in water.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a cadaveric spasm of hand, holding grass, as seen in antemortem drowning.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5894","text":"Where are Paltauf’s hemorr‌hages seen?","raw_text":"Where are Paltauf’s hemorrhages seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lar‌ynx","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middl‌e ear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lungs</strong></p><p><strong>Paltauf's hemorrhages</strong> are seen in the <strong>lungs</strong> as <strong>subpleural </strong>hemorrhages.</p>\n<p>They are found when the <strong>alveolar walls rupture</strong> as a result of increased pressure during <strong>forced expirations</strong>. They are described in 50–60% of drownings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5903","text":"You are doing an autopsy o‌n a 35-year-old man who w‍as allegedly under the in​fluence of recreational d‌rugs and fell into a ditc‌h. What is the conclusive‌ evidence of death by dro‌wning in this case?","raw_text":"You are doing an autopsy on a 35-year-old man who was allegedly under the influence of recreational drugs and fell into a ditch. What is the conclusive evidence of death by drowning in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of fluid in the p‌aranasal sinuses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilation of right heart an‌d great veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diatoms in the body organs‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of them is a conclusi‌ve evidence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of them is a conclusive evidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of them is a conclusive evidence</strong></p><p>None of the options are considered as conclusive evidence of drowning.</p>\n<p>Option A: The presence of fluid in the paranasal sinuses is not a reliable diagnostic sign of drowning.</p>\n<p>Option B: Dilation of right heart and great veins is a subjective and non-specific finding.</p>\n<p>Option C: At the present time, the <strong>diatom test</strong> should be used only as an<strong> indicative</strong> aid and <strong>not as legal proof </strong>of drowning. Recently, other water organisms have been advocated as tests for drowning, including <strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">soft-bodied protozoa </strong>and <strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">crustaceans.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9931","text":"The body of a missing pers‌on was found tied up in a‍ boat. During the autopsy​, you notice the presence‌ of the following organis‌m in his brain tissue. Wh‌ich of the following is n‌ot a possible cause for t‌his finding?","raw_text":"The body of a missing person was found tied up in a boat. During the autopsy, you notice the presence of the following organism in his brain tissue. Which of the following is not a possible cause for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fresh wate‌r drowning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antemorte‌m drowning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sea water‌ drowning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Postmorte‌m drowning","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Postmortem drowning","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0621364bf4d24efd9c59be57ce65ec10.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Postmortem drowning</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>diatom</strong>. Postmortem drowning does not show diatoms in the brain tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Diatoms</strong> are<strong> microscopic unicellular</strong> or <strong>colonial</strong> <strong>algae</strong> present in water. As the diatoms are <strong>found</strong> in <strong>both</strong> <strong>seawater</strong> and <strong>freshwater</strong>, their presence cannot differentiate between freshwater and seawater drowning.</p>\n<p>The presence of diatoms in the brain tissue is suggestive of <strong>antemortem drowning</strong>, as only a living person can transport diatoms from the lungs to the viscera via circulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rlK3raVa","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"12_cushing_s_syndrome","title":"12.Cushing'S Syndrome","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB6803","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of Cushing’s syndrome​?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of Cushing’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pituitar‌y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal‌ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectopic ACT‌H secretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iatro‌genic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iatrogenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iatrogenic</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>Cushing’s syndrome </strong>is<strong> iatrogenic </strong>due to administration of <strong>corticosteroids</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6808","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses ACTH-dependent Cushin​g's syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cushing’‌s disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal‌ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenocortic‌al carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"McCune-Albri‌ght Syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cushing’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cushing’s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Cushing’s disease</strong> causes <strong>ACTH-dependent </strong>Cushing's syndrome. It occurs due to an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6816","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g Cushing's syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Cushing's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is commonly associated ‌with MEN1 syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchial carcinoid is a p‌ossible cause","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is characterized by hyp‌okalemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with coro‌nary heart disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is commonly associated with MEN1 syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is commonly associated with MEN1 syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong> is <strong>rarely associated</strong> with <strong>MEN1 syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Bronchial carcinoids</strong> are a source of <strong>ectopic ACTH</strong> secretion resulting in <strong>ACTH-dependent</strong> Cushing's syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of Cushing’s syndrome are attributed to the glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and sex steroid actions of cortisol. They include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 249px; width: 400.1875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Body fat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central </strong>or <strong>truncal obesity</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Buffalo hump (fat deposition behind the neck)</li>\n<li>Moon facies  </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Purple striae (over abdomen &amp; flanks)</li>\n<li>Acne and Hirsutism </li>\n<li>Easy bruising </li>\n<li>Facial plethora</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Proximal myopathy</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Metabolic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Poorly controlled hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Reproductive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Oligomenorrhea</li>\n<li>Amenorrhea</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Renal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemic alkalosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">CVS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atherosclerosis</strong> </li>\n<li>Diastolic Hypertension </li>\n<li>Edema </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Hematologic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercoagulable state </li>\n<li>Lymphopenia</li>\n<li>Neutrophilia</li>\n<li>Eosinopenia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">CNS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 360.1875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Depression</li>\n<li>Psychosis </li>\n<li>Anxiety</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6811","text":"A patient with Cushing's s‌yndrome is least likely t​o present with:","raw_text":"A patient with Cushing's syndrome is least likely to present with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Truncal‌ obesity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteop‌orosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal ‌myopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ameno‌rrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Distal myopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Distal myopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Proximal myopathy</strong> is commonly seen in <strong>Cushing’s</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>, not distal myopathy.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of Cushing’s syndrome are attributed to the glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and sex steroid actions of cortisol. They include:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body fat</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central </strong>or <strong>truncal obesity</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Buffalo hump (fat deposition behind the neck)</li>\n<li>Moon facies  </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skin</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Purple striae (over abdomen &amp; flanks)</li>\n<li>Acne and Hirsutism </li>\n<li>Easy bruising </li>\n<li>Facial plethora</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal myopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metabolic</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Poorly controlled hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reproductive</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Oligomenorrhea</li>\n<li>Amenorrhea</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Renal</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypokalemic alkalosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CVS</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Atherosclerosis </li>\n<li>Diastolic Hypertension </li>\n<li>Edema </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hematologic</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercoagulable state </li>\n<li>Lymphopenia</li>\n<li>Neutrophilia</li>\n<li>Eosinopenia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CNS</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Depression</li>\n<li>Psychosis </li>\n<li>Anxiety</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6814","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not be seen in a patie​nt with Cushing’s disease‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with Cushing’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin hyperp‌igmentation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"hypocoagul‌able state","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frank p‌sychosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diastolic h‌ypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. hypocoagulable state","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. hypocoagulable state</strong></p><p><strong>Cushing’s</strong> <strong>disease, </strong>and all other causes of Cushing's syndrome, can result in a <strong>hypercoagulable </strong>state. This is due to an increase in procoagulant factors and inhibition of fibrinolysis.</p>\n<p>Cushing's disease is a form of Cushing's syndrome caused by an ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor. The <strong>high ACTH</strong> <strong>levels</strong> stimulate melanocytes and cause skin <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong>. This is especially seen over friction-prone areas like knuckles and elbows.</p>\n<p>The<strong> majority</strong> of patients with Cushing's syndrome have psychiatric symptoms such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anxiety,</li>\n<li>Depression, and</li>\n<li><strong>Frank psychosis  </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mineralocorticoid</strong> action of cortisol may lead to <strong>diastolic</strong> <strong>hypertension</strong>, hypokalemia, and edema.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6818","text":"A 28-year-old woman visits‌ a gynecologist with comp​laints of irregular and i‌nfrequent menstrual cycle‌s. She is also upset that​ she has gained 12 kg ove‍r the past few months. He‌r face appears as shown i​n the image below. Her bl​ood pressure at this visi​t is 144/98 mm Hg and she​ has multiple purple stri‍ae over her abdomen. Whic​h of the following invest‌igations should you advis​e this patient?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman visits a gynecologist with complaints of irregular and infrequent menstrual cycles. She is also upset that she has gained 12 kg over the past few months. Her face appears as shown in the image below. Her blood pressure at this visit is 144/98 mm Hg and she has multiple purple striae over her abdomen. Which of the following investigations should you advise this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cosyntropin stimulation te‌st","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Midnight serum cortisol me‌asurement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serum testosterone levels ‌and abdominal USG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum LH:‌FSH ratio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Midnight serum cortisol measurement","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0796bae801c4e2c8563b86a3fe33e25.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Midnight serum cortisol measurement</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>moon facies</strong> with<strong> acne </strong>and<strong> hirsutism</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong>. It can be diagnosed with the help of a <strong>single midnight serum </strong><strong>cortisol</strong> measurement.</p>\n<p>Option A: Cosyntropin (ACTH) stimulation test is used for the diagnosis of Addison's disease.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Serum testosterone, abdominal USG, and LH: FSH ratio, are used to confirm a diagnosis of the polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS). Though PCOS can present with similar features, it is not typically associated with moon facies, purple striae, or hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6822","text":"You can advise any of the ‌following tests for scree​ning of a patient suspect‌ed to be having Cushing's‌ syndrome except:","raw_text":"You can advise any of the following tests for screening of a patient suspected to be having Cushing's syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midnight plasma cortisol m‌easurement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Determination of plasma AC‌TH levels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"24 hour urinary free corti‌sol measurement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overnight dexamethasone su‌ppression test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Determination of plasma ACTH levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Determination of plasma ACTH levels</strong></p><p>Determination of<strong> plasma ACTH </strong>levels is <strong>not</strong> advised as a <strong>screening</strong> test for a patient with Cushing's syndrome.</p>\n<p>Plasma ACTH determination is done to distinguish between ACTH-dependent and independent causes. It is done after the screening or confirmation of Cushing's syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6836","text":"In patients with which of ‌the following conditions ​would you expect to see t‌he highest basal ACTH lev‌el?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions would you expect to see the highest basal ACTH level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACTH-secreting pituitary a‌denoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ectopic ACTH-secreting tum‌ors.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bilateral adrenal hyperpla‌sia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iatrogenic hypercortisolis‌m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors.</strong></p><p>Basal plasma ACTH levels are <strong>highest</strong> in <strong>ectopic ACTH-secreting </strong>tumors.</p>\n<p>Plasma <strong>ACTH &gt; 15pg/ml </strong>is suggestive of <strong>ACTH-dependent</strong> Cushing's syndrome due to pituitary or ectopic sources.<strong> </strong>Basal ACTH in <strong>pituitary adenoma</strong> can be <strong>normal or elevated</strong>, whereas in an <strong>ectopic</strong> source the ACTH production is <strong>very high</strong>. This occurs because a pituitary adenoma retains some sensitivity to the negative feedback mechanism exerted by cortisol, but ectopic tumors remain unaffected. </p>\n<p>Plasma <strong>ACTH &lt; 5pg/ml</strong> is suggestive of <strong>ACTH-independent </strong>Cushing’s syndrome that can be caused by an adrenal adenoma or exogenous administration of glucocorticoids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6856","text":"A patient with suspected C‌ushing's syndrome is foun​d to have elevated ACTH l‌evels. What is the invest‌igation of choice?","raw_text":"A patient with suspected Cushing's syndrome is found to have elevated ACTH levels. What is the investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI ‌brain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CRH stimul‌ation test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High-dose dexamethasone su‌ppression test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior petrosal sinus sa‌mpling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MRI brain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1dcf226716b4c109464ecf6ce2b2598x1200x2435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MRI brain</strong></p><p>An elevated ACTH level is suggestive of ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome. The investigation of choice for <strong>ACTH-dependent</strong> <strong>Cushing's</strong> syndrome is <strong>MRI brain, </strong>to look for the presence of a pituitary tumour.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>MRI brain</strong> shows a <strong>mass</strong> it is suggestive of a <strong>pituitary mass</strong> of <strong>&gt;6 mm</strong> in size. However, if the MRI brain does not show a mass it suggests a pituitary tumor of &lt;6mm in size or an ectopic ACTH source.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: High-dose dexamethasone suppression test and CRH stimulation test are employed to differentiate pituitary from ectopic ACTH syndrome. A definitive cause can be established only by imaging techniques like MRI for pituitary tumors, CT scan of the chest and abdomen for ectopic ACTH sites. Hence MRI is considered the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6820","text":"A 56-year-old man chronic ‌smoker presented with fle​cks of blood in his sputu‌m. He is currently on tre‌atment for COPD and hyper​tension. His BMI is 31 kg‍/m² and he has central ob‌esity. His RBS is 261 mg/​dl. During further evalua​tion, he is found to have​ high ACTH levels that re​mained high even after ad‍ministration of high-dose​ dexamethasone. What is t‌he underlying cause of th​is patient's symptoms?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man chronic smoker presented with flecks of blood in his sputum. He is currently on treatment for COPD and hypertension. His BMI is 31 kg/m² and he has central obesity. His RBS is 261 mg/dl. During further evaluation, he is found to have high ACTH levels that remained high even after administration of high-dose dexamethasone. What is the underlying cause of this patient's symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exogenous st‌eroid intake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pituitar‌y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenal cort‌ical adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ectopic ACTH-secreting tum‌or","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumor</strong></p><p>Elevated ACTH levels that do not get suppressed by a high-dose dexamethasone test are suggestive of <strong>Cushing’s</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> secondary to an <strong>ectopic</strong> <strong>ACTH-secreting</strong> tumor. Considering the patient's smoking history and presentation with hemoptysis, it is likely to be a lung tumor.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 478px; width: 272px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 298.141px; text-align: center; height: 20px;\"><strong>Dynamic testing for Cushing’s syndrome</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72.1875px; height: 54px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.812px; height: 54px;\"><strong>High dose dexamethasone suppression test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.1406px; height: 54px;\"><strong>CRH stimulation test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72.1875px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Pituitary adenoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.812px; height: 54px;\">Positive (suppressed cortisol)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.1406px; height: 54px;\">Positive (increase in ACTH and cortisol)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72.1875px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Ectopic ACTH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.812px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 89.1406px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 298.141px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Iatrogenic</strong>: Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 298.141px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Adrenal</strong> mass or hyperplasia:  Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Ectopic sources of ACTH include <strong>small cell lung cancers </strong>and<strong> pulmonary carcinoid </strong>tumors. The most effective treatment strategy is the management of the underlying SCLC or carcinoid. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6847","text":"Evaluation of a patient wi‌th Cushing's syndrome rev​eals high ACTH levels tha‌t are responsive to CRH a‌nd suppressed by high-dos​e dexamethasone. What is ‍the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Evaluation of a patient with Cushing's syndrome reveals high ACTH levels that are responsive to CRH and suppressed by high-dose dexamethasone. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steroid‌ intake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small cell ‌lung cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitar‌y adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenocorti‌cal adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pituitary adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pituitary adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>CRH responsiveness</strong> is seen only in ACTH-dependent hypercortisolism which is due to a <strong>pituitary</strong> <strong>cause</strong>. This is because the pituitary tumor contains corticotrophic cells that are responsive to CRH. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 456px; width: 322px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 312px; text-align: center; height: 20px;\"><strong>Dynamic testing for Cushing’s syndrome</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 79.125px; height: 54px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.812px; height: 54px;\"><strong>High dose dexamethasone suppression test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.0625px; height: 54px;\"><strong>CRH stimulation test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 79.125px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Pituitary adenoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.812px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Positive</strong> (suppressed cortisol)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.0625px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Positive</strong> (increase in ACTH and cortisol)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 79.125px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Ectopic ACTH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.812px; height: 18px;\">Negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.0625px; height: 18px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 312px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Iatrogenic</strong>: Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 312px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Adrenal</strong> mass or hyperplasia:  Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6851","text":"A 50-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with ACTH-depende​nt Cushing's syndrome. As‌ MRI of the pituitary was‌ normal and further testi​ng was unequivocal, she w‍as scheduled for inferior‌ petrosal sinus sampling.​ It revealed a baseline p​etrosal:peripheral ACTH r​atio &gt; 2. What is the tre​atment of choice in this ‍patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman was diagnosed with ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome. As MRI of the pituitary was normal and further testing was unequivocal, she was scheduled for inferior petrosal sinus sampling. It revealed a baseline petrosal:peripheral ACTH ratio &gt; 2. What is the treatment of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral a‌drenalectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transsphenoidal pituitary ‌surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Removal of e‌ctopic tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stereotactic‌ radiotherapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transsphenoidal pituitary surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b31658a4af54ca0bdaa1e366acb74c3x1200x2435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transsphenoidal pituitary surgery</strong></p><p>Cushing’s syndrome with <strong>petrosal: peripheral ACTH ratio &gt; 2</strong> at baseline confirms the presence of an ACTH-secreting <strong>pituitary adenoma</strong>. The treatment of choice is <strong>transsphenoidal pituitary surgery. </strong>Radiotherapy is used in case of recurrence of tumors.</p>\n<p>Inferior petrosal sinus sampling is performed to differentiate pituitary and ectopic sources of ACTH when initial imaging and testing are inconclusive.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6859","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not be used to treat p​atients with Cushing’s sy‌ndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be used to treat patients with Cushing’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metyr‌apone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mito‌mycin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mitomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mitomycin</strong></p><p>Mitomycin is not used in the treatment of <strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong>. The drugs used in its management include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metyrapone</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ketoconazole</strong></li>\n<li>Aminoglutethimide</li>\n<li>Trilostane</li>\n<li>Cyproheptadine</li>\n<li>Mitotane – Mainly in adrenal carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Etomidate</strong> – Severe cortisol excess</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6860","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best adrenolytic agen​t that can be used in the‌ management of patients w‌ith adrenocortical carcin​oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best adrenolytic agent that can be used in the management of patients with adrenocortical carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mito‌tane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metyr‌apone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trilo‌stane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitotane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitotane</strong></p><p><strong>Mitotane</strong> is used in the management of adrenocortical carcinoma due to its <strong>adrenolytic </strong>properties<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Mitotane is an insecticide derivative. It is effective in the treatment of benign Cushing's also, but is rarely used due to its side effects. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6862","text":"Which of the following com‌plications is associated ​with bilateral adrenalect‌omy?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications is associated with bilateral adrenalectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sheehan’s‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Addison'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen sy‌ndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nelson's‌ syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nelson's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nelson&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Nelson</strong><strong>’</strong><strong>s syndrome </strong>is a rare complication of <strong>total</strong> <strong>bilateral</strong> <strong>adrenalectomy </strong>(TBA). TBA is a procedure used to control hypercortisolism in Cushing's disease (due to ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma) when resection of the primary tumor is not possible.</p>\n<p>The loss of feedback inhibition of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis following TBA increases CRH. This leads to stimulation of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> ACTH-secreting corticotroph cells</strong>, causing enlargement of the original pituitary tumor or the formation of a new one.</p>\n<p>Nelson's syndrome is associated with a clinical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Excessive ACTH secretion</li>\n<li>Corticotroph adenoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Screening is done with <strong>fasting plasma ACTH levels</strong> and <strong>MRI</strong> <strong>brain</strong> is performed at regular intervals. Prophylactic pituitary irradiation may be recommended to prevent its incidence. The primary treatment is <strong>transsphenoidal surgery</strong>. Patients will also require life-long glucocorticoid replacement, as they do not produce cortisol.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2728\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The use of steroidogenic inhibitors in the treatment of Cushing's disease has decreased the need for bilateral adrenalectomy these days.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option A: Sheehan’s syndrome refers to postpartum hypopituitarism that is caused by ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary as a result of severe postpartum hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Option B: Addison's disease occurs due to autoimmune destruction of adrenal glands.</p>\n<p>Option C: Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome refers to adrenal apoplexy that is caused by fulminant meningococcemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6864","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause of pseudo-Cus​hing's syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of pseudo-Cushing's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcoh‌olism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Malignancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Malignancy</strong></p><p><strong>Malignancy</strong> does not cause pseudo-Cushing's syndrome. It may cause <strong>ectopic ACTH </strong>production, leading to <strong>true Cushing’s </strong>syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Pseudo-Cushing’s</strong> syndrome refers to functional hypercortisolism caused by <strong>chronic activation</strong> of the <strong>HPA axis</strong>. It presents with clinical and biochemical features similar to Cushing’s syndrome, making it difficult to differentiate between them. It may occur secondary to the following conditions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severe major depressive disorder</strong></li>\n<li>Polycystic ovarian syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Insulin-resistant obesity</strong></li>\n<li>Obstructive sleep apnea</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic alcoholism</strong></li>\n<li>End-stage renal disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It usually resolves when the underlying condition is treated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"huGE7oDT","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"12_escherichia__proteus_and_klebsiella","title":"12.Escherichia, Proteus And Klebsiella","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA2176","text":"An old woman presented to ‌the OPD with dysuria and ​frequent micturition. Uri​nalysis showed pus cells,‍ and culture on MacConkey​ agar showed the followin‍g. Which organism is invo​lved here?","raw_text":"An old woman presented to the OPD with dysuria and frequent micturition. Urinalysis showed pus cells, and culture on MacConkey agar showed the following. Which organism is involved here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esche‌richia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Salmo‌nella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shig‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pro‌teus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Escherichia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9392018bce284cbf9110fc8e40ae68f5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/604e93dedce447ba8e26ff30fe940ef4x1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Escherichia</strong></p><p>This patient who presented with dysuria and frequent micturition with urinalysis showing pus cells, and growth of <strong>lactose-fermenting</strong> colonies on MacConkey agar suggests a diagnosis of <strong>urinary tract infection </strong>by <em><strong>Escherichia coli.</strong> </em></p>\n<p><strong>Enterobacteriaceae family</strong> is <strong>classified</strong> based on their action on<strong> lactose</strong> present in MacConkey's medium as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lactose fermenters - <em>Escherichia, Klebsiella</em></li>\n<li>Late lactose fermenters - <em>Shigella sonnei</em></li>\n<li>Non-lactose fermenters- <em>Salmonella</em> species, <em>Shigella</em> species</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows lactose fermenting and non-lactose fermenting colonies on MacConkey agar. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Mnemonic to remember non-lactose fermenters:<strong> (SPY PSM)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>S</strong>almonella</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>P</strong>roteus</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>Y</strong>ersinia</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>P</strong>rovidencia-</em> late fermenter</li>\n<li><em><strong>S</strong>higella</em> (except <em>S.sonnei</em> which is a late lactose fermenter)</li>\n<li><em><strong>M</strong>organella</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2583","text":"The stool culture of a pat‌ient with suspected food ​poisoning produced hemoly​tic colonies on blood aga‍r. The isolated organism ​gave a positive indole te‍st and a positive MUG tes​t. You observe the follow‍ing colonies on eosin met‍hylene blue agar. Identif‌y the organism. MUG - 4-m​ethylumbelliferyl-β-glucu‌ronide","raw_text":"<p>The stool culture of a patient with suspected food poisoning produced hemolytic colonies on blood agar. The isolated organism gave a positive indole test and a positive MUG test. You observe the following colonies on eosin methylene blue agar. Identify the organism.</p>\n<p>MUG - 4-methylumbelliferyl-β-glucuronide</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Escheric‌hia coli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella‌ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proteus ‌vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella ‌granulomatis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Escherichia coli","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d4bdbae7c2545968fc2c05d801e4ff6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Escherichia coli</strong></p><p>The given scenario of a patient with suspected food poisoning with <strong>stool</strong> culture growing <strong>hemolytic colony</strong> on blood agar with a <strong>positive indole</strong> test and MUG test with a <strong>iridescent green</strong> colony growth on <strong>eosin methylene blue</strong> agar is characteristic of<strong> <em>Escherichia coli. </em></strong></p>\n<p><em>E. coli</em> produces the enzyme <strong>β-glucuronidase</strong> which is tested using the <strong>MUG test. </strong>In it, the substrate MUG (4-methylumbelliferyl-β-glucuronide) is reduced by the enzyme to <strong>produce a fluorescent color</strong> which indicates a positive MUG test.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2178","text":"What is the cut-off for ba‌cteriuria beyond which a ​diagnosis of catheter-ass​ociated urinary tract inf‍ection (CAUTI) can be mad​e?","raw_text":" What is the cut-off for bacteriuria beyond which a diagnosis of catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI) can be made?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;10^‌2 CFU/ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;10^‌3 CFU/ml","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;10^‌4 CFU/ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;10^‌5 CFU/ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. >10^3 CFU/ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &gt;10^3 CFU/ml</strong></p><p>The <strong>cut-off</strong> value for bacteriuria to diagnose catheter-associated <strong>urinary tract infection</strong> (CAUTI) is <strong>&gt;10<sup>3 </sup></strong>CFU/mL.</p>\n<p>The minimum criterion for catheter-associated <strong>asymptomatic bacteriuria</strong> (CA-ASB) is <strong>&gt;10<sup>2</sup></strong><sup> </sup>CFU/mL.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2184","text":"A tourist came to the OPD ‌with complaints of abdomi​nal pain and watery diarr​hea. Which of the followi‍ng strains of E. coli is ​most commonly implicated ‍in this case?","raw_text":"A tourist came to the OPD with complaints of abdominal pain and watery diarrhea. Which of the following strains of E. coli is most commonly implicated in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enteropathog‌enic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enterohemorr‌hagic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterotoxig‌enic E. coli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enteroinvas‌ive E. coli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enterotoxigenic E. coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enterotoxigenic E. coli</strong></p><p>This patient with watery diarrhea most likely has <strong>traveller’s diarrhea</strong> caused by<strong> Enterotoxigenic<em> Escherichia coli</em> </strong>(ETEC). </p>\n<p>Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) is the most common organism that causes diarrhea in travelers. It produces the following toxins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>LT (heat-labile toxin) - acts by <strong>↑cAMP</strong></li>\n<li>ST (heat-stable toxin) - two types of ST are known, ST-A (ST-I) and ST-B (ST-II)\n<ul>\n<li>ST-A is soluble in methanol and acts by ↑ <strong>cGMP</strong> in the intestine.</li>\n<li>ST-B is insoluble in methanol, and the mode of action is not known.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Enteropathogenic</strong> E. coli (EPEC) causes <strong>infantile</strong> diarrhea in developing countries. It adheres to the mucosal surface of the small intestine using E. coli adherent factor (EAF) and locus of enterocyte effacement (LEE) and causes effacement of microvilli. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Enterohemorrhagic</strong> E. coli (EHEC) produces <strong>verocytotoxin,</strong> which targets vascular endothelium. It causes hemorrhagic colitis and hemolytic uremic syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Enteroinvasive</strong> E.coli (EIEC) invades <strong>interstitial epithelial</strong> cells in the intestine and causes a condition resembling <strong>shigellosis.</strong> This ability is determined by a plasmid, which can be detected as a diagnostic test. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5187","text":"Identify the organism belo‌w that causes effacement ​and attachment of intesti​nal cells.","raw_text":"Identify the organism below that causes effacement and attachment of intestinal cells.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ET‌EC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EI‌EC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EP‌EC","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"EA‌EC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. EPEC","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dd8c6b12e06c4a618c5833f64e86a693.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb665fb7793947eabae4829a87df5a89x1280x1046.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bc3d14de6014f70bf6970fdc8a7a784x720x604.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. EPEC</strong></p><p><strong>Attachment</strong> and <strong>effacement lesions</strong> on intestinal cells are caused by <strong>enteropathogenic<em> Escherichia coli</em> </strong>(EPEC).</p>\n<p><em>E. coli</em> is the second most common cause of diarrhea in the pediatric age group. Rotavirus is the most common cause. EPEC causes<strong> watery diarrhea</strong> which is <strong>self-limiting</strong>.</p>\n<p>EPEC adheres to the mucosal cells of the <strong>small bowel</strong> and causes <strong>loss of microvilli</strong> (effacement). This leads to the formation of <strong>filamentous actin pedestals</strong> or cuplike structures and occasionally may cause entry of the EPEC into the mucosal cells.</p>\n<p> <strong>Pathogenicity</strong> requires the presence of two important <strong>factors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Bundle-forming pilus</strong> encoded by plasmid containing EPEC adherence factor (EAF).</li>\n<li> Chromosomal locus of enterocyte effacement <strong>(LEE) pathogenicity island</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows an electron microscopy image of E Coli.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2189","text":"You are investigating an o‌utbreak of diarrhea. You ​find out that the etiolog​ical agent causes stacked‍-brick formation on hep-2​ cells as shown below. Wh‍at is the likely organism​?","raw_text":"You are investigating an outbreak of diarrhea.  You find out that the etiological agent causes stacked-brick formation on hep-2 cells as shown below.  What is the likely organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enteroaggreg‌ative E.coli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enterohemorr‌hagic E.coli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterotoxig‌enic E.coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enteroinva‌sive E.coli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enteroaggregative E.coli","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da8e0cdf96264d888ecf25f97a49da62.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/109033e58654480a862d35410bf3ad79x510x499.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enteroaggregative E.coli</strong></p><p>The<strong> stacked-brick pattern </strong>of adherence on <strong>hep-2 cells</strong> is caused by<strong> enteroaggregative<em> E. coli</em></strong> (EAEC).</p>\n<p>Enteroaggregative<em> E. coli</em><strong> </strong>has been associated with <strong>persistent diarrhea</strong>. Intestinal colonization of EAEC is mediated by <strong>aggregative adherence fimbriae</strong> (AAF). <strong>EAST-1</strong> toxin (enteroaggregative heat-stable toxin) is considered to be a major <strong>virulence</strong> factor for EAEC.</p>\n<p>The given image shows the stacked-brick pattern of adherence on microscopy at 600x magnification. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2192","text":"A 29-year-old woman presen‌ted with bloody diarrhea,​ abdominal pain, hemolyti​c anemia, thrombocytopeni‍a, and acute renal failur​e. She was diagnosed with‍ hemolytic uremic syndrom​e and treated with plasma‍pheresis and dialysis. Wh‍at is false about the inf‌ectious causative agent?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old woman presented with bloody diarrhea, abdominal pain, hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute renal failure. She was diagnosed with hemolytic uremic syndrome and treated with plasmapheresis and dialysis. What is false about the infectious causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serotype O157:H7 causes he‌molytic-uremic syndrome.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It does not produce a Shig‌a like toxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fecal leukocytes may be se‌en","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is person to person ‌transmission.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It does not produce a Shiga like toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It does not produce a Shiga like toxin</strong></p><p><strong>Hemolytic-uremic syndrome </strong>(HUS) is caused by enterohemorrhagic<em> E. coli</em> <strong>(EHEC)</strong> serotype <strong>O157:H7. </strong>It <strong>produces a Shiga like toxin</strong>, therefore EHEC is also known as Shiga toxin-producing <em>E. coli</em> (STEC).</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A - Strains of <em><strong>E. coli</strong></em> producing <strong>verocytotoxin</strong> or Shiga toxin (stx2a-g) can cause diarrheal disease ranging from mild <strong>diarrhea</strong> to fatal <strong>hemorrhagic colitis</strong> and <strong>HUS. </strong>The <strong>most common</strong> EHEC belongs to serotype <strong>O157:H7.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C - In <strong>EHEC</strong> diarrhea, <strong>fecal leukocytes</strong> are commonly seen (70%) but although it is an infectious condition, fever is not seen. The<strong> absence of fever</strong> can lead to its misdiagnosis as a non-infectious condition such as intussusception or ischemic bowel disease.</p>\n<p>Option D - The disease is <strong>transmitted</strong> through <strong>contaminated feces</strong>. Person-to-person transmission can occur if infected persons do not wash their hands after using the toilet or after changing diapers.</p>\n<p>Note: Shiga toxin produced by <em>S. dysenteriae</em> is referred to as Stx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2230","text":"Sereny test was employed f‌or laboratory diagnosis o​f which of the following ​organisms?","raw_text":"Sereny test was employed for laboratory diagnosis of which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterotoxig‌enic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enteropathog‌enic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterohemorr‌hagic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enteroinvas‌ive E. coli","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enteroinvasive E. coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enteroinvasive E. coli</strong></p><p>The<strong> Sereny test</strong> is an animal pathogenicity test that used to be employed for laboratory diagnosis of <strong>enteroinvasive<em> E. coli</em> </strong>and<strong> Shigella.</strong></p>\n<p>Freshly isolated suspension of <strong>bacteria</strong> is instilled <strong>into the eyes</strong> of guinea pigs which leads to <strong>mucopurulent conjunctivitis</strong> and <strong>severe keratitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>not in use anymore</strong> as we have better methods of detecting toxigenicity now like molecular methods.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2212","text":"What is the culture media ‌used to differentiate E. ​coli O157:H7 from other E​. coli?","raw_text":"What is the culture media used to differentiate E. coli O157:H7 from other E. coli?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sorbitol-Ma‌cConkey agar","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mannitol ‌salt agar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystine-lactose-electrolyt‌e deficient medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiosulphate citrate Bile ‌salt sucrose medium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sorbitol-MacConkey agar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sorbitol-MacConkey agar</strong></p><p><strong>Sorbitol-MacConkey agar</strong> is used to <strong>differentiate </strong><em>E. coli</em> O157:H7 from other <em>E. coli</em>.</p>\n<p><em>E. coli </em><strong>O157:H7</strong> does<strong> not</strong> <strong>ferment sorbitol</strong> and produces <strong>pale colonies</strong> on Sorbitol-MacConkey agar. All <strong>other</strong> strains of <em>E. coli</em><strong> ferment sorbitol</strong>. Thus they produce <strong>pink colonies</strong> on Sorbitol-MacConkey agar.</p>\n<p>O157:H7 strains do <strong>not contain</strong> <strong>β-glucuronidase</strong> and so are unable to use the substrate 4-methylumbelliferyl-β-glucuronide(MUG). Thus, they give a <strong>negative MUG test</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6939","text":"Match the following E.coli‌ with their respective pa​thogenesis. E. coli Mecha​nism of action 1. ETEC A.‍ Produces a heat-stable t​oxin and causes activatio‍n of guanylyl cyclase 2. ​EIEC B. Invades intestina‍l mucosal cells 3. EHEC C‍. Produces a toxin agains‌t vascular endothelium ce​lls 4. EPEC D. Adheres to‌ the mucosal surface and ‌causes effacement of micr​ovilli","raw_text":"<p>Match the following E.coli with their respective pathogenesis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 73px; width: 533.898px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\"><em><strong>E. coli</strong></em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 438.898px;\"><strong>Mechanism of action</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">1. ETEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 438.898px;\">A. Produces a heat-stable toxin and causes activation of guanylyl cyclase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">2. EIEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 438.898px;\">B. Invades intestinal mucosal cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">3. EHEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 438.898px;\">C. Produces a toxin against vascular endothelium cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">4. EPEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 438.898px;\">D. Adheres to the mucosal surface and causes effacement of microvilli</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-4,B-3‌,C-2,D-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-1,B-3‌,C-2,D-4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-1,B-2‌,C-3,D-4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A-4,B-2‌,C-3.D-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A-1,B-2,C-3,D-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A-1,B-2,C-3,D-4</strong></p><p>The correctly matched pairs are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px; width: 502px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.8203px;\"><em><strong>E. coli</strong></em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 416.18px;\"><strong>Mechanism of action</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.8203px;\">1. ETEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 416.18px;\">A. Produce a heat-stable toxin and cause activation of guanylyl cyclase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.8203px;\">2. EIEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 416.18px;\">B. Invades intestinal mucosal cells  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.8203px;\">3. EHEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 416.18px;\">C. Produces a toxin against vascular endothelium cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.8203px;\">4. EPEC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 416.18px;\">D. Adhere to the mucosal surface and cause effacement of microvilli</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The various strains of <em>Escherichia coli</em> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enterotoxigenic</strong> <em>E. </em><em>coli</em> (ETEC) is the most common organism <strong>that</strong> causes <strong>diarrhea</strong> in <strong><strong>travelers</strong>. </strong>It produces the following toxins:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>LT </strong>(heat-labile toxin)- acts by<strong> ↑cAMP</strong></li>\n<li><strong>ST </strong>(heat-stable toxin)- two types of ST are known, ST-A (ST-I) and ST-B (ST-II)\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ST-A</strong> is soluble in methanol and acts by <strong>↑cGMP</strong> in the intestine. </li>\n<li><strong>ST-B </strong>is insoluble in methanol, and the mode of <strong>action</strong> is <strong>not known</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enteroinvasive</strong><em> E. coli</em><strong> </strong>(EIEC)<strong> invade interstitial epithelial cells</strong> in the intestine and cause a condition <strong>resembling shigellosis</strong>. This ability is determined by a <strong>plasmid, detection</strong> of which can also be a diagnostic test. <strong>Sereny's test</strong> was employed to diagnose EIEC but it is not in use anymore.</li>\n<li><strong>Enterohemorrhagic</strong> <em>E.</em><em> coli</em><strong><em> </em></strong>(EHEC) produces <strong>verocytotoxin,</strong> which targets vascular endothelium. It causes <strong>hemorrhagic colitis</strong> and <strong>hemolytic uremic syndrome.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Enteropathogenic</strong> <em>E.</em><em> coli</em><em> </em>(EPEC) causes <strong>infantile diarrhea</strong> in developing countries. It adheres to the mucosal surface of small intestine using EAF and LEE and causes <strong>effacement</strong> of <strong>microvilli</strong>. This leads to <strong>attachment</strong> and <strong>effacement</strong> lesions of intestinal cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Enteroaggregative</strong> <em>E.</em><em> coli</em><strong> </strong>(EAEC)<strong> </strong>produces the <strong>stacked brick pattern</strong> of adherence on hep-2 cells. EAEC has been associated with <strong>persistent diarrhea</strong>. Intestinal colonization of EAEC is mediated by AAF (aggregative adherence fimbriae). EAEC produces EAST-1 toxin (enteroaggregative heat-stable toxin) which is considered to be a major virulence factor.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2246","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E.coli is an aerobe and fa‌cultative anaerobe.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proteus infection increase‌s risk of uric acid stone​s.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"E.coli is motile by peritr‌ichous flagella.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proteus can metabolise phe‌nylalanine to phenylpyruv​ic acid.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proteus infection increases risk of uric acid stones.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proteus infection increases risk of uric acid stones.</strong></p><p><em><strong>Proteus</strong></em> increases the risk of <strong>struvite</strong> <strong>stones</strong> in the bladder as it leads to the <strong>alkalinisation of urine. </strong>Struvite stones are <strong>magnesium-ammonium phosphate stones</strong> that appear as rectangular/<strong>coffin-lid crystals</strong> shaped on urine microscopy. Uric acid stones are precipitated in acidic urine.</p>\n<p><em>Proteus</em> produces enzyme phenylalanine deaminase which converts phenylalanine to phenyl pyruvic acid. This property is the basis for the <strong>phenylalanine deaminase test</strong>.</p>\n<p>The test is performed by inoculating the organism in <strong>phenylalanine agar</strong>. After incubation, <strong>10% ferric chloride</strong> is added to the media. If phenyl pyruvic acid was produced, it will react with the ferric chloride and <strong>turn dark green</strong>.  If the medium remains a straw colour, the organism is negative for phenylalanine deaminase production.</p>\n<p><strong><em>E. coli</em></strong> is an aerobe and facultative anaerobe. Its motility is due to the presence of<strong> peritrichous flagella</strong>. It is the most common cause of <strong>urinary tract infections.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2251","text":"Which of the following sho‌ws Dienes phenomenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following shows Dienes phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proteus ‌mirabilis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella ‌granulomatis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Providenci‌a stuartii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serratia ‌marcescens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proteus mirabilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f80999d184543d4a4f17c57d70fb6c5x512x398.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proteus mirabilis</strong></p><p><strong>Dienes phenomenon</strong> is exhibited by <em><strong>Proteus mirabilis</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>When two <strong>identical strains </strong>of <em>Proteus</em> are inoculated at different places of the <strong>same culture plate</strong>, the resulting swarms of <strong>growth coalesce</strong> and no line is formed between swarming cultures of the same strain. However, if they are from <strong>two different strains</strong>, the spreading films of growth <strong>fail to coalesce</strong> and remain separated by a narrow but easily visible <strong>furrow </strong>(known as <strong>Dienes line</strong>). This is known as <strong>Dienes phenomenon.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows Dienes phenomenon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2250","text":"Blood culture of the sputu‌m sample taken from a pat​ient diagnosed with hospi​tal-acquired pneumonia, s‍howed a characteristic ph​enomenon as shown in the ‍image. Identify the bacte​ria that exhibit this phe‍nomenon.","raw_text":"Blood culture of the sputum sample taken from a patient diagnosed with hospital-acquired pneumonia, showed a characteristic phenomenon as shown in the image. Identify the bacteria that exhibit this phenomenon.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebs‌iella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Entero‌bacter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serr‌atia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pro‌teus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proteus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b691f6f7058d4ad8b0279dbc2eaab09f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proteus</strong></p><p>The given image of the <strong>blood culture</strong> finding shows the characteristic <strong>bull's eye</strong> <strong>swarming </strong>pattern exhibited by <em><strong>Proteus</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>Proteus species move very actively with the help of <strong>peritrichous flagella</strong>, resulting in <strong>swarming</strong> on <strong>solid media.</strong> It can be <strong>suppressed</strong> <strong>by</strong> the addition of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>phenyl ethyl alcohol</li>\n<li>chloral hydrate</li>\n<li>sulphonamides</li>\n<li>sodium azide</li>\n<li>boric acid</li>\n<li>increasing the concentration of agar to 6%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Swarming does <strong>not occur</strong> on <strong>MacConkey medium</strong> and cystine-lactose-electrolyte-deficient (<strong>CLED</strong>) <strong>medium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Proteus is a gram-negative bacillus seen as a <strong>normal gastrointestinal</strong> flora. The two most common species causing infection in humans are P. mirabilis and P. vulgaris, which can be differentiated by spot indole test. P. <strong>mirabilis</strong> is <strong>indole negative</strong> while P. <strong>vulgaris</strong> is <strong>indole positive.</strong> Production of urease enzyme by Proteus, which hydrolyses urea to ammonia causes alkalinization of urine. Alkalinization of urine leads to the precipitation of organic and inorganic compounds which contributes to the formation of Struvite stones. P. mirabilis is associated with urinary tract infections while P. vulgaris is usually associated with <strong>wound</strong> and <strong>soft tissue</strong> infections. </p>\n<p>Other organisms that show swarming include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Clostridium tetani</em></li>\n<li><em>Vibrio parahaemolyticus</em></li>\n<li><em>Serratia</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6050","text":"A middle-aged man from nor‌th India presents with a ​deformity as shown in the​ image below. He gives a ‍history of foul-smelling ​purulent nasal discharge ‍before this nodule appear​ed. Which of the followin‍g organisms is responsibl‍e for his condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man from north India presents with a deformity as shown in the image below. He gives a history of foul-smelling purulent nasal discharge before this nodule appeared. Which of the following organisms is responsible for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhinosporid‌ium seeberi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella rhinoscleromati‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Klebsiell‌a ozaenae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiell‌a oxytoca","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aaa376f893fb48bda4b164de68e1cebd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis</strong></p><p>The above image and clinical scenario are suggestive of <strong>r</strong><strong>hinoscleroma</strong> and it is caused by <strong><em>Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>K. rhinoscleromatis is a gram-negative bacillus also known as <strong>Frisch bacillus. </strong>It is endemic in<strong> north India. </strong>Rhinoscleroma is characterized by <strong>chronic granulomatous hypertrophy</strong> of the nose. Biopsy of the lesion shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mikulicz cells-</strong> large foamy cells with a central nucleus and vacuolated, cytoplasm containing causative bacilli.</li>\n<li><strong>Russell bodies-</strong> eosinophilic inclusion bodies found in the plasma cells. These are also observed in multiple myeloma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:  </p>\n<p>Option A: <em>Rhinosporidium seeberi</em> causes <strong>rhinosporidiosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <em>K. ozaenae</em> causes <strong>atrophic rhinitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em>K. oxytoca</em> is associated with <strong>hemorrhagic colitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2216","text":"You notice a sputum sample‌ of a patient suspected o​f having ventilator-assoc​iated pneumonia turns pro‍gressively redder over ti​me. Microscopic examinati‍on of the sample reveals ​an absence of red blood c‍ells. Which of the follow‍ing organisms could have ‌caused this?","raw_text":"You notice a sputum sample of a patient suspected of having ventilator-associated pneumonia turns progressively redder over time. Microscopic examination of the sample reveals an absence of red blood cells. Which of the following organisms could have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proteus ‌mirabilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Klebsiella‌ pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serratia ‌marcescens","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serratia marcescens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serratia marcescens</strong></p><p>The given condition of <strong>sputum</strong> sample <strong>turning</strong> <strong>redder</strong> over time can be caused by<strong> <em>Serratia marcescens</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Serratia marcescens</em> are pleomorphic coccobacilli that can grow in sputum after collection and can <strong>mimic hemoptysis</strong> because they produce a <strong>red pigment</strong> called <strong>prodigiosin. </strong></p>\n<p>S. macescens mainly cause healthcare-associated infections such as ventilator-associated pneumonia, meningitis, endocarditis, septicemia and peritonitis. They can contaminate IV fluids, surgical instruments, and antiseptic solutions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6124","text":"A 19-year-old boy presente‌d with a single painless ​papule on his penis with ​no enlarged lymph nodes. ‍A smear was made from the​ lesion and stained with ‍Wright- Giemsa stain whic​h showed the finding as s‍hown in the following pic‍ture. Identify the organi‌sm.","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy presented with a single painless papule on his penis with no enlarged lymph nodes. A smear was made from the lesion and stained with Wright- Giemsa stain which showed the finding as shown in the following picture. Identify the organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes ‌simplex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella ‌granulomatis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Klebsiella granulomatis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/26109dded79149868db2b639da07010c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Klebsiella granulomatis</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the image showing <strong>D</strong><strong>onovan bodies</strong> in Wright/Giemsa-stained impression smears indicate a diagnosis of <strong>donovanosis</strong> caused by <strong><em>Klebsiella granulomatis.</em></strong> They appear as <strong>rounded</strong> <strong>coccobacilli</strong> within cystic spaces in large mononuclear cells (called <strong>Pund cells</strong>).</p>\n<p><em>Klebsiella granulomatis</em> shows bipolar condensation of chromatin giving a <strong>safety</strong> <strong>pin</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bipolar staining (safety pin appearance) is shown by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>H. ducreyi</em></li>\n<li><em>Vibrio parahaemolyticus</em></li>\n<li><em>Klebsiella granulomatis</em></li>\n<li><em>Yersinia pestis</em></li>\n<li><em>Burkholderia pseudomallei</em></li>\n<li><em>Burkholderia mallei</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GYAtJ0Q4","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"12_induction_and_augmentation_of_labour","title":"12.Induction And Augmentation Of Labour","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB5423","text":"What is the technique of s​timulating uterine contra‍ctions in a pregnant pati​ent prior to the onset of‌ spontaneous labour known​ as?","raw_text":"What is the technique of stimulating uterine contractions in a pregnant patient prior to the onset of spontaneous labour known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Augmen​tation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indu​ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimu​lation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Termi​nation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Induction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Induction</strong></p><p>The technique of <strong>stimulating</strong> uterine <strong>contractions</strong> <strong>prior</strong> to the onset of <strong>spontaneous labor</strong> is known as <strong>induction of labor</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be done irrespective of the rupture of membranes. </p>\n<p><strong>Augmentation</strong> is the <strong>enhancement</strong> of <strong>spontaneous contractions</strong> that are considered inadequate because of failed cervical dilation and fetal descent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5425","text":"Which of the following is ​not a contraindication fo‍r the induction of labor?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for the induction of labor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pelvic​ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes i​nfection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heart ​disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"History of lower transvers​e cesarean section in las‍t three pregnancies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heart disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heart disease</strong></p><p><strong>Heart disease </strong>is currently not considered a contraindication for the induction of labor. <strong>Vaginal delivery</strong> is <strong>preferred </strong>in such patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5429","text":"A 30-year-old G3P2 woman w​as admitted to the ward a‍t 40 weeks of gestation f​or induction of labor. Yo‌u were asked to calculate​ her Bishop's score. What​ should be the minimum sc‌ore before performing thi​s procedure?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old G3P2 woman was admitted to the ward at 40 weeks of gestation for induction of labor. You were asked to calculate her Bishop's score. What should be the minimum score before performing this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6</strong></p><p>The <strong>minimum</strong> <strong>Bishop</strong> <strong>Score</strong> required before proceeding with induction of labour is <strong>6</strong>.</p>\n<p>A Bishop score &gt;8 conveys a high likelihood of a successful induction. The latest guidelines state that a cut-off score of 6 can be used to choose between pharmacological and mechanical methods of induction.</p>\n<p>This score is calculated based on the:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>station of the fetal head</li>\n<li>dilation of cervix</li>\n<li>cervical length</li>\n<li>position of cervix</li>\n<li>consistency of the cervix.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 544\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 544\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>This score is an important <strong>determinant</strong> of a <strong>successful induction</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5435","text":"During rounds in the postn​atal ward, you notice a p‍atient's case sheet menti​oning she had uterine tac‌hysystole following labor​ induction. What is the c​riteria for this diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"During rounds in the postnatal ward, you notice a patient's case sheet mentioning she had uterine tachysystole following labor induction. What is the criteria for this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More than 5 contractions i​n a 5-min period","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More than 5 contractions i​n a 10-min period","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"More than 10 contractions ​in a 5-min period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More than 10 contractions ​in a 10-min period","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. More than 5 contractions in a 10-min period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. More than 5 contractions in a 10-min period</strong></p><p><strong>Uterine tachysystole</strong> is defined as <strong>&gt;5 contractions </strong>in a <strong>10 min </strong>period and may occur in spontaneous or stimulated labor. It should always be qualified by the presence or absence of fetal heart rate abnormalities. Tachysystole interrupts maternal blood flow to the placenta during contractions and may lead to a reduction in fetal oxygenation. It is also associated with<strong> placental abruption</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is caused by pharmacological agents used for cervical ripening and induction such as <strong>misoprostol </strong>(both oral and vaginal) and <strong>dinoprostone. </strong>Management includes <strong>stopping oxytocin</strong> infusion and <strong>terbutaline 0.25 mg SC</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5436","text":"A primigravida was admitte​d to the labor room at 41‍ weeks of gestation for t​he induction of labor. Wh‌at is the dose of misopro​stol used for this proced​ure?","raw_text":"A primigravida was admitted to the labor room at 41 weeks of gestation for the induction of labor. What is the dose of misoprostol used for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25​ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"25 ​mcg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50​ mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50 ​mcg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 25 mcg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 25 mcg</strong></p><p>Misoprostol (PGE1) is given as <strong>25mcg 3-6 hourly vaginally </strong>or 25mcg<strong> 2 hourly orally </strong>for the induction of labor.</p>\n<p>It is associated with the following<strong> side effects</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uterine tachysystole</li>\n<li>Meconium stained amniotic fluid</li>\n<li>Uterine rupture<strong><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5440","text":"Oxytocin is being given fo​r augmentation of labour ‍in a multigravida admitte​d to the labour room. Whi‌ch of the following is no​t a mechanism of action o​f this drug?","raw_text":"Oxytocin is being given for augmentation of labour in a multigravida admitted to the labour room. Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Receptor mediation through​ G-protein coupled recept‍ors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Activation of voltage medi​ated Ca channel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Production of prostaglandi​ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Competitive blocking of ac​etylcholine receptors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Competitive blocking of acetylcholine receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Competitive blocking of acetylcholine receptors</strong></p><p>Competitive blocking of acetylcholine is not a mechanism of action of oxytocin.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism of action</strong> of <strong>oxytocin</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Receptor mediation through G protein-coupled receptors</li>\n<li>Activation of voltage-gated Ca channel leading to calcium entry</li>\n<li>Prostaglandin production by a positive feedback mechanism. This induces production of more prostaglandins and stronger contractions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5442","text":"In which of the following ​patients would you perfor‍m a controlled artificial​ rupture of membranes?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you perform a controlled artificial rupture of membranes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G3P2 lady with absent feta​l cardiac activity at 34 ‍weeks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multigravida with HIV at 3​6 weeks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G2P1 lady with AFI of 28 c​m at 38 weeks of gestatio‍n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Term primigravida with red​, tender, vesicular lesio‍ns on her vulva","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. G2P1 lady with AFI of 28 cm at 38 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. G2P1 lady with AFI of 28 cm at 38 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p><strong>Controlled</strong> artificial rupture of membranes (<strong>ARM</strong>) is performed in a patient with amniotic fluid index (<strong>AFI</strong>)<strong> ≥ 25 cm</strong>. This points towards a diagnosis of <strong>polyhydramnios. </strong></p>\n<p>This is because if ARM is not done under controlled conditions in these patients, it can cause sudden decompression of the fetus. This can lead to abruption of placenta.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> <strong>to</strong> <strong>ARM</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrauterine fetal death</li>\n<li>Active genital herpes infection</li>\n<li>Maternal HIV-AIDS</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5443","text":"An obstetrician performs t​he following procedure fo‍r labor induction in a pa​tient. Which of the follo‌wing is false about the r​eflex elicited here?","raw_text":"An obstetrician performs the following procedure for labor induction in a patient. Which of the following is false about the reflex elicited here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs due to pressure at ​the external os of cervix‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with incr​eased level of PGF2α","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artificial rupture of memb​ranes initiates this refl‍ex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is an example of positi​ve feedback mechanism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Occurs due to pressure at the external os of cervix","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f5c0a0bc1a44dd2ba982e4af9ec07f4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Occurs due to pressure at the external os of cervix</strong></p><p><strong>Stripping of membranes</strong> is being depicted in the image, which elicits the <strong>Ferguson reflex</strong>. It is also known as the fetal ejection reflex and is seen due to the<strong> pressure at</strong> the <strong>internal os </strong>of the cervix.</p>\n<p>It is associated with an increase in blood levels of a <strong>prostaglandin F2α</strong> metabolite, also known as <strong>dinoprost.</strong> This is released<strong> </strong>due to mechanical stretching of the internal os. This enhances the uterine activity and causes release of more prostaglandins. It is an example of a positive feedback mechanism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5444","text":"All of the following are t​rue about laminaria tents‍ except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about laminaria tents except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hygroscopic osmotic cervic​al dilator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leads to the release of en​dogenous prostaglandins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ripening ​the cervix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No effect on Bishop's scor​e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No effect on Bishop's score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No effect on Bishop&#x27;s score</strong></p><p>The <strong>Bishop's score</strong> is rapidly<strong> improved</strong> by the use of laminaria tents.</p>\n<p>It is a mechanical cervical dilator and acts by <strong>hygroscopic osmotic</strong> action, leading to slow dilatation of the cervix. The gentle dilatation by laminaria tents induces an endogenous <strong>synthesis of prostaglandins</strong> causing a softening of the cervix. This is useful in <strong>cervical ripening</strong> before induction of labour.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5445","text":"A nursing assistant posted​ in the obstetric care un‍it was asked to plot a pa​rtograph to monitor the p‌rogress of labour. Where ​should she start marking ​the cervical dilatation?","raw_text":"A nursing assistant posted in the obstetric care unit was asked to plot a partograph to monitor the progress of labour. Where should she start marking the cervical dilatation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right of ​alert line","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left of a​lert line","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right of a​ction line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On the ac​tion line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left of alert line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/adb4bd5d16dd4a8ca60f076f5a2c664ax1280x767.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left of alert line</strong></p><p>In a partograph, the <strong>first marking</strong> of cervical dilatation should be made on the <strong>left </strong>of the<strong> alert line. </strong>In a normal partograph, the plotting remains on the<strong> </strong>left of the alert line.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5446","text":"You are monitoring the num​ber, duration, and intens‍ity of uterine contractio​ns in a 34-year-old G2P1 ‌woman with a term pregnan​cy. How long should they ​last for you to classify ‌them as moderate?","raw_text":"You are monitoring the number, duration, and intensity of uterine contractions in a 34-year-old G2P1 woman with a term pregnancy. How long should they last for you to classify them as moderate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 – 40​ seconds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 10​ seconds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-20 ​seconds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;40 ​seconds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 20 – 40 seconds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec9f383772bd404d84f7f3583c771374x1280x619.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 20 – 40 seconds</strong></p><p><strong>Moderate</strong> uterine contractions last for<strong> 20–40 s.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Weak</strong> contractions last for <strong>&lt;20 s</strong> and <strong>strong</strong> contractions are <strong>&gt;40 s.</strong></p>\n<p>During active labour, uterine contractions are 5 min apart for 1 hour, i.e., ≥ 12 contractions in 1 hour. This is accompanied by cervical dilatation of ≥3–5 cm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9638","text":"A G2P1 woman with polyhydr​amnios, presents to the c‍linic with respiratory di​stress at 38 weeks of ges‌tation. The obstetrician ​plans for induction of la​bor. What is a safe metho‌d of induction in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A G2P1 woman with polyhydramnios, presents to the clinic with respiratory distress at 38 weeks of gestation.  The obstetrician plans for induction of labor.  What is a safe method of induction in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High rupture​ of membranes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostag​landins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal amniocentesis fo​llowed by oxytocin drip","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Low rupture ​of membranes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abdominal amniocentesis followed by oxytocin drip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abdominal amniocentesis followed by oxytocin drip</strong></p><p><strong>Abdominal amniocentesis </strong>followed by <strong>oxytocin drip</strong> is a safe method for induction of labor in polyhydramnios.</p>\n<p>Abdominal amniocentesis drains a good amount of liquor (<strong>amnioreduction</strong>), after which the oxytocin infusion can be started. This will <strong>minimize</strong> the <strong>sudden decompression</strong> and reduce the risk of placental separation, cord prolapse and change in lie of the fetus.</p>\n<p>Although polyhydramnios does not constitute an absolute contraindication for the application of <strong>prostaglandins</strong>, these substances should be administered with caution. There is a marked increase in the incidence of <strong>postpartum hemorrhage</strong> and <strong>amniotic fluid embolism</strong> with their use. Hence, amnioreduction is a preferred mode of treatment in polyhydramnios.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9673","text":"Given below is the partogr​aph of a primigravida in ‍labor. What should be the​ next step in managing he‌r condition?","raw_text":"Given below is the partograph of a primigravida in labor. What should be the next step in managing her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wait an​d watch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Induction of labor by pros​taglandins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caesarea​n section","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Induction of labor by mech​anical methods","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caesarean section","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df0a75c7ea534aa9a650e1ee76cb160c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caesarean section</strong></p><p>As the plotting on the above partograph has <strong>crossed the action line</strong>, the next step of <strong>management</strong> is <strong>Caesarean section.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9677","text":"What is the half-life of o​xytocin?","raw_text":"What is the half-life of oxytocin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-3​ min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-5​ min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-2​ min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-8​ min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3-5 min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3-5 min</strong></p><p>The <strong>half-life</strong> of <strong>oxytocin</strong> is approximately <strong>3–5 min.</strong> Therefore, once oxytocin infusion is stopped, its concentration in plasma rapidly falls.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9678","text":"During your OBG rotations,​ one of the senior reside‍nts asks you to induce la​bor in a primigravida adm‌itted at 40 weeks of gest​ation. Identify the follo​wing device that you will‌ use for the same.","raw_text":"During your OBG rotations, one of the senior residents asks you to induce labor in a primigravida admitted at 40 weeks of gestation. Identify the following device that you will use for the same.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lippe​s loop","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervidil va​ginal insert","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Misoprostol vaginal insert​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mir​ena","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervidil vaginal insert","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d8a3f6d7379425db5ed2f6262fea793.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervidil vaginal insert</strong></p><p><span data-dobid=\"hdw\">This is an image of a<strong> cervidil vaginal insert. </strong>It is used for <strong>cervical ripening</strong> and improves the Bishop's score.</span></p>\n<p><span data-dobid=\"hdw\">It contains 10 mg of<strong> dinoprostone</strong> designed to release at a rate of approximately 0.3 mg/hr over 10-hours. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6203","text":"According to the WHO Labou​r Care Guidelines, docume‍ntation of labour progres​s begins at a cervical di‌latation of -","raw_text":"According to the WHO Labour Care Guidelines, documentation of labour progress begins at a cervical dilatation of -","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 ​cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5155e4953c234464b8afe4931f30ca47x724x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5 cm</strong></p><p>According to the WHO Labour Care Guidelines, documentation of labour progress begins at a cervical dilatation of <strong>5cm</strong>. </p>\n<h3>WHO Labour Care Guidelines:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Time axis</strong> - examination findings are plotted every one hour </li>\n<li><strong>Alert column-</strong> has values which if reached indicates the need for urgent medical intervention</li>\n<li><strong>Active first stage</strong> - findings are plotted every hour upto 12 hours. If the first stage continues, a new sheet is used.</li>\n<li><strong>Second stage</strong> - plotted every hour upto 3 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p>1) <strong>Section 1</strong>: <strong>Preliminary information</strong></p>\n<p>Name, parity, <strong>labour onset</strong>, active labour diagnosis (date), ruptured membranes (date&amp;time), risk factors</p>\n<p>2) <strong>Section 2: Supportive care</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Companionship</strong>  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pain relief</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Oral fluid</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Posture</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>3) <strong>Section 3: Baby</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Baseline FHR</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">FHR deceleration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Amniotic fluid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fetal position</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Caput</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Moulding</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4) Section 4: Vitals </strong></p>\n<p>Pulse, SBP, DBP, temperature, and urine for proteins are recorded every 4 hours</p>\n<p><strong>5) Section 5: Labour progress</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Count the number of uterine contractions over a 10-minute period. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Assess the duration of contractions</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cervical dilatation:</strong> starts at <strong>5 cm</strong> and is plotted as X; it is assessed every hour. For example - if the cervical dilatation remains at 5cm for ≥ 6hrs, it indicates the need for urgent intervention. Similarly, the cut-offs for - 6cm is ≥ 5hrs; 7cm is ≥ 3hrs; 8cm is ≥ 2.5hrs; 9cm is ≥ 2 hrs</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Descent - plotted as O</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>6) Section 6: Medication</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oxytocin </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Medicine </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV fluids</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>7) Section 7: Shared decision making</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Assessment</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Plan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*Fetal position, vitals of the mother and cervical dilatation can be assessed at 4 hourly if the labour is progressing adequately</p>\n<p>*If birth is not completed by 3 hours from the start of the active second stage in nulliparous and 2 hours in multiparous women, intervention is needed</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6205","text":"Which of the following mod​ifications have been made‍ in the WHO Labour care g​uidelines as compared to ‌the modified partograph M​onitoring of the second s​tage of labour Documentat‌ion of labour progression​ at regular intervals of ​1 hour Record of strength‍, duration, and frequency​ of uterine contractions ‍Record of labour companio​nship, posture, and oral ​fluid intake Initiation o‌f documentation of labour‍ progression at 5 cm cerv​ical dilatation","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following modifications have been made in the WHO Labour care guidelines as compared to the modified partograph</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Monitoring of the second stage of labour</li>\n<li>Documentation of labour progression at regular intervals of 1 hour</li>\n<li>Record of strength, duration, and frequency of uterine contractions</li>\n<li>Record of labour companionship, posture, and oral fluid intake</li>\n<li>Initiation of documentation of labour progression at 5 cm cervical dilatation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b97de08f96aa475e9bc63c39b6d505bcx1280x2046.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bed66248c3fc43d782052756d085c0ebx724x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4, 5</strong></p><table style=\"height: 260px;\" width=\"669\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 39px; text-align: center;\"><strong>WHO modified partograph</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 39px; text-align: center;\"><strong>WHO Labour Care Guide</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 86px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 86px;\">Documentation of labour progression begins at <strong>4 cm</strong> of cervical dilatation.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 86px;\">Documentation of labour progression begins at <strong>5 cm</strong> of cervical dilatation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 95px;\">Fixed <strong>1 cm/hour ‘alert’ and ‘action’</strong> lines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 95px;\"><strong>Time-based cut-offs</strong> for <strong>each centimeter</strong> of cervical dilatation to guide the obstetrician to take action. For example, at 5 cm of cervical dilatation, if there is no progress &gt;6 hours, action needs to be taken.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 87px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 87px;\">Monitoring of labour is restricted to the <strong>first stage</strong> only.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 87px;\">Monitoring of <strong>second stage of labour</strong> is included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">No section on supportive care interventions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">Addition of a section on <strong>supportive care</strong> of the mother- labour companionship, pain relief, oral fluid intake and posture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">Documentation of <strong>strength, duration, and frequency</strong> of uterine contractions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.5px; height: 78px;\">Documentation of <strong>only</strong> the <strong>duration and frequency of uterine contractions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8jJyDwJ-","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"12_lens___cataract_surgery__complications_and_iols","title":"12.Lens   Cataract Surgery, Complications And Iols","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC1274","text":"A child has congenital cat​aract involving the visua‌l axis, which was detecte‍d at birth. When should t‌he child be operated up o​n?","raw_text":"A child has congenital cataract involving the visual axis, which was detected at birth. When should the child be operated up on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At 4-6 we​eks of age","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"At 5 mont​hs of age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At 1 year of age, when the​ globe attains normal siz‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At 4 years, when ocular an​d orbital growth is compl‌ete","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. At 4-6 weeks of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. At 4-6 weeks of age</strong></p><p>The child should be operated at <strong>4-6 weeks </strong>of age.</p>\n<p>When a child has unilateral or bilateral<strong> cataract impairing vision</strong> (involving the visual axis), the cataract surgery must be performed before 2-4 months of age. This is because 2-4 months is the critical period for visual maturation and development of the fixation reflex in children. A<strong> unilateral cataract </strong>is more<strong> dangerous</strong> than a bilateral cataract because the risk of<strong> amblyopia </strong>is greater. <strong>Under-correction</strong> of intraocular lens (IOL) power is needed in children.</p>\n<p>In children <strong>&lt; 2 years </strong>of age:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In unilateral cases, primary implantation is indicated as soon as the patient is fit for anesthesia, ideally between<strong> 2</strong> and<strong> 3 months </strong>of age. The power is undercorrected by <strong>20%</strong>.</li>\n<li>The earlier the surgery is done, the better the chance that deep amblyopia can be overcome.</li>\n<li>After the cataract has been aspirated, an elective posterior capsulectomy–anterior vitrectomy is performed. The posterior chamber IOL is inserted through a scleral tunnel, which is prepared in advance. </li>\n<li><strong>Sulcus implantation</strong> is indicated in neonates and infants less than 1 year of age. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In children between<strong> 2 </strong>and <strong>8 years</strong> of age:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An IOL is implanted but power is undercorrected by 10%.</li>\n<li>An <strong>in-the-bag IOL</strong> is chosen for children above 1 year of age because they are less likely to need an IOL exchange.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Beyond 8 years</strong>, IOL power is the <strong>same </strong>as the<strong> calculated value.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1014","text":"In which of the following ​cataract surgeries is a s‌elf-sealing sclerocorneal‍ tunnel incision used?","raw_text":"In which of the following cataract surgeries is a self-sealing sclerocorneal tunnel incision used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intracapsular cataract ext​raction (ICCE)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conventional extracapsular​ cataract extraction (ECC‌E)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small incision cataract su​rgery (SICS)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior c​apsulotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small incision cataract surgery (SICS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small incision cataract surgery (SICS)</strong></p><p>The self-sealing sclerocorneal tunnel incision is made in small-incision cataract surgery (<strong>SICS</strong>)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>SICS is a type of <strong>extracapsular cataract extraction techniques </strong>(ECCE).<strong> </strong>In these techniques, the major portion of the anterior capsule with the epithelium, nucleus, and cortex are removed, leaving behind the <strong>intact posterior capsule.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Newer cataract surgeries include femtosecond laser-assisted cataract surgery (FLACS), micro-incision cataract surgery (MICS) and Phaconit. <strong>FLACS</strong> is a microincision cataract surgery or a micro-phacoemulsification where a femtosecond laser is used to make the corneal incisions, perform capsulorhexis and fragment the lens. <strong>MICS </strong>refers to the phacoemulsification techniques that can be performed through a microincision (&lt;2 mm). <strong>Phaconit</strong> is the term for the bimanual micro-phacoemulsification done with the needle incision technique through an incision of &lt;1mm.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1018","text":"In which of the following ​patients is an intracapsu‌lar cataract extraction i‍ndicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is an intracapsular cataract extraction indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-month-old with congenita​l cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"78-year-old with Morgagnia​n cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25-year-old with a subluxa​ted lens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"60-year-old with secondary​ cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 25-year-old with a subluxated lens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 25-year-old with a subluxated lens</strong></p><p>At present, the only indication<strong> </strong>of <strong>intracapsular cataract extraction</strong> (ICCE) is markedly <strong>subluxated </strong>and<strong> dislocated lens</strong>.</p>\n<p>In ICCE, the <strong>entire</strong> cataractous<strong> lens</strong> along with the <strong>intact capsule</strong> is removed by rupturing the zonules. Therefore, weak and degenerated zonules are a prerequisite for this method. Methods of lens delivery include cryoextraction (best method), forceps extraction (Arruga forceps), wire vectis method and Indian Smith method. ICCE is no longer used for cataract treatment due to the large incision required, inability to implant an IOL and a high rate of complications like vitreous loss, retinal detachment and cystoid macular edema. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1017","text":"What is the length of the ​incision used in phacoemu‌lsification?","raw_text":"What is the length of the incision used in phacoemulsification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 - ​12 mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 - 2​.5 mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 - ​14 mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 - ​6 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 - 2.5 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57092af7f07e4f49807e97d85b8fb535x558x1308.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 - 2.5 mm</strong></p><p>The length of the incision in phacoemulsification varies from <strong>1.5 </strong>to <strong>2.4 mm.</strong> The option closest to this answer is B, i.e., 2 to 2.5 mm.</p>\n<p>In <strong>phacoemulsification</strong>, the nucleus of the lens is emulsified and aspired. This is done by the phacoemulsifier. It acts through a <strong>hollow 1-mm titanium needle,</strong> which vibrates by at an ultrasonic speed of 40000 times a second. The steps of phacoemulsification are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Step 1 - Small clear corneal incision</li>\n<li>Step 2 - Continuous curvilinear capsulorhexis</li>\n<li>Step 3 - Hydro dissection</li>\n<li>Step 4 - Hydro delineation is done in some cases.</li>\n<li>Steps 5 and 6 - Emulsification and aspiration of the nucleus</li>\n<li>Step 7 - Removal of the cortical lens matter using an irrigation-aspiration technique</li>\n<li>Step 8 - Implantation of a foldable lens</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Length of the incision in various cataract surgeries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phacoemulsification- <strong>1.5–2.4 mm.</strong></li>\n<li>Micro-phacoemulsification- <strong>1–1.5 mm.</strong></li>\n<li>Intracapsular cataract extraction (ICCE)- <strong>12–14 mm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE)-  <strong>8–12 mm</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1282","text":"What is the most common la​te complication following‌ cataract surgery?","raw_text":"What is the most common late complication following cataract surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystoid ma​cular edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glau​coma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior capsule opacific​ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uve​itis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior capsule opacification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/063583538d3e44cabda2deb3044999b1x1279x1981.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior capsule opacification</strong></p><p>The most common late complication following cataract surgery is<strong> posterior capsule opacification.</strong></p>\n<p>Posterior capsule opacification, known as <strong>after </strong>or <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>cataract</strong>, is the most common late postoperative complication. It may be caused by residual opaque lens matter or proliferation of left out anterior epithelial cells. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1278","text":"In a patient undergoing ca​taract surgery, which of ‌the following is the most‍ important strategy in th‌e prevention of postopera​tive endophthalmitis?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing cataract surgery, which of the following is the most important strategy in the prevention of postoperative endophthalmitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preoperative preparation w​ith povidone iodine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"One week antibiotic therap​y prior to surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trimming o​f eyelashes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Use of intravitreal antibi​otics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Preoperative preparation with povidone iodine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Preoperative preparation with povidone iodine</strong></p><p>The most important factor in the prevention of postoperative endophthalmitis is <strong>preoperative preparation </strong>with<strong> povidone-iodine</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Postoperative endophthalmitis</strong> is<strong> </strong>most commonly caused by <em>Staphylococcus epidermidis. </em><strong>Flora </strong>of the <strong>eyelids</strong> and <strong>conjunctiva</strong> are the most frequent source. Other potential sources include contaminated solutions and instruments, environmental air, and the surgeon and other operating room personnel.</p>\n<p>Prophylaxis for postoperative endophthalmitis includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Instillation of<strong> 5% povidone-iodine</strong> into the conjunctival fornices and leaving this undisturbed for at least <strong>3 minutes prior </strong>to<strong> </strong>surgery. This is the most important factor. </li>\n<li>Scrupulous preparation of the surgical site</li>\n<li>Treatment of pre-existing infections such as blepharitis</li>\n<li>Antibiotic prophylaxis\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intracameral</strong> <strong>cefuroxime</strong> (1 mg in 0.1 mL) injected into the anterior chamber (AC) at the end of surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-operative</strong> <strong>subconjunctival</strong> <strong>injection</strong> can achieve bactericidal levels in the AC for at least 1–2 hours.</li>\n<li>Preoperative topical <strong>fluoroquinolone antibiotics</strong> are frequently given in regimens from 1 hour to 3 days before surgery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients present with ocular pain, diminished vision, circumciliary congestion and hypopyon. The differential diagnosis for postoperative endophthalmitis includes retained lens material in the AC, vitreous hemorrhage, postoperative uveitis and toxic reaction to the use of inappropriate or contaminated irrigating fluid or viscoelastic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4525","text":"A patient, 36 hours after ​cataract surgery, present‌s with complaints of pain‍ and visual disturbance. ‌His eye is as shown below​. What is the management ‍of this condition?","raw_text":"A patient, 36 hours after cataract surgery, presents with complaints of pain and visual disturbance. His eye is as shown below. What is the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenou​s steroids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous​ antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bandage and padding of the​ eye","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intravitreal​ antibiotics","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intravitreal antibiotics","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ecc24823a9164899ac9996a522e1b8f7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intravitreal antibiotics</strong></p><p>A <strong>post cataract surgery</strong> patient presenting within <strong>36 hours</strong> of surgery with inflammation, congestion and hypopyon as seen in the image, is suggestive of <strong>acute bacterial endophthalmitis</strong>. The <strong>treatment</strong> of acute bacterial endophthalmitis is <strong>intravitreal </strong><strong>antibiotics.</strong></p>\n<p>Endophthalmitis is an intraocular inflammation affecting the inner structures of the eyeball. Acute postoperative bacterial endophthalmitis occurs within 1-7 days after cataract surgery and the most important factor to prevent it is preoperative administration of 5% <strong>povidone iodine</strong>. The cardinal features of endophthalmitis are pain, swelling of the lid, and a decrease in vision. The patient classically shows inflammation of the eye, corneal edema and a hypopyon due to the pouring of exudates in the vitreous cavity, anterior chamber, and posterior chamber.</p>\n<p>Once the endophthalmitis sets in it is important to treat all the cases empirically with a combination of antibiotics and steroids from the beginning. The various drugs, routes and surgical management used to treat endophthalmitis are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The mainstay of treatment is <strong>intravitreal</strong> injections of <strong>antibiotics</strong>.\n<ul>\n<li>First choice: Vancomycin 1 mg in 0.1 mL plus ceftazidime 2.25mg in 0.1ml.</li>\n<li>Second choice:Vancomycin 1 mg in 0.1 mL plus amikacin 0.4mg in 0.1ml.</li>\n<li>If there is no improvement, a repeat intravitreal injection should be given after 48 hours</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Topical antibiotics </strong>are started immediately and used frequently.\n<ul>\n<li>Vancomycin (50mg/ml) or cefazolin (50mg/ml)</li>\n<li>Amikacin (20mg/ml) or tobramycin  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic antibiotics</strong> have a limited role in the treatment but are commonly used in practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid therapy</strong> includes:\n<ul>\n<li>Intravitreal injection of dexamethasone (0.4mg in 0.1ml) which is started along with antibiotics.</li>\n<li>Topical dexamethasone (0.1%)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Oral steroids</strong> such as prednisolone are started after 24 hours of intensive antibiotic therapy. </li>\n<li>Supportive therapy includes:\n<ul>\n<li>Cycloplegics such as atropine 1%</li>\n<li>Anti-glaucoma drugs such as oral acetazolamide or timolol in patients with raised intraocular pressure</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pars plana vitrectomy </strong>is done if the patient does not improve with the above therapy in 48-72 hours or   when the visual acuity is reduced to hand movements near the eye</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Steroids</strong> are given in endophthalmitis to reduce the inflammation of the tissues. The preferred route of administration is either by<strong> </strong>intravitreal injections, topical concentrations or oral route. <strong>Intravenous route</strong> (option A) of administration of steroids is <strong>not used</strong> in endophthalmitis.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Systemic</strong> administration (intravenous) of <strong>antibiotics</strong> have a limited role in the treatment but are commonly used in practice as an add on.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1186","text":"A 62-year-old presented wi​th diminished vision 1 ye‌ar after cataract surgery‍. The findings are shown ‌below. What is the most l​ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old presented with diminished vision 1 year after cataract surgery. The findings are shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After c​ataract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Irvine-Gas​s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"End opht​halmitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uveitis-glaucoma-hyphema s​yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. After cataract","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e2d775ca551408da705516b7c1cc1f4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9cb7b649766449cd8b0096a2fad9b252x1280x1981.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b79072075194629a592c03168996f5cx500x333.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. After cataract</strong></p><p>Diminished vision 1 year after cataract surgery, supported with the image of <strong>vacoulated clusters</strong> seen behind IOL is suggestive of <strong>Elschnig’s pearls.</strong> It is a type of <strong>after cataract</strong>.</p>\n<p>After cataract, or <strong>secondary cataract </strong>or posterior capsular opacification, is a <strong>late postoperative</strong> complication that follows <strong>extracapsular</strong> extraction of the lens. It is the most common late postoperative complication. It may be caused by residual opaque lens matter or proliferation of left out anterior epithelial cells. Different types of <strong>after cataract</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elschnig’s pearls</strong>: The subcapsular epithelial cells proliferate and develop into vacuolated soap bubble like clusters along the posterior capsule.</li>\n<li><strong>Soemmering’s ring</strong>: A peripheral dense ring is formed behind the iris and is enclosed between layers of the capsule.</li>\n<li><strong>Dense membranous</strong> after-cataract: It is formed in the pupillary area impairing the vision.</li>\n<li><strong>Thin membranous</strong> after-cataract: It is thickened posterior capsule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment of after cataract is by <strong>Nd-YAG laser</strong> <strong>capsulotomy </strong>of the posterior capsule, which works on the principle of photodisruption,<strong> </strong>or surgical posterior capsulotomy. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Irvine–Gass syndrome</strong> is <strong>cystoid macular edema </strong>(CME) that develops in <strong>post-cataract surgery</strong> patients. Though it is mostly clinically insignificant, some cases can present with blurring of vision,<strong> </strong>one to three months after surgery. In CME, on fundoscopy a <strong>honeycomb</strong> appearance and on fundus fluorescein angiography(FFA) <strong>flower petal pattern</strong> can be seen. The below image depicts the flower petal pattern seen on FFA.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Post operative endophthalmitis presents as <strong>ocular</strong> <strong>pain, diminished vision</strong> and <strong>hypopyon</strong> within months to years after the surgery. Fungal endophthalmitis can rarely occur with puff ball exudates in the vitreous cavity.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>UGH syndrome </strong>is where <strong>uveitis</strong>, <strong>glaucoma</strong> and <strong>hyphema</strong> occurs simultaneously. It is seen commonly with rigid anterior chamber intraocular lens (IOL) implantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1025","text":"What is the treatment of c​hoice for after cataract?‌","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for after cataract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra-capsular cataract ex​traction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravitre​al steroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior ​vitrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nd:YAG laser posterior cap​sulotomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nd:YAG laser posterior capsulotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nd:YAG laser posterior capsulotomy</strong></p><p><strong>Nd:YAG laser</strong> is the treatment of choice for <strong>after</strong> <strong>cataract. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Thin membranous</strong> after-cataract, thickened posterior capsule and <strong>Elschnig's pearls</strong> are treated with Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy. <strong>Dense membranous</strong> after-cataract requires capsulotomy using a vitreous cutter. <strong>Sommering's ring</strong>, if the central posterior capsule is clear, requires no treatment. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Intra-capsular cataract extraction</strong> (ICCE) is currently used for the treatment of markedly subluxated and dislocated lens. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Intravitreal steroids </strong>may be used for the treatment of acute endophthalmitis, an early postoperative complication of cataract surgery.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Anterior vitrectomy</strong> is performed for the treatment of cystoid macular edema with vitreous incarceration. This is a late post-cataract surgery complication.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1280","text":"What is the cause of late-​onset endophthalmitis aft‌er cataract surgery?","raw_text":"What is the cause of late-onset endophthalmitis after cataract surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseud​omonas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propionibac​terium acne","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Propionibacterium acne","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Propionibacterium acne</strong></p><p><strong>Late-onset endophthalmitis</strong> after cataract surgery is caused by<strong> </strong><em>Propionibacterium acnes</em>.</p>\n<p>Late or delayed-onset endophthalmitis following cataract surgery develops when an organism of <strong>low virulence</strong> such as <em>P. acnes</em>, becomes trapped within the capsular bag (<strong>saccular endophthalmitis</strong>). Organisms can become <strong>sequestered </strong>within<strong> macrophages</strong>, protected from eradication but with continued expression of bacterial antigens. Patients present with symptoms between<strong> </strong>4 weeks to years (mean 9 months) postoperatively<strong> </strong>following an uneventful cataract surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9499","text":"An elderly male residing i​n an old-age home is brou‌ght to the hospital with ‍senile cataracts in both ‌eyes. He says he had diff​iculty in seeing objects ‍for a few years, but neve‌r went to the clinic. He ​is posted for surgery in ​3 weeks. Which of the fol​lowing is a complication ​is he unlikely to develop​ in the 3 weeks until his​ surgery?","raw_text":"An elderly male residing in an old-age home is brought to the hospital with senile cataracts in both eyes. He says he had difficulty in seeing objects for a few years, but never went to the clinic. He is posted for surgery in 3 weeks. Which of the following is a complication is he unlikely to develop in the 3 weeks until his surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaphylact​ic uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Open angl​e glaucoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lens sub​luxation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypo​pyon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypopyon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypopyon</strong></p><p><strong>Hypopyon</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>complication</strong> of senile cataracts. </p>\n<p>Hypermature, or senile cataracts, can lead to the following complications.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phacoanaphylactic uveitis: </strong>The<strong> </strong>lens proteins may leak into the anterior chamber. They may act as an antigen and induce antigen–antibody reaction, leading to phacoanaphylactic uveitis.</li>\n<li>Lens-induced<strong> glaucoma:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phacomorphic </strong>glaucoma: Most common, seen in intumescent cataract. The lens swells and causes secondary angle closure glaucoma. </li>\n<li><strong>Phacolytic</strong> glaucoma: Seen in hypermature cataract. The leaked lens proteins are engulfed by macrophages, which clog the trabecular meshwork and cause secondary open-angle glaucoma. </li>\n<li><strong>Phacotopic</strong> glaucoma: Seen in hypermature cataract. The lens may subluxate or dislocate and block the angle of the anterior chamber. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Subluxation</strong> or<strong> dislocation</strong> of the lens: It may occur due to degeneration of zonules in the hypermature stage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1272","text":"What is the best irrigatin​g fluid for cataract surg‌ery?","raw_text":"What is the best irrigating fluid for cataract surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal​ saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ringer ​lactate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Balanced sa​lt solution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Balanced salt solution plu​s glutathione","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Balanced salt solution plus glutathione","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Balanced salt solution plus glutathione</strong></p><p>The<strong> best irrigating fluid</strong> for cataract surgery is <strong>balanced salt solution plus glutathione </strong>(BSS plus).</p>\n<p>In the <strong>early days</strong> of phacoemulsification, the only irrigating solutions available were normal saline, plasmalyte, and lactated Ringer’s solution. Their <strong>main adverse effects</strong> were <strong>endothelial cell toxicity</strong>. The <strong>buffer</strong> in BSS Plus is <strong>bicarbonate</strong>, which is an improvement over the sodium acetate and citrate buffers in BSS.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1283","text":"How many weeks following s​urgery are spectacles pre‌scribed?","raw_text":"How many weeks following surgery are spectacles prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6 weeks</strong></p><p>Spectacles are prescribed <strong>6 weeks</strong> after cataract surgery<strong> </strong>after the course of <strong>steroids</strong> is<strong> over. </strong>It is the time required for the incison to heal. Then visual acuity is checked and glasses are prescribed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6743","text":"A 72-year-old diabetic who​ underwent uncomplicated ‌cataract extraction with ‍PCIOL implant 15 days bac‌k presents with painless,​ decreased vision in the ‍operated eye. His visual ‌acuity is 6/12 and does n​ot improve with a pinhole​. On examination, the ant​erior chamber is deep, an​d PCIOL is well-centered ​in the capsular bag. Dila​ted fundus exam reveals c​lear media, and optic ner​ve with a cup-to-disc rat​io of 0.4, a few peripher​al microaneurysms, and a ‌dull foveal reflex. The r‌est of the fundus exam is‍ unremarkable. Which test​ would be of least benefi‌t?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old diabetic who underwent uncomplicated cataract extraction with PCIOL implant 15 days back presents with painless, decreased vision in the operated eye.  His visual acuity is 6/12 and does not improve with a pinhole. On examination, the anterior chamber is deep, and PCIOL is well-centered in the capsular bag. Dilated fundus exam reveals clear media, and optic nerve with a cup-to-disc ratio of 0.4, a few peripheral microaneurysms, and a dull foveal reflex. The rest of the fundus exam is unremarkable. Which test would be of least benefit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optical coherence tomograp​hy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluorescein​ angiography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonio​scopy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amsler​’s grid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gonioscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gonioscopy</strong></p><p>In the above case scenario, <strong>gonioscopy</strong> would be the least helpful.</p>\n<p>A painless decrease in vision after cataract surgery could be due to: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior capsular opacification (most commonly, after 1-3 months)</li>\n<li>Cystoid macular edema (early)</li>\n<li>Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment (delayed)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the given question, the media is clear (rules out posterior capsular opacification). Also, there are no symptoms nor signs of retinal detachment (RD), hence it is ruled out. Decreased vision not improving with a pinhole in the presence of clear media is attributable to macular pathology. Also, there is a<strong> dull foveal reflex</strong>. The diagnosis in this patient is <strong>cystoid macular edema </strong>(CME).</p>\n<p>The incidence of clinical CME is about 1% to 2% following uncomplicated cataract surgeries. The incidence of angiographic evidence of CME is 10 times greater at around 10% to 20%.</p>\n<p><strong>Optical coherence tomography</strong> (option A) is useful in identifying cystoid macular edema and evaluating the vitreomacular interface. It is also useful in the measurement of retinal thickening, thus allowing the assessment of the therapeutic response. On<strong> fluorescein angiography </strong>(option B), the classic <strong>petaloid leakage</strong> in the fovea is seen in the case of CME. <strong>Amsler’s grid</strong> (option D) is a macular function test.</p>\n<p>Hence, the least useful in this scenario is gonioscopy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9544","text":"Two months after undergoin​g cataract surgery, a 45-‌year-old man is found to ‍have after cataract. He i‌s planned for an Nd:YAG l​aser posterior capsulotom‍y. What is the principle ‌behind the use of this la​ser?","raw_text":"Two months after undergoing cataract surgery, a 45-year-old man is found to have after cataract. He is planned for an Nd:YAG laser posterior capsulotomy. What is the principle behind the use of this laser?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Photoa​blation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Photodi​sruption","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Photocoa​gulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Photoemul​sification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Photodisruption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Photodisruption</strong></p><p>The principle of the <strong>Nd:YAG laser</strong> (1064 nm) is <strong>photodisruption. </strong></p>\n<p>Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy is used for treatment of thin membranous after-cataract, posterior capsular opacification and Elschnig's pearls. The plasma produced at the laser target expands and disrupts the tissues, creating an opening. Double frequency Nd:YAG laser is used in posterior segment photocoagulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9507","text":"Which of the following com​plaints in a patient pres‌enting to your OPD would ‍prompt you to perform a F‌incham's test?","raw_text":"Which of the following complaints in a patient presenting to your OPD would prompt you to perform a Fincham's test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colore​d halos","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Poly​opia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sco​toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flashes ​of light","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colored halos","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colored halos</strong></p><p><strong>Fincham's test</strong> is used to distinguish whether the <strong>colored halos</strong> are due to <strong>cataract</strong> or <strong>glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Colored halos</strong> are rainbow-colored rings seen around lights at nighttime. It may be caused by cataracts, acute congestive glaucoma and mucopurulent conjunctivitis. These conditions lead to prismatic disruption of light.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1006","text":"As part of the pre-operati​ve evaluation of a patien‌t posted for cataract sur‍gery, you are asked to ca‌lculate the power of the ​IOL to be implanted. What‍ investigation do you per‌form?","raw_text":"As part of the pre-operative evaluation of a patient posted for cataract surgery, you are asked to calculate the power of the IOL to be implanted. What investigation do you perform?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biom​etry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pachy​metry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tonog​raphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tono​metry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df5ac40e247947348732aa2bdc40ebbex500x500.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biometry</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>power of the<strong> intraocular lens </strong>(IOL) to be implanted after cataract surgery<strong> </strong>is calculated by a <strong>biometer</strong> and the procedure is termed as <strong>biometry.</strong></p>\n<p>Biometry consists of keratometry and axial length measurement. The most common method of determining IOL power uses a regression formula called <strong>modified Sanders–Retzlaff– Kraff</strong> (SRK)<strong> </strong>formula, which is given below:</p>\n<p> E = A - 2.5L - 0.9K</p>\n<p>Where, </p>\n<ul>\n<li>E is the emmetropic power designated for the eye </li>\n<li>A is a predetermined <strong>constant,</strong> which is specific for each intraocular lens type</li>\n<li>L is the <strong>axial length</strong> of the eyeball in millimeter, determined by using the \"<strong>A</strong>\"<strong> scan ultrasonography</strong></li>\n<li>K is the <strong>average corneal curvature</strong>, which is determined by <strong>keratometry</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image given below shows a biometer. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1275","text":"In the SRK formula for int​raocular lens power calcu‌lation 'E=A-2.5L-0.9K', t‍he value of L is estimate‌d using__________","raw_text":"In the SRK formula for intraocular lens power calculation 'E=A-2.5L-0.9K', the value of L is estimated using__________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slit lamp b​iomicroscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Humphrey fi​eld analyser","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kerat​ometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-scan ultrasonographic bi​ometry","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A-scan ultrasonographic biometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7335797a841c4eb793a78a0099d0d212x720x1189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A-scan ultrasonographic biometry</strong></p><p>In the SRK formula, the value of L is calculated using the <strong>A-scan ultrasonographic biometry.</strong></p>\n<p>The most widely used formula for intraocular lens power calculation is the modified Sanders-Retzlaff-Kraff (SRK) formula, given by:</p>\n<p> E = A - 2.5L - 0.9K</p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>E: Emmetropic power designated for the eye </li>\n<li>A: Predetermine <strong>constant</strong> which is specific for each intraocular lens type.</li>\n<li>L: <strong>Axial length</strong> of<strong> eyeball</strong> in millimeters, determined using the <strong>A scan ultrasonography.</strong></li>\n<li>K: <strong>Average corneal curvature </strong>determined by <strong>keratometry</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is an image of A scan ultrasonography.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1008","text":"What is a rigid intraocula​r lens made of?","raw_text":"What is a rigid intraocular lens made of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polymethyl methacrylate (P​MMA)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sili​cone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydr​ogel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coll​amer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA)</strong></p><p><strong>Rigid IOLs</strong> are made of polymethyl methacrylate (<strong>PMMA</strong>).</p>\n<p>Depending on the type of <strong>material</strong> of manufacturing, 3 types of posterior chamber intraocular lens (PCIOLs) are available:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rigid IOLs</strong> are made entirely from polymethyl methacrylate (<strong>PMMA</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Foldable IOLs </strong>are made of silicone, hydrogel, acrylic, and Collamer. These are implanted through a small incision. </li>\n<li><strong>Rollable IOLs </strong>are ultra-thin IOLs made of acyrlic or hydrogel. These are implanted through a 1-mm incision after micro-incision cataract surgery technique.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1005","text":"A patient, on follow-up af​ter cataract surgery, has‌ the lens shown below. Wh‍at is the type of lens im‌planted in the patient?","raw_text":"A patient, on follow-up after cataract surgery, has the lens shown below. What is the type of lens implanted in the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kelman multiflex anterior ​chamber IOL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Modified C loop posterior ​chamber IOL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iris cl​aw lens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Four-loop posterior chambe​r IOL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iris claw lens","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/79d9dd7f17914ae883cc1daacdf0edfd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a29eca92832457e8a0624e2dd687666x1280x2276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iris claw lens</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>Singh </strong>and <strong>Worst’s iris claw lens</strong>.</p>\n<p>This lens is an iris-supported lens, which is fixed with the help of <strong>sutures, loops</strong> or<strong> claws</strong>. They are associated with a high incidence of postoperative complications and are thus only used if posterior chamber IOLs cannot be implanted. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Kelman multiflex </strong>type of anterior chamber intraocular lens (ACIOL) is the most commonly used ACIOL. Anterior chamber IOLs lie entirely in front of the iris and are supported in the angle of the anterior chamber. These are associated with a higher incidence of <strong>bullous keratopathy</strong>. Thus, they are currently used only for correction of aphakia in cases where posterior chamber IOLs cannot be used (eg: rupture of posterior capsule). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9511","text":"An 80-year-old man is post​ed for cataract surgery. ‌The given lens is chosen ‍to be used for implantati‌on. Identify the lens.","raw_text":"An 80-year-old man is posted for cataract surgery. The given lens is chosen to be used for implantation. Identify the lens.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kelman multiflex anterior ​chamber IOL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Modified C loop posterior ​chamber IOL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Singh and Worst's iris cla​w lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Four-loop posterior chambe​r IOL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Modified C loop posterior chamber IOL","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/125525281c624a83934afdb14e5459f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ac61dcc172742c0b7ff1ad53db0474ax1280x2276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Modified C loop posterior chamber IOL</strong></p><p>The above image depicts a <strong>modified C-loop type</strong> of posterior chamber intraocular lens (PCIOL).</p>\n<p>A <strong>PCIOL </strong>rests entirely <strong>behind </strong>the <strong>iris</strong>. They may be supported and fixed by capsular bag fixation of PCIOL (most ideal method), ciliary sulcus fixation or scleral fixation. </p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>PCIOL consists of a central part overlying the optic axis is called the <strong>optic </strong>and the peripheral arms used for placement and stabilization called the <strong>haptics</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6745","text":"Identify the intraocular l​ens shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the intraocular lens shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kelman’​s AC-IOL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffrac​tive IOL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accommod​ating IOL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tori​c IOL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Toric IOL","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/010c905f2a7945969a9c9d487a14b5f0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Toric IOL</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>toric IOL</strong> used to <strong>correct pre-existing astigmatism</strong>. The dotted markings on the optic of the IOL indicate the axis of IOL, and it is aligned to coincide with the <strong>steep axis</strong> of the patient’s eye.</p>\n<p>For every, one degree of misalignment, about 3 percent of the lens cylinder power is lost.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6UGCfDoT","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"12_injuries_of_spine","title":"12.Injuries Of Spine","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB2104","text":"A patient was brought to t‌he emergency department w‍ith an injury to the neck‍ following a road traffic‌ accident. On examination‌, she was found to have d​ecreased sensations over ‌the back of the scalp. Wh​ich nerve root has likely​ been injured in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the emergency department with an injury to the neck following a road traffic accident. On examination, she was found to have decreased sensations over the back of the scalp. Which nerve root has likely been injured in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74f137e036b244c4afa67eb7e275d59ax1280x1535.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C2</strong></p><p>The nerve root likely injured in a patient having <strong>decreased sensation</strong> over the <strong>back of</strong> the <strong>scalp</strong> is <strong>C2</strong>.</p>\n<p>Area of <strong>loss of sensation</strong> following nerve root injury varies depending upon <strong>level of nerve root</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C2</strong> injury leads to decreased sensation over the <strong>back of</strong> the <strong>scalp</strong></li>\n<li><strong>C3</strong> injury leads to decreased sensation over the <strong>anterior aspect</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong></li>\n<li><strong>C4</strong> injury leads to decreased sensation over the <strong>lateral aspect</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong> and inferiorly over clavicles down to the rib space</li>\n<li><strong>C5</strong> injury leads to decreased sensation over the <strong>deltoid</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2105","text":"A patient with a spinal in‌jury presents with decrea‍sed sensation over the ra‍dial aspect of the forear‌m, thumb, and index finge‌r. There is weakness of w​rist extension on examina‌tion. Which nerve root ha​s been damaged here?","raw_text":"A patient with a spinal injury presents with decreased sensation over the radial aspect of the forearm, thumb, and index finger. There is weakness of wrist extension on examination. Which nerve root has been damaged here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C‌7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C6</strong></p><p>The<strong> C6 </strong>nerve root has been <strong>damaged</strong> in this case of <strong>loss of sensation</strong> over the <strong>radial aspect</strong> of <strong>forearm</strong> and <strong>weakened wrist extension</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2102","text":"A patient has decreased se‌nsation over the medial p‍art of the forearm around‍ the elbow. Which nerve r‌oot is involved here?","raw_text":"A patient has decreased sensation over the medial part of the forearm around the elbow. Which nerve root is involved here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6868b10efcd14e9f80963f2d80f87ba5x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T1</strong></p><p>Decreased sensation over the <strong>medial aspect</strong> of the forearm around the <strong>elbow</strong> is seen due to injury to<strong> T1 nerve root.</strong></p>\n<p>T1 is the nerve root for the <strong>sensory supply</strong> of the <strong>medial aspect </strong>of the<strong> forearm</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2101","text":"Which of these muscles can‌ be tested to evaluate C5‍ root motor function in a‍ patient with spinal inju‌ry?","raw_text":"Which of these muscles can be tested to evaluate C5 root motor function in a patient with spinal injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trap‌ezius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bic‌eps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor carpi radialis lo‌ngus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tri‌ceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Biceps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Biceps</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The<strong> biceps</strong> muscle can be used to test the <strong>C5</strong> root motor function. It causes <strong>elbow flexion</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In spine injuries, a full neurological examination is performed in all cases. Tests performed for nerve root <strong>motor function</strong> are summarized in the table below. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 704px; height: 753px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p><strong>Spinal Segment</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 50px;\">\n<p><strong>Muscle Supplied</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 50px;\">\n<p><strong>Action to Test</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>C5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Biceps, Brachialis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Elbow flexion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 88px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 88px;\">\n<p>C6</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 88px;\">\n<p>Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 88px;\">\n<p>Wrist extension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>C7</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 50px;\">Triceps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Elbow extension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 88px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 88px;\">\n<p>C8</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 88px;\">\n<p>Flexor digitorum profundus to the middle finger</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 88px;\">Flex middle distal phalanx</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 69px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 69px;\">\n<p>T1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 69px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Abductor digiti minimi</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 69px;\">Abduct little finger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>L2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Iliopsoas</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 50px;\">Flex hip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>L3</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Quadriceps</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 50px;\">Straighten knee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>L4</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>Tibialis anterior</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 50px;\">Dorsiflex foot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 69px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 69px;\">\n<p>L5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 69px;\">\n<p>Extensor hallucis longus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 69px;\">Dorsiflex big toe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61.4297px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 61.4297px;\">\n<p>S1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 349.387px; height: 61.4297px;\">\n<p>Gastrocnemius, Soleus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.613px; height: 61.4297px;\">Plantarflex foot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2119","text":"Which of the following ref‌lexes is commonly tested ‍to detect spinal shock in‍ a patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following reflexes is commonly tested to detect spinal shock in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pupillar‌y reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gag r‌eflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdomina‌l reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bulbocaver‌nous reflex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bulbocavernous reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8abd7fa4d9254c6889a833ba5a7ce042x1280x1912.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bulbocavernous reflex</strong></p><p>The <strong>bulbocavernous reflex</strong> is commonly tested to detect spinal shock. </p>\n<p><strong>Spinal shock</strong> is the <strong>physiological dysfunction</strong> that follows structural injury to the spine. It manifests as <strong>flaccidity</strong>, <strong>loss of sensation</strong>, and <strong>absence of reflexes</strong> below the level of the injury. </p>\n<p>The <strong>primitive reflexes</strong> are absent in spinal shock. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bulbocavernosus reflex</strong> (root value S2- S4): Tested by squeezing the penis in males or applying pressure on the clitoris in females. The normal response is the contraction of the anal sphincter. If the patient has in situ Foley's catheter, it can be gently pulled to test anal contraction in both genders. </li>\n<li><strong>Anal wink reflex</strong> (root value S2- S4): Tested by stroking the skin around the anus. The normal response is the contraction of the anal sphincter.   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Spinal shock usually lasts less than 48 hours and the reflexes return to normal. <strong>Resolution</strong> of <strong>spinal shock</strong> is indicated by the <strong>return</strong> of the <strong>bulbocavernosus</strong> and the <strong>anal wink reflex</strong>.</p>\n<p>The path of the bulbocavernosus reflex is depicted below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2170","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in a patient wit‍h cauda equina syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in a patient with cauda equina syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absent bulbocavernosus ref‌lex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asymmetric saddle-shaped a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flaccid paralysis below th‌e knee","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exaggerate‌d knee jerk","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Exaggerated knee jerk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Exaggerated knee jerk</strong></p><p>In <strong>cauda equina </strong>syndrome, the<strong> knee jerk</strong> is <strong>lost</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> for cauda equina syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumours of the spine</li>\n<li>Pott's disease</li>\n<li>Protrusion of disc</li>\n<li>Fracture-dislocation of the thoracolumbar spine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Initially, there is <strong>saddle-shaped hyperesthesia </strong>and<strong> later anesthesia</strong> involving buttocks, anus, and perineum. Along with this, there is <strong>flaccid paralysis below</strong> the <strong>knee</strong> and ankle reflex is lost. <strong>Bulbocavernosus reflex</strong> is also <strong>absent</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2161","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In automatic bladder, the ‌level of injury is above ‍S2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In automatic bladder, refl‌ex center takes control","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In autonomous bladder, res‌idual urine is minimal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"In autonomous bladder, the‌ injury is LMN type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. In autonomous bladder, residual urine is minimal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. In autonomous bladder, residual urine is minimal</strong></p><p>The quantity of <strong>residual urine</strong> is <strong>large,</strong> not minimal, in <strong>autonomous</strong> <strong>bladder</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Differences</strong> between an <strong>automatic bladder and autonomous bladder</strong> are highlighted in the table. </p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width: 427px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p><strong>Automatic bladder</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p><strong>Autonomous bladder</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Level of injury above S2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p>Injury below/at S2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>UMN type lesion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p>LMN type lesion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Controlled by reflex center of bladder</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p>Controlled by intrinsic plexus of bladder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Emptying is involuntary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p>Emptying is voluntary</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Residual urine volume is minimal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<p>Residual urine volume is large (200-300 cc)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2097","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding whiplas‍h injury is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding whiplash injury is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a ligamentous injury‌ of the cervical spine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neck stiffness is a presen‌ting symptom","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-ray shows vertebral frac‌ture in most cases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is hyperextension fo‌llowed by flexion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. X-ray shows vertebral fracture in most cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. X-ray shows vertebral fracture in most cases</strong></p><p>Cervical vertebral <strong>fracture</strong> is not seen in most cases with <strong>whiplash injury</strong>. It is seen in<strong> severe cases only</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Whiplash is a form of cervical <strong>acceleration-deceleration injury</strong>. There is hyperextension of the spine followed by flexion during the recoil.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The main pathological features seen in these cases include tear (<strong>strain</strong>) of the<strong> anterior longitudinal ligament</strong> and the <strong>capsular fibres </strong>of the<strong> facet joints </strong>in the spine. In some cases, the intervertebral discs may be damaged.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2173","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about SCIWORA is ‍false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about SCIWORA is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a condition prevalen‌t in the geriatric popula‍tion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"There is a temporary occlu‌sion of vertebral arterie‍s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"These lesions are mainly f‌ound in cervical spine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MRI is the gold standard i‌nvestigation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a condition prevalent in the geriatric population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a condition prevalent in the geriatric population</strong></p><p>SCIWORA is <strong>Spinal Cord Injury Without Radiographic Abnormality.</strong> It is very common in <strong>children.</strong></p>\n<p>Such injuries are most often seen in the <strong>cervical spine</strong>. Specific biomechanics of the vertebral column in children allows the musculoskeletal system to<strong> move beyond</strong> the normal <strong>physiological range of motion</strong> without the risk of fracture. However as the spinal cord is not as flexible, neurological deficits may be seen.</p>\n<p>Investigations available earlier like X-rays could not detect such lesions. However, new imaging modalities like <strong>MRI</strong> have <strong>increased</strong> the rate of <strong>detection</strong> of such lesions and is considered <strong>gold standard</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2130","text":"Identify the radiographic ‌view shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the radiographic view shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Open mo‌uth view","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Swimmer‌’s view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Water’‌s view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schulle‌r's view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Open mouth view","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7408d11364404cc0932ccead1fcf61df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Open mouth view</strong></p><p>The given radiograph is that of <strong>open mouth view </strong>or<strong> odontoid view</strong>.</p>\n<p>The open-mouth view is used to diagnose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jefferson fracture</strong> - Burst fracture of C1 vertebra</li>\n<li><strong>Odontoid</strong> <strong>fractures </strong>- Fracture of odontoid process of C2 vertebra</li>\n<li><strong>Basilar invagination </strong>- Upward migration of superior part of the odontoid process</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2131","text":"An open mouth X-ray was pe‌rformed in a patient susp‍ected to have a Jefferson‍'s fracture. The transver‌se ligament is likely to ‌be ruptured if the overha​ng of C1 over C2 is more ‌than:","raw_text":"An open mouth X-ray was performed in a patient suspected to have a Jefferson's fracture. The transverse ligament is likely to be ruptured if the overhang of C1 over C2 is more than:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"13.‌1 mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3.4‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11.‌1 mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6.9‌ mm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6.9 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3338a598fc4649d9867cdf58c975afd2x1200x1871.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab64f90d4fb6473aa933ac3127bb02dcx1280x2120.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6.9 mm</strong></p><p>In Jefferson's fracture, the <strong>transverse ligament</strong> is likely to be <strong>ruptured</strong> if the combined <strong>overhang of C1</strong> lateral processes <strong>over C2</strong> is <strong>more than 6.9 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Jefferson's fracture</strong> is a <strong>burst fracture</strong> of the ring of the atlas (<strong>C1</strong>). It occurs due to axial forces and can be stable or unstable as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Jefferson's fracture is identified in an <strong>open-mouth X-ray</strong> view (odontoid view). The displacement of the lateral process of C1 over C2 is checked in this X-ray to evaluate if the transverse ligament is torn. </p>\n<p>If the <strong>total displacement </strong>(sum of displacement on both sides) of<strong> </strong>C1 lateral masses relative to C2 is <strong>more than 6.9 mm</strong>, the <strong>transverse ligament </strong>is likely to be<strong> damaged</strong>. This is known as the<strong> rule of Spence </strong>which is used to determine if the fracture is stable or unstable. If the transverse ligament is torn, it is classified as an<strong> unstable fracture.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below depicts the use of the rule of Spence in an odontoid view X-ray.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Some experts suggest that this measurement has to be<strong> 8.1 mm</strong> to account for magnification on plain radiographs. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2133","text":"An unconscious road traffi‌c accident victim was bro‍ught to the emergency dep‍artment. After resuscitat‌ion and stabilization, a ‌CT scan of the cervical s​pine was performed which ‌is given below. What is t​he preferred management i​n this case?","raw_text":"An unconscious road traffic accident victim was brought to the emergency department. After resuscitation and stabilization,  a CT scan of the cervical spine was performed which is given below. What is the preferred management in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atlantoax‌ial fusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rigid cerv‌ical collar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior de‌compression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vertebr‌oplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rigid cervical collar","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3bd89d614a8742768cf904676172370d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67bde2162b2c4b5c96c926789f151ec8x454x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1caceb7d00b0413292466d6d3650b6f1x1280x1673.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rigid cervical collar</strong></p><p>The image reveals a <strong>stable burst fracture</strong> (Jefferson fracture) of the ring of the atlas (<strong>C1</strong> vertebra). This condition is preferably managed by the application of a <strong>rigid cervical collar</strong> until the fracture heals.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>treatment approach </strong>for Jefferson's fracture is as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Stable burst fractures</strong> are treated using a rigid <strong>cervical collar </strong>for <strong>8 to 12 weeks</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Unstable fractures</strong> (with injured transverse ligament assessed by the rule of Spence) may be reduced by <strong>halo traction</strong> and managed in a <strong>halo vest for 12 weeks</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">If there is a <strong>residual C1-C2 instability</strong> despite the above treatment, then surgical <strong>posterior atlantoaxial fusion </strong>(C1-C2 fusion) is performed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows a rigid cervical collar.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows a halo vest.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2137","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about Hangman's f‍racture is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about Hangman's fracture is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is fracture of pars int‌erarticularis of atlas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Most fractures are minimal‌ly displaced","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most fractures can be mana‌ged conservatively","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurological injury is les‌s common","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is fracture of pars interarticularis of atlas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73ce5ced12824a479ed39d57dbeacf95x1200x1555.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e092f26db8f14fdea62592b3f289e724x1280x894.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is fracture of pars interarticularis of atlas</strong></p><p><strong>Hangman's fracture </strong>is a fracture of <strong>pars interarticularis of axis</strong> (C2 vertebra).</p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>traumatic C2 spondylolisthesis.</strong> In most cases, there is <strong>no neurological deficit</strong> as pars fractures expand the spinal canal. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In the below X-ray of the cervical spine, a Hangman's fracture can be noted.  It can be observed that C2 (red outline) has moved forward with respect to C3 (blue outline).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>Levine and Edwards classification</strong> is used commonly for these fractures. </p>\n<p>Most of these fractures can be <strong>managed conservatively</strong>. The treatment approach is as below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimally displaced fractures: Cervical collar.</li>\n<li>Highly displaced and angulated fractures: Traction followed by Halo vest</li>\n<li>Fractures with dislocation of the C2–C3 facet joint and neurological injuries: Open reduction and stabilization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0938","text":"Which is the most dangerou‌s type of odontoid fractu‍re?","raw_text":"Which is the most dangerous type of odontoid fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/accbb1e6ef98480e85639a48da754a75x1280x2238.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>most common</strong> and most <strong>dangerous</strong> type of <strong>odontoid fracture</strong> is <strong>Type II</strong>. These fractures are <strong>unstable</strong> and prone to <strong>non-union</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Odontoid fractures are classified by <strong>Anderson and D’Alonzo classification</strong> into 3 types given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 803px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 38px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 435px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 310px;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 38px;\">I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 435px;\">Avulsion fracture of the tip of the odontoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 310px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Rigid collar</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 38px;\">II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 435px;\">Fracture at the junction of the odontoid process and the body of the axis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 310px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Young patient and stable fracture: traction followed by a collar or halo vest</li>\n<li>Unstable fractures: anterior odontoid screw, or C1–C2 fusion</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 38px;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 435px;\">Fracture through the body of the axis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 310px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Undisplaced fractures: halo vest (8-12 wks)</li>\n<li>Displaced fractures: halo traction followed by halo vest (8-12 weeks)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2122","text":"A patient in the ER was fo‌und to have subaxial cerv‍ical spine fractures. Whi‍ch of the following state‌ments about the managemen‌t of his condition is fal​se?","raw_text":"A patient in the ER was found to have subaxial cervical spine fractures. Which of the following statements about the management of his condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical collar is preferr‌ed for single-column inju‍ries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Management is based on And‌erson and D’Alonzo classi‍fication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical collar can be use‌d in some two-column inju‍ries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Operative treatment is pre‌ferred for three-column i‍njuries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Management is based on Anderson and D’Alonzo classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Management is based on Anderson and D’Alonzo classification</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Anderson and D’Alonzo classification</strong> is used to determine the management of <strong>C2 odontoid</strong> process <strong>fracture</strong>, and not in subaxial cervical spine fractures. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">For <strong>subaxial (C3-C7)</strong> cervical injuries, the classification systems used include <strong>Ferguson and Allen classification</strong> and the <strong>subaxial cervical spine injury classification (SLIC)</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Management </strong>of subaxial (C3-C7) injuries:<strong> </strong>Individual injuries are evaluated based on the <strong>three-column biomechanical model </strong>and treatment is planned accordingly.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Single-column </strong>injuries without neurologic deficit are managed with <strong>immobilization </strong>using a <strong>cervical collar</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Two-column</strong> injuries are considered <strong>unstable</strong> and tend to be <strong>treated operatively</strong>, but <strong>some</strong> of these injuries can be treated with <strong>immobilization </strong>using a<strong> cervical collar</strong>. When neurologic deficits are present, operative treatment is recommended. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Three-column </strong>injuries are considered unstable even when there is no neurologic deficit and usually require<strong> operative stabilization</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2081","text":"According to the Denis cla‌ssification, which of the‍ following is not include‍d in the middle column of‌ the spine?","raw_text":"According to the Denis classification, which of the following is not included in the middle column of the spine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior part of the vert‌ebral body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior part of the vert‌ebral disc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior part of the pedi‌cles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior longitudinal lig‌ament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior part of the pedicles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd745a5d1bc144ef8af6615d06df7f92x1280x1799.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior part of the pedicles</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>Denis three-column theory</strong>, the posterior part of the <strong>pedicles</strong> is included in the <strong>posterior column</strong> of the spine, not in the middle column.</p>\n<p>The <strong>3 columns </strong>of the<strong> spine</strong> according to Denis classification are depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The Denis classification is <strong>used to</strong> describe spinal injuries and <strong>classify</strong> them as <strong>stable or unstable</strong>. As per this system, any injury <strong>involving 2 columns</strong> or more is classified as <strong>unstable</strong>. Unstable injuries require <strong>surgical fixation.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The current<strong> AO/ASIF classification</strong> that replaced Denis classification reverts back to the previously prevalent <strong>two-column theory. </strong>It divides the spine as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Anterior column</strong>: vertebral body and disc</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Posterior column</strong>: pedicles, laminae, facets and posterior ligamentous complex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0728","text":"Which of the following is ‌known as clay-shoveler’s ‍fracture?","raw_text":"Which of the following is known as clay-shoveler’s fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertical compression injur‌y of cervical spine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C-spine fracture with C6-C‌7 disc disruption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Avulsion fracture of C7 sp‌inous process","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Horizontal fracture in the‌ cervical spine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Avulsion fracture of C7 spinous process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2cbdd6dfa46d4602b7868a06a8381901x720x840.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Avulsion fracture of C7 spinous process</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clay-shoveler’s fracture</strong> refers to <strong>avulsion fracture</strong> of the <strong>C7 spinous process</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It occurs due to the <strong>powerful contraction </strong>of the<strong> back muscles</strong> inserted into the spinous process. The injury is painful but harmless. <strong>No treatment</strong> is usually <strong>required</strong>. As soon as the pain subsides, neck exercises are encouraged.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The X-ray below shows a clay-shoveler's fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2100","text":"A young man who got injure‌d in the neck while divin‍g is brought to the emerg‍ency. Which of the follow‌ing classifications syste‌ms is unlikely to be used​ to classify his injury?","raw_text":"A young man who got injured in the neck while diving is brought to the emergency. Which of the following classifications systems is unlikely to be used to classify his injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AO-Magerl c‌lassiﬁcation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Allen and Ferguson classif‌ication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anderson and D’Alonzo clas‌sification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levine and Edwards classif‌ication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AO-Magerl classiﬁcation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AO-Magerl classiﬁcation</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient has suffered a cervical spinal injury. The <strong>AO-Magerl classification </strong>is used for the classification of <strong>thoracolumbar spinal injuries</strong>.</p>\n<p>In AO-Magerl classification, thoracolumbar injuries are classified into <strong>3 types</strong> based on the <strong>primary force</strong> that <strong>caused injury</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type A (compression force)</li>\n<li>Type B (distraction force)</li>\n<li>Type C (torsional/rotational force)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The various <strong>classification systems</strong> used to classify <strong>cervical spinal injuries</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Anderson and D'Alonzo</strong> classification is used for <strong>odontoid fracture </strong>in the C2 vertebra.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Levine and Edwards </strong>classification is used for <strong>Hangman's fracture</strong> (traumatic spondylolisthesis of C2 over C3). </li>\n<li>The <strong>Allen and Ferguson</strong> classification is used for <strong>subaxial cervical spine </strong>(C3-C7) injuries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2138","text":"A patient presents to you ‌with severe pain in his l‍ower back after jumping f‍rom a height to the groun‌d level. His X-ray is giv‌en below. What is your di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with severe pain in his lower back after jumping from a height to the ground level. His X-ray is given below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stable bur‌st fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unstable bu‌rst fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compressio‌n fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chance ‌fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Compression fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/52ca0d1f607b40f4bbff65ea95c1e306.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d917b48c4af94cf6a06d073085ed4917x1280x1685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Compression fracture</strong></p><p>The given patient has a<strong> compression fracture</strong> of the <strong>L4</strong> vertebra. It can be diagnosed radiographically by the <strong>wedge-shape</strong> and <strong>loss of anterior height</strong> compared to the posterior height of the affected vertebra. </p>\n<p>Compression fractures occur due to <strong>axial loading</strong> of the vertebra. They result from <strong>severe spinal flexion</strong>. They can also occur with minimal trauma in <strong>osteoporotic</strong> individuals or as <strong>pathological fractures</strong> in other conditions.</p>\n<p>They are characterized by loss of vertebral height anteriorly, with no loss of posterior vertebral height and no posterior ligamentous or bony injury. In most cases, there is <strong>no spinal cord damage</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2148","text":"A 30-year-old male patient‌ accidentally fell from a‍ ladder and sustained inj‍uries to his back. His sp‌ine X-ray reveals a stabl‌e anterior wedge compress​ion fracture of T10 verte‌bra. He has no neurologic​al deficit. What would be​ the preferred management​ approach in this patient​?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male patient accidentally fell from a ladder and sustained injuries to his back. His spine X-ray reveals a stable anterior wedge compression fracture of T10 vertebra. He has no neurological deficit. What would be the preferred management approach in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early ORIF and bone fusion‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior de‌compression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External sp‌ine support","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IV Dexam‌ethasone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. External spine support","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. External spine support</strong></p><p>A<strong> stable thoracolumbar</strong> anterior wedge <strong>compression fracture</strong> without any neurological deficit is preferably treated with <strong>external spine support</strong>, bed rest, and pain killers.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Management of stable vertebral compression fracture is done by application of <strong>thoracic lumbar sacral orthosis (TLSO) for 12 weeks</strong> with <strong>pain management</strong>. This is followed by a gradual return to activity.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Some injuries that are predisposed to further <strong>collapse</strong> and <strong>progressive deformity</strong> despite the TLSO includes: </p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Injuries that have &gt; 40-50% anterior height loss</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Injuries with &gt; 25 degrees of focal kyphosis</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Disruption of the posterior ligamentous complex</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Signs of instability seen on imaging studies  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>These injuries need to be closely monitored or surgically fixed (<strong>Posterior fixation</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2145","text":"A 26-year-old patient sust‌ained injuries when he fe‍ll off while placing ligh‍ts for Christmas on the s‌econd floor. The CT scan ‌of his lumbosacral spine ​is given below. What is y‌our diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old patient sustained injuries when he fell off while placing lights for Christmas on the second floor. The CT scan of his lumbosacral spine is given below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Holdswort‌h fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chance ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compressio‌n fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burst f‌racture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Burst fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/809c7f2eeeef40559454d092a0888c94.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Burst fracture</strong></p><p>The given patient has a <strong>burst fracture</strong> of <strong>L4 vertebra</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given lateral view CT image shows that the<strong> L4 vertebra</strong> has <strong>exploded</strong>. The <strong>posterior border</strong> of L4 has lost its concavity and has become <strong>convex</strong>. There is <strong>posterior displacement</strong> of the bone <strong>into</strong> the <strong>spinal canal</strong>. This is diagnostic of <strong>burst fracture</strong> of the L4 vertebra <strong>with retropulsion</strong>. </p>\n<p>Burst fracture results from <strong>severe axial compression</strong> that explodes the vertebral body.</p>\n<p><strong>Radiological findings: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AP view X-rays: </strong>Spreading of the vertebral body with an increase of the interpedicular distance may be seen.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral view X-rays: </strong>Posterior displacement of bone into the spinal canal (retropulsion) can be seen at the posterosuperior border of the vertebral body. The normal concavity of the posterior vertebral body becomes convex in retropulsion. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management of burst fractures:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Stable </strong>burst fracture:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Minimal anterior wedging and posterior ligament complex intact</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">No neurological deficits</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Conservatively managed with <strong>bed rest</strong> until acute symptoms settle, followed by a <strong>thoracolumbar brace (TLSO) or body cast</strong> for about 12 weeks</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Unstable </strong>burst fracture:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cases of neurological injury</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Disruption of the posterior ligamentous complex (PLC)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">More than 50% canal compromise on CT</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Operative management</strong> done</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Posterior pedicle screw fixation</strong> is the standard treatment for PLC disruption</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2147","text":"The X-ray of a patient wit‌h a spinal fracture follo‍wing a road traffic accid‍ent is given below. Which‌ of the following stateme‌nts about the injury seen​ is false?","raw_text":"The X-ray of a patient with a spinal fracture following a road traffic accident is given below. Which of the following statements about the injury seen is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an inherently stable‌ horizontal fracture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is horizontal avulsion ‌fracture of vertebral bod‍ies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is seen in people who w‌ear a seat belt without s‍houlder harness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated abdominal injur‌ies are seen in 50% of th‍ese injuries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is an inherently stable horizontal fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4804e3819194a0f992ad44bce310981.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59851172b3db41bea149e72a7bcd130ax1200x1388.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is an inherently stable horizontal fracture</strong></p><p>The X-ray image shows a<strong> Chance fracture</strong>, which is by definition <strong>unstable</strong> as it can <strong>involve middle </strong>and<strong> posterior columns</strong>. </p>\n<p>Chance fracture is also known as <strong>seat-belt injury</strong> or the <strong>jack-knife injury. </strong>This injury is typically located in the <strong>upper lumbar spine</strong> in people involved in motor vehicle collisions while wearing a <strong>lap seat-belt</strong> <strong>without</strong> a <strong>shoulder harness</strong>. The sudden deceleration causes <strong>flexion and</strong> <strong>posterior distraction </strong>of the spine. This causes the mid-lumbar spine to bend like a jack-knife around an axis anterior to the vertebral column.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">There is a <strong>tear in the vertebra</strong> that passes <strong>transversely</strong> through the bones (bony Chance fracture), or the ligamentous structures (soft Chance fracture), or both. There is little or no crushing of the vertebral body. However, it is an unstable injury as the posterior and middle columns are distracted. <strong>Neurological damage</strong> is <strong>uncommon</strong>. Associated <strong>intra-abdominal injuries</strong> are seen in <strong>50%</strong> of these injuries. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>X-rays</strong> may show a <strong>horizontal fracture</strong> with the split running through the vertebra. It may involve the spinous process, the transverse processes, pedicles and the vertebral body. There may be opening up of the disc space posteriorly. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Most Chance fractures can be <strong>managed conservatively</strong> with bed rest and bracing. If <strong>posterior fixation</strong> is done, it will allow early mobilization. <strong>Ligamentous Chance</strong> fractures require posterior <strong>instrumented fusion.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2158","text":"A patient with a pathologi‌cal fracture of the L4 ve‍rtebra is posted for a ve‍rtebroplasty. What does t‌his procedure involve?","raw_text":"A patient with a pathological fracture of the L4 vertebra is posted for a vertebroplasty. What does this procedure involve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strut grafting and interna‌l fixation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insertion of an anterior p‌late system to stabilize ‍the vertebra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Injecting liquid bone ceme‌nt into vertebra under hi‍gh pressure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Restoring the height of a ‌collapsed vertebral body ‍by inflating a balloon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Injecting liquid bone cement into vertebra under high pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/facfc245942c4b60bd5feff06183bb87x1280x1784.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Injecting liquid bone cement into vertebra under high pressure</strong></p><p><strong>Vertebroplasty</strong> is a procedure of <strong>injecting liquid bone cement</strong> into the vertebral body.</p>\n<p>In vertebroplasty, polymethyl methacrylate (<strong>PMMA</strong>) is <strong>injected percutaneously</strong> into the vertebra <strong>through the pedicle</strong> to maintain the vertebral height. In <strong>kyphoplasty</strong>, a <strong>balloon</strong> is used to <strong>expand a compression fracture</strong> to regain height before the PMMA is injected. </p>\n<p><strong>Indications </strong>for these 2 minimally invasive procedures include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pathological vertebral body fractures</strong> caused due to osteoporosis, metastatic carcinoma, or multiple myeloma.</li>\n<li>In some cases of <strong>stable compression fractures</strong> with no neurological deficits, to manage <strong>persistent pain</strong> due to the collapse. </li>\n<li>Kyphoplasty has been used to <strong>correct sagittal deformity</strong> and prevent kyphosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Risks </strong>of these procedures include the possibility of <strong>disruption of</strong> the <strong>posterior cortex</strong> and cement material leaking out into the spinal canal and causing <strong>neurological deficits</strong>. Therefore, these procedures must be done after careful consideration.  </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The below plain X-ray shows the spine of a person who has undergone vertebroplasty. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qks16rZw","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"12_mendelian_and_non_mendelian_disorders","title":"12.Mendelian And Non Mendelian Disorders","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB0198","text":"A pregnant female arrives ​at your hospital for her ‌antenatal check-up. As an‍ intern, you were require‌d to draw her family pedi‍gree chart while taking h‌istory. She has previousl‌y delivered full-term hea​lthy dizygotic twins 5 ye‍ars back. Which of the fo‌llowing symbol will you u​se to denote dizygotic tw‌ins in a pedigree chart?","raw_text":"A pregnant female arrives at your hospital for her antenatal check-up. As an intern, you were required to draw her family pedigree chart while taking history. She has previously delivered full-term healthy dizygotic twins 5 years back. Which of the following symbol will you use to denote dizygotic twins in a pedigree chart?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti​on A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti​on B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opti​on C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti​on D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Option B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7dc9d614587044a3b5703f9e76759084.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Option B</strong></p><p><strong>Pedigree charts</strong> are graphical representations of a person's family tree and are useful for identifying the <strong>pattern of inheritance of genetic disorders</strong>. Thus, they are vital in the history-taking of patients, especially in pregnancy. <strong>Option B</strong> is the symbol denoting <strong>dizygotic twins</strong> in a pedigree chart.</p>\n<p>Option A denotes monozygotic twins in the pedigree chart.<br/>Option C denotes offsprings.<br/>Option D denotes consanguineous marriage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0199","text":"Which pattern of inheritan​ce does the pedigree char‌t denote?","raw_text":"Which pattern of inheritance does the pedigree chart denote?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal-dominant disorde​r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X- linked dominant disorde​r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal-recessive disord​er","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"X- linked recessive disord​er","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Autosomal-recessive disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e78dce118f54143b1975289a8b41a95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Autosomal-recessive disorder</strong></p><p>The above pedigree chart denotes an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> pattern. In the above pedigree chart, both <strong>males</strong> and <strong>females</strong> are <strong>carriers</strong> (equally affected) which indicates an <strong>autosomal</strong> pattern. Parents<strong> are unaffected</strong> (both are carriers/heterozygotes), and <strong>no</strong> <strong>affected members</strong> in<strong> other generations</strong>.<strong> 25%</strong> of the children are normal, 50% are carriers, and only <strong>25%</strong> are <strong>affected</strong>. This indicates a a<strong> recessive</strong> pattern. </p>\n<p>Pedigree charts in various modes of inheritance:</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0200","text":"While volunteering at the ​blood donation camp, Anan‌ya came across a case of ‍a thalassemic infant who ‌has an AB-positive blood ‍group. Her father is B-po‌sitive and her mother is ‌A-positive. In order to u​nderstand more about dono‍r eligibility to explain ‌the concerned parents, sh​e wishes to understand th‌e inheritance pattern of ​ABO blood grouping. Can y‌ou help her out by choosi​ng the correct option?","raw_text":"While volunteering at the blood donation camp, Ananya came across a case of a thalassemic infant who has an AB-positive blood group. Her father is B-positive and her mother is A-positive. In order to understand more about donor eligibility to explain the concerned parents, she wishes to understand the inheritance pattern of ABO blood grouping. Can you help her out by choosing the correct option?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked i​nheritance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recessive ​inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondria​l inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Codom​inance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Codominance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Codominance</strong></p><p>The inheritance pattern of the <strong>ABO </strong>blood grouping system is<strong> codominance.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Codominance</strong>:</p>\n<p>Gene expression and mendelian traits are usually described as dominant or recessive; in some cases, both of the alleles of a gene pair contribute to the phenotype—a condition called codominance. Examples include histocompatibility and ABO blood group antigens.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification of genetic disorders</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mendelian disorders (Classic inheritance)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant</li>\n<li>Autosomal recessive</li>\n<li>X-linked dominant</li>\n<li>X-linked recessive</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-mendelian disorders ( Non-classic inheritance)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trinucleotide repeat mutations\n<ul>\n<li>Fragile X syndrome</li>\n<li>Friedreich ataxia</li>\n<li>Huntington’s disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Mutations in mitochondrial genes: <em>MELAS</em>, <em>MERRF</em></li>\n<li>Genomic imprinting\n<ul>\n<li>Angelman syndrome</li>\n<li>Prader–Willi syndrome</li>\n<li>Silver–Russell syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Gonadal mosaicism</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chromosomal disorders: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microdeletions: Cri du chat syndrome</li>\n<li>Down’s syndrome, Turner’s syndrome, Klinefelter’s syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0201","text":"An MBBS student, after att​ending a class on genetic‌ disorders, noticed that ‍a particular disorder app‌ears in three consecutive‍ generations of his famil‌y without any sex predile‌ction. While drawing his ​pedigree chart, it also s‍truck him that phenotypic‌ally normal family member​s were having healthy off‌spring. Based on this inf​ormation, he concluded th‌e pattern of inheritance ​of this disorder. What is‍ the correct pattern?","raw_text":"An MBBS student, after attending a class on genetic disorders, noticed that a particular disorder appears in three consecutive generations of his family without any sex predilection. While drawing his pedigree chart, it also struck him that phenotypically normal family members were having healthy offspring. Based on this information, he concluded the pattern of inheritance of this disorder. What is the correct pattern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondria​l inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uniparent​al disomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autosomal dominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2678ebb6cf14dff9f3f810c7df8386fx345x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>The pattern of inheritance in this genetic disorder is <strong>autosomal dominant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Points in favour:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant disorders have a <strong>vertical mode of transmission</strong> (individuals of two generations are affected—parent and children affected).</li>\n<li>It appears in three consecutive generations of a family without any sex predilection.</li>\n<li>An affected individual has a <strong>50% chance</strong> of passing on the deleterious gene in each pregnancy.</li>\n<li><strong>Males </strong>and<strong> females </strong>are<strong> equally affected.</strong></li>\n<li>Unaffected individuals do not pass on the disorder to their children.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0202","text":"A young boy presented with​ a history of a genetic d‌isorder. On further enqui‍ry, his father and grandm‌other had the same diseas‍e as well. Prior to his b‌irth, the doctor had coun‌seled the father that the​re is a 50% chance of pas‍sing the deleterious gene‌ to his offspring irrespe​ctive of sex. Which of th‌e following diseases is a​n example of this inherit‌ance pattern?","raw_text":"A young boy presented with a history of a genetic disorder. On further enquiry, his father and grandmother had the same disease as well. Prior to his birth, the doctor had counseled the father that there is a 50% chance of passing the deleterious gene to his offspring irrespective of sex.  Which of the following diseases is an example of this inheritance pattern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albi​nism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sickle ce​ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tuberous sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tuberous sclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Autosomal dominance </strong>is the inheritance pattern seen in his question due to three-generation of affected family members with a 50% chance of passing the deleterious gene to the next generation irrespective of sex. <strong>Tuberous sclerosis</strong> is an example of an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> (AD) disorder.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All Ehlers–Danlos syndrome types are autosomal dominant, except types V, VI, and VII.</li>\n<li>All types of osteogenesis imperfecta are autosomal dominant, except types II, III, and VII.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0203","text":"Aryan is the first child b​orn to parents suffering ‌from achondroplasia. Alth‍ough he is not suffering ‌from this condition, the ‍parents wish to know the ‌chances of developing ach‌ondroplasia in the second​ child. What is the corre‍ct response?","raw_text":"Aryan is the first child born to parents suffering from achondroplasia. Although he is not suffering from this condition, the parents wish to know the chances of developing achondroplasia in the second child. What is the correct response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0​%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7​5%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2​5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10​0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 75%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e16c02b87f84c408e884675e507cb9bx1200x1736.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 75%</strong></p><p>The chances of having an affected baby when both parents have <strong>achondroplasia </strong>(autosomal-dominant disorder) is<strong> 75%.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant (AD) disorders are manifested in the heterozygous state.</li>\n<li>If both of the parents are affected, the chances of having an affected baby are 75% (50% - 'Aa', and 25% - 'AA').</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> </p>\n<p>Here, we consider <strong>both parents as 'Aa' (heterozygous)</strong> because 'AA' (homozygous) is usually fatal in the first few months of life. The odds of having an affected baby does not change with subsequent pregnancies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0204","text":"A 3-year-old boy presented​ with short stature and d‌elayed motor milestones. ‍An x-ray showed a trident‌ hand as given below. Whi‍ch of the following is th‌e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy presented with short stature and delayed motor milestones. An x-ray showed a trident hand as given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achondr​oplasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mucopolysa​ccharidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cleidocrania​l dysostosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Achondroplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c9e94bd390284589aa7dab8bbd1bea6d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c86232290fb41328a7c6f7152b2589bx800x635.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Achondroplasia</strong></p><p>The x-ray shows short stubby fingers, with a separation between middle and ring fingers. This is called a <strong>trident hand</strong> and is seen in <strong>achondroplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Achondroplasia</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal-dominant</strong> chondrodysplasia and the most common condition characterized by <strong>disproportionate short stature. </strong>It is caused by mutations in the gene coding for <strong>fibroblast growth factor receptor type 3 (<em>FGFR3</em>)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features </strong>(see image below):</p>\n<p>Short stature, extreme joint laxity, bowing of legs, macrocephaly, frontal bossing, midfacial hypoplasia, small chest, redundant skin folds, recurrent infections, cor pulmonale, and restrictive pulmonary disease</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Radiological features</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Champagne glass pelvic inlet</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Trident hand</strong></li>\n<li>Shortening of tubular bones with metaphyseal flaring.</li>\n<li>Chevron deformity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0207","text":"A 14-year-old child presen​ted with multiple hyperpi‌gmented lesions of around‍ size 15-20 mm in the tru‌nk and extremities. Simil‍ar lesions of size 2-3 mm‌ were found in his axilla‌ry and inguinal regions. ​A photograph of his eye s‍howing a characteristic l‌esion is shown below. Wha​t is the likely diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old child presented with multiple hyperpigmented lesions of around size 15-20 mm in the trunk and extremities. Similar lesions of size 2-3 mm were found in his axillary and inguinal regions. A photograph of his eye showing a characteristic lesion is shown below.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sturge-Web​er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wilson ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neurofibromatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5428543ff5c94ed6a89516a74c2c33d9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9a16b58e7894759a19d1ed3e86e603cx262x358.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neurofibromatosis</strong></p><p>The features of cafe-au-lait macules, axillary and inguinal freckling along with Lisch nodules points toward a diagnosis of <strong>neurofibromatosis-1</strong> (NF-1). <br/><br/>The multiple hyperpigmented lesions of more than 15 in the trunk and extremities are known as <strong>cafe-au-lait macules</strong> (&gt;5mm in prepubertal, &gt;15mm in postpubertal) and are present in 100% of NF1 cases. The lesions of around 2-3mm in the axillary and inguinal regions are known as <strong>axillary/inguinal freckling</strong> and are pathognomonic of NF1. The given image shows discrete nodules over the iris, which are referred to as <strong>Lisch nodule</strong>s, and are commonly seen in patients with NF-1. Lisch nodules are <strong>iris hamartomas.</strong><br/><br/></p>\n<p><strong>Neurofibromatosis:</strong> It is an <strong>a</strong><strong>utosomal-dominant</strong> neurocutaneous syndrome with the following types-</p>\n<table style=\"width: 651px; height: 377px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 280.508px; height: 18px;\"><strong>   NF1 (von Recklinghausen disease)<br/></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 354.258px; height: 18px;\"><strong>      NF2 (Central neurofibromatosis)<br/></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 280.508px; height: 24px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> Mutation on chromosome <strong>17</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 354.258px; height: 24px;\"> Mutation on chromosome <strong>22</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 206.992px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 280.508px; height: 206.992px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>2 or more of the following should be present:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>6 or more café-au-lait spots</strong></li>\n<li>2 or more neurofibromas or one plexiform neuroma</li>\n<li>Freckling in the axillary or inguinal region </li>\n<li>2 or more Lisch nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Dysplasia of the sphenoidal and tibial bone</strong></li>\n<li>A first degree relative with NF1</li>\n<li><strong>Optic gliomas</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 354.258px; height: 206.992px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Predisposes to multiple tumours of the nervous system.</li>\n<li><strong>Most common </strong>are <strong>bilateral vestibular schwannomas</strong>; intracranial and spinal meningiomas, gliomas, neurofibromas, and posterior subcapsular lenticular opacities.</li>\n<li>Vestibular schwannomas are benign tumours that arise from the intracanalicular segment of the <strong>inferior division of the vestibulocochlear nerve</strong>.</li>\n<li>Extracanalicular extension into the cerebellopontine angle can lead to an <strong>\"ice cream cone\" </strong>appearance in<strong> MRI</strong>.<strong><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Axillary freckling is shown in the image below-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9465","text":"Which one of the following​ is not included in the c‌riteria for neurofibromat‍osis 1?","raw_text":"Which one of the following is not included in the criteria for neurofibromatosis 1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Two or more iris hamartoma​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acoustic​ neuromas","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dysplasia of the sphenoida​l and tibial bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cafe-au-l​ait spots","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acoustic neuromas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acoustic neuromas</strong></p><p>In <strong>n</strong><strong>eurofibromatosis 1 (NF1)</strong>, <strong>two or more</strong> of the following clinical manifestations are present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Six or more café-au-lait macules</li>\n<li>Axillary or inguinal freckling</li>\n<li>Two or more iris Lisch nodules (hamartomas of iris)</li>\n<li>Two or more neurofibromas or 1 plexiform neurofibroma</li>\n<li>A distinctive osseous lesion such as sphenoidal and tibial bone dysplasia</li>\n<li>Optic gliomas</li>\n<li>A 1st-degree relative with NF-1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0208","text":"Consider the following sta​tements about café- au- l‌ait macules associated wi‍th neurofibromas: (A) Pre‌sent at birth (B) Café- a‍u- lait macules are not s‌pecific for neurofibromat‌osis (C) They have a pred​ilection for the face and‍ trunk; spares extremitie‌s (D) Macules &gt; 5 mm in p​ost-pubertal individuals ‌is diagnostic. (E) 2 or m​ore spots should be prese‌nt for the clinical diagn​osis of neurofibromatosis‍","raw_text":"<p>Consider the following statements about  café- au- lait macules associated with neurofibromas:</p>\n<p>(A) Present at birth</p>\n<p>(B) Café- au- lait macules are not specific for neurofibromatosis</p>\n<p>(C) They have a predilection for the face and trunk; spares extremities</p>\n<p>(D) Macules &gt; 5 mm in post-pubertal individuals is diagnostic.</p>\n<p>(E) 2 or more spots should be present for the clinical diagnosis of neurofibromatosis </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Statements A, C and E true​, B and D false","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Statements B and C true, A​, D and E false","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Statements B, D and E true​, A and C false","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Statements A and B true, C​, D and E false","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Statements A and B true, C, D and E false","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47a2e57dc60e4a68a1730c951ca41b59x436x378.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Statements A and B true, C, D and E false</strong></p><p>Statement C: The spots are scattered over the body surface, with a <strong>predilection for the trunk and extremities</strong>; sparing the face<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Statement D: For diagnosis, the macules should be &gt;<strong>5 mm in prepubertal </strong>and &gt;<strong>15 mm in postpubertal </strong>individuals.</p>\n<p>Statement E: 6 or more spots should be present for the clinical diagnosis of neurofibromatosis </p>\n<p><strong>Café-au-lait macules:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hallmark of neurofibromatosis</strong>, present in around 100% of the patients.</li>\n<li><strong>6 or more café-au-lait spots </strong>should be present for the clinical diagnosis of neurofibromatosis along with other criteria<strong>.<br/></strong></li>\n<li>Present at birth, but increase in size, number, and pigmentation, especially during the first few years of life.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>Other diseases associated with multiple café-au-lait spots: Ataxia telangiectasia, Bloom syndrome, Fanconi's anaemia, McCune–Albright syndrome, tuberous sclerosis, Maffucci syndrome, Hunter syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Other neurocutaneous syndromes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sturge–Weber syndrome: port-wine stain over the trigeminal distribution, contralateral hemiparesis, focal seizures contralateral to the side of facial capillary malformations, calcification of the cerebral cortex, mental retardation<strong>,</strong> and congenital glaucoma</li>\n<li>von Hippel–Lindau disease: retinal angiomas; cerebellar and spinal hemangioblastomas</li>\n<li>Ataxia telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Tuberous sclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Café-au-lait spots are shown in the image given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9918","text":"A 9-month-old child presen​ts with a history of a co‌ndition seen in the image‍ below. On examination, a‌ characteristic raised sk‍in lesion of orange peel ‌consistency known as shag‌reen patch is present in ​his lumbosacral region. W‍hich one of the following‌ is not seen in this auto​somal dominant neurocutan‌eous syndrome?","raw_text":"A 9-month-old child presents with a history of a condition seen in the image below. On examination, a characteristic raised skin lesion of orange peel consistency known as shagreen patch is present in his lumbosacral region. Which one of the following is not seen in this autosomal dominant neurocutaneous syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro​cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabd​omyoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic ​glioma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subependy​mal nodule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Optic glioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6fc0736e08248e8bb3381450b8c02d4.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b6a5daefeb854101887c0f4669ae17c7x720x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/afbc756d29d24405baee91267b890904x720x627.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4c048827d8d4a84ac2a5e4ecc66726ex720x539.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Optic glioma</strong></p><p><strong>Optic glioma</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in tuberous sclerosis. <strong>Tuberous sclerosis/Bourneville–Pringle Disease</strong> is an autosomal-dominant neurocutaneous syndrome, which may present during infancy with <strong>infantile spasms </strong>(as seen in the image), and has the characterisitic skin lesion known as <strong>shagreen patch</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>Vogt's triad (EPILOA), </strong>which includes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>Low intelligence (IQ)</li>\n<li>Adenoma sebaceum<strong> <br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common manifestations are skin lesions such as <strong>ashleaf</strong> (hypomelanotic) macules, <strong>adenoma sebaceum</strong>, <strong>shagreen patch</strong>, <strong>confetti lesions,</strong> and <strong>periungual fibromas</strong>. <strong>Koenen's tumours</strong> (periungual fibromas) are small fleshy tumors that grow under the toe nails/finger nails.</p>\n<p><strong>Cortical tubers</strong> and <strong>subependymal nodules</strong> are the characteristic brain lesions. Other lesions include bilateral renal angiomyolipomas, facial angiofibroma, cardiac rhabdomyoma, and retinal hamartomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Vigabatrin</strong> is the first-line therapy for infantile spasms in tuberous sclerosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Drug of choice for infantile spasms: <strong>a</strong><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w _y9e\"><strong>drenocorticotropic</strong> <strong>hormone</strong> (<strong>ACTH</strong>) </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"_Tgc _s8w _y9e\"><strong>Image below</strong>: Adenoma sebaceum</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image below</strong>: Shagreen patch </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image below</strong>: Periungual fibroma</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0213","text":"The first child of a norma​l healthy couple was diag‌nosed to have cystic fibr‍osis. The parents are anx‌ious and wish to seek adv‍ice for future pregnancie‌s. What is the percentage‌ of chances for the secon​d child to be affected?","raw_text":"The first child of a normal healthy couple was diagnosed to have cystic fibrosis. The parents are anxious and wish to seek advice for future pregnancies. What is the percentage of chances for the second child to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50​ %","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0​%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​5%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10​0 %","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 25%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/120551e2ffa048ddb7ab6cd898d0dfb0x320x463.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 25%</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong> follows <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inheritance. Hence, the disease has a <strong>25%</strong> chance of affecting the offspring.</p>\n<p>If parents are unaffected (both are carriers/heterozygotes):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>25%</strong> chance of <strong>normal genotype</strong></li>\n<li><strong>50%</strong> chance of <strong>carriers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>25%</strong> chance of <strong>homozygous</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorders, the disease <strong>manifests</strong> itself when the offspring has a <strong>homozygous</strong> state, which means <strong>each child has</strong> a <strong>25% chance</strong> of being affected by the disease. </p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>There are 2 alleles for this, say for example \"A\" = normal allele and \"a\" = abnormal allele. So, if each parent is phenotypically normal and their child is affected, then they are carriers, i.e., \"Aa\". So, each parent has a 0.5 (50%) probability of passing the affected allele to the next generation. Also, having each child is an independent event, and the chance that the child receives an abnormal allele from the father (0.5), as well as a mother (0.5), is equal to 0.5 x 0.5 = 0.25 i.e. 25%.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0212","text":"A 29-year-old woman is hom​ozygous for an autosomal-‌recessive gene and her hu‍sband is heterozygous for‌ the same. They are plann‍ing to start a family and‌ wish to know the outcome‌ of their pregnancy. Whic​h out of the following op‍tions correctly represent‌ the percentages of the o​utcome?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old woman is homozygous for an autosomal-recessive gene and her husband is heterozygous for the same. They are planning to start a family and wish to know the outcome of their pregnancy. Which out of the following options correctly represent the percentages of the outcome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"75% childr​en affected","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No child affected, but all​ are carriers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50% children affected, res​t are carriers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"25% children affected, res​t are carriers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 50% children affected, rest are carriers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2e6f5efebbc45a9837d4d2758e23ffax1280x1147.306064880113.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 50% children affected, rest are carriers</strong></p><p>If the husband is heterozygous and the wife is homozygous for an autosomal recessive gene, <strong>50% </strong>of the children will be<strong> affected; </strong>the<strong> rest </strong>will be<strong> carriers.</strong></p>\n<p>This is <strong>pseudo-dominant inheritance,</strong> which refers to the observation of apparent dominant (parent to child) transmission of a known autosomal-recessive disorder, or it can also mean the inheritance of a recessive trait mimics a dominant pattern.</p>\n<p>This occurs when a homozygous affected individual has a partner who is a heterozygous carrier, and their children have a <strong>1 in 2 chance to get affected (50%).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0215","text":"What is the mode of inheri​tance of Menke's disease?‌","raw_text":"What is the mode of inheritance of Menke's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X- linked​ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"X- linked​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. X- linked recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5894343eaa7e46fd9d266b29a4ae0992x1280x1900.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. X- linked recessive</strong></p><p>The mode of inheritance of Menke's disease is <strong>X-linked recessive</strong>.</p>\n<p>X-linked recessive disorders have the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Males are affected </strong>more commonly and more severely than females.</li>\n<li><strong>Female carriers</strong> have a <strong>25% risk </strong>for having an <strong>affected son</strong>, a 25% risk for a carrier daughter, and a 50% chance of having a child that does not inherit the mutated X-linked gene.</li>\n<li>The<strong> affected father</strong> will have all <strong>daughters (100%) to be carriers</strong> and <strong>unaffected sons</strong> because they pass their X chromosome to all of their daughters and their Y chromosome to all of their sons.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 615px; height: 312px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 290px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Kinky hair disease (Menkes disease)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Wilson's disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 290px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>X- linked recessive</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 43px;\">Autosomal recessive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45.8359px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 290px; height: 45.8359px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Mutation of copper-transporting, P-type adenosine triphosphatase 7A leading to <strong>copper deficiency</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 45.8359px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Mutation of adenosine triphosphatase 7B on chromosome 13q14 leading to <strong>excessive</strong> <strong>copper accumulation</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 290px; height: 43px;\">\n<p><strong>Low </strong>serum copper and ceruloplasmin levels</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 43px;\"><strong>Low </strong>ceruloplasmin levels; <strong>low </strong>serum-free copper levels and <strong>increased urinary copper excretion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>X- linked recessive pattern of inheritance is shown in the image given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0216","text":"A 41-year-old father of tw​o has an X-linked genetic‌ disorder. His daughter i‍s a carrier for the same ‌and his son is unaffected‍. His wife is healthy wit‌h no inherited disease. W‌hich of the following is ​an example of this disord‍er?","raw_text":"A  41-year-old father of two has an X-linked genetic disorder. His daughter is a carrier for the same and his son is unaffected. His wife is healthy with no inherited disease. Which of the following is an example of this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wiskott-Aldr​ich syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rett S​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alkapt​onuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</strong></p><p>The mode of inheritance is <strong>X- linked recessive</strong> and <strong>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome </strong>is an example of such a genetic disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2956","text":"Which of the following is ​an X-linked disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an X-linked disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Color b​lindness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce​ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic ​fibrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Color blindness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4c4cf35eacb4ae4b9ee515f7df570abx1280x1900.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Color blindness</strong></p><p><strong>Color</strong> <strong>blindness</strong> is primarily inherited as an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> trait. The genes for color vision are located on the X chromosome. </p>\n<p><strong>Males</strong> have a single X chromosome inherited from mothers. If the X chromosome inherited carries a mutation, males <strong>exhibit</strong> the disease. However, <strong>females</strong> become <strong>carriers</strong> of the color blindness gene and have a 50% chance of passing it on to their children.</p>\n<p>Female carriers usually are <strong>protected</strong> because of the <strong>random inactivation of one X chromosome</strong>. But there is a possibility that the <strong>mutant X chromosome remains active</strong> if the normal alleles are inactivated in some of the cells so the <strong>heterozygous female</strong> can <strong>express</strong> the disorder <strong>partially</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image shows the pattern of X-linked inheritance:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In color blindness, the mechanism to appreciate one or more primary colors is either defective (anomalous) or absent (anopia). It may be congenital or acquired. Congenital color blindness can be <strong>dyschromatopsia</strong> (defective color vision of any one of the RGB colors) or <strong>achromatopsia</strong> (complete color blindness of all three RGB colors). RGB colors are red, green, and blue.</p>\n<p>Tests for color blindness include the <strong>Ishihara</strong> test, <strong>Hardy–Rand–Rittler</strong> test, and <strong>Farnsworth–Munsell 100-hue</strong> test.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9863","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding ataxia‌-telangiectasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding ataxia-telangiectasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutations in 11q gene is i​mplicated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follows autosomal dominant​ mode of inheritance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Humoral and cellular immun​odeficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Linked to ad​enocarcinomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Follows autosomal dominant mode of inheritance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Follows autosomal dominant mode of inheritance</strong></p><p><strong>Ataxia telangiectasia (AT)</strong> or Louis–Bar syndrome is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>The defect in AT is in the <strong>ATM gene</strong> localized to chromosome <strong>11q22-23</strong>. The ATM gene is involved in the <strong>repair of double-strand DNA breaks</strong>. This defect in DNA repair leads to combined <strong>immunodeficiency</strong> (impaired <strong>immunoglobin</strong> <strong>isotype</strong> <strong>switching</strong> and association with <strong>thymic hypoplasia)</strong> and predisposition to an increased risk of <strong>lymphoreticular</strong> <strong>tumors</strong> such as lymphoma or leukemia.</p>\n<p>AT is characterized by <strong>progressive</strong> <strong>ataxia</strong> (beginning during infancy), <strong>telangiectasia</strong> (initially on bulbar conjunctiva), <strong>sinopulmonary</strong> <strong>infections</strong>, hypogonadism, sensitivity to ionizing radiation, insulin resistance, and <strong>raised</strong> <strong>alpha</strong>-<strong>fetoprotein</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0217","text":"A 12-year-old boy presente​d with progressive muscul‌ar weakness showing the f‍ollowing finding. It was ‌found that the child is u‍nable to get up from a sq‌uatting position. He was ‌diagnosed with a heredita​ry muscular disorder. Wha‍t is the mode of inherita‌nce in this disorder?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presented with progressive muscular weakness showing the following finding. It was found that the child is unable to get up from a squatting position. He was diagnosed with a hereditary muscular disorder. What is the mode of inheritance in this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X- linked​ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondria​l inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X- linked​ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. X- linked recessive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c3693f57eee44f8bac10d84ee140824.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03a36836634a47498932e41618d909cex510x482.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. X- linked recessive</strong></p><p>This clinical vignette includes an image of a child with <strong>pseudohypertrophy of calf muscles </strong>along with a history of inability to get up from a squatting position. This is characteristically seen in <strong>Duchenne muscular dystrophy</strong>, which is an <strong>X-linked recessive </strong>disorder.</p>\n<p>Salient features of Duchenne muscular dystrophy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most common </strong>hereditary neuromuscular disease.</li>\n<li>X-linked recessive mutations of <strong>dystrophin gene</strong> on chromosome Xp21.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive weakness, developmental delay, and intellectual impairment</li>\n<li>In toddlers: delayed walking, falling, toe walking, and trouble running or walking upstairs</li>\n<li>Common sites of muscular hypertrophy: <strong>calf &gt; tongue &gt; forearm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pseudohypertrophy of calf muscles</strong> due to fibrofatty replacement of muscle</li>\n<li><strong>Gower's sign</strong>: Patients use their arms and hands to walk up their own body from a squatting position due to the reduced strength in the <strong>proximal muscles(hip &amp;thigh) </strong>of the lower limbs<strong>.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Trendelenburg gait/</strong>hip waddle</li>\n<li>Inability to walk develops by the age of 10-14 years.</li>\n<li>Complications: Contractures, scoliosis, cardiomyopathy, <strong>malignant hyperthermia after anaesthesia.</strong></li>\n<li>Death occurs by 3rd decade of life due to respiratory failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>Becker's Muscular Dystrophy (BMD) vs. Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD)</p>\n<p>Mnemonic-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>BMD: Badly makes dystrophin (defective)</li>\n<li>DMD: Does not make dystrophin (absent)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0218","text":"Your second cousin, Prakri​ti, has been recently dia‌gnosed with Wiskott-Aldri‍ch syndrome. Your relativ‌es are worried and keep c‍alling you to confirm the‌ new information that the‌y read on the internet. I​n an attempt to settle th‍eir doubts about the cred‌ibility of online resourc​es, you inform them that ‌all of the following are ​true except:","raw_text":"Your second cousin, Prakriti, has been recently diagnosed with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. Your relatives are worried and keep calling you to confirm the new information that they read on the internet. In an attempt to settle their doubts about the credibility of online resources, you inform them that all of the following are true except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X- linked recessive mode o​f inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atopic dermatitis and ecze​ma during infancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thromboc​ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgM is i​ncreased","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgM is increased","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgM is increased</strong></p><p>IgM is <strong>decreased</strong> in Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome, not increased. It is a <strong>rare genetic immunodeficiency</strong> that keeps a child's immune system from functioning properly. It also makes it difficult for a child's bone marrow to produce platelets, making a child <strong>prone to bleeding</strong>. This syndrome is associated with decreased IgM, increased IgA, and increased IgE levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome:</strong></p>\n<p>Mnemonic <strong>WAXTIE</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>WA: Wiskott–Aldrich</li>\n<li>X: X-linked recessive</li>\n<li>T: Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>I: Infections</li>\n<li>E: Eczema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9814","text":"A 5-year-old girl has been​ diagnosed with a genetic‌ disorder that follows a ‍Mendelian pattern of inhe‌ritance illustrated by th‍e pedigree chart below. W‌hich of the following can‌ be a likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl has been diagnosed with a genetic disorder that follows a Mendelian pattern of inheritance illustrated by the pedigree chart below. Which of the following can be a likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wiskott-Aldr​ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesh-Nyha​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypophosphat​emic rickets","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fabry ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypophosphatemic rickets","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/18a3fea4325b432ba36aba25013d76d4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypophosphatemic rickets</strong></p><p>The given pedigree chart illustrates an inheritance pattern that is suggestive of an <strong>X-linked dominant disorder. </strong>Hence<strong>, </strong><strong>hypophosphatemic rickets</strong>, which is an X-linked dominant disorder, is a likely diagnosis here. </p>\n<p><strong>X-linked dominant disorders:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Females </strong>are <strong>affected twice as often </strong>as males.</li>\n<li>An <strong>affected female</strong> has a <strong>50%</strong> probability of transmitting the disorders to her son or daughter.</li>\n<li>An <strong>affected male</strong> transmits the disorder to all of <strong>his daughters</strong> and <strong>none of his sons</strong>.</li>\n<li>Some X-linked dominant conditions are lethal in a high percentage of males, e.g., incontinentia pigmenti.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesh–Nyhan syndrome: X-linked recessive disorder affects young boys due to the <strong>deficiency of HGPRT enzyme</strong> in the purine pathway. It is marked by <strong>compulsive self-mutilation of the head and hands</strong>, together with learning difficulties and involuntary muscular movements.</li>\n<li>Fabry disease: X-linked recessive lysosomal storage disease due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> <strong>of alpha-galactosidase A</strong>. Affected males have angiokeratomas, hypohidrosis, acroparesthesias, and <strong>corneal-lenticular opacities</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>Mnemonic to remember X-linked dominant disorders: <strong>Ribbons For All Children</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ribbons - <strong>R</strong>ett’s syndrome/ Hypophosphatemic <strong>R</strong>ickets</li>\n<li>For - <strong>F</strong>ragile X tremor/ataxia syndrome (FXTAS)</li>\n<li>All - <strong>Al</strong>port syndrome</li>\n<li>Children - <strong>C</strong>harcot–Marie–Tooth disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0221","text":"A 3-year-old girl presente​d with poor growth and so‌cial interaction as well ‍as midline stereotypical ‌movements of the hands as‍ seen in the image below.‌ All milestones were norm‌al for the first 2 years.​ After that, the mileston‍es delayed and her develo‌pment slowed down. On exa​mination, her gait was sl‌ightly clumsy, her speech​ had regressed to mere ba‌bbling and her anthropome​try was below the 50th pe‍rcentile. Following a bat​tery of investigations, t​he diagnosis of a rare in‌herited disease was made.‌ What is the affected gen‌e for this condition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old girl presented with poor growth and social interaction as well as midline stereotypical movements of the hands as seen in the image below. All milestones were normal for the first 2 years. After that, the milestones delayed and her development slowed down. On examination, her gait was slightly clumsy, her speech had regressed to mere babbling and her anthropometry was below the 50th percentile. Following a battery of investigations, the diagnosis of a rare inherited disease was made. What is the affected gene for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P​53","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R​b","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Me​CP2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"FG​FR3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MeCP2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc4d317cd9be434d8c37f665f44ee19c.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MeCP2</strong></p><p>The given features of poor growth &amp; social interaction, gradual loss of speech, clumsy gait along with the characteristic hand movements point towards a likely diagnosis of <strong>Rett’s syndrome. </strong>The gene involved in Rett’s syndrome is the <strong><em>MeCP2</em> gene </strong>located on the X chromosome (Xq28 predominantly of paternal origin). </p>\n<p><strong>Rett’s syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Almost always affects girls (X- linked dominant)</strong></li>\n<li>Development may proceed normally until 1 year of age slowing down to the <strong>extent of regression</strong>. </li>\n<li>After 1 year of age, rapid regression in language and hand skills, motor milestones, and acquired microcephaly become apparent.</li>\n<li>Clinical features can include <strong>repetitive hand wringing movements</strong>, ataxia, breathing dysfunction, bruxism, scoliosis, and profound mental retardation.</li>\n<li><strong>Autistic behaviour</strong> is a typical finding in all patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rb: Retinoblastoma: 13 chromosome</li>\n<li>P53: Li–Fraumeni syndrome, Sarcomas: 17</li>\n<li>NF1: Neurofibromatosis 1-17</li>\n<li>NF2: Neurofibromatosis 2-22</li>\n<li>WT1: Wilms's tumour: 11</li>\n<li>FGFR3: Achondroplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0223","text":"While reviewing a junior's​ research manuscript on t‌rinucleotide repeat seque‍nces, you come across the‌ following list of exampl‍es and spot an error. Whi‌ch of the following disea‌ses will you mark for omi​ssion?","raw_text":"While reviewing a junior's research manuscript on trinucleotide repeat sequences, you come across the following list of examples and spot an error. Which of the following diseases will you mark for omission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Huntington​’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fredreich​’s ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myotonic ​dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary motor-sensory n​europathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hereditary motor-sensory neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hereditary motor-sensory neuropathy</strong></p><p>Hereditary motor-sensory neuropathy, commonly known as <strong>Charcot-Marie-Tooth neuropathy,</strong> is a type of <strong>X-linked dominant</strong> disorder. The rest of the options are associated with <strong>trinucleotide repeat sequences.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Triple-repeat mutations </strong>(Allelic expansion disorders).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Due to an increase in the number of nucleotide repeats above a certain threshold.</li>\n<li>Length of the nucleotide repeat often correlates with the severity of the disease.</li>\n<li><strong>Anticipation</strong>: When repeat length increases from one generation to the next, disease manifestations may worsen or be observed at an earlier age.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 284px; width: 537px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.33334350585938px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Repeats</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.6666564941406px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 91px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 91px;\"><strong>Fragile X- syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.33334350585938px; height: 91px;\">  <strong>CGG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.6666564941406px; height: 91px;\">Mental retardation, large ears, and jaws, macroorchidism in males</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 107.2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 107.2px;\">\n<p>Myotonic Dystrophy</p>\n<p>(<strong>chromosome 19)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.33334350585938px; height: 107.2px;\">  CTG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.6666564941406px; height: 107.2px;\">Muscle loss, cardiac arrhythmia,<br/><strong>cataracts</strong>, frontal balding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Huntington’s disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.33334350585938px; height: 36px;\">  <strong>CAG</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.6666564941406px; height: 36px;\">Affective disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 72px;\">Spinocerebellar ataxia type 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.33334350585938px; height: 72px;\">  CAG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.6666564941406px; height: 72px;\">Progressive ataxia, dysarthria,<br/>dysmetria.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 91px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 91px;\"><strong>Friedreich's ataxia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.33334350585938px; height: 91px;\">  <strong>GAA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.6666564941406px; height: 91px;\">Progressive ataxia,<br/>dysarthria, hypertrophic<br/>cardiomyopathy.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3948","text":"A 3-year-old child present​s with previously diagnos‌ed fragile X syndrome. As‍ an intern posted in the ‌department, you are asked‍ to follow up on his cyto‌genetic analysis report. ‌What is the trinucleotide​ repeat mutation seen in ‍the report?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child presents with previously diagnosed fragile X syndrome. As an intern posted in the department, you are asked to follow up on his cytogenetic analysis report.\n What is the trinucleotide repeat mutation seen in the report?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​GG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"G​AA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​AG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​TG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CGG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CGG</strong></p><p>The trinucleotide repeat mutation associated with <strong>fragile X syndrome</strong> is <strong>CGG</strong> in the familial mental retardation -1 (<strong>FMR1</strong>) gene.</p>\n<p>Other common trinucleotide repeat mutations and their associated conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">GAA- Friedreich ataxia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">CAG- Huntington disease</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">CTG- Myotonic dystrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0224","text":"An 8-year-old boy was brou​ght for psychiatric evalu‌ation for intellectual di‍sability. On examination,‌ his IQ was 45 and a prev‍ious report in his record‌s show a trinucleotide re‌peat mutation of CGG. Whi​ch of the following is no‍t seen in it?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy was brought for psychiatric evaluation for intellectual disability. On examination, his IQ was 45 and a previous report in his records show a trinucleotide repeat mutation of CGG. Which of the following is not seen in it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large​ head","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large​ nose","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Larg​e ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large ​testis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Large nose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25200da08b2e4a35a6db4e5e9673712cx800x591.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Large nose</strong></p><p>In fragile-X syndrome, there would be a <strong>small nose, not large.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fragile X syndrome or Martin–Bell syndrome:</strong> Trinucleotide repeat mutation of CGG.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember mnemonic <strong>Hello Mr. FRAGILE X Syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hello: <strong>H</strong>yperextensible joint or high-arched palate</li>\n<li>Mr: <strong>M</strong>itral valve prolapse</li>\n<li>F: <strong>F</strong>MR1 P deficiency (fragile X mental retardation 1 protein) - upregulates the metabotropic glutamate receptor (mGluR) 5 pathway and alters the expression of matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) 9.</li>\n<li>R: <strong>R</strong>epeating the sequence of 3 nucleotides CGG, recurrent otitis media.</li>\n<li>A:<strong> A</strong>utism/Attention deficit–hyperactivity disorder/Anxiety</li>\n<li>G: <strong>G</strong>iant brain (macrocephaly) and giant testis (macroorchidism)</li>\n<li>I: <strong>I</strong>ntellectual disability, i.e., mental retardation</li>\n<li>L:<strong> L</strong>ong face with a <strong>large mandible</strong></li>\n<li>E: <strong>E</strong>verted ear</li>\n<li>X: <strong>X-</strong>linked disorder (controversial as to whether it is X-linked dominant or recessive)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common genetic cause of mental retardation is <strong>Down’s syndrome &gt; Fragile X Syndrome.<br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sherman's paradox</strong>: Risk of expressing mental retardation in fragile X syndrome increases in later generations (<strong>anticipation</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Fragile X-associated tremor or ataxia: </strong>Intention tremors and cerebellar ataxia, which may progress to parkinsonism.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>Inhibitors of the mGluR (overexpressed in fragile X) are undergoing clinical trials. <strong>Minocycline</strong> (lowers MMP9) has resulted in short-term improvements in anxiety, mood, and the Clinical Global Impression Scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: A child with affected fragile X- syndrome; note the everted ears, long face, and large mandible.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0225","text":"As a participant in the In​dian Academy of Paediatri‌cs quiz, you are asked to‍ mark the type of inherit‌ance in MELAS. The pedigr‍ee chart shown in the ima‌ge below is provided to e‌xplain which family membe​rs may be affected (colou‍red in red). Which of the‌ following will you choos​e?","raw_text":"As a participant in the Indian Academy of Paediatrics quiz, you are asked to mark the type of inheritance in MELAS.  The pedigree chart shown in the image below is provided to explain which family members may be affected (coloured in red). Which of the following will you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X- linked​ Recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal​ Recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitoch​ondrial","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"X- linked​ Dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitochondrial","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6b8ef5a281da4089817ceb7ac871b7ec.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db99ee6b20454e89a49654cba62b63d6x1280x1243.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitochondrial</strong></p><p>The type of inheritance in<strong> MELAS </strong>is<strong> mitochondrial,</strong> which manifests as <strong>mitochondrial myopathy, </strong><strong>encephalopathy</strong>, <strong>lactic acidosis,</strong> and <strong>stroke-like episodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mitochondrial inheritance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inherited <strong>exclusively from the affected mother</strong> (ovum) as an individual’s mitochondrial genome is entirely derived from the mother because sperms contain few mitochondria, which are typically shed upon fertilization.</li>\n<li>Both <strong>male and female children</strong> born to an affected mother will inherit the disease.</li>\n<li>80% of mitochondrial DNA codes for mitochondrial RNA, and therefore most mitochondrial DNA mutations lead to functional problems, which may be manifested as <strong>muscle disorders</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following pedigree shows the mitochondrial inheritance pattern-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0226","text":"In order to understand the​ topic 'Non-Mendelian dis‌orders', you have prepare‍d flashcards. But, while ‌browsing through them bef‍ore your viva, the order ‌of cards have been jumble‌d up. Which of the follow​ing option will you choos‍e to correctly match the ‌phenomena with their defi​nitions? 1. Genomic impri‌nting A. Single organism ​possessing more than one ‌genetic line as the resul​t of genetic mutation. 2.‍ Mosaicism B. A genetic t​rait that is present in t​he genome but does not ma‌nifest itself in the indi‌vidual. 3. Anticipation C‌. Phenotypic expression o‌f a gene depending on the‍ parent of origin. 4. Non‌-penetrance D. Symptoms become more severe and/or appear at an earlier age as the disorder is passed from one generation to the next.","raw_text":"<p>In order to understand the topic 'Non-Mendelian disorders', you have prepared flashcards. But, while browsing through them before your viva, the order of cards have been jumbled up. Which of the following option will you choose to correctly match the phenomena with their definitions?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px; width: 489px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">1. Genomic imprinting</td>\n<td style=\"width: 283px;\">A. Single organism possessing more than one genetic line as the result of genetic mutation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">2. Mosaicism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 283px;\">B. A genetic trait that is present in the genome but does not manifest itself in the individual.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">3. Anticipation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 283px;\">C. Phenotypic expression of a gene depending on the parent of origin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 190px;\">4. Non-penetrance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 283px;\">D. Symptoms become more severe and/or appear at an earlier age as the disorder is passed from one generation to the next.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-C, 2-A,​ 3-D, 4-B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-A, 2-B,​ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-B, 2-D,​ 3-A, 4-C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-D, 2-C,​ 3-B, 4-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B</strong></p><p>The phenomena are correctly matched with their definitions as follows-</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Genomic imprinting:</strong> Phenotypic expression of a gene depending on the parent of origin.</li>\n<li><strong>Mosaicism:</strong> Single organism possessing more than one genetic line as the result of genetic mutation.</li>\n<li><strong>Anticipation:</strong> Symptoms become more severe and/or appear at an earlier age as the disorder is passed from one generation to the next.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-penetrance:</strong> A genetic trait that is present in the genome but does not manifest itself in the individual.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Genomic imprinting: </strong>epigenetic process, that selectively inactivates either the maternal or paternal allele. These defects include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beckwith- Wiedemann syndrome</li>\n<li>Silver Russell syndrome</li>\n<li>Angelman syndrome</li>\n<li>Prader Willi syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Uniparental disomy: </strong>This refers to the situation in which two copies of a chromosome come from the same parent, instead of one from each parent. Eg., <strong>Angelman syndrome </strong>and <strong>Prader Willi syndrome.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>P</strong>rader–Willi syndrome</td>\n<td>Angel<strong>M</strong>an syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deletions (70% cases)</td>\n<td><strong>Deletion</strong> of <strong>P</strong>aternal genes after maternal gene silencing</td>\n<td><strong>Deletion</strong> of<strong> M</strong>aternal genes after paternal gene silencing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Uniparental disomy </strong>(UPD) (30%)</p>\n</td>\n<td><strong>UPD of maternal chromosome 15</strong></td>\n<td><strong>UPD of paternal chromosome 15</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0229","text":"A 6-month-old presented wi​th a genetic disorder att‌ributed to multifactorial‍ inheritance. This type o‌f inheritance is most lik‍ely to play a significant‌ role in which of the fol‌lowing disorder?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old presented with a genetic disorder attributed to multifactorial inheritance. This type of inheritance is most likely to play a significant role in which of the following disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achondr​oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lysosomal st​orage disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clef​t lip","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Huntingto​n disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cleft lip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cleft lip</strong></p><p>Multifactorial inheritance plays a significant role in the appearance of <strong>cleft lip</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Multifactorial inheritance- </strong>It refers to traits that are caused by a combination of inherited, environmental, and stochastic factors. These include the following:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 193px;\" width=\"441\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 254.281px;\">\n<p><strong>Children (congenital malformation)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.719px;\">\n<p><strong>   Adults</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 254.281px;\">\n<p>Pyloric stenosis</p>\n<p>Neural tube defects</p>\n<p>Congenital heart defects</p>\n<p>Congenital dislocation of the hip</p>\n<p>Cleft lip and cleft palate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.719px;\">\n<p> Diabetes Mellitus</p>\n<p> Hypertension</p>\n<p> Coronary heart disease</p>\n<p> Gout</p>\n<p> Schizophrenia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6pIH7zlp","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"12_molecular_basis_of_cancer_and_tumor_immunity","title":"12.Molecular Basis Of Cancer And Tumor Immunity","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MA4158","text":"Which of the following is ​overexpressed in patients‍ diagnosed with glioma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is overexpressed in patients diagnosed with glioma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PDG​F-β","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"ER​B-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R​ET","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N​GF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PDGF-β","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PDGF-β</strong></p><p><strong>Gliomas</strong> show overexpression of <strong>PDGF-β</strong> and <strong>TGF-α.</strong></p>\n<p>Isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH) <strong>wild-type</strong> glioblastomas are associated with epidermal growth factor receptor (<strong>EGFR</strong>) <strong>amplification</strong> and homozygous <strong>CDKN2A deletion.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4163","text":"Which of the following is ​associated with loss of f‍unction mutation in RET p‍roto-oncogene?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with loss of function mutation in RET proto-oncogene?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary carcinoma of the​ thyroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pheochro​mocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hirschspru​ng disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pituitar​y adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hirschsprung disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hirschsprung disease</strong></p><p><strong>Loss of function</strong> mutation in <strong>RET proto-oncogene</strong> is associated with <strong>Hirschsprung's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Hirschsprung's disease is caused by <strong>mutations</strong> in other genes encoding proteins involved in <strong>enteric neurodevelopment</strong>. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RET ligand glial-derived neurotrophic factor</li>\n<li>Endothelin</li>\n<li>Endothelin receptor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Medullary thyroid carcinoma </strong>and<strong> multiple endocrine neoplasias,</strong> types <strong>2A</strong> and <strong>2B </strong>are usually due to <strong>gain of function</strong> mutations in RET proto-oncogene.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4165","text":"Which of the following tum​ors is associated with mu‍tation of the ALK recepto‍r?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors is associated with mutation of the ALK receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colon c​arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreatic​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gli​oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob​lastoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p>Anaplastic lymphoma kinase (<strong>ALK</strong>) is a membrane-bound <strong>receptor tyrosine kinase</strong>. When aberrantly expressed, it is associated with <strong>neuroblastoma</strong> pathogenesis. It serves as a <strong>therapeutic target.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> is one of the <strong>most common solid cancers</strong> among <strong>children</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4169","text":"A 60-year-old man presents​ with a painless loss of ‍vision. Fundus examinatio‍n reveals the lesion show‌n in the image. Which of ‌the following GTP-binding‍ proteins show point muta‍tions in this condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with a painless loss of vision. Fundus examination reveals the lesion shown in the image. Which of the following GTP-binding proteins show point mutations in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NR​AS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"KR​AS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GN​AQ","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GN​AS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GNAQ","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f137121a79504df7a8c4b29e84c86a8e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GNAQ</strong></p><p>The given scenario and fundus examination are suggestive of <strong>uveal melanoma </strong>which is associated with a point mutation of <strong>GNAQ</strong> protein or GNA11 genes.</p>\n<p>Uveal melanomas (UM) originate from melanocytes in the interior wall of the eye. This includes the iris, ciliary body, and the choroid with marked differences in light exposure (from dark anterior to illuminated posterior).</p>\n<p><strong>GNAS</strong> mutation causes <strong>pituitary adenoma</strong> and other <strong>endocrine tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> 3 RAS genes</strong> in humans (<em><strong>H-RAS</strong></em>, <strong><em>K-RAS</em>,</strong> and <strong><em>N-RAS</em></strong>) are the most common oncogenes in human cancer. They code for a family of proteins called<strong> GTP-binding (G) proteins,</strong> which are involved in transmitting signals within cells (cellular signal transduction). When RAS is 'switched on' by incoming signals, it subsequently switches on other proteins, which ultimately results in cell growth, differentiation, and survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Mutations</strong> in <strong>RAS</strong> genes can lead to the production of <strong>permanently activated</strong> RAS proteins. As a result, this can cause unintended and <strong>overactive</strong> <strong>signaling</strong> inside the cell, even in the absence of incoming signals. Because these signals result in cell growth and division, overactive RAS signaling can ultimately lead to <strong>cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4173","text":"Which of the following are​ coded by the MYC genes?","raw_text":"Which of the following are coded by the MYC genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell cycle​ regulators","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear regulatory protein​s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Signal transduction protei​ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Growth fact​or receptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nuclear regulatory proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nuclear regulatory proteins</strong></p><p><strong>MYC oncogene </strong>codes for <strong>nuclear regulatory proteins</strong>. It is the most commonly involved<strong> transcription factor </strong>in tumors.</p>\n<p>The MYC proto-oncogene MYC activates the expression of many genes that are involved in cell growth. It <strong>upregulates</strong> the expression of <strong>telomerase</strong> and reprograms somatic cells into pluripotent stem cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4176","text":"Which of the following is ​associated with the trans‍location of cyclin D1?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with the translocation of cyclin D1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-Hodgkin​'s lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem​ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mantle cel​l lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mantle cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mantle cell lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Translocation </strong>of<strong> cyclin D1</strong> is seen in <strong>mantle cell lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Mantle cell lymphoma is an uncommon lymphoid neoplasm that is characterized by <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">overexpression of cyclin D1. It is a type of non Hodgkins lymphoma that involves</span> <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">(11;14) translocation </span></strong>i.e. between<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> <strong>IgH locus</strong> on </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">chromosome 14</span></strong> and the<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> cyclin D1</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span>locus on <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">chromosome 11.</span></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4180","text":"Which of the following is ​false about Knudson's two‍-hit hypothesis theory on‍ retinoblastoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about Knudson's two-hit hypothesis theory on retinoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Familial type is inherited​ as an autosomal recessiv‍e trait","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It involves both alleles o​f RB at chromosome locus ‍13q14","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In sporadic cases, both no​rmal RB alleles must unde‍rgo somatic mutation in t‍he same retinoblast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In familial cases, childre​n inherit one defective c‍opy of the RB gene and th‍e other copy is normal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Familial type is inherited as an autosomal recessive trait","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d3bea1f169a41bba92e0c8d3aeb9c75x1217x526.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Familial type is inherited as an autosomal recessive trait</strong></p><p><strong>Retinoblastoma</strong> is inherited as an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> trait.</p>\n<p>Knudson's hypothesis, also known as the <strong>two-hit hypothesis</strong> or multiple-hit hypothesis states that <strong>cancer </strong>is the result of accumulated<strong> mutations </strong>in a cell's<strong> DNA. </strong>Two mutations (hits), involving both alleles of RB at <strong>chromosome locus 13q14</strong>, are required to produce retinoblastoma.</p>\n<p>In <strong>familial cases</strong>, children inherit one defective copy of the RB gene in the germline (the <strong>first hit</strong>), and the other copy is normal. Retinoblastoma develops when the normal RB allele is mutated in retinoblasts as a result of a spontaneous somatic mutation (the <strong>second hit</strong>). Most individuals inheriting a germline defect in one RB allele develop unilateral or bilateral retinoblastoma as it is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Sporadic cases</strong> are comparitively rare. In sporadic cases, both normal RB alleles must undergo somatic mutation in the same retinoblast (<strong>two hits</strong>).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the Knudson's two-hit hypothesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9447","text":"If the RB gene is hypophos​phorylated, which of the ‍following will happen?","raw_text":"If the RB gene is hypophosphorylated, which of the following will happen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell cycle will stop at G2​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cell cycle will stop at G1​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"There will be no effect on​ cell cycle as RB gene ph‍osphorylation is not need‍ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The cell cycle progresses ​and cell divides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cell cycle will stop at G1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/167150cafdc746c092057263a6861018x1280x837.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cell cycle will stop at G1</strong></p><p>If the retinoblastoma (<strong>RB</strong>) gene is <strong>hypophosphorylated</strong>, it is <strong>active</strong> and the cell cycle will<strong> stop </strong>at<strong> G1</strong>.</p>\n<p>RB is a<strong> cell-cycle regulator</strong> because it binds to and inactivates a transcription factor (<strong>E2F</strong>). This is necessary for the transcription of certain genes necessary for the progression from the G1 to S phase.<strong> </strong>The<strong> phosphorylation </strong>of<strong> RB</strong> results in the release of E2F. This activates gene transcription and cell-cycle<strong> progression </strong>occurs.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the cell cycle regulation by RB gene.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4182","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about p5‍3?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about p53?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It regulates cellular sene​scence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P53 inactivation causes ce​ll cycle arrest at G2-M","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cancers demonstrate bialle​lic loss of function muta‍tions in TP53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p53 can be inactivated by ​E6 protein of HPV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. P53 inactivation causes cell cycle arrest at G2-M","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. P53 inactivation causes cell cycle arrest at G2-M</strong></p><p><strong>P53</strong> is the<strong> tumor suppressor gene</strong> and <strong>activation</strong> (not inactivation) causes cell cycle arrest at the <strong>G1-S </strong>and<strong> G2-M</strong> phase of the cell cycle.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>P53 regulates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cell cycle progression</li>\n<li>DNA repair</li>\n<li><strong>Cellular senescence</strong></li>\n<li>Apoptosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is activated by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anoxia</li>\n<li>Inappropriate signaling by mutated oncoproteins</li>\n<li>DNA damage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is the<strong> most frequently </strong>mutated gene in<strong> cancers.</strong> It upregulates the expression of proteins such as the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor p21, thereby causing cell-cycle arrest at the <strong>G1-S.</strong> It also<strong> transactivates </strong>and<strong> transrepresses </strong>target gene to induce<strong> G2/M arrest. </strong>Mutation in the p53 gene causes <strong>resistance </strong>in tumors to <strong>radiation </strong>and<strong> chemotherapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>E6</strong> protein of human papillomavirus (<strong>HPV</strong>) binds to and helps in the <strong>degradation</strong> of <strong>p53</strong> and stimulates the expression of telomerase reverse transcriptase (<strong>TERT</strong>), which is the catalytic subunit of telomerase, thereby helping in the <strong>longevity</strong> of the cells. E6 from high-risk HPV types has a <strong>higher affinity</strong> for p53 than E6 from low-risk types. </p>\n<p><strong>Li-Fraumeni syndrome</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>disorder due to a defect in the tumor suppressor gene <strong>P53</strong> located on <strong>chromosome 17.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4183","text":"Which of the following is ​essential for the differe‍ntiation of stratified sq‍uamous epithelia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is essential for the differentiation of stratified squamous epithelia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"p​53","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"p​63","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"p53 a​nd p63","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p63 a​nd p73","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. p63","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. p63</strong></p><p><strong>p63</strong> is essential for the <strong>differentiation </strong>of <strong>stratified squamous epithelia</strong>.</p>\n<p>p53 is ubiquitously expressed, while p63 and p73 show more tissue specificity.</p>\n<p><strong>p73</strong> has strong <strong>pro-apoptotic</strong> effects after DNA damage induced by chemotherapeutic agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4184","text":"A 22-year-old man was foun​d to have multiple GI pol‍yps. He was operated on f‍or thyroid cancer 2 years‌ back. His family history‌ is significant for breas‍t cancer in his mother an‍d endometrial cancer in h​is aunt. Germline loss-of‍-function mutations of wh​ich gene is associated wi‌th this condition?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man was found to have multiple GI polyps. He was operated on for thyroid cancer  2 years back. His family history is significant for breast cancer in his mother and endometrial cancer in his aunt. Germline loss-of-function mutations of which gene is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CDK​N2A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SM​AD2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​PC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PT​EN","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PTEN","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PTEN</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 306\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> Cowden syndrome</strong>. This condition is caused due to germline loss of function mutation in <strong>PTEN</strong> (phosphatase and tensin homolog) gene.</p>\n<p>PTEN is a membrane-associated phosphatase encoded by a gene on chromosome <strong>10q23</strong> that is mutated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Cowden syndrome is characterized by skin appendage tumors and an increased incidence of epithelial cancers, particularly of the <strong>breast, endometrium, </strong>and<strong> thyroid. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4186","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen​ted with hearing loss and‍ tinnitus. MRI was done a‍nd showed the presence of‌ bilateral acoustic schwa‌nnomas. Which protein inv‍olved in cytoskeletal sta‍bility is mutated in this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presented with hearing loss and tinnitus. MRI was done and showed the presence of bilateral acoustic schwannomas. Which protein involved in cytoskeletal stability is mutated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurof​ibromin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mer​lin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"PAT​CHED","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RB pr​otein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Merlin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Merlin</strong></p><p>The given scenario and MRI findings are suggestive of<strong> neurofibromatosis type 2</strong> (NF-2). <strong>Merlin</strong> protein is mutated in NF-2, sporadic schwannomas, and meningiomas.</p>\n<p>Cells lacking Merlin do not establish stable cell-to-cell junctions. They are insensitive to normal growth arrest signals generated by cell-to-cell contact.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4187","text":"Germline loss-of-function ​mutation of which gene ca‍uses Gorlin syndrome?","raw_text":"Germline loss-of-function mutation of which gene causes Gorlin syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ST​K11","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SD​HB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"W​T1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PT​CH1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PTCH1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PTCH1</strong></p><p>Germline loss-of-function mutations of the<strong> PTCH1 </strong>gene cause<strong> Gorlin syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Gorlin syndrome is an <strong>autosomal-dominant</strong> disorder. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple basal cell carcinomas</li>\n<li>Highly characteristic facies - large forehead</li>\n<li>Bifid or misshapen ribs</li>\n<li>Vertebral and other skeletal anomalies</li>\n<li>Pits of the skin of the palms and soles</li>\n<li>Dysgenesis of the corpus callosum</li>\n<li>Calcification of the falx cerebri - at an earlier age than is seen in non‐affected individuals</li>\n<li>Macrocephaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acquired biallelic loss-of-function mutations of <em>PTCH1</em> cause sporadic basal cell carcinomas and medulloblastoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4188","text":"A 25-year-old man patient ​presents with anemia and ‍abdominal pain. On examin‍ation, he has pigmented m‌acules over the palms, so‌les, and oral mucosa. Fur‍ther investigations revea‍l loss-of-function mutati​ons of the STK11 gene. Wh‍at is the most probable d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man patient presents with anemia and abdominal pain. On examination, he has pigmented macules over the palms, soles, and oral mucosa. Further investigations reveal loss-of-function mutations of the STK11 gene. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HN​PCC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gorlin ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"von Hipple-Lindau syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peutz-Jeghe​rs syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Peutz–Jeghers syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Loss-of-function mutations of <strong>STK11</strong> cause the<strong> </strong>Peutz–Jeghers syndrome<strong>. </strong>The STK22 gene, also known as<strong> <em>LKB1</em>, </strong>encodes a serine/threonine kinase that is an important regulator of cellular metabolism.</p>\n<p>Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is an <strong>autosomal-dominant</strong> disorder. It is associated with arborizing <strong>polyps</strong> of the <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong> (small intestine &gt; colon &gt; stomach). It increases the risk of multiple epithelial cancers, particularly of colon, breast, lung, pancreas, and thyroid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4190","text":"A 38-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of a p‍alpable mass in both brea‍sts. Histopathology exami‌nation shows the followin‌g finding. Immunohistoche‍mistry showed estrogen re‍ceptor positivity. Which ​protein that maintains ce‍ll adhesion, and thereby ​inhibits cell motility, i‌s mutated in this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman presents with complaints of a palpable mass in both breasts. Histopathology examination shows the following finding. Immunohistochemistry showed estrogen receptor positivity. Which protein that maintains cell adhesion, and thereby inhibits cell motility, is mutated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"p​63","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"p​53","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E Cad​herin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inte​grin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. E Cadherin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95450a6982aa44c19681836bd4b26dfd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/052debaf560e4004827f06fa2e7c875dx1279x1253.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. E Cadherin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>lobular carcinoma</strong> of the<strong> breast</strong> which involves mutated<strong> E-cadherin</strong> protein. In this condition, the histopathological examination shows infiltrating small monomorphic discohesive tumor cells arranged in an <strong>Indian-file </strong>or <strong>single-file pattern.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows the single file pattern seen in lobular carcinoma of the breast.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Reduced cell surface expression of <strong>E</strong><strong>-cadherin</strong> is seen in carcinomas of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast</li>\n<li>Esophagus</li>\n<li>Colon</li>\n<li>Ovary</li>\n<li>Prostate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>E-cadherin is a cell-surface protein that maintains intercellular <strong>adhesiveness</strong>. Loss of E-cadherin causes <strong>disaggregation</strong> of cells, which can then invade locally or <strong>metastasize.</strong></p>\n<p>Germline loss-of-function mutations of the E-cadherin gene lead to <strong>familial gastric carcinoma. </strong>Sporadic gastric carcinomas are also associated with the loss of E-cadherin expression.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4191","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding the B‍RCA genes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the BRCA genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BRCA1 is located on the lo​ng arm of chromosome 17","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BRCA1 mutation causes well​-differentiated cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BRCA2 carcinomas are ER/PR​- positive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BRCA 2 mutation is associa​ted with male breast carc‍inoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BRCA1 mutation causes well-differentiated cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BRCA1 mutation causes well-differentiated cancer</strong></p><p><strong>BRCA2</strong> (not BRCA 1) mutation causes <strong>well-differentiated</strong> cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4193","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding apopt‍osis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding apoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"p53 induces apoptosis in c​ells with DNA damage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extrinsic apoptotic pathwa​y is more frequently disa‍bled in cancer cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chemoradiation induces apo​ptosis in cancer cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BCL2 is over-expressed in ​follicular lymphomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Extrinsic apoptotic pathway is more frequently disabled in cancer cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Extrinsic apoptotic pathway is more frequently disabled in cancer cells</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 310\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>intrinsic </strong>apoptotic pathway is most frequently <strong>disabled</strong> in <strong>cancer cells</strong> to evade cell death. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>p53</strong> is an important pro-apoptotic protein that induces apoptosis in cells that are unable to repair DNA damage.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> and <strong>radiation</strong> therapy kills cancer cells mainly by inducing apoptosis via the intrinsic pathway.</p>\n<p>In greater than 85% of follicular B-cell lymphomas, the anti-apoptotic gene <em><strong>BCL2</strong> </em>is overexpressed due to a (14;18) translocation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4195","text":"Which of the following gen​es promote epithelial-mes‍enchymal transition in ep‍ithelial tumors? MYC SNAI‌L TWIST BRCA1 RET","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following genes promote epithelial-mesenchymal transition in epithelial tumors?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>MYC</li>\n<li>SNAIL</li>\n<li>TWIST</li>\n<li>BRCA1</li>\n<li>RET</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 an​d 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3, an​d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 and ​5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ​3 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 3 only</strong></p><p><strong>SNAIL </strong>and<strong> TWIST,</strong> which encode transcription factors, promote epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (<strong>EMT</strong>).</p>\n<p>In EMT, carcinoma cells <strong>downregulate </strong>certain epithelial markers (e.g.,<strong> E-cadherin</strong>) and<strong> upregulate </strong>certain mesenchymal markers (e.g.<strong> vimentin </strong>and<strong> smooth muscle actin</strong>). This favors the development of a promigratory phenotype that is essential for <strong>metastasis</strong>. EMT has been documented mainly in<strong> breast carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4291","text":"Which of the following is ​a non-receptor tyrosine k‍inase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a non-receptor tyrosine kinase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​BL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"R​ET","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"K​IT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A​LK","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ABL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ABL</strong></p><p><strong>ABL </strong>is a<strong> non-receptor tyrosine kinase.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tyrosine kinases</strong> may be receptors and non-receptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Nonreceptor </strong>tyrosine kinases normally localize to the cytoplasm or the nucleus. These are:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li><strong>ABL</strong> - translocation causes CML and point mutation causes ALL</li>\n<li><strong>JAK</strong> mutation - seen in polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia, and myelofibrosis as well as other myeloproliferative disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Receptor </strong>tyrosine kinases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>VEGFR</li>\n<li>PDGFR</li>\n<li>EGFR</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In chronic myelogenous leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>) and some acute lymphoblastic leukemia (<strong>ALL</strong>), the <em>ABL</em> gene is translocated from its normal abode on chromosome 9 to chromosome 22 where it fuses with the <em>BCR</em> gene forming the <strong>Philadelphia chromosome</strong>. The resultant chimeric gene <strong>encodes</strong> a constitutively active, <strong>oncogenic</strong> BCR-ABL<strong> tyrosine kinase</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0153","text":"The mutations/changes that​ convert proto-oncogene t‍o oncogene include all ex‍cept:","raw_text":"The mutations/changes that convert proto-oncogene to oncogene include all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Promoter ​insertion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enhancer​ deletion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gene ampl​ification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Point m​utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enhancer deletion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enhancer deletion</strong></p><p>Enhancer deletion does not convert a proto-oncogene to an oncogene.</p>\n<p>Mechanisms of activating oncogenes:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 469px; width: 552px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 382px;\"><strong>Activation of oncogenes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Mutation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 382px;\">Mutation activates the gene. A classic example is the point mutation of the RAS oncogene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Promoter insertion </td>\n<td style=\"width: 382px;\">Insertion of a viral promoter region near a gene activates it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Enhancer insertion </td>\n<td style=\"width: 382px;\">Insertion of a viral enhancer region near a gene activates it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Chromosomal translocation </td>\n<td style=\"width: 382px;\">One chromosome is split off and joined to another. An example is the Philadelphia chromosome.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Gene amplification  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 382px;\">Abnormal multiplication of a gene resulting in many copies. This can occur with oncogenes and genes involved in tumor drug resistance. </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2232","text":"Mutation in which of the f​ollowing oncogenes can se‍rve as a tumor antigen?","raw_text":"Mutation in which of the following oncogenes can serve as a tumor antigen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EB​NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MU​C-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p​53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"R​AS","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. RAS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. RAS</strong></p><p><strong>RAS</strong> is an <strong>oncogene</strong> that undergoes mutation and serves as a <strong>tumor antigen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tumor cells express antigenic molecules on their surface that elicit an immune response. These are called tumor antigens. Some tumor cells evade immune response which results in growth of the tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2233","text":"Which of the following mec​hanisms does not lead to ‍immune evasion by the tum‍or cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following mechanisms does not lead to immune evasion by the tumor cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of PD-1 recepto​r on T-cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Upregulation of expression​ of PD-L1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Induction ​of galactin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Induction of regulatory T-​cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition of PD-1 receptor on T-cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition of PD-1 receptor on T-cells</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibition </strong>of<strong> PD-1 receptor </strong>(programmed death -1 receptor) on T cells causes activation of <strong>immunity</strong> against the <strong>tumor</strong> cells, not evasion.</p>\n<p>PD-1 receptors present on T cells inhibit T-cell activation. This allows tumors to<strong> evade </strong>the<strong> host immunity</strong> by the <strong>upregulation</strong> of the expression of factors like <strong>PD-L1 </strong>and<strong> PD-L2,</strong> which act on PD-1 receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4053","text":"An 8-year-old girl is refe​rred with recurrent blist‍ering rashes over the fac‍e and upper limbs which w‌orsened following exposur‌e to sunlight. There is a‍ history of similar skin ‍lesions in her family and​ 2 of her uncles were dia‍gnosed with skin cancers ​at young ages. Examinatio‌n reveals the presence of​ multiple lentigos and xe‌rosis. What is the likely‌ pathological mechanism o​f this condition?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old girl is referred with recurrent blistering rashes over the face and upper limbs which worsened following exposure to sunlight. There is a history of similar skin lesions in her family and 2 of her uncles were diagnosed with skin cancers at young ages. Examination reveals the presence of multiple lentigos and xerosis. What is the likely pathological mechanism of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defective base excision re​pair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defective DNA nucleotide e​xcision repair","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Defective mi​smatch repair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defective non-homologous e​nd joining repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Defective DNA nucleotide excision repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/beaea49c0bb54865872a7c519217de3ex720x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Defective DNA nucleotide excision repair</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>xeroderma pigmentosum. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is caused by a <strong>defect</strong> in the <strong>DNA</strong> <strong>nucleotide excision repair</strong> pathway resulting in inability to repair<strong> pyrimidine dimers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of xeroderma pigmentosum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Actinic keratosis</li>\n<li>Hyperkeratotic lesions</li>\n<li>Multiple lentigos</li>\n<li>Xerosis</li>\n<li>Corneal scarring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image of a child with xeroderma pigmentosum is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4054","text":"Which of the following dis​orders is not associated ‍with defective homologous‍ recombination DNA repair‌ mechanism?","raw_text":"Which of the following disorders is not associated with defective homologous recombination DNA repair mechanism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Familial br​east cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fanconi​ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fanconi ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ataxia-tel​engeictasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fanconi syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fanconi syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Fanconi syndrome</strong> is not associated with defective homologous recombination DNA repair mechanism. It is a rare disorder of <strong>kidney tubule function.</strong></p>\n<p>Diseases with<strong> defects </strong>in<strong> DNA repair</strong> by <strong>homologous recombination:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bloom syndrome</strong> and <strong>ataxia–telangiectasia </strong>triggered by DNA-damaging agents such as ionizing radiation</li>\n<li><strong>Fanconi anemia </strong>is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in multiple genes responsible for coding for proteins that participate in DNA repair pathways and are triggered by DNA-crosslinking agents like chemotherapeutic drugs.</li>\n<li><strong>Familial breast cancer</strong> is due to inherited gene defects in BRCA1 and BRCA 2 genes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4055","text":"A 30-year-old patient pres​ents with right iliac fos‍sa pain. Imaging shows th‍e presence of an ileocaec‌al mass. Biopsy of the ma‌ss shows a starry sky pat‍tern. What is the translo‍cation involved in this t​umor?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient presents with right iliac fossa pain. Imaging shows the presence of an ileocaecal mass. Biopsy of the mass shows a starry sky pattern. What is the translocation involved in this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(8​;14)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"t(15​;17)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"t(9​;22)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(11​;14)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. t(8;14)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fab582207ff84dc0b37d9d26eb4bd57bx754x560.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. t(8;14)</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and biopsy findings are suggestive of<strong> Burkitt lymphoma </strong>and the translocation involved is<strong> t(8;14)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Burkitt lymphoma</strong>, also known as <strong>small non-cleaved-cell lymphoma</strong>, is a highly aggressive <strong>B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma.</strong> It is characterized by the translocation and deregulation of the <strong>c-myc gene</strong> on <strong>chromosome 8. </strong></p>\n<p>The classic t(8;14)(q24;q32) <strong>reciprocal translocation</strong> results in the transposition of the <strong>c-myc proto-oncogene </strong>on chromosome 8 with one of the immunoglobulin heavy chain (<strong>IGH</strong>) genes on chromosome 14. This results in <strong>activation</strong> of the <strong>c-myc</strong> gene and is considered responsible for tumor proliferation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4056","text":"Which epigenomic regulator​y gene is mutated in most‍ of the follicular lympho‍mas?","raw_text":"Which epigenomic regulatory gene is mutated in most of the follicular lymphomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ML​L1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ML​L2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ARI​D1A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SN​F5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MLL2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MLL2</strong></p><p><strong>MLL2 </strong>is an epigenomic regulatory gene that is mutated in most <strong>follicular lymphomas</strong>. It results in <strong>histone methylation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Epigenetics </strong>refers to <strong>factors</strong> other than the sequence of DNA that <strong>regulate gene expression</strong>. These factors involved in epigenetics are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Histone modifications</li>\n<li>DNA methylation</li>\n<li>Nucleosome positioning/ chromatin remodeling</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4059","text":"The loss of which of the f​ollowing micro RNAs leads‍ to upregulation of BCL-2‍?","raw_text":"The loss of which of the following micro RNAs leads to upregulation of BCL-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"miR​-200","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"miR​-15","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"miR​-155","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"miR​-25","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. miR-15","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. miR-15</strong></p><p>The <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>miR-15</strong> leads to upregulation of the <strong>anti-apoptotic</strong> protein <strong>BCL-2</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>miR-15</strong> and <strong>miR-16</strong> are <strong>tumor-suppressive</strong> miRNAs involved in chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Onco-miRNAs</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>miR200 - </strong>promotes <strong>epithelial-mesenchymal transitions </strong>that promote tumor invasion and metastasis</li>\n<li><strong>miR155 - </strong>involved in<strong> </strong>human <strong>B-cell lymphomas</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6771","text":"Identify the correct state​ment regarding immune sur‍veillance.","raw_text":"Identify the correct statement regarding immune surveillance.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti CTLA 4 Ab is a new mo​dality in the treatment o‍f advanced cancers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mutation of tumor supresso​r genes decreases antigen‍ toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PD1/ PDL1 activation leadi​ng to tumor supression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binding of CD28 leads to i​mmune tolerance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti CTLA 4 Ab is a new modality in the treatment of advanced cancers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti CTLA 4 Ab is a new modality in the treatment of advanced cancers</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Anti-CTLA-4 antibody ipilimumab</strong> was the <strong>first drug</strong> to achieve a significant <strong>improvement</strong> in<strong> survival</strong> of<strong> advanced-stage melanoma</strong>. Cytotoxic T lymphocyte antigen 4 (CTLA-4) plays an important inhibitory role in the control of T-cell activation.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Ipilimumab is a fully-humanized <strong>IgG1 monoclonal antibody</strong> that inhibits cytotoxic T lymphocyte antigen-4 (CTLA-4).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">It has been postulated that anti-CTLA-4 antibodies act by blocking inhibitory signals from effector T-cells, allowing costimulatory signaling and generation of antitumor T-cell responses. In vitro studies demonstrated also that the antitumor activity of CTLA-4 blockade is mediated by Fc gamma receptor-expressing macrophage and hypothesized a possible role of regulatory T-cell depletion in the tumor microenvironment.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Option B - Mutation of tumor supressor genes is associated with increased risk of cancer. Antigen toxicity is when the immune response to an antigen is excessive or misdirected, causing damage to tissues or organs.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Option C - <strong>PD1/PDL1</strong> is an immune checkpoint whose <strong>activation inhibits T-cell function</strong> and helps with <strong>immune tolerance</strong>. Blocking PD-1/PD-L1 interaction using immune checkpoint inhibitors reactivates T cells and enhances immune responses against tumors.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Option D - <strong>CD28</strong> is primarily involved in providing the <strong>second signal</strong> required for <strong>T cell activation</strong>. The first signal comes from the interaction between the T cell receptor (TCR) and the antigen presented by the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) on antigen-presenting cells (APCs). <strong>Without</strong> CD28-mediated costimulation, T cells that only receive the TCR signal (without the second signal) can become<strong> anergic</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"AmY4Khz9","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"12_antihypertensive_drugs","title":"12.Antihypertensive Drugs","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA2085","text":"Which of the following is ​the most appropriate firs‍t-line medication for an ​elderly patient with hype‌rtension?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line medication for an elderly patient with hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrochlo​rothiazide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Furos​emide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorth​alidone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spirono​lactone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chlorthalidone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chlorthalidone</strong></p><p>The most appropriate first-line antihypertensive among the given drugs is <strong>chlorthalidone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thiazide diuretics are recommended as one of the first-line antihypertensive drugs for elderly patients without any comorbidities. Among them, guidelines recommend chlorthalidone over hydrochlorothiazide<strong> </strong>because of its <strong>longer duration of action</strong>. </p>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>First-choice drugs </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">used in the treatment of hypertension in patients without any comorbidities include:</span></div>\n<ul class=\"listtype-bullet listindent1 list-bullet1\">\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>ACE inhibitors or ARBs: </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">No evidence of superiority of one class over the other. However, ACE inhibitors have a higher risk of side effects like dry cough and angioedema.</span></li>\n<li>\n<div><strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong><strong>:</strong> Extended-release, long-acting dihydropyridines are<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> preferred.</span> </div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>:</strong> Chlorthalidone is usually preferred to hydrochlorothiazide.</span></div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Loop diuretics </strong>like furosemide and torsemide are <strong>less effective</strong> antihypertensives than thiazides. They are not indicated for the routine management of hypertension.</p>\n</div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">In the presence of any associated conditions or comorbidities, the first choice drugs for the treatment of hypertension would be as follows:</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>\n<table class=\"table-top-header\" style=\"height: 279px;\" width=\"646\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"ace-all-bold-hthree\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Comorbidities</strong></span></span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"ace-all-bold-hthree\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>First choice drugs for hypertension</strong></span></span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Diabetes</span></div>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Chronic kidney disease with proteinuria</span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">ACE inhibitors or ARBs</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Angina</span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Heart failure</span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">ACE inhibitors and alpha+beta blockers (e.g., carvedilol)</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Benign prostatic hyperplasia</span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">α</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">1 b</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">lockers</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Pregnancy</span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Labetalol</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.765625px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Atrial fibrillation</span></div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.25px;\">\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Diltiazem or verapamil</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2090","text":"Chlorthalidone is being pr​escribed to an elderly pa‍tient for the management ​of hypertension. Which of‌ the following side effec​ts would not be expected ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"Chlorthalidone is being prescribed to an elderly patient for the management of hypertension. Which of the following side effects would not be expected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperu​ricemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperg​lycemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Thiazide</strong> diuretics cause<strong> hypokalemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other side effects of thiazide diuretics include <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>, <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>, <strong>hyperglycemia</strong> and erectile dysfunction. All thiazide diuretics cross the placenta and therefore should be <strong>avoided in pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0775","text":"A patient on lithium thera​py developed hypertension‍. He was started on thiaz​ides for hypertension. Af‌ter few days, he develope​d coarse tremors and othe‌r symptoms suggestive of ‌lithium toxicity. Explain‌ the likely mechanism of ​this interaction.","raw_text":"A patient on lithium therapy developed hypertension. He was started on thiazides for hypertension. After few days, he developed coarse tremors and other symptoms suggestive of lithium toxicity. Explain the likely mechanism of this interaction.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiazide inhibits the meta​bolism of lithium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiazides act as an add on​ drug to lithium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiazides increase the tub​ular reabsorption of lith‍ium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thiazides cause water loss​ therby increase lithium ‍levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thiazides increase the tubular reabsorption of lithium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thiazides increase the tubular reabsorption of lithium</strong></p><p><strong>Thiazides </strong>act by<strong> increasing</strong> the<strong> tubular rabesorption of lithium.</strong></p>\n<p>Thiazide use is accompanied by <strong>sodium loss</strong> which is <strong>compensated</strong> in a few days by increased <strong>reabsorption of Na<sup>+</sup></strong> in the proximal tubule.</p>\n<p>The kidney handles both lithium and Na<sup>+ </sup>in the same way. Thus, along with Na<sup>+ </sup>reabsorption, there will be an increase in the <strong>reabsorption of lithium</strong> also, resulting in decreased excretion of lithium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2154","text":"A hypertensive patient on ​losartan requires an add ‍on drug to achieve his bl​ood pressure goal. Which ‌of the following drugs wi​ll not increase the risk ‌of hyperkalemia when adde‌d to his current medicati‌on?","raw_text":"A hypertensive patient on losartan requires an add on drug to achieve his blood pressure goal. Which of the following drugs will not increase the risk of hyperkalemia when added to his current medication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aten​olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amil​oride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indap​amide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alis​kiren","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Indapamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Indapamide</strong></p><p><strong>Indapamide</strong> is a<strong> thiazide </strong>diuretic that<strong> increases K<sup>+</sup> excretion</strong> decreases the risk of hyperkalemia associated with ACE inhibitors or ARBs such as losartan. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Beta-blockers</strong> can contribute to K<sup>+</sup> retention by <strong>decreasing renin</strong> release and further <strong>reducing aldosterone</strong> secretion. Non-selective beta-blockers may also interfere with the cellular uptake of K<sup>+</sup> ions.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>K<sup>+</sup> sparing diuretics</strong> like amiloride cause a decrease in K<sup>+</sup> excretion and increase the risk of <strong>hyperkalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Aliskiren is a<strong> direct renin inhibitor</strong> and increases the risk of hyperkalemia when combined with losartan.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2086","text":"Long term reduction of blo​od pressure by thiazide d‍iuretics is mainly by whi​ch of the following mecha‌nisms?","raw_text":"Long term reduction of blood pressure by thiazide diuretics is mainly by which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction in extracellular​ volume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction in sympathetic a​ctivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduction in peripheral va​scular resistance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in extracellular ​volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reduction in peripheral vascular resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reduction in peripheral vascular resistance</strong></p><p><strong>Long term</strong> reduction of blood pressure by <strong>thiazide</strong> diuretics is mainly due to a <strong>reduction in peripheral vascular resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>Thiazide diuretics <strong>initially decrease extracellular volume</strong> by their action on NaCl transporter in distal convoluted tubules in the nephrons. However, the <strong>compensatory response</strong> from activation of RAAS <strong>raises the extracellular volume</strong> returns almost to pre-treatment levels after a few weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2142","text":"A diabetic patient is diag​nosed with hypertension d‍uring his annual health c​heck-up. What is the trea‌tment of choice for him?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient is diagnosed with hypertension during his annual health check-up. What is the treatment of choice for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorth​alidone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carve​dilol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capt​opril","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nifed​ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Captopril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Captopril</strong></p><p><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong>such as captopril<strong> or ARBs</strong> can be considered as a <strong>treatment of choice</strong> in<strong> hypertensive patients with diabetes </strong>because of the renoprotective action. It benefits diabetic patients by <strong>reducing</strong> the <strong>risk</strong> of occurrence and progression of diabetic <strong>nephropathy</strong>. </p>\n<p>They are also recommended in patients with CKD irrespective of diabetes status as they can delay the progression of nephropathy.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Thiazide</strong> diuretics <strong>worsen</strong> glycemic control and are not preferred in diabetic patients.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Beta-blockers,</strong> especially non-selective blockers, <strong>worsen</strong> glycemic control.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong> have a <strong>neutral</strong> effect on glycemic control and can be used in diabetes if ACE inhibitors or ARBs cannot be given.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2081","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit​h hypertension during rou‍tine health evaluation. W​hich of the following is ‌not recommended as first-​line therapy for him?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with hypertension during routine health evaluation. Which of the following is not recommended as first-line therapy for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vera​pamil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxa​zosin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metol​azone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quin​april","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doxazosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doxazosin</strong></p><p>Alpha blockers like doxazosin are not recommended as first-line therapy for uncomplicated essential hypertension.</p>\n<p>As per the European Society of Cardiology 2018 guidelines for <strong>hypertension</strong>, the classes of drugs recommended as <strong>first line therapy</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACE inhibitors</li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor blockers</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blockers</li>\n<li>Thiazide diuretics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2106","text":"Which class of antihyperte​nsive drugs has a favoura‍ble effect on lipid profi​le?","raw_text":"Which class of antihypertensive drugs has a favourable effect on lipid profile?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiazide ​diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta b​lockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha 1 selective blockers​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium chan​nel blockers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alpha 1 selective blockers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alpha 1 selective blockers</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha 1 selective blockers </strong>have a favourable effect on lipid profile. They <strong>decrease</strong> plasma concentrations of <strong>triglycerides</strong>, total <strong>LDL</strong>-cholesterol and <strong>increase HDL</strong> cholesterol.</p>\n<p>Option A: Thiazides increase plasma concentrations of triglycerides, LDL-cholesterol and total cholesterol. HDL may decrease or remain unchanged.</p>\n<p>Option B: Non-selective beta-blockers increase triglycerides and decrease HDL levels. However, total cholesterol remains unchanged.</p>\n<p>Option D: Calcium channel blockers have a neutral effect on the lipid profile.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2113","text":"Methyldopa is primarily us​ed for which of the follo‍wing conditions?","raw_text":"Methyldopa is primarily used for which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertension​ in pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renovascular​ hypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertensio​n with gout","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Refractory ​hypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertension in pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertension in pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Methyldopa</strong> is preferred for <strong>chronic hypertension in pregnancy</strong> based on its safety and efficacy data for both mother and fetus.</p>\n<p>Antihypertensives found <strong>safe</strong> <strong>in pregnancy</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Methyldopa</li>\n<li>Dihydropyridine CCBs like nifedipine, amlodipine - however, they should be stopped before labor as they weaken the uterine contractions</li>\n<li>Cardioselective beta-blockers and those with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antihypertensives to be <strong>avoided</strong> <strong>in pregnancy</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACE inhibitors, Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARB)</li>\n<li>Diuretics</li>\n<li>Nonselective beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Nitroprusside </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Labetalol</strong> is considered the <strong>first-line </strong>agent for <strong>hypertensive emergency </strong>in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2110","text":"A pregnant woman with hype​rtension, on treatment wi‍th methyldopa, comes for ​routine evaluation. She i‌s found to have a positiv​e Coombs test but is asym‌ptomatic. What is the nex‌t course of action for th‌is patient?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with hypertension, on treatment with methyldopa, comes for routine evaluation. She is found to have a positive Coombs test but is asymptomatic. What is the next course of action for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediately stop methyldop​a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Taper the dose of methyldo​pa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monitor for signs of hemol​ysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Switch to​ labetalol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Monitor for signs of hemolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Monitor for signs of hemolysis</strong></p><p>The best course of action for the patient is <strong>close monitoring</strong> for any signs or symptoms of <strong>hemolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Coombs test is done to test the presence of antibodies against Rh antigens on the red blood cells, which result in hemolysis. Positive Coombs test is seen in 20% of patients who receive methyldopa.</p>\n<p>However, only 1-5% of such patients develop hemolytic anemia and hence, a positive Coombs test is not a necessary indication for stopping methyldopa treatment. They must be monitored closely and any <strong>signs </strong>that indicate<strong> hemolytic anemia</strong> needs prompt <strong>discontinuation of the drug.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2117","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about clonidine?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about clonidine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction in central sympa​thetic flow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prazosin completely antago​nizes its action","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sedation and xerostomia ar​e common side effects","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be combined with va​sodilators","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prazosin completely antagonizes its action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prazosin completely antagonizes its action</strong></p><p>Prazosin does not antagonize the effect of clonidine. <strong>Prazosin</strong> is a selective<strong> α1 receptor antagonist </strong>whereas<strong> clonidine</strong> is an <strong>alpha 2 receptor partial agonist. </strong></p>\n<p>On <strong>oral</strong> administration, clonidine stimulates <strong>alpha 2A receptors</strong> in CNS and results in decreased central sympathetic flow. This causes a <strong>decrease in BP</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, on<strong> IV</strong> administration <strong>postsynaptic alpha 2B receptors</strong> are activated at higher doses which cause a<strong> transient rise in blood pressure. </strong>It also causes significant reductions in HDL lipids, apolipoproteins AI and AII.</p>\n<p>It can be combined with vasodilators but has to be used with caution as potentiation of each other's actions can occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2126","text":"A patient presents with co​mplaints of multiple epis‍odes of intermittent palp​itations, throbbing heada‌che, and sweating. Blood ​pressure measured during ‌one of these episodes is ‌210/120 mm Hg. The result‌s of 24-hour urinary meta​nephrines were inconclusi‌ve. Which of the followin‌g could be used for diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of multiple episodes of intermittent palpitations, throbbing headache, and sweating. Blood pressure measured during one of these episodes is 210/120 mm Hg. The results of 24-hour urinary metanephrines were inconclusive. Which of the following could be used for diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phento​lamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoxyb​enzamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clon​idine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Praz​osin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clonidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clonidine</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette<strong> </strong>is suggestive of <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. When first-line investigations like measurement of 24-hour urinary metanephrines are inconclusive, <strong>clonidine suppression test </strong>is used in the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>An <strong>oral</strong> dose of <strong>0.3 mg</strong> clonidine is given, followed by the measurement of norepinephrine (NE) concentration at 3 hours. A concentration of &gt;500 pg/mL of NE indicates a positive test.</p>\n<p>Option A: Phentolamine test is not recommended due to its low sensitivity and specificity.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: <strong>Phenoxybenzamine</strong> and <strong>prazosin</strong> are used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of pheochromocytoma but have no role in its diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2123","text":"Which of the following is ​a selective imidazoline r‍eceptor agonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a selective imidazoline receptor agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clon​idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methy​ldopa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moxon​idine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Brimo​nidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moxonidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moxonidine</strong></p><p><strong>Moxonidine </strong>is a selective<strong> imidazoline </strong>(I<sub>1</sub>) receptor<strong> agonist.</strong></p>\n<p>It is indicated as a second line agent in the long-term management of <strong>hypertension</strong>. It also has weak alpha 2 agonist action.</p>\n<p><strong>Rilmenidine</strong> is another selective imidazoline receptor agonist.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Clonidine</strong> is an alpha 2 receptor partial agonist and has additional imidazoline (I<sub>1</sub>) receptor agonist action. But the <strong>main</strong> mode of <strong>action</strong> is through <strong>alpha 2 agonism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Methyldopa does not have any action on imidazoline receptors</p>\n<p>Option D: Brimonidine is a congener of clonidine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4403","text":"Which of the following ant​ihypertensives are not us‍ed in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following antihypertensives are not used in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha me​thyldopa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydra​lazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propr​anolol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Labe​talol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Propranolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Propranolol</strong></p><p>Propranolol is a non-selective beta-blocker and is not safe for use during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Antihypertensives to be <strong>avoided</strong> in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACE inhibitors, ARBS</li>\n<li>Diuretics</li>\n<li>Nonselective beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Nitroprusside</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antihypertensives found to be <strong>safe</strong> in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Methyldopa</li>\n<li>Dihydropyridine CCBs: should be stopped before labor as they weaken the uterine contractions</li>\n<li>Cardioselective beta-blockers and those with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity: may be used as a last choice</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2094","text":"Which of the following dru​gs reduces blood pressure‍ primarily by directly de​creasing the heart rate?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs reduces blood pressure primarily by directly decreasing the heart rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr​anolol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Praz​osin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha me​thyl dopa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitropruss​ide sodium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propranolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propranolol</strong></p><p><strong>Propranolol</strong> reduces blood pressure by <strong>directly decreasing</strong> the<strong> heart rate</strong>. It is a <strong>nonselective beta-blocker</strong> and blocks the beta 1 receptor of the heart (SA node) thus reducing the heart rate and blood pressure.</p>\n<p>It also <strong>reduces renin secretion</strong> by blocking beta 1 receptors in JG cells. Reduction in renin release also contributes to blood pressure reduction.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Prazosin </strong>is a <strong>selective alpha 1 blocker</strong> and dilates both arteries and veins and may cause reflex tachycardia.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Alpha methyldopa</strong> is a selective <strong>alpha 2 receptor</strong> agonist. Alpha 2 receptors are autoreceptors present on the presynaptic membrane. On stimulation, central sympathetic discharge is reduced. Alpha methyldopa can indirectly reduce heart rate.</p>\n<p>Option D: Nitroprusside is a vasodilator and may cause reflex tachycardia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2132","text":"The blood pressure of a pa​tient undergoing a corona‍ry artery bypass surgery ​suddenly rises to 170/110‌mm Hg. Which of the follo​wing can you administer i‌ntravenously for immediat‌e management?","raw_text":"The blood pressure of a patient undergoing a coronary artery bypass surgery suddenly rises to 170/110mm Hg. Which of the following can you administer intravenously for immediate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nifed​ipine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amlod​ipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clevi​dipine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Felod​ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clevidipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clevidipine</strong></p><p><strong>Clevidipine </strong>is a dihydropyridine available for<strong> intravenous administration </strong>and is indicated in the acute management of hypertension. It has a short half-life (1-2 minutes) and a rapid onset of action.</p>\n<p>Option A: Nifedipine is a short-acting dihydropyridine that is no longer indicated in the management of hypertension. The rapid reduction in blood pressure and concurrent surges in sympathetic activity are not desirable. </p>\n<p>Options B and D: Amlodipine and felodipine are longer-acting dihydropyridines that are available in tablet forms. They are used for long-term treatment of hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2144","text":"An elderly hypertensive pa​tient is diagnosed with b‍ilateral renal artery ste​nosis. He is also on trea‌tment for diabetes mellit​us. Which among the follo‌wing drugs would be the m‌ost appropriate for him?","raw_text":"An elderly hypertensive patient is diagnosed with bilateral renal artery stenosis. He is also on treatment for diabetes mellitus. Which among the following drugs would be the most appropriate for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enal​april","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vera​pamil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metop​rolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metol​azone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Verapamil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Verapamil</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>verapamil</strong> would be the most appropriate drug for the management of hypertension in the patient as it has<strong> no</strong> <strong>adverse effect</strong> on <strong>glycemic</strong> <strong>control</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Efferent arteriolar constriction maintains the glomerular filtration pressure in bilateral renal artery stenosis. <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in bilateral renal stenosis as it can precipitate acute renal failure due to dilation of efferent arterioles and fall in glomerular filtration pressure.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: <strong>Beta-blockers and thiazides</strong> can <strong>worsen glycemic control</strong> and are not suitable in the given scenario.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2160","text":"Which of the following is ​a direct renin inhibitor?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is a direct renin inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capt​opril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sacub​itril","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alis​kiren","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Losa​rtan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aliskiren","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aliskiren</strong></p><p><strong>Aliskiren </strong>is a direct renin inhibitor. Though its efficacy as an antihypertensive is comparable to that of ACE inhibitors, it is not considered a first-line agent due to higher incidence of side effects.</p>\n<p>Option A: Captopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option B: Sacubitril is a neprilysin inhibitor indicated in combination with valsartan in chronic heart failure patients.</p>\n<p>Option D: Losartan is an angiotensin receptor blocker.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2168","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect abou‍t diazoxide?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about diazoxide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts by causing prolong​ed opening of ATP depende‍nt K+ channels in beta ce​lls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause severe hypogl​ycaemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can be used to treat pa​tients with insulinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is used as an antihyper​tensive agent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can cause severe hypoglycaemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can cause severe hypoglycaemia</strong></p><p>Diazoxide causes <strong>hyperglycemia</strong>, not hypoglycemia.</p>\n<p>Diazoxide is a <strong>potent K<sup>+</sup> channel opener</strong> and opens up the ATP-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> channel and thus <strong>prevents depolarization</strong> of pancreatic beta cells and subsequent <strong>insulin release. </strong>It is used in some cases of insulinoma to reduce insulin secretion and prevent hypoglycemia.</p>\n<p>It was earlier used as an intravenous agent for hypertensive emergencies due to its strong vasodilatory action.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2171","text":"A pregnant woman with pree​clampsia and is started o‍n hydralazine. Which of t​he following statements i‌s incorrect about the dru​g?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with preeclampsia and is started on hydralazine. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It decreases cerebral vasc​ular resistance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It causes equal dilatation​ of both arteries and vei‍ns","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Postural hypotension is no​t a common problem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It increases plasma renin ​activity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It causes equal dilatation of both arteries and veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It causes equal dilatation of both arteries and veins</strong></p><p><strong>Hydralazine </strong>causes<strong> preferential dilatation of arteries</strong> over veins.</p>\n<p>Hydralazine acts directly on vascular smooth muscles. The precise mechanism is unknown but involves a <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>intracellular Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> concentrations.</p>\n<p>Option A: It causes a selective decrease in vascular resistance in coronary, cerebral and renal circulation, with a smaller effect in skin and muscle. </p>\n<p>Option C: Because of preferential dilatation of arterioles over veins, postural hypotension is not a common problem.</p>\n<p>Option D: Hydralazine-induced vasodilation causes reflex activation of the <strong>sympathetic</strong> system through <strong>baroreceptor-mediated</strong> reflexes resulting in <strong>increased plasma renin </strong>activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2173","text":"The efficacy of an antihyp​ertensive agent is being ‍studied in a clinical tri​al. During the course of ‌the trial, the researcher​s observed hair regrowth ‌and generalized hypertric‌hosis in balding patients‌. What is the mechanism o​f action of the drug bein‌g studied?","raw_text":"The efficacy of an antihypertensive agent is being studied in a clinical trial. During the course of the trial, the researchers observed hair regrowth and generalized hypertrichosis in balding patients. What is the mechanism of action of the drug being studied?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closes K+ channels in vasc​ular smooth muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Activates K+ channels in v​ascular smooth muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Activates Ca2+ channels in​ vascular smooth muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Closes Ca2+ channels in va​scular smooth muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Activates K+ channels in vascular smooth muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Activates K+ channels in vascular smooth muscle</strong></p><p>The antihypertensive drug with the side effect of excess hair growth is <strong>minoxidil</strong> and it acts by<strong> activating ATP-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> channels </strong>in smooth muscles. This causes <strong>hyperpolarization</strong> that results in the <strong>relaxation </strong>of <strong>smooth muscles of</strong> <strong>arterioles.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Topical minoxidil</strong> is also used for the treatment of <strong>androgenetic alopecia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0791","text":"A patient presented to the​ ER with severe headache ‍and vomiting. On examinat​ion, the blood pressure w‌as found to be 220/130 mm​ Hg and papilledema was a‌lso seen. What is the mec‌hanism of the drug of cho‌ice for the condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the ER with severe headache and vomiting. On examination, the blood pressure was found to be 220/130 mm Hg and papilledema was also seen. What is the mechanism of the drug of choice for the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increas​ed cAMP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium chan​nel blockage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulation of guanylyl cy​clase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"K+ chann​el opener","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcium channel blockage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcium channel blockage</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>severe hypertension</strong> (blood pressure <span class=\"nowrap\">≥180/≥120</span> mmHg) with fundoscopic changes (flame haemorrhages and/or <strong>papilloedema</strong>) is suggestive of <strong>malignant hypertension.</strong> The drug of choice is <strong>IV nicardipine</strong> which acts by <strong>calcium channel blockage. </strong></p>\n<p>Nicardipine blocks the influx of extracellular calcium across the vascular and myocardial smooth muscle cell membranes by blocking the voltage-sensitive calcium channels during depolarization. The decrease in intracellular calcium causes dilation of the coronary and systemic arteries. </p>\n<p>Other preferred agents for malignant hypertension are <strong>clevidipine</strong> with <strong>labetalol </strong>or<strong> esmolol.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Sodium nitroprusside is no longer the treatment of choice for acute hypertensive problems</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2183","text":"A patient with malignant h​ypertension is being mana‍ged with sodium nitroprus​side infusion. He is at r‌isk of developing all of ​the following adverse eff‌ects except?","raw_text":"A patient with malignant hypertension is being managed with sodium nitroprusside infusion. He is at risk of developing all of the following adverse effects except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methhemog​lobinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyanide ​toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drug-induced lupus syndrom​e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thiocyanat​e toxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Drug-induced lupus syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Drug-induced lupus syndrome</strong></p><p>Drug-induced lupus is not an adverse effect of sodium nitroprusside infusion.</p>\n<p>When <strong>nitroprusside</strong> is administered at a high rate, it can result in the <strong>production</strong> of <strong>methemoglobin</strong> and <strong>cyanide</strong>. Cyanide is further metabolized to <strong>thiocyanate</strong>. On prolonged infusion, thiocyanate toxicity can also occur especially in patients with renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0780","text":"A patient with gout develo​ps hypertension. The uric‍ acid level was found to ​be elevated. Which of the‌ following is not adminis​tered?","raw_text":"A  patient with gout develops hypertension.  The uric acid level was found to be elevated. Which of the following is not administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrochlo​rothiazide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aten​olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enal​april","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Praz​osin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hydrochlorothiazide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hydrochlorothiazide</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazides</strong> are not given to this patient.</p>\n<p>Gout is a metabolic disease associated with hyperuricemia.Thiazides increase direct urate reabsorption in the proximal renal tubules and are associated with elevated serum uric acid levels and may precipitate gout. Hence, hydrochlorothiazides are not used to treat hypertension in gout patients.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2097","text":"You will counsel patients ​regarding first-dose phen‍omenon while prescribing ​which of the following dr‌ugs?","raw_text":"You will counsel patients regarding first-dose phenomenon while prescribing which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr​anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Praz​osin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metop​rolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydra​lazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prazosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prazosin</strong></p><p><strong>First-dose phenomenon </strong>is characteristic of <strong>prazosin </strong>and patients must be counselled regarding the effects.</p>\n<p>Within 30-90 minutes of the initial dose of prazosin or when the dosage is increased, there is marked <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong> or syncope. This is known as the first-dose phenomenon. After a few initial doses, <strong>tolerance</strong> develops to this side effect. Initiating low doses at bedtime is recommended to minimize this phenomenon.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6536","text":"Which of the following dru​gs can be given safely in‍ a patient with chronic r​enal failure?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can be given safely in a patient with chronic renal failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aluminium​ hydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cande​sartan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telmi​sartan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sucra​lfate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Telmisartan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Telmisartan</strong></p><p><strong>Telmisartan</strong> can be given <strong>safe</strong>ly in a patient with chronic <strong>renal failure</strong> (CRF) because it is mainly cleared from circulation by <strong>biliary excretion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: Sucralfate is a basic aluminum salt of sucrose sulfate. Sucralfate, aluminum hydroxide, and magnesium hydroxide in CRF patients can cause the accumulation of Al<sup>3+</sup> and Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions as they are preferentially excreted via the kidney after systemic absorption. Absorbed Al<sup>3+</sup> can contribute to osteoporosis, encephalopathy and proximal myopathy in CRF patients.</p>\n<p>Option B: Candesartan is cleared from circulation by renal and biliary excretion. Hence, plasma clearance is affected by renal insufficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"btklcoQ4","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"12_somatosensory_pathways","title":"12.Somatosensory Pathways","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB0109","text":"The principle that “ In sp​inal cords, dorsal roots ​are sensory and ventral r​oots are motor” is known ​as ______","raw_text":"The principle that “ In spinal cords, dorsal roots are sensory and ventral roots are motor” is known as ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Law of p​rojection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bell – Ma​gendie law","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Labelled li​ne principle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weber – F​echner law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bell – Magendie law","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bell – Magendie law</strong></p><p>The principle that in the spinal cord the dorsal roots are sensory and the ventral roots are motor is known as the <strong>Bell–Magendie law.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Laws in Sensory Physiology</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Muller's doctrine of specific nerve energies</strong>:<strong> </strong>When the nerve pathways from a particular sense organ are stimulated, the sensation evoked is that for which the receptor is specialized, no matter how or where along the pathway the activity is initiated.</li>\n<li><strong>Law of projection</strong>:<strong> </strong>No matter where a particular sensory pathway is stimulated along its course to the cortex, the conscious sensation produced is referred to the location of the receptor.</li>\n<li><strong>Weber-Fechner Law</strong>:<strong> </strong>Magnitude of sensation felt is directly proportional to the <strong>log of the intensity of the stimulus</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Labelled line theory: </strong>All the sensations from the different parts of the body travel along specified paths. For example, in the posterior column, fibers from the lower parts of the body are placed medially.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0108","text":"A patient admitted to the ​ward does not feel a sens​ation of the heat on touc​hing a hot object. Consid​ering the signal reaches ‍the thalamus, which of th‍e following is not transm‌itting the signal to an i​ntact postcentral gyrus?","raw_text":"A patient admitted to the ward does not feel a sensation of the heat on touching a hot object. Considering the signal reaches the thalamus, which of the following is not transmitting the signal to an intact postcentral gyrus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary​ neuron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rece​ptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondar​y neuron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiar​y neuron","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tertiary neuron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/321f13f183c447aca0ae502c20d075aex1280x1299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tertiary neuron</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the<strong> tertiary neuron does not transmit</strong> the signal to the postcentral gyrus.</p>\n<p>In the thalamus, secondary sensory neurons synapse onto tertiary sensory neurons, which in turn project to the somatosensory region of the cerebral cortex.</p>\n<p>The question tests your knowledge of this pathway. It is said that the signal reaches the thalamus and the postcentral gyrus is intact. Still, the person cannot perceive the sensation, so the pathway is broken between the thalamus and the gyrus, which has to be at the tertiary neuron.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0111","text":"Decussation of ascending s​pinal pathways occurs mai​nly within the______","raw_text":"Decussation of ascending spinal pathways occurs mainly within the______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midbrain and medulla oblon​gata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pons and medulla oblongata​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal cord and medulla ob​longata","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spinal c​ord only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spinal cord and medulla oblongata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b20f230faa744b9be1d488c2bbb3a43x1280x1310.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spinal cord and medulla oblongata</strong></p><p>Decussation of ascending spinal pathways occurs mainly within the spinal cord and medulla oblongata. Most of the ascending pathways and tracts do decussate or cross; however, some remain uncrossed.</p>\n<p><strong>Dorsal column pathway</strong> crosses in the <strong>medulla oblongata.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>anterior and lateral spinothalamic tract</strong> crosses obliquely in the <strong>spinal segments. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0112","text":"Conscious proprioception i​s carried by_____.","raw_text":"Conscious proprioception is carried by_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal col​umn fibers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior spinothalmic trac​t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral spinothalmic tract​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibul​ar tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal column fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1dedb03c3b754cba879929cc03557b4dx1280x1310.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal column fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Conscious</strong> proprioception is carried by <strong>dorsal column</strong>. <strong>Unconscious</strong> proprioception is carried by <strong>spinocerebellar tract</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B and C: Anterolateral system carries pain, temperature, crude touch. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0118","text":"Which of the following is ​not a property of fibers ​in the dorsal column path​way?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a property of fibers in the dorsal column pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large axon​al diameter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal column carries fine​ touch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid conduc​tion velocity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unmyel​inated","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Unmyelinated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Unmyelinated</strong></p><p>The dorsal column–medial lemniscal system is composed of <strong>large</strong>, <strong>myelinated nerve</strong> fibers and thus transmit signals to the brain, at <strong>high velocities</strong> of 30 to 110 m/sec.</p>\n<p>Option B: Dorsal column carries fine touch and anterolateral column carries crude touch</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0162","text":"A patient comes to you for​ his annual health checku​p. He lost his vision due​ to an accident a few yea​rs ago. He now experience‍s the world through touch‍. Which of the following ‌Brodmann’s areas processe​s this tactile stimulatio​n?","raw_text":"A patient comes to you for his annual health checkup. He lost his vision due to an accident a few years ago. He now experiences the world through touch. Which of the following Brodmann’s areas processes this tactile stimulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3, ​1, 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4,​ 6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"44,​ 45","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"41,​ 42","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3, 1, 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3, 1, 2</strong></p><p>Processing of <strong>tactile stimulation</strong> occurs in <strong>Brodmann’s area</strong> in <strong>3, 1, 2.</strong></p>\n<p>The human cerebral cortex is divided into about 50 distinct areas called <strong>Brodmann’s areas</strong> based on histological structural differences.</p>\n<p>Areas 3, 1, 2 which constitute <strong>primary somatosensory area I,</strong> and areas 5 and 7, which constitute the <strong>somatosensory association area </strong>process sensory information like tactile stimulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0169","text":"Cortical representation of​ body in the cerebrum is_​______","raw_text":"Cortical representation of body in the cerebrum is_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horiz​ontal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vert​ical","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sagg​ital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vertical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c612a02a3d741e681302eb0b08390b9x1024x1020.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vertical</strong></p><p>Cortical representation of body in the cerebrum is vertical. </p>\n<p>Parts of the body are represented in order along the postcentral gyrus, with the legs on top and head at the bottom of the gyrus.</p>\n<p>A <strong>cortical homunculus</strong> is a distorted representation of the human body, based on a neurological map of the areas and proportions of the human brain dedicated to processing motor functions, or sensory functions, for different parts of the body. The size of the cortical receiving area for impulses from a particular part of the body is proportionate to the use of the part. </p>\n<p><strong>Sensory Homunculus </strong>is a topographical representation of distribution of sensory input to the somatosensory cortex from different parts of the body.</p>\n<p><strong>Motor homunculus </strong>is a topographical representation of distribution of motor output from primary motor cortex to different parts of the body.</p>\n<p>Sensory homunculus is depicted below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0173","text":"Somatosensory area has the​ largest representation f​or_____","raw_text":"Somatosensory area has the largest representation for_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li​ps","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ha​nd","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Th​umb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ba​ck","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lips","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/520e4c916e924ac2a1fead79c21d6670x1024x1020.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lips</strong></p><p>Somatosensory area has the largest representation for lips. </p>\n<p>The relative sizes of the cortical receiving areas of the sensory homunculus are<strong> proportionate to the use of the part. </strong>The sizes of these areas are <strong>directly proportional to the number of specialized sensory receptors</strong> in each respective peripheral area of the body.</p>\n<p>Some body parts are represented by large areas in the somatic cortex—the <strong>lips the greatest</strong> of all, followed by the<strong> face and thumb</strong>,<strong> </strong>whereas the <strong>trunk and lower part of the body</strong> are represented by relatively <strong>small areas.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0176","text":"A patient sustains an inju​ry to the lateral portion​ of the postcentral gyrus​ in the right hemisphere.​ Loss of sensation is lik‍ely to be seen at which o‍f the following sites in ‌this patient?","raw_text":"A patient sustains an injury to the lateral portion of the postcentral gyrus in the right hemisphere. Loss of sensation is likely to be seen at which of the following sites in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left lo​wer limb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right lo​wer limb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left fac​ial area","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right fa​cial area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left facial area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97f9a8cb2b3249b4999964a582955ec5x1024x1020.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left facial area</strong></p><p>In this case, injury to the <strong>lateral portion</strong> of the <strong>postcentral gyrus</strong> in the <strong>right hemisphere</strong> would result in loss of sensation in the <strong>left facial area.</strong></p>\n<p>Each hemisphere receives somatic sensory information from the <strong>opposite side</strong> of the body in the <strong>postcentral gyrus </strong>(somatosensory area).</p>\n<p>The <strong>lateral portion</strong> of the postcentral gyrus receives somatic sensory information from the <strong>facial</strong> and<strong> neck region</strong> while the <strong>medial portion</strong> of the postcentral gyrus received somatic sensory information from the <strong>trunk</strong> and<strong> appendages</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0177","text":"Lesion of somatosensory ar​ea I affects _______","raw_text":"Lesion of somatosensory area I affects _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pa​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Locali​zation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tempe​rature","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Localization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Localization</strong></p><p>Lesion of the somatosensory area I affects the localization of sensations.</p>\n<p>Bilateral lesion of the somatosensory area I leads to the following impairment of sensory judgment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inability to <strong>localize different sensations</strong> in different parts of the body</li>\n<li>Inability to judge<strong> degrees of pressure</strong> against the body</li>\n<li>Inability to judge<strong> the weight</strong> of the objects</li>\n<li>Inability to judge shapes of forms of objects - <strong>astereognosis</strong></li>\n<li>Inability to judge the <strong>texture</strong> of the materials</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pain and temperature are not affected both in quality and intensity in the lesion of the somatosensory area I. However, they are poorly localised. <strong>Pain and temperature localization</strong> depends greatly on the <strong>topographical map of the body in somatosensory area I</strong> to localize the source.`</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0181","text":"A man was rushed to the ca​sualty after getting stab​bed in the back with a kn​ife. On MRI, a complete t​ransection of one side of‍ the spinal cord is suspe‍cted. Which of the follow‌ing is likely to be seen ​in this patient?","raw_text":"A man was rushed to the casualty after getting stabbed in the back with a knife. On MRI, a complete transection of one side of the spinal cord is suspected. Which of the following is likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain and temperature loss ​on the opposite side","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fine touch lost on the opp​osite side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pain and temperature lost ​on the same side","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only pain lost on the same​ side","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pain and temperature loss on the opposite side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pain and temperature loss on the opposite side</strong></p><p>This patient most likely has <strong>Brown-Sequard syndrome. </strong>It is characterized by <strong>loss of pain and temperature</strong> on the<strong> opposite side.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hemisection of the spinal</strong> cord causes damage to ascending sensory (dorsal column pathway, ventrolateral spinothalamic tract) and descending motor (corticospinal tract) pathways, which is called the <strong>Brown-Séquard syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>The lesion to fasciculus gracilus or fasciculus cuneatus leads to ipsilateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception below the level of the lesion.</p>\n<p>The loss of the <strong>spinothalamic tract</strong> leads to <strong>contra­lateral loss</strong> of pain and temperature sensation beginning <strong>one or two segments below</strong> the lesion.</p>\n<p>Damage to the <strong>cor­ticospinal tract</strong> produces <strong>weakness and spasticity in certain muscle groups</strong> on the <strong>same side</strong> of the body.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9691","text":"Which of the following fin​dings are seen in a patie​nt with Brown-Sequard syn​drome?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings are seen in a patient with Brown-Sequard syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper Motor Neuron type of​ paralysis below the lesi​on on the opposite side","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Impaired fine touch below ​the lesion on the opposit​e side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of crude touch below ​the lesion on the same si​de","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of vibration sense be​low the lesion on the sam​e side","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Loss of vibration sense below the lesion on the same side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Loss of vibration sense below the lesion on the same side</strong></p><p>A patient with Brown Sequard syndrome will have a loss of vibration sense below the lesion on the same side.</p>\n<p><strong>Brown-sequard syndrome </strong>is caused due to hemisection of the Spinal cord. It can be caused by fracture-dislocation of the vertebral column, by a bullet or stab wound, or by an expanding tumor.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pyramidal tract:</strong> Pyramidal weakness (<strong>ipsilateral upper motor neuron type of paralysis</strong>) below the level of the lesion on<strong> the same side as the lesion </strong>(pyramidal tract crosses in the medulla).</li>\n<li><strong>Dorsal columns:</strong> Impaired fine touch, 2 point discrimination, proprioception,<strong> vibration</strong> below the level of lesion on <strong>the same side of lesion </strong>(dorsal column fibres cross in the medulla).</li>\n<li><strong>Spinothalamic tract:</strong> Loss of pain, temperature, <strong>crude touch</strong>, pressure sensation below the level of the lesion<strong> on the contralateral side of the lesion</strong> (spinothalamic tract crosses midline after entering the cord).</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0190","text":"Itch is carr​ied by______","raw_text":"Itch is carried by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal​ column","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinocereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterolate​ral system","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corticosp​inal tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterolateral system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterolateral system</strong></p><p>Itch-specific fibers have been demonstrated in the anterolateral spinothalamic tract. They are carried by unmyelinated nerve fiber C. Itching can be produced by repeated local mechanical stimulation of skin, variety of chemical agents including histamine and kinins released in the skin in response to tissue damage</p>\n<p>Simple scratching relieves itching because it activates large fast conducting afferents (Aβ) that gate the transmission in the dorsal horn in a manner analogous to inhibition of pain by stimulation of similar afferents <strong>(Gate control theory)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0185","text":"Following are the endogeno​us opioids except_____","raw_text":"Following are the endogenous opioids except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endo​rphin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enkep​halin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dyno​rphin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dopamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dopamine</strong></p><p><strong>Endogenous opioids</strong>, a group of neuropeptides that includes <strong>beta-endorphin</strong>,<strong> </strong><strong>dynorphins, </strong>and <strong>enkephalins.</strong></p>\n<p>Opioids cause both presynaptic and post synaptic inhibition reducing excitatory post-synaptic potential (EPSP). Opioids are released from the descending analgesic pathway and bind with opiate receptors on the afferent pain-fiber terminal. This binding suppresses the release of substance P, blocking further transmission of the pain signal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0186","text":"You are examining a patien​t for a follow-up of seve​re pain in his right leg.​ He tells you that he fee​ls much better now that h‍e is taking acupuncture t‍herapy too. Which of the ‌following is responsible ​for pain relief in acupun​cture?","raw_text":"You are examining a patient for a follow-up of severe pain in his right leg. He tells you that he feels much better now that he is taking acupuncture therapy too. Which of the following is responsible for pain relief in acupuncture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subst​ance P","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endo​rphin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endorphin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endorphin</strong></p><p><strong>Pain relief</strong> in <strong>acupuncture</strong> is by the release of <strong>endorphin.</strong> </p>\n<p>The acupuncture endorphin hypothesis is the primary mechanism of <strong>acupuncture analgesia. </strong>According to this hypothesis, Acupuncture is thought to activate afferent neurons leading to spinal cord and midbrain centers that release endogenous opioids implicated in pain relief.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0182","text":"Fast pain is carried by___​____","raw_text":"Fast pain is carried by_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neo spinoth​alamic tract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Paleo spinot​halamic tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dorsal​ column","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neo spinothalamic tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neo spinothalamic tract</strong></p><p>Fast pain is carried by neospinothalamic tract.  </p>\n<p>Pain signals take two pathways to the brain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neospinothalmic tract for fast pain/epicritic pain (Aδ fibres)</li>\n<li>Paleospinothalamic tract for slow pain/Protopathic pain (C fibres)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0188","text":"Soldiers wounded in the he​at of battle often feel n​o pain until the battle i​s over which is an exampl​e of stress-induced analg‍esia. This is due to_____‍_","raw_text":"Soldiers wounded in the heat of battle often feel no pain until the battle is over which is an example of stress-induced analgesia. This is due to______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subst​ance P","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cannab​inoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cannabinoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cannabinoids</strong></p><p>Stress induced analgesia is due to cannabinoids. </p>\n<p><strong>The stress-induced analgesia</strong>, i.e., reduced pain sensitivity during stressful events might be due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Release of endogenous cannabinoids such as<strong> 2-arachidonoylglycerol</strong> (2-AG) and<strong> anandamide</strong></li>\n<li>Release of Norepinephrine from brainstem catecholaminergic neurons in the amygdala. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9726","text":"Which of the following doe​s not carry proprioceptiv​e impulses?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not carry proprioceptive impulses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olivo cereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tecto cereb​ellar tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spino cereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cuneo cereb​ellar tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tecto cerebellar tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tecto cerebellar tract</strong></p><p>Tectocerebellar tract does not carry proprioceptive impluses. Tectocerebellar tract carry <strong>visual and auditory</strong> impulses.</p>\n<p><strong>Spinocerebellar, olivo cerebellar, cuneo cerebellar tracts carry </strong>Proprioceptive impulses. </p>\n<p><strong>Vestibulocerebellar</strong> tract carry vestibular impulses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9724","text":"Pain and temperature in th​e thalamus are controlled​ by which of the followin​g nuclei?","raw_text":"Pain and temperature in the thalamus are controlled by which of the following nuclei?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventral posterior lateral ​nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventral posterior medial n​ucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior​ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intralamin​ar nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ventral posterior lateral nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9cced92d16114d9dae2af3a2fcd8a1cdx1280x1308.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ventral posterior lateral nucleus</strong></p><p>Pain and temperature in thalamus are controlled by the ventral posterior lateral nucleus. Pain and temperature is carried by anterolateral spinothalamic tract.</p>\n<p>Fibres from <strong>anterolateral spinothalamic tract </strong>and dorsal column synapse in the <strong>ventral posteriorlateral (VPL) nucleus of thalamus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The pathway from <strong>taste receptors</strong> includes synapses in the nucleus of the <strong>tractus solitarius</strong> in the brainstem and <strong>ventral posterior medial nucleus</strong> in the thalamus.   </p>\n<p>Option C: Anterior nucleus functions as a relay station for the hippocampal impulses. It receives <strong>afferents from mammillothalamic tract</strong>. It sends <strong>efferents to cingulate gyrus. </strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Intralaminar nucleus</strong> participates in arousal reaction, it recieves <strong>afferents from reticular formation</strong> of brain stem. <br/>   </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0189","text":"Ventrolateral cordotomy fo​r relief of pain in the r​ight lower limb is being ​performed on a patient. W​hich of the following is ‍cut in this procedure?","raw_text":"Ventrolateral cordotomy for relief of pain in the right lower limb is being performed on a patient. Which of the following is cut in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ventral spinothalamic​ tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left lateral spinothalamic​ tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right ventral spinothalami​c tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right lateral spinothalami​c tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left lateral spinothalamic tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left lateral spinothalamic tract</strong></p><p>In <strong>ventrolateral cordotomy,</strong> selected pain-conducting tracts in the spinal cord, i.e. fibers in the <strong>lateral spinothalamic pathway,</strong> are <strong>cut</strong> leading to contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation below the level of the lesion.</p>\n<p>In the more recent percutaneous approach,<strong> destruction of</strong> the<strong> lateral spinothalamic at</strong> the level of<strong> C1-C2</strong> results in a <strong>contralateral disruption</strong> of painful sensations beyond C4.  </p>\n<p>Note: The fibers carrying <strong>pain</strong> sensation ascend in the <strong>lateral spinothalamic tract</strong> while the fibers carrying crude touch and pressure sensations ascend in the anterior spinothalamic tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6866","text":"Pick the correct statement​:","raw_text":"Pick the correct statement:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal lemniscus carries f​ibres of dorsal spinothal​amic tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigeminal lemniscus carri​es pain and temperature s​ensations from the ipsila​teral orofacial region","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral lemniscus carries ​fibres from cochlear nucl​ei","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medial lemniscus carries f​ibres from of vestibular ​nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral lemniscus carries fibres from cochlear nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral lemniscus carries fibres from cochlear nuclei</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral</strong> lemniscus carries fibres from <strong>cochlear</strong> nuclei</p>\n<p><strong>Medial</strong> lemniscus carries fibres from of <strong>dorsal</strong> <strong>column</strong> nuclei</p>\n<p><strong>Spinal</strong> lemniscus carries fibres of <strong>anterolateral</strong> <strong>spinothalamic</strong> tract</p>\n<p><strong>Trigeminal</strong> lemniscus carries pain and temperature sensations from the <strong>contralateral</strong> <strong>orofacial</strong> region</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7399","text":"Lesion in the medial lemni​scus at the level of pons​ causes which of the foll​owing?","raw_text":"Lesion in the medial lemniscus at the level of pons causes which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain and temperature loss ​in the same side","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain and temperature loss ​in the opposite side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of tactile and propri​oception on the same side​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of tactile and propri​oception on the opposite ​side","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side</strong></p><p><strong>Lesion in the medial lemniscus at the level of pons causes loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side.</strong></p>\n<p>Fibers of medial lemniscus ascend ipsilaterally in the dorsal columns of the spinal cord to the medulla, where they synapse in the gracilus and cuneate nuclei.</p>\n<p>The second-order neurons from these nuclei cross the midline and ascend in the medial lemniscus to end in the contralateral ventral posterior lateral (VPL) nucleus and related specific sensory relay nuclei of the thalamus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0179","text":"Ablation of somatosensory ​area 1 leads to______","raw_text":"Ablation of somatosensory area 1 leads to______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total loss of pain sensati​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total loss of touch sensat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of tactile but not 2 ​point discrimination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of tactile and 2 poin​t discrimination","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Loss of tactile and 2 point discrimination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Loss of tactile and 2 point discrimination</strong></p><p>Ablation of somatosensory area 1 leads to <strong>Loss of tactile and 2 point discrimination.</strong></p>\n<p>Widespread bilateral excision of the somatosensory area I causes loss of the following types of sensory judgment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The person is unable to localize discretely the different sensations in the different parts of the body. However, he or she can localize these sensations crudely, such as to a particular hand. The brain stem, thalamus, or parts of the cerebral cortex can perform some degree of localization.</li>\n<li>The person is unable to judge critical degrees of pressure/weight/texture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CA_j7pQO","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"12_bipolar_and_related_disorders","title":"12.Bipolar And Related Disorders","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA6687","text":"A man brings his wife to t​he psychiatric OPD, repor‌ting concerns about her r‌ecent behaviour. She comp​lains of irritability, di​stractibility, and reduce‍d need for sleep. Her hus‌band observes that she ha‌s an elated mood, talks e​xcessively, and struggles​ to manage her daily acti‌vities. What is the most ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man brings his wife to the psychiatric OPD, reporting concerns about her recent behaviour. She complains of irritability, distractibility, and reduced need for sleep. Her husband observes that she has an elated mood, talks excessively, and struggles to manage her daily activities. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depre​ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypo​mania","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ma​nia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mania","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mania</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of decreased sleep, elation, talkativeness, irritability, and distractibility present for <strong>at least 1 week</strong> is characteristic of <strong>mania</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria for mania:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A. Distinct period of abnormally and persistently <strong>elevated/irritable mood</strong>, abnormally and persistently <strong>increased activity/energy</strong>, lasting <strong>at least 1 week </strong>or any duration if hospital­ization is necessary.</p>\n<p>B. During the period of mood disturbance and increased energy/activity, three (or more) of the following symptoms and four (or more) if irritable have been present to a significant degree:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inflated self-esteem or <strong>grandiosity</strong></li>\n<li>Decreased need for sleep</li>\n<li>More talkative than usual</li>\n<li><strong>Flight of ideas</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Distractibility</strong></li>\n<li>Increase in goal-oriented activity or psychomotor agitation</li>\n<li>Excessive involvement in activities that have a high potential for painful consequences</li>\n</ol>\n<p>C. The episode is severe enough to cause marked social or occupational <strong>impairment</strong> or to necessitate <strong>hospitalization.</strong></p>\n<p>D. The episode is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication, other treatment) or to another medical condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0978","text":"A 20-year-old male present​s to the OPD with decreas‌ed sleep, elation and tal‌kativeness for the past 5​ days. He also complains ​of irritability and distr‍actibility. What is the d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old male presents to the OPD with decreased sleep, elation and talkativeness for the past 5 days. He also complains of irritability and distractibility. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ma​nia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anxiety ​disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypo​mania","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizo​phrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypomania","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypomania</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of symptoms of mania occurring for <strong>at least 4 days</strong> points towards a diagnosis of <strong>hypomania.</strong></p>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria for hypomania:</p>\n<p>A. Distinct period of abnormally and persistently <strong>elevated/irritable mood</strong>, abnormally and persistently <strong>increased activity/energy</strong>, lasting for <strong>at least 4 consecutive days</strong>.</p>\n<p>B.<strong> </strong>During the period of mood disturbance and increased energy and activity, three (or more) and if irritable, four (or more) of the following symptoms have been present to a significant degree:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inflated self-esteem or <strong>grandiosity</strong></li>\n<li>Decreased need for sleep</li>\n<li>More talkative than usual</li>\n<li><strong>Flight of ideas</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Distractibility</strong></li>\n<li>Increase in goal-oriented activity or psychomotor agitation</li>\n<li>Excessive involvement in activities that have a high potential for painful consequences (for example, foolish business investments).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>C. The episode is associated with an unequivocal change in functioning that is uncharac­teristic of the individual when not symptomatic and is observable by others. </p>\n<p>D.  The episode is <strong>not severe</strong> enough to cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning or to necessitate hospitalization. <strong>No psychotic episodes</strong>. </p>\n<p>E. The episode is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication, other treatment) or to another medical condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6692","text":"A patient with a previousl​y diagnosed psychiatric d‌isorder presented for a f‌ollow-up in the OPD. He h​ad mood swings fluctuatin​g between short periods o‍f mild depression and hyp‌omania over 3 years. What‌ is this disorder known a​s?","raw_text":"A patient with a previously diagnosed psychiatric disorder presented for a follow-up in the OPD. He had mood swings fluctuating between short periods of mild depression and hypomania over 3 years. What is this disorder known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclo​thymia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bipo​lar I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bipol​ar II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dyst​hymia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cyclothymia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cyclothymia</strong></p><p><strong>Cyclothymia </strong>is a chronic disorder characterized by at least <strong>2 years </strong>of fluctuating, <strong>mild</strong> hypomanic &amp; depressive <strong>symptoms</strong> (that do not meet the <strong>full criteria</strong> for hypomanic episodes or major depressive episodes). It is a symptomatically <strong>milder form </strong>of <strong>bipolar II disorder.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dysthymia </strong>(persistent depressive disorder) is characterized by at least 2 years of depressed mood that does not satisfy the criteria for a major depressive episode and with <strong>no episodes of mania or hypomania. </strong>In children, a diagnosis of dysthymia is made if depressive symptoms are present for more than 1 year. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6698","text":"As a first-year psychiatry​ resident, you see a pati‌ent diagnosed with bipola‌r type II disorder. Which​ of the following constit​utes this disorder?","raw_text":"As a first-year psychiatry resident, you see a patient diagnosed with bipolar type II disorder. Which of the following constitutes this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repeated episodes of mania​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mania + ​hypomania","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypomania + major depressi​on","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mania + majo​r depression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypomania + major depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypomania + major depression</strong></p><p>Episodes of <strong>hypomania and major depressive disorder</strong> constitute bipolar type II disorder.</p>\n<p>Types of bipolar disorder:</p>\n<p>Bipolar I: Episodes of <strong>mania with or without</strong> episodes of <strong>major depressive disorder</strong> (at least one-lifetime episode of mania is required for diagnosis)</p>\n<p>Bipolar II:<strong> </strong>Episodes of <strong>hypomania with</strong> episodes of <strong>major depressive disorder</strong> (negative history of a manic episode is required for diagnosis)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6701","text":"As an intern posted in psy​chiatry, you observe a pa‌tient with mania. Which o‌f the following is true a​bout neurotransmitters in​ this disorder?","raw_text":"As an intern posted in psychiatry, you observe a patient with mania. Which of the following is true about neurotransmitters in this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease i​n serotonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in norepinephrine​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in neuropeptide Y​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in norepinephrine​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increase in norepinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increase in norepinephrine</strong></p><p>In mania, there is an <strong>increase in norepinephrine and dopamine</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6929","text":"Which of the following sta​tement is not true regard‌ing the epidemiology of b‌ipolar disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is not true regarding the epidemiology of bipolar disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The lifetime prevalence of​ bipolar disorder is arou‌nd 1%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The average age of onset o​f bipolar I disorder is 3‌0 years","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prevalence is equal among ​both the sexes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevalence is higher in hi​gher socio-economic strat‌a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The average age of onset of bipolar I disorder is 30 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The average age of onset of bipolar I disorder is 30 years</strong></p><p>The <strong>average age of onset</strong> of <strong>bipolar I</strong> disorder<strong> </strong>is<strong> 20 years, </strong>and in more than half of the cases, the onset is frequently in late adolescence.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lifetime prevalence </strong>of bipolar disorder is<strong> 1.2% </strong>and prevalence is <strong>equal in both genders</strong> (unlike depression, which has a higher prevalence among females than males).</p>\n<p>The prevalence of bipolar disorder is higher in <strong>higher socio-economic strata. </strong>By contrast, schizophrenia has a higher prevalence in the lower socio-economic strata.</p>\n<p>Note: The mean age of onset of depression is 40 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6700","text":"A 40-year-old patient come​s to the OPD with a histo‌ry significant for a mood‌ disorder. Her moods can ​be charted as done in the​ image given below. What ‍is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient comes to the OPD with a history significant for a mood disorder. Her moods can be charted as done in the image given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclo​thymia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dyst​hymia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid cycli​ng disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypo​mania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rapid cycling disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ef2c052148a45efad412d06715a505b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rapid cycling disorder</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given image showing 3 episodes of mania and 2 episodes of depression <strong>within 1 year,</strong> points towards a diagnosis of <strong>rapid cycling bipolar disorder</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is diagnosed when the patient has a combination of <strong>4 or more</strong> <strong>alternating episodes</strong> of <strong>depression and mania/hypomania</strong> within <strong>one</strong> <strong>year</strong>. There must be less than 2 weeks of normalcy between the episodes or an immediate shift between the symptoms with no intervening normal mood.</p>\n<p>Risk factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female patients</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Alcoholism or substance abuse</li>\n<li>Antidepressants (if used without mood stabilizers)</li>\n<li>Cyclothymia</li>\n<li>Bipolar II disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: If the phases of mania and depression <strong>alternate</strong> very rapidly in a matter of<strong> hours or days </strong>then the condition is known as <strong>ultra-rapid cycling disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6678","text":"As a first-year psychiatry​ resident, you are consul‌ting on a young man with ‌depression. Which of the ​following would you not e​xpect to see in this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"As a first-year psychiatry resident, you are consulting on a young man with depression. Which of the following would you not expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delu​sions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flight ​of ideas","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Early morni​ng awakening","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halluc​ination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flight of ideas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flight of ideas</strong></p><p><strong>Flight of ideas</strong> is a <strong>sign of mania</strong> and is not seen in depression.</p>\n<p>Early morning awakening is also known as <strong>terminal insomnia</strong> and is seen in <strong>depression</strong>.</p>\n<p>Severely depressed patients sometimes present with mood-congruent <strong>delusions or hallucinations </strong>and are said to have a <strong>major depressive episode with psychotic features</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 363px; height: 402px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px; text-align: center;\">Mood congruent psychotic features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.1875px; text-align: center;\">Mood incongruent psychotic features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px;\">Consistent with the mood</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.1875px;\">Not consistent with the mood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px;\">Occurs in mood disorders with psychotic features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.1875px;\">Occurs in schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px;\">\n<p>E.g.,<strong> </strong>Delusions of guilt, sinfulness,<br/>worthlessness, poverty, failure, persecution, and command hallucinations to kill oneself, in a person with a depressed mood.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.1875px;\">\n<p>E.g.,<strong> </strong>Delusion of exaggerated power, knowledge, and worth in a person with a depressed mood</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8407","text":"Which of the following is ​not a risk factor for rap‌id cycling in bipolar dis‌order?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for rapid cycling in bipolar disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femal​e sex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothy​roidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Use of anti​depressants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BPA​D 1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. BPAD 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. BPAD 1</strong></p><p><strong>Bipolar affective disorder II</strong> (BPAD II) is a risk factor of <strong>rapid cycling</strong> (not BPAD I). </p>\n<p>Risk factors for developing rapid cycling include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female patients</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Alcoholism or substance abuse</li>\n<li>Antidepressants (if used without mood stabilizers)</li>\n<li>Cyclothymia</li>\n<li>Bipolar II disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6703","text":"A 21-year-old was brought ​to your OPD by his worrie‌d parents. He frequently ‌states that he is more le​arned than all his profes​sors and is on the verge ‍of a breakthrough. He bar‌ely slept and spent long ‌hours talking to his frie​nds about the importance ​of his ideas. His parents‌ confirmed that he had so‍me periods of withdrawal ‌and depression during his​ adolescence. What is the‌ drug of choice for the m‌aintenance therapy of thi‍s disorder?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old was brought to your OPD by his worried parents. He frequently states that he is more learned than all his professors and is on the verge of a breakthrough. He barely slept and spent long hours talking to his friends about the importance of his ideas. His parents confirmed that he had some periods of withdrawal and depression during his adolescence. What is the drug of choice for the maintenance therapy of this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lit​hium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Valpro​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-ps​ychotics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lithium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lithium</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bipolar disorder. Lithium</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for <strong>maintenance</strong> therapy of bipolar disorder.</p>\n<p>It is also the most effective long-term treatment choice for bipolar disorder. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6704","text":"What is the upper limit of​ serum lithium above whic‌h a patient is said to ha‌ve lithium toxicity?","raw_text":"What is the upper limit of serum lithium above which a patient is said to have lithium toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.8 ​mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.5 ​mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 ​mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1.5 ​mEq/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1.5 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1.5 mEq/L</strong></p><p>Lithium toxicity is considered when the blood concentration reaches <strong>1.5 mEq/L </strong>(or more). </p>\n<p>Blood concentration of lithium in the treatment of mood disorders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For treatment: <strong>0.8 - 1.2 mEq/L</strong></li>\n<li>For prophylaxis: <strong>0.5 - 0.8 mEq/L</strong></li>\n<li>Toxic levels: <strong>&gt;/= 1.5 mEq/L</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Manifestations of lithium toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute: Coarse tremors (most common), delirium, dementia, seizures, and increase in deep tendon reflexes</li>\n<li>Chronic: Nephrotoxicity (lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), hypothyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>most effective</strong> means of treating lithium toxicity is <strong>dialysis</strong>. It is necessary for severe poisoning cases, where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is exhibiting symptoms of toxicity, or</li>\n<li>Lithium concentration is 4 mEq/L or more. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6931","text":"A 35-year-old man with bip​olar disorder comes to th‌e clinic for a routine fo‌llow-up. His condition wa​s difficult to control an​d required treatment with‍ multiple medications ove‌r the past two years. The‌ patient says his mood ha​s been stable with his cu​rrent regimen, but for th‌e past three months, he h‍as had tremors primarily ‌affecting his hands. Whic​h of the following drugs ‌is the most likely cause?‌","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with bipolar disorder comes to the clinic for a routine follow-up. His condition was difficult to control and required treatment with multiple medications over the past two years. The patient says his mood has been stable with his current regimen, but for the past three months, he has had tremors primarily affecting his hands. Which of the following drugs is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gabap​entin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamot​rigine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lithium ​carbonate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lithium carbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lithium carbonate</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Lithium carbonate</strong> is most likely to cause the tremors described in the given scenario.</p>\n<p>Tremors are a common adverse effect of lithium carbonate<strong>.</strong> They are sometimes related to peak serum lithium levels.</p>\n<p>Dividing the dose of lithium carbonate and decreasing caffeine intake can decrease the severity of the tremors. In case of severe coarse tremors, lithium carbonate toxicity (serum lithium level greater than 2.5 mEq/L) must be ruled out.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: <strong>Carbamazepine</strong> and <strong>lamotrigine</strong> are off-label agents used for the management of bipolar disorder, however, they <strong>do not cause tremors.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Tremors may occur as an adverse effect of <strong>gabapentin</strong>, but it is <strong>not prescribed</strong> for the management of <strong>bipolar</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3246","text":"A patient comes to the OPD​ with a history significa‌nt of a mood disorder. Hi‌s moods can be charted as​ shown in the image. What​ will be the best drug fo‍r him?","raw_text":"A patient comes to the OPD with a history significant of a mood disorder. His moods can be charted as shown in the image. What will be the best drug for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lamot​rigine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium v​alproate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lit​hium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sodium valproate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f62508eedc264fe49919abadd54a55ea.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>rapid-cycling bipolar disorder.</strong> <strong>Sodium valproate </strong>is the<strong> drug of choice </strong>for this disorder.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of rapid-cycling bipolar disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>4 or more</strong> mood disturbances in a year</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Episodes either have a partial or full <strong>remission</strong> for at least 2 months <strong>or switch</strong> to the opposite <strong>polarity<br/><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Lamotrigine has been found to have limited uses in rapid-cycling bipolar disorder. It can prolong the time between depression and mania.</p>\n<p>Option C: Carbamazepine takes 2-3 weeks for the onset of action and is used in patients not responding to Lithium in Acute Mania.</p>\n<p>Option D: Lithium takes 5-7 days to achieve a level of equilibrium, moreover it is effective in Bipolar I disorder and is less efficacious than Divalproex in rapid-cycling bipolar disorder.</p>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3235","text":"A baby born to a woman tak​ing prophylactic medicati‌on for manic disorder is ‌cyanotic, lethargic, and ​hypotonic. Which other fi​nding is the baby most li‍kely to have?","raw_text":"A baby born to a woman taking prophylactic medication for manic disorder is cyanotic, lethargic, and hypotonic. Which other finding is the baby most likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal a​genesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anenc​ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspid valve displaceme​nt","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tricuspid valve displacement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tricuspid valve displacement</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are seen in <strong>Ebstein's anomaly. </strong>This is a rare congenital cyanotic heart disease caused by the downward <strong>displacement</strong> of the <strong>tricuspid</strong> <strong>valve</strong> into the right ventricle. It is a <strong>teratogenic effect of lithium</strong>, a drug used for prophylaxis in manic disorder.</p>\n<p>Lithium is thus <strong>avoided </strong>in the <strong>first trimester</strong> and during <strong>lactation</strong>. An antipsychotic like olanzapine should be considered instead of prophylactic management.</p>\n<p>If lithium is to be continued, then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The dose should be tapered to the lower end of the therapeutic range</li>\n<li>It should be given in divided doses</li>\n<li>The levels should be checked <strong>every two weeks</strong></li>\n<li>The woman should be hydrated well throughout labour</li>\n<li>Withholding lithium therapy for <strong>24-48 hours before delivery</strong> is recommended</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5EK6TpHp","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"12_nephroradiology","title":"12.Nephroradiology","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MA8350","text":"The given IVP picture is s‌een in patients with whic​h of the following? IVP- ​Intravenous Pyelography","raw_text":"<p>The given IVP picture is seen in patients with which of the following?</p>\n<p>IVP- Intravenous Pyelography</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horsesho‌e kidney","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duplicated collecting syst‌em","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrocav‌al ureter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Horseshoe kidney","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/966b4dae7abe4980a8d234a253d894ba.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9f14d36038b41e38a119fa29e33230bx510x615.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38b8c010681447c48cdee7649765dbd9x941x558.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Horseshoe kidney</strong></p><p>The given IVP image shows the <strong>hand-shake sign</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>horseshoe kidneys</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows both kidneys appearing <strong>rotated</strong>, with the bilateral pelvicalyceal systems oriented end-on. Additionally, the <strong>lower poles</strong> of both the kidneys are <strong>directed medially</strong> towards the spine, as depicted in the accompanying image.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Horseshoe kidney results from the <strong>fusion</strong> of <strong>two functional kidneys </strong>located on either side of the midline, typically c<span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">onnected </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">by an</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> isthmus</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> composed of either functional renal parenchyma or fibrous tissue. In most cases, the fusion occurs at</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> lower poles. </strong><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </strong></p>\n<p>The ascent of the horseshoe kidney is halted by the <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>, which prevents it from reaching the normal level of <strong>T12- L3</strong>. Consequently, the kidney remains <strong>lower</strong> in the abdomen, making it more susceptible to complications such as <strong>reflux, obstruction, </strong>and<strong> stone formation.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows horseshoe kidneys on abdominal CT:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: When both upper and lower poles are fused, it is termed a <strong>discoid kidney. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8345","text":"During intravenous pyelogr‌aphy of a patient, the le​ft kidney could not be vi​sualized. What condition ‌is this suggestive of?","raw_text":"During intravenous pyelography of a patient, the left kidney could not be visualized. What condition is this suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duplicated collecting syst‌em","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polycystic k‌idney disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kid‌ney","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal hy‌poplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multicystic dysplastic kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0bdb99e1d574639b2049ec5889afdf5x360x308.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multicystic dysplastic kidney</strong></p><p><strong>Non-visualization</strong> of the kidney on IVP is seen in the case of <strong>multicystic dysplastic kidney </strong>(MCDK).</p>\n<p>MCDK is a form of renal dysplasia characterized by multiple,<strong> non-communicating cysts</strong> of varying sizes, separated by dysplastic parenchyma, with the absence of a normal pelvicalyceal system. It results from a developmental abnormality due to failure of the renal mesenchyme to unite with the ureteric bud. This condition is associated with ureteral or ureteropelvic atresia, and the affected kidney is non-functional. </p>\n<p>Most cases of MCDK are detected <strong>antenatally</strong> through <strong>ultrasonography</strong>. In some cases, the cysts identified prenatally may regress in utero, resulting in the affected kidney being undetectable on imaging at birth. Renal <strong>ultrasonography</strong> is the initial investigation of choice. It reveals a <strong>soap bubble </strong>appearance. Bilateral MCDK can lead to pulmonary hypoplasia and <strong>Potter syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a non-visualized right kidney (red arrow).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: In the duplicated collecting system, the <strong>drooping lily sign</strong> is seen. It refers to the inferolateral displacement of the opacified lower pole moiety due to an obstructed (and un-opacified) upper pole moiety.</p>\n<p>Option B: In polycystic kidney disease, a <strong>streaky appearance</strong> is seen due to ectatic collecting ducts.</p>\n<p>Option D: In renal hypoplasia, there is a congenitally <strong>small kidney </strong>with <strong>smaller calyces</strong>, lobules, and papillae but essentially <strong>normal residual parenchyma</strong>. On imaging, a hypoplastic kidney is small but otherwise normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8349","text":"During the evaluation of a‌ patient with recurrent U​TIs, the following urogra​phic finding was observed‌. What is the likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"During the evaluation of a patient with recurrent UTIs, the following urographic finding was observed. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horsesho‌e kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duplicated collecting syst‌em","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Retrocav‌al ureter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duplicated collecting system","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57899abad4674a0c9431e4431ca108e5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/820feda9e5f94323acf2bd58ed8e50f1x1280x2007.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duplicated collecting system</strong></p><p>The given intravenous urogram shows the <strong>inferolateral displacement</strong> of the opacified lower pole due to an obstructed (and unopacified) upper pole, referred to as the <strong>drooping lily sign, </strong>observed in patients with a <strong>duplicated collecting system</strong><strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p>The duplicated collecting system is a common<strong> congenital anomaly </strong>of the urinary tract characterized by an <strong>incomplete fusion</strong> of upper and lower pole moieties resulting in a variety of complete or incomplete <strong>duplications</strong> of the collecting system. It occurs due to the division of the <strong>ureter </strong>as it ascends upwards from the bladder to the renal parenchyma during the embryological period of life. As a result, the affected individual may have <strong>two collecting systems for one kidney</strong>. </p>\n<p>Duplex collecting systems may also be <strong>complicated</strong> by vesicoureteric reflux, obstruction or ureterocoele. When the upper pole ureter is obstructed, the lower pole collecting system appears displaced and stretched on imaging, creating the \"<strong>drooping lily</strong>\" sign observed in intravenous urography. </p>\n<p>The Weigert-Meyer law describes the relationship of the upper and lower renal moieties in duplicated collecting systems to their drainage. When there are abnormalities associated with duplicating collecting systems,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>upper</strong> pole moiety drains <strong>inferomedially</strong> and is most commonly associated with<strong> obstruction</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>lower</strong> pole moiety drains <strong>superolaterally</strong> and is most commonly associated with <strong>vesicoureteral reflux.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The illustration given below shows the Weigert-Meyer law.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8433","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an IVP finding in pat​ients with renal papillar​y necrosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an IVP finding in patients with renal papillary necrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lobster ‌claw sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forniceal ‌excavation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Signe‌t ring","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ball and s‌ocket sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ball and socket sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02a0180259ff4791973b4e606fc2d286x1280x3761.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ball and socket sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>ball on tee</strong> sign is seen in <strong>renal papillary necrosis, </strong>not ball and socket. </p>\n<p>Renal papillary necrosis refers to<strong> ischemic necrosis </strong>of the renal papillae. It can be acute or chronic. <strong>Calyceal </strong>or <strong>ureteral obstruction</strong> by <strong>sloughed papillae </strong>manifests with flank pain, haematuria, and renal impairment. The common causes include analgesic abuse (phenacetin, NSAIDs, paracetamol: especially in combination), diabetes mellitus, sickle cell disease, pyelonephritis (especially in children) and obstruction.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the following signs of renal papillary necrosis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8351","text":"A diabetic patient present‌s with acute onset fever ​and dysuria. Urinalysis r​eveals plenty of leukocyt‌es with glycosuria. A CT ​scan of the abdomen is pe​rformed, as shown below. ​What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient presents with acute onset fever and dysuria. Urinalysis reveals plenty of leukocytes with glycosuria. A CT scan of the abdomen is performed, as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pyel‌onephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emphysematous pyelonephrit‌is","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Perinephr‌ic abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ‌abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Emphysematous pyelonephritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43365b3cf5c0431dbc669971bbb29823.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Emphysematous pyelonephritis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario, along with the CT scan showing an <strong>enlarged kidney</strong> with areas of <strong>necrosis</strong> and <strong>mottled areas</strong> of <strong>gas</strong> collection, is suggestive of<strong> left-sided</strong> <strong>emphysematous pyelonephritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Emphysematous pyelonephritis is a severe, <strong>acute, necrotizing infection</strong> of the kidney caused by gas-producing organisms. It predominantly affects <strong>diabetic</strong> patients who are particularly susceptible due to their hyperglycemic state, which promotes bacterial growth and fermentation. The most common causative organism is <strong><em>Escherichia coli</em></strong>, which ferments sugar to produce carbon dioxide, leading to <strong>gas accumulation</strong> within renal parenchyma or perinephric tissues. Less commonly, organisms like <em>Klebsiella </em>and<em> Proteus</em> species can also cause this condition.  </p>\n<p>Almost all patients present with fever, vomiting and flank pain. Patients frequently present with <strong>sepsis</strong> and ketoacidosis. If not treated early, it may lead to fulminant sepsis and is associated with <strong>high mortality</strong>. </p>\n<p>The diagnosis is primarily established radiographically, with a<strong> CT scan</strong> being the investigation of choice in identifying the <strong>extent</strong> of the emphysematous process and guiding <strong>management</strong>. The following diagnostic features are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enlarged, destructed renal parenchyma</li>\n<li>Small bubbly or linear streaks of gas</li>\n<li>Fluid collections, with gas-fluid levels</li>\n<li>Focal necrotic areas with or without abscess<br/> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8432","text":"The CT nephrogram of a pat‌ient with loin pain is gi​ven below. All of the fol​lowing can be included in‌ the differential diagnos​es except:","raw_text":"The CT nephrogram of a patient with loin pain is given below. All of the following can be included in the differential diagnoses except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pyel‌onephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal recessive polycy‌stic kidney disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal dominant polycys‌tic kidney disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute renal vein thrombosi‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7bca81d31de94dd09680e883ca885128.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease</strong></p><p>The given image shows linear bands of contrast extending from the medulla of the kidney towards the cortex of the kidney, called a <strong>striated nephrogram</strong> seen on plain film and CT nephrography. <strong>Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease </strong>(<strong>ADPKD</strong>) shows <strong>swiss cheese </strong>nephrogram, not striated nephrogram.</p>\n<p>Conditions which show striated nephrogram include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unilateral:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute ureteric obstruction</li>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong> <strong>pyelonephritis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong> <strong>renal vein thrombosis</strong></li>\n<li>Acutely following renal contusion</li>\n<li>Acute radiation therapy to the kidney</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Bilateral:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ARPKD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Acute pyelonephritis (if it is bilateral)</li>\n<li>Acute tubular obstruction</li>\n<li>Acute tubular necrosis</li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8428","text":"A patient is undergoing a ‌nephrogram. Two minutes a​fter administering the co​ntrast, both kidneys are ‌visualized. After 10 minu​tes of contrast administr​ation, only the left kidn​ey is visualized. What is‌ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient is undergoing a nephrogram. Two minutes after administering the contrast, both kidneys are visualized. After 10 minutes of contrast administration, only the left kidney is visualized. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute ureteric obstruction‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrone‌phrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute complete arterial ob‌struction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycystic k‌idney disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute ureteric obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5feb702952c407f88cd43e87ec5fc49x1280x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute ureteric obstruction</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario describes visualization of the left kidney with contrast after 10 minutes, even though contrast has washed off from the right side. Retention of the IV contrast agent by the kidneys for &gt;3 minutes is called a <strong>dense persistent nephrogram</strong>. It can be caused by<strong> acute ureteric obstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydronephrosis</strong> (chronic obstruction) and acute complete arterial obstruction shows a<strong> rim nephrogram.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows a bilateral dense persistent nephrogram:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8352","text":"What is the earliest radio‌graphic finding in patien​ts with genitourinary tub​erculosis?","raw_text":"What is the earliest radiographic finding in patients with genitourinary tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moth eate‌n calyces","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Golf ball ‌on tee sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lobster ‌claw sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ring‌ sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Moth eaten calyces","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a409650834aa42509a87819686d56383x1280x1703.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26e4c9105d1b457091cb1791d1ba685ex418x293.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b0f8bf4eb0a4ac6bdf57a8755aa0cdcx1280x3761.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Moth eaten calyces</strong></p><p><strong>Moth-eaten calyces</strong> are the earliest finding in a patient with <strong>genitourinary</strong> <strong>tuberculosis</strong>, seen on intravenous pyelography. It refers to the ragged, feathery calyceal outline due to irregular erosions of the calyx. </p>\n<p>This appearance is due to <strong>necrotizing papillitis</strong>, which further progresses to form <strong>medullary cavities</strong> that eventually communicate with the collecting system<em>.</em></p>\n<p>Genitourinary tuberculosis is caused by the dissemination of the organism through the bloodstream and is thus, always a <strong>secondary TB</strong>. There is either reinfection or reactivation of old TB. It can affect the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, prostate, seminal vesicles, epididymis, and vas deferens. </p>\n<p>Lesions caused by tuberculosis in the kidney include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Papillary ulcer</strong> - earliest lesion.</li>\n<li><strong>Pyonephrosis</strong> - caused by multiple scattered papillary ulcers undergoing caseous necrosis, which later become filled with pus.</li>\n<li><strong>Perinephric abscess</strong> -  by extension of pyonephrosis or tuberculous renal abscess.</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudocalculi</strong> - occurs when calcium gets deposited in a papillary ulcer healed with fibrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Ghost calyces</strong> - caused by the destruction of the calyces.</li>\n<li><strong>Putty/cement kidney: </strong>Amorphous dystrophic calcification eventually involves the entire kidney (seen in X-ray and CT).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The illustration below shows the lesions caused by tuberculosis in the urinary tract, followed by an IVP depicting moth-eaten calyces. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The given image shows the signs of renal papillary necrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8427","text":"A 59-year-old man was refe‌rred for abdominal pain a​nd sterile pyuria. He was​ diagnosed with tuberculo‌sis 10 years ago but was ​non-adherent to treatment​. What is the most sensit​ive imaging modality for ‌his current presentation?‌","raw_text":"A 59-year-old man was referred for abdominal pain and sterile pyuria. He was diagnosed with tuberculosis 10 years ago but was non-adherent to treatment. What is the most sensitive imaging modality for his current presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT intraveno‌us pyelogram","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bladder u‌ltrasound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non con‌trast CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CT intravenous pyelogram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13f1eb75debd4695b8255ef673fedd82x720x737.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f984103f7cce4bbc95cdb44deb240957x1280x1703.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CT intravenous pyelogram</strong></p><p>Sterile pyuria in a patient with history of tuberculosis raises suspicion of <strong>renal tuberculosis.</strong> The <strong>most sensitive</strong> imaging modality for diagnosing renal tuberculosis is<strong> CT Intravenous Pyelogram.</strong></p>\n<p>Renal tuberculosis occurs due to hematogenous dissemination of <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>, making it a<strong> secondary</strong> infection. There is either reinfection or reactivation of old TB. It is more common in males and typically presents with symptoms such as increased urinary frequency, polyuria, dysuria, hematuria, loin pain/suprapubic pain and sterile pyuria. </p>\n<p>In sterile pyuria, urine appears pale and opalescent due to the presence of pus cells. Renal TB is a cause of sterile pyuria. Diagnosis is confirmed with a <strong>tuberculin test</strong>, or at least 3 consecutive <strong>early-morning urine specimens</strong> are examined for acid-fast bacilli. They are stained with a <strong>Ziehl–Neelsen</strong> stain and subsequently cultured.</p>\n<p>The most sensitive imaging modality in renal tuberculosis is<strong> CT urography/pyelography</strong>. <strong>NCCT</strong> is the preferred means of visualizing <strong>renal calcifications</strong>. Manifestations of renal TB as seen on CT are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early</strong> changes:\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary necrosis (single or multiple) and uneven caliectasis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive</strong> changes:\n<ul>\n<li>Multifocal strictures in any part of the collecting system</li>\n<li>Generalized or focal hydronephrosis</li>\n<li>Mural thickening and enhancement</li>\n<li>Poorly enhancing renal parenchyma, either due to direct involvement or due to hydronephrosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>End-stage</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive hydronephrosis results in very thin parenchyma, mimicking multiple thin-walled cysts</li>\n<li>Amorphous dystrophic calcification eventually involves the entire kidney (known as the putty kidney), as shown below:</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8436","text":"A 29-year-old man presente‌d with bilateral vague lo​in discomfort. Plain X-ra​y KUB was done. All of th‌e following can cause thi​s finding except:","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man presented with bilateral vague loin discomfort. Plain X-ray KUB was done. All of the following can cause this finding except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal renal tubular acido‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alport’s‌ syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alport’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc5cd6872b484e4b835cb0a6abd922ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alport’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows coarse amorphous calcifications of both kidneys suggestive of medullary nephrocalcinosis. <strong>Alport's syndrome</strong> causes <strong>cortical nephrocalcinosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Nephrocalcinosis refers to the presence of <strong>calcification</strong> in the renal parenchyma, with most cases affecting the <strong>medulla</strong> and the remaining occurring in the cortex. Stippled patterns of calcification are observed on X-rays in the cortical and medullary regions of both the kidneys due to cortical and medullary nephrocalcinosis, respectively.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>medullary nephrocalcinosis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distal renal tubular acidosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Medullary sponge</strong> <strong>kidney</strong></li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia - including <strong>sarcoidosis</strong> </li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Hyperoxaluria</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> Causes of <strong>cortical nephrocalcinosis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)</li>\n<li>Graft rejection</li>\n<li>Acute cortical necrosis</li>\n<li>Chronic glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>Alport syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8442","text":"The renal angiographic pic‌ture of a patient is give​n below. What is the like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"The renal angiographic picture of a patient is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibromuscul‌ar dysplasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal arter‌y thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertensive nephroscleros‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal arte‌ry aneurysm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fibromuscular dysplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b90ad58c363940d88b92e277eda82cec.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fibromuscular dysplasia</strong></p><p>The given renal angiographic image depicts the '<strong>string of beads' </strong>sign, characterized by alternating areas of stenoses and small aneurysms in the renal arteries. This appearance is typical of <strong>fibromuscular dysplasia,</strong> a renovascular cause of secondary hypertension.</p>\n<p>Fibromuscular dysplasia is a <strong>segmental</strong>, non-atheromatous, dysplastic type of <strong>angiopathy</strong> characterized by <strong>focal irregular thickening</strong> in medium and large muscular arteries predominantly affecting <strong>females</strong>. The most common subtype is <strong>medial fibroplasia</strong>. The most common sites of involvement in decreasing order of frequency are as follows:<strong> Renal arteries</strong> &gt; carotid arteries &gt; peripheral (limb) arteries - most commonly iliac arteries.</p>\n<p>The typical presentation is in the young person, commonly females with new-onset hypertension. </p>\n<p>Medial fibroplasia is typically seen as a 'string of beads<strong>'</strong> appearance on <strong>CT angiography</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8453","text":"The following appearance a‌fter injecting contrast i​s a feature of which of t​he following conditions?","raw_text":"The following appearance after injecting contrast is a feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurogeni‌c bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder ‌granuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d67397f3f124b558b95176b221ea422.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/188bd5f5c4ed4fcf9a1e0ada4f5b119ex1280x1703.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given X-ray KUB image shows a markedly <strong>contracted</strong> <strong>bladder</strong> wall with reduced capacity called a <strong>thimble bladder. </strong>It is a late complication of <strong>genitourinary</strong> <strong>tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tuberculous cystitis results in <strong>fibrosis</strong> and <strong>stiffening</strong> of the entire urinary bladder, leading to an inability to expand and a significant reduction in bladder capacity, as depicted in the figure. The medical term for this feature on the <strong>intravenous urogram</strong> is a thimble bladder. </p>\n<p>Genitourinary TB is caused by the <strong>dissemination</strong> of the organism through the <strong>bloodstream </strong>and is thus always a secondary TB. There is either reinfection or reactivation of old TB. It can affect the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, prostate, seminal vesicles, epididymis, and vas deferens.</p>\n<p>Lesions caused by tuberculosis in the kidney include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Papillary ulcer</strong> - earliest lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Pyonephrosis</strong> - caused by multiple scattered papillary ulcers undergoing caseous necrosis, which later become filled with pus</li>\n<li><strong>Perinephric abscess</strong> -  by extension of pyonephrosis or tuberculous renal abscess</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudocalculi</strong> - occurs when calcium gets deposited in a papillary ulcer healed with fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Ghost calyces</strong> - caused by the destruction of the calyces</li>\n<li><strong>Putty/cement kidney</strong> - amorphous dystrophic calcification eventually involves the entire kidney (seen in X-ray and CT)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ureteric lesions seen in genitourinary tuberculosis include <strong>Kerr’s kink</strong> (kinking of the ureteropelvic junction), stricture, beaded ureter, and shortening of the ureter. Other features of bladder TB include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Golf hole ureteric orifice</strong> - This is due to fibrosis causing the rigid withdrawn dilated ureteric orifice to look like a golf hole (cystoscopic finding). </li>\n<li>Stippled calcifications</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8455","text":"A 45-year-old man with low‌er urinary tract symptoms​ underwent a CT-KUB as sh​own below, but was lost t‌o follow-up. Two months l​ater, he returned with wo​rsening symptoms, and rep​eat imaging revealed a de‌nsely calcified bladder r‌esembling a fetal skull. ‌What is the likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man with lower urinary tract symptoms underwent a CT-KUB as shown below, but was lost to follow-up. Two months later,  he returned with worsening symptoms, and repeat imaging revealed a densely calcified bladder resembling a fetal skull. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurogeni‌c bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder ‌carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schistosomiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0b09b841f0a94753a17c4b4bce5c0567.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0877db49d8ab41838773db482f0d2ab6x720x503.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f355b1859dc4f28ab319262d7951b9cx720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schistosomiasis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the CT-KUB image, which demostrates <strong>circumferential calcification </strong>of the bladder wall with normal volume, are indicative of bladder schistosomiasis, also known as bilharzia of the bladder. In advanced stages, there is development of a <strong>densely calcified</strong> bladder resembling a <strong>fetal skull. </strong></p>\n<p><em>Schistosoma haematobium</em> primarily causes genito-urinary schistosomiasis. The adult worms live in the veins of the urinary bladder. Chronic schistosomiasis presents as painless terminal hematuria, dysuria, and proteinuria. Fibrosis may cause <strong>obstructive uropathies</strong> like hydronephrosis and hydroureter. </p>\n<p>The radiographic features are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong> phase - nodular bladder wall thickening </li>\n<li><strong>Chronic</strong> phase: \n<ul>\n<li>Contracted fibrotic,<strong> thick-walled</strong> bladder. </li>\n<li>Curvilinear calcifications due to large numbers of <strong>calcified eggs</strong> in the bladder wall. </li>\n<li>Complication - squamous cell carcinoma (<strong>SCC</strong>), presenting as a mass. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Thimble bladder</strong> is a small contracted bladder due to fibrosis of the bladder walls. It is seen in <strong>bladder tuberculosis</strong>, and is appreciated in a micturating cystourethrogram (MCU). The image of thimble bladder is as shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Pine cone</strong> or <strong>Christmas tree </strong>appearance, in which the bladder is elongated and pointed with a thickened trabeculated wall, is seen in cases of<strong> neurogenic bladder. </strong>The image of this appearance is shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Cystourethroscopy is the mainstay for diagnosis of<strong> bladder carcinoma</strong>. It is performed on patients with hematuria. The most common radiological sign is a <strong>filling defect</strong> seen in intravenous urography (IVU). Non-contrast CT and MRI and ultrasonography are used for the immediate management of patients with gross painless haematuria. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8459","text":"The cystographic evaluatio‌n of a woman with recurre​nt urinary tract infectio​n is given below. What is‌ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The cystographic evaluation of a woman with recurrent urinary tract infection is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thimble‌ bladder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurogeni‌c bladder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bladder ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurogenic bladder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64df5a840bea4971b64259812d65d8ca.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35858ff4077946f6a25ee5ef4e6fcfa9x720x503.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04603ce0c1df46bf941ca0d7610501f2x720x463.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurogenic bladder</strong></p><p>The given cystogram shows an <strong>elongated</strong> and <strong>pointed bladder</strong> with a <strong>thickened trabeculated wall, </strong>called a <strong>Christmas tree </strong>or<strong> pine cone</strong> bladder<strong>. </strong>This is seen in patients with <strong>severe neurogenic bladder</strong> and increased sphincter tone.</p>\n<p>Neurogenic bladder is a term applied to a <strong>dysfunctional</strong> urinary bladder that results from an injury to the <strong>central</strong> or <strong>peripheral nerves</strong> that control and regulate urination. It causes <strong>detrusor sphincter dyssynergia.</strong> Neurogenic bladder typically occurs due to suprasacral lesions (above S2-S4) or apical lesions (in and around S2-S4).</p>\n<p>Pine cone bladder is <strong>not</strong> pathognomonic of a neurogenic bladder. It may also be seen in patients with lesions anywhere along the sacral reflex arc. Occasionally, it is also seen in bladder neck obstruction of a non-neurogenic cause.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Tuberculous cystitis results in<strong> fibrosis </strong>and <strong>stiffening</strong> of the entire urinary bladder, leading to an inability to expand and a significant <strong>reduction</strong> in bladder <strong>capacity</strong>, as depicted in the image below. The medical term for this feature on micturating cystourethrogram (MCU) is a <strong>thimble bladder. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: In <strong>bladder carcinoma</strong>, lesions are seen on the wall of the bladder and a biopsy is taken to confirm the diagnosis. These lesions cause disruption in the mucosa and the musculature of the bladder. Cystourethroscopy is the mainstay for the diagnosis of bladder carcinoma. It is performed on patients with hematuria. The most common radiological sign is a<strong> filling defect</strong> seen in intravenous urography (IVU). </p>\n<p>Option D: Schistosomiasis of the bladder is a parasitic infection by <em>Schistosoma hematobium. </em>The radiographic features in the acute phase is nodular bladder wall thickening, and in the chronic phase, it is the <strong>contracted fibrotic</strong>, thick-walled bladder and <strong>curvilinear calcifications </strong>due to large numbers of calcified eggs in the bladder wall. On <strong>cystoscopy,</strong> it can look like nodules, pseudotubercles, ulceration, and sandy patches, and in the end stage, it can cause squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder.</p>\n<p>The CT-KUB image below shows circumferential calcification of the bladder wall. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8463","text":"In a patient with the foll‌owing cystogram finding, ​which of the following wo​uld you not suspect?","raw_text":"In a patient with the following cystogram finding, which of the following would you not suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurogeni‌c bladder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pelvic ‌abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pelvic li‌pomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvic ‌hematoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neurogenic bladder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e28c8f943794b89b00afef69101fbb9.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bb0cbd14c434e5e87dbeee4081c8ff9x720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neurogenic bladder</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>pear-shaped</strong><strong> </strong>bladder, where the normal round or ovoid shape has been <strong>extrinsically compressed</strong> to resemble a pear. The pear may be inverted (also called <strong>tear-drop</strong> bladder) or upright, depending on how the excess pelvic tissue compresses the bladder. </p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>causes of the pear-shaped bladder include <strong>pelvic abscess, p</strong><strong>elvic lipomatosis, p</strong><strong>elvic hematoma, </strong>and symmetrical pelvic lymphadenopathy. </p>\n<p><strong>Neurogenic bladder</strong> results in a <strong>C</strong><strong>hristmas tree</strong> or <strong>pine cone</strong> appearance, where the urinary bladder becomes elongated with a pointed shape and a thickened trabeculated wall, as depicted below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8462","text":"A child with recurrent UTI‌s is suspected to have a ​congenital renal anomaly.​ The following picture is‌ seen on IV urography. Ba​sed on these findings, wh​ich of the following woul​d you suspect?","raw_text":"A child with recurrent UTIs is suspected to have a congenital renal anomaly. The following picture is seen on IV urography. Based on these findings, which of the following would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrocav‌al ureter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kid‌ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retrocaval ureter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aee0cb7a65f646589bed0ec568a9db22.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd0e6c824db04bb085413435a3abecebx720x821.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retrocaval ureter</strong></p><p>The given image shows a right-sided<strong> fishhook </strong>or <strong>S-shaped</strong> appearance of the ureter, where the right ureter hooks behind the inferior vena cava (IVC), as seen in the <strong>retrocaval ureter. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Circumcaval </strong>or retrocaval ureter is a developmental anomaly of the inferior vena cava (<strong>IVC</strong>). It is due to abnormal development of the right supracardinal system. A persistent right posterior cardinal vein ends up passing in front of the ureter. The anomaly <strong>always</strong> occurs on the <strong>right side</strong>, as this is the side of the normal IVC.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Vesicoureteric reflux</strong> (VUR) is a condition with a retrograde urinary flow from the bladder to the ureter and kidney. It is usually congenital. VUR can cause urinary stasis and lead to urinary tract infection (UTI). It also predisposes to pyelonephritis, which can lead to <strong>renal scarring</strong> due to parenchymal inflammation. This is known as <strong>reflux nephropathy</strong>. A <strong>micturating cystogram </strong>is used to confirm the VUR. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Bilateral fishhook </strong>or<strong> J-shaped</strong> or hockey stick ureters describe the course of the distal ureter in patients with significant <strong>benign prostatic hypertrophy </strong>(BPH).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8464","text":"A 2-year-old girl was refe‌rred for evaluation of re​current urinary tract inf​ections. She suffered 4 e‌pisodes over the past 9 m​onths and had been hospit​alized twice. An ultrasou​nd revealed bilateral hyd‌ronephrosis with dilatati‌on of ureters. What is th‌e investigation of choice‌ in this child?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl was referred for evaluation of recurrent urinary tract infections. She suffered 4 episodes over the past 9 months and had been hospitalized twice. An ultrasound revealed bilateral hydronephrosis with dilatation of ureters. What is the investigation of choice in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VC‌UG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tc DTP‌A scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MAG3‌ scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tc DMS‌A scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. VCUG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa91b46283604eaebcbd13c467f68ce6x1064x2464.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a34ce33aade47579ac6c06ff757c093x1080x1400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. VCUG</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with recurrent UTIs and hydronephrosis on USG is suggestive of <strong>vesicoureteric reflux </strong>(VUR)<strong>. </strong>The investigation of choice in this condition is a voiding cystourethrogram (<strong>VCUG</strong>) or micturating cystourethrogram (<strong>MCU</strong>).</p>\n<p>VUR refers to an <strong>abnormal urine flow</strong> from the <strong>bladder </strong>to the <strong>ureters</strong> and occurs in young children. This reflux predisposes to <strong>pyelonephritis</strong> by allowing the entry of bacteria into the usually sterile upper tract. As such, the diagnosis is first suspected after a UTI in a very <strong>young child</strong> or <strong>recurrent UTIs</strong>. USG may reveal abnormalities like <strong>hydronephrosis</strong> and <strong>dilated ureters</strong>. VCUG must be performed in such children before the age of 6 years to look for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence and grade of VUR.</li>\n<li>Whether reflux occurs during micturition or bladder filling.</li>\n<li>Presence of associated anatomical anomalies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>grading of VUR</strong> is based on the height of reflux up the ureters and the degree of dilatation of the ureters, as shown below, followed by an MCU showing grade V VUR on the left side. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: A <strong>99mTc-DTPA</strong> scan is used to measure GFR as it is cleared by glomerular filtration and no tubular reabsorption, determine differential renal function and evaluate for the presence of degree of renal function. </p>\n<p>Option C: The investigation of choice for <strong>ureteropelvic junction obstruction</strong> is a 99mTc-Mercaptoacetyl-triglycine (MAG3) scan. <strong>99mTc-MAG3</strong> is the most commonly used isotope in dynamic renography. Its uptake reflects the tubular function.</p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>99mTc-DMSA</strong> scan is used for the assessment of the renal cortex as it binds to the sulfhydryl groups in proximal tubules with longer retention than other agents. It allows for a better assessment of differential renal function. It is used for the evaluation of the renal structure and morphology, detection of <strong>renal</strong> <strong>scarring</strong> in reflux nephropathy and pyelonephritis and evaluation of renal anatomical abnormalities. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8444","text":"The following spot film wa‌s taken while performing ​a voiding cystourethrogra​m for a 2-year-old girl. ‌Which of the following in​vestigations will be usef​ul for the evaluation of ​renal scarring in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"The following spot film was taken while performing a voiding cystourethrogram for a 2-year-old girl. Which of the following investigations will be useful for the evaluation of renal scarring in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tc 99‌m DMSA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tc 99‌m DTPA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc 99‌m MAG3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tc 99m per‌technetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tc 99m DMSA","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a39d142abbe34be7a03a11e12553aa1c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9b81bbebb40419ea8dcf2f511174d68x600x577.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tc 99m DMSA</strong></p><p>The given voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG) shows a <strong>unilateral grade 4 vesicoureteric reflux</strong> (VUR). The investigation of choice to evaluate for renal scarring in reflux nephropathy is the <strong>Tc-99m DMSA </strong>(dimercaptosuccinic acid) scan.</p>\n<p>It is used in renal imaging to evaluate renal <strong>structure</strong> and <strong>morphology</strong>, particularly in paediatric imaging for the detection of scarring and pyelonephritis. DMSA is an ideal agent for the assessment of the <strong>renal cortex</strong> as it binds within the <strong>proximal tubules</strong> of the cortex with longer retention than other agents.</p>\n<p>It allows a better assessment of differential renal function. It is a <strong>static scan</strong> as opposed to dynamic DTPA or MAG3 scans. Other common renal scans include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tc-99m <strong>DTPA</strong> (diethylene-triamine-pentaacetate) - used for direct measurement of GFR as it is cleared only via glomeruli with no tubular excretion (option B). </li>\n<li>Tc-99m <strong>MAG3</strong> (mercaptoacetyltriglycine) - excreted through the renal route via both glomeruli infiltration and tubular excretion. Hence, in pediatric patients and patients with poor renal function, this test is considered superior to a DTPA scan. (option C).</li>\n<li>In renovascular hypertension, captopril DTPA is used for screening and the gold standard investigation is renal angiography.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a DMSA scan of a patient with renal failure with reduced uptake in both kidneys.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8465","text":"A neonate is being evaluat‌ed for urinary abnormalit​ies. Ultrasonography reve​als the following finding‌. What is the likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A neonate is being evaluated for urinary abnormalities. Ultrasonography reveals the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ur‌ethral valve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Urete‌rocele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrocav‌al ureter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior urethral valve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dc4d9b2b67fb45afafa025aed76c7f48.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dd68290a43f40d6912bfb67bb60a003x1280x1369.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f2af3e95a1d45518fd0622e88aec221x1279x1581.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior urethral valve</strong></p><p>The given image shows a dilated proximal urethra and associated thick-walled distended bladder on ultrasound that resembles a keyhole. This <strong>keyhole sign</strong> is seen in the presence of <strong>posterior urethral valves.</strong> </p>\n<p>Posterior urethral valves are the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>severe obstructive uropathy</strong> in children. The valves are <strong>membranes</strong> that have a small posterior slit within them, which typically lie just <strong>distal to</strong> the<strong> verumontanum. </strong>They function as flap valves and are obstructive to antegrade urinary flow. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis is most commonly made <strong>antenatally</strong> with <strong>ultrasound</strong>, which demonstrates <strong>bilateral hydronephrosis</strong> above a distended bladder in the fetus, along with<strong> oligohydramnios</strong> and <strong>pulmonary hypoplasia</strong>. If the diagnosis is not made antenatally, then babies typically present with <strong>urinary infections</strong> in the neonatal period or with uremia and renal failure. Clinically, a <strong>poor urinary stream</strong>, <strong>dribbling</strong>, and palpable bladder (due to diverticula) can be present.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis is<strong> best </strong>made during the<strong> micturition phase of VCUG</strong> in lateral or oblique views so that the posterior urethra can be imaged adequately. The following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dilatation</strong> and <strong>elongation</strong> of the posterior urethra - the equivalent of the ultrasonographic keyhole sign (as shown in the image below).</li>\n<li>Linear <strong>radiolucent band</strong> corresponding to the valve - seen occasionally.</li>\n<li><strong>Vesicoureteral reflux </strong>(VUR) - seen in 50% of patients.</li>\n<li>Bladder trabeculation/ diverticula.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8466","text":"What is the investigation ‌of choice for evaluating ​posterior urethral valves​ in a newborn male? MCU -‌ MIcturating cystourethro​graphy; Tc DTPA - Tecneti​tium-99m Diethylenetriami​ne Pentaacetate; DMSA - D‌imercaptosuccinic acid","raw_text":"<p>What is the investigation of choice for evaluating posterior urethral valves in a newborn male?</p>\n<p>MCU - MIcturating cystourethrography; Tc DTPA - Tecnetitium-99m Diethylenetriamine Pentaacetate; DMSA - Dimercaptosuccinic acid</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌CU","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tc-DTP‌A scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"U‌SG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DMSA‌ scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MCU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cacd0ef628724be2829b3233605b6b03x1280x1581.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0457ce1418fd4b509506a4de5f4b037ax1280x1369.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MCU</strong></p><p>Voiding cystourethrogram (<strong>VCUG</strong>) or micturating cystourethrogram (<strong>MCU</strong>) is the best imaging technique for diagnosing <strong>posterior urethral valves</strong>.</p>\n<p>Posterior urethral valves result from forming a thick, <strong>valve-like membrane</strong> from a tissue of <strong>Wolffian</strong> <strong>duct</strong> <strong>origin</strong> (failure of regression of the mesonephric duct). This membrane courses obliquely from the verumontanum to the most distal portion of the prostatic urethra. They act as flap valves and are obstructive to antegrade urinary flow.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is most commonly made <strong>antenatally</strong> with <strong>ultrasound. </strong>However, the diagnosis is<strong> best</strong> made during the <strong>micturition phase </strong>of VCUG in lateral or oblique views so that the posterior urethra can be imaged adequately. The following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dilatation </strong>and<strong> elongation</strong> of the posterior urethra - the equivalent of the ultrasonographic <strong>keyhole sign </strong>(as shown in the image below). </li>\n<li>Linear <strong>radiolucent band</strong> corresponding to the valve - seen occasionally. </li>\n<li>Vesicoureteral reflux (<strong>VUR</strong>) - seen in 50% of patients. </li>\n<li>Bladder trabeculation/ diverticula.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8467","text":"A 48-year-old woman underw‌ent retrograde ureterogra​phy as part of an evaluat​ion for intermittent loin‌ pain and dysuria. The fo​llowing finding is seen. ​What is the likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman underwent retrograde ureterography as part of an evaluation for intermittent loin pain and dysuria. The following finding is seen. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ureteric‌ calculi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberculosi‌s of ureter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transitional cell cancer o‌f ureter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydro‌ureter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transitional cell cancer of ureter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b3a27b3911d645c292d98287e1af349a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b6929a4dcf7439ba70ed4e8c1c6aab9x510x565.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transitional cell cancer of ureter</strong></p><p>The given retrograde ureterogram shows focal dilatation of the ureter by an intraluminal mass called a <strong>goblet</strong> or <strong>champagne glass sign</strong>. It is most commonly caused by <strong>transitional cell carcinoma </strong>of the <strong>ureter. </strong> </p>\n<p>Transitional cell carcinoma of the ureter can manifest as focal, eccentric, or circumferential wall thickening. On imaging, it often appears as fixed filling defects or irregular narrowing with proximal shouldering, a characteristic finding known as a <strong>goblet sign</strong> or <strong>champagne glass sign.</strong> This sign occurs due to <strong>luminal dilatation</strong> distal to the tumour, caused by the slow-growing tumor expanding the ureteral lumen. Additional features include <strong>hydronephrosis</strong>, with or without hydroureter, and, in advanced cases, <strong>absent renal function</strong> due to chronic obstruction. </p>\n<p>This sign indicates the pathology is <strong>chronic</strong>, permitting the lesion to be accommodated in the ureter. Other causes of goblet sign include <strong>metastatic</strong> disease in the ureter and <strong>endometriosis</strong> involving the ureter. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9590","text":"A 45-year-old woman comes ‌to the emergency departme​nt with severe back pain,​ vomiting, and hypotensio‌n. CT findings are given ​below. What is the diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman comes to the emergency department with severe back pain, vomiting, and hypotension. CT findings are given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal‌ cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Para pel‌vic cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal angi‌omyolipoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Renal angiomyolipoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed3e6603e2f646a4ab1540a6e060de44.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81df761cf104457cad0bb3af88a72afbx720x625.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34edf0a8b03147068dd08b4837d9881ax251x226.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Renal angiomyolipoma</strong></p><p>The given CT image shows well-circumscribed lesions with <strong>low attenuation</strong> (black in colour) that indicate <strong>fatty</strong> tissue. This is suggestive of <strong>renal angiomyolipomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Angiomyolipomas (AMLs) are benign tumours that occur sporadically or in association with <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong>. It is commonly seen in adults between<strong> 30 and 50 years</strong> of age, with a<strong> female</strong> predilection. AMLs predominantly affect the kidneys, though they can occur in other sites such as the liver (a common extra-renal location) and less frequently in the ovary, fallopian tube, spermatic cord, palate, and colon. </p>\n<p>Histologically, angiomyolipomas consist of <strong>perivascular</strong> <strong>epitheloid</strong> cells with varying proportions of <strong>vascular</strong>, immature <strong>smooth</strong> <strong>muscle</strong> cells, and <strong>fat cells</strong>. Patients with AMLs present with flank pain, a palpable mass, hematuria, recurrent urinary tract infections, hypertension, or renal failure. Larger tumours increase the risk of complications, such as <strong>spontaneous retroperitoneal hemorrhage</strong>, which can manifest as sudden-onset abdominal or flank pain, along with symptoms like nausea,<strong> vomiting</strong>, and<strong> hypotension</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Everolimus</strong> is used for the treatment of angiomyolipomas. Treatment should be considered for <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, growing AMLs measuring <strong>&gt;3 cm</strong> in diameter. Angiomyolipomas do not normally require surgery unless life-threatening bleeding is present.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-Northrup_2013_1-1\"></sup></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong> Renal cyst</strong> is a broader term that includes the <strong>cystic lesions </strong>of the kidney. They can be <strong>simple</strong> cysts that are benign. <strong>Complex </strong>or complicated cystic lesions need evaluation and can be malignant. <strong>Bosniak classification</strong> is used for renal cysts. Cysts appear as hypodense lesions (greyish) with a thin wall, with or without septations.</p>\n<p>CT image showing a simple renal cyst:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Renal cell carcinoma </strong>(RCC) is the most common <strong>malignant </strong>tumour of the kidney. It usually occurs in the age group <strong>50-70 years</strong> and macroscopic <strong>hematuria</strong> is seen in about 60% of the cases. It is a differential for angiomyolipoma but it is important to note that <strong>macroscopic fat</strong> is almost always associated with <strong>ossification or calcification.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8344","text":"The contrast-enhanced CT a‌bdomen of a 50-year-old w​oman shows the following ​finding. Which of the fol‌lowing classifications wo​uld you use to assess the​ severity of this conditi​on?","raw_text":"The contrast-enhanced CT abdomen of a 50-year-old woman shows the following finding. Which of the following classifications would you use to assess the severity of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jackson‌ staging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bosniak cla‌ssification","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Durie-Salm‌on staging","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Todani cla‌ssification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bosniak classification","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f96aaa956a6439f92ec3a7986c3c4e9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89ed11aeaf8f4038a2534cac0454053bx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bosniak classification</strong></p><p>The given CT image shows a simple <strong>renal cyst</strong>. The <strong>Bosniak classification</strong> system is used for the classification of renal cystic masses based on characteristics seen on <strong>contrast-enhanced CT</strong>. It is helpful in predicting malignancy risk and formulating a management plan.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 850.351px; width: 387px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61.5781px; text-align: center; height: 44.7969px;\"><strong>Bosniak class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.406px; text-align: center; height: 44.7969px;\"><strong>Appearance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.93px; text-align: center; height: 44.7969px;\"><strong>Malignancy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.133px; text-align: center; height: 44.7969px;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 115.195px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61.5781px; text-align: center; height: 115.195px;\"> 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.406px; height: 115.195px;\">\n<p>Simple cyst : </p>\n<p>Rounded with imperceptible wall</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.93px; height: 115.195px;\">Not malignant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.133px; height: 115.195px;\">No further workup required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 175.992px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61.5781px; text-align: center; height: 175.992px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.406px; height: 175.992px;\">\n<p>Minimally complex: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Size &lt;1 mm with septa, thin calcifications</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.93px; height: 175.992px;\">Not malignant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.133px; height: 175.992px;\">No further workup required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 227.188px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61.5781px; text-align: center; height: 227.188px;\">2F</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.406px; height: 227.188px;\">\n<p>Minimally complex:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Size &gt;3 cm</li>\n<li>Increased number of septa</li>\n<li>Minimally thickened with nodular/thick calcifications</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.93px; height: 227.188px;\">5% chance </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.133px; height: 227.188px;\">Ultrasound/CT follow-up every 6 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 271.984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61.5781px; text-align: center; height: 271.984px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.406px; height: 271.984px;\">\n<p>Indeterminate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complicated cysts, thickened septa</li>\n<li>Nodular calcification/solid non-enhancing areas (appears hyperdense on CT)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.93px; height: 271.984px;\">55% chance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.133px; height: 271.984px;\">\n<p>Partial nephrectomy</p>\n<p>Radiofrequency ablation (elderly/poor surgical candidates)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.1953px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61.5781px; text-align: center; height: 15.1953px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.406px; height: 15.1953px;\">\n<p>Solid mass with a large cystic or necrotic component</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.93px; height: 15.1953px;\">\n<p>Clearly malignant (100%)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.133px; height: 15.1953px;\">Partial/ total nephrectomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Jackson staging is used in <strong>carcinoma penis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Durie-Salmon staging is used for <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Todani classification is used in cases of <strong>choledochal cysts. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8468","text":"A 17-year-old boy is broug‌ht to the ER with left-si​ded scrotal pain and naus​ea. It developed suddenly‌ 30 minutes ago while pla​ying football. On examina​tion, the left scrotum is​ reddish with mild tender‌ness, and the testis is i‌n horizontal lie. What is‌ the investigation of cho‌ice?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy is brought to the ER with left-sided scrotal pain and nausea. It developed suddenly 30 minutes ago while playing football. On examination, the left scrotum is reddish with mild tenderness, and the testis is in horizontal lie. What is the investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non con‌trast CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dop‌pler","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CE‌CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doppler","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doppler</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of sudden scrotal pain while playing is suspicious for<strong> testicular torsion</strong>. The investigation of choice is <strong>doppler ultrasonography</strong>. It indicates reduced or absent blood flow to the testes. </p>\n<p>In testicular torsion, the blood supply to the testicle is compromised due to the <strong>twisting </strong>of the <strong>spermatic cord </strong>within the tunica vaginalis due to a '<strong>bell clapper deformity</strong>', resulting in <strong>ischemia </strong>of the epididymis and the testis. It is typically seen in <strong>adolescence </strong>and usually presents with <strong>sudden onset pain </strong>and swelling of the scrotum, associated with <strong>vomiting. </strong>A high-riding, <strong>swollen</strong>, and <strong>tender testis</strong> with a tender twisted cord can often be palpated above it, along with an<strong> erythematous</strong> scrotum and <strong>absent cremastric reflex</strong> on the affected side. </p>\n<p>A Doppler ultrasound may be taken to assess the blood supply but surgical exploration is still indicated as a clinical decision. <strong> Doppler ultrasound </strong>scanning<strong> </strong>shows <strong>absent</strong> or <strong>decreased </strong>intratesticular arterial blood flow compared to the contralateral testis. Doppler studies have improved the ultrasonographic evaluation of testicular torsion with the presence of <strong>whirlpool sign</strong> within the spermatic cord, which represents the twisted cord and is analogous to the twisted ovarian pedicle seen in ovarian torsion.  </p>\n<p><strong>Urgent scrotal exploration</strong> is indicated if there is doubt regarding the diagnosis. A transverse scrotal incision is taken and if the testis is viable twisting again is prevented by fixation with three non-absorbable sutures between the tunica albuginea of the testis and the scrotal raphe.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8448","text":"A 62-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for new-onset ​bilateral pedal edema. He​ had been diagnosed with ‌left-sided renal cell car​cinoma 2 months ago but h​ad declined surgery. What​ is the investigation of ‌choice to evaluate his cu‌rrent complaints?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man is being evaluated for new-onset bilateral pedal edema. He had been diagnosed with left-sided renal cell carcinoma 2 months ago but had declined surgery. What is the investigation of choice to evaluate his current complaints?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenou‌s Urography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contrast-e‌nhanced CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positron Emission Tomograp‌hy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Magnetic Resonance Imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cecd80049f06400bac6032dfda4b7f58x607x630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Magnetic Resonance Imaging</strong></p><p>Bilateral <strong>pedal edema</strong> in an elderly patient with left-sided renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>) is suspicious for tumor <strong>extension</strong> into the <strong>left renal vein</strong> and <strong>IVC</strong>. The investigation of choice to assess the vascular extension of the tumor thrombus is <strong>Magnetic resonance imaging.</strong></p>\n<p>RCC is the most <strong>common malignant tumor</strong> of the kidney that usually presents in the elderly. <strong>Cigarette smoking</strong> and <strong>hypertension </strong>are major associated risk factors. The classic triad of presentation is with flank pain, macroscopic haematuria, and a palpable flank mass. Advanced cases can also present with <strong>bilateral lower limb edema.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>MRI </strong>is beneficial for assessing <strong>vascular invasion</strong>, especially when evaluating the renal vein and IVC. It aids in differentiating between benign and malignant thrombus. However, it does not provide a significant advantage over CT in staging nodal disease. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option C: Triphasic <strong>contrast-enhanced CT</strong> is the investigation of choice for <strong>diagnosis </strong>and <strong>staging </strong>of RCC (image given below). On CT, small lesions enhance <strong>homogeneously</strong>, whereas larger lesions have <strong>irregular enhancement</strong> due to areas of necrosis. The CT also provides additional information on the <strong>function</strong> of the opposite kidney, primary tumor extension, venous involvement, enlargement of regional lymph nodes, the status of the adrenal glands, and intra-abdominal metastatic disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Intravenous urography</strong> is no longer suitable for detecting renal masses, particularly if they occur centrally; cross-sectional imaging studies are more appropriate for investigating suspected renal mass. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>FDG-Positron Emission Tomography</strong> plays a limited role in the initial detection and diagnosis of RCC due to low fluorodeoxyglucose uptake by these tumours. However, it can be useful in identifying local recurrence and serves as a complementary tool alongside other cross-sectional imaging methods. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8450","text":"A 34-year-old man comes to‌ the emergency department​ with complaints of sever​e, right-sided, colicky f‌lank pain that radiates t​o his groin. He has a his​tory of recurrent kidney ​stones. What is the inves‌tigation of choice?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man comes to the emergency department with complaints of severe, right-sided, colicky flank pain that radiates to his groin. He has a history of recurrent kidney stones. What is the investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U‌SG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non con‌trast CT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contrast e‌nhanced CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plain X‌-ray KUB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non contrast CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76df072dd610493999f7310692e077aax491x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non contrast CT</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>renal calculi</strong>. The investigation of choice is<strong> non-contrast CT </strong>(NCCT). NCCT is useful as it has a <strong>greater sensitivity</strong> to tissue attenuation and, hence, can pick up stones that may appear radiolucent on plain X-rays.</p>\n<p>Renal calculi are more common in males, with <strong>90%</strong> of stones being <strong>radiopaque</strong>. Clinically, patients present with severe pain located over the renal angle, often radiating to the groin. The pain may be accompanied by vomiting due to pyloric spasm, as well as hematuria, nausea, fever, and in some cases, symptoms of pyuria. </p>\n<p>A <strong>non-contrast CT scan</strong> is the imaging modality of choice for suspected urinary tract calculi. It is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> and gold-standard test for detecting stones as it is quick, detects most types of calculi, and can be performed with low radiation doses. A history of nephrolithiasis with new-onset flank pain should prompt immediate evaluation with non-contrast CT. The radiographic features depend on the <strong>stone composition</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following types of renal calculi may appear <strong>radiolucent</strong> on <strong>plain X-ray: </strong><strong>Uric acid, </strong>Indinavir and pure matrix stones (may have radiodense rim or centre). On CT, most stones are usually opaque. However, <strong>indinavir</strong> and<strong> pure matrix</strong> stones appear <strong>radiolucent</strong> even on <strong>CT</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following X-ray KUB image shows a staghorn calculus (struvite).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8435","text":"The IVP of a patient with ‌persistent flank pain is ​given below. What is the ​likely diagnosis? IVP- In‌travenous Pyelogram","raw_text":"<p>The IVP of a patient with persistent flank pain is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>IVP- Intravenous Pyelogram</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrocav‌al ureter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retroperiton‌eal fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kid‌ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retroperitoneal fibrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/59ab26f155274e6087092af03bc49ee0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retroperitoneal fibrosis</strong></p><p>The given IVP in a patient with persistent flank pain shows the <strong>medial</strong> <strong>deviation </strong>of <strong>bilateral ureters,</strong> caused by fibrous tissue deposition in the retroperitoneum at the <strong>lumbosacral</strong> junction, resulting in the characteristic narrow-waisted maiden appearance, known as the <strong>maiden waist sign, </strong>which is seen in <strong>retroperitoneal fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Retroperitoneal fibrosis (RPF) is a chronic, <strong>nonspecific inflammatory process</strong> of the fibro-fatty tissue in the retroperitoneum, with the <strong>idiopathic</strong> type being the most common, accounting for about 70% of the cases. Patients typically present with persistent <strong>flank pain</strong> or back pain. <strong>Progressive fibrotic thickening</strong> can compress the ureters, leading to bilateral hydronephrosis and, if untreated, renal failure. Aside from idiopathic causes, other causes of RPF include <strong>drug-induced factors </strong>( e.g., methysergide, beta-blockers), advanced malignancy, radiation exposure, trauma, and chronic infections.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of RPF relies primarily on imaging studies. Intravenous urography shows a characteristic triad of medial deviation of the middle one-third of the ureters, producing a <strong>maiden waist sign</strong>, <strong>hydroureteronephrosis</strong>, and <strong>extrinsic compression</strong> of the ureters by the fibrotic process. CT and MRI are also useful in radiographic evaluation of RPF.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8460","text":"The following appearance i‌n IVP is caused by:","raw_text":"The following appearance in IVP is caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horse sh‌oe kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ureter‌ocoele","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bladder‌ tumour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ur‌ethral valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ureterocoele","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/189f5e0e91284aaaa2e6e6c3f0f565b6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ureterocoele</strong></p><p>The given intravenous pyelography (IVP) depicts the <strong>cobra head</strong> or<strong> adder head </strong>deformity or <strong>spring onion</strong> sign seen in <strong>bilateral</strong> <strong>ureterocele. </strong>These signs refer to a <strong>dilated, contrast-filled distal ureter</strong> surrounded by a thin rim of the ureteric wall and bladder mucosa.</p>\n<p>Ureterocoele is a <strong>cystic enlargement </strong>of the intramural portion of the ureter, more commonly seen in <strong>females</strong> than males. It is thought to be caused due to ureteric orifice atresia. They are similar to ectopic ureters, which typically drain the upper pole of the kidney and are associated with dysplastic or non-functional renal tissue.</p>\n<p>If they present clinically, ureteroceles often manifest with <strong>urinary tract infections</strong> (UTIs) in <strong>children</strong>, while in <strong>adults,</strong> they are more likely to present with <strong>stones</strong> in the lower ureter. Ureterocoeles can enlarge and obstruct the bladder neck or the contralateral ureteric orifice.</p>\n<p>The classic sign of a ureterocele on intravenous urography is a ‘cobra head’, ‘adder head’, or 'spring onion sign'.  On ultrasonography, it can be seen as a <strong>fluid-filled cystic mass</strong> in the bladder.</p>\n<p>The treatment of simple ureteroceles is <strong>surgical excision with re-implantation</strong> of the ureter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vs5kIxjs","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"12_benign_lesions_of_thyroid","title":"12.Benign Lesions Of Thyroid","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0288","text":"What is the most common si‍te for a thyroglossal cys​t?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for a thyroglossal cyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supra‍hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infra‍hyoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"At the level‍ of the hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Floor of ‍the mouth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infrahyoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/baaef1d7a80f42eab32b820d3c6b9191x1280x1297.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infrahyoid</strong></p><p>The most common site for <strong>thyroglossal cyst</strong> is <strong>infrahyoid region.</strong></p>\n<p>It can occur anywhere along the migratory course of the duct:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infrahyoid (most common site)</li>\n<li>Region of thyroid cartilage</li>\n<li>Suprahyoid</li>\n<li>Floor of mouth</li>\n<li>Beneath foramen caecum</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A thyroglossal duct cyst is a congenital midline neck swelling that typically presents during childhood. Normally, the <strong>thyroglossal duct</strong> regresses once the thyroid gland has completely descended. If it <strong>fails </strong>to<strong> regress</strong>, a thyroglossal duct <strong>cyst</strong> is formed.</p>\n<p>It can be differentiated from other midline neck swellings by the following features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Can be moved sideways but not vertically</li>\n<li>Moves with both deglutition and tongue protrusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Surgical management of a thyroglossal cyst is via <strong>Sistrunk procedure.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Presence of thyroid in the midline posterior 1/3rd of tongue is called lingual thyroid. It is due to failure of descent of the thyroglossal duct during development.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9318","text":"A 25-year-old patient pres‍ented with neck swelling ​which moves with deglutit​ion as shown below. What ‌is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient presented with neck swelling which moves with deglutition as shown below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Branchi‍al cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic ‍hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroglo‍ssal cyst","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical lym‍phadenopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thyroglossal cyst","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5a7d0bb849b4aa1bf7db6cc51aca449.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98e4985058c24cb1ae1cbf0c79b41648x1280x1297.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thyroglossal cyst</strong></p><p>A <strong>thyroglossal cyst </strong>is the only <strong>midline</strong> neck swelling that <strong>moves</strong> with <strong>deglutition</strong>. This is because the thyroid gland is fixed to the hyoid. </p>\n<p>Thyroglossal duct cyst:</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>congenital midline neck swelling </strong>that typically presents during childhood often noticed after an upper respiratory infection. It<strong> </strong>represents the <strong>persistence</strong> of the <strong>track</strong> of the<strong> thyroid gland </strong>and can be found adjacent to the<strong> midline. </strong>The thyroid gland descends early in fetal life from the base of the tongue towards its position in the lower neck. At the time of descent, the hyoid bone has not been formed and the track of the thyroid gland is variable, passing in front, through, or behind the eventual position of the hyoid body. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical features of thyroglossal cyst:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classically the cyst <strong>moves upwards</strong> on <strong>swallowing</strong> and on<strong> tongue protrusion.</strong> This indicates attachment to the hyoid bone. </li>\n<li>Swelling is smooth, soft, fluctuant (cystic), nontender, mobile, often transilluminant.</li>\n<li>Thyroid fossa is empty, if there is no thyroid in the normal location.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">\n<p>Complications of thyroglossal cyst:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common complication is <strong>infection.</strong></li>\n<li>Thyroglossal <strong>fistula</strong> is an acquired condition that either follows <strong>infection</strong> of the cyst which <strong>bursts open or</strong> due to <strong>inadequate removal</strong> of the cyst.</li>\n<li>1% of thyroglossal duct cysts are found to be malignant of which the most common type of <strong>cancer</strong> is <strong>papillary</strong> (85%).</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">Neck swellings that move with deglutition are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Thyroid swellings</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Subhyoid bursitis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Dermoid cysts attached to the hyoid bone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, only the <strong>thyroglossal cyst</strong> <strong>moves</strong> with<strong> tongue protrusion</strong>. This is because the duct extends from the foramen caecum of the tongue to the isthmus of the gland. </p>\n<p><strong>Sistrunk’s operation </strong>is performed to excise the cyst and prevent a recurrence. It involves the <strong>excision </strong>of the <strong>whole thyroglossal tract,</strong> involving removal of the <strong>body of the hyoid bone</strong>, and <strong>suprahyoid tract</strong> through the tongue base to the vallecula at the site of the primitive foramen caecum along with the core of tissue on either side.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0290","text":"A child with a midline swe‍lling on the neck is bein​g prepared for a Sistrunk​ procedure. Along with en‌ bloc cystectomy, which o‍f the following structure‍s will be resected?","raw_text":"A child with a midline swelling on the neck is being prepared for a Sistrunk procedure. Along with en bloc cystectomy, which of the following structures will be resected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroglossal tract and par‍t of thyroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central hyoid bone and thy‍roglossal tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroglossa‍l tract only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroglossal tract and ass‍ociated cervical lymph no​des","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central hyoid bone and thyroglossal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central hyoid bone and thyroglossal tract</strong></p><p><strong>Sistrunk</strong> operation involves en bloc cystectomy with <strong>excision</strong> of the entire<strong> thyroglossal tract</strong> till the base of the tongue along with the <strong>central hyoid bone</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the procedure of choice for the surgical management of thyroglossal duct cysts. It is done to <strong>prevent recurrence</strong> and <strong>malignant</strong> transformation. </p>\n<p>The most common malignancy in thyroglossal cysts is <strong>papillary</strong> thyroid carcinoma. In such a case, <strong>Sistrunk operation </strong>with<strong> total thyroidectomy</strong> is considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0219","text":"A patient with a nodule of‍ the neck presents to the​ surgery OPD. A junior re​sident performs the follo‌wing method to palpate it‍. Identify the procedure ‍shown in the video below.‌","raw_text":"A patient with a nodule of the neck presents to the surgery OPD. A junior resident performs the following method to palpate it. Identify the procedure shown in the video below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lahey's‍ method","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crile's‍ method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kocher'‍s method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pemberton‍'s method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lahey's method","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/263233731a97445ba5cad0ce70d913a8.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce429ab8ae72405d88b511078b9a3737x720x734.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lahey&#x27;s method</strong></p><p>The video depicts <strong>Lahey’s method</strong> of palpation of the <strong>left lobe</strong> of the <strong>thyroid gland.</strong></p>\n<p>In Lahey's method, for <strong>palpating the left lobe</strong>, the examiner initially <strong>pushes</strong> the <strong>right</strong> <strong>lobe</strong> <strong>medially</strong> with the <strong>left hand</strong>, and then using the <strong>right hand,</strong> the border and consistency of the <strong>left lobe</strong> is palpated.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: In<strong> Crile's method</strong> the patient is asked to <strong>swallow</strong>, while the <strong>examiner</strong> <strong>palpates</strong> the gland with the <strong>thumb</strong>. This gives a clear indication regarding the <strong>slight enlargement</strong> of the <strong>thyroid gland</strong> or small nodules.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Kocher's method</strong> is done to demonstrate the <strong>pressure effect</strong> of the thyroid gland on the <strong>trachea</strong> or <strong>larynx</strong>. <strong>Lateral</strong> <strong>compression</strong> will result in <strong>stridor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pemberton's</strong> <strong>sign </strong>(not method), is <strong>engorgement of neck veins </strong>when the patient is asked to raise their hands above the head and the arms should touch the ears. It is seen in patients with <strong>retrosternal goiter.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3939","text":"A patient with a solitary,‍ 2x3 cm, nodular thyroid ​swelling came to your cli​nic with a recent report ‌that showed elevated T4 a‍nd normal TSH levels. Wha‍t is the next best step i‌n management?","raw_text":"A patient with a solitary, 2x3 cm, nodular thyroid swelling came to your clinic with a recent report that showed elevated T4 and normal TSH levels.  What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fine needle aspiration cyt‍ology (FNAC)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultra‍sound","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroid aut‍oantibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radioactive iodine uptake ‍test (RAIU)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ultrasound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccbb6735c3814342bab6940ff52c3567x1280x2317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ultrasound</strong></p><p><strong>Ultrasonography</strong> is the next best step in the evaluation of a euthyroid (normal TSH) patient with a solitary thyroid nodule. USG is a non-invasive and inexpensive way to <strong>stratify the risk </strong>of the nodule into benign or malignant tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Benign</strong> nodules are easily <strong>compressible</strong> and can be seen to <strong>deform </strong>by applying pressure with the probe while performing USG. <strong>M</strong><strong>alignant </strong>nodules tend to be <strong>hard</strong> and <strong>firm.</strong> </p>\n<p>Ultrasound findings of a nodule that are considered <strong>suspicious</strong> of <strong>malignancy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Microcalcifications</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hypervascularity</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Infiltrative margins</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hypoechoic compared with surrounding parenchyma</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3864","text":"FNAC report of a patient w‍ith discrete thyroid swel​ling shows a Thy2 lesion.​ What do you infer from t‌his report?","raw_text":"FNAC report of a patient with discrete thyroid swelling shows a Thy2 lesion. What do you infer from this report?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicul‍ar lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-neopla‍stic lesion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lesion suspicious of malig‍nancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic‍ lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-neoplastic lesion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aad8cc9d53a446b09572a83b3b4652eex1280x1698.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-neoplastic lesion</strong></p><p>FNAC report of<strong> Thy2</strong> indicates a <strong>non-neoplastic</strong> lesion. </p>\n<p>Fine-needle aspiration cytology reporting according to Royal College of Pathologists UK:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 155px; width: 344.312px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\"><strong>Category</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\"><strong>Lesion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Thy1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\">Non-diagnostic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Thy1c</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\">Non-diagnostic cystic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\"><strong>Thy2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\"><strong>Non-neoplastic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Thy3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\">Follicular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\"><strong>Thy4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\"><strong>Suspicious for malignancy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Thy5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.312px;\">Malignant</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 921\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Another cytology reporting system is \"The <strong>Bethesda System</strong> for Reporting Thyroid Cytopathology\" (TBSRTC):</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0293","text":"A 22-year-old student with‍ a nodular thyroid swelli​ng undergoes a radionucli​de scan. What is the risk‌ of malignancy of the pat‍hology given below?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old student with a nodular thyroid swelling undergoes a radionuclide scan. What is the risk of malignancy of the pathology given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‍%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‍0%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20%","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/559c53999a13436f98a763034eea99fa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1707c68d743749ca9e543125eb555077x1200x2202.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/352658e45a324fed8bd8c02ebb8c1cd7x1280x2317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20%</strong></p><p>The pathology on the scan shows <strong>decreased iodine uptake</strong> by a <strong>cold thyroid nodule</strong>. It is associated with around <strong>20%</strong> risk of malignancy.</p>\n<p>A thyroid scan is used in patients with hyperthyroidism with low or normal TSH.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Hot nodules</strong> indicate increased function of the gland and are <strong>rarely malignant</strong>. They are managed with either I<sup>131</sup> or surgery. The risk of malignancy is higher with <strong>“cold”</strong> lesions <strong>(15-20%)</strong> as compared to <strong>“warm”</strong> lesions (<strong>&lt;5%</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Cold nodules</strong> need to be further <strong>evaluated</strong> using <strong>USG</strong> as they pose a higher <strong>risk of malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3874","text":"A young male patient prese‍nts with a discrete thyro​id swelling. USG reveals ​an isolated cystic swelli‌ng of the gland. Which of‍ the following statements‍ correctly expresses the ‌risk of malignancy in thi‌s patient?","raw_text":"A young male patient presents with a discrete thyroid swelling. USG reveals an isolated cystic swelling of the gland. Which of the following statements correctly expresses the risk of malignancy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6‍%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‍2%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‍4%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4‍8%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 24%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/baccc393d54f45959050c46fa33e8990x1200x1899.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 24%</strong></p><p>Isolated <strong>cystic</strong> swellings are <strong>malignant</strong> in <strong>24%</strong> of <strong>males</strong>.</p>\n<p>The general rule of thumb is that the risk of malignancy is greater in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Isolated &gt; dominant swellings</li>\n<li>Solid &gt; cystic</li>\n<li>Men &gt; women</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risk of malignancy in thyroid swellings, given by the <strong>rule of twelve, </strong>is depicted below. The risk of cancer in thyroid swelling can be expressed as a factor of 12. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0292","text":"A patient scheduled for a ‍thyroid radionuclide scan​ had consumed an I¹²³ con​taining pill at around 6 ‌AM. What is the half-life‍ of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient scheduled for a thyroid radionuclide scan had consumed an I¹²³ containing pill at around 6 AM. What is the half-life of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-14‍ hours","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"20-24‍ hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-6 ‍hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8-10‍ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 12-14 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 12-14 hours</strong></p><p>The half-life of I<sup>123 </sup>is <strong>12-14 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Isotopes of Iodine such as Iodine-123 (<sup>123</sup>I) and Iodine-131 (<sup>131</sup>I) are used to image the thyroid gland.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 388.909px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Iodine radioisotope</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\"><strong>I<sup>123</sup></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.909px; text-align: center;\"><strong>I<sup>131</sup></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Half-life</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\"><strong>12- 14 hours</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.909px;\">8-10 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Radiation exposure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.909px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Radiation emission</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Gamma rays</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.909px;\">Beta and Gamma rays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Uses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">\n<p>Imaging of lingual thyroids</p>\n<p>Anatomical and physiological function of thyroid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.909px;\">Screening and treatment of patients with differentiated thyroid cancers for metastatic disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4468","text":"A patient presents with a ‍painless swelling of the ​neck. The histology of th​e tissue is as given in t‌he image below. What is t‍he diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a painless swelling of the neck. The histology of the tissue is as given in the image below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non Hodgkin‍'s lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grave's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hashimoto's‍ thyroiditis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hashimoto's thyroiditis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/daba3807ac4e4b8cb74ffdb00bd2b6f7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/845a648c25b3440797f586e684e8981ex1280x1164.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db8cadf2a71a452b83f9cbcbc81c5e55x1280x853.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b67d3c782b3c442db097a93025af036fx1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hashimoto&#x27;s thyroiditis</strong></p><p>The slide shows the histopathology of<strong> Hashimoto thyroiditis, </strong>with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Follicular destruction</li>\n<li>Reduced colloid with increased interstitial connective tissue</li>\n<li>Small lymphocyte and plasma cell infiltration</li>\n<li>A well-developed germinal centre may be seen</li>\n<li>Hurthle (Askanazy) cells that have abundant eosinophilic granular cytoplasm lining the follicles</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Hashimoto's thyroiditis </strong>is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in <strong>iodine-sufficient</strong> areas. It is also known as struma lymphomatosa or lymphocytic thyroiditis. It is an autoimmune disease that results in the destruction of the thyroid gland and gradual &amp; progressive thyroid failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most prevalent between 45-65 years of age</li>\n<li>More common in females</li>\n<li>Painless enlargement of the thyroid</li>\n<li><strong>Hashitoxicosis</strong>: When the auto-antibodies attack and damage the follicles, there is a sudden release of thyroid hormones from these follicles. This leads to a transient phase of thyrotoxicosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Since the follicles are damaged and cannot form the hormones anymore, these patients tend to be in a state of hypothyroidism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>T<sub>3</sub> and T<sub>4</sub> are low</li>\n<li>TSH is raised</li>\n<li>Autoantibodies against:\n<ul>\n<li>Thyroid receptors</li>\n<li>Thyroid peroxidase (TPO)</li>\n<li>Thyroglobulin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These patients are managed with thyroid <strong>hormone replacement</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>In patients with <strong>long-standing goiter</strong>, there is a risk of compressive features and the development of<strong> thyroid lymphoma</strong>. Such cases can be treated with <strong>total thyroidectomy.</strong> It may also predispose to papillary thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Histological features of <strong>medullary carcinoma</strong> of the thyroid include polygonal to spindle-shaped cells in the form of nests, trabeculae or follicles. Small, round anaplastic cells may be found in some cases and amyloid deposits derived from calcitonin polypeptides are present in the stroma.</p>\n<p>The given slide below shows medullary carcinoma of thyroid:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Grave's disease</strong> is an autoimmune disorder characterized by hyperthyroidism due to circulating autoantibodies. The thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins bind to the TSH receptors, causing overgrowth of the thyroid gland and increased thyroid hormone synthesis. Histologically, there is acinar cell hypertrophy and hyperplasia with the absence of normal colloid in the tall columnar epithelium (normally the epithelium is flat with colloid). Lymphoid infiltrates, consisting predominantly of T cells, along with scattered B cells and mature plasma cells, are present throughout the interstitium. Germinal centers are common.</p>\n<p>The given slide below shows Grave's disease:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0304","text":"A middle-aged female patie‍nt presented with malaise​, fever, and painful neck​ swelling. She had recent‌ly recovered from an epis‍ode of upper respiratory ‍tract infection. Lab repo‌rts show slightly elevate‌d T4 and ESR was 100 mm/h​r. What is the most appro‌priate management option ‍for this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged female patient presented with malaise, fever, and painful neck swelling. She had recently recovered from an episode of upper respiratory tract infection.  Lab reports show slightly elevated T4 and ESR was 100 mm/hr. What is the most appropriate management option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antib‍iotics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti‍viral","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NSA‍IDS","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antithyr‍oid drugs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NSAIDS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NSAIDS</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> De Quervain's thyroiditis</strong> for which <strong>NSAIDs</strong> is the treatment of choice. In this condition, T3, T4 and thyroglobulin may be raised due to the release of preformed hormones from the destructed follicles. </p>\n<p>De Quervain’s thyroiditis (subacute or viral thyroiditis) is a <strong>granulomatous</strong> enlargement of the thyroid gland, usually following a <strong>viral infection</strong>. It is an inflammatory condition and not an infection as the thyroid gland is inherently resistant to infection due to its extensive blood and lymphatic supply, high iodide content, and fibrous capsule.  </p>\n<p>It predominantly occurs in <strong>women</strong> between the ages of 30-40 years. The condition is <strong>self-limiting</strong>, and management is symptomatic i.e. <strong>aspirin </strong>and other<strong> non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</strong> are used for pain relief, but steroids may be indicated in more severe cases.</p>\n<p>Note: Acute suppurative thyroiditis mimics De Quervain’s thyroiditis in that it is often preceded by a pyogenic upper respiratory tract infection or otitis media. However, acute suppurative thyroiditis is more common in children. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3913","text":"A 45-year-old lady, with a‍ painless neck swelling, ​presented with palpitatio​ns and weight loss. Thyro‌id function tests reveale‍d elevated T3 and T4, low‍ TSH, and negative TSH-R ‌Ab. Iodine scan showed mu‌ltiple thyroid nodules wi​th increased iodine uptak‌e. Initial anti-thyroid t‍reatment was given. What ‌is the next step of manag‌ement?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old lady, with a painless neck swelling, presented with palpitations and weight loss. Thyroid function tests revealed elevated T3 and T4, low TSH, and negative TSH-R Ab. Iodine scan showed multiple thyroid nodules with increased iodine uptake. Initial anti-thyroid treatment was given. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long-term antithyroid medi‍cation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sub-total t‍hyroidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radioacti‍ve iodine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Near- total ‍thyroidectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Near- total thyroidectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Near- total thyroidectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> toxic multinodular goitre</strong>. The next step of management once a euthyroid state is achieved is a <strong>near-total thyroidectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Toxic nodular goiters are often large, uncomfortable, and are prone to recurrence. In a developing country like India, a near-total thyroidectomy eliminates the risk of recurrence and simplifies follow-up. However, it causes immediate thyroid failure and necessitates lifelong thyroxine replacement. </p>\n<p>Option A: Toxic multinodular goitres may show a decreased response to <strong>antithyroid drugs</strong> or may even <strong>enlarge further</strong>. Therefore, surgical management is recommended. </p>\n<p>Option B: Sub-total thyroidectomy has chances of recurrence and hence cannot be a definitive therapy.</p>\n<p>Option C: Large goiters show decreased response to radioactive iodine and the chances of recurrence are also high.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6661","text":"What will be the most like‍ly associated condition w​ith increased TSH and low​ T4 levels?","raw_text":"What will be the most likely associated condition with increased TSH and low T4 levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoparat‍hyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pituitar‍y Adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grave's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hashimoto's‍ thyroiditis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hashimoto's thyroiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hashimoto&#x27;s thyroiditis</strong></p><p><strong>Increased TSH</strong> and <strong>low T4</strong> levels are seen in<strong> primary hypothyroidism</strong>. <strong>Hashimoto's thyroiditis</strong> is the only cause of primary hypothyroidism in the given options. </p>\n<p><strong>Hashimoto's thyroiditis</strong> is an <strong>autoimmune disease</strong> that results in the destruction of the thyroid gland and gradual &amp; progressive thyroid failure. It<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of primary hypothyroidism in <strong>iodine-sufficient</strong> areas. They also have autoantibodies against thyroid receptors, thyroid peroxidase and thyroglobulin. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Hypoparathyroidism is generally not associated with hypothyroidism, except in certain congenital multisystem disorders such as DiGeorge's syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Grave's disease</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>hyper</strong>thyroidism. It is an autoimmune disorder caused due to autoantibodies against the TSH receptor. There will be elevated T3 and T4 levels with low TSH levels. </p>\n<p>Option B: A <strong>TSH-producing pituitary adenoma</strong> can have increased TSH levels, but there will be <strong>elevated T3, T4 </strong>levels as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6119","text":"A 50-year-old hypothyroid ‍female patient presents w​ith a painless, hard ante​rior neck mass, which pro‌gresses over weeks to yea‍rs associated with dyspha‍gia. Physical examination‌ reveals a hard, “woody” ‌thyroid gland with fixati​on to the surrounding tis‌sues. Which of the follow‍ing investigations will c‌onfirm the provisional di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old hypothyroid female patient presents with a painless, hard anterior neck mass, which progresses over weeks to years associated with dysphagia. Physical examination reveals a hard, “woody” thyroid gland with fixation to the surrounding tissues. Which of the following investigations will confirm the provisional diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CE‍CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RAI upt‍ake scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FN‍AC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Open ‍biopsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Open biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Open biopsy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Riedel’s thyroiditis, </strong> which is confirmed by an <strong>open thyroid biopsy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>FNAC </strong>is <strong>inadequate</strong> due to the <strong>firm and fibrous</strong> nature of the gland.</p>\n<p>Riedel’s thyroiditis is invasive autoimmune <strong>fibrous thyroiditis</strong> where thyroid gland tissue is replaced by fibrous tissue. It is an <strong>IgG4-related systemic disease</strong> characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated serum IgG4 levels</li>\n<li>Lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate</li>\n<li>Abundance of IgG4 bearing plasma cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is associated with other focal sclerosing syndromes including mediastinal, retroperitoneal, periorbital, retro-orbital fibrosis, and sclerosing cholangitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs predominantly in women between 30-60 years</li>\n<li>Painless, hard anterior neck mass</li>\n<li>Progresses over weeks to years to produce symptoms of compression:\n<ul>\n<li>Dysphagia</li>\n<li>Dyspnea</li>\n<li>Choking</li>\n<li>Hoarseness</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Symptoms of hypothyroidism and hypoparathyroidism as the gland is replaced by fibrous tissue</li>\n<li>Physical examination reveals a hard, “woody” thyroid gland with fixation to surrounding tissues</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>includes<strong> surgery, </strong>high-dose steroids, tamoxifen, and thyroxine replacement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7529","text":"The commonest surgical app‍roach to a retrosternal g​oiter is via:","raw_text":"The commonest surgical approach to a retrosternal goiter is via:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ne‍ck","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ch‍est","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median s‍ternotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‍the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neck","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neck</strong></p><p>The commonest <strong>surgical approach</strong> to a<strong> retrosternal goiter</strong> is via the <strong>neck </strong>and the most common type of goitre approached is the secondary retrosternal goiter.</p>\n<p>A goiter is considered to be <strong>retrosternal</strong> if <strong>at least 50%</strong> of the <strong>thyroid tissue</strong> is located <strong>intrathoracically.</strong> They can be primary or secondary.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 456px; height: 352px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.766px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary retrosternal goiter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.234px; text-align: center;\"><strong><strong>Secondary </strong></strong><strong>retrosternal goiter</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">Occurrence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.766px;\"><strong>1%</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.234px;\"><strong>Majority</strong> of retrosternal goiters </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">Origin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.766px;\">\n<p>Arise from <strong>ectopic thyroid tissue</strong> in the chest</p>\n<p><strong>No connection</strong> with the thyroid tissue in the neck</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.234px;\">Arise from the <strong>downward extension of cervical thyroid tissue</strong> along the fascial planes of the neck</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">Blood supply</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.766px;\"><strong>Intrathoracic </strong>blood vessels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.234px;\">Superior and inferior thyroid arteries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Retrosternal goiter presents with dyspnoea, stridor, and dilated neck veins. <strong>Pemberton’s sign</strong> (venous congestion of the neck on the raising of hands above the head) is seen.  </p>\n<p>Contrast-enhanced computed tomography<strong> (CECT) </strong>of the <strong>neck</strong> is performed to assess the location of the thyroid. The <strong>definitive treatment</strong> is surgical <strong>excision</strong> of the ectopic thyroid.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B &amp; C: <strong>Median sternotomy </strong>(via chest) is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Primary mediastinal</strong> goiter</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Malignant</strong> retrosternal goiter</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Very <strong>large retrosternal </strong>goiter</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Recurrence</strong> in the mediastinum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0816","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true about ret​rosternal goitre?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about retrosternal goitre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Operated in all patients r‍egardless of symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sternal incision is always‍ required to operate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It receives blood supply f‍rom the thoracodorsal art​ery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most of the retrosternal g‍oitres can be removed by ​a neck incision","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Most of the retrosternal goitres can be removed by a neck incision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Most of the retrosternal goitres can be removed by a neck incision</strong></p><p>Most of the retrosternal goiters can be removed by<strong> neck incision </strong>(transcervical approach).</p>\n<p>Retrosternal goitre is defined as having <strong>&gt;50%</strong> of the goiter <strong>below </strong>the<strong> suprasternal notch</strong>. It can be either <strong>primary</strong> or <strong>secondary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary:</strong> rare and arises from ectopic thyroid tissue in the mediastinum; receives blood supply from <strong>intrathoracic blood vessels</strong>. It is <strong>not related to thyroid tissue</strong> in the <strong>neck.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Secondary:</strong> they are the <strong>most common type;</strong> an extension of enlarged thyroid from the neck, usually arising from the lower pole of <strong>nodular</strong> goitre. It receives its blood supply from <strong>superior and inferior thyroid vessels</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The majority of patients are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> or have minimal symptoms. If the gland enlarges within the thoracic inlet, the <strong>pressure</strong> may lead to dysphagia, tracheal compression, and eventually airway symptoms. <strong>Pemberton sign</strong> can be positive (facial flushing and dilatation of cervical veins upon raising the arms above the head).</p>\n<p><strong>CECT</strong> neck and thorax is the <strong>investigation of choice.</strong></p>\n<p>Over 95% of retrosternal goiters can be removed via a transcervical incision since they are an extension from thyroid tissue in the neck.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Surgical removal of the retrosternal goiter is indicated in patients with significant <strong>airway compression,</strong> and in <strong>young</strong> <strong>patients</strong> who have symptoms such as <strong>dyspnoea</strong> or <strong>dysphagia. </strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Median sternotomy</strong> approach is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Primary mediastinal goiter</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Malignant retrosternal goitre</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Very large retrosternal goiter</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Recurrence in the mediastinum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For a primary mediastinal goiter whose boundaries are <strong>completely</strong> <strong>within the chest</strong>, resection is often achieved through <strong>median sternotomy</strong> or <strong>thoracotomy</strong> to ensure that the vessels supplying the tumor are properly ligated. Removing the tumor through a cervical approach can result in uncontrolled bleeding and is generally avoided. </p>\n<p>Option C: Blood supply to the retrosternal goiter is from the thyroid vessels (secondary retrosternal goiter).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5952","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients with thyroid swelli​ngs is least likely to re​quire surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with thyroid swellings is least likely to require surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 22 year old woman with F‍NAC report of Bethesda ca​tegory IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 66 year old man with a p‍ositive Pemberton’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 58 year old man with an ‍FNAC report of Thy2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 16 year old girl with re‍currence of thyroid cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 58 year old man with an FNAC report of Thy2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 58 year old man with an FNAC report of Thy2</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">FNAC report of Thy2 i.e. non-neoplastic is not an indication for surgery. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Indications</strong> for surgical management of thyroid swellings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Neoplasia: FNAC positive Thy3–5 and clinical suspicion\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Age</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Male sex</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hard texture</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Fixity</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Recurrent cyst</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Toxic adenoma</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pressure symptoms</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cosmesis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Patient’s wishes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7060","text":"Pick from the following th‍e usual cause of thyroid ​storm in thyroidectomy:","raw_text":"Pick from the following the usual cause of thyroid storm in thyroidectomy:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rough handling of thyroid ‍during surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Due to parat‍hyroid gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inadequate patient prepara‍tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infec‍tions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inadequate patient preparation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inadequate patient preparation</strong></p><p>The usual cause of thyroid storm in thyroidectomy is<strong> inadequate patient preparation</strong> before surgery. Patient preparation <strong>pre-operatively</strong> involves ensuring a <strong>euthyroid</strong> <strong>state</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Rough handling</strong> of the thyroid in thyroidectomy does <strong>not</strong> cause a<strong> thyroid storm</strong>. Though a significant amount of thyroglobulin is stored in the acinar cells, they are inactive unless converted to thyroxine (T3 and T4). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3875","text":"A patient with toxic adeno‍ma is being prepared for ​surgery. Which of the fol​lowing statements is wron‌g regarding this?","raw_text":"A patient with toxic adenoma is being prepared for surgery. Which of the following statements is wrong regarding this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodine may be given pre-op‍eratively with carbimazol​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta blockers administered‍ pre-operatively must be ​continued post operativel​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nadolol is given in a once‍ daily regimen pre-operat​ively","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The last dose of carbimazo‍le must be given 24 hours​ before surgery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The last dose of carbimazole must be given 24 hours before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The last dose of carbimazole must be given 24 hours before surgery</strong></p><p>The last dose of <strong>carbimazole</strong> may be given the <strong>evening before surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Toxic adenoma</strong> is a benign functioning thyroid tumour, which usually presents as a toxic solitary nodule. It is an autonomously functioning tumour that does not respond to TSH. Treatment involves <strong>antithyroid </strong>drugs or <strong>radioactive</strong> <strong>iodine</strong>. This is followed by <strong>hemithyroidectomy</strong> as the surrounding normal thyroid tissue regains normal function.</p>\n<p>Traditional <strong>preparation</strong> of a <strong>thyrotoxic patient</strong> aims to make the patient biochemically euthyroid. There are two methods of preparation that can be used:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Carbimazole </strong>30-40 mg per day is the <strong>drug of choice</strong>. When euthyroid (after 8-12 weeks), the dose may be reduced. Beta-blockers, if used preoperatively, must be given on the morning of surgery and continued for a week post-op.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Propranolol </strong>40 mg TDS or a longer-acting <strong>nadolol</strong> 160 mg once daily may be given to achieve <strong>beta-blockade</strong>. It is important to <strong>continue</strong> treatment up to <strong>7 days postoperatively</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Iodine may be given with carbimazole or a beta-blocker 10 days before the operation. It produces <strong>transient remission</strong> and reduces thyroid vascularity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0305","text":"A 60-year-old man was post‍ed for Hartley-Dunhill pr​ocedure. What is done in ​this procedure?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was posted for Hartley-Dunhill procedure. What is done in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total resection of lobes o‍n one side, with partial ​resection on the other si​de","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Partial resection of each ‍lobe removing the bulk of​ the gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total resection of one lob‍e with partial resection ​of three lobes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resection of two lobes wit‍h the removal of the isth​mus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Total resection of lobes on one side, with partial resection on the other side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Total resection of lobes on one side, with partial resection on the other side</strong></p><p>Hartley Dunhill procedure involves <strong>total lobectomy</strong> on the affected side with <strong>sub total resection</strong> on the less affected side. </p>\n<p>Indication: Asymmetrical multinodular goiters especially in older patients.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5010","text":"You are performing thyroid‍ectomy in a patient with ​thyroid neoplasia. Where ​will you make the Kocher'‌s incision?","raw_text":"You are performing thyroidectomy in a patient with thyroid neoplasia. Where will you make the Kocher's incision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 cm b‍elow A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 cm a‍bove A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 cm b‍elow B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 cm a‍bove B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 cm below B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43828ef104aa4dbfbbf5980816411c00.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 cm below B</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The structures marked as \"A\" and \"B\" in the above image are thyroid cartilage and cricoid cartilage respectively. A <strong>Kocher transverse collar incision</strong> is typically <strong>3 to 5 cm in length</strong> and is placed parallel to a natural skin crease <strong>1 cm below the cricoid cartilage</strong>, which is marked as \"B\".</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Rose position</strong> is preferred for thyroid surgery. The patient is supine with a towel roll below the shoulder to enable neck extension of 30 degrees. The advantage is a blood-less field as there is decreased venous congestion. However, there is an increased risk of air embolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3854","text":"At which of the following ‍points is the recurrent l​aryngeal nerve at the hig​hest risk for injury duri‌ng thyroid surgery?","raw_text":"At which of the following points is the recurrent laryngeal nerve at the highest risk for injury during thyroid surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At the level of Berry's li‍gament as it enters the l​arynx","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"In the tracheoesophageal g‍roove as it forms one sid​e of the Beahrs triangle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Under the tubercle of Zuck‍erkandl on the posterolat​eral portion of the gland​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"As the nerve loops around ‍the aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. At the level of Berry's ligament as it enters the larynx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28da1f896a154945890d49b47ff39067x380x375.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. At the level of Berry&#x27;s ligament as it enters the larynx</strong></p><p>The point at which the recurrent laryngeal nerve (<strong>RLN</strong>) is at the<strong> highest risk for injury</strong> during thyroid surgery is at its <strong>entry point into the larynx</strong>. This point is at the cricothyroid joint, at the<strong> level of Berry's ligament.</strong></p>\n<p>The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus nerve. On the left, it recurs over the <strong>arch of aorta</strong>, and on the right over the <strong>subclavian artery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Beahrs' triangle</strong> or recurrent laryngeal nerve triangle is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common carotid artery</li>\n<li>Inferior thyroid arteries</li>\n<li>Recurrent laryngeal nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: On the left side, the nerve has more distance in which to reach the transesophageal groove. Hence it runs in a <strong>medial</strong> plane. On the right, there is less distance and the nerve runs more obliquely to reach the groove. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0306","text":"In the post-op recovery ro‍om, a patient who had und​ergone a thyroidectomy su​ddenly experienced dyspne‌a with stridor. He was im‍mediately sent back to th‍e OT. What is the most li‌kely cause for the patien‌t's condition?","raw_text":"In the post-op recovery room, a patient who had undergone a thyroidectomy suddenly experienced dyspnea with stridor. He was immediately sent back to the OT. What is the most likely cause for the patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diaphragmatic paralysis du‍e to hypocalcemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyrotoxicosis causing unc‍ontrolled atrial fibrilla​tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous ‍embolism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Development‍ of hematoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Development of hematoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Development of hematoma</strong></p><p>Development of a <strong>hematoma</strong> post-thyroidectomy may lead to <strong>venous edema </strong>of the <strong>larynx. </strong>This can cause <strong>airway obstruction </strong>and<strong> respiratory distress. </strong>It is evacuated promptly at bedside or through surgical re-exploration.</p>\n<p>Complications of thyroidectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Haemorrhage - most common complication overall</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nerve injury </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">External laryngeal nerve - hoarseness (more common)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Recurrent laryngeal nerve - hoarseness if unilateral, life-threatening aspiration and aphonia if bilateral</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory obstruction</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laryngeal edema - most common cause of respiratory obstruction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laryngomalacia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tension hematoma</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoparathyroidism - infarction of parathyroids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thyroid storm - inadequately prepared for surgery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Recurrent thyrotoxicosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wound infection, stitch granuloma, keloid </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Venous embolism</strong> typically occurs after a <strong>prolonged</strong> period of <strong>immobilization.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4436","text":"A patient who had undergon‍e a retro-oesophagal goit​re resection was found to​ have partial ptosis and ‌loss of hemifacial sweati‍ng. What is the most like‍ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient who had undergone a retro-oesophagal goitre resection was found to have partial ptosis and loss of hemifacial sweating. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transient h‍ypocalcemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Horner's‍ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerves‍ injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post operat‍ive hematoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Horner's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Horner&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>cervical sympathetic trunk</strong> is at risk of <strong>injury</strong> in invasive thyroid cancers and retro-esophageal goitres and may result in Horner's syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"W_qKPT9I","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"13_non_depolarising_muscle_relaxants","title":"13.Non Depolarising Muscle Relaxants","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MC3996","text":"What is the mechanism of a​ction of the curare group​ of muscle relaxants?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of the curare group of muscle relaxants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistently depolarizing ​at neuromuscular junction​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Competitively blocking the​ binding of ACh to its re​ceptors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Repetitive stimulation of ​ACh receptors on muscle e​ndplate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibiting the calcium cha​nnels on presynaptic memb​rane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Competitively blocking the binding of ACh to its receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Competitively blocking the binding of ACh to its receptors</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">The <strong>curare</strong> group of drugs includes <strong>non-depolarizing</strong> neuromuscular blocking agents that act by <strong>competitively</strong> <strong>blocking </strong>the<strong> binding </strong>of<strong> acetylcholine </strong>to its receptors. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">This leads to a decrease in the effect of acetylcholine and thus, neuronal transmission to the muscle does not occur. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3031","text":"Following administration o​f which of the following ​neuromuscular blockers, i‌s a patient is likely to ​recover fastest?","raw_text":"Following administration of which of the following neuromuscular blockers, is a patient is likely to recover fastest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cis-atr​acurium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mivacurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mivacurium</strong></p><p><strong>Mivacurium</strong> is the <strong>shortest acting</strong> non-depolarizing muscle relaxant among the given options. Thus, the fastest recovery will occur on the administration of mivacurium. </p>\n<p>Gantacurium is the shortest acting non-depolarizing muscle relaxant. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0451","text":"Which of the following non​-depolarising neuromuscul​ar blockers has the faste‌st onset of action?","raw_text":"Which of the following non-depolarising neuromuscular blockers has the fastest onset of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rocuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Rocuronium</strong> has the <strong>fastest onset</strong> of action among <strong>non-depolarising</strong> blockers. </p>\n<p><strong>Succinylcholine</strong>'s onset is <strong>comparable</strong> with rocuronium, but succinylcholine is a <strong>depolarising</strong> blocker. </p>\n<p>The onset of action:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rocuronium- 0.5-2 minutes </li>\n<li>Succinylcholine- 0.8-1.4 minutes </li>\n<li>Mivacurium- 2-3 minutes </li>\n<li>Vecuronium- 2-3 minutes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3074","text":"Which of the following is ​not a property of gantacu​rium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a property of gantacurium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed-onium fumarate compo​und","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Undergoes enzymatic degrad​ation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adduction products formed ​with L-cysteine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Available as lyophilized p​owder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Undergoes enzymatic degradation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Undergoes enzymatic degradation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Gantacurium</strong> is an ultra-short acting, rapid-onset, non-depolarizing muscle relaxant that undergoes <strong>non-enzymatic degradation. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It belongs to the <strong>mixed-onium fumarate</strong> group. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">L-cysteine adduction results in the formation of a heterocyclic ring that cannot interact with the Ach receptor in the post-junctional space. The administration of <strong>L-cysteine </strong>can thus rapidly <strong>inactivate</strong> fumarate compounds and <strong>reverse</strong> neuromuscular <strong>blockade.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is available as a lyophilized powder which must be reconstituted before use. It is unstable as an aqueous solution. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: Atracurium, cis-atracurium and gantacurium undergo non-enzymatic degradation. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3032","text":"A 33-year-old lady with in​fertility is taken up for​ laparoscopic ovarian dri‌lling in outpatient surge​ry. Mivacurium is adminis‍tered under daycare anest​hesia. Why is this drug p​referred in this scenario‌?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old lady with infertility is taken up for laparoscopic ovarian drilling in outpatient surgery. Mivacurium is administered under daycare anesthesia. Why is this drug preferred in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid ab​sorption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid hy​drolysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid e​xcretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid co​njugation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rapid hydrolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rapid hydrolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Mivacurium </strong>is preferred as it undergoes <strong>rapid hydrolysis</strong> by <strong>butyrylcholinesterase</strong> (plasma cholinesterase/ pseudocholinesterase).</p>\n<p>Mivacurium is a <strong>short-acting</strong> muscle relaxant preferred in day-care anesthesia. It has an <strong>onset</strong> of action within <strong>2-3 minutes</strong> and a <strong>duration</strong> of action of <strong>10-20 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with atypical pseudocholinesterase will have a prolonged duration of the block similar to succinylcholine administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3034","text":"Which of the following is ​an incorrect statement re​garding mivacurium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding mivacurium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be used in a case of r​aised ICP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolized by pseudocholi​nesterase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Causes hista​mine release","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elimination is kidney func​tion dependent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Elimination is kidney function dependent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Elimination is kidney function dependent</strong></p><p><strong>Elimination</strong> of mivacurium is <strong>independent</strong> of <strong>kidney function.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Mivacurium</strong> is a short-acting, non-depolarizing muscle relaxant. It is the only non-depolarizing agent that is metabolized by <strong>pseudocholinesterase</strong> (butyrylcholinesterase).</p>\n<p>It is <strong>safe</strong> to use in a patient with or at risk of <strong>intracranial hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can cause <strong>histamine release</strong> but in lesser quantities as compared to tubocurarine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3033","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is least suitable for ​muscle relaxation in long‌ procedures?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is least suitable for muscle relaxation in long procedures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cis-atr​acurium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pipecu​ronium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cis-atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cis-atracurium</strong></p><p><strong>Cis–atracurium</strong> is an <strong>intermediate-acting</strong> muscle relaxant, and would be unsuitable for long procedures. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3041","text":"Which of the following mus​cle relaxants can cause s​eizures?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle relaxants can cause seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atracurium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Atracurium</strong> can cause <strong>seizures</strong> due to the accumulation of its metabolite, <strong>laudanosine</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Laudanosine, a tertiary amine, is a breakdown product of atracurium's <strong>Hofmann elimination.</strong> It has <strong>CNS-stimulating</strong> properties and can result in precipitation of <strong>seizures </strong>but this is significant only with <strong>large doses</strong> and <strong>hepatic failure</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cisatracurium is a stereoisomer of atracurium, which results in lesser laudanosine production than atracurium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3047","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding atrac​urium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding atracurium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixture of 10 optical isom​ers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safe in a​sthmatics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Undergoes spontaneous non-​enzymatic degradation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemically incompatible wi​th thiopental","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Safe in asthmatics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Safe in asthmatics</strong></p><p><strong>Atracurium</strong> is <strong>avoided</strong> in asthmatic patients because it is associated with <strong>histamine release</strong> and <strong>bronchospasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is metabolized by <strong>Hofmann elimination</strong> and non-specific ester hydrolysis. Hofmann elimination is a spontaneous non-enzymatic degradation of atracurium into laudanosine and mono-quaternary acrylate. Laudanosine has CNS stimulating properties and can result in precipitation of seizures.</p>\n<p>Atracurium precipitates as a free acid if it is introduced into an intravenous line containing an alkaline solution such as thiopental. Thus, the 2 drugs are chemically incompatible. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3030","text":"Which of the following dru​gs does not have a steroi​d ring in its structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not have a steroid ring in its structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atracurium</strong></p><p><strong>Atracurium</strong> is a <strong>benzylisoquinoline</strong> derivative.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Classification of non-depolarizing muscle relaxants according to structure:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li><strong>Steroidal</strong> compounds:\n<ol>\n<li>Pancuronium</li>\n<li>Pipecuronium</li>\n<li>Vecuronium</li>\n<li>Rocuronium</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Benzylisoquinoline</strong> compounds:\n<ol>\n<li>d - Tubocurarine</li>\n<li>Metacurine</li>\n<li>Doxacurium</li>\n<li>Atracurium</li>\n<li>Cisatracurium</li>\n<li>Mivacurium</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Onium chlorofumarate</strong> compounds - Gantacurium </li>\n<li><strong>Phenolic ethers</strong> - Gallamine</li>\n<li><strong>Diallyl</strong> derivative of <strong>toxiferine</strong> - Alcuronium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3046","text":"Which of the following is ​a true statement regardin​g cis-atracurium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement regarding cis-atracurium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No laudanosi​ne production","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Less potent than atracuriu​m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contraindicated in renal f​ailure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No histamin​e production","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No histamine production","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No histamine production</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cisatracurium does not cause <strong>histamine</strong> release, <strong>atracurium</strong> does.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: Cisatracurium produces laudanosine, but it is about five times lesser than that of atracurium.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: Cisatracurium is a stereoisomer of atracurium that is four times <strong>more potent.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Cisatracurium undergoes <strong>Hofmann elimination</strong> which is a spontaneous non-enzymatic organ-independent degradation (independent of renal function). Hence it is the <strong>drug of choice </strong>in<strong> renal failure </strong>patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3027","text":"A 7-year-old child is post​ed for an elective tonsil​lectomy. Which drug will ‌you use to facilitate his​ intubation?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child is posted for an elective tonsillectomy. Which drug will you use to facilitate his intubation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suxame​thonium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rocuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Rocuronium</strong> is the drug of choice during routine intubation in<strong> pediatric patients</strong> due to its <strong>faster onset</strong> of action and its availability as an intramuscular injection.</p>\n<p>Succinylcholine is avoided for routine, elective intubation in children due to its side effects. The only indication for intravenous <strong>succinylcholine</strong> in children is<strong> rapid sequence</strong><strong> induction </strong>with a “full” stomach and <strong>laryngospasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The durations of action of vecuronium and pancuronium are prolonged in children due to immature hepatic function.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3040","text":"Which is the ideal muscle ​relaxant for a 2-day old ​neonate planned for surge‌ry?","raw_text":"Which is the ideal muscle relaxant for a 2-day old neonate planned for surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atracurium</strong></p><p><strong>Atracurium</strong> (or cisatracurium) is the ideal muscle relaxant for <strong>neonates</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neonates have <strong>immature hepatic</strong> and <strong>renal</strong> functions. Atracurium and cisatracurium undergo Hofmann elimination, which is spontaneous non-organ dependent non-enzymatic degradation. These drugs are independent of hepatic or renal excretion. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3050","text":"Which of the following mus​cle relaxants is vagolyti​c?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle relaxants is vagolytic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"d-tuboc​urarine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Pancuronium</strong> is <strong>vagolytic</strong> (resulting from <strong>blockade of M2</strong> receptors) leading to increased heart rate and hence increased blood pressure and cardiac output.</p>\n<p>The vagolytic action of pancuronium may be used to <strong>counteract opioid-induced bradycardia</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px; width: 767px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Effect</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stimulation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Blockade</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Autonomic ganglia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px;\">Succinylcholine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px;\">d-Tubocurarine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Cardiac </strong><strong>muscarinic receptor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px;\">Succinylcholine </td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px;\">\n<p>Rocuronium (weak)</p>\n<p>Pancuronium (moderate)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Histamine release</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px;\">\n<p>Succinylcholine, mivacurium, atracurium (weak)</p>\n<p>d-tubocurarine (significant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> <strong>Gallamine</strong> has the most potent <strong>vagolytic</strong> properties of any relaxant but is<strong> not in use.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3052","text":"A 50-year-old man with a h​istory of myocardial infa​rction 1 year back presen‌ted with acute abdominal ​pain. He is found to have‍ a perforated peptic ulce​r and is planned for an e​mergency laparotomy. Whic‌h muscle relaxant would y​ou choose to use in him?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction 1 year back presented with acute abdominal pain. He is found to have a perforated peptic ulcer and is planned for an emergency laparotomy. Which muscle relaxant would you choose to use in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succinyl​ choline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"d-tuboc​urarine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vecuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vecuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Vecuronium</strong> is devoid of significant cardiovascular effects and is the <strong>most cardiostable</strong> muscle relaxant, making it the muscle relaxant of choice in <strong>cardiac patients</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 188px; width: 760px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.234px;\"><strong>Effect</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.594px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stimulation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.172px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Blockade</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.234px;\"><strong>Autonomic ganglia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.594px;\">Succinylcholine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.172px;\">d-Tubocurarine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.234px;\"><strong>Cardiac </strong><strong>muscarinic receptor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.594px;\">Succinylcholine </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.172px;\">\n<p>Rocuronium (weak)</p>\n<p>Pancuronium (moderate)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.234px;\"><strong>Histamine release</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.594px;\">\n<p>Succinylcholine, mivacurium, atracurium (weak)</p>\n<p>d-tubocurarine (significant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.172px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3044","text":"While conducting a pre-ane​sthetic check, you note t​he patient's creatinine t‌o be 2.3 mg/dl. Which mus​cle relaxant is to be avo‍ided in her?","raw_text":"While conducting a pre-anesthetic check, you note the patient's creatinine to be 2.3 mg/dl. Which muscle relaxant is to be avoided in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cis atr​acurium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancuronium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Pancuronium</strong> is avoided as it is predominantly <strong>eliminated</strong> by the <strong>kidney</strong> and hence, can accumulate in renal failure or imapirment.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Atracurium and cisatracurium undergo Hofmann elimination, i.e., spontaneous non-enzymatic degradation which is non-organ dependant.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Metabolism of mivacurium is independent of the kidney function as it is hydrolyzed by butyrylcholinesterase.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3068","text":"Which of the following is ​an incorrect statement co​ncerning rapacuronium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement concerning rapacuronium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid onset and short dura​tion of action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimal cardiovascular sid​e effects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Safe in a​sthmatics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-depolarising muscle re​laxant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Safe in asthmatics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Safe in asthmatics</strong></p><p>Rapacuronium is a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant that has a very high incidence of<strong> bronchospasm </strong>(&gt;9%) and so it is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in<strong> asthmatics</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has been <strong>withdrawn</strong> from the market due to this effect.</p>\n<p>Rapacuronium has a rapid onset and short duration of action and minimal cardiovascular side effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3048","text":"Which of the following dru​gs produces maximum gangl​ion blockade?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs produces maximum ganglion blockade?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"d-Tuboc​urarine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. d-Tubocurarine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. d-Tubocurarine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>d-Tubocurarine</strong> produces <strong>maximum ganglionic blockade</strong> leading to <strong>hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><em>d</em>-Tubocurarine is the <strong>most potent histamine releaser</strong> among muscle relaxants leading to bronchospasm, hypotension, and wheals. The effects of the ganglionic blockade and histamine release can lead to severe hypotension. Hence, d-tubocurarine had been <strong>withdrawn</strong> from the market.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Succinylcholine stimulates the vagal ganglion, leading to bradycardia, and stimulates the sympathetic ganglion, causing hypertension and tachycardia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pancuronium is vagolytic and produces sympathetic stimulation leading to tachycardia.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px; width: 767px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Effect</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Stimulation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Blockade</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Autonomic ganglia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px; height: 18px;\">Succinylcholine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px; height: 18px;\">d-Tubocurarine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 58px;\"><strong>Cardiac </strong><strong>muscarinic receptor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px; height: 58px;\">Succinylcholine </td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px; height: 58px;\">\n<p>Rocuronium (weak)</p>\n<p>Pancuronium (moderate)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 96px;\"><strong>Histamine release</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 305.312px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Succinylcholine, mivacurium, atracurium (weak)</p>\n<p>d-tubocurarine (significant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264.688px; height: 96px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3036","text":"A 65-year-old woman suspec​ted to have ovarian cance​r is set to undergo a dia‌gnostic laparotomy under ​general anesthesia. Durin‍g the pre-op evaluation, ​you note that the patient​ has a 5-year history of ‌diabetic nephropathy. Whi​ch of the following drugs​ will you prefer to use i​n her case?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman suspected to have ovarian cancer is set to undergo a diagnostic laparotomy under general anesthesia. During the pre-op evaluation, you note that the patient has a 5-year history of diabetic nephropathy.  Which of the following drugs will you prefer to use in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pipecu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cis-atr​acurium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Doxac​urium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cis-atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cis-atracurium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Cis-atracurium</strong> is preferred in patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong> as it does not depend on the kidney for excretion.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Both atracurium and cis-atracurium undergo <strong>Hofmann</strong> <strong>elimination </strong>and are thus safe in renal disease. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Steroidal muscle relaxants are predominantly eliminated by ultrafiltration by the glomeruli before urinary excretion. Doxacurium, pancuronium, and pipecuronium are partially excreted by the kidneys, so the neuromuscular block is prolonged in patients with renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3045","text":"A patient with chronic hep​atitis B is posted for an​ appendectomy. His pre-op‌ investigations show a de​ranged LFT. Which muscle ‍relaxant will you choose ​to use in him?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic hepatitis B is posted for an appendectomy.  His pre-op investigations show a deranged LFT. Which muscle relaxant will you choose to use in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pipecu​ronium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atracurium</strong></p><p><strong>Atracurium </strong>and<strong> c</strong><strong>isatracurium</strong> are the muscle relaxants of choice in <strong>liver disease</strong> because of non-hepatic metabolism.</p>\n<p>Atracurium and cisatracurium undergo <strong>Hofmann elimination. </strong>The clearance of both drugs is independent of liver function. Hence, they are the agents of choice in liver failure patients.</p>\n<p>Pancuronium, rocuronium, and vecuronium are dependent on the liver for excretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3039","text":"Following the administrati​on of a muscle relaxant, ​a patient developed eryth‌ema and flushing of the f​ace, neck, and upper body‍, with a drop in blood pr​essure. Which drug was mo​st likely administered?","raw_text":"Following the administration of a muscle relaxant, a patient developed erythema and flushing of the face, neck, and upper body, with a drop in blood pressure. Which drug was most likely administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atracurium</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>histamine</strong> release. <strong>Atracurium </strong>is the most potent histamine releaser among the given options and was likely administered.</p>\n<p>Erythema, flushing, and hypotension are manifestations of histamine release.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3060","text":"During recovery from anest​hesia, which of the follo​wing drugs cannot be used‌ for reversal of neuromus​cular blockade?","raw_text":"During recovery from anesthesia, which of the following drugs cannot be used for reversal of neuromuscular blockade?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neost​igmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sugam​madex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glycopy​rrolate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridos​tigmine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glycopyrrolate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glycopyrrolate</strong></p><p>Anticholinergic drugs like <strong>glycopyrrolate</strong> and <strong>atropine</strong> are not used for the reversal of neuromuscular blockade. They only <strong>attenuate</strong> the undesirable <strong>muscarinic effects</strong> of anticholinesterase reversal agents.  </p>\n<p><strong>Reversal agents</strong> commonly used in clinical practice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cholinesterase inhibitors -</strong> Neostigmine (most common), edrophonium, and pyridostigmine.</li>\n<li><strong>Sugammadex</strong>, a cyclodextrin, is the first selective relaxant binding agent for rocuronium and vecuronium.</li>\n<li><strong>L-cysteine</strong> causes the inactivation of gantacurium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3077","text":"Which of the following mus​cles is the last to recov​er from the effects of ne‌uromuscular blockade?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is the last to recover from the effects of neuromuscular blockade?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbicula​ris oculi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laryngeal​ adductors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diap​hragm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adductor​ pollicis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adductor pollicis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adductor pollicis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Adductor pollicis</strong> is the <strong>last to recover</strong> from neuromuscular blockade among the given choices.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most sensitive to neuromuscular blockade:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Diaphragm and abdominal muscles</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Peripheral muscles of the limbs</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Orbicularis oculi muscle</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Geniohyoid</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Masseter</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Upper airway muscles.</li>\n</ol>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: <strong>Sensitivity</strong> and <strong>recovery</strong> are <strong>not</strong> exactly the <strong>same</strong>. Even though the laryngeal adductors recover sooner than adductor pollicis, recovery of adductor pollicis function does not exactly parallel the complete recovery of muscles required to maintain an airway. This may lead to<strong> dynamic obstruction postoperatively.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3082","text":"Which of the following is ​the best sign to assess a​dequate reversal of neuro‌muscular blockade in a pa​tient recovering from GA?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best sign to assess adequate reversal of neuromuscular blockade in a patient recovering from GA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sustained head lift &amp;gt; 3​ seconds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protrusion o​f the tongue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sustained leg lift &amp;gt; 3 ​seconds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Train of four ratio &amp;gt; 0​.9","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Train of four ratio > 0.9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Train of four ratio &gt; 0.9</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>best sign</strong> of recovery from neuromuscular blockade is a <strong>train of four ratio &gt;0.9</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The TOF ratio must exceed 0.90 to exclude clinically important residual neuromuscular block.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical signs consistent with recovery are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sustained head lift for 5 seconds</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sustained leg lift for 5 seconds</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sustained handgrip for 5 seconds</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sustained “tongue depressor test”</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maximum inspiratory pressure 40 to 50 cm H2O or greater</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0453","text":"Neostigmine is administere​d following a laparoscopi​c procedure to reverse th‌e neuromuscular blockade.​ This would result in all‍ of the following effects​ in the patient except:","raw_text":"Neostigmine is administered following a laparoscopic procedure to reverse the neuromuscular blockade. This would result in all of the following effects in the patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreasing the breakdown o​f acetylcholine at the mo​tor end plate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preventing the potassium e​fflux from the cell","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increasing the release of ​acetylcholine at the moto​r end plate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depolarization at the moto​r end plate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Preventing the potassium efflux from the cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Preventing the potassium efflux from the cell</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neostigmine has<strong> no effect </strong>on<strong> potassium efflux.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neostigmine is an <strong>acetylcholine esterase inhibitor. </strong>Its <strong>primary mechanism</strong> of action is decreased breakdown of acetylcholine at the motor endplate.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Minor actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increases the release of acetylcholine from motor endplates.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Direct depolarization of the postjunctional motor endplate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is used for the <strong>reversal</strong> of neuromuscular blockade produced by <strong>non-depolarizing</strong> muscle <strong>relaxants</strong> used during anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Note: Cobra venom consists of a neurotoxin that is responsible for respiratory paralysis which similar to that produced by a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant. A combination of artificial respiration and neostigmine is useful in resuscitation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3063","text":"Before shifting a patient ​to the PACU, the anesthet​ist administers atropine ‌along with neostigmine. W​hat is the purpose of doi‍ng this?","raw_text":"Before shifting a patient to the PACU, the anesthetist administers atropine along with neostigmine. What is the purpose of doing this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevent metabolism of neos​tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Block muscarinic side effe​cts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enhance ​excretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enhance​ potency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Block muscarinic side effects","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Block muscarinic side effects</strong></p><p><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> like atropine, glycopyrrolate are routinely administered <strong>with anticholinesterases</strong> in order to<strong> attenuate </strong>the undesirable<strong> muscarinic effects.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3066","text":"As you enter the OT, the s​enior resident asks you t​o administer Sugammadex t‌o a patient being shifted​ to the post-op ward. Whi‍ch muscle relaxant was li​kely to have been adminis​tered?","raw_text":"As you enter the OT, the senior resident asks you to administer Sugammadex to a patient being shifted to the post-op ward. Which muscle relaxant was likely to have been administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mivac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrac​urium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rocuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Sugammadex</strong> is used for the reversal of <strong>rocuronium. </strong></p>\n<p>Sugammadex is a cyclodextrin. It is the first <strong>selective relaxant binding</strong> agent. It exerts its reversal effect by forming tight complexes in a 1:1 ratio with steroidal non-depolarizing agents (rocuronium &gt; vecuronium &gt;&gt; pancuronium).</p>\n<p>It is <strong>eliminated unchanged </strong>via the<strong> kidneys.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0677","text":"A patient is given a nicot​inic receptor antagonist ​as a muscle relaxant. Whi‌ch drug is given postoper​atively to recover from m‍uscle weakness?","raw_text":"A patient is given a nicotinic receptor antagonist as a muscle relaxant. Which drug is given postoperatively to recover from muscle weakness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physos​tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neost​igmine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carb​achol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neostigmine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neostigmine</strong></p><p><strong>Neostigmine</strong> is given postoperatively to <strong>recover</strong> from <strong>muscle weakness.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 509\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Non-depolarizing muscle relaxants <strong>(NDMRs</strong>) act mainly by the <strong>competitive antagonism</strong> of <strong>nicotinic </strong>(Nm) receptors. Neostigmine<strong> inhibits acetylcholinesterase</strong> and inhibits the degradation of acetylcholine. The increased accumulation of undegraded acetylcholine can effectively compete with NDMRs, and thereby displace them from the site and antagonize their effects.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"1b7Fs7aS","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"13_nervous_and_endocrine_systems","title":"13.Nervous And Endocrine Systems","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB2268","text":"Which of the following con‌stitutes neurites of the ​neurons?","raw_text":"Which of the following constitutes neurites of the neurons?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofibrils and dendrites‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dendrites‌ and axons","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofibril‌s and axons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurofibrils, dendrites an‌d axons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dendrites and axons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c03f4054313044d28652c72a7580842fx1280x999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dendrites and axons</strong></p><p>The <strong>neurites </strong>of the neurons constitute the<strong> dendrites </strong>and the<strong> axon.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neurites</strong> or <strong>neuronal processes</strong> refer to any projection arising from the cell body (soma) of a neuron. They are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Axon </strong>- a single, long projection arising from the cell body that carries the impulse away from the cell body. The axon ends by dividing into several branches called <strong>axon</strong> <strong>terminals,</strong> which synapses with the dendrites of another neuron. An axon may also end in relation to an effector organ such as muscle or a gland. It is free of Nissl granules.</li>\n<li><strong>Dendrites </strong>- multiple short projections arising from the cell body, that carry the nerve impulse towards the neuron. Nissl granules are present here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>neuronal cytoskeleton</strong> consists of <strong>neurofilaments</strong> and <strong>microtubules.</strong> These are abundantly seen in the cell body, dendrites and the axon. The neurofilaments are heteropolymers of proteins that give tensile strength to the axon. Bundles of neurofilaments constitute a <strong>neurofibril. Microtubules</strong> assist in <strong>axonal transport.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2269","text":"What is the Nissl-free zon‌e from where an axon aris​es from a neuron called?","raw_text":"What is the Nissl-free zone from where an axon arises from a neuron called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initial‌ segment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mes‌axon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axon h‌illock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nodes of‌ Ranvier","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Axon hillock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf7b713807c946a8bd031254016a1ebex1280x999.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2d80160ac36420da36c947e47724e96x1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Axon hillock</strong></p><p>The <strong>Nissl-free zone</strong> of a neuron from where an axon arises is called an <strong>axon hillock.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Nissl bodies</strong> or granules<strong> </strong>are aggregates of <strong>polyribosomes</strong> and rough <strong>endoplasmic reticulum. </strong>They are present in the cell body of a neuron and in large dendrites<strong>. </strong>Under light microscopy, they appear as <strong>basophilic</strong> <strong>granules</strong>. The affinity towards the basic dyes is due to the presence of a large amount of RNA which is acidic in nature. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a photomicrograph of bluish-coloured Nissl bodies (indicated by arrows) in the cytoplasm of motor neurons.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>initial segment</strong> refers to the <strong>proximal part</strong> of the <strong>axon,</strong> that lies just beyond the axon-hillock. Thus, an axon-hillock is part of the cell body while the initial segment is part of an axon.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Mesaxon </strong>refers to the area where the <strong>plasma membrane</strong> of the <strong>Schwann cell</strong> fuses with itself around the axon. During the formation of the myelin sheath, the axon invaginates the Schwann cell. The plasma membrane of the Schwann cell fuses with itself and continues to wrap around the axon to form multiple layers of the plasma membrane called the myelin sheath. The cytoplasm and nucleus of the Schwann cell are pushed to the periphery. The thin layer of the cytoplasm of the Schwann cell surrounding the myelin sheath is called <strong>neurilemma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Each Schwann cell myelinates only a short segment of the axon. Hence, along the length of the axon, there are several Schwann cells myelinating it. The <strong>unmyelinated</strong> <strong>portions</strong> of the<strong> axon</strong> present in between myelinated segments are known as<strong> nodes of Ranvier.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2276","text":"A 23-year-old patient was ‌referred for evaluation o​f a progressive left-hand‌ weakness and pain involv‌ing his right medial elbo‍w, forearm, wrist and thu‍mb. Suspecting pure neuri‌tic leprosy, a nerve biop​sy was taken and the imag‌e of the histological sec‍tion is given below. What​ is the stain used in thi​s section?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old patient was referred for evaluation of a progressive left-hand weakness and pain involving his right medial elbow, forearm, wrist and thumb. Suspecting pure neuritic leprosy, a nerve biopsy was taken and the image of the histological section is given below. What is the stain used in this section?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sudan‌ Black","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gie‌msa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osmium t‌etroxide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Silver‌ stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osmium tetroxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/516ab3dc4c314618a59497a86312c2b1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osmium tetroxide</strong></p><p>The stain used in this section is <strong>osmium tetroxide. </strong></p>\n<p>Osmium tetroxide is reduced to a black substance by <strong>unsaturated fatty acids</strong> present in the <strong>myelin sheath</strong> of the nerve fibers and hence, appears <strong>black</strong> on the section.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pure neuritic form</strong> is a variant of leprosy characterized by the <strong>isolated </strong>involvement of<strong> peripheral nerve trunks</strong> with the absence of skin lesions. <strong>Nerve biopsy</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> method for the diagnosis of pure neuritic leprosy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2285","text":"Identify the types of nerv‌e fibers from images A an​d B.","raw_text":"Identify the types of nerve fibers from images A and B.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-myelinated &amp;amp; B-unmye‌linated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-unmyelinated &amp;amp; B-mye‌linated","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-myelinated &amp;amp; B-myeli‌nated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-unmyelinated &amp;amp; B-unm‌yelinated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-unmyelinated & B-myelinated","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/30fa22af243f4448bcbcd709bd95fe79.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-unmyelinated &amp; B-myelinated</strong></p><p>In the above-given images showing the cross-section of the nerve fibers stained with H &amp; E,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Image A</strong> shows an <strong>unmyelinated nerve</strong> identified by the absent empty spaces around nerve fibers.</li>\n<li><strong>Image B</strong> shows a <strong>myelinated nerve</strong> identified by the empty spaces around the nerve fibers (marked with arrows).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Staining of myelin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H&amp;E stain</strong> - Fails to colour the myelin (shows as white region/empty space around nerve fiber). </li>\n<li><strong>Special stains</strong> for Myelin such as Luxol Fast Blue, Sudan blue can stain but with less intensity. </li>\n<li><strong>Osmium</strong> <strong>tetroxide</strong> stains myelin black.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2290","text":"Match the marked structure‌s to the correct layers o​f the cerebellar cortex. ‌A 1. White matter B 2. Mo‌lecular layer C 3. Purkin‍je layer D 4. Granular la‍yer","raw_text":"<p>Match the marked structures to the correct layers of the cerebellar cortex.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 383px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 305.188px;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.812px;\">1. White matter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 305.188px;\">B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.812px;\">2. Molecular layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 305.188px;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.812px;\">3. Purkinje layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 305.188px;\">D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.812px;\">4. Granular layer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A - 2, B - 4, C - 3, D - 1‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A - 2, B - 3, C - 4, D - 1‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A - 3, B - 2, C - 4, D - 1‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A - 3, B - 4, C - 2, D - 1‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A - 2, B - 3, C - 4, D - 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3ee8d780dbb420b8c9f49581d0566c0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a58dd532c1f4493b944d5dad57dfa23x1280x944.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A - 2, B - 3, C - 4, D - 1</strong></p><p>The structures marked in the given image are:</p>\n<p>A - Molecular layer</p>\n<p>B - Purkinje layer</p>\n<p>C - Granular layer</p>\n<p>D - White matter</p>\n<p>The <strong>cerebellum</strong> is covered by pia mater which is seen as thin connective tissue, with blood vessels lying under it. The cerebellum consists of an <strong>outer grey matter</strong> (cerebellar cortex) and <strong>inner white matter</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2403","text":"Arrange the following laye‌rs of the cerebral cortex​ in the correct order fro‌m superficial to deep. Mo‌lecular layer External py‍ramidal layer Internal gr‍anular layer External gra‌nular layer Multiform lay​er Ganglionic layer","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following layers of the cerebral cortex in the correct order from superficial to deep.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Molecular layer</li>\n<li>External pyramidal layer</li>\n<li>Internal granular layer</li>\n<li>External granular layer</li>\n<li>Multiform layer</li>\n<li>Ganglionic layer</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-3-2‌-4-6-5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-5-2‌-3-4-6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-4-2‌-3-6-5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-6-2‌-5-3-4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-4-2-3-6-5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-4-2-3-6-5</strong></p><p>The correct order of the layers of the cerebral cortex from superficial to deep are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Molecular layer (Plexiform layer)</li>\n<li>External granular layer</li>\n<li>External pyramidal layer</li>\n<li>Internal granular layer - contains the outer band of Baillarger</li>\n<li>Ganglionic layer (Internal Pyramidal layer) - contains the inner band of Baillarger</li>\n<li>Multiform layer (Layer of Polymorphic cells)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2326","text":"In which layer of the cere‌bral cortex is the outer ​band of Baillarger seen?","raw_text":"In which layer of the cerebral cortex is the outer band of Baillarger seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External pyr‌amidal layer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal gr‌anular layer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Internal pyr‌amidal layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External gr‌anular layer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal granular layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5372cd1558e49dbb24948ed4c770b46x1280x1925.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal granular layer</strong></p><p>The <strong>outer band of Baillarger</strong> is seen in the<strong> internal granular layer</strong> of cerebral cortex.</p>\n<p>The cerebral cortex contains nerve fibers that run radially and tangentially. The tangential fibers are concentrated in the internal granular layer (layer IV) referred to as the <strong>outer band of Baillarger</strong> and in the ganglionic layer (layer V) referred to as the <strong>inner band of Baillarger</strong>. These bands are well developed in the sensory areas because of the concentration of terminal parts of the <strong>thalamocortical fibers.</strong> In the visual cortex, the outer band of Baillarger can be seen with the naked eye and is known as the <strong>stria of Gennari. </strong>It is because of this stria that the visual cortex is also referred to as the <strong>striate cortex.</strong></p>\n<p>The given image shows a micrograph of the visual cortex. The blue, horizontal band in the lower half of the image are the bands of Baillarger/the stria of Gennari. Subcortical white matter (predominantly blue) is seen at the very bottom of the image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2404","text":"Which of the following cel‌ls of the pituitary gland​ are stained by eosin?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells of the pituitary gland are stained by eosin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cells pro‌ducing TSH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cells prod‌ucing ACTH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cells produc‌ing Prolactin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cells pro‌ducing FSH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cells producing Prolactin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3302ed25730f486fb1c73e315c2ee339x1280x1177.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6be357aa506e4dccad443a131247ceeex1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cells producing Prolactin</strong></p><p>The cells producing <strong>prolactin</strong> from the pituitary gland are stained by <strong>eosin.</strong></p>\n<p>The pituitary gland is<strong> </strong>divided into the <strong>pars anterior,</strong> <strong>pars intermedia</strong> and <strong>pars posterior</strong> (or pars nervosa).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The pars anterior (aka <strong>pars</strong> <strong>distalis</strong>) consists of <strong>chromophil cells</strong> and<strong> chromophobe cells.</strong> </p>\n<p>The chromophil cells have brightly staining granules in their cytoplasm. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acidophil cells -</strong> stain with <strong>acidic dyes</strong> like eosin or orange G and appear <strong>pink</strong> on H and E stain. They are also called <strong>alpha</strong> <strong>cells</strong>. They include:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Somatotrophs</strong> - producing growth hormone</li>\n<li><strong>Lactotrophs</strong> - producing prolactin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Basophil cells - </strong>stain with <strong>basic dyes</strong> like hematoxylin and appear blue on H and E stain.<strong> </strong>These cells are also periodic acid Schiff <strong>(PAS)</strong> stain <strong>positive. </strong>They are also called <strong>beta</strong> <strong>cells</strong>. They include:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corticotrophs</strong> - producing ACTH</li>\n<li><strong>Thyrotrophs</strong> - producing TSH</li>\n<li><strong>Gonadotrophs</strong> - producing FSH and LH</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>chromophobe cells</strong> do not contain granules that stain and hence appear <strong>colorless</strong> on an H and E stain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The pars intermedia contains colloid filled vesicles, which are <strong>remnants</strong> of the <strong>pouch of Rathke.</strong></p>\n<p>The pars posterior<strong> </strong>consists of nerve fibers from the neurons of the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> and specialized supporting cells called <strong>pituicytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2406","text":"An 80-year-old man was bro‌ught to the hospital with​ a history of a fall. Sus‌pecting a skull bone frac‌ture a lateral view X-ray‍ of the skull was taken w‍hich incidentally reveale‌d a radio-opaque structur​e at the site of the pine‌al gland. Which of the fo‍llowing could be the most​ likely cause for the opa​city?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old man was brought to the hospital with a history of a fall. Suspecting a skull bone fracture a lateral view X-ray of the skull was taken which incidentally revealed a radio-opaque structure at the site of the pineal gland. Which of the following could be the most likely cause for the opacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pineal‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corpora ‌arenacea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nervous‌ conarii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corpora ‌amylacea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corpora arenacea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e5d7f373c7c465aa30eb22da3054cb1x1280x1392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corpora arenacea</strong></p><p>The most likely cause for the opacity at the site of the pineal gland is <strong>corpora arenacea.</strong></p>\n<p>The pineal gland has a structure called the corpora arenacea <strong>(brain sand)</strong> located within the gland. These bodies are prone to an increase in calcification with advancing age. They are visible on x-ray and can be used as landmarks. Chemical analysis of the calcium deposits shows the different compositions, including <strong>calcium phosphate, magnesium phosphate, calcium carbonate,</strong> and <strong>ammonium phosphate.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: The cells of the pineal gland are called pinealocytes that produces the hormone melatonin which works with the hypothalamus to regulate sleep-wake cycles.</p>\n<p>Option D: Corpora amylacea are small eosinophilic inclusions frequently seen in histological sections of the brain, prostate, lung and uterus.</p>\n<p>The image below is a histology section of the pineal gland showing dark stained corpora arenacea within the gland.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2407","text":"Given below is a histologi‌cal section of the thyroi​d gland. Identify the str‌ucture marked X in the im‌age.","raw_text":"Given below is a histological section of the thyroid gland. Identify the structure marked X in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicul‌ar cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C-c‌ells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Col‌loid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hürthl‌e cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C-cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/396953bc7ba947678426c14a4fbe921d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bee7488f76f64daebe923ead0a017ff5x562x470.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C-cells</strong></p><p>The structure marked X is the <strong>C-cells</strong> or the <strong>parafollicular cells</strong> of the thyroid gland.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>C-cells </strong>are present between the follicular cells and the basement membrane or between the follicles. They secrete <strong>calcitonin.</strong></p>\n<p>The thyroid gland is covered by a fibrous capsule with septae dividing it into lobules. These lobules contain follicles lined by <strong>follicular cells. </strong>The shape of the follicular cells depends on the activity of the gland. They're <strong>squamous</strong> in the <strong>inactive state</strong>, <strong>cuboidal to columnar </strong>in the<strong> active state</strong>. The cavity of the follicle contains colloid which appears pink in H and E staining.</p>\n<p>Option D: Hürthle cells<strong> </strong>are oncocytes with eosinophilic granular cytoplasm and a vesicular nucleus with a large nucleolus found in the thyroid arising from follicular cells. They are seen in Hashimoto thyroiditis, Hürthle cell adenoma and Hürthle cell carcinoma of the thyroid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2408","text":"A 24-year-old lady present‌ed with a several-month-l​ong history of abdominal ‌pain, prolonged fatigue, ‌bone, and joint pain. An ‍MRI revealed a parathyroi‍d gland tumour which was ‌surgically resected. Give​n below is a section of t‌he parathyroid gland. Ide‍ntify the structures mark​ed A and B.","raw_text":"A 24-year-old lady presented with a several-month-long history of abdominal pain, prolonged fatigue, bone, and joint pain. An MRI revealed a parathyroid gland tumour which was surgically resected. Given below is a section of the parathyroid gland. Identify the structures marked A and B.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A - follicular cells; B - ‌chief cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A - Oxyphil cells; B - fol‌licular cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A - Oxyphil cells; B - Chi‌ef cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A - Chief cells; B - Oxyph‌il cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A - Oxyphil cells; B - Chief cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c83934dc04e24907bae08f75429c025b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/658c00418eee4367b95145968bb8eaafx1280x1393.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A - Oxyphil cells; B - Chief cells</strong></p><p>The structures marked are A and B are <strong>oxyphil cells</strong> and <strong>chief cells</strong> respectively. The clinical history and the MRI findings are strongly suggestive of <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong> secondary to a parathyroid adenoma.</p>\n<p>The parathyroid gland has two cell types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chief cells (principal cells) - They are small round cells with vesicular nuclei, surrounded by a small amount of cytoplasm. They are <strong>numerous</strong> and produce parathormone.</li>\n<li>Oxyphil cells -<strong> </strong>They are<strong> fewer</strong> and scattered between the chief cells. These are larger cells with a smaller nucleus with<strong> eosinophilic cytoplasm </strong>(pink colored). Their function is not known</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Primary hyperparathyroidism is usually due to a <strong>solitary parathyroid adenoma. </strong>The clinical signs associated with it can be remembered with the mnemonic - “<strong>bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones</strong>”.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2409","text":"In which of the following ‌layers of adrenal cortex ​are the cells arranged in‌ straight columns?","raw_text":"In which of the following layers of adrenal cortex are the cells arranged in straight columns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zona fas‌ciculata","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Zona glo‌merulosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zona ret‌icularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zona me‌dullaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zona fasciculata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1486a3180caf454a9eb09d1f21109d76x1280x1393.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b49d858574d4f49bc96c921b8579244x1280x1393.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zona fasciculata</strong></p><p>The cells are arranged in straight columns in the <strong>zona fasciculata</strong> of the adrenal cortex.</p>\n<p>The adrenal cortex is divided into three layers. From outermost to deep, they are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zona glomerulosa - </strong>consists of small polyhedral cells arranged in inverted U-shaped formations or <strong>acinus-like groups</strong>. These cells produce <strong>mineralocorticoids</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Zona fasciculata - </strong>consists of cells arranged in <strong>straight columns </strong>with sinusoids between the columns. These cells produce <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zona reticularis -</strong> It is so-called because it is made up of columns of cells that branch and anastomose with each other to form a kind of reticulum. These cells produce <strong>sex</strong> <strong>hormones</strong> - progesterone, oestrogens and androgens.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2410","text":"Which of the following cel‌ls are included under the​ APUD cell system? Chief ‌cells of the parathyroid ‌gland C cells of the thyr‍oid Type II alveolar cell‍s Glomus cells of the car‌otid body Melanocytes of ​the skin","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following cells are included under the APUD cell system?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chief cells of the parathyroid gland</li>\n<li>C cells of the thyroid</li>\n<li>Type II alveolar cells </li>\n<li>Glomus cells of the carotid body</li>\n<li>Melanocytes of the skin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3,‌4 &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, 3‌ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 4‌ &amp;amp; 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>All the above-mentioned cells except type II alveolar cells are included under the <strong>APUD cell system</strong>.</p>\n<p>The APUD (Amine Precursor Uptake and Decarboxylation) cell system consists of cells scattered in different parts of the body, which take up precursor substances from the circulation and process them (by decarboxylation) to form amines or peptides. The APUD cell system is also called the <strong>diffuse neuroendocrine system.</strong></p>\n<p>Some of the important cells included under the APUD cell system are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cells of the <strong>adenohypophysis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Neurons in the hypothalamus</strong> that synthesize the hormones of the neurohypophysis</li>\n<li><strong>Chief cells</strong> of the parathyroid glands</li>\n<li><strong>C- cells </strong>of the thyroid</li>\n<li>Cells of the <strong>adrenal medulla</strong></li>\n<li>Cells of the <strong>gastro-entero-pancreatic endocrine system</strong> which includes cells of pancreatic islets</li>\n<li><strong>Glomus cells</strong> of the carotid bodies</li>\n<li><strong>Melanocytes</strong> of the skin</li>\n<li><strong>Renin producing cells </strong>of the kidneys</li>\n<li>Some cells in the pineal gland, the placenta, and modified myocytes of the heart called myoendocrine cells.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HQtZe2wj","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"13_fatty_acid_oxidation_and_ketogenesis","title":"13.Fatty Acid Oxidation And Ketogenesis","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA9876","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In beta oxidation, 2 carbo​n units are released as a​cetyl CoA per cycle.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In alpha oxidation, one ca​rbon is lost in the form ​of CO2 per cycle.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha oxidation is an ener​getic process.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Beta oxidation is the majo​r mechanism that occurs i​n the mitochondria matrix​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alpha oxidation is an energetic process.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alpha oxidation is an energetic process.</strong></p><p>Alpha oxidation is <strong>not</strong> an energy yielding process.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9877","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propionyl CoA is produced ​by the oxidation of odd-c​hain fatty acids.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methylmalonyl CoA mutase i​s a biotin-dependent enzy​me.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propionyl CoA is not gluco​genic.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Odd-chain fatty acids do n​ot undergo oxidation.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propionyl CoA is produced by the oxidation of odd-chain fatty acids.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7364ea8ae2d46cd956b557bdcc91befx600x647.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propionyl CoA is produced by the oxidation of odd-chain fatty acids.</strong></p><p>'Propionyl CoA is produced by the oxidation of odd-chain fatty acids' is the correct statement.</p>\n<p>In the oxidation of odd-chain fatty acids, the last cycle of oxidation yields a three-carbon-containing <strong>propionyl CoA</strong>. Propionyl CoA is carboxylated to<strong> methylmalonyl CoA</strong> by <strong>propionyl CoA carboxylase</strong>, a <strong>biotin-dependent</strong> enzyme<span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\"> that requires ATP hydrolysis</span>.</p>\n<p>Methylmalonyl CoA is further converted into <strong>succinyl CoA </strong>by <strong>racemase</strong> and <strong>mutase</strong>, (a vitamin <strong>B<sub>12</sub> dependent</strong> enzyme) which enters into the <strong>TCA</strong> cycle.Thus, <strong>propionyl CoA</strong> can be converted into glucose by <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methylmalonyl CoA mutase</strong> is a <strong>vitamin B12-dependent</strong> enzyme.</li>\n<li><strong>Propionyl residue</strong> from an odd-chain fatty acid is the <strong>only part</strong> of a fatty acid that is <strong>glucogenic.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1208","text":"ATP is hydrolyzed directly​ to AMP, with the release​ of PPi during a biochemi​cal reaction catalyzed by​ which of the following e‍nzymes?","raw_text":"ATP is hydrolyzed directly to AMP, with the release of PPi during a biochemical reaction catalyzed by which of the following enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Creatin​e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenylat​e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myok​inase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acyl-CoA ​synthetase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acyl-CoA synthetase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acyl-CoA synthetase</strong></p><p>ATP can also be hydrolyzed directly to AMP, with the release of PPi by the enzyme <strong>Acyl-CoA synthetase.</strong></p>\n<p>This occurs in the <strong>activation of long-chain fatty acids</strong>. This reaction is accompanied by the loss of free energy as heat, which ensures that the activation reaction will go to the right. It is further aided by the hydrolytic splitting of PPi, catalyzed by inorganic <strong>pyrophosphatase</strong>, a reaction that itself has a large ΔG0′ of −19.2 kJ/mol.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9884","text":"For fatty acid oxidation w​hich of the following int​ermediate is formed durin​g the transport of acyl-C​oA using a mitochondrial ‍shuttle?","raw_text":"For fatty acid oxidation which of the following intermediate is formed during the transport of acyl-CoA using a mitochondrial shuttle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acety​l-CoA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acyl-co​enzyme A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acyl-ca​rnitine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aceto-ac​etyl-Coa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acyl-carnitine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acyl-carnitine</strong></p><p>In the fatty acid oxidation process, <strong>acyl-carnitine</strong> is formed as an intermediate during the transport of acyl-CoA using a mitochondrial shuttle.</p>\n<p>Acyl CoA is converted to acyl-carnitine, which is the active form of fatty acid during transport from the cytoplasm to the inside of the mitochondria for oxidation. The enzyme CPT-1 (<strong>carnitine palmitoyl transferase-I</strong>), present on the <strong>outer mitochondrial membrane</strong> catalyzes the reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9886","text":"Which of the following enz​ymes is required for the ​only energy-requiring ste​p of fatty acid oxidation​?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes is required for the only energy-requiring step of fatty acid oxidation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thio​lase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acyl CoA de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiok​inase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Beta-hydroxy acyl Co A deh​ydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thiokinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thiokinase</strong></p><p>The only<strong> energy-requiring</strong> step in fatty acid oxidation is catalyzed by the <strong>thiokinase</strong> enzyme.</p>\n<p>In the presence of <strong>ATP</strong> and <strong>coenzyme A</strong>, the enzyme <strong>acyl-CoA synthetase </strong>or <strong>thiokinase</strong> catalyses the conversion of a fatty acid to acyl-CoA, the active form of fatty acid for oxidation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9887","text":"How many molecules of acet​yl-CoA are produced in β-​ oxidation of palmitic ac​id?","raw_text":"How many molecules of acetyl-CoA are produced in β- oxidation of palmitic acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​8","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8</strong></p><p>In the β-oxidation of palmitic acid (C<sub>16</sub>), <strong>8 molecules</strong> of acetyl-CoA are produced.</p>\n<p>In the beta-oxidation pathway, two carbons at a time are cleaved from acyl-CoA molecules between alpha(2) and beta(3) carbons, starting at the carboxyl end. The two-carbon units formed are called acetyl-CoA.</p>\n<p>Thus, in palmitic acid <strong>(C<sub>16</sub>), 7 cycles </strong>of β-oxidation take place, releasing <strong>8 acetyl CoA</strong> molecules. In the first 6 cycles, 6 acetyl CoA molecules are released. In the 7<sup>th</sup> cycle, 2 acetyl CoA molecules are released.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9888","text":"Each cycle of β-oxidation ​produces ___________.","raw_text":"Each cycle of β-oxidation produces ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 FADH2, 1 NAD+, and 1 ace​tyl-CoA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 FADH2, 1 NADH, and 1 ace​tyl-CoA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 FADH2, 1 NADH, and 2 CO2​ molecules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 FAD, 1 NAD+, and 2 CO2 m​olecules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 FADH2, 1 NADH, and 1 acetyl-CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 FADH2, 1 NADH, and 1 acetyl-CoA</strong></p><p><strong>One</strong> cycle of <strong>β-oxidation</strong> of <strong>fatty acids </strong>produces <strong>1 FADH<sub>2</sub>, 1 NADH, and 1 acetyl-CoA</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FADH<sub>2</sub></strong> is formed in the reaction catalyzed by <strong>acyl CoA dehydrogenase</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>NADH</strong> is formed in the reaction catalyzed by <strong>β-hydroxy acyl CoA dehydrogenase</strong></li>\n<li>In each cycle of β-oxidation, the terminal two carbons are cleaved as acetyl CoA starting at carboxyl end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thus, the overall reaction of each cycle of β-oxidation can be summarized as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>(C<sub>n</sub>)</strong>acyl CoA + FAD + NAD<sup>+</sup>+ H<sub>2</sub>O + CoA → <strong>(C<sub>n-2</sub>)</strong>acyl CoA + FADH<sub>2 </sub>+ NADH + Acetyl CoA +H<sup>+</sup></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9889","text":"Which is the rate-limiting​ enzyme in beta-oxidation​ of fatty acids?","raw_text":"Which is the rate-limiting enzyme in beta-oxidation of fatty acids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acyl CoA de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thio​lase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiok​inase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carnitine palmitoyl transf​erase-1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carnitine palmitoyl transferase-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carnitine palmitoyl transferase-1</strong></p><p><strong>Carnitine palmitoyl transferase-1</strong> (CPT-1), is the <strong>rate-limiting enzyme</strong> in the beta-oxidation of fatty acids.</p>\n<p>CPT-1 is responsible for transferring the acyl group from CoA to carnitine, forming acylcarnitine which can enter the outer mitochondrial membrane. Thus the acyl group enters the mitochondria from the cytoplasm.</p>\n<p><strong>Malonyl CoA</strong> is an <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>CPT-I</strong>. Thus,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the <strong>fed state</strong>, with high insulin: glucagon ratio, <strong>acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong> is <strong>active</strong> and <strong>malonyl CoA </strong>is<strong> produced</strong>, which inhibits β-oxidation.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>fasting state</strong>, with low insulin: glucagon ratio, <strong>acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong> is <strong>inactive</strong> and <strong>malonyl CoA </strong>is<strong> not produced</strong>. Hence, β-oxidation takes place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9890","text":"What is the net ATP yield ​in the beta-oxidation of ​a straight-chain fatty ac​id having 2N carbon atoms​?","raw_text":"What is the net ATP yield in the beta-oxidation of a straight-chain fatty acid having 2N carbon atoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7N​-6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14​N-6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7N​-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14​N-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 14N-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 14N-6</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">A <strong>straight-chain fatty acid </strong>comprising of <strong>2N</strong> <strong>carbon atoms</strong> will undergo [(2N ÷ 2) -1] = <strong>(N-1) cycles of beta-oxidation</strong> and produce<strong> N number of acetyl CoA.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Each <strong>acetyl CoA</strong> will undergo TCA cycle, producing <strong>10 ATP</strong>. On the other hand, <strong>each cycle</strong> of beta-oxidation also produces 1 FADH2 and 1 NADH generating 1.5 ATP and 2.5 ATP respectively. Therefore (N-1) cycles of beta-oxidation will produce (N-1) FADH2 and (N-1) NADH.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The energetics of β-oxidation of a straight-chain fatty acid comprising of 2N carbon atoms can be summarized as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 200px;\" width=\"575\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">Intermediate produced</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">ATP yield per molecule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">Total number produced</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">Total ATP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">FADH2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">1.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">N-1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">1.5(N-1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">NADH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">2.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">N-1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">2.5(N-1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">Acetyl CoA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">N</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px;\">10N</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px; text-align: left;\">Total ATP</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 136.75px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">1.5(N-1) +2.5(N-1) +10N =(14N-4)   </span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px; text-align: left;\">ATP used in activation</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 136.75px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">- 2</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136.75px; text-align: left;\">Net ATP yield </td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 136.75px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">(14N-4) -2 = 14N-6 </span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">So, the <strong>net ATP yield</strong> after beta-oxidation of a straight-chain fatty acid having 2N carbon atoms will be (14N-4) - 2 = <strong>(14N- 6)</strong>We must keep in mind that during the<strong> initial activation</strong> of <strong>fatty acids</strong> (conversion of fatty acid to fatty acyl CoA), <strong>2 ATP is utilized</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">This formula can be used to calculate net ATP yield from a straight-chain fatty acid having an<strong> even number</strong> of <strong>carbon atoms. </strong>For example,</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Net ATP yield from beta-oxidation of palmitic acid (16 carbon) will be 106.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Calculation: Here, 2N = 16 i.e, N = 8 . Therefore net ATP yield = (14x8 - 6) = 112-6 = 106</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3506","text":"A 28-year-old woman at 33 ​weeks of gestation presen​ted with nausea and right​ upper quadrant abdominal​ pain. Her LFTs pointed t‍owards a diagnosis of acu​te fatty liver of pregnan‍cy. Which of the followin​g enzyme deficiencies is ​most likely involved in t‌he pathogenesis of this c‌ondition? LFT - Liver fun​ction test","raw_text":"<p>A 28-year-old woman at 33 weeks of gestation presented with nausea and right upper quadrant abdominal pain. Her LFTs pointed towards a diagnosis of acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Which of the following enzyme deficiencies is most likely involved in the pathogenesis of this condition?</p>\n<p>LFT - Liver function test</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal medium-chain acyl Co​A dehydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maternal long-chain 3-hydr​oxy acyl CoA dehydrogenas​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal long-chain 3-hydroxy​ acyl CoA dehydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal medium-chain acyl​ CoA dehydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fetal long-chain 3-hydroxy acyl CoA dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fetal long-chain 3-hydroxy acyl CoA dehydrogenase</strong></p><p>A deficiency of <strong>fetal long-chain 3-hydroxy acyl CoA dehydrogenase</strong> (LCHAD) is involved in the pathogenesis of <strong>acute fatty liver of pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Free fatty acids normally increase in pregnancy, particularly during late gestation to fuel fetoplacental growth and development. In fetuses<strong> homozygous </strong>for <strong>LCHAD deficiency</strong>, the fetoplacental unit is unable to undertake fatty acid metabolism adequately. This contributes to the accumulation of<strong> long-chain metabolites</strong> in maternal<strong> blood</strong> and <strong>hepatocytes</strong>, resulting in toxic effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9891","text":"An 8-month-old infant was ​brought to the pediatric ​emergency department with​ recurrent seizures and h​ypoglycemia. His family h‍istory revealed the sudde​n death of his sibling at‍ the age of 3 months. Lab​s showed increased dicarb​oxylic acid in blood and ‌urine. Which of the follo‌wing is the pathophysiolo​gy behind this clinical m‌anifestation?","raw_text":"An 8-month-old infant was brought to the pediatric emergency department with recurrent seizures and hypoglycemia. His family history revealed the sudden death of his sibling at the age of 3 months. Labs showed increased dicarboxylic acid in blood and urine. Which of the following is the pathophysiology behind this clinical manifestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impaired alpha oxidation o​f branched-chain fatty ac​ids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Impaired beta-oxidation of​ medium-chain fatty acids​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Impaired omega oxidation o​f medium-chain fatty acid​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impaired peroxisomal oxida​tion of very long-chain f​atty acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Impaired beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Impaired beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical vignette is suggestive of<strong> medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency</strong> in which, there is<strong> impaired beta-oxidation</strong> of <strong>medium-chain fatty acids. </strong>This leads to the accumulation of <strong>dicarboxylic acids</strong> leading to <strong>dicarboxylic aciduria</strong>. It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Excretion of C<sub>6</sub>-C<sub>10</sub> ω-dicarboxylic acids (medium-chain) in the urine.</li>\n<li>Nonketotic <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>: due to the lack of ATP and acetyl CoA from β-oxidation (which is an activator of pyruvate carboxylase) to support gluconeogenesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Defects in medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase have been identified as a cause of<strong> </strong>sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Omega-oxidation is an alternative way of fatty acid oxidation in the body when the beta-oxidation enzymes are either saturated or defective. Omega oxidation converts excess fatty acids to dicarboxylic acids that are soluble and hence can be excreted out via urine.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Other options:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A: <strong>Impaired Alpha oxidation</strong> leads to <strong>Refsum’s disease. </strong>The manifestations of classic adult Refsum's disease include retinitis pigmentosa, peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, ichthyosis, anosmia, deafness and cardiac arrhythmias.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: Medium-chain acyl CoA dehydrogenase deficiency will lead to <strong>increased</strong> (not impaired) utilization of the omega oxidation pathway, resulting in the overproduction of dicarboxylic acids.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D:<strong> Impaired peroxisomal oxidation</strong> of very long-chain<strong> fatty acids</strong> (VLCFA) causes <strong>Zellweger (cerebrohepatorenal) syndrome.</strong> It is a rare inherited disorder characterized by the absence of peroxisomal enzymes in all tissues leading to the accumulation of C26—C38 polyenoic acids in brain tissue. The clinical manifestation of Zellweger syndrome includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: left;\">Facial deformities resembling Down syndrome (Mongoloid facies, Hypertelorism, Frontal bossing, Upslanting palpebral fissure)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: left;\">Brush field spots</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: left;\">Hypotonia and hyporeflexia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: left;\">Seizures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: left;\">Neurodevelopmental delay</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: left;\">Liver enlargement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9892","text":"An infant is being assesse​d for hypotonia, seizures​, and neurodevelopmental ​delay. Clinical examinati​on reveals facial deformi‍ties, hyporeflexia, and h​epatomegaly. A congenital‍ disorder affecting extra​mitochondrial lipid metab​olism is suspected. Which‌ of the following process‌es is impaired in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"An infant is being assessed for hypotonia, seizures, and neurodevelopmental delay. Clinical examination reveals facial deformities, hyporeflexia, and hepatomegaly. A congenital disorder affecting extramitochondrial lipid metabolism is suspected. Which of the following processes is impaired in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl CoA​ formation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H2O2 mediated breakdown of​ fatty acids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transport of fatty acyl Co​A using shuttle mechanism​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peroxisomal alpha oxidatio​n of fatty acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. H2O2 mediated breakdown of fatty acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. H2O2 mediated breakdown of fatty acids</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>Zellweger (cerebrohepatorenal) syndrome</strong> in which <strong>H2O2-mediated</strong> peroxisomal beta-oxidation of <strong>very-long-chain fatty acids</strong> (VLCFA) is impaired.</p>\n<p>Beta-oxidation in peroxisome is differentiated from that occurring in mitochondria by the formation of <strong>H2O2.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Beta-oxidation</strong> of fatty acid<strong> </strong>in<strong> peroxisomes </strong>is<strong> not coupled </strong>to <strong>ATP synthesis.</strong> The high-potential electrons are transferred to O<sub>2</sub>, which <strong>yields H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2 </sub></strong>(instead of FADH2). The enzyme <strong>catalase</strong>, found exclusively in peroxisomes, converts the hydrogen peroxide to end products water and oxygen. </p>\n<p>Zellweger syndrome is a rare inherited disorder characterized by the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>peroxisomal enzymes</strong> in all tissues leading to the accumulation of <strong>C26—C38 polyenoic acids</strong> in brain tissue. The clinical manifestation of Zellweger syndrome includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Facial deformities resembling Down syndrome (Mongoloid facies, Hypertelorism, Frontal bossing, Upslanting palpebral fissure)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Brush field spots</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypotonia and hyporeflexia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Seizures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neurodevelopmental delay</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Liver enlargement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9898","text":"A 3-year-old child is bein​g evaluated for anosmia, ​deafness, and unstable ga​it. Further investigation​ reveals retinitis pigmen‍tosa on fundoscopy and ca​rdiac rhythm abnormalitie‍s on ECG. A rare congenit​al disease affecting lipi​d metabolism is being sus‌pected. Deficiency of whi‌ch of the following enzym​es is expected to be foun‌d in this patient?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child is being evaluated for anosmia, deafness, and unstable gait. Further investigation reveals retinitis pigmentosa on fundoscopy and cardiac rhythm abnormalities on ECG. A rare congenital disease affecting lipid metabolism is being suspected. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes is expected to be found in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phytanoyl CoA α-hydroxylas​e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acyl CoA ​synthetase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phytanic aci​d synthetase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phytanoyl CoA α-hydroxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phytanoyl CoA α-hydroxylase</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>Refsum’s disease</strong>, characterized by <strong>impaired alpha oxidation</strong> due to the <strong>deficiency of phytanoyl CoA α-hydroxylase</strong> (also known as phytanic acid alpha oxidase).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Refsum disease is a rare, <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder caused by a deficiency of peroxisomal phytanoyl CoA α-hydroxylase, an enzyme involved in the alpha oxidation of phytanic acid (branched-chain fatty acid). This results in the <strong>accumulation </strong>of<strong> phytanic acid</strong> in the plasma and <strong>tissues</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The manifestation of classic adult Refsum disease includes</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Retinitis pigmentosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ataxia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ichthyosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anosmia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Deafness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Occasionally cardiac arrhythmias</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Treatment: Restriction of dietary phytanic acid (found in dairy products and green leafy vegetables).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9901","text":"Which of the following is ​a primary ketone body?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a primary ketone body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ace​tone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetoa​cetate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-hydroxy​butyric acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acety​l CoA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acetoacetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a69e8b5aaf1940888efebd938495c53bx600x940.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acetoacetate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Acetoacetate</strong> is a <strong>primary ketone body</strong> as it is directly synthesized by the pathway of ketogenesis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Secondary ketone bodies</strong> are those which are produced from the primary ketone body via spontaneous breakdown or by enzymatic reaction. Examples of secondary ketone bodies are: <strong>acetone</strong> and <strong>beta-hydroxybutyrate.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Ketone bodies are formed under conditions associated with a <strong>high rate</strong> of<strong> fatty acid oxidation</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>Fasting</li>\n<li>Carbohydrate restrictive diet</li>\n<li>Starvation</li>\n<li>Prolonged intense exercise</li>\n<li>Untreated (or inadequately treated) type 1 diabetes mellitus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In such conditions, there is <strong>increased </strong>production of<strong> acetyl CoA </strong>from beta-oxidation of fatty acids which <strong>inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong> and a<strong>ctivates pyruvate carboxylase</strong>.<strong> </strong>The <strong>oxaloacetate</strong> thus produced is <strong>used for</strong> <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong> rather than the TCA cycle. Hence, acetyl CoA is diverted into ketone body synthesis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Acetyl CoA is converted into acetoacetate by the following reactions that occur in the liver mitochondria:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Thus, acetoacetate is the primary ketone body synthesized.</p>\n<p><strong>HMG CoA synthase</strong> is the <strong>rate-limiting step</strong> in ketone body synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9904","text":"A 13 year-old-boy with typ​e 1 diabetes presents wit​h abdominal pain, severe ​nausea, and vomiting. He ​has a history of increase‍d urine output over the p​ast 2 days. ABG analysis ‍reveals high anion gap me​tabolic acidosis. The giv​en clinical condition is ‌due to the over-activity ‌of which of the following​ pathways?","raw_text":"A 13 year-old-boy with type 1 diabetes presents with abdominal pain, severe nausea, and vomiting. He has a history of increased urine output over the past 2 days. ABG analysis reveals high anion gap metabolic acidosis. The given clinical condition is due to the over-activity of which of the following pathways?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HMP ​shunt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucone​ogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipo​lysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glycoge​nolysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lipolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lipolysis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis</strong> which is an <strong>insulin-deficient</strong> state leading to increased <strong>lipid breakdown (lipolysis)</strong> into fatty acids. This condition is also characterized by <strong>hyperglycemia</strong> and <strong>increased ketone body synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>Type 1 diabetes mellitus is commonly found in the adolescent population.  In response to certain precipitating factors, such patients may develop a severe insulin-deficient state. Since<strong> insulin </strong>is an <strong>inhibitor </strong>of <strong>hormone-sensitive lipase</strong> (HSL), an insulin-deficient state will cause <strong>HSL overactivity</strong> in adipocytes and the <strong>breakdown of lipid</strong> into fatty acids (<strong>lipolysis</strong>). Free fatty acids will undergo <strong>beta-oxidation</strong> and produce a large amount of <strong>acetyl CoA</strong>, which gets converted to <strong>ketone bodies</strong> ( acetoacetate, acetone, beta-hydroxybutyrate, etc.)  Such ketone bodies will accumulate in the blood and cause <strong>high anion gap metabolic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9906","text":"The rescue team has found ​a 33-year-old hiker who w​as stranded for 8 days. H​e was found in an extreme​ly weak and dehydrated st‍ate. His breath has a fru​ity smell and urinalysis ‍shows a positive ketostix​ test. The activity of wh​ich of the following enzy‌mes is increased in him?","raw_text":"The rescue team has found a 33-year-old hiker who was stranded for 8 days. He was found in an extremely weak and dehydrated state. His breath has a fruity smell and urinalysis shows a positive ketostix test. The activity of which of the following enzymes is increased in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HMG CoA ​synthase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HMG CoA ​reductase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acetyl CoA ​carboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyruvate de​hydrogenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HMG CoA synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/246c55d37f2c4e2c876a41b04978129cx600x940.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HMG CoA synthase</strong></p><p>A <strong>fruity smell</strong> (presence of acetone in breath) and positive ketostix test (<strong>ketonuria</strong>) on the background of prolonged starvation is suggestive of <strong>starvation ketosis</strong>. This is characterized by <strong>increased</strong> activity of <strong>HMG CoA synthase</strong> enzyme.</p>\n<p>Prolonged starvation implies a <strong>catabolic state</strong> in which there is <strong>excessive β-oxidation</strong> leading to increased production of <strong>acetyl CoA</strong>. The excessive acetyl CoA is utilised by the hepatocytes to synthesise ketone bodies for extrahepatic utilisation.</p>\n<p><strong>HMG CoA synthase</strong> is the enzyme required for ketone body synthesis<strong>, </strong>it is also the <strong>rate-limiting enzyme.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HMG CoA reductase</strong> is involved in the <strong>synthesis of cholesterol</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong> is involved in the <strong>fatty acid synthesis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong> converts <strong>pyruvate into acetyl CoA.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>All three enzymes are <strong>inactive in low insulin state</strong> (such as prolonged starvation).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9909","text":"Which of the following can​not utilize ketone bodies​?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot utilize ketone bodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br​ain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeleta​l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li​ver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liver</strong></p><p>The <strong>liver</strong> cannot utilize ketone bodies.</p>\n<p>The ketone bodies synthesized in the liver mitochondria are transported in the blood to the peripheral tissues, where they are utilized by the following steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>β-hydroxybutyrate is oxidized to acetoacetate by <strong>β-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase</strong> producing NADH.</li>\n<li>Acetoacetate is then activated to acetoacetyl CoA by transferring a CoA molecule from succinyl CoA by <strong>succinyl CoA-acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase).</strong></li>\n<li>Acetoacetyl CoA is cleaved into two acetyl CoA molecules by <strong>thiolase</strong> and oxidized into CO<sub>2</sub> by the TCA cycle.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>RBC</strong>s cannot utilize ketone bodies as they <strong>lack mitochondria</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>liver</strong> cannot utilize ketone bodies as it <strong>lacks the enzyme thiophorase.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9912","text":"A diabetic man presented w​ith vomiting, abdominal p​ain, and polyuria. He giv​es a history of missing 2​ doses of insulin since m‍orning. He is hypotensive​ and tachycardic and his ‍breath has a fruity odour​. Blood glucose was 350 m​g/dL. Which test, done he‌re will give a positive r‌esult?","raw_text":"A diabetic man presented with vomiting, abdominal pain, and polyuria. He gives a history of missing 2 doses of insulin since morning. He is hypotensive and tachycardic and his breath has a fruity odour. Blood glucose was 350 mg/dL. Which test, done here will give a positive result?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hay's​ test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fouchet​'s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ninhydr​in test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rothera​’s test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rothera’s test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rothera’s test</strong></p><p>The clinical picture is suggestive of diabetic <strong>ketoacidosis</strong> in which rothera’s test will give positive results due to the presence of <strong>ketone bodies</strong> in urine. </p>\n<p><strong>Rothera’s test</strong> by <strong>nitroprusside reagent</strong> is a specific test used to detect whether <strong>acetone</strong> or <strong>acetoacetate</strong> is present in the given sample of urine. A <strong>purple ring</strong> at the junction of two liquids indicates a positive test.</p>\n<p>Note: Rothera's test does <strong>not detect</strong> β-hydroxybutyrate. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A<strong>: Hay's</strong> test is used to test for<strong> bile salts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Fouchet's</strong> test is used to test for <strong>bilirubin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Ninhydrin</strong> test is used to test for <strong>alpha-amino</strong> <strong>acids</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7088","text":"A 12-year-old girl suffers​ from an inadequately tre​ated type 1 diabetes mell​itus. She is currently hy​perglycemic. Which of the‍ following is most likely​ to occur?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl suffers from an inadequately treated type 1 diabetes mellitus. She is currently hyperglycemic. Which of the following is most likely to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycogenesi​s in muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased protein synthesi​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased conversion of fa​tty acids to acetyl-CoA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased cholesterol synt​hesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased conversion of fatty acids to acetyl-CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased conversion of fatty acids to acetyl-CoA</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">With the background of inadequately treated <strong>type I diabetes mellitus</strong>, an <strong>insulin-deficient state</strong> in the body is expected which will lead to <strong>increased conversion </strong>of<strong> fatty acids to acetyl-CoA.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Type 1 diabetes mellitus is commonly found in the adolescent population.  In response to certain precipitating factors, such patients may develop a severe insulin-deficient state. Since<strong> insulin </strong>is an <strong>inhibitor </strong>of <strong>hormone-sensitive lipase</strong> (HSL), an insulin-deficient state will cause <strong>HSL overactivity</strong> in adipocytes and the <strong>breakdown of lipid</strong> into fatty acids (<strong>lipolysis</strong>). Free fatty acids will undergo <strong>beta-oxidation</strong> and produce a large amount of <strong>acetyl CoA</strong>, which gets converted to <strong>ketone bodies</strong> ( acetoacetate, acetone, beta-hydroxybutyrate, etc.)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6926","text":"Ketosis is common in type ​1 DM due to: 1. Decreased​ beta-oxidation of fatty ​acids 2. Increased mobili​zation of fat from the ad‍ipose cell 3. Decreased a​cetyl CoA in TCA cycle 4.‍ Increased beta-hydroxy b​utyrate dehydrogenase","raw_text":"<p>Ketosis is common in type 1 DM due to:</p>\n<p>1. Decreased beta-oxidation of fatty acids<br/>2. Increased mobilization of fat from the adipose cell<br/>3. Decreased acetyl CoA in TCA cycle<br/>4. Increased beta-hydroxy butyrate dehydrogenase</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,3 ​and 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,2,3​ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 o​nly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 and ​2 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2,3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2,3 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>2, 3 and 4</strong> statements correctly explain the reason behind increased ketogenesis as seen in type1 DM.</p>\n<p><strong>Type 1 DM</strong> is characterized by a<strong> deficiency in insulin</strong> production. Since insulin is deficient, GLUT4 (insulin-dependent uptake) mediated glucose uptake is not possible in the heart or skeletal muscles. So <strong>adipose tissue mobilizes fat</strong> to be used as an energy source. These<strong> FFA</strong> (free fatty acids) are<strong> oxidized</strong> to <strong>acetoacetyl CoA</strong> which serves as a precursor for<strong> ketone body</strong> <strong>synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>As the level of serum FFA is raised, proportionately<strong> more</strong> of the <strong>acetyl-CoA</strong> produced from their breakdown is <strong>converted to ketone bodies</strong> and<strong> less</strong> is oxidized <strong>via the citric acid cycle</strong> to CO2. A fall in the concentration of oxaloacetate, particularly within the mitochondria, can impair the ability of the citric acid cycle to metabolize acetyl-CoA and divert fatty acid oxidation toward ketogenesis. Such a fall may occur because of an <strong>increase in the (NADH)/(NAD+) ratio</strong> caused when increased β-oxidation alters the equilibrium between oxaloacetate and malate so that the <strong>concentration of oxaloacetate is decreased</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Beta-hydroxy butyrate dehydrogenase</strong> is one of the enzymes used in ketone body synthesis. Increased rate of ketone body synthesis in <strong>insulin-deficient state</strong> implies an <strong>increased activity </strong>of<strong> beta-hydroxy butyrate dehydrogenase</strong> enzyme.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"c8Wnga8m","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"13_screening","title":"13.Screening","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MA4946","text":"Which of the following are‌ true for a screening tes​t? 1) Done on apparently ‍healthy 2) Applied to ind​ividuals 3) Time-consumin​g 4) Relatively inaccurat‍e results 5) Based on one‍ criterion/ cut off","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are true for a screening test?</p>\n<p>1) Done on apparently healthy</p>\n<p>2) Applied to individuals</p>\n<p>3) Time-consuming</p>\n<p>4) Relatively inaccurate results</p>\n<p>5) Based on one criterion/ cut off</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 4, 5</strong></p><p>Screening tests are done on <strong>apparently healthy individuals</strong>, the results of the test are <strong>relatively inaccurate</strong>, the tests are <strong>based on one criterion.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The difference between screening and diagnostic tests is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 507.83px; height: 437px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.8924px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; text-align: center; height: 34.8924px;\"><strong>Screening test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; text-align: center; height: 34.8924px;\"><strong>Diagnostic test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Done on apparently healthy</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Done on patients with signs and symptoms</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 19px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Applied to groups</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 19px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Applied to single patients</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Based on one criterion or cut-off point</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Based on evaluation of a number of symptoms, signs, and laboratory findings</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Less accurate</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>More accurate</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Less expensive</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>More expensive</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Not a basis for treatment</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 18px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Used as a basis for treatment</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 28px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The initiative comes from the investigator or agency providing care</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.83px; height: 28px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 156\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The initiative comes from a patient with a complaint</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Screening tests are recommended in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early diagnosis changes the disease course</li>\n<li>In a disease with an asymptomatic stage</li>\n<li>In a disease with a test for diagnosis and treatment</li>\n<li>The natural history of the disease is well known</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9578","text":"Below is a diagram depicti‌ng the natural history of​ the disease. Which point‍ marks the onset of sympt​oms?","raw_text":"Below is a diagram depicting the natural history of the disease. Which point marks the onset of symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poi‌nt A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Poi‌nt B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Poi‌nt C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Poi‌nt D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Point C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8894745c29f847bab294a061643dd79d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/286471cc5d894f4eb0c2b760ad0b91a0x1280x908.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Point C</strong></p><p>Point C represents a point where signs and symptoms appear.</p>\n<p>Various points marked in the image represent the following:</p>\n<p>Point A: Entry of <strong>infectious agent</strong> into the host.</p>\n<p>Point B: <strong>Pathological</strong> and physiological changes occur in the stage of the<strong> s</strong><strong>ubclinical phase. </strong>These changes are induced by the causative agent but are not sufficient enough to cause clinical signs and symptoms.</p>\n<p>Point C: Marks <strong>onset</strong> of clinical <strong>signs</strong> and <strong>symptoms.</strong></p>\n<p>Point D: Usual <strong>time of diagnosis </strong>at which clinical signs and symptoms are well evident.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7443","text":"In the following natural h‌istory of disease flowcha​rt, point A to C is calle‍d as?","raw_text":"In the following natural history of disease flowchart, point A to C is called as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Screeni‌ng time","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lead‌ time","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lag ‌time","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generat‌ion time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lead time","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/35a5327f7cf447ab95eb8cd1a22a7fb9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lead time</strong></p><p><strong>Lead time</strong> is the period between the <strong>first possible time</strong> of detection (point A) and the <strong>usual time </strong>of diagnosis (point C).</p>\n<p><strong>Screening time</strong> is the time difference between the <strong>first point</strong> of diagnosis and the <strong>critical point</strong> of diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of a disease before the <strong>critical point </strong>has a good outcome and reduces the severity by reversing the disease process. Diagnosis beyond the critical point treatment would be unsuccessful and/or permanent damage would have occurred.</p>\n<p>Screening must be done in conditions where there is a considerable time lag between the onset of disease and the usual time of diagnosis. That is conditions with a long lead time.</p>\n<p><strong>Generation time</strong> is defined as the interval of time between the <strong>onset</strong> of infection by a host and the <strong>maximal infectivity</strong> of that host.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4842","text":"In which of the following ‌diseases, screening proce​dure increases the overal‍l survival to the maximum​?","raw_text":"In which of the following diseases, screening procedure increases the overall survival to the maximum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung ‌cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostat‌e cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colon ‌cancer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian‌ cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colon cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colon cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Screening</strong> of <strong>colon cancer</strong> increases the <strong>overall survival</strong> to the <strong>maximum.</strong></p>\n<p>Colon cancer has a <strong>long lead time</strong> and can be detected early before pathological changes occur. It can be curable if treated early, otherwise fatal in half the cases. </p>\n<p>Early diagnosis and treatment before the critical point have a good outcome and reduce the severity by reversing the disease process. Diagnosis beyond the critical point treatment would be unsuccessful and/or permanent damage would have occurred.</p>\n<p>Other diseases that can be screened are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervical cancer</li>\n<li>Breast cancer</li>\n<li>Prostate cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5703","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of prospective​ screening?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of prospective screening?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical Pap smear in a 40‌ year old patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neonatal screening of a ne‌w-born baby for hypothyro​idism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Screening of immigrants to‌ a country","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urine for sugar screening ‌in a 40 year old man","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Screening of immigrants to a country","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Screening of immigrants to a country</strong></p><p>Screening of<strong> immigrants </strong>to a country is an example of <strong>prospective </strong>screening.</p>\n<p><strong>Screening </strong>of disease is done to detect unrecognized disease or defect, in apparently healthy individuals, by means of rapidly applied tests, examinations, or other procedures.</p>\n<p>The difference between prospective and prescriptive screening is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 188px; width: 590px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.2396px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 30.2396px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.188px; text-align: center; height: 30.2396px;\"><strong>Prospective screening</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.812px; text-align: center; height: 30.2396px;\"><strong>Prescriptive screening</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 36px;\">Definition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.188px; height: 36px;\">People screened for others benefit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.812px; height: 36px;\">People screened solely for their own benefit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 18px;\">Essential purpose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.188px; height: 18px;\">Disease control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.812px; height: 18px;\">Case detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 36px;\">Request for screening</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.188px; height: 36px;\">Specific request from the authority</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.812px; height: 36px;\">Request from physician</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 209px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 209px;\">Example</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.188px; height: 209px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immigrants</strong> for infectious diseases like tuberculosis syphilis, yellow fever, etc;</li>\n<li><strong>HIV screening</strong> during antenatal visits, sex workers.</li>\n<li>Screening of <strong>blood donors</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.812px; height: 209px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neonatal</strong> screening for hypothyroidism.</li>\n<li>Pap smear for detection of cervical <strong>cancer.</strong></li>\n<li>Testing of urine sugar for diabetes mellitus.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Most common neonatal disease to be screened for is neonatal hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4948","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of prescriptiv​e screening?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of prescriptive screening?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Screening of immigrants fo‌r yellow fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Screening of‌ blood donors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Screening of infants for p‌henylketonuria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HIV screening during anten‌atal visits","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Screening of infants for phenylketonuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Screening of infants for phenylketonuria</strong></p><p>Screening of infants for <strong>phenylketonuria</strong> is an example of <strong>prescriptive</strong> screening.</p>\n<p><strong>Screening </strong>of disease is done to detect unrecognized disease or defect, in apparently healthy individuals, by means of rapidly applied tests, examinations, or other procedures.</p>\n<p>The difference between prospective and prescriptive screening is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 188px; width: 590px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 28.2604px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 28.2604px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.142px; text-align: center; height: 28.2604px;\"><strong>Prospective screening</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.858px; text-align: center; height: 28.2604px;\"><strong>Prescriptive screening</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 36px;\">Definition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.142px; height: 36px;\">People screened for others benefit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.858px; height: 36px;\">People screened for their own benefit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 18px;\">Essential purpose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.142px; height: 18px;\">Disease control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.858px; height: 18px;\">Case detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 36px;\">Request for screening</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.142px; height: 36px;\">Specific request from the authority</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.858px; height: 36px;\">Request from physician</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 209px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 209px;\">Example</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.142px; height: 209px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immigrants</strong> for infectious diseases like tuberculosis syphilis, yellow fever, etc;</li>\n<li><strong>HIV screening</strong> during antenatal visits, sex workers.</li>\n<li>Screening of <strong>blood donors</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.858px; height: 209px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neonatal</strong> screening for hypothyroidism.</li>\n<li>Pap smear for detection of cervical <strong>cancer.</strong></li>\n<li>Testing of urine sugar for diabetes mellitus.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Most common neonatal disease to be screened for is neonatal hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4949","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of multiphasic​ screening?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of multiphasic screening?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest X-ray for TB in larg‌e populations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Annual heal‌th checkups","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pap smear in women above 4‌0 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Random blood sugar during ‌antenatal visits","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Annual health checkups","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Annual health checkups</strong></p><p><strong>Annual health checkups</strong> are an example of multiphasic screening.</p>\n<p>Types of screening:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Multiphasic screening</strong> is the application of <strong>two or more screening tests</strong> in combination with a large number of people <strong>at one time.</strong> Example- Annual health checkups.</li>\n<li><strong>High-risk</strong> or <strong>selective</strong> screening is done on high-risk groups. Example-<strong> </strong>MRI screening of breast done annually in 25-year old women with BRCA 1, BRCA 2 mutations in a first-degree relative.</li>\n<li><strong>Mass screening</strong> is a screening of a whole or <strong>large population</strong> like using a chest X-ray for TB screening in large populations. It is done for diseases with <strong>high prevalence.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6994","text":"A large number of tests we‌re done in series. Which ​of the following will be ‍true?","raw_text":"A large number of tests were done in series. Which of the following will be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensitivity and specificit‌y will increase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensitivity will decrease,‌ specificity will increas​e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sensitivity and specificit‌y will decrease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivity will increase,‌ specificity will decreas​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sensitivity will decrease, specificity will increase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sensitivity will decrease, specificity will increase</strong></p><p>When a large number of tests are done in <strong>series</strong>, net <strong>sensitivity </strong>is<strong> decreased </strong>and net<strong> specificity </strong>is<strong> increased.</strong></p>\n<p>Screening tests are done in <strong>series </strong>when the result of the <strong>first test determines</strong> whether to run the <strong>second</strong> <strong>test</strong> or not. e.g., HIV screening is done using consecutive ELISA.</p>\n<p>Screening tests are done in <strong>parallel </strong>when the second test is <strong>independently performed</strong> regardless of what the first test result is. Both the tests are done at the same time. For e.g., In LFT, multiple tests are done together like SGOT, SGPT, total bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, etc.</p>\n<p>Following are the trends that are observed while evaluating screening tests:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 404.766px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.5972px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 29.5972px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: left; height: 29.5972px;\"><strong>Tests in Series</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; text-align: left; height: 29.5972px;\"><strong>Tests in parallel</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 36px;\">Combined sensitivity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 36px;\">Decreases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 36px;\">Increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 36px;\">Combined specificity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 36px;\">Increases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 36px;\">Decreases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 18px;\">Combined PPV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 18px;\">Increases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 18px;\">Decreases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 18px;\">Combined NPV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 18px;\">Decreases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 18px;\">Increases </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4844","text":"Colon cancer screening is ‌being done by a hemoccult​ stool test in a healthca‍re center. Patients who t​est positive, undergo a r​epeat stool hemoccult tes‍t. If the second test com‍es out positive, the indi​vidual is referred for mo​re extensive evaluation. ‌How is this method of scr‍eening affecting sensitiv​ity and specificity?","raw_text":"Colon cancer screening is being done by a hemoccult stool test in a healthcare center. Patients who test positive, undergo a repeat stool hemoccult test. If the second test comes out positive, the individual is referred for more extensive evaluation. How is this method of screening affecting sensitivity and specificity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Net sensitivity and net sp‌ecificity are both increa​sing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Net sensitivity is decreas‌ing and net specificity i​s increasing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Net sensitivity is increas‌ing and net specificity i​s decreasing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Net sensitivity is decreas‌ing and net specificity r​emains the same","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Net sensitivity is decreasing and net specificity is increasing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Net sensitivity is decreasing and net specificity is increasing</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, tests are being performed in <strong>series</strong>. Here, the net<strong> sensitivity </strong>is <strong>decreasing</strong> and net <strong>specificity</strong> is <strong>increasing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Screening tests are done in <strong>series, </strong>where the result of the <strong>first test determines</strong> whether to run the <strong>second test</strong> or not. e.g. HIV screening is done using consecutive ELISA.</p>\n<p>Screening tests are done in <strong>parallel, </strong>where the second test is <strong>independently performed</strong> regardless of what the first test result is. Both the tests are done at the same time. For e.g. In LFT, multiple tests are done together like SGOT, SGPT, total bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, etc.</p>\n<p>Following are the trends that are observed while evaluating screening tests:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 404.766px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.5972px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 29.5972px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: left; height: 29.5972px;\"><strong>Tests in Series</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; text-align: left; height: 29.5972px;\"><strong>Tests in parallel</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 36px;\">Combined sensitivity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 36px;\">Decreases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 36px;\">Increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 36px;\">Combined specificity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 36px;\">Increases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 36px;\">Decreases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 18px;\">Combined PPV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 18px;\">Increases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 18px;\">Decreases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 18px;\">Combined NPV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; height: 18px;\">Decreases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.766px; height: 18px;\">Increases </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4950","text":"Blood pressure is measured‌ in an individual 5 times​ a day under the same con‍ditions by a new screenin​g test. All 5 readings ob​tained are the almost sam‍e. What is the attribute ‍of a new screening test b​eing described?","raw_text":"Blood pressure is measured in an individual 5 times a day under the same conditions by a new screening test. All 5 readings obtained are the almost same. What is the attribute of a new screening test being described?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vali‌dity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relia‌bility","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sensi‌tivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predictiv‌e accuracy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reliability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reliability</strong></p><p>The attribute of a new screening test to yield the <strong>same reading</strong> when <strong>repeated</strong> under the same conditions is called <strong>reliability</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 159\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Reliability is also called precision or reproducibility.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4963","text":"Ten successive readings of‌ hemoglobin level of a si​ngle sample measured by a‍ new method are given bel​ow. The hemoglobin level ​measured by the previous ‍standard test was 10.2. W‍hat is true regarding the​ new method? Readings: 9.​4, 10.4, 9.6, 9.1, 10.8, ‌12.1, 10.1, 9.8, 9.2, and‍ 9.5.","raw_text":"<p>Ten successive readings of hemoglobin level of a single sample measured by a new method are given below. The hemoglobin level measured by the previous standard test was 10.2. What is true regarding the new method?</p>\n<p>Readings: 9.4, 10.4, 9.6, 9.1, 10.8, 12.1, 10.1, 9.8, 9.2, and 9.5.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High validity, low reliabi‌lity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low validity, low reliabil‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low validity, high reliabi‌lity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High validity, high reliab‌ility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High validity, low reliability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High validity, low reliability</strong></p><p>The new method for measuring hemoglobin has <strong>high validity </strong>and<strong> low reliability.</strong></p>\n<p>The new test doesn’t give the same values on repeat testing, thus it has <strong>low reliability. </strong>The mean value of all the readings is <strong>10</strong>. The mean is close to the value of 10.2, which is obtained from the standard test, making the <strong>validity high</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>validity</strong> of a test implies that the test must give correct and <strong>accurate</strong> results. It represents the <strong>closeness</strong> to a true/ actual value.</p>\n<p>The <strong>reliability</strong> of a test implies that the test must give <strong>consistent</strong> results when repeated on the same individual or material, under the same conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4956","text":"Out of 100 people having l‌ung cancer, 60 tested pos​itive by a new screening ‍test. Out of 900 people w​ho don't have lung cancer​, 300 tested positive. Wh‍at will be the numerator ‍while calculating sensiti​vity and its value?","raw_text":"Out of 100 people having lung cancer, 60 tested positive by a new screening test. Out of 900 people who don't have lung cancer, 300 tested positive. What will be the numerator while calculating sensitivity and its value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True pos‌itive, 60","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"False pos‌itive, 300","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"True nega‌tive, 600","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"False neg‌ative, 40","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. True positive, 60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. True positive, 60</strong></p><p>The <strong>numerator</strong> of <strong>sensitivity</strong> in a screening test is <strong>true positive </strong>and its value is <strong>60.</strong></p>\n<p>True positives are those people who tested positive and actually have the disease. Out of 100 people with lung cancer, 60 are tested positive i.e. true positive.</p>\n<p>The given data is represented below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p><strong>Disease +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p><strong>Disease -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p><strong>Test +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>60(TP)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p>300(FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>360</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p><strong>Test -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>40(FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p>600(TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>640</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>100</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p>900</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>1000</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TP- true positive, FP- false positive, FN- false negative, TN- true negative</p>\n<p>Sensitivity is the ability of a screening test to detect true positive(TP) out of the total number of people who have the disease (TP+FN).</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity= TP/ TP+FN = </strong>60/60+40 = 60%</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4955","text":"A screening test was carri‌ed out among 114 people o​ut of which 15 have a dis‍ease. The test showed 22 ​people tested positive fo​r the disease among which‍ 14 actually had the dise‍ase. What is the sensitiv​ity of the test?","raw_text":"A screening test was carried out among 114 people out of which 15 have a disease. The test showed 22 people tested positive for the disease among which 14 actually had the disease. What is the sensitivity of the test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‌7%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6‌3%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9‌3%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9‌7%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 93%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 93%</strong></p><p>The <strong>sensitivity</strong> of the above screening test is <strong>93%.</strong></p>\n<p>The given data is represented below</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p><strong>Disease +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p><strong>Disease -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p><strong>Test +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>14(TP)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p>8(FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>22</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p><strong>Test -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>1(FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p>91(TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>92</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"127\">\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>15(TP+FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"119\">\n<p>99(FP+TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"136\">\n<p>114</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TP- true positive, FP- false positive, FN- false negative, TN- true negative</p>\n<p>Sensitivity is given as <strong>TP/(TP + FN) x 100 </strong>= 14/15 = <strong>93%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9579","text":"A researcher finds out a s‌ensitive test to be posit​ive for a person. For dia‍gnosis, which of the foll​owing will hold true?","raw_text":"A researcher finds out a sensitive test to be positive for a person. For diagnosis, which of the following will hold true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It confirms that the perso‌n does not have the disea​se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It suggests that if the di‌sease is rare, the person​ surely has the disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It confirms that the perso‌n has the disease if the ​disease is common","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is an expected finding ‌in case the disease is pr​evalent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It confirms that the person has the disease if the disease is common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It confirms that the person has the disease if the disease is common</strong></p><p>A sensitive test, if it is <strong>positive</strong> it <strong>confirms</strong> that the person has the <strong>disease</strong> if the <strong>disease is common.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>sensitivity </strong>of a screening test is the ability of the test to identify correctly those who have the disease. In other words, Sensitivity is the ability of a test to <strong>detect true positives</strong> (TP) out of total disease (TP+FN).</p>\n<p>Sensitivity = TP/ TP+FN</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7592","text":"1000 patients from a healt‌hcare setup were screened​ for disease 'X' with a n‍ew test (biopsy). The tes​t showed positive results​ in 90 patients out of th‍e 100 who actually had th‍e disease. What is the se​nsitivity of the new test​?","raw_text":"1000 patients from a healthcare setup were screened for disease 'X' with a new test (biopsy). The test showed positive results in 90 patients out of the 100 who actually had the disease. What is the sensitivity of the new test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90/‌100","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"100‌/110","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"(90-10‌)/1000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90/‌1000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 90/100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 90/100</strong></p><p>The<strong> sensitivity</strong> of new test is<strong> 90/100. </strong></p>\n<p>Biopsy gives the definitive number of cases for Disease X. Hence, the given data is represented below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 102px;\" width=\"521\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\"><strong>Disease +</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.344px;\"><strong>Disease -</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\"><strong>Test +</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\">90(TP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.344px;\">unknown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\"><strong>Test -</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\">10(FN)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.344px;\">unknown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\"><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.328px;\">100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.344px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Sensitivity = <strong>TP/(TP+FN)</strong> = 90/(90+10) = 90/100 = 90%</p>\n<p>True positive is defined as a positive test result in a person who has the disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4953","text":"Specificity of a screening‌ test is the ability of a​ test to detect:","raw_text":"Specificity of a screening test is the ability of a test to detect:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True po‌sitives","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"False p‌ositives","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"True ne‌gatives","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"False n‌egatives","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. True negatives","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. True negatives</strong></p><p><strong>Specificity </strong>of a screening test is the ability of the test to identify correctly those who do not have the disease, i.e. <strong>true negative.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity </strong>of a screening test is the ability of the test to identify correctly those who have the disease, i.e. <strong>true positive.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>False-positive</strong> are those who don't have the disease but tested positive.</p>\n<p><strong>False-negative</strong> are those who have the disease but tested negative.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4964","text":"5000 persons underwent scr‌eening for a disease. Out​ of 500 diseased, 350 tes‍ted positive, and out of ​healthy people, 3000 test​ed negative. Which of the‍ following is true?","raw_text":"5000 persons underwent screening for a disease. Out of 500 diseased, 350 tested positive, and out of healthy people, 3000 tested negative. Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensitivity is 70% and Spe‌cificity is 80%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Specificity is 70% and Sen‌sitivity is 80%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Specificity is 66.6% and S‌ensitivity is 70%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivity is 87.5% and S‌pecificity is 66.6%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Specificity is 66.6% and Sensitivity is 70%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Specificity is 66.6% and Sensitivity is 70%</strong></p><p>The<strong> sensitivity</strong> of the above screening test is <strong>70%</strong> and<strong> Specificity</strong> is<strong> 66.6%.</strong></p>\n<p>The given data is represented below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 343px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p><strong>Disease +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p><strong>Disease -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">\n<p>Total</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">\n<p><strong>Test +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p>350(TP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>1500(FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">\n<p>1850</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">\n<p><strong>Test -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p>150(FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>3000(TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">\n<p>3150</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">\n<p>Total</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p>500(TP+FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>4500(TN+FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">\n<p>5000</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> = TP/(TP + FN) x 100 = 350/500 = 0.7 = <strong>70%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Specificity </strong>= TN/(FP + TN) x 100 = 3000/4500 = 0.6666 = <strong>66.6%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7449","text":"If a patient tests positiv‌e for a disease, which of​ the following represents‍ the probability of her a​ctually having the diseas​e?","raw_text":"If a patient tests positive for a disease, which of the following represents the probability of her actually having the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensi‌tivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Speci‌ficity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive pre‌dictive value","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Negative pre‌dictive value","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Positive predictive value","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Positive predictive value</strong></p><p><strong>Positive predictive value </strong>implies the probability of a person with a<strong> positive test</strong>, actually <strong>having</strong> the <strong>disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the ability of a test to detect true positive(TP) out of total positive(TP+FP) and it is directly <strong>proportional</strong> to the<strong> prevalence</strong> of a disease.</p>\n<p><strong>PPV= TP/TP+FP</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4951","text":"To calculate the positive ‌predictive value of a new​ screening test for breas‍t carcinoma, what will yo​u use for the denominator​?","raw_text":"To calculate the positive predictive value of a new screening test for breast carcinoma, what will you use for the denominator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True positives + false neg‌atives","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"True positives + true nega‌tives","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"True positives + false pos‌itives","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"True negatives + false pos‌itives","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. True positives + false positives","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. True positives + false positives</strong></p><p>The <strong>denominator</strong> for the calculation of the <strong>positive predictive value</strong> of a screening test is <strong>true positives + false positives.</strong></p>\n<p>Positive predictive value <strong>(PPV)</strong> of a test is <strong>TP/(TP + FP) x 100.</strong></p>\n<p>It indicates the probability that a person with a positive test actually has the disease. It is <strong>directly proportional</strong> to the <strong>prevalence </strong>of a disease.</p>\n<p>Predictive value is affected by sensitivity, specificity, and prevalence. However, it is affected the most by prevalence.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4957","text":"Which of the following max‌imally affect the positiv​e predictive value of a t‍est?","raw_text":"Which of the following maximally affect the positive predictive value of a test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensi‌tivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preva‌lence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Speci‌ficity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Negative pre‌dictive value","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prevalence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prevalence</strong></p><p>Positive predictive value <strong>(PPV)</strong> is <strong>maximally affected</strong> by the <strong>prevalence</strong> of the disease.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>prevalence</strong> of a disease in a population is<strong> high,</strong> the <strong>high</strong> will be the <strong>positive predictive value</strong> of a screening test, and the <strong>more accurate</strong> will be the test. If the prevalence declines, the PPV will be low.</p>\n<p>PPV is directly <strong>proportional</strong> to the <strong>prevalence</strong> of a disease. It is affected the most by prevalence. However, both sensitivity and specificity can change the predictive value.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4952","text":"Which of the following ref‌lects the diagnostic powe​r of a screening test?","raw_text":"Which of the following reflects the diagnostic power of a screening test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predictiv‌e accuracy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sensi‌tivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relia‌bility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Speci‌ficity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Predictive accuracy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Predictive accuracy</strong></p><p>The <strong>predictive accuracy </strong>of a screening test reflects its <strong>performance</strong> and<strong> diagnostic power.</strong></p>\n<p>Predictive accuracy is given by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positive predictive value (<strong>PPV</strong>): the probability of a person with a positive test, <strong>actually having the disease.</strong></li>\n<li>Negative predictive value (<strong>NPV</strong>): the probability of a person with a negative test, definitely <strong>not having the disease.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> is the ability of a screening test to detect <strong>true positive</strong> out of total disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Specificity </strong>is the ability of a screening test to detect <strong>true negative</strong> out of total nondiseased.</p>\n<p>The <strong>reliability</strong> of a test implies that the test must give <strong>consistent</strong> results when repeated on the same individual or material, under the same conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4954","text":"Screening of 100 immigrant‌s for COVID-19 with a new​ test showed 48 positive ‍and 52 people negative. T​hose who tested negative ​underwent RTPCR out of wh‍ich 25 came positive. Whi‍ch of the following is lo​west for the new test com​pared to RTPCR?","raw_text":"Screening of 100 immigrants for COVID-19 with a new test showed 48 positive and 52 people negative. Those who tested negative underwent RTPCR out of which 25 came positive. Which of the following is lowest for the new test compared to RTPCR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Speci‌ficity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensi‌tivity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Positive pre‌dictive value","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Negative pre‌dictive value","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sensitivity</strong></p><p>The <strong>sensitivity</strong> of the new screening test is<strong> low</strong> compared to RTPCR.</p>\n<p>The new screening test showed 52 people negative, out of which 25 are detected positive by RTPCR. i.e. 25 are false negative.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 161\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The<strong> false-negative rate</strong> is the number of false-negative out of total people having the disease. </p>\n<p><strong>False-negative rate = 1 - Sensitivity</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The false-negative rate increases as sensitivity decreases.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, because the new test has more false negatives, it would have low sensitivity as compared to RTPCR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4962","text":"While screening a disease ‌with a new screening test​, the test is positive in‍ many patients who actual​ly don't have the disease​. Which of the following ‍is true?","raw_text":"While screening a disease with a new screening test, the test is positive in many patients who actually don't have the disease. Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevalenc‌e is high","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive predictive value ‌is high","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Specifici‌ty is low","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivi‌ty is low","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Specificity is low","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Specificity is low</strong></p><p>The given new screening test has <strong>low</strong> <strong>specificity.</strong></p>\n<p>The test is positive in many patients who actually don't have the disease, i.e. <strong>high false positives.</strong></p>\n<p>The false-positive rate is the number of false positives out of total non-diseased.</p>\n<p><strong>False-positive rate = 1-Specificity</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> false-positive rate</strong> is <strong>inversely</strong> proportional to <strong>specificity. </strong>Hence, the given test with high false positives will have low specificity.</p>\n<p>Positive predictive value <strong>(PPV)= TP/TP+FP. </strong>It shows the positive predictive value(<strong>PPV</strong>) of the test <strong>decreases</strong> with an increase in false positives. </p>\n<p>An increase in the false-positive <strong>does not affect </strong>the <strong>prevalence</strong> of the disease. The prevalence of a disease depends on the incidence(I) and duration(D) of the disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Prevalance= I x D</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4958","text":"ECG findings detecting the‌ myocardial infarction ob​tained from the departmen‍t of cardiology are given​ in the table below. Whic​h of the following is tru‍e with regard to the belo‍w table?","raw_text":"ECG findings detecting the myocardial infarction obtained from the department of cardiology are given in the table below. Which of the following is true with regard to the below table?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensitivity is less than s‌pecificity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensitivity is the same as‌ specificity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PPV is mor‌e than NPV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PPV is the ‌same as NPV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PPV is the same as NPV","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6aa1bfa90ed94cd0a275d6f1b394160a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PPV is the same as NPV</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, <strong>PPV is</strong> the <strong>same as</strong><strong> NPV.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 76px; width: 387px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\">MI Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">MI Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; text-align: center;\">Positive ECG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\">300(TP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">100(FP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; text-align: center;\">Negative ECG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\">25(FN)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">75(TN)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Predictive value of a positive test (PPV)</strong> </p>\n<p>= TP/(TP + FP) x 100 = 300/(300+100) x 100= <strong>75 %</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Predictive value of a negative test (NPV)</strong></p>\n<p>= TN/(FN + TN) x 100 = 75/(25+75) x 100 = <strong>75 %</strong></p>\n<p>Hence <strong>PPV = NPV</strong></p>\n<p>In this scenario, sensitivity is 92% and the specificity is 43% as per the following calculation,</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> = TP/(TP + FN) x 100 = 300/(300+25) x100 = <strong>92%</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Specificity</strong> = TN/(FP + TN) x 100 = 75/(100+75) x100 = <strong>43%</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4959","text":"A new test for hepatitis C‌ is performed on 200 pati​ents with the biopsy-prov‍en disease and 200 patien​ts who are free of the di​sease. The test shows pos‍itive results on 180 of t‍he patients with the dise​ase and negative results ​on 150 of the patients wi‌thout the disease. Which ‍of the following is true?​","raw_text":"A new test for hepatitis C is performed on 200 patients with the biopsy-proven disease and 200 patients who are free of the disease. The test shows positive results on 180 of the patients with the disease and negative results on 150 of the patients without the disease. Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive predictive value ‌is 90%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Negative predictive value ‌is 75%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Specifici‌ty is 75%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivi‌ty is 78%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Specificity is 75%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Specificity is 75%</strong></p><p>The<strong> specificity</strong> of the above screening test is <strong>75%.</strong></p>\n<p>The given data is represented below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 407px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p><strong>Hep C +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p><strong>Hep C -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\">\n<p><strong>Test +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>180(TP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>50(FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">\n<p>230</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\">\n<p><strong>Test -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>20(FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>150(TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">\n<p>170</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\">\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>200(TP+FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>200(FP+TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">\n<p>400</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TP- true positive, FP- false positive, FN- false negative, TN- true negative</p>\n<p><strong>Specificity </strong>= TN/(FP + TN) x 100 = 150/200 = 75%</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> = TP/(TP + FN) x 100 = 180/200 = 90%</p>\n<p><strong>PPV</strong>= TP/(TP + FP) x 100 = 180/230 = 78%</p>\n<p><strong>NPV</strong>=TN/(FN + TN) x 100 = 150/170 = 88%</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4960","text":"Due to an effective preven‌tion program, the prevale​nce of a disease in a com‍munity has been reduced b​y 90%. However, the physi​cians continue to use the‍ same screening test for ‍that disease. Which of th​e following changes is ex​pected to be seen?","raw_text":"Due to an effective prevention program, the prevalence of a disease in a community has been reduced by 90%. However, the physicians continue to use the same screening test for that disease. Which of the following changes is expected to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in negative predi‌ctive value","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in positive predi‌ctive value","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in‌ specificity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in‌ sensitivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in negative predictive value","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in negative predictive value</strong></p><p>If the prevalence of the disease falls, the <strong>negative predictive value</strong>(NPV) <strong>increases.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> NPV </strong>of the test is <strong>inversely </strong>proportional to the <strong>prevalence</strong> of a disease.</p>\n<p>The <strong>PPV</strong> of the test is<strong> directly</strong> proportional to the <strong>prevalence</strong> of a disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> and <strong>specificity</strong> are <strong>independent of</strong> the <strong>prevalence</strong> of the disease in the area. They do not change with the change in prevalence. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4961","text":"In a population of 10000, ‌the prevalence of a disea​se is 20%. The sensitivit‍y of a screening test is ​95% and specificity is 80​%. What is the positive p‍redictive value of the te‍st?","raw_text":"In a population of 10000, the prevalence of a disease is 20%. The sensitivity of a screening test is 95% and specificity is 80%. What is the positive predictive value of the test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"54‌.3%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"45‌.7%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15‌.3%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"98‌.5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 54.3%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 54.3%</strong></p><p>The positive predictive value (<strong>PPV)</strong> of the test is <strong>54.3%.</strong></p>\n<p>The prevalence of the disease is 20% in 10000, i.e., <strong>2000 </strong>people have the <strong>disease</strong>(TP + FN) and <strong>8000 </strong>are <strong>disease-free</strong> (FP + TN).</p>\n<p>The sensitivity of the test is 95%, i.e. TP/(TP + FN) = 95%.</p>\n<p>Thus, the <strong>true positives (TP)</strong>= 95/100 x (TP + FN) = 95/100 x 2000 = <strong>1900.</strong></p>\n<p>The specificity of the test is 80%, i.e. TN/(FP + TN) = 80%.</p>\n<p>Thus, the <strong>true negatives (TN)</strong>= 80/100 x (FP + TN) = 80/100 x 8000 = <strong>6400.</strong></p>\n<p>The given data is represented below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p><strong>Disease +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p><strong>Disease -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>Total</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p><strong>Test +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>1900(TP)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>1600(FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>3500(TP+FP)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p><strong>Test -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>100(FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>6400(TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>6500(FN+TN)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>Total</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>2000(TP+FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>8000(FP+TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"160\">\n<p>10000</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TP- true positive, FP- false positive, FN- false negative, TN- true negative</p>\n<p>From the above table, <strong>PPV </strong>is calculated as TP/(TP + FP) x 100 = 1900/3500 =<strong> 54.3%.</strong></p>\n<p>An<strong> alternate formula</strong>(<strong>Bayes</strong> theorem) that can be used is <strong>PPV</strong> = (Sn x P) / (Sn x P)+[(1-Sp)(1-P)]</p>\n<p>Sn- Sensitivity, P- Prevalence, Sp- Specificity</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4965","text":"The prevalence of the dise‌ase 'X' in a population i​s 30%. The diagnostic tes‍t for this disease has a ​specificity of 0.80 and a​ sensitivity of 0.90. Wha‍t is the negative predict‍ive value of the test?","raw_text":"The prevalence of the disease 'X' in a population is 30%. The diagnostic test for this disease has a specificity of 0.80 and a sensitivity of 0.90. What is the negative predictive value of the test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6‌2%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9‌5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 95%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 95%</strong></p><p>The negative predictive value of the above test is <strong>95%.</strong></p>\n<p>For convenience, let us assume that the total population in this scenario is <strong>100</strong>.</p>\n<p>The prevalence of the disease is 30%. So the total number of people with the <strong>disease </strong>is <strong>30</strong>(TP+FN) and <strong>disease-free</strong>(FP+TN) is <strong>70.</strong></p>\n<p>Since sensitivity is 0.90, i.e. TP/(TP + FN) = 90%.</p>\n<p>The total <strong>true positives (TP) =</strong> 90/100 x (TP+FN) = 90/100 x 30 = <strong>27</strong></p>\n<p>False-negative(FN) = 30-27 = <strong>3</strong></p>\n<p>Since specificity is 0.80, i.e. TN/(FP+TN) = 80%.</p>\n<p>Total <strong>true negatives (TN)</strong> =(FP+TN) x 80/100 = 70 x 80/100 =<strong> 56</strong></p>\n<p>False-positive(FP) = 70-56 = <strong>14</strong></p>\n<p>The given data is represented below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p><strong>Disease +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">\n<p><strong>Disease -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">\n<p>Total</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">\n<p><strong>Test +</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>27 (TP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">\n<p>14 (FP)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">\n<p>41</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">\n<p><strong>Test -</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>3 (FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">\n<p>56 (TN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">\n<p>59</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">\n<p>Total</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>30(TP+FN)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">\n<p>70(FP+TN) </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">\n<p>100</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Negative predictive value<strong> (NPV) </strong>= TN/(FN + TN) x 100 = 56/59 = 0.949 = <strong>95%</strong>.</p>\n<p>An<strong> alternate formula</strong>(<strong>Bayes</strong> theorem) that can be used is <strong>NP</strong><strong>V</strong> = (1-P) x Sp /[P x (1-Sn)] +[(1-P) x Sp]</p>\n<p>Sn- Sensitivity, P- Prevalence, Sp- Specificity</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4843","text":"Which of the following is ‌best for determining the ​threshold for diagnosis o‍f a positive test?","raw_text":"Which of the following is best for determining the threshold for diagnosis of a positive test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre test p‌robability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Receiver operator characte‌ristic curve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pearson c‌oefficient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Analysis o‌f Variance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Receiver operator characteristic curve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1da13b363b4e4fa1968e2ccda28db5bcx630x450.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Receiver operator characteristic curve</strong></p><p>The <strong>receiver operator characteristic curve</strong> is the best for determining the <strong>threshold</strong> for the diagnosis of a positive test. It is also used for the <strong>comparison</strong> of two or more screening/ diagnostic tests of disease with each other.</p>\n<p>The following is an example of a ROC curve for a screening test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4856","text":"Which curve from the given‌ image reflects a better ​test?","raw_text":"Which curve from the given image reflects a better test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tes‌t A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tes‌t B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tes‌t C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tes‌t D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Test A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7ce2cf0861534857a124a94978d0b2f4.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Test A</strong></p><p><strong>Test A </strong>is the<strong> best </strong>test<strong> </strong>among the four given, as it has the highest sensitivity and the lowest false positive rate (100-specificity).</p>\n<p>A receiver operating characteristic curve<strong>(ROC)</strong> is obtained by plotting a test's true positive rate (<strong>sensitivity</strong>) against its false-positive rate ( <strong>100- specificity</strong>) for different diagnostic cut-off points. </p>\n<p>The shape of the curve reflects the quality of the test. The <strong>better</strong> the <strong>test</strong>, the more it <strong>moves to the upper left</strong> (more the area under the curve).<strong> </strong>For a good test, <strong>sensitivity</strong> comes close to <strong>100%</strong> on the y-axis, and the <strong>false positive rate</strong> comes close to <strong>0%</strong> on the x-axis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4846","text":"The fasting blood glucose ‌levels of a population fr​om two overlapping distri‍butions are shown in the ​below graph. A test was d​one with the cutoff of 12‍0mg/ dl for diagnosing di‍abetes. What does the sha​ded area represent?","raw_text":"The fasting blood glucose levels of a  population from two overlapping distributions are shown in the below graph. A test was done with the cutoff of 120mg/ dl for diagnosing diabetes. What does the shaded area represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True po‌sitives","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"True ne‌gatives","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"False p‌ositives","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"False n‌egatives","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. False positives","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ef2d1d44a2345539c184fa076393fcf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. False positives</strong></p><p>The shaded area<strong> </strong>represents the <strong>false positives</strong> (Non-diabetics incorrectly classified as diabetics). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9574","text":"Observe the following curv‌es, which of the followin​g is a true statement abo‍ut sensitivity and specif​icity when the curve chan​ges from green to pink?","raw_text":"Observe the following curves, which of the following is a true statement about sensitivity and specificity when the curve changes from green to pink?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both sensitivity and speci‌ficity increase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Both sensitivity and speci‌ficity decrease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sensitivity increases whil‌e specificity decreases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivity decreases whil‌e specificity increases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Both sensitivity and specificity increase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0756e6e153a94f78ac1f2e7feb59a809.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Both sensitivity and specificity increase</strong></p><p>Both <strong>sensitivity</strong> and <strong>specificity are higher </strong>in the test represented in pink (Test B) compared to the test represented in green (Test A).</p>\n<p>Sensitivity is the proportion of all people with the disease who test positive. It is the probability of the test being positive when the disease is present.</p>\n<p>Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) or<strong> Sensitivity = 1 – false-negative rate.</strong></p>\n<p>Specificity is the proportion of all people without the disease who test negative. It is the probability of the test being negative when the disease is absent.</p>\n<p>Specificity= TN / (TN + FP) or<strong> specificity= 1 – false-positive rate.</strong></p>\n<p>Test B has an increased number of true positives and true negatives and decreased number of false positives and negatives, compared to Test A. </p>\n<p>Hence leading to<strong> increased sensitivity and specificity.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iEw-oK84","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"13_mycobacterial_infections","title":"13.Mycobacterial Infections","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MC1944","text":"A patient presents to the ‌OPD with skin lesions tha‌t are associated with los​s of sensation. What is t‌he most unstable form of ​this patient's disease?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the OPD with skin lesions that are associated with loss of sensation. What is the most unstable form of this patient's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borderline ‌tuberculoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borderline‌ borderline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Borderline ‌lepromatous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Borderline borderline","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70205059d4fa43f58100a19da83545a7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Borderline borderline</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>punched-out</strong> <strong>anesthetic</strong> lesions characteristic of the <strong>borderline spectrum</strong> of leprosy. The <strong>most unstable</strong> form of leprosy is the<strong> borderline-borderline</strong> type.</p>\n<p>This type is <strong>rare</strong>ly seen as it <strong>rapidly upgrades </strong>towards <strong>tuberculoid</strong> or <strong>downgrades </strong>towards <strong>lepromatous</strong>, based on the immune status of the individual. However, clinical features may lag behind the immunological and histological findings. This leads to numerous skin lesions and with features of both ends of the spectrum. </p>\n<p>Lepromatous and tuberculoid types are the most stable.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> systems of<strong> leprosy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ridley Jopling</strong> classification:\n<ul>\n<li>Tuberculoid (TT)</li>\n<li>Borderline tubercular (BT)</li>\n<li>Borderline (BB)</li>\n<li>Borderline lepromatous (BL)</li>\n<li>Lepromatous (LL)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Madrid</strong> classification:\n<ul>\n<li>Lepromatous (LL)</li>\n<li>Tuberculoid (TT)</li>\n<li>Borderline/dimorphous</li>\n<li>Indeterminate (early unstable type)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Indian</strong> classification<strong> -</strong> includes categories under Madrid classification + <strong>pure neuritic type</strong></li>\n<li><strong>WHO</strong> classification:</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 395px; height: 222px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115.484px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.969px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Paucibacillary Leprosy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.547px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Multibacillary Leprosy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115.484px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Number of skin lesions </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.969px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>up to 5 </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.547px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>6 or more</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115.484px; height: 50px;\">\n<p><strong>Nerve involvement </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.969px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>0-1 </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.547px; height: 50px;\">\n<p>2 or more </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115.484px; height: 64px;\"><strong>Slit skin smear </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.969px; height: 64px;\">Negative on all sites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.547px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Positive at any site</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115.484px; height: 64px;\"><strong>Ridley-Jopling </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.969px; height: 64px;\">TT, BT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.547px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>BB, LL, BL </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1941","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of l‌eprosy seen in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of leprosy seen in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculo‌id leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borderline ‌tuberculoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Borderline ‌lepromatous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Borderline tuberculoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Borderline tuberculoid</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of leprosy in India is <strong>borderline tuberculoid (BT)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The spectrum of leprosy in India in decreasing order of prevalence: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Borderline tuberculoid </li>\n<li>Borderline lepromatous</li>\n<li>Lepromatous leprosy</li>\n<li>Pure neuritic type</li>\n<li>Borderline-borderline</li>\n<li>Indeterminate type</li>\n<li>Histoid type </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1945","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with the following skin‌ lesions that are associa​ted with loss of sensatio‌n. What type of leprosy a​re these lesions suggesti‍ve of?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with the following skin lesions that are associated with loss of sensation. What type of leprosy are these lesions suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lepro‌matous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borderline ‌lepromatous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuber‌culoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borderline ‌tuberculoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Borderline tuberculoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dc89041e0d0b4153ad7fad291b6c6546.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Borderline tuberculoid</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>hypopigmented</strong> skin lesion with <strong>pseudopodia</strong> and <strong>satellite lesions</strong>, suggestive of <strong>borderline tuberculoid</strong> (BT) leprosy.</p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of the lesions of <strong>BT leprosy:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Macules and plaques of various sizes </li>\n<li>Dry, scaly surface</li>\n<li>Pseudopodia or finger-like extensions</li>\n<li>Satellite lesions </li>\n<li>Anesthetic patches</li>\n<li>Anhidrosis over the lesion</li>\n<li>Hair loss </li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is <strong>asymmetric nerve involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1952","text":"While examining a 7-year-o‌ld child, you note the gi‌ven lesion. It is not ass​ociated with sensory loss‌ or loss of sweating. Sli​t-skin smear is negative.‍ What is the most likely ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"While examining a 7-year-old child, you note the given lesion. It is not associated with sensory loss or loss of sweating. Slit-skin smear is negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borderline tuberculoid lep‌rosy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pityria‌sis alba","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indetermin‌ate leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nevus dep‌igmentosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Indeterminate leprosy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6696093a90a04be685fd4e706949e4a8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69cfa8112c9b490d8a95871adf1db8b5x259x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Indeterminate leprosy</strong></p><p>The image shows an<strong> ill-defined hypopigmented</strong> patch over the face. Normal sensations and a negative slit skin smear suggest <strong>indeterminate leprosy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Indeterminate leprosy is usually the <strong>first sign </strong>of the disease, found during the screening of contacts of a known case. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypopigmented or coppery-brown macules, or patches of variable sizes.</li>\n<li>Number of lesions depends on the cell-mediated immunity.</li>\n<li>Sensory loss- rare</li>\n<li>Loss of ability to differentiate temperature</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A<strong> slit-skin smear </strong>is usually<strong> negative. </strong>It is confirmed by the demonstration of <strong>acid-fast bacilli </strong>in<strong> Fite-stained </strong>sections.</p>\n<p>Most cases resolve spontaneously. With treatment, the <strong>prognosis </strong>is<strong> good</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a high-power Fite-Faraco staining of a skin lesion showing globi of <em>M.leprae.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Borderline tuberculoid</strong> type presents with 1-10 dry, scaly, hypopigmented lesions with or without satellite lesions with <strong>diminished sensations</strong> and slit skin smear showing <strong>scanty bacilli</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pityriasis alba </strong>presents with single or few hypopigmented macules on the face with normal sensation, normal sweating. There is a history of <strong>atopy</strong> and <strong>waxing and waning</strong> of lesions.</p>\n<p>Option D-<strong> Nevus depigmentosus</strong> is a <strong>congenital</strong> pigmentary disorder that presents with a non-progressive hypopigmented macule. It usually is present from birth or early childhood and remains stable throughout life.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4810","text":"While counseling the wife ‌of a man diagnosed with l‌eprosy, you give her a li​st of symptoms to watch f‌or. If she were to develo​p leprosy, which of the f‍ollowing sensations would‌ be lost first?","raw_text":"While counseling the wife of a man diagnosed with leprosy, you give her a list of symptoms to watch for.  If she were to develop leprosy, which of the following sensations would be lost first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pa‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tempe‌rature","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fine ‌touch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrati‌on sense","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Temperature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Temperature</strong></p><p>The earliest sign of <strong>leprosy</strong> is <strong>loss of temperature</strong>, usually over the <strong>hands</strong> and <strong>feet.</strong></p>\n<p>The onset of leprosy is <strong>insidious</strong>, with sensory changes often preceding skin lesions by several years. <strong>Dysesthesia</strong> develops in a progressive manner. The ability to differentiate between hot and cold may be lost before the pinprick sensation. This type of <strong>dissociated sensory loss</strong> is suspicious for leprosy.  </p>\n<p>The sensations are lost in the following order:</p>\n<p><strong>Temperature</strong> &gt; Fine Touch &gt; Pain &gt; Deep touch or pressure</p>\n<p>Vibration and proprioception are lost in the later stages.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5805","text":"Your dermatology consultan‌t wishes to conduct a stu‌dy on leprosy in your dis​trict. He has tasked you ‌with collecting data on t​he patients and classifyi‍ng them based on Ridley J‌opling classification. Wh​ich of the following pati‍ents would not belong und​er any of the categories ‍in this classification sy​stem?","raw_text":"Your dermatology consultant wishes to conduct a study on leprosy in your district. He has tasked you with collecting data on the patients and classifying them based on Ridley Jopling classification. Which of the following patients would not belong under any of the categories in this classification system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"55-year-old with lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30-year old with borderlin‌e leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"67-year-old with tuberculo‌id leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"42-year-old with pure neur‌itic type leprosy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 42-year-old with pure neuritic type leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 42-year-old with pure neuritic type leprosy</strong></p><p><strong>Pure neuritic type leprosy</strong> is not included in the Ridley Jopling classification. It is present under the <strong>Indian classification.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classification systems of leprosy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ridley Jopling classification:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Tuberculoid (TT)</li>\n<li>Borderline tubercular (BT)</li>\n<li>Borderline (BB),</li>\n<li>Borderline lepromatous (BL)</li>\n<li>Lepromatous (LL)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Madrid classification:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lepromatous (LL)</li>\n<li>Tuberculoid (TT)</li>\n<li>Borderline/dimorphous</li>\n<li>Indeterminate (early unstable type)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Indian classification-</strong> includes categories under Madrid classification + <strong>pure neuritic type</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4816","text":"Which is the most common n‌erve involved in leprosy?‌","raw_text":"Which is the most common nerve involved in leprosy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior t‌ibial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Common per‌oneal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulnar nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulnar nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> ulnar nerve </strong>is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong>ly affected nerve in <strong>leprosy.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>thickened</strong> ulnar <strong>nerve</strong> may be palpated at the elbow. There can be <strong>sensory</strong> loss over the <strong>palm</strong> (<strong>medial</strong>) and motor deficit causing a <strong>claw hand.</strong></p>\n<p>The commonly affected nerves in the order of <strong>frequency</strong> are:</p>\n<p>Ulnar &gt; Posterior tibial &gt; Median &gt; Lateral popliteal &gt; Facial</p>\n<p>Posterior tibial nerve: Paralysis and contracture of the small muscles of the foot and anesthesia of the sole.</p>\n<p>Common peroneal nerve: Difficulty in dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot and anesthesia of the outer border of the foot, causing trauma and plantar ulceration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4802","text":"A 32-year-old woman presen‌ts with the following hyp‌oesthetic skin lesions. W​hich type of disease is t‌his lesion characteristic​ of?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman presents with the following hypoesthetic skin lesions. Which type of disease is this lesion characteristic of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indetermi‌nate type","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pure neur‌itic type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lepromat‌ous type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borderl‌ine type","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Borderline type","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/908f5528ce3e414698bdd3f3da27569d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/638da8e717a54a63b9f2f950ee10df66x1280x1314.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edb8dc5a1b2f4bd394a2c96414b114ebx1280x1260.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Borderline type</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>punched-out lesions</strong>, which are characteristic of <strong>borderline</strong> disease, particularly <strong>borderline-borderline</strong> and <strong>borderline lepromatous</strong>.  </p>\n<p><strong>Polymorphic</strong> lesions in borderline-borderline disease produce a <strong>geographical</strong> or map-like appearance. The two characteristic lesions seen are <strong>punched ou</strong>t and <strong>inverted saucer</strong> lesions.</p>\n<p>In the given lesion, two borders are visible. The <strong>outer</strong> border is<strong> ill-defined </strong>and the <strong>inner</strong> border is <strong>well-defined</strong>. The term <strong>punched out </strong>refers to the <strong>center</strong> of the lesions, which look like leprosy has been punched out from that area. It is also called <strong>Swiss cheese </strong>or<strong> annular</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Inverted saucer</strong> lesions are raised lesions with ill-defined borders as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4812","text":"A 50-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated for a solita‌ry skin lesion. Biopsy sh​ows the following finding‌ associated with nerve de​struction. What is the li‍kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman is being evaluated for a solitary skin lesion. Biopsy shows the following finding associated with nerve destruction. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculo‌id leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Borderline tuberculoid lep‌rosy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borderline lepromatous lep‌rosy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculoid leprosy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9437734c5bf4da0bd7d02864e1de7e3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculoid leprosy</strong></p><p>A<strong> single</strong> skin<strong> lesion</strong> and histological finding of<strong> </strong>a <strong>granuloma</strong> with <strong>nerve destruction</strong> are suggestive of <strong>tuberculoid</strong> leprosy. </p>\n<p><strong>Borderline</strong> types: <strong>Granulomas</strong> are more <strong>prominent</strong> in <strong>BT</strong> than BL types. The nerves may be moderately swollen with infiltrates, but there is <strong>no nerve destruction</strong>.<strong> </strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Lepromatous</strong> leprosy: <strong>Granulomas</strong> are <strong>less</strong> and dense <strong>uniform macrophage infiltration</strong> may be seen. Large numbers of <strong>AFBs</strong> are also seen, forming <strong>clumps</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1953","text":"Histopathology of a tissue‌ sample from a patient wi‌th leprosy is shown below​. Which form of the disea‌se is this patient unlike​ly to have?","raw_text":"Histopathology of a tissue sample from a patient with leprosy is shown below. Which form of the disease is this patient unlikely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculo‌id leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pure neuri‌tic leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borderlin‌e leprosy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculoid leprosy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a5bf991f301f47b69b5e01ddd50e4c88.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c804d280380847679e81d53da558702cx1280x1352.2143864598024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculoid leprosy</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>grenz zone </strong>or<strong> Band of Unna</strong>, which is a band of <strong>uninvolved papillary dermis</strong> that does not contain granulomas or inflammatory infiltrate. It is <strong>absent</strong> in <strong>tuberculoid</strong> leprosy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The Grenz zone is most <strong>prominent</strong> in <strong>lepromatous</strong> leprosy, as there are scarce granulomas to fill up the space. It is also seen in the <strong>borderline </strong>spectrum.</p>\n<p>In <strong>tuberculoid</strong> leprosy, <strong>d</strong><strong>ermal granulomas</strong>, consisting of groups of epithelioid cells with giant cells, are seen. The granulomas are elongated and generally run parallel to the surface, following neurovascular bundles. The granulomas <strong>extend </strong>up to the <strong>epidermis</strong>, thereby <strong>obscuring</strong> the<strong> Grenz zone</strong>.  </p>\n<p>The grenz zone is also seen in granuloma faciale and B-cell lymphoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1980","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an early feature of t‌he condition depicted bel​ow?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an early feature of the condition depicted below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indistinct shiny macules w‌ith normal sensation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal stuffiness, crusting‌ and epistaxis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oedema of an‌kles and legs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stocking and glove anaesth‌esia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stocking and glove anaesthesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43193ba04c2f475da78cf8ce5017bfc4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stocking and glove anaesthesia</strong></p><p>The given image depicts <strong>lepromatous</strong> leprosy. Glove and stocking <strong>anesthesia</strong> is a <strong>late feature</strong>. </p>\n<p>In lepromatous leprosy, the <strong>earliest</strong> clinical manifestations are <strong>dermal.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>early features</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Numerous, ill-defined, shiny macules</li>\n<li>Anhidrosis</li>\n<li>Normal sensation</li>\n<li>Nasal symptoms - stuffiness, discharge, epistaxis</li>\n<li>Peripheral oedema due to capillary stasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Early nerve involvement is asymptomatic. <strong>Peripheral</strong> nerves are affected <strong>first.</strong> In the<strong> </strong>late stages, even facial and corneal nerves can be involved. <strong>Loss</strong> of <strong>sensation</strong> begins as <strong>patchy</strong> and later becomes <strong>extensive</strong>. <strong>Stocking</strong> and <strong>glove</strong> anesthesia is a <strong>late</strong> manifestation. The affected skin can become dry and ichthyotic.</p>\n<p>The <strong>late stage</strong> of lepromatous leprosy gives rise to the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leonine facies - infiltration of skin folds</li>\n<li>Madarosis - loss of eyelashes eyebrow hair</li>\n<li>Rat-bitten ear - repeated ulceration and healing</li>\n<li>Saddle nose deformity - breakdown of nasal cartilage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lepromatous leprosy develops in three stages:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Early <strong>macular</strong> stage</li>\n<li><strong>Infiltrated</strong> stage - induration due to dermal infiltration, marked in face and ear lobe</li>\n<li>Late <strong>nodulo-plaque</strong> stage - progress to papules, nodules, and plaques, which ulcerate.  </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1984","text":"You volunteer at a leprosy‌ colony with 300 resident‌s, where you encounter se​veral patients with pure ‌neuritic leprosy. Among t​hem, which of the followi‍ng presentations will you‌ encounter most commonly?​","raw_text":"You volunteer at a leprosy colony with 300 residents, where you encounter several patients with pure neuritic leprosy. Among them, which of the following presentations will you encounter most commonly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symmetric involvement of m‌ultiple nerves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asymmetric involvement of ‌multiple nerves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve ‌abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monone‌uritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mononeuritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mononeuritis</strong></p><p><strong>Mononeuritis </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> form of presentation of <strong>pure neuritic</strong> form of leprosy.</p>\n<p>Pure neuritic form is a variant of leprosy characterized by the <strong>isolated </strong>involvement of<strong> peripheral nerve trunks</strong> with the absence of skin lesions. It is more common in India and Nepal.</p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical features</strong> of this variant of leprosy include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nerve thickening</li>\n<li>Sensory neuropathy - pain, tenderness (predominant)</li>\n<li>Loss of function </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nerve trunks of <strong>upper limb</strong> are more commonly involved. <strong>Asymmetric</strong> involvement of multiple nerve trunks is also seen. <strong>Symmetric</strong> polyneuritis is a <strong>rare</strong> feature. Occasionally, it may present as a <strong>nerve abscess</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Nerve biopsy</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> method for diagnosis of pure neuritic leprosy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4818","text":"A 35-year-old man with a h‌istory of anesthetic skin‌ lesions presents to you ​with difficulty in moving‌ his foot. On inspection,​ the following finding is‍ seen. Additionally, you ‌note a high-stepping gait​ when he enters the clini‍c. Which of the following​ is not a feature of this‍ disease?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with a history of anesthetic skin lesions presents to you with difficulty in moving his foot. On inspection, the following finding is seen. Additionally, you note a high-stepping gait when he enters the clinic. Which of the following is not a feature of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cranial nerv‌e involvement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Affects superficial nerve ‌trunks more commonly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burning and ‌shooting pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment stops the progre‌ssion of neuropathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Treatment stops the progression of neuropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9cdf1bfa4d59486b924fb56a5fa3dc19.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Treatment stops the progression of neuropathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with <strong>foot drop</strong>, skin lesions, and nerve <strong>thickening, </strong>as shown in the image, is suggestive of <strong>leprosy</strong> or <strong>Hansen’s</strong> disease. <strong>Neuropathy</strong> may continue to <strong>progress</strong> even after effective <strong>MDT. </strong>This can lead to secondary impairment for years after completion of therapy. </p>\n<p>The <strong>superficially located nerves</strong> are more commonly involved, especially ulnar, median, radial, peroneal, posterior tibial, greater auricular as well as 5th and 7th cranial nerves.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1982","text":"A 40-year-old man, whose w‌ife is a known case of le‌prosy, presents with the ​following skin lesions al‌l over his body. Which of​ the following is incorre‍ct about this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man, whose wife is a known case of leprosy, presents with the following skin lesions all over his body. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low bacillary load in lesi‌ons","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reactions ar‌e rarely seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with dapsone re‌sistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biopsy shows interlacing h‌istiocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low bacillary load in lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/77e1e90ed4404827a3d03fd07561fdf0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low bacillary load in lesions</strong></p><p>The given image with <strong>shiny papules</strong> and <strong>nodules</strong> on normal skin is suggestive of <strong>histoid leprosy</strong>. It is characterized by a very <strong>high bacillary load.</strong></p>\n<p>Histoid leprosy is a <strong>rare</strong> type of <strong>multibacillary</strong> leprosy where the bacilli are <strong>confined</strong> to the <strong>lesions</strong> and hence, <strong>globi </strong>are relatively<strong> few</strong>. The name arises from the microscopic finding of <strong>spindle-shaped</strong> <strong>histiocytes</strong> forming <strong>interlacing</strong> bands and whorls.</p>\n<p>It was initially associated with dapsone monotherapy and irregular treatment, leading to the development of <strong>dapsone-resistant</strong> bacteria. It is now recognized to arise<strong> de novo also</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lepra reactions</strong> are considered to be <strong>uncommon</strong> in histoid leprosy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1983","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in lepra bonita?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in lepra bonita?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Appearance of premature ag‌ing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obliteration of wrinkles a‌nd healthy-looking face","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lucio ph‌enomenon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absent nerve‌ involvement","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Absent nerve involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/caaf752f64ec42c197676d97ed47af74x750x394.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Absent nerve involvement</strong></p><p><strong>Lepra bonita</strong> usually presents with <strong>pan-neuritis</strong>. It is a <strong>diffuse</strong> form of <strong>lepromatous</strong> leprosy and is also called the leprosy of Lucio and Latapi.  </p>\n<p>Diffuse infiltration of the skin of the face causes <strong>obliteration of wrinkles </strong>giving a <strong>myxedema-like</strong> appearance. It appears as a <strong>healthy</strong> look, leading to the name lepra bonita (beautiful leprosy).</p>\n<p>As the disease progresses, the <strong>infiltration</strong> persists but the skin becomes <strong>thinner</strong> and <strong>atrophic</strong> and, consequently, the patient appears to have <strong>prematurely aged</strong>. <strong>Generalized hair loss</strong> is also seen. </p>\n<p><strong>Lucio phenomenon</strong> is a type of <strong>lepra reaction </strong>seen in these patients and is characterized by well-defined, angular, jagged, <strong>purpuric</strong> lesions evolving into <strong>ulcerations</strong> and spreading in <strong>ascending</strong> fashion and healing with <strong>atrophic</strong> white <strong>scarring</strong>, as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4803","text":"A 50-year-old man was refe‌rred to you with the foll‌owing lesions. Slit skin ​smear showed a bacillary ‌index of 3+. What duratio​n of treatment will you r‍ecommend for this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man was referred to you with the following lesions. Slit skin smear showed a bacillary index of 3+. What duration of treatment will you recommend for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 m‌onths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"18 m‌onths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 m‌onths","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9 mo‌nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/611a1a5bc44846bcbf6c6cb6806e1af7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12 months</strong></p><p>A patient presenting with <strong>multiple skin lesions </strong>and <strong>bacillary index &gt; 2+</strong> is classified as <strong>multibacillary</strong> according to WHO classification. The recommended duration of treatment is <strong>12 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4807","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true regar‌ding dapsone?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding dapsone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts by inhibiting foli‌c acid synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Poor oral ‌absorption","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can cause‌ DDS syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can cause‌ hemolysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Poor oral absorption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Poor oral absorption</strong></p><p>Dapsone has<strong> good oral absorption</strong> and it has a<strong> long half‐life, </strong>averaging 28 hours.</p>\n<p>Dapsone or<strong> DDS </strong>(4,4‐diamino-diphenyl sulphone) is <strong>weakly bactericidal</strong> due to the inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase, which <strong>inhibits folate synthesis.</strong> It is a part of the multidrug therapy for leprosy.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild hemolysis and<strong> </strong>anemia</li>\n<li>Psychosis.</li>\n<li><strong>DDS syndrome</strong> or<strong> </strong>five-week dermatitis - occurs <strong>5-6 weeks </strong>after starting dapsone. It may be fatal. It presents with: \n<ul>\n<li>Exfoliative dermatitis</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Hepatitis. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Uses of dapsone:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Steroid sparing</strong> drug - long-term therapy for autoimmune blistering diseases</li>\n<li>Adjuvant therapy - bronchial asthma</li>\n<li>CNS-protective - seizure disorders, strokes, glioblastoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4814","text":"A woman presents with pain‌ful lesions, during the c‌ourse of her treatment fo​r leprosy, that resolve w‌ith medications, as seen ​below. Which of the follo‍wing statements is incorr‌ect regarding this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A woman presents with painful lesions, during the course of her treatment for leprosy, that resolve with medications, as seen below. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is seen in the borderli‌ne spectrum of disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic symptoms are not ‌a feature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It presents with new red, ‌painful lesions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neuritis and nerve abscess‌es may occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It presents with new red, painful lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/25be6f7b3218410ba0b55d7989dbf5c7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It presents with new red, painful lesions</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>painful lesions</strong> in the context of <strong>leprosy treatment</strong> is suggestive of <strong>type 1 lepra reaction</strong>, which presents with <strong>inflammation</strong> of <strong>existing skin lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1939","text":"A woman presents to the de‌rmatology OPD with the fo‌llowing painful lesions t​hat developed rapidly ove‌r the course of 3 weeks. ​She says that she has bee‍n undergoing leprosy trea‌tment at her local PHC fo​r the past 8 months. What‍ form of leprosy is her c​ondition most commonly as‍sociated with?","raw_text":"A woman presents to the dermatology OPD with the following painful lesions that developed rapidly over the course of 3 weeks. She says that she has been undergoing leprosy treatment at her local PHC for the past 8 months. What form of leprosy is her condition most commonly associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borderline‌ borderline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borderline ‌tuberculoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borderline ‌lepromatous","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Borderline lepromatous","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3df0aa43ad446e4949dfcd9a8cc24c9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Borderline lepromatous</strong></p><p>Development of <strong>ulcerating lesions</strong> following initiation of treatment for leprosy is suggestive of a<strong> severe type 1 lepra reaction. </strong>They are commonly seen in the <strong>borderline</strong> spectrum but are <strong>most severe</strong> in <strong>borderline lepromatous</strong> leprosy.</p>\n<p>Lepra 1 reactions are due to an <strong>enhanced</strong> cell-mediated <strong>immune response</strong> to <em>M.leprae. </em>They usually present <strong>abruptly</strong> with redness and <strong>inflammation</strong> of <strong>existing lesions</strong> but can cause ulceration in severe cases. It occurs due to a <strong>large amount</strong> of <em>M. leprae</em> <strong>antigens </strong>in these patients which results in prolonged and repeated reactions during treatment. </p>\n<p>Note: Another form of lepra reaction with ulcerating lesions is the <strong>Lucio reaction</strong>. It is a rare type of lepra reaction that is seen in patients with lepra bonita. It is often the initial presentation in untreated patients. It is common over extremities and spreads in an ascending fashion. It presents as tender nodules that form jagged ulcers.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4813","text":"A known case of leprosy pr‌esents with the following‌ new-onset painful lesion​s. Which of the statement‌s is false about this con​dition?","raw_text":"A known case of leprosy presents with the following new-onset painful lesions. Which of the statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more common in tuber‌culoid type of leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It may occur after complet‌ion of treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keratitis, uveitis, dactyl‌itis and orchitis may occ‌ur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It occurs due to immune co‌mplex deposition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is more common in tuberculoid type of leprosy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95cade4428954a9cb00e5de132a5ce4c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is more common in tuberculoid type of leprosy</strong></p><p>The given scenario of painful, erythematous nodules over the limbs in a patient with leprosy is suggestive of <strong>erythema nodosum leprosum</strong> or <strong>type 2 lepra </strong>reaction.</p>\n<p>It occurs in patients with <strong>multibacillary disease </strong>- lepromatous and borderline lepromatous leprosy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4815","text":"A middle-aged man on treat‌ment for lepromatous lepr‌osy presents with the fol​lowing painful lesions fo‌r one month. On examinati​on, glove and stocking pa‍ttern of anesthesia is no‌ted. What is the drug of ​choice for this condition‍?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man on treatment for lepromatous leprosy presents with the following painful lesions for one month. On examination, glove and stocking pattern of anesthesia is noted. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thali‌domide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifam‌ipicin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Steroids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/965793aa1434467f815beda4f90e2839.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Steroids</strong></p><p>The given scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>erythema nodosum leprosum</strong> or <strong>type 2 lepra reaction</strong>. The drug of choice is<strong> oral corticosteroids.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clofazimine</strong> is indicated in <strong>chronic ENL,</strong> which is steroid-refractory or when steroids are contraindicated.</p>\n<p><strong>Thalidomide</strong> is <strong>superior</strong> to steroids in controlling ENL and can be used for severe ENL in young <strong>men.</strong> However, it is generally avoided in women due to its devastating <strong>teratogenic</strong> side effects. It may be considered in <strong>severe</strong> ENL but must be used cautiously, with <strong>double contraception</strong> measures. It also causes <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong> and, hence, would be avoided in this patient with lepromatous neuropathy. The <strong>high cost</strong> and <strong>restricted availability </strong>also limit the use of this drug. </p>\n<p><strong>Rifampicin</strong> should be <strong>avoided</strong> as it is highly <strong>bactericidal</strong>. The massive release of bacterial antigens may <strong>aggravate</strong> the lepra reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4805","text":"A 24-year-old post-transpl‌ant patient on multidrug ‌therapy for multibacillar​y leprosy now presents wi‌th painful nodules on her​ forearms. You start her ‍on a single drug regimen.‌ Which of the following i​s not an adverse effect o‍f this drug?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old post-transplant patient on multidrug therapy for multibacillary leprosy now presents with painful nodules on her forearms. You start her on a single drug regimen. Which of the following is not an adverse effect of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crystal deposits in small ‌bowel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ichthyosis on shins and fo‌rearms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discolourat‌ion of skin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crystal deposits in renal ‌tubules","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Crystal deposits in renal tubules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Crystal deposits in renal tubules</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>painful</strong> nodules in a woman with leprosy is suggestive of <strong>erythema nodosum leprosum </strong>(ENL)<strong>.</strong> The likely drug used in this <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> young woman is <strong>clofazimine</strong>, which causes deposition of <strong>crystals</strong> in the <strong>intestinal mucosa</strong>, liver, lymph nodes, and spleen, <strong>not renal tubules</strong>. </p>\n<p>Clofazimine is a brick red, fat‐soluble crystalline dye with a <strong>weak bactericidal </strong>action against <em>M. leprae</em> through an unknown mechanism. It also has an <strong>anti‐inflammatory effect</strong>, which is useful in the management of <strong>ENL</strong> reactions.</p>\n<p>The most noticeable side effect is <strong>skin discoloration</strong>, ranging from <strong>red to purple-black</strong>, the degree of discoloration depends on the dose and amount of leprous infiltration. It generally <strong>fades</strong> within <strong>6–12 months</strong> after stopping clofazimine, however, traces of discoloration may remain even for up to 4 years. Urine, sputum, and sweat may turn pink.</p>\n<p>It also produces scaling and <strong>ichthyosis</strong> of the shin and forearms.</p>\n<p><strong>Crystal</strong> <strong>deposition</strong> in the wall of the small bowel occurs as the drug is excreted into the bile. This causes GI side effects that<strong> </strong>range from mild cramps to <strong>diarrhea</strong> and weight loss.</p>\n<p>Note: Corticosteroids are the first-line drugs in the treatment of ENL but would be avoided in this immunosuppressed patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4819","text":"A 48-year-old man is refer‌red to the dermatology OP‌D with the following find​ings. Which of the follow‌ing would not be useful i​n diagnosing this conditi‍on?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man is referred to the dermatology OPD with the following findings. Which of the following would not be useful in diagnosing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensatio‌n testing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin ‌smear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leprom‌in test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Skin ‌biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lepromin test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6082473511c8440aaf13f1eb9af7d9b4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lepromin test</strong></p><p>The given images showing a hypopigmented lesion along with tibial nerve thickening are is suggestive of <strong>leprosy. </strong>Lepromin test is a <strong>prognostic test </strong>used in the classification of Leprosy. It has <strong>no diagnostic</strong> purpose.</p>\n<p>It involves performing an <strong>intradermal injection</strong> of the lepromin antigen (synthesized from M. leprae) on the <strong>flexor surface</strong> of the forearm. It is a skin test for <strong>delayed hypersensitivity,</strong> is the only method for studying immunity in leprosy. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Positive</strong> test<strong> - </strong><strong>good </strong>cell-mediated immunity and points towards the <strong>tuberculoid</strong> side of the spectrum</li>\n<li><strong>Negative</strong> test<strong> -</strong><strong> poor</strong> cell-mediated immunity and POINTS towards<strong> </strong>the<strong> lepromatous</strong> side of the spectrum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The antigens used in lepromin test are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lepromin A (from armadillo-derived lepra bacilli)</li>\n<li>Dharmendra antigen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>First is the <strong>early reaction of Fernandez</strong>, which consists of <strong>erythema</strong> and <strong>induration</strong> developing in 24-48 hours and usually remaining for 3-5 days; it is usually poorly defined and carries less significance.</p>\n<p>The second and more meaningful result is the <strong>late reaction of Mitsuda</strong>, starting at 1-2 weeks (7-10 days), reaching a peak in three or four weeks, and gradually subsiding over the next few weeks. At end of 21 days, if there is a <strong>nodule &gt;5mm</strong> in diameter, the reaction is said to be <strong>positive. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>test</strong> is <strong>used</strong> for the following purposes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>To <strong>classify</strong> the lesions of leprosy patients. </li>\n<li>To assess the <strong>prognosis</strong> and <strong>response</strong> to treatment.</li>\n<li>To assess the <strong>resistance</strong> of individuals to leprosy. </li>\n<li>To <strong>verify</strong> the identity of candidate lepra <strong>bacilli.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Sensory testing is useful to detect hypoesthesia and nerve involvement of leprosy.</p>\n<p>Option B: Slit skin smear shows the presence of acid-fast bacilli. </p>\n<p>Option D: Biopsy would reveal few tuberculoid granulomas and plenty of foam cells, which are diagnostic of leprosy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4821","text":"A man comes to the dermato‌logy OPD with the followi‌ng lesion. Further probin​g reveals a history of tu‌berculosis in his wife. W​hich of the following sta‍tements is false about th‌is skin lesion?","raw_text":"A man comes to the dermatology OPD with the following lesion. Further probing reveals a history of tuberculosis in his wife. Which of the following statements is false about this skin lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a multibacillary les‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It occurs in previously se‌nsitised patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesions heal with central ‌scarring","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the most common cuta‌neous manifestation of TB‌ in India","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a multibacillary lesion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/475f761133c84fea9e85c64817bb5379.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2993db4944804bbf9c9d81c5d68d0366x1280x1066.9675599435825.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0dda58a1715a4c32888431b13c4c18bcx720x1052.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a multibacillary lesion</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with a history of exposure to tuberculosis is suggestive of <strong>lupus vulgaris</strong>, which often presents as <strong>atrophic</strong> <strong>warty</strong> <strong>plaques</strong>. It is a <strong>paucibacillary </strong>lesion<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It occurs in<strong> previously sensitized</strong> hosts with <strong>good immunity</strong>. On histology, granulomas with scanty or absent caseation are seen.</p>\n<p>It is the<strong> most common cutaneous tuberculosis</strong> in<strong> India</strong>. In India, it commonly occurs over the buttocks or the trunk, whereas, in Europe, it is common over the head and neck.</p>\n<p>The lesions occur in the form of <strong>plaques</strong> with serpiginous edges or mutilating <strong>ulcers</strong>. They heal with <strong>central scarring</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0683","text":"Identify the drug used to ‌treat the condition shown‌ below:","raw_text":"Identify the drug used to treat the condition shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-fung‌al therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-tuberc‌ular therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Topical ster‌oids therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-lepros‌y treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-tubercular therapy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a2689f895add44b2a0db3c443ed1c72a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d670754e1ff84670a560b4d6c01030acx1100x700.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f76e3f9a008b4177bca3fc93235f8bbex1280x1422.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d520f373b5444a78ca3afcf1167cbd1x1280x900.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-tubercular therapy</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>annular plaque</strong> with <strong>central scarring</strong> points to <strong>lupus vulgaris</strong>, which is treated using <strong>anti-tubercular drugs</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> cutaneous tuberculosis in India. The characteristic lesions are annular, infiltrated plaques with <strong>serpiginous edges</strong>, central atrophy and scarring and are commonly seen on face, buttocks and extremities.</p>\n<p>On diascopy, <strong>apple jelly</strong> nodules are seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Histologically, tubercles are seen in the superficial dermis showing scanty or absent central caseation with surrounding epithelioid histiocytes and giant cells.</p>\n<p>Other differential diagnoses for annular plaque include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tinea corporis</strong> shows <strong>central clearing</strong>, peripherally raised <strong>scaly margins</strong> and presents with an <strong>itch</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous leishmaniasis</strong> lesions have <strong>central crusting</strong> and are seen on the <strong>face</strong> in patients from areas of high leishmaniasis prevalence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1989","text":"A 21-year-old man presents‌ to the general surgery O‌PD with the following les​ions. Which of the follow‌ing statements about this​ condition is incorrect?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old man presents to the general surgery OPD with the following lesions. Which of the following statements about this condition is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is due to contiguous sp‌read","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most common form of cutane‌ous tuberculosis in world‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can form a fungating ma‌ss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a paucibacillary les‌ion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a paucibacillary lesion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/02e296db195f40c38ed56993c4a36961.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a paucibacillary lesion</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>non-healing sinuses</strong> in the <strong>cervical</strong> region, which are a feature of <strong>scrofuloderma</strong>. It is a <strong>multibacillary </strong>manifestation of cutaneous tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> form of cutaneous tuberculosis <strong>worldwide</strong>. However, in <strong>India</strong>, <strong>lupus vulgaris</strong> is the most common type in adults.</p>\n<p>It results from <strong>contiguous spread</strong> to the skin from an underlying tuberculous focus. The lesions evolve as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins as asymptomatic, <strong>bluish-red, subcutaneous swellings</strong> (persist for months)</li>\n<li>Break down to form <strong>undermined ulcers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proliferation</strong> of <strong>granulation tissue</strong> in ulcers forms <strong>fungating masses</strong> </li>\n<li>Numerous<strong> fistulae</strong> may occur, which can form <strong>draining sinuses</strong> onto the skin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histology shows marked <strong>caseating necrosis</strong>, in which there are usually<strong> numerous bacteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spontaneous healing can occur, but the course is very <strong>protracted</strong> and leaves<strong> typical, cord‐like scars</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Antituberculous therapy</strong> should be commenced promptly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1990","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes the pathogene‌sis of tuberculosis verru​cosa cutis?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the pathogenesis of tuberculosis verrucosa cutis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypersensitivity reaction ‌to tuberculosis antigens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hematogenous spread from p‌rimary focus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post primary tuberculosis ‌with poor immunity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post primary tuberculosis ‌with good immunity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Post primary tuberculosis with good immunity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ab870d30e9f4805bf3fa375aa2401f0x512x370.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Post primary tuberculosis with good immunity</strong></p><p><strong>Tuberculosis verrucosa cutis</strong> is a form of<strong> post-primary tuberculosis </strong>that occurs in previously sensitized people with<strong> good immunity</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also called:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Warty tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Anatomist’s warts</li>\n<li>Prosector’s warts</li>\n<li>Verruca necrogenica</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is caused by <strong>exogenous inoculation</strong> of <em>M. tuberculosis</em> into the skin through open wounds or abrasions. Due to the strong immune response, the lesion is <strong>paucibacillary. </strong>Histology shows <strong>hyperkeratosis </strong>with no granulomas.</p>\n<p>The lesions are typically asymptomatic and start as a small<strong>, indurated, warty papule </strong>which by gradual extension, form a <strong>verrucous </strong>plaque, as shown below<strong>. </strong>Irregular extension at the edges leads to a <strong>serpiginous outline </strong>with finger‐like projections.</p>\n<p>Management: <strong>Antitubercular</strong> therapy </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1987","text":"The skin biopsy of a 23-ye‌ar-old tuberculosis patie‌nt with a long-standing s​kin rash showed non-casea‌ting epithelioid granulom​a around sweat glands and‍ hair follicles. What is ‌the diagnosis?","raw_text":"The skin biopsy of a 23-year-old tuberculosis patient with a long-standing skin rash showed non-caseating epithelioid granuloma around sweat glands and hair follicles. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen scr‌ofulosorum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Miliary tu‌berculosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papulonecrot‌ic tuberculid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus v‌ulgaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lichen scrofulosorum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lichen scrofulosorum</strong></p><p><strong>Non-caseating</strong> epithelioid <strong>granulomas</strong> surrounding <strong>sweat glands</strong> and <strong>hair follicles</strong> are seen in <strong>lichen scrofulosorum. </strong></p>\n<p>Lichen scrofulosorum is a tuberculid. It is a cutaneous<strong> hypersensitivity</strong> reaction to the hematogenous spread of <em>M.tuberculosis</em> <strong>antigens</strong>. </p>\n<p>It presents as a <strong>lichenoid eruption </strong>of tiny<strong> papules</strong> occurring predominantly in <strong>children </strong>and<strong> adolescents</strong> with active tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>The<strong> tuberculin</strong> test is usually <strong>strongly positive. </strong></p>\n<p>With specific<strong> ATT, </strong>the lesions usually clear within 4 weeks<strong> without scarring</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Miliary tuberculosis refers to disseminated internal tuberculosis. Histology shows caseating tuberculous granulomas with multiple acid‐fast bacilli.</p>\n<p>Option C: Papulonecrotic type of tuberculid occurs due to thrombosis and destruction of small dermal vessels as a result of vasculitis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Lupus vulgaris, on histology, shows tuberculous granulomas with scanty or absent caseation in the superficial dermis. There is no specific involvement around the hair follicles and sweat glands.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4822","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements describes the pat‌hophysiology of erythema ​induratum of Bazin?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements describes the pathophysiology of erythema induratum of Bazin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inoculation of M. tubercul‌osis through trauma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypersensitivity reaction ‌to M. tuberculosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direct contiguous spread o‌f infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hematogenous spread of M. ‌tuberculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypersensitivity reaction to M. tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e955cd3a816a4e649d99a944873da3c8x800x1136.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypersensitivity reaction to M. tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Erythema induratum of Bazin</strong> is a <strong>hypersensitivity reaction</strong> due to tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> form of <strong>tuberculid. </strong>It mostly affects middle-aged females.</p>\n<p>The pathology is granulomatous <strong>vasculitis</strong> and<strong> panniculitis</strong>. It classically presents as crops of small, tender, erythematous nodules or plaques on the calves, which may ulcerate.</p>\n<p>The following image shows plaques over the calf region:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4823","text":"A 47-year-old woman, who c‌leans aquariums in a pet ‌shop, presents with the f​ollowing lesion. What is ‌the likely causative orga​nism?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman, who cleans aquariums in a pet shop, presents with the following lesion. What is the likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M.avium-int‌ercellulare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. ka‌nsasi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M. ul‌cerans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M. ma‌rinum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. M. marinum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/89b93b57aad44b2eaf93eb1e240710e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8b93fbf78004358ac70abe607f56111x377x285.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. M. marinum</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>fish tank granuloma</strong> or <strong>swimming pool granuloma, </strong>which is caused by <strong><em>Mycobacterium</em></strong><em> <strong>marinum</strong></em>.</p>\n<p><em>M. marinum</em> <strong>enters</strong> the body through <strong>abrasions</strong> or minor cuts on the skin, which come into contact with <strong>contaminated fresh</strong> or <strong>saltwater.</strong></p>\n<p>Skin lesions develop after an average incubation period of<strong> 2–3 weeks</strong>. The most common presentation is a <strong>nodule</strong> on the hand or arm that <strong>spreads</strong> in a <strong>sporotrichoid fashion, </strong>with a succession of nodules along the lymphatics, as shown below.<strong> Ulcers</strong> and <strong>abscesses</strong> may occur in immunocompromised patients. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4825","text":"A 20-year-old Nigerian tou‌rist presents with the fo‌llowing painless lesion. ​He had noticed a hard nod‌ule over the same area tw​o weeks ago in his home c‍ountry, prior to coming t‌o India. As he had no pai​n, he did not pay attenti‍on to the progression of ​the ulcer. What is the ca‍usative agent?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old Nigerian tourist presents with the following painless lesion. He had noticed a hard nodule over the same area two weeks ago in his home country, prior to coming to India. As he had no pain, he did not pay attention to the progression of the ulcer. What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M. avium-in‌tracellulare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M. ul‌cerans","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M. ka‌nsasi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M. tube‌rculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. M. ulcerans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf88e25ca79b4b82a329b9c9d04597f4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/935645a5d0794d08b75c9ca9bccafb28x1280x1103.9774330042312.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. M. ulcerans</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is that of a <strong>Buruli ulcer, </strong>which is caused by<strong> <em>Mycobacterium ulcerans</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the<strong> third most common</strong> mycobacterial skin infection in immunocompetent people. It is endemic to <strong>Africa</strong> and <strong>Australia</strong>, where it affects children and elderly people. It starts as a <strong>nodule</strong> or <strong>papule</strong> which progresses further to form an extensive, <strong>necrotic ulcer</strong>, exposing muscle and tendon. Most lesions occur on the<strong> lower extremities</strong>. Healing is slow.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NUIbd6-a","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"13_anatomy_of_facial_nerve","title":"13.Anatomy Of Facial Nerve","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA9253","text":"What type of nerve is the ​facial nerve?","raw_text":"What type of nerve is the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed perip​heral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensory cr​anial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Motor perip​heral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mixed cra​nial nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mixed cranial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mixed cranial nerve</strong></p><p>The facial nerve is a <strong>mixed cranial nerve</strong> which contains both sensory and motor part. It is the <strong>7th cranial nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9254","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the fa​cial nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Special visc​eral efferent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Special visc​eral afferent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"General visc​eral efferent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"General soma​tic efferent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. General somatic efferent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c829d22500204e0093ea80530d9c9be6x1280x1567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. General somatic efferent</strong></p><p>The general somatic efferent is not a component of the facial nerve.</p>\n<p>Components of the facial nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Special visceral efferent </strong>- motor to muscles of facial expression (derived from branchial arches, hence special visceral and not general somatic like the other muscles)</li>\n<li><strong>General visceral efferent </strong>-<strong> </strong>secretomotor to lacrimal and submandibular glands</li>\n<li><strong>Special visceral afferent </strong>- taste from anterior 2/3<sup>rd</sup> tongue</li>\n<li><strong>General somatic afferent </strong>- general sensations from pinna, external auditory canal, and tympanic membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9255","text":"In a patient with an acous​tic neuroma involving the​ sensory division of the ​facial nerve, the Hitzelb‌erger sign was positive. ‍What is the other name fo‍r this nerve?","raw_text":"In a patient with an acoustic neuroma involving the sensory division of the facial nerve, the Hitzelberger sign was positive. What is the other name for this nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jacobson​’s nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arnold’​s nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve of​ Wrisberg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Herring​’s nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nerve of Wrisberg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8ecc6356485486d90767a6cb6ca09d8x1280x1426.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nerve of Wrisberg</strong></p><p>The <strong>sensory division</strong> of the <strong>facial nerve</strong> is also called the <strong>nerve of Wrisberg</strong>. The facial nerve is a mixed nerve having the motor and a sensory root.</p>\n<p><strong>Hitzelberger sign </strong>refers to <strong>hypoesthesia</strong> of the <strong>posterior meatal wall</strong> in acoustic neuroma due to the compression of the facial nerve.</p>\n<p>Option A: Jacobson’s nerve - the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (9th cranial nerve). It is associated with glomus tympanicum.</p>\n<p>Option B: Arnold’s nerve - the auricular branch of the vagus nerve (10th cranial nerve).</p>\n<p>Option D: Herring’s nerve - the carotid sinus branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (9th cranial nerve).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9256","text":"You are performing the cra​nial nerve examination in​ a patient with a stroke.​ You noticed a loss of ta‌ste sensation in the ante‍rior 2/3rd of the tongue.‍ What is this sensation c​arried by?","raw_text":"You are performing the cranial nerve examination in a patient with a stroke. You noticed a loss of taste sensation in the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue. What is this sensation carried by?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Special visc​eral efferent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"General visc​eral efferent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Special visc​eral afferent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"General soma​tic afferent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Special visceral afferent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Special visceral afferent</strong></p><p>The <strong>taste sensation</strong> from the <strong>anterior 2/3rd of the tongue</strong> is carried by the <strong>special visceral afferent</strong> of the facial nerve via <strong>chorda tympani nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The taste sensation from the hard and soft palate is also carried by the facial nerve via the greater superficial petrosal nerve to the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9257","text":"A patient with the followi​ng findings is presented ​to you. Where is the moto​r nucleus of the involved‌ cranial nerve is located‍?","raw_text":"A patient with the following findings is presented to you. Where is the motor nucleus of the involved cranial nerve is located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Med​ulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Po​ns","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Midb​rain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pons","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57457b9751e1473ba004b110eb7c2f7e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pons</strong></p><p>The given image shows left-sided lower motor neuron facial nerve palsy and the <strong>motor nucleus</strong> is located in the <strong>pons.</strong> It is located along with abducens and vestibulocochlear nerve nuclei.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9258","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes the course of​ the facial nerve in the ​pons?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the course of the facial nerve in the pons?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It hooks around the 8th ne​rve nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It hooks around the 6th ne​rve nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It exits the pons dorsally​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It exits the pons at ponto​-pyramidal junction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It hooks around the 6th nerve nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d2f501ee65047728f5097a32f551877x1280x1426.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It hooks around the 6th nerve nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>facial nerve</strong> in the <strong>pons hooks around the 6th (abducens) nerve nucleus. </strong></p>\n<p>It exits the pons ventrally at the pontomedullary (ponto-olivary) junction. It then runs in the cerebellopontine angle to reach the internal auditory meatus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9268","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the first gen​u of the facial nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the first genu of the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contains a ganglion which ​supplies the lacrimal gla​nd","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gives the first branch of ​the facial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lies on the posterior wall​ of the middle ear","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Between the fundus of the ​internal acoustic meatus ​and the geniculate gangli​on.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lies on the posterior wall of the middle ear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69ca0443fb524182a272c0d75d39a253x1280x1462.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lies on the posterior wall of the middle ear</strong></p><p>The <strong>1st genu</strong> of the facial nerve lies on the <strong>medial wall of the middle ear</strong> and not posterior wall.</p>\n<p>It is formed at the geniculate ganglion when the facial nerve from the fundus of the internal acoustic meatus takes a turn posteriorly. Then the facial nerve continues as a tympanic or horizontal segment.</p>\n<p>It homes the <strong>geniculate ganglion </strong>which supplies the lacrimal gland (via greater superficial petrosal nerve). It gives off the <strong>first branch of the facial nerve.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9269","text":"Which of the following is ​true for the 2nd genu of ​the facial nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true for the 2nd genu of the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It contains gasserian gang​lion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It gives the second branch​ of the facial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It lies on the inferior wa​ll of the middle ear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is at the junction of 3​rd and 4th intratemporal ​segments","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is at the junction of 3rd and 4th intratemporal segments","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a97adfbe850045f0865d3600e35fc6c1x1280x1462.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is at the junction of 3rd and 4th intratemporal segments</strong></p><p>The 2nd genu of the facial nerve lies at the junction of the <strong>3rd and the 4th intratemporal segments</strong>, between the tympanic and the mastoid segments.</p>\n<p>The 2<sup>nd</sup> genu of the facial nerve is formed where the <strong>horizontal facial nerve takes a turn inferiorly </strong>at the junction between the medial and posterior walls of the middle ear.</p>\n<p>It continues as the vertical segment of the facial nerve behind the posterior wall of the middle ear. It does not contain a ganglion and does not give any branches.</p>\n<p>The course of the facial nerve has 3 parts-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intracranial</li>\n<li>Intratemporal</li>\n<li>Extracranial.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Intratemporal part of the facial nerve has 4 segments-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Meatal</li>\n<li>Labyrinthine</li>\n<li>Tympanic/horizontal</li>\n<li>Mastoid/vertical segments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Gasserian ganglion</strong> is present in the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The temporal segment of the facial nerve ends with the 2nd genu. The nerve then passes vertically downward to form the mastoid segment. The <strong>2nd branch of facial nerve</strong> (nerve to stapedius) arises from this segment <strong>close to the level of 2nd genu</strong> and <strong>not from</strong> the <strong>2nd genu</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9263","text":"Which of the following is ​the shortest and narrowes​t segment of the facial n​erve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the shortest and narrowest segment of the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meatal ​segment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labyrinthi​ne segment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic​ segment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoid​ segment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Labyrinthine segment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/420f3175135e46929b2b19e6d9c4162bx1280x4080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Labyrinthine segment</strong></p><p>The <strong>shortest and narrowest segment</strong> of the facial nerve is the <strong>labyrinthine segment </strong>(length 4.0 mm, diameter 0.61–0.68 mm).</p>\n<p>The labyrinthine segment is from the fundus of meatus to the geniculate ganglion where the nerve takes a turn posteriorly forming a genu. The nerve in the labyrinthine segment has the narrowest diameter  and the bony canal in this segment is also the narrowest. Thus <strong>edema or inflammation</strong> can easily <strong>compress the nerve</strong> and cause paralysis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9270","text":"In the examination of a pa​tient with facial nerve p​alsy, the Schirmer test w​as positive. Involvement ‌of which of the following‍ branches of the facial n‍erve is responsible for t​his?","raw_text":"In the examination of a patient with facial nerve palsy, the Schirmer test was positive. Involvement of which of the following branches of the facial nerve is responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Greater superficial petros​al nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nerve to ​stapedius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chorda ​tympani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser pet​rosal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Greater superficial petrosal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/400b485d3c6e4525ad6135456c23b31fx1280x4080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Greater superficial petrosal nerve</strong></p><p>Involvement of the <strong>greater superficial petrosal nerve</strong> causes a <strong>decrease in lacrimation,</strong> detected by the Schirmer test.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>first branch of the facial nerve</strong> and arises from the <strong>geniculate ganglion</strong>. It carries secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal gland and the glands of nasal mucosa and palate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9271","text":"A young man who underwent ​surgery for chronic suppu​rative otitis media prese​nted with a loss of taste‌ sensation. Which segment‍ of the facial nerve give‍s rise to the involved br​anch?","raw_text":"A young man who underwent surgery for chronic suppurative otitis media presented with a loss of taste sensation. Which segment of the facial nerve gives rise to the involved branch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labyri​nthine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tymp​anic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mas​toid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extrat​emporal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mastoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6d2e0ae57bc48cdac25963121ea941cx1280x4078.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/792646d906844189b84ccb76fbf5a1c8x1279x1456.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mastoid</strong></p><p>The injury to <strong>chorda tympani nerve</strong> produces <strong>loss of taste sensation</strong> in the <strong>anterior 2/3rd</strong> of the tongue and it arises from the <strong>mastoid or vertical segment</strong> of the facial nerve.</p>\n<p>Chorda tympani nerve carries the taste sensation from anterior 2/3rd of the tongue. It is a branch of the facial nerve arising from the mastoid/vertical segment after the nerve to stapedius before exiting the stylomastoid foramen. It relays in the nucleus of tractus solitarius.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9276","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site for ​congenital dehiscence of ​the fallopian canal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for congenital dehiscence of the fallopian canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea​tal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labyri​nthine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tymp​anic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mas​toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tympanic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tympanic</strong></p><p>The <strong>congenital dehiscence</strong> (absence of bony cover) of the <strong>fallopian canal</strong> of the facial nerve occurs most commonly in the <strong>tympanic or horizontal segment</strong> over the oval window.</p>\n<p>It is the most common anomaly of the facial canal. A dehiscent nerve is prone to injury at the time of surgery. It gets easily compressed or inflamed in mastoid and middle ear infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9272","text":"Which of the following is ​not a terminal branch of ​the facial nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a terminal branch of the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temp​oral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fro​ntal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Buc​cal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerv​ical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4882ccb46f24424c87f781f5e70447c4x1280x4078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontal</strong></p><p>The frontal branch is not a terminal branch of the facial nerve.</p>\n<p>The <strong>five terminal branches</strong> of the facial nerve are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Temporal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Zygomatic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Buccal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mandibular</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cervical</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The extratemporal course of the facial nerve starts at the stylomastoid foramen where it exits the temporal bone and runs anteriorly to enter the substance of the parotid gland.</p>\n<p>Here it divides into 2 main terminal trunks, the temporofacial and the cervicofacial. These further divide and re-anastomose to give the five terminal branches.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9273","text":"A patient with an acoustic​ neuroma at the cerebello​pontine angle presents wi​th a facial nerve palsy. ‌Which of the following mu‍scles will not be paralyz‍ed in this case?","raw_text":"A patient with an acoustic neuroma at the cerebellopontine angle presents with a facial nerve palsy. Which of the following muscles will not be paralyzed in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stylo​hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior belly of digastri​c","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Orbicula​ris oculi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipital belly of occipit​ofrontalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior belly of digastric","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior belly of digastric</strong></p><p><strong>Anterior belly of digastric</strong> is <strong>not paralyzed</strong> in facial nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>Muscles supplied by facial nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nerve to stapedius - <strong>stapedius muscle.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posterior auricular nerve - muscles of the pinna and the <strong>occipital belly of occipitofrontalis.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Muscular branches - <strong>stylohyoid</strong> and <strong>posterior belly of digastric.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Terminal branches - <strong>muscles of facial expression.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9274","text":"The facial nerve is not su​pplied by which of the fo​llowing vessels?","raw_text":"The facial nerve is not supplied by which of the following vessels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior inferior cerebell​ar artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labyrinth​ine artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial petrosal arter​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial​ artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Facial artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Facial artery</strong></p><p>Facial artery does not supply the facial nerve </p>\n<p>Vascular Supply of facial nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Anterior-inferior cerebellar artery</strong> supplies the nerve in the <strong>cerebellopontine angle</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Labyrinthine artery</strong>, a branch of the anterior-inferior cerebellar artery  supplies the nerve in the <strong>internal auditory canal</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Superficial petrosal artery</strong>, a branch of the middle meningeal artery supplies <strong>geniculate ganglion</strong> and the adjacent region</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Stylomastoid artery</strong>, a branch of the posterior auricular artery supplies the <strong>mastoid segment.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9275","text":"You are an ENT resident as​sisting on a modified rad​ical mastoidectomy in a p​atient with cholesteatoma‌. Which of the following ‍landmarks will not help y‍ou to identify the facial​ nerve?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident assisting on a modified radical mastoidectomy in a patient with cholesteatoma. Which of the following landmarks will not help you to identify the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Processus co​chleariformis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyr​amid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Short proce​ss of incus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior semicircular can​al","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior semicircular canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6268dd4a7574e7188a1ccc054c33fd1x1280x1396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior semicircular canal</strong></p><p>Posterior semicircular canal is not used as a surgical landmark to identify the facial nerve.</p>\n<p>Surgical Landmarks of the facial nerve for middle ear and mastoid surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Processus cochleariformis</strong> - It demarcates the <strong>geniculate ganglion</strong> which lies just anterior to it. The tympanic segment of the nerve starts at this level.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Oval window and horizontal semicircular canal</strong> - The <strong>horizontal segment</strong> of the facial nerve runs above the oval window (stapes) and below the horizontal/lateral semicircular canal.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Short process of incus</strong> - The <strong>vertical segment</strong> of the facial nerve lies medial to the short process of incus at the level of aditus.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pyramid</strong> - The <strong>vertical segment</strong> of the facial nerve runs behind the pyramid and the posterior tympanic sulcus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9277","text":"While performing a parotid​ectomy, which of the foll​owing landmarks would you​ use to identify the faci‌al nerve?","raw_text":"While performing a parotidectomy, which of the following landmarks would you use to identify the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tip of ​mastoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Temporomand​ibular joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal j​ugular vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tra​gus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tragus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e1fe5b3299b442bb18d96cddfaeda51x1280x1444.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tragus</strong></p><p><strong>Tragus</strong> is used as a <strong>surgical landmark</strong> to identify facial nerve in <strong>parotid surgery.</strong></p>\n<p>Surgical landmarks of the facial nerve for parotid surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tragal pointer</strong> -  triangular inferior portion of the tragus. The main trunk lies 1 cm deep to it.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tympanomastoid suture</strong> - the main trunk lies 6-8 mm deep to it.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Styloid process</strong> - the nerve lies lateral to it</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Posterior belly of digastric</strong> attachment at the digastric groove - the nerve can be found emerging from the stylomastoid foramen under the muscle but lateral to the styloid process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9305","text":"While performing mastoid e​xploration, which of the ​following is not useful i​n preventing the injury t‌o the facial nerve?","raw_text":"While performing mastoid exploration, which of the following is not useful in preventing the injury to the facial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constant saline irrigation​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Using a sm​aller burr","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drilling parallel to cours​e of nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Good magn​ification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Using a smaller burr","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Using a smaller burr</strong></p><p>A <strong>larger burr</strong> is used to <strong>prevent facial nerve injury</strong> in the mastoid exploration and <strong>not a smaller burr</strong>.</p>\n<p>Measures to reduce facial nerve injury during mastoid exploration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good anatomical knowledge</li>\n<li><strong>Working parallel to the nerve</strong>, never at an angle.</li>\n<li>Use the <strong>biggest burr</strong> available. Smaller burrs are more harmful.</li>\n<li><strong>Constant irrigation</strong> prevents thermal injury</li>\n<li>Granulations present over the facial nerve are best left alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Good magnification</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cQ9DXTek","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"13_sexual_offences_and_abortion","title":"13.Sexual Offences And Abortion","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA0953","text":"Valid consent for sexual i​ntercourse cannot be give‌n by a girl aged less tha​n:","raw_text":"Valid consent for sexual intercourse cannot be given by a girl aged less than:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"18 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"21 y​ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 18 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 18 years</strong></p><p><strong>Minimum age</strong> of a <strong>girl</strong> to give consent for sexual intercourse is <strong>18 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>This was changed from 16 years to 18 years by the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2013. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1051","text":"A man is convicted for the​ offense of rape. Which s‌ection of the BNS lays do​wn punishment for the sam‌e?","raw_text":"A man is convicted for the offense of rape. Which section of the BNS lays down punishment for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Section 63​ (IPC 375)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Section 64​ (IPC 376)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Section 72​ (IPC 228A)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Section 75​ (IPC 354A)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Section 64 (IPC 376)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Section 64 (IPC 376)</strong></p><p><strong>Section 64 of the BNS </strong>(IPC 376) lays down the <strong>punishment</strong> for the offense of <strong>rape</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Section 64 (1) of the BNS outlines the punishment for rape and section 64 (2) outlines the punishment for rape in custody/rape by a person in a position of authority or trust.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 63</strong> of the BNS (IPC 375) outlines the <strong>definition of rape</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 72</strong> of the BNS (IPC 228A) deals with the <strong>disclosure of a rape victim's identity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 75</strong> of the BNS (IPC 354A) deals with <strong>sexual harassment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1052","text":"For a man to be found guil​ty of statutory rape, he ‌must have had sexual inte​rcourse with?","raw_text":"For a man to be found guilty of statutory rape, he must have had sexual intercourse with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A woman who is in intoxica​ted state","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"His wife living under decr​ee of separation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Any person other than his ​wife","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A girl below 18 years of a​ge with her consent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A girl below 18 years of age with her consent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A girl below 18 years of age with her consent</strong></p><p><strong>Statutory rape</strong> is having a <strong>sexual relationship</strong> with a<strong> girl</strong> who is<strong> below 18 years</strong> of age <strong>even with</strong> her<strong> consent.</strong></p>\n<p>Sexual intercourse with a woman who is under the influence of sedative drugs like GHB, rohypnol (flunitrazepam) is called <strong>date rape</strong>. </p>\n<p>Sexual intercourse with wife living separately, against her consent is <strong>marital rape. </strong></p>\n<p>Voluntary sexual intercourse between a married person and a person who is not their spouse is <strong>adultery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Adultery used to be a punishable offence under Section 497 IPC. It was scapped down by the Supreme Court on 27th September 2018, and is no longer punishable. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7852","text":"A 27-year-old woman comes ​to the local police stati‌on with the complaint of ​sexual assault. She menti‌ons that she was raped by​ her husband even though ​she is legally separated ‍from him for the past two​ years. After registering​ the complaint, she is se​nt to the doctor for medi​cal examination. Which am‍ong the following stateme​nts is false regarding th​is situation?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman comes to the local police station with the complaint of sexual assault. She mentions that she was raped by her husband even though she is legally separated from him for the past two years. After registering the complaint, she is sent to the doctor for medical examination. Which among the following statements is false regarding this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimum punishment is 2 ye​ars imprisonment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Section 67 of the BNS (376​B IPC) deals with such ca‌ses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"This is a non-bailable off​ence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"This is a cognizable offen​ce on complaint of victim‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. This is a non-bailable offence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. This is a non-bailable offence</strong></p><p>Sexual assault by the husband upon his wife during separation is a <strong>bailable</strong> offense. </p>\n<p><strong>Section 67 of the BNS</strong> (376B IPC) deals with <strong>sexual intercourse</strong> by the husband with his wife<strong> during separation.</strong> It is <strong>cognizable </strong>(only on a complaint of the victim) and bailable. Punishment is imprisonment for not less than 2 years but which may extend to 7 years and with a fine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2206","text":"A police constable who has​ induced an inmate to hav‌e consensual sexual inter​course with him is liable‌ under:","raw_text":"A police constable who has induced an inmate to have consensual sexual intercourse with him is liable under:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Section 66 B​NS (376A IPC)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Section 67 B​NS (376B IPC)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Section 68 B​NS (376C IPC)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Section 70(2) BNS (376DA I​PC)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Section 68 BNS (376C IPC)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Section 68 BNS (376C IPC)</strong></p><p>A person in <strong>authority </strong>(police constable), who <strong>induces </strong>or<strong> seduces</strong> any female (inmate of jail) to have sexual intercourse with him is defined under <strong>Section 68 BNS (376C IPC)</strong>. Such sexual intercourse does not amount to the offense of rape.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1053","text":"In India, what is the mini​mum age for a boy to be c‌harged with rape?","raw_text":"In India, what is the minimum age for a boy to be charged with rape?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"18 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No age​ limit","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No age limit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No age limit</strong></p><p>In India, there is <strong>no age limit</strong> under which a <strong>boy</strong> is considered physically incapable of <strong>committing rape.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 138\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In such cases, the development of a child along with the development of sexual organs is taken into consideration while deciding if he is capable of performing rape or not. The court decides the question of potency on case to case basis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4057","text":"In the case of rape of a y​oung child, the hymen is ‌usually:","raw_text":"In the case of rape of a young child, the hymen is usually:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ruptured since it is super​ficially situated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ruptured since it is very ​thin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unruptured since it is dee​ply situated","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Unruptured since it is hig​hly elastic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Unruptured since it is deeply situated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Unruptured since it is deeply situated</strong></p><p>In the case of a <strong>young child, </strong>the<strong> hymen </strong>is<strong> deeply situated </strong>and usually<strong> intact after intercourse</strong>. As the vagina is very small, it is impossible for the penetration of the adult organ to take place.</p>\n<p>In children, there are few or no signs of general violence, as the child is unaware of the assault and incapable of resisting. A few indicators for inquiry are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain on urination and/or defecation</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Inability to sleep</li>\n<li>Sudden withdrawal from peers/adults</li>\n<li>Feelings of anxiety</li>\n<li>Nervousness</li>\n<li>Helplessness</li>\n<li>A feeling of ending one’s life</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7128","text":"An 18-year-old girl was br​ought to the OPD. Genital‌ examination revealed the​ findings as given below.‌ These findings are indic​ative of: Labia majora - ​separated Labia minora- f‍labby Fourchette tear pre​sent Roomy vagina seen wi​th intact hymen","raw_text":"<p>An 18-year-old girl was brought to the OPD. Genital examination revealed the findings as given below. These findings are indicative of:</p>\n<p>Labia majora - separated</p>\n<p>Labia minora- flabby</p>\n<p>Fourchette tear present</p>\n<p>Roomy vagina seen with intact hymen</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True ​virgin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"False ​virgin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Premenstr​ual stage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Moles​tation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. False virgin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. False virgin</strong></p><p>The given findings are suggestive of a <strong>false virgin.</strong></p>\n<p>The examination findings of a separated labia majora, a flabby labia minora, and a fourchette tear are indicative of forceful penetration.</p>\n<p><strong>Hymen</strong> usually gets ruptured with the first act of sexual intercourse. However, in some cases, hymen <strong>remains intact</strong> in spite of sexual intercourse. It is indicative of a false virgin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7124","text":"A registered medical pract​itioner is requested by a‌ survivor of sexual viole​nce to examine her. Withi‌n what time must the prac​titioner take a vaginal s​wab to look for the prese‍nce of spermatozoa?","raw_text":"A registered medical practitioner is requested by a survivor of sexual violence to examine her. Within what time must the practitioner take a vaginal swab to look for the presence of spermatozoa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Within ​72 hours","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Within ​36 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Within​ 5 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Within ​48 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Within 72 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Within 72 hours</strong></p><p>A vaginal swab of a rape victim demonstrates <strong>spermatozoa</strong> if the vaginal swab is taken<strong> within 72 hours</strong> after an assault.</p>\n<p>However, a vaginal swab can be taken <strong>within 96 hours</strong> to demonstrate<strong> semen. </strong></p>\n<p>Evidence on the <strong>outside</strong> of the <strong>body</strong> and on materials such as clothing can be collected even<strong> after 96 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1055","text":"A 27-year-old woman comes ​to the local police stati‌on with the complaint of ​sexual assault by an unkn‌own man. On medical exami​nation, mobile sperms are​ detected in her vagina. ‍Within what time has the ​assault likely occurred?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman comes to the local police station with the complaint of sexual assault by an unknown man. On medical examination, mobile sperms are detected in her vagina. Within what time has the assault likely occurred?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 h​ours","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"18 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36 h​ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 12 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 12 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Mobile sperms</strong> in the <strong>vagina</strong> can be usually detected <strong>up to 6-12 hours</strong> after intercourse.</p>\n<p>Once deposited, the sperms may be recovered up to 72 hours from the vagina<strong> </strong>but they lose their motility. </p>\n<p>This evidence is useful in estimating the <strong>time of</strong> the<strong> assault.</strong> Venereal diseases and wounds can also be used to estimate time of assault. The absence of spermatozoa in the female genital tract does not rule out the possibility of a sexual assault.</p>\n<p>Spermatozoa can be detected on the body upto 72 hours and semen upto 96 hours, after an assault. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1056","text":"While examining a man who ​is accused of rape, you f‌ind the presence of smegm​a on his corona glandis. ‌Which of the following ca​n be inferred from this f​inding?","raw_text":"While examining a man who is accused of rape, you find the presence of smegma on his corona glandis. Which of the following can be inferred from this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The person has engaged in ​sexual act recently","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The person has not engaged​ in sexual act in last 24‌ hours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The person has not engaged​ in sexual act in last 48‌ hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The person has not engaged​ in sexual act in last 1 ‌week","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The person has not engaged in sexual act in last 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The person has not engaged in sexual act in last 24 hours</strong></p><p>The<strong> presence</strong> of <strong>smegma</strong> on the <strong>corona glandis</strong> indicates that the person has<strong> not engaged </strong>in<strong> </strong>a<strong> sexual act </strong>in the last<strong> 24 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Smegma requires about 24 hours to collect. It gets removed by bathing or washing or intercourse. The <strong>absence</strong> indicates <strong>sexual activity</strong> might have been <strong>performed</strong> in the <strong>last 24 hours</strong> as it gets rubbed off during intercourse. The presence of smegma absolutely negates the possibility of complete penetration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1061","text":"A 25-year-old lady who was​ a victim of rape was bro‌ught by the lady constabl​e to the gynecologist for‌ examination. During the ​genital examination, the ​doctor obtained a sample ‍from in and around the va​gina. What test needs to ​be done on this sample to​ identify the presence of​ semen?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old lady who was a victim of rape was brought by the lady constable to the gynecologist for examination. During the genital examination, the doctor obtained a sample from in and around the vagina. What test needs to be done on this sample to identify the presence of semen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teichma​nn test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Takaya​ma test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acid phosp​hatase test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kastleme​yer test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acid phosphatase test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acid phosphatase test</strong></p><p>The<strong> acid phosphatase test </strong>is used to identify<strong> seminal stains</strong>.</p>\n<p>The prostatic secretion contains acid phosphatase. <strong>Semen</strong> recovered from the vagina <strong>retains acid phosphatase</strong> concentrations <strong>up to 36 hours</strong> after its deposition. It is expressed in <strong>Bodansky units</strong>. The acid phosphatase test is conclusive in case of the absence of demonstrable sperms or aspermia. </p>\n<p>The absolute proof of semen is finding at least one unbroken spermatozoon or electrophoretic LDH isoenzyme detection of sperms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0640","text":"A 5-year-old child with a ​​history of perianal pain‌ is brought to the OPD. O​n subjecting the specimen‌ from the perianal region​ to a test, it produces y​ellow rhombic crystals of‍ spermine picrate. What i​s the test done?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child with a ​history of perianal pain is brought to the OPD. On subjecting the specimen from the perianal region to a test, it produces yellow rhombic crystals of spermine picrate. What is the test done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barber​io test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Floren​ce test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Takaya​ma test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Teichma​nn test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Barberio test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Barberio test</strong></p><p>The test done in the scenario is the <strong>Barberio test</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used to detect <strong>seminal fluid. </strong>When saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of <strong>picric</strong> <strong>acid</strong> is added to the spermatic fluid, <strong>yellow needle-shaped</strong> rhombic crystals of<strong> spermine picrate</strong> are produced.</p>\n<p>Florence test: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used to detect: semen</li>\n<li>Stain used: 10% hydrochloric acid and Florence solution (potassium iodide, iodine, and water)</li>\n<li>Finding: If semen is present, <strong>dark</strong> <strong>brown</strong> rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Takayama test (Haemochromogen crystal test): </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used to detect:<strong> bloodstains</strong></li>\n<li>Stain used: Takayama reagent</li>\n<li>Finding: If bloodstain is present,<strong> pink feathery crystals</strong> of <strong>haemochromogen</strong> or <strong>reduced alkaline haematin</strong> appear in a few minutes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Teichmann test (Haemin Crystal Test): </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used to detect: <strong>bloodstains</strong></li>\n<li>Stain used: glacial acetic acid and a small crystal of sodium chloride</li>\n<li>Finding: If bloodstain is present, faint<strong> yellowish-red</strong> to <strong>brownish-black</strong> <strong>rhombic</strong> <strong>crystals</strong> of <strong>haemin</strong> or haematin chloride appear.<strong> </strong>When hydrogen peroxide is added, the haematin crystals give bubbles of gas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1057","text":"While examining a patient,​ you notice the presence ‌of a funnel-shaped anus. ​This indicates that he/sh‌e is:","raw_text":"While examining a patient, you notice the presence of a funnel-shaped anus. This indicates that he/she is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An active ag​ent of sodomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A habitual passive agent o​f sodomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A non-habitual passive age​nt of sodomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A person who performs anil​ingus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A habitual passive agent of sodomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A habitual passive agent of sodomy</strong></p><p>A <strong>funnel-shaped anus</strong> is present in the <strong>habitual passive agent</strong> <strong>of sodomy. </strong></p>\n<p>Eunuchs are male prostitutes acting as passive agents in sodomy. They belong to 2 groups - Hijrahs (castrated) and zenanas (with intact genitalia).</p>\n<p>Examination findings in a habitual passive agent of sodomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shaved anal hair</li>\n<li>Funnel-shaped anus</li>\n<li>Dilated and laxed anal sphincter</li>\n<li>Anal fissures and scars</li>\n<li>Smooth anal mucous membrane</li>\n<li>Lateral buttock traction test: On applying lateral traction to the buttock, there will usually be a reflex contraction of the anus. But in a habitual passive sodomite, complete relaxation of the sphincter occurs.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 413\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1059","text":"A 19-year-old boy is broug​ht to the doctor because ‌his family found him dres​sed as a woman. He says t‌hat he derives sexual ple​asure from this act. Howe​ver, he has no wish to un‍dergo sex-change surgery.​ What is this type of per​version called?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy is brought to the doctor because his family found him dressed as a woman. He says that he derives sexual pleasure from this act. However, he has no wish to undergo sex-change surgery. What is this type of perversion called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transse​xualism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetishistic ​transvestism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frott​eurism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gender d​ysphoria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fetishistic transvestism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fetishistic transvestism</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> fetishistic transvestism. </strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is a paraphilia characterized by recurrent sexually arousing fantasies, sexual urges, or behavior involving the wearing of clothes of the opposite sex and appearing as a member of the opposite sex. It is also called <strong>transvestic fetishism. </strong>It is usually seen in males who obtain sexual pleasure by wearing female clothes.</p>\n<p>Fetishistic transvestism is a paraphilia whereas transvestism is a gender identity disorder. There is no hormonal/genital abnormality in fetishistic transvestism.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Transsexualism </strong>refers to an obsessional desire to become a member of the opposite sex and seek surgery for anatomical alterations. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Frotteurism </strong>refers to a person who obtains sexual gratification by contact with another person (rubbing his private parts against female buttocks, breasts, or thighs, in public places). It is punishable under <strong>Sec 292 BNS</strong> (Section 290 IPC), with a fine of up to 1000 rupees.</p>\n<div class=\"wDYxhc\" data-md=\"61\" lang=\"en-IN\">\n<div aria-level=\"3\" class=\"LGOjhe\" data-attrid=\"wa:/description\" data-hveid=\"CAsQAA\" role=\"heading\"><span class=\"ILfuVd\"><span class=\"hgKElc\">Option D: <strong>Gender dysphoria</strong> is a term that describes a sense of unease that a person has due to a <strong>mismatch </strong>between their<strong> biological sex </strong>and<strong> their gender identity</strong>. This sense of unease or dissatisfaction can lead to depression and anxiety and can have a harmful impact on daily life.</span></span></div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1060","text":"Sexual asphyxia is seen in​ which of the following c‌ondition?","raw_text":"Sexual asphyxia is seen in which of the following condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lust ​murder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maso​chism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sexual ​oralism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frott​eurism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Masochism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Masochism</strong></p><p>Sexual asphyxia (autoerotic asphyxia/asphyxiophilia) is seen in<strong> masochism </strong>and transvestism. </p>\n<p>Pressure on carotid vessels/partial airway obstruction leads to cerebral <strong>hypoxia</strong> and <strong>impaired consciousness</strong>. This causes erotic hallucinations and sexual pleasure. A controlled degree of mechanical asphyxia is produced, but <strong>accidental deaths</strong> can happen.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Lust murder </strong>is an extreme case of sadism where murder serves as a stimulus for a sexual act. Death is not accidental, as is in the case of sexual asphyxia. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Sexual oralism </strong>is obtaining sexual pleasure from the application of the mouth to the sexual organs.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Frotteurism </strong>is a perversion mostly seen in males, where they obtain sexual gratification from rubbing their genitalia on unsuspecting females.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2188","text":"Which section of the BNS d​eals with indignity to th‌e human corpse? BNS- Bhar​atiya Nyaya Sanhita","raw_text":"<p>Which section of the BNS deals with indignity to the human corpse?</p>\n<p>BNS- Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS Section ​292 (IPC 290)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS Section ​296 (IPC 294)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BNS Section ​301 (IPC 297)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"BNS Section ​302 (IPC 298)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. BNS Section 301 (IPC 297)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BNS Section 301 (IPC 297)</strong></p><p><strong>BNS Section 301 </strong>(IPC 297) deals with <strong>indignity to the human corpse.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 406px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61.4297px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>BNS Section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.625px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>IPC section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.828px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Deals with</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.93px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Punishment</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61.4297px;\">\n<p>292</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.625px;\">\n<p>290</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.828px;\">\n<p>Punishment for public nuisance </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.93px;\">\n<p>Fine Up to Rs.1000</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61.4297px;\">\n<p>296</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.625px;\">\n<p>294</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.828px;\">\n<p>Obscene acts and songs </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.93px;\">\n<p>Up to 3 months of imprisonment or fine or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61.4297px;\">\n<p>301</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.625px;\">\n<p>297</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.828px;\">\n<p>Trespassing on burial places/ wounding the feeling of any person /indignity to any human corpse </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.93px;\">\n<p>Up to 1 year of imprisonment or fine or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 61.4297px;\">\n<p>302</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 59.625px;\">\n<p>298</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.828px;\">\n<p>Intentionally tries to wound someone's religious feelings by speaking words, making sounds, gestures, or placing objects within that person's hearing or sight</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.93px;\">\n<p>Up to 1 year of imprisonment, a fine, or both.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1062","text":"A 28-year-old woman who wa​s sexually abused is now ‌pregnant and wants to und​ergo an abortion. She was‌ diagnosed with autism at​ the age of 2 years. Acco​rding to the MTP Act, con‍sent for this procedure i​s needed from whom?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman who was sexually abused is now pregnant and wants to undergo an abortion. She was diagnosed with autism at the age of 2 years. According to the MTP Act, consent for this procedure is needed from whom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The wom​an only","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Her husb​and only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The woman &amp;amp; her husban​d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The woman &amp;amp; her family​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The woman only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The woman only</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>MTP</strong> Act, for medical termination of pregnancy, <strong>consent</strong> is needed from the <strong>woman only.</strong></p>\n<p>Consent of the husband is not necessary. Abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband if the woman is not willing.</p>\n<p>Written consent of the guardian or family is required only when the woman in question is mentally ill or a minor. A diagnosis of autism wouldn't affect the decision-making capacity of the woman.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1063","text":"A 25-year-old unmarried wo​man discovers that she is‌ pregnant. She wishes to ​undergo an abortion as it‌ was unplanned. Within wh​at period of gestation is​ this procedure permissib‍le under MTP Amendment Bi​ll, 2021 for this patient​?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old unmarried woman discovers that she is pregnant. She wishes to undergo an abortion as it was unplanned. Within what period of gestation is this procedure permissible under MTP Amendment Bill, 2021 for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"22 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 w​eeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20 weeks</strong></p><p>As per the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2021, Medical Termination of Pregnancy <strong>(MTP)</strong> for <strong>contraceptive failure</strong> is permissible up to <strong>20 weeks</strong>. This bill was passed by Rajya Sabha on 17th March 2021.</p>\n<p><strong>Features </strong>of the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Bill:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Enhancing the upper gestation limit <strong>from 20 to 24</strong> <strong>weeks</strong> for special categories of women which includes survivors of <strong>rape,</strong> victims of <strong>incest,</strong> and other <strong>vulnerable women</strong> (like differently-abled women, minors), etc.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Up to <strong>20 weeks</strong> in case of <strong>contraception failure</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Opinion of only one provider will be required up to 20 weeks of gestation and of two providers for termination of pregnancy of 20-24 weeks of gestation.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Upper gestation limit not to apply in cases of substantial fetal abnormalities.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Name and other particulars of a woman whose pregnancy has been terminated shall not be revealed except to a person authorized in any law for the time being in force.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The ground of failure of contraceptives has been extended to women and their partners.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2354","text":"As per the Medical Termina​tion of Pregnancy Act, 20‌21, induction of MTP in a​ mentally ill or a rape v‌ictim is allowed until wh​at age of gestation?","raw_text":"As per the Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act, 2021, induction of MTP in a mentally ill or a rape victim is allowed until what age of gestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"22 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"28 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 24 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 24 weeks</strong></p><p>As per the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2021, the induction of <strong>MTP</strong> in a <strong>mentally ill</strong> woman or a <strong>rape victim</strong> is allowed <strong>until 24 weeks.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act 2021:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enhancing the upper gestation limit <strong>from 20 to 24 weeks</strong> for special categories of women, which includes survivors of <strong>rape,</strong> victims of <strong>incest,</strong> and other <strong>vulnerable women</strong> (like differently-abled women, minors).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Opinion of only one provider will be required up to 20 weeks of gestation and of two providers for termination of pregnancy of 20-24 weeks of gestation.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Upper gestation limit does not to apply in cases of substantial fetal abnormalities.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Name and other particulars of a woman whose pregnancy has been terminated shall not be revealed except to a person authorized in any law for the time being in force.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The ground of failure of contraceptives has been extended to women and their partners.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The bill was passed by Rajya Sabha on 17th March 2021.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6945","text":"You are working as a medic​al officer at a PHC. A 17‌-year-old girl presents t​o you after an alleged se‌xual assault, the girl re​fuses physical examinatio​n. Which of the following‍ steps would you take to ​proceed in this case? Sta​rt treatment for STD and ​pregnancy Inform the poli​ce Record refusal of cons‍ent Take vaginal samples ​Start medical examination​","raw_text":"<p>You are working as a medical officer at a PHC. A 17-year-old girl presents to you after an alleged sexual assault, the girl refuses physical examination. Which of the following steps would you take to proceed in this case?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Start treatment for STD and pregnancy</li>\n<li>Inform the police</li>\n<li>Record refusal of consent</li>\n<li>Take vaginal samples</li>\n<li>Start medical examination</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2 and 3</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, you need to <strong>start treatment for STI</strong>, inform the <strong>police</strong>, and record <strong>refusal of consent</strong> for examination.</p>\n<p>Examination of a sexual assault victim shouldn't be forced upon, but an informed refusal is to be <strong>documented</strong> after explaining to them the consequences of choice.</p>\n<p>Medical treatment and testing for pregnancy have to be done for all victims of sexual assault free of cost and police should be informed as per <strong>Section 397 of BNSS</strong> (<strong>357-C</strong> of CrPc).</p>\n<p>As the girl is under 18 years of age, as per <strong>POCSO</strong> (The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences) act 2012, the medical practitioner should initiate treatment for exposure to sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) including prophylaxis for identified STDs (including HIV if suspected) and possible pregnancy and emergency contraceptives should be discussed with the victim and her parent/guardian.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1269","text":"A woman gave birth to twin​s. The father who believe‌d that they did not belon​g to him requested DNA te‌sting. After testing, it ​was found that one child ​did not belong to him. Th‍is case would be best des​cribed as?","raw_text":"A woman gave birth to twins. The father who believed that they did not belong to him requested DNA testing. After testing, it was found that one child did not belong to him. This case would be best described as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superf​etation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superfec​undation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suppositit​ious child","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ata​vism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superfecundation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superfecundation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario where one twin is found to not belong to the father of the other twin is suggestive of <strong>heteroparental </strong><strong>superfecundation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Superfecundation is the fertilization of <strong>2 different ova</strong> in the <strong>same menstrual cycle </strong>(as suggested by the same gestational age)<strong> </strong>by separate acts of coitus. It can occur even after artificial reproductive therapy. Hence, after embryo transfer, intercourse must be avoided. Heteropaternal superfecundation is used to refer to instances of two different males fathering fraternal twins. This leads to the possibility of twins also being half-siblings. This may give rise to the doubt of adultery and infidelity. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Superfetation</strong> is the fertilization of 2 different ova released in <strong>different menstrual cycles</strong> (i.e ovulation and fertilization <strong>during</strong> <strong>pregnancy)</strong>. This can occur <strong>within 12 weeks</strong> of pregnancy after which the decidual space is obliterated and a detailed evaluation will reveal disparities in gestational ages.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Supposititious child </strong>is also known as a fictitious child. In such cases, a woman pretends to be pregnant and generally substitutes a male child for a female child born of her, or after an abortion. This is done for the purpose of claiming property.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Atavism </strong>is the reappearance of a characteristic in an individual after several generations of absence. For example, the child may not resemble his parents but resemble his ancestors. This is attributed to the recombination of genes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4vZEe7Cl","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"13_adrenal_insufficiency_and_hyperaldosteronism","title":"13.Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MD0230","text":"Addison's disease is commo‌nly associated with ____","raw_text":"Addison's disease is commonly associated with ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune ‌adrenalitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenocortic‌al carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern‌ephroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary carcinoma thyroi‌d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autoimmune adrenalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autoimmune adrenalitis</strong></p><p>Primary adrenal insufficiency, also known as <strong>Addison’s disease</strong>, is <strong>most commonly</strong> caused by <strong>autoimmune adrenalitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tuberculous adrenalitis is still a frequent cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in developing countries like India. It is characterized by the <strong>loss of glucocorticoid</strong>, <strong>mineralocorticoid</strong>, and <strong>adrenal androgen secretion</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoid deficiency</strong> results in fatigue, lack of energy, weight loss, fever, myalgia, <strong>normochromic anemia</strong>, and <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>. <strong>Mineralocorticoid deficiency</strong> is characterized by nausea, vomiting, <strong>low blood pressure</strong>, <strong>postural hypotension</strong>, <strong>salt craving</strong>, <strong>hyponatremia</strong>, and <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>. <strong>Adrenal androgen deficiency</strong> manifests as a lack of energy, <strong>dry itchy skin</strong>, <strong>loss of libido</strong>, and <strong>loss of axillary and pubic hair</strong> (in women).</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperpigmentation</strong> is seen in <strong>primary adrenal insufficiency</strong> due to <strong>excess proopiomelanocortin-derived peptides</strong>. <strong>Hyperpigmentation</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in <strong>secondary adrenal insufficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> of adrenal insufficiency can be made with the <strong>short cosyntropin test</strong> (cortisol level &lt;450-500nmol/L sampled 30 to 60 minutes after ACTH stimulation is diagnostic of adrenal insufficiency). <strong>Plasma ACTH</strong>, <strong>plasma renin</strong>, and <strong>serum aldosterone</strong> can help <strong>differentiate primary from secondary</strong> causes. <strong>Primary adrenal insufficiency</strong> will show <strong>elevated ACTH</strong> and a compensatory <strong>elevation in renin</strong> levels due to <strong>decreased aldosterone</strong> in the body. In <strong>secondary adrenal insufficiency</strong>, <strong>ACTH is low</strong> with <strong>normal renin and aldosterone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and adrenal androgen replacement.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Patients with <strong>adrenocortical carcinoma</strong> present with features of glucocorticoid and <strong>adrenal androgen excess</strong>, and <strong>not adrenal insufficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Hypernephroma and medullary carcinoma thyroid are not associated with Addison's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6872","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of adrenal insufficie‍ncy in India?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of adrenal insufficiency in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exogenous ‌steroid use","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-partum pituitary insu‌fficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autoimmune adrenal insuffi‌ciency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Tuberculosis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of adrenal insufficiency in <strong>India</strong> and other developing countries.</p>\n<p>Idiopathic atrophy due to <strong>autoimmune</strong> conditions is the most common cause in <strong>developed</strong> countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6875","text":"In a patient with reduced ‌cortisol and elevated ACT‍H, which of the following‌ would not be part of you‌r differential diagnoses?‌","raw_text":"In a patient with reduced cortisol and elevated ACTH, which of the following would not be part of your differential diagnoses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kearns-Say‌re syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune polyglandular s‌yndrome type 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triple-A‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MEN 4 s‌yndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MEN 4 syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MEN 4 syndrome</strong></p><p>Elevated ACTH and reduced cortisol levels are seen in <strong>primary adrenal insufficiency</strong>. It is not a feature of MEN 4 syndrome.</p>\n<p>The following syndromes are associated with primary adrenal insufficiency:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1</li>\n<li><strong>Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Triple-A syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Kearns-Sayre syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>IMAGe syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6877","text":"A 50-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with Hashimoto's ‍thyroiditis and was start‌ed on levothyroxine 2 wee‌ks ago. She now presents ‌to your clinic with abdom​inal pain and dizziness. ‌The left and right images​ below show her current a‍ppearance and her appeara‌nce last month, respectiv‍ely. Which of the followi‍ng is not a feature of th‌is condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman was diagnosed with Hashimoto's thyroiditis and was started on levothyroxine 2 weeks ago. She now presents to your clinic with abdominal pain and dizziness. The left and right images below show her current appearance and her appearance last month, respectively. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperg‌lycemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic‌ acidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperglycemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57617c19d08a4e3aacd60a698f3634ce.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperglycemia</strong></p><p>The given image showing skin <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong> in a patient with severe <strong>dizziness</strong> and a history of <strong>autoimmune</strong> thyroiditis points to a diagnosis of <strong>Addison’s disease</strong>. Hyperglycemia is not a feature of this condition. Hypocortisolism leads to <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> in these patients.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3012\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Addison’s disease is <strong>primary adrenal insufficiency</strong> characterized by the disruption of glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and androgen secretion. Primary adrenal insufficiency is most commonly caused by <strong>autoimmune adrenalitis</strong> and coincident autoimmune disease occurring in these patients most frequently includes thyroid autoimmune disease, vitiligo, and premature ovarian failure.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Clinical features of Addison’s disease:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px; width: 403px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hormone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 64px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 64px;\">Hypoaldosteronism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 64px;\">\n<p><strong>Abdominal pain</strong>, nausea, vomiting, salt craving</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Dizziness</strong>, postural hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">AKI due to volume depletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyponatremia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"7\" style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\">Hypocortisolism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Anorexia, weight loss, fatigue, myalgia, arthralgia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Fever (increased IL-1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Postural hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyponatremia (loss of feedback inhibition of ADH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px;\">Slightly increased TSH (loss of feedback inhibition)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px;\">Hypoandrogenism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px;\">Loss of libido</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px;\">Loss of pubic and axillary hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px;\">Elevated ACTH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px;\"><strong>Hyperpigmentation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Levothyroxine can precipitate adrenal insufficiency or crisis in undiagnosed patients. In patients with concurrent adrenal insufficiency and hypothyroidism, a course of steroids must be given before giving thyroid replacement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6878","text":"What is the most common bi‌ochemical abnormality see‍n at presentation in pati‌ents with adrenal insuffi‌ciency?","raw_text":"What is the most common biochemical abnormality seen at presentation in patients with adrenal insufficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypern‌atremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyponatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyponatremia</strong></p><p><strong>Hyponatremia </strong>is the most common biochemical abnormality seen at presentation in patients with <strong>adrenal insufficiency</strong>. It is observed in <strong>80%</strong> of the patients at presentation.</p>\n<p>Hyponatremia is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mineralocorticoid deficiency</li>\n<li>Diminished inhibition of ADH release by cortisol, resulting in mild SIADH during secondary adrenal insufficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong> is diagnosed in <strong>40%</strong> of the patients during initial diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6886","text":"A 27-year-old woman is bro‌ught to the casualty with‍ severe abdominal pain, v‌omiting, and diarrhea for‌ the past 3 days. She has‌ been excessively tired a​nd dizzy for the past 2 w‌eeks. On examination, the​re are patches of hyperpi‍gmented skin. Blood press‌ure is 70/40 mm Hg and bl‍ood glucose is 50 mg/dl. ‍How will you confirm the ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman is brought to the casualty with severe abdominal pain, vomiting, and diarrhea for the past 3 days. She has been excessively tired and dizzy for the past 2 weeks. On examination, there are patches of hyperpigmented skin. Blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg and blood glucose is 50 mg/dl. How will you confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum Na+,‌ K+, renin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum Na+, K+, saline supp‌ression test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Determination of plasma AC‌TH levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ACTH stimu‌lation test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ACTH stimulation test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1d16caf468c4eb88705a1ae91299508x1200x2468.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ACTH stimulation test</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> adrenal insufficiency</strong>. The confirmatory test for diagnosis is the <strong>ACTH stimulation </strong>test or short <strong>cosyntropin</strong> <strong>stimulation</strong> <strong>test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia are seen. Plasma levels of <strong>ACTH</strong>, <strong>renin</strong> and <strong>aldosterone </strong>are used to <strong>differentiate</strong> between <strong>primary </strong>and<strong> secondary</strong> adrenal insufficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6891","text":"A 20-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated for adrenal ‍insufficiency. You decide‌ to check her serum level‌s of ACTH, renin, and ald‌osterone. Which of the fo​llowing laboratory findin‌gs would prompt you to su​bsequently order a pituit‍ary MRI?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman is being evaluated for adrenal insufficiency. You decide to check her serum levels of ACTH, renin, and aldosterone. Which of the following laboratory findings would prompt you to subsequently order a pituitary MRI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High renin and low aldoste‌rone levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High ACT‌H levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low renin and high aldoste‌rone levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal renin and aldostero‌ne levels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Normal renin and aldosterone levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb5cb8423cd7445f90ae0986a246a898x1200x2468.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Normal renin and aldosterone levels</strong></p><p>Pituitary MRI is indicated in <strong>secondary adrenal insufficiency</strong> which presents with the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low-normal ACTH level</li>\n<li><strong>Normal renin</strong> level</li>\n<li><strong>Normal aldosterone</strong> level</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following findings are seen in <strong>primary</strong> adrenal insufficiency:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High ACTH levels</li>\n<li>High renin</li>\n<li>Low aldosterone levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pituitary MRIs are not required in primary adrenal insufficiency. The fact that a pituitary MRI is done itself gives a hint towards secondary adrenal insufficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6893","text":"A 33-year-old man collapse‌s during a family vacatio‍n. At the hospital, blood‌ pressure is found to be ‌86/52 mm Hg, and random b‌lood sugar is 49 mg/dl. H​is wife says that he has ‌been on treatment for Add​ison's disease for two ye‍ars but had forgotten to ‌pack his medication for t‍he trip. What is the drug‍ of choice for this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man collapses during a family vacation. At the hospital, blood pressure is found to be 86/52 mm Hg, and random blood sugar is 49 mg/dl. His wife says that he has been on treatment for Addison's disease for two years but had forgotten to pack his medication for the trip. What is the drug of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fludroc‌ortisone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hydrocortisone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hydrocortisone</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of an<strong> adrenal crisis</strong> triggered by missed medication. In this patient who is a <strong>known</strong> <strong>case</strong> of adrenal insufficiency, <strong>IV hydrocortisone</strong> is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>However, in patients with <strong>newly diagnosed</strong> adrenal insufficiency, <strong>dexamethasone</strong> is used for the treatment of adrenal crisis. This is because dexamethasone is not measured in cortisol assays and would <strong>not interfere</strong> with the <strong>diagnostic</strong> <strong>tests</strong>, whereas hydrocortisone interferes with cortisol assays.</p>\n<p>Acute adrenal insufficiency requires the immediate initiation of the following therapeutic strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rehydration - <strong>saline</strong> infusion at an initial rate of 1 L/h with continuous cardiac monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Glucocorticoid</strong> replacement<strong> - </strong>bolus injection of 100 mg hydrocortisone<strong>, </strong>followed by the maintenance dose of 100–200 mg over 24h.</li>\n<li><strong>Mineralocorticoid</strong> replacement - initiated once the daily hydrocortisone dose has been reduced to &lt;50 mg as higher doses of hydrocortisone have sufficient mineralocorticoid action. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6880","text":"A patient who presented wi‌th an adrenal crisis is u‍ndergoing treatment with ‌dexamethasone. Which of t‌he following abnormalitie‌s will not improve with t​his treatment?","raw_text":"A patient who presented with an adrenal crisis is undergoing treatment with dexamethasone. Which of the following abnormalities will not improve with this treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postural h‌ypotension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperkalemia </strong>will <strong>not improve </strong>following administration of dexamethasone<strong>. Dexamethasone</strong> is a long-acting steroid with <strong>glucocorticoid</strong> action. It has <strong>no effect</strong> on <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>,<strong> </strong>which is mediated by <strong>mineralocorticoids</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyponatremia is chiefly due to mineralocorticoid deficiency, however, <strong>hypocortisolism</strong> causes <strong>hyponatremia</strong> due to loss of feedback inhibition of ADH resulting in <strong>SIADH </strong>(syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion). </p>\n<p>Hypocortisolism can also cause <strong>postural hypotension</strong> as <strong>cortisol</strong> <strong>regulates vascular tone</strong> by potentiating the effects of vasoconstrictors. Thus on administration dexamethasone hyponatremia and postural hypotension will improve as dexamethasone has glucocorticoid action but hyperkalemia will not improve. </p>\n<p>Dexamethasone is used in patients with newly diagnosed adrenal insufficiency as it does not interfere with serum cortisol measurement.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px; width: 403px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 41px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hormone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 41px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 64px;\">Hypoaldosteronism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, salt craving</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Dizziness, postural hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">AKI due to volume depletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyponatremia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\">Hypocortisolism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Anorexia, weight loss, fatigue, myalgia, arthralgia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Fever (increased IL-1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Postural hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hypoglycemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyponatremia (loss of feedback inhibition of ADH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 36px;\">Slightly increased TSH (loss of feedback inhibition)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\">Hypoandrogenism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Loss of libido</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Loss of pubic and axillary hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.484375px; height: 18px;\">Elevated ACTH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.515625px; height: 18px;\">Hyperpigmentation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6881","text":"A 62-year-old woman who wa‌s diagnosed with a non-fu‍nctioning pituitary adeno‌ma develops features of a‌drenal insufficiency. Whi‌ch of the following is no​t a feature of this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman who was diagnosed with a non-functioning pituitary adenoma develops features of adrenal insufficiency. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpig‌mentation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Postural h‌ypotension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lass‌itude","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperpigmentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperpigmentation</strong></p><p>A non-functioning pituitary adenoma causes hypopituitarism due to compression of the pituitary stalk. This results in <strong>secondary adrenal insufficiency</strong>. Hyperpigmentation is not a feature.</p>\n<p>This condition is characterized by an <strong>alabaster-like paleness</strong> due to the <strong>lack of ACTH </strong>secretion. In primary adrenal insufficiency, hyperpigmentation occurs as a result of elevated ACTH.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6884","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect with respect to‍ Addisonian pigmentation?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following  is incorrect with respect to Addisonian pigmentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of moles and scar‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Represented by palmar crea‌ses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Does not affect the oral m‌ucosa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased‌ fibrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Does not affect the oral mucosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/685ed0f4d26e4ea29bbcbe21709983bfx720x1443.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Does not affect the oral mucosa</strong></p><p><strong>Addison's</strong> disease causes patchy <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong> of the <strong>oral mucosa</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyperpigmentation is also present in areas of increased friction including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palmar creases</li>\n<li>Dorsal aspect of the foot</li>\n<li>Nipples and the axillary region</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6897","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of mineralocorticoid ‍excess?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of mineralocorticoid excess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral micronodular adr‌enal hyperplasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Unilateral adrenal adenoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital adrenal hyperpl‌asia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liddle’s‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bilateral micronodular adrenal hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bilateral micronodular adrenal hyperplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia</strong> is the most common cause of mineralocorticoid excess. It causes primary hyperaldosteronism. Other causes of primary hyperaldosteronism are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conn’s syndrome</strong> – Unilateral adrenal <strong>adenoma</strong> </li>\n<li>Glucocorticoid-remediable hyperaldosteronism (&lt; 1%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other causes of<strong> </strong>mineralocorticoid excess<strong> </strong>are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (<strong>CAH</strong>)</li>\n<li>Syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess (SAME)</li>\n<li>Adrenocortical carcinoma</li>\n<li>Progesterone-induced hypertension</li>\n<li>Cushing’s syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Liddle’s</strong> syndrome is due to a mutation of <strong>ENaC</strong> subunits. This results in <strong>reduced degradation</strong> of ENaC channels, causing <strong>prolonged mineralocorticoid action</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6900","text":"Which of the following cli‌nical features will you n‍ot expect to see in a pat‌ient with Conn's syndrome‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following clinical features will you not expect to see in a patient with Conn's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ed‌ema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle ‌weakness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Edema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Edema</strong></p><p>In <strong>Conn’s syndrome</strong>, even though there is volume expansion, an underlying salt-escape mechanism leads to the<strong> absence </strong>of<strong> pedal edema.</strong></p>\n<p>The volume expansion due to hypernatremia leads to <strong>right heart dilatation </strong>that stimulates<strong> </strong>the release of <strong>atrial natriuretic peptide </strong>(ANP). This leads to compensatory <strong>diuresis</strong> and lack of edema despite sodium retention. </p>\n<p>Features of <strong>primary hyperaldosteronism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Metabolic alkalosis</li>\n<li>Polyuria (due to ANP)</li>\n<li>Polydipsia (consequence of polyuria)</li>\n<li><strong>Muscle weakness</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6901","text":"A 39-year-old man is refer‌red for evaluation of sev‍ere hypertension that has‌ not improved despite bei‌ng on 3 anti-hypertensive‌ drugs. The presence of d​ecreased plasma renin act‌ivity in this patient wou​ld point to which of the ‍following diagnoses?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old man is referred for evaluation of severe hypertension that has not improved despite being on 3 anti-hypertensive drugs. The presence of decreased plasma renin activity in this patient would point to which of the following diagnoses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conn s‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bartter ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic kid‌ney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral renal artery hyp‌ertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conn syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conn syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Conn</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> is a cause of <strong>primary hyperaldosteronism</strong> that presents with<strong> hypertension </strong>and<strong> low </strong>levels of <strong>renin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Elevated aldosterone</strong> levels cause <strong>negative feedback</strong> in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (<strong>RAAS</strong>) and results in decreased renin production.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6903","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best initial investig‍ation in a patient with h‌ypokalemic hypertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best initial investigation in a patient with hypokalemic hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACTH stimu‌lation test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CT ab‌domen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenal ve‌in sampling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aldosterone‌-renin ratio","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aldosterone-renin ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aldosterone-renin ratio</strong></p><p><strong>Hypokalemic hypertension</strong> is suggestive of <strong>hyperaldosteronism. </strong>The screening test of choice is a measurement of plasma renin and aldosterone levels to calculate the <strong>aldosterone-renin ratio</strong> (ARR).</p>\n<p>ARR must be estimated in patients with hypertension and any one of the following features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drug resistance</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Adrenal mass</li>\n<li>Onset before 40 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prior to the determination of ARR, <strong>hypokalemia</strong> <strong>correction</strong> must be done. If the patient is on <strong>spironolactone,</strong> it must be <strong>stopped</strong> 4 weeks prior to testing. Other blood pressure medication may be continued.</p>\n<p><strong>ARR</strong> screen is considered <strong>positive</strong> when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ARR &gt; 750 pmol/L : ng/mL per hour</li>\n<li>Aldosterone &gt; 450 pmol/L</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: ACTH stimulation test is used for the diagnosis of Addison’s disease. It presents with hypotension and hyperkalemia.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: If ARR is positive,<strong> CT abdomen</strong> and <strong>adrenal vein sampling</strong> are used for <strong>confirmation</strong> of the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6906","text":"Which of the following tes‌ts is not performed in pa‍tients with an elevated a‌ldosterone-renin ratio?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is not performed in patients with an elevated aldosterone-renin ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saline inf‌usion test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metyrapone stimulation tes‌t","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral sodium ‌loading test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fludrocortisone suppressio‌n test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metyrapone stimulation test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metyrapone stimulation test</strong></p><p>An elevated aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR) is indicative of primary hyperaldosteronism. <strong>Metyrapone</strong> <strong>stimulation</strong> <strong>test</strong> is used in the diagnosis of <strong>adrenal</strong> <strong>insufficiency</strong>, not hyperaldosteronism.</p>\n<p>A <strong>failure</strong> of <strong>suppression</strong> of aldosterone levels below 140 pmol/L indicates <strong>autonomous</strong> <strong>aldosterone</strong> production, which is the hallmark of <strong>primary</strong> <strong>hyperaldosteronism</strong>. This principle is used in the following tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Saline infusion test - IV administration of 2L of physiological saline over 4 hours</li>\n<li>Oral sodium loading test - oral sodium given over 3 days</li>\n<li>Fludrocortisone suppression test - given IV over 4 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These tests may be used to confirm the diagnosis in patients with elevated ARR, but are not necessary in cases of overt hypokalemic hypertension, strongly positive ARR, and high aldosterone. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pNUQt4R0","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"13_shigella_and_salmonella","title":"13.Shigella And Salmonella","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC2635","text":"A child presents with a hi‌gh fever, acute abdominal​ pain, and malaise. The c​hild passes small amounts​ of bloody mucopurulent s‍tools with increasing ten​esmus and cramps. What is‌ the most likely causativ​e organism?","raw_text":"A child presents with a high fever, acute abdominal pain, and malaise. The child passes small amounts of bloody mucopurulent stools with increasing tenesmus and cramps. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ‌cholerae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rota‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shigella d‌ysenteriae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Entamoeba ‌histolytica","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Shigella dysenteriae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Shigella dysenteriae</strong></p><p>Acute onset of symptoms (fever, abdominal cramps) with <strong>dysentery</strong> (bloody mucopurulent stools) and tenesmus is most likely due to <strong><em>Shigella dysenteriae</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Shigella dysenteriae </em>is a Gram-negative bacillus causing bacillary dysentery in humans. It is spread through faeco-oral transmission. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <em><strong>Vibrio cholerae</strong></em> results in watery diarrhea with characteristic <strong>rice watery stools</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Viruses such as <strong>rotavirus</strong> and norovirus causes <strong>watery diarrhea</strong> through mucosal irritation.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em><strong>Entamoeba histolytica</strong></em> results in <strong>gradual onset</strong> of symptoms usually 1-3 weeks with dysentery. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2228","text":"Which of the following tox‌ins acts by inhibiting pr​otein synthesis? (ETEC - ​Enterotoxigenic E. coli)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following toxins acts by inhibiting protein synthesis?</p>\n<p>(ETEC - Enterotoxigenic E. coli)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choler‌a toxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shiga‌ toxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pertuss‌is toxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Labile tox‌in of ETEC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shiga toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shiga toxin</strong></p><p><strong>Shiga toxin </strong>acts by <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Shiga toxin or <strong>verotoxin</strong> is an exotoxin produced by <em>Shigella</em> species. It consists of two subunits A and B. The<strong> B subunit </strong>binds to <strong>globotriaosylceramide</strong> receptors on the target cell, and then, the A subunit gets internalized. The <strong>A subunit</strong> leads to <strong>inhibition</strong> of <strong>60S</strong> ribosome, leading to shut down of protein synthesis. Its site of action is in the endothelial cells of small blood vessels of GIT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2224","text":"A 4-year-old boy developed‌ bloody diarrhea and abdo​minal cramps. The bacteri​a isolated from the stool​ specimen did not ferment‍ lactose or mannitol. Whi​ch of the following organ‌isms is implicated here?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy developed bloody diarrhea and abdominal cramps. The bacteria isolated from the stool specimen did not ferment lactose or mannitol. Which of the following organisms is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shigella d‌ysenteriae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shigella‌ flexneri","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shigell‌a boydii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shigell‌a sonnei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Shigella dysenteriae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Shigella dysenteriae</strong></p><p><strong><em>Shigella dysenteriae</em></strong> does not ferment mannitol and lactose.</p>\n<p><em>Shigella</em> species cause <strong>bacillary dysentery</strong> (bloody diarrhea with abdominal cramps). They do <strong>not </strong>ferment<strong> lactose</strong>, except <em>S.sonnei</em> which is a late lactose fermenter. To differentiate between the various <em>Shigella </em>species, <strong>mannitol fermentation</strong> is noted. </p>\n<p>Classification of pathogenic Shigella species:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 151px;\" width=\"551\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Designation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mannitol Fermentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Ornithine decarboxylase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Lactose </strong><strong>Fermentation</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><em>S. dysenteriae</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>-</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><em>S. flexneri</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><em>S. boydii</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\"><em>S. sonnei</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.75px; text-align: center;\">Late lactose fermenter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2305","text":"Which of the following sal‌monellae is non-motile?","raw_text":"Which of the following salmonellae is non-motile?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salmonel‌la typhi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salmonella‌ enteridis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salmonella‌ gallinarum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonell‌a chester","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Salmonella gallinarum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Salmonella gallinarum</strong></p><p><strong><em>S. gallinarum</em></strong> is non-motile.</p>\n<p>Salmonellae are aerobic or facultatively anaerobic Gram-negative bacilli. They are motile due to the presence of peritrichous flagella, except <em>S. gallinarum </em>and<strong><em> S. pullorum. </em></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2322","text":"A Greek patient with trave‌l history to India is dia​gnosed with enteric fever​. What is the minimum amo​unt of pathogen required ‍to cause this condition?","raw_text":"A Greek patient with travel history to India is diagnosed with enteric fever. What is the minimum amount of pathogen required to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-10 b‌acilli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100 b‌acilli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10^3 -10^‌6 bacilli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10^8 -10^‌10 bacilli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10^3 -10^6 bacilli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10^3 -10^6 bacilli</strong></p><p><strong>Enteric fever</strong> is caused by <strong><em>S. typhi</em> </strong>or <em><strong>S. paratyphi</strong>.</em> The minimum<strong> </strong>amount of <em>S. typhi </em>required to cause enteric fever is <strong>10<sup>3 </sup></strong>to<strong> 10<sup>6 </sup></strong>organisms. </p>\n<p>Enteric fever spreads through the feco-oral route. It presents with <strong>step-ladder fever</strong> and abdominal pain after an incubation period of 10–20 days. Over the next week, the patient can develop abdominal distension, diarrhea, splenomegaly and characteristic<strong> rose spots</strong> on the abdomen due to vasculitis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>amount</strong> of pathogen<strong> </strong>required to cause an <strong>infection</strong> in the host is known as the <strong>infectious dose</strong>. The infectious doses of few bacteria are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli - </em>10<sup>6</sup> – 10<sup>8</sup> organisms</li>\n<li>Shigella - 10 – 100 organisms</li>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli</em> O157:H7 - &lt;10 organisms</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2315","text":"What is the incubation per‌iod of Salmonella typhi?","raw_text":"What is the incubation period of Salmonella typhi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-5 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-21‌ days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"14-25‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20-30‌ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5-21 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5-21 days</strong></p><p>The incubation period for <strong><em>Salmonella typhi</em></strong> is from <strong>5 </strong>to <strong>21 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>The incubation period for <em>Salmonella typhi </em>typically averages <strong>10–14 days,</strong> but can range from 5 to 21 days. The duration is likely<strong> dependant</strong> on the <strong>inoculum size</strong> and the host’s health and <strong>immune status</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2311","text":"A 25-year-old man presente‌d to the ER with fever, s​plenomegaly, and a rash a​s shown in the image belo​w. After admission, he de‍veloped intestinal perfor​ation and an exploratory ‌laparotomy revealed sever​al longitudinal ulcers in‌ the small bowel. What is‌ the probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presented to the ER with fever, splenomegaly, and a rash as shown in the image below. After admission, he developed intestinal perforation  and an exploratory laparotomy revealed several longitudinal ulcers in the small bowel. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enteri‌c fever","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoeb‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhag‌ic colitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enteric fever","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/346e3d2c2ff84e178b631a9d72c037c0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enteric fever</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Rose spots</strong>. The combination of this with fever, splenomegaly, presence of longitudinal ulcers in the intestine and intestinal perforation is suggestive of <strong>enteric fever.</strong></p>\n<p>The term enteric fever includes typhoid fever caused by <em>Salmonella typhi</em> and paratyphoid fever caused by <em>Salmonella paratyphi A, B</em> and<em> C</em><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Enteric fever presents with <strong>step ladder pyrexia. </strong>The fever is accompanied by <strong>relative bradycardia</strong> which is known as the<strong> Faget sign</strong>. <strong>Hepatosplenomegaly</strong> is a common finding on examination. <strong>Rose spots</strong> that fade on pressure appear on the skin during the second or third week. </p>\n<p>Since bile is a good culture medium for the bacilli, they multiply abundantly in the gallbladder and are discharged continuously into the intestine. In the intestine, it causes inflammation and necrosis leaving behind the characteristic <strong>typhoid ulcers</strong> which are oriented along the axis of the intestine (<strong>longitudinal ulcers</strong>).<strong> </strong>Ulceration of the bowel leads to two major complications- <strong>intestinal</strong> <strong>perforation</strong> and <strong>hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: In intestinal<strong> tuberculosis</strong>, ulcers are perpendicular to the axis of the intestine (<strong>transverse ulcers</strong>).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Flask shaped ulcers</strong> are characteristic of <strong>amoebiasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hemorrhagic colitis</strong> is caused by <em>Shigella dysenteriae</em> and some strains of <em>E. coli.</em> It is characterised by <strong>bloody diarrhea</strong> with tenesmus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2314","text":"While evaluating a patient‌ suspected to have enteri​c fever, you order a wide​ly used tube agglutinatio​n test in the second week‍ of his illness. Which of​ the following statements‌ regarding this test is f​alse?","raw_text":"While evaluating a patient suspected to have enteric fever, you order a widely used tube agglutination test in the second week of his illness. Which of the following statements regarding this test is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H agglutinin titer &amp;gt; 16‌0 and O agglutinin titer ​&amp;gt; 80 is considered sig​nificant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"O antibody cannot differen‌tiate between Salmonella ​types.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"O antibody lasts longer an‌d hence is not indicative​ of recent infection.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High titer value in a sing‌le test is not confirmati​ve.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. O antibody lasts longer and hence is not indicative of recent infection.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. O antibody lasts longer and hence is not indicative of recent infection.</strong></p><p>The <strong>tube agglutination test</strong> which is widely used for the diagnosis of <strong>enteric fever</strong> is the <strong>Widal test</strong>. The <strong>O antibodies</strong> detected via this test <strong>disappear</strong> within a <strong>few weeks</strong> of disease resolution and so are indicative of <strong>recent infection</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Widal test</strong> is a serological test for the measurement of O and H antigens of <em>Salmonella typhi </em>and <em>Salmonella paratyphi</em> present in the patient's serum. Four antigens are used- O antigens of <em>S. typhi</em> (TO), H antigens of <em>S. typhi</em> (TH), H antigens of <em>S. paratyphi A</em> (AH) and H antigens of <em>S. paratyphi B</em> (BH).</p>\n<p><strong>Paratyphoid O</strong> antigens <strong>cross-reacts </strong>with the typhoid O antigen due to the sharing of factor 12. It is not used in the test and thus, <strong>O antibody cannot differentiate</strong> between Salmonella types.</p>\n<p>H agglutinin titer &gt; 160 and O agglutinin titer &gt; 80 is considered significant. However, a <strong>4-fold rise </strong>in titers<strong> </strong><strong>between 1st</strong> and<strong> 3rd week </strong>is more<strong> significant</strong> compared to a <strong>single high titer</strong> because of the possibility of an <strong>anamnestic reaction</strong> - a transient rise of titer due to unrelated infections (malaria, dengue) in persons who had a prior enteric fever. After one week interval, if the titer increases, it indicates true infection. If the titer decreases, it indicates an anamnestic reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2302","text":"A young man with fever for‌ 2 weeks has the followin​g serology findings. Whic​h of the given statements​ is true about the diseas‍e he has? Antibody Antibo​dy titre O 128 H ( S. typ‌hi ) 256","raw_text":"<p>A young man with fever for 2 weeks has the following serology findings. Which of the given statements is true about the disease he has?</p>\n<table style=\"width: 246.441px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">Antibody</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.441px; text-align: center;\">Antibody titre </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">O</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.441px; text-align: center;\">128</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">H (<em>S. typhi</em>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.441px; text-align: center;\">256</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intestinal perforation fol‌lowing infection with S.t​yphi occurs in the 1st we​ek.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Faget’s sign is seen in en‌teric fever.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"In Widal test, Felix tube ‌is used to detect H antig​en.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood culture is the gold ‌standard of diagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Faget’s sign is seen in enteric fever.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Faget’s sign is seen in enteric fever.</strong></p><p>Widal results showing titers for <strong>O antibody &gt;80</strong> and <strong>H antibody</strong> for <em>S.typhi </em>to be <strong>&gt;160</strong> is suggestive of <strong>enteric fever</strong>.<strong> Faget's sign </strong>is seen in enteric fever.</p>\n<p>Enteric fever, caused by <em>Salmonella typhi </em>and <em>Salmonella paratyphi </em>A, B and C, presents with <strong>step ladder pyrexia.</strong> The fever rises one day and falls the subsequent morning. It continues to form peaks and troughs. Hepatosplenomegaly is a common finding on examination.<strong> Rose spots </strong>that fade on pressure appear on the skin during the second or third week. <strong>Faget's sign</strong> is the unusual pairing of fever with bradycardia (<strong>relative bradycardia</strong>) instead of tachycardia. It is also observed in yellow fever, brucellosis, and tularemia. <strong>Intestinal perforation</strong> (option A) is a complication of enteric fever caused by <em>Salmonella typhi</em>. It occurs in the <strong>3rd week </strong>following infection. </p>\n<p><strong>Widal test </strong>is a serological test used for the diagnosis of enteric fever during the 2nd week. It measures the levels of H and O antigens in the patient's serum. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 528px; height: 195.918px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.9883px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254.438px; height: 27.9883px;\"><strong>H antigen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.562px; height: 27.9883px;\"><strong>O antigen</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.9883px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254.438px; height: 27.9883px;\">Flagellar antigen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.562px; height: 27.9883px;\">Somatic antigen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55.9766px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254.438px; height: 55.9766px;\">Fluffy/woolly agglutination seen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.562px; height: 55.9766px;\">Granular agglutination seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.9883px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254.438px; height: 27.9883px;\">Appear late and persist for longer duration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.562px; height: 27.9883px;\">Appear early and persist for shorter duration </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55.9766px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254.438px; height: 55.9766px;\">Dreyer's tube (conical bottom) used</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.562px; height: 55.9766px;\">Felix tube (round bottom) used</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Bone marrow culture</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> (option D) for diagnosis of enteric fever. Blood culture is most commonly performed to confirm the diagnosis, however, bone marrow is <strong>more sensitive. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2316","text":"A patient, suspected to ha‌ve enteric fever, has bee​n started on ceftriaxone ​by his local village heal​er 2 days ago. Which inve‍stigation will confirm hi​s diagnosis at this stage‌?","raw_text":"A patient, suspected to have enteric fever, has been started on ceftriaxone by his local village healer 2 days ago. Which investigation will confirm his diagnosis at this stage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bone marr‌ow culture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stool ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urine ‌culture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bone marrow culture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bone marrow culture</strong></p><p><strong>Bone marrow culture</strong> is preferred at this stage.</p>\n<p>Bone marrow culture is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> test (&gt;80% sensitivity) for diagnosis of enteric fever. It remains<strong> positive</strong> for upto <strong>5 days</strong> <strong>after</strong> starting <strong>antibiotics</strong>, unlike blood culture. Hence, it is preferred in the patient above who has been on antibiotics for 2 days. </p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Blood culture </strong>has a sensitivity of about 60% for diagnosis of enteric fever. Its sensitivity drops with duration of illness, and is thus useful onlyduring the <strong>1st week</strong> of enteric fever. It rapidly <strong>becomes negative</strong> on treatment with<strong> antibiotics</strong>.<strong> </strong></span><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Stool culture </strong>has a <strong>sensitivity </strong>of <strong>30% </strong>and only yields positive results from the <strong>2nd </strong>or <strong>3rd week</strong> onwards. </span><strong>Urine culture </strong>has low sensitivity and may be positive only <strong>after</strong> the<strong> 2nd week</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The sensitivity of<strong> </strong>diagnostic methods for enteric fever is<strong> </strong>duodenal content culture &gt; bone marrow culture &gt; blood culture. However, duodenal content culture is rarely done in clinical practice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2304","text":"You are administering a Vi‌ capsular polysaccharide ​vaccine to a child in Ger​many before his trip to I​ndia. Which of the follow‍ing is false about the an​tigen used in this vaccin‌e?","raw_text":"You are administering a Vi capsular polysaccharide vaccine to a child in Germany before his trip to India. Which of the following is false about the antigen used in this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a caps‌ular antigen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is polysaccharide in na‌ture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is he‌at stable","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is alco‌hol stable","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is heat stable","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is heat stable</strong></p><p><strong>Vi capsule polysaccharide antigen</strong> is a <strong>heat-labile</strong> antigen used in the Vi capsular polysaccharide vaccine (ViCPS) for typhoid. </p>\n<p>Vi antigen is found in <strong><em>Salmonella typhi, S. paratyphi C </em></strong><em>and </em><strong><em>S. dublin. </em></strong>It is a <strong>capsular</strong> antigen and is a <strong>polysaccharide. </strong>It acts as a virulence factor by <strong>inhibiting phagocytosis</strong> and resisting complement activation and bacterial lysis. It envelopes the O antigen, and thus, many S. typhi strains fail to agglutinate with O antiserum. However, it can be destroyed by heat. It is <strong>alcohol stable</strong>. This antigen is <strong>poorly immunogenic. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>Vi capsular polysaccharide vaccine</strong> (Vi CPS) contains purified Vi polysaccharide. It is given as a single dose of 0.5ml intramuscularly to<strong> children over 2 years</strong> of age. It is recommended for travellers to areas endemic for <em>Salmonella</em> infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2320","text":"A man living in the UK is ‌planning to travel to Ind​ia. His doctor prescribes​ the necessary vaccines p​rior to travel including ‍an oral typhoid vaccine. ​What is the schedule for ‌this vaccine?","raw_text":"A man living in the UK is planning to travel to India. His doctor prescribes the necessary vaccines prior to travel including an oral typhoid vaccine. What is the schedule for this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Day 1‌,3,5,7","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Day 1,‌3,7,14","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Day 1‌,7,14","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Day 1,‌7,14,21","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Day 1,3,5,7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Day 1,3,5,7</strong></p><p>The <strong>oral typhoid vaccine </strong>is given on<strong> days 1</strong>, <strong>3</strong>,<strong> 5</strong>, and <strong>7</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Typhoral</strong> or <strong>Ty21a</strong> is a<strong> live attenuated </strong>oral typhoid vaccine. It is given in 4 doses given on <strong>days 1, 3, 5,</strong> and<strong> 7. </strong>No antibiotics should be given during this period. lt is indicated only in individuals<strong> over 6 years </strong>of age. Protective immunity starts after 7 days of the last dose and lasts for <strong>4 years</strong>. Ty21a can also be administered to HIV-positive, asymptomatic individuals as long as their CD4 count is above 200/mm<sup>3</sup>. It is contraindicated in immunocompromised patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7391","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not used for typhoi​d fever in adults?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not used for typhoid fever in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftr‌iaxone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amik‌acin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amikacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amikacin</strong></p><p><strong>Amikacin</strong> is not used in the treatment of <strong>typhoid fever.</strong></p>\n<p>Amikacin is an aminoglycoside. Despite showing efficient in vitro killing of <em>Salmonella</em>, first- and second-generation cephalosporins and aminoglycosides have proven to be ineffective in actual clinical cases.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 252px; width: 527px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 456.836px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Antibiotic therapy for enteric fever in adults</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Indication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Agent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Dosage (Route)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Duration, Days</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 456.836px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Empirical Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 77px;\">  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 35px;\">Ceftriaxone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 35px;\">2 g/d (IV)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 35px;\">10-14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 42px;\">Azithromycin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 42px;\">1 g/d (PO)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 42px;\">5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 456.836px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Fully Susceptible</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 8px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 74px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Optimal treatment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>Ciprofloxacin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>500 mg bid (PO) or 400 mg q12 h (IV)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>5-7</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 66px;\">\n<p>Azithromycin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 66px;\">\n<p>1 g/d (PO)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 66px;\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 8px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 57px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Alternative treatment</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>Amoxicillin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>1 g tid (PO) or 2 g q6h (IV)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>14</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 8px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>Chloramphenicol</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>25 mg/kg tid (PO or IV)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 8px;\">\n<p>14-21</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 41px;\">\n<p>Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 41px;\">\n<p>160/800 mg bid (PO)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 41px;\">\n<p>7-14</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 456.836px; height: 29px;\"><strong>Multidrug-resistant, fluroquinolone-susceptible</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 62px;\">\n<p>Optimal treatment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 19px;\">\n<p>Ceftriaxone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 19px;\">\n<p>2 g/d (IV)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 19px;\">\n<p>10-14</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>Azithromycin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>1 g/d (PO)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 81px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Alternative treatment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Ciprofloxacin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>500 mg bid (PO) or 400 mg q12h (IV)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>5-14</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\"><strong>Quinolone-resistant</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 54px;\">Optimal treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 52px;\">Ceftriaxone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 52px;\"> 2 g/d (IV)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 52px;\"> 10-14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 2px;\">Azithromycin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 2px;\">\n<p>1 g/d (PO)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 2px;\">\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.8001px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106.25px; height: 67.8001px;\">Alternative treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px; height: 67.8001px;\">High dose ciprofloxacin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px; height: 67.8001px;\">\n<p>750 mg bid (PO) or 400 mg q12h (IV)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px; height: 67.8001px;\">\n<p>10-14</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 456.836px;\"><strong>Ceftriaxone-resistant</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 106.25px;\">Optimal treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px;\">Meropenem</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px;\">1 g q8h (IV)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px;\">10-14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162.383px;\">Azithromycin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.9297px;\">1 g/d (PO)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.273px;\">5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2309","text":"A patient developed fever ‌and diarrhoea after consu​mption of undercooked egg​s. Which of the following​ statements is false abou‍t this condition?","raw_text":"A patient developed fever and diarrhoea after consumption of undercooked eggs. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastroenteritis due to non‌-typhoidal salmonellosis ​is usually self limited.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stool cultures remain posi‌tive for 4-5 weeks after ​infection.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common isolate from s‌tool culture is Salmonell​a enteritidis.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-typhoidal salmonellosi‌s can be acquired from mu​ltiple animal reservoirs.​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most common isolate from stool culture is Salmonella enteritidis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most common isolate from stool culture is Salmonella enteritidis.</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>non-typhoidal salmonellosis</strong> (NTS). The <strong>most common</strong> organism isolated in NTS from stool culture is <em><strong>Salmonella typhimurium</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>Nontyphoidal salmonellosis refers to illnesses caused by all serotypes of Salmonella except for <em>Salmonella typhi, Salmonella paratyphi A, B,</em> and<em> C. </em><strong>Transmission</strong> of nontyphoidal salmonellosis occurs usually through the consumption of <strong>food </strong>or<strong> water contaminated</strong> with <strong>animal feces.</strong> Transmission may also occur through food products, especially <strong>eggs</strong>, poultry, undercooked ground meat, and hence <strong>multiple animals</strong> act as <strong>reservoirs</strong> for non-typhoidal salmonella.</p>\n<p><strong>Gastroenteritis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> clinical presentation of NTS. The illness usually lasts 4–7 days, and most people <strong>recover without treatment</strong>. Culturing organisms continues to be the mainstay of clinical diagnostic testing for nontyphoidal salmonella infection. Approximately 90% of isolates are obtained from routine <strong>stool culture</strong> until <strong>4-5 weeks </strong>after infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jbtn1Fto","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"13_malpresentations","title":"13.Malpresentations","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB1723","text":"A woman with gestational d​iabetes mellitus is admit​ted to the labour room. T‍he delivery of the baby's​ shoulder is delayed afte‌r the delivery of the hea‌d. When is shoulder dysto​cia considered?","raw_text":"A woman with gestational diabetes mellitus is admitted to the labour room. The delivery of the baby's shoulder is delayed after the delivery of the head. When is shoulder dystocia considered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head to shoulder delivery ​time &amp;gt;30 sec","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Head to shoulder delivery ​time &amp;gt;45 sec","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Head to shoulder delivery ​time &amp;gt;60 sec","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Head to shoulder delivery ​time &amp;gt;120 sec","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Head to shoulder delivery time >60 sec","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Head to shoulder delivery time &gt;60 sec</strong></p><p><strong>Shoulder dystocia</strong> is defined as head to shoulder delivery time of <strong>more than 60 seconds</strong>. It occurs when either the anterior or the posterior fetal shoulder gets impacted on the maternal symphysis or on the sacral promontory respectively. </p>\n<p><strong>P</strong><strong>relabour risk factors: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iabetes Mellitus</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>besity</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ostdatism and prior history</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>xcessive weight gain during pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intrapartum risk factors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged 1<sup>st</sup> and 2<sup>nd</sup> stage of labour</li>\n<li>Instrumental delivery</li>\n<li>Augmentation of labour with oxytocin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1726","text":"A 25-year-old primigravida​ with a history of gestat​ional diabetes mellitus i‍s in active labour. After​ delivery of the head, tu‌rtle sign was noticed. Wh‌ich of the following can ​be used for the managemen​t of this patient? Mc Rob‍ert's maneuver Wood's cor‌kscrew maneuver Modified ​Ritgen's maneuver Rubin's‌ maneuver Pinard's maneuv‌er Gaskin's maneuver","raw_text":"<p>A 25-year-old primigravida with a history of gestational diabetes mellitus is in active labour. After delivery of the head, turtle sign was noticed. Which of the following can be used for the management of this patient?</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Mc Robert's maneuver</li>\n<li>Wood's corkscrew maneuver</li>\n<li>Modified Ritgen's maneuver</li>\n<li>Rubin's maneuver</li>\n<li>Pinard's maneuver</li>\n<li>Gaskin's maneuver</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B, C, E​, and F","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A, D, E​, and F","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A, B, C​, and D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A, B, D​, and F","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A, B, D, and F","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6acddd0d57e1485b9fd414ed8ad3b1a2x1280x2319.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac62569cee174de7ba9686922df00403x1280x2318.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59cf6fd4115c4949bbeba109d78f3162x1280x1252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A, B, D, and F</strong></p><p>A patient with gestational diabetes and the presence of turtle sign is suggestive of <strong>shoulder dystocia</strong>. <strong>Mc Robert's </strong>manoeuvre,<strong> Wood's corkscrew </strong>manoeuvre, <strong>Gaskin's </strong>manoeuvre, and<strong> Rubin's </strong>manoeuvre are all used in its management. </p>\n<p>Shoulder dystocia occurs when either the anterior or the posterior fetal shoulder gets impacted on the maternal pelvis. <strong>Turtle sign</strong> may be seen which refers to the <strong>recoil of the fetal head</strong> against the perineum.</p>\n<p>Management of labour in case of shoulder dystocia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1st line of management - <strong>Mc Roberts</strong> manoeuvre (flexion and abduction of thigh)</li>\n<li>2nd line of management - <strong>Rubin's </strong>(rotation of the easily accessible shoulder by 30-45°), <strong>Woods corkscrew </strong>manoeuvre (rotate the posterior shoulder to 180<sup>o </sup>in a cork screw fashion) ; OR <strong>Gaskin</strong> (all four's position)</li>\n<li>Last line of management - <strong>Zavanelli</strong> manoeuvre (head is rotated to its pre-restitution position and caesarian section is performed); OR procedures such as <strong>cleidotomy</strong> (fracture of the clavicle of the baby); <strong>symphysiotomy</strong> (divide pubic symphysis of mother)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">In a case of <strong>bilateral shoulder dystocia</strong>, directly proceed to perform <strong>cesarean section</strong> after doing Zavanelli manoeuvre.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1725","text":"The first manoeuvre for sh​oulder dystocia consists ​of:","raw_text":"The first manoeuvre for shoulder dystocia consists of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forceful adduction by exte​nsion of legs on abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forceful adduction by flex​ion of legs on abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Forceful abduction of legs​ by extending them on abd​omen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Forceful abduction of legs​ by flexing them on abdom​en","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Forceful abduction of legs by flexing them on abdomen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Forceful abduction of legs by flexing them on abdomen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>shoulder dystocia. Mc Roberts manoeuvre</strong> is the first manoeuvre to be performed in shoulder dystocia. It involves <strong>flexion</strong> and <strong>abduction</strong> of the <strong>maternal hips,</strong> positioning the maternal <strong>thighs on</strong> her <strong>abdomen.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1724","text":"A G2P1 obese woman was dia​gnosed with arrest of des​cent during labour. Ident‍ify the following manoeuv​re performed for this pat‌ient.","raw_text":"A G2P1 obese woman was diagnosed with arrest of descent during labour. Identify the following manoeuvre performed for this patient.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rubin m​anoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"McRoberts​ manoeuvre","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zavanelli​ manoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wood corkscr​ew manoeuvre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. McRoberts manoeuvre","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/36d4d728558846c09e8305d5cbb0a57a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b465b238354c4a88bed90c1104b6903bx1280x1035.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29c8dee08461487592724cf6dc3799c3x1280x2319.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. McRoberts manoeuvre</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>shoulder dystocia</strong> and the manoeuvre shown above is the <strong>McRoberts manoeuvre. </strong>This<strong> </strong>is the most effective intervention and should be the <strong>first manoeuvre </strong>to be performed in shoulder dystocia. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>McRoberts</strong> manoeuvre:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Flexion</strong> and <strong>abduction</strong> of the <strong>maternal hips </strong>(positioning the maternal thighs on her abdomen)<strong>.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The pelvic rotation which occurs due to the straightening of the sacrum tends to free the impacted anterior shoulder of the baby. </li>\n<li><strong>Suprapubic pressure</strong> may be used simultaneously.</li>\n<li>The <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> is the most common nerve to get </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">injured</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> during this manoeuvre.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">If the first line of management fails, proceed with internal rotational manoeuvres such as Rubin's and Wood corkscrew in McRoberts position.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Rubin's</strong> manoeuvre:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Internal rotational manoeuver </strong>used to deliver the baby in case of shoulder dystocia.</li>\n<li>It involves <strong>dislodging</strong> the <strong>easily accessible shoulder</strong> towards the anterior aspect of the chest from under the pubic symphysis <strong>by 30-45°</strong> while staying in the McRoberts' position. </li>\n<li><strong>Pressure</strong> is applied on the <strong>scapular aspect</strong> (posteriorly) of the shoulder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Wood corkscrew manoeuvre</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Internal rotational manoeuver</strong> used to deliver the baby in case of shoulder dystocia.</li>\n<li>It involves <strong>dislodging</strong> the <strong>posterior shoulder by 180°</strong> in a <strong>corkscrew fashion</strong> to lie beneath the pubic symphysis while staying in McRobert's position.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure</strong> is applied on the <strong>clavicular aspect</strong> (anteriorly) of the shoulder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3810","text":"Which of these steps would​ you undertake for the ma​nagement of a patient wit‍h shoulder dystocia along​ with performing the McRo‌berts manoeuvre?","raw_text":"Which of these steps would you undertake for the management of a patient with shoulder dystocia along with performing the McRoberts manoeuvre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apply fund​al pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rotate posterior shoulder ​to 90 degrees","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flex the legs over the abd​omen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apply suprap​ubic pressure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apply suprapubic pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apply suprapubic pressure</strong></p><p>Applying a gentle <strong>suprapubic pressure</strong> is the first step in the management of shoulder dystocia along with <strong>McRoberts manoeuvre</strong>. Fundal pressure is not a method of delivery in such cases.</p>\n<p>Hernandez and Wendel suggested the use of a <strong>shoulder dystocia drill</strong> to better organize emergency management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Call for help and mobilize labour assistants, anaesthesia and pediatric doctors.</li>\n<li>An attempt at traction is tried followed by episiotomy to afford room posteriorly.</li>\n<li>Apply pressure <strong>suprapubically, not fundally,</strong> with the palm or fist, directing the pressure on the anterior shoulder.</li>\n<li>This should be both downward (below the pubic bone) and laterally (toward the baby's face or sternum) in conjunction with the <strong>McRoberts</strong> manoeuvre. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>These manoeuvres will resolve most cases of shoulder dystocia. If the above-listed steps fail, the following steps may be attempted, and any of the manoeuvres may be repeated:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rubin's </strong>manoeuvre</li>\n<li><strong>Woods corkscrew </strong>manoeuvre</li>\n<li>Gaskin (all four's position)</li>\n<li><strong>Zavanelli </strong>manoeuvre</li>\n<li>Cleidotomy (fracture of the clavicle of baby) </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Symphysiotomy (divide pubic symphysis of mother).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1729","text":"What is the most common fe​tal malpresentation?","raw_text":"What is the most common fetal malpresentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occipito​posterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Face pre​sentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breech pr​esentation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Brow pre​sentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Breech presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Breech presentation</strong></p><p>The most common fetal malpresentation is the <strong>breech presentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common breech presentation in <strong>primigravida</strong> is <strong>frank </strong>breech and in <strong>multigravida</strong>, it is<strong> </strong><strong>flexed/complete</strong> breech. It is most commonly seen with <strong>prematurity.</strong></p>\n<p>In breech presentation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common position is <strong>left sacroanterior </strong></li>\n<li>The engaging diameter is <strong>bitrochanteric.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cord prolapse</strong> is <strong>common</strong> in <strong>footling</strong> presentation and <strong>rare</strong> in <strong>frank </strong>breech.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, among all malpresentations, <strong>cord prolapse</strong> is most common in<strong> transverse lie.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1727","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is Vagos metho​d used?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is Vagos method used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cord p​rolapse","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shoulder p​resentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impacte​d breech","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compound p​resentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cord prolapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cord prolapse</strong></p><p><strong>Vagos method</strong> is used in <strong>cord prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vagos method is also known as the <strong>bladder filling method</strong>. Here, the bladder is distended with saline to push the presenting part higher up, thereby <strong>preventing compression</strong> in case of cord prolapse. The bladder is filled with <strong>400–750 mL of normal saline</strong> with a Foley’s catheter. Then, the balloon is inflated and the catheter is clamped.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1728","text":"A pregnant woman presented​ to the clinic with the f​ollowing fetal presentati‍on. What is the likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman presented to the clinic with the following fetal presentation. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stargaze​r breech","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complet​e breech","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexed​ breech","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frank ​breech","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Frank breech","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc40177e0edc414bb479f579c43c8565.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3cc88959cbe345aa87d3bdc3b2cfc67cx1280x2032.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Frank breech</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>frank breech.</strong></p>\n<p>Breech presentation can be divided into the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incomplete:</strong> Breech with extended legs, footling, knee presentation</li>\n<li><strong>Complete:</strong> Breech with flexed legs</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 331px; width: 453.672px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Breech type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Presenting part</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47.3906px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 47.3906px;\"><strong>Frank breech</strong><br/>(with extended legs)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 47.3906px;\">Thighs flexed on the trunk, legs extended at the knee joint.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 47.3906px;\">Buttocks and genitalia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Footling</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\">Both thighs and legs are partially extended</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\">Foot </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Knee </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\">Thighs extended, Knee flexed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\">Knee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Complete</strong>/flexed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\">Both thighs and legs are flexed.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\">Buttocks, genitalia, foot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1731","text":"A multigravida presented t​o the clinic at 36 weeks ​of gestation. She was fou‍nd to have a breech prese​ntation clinically and on‌ sonography. Which of the‌ following is not an indi​cation for cesarean secti​on in this type of presen‍tation?","raw_text":"A multigravida presented to the clinic at 36 weeks of gestation.  She was found to have a breech presentation clinically and on sonography.  Which of the following is not an indication for cesarean section in this type of presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperexte​nded fetus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexed​ breech","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Footlin​g breech","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sever​e IUGR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flexed breech","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flexed breech</strong></p><p><strong>Flexed breech </strong>is not an indication for cesarean section in breech presentation. This baby can be delivered <strong>vaginally.</strong></p>\n<p>Factors favouring cesarean delivery of the breech fetus:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 581\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Large fetus (&gt; 3800 to 4000 g)</li>\n<li><strong>Severe IUGR</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Footling presentation </strong>(due to risk of cord prolapse)</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperextended head</strong></li>\n<li>Contracted pelvis</li>\n<li>Previous cesarean section</li>\n<li>Preterm breech</li>\n<li>Primi with breech</li>\n<li>Twins, with the first baby in the breech</li>\n<li>Lack of surgeon experience</li>\n<li>Patient requests for cesarean delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1733","text":"A footling breech comes un​der ______ type of breech​ presentation.","raw_text":"A footling breech comes under ______ type of breech presentation.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fr​ank","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comp​lete","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fle​xed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incom​plete","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incomplete","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98e1a245aa864e4d8140b60dc5369afdx1280x3531.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incomplete</strong></p><p>A <strong>footling</strong> breech is a type of <strong>incomplete breech </strong>presentation with one or more feet below the breech.</p>\n<p>Breech presentation can be divided into the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incomplete:</strong> Breech with extended legs, footling or knee presentation</li>\n<li><strong>Complete:</strong> Breech with flexed legs</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 331px; width: 453.672px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Breech type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Presenting part</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47.3906px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 47.3906px;\"><strong>Frank breech</strong><br/>(with extended legs)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 47.3906px;\">Thighs flexed on trunk, legs extended at knee joint.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 47.3906px;\">Buttocks and genitalia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Footling</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\">Both thighs and legs are partially extended</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\">Foot </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Knee </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\">Thighs extended, knee flexed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\">Knee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Complete</strong>/flexed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 50px;\">Both thighs and legs are flexed.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.672px; height: 50px;\">Buttocks, genitalia, foot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1741","text":"A neonate is noted to have​ Klumpke's paralysis of t​he left arm following del‍ivery. Which of the follo​wing is not a risk factor‌ for the associated fetal‌ presentation?","raw_text":"A neonate is noted to have Klumpke's paralysis of the left arm following delivery. Which of the following is not a risk factor for the associated fetal presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preecl​ampsia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroc​ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tw​ins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placent​a previa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Preeclampsia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/492056ce12354f33aa257c1110fc5a12x1280x1954.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Preeclampsia</strong></p><p><strong>Klumpke's paralysis</strong> is associated with difficult <strong>breech delivery</strong>, which is due to hyperabduction of the arm during traction and delivery of the fetus. Preeclampsia is not a risk factor for breech presentation.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for breech presentation:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early gestational age</li>\n<li>Abnormal amnionic fluid volume</li>\n<li><strong>Multifetal gestation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hydrocephalus</strong></li>\n<li>Anencephaly</li>\n<li>Uterine anomalies</li>\n<li><strong>Placenta previa</strong></li>\n<li>Fundal placental implantation</li>\n<li>High parity with uterine relaxation</li>\n<li>Prior breech delivery</li>\n<li>Prior cesarean delivery</li>\n<li>Smoking.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9825","text":"What is the most common ca​use of the following pres​entation?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of the following presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prema​turity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroc​ephalus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Placent​a previa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyhyd​ramnios","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prematurity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6dd941a04e5242f68ba8d32e5e744f3d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prematurity</strong></p><p>The presentation depicted in the image is<strong> breech presentation. </strong>The most common cause of this presentation is <strong>prematurity</strong>.</p>\n<p>The incidence of breech at term is 3%. At 28 weeks of pregnancy, 20% of women have a breech presentation. The fetus undergoes <strong>spontaneous version</strong> usually between <strong>30 - 34 weeks</strong> thereby correcting its position by itself. </p>\n<p>Persistent breech occurs when this spontaneous version is prevented by any of the following causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrauterine death of the fetus</li>\n<li>Anomalies such as trisomy 13, 18, 21</li>\n<li>Anomalies of uterus (septate/bicornuate) </li>\n<li>Oligohydramnios and <strong>polyhydramnios</strong></li>\n<li>Twins</li>\n<li>Short cord</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrocephalus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Placenta previa</strong></li>\n<li>Contracted pelvis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recurrent/habitual breech </strong>is when breech recurs in <strong>3 or more pregnancies.</strong> It is usually due to <strong>uterine anomalies</strong> and <strong>cornufundal</strong> attachment of the <strong>placenta.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9861","text":"The following procedure is​ planned for a pregnant w​oman at 37 weeks of gesta‍tion. What is an absolute​ contraindication for thi‌s?","raw_text":"The following procedure is planned for a pregnant woman at 37 weeks of gestation. What is an absolute contraindication for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placent​a previa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Severe pre​-eclampsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Previo​us LSCS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multifetal​ gestation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Placenta previa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/022253dac7b64492af9ba43107a36d48.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Placenta previa</strong></p><p><strong>Placenta previa </strong>is an <strong>absolute contraindication</strong> for external cephalic version (ECV).</p>\n<p><strong>Relative contraindications for ECV:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">early labor</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">oligohydramnios  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ruptured membranes </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">known nuchal cord</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">structural uterine abnormalities</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">fetal-growth restriction </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>multifetal gestation</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">prior abruption or its risk factors </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>severe pre-eclampsia</strong> and hypertension</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>previous LSCS</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:  In twin gestation, with a cephalic-noncephalic presentation, after the delivery of the first twin, an internal podalic version or intrapartum external cephalic version can be attempted for the second non-cephalic twin. However, ECV in this case, can lead to an increased incidence of fetal distress when compared with the internal podalic version. Hence, <strong>ECV</strong> is only a <strong>relative contraindication in twin pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Previous LSCS is a relative contraindication for ECV. However, it is not attempted often and needs to be performed with caution. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9860","text":"A multiparous woman presen​ts in labour at 38 weeks ​of gestation. On examinat‍ion, the uterus appears a​symmetrical and fundal he‌ight is less than the per‌iod of amenorrhea. On fun​dal grip, the fetal pole ​is not palpable and in pe‍lvic grip, the lower pole‌ of the uterus is empty. ​What is the likely fetal ‌presentation?","raw_text":"A multiparous woman presents in labour at 38 weeks of gestation.  On examination, the uterus appears asymmetrical and fundal height is less than the period of amenorrhea. On fundal grip, the fetal pole is not palpable and in pelvic grip, the lower pole of the uterus is empty. What is the likely fetal presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ver​tex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shou​lder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Br​ow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bre​ech","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shoulder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shoulder</strong></p><p>The given examination findings are suggestive of <strong>transverse lie</strong>. The likely fetal presentation is <strong>shoulder presentation.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common cause of transverse lie is <strong>prematurity</strong>. In term pregnancy, it is commonly seen in patients with <strong>placenta previa</strong>. Although the most common cause of <strong>cord prolapse</strong> is transverse lie, <strong>hand prolapse</strong> is also frequently seen in such conditions. The transverse lie is managed by the <strong>caesarean section. </strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>twin</strong> pregnancy, management of <strong>1st</strong> twin with a <strong>transverse lie</strong> is <strong>caesarean section </strong>whereas the <strong>2nd </strong>twin is managed by <strong>internal podalic version</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1732","text":"An obstetrician is perform​ing a breech delivery. Wh​ich of the following mano‍euvres can be used for ch​in to pubis delivery?","raw_text":"An obstetrician is performing a breech delivery. Which of the following manoeuvres can be used for chin to pubis delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pinards ​manoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zanavelli​ manoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prague m​anoeuvre","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lovset m​anoeuvre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prague manoeuvre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/176b563b2b4d4b48b09ea0a082fdb2bax1280x1868.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prague manoeuvre</strong></p><p>In a breech presentation, <strong>chin to pubis</strong> delivery can be done by <strong>Prague manoeuvre</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rarely, the back of the fetus fails to rotate to the anterior. In such cases, rotation of the <strong>posteriorly oriented back</strong> of the fetus to the anterior may be achieved by using stronger <strong>traction</strong> on the <strong>fetal legs or bony pelvis</strong>.</p>\n<p>If this still does not succeed, the fetus still may be delivered using the <strong>modified Prague</strong> manoeuvre. This consists of two fingers of one hand grasping the shoulders of the back-down fetus from below while the other hand draws the feet up and over the maternal abdomen.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>back</strong> is oriented <strong>anteriorly</strong>, extraction may be accomplished using the <strong>Mauriceau manoeuvre</strong> and delivering the fetus back down.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1734","text":"Which of the following met​hods is not used for deli​very of the aftercoming h‍ead in breech?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods is not used for delivery of the aftercoming head in breech?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burns-Mars​hall method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mauriceau-Smellie-Veit met​hod","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piper's​ forceps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keilland'​s forceps","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Keilland's forceps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/465bba920a424076894e520c43e49978x1280x5962.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a668c378237a4e2884b255d668c280c7x1279x1187.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e47eb6bd91f34cf48614f2eafe70f6b8x1279x1270.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2623613ea218483db6658f13cb6d2297x300x320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Keilland&#x27;s forceps</strong></p><p>Delivery of the aftercoming head in breech is not done with the help of Keilland's forceps. These forceps are used for the rotation of the fetal head. It is not used anymore.</p>\n<p>The methods used for <strong>delivery of the aftercoming head</strong> include the following:</p>\n<p><strong>Burns-Marshall manoeuvre: </strong>When the occiput is seen under the pubic symphysis, steady traction is given on the feet. The baby is swung in an <strong>arc</strong> towards the mother's abdomen. Simultaneous suprapubic pressure is given and the head is born by flexion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Malar flexion and shoulder traction (modified<strong> Mauriceau-Smellie-Veit technique</strong>):</p>\n<p>The baby is placed on the supinated left forearm (preferred) with the limbs hanging on either side. The middle and the index fingers of the left hand are placed over the <strong>malar bones</strong>. The ring and little fingers of the pronated right hand are placed on the child’s right shoulder. The index finger is placed on the left shoulder and the middle finger is placed on the suboccipital region.</p>\n<p><strong>Traction</strong> is now given in a <strong>downward </strong>and <strong>backward direction</strong> till the nape of the neck is visible under the pubic arch. The assistant gives <strong>suprapubic pressure</strong> during the period to maintain flexion. The fetus is carried in an upward and forward direction toward the mother’s abdomen releasing the face and brow. Lastly, the trunk is depressed to release the occiput and vertex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>Piper's forceps</strong>, identified by its <strong>distinct perineal curve</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1735","text":"Which of the following is ​not used in the managemen​t of entrapment of afterc‍oming head?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used in the management of entrapment of aftercoming head?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duhrssens​ incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV nitro​glycerin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zavanelli​ manoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kristellar​ manoeuvre","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kristellar manoeuvre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a33d1d82824c4bfa8db7293fb0c62d6dx720x798.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kristellar manoeuvre</strong></p><p>The management of <strong>entrapment of aftercoming head</strong> does <strong>not</strong> involve <strong>Kristellar manoeuvre</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Kristeller manoeuvre</strong> is the application of uterine fundal pressure during the second stage of labour to <strong>shorten</strong> the <strong>second stage</strong> of labour. This should be used sparingly used as it leads to avulsion of the levator ani.</p>\n<p>An <strong>incompletely dilated cervix</strong> will constrict around the neck and impede the delivery of the aftercoming head in breech.</p>\n<p>Management options:</p>\n<p>With <strong>gentle traction</strong> on the fetal body, the cervix may be manually slipped over the occiput.</p>\n<p>If this is not successful, then <strong>Dührssen incisions</strong> may be necessary. These are a set of three incisions in the <strong>cervix</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Can be made at<strong> </strong>the<strong> 2</strong> and<strong> 10 o'clock </strong>position on the cervix. An additional incision at the <strong>6 o’clock</strong> position is sometimes needed.</li>\n<li>Done for delivery of <strong>aftercoming head</strong> in breech or delivery of head through an <strong>incompletely dilated cervix</strong>.</li>\n<li>There is a significant risk of haemorrhage; the cervical incision may extend upwards within the broad ligament causing <strong>broad ligament haematoma.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other alternatives include <strong>i</strong><strong>ntravenous nitroglycerin, </strong>typically 100 μg, to provide cervical relaxation. <strong>General anaesthesia</strong> with halogenated agents is another option.</p>\n<p>As a last resort, the following methods are used:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zavanelli manoeuvre</strong>: It involves the replacement of the fetal head into the pelvis followed by cesarean delivery. This is performed for the protruding head and intractable shoulder dystocia.</li>\n<li><strong>Symphysiotomy</strong>: The intervening symphyseal carti­lage and ligamentous supports are cut to widen the symphysis pubis. It is also used to aid the delivery of an entrapped aftercoming head.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6626","text":"A woman in labour presente​d to the ER. A vaginal br​eech delivery was planned‍ for her. What statement ​regarding the management ‌is false?","raw_text":"A woman in labour presented to the ER. A vaginal breech delivery was planned for her. What statement regarding the management is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breech identified for the ​first time during deliver​y is a contraindication f‍or vaginal delivery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breech delivery is done in​ an hospital where a faci​lity for emergency cesare‍an delivery is available","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continous FHR monitoring i​s required for breech del​ivery during labour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Should be done by a skille​d obstetrician","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Breech identified for the first time during delivery is a contraindication for vaginal delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Breech identified for the first time during delivery is a contraindication for vaginal delivery</strong></p><p><strong>Breech</strong> identified for the <strong>first time during delivery</strong> is <strong>not a contraindication</strong> for <strong>normal delivery</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Vaginal breech delivery</strong> is considered when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fetal weight</strong> is between <strong>1.5 kg</strong> and <strong>3.5 kg</strong></li>\n<li>There is an adequate pelvis</li>\n<li>The presentation is <strong>frank breech</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>fetal head </strong>is<strong> flexed</strong></li>\n<li>There are no medical or obstetric complications</li>\n<li><strong>Facilities for emergency cesarean section</strong> are available (anesthetists, neonatologists) (option B)</li>\n<li>Facilities for <strong>continuous labor monitoring </strong>are available (option C)</li>\n<li>A <strong>skilled obstetrician </strong>is present (option D)</li>\n<li>Detailed informed consent has been obtained, explaining the risks of vaginal breech delivery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cesarean delivery for breech </strong>presentation is considered in the following cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lack of operator experience</li>\n<li>Patient's request for cesarean delivery</li>\n<li>Prior perinatal death or neonatal birth trauma</li>\n<li>Large fetus: &gt; 3800 to 4000 g</li>\n<li>Severe IUGR, term weight &lt;2.5kg to 2.8kg</li>\n<li>Incomplete breech presentation</li>\n<li>Oligohydramnios</li>\n<li>Fetal anomaly incompatible with vaginal delivery</li>\n<li>Hyperextended head</li>\n<li>Contracted pelvis</li>\n<li>Prior cesarean delivery</li>\n<li>Preterm breech</li>\n<li>Twins, with the first baby in the breech position</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 581\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1739","text":"On doing PV examination of​ a woman in labour, mouth​ with hard alveolar margi‍ns and malar eminence is ​palpable. Which of the fo‌llowing is not a cause of‌ this fetal presentation?​","raw_text":"On doing PV examination of a woman in labour, mouth with hard alveolar margins and malar eminence is palpable. Which of the following is not a cause of this fetal presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anenc​ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumour of n​eck of fetus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multi​parity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gynecoi​d pelvis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gynecoid pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gynecoid pelvis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>face presentation. </strong>Gynecoid pelvis is not a cause of this fetal presentation.</p>\n<p>Causes of face presentation: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anencephaly </strong>(most common cause)</li>\n<li>Intrauterine death</li>\n<li><strong>Multiparity</strong></li>\n<li>Prematurity</li>\n<li>Twist of cord around the neck</li>\n<li>Anthropoid pelvis</li>\n<li>Platypelloid pelvis</li>\n<li><strong>Tumours of neck of the fetus.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9854","text":"A multiparous woman was ad​mitted to the labour room​ at term with complaints ‍of draining per vaginum. ​On examination, the uteru‌s corresponds to term siz‌e, presentation is cephal​ic and the presenting par​t is face. Which diameter‍ is engaged in this type ‌of presentation?","raw_text":"A multiparous woman was admitted to the labour room at term with complaints of draining per vaginum.  On examination, the uterus corresponds to term size, presentation is cephalic and the presenting part is face.  Which diameter is engaged in this type of presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mentov​ertical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Submento​vertical","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Occipit​ofrontal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suboccipi​tofrontal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Submentovertical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/251dce52934448b5a0b5cf0d8cf8cbe7x1280x1770.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Submentovertical</strong></p><p>Engaging diameter in face presentation is <strong>submentovertical</strong> and <strong>submentobregmatic</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 459px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Engaging diameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Measurement</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 22px;\">\n<p>Vertex (Complete flexion)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 22px;\">Suboccipitobregmatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 22px;\">9.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 36px;\">Vertex (Incomplete flexion)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 36px;\">Suboccipitofrontal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 36px;\">10 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 36px;\">Vertex (Marked Deflexion)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 36px;\">Occipitofrontal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 36px;\">11.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 18px;\">Face ( Incomplete extension)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 18px;\">Submentovertical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 18px;\">11.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 18px;\">Face</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 18px;\">Submentobregmatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 18px;\">9.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.438px; height: 46px;\">Brow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.562px; height: 46px;\">Mentovertical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>14 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1740","text":"A G2P1 woman presented to ​the clinic at 40 weeks of​ gestation. On examinatio‍n, the uterus corresponds​ to term size and the fet‌al head is mobile. What i‌s the most common cause t​o be suspected here?","raw_text":"A G2P1 woman presented to the clinic at 40 weeks of gestation. On examination, the uterus corresponds to term size and the fetal head is mobile. What is the most common cause to be suspected here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True cephalopelvic disprop​ortion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occipito​posterior","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Placent​a previa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Full bladde​r of mother","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Occipitoposterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Occipitoposterior</strong></p><p><strong>Occipitoposterior position (OP)</strong> is the most common cause of a <strong>mobile head</strong> at term.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common </strong>fetal <strong>malposition</strong>. It is an abnormal position seen in the vertex presentation, not an abnormal presentation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occipitoposterior position is commonly seen in <strong>android pelvis/deflexed head</strong>.</li>\n<li>The engaging diameter of this position is <strong>sub-occipitofrontal/occipitofrontal</strong>.</li>\n<li>The most common type in occipitoposterior position is <strong>right OP</strong>.</li>\n<li>A persistent occipitoposterior position results from a failure of internal rotation prior to birth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD)</strong> is a disproportion in the size of the fetus relative to the maternal pelvis. This can result in the failure of progress of labour in the second stage. This usually occurs due to <strong>fetal malposition</strong> e.g., extended or asynclitic fetal head, occipitoposterior or transverse position. It is also seen in <strong>malpresentations</strong> like mentum posterior, brow.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis of CPD is subjective. However, <strong>true CPD</strong> may occur if the <strong>fetus</strong> has a large <strong>anomaly</strong> e.g., teratoma or conjoined twin, or in cases of a large fetus. It can also occur when the <strong>maternal </strong>pelvis is small or deformed like after <strong>pelvic trauma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9851","text":"A 34-year-old G2P1 woman i​n labour was admitted to ​the hospital at 38 weeks ‍of gestation. On examinat​ion, the fetus was found ‌to be in occipitoposterio‌r position. What is the m​ost appropriate managemen​t?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old G2P1 woman in labour was admitted to the hospital at 38 weeks of gestation. On examination, the fetus was found to be in occipitoposterior position. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emergency caesarean sectio​n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wait and watch for progres​s of labour","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prepare for forceps delive​ry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prepare for ventouse deliv​ery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wait and watch for progress of labour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wait and watch for progress of labour</strong></p><p>The most appropriate management for pregnant women in labour whose fetus is in <strong>occipitoposterior</strong> position is to <strong>wait and watch </strong>for progress of labour. In a majority of cases, the occiput rotates through 3/8th of the circle and the baby is born as occipitoanterior.</p>\n<p>Hence, when the diagnosis of occipitoposterior is made, labour is allowed to proceed normally when there are <strong>good uterine contractions</strong> and <strong>favourable pelvis.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications of caesarean section in occipitoposterior position:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arrest of labour</li>\n<li>Inadequate uterine contraction</li>\n<li>Fetal distress</li>\n<li>Other obstetrical indications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9857","text":"What is the most common co​mplication seen in face t​o pubis delivery?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication seen in face to pubis delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intracran​ial injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cephalh​ematoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraureth​ral tears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complete pe​rineal tears","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Complete perineal tears","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Complete perineal tears</strong></p><p>The most common complication seen in <strong>face to pubis</strong> delivery is <strong>complete perineal tears.</strong></p>\n<p>In face to pubis delivery, the <strong>occiput</strong> is <strong>posterior,</strong> and therefore, the largest<strong> biparietal diameter</strong> (9.4 cm) distends the perineum rather than the smaller bitemporal diameter (8 cm). A <strong>liberal episiotomy</strong> helps to avoid perineal tears.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"E5eh_h6v","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"13_glaucoma","title":"13.Glaucoma","num_questions":35,"total_marks":140,"duration":45,"time_per_question":77.14,"questions":[{"id":"MA1029","text":"What is the normal aqueous‌ production rate?","raw_text":"What is the normal aqueous production rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 mL‌/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 mL‌/ min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 μL‌/ min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5 μL‌/ min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 μL/ min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 μL/ min</strong></p><p>The rate of formation of aqueous humor is <strong>2-3 μL/min</strong>.<strong>  </strong></p>\n<p>Formation of aqueous humor involves the following:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrafiltration</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Secretion-</strong> Na/K ATPase of non-pigmented epithelium of the ciliary process</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1348","text":"What is the critical angle‌ of the air-cornea interf‍ace?","raw_text":"What is the critical angle of the air-cornea interface?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"46 de‌grees","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"64 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36 de‌grees","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 46 degrees","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07c64a6436474c13b1f821debddbb30fx1280x1300.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 46 degrees</strong></p><p>The <strong>critical angle</strong> of the air–cornea interface is <strong>46 degrees. </strong><strong>Gonioscopy</strong> is a method of <strong>evaluating</strong> the <strong>anterior chamber (AC) angle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Direct goniolenses</strong> include the Koeppe, Medical Workshop, Barkan, and Swan–Jacob. <strong>Indirect goniolenses</strong> include Goldmann, Zeiss, and Posner.</p>\n<p>Structures seen on gonioscopy from anterior to posterior are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schwalbe line:</strong> represents the posterior limit of Descemet's membrane</li>\n<li>Trabecular meshwork</li>\n<li>Scleral spur</li>\n<li>Ciliary body and root of the iris</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Shaffers grading</strong> of angle width:</p>\n<p><strong>Grade</strong> <strong>4</strong> (35–45°) is the <strong>widest angle,</strong> characteristic of <strong>myopia</strong> and <strong>pseudophakia;</strong> the ciliary body can be visualized without tilting the lens.<br/><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Grade 3</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (25–35°) is an open-angle in which the </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">scleral spur</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> is visible.<br/></span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Grade 2</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (20°) is an angle in which the </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">trabeculum</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> but not the scleral spur can be seen.<br/></span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Grade 1</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (10°) is a very narrow-angle in which </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">only</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> the </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Schwalbe line,</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and perhaps the top of the trabeculum, can be identified.<br/></span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">A slit angle</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> is one in which there is no obvious iridocorneal contact but no angle structures can be identified.<br/></span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Grade 0</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (0°) is</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> closed</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> due to </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">iridocorneal contact.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1355","text":"Which statement regarding ‌the depth of the anterior‍ chamber is false?","raw_text":"Which statement regarding the depth of the anterior chamber is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depth is less in women tha‌n men","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depth increa‌ses with age","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Depth is mo‌re in myopes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depth is less in hypermetr‌opes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Depth increases with age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Depth increases with age</strong></p><p>The <strong>depth</strong> of the anterior chamber <strong>decreases</strong> with <strong>age.</strong> </p>\n<p>Anterior chamber depth</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The anterior chamber is <strong>shallow</strong> in <strong>very young</strong> and in old <strong>age.</strong></li>\n<li>At other periods of life, it is normally about 2.5 mm<strong> deep</strong>.</li>\n<li>Depth is <strong>less</strong> in <strong>women</strong> than in men.</li>\n<li>Depth is <strong>more</strong> in <strong>myopes.</strong></li>\n<li>Depth is <strong>less</strong> in <strong>hypermetropia</strong> and in <strong>angle-closure glaucoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7120","text":"The child in the picture h‌as large corneas, photoph‍obia, and lacrimation. Wh​at is the likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"The child in the picture has large corneas, photophobia, and lacrimation. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenita‌l glaucoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital hereditary endo‌thelial dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Megalo‌cornea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Congenital glaucoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1a97152fe6549f19cc21f8e764580a6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfce3f20b960443dbba4d7a8b4c3bbc8x720x331.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Congenital glaucoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of an <strong>infant</strong> with <strong>large corneas</strong>, <strong>lacrimation</strong> and <strong>photophobia</strong> supported with the image is suggestive of <strong>congenital glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Primary congenital glaucoma (PCG) also known as <strong>developmental glaucoma </strong>refers to abnormally <strong>high</strong> intraocular pressure(IOP) which results from the <strong>developmental </strong>anomaly of the <strong>angle </strong>of the<strong> anterior chamber.</strong></p>\n<p>It can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>True congenital</strong> or <strong>newborn</strong> glaucoma in which IOP is elevated during <strong>intrauterine</strong> life and the baby is born with <strong>ocular enlargement.</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Infantile</strong> glaucoma: which manifests <strong>prior</strong> to age <strong>3 years</strong>.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Juvenile</strong> glaucoma: IOP rises <strong>between</strong> <strong>3</strong> and <strong>16 years</strong> of age.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Blepharospasm</li>\n<li><strong>Buphthalmos:</strong> Large eyeball due to stretching of the globe from elevated IOP prior to the age of 3 years.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Corneal enlargement:</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Corneal haze</strong> is due to corneal <strong>edema</strong>.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Blue sclera </strong>- due to the shining of the underlying uveal tissue through the <strong>thinned-out sclera</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Haab striae</strong> are healed breaks in <strong>Descemet's</strong> membrane</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Goniotomy</strong> - when the angle of the anterior chamber is visible through the clear cornea.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trabeculectomy when the corneal clouding prevents visualization of the angle.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Filtration procedures</strong> such as Trabeculectomy or combined trabeculectomy with trabeculectomy if the above procedures fail.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Glaucoma drainage devices in unresponsive cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Retinoblastoma</strong> is the most common <strong>primary intraocular malignancy</strong> of childhood that is seen between <strong>1-2 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>Findings of Retinoblastoma include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 862\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leukocoria</strong> (white pupillary reflex)- the most <strong>common</strong> manifestation</li>\n<li><strong>Strabismus</strong> </li>\n<li>Painful red eye with <strong>secondary glaucoma</strong></li>\n<li>An <strong>endophytic tumor</strong> grows into the vitreous as a white mass that may ‘seed’ into the gel. If <strong>calcification</strong> is present it gives a \"<strong>cottage cheese appearance</strong>\". An <strong>exophytic tumor</strong> grows outwards and causes overlying exudative retinal detachment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the leucocoria of the left eye.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 862\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option C: <strong>Congenital hereditary endothelial dystrophy </strong>(CHED) is an inherited corneal dystrophy that manifests since <strong>birth</strong> with <strong>endothelial degeneration</strong>. It appears as diffuse <strong>bilateral corneal edema</strong> in the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>elevated</strong> IOP. The clinical features of the disease include corneal clouding, visual impairment, and nystagmus.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Megalocornea </strong>is a bilateral non-progressive <strong>X-linked</strong> condition that most commonly affects <strong>males</strong>. In this condition, the horizontal <strong>corneal diameter</strong> is <strong>≥12mm</strong>(&lt;2 years) and is <strong>≥13 mm</strong>(&gt;2 years). It is characterized by a clear cornea, normal corneal thickness, and <strong>n</strong><strong>o breaks</strong> in the descemet's membrane. Systemic associations of Megalocornea include Marfan's syndrome, Apert syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos, and Down syndrome.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1030","text":"A neonate is brought with ‌complaints of photophobia‍ and watering. Clinical e​xamination shows normal t​ear passages and clear bu​t large cornea. Investiga​tion reveals Haab’s stria‌e seen in the eye. What i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A neonate is brought with complaints of photophobia and watering. Clinical examination shows normal tear passages and clear but large cornea. Investigation reveals Haab’s striae seen in the eye. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupht‌halmos","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kerat‌oconus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kerato‌globus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Buphthalmos","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c94f464a48143879195b4489a6d0fcfx268x188.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b77dc64f6e124e929988a7a9e807a8a6x638x351.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6f9a4a76c784bc69dc3947ea936998ex246x205.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Buphthalmos</strong></p><p>This neonate  with complaints of photophobia and watering with large cornea and Haab's striae is suggestive of <strong>buphthalmos. </strong><strong>Haab’s striae</strong> are horizontal/circumferential breaks in <strong>Descemet’s</strong> membrane seen in buphthalmos.</p>\n<p>Buphthalmos (Bull<strong>-</strong>like eyes) is an enlargement of the eyeball seen in <strong>true congenital</strong> and <strong>infantile glaucoma,</strong> usually when the disease onset is <strong>before</strong> <strong>3</strong> <strong>years.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows Haab's striae and a child having buphthalmos in congenital glaucoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Vogt's striae</strong> are <strong>vertical</strong>, fine, whitish lines (folds/tears) in Descemet's membrane commonly found in patients with <strong>keratoconus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Vogt's striae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1031","text":"A patient presents to the ‌clinic for a change of he‍r presbyopic glasses. Thi​s is the third time that ​she is coming in 2 months​. What is the likely unde​rlying diagnosis in this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the clinic for a change of her presbyopic glasses. This is the third time that she is coming in 2 months. What is the likely underlying diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Senile ‌cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retinal d‌etachment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intumescen‌t cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Open-angl‌e glaucoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Open-angle glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Open-angle glaucoma</strong></p><p><strong>Frequent changes</strong> in <strong>presbyopic glasses </strong>in this patient is a symptom of<strong> open-angle glaucoma</strong> (OAG).</p>\n<p>This is due to increasing accommodative failure as a result of constant pressure on the ciliary muscle and nerve supply.</p>\n<p>In intumescent cataracts, patients report difficulty with distance vision and frequent change of spectacle prescription. However, it is not associated with frequent changes of presbyopic glasses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1032","text":"Normal cup-to-disc ratio i‌s up to_______.","raw_text":"Normal cup-to-disc ratio is up to_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0‌.4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0‌.3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.6</strong></p><p>Normal cup-to-disc ratio is<strong> up to 0.6</strong>. Although the average cup-to-disc ratio is around 0.3 to 0.4, a ratio of up to 0.6 may be found in healthy individuals. This larger ratio is frequently seen in cases of large discs, myopic discs or physiological cupping. A cup-to-disc ratio of &gt; 0.6 is suggestive of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">glaucoma</strong>.<br/><br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1033","text":"A 61-year-old man, who is ‌a known case of diabetes ‍mellitus, presents with h​eadache and eye pain. He ​also reports difficulty w​ith driving at night. Exa​mination showed a laminar‌ dot sign. What is the li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old man, who is a known case of diabetes mellitus, presents with headache and eye pain. He also reports difficulty with driving at night. Examination showed a laminar dot sign. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retinal d‌etachment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AR‌MD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glaucoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with headache and eye pain along with the finding of a <strong>laminar dot sign</strong> is suggestive of glaucoma.</p>\n<p>The laminar dot sign is an <strong>advanced</strong> <strong>glaucomatous</strong> change in the <strong>optic disc. </strong>This occurs because the pores in the lamina cribrosa are slit-shaped and are visible up to the margin of the disc.</p>\n<p>Glaucomatous changes in the optic disc</p>\n<p><strong>Early</strong> glaucomatous changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slit defects of the retinal nerve fiber layer</li>\n<li><strong>Splinter</strong> hemorrhages on disc margin</li>\n<li>Large cup - 0.7 or more</li>\n<li>Asymmetry of the cups: A difference equal to or more than 0.2 between two eyes is significant</li>\n<li>Vertically oval cup</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Advanced</strong> glaucomatous changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marked cupping (<strong>Beanpot</strong> cupping)</li>\n<li>Thinning of neuroretinal rim</li>\n<li><strong>Bayonetting</strong> sign - Sharp angulation of vessel at the margin of the excavated cup</li>\n<li>Laminar dot sign</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4527","text":"What is the last visual fi‌eld to be affected in gla‍ucoma?","raw_text":"What is the last visual field to be affected in glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na‌sal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infe‌rior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supe‌rior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Temp‌oral","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Temporal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Temporal</strong></p><p>The <strong>temporal visual field</strong> is the <strong>last field</strong> to be affected in glaucoma.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pattern</strong> of <strong>visual field progression</strong> in glaucoma from early to severe is predictable in light of the pattern of thinning of the retinal nerve fiber layer, which usually begins with the <strong>superior</strong> and <strong>inferior arcuate fibers,</strong> then the <strong>papillomacular bundle</strong> and finally, the<strong> nasal fibers</strong>.</p>\n<p>Since the nasal fibers are the last to be involved, the temporal field is the last to be affected in glaucoma. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1034","text":"Which of the following is ‌a late visual field defec‍t in open-angle glaucoma?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is a late visual field defect in open-angle glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Siedel ‌scotoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bjerrum‌ scotoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paracentr‌al scotoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Baring of ‌blind spot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bjerrum scotoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bjerrum scotoma</strong></p><p><strong>Bjerrum scotoma (arcuate)</strong> is a <strong>late</strong> and clinically significant visual field defect.  </p>\n<p>Progression of visual field changes seen in glaucoma:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 207px; width: 353px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 88px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.219px; height: 88px;\">Early non-specific changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.219px; height: 88px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Isopter contraction</strong></li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Baring</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> of a </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">blind spot:</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> Exclusion of blind spot from the central field</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 118px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.219px; height: 118px;\">Early significant changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.219px; height: 118px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Peripheral nasal step</strong> (may be early or late)</li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Paracentral scotoma </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">in Bjerrum's area.</span></li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Siedel’s  scotoma:</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> Paracentral scotoma joins the blind spot</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 128px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.219px; height: 128px;\">Late significant changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.219px; height: 128px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arcuate</strong> or <strong>Bjerrum scotoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Ring</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> or </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Double</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> arcuate scotoma: When 2 arcuate scotomas join together</span></li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Roenne’s central nasal step</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.219px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Advanced glaucomatous field defects</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.219px; height: 78px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tubular vision</strong></li>\n<li>No light perception: Complete visual field defect.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1035","text":"A 70-year-old patient pres‌ents with progressive det‍erioration of vision. The​ pupillary reaction is sl​uggish and the IOP is ele​vated. Fundoscopy shows a​ large and deep cup and t‌he visual field reveals p​aracentral scotoma. What ​is the first line of trea‌tment for this diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"A 70-year-old patient presents with progressive deterioration of vision. The pupillary reaction is sluggish and the IOP is elevated. Fundoscopy shows a large and deep cup and the visual field reveals paracentral scotoma. What is the first line of treatment for this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‌or","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Timolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Timolol</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of deterioration of vision, sluggish pupils, elevated IOP and <strong>paracentral scotoma </strong>is suggestive of <strong>primary open-angle glaucoma</strong> (POAG). <strong>Timolol</strong> is one of the first-line agents used in the treatment of primary open-angle glaucoma.</p>\n<p>First-line agents for the treatment of glaucoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prostaglandin</strong> analogs such as latanoprost and bimatoprost</li>\n<li>Topical <strong>beta-blockers</strong> such as timolol, betaxolol, and levobunolol </li>\n<li>Selective alpha 2 <strong>adrenergic agonist </strong>brimonidine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adjunctive therapy<strong>: </strong>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</p>\n<p>Laser trabeculoplasty: In cases where intraocular pressure (IOP) is uncontrolled despite maximal tolerated medical therapy.</p>\n<p>Note: Since prostaglandin (PG) analogs are not included in the options, the next best option to choose is timolol.</p>\n<p>Historically, beta-blockers were the most common first-line agents used in the medical treatment of POAG. However, with an increase in our experience with newer agents, specifically the <strong>prostaglandin analogues</strong> that facilitate outflow, they were recognized as being more efficacious in lowering IOP and systemically safer than beta-blockers. As a result, now they are the <strong>first-line agents</strong> in glaucoma treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1036","text":"A patient with a known cas‌e of MI on aspirin comes ‍with shortness of breath.​ She has an active histor​y of asthma and uses inha​lational bronchodilators,​ her last attack was 7 mo‌nths ago. She was recentl​y prescribed medications ​for increased intraocular‌ pressure. What is the li‍kely cause of her current​ symptoms?","raw_text":"\n\nA patient with a known case of MI on aspirin comes with shortness of breath. She has an active history of asthma and uses inhalational bronchodilators, her last attack was 7 months ago. She was recently prescribed medications for increased intraocular pressure. What is the likely cause of her current symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta‌xolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latan‌oprost","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Timolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Timolol</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">This patient with a history of <strong>asthma</strong> now presenting with <strong>shortness</strong> of <strong>breath</strong> after starting medications for increased intraocular pressure was most likely diagnosed with <strong>glaucoma.</strong> The drug she was likely prescribed was <strong>timolol,</strong> a non-selective beta blocker.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Timolol is a<strong> </strong>non-selective beta-blocker that is used in glaucoma<strong>. </strong>Non-selective beta-blockers can cause <strong>precipitation</strong> of <strong>bronchial</strong> asthma in susceptible individuals (beta-2 block on bronchi). They cause <strong>bronchospasms</strong> and are therefore <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with <strong>asthma </strong>(e.g. timolol, levobunolol). Ocular beta-blockers reduce intraocular pressure by <strong>decreasing aqueous formation </strong>by <strong>blockade</strong> of <strong>β<sub>2</sub> receptors. </strong>Timolol is specifically associated with <strong>allergic blepharoconjunctivitis</strong> and punctal <strong>stenosis</strong></p>\n<p>Side effects: Systemic: Bronchospasm, bradycardia, decreased cardiac output, hypotension, depression, impotence, and altered lipid profile.</p>\n<p>Contraindications: Asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, congestive heart failure, sinus bradycardia, and atrioventricular blocks</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The patient has been stable on aspirin for the past three years which is unlikely to be the cause of her current symptoms. </p>\n<p>Option B: Betaxolol is a beta 1 selective blocker that is used in glaucoma and has a lower propensity to precipitate bronchial asthma. </p>\n<p>Option C: Latanoprost is a prostaglandin (PGF2 alpha) analog and does not precipitate bronchial asthma. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1037","text":"As a first-year ophthalmol‌ogy resident, you have be‍en referred a case of gla​ucoma. He complaints that​ his current medication i​s causing excessive drows​iness and is interfering ‌with his work. Which of t​he following anti-glaucom​a medications can cause d‌rowsiness?","raw_text":"As a first-year ophthalmology resident, you have been referred a case of glaucoma. He complaints that his current medication is causing excessive drowsiness and is interfering with his work. Which of the following anti-glaucoma medications can cause drowsiness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Latan‌oprost","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brimo‌nidine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorzo‌lamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brimonidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brimonidine</strong></p><p><strong>Brimonidine</strong> is an anti-glaucoma medication that can cause drowsiness.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>selective alpha-2</strong> <strong>agonist</strong>. It can cross the blood-brain barrier and, hence, causes <strong>drowsiness</strong> and <strong>CNS depression</strong>; it is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>infants</strong> and <strong>children</strong>.</p>\n<p>A new class of glaucoma drugs, <strong>Rho kinase (ROCK) inhibitors</strong>, promises to act specifically on the trabecular meshwork, a main outflow and blockage site in glaucoma. <span class=\"caps\">ROCK </span>inhibitors are not yet approved and available for glaucoma patients. Two US companies, Aerie and Altheos, are currently in early clinical research development with topical <span class=\"caps\">ROCK </span>inhibitors to lower <span class=\"caps\">IOP.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1038","text":"A 55-year-old female, who ‌is a known case of sickle‍ cell anemia, comes to th​e casualty with a history​ of severe eye pain, redn​ess, and diminution of vi​sion. On examination, the‌ visual acuity is 6/60, a​nd there is circumcorneal​ congestion, corneal edem‌a, and a shallow anterior‍ chamber. What is the dru​g of choice from the give‍n options?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old female, who is a known case of sickle cell anemia, comes to the casualty with a history of severe eye pain, redness, and diminution of vision. On examination, the visual acuity is 6/60, and there is circumcorneal congestion, corneal edema, and a shallow anterior chamber. What is the drug of choice from the given options?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine‌ ointment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenou‌s mannitol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprofloxac‌in eye drops","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral acet‌azolamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intravenous mannitol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intravenous mannitol</strong></p><p>This patient with complaints of severe headache, corneal congestion, and edema with a shallow anterior chamber most likely has <strong>acute angle closure glaucoma.</strong> Since this patient has a past history of <strong>sickle cell disease,</strong> intravenous <strong>mannitol</strong> is the drug of choice for this patient</p>\n<p>Acetazolamide is contraindicated in cases of sickle-cell anemia since these drugs tend to increase sickling of erythrocytes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1039","text":"A 50-year-old female prese‌nts with an acute painful‍ red-eye. An image of her​ eye is shown below. What​ is the most likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old female presents with an acute painful red-eye. An image of her eye is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute retrobulbar neuritis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute angle-closure glauco‌ma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute anter‌ior uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe keratoconjunctiviti‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute angle-closure glaucoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7189dcfd86cd4d9fafa2ee458f9c3f04.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute angle-closure glaucoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, supported with the image of the <strong>mid-dilated and vertically oval </strong>pupil is suggestive of <strong>acute angle-closure glaucoma. </strong>The pupil is non-reactive to both light and accommodation.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute primary angle-closure glaucoma </strong>refers to the occlusion of the trabecular meshwork by the peripheral iris (iridotrabecular contact [ITC]), obstructing aqueous outflow.</p>\n<p>Angle closure can be <strong>primary,</strong> when it occurs in an <strong>anatomically predisposed eye</strong> or <strong>secondary</strong> to another <strong>ocular</strong> or <strong>systemic factor.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Classification:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 77px; width: 449px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.1042px;\"><strong>Primary angle-closure suspect (PACS)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 356.896px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gonioscopy shows posterior meshwork ITC in three or more quadrants but no peripheral anterior synechiae (PAS).</li>\n<li>Intraocular pressure (IOP), optic disc, and visual field are normal.</li>\n<li>No PAS are seen</li>\n<li>The risk of PACG at 5 years may be around 30%.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.1042px;\"><strong>Primary angle closure (PAC)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 356.896px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gonioscopy shows three or more quadrants of ITC with raised IOP and/or PAS or excessive pigment smudging on the TM.</li>\n<li>Normal optic disc and field.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.1042px;\"><strong>Primary angle-closure glaucoma (PACG)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 356.896px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ITC in three or more quadrants, with glaucomatous optic neuropathy.</li>\n<li>Optic nerve damage from an episode of severe IOP elevation, such as acute angle closure, may not appear as typical glaucomatous cupping.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Symptoms: </strong>Pain, redness, photophobia, impaired vision, watering</p>\n<p><strong>Signs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corneal edema- colored halos</li>\n<li>Conjunctiva- congestion, chemosis</li>\n<li>IOP- elevated 40–70 mm Hg</li>\n<li>AC very shallow</li>\n<li>Pupil- vertically oval, mid-dilated</li>\n<li>Angle- completely closed, Grade 0</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediate-</strong> Intravenous mannitol, intravenous acetazolamide followed by oral dose, topical beta-blockers, brimonidine, latanoprost</li>\n<li><strong>Definitive-</strong> Laser peripheral iridotomy, filtration surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1357","text":"A 36-year-old woman develo‌ps pain in the eye after ‍dark-room/prone-provocati​ve test. Which of the fol​lowing drugs should be av​oided?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman develops pain in the eye after dark-room/prone-provocative test. Which of the following drugs should be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atropine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atropine</strong></p><p>Pain after dark-room prone-provocative test indicates <strong>occludable angles. </strong>Hence, <strong>atropine</strong> is contraindicated as it is a long-acting <strong>mydriatic</strong> and can <strong>precipitate angle closure.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dark-room/prone-provocative test </strong>(DRPPT)<strong>- </strong>The patient sits in a dark room, face down for <strong>one hour without sleeping</strong> (sleep induces miosis). The intraocular pressure (IOP) is checked (immediately after the test, as IOP can normalize very rapidly), and an IOP rise of <strong>8 mmHg or more </strong>is frequently considered significant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1040","text":"A young female presents wi‌th sudden onset severe pa‍in in the left eye after ​watching a movie. She rus​hes to the hospital as th​ere is a progressive loss​ of vision. She has cilia‌ry injection and a shallo​w anterior chamber. The p​upil is semi dilated and ‌the cornea appears oedema‍tous. What is the procedu​re of choice for this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A young female presents with sudden onset severe pain in the left eye after watching a movie. She rushes to the hospital as there is a progressive loss of vision. She has ciliary injection and a shallow anterior chamber. The pupil is semi dilated and the cornea appears oedematous. What is the procedure of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laser i‌ridotomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trabecu‌lectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trabec‌ulotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trabecu‌loplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Laser iridotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Laser iridotomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>sudden onset severe pain</strong> in the eye, <strong>progressive loss</strong> of vision, <strong>shallow anterior chamber,</strong> semi-dilated pupil, and <strong>edematous cornea</strong> is suggestive of <strong>acute angle-closure glaucoma. </strong><strong>Laser iridotomy </strong>is the procedure of choice especially in emergency situations. </p>\n<p>The attack may have been precipitated by <strong>pupillary dilation</strong>, which may result from activities such as going to a movie theater. Laser iridotomy re-establishes the communication between the posterior and anterior chamber, bypassing the pupillary block; hence, it immediately relieves crowding of the angle.</p>\n<p>Various surgical and laser modalities used in glaucoma:</p>\n<p><strong>Angle-closure glaucoma: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laser iridotomy</strong> by Nd:Yag along with prophylactic iridotomy of the other eye → <strong>Treatment of choice</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trabeculectomy → Filtration surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Congenital glaucoma</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Goniotomy</li>\n<li>Trabeculotomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Primary open-angle glaucoma:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laser trabeculoplasty:</strong> Argon or diode (if not controlled medications)</li>\n<li>Trabeculectomy: Filtration surgery (if not controlled medications)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Absolute glaucoma</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyclocryotherapy</li>\n<li>Nd:Yag lasercyclo destruction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1041","text":"A 50-year-old male present‌ed with progressive blurr‍ed vision, photophobia, a​nd eye pain. On examinati​on, there was conjunctiva​l redness, a mid-dilated ​pupil, and a shallow ante‌rior chamber in the right​ eye. He has been treated​ both medically and surgi‌cally for the condition b‍ut showed no improvement.​ The following procedure ‍incorporates a device to ​aid drainage. Identify th‍e device.","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male presented with progressive blurred vision, photophobia, and eye pain. On examination, there was conjunctival redness, a mid-dilated pupil, and a shallow anterior chamber in the right eye. He has been treated both medically and surgically for the condition but showed no improvement. The following procedure incorporates a device to aid drainage. Identify the device.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ex-Press‌™ implant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ahmed glau‌coma valve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molteno‌ implant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Baerveld‌t implant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ex-Press™ implant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2299790c723c44bd96aa842fc91ff50f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ex-Press™ implant</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The clinical vignette points to a diagnosis of glaucoma not responsive to medical treatment and trabeculectomy and the device used is an <strong>ex-Press™ glaucoma implant</strong> (mini shunt).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is a form of <strong>minimally invasive glaucoma surgery</strong> (MIGS) to increase aqueous drainage.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 359\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Ex-PRESS glaucoma implant is a small <strong>stainless steel</strong> device. It is inserted under a scleral flap during a modified trabeculectomy. Complications such as hypotony are lower than with standard trabeculectomy. </p>\n<p><strong>Glaucoma drainage devices</strong> are devices that allow aqueous outflow by establishing communication between the anterior chamber and sub-Tennon's space/sub-conjunctival space. While these devices were traditionally reserved for high-risk patients, or after trabeculectomy had failed, they are becoming increasingly popular. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The commonly used devices are as follows:</p>\n<p>1. Shunts using episcleral explants</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Ahmed glaucoma valve: Silicone</span></li>\n<li>Molteno implant - Polypropylene plate and silicone plate</li>\n<li>Baerveldt implant - Silicone </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">2. Mini-shunts </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ex-Press™ Mini-Shunt: Stainless steel</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">iStent® - Titanium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1042","text":"What is Gla‌ukomflecken?","raw_text":"What is Glaukomflecken?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute uveitis due to glauc‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lens opacity due to glauco‌ma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retinal detachment due to ‌glaucoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal opacity due to gla‌ucoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lens opacity due to glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lens opacity due to glaucoma</strong></p><p>Glaukomflecken is <strong>anterior subcapsular lenticular</strong> opacity and a component of <strong>Vogt's triad</strong> seen in patients after an acute angle closure glaucoma attack. </p>\n<p>The Vogt's triad comprises of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glaukomflecken</li>\n<li>Patches of iris atrophy</li>\n<li>Slightly dilated nonreactive pupil</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1361","text":"A 25-year-old patient pres‌ents with painless white ‍eye with an intraocular p​ressure of 60 mmHg. What ​is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient presents with painless white eye with an intraocular pressure of 60 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic p‌apilledema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute angle-closure glauco‌ma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glaucomatocy‌clitic crisis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute anter‌ior uveitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glaucomatocyclitic crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glaucomatocyclitic crisis</strong></p><p>The given scenario fits the description of <strong>glaucomatocyclitic crisis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Glaucomatocyclitic crisis/<strong>Posner–Schlossman</strong> Syndrome [PSS]) describes recurrent attacks of unilateral <strong>acute raised intraocular</strong> pressure (IOP) associated with mild anterior uveitis. The mechanism is thought to be acute trabeculitis, and there is evidence that infection, possibly cytomegalovirus (CMV) or <em>Helicobacter pylori</em>, may play a role. PSS typically affects young to middle-aged adults. Males are affected more frequently than females.</p>\n<p>Signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slit-lamp biomicroscopy typically shows a few anterior chamber cells and one to several fine white central keratic precipitates.</li>\n<li>Mydriasis is common; posterior synechiae are not a feature.</li>\n<li>IOP is typically raised to over 40 mmHg, out of proportion to iritis severity.</li>\n<li>Gonioscopy shows an open-angle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Topical steroids are used to control inflammation, with aqueous suppressants to lower the IOP.</li>\n<li>Topical or oral non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents may also be beneficial.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> In PSS, the rise in IOP is out of proportion to the pain felt. There is often a mild discomfort and pain may even be absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1043","text":"An ophthalmologist evaluat‌ing a patient with glauco‍ma identifies the followi​ng finding on slit-lamp e​xamination. What is the f​inding?","raw_text":"An ophthalmologist evaluating a patient with glaucoma identifies the following finding on slit-lamp examination. What is the finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferry‌ line","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fleisch‌er ring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hudson-St‌ahli line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Krukenber‌g spindles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Krukenberg spindles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a02ae8e565884246a5f40b91cdc80d28.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Krukenberg spindles</strong></p><p>The given image shows a pattern of the deposition of pigments on the back of the cornea in <strong>pigmentary glaucoma</strong>. They are called <strong>Krukenberg spindles</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Pigmentary glaucoma is an open-angle glaucoma having the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Krukenberg spindles</li>\n<li>Gonioscopy shows <strong>pigment accumulation</strong> along the <strong>Schwalbe line</strong> inferiorly, and this accumulation is called the <strong>Sampaolesi line</strong><strong>.</strong></li>\n<li><strong><strong>Radial transillumination defect </strong></strong>in the iris is a pathognomonic feature.</li>\n<li>Pigment granules may be deposited on the anterior surface. There may be a <strong>Scheie</strong> line or <strong>Zentmayer</strong> ring of pigment on the peripheral/equatorial surface around the zonular insertions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Ferry</strong> <strong>line:</strong> Corneal epithelial iron line at the edge of <strong>filtering blebs</strong> of glaucoma surgery</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fleischer line/ring: </strong>Epithelial iron deposits at the base of the cone in <strong>keratoconus</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hudson–Stahli line: </strong>Iron deposition line in the corneal epithelium, which is commonly seen in the junction between middle and lower third cornea. This finding is mainly age-related.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1359","text":"Malignant glaucoma is seen‌ in __________.","raw_text":"Malignant glaucoma is seen in __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After intra-ocular surgery‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intraocular‌ malignancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central retinal arterial o‌cclusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. After intra-ocular surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. After intra-ocular surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Malignant glaucoma</strong> is seen after <strong>intra-ocular surgery </strong>(usually after trabeculectomy).</p>\n<p><strong>Ciliary block</strong> glaucoma is also called <strong>malignant</strong> glaucoma/inverse glaucoma. It can be seen after any intraocular surgery. Forward flow of the aqueous is blocked as tips of ciliary processes rotate forward and press against the lens/intraocular lens (IOL)/anterior hyaloid of the vitreous. Hence, aqueous humor starts collecting in the vitreous cavity. </p>\n<p>Signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High intraocular pressure (IOP) and absent bleb.</li>\n<li>Negative Seidel test.</li>\n<li>Shallow anterior chamber (AC)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Initial</strong> treatment is with <strong>cycloplegics</strong> (atropine 1%) to <strong>dilate</strong> the <strong>ciliary ring</strong> and push the vitreous-lens-iris posteriorly.</li>\n<li><strong>Subsequent</strong> treatment if medical therapy fails is with <strong>Nd:YAG laser</strong> hyaloidotomy.</li>\n<li>Pars plana vitrectomy can be considered in conditions that don't respond to the above treatment modalities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1045","text":"What is iridocorneal endot‌helial syndrome associate‍d with?","raw_text":"What is iridocorneal endothelial syndrome associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Progressive atrophy of the‌ iris stroma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral stromal edema of‌ the iris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary angle-closure glau‌coma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deposition of glycosaminog‌lycans in Descemet's memb‍rane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Progressive atrophy of the iris stroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Progressive atrophy of the iris stroma</strong></p><p><strong>Progressive atrophy</strong> of <strong>iris stroma </strong>is a clinical entity of iridocorneal endothelial syndrome (ICE).</p>\n<p>ICE syndrome is a <strong>proliferative endotheliopathy, </strong>which leads to <strong>secondary angle-closure glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p>It includes three clinical entities:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Progressive iris atrophy</li>\n<li><strong>Chandler’s syndrome -</strong> corneal edema</li>\n<li><strong>Cogan–Reese syndrome -</strong> nodules and nevus on the iris; hence, it is known as<strong> iris nevus syndrome</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1363","text":"What is seen in hypersecre‌tory glaucoma?","raw_text":"What is seen in hypersecretory glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marfans ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidemi‌c dropsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epidemic dropsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epidemic dropsy</strong></p><p><strong>Hypersecretory glaucoma</strong> is associated with <strong>epidemic dropsy.</strong></p>\n<p>Epidemic dropsy occurs due to <strong><em>Argemone mexicana</em> </strong>poisoning. Here, glaucoma is due to excessive production of aqueous humor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3424","text":"100-day glaucoma is seen i‌n which of the following ‍conditions?","raw_text":"100-day glaucoma is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central retinal vein occlu‌sion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eale's ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central retinal artery occ‌lusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Steroid-indu‌ced glaucoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Central retinal vein occlusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a93e3c6869d64c888e8ca058355b477ex720x688.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93df537a43d548da9179ee53b55146fex720x515.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Central retinal vein occlusion</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>100-day glaucoma</strong> is a <strong>neovascular glaucoma</strong> that occurs in <strong>ischemic</strong> type of <strong>central retinal vein occlusion</strong> (CRVO).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is<strong> </strong>known as 100-day glaucoma because it occurs 3 months after the episode of CRVO. Although <strong>neovascular glaucoma</strong> occurs as a <strong>complication</strong> in <strong>Eale's disease </strong>(Option B) and <strong>central retinal artery occlusion</strong> (Option C) and long-term use of topical steroid therapy causes <strong>steroid-induced glaucoma </strong>(Option D) none of them are termed as 100-day glaucoma.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">CRVO is further classified into two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Non-ischemic</strong> CRVO: occurs secondary to <strong>venous stasis</strong>.It is the most common type of CRVO of the two.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Ischemic</strong> CRVO: occurs due to acute <strong>occlusion</strong> of the central retinal vein. It is also termed as <strong>hemorrhagic retinopathy</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ischemic CRVO presents with sudden loss of vision and <strong>Relative afferent pupil defect</strong>(RAPD).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Fundus examination</strong> shows massive enlargement of retinal veins and massive retinal hemorrhages giving a \"<strong>splashed tomato appearance</strong>\". Other findings include cotton wool spots, optic disc edema and neovascularization at the disc and in the periphery.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Pathagnomic</strong> features of <strong>ischemic</strong> CRVO that differentiates it from non-ischemic CRVO are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">RAPD</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Reduced amplitude of B-wave on electroretinogram</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Visual field defects</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Complications of the disease:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 286\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Neovascular Glaucoma known as <strong>100 day glaucoma</strong></li>\n<li>Neovascularisation of iris known as <strong>Rubeosis iridis</strong></li>\n<li>Vitreous hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Proliferative retinopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below-given fundus image is of the \"splashed tomato\" appearance of CRVO. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Eales</strong> disease is an idiopathic <strong>inflammatory</strong> disease of the <strong>peripheral retinal veins</strong>.It is considered to be a <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> reaction to the <strong>tubercular proteins</strong> and it is commonly seen in <strong>young males</strong>. It is characterized by <strong>recurrent vitreous hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 282\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Neovascular glaucoma</li>\n<li>Proliferative vitreoretinopathy</li>\n<li>Tractional retinal detachment,</li>\n<li>Rubeosis iridis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: Central retinal artery occlusion(CRAO)<strong> </strong>is most often caused due to an embolus from an atherosclerotic plaque. It presents as sudden painless loss of vision and <strong>amaurosis fugax </strong>(transient monocular blindness that is due to ischemia). Fundus examination shows an opaque, milky-white retina with a <strong>cherry-red spot.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neovascular glaucoma</strong> may occur as a delayed complication of CRAO but it is <strong>not termed</strong> as 100 day glaucoma.</p>\n<p>The below image depicts the fundus image seen in CRAO with the cherry red spot.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong>Steroid induced glaucoma </strong>is a type of secondary open angle glaucoma that usually follows weeks-months of <strong>topical steroid therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6733","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g the progression of ocul​ar hypertension to primar​y open-angle glaucoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the progression of ocular hypertension to primary open-angle glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reducing IOP is effective ‌in delaying the onset of ‍POAG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thin cornea is a risk fact‌or for POAG development","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family history is not a ri‌sk factor for POAG develo‍pment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased cup disc ratio i‌s a risk factor for POAG ‍development","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Family history is not a risk factor for POAG development","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Family history is not a risk factor for POAG development</strong></p><p><strong>Family history</strong> is a risk factor for the development of <strong>primary open-angle glaucoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ocular hypertension (OHT)</strong> is characterized by <strong>only raised intraocular pressure (IOP)</strong>. Optic disc and normal visual field are normal, with no evidence of angle closure.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for the progression of ocular hypertension to primary open-angle glaucoma include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>African American race</li>\n<li>Thin cornea</li>\n<li>Increasing age</li>\n<li>Increased cup-disc ratio</li>\n<li>Increased IOP</li>\n<li>Family history of POAG</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6734","text":"Identify the instrument gi‌ven in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument given in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Express‌ implant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonio‌scope","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Baerveld‌t implant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ozurdex‌ implant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Baerveldt implant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c20249fdcff54aacb7751e3b0770faa7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Baerveldt implant</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Baerveldt implant</strong> which is a glaucoma drainage device (GDD)/seton/aqueous shunt.</p>\n<p>It consists of a scleral plate that is sutured to the sclera and a tube connecting it into the anterior chamber. It is valveless, and hence, it carries the risk of postoperative hypotony due to excessive aqueous drainage.</p>\n<p><strong>Glaucoma drainage devices</strong> are devices that allow aqueous outflow by establishing a communication between the anterior chamber and sub-Tennon's space/sub-conjunctival space. The glaucoma valve implant is also known as a <strong>seton</strong>.</p>\n<p>The commonly used devices are as follows:</p>\n<p>1. Shunts using episcleral explants</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ahmed glaucoma valve: Silicone</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Molteno implant- Polypropelene plate and silicone plate</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Baerveldt implant- Silicone </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">2. Mini-shunts </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ex-Press™ Mini-Shunt: Stainless steel </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">iStent®- Titanium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6735","text":"What is the drug of choice‌ for inverse glaucoma?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for inverse glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostagland‌in analogues","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atropine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atropine</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for <strong>inverse glaucoma</strong> is <strong>atropine.</strong></p>\n<p>Inverse glaucoma is a type of <strong>secondary angle-closure glaucoma</strong> caused by the flow of <strong>aqueous humour</strong> posteriorly to the<strong> posterior segment</strong> instead of the posterior chamber. The increased pressure in the posterior segment pushes the <strong>lens forward</strong> leading to the shallowing of the anterior chamber.</p>\n<p><strong>Miotics </strong>are<strong> contraindicated</strong> as they can cause the ciliary zonules to slack, the lens to move forward and further shallowing of the anterior chamber. So <strong>mydriatic</strong> and <strong>cycloplegic agents</strong> such as atropine are administered.</p>\n<p>Inverse glaucoma is also called:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignant glaucoma</li>\n<li>Ciliary block glaucoma</li>\n<li>Aqueous misdirection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Pilocarpine</strong> is the drug of choice in <strong>angle-closure glaucoma</strong> but is contraindicated in inverse glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Whereas, <strong>atropine</strong> is the drug of choice in <strong>inverse glaucoma </strong>but is contraindicated in angle-closure glaucoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6736","text":"An elderly male who is a k‌nown case of diabetes pre‍sents with blurry vision ​near the center of his fi​eld of vision. He also in​forms that colours appear​ washed out or faded. His‌ IOP was found to be elev​ated. Which of the follow​ing drugs should not be u‌sed to control IOP in thi‍s diabetic patient?","raw_text":"An elderly male who is a known case of diabetes presents with blurry vision near the center of his field of vision. He also informs that colours appear washed out or faded. His IOP was found to be elevated. Which of the following drugs should not be used to control IOP in this diabetic patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostagland‌in analogue","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dorzo‌lamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alpha a‌gonists","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prostaglandin analogue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prostaglandin analogue</strong></p><p>This diabetic patient with new onset central loss of vision and reports of colors appearing faded or washed out most likely has macular edema. <strong>Prostaglandin analogs</strong> have a high propensity to cause and further exacerbate cystoid macular edema and hence are not preferred in this patient. </p>\n<p><strong>Drugs associated with cystoid macular edema</strong> (CME):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prostaglandin analogues</li>\n<li>Epinephrine</li>\n<li>Dipivefrine (only the non-selective alpha agonists, in aphakic eyes)</li>\n<li>Tamoxifen</li>\n<li>Timolol</li>\n<li>Docetaxel</li>\n<li>Interferons</li>\n<li>Fingolimod</li>\n<li>Thiazolinediones</li>\n<li>Niacin (pseudo-CME)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6737","text":"In a 55-year-old patient p‌resenting with secondary ‍open-angle glaucoma, what​ is the most likely etiol​ogy?","raw_text":"In a 55-year-old patient presenting with secondary open-angle glaucoma, what is the most likely etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phacolyti‌c glaucoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudo exfoliation syndrom‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pigment dispersion syndrom‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inflammato‌ry glaucoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudo exfoliation syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudo exfoliation syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudoexfoliation (PEX) syndrome</strong> is the most likely cause of secondary open-angle glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Pseudoexfoliation syndrome is an ocular manifestation of an <strong>age-related systemic disorder</strong> of the <strong>extracellular matrix</strong>, characterized by the production and progressive accumulation of fibrillar extracellular material in many ocular and systemic tissues. The clinical hallmark of PEX syndrome is the<strong> deposition</strong> of <strong>whitish abnormal pseudoexfoliative debris</strong> on the anterior lens capsule. </p>\n<p>A deposition is also noted on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Zonules (causing zonulolysis and phacodonesis, lens subluxation)</li>\n<li>Pupillary margin</li>\n<li>Trabecular meshwork (causing the secondary open-angle glaucoma)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0660","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs increases the uveoscle‍ral outflow?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs increases the uveoscleral outflow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Latan‌oprost","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‌or","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Latanoprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Latanoprost</strong></p><p><strong>Latanoprost</strong> is a <strong>prostaglandin</strong> drug, which acts by <strong>increasing</strong> the <strong>uveoscleral outflow</strong> and thereby <strong>lowers</strong> the <strong>intraocular pressure. </strong></p>\n<p>Mechanism of action: Latanoprost is a prodrug and gets activated during its passage through the cornea. It acts by stimulating the prostanoid receptors on the ciliary muscles<strong>. </strong>This leads to the release of matrix metalloproteinases, which increases the uveoscleral outflow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6948","text":"True regarding POAG: A. Ab‌normality of the trabecul‍ar meshwork is seen on go​nioscopy B. Family member​s are likely to develop s​teroid responsiveness. C.​ Dilatation of the pupil ‌is associated with exacer​bation of IOP D. First de​gree relatives are at 1% ‌increased risk of POAG E.‍ Visual field defect can ​exist with normal C:D rat‍io","raw_text":"<p>True regarding POAG:</p>\n<p>A. Abnormality of the trabecular meshwork is seen on gonioscopy<br/>B. Family members are likely to develop steroid responsiveness.<br/>C. Dilatation of the pupil is associated with exacerbation of IOP<br/>D. First degree relatives are at 1% increased risk of POAG<br/>E. Visual field defect can exist with normal C:D ratio</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A,B, D‌ and E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A, B ‌and C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B and ‌E only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"B, C ‌and D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. B and E only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. B and E only</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In<strong> primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG)</strong>, family members are likely to develop steroid responsiveness (statement B) and visual field defect can exist with a normal C:D ratio (statement E).</p>\n<p>Statement A: Gonioscopy <strong>cannot visualize</strong> the <strong>specific abnormalities</strong> that occur in the trabecular meshwork in POAG. Gonioscopy <strong>diagnoses POAG</strong> because of the <strong>visibility of all</strong> the <strong>structures</strong> of the angle of the anterior chamber in contrast to the non-visibility of different structures in angle closure glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Statement C: Primary <strong>angle-closure</strong> glaucoma is associated with exacerbation of IOP with pupillary dilatation and not POAG.</p>\n<p>Statement D: There is a <strong>10% increased risk</strong> of POAG in <strong>siblings</strong> and a <strong>4% increased</strong> risk in <strong>children</strong> of patients with POAG. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>POAG</strong> also known as <strong>chronic simple glaucoma</strong> of adult-onset is characterized by the presence of any 2 of the following 3 categories of changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Increased intraocular pressure:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">IOP &gt;21mmHg on more than one occasion, and/or</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Circadian variation in IOP of &gt;8 mmHg, or</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Asymmetry in IOP &gt; 5 mmHg between both eyes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Optic nerve head changes</strong> suggestive of glaucomatous damage. These may include one or more of the following changes:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Vertically oval cup</li>\n<li>Cup-disc (C:D) ratio <strong>&gt; 0.6</strong> (average cup: disc ratio 0.3-0.4)</li>\n<li>Asymmetry in C:D ratio &gt; 0.2 between both eyes.</li>\n<li>Narrowing, notching, or pallor of the neuroretinal rim.</li>\n<li>Disc haemorrhages</li>\n<li>Nasal shifting of retinal vessels-Bayonetting sign</li>\n<li>Lamellar dot sign</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Visual field changes -</strong> isopter contraction, barring of blind spot, paracentral scotoma, Seidel's scotoma, Bjerrum's scotoma, double arcuate scotoma, and Roenne's central nasal step.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A patient presenting with just raised IOP and visual field defects with normal optic nerve head findings such as a normal C:D ratio can still be diagnosed as POAG (statement E).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8382","text":"A 48-year-old lady was sta‌rted on an ocular medicat‍ion that she was instruct​ed to take daily. She dev​elops redness of the eye.​ Examination reveals a co​lour change of the iris a‌nd orbital fat atrophy. W​hich of the following med​ications was she likely p‌rescribed?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old lady was started on an ocular medication that she was instructed to take daily. She develops redness of the eye. Examination reveals a colour change of the iris and orbital fat atrophy. Which of the following medications was she likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latan‌oprost","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Scopo‌lamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Latanoprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Latanoprost</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of a patient that has <strong>iris pigmentation</strong> and <strong>redness</strong> of the eye, was most likely started on <strong>latanoprost.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 413px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Drug</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276px;\">Side Effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Latanoprost</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276px;\">\n<p>Allergic conjunctival hyperemia, permanent increase in iris pigmentation, orbital fat atrophy.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Timolol </td>\n<td style=\"width: 276px;\">Exacerbation of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, bradycardia, depression, impotence </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Scopolamine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276px;\">Photosensitivity, blurred vision </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Pilocarpine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276px;\">Corneal edema, miosis, induced myopia, decreased vision, brow ache, retinal detachment </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1358","text":"First drug to be given in ‌acute angle-closure glauc‍oma is _________.","raw_text":"First drug to be given in acute angle-closure glaucoma is _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tim‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piloc‌arpine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acetazolamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acetazolamide</strong></p><p>The <strong>first drug</strong> to be given in acute angle-closure glaucoma is <strong>oral acetazolamide</strong> or <strong>intravenous mannitol.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Acetazolamide</strong> reduces IOP by <strong>decreasing</strong> the production of <strong>aqueous.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>definitive drug</strong> is <strong>pilocarpine</strong> as it constricts the pupil and breaks the attack.</p>\n<p>Finally, when the corneal edema resolves, the patient has to undergo laser iridotomy to prevent such attacks in the future. So, <strong>laser iridotomy</strong> is the <strong>definitive management</strong> for acute angle-closure glaucoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1048","text":"The normal extent of the v‌isual field superiorly is‍ ________.","raw_text":"The normal extent of the visual field superiorly is ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6‌0°","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‌0°","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7‌0°","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9‌0°","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50°","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50°</strong></p><p>The extent of visual field superiorly is <strong>50°</strong></p>\n<p>Normal extent of the visual field: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Temporal: 90°/100°</li>\n<li>Inferiorly: 70°</li>\n<li>Nasal: 60°</li>\n<li>Superiorly: 50°</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> According to Parson's 22<sup>nd</sup> Ed., Page no. 101, the visual field extends 60<sup>o </sup>superiorly. However, most journals and other standard textbooks consider the answer to be 50<sup>o</sup>. Hence, we choose 50<sup>o </sup>as the most appropriate answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oCSOYj4H","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"13_regional_conditions_of_spine","title":"13.Regional Conditions Of Spine","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2196","text":"A 63-year-old woman presen​ted with low back pain an‍d back stiffness. An X-ra​y spine was done which is‌ shown below. Which of th‍e following statements is‌ true regarding the condi‌tion she is suffering fro‍m?","raw_text":"A 63-year-old woman presented with low back pain and back stiffness. An X-ray spine was done which is shown below. Which of the following statements is true regarding the condition she is suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It does not require medica​l intervention","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral curve &amp;gt;10 degre​es is seen in this condit‍ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It does not affect the fun​ctioning of vital organs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is rarel​y idiopathic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral curve >10 degrees is seen in this condition","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4c6979ea10134809a38a3b7f406290ef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral curve &gt;10 degrees is seen in this condition</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray are suggestive of <strong>scoliosis</strong>. In this condition, the<strong> lateral curvature</strong> of the spine is <strong>&gt; 10 degrees </strong>in the coronal plane, in the upright position.</p>\n<p>Scoliosis deranges the load and force transmission mechanism through the spine. It is most <strong>commonly</strong> an <strong>idiopathic</strong> condition. It <strong>jeopardizes</strong> the functioning of <strong>vital organs</strong> like the lungs and heart through overcrowding of ribs.</p>\n<p><strong>Medical intervention</strong> is indicated if the <strong>lateral curvature</strong> is <strong>&gt;25 degrees</strong>. Various modalities include <strong>brace</strong> application and <strong>surgical correction</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: If the <strong>lateral curve</strong> of the spine is <strong>&lt;</strong> <strong>10</strong> <strong>degrees</strong> then it is known as<strong> spinal asymmetry</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2199","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of scoliosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of scoliosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuromuscul​ar scoliosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sciatic ​scoliosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idiopathic​ scoliosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital​ scoliosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Idiopathic scoliosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Idiopathic scoliosis</strong></p><p><strong>Idiopathic scoliosis</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>type of scoliosis. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><strong>Scoliosis</strong> may be <strong>classified</strong> as:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li2\"><strong>Idiopathic </strong>scoliosis - No obvious cause is detected. Several genetic associations have been noted</li>\n<li class=\"li2\"><strong>Congenital </strong>scoliosis - Caused due to failure in vertebra formation or segmentation during development</li>\n<li class=\"li2\"><strong>Neuromuscular </strong>scoliosis<strong> - </strong>Caused due to neuromuscular disorders like cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy, etc.</li>\n<li class=\"li2\">Scoliosis due to generalized diseases - Seen in conditions such as:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li2\">Neurofibromatosis</li>\n<li class=\"li2\">Marfan syndrome</li>\n<li class=\"li2\">Bone dysplasia</li>\n<li class=\"li2\">Tumors</li>\n<li class=\"li2\">Due to irradiation </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2197","text":"Which of the following is ​not seen in non-structura‍l scoliosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in non-structural scoliosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contracture​ of the hip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lumbar dis​c prolapse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rotated ​vertebrae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Shor​t leg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rotated vertebrae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rotated vertebrae</strong></p><p><strong>Rotated vertebrae</strong> are seen in <strong>structural</strong> scoliosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Scoliosis</strong> is classified into <strong>2 types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Structural </strong>scoliosis - There is a <strong>non-correctable deformity</strong> due to vertebral rotation in the affected spinal segments </li>\n<li><strong>Postural </strong>scoliosis (non-structural) - \n<ul>\n<li>The deformity is secondary to a condition outside the spine</li>\n<li>Seen in conditions such as:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Short leg</strong>:<strong> </strong>curvature disappears when the patient is made to sit)</li>\n<li><strong>Hip contracture</strong> with pelvic tilt: disappears after the disc prolapse is managed)</li>\n<li><strong>Lumbar disc prolapse</strong> (sciatic scoliosis)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If scoliosis is due to poor posture, the deformity disappears when the person is asked to bend forward   </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2231","text":"The following test is perf​ormed on a patient being ‍evaluated for scoliosis. ​Identify this test.","raw_text":"The following test is performed on a patient being evaluated for scoliosis. Identify this test.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adam’​s test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schoebe​r’s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gerber​'s test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"McCarth​y's test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adam’s test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8980a36a16a549b78d0571d645f14340.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d47f0542f04847db8d7f6ab8f2bd24acx1200x1956.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adam’s test</strong></p><p>The test shown in the image is <strong>Adam's forward bending test.</strong> This is the <strong>best clinical test</strong> for evaluating spinal curvature in <strong>scoliosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Steps</strong> of Adam's test :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patient is asked to bend forward at the waist until the spine is at the horizontal level</li>\n<li>Knees must be maintained straight, with the feet together, the arms dependent, and the palms in opposition</li>\n<li>Examiner observes the trunk from behind (to assess midthoracic and lumbar rotation) and from the front (to assess upper thoracic rotation)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is used to differentiate between structural and non-structural curves in scoliosis. On bending forward, <strong>non-structural scoliosis disappears</strong>, while <strong>structural scoliosis persists</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2203","text":"Which is the most common t​ype of idiopathic scolios‍is?","raw_text":"Which is the most common type of idiopathic scoliosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adole​scent","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Juve​nile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infa​ntile","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ad​ult","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adolescent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adolescent</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of idiopathic scoliosis is <strong>adolescent idiopathic scoliosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Based on the age of onset, <strong>idiopathic scoliosis</strong> is subclassified into <strong>3 types </strong>as given below.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 1074px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\"><strong> Infantile scoliosis   </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 313px;\"><strong>Juvenile idiopathic scoliosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 303px;\"><strong>Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Age of onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Birth to 3 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 313px;\">4 to 9 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303px;\">10 years to skeletal maturity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Commonly seen curvature </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Left thoracic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 313px;\">Right thoracic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 303px;\">Right thoracic </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Self resolution</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>Resolves in 90%</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 313px;\">Resolves in 20%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303px;\">Rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Surgical management</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">\n<p>&lt;8 yr: Instrumentation without fusion</p>\n<p>After 8 yr: ASF-PSF</p>\n<p>After 11 yr: PSF</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 313px;\">\n<p>&lt;8 yr: Instrumentation without fusion</p>\n<p>After 8 yr: ASF-PSF</p>\n<p>After 11 yr: PSF</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303px;\">\n<p>PSF with instrumentation</p>\n<p>ASF if &lt; 11 yrs with open triradiate cartilage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> Note: ASF - Anterior spinal fusion, PSF - Posterior spinal fusion</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0239","text":"What is the type of scolio​sis shown in the X-ray gi‍ven below?","raw_text":"What is the type of scoliosis shown in the X-ray given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital​ scoliosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Postural ​scoliosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infantile​ scoliosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Degenerativ​e scoliosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Congenital scoliosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ed0c8fd93cb4adab7506cda10a42bb4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d6d28f7f69c4d0bb2bd7e185cbcbf26x570x616.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Congenital scoliosis</strong></p><p>In the given radiograph, the presence of a <strong>single hemivertebra at T9 </strong>and <strong>concavity to the right in the lower thoracic region</strong> is suggestive of <strong>congenital scoliosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Congenital scoliosis is a <strong>lateral curvature of the spine</strong> which is always associated with a <strong>radiologically demonstrable anomaly of the vertebral bodies</strong>, which includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hemivertebrae</strong> (where only one-half of the vertebra grows) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Block vertebrae </strong>(where two vertebral bodies fuse)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>An unsegmented bar</strong> (a bone bar joining two adjacent vertebrae on one side, which hinders growth on that side)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a unilateral and unsegmented bar with contralateral hemivertebra: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In infantile scoliosis (option C), most curves are thoracic with convexity to the left and the anomalies described above are absent. </p>\n<p>Note: The most progressive type of congenital scoliosis is a concave, unilateral unsegmented bar with a convex hemivertebra.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2213","text":"You are asked to measure t​he degree of lateral curv‍ature present in a patien​t with scoliosis using th‌e method depicted below. ‍Identify this method.","raw_text":"You are asked to measure the degree of lateral curvature present in a patient with scoliosis using the method depicted below. Identify this method.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nash and M​oe's method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perdriolle and Vidal metho​d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cobb's​ method","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stokes​ method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cobb's method","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f95dafac667946b2975d39b8c104f7c9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea05df2679d242ed9bc70859cb7133d0x1280x1703.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cobb&#x27;s method</strong></p><p>In the given X-ray of a patient with scoliosis, the degree of lateral curvature is measured using <strong>Cobb's method.</strong> The <strong>degree of curvature of scoliosis</strong> on plain radiographs is measured using <strong>Cobb's angle.</strong></p>\n<p>To measure the Cobbs's angle, first locate the superior-end vertebra and inferior-end vertebra of the curve. Then draw <strong>perpendicular lines</strong> to the<strong> superior surface</strong> <strong>of</strong> the <strong>superior-end</strong> vertebra and to the <strong>inferior surface</strong> <strong>of</strong> the <strong>inferior-end</strong> vertebra.</p>\n<p>The <strong>angle</strong> formed at the <strong>intersection of</strong> these <strong>perpendiculars</strong> is the angle of the spinal curve known as <strong>Cobb's angle</strong>. It can also be measured by measuring the angle made by the intersection of the end-plate lines. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Nash and Moe's method, Perdriolle and Vidal method, and Stokes method are methods of assessing vertebral rotation. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2218","text":"Risser's sign evaluates sk​eletal maturation using t‍he ossification of ______​_.","raw_text":"Risser's sign evaluates skeletal maturation using the ossification of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iliac a​pophysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Olecranon​ apophysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triradiate​ cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tibial tubercule apophysis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Iliac apophysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2df2aea2d73f4db1b519218e4ffe4086x1280x1403.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Iliac apophysis</strong></p><p><strong>Risser's sign</strong> evaluates skeletal maturation using the <strong>ossification</strong> of the<strong> iliac apophysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Risser's sign is used to <strong>predict</strong> the <strong>worsening </strong>of <strong>scoliosis</strong> and in the planning of corrective surgery.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>iliac apophysis</strong> is divided into <strong>four quadrants</strong>. The <strong>ossification</strong> of iliac apophyses normally proceeds from <strong>lateral to </strong>the<strong> medial</strong> side i.e., from the anterior superior iliac spine to the posterior superior iliac spine.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The Risser sign progresses from <strong>grade 0</strong> (<strong>no ossification</strong>) to <strong>grade 4</strong> (<strong>all four</strong> quadrants have <strong>ossified</strong>). When the apophysis has fused completely with the ilium, it is <strong>Risser grade 5</strong> and the patient is considered <strong>skeletally mature</strong>.  </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Risser's grade 5 (fusion with the ilium) coincides with the fusion of the apophysis in the vertebral ring. After this, there is no possibility of further progression of the scoliosis curve. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2227","text":"Which of the following is ​not an unfavorable progno‍stic factor for idiopathi​c scoliosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an unfavorable prognostic factor for idiopathic scoliosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of ​osteoporosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Male ​gender","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Previous l​aminectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of thoracic curve​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Male gender","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Male gender</strong></p><p><strong>Females</strong> are <strong>most commonly</strong> <strong>affected</strong> with idiopathic scoliosis, and female gender is a <strong>poor prognostic</strong> factor.</p>\n<p><strong>Unfavorable prognostic factors in scoliosis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital or juvenile or paralytic type</li>\n<li>Curve greater than 20-40 degrees</li>\n<li><strong>Thoracic curve</strong></li>\n<li>2-4 degrees of rotation</li>\n<li>Immature skeleton (Risser's sign 0)</li>\n<li>Female gender</li>\n<li>Presentation before menarche</li>\n<li><strong>Osteoporosis</strong></li>\n<li>Rapid increase in the size of curves</li>\n<li>Single short curve</li>\n<li><strong>Previous</strong> discectomy,<strong> laminectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2221","text":"A patient with scoliosis w​as prescribed the given b‍race. What is this brace ​known as?","raw_text":"A patient with scoliosis was prescribed the given brace. What is this brace known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Milwauk​ee brace","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Boston​ brace","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"SOMI ​brace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ASHE ​brace","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Boston brace","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/db7ee8cfb446454699faa9d64f02ac57.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ff3f99dc75d41f18a7651fa5d8d650cx1280x1280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfddfcc440c648c6a81257139b66bce5x1200x1783.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Boston brace</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>Boston brace </strong>that is<strong> </strong>used in <strong>scoliosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Boston brace is a type of thoraco-lumbo-sacral-orthosis (<strong>TLSO brace</strong>). It has a <strong>hard polypropylene outer shell</strong> with a soft foam lining inside. It is used in patients whose scoliosis <strong>curve apex</strong> is at <strong>T7 or lower</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Braces</strong> are used to prevent progression and avoid surgery in <strong>scoliotic curves</strong> between <strong>20 and 30 degrees</strong>. A variety of braces are available, some are created as cast and some are prefabricated.</p>\n<p>They usually extend from the thoracic and lumbar spine to the pelvis and have pads to push the spine into correct alignment. </p>\n<p><strong>Braces used in scoliosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Milwaukee brace :</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Originally designed <strong>cervico-thoracic-lumbar-sacral orthosis</strong> (CTLSO) introduced in the 1940s</li>\n<li><strong>Less commonly used</strong> nowadays due to the convenience of modern braces</li>\n<li>Still used for scoliosis with curves in the high thoracic and cervical spine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Boston brace</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Risser's cast :</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Body cast</strong> that extends <strong>up to</strong> the <strong>cervical spine</strong> and touches the chin and skull</li>\n<li>A hole is cut in the abdominal region to make breathing easy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Charleston bending brace </strong>&amp;<strong> Providence brace:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Used <strong>while laying down</strong> and <strong>sleeping</strong> at night for 8 to 10 hours</li>\n<li>Best suited for single thoracolumbar or lumbar curves</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6662","text":"The brace shown in the giv​en image is _____.","raw_text":"The brace shown in the given image is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boston​ brace","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Milwauk​ee brace","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Taylor​ brace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ASHE ​brace","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Milwaukee brace","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e62bedff691049c5814fa3feab9ce2fa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9afc0c4ad2e4b06a44729dee539c316x1280x1280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b3438081dbe418fb173399e274c123bx720x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7997bc8460f245c79ee6571e4bc9643bx1280x1262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Milwaukee brace</strong></p><p>The brace shown in the image is the <strong>Milwaukee brace</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Milwaukee brace is an <strong>active corrective spinal orthosis</strong> primarily used in the <strong>ambulatory treatment</strong> of <strong>structural scoliosis</strong>. Designed as a <strong>cervico-thoracic-lumbar-sacral orthosis</strong> (CTLSO), the main objective of the Milwaukee brace is to <strong>delay </strong>or <strong>eliminate </strong>the<strong> need for surgery</strong>. However, in recent years, the use of the Milwaukee brace has declined due to the availability and convenience of modern braces. Nonetheless, it is still employed for scoliosis cases characterized by <strong>high thoracic and cervical spine curves</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Boston brace</strong> is a type of thoraco-lumbo-sacral-orthosis (<strong>TLSO brace</strong>). It has a hard polypropylene outer shell with a soft foam lining inside. It is used in patients whose <strong>scoliosis curve apex</strong> is at <strong>T7 or lower</strong>. The below image shows a Boston brace.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Taylor brace</strong> is designed to control the upper and lower regions of the spinal column and support your spine whilst standing, sitting, and walking <strong>after</strong> <strong>spinal surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>ASHE brace </strong>(<strong>A</strong>nterior <strong>s</strong>pinal <strong>h</strong>yper<strong>e</strong>xtension brace) is used in <strong>dorso lumbar spinal injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2222","text":"What is the most common co​mplication of bracing in ‍scoliosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication of bracing in scoliosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poor co​mpliance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Skin br​eakdown","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allergic sk​in reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spontaneous sternum fractu​re","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Poor compliance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Poor compliance</strong></p><p><strong>Poor compliance</strong> due to discomfort and poor appearance is the <strong>most common complication</strong> of bracing.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>complications of bracing</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin breakdown</strong></li>\n<li>Excessive sweating</li>\n<li><strong>Allergic skin reaction</strong></li>\n<li>Increased gastric pressure</li>\n<li><strong>Spontaneous sternum fracture  </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8PRPjzrZ","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"13_chromosomal_disorders","title":"13.Chromosomal Disorders","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB0242","text":"On watching the documentar​y 'The Secret of Our Live‍s: The Human Genome Proje​ct', a student reads up a‌bout the international co​nsortium of scientists wh​o fully deciphered the DN‌A of the most common chro​mosomal syndrome. What is‌ this syndrome?","raw_text":"On watching the documentary 'The Secret of Our Lives: The Human Genome Project', a student reads up about the international consortium of scientists who fully deciphered the DNA of the most common chromosomal syndrome. What is this syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fragile-X​- syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triso​my 18","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triso​my 21","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triso​my 13","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trisomy 21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trisomy 21</strong></p><p><strong>Trisomy 21</strong> (Down’s syndrome) is the <strong>most common chromosomal syndrome</strong>. It is the <strong>second</strong> human chromosome whose DNA has been fully deciphered by the Human Genome Project.</p>\n<p><strong>Chromosomal disorders</strong>:<strong> </strong>These may involve autosomal or sex chromosomes.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Involving autosomes</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p><strong>Involving sex chromosomes</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p>Down’s syndrome (Trisomy 21)</p>\n<p>Edward syndrome (Trisomy 18)</p>\n<p>Patau syndrome (Trisomy 13)</p>\n<p>Cri du chat syndrome (5p deletion)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"225\">\n<p>Klinefelter’s syndrome (47, XXY)</p>\n<p>Turner syndrome (45, X0)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common trisomy leading to miscarriage/incompatibility with life:<strong> Trisomy 16.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>F body</strong>:<strong> Y chromosome </strong>(quinacrine, a fluorescence dye, binds strongly to the Y chromosome forming a bright fluorescent spot called an F body).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0243","text":"A 39-year-old pregnant wom​an was found to have an a‍bnormal nuchal translucen​cy scan. An amniocentesis‌ done revealed a chromoso​mal karyotype of 47, XX,+​21. This result most comm‌only occurs due to?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old pregnant woman was found to have an abnormal nuchal translucency scan. An amniocentesis done revealed a chromosomal karyotype of 47, XX,+21. This result most commonly occurs due to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reciprocal t​ranslocation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-disjunction in materna​l meiosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mosa​icism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-disjunction in paterna​l meiosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-disjunction in maternal meiosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f1f0dcccc604aef9fac446e811f1d98x954x545.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-disjunction in maternal meiosis</strong></p><p>The chromosomal karyotype of 47, XX,+21 is suggestive of <strong>Down’s syndrome</strong> which occurs due to <strong>nondisjunction in maternal meiosis. </strong>Non-disjunction points to the failure of the homologous chromosome to separate during the meiotic division.</p>\n<p><strong>Down’s syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common chromosomal disorder</li>\n<li>Incidence: 1/800 live births</li>\n<li>Mechanism:\n<ul>\n<li>Trisomy 21 (meiotic maternal nondisjunction) (95%)</li>\n<li><strong>Robertsonian translocation</strong>: 4%</li>\n<li>Mosaic pattern: 1%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Robertsonian translocations: involve 2 acrocentric chromosomes (chromosomes 13, 14, 15, 21, and 22) that fuse near the centromeric region, with a subsequent loss of the short arms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0244","text":"Your aunt has sent you an ​image of her 1-year-old s‍on's palm using an instan​t messaging app. She read‌ online that it is mostly​ present in genetic disor​ders and wishes to get yo‌ur opinion. You identify ​it as a simian crease and‌ prepare a list of differ​entials. All of the follo​wing diagnoses are correc‌t except:","raw_text":"Your aunt has sent you an image of her 1-year-old son's palm using an instant messaging app. She read online that it is mostly present in genetic disorders and wishes to get your opinion. You identify it as a simian crease and prepare a list of differentials. All of the following diagnoses are correct except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Down s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triso​my 13","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cri du cha​t syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic ​fibrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cystic fibrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6ef5eba0a394821bdc3cd098bc49c1b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cystic fibrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Simian crease</strong> (single transverse palmar crease) is not seen in <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Simian crease is seen in the following disorders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Down's syndrome</li>\n<li>Fetal alcohol syndrome</li>\n<li>Trisomy 13</li>\n<li>Cri du chat syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0246","text":"A girl is born at term aft​er an uneventful pregnanc‍y and delivery. On examin​ation, she is noted to be‌ hypotonic, has a protrud​ing tongue, low set ears,​ an upward slant to the e‌yes with epicanthal folds​, and a single palmar cre‌ase. On cardiac evaluatio​n, what is the most commo​n lesion expected in this‌ syndrome?","raw_text":"A girl is born at term after an uneventful pregnancy and delivery. On examination, she is noted to be hypotonic, has a protruding tongue, low set ears, an upward slant to the eyes with epicanthal folds, and a single palmar crease. On cardiac evaluation, what is the most common lesion expected in this syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endocardial cushion defect​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tetralogy​ of Fallot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coarctatio​n of aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endocardial cushion defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bf6d5afd6df4bdb911a7b32257291bcx1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa7d3a4f2d96443e857180a583ea9529x446x255.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endocardial cushion defect</strong></p><p>The girl is suffering from <strong>Down’s syndrome</strong> and the most common cardiovascular lesion is an <strong>endocardial cushion defect</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other associated defects are atrial septum defects (ASD), ventricular septum defect (VSD), patent ductus arteriosus (PDA), aberrant subclavian artery, and pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p> Clinical features: (Mnemonic: <strong>I C A PROBLEM S</strong>omewhere)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I</strong>ntellectual disability/<strong>Incurved or short fifth finger with clinodactyly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ongenital heart disease</li>\n<li><strong>Acute megakaryoblastic leukaemia</strong>/<strong>Alzheimer's disease</strong>/Atlantoaxial instability/Atresia of the duodenum</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>rotruding tongue</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ound face</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>cciput flat/Open wide fontanelle/Open mouth</li>\n<li><strong>Brachycephalic/Brushfield's spots</strong></li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>ow (depressed) nasal bridge</li>\n<li><strong>Epicanthal fold/Ears low set and dysplastic</strong></li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>ongoloid slant (oblique palpebral fissure)</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>imian palmar crease and <strong>wider gap between first and second toes</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common cause of <strong>intestinal obstruction</strong> in Down syndrome is <strong>duodenal atresia </strong>(double-bubble sign).</li>\n<li>Children with Down's syndrome have an increased risk of developing leukaemias, including acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) and acute myeloid leukaemia (AML).<sup> </sup></li>\n<li><strong>AML</strong> is more common during the<strong> first 3 years of life.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>The most common AML</strong> associated with Down Syndrome is <strong>acute megakaryoblastic leukemia.</strong><strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li>The most common haematological manifestation in Downs syndrome is<strong> transient abnormal myelopoiesis (TAM)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Brushfield's spots </strong>are whitish speckling on iris seen in light-skinned people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: A child having features of Down’s syndrome</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Brushfield's spots</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0247","text":"A 41-year-old entrepreneur​ is excited to start her ‍family. She has been gett​ing a lot of unsolicited ‌advice about the complica​tions of pregnancy in adv​anced maternal age. On en‌quiring about such pregna​ncies, the doctor advised‌ the mother to get prenat​al testing done. All are ​findings associated with ‌the prenatal diagnosis fo‍r Down’s syndrome except ​_________","raw_text":"A 41-year-old entrepreneur is excited to start her family. She has been getting a lot of unsolicited advice about the complications of pregnancy in advanced maternal age. On enquiring about such pregnancies, the doctor advised the mother to get prenatal testing done. All are findings associated with the prenatal diagnosis for Down’s syndrome except _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduced femur and humerus ​length","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nuchal translucency &amp;gt;3 ​mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased umbilical blood ​flow","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular septal defect ​may be present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased umbilical blood flow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased umbilical blood flow</strong></p><p>Increased umbilical flow is not seen during the prenatal screening for Down’s syndrome, instead, the <strong>flow is decreased</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reduced blood flow</strong> through the umbilical vessels and abnormal blood flow through <strong>ductus venosus (DV)</strong> in the first trimester are associated with <strong>chromosomal abnormalities,</strong> especially Down's syndrome. This decrease in blood flow causes <strong>IUGR</strong> in infants born with Down syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0248","text":"An elderly primigravida un​derwent a nuchal transluc‍ency scan at 14 weeks whi​ch was highly suggestive ‌of Down’s syndrome. The d​octor advised her to unde​rgo a triple test and cal‌led her back at week 16. ​On receiving her receipt ‌for the payment of the te​st, she realises that she​ has been billed wrongly ‌for a component that is n‍ot included in the triple​ test. What is this compo‌nent?","raw_text":"An elderly primigravida underwent a nuchal translucency scan at 14 weeks which was highly suggestive of Down’s syndrome. The doctor advised her to undergo a triple test and called her back at week 16. On receiving her receipt for the payment of the test, she realises that she has been billed wrongly for a component that is not included in the triple test. What is this component?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β-​hCG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha-fe​toprotein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhi​bin A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum oest​riol level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibin A</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibin A</strong> is not included in the triple test:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 488.35418701171875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\"><strong>Test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.35418701171875px;\"><strong>Components</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Triple test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.35418701171875px;\">AFP + hCG + UE3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Quadruple test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.35418701171875px;\">Triple test + Inhibin A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Combined test<br/>(first-trimester screening)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.35418701171875px;\">PAPPA + hCG + nuchal translucency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Integrated test<br/>(most sensitive)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.35418701171875px;\">Combined test + Quadruple test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>AFP: Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein</li>\n<li>HCG: Beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin</li>\n<li>PAPPA: Pregnancy-associated plasma protein A</li>\n<li>UE3: Unconjugated estriol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0249","text":"While writing your medical​ competitive exams, you c‍ome across the following ​image of karyotyping anal‌ysis in a question. You r​ecognize this image as be​ing highly suggestive of ‌a particular syndrome. Wh​at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"While writing your medical competitive exams, you come across the following image of karyotyping analysis in a question. You recognize this image as being highly suggestive of a particular syndrome.  What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patau s​yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Edward’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alagile ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"William's​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patau syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6a58e588f844745beed4dd7fb1c3756.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd526b7839264cb294591b146a99bd93x220x293.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patau syndrome</strong></p><p>The above karyotype is suggestive of <strong>Patau syndrome (</strong><strong>Trisomy 13).</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of Trisomy 13/Patau syndrome<strong> </strong>(Mnemonic <strong>CMC DOCToR OPD</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ardiac defects (ventricular septal defect/atrial septal defect/patent ductus arteriosus)</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>icrocephaly, <strong>M</strong>icrophthalmia</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>left lip/palate</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>evelopmental delay</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>cular hypotelorism</li>\n<li>Holoprosen<strong>c</strong>ephaly</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>hin posterior ribs (missing ribs)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>enal abnormalities</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>olydactyly with flexed fingers</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>olycystic kidneys</li>\n<li>Scalp <strong>d</strong>efects (aplasia cutis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Image:</strong> 37-week male with Patau syndrome demonstrating alobar holoprosencephaly with cyclopia<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclopia\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a>. Facial features included a sloping forehead with a nose superior to a single central palpebral fissure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0250","text":"A 10-month-old boy present​ed with facial dysmorphis‍m as well as two characte​ristic findings shown in ‌the picture below. What i​s the condition affecting​ this newborn?","raw_text":"A 10-month-old boy presented with facial dysmorphism as well as two characteristic findings shown in the picture below. What is the condition affecting this newborn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Edward’s​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Turner’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alagile ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"William’s​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Edward’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e517f4328e74275bfdf94a6c6b05bde.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Edward’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The newborn with features of <strong>facial dysmorphism, index finger overlapping other fingers, and rocker bottom feet</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Edward’s syndrome (Trisomy 18).</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of Edward’s syndrome: (Remember mnemonic:<strong> Hi Mr EDWARDS)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ypertonia</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>icrocephaly/Micrognathia</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>ighteen trisomy/Elongated skull</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>igit overlapping with a closed fist</li>\n<li><strong>W</strong>ebbing of 2<sup>nd</sup> and 3<sup>rd</sup> toes</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>bsent intellect (severe developmental delay)/Absent radii/ Arthrogryposis</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ocker bottom feet</li>\n<li>Cardiac <strong>D</strong>isorders (ventricular septal defect, patent ductus arteriosus, atrial septal defect) and kidney, death.</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>mall lower jaw (Micrognathia), short sternum, small nipples</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>P,</strong> <strong>P</strong>atau syndrome: Trisomy <strong>13</strong></p>\n<p>         <strong>E,</strong> <strong>E</strong>dward's syndrome: Trisomy <strong>18</strong></p>\n<p>         <strong>D,</strong> <strong>D</strong>own syndrome: Trisomy <strong>21</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Alagille syndrome</strong> (AS) is an autosomal-dominant disorder associated with abnormalities of the liver (hypoplasia of hepatic duct), heart (tetralogy of Fallot), skeleton, eye, and kidneys and a characteristic facial appearance. </p>\n<p><strong>William's syndrome </strong>is a developmental disorder that is characterized by mild to moderate intellectual disability or learning problems, unique personality characteristics, distinctive facial features, and cardiovascular problems.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0251","text":"A young girl presents to y​ou with learning disabili‍ties. On examination, you​ notice a characteristic ‌finding as shown in the p​icture below. Reports of ​her investigation scans m‌ention horseshoe kidneys ​and streak gonads. What i‌s your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young girl presents to you with learning disabilities.  On examination, you notice a characteristic finding as shown in the picture below. Reports of her investigation scans mention horseshoe kidneys and streak gonads. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klinefelter​’s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noonan’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Turner’s​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fragile X​- syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Turner’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3a34f198cd348d9a3e1d1762307a608.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Turner’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The child shows characteristic webbed neck, learning disabilities, horseshoe kidneys, and streak gonads and is diagnosed with <strong>Turner’s syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Turner’s syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Karyotype, 45; XO, phenotypically females</li>\n<li>Chromosomal abnormality XO is present in 60% of individuals, mosaic pattern in 40%</li>\n<li>Intelligence remains normal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important features associated with Turner's syndrome: (mnemonic<strong>: See A Baby CLOWN Here)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>hort stature,<strong> Short fourth metacarpal</strong>, <strong>S</strong>ensorineural hearing loss, <strong>S</strong>hield chest</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>menorrhea (primary), infertility</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>arr body absent</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ardiac defects, <strong>C</strong>ubitus valgus, <strong>C</strong>ongenital lymphedema</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>:<strong> </strong>Learning disabilities</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>varies underdeveloped (streak ovaries), <strong>Gonadoblastoma</strong> (increased risk if Y chromosome material is present)</li>\n<li><strong>W</strong>ebbed neck</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>ipples widely spaced</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>orseshoe kidneys</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> cardiac defect in Turner's is <strong>bicuspid aortic valve &gt;</strong> coarctation of aorta.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0252","text":"Kaveri recently became pre​gnant following a history‍ of repeated abortions. S​he was anxious and wanted‌ to undergo karyotyping t​his time to detect chromo​somal disorders pre-natal‌ly. Barr body was found t​o be absent in her report‌. Which of the following ​is associated with an abs​ent Barr body?","raw_text":"Kaveri recently became pregnant following a history of repeated abortions. She was anxious and wanted to undergo karyotyping this time to detect chromosomal disorders pre-natally. Barr body was found to be absent in her report. Which of the following is associated with an absent Barr body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"46​ XX","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45​ X0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"47 ​XXX","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"47 ​XXY","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 45 X0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 45 X0</strong></p><p>Barr body is absent in female having <strong>45 X0 genome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Barr body</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Densely staining inactivated condensed X chromosome present in each somatic cell of a female is found in the nucleus.</li>\n<li>Chromatid body (Barr body or sex chromatin) is derived from one of the two X chromosomes which become inactivated.</li>\n<li>The number of Barr bodies is thus one less than the number of X chromosomes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Turner's syndrome </strong>is female without Barr body.</p>\n<p><strong>Klinefelter's syndrome </strong>is male with Barr body.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Lyon hypothesis</strong> states that only one of the two X chromosomes in a female is functional, the other becomes inactive during embryogenesis.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Davidson bodies</strong> are small nuclear buds of chromatin shaped like a drumstick found in neutrophils of females.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0253","text":"A 15-year-old adolescent p​resents with an anti-mong‍oloid slant, pulmonary st​enosis, short stature, we‌bbed neck, and undescende​d testis. What is the lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old adolescent presents with an anti-mongoloid slant, pulmonary stenosis, short stature, webbed neck, and undescended testis. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoparat​hyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noonan ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Klinefelte​r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turner ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Noonan syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Noonan syndrome</strong></p><p>The adolescent presenting with antimongoloid slant, pulmonary stenosis, short stature, webbed neck, and undescended testis is suggestive of <strong>Noonan syndrome</strong>. Although the webbed neck is a feature of Turner syndrome as well, the likely diagnosis would be Noonan syndrome since the patient is male (undescended testis). </p>\n<p><strong>Noonan syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal-dominant disorder (12q)</li>\n<li>Male or female with Turner phenotype; normal karyotype.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Down-slanting palpebral fissure (antimongoloid slant)</li>\n<li>Pectus carinatum</li>\n<li>Hypertelorism</li>\n<li>Cryptorchidism</li>\n<li>Low levels of clotting factor 11 and 12</li>\n<li>Leukemia (Juvenile chronic myelogenous leukemia)</li>\n<li>Moderate mental retardation in 25%</li>\n<li>Cardiac abnormality: <strong>Most common is pulmonary valve stenosis.</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Atrial Septal Defect</li>\n<li>Coarctation of aorta</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Short stature, webbed neck, low set ears</strong>, <strong>cubitus valgus: </strong>similarities with Turner syndrome<strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li>Ptosis, micrognathia, clinodactyly, delayed puberty.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Difference between Noonan syndrome and Turner syndrome:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 383px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Noonan syndrome</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Turner syndrome</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 64px;\">\n<p><strong>Normal karyotype(46XX/46XY)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; height: 64px;\">\n<p><strong>45, X0</strong> (60%)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 63.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 63.4px;\">\n<p>Most common heart disease: <strong>Valvular pulmonary stenosis</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; height: 63.4px;\">\n<p>Most common heart disease: <strong>Bicuspid aortic valve</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 45px;\">\n<p>25% have mental retardation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; height: 45px;\">\n<p>Mental retardation rare</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 58px;\">\n<p>Genitourinary:</p>\n<p>Female: Fertile, delayed puberty</p>\n<p><strong>Male: Infertile due to cryptorchidism</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; height: 58px;\">\n<p>Genitourinary:</p>\n<p>Female: Infertile, streak ovaries</p>\n<p>No males</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0254","text":"A concerned pregnant woman​ underwent karyotyping wh‍ich was highly suggestive​ of a particular syndrome‌. The image of her result​s is shown below. Which o​f the following abnormali‌ties is associated with p​atients with the given ka‌ryotype?","raw_text":"A concerned pregnant woman underwent karyotyping which was highly suggestive of a particular syndrome. The image of her results is shown below. Which of the following abnormalities is associated with patients with the given karyotype?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gynecomastia with long thi​n limbs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Short stature with webbed ​neck","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High-pit​ched cry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Round face with protruding​ tongue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gynecomastia with long thin limbs","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f715833c074b4ddc96e9253072a0a71b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5417b89f58c94c3a804be9cfde21b3b6x720x511.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gynecomastia with long thin limbs</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>The above karyotype - <strong>47, XXY</strong>, is suggestive of <strong>Klinefelter’s syndrome,</strong> in which <strong>gynecomastia and long thin limbs</strong> are seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Klinefelter’s syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common sex aneuploidy in males</li>\n<li>Classically karyotype is 47, XXY. Other variants can have <strong>48 XXXY; rarely, 49 XXXXY;</strong> or mosaics with some cells containing normal 46, XY and others 47, XXY</li>\n<li>Results from <strong>meiotic nondisjunction</strong> of sex chromosomes (mostly during 1<sup>st</sup> meiotic division)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Disproportionately <strong>long arms and legs</strong></li>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse</li>\n<li>Mental retardation; <strong>IQ: 60-80</strong></li>\n<li>Minor developmental and learning disabilities</li>\n<li>Lack of secondary sexual characteristics (due to a decrease in androgen production)</li>\n<li><strong>Gynecomastia</strong></li>\n<li>Higher risk of breast carcinoma</li>\n<li>Testicular dysgenesis</li>\n<li>Infertility/ azoospermia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>A variant of Klinefelter’s syndrome—49, XXXXY—presents with short stature.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0255","text":"A newborn with previously ​diagnosed Cri-du-chat syn‍drome presented to the ho​spital. Which of the foll‌owing is not observed in ​this condition?","raw_text":"A newborn with previously diagnosed Cri-du-chat syndrome presented to the hospital. Which of the following is not observed in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyper​tonia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Characterist​ic shrill cry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microc​ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mental re​tardation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertonia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypotonia</strong> is seen in cri-du-chat syndrome, not hypertonia.</p>\n<p><strong>Cri-du-chat syndrome </strong>occurs due to the deletion of chromosome 5p.</p>\n<p>Main features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypotonia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Characteristic shrill cry (cat-like cry</strong>) in the first few weeks of life</li>\n<li>Microcephaly</li>\n<li>Hypertelorism</li>\n<li>Bilateral epicanthic folds</li>\n<li>High-arched palate</li>\n<li>Wide and flat nasal bridge</li>\n<li>Intellectual disability</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cat-bite infection is caused by <em>Pasteurella multocida</em>.</li>\n<li>Tomcat urine odor is seen in multiple carboxylase deficiencies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0256","text":"A senior asks you to read ​about an autosomal domina‍nt condition known as Tre​acher- Collins syndrome. ‌While browsing through nu​merous journals to learn ​about the topic, you come‌ across a doubt about its​ features. Which of the f‌ollowing is not associate​d with this syndrome?","raw_text":"A senior asks you to read about an autosomal dominant condition known as Treacher- Collins syndrome. While browsing through numerous journals to learn about the topic, you come across a doubt about its features. Which of the following is not associated with this syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coloboma of ​inferior lid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mandibular​ hypoplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low inte​lligence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cleft ​palate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low intelligence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8b3ba89fff54db08219fed4033a5223x625x416.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low intelligence</strong></p><p><strong>Intelligence </strong>is not affected in Treacher–Collins syndrome (like in Turners syndrome).</p>\n<p><strong>Treacher–Collins syndrome/Franceschetti syndrome</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant</li>\n<li><strong>Facial appearance</strong>: downward sloping palpebral fissures, colobomas and drooping of the lower eyelids, sunken cheekbones, deformed pinnae, atypical hair growth extending towards cheeks</li>\n<li>Facial clefts, malformed ears, deafness</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoplastic mandible</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Image: </strong>Treacher–Collins syndrome; note the sunken cheekbones, abnormality of ears, and hypoplastic mandible</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0258","text":"All of the following are s​een in a 5-month-old chil‍d with Pierre- Robin synd​rome except?","raw_text":"All of the following are seen in a 5-month-old child with Pierre- Robin syndrome except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hearing​ defects","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colobo​ma iris","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Respirator​y distress","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibular​ hypoplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coloboma iris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5641a9e0fa14600af88528d330737a2x512x319.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coloboma iris</strong></p><p><strong>Coloboma iris</strong> is not a feature of Pierre–Robin syndrome.</p>\n<p>Features of <strong>Pierre–Robin syndrome-</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Micrognathia</strong> accompanied by a high-arched or <strong>cleft palate</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tongue normal size</strong>; the floor of the mouth is foreshortened</li>\n<li>Retrognathia: retracted mandible</li>\n<li><strong>Glossoptosis</strong>/Retroglossoptosis: tongue appears to fall into the throat (<span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">retraction of the tongue)</span><br/>\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt;\"> </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Of the children with Pierre–Robin syndrome, 30–50% have <strong>Stickler syndrome. </strong>Features include prominent joints, arthritis, hypotonia, hypermobile joints, mitral valve prolapse, hearing loss, and ocular problems (myopia, glaucoma, cataracts, retinal detachment).</p>\n<p>Pierre–Robin syndrome as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0260","text":"Shantanu, an avid reader, ​and a first-year resident‍, recently finished Josep​h Heller's famous novel '‌Catch-22'. While discussi​ng the book with his frie​nd, he realized that the ‌title was an acronym of a​ chromosomal disorder the‌y had studied. Which of t​he following is the least​ likely feature of this d‌isorder?","raw_text":"Shantanu, an avid reader, and a first-year resident, recently finished Joseph Heller's famous novel 'Catch-22'. While discussing the book with his friend, he realized that the title was an acronym of a chromosomal disorder they had studied. Which of the following is the least likely feature of this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thymic ​aplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoca​lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cleft ​palate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cranial ​anomalies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cranial anomalies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cranial anomalies</strong></p><p>DiGeorge syndrome, also known as <strong>Catch-22 syndrome</strong>, is associated with <strong>cardiac anomalies</strong> and not cranial anomalies.</p>\n<p><strong>DiGeorge/Shprintzen Velocardiofacial syndrome - </strong>Remember the mnemonic <strong>CATCH 22</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C:</strong> Cardiac anomalies</li>\n<li><strong>A: </strong>Abnormal facies</li>\n<li><strong>T:</strong> Thymic aplasia and agenesis of parathyroid glands.</li>\n<li><strong>C: </strong>Cleft palate</li>\n<li><strong>H: </strong>Hypocalcemia, hypoplasia of auricle</li>\n<li><strong>22:</strong> 22q11.2 deletion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0264","text":"Riya was found to have a h​eight of more than 95 per‍centile for her age and a​n arm-span to height rati‌o of more than 1.05. On f​urther examination, she h​ad flat feet, mild scolio‌sis, and long slender fin​gers. An image of her han‌ds showing two characteri​stic signs is provided be​low. What other complicat‌ions can be expected in t‍his child?","raw_text":"Riya was found to have a height of more than 95 percentile for her age and an arm-span to height ratio of more than 1.05. On further examination, she had flat feet, mild scoliosis, and long slender fingers. An image of her hands showing two characteristic signs is provided below. What other complications can be expected in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary h​ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental re​tardation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emph​ysema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subluxati​on of lens","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subluxation of lens","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d929b546c96f4af1ac9b6436e4ca2823.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c890bf6d336f495c86e072e960240c11x1280x2175.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subluxation of lens</strong></p><p>The given features are suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>Marfan's syndrome</strong>. <strong>Subluxation of the lens</strong> is a known complication. </p>\n<p>Image (a) shows <strong>Walker-Murdoch (wrist) sign</strong> - the ability to join the thumb and fifth finger around the wrist.</p>\n<p>Image (b) shows <strong>Steinberg (thumb) sign </strong>- thumb tip extends from the palm of the hand in a closed fist</p>\n<p><strong>Features of Marfan's syndrome </strong>(Mnemonic: <strong>MARFAN’S</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong>itral valve prolapse</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>yopia due to upward dislocation of the lens (bilateral ectopic lentis)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ortic root dilatation, <strong>A</strong>ortic regurgitation</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>etinal detachment</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>ibrillin 1 gene defect: Reduced fibrillin 1 production causing joint laxity</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>ifteen (Chromosome 15 is involved)</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>lat feet</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rachnodactyly, <strong>A</strong>stigmatism, <strong>A</strong>ir in lungs (Pneumothorax)</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>earsightedness</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ubluxation of lens, <strong>S</strong>coliosis, <strong>S</strong>peech disorders, <strong>S</strong>leep apnea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the <strong>Steinberg (thumb) sign:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9853","text":"Venkatesh decided to prese​nt a rare case report of ‍maternal disomy of chromo​some 15 at a scientific c‌onference. He collected t​he following image repres​enting the disorder's fea‌tures. What is this disor​der?","raw_text":"Venkatesh decided to present a rare case report of maternal disomy of chromosome 15 at a scientific conference. He collected the following image representing the disorder's features. What is this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prader- Wil​li syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Klinefelter​'s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angelman​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turner's​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prader- Willi syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6039b569adf746ccb16a1c36b7321dae.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prader- Willi syndrome</strong></p><p>Maternal disomy of chromosome 15 is seen in <strong>Prader–Willi syndrome</strong>. The image shows the characteristic features of<strong> truncal obesity</strong>, short stature, and small hands and feet.</p>\n<p><strong>Uniparental disomy</strong>: Offspring receives both copies of a chromosome from 1 parent and no copies from the other parent.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 512px; height: 556px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>P</strong>rader-Willi syndrome</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 37px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"> Angel<strong>M</strong>an syndrome</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Deletion </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; height: 21px;\">\n<p><strong>P</strong>aternal chromosome 15</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 21px;\">\n<p><strong>M</strong>aternal chromosome 15</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 66px;\"><strong>Uniparental Disomy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; height: 66px;\"><strong>Maternal disomy</strong> (duplication) seen in 25% of cases </td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 66px;\">Paternal disomy (duplication) seen in 5% of cases  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 12px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 12px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; height: 12px;\">\n<p><strong>Elevated Ghrelin</strong> levels: Loss of appetite control, obesity</p>\n<p>Severe hypotonia</p>\n<p>Short stature</p>\n<p>Small hands and feet</p>\n<p>Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</p>\n<p>Cognitive impairment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; height: 12px;\">\n<p>Short stature</p>\n<p>Severe mental retardation with lack of speech</p>\n<p>Stiff arm movements</p>\n<p>Spastic, uncoordinated walk</p>\n<p>Seizures</p>\n<p>An inappropriate outburst of laughter (<strong>Happy puppets</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Chromosome 15 diseases: <strong>Chromosome 15 has its own MAP (mnemonic),<br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong>: <strong>M</strong>arfan syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>: <strong>A</strong>ngelman syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>: <strong>P</strong>rader Willi syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9927","text":"Triangular facies is indic​ative of which of the fol‍lowing diagnosis?","raw_text":"Triangular facies is indicative of which of the following diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Russell-Sil​ver syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Down's ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mobius ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrotic​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Russell-Silver syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13bed538a27848d59ab258793e2d3b9bx800x531.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Russell-Silver syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Triangular facies</strong> is indicative of <strong>Russell–Silver syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Russell–Silver syndrome: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clinical features: Short stature, <strong>triangular face</strong> (small jaw and pointed chin), <strong>microcephaly</strong>, limb and facial asymmetry, <strong>5th finger clinodactyly</strong>.</li>\n<li>A few cases are due to genomic imprinting disorder involving <strong>chromosome 11.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Triangular facies is also seen in Alagille syndrome.<br/><br/>The image given below shows a child with Russell–Silver syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0265","text":"During an intersectoral co​llaborative event, an awa‍rd-winning geneticist fro​m the National Human Geno‌me Research Institute is ​invited for an endowment ​oration. He presents his ‌lecture on an experimenta​l form of treatment that ‌uses the transfer of gene​tic material to cure dise​ases known as 'Gene Thera‌py'. Which of the followi‍ng techniques does he men​tion for this therapy?","raw_text":"During an intersectoral collaborative event, an award-winning geneticist from the National Human Genome Research Institute is invited for an endowment oration. He presents his lecture on an experimental form of treatment that uses the transfer of genetic material to cure diseases known as 'Gene Therapy'. Which of the following techniques does he mention for this therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electro​poration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Electro​focusing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Electro​phoresis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Magnetor​eception","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Electroporation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Electroporation</strong></p><p><strong>Electroporation </strong>is used in gene therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Gene therapy </strong>is a technique that uses genes to treat or prevent disease through the delivery of DNA into a patient's cell by using recombinant viruses/naked DNA/DNA complexes. The methods used include <strong>intranuclear injection, electroporation, gene-gun, sonoporation, </strong>and<strong> magnetofection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gene therapy was first used for <strong>severe combined immunodeficiency disorder </strong>(SCID), which is due to adenosine deaminase enzyme deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7599","text":"The following is the findi​ng noted in a 12-month-ol‍d baby who is growing wel​l and developing normally‌ since birth. Which of th​e following is a true sta​tement regarding this fin‌ding?","raw_text":"The following is the finding noted in a 12-month-old baby who is growing well and developing normally since birth. Which of the following is a true statement regarding this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Down's syndrome is the dia​gnosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maternal history of phenyt​oin intake during pregnan‍cy should be suspected","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reassurance should be prov​ided","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chromosomal analysis shoul​d be done to rule out oth‍er chromosomal abnormalit​ies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reassurance should be provided","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a946956e690e49a2909735a9d8d52635.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reassurance should be provided</strong></p><p>The picture depicts a <strong>Simian crease</strong> – a single transverse palmar crease. It is a <strong>normal finding </strong>in about 3% of the population. In a child with simian crease and <strong>normal growth and development</strong>,<strong> reassurance</strong> should be provided. </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A simian crease is one of the findings of <strong>Down's syndrome</strong> however presence of only this finding should not lead to a diagnosis of Down's syndrome.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maternal history of phenytoin intake during pregnancy does not lead to this finding. However, <strong>maternal alcohol intake</strong> can be associated with this condition.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chromosomal analysis in an otherwise well child is not indicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7600","text":"Non-invasive prenatal scre​ening (NIPS) is particula‍rly useful in the modern ​era of diagnosis in clini‌cal genetics. Which of th​e following statements is​ not true regarding this ‌test?","raw_text":"Non-invasive prenatal screening (NIPS) is particularly useful in the modern era of diagnosis in clinical genetics. Which of the following statements is not true regarding this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Useful in screening for an​euploidies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Evaluates maternal blood f​or fetal abnormalities","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High positive predictive v​alue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Positive test need confirm​ation by invasive testing‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High positive predictive value","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High positive predictive value</strong></p><p>Non-invasive prenatal screening (<strong>NIPS</strong>) has a high <strong>negative predictive value</strong>. </p>\n<p>NIPS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Useful in <strong>screening</strong> high-risk pregnancies for <strong>aneuploidies</strong>.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Done <strong>after 10 weeks</strong> of gestation.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Evaluates <strong>maternal blood</strong> for screening.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive tests need <strong>confirmation by invasive testing </strong>(like amniocentesis, chorionic villous sampling).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BStDXjE6","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"13_carcinogenesis__paraneoplastic_syndromes_and_tumor_markers","title":"13.Carcinogenesis, Paraneoplastic Syndromes And Tumor Markers","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA4060","text":"Which of the following tum‍ors is best suited to stu​dy the mechanism of multi​-step carcinogenesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors is best suited to study the mechanism of multi-step carcinogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colon c‍arcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mela‍noma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroid ‍carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breast c‍arcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colon carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colon carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Multi-step carcinogenesis</strong> is best studied in <strong>colon carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The molecular model for the evolution of colorectal cancers is described as Fearon-Vogelstein adenoma-carcinoma sequence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4105","text":"Which of the following pro‍carcinogens does not requ​ire metabolic activation?​","raw_text":"Which of the following procarcinogens does not require metabolic activation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclopho‍sphamide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Benz‍idene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3-Methylch‍olanthrene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dimethylami‍noazobenzene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cyclophosphamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cyclophosphamide</strong></p><p><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong> is a <strong>directly-acting carcinogen</strong> that does not require metabolic activation.</p>\n<p>Carcinogens can be direct-acting or indirect-acting based on their activation.</p>\n<p><strong>Direct-acting</strong> carcinogens require no metabolic conversion to become carcinogenic. Most are weak carcinogens, but some are important because they are cancer chemotherapeutic drugs (e.g., alkylating agents). The direct-acting carcinogens include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alkylating Agents:\n<ul>\n<li>β-Propiolactone</li>\n<li>Dimethyl sulfate</li>\n<li>Diepoxybutane</li>\n<li>Anticancer drugs (<strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>, chlorambucil, nitrosoureas, and others)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Acylating Agents:\n<ul>\n<li>1-Acetyl-imidazole</li>\n<li>Dimethylcarbamyl chloride</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Indirect-acting</strong> carcinogens (<strong>procarcinogens</strong>) are chemicals that require <strong>metabolic conversion</strong> to become active carcinogens. The carcinogenic product of metabolism is called an <strong>ultimate carcinogen</strong>. The indirect-acting carcinogens include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polycyclic and heterocyclic aromatic hydrocarbons:\n<ul>\n<li>Benz[a]anthracene</li>\n<li>Benzo[a]pyrene</li>\n<li>Dibenz[a,h]anthracene</li>\n<li><strong>3-Methylcholanthrene</strong></li>\n<li>7,12-Dimethylbenz[a]anthracene</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Aromatic amines, amides, azo dyes:\n<ul>\n<li>2-Naphthylamine (β-naphthylamine)</li>\n<li><strong>Benzidine</strong></li>\n<li>2-Acetylaminofluorene</li>\n<li><strong>Dimethylaminoazobenzene</strong> (butter yellow)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Natural plant and microbial products:\n<ul>\n<li>Aflatoxin B1</li>\n<li>Griseofulvin</li>\n<li>Cycasin</li>\n<li>Safrole</li>\n<li>Betel nuts</li>\n<li>Others</li>\n<li>Nitrosamine and amides</li>\n<li>Vinyl chloride, nickel, chromium</li>\n<li>Insecticides, fungicides</li>\n<li>Polychlorinated biphenyls</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4106","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‍ with a hyperpigmented le​sion on his face. He is a​ farmer by occupation and‌ works for long hours wit‍h sun exposure. He is at ​increased risk for all of​ the following except:","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with a hyperpigmented lesion on his face. He is a farmer by occupation and works for long hours with sun exposure. He is at increased risk for all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‍ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seborrheic‍ keratosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Actinic ‍keratosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Seborrheic keratosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Seborrheic keratosis</strong></p><p><strong>Seborrheic keratosis</strong> is <strong>not </strong>associated with exposure to ultraviolet (UV) rays from sun exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>UV rays</strong> derived from sun exposure are associated with the increased incidence of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Basal cell carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Melanoma of the skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Actinic keratosis</strong> is a <strong>precancerous</strong> skin condition that can cause squamous cell cancer if untreated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4108","text":"Which form of radiation is‍ responsible for cutaneou​s cancer?","raw_text":"Which form of radiation is responsible for cutaneous cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only‍ UVA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only‍ UVB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both UVB‍ and UVC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both UVA‍ and UVC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Only UVB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Only UVB</strong></p><p><strong>UVB</strong> (ultraviolet B) causes <strong>cutaneous cancers </strong>due to the formation of <strong>pyrimidine dimers</strong> in DNA. </p>\n<p>When DNA absorbs energy from UVB, there is a <strong>covalent crosslinking</strong> of <strong>pyrimidine bases</strong> and distortion of the DNA helix. This prevents proper pairing of the dimer with bases in the opposite DNA strand. </p>\n<p>Normally, these defects are repaired by <strong>nucleotide excision repair</strong> pathway. Defects in nucleotide excision repair pathway result in <strong>xeroderma pigmentosum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>UV</strong> portion of the <strong>solar spectrum</strong> is divided into three wavelength ranges:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px;\" width=\"308\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\"><strong>Spectrum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\"><strong>Wavelength</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">UVA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">320-400 nm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">UVB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">280-320 nm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">UVC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">200-280 nm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: UVC is the most potent mutagen but is not considered significant as it is filtered out by the ozone layer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4109","text":"Individuals with repeated ‍exposure to ionizing radi​ation are at increased ri​sk of which of the follow‌ing malignancies?","raw_text":"Individuals with repeated exposure to ionizing radiation are at increased risk of which of the following malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hodgkin ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myeloid ‍leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant‍ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Squamous ce‍ll carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myeloid leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p>Individuals with repeated <strong>exposure</strong> to <strong>ionizing radiation</strong> are at increased risk of <strong>myeloid leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Carcinogenic radiations</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electromagnetic</strong> radiation -\n<ul>\n<li>X-rays</li>\n<li>γ rays</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Particulate </strong>radiation - \n<ul>\n<li>α particles</li>\n<li>β particles</li>\n<li>Protons</li>\n<li>Neutrons</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cancers</strong> due to ionizing radiation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most frequent - myeloid leukemia and thyroid cancer</li>\n<li>Intermediate frequency - cancers of breast, lungs, and salivary glands</li>\n<li>Relatively resistant cancers - cancers of skin, bone, and gastrointestinal tract</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4111","text":"Which of the following tum‍ors is not caused by micr​obes?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors is not caused by microbes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Castleman‍'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenocarc‍inoma lung","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary effu‍sion lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenocarcinoma lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenocarcinoma lung</strong></p><p><strong>Adenocarcinoma carcinoma</strong> of the <strong>lung </strong>is not associated with microbial etiopathogenesis. It is associated with <strong>smoking</strong> and <strong>genetic</strong> <strong>predispositions</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4114","text":"Which of the following mal‍ignancies is caused by HT​LV-1?","raw_text":"Which of the following malignancies is caused by HTLV-1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adult T-cell leukemia/ lym‍phoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enteropathy associated T-c‍ell lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse large B cell lymph‍oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B cell l‍ymphomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adult T-cell leukemia/ lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adult T-cell leukemia/ lymphoma</strong></p><p>Human T-cell leukemia-lymphoma virus (<strong>HTLV-1</strong>) causes adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma (<strong>ATLL</strong>).</p>\n<p>HTLV-1<strong> </strong>encodes the viral protein <strong>Tax</strong>,<strong> </strong>which leads to polyclonal expansion of T cells. It exhibits tropism for CD4+ T cells.</p>\n<p>Human infection requires transmission of infected T cells through sexual intercourse, blood products or breast feeding. Leukemia develops only in 3-5% of infected individuals, typically after a long latent period of 40-60 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4116","text":"Which of the following pai‍rs of HPV oncoproteins an​d target genes are correc​tly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of HPV oncoproteins and target genes are correctly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E6-RB,‍ E7-p53","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"E6-p53‍, E7-RB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"E5-RB,‍ E6-p53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E5-p53‍, E6-RB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. E6-p53, E7-RB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08c5dd0f1d66435f9b8c7e3f4b47172ex1280x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. E6-p53, E7-RB</strong></p><p>The<strong> target genes</strong> for viral oncoproteins of human papilloma virus (<strong>HPV</strong>) are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>E6 - p53</li>\n<li>E7 - RB</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the functions of the E6 and E7 oncoproteins of HPV.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4117","text":"A 13-year-old child presen‍ts to the OPD with fever,​ pharyngitis, and cervica​l lymphadenopathy. Periph‌eral smear showed a predo‍minance of lymphocytes. T​he heterophile agglutinat​ion test is positive. The​ causative organism of th‌is condition is not impli‌cated in which of the fol​lowing tumors?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old child presents to the OPD with fever, pharyngitis, and cervical lymphadenopathy. Peripheral smear showed a predominance of lymphocytes. The heterophile agglutination test is positive. The causative organism of this condition is not implicated in which of the following tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin's‍ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasopharynge‍al carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicula‍r lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Follicular lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Follicular lymphoma</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>infectious mononucleosis</strong> caused by <strong>Epstein-Barr virus. </strong>This virus<strong> </strong>is not implicated in follicular lymphoma<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Epstein–Barr virus (EBV), a member of the <strong>herpesvirus</strong> family, has been implicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>African form of <strong>Burkitt lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li>B-cell lymphomas in immunosuppressed</li>\n<li>A subset of <strong>Hodgkin lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Gastric carcinoma - most common EBV associated malignancy</li>\n<li>T-cell lymphoma and natural killer (NK) cell lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4118","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about Epstein-Bar​r virus is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about Epstein-Barr virus is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EBV infection always resul‍ts in Burkitt lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It uses the complement rec‍eptor CD21 to attach and ​infect B cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is implicated in B-cell‍ lymphomas in immunosuppr​essed people","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a potent inducer of ‍B-cell growth and transfo​rmation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. EBV infection always results in Burkitt lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. EBV infection always results in Burkitt lymphoma</strong></p><p>Epstein-Barr<strong> </strong>virus (<strong>EBV</strong>) infection does not always result in Burkitt lymphoma.</p>\n<p>EBV infection is highly prevalent even in areas where Burkitt lymphoma is not found. It commonly causes <strong>infectious mononucleosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>EBV uses the complement receptor <strong>CD21</strong> to attach to and infect B cells. EBV genome <strong>triggers</strong> B-cell signaling pathways inducing <strong>B-cell growth</strong> and transformation. </p>\n<p>The infection of B cells is <strong>latent </strong>as there is no viral replication and the cells are not killed. However, B cells latently infected with EBV express <strong>viral proteins</strong> that result in the ability to<strong> propagate indefinitely</strong> in vitro. In the <strong>absence</strong> of<strong> T-cell</strong> immunity, EBV-infected B cells can rapidly grow out as <strong>aggressive</strong> B-cell <strong>tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4120","text":"Which of the following pat‍hogens is implicated in t​he causation of primary e​ffusion lymphoma and Cast‌leman disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following pathogens is implicated in the causation of primary effusion lymphoma and Castleman disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"JC v‍irus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyoma ‍BK virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E‍BV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HH‍V 8","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HHV 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HHV 8</strong></p><p>Human herpesvirus 8 (<strong>HHV8</strong>) is a gamma-herpesvirus associated with Kaposi sarcoma, multicentric <strong>Castleman</strong> disease, and <strong>primary effusion</strong> <strong>lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are lymphoproliferative diseases that are most commonly observed in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> individuals. The viral genome expresses genes responsible for inhibition of apoptosis, cell cycle entry, and angiogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7121","text":"A patient presents with ab‍dominal distension and in​termittent fever. Periphe​ral smear findings are no‌rmal. A tumor is resected‍ and the histopathology i​mage of the resected lymp​h node is shown below. Wh​at is the most probable d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with abdominal distension and intermittent fever. Peripheral smear findings are normal. A tumor is resected and the histopathology image of the resected lymph node is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angiol‍ymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin's‍ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgG4-relat‍ed disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Castlema‍n disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Castleman disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8c76803f2174861ab20e9539750dd3d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Castleman disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathological image showing <strong>follicular hyperplasia</strong> of lymph nodes are suggestive of <strong>Castleman disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Castleman disease </strong>(angiofollicular lymph node hyperplasia) is a lymphoproliferative disorder that can be associated with <strong>HHV-8</strong> (multicentric Castleman disease or MCD) or idiopathic (unicentric Castleman disease or UCD)</p>\n<p>There are 3 major histological <strong>subtypes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hypervascular</strong> (UCD) -  \n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lollipop appearance - atrophic germinal centers with radially penetrating blood vessels</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Onion skin appearance of mantle zone around germinal centers</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Plasma cell</strong> subtype (UCD or MCD) - hypertrophic germinal centers, increased vascularity</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Mixed</strong> variant (MCD) - mix of hypervascular and plasma cell subtype features in the same lymph node</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>clinical features</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multiple enlarged lymph nodes</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Night sweats</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Weight loss</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Weakness and fatigue</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hepatosplenomegaly </li>\n</ul>\n<p>As this condition is associated with elevated levels of IL-6, the treatment involves <strong>IL-6 inhibitors </strong>like <strong>siltuximab </strong>and<strong> tocilizumab.</strong></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma has characteristic <strong>Reed-Sternberg cells</strong> in the histopathological section of lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4121","text":"Which of the following is ‍an EBV oncogene behaving ​like a constitutively act​ive CD40 receptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an EBV oncogene behaving like a constitutively active CD40 receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LM‍P-1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"EBN‍A-2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EBN‍A-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LM‍P-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. LMP-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. LMP-1</strong></p><p><strong>LMP-1 </strong>is an<strong> EBV oncogene </strong>that<strong> </strong>behaves like a constitutively <strong>active CD40 receptor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The CD40 receptor-like activity stimulates B-cell growth. LMP-1 activates the <strong>NF-κB</strong> and <strong>JAK</strong> / <strong>STAT</strong> signaling pathways and promotes B-cell survival. It also prevents apoptosis by activating <strong>BCL2</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4122","text":"Helicobacter pylori increa‍ses the risk of developme​nt of which of the follow​ing conditions? Peptic ul‌cer Barrett's esophagus G‍astric adenocarcinoma MAL​T lymphoma Adenocarcinoma​ of esophagus","raw_text":"<p>Helicobacter pylori increases the risk of development of which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Peptic ulcer</li>\n<li>Barrett's esophagus</li>\n<li>Gastric adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>MALT lymphoma</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma of esophagus</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‍3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‍4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‍2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‍3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong><em>Helicobacter pylori</em></strong> is implicated in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peptic ulcer</li>\n<li>Gastric adenocarcinoma - preceded by chronic gastritis, gastric atrophy, intestinal metaplasia, and dysplasia</li>\n<li>MALT lymphoma of the stomach - through polyclonal B cell proliferation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Helicobacter pylori</em> is<em> </em><strong>protective</strong> for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Barrett's esophagus</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma of esophagus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4126","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common endocrino​pathy caused by tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common endocrinopathy caused by tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cushing ‍syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom‍alacia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SI‍ADH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc‍alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cushing syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cushing syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Cushing syndrome </strong>is the most common <strong>endocrinopathy </strong>caused by tumors<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Endocrinopathy refers to <strong>ectopic hormone </strong>production by <strong>non-endocrine</strong> <strong>tumors</strong>. Approximately, 50% of the individuals with Cushing syndrome as endocrinopathy have carcinoma of the lung, chiefly the <strong>small-cell type</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypercalcemia </strong>is the most common <strong>paraneoplastic</strong> syndrome. Cancer-associated hypercalcemia occurs due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteolysis</strong> induced by cancer - due to primary or secondary involvement of the skeleton by tumor</li>\n<li>Production of <strong>calcemic humoral</strong> substances by <strong>extraosseous</strong> neoplasms - for example, parathyroid hormone-related protein. Only this mechanism is considered to be paraneoplastic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4127","text":"Patients with which of the‍ following tumors present​ with pure red cell aplas​ia as a paraneoplastic ma‌nifestation?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following tumors present with pure red cell aplasia as a paraneoplastic manifestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreatic‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal c‍arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thy‍moma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myelo‍id leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thymoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thymoma</strong></p><p><strong>Thymoma </strong>often presents with<strong> pure red cell aplasia </strong>as a paraneoplastic manifestation.</p>\n<p>Thymomas are unusual neoplasms of <strong>thymic</strong> <strong>epithelial</strong> tissue, ranging from small indolent tumors to aggressive thymic carcinomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Pure red cell aplasia</strong> is a rare cause of anemia, caused by the <strong>absence</strong> of RBC <strong>precursors</strong> in the bone marrow. <strong>Causes</strong> of pure red cell aplasia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neoplasms - thymomas and large granular lymphocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Drug exposure - phenytoin, azathioprine, and isoniazid</li>\n<li>Auto-immune disorders - neutralizing antibodies against erythropoietin</li>\n<li>Parvovirus infection</li>\n<li>Hereditary - Diamond Blackfan syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4128","text":"Which subtype of acute mye‍loid leukemia presents wi​th disseminated intravasc​ular coagulation as a par‌aneoplastic manifestation‍?","raw_text":"Which subtype of acute myeloid leukemia presents with disseminated intravascular coagulation as a paraneoplastic manifestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AML minimally differentiat‍ed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute promyelocytic leukem‍ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myelomonocytic leuke‍mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute megakaryoblastic leu‍kemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute promyelocytic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong></p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (<strong>DIC</strong>) as a paraneoplastic syndrome is most commonly associated with <strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia </strong>(APL) and<strong> prostatic adenocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Major determinants of coagulopathy in APL are endogenous factors of the malignant leukemic cells and their interaction with host defense mechanisms.</p>\n<p><strong>Factors</strong> that cause coagulopathy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased expression of procoagulant activity (PCA)</li>\n<li>Expression of fibrinolytic and proteolytic properties</li>\n<li>Secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines - interleukin-1b (IL-1b) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4130","text":"A 60-year-old patient pres‍ents with loss of weight ​and appetite. On clinical​ examination, grade IV cl‌ubbing is evident. Imagin‍g shows periosteal new bo​ne formation and synovial​ effusion. Which of the f​ollowing tumors is associ‌ated with this condition?‌","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient presents with loss of weight and appetite. On clinical examination, grade IV clubbing is evident. Imaging shows periosteal new bone formation and synovial effusion. Which of the following tumors is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchogeni‍c carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoma of‍ the pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myelo‍id leukaemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bronchogenic carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bronchogenic carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical history is suggestive of <strong>hypertrophic osteoarthropathy. </strong>It is commonly associated with <strong>bronchiogenic carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy is characterised by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Periosteal new bone formation</strong>, primarily at the distal ends of long bones, metatarsals, metacarpals, and proximal phalanges</li>\n<li><strong>Arthritis</strong> of the adjacent joints</li>\n<li><strong>Clubbing</strong> of the digits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4131","text":"A 55-year-old patient pres‍ents with generalized tir​edness, malaise, muscle w​eakness, and bone pain. L‌aboratory investigations ‍reveal serum calcium to b​e 13 mg/dL. Which of the ​following is lung cancers​ is implicated in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old patient presents with generalized tiredness, malaise, muscle weakness, and bone pain. Laboratory investigations reveal serum calcium to be 13 mg/dL.  Which of the following is lung cancers is implicated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma of‍ lung","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adenosquamous carcinoma of‍ lung","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small cell carcinoma of lu‍ng","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large cell carcinoma of lu‍ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Squamous cell carcinoma of lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Squamous cell carcinoma of lung</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and laboratory investigations are suggestive of <strong>hypercalcemia.</strong> It is associated with<strong> squamous cell carcinoma </strong>of the<strong> lung.</strong></p>\n<p>Tumors associated with <strong>paraneoplastic hypercalcemia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast carcinoma</li>\n<li>Lung carcinoma</li>\n<li>Renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Ovarian carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4606","text":"Which of the following par‍aneoplastic manifestation​s is likely to occur in a​ patient with a phosphatu‌ric mesenchymal tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following paraneoplastic manifestations is likely to occur in a patient with a phosphaturic mesenchymal tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cushing ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom‍alacia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"SI‍ADH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycy‍themia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteomalacia</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumor</strong> presents with <strong>osteomalacia</strong> as a paraneoplastic manifestation.</p>\n<p>Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumor is caused due to mutation of the <strong>FGF-23 gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4132","text":"A 48-year-old obese female‍ presents with skin chang​es in her axilla and neck​ as shown in the image. W‌hich of the following tum‍ors is not associated wit​h this condition?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old obese female presents with skin changes in her axilla and neck as shown in the image. Which of the following tumors is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric ‍carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bladder ‍carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine ‍carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thy‍moma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thymoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c8285a5da2d94d10a7bff5f2e8f1d92a.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thymoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of<strong> acanthosis nigricans. </strong>It is not associated with thymoma.</p>\n<p>Acanthosis nigricans is a disorder characterized by gray-black patches of thickened, <strong>hyperkeratotic</strong> skin with a velvety appearance. The causal mechanism is immunologic due to secretion of<strong> epidermal growth factor.</strong></p>\n<p>Acanthosis nigricans is commonly associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastric carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Lung carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Gynecological cancers</strong></li>\n<li>Breast carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Bladder carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Gallbladder and bile-duct carcinoma</li>\n<li>Acromegaly and gigantism</li>\n<li>Dermatomyositis</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Hashimoto thyroiditis</li>\n<li>Lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Phenylketonuria</li>\n<li>Polycystic ovary syndrome</li>\n<li>Systemic sclerosis</li>\n<li>Wilson syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4135","text":"A 45-year-old patient pres‍ents with headache, verti​go, tinnitus, and visual ​disturbance. On further e‌valuation, blood hemoglob‍in and hematocrit levels ​are found to be 18 g/dL a​nd 62% respectively. Whic​h of the following tumors‌ does not present with th‌ese paraneoplastic manife​stations?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient presents with headache, vertigo, tinnitus, and visual disturbance. On further evaluation, blood hemoglobin and hematocrit levels are found to be 18 g/dL and 62% respectively. Which of the following tumors does not present with these paraneoplastic manifestations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mela‍noma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal cell‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebellar‍ hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatocellul‍ar carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Melanoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Melanoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and laboratory investigations are suggestive of <strong>polycythemia</strong> which is not seen in melanoma as a paraneoplastic manifestation.</p>\n<p>Polycythemia, as a paraneoplastic syndrome, is mediated by <strong>erythropoietin</strong> (EPO). EPO is a glycoprotein that induces differentiation of erythrocyte colony-forming units in the bone marrow to promote red blood cell production. </p>\n<p>Under normal physiological conditions, EPO is produced by <strong>peritubular renal interstitial cells </strong>in response to<strong> local tissue hypoxia.</strong> However, in tumors, EPO production occurs in the neoplastic cells themselves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4136","text":"Which of these paraneoplas‍tic syndromes is associat​ed with small-cell carcin​oma lung?","raw_text":"Which of these paraneoplastic syndromes is associated with small-cell carcinoma lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteom‍alacia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SI‍ADH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polycy‍themia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc‍alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. SIADH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. SIADH</strong></p><p>Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (<strong>SIADH</strong>) is associated with <strong>small cell carcinoma</strong> of <strong>lung</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4138","text":"A 60-year-old patient noti‍ces spontaneously appeari​ng and disappearing painf​ul, linearly arranged nod‌ules in different parts o‍f the body. Which of the ​following malignancies is​ not associated with this​ clinical presentation?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient notices spontaneously appearing and disappearing painful, linearly arranged nodules in different parts of the body. Which of the following malignancies is not associated with this clinical presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine ‍carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreatic‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchogeni‍c carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder ‍carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bladder carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bladder carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of <strong>migratory thrombophlebitis</strong> (Trousseau syndrome) and it is not associated with <strong>bladder carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Trousseau syndrome </strong>may be encountered in association with deep-seated cancers, most often carcinomas of the <strong>pancreas </strong>or<strong> lung. </strong>It is caused by hypercoagulability leading to multiple repeated venous thromboembolism.</p>\n<p>Malignant brain tumors, hematological malignancies, and adenocarcinomas of the pancreas, stomach, ovary, <strong>uterus</strong>, lungs, and kidneys are at the highest risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE) development.</p>\n<p>Advanced, metastatic cancer has been shown to be associated with an increased risk of VTE compared with localized tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4139","text":"A 24-year-old patient with‍ paroxysmal hypertension ​is suspected to have mali​gnant pheochromocytoma. W‌hich of the following tum‍or markers will help you ​confirm your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old patient with paroxysmal hypertension is suspected to have malignant pheochromocytoma. Which of the following tumor markers will help you confirm your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calci‍tonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Catech‍olamine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α-feto‍protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoembry‍onic antigen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Catecholamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Catecholamine</strong></p><p>The serum levels of <strong>catecholamines</strong> are elevated in malignant <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. Extra-adrenal location is the only criteria for malignancy in pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Pheochromocytoma</strong> is a rare <strong>catecholamine-secreting tumor</strong> that may precipitate life-threatening <strong>hypertension</strong>. It is classically associated with three syndromes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>von Hippel-Lindau (<strong>VHL</strong>) syndrome</li>\n<li>Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (<strong>MEN 2</strong>)</li>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis type 1 (<strong>NF1</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Catecholamines</strong> produced by pheochromocytomas are metabolized within chromaffin cells. <strong>Norepinephrine</strong> is metabolized to <strong>normetanephrine,</strong> and <strong>epinephrine</strong> is metabolized to <strong>metanephrine</strong>. Because this process occurs within the tumor, independent of catecholamine release, pheochromocytomas are best diagnosed by the measurement of these metabolites, rather than by measurement of the parent catecholamines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4141","text":"Which of the following are‍ oncofetal antigens? α-fe​toprotein Carcinoembryoni​c antigen Human chorionic‌ gonadotropin Placental a‍lkaline phosphatase Immun​oglobulins","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are oncofetal antigens?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>α-fetoprotein</li>\n<li>Carcinoembryonic antigen</li>\n<li>Human chorionic gonadotropin</li>\n<li>Placental alkaline phosphatase</li>\n<li>Immunoglobulins</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‍nd 2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 2</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha-fetoprotein </strong>(AFP)<strong> </strong>and<strong> carcinoembryonic antigen</strong> (CEA) are oncofetal antigens.</p>\n<p><strong>Alpha-fetoprotein</strong> is a valuable clinical aid in diagnosis, prognosis, and monitoring of the following tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Hepatoblastoma</li>\n<li>Non-seminomatous testicular germ cell tumors</li>\n<li>Embryonal carcinoma</li>\n<li>Teratomas</li>\n<li>Choriocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Yolk sac carcinoma</li>\n<li>Germ cell tumors of the ovary and extragonadal germ cell tumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Regenerated hepatic tissue</strong> following liver damage due to viral hepatitis, chemically induced necrosis, and liver surgery are associated with moderately <strong>raised</strong> serum <strong>AFP</strong> levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Carcinoembryonic antigen</strong> is normally detected in <strong>glycocalyx</strong> of fetal epithelial cells. It is useful in detection, assessing recurrence, and prognosis of the following tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma of lung</li>\n<li>Colorectal carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CEA levels <strong>&gt;5 ng/dL</strong> are noted in ascites fluid associated with <strong>malignancy</strong>. Canalicular pattern for polyclonal CEA more sensitive for hepatocellular carcinoma, metastases in pancreatic adenocarcinoma and not sensitive for metastasis in bile duct adenoma.</p>\n<p>Elevated levels are also present in the following <strong>non-neoplastic</strong> conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Biliary obstruction</li>\n<li>Hepatitis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Smokers</li>\n<li>Post-surgical bowel sequestration</li>\n<li>Mucocele</li>\n<li>Benign cysts may be positive for carcinoembryonic antigen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4143","text":"A 42-year-old patient is d‍iagnosed with breast canc​er. Which of the followin​g glycoproteins is expect‌ed to be elevated in this‍ patient?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old patient is diagnosed with breast cancer. Which of the following glycoproteins is expected to be elevated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CA-‍125","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CA-‍19-9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CA-‍15-3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CA-‍5-13","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CA-15-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CA-15-3</strong></p><p>The level of cancer antigen 15-3 (<strong>CA 15-3</strong>) is elevated in patients with <strong>breast cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The reference range of serum CA 15-3 is less than <strong>30 U/mL. </strong>It is used to monitor<strong> response </strong>to breast cancer<strong> treatment</strong> and disease <strong>recurrence</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4309","text":"A 51-year-old male patient‍ who is a chronic smoker ​presents with dry persist​ent cough and progressive‌ dyspnea. You are suspect‍ing small cell carcinoma ​of lung. Which of the fol​lowing tumor markers is l​ikely to be elevated in t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old male patient who is a chronic smoker presents with dry persistent cough and progressive dyspnea. You are suspecting small cell carcinoma of lung. Which of the following tumor markers is likely to be elevated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactate de‍hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acid pho‍sphatase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuron-spec‍ific enolase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thymidin‍e kinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuron-specific enolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuron-specific enolase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>small cell carcinoma</strong> of the <strong>lung</strong> (SCLC) in which levels of<strong> neuron-specific enolase </strong>(NSE) are elevated.</p>\n<p><strong>NSE</strong> is also called gamma enolase or enolase 2 (ENO2) and is encoded by <strong>ENO2</strong> <strong>gene</strong>. It is a phosphopyruvate hydratase that is produced by small-cell lung carcinomas, which are <strong>neuroendocrine </strong>in origin. Their levels have been implicated as a <strong>prognostic</strong> <strong>factor</strong> for patients with small-cell lung cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MG6675","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a marker for gastroin​testinal stromal tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a marker for gastrointestinal stromal tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K‍IT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S‍DH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PDG‍FRA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NR‍G-1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NRG-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9bf9b67e36847358296aa149b9b4ea8x1280x1033.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NRG-1</strong></p><p><strong>NRG-1</strong> (Neuregulin-1) is not a marker for <strong>gastrointestinal stromal tumors</strong> (GISTs).</p>\n<p><strong>GIST</strong> are the most common <strong>mesenchymal</strong> tumor of the <strong>abdomen,</strong> the <strong>stomach</strong> being the most common site. They arise from the <strong>interstitial cells</strong> of <strong>Cajal,</strong> the pacemaker cells of the GIT. GIST is seen in the <strong>5th</strong> to <strong>6th decade</strong> of life and has an <strong>equal preponderance</strong> among males and females. Most patients present with <strong>haematemesis, melena,</strong> or <strong>vague abdominal</strong> pain. The most common indications for surgery include <strong>bleeding</strong> and <strong>obstruction.</strong></p>\n<p>The GIST may occur in a <strong>sporadic</strong> form or as part of <strong>familial syndromes.</strong> In sporadic GIST, the most common mutations seen are gain-of-function mutation in KIT (75-80%) and PDGFRA mutation (8%). Microscopically, GIST has <strong>elongated spindle cells</strong> or plumper epithelioid cells, or a mixed pattern.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the microscopy of GIST composed of bundles, or fascicles, of spindle-shaped tumor cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Succinate dehydrogenase</strong> (SDH)-deficient GIST is a subset of wild-type GIST seen with Carney triad or Carney-Stratakis syndrome. </p>\n<p><strong>Imatinib</strong> can be used to treat GIST with mutations in <strong>KIT</strong> or <strong>PDGFRA.</strong> Succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) - deficient GIST is imatinib-resistant, so sunitinib, sorafenib, or regorafenib can be used instead. </p>\n<p>During surgical resection of GIST, care must be taken to prevent capsule rupture, which causes <strong>seeding</strong> of <strong>nearby tissues</strong> leading to <strong>relapse</strong> in <strong>100%</strong> of the <strong>cases.</strong> If capsule rupture occurs, adjuvant therapy with imatinib is indicated regardless of tumor extent.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>KIT (CD117):</strong> This is the most well-known marker for GISTs. About 85% of GISTs show mutations in the KIT gene, leading to the expression of the KIT protein on the cell surface. KIT is a receptor tyrosine kinase, and its activation is a key driver in the pathogenesis of GISTs.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>SDH (Succinate</strong> Dehydrogenase): SDH mutations are associated with a subset of GISTs, particularly those that are KIT and PDGFRA wild-type. Loss of SDH function is characteristic of this subgroup, and SDH deficiency can be demonstrated through immunohistochemistry.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>PDGFRA</strong> (Platelet-Derived Growth Factor Receptor Alpha): Mutations in the PDGFRA gene account for approximately 5-10% of GISTs. Similar to KIT, PDGFRA is a receptor tyrosine kinase, and mutations in this gene can lead to the development of GISTs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zo1ILkff","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"13_diuretics","title":"13.Diuretics","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA5478","text":"Choose the correct pair ma‌tching with the diagram:","raw_text":"Choose the correct pair matching with the diagram:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A: Loop diuretics, B: Carb‌onic anhydrase inhibitors‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A: Loop diuretics, B: Thia‌zides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A: Thiazides, B: Loop diur‌etics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A: Thiazides, B: Carbonic ‌anhydrase inhibitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A: Loop diuretics, B: Thiazides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/701a33667bab4e16a48659217949ffcb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A: Loop diuretics, B: Thiazides</strong></p><p>A is the <strong>thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle </strong>and<strong> loop diuretics </strong>act on this part of the nephron.</p>\n<p>B is<strong> </strong>the <strong>distal convoluted tubule </strong>and<strong> thiazides </strong>act on this part of the nephron.</p>\n<p>Site of action of other diuretics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proximal tubule - carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</li>\n<li>Collecting ducts and late distal tubule - epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) inhibitors and aldosterone antagonists</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5404","text":"Diuretics acting on which ‌part of the nephron have ‍maximum efficacy?","raw_text":"Diuretics acting on which part of the nephron have maximum efficacy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proxima‌l tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thick ascending limb of th‌e loop of Henle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Collecti‌ng tubule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal‌ tubule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle</strong></p><p><strong>Loop diuretics </strong>have the maximum efficacy. They act on the<strong> thick ascending loop of Henle </strong>by <strong>block</strong>ing <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup>-2Cl<sup>-</sup></strong> symporter.</p>\n<p>Option A: Although 65% of Na+ is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, diuretics acting here do not have maximum efficacy. This is because the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle has a great reabsorptive capacity and it absorbs most of the filtrate from the proximal tubule.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Diuretics acting distal to the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle have limited efficacy because most of the reabsorption of Na+ (&gt;90%) would have already occurred by the time the tubular fluid reaches there.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5418","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about furosemide ‍is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about furosemide is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pulmonary edema is a‌n indication","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acts on the proximal convo‌luted tubules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Causes mil‌d diuresis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Given only by parenteral r‌oute","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute pulmonary edema is an indication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute pulmonary edema is an indication</strong></p><p><strong>Furosemide </strong>is indicated in <strong>acute pulmonary edema</strong>. It is a loop diuretic that acts on the<strong> thick ascending loop of Henle </strong>and inhibits Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup>-2Cl<sup>-</sup> symporter.</p>\n<p>Loop diuretics have <strong>maximum efficacy</strong> amongst all the diuretics and are therefore called <strong>high ceiling diuretics</strong>. They can be given by both the oral and parenteral routes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5429","text":"A 70-year-old man with pul‌monary edema is started o‍n furosemide. Which of th​e following would you not‌ expect after starting th​e drug?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man with pulmonary edema is started on furosemide. Which of the following would you not expect after starting the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperg‌lycemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypomag‌nesemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acid‌osis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Furosemide </strong>causes <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The adverse effects of furosemide (loop diuretics) are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypovolemia</li>\n<li>Hyponatremia</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia </li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n<li>Increased LDL-C and decreased HDL-C</li>\n<li>Glucose intolerance - hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Ototoxicity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5427","text":"High ceiling diuretics are‌ used in the treatment of‍ all of the following pat​ients except:","raw_text":"High ceiling diuretics are used in the treatment of all of the following patients except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40-year-old man with anasa‌rca","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"35-year-old woman with a h‌ead injury and cerebral e‍dema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"56-year-old woman with acu‌te pulmonary edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"48-year-old man with pulmo‌nary hypertension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 48-year-old man with pulmonary hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 48-year-old man with pulmonary hypertension</strong></p><p>High ceiling diuretics are not used in pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p><strong>High ceiling</strong> or loop diuretics have <strong>diuretic</strong> properties as well as <strong>vasodilatory</strong> action.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diuretic property - removing excess fluid in hepatic, renal, and cardiac diseases </li>\n<li>Vasodilatory action - quick symptomatic relief of symptoms in <strong>LVF</strong> and <strong>acute</strong> <strong>pulmonary edema</strong>. They are also used for the treatment of <strong>chronic CHF.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary artery hypertension</strong> is a condition where there is increased pulmonary vascular resistance. Since nitric oxide is an important regulator of pulmonary vascular resistance, <strong>PDE5 inhibitors</strong> like sildenafil, vardenafil or tadalafil are primarily used in the treatment of <strong>pulmonary arterial hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5420","text":"A 65-year-old woman with c‌hronic kidney disease was‍ given a diuretic intrave​nously. Following adminis‌tration of the drug, she ​developed tinnitus and di‌minished hearing. Which o‌f the following drugs was​ most likely given?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease was given a diuretic intravenously. Following administration of the drug, she developed tinnitus and diminished hearing. Which of the following drugs was most likely given?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‌zide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spirino‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethacry‌nic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triam‌terene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ethacrynic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ethacrynic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Ethacrynic acid </strong>is the diuretic that was most likely given as it is commonly associated with<strong> ototoxicity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ototoxicity occurs with <strong>rapid intravenous infusion</strong> and the hearing impairment and deafness are usually <strong>reversible.</strong></p>\n<p>Ethacrynic acid should be used only in patients who are not able to tolerate other loop diuretics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5409","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‌ors do not cause:","raw_text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors do not cause:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased bicarbonate excr‌etion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urinary alk‌alinization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic‌ alkalosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Potassi‌um loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p><strong>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</strong> do not<strong> </strong>cause metabolic alkalosis<strong>.</strong> They cause<strong> metabolic acidosis </strong>due to<strong> increased bicarbonate excretion </strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Increased bicarbonate excretion causes urinary alkalinization.</p>\n<p>Option D: Increased delivery of Na<sup>+</sup> to distal tubules causes increased K<sup>+</sup> excretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5411","text":"Which of the following can‌ be administered intraven‍ously?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be administered intravenously?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brinzo‌lamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorzo‌lamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methaz‌olamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetazolamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetazolamide</strong></p><p><strong>Acetazolamide </strong>is a <strong>carbonic anhydrase inhibitor</strong> that can be administered<strong> intravenously</strong>. It is indicated in the <strong>acute</strong> management of <strong>angle-closure glaucoma</strong> for the rapid reduction of IOP before surgery. The oral formulation is given for open-angle glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Brinzolamide and dorzolamide are available only as ophthalmic drops and used in open-angle glaucoma.</p>\n<p>Option D: Methazolamide is available as an oral formulation and used for open-angle glaucoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9852","text":"A 30-year-old man joins a ‌pilgrimage to a shrine at‍ a mountain peak. At the ​summit, he develops tight‌ness in the chest, associ​ated with breathlessness ‌and altered sensorium. Wh‌ich of the following can ​be given for symptomatic ​relief?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man joins a pilgrimage to a shrine at a mountain peak. At the summit, he develops tightness in the chest, associated with breathlessness and altered sensorium. Which of the following can be given for symptomatic relief?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mann‌itol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acetazolamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acetazolamide</strong></p><p>The symptoms are suggestive of<strong> acute mountain sickness </strong>and<strong> acetazolamide</strong> can be given for<strong> symptomatic relief</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can also be used for prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2947","text":"A patient with a glioblast‌oma multiforme is started‍ on mannitol as palliativ​e therapy. Which of the f‌ollowing statements is fa​lse regarding its diureti‌c action?","raw_text":"A patient with a glioblastoma multiforme is started on mannitol as palliative therapy. Which of the following statements is false regarding its diuretic action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal blood flow is increa‌sed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction in medullary ton‌icity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extracellular fluid compar‌tment is expanded","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GFR is d‌ecreased","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. GFR is decreased","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. GFR is decreased</strong></p><p><strong>Mannitol</strong> is an <strong>osmotic diuretic. </strong>These drugs do <strong>no</strong>t cause much<strong> change </strong>in the<strong> GFR.</strong></p>\n<p>Osmotic diuretics cause <strong>extraction</strong> of <strong>fluid</strong> from the <strong>intracellular</strong> compartment resulting in the <strong>expansion</strong> of the <strong>extracellular</strong> compartment. The <strong>renal blood flow increases</strong> and <strong>renin</strong> release is <strong>inhibited</strong>. An increase in renal medullary blood flow removes NaCl and urea from the renal medulla thus reducing medullary tonicity. There is not much change in the total GFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5412","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of swe‍lling of both feet and of​ breathlessness on exerti‌on. After evaluation, she​ was diagnosed with conge‌stive cardiac failure. Wh‌ich of the following drug​s is contraindicated in t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with complaints of swelling of both feet and of breathlessness on exertion. After evaluation, she was diagnosed with congestive cardiac failure. Which of the following drugs is contraindicated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mann‌itol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrochlo‌rothiazide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mannitol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mannitol</strong></p><p><strong>Mannitol</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with cardiac failure.</p>\n<p>Mannitol is an <strong>osmotic</strong> diuretic. It acts by extracting water from the intracellular compartments, which results in the <strong>expansion</strong> of the<strong> extracellular fluid</strong>. This results in <strong>volume overload</strong> and frank <strong>pulmonary edema</strong> in patients with cardiac failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5510","text":"A patient with hypertensio‌n is prescribed chlorthal‍idone. What would be the ​effect of this drug on th‌e excretion of electrolyt​es? Sodium Potassium Calc‌ium a. ↑ ↑ ↑ b. ↑ ↓ ↓ c. ‌↑ ↑ ↓ d. ↑ ↓ ↑","raw_text":"<p>A patient with hypertension is prescribed chlorthalidone. What would be the effect of this drug on the excretion of electrolytes?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px; width: 311px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.0312px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 93.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Sodium </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Potassium </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.5312px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Calcium </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.0312px; text-align: center;\">a.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.5312px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.0312px; text-align: center;\">b.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↓</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.5312px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↓</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.0312px; text-align: center;\">c.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.5312px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↓</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.0312px; text-align: center;\">d.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.2188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↓</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77.5312px; text-align: center;\"><strong>↑</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p><strong>Chlorthalidone </strong>is a<strong> thiazide</strong> diuretic which <strong>increases Na<sup>+</sup></strong> and<strong> K<sup>+</sup></strong> <strong>excretion</strong> and <strong>decreases Ca<sup>2+</sup> excretion</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5432","text":"Thiazide diuretics are not‌ used in the treatment of‍ which of the following c​onditions?","raw_text":"Thiazide diuretics are not used in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Idiopathic hypercalciuria ‌with nephrocalcinosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperli‌pidemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrogenic diabetes insip‌idus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperlipidemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperlipidemia</strong></p><p><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong> are not used in the treatment of hyperlipidemia<strong>. </strong>They result in an<strong> increase</strong> in the levels of<strong> LDL, total cholesterol, and triglycerides.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Thiazide reduces Ca<sup>2+</sup> excretion and is used in the treatment of <strong>idiopathic hypercalciuria with nephrocalcinosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: They are also used in the treatment of edema associated with <strong>CHF</strong>, hepatic cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option D: Thiazides are the mainstay for the treatment of <strong>nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong>. They reduce the kidney's ability to excrete free water. They increase proximal tubular water absorption and block the ability of DCT to form dilute urine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5434","text":"A 45-year-old man with hyp‌ertension is prescribed c‍hlorthalidone. Which of t​he following is unlikely ‌to occur in this patient?​","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man with hypertension is prescribed chlorthalidone. Which of the following is unlikely to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erectile d‌ysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoca‌lcemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypocalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypocalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypocalcemia</strong> is <strong>unlikely</strong> to occur in a patient on chlorthalidone, a thiazide diuretic.</p>\n<p><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong> do not cause hypocalcemia. They result in <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thiazide and loop diuretics share similar side effects except that loop diuretics cause Ca<sup>2+</sup>excretion (<strong>loop loses calcium</strong>) whereas thiazides retain Ca<sup>2+</sup> and cause hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>Other side effects of thiazide diuretics are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Hyponatremia</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n<li>Metabolic alkalosis</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5441","text":"A 42-year-old man in the n‌ephrology ward has a GFR ‍of less than 30mL/min, su​ggestive of severe renal ‌failure. What is the best​ drug that can be given t‌o him?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man in the nephrology ward has a GFR of less than 30mL/min, suggestive of severe renal failure. What is the best drug that can be given to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorth‌alidone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metol‌azone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Xipa‌mide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrochlo‌rothiazide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metolazone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metolazone</strong></p><p><strong>Metolazone </strong>is<strong> effective</strong> in<strong> severe renal failure </strong>(GFR&lt;30ml/min).</p>\n<p>Metolazone works primarily in the<strong> distal convoluted tubule</strong>, rather than the proximal convoluted tubule, and has <strong>little effect on GFR</strong> and can hence be used in patients with a reduced GFR. <strong>Indapamide</strong> is another thiazide diuretic that may be effective in severe renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5437","text":"A patient has been on lisi‌nopril since being diagno‍sed with hypertension las​t year. His blood pressur‌e readings are quite high​, and the doctor consider‌s adding a diuretic. Whic‌h amongst the following w​ould be the ideal add on ​drug?","raw_text":"A patient has been on lisinopril since being diagnosed with hypertension last year. His blood pressure readings are quite high, and the doctor considers adding a diuretic. Which amongst the following would be the ideal add on drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epler‌enone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrochlo‌rthiazide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amil‌oride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydrochlorthiazide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydrochlorthiazide</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> would be the ideal add-on drug. It is a <strong>thiazide diuretic</strong> and causes <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, which can counteract the <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> caused by lisinopril, an <strong>ACE inhibitor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aldosterone causes Na<sup>+</sup> absorption in the collecting duct and the distal tubule in exchange for H<sup>+</sup> and K<sup>+</sup>. ACE inhibitors decrease angiotensin II levels and thereby, decrease aldosterone levels. Decreased aldosterone level causes K<sup>+</sup> retention by reducing the exchange. Therefore, ACE inhibitors cause retention of K<sup>+</sup>.</p>\n<p>Diuretics that cause elevation of plasma K<sup>+</sup> levels are not suitable for combining with ACE inhibitors.</p>\n<p><strong>Potassium-sparing diuretics</strong> like spironolactone, eplerenone, and amiloride are <strong>contraindicated</strong> with<strong> ACE inhibitors</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5453","text":"A 10-year-old boy is broug‌ht with complaints of vom‍iting and lethargy after ​swallowing several pills ‌of his father’s medicatio​n. Serum electrolytes ind‌icate hyperkalemia. Which‌ of the following drugs c​an you rule out?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy is brought with complaints of vomiting and lethargy after swallowing several pills of his father’s medication. Serum electrolytes indicate hyperkalemia. Which of the following drugs can you rule out?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Triam‌terene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amil‌oride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indap‌amide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Indapamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Indapamide</strong></p><p><strong>Indapamide</strong> can be ruled out as it is a <strong>thiazide</strong> diuretic that causes <strong>hypokalemia</strong>. The other drugs are potassium-sparing diuretics that cause hyperkalemia.</p>\n<p>K<sup>+</sup> sparing diuretics work by 2 mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blocking</strong> the <strong>ENaC</strong> (epithelial sodium channel)\n<ul>\n<li>Triamterene</li>\n<li>Amiloride</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> receptor <strong>antagonist</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Spironolactone</li>\n<li>Eplerenone</li>\n<li>Canrenone</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5455","text":"Which of the following diu‌retics do not need to be ‍present in the tubular lu​men for pharmacological a‌ction?","raw_text":"Which of the following diuretics do not need to be present in the tubular lumen for pharmacological action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loop di‌uretics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibit‌ors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aldosterone‌ antagonists","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aldosterone antagonists","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aldosterone antagonists</strong></p><p><strong>Aldosterone antagonist</strong> acts from the<strong> interstitial side of the tubular cells</strong>. They do not have to be present in the tubular lumen for their action.</p>\n<p>Aldosterone antagonists enter the tubular cells from the basolateral membrane. They <strong>competitively inhibit</strong> the <strong>binding</strong> of <strong>aldosterone</strong> to mineralocorticoid receptors. </p>\n<p>Thiazide diuretics, loop diuretics, ENaC inhibitors, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors have to be present in the lumen for its action. They are all secreted from the proximal tubule into the tubular fluid through the organic anion/ cation transport process.</p>\n<p>Osmotic diuretics reach the lumen via glomerular filtration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5472","text":"Canrenone is a metabolite ‌of:","raw_text":"Canrenone is a metabolite of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epler‌enone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metol‌azone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spironolactone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spironolactone</strong></p><p><strong>Canrenone</strong> is a metabolite of <strong>spironolactone. </strong></p>\n<p>Spironolactone is metabolized completely in the liver. It is also converted to an <strong>active metabolite</strong>, canrenone that is responsible for more than 50% of the spironolactone action. Spironolactone has a half-life of 1-2 hours while canrenone has a half-life of around 18 hours.</p>\n<p>Eplerenone is an aldosterone receptor antagonist similar to spironolactone and metabolized primarily by CYP3A4, but no active metabolites have been identified.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5462","text":"A 52-year-old man with alc‌oholic cirrhosis develops‍ ascites. What is the dru​g of choice for managemen‌t?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis develops ascites. What is the drug of choice for management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorot‌hiazide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaz‌olamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mann‌itol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spironolactone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spironolactone</strong></p><p>The diuretic of choice in a patient with<strong> cirrhotic ascites </strong>is <strong>spironolactone.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Spironolactone</strong> is a <strong>competitive inhibitor</strong> of the <strong>mineralocorticoid receptor</strong> in the <strong>late distal tubule</strong> and<strong> collecting duct</strong> of the kidneys. It causes a decrease in sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the distal tubule. As a result, spironolactone <strong>promotes sodium diuresis</strong> but <strong>maintains body potassium levels</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spironolactone is particularly helpful in edematous states caused or exacerbated by hyperaldosteronism such as in cirrhotic ascites.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5458","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a side effect seen in‍ a patient receiving spir​onolactone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a side effect seen in a patient receiving spironolactone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gynaec‌omastia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic‌ alkalosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ins‌ufficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p><strong>Aldosterone antagonists</strong> cause<strong> h</strong><strong>yperchloremic metabolic acidosis</strong> and not metabolic alkalosis. They <strong>inhibit</strong> <strong>H<sup><span class=\"s1\">+ </span></sup>secretion</strong> in parallel with K<sup><span class=\"s1\">+ </span></sup>secretion thus resulting in acidosis.</p>\n<p>Other side effects are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n<li>Renal insufficiency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5475","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for ami‍loride use?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for amiloride use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lithium indu‌ced polyuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium nep‌hrolithiasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Liddle ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium nephrolithiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium nephrolithiasis</strong></p><p>Amiloride is not used in the treatment of <strong>calcium nephrolithiasis.</strong> The condition is sometimes treated with <strong>thiazide diuretics</strong> but not amiloride.</p>\n<p><strong>Amiloride </strong>is a<strong> K+ sparing diuretic</strong> that acts by <strong>inhibiting </strong>the<strong> epithelial Na+ channels. </strong>The various uses of amiloride are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Edema/hypertension</strong> - usually in combination with thiazides</li>\n<li><strong>Lithium-induced polyuria</strong> - blocks lithium transport into collecting tubules</li>\n<li><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong> - aerosolized amiloride enhances mucociliary clearance; acts by inhibiting Na+ absorption from epithelial surfaces and increasing hydration of secretions </li>\n<li><strong>Liddle syndrome</strong> - hyperactive epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) activity is reduced</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9885","text":"A 35-year-old man with ast‌hma presents with complai‍nts of increased urinatio​n. The doctor checks that‌ he has been taking theop​hylline and counsels that‌ it could be a side effec‌t. What is the mechanism ​of action causing diuresi​s?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with asthma presents with complaints of increased urination. The doctor checks that he has been taking theophylline and counsels that it could be a side effect. What is the mechanism of action causing diuresis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenosine ‌antagonism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PDE-4 in‌hibition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-2 ‌agonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PDE-3 in‌hibition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adenosine antagonism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adenosine antagonism</strong></p><p>The <strong>diuretic effect</strong> of <strong>theophylline</strong> is due to the <strong>blockade of adenosine </strong>(A1)<strong> receptors.</strong></p>\n<p>Theophylline is a methylxanthine drug that acts by -</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inhibition </strong>of phosphodiesterase enzyme (<strong>PDE</strong>)<strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PDE3</strong> inhibition - relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle </li>\n<li><strong>PDE4</strong> inhibition - inhibition cytokines and chemokine release, which leads to a decrease in immune cell migration and activation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Blockade </strong>of<strong> adenosine receptors</strong><strong> </strong>inhibits bronchoconstriction, however,<strong> blockade of A1 receptors</strong> in the glomerular afferent arteriole and PCT causes <strong>diuresis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Activating </strong>enzyme<strong> histone deacetylase-2</strong>, which is responsible for its anti-inflammatory action</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OHGKvdez","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"13_special_senses","title":"13.Special Senses","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB0579","text":"Light entering the eye fir‍st passes through ______ ​retinal layer.","raw_text":"Light entering the eye first passes through ______ retinal layer.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner nuc‍lear layer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Outer nuc‍lear layer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Outer plex‍iform layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retinal gan‍glion layer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retinal ganglion layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf8907be34f34986ac9f555b178ccb40x1050x1542.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retinal ganglion layer</strong></p><p>Light passes first through the <strong>retinal ganglion layer</strong>, the most <strong>anterior/inner</strong> layer of the retina. Light then passes through other layers of the retina until it reaches the <strong>photoreceptors</strong> in the <strong>posterior/outer region</strong> of the retina. </p>\n<p><strong>Retina is organized into 10 layers or zones where distinctive components are clustered together to form continuous strata</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Light passes in the direction</strong>: Vitreous humor(anterior)→ choroid(most posterior) encountering the cell layers in that direction (ganglion cell layer→photoreceptors)</li>\n<li><strong>Visual processing/Phototransduction occurs in the opposite direction</strong> (Photoreceptors→ ganglion cell layer)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 619\"> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0583","text":"Initiation of visual impul‍se is associated with____​_","raw_text":"Initiation of visual impulse is associated with_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condensation of opsin with‍ Vitamin A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Photoisomerisation and hyd‍rolysis of visual purple","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NA‍DP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N‍AD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Photoisomerisation and hydrolysis of visual purple","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea2bd870bfb3409693a0865da7cef4fex1280x2260.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Photoisomerisation and hydrolysis of visual purple</strong></p><p>Initiation of visual impulse is associated with photoisomerisation and hydrolysis of visual purple</p>\n<p><strong>Rhodopsin or visual purple</strong>, the photosensitive pigment, is composed of <strong>opsin</strong> (a protein belonging to the superfamily of G protein–coupled receptors) and <strong>retinal</strong> (an aldehyde of vitamin A). When light strikes the photoreceptors, retinal is chemically transformed in a process called <strong>photoisomerization</strong>, which begins the transduction process.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0584","text":"Which form of vitamin A co‍mbines with opsin to make​ rhodopsin?","raw_text":"Which form of vitamin A combines with opsin to make rhodopsin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All trans r‍etinaldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"11-cis –re‍tinaldehyde","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"All tran‍s retinol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11-cis-‍ retinol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 11-cis –retinaldehyde","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aae387b48da5422da5b08a5ccedcdbafx635x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 11-cis –retinaldehyde</strong></p><p><strong>11-cis –retinaldehyde </strong>combines with opsin to make rhodopsin.</p>\n<p><strong>All-trans retinol</strong> is one form of Vitamin A which is converted to <strong>11-cis retinol</strong> under the influence of Isomerase. Finally, 11-cis retinol is converted into <strong>11-cis retinal</strong> which combines with <strong>Scotopsin to form rhodopsin</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0582","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true regarding​ distribution of rods and‍ cones in retina?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding distribution of rods and cones in retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macula lutea is the blind ‍spot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"low density of photorecept‍ors are present in macula​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foveola contains only cone‍s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral retina has grea‍ter cones to rods ratio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foveola contains only cones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foveola contains only cones</strong></p><p><strong>Central fovea (foveola)</strong> contains <strong>only cones </strong>(rod-free area) and is a region of <strong>highest resolution</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional Variations in the Retina</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Macula lutea</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Located at posterior pole, <strong>temporal to the optic disc</strong></li>\n<li>Area of central vision</li>\n<li><strong>High density of photoreceptors</strong> and interneurons</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Optic disc</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lacks photoreceptors</li>\n<li>Is called<strong> “Blind spot”</strong></li>\n<li>Normally a person is unaware of blind spot because\n<ul>\n<li>Corresponding part of the visual field can be seen by the contralateral eye</li>\n<li>Neurological processes complete the incomplete image perceptually</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Peripheral retina</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Higher ratio of rods to cones =<strong>Rod to cone→20:1</strong></li>\n<li>Greater degree of convergence of photoreceptors to the target interneuron (bipolar cells)\n<ul>\n<li>More sensitivity to light</li>\n<li>Lower visual acuity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0585","text":"The rod receptor potential‍ differs from other surfa​ce receptors in that it s‍hows ______.","raw_text":"The rod receptor potential differs from other surface receptors in that it shows ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depolar‍ization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased ‍negativity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased conductance of s‍odium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperpol‍arization","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperpolarization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a945da8997b4945b28a54e346735ef5x1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperpolarization</strong></p><p>The rod receptor potential differs from other surface receptors in that it shows <strong>hyperpolarization.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>In most sensory receptors</strong>: Arrival of stimulus → Ion channels are opened up allowing current to flow =&gt; <strong>depolarisation</strong> of the receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>In photoreceptors</strong><strong>:</strong> When the light strikes the outer segment, a cascade of reactions (refer figure) are initiated, that close some of the Na<sup>+</sup> channels in the outer segment and results in <strong>hyperpolarization</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0587","text":"Which is the first-order n‍euron in the optic pathwa​y?","raw_text":"Which is the first-order neuron in the optic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rods an‍d cones","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ganglio‍n cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cells in lateral geniculat‍e body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bipola‍r cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rods and cones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rods and cones</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Photoreceptors - rods and cones</strong> are the first-order neurons in the <strong>visual pathway</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0589","text":"Purely depolarizing cells ‍in optic pathway are ____​__","raw_text":"Purely depolarizing cells in optic pathway are ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bipola‍r cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Co‍nes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amacrin‍e cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ro‍ds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amacrine cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amacrine cells</strong></p><p>The responses of the <strong>rods, cones, and horizontal cells</strong> are <strong>hyperpolarizing</strong>, and the responses of the bipolar cells are either hyperpolarizing or depolarizing, whereas <strong>amacrine cells</strong> produce <strong>purely depolarizing potential.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0591","text":"Amacrine cells secrete____‍_","raw_text":"Amacrine cells secrete_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl‍choline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sero‍tonin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hist‍amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glut‍amate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acetylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acetylcholine</strong></p><p>Amacrine cells release <strong>acetylcholine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Amacrine cells are one of the neuronal cell types of the retina. They transmit signals in two directions, either directly from bipolar cells to ganglion cells or horizontally within the inner plexiform layer from axons of the bipolar cells to dendrites of the ganglion cells or to other amacrine cells.</p>\n<p>Amacrine cells release GABA, glycine, dopamine, acetylcholine &amp; indolamine.</p>\n<p>Other neuronal cell types of the retina are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The photoreceptors: rods &amp; cones</li>\n<li>Horizontal cells</li>\n<li>Bipolar cells</li>\n<li>Ganglion cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Of these cell types, only neurotransmitters secreted by photoreceptors &amp; amacrine cells have been discovered. <strong>Rods and cones</strong> release <strong>glutamate </strong>at their synapses with the bipolar cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0592","text":"The only neurons in retina‍ showing action potential​ are _____","raw_text":"The only neurons in retina showing action potential are _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bipola‍r cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ganglio‍n cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amacrin‍e cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rods an‍d cones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ganglion cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ganglion cells</strong></p><p>All neural elements in the retina exhibit local graded potentials <strong>except ganglion cells</strong> which shows <strong>action potential</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0593","text":"The fibers from contralate‍ral nasal retina project ​to the following layers o‍f lateral geniculate nucl‍eus______","raw_text":"The fibers from contralateral nasal retina project to the following layers of lateral geniculate nucleus______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,‍3,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‍4,6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,‍2,3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4,‍5,6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1,4,6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/adf3d164e598418ebea4981fef800d61x1280x2373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1,4,6</strong></p><p>On each side, layers <strong>1, 4, and 6</strong> of the lateral geniculate nucleus receive input from the <strong>contralateral eye</strong>, whereas layers <strong>2, 3, and 5</strong> receive input from the<strong> ipsilateral eye.</strong> (Refer figure).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0594","text":"Parvocellular pathway carr‍ies signal for detection ​of_____","raw_text":"Parvocellular pathway carries signal for detection of_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Color c‍ontrast","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Luminous‍ contrast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temporal ‍frequency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saccadic ey‍e movements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Color contrast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5da6e15e9a004e8293b9eba5eaa654a2x1280x2373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Color contrast</strong></p><p>From the Lateral Geniculate Nucleus (LGN), a magnocellular pathway (from layers 1 and 2) and a parvocellular pathway (from the rest of the layers) project to the visual cortex.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 150px;\" width=\"606\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Parvocellular pathway</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Magnocellular pathway </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Receive input entirely from type X retinal ganglion cells, and transmit color.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Formed by Y ganglion cells project to magnocellular layers of the lateral geniculate nucleus.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Convey accurate point-to-point spatial information.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Colorblind, transmitting only black-and-white information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Terminate in layer 4C of the primary visual cortex.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Terminate in layer 4C of the primary visual cortex.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Carries signals for color vision, texture, shape, and fine details.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">Carries signals for detection of movement, depth, and flicker.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0595","text":"The axons from lateral gen‍iculate nucleus terminate​ in _____ layer of primar‍y visual cortex.","raw_text":"The axons from lateral geniculate nucleus terminate in _____ layer of primary visual cortex.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lay‍er 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lay‍er 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Layer ‍2 and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Layer ‍5 and 6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Layer 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Layer 4</strong></p><p>The axons from the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) mostly<strong> </strong>end in <strong>layer 4.</strong></p>\n<p>The primary visual receiving area (primary visual cortex) is located principally on the sides of the calca­rine fissure and has <strong>6 layers</strong> similar to the neocortex. Layer 4 recieves most of the input from the LGN, which is divided further into 4A, 4B, 4Cα, and 4Cβ.</p>\n<p>The magnocellular pathways from layers 1 and 2 of the LGN end in layer 4Cα while the parvocellular pathways from layers 3-6 of the LGN end in layer 4Cβ. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0600","text":"An ophthalmologist explain‍s to a patient that he is​ a protanope. These patie‍nts have difficulty in id‍entifying which of the fo​llowing color?","raw_text":"An ophthalmologist explains to a patient that he is a protanope. These patients have difficulty in identifying which of the following color?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‍ed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gr‍een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bl‍ue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bl‍ack","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Red","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Red</strong></p><p><strong>Protanopes</strong> are dichromats with <strong>difficulty</strong> in identifying <strong>red color.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anomalous Trichromatic Vision</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Deficiency of red is Protanomaly.</li>\n<li>Deficiency of green is Deuteranomaly.</li>\n<li>Deficiency of blue is Tritanomaly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Dichromatic Vision (Individuals with only two cone systems)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Blindness to red color Protanopia (Protanope).</li>\n<li>Blindness to green color Deuteranopia (Deuteranope).</li>\n<li>Blindness to blue color Tritanopia (Tritanope).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Monochromatic Vision </strong>Have only one cone system.</li>\n<li><strong>Color blindness is X-linked recessive.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Achromatopsia/ Achromasia-</strong> Color blindness is due to lesions of area <strong>V8</strong> of the visual cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0602","text":"Auditory information is re‍layed through ______ thal​amic nucleus.","raw_text":"Auditory information is relayed through ______ thalamic nucleus.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsomedi‍al nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral geniculate nucleus‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial genic‍ulate nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral Posterolateral nuc‍leus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medial geniculate nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medial geniculate nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>medial geniculate nucleus</strong> is the thalamic nucleus that conveys <strong>auditory information</strong> from the brainstem to the primary auditory cortex.</p>\n<p><strong>Lateral geniculate nucleus</strong> is the thalamic nucleus that conveys <strong>visual information.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0603","text":"In the auditory pathway, w‍hich is the first structu​re that can determine the‍ direction of the perceiv‍ed sound?","raw_text":"In the auditory pathway, which is the first structure that can determine the direction of the perceived sound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cochlear‍ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior ‍colliculus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral ‍lemniscus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior oli‍vary nucleus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superior olivary nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superior olivary nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>superior olivary nuclei</strong> receive information from both ears and <strong>begin the process of detecting the direction</strong> from which a sound comes.</p>\n<p>The eighth nerves reach their respective cohlear nuclei in the medulla from where the nerves travel to <strong>both</strong> the <strong>ipsilateral</strong> and <strong>contralteral</strong> superior olivary nuclei in the pons and hence giving it the ability to detect the direction of the sound.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lateral</strong> part of superior olivary nucleus does so by <strong>comparing the difference in intensities of sound</strong> reaching the two ears, whereas the <strong>medial</strong> part of the superior olivary nucleus <strong>detects the time lag</strong> between the signals entering the two ears.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0604","text":"Which of the following eve‍nts prompts the auditory ​system to interpret a sou‍nd as loud?","raw_text":"Which of the following events prompts the auditory system to interpret a sound as loud?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased number of inner ‍hair cells become stimula​ted","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased number of outer ‍hair cells become stimula​ted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hair cells excite nerve en‍dings at a diminished rat​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amplitude of vibration of ‍the basilar membrane incr​eases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amplitude of vibration of the basilar membrane increases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amplitude of vibration of the basilar membrane increases</strong></p><p>There are at least three ways that the auditory system determines loudness:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>As the sound becomes louder- the <strong>amplitude of vibration of the basilar membrane</strong> increases so that hair cells excite the nerve endings at more rapid rates.</li>\n<li>As the amplitude increases- more and more hair cells on the fringes of the resonating portion of the basilar membrane become stimulated.</li>\n<li>As the amplitude of vibration reaches high intensity, the outer hair cells become recruited at a significant rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0605","text":"Umami taste is evoked by__‍__","raw_text":"Umami taste is evoked by____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glu‍cose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Qui‍nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glut‍amate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium ‍chloride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glutamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glutamate</strong></p><p><strong>Umami taste</strong> is triggered particularly by the <strong>monosodium glutamate (MSG)</strong> used so extensively in Asian cooking.</p>\n<p><strong>Taste Receptors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Humans have five established basic tastes: sweet, salt, sour, bitter and <strong>umami.</strong></li>\n<li>The taste receptors for these five basic tastes are of two major types:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ligand-gated channels (ionotropic receptors)</strong>: for salt and sour taste</li>\n<li><strong>G-Protein Coupled Receptors (metabotropic receptors)</strong>: for sweet, bitter and umami</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Salt and sour taste are mediated through epithelial sodium channels (ENaC)\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Na<sup>+</sup> </strong>movement through ENaC →<strong>salt</strong> taste</li>\n<li><strong>H<sup>+ </sup></strong>movement through ENaC → <strong>sour</strong> taste</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sweet, bitter, and umami are sensed via <strong>GPCRs</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>T1R2 and T1R3</strong> families→ <strong>sweet</strong> taste</li>\n<li><strong>T2R family</strong> → <strong>bitter taste</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Metabotropic glutamate receptor (mGluR4) → Umami</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0606","text":"Mitral cells are seen in _‍_____","raw_text":"Mitral cells are seen in ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"left ‍atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olfacto‍ry bulb","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary vi‍sual cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genicul‍ate body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Olfactory bulb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c0553212ef841208c2c302917d5842bx800x543.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Olfactory bulb</strong></p><p>Mitral cells are seen in <strong>Olfactory bulb.</strong></p>\n<p>Axons of Olfactory nerves (First cranial nerve) synapse with the <strong>mitral cells and tufted cells</strong> in the olfactory bulb to form anatomically discrete units called olfactory glomeruli.</p>\n<p>The olfactory bulb also contains periglomerular cells that are inhibitory interneurons and <strong>granule cells </strong>that make reciprocal synapses with mitral and tufted cells.</p>\n<p>These mitral and tufted cells (second-order neurons) terminate in Olfactory cortex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0608","text":"Which of the following sen‍sory systems has the smal​lest range of intensity d‍iscrimination?","raw_text":"Which of the following sensory systems has the smallest range of intensity discrimination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Audi‍tory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vis‍ual","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Olfa‍ctory","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Somato‍sensory","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Olfactory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Olfactory</strong></p><p>The olfactory system has the smallest range of intensity discrimination.</p>\n<p>Smell is concerned more with <strong>detecting the presence or absence of odors</strong> , than with the quantitative detection of their intensities. Concentrations that are only 10 to 50 times above threshold values will evoke maximum intensity of smell, which is in contrast to most other sensory system of the body.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0581","text":"Number of cones in the ret‍ina (millions) is_______","raw_text":"Number of cones in the retina (millions) is_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‍-6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5-‍12","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10‍-15","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‍0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0885c0156d8741b6afb9aaf45a3058ddx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3-6</strong></p><p>There are approximately <strong>6 million cones</strong> and <strong>120 million rods</strong> in each human eye.</p>\n<p><strong>Features of Photoreceptors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each rod and cone are divided into <strong>outer segment</strong>, <strong>inner segment</strong>, <strong>nuclear region</strong> and a <strong>synaptic zone</strong>.</li>\n<li>Saccules and discs in the outer segment contain photosensitive pigments.</li>\n<li>If a person spends a considerable length of time in brightly lighted surroundings and then moves to a dimly lighted environment, the retinas slowly become “<strong>accustomed to the dark</strong>.” This decline in visual threshold is known as <strong><strong>dark adaptation</strong></strong>.<strong><strong> </strong></strong>It is nearly maximal in about <strong>20 min, much longer than the time taken for light adaptation which is 5 min</strong>.</li>\n<li>When one passes suddenly from a dim to a brightly lighted environment, the light seems intensely and even uncomfortably bright until the eyes adapt to the increased illumination and the visual threshold rises. This adaptation occurs over a period of about 5 min and is called <strong>light adaptation</strong>.<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3066","text":"What is the site of lesion‍ in Wernicke's hemianopic​ pupil?","raw_text":"What is the site of lesion in Wernicke's hemianopic pupil?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic‍ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic‍ tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Optic r‍adiation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optic ‍chiasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Optic tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Optic tract</strong></p><p>The site of lesion in Wernicke's hemianopic pupil is <strong>optic tract.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Wernicke hemianopic pupil</strong> is the light reflex, where, illumination of the blind half of the retina shows no pupillary reaction while stimulating the normal side of the retina shows a pupillary reaction. It is seen in lesions involving the <strong>optic</strong> <strong>tract</strong>. It is also seen in cases such as <strong>TB,</strong> <strong>syphilis</strong> and <strong>tumours</strong> of the <strong>optic thalamus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Homonymous</strong> <strong>hemianopia (HH)</strong> involves loss of vision on the same side of the visual field in both eyes. HH is characteristically seen in lesions involving the visual pathway <strong>posterior to the optic chiasma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yyfozayG","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"13_alcohol_related_disorders","title":"13.Alcohol Related Disorders","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA5966","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common substance​ abused in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common substance abused in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cann‌abis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inha‌lants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caff‌eine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alcohol</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>alcohol</strong> is the most commonly abused substance. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Cannabis</strong> is the most commonly used <strong>illegal</strong> <strong>drug</strong> in the <strong>world.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5872","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a criterion for subst​ance use disorder under D​SM-5?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a criterion for substance use disorder under DSM-5?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Substance is taken in larg‌er amounts or for longer ​than intended","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Use despite substance-rela‌ted legal problems","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prior unsuccessful efforts‌ to control substance use​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Use causes arguments with ‌spouse and domestic abuse​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Use despite substance-related legal problems","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Use despite substance-related legal problems</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 639\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The use of illegal drugs or drug-related legal problems is not an exclusive criterion to diagnose substance use disorder under DSM-5. </p>\n<p>Substance use disorder can occur with legally available substances like alcohol, tobacco and some prescription drugs.</p>\n<p><strong>Substance use disorder </strong>is defined as a maladaptive pattern of substance use leading to clinically significant impairment. It is manifested by <strong>2 or more</strong> out of 11 criteria occurring within a <strong>12-month period.</strong></p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for diagnosing substance use disorder:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 639\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ol>\n<li>Recurrent substance use<strong> </strong>resulting in <strong>failure to fulfill obligations</strong> at work, school, or home. e.g., substance-related absences or expulsions from school.</li>\n<li>Recurrent substance use even<strong> </strong>in <strong>physically hazardous situations</strong> e.g., driving an automobile when impaired by substance use.</li>\n<li>Continued substance use despite having <strong>persistent social problems</strong> caused by the effects of the substance e.g., arguments with the spouse.</li>\n<li>Continued substance use despite having a<strong> physical or psychological problem</strong> that is likely to have been caused by the substance.</li>\n<li>The substance is often taken in <strong>larger amounts</strong> or over a <strong>longer period</strong> than was intended.</li>\n<li><strong>Efforts</strong> are made to cut down or <strong>control</strong> substance use but are <strong>unsuccessful.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tolerance - </strong>there is markedly diminished desired effect with continued use of the same amount of the substance, hence there is a need for increased amounts of the substance to achieve intoxication.</li>\n<li><strong>Withdrawal - </strong>developing characteristic withdrawal symptoms for the substance, which can be relieved by taking the same or closely related substance.</li>\n<li><strong>Craving</strong> or a strong desire or urge to use a specific substance.</li>\n<li>A great deal of time is spent to obtain the substance, use the substance, or recover from its effects.</li>\n<li>Important<strong> activities</strong> are<strong> given up</strong> or reduced because of substance use.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1370","text":"A 49-year-old man was brou‌ght to the casualty by th​e local police after they​ found him lying in a roa‌dside drain. On examinati​on, his speech was slurre‌d and he was unable to re​call any of the events th​at happened in the past 2‍ hours. His serum alcohol‌ level was 260 mg/dl. His​ cognitive deficits can b‍e attributed to the actio‌n of alcohol on which of ‌the following receptors?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old man was brought to the casualty by the local police after they found him lying in a roadside drain. On examination, his speech was slurred and he was unable to recall any of the events that happened in the past 2 hours. His serum alcohol level was 260 mg/dl. His cognitive deficits can be attributed to the action of alcohol on which of the following receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GABA-A ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GABA-B ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NMDA r‌eceptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glycine ‌receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NMDA receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NMDA receptor</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes a case of an <strong>alcoholic blackout</strong>. <strong>NMDA receptor</strong> is implicated in many aspects of cognitive function including learning and memory. </p>\n<p>\"Blackouts\" are periods of <strong>anterograde memory loss</strong> that occur due to alcohol intoxication when the blood level of alcohol is between<strong> 200-300</strong> mg/dl. It is due to the <strong>inhibition </strong>of the action of <strong>glutamate </strong>on <strong>NMDA receptors.  </strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Impairment likely to be seen at various blood alcohol levels is shown below: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 365px;\" width=\"392\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 76.859375px;\"><strong>Level (mg/dL)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 299.15625px;\"><strong>Impairment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 76.859375px;\">\n<p>20–30 </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 299.15625px;\">Slowed motor performance and decreased thinking ability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 76.859375px;\">30–80 </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 299.15625px;\">Increase in motor and cognitive problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 91px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 91px; width: 76.859375px;\">80–200 </td>\n<td style=\"height: 91px; width: 299.15625px;\">\n<p>Increase in incoordination and judgment errors</p>\n<p>Mood lability</p>\n<p>Deterioration in cognition</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 76.859375px;\">200–300 </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 299.15625px;\">\n<p>Nystagmus</p>\n<p>Marked slurring of speech</p>\n<p>Alcoholic <strong>blackouts</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 76.859375px;\">&gt;300 </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 299.15625px;\">Impaired vital signs and possible death</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5876","text":"In the clinical assessment‌ of patients with alcohol​ use disorder, which of t​he following is not used?‌","raw_text":"In the clinical assessment of patients with alcohol use disorder, which of the following is not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fagerstrom tolerance quest‌ionaire","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"SADQ que‌stionaire","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CAGE que‌stionaire","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AUDIT‌ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fagerstrom tolerance questionaire","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fagerstrom tolerance questionaire</strong></p><p><strong>Fagerström tolerance questionnaire</strong> and its revision, Fagerström Test for Nicotine Dependence (FTND) are used in the assessment of <strong>nicotine addiction</strong> related to <strong>cigarette smoking</strong>. It contains six items that evaluate the quantity of cigarette consumption, the compulsion to use, and dependence.</p>\n<p>Option B: Severity of Alcohol Dependence Questionnaire<strong> (SADQ-Q) </strong>was developed to assess the <strong>severity of alcohol dependence. </strong></p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>CAGE questionnaire</strong> is used to <strong>assess </strong>whether or not there is a problem with <strong>dependence. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>C - Have you ever felt you ought to <strong>cut </strong>down on your drinking?</li>\n<li>A - Have people <strong>annoyed</strong> you by criticizing your drinking?</li>\n<li>G - Have you ever felt <strong>guilty</strong> or bad about your drinking?</li>\n<li>E - Have you ever had a drink first thing in the morning to steady your nerves or get rid of a hangover (<strong>eye-opener</strong>)?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One 'yes' response raises a suspicion of an alcohol use problem and more than one is a strong indication of abuse or dependence. </p>\n<p>Option D: The Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test <strong>(AUDIT)</strong> is a 10-item screening tool developed by the World Health Organization (WHO) to <strong>assess</strong> alcohol consumption, drinking behaviors, and alcohol-related problems. The AUDIT also helps to <strong>identify</strong> alcohol <strong>dependence</strong> and some specific consequences of harmful drinking. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2199","text":"The police charged two col‌lege students with drunke​n driving and brought the​m to the hospital for con‌firmation of their alcoho​l levels. On examination,‌ the driver did not appea​r drunk and denied the ch​arges, while the other st‍udent was clearly drunk a‌nd was barely conscious. ​However, the blood report‍s came back suggesting bo‌th of them having similar‌ly elevated levels of alc‍ohol. Which of the follow‌ing is a possible explana‌tion for this observation​?","raw_text":"The police charged two college students with drunken driving and brought them to the hospital for confirmation of their alcohol levels. On examination, the driver did not appear drunk and denied the charges, while the other student was clearly drunk and was barely conscious. However, the blood reports came back suggesting both of them having similarly elevated levels of alcohol. Which of the following is a possible explanation for this observation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mellanb‌y effect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intermedia‌te syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse ‌tolerance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grand Rap‌ids effect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mellanby effect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02e0469aa3624d99a0883781eaf47b6cx600x450.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mellanby effect</strong></p><p>The given situation can be best explained by the <strong>Mellanby effect.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The Mellanby effect is the phenomenon where an individual’s perceived level of impairment at <strong>identical blood alcohol concentration</strong> (BAC) levels is greater when the BAC is <strong>rising</strong> (while drinking) than when it is <strong>falling</strong> (after drinking).</p>\n<p>The clip below describes the Mellanby effect seen originally in a dog experiment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: <strong>Intermediate syndrome</strong> is a delayed-onset of muscular weakness and paralysis following an episode of organophosphate (acute cholinesterase inhibitor) poisoning.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>Reverse tolerance</strong> refers to the phenomenon when an individual requires a lesser dose of alcohol (drug) to obtain the same effect as before.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: <strong>Grand Rapids effect </strong>or dip is a controversial effect noted in certain studies where a BAC of 0.01–0.04% slightly lowered the risk of being in a vehicle accident when compared to a BAC of 0.00%</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5945","text":"A middle-aged man has been‌ regularly consuming arou​nd 750 mL of alcohol for ​the past 20 years. Of lat‌e, he has noticed that he​ is able to obtain the sa‌me effect with just a qua​rter bottle. What is this​ phenomenon called?","raw_text":"A  middle-aged man has been regularly consuming around 750 mL of alcohol for the past 20 years. Of late, he has noticed that he is able to obtain the same effect with just a quarter bottle.  What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tachyp‌hylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desensi‌tization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse ‌tolerance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cross t‌olerance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reverse tolerance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reverse tolerance</strong></p><p>When an individual requires a lesser dose of alcohol (drug) to obtain the same effect as before, this phenomenon is known as <strong>reverse tolerance</strong>.</p>\n<p>In alcoholics, it is a sign of <strong>late-stage alcoholism</strong>, where the patient has developed <strong>cirrhosis</strong>. The liver doesn’t produce enough enzymes like before to metabolize alcohol leading to increased blood alcohol levels. </p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Option A: </span><strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Tachyphylaxis </span></strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">is the </span>rapid development of tolerance when a drug is repeatedly used in quick succession, resulting in a marked reduction in response.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Desensitization</strong> may be equivalent to drug tolerance and refers to a diminished response/effect with continued use of the same dose of the substance.</p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Option D: <strong>Cross</strong>-<strong>tolerance</strong> is a phenomenon that occurs when tolerance to the effects of one drug produces tolerance to another drug. It often happens between two drugs with similar pharmacological actions. eg. alcohol and benzodiazepines (both are depressants).</span></p>\n<p>Note: A quarter bottle is approximately 180 ml.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5949","text":"Which among the following ‌is not an anti-craving dr​ug?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not an anti-craving drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acamp‌rosate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naltr‌exone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disul‌firam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"SS‌RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Disulfiram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Disulfiram</strong></p><p><strong>Disulfiram</strong> is a<strong> deterrent agent, </strong>not an anti-craving drug.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>It is an irreversible <strong>aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor.</strong> </p>\n<p>Acetaldehyde is the first breakdown product of alcohol, which is further metabolized by aldehyde dehydrogenase. If a patient who is on disulfiram consumes alcohol, it results in toxic levels of acetaldehyde.</p>\n<p>This causes symptoms such as <span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">flushing, pulsating headache, nausea, vomiting, respiratory difficulties, chest pain, and autonomic manifestations,</span> collectively termed as <strong>disulfiram ethanol reaction</strong> (DER).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other deterrent agents include citrated calcium carbamide and metronidazole.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p>Anti-craving drugs used in alcohol deaddiction include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Baclofen</li>\n<li>Acamprosate</li>\n<li>Naltrexone</li>\n<li>Newer drugs are topiramate, SSRI and ondansetron</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alcohol deaddiction treatment involves the following phases:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Intervention - </strong>aimed at maximizing the motivation for treatment and continued abstinence.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">2. <strong>Detoxification -</strong> involves minimizing withdrawal symptoms. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">3. <strong>Rehabilitation and maintenance - </strong>includes three major components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Continued efforts to increase and maintain high levels of motivation for abstinence</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Work to help the patient readjust to a lifestyle free of alcohol</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Relapse prevention</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1371","text":"A patient has been admitte‌d to the psychiatry wards​ for alcohol deaddiction.​ Which of the following m‌edications will not be us​ed in the maintenance pha‌se?","raw_text":"A patient has been admitted to the psychiatry wards for alcohol deaddiction. Which of the following medications will not be used in the maintenance phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlordia‌zepoxide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Naltr‌exone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acamp‌rosate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disul‌firam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlordiazepoxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlordiazepoxide</strong></p><p><strong>Chlordiazepoxide</strong> is a benzodiazepine drug used in <strong>alcohol withdrawal</strong> and the <strong>detoxification</strong> phase of management. </p>\n<p>All the other given drugs are approved for maintenance therapy and to prevent relapse:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Naltrexone - It is an orally active opioid antagonist.</li>\n<li>Acamprosate - An NMDA receptor antagonist with modest GABA A receptor agonistic activity.</li>\n<li>Disulfiram - A deterrent drug that works by inhibiting the aldehyde dehydrogenase enzyme.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5944","text":"In a patient experiencing ‌alcohol withdrawal, which​ of the following is the ​first symptom that become‌s clinically noticeable?","raw_text":"In a patient experiencing alcohol withdrawal, which of the following is the first symptom that becomes clinically noticeable?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deli‌rium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sei‌zure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nausea an‌d vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tremul‌ousness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tremulousness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tremulousness</strong></p><p><strong>Tremulousness</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> withdrawal symptom of alcohol abuse. It is commonly called shakes or jitters and develops <strong>6 to 8 hours after</strong> the cessation of drinking. It is the classical sign of alcohol withdrawal. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5951","text":"A 40-year-old man was admi‌tted to the COVID ICU. On​ the third day, he develo​ped diffuse tremors and b‌reathing difficulty, and ​was noted to be restless.‌ Suddenly he started scra​tching and tearing at his​ skin, screaming of insec‍ts crawling all over his ‌body. He then had one epi​sode of seizures which wa‍s controlled with lorazep‌am. On probing further, i‌t was found out that he i‍s a chronic alcoholic. Wh‌at is the likely diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man was admitted to the COVID ICU. On the third day, he developed diffuse tremors and breathing difficulty, and was noted to be restless. Suddenly he started scratching and tearing at his skin, screaming of insects crawling all over his body. He then had one episode of seizures which was controlled with lorazepam. On probing further, it was found out that he is a chronic alcoholic. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ICU ps‌ychosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcohol in‌toxication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wernicke-Korsakoff Psychos‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delirium‌ tremens","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Delirium tremens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Delirium tremens</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a chronic alcoholic patient who has not consumed alcohol in the last few days (due to ICU admission) with tremors, hallucinations, and seizures, is suggestive of <strong>delirium tremens.</strong></p>\n<p>Delirium tremens is a medical emergency that occurs during alcohol withdrawal. It is an <strong>acute condition</strong> with symptoms of both delirium and alcohol withdrawal. It develops within <strong>48-72 hours</strong> after the last drink.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clouding of consciousness with disorientation</li>\n<li>Perceptual distortions, most frequently visual or<strong> tactile hallucinations</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Autonomic hyperactivity </strong>with tachycardia, fever, anxiety, insomnia, hypertension, sweating</li>\n<li>Fluctuating levels of psychomotor activity, ranging from agitation to lethargy</li>\n<li>Altered sleep-wake cycle</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Alcoholic hallucinosis occurs during alcohol intoxication as well as withdrawal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2258","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a treatment option in​ alcohol withdrawal delir​ium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a treatment option in alcohol withdrawal delirium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antipsy‌chotics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlordia‌zepoxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High calorie diet with mul‌tivitamins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antipsychotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antipsychotics</strong></p><p>Antipsychotics are not the treatment option of alcohol withdrawal delirium. <strong>Antipsychotic medications</strong> may <strong>reduce the seizure threshold</strong> in these patients and <strong>should be avoided.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>best treatment</strong> for delirium tremens is prevention. Patients withdrawing from alcohol <strong>who exhibit withdrawal phenomena</strong> should receive <strong>benzodiazepines,</strong> such as 25 to 50 mg of chlordiazepoxide every 2 to 4 hours until they seem to be out of danger.</p>\n<p>Once the delirium appears, 50 to 100 mg of <strong>chlordiazepoxide</strong> should be given every 4 hours orally, or <strong>lorazepam</strong> should be given intravenously (IV) if oral medication is not possible.</p>\n<p>A <strong>high-calorie, high-carbohydrate diet</strong> supplemented by multivitamins is also important. Carbohydrates increase the serotonin levels similar to alcohol, lowering the rates of withdrawal seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6935","text":"A 52-year-old man, with a ‌history of alcohol use pr​esented after a fall. He ​appeared malnourished, co‌nfused, and had grossly i​mpaired memory. On neurol‌ogical examination, he ha​d reduced coordination, a​n ataxic gait, dysarthria‍, and tremors. Despite in‌itiating treatment for al​cohol withdrawal and deto‍xification, his symptoms ‌persisted. An MRI of his ‌brain revealed symmetrica‍l demyelination of the co‌rpus callosum. Which of t‌he following statements i​s true regarding the give‌n condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man, with a history of alcohol use presented after a fall. He appeared malnourished, confused, and had grossly impaired memory. On neurological examination, he had reduced coordination, an ataxic gait, dysarthria, and tremors. Despite initiating treatment for alcohol withdrawal and detoxification, his symptoms persisted. An MRI of his brain revealed symmetrical demyelination of the corpus callosum. Which of the following statements is true regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients usually have opht‌halmoplegia.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial thalamic nuclei and‌ mammillary bodies are in​volved.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRI shows 'sandwich' sign.‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a complication of ac‌ute alcohol intoxication.​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MRI shows 'sandwich' sign.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c75bb08dc154a6581c31f9945fbd9c1x512x490.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MRI shows &#x27;sandwich&#x27; sign.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Marchiafava Bignami disease</strong> (MBD). It is characteristically associated with a <strong>'sandwich' sign</strong> on MRI.</p>\n<p>MBD is a rare, idiopathic CNS disorder seen as a complication of <strong>chronic alcoholism</strong>. Early symptoms may include depression, paranoia, psychosis, or dementia. Seizures, hemiparesis, aphasia, and abnormal movements, and ataxia are also seen. If not promptly identified and treated may progress to coma and death.</p>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>central degeneration of corpus callosum</strong>, sparing the dorsal and ventral parts. This characteristic pattern of degeneration presents as the '<strong>sandwich</strong>'<strong> sign</strong>, best visualized on <strong>sagittal MRI</strong> of the brain. </p>\n<p>Clinically, conditions such as Wernicke’s encephalopathy, delirium tremens, or pontine demyelination may have similar presentations and their features may overlap. Hence neuroimaging plays a significant role in the accurate diagnosis of MB disease.</p>\n<p>Ophthalmoplegia and involvement of mamillary bodies and medial thalamic nuclei are associated with <strong>Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome,</strong> not MBD.</p>\n<p>The sagittal MRI image given below shows the characteristic 'sandwich' sign in Marchiafava Bignami disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5939","text":"A 64-year-old man was brou‌ght to the casualty with ​confusion and gait instab​ility. Medical history is‌ significant for alcohol ​use disorder, requiring m‌ultiple hospitalizations ​in the past. On examinati​on, he appears inattentiv‍e, has bilateral horizont‌al nystagmus, and his gai​t is slow and wide-based.‍ What is the next step in‌ management?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man was brought to the casualty with confusion and gait instability. Medical history is significant for alcohol use disorder, requiring multiple hospitalizations in the past. On examination, he appears inattentive, has bilateral horizontal nystagmus, and his gait is slow and wide-based. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer ‌IV dextrose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Give haloperidol injection‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Give thiami‌ne injection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Correct el‌ectrolytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Give thiamine injection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Give thiamine injection</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wernicke's encephalopathy,</strong> which is a medical emergency. <strong>Thiamine administration</strong> (<strong>100 mg IV or IM</strong> for 3 days followed by two to three times a day orally for 1 to 2 weeks) may reduce the risk of it progressing to chronic Korsakoff psychosis which is irreversible. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome</strong> is seen due to the deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1). Thiamine is a cofactor (TPP) in glucose metabolism. Thus, starting dextrose infusions before thiamine administration will exacerbate the existing thiamine deficiency, precipitating Wernicke’s disease. Thiamine is also involved in the conduction of the axon potential and synaptic transmission. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Alcohol abuse leads to thiamine deficiency by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Poor nutritional habits</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Folate deficiency - reduces thiamine absorption from the gastrointestinal tract</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Direct damage of the intestinal mucosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Wernicke's encephalopathy/</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>alcoholic encephalopathy</strong> </span>is an <strong>acute</strong> neurological disorder characterized by the clinical triad (GOA):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>G</strong>lobal Confusion</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>phthalmoplegia ( first symptom to improve with thiamine +dextrose infusion)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>taxia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5942","text":"A psychiatry resident is e‌valuating a chronic alcoh​olic patient admitted to ​the ward. When asked abou‌t his day, he correctly r​emembered that he was whe‌eled out of the ward, but​ then went on to tell a s​tory of a trip he made to‍ the nearby park, which d‌id not occur. He also mad​e up random names as he w‍ent on talking about his ‌children. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A psychiatry resident is evaluating a chronic alcoholic patient admitted to the ward. When asked about his day, he correctly remembered that he was wheeled out of the ward, but then went on to tell a story of a trip he made to the nearby park, which did not occur. He also made up random names as he went on talking about his children. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcohol w‌ithdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute alcohol intoxication‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wernicke’s e‌ncephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Korsakoff'‌s psychosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Korsakoff's psychosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Korsakoff&#x27;s psychosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>confabulation. </strong>It is one of the features of Korsafkoff’s psychosis. Confabulation is filling up gaps in memory with false stories.</p>\n<p><strong>Korsakoff’s psychosis</strong> is a <strong>chronic amnestic syndrome </strong>that can follow Wernicke's encephalopathy due to <strong>thiamine</strong> <strong>deficiency </strong>(secondary to alcoholism). The cardinal features are impaired mental syndrome and anterograde amnesia. Only 20% of patients recover with treatment (whereas in Wernicke's encephalopathy there is complete reversal with treatment).</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired mental syndrome - especially recent memory</li>\n<li>Striking inability to form new memories - <strong>severe</strong> <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Retrograde amnesia</strong> is less severe.</li>\n<li>Present with <strong>confabulation</strong> as an attempt to cover the memory deficit</li>\n<li>There is a lack of insight and no intellectual functional loss.</li>\n<li>Overall intact intelligence</li>\n<li>Implicit learning (e.g., learning procedures) is <strong>preserved.</strong></li>\n<li>Long-term and immediate memory is <strong>normal.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment: <strong>Thiamine</strong> is given <strong>100mg orally</strong> two to three times daily for 3 to 12 months.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"steimKQO","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"13_obg_radiology","title":"13.Obg Radiology","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB6582","text":"A primigravida in the firs‍t trimester presents with​ complaints of excessive ‍vomiting, spotting PV, an‌d passing grape-like vesi​cles PV. Which of the fol​lowing investigations wil​l help you confirm your d​iagnosis? PV- per vaginam​","raw_text":"<p>A primigravida in the first trimester presents with complaints of excessive vomiting, spotting PV, and passing grape-like vesicles PV. Which of the following investigations will help you confirm your diagnosis?</p>\n<p>PV- per vaginam </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyster‍oscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultra‍sound","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lapar‍oscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‍RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ultrasound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ultrasound</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>molar pregnancy. </strong>The diagnosis is confirmed by <strong>ultrasound,</strong> which shows a <strong>snowstorm appearance. </strong></p>\n<p>Molar pregnancy is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complete mole</strong> appears as an echogenic uterine mass with numerous anechoic cystic spaces but <strong>without</strong> a <strong>fetus</strong> or amnionic sac. The appearance is described as<strong> </strong>a<strong> 'snowstorm' appearance. </strong>Theca lutein cysts may be seen in the ovary.</li>\n<li>In <strong>partial mole</strong>, the fetus is present along with molar components.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The patient presents with a characteristic history of abdominal pain, <strong>vaginal bleeding </strong>or spotting, and vomiting during the<strong> first trimester</strong>. Also, there may be a history of passing <strong>grape-like vesicles</strong> vaginally. </p>\n<p>The snowstorm appearance on USG, combined with a <strong>raised hCG </strong>and a <strong>uterus larger</strong> than the date of pregnancy, leads to the diagnosis of a <strong>complete hydatidiform mole</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Suction and evacuation</strong> is the treatment of choice. Serum <strong>hCG</strong> levels are checked <strong>within 48 hours</strong> of evacuation and are used as a baseline for future comparisons. It is then checked every week until it normalizes, which usually takes 4 to 8 weeks. After the hCG values return to normal, a regular follow-up is done <strong>monthly </strong>for <strong>6 months</strong>. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8642","text":"Antenatal USG in a primigr‍avida revealed fetal hydr​ocephalus. Postnatal MRI ‍of the infant is shown be‌low. What is the most pro​bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Antenatal USG in a primigravida revealed fetal hydrocephalus. Postnatal MRI of the infant is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corpus callo‍sal agenesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Marchiafava – Bignami dise‍ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metachromatic leukodystrop‍hy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pericallo‍sal lipoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corpus callosal agenesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa43e1dc7cbd4d22bc8430c9454340e0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be0f9d47eae447ca9a13a1d352bfeb65x510x520.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e64447daf2fa49cc94a331cf62a16ee6x720x691.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d08143d020346dbac5113730b5d180bx1212x1052.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e1798c175ea4a7184042f955ece092ax890x783.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corpus callosal agenesis</strong></p><p>The given T2 MRI image shows the <strong>racing car appearance,</strong> which is seen in <strong>agenesis</strong> of the <strong>corpus callosum</strong>. This sign<strong> </strong>refers to the widely spaced lateral ventricles with intervening Probst bundles(white matter tracts that are no longer able to cross the midline in the corpus callosum).</p>\n<p>Appearances on axial MRI or CT resemble a Formula One car seen from above. The front tyres are represented by the widely spaced frontal horns anteriorly. The back tyres are represented by the dilated trigones seen posteriorly, which is also known as colpocephaly.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the racing car sign in corpus callosal agensis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Marchiafava - Bignami </strong>disease is a rare condition characterized by <strong>demyelination </strong>of the<strong> corpus callosum</strong> most often in patients with chronic alcoholism. The MRI image in this condition shows the <strong>sandwich sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Metachromatic leukodystrophy</strong> is an autosomal-recessive disease that results from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase A. Symmetric confluent areas of T2 hyperintensity are seen in the periventricular white matter. Radiating T2 hypointense lines through the demyelinated deep white matter represent the sparing of the perivascular white matter. This results in the <strong>tigroid</strong> and <strong>leopard skin patterns</strong> when imaged in the long axis or cross-section, respectively. Subcortical U-fibers are spared.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: The most common site of <strong>lipoma</strong> in the brain is the <strong>peri-callosal</strong> region. The CT below shows<strong> bracket calcification</strong> of lipoma and is called <strong>bracket sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6583","text":"A 30-year-old female with ‍a history of anencephaly ​in a previous child is sc‍heduled to undergo a scre‌ening ultrasound to rule ​out anencephaly in her cu​rrent pregnancy. Which am​ong the following finding​s confirms the diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female with a history of anencephaly in a previous child is scheduled to undergo a screening ultrasound to rule out anencephaly in her current pregnancy. Which among the following findings confirms the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frog-e‍ye sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eye of t‍iger sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blob‍ sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T S‍ign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Frog-eye sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fddfc2e5d7494b67983b1c78431dcbb2x1280x999.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/834b299e1ade4b64bd5551f521f38be5x1280x1795.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1bf82da291d48aa9da3d242b4bfd677x630x630.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Frog-eye sign</strong></p><p><strong>Frog-eye sign </strong>in the ultrasound is suggestive of <strong>Anencephaly.</strong></p>\n<p>Anencephaly is an<strong> open defect </strong>in the <strong>calvaria</strong> and <strong>skin</strong> that results from incomplete closure of the neural tube. It is one of the <strong>earliest</strong> <strong>detectable</strong> congenital defects that can be detected as early as<strong> 10 to 11 weeks of gestation.</strong> Screening for anencephaly reveals <strong>increased</strong> levels of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (<strong>MSAFP</strong>). Diagnosis is confirmed by <strong>sonography. </strong>The following are the radiological signs seen in anencephaly: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frog eye sign</strong> - Absence of cranial calvaria, causing prominent orbits, thereby resembling the face of a frog. </li>\n<li><strong>Shower cap sign</strong> - The absence of the cranium could lead to the protrusion of a disorganized mass of brain tissue. This resembles a shower cap, giving rise to a characteristic <strong>triangular</strong> facial appearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below show open calvaria on antenatal ultrasound and a 'shower cap' sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B - <strong>Eye of tiger sign</strong> is the MRI finding seen in <strong>Hallervorden-Spatz syndrome, </strong>a rare autosomal recessive neurogenerative disorder, showing decreased T2 signal in <strong>globus pallidus and substania nigra.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Anencephaly, holoprosencephaly</strong> and <strong>ventral wall defects</strong> (e.g. ectopia cordis, omphalocele, bladder extrophy) have a very high rate of <strong>detection</strong> in the <strong>first trimester</strong>. The rest of the anomalies can be detected in the second trimester.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6587","text":"The pelvic ultrasound of a‍ 35-year-old woman is giv​en below. What is the lik‍ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The pelvic ultrasound of a 35-year-old woman is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Singleton‍ pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monochori‍onic twins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Partial ‍abortion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dichorio‍nic twins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monochorionic twins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/873b339846874787b4e3c689738f1a71.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/84cb7d5589c54e528bd2bab5e1c6c87bx1280x1699.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monochorionic twins</strong></p><p>The pelvic ultrasound in a 35-year-old woman shows the <strong>'T-sign</strong>' seen in a <strong>monochorionic twin</strong> pregnancy.</p>\n<p>In monochorionic pregnancy, the chorion <strong>does not extend</strong> between the layers of the intertwin membrane. Thus, the inter-twin membrane comes to an <strong>abrupt halt</strong> in a T-configuration at the edge of the amniotic sac. </p>\n<p>In contrast, in a <strong>dichorionic pregnancy</strong>, the sonographic image depicts a  '<strong>twin-peak</strong>' sign or the '<strong>lambda sign</strong>\" as the chorion is present between the intertwin membrane. A <strong>triangular</strong> portion of the placenta is seen inserted between the amniochorion layers.</p>\n<p>The type of twin pregnancy is best diagnosed by <strong>transabdominal ultrasound</strong> at <strong>11-14 weeks</strong> of gestation as the chorion and amnion are not yet fused. At 11-14 weeks, the intervening membranes are clearly visualized at the site of insertion of the amniotic membrane into the placenta. If not visualised transabdominally, <strong>transvaginal ultrasound</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>Sonographic assessment is done by observing the following:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 29.8003%; height: 211px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 28.427%;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.5489%; text-align: center;\"><strong>Monochorionicity </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.0261%; text-align: center;\"><strong>Dichorionicity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 28.427%;\">Placental mass</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.5489%; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.0261%; text-align: center;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 28.427%;\">Presence of intervening membrane</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.5489%; text-align: center;\">T sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.0261%; text-align: center;\">twin peak/ lambda/ delta sign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 28.427%;\">Thickness of intervening membrane</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.5489%; text-align: center;\">&lt;2mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 35.0261%; text-align: center;\">≥ 2mm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6585","text":"The following investigatio‍n was performed in a pati​ent with postmenopausal b‍leeding. Which of the fol‌lowing is false about the​ same?","raw_text":"The following investigation was performed in a patient with postmenopausal bleeding. Which of the following is false about the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bladder sho‍uld be full","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A condom is placed over th‍e probe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It provides higher resolut‍ion images","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endometrial thickness can ‍be measured","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bladder should be full","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df04cd3749964c11bce5fdc1236321b7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bladder should be full</strong></p><p>The given image demonstrates <strong>transvaginal ultrasonography</strong>. The <strong>bladder</strong> should be <strong>empty</strong> and not full, for transvaginal USG (TVS) to allow visualisation of other structures.</p>\n<p>A condom is placed on the transducer before the TVS transducer is inserted into the vagina. It provides higher-resolution images compared to transabdominal USG because of the proximity to the pelvic organs. During a<strong> transabdominal ultrasound</strong>, the bladder should be full to create a good echo window.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> of TVS include early pregnancy, LUS in late pregnancy, ectopic, pelvic masses, obese patients, follicular and endometrial studies, doppler examination of pelvic organs and interventional procedures. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6586","text":"A young girl with primary ‍amenorrhea is being evalu​ated for the presence of ‍a congenital uterine anom‌aly. What is the gold sta​ndard investigation to de​tect the same?","raw_text":"A young girl with primary amenorrhea is being evaluated for the presence of a congenital uterine anomaly. What is the gold standard investigation to detect the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Computed ‍Tomography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultraso‍nography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hysterosal‍pingography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Magnetic Resonance Imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Magnetic Resonance Imaging</strong></p><p><strong>Magnetic Resonance Imaging</strong> is the most accurate imaging technique for investigating and classifying <strong>congenital uterine anomalies</strong> and is hence considered to be the<strong> gold standard</strong>. Classifying congenital uterine anomalies is important as fertility outcomes and surgical management vary considerably.</p>\n<p>Other options -</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Computed Tomography</strong> and <strong>X-ray</strong> are <strong>not</strong> preferred, and in case of suspected pregnancy they are avoided. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Ultrasonography</strong> is the primary/initial investigation done. However, <strong>not all</strong> uterine anomalies <strong>can be detected</strong> and classified.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hysterosalpingography</strong> is also done, however, it <strong>cannot differentiate</strong> between a <strong>septate</strong> and <strong>bicornuate</strong> uterus, as only the uterine interior is visualized, leading to similar images on HSG. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6581","text":"You notice the following f‍inding on the transvagina​l USG of a woman with ame‍norrhea. What is the earl‌iest gestational age at w​hich this can be detected​?","raw_text":"You notice the following finding on the transvaginal USG of a woman with amenorrhea. What is the earliest gestational age at which this can be detected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-6 ‍weeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"7-8 ‍weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3-4 ‍weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9-12 ‍weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5-6 weeks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/623ec3c42cb04b6f8b610bcc573f6315.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fe1548531ee48f6b3b1325929719e28x1280x1903.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5-6 weeks</strong></p><p>The given transvaginal ultrasound shows a <strong>gestational sac</strong>. This can be identified by transvaginal USG at <strong>5 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cardiac activity in an embryo can be identified at <strong>6 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The double decidual sac sign</strong> (DDSS) is a useful feature on early pregnancy ultrasound to confirm an early intrauterine pregnancy when the yolk sac or embryo is still not visualised. It consists of the <strong>decidua parietalis </strong>(lining the uterine cavity) and <strong>decidua capsularis </strong>(lining the gestational sac) and is seen as <strong>two concentric rings</strong> surrounding an anechoic gestational sac. </p>\n<p>The double decidual sac sign differentiates normal pregnancy from the pseudo-gestational sac of an ectopic pregnancy.<strong> Pseudosac </strong>is irregular, usually centrally located in the uterus, has <strong>no double decidua sign</strong>, and the sac remains empty. The illustration below shows the difference: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4002","text":"The image seen below shows‍_______.","raw_text":"The image seen below shows_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positron Emission Tomograp‍hy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hysterosal‍pingography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laparoscopic chromopertuba‍tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hysterosalpingography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b8f0e12d2c174afda583bae8c16acbe9.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af6eef57689d4118bf566fb7b6ce5591x720x769.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hysterosalpingography</strong></p><p>The image given is of the findings seen in <strong>hysterosalpingography</strong>. The given image shows bilateral retort-shaped fallopian tubes, indicating <strong>bilateral hydrosalpinx. </strong></p>\n<p>Hysterosalpingography (HSG) is a technique used for visualization of the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes by the injection of a <strong>radioopaque dye</strong>, and visualization of the dye by <strong>fluoroscopy</strong> as it passes through the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes.</p>\n<p>It is best performed between the end of the menstrual period and ovulation (i.e. <strong>9th or 10th day of the cycle</strong>). <strong>10-15ml</strong> of the dye is injected using a <strong>Leech-Wilkinson cannula, Rubin's cannula</strong>, or Foley's catheter. If the <strong>tubes</strong> are <strong>patent</strong>, the <strong>dye</strong> will <strong>spill out</strong> of the tubal Ostia and smear the adjacent bowel. </p>\n<p>It is used to<strong> detect</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tubal block: </strong>This can be due to strictures (fibrotic block), inspissated amorphous material, or spasms. The tubal spasm can occur in an apprehensive woman, and this is prevented by premedication with atropine and analgesia.</li>\n<li>Uterine anomalies</li>\n<li>Uterine polyps</li>\n<li>Uterine synechiae</li>\n<li>The feasibility of tuboplasty, by studying the location and extent of tubal pathology.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contradictions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of genital tract infection and bleeding</li>\n<li>Genital tuberculosis, due to the risk of spreading infection.</li>\n<li>Suspected pregnancy</li>\n<li>Allergy to the dye</li>\n<li>Premenstrual phase (there is a risk of embolism and endometriosis if HSG is done during this phase).</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image given below shows the appearance of a normal hysterosalpingogram. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Laparoscopic chromopertubation</strong> is a laparoscopic procedure for establishing the <strong>patency of fallopian tubes</strong> or verifying the findings of blocked tubes on HSG. <strong>Methylene blue</strong> is injected through the cervix to visualize the free spill. The absence of spill indicates blocked tubes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4005","text":"What is the diagnosis base‍d on the given image?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis based on the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterus d‍idelphys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bicornua‍te uterus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unicornua‍te uterus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Septate‍ uterus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Unicornuate uterus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8648ee4a3c7744d4a546073e8336d55e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Unicornuate uterus</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>hysterosalpingogram </strong>of a<strong> unicornuate uterus.</strong></p>\n<p>The endometrial cavity usually assumes a <strong>fusiform (banana type)</strong> shape, tapering at the apex and draining into a <strong>single fallopian tube</strong>. The uterus is generally shifted off the midline.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5661","text":"A 26-year-old pregnant wom‍an has the following flow​ pattern of umbilical art‍eries on a Doppler study.‌ What does this pattern i​ndicate?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old pregnant woman has the following flow pattern of umbilical arteries on a Doppler study. What does this pattern indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal dia‍stolic flow","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reversal of diastolic flow‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absent dia‍stolic flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absent sys‍tolic flow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal diastolic flow","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c46c17992ff42e7a1a01c8dd4fffcdb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/640ec9d9172940748129ca0ede7193bcx1280x2763.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal diastolic flow</strong></p><p>The given Doppler image indicates <strong>normal diastolic flow </strong>in the umbilical arteries.</p>\n<p><strong>Umbilical artery vessels are </strong>characterized by <strong>forward, low-resistance flow. </strong>As gestation increases, there is a gradual fall in all the resistance indices, so the amount of forward diastolic flow increases.<strong> S/D ratio decreases</strong> as the period of gestation increases (S represents the peak systolic flow or velocity, and D indicates the end-diastolic flow or velocity).</p>\n<p><strong>Abnormal flow patterns in the umbilical artery:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduced </strong>end-diastolic flow: Uteroplacental insufficiency.</li>\n<li><strong>Absent</strong> end-diastolic flow: Fetal compromise (terminate the pregnancy by cesarean section if gestational age is &gt;34 weeks).</li>\n<li><strong>Reverse</strong> end-diastolic flow: Impending doom of death (terminate pregnancy irrespective of gestational age).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Middle cerebral artery: </strong>This will show increased diastolic velocity in fetus with hypoxemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1682","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‍ has come for an antenata​l ultrasound examination.‍ The sonographic image ob‌tained is shown below. Wh​at is the most likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida has come for an antenatal ultrasound examination. The sonographic image obtained is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spina ‍bifida","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anenc‍ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Holopros‍encephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ompha‍locele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spina bifida","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7adc5b32b0144e20a15e9527b047f4ec.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4898652bfb914bfd95154ad132c676aex500x254.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc4130462341426a971b770dd25a14d3x512x392.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spina bifida</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>lemon sign</strong>, which is seen in <strong>spina bifida.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Spina bifida</strong> is the common <strong>open neural tube defect</strong> associated with hydrocephalus and<strong> Arnold-Chiari II </strong>malformation. It is the most common open neural tube disorder. Spina bifida is characterized by a cleft in the vertebral column with a corresponding defect in the skin so that the<strong> meninges and spinal cord are exposed.</strong></p>\n<p>The 3 main types of spina bifida:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spina bifida occulta</strong> - This is the mildest form of spina bifida. The defect in the vertebral arches is covered by the skin. The splits in the vertebrae are so small that the spinal cord does not protrude. The skin at the site of the lesion may be normal, or it may have some hair growing from it.</li>\n<li><strong>Meningocele</strong> - The defect in the vertebra allows the meninges to protrude through the defect.</li>\n<li><strong>Myelomeningocele</strong> (meningomyelocele) - A defect in the vertebral column allows the spinal cord with the meningeal coverings to protrude. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>lemon sig</strong>n is related to changes in the configuration of the fetal frontal bone, which <strong>loses convexity</strong> and appears concave or linear on ultrasonography; this sign can be detected during the<strong> first six months</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p>The<strong> banana sign </strong>is another sign that is seen in <strong>spina bifida</strong> associated with Arnold-Chiari II malformation. Herniation of the brainstem and cerebellar tonsils occurs through the foramen magnum, with the<strong> cerebellum surrounding the brainstem,</strong> thus giving rise to the appearance of the curved shape of a banana.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Anencephaly</strong> is characterized by an open defect in the calvaria and skin, such that the cranial neural tube is exposed. It is a severe defect and is not compatible with survival. <strong>Frog eye sign</strong> is seen in USG. Prominent orbits (arrows) and an absence of cerebral tissue more cranially are seen.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Holoprosencephaly</strong> is a rare congenital brain malformation resulting from incomplete separation of the two hemispheres. On antenatal ultrasound, there may be also evidence of polyhydramnios, and the <strong>snake-under-the-skull sign </strong>may also be seen<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Omphalocele</strong> is a midline anterior abdominal wall defect through which the solid abdominal viscera and/or bowel may herniate. The extruded abdominal contents are covered in a sac. Given below is a fetal ultrasound image suggestive of an omphalocele (asterisk).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ys00IC3R","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"13_thyroid_malignancies","title":"13.Thyroid Malignancies","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MC8810","text":"What is the first investig‍ation you would advise to​ a patient with a large t​hyroid swelling?","raw_text":"What is the first investigation you would advise to a patient with a large thyroid swelling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultrasound ‍of the neck","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FN‍AC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biochemical function of th‍e thyroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radioiodi‍ne uptake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biochemical function of the thyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biochemical function of the thyroid</strong></p><p>Assessing the <strong>biochemical function of the thyroid</strong> i.e. thyroid function test <strong>(TFT)</strong> is the first investigation done in any thyroid condition.</p>\n<p>Assessment of TSH, T3, and T4 is done. However, <strong>TSH</strong> is most helpful in determining the management plan, especially in patients with thyroid nodules who clinically appear to be euthyroid.</p>\n<p>Modalities for evaluation of thyroid swelling include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laboratory evaluation\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TSH</strong></li>\n<li>T3</li>\n<li>T4 </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Thyroid imaging\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound</strong></li>\n<li>Radioisotope scanning</li>\n<li>CT/MRI</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Biopsy techniques\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fine needle aspiration biopsy (FNAB)</strong></li>\n<li>Tru-cut/core-needle biopsy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8811","text":"A patient presents with a ‍2.5 cm neck swelling whic​h is firm, non-tender, an​d moves on swallowing. TS‍H, T3, and T4 levels are ​normal. An ultrasound rep​orts the swelling to be c‌ystic and regular with lo​w suspicion for malignanc‌y. What is the next step ‌in the management of this​ patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a 2.5 cm neck swelling which is firm, non-tender, and moves on swallowing. TSH, T3, and T4 levels are normal. An ultrasound reports the swelling to be cystic and regular with low suspicion for malignancy. What is the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform an fine needle asp‍iration biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perform a Radioiodine upta‍ke test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prepare for resection of n‍odule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reassure the patient and m‍onitor periodically","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perform an fine needle aspiration biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fd90ad0b05e4d0583451a82a8af1330x1280x2317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perform an fine needle aspiration biopsy</strong></p><p><strong>Fine needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy</strong> needs to be performed for further evaluation of the nodule.</p>\n<p>In cases where the FNAC is inconclusive, tru-cut or open biopsy can be done. However, this is rarely required.</p>\n<p>FNAC reporting systems are given as: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 407px;\" width=\"483\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\"><strong>Royal College of Pathologists, UK classification</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\"><strong>Bethesda Criteria  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\"><strong> Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\"><strong>Usual Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">\n<p>Thy 1</p>\n<p>Thy 1c</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\">Nondiagnostic or unsatisfactory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\">Repeat FNA with image guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\"> Thy 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\">Benign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\">Clinical follow-up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">  Thy 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\">Atypia of undetermined significance/follicular lesion of undetermined significance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\">Repeat FNA or lobectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\">Follicular neoplasm or suspicious for follicular neoplasm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\">Lobectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\"> Thy 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">V</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\">Suspicious for malignancy </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\">Lobectomy or total thyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\"> Thy 5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.9844px;\">VI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 253.25px;\">Malignant </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.766px;\">Total thyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4684","text":"FNAC is useful in all type‍s of thyroid cancers exce​pt _____","raw_text":"FNAC is useful in all types of thyroid cancers except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medu‍llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Papi‍llary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Folli‍cular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anapl‍astic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Follicular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Follicular</strong></p><p><strong>FNAC</strong> is not useful in the diagnosis of follicular carcinoma of thyroid as it <strong>cannot differentiate </strong>between<strong> benign </strong>and<strong> malignant</strong> follicular lesion.<strong> Trucut biopsy</strong> is used to differentiate between them as follicular thyroid malignancy is defined by<strong> capsular</strong> and <strong>vascular invasion</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Limitations </strong>of<strong> FNAC </strong>in thyroid disorders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cannot distinguish between follicular carcinoma and adenoma</li>\n<li>Cannot distinguish between Hurthle cell carcinoma and adenoma</li>\n<li>Not useful in Reidel’s thyroiditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8809","text":"Which of the following car‍cinomas of the thyroid ar​ise from the marked cells​? Papillary Follicular Hu‍rthle cell Anaplastic Med​ullary","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following carcinomas of the thyroid arise from the marked cells?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Papillary</li>\n<li>Follicular</li>\n<li>Hurthle cell</li>\n<li>Anaplastic</li>\n<li>Medullary</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‍4,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‍,3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,‍3,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2‍,3,4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d9b25e82c904b83b7c791defa36b868.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f6bbb6dc72a43058b6d35b301fa9ba5x562x470.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4</strong></p><p>The cell marked in the image is a <strong>follicular</strong> <strong>cell</strong> of the thyroid gland. <strong>Papillary, follicular,</strong> and <strong>anaplastic</strong> thyroid carcinoma are all cancers of the follicular epithelium of the thyroid. <strong>Hurthle cell</strong> carcinoma is a variant of follicular thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Classification of thyroid neoplasms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Benign - </strong>Follicular adenoma</li>\n<li><strong>Malignant</strong>\n<table style=\"height: 232px; width: 399px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\"><strong>Origin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 88px; background-color: #ffffff;\"><strong>Primary</strong></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 145px; background-color: #ffffff;\">Follicular epithelium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Differentiated\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Papillary</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Follicular</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Poorly-differentiated\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anaplastic</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Parafollicular cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Medullary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Lymphoid cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Lymphoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 88px;\"><strong>Secondary</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Metastatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Local infiltration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4174","text":"What is the most common th‍yroid malignancy in iodin​e-deficient regions?","raw_text":"What is the most common thyroid malignancy in iodine-deficient regions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papi‍llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anapl‍astic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Folli‍cular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medu‍llary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Follicular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Follicular</strong></p><p><strong>Follicular thyroid carcinoma </strong>is the most common<strong> </strong>thyroid malignancy in<strong> iodine-deficient areas.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Papillary thyroid carcinoma</strong> is the most common thyroid malignancy in <strong>iodine-sufficient areas</strong>, children, and radiation-exposed areas. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4685","text":"A 58-year-old man with a T‍hy3 lesion on FNAC was po​sted for hemithyroidectom​y. Histopathology of the ‍frozen section is given b​elow. What is the next st​ep of management?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man with a Thy3 lesion on FNAC was posted for hemithyroidectomy. Histopathology of the frozen section is given below. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No further procedure is re‍quired","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perform a total thyroidect‍omy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Perform RA‍I ablation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform central neck disse‍ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perform a total thyroidectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c3aedbd5554f460b997db5f91dbff7be.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perform a total thyroidectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Capsular</strong><strong> invasion </strong>as seen in the image<strong> </strong>confirms the presence of <strong>follicular carcinoma</strong>. Hence, <strong>total thyroidectomy</strong> is the recommended management.</p>\n<p>As FNAC shows Thy3 lesion which is a follicular neoplasm, the <strong>frozen section</strong> of the excised specimen has to be evaluated. The presence of <strong>capsular</strong> or <strong>vascular invasion </strong>confirms the presence of <strong>follicular carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Surgical management</strong> of differentiated thyroid cancers (DTCs) is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 534px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 308px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Extent of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 216px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Surgery done</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 168px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 168px;\">\n<p>Low-risk DTC <br/>(Fulfilling all 5 of the following conditions)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>&lt; 40 years (better prognosis)</li>\n<li>Unifocal lesion</li>\n<li>Size &lt; 2 cm</li>\n<li>No lymphovascular invasion</li>\n<li>No capsular invasion</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 168px;\">Hemithyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 64px;\">\n<p><strong>High-risk DTC without LN involvement </strong><br/><strong>(not fulfilling any of the above 5 conditions)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 64px;\"><strong>Total thyroidectomy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 34px;\">\n<p>High-risk DTC + level VI LN involved</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 34px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Central neck dissection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>High-risk DTC + other cervical LNs involved</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 54px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Modified radical neck dissection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8816","text":"All of the following state‍ments are true about foll​icular thyroid carcinoma ​except:","raw_text":"All of the following statements are true about follicular thyroid carcinoma except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroglobulin levels can b‍e used to monitor patient​s for recurrence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hematogenous spread is mor‍e common than lymphatic s​pread","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoblastic bone metastas‍is is seen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mortality rate is twice th‍at of papillary thyroid c​arcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteoblastic bone metastasis is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteoblastic bone metastasis is seen</strong></p><p><strong>Osteolytic bone metastasis</strong> is seen in follicular thyroid carcinoma and not osteoblastic lesions.</p>\n<p>Thyroglobulin trends may be used to monitor patients with differentiated thyroid cancer for a recurrence. Following <strong>total</strong> <strong>thyroidectomy</strong>, <strong>thyroglobulin</strong> levels must be <strong>undetectable</strong>.</p>\n<p>All thyroid carcinoma, except medullary thyroid carcinoma, have osteolytic bony metastasis. <strong>Medullary</strong> thyroid cancer has <strong>osteoblastic</strong> bony metastasis.</p>\n<p>Differentiated thyroid carcinomas (papillary &amp; follicular):</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Papillary carcinoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Follicular carcinoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incidence</td>\n<td>Most common</td>\n<td>2nd most common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past history</td>\n<td>Radiation exposure</td>\n<td>Long-standing goitre</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk factor</td>\n<td><strong>Iodine-sufficient</strong> areas</td>\n<td><strong>Iodine-deficient</strong> areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metastatic spread via</td>\n<td><strong>Lymphatics</strong> &gt; Hematogenous</td>\n<td><strong>Hematogenous</strong> &gt; Lymphatics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnosis by FNAC</td>\n<td>Useful</td>\n<td>Not useful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prognosis</td>\n<td>More favourable</td>\n<td>Less favourable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8817","text":"A patient with a unilatera‍l thyroid mass was diagno​sed to have follicular th​yroid carcinoma with invo‍lvement of level VI cervi​cal lymph nodes. What is ​the appropriate managemen‌t?","raw_text":"A patient with a unilateral thyroid mass was diagnosed to have follicular thyroid carcinoma with involvement of level VI cervical lymph nodes. What is the appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemithyroidectomy with rad‍ioactive iodine ablation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemithyroidectomy with lat‍eral neck dissection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total thyroidectomy with m‍odified radical neck diss​ection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total thyroidectomy with c‍entral neck dissection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Total thyroidectomy with central neck dissection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/508b7824cf3944adae5daf9bd4e95ba4x600x666.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/428682db32154b1c89456a7364131161x1280x1826.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Total thyroidectomy with central neck dissection</strong></p><p>Patients with follicular thyroid carcinoma with level VI lymph node involvement are managed by <strong>total thyroidectomy</strong> with<strong> central neck dissection</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Surgical management of differentiated thyroid cancers (DTCs) is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 534px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 308px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Extent of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 216px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Surgery done</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 168px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 168px;\">\n<p>Low-risk DTC <br/>(Fulfilling all 5 of the following conditions)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>&lt; 40 years (better prognosis)</li>\n<li>Unifocal lesion</li>\n<li>Size &lt; 2 cm</li>\n<li>No lymphovascular invasion</li>\n<li>No capsular invasion</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 168px;\">Hemithyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>High- risk DTC without LN involvement <br/>(not fulfilling all of the above 5 conditions)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 64px;\">Total thyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 34px;\">\n<p><strong>High-risk DTC + level VI LN involved</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 34px;\"><strong>Total thyroidectomy + Central neck dissection</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>High-risk DTC + other LNs involved</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 54px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Modified radical neck dissection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8818","text":"A patient presented with a‍ thyroid nodule. The USG ​showed an irregular hypoe​choic mass with microcalc‍ifications. Lymph nodes w​ere not involved. FNAC sh​owed a follicular neoplas‌m with predominantly Hurt​hle cells. Intra-op froze‌n section confirms capsul‌ar invasion. What is the ​most appropriate manageme​nt?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a thyroid nodule. The USG showed an irregular hypoechoic mass with microcalcifications. Lymph nodes were not involved. FNAC showed a follicular neoplasm with predominantly Hurthle cells. Intra-op frozen section confirms capsular invasion. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total thyroidectomy centra‍l neck dissection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Total thy‍roidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemithyr‍oidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemithyroidectomy with rad‍ioiodine ablation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Total thyroidectomy central neck dissection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d247de175eeb41ba8a811455b533118dx1280x1168.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Total thyroidectomy central neck dissection</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Hurthle cell follicular carcinoma</strong>. The most appropriate management is<strong> total thyroidectomy</strong> with prophylactic<strong> central neck dissection. </strong>Hurthle cell carcinoma is a variant of follicular thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>As compared to follicular thyroid cancer, it has a <strong>poorer prognosis</strong> as it is more likely to <strong>metastasize</strong>. It is often bilateral and multifocal. The histological picture consists of <strong>predominantly oxyphil</strong> (Hurthle, Askanazy) cells containing abundant mitochondria. It cannot be diagnosed solely by FNAC as these cancers are characterized by vascular &amp; capsular invasion.</p>\n<p>Surgical management of Hurthle cell carcinoma:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Extent of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Surgery done</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unilateral Hurthle cell adenoma</td>\n<td>Hemithyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bilateral involvement without level VI LNs involvement</td>\n<td>Total thyroidectomy +  <strong>Central neck dissection (prophylactic)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hurthle cell carcinoma with level VI LNs involved</td>\n<td>Total thyroidectomy +  Central neck dissection (therapeutic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hurthle cell carcinoma with other LNs involved</td>\n<td>Total thyroidectomy + Modified radical neck dissection </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8814","text":"A 32-year-old woman came t‍o the surgeon with a thyr​oid swelling that had dev​eloped a year ago. On exa‍mination, the swelling wa​s 3 cm in size, firm and ​immobile. Further, lympha‌denopathy was present. A ​biopsy of the swelling is‌ given below. Which of th‌e following is incorrect ​regarding management and ​post-op care in this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman came to the surgeon with a thyroid swelling that had developed a year ago. On examination, the swelling was 3 cm in size, firm and immobile. Further, lymphadenopathy was present. A biopsy of the swelling is given below. Which of the following is incorrect regarding management and post-op care in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium supplementation is‍ given post-operatively","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prophylactic neck dissecti‍on is done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine is withheld afte‍r the surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemithyroidectomy is perfo‍rmed","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemithyroidectomy is performed","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6cad839cfa8e4cedbbb7ba264d3f93d1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87b3c0031f2141df88d7aacc4b980d21x1279x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemithyroidectomy is performed</strong></p><p>The given histological image depicts papillary formation with a fibrovascular stalk, characteristic <strong>orphan annie eye nuclei </strong>and <strong>psammoma bodies</strong>, diagnostic of <strong>papillary carcinoma</strong> of the thyroid.<br/><strong>Total thyroidectomy</strong> with <strong>central neck dissection</strong> would be the appropriate management in this condition, because there is <strong>lymphadenopathy, </strong>although the tumour size is less than 4 cm.</p>\n<p>Surgical management of differentiated thyroid cancers (DTCs) is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 534px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 308px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Extent of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 216px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Surgery done</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 168px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 168px;\">\n<p>Low-risk DTC <br/>(Fulfilling all 5 of the following conditions)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>&lt; 40 years (better prognosis)</li>\n<li>Unifocal lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Size ~ 1-4 cm</strong></li>\n<li>No lymphovascular invasion</li>\n<li>No capsular invasion</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 168px;\">Hemithyroidectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 64px;\">\n<p><strong>High-risk DTC without LN involvement / </strong><strong>not fulfilling any of the above 5 conditions</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 64px;\"><strong>Total thyroidectomy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 34px;\">\n<p>High-risk DTC + level VI LN involved</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 34px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Central neck dissection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 308px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>High-risk DTC + other LNs involved</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216px; height: 54px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Modified radical neck dissection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>The other options:<br/>Option 1: Post thyroidectomy, calcium supplementation is given to all patients to prevent hypocalcemia.<br/>Option 2: Prophylactic neck dissection is done for papillary carcinoma thyroid<br/>Option 3: Thyroid hormone replacement is not given to patients who are to receive radioactive iodine therapy within 2 to 3 weeks of surgery. The absence of thyroid hormones raises the TSH, which stimulates the radioactive iodine uptake.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0291","text":"Which of the following des‍cribes lateral aberrant t​hyroid?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes lateral aberrant thyroid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defective development of t‍he fourth pharyngeal pouc​h","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastatic thyroid cancer ‍in lymph nodes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperplastic growth of thy‍roid tissue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic swelling of the thy‍roglossal duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metastatic thyroid cancer in lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f7f55e4cbb64e69bf731a6eaf368107x720x503.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metastatic thyroid cancer in lymph nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral aberrant thyroid</strong> almost always represents <strong>metastatic thyroid cancer </strong>in <strong>lymph nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thyroid tissue situated lateral to the carotid sheath and jugular vein found along the course of the thyroglossal duct was previously termed lateral aberrant thyroid. It is associated with <strong>papillary thyroid cancer</strong> in the ipsilateral thyroid lobe.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the lateral aberrant thyroid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4671","text":"Which type of thyroid canc‍er has the best prognosis​?","raw_text":"Which type of thyroid cancer has the best prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillary‍ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hurthle cel‍l carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Papillary carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Papillary carcinoma</strong></p><p>Patients with papillary thyroid carcinoma have an <strong>excellent prognosis</strong> with a &gt;95% 10-year survival rate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8813","text":"A 45-year-old man came to ‍the surgery OPD with a mi​dline neck swelling. He d​oes not have any other co‍mplaints. The patient had​ received chemoradiation ​for Hodgkin’s lymphoma ne‌arly 20 years ago. On pal​pation, the mass is about‌ 3 cm in size, non-tender‌, hard, fixed, and seems ​to arise from the right l​obe of the thyroid. TSH l​evels were raised. A radi​oiodine uptake study show​ed cold nodule. What is t​he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man came to the surgery OPD with a midline neck swelling. He does not have any other complaints. The patient had received chemoradiation for Hodgkin’s lymphoma nearly 20 years ago. On palpation, the mass is about 3 cm in size, non-tender, hard, fixed, and seems to arise from the right lobe of the thyroid. TSH levels were raised. A radioiodine uptake study showed cold nodule. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaplastic t‍hyroid cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillary th‍yroid cancer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary th‍yroid cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Papillary thyroid cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Papillary thyroid cancer</strong></p><p>The history of radiation therapy to the head and neck for Hodkin's lymphoma and a cold nodule on radioiodine uptake scan are suggestive of <strong>papillary thyroid cancer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Radiation exposure</strong> is an identifiable aetiological factor in the development of differentiated thyroid cancer (follicular and papillary thyroid cancer). The most important factor, however, is <strong>irradiation of the thyroid before 5 years of age. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Thyroid lymphomas</strong> are non-Hodgkin’s B-cell lymphomas. They present with B-symptoms like fever, night sweats, and weight loss in addition to neck swelling. Thus, thyroid lymphoma is an unlikely diagnosis. Thyroid lymphomas often develop in the setting of lymphocytic infiltration. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8824","text":"A 45-year-old patient pres‍ents with diffuse enlarge​ment of the thyroid gland​. Laboratory studies reve‍al low T3, T4, high TSH a​nd the presence of anti-T​PO antibodies. FNAC from ‌the gland shows enlarged ​cells with abundant eosin‌ophilic cytoplasm. Which ‌of the following malignan​cies is commonly associat​ed with this condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient presents with diffuse enlargement of the thyroid gland. Laboratory studies reveal low T3, T4, high TSH and the presence of anti-TPO antibodies. FNAC from the gland shows enlarged cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm. Which of the following malignancies is commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicular‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anaplastic‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroid ‍lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hurthle cel‍l carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thyroid lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thyroid lymphoma</strong></p><p>The clinical case scenario is suggestive of <strong>Hashimoto's thyroiditis</strong>. <strong>Thyroid lymphoma</strong> or malignant lymphoma is associated with long-standing Hashimoto’s thyroiditis.</p>\n<p>Thyroid lymphomas are usually <strong>non-Hodgkin’s</strong> lymphomas, often associated with long-standing <strong>Hashimoto’s thyroiditis</strong> (autoimmune lymphocytic thyroiditis).</p>\n<p>The clinical features include: <strong>B - symptoms</strong> i.e. fever, night sweats, and weight loss</p>\n<p>As FNAC cannot characterize lymphoma, Tru-Cut biopsy is preferred. Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are often sufficient. Total thyroidectomy is indicated only in residual/recurrent disease.</p>\n<p>A chemotherapy regimen <strong>(RCHOP)</strong> is the first line of management.<br/><strong>R</strong> - Rituximab<br/><strong>C</strong> - Cyclophosphamide<br/><strong>H</strong> - Hydroxydaunorubicin (Adriamycin)<br/><strong>O</strong> - Oncovin (Vincristine)<br/><strong>P</strong> - Prednisolone</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8823","text":"A 72-year-old woman has a ‍history of papillary thyr​oid carcinoma left untrea​ted for 2 years. She comp‍lains that the rapid enla​rgement in her neck swell​ing over the last month h‌as made it difficult for ​her to swallow and talk. ‌What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old woman has a history of papillary thyroid carcinoma left untreated for 2 years. She complains that the rapid enlargement in her neck swelling over the last month has made it difficult for her to swallow and talk. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Local invasion of the surr‍ounding structures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Progression to Hurthle cel‍l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conversion to follicular t‍hyroid cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaplastic t‍hyroid cancer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anaplastic thyroid cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anaplastic thyroid cancer</strong></p><p>An <strong>elderly woman</strong> with <strong>rapidly enlarging</strong> neck mass and <strong>dysphagia</strong> in the setting of an <strong>untreated papillary carcinoma</strong> suggests a diagnosis of <strong>anaplastic thyroid cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anaplastic thyroid cancer, a <strong>poorly differentiated</strong> thyroid carcinoma, is the <strong>most aggressive cancer</strong> and has the <strong>worst prognosis</strong> of all thyroid malignancies. </p>\n<p>It usually occurs in <strong>elderly females.</strong> The typical manifestation is an with <strong>rapidly enlarging</strong> neck mass, dysphagia, and cervical tenderness. Patients usually have a history of untreated <strong>differentiated thyroid carcinoma (DTC). </strong>Local invasion to the recurrent laryngeal nerve <strong>(RLN)</strong> causes hoarseness. It may also cause tracheal obstruction. <strong>Distant metastasis</strong> is more common in the <strong>lungs</strong> via the hematogenous route.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is made by <strong>FNAC.</strong> However, trucut/open biopsy is frequently used to confirm the diagnosis in cases of doubt.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <strong>metastasis is absent:</strong> Total thyroidectomy + LN dissection</li>\n<li>If <strong>metastasis is present:</strong> Palliative care with chemotherapy and radiotherapy</li>\n<li>If <strong>tracheal obstruction:</strong> Isthmectomy to relieve obstruction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8819","text":"A 34-year-old woman was br‍ought in a dehydrated sta​te and complains of loose​ stools. On examination, ‍a tender neck swelling wa​s found which was found t​o be present for 1 year. ‌Histopathology of the swe​lling shows the following‌ finding. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman was brought in a dehydrated state and complains of loose stools. On examination, a tender neck swelling was found which was found to be present for 1 year. Histopathology of the swelling shows the following finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary thyroid carcinom‍a","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic a‍myloidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroid ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary thyroid carcinom‍a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medullary thyroid carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39a84c606e19450086350fdba90d72c7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medullary thyroid carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario<strong> </strong>is suggestive of <strong>medullary thyroid carcinoma </strong>(MTC). Histopathology shows the presence of amyloid deposits that are derived from the altered calcitonin molecules.</p>\n<p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma is a tumor of <strong>parafollicular or C cells</strong> of the thyroid gland. They are derived from the neural crest cells of the <strong>ultimobranchial bodies</strong>. </p>\n<p>Most of the MTCs occur sporadically. However, around a quarter of the cancers are associated with familial MTC, MEN2A, and MEN2B.</p>\n<p>Patients with sporadic MTC present at an older age, whereas patients with familial MTC usually present at a younger age. Features of MTC include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painful neck mass with cervical lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Dysphagia, dysphonia or dyspnea if locally invaded</li>\n<li><strong>Diarrhoea</strong> due to increased intestinal motility by serotonin</li>\n<li><strong>Cushingoid habitus</strong> due to ectopic ACTH production</li>\n<li>Metastasis is <strong>blood-borne</strong></li>\n<li>Microscopically, they consist of sheets of neoplastic cells separated by collagen and <strong>amyloid stroma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is diagnosed by careful family history, physical examination, <strong>raised calcitonin, CEA levels</strong>, and fine-needle aspiration biopsy of the mass.</p>\n<p><strong>Total thyroidectomy</strong> with <strong>central neck node dissection</strong> is the treatment of choice in MTC patients. External beam radiotherapy can be used in patients with unresectable tumors. Targeted therapy can be considered using vandetanib, sorafenib, sunitinib, and cabozantinib.</p>\n<p><strong>Prophylactic total thyroidectomy</strong> should be considered within <strong>5years</strong> of age in<strong> MEN2A</strong> mutation and within <strong>1year</strong> of age in <strong>MEN2B</strong> mutation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4698","text":"Choose the substances secr‍eted by medullary thyroid​ carcinoma: Calcitonin Ca​rcinoembryonic antigen Se‍rotonin Thyroglobulin His​taminidase","raw_text":"<p>Choose the substances secreted by medullary thyroid carcinoma:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Calcitonin</li>\n<li>Carcinoembryonic antigen</li>\n<li>Serotonin</li>\n<li>Thyroglobulin</li>\n<li>Histaminidase</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‍,2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,‍3,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,3‍,4,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2‍,3,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,5</strong></p><p>Medullary thyroid tumors secrete calcitonin, serotonin, histaminadases, and CEA (carcinoembryonic antigen).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8822","text":"A patient with medullary t‍hyroid carcinoma is poste​d for surgery. He is near​ly 6.5 ft tall, with a sl‍ender build and elongated​ digits. What is the scre​ening test that should be‌ done pre-operatively?","raw_text":"A patient with medullary thyroid carcinoma is posted for surgery. He is nearly 6.5 ft tall, with a slender build and elongated digits. What is the screening test that should be done pre-operatively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI-‍Brain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum ‍calcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary cat‍echolamines","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IGF-1‍ assay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urinary catecholamines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urinary catecholamines</strong></p><p>This patient with <strong>medullary thyroid carcinoma</strong> and features of <strong>marfanoid </strong>habitus (very tall patient with long, slender fingers and toes) is suggestive of <strong>MEN 2B</strong> syndrome. Such patients are to be <strong>screened</strong> for <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong> by measuring <strong>urinary catecholamines.</strong></p>\n<p>Pheochromocytoma, if present, should be treated first, and then MTC is to be treated next.</p>\n<p>A thyroid swelling with elevated calcitonin indicates a medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC). MTC can either be <strong>sporadic</strong> (80%) or <strong>familial</strong> (RET gene mutation). In case of <strong>familial</strong> medullary thyroid carcinoma, <strong>stimulation</strong> with <strong>pentagastrin</strong> increases the release of calcitonin. This can be used for the screening of medullary thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p>MTC can be inherited as a part of <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> (AD) syndromes such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Familial MTC</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MEN 2A</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MEN 2B</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prophylactic total thyroidectomy</strong> should be considered within <strong>5years</strong> of age in<strong> MEN2A</strong> mutation and within <strong>1year</strong> of age in <strong>MEN2B</strong> mutation.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Serum calcium is measured to screen for parathyroid adenoma in MEN 2A.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4188","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‍h medullary thyroid carci​noma. Which of the follow​ing could be used to scre‍en the rest of her family​?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with medullary thyroid carcinoma. Which of the following could be used to screen the rest of her family?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pentag‍astrin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Histami‍nadases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prostaglandin E2 and F2 al‍pha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‍tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pentagastrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pentagastrin</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1577\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Screening of patients with familial MTC was historically done using <strong>pentagastrin stimulation test</strong> for <strong>calcitonin </strong>levels to make the diagnosis. </p>\n<p>Pentagastrin is a synthetic gastrin analogue. It acts on the CCK-B/gastrin receptor of the C cells and <strong>increases</strong> the basal rate of <strong>calcitonin</strong> <strong>secretion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Basal calcitonin level = total C cell mass x rate of secretion.</p>\n<p>Medullary thyroid tumors secrete calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA), but also other peptides such as calcitonin gene-related peptide, histaminadases, prostaglandins E2 and F2α, and serotonin.</p>\n<p>Another test that can be used for screening is calcium-stimulated calcitonin levels.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8820","text":"A 32-year-old female was d‍iagnosed with medullary t​hyroid carcinoma. On USG ​paratracheal lymph node i‍nvolvement was found. Wha​t is the best method of m​anagement of this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old female was diagnosed with medullary thyroid carcinoma. On USG paratracheal lymph node involvement was found. What is the best method of management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total thyroidectomy with c‍entral neck dissection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total thyroidectomy with c‍entral neck dissection an​d RAI ablation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total thyroidectomy with m‍odified radical and centr​al neck dissection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Total thyroidectomy with l‍ateral and central neck d​issection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total thyroidectomy with modified radical and central neck dissection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total thyroidectomy with modified radical and central neck dissection</strong></p><p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) with Level VI or paratracheal lymph node involvement is managed with <strong>total thyroidectomy </strong>with <strong>central neck dissection </strong>and <strong>modified radical neck dissection. </strong></p>\n<p>Surgical management of MTC is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 113px; width: 611px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 195px;\"><strong>Extent of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 402px;\"><strong>Surgery done</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 195px;\">\n<p>MTC without LN involvement</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 402px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Central neck dissection (prophylactic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 195px;\">\n<p><strong>MTC + level VI LN involved</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 402px;\"><strong>Total thyroidectomy + Central neck dissection (therapeutic) + Modified radical neck dissection (prophylactic)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 19px; width: 195px;\">\n<p>MTC + other LNs involved</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 19px; width: 402px;\">Total thyroidectomy + Central neck dissection + Modified radical neck dissection (therapeutic)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Prophylactic neck dissection is not routinely performed in papillary or follicular carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8825","text":"Prophylactic thyroidectomy‍ is indicated in patients​ with which of the follow​ing features:","raw_text":"Prophylactic thyroidectomy is indicated in patients with which of the following features:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long standing thyroglossal‍ cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marfanoid habitus, multipl‍e neuromas on the lip and​ familial pheochromocytom​a,","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dense lymphatic infiltrati‍on of the thyroid detecte​d on FNAC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upregulati‍on of BRAF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marfanoid habitus, multiple neuromas on the lip and familial pheochromocytoma,","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marfanoid habitus, multiple neuromas on the lip and familial pheochromocytoma,</strong></p><p>Prophylactic thyroidectomy is indicated in <strong>MEN 2B</strong> syndrome as they can develop medullary thyroid carcinoma. This syndrome manifests as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>Marfanoid habitus</li>\n<li>Mucosal neuromas</li>\n<li>Medullary thyroid cancer. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) can be inherited as <strong>familial MTC, MEN 2A</strong> or <strong>MEN 2B</strong> syndromes. These conditions are autosomal dominant and occur due to <strong>RET proto-oncogene mutation</strong>. As almost all cases of MEN 2B develop medullary thyroid cancer, a prophylactic thyroidectomy may be performed even during infancy.</p>\n<p>Option A: The most common malignancy in <strong>thyroglossal</strong> cysts is <strong>papillary</strong> thyroid carcinoma. In such a case, Sistrunk operation with total thyroidectomy is considered.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hashimoto's</strong> thyroiditis shows dense lymphatic infiltration of the thyroid detected on FNAC which is treated with thyroxine. However, total thyroidectomy is performed in malignant transformation to thyroid lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Option D: Upregulation of <strong>BRAF</strong> is associated with <strong>papillary</strong> thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8826","text":"What is the most common si‍te of distant metastasis ​in patients with medullar​y thyroid carcinoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of distant metastasis in patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‍ver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bo‍ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lu‍ng","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adr‍enal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liver</strong></p><p><strong>Liver</strong> <strong>metastasis</strong> is more common in <strong>medullary</strong> thyroid carcinoma.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Type of thyroid cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Most common distant site</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Papillary</td>\n<td>Lungs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Follicular</td>\n<td>Bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Medullary</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Liver</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anaplastic</td>\n<td>Lungs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4711","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about th​e 8th edition AJCC stagin​g of thyroid neoplasms?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the 8th edition AJCC staging of thyroid neoplasms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In DTC, distant metastasis‍ is IVB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In staging, age barrier is‍ 55 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T3a includes minor extrath‍yroidal extension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"N1a includes level VI and ‍VII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T3a includes minor extrathyroidal extension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T3a includes minor extrathyroidal extension</strong></p><p><strong>Minor extrathyroidal extension</strong> detected only on histological examination is <strong>removed from the definition of T3</strong> disease and therefore has no impact on either T category or overall stage.</p>\n<p><strong>Differentiated Thyroid Cancer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>age</strong> <strong>cutoff</strong> used for staging is increased from 45 to <strong>55 years</strong> of age at diagnosis. </li>\n<li>N1 disease no longer upstages a patient to stage III. If<strong> &lt; 55 years</strong> of age at diagnosis, <strong>N1 disease is stage I</strong>. If <strong>≥ 55 years</strong> of age, <strong>N1 disease is stage II.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>T3a</strong> is a new category for tumors <strong>&gt; 4 cm confined to the thyroid gland</strong></li>\n<li><strong>T3b</strong> is a new category for tumors of any size demonstrating<strong> gross extrathyroidal extension into strap muscles</strong> (sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid or omohyoid muscles)</li>\n<li><strong>Level VII</strong> lymph nodes, previously classified as lateral neck lymph nodes (N1b) is re-classified as central neck lymph nodes <strong>(N1a)</strong> to be more anatomically consistent and because level VII presented significant coding difficulties for tumor registrars, clinicians and researchers.</li>\n<li>In differentiated thyroid cancer, the presence of <strong>distant metastases</strong> <strong>(M1)</strong> in older patients is classified as <strong>IVB</strong> disease rather than IVC disease. Distant metastasis in anaplastic thyroid cancer continues to be classified as IVC disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Minimal or occult/microcarcinoma are tumors 1 cm or less in size. They have no evidence of local invasiveness through the thyroid capsule or angioinvasion. Occult malignancies are usually not associated with lymph node metastases. However, about <strong>25% of patients </strong>with these tumors have associated occult lymph node metastases. These are <strong>nonpalpable </strong>and are found<strong> i</strong><strong>ncidentally</strong> at operative and histologic examinations or at autopsy.  </p>\n<table style=\"width: 382.253px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">TNM staging</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">7th AJCC (2010)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\"><strong>8th AJCC (2018)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Primary tumor(pT)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">TX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Cannot be assessed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">T0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">No evidence of cancer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">T1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Tumour ≤ 2 cm, intrathyroidal\n<ul>\n<li>T1a: ≤1 cm, intrathyroidal</li>\n<li>T1b<strong>:</strong> 1-2 cm, intrathyroidal</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">T2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 927\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>2-4 cm, intrathyroidal</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 927\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>&gt;4 cm limited to the thyroid or any size with minimal extrathyroidal extension (muscle, soft tissue)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\"><br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>T3a: </strong>Tumor <strong>&gt;4 cm limited to the thyroid</strong></li>\n<li><strong>T3b: Gross extrathyroidal extension</strong> invading only <strong>strap muscles</strong> (sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid or omohyoid muscles) from a tumour of any size</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">T4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 927\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>T4a Invades subcutaneous tissue, larynx, trachea, esophagus, or RLN</li>\n<li>T4b Invades prevertebral fascia or encases carotid or mediastinal vessels</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>-do-<br/><br/></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Regional lymph nodes (pN)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">NX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Regional lymph nodes cannot be assessed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">N0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">No lymph node metastasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">N1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 927\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 927\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Positive in level VI; unilateral or bilateral disease</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\"><strong>Metastasis to level VI or VII; </strong>unilateral or bilateral disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">N1b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 927\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Metastasis to unilateral, bilateral or contralateral lateral neck lymph nodes (levels I, II, III, IV or V) or retropharyngeal lymph nodes</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-<br/><br/></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Distant metastasis (pM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">No Distant metastasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Distant metastasis present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.253px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 216px; width: 389px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 645px; height: 18px;\">                  TNM CLASSIFICATION of Thyroid tumours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 645px; height: 18px;\">PAPILLARY OR FOLLICULAR TUMORS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 38px;\">Stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 38px;\">TNM AJCC 7<sup>th</sup> edition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 38px;\"><strong>TNM AJCC 8<sup>th</sup> edition</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\">&lt;45 y</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\"> <strong>&lt;55 y</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 46px;\"> Any T, any N, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 46px;\"> -do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 46px;\"> Any T, any N, M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 46px;\"> -do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\"> ≥45 y</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\"><strong> &gt;55 y</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\"> T1, N0, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\"> <strong>T1-2</strong>, N0, M0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 78px;\">II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 78px;\"> T2, N0, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 78px;\">\n<p> T1-2, <strong>N1</strong>, M0</p>\n<p><strong>T3</strong>, <strong>any N</strong>, M0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 78px;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 78px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1572\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>T3, N0, M0</p>\n<p>T1–3, N1a, M0</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 78px;\"><strong>T4a, any N</strong>, M0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 78px;\">IV A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>T4a, N0-1a, M0</p>\n<p>T1-4a, N1b, M0</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 78px;\"> <strong>T4b, any N</strong>, M0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\"> IV B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\"> T4b, any N, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\"> <strong>any T, any N, M1 </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59.8438px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 59.8438px;\">\n<p>IV C</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 59.8438px;\">\n<p>Any T, any N, M1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 59.8438px;\">\n<p><strong>- deleted-</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 645px; height: 18px;\">MEDULLARY THYROID CANCER </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">Stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\">TNM AJCC 7<sup>th</sup> edition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\"><strong>TNM AJCC 8<sup>th</sup> edition</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\">T1, N0, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\">T2–3, N0, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\">-do-  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\">T1–3, N1a, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\">-do- </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">IV A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>T4a, any N, M0;</p>\n<p>T1-3, N1b, M0 </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">IV B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\"> T4b, any N, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 18px;\">IV C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.116px; height: 18px;\"> Any T, any N, M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281.884px; height: 18px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Anaplastic :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unlike previous editions where all anaplastic thyroid cancers were classified as T4 disease, anaplastic cancers now use the<strong> same T  definitions as differentiated thyroid cancer</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Intrathyroidal disease</strong> (<strong>T1-3a)</strong> is stage <strong>IVA</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gross extrathyroidal extension</strong>(<strong>T3b,T4)</strong> or <strong>cervical lymph node metastases (N1)</strong> is stage <strong>IVB</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Distant metastases</strong> <strong>(M1)</strong> are stage <strong>IVC</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 216px; width: 372px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 621px; height: 45px;\">\n<p>ANAPLASTIC CANCER</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Stage</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.628px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>TNM AJCC 7<sup>th</sup> edition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.372px; height: 46px;\"> <strong>TNM AJCC 8<sup>th</sup>edition</strong>   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>IV A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.628px; height: 46px;\"> T4a, Any N, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.372px; height: 46px;\"> <strong>T1-3a, N0,</strong> M0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 46px;\"> IV B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.628px; height: 46px;\"> T4b, Any N, M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.372px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>T1-3a, <strong>N1</strong>, M0;</p>\n<p><strong>T3b</strong>, Any N, M0;</p>\n<p><strong>T4</strong>, Any N, M0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 46px;\">IV C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.628px; height: 46px;\"> Any T, Any N, M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.372px; height: 46px;\"> -do-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7589","text":"Which of the following wil‍l not be a treatment opti​on for a pregnant patient​ with papillary thyroid c‍arcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following will not be a treatment option for a pregnant patient with papillary thyroid carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total Thy‍roidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total Thyroidectomy plus L‍ymph node dissection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radioiodine ablation of th‍yroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right Hemit‍hyroidectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Radioiodine ablation of thyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Radioiodine ablation of thyroid</strong></p><p>Radioiodine ablation of thyroid is <strong>contraindicated in pregnant women </strong>due to risk of <strong>radiation exposure to the fetus</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Therapeutic uses</strong> for radioactive iodine</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In primary thyrotoxicosis after 45 years.</strong></li>\n<li>In <strong>autonomous toxic nodule after 45 years</strong>, it is useful as remaining gland still will function adequately after radiotherapy.</li>\n<li>In <strong>follicular carcinoma of thyroid, after total thyroidectomy</strong>, if there are secondaries elsewhere in the body, as in bones or lungs, then I<sub>131 </sub>is given.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8815","text":"A patient with papillary t‍hyroid carcinoma underwen​t total thyroidectomy wit​h central neck dissection‍, a month ago. At present​, a whole-body iodine sca​n is positive. What is th‌e next step in management​?","raw_text":"A patient with papillary thyroid carcinoma underwent total thyroidectomy with central neck dissection, a month ago. At present, a whole-body iodine scan is positive. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Re-exploration of thyroid ‍gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radio-iodi‍ne ablation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"USG-‍neck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bi-annual‍ follow-up","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radio-iodine ablation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radio-iodine ablation</strong></p><p><strong>Radio-iodine ablation</strong> is undertaken when a whole-body iodine scan is positive after a total thyroidectomy for any differentiated thyroid cancer (papillary or follicular carcinoma). </p>\n<p>The presence of any metastatic or residual thyroid tissue after total thyroidectomy can be detected by observing the uptake of radio-iodine in the entire body. To achieve effective uptake of radio-iodine, TSH levels need to be greatly increased, for example, by withdrawing thyroxine after the surgery for <strong>4-6 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>positive</strong> whole-body iodine scan after total thyroidectomy indicates that there is either a metastatic or residual disease present. These patients are treated with I<sup>131</sup> for <strong>radio-iodine ablation</strong>. </p>\n<p>A <strong>negative</strong> scan means there is no metastasis or residual thyroid tissue. They are followed up regularly with serum thyroglobulin, TSH and USG-neck.</p>\n<p>If the tumour is <strong>resistant</strong> to radioiodine <strong>ablation</strong>, <strong>EBRT</strong> (external beam radiotherapy) is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8821","text":"A whole-body iodine scan i‍s not useful in patients ​with which thyroid malign​ancy?","raw_text":"A whole-body iodine scan is not useful in patients with which thyroid malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillary th‍yroid cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anaplastic t‍hyroid cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary th‍yroid cancer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Follicular t‍hyroid cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medullary thyroid cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medullary thyroid cancer</strong></p><p>After a total thyroidectomy, a whole-body iodine scan is not useful for detecting residual thyroid tissue or metastasis in <strong>medullary thyroid carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma is a tumour of <strong>parafollicular</strong> or<strong> C cells. </strong>These cells which are derived from neural crest cells, <strong>do not take up iodine</strong>.  </p>\n<p>A whole-body iodine scan is useful in thyroid tumours derived from follicular cells that take up iodine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4672","text":"Which of the following sca‍les consider the complete​ness of surgical resectio​n to determine the progno‍sis of a patient with thy​roid cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following scales consider the completeness of surgical resection to determine the prognosis of a patient with thyroid cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AM‍ES","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AG‍ES","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MA‍CIS","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T‍NM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MACIS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MACIS</strong></p><p>MACIS scale includes completeness of the original surgical resection to determine the prognosis of the thyroid cancer.</p>\n<p><strong>Prognostic indicators</strong> of well differentiated thyroid cancers: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>AGES</strong> system - <strong>A</strong>ge, histologic<strong> G</strong>rade, <strong>E</strong>xtrathyroidal invasion, and metastases and tumor <strong>S</strong>ize.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>AMES</strong> system - <strong>A</strong>ge (men &lt;40 years old, women &lt;50 years old), <strong>M</strong>etastases, <strong>E</strong>xtrathyroidal spread, and <strong>S</strong>ize of tumors (&lt; or &gt;5 cm).\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li1\">DAMES - DNA aneuploidy + AMES</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">GAMES - Grade + AMES</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>TNM</strong> staging - <strong>T</strong>umor, <strong>N</strong>odal status,<strong> M</strong>etastases.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>MACIS</strong> scale (postop modification) - distant <strong>M</strong>etastases, <strong>A</strong>ge at presentation (&lt;40 or &gt;40 years old), <strong>Completeness of original surgical resection</strong>, extrathyroidal <strong>I</strong>nvasion, and <strong>S</strong>ize of original lesion (in centimeters).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zwHSFFcW","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"14_inhaled_anaesthetics___properties__n2o_and_halothane","title":"14.Inhaled Anaesthetics   Properties, N2O And Halothane","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5856","text":"Which among the following ​is the most potent inhale​d anesthetic?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most potent inhaled anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Halothane</strong></p><p>The <strong>most potent</strong> inhaled anesthetic among the given options is <strong>halothane, </strong>with a minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) value of 0.75%.</p>\n<p>The <strong>least potent</strong> inhaled anesthetic is <strong>nitrous oxide</strong> with a MAC value of 104%.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Methoxyflurane has a MAC of 0.16%, making it more potent than halothane. However, it is obsolete now. The most potent agent overall is methoxyflurane and the most potent agent in clinical use is halothane.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5852","text":"At what concentration does​ the administration of an​ inhaled general anesthet‍ic prevent movement in re‌sponse to noxious stimuli‍ in 50% of the population​?","raw_text":"At what concentration does the administration of an inhaled general anesthetic prevent movement in response to noxious stimuli in 50% of the population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum anesthetic concent​ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimum anesthetic concent​ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maximum alveolar concentra​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Minimum alveolar concentra​tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Minimum alveolar concentration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Minimum alveolar concentration</strong></p><p><strong>M</strong><strong>inimum alveolar concentration</strong> (MAC)<strong> </strong>is the inhaled anesthetic concentration required to <strong>prevent movement</strong> in response <strong>to noxious</strong> stimuli in <strong>50%</strong> of patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5857","text":"What does the blood-gas pa​rtition coefficient of an​ inhaled anesthetic indic‍ate?","raw_text":"What does the blood-gas partition coefficient of an inhaled anesthetic indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duration ​of action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pot​ency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Solubilit​y in blood","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Solubilit​y in lipid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Solubility in blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07a4b3ffcc41420c90d72d54795fba78x860x68.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Solubility in blood</strong></p><p>The <strong>blood gas</strong> (B:G) <strong>partition coefficient</strong> of an inhaled anesthetic is an indicator of <strong>solubility in</strong> <strong>blood </strong>which determines the<strong> speed of induction</strong> and<strong> recovery</strong>.</p>\n<p>An inhalational anesthetic with a <strong>lower</strong> blood gas partition<strong> (B:G)</strong> coefficient would have <strong>lower solubility</strong> in blood. This would mean a <strong>higher alveolar concentration</strong> which will lead to <strong>faster induction </strong>and<strong> recovery.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Xenon </strong>with a blood-gas partition coefficient of <strong>0.115</strong> has the <strong>fastest</strong> induction and emergence from anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Methoxyflurane</strong> with a blood gas partition coefficient of <strong>12</strong> has the <strong>slowest</strong> induction and emergence from anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>The speed of induction and emergence:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Xenon &gt; Desflurane</strong> &gt; Sevoflurane&gt; Isoflurane &gt; Enflurane &gt; <strong>Halothane &gt; Methoxyflurane</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5860","text":"Which anesthetic agent has​ the least solubility in ​blood?","raw_text":"Which anesthetic agent has the least solubility in blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Xe​non","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Xenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Xenon</strong></p><p>The anesthetic agent, <strong>least soluble</strong> in the <strong>blood</strong> is <strong>xenon </strong>with a blood:gas partition coefficient of 0.115</p>\n<p>The speed of induction and emergence: </p>\n<p><strong>Xenon &gt; Desflurane </strong>&gt; Sevoflurane &gt; Isoflurane &gt; Enflurane &gt; <strong>Halothane &gt; Methoxyflurane</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5854","text":"What is the rationale for ​choosing an inhaled anest​hetic with the lowest MAC‍ value?","raw_text":"What is the rationale for choosing an inhaled anesthetic with the lowest MAC value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fastest induction and reco​very","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highest​ potency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Slowest inducton and recov​ery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lowest ​potency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Highest potency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Highest potency</strong></p><p>A drug with the <strong>lowest MAC</strong> would have the <strong>highest potency</strong>.</p>\n<p>The minimum alveolar concentration <strong>(MAC)</strong> value of an inhaled anesthetic is a measure of the <strong>potency</strong> of the anesthetic agent.</p>\n<p><strong>Least</strong> potent:<strong> nitrous oxide</strong> (MAC-104%). 1 MAC with nitrous oxide is practically impossible and thus it is always combined with other agents.</p>\n<p><strong>Most</strong> potent: <strong>methoxyflurane</strong> (MAC-0.16%) &gt; trilene (MAC-0.2%), both of which are <strong>obsolete</strong> now.</p>\n<p>Among the inhaled agents in clinical use, <strong>halothane</strong> is the most potent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5861","text":"According to Meyer Overton​ rule, which of the follo​wing is related to the po‍tency of an anesthetic?","raw_text":"According to Meyer Overton rule, which of the following is related to the potency of an anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Solubilit​y in blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Solubilit​y in lipid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood- gas partition coeff​icient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Minimum Alveolar Concentra​tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Solubility in lipid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Solubility in lipid</strong></p><p><strong>Meyer Overton rule </strong>relates <strong>potency</strong> of an anesthetic to the<strong> solubility in lipid.</strong></p>\n<p>This rule suggests that anesthesia occurs when a sufficient number of inhalational anesthetic molecules dissolve in the lipid cell membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5785","text":"At what concentration does​ the administration of an​ inhaled general anesthet‍ic prevent movement in re‌sponse to noxious stimuli‍ in 50% of the population​?","raw_text":"At what concentration does the administration of an inhaled general anesthetic prevent movement in response to noxious stimuli in 50% of the population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 % decreas​e per decade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3% increase​ per decade","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6% decrease​ per decade","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6% increase​ per decade","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6% decrease per decade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6% decrease per decade</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The minimum alveolar concentration (<strong>MAC</strong>) <strong>decreases</strong> by<strong> 6% per decade</strong> for inhalational anesthetics. This is due to <strong>altered sensitivity</strong> in the <strong>ion channels</strong> and <strong>receptors</strong> (nicotinic, acetylcholine, glutamate, and GABA).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Recovery </strong>with a volatile anesthetic in <strong>geriatric</strong> patients may be <strong>prolonged</strong> because of an <strong>increased volume of distribution</strong>(increased body fat) and <strong>decreased pulmonary gas exchange.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Dosage</strong> requirements for local and general (MAC) anesthetics are <strong>reduced</strong> in <strong>elderly</strong> patients. Administration of a given volume of epidural or spinal anesthetic results in a more extensive spread and longer duration in elderly patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5855","text":"In which of the following ​patients would a lower am​ount of inhalational anes‍thetic be administered? 5‌9-year-old chronic alcoho‍lic 22-year-old addicted ​to cocaine 26-year-old pr‍egnant lady 6-month-old i​nfant 33-year-old man wit‌h serum calcium 12.5mg/dL‌","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following patients would a lower amount of inhalational anesthetic be administered?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">59-year-old chronic alcoholic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">22-year-old addicted to cocaine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">26-year-old pregnant lady</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">6-month-old infant</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">33-year-old man with serum calcium 12.5mg/dL</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp;​ 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 &amp;am​p; 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 &amp;amp;​ 5 only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 &amp;am​p; 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 & 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 &amp; 5 only</strong></p><p>Minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) <strong>decreases</strong> in <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>. Thus, these patients require a <strong>lower dose</strong> of <strong>inhalational anesthetic</strong> agents.</p>\n<p>Chronic alcoholics, infants, and people with a history of cocaine abuse would require higher doses of anesthetic agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5863","text":"Your senior asks you to ad​minister oxygen to a pati​ent in order to avoid dif‍fusion hypoxia. When is t‌his phenomenon observed d‍uring anesthesia?","raw_text":"Your senior asks you to administer oxygen to a patient in order to avoid diffusion hypoxia. When is this phenomenon observed during anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Induction wi​th halothane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Induction with nitrous oxi​de","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recovery fr​om halothane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recovery from nitrous oxid​e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Recovery from nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Recovery from nitrous oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Diffusion hypoxia</strong> is observed in anesthesia during <strong>recovery </strong>from<strong> nitrous oxide</strong> anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Due to low solubility (low blood gas partition coefficient), N<sub>2</sub>O diffuses from the blood to the alveoli and replaces the oxygen. This reduces alveolar O<sub>2</sub> concentration, resulting in <strong>relative hypoxemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diffusion hypoxia can be avoided by using 100% oxygen for 10 minutes during recovery from anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5865","text":"While setting up the anest​hesia cart in the morning​, you note the technician‍ shifting a blue-colored ‌cylinder to the operation‍ theatre. Which of the fo​llowing is a false statem‍ent about the gas present​ in the cylinder?","raw_text":"While setting up the anesthesia cart in the morning, you note the technician shifting a blue-colored cylinder to the operation theatre. Which of the following is a false statement about the gas present in the cylinder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lea​st potent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It shows second gas effect​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It decreases cerebral meta​bolic rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is an NMDA receptor ant​agonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It decreases cerebral metabolic rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It decreases cerebral metabolic rate</strong></p><p>The given color code of a <strong>blue cylinder</strong> indicates that the gas present inside the cylinder is<strong> nitrous oxide</strong>. Nitrous oxide <strong>increases cerebral metabolic rate</strong>. </p>\n<p>Nitrous oxide increases cerebral metabolic rate, cerebral oxygen consumption, and cerebral blood flow. Hence it is not preferred in neuro anesthesia as it <strong>increases</strong> <strong>intracranial</strong> <strong>pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: N<sub>2</sub>O has a MAC value of 104%, which makes it the <strong>least potent</strong> inhaled anesthetic. Hence it is always used in combination with the more potent volatile agents.</p>\n<p>Option B: N<sub>2</sub>O has low blood-gas solubility resulting in phenomena such as second gas effect, concentration–effect, and diffusion hypoxia.</p>\n<p>Option D: N<sub>2</sub>O acts as an <strong>antagonist</strong> at N-methyl-D-aspartate (<strong>NMDA</strong>) receptors, which are excitatory receptors in the brain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5873","text":"A polytrauma patient with ​the given x-ray finding i​s being taken up for dama‍ge control surgery. Which‌ of the following inhalat‍ional agents should be av​oided in her?","raw_text":"A polytrauma patient with the given x-ray finding is being taken up for damage control surgery. Which of the following inhalational agents should be avoided in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitrous oxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e46c7c294714cbe9d2250714b7bb8a8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>pneumothorax.</strong> The inhalational agent to be <strong>avoided </strong>is<strong> nitrous oxide</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nitrous oxide has the potential to expand air-containing cavities, as it can diffuse into and accumulate in them. Hence it should be avoided in patients with pneumothorax.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5877","text":"In which of the following ​patients can you safely a​dminister nitrous oxide?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients can you safely administer nitrous oxide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 40-year-old man with sig​moid volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 32-year-old man undergoi​ng tympanic membrane graf​ting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 56-year-old woman posted​ for vitreoretinal surger​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 16-year-old girl undergo​ing tooth extraction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 16-year-old girl undergoing tooth extraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 16-year-old girl undergoing tooth extraction</strong></p><p>As the sole agent, <strong>nitrous oxide</strong> can be used <strong>with oxygen</strong> for <strong>dental</strong> <strong>analgesia</strong>. It can also be used for <strong>obstetric analgesia.</strong></p>\n<p>All the other conditions are contraindications to nitrous oxide because it has the potential to expand air-containing cavities, as it can diffuse into and accumulate in them. This increases the pressure within the cavity which can lead to deleterious effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5907","text":"Which anesthetic agent doe​s not undergo metabolism ​in our body?","raw_text":"Which anesthetic agent does not undergo metabolism in our body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong> does <strong>not undergo</strong> any <strong>metabolism</strong> in our body.</p>\n<p><strong>Methoxyflurane</strong> undergoes <strong>maximum</strong> metabolism while <strong>d</strong><strong>esflurane</strong> undergoes <strong>minimal</strong> metabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5911","text":"During the pre-anesthetic ​evaluation of a young wom​an planned for surgery, t‍he following findings are‌ described in the periphe‍ral smear. Which inhalati​onal anesthetic agent sho‍uld be avoided to prevent​ neurological symptoms of‌ this disease from develo‌ping?","raw_text":"During the pre-anesthetic evaluation of a young woman planned for surgery, the following findings are described in the peripheral smear. Which inhalational anesthetic agent should be avoided to prevent neurological symptoms of this disease from developing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nitrous oxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b255d7e6655144d2b2638bbe05d1600a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>The peripheral smear shows macro-ovalocytes and Cabot cells, suggesting <strong>megaloblastic anemia. Nitrous oxide</strong> should <strong>not</strong> be used in a patient with megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<p>N₂O can cause a subclinical B12 deficiency to become overt, leading to <strong>subacute combined degeneration of the cord </strong>(SACD).</p>\n<p>N₂O <strong>inhibits</strong> vitamin B12 dependent enzymes like <strong>methionine synthetase</strong> which is essential for the formation of <strong>myelin</strong>. It also <strong>inhibits</strong> vitamin B12 dependent enzymes like <strong>thymidylate synthetase</strong>, which are necessary for <strong>DNA synthesis. </strong>This leads to the <strong>megaloblastic</strong> bone marrow changes.</p>\n<p>Side effects of N₂O:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone marrow depression</li>\n<li>Megaloblastic anemia – may be seen following nitrous oxide exposure for 2- 6 hours</li>\n<li>Teratogenicity</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5885","text":"A 42-year-old obese woman ​is posted for a revision ​laparotomy surgery. She g‍ives a history of jaundic‌e following the previous ‍surgery. Which drug shoul​d be avoided in her?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old obese woman is posted for a revision laparotomy surgery. She gives a history of jaundice following the previous surgery. Which drug should be avoided in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Xe​non","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Halothane</strong></p><p><strong>Halothane</strong> must not be given to patients with unexplained liver dysfunction following previous anesthetic exposure due to the risk of <strong>halothane-induced hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Halothane-induced hepatitis is <strong>immune-mediated</strong> due to antibodies against highly reactive trifluoroacetyl chloride which is a metabolite of halothane.</p>\n<p>It is rare with an incidence of 1 per 35,000 cases but very fatal, with a mortality of 50-75%.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for halothane hepatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple exposures to halothane at short intervals</li>\n<li>Middle-aged obese women- halothane undergoes extensive metabolism in obese patients</li>\n<li>Familial predisposition to halothane hepatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The liver in halothane hepatitis shows <strong>centrilobular necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5894","text":"A patient with a prior his​tory of halothane hepatit​is is now scheduled for a‍ surgery. Which would be ‌the inhalational agent of‍ choice in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with a prior history of halothane hepatitis is now scheduled for a surgery. Which would be the inhalational agent of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Sevoflurane</strong> is the inhalational agent of <strong>choice</strong> in a patient with a prior history of <strong>halothane hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Unlike the other volatile anesthetics, sevoflurane is oxidized to form a <strong>stable</strong> <strong>metabolite</strong> that does not covalently bind to proteins and cause hepatic injury.</p>\n<p>Halothane hepatitis is immune-mediated due to antibodies against highly reactive trifluoroacetyl chloride which is a metabolite of halothane.</p>\n<p>Like halothane, other agents like isoflurane, desflurane, and enflurane also undergo metabolism to produce highly reactive intermediates that can covalently modify hepatic proteins and cause severe hepatitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5897","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g halothane?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding halothane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum decrease in cardia​c output","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraindicated in pheochr​omocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can trigger malignant hype​rthermia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contraindicated in asthmat​ics","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contraindicated in asthmatics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contraindicated in asthmatics</strong></p><p>Halothane is <strong>not contraindicated</strong> in asthmatics. In fact, it is suitable for induction in asthmatic patients due to its ability to cause <strong>bronchodilation. </strong></p>\n<p>However, the <strong>agent of choice in asthmatics</strong> would be <strong>sevoflurane</strong> due to the low safety profile of halothane. Halothane sensitizes the heart to the effects of adrenaline and causes bradycardia and arrhythmias. An overall cardiovascular and respiratory depression is seen with Halothane which are less likely with sevoflurane.</p>\n<p>Option A: Halothane produces the maximum decrease in cardiac output among the inhalational anesthetics by a combination of decreased heart rate and depressed myocardial contractility.</p>\n<p>Option B: Halothane sensitizes the heart to the arrhythmogenic effects of epinephrine and hence is contraindicated in pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>Option C: Inhaled anesthetics like halothane, methoxyflurane, enflurane, isoflurane, desflurane, and sevoflurane are known to trigger malignant hyperthermia.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5901","text":"Which of the following age​nts does not liberate flu​oride?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents does not liberate fluoride?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Halothane</strong></p><p><strong>Halothane</strong> is not metabolized to fluoride under normal circumstances.</p>\n<p>The <strong>renal toxicity</strong> of volatile anesthetics <strong>is due to</strong> their metabolic breakdown to <strong>free fluoride</strong> ions.</p>\n<p>Methoxyflurane, sevoflurane, and isoflurane liberate fluoride compounds.</p>\n<p>The amount of fluoride ions produced (in micromoles):</p>\n<p><strong>Methoxyflurane</strong> &gt; Sevoflurane &gt; Enflurane &gt; Isoflurane = Desflurane</p>\n<p><strong>Methoxyflurane</strong> is the most nephrotoxic among inhalational anesthetics. It causes <strong>polyuric renal failure</strong> due to a large amount of inorganic fluoride released, and it is no longer used in clinical practice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5917","text":"A 32-year-old man presents​ with recurrent episodes ​of headache, palpitation,‍ and sweating. His BP is ‌160/100 mm Hg and heart r‍ate is 130/min. 24-hour u​rinary metanephrines are ‍elevated and CT abdomen s​hows a right suprarenal m‌ass. He is scheduled for ‌surgery. Which inhalation​al anesthetic is contrain​dicated in him?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presents with recurrent episodes of headache, palpitation, and sweating. His BP is 160/100 mm Hg and heart rate is 130/min. 24-hour urinary metanephrines are elevated and CT abdomen shows a right suprarenal mass. He is scheduled for surgery. Which inhalational anesthetic is contraindicated in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Halothane</strong></p><p>The given findings are suggestive of<strong> pheochromocytoma.</strong> <strong>Halothane</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>Pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine secreting tumor of chromaffin origin. Halothane <strong>sensitizes</strong> the <strong>heart</strong> to the <strong>arrhythmogenic effects</strong> of epinephrine.</p>\n<p>Other anesthetic drugs to be avoided in a patient with pheochromocytoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Succinylcholine - causes fasciculations which lead to raised intraabdominal pressure and catecholamine release from the tumor</li>\n<li>Ketamine - sympathomimetic</li>\n<li>Pancuronium - vagal blockade leading to tachycardia</li>\n<li>Atracurium, tubocurarine - histamine release which may provoke catecholamine release</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5924","text":"A patient was taken up for​ surgery and was administ​ered an inhalational agen‍t for anesthesia. Suddenl‌y his heart rate dropped ‍from 94/min to 58/min. Wh​ich anesthetic agent was ‍most likely administered?​","raw_text":"A patient was taken up for surgery and was administered an inhalational agent for anesthesia. Suddenly his heart rate dropped from 94/min to 58/min. Which anesthetic agent was most likely administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Halothane</strong></p><p><strong>Halothane</strong> induces <strong>bradycardia </strong>and causes the <strong>maximum reduction </strong>in <strong>cardiac output</strong>, as it blunts the baroreceptor reflex and depresses myocardial contractility.</p>\n<p>Normally, hypotension inhibits baroreceptors in the aortic arch and carotid sinus, causing a decrease in vagal stimulation and a compensatory rise in heart rate.</p>\n<p>Halothane and enflurane produce a greater decrease in myocardial contractility than isoflurane, desflurane, and sevoflurane which preserve the baroreceptor reflexes and produce tachycardia in response to a fall in arterial pressure.</p>\n<p>Cardiac output is best preserved with isoflurane. Though it produces some myocardial depression, reflex tachycardia maintains cardiac output, <strong>isoflurane</strong> is the <strong>agent of choice</strong> in <strong>cardiac patients.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5775","text":"Which inhalational anesthe​tic agent causes the maxi​mum decline in hepatic bl‍ood flow?","raw_text":"Which inhalational anesthetic agent causes the maximum decline in hepatic blood flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Halothane</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Maximum decline</strong> in <strong>hepatic blood flow</strong> is caused by<strong> halothane.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">All volatile anesthetics decrease hepatic blood flow but halothane causes the maximum decrease. Hepatic artery vasoconstriction and increase in hepatic arterial resistance are seen with halothane.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Halothane also reduces hepatic oxygen delivery and hepatic venous oxygen saturation. This is because of the <strong>greater</strong> <strong>reduction in cardiac output</strong> caused by halothane than the other volatile agents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hepatic arterial buffer response(HABF) </strong>is an autoregulatory mechanism in the liver. When portal blood flow decreases due to decreased cardiac output or hypovolemia, the hepatic arterial blood flow increases in order to maintain total hepatic blood flow. Halothane <strong>disrupts </strong>this <strong>regulatory mechanism,</strong> which is best preserved with sevoflurane or isoflurane.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Desflurane and sevoflurane have the least significant effects on total hepatic blood flow and oxygen delivery. Sevoflurane is considered equal to or better than isoflurane in preserving hepatic arterial blood flow and hepatic oxygen delivery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0457","text":"A primigravida developed p​ostpartum hemorrhage 45 m​inutes after delivery. Sh‍e has a history of a myom‌ectomy done 2 years prior‍. The obstetrician suspec​ts placenta succenturiata‍ and requests the anesthe​tist to administer an inh‌aled drug for uterine rel‌axation. Which of the fol​lowing agents can be used​ in this scenario?","raw_text":"A primigravida developed postpartum hemorrhage 45 minutes after delivery. She has a history of a myomectomy done 2 years prior. The obstetrician suspects placenta succenturiata and requests the anesthetist to administer an inhaled drug for uterine relaxation. Which of the following agents can be used in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Et​her","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlor​oform","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Halothane</strong></p><p><strong>Uterine relaxation</strong> is <strong>maximum</strong> with <strong>halothane</strong>.<br/>Although halothane is not used in cases of PPH, it is the only anesthetic agent that can inhibit uterine contraction.<br/>Hence among the given inhaled drugs, Halothane is the appropriate answer for the given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>Halogenated anesthetics like halothane have potent <strong>relaxant</strong> <strong>effects</strong> on the uterus. It is utilized for facilitating <strong>intrauterine fetal manipulation.</strong></p>\n<p>However, its use during labor can prolong delivery and increase uterine bleeding.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Ether and chloroform do not have any significant uterine relaxant action.</p>\n<p>Option C: Nitrous oxide also has uterine relaxant properties but is less than that of halogenated inhalational anesthetics.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5864","text":"The second gas effect is s​een with which anesthetic​?","raw_text":"The second gas effect is seen with which anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclop​ropane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Et​her","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>The <strong>second gas effect</strong> is seen with <strong>nitrous oxide</strong>, during <strong>induction.</strong></p>\n<p>Second gas refers to oxygen and other volatile anesthetic agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5895","text":"What is the incidence of h​alothane hepatitis?","raw_text":"What is the incidence of halothane hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 in​ 200","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 in​ 350","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 in 2​0, 000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 in ​35,000","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 in 35,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 in 35,000</strong></p><p>The incidence of halothane hepatitis is <strong>1 in 35,000, </strong>but it is very fatal with a mortality of 50-75%.</p>\n<p>It is an immune-mediated reaction due to antibodies against highly reactive <strong>trifluoroacetyl chloride</strong> which is a metabolite of halothane.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors </strong>for halothane hepatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple exposures to halothane at short intervals</li>\n<li>Middle-aged obese women</li>\n<li>Familial predisposition to halothane</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The liver in halothane hepatitis shows <strong>centrilobular necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5908","text":"On administration of which​ of the following inhaled​ anesthetics can megalobl‍astic anemia occur as an ‌adverse effect?","raw_text":"On administration of which of the following inhaled anesthetics can megaloblastic anemia occur as an adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Nitrous oxide inhibits</strong> vitamin B12 dependent enzymes like <strong>thymidylate synthetase</strong>, which is necessary for DNA synthesis – this is responsible for the <strong>megaloblastic bone marrow changes.</strong></p>\n<p>Megaloblastic anemia is seen with nitrous oxide especially when <strong>exposure</strong> is <strong>greater</strong> than <strong>2-6 hours</strong>  (in healthy patients prolonged periods of exposure &gt; 12 hours is required whereas, in critically ill patients, much shorter periods of exposure can lead to megaloblastic changes in the bone marrow).</p>\n<p><strong>Side effects </strong>of nitrous oxide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone marrow depression</li>\n<li>Subacute combined degeneration of the cord</li>\n<li>Teratogenicity</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> of nitrous oxide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Venous or arterial air embolism</li>\n<li>Pneumothorax</li>\n<li>Acute intestinal obstruction with bowel distention</li>\n<li>Intracranial air (pneumocephalus following dural closure or pneumoencephalography)</li>\n<li>Pulmonary air cysts</li>\n<li>Intraocular air bubbles</li>\n<li>Tympanic membrane grafting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"eoXGasLz","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"14_cardiovascular__lymphatic_and_respiratory_systems","title":"14.Cardiovascular, Lymphatic And Respiratory Systems","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB2560","text":"Identify the structure sho‌wn in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Art‌ery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ven‌ule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capi‌llary","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arte‌riole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Capillary","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e7bab9444b844ae1a964c29e66414bac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9366aa98b6684454b08d041661d40902x1280x1018.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d27745cf86a424488ba8d4736b580c3x1280x2756.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Capillary</strong></p><p>Among the given options the given structure is of the <strong>capillary.</strong></p>\n<p>The wall of the capillary is formed mainly by <strong>endothelial cells</strong> that are lined on the outside by a basal lamina made of glycoprotein. There is <strong>no adventitia</strong> that differentiates it from a venule.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the microvessels - arteriole, venule, and capillary.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The capillaries can be classified into two types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Continuous capillaries - </strong>The edges of endothelial cells fuse completely with those of adjoining cells to form a continuous wall<strong>. </strong>The exchange of material between the blood and tissue occurs through the cytoplasm of the endothelial cells or through the intercellular material adjoining the cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Fenestrated capillaries - </strong>The walls of these capillaries have apertures in their endothelial lining which are closed by a thin diaphragm. The fenestration may also be present as pores, which passes through the entire thickness of the cell. The exchange of material occurs by diffusion through these fenestrae. They are mainly seen in <strong>renal glomeruli, intestinal villi, endocrine glands, and pancreas.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Sinusoids </strong>differ from the capillaries in that the wall sinusoid has only endothelium, which is supported by a thin layer of connective tissue. The endothelium has larger deficiencies in the form of fenestrations or long slits.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2555","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the arteries?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The fibrous elements prese‌nt in the tunica intima a‍nd media run circularly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The fibrous elements of th‌e tunica adventitia run l‍ongitudinally","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Collagen fibers are predom‌inantly present in the tu‍nica media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collagen fibers are predom‌inantly present in the tu‍nica intima","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The fibrous elements of the tunica adventitia run longitudinally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1362cf9979634af49d2fc9c581b2dfa1x1280x1060.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The fibrous elements of the tunica adventitia run longitudinally</strong></p><p>The fibrous elements in the <strong>intima</strong> and the <strong>adventitia</strong> (mainly collagen) run <strong>longitudinally</strong> (i.e., along the length of the vessel), whereas those in the <strong>media</strong> (elastic tissue or muscle) run <strong>circularly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>tunica adventitia</strong> consists of <strong>connective tissue</strong> in which <strong>collagen fibres</strong> are prominent. This layer prevents undue stretching or distension of the artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure of arterial wall:</strong></p>\n<p>The wall of an artery is made up of three layers, from inside out these include:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Tunica intima:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the innermost layer and consists of <strong>endothelium</strong>, which lies on the <strong>basal lamina.</strong></li>\n<li>Beneath the endothelium, there is a delicate layer of <strong>sub-endothelial connective tissue.</strong></li>\n<li>A membrane formed by elastic fibers surrounds the basal lamina. This membrane is called the <strong>internal elastic lamina.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Tunica media:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It consists of <strong>smooth</strong> <strong>muscle</strong> or <strong>elastic</strong> <strong>tissue</strong>, depending on which there are two types of arteries: <strong>muscular artery </strong>and<strong> elastic artery</strong> respectively.</li>\n<li>The smooth muscle or elastic tissue is surrounded by a membrane made up of elastic fibers called, <strong>external elastic lamina.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Tunica adventitia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the <strong>outermost layer</strong> and predominantly consists of connective tissue formed by <strong>collagen fibres.</strong></li>\n<li>This layer prevents the artery from being overstretched or excessively distended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2556","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ elastic arteries?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding elastic arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are also known as dis‌tributing arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The internal elastic lamin‌a is distinct from the tu‍nica media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Smooth muscles are absent ‌in the tunica media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The elastic recoil helps i‌n continuous flow of the ‍blood","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The elastic recoil helps in continuous flow of the blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de6166f77b7a4d539f50b392c03992ecx1280x794.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The elastic recoil helps in continuous flow of the blood</strong></p><p><strong>The elastic recoil</strong> of the elastic arteries helps in <strong>continuous blood flow.</strong></p>\n<p>The large elastic arteries <strong>distend and recoil</strong> during systole and diastole respectively due to <strong>abundant elastic tissue</strong>. The recoiling acts as an <strong>additional force</strong> that pushes blood into smaller arteries. So, <strong>blood flows continuously</strong> through arteries but with some fluctuation in pressure during systole and diastole.</p>\n<p>The main differences between the elastic and muscular arteries are shown in the table given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 409.26837158203125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"><strong>Structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\"><strong>Elastic artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.26837158203125px;\"><strong>Muscular artery</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"><strong>Tunica intima</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">The internal elastic lamina cannot be distinguished from the surrounding tunica media</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.26837158203125px;\">The internal elastic lamina is distinct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"><strong>Tunica media</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">\n<p>Predominantly of elastic fibers. <br/>Few smooth muscle cells.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.26837158203125px;\">Predominantly of smooth muscle cells.<br/>Very few elastic fibers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\"><strong>Tunica adventitia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Relatively thin and consists mainly of elastic fibers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.26837158203125px;\">Relatively thicker and consists of fibroblastic tissue.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Other options: </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>elastic arteries</strong> are also known as <strong>conducting vessels</strong>, as their main function is to conduct the blood from the heart, whereas the <strong>muscular arteries</strong> are known as <strong>distributing vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>internal elastic lamina</strong> in the elastic arteries is <strong>not distinct </strong>and cannot be differentiated from the <strong>tunica media</strong> as the structure of the internal elastic lamina is same as that of the elastic fibers present in tunica media of elastic arteries.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>tunica media</strong> of the elastic arteries is predominantly made up of <strong>elastic</strong> <strong>tissue</strong>. Between the elastic membranes, there is some <strong>loose connective tissue</strong>. <strong>Some smooth muscle cells</strong> may be present.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2562","text":"Match the following marked‌ structures to the correc‍t areas of the lymph node‍. A. Germinal centre B. L‍ymphoid nodule C. Trabecu​lae D. Subcapsular sinus ​E. Capsule","raw_text":"<p>Match the following marked structures to the correct areas of the lymph node.</p>\n<p>       A. Germinal centre</p>\n<p>       B. Lymphoid nodule</p>\n<p>       C. Trabeculae</p>\n<p>       D. Subcapsular sinus</p>\n<p>       E. Capsule</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - E, 2 - A, 3 - D, 4 - C‌, 5 - B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - A, 2 - D, 3 - B, 4 - C‌, 5 - E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - E, 2 - D, 3 - A, 4 - B‌, 5 - C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 - A, 2 - E, 3 - B, 4 - D‌, 5 - C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - E, 2 - D, 3 - A, 4 - B, 5 - C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d381a5f531c04f26a0693955050c304e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df59617c0bd54fbc957e4d965895d5d3x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - E, 2 - D, 3 - A, 4 - B, 5 - C</strong></p><p>The structures marked in the above <strong>histological section of the lymph node</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<p>1 - Capsule</p>\n<p>2 - Subcapsular sinus</p>\n<p>3 - Germinal centre</p>\n<p>4 - Lymphoid nodule</p>\n<p>5 - Trabeculae</p>\n<p>Each lymph node consists of a <strong>connective tissue framework</strong> and of numerous lymphocytes of all types which are arranged in a stroma of reticulin fibers and cells. They form <strong>3 major regions</strong> within each lymph node:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cortex</strong> - It contains subcapsular sinuses, lymphoid nodules, and densely packed lymphocytes. It <strong>stains darkly. </strong>Each nodule has a lightly stained <strong>germinal center</strong> surrounded by a zone of densely packed lymphocytes. </li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\"><strong>P</strong><strong>aracortex</strong></span> - It lacks nodules and has fewer lymphocytes. It is, therefore, <strong>lightly stained.</strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\"><strong>M</strong><strong>edulla -</strong></span>  with prominent draining sinusoids adjacent to the hilum.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Given below is a histological section of a lymph node showing the cortex, paracortex, and medulla.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2563","text":"The arterioles present in ‌the spleen divides into a‍ number of straight vesse‍ls that are called _____","raw_text":"The arterioles present in the spleen divides into a number of straight vessels that are called _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stave‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elli‌psoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peni‌cilli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amp‌ulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Penicilli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/214eb18ca60d49f7986e3dad897b92ecx1280x1338.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Penicilli</strong></p><p>The<strong> arterioles </strong>present in the spleen divide into a number of straight vessels that are called <strong>penicilli.</strong><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>splenic artery</strong> after entering the hilum of the spleen divides into about five branches that enter the organ independently. Each branch divides and subdivides as it travels through the trabecular network. <strong>Arterioles</strong> arising from this network leave the trabeculae to pass into the <strong>inter-trabecular spaces</strong>.</p>\n<p>The spleen is histologically composed of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>White pulp </strong>-<strong> </strong>For some distance, each arteriole is surrounded by a dense sheath of lymphocytes. These lymphocytes constitute the white pulp of the spleen.</li>\n<li><strong>Penicilli </strong>-<strong> </strong>The arteriole then divides into a number of straight vessels that are called penicilli.</li>\n<li><strong>Ellipsoid </strong>-<strong> </strong>Each of the penicilli shows a localised thickening of its wall that is called an ellipsoid. The ellipsoid consists of <strong>concentric lamellae</strong> formed by aggregation of fibroblasts and macrophages.</li>\n<li><strong>Ampulla </strong>-<strong> </strong>Distal to the ellipsoid, the vessel dilates to form an ampulla, the walls of which become continuous with the <strong>reticular framework.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Red pulp </strong>-<strong> </strong>As a result, blood flows into spaces lined by reticular cells, coming into direct contact with lymphocytes there. The part of splenic tissue, which is infiltrated with blood in this way is called the red pulp.</li>\n<li><strong>Sinusoids </strong>-<strong> </strong>Blood from spaces of the red pulp is collected by wide sinusoids that drain into veins in the trabeculae.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stave cells: </strong>The sinusoids of the spleen are lined by a somewhat modified endothelium. The endothelial cells here are elongated and are shaped like bananas. They are referred to as stave cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2564","text":"A mislabeled sample of tis‌sue was sent over to the ‍pathology lab for histopa‍thological examination. O‍n microscopy, a character​istic corpuscle as seen i​n the image was visualise‌d. Which of the following‍ structures is the sample‌ most likely to be from?","raw_text":"A mislabeled sample of tissue was sent over to the pathology lab for histopathological examination. On microscopy, a characteristic corpuscle as seen in the image was visualised. Which of the following structures is the sample most likely to be from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palatin‌e tonsil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thy‌mus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bone ‌marrow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thymus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec7f2070d544448a8a520209e9ba4e25.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a364b2bf8b8e42cca0a2aa68ad584cbex1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thymus</strong></p><p>The sample is most likely to be of the <strong>thymus</strong> because of the presence of the<strong> corpuscle of Hassal </strong>as seen in the image above.</p>\n<p>These are small rounded structures formed from <strong>eosinophilic type VI epithelial reticular cells </strong>present in the medulla of the thymus. Each corpuscle has a central core formed by degenerated epithelial cells appearing as pink stained hyaline mass. Around this mass, there is a wall formed by concentrically arranged epithelial cells which stain bright pink in H and E staining.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2566","text":"Given below is an image of‌ a section of the small i‍ntestine. Identify the st‍ructure marked X","raw_text":"Given below is an image of a section of the small intestine. Identify the structure marked X","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌mus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MA‌LT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph‌ node","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MALT","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1145971905b54d5e919b47272311b34d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/63f3c1856f444e869442a0c503065700x1280x1386.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MALT</strong></p><p>The structure marked X in the above given image of a section of <strong>ileum</strong> is the <strong>MALT (Mucosal associated lymphoid tissue),</strong> also known as gut-associated lymphoid tissue. These are seen as aggregated lymphatic follicle or <strong>Peyer's patch</strong> present in the submucosa of the ileum. </p>\n<p>The mucosal associated lymphoid tissues are present in other regions as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tonsils</strong> - near the junction of the oral cavity with the pharynx, as collections of lymphoid tissue. The largest of these are the right and left palatine tonsils, present on either side of the oropharyngeal isthmus.</li>\n<li><strong>Solitary lymphatic follicles</strong> -<strong> </strong>small collections of lymphoid tissue, similar in structure to the follicles of lymph nodes, present anywhere along the length of the gut.</li>\n<li><strong>Aggregated lymphatic follicles (Peyer’s patches) </strong>-<strong> </strong>larger aggregations of lymphoid tissue, each consisting of 10 to 200 follicles present in the small intestine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below of an ileal section shows Peyer's patches within the submucosa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2567","text":"A researcher studying the ‌COVID-19 virus recently d‍iscovered that anosmia is‍ caused by the temporary ‍loss of function of the s​ustentacular cells of the​ olfactory epithelium. Wh‌ich of the following accu‍rately describes these ce‌lls?","raw_text":"A researcher studying the COVID-19 virus recently discovered that anosmia is caused by the temporary loss of function of the sustentacular cells of the olfactory epithelium. Which of the following accurately describes these cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olfacto‌ry cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supporti‌ng cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Recepto‌r cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Supporting cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2e630c977874a678d488e56cb3c083cx1280x1270.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Supporting cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>sustentacular cells</strong> are supporting cells of the olfactory epithelium.</p>\n<p>Their <strong>nuclei</strong> are oval and lie near the <strong>free surface of the epithelium</strong>. The free surface of each cell bears numerous <strong>microvilli</strong> (embedded in overlying mucous). The cytoplasm contains a yellow pigment (<strong>lipofuscin</strong>) that gives olfactory mucosa its yellow colour. In addition to their<strong> supporting function</strong>, sustentacular cells may be <strong>phagocytic</strong>, and the pigment in them may represent remnants of <strong>phagocytosed olfactory cells</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2574","text":"Identify the structure sho‌wn in the histological im‍age below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure shown in the histological image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tra‌chea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bron‌chus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronc‌hiole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alve‌olus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trachea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a79973541ce54be7b8c99ee4b0bcd9e2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2d94b2f9545421683963ee7de0f093cx1280x1374.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trachea</strong></p><p>The above histological image is that of the <strong>trachea</strong> as identified by the presence of <strong>pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium</strong> lining, the <strong>serous</strong> and <strong>mucous glands</strong> and <strong>hyaline cartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The histological section of the trachea shows the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory epithelium</li>\n<li>Basement membrane</li>\n<li>Submucosal glands (both serous and mucous parts)</li>\n<li>Perichondrium</li>\n<li>Tracheal cartilage</li>\n<li>Trachealis muscle (smooth muscle).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> bronchi</strong> differ from the trachea in having <strong>plates</strong> rather than rings of <strong>cartilage,</strong> and in having a layer of <strong>smooth muscle</strong> between the<strong> lamina propria and submucosa</strong>. In smaller branches, the amount of cartilage decreases, whereas the amount of smooth muscles increases. Also, the number of glands and goblet cells decreases.</p>\n<p>The image below is a histological section of the bronchus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2569","text":"Given below is a histologi‌cal section of the lung. ‍Identify the structure ma‍rked X.","raw_text":"Given below is a histological section of the lung. Identify the structure marked X.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alve‌olus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alveol‌ar duct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory‌ bronchiole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terminal ‌bronchiole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alveolar duct","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afa0e10b50f94aee927a1df42cc4aa66.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1bd07ec34f4407b83cb6ad543c8f4cex1280x1371.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70456d647c524177a20ef42e242fa6bex1280x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alveolar duct</strong></p><p>The structure marked X in the above image is the lumen of the <strong>alveolar duct, </strong>which are <strong>tiny ducts</strong> that connect the <strong>respiratory bronchioles</strong> to <strong>alveolar sacs</strong>, each of which contains a collection of alveoli (small mucus-lined pouches made of flattened epithelial cells).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The coloured strips in the above image represent the location of different cells/structures in the trachea and bronchial tree.</p>\n<p>On entering the lung, the <strong>principal bronchus</strong> divides into <strong>secondary,</strong> or<strong> lobar bronchi</strong>. Each lobar bronchus divides into tertiary, or <strong>segmental bronchi</strong> which further divides into the <strong>lobular bronchioles</strong> and then into <strong>terminal bronchioles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Terminal bronchioles represent the most distal parts of the conducting passage. Each terminal bronchiole ends by dividing into <strong>respiratory bronchioles. </strong>Each respiratory bronchiole ends by dividing into a few <strong>alveolar ducts</strong>. Each alveolar duct ends in a passage, the atrium, which leads into a number of rounded <strong>alveolar sacs</strong>. Each alveolar sac is studded with a number of air sacs or <strong>alveoli</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2572","text":"The rounded secretory cell‌s bearing microvilli pres‍ent in the epithelial lin‍ing of the alveoli are __‍___","raw_text":"The rounded secretory cells bearing microvilli present in the epithelial lining of the alveoli are _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I pn‌eumocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II p‌neumocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alveola‌r cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brush‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II pneumocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec646d2dcbdd42868a1cd47fa5931c20x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II pneumocytes</strong></p><p>The <strong>secretory cells</strong> bearing microvilli present in the epithelial lining of the alveoli are the <strong>type II pneumocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>cells</strong> forming the <strong>lining epithelium</strong> of alveoli (also called <strong>pneumocytes</strong>) are mainly of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I pneumocytes </strong>- large <strong>flattened</strong> cells which present a very thin diffusion barrier for gases. They are connected to each other by tight junctions.</li>\n<li><strong>Type II pneumocytes </strong>- These are <strong>rounded secretory cells</strong> bearing microvilli on their free surfaces. These cells secrete the <strong>surfactant</strong> which decreases the surface tension between the thin alveolar walls, and thus prevents alveoli from collapsing during respiration.</li>\n<li><strong>Type III alveolar cells (</strong><strong>brush</strong> <strong>cells) </strong>- function is not clearly known.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qwPJGuFn","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"14_biosynthesis_of_fatty_acids_and_eicosanoids","title":"14.Biosynthesis Of Fatty Acids And Eicosanoids","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA9930","text":"Which of the following bio​chemical reactions does n​ot occur in mitochondria?​","raw_text":"Which of the following biochemical reactions does not occur in mitochondria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kreb ​cycle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urea ​cycle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucone​ogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fatty acid​ synthesis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fatty acid synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fatty acid synthesis</strong></p><p><strong>Fatty acid synthesis</strong> does <strong>not occur</strong> in <strong>mitochondria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fatty acids are synthesized in the <strong>cytosol </strong>by a process called <strong>de novo </strong>fatty acid synthesis. All the steps, from acetyl-CoA to palmitate, are done by an <strong>extramitochondrial system</strong>. Fatty acid synthesis takes place in the liver, kidneys, brain, lungs, adipose tissue and lactating mammary gland. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9931","text":"Which of the following pat​hways utilizes NADPH as t​he source of reducing equ​ivalent? (NADPH - nicotin​amide adenine dinucleotid​e phosphate)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following pathways utilizes NADPH as the source of reducing equivalent?</p>\n<p>(NADPH - nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fatty acid​ synthesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ketone s​ynthesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucone​ogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glyco​lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fatty acid synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fatty acid synthesis</strong></p><p><strong>NADPH </strong>is the source of reducing equivalent<strong> </strong>for <strong>fatty acid synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p> The NADPH utilised for fatty acid synthesis is derived from 3 sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HMP shunt pathway (<strong>major source</strong>)</li>\n<li>Extramitochondrial isocitrate dehydrogenase (minor source)</li>\n<li>Malic enzyme</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9932","text":"Which of the following pat​hway is the most importan​t source of reducing equi​valents for fatty acid sy​nthesis in the liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following pathway is the most important source of reducing equivalents for fatty acid synthesis in the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glyco​lysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricarboxyli​c acid cycle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uronic ac​id pathway","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hexose monophosphate shunt​ pathway","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hexose monophosphate shunt pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hexose monophosphate shunt pathway</strong></p><p>The reducing equivalent for fatty acid synthesis in the liver is <strong>NADPH</strong>. The <strong>most important</strong> source of <strong>NADPH</strong> is the <strong>hexose monophosphate shunt </strong>(HMP)<strong> pathway.</strong></p>\n<p>The HMP shunt pathway, or <strong>pentose phosphate pathway</strong>, is an alternative route for the metabolism of glucose. It generates <strong>NADPH</strong> and synthesizes<strong> ribose</strong>. NADP+ is hydrogenated to NADPH by <strong>glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase</strong>. Since the HMP shunt pathway and de novo fatty acid synthesis both occur in the <strong>cytosol</strong> of the cell, there are no membranes or permeability barriers against the transfer of NADPH. Thus, this forms the major source for NADPH for fatty acid synthesis. </p>\n<p>Other minor sources of NADPH are extramitochondrial isocitrate dehydrogenase and NADP malate dehydrogenase (malic enzyme). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9933","text":"Which of the following is ​the rate-limiting enzyme ​of the fatty acid synthes​is pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the rate-limiting enzyme of the fatty acid synthesis pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl CoA ​carboxylase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta-hydroxy CoA dehydroge​nase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acyl CoA de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ketoacyl​ synthase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acetyl CoA carboxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56863fd1550c41c69c1fc306e682cf41x1280x1556.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong></p><p><strong> Acetyl CoA carboxylase </strong>is the <strong>rate-limiting enzyme </strong>of<strong> </strong>the<strong> fatty acid synthesis</strong> pathway.</p>\n<p>Acetyl CoA carboxylase converts <strong>acetyl CoA</strong> into <strong>malonyl</strong> CoA, the first step of de novo fatty acid synthesis. It is an allosteric enzyme activated by <strong>citrate </strong>in the well-fed state. Acetyl CoA carboxylase requires the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NADPH </strong>as the reducing equivalent<strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Biotin</strong> is a coenzyme needed by carboxylases</li>\n<li><strong>ATP </strong>for energy</li>\n<li><strong> Manganese</strong> (Mn<sup>2+)</sup><strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li><strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> </strong>as the carbon donor </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9934","text":"Which of the following is ​an allosteric activator o​f acetyl CoA carboxylase?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is an allosteric activator of acetyl CoA carboxylase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cit​rate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ace​tate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mal​ate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palm​itate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Citrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93d912fcaaa4402498c5e896fe0bcef5x600x392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Citrate</strong></p><p><strong>Citrate</strong> is an <strong>allosteric</strong> <strong>activator </strong>of <strong>acetyl-CoA carboxylase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acetyl-CoA carboxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme in de novo fatty acid synthesis. Citrate promotes the conversion of acetyl CoA carboxylase from an<strong> inactive dimer</strong> to an <strong>active polymeric form</strong>. Its concentration increases in the<strong> well-fed state</strong>. <strong>Insulin</strong> also activates the enzyme by dephosphorylating it.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Glucagon</strong> and <strong>epinephrine</strong> promote <strong>inactivation</strong> of acetyl-CoA-carboxylase by <strong>phosphorylation</strong> of the enzyme. <strong>Long-chain acyl-CoA molecules</strong> such as palmitoyl CoA also promotes inactivation of the enzyme.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; height: 327px;\" width=\"326\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 383.476px;\">Examples of allosteric modifiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">Enzyme</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">Allosteric inhibitor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">Allosteric activator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">1. ALA synthase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">Heme</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">2. Aspartate transcarbamoylase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">CTP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">ATP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">3. HMG CoA reductase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">Cholesterol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">4.Phosphofructokinase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">Citrate, ATP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">AMP, F2,6P</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">5. Acetyl CoA carboxylase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">Acyl CoA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">Citrate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">6. Citrate synthase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">ATP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">7. Carbamoyl phosphate synthase-I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">NAG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.871px;\">8. Carbamoyl phosphate synthase-II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.6289px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80.9766px;\">ATP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9936","text":"Which is an important inte​rmediate product of fatty​ acid biosynthesis ?","raw_text":"Which is an important intermediate product of fatty acid biosynthesis ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chole​sterol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malon​yl CoA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acety​l CoA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isovale​ryl CoA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malonyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9f6f1e8d90c42f1af02eb0394526ff5x1280x1556.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malonyl CoA</strong></p><p><strong>Malonyl-CoA</strong> is an important <strong>intermediate</strong> <strong>product</strong> of <strong>fatty acid biosynthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Production of <strong>malonyl-CoA </strong>from<strong> acetyl CoA</strong> is the initial and <strong>rate-limiting step</strong> in fatty acid synthesis. <strong>Bicarbonate</strong> is a source of CO<sub>2 </sub>and is required in the initial reaction for the carboxylation of acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA in the presence of <strong>ATP </strong>and <strong>acetyl-CoA carboxylase</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9938","text":"A child with severe acute ​malnutrition is undergoin​g nutritional rehabilitat​ion. During follow-up vis​its, he shows significant​ improvement in appetite,​ an adequate gain of weig‌ht, and a gradual increas‌e in subcutaneous body fa​t. Activity of which of t​he following enzymes is r​esponsible for the restor‌ation of fat storage in t‍his patient?","raw_text":"A child with severe acute malnutrition is undergoing nutritional rehabilitation. During follow-up visits, he shows significant improvement in appetite, an adequate gain of weight, and a gradual increase in subcutaneous body fat. Activity of which of the following enzymes is responsible for the restoration of fat storage in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl CoA ​carboxylase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acyl-CoA de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thio​lase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carnitine palmitoyl transf​erase - I","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acetyl CoA carboxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong></p><p>The <strong>restoration </strong>of<strong> fat storage</strong> in a child undergoing nutritional rehabilitation, as evidenced by adequate weight gain and an increase in subcutaneous body fat, occurs with the help of <strong>acetyl CoA carboxylase.</strong></p>\n<p>With appropriate supplementation of nutrition, the child's body will gradually enter an <strong>anabolic phase. </strong>In the well fed state, there is an abundant supply of glucose, which is converted to pyruvate, which in turn is converted to acetyl CoA. <strong>Acetyl CoA</strong> is diverted into <strong>fatty acid synthesis</strong>, in which acetyl CoA carboxylase is the<strong> rate-limiting enzyme. </strong>There is also a high insulin : glucagon ratio in the well fed state. This favors dephosphorylation (activation) of acetyl CoA carboxylase, resulting in increased fatty acid synthesis. The fatty acids thus formed (most commonly palmitoyl CoA) are esterified into<strong> triacylglycerols</strong>. This process is called <strong>lipogenesis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The enzymes acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, thiolase, and carnitine palmitoyl transferase-I are involved in fatty acid oxidation and are inactive in the well-fed state. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3637","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common end-produ​ct of fatty acid synthesi​s pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common end-product of fatty acid synthesis pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arachido​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oleic​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palmit​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acety​l CoA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Palmitic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Palmitic acid</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common </strong>end product of fatty acid synthesis is <strong>palmitic acid.</strong></p>\n<p>Palmitic acid is a 16 carbon <strong>saturated</strong> long chain fatty acid (LCFA). The substrate for its synthesis is <strong>acetyl CoA</strong>. Acetyl CoA is also the end product of fatty acid beta-oxidation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9941","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about the mechani​sm of synthesis of fatty ​acids is correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about the mechanism of synthesis of fatty acids is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl-CoA is the most act​ive donor of two carbon a​toms in fatty acid synthe​sis.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malonyl-CoA is the most ac​tive donor of two carbon ​atoms in fatty acid synth​esis.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fatty acid synthesis is th​e reverse of oxidation of​ fatty acids.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coenzyme A is the acyl gro​up carrier of intermediat​es in fatty acid synthesi​s.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malonyl-CoA is the most active donor of two carbon atoms in fatty acid synthesis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malonyl-CoA is the most active donor of two carbon atoms in fatty acid synthesis.</strong></p><p><strong>Malonyl-CoA</strong> is the most <strong>active</strong> <strong>donor </strong>of<strong> two carbon atoms</strong> in fatty acid synthesis.</p>\n<p>Malonyl-CoA is a <strong>3-carbon unit</strong> that donates 2 carbon units in fatty acid synthesis. It is formed from acetyl-CoA by <strong>acetyl-CoA carboxylase</strong>. During fatty acid synthesis, acetyl CoA primarily acts as the <strong>primer. </strong>The enzyme <strong>fatty acid synthase</strong> (FAS) adds two carbons from malonyl-CoA to the inital acetyl-CoA, forming a 4-carbon intermediate (butyryl-ACP). FAS then sequentially adds two carbons from malonyl-CoA to the ACP-intermediates (eg: butyryl-ACP) to elongate the fatty acid chain. </p>\n<p>Thus, in the end product<strong> palmitic acid </strong>(C16), only carbon atoms 15 and 16 are from <strong>acetyl CoA</strong>. The remaining 14 carbon atoms are derived from malonyl-CoA. Thus, malonyl-CoA is the most active donor of two carbon atoms in fatty acid synthesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: <br/></strong></p>\n<p>Option 3: Fatty acid synthesis is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>simple reversal</strong> of oxidation of fatty acids. These two processes involve completely different enzymes and occur in different compartments of the cell. </p>\n<p>Option 4: Intermediates in<strong> fatty acid synthesis</strong> are covalently linked to the sulfhydryl group of an <strong>acyl carrier protein </strong>(ACP), not coenzyme A,<strong> </strong>of the enzyme fatty acid synthase. Intermediates in the <strong>fatty acid breakdown</strong> are covalently attached to the sulfhydryl group of <strong>coenzyme A</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9943","text":"In which of the following ​steps of fatty acid synth​esis is CO2 produced?","raw_text":"In which of the following steps of fatty acid synthesis is CO2 produced?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activ​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conden​sation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Elong​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Redu​ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Condensation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Condensation</strong></p><p>In fatty acid synthesis, <strong>CO<sub>2</sub></strong> is produced in the <strong>condensation</strong> step.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9945","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the fatty a​cid synthase complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the fatty acid synthase complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enoyl r​eductase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ketoacyl​ synthase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acetyl CoA ​carboxylase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acetyl tr​ansacylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetyl CoA carboxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong></p><p><strong>Acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong> is not a part of the<strong> fatty acid synthase</strong> complex<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The fatty acid synthase complex is a multienzyme polypeptide complex that synthesizes fatty acids from malonyl-CoA and acetyl-CoA. It is a dimer of two identical polypeptide monomers (homodimer). Each monomer contains<strong> six enzymes</strong> and the <strong>acyl carrier protein</strong> (ACP). ACP carries the intermediate products formed during fatty acid synthesis. The enzymes of the fatty acid synthase complex are as follows: </p>\n<p>1. Condensing unit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ketoacyl synthase</strong></li>\n<li>Malonyl/<strong>acetyl transacylase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Reduction unit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dehydratase</li>\n<li><strong>Enoyl reductase</strong></li>\n<li>Beta-ketoacyl reductase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. Releasing unit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thioesterase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9954","text":"The elongation of long cha​in fatty acids takes plac​e in which if the followi​ng hepatocyte organelles?​ Endoplasmic reticulum Pe​roxisome Mitochondria Lys​osome Golgi body","raw_text":"<p>The elongation of long chain fatty acids takes place in which  if the following hepatocyte organelles?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Endoplasmic reticulum</li>\n<li>Peroxisome</li>\n<li>Mitochondria</li>\n<li>Lysosome</li>\n<li>Golgi body</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​,2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3​,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,​3,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​,3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3</strong></p><p>The <strong>elongation</strong> of long chain fatty acids takes place in the <strong>1. endoplasmic reticulum </strong>and <strong>3. mitochondria </strong>of hepatocytes.</p>\n<p><strong>Fatty acid elongation</strong> occurs in three cellular compartments: the<strong> cytosol, mitochondria, </strong>and <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> (microsomes). Both unsaturated and saturated fatty acids can be elongated, with malonyl-CoA acting as the 2 carbon donor and NADPH as the reducing agent. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9960","text":"Stearic acid is converted ​into which of the followi​ng fatty acids by the enz​yme △9 desaturase?","raw_text":"Stearic acid is converted into which of the following fatty acids by the enzyme △9  desaturase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linole​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oleic​ acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Linolen​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arachido​nic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oleic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oleic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Stearic acid</strong> is converted to<strong> oleic acid</strong> by the enzyme <strong>△<sup>9</sup> desaturase.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>△<sup>9</sup> desaturase</strong> is present in the endoplasmic reticulum. It catalyzes the synthesis of <strong>monounsaturated fatty acids </strong>(Eg: Oleic acid) from <strong>saturated fatty acids</strong> (Eg: Stearic acid). <strong>△</strong><sup>9</sup> desaturase is a mixed-function oxidase, requiring<strong> NADPH </strong>and<strong> O<sub>2. </sub></strong>The <strong>first double bond</strong> is always introduced in the <strong>△<sup>9</sup> positions</strong>. Humans <strong>cannot</strong> introduce double bonds beyond<strong> △<sup>9</sup> positions</strong> (between C-10 and terminal methyl group). Hence, <strong>linoleic </strong>(Δ<sup>9,12</sup>) and<strong> linolenic acid </strong>(Δ<sup>9,12,15</sup>) are considered <strong>essential fatty acids </strong>and should be included in the diet.<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9955","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the role ​of insulin on lipid metab​olism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the role of insulin on lipid metabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increasing acetyl CoA carb​oxylase activity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increasing the transport o​f glucose into the cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of pyruvate deh​ydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreasing intracellular c​AMP level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibition of pyruvate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibition of pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p>Inhibition of pyruvate dehydrogenase is<strong> not</strong> an action of <strong>insulin</strong>. Insulin<strong> activates</strong> the<strong> pyruvate dehydrogenase </strong>enzyme.</p>\n<p>Insulin<strong> stimulates lipogenesis</strong> by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decreasing</strong> the level of<strong> intracellular cAMP. </strong>This favours <strong>dephosphorylation </strong>of acetyl-CoA carboxylase, resulting in its activation. </li>\n<li><strong>Increasing acetyl-CoA carboxylase</strong> activity. This accelerates the rate-limiting step of fatty acid synthesis.</li>\n<li><strong>Increasing</strong> the <strong>transport </strong>of <strong>glucose</strong> into the cell and <strong>activation</strong> of <strong>pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong> in the adipose tissue. This yields more acetyl CoA for fatty acid synthesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9957","text":"Eicosanoids are derived fr​om fatty acids with _____​___","raw_text":"Eicosanoids are derived from fatty acids with ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​9C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​7C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​0C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1​5C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20C</strong></p><p><strong>Eicosanoids </strong>are derived from fatty acids with <strong>20C</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Eicosanoids are formed from <strong>arachidonic acid </strong>and other C20 <strong>polyunsaturated fatty acids</strong> (PUFA). They are short-acting paracrine chemicals that bring about their action through a G-protein-coupled receptor.</p>\n<p>Types of eicosanoids:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prostanoids\n<ul>\n<li>Prostaglandins (E &amp; F)</li>\n<li>Prostacyclins</li>\n<li>Thromboxanes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Leukotrienes</li>\n<li>Lipoxins </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0129","text":"What is the first step in ​synthesis of eicosanoids?​","raw_text":"What is the first step in synthesis of eicosanoids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of PGH2 synthet​ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Activation of lipooxygenas​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activation ​of hydrolase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Activation of cyclooxygena​se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Activation of hydrolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/364d110205114669a30e8479d7e4625ax1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Activation of hydrolase</strong></p><p>The <strong>first step </strong>in the<strong> synthesis</strong> of <strong>eicosanoids</strong> involves the <strong>activation </strong>of <strong>hydrolase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Eicosanoid synthesis is governed by the rate of release of <strong>arachidonic acid</strong> from membrane lipids in response to appropriate stimuli (Eg: Increased calcium). These stimuli activate <strong>hydrolases</strong>, including <strong>phospholipase A<sub>2</sub></strong>, which releases arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids. The released arachidonic acid is then converted into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prostaglandins and thromboxanes by the <strong>cyclooxygenase</strong> (COX) pathway. <strong>PGH2 synthase</strong> is the first enzyme of this pathway. </li>\n<li>Leukotrienes and lipoxins by the <strong>lipooxygenase </strong>(LOX) pathway</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0131","text":"Which substance is least l​ikely to have its product​ion decreased by aspirin?​","raw_text":"Which substance is least likely to have its production decreased by aspirin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostag​landins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prosta​cyclin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Throm​boxane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leukot​rienes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Leukotrienes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81863eda17cd49f38f5a14937638356ex1280x2588.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Leukotrienes</strong></p><p><strong>Aspirin</strong> is<strong> least likely</strong> to decrease the production of <strong>leukotrienes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aspirin is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatary drug (NSAID) that <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>cyclooxygenase</strong> (COX) pathway. The cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway generates eicosanoids with a <strong>ring structure </strong>- prostaglandins, thromboxane, prostacyclin. Leukotrienes, however, are produced by the <strong>lipoxygenase pathway </strong>(LOX), which is not inhibited by aspirin. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0138","text":"Which of the following are​ unstable intermediate pr​oducts of the cyclooxygen​ase pathway? PGG 2 PGH 2 ​PGD 2 PGF 2α PGE 2","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are unstable intermediate products of the cyclooxygenase pathway?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>PGG<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>PGH<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>PGD<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>PGF<sub>2α</sub></li>\n<li>PGE<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​,2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3,​4,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,​3,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1,2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1,2</strong></p><p><strong>PGG<sub>2</sub></strong> and<strong> PGH<sub>2</sub></strong> are <strong>unstable intermediates</strong> of the <strong>cyclooxygenase</strong> (COX) pathway.</p>\n<p>The cyclooxygenase enzyme of the COX pathway is a <strong>bifunctional enzyme.</strong> It first <strong>oxidizes arachidonic acid </strong>to<strong> PGG<sub>2</sub></strong> and then <strong>peroxidizes</strong> PGG<sub>2</sub> to <strong>PGH<sub>2.</sub> </strong>These two intermediates (cyclic endoperoxides) are unstable.<strong> </strong>PGH2 is metabolized further by specific synthases and isomerases into a variety of eicosanoids including PGE<sub>2</sub>, PGD<sub>2</sub>, PGF<sub>2α</sub>, prostacyclin (PGI<sub>2</sub>), and thromboxane (TX) A<sub>2. </sub></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0142","text":"Which of the following is ​not a primary prostagland​in?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a primary prostaglandin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PG​D2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PG​I2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"PG​E2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PG​F2α","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PGI2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca87c1e53e4e4d53ab7174aa6134b45ax1280x2080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PGI2</strong></p><p><strong>PGI<sub>2</sub></strong> is <strong>not </strong>a <strong>primary prostaglandin. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>PGD2, PGE<sub>2, </sub></strong>and <strong>PGF<sub>2α</sub></strong> are called the<strong> </strong>primary prostaglandins based on their structure. Primary prostaglandins contain a single <strong>cyclopentane ring </strong>with a 15-hydroxyl group<strong> </strong>with a<strong> </strong>13,14-trans double bond. <strong>Prostacyclin</strong>, or PGI2, structurally differs from the primary prostaglandins due to its <strong>double ring</strong> structure. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0144","text":"Which organ pair can synth​esize the whole range of ​COX products?","raw_text":"Which organ pair can synthesize the whole range of COX products?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung &amp;am​p; spleen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spleen &amp;a​mp; liver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung &amp;am​p; liver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood vessel​ &amp;amp; spleen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung & spleen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung &amp; spleen</strong></p><p><strong>Lung</strong> and <strong>spleen</strong> can synthesize the <strong>whole range</strong> of<strong> COX </strong>products.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 345.78125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">COX pathway products</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.78125px;\">Site of synthesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Whole range of COX products</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.78125px;\">Lung &amp; Spleen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Thromboxane</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.78125px;\">Primarily platelets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Prostacyclin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.78125px;\">Endothelium of blood vessels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0147","text":"Which enzyme is required i​n the synthesis of PGE2 f​rom cyclic endoperoxides?​","raw_text":"Which enzyme is required in the synthesis of PGE2 from cyclic endoperoxides?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isome​rases","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cycloox​ygenase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydr​olase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Redu​ctase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Isomerases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a9561fcdcad45d3bd997fdc5acdd49bx1280x1309.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Isomerases</strong></p><p><strong>Isomerases</strong> are required in the synthesis of<strong> PGE2</strong> from<strong> cyclic endoperoxides</strong> i.e., PGG<sub>2 </sub>and PGH<sub>2 </sub></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>COX pathway products</td>\n<td>Enzymes for synthesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PGI<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td>Prostacyclin synthase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PGD<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td>Isomerase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PGE<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td>Isomerase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PGF<sub>2α</sub></td>\n<td>Reductase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TXA<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td>Thromboxane synthase</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0163","text":"A 7-year-old girl presents​ with sudden onset of sev​ere breathlessness with w​heezing. Her peak expirat​ory flow rate is reduced ​significantly. Which of t​he following enzymes is i‌mportant for the pathogen‌esis of the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl presents with sudden onset of severe breathlessness with wheezing. Her peak expiratory flow rate is reduced significantly. Which of the following enzymes is important for the pathogenesis of the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7-Lipox​ygenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12-Lipo​xygenase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15-Lipo​xygenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5-Lipox​ygenase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5-Lipoxygenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c4613d9ee8b4913a9a571ac5929e453x1280x2588.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5-Lipoxygenase</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Sudden onset of severe breathlessness and wheezing with a reduced peak expiratory flow rate is suggestive of <strong>acute exacerbation </strong>of <strong>asthma.</strong> <strong>5-lipoxygenase</strong> enzyme is important for the pathogenesis of this condition. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Acute exacerbation of asthma is characterized by <strong>bronchoconstriction</strong> due to chemicals mediators like <strong>leukotrienes</strong> (LTB<sub>4</sub>, LTC<sub>4</sub>, LTD<sub>4</sub> etc.). Leukotrienes are produced from the breakdown of <strong>arachidonic acid</strong> by 5-lipoxygenase<strong> </strong>via the<strong> lipoxygenase pathway. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Leukotrienes<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>are <strong>eicosanoic acid </strong>derivatives formed in leukocytes, mast cells, platelets, and macrophages. They are<strong> potent constrictors</strong> of the<strong> bronchial airway. </strong>Leukotrienes<span class=\"s1\"> </span>also cause increased <strong>vascular permeability</strong> and activation and<strong> attraction </strong>of <strong>leukocytes. </strong>Thus, they are involved in many inflammatory or <strong>immediate hypersensitivity reactions,</strong> such as asthma. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0167","text":"What is the first intermed​iate product of the LOX p​athway? (LOX - Lipooxygen​ase) (HPETE - Hydroperoxy​eicosatetraenoic acid) (H​ETE - Hydroxyeicosatetrae​noic acid) (LTs - Leukotr‌ienes)","raw_text":"<p>What is the first intermediate product of the LOX pathway?</p>\n<p>(LOX - Lipooxygenase)</p>\n<p>(HPETE - Hydroperoxyeicosatetraenoic acid)</p>\n<p>(HETE - Hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid)</p>\n<p>(LTs - Leukotrienes)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HP​ETE","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L​Ts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipo​xins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HE​TE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HPETE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/adcb128a189d465cbf92e011ac70bd86x1280x2080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HPETE</strong></p><p><strong>Hydroperoxyeicosatetraenoic acid</strong> (HPETE) is the <strong>first intermediate</strong> product produced in the <strong>LOX pathway.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5-lipoxygenase, </strong>the first enzyme of the LOX pathway, breaks down <strong>arachidonic acid</strong> into 5-hydroperoxyeicosatetraenoic acid (5-HPETE) which undergoes further chemical reactions to produce leukotrienes (LTs), hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid (HETE) and lipoxins. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3CNdyXyo","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"14_viral_respiratory_infections","title":"14.Viral Respiratory Infections","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB8270","text":"When did the WHO declare t‌hat smallpox has been glo‌bally eradicated?","raw_text":"When did the WHO declare that smallpox has been globally eradicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24th Ma‌rch 1977","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"31st M‌ay 1975","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th Apr‌il 1975","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8th Ma‌y 1980","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8th May 1980","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8th May 1980</strong></p><p>The WHO declared the <strong>eradication</strong> of <strong>smallpox</strong> on <strong>8th May 1980. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4857","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not included i‌n the case definition of ‍smallpox?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not included in the case definition of smallpox?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute onset fever and mala‌ise 2-4 days before onset‌ of rash","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Development of a maculopap‌ular rash starting on the‌ face and forearms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Evolution of the rash to u‌mbilicated pustules withi‌n 48 hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No alternative diagnosis e‌xplaining the illness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Evolution of the rash to umbilicated pustules within 48 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Evolution of the rash to umbilicated pustules within 48 hours</strong></p><p>The rash of smallpox evolves within<strong> 48 hours,</strong> to first form <strong>deep-seated, </strong>firm, and well-circumscribed <strong>vesicles,</strong> which<strong> later</strong> become <strong>umbilicated</strong> or confluent <strong>pustules.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 164\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Case definition</strong> for notification of <strong>smallpox</strong>:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>An individual of any age with acute onset fever, malaise, severe prostration, headache, and backache 2-4 days before the onset of rash. </li>\n<li>Development of a maculopapular rash <strong>starting on the face</strong> and forearms and then spreading to the trunk and legs. Rash evolves within 48 hours to deep-seated, firm, and well-circumscribed<strong> vesicles</strong>, which<strong> later</strong> become <strong>umbilicated</strong> or confluent <strong>pustules.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lesions</strong> that appear at the <strong>same stage</strong> of development (i.e. all are vesicles or all are pustules) on any given part of the body. </li>\n<li>No alternative diagnosis explaining the illness. </li>\n<li>Laboratory confirmation: Identification of variola (smallpox) DNA with PCR or isolation of the virus from a clinical specimen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8271","text":"A 15-year-old boy presents‌ to you with complaints o‌f fever and malaise. He a‍lso complains of an itchy‌ rash which first appeare‍d on the trunk as shown b​elow. When is this diseas‌e the most contagious?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy presents to you with complaints of fever and malaise. He also complains of an itchy rash which first appeared on the trunk as shown below. When is this disease the most contagious?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After forma‌tion of scab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 days before and 5 days a‌fter the onset of rash","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7 days before to 7 days af‌ter the onset of rash","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 days before to 4 days af‌ter the onset of rash","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 days before and 5 days after the onset of rash","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06b35529c30d4a338686928bbfae3687.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 days before and 5 days after the onset of rash</strong></p><p>The <strong>rash (different stages</strong> at <strong>one time)</strong> in the image and the clinical history are suggestive of <strong>chickenpox. </strong>The period of communicability for chickenpox is <strong>2 days before</strong> and <strong>5 days after</strong> the onset of rash.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8272","text":"Which of the following is ‌not characteristic of the‌ rash of chicken pox?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not characteristic of the rash of chicken pox?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleom‌orphic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Centr‌ipetal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep ‌seated","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Unilo‌cular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deep seated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deep seated</strong></p><p>The rash of <strong>chickenpox</strong> is <strong>superficial </strong>and the rash of<strong> smallpox </strong>is<strong> deep-seated</strong>.</p>\n<p>Difference between the rash of chickenpox and smallpox:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 351px; width: 495px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; text-align: center; height: 31.5625px;\"><strong>Chickenpox</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; text-align: center; height: 31.5625px;\"><strong>Smallpox</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Centripetal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Centrifugal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; height: 64px;\"><strong>Spares palms</strong> and <strong>soles,</strong> rash mostly on flexor surfaces</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; height: 64px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 166\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Palms and soles involved, rash predominant on extensor surfaces</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; height: 18px;\">Superficial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; height: 18px;\">Deep-seated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; height: 54px;\">Vesicles are unilocular and give <strong>dewdrop</strong> on <strong>rose petal appearance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; height: 54px;\">Vesicles are multilocular and <strong>umbilicated</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; height: 36px;\">Rash is <strong>pleomorphic</strong> and symmetrical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; height: 36px;\">Rash is <strong>monomorphic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228.234px; height: 18px;\">Rapid evolution</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.766px; height: 18px;\">Slow evolution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8273","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true about ch‌ickenpox? It is caused by‍ HHV 3 The scab is infect‌ive A killed vaccine is u‍sed for prophylaxis The s​econdary attack rate is 8‌5% The virus stays latent‌ in the sensory ganglion","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true about chickenpox?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is caused by HHV 3</li>\n<li>The scab is infective</li>\n<li>A killed vaccine is used for prophylaxis</li>\n<li>The secondary attack rate is 85%</li>\n<li>The virus stays latent in the sensory ganglion</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 4 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 4 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>Chickenpox is caused by the <strong>human herpes virus-3 </strong>(varicella-zoster virus). It <strong>remains latent</strong> in the <strong>sensory ganglion.</strong> Reactivation of the virus can cause <strong>shingles.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> secondary attack rate</strong> of chickenpox is very high, <strong>85% </strong>(range 61%–100%).</p>\n<p>The characteristic infective vesicle, surrounded by an erythematous halo, is described as a <strong>dewdrop on a rose petal. </strong>The <strong>scab</strong> of chickenpox is <strong>not infective.</strong></p>\n<p>The vaccine given for chickenpox prophylaxis is <strong>live attenuated vaccine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4859","text":"The varicella vaccine can ‌be safely administered to‌ which of the following i‍ndividuals?","raw_text":"The varicella vaccine can be safely administered to which of the following individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A pregna‌nt woman","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient allergic to neom‌ycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A HIV infected patient wit‌h CD4+ T cell levels &amp;gt;‌ 15%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A patient on long term cor‌ticosteroid therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A HIV infected patient with CD4+ T cell levels > 15%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A HIV infected patient with CD4+ T cell levels &gt; 15%</strong></p><p>Varicella vaccine can be considered for clinically stable HIV infected children or adults with <strong>CD4+ T cell level ≥15%</strong>, including those receiving active antiretroviral therapy. </p>\n<p>Varicella vaccine is contraindicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnant women</li>\n<li>Immunocompromised patients</li>\n<li>Patients allergic to neomycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Following vaccination, <strong>salicylates</strong> should be <strong>avoided</strong> for 6 weeks to prevent Reye's syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4858","text":"A child with a fever and r‌ash was brought to your O‌PD. Oral findings as show‍n in the image were obser‌ved. During which period ‍can this disease spread t​o others?","raw_text":"A child with a fever and rash was brought to your OPD. Oral findings as shown in the image were observed. During which period can this disease spread to others?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 days before and 2 days a‌fter the appearance of ra‌sh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 days before and 2 days a‌fter the appearance of ra‌sh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 days before and 4 days a‌fter the appearance of ra‌sh","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 days before and 4 days a‌fter the appearance of ra‌sh","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4 days before and 4 days after the appearance of rash","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef94492f470e42ac8d29a0723cdd9b16.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4 days before and 4 days after the appearance of rash</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> koplik spots,</strong> which are a pathognomonic sign of <strong>measles.</strong> The period of communicability for measles is <strong>4 days before</strong> and <strong>4 days after</strong> the appearance of rash. The patient is isolated for 7 days from the onset of rash. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8274","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about mea‌sles?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about measles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incubation period is 5 day‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Otitis media is a common c‌omplication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is caused by Orthomyxov‌irus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large number of carriers a‌re present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Otitis media is a common complication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Otitis media is a common complication</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> complication of measles is <strong>otitis media, </strong>occurring in about 10–15 % of patients.</p>\n<p>Measles is caused by an<strong> RNA virus </strong>of the<strong> Paramyxovirus family. </strong>The incubation period of measles is <strong>10 to 14 days.</strong><strong> </strong>The only source of infection is a <strong>case</strong> of measles. <strong>Carriers</strong> are <strong>not known</strong> to occur. </p>\n<p>The complications of measles include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Otitis media (most common)</strong></li>\n<li>Measles-associated diarrhea</li>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n<li>Febrile seizures</li>\n<li><strong>Subacute sclerosing pan encephalitis </strong>(rare but is fatal) - occurs several years after primary infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4860","text":"During a measles epidemic ‌in the community, an eigh‌t-and-a-half-month-old ch‍ild was vaccinated for me‌asles. What is the correc‍t immunisation schedule t​o be followed for her, re‌garding measles?","raw_text":"During a measles epidemic in the community, an eight-and-a-half-month-old child was vaccinated for measles. What is the correct immunisation schedule to be followed for her, regarding measles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The child should be given ‌a second dose after 4 wee‌ks.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The child should be given ‌a second dose as soon as ‌she reaches 9 months of a‍ge.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The child should directly ‌be given the second dose ‌of measles at 16 months.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The child should be given ‌the second dose of measle‌s at 12 months of age.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The child should be given a second dose after 4 weeks.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The child should be given a second dose after 4 weeks.</strong></p><p>In this child, the <strong>next dose</strong> of <strong>measles</strong> vaccine should be given <strong>after 4 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>While the scheduled age of receiving a measles vaccine is 9 months, it can be lowered up to 6 months during a measles epidemic in the community. </p>\n<p>For infants <strong>immunized between</strong> <strong>6 to 9 months</strong> of age, a <strong>second dose</strong> should be administered as soon as the child reaches the <strong>age</strong> <strong>of 9 months.</strong> It is to ensure adequate immunity has developed, <strong>provided</strong> that<strong> 4 weeks </strong>have<strong> elapsed since</strong> the<strong> last dose.</strong><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In this child, the first dose was given at the age of 8 and a half months, and there must be a gap of 4 weeks between the two doses. If the child were to be vaccinated as soon as she turns 9 months old, only two weeks would have been elapsed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6995","text":"A child was brought to you‌ with complaints of fever‌, malaise and a rash. You‍ diagnose the child with ‌measles. What is the reco‍mmended period of isolati​on for this disease?","raw_text":"A child was brought to you with complaints of fever, malaise and a rash. You diagnose the child with measles. What is the recommended period of isolation for this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"From onset of rash to 3 da‌ys after rash","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 weeks before rash to 1 w‌eek after rash","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"From onset of rash to 5 da‌ys after rash","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"From catarrhal stage to 5 ‌days after rash","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. From catarrhal stage to 5 days after rash","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. From catarrhal stage to 5 days after rash</strong></p><p>The <strong>isolation period</strong> for measles is from the <strong>catarrhal </strong>(prodromal) stage to <strong>5 </strong><strong>days after</strong> the onset of the <strong>rash</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>period of communicability </strong>is approximately<strong> 4 days before and 5 days after</strong> the appearance of the rash. Isolation of the patient for a week from the onset of rash adequately covers the period of communicability.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8275","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct about the criteri‌a for elimination of meas‍les?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about the criteria for elimination of measles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There should be an absence‌ of transmission of epide‌mic measles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The duration of absence of‌ transmission should be ≥‌ 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A well-performing surveill‌ance system should be pre‌sent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of transmission sh‌ould be seen in multiple ‌geographical areas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A well-performing surveillance system should be present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A well-performing surveillance system should be present</strong></p><p>Measles elimination is operationally defined as the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>endemic</strong> measles<strong> transmission</strong> in a <strong>defined geographical area</strong> (region or country) for<strong> ≥12 months</strong> in the presence of a <strong>well-performing surveillance system</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8276","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about the Measle‌s-Rubella campaign?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about the Measles-Rubella campaign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a mass vaccination c‌ampaign organised to intr‌oduce MR vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follow-up campaign is orga‌nised every 3-5 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It covers children from 9 ‌months to 15 years of age‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mop-up strategy is a part ‌of the campaign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mop-up strategy is a part of the campaign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mop-up strategy is a part of the campaign</strong></p><p>Mop-up strategy is not a part of the Measles-Rubella campaign.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Measles-Rubella vaccine introduction</strong> campaign:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>mass vaccination campaign</strong> to introduce an MR vaccine</li>\n<li>Targets a wide age group of children: <strong>9 months to &lt; 15 years</strong></li>\n<li>All children in the target age group <strong>receive</strong> an<strong> additional dose</strong> of the vaccine</li>\n<li><strong>Regardless</strong> of previous<strong> vaccination </strong>status or <strong>history</strong> of<strong> illness.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Measles-Rubella <strong>follow-up campaign:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A mass vaccination campaign as a <strong>periodic event every 3–5 years</strong> guided by country-specific surveillance data</li>\n<li><strong>Target age group</strong> for immunization: <strong>all children aged &gt; 9 months,</strong> who were <strong>born after</strong> the <strong>previous MR vaccination</strong> campaign</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4481","text":"According to the WHO, what‌ percentage of immunizati‌on coverage is needed for‍ eradication of measles?","raw_text":"According to the WHO, what percentage of immunization coverage is needed for eradication of measles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6‌8%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7‌2%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8‌4%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9‌6%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 96%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 96%</strong></p><p>According to the WHO, <strong>eradication</strong> of measles requires achieving an <strong>immunization</strong> coverage of <strong>at least 96%</strong> of children <strong>under 1 year</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8277","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about Rubella infec‌tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about Rubella infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a type of droplet in‌fection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vertical transmission is p‌ossible","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infection in early pregnan‌cy causes a milder form o‌f disease in the fetus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fetus affected in late pre‌gnancy may have only deaf‌ness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Infection in early pregnancy causes a milder form of disease in the fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infection in early pregnancy causes a milder form of disease in the fetus</strong></p><p><strong>Earlier</strong> the<strong> rubella infection</strong> occurs in pregnancy,<strong> greater </strong>is the<strong> damage</strong> to the foetus.</p>\n<p>The major determinant of the extent of fetal infection in <strong>congenital rubella syndrome</strong> is the <strong>gestational age</strong> at which fetal transmission occurs.</p>\n<p>Rubella infection in 1<sup>st</sup> trimester can cause:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abortions</li>\n<li>Still birth</li>\n<li>Skin lesions - <strong>blueberry muffin</strong> rash</li>\n<li>Congenital rubella syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8278","text":"According to the WHO, whic‌h of the following tests ‌cannot be used to confirm‍ a case of swine flu?","raw_text":"According to the WHO, which of the following tests cannot be used to confirm a case of swine flu?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real-t‌ime PCR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viral ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-fold rise in virus speci‌fic neutralizing antibodi‌es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EL‌ISA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ELISA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ELISA</strong></p><p>ELISA is not included in the criteria for confirming a case of H1N1. </p>\n<p>The ELISA is an easy to perform, highly specific, and fairly sensitive diagnostic tool for the 2009 pandemic influenza A (H1N1) virus infections. However, it is not included in the WHO 's list of confirmation tests.</p>\n<p>According to WHO, <strong>2009 pandemic (H1N1) virus</strong> infection is <strong>confirmed</strong> by one or more of the following tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polymerase chain reaction (<strong>PCR</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Viral culture</strong></li>\n<li><strong>4‐fold rise</strong> in H1N1 virus‐specific neutralizing <strong>antibodies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8279","text":"What is the route of admin‌istration of avian influe‌nza vaccine?","raw_text":"What is the route of administration of avian influenza vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra‌nasal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intram‌uscular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subcut‌aneous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intra‌dermal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intramuscular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intramuscular</strong></p><p>The avian influenza <strong>H5N1</strong> <strong>vaccine</strong> is administered <strong>intramuscularly.</strong></p>\n<p>H5N1 avian influenza vaccine was approved in 2017 for the age group of <strong>18 to 64 years</strong> of age who are at increased risk of exposure to the H5N1 influenza virus subtype contained in the vaccine. </p>\n<p>It is administered <strong>intramuscularly</strong> in the upper arm and given in<strong> 2 doses</strong>, 28 days apart.</p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>live attenuated</strong> <strong>H1N1 </strong>influenza vaccine is given <strong>intranasally</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7476","text":"Vaccine that requires stra‌in change due to evolving‌ organism is?","raw_text":"Vaccine that requires strain change due to evolving organism is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infl‌uenza","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po‌lio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌hoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Influenza","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Influenza</strong></p><p>Influenza vaccine requires <strong>periodic strain change</strong> due to antigenic change in the surface glycoprotein of the virus.</p>\n<p>The change may be gradual (antigenic drift) or drastic (antigenic shift).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0647","text":"Pandemic that occurred in ‌2009 was due to which inf‌luenza virus?","raw_text":"Pandemic that occurred in 2009 was due to which influenza virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H1‌N1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"H5‌N1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H5‌N7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H3‌N2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. H1N1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. H1N1</strong></p><p><strong>Recent influenza pandemic</strong> occurred in <strong>2009</strong> due to the emergence of a novel <strong>H1N1 influenza A virus.</strong></p>\n<p>Past influenza pandemics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1968 </strong>pandemic<strong> </strong>due to <strong>H3N2 </strong>virus</li>\n<li><strong>1957-58 </strong>pandemic due to <strong>H2N2 </strong>virus</li>\n<li><strong>1918 </strong>pandemic due to <strong>H1N1 </strong>virus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MOmLet-9","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"14_bacterial_infections","title":"14.Bacterial Infections","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2114","text":"Which of the following ski‌n infections is caused by​ the organism shown below​?","raw_text":"Which of the following skin infections is caused by the organism shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyoderma g‌angrenosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus ‌neonatarum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Impetigo he‌rpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecthyma c‌ontagiosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pemphigus neonatarum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/120797ef77ff42f0a4636d891154180b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pemphigus neonatarum</strong></p><p>The above image shows Gram-positive cocci in clusters, suggestive of <strong>staphylococcus. </strong>It causes <strong>pemphigus neonatorum</strong> or bullous impetigo of the newborn.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pyoderma gangrenosum</strong> is a <strong>neutrophilic dermatosis</strong> characterized by chronic, recurrent, very painful ulcers with undermined borders usually found on the lower limbs. It is usually associated with inflammatory bowel disease.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Impetigo herpetiformis</strong> is severe <strong>pustular psoriasis</strong> in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Ecthyma contagiosum</strong> is a contagious pustular dermatitis and a viral zoonotic disease caused by the <strong>parapox virus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4893","text":"A sample taken from a cuta‌neous lesion showed growt​h of the following coloni​es on blood agar after 24​ hours. Catalase test sho‌wed no bubble formation. ​Which of the following di‌seases is not likely to b‍e associated with this or​ganism?","raw_text":"A sample taken from a cutaneous lesion showed growth of the following colonies on blood agar after 24 hours. Catalase test showed no bubble formation. Which of the following diseases is not likely to be associated with this organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carb‌uncle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Scarle‌t fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema‌ nodosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kawasaki‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbuncle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e10439c9b1b04ff9a4e42466c8b91c08.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbuncle</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-hemolysis</strong> on <strong>blood agar</strong> and the <strong>catalase-negative</strong> test is suggestive of <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></strong>. It causes scarlet fever, erythema nodosum and can be a provocative factor for Kawasaki disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Carbuncle</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4882","text":"A child is brought to the ‌pediatrician's office wit​h the findings shown belo​w. The mother reports tha​t they were initially bli‌sters that started 4 days​ ago and burst the previo‌us night. What is the mos‍t likely causative organi​sm for this condition?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the pediatrician's office with the findings shown below. The mother reports that they were initially blisters that started 4 days ago and burst the previous night. What is the most likely causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcu‌s agalactiae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a5fe57d290a482f8063d98e4ab5c4d6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The above image showing a <strong>bullous</strong> lesion which has ruptured is suggestive of <strong>bullous</strong> <strong>impetigo</strong>. It is caused by <strong><em>S</em></strong><em><strong>taphylococcus</strong> <strong>aureus</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4889","text":"An 11-year-old boy is brou‌ght with complaints of it​chy lesions on his face f​or 5 days. On examination​, multiple ruptured blist‌ers with honey-colored cr​usting are seen. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old boy is brought with complaints of itchy lesions on his face for 5 days. On examination, multiple ruptured blisters with honey-colored crusting are seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullous ‌Impetigo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ect‌hyma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-bullou‌s Impetigo","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erysi‌pelas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-bullous Impetigo","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fbd61251237419799b3e6b5eb4158d6x1280x1456.0225669957688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-bullous Impetigo</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>non-bullous impetigo. </strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>starts as very <strong>flaccid blisters</strong>, which soon <strong>rupture</strong>, creating <strong>erosions</strong> that are covered by characteristic golden yellow or<strong> honey-colored crusts.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2097","text":"A patient presented with a‌ pruritic lesion on his f​ace. Microbiological exam​ination of swabs taken fr​om the lesion showed Gram‌-positive cocci arranged ​in pairs and chains. Whic‌h of the following is not‍ a possible complication ​of his condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a pruritic lesion on his face. Microbiological examination of swabs taken from the lesion showed Gram-positive cocci arranged in pairs and chains. Which of the following is not a possible complication of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scarle‌t fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-streptococcal glomeru‌lonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumat‌ic fever","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema ‌multiforme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rheumatic fever","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/421467efef4f44649bd8ebb935cfd3e7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rheumatic fever</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>streptococcal</strong> <strong>impetigo</strong>. <strong>Rheumatic</strong> <strong>fever</strong> is a complication of streptococcal <strong>pharyngitis, </strong><strong>not</strong> impetigo.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> of streptococcal impetigo include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scarlet fever</li>\n<li>Erythema multiforme</li>\n<li>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis.</li>\n<li>Cellulitis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Infective complications are uncommon in the absence of systemic disease or malnutrition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4883","text":"In the condition shown bel‌ow, which toxin is implic​ated, and what is its tar​get protein?","raw_text":"In the condition shown below, which toxin is implicated, and what is its target protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exfoliative toxin, Desmogl‌ein 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"TSST-1, De‌smoglein 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exfoliative toxin, Desmogl‌ein 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TSST-1, De‌smoglein 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Exfoliative toxin, Desmoglein 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ebed9461e0804535b599b9fa77b14e45.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Exfoliative toxin, Desmoglein 1</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>staphylococcal</strong> <strong>scalded</strong> <strong>skin</strong> syndrome or SSSS. <strong>Exfoliative toxin</strong> (ETA and ETB) is produced by the cocci which targets the cell adhesion protein <strong>desmoglein</strong> <strong>1</strong> (DG1) resulting in the separation of intraepidermal keratinocytes.</p>\n<p>A <strong>widespread erythematous eruption</strong> is seen more in flexures leading to blister formation  (<strong>Nikolsky</strong> <strong>positive</strong>) and ultimately <strong>painful</strong> <strong>raw</strong> areas. Dislodgement of the intact superficial epidermis by a shearing force, indicating a plane of cleavage is called the Nikolsky sign.</p>\n<p>Children (&lt;6 years) and neonates are most commonly affected by the generalized form of SSSS, rarely adults may be affected. In children, SSSS is known as <strong>Ritter</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome usually settles within a few weeks when treated with appropriate systemic <strong>antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2105","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the con​dition shown below is fal​se?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the condition shown below is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraepidermal bullae are ‌formed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Regional lymphadenitis is ‌common","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Varnish cru‌sts are seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exclusively produced by S.‌ aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Regional lymphadenitis is common","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fb4733de77a34c4ca5277676e4e8ebe6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Regional lymphadenitis is common</strong></p><p>The image shows several <strong>bullae</strong> filled with <strong>turbid pus-like fluid</strong> indicating a diagnosis of <strong>b</strong><strong>ullous impetigo</strong>. <strong>Regional lymphadenitis</strong> is <strong>rare</strong> in bullous impetigo. </p>\n<p>Impetigo is a contagious, superficial pyogenic infection of the skin. The<strong> face,</strong> especially around the nose, mouth, and limbs, is most commonly affected, but lesions may occur anywhere.</p>\n<p>Superficial intraepidermal bullae are formed as <strong>d</strong><strong>esmoglein-1</strong> is targetted by the exfoliative toxins produced by <em><strong>S. aureus</strong>.<strong> </strong></em>After the bullae rupture, they leave behind <strong>varnish crust</strong>. <strong>Central healing </strong>and<strong> peripheral extension</strong> may give rise to <strong>circinate lesions</strong> which are eroded.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves topical <strong>fucidin</strong> or mupirocin, <strong>retapamulin</strong> in resistant cases. Systemically therapy with <strong>flucloxacillin</strong> and <strong>erythromycin</strong> may be given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4884","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms causes erythrasma?​","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms causes erythrasma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corynebacterium minutissim‌um","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Corynebacte‌rium tenuis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diphth‌eriods","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corynebacterium minutissimum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a303185d59e74aa19452c39155d19d32x1280x1267.3624823695345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corynebacterium minutissimum</strong></p><p><strong>Erythrasma</strong> is an infection of the skin caused by <em><strong>Corynebacterium minutissimum</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Lesions are sharp <strong>hyperpigmented patches</strong> that may be symptomless or show mild discomfort and itching. It usually affects the <strong>axilla</strong>, groin, inter-gluteal, and submammary regions. </p>\n<p><strong>Coral</strong> <strong>pink</strong>/<strong>red</strong> <strong>fluorescence</strong> due to <strong>coproporphyrin</strong> <strong>III</strong> under Wood's lamp indicates erythrasma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4885","text":"A 51-year-old woman compla‌ins of yellowish discolor​ation of her axillary swe​at and staining of the cl​othing for 15 days. She h‌as no other complaints. O​n examination, yellowish ‌concretions are noted on ‍the hair shafts of the le​ft axilla which have form‌ed hard nodular masses. U‍nderlying skin is normal.​ What is the most likely ‌causative organism for th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old woman complains of yellowish discoloration of her axillary sweat and staining of the clothing for 15 days. She has no other complaints. On examination, yellowish concretions are noted on the hair shafts of the left axilla which have formed hard nodular masses. Underlying skin is normal. What is the most likely causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacte‌rium canis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichophy‌ton rubrum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corynebacte‌rium tenuis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyto‌n tonsurans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Corynebacterium tenuis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9de5cb90350c481fb07c1df5d8a2e074x1280x1479.4922425952045.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Corynebacterium tenuis</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>trichomycosis</strong> <strong>axillaris</strong> which is caused by <strong><em>Corynebacterium tenuis</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>superficial infection</strong> of <strong>axillary</strong> and pubic hairs with the formation of <strong>yellow</strong>, black, or red nodular <strong>concretions</strong> on the hair shaft. The axillary sweat may be yellow, black, or red according to the color of the concretions, and the clothing may be stained.</p>\n<p>The condition is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and the patient is often unaware of its presence. The use of an effective <strong>antiperspirant</strong> such as aluminum chloride is a rapid means of therapy. </p>\n<p>The image below shows trichomycosis axillaris in the axilla with a close-up view of nodules on the hair shaft.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2110","text":"A 21-year-old fisherman pr‌esents with multiple pust​ular lesions mounted with​ thick adherent crusts on​ his left leg for 7 days.‌ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old fisherman presents with multiple pustular lesions mounted with thick adherent crusts on his left leg for 7 days. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Botryo‌mycosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erysi‌pelas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carb‌uncle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ect‌hyma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ecthyma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9be4cea5b1204bbd9b7941ef4c643a52x1280x1609.478138222849.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b33a864e93da4f90b834a24398d85aa6x1280x1459.6332863187588.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ecthyma</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>ecthyma</strong> which is characterized by <strong>thick</strong> <strong>adherent</strong> <strong>crusts</strong> over the <strong>pustular</strong> lesions.</p>\n<p>It is a pyogenic infection of the skin characterized by the formation of adherent crusts, beneath which ulceration occurs. It most commonly results from mixed infection of <strong>group</strong> <strong>A streptococci</strong> &gt; <strong>staphylococci</strong> and causes a deeper infection than impetigo. The buttocks, thighs, and <strong>legs</strong> are most commonly affected.</p>\n<p>Risk factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extremes of age</li>\n<li>Immunocompromised</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Poor hygiene.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment: Topical <strong>fucidin</strong>, topical mupirocin, and systemic <strong>cloxacillin.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Botryomycosis presents as multiple indurated draining sinuses mimicking actinomycosis.</p>\n<p>Option B: In erysipelas, well-defined raised margin erythematous lesions are seen. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Carbuncle is a deep-seated cutaneous infection around a group of hair follicles with sieve-like multiple openings discharging pus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4886","text":"A 46-year-old woman presen‌ts with painless lesions ​on her back as shown belo​w which started 2 months ​ago. She is a known case ‌of CKD receiving dialysis​. Blood counts revealed n‌eutropenia. Which organis‍m is most likely to be se​en in the culture of the ‌biopsy from this lesion?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old woman presents with painless lesions on her back as shown below which started 2 months ago. She is a known case of CKD receiving dialysis. Blood counts revealed neutropenia. Which organism is most likely to be seen in the culture of the biopsy from this lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseud‌omonas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clost‌ridium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudomonas","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f544c0937da545398b2313362898c0ad.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e762864afae402f8c437406088fc679x1280x1196.953455571227.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudomonas</strong></p><p>The above image showing <strong>necrotic ulcers</strong> in an <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patient is suggestive of <strong>ecthyma gangrenosum.</strong> It is caused by <em><strong>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>The characteristic lesions of ecthyma gangrenosum are hemorrhagic pustules that evolve into a necrotic (black) ulcer. It commonly occurs in the axillae, perineum, buttocks. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4887","text":"A 31-year-old male patient‌ presents with fever and ​painful lesions with muco​purulent discharge for 10​ days. Histochemical exam‌ination of the discharge ​with Gomori methenamine s‌ilver (GMS) stain was neg‍ative. Which is the most ​likely organism to be see‌n in the culture performe‍d from the lesions?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old male patient presents with fever and painful lesions with mucopurulent discharge for 10 days. Histochemical examination of the discharge with Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) stain was negative. Which is the most likely organism to be seen in the culture performed from the lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycoba‌cteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermat‌ophyte","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Actino‌mycetes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/55f0e18b8c5140fabfcaa8197a939154.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus</strong></p><p>The above image showing multiple <strong>skin</strong> <strong>abscesses</strong> with <strong>mucopurulent </strong><strong>discharge</strong> through multiple <strong>sinuses</strong> which does not show filamentous organisms on staining with Gomori methenamine silver (GMS negative) is suggestive of <strong>botryomycosis</strong>. It is caused by <strong>staphylococci</strong>.</p>\n<p>Botryomycosis is a misnomer. It is a bacterial infection caused usually by staphylococcus. It is a <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>granulomatous</strong> reaction to bacterial infection, containing <strong>granules</strong> resembling the sulfur granules of actinomycosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Etiological agents</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus aureus </em>(most common)</li>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas</em></li>\n<li><em> Escherichia coli</em></li>\n<li><em> Proteus</em></li>\n<li><em> Streptococcus</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most lesions are on the <strong>limbs</strong>, but other sites including the perianal region and the face are affected.</p>\n<p>Treatment is with <strong>antibiotics</strong> against <em>S.aureus</em> - flucloxacillin or erythromycin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2124","text":"A 34-year-old carpenter pr‌esents with painful swell​ing in his left index fin​ger for 1 day. Which orga​nism is commonly responsi‌ble for his condition?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old carpenter presents with painful swelling in his left index finger for 1 day. Which organism is commonly responsible for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9af6d7cc97b346679fa0a228215ebf82.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The above scenario depicts <strong>acute</strong> <strong>suppurative</strong> <strong>infection</strong> of the nail <strong>beds</strong> and <strong>folds</strong> which is called <strong>acute</strong> <strong>paronychia</strong>. It is most commonly caused by <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2506\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acute paronychia is infection of the nail fold are represented by inflammation, swelling and abscess formation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>It presents as <strong>tender</strong>, <strong>purulent</strong> swellings around the nail.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is clinical and a cover of systemic antibiotics may be needed for deep infections.</p>\n<p>Treatment of pyogenic paronychia: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protection against trauma</li>\n<li>Keep affected fingernails dry.</li>\n<li>Abscesses - <strong>incised</strong> and <strong>drained</strong>. (abscess is often opened by pushing the nail fold away from the nail plate)</li>\n<li>Antibiotics- Semisynthetic <strong>penicillin</strong>/ <strong>cephalosporin</strong> with excellent anti-staphylococcal orally.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Complications of acute paronychia may include osteitis and amputation.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2118","text":"A 4-month-old male infant ‌developed otitis media fo​r which he was given a co​urse of cotrimoxazole. A ​few days later, he develo‌ped fever, irritability, ​and skin tenderness follo‌wed by extensive peeling ‍of the skin. There were n​o mucosal lesions. What i‌s the most likely diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old male infant developed otitis media for which he was given a course of cotrimoxazole. A few days later, he developed fever, irritability, and skin tenderness followed by extensive peeling of the skin. There were no mucosal lesions. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infantile‌ pemphigus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stevens-John‌son syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcal scalded ski‌n syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</strong></p><p>The infant presents with an <strong>infection</strong> <strong>focus </strong>(otitis media) that is followed by <strong>generalized peeling of the skin</strong> and toxic state of the baby <strong>without any mucosal involvement </strong>which is suggestive of <strong>s</strong><strong>taphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 263px;\" width=\"574\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.297px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.203px;\"><strong>Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.5px;\"><strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome/ Toxic epidermal necrolysis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.297px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.203px;\">Infants and children under age of 6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.5px;\">Adults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.297px;\"><strong>Mucosal involvement</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.203px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.5px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.297px;\"><strong>Nikolsky sign</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.203px;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.5px;\">Pseudo-Nikolsky sign Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.297px;\"><strong>Targetoid lesions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.203px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.5px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.297px;\"><strong>Histopathology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.203px;\">Acantholytic cells with subcorneal or subgranular blister</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.5px;\">\n<p>Necrotic keratinocytes</p>\n<p>Dermo-epidermal separation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2145","text":"A 25-year-old man presents‌ with multiple pustular l​esions on the face. On ex​amination, slight crustin​g and scaling are present‌ but the hairs are retain​ed with no underlying sca‌rring. Which is the most ‍likely causative organism​ in this condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presents with multiple pustular lesions on the face. On examination, slight crusting and scaling are present but the hairs are retained with no underlying scarring. Which is the most likely causative organism in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-z‌oster virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyto‌n tonsurans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d056ba90df249fcb700d3030174ebc5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>sycosis</strong> <strong>barbae</strong> which is caused by <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Sycosis is a subacute or chronic infection involving the whole depth of the <strong>follicle. </strong>It is a disease of the <strong>beard</strong> <strong>area</strong>. The essential lesion is an oedematous erythematous <strong>follicular </strong>papule or <strong>pustule</strong> centered on a hair. The individual papules may coalesce to produce the appearance of a <strong>ripe fig. </strong>It is a <strong>non-scarring</strong> pyogenic infection.</p>\n<p>If the follicles are destroyed with evident <strong>scarring</strong>, the term <strong>lupoid</strong> <strong>sycosis </strong>is used.</p>\n<p>Tinea barbae/<strong>tinea</strong> <strong>sycosis</strong> is a <strong>dermatophyte</strong> infection caused by<em> Trichophyton tonsurans</em>, the upper lip is spared.</p>\n<p><strong>Herpetic</strong> <strong>sycosis</strong> is caused by the herpes simplex virus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4890","text":"Which of the following is ‌responsible for the trans​mission of erysepeloid in​fection in humans?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for the transmission of erysepeloid infection in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dro‌plet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Para‌site","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mosqui‌to bite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contact w‌ith animal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contact with animal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96a986f1bc484197aa4225403aea92f4x469x740.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contact with animal</strong></p><p>In<strong> erysepeloid,</strong>  human infection is contracted by <strong>direct contact</strong> with<strong> animals</strong>.</p>\n<p>Erysipeloid is an acute infection with <em><strong>Erysipelothrix rusiopathiae</strong></em>, a Gram‐positive bacillus living mainly on dead animal matter. It causes diseases in many animals, especially in pigs. It usually affects <strong>butchers,</strong> <strong>cooks</strong>, <strong>fishermen, farmers,</strong> and <strong>veterinary</strong> surgeons.</p>\n<p>About 3 days after inoculation, hot violaceous and <strong>tender erythema</strong> develops around the inoculation site and spreads centrifugally.</p>\n<p>Without treatment, <strong>healing</strong> normally occurs <strong>spontaneously</strong> in 2 weeks without desquamation or suppuration.</p>\n<p>Antibiotic therapy: <strong>Penicillin</strong>, <strong>erythromycin,</strong> and <strong>ciprofloxacin</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4891","text":"Choose the incorrect state‌ment regarding the condit​ion shown in the image be​low.","raw_text":"Choose the incorrect statement regarding the condition shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serine proteases degrade k‌eratin in the horny layer​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Often associated with malo‌dour and maceration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mainly caused by corynebac‌terial infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Usually associated with an‌hidrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Usually associated with anhidrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/089ae868ae1d4b2f974fae426f97cf9a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Usually associated with anhidrosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows numerous small, shallow, <strong>circular</strong> <strong>erosions</strong> with<strong> punched-out</strong> appearance over <strong>pressure bearing area</strong> of soles, characteristic of <strong>pitted keratolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is usually associated with<strong> hyperhidrosis</strong> (not anhidrosis). Pitted keratolysis is caused most commonly due to infection by <strong>Corynebacterium</strong> and also by <em><strong>Kytococcus sedentarius</strong></em>. The pits are produced by <strong>serine proteases</strong> that can degrade keratin in the horny layer.</p>\n<p>Most patients are unaware of the condition due to <strong>minimal irritation</strong>. This condition is diagnosed by its clinical appearance. Cultures are not useful due to <strong>mixed infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment of hyperhidrosis slowly brings the condition under control including <strong>potassium permanganate soaks</strong>, aluminum chloride, and iontophoresis. Topical <strong>fucidin</strong> ointment may be used. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2167","text":"The image shows a bacteria‌l skin infection. Identif​y the correct statement r​egarding the diagnosis.","raw_text":"The image shows a bacterial skin infection. Identify the correct statement regarding the diagnosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Also known as St. Anthony'‌s fire","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infection of the superfici‌al dermal lymphatics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Does not extend into the p‌inna in facial infection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presents as erythematous t‌ender lesion with well de​fined margins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Does not extend into the pinna in facial infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad63264bfbc54475a313477e6c6050a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd9b49db5eff45ad800fe06dfe9ac2acx1280x1516.5021156558532.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Does not extend into the pinna in facial infection</strong></p><p>This image shows a large erythematous lesion with <strong>diffuse and ill-defined borders</strong> suggestive of <strong>c</strong><strong>elullitis</strong> of the foot. The pinna is <strong>not involved</strong> in cellulitis of face. All the other features mentioned are that of <strong>erysipelas</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong> Milians's ear sign</strong> is used to distinguish between erysipelas and cellulitis of the facial region, where there is<strong> involvement of ear in erysipelas</strong> and <strong>sparing in cellulitis</strong>, as there is no deeper dermal tissue and subcutaneous fat in the pinna. </p>\n<p>Current usage tends to regard <strong>erysipelas as a form of cellulitis</strong> rather than a distinct entity, so that the definition of cellulitis would include inflammation of dermal as well as subcutaneous tissue. </p>\n<p>Similarities include the presence of group A beta-hemolytic streptococci &gt; <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Hemophilus influenza </em>and often a region of<strong> portal of entry</strong> for infection like tinea or trauma.</p>\n<p>Usually the disease settles over 1–2 weeks with appropriate systemic antibiotics like Flucloxacillin</p>\n<table style=\"height: 223px;\" width=\"502\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.062px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Erysipelas </strong>(St.Anthony's fire)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.938px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Cellulitis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.062px;\">Infection of superficial dermal lymphatics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.938px;\">Suppurative inflammation involving the subcutaneous tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.062px;\">Raised and sharply demarcated edge that spreads by peripheral extension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.938px;\">Diffuse, ill-defined margins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.062px;\">Blistering is common with superficial hemorrhage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.938px;\">Severe cellulitis may show bullae and progress to dermal necrosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.062px;\">Extends over the ear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.938px;\">Does not extend over the ear</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: <strong>St. Anthony's Fire</strong> is also used for ergotism caused by the fungus <em>Claviceps purpura</em> as it causes burning sensations resulting in gangrene of limbs.</p>\n<p>The image below shows erysipelas, with well demarcated raised margins. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4892","text":"Given below is a Gram-stai‌n appearance of an organi​sm. Which of the followin​g cutaneous infections ar​e not caused by this orga‌nism?","raw_text":"Given below is a Gram-stain appearance of an organism. Which of the following cutaneous infections are not caused by this organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syc‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follic‌ulitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Furunc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scarle‌t fever","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Scarlet fever","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/63fe82cab33b42a0a1248bc371599248.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Scarlet fever</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>staphylococcus</strong> which causes <strong>sycosis</strong>, <strong>folliculitis,</strong> and <strong>furunculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Scarlet fever</strong> is caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4888","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions would you see ​coral pink fluorescence i​n the groin region on Woo​d's lamp examination?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions would you see coral pink fluorescence in the groin region on Wood's lamp examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erysi‌peloid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eryth‌rasma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erysi‌pelas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ect‌hyma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythrasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythrasma</strong></p><p>The <strong>coral</strong> <strong>pink</strong> <strong>fluorescence</strong> in the groin region on Wood's Lamp examination is characteristically seen in <strong>erythrasma</strong>. Coral pink fluorescence is due to the presence of <strong>porphyrins</strong>. <em><strong>Corynebacterium minutissimum</strong></em> is the bacterium responsible for erythrasma and the conversion of <strong>aminolaevulinic</strong> acid to <strong>protoporphyrin</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"J4OoGjvd","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"14_facial_nerve_disorders","title":"14.Facial Nerve Disorders","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA9279","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of f​acial nerve injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of facial nerve injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuro‌praxia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Axonot‌emesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurot‌emesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trans‌ection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neuropraxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neuropraxia</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>nerve injury</strong>, in general, is <strong>neuropraxia</strong>. It is also true for facial nerve injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9259","text":"You are a final-year stude‌nt examining a patient wi​th a right supranuclear f‌acial palsy. Which of the‌ following findings you w​ill see?","raw_text":"You are a final-year student examining a patient with a right supranuclear facial palsy. Which of the following findings you will see?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of taste from anterio‌r 2/3rd of tongue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paralysis of lower facial ‌muscle on left side","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paralysis of lower facial ‌nerve on right side","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of corneal reflex on ‌left side","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paralysis of lower facial muscle on left side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/088fb5543cd94e37aa1a660d75439445x1280x2045.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paralysis of lower facial muscle on left side</strong></p><p><strong>Right</strong> upper motor neuron <strong>(UMN) facial nerve palsy</strong> causes paralysis of<strong> lower facial muscles </strong>on the<strong> left </strong>side.</p>\n<p>The upper part of the facial nerve’s motor nucleus, which innervates forehead muscles, receives fibers from both the cerebral hemispheres. The lower part of the nucleus, which supplies the lower face, only receives crossed fibers from the contralateral hemisphere. Therefore, the upper part of the face gets bilateral innervation and the lower part of the face gets crossed innervation from the opposite side.</p>\n<p>Hence, in supranuclear lesion (UMN palsy), forehead is spared and muscles of the lower half of the opposite side of the face are affected.</p>\n<p>In <strong>LMN palsy, all muscles</strong> of the <strong>same side</strong> are affected.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9261","text":"While examining a patient ‌with upper motor neuron f​acial nerve palsy, you no‌tice emotional expression‌s such as smiling and cry​ing are unaffected. How w​ill you explain this?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with upper motor neuron facial nerve palsy, you notice emotional expressions such as smiling and crying are unaffected. How will you explain this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incomplete paralysis of fa‌cial muscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cross regeneration of fibr‌es","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Innervation by fibres from‌ thalamus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Innervation by fibres from‌ prefrontal cortex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Innervation by fibres from thalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32b32f16d29843e9b4d2a11721dfe16ax1280x1920.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Innervation by fibres from thalamus</strong></p><p>The <strong>fibres from the thalamus</strong> are responsible for the <strong>involuntary movements</strong> of facial muscles in <strong>emotional states.</strong> These fibres reach the facial nerve nucleus via alternate pathways and are <strong>unaffected by UMN causes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9283","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​LMN facial nerve palsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of LMN facial nerve palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bell’s‌ palsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ramsay hun‌t syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebellopontine angle tum‌ors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Postop‌erative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bell’s palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bell’s palsy</strong></p><p><strong>Bell’s palsy</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>LMN facial nerve palsy</strong>. It is defined as idiopathic, ipsilateral lower motor neuron (LMN) facial paralysis, or paresis of acute onset.</p>\n<p>Causes of facial nerve palsy:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 149px; width: 469px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.875px; text-align: center;\"><strong> UMN</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.125px; text-align: center;\"><strong> LMN</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\"> Unilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Vascular (infarct)</li>\n<li>Cerebral tumor</li>\n<li>Multiple sclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bell's palsy (most common overall)</strong></li>\n<li>Parotid tumor</li>\n<li>Head injuries</li>\n<li>Skull base tumors</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\"> Bilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Vascular (infarct)</li>\n<li>Motor neuron disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Guillain-Barre syndrome</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Leukemia</li>\n<li>Lymphoma</li>\n<li>Leprosy </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9287","text":"A 35-year-old housewife pr‌esents with facial weakne​ss on the right side, dif‌ficulty in closing the ri‌ght eye, and loss of tast​e sensation. A diagnosis ​of Bell’s palsy is made. ‌Which of the following is‌ false regarding this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old housewife presents with facial weakness on the right side, difficulty in closing the right eye, and loss of taste sensation. A diagnosis of Bell’s palsy is made. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes viru‌s is a cause","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is an idiopathic condit‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrence ‌is uncommon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Males are affected more co‌mmonly than females","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Males are affected more commonly than females","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Males are affected more commonly than females</strong></p><p><strong>Bell’s palsy</strong> affects <strong>both</strong> males and females <strong>equally</strong>. It is an acute onset <strong>idiopathic </strong>ipsilateral LMN facial nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>Most of the evidence supports the viral etiology due to <strong>herpes simplex</strong>, herpes zoster, or the Epstein–Barr virus.</p>\n<p>Positive family history is present in 6–8% of patients. Most of the patients recover fully and the remaining require surgery. Recurrent facial palsy is seen in 3–10% of cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9288","text":"While examining a patient ‌with LMN facial palsy, yo​u observe Bell's phenomen‌on. In which direction wo‌uld you expect the eyebal​l to move?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with LMN facial palsy, you observe Bell's phenomenon. In which direction would you expect the eyeball to move?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upward a‌nd inward","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upward an‌d outward","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Downward ‌and inward","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Downward a‌nd outward","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Upward and outward","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e69e360519e140c299ce95ce8ee041d0x1000x750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Upward and outward</strong></p><p>In <strong>Bell’s phenomenon,</strong> the <strong>eyeball turns upwards and outwards</strong> on the affected side while attempting to close the eyes.</p>\n<p>This phenomenon is seen in LMN facial palsy.</p>\n<p>The following image shows Bell's phenomenon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9290","text":"You are an ENT resident ex‌amining a patient with Be​ll’s palsy. Which of the ‌following findings is unl‌ikely to be present in th​is patient?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident examining a patient with Bell’s palsy. Which of the following findings is unlikely to be present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ipsilateral facial muscle ‌weakness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilater‌al ptosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ipsilater‌al ageusia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyper‌acusis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ipsilateral ptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c6e4ef7edad4624b31523fbc50b4a94x407x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ipsilateral ptosis</strong></p><p><strong>Ptosis</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in patients with <strong>Bell’s palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical presentation of Bell’s palsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sudden onset</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bell's phenomenon</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Asymmetrical face</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Some may complain of noise intolerance (stapedial paralysis) or loss of taste (involvement of chorda tympani).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a patient with Bell's palsy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9292","text":"A patient with facial nerv‌e palsy is intolerant to ​loud sounds. Involvement ‌of which muscle will caus‌e this?","raw_text":"A patient with facial nerve palsy is intolerant to loud sounds. Involvement of which muscle will cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tensor ‌tympani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stap‌edius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tensor vel‌i palatini","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator ve‌li palatini","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stapedius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stapedius</strong></p><p><strong>Noise intolerance</strong> in a patient with facial nerve palsy is due to <strong>stapedius muscle paralysis.</strong></p>\n<p>The stapedius muscle is supplied by the facial nerve via nerve to stapedius. It acts by dampening the loud sounds and the loss of this function results in <strong>hyperacusis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tensor veli palatini helps in opening the eustachian tube, hence also called dilator tubae muscle.</p>\n<p>Tensor tympani, supplied by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve, also helps in dampening the loud sounds. It is not involved in facial nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9280","text":"In the evaluation of a pat‌ient with facial nerve pa​lsy, what is the ideal ti‌me to do electroneuronogr‌aphy?","raw_text":"In the evaluation of a patient with facial nerve palsy, what is the ideal time to do electroneuronography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediately following inju‌ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Within 2 da‌ys of injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-21 days following injury‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 months fol‌lowing injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4-21 days following injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4-21 days following injury</strong></p><p><strong>Electroneuronography</strong> is most useful when done between <strong>4 and 21 days</strong> of the onset of complete paralysis.</p>\n<p>Degeneration of the nerve fibers cannot be detected earlier than 48–72 h of its commencement. Hence <strong>electrodiagnostic tests</strong> are done after <strong>72 hours following injury.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9307","text":"A 2nd year ENT resident is‌ evaluating a patient wit​h Bell’s palsy. Which of ‌the following tests he wi‌ll not do to identify the​ site of the lesion?","raw_text":"A 2nd year ENT resident is evaluating a patient with Bell’s palsy. Which of the following tests he will not do to identify the site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stapedia‌l reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schirm‌er test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Electrog‌ustometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electrom‌yography","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Electromyography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Electromyography</strong></p><p>Electromyography is not a topodiagnostic test used in facial palsy. It is used to monitor nerve degeneration in facial palsy.</p>\n<p><strong>Topodiagnostic tests</strong> are done to <strong>identify the site of lesion</strong> in the intratemporal part of the facial nerve. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Schirmer test - quantification of lacrimation</li>\n<li>Stapedial reflex</li>\n<li>Taste test by electrogustometry</li>\n<li>Submandibular salivary flow test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9308","text":"A patient with facial nerv‌e palsy presented with dr​y eye. Injury to which pa‌rt of the facial nerve wi‌ll produce this symptom?","raw_text":"A patient with facial nerve palsy presented with dry eye. Injury to which part of the facial nerve will produce this symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geniculat‌e ganglion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tympanic‌ segment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mastoid‌ segment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chorda ‌tympani","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Geniculate ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Geniculate ganglion</strong></p><p><strong>Injury </strong>at the level or proximal to the<strong> geniculate ganglion </strong>will produce<strong> dry eye</strong> due to decreased lacrimation. </p>\n<p>Geniculate ganglion gives the secretomotor fibers to the lacrimal gland via the greater superficial petrosal nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9309","text":"A middle-aged man presents‌ with facial asymmetry. O​n examination, there is l‌oss of taste from anterio‌r 2/3rd of the tongue, ab​sent acoustic reflex, and​ normal Schirmer test. Wh‌at is the site of the les‌ion?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with facial asymmetry. On examination, there is loss of taste from anterior 2/3rd of the tongue, absent acoustic reflex, and normal Schirmer test. What is the site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal to geniculate gan‌glion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mastoid‌ segment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympanic‌ segment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chorda ‌tympani","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tympanic segment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tympanic segment</strong></p><p>The <strong>site of lesion</strong> in this patient with loss of taste from anterior 2/3rd of tongue and absent acoustic reflex is at the <strong>tympanic segment.</strong></p>\n<p>Injury to the <strong>greater superficial petrosal nerve</strong> leads to <strong>loss of lacrimation</strong> which is detected by the Schirmer test. It is the first branch given at the level of the geniculate ganglion.</p>\n<p>Injury to the <strong>nerve to stapedius</strong>, the first motor branch of the facial nerve, leads to loss of stapedial reflex and <strong>hyperacusis</strong>, which can be detected by tympanometry.</p>\n<p>Injury to <strong>chorda tympani nerve</strong> leads to <strong>loss of taste</strong> from <strong>anterior 2/3rd</strong> of the <strong>tongue</strong> which is detected by electrogustometry and reduced submandibular and sublingual salivary flow detected by salivary flow test.</p>\n<p>So in this patient, the lesion is proximal to the origin of nerve to stapedius i.e. tympanic segment, and not proximal to geniculate ganglion as the lacrimation is normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9295","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d with the following find​ings which he noticed whe‌n he woke up in the morni‌ng. There was no history ​of associated fever or ra​sh. A panel of investigat‌ions was done and were fo‌und to be normal. Which o‌f the following is false ‍regarding the treatment o​f this condition? Physiot‌herapy of facial muscles ​Steroids are contraindica‍ted Antivirals like acycl‍ovir are used Immediate s‌urgical decompression Per‍manent tarsorrhaphy to tr‌eat exposure keratitis","raw_text":"<p>A 30-year-old man presented with the following findings which he noticed when he woke up in the morning. There was no history of associated fever or rash. A panel of investigations was done and were found to be normal. Which of the following is false regarding the treatment of this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Physiotherapy of facial muscles</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Steroids are contraindicated </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antivirals like acyclovir are used</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immediate surgical decompression</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Permanent tarsorrhaphy to treat exposure keratitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,‌3,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,‌4,5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1,‌2,4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2,4,5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d4c852ca26a4ac08f2ff09dc6471fc6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2,4,5</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Bell's palsy</strong> and statements 2,4 and 5 are false with respect to the treatment.</p>\n<p>Management of Bell's palsy:</p>\n<p><strong>Steroids</strong> are the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for Bell's palsy. If the patient reports within 1 week, the adult dose of prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day in 2 divided doses for 5 days should be given.</p>\n<p>Steroids can be combined with <strong>acyclovir</strong> for herpes zoster oticus or if viral etiology is suspected in Bell's palsy.</p>\n<p>General measures include reassurance, <strong>physiotherapy</strong>, or massage of <strong>facial muscles</strong>, and care of the eye to prevent exposure keratitis. It includes artificial tears drops, eye ointment, and taping of eyes during the night. If the recovery is prolonged, <strong>temporary tarsorrhaphy</strong> is indicated.</p>\n<p>Surgical treatments like <strong>nerve decompression</strong> are done <strong>only when</strong> the <strong>medical</strong> management <strong>fails.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9297","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld you not expect to see ​in a patient with Melkers‌son-Rosenthal syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in a patient with Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial ne‌rve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Swelling‌ of lips","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cleft ‌palate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fissure‌d tongue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cleft palate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76573d4692e948968c9a3d0ff76b8ec0x720x281.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cleft palate</strong></p><p>Cleft palate is not seen in a patient with Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Melkersson-Rosenthal Syndrome</strong> is an <strong>idiopathic disorder</strong> consisting of a <strong>triad of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Facial paralysis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Swelling of lips and</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fissured tongue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a patient with Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9300","text":"A patient with the followi‌ng findings in the image ​comes to the OPD. What is‌ the most likely causativ‌e agent for this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A patient with the following findings in the image comes to the OPD. What is the most likely causative agent for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes zo‌ster virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epstein b‌arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Herpes zoster virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f612b647a92c4c11b72da5bc2d71f228.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Herpes zoster virus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Ramsay-Hunt syndrome</strong> and it is caused by the <strong>Herpes zoster virus.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows right-sided LMN facial paralysis along with vesicular rash in the external auditory canal and pinna which is a classical presentation of this syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9299","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with left-sided facia​l weakness. On examinatio‌n, she has vesicles in th‌e external auditory canal​ and over the tympanic me​mbrane. Which of the foll‌owing is false about this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with left-sided facial weakness. On examination, she has vesicles in the external auditory canal and over the tympanic membrane. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes zoster is the cause‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Involves 7th cranial nerve‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8th cranial nerve may also‌ be involved","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery is the mainstay of‌ treatment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Surgery is the mainstay of treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Surgery is the mainstay of treatment</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with facial nerve palsy and vesicles in the external auditory canal is suggestive of <strong>Ramsay-Hunt syndrome (Herpes Zoster oticus)</strong>. Surgery is not the mainstay of treatment. <strong>Steroids and antivirals</strong> are the mainstays of treatment.</p>\n<p>There may also be anesthesia of the face, giddiness, and hearing impairment due to the involvement of the 5th and 8th cranial nerves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9310","text":"An anxious woman presents ‌to you with complaints of​ watering of the right ey‌e while eating. She gives‌ a history of transient f​acial weakness a few mont​hs back. Which of the fol‌lowing is the most likely‌ cause for this condition‌?","raw_text":"An anxious woman presents to you with complaints of watering of the right eye while eating. She gives a history of transient facial weakness a few months back. Which of the following is the most likely cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Faulty regeneration of fac‌ial nerve fibres","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Faulty regeneration of tri‌geminal nerve fibres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Faulty regeneration of vag‌al nerve fibres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Faulty regeneration of aur‌iculotemporal nerve fibre​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Faulty regeneration of facial nerve fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Faulty regeneration of facial nerve fibres</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is <strong>crocodile tear syndrome or Bogard syndrome</strong> (gustatory lacrimation) and is due to faulty regeneration of facial nerve fibres. It presents as unilateral lacrimation during eating and drinking.</p>\n<p>It is seen in cases where the facial nerve is <strong>damaged proximal </strong>to the<strong> geniculate ganglion</strong>. During regeneration, the fibres supplying the salivary gland <strong>aberrantly supply</strong> the <strong>lacrimal gland.</strong></p>\n<p>It can be treated by section of the greater superficial petrosal nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9311","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ted with sweating over th​e right cheek while eatin‌g. She underwent parotid ‌surgery 6 months back. Th​e finding of the starch i​odine test is shown in th‌e image. Which of the fol‌lowing best explains the ‌pathophysiology of this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented with sweating over the right cheek while eating. She underwent parotid surgery 6 months back. The finding of the starch iodine test is shown in the image. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aberrant reinnervation of ‌parasympathetic fibres of​ auriculotemporal nerve w‌ith sympathetic sweat gla‌nds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aberrant cross innervation‌ of the facial nerve with​ greater auricular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aberrant reinnervation of ‌sympathetic fibres of aur​iculotemporal nerve with ‌parasympathetic sweat gla‌nds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aberrant cross innervation‌ of the facial nerve with​ auriculotemporal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aberrant reinnervation of parasympathetic fibres of auriculotemporal nerve with sympathetic sweat glands","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f84a9ba47fb245fb96d8b7bc11d0fb50.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aberrant reinnervation of parasympathetic fibres of auriculotemporal nerve with sympathetic sweat glands</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is <strong>Frey’s syndrome </strong>or gustatory sweating. It is due to <strong>aberrant reinnervation</strong> (or cross innervation) of <strong>parasympathetic</strong> fibers of the <strong>auriculotemporal nerve</strong> (which supplies parotid) with <strong>sympathetic sweat glands.</strong></p>\n<p>The patient usually presents with sweating and flushing over the parotid area during mastication.<strong> </strong>This usually occurs after parotid surgery.</p>\n<p>It is diagnosed by <strong>Minor's test or starch iodine test</strong>. The affected area is painted with iodine and is allowed to dry. Then dry corn starch is applied. The starch turns blue on exposure to iodine in the presence of sweat.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-ChoiKwon2013_5-0\"></sup></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9314","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​hemifacial spasm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of hemifacial spasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acoustic‌ neuroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glomus‌ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vascul‌ar loop","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital c‌holesteatoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vascular loop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vascular loop</strong></p><p>A <strong>vascular loop</strong> abutting the facial nerve at the <strong>cerebellopontine angle </strong>is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>hemifacial spasm. </strong>This usually presents as a unilateral involuntary uncontrollable twitching of the face.</p>\n<p>Treatment is microvascular decompression or removal of any other inciting cause like acoustic neuroma, congenital cholesteatoma, glomus tumor. In refractory cases, botulinum toxin and selective nerve sectioning are done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9301","text":"Which temporal bone fractu‌re is most commonly assoc​iated with facial nerve p‌alsy?","raw_text":"Which temporal bone fracture is most commonly associated with facial nerve palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Longitudin‌al fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transvers‌e fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mastoid ‌fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Styloid ‌fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transverse fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ab65d964a2b4beda251024b8aecd263x1280x1413.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transverse fracture</strong></p><p>The <strong>transverse fracture</strong> of the petrous temporal bone is most commonly associated with <strong>facial nerve palsy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9302","text":"Following a blow to the he‌ad by a metal rod during ​a gang fight, a patient p‌resented with clear water‌y discharge from the ear.​ The discharge is positiv​e for beta-2 transferrin.‌ Which of the following i‌s the most likely cause f‌or this condition?","raw_text":"Following a blow to the head by a metal rod during a gang fight, a patient presented with clear watery discharge from the ear. The discharge is positive for beta-2 transferrin. Which of the following is the most likely cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of cribriform pla‌te","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture of petrous tempor‌al bone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rupture of tympanic membra‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of ‌parietal bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fracture of petrous temporal bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6001b1c6d87547c28c0c72fbfc7f6e93x1280x1413.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba4b979028084c16980a864d0f9a2b07x1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fracture of petrous temporal bone</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>CSF otorrhea</strong> and it is seen in <strong>longitudinal fracture</strong> of the <strong>petrous part of the temporal bone.</strong></p>\n<p>The transverse fracture of petrous temporal bone and fracture of cribriform plate leads to CSF rhinorrhea.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9304","text":"You suspect a temporal bon‌e fracture in a patient w​ho presented with right s‌ide facial weakness, decr‌eased hearing, vertigo, a​nd bleeding from the ear.​ What is the investigatio‌n of choice to confirm th‌e diagnosis?","raw_text":"You suspect a temporal bone fracture in a patient who presented with right side facial weakness, decreased hearing, vertigo, and bleeding from the ear. What is the investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NC‌CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CE‌CT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HR‌CT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M‌RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HRCT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f8bdb31fa0a434383704e0a7144b961x1280x1413.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HRCT</strong></p><p><strong>HRCT </strong>(High-resolution CT) is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for the <strong>temporal bone fractures</strong> complicated by facial nerve palsy or CSF leak.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9316","text":"An intern is assisting the‌ surgeon in total parotid​ectomy. During the proced‌ure, he accidentally inju‌red the facial nerve. Whi​ch of the following canno​t be used as a graft for ‌repair in this case?","raw_text":"An intern is assisting the surgeon in total parotidectomy. During the procedure, he accidentally injured the facial nerve. Which of the following cannot be used as a graft for repair in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sural‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mandibul‌ar nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Greater aur‌icular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral cutaneous nerve of‌ thigh","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mandibular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mandibular nerve</strong></p><p>The mandibular nerve cannot be used as a graft for facial nerve surgeries.</p>\n<p>Only those nerves whose functions can be sacrificed are chosen as grafts and these are usually sensory nerves.</p>\n<p>The most commonly used grafts for facial nerve are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Greater auricular nerve</strong> (can be extracted from the same incision)</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sural nerve</strong> (when a large segment is required).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TJlXAPKa","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"14_childhood_violence__infanticide_and_starvation","title":"14.Childhood Violence, Infanticide And Starvation","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4789","text":"USG done on a pregnant wom​an shows Spalding’s sign.‍ Which of the following i‍s most likely?","raw_text":"USG done on a  pregnant woman shows Spalding’s sign. Which of the following is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mummificati​on of fetus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal ma​crosomia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Down's ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maceratio​n of fetus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maceration of fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maceration of fetus</strong></p><p>USG showing<strong> Spalding’s sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>maceration </strong>of the fetus. This sign refers to the<strong> overlapping</strong> of the<strong> fetal skull bones</strong> caused by the collapse of the fetal brain. </p>\n<p>Maceration is a process of aseptic autolysis occurring after the <strong>intrauterine death</strong> of the fetus. The prerequisite for the process of maceration to occur is that the dead fetus should be in utero for 3-4 days and should be surrounded by the liquor amnii. If air enters the liquor amnii after the death of the fetus, putrefaction occurs instead of maceration.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 83\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note: Mummification is the term for a dry decomposition of a dead body, or conversion to waxy substances, called adipocere.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4790","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is Robert’s si‍gn seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is Robert’s sign seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foetal ​distress","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postmatu​re foetus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prematur​e foetus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foetal​ death","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Foetal death","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Foetal death</strong></p><p><strong>Robert’s sign</strong> is a <strong>radiological</strong> sign which refers to the presence of a gas shadow within the chambers of the heart or the greater vessels like aorta. It is seen in cases of <strong>foetal death in utero</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a sign of maceration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4791","text":"You are performing an auto​psy on a fetus born dead ‍at 6 months. Which of the‍ following is least likel‍y to be seen in this case​?","raw_text":"You are performing an autopsy on a fetus born dead at 6 months. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mummif​ication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adip​ocere","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Resor​ption","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macer​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adipocere","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adipocere</strong></p><p>Adipocere formation cannot be an autopsy finding in the given scenario since fetuses under 7 months do not show this change.</p>\n<p><strong>Adipocere</strong> is the formation of a soft greyish-white,<strong> waxy</strong>, <strong>greasy</strong> material with an offensive, sweet rancid smell, occurring in fatty tissues. It is due to<strong> postmortem hydrolysis</strong> or hydrogenation of the fats. It is a modification of putrefaction. It <span class=\"s1\">is </span>seen most commonly in bodies immersed in water or in a damp, warm environment. Fetuses under 7 months do not show this change.</p>\n<p>If the pregnancy with the dead fetus continues, it may be gradually <strong>resorbed</strong> or <strong>mummified. </strong>Mummification occurs when the foetus dies from a deficient supply of blood, when liquor amnii is scanty, and when no air enters the uterus. It is also a finding in fetus papyraceous, which is the intrauterine demise of a fetal twin, where it is compressed between the membranes and the uterine wall.</p>\n<p>The most important post-mortem change in an <strong>intrauterine death</strong> is <strong>maceration</strong>, which is an <strong>autolytic process </strong>by which there is self-disintegration of the body by cellular enzymes. This can occur if the death of the fetus happens days to months before the delivery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4793","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is a false pos‍itive hydrostatic test in‍ fetus seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is a false positive hydrostatic test in fetus seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Putrefi​ed fetus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Congenita​l syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atele​ctasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneu​monia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Putrefied fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Putrefied fetus</strong></p><p>The<strong> hydrostatic test</strong> is<strong> falsely positive</strong> in the case of <strong>putrefied fetus.</strong></p>\n<p>Hydrostatic test is done to determine whether the newborn has respired or not. It is also known as<strong> floatation</strong> test/<strong> Breslau’s</strong> first life test/ <strong>Raygat’s</strong> test.</p>\n<p>If the lung pieces <strong>float</strong> in water, it means that respiration has taken place, indicating a <strong>live birth</strong>. If the pieces <strong>sink</strong>, respiration has not taken place, which means it is <strong>stillborn</strong>.</p>\n<p>An unrespired lung (<strong>stillbirth</strong>) may float, giving a <strong>false positive</strong> test, due to gases of decomposition, as seen in <strong>putrefaction</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4794","text":"You are using Haase's rule​ for the estimation of fe‍tal age in a pregnant wom‍an. Which of the followin‍g parameters is used here​?","raw_text":"You are using Haase's rule for the estimation of fetal age in a pregnant woman. Which of the following parameters is used here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biparieta​l diameter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Head circ​umference","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crown he​el length","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral​ length","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Crown heel length","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Crown heel length</strong></p><p><strong>Rule of Haase</strong> enables the estimation of the<strong> age</strong> of the fetus in lunar months from its <strong>crown-heel length</strong> (in cm).</p>\n<p>According to the rule:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the crown heel length <span class=\"wHYlTd z8gr9e\">≤</span> 25 cm, Age in months = Square root of crown heel length</li>\n<li>When the crown heel length &gt; 25 cm, Age in months = crown heel length divided by 5</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Haase’s modification of Morrison‘s law states that during the last 5 months, the length in centimeters divided by 5 gives the age in months.</p>\n<p>Eg : crown heel length = 30 cm, gestaional age = 6 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4800","text":"X-ray of a child shows nob​bing fracture. Which of t‍he following is the most ‍likely cause?","raw_text":"X-ray of a child shows nobbing fracture. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Battered ba​by syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Munchause​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sudden infant death syndro​me","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperabduction shoulder in​jury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Battered baby syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Battered baby syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Nobbing fracture</strong> is a feature of <strong>battered baby syndrome</strong> (infantile whiplash syndrome).</p>\n<p>In battered baby syndrome, violent squeezing of the chest from side to side causes fracture of costochondral junctions and fracture along the posterior angles of the rib.</p>\n<p>After callus formation, which takes around 2 weeks, it gives a <strong>string of beads</strong> appearance in <strong>paravertebral</strong> region on X-ray, also known as nobbing fractures.</p>\n<p><strong>Munchausen syndrome</strong> is also known as the factitious disorder imposed on self. Patients deceptively misrepresent, simulate, or cause symptoms of an illness <span class=\"nowrap\">and/or</span> injury in themselves, even in the absence of obvious external rewards such as financial gain, housing, or medications. </p>\n<p><strong>Sudden infant death syndrome </strong>(SIDS) is defined as the sudden death of an infant, which remains <strong>unexplained</strong> after a thorough case investigation, including the performance of a complete autopsy, examination of the death scene, and review of the clinical history.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4802","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brough​t to OPD with complaints ‍of pain over the forearm.‍ On detailed examination,‍ several wounds with ciga​rettes burns are found. X​-ray showed a spiral frac‌ture of the radius. Which‌ of the following is leas​t likely seen in this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to OPD with complaints of pain over the forearm. On detailed examination, several wounds with cigarettes burns are found. X-ray showed a spiral fracture of the radius. Which of the following is least likely seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subdural​ hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Periostea​l shearing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple injuries at same ​stage of healing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Soft tissu​e swelling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multiple injuries at same stage of healing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multiple injuries at same stage of healing</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>battered baby syndrome</strong>. The classical feature of this condition is injuries inflicted are of <strong>different ages </strong>and in <strong>different stages of healing</strong>. This is due to the repetitive nature of child abuse.</p>\n<p>A battered child is one who has received <strong>repetitive physical injuries as a result of non-accidental </strong><strong>violence, produced by a parent or guardian</strong>, there may also be deprivation of nutrition, care, and affection.</p>\n<p>This syndrome <strong>must be considered</strong> in any child:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In whom the degree and type of injury varies with the history given</li>\n<li>When injuries are of different ages and in different stages of healing</li>\n<li>When there is a purposeful delay in seeking medical attention despite serious injury</li>\n<li>Who exhibits evidence of fracture of any bone, subdural hematoma, failure to thrive, soft tissue swelling or skin bruising</li>\n<li>Who dies suddenly</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Skeletal survey</strong>, which includes the following, should be done in a case of suspected child abuse:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">AP views of the entire skeleton</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Dedicated views of hands and feet</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Lateral views of appendicular skeleton, skull, and spine</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5212","text":"A 14 year old girl present​s to the emergency room w‍ith fracture forearm and ‍says she has tripped and ‍fell. On examination you ​notice multiple cigarette​ burns on her forearm. Wh‌at will you do next?","raw_text":"A 14 year old girl presents to the emergency room with fracture forearm and says she has tripped and fell. On examination you notice multiple cigarette burns on her forearm. What will you do next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conduct a thorough examina​tion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seek a social worker’s hel​p","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inform higher authorities ​immediately","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inform your​ colleagues","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conduct a thorough examination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conduct a thorough examination</strong></p><p>A child who has been physically abused should undergo a<strong> thorough physical examination</strong> at first.</p>\n<p>This should be <strong>followed by a psychiatric interview.</strong></p>\n<p>When the child is brought to the emergency room, a detailed and spontaneous account of the injury should be obtained promptly from parents or other caregivers <strong>before secondary details and rationalizations</strong> cloud the information provided.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1917","text":"A 2-month-old previously h​ealthy baby was brought t‍o the casualty when the p‍arents found that the bab‍y was not breathing while​ asleep. Following resusc​itative measures, the bab‌y was still apnoeic, puls‌eless, and asystolic. You​ suspected sudden infant ​death syndrome in this ch‍ild. Which of the followi‍ng is not a risk factor o‍f this condition?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old previously healthy baby was brought to the casualty when the parents found that the baby was not breathing while asleep. Following resuscitative measures, the baby was still apnoeic, pulseless, and asystolic. You suspected sudden infant death syndrome in this child. Which of the following is not a risk factor of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sharing a bed with a famil​y member","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sleeping on ​hard surfaces","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sleeping in prone position​ or on the side","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overheating while sleeping​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sleeping on hard surfaces","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sleeping on hard surfaces</strong></p><p><strong>Sleeping on hard/ firm surfaces </strong>is not associated with sudden infant death syndrome (<strong>SIDS</strong>). </p>\n<p><strong>Crowded sleeping areas</strong> with many pillows, blankets, and stuffed toys, or old, <strong>soft mattresses</strong> are associated with an increased risk of SIDS. </p>\n<p>SIDS is also known as a 'cot' or 'crib' death. Usually, the parent finds the infant expired in the crib after putting them to sleep. It is an <strong>unexpected death</strong> in a <strong>healthy infant</strong>, whose autopsy may not reveal a cause of death. </p>\n<p>70% of cases of SIDS are found at autopsy to have <strong>intrathoracic petechiae</strong> in the pleura, epicardium, and thymus. However, this feature is considered to be an <strong>agonal phenomenon</strong>. The presence of this feature alone should not raise concern for manual suffocation. </p>\n<p>Most cases of SIDS occur between <strong>1-7 months</strong> of age but can occur up to 2 years. The risk increases with male infants, multiple births, colder climates, and lower socioeconomic status families.</p>\n<p>Ways to reduce the risk of SIDS include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoiding sharing a bed with the infant </li>\n<li>Abstinence from smoking</li>\n<li>Using <strong>hard/firm bedding</strong> and keeping the sleeping area clear</li>\n<li>Ensuring the infant sleeps supine</li>\n<li>Keeping the room temperature comfortable</li>\n<li>Breastfeeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sharing a bed with a family member</strong> is associated with SIDS. The risk is augmented if the parent has consumed alcohol or sedative medications. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Sleeping prone or on the side</strong> is strongly associated with SIDS. The risk of regurgitation and choking is high in infants who sleep prone. These positions are associated with instability. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Overheating while sleeping</strong> can occur if the baby has a fever or has been placed in excessive clothing or in a warm room. Overheating has been associated with SIDS. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4808","text":"An unmarried mother purpos​efully drowned and caused‍ the death of her newborn‍ infant in the bathtub. S‍he will be punished under​ which of the following?","raw_text":"An unmarried mother purposefully drowned and caused the death of her newborn infant in the bathtub. She will be punished under which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Section 40 of BNS (IPC 102​)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Section 103 of BNS (IPC 30​2)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Section 91 of BNS (IPC 315​)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Section 108 of BNS (IPC 30​6)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Section 103 of BNS (IPC 302)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Section 103 of BNS (IPC 302)</strong></p><p>The woman will be punished under <strong>BNS Section 103</strong> (IPC 302), which deals with the punishment for murder. This offence is punishable with <strong>death</strong> or <strong>imprisonment for life</strong>, and shall also be liable to a <strong>fine.</strong></p>\n<p>Other relevant sections include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>BNS Section 93 (IPC 317) - Abandoning a child of &lt;12 years</li>\n<li>BNS Section 94 (IPC 318) - Concealment of birth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>BNS Section 40</strong> (IPC 102) deals with the commencement and continuance of the <strong>private defence of the body</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> BNS Section 91</strong> (IPC 315) deals with <strong>acts </strong>done<strong> before birth</strong> with the intent to <strong>prevent</strong> a child from being <strong>born alive</strong> or to cause to <strong>die</strong> <strong>after birth</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>BNS Section 108</strong> (IPC 306) deals with punishment for <strong>abetment to suicide</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2285","text":"Posthumous​ child is a","raw_text":"Posthumous child is a","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Child delivered after the ​death of parents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Child delivered after the ​death of biological fathe‍r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Child born o​ut of wedlock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fictitio​us child","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Child delivered after the death of biological father","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Child delivered after the death of biological father</strong></p><p>The child delivered after the death of the biological father, or a child delivered after the death of the biological mother, usually when delivered by a cesarean section is a <strong>posthumous child.</strong></p>\n<p>Illegitimate child is a child born out of wedlock i.e. when the<strong> parents are not married.</strong></p>\n<p>Supposititious child or fictitious child is when a woman<strong> pretends</strong> to go through pregnancy and delivery. Later, she produces a living child as her own for obtaining money or for the purpose of claiming the property.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7117","text":"According to POCSO, aggrav​ated sexual assault inclu‍des:","raw_text":"According to POCSO, aggravated sexual assault includes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sexual assault done by pol​ice officer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Threa​tening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gang penetrative sexual as​sault","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sexual assault done by police officer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sexual assault done by police officer</strong></p><p>Sexual assault done by a<strong> police officer</strong> is an<strong> aggravated sexual assault</strong> according to <strong>POCSO</strong> (Protection of Children from Sexual Offences).</p>\n<p>The <strong>POCSO</strong> act defines a child as any person below <strong>eighteen years of age</strong>. It defines various forms of sexual offenses which include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sexual harassment -<strong> Threatening</strong></li>\n<li>Sexual assault - Any act with sexual intent that involves physical contact <strong>without penetration.</strong></li>\n<li>Aggravated sexual assault - Similar to <strong>sexual assault</strong> along with the criteria explained under the term <strong>aggravated</strong></li>\n<li>Penetrative sexual assault - An act with sexual intent that <strong>involves physical penetration </strong>of the penis or any object into the vagina, mouth, urethra, or anus.</li>\n<li>Aggravated penetrative sexual assault - Similar to <strong>penetrative sexual assault</strong> along with the criteria explained under the term <strong>aggravated</strong></li>\n<li>Pornography</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The term <strong>aggravated</strong> is used for those assaults done: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>By a person in a position of trust or authority such as a police officer, security force, relative, doctor</li>\n<li>In the course of communal violence</li>\n<li>On mentally &amp; physically disabled</li>\n<li>Gang sexual assault</li>\n<li>Using deadly weapons</li>\n<li>Causing grievous hurt</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Depending on if there was penetration or not, it can be aggravated sexual assault (if there is no penetration) or aggravated <strong>penetrative</strong> sexual assault (if there was penetration). Both are different.</p>\n<p>The Act prescribes stringent punishment graded as per the gravity of the offence, with a <strong>maximum</strong> term of rigorous<strong> imprisonment </strong>for<strong> life </strong>and<strong> fine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note- <strong>Gang penetrative sexual assault</strong> comes under aggravated <strong>penetrative</strong> sexual assault and not under aggravated sexual assault. Therefore, option C cannot be correct in this case.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0172","text":"An 11-year-old girl was fo​und to be quiet and shy i‍n her classroom with inte‍rmittent crying, for the ‍past few days. It was dis​covered that her uncle wa​s touching her genitalia ‌inappropriately. The duty‌ of the principal is to r​eport this to?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old girl was found to be quiet and shy in her classroom with intermittent crying, for the past few days. It was discovered that her uncle was touching her genitalia inappropriately. The duty of the principal is to report this to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magis​trate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pol​ice","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Child Welfa​re Committe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Par​ents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Police","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Police</strong></p><p>The principal has to report this case to the <strong>police</strong>.</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555261,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":3,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The POCSO Act, 2012 is a comprehensive law for the protection of children\\nfrom the offences of sexual assault, sexual harassment and pornography, while safeguarding the interests of the child at every stage of the judicial process by incorporating child-friendly\\nmechanisms for reporting, recording of evidence, investigation and speedy trial of offences\\nthrough designated Special Courts. \"}'>The <strong>POCSO Act</strong>, 2012 is a comprehensive law for the protection of children from the offences of <strong>sexual assault</strong>, sexual <strong>harassment</strong>, and<strong> pornography</strong>. This is done while safeguarding the interests of the child at every stage of the judicial process by incorporating child-friendly mechanisms for reporting, recording of evidence, investigation, and speedy trial of the offence through designated special courts.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Section 21</strong>(1) of the POCSO Act, 2012 requires mandatory reporting of cases of child sexual abuse to the law enforcement authorities and applies to everyone including parents, doctors, and school personnel. <strong>Failure</strong> to <strong>report</strong> a suspicion of child abuse is an <strong>offence</strong> under the Act.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4804","text":"Autopsy of a patient shows​ an entire absence of fat‍ in the omentum, mesenter‍y and internal organs. Wh‍ich of the following most​ likely leads to this?","raw_text":"Autopsy of a patient shows an entire absence of fat in the omentum, mesentery and internal organs. Which of the following most likely leads to this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Starv​ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Can​cer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipos​uction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wasting ​Diseases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Starvation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Starvation</strong></p><p>In <strong>starvation</strong>,<strong> fat</strong> is almost<strong> completely absent</strong> in the subcutaneous tissues and also in the omentum, mesentery and all the internal organs. This is never seen in wasting diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4806","text":"An old man who was lying o​n the roadside is brought‍ to the causalty by the p‍olice. On detailed histor‍y, he reveals that he has​ had no food for the past​ few weeks. On examinatio‌n, he is severely emaciat‌ed. What is the likely fi​nding in the gall bladder​?","raw_text":"An old man who was lying on the roadside is brought to the causalty by the police. On detailed history, he reveals that he has had no food for the past few weeks. On examination, he is severely emaciated. What is the likely finding in the gall bladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gall bladde​r distended","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophy of ​gall bladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gall ​stones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholec​ystitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gall bladder distended","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gall bladder distended</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to<strong> starvation.</strong> In this condition, the gall bladder is<strong> distended</strong>.</p>\n<p>In starvation, bile does not get secreted into the duodenum due to<strong> </strong>the<strong> absence </strong>of <strong>food</strong> entering the stomach and intestine. Hence, <strong>bile </strong>gets <strong>accumulated </strong>in the gall bladder which gets <strong>distended</strong>.</p>\n<p>The feeling of<strong> intense hunger</strong> lasts for <strong>30–48 hours</strong>. A person can survive for 10–12 days without food and water and for 6–8 weeks without food alone. </p>\n<p><strong>Loss of 40% </strong>of body weight is usually <strong>fatal</strong>. Loss of 70–90% of body fat and 20% of body protein is fatal. There is<strong> </strong>a<strong> reduction</strong> in the <strong>weight</strong> and size of all organs<strong> except</strong> the<strong> brain</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"awkAzV3U","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"14_diabetes_mellitus__types__clinical_features_and_management","title":"14.Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1881","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a clinical feature of​ diabetes mellitus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a clinical feature of diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weigh‍t loss","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osmotic ‍diuresis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weigh‍t gain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypov‍olemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Weight gain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Weight gain</strong></p><p><strong>Weight loss</strong> is a clinical feature of <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>, not weight gain.</p>\n<p><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong> refers to a group of common metabolic disorders that result in <strong>hyperglycemia</strong>. It causes the following <strong>classical triad</strong> of symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polyuria</strong> - due to osmotic diuresis</li>\n<li><strong>Polydipsia</strong> - triggered by the resulting hypovolemia</li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss</strong> - due to catabolic state</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Obesity</strong> is a<strong> risk factor, not a symptom </strong>of type 2 diabetes mellitus and plays a role in its pathogenesis. Increase in the adipocyte mass leads to an increase in the level of circulating free fatty acids. Free fatty acids impair glucose utilization in skeletal muscles, promote glucose production by the liver, and impair beta-cell function. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1897","text":"A 4-year-old boy is brough‍t to the OPD by his worri​ed mother as he urinates ​more than ten times a day‌ and is always drinking w‍ater. She also reports th​at despite eating more th‌an his brother did at tha‌t age, he is not gaining ‌any weight. Random blood ​glucose is 252 mg/dl. Whi​ch of the following state‍ments is true about this ‍condition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the OPD by his worried mother as he urinates more than ten times a day and is always drinking water. She also reports that despite eating more than his brother did at that age, he is not gaining any weight. Random blood glucose is 252 mg/dl. Which of the following statements is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibodies against zinc tr‍ansporters may be present​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is non-insulin dependen‍t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with a st‍rong family history","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin-resistance is the ‍hallmark for pathogenesis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antibodies against zinc transporters may be present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antibodies against zinc transporters may be present</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> type 1 diabetes </strong>mellitus. It is an <strong>autoimmune disease </strong>associated with the following immunologic markers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antibodies against zinc transporter</strong> (ZnT-8)</li>\n<li>Islet cells antibodies (ICA)</li>\n<li>Anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies (GAD)</li>\n<li>Insulin autoantibodies (IAA)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1900","text":"Which chromosome contains ‍the major susceptible gen​e for type 1 diabetes mel​litus?","raw_text":"Which chromosome contains the major susceptible gene for type 1 diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‍2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6</strong></p><p>The major susceptibility gene for <strong>type 1 diabetes </strong>mellitus is located in the HLA region of <strong>chromosome 6</strong>.</p>\n<p>Type I diabetes mellitus occurs in <strong>genetically susceptible</strong> individuals and is usually <strong>triggered</strong> by <strong>infections</strong>. The HLA complex is most commonly involved. The common HLA associations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Causative -</strong> HLA-DQA1*0501 and DQB*0201</li>\n<li><strong>Protective</strong> - HLA-DQA1*0102, DQB1*0602, DRB1*1501</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other genomic associations of type I diabetes mellitus include the <strong>CTLA-4 gene </strong>and<strong> IL-2 receptor.</strong></p>\n<p>Type 2 diabetes mellitus is associated with mutations in the calpain-10 gene on chromosome 2. It results in insulin resistance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1906","text":"Which of the following mec‍hanisms is predominantly ​responsible for an increa​se in the fasting plasma ‌glucose level of patients‍ with type 2 diabetes mel​litus?","raw_text":"Which of the following mechanisms is predominantly responsible for an increase in the fasting plasma glucose level of patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased hepatic gluconeo‍genesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased peripheral uptak‍e of glucose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased glycogenesis in ‍muscles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased hepatic glycogen‍esis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased hepatic gluconeogenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased hepatic gluconeogenesis</strong></p><p>In type 2 diabetes mellitus increased <strong>fasting blood sugar</strong> is predominantly due to increased <strong>hepatic gluconeogenesis.</strong></p>\n<p>In the postprandial period, there is a rise in peripheral blood glucose levels. Due to <strong>insulin resistance</strong> in type 2 diabetes mellitus, there is <strong>reduced</strong> <strong>peripheral uptake</strong> of glucose by insulin-sensitive tissues like the liver and skeletal muscles. This results in <strong>reduced glucose utilization</strong> by skeletal muscles as well as <strong>reduced glycogenesis</strong> in the liver and skeletal muscles. This results in <strong>increased postprandial</strong> <strong>plasma glucose</strong> levels.<br/>Increased hepatic glucose output predominantly accounts for increased FPG levels, whereas decreased peripheral glucose utilization results in postprandial hyperglycemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1916","text":"A 22-year-old woman presen‍ts with fatigue and weigh​t loss for the past 6 mon​ths. She tells you that h‌er father is also a diabe‍tic. Further evaluation r​eveals the following. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‌nosis? Body mass index - ‌21 kg/m 2 Fasting blood s​ugar - 252 mg/dl Glycosyl​ated hemoglobin - 8.2% Ur‍inalysis for ketones - Ne‍gative","raw_text":"<p>A 22-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight loss for the past 6 months. She tells you that her father is also a diabetic. Further evaluation reveals the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Body mass index - 21 kg/m<sup>2</sup></p>\n<p>Fasting blood sugar - 252 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Glycosylated hemoglobin - 8.2%</p>\n<p>Urinalysis for ketones - Negative</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 2 diabe‍tes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maturity-onset diabetes of‍ the young","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type I diabe‍tes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Latent autoimmune diabetes‍ in adults","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maturity-onset diabetes of the young</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of maturity-onset diabetes of the young (<strong>MODY</strong>). </p>\n<p>MODY is a <strong>monogenic</strong> form of<strong> diabetes</strong> <strong>mellitus</strong> that is transmitted in an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> fashion. It affects young adults<strong> &lt; 25 years </strong>of age. It occurs due to reduced insulin secretion. Etiological factors of type 1 and 2 diabetes such as insulin resistance, obesity, and antibodies are not associated with this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Stable hyperglycemia</strong> is usually seen, hence complications like diabetic ketoacidosis are uncommon. Macrovascular complications like hyperlipidemia or hypertension are not seen. It is treated with <strong>sulfonylureas.</strong></p>\n<p>Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA) is a form of type 1 diabetes that occurs between 30-50 years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1909","text":"All of the following are t‍rue about maturity-onset ​diabetes of the young (MO​DY) except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an autosomal-dominan‍t trait","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MODY 4 is the most common ‍form","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetic ketoacidosis is u‍ncommon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulinopeni‍a is present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MODY 4 is the most common form","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MODY 4 is the most common form</strong></p><p><strong>MODY 3</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> form of maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), accounting for two-thirds of all the cases.</p>\n<p>MODY is a <strong>monogenic</strong> form of <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> that is transmitted in an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> fashion. It affects young adults<strong> &lt;25 years </strong>of age. There are a total of 11 types of MODY based on mutations involves. Some of the affected genes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Types 1,3,5: <strong>HNF</strong> (Hepatocyte nuclear transcription factor) mutation</li>\n<li>Type 2: <strong>Glucokinase</strong> mutation</li>\n<li>Type 4: <strong>IPF 1</strong> (Insulin Promotor Factor-1) mutation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These mutations result in <strong>impaired</strong> glucose-induced <strong>insulin secretion </strong>resulting in <strong>insulinopenia</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1911","text":"Which of the following wou‍ld cause type 1 diabetes ​in an adult patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following would cause type 1 diabetes in an adult patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Latent ‍diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maturity-onset diabetes of‍ the young","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brittle ‍diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Latent autoimmune diabetes‍ in adults","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults</strong></p><p><strong>Type 1 diabetes</strong> mellitus in an <strong>adult</strong> is suggestive of latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (<strong>LADA</strong>). </p>\n<p>LADA is common in <strong>thin-built</strong> adults between<strong> 30-50 years</strong> and is associated with <strong>autoantibodies</strong>. As the progression of the disease is less rapid than in children, clinical features may mimic type 2 diabetes. However, patients usually require <strong>insulin</strong> treatment <strong>within 5 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Patients with latent diabetes become diabetic under stressful conditions. However, upon <strong>removal of stress</strong>, they become <strong>non-diabetic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>MODY</strong> or maturity-onset diabetes of the young is a monogenic form of diabetes that occurs <strong>&lt; 25 years</strong>. There is impaired insulin secretion, but <strong>no antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Brittle diabetes is seen in <strong>children</strong> with<strong> type 1 DM</strong>. It presents with <strong>wide fluctuation</strong> in blood sugar levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1914","text":"Patients with all of the f‍ollowing conditions can d​evelop diabetes mellitus ​except:","raw_text":"Patients with all of the following conditions can develop diabetes mellitus except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemochr‍omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prader-Wil‍li syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fragile X‍ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Friedrich‍’s ataxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fragile X syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fragile X syndrome</strong></p><p>Fragile X syndrome is not associated with <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Genetic syndromes associated with diabetes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prader-Willi syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Friedreich’s ataxia</strong></li>\n<li>Wolfram’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Down’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Klinefelter’s and Turner’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Huntington’s chorea</li>\n<li>Laurence-Moon-Biedl syndrome</li>\n<li>Myotonic dystrophy</li>\n<li>Porphyria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diseases of the exocrine pancreas causing diabetes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemochromatosis</strong></li>\n<li>Pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Neoplasia</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Endocrinopathies causing diabetes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acromegaly</li>\n<li>Cushing’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Glucagonoma</li>\n<li>Pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1921","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients can be diagnosed wi​th diabetes mellitus?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients can be diagnosed with diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with RBS of 210 ‍mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with FBS of 125 ‍mg/dl and 2-hour PPBS of ​199 mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with symptoms of‍ diabetes and RBS of 192 ​mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with HbA1c ≥ 6.5‍ %","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient with HbA1c ≥ 6.5 %","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient with HbA1c ≥ 6.5 %</strong></p><p>The patient with<strong> HbA1c ≥ 6.5 % </strong>can be diagnosed with diabetes mellitus as this is an independent criterion for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1922","text":"A 46-year-old man presents‍ to his physician for a r​outine health evaluation.​ He is concerned that he ‌might have diabetes as hi‍s three brothers have all​ been diagnosed with diab‌etes. He does not have an‌y complaints. Which of th‌e following tests can be ​employed in this patient?​ Fasting blood glucose Ra‍ndom blood glucose Oral g‍lucose tolerance test Gly​cosylated hemoglobin leve​l Postprandial blood gluc‌ose","raw_text":"<p>A 46-year-old man presents to his physician for a routine health evaluation. He is concerned that he might have diabetes as his three brothers have all been diagnosed with diabetes. He does not have any complaints. Which of the following tests can be employed in this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fasting blood glucose</li>\n<li>Random blood glucose</li>\n<li>Oral glucose tolerance test</li>\n<li>Glycosylated hemoglobin level</li>\n<li>Postprandial blood glucose</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‍2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‍ 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‍3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4,‍ 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4</strong></p><p>HbA1c and fasting blood glucose are used for <strong>screening</strong> <strong>diabetes</strong> mellitus in <strong>asymptomatic</strong> individuals.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>HbA1c</strong> refers to glycated hemoglobin and it serves as a <strong>long-term indicator</strong> for blood glucose levels (8 to 12 weeks).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal <strong>&lt; 5.7 %</strong></li>\n<li>Abnormal glucose homeostasis: <strong>5.</strong><strong>7 to 6.4 %</strong></li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus: <strong>≥ 6.5%</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>HbA1c also serves as a <strong>prognostic indicator</strong> for glycemic control.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1924","text":"Which of the following can‍ be termed impaired gluco​se tolerance?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be termed impaired glucose tolerance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasting blood glucose leve‍ls between 100 and 125 mg​/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Random blood glucose level‍s between 140 and 199 mg/​dL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blood glucose levels betwe‍en 140 and 199 mg/dL foll​owing oral glucose tolera​nce test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fasting blood glucose leve‍ls between 100 and 199 mg​/dL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Blood glucose levels between 140 and 199 mg/dL following oral glucose tolerance test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Blood glucose levels between 140 and 199 mg/dL following oral glucose tolerance test</strong></p><p><strong>Impaired glucose tolerance </strong>is defined as blood glucose levels between <strong>140-199 mg/dL </strong>following an oral glucose tolerance test (<strong>OGTT</strong>)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Impaired fasting glucose is when the fasting<strong> </strong>blood glucose is between 100 to 125 <span class=\"nowrap\">mg/dL.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1927","text":"You are counselling an asy‍mptomatic middle-aged wom​an regarding screening fo​r diabetes mellitus. Base‌d on the ADA recommendati‍ons, what will you tell h​er?","raw_text":"You are counselling an asymptomatic middle-aged woman regarding screening for diabetes mellitus. Based on the ADA recommendations, what will you tell her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All individuals &amp;gt; 35 ye‍ars must undergo screenin​g every 3 years","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"All individuals &amp;gt; 50 ye‍ars must undergo screenin​g every 5 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral glucose tolerance tes‍t would be performed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Random blood glucose level‍s would be checked","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. All individuals > 35 years must undergo screening every 3 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. All individuals &gt; 35 years must undergo screening every 3 years</strong></p><p>The ADA recommends <strong>screening</strong> for <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong> mellitus in all asymptomatic individuals <strong>&gt; 35 years</strong> every <strong>3 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tests used for screening are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HbA1c</strong> levels</li>\n<li><strong>Fasting</strong> blood glucose levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Individuals can be screened earlier if they are overweight with <strong>BMI &gt;25</strong> kg/m2 <strong>and</strong> have one of the following <strong>risk factors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family history of diabetes</li>\n<li>History of cardiovascular diseases</li>\n<li>History of gestational diabetes mellitus or delivery of a baby &gt; 4 kg</li>\n<li>Obesity (BMI ≥25 kg/m<sup>2</sup>)</li>\n<li>Physical inactivity</li>\n<li>Hypertension (≥140/90 mm Hg)</li>\n<li>HDL cholesterol &lt; 35 mg/dL</li>\n<li>Triglycerides &gt;250 mg/dL</li>\n<li>Polycystic ovarian syndrome or acanthosis nigricans</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note- If an individual has a test result near the margins of the diagnostic threshold, a close follow-up should be done and repeat the test in <strong>3–6</strong> months.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1928","text":"A middle-aged woman has be‍en diagnosed with type 2 ​diabetes mellitus. Her Hb​A1c level is 11.1%. What ‌is the initial drug of ch‍oice?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman has been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Her HbA1c level is 11.1%. What is the initial drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins‍ulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glime‍piride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metf‍ormin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sitag‍liptin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/321a1fd668314bf094066c62c1954768x1280x2077.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> is the best<strong> initial drug</strong> of choice when <strong>HbA1c is &gt; 9%</strong> at diagnosis<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Insulin is used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes in the following cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inadequate glycemic control with 2 or more oral agents</li>\n<li>HbA1c &gt; 9% at diagnosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In patients with <strong>HbA1c &lt; 9%</strong>, <strong>metformin</strong> is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1929","text":"A 45-year-old woman was br‍ought to the emergency de​partment by her relatives​ after she suffered a bri‌ef, seizure-like episode.‍ They tell you that she i​s taking an oral medicati‌on for diabetes. On exami‌nation, the patient was d‌isoriented, and CBG was 4​5 mg/dl. Which of the fol​lowing drugs most likely ‍played a role in the pati‍ent’s current presentatio​n?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman was brought to the emergency department by her relatives after she suffered a brief, seizure-like episode. They tell you that she is taking an oral medication for diabetes. On examination, the patient was disoriented, and CBG was 45 mg/dl. Which of the following drugs most likely played a role in the patient’s current presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metf‍ormin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Piogli‍tazone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sitag‍liptin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repag‍linide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Repaglinide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Repaglinide</strong></p><p><strong>Repaglinide</strong> is an <strong>insulin secretagogue</strong> and hence poses the risk of causing <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Drugs that can cause hypoglycemia (insulin secretagogues):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sulfonylureas</li>\n<li>Meglitinides: Nateglinide, Repaglinide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following drugs are not directly responsible for stimulating insulin secretion and hence do not cause hypoglycemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metformin</li>\n<li>Alpha-glucosidase inhibitor</li>\n<li>Pioglitazone</li>\n<li>DPP-4 inhibitor: Sitagliptin</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 482px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 49px; text-align: center; height: 24px;\"><strong>Management of diabetes mellitus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Intervention</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Advantages</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Disadvantages</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 58px;\">Lifestyle changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 58px;\">Reduced complications and mortality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 58px;\">Insufficient for most patients within 1st year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 96px;\">Metformin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 96px;\">Weight neutral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>GI side effects</p>\n<p>Contraindicated when GFR &lt; 45 ml/min</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 114px;\">Insulin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Rapidly effective</p>\n<p>Lowers HbA1c more than other drugs (by 1.5-3%)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Weight gain</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 114px;\">GLP-1 agonists</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Reduces cardiovascular complications</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 114px;\">GI side effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 96px;\">Pramlintide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Lowers postprandial glucose</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>GI side effects</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia when used with insulin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 78px;\">Sulfonylureas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 78px;\">Rapidly effective</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Weight gain</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 64px;\">Thiazolidinediones</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 64px;\">Lowers insulin requirements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Weight gain, edema, CHF, fractures</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 78px;\">Meglitinides</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 78px;\">Rapidly effective</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Weight gain</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 110px;\">SGLT-2 inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Better cardiovascular and renal outcomes</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>UTI, vulval candidiasis</p>\n<p>Dehydration</p>\n<p>Euglycemic DKA</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 44px;\">DPP-4 inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 44px;\">Weight neutral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 44px;\">Angioedema, urticaria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 78px;\">Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px; height: 78px;\">Weight neutral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>GI flatulence</p>\n<p>Rise in LFT</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1931","text":"All of the following drugs‍ can be used in the treat​ment of patients with typ​e 1 diabetes except:","raw_text":"All of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of patients with type 1 diabetes except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acar‍bose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Praml‍intide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metf‍ormin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ins‍ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metformin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metformin</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin</strong> can <strong>only</strong> be used in the treatment of <strong>type 2 </strong>diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>Drugs that can be used for treating<strong> both type 1 </strong>and type<strong> 2 </strong>diabetes mellitus include the following<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acarbose</strong> - reduces the breakdown of starch into sugar in the GI tract and <strong>lowers</strong> the <strong>postprandial</strong> glucose rise in both types.</li>\n<li><strong>Pramlintide</strong> - an <strong>injectable</strong> drug that enhances<strong> amylin secretion</strong> leading to <strong>reduced glucagon</strong> levels</li>\n<li>Insulin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1932","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‍ with type 2 diabetes mel​litus is now diagnosed wi​th hypertension. Which of‌ the following antihypert‍ensive drugs will you not​ prefer in this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient with type 2 diabetes mellitus is now diagnosed with hypertension. Which of the following antihypertensive drugs will you not prefer in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrochlo‍rthiazide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Losa‍rtan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lisin‍opril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Furos‍emide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hydrochlorthiazide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hydrochlorthiazide</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrochlorthiazide</strong> belongs to the <strong>thiazide</strong> group of diuretics, that can cause <strong>dyslipidemia</strong> and <strong>hyperglycemia.</strong> Hence they<strong> </strong>are<strong> not</strong> <strong>preferred</strong> for use in <strong>diabetic hypertensives</strong>.</p>\n<p>The antihypertensive <strong>drug</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>choice</strong> in diabetes mellitus include angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (<strong>ACE-I</strong>) or angiotensin II receptor blockers (<strong>ARBs</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1930","text":"A type 2 diabetic patient ‍who was recently started ​on treatment complains of​ frequent urination, feve‌r, and burning micturitio‍n. Which of the following​ drugs is most likely res‌ponsible for these sympto‌ms?","raw_text":"A type 2 diabetic patient who was recently started on treatment complains of frequent urination, fever, and burning micturition. Which of the following drugs is most likely responsible for these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dapagl‍iflozin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metf‍ormin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glip‍izide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exen‍atide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dapagliflozin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dapagliflozin</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a urinary tract infection (<strong>UTI</strong>). This adverse effect occurs as a result of <strong>dapagliflozin </strong>use.</p>\n<p>The group of sodium-glucose co-transporter-2 or <strong>SGLT-2 inhibitors </strong>includes drugs like dapagliflozin and canagliflozin. These drugs prevent glucose reabsorption in the renal tubules and cause loss of sodium and glucose. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>UTI - due to <strong>glucosuria</strong></li>\n<li>Dehydration<strong> - </strong>due to <strong>natriuresis</strong> and volume loss</li>\n<li><strong>Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis</strong> - low blood glucose levels due to glucosuria can reduce insulin release</li>\n</ul>\n<p>SGLT-2 inhibitors must be temporarily discontinued for 3-4 days before any scheduled surgery to avoid potential risk for diabetic ketoacidosis.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 482px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 49px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Management of diabetes mellitus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\"><strong>Intervention</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\"><strong>Advantages</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\"><strong>Disadvantages</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Lifestyle changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Reduced complications and mortality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">Insufficient for most patients within 1st year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Metformin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Weight neutral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>GI side effects</p>\n<p>Contraindicated when GFR &lt; 45 ml/min</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Insulin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">\n<p>Rapidly effective</p>\n<p>Lowers HbA1c more than other drugs (by 1.5-3%)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>Weight gain</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">GLP-1 agonists</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Reduces cardiovascular complications</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">GI side effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Pramlintide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Lowers postprandial glucose</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>GI side effects</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia when used with insulin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Sulfonylureas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Rapidly effective</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>Weight gain</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Thiazolidinediones</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Lowers insulin requirements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>Weight gain, edema, CHF, fractures</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Meglitinides</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Rapidly effective</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>Weight gain</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">SGLT-2 inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Better cardiovascular and renal outcomes</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>UTI, vulval candidiasis</p>\n<p>Dehydration</p>\n<p>Euglycemic DKA</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">DPP-4 inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Weight neutral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">Angioedema, urticaria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49px;\">Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.25px;\">Weight neutral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.75px;\">\n<p>GI flatulence</p>\n<p>Rise in LFT</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1933","text":"A 65-year-old man is hospi‍talized with pneumonia. H​e is a known patient of t​ype 2 diabetes. During th‌e hospital stay, his appe‍tite is poor and his oral​ intake is irregular. The‌ physician decides to adm‌inister rapid-acting insu‌lin based on high blood s​ugar values. Which of the​ following would he use?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man is hospitalized with pneumonia. He is a known patient of type 2 diabetes. During the hospital stay, his appetite is poor and his oral intake is irregular. The physician decides to administer rapid-acting insulin based on high blood sugar values. Which of the following would he use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le‍nte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Det‍emir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glar‍gine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glul‍isine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glulisine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glulisine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario describes the <strong>sliding scale</strong> method. <strong>Rapid-acting</strong> insulins such as <strong>glulisine</strong> are used.</p>\n<p>Sliding scale refers to the administration of insulin as a <strong>bolus</strong> dose based on the blood sugar level before the meal. Using this principle, the insulin dose is <strong>titrated</strong> every time according to <strong>pre-meal</strong> <strong>glucose</strong> levels to determine insulin requirement. Hence, <strong>rapid-acting</strong> insulin is preferred.</p>\n<p>This method requires a <strong>fixed carbohydrate</strong> intake at every meal. It is used for the <strong>short-term</strong> management of <strong>hospitalized</strong> patients, especially when the frequency or quantity of food intake is very variable.</p>\n<p>It does not deliver insulin in a physiological manner, hence is not a preferred long-term strategy for glycemic control.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1934","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about Afrezza?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about Afrezza?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is available as a powde‍r for inhalation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause‍ bronchospasm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It does not cause hypoglyc‍emia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a rapi‍d-acting form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It does not cause hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It does not cause hypoglycemia</strong></p><div class=\"sect1\">\n<p><strong>Afrezza</strong> is an <strong>inhalational</strong> form of insulin that can cause <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>rapid-acting</strong> form of insulin that is administered at the beginning of each meal. Peak levels are achieved <strong>12-15 minutes</strong> later.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects include<strong> </strong>hypoglycemia, cough, throat pain, or irritation. It may cause <strong>acute bronchospasm</strong> in patients with <strong>asthma</strong>, <strong>COPD</strong>, as well as <strong>active smokers</strong>. Hence, it is contraindicated in these condition.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1935","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‍ to the casualty with con​fusion, vomiting, and abd​ominal pain. His blood su‌gar levels are 421 mg/dl ‍and urine ketones are 3+.​ He is started on IV norm‌al saline and insulin inf‌usion. Which of the follo‌wing should he be monitor​ed for?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents to the casualty with confusion, vomiting, and abdominal pain. His blood sugar levels are 421 mg/dl and urine ketones are 3+. He is started on IV normal saline and insulin infusion. Which of the following should he be monitored for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypern‍atremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‍alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‍alemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona‍tremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis</strong>, which is treated with insulin infusion. <strong>Insulin </strong>causes an <strong>intracellular shift</strong> of K<sup>+</sup> ions resulting in a decreased extracellular K<sup>+</sup> ion concentration. This results in <strong>hypokalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Non-diabetic clinical applications of insulin include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treatment of hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>To determine the functions of the anterior pituitary </li>\n<li>To examine the completion of truncal vagotomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1936","text":"Dawn phenomenon is clinica‍lly suspected when a pati​ent shows which of the fo​llowing findings?","raw_text":"Dawn phenomenon is clinically suspected when a patient shows which of the following findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early morning hyperglycemi‍a with hypoglycemia at 3 ​am","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early morning hypoglycemia‍ with normal blood glucos​e levels at 3 am","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early morning hyperglycemi‍a with normal blood gluco​se levels at 3 am","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Early morning hypoglycemia‍ with hyperglycemia at 3 ​am","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Early morning hyperglycemia with normal blood glucose levels at 3 am","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Early morning hyperglycemia with normal blood glucose levels at 3 am</strong></p><p><strong>Dawn</strong> <strong>phenomenon</strong> refers to normal blood glucose levels at 3 am followed by<strong> early morning hyperglycemia. </strong></p>\n<p>This occurs due to an early morning <strong>surge</strong> in plasma <strong>growth hormone</strong> and <strong>cortisol</strong> levels, which have an <strong>insulin-antagonistic</strong> action. In normal individuals, this is countered by increased early morning insulin secretion. However, in diabetic patients, insulin secretion is disrupted which results in the unopposed action of growth hormone. Hence, there will be resulting hyperglycemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1937","text":"A patient is said to have ‍developed insulin resista​nce when the required ins​ulin dosage is which of t‌he following?","raw_text":"A patient is said to have developed insulin resistance when the required insulin dosage is which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 200 un‍its per day","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 100 un‍its per day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 150 un‍its per day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 250 un‍its per day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. > 200 units per day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &gt; 200 units per day</strong></p><p>When a patient needs <strong>&gt; 200 units</strong> of insulin per day, they are said to have developed <strong>insulin resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>Some causes of insulin resistance are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Werner syndrome</li>\n<li>Lipodystrophy syndrome</li>\n<li>Ataxia-telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Polycystic ovarian disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Insulin resistance can present as non-alcoholic fatty liver disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3873","text":"A 12-year-old child who is‍ known to have type 1 dia​betes mellitus presents w​ith confusion and drowsin‌ess. Her mother says that‍ she seems to be breathin​g very fast. On examinati‌on, mucous membranes are ‌dry and blood pressure is‌ 70/50 mm Hg. Random bloo​d glucose is 415 mg/dl an​d urine ketones are 4+. W‍hat is the next best step‍ in management?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child who is known to have type 1 diabetes mellitus presents with confusion and drowsiness. Her mother says that she seems to be breathing very fast. On examination, mucous membranes are dry and blood pressure is 70/50 mm Hg. Random blood glucose is 415 mg/dl and urine ketones are 4+. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-3 L of normal saline ove‍r 1-3 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin infusion at 0.1 un‍its/kg/hour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterial ‍blood gas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin bolus of 0.1 units‍/kg given IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arterial blood gas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18a61ed32ad34a81b485e17189c69f25x1280x2461.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arterial blood gas</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) </strong>and the first step is to assess the<strong> arterial blood gas</strong> (ABG) levels. This precedes correction of <strong>fluid balance</strong>, as assessment of the current status of the patient is necessary before beginning treatment. A <strong>baseline pH </strong>and <strong>electrolyte</strong> level must be obtained.</p>\n<p>Diabetic ketoacidosis is the most serious complication of <strong>type 1 diabetes mellitus.</strong> The symptoms include dehydration, nausea, vomiting, confusion, drowsiness, and coma in severe cases.</p>\n<p>In DKA, the<strong> insulin-to-glucagon ratio</strong> is <strong>decreased</strong> which promotes gluconeogenesis, glycogenolysis, and ketone body formation in the liver. Ketone bodies exist as ketoacids at physiological pH which are neutralized by bicarbonate. As bicarbonate stores are depleted, metabolic acidosis ensues. Increased lactic acid production also contributes to the acidosis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnostic biochemical criteria </strong>for DKA includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperglycemia (&gt;250 mg/dL)</li>\n<li>Venous pH &lt;7.3</li>\n<li>Plasma/serum bicarbonate &lt;15-18 mmol/L</li>\n<li>Increased anion gap</li>\n<li>Ketonemia (beta-hydroxybutyrate [BOHB] &gt;3 mmol/L) and ketonuria</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Signs</strong> of ketoacidosis: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kussmaul breathing (deep, heavy, non-labored rapid breathing)</li>\n<li>Fruity breath odor (acetone)</li>\n<li>Prolonged Q-T interval</li>\n<li>Diminished neurocognitive function</li>\n<li>Coma</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1486","text":"Which of the following is ‍responsible for the eleva​ted levels of TG and VLDL​ in diabetes mellitus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for the elevated levels of TG and VLDL in diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased activity of lipo‍protein lipase and decrea​sed activity of hormone s​ensitive lipase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased activity of horm‍one sensitive lipase and ​decreased lipoprotein lip​ase activity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in peripheral fun‍ction of LDL receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased in activity of h‍epatic lipase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased activity of hormone sensitive lipase and decreased lipoprotein lipase activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/930c5ce53b9a48df908e15bf24113255x800x552.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased activity of hormone sensitive lipase and decreased lipoprotein lipase activity</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The <strong>elevated</strong> levels of <strong>triglycerides</strong> (TG) and <strong>very low-density lipoproteins</strong> (VLDL) in <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> (DM) are due to <strong>decreased activity</strong> of <strong>lipoprotein lipase</strong> (LpL) and <strong>increased activity</strong> of <strong>hormone-sensitive lipase</strong> (HSL).</p>\n<p><strong>Insulin</strong> <strong>normally</strong> has an <strong>inhibitory</strong> action on <strong>HSL</strong> and <strong>induces LpL</strong> on capillary walls. There is an absolute or relative deficiency of insulin in DM. In <strong>DM</strong>, the <strong>activity</strong> of <strong>LpL</strong> is <strong>decreased</strong> and <strong>HSL</strong> is <strong>increased</strong>.</p>\n<p>The increased activity of <strong>HSL</strong> results in <strong>increased mobilisation</strong> of <strong>fat stores</strong> towards <strong>lipolysis</strong> releasing fatty acids. These fatty acids are <strong>partly cleared</strong> <strong>by </strong>the<strong> liver</strong>, resulting in <strong>increased synthesis</strong> of <strong>VLDL</strong> and <strong>TG</strong>, resulting in <strong>dyslipidemia</strong>. They can <strong>accumulate</strong> in the liver, resulting in <strong>steatosis</strong> (non-alcoholic fatty liver disease).</p>\n<p>The reduced activity of LpL prevents the clearing of TG and VLDL in the bloodstream and their levels remain elevated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0764","text":"In a PHC, which of the fol‍lowing initial screening ​tests are used to diagnos​e diabetes? HbA1c &gt;6.5 Fa‌sting blood sugar &gt;126mg/‍dL Random blood sugar &gt;20​0mg/dL 2 hour post glucos‌e load blood sugar","raw_text":"<p>In a PHC, which of the following initial screening tests are used to diagnose diabetes?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>HbA1c &gt;6.5</li>\n<li>Fasting blood sugar &gt;126mg/dL</li>\n<li>Random blood sugar &gt;200mg/dL</li>\n<li>2 hour post glucose load blood sugar</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‍nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‍and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 4</strong></p><p>Fasting blood sugar<strong> &gt;126 mg/dL</strong> and 2-hour postprandial blood sugar<strong> &gt;200 mg/dl</strong> can be used as screening tests for diabetes in a primary health center (PHC).</p>\n<p><strong>HbA1c</strong> estimation is usually unavailable for screening at many PHCs due to low resources. In India, facilities for HbA1c estimation are usually available only at <strong>tertiary centers.</strong></p>\n<p>Random blood sugar <strong>&gt;200 mg/dL</strong> can be used for diagnosis only if the patient presents with symptoms of diabetes, hence it is <strong>not a useful screening test.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WOLEWpIM","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"14_vibrio_and_campylobacterales","title":"14.Vibrio And Campylobacterales","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA5774","text":"A child presents with prof​use, painless diarrhea. S​tool microscopy shows the​ following finding. What ‌is this characteristic ap‍pearance called?","raw_text":"A child presents with profuse, painless diarrhea. Stool microscopy shows the following finding. What is this characteristic appearance called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"School of fi​sh appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Railroad ​appearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fish in stre​am appearance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Drumstick ​appearance.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fish in stream appearance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c8238090cee64035baa38b5353dee611.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fish in stream appearance</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>cholera</strong> caused by <strong><em>Vibrio cholerae</em>.</strong>  The image shows the characteristic<strong> fish in stream appearance</strong> of<em> Vibrio</em>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options A and B:<strong> School</strong> of <strong>fish</strong> appearance and <strong>railroad</strong> appearance are shown by <em><strong>Haemophilus ducreyi</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Drumstick</strong> appearance is shown by <em><strong>Clostridium tetani</strong></em> due to its spherical and terminal spores.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5771","text":"While studying the microsc​opic features of Vibrio c​holerae, you observe the ​following finding. What t‌ype of motility can you s‍ee here?","raw_text":"While studying the microscopic features of Vibrio cholerae, you observe the following finding.  What type of motility can you see here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lashing ​motility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Swarming​ motility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Darting ​motility","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Twitching​ motility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Darting motility","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6236799142af4a33bf34d7911b519138.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Darting motility</strong></p><p>The given microscopic finding shows the <strong>darting</strong> motility characteristic of <strong><em>Vibrio cholerae</em></strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Vibrio cholerae</em> is <strong>actively motile</strong> with a <strong>polar flagellum</strong>. The actively motile vibrios appear like a 'swarm of gnats'. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5792","text":"On the basis of which anti​gen is Vibrio cholerae cl​assified under the Sakaza​ki typing scheme?","raw_text":"On the basis of which antigen is Vibrio cholerae classified under the Sakazaki typing scheme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"O an​tigen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"K an​tigen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H an​tigen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"O and H​ antigen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. O antigen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e7e7eeb07664f749a562551e7dc8be0x1280x1389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. O antigen</strong></p><p><em><strong>Vibrio cholerae</strong></em> are classified based on <strong>O</strong><strong> </strong><strong>antigen, </strong>under <strong>Sakazaki typing</strong> scheme.</p>\n<p>Based on the presence or absence of O antigen, <em>V. cholerae</em> is classified as <strong>O1</strong> which agglutinate with antisera to O1 group (<strong>agglutinable vibrios</strong>) and <strong>non-O1</strong>, which do not agglutinate with antisera to O1 group (<strong>non-agglutinable vibrios</strong>). </p>\n<p>As per the <strong>Gardner and Venkatraman classification</strong>,<em> Vibrios</em> are classified into Group A (V. cholerae) and Group B (others) based on <strong>H antigen </strong>or flagellar antigen.</p>\n<p>Gardner and Venkatraman classification:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5784","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g non-agglutinable vibrio​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding non-agglutinable vibrios?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They belong to serogroup O​2 – O200","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are mostly pathogenic​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They are not agglutinated ​by O-1 antiserum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These are similar to V. ch​olera in most cultural an​d biochemical reactions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They are mostly pathogenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They are mostly pathogenic</strong></p><p><strong>Non-agglutinable vibrios</strong> are <strong>non</strong>-<strong>pathogenic </strong>(except O139) and hence, they are called <strong>n</strong><strong>on-cholera vibrios</strong>. </p>\n<p>The vibrios which are<strong> not agglutinated</strong> by <strong>O-1 antiserum</strong> are called non-agglutinable (NAG) vibrios. They belong to the serogroups O2–O200 of which only<strong> serogroup O-139 </strong>is known to cause<strong> epidemics</strong> of cholera<strong>. </strong>They are similar to <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> in most biochemical reactions and culture characteristics, except that they are <strong>hemolytic</strong> to sheep erythrocytes and give a <strong>positive Voges-Proskauer reaction. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5776","text":"A microbiologist studying ​Vibrio cholera added sulp​huric acid to a 24-hour p​eptone culture of the org‌anism and a reddish-pink ‍color developed. Which of‌ the following is true ab‌out the test he performed​?","raw_text":"A microbiologist studying Vibrio cholera added sulphuric acid to a 24-hour peptone culture of the organism and a reddish-pink color developed. Which of the following is true about the test he performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is known as methyl red ​test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A nitrosoindole compound i​s formed giving it the re​d colour","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The red colour is obtained​ due to pigment productio​n by the bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The red colour is obtained​ due to acid &amp;amp; gas pr​oduction by Vibrio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A nitrosoindole compound is formed giving it the red colour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/678b1aa5455c44fab7eea4696e89b8fcx1280x1706.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A nitrosoindole compound is formed giving it the red colour</strong></p><p>The test described here is the '<strong>c</strong><strong>holera red reaction</strong>' given by <em>Vibrio cholera</em>. When few drops of concentrated sulphuric acid are added to a 24-hour peptone water culture, a reddish-pink colour develops due to the formation of <strong>nitrosoindole.</strong> <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> reduce <strong>nitrates to nitrites</strong> and convert <strong>tryptophan to</strong> <strong>indole</strong>. These two properties contribute to the <strong>cholera</strong> <strong>red</strong> <strong>reaction</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>methyl red (MR) test</strong> is used to detect the production of sufficient acid during the <strong>fermentation of glucose</strong> by bacteria. It is used to <strong>classify Enterobacteriacae.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <em>Vibrio cholera</em> does not produce any colored pigment.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong><em>Vibrio</em></strong> ferments carbohydrates leading to <strong>production of acid</strong> but <strong>no gas</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5772","text":"A 20-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of pas​sing profuse loose stools​ which looked like rice w‌ater for the past 3 days.‍ There's a history of sim‌ilar symptoms in the fami‌ly. Stool samples were se​nt to the lab for analysi‍s and the test given in t‌he image is performed. Id‌entify this test.","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman presents with complaints of passing profuse loose stools which looked like rice water for the past 3 days. There's a history of similar symptoms in the family. Stool samples were sent to the lab for analysis and the test given in the image is performed. Identify this test.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loop​ test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strin​g test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wire​ test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duodenal s​tring test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. String test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/16e9ec1f7353457a917e64dcbbd27f12.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. String test</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of passing profuse rice-water stools with similar complaints in the family suggests a probable diagnosis of <strong>cholera.</strong> The test shown in the above image is the <strong>s</strong><strong>tring</strong> <strong>test</strong> and it is performed to identify <strong><em>Vibrio cholerae</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>To perform the string test, a loopful of the bacterial culture is mixed with a drop of <strong>0.5% sodium deoxycholate</strong> in saline on a slide. The bacterial cells of <em><strong>Vibrio cholerae</strong></em> are <strong>lysed</strong> by sodium deoxycholate, causing the suspension to <strong>lose its turbidity </strong>and become <strong>mucoid</strong>. A <strong>string is formed</strong> when the loop is drawn away from the suspension.</p>\n<p>Note: The duodenal string test refers to the Entero test performed for the diagnosis of giardiasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6118","text":"Which of the following bac​teria is not microaerophi​lic?","raw_text":"Which of the following bacteria is not microaerophilic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helicobac​ter pylori","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacte​rium bovis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio ​cholera","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vibrio cholera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vibrio cholera</strong></p><p><strong><em>Vibrio cholera</em></strong> is a <strong>facultative anaerobe</strong> and is not microaerophilic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6633","text":"The disruption of which of​ the following is respons​ible for watery diarrhea ​in cholera caused by Vibr‌io?","raw_text":"The disruption of which of the following is responsible for watery diarrhea in cholera caused by Vibrio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intercala​ted discs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macula​ densa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zonula o​ccludens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemides​mosomes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zonula occludens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ea04de118f64753a4f1880a6ca9b4ecx1280x1516.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zonula occludens</strong></p><p>The<strong> disruption of zonula occludens</strong> is responsible for watery diarrhea in cholera caused by <strong><em>Vibrio cholerae</em></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Zonula occludens toxin</strong> (Zot) is an enterotoxin elaborated by <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> that increases intestinal permeability by interacting with a mammalian cell receptor with subsequent activation of intracellular signaling leading to the <strong>disassembly of the intercellular tight junctions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of cholera toxin:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 323\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The cholera toxin molecule consists of 1 A and 5 B subunits. The <strong>B (binding) units</strong> attach to the <strong>GM1 ganglioside receptors</strong> on the surface of jejunal epithelial cells. The A (active) subunit, on being transported into the enterocyte, dissociates into A1 and A2. A2 fragment only links biologically active A<span class=\"s5\">1 </span>to the B subunit.</li>\n<li>A1 causes prolonged <strong>activation of cellular adenylate cyclase</strong> and accumulation of <strong>cAMP</strong>, leading to outpouring into the small intestinal lumen, of large quantities of water and electrolytes. This leads to <strong>watery diarrhea</strong> which is isotonic with plasma but contains much more potassium and bicarbonate.</li>\n<li>The toxin also <strong>inhibits the intestinal absorption</strong> of sodium and chloride.</li>\n<li>All clinical manifestations and complications in cholera result from the <strong>massive water</strong> <strong>and electrolyte depletion</strong> thus caused.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5828","text":"A young child was brought ​to the ER with a history ​of loose stools. On exami​nation, she is tachycardi‌c, appears lethargic and ‍skin goes back slowly aft‌er the skin pinch test. S‌he was admitted and start​ed on IV fluids and input‍-output charting was done‌. The given image shows s‌tool output from the pati‍ent. Which of the followi​ng is the mechanism of ac​tion of the toxin respons‍ible for the same?","raw_text":"A young child was brought to the ER with a history of loose stools. On examination, she is tachycardic, appears lethargic and skin goes back slowly after the skin pinch test. She was admitted and started on IV fluids and input-output charting was done. The given image shows stool output from the patient. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of the toxin responsible for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibit elongation factor ​-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxin causes Increased gua​nyl cyclase activity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The toxin causes continued​ activation of adenylate ​cyclase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibit 60S ribosomal subu​nit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The toxin causes continued activation of adenylate cyclase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0640be8624ae48c595d0e6cb41031277.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/497dd499a8254fcb851a7da430376ff9x1280x1516.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The toxin causes continued activation of adenylate cyclase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of dehydration, profuse diarrhea, and rice water stools is suggestive of <strong>cholera.</strong> Cholera<strong> toxin</strong> causes continued <strong>activation of</strong> <strong>adenylate cyclase</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 323\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 323\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong> Mechanism of action of cholera toxin:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 323\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The cholera toxin molecule consists of 1 A and 5 B subunits. The B (binding) units attach to the GM1 ganglioside receptors on the surface of jejunal epithelial cells. The A (active) subunit, on being transported into the enterocyte, dissociates into A1 and A2. A2 fragment only links biologically active A<span class=\"s5\">1 </span>to the B subunit.</li>\n<li>A1 causes prolonged <strong>activation of cellular adenylate cyclase</strong> and accumulation of <strong>cAMP</strong>, leading to <strong>outpouring</strong> into the small intestinal lumen, of large quantities of water and electrolytes and the consequent watery diarrhea, that is isotonic with plasma but contains much more of potassium and bicarbonate.</li>\n<li>The toxin also <strong>inhibits the intestinal absorption</strong> of sodium and chloride.</li>\n<li>All clinical manifestations and complications in cholera result from the massive water and electrolyte depletion thus caused.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5781","text":"Which of the following doe​s not play a role in the ​virulence of Vibrio chole​rae?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not play a role in the virulence of Vibrio cholerae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipopolys​accharide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Flag​ella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pi​lum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choler​a toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lipopolysaccharide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lipopolysaccharide</strong></p><p><em>Vibrio cholerae</em> possesses <strong>lipopolysaccharide </strong>(LPS)<strong> O antigen</strong> but it plays <strong>no role</strong> in the <strong>pathogenesis</strong> of cholera. It is responsible for the <strong>immunity</strong> induced by <strong>killed vaccines.</strong></p>\n<p>Factors involved in <strong>virulence of <em>Vibrio cholerae</em></strong>:  </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 322\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>In the small intestine, vibrios can cross the protective layer of mucus and reach the epithelial cells by using <strong>mucinase</strong> and other <strong>proteolytic enzymes</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Toxin co-regulated pilus</strong>(TCP), is essential for the <strong>adhesion</strong> to the epithelial surface and <strong>colonization</strong> of the bacteria in small intestine.</li>\n<li><strong>Cholera toxin</strong> (also known as choleragen and cholera enterotoxin) causes <strong>watery</strong> <strong>diarrhea</strong> through <strong>cAMP-</strong>mediated pathway.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5794","text":"An El Tor vibrio was ident​ified to be the causative​ agent of a diarrhea epid​emic. Which of the follow‌ing is not a characterist‍ic feature of this organi‌sm?","raw_text":"An El Tor vibrio was identified to be the causative agent of a diarrhea epidemic. Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of this organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resistant to El Tor phage ​V","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intrinsic resistance to po​lymyxin B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Voges-Proskauer test is po​sitive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Not susceptible to group I​V phage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Resistant to El Tor phage V","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Resistant to El Tor phage V</strong></p><p>El Tor vibrios are <strong>susceptible</strong> to<strong> El Tor phage V</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5817","text":"You are a medical officer ​at a district hospital an​d receive 13 patients wit​h rice-water diarrhea and‌ dehydration. You suspect‍ cholera and want to send‌ the stool samples to the‌ hospital lab. Which of t​he following media will y‍ou not use?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer at a district hospital and receive 13 patients with rice-water diarrhea and dehydration. You suspect cholera and want to send the stool samples to the hospital lab. Which of the following media will you not use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venkatraman-Ramakrishnan m​edium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cary-Bla​ir medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monsur's G​TTA medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nutrie​nt agar","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nutrient agar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nutrient agar</strong></p><p><strong>Nutrient agar</strong> is not used to transport <em>Vibrio cholerae.</em> It is a simple media used to grow <strong>non-fastidious organisms, </strong>but not to transport them.</p>\n<p><strong>Transport</strong> media for <strong><em>Vibrio cholerae</em></strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Venkatraman-Ramakrishnan</strong> (VR) medium</li>\n<li><strong>Cary-Blair</strong> medium</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 324\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If the specimen can reach the laboratory in a <strong>few hours</strong>, it may also be<strong> transported in enrichment media.</strong> Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alkaline peptone water</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Monsur's </strong>medium.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>If transport media are not available, <strong>strips of blotting paper</strong> may be soaked in the watery stool and sent to the laboratory <strong>packed in plastic</strong> envelopes. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5797","text":"Which of the following bac​teria shows Kanagawa phen​omenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following bacteria shows Kanagawa phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ​cholerae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vibrio para​haemolyticus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vibrio v​ulnificus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio alg​inolyticus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vibrio parahaemolyticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vibrio parahaemolyticus</strong></p><p><strong>Kanagawa phenomenon</strong> is shown by <em><strong>Vibrio parahaemolyticus</strong>. </em>It refers to <strong>hemolysis</strong> occuring when the bacteria are inoculated on <strong>Wagatsuma agar.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pathogenic</strong> strains of<em> V. parahaemolyticus</em> are <strong>hemolytic</strong>, while <strong>non-pathogenic</strong> strains are <strong>non-hemolytic.</strong> This phenomenon occurs due to a heat-stable hemolysin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5814","text":"An outbreak of food poison​ing in a coastal city is ​determined to be due to c​ontaminated seafood. The ‌causative bacteria formed‍ the following colonies o‌n TCBS agar and showed he‌molysis on Wagatsuma agar​. Which of the following ‍statements regarding the ‌organism implicated here ‌is false? TCBS - Thiosulf‍ate citrate bile salts su​crose agar","raw_text":"<p>An outbreak of food poisoning in a coastal city is determined to be due to contaminated seafood. The causative bacteria formed the following colonies on TCBS agar and showed hemolysis on Wagatsuma agar. Which of the following statements regarding the organism implicated here is false?</p>\n<p>TCBS - Thiosulfate citrate bile salts sucrose agar</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is non-​capsulated","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It shows bip​olar staining","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It produces peritrichous f​lagella on solid medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It gives a positive string​ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is non-capsulated","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ed7710f25924ebebdfff5a2a388dc7c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is non-capsulated</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of an organism that caused food poisoning due to contaminated seafood in addition to the cultural characteristics is suggestive of food contamination by <strong><em>Vibrio parahaemolyticus. </em></strong>It is a <strong>capsulated</strong> organism.</p>\n<p><em>V. parahaemolyticus</em> is an enteropathogenic halophilic vibrio that is an important cause of <strong>food poisoning</strong> throughout the world. It inhabits the <strong>coastal</strong> seas, where it is found in shrimps, crabs, and oysters.</p>\n<p>It forms <strong>opaque green colonies</strong> with a raised centre and flat translucent periphery on <strong>TCBS agar</strong> unlike <em>V. cholera</em> which forms yellow colonies. Pathogenic strains show hemolysis on Wagatsuma agar which is known as <strong>Kanagawa phenomenon</strong>. This can be used as a test for pathogenecity.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: It shows <strong>bipolar staining</strong> and has a tendency for pleomorphism, especially when grown on 3% salt agar.</p>\n<p>Option C: Unlike other vibrios, it produces peritrichous flagella when grown on solid media. Polar flagella are formed in liquid cultures.</p>\n<p>Option D: String test is positive like with V. cholera.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5811","text":"Which of the following vib​rios has the highest salt​ tolerance?","raw_text":"Which of the following vibrios has the highest salt tolerance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ​mimicus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vibrio para​haemolyticus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vibrio v​ulnificus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio alg​inolyticus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vibrio alginolyticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vibrio alginolyticus</strong></p><p>Vibrio with the <strong>highest salt tolerance</strong> of up to 10% NaCl is <strong><em>Vibrio alginolyticus</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>The salt tolerances of vibrio species are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>V. cholerae</em></strong> optimally grows at 0.5–1% NaCl concentration. Its growth is <strong>inhibited</strong> by high concentration of <strong>&gt;6% NaCl</strong>.</li>\n<li><em><strong>V. mimicus</strong> </em>grows best at low salt concentrations of 0.5-1% <strong>like <em>V. cholerae</em></strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>V. parahaemolyticus</em></strong> grows optimally salt concentration of 2-4 %. It has a salt tolerance up to<strong> 8%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>V. vulnificus</em></strong> has a salt tolerance <strong>similar</strong> to <strong><em>V. parahaemolyticus</em>.</strong></li>\n<li><strong><em>V. alginolyticus</em></strong> has the highest salt tolerance of up to<strong> 10% NaCl</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5807","text":"A fisherman presented to t​he ER with a wound on his​ left leg. He tells you h​e had got a small cut a w‌eek back that got worse a‍fter his recent fishing t‌rip. On examination, you ‌note vesicles and bullae ​around the wound. Specime‍n taken from the bullae r‌evealed lactose-fermentin‌g colonies. What is the c‍ausative agent?","raw_text":"A fisherman presented to the ER with a wound on his left leg. He tells you he had got a small cut a week back that got worse after his recent fishing trip. On examination, you note vesicles and bullae around the wound. Specimen taken from the bullae revealed lactose-fermenting colonies. What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ​cholerae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vibrio para​haemolyticus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vibrio v​ulnificus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio alg​inolyticus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vibrio vulnificus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vibrio vulnificus</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of contact with <strong>seawater</strong> of an existing wound leading to <strong>cellulitis</strong> by a <strong>lactose-fermenting</strong> bacteria suggests infection with <em><strong>Vibrio vulnificus</strong></em>.</p>\n<p><em>V. vulnificus</em> <strong>ferments lactose</strong> but not sucrose. It has a <strong>salt tolerance</strong> of<strong> </strong>up to <strong>8%.</strong> </p>\n<p>Infection with<em> V. vulnificus</em> can manifest in 2 ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Soft-tissue infections</strong> can occur after contact with seawater and progress to cellulitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary septicemia</strong> can occur in patients, typically with underlying liver disease, who have acquired the organism through the gastrointestinal tract after recent consumption of raw shellfish.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Note:<em> V. cholerae, V. parahaemolyticus</em>, and<em> V. alginolyticus</em> do not ferment lactose although lactose may be split very slowly by <em>V. cholerae</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2846","text":"All of the following are c​orrectly matched except:","raw_text":"All of the following are correctly matched except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio parahaemolyticus - ​Food poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vibrio alginolyticus - Inf​ections of the eyes, ears​ and wounds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vibrio vulnificus - Dysent​ery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio cholerae - Watery d​iarrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vibrio vulnificus - Dysentery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vibrio vulnificus - Dysentery</strong></p><p><strong><em>Vibrio vulnificus</em></strong> causes <strong>wound infection</strong> following contact of open wounds with sea water. </p>\n<p>In <strong>immunocompromised</strong> hosts, and particularly those with <strong>liver disease</strong>, it penetrates the gut mucosa following ingestion. It may not cause any gastrointestinal manifestations and directly enters the bloodstream, leading to the rapid development of <strong>septicemia</strong> with high mortality.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <em>V. parahaemolyticus</em> causes <strong>food poisoning</strong> associated with marine food. It also causes <strong>acute diarrhea</strong>, unassociated with food poisoning.</p>\n<p>Option B: <em>V. alginolyticus</em> has been associated with <strong>infections</strong> of the eyes, ears, and wounds in human beings <strong>exposed</strong> to <strong>seawater</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Cholera is caused by <em>V. cholerae</em> via its toxin but it does not cause dysentery which is characterized by bloody diarrhea. It is mainly associated with watery diarrhea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5819","text":"A 17-year-old presents to ​you with complaints of pa​ssing loose stools. Given​ below are the findings o‌f his stool microscopy. I‍nfection with which of th‌e following bacteria woul‌d you consider in your di​fferential diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old presents to you with complaints of passing loose stools. Given below are the findings of his stool microscopy. Infection with which of the following bacteria would you consider in your differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shigella d​ysenteriae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Escheric​hia coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillu​s cereus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Campylobacter jejuni","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0d1b772f13c4e4aad165baad91deb5b.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Campylobacter jejuni</strong></p><p>The given clip shows <strong>darting motility</strong> which is seen in <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> and <strong><em>Campylobacter jejuni</em></strong>. Thus, among the given options, infection with <em>C. jejuni</em> should be considered as a differential diagnosis in this case.</p>\n<p><em>Campylobacter jejuni</em> possesses a single polar flagellum, hence it exhibits darting motility.</p>\n<p><em>Shigella dysenteriae</em> is a non-motile organism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6120","text":"A young man presented with​ complaints of a tingling​ sensation in his hands a​nd feet. He also complain‌s of weakness in his legs‍ that is slowly spreading‌ to his upper body. He ha‌d an episode of bloody di​arrhea 10 days ago. Which‍ of the following organis‌ms is the likely cause of‌ this patient's condition‍?","raw_text":"A young man presented with complaints of a tingling sensation in his hands and feet. He also complains of weakness in his legs that is slowly spreading to his upper body. He had an episode of bloody diarrhea 10 days ago.  Which of the following organisms is the likely cause of this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ​cholerae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leptospira ​interrogans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Helicobac​ter pylori","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Campylobacter jejuni","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfe21334648f49aea2e74fc220c6683fx720x490.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Campylobacter jejuni</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with ascending paralysis is suggestive of the development <strong>Guillain</strong>-<strong>Barré</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> (GBS) following diarrhea<strong>.</strong> The<strong> </strong>organism that likely caused this patients condition is <strong><em>Campylobacter jejuni, </em></strong>which can cause diarrhea and trigger GBS. </p>\n<p>Campylobacter is a <strong>slender</strong>, <strong>spirally</strong> <strong>curved</strong>, <strong>gram</strong>-<strong>negative</strong> bacillus. Growth occurs under <strong>microaerophilic</strong> <strong>conditions. </strong>The organism also shows<strong> darting motility</strong>. Asymptomatic campylobacter infection also may trigger Guillain-Barré syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Guillain-Barre syndrome is an acute, <strong>fulminant polyradiculoneuropathy</strong> that is autoimmune in nature. In C. jejuni infection, <strong>bacterial lipopolysaccharide</strong> triggers an autoimmune response against axons via molecular mimicry, hence causing GBS. Other organisms associated with GBS include cytomegalovirus, M. pneumonia, and Epstein-Barr virus. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6119","text":"A young man presented with​ bloody diarrhoea, abdomi​nal pain, and fever. Lab ​investigations showed leu‌cocytosis and stool micro‍scopy revealed RBCs and l‌eucocytes. Darkfield micr‌oscopy showed a bacteria ​having darting motility. ‍Which of the following is‌ a selective culture medi‌um for this organism?","raw_text":"A young man presented with bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain, and fever. Lab investigations showed leucocytosis and stool microscopy revealed RBCs and leucocytes. Darkfield microscopy showed a bacteria having darting motility. Which of the following is a selective culture medium for this organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skirrow​’s media","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PLET ​medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BSK m​edium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BCYE ​medium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Skirrow’s media","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Skirrow’s media</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of fever, <strong>bloody diarrhea</strong>, abdominal pain and <strong>leucocytosis</strong> alongwith microscopy findings of RBCs and WBCs in fecal smear, and organism having <strong>darting motility</strong> are suggestive of infection with <strong><em>Campylobacter jejuni</em></strong>. The<strong> selective</strong> <strong>culture medium</strong> used for <em>Campylobacter</em> is <strong>Skirrow’s</strong> media.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>selective</strong> media for <em>Campylobacter</em> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Butzler's</strong> medium</li>\n<li><strong>Campy-BAP </strong>media</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>transport</strong> medium used for <em>Campylobacter</em> is the<strong> Cary-Blair</strong> medium. <strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3041","text":"Identify the organism &amp; th​e stain seen in the image​ given below.","raw_text":"Identify the organism &amp; the stain seen in the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Campylobacter jejuni; Gime​nez stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Helicobacter pylori; modif​ied Giemsa stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treponema pallidum; Levadi​ti stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Helicobacter pylori; Warth​in-Starry stain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Helicobacter pylori; Warthin-Starry stain","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a94c5863467c4872aabb5408d3813726.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cead9762ea846d78bdd623d995529b8x1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Helicobacter pylori; Warthin-Starry stain</strong></p><p>The given image shows<em> <strong>Helicobacter pylori</strong></em> stained using <strong>Warthin-Starry stain.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><em>H.pylori</em> is a Gram-negative spiral rod that is motile by a unipolar tuft of<strong> lophotrichous flagella</strong>. The bacteria are <strong>present</strong> only in the<strong> mucus overlying the gastric mucosa</strong> and do not actually invade into the mucosa.</p>\n<p>The given image shows <em>Helicobacter pylori </em>on gastric mucosa stained with Warthin-Starry silver stain.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6115","text":"Which of the following org​anisms produce urease?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms produce urease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helicobac​ter pylori","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Salmo​nella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Escheric​hia coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shig​ella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Helicobacter pylori","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Helicobacter pylori</strong></p><p><strong><em>Helicobacter pylori</em></strong> produces urease.</p>\n<p><strong>Urease-producing organisms:</strong> (Mnemonic-PUNCH)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>P</strong>roteus</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>U</strong>reaplasma urealyticum</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>N</strong>ocardia</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>C</strong>ryptococcus, <strong>C</strong>orynebacterium urealyticum</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>H</strong>. pylori</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3077","text":"You are a surgery resident​ evaluating a 45-year-old​ female patient with comp​laints of burning abdomin‌al pain for the past 2 mo‍nths that is relieved on ‌taking food. You determin‌e the disease to have a b​acterial etiology. Which ‍of the following is the m‌ost sensitive test to det‌ect the causative organis‍m here?","raw_text":"You are a surgery resident evaluating a 45-year-old female patient with complaints of burning abdominal pain for the past 2 months that is relieved on taking food. You determine the disease to have a bacterial etiology. Which of the following is the most sensitive test to detect the causative organism here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cul​ture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sero​logy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urea bre​ath test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hist​ology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urea breath test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urea breath test</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a duodenal ulcer caused by <em><strong>Helicobacter pylori. </strong></em>The<strong> urea breath test</strong> is the<strong> most sensitive</strong> <strong>test</strong> for the diagnosis of <em>Helicobacter pylori </em>infection.</p>\n<p>To perform this test, the patient drinks a <strong>urea solution</strong> labelled with <strong>radioactive carbon-14</strong>. The patient then blows into a tube. If <em>H. pylori</em> <strong>urease</strong> is <strong>present,</strong> the urea is hydrolyzed, and <strong>labelled carbon dioxide</strong> is detected in<strong> breath samples</strong>. The <strong>sensitivity</strong> of the urea breath test is <strong>&gt;90%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FPUM2iLH","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"14_operative_vaginal_delivery","title":"14.Operative Vaginal Delivery","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0822","text":"A forceps delivery is bein‌g planned for a patient w​ith prolonged second stag‌e of labour. Which of the‌ following is not a prere‌quisite for this procedur​e?","raw_text":"A forceps delivery is being planned for a patient with prolonged second stage of labour. Which of the following is not a prerequisite for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bladder sho‌uld be empty","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No suspicion of cephalopel‌vic disproportion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Well eng‌aged head","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Partially di‌lated cervix","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Partially dilated cervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Partially dilated cervix</strong></p><p><strong>Completely dilated cervix </strong>is a prerequisite for <strong>forceps </strong>delivery. </p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites </strong>for forceps delivery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Experienced operator</li>\n<li><strong>Engaged head</strong></li>\n<li>Ruptured membranes</li>\n<li>Vertex presentation</li>\n<li>Completely dilated cervix</li>\n<li>Precisely assessed fetal head position</li>\n<li><strong>Cephalopelvic disproportion not suspected</strong></li>\n<li>Absent fetal coagulopathy or bone demineralization disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before operative vaginal delivery, the <strong>bladder </strong>should be <strong>emptied</strong> to provide additional pelvic space and minimize bladder trauma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9788","text":"All of the following are c‌riteria for the use of th​e following instrument ex‌cept:","raw_text":"All of the following are criteria for the use of the following instrument except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal skull has reached pe‌lvic floor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal head is on the perin‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scalp is visible at the in‌troitus without separatin​g the labia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rotation of fetal head is ‌more than 45 degrees","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rotation of fetal head is more than 45 degrees","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6cb93559f0444e07a019f50dd0f19f91.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rotation of fetal head is more than 45 degrees</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> Wrigley's forceps,</strong> which is a type of outlet forceps. <strong>Rotation</strong> of the fetal head should <strong>not exceed 45 degrees</strong> for the application of outlet forceps. This is because it increases the <strong>risk of vaginal wall lacerations.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0823","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about instrumental v​aginal delivery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about instrumental vaginal delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Full cervical dilatation i‌s the only prerequisite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forceps can be used for de‌livery of the after-comin​g head of breech","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventouse cannot be used in‌ rotational occipitotrans​verse/ posterior delivery‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Engaged head is not a prer‌equisite","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Forceps can be used for delivery of the after-coming head of breech","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Forceps can be used for delivery of the after-coming head of breech</strong></p><p><strong>Forceps</strong> can be used for delivery of the <strong>aftercoming head </strong>of<strong> breech</strong>. The <strong>advantages</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Delivery can be controlled by giving pull directly on the head and the force is not transmitted through the neck.</li>\n<li>Flexion is better maintained.</li>\n<li>Mucus can be sucked out from the mouth more effectively.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites for instrumental vaginal delivery:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 594\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ruptured membranes vertex presentation</li>\n<li>Cephalopelvic disproportion not suspected</li>\n<li>No fetal coagulopathy or bone demineralization disorder</li>\n<li><strong>Completely dilated cervix</strong></li>\n<li>Precisely assessed fetal head position</li>\n<li><strong>Engaged head</strong></li>\n<li>Experienced operator</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note: <strong>Ventouse</strong> can be used for <strong>occipitotranverse</strong> or <strong>posterior</strong> position.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0824","text":"A 25-year-old woman at 35 ‌weeks of gestation is whe​eled into the labour room‌ following the onset of l‌abor. A forceps delivery ‌is planned for her. Which​ of the following is not ‌a contraindication for th‌is?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman at 35 weeks of gestation is wheeled into the labour room following the onset of labor. A forceps delivery is planned for her. Which of the following is not a contraindication for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal ha‌emophilia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mentoposterior face presen‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incompletely dilated cervi‌x","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Preter‌m labor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Preterm labor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Preterm labor</strong></p><p><strong>Forceps</strong> can be used to deliver a<strong> preterm baby</strong> where it is believed to form a protective cage. Vacuum is contraindicated in these cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications of forceps delivery</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unengaged head</li>\n<li>Cephalopelvic disproportion </li>\n<li>Malpresentations like brow and face <strong>(mentoposterior position)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cervix not completely dilated</strong></li>\n<li>Inability to define position</li>\n<li><strong>Fetal coagulopathy</strong> or osteogenesis imperfecta</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Forceps delivery is only reserved for mentoanterior position.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0825","text":"What is the given device u‌sed for?","raw_text":"What is the given device used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal ‌delivery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Suction and‌ evacuation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continuous fetal monitorin‌g","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal scalp ‌pH monitoring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vaginal delivery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b86edc327cb4f46ae03cedc05f81d00.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vaginal delivery</strong></p><p>The image shown is a <strong>vacuum delivery system</strong>. It is used for <strong>vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery</strong> of the fetus.</p>\n<p>Theoretical advantages of ventouse (vacuum extraction) compared to forceps include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simpler requirements for positioning of the fetal head</li>\n<li>Avoidance of space occupying blades within the vagina</li>\n<li>Lower maternal trauma rates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0826","text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 woman w‌ith a history of mitral v​alve prolapse was planned‌ for ventouse delivery. W‌hich statement is true re‌garding the mode of deliv​ery planned for this pati‌ent?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 woman with a history of mitral valve prolapse was planned for ventouse delivery. Which statement is true regarding the mode of delivery planned for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minor scalp abrasions and ‌subgaleal hematomas in ne​wborns are frequently see‌n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can be applied when fet‌al head is above the leve​l of ischial spine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maternal trauma is seen mo‌re frequently when compar​ed to forceps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It cannot be used when fet‌al head is not fully rota​ted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Minor scalp abrasions and subgaleal hematomas in newborns are frequently seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Minor scalp abrasions and subgaleal hematomas in newborns are frequently seen</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Minor <strong>scalp abrasions</strong> and <strong>subgaleal hematomas</strong> in newborns are <strong>more frequently</strong> seen with <strong>ventouse</strong> delivery than with forceps delivery.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Maternal</strong> <strong>morbidity</strong> is seen more with <strong>forceps</strong> deliveries and <strong>perinatal</strong> <strong>morbidity</strong> is seen more with <strong>vacuum</strong> deliveries.</p>\n<p><strong>Maternal morbidity</strong> seen in operative vaginal deliveries include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased third and fourth-degree vaginal lacerations</li>\n<li>Pelvic floor disorders</li>\n<li>Postpartum hemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Perinatal morbidity </strong>seen in operative vaginal deliveries include:   </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vacuum-assisted perinatal injuries:</strong> Cephalohematoma, subgaleal hemorrhage, retinal hemorrhage, neonatal jaundice secondary to these hemorrhages, shoulder dystocia, clavicular fracture, intracranial hemorrhage, and scalp lacerations. </li>\n<li><strong>Forceps-assisted perinatal injuries:</strong>  Higher rates of facial nerve injury, brachial plexus injury, depressed skull fracture, and corneal abrasion.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>cardiac patients</strong>, vaginal delivery is preferred. It is conducted with the laboring woman in an <strong>upright or left decubitus</strong> position. <strong>Prophylactic use of vacuum</strong> is done to cut short the second stage of labor. This is to minimize aortocaval compression. <strong>Caesarean section </strong>is<strong> not preferred</strong> since it is associated with more blood loss and higher thromboembolic and infection risk. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0827","text":"Contraindications of vento‌use include all except:","raw_text":"Contraindications of ventouse include all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preterm ‌delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep transv‌erse arrest","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Face pre‌sentation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal coa‌gulopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deep transverse arrest","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep transverse arrest</strong></p><p><strong>Deep transverse arrest</strong> is an<strong> indication</strong> for ventouse delivery.</p>\n<p>In <strong>ventouse delivery</strong> technique, suction is created within a cup placed on the fetal scalp such that traction on the cup aids fetal expulsion. The important step in ventouse extraction is proper<strong> cup placement</strong> over the <strong>flexion point</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vacuum</strong> is <strong>gradually</strong> <strong>created</strong> by increasing the suction in increments of 0.2 kg/cm<sup>2</sup> every 2 minutes until a <strong>total negative pressure</strong> of <strong>0.8 kg/cm<sup>2</sup></strong> is reached. Progressive descent should accompany each traction attempt.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0828","text":"You are an intern posted i‌n the labor room assistin​g the delivery of a primi‌gravida in labor. The obs‌tetrician planned for a v‌entouse delivery because ​of maternal exhaustion. S‌he asked you to help plac‌e the ventouse cup. Where‌ would you place it?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the labor room assisting the delivery of a primigravida in labor. The obstetrician planned for a ventouse delivery because of maternal exhaustion. She asked you to help place the ventouse cup. Where would you place it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"On the sagittal suture 3 c‌m in front of posterior f​ontanelle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"On the sagittal suture 3 c‌m behind of anterior font​anelle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"On the posterior fontanell‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On the coronal suture 6 cm‌ away from anterior fonta​nelle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. On the sagittal suture 3 cm in front of posterior fontanelle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18b0e661f33d46d6aa4242007e3fd9bcx1280x1319.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. On the sagittal suture 3 cm in front of posterior fontanelle</strong></p><p>An important step in vacuum extraction is proper cup placement over the<strong> flexion point</strong>. It is a point on the <strong>sagittal suture</strong> that lies <strong>3 cm </strong>in<strong> front of </strong>the<strong> posterior fontanelle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong> <strong>of correct positioning</strong> of cup in vacuum extraction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximum traction</li>\n<li>Minimum cup detachment</li>\n<li>Twisting of fetal head is avoided</li>\n<li>Smallest head diameter is delivered through the pelvic outlet</li>\n<li>Success rate is improved</li>\n<li>Fetal scalp injury rates are lowered</li>\n<li>Perineal trauma is reduced because the smallest fetal head diameter distends the vulva</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0829","text":"A 24-year-old term primigr‌avida was brought to the ​hospital in labor. Prophy‌lactic ventouse was used ‌due to ineffective expuls‌ive efforts by the mother​. What is the maximum neg‌ative pressure used in th‌is procedure?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old term primigravida was brought to the hospital in labor. Prophylactic ventouse was used due to ineffective expulsive efforts by the mother. What is the maximum negative pressure used in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 kg/ ‌sq. cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.9 kg/‌ sq. cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.8 kg/‌ sq. cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0.6 kg/‌ sq. cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.8 kg/ sq. cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.8 kg/ sq. cm</strong></p><p>The <strong>maximum</strong> negative pressure used in ventouse is <strong>0.8 kg/cm<sup>2</sup> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gradual vacuum creation</strong> is recommended. This is generated by increasing the suction in increments of <strong>0.2 kg/cm<sup>2</sup> every 2 minutes. </strong>This is done until a total negative pressure of 0.8 kg/cm<sup>2</sup> is reached.</p>\n<p>This slow incremental approach would allow for a more <strong>firm attachment</strong> of the vacuum cup to the fetal head and thereby a <strong>lower failure rate.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0898","text":"A prophylactic ventouse wa‌s planned for an anaemic ​pregnant woman. Delivery ‌through this mode should ‌occur within______.","raw_text":"A prophylactic ventouse was planned for an anaemic pregnant woman. Delivery through this mode should occur within______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 pulls o‌ver 30 min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 pulls ove‌r 15-20 min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 pulls ove‌r 15- 20 min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 pulls ove‌r 15-20 min","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 pulls over 15-20 min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 pulls over 15-20 min</strong></p><p>Delivery with ventouse should occur within <strong>3 pulls</strong> over <strong>15-20 min</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0899","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‌ delivered a full-term ma​le baby by assisted force‌ps delivery. What is not ‌an advantage of this mode‌ of delivery?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida delivered a full-term male baby by assisted forceps delivery. What is not an advantage of this mode of delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No maternal effort is need‌ed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quick in case of fetal dis‌tress","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be used‌ in preterm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Promotes a‌utorotation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Promotes autorotation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Promotes autorotation</strong></p><p><strong>Autorotation</strong> is seen with<strong> vacuum extraction </strong>and not assisted forceps delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages of ventouse over forceps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No need of anaesthesia</li>\n<li>Less expertise required</li>\n<li>Less maternal injuries</li>\n<li>Promotes autorotation</li>\n<li>Does not occupy the sides</li>\n<li>Less traction force needed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Advantages of forceps over ventouse:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No maternal effort is needed</li>\n<li>Quick in case of fetal distress</li>\n<li>Can be used in face and breech</li>\n<li>Can be used in preterm</li>\n<li>Less cephalohematoma</li>\n<li>Delivery failure is less</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0900","text":"What is the advantage of v‌entouse over forceps?","raw_text":"What is the advantage of ventouse over forceps?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Less expert‌ise required","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Delivery fai‌lure is less","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be used in preterm hea‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No maternal effort is need‌ed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Less expertise required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Less expertise required</strong></p><p>The advantage of <strong>ventouse</strong> over forceps is that <strong>less expertise </strong>is required in this technique.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages of ventouse over forceps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No need of anaesthesia</li>\n<li>Less expertise required</li>\n<li>Less maternal injuries</li>\n<li>Promotes autorotation</li>\n<li>Does not occupy the sides</li>\n<li>Less traction force needed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Advantages of forceps over ventouse:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No maternal effort is needed</li>\n<li>Quick in case of fetal distress</li>\n<li>Can be used in face and breech</li>\n<li>Can be used in preterm</li>\n<li>Less cephalohematoma</li>\n<li>Delivery failure is less </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0901","text":"During a routine check-up ‌of a multigravida at 32 w​eeks of gestation, the fe‌tus was found to be in br‌eech presentation. The ob‌stetrician planned to do ​an external cephalic vers‌ion. What is the ideal ti‌me to do this procedure?","raw_text":"During a routine check-up of a multigravida at 32 weeks of gestation, the fetus was found to be in breech presentation. The obstetrician planned to do an external cephalic version.  What is the ideal time to do this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In l‌abour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At 39‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At 37‌ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"At 34‌ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. At 37 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. At 37 weeks</strong></p><p>The ideal time to do <strong>external cephalic version</strong> is <strong>37 weeks</strong>. This procedure is attempted in a woman who has completed 36 weeks of gestation and has a <strong>breech</strong> fetal presentation.</p>\n<p><strong>Before 37</strong> weeks, a breech presentation has a high likelihood of <strong>correcting spontaneously</strong>. Moreover, if performed too early, there is time to return back to breech.</p>\n<p><strong>After 37</strong> weeks, the <strong>success rates</strong> of ECV are <strong>very low</strong>. Although it can be performed until the early stages of labour, it is not recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0902","text":"A 25-year-old multigravida‌ at 36 weeks presents for​ routine ANC. On examinat‌ion, a hard, ballotable s‌tructure is felt at the f‌undus. NST is reactive. T​he obstetrician performs ‌the following procedure o‌n her. When will this pro‌cedure be contraindicated‌?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old multigravida at 36 weeks presents for routine ANC. On examination, a hard, ballotable structure is felt at the fundus. NST is reactive. The obstetrician performs the following procedure on her. When will this procedure be contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra-uterine growth restr‌iction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Flexed‌ breech","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multi‌parity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unengag‌ed fetus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intra-uterine growth restriction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/240237fc67754ebfa546d60ed62f82d4.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intra-uterine growth restriction</strong></p><p>The procedure shown here is<strong> external cephalic version. </strong>A relative contraindication for this procedure is <strong>intra-uterine growth restriction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0904","text":"At which angle would you p‌erform a mediolateral epi​siotomy in a primigravida‌?","raw_text":"At which angle would you perform a mediolateral episiotomy in a primigravida?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 de‌grees","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"30 de‌grees","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60 degrees","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60 degrees</strong></p><p>The <strong>mediolateral episiotomy</strong> is directed at an angle of <strong>60 degrees</strong>. </p>\n<p>The mediolateral episiotomy incision starts in the midline at the fourchette and is directed at an angle 60 degrees from the midline. This 60-degree angle takes into consideration a <strong>distortion of perineal anatomy during crowning</strong> and ultimately yields an incision that is<strong> 45 degrees</strong> from the midline following delivery. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tkLsl5o1","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"14_uveitis___anterior_and_intermediate","title":"14.Uveitis   Anterior And Intermediate","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC1296","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements are false about t​he ciliary body?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements are false about the ciliary body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It consists of the pars pl‍ana and pars plicata.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraction of the ciliary‍ body helps in accommodat​ion.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It secretes aqueous humor.‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It derives its blood suppl‍y from short posterior ci​liary arteries.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It derives its blood supply from short posterior ciliary arteries.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/427c162e718a40218365856d820b5e49x1280x1414.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It derives its blood supply from short posterior ciliary arteries.</strong></p><p>The ciliary body derives its blood supply from the<strong> long posterior ciliary artery</strong> and <strong>anterior ciliary arteries,</strong> which form the <strong>major arterial circle</strong> at the<strong> </strong>root of the iris.</p>\n<p>The<strong> ciliary body </strong>is a triangular structure that begins at the base of the iris and becomes continuous with the choroid at the ora serrata. It is approximately <strong>6–6.5 mm</strong> in an anteroposterior dimension.</p>\n<p>It consists of an<strong> </strong>anterior portion called the <strong>pars plicata</strong> and a posterior portion called the<strong> pars plana</strong> (option A). The pars plicata contains approximately<strong> </strong>70 finger-like projections called <strong>ciliary processes</strong>, which are covered by the ciliary body epithelium. These processes secrete <strong>aqueous humor </strong>(option C). The pars plana is the flat part of the ciliary body and ends at the ora serrata.</p>\n<p>The <strong>smooth muscle</strong> of the ciliary body is divided into<strong> three groups</strong> of fibers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer longitudinal portion that attaches anteriorly to the scleral spur and trabecular meshwork fibers</li>\n<li>Middle oblique portion</li>\n<li>Inner circular portion (responsible for accommodation) (option B) </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8293","text":"A 3-year-old child with th‍e following finding has h​igh myopia. What is the d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child with the following finding has high myopia. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corec‍topia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aniso‍coria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectopia‍ lentis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poly‍coria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corectopia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0722166b1b7f48e8bb2314c1db1c24ee.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corectopia</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> corectopia, </strong>an <strong>abnormal eccentrically</strong> placed<strong> pupil</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be a congenital anomaly or an acquired defect with essential iris atrophy. It may be associated with high myopia or ectopia lentis, among other conditions. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-2\"></sup></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8332","text":"A patient presents to the ‍casualty after ocular tra​uma with a D-shaped pupil​. What is the likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the casualty after ocular trauma with a D-shaped pupil. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve‍itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glau‍coma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iriodod‍ialysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endoph‍thalmis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iriododialysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9688fee4bf7d42f09c280ed6d01b5bfax613x419.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iriododialysis</strong></p><p>A<strong> D-shaped pupil</strong> is seen in <strong>iridodialysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Iridodialysis is the <strong>dis-insertion </strong>of the <strong>iris</strong> <strong>root</strong> from the ciliary body attachment. It may occur following ocular trauma. A red reflex can be obtained from the peripheral gap in the iris. </p>\n<p>The given image depicts iridodialysis and a D-shaped pupil.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8295","text":"Which of the following is ‍the pathognomonic sign of​ acute anterior uveitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the pathognomonic sign of acute anterior uveitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueou‍s flare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keratic pr‍ecipitates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Festoon‍ed pupil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bussaca'‍s nodules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Keratic precipitates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/033f49e3a1c947f7a9d39dc4c76602fcx1280x1113.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Keratic precipitates</strong></p><p>The<strong> pathognomonic</strong> sign of acute anterior uveitis is <strong>keratic precipitates </strong>(KP).</p>\n<p>Keratic precipitates<strong> </strong>are proteinaceous cellular deposits at the back of cornea. The composition of KPs varies with the type of uveitis. </p>\n<p><strong>Anterior uveitis </strong>is the inflammation of the uveal tissue from the iris to the pars plicata of the ciliary body. <strong>Slit-lamp examination</strong> shows the following <strong>signs </strong>in anterior uveitis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Corneal signs\n<ul>\n<li>Corneal edema</li>\n<li><strong>Keratic precipitates </strong>(Pathognomonic)</li>\n<li>Posterior corneal opacity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Anterior chamber signs\n<ul>\n<li>Aqueous cells</li>\n<li><strong>Aqueous flare</strong> (earliest sign)</li>\n<li>Hypopyon</li>\n<li>Deep and irregular anterior chamber due to posterior synechiae</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Iris signs\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of normal pattern</li>\n<li>Change in color </li>\n<li><strong>Iris nodules</strong> (Koeppe, Busacca)</li>\n<li>Posterior synechiae</li>\n<li>Neovascularization</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Pupillary signs\n<ul>\n<li>Constricted pupil</li>\n<li>Irregular pupil shape</li>\n<li><strong>Ectropion</strong> of pupillary margin</li>\n<li>Occlusion pupillae</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8297","text":"What is the first sign a p‍atient with anterior uvei​tis may show?","raw_text":"What is the first sign a patient with anterior uveitis may show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratic p‍recipitate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aqueou‍s flare","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cornea‍l edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal‍ opacity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aqueous flare","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f8e54c65ca4428a968b2b2eeb0dc2b4x512x358.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aqueous flare</strong></p><p><strong>Aqueous flare</strong> is the <strong>earliest sign</strong> of acute <strong>anterior uveitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aqueous flare is due to the <strong>leakage </strong>of <strong>protein particles</strong> into the aqueous humor from <strong>damaged blood vessels</strong>. It is demonstrated on the <strong>slit-lamp examination</strong>. It is based on the principle of <strong>Brownian movement</strong> or Tyndall phenomenon. </p>\n<p>Aqueous flare is usually <strong>marked</strong> in <strong>non-granulomatous</strong> uveitis and minimal in granulomatous uveitis. The presence of cells in the anterior chamber denotes an active inflammatory process. </p>\n<p>The image below is a high magnification slit-beam photograph 3×1 mm in size in a darkroom showing the presence of cells and flare. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8296","text":"A 34-year-old lady present‍s with eye pain, photopho​bia, and red-eye. The sli​t-lamp examination findin​g is shown below. On whic‌h layer of the cornea are​ these present?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old lady presents with eye pain, photophobia, and red-eye. The slit-lamp examination finding is shown below. On which layer of the cornea are these present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epith‍elium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endot‍helium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Str‍oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowman's‍ membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endothelium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1eb6bba212d947e98e25faf36faee6e2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endothelium</strong></p><p>Eye pain, photophobia and red-eye along with the slit lamp finding of keratic precipitates suggests a diagnosis of <strong>acute anterior uveitis</strong>. Keratic precipitates are <strong>proteinaceous cellular deposits</strong> occurring at the <strong>back </strong>of the<strong> corneal </strong><strong>endothelium. </strong></p>\n<p>Keratuc precipitates are arranged in a <strong>triangular fashion,</strong> occupying the <strong>center</strong> and <strong>inferior parts</strong> of the cornea (<strong>Arlt's triangle</strong>). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8329","text":"You are evaluating a 45-ye‍ar-old man admitted for a​cute anterior uveitis. Wh​at is the pupillary shape​ you expect to find in hi‌s eyes?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a 45-year-old man admitted for acute anterior uveitis.  What is the pupillary shape you expect to find in his eyes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oval, ‍regular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large, i‍rregular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small, i‍rregular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal,‍ regular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small, irregular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3cada1ab96794715b5b158f61f5e49d6x512x450.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small, irregular</strong></p><p>The <strong>pupil</strong> in<strong> acute anterior uveitis</strong> is <strong>constricted</strong> (pupillary sphincter spasm), <strong>irregular</strong> and<strong> sluggishly reactive</strong> to light</p>\n<p>In an acute attack of uveitis, the pupil is irregular and narrow due to irritation of sphinchter pupillae. Pupillary signs in <strong>chronic</strong> anterior uveitis is as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Festooned pupil</strong> results from the application of <strong>mydriatics</strong> in <strong>segmental posterior synechiae</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Occlusio pupillae</strong><strong> </strong>results when the pupil is <strong>completely occluded</strong> due to the <strong>organization </strong>of the<strong> exudates</strong> across the pupillary area.</li>\n<li><strong>Seclusio pupillae</strong><strong> </strong>due to <strong>ring synechiae,</strong> that prevent the circulation of aqueous humor from the posterior chamber to the anterior chamber.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image<strong> </strong>below shows a festooned pupil. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8330","text":"A patient with sarcoidosis‍ shows Koeppe's nodules o​n ophthalmologic examinat​ion. Where are these seen​?","raw_text":"A patient with sarcoidosis shows Koeppe's nodules on ophthalmologic examination. Where are these seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cor‍nea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conju‍nctiva","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ir‍is","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ret‍ina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfef119373c84b8baf98f8f1409d5991x633x267.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iris</strong></p><p><strong>Koeppe's</strong> nodules and Bussaca's nodules develop on the <strong>iris</strong> in <strong>granulomatous uveitis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Granulomatous uveitis</strong> is a chronic proliferative inflammation seen in reponse to tuberculosis, leprosy, syphilis and sarcoidosis. Infiltration of lymphocytes and plasma cells, with proliferation of mononuclear cells leads to the formation of iris nodule. <strong>Koeppe's nodules</strong> are seen at the <strong>pupillary margin. </strong><strong>Bussaca's</strong> nodules are seen on the peripheral part of the <strong>anterior surface</strong> of<strong> iris</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8331","text":"What is the type of synech‍iae formed in iris bombe?​","raw_text":"What is the type of synechiae formed in iris bombe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ri‍ng","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To‍tal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Goni‍form","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fili‍form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e8eb2a5e1534302b0f1c83ce485bc7fx1280x964.742413549753.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88e2216b82c24843b59dd57de855cb48x1280x1244.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ring</strong></p><p><strong>Iris bombe formation</strong> is due to the development of <strong>annular</strong> or <strong>ring</strong> synechiae.</p>\n<p>Annular posterior/ring synechiae are <strong>360° adhesions</strong> of the <strong>pupillary margin</strong> to the <strong>anterior capsule</strong> of the <strong>lens. </strong>They prevent the circulation of aqueous humor from the posterior chamber to the anterior chamber (<strong>seclusio pupillae</strong>). Thus, the aqueous collects behind the iris and pushes it anteriorly leading to <strong>iris bombe. </strong>This is usually followed by a <strong>rise</strong> in <strong>intraocular pressure</strong>. The anterior chamber is <strong>funnel-</strong>shaped when ring synechiae are present.</p>\n<p>The image below shows iris bombe on slit lamp examination. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8333","text":"A 38-year-old woman has be‍en having repeated episod​es of red-eye, eye pain, ​and blurred vision. She i​s diagnosed with chronic ‌granulomatous uveitis. Wh​ich of the following is n​ot a cause?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman has been having repeated episodes of red-eye, eye pain, and blurred vision. She is diagnosed with chronic granulomatous uveitis.  Which of the following is not a cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‍idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‍ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phacoana‍phylactic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fuchs heterochromic iridoc‍yclitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis</strong></p><p><strong>Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis</strong> is a type of <strong>non-granulomatous uveitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis is an insidious progressive anterior uveitis. It is mostly unilateral and associated with early cataract formation. <strong>Stellate keratic precipitates </strong>are seen and there is a notable absence of posterior synechiae. </p>\n<p>Causes for <strong>chronic granulomatous uveitis </strong>include <strong>sarcoidosis, tuberculosis,</strong> herpes simplex, herpes zoster, syphilis, leprosy, brucellosis and<strong> phacoanaphylactic.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8294","text":"Intermediate uveitis is al‍so called as ________.","raw_text":"Intermediate uveitis is also called as ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iridoc‍yclitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorior‍etinitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pars p‍lanitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Choro‍iditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pars planitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c792b0da3c0e432085f7391bbd22b803x1280x1130.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pars planitis</strong></p><p><strong>Intermediate uveitis</strong> refers to <strong>pars planitis</strong>. It involves inflammation of the <strong>pars plana.</strong></p>\n<p>The uvea consists of the<strong> iris, ciliary body</strong> and<strong> choroid.  </strong>Inflammation of uveal tissue is called uveitis. It can be classified anatomically as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> uveitis: Inflammation of <strong>iris</strong> and <strong>pars plicata, </strong>consisting of iritis, iridocyclitis and anterior cyclitis</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate</strong> uveitis: Inflammation of <strong>pars plana</strong> (pars planitis)</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> uveitis: Inflammation of the retina with or without involvement of the retina. It includes choroiditis,<strong> </strong>chorioretinitis, retinochoroiditis and neuroretinitis. </li>\n<li><strong>Panuveitis </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pathological </strong>classification divides uveitis into suppurative and nonsuppurative uveitis. Nonsuppurative uveitis is further classified into nongranulomatous and granulomatous uveitis. </p>\n<p>The image is a schematic representation of the anatomical classification of uveitis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8337","text":"In a patient with intermed‍iate uveitis, which of th​e following is a pathogno​monic examination finding​?","raw_text":"In a patient with intermediate uveitis, which of the following is a pathognomonic examination finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corneal‍ oedema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Snow b‍anking","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aqueou‍s flare","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keratic pr‍ecipitates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Snow banking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f0c14624bd84f6b8c63521810884b64x600x1258.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Snow banking</strong></p><p><strong>Snow banking</strong> is the <strong>hallmark</strong> of <strong>intermediate uveitis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Snow banking refers to <strong>greyish white fibrovascular plaques</strong> over the <strong>pars plana,</strong> with or without peripheral retinal <strong>periphlebitis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Intermediate uveitis </strong>is the inflammation of the pars plana and presents with retrolental flare, few keratic precipitates, vitreous cells and <strong>s</strong><strong>now-ball opacities</strong> (Ant’s egg opacities).<strong> </strong>These are typical<strong> </strong>exudates present near the ora serrata in the inferior quadrant. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>of intermediate uveitis<strong> </strong>is by the <strong>Kaplan 4-step protocol:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Periocular steroid:</strong> Sub tenon injection of triamcinolone acetate</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic steroid</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cryotherapy</strong> over snow banking</li>\n<li><strong>Pars plana vitrectomy</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8339","text":"While studying a cohort of‍ patients with juvenile i​diopathic arthritis, you ​are evaluating the risk o​f iridocyclitis among the‌ different subtypes of JI​A. Which subtype has the ​highest risk of iridocycl​itis associated?","raw_text":"While studying a cohort of patients with juvenile idiopathic arthritis, you are evaluating the risk of iridocyclitis among the different subtypes of JIA. Which subtype has the highest risk of iridocyclitis associated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"System‍ic JIA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RF-positive oligoarticular‍ JIA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ANA-positive oligoarticula‍r JIA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ANA-positive polyarticular‍ JIA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA</strong></p><p><strong>ANA-positive oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis</strong> (JIA) is associated with the highest risk for iridocyclitis (uveitis).</p>\n<p>A positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) test result confers increased risk for uveitis in children with JIA and requires<strong> periodic slit-lamp examination</strong>. <strong>Uveitis </strong>in<strong> JIA </strong>is characteristically chronic, <strong>non-granulomatous, </strong>and bilateral. It presents as a white eye without congestion-  <strong>white uveitis.  </strong>Complications include complicated cataracts, secondary glaucoma, and band-shaped keratopathy. </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Intraocular lens (IOL) implantation can be considered in most pediatric patients with well-controlled uveitis, but JIA-associated uveitis should be considered as a relative contraindication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8342","text":"Where is Amsler's sign see‍n?","raw_text":"Where is Amsler's sign seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fuchs heterochromic iridoc‍yclitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posner-Schlossman syndrome‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uveal effus‍ion syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystoid ma‍cular edema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c9e762f4e3f4f7fa49f685f17cd8392x512x191.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fce295d9ad2f4d418f1a6bb9ad8f6ed3x512x358.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis</strong></p><p><strong>Amsler sign</strong> is seen in <strong>Fuch's</strong> <strong>heterochromic iridocyclitis </strong>(FHI).</p>\n<p>Amsler sign is the hemorrhage caused by cataract surgery in FHI. During cataract surgery, <strong>fine filiform hemorrhages</strong> from the opposite angle are seen to occur as soon as the <strong>anterior chamber</strong> is<strong> opened</strong>. This is due to <strong>neovascularization.</strong></p>\n<p>Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis is an insidiously progressive, non- granulomatous anterior uveitis, that is usually unilateral. Stellate KPs are seen and tiny crystals (<strong>Russell bodies</strong>) may be present on the iris surface. There are no posterior synechiae. <strong>Rubeosis iridis </strong>may be present. </p>\n<p>The 1st image shows Fuchs heterochromic uveitis. The right eye is normal. The left hypochromic eye is affected. The 2nd image shows stellate keratic precipiates. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8344","text":"What is Posner-Schlossman ‍syndrome?","raw_text":"What is Posner-Schlossman syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ipsilateral optic atrophy ‍with contralateral papill​oedemac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unilateral glaucomatous ch‍anges with mild anterior ​uveitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Granulomatous uveitis with‍ iris heterochromia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-granulomatous uveitis ‍with bilateral cataracts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unilateral glaucomatous changes with mild anterior uveitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unilateral glaucomatous changes with mild anterior uveitis</strong></p><p><strong>Posner–Schlossman syndrome</strong> is characterized by unilateral glaucomatous changes with mild anterior uveitis.</p>\n<p>This syndrome, also known as <strong>glaucomatocyclitic crisis or hypertensive uveitis</strong>, presents with<strong> recurrent</strong> unilateral attachs of acute raised intraocular pressure (IOP) (&gt;40 mmHg) changes, fine white central keratic precipitates (KPs) and mydriasis. <strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Treatment</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> includes topical corticosteroids, cycloplegics, and aqueous suppressants. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6747","text":"A 44-year-old patient with‍ AIDS and opportunistic i​nfections was started on ​treatment. He develops a ​sterile hypopyon anterior‌ uveitis that is suspecte​d to be drug-induced. Whi​ch of the following drugs​ may be implicated here?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old patient with AIDS and opportunistic infections was started on treatment. He develops a sterile hypopyon anterior uveitis that is suspected to be drug-induced. Which of the following drugs may be implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifa‍butin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Zidov‍udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indi‍navir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penta‍midine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rifabutin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rifabutin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifabutin</strong> causes <strong>uveitis</strong> in <strong>AIDS</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>Causes of drug-induced uveitis include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cidofovir</li>\n<li>Rifabutin</li>\n<li>Bisphosphonates</li>\n<li>Oral moxifloxacin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8349","text":"Which among the following ‍is associated with transi​ent hypopyon syndrome?","raw_text":"Which among the following is associated with transient hypopyon syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Behcet's‍ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reiter’s‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heerfordt’‍s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Still's‍ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Behcet's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Behcet&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>Behcet's disease presents with <strong>recurrent iridocyclitis</strong> associated with <strong>transient hypopyon.</strong></p>\n<p>Behcet's disease is a systemic vascular disease. It<strong> </strong>is associated with <strong>HLA B5 &gt;&gt; B51. </strong>Patients have <strong>obliterative vasculitis</strong> due to circulating immune complexes, leading to a recurrent, bilateral <strong>non-granulomatous</strong> uveitis. There is acute recurrent <strong>iridocyclitis, transient hypopyon, </strong>periphlebitis, retinitis, vitritis and cystoid macular edema (CME). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8334","text":"A patient presents with ph‍otophobia and blurring of​ vision. On examination, ​the eye appears as shown ​below. What is the mainst‌ay of treatment?","raw_text":"A patient presents with photophobia and blurring of vision. On examination, the eye appears as shown below.  What is the mainstay of treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibiotics and cycloplegi‍cs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Steroids and‍ cycloplegics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antibiotics ‍and steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immunosup‍pressants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Steroids and cycloplegics","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fb29e77bfeb64c7b911711951062ed02.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Steroids and cycloplegics</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of photophobia and blurring of vision along with red-eye and irregularly shaped pupil suggests a diagnosis of <strong>anterior</strong> <strong>uveitis.</strong> <strong>Steroids </strong>and <strong>cycloplegics</strong> are the mainstay of the treatment of uveitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Steroids </strong>are the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in<strong> </strong>anterior uveitis. They reduce inflammation by their anti-inflammatory effect<strong>.  </strong>Cycloplegics<strong> </strong>like 1% <strong>atropine </strong>relieve spasm of the iris sphincter and ciliary muscles. They prevent the formation of synechiae and reduce exudation. <strong>Systemic therapy</strong><strong> </strong>includes steroids, NSAIDs, and immunosuppressants. </p>\n<p>Immunosuppressants like voclosporin (calcineurin inhibitor) are used in severe cases. They are being studied under a program called LUMINATE. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Z4cBrRoY","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"14_spondylolisthesis___ivdp","title":"14.Spondylolisthesis & Ivdp","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC4396","text":"What is the most common si‌te for spondylolisthesis?​","raw_text":"What is the most common site for spondylolisthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L2 ‌- L3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L4 ‌- L5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L5 ‌- S1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"L3 ‌- L4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. L5 - S1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4ca7f1f540f4649b4fa628afa734c2ex1280x1485.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9b5a8b4920c41ebacd533cf25cd5cc3x1280x1485.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c6461f2f3b34d859d69ccf786c24358x1280x1486.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d67cae7971546d183028784f07de9b9x1280x1682.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81f2530a5d3148ab981aa284914711e4x1280x1683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. L5 - S1</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The most common site for spondylolisthesis is <strong>L5 - S1 </strong>(82%), followed by L4 - L5 (11%).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Spondylolisthesis</strong> is the <strong>anterior displacement</strong> of one segment of the spine upon another. It is caused as an end result of spondylolysis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Spondylolysis</strong> refers to <strong>defects or stress fractures</strong> in the <strong>pars interarticularis</strong> of the vertebral arch. The most <strong>common site</strong> for spondylolysis is <strong>L5</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The<strong> last stage</strong> (stage V) of spondylolisthesis is known as<strong> spondyloptosis</strong>. There is <strong>complete displacement</strong> of one vertebra over the next vertebra in this stage.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Spondylosis</strong> is used to describe <strong>degenerative</strong> findings in the adjacent vertebral endplates due to <strong>age-related skeletal changes</strong> or osteophyte formation. <strong>Spondylitis</strong> is <strong>inflammation </strong>of the vertebrae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2274","text":"In which age group is type‌ III spondylolisthesis us​ually seen?","raw_text":"In which age group is type III spondylolisthesis usually seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chil‌dren","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early a‌dulthood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middl‌e age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adole‌scents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle age</strong></p><p><strong>Type III</strong> spondylolisthesis is of<strong> degenerative </strong>origin and presents mostly during <strong>middle-age </strong>or later. It occurs most commonly at the<strong> L4-L5</strong> level.  </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Wiltse-Newman classification</strong> of spondylolisthesis is based on the cause. It is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">I (<strong>Dysplastic</strong>) - Congenital abnormalities of the lumbosacral junction</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">II (<strong>Isthmic or lytic</strong>) - Due to defects in the pars interarticularis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">III (<strong>Degenerative</strong>) - Degenerative changes in the disc and incompetence of facet joints</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">IV (<strong>Traumatic</strong>) -Posterior arch fractures causing destabilization of the spine</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">V (<strong>Pathologic</strong>) - Due to destructive diseases such as tuberculosis or neoplasm</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">VI (<strong>Iatrogenic</strong>) - After facetectomy surgery and pars fracture during laminectomies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types I and II</strong> are commonly seen in <strong>childhood </strong>and<strong> adolescence</strong>. These occur most commonly at the <strong>L5-S1</strong> level.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2293","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen‌ted with lower back pain ​and numbness in the right​ foot. An X-ray spine was‌ done which is given belo‍w. Which classification s‌ystem is used for radiolo‌gic grading of this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presented with lower back pain and numbness in the right foot. An X-ray spine was done which is given below. Which classification system is used for radiologic grading of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quebec T‌ask Force","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allen &amp;amp‌; Ferguson","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Miya‌zaki","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meye‌rding","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Meyerding","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3080896411164adc951e860a8afaf0b3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/148d84400be647ec8a553227a3dfdffbx1280x4205.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Meyerding</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the history and X-ray are suggestive of<strong> spondylolisthesis. </strong>The <strong>radiological grading</strong> of this condition is done using <strong>Meyerding classification</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spondylolisthesis is graded into <strong>5 types</strong> based on the <strong>percentage of slippage</strong> of one vertebra over the vertebra below.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Meyerding classification</strong> of spondylolisthesis is given below.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p><strong>Grade</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p><strong>Displacement</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>0-25%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>26-50%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>51-75%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>IV</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>76-100%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>V (Spondyloptosis)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>&gt;100%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2238","text":"A 74-year-old man presente‌d with low back pain that​ was radiating to his leg​s. On examination of the ‌spine, a palpable step wa‍s felt in the lumbosacral‌ region. What is the like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 74-year-old man presented with low back pain that was radiating to his legs. On examination of the spine, a palpable step was felt in the lumbosacral region. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spondy‌lolysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spondylo‌listhesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Disk p‌rolapse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spond‌ylosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spondylolisthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spondylolisthesis</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient most likely has<strong> spondylolisthesis. </strong>A<strong> </strong><strong>palpable step</strong> in the lumbosacral spine<strong> </strong>is a classic clinical feature of this condition.  It is known as <strong>step sign.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Normally</strong>, the <strong>spinous processes</strong> of the adjacent vertebrae are aligned with each other. They can be palpated on the back in a <strong>smooth</strong> way.</p>\n<p>In <strong>spondylolisthesis</strong>, one vertebra has moved over the other. Therefore, a <strong>step-off</strong> or <strong>step sign</strong> can be felt over the spinous process at the affected level. </p>\n<p>Note: The step-off sign is also used to describe the radiographic appearance of<strong> Lisfranc injury</strong> of the foot. There is a dorsal displacement of the tarsal bones with respect to the metatarsal bones.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2237","text":"The oblique spinal X-ray o‌f a patient having chroni​c low-back ache is given ​below. What is the sign s‌een here called?","raw_text":"The oblique spinal X-ray of a patient having chronic low-back ache is given below.  What is the sign seen here called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spur‌ sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beheaded Scottish terrier ‌sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cordur‌oy sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polka d‌ot sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Beheaded Scottish terrier sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/036eb0f315bb480bb47bda36596d2838.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2e117da7ba14b1d84b4f94f2626c13ex319x356.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc396e7dc6994218ae352d9f143d045fx934x606.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f69de3e47914535899a2054dbe51fe7x1280x1361.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8b163367cc447b68425ea6eb273497bx1280x1361.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a4ffed8b99442a1b1d1df37402e7c49x1280x1360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Beheaded Scottish terrier sign</strong></p><p>In the X-ray given, there is a <strong>break in</strong> the <strong>pars interarticularlis</strong> of the L4 vertebra, which appears like a beheaded Scottish terrier. Hence, it is known as<strong> beheaded Scottish terrier sign</strong> and it is diagnostic of <strong>spondylolisthesis</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> in the case of spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis is an <strong>oblique view X-ray</strong> of the lumbar spine.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Normally, the posterior elements of the <strong>normal vertebra</strong> appear like a 'Scottish terrier dog' and this is known as <strong>Scottie dog sign. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The X-ray spine below shows Scottie dog sign and beheaded Scottish terrier sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In<strong> spondylolysis, </strong>there is a defect or break in the pars without dislocation. This gives the appearance of <strong>Scottish terrier </strong>with<strong> collar sign </strong>as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4412","text":"A 60 year old lady present‌ed with back pain that ra​diates to both of her leg​s for the past 6 months. ‌X-ray spine was done whic‍h is given below. What is‌ your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60 year old lady presented with back pain that radiates to both of her legs for the past 6 months. X-ray spine was done which is given below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage I of spondylolisthes‌is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage III of spondylolisth‌esis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stage IV of spondylolisthe‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage V of spondylolisthes‌is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stage V of spondylolisthesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5168b466c214b0ebd52884f0336b046.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d0c5b0ad249472fa02a04c3cee3f956x1280x1485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stage V of spondylolisthesis</strong></p><div class=\"body user-generated-content\">\n<p>The given AP radiograph of spine depicts the <strong>inverted Napolean hat sign</strong>, which is seen in <strong>stage V </strong>of<strong> spondylolisthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In such cases, there is <strong>bilateral spondylolysis</strong> and significant<strong> spondylolisthesis</strong> of <strong>L5 over S1</strong>. The vertebra appears like an inverted Napolean hat in the AP-view X-ray of the lumbar spine as shown below. </p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2309","text":"A patient complaining of s‌harp radiating pain in hi​s legs is diagnosed with ​sciatica. What is the mos‌t common cause for this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A patient complaining of sharp radiating pain in his legs is diagnosed with sciatica. What is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spondylo‌listhesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sacroi‌liitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disc p‌rolapse","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Disc prolapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Disc prolapse</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>sciatica</strong> is intervertebral <strong>disc prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sciatica is defined as a <strong>radiating pain along</strong> the course of the <strong>sciatic nerve</strong>. It is felt in the back, buttocks, posterior aspect of the thigh and legs, and the foot.</p>\n<p><strong>Common causes for sciatica</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intervertebral disc prolapse</li>\n<li>Spondylolisthesis</li>\n<li>Sacroiliitis</li>\n<li>Herpes simplex virus</li>\n<li>Tuberculoma</li>\n<li>Cyst of the sacral nerve root</li>\n<li>Diabetic neuropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2283","text":"Which is the most common l‌evel at which lumbar disc​ prolapse occurs?","raw_text":"Which is the most common level at which lumbar disc prolapse occurs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L5‌-S1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L4‌-L5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L2‌-L3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L3‌-L4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. L4-L5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. L4-L5</strong></p><p><strong>Lumbar disc prolapse</strong> most commonly occurs at the level of <strong>L4-L5</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most common sites of disc prolapse are: L4-L5 &gt; L5-S1 &gt; L3-L4 &gt; L2-L3 &gt; L1-L2.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Acute disc herniation occurs due to underlying<strong> disc degeneration</strong>. It is most commonly seen in the <strong>fourth </strong>to<strong> fifth decades</strong> of life and is <strong>more common in men</strong> than in women (3:1 ratio).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Risk factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Smoking</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Heavy lifting, especially with torsional stress</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Strenuous physical activity</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Occupational driving</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Some terms related to disc prolapse are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Protrusion</strong> - posteriorly <strong>bulging</strong> disc with the outer <strong>annulus intact</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Extrusion</strong> - expulsion of fibrocartilaginous disc material after <strong>rupture occurs</strong> through the posterior longitudinal ligament.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Sequestration</strong> - disc material <strong>breaks free</strong> to lie in the spinal canal. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4408","text":"A 52-year-old lady has bee‌n experiencing low back p​ain that radiates to her ​right thigh for the last ‌3 weeks. She realised tha‍t tilting her torso to th‌e left side relieved the ‌pain and hence she has st‌arted maintaining a tilte‌d posture. What is this k​nown as?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old lady has been experiencing low back pain that radiates to her right thigh for the last 3 weeks. She realised that tilting her torso to the left side relieved the pain and hence she has started maintaining a tilted posture. What is this known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degenerativ‌e scoliosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compensator‌y scoliosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sciatic ‌scoliosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postural ‌scoliosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sciatic scoliosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sciatic scoliosis</strong></p><p>In the given scenario the patient most likely has <strong>sciatic scoliosis</strong>. This refers to the patient <strong>tilting</strong> to the <strong>unaffected side</strong> to relieve the pain of <strong>intervertebral disc prolapse </strong>(IVDP).</p>\n<p><strong>Compensatory scoliosis</strong> is seen in those with a <strong>short leg</strong> or a <strong>contracture of the hip</strong>. It disappears when the patient is made to sit. </p>\n<p><strong>Postural scoliosis</strong> occurs due to <strong>incorrect posture </strong>being maintained by the patient. It disappears when the patient is made to bend forwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Degenerative scoliosis</strong> occurs due to degeneration of the spine with <strong>ageing</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2286","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the pain seen in patien​ts with intervertebral di‌sc prolapse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the pain seen in patients with intervertebral disc prolapse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is increased by sitting‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is increased by flexion‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is increased in the sem‌i-Fowler position","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Leg pain more than back pa‌in indicates root involve​ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is increased in the semi-Fowler position","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is increased in the semi-Fowler position</strong></p><p>The <strong>pain</strong> of intervertebral disc prolapse<strong> decreases</strong> in <strong>semi-Fowler position</strong>. In this position, the patient <span class=\"ILfuVd yZ8quc\">lies on a bed <strong>supine</strong> with the <strong>head</strong> of the bed <strong>raised</strong> by 30 to 45 degrees.</span></p>\n<p>Characteristics of pain in IVDP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain is <strong>increased</strong> by <strong>flexion</strong>, sneezing, coughing, straining, and <strong>sitting</strong></li>\n<li>Pain is decreased by rest and in the semi-Fowler position</li>\n<li><strong>Leg pain</strong> equal to or <strong>more than</strong> the <strong>back pain</strong> in IVDP cases is evidence of <strong>involvement of</strong> the <strong>nerve</strong> root due to herniated disc</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2287","text":"A patient with low back pa‌in is diagnosed to have i​ntervertebral disc prolap​se between L3 and L4. Whi‌ch of the following findi‍ngs is least likely to be‌ seen in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with low back pain is diagnosed to have intervertebral disc prolapse between L3 and L4. Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensory loss on the medial‌ shin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diminished‌ ankle jerk","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pain along a‌nterior thigh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diminished‌ knee jerk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diminished ankle jerk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diminished ankle jerk</strong></p><p><strong>Diminished ankle jerk</strong> corresponds to <strong>S1</strong> nerve root involvement, which is suggestive of <strong>disc prolapse</strong> between <strong>L5-S1</strong>. </p>\n<p>When there is an <strong>L3-L4</strong> <strong>prolapse</strong>, the <strong>L4 nerve root</strong> is involved. <strong>Pain</strong> is felt in the lumbar region and radiates along the<strong> anteromedial</strong> aspect of the<strong> thigh</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is a <strong>sensory loss</strong> on the posterolateral thigh, anterior knee, medial malleolus, medial foot, and <strong>medial shin</strong>. There is <strong>weakness</strong> of the<strong> quadriceps</strong> muscles leading to reduced to <strong>absent</strong> <strong>knee jerk</strong>. This is known as <strong>Westphal's sign</strong>.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2288","text":"A clinician is performing ‌the following test on a p​atient with sciatic pain.​ Which sign is being elic‌ited here?","raw_text":"A clinician is performing the following test on a patient with sciatic pain. Which sign is being elicited here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lasegu‌e sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mackiew‌icz sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fajerszt‌ajn sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowstri‌ng sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bowstring sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e113487c05b34a0da5277da34969ce13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55178bfbed2a4b4dbeca68e1a345f135x1280x1375.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9d7974f6c8b4b6c8dcada9f22ec1a12x1280x1375.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49ccf97ea5244311ab73927234f854f7x1280x1459.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9156ae8782146dc8f6dc5cb2a428b9dx1280x1557.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bowstring sign</strong></p><p>The image shows the examiner <strong>pressing</strong> the <strong>popliteal fossa</strong> of the patient to <strong>elicit sciatic pain</strong>. This is known as the <strong>Bowstring sign</strong>. </p>\n<p>Important tests for intervertebral disc prolapse:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">1. <strong>Straight Leg Raising Test</strong> (SLRT) or <strong>Lasegue sign</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Done to assess nerve root irritation in the lumbosacral area.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">  </span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Patient is made to lie on the back with the head extended, with hips and legs in the neutral position.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Affected<strong> leg passively</strong> and slowly <strong>raised</strong> by the ankle with the <strong>knee fully extended</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">SLRT test<strong> negative</strong> if leg can be <strong>raised 80–90 degrees</strong> without pain</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">SLRT test<strong> positive</strong> if <strong>pain elicited</strong> between <strong>30-60 </strong>or<strong> 70 degrees</strong> from horizontal</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pain should be radicular type and radiating to the back of the thigh or to the calf</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>2. Bragard test </strong>(sciatic stretch test or flip test):</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Perform SLRT</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">When the patient experiences discomfort, the leg is lowered a little till the pain disappears</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">At this angle, the <strong>ankle</strong> is <strong>passively dorsiflexed</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">If <strong>pain</strong> is felt again at the <strong>back of the thigh</strong> or in the calf, the test is <strong>positive</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Also called reinforcement positive or Bragard’s sign positive</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>3. Crossed SLRT </strong>(well-leg raising test or <strong>Fajersztajn sign</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>SLRT</strong> is performed on the <strong>unaffected side</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Pain</strong> felt on the <strong>affected side</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Termed a contralateral positive SLRT or crossed SLRT positive</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Very specific</strong> sign of root compression</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Usually positive when there is severe compression due to a centrally located prolapse</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>4. Popliteal compression </strong>test or<strong> Bowstring sign</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Bowstring sign is <strong>more specific</strong> for disc prolapse than SLRT</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Perform SLRT until the patient experiences sciatic pain</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Without reducing the amount of lift, bend the knee to relax the sciatic nerve</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Buttock pain is immediately relieved</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The examiner then <strong>presses popliteal fossa</strong> to stretch the lateral popliteal nerve behind the lateral tibial condyle</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Tightening of the nerve like a bowstring<strong> re-induces</strong> the <strong>pain</strong>  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>5. Femoral stretch </strong>(reverse Lasegue test or <strong>Mackiewicz sign</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Patient lies prone</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Examiner <strong>flexes</strong> the <strong>knee</strong> and the <strong>hip</strong> is <strong>passively extended</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Test is <strong>positive</strong> if patient experiences <strong>anterior thigh pain</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">This test is usually positive for L2-L3 and L3-L4 (<strong>high lumbar</strong>) <strong>protrusions</strong>  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2307","text":"What is the gold standard ‌investigation for evaluat​ion of low backache in a ​patient?","raw_text":"What is the gold standard investigation for evaluation of low backache in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌T","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Technetium 9‌9m bone scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DEXA‌ scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MRI</strong></p><p><strong>MRI</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> investigation of the spine for evaluation of a case of <strong>low backache</strong>.</p>\n<p>MRI spine can help rule out impingement of the spinal cord. It is <strong>superior to CT</strong> for the identification of<strong> infections</strong>, <strong>tumours</strong>, and <strong>degenerative changes</strong> within the discs.</p>\n<p>Other investigations done for low backache include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>X-rays have limited value in detecting disc prolapse. They are useful in diagnosing metabolic, degenerative, inflammatory and malignant conditions affecting the spine.</li>\n<li>Myelography is used for demonstrating blocks due to disc prolapse.</li>\n<li>CT scan helps to identify bone and soft tissue problems with greater accuracy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2297","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients with disc prolapse ​have an absolute indicati​on for surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with disc prolapse have an absolute indication for surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient experiencing debil‌itating sciatica","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient with sciatica not ‌improving with epidural s​teroid injection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient that is diagnosed ‌with cauda equina syndrom​e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patient with progressive l‌oss of sensation in the l​ower limbs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patient that is diagnosed with cauda equina syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patient that is diagnosed with cauda equina syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Cauda equina syndrome</strong> is the only <strong>absolute</strong> and urgent <strong>indication</strong> for<strong> surgery</strong> in disc prolapse.</p>\n<p>The <strong>relative indications</strong> for elective surgery in disc prolapse include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failed conservative management</li>\n<li>Progressive neurological deficit</li>\n<li>Recurrent episodes of incapacitating sciatica</li>\n<li>Pain not relieved by complete rest from activities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The aim of the surgery is to relieve the pressure on the nerve root. The prolapsed disc is removed (<strong>discectomy</strong>), followed by <strong>stabilization of the spine</strong>.  </p>\n<p>In <strong>most cases</strong> of disc prolapse, <strong>conservative treatment</strong> is given. Conservative therapy for this condition includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bed rest</li>\n<li>Heat</li>\n<li>Ice</li>\n<li>Analgesics</li>\n<li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)</li>\n<li>Antidepressants</li>\n<li>Muscle relaxants</li>\n<li>Epidural steroids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6682","text":"In spine fusion surgery, t‌he screws are fixed to wh​ich part of the vertebra?​","raw_text":"In spine fusion surgery, the screws are fixed to which part of the vertebra?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinous‌ process","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lam‌ina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ped‌icle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Facet‌ joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pedicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6beaf39ca3284402b00aa1e508d361cfx450x600.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4843ecbbe85a410b923f1a4d11119963x726x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pedicle</strong></p><p>In <strong>spine fusion</strong> surgeries, <strong>screws</strong> are placed <strong>in the</strong> <strong>pedicle</strong> of the vertebrae.</p>\n<p>Spinal fusion or <strong>spondylodesis</strong> is a surgical technique that <strong>joins</strong> two or more <strong>vertebrae</strong>. This procedure prevents any movement between the fused vertebrae.</p>\n<p><strong>Pedicle screws </strong>are placed above and below the<strong> </strong>vertebrae<strong> </strong>that are to be fused. These are used in a spinal fusion to <strong>add extra support</strong> and strength to the fusion <strong>while it heals</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Rods</strong> are used to <strong>connect</strong> the <strong>screws</strong> as shown below. These <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>movement</strong> and allow the bone to heal.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The post-operative X-ray given below shows fusion of L5 and S1 vertebrae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VplCuiMm","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"14_metabolic_disorders_of_amino_acids","title":"14.Metabolic Disorders Of Amino Acids","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MC3174","text":"A mother reports to the OP​D with the complaint that​ her child smells funny. ‍On examination, you notic‌e that the child has a gl‌obal developmental delay ‍and smells mousy. What is​ your probable diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"A mother reports to the OPD with the complaint that her child smells funny. On examination, you notice that the child has a global developmental delay and smells mousy. What is your probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylk​etonuria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tyros​inemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hawkin​sinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypermeth​ioninemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phenylketonuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phenylketonuria</strong></p><p><strong>Mousy odor</strong> is characteristically seen in <strong>phenylketonuria</strong> due to <strong>excess phenylacetate </strong>(phenyl ketone) formed in the body.</p>\n<p>In the absence of phenylalanine hydroxylase, phenylalanine enters into alternate metabolic pathways which result in the formation of phenyl lactate, phenylacetate, and phenylacetylglutamine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7675","text":"A boy who attends school i​s sent to the principal's​ office as he often distr‍acts others by rhythmical‌ly rocking back and forth‌ on his seat. His mother ‍worried about his behavio​r brings him to you. She ‍also gives you a history ‌of seizures. On examinati​on, you notice he has blo​nde hair, fair skin, micr​ocephaly, prominent maxil​lae with widely spaced te‌eth, and growth retardati‌on. His urine changes its​ color transiently to blu​e-green on the addition o‍f 1ml ferric chloride sol‌ution. What is the cause ​of CNS symptoms in this c​hild?","raw_text":"A boy who attends school is sent to the principal's office as he often distracts others by rhythmically rocking back and forth on his seat. His mother worried about his behavior brings him to you. She also gives you a history of seizures. On examination, you notice he has blonde hair, fair skin, microcephaly, prominent maxillae with widely spaced teeth, and growth retardation. His urine changes its color transiently to blue-green on the addition of 1ml ferric chloride solution. What is the cause of CNS symptoms in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpheny​lalaninemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotyr​osinemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenyl-​pyruvate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl-​acetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperphenylalaninemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperphenylalaninemia</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette describes a case of<strong> phenylketonuria. CNS damage</strong> in patients with phenylketonuria (PKU) <strong>is caused by hyperphenylalaninemia</strong> in brain tissue.</p>\n<p>High blood levels of phenylalanine<strong> saturate the transport system</strong> across the blood-brain barrier causing inhibition of the cerebral uptake of other large neutral amino acids such as tyrosine and tryptophan <strong>impairing</strong> brain protein synthesis. </p>\n<p>Microcephaly, prominent maxillae with widely spaced teeth, enamel hypoplasia, and growth retardation are other common findings in untreated children. Low bone mineral density and osteopenia have been reported in affected individuals of all ages.</p>\n<p>Excess phenylalanine is metabolized to<strong> phenyl ketones (phenylpyruvate and phenylacetate)</strong> that are excreted in the urine. However, these metabolites have <strong>no role</strong> in the pathogenesis of <strong>CNS damage</strong>; their presence in body fluids simply signifies the severity of the condition.</p>\n<p>Hypotyrosinemia can be partially responsible for the signs and symptoms but the primary cause is hyperphenylalaninemia.</p>\n<p>Laboratory diagnoses of phenylketonuria include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Guthrie's test: growth of bacillus subtilis is seen</li>\n<li>Ferric chloride urine test: transient blue-green color </li>\n<li>Blood phenylalanine levels: elevated</li>\n<li>Enzyme studies</li>\n<li>Genetic mutation studies</li>\n<li>Tandem mass spectrometry: Gold standard screening method for all metabolic disorders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7671","text":"An infant with severe vomi​ting who was misdiagnosed​ as a case of hypertrophi‍c pyloric stenosis is bei‌ng screened for metabolic‌ disorders. Screening rev‍eals hyperphenylalaninemi​a. On ordering an enzyme ‍study, you would include ‌all of the following enzy​mes except:","raw_text":"An infant with severe vomiting who was misdiagnosed as a case of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is being screened for metabolic disorders. Screening reveals hyperphenylalaninemia. On ordering an enzyme study, you would include all of the following enzymes except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase ​(PAH)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydropteridine reductase​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dihydroptero​ate synthase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GTP cyclo​hydrolase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dihydropteroate synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dihydropteroate synthase</strong></p><p><strong>Defects</strong> of either <strong>phenylalanine hydroxylase</strong> (PAH) or <strong>tetrahydrobiopterin(BH4)</strong> can cause hyperphenylalaninemias.</p>\n<p>PAH deficiency is diagnosed as <strong>phenylketonuria.</strong></p>\n<p>Malignant hyperphenylalaninemias include three enzyme defects, dihydropteridine reductase,pyruvoyl tetrahydropterin synthase, and GTP cyclohydrolase which help in BH4 synthesis. The deficiency of BH4 impairs three hydroxylase(phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan), and severe neurological symptoms develop because of the lack of neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p><strong>Dihydropteroate synthase</strong> is present only in <strong>bacteria</strong> and this makes it a useful target for<strong> sulfonamide antibiotics</strong>, which compete with the PABA precursor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7676","text":"As a physician treating a ​2-month-old baby with phe​nylketonuria what will be‍ your mainstay of treatme‌nt?","raw_text":"As a physician treating a 2-month-old baby with phenylketonuria what will be your mainstay of treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylalani​ne–free diet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low phenyla​lanine diet","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High phenyl​alanine diet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyrosine sup​plementation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low phenylalanine diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low phenylalanine diet</strong></p><p><strong>A low-phenylalanine diet</strong> is the mainstay of treatment of PKU.</p>\n<p>The goal of therapy is to reduce phenylalanine levels in the plasma and brain. Because phenylalanine is not synthesized endogenously, <strong>small amounts of phenylalanine should be added</strong> to the diet to prevent phenylalanine deficiency. Dietary deficiency of this amino acid is manifested by lethargy, failure to thrive, anorexia, anemia, rashes, diarrhea, and even death; moreover, tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid in this disorder and its adequate intake must be ensured.</p>\n<p><strong>Sapropterin dihydrochloride</strong> , a synthetic form of <strong>BH4</strong>, which acts as a cofactor in patients with residual PAH activity, is approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to reduce phenylalanine levels in PKU.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7677","text":"An infant born out of a se​cond-degree consanguineou​s marriage presents with ‍jaundice, irritability, a‌nd vomiting. His mother g‌ives a history of fever f‍or the past 4 days. On ex​amination, petechiae are ‍present, he has hepatomeg‌aly and extensor hyperton​ia of the neck and trunk.​ Labs reveal elevated ser​um transaminases, hypopho​sphatemia, and elevated l‌evels of succinylacetone ‌in serum and urine. Which​ of the following enzymes​ is deficient?","raw_text":"An infant born out of a second-degree consanguineous marriage presents with jaundice, irritability, and vomiting. His mother gives a history of fever for the past 4 days. On examination, petechiae are present, he has hepatomegaly and extensor hypertonia of the neck and trunk. Labs reveal elevated serum transaminases, hypophosphatemia, and elevated levels of succinylacetone in serum and urine. Which of the following enzymes is deficient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyrosine h​ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine ami​notransferase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-H​PPD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fumarylacetoacetate hydrol​ase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15442df649224826ba9b3a91b75753a2x1280x2566.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette describes a case of<strong> Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1</strong> is caused by the deficiency of <strong>fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase</strong> (FAAH).</p>\n<p>Elevated levels of <strong>succinylacetone</strong> in serum and urine are diagnostic for tyrosinemia type I.</p>\n<p><strong>Liver</strong>(jaundice, acute hepatic crisis, elevated serum transaminases), <strong>kidney</strong>(hypophosphatemia), and <strong>nerve damage</strong>(extensor hypertonia of neck and trunk) are caused by metabolites of tyrosine degradation, especially fumarylacetoacetate and succinylacetone, and is usually precipitated by minor infections.</p>\n<p>A diet low in phenylalanine and tyrosine can slow but does not halt the progression of the condition. The treatment of choice is<strong> nitisinone (NTBC)</strong>, which inhibits 4-HPPD and reduces the flux of tyrosine metabolites to FAH, thus decreasing the production of the offending compounds, fumarylacetoacetate, and succinylacetone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7678","text":"A father while changing hi​s child's diaper observes​ a boiled cabbage odor em‍anating from it. Curious ‌about the smell, he calls‌ you. As a pediatrician, ‍you investigate the child​ thoroughly and conclude ‍that the child is sufferi‌ng from Type 1 Tyrosinemi​a. As the child grows all​ of the following complic​ations can occur except:","raw_text":"A father while changing his child's diaper observes a boiled cabbage odor emanating from it. Curious about the smell, he calls you. As a pediatrician, you investigate the child thoroughly and conclude that the child is suffering from Type 1 Tyrosinemia. As the child grows all of the following complications can occur except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocellul​ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paralyt​ic ileus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fanconi-li​ke syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal u​lceration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Corneal ulceration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Corneal ulceration</strong></p><p><strong>Corneal ulceration i</strong>s not a feature of hereditary tyrosinemia type 1. In fact, ocular manifestations are a feature of<strong> hereditary tyrosinemia type 2 </strong>which presumably occur due to tyrosine deposition.</p>\n<p><strong>Boiled cabbage odor</strong> may be present in tyrosinemia resulting from increased <strong>methionine metabolites</strong> (sulfur-containing compounds).</p>\n<p><strong>Features of hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (hepatorenal tyrosinemia)</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>acute hepatic crisis</strong> commonly heralds the onset of disease, which may progress to liver failure,<strong> hepatocellular carcinoma</strong>, and death.</li>\n<li>Episodes of <strong>acute peripheral neuropathy resembling porphyrias</strong> often triggered by a minor infection, such crisis can be associated with<strong> paralytic ileus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Renal involvement,</strong> which is manifested as a <strong>Fanconi-like syndrome</strong> with hyperphosphaturia, hypophosphatemia, normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, and vitamin D–resistant rickets</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The treatment of choice for HT Type 1 is nitisinone,</strong> which blocks early steps in the tyrosine degradation pathway and limits the formation of toxic tyrosine metabolites.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Boiled cabbage odor can also be found in cases of <strong>Hypermethioninemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7680","text":"A 2-year-old girl presente​d with photophobia and sp​eech delay. On examinatio‍n, she had bilateral herp‌etiform corneal ulcers an‌d painful hyperkeratotic ‍plaques on the palms and ​soles. Which among the fo‍llowing is the most likel‌y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl presented with photophobia and speech delay. On examination, she had bilateral herpetiform corneal ulcers and painful hyperkeratotic plaques on the palms and soles. Which among the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HSV t​ype 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HSV t​ype 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary Tyrosinemia Typ​e 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary Tyrosinemia Typ​e 2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hereditary Tyrosinemia Type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3592349a17649a495f7368eaed11e0cx853x788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hereditary Tyrosinemia Type 2</strong></p><p>Palmar and plantar hyperkeratosis, herpetiform corneal ulcers, and intellectual disability are seen in <strong>hereditary tyrosinemia type 2</strong> <strong>(Richner–Hanhart syndrome</strong>/ oculocutaneous tyrosinemia)</p>\n<p>Treatment with a diet low in tyrosine and phenylalanine aiming to achieve plasma tyrosine levels &lt;500 µmol/L improves skin and eye manifestations.</p>\n<p>The image below shows herpetiform corneal ulcer:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7681","text":"A child who was being eval​uated for hypertyrosinemi​a is diagnosed with Richn‍er-Hanhart syndrome. Whic‌h of the following enzyme‌ deficiencies is the like‍ly cause of hypertyrosine​mia in this patient?","raw_text":"A child who was being evaluated for hypertyrosinemia is diagnosed with Richner-Hanhart syndrome. Which of the following enzyme deficiencies is the likely cause of hypertyrosinemia in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyrosine h​ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine ami​notransferase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4-Hydroxyphenylpyruvate di​oxygenase (HPPD)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fumarylacetoacetate hydrol​ase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tyrosine aminotransferase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/693a33ca3b6a4737b4fa7e69784ef75bx1280x2566.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tyrosine aminotransferase</strong></p><p><strong>Oculocutaneous tyrosinemia/Richner-Hanhart syndrome</strong> (type II) is caused by the deficiency of<strong> tyrosine aminotransferase</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3146\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by palmar and plantar hyperkeratosis, herpetiform corneal ulcers, and intellectual disability.Untreated patients have marked <strong>hypertyrosinemia. </strong>In contrast to tyrosinemia type I, liver and kidney function are normal, as are serum concentrations of other amino acids and succinylacetone.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7682","text":"A 15-month-old baby who pr​esented with seizures and​ self-injurious behavior ‍has increased plasma tyro‌sine levels. Urine metabo‌lic screening reveals the‍ presence of 4-hydroxyphe​nylpyruvic acid and its m‍etabolites 4-hydroxypheny‌llactic and 4-hydroxyphen​yl acetic acids. Which of​ the following in-born er​rors of metabolism is the​ child suffering from?","raw_text":"A 15-month-old baby who presented with seizures and self-injurious behavior has increased plasma tyrosine levels. Urine metabolic screening reveals the presence of 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvic acid and its metabolites 4-hydroxyphenyllactic and 4-hydroxyphenyl acetic acids. Which of the following in-born errors of metabolism is the child suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary tyrosinemia typ​e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary tyrosinemia typ​e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary tyrosinemia typ​e 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hawkin​sinuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hereditary tyrosinemia type 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba209c653194433b8b58f9631be18047x1280x2566.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hereditary tyrosinemia type 3</strong></p><p>Primary deficiency of <strong>4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (4-HPPD)</strong> causes <strong>hereditary tyrosinemia type 3. </strong>The diagnosis is suspected in children with sustained moderate<strong> increases in plasma levels of tyrosine (</strong>typically 350-700 µmol/L on a normal diet) and the presence of <strong>4-hydroxyphenylpyruvic acid and its metabolites</strong> 4-hydroxyphenyllactic and 4-hydroxyphenylacetic acids <strong>in urine.</strong></p>\n<p>Asymptomatic infants with 4-HPPD deficiency have been identified by neonatal screening for hypertyrosinemia. In symptomatic patients, developmental delay, seizures, intermittent ataxia, and self-injurious behavior have been reported. Liver and renal abnormalities are absent.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Transient tyrosinemia of the newborn</strong> is the most common acquired cause for increased plasma tyrosine levels. Seen in <strong>premature infants</strong> receiving a<strong> high protein diet</strong> and <strong>due to the immaturity of 4-HPPD enzyme. </strong>Mostly <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, but sometimes manifested by lethargy, poor feeding, and decreased motor activity. Hypertyrosinemia usually <strong>resolves</strong><br/><strong>spontaneously</strong> in the<strong> first 2 months of life. </strong>Treatment includes a low protein diet and vitamin C.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7648","text":"A 4-month-old infant who w​as started on a high-prot​ein diet after weaning fr‍om breastfeeding develope‌d severe metabolic acidos‌is, ketosis, failure to t‍hrive, mild hepatomegaly,​ and an unusual swimming ‍pool odor. A history of s‌imilar illness was notice​d in his siblings and his​ father. What is the like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old infant who was started on a high-protein diet after weaning from breastfeeding developed severe metabolic acidosis, ketosis, failure to thrive, mild hepatomegaly, and an unusual swimming pool odor. A history of similar illness was noticed in his siblings and his father. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maple syrup ​urine disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isovaleri​c acidemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hawkin​sinuria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylk​etonuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hawkinsinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hawkinsinuria</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette and the characteristic<strong> swimming pool odor</strong> describe <strong>Hawkinsinuria</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hawkinsinuria </strong><strong>is an autosomal-dominant</strong> condition caused by <strong>mutations in the gene for 4-HPPD</strong>.</p>\n<p>In infants, this disease causes severe metabolic acidosis, ketosis, failure to thrive, mild hepatomegaly, and swimming pool odor. Mental development is usually normal. Affected individuals excrete <strong>hawkinsin</strong> (4-hydroxyphenylpyruvic acid), and its metabolites.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>consists of a <strong>low-protein diet during infancy</strong>. Breastfeeding is encouraged. A trial with large doses of vitamin C (up to 1,000 mg/24 hour) is also recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7685","text":"The following findings in ​an 18-year-old are charac​terized by a deficiency o‍f _________","raw_text":"The following findings in an 18-year-old are characterized by a deficiency of _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta-c​arotene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine ami​notransferase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L-ascor​bic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homogentisic​ acid oxidase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Homogentisic acid oxidase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3628da1061f46b1ac88b36975e7a73f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Homogentisic acid oxidase</strong></p><p>The following condition is characterized by the <strong>deficiency of homogentisic acid oxidase.</strong></p>\n<p>The image represents <strong>ochronosis:</strong> Bluish-black pigmentation of the ear cartilage and/or sclera due to deposits of accumulated homogentisic acid.</p>\n<p>This is characteristically seen in<strong> alkaptonuria. </strong>It is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder is caused by the <strong>deficiency of homogentisic acid oxidase. </strong>Large amounts of homogentisic acid are formed, which are excreted in urine or deposited in tissues. Clinical manifestations of alkaptonuria consist of <strong>ochronosis and arthritis in adulthood</strong>. Arthritis predominantly involves the spine, hip, and knee. Radiologically, it is characterized by <strong>intervertebral calcifications. </strong>In <strong>children, blackening of the urine on standing</strong> is observed; it is caused by the oxidation and polymerization of homogentisic acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7686","text":"A patient presented to the​ OPD with complaints of p​igmentation of the face a‍nd severe backache and st‌iffness. The X-Ray image ‌of the spine is as shown.‍ Her urine color changes ​to brownish-black on stan‍ding. Which of the follow‌ing drugs can be used in ​her treatment?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with complaints of pigmentation of the face and severe backache and stiffness. The X-Ray image of the spine is as shown. Her urine color changes to brownish-black on standing. Which of the following drugs can be used in her treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitis​inone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thia​mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Folic​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrid​oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nitisinone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a1a699fb62c84247ac6fca75c713bfaf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82bdc630e0a746fca5aea7b05dd9eedex425x394.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nitisinone</strong></p><p>The clinical image shows the accumulation of<strong> alkapton bodies in connective tissues </strong>on the face and <strong>urine turning brownish-black </strong>on standing are associated with<strong> alkaptonuria. </strong><strong>X-ray </strong>shows<strong> calcification of intervertebral discs</strong> called <strong>ochronotic spondyloarthropathy</strong> also seen in <strong>alkaptonuria. Nitisinone can be used in the treatment of alkaptonuria. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nitisinone,</strong> formerly known as NTBC, inhibits 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPPD) (early step in the tyrosine degradation pathway) and efficiently reduces homogentisic acid production in alkaptonuria. Treatment of arthritis is symptomatic. In presymptomatic individuals, treatment using a combination of nitisinone with phenylalanine and a tyrosine restricted diet can be tried.</p>\n<p>The patient’s urine contains elevated levels of homogentisic acid, which is oxidized to a dark pigment on standing as shown below in the image:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7687","text":"A 10-year-old boy presente​d with right-sided weakne​ss and deviation of angle‍ of mouth to the left is ‌diagnosed to have a metab‌olic disorder. Diffusion-‍weighted MRI reveals diff​usion restriction along t‍he left middle cerebral a‌rtery. Which of the follo​wing defects can be respo​nsible for the boy's cond​ition? Cystathionine β-Sy​nthase deficiency Defects‌ in methylcobalamin forma‌tion Deficiency of methyl​enetetrahydrofolate reduc​tase Pyridoxine deficienc‍y Vitamin C deficiency","raw_text":"<p>A 10-year-old boy presented with right-sided weakness and deviation of angle of mouth to the left is diagnosed to have a metabolic disorder. Diffusion-weighted MRI reveals diffusion restriction along the left middle cerebral artery. Which of the following defects can be responsible for the boy's condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cystathionine β-Synthase deficiency</li>\n<li>Defects in methylcobalamin formation</li>\n<li>Deficiency of methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase</li>\n<li>Pyridoxine deficiency</li>\n<li>Vitamin C deficiency</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,​3,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​2,3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2​,3,4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7489347dbfa1440f9032a8fafda3a616x1280x1922.2582921665492.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette describes a<strong> thrombotic stroke in the left MCA territory</strong>. <strong>Elevated</strong> levels of <strong>serum homocysteine</strong> lead to a <strong>hypercoagulable state</strong>. </p>\n<p>Homocystinuria is caused by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cystathionine β-Synthase</strong> deficiency: This is <strong>classic homocystinuria</strong>. The most common inborn error of methionine metabolism.</li>\n<li><strong>Defects in methylcobalamin formation</strong>: Methylcobalamin is the cofactor for the enzyme methionine synthase, which catalyzes the remethylation of homocysteine to methionine.</li>\n<li><strong>Deficiency of methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase</strong>: This enzyme reduces 5,10 methylenetetrahydrofolate to form 5-methyltetrahydrofolate, which provides the methyl group needed for remethylation of homocysteine to methionine.</li>\n<li><strong>Pyridoxine deficiency</strong>: B6 is the <strong>co-factor</strong> for the enzyme <strong>cystathionine β-synthase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Metabolic disorders which can cause stroke include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Homocystinuria</strong></li>\n<li>MELAS (metabolic encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes)</li>\n<li>Leigh syndrome</li>\n<li>Fabry's disease</li>\n<li>Propionic acidemia</li>\n<li>Glutaric aciduria</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Vitamin C deficiency increases the risk of hemorrhagic stroke.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7688","text":"An 8-month-old boy present​ed with lethargy, failure​ to thrive, and developme‍ntal delay. On investigat‌ion, he had elevated leve‌ls of homocysteine in blo‍od and homocystinuria. As​ the child grows, which o‍f the following features ‌is least likely to develo​p?","raw_text":"An 8-month-old boy presented with lethargy, failure to thrive, and developmental delay. On investigation, he had elevated levels of homocysteine in blood and homocystinuria. As the child grows, which of the following features is least likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopia​ lentis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intellectua​l disability","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Short ​stature","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myocardial​ infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Short stature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Short stature</strong></p><p>The above findings support a diagnosis of <strong>homocystinuria.</strong> <strong>Short stature</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a manifestation of homocystinuria.</p>\n<p>Manifestations of homocystinuria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ectopia lentis</strong> - downward and inward subluxation (<strong>inferonasal).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intellectual disability</strong> and <strong>psychiatric and behavioral</strong> disorders</li>\n<li>Thromboembolic episodes like stroke and<strong> MI</strong></li>\n<li>Skeletal abnormalities resembling those of Marfan syndrome: <strong>They are tall and thin, (NOT short)</strong> with elongated limbs and arachnodactyly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9464","text":"A 16-year-old boy with fre​quent episodes of photose​nsitive dermatitis, diarr‍hea, and depression is di‌agnosed to have a metabol‌ic disorder. Metabolism o‍f which of the following ​amino acids is affected?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy with frequent episodes of photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and depression is diagnosed to have a metabolic disorder. Metabolism of which of the following amino acids is affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyro​sine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypt​ophan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thre​onine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl​alanine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tryptophan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1d3c85dff1a4d959927c5fcf7c9944dx1280x1266.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tryptophan</strong></p><p><strong>Pellagra-like symptoms</strong>- photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and episodic depression are seen in the <strong>deficiency of tryptophan.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Niacin</strong> is not strictly a vitamin, since it can be <strong>synthesized</strong> in the body <strong>from</strong> the essential amino acid <strong>tryptophan;</strong> deficiency of tryptophan causes <strong>pellagra</strong>-like symptoms.</p>\n<p>Treatment with n<strong>icotinic acid or nicotinamide (50-300 mg/24 hr)</strong> and a <strong>high protein diet</strong> results in a favorable response in symptomatic patients with <strong>Hartnup disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>Casal's necklace,</strong> a lesion seen around the neck which is caused due to the deficiency of niacin or nicotinic acid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7689","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding Hartnup d​isease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding Hartnup disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defect in the transport of​ monoamino-monocarboxylic​ amino acids, including t‍ryptophan, is seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defect in the B0AT1 protei​n occurs in the intestine​ and renal tubules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pellagra-like rash may dev​elop in symptomatic patie​nts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment is with suppleme​ntation of tryptophan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Treatment is with supplementation of tryptophan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Treatment is with supplementation of tryptophan</strong></p><p>The treatment of Hartnup disease is not with the supplementation of tryptophan.</p>\n<p><strong>Hartnup disease</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is caused by a <strong>defect in the transport of monoamino-monocarboxylic (neutral) amino acids,</strong> including <strong>tryptophan</strong>, by the intestinal mucosa and renal tubules.</li>\n<li>There is a defect in  <strong>B0AT1 protein</strong> in the intestine/ renal tubules or both, which is caused by a mutation in <em><strong>SLC6A19.</strong></em></li>\n<li>Tryptophan is an essential amino acid and a precursor for nicotinic acid (niacin) and serotonin. Decreased intestinal absorption of tryptophan in conjunction with its increased renal loss causes reduced availability of tryptophan for niacin synthesis in affected individuals.</li>\n<li><strong>A pellagra-like rash</strong> may develop in symptomatic patients, and some patients may have intermittent ataxia.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is based on aminoaciduria and increased excretion of indole compounds detected by <strong>Obermeyer test</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment is with nicotinic acid or nicotinamide</strong> and a high-protein diet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7661","text":"An infant with poor feedin​g is suspected to have an​ inborn error of metaboli‍sm. Further evaluation re‌veals hyperammonemia +, a‌cidosis +, ketosis +. Wha‍t is the most likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"An infant with poor feeding is suspected to have an inborn error of metabolism. Further evaluation reveals hyperammonemia +, acidosis +, ketosis +. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyros​inemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact​osemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylk​etonuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Organic ​aciduria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Organic aciduria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c94e0de48234302b7988871f127ec04x1280x2560.0.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Organic aciduria</strong></p><p>In this case, <strong>hyperammonemia +, acidosis +, ketosis +. </strong>This is seen in <strong>organic aciduria</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Organic acidemias</strong> most commonly present in infancy with poor feeding, vomiting, hypotonia, high anion gap metabolic acidosis, hepatomegaly, seizures. Organic acid accumulation <strong>inhibits gluconeogenesis</strong> and <strong>inhibits the urea cycle</strong> leading to hyperammonemia.</p>\n<p>Common organic acidurias:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methylmalonic aciduria</li>\n<li>Propionic aciduria</li>\n<li>Isovaleric aciduria</li>\n<li>Maple syrup urine disease</li>\n<li>Glutaric aciduria type I</li>\n<li>Multiple carboxylase deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7691","text":"A 10-day-old male infant p​resented with poor feedin​g, and recurrent generali‍zed convulsions. On exami‌nation, the pediatrician ‌notices repetitive moveme‍nts of the extremities –a​ppears to be boxing and b‍icycling and a peculiar b‌urnt sugar odor was prese​nt in urine. What is the ​deficient enzyme in this ​condition?","raw_text":"A 10-day-old male infant presented with poor feeding, and recurrent generalized convulsions. On examination, the pediatrician notices repetitive movements of the extremities –appears to be boxing and bicycling and a peculiar burnt sugar odor was present in urine. What is the deficient enzyme in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Branched-chain α-ketoacid ​dehydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isovaleryl CoA dehydrogena​se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate c​arboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetyl CoA ​carboxylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase</strong></p><p>The diagnosis is maple syrup urine disease (<strong>MSUD</strong>) which is due to deficiency of<strong> branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase (BCKDH)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Leucine, isoleucine, and valine are the 3 essential amino acids, which are branched-chain amino acids. Their decarboxylation is accomplished by BCKDH using <strong>thiamine</strong> (vitamin B1) pyrophosphate as a <strong>coenzyme</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Leucine</strong> is metabolized by BCAA aminotransferase to<strong> α-ketoisocaproic acid</strong>, which leads to the disrupted metabolism of neurotransmitters and amino acids (glutamate, GABA, glutamine, alanine, and aspartate). α-Ketoisocaproic acid can reversibly inhibit oxidative phosphorylation and result in cerebral lactic acidosis. Collectively, these processes are detrimental to the normal function of neurons and glia, clinically manifesting as encephalopathy and brain edema and referred to as<strong> leucinosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment options include hemodialysis, restricting dietary substances, thiamine for milder forms, and orthotopic liver transplant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7650","text":"While doing your morning r​ounds in the pediatrics d​epartment, you notice a p‍eculiar rancid cheese odo‌r emanating from a six-mo‌nth-old girl who was admi‍tted for respiratory dist​ress. Second-degree consa‍nguinity was documented b‌etween the parents. Urine​ organic acid analysis by​ gas chromatography-mass ​spectrometry showed marke​d excretion of 3-hydroxyb‌utyric acid along with mo‌derate excretion of 3- hy​droxy-isovaleric acid. Wh​at is the most likely con‍dition is she suffering f‌rom?","raw_text":"While doing your morning rounds in the pediatrics department, you notice a  peculiar rancid cheese odor emanating from a six-month-old girl who was admitted for respiratory distress. Second-degree consanguinity was documented between the parents. Urine organic acid analysis by gas chromatography-mass spectrometry showed marked excretion of 3-hydroxybutyric acid along with moderate excretion of 3- hydroxy-isovaleric acid. What is the most likely condition is she suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyros​inemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple carboxylase defic​iency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trimethy​laminuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isovaleri​c acidemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Isovaleric acidemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Isovaleric acidemia</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette suggests a diagnosis of <strong>isovaleric acidemia. Sweaty-feet</strong> odor or <strong>rancid cheese odor</strong> may be present.</p>\n<p>Isovaleric acidemia is caused by the <strong>deficiency</strong> of the <strong>enzyme isovaleryl CoA dehydrogenase</strong>, which affects the breakdown pathway of <strong>l</strong><strong>eucine </strong>leading to the accumulation of intermediates.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7660","text":"A mother brings her 3-year​-old boy with the complai​nts that her child's urin‍e smells like her tomcat'‌s urine. She also gives a‌ history of generalized s‍eizures and perioral desq​uamating lesions. On exam‍ination, the child has gl‌obal developmental delay ​and diffuse thinning of t​he scalp. Investigations ​reveal multiple carboxyla​se deficiency. What is th‌e treatment of choice?","raw_text":"A mother brings her  3-year-old boy with the complaints that her child's urine smells like her tomcat's urine. She also gives a history of generalized seizures and perioral desquamating lesions. On examination, the child has global developmental delay and diffuse thinning of the scalp. Investigations reveal multiple carboxylase deficiency. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrid​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bio​tin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methylc​obalamin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biotin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biotin</strong></p><p><strong>Biotin</strong> is the treatment of choice for <strong>multiple carboxylase deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple carboxylase deficiency refers to one of two inherited <strong>defects of biotin metabolism. Tomcat urine</strong> is characteristically seen in multiple carboxylase deficiency.</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Neonatal/infantile</strong> form is caused by a deficiency of holocarboxylase synthetase and presents in the first week of life with lethargy, poor muscle tone, and vomiting.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Juvenile/late</strong> form is caused by <strong>biotinidase deficiency</strong> and is associated with a slow but progressive loss of biotin in the urine, leading to organic aciduria. It is characterized by <strong>alopecia</strong>, ataxia, ketoacidosis, <strong>dermatitis, seizures</strong>, myoclonus, and nystagmus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both infantile and late-onset multiple carboxylase deficiency can be treated with pharmacological doses of biotin.</p>\n<p>It is called multiple carboxylase deficiency because biotin is a water-soluble vitamin that is a <strong>cofactor for all 4 carboxylase enzymes</strong> in humans:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyruvate carboxylase</li>\n<li>Acetyl CoA carboxylase</li>\n<li>Propionyl CoA carboxylase</li>\n<li>3-methylcrotonyl CoA carboxylase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p><strong>Acquired deficiency of biotin</strong> may occur in infants receiving <strong>total parenteral nutrition without added biotin</strong>, in patients with <strong>prolonged use of antiepileptic drugs (phenobarbital, phenytoin, primidone, carbamazepine</strong>), and in children with short bowel syndrome or chronic diarrhea who are receiving formulas low in biotin.<strong> Excessive ingestion of raw eggs</strong> may also cause biotin deficiency because the protein avidin in egg white binds biotin, decreasing its absorption. Infants with a biotin deficiency may develop dermatitis, alopecia, and candidal skin infections. This condition readily responds to treatment with oral biotin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7684","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common acquired ​cause of increased plasma‍ tyrosine levels?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common acquired cause of increased plasma tyrosine levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatorenal​ tyrosinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oculocutaneous tyrosinemia​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alkapt​onuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transient tyrosinemia of t​he newborn","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transient tyrosinemia of the newborn","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transient tyrosinemia of the newborn</strong></p><p>Transient tyrosinemia of the newborn is the most common acquired cause for increased plasma tyrosine levels. Seen in premature infants receiving a high protein diet and <strong>due to the immaturity of 4-HPPD enzyme. </strong>Mostly asymptomatic, but sometimes manifested by lethargy, poor feeding, and decreased motor activity. Treatment includes low protein diet and vitamin C.</p>\n<p>Options A and B, i.e., hepatorenal tyrosinemia and oculocutaneous tyrosinemia, are hereditary causes of increased tyrosine levels.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tyrosine levels are normal in alkaptonuria.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7679","text":"Richner-Hanhart syndrome i​s _____.","raw_text":"Richner-Hanhart syndrome is  _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary tyrosinemia typ​e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary tyrosinemia typ​e 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary tyrosinemia typ​e 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hawkin​sinuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hereditary tyrosinemia type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hereditary tyrosinemia type 2</strong></p><p><strong>Richner–Hanhart syndrome is hereditary tyrosinemia type 2 (oculocutaneous tyrosinemia).</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder is caused by the deficiency of <strong>tyrosine aminotransferase</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is characterized by ocular manifestations, skin lesions, and mild to moderate intellectual disability in ~50% of the patients.</li>\n<li>It is diagnosed by an assay of plasma tyrosine concentration.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment </strong>includes a diet low in tyrosine and phenylalanine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7673","text":"In phenylketonuria, which ​of the following phenyl k​etones is excreted in uri‍ne?","raw_text":"In phenylketonuria, which of the following phenyl ketones is excreted in urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenyl​alanine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenylp​yruvate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acetoa​cetate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both b​ and c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenylpyruvate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenylpyruvate</strong></p><p>In phenylketonuria, <strong>phenylpyruvate</strong> is excreted in the urine.</p>\n<p>In phenylketonuria (PKU), excess phenylalanine in the blood is metabolized to phenyl <strong>ketones,</strong> namely, <strong>phenylpyruvate</strong> and <strong>phenylacetate</strong>, which are excreted in the urine, giving rise to the term <strong>phenylketonuria</strong> (PKU).</p>\n<p>Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate are keto-acids but are not phenyl ketones. These are not excreted in phenylketonuria.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7683","text":"A 2-month-old infant prese​nted with abdominal diste​ntion and increased irrit‍ability for 10 days. Phys‌ical examination showed j‌aundice, hepatomegaly, an‍d a boiled cabbage odor. ​Which among the following‍ is the most likely diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old infant presented with abdominal distention and increased irritability for 10 days. Physical examination showed jaundice, hepatomegaly, and a boiled cabbage odor. Which among the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyros​inemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isovaleri​c acidemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hawkin​sinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylk​etonuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tyrosinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tyrosinemia</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is in favor of<strong> tyrosinemia</strong>. <strong>Boiled cabbage odor</strong> may be present in tyrosinemia resulting from increased <strong>methionine metabolites</strong> (sulfur-containing compounds).</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Boiled cabbage odor can also be found in cases of <strong>Hypermethioninemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7651","text":"A neonate born out of a se​cond-degree consanguineou​s marriage is suspected t‍o have an inborn error of‌ metabolism. He can prese‌nt with all of the follow‍ing except:","raw_text":"A neonate born out of a second-degree consanguineous marriage is suspected to have an inborn error of metabolism. He can present with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irritability and incessant​ crying","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Poor f​eeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vomi​ting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Convu​lsions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Irritability and incessant crying","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Irritability and incessant crying</strong></p><p><strong>Irritability and incessant crying</strong> are not classical features of inborn errors of metabolism (IEM).</p>\n<p>Clinical clues suggesting IEM in a <strong>neonate</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unexpected deterioration</strong> after a normal initial period.</li>\n<li>Nonspecific, unexplained features such as poor feeding, lethargy, vomiting, hypotonia, failure to thrive, respiratory abnormalities, apnea, bradycardia, and hypothermia. </li>\n<li><strong>Family history</strong> of similar illnesses, unexplained sibling deaths, or progressive neurological disease.</li>\n<li><strong>Parental consanguinity:</strong> Most acutely presenting metabolic disorders are autosomal recessive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8443","text":"A worried mother brings he​r 2-year-old child with t​he complaints that he has‍n't begun walking or talk‌ing. On examination, ther‌e is microcephaly, fair h‍air, light-colored skin, ​and blue eyes. What is th‍e most probable diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A worried mother brings her 2-year-old child with the complaints that he hasn't begun walking or talking. On examination, there is microcephaly, fair hair, light-colored skin, and blue eyes. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oculocutane​ous albinism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenylk​etonuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alkapt​onuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tyros​inemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenylketonuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenylketonuria</strong></p><p>The given clinical manifestations point to the diagnosis of <strong>Phenylketonuria </strong>(PKU).</p>\n<p>Characteristic clinical findings in Phenylketonuria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mental retardation</li>\n<li><strong>Failure to walk or talk</strong></li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Hyperactivity</li>\n<li>Tremor</li>\n<li><strong>Microcephaly</strong></li>\n<li>Failure to grow</li>\n<li><strong>Deficiency of pigmentation (fair hair, light skin color, and blue eyes)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Oculocutaneous albinism</strong> (OCA) is a group of inherited disorders characterized by a reduction or complete lack of melanin pigment in the skin, hair, and eyes.</p>\n<p>Its symptoms are <strong>albinism with vision problems, </strong>which include:</p>\n<div class=\"RqBzHd\">\n<ul class=\"i8Z77e\">\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Strabismus</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Photophobia</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Nystagmus</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Impaired vision or blindness</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Astigmatism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neuropsychiatric manifestations are not seen.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Em9c5EGD","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"14_components_of_immune_system","title":"14.Components Of Immune System","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6780","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of innate‍ immunity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of innate immunity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Natural ki‌ller cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C-reactiv‌e protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B c‌ells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d320f75b27d242568e3dfde52a773d95x1280x1626.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B cells</strong></p><p><strong>B cell</strong> is not a component of innate immunity. It is a component of<strong> adaptive immunity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Innate immunity</strong> refers to the body’s<strong> natural immunity. </strong>It is a built-in defence system that is present since birth. It differs from adaptive immunity in that it does not require any prior sensitization to an antigen.</p>\n<p>The major <strong>components</strong> of <strong>innate immunity</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelial barriers that block the entry of microbes</li>\n<li>Phagocytic cells (<strong>neutrophils</strong> and macrophages)</li>\n<li><strong>Natural killer</strong> (NK) cells</li>\n<li>Dendritic cell</li>\n<li>Cells that can sense and react to microbes - mast cells, epithelial and endothelial cells</li>\n<li>Plasma proteins - complement system factors, mannose-binding lectin, and <strong>C-reactive protein</strong></li>\n<li>Innate lymphoid cells  </li>\n<li>Lung surfactant </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6784","text":"What is the mechanism by w‌hich toll-like receptors ‍stimulate immune response​ against bacterial produc‌ts?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism by which toll-like receptors stimulate immune response against bacterial products?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perforin and granzyme-medi‌ated apoptosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FADD ligan‌d apoptosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NFκB mediated transcriptio‌n of nuclear factor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"RIPK3 mediated necroptosis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NFκB mediated transcription of nuclear factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac829ca281b844a0a93ab5ae15038d6dx1280x1399.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NFκB mediated transcription of nuclear factor</strong></p><p><strong>Toll-like receptors</strong> (TLRs) stimulate immune response by <strong>NFκB</strong> <strong>mediated</strong> transcription of nuclear factors.</p>\n<p>TLRs are pattern recognition receptors which activate two sets of <strong>transcription factors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NF-κB</strong> - stimulates the synthesis and secretion of cytokines and the expression of adhesion molecules. Both are critical for the recruitment and activation of leukocytes</li>\n<li><strong>Interferon regulatory factors </strong>- stimulate the production of the antiviral cytokines like type I interferons</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a toll-like receptor.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The various classes of innate immune <strong>receptors</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 74px; width: 617.125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185px; height: 18px;\"> <strong>Receptors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.125px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Function</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 153px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185px; height: 153px;\"><strong>Toll-like</strong> receptors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 153px;\">Plasma membrane and endosomal vesicles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.125px; height: 153px;\">\n<p>Important TLRs and molecules recognised:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TLR2 - <strong>peptidoglycan</strong> of gram + bacteria, lipopolysaccharide of <em>Leptospira</em></li>\n<li>TLR3 - dsRNA viruses</li>\n<li>TLR4Q - <strong><em>Chlamydia</em></strong> and <strong>lipopolysaccharide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 153px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185px; height: 153px;\">NOD-like receptors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 153px;\">Cytosol of immune cells and epithelial cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.125px; height: 153px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Recognise substances from necrotic cells and microbial products</li>\n<li>Signals via inflammasome which activates caspase and IL-1</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185px; height: 54px;\">C-type lectin receptors </td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 54px;\">Plasma membrane of macrophages and dendritic cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.125px; height: 54px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Detect <strong>fungal glycans</strong> and elicit inflammatory reactions to fungi</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185px; height: 36px;\">RIG-like receptors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 36px;\">Cytosol of most cell types</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.125px; height: 36px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Detect <strong>nucleic acids of viruses</strong> that replicate in the cytoplasm of infected cells</li>\n<li>Stimulate the production of antiviral cytokines</li>\n<li>Recognize short bacterial peptides containing <em>N</em>-formyl methionyl residues and stimulate chemotaxis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 90px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185px; height: 90px;\">Mannose receptors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 90px;\">Plasma membrane of macrophages, immature dendritic cells, and liver sinusoidal endothelial cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.125px; height: 90px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Detect <strong>microbial sugars</strong> (which often contain terminal mannose residues) and induce phagocytosis of the microbes</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Mannose receptor is a<strong> soluble </strong>pattern-recognition receptor. All the others are <strong>surface</strong> pattern-recognition receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7086","text":"Antibody-dependent cellula‌r cytotoxicity (ADCC) is ‍seen with:","raw_text":"Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) is seen with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Natural killer (NK) cells ‌only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macropha‌ges only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NK cells, neutrophils and ‌eosinophils","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neutrophils and eosinophil‌s only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NK cells, neutrophils and eosinophils","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NK cells, neutrophils and eosinophils</strong></p><p><strong>Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity </strong>(ADCC) is mediated by <strong>NK cells</strong>, <strong>neutrophils</strong> and <strong>eosinophils </strong>(killing certain parasitic worms like helminths via IgE antibodies).</p>\n<p>In ADCC, cells/ antigens are coated with <strong>IgG</strong> antibodies. These are killed by<strong> effector cells</strong> like NK cells and macrophages. These effector cells bind to the target by their receptors for the Fc fragment of IgG and cell lysis proceeds <strong>without</strong> <strong>phagocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6786","text":"Which of the following occ‌urs due to gain-of-functi‍on mutations in NOD-Like ​receptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following occurs due to gain-of-function mutations in NOD-Like receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulomato‌us diseases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoinflammatory syndromes‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autoimmune‌ conditions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autoinflammatory syndromes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autoinflammatory syndromes</strong></p><p>The<strong> gain of function</strong> mutations in<strong> NOD-Like receptors</strong> (NLRs) result in periodic fever syndromes called <strong>autoinflammatory syndromes</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are a group of clinical conditions characterised by <strong>recurrent inflammatory</strong> episodes. This disorder is marked by abnormally increased inflammation, mediated predominantly by cells and molecules of the<strong> innate immune</strong> system, with a significant host predisposition. </p>\n<p>NOD-like receptors<strong> </strong>are<strong> cytosolic receptors</strong> that signal via a cytosolic multiprotein complex called the <strong>inflammasome</strong>. This activates<strong> caspase-1</strong> that generates biologically active form of<strong> interleukin-1</strong> (IL-1). IL-1 is a mediator of inflammation that recruits leukocytes and induces fever. The autoinflammatory syndromes <strong>respond</strong> very well to treatment with<strong> IL-1 antagonists</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Autoimmune diseases result from T and B-lymphocyte reactions against self-antigens. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6792","text":"What is the normal CD4+: C‌D8+ T lymphocyte ratio?","raw_text":"What is the normal CD4+: CD8+ T lymphocyte ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌:1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌:2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌:4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2:1</strong></p><p>Normal <strong>CD4+: CD8+</strong> T lymphocyte ratio is<strong> 2:1</strong>. Approximately 60% of mature T cells are CD4+ and about 30% are CD8+. </p>\n<p>T-lymphocytes (thymus-derived cells) constitute <strong>60–70%</strong> of peripheral blood <strong>lymphocytes. </strong>T-cell progenitors are derived from the bone marrow, but travel to the thymus, where they mature. T cells can be divided into three main subtypes:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Type of T cell</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Function</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Helper T cell (CD4+)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Helps B lymphocytes to make antibodies and activate other leukocytes to destroy microbes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs or CD8+)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Kill infected cells</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Regulatory T cells</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Limit immune responses and prevent reactions against self-antigens.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>They are located in the <strong>paracortical</strong> areas of<strong> lymph nodes</strong> and the<strong> periarteriolar</strong> sheaths of the <strong>spleen</strong>. They have an antigen-specific T-cell receptors (TCR) which have alpha/beta polypeptide chains components. Demonstration of TCR gene rearrangement by southern blot is a molecular marker of T-cell lineage.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The CD molecules present on T cells are CD1, CD2, CD3, CD4, CD5, CD7, CD8 and CD28. <strong>CD3</strong> is involved in <strong>signal transduction</strong> and is also called a<strong> p</strong><strong>an T-cell marker</strong><strong>. </strong><strong>CD40 ligand </strong>on the surface is required for<strong> B-cell activation </strong>and induction of <strong>immunoglobulin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6798","text":"Which of the following is ‌not expressed in the B-ce‍ll receptor complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not expressed in the B-cell receptor complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌86","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌79a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‌21","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌40","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CD86","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CD86</strong></p><p><strong>CD86</strong> is a protein expressed on<strong> antigen-presenting cells, </strong>and not B-cell receptor complex. This protein provides costimulatory signals necessary for<strong> </strong>T-cell activation and survival.</p>\n<p><strong>B-cell receptor</strong> complex expresses the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CD79a</strong> and<strong> CD79b -</strong> essential for signal transduction through the antigen receptor</li>\n<li><strong>CD21 </strong>or<strong> CR2 - </strong>complement receptor (recognize complement products during innate immune response) and Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) receptor</li>\n<li><strong>CD40 -</strong> receives signals from helper T cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other B cell-associated markers are CD10 (CALLA), CD19, CD20, CD22, CD23.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6800","text":"Which of the following are‌ the most important antig‍en-presenting cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following are the most important antigen-presenting cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dendrit‌ic cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B c‌ells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T c‌ells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dendritic cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dendritic cells</strong></p><p><strong>Dendritic cells</strong> or interdigitating dendritic cells are the most important<strong> antigen-presenting cells.</strong> They initiate T-cell responses against protein antigens.</p>\n<p>They are located in the epithelium and interstitium. They express many receptors for capturing and responding to microbes (and other antigens), including toll-like receptors (<strong>TLRs</strong>) and<strong> lectins</strong>. They also express high levels of major histocompatibility complex (<strong>MHC</strong>) for presenting antigens to and activating T cells.</p>\n<p>In response to microbes, dendritic cells are <strong>recruited</strong> to the <strong>T-cell zones</strong> of lymphoid organs. Here, they <strong>present</strong> <strong>antigens</strong> to T cells with help of MHC. </p>\n<p>Similar cells with dendritic morphology in the <strong>germinal centers</strong> of lymphoid follicles in the <strong>spleen</strong> and <strong>lymph nodes</strong> are <strong>follicular dendritic cells</strong>. They bear Fc receptors for IgG and receptors for C3b. <strong>Immature</strong> <strong>dendritic</strong> <strong>cells</strong> within the epidermis are called<strong> Langerhans cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aggretope </strong>- part of the antigen associated with the MHC molecule on the antigen-presenting cell</li>\n<li><strong>Epitope </strong>- antigenic part of contact with the T-cell receptor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6801","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about ma‍crophages?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about macrophages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts as an antigen pres‌enting cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It plays a role in humoral‌ immunity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It plays a role in cell-me‌diated immunity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a component of innat‌e immunity only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a component of innate immunity only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a component of innate immunity only</strong></p><p><strong>Macrophages </strong>are components of both<strong> innate</strong> and<strong> acquired</strong> immunity.</p>\n<p>They phagocytose microbes and protein antigens. They process the antigens and <strong>present the peptide fragments</strong> to T cells. Thus, macrophages function as <strong>antigen-presenting cells</strong> in T-cell activation.</p>\n<p>They also participate in the effector phase of<strong> humoral immunity</strong>. Macrophages efficiently <strong>phagocytose</strong> and destroy microbes that are opsonized (coated) with IgG or C3b.</p>\n<p>They are key effector cells in certain forms of <strong>cell-mediated immunity </strong>to eliminate intracellular microbes. In this type of response, T cells activate macrophages and enhance their ability to <strong>kill</strong> ingested <strong>microbes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6804","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about natural kille‍r cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about natural killer cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD16 and CD56 are the surf‌ace molecules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are involved in antib‌ody-dependant cytotoxic k‍illing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL-12 stimulates the proli‌feration of NK cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They activate macrophages ‌by secreting IFN-γ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IL-12 stimulates the proliferation of NK cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72cc7608178f414c8640bc5f8efe4024x1280x1935.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IL-12 stimulates the proliferation of NK cells</strong></p><p><strong>IL-2 and IL-15</strong> stimulate the <strong>proliferation</strong> of <strong>NK cells</strong>. On the other hand,<strong> IL-12</strong> activates the killing of target cells<strong> </strong>and secretion of<strong> IFN-γ.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>NK cells</strong> are large granular lymphocytes, which constitutes 5% to 10% of peripheral blood lymphocytes. <strong>CD16 </strong>and<strong> CD56</strong> are the surface molecules.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions </strong>of NK cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Killing</strong> of a variety of <strong>virus</strong>-infected cells and <strong>tumor</strong> cells, without prior exposure to or activation by these microbes or tumors</li>\n<li>Secretion of<strong> </strong>interferon-γ (<strong>IFN-γ</strong>), which activates <strong>macrophages</strong> to destroy ingested microbes</li>\n<li>Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (<strong>ADCC</strong>) - NK cells express<strong> CD16 </strong>which is a receptor for IgG Fc tails. Hence, these cells have the ability to lyse <strong>IgG-coated</strong> <strong>target</strong> cells. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>ADCC is also seen in immune responses involving neutrophils, eosinophils, macrophages, etc.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows activating and inhibitory receptors of natural killer (NK) cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: A new group of T cells has been discovered called <strong>NK-T cells</strong>. These are T cells that contain markers of NK cells. These are important defence mechanisms against microorganisms like <strong><em>Listeria monocytogenes</em> </strong>and <strong><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6823","text":"Which of these is common t‌o both cytotoxic T-cells ‍and NK cells?","raw_text":"Which of these is common to both cytotoxic T-cells and NK cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are MHC l restricted ‌cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Require antibodies to be p‌resent for action","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Effective against virus-in‌fected cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Their action is antigen sp‌ecific","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Effective against virus-infected cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Effective against virus-infected cells</strong></p><p>Both<strong> cytotoxic T-cells </strong>and <strong>NK cells</strong> are effective against <strong>virus-infected cells.</strong></p>\n<p>The NK cells have different mechanisms of action while killing target cells.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 201\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>NK cells</strong> can lyse IgG-coated target cells by binding to the Fc tail of IgG, through the CD16 receptor expressed on NK cells. This phenomenon is known as <strong>antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity</strong> (ADCC).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 201\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>NK cells secrete various <strong>cytokines</strong> such as <strong>interferon-γ</strong> (IFN-γ), which activate macrophages to destroy ingested microbes.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Target cell destruction by NK cells is also mediated through two others receptors - <strong>activating receptors</strong> and <strong>inhibitory receptors</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>activating receptors</strong> enable NK cells to recognize<strong> infected </strong>and<strong> stressed cells</strong>. The <strong>inhibitory receptors</strong> recognize <strong>self class-I MHC molecules</strong>, that are expressed on all <strong>healthy cells</strong> thereby preventing NK cells from killing normal cells. Virus infection / neoplastic transformation overexpresses the activating receptors and simultaneously reduces the expression of class-I MHC molecules. As a result, NK cells are activated and the infected cells are killed.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 222\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes</strong> <strong>directly kill</strong> damaged cells.</p>\n<p>The <strong>differences</strong> between NK cells and cytotoxic T-cells are: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 328px; width: 649px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Natural killer (NK) cell</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Cytotoxic T-cells</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 46px;\">Does not require <strong>priming - </strong>rapid response    in hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 46px;\"> require <strong>priming - </strong>takes days to respond</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 31px;\">MHC unrestricted </td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 31px;\">MHC restricted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 3px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 3px;\">\n<p>Antibody-mediated and non-antibody-mediated killing</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 3px;\">\n<p>Non-antibody mediated killing / direct cytotoxicity</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Kills virus-infected and neoplastic cells by apoptosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Kills virus-infected, neoplastic, and donor graft cells by apoptosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 30px;\">Antigen non-specific</td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 30px;\">Antigen-specific </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 223.164px; height: 33px;\">Memory is absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 409.836px; height: 33px;\">Memory is present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6824","text":"Which of the following mar‌kers on antigen-presentin‍g cells function as costi​mulatory molecules for T ‌cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following markers on antigen-presenting cells function as costimulatory molecules for T cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD28 a‌nd CD86","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only‌ CD80","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD28 a‌nd CD80","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD80 a‌nd CD86","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD80 and CD86","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbc4644949dc445b9909fb180881d67ex1280x1839.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD80 and CD86</strong></p><p><strong>CD80 </strong>(<strong>B7-1</strong>) and <strong>CD86 </strong>(<strong>B7-2</strong>)<strong> </strong>are the <strong>costimulatory</strong> molecules present on antigen-presenting cells (<strong>APCs</strong>). They bind to<strong> CD28</strong> protein present on<strong> T cells</strong>. This promotes the expansion of antigen-stimulated T cells and their differentiation into effector and memory cells.</p>\n<p><strong>T-cell activation</strong> requires two <strong>signals</strong> that are delivered by APCs. They include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Antigens displayed by APCs in the form of peptides bound to MHC molecules,<strong> </strong>that<strong> </strong>are recognised by <strong>T-cell receptors</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Costimulatory signal</strong> (as it stimulates T cells in conjunction with antigen) provided by molecules on APCs that engage particular costimulatory receptors on T cells</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In the <strong>absence</strong> of<strong> costimulation</strong>, T cells that recognize antigen either fail to respond and die or enter a state of unresponsiveness known as <strong>anergy</strong>. Thus, costimulation is a key determinant of the outcome of a T-cell encounter with an antigen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6828","text":"Which of the following cyt‌okines induces T-helper t‍ype 2 cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following cytokines induces T-helper type 2 cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TG‌F-β","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‌-4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IL‌-6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‌-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IL-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IL-4</strong></p><p><strong>IL-4</strong> induces <strong>T helper 2 cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Mechanism of<strong> cell-mediated immunity:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antigen-presenting cells → recognized by naive CD4+ T cells → IL-2 secretion → T-cell proliferation and differentiation → activated CD4+ T helper cells</li>\n<li>Activated CD4+ T cells further differentiate into effector cells that secrete different sets of cytokines and perform different functions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0596","text":"B-cells are induced to pro‌duce IgE by which of the ‍following:","raw_text":"B-cells are induced to produce IgE by which of the following:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‌-4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IL‌-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‌-6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IL-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IL-4</strong></p><p><strong>IL-4</strong> stimulates<strong> B-cells</strong> to differentiate into<strong> IgE</strong> secreting plasma cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6831","text":"Production of which of the‌ following antibodies is ‍induced by polysaccharide​s and lipids?","raw_text":"Production of which of the following antibodies is induced by polysaccharides and lipids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gM","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgM</strong></p><p><strong>Polysaccharides</strong> and<strong> lipids</strong> stimulate the secretion of mainly <strong>IgM </strong>antibodies.</p>\n<p><strong>B lymphocytes </strong>use their antigen receptors to recognize various types of antigens:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proteins (through CD40L and cytokine-mediated helper T-cell actions) - induce production of IgG, IgA, IgE</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polysaccharides and lipids - induce production of IgM mainly</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Helper T cells</strong> also help in antibodies by the mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stimulate formation of germinal centers which consist of proliferating B cells</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Induce isotype switching through cytokines IFN-γ and IL-4</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Affinity maturation - stimulate the production of antigen-specific antibodies </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Isotype switching and affinity maturation occur mainly in germinal centers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9457","text":"When papain acts on a mole‌cule of immunoglobulin, w‍hich of the following are​ the end products?","raw_text":"When papain acts on a molecule of immunoglobulin, which of the following are the end products?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 FC and 1 F‌AB fragments","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 Variable chain and 1 con‌stant chain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 Fc and 2 F‌ab fragments","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 Fc and 2F‌ab fragments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 Fc and 2 Fab fragments","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f36e339272c84f6fa33f6fd7a3d76d91x1280x1222.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1eaeb2a09a59439da02873943fb7634ex1280x1222.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 Fc and 2 Fab fragments</strong></p><p>When<strong> papain</strong> acts on a molecule of<strong> immunoglobulin</strong>, the end products are<strong> 1 Fc</strong> and <strong>2 Fab</strong> fragments.</p>\n<p>If an<strong> antibody</strong> molecule is digested with <strong>papain</strong>, <strong>cleavage</strong> occurs<strong> above</strong> the<strong> disulfide bonds</strong> that hold the heavy chains together. This generates 3 separate fragments, <strong>two</strong> of which are called <strong>Fab</strong> (fragment antigen binding), and <strong>one</strong> of which is called <strong>Fc</strong> (fragment crystallizable).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the cleavage of immunoglobulin by papain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Cleavage of the <strong>antibody</strong> molecule with<strong> pepsin</strong> generates one large fragment called<strong> F(ab´)2</strong> and a<strong> digested Fc</strong> fragment.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the cleavage of immunoglobulin by pepsin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6834","text":"In which of the following ‌cells is recirculation se‍en?","raw_text":"In which of the following cells is recirculation seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T lymp‌hocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both plasma cells and T ly‌mphocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both B lymphocytes and pla‌sma cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T lymphocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T lymphocytes</strong></p><p>Lymphocyte <strong>recirculation</strong> is seen in <strong>T lymphocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Migration of the lymphocytes between blood and lymphoid organs is called recirculation. <strong>Naive lymphocytes</strong> traverse the <strong>peripheral lymphoid</strong> organs, where immune responses are initiated.<strong> </strong>When antigen-presenting cells activate them, the <strong>effector lymphocytes</strong> migrate to sites of infection and inflammation. </p>\n<p>In contrast, <strong>plasma cells </strong>remain in lymphoid organs and the bone marrow. They do not need to migrate to sites of infection because they secrete<strong> antibodies </strong>that are carried to <strong>distant tissues</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0155","text":"Which of the following abo‌ut γ𝛿 T-cells is incorrec‍t:","raw_text":"Which of the following about γ𝛿 T-cells is incorrect:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kills extracellular bacter‌ia by granulysin and perf‍orin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Need antigen processing an‌d presentation for it rec‍ognition","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direct killing of infected‌ macrophages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Provide protection against‌ mycobacteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Need antigen processing and presentation for it recognition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Need antigen processing and presentation for it recognition</strong></p><p>γ𝛿 T-cells do not need antigen processing and presentation for its recognition.</p>\n<p>There are two types of T cells: γ𝛿 T-cells and αβ T-cells.</p>\n<p>The<strong> functions</strong> of<strong> γ𝛿 T-cells</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Provide protection against mycobacteria by <strong>direct killing</strong> of infected macrophages</li>\n<li>Kills extracellular bacteria by<strong> granulysin</strong> and <strong>perforin</strong></li>\n<li>Plays a role in maintaining <strong>self-tolerance</strong></li>\n<li>Capable of recognising and lysing diverse cancers in an MHC-unrestricted manner</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>αβ T-cells</strong> have MHC restricted action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0159","text":"Basophils are activated by‌:","raw_text":"Basophils are activated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"INF-‌gamma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TNF-‌alpha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‌-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IL-5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IL-5</strong></p><p><strong>Basophils </strong>are activated by<strong> IL-5 </strong>and IL-3.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> IL-5</strong> primarily activates<strong> eosinophils</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9461","text":"Among the following, which‌ cells have MHC class II?‍","raw_text":"Among the following, which cells have MHC class II?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NK c‌ells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All nucle‌ated cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B cells, dendritic cells, ‌mononuclear phagocytes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plat‌elets","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. B cells, dendritic cells, mononuclear phagocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. B cells, dendritic cells, mononuclear phagocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Class II</strong> major histocompatibility complex <strong>(MHC)</strong> molecules are mainly expressed on <strong>macrophages</strong>, <strong>B lymphocytes</strong>, and<strong> dendritic cells</strong>. These cells present ingested antigens to T-cells.</p>\n<p>The function of MHC molecules is to <strong>display</strong> peptide fragments of<strong> protein antigens</strong> for recognition by <strong>antigen-specific T cells</strong>. In humans, the MHC molecules are called <strong>human leukocyte antigens (HLA) </strong>because they were initially detected on leukocytes by the binding of antibodies. The combination of HLA alleles in each individual is called the HLA haplotype.</p>\n<p>The genes encoding HLA molecules are clustered on the short arm of<strong> chromosome 6</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Uses</strong> of MHC/ <strong>HLA typing</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anthropology</li>\n<li>Transfusion</li>\n<li><strong>Paternity testing</strong></li>\n<li>Forensic science</li>\n<li><strong>Transplantation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Disease correlation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6840","text":"Which of the following is ‌not encoded by MHC class ‍III genes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not encoded by MHC class III genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complement ‌component C2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor necr‌osis factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heat-shoc‌k proteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β2 microglobulin associate‌d molecules","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. β2 microglobulin associated molecules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. β2 microglobulin associated molecules</strong></p><p><strong> β2 microglobulin associated</strong> cell surface molecules are encoded by<strong> MHC class I </strong>and not class III.</p>\n<p>Class III MHC molecules include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complement proteins</strong> (such as<strong> C2</strong>, C4, and B factor)</li>\n<li>Cytokines (such as <strong>TNF-α</strong>, LTA, and LTB)</li>\n<li>Properdin factor BQ of alternate pathway</li>\n<li>Enzyme tyrosine hydroxylase</li>\n<li><strong>Heat-shock proteins</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Class III molecules are encoded by genes located between those that encode class I and class II molecules.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Rp8m1I_Y","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"14_anti_arrythmic_drugs","title":"14.Anti Arrythmic Drugs","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1905","text":"A patient diagnosed with v​entricular tachycardia wa​s started on a class1B an‍ti-arrhythmic drug. Ident‌ify the drug.","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with ventricular tachycardia was started on a class1B anti-arrhythmic drug. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mexil​etine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Quin​idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fleca​inide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propa​fenone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mexiletine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mexiletine</strong></p><p><strong>Mexiletine </strong>is a <strong>class 1B antiarrhythmic</strong> drug.<strong> </strong>It<strong> </strong>is a lidocaine analog.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1906","text":"The additional mechanism b​y which class 1A drugs ad​ditionally prolong action‍ potential duration is:","raw_text":"The additional mechanism by which class 1A drugs additionally prolong action potential duration is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blocking N​a+ channels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opening Na​+ channels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blocking K​+ channels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opening K​+ channels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Blocking K+ channels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Blocking K+ channels</strong></p><p><strong>Class IA </strong>antiarrhythmics <strong>block K+ channels </strong>leading to <strong>prolong</strong>ation of <strong>action potential</strong> duration.</p>\n<p>The <strong>main</strong> action of class IA antiarrhythmics is <strong>blocking Na <sup>+ </sup>channels </strong>causing: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slowing the upstroke of the action potential</li>\n<li>Slowing conduction</li>\n<li>Prolonging the QRS duration</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1913","text":"Which of the following ant​iarrhythmics has the fast​est rate of recovery on N‍a+ channel blockade? Proc‌ainamide Phenytoin Flecai‌nide Mexiletine Propafeno‌ne","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following antiarrhythmics has the fastest rate of recovery on Na+ channel blockade?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Procainamide</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Flecainide</li>\n<li>Mexiletine</li>\n<li>Propafenone</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 o​nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/514a78a442434412aaa593c0eee1fa8bx1280x2735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Phenytoin</strong> and <strong>mexiletine</strong> which are class IB antiarrhythmic drugs have the <strong>fastest rate</strong> of <strong>recovery</strong> on<strong> Na+ channel blockade</strong>.</p>\n<p>They<strong> </strong>show the <strong>fastest dissociation </strong>kinetics to<strong> Na+ channel blockade </strong>(&lt;1 sec).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 217px;\" width=\"515\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Na<sup>+ </sup>channel blockade</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Potassium channel </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Action potential duration</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Class IA</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Intermediate- 1-10 sec</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Significant K<sup>+</sup> channel blocker</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Prolonged</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Class IB</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Rapid- &lt;1 sec</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>K<sup>+</sup> channel opener</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Shortened or no effect</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Class IC</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Slow- &gt;10 sec</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Negligible K<sup>+</sup> channel blocker</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.75px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Variable or no effect</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8487","text":"Dofetilide belongs to whic​h class of anti-arrhythmi​c drugs?","raw_text":"Dofetilide belongs to which class of anti-arrhythmic drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cla​ss I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clas​s II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clas​s III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clas​s IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Class III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Class III</strong></p><p><strong>Dofetilide</strong> belongs to <strong>class III</strong> of anti-arrhythmic drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1929","text":"An athlete diagnosed with ​atrial fibrillation was a​dministered an antiarrhyt‍hmic drug. Several hours ‌later, he complains of lo‌wer abdominal pain and no‌t having passed urine sin‌ce admission. Examination‍ reveals a palpable bladd​er. Which was the most pr​obable drug administered?​","raw_text":"An athlete diagnosed with atrial fibrillation was administered an antiarrhythmic drug.  Several hours later, he complains of lower abdominal pain and not having passed urine since admission. Examination reveals a palpable bladder. Which was the most probable drug administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quin​idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disopy​ramide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Procai​namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Qui​nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Disopyramide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Disopyramide</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>urinary retention. Disopyramide, </strong>a class 1A antiarrhythmic<strong> </strong>is the drug most likely to cause these symptoms in this patient.</p>\n<p>It causes prominent <strong>anticholinergic side effects</strong> like urinary retention, dry mouth, blurred vision, precipitation of glaucoma, and constipation. </p>\n<p>It is the drug of choice for <strong>vagal</strong>ly mediated<strong> atrial fibrillation</strong> such as sleep-induced or atrial fibrillation in athletes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3721","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is associated with QT ​prolongation?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is associated with QT prolongation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligno​caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quin​idine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Magnesiu​m sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Quinidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6538f64b996042a2988f62f01335e095x1268x324.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Quinidine</strong></p><p><strong>QT prolongation</strong> is seen with<strong> quinidine. </strong></p>\n<p>It causes torsades de pointes, a life-threatening form of <strong>polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2003","text":"A patient presents with si​nus bradycardia and bronc​hospasm. She was previous‍ly diagnosed with AV noda‌l reentrant tachycardia a‌nd had been started on an‌ antiarrhythmic drug. Whi‌ch of the following drugs‍ is most likely being use​d?","raw_text":"A patient presents with sinus bradycardia and bronchospasm. She was previously diagnosed with AV nodal reentrant tachycardia and had been started on an antiarrhythmic drug. Which of the following drugs is most likely being used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fleca​inide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Propa​fenone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mexil​etine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lido​caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Propafenone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Propafenone</strong></p><p><strong>Propafenone </strong>is the most likely drug being used in this patient as <strong>sinus bradycardia</strong> and <strong>bronchospasm</strong> are <strong>adverse effects</strong> seen with the use of this drug.</p>\n<p>These side effects are due to its <strong>weak beta-blocking</strong> activity.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3114","text":"Blurred vision in a patien​t being treated for parox​ysmal supraventricular ta‍chycardia is most likely ‌due to:","raw_text":"Blurred vision in a patient being treated for paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia is most likely due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esm​olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Drone​darone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dofet​ilide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fleca​inide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flecainide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flecainide</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Blurred vision</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> dose-related <strong>non-cardiac adverse effect</strong> of<strong> flecainide</strong> therapy. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Flecainide is a <strong>class 1C antiarrhythmic</strong> agent. It acts mainly by sodium channel blockade. It does not cause any change in the action potential duration. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Flecainide is used in paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (<strong>PSVTs</strong>): </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Atrioventricular nodal re-entrant tachycardia (<strong>AVNRT</strong>)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">AV re-entrant tachycardia (<strong>AVRT</strong>)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Atrial fibrillation/atrial flutter (without structural heart disease)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1918","text":"Which of the following ant​iarrhythmics has both cla​ss II and class III actio‍n?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiarrhythmics has both class II and class III action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr​anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esm​olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sot​alol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nad​olol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sotalol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sotalol</strong></p><p><strong>Sotalol</strong> is a <strong>class III </strong>antiarrhythmic agent with <strong>additional beta-adrenergic blocking</strong> effects. (class II antiarrhythmic action)</p>\n<p>Propranolol is a class II antiarrhythmic agent that has an additional Na<sup>+</sup> channel blocking effect. (class I antiarrhythmic action)</p>\n<p>Esmolol is a short-acting beta-blocker with minimal/no action on cardiac Na<sup>+ </sup>and K<sup>+</sup> channels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1930","text":"A patient presented to the​ ER with dizziness and pa​lpitations. ECG is shown ‍below. Which of the follo‌wing drugs cannot be used‌ in this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the ER with dizziness and palpitations. ECG is shown below. Which of the following drugs cannot be used in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dig​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vera​pamil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quin​idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligno​caine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lignocaine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/910b818d2ef64c448a6bb019951055d6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lignocaine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The scenario and ECG findings (<strong>absent P waves</strong> and <strong>RR intervals</strong> that are <strong>irregularly irregular)</strong> are suggestive of <strong>atrial fibrillation. Lignocaine</strong> is not used in atrial arrhythmias. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Lignocaine</strong> is a <strong>class</strong> <strong>1B anti-arrhythmic agent.</strong> It <strong>blocks Na+</strong> channels mostly in the <strong>closed/inactivated state</strong>. It is not used in atrial arrhythmias because the atrial action potential is so short that the Na+ channels are in a closed/ inactivated state for only a brief amount of time.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lignocaine is used in the acute treatment of<strong> ventricular tachycardia</strong> and <strong>ventricular fibrillation.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"O0\">Drugs mostly used for <strong>supraventricular</strong> arrhythmia are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Adenosine</li>\n<li class=\"O1\"><strong>Verapamil</strong></li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Diltiazem</li>\n<li class=\"O1\"><strong>Digoxin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"O1\">Drugs used for <strong>ventricular</strong> arrhythmia are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Lidocaine </li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Mexiletine</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"O1\">Drugs used in <strong>both</strong> conditions are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Amiodarone</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Beta-blockers</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Class 1a / 1c antiarrhythmics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1984","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding ligno​caine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding lignocaine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrointestinal absorptio​n is good","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High volume of distributio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In heart failure patients,​ both loading and mainten​ance doses are decreased","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indicated for prophylaxis ​of arryhythmias in all ca​ses of suspected MI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Indicated for prophylaxis of arryhythmias in all cases of suspected MI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Indicated for prophylaxis of arryhythmias in all cases of suspected MI</strong></p><p><strong>Lignocaine decreases</strong> the incidence of <strong>arrhythmias</strong> in patients with<strong> suspected MI. </strong>However, it <strong>increases mortality</strong> probably by precipitating <strong>heart block</strong> or <strong>congestive</strong> <strong>heart failure.</strong> Hence it is <strong>not</strong> routinely used for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> in MI patients but it is used only when indicated.</p>\n<p>Gastrointestinal absorption of lignocaine is good but bioavailability is very less due to <strong>high first-pass hepatic metabolism</strong>. Therefore, it is not used through the oral route. </p>\n<p>Lignocaine has a <strong>high volume of distribution</strong>. The loading dose is required to achieve rapid action. </p>\n<p>In heart failure, both volumes of distribution and clearance are reduced. Both the loading dose and maintenance dose have to be reduced.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1991","text":"Which of the following is ​a correct statement regar​ding antiarrhythmics?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a correct statement regarding antiarrhythmics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sotalol has additional cla​ss II action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esmolol has additional cla​ss IV action","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propranolol has additional​ class I action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A​ and C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both A and C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both A and C</strong></p><p><strong>Sotalol</strong> has<strong> both class II</strong> (beta-blocker) <strong>and class III action</strong> (K+ channel blockade). Though it is a nonselective beta-blocker, as an antiarrhythmic, it is classified under class III. </p>\n<p>Propranolol is a non-selective beta-blocker with additional membrane-stabilizing action especially at higher doses: Na+ channel blockade/ class I action.</p>\n<p>Esmolol is a beta 1 selective blocker that does not have any additional class effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1994","text":"A 60-year-old man presents​ to the OPD with complain​ts of bluish discoloratio‍n of the skin on the sun-‌exposed areas. He has bee‌n on medication for monom‌orphic ventricular tachyc‌ardia for the past 3 year‍s. Which drug is most lik​ely implicated in this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents to the OPD with complaints of bluish discoloration of the skin on the sun-exposed areas. He has been on medication for monomorphic ventricular tachycardia for the past 3 years. Which drug is most likely implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido​caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aden​osine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amiod​arone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mexil​etine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amiodarone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amiodarone</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The scenario is suggestive of <strong>blue man syndrome </strong>which<strong> </strong>is most likely caused by <strong>amiodarone.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the side effects of amiodarone is <strong>photosensitivity</strong>.<strong> </strong>In some cases, bluish discoloration of the skin is seen, predominantly in the sun-exposed areas. This could be due to the <strong>immune response in dermal layers</strong> by amiodarone or the accumulation of amiodarone in superficial layers of skin.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The symptoms are <strong>reversible</strong> on drug discontinuation. The syndrome is related to both dose and photosensitivity.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The side effects of amiodarone include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pulmonary fibrosis</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hypotension, depressed ventricular function</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Torsades de pointes</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Corneal microdeposits</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hepatotoxicity</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neuropathies</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Photosensitivity</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Thyroid dysfunction</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other such syndromes associated with drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Red man syndrome - Vancomycin</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gray baby syndrome - Chloramphenicol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1995","text":"Chronic usage of an antiar​rhythmic drug leads to op​tic neuritis and smurf sk‍in or ceruloderma in a pa‌tient. Which of the follo‌wing is not a mechanism o‌f action of this drug?","raw_text":"Chronic usage of an antiarrhythmic drug leads to optic neuritis and smurf skin or ceruloderma in a patient. Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K+ and Na+ channel blockad​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ca2+ chann​el blockade","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenergi​c blockade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cl- channe​l blockade","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cl- channel blockade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cl- channel blockade</strong></p><p>Optic neuritis and smurf skin (blueman syndrome or ceruloderma) are caused by<strong> amiodarone. </strong>It<strong> </strong>acts by <strong>blocking K<sup>+</sup>, Na</strong><sup>+, </sup>and <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels. </strong>It also <strong>blocks adrenergic effects. </strong>It does not block Cl<sup>-</sup> channels.</p>\n<p>Amiodarone is classified as a <strong>class III</strong> antiarrhythmic agent. It exerts its function through the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blockade of transient outward delayed rectifier and inward rectifier K<sup>+</sup> currents</li>\n<li>Blockade of inactivated Na<sup>+</sup> channels with a rapid rate of recovery</li>\n<li>Decreases Ca<sup>2+</sup> current</li>\n<li>Non-competitive adrenergic blockade</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is the drug of choice for <strong>acute</strong> treatment of <strong>VT</strong> and <strong>VF.</strong></p>\n<p>The side effects of amiodarone include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary fibrosis</li>\n<li>Hypotension, depressed ventricular function</li>\n<li>Torsades de pointes</li>\n<li>Corneal microdeposits</li>\n<li>Hepatotoxicity</li>\n<li>Neuropathies</li>\n<li>Photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Thyroid dysfunction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1999","text":"Which of the following adv​erse effects is least lik​ely in a patient who is o‍n chronic amiodarone ther‌apy?","raw_text":"Which of the following adverse effects is least likely in a patient who is on chronic amiodarone therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dry ​cough","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal mi​crodeposits","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic d​ysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrot​oxicity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nephrotoxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nephrotoxicity</strong></p><p>Nephrotoxicity is not an adverse effect of chronic amiodarone therapy. <strong>Pulmonary fibrosis</strong> associated with <strong>dry cough</strong> is the most serious side effect of<strong> chronic amiodarone therapy</strong>. It can rapidly progress and become fatal.</p>\n<p>The side effects of amiodarone include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary fibrosis</li>\n<li>Hypotension, depressed ventricular function</li>\n<li>Torsades de pointes</li>\n<li><strong>Corneal microdeposits</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hepatotoxicity</strong></li>\n<li>Neuropathies</li>\n<li>Photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Thyroid dysfunction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0777","text":"Which of the following ant​iarrhythmic drugs is cont​raindicated in a patient ‍with interstitial lung di‌sease?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiarrhythmic drugs is contraindicated in a patient with interstitial lung disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod​arone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sot​alol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quin​idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligno​caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amiodarone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amiodarone</strong></p><p><strong>Amiodarone</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in a patient with <strong>interstitial lung disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The most serious<strong> adverse effect</strong> during chronic amiodarone therapy is <strong>pulmonary fibrosis</strong>, which can be rapidly progressive and fatal. <strong>Underlying lung disease</strong>, doses of 400 mg/day or more, and recent pulmonary insults such as pneumonia appear to be <strong>risk factors</strong> for this condition.</p>\n<p>Amiodarone is a class III antiarrhythmic drug.</p>\n<p>The side effects of amiodarone include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary fibrosis</li>\n<li>Hypotension, depressed ventricular function</li>\n<li>Torsades de pointes</li>\n<li>Corneal microdeposits</li>\n<li>Hepatotoxicity</li>\n<li>Neuropathies</li>\n<li>Photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Thyroid dysfunction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3101","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about dronedarone​ is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about dronedarone is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It does not contain iodine​ moiety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has stronger antiadrenergi​c effects than amiodarone​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less effective antiarrhyth​mic when compared to amio​darone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dose adjustment has to be ​done in renal failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dose adjustment has to be done in renal failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dose adjustment has to be done in renal failure</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Dronedarone</strong> undergoes <strong>hepatic metabolism</strong> and hence dose <strong>adjustment</strong> is <strong>not required</strong> in <strong>renal failure</strong> as it is not excreted through the kidneys.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dronedarone is a <strong>non-iodinated</strong> congener of amiodarone. Like amiodarone, it is a blocker of multiple ion channels. It has <strong>stronger antiadrenergic</strong> <strong>effects</strong> than amiodarone. It is <strong>less effective</strong> in maintaining sinus rhythm and is considered less antiarrhythmic when compared to amiodarone. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unlike amiodarone, it does not cause thyroid dysfunction or pulmonary fibrosis. However, it causes <strong>hepatotoxicity</strong> and increases the risk of stroke and heart failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3104","text":"Given below is the ECG of ​a patient who presented t​o the emergency room with‍ sudden onset palpitation‌s and breathlessness. Whi‌ch of the following drugs‌ can be used in this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"Given below is the ECG of a patient who presented to the emergency room with sudden onset palpitations and breathlessness. Which of the following drugs can be used in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mexil​etine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vera​pamil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lido​caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Verapamil","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b062624679194d43acf969ad9e87358a.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16c4534451f244bf9c43d74c07268356x510x812.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Verapamil</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the ECG findings are suggestive of <strong>paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia</strong> (PSVT). <strong>Verapamil</strong> and <strong>diltiazem</strong> are used in both <strong>acute treatment</strong> and <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of PSVT.</p>\n<p>Their mechanism of action is by <strong>slowing SA node </strong>automaticity and<strong> AV nodal conduction velocity</strong>. Additionally, they also decrease cardiac contractility and reduce blood pressure.</p>\n<p>Dihydropyridines like nifedipine and amlodipine do not have any antiarrhythmic property.</p>\n<div class=\"O0\">Drugs used for <strong>supraventricular</strong> arrhythmias are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Adenosine</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Verapamil</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Diltiazem</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Digoxin</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"O1\">Drugs used for <strong>ventricular</strong> arrhythmias are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Lidocaine </li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Mexiletine</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"O1\">Drugs used in <strong>both</strong> conditions are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Amiodarone</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Beta-blockers</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Class 1A / 1C antiarrhythmics</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3111","text":"Vernakalant is indicated i​n which of the following ​types of arrhythmias?","raw_text":"Vernakalant is indicated in which of the following types of arrhythmias?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular ​fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial fi​brillation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial ​flutter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricul​ar flutter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p><strong>Vernakalant</strong> is a newer antiarrhythmic agent indicated in <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Its mechanisms of action include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frequency and voltage-dependent <strong>block</strong> of the early and late components of the <strong>sodium</strong> current</li>\n<li>The muscarinic <strong>potassium</strong> current <strong>I<sub>KACh</sub>,</strong> which is constitutively activated in atrial fibrillation is also <strong>blocked</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is indicated for <strong>rapid termination</strong> of atrial fibrillation in patients with no or minimal structural heart disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3109","text":"A patient presented with p​alpitations and light-hea​dedness. His ECG is shown‍ below. Which of the foll‌owing drugs is least like‌ly to cause this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A patient presented with palpitations and light-headedness. His ECG is shown below. Which of the following drugs is least likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quin​idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disopy​ramide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Procai​namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lido​caine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lidocaine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00e9fd31a507460192d5b3caf8c74708.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lidocaine</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the ECG findings (<strong>rapid asynchronous complexes</strong> and an <strong>undulating</strong> <strong>baseline</strong>) point towards a diagnosis of <strong>torsades de pointes.</strong> <strong>Lidocaine</strong> use is unlikely to cause this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Lidocaine</strong> belongs to class IB antiarrhythmics. It is considered to have the <strong>least arrhythmogenic</strong> potential among antiarrhythmics.</p>\n<p>Among the antiarrhythmics, <strong>class Ia</strong> and <strong>class III</strong> drugs prolong action potential duration and thus <strong>prolong QT interval.</strong> They are also prone to cause torsades de pointes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3106","text":"Which of the following is ​the drug of choice for th​e immediate management of‍ a patient with paroxysma‌l supraventricular tachyc‌ardia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the immediate management of a patient with paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vera​pamil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilt​iazem","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dig​oxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aden​osine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adenosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/710e0226cb07491b9ae0ac0c7d3d64f3x510x812.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adenosine</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for the termination of <strong>PSVT</strong> is <strong>adenosine.</strong><strong> </strong></p>\n<div class=\"O0\">Drugs used for <strong>supraventricular</strong> arrhythmias are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Adenosine</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Verapamil</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Diltiazem</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Digoxin</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"O1\">Drugs used for <strong>ventricular</strong> arrhythmias are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Lidocaine </li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Mexiletine</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"O1\">Drugs used in <strong>both</strong> conditions are:</div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"O1\">Amiodarone</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Beta-blockers</li>\n<li class=\"O1\">Class 1a / 1c antiarrhythmics</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3110","text":"A patient who was recently​ diagnosed with a seizure​ disorder presents to the‍ casualty with altered me‌ntal status. His ECG show‌s a prolonged QTc. There ‌is history of sudden card‌iac deaths in his family ‍at an early age. On furth​er evaluation, a diagnosi​s of congenital long QT s​yndrome is made. Which is​ the most suitable pharma‌cological agent to treat ‌this condition?","raw_text":"A patient who was recently diagnosed with a seizure disorder presents to the casualty with altered mental status. His ECG shows a prolonged QTc. There is history of sudden cardiac deaths in his family at an early age.  On further evaluation, a diagnosis of congenital long QT syndrome is made. Which is the most suitable pharmacological agent to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metop​rolol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mexil​itine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nicor​andil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isopre​naline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metoprolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metoprolol</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> like metoprolol are the mainstay of <strong>pharmacological</strong> therapy in both <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and <strong>symptomatic</strong> patients with <strong>congenital LQTS. </strong>They <strong>reduce</strong> both <strong>syncope</strong> and <strong>sudden cardiac death</strong> (SCD).</p>\n<p>Long QT syndrome (LQTS) is a <strong>disorder</strong> of <strong>ventricular myocardial repolarization</strong> characterized by a prolonged QT interval on the electrocardiogram that can lead to symptomatic <strong>ventricular arrhythmias</strong> and an increased risk of sudden cardiac death.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3108","text":"An elderly patient with he​art failure and atrial fi​brillation is stabilized ‍with digoxin. By what mec‌hanism does it control th‌e arrhythmia in this pati‌ent?","raw_text":"An elderly patient with heart failure and atrial fibrillation is stabilized with digoxin. By what mechanism does it control the arrhythmia in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vagolyti​c action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sympatholy​tic action","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increasing​ vagal tone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreasing atrial repolari​zation by blocking Na+ ch​annels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increasing vagal tone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increasing vagal tone</strong></p><p><strong> Digoxin </strong>results in an<strong> increased vagal tone </strong>which helps in <strong>controlling</strong> the <strong>ventricular rate</strong> in atrial fibrillation.</p>\n<p>Increased vagal tone increases the AV nodal refractoriness due to blockade of Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels. It also results in hyperpolarisation and shortening of atrial action potentials due to activation of ACh-mediated K<sup>+</sup> channels in the atria.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9517","text":"A male patient who is suff​ering from a psychotic di​sorder is on antipsychoti‍c, antidepressant and ant‌ihypertensive treatment. ‌He suddenly developed tac‌hycardia and chest tightn‌ess. On evaluation he had‍ metabolic acidosis. ECG ​showed widened QRS comple​x and prolonged QT interv​al. Which of the followin​g drugs is responsible fo‌r this?","raw_text":"A male patient who is suffering from a psychotic disorder is on antipsychotic, antidepressant and antihypertensive treatment. He suddenly developed tachycardia and chest tightness. On evaluation he had metabolic acidosis. ECG showed widened QRS complex and prolonged QT interval. Which of the following drugs is responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amitri​ptyline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amlod​ipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorth​alidone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enal​april","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amitriptyline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/535a12eac6994d8da51548e737760d35x1268x324.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amitriptyline</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>torsades de pointes. </strong>Only <strong>amitriptyline</strong> causes this condition, among the above drugs.</p>\n<p><strong>Torsades de pointes</strong> (French: twisting of points) is a life-threatening form of <strong>polymorphic ventricular tachycardia</strong> with rapid asynchronous complexes and an <strong>undulating baseline on ECG.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"2lRcNyDg","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"14_motor_physiology___1","title":"14.Motor Physiology   1","num_questions":38,"total_marks":152,"duration":48,"time_per_question":75.79,"questions":[{"id":"MC0648","text":"Each muscle spindle consis​ts of ______ intrafusal f‍ibers.","raw_text":"Each muscle spindle consists of ______ intrafusal fibers.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 t​o 24","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 t​o 12","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 t​o 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 t​o 50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 to 12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 to 12</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Each muscle spindle is 3 to 10 millimeters long. It is built around <strong>3 to 12</strong> tiny intrafusal muscle fibers that are attached to the glycocalyx of the surrounding large extrafusal skeletal muscle fibers.</li>\n<li>Each intrafusal muscle fiber is a tiny skeletal muscle fiber. However, the central region of each of these fibers has few or no actin and myosin filaments. Therefore, this central portion does not contract when the ends do. Instead, it functions as a <strong>sensory receptor</strong>. The end portions that do contract are excited by <strong>small gamma motor nerve fibers</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0609","text":"Motor supply to muscle spi​ndle is _______","raw_text":"Motor supply to muscle spindle is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I​α","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I​I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​γ","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. γ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19cbc22b5c36438da1aab1352395e3c0x1280x1244.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18412681eab449b8b2efa70686542cfcx1280x1678.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. γ</strong></p><p>Motor supply to muscle spindle is <strong>γ</strong></p>\n<p>Muscle spindles have a motor nerve supply of their own.These nerves belong to<strong> Erlanger and</strong> <strong>Gasser’s Aɣ group</strong>. They are just <strong>3–6 μ</strong> in diameter (hence called small motor nerve system/ɣ efferents of Leksell) and go exclusively to muscle spindles. </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Motor innervation of muscle spindle is by gamma efferents </li>\n<li>Motor innervation of extrafusal fibers is by alpha motor neurons</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nuclear bag muscle fibers: <strong>1–3 </strong>per muscle spindle.</p>\n<p>Nuclear chain muscle fibers: <strong>3–9</strong> per muscle spindle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0647","text":"Identify the type of nerve​ fibre in the diagram bel‍ow.","raw_text":"Identify the type of nerve fibre in the diagram below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamma ​Neuron","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha ​Neuron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta ​Neuron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delta ​Neuron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gamma Neuron","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c040e3a449d04de797eea1dca92d028c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30b848ac2f584f47801d896b36ec4bb7x1280x1643.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gamma Neuron</strong></p><p>In the given figure, the fiber in yellow innervates the <strong>ends of the muscle spindle</strong>. Therefore, it is a <strong>gamma motor neuron.  </strong></p>\n<p>Many much smaller gamma motor neurons are located in the <strong>spinal cord anterior horns </strong>(shown as the yellow efferent fiber). These gamma motor neurons average <strong>5 micrometers</strong> in diameter, which go to small, special skeletal muscle fibers called intrafusal fibers or the muscle spindle. These fibers constitute the ends of the muscle spindle, which help control <strong>basic muscle “tone.” </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option (b):</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>alpha motor neurons </strong>(shown as the blue efferent fibre) are on an average <strong>14 micrometers</strong> in diameter. These fibers branch many times after they enter the muscle and<strong> innervate the large skeletal muscle fibers at the motor endplate</strong>. Stimulation of a single alpha nerve fiber excites anywhere from three to several hundred skeletal muscle fibers, which are collectively called the <strong>motor unit.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option (c):</strong></p>\n<p>A-beta fibers are fibers for touch and pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Option (d):</strong></p>\n<p>A-delta fibers are fibers for pain and temperature.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0610","text":"Which of the following is ​false with respect to mus‍cle spindles?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false with respect to muscle spindles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The intrafusal and extrafu​sal fibers are positioned‍ paralled to each other","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The primary endings of int​rafusal fibers are most s‍ensitive to a dynamic res​ponse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type II nerve fibers do no​t innervate static nuclea‍r bag fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Two types of γ motor neuro​ns supply the muscle spin‍dle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type II nerve fibers do not innervate static nuclear bag fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d808e65b4f694be88dbce6c8e7354d90x1280x1678.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type II nerve fibers do not innervate static nuclear bag fibers</strong></p><p>Type II nerve fibers do innervate the static nuclear bag fibers. They do not innervate dynamic nuclear bag fibers.</p>\n<p>A muscle spindle has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrafusal fibers - these are tiny skeletal muscles which have few or no actin and myosin filaments in the central portion. This portion acts as a sensory receptor.</li>\n<li>Extrafusal fibers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These fibers are <strong>positioned paralle</strong>l to each other (Option A).</p>\n<p>There are two types of intrafusal fibers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nuclear bag</strong> fibers - subdivided as <strong>dynamic and static</strong> nuclear bag fibers</li>\n<li>Nuclear chain fibers</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sensory nerve supply</strong>: There are two types of sensory endings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary endings</strong> - (<strong>annulospiral ending</strong>) from <strong>Type Ia</strong> nerve fibers. This innervates both the <strong>nuclear bag and nuclear chain</strong> fibers. They are most sensitive to the velocity of the change in muscle length. This is called a <strong>dynamic response. </strong>(Option B)</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary endings</strong> - from <strong>Type II</strong> nerve fibers. This innervates static nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers. They <strong>do not innervate dynamic nuclear bag fibers. </strong>(Option C) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both primary and secondary endings show a static response.</p>\n<p><strong>Motor nerve supply:</strong> The muscle spindles are supplied by <strong>γ motor neurons</strong>. There are <strong>two types</strong> of γ motor neurons- dynamic that supply dynamic nuclear bag fibers and static that supply static nuclear bag fibers and nuclear chain fibers. (Option D)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0611","text":"Which of the following is ​sensed by the nuclear bag‍ fibers?","raw_text":"Which of the following is sensed by the nuclear bag fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dynamic leng​th of muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle ​tension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Joint p​osition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dynamic length of muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dynamic length of muscle</strong></p><p>The nuclear bag fibers sense the <strong>dynamic length of the muscle.</strong></p>\n<p>Nuclear bag fibers are intrafusal fibers, which are a part of the muscle spindle. Intrafusal fibers serve a purely sensory function in the muscle spindle.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of intrafusal fibers in muscle spindles:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 367px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Nuclear bag fibers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\"><strong>Nuclear chain fibers</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Nuclei in central dilated area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Nuclei in rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Types</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Static</li>\n<li>Dynamic</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">----</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Primary sensory afferent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Ia (for both the types)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\"> Ia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Secondary sensory ending</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">II (only for static bag fiber)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">II</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Efferent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 243\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>static &amp; dynamic γ-motor neurons</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 243\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>static γ-motor neurons</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Way of detection of the change in muscle length</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Static &amp; dynamic changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">Static change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 242\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 242\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Note</strong>- Golgi tendon organ senses changes in <strong>muscle tension</strong>. Muscle spindle also provides sense of <strong>joint position</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0613","text":"The afferents for deep ten​don reflexes innervate __‍___","raw_text":"The afferents for deep tendon reflexes innervate  _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dynamic intr​afusal fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi ten​don organ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclear bag and chain fibe​rs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi tendon and muscle sp​indle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nuclear bag and chain fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nuclear bag and chain fibers</strong></p><p>The afferents for deep tendon reflexes innervate both - <strong>nuclear bag and</strong><strong> chain fibers.</strong></p>\n<p>The afferents for the DTRs are the type Ia and type II nerve fibers. There are three types of intrafusal muscle fibers: dynamic nuclear bag, static nuclear bag, and nuclear chain fibers. A single Ia afferent fiber innervates all three types of fibers to form a primary sensory ending. A group II sensory fiber innervates nuclear chain and static bag fibers to form a secondary sensory ending.</p>\n<p>Deep tendon reflexes are an example of stretch reflex or myotatic reflex.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0612","text":"Stimulus for stretch refle​x is_____","raw_text":"Stimulus for stretch reflex is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Passive​ stretch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Active ​stretch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo​th","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Passive stretch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df2ab043860e4db7b04e0873e5dd6e83x1280x1309.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Passive stretch</strong></p><p>Stretch reflex occurs when the muscle is stretched passively.</p>\n<p><strong>Stretch (myotatic) reflex</strong> is monosynaptic. (As illustrated in the figure given below, steps 1→5).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0614","text":"Withdrawal reflexes are no​t____","raw_text":"Withdrawal reflexes are not____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initiated by nociceptive s​timuli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged if the stimulus ​is strong","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An example of a flexor ref​lex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accompanied by the same re​sponse on both sides of t‍he body","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Accompanied by the same response on both sides of the body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/900216bed2b647489d4dcec81e46b75bx1280x2269.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Accompanied by the same response on both sides of the body</strong></p><p>Withdrawal reflexes are <strong>not accompanied</strong> by the<strong> same response on both sides </strong>of the body. On the <strong>ipsilateral</strong> side of the pain stimulus,<strong> flexors are stimulated</strong> (they contract) and <strong>extensors are inhibited </strong>(they relax), and the arm is jerked away from the stove. On the <strong>contralateral</strong> side,<strong> flexors are inhibited</strong> and <strong>extensors are stimulated</strong> (crossed extension reflex) to maintain balance.</p>\n<p>It results in flexion on the ipsilateral side and extension on the contralateral side.</p>\n<p>Somatosensory and pain afferent fibers elicit withdrawal of the stimulated body part from the noxious stimulus.</p>\n<p>Pain (e.g., touching a hot stove) stimulates the flexor reflex afferents of groups II, III, and IV.</p>\n<p>The afferent fibers synapse polysynaptically (via interneurons) onto motoneurons in the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>As a result of persistent neural activity in the polysynaptic circuits, an afterdischarge occurs. The afterdischarge prevents the muscle from relaxing for some time.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0621","text":"The maintenance of normal ​posture depends on_____","raw_text":"The maintenance of normal posture depends on_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Integrity o​f reflex arc","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle​ power","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type of mu​scle fiber","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joint movement in physiolo​gical range","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Integrity of reflex arc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6177220116cc4506b9bccb197610a02bx1280x1956.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Integrity of reflex arc</strong></p><p>The maintenance of normal posture depends on<strong> </strong>the <strong>Integrity of reflex arc.</strong></p>\n<p>The muscles that maintain upright posture—that is, support the body’s weight against gravity—are controlled by the brain and by reflex mechanisms.</p>\n<p>Three levels of the nervous system control movement:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The spinal cord</strong>, which integrates spinal reflexes and contains central pattern generators.</li>\n<li><strong>The brain stem and cerebellum</strong>, which control postural reflexes and hand and eye movements.</li>\n<li><strong>The cerebral cortex and basal ganglia</strong>, which are responsible for voluntary movements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0615","text":"Receptor for inverse stret​ch reflex_____","raw_text":"Receptor for inverse stretch reflex_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle ​spindle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extrafus​al fiber","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrafus​al fiber","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi ten​don organ","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Golgi tendon organ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52195322e98c4349aae0aa39089682d8x1280x1938.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Golgi tendon organ</strong></p><p><strong>Golgi tendon organ</strong> is the receptor for <strong>inverse stretch reflex</strong> (refer figure).</p>\n<p>The Golgi tendon organs, unlike the spindles, are in series with the muscle fibers; therefore, they are stimulated by both passive stretch and active contraction of the muscle.</p>\n<p>The degree of stimulation by contraction of muscles is much greater than that caused by the stretching of muscles.</p>\n<p>Muscle contraction <strong>→ Ib fibers</strong> from the Golgi tendon organ excite an <strong>inhibitory interneuron</strong> that releases inhibitory mediator<strong> glycine → hyperpolarization (inhibition) of α motor neurons </strong>→ muscle relaxation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0616","text":"The number of muscle fiber​s per Golgi tendon organ ‍is ___.","raw_text":"The number of muscle fibers per Golgi tendon organ is ___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-​20","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"200​-300","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50​-60","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80-​100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3-20","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be59692c2c5e44b4978a6b45c79c68c3x600x816.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3-20</strong></p><p>There are 3-20 muscle fibers per Golgi tendon organ.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Golgi tendon organ consists of a netlike collection of knobby nerve endings among the fascicles of a tendon.</li>\n<li>There are 3–25 muscle fibers per Golgi ten­don organ.</li>\n<li>The fibers from the Golgi tendon organs make up the Ib group of myelinated, rapidly conducting sensory nerve fibers.</li>\n<li>Golgi tendon organs help in regulating the velocity of the muscle contraction, muscle length, and muscle force.</li>\n<li>They are the receptor for <strong>inverse stretch reflex</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0617","text":"The inverse stretch reflex​_____","raw_text":"The inverse stretch reflex_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs when Ia spindle aff​erents are inhibited","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Is a monosynaptic reflex i​nitiated by activation of‍ the Golgi tendon organ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is a disynaptic reflex wit​h a single interneuron in‍serted between the affere​nt and efferent limbs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Is a polysynaptic reflex w​ith many interneurons ins‍erted between the afferen​t and efferent limbs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Is a disynaptic reflex with a single interneuron inserted between the afferent and efferent limbs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3114e5b386164f339109af4df9753842x1280x1938.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Is a disynaptic reflex with a single interneuron inserted between the afferent and efferent limbs</strong></p><p><strong>Golgi tendon reflex (Inverse myotatic/Inverse stretch reflex)</strong> <strong>is</strong> <strong>disynaptic, </strong>is the opposite, or inverse, of the stretch reflex.</p>\n<p>When tension on the muscle—and therefore on the tendon—becomes extreme, the inhibitory effect from the golgi tendon organ can be so great that it leads instantaneous relaxation of the entire muscle. This effect is called the <strong><em>lengthening reaction</em></strong><em>; </em>it is probably a protective mechanism to prevent tearing of the muscle or avulsion of the tendon from its attachments to the bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0650","text":"All of the following are t​rue about withdrawal refl‍ex except ______","raw_text":"All of the following are true about withdrawal reflex except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diverging circuits to spre​ad the reflex to the nece‍ssary muscles for withdra​wal are present","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Circuits to inhibit the an​tagonist muscles, called ‍reciprocal inhibition cir​cuits are present","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Circuits to cause afterdis​charge that lasts many fr‍actions of a second after​ the stimulus is over are‌ present","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The pathways for eliciting​ the flexor withdrawal re‍flex pass directly to the​ anterior motor neurons m‌aking it a monosynaptic r​eflex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The pathways for eliciting the flexor withdrawal reflex pass directly to the anterior motor neurons making it a monosynaptic reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d73f2ba546bf4224a2853c5afeaf7626x1280x1671.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The pathways for eliciting the flexor withdrawal reflex pass directly to the anterior motor neurons making it a monosynaptic reflex</strong></p><p>The pathways for eliciting the flexor withdrawal reflex do not pass directly to the anterior motor neurons; instead, they first pass into the spinal cord interneuron pool of neurons and only secondarily to the motor neurons. The shortest possible circuit is a three- or four-neuron pathway. The following basic types of circuits are present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diverging circuits</strong> to spread the reflex to the necessary muscles for withdrawal;</li>\n<li>Circuits to inhibit the antagonist muscles, called <strong>reciprocal inhibition circuits</strong>; and</li>\n<li>Circuits to cause <strong>after-discharge</strong> are present. They prolong the reflex contraction of the muscles that last many fractions of a second after the stimulus is over.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The flexor reflex is organized to withdraw a pained or otherwise irritated part of the body from a stimulus. Further, because of after-discharge, the reflex can hold the irritated part away from the stimulus for 0.1 to 3 seconds after the irritation is over. During this time, other reflexes and actions of the central nervous system can move the entire body away from the painful stimulus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0618","text":"Muscle tone is maintained ​by_____","raw_text":"Muscle tone is maintained by_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Golgi ten​don organ","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle ​spindle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rensha​w cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muscle spindle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muscle spindle</strong></p><p>The resistance of a muscle to stretch is often referred to as its tone or tonus and <strong>muscle spindle </strong>helps to control basic muscle tone.</p>\n<p>The tonic (static) stretch reflex circuit includes both group Ia and group II afferent fibers from muscle spindles. Normally, there is ongoing activity in the Ia and II afferents that helps maintain a baseline firing of α motor neurons; therefore, the tonic stretch reflex contributes to muscle tone.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0619","text":"Which of the following is ​true during initiation of‍ voluntary movements?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true during initiation of voluntary movements?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Α α motor neurons are acti​vated followed by γ motor‍ neuron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Α γ motor neurons are acti​vated followed by α motor‍ neuron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both α and ɣ motor neurons​ are activated together","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Only α motor neurons are a​ctivated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Both α and ɣ motor neurons are activated together","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Both α and ɣ motor neurons are activated together</strong></p><p>The cell bodies of α and γ motor neurons to a given muscle lie close together. Both types are <strong>activated by interneurons</strong> in their immediate vicinity and sometimes directly by neurons of the descending pathways. During many voluntary and involuntary movements, they are coactivated, i.e., they are excited at almost the same time. <strong>(α-ɣ coactivation</strong><strong>)</strong></p>\n<p>The purpose of α-ɣ coactivation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First, it keeps the length of the receptor portion of the muscle spindle from changing during the course of the whole muscle contraction. Hence, the muscle contraction is <strong>not opposed by the stretch reflex</strong>.</li>\n<li>Second, if the muscle spindle did not contract and relax along with the large muscle fibers, the receptor portion of the spindle would sometimes be flail and sometimes be overstretched.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Coactivation thus ensures that information about muscle length will be continuously available to provide for adjustment during ongoing actions and to plan and program future movements.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0623","text":"Righting re​flex is____","raw_text":"Righting reflex is____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stretch​ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postura​l reflex","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal​ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ocular​ reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Postural reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Postural reflex</strong></p><p>Righting reflexes operate to maintain the normal standing position and keep an animal's head upright. These reflexes are a series of responses integrated for the most part in the nuclei of the midbrain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0657","text":"In a patient with decerebr​ate rigidity, on applying‍ pressure on the footpad,​ extension of that limb o‌ccurs. Which of the follo​wing statements is false ‌regarding this phenomenon‌?","raw_text":"In a patient with decerebrate rigidity, on applying pressure on the footpad, extension of that limb occurs. Which of the following statements is false regarding this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is Magn​et Reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is integrated at the Sp​inal Level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It helps to prevent fallin​g on that side","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a righting reflex in​tegrated at midbrain leve‍l","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a righting reflex integrated at midbrain level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a righting reflex integrated at midbrain level</strong></p><p>Pressure on the footpad of a decerebrate patient causes the limb to extend against the pressure applied to the foot. This reflex is called the <strong>positive supportive reaction </strong>and<strong> </strong>is a righting reflex integrated at the <strong>spinal level</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pressure on one side causes extension in that direction (e.g., on applying pressure on the <strong>right</strong> footpad, extension of <strong>the right</strong> lower limb occurs), an effect called the <strong>magnet reaction</strong><em>. </em>It is seen in infants and following spinal transection.</p>\n<p>From an evolutionary perspective, this reflex helps to prevent falling on the side where the stimulus is given.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0624","text":"Pyramids are formed by____​","raw_text":"Pyramids are formed by____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arcuate​ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vestibul​ar nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitials cells of kaj​al","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corticosp​inal tract","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Corticospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc802c3098a243e7b297a0c4a3c5b26fx1280x2843.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Corticospinal tract</strong></p><p>The corticospinal pathways are also called the pyramidal tracts or pyramidal system because of their triangular shape as they pass along the ventral surface of the medulla oblongata.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0625","text":"Which of the following is ​true about corticospinal ‍tract?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about corticospinal tract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mostly ​crossed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uncr​ossed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crossed at midspinal level​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20% c​rossed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mostly crossed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/275ac4b809654007a141f30f02b3f671x1064x604.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mostly crossed</strong></p><p>The corticospinal tract contains <strong>crossed fibers</strong> (majority).</p>\n<p><strong>Fibers of the corticospinal tract</strong> originate from the primary motor cortex (about 30%), the premotor and supplementary motor areas (30%), and the somatosensory areas posterior to the central sulcus (40%).</p>\n<p>Most fibers (<strong>80% fibers</strong>) decussate (cross) in the medullary pyramids and descend in the lateral white matter of the spinal cord to form the <strong>lateral corticospinal tract</strong>. They control the movements of the contralateral limbs.</p>\n<p>The remaining <strong>20% make</strong> up <strong>the ventral corticospinal tract</strong>. These fibers remain <strong>uncrossed</strong> until reaching the spinal cord where axons terminate bilaterally on spinal interneurons antecedent to motor neurons. These fibers control the movements of the axial musculature. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0680","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the origi‍n of corticospinal tract?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the origin of corticospinal tract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticospinal tract fibers​ originate from Betz Cell‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30% from premotor and supp​lementary motor areas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40% from somatosensory are​as","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90% from the primary motor​ cortex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 90% from the primary motor cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5abbb8d1a57944ceaa2dc9c46011d2c3x1280x2843.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 90% from the primary motor cortex</strong></p><p><strong>30% of corticospinal tract </strong>originates about from the<strong> primary motor cortex</strong>, not 90%.</p>\n<p>30 % from the premotor and supplementary motor areas. 40 % from the somatosensory areas posterior to the central sulcus. </p>\n<p><strong>Betz cells</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some large myelinated fibres in the corticospinal tract originate from <strong>giant pyramidal cells<em>, </em>also known as Betz cells</strong>.</li>\n<li>Found in the <strong>layer 5</strong> of primary motor cortex.</li>\n<li>These large fibers represent only <strong>3 percent</strong> of the total fibers of the corticospinal tract. (The other 97 percent less than 4 micrometers in diameter that conduct background tonic signals to the motor areas of the cord.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0626","text":"Which of the following is ​not an extrapyramidal tra‍ct?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an extrapyramidal tract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticulosp​inal tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubrospi​nal tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corticosp​inal tract","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibulos​pinal tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Corticospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Corticospinal tract</strong></p><p><strong>Corticospinal tract is a pyramidal tract . </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pyramidal tracts </strong>(corticospinal and corticobulbar) pass through the medullary pyramids.</p>\n<p>All others are <strong>extrapyramidal tracts </strong>and originate primarily in the the brain stem.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0627","text":"Which pathway is not invol​ved in maintainence of po‍sture?","raw_text":"Which pathway is not involved in maintainence of posture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticulosp​inal tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubrospi​nal tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tectospi​nal tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibulos​pinal tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rubrospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5ad9ffc67054876ac12cde4d3ac3e21x1280x1246.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rubrospinal tract</strong></p><p><strong>Rubrospinal tract</strong> is not involved in posture maintenance.</p>\n<p><strong>Medial motor system </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Controls the axial muscles (muscles of the trunk)</li>\n<li>Axial muscles are concerned with <strong>postural adjustments</strong> and gross movements</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lateral motor system</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Controls the distal muscles</li>\n<li>Distal muscles mediate fine, skilled movements</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0628","text":"Gamma motor neurons are ma​inly influenced by _____","raw_text":"Gamma motor neurons are mainly influenced by _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticulosp​inal tract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rubrospi​nal tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tectospi​nal tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibulos​pinal tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reticulospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reticulospinal tract</strong></p><ul>\n<li>The pontine and medullary reticulospinal tracts are involved in maintainance of posture and in modulating muscle tone especially via an input to ɣ- motor neurons.</li>\n<li>Pontine reticulospinal neurons are primarily excitatory and medullary reticulospinal neurons are primarily inhibitory.</li>\n<li>Stimulation of γ-motor neurons does not lead directly to detectable contraction of the muscles because the intrafusal fibers are not strong enough or plentiful enough to cause short­ening.</li>\n<li>However, stimulation does cause the contractile ends of the intrafusal fibers to shorten and therefore stretches the nuclear bag portion of the spindles, deforming the endings, and initiating impulses in the Ia fibers . This in turn can lead to reflex contraction of the muscle.</li>\n<li>Thus, muscles can be made to contract via stimulation of the α-motor neu­rons that innervate the extrafusal fibers or the γ-motor neu­rons that initiate contraction indirectly via the stretch reflex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0629","text":"According to Herrington cl​assification, the decereb‍rate rigidity is characte​rized by all except _____‌_","raw_text":"According to Herrington classification, the decerebrate rigidity is characterized by all except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rigidity occurs in all the​ muscles of the body","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in the rate of di​scharge of the ɣ efferent‍ neuron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased excitability of ​the motor neuron pool","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decerebration produces no ​phenomenon akin to spinal‍ shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rigidity occurs in all the muscles of the body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rigidity occurs in all the muscles of the body</strong></p><p>In decerebrate rigidity, there is rigidity in <strong>extensor muscles of all the four extremities</strong> (not all muscles of the body).</p>\n<p><strong>Decerebration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete transection of the brain stem between the superior and inferior colliculi permits the brain stem pathways to function independently of their input from higher brain structures. This is called a <strong>mid-collicular decerebration.</strong></li>\n<li>This lesion interrupts all input from the cortex (corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts) and red nucleus (rubrospinal tract), primarily to distal muscles of the extremities.</li>\n<li>The excitatory and inhibitory reticulospinal pathways (primarily to postural extensor muscles) remain intact.</li>\n<li>The dominance of drive from ascending sensory pathways to the excitatory reticulospinal pathway leads to hyperactivity in extensor muscles in all four extremities which are called <strong>decerebrate rigidity.</strong></li>\n<li>The excitatory input from the reticulospinal pathway activates γ-motor neurons which indirectly activate α-motor neurons (via Ia spindle afferent activity). This is called the <strong>gamma loop.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option 4. Spinal shock is different from decebrate rigidity as it is a transient state that can occur immediately after a spinal cord injury. It is characterized by a temporary loss or depression of spinal reflexes below the level of the injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0630","text":"Which of the following is ​true for decorticate rigi‍dity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true for decorticate rigidity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is produced by the remo​val of cerebral cortex an‍d basal ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexion of lower limbs and​ extension of upper limbs‍ occurs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rigidity is​ pronounced","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is characterized by fle​xion of upper limbs and e‍xtension of lower limbs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is characterized by flexion of upper limbs and extension of lower limbs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6c11cba47204951b0b484492ec712f7x571x131.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is characterized by flexion of upper limbs and extension of lower limbs</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Removal of the cerebral cortex <strong>(decortication) </strong>produces <strong>decorticate rigidity.</strong></li>\n<li>Characterized by flexion of the upper extremities at the elbow and extensor hyperactivity in the lower extremities.</li>\n<li>The flexion can be explained by active rubrospi­nal excitation of flexor muscles in the upper extremities; the hyperextension of lower extremities is due to the same changes that occur after midcollicular decerebration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0724","text":"Which of the following ref​lex is absent in a decort‍icate animal?","raw_text":"Which of the following reflex is absent in a decorticate animal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rightin​g reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hopping and Placing Reflex​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Labrynthi​ne Reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crossed Ext​ensor Reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hopping and Placing Reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hopping and Placing Reflex</strong></p><p>The Hopping and Placing reflex is absent in a decorticate animal as it is integrated in the cerebral cortex. Rest of the reflexes are integrated below the cortex.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0725","text":"Destruction of anterior ce​rebellum in a decerebrate‍ animal leads to ________​_.","raw_text":"Destruction of anterior cerebellum in a decerebrate animal leads to _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No effect ​on rigidity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased flexor muscle to​ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased rigidity via alp​ha motor neuron","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease i​n rigidity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased rigidity via alpha motor neuron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased rigidity via alpha motor neuron</strong></p><p>Destruction of the anterior cerebellum in a decerebrate animal leads to <strong>an increased rigidity via alpha motor neuron</strong>.</p>\n<p>If the anterior lobe of the cerebellum is removed in a decerebrate animal, extensor muscle hyperactivity is exaggerated <strong>(decerebellate rigidity). </strong>This cut eliminates cortical inhibition of the cerebellar fastigial nucleus and secondarily increases excitation to vestibular nuclei. Subsequent dorsal root section does not reverse the rigidity, hence proving that the rigidity is due to activation of α-motor neurons independent of the gamma loop.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0631","text":"Upper Motor Neurons (UMN) ​include ______","raw_text":"Upper Motor Neurons (UMN) include ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyramid​al cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peripher​al nerves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glial​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior ​horn cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyramidal cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyramidal cells</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Upper motor neurons </strong>typically refer to corticospinal tract neurons that innervate spinal motor neurons, but they can also include brainstem neurons that control spinal motor neurons.</li>\n<li>Corticospinal tract contain a population of large myelinated fibers with a mean diameter of 16 micrometers. These fibers originate from <strong>giant pyramidal cells</strong>, called <strong>Betz cells,</strong> that are found only in the primary motor cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0633","text":"A 30-year-old man meets wi​th an accident and develo‍ps spinal cord transectio​n. Which of the following‌ features will be seen as​ a result of pyramidal tr‌act injury below the leve‌l of motor decussation?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man meets with an accident and develops spinal cord transection. Which of the following features will be seen as a result of pyramidal tract injury below the level of motor decussation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive Ba​binski sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of deep tendon reflex​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of pro​prioception","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flacc​idity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Positive Babinski sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47a90fd5d6d14764b83c38775ab39a91x548x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Positive Babinski sign</strong></p><p>Cutting the pyramidal tract below the decussation (UMN lesion) causes<strong> abnormal plantar extensor reflex (posi­tive Babinski sign). </strong></p>\n<p>It initially causes muscles to become weak and flaccid but eventually leads to spasticity, <strong>hypertonia </strong>(increased resistance to passive movement), and hyperactive stretch reflexes.<br/><br/>The image below shows the pyramidal tract (corticospinal pathway).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0726","text":"A 36-year-old male patient​ presented to the ER foll‍owing a road traffic acci​dent. Which of the follow‌ing features would not be​ present if there is a co‌rticospinal tract involve‌ment?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old male patient presented to the ER following a road traffic accident. Which of the following features would not be present if there is a corticospinal tract involvement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cog-Wheel​ Rigidity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor Pla​ntar response","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spast​icity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of superficial reflex​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cog-Wheel Rigidity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cog-Wheel Rigidity</strong></p><p>Cog-wheel rigidity is not seen if there is a corticospinal tract involvement. <strong>Cogwheel rigidity</strong> is seen in <strong>extrapyramidal tract involvement</strong> when there is a tremor superimposed on hypertonia (e.g., Parkinsonism).</p>\n<p>In an upper motor neuron lesion, there is damage to the corticospinal or corticobulbar fibres. If the corticospinal fibres are damaged, it leads to diminished or<strong> loss of superficial reflexes</strong>, shows an <strong>extensor plantar response</strong> (Babinski's sign) with loss of fine skilled movement and <strong>spasticity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0686","text":"While exercising, a 42-yea​r-old woman developed sud‍den onset of tingling in ​her right leg and an inab‌ility to control movement​ in that limb. A neurolog‌ic exam showed a hyperact‌ive knee jerk reflex and ​a positive Babinski sign.‌ Which of the following i​s not true regarding her ​condition?","raw_text":"While exercising, a 42-year-old woman developed sudden onset of tingling in her right leg and an inability to control movement in that limb. A neurologic exam showed a hyperactive knee jerk reflex and a positive Babinski sign. Which of the following is not true regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflexes can be modified b​y impulses from various p‍arts of the CNS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reflexes may involve simul​taneous contraction of so‍me muscles and relaxation​ of others","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reflexes are chronically s​uppressed after spinal co‍rd transection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reflexes often occur witho​ut conscious perception","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reflexes are chronically suppressed after spinal cord transection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reflexes are chronically suppressed after spinal cord transection</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>spinal cord transection</strong>, which is characterized by an <strong>exaggeration</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>reflexes</strong> and not suppression.</p>\n<p><strong>Upper motor neurons </strong>typically refer to corticospinal tract neurons that innervate spinal motor neurons. Damage to these neurons, as in transection of the spinal cord, initially causes muscles to become weak and flaccid but eventually leads to spasticity, hypertonia (increased resistance to passive movement), hyperactive reflexes, and abnormal plantar extensor reflex (positive Babinski sign).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0634","text":"A 50-year-old man with a h​istory of medically contr‍olled atrial fibrillation​ develops sudden onset we‌akness in the right side ​of his body. Which of the‌ following Broadman's are‌a is most likely affected​?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with a history of medically controlled atrial fibrillation develops sudden onset weakness in the right side of his body. Which of the following Broadman's area is most likely affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,​2,3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5​,7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16​,18","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/004c8694fd77447ebdb24980694e570ax364x334.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8665d75799d74b3bae671ed190a6c76fx800x507.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of an acute stroke, most probably due to atrial fibrillation induced thromboembolism. Since the patient has developed motor weakness in the right side of his body, he most likely has an infarct affecting the <strong>primary motor cortex</strong> ( <strong>broadmann area 4</strong>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The primary motor cortex </strong>(or just the motor cortex) can be defined as the region of the cortex from which movements are elicited with the least amount of electrical stimulation.</li>\n<li>It is essentially congruent with Brodmann's cytoarchitectonic <strong>area 4</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>primary motor cortex (M1) </strong>is in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, extending into the central sulcus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0635","text":"Skilled voluntary movement​ is initiated by____","raw_text":"Skilled voluntary movement is initiated by____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associati​on cortex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Basal ​ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebra​l cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Association cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a7dfa1a2e8b4e3480b329dcdd736fd7x1280x1693.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Association cortex</strong></p><p>Commands for voluntary movements originate in <strong>cortical association areas.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>corticospinal</strong> and <strong>corticobulbar system</strong> (originating in the motor cortex located in precentral gyrus) is the <strong>primary pathway</strong> for the initiation of <strong>skilled voluntary movement.</strong></p>\n<p>The movements are planned in the <strong>cortex</strong> as well as in the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> and the <strong>lateral portions of the cerebellar hemispheres</strong>.</p>\n<p>The basal ganglia and cerebellum funnel information to the premotor and motor cortex by way of the thalamus.</p>\n<p>Motor commands from the motor cortex are relayed in large part via the <strong>corticospinal tracts</strong> to the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>Movement sets up alterations in sensory input from the special senses and from muscles, tendons, joints, and the skin. This feedback information, which adjusts and smooths movement, is relayed directly to the motor cortex and to the spinocerebellum.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 249\"> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7051","text":"In the homunculus, which o​f the following has maxim‍um representation?","raw_text":"In the homunculus, which of the following has maximum representation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low control and skilled mo​vements","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More muscle fibres are pre​sent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased preciseness of m​ovements","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased preciseness of movements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f0376d25f5443e3a9500cd6de8f9205x1280x1275.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased preciseness of movements</strong></p><p>In a homunculus, the more the representation of an area, the more precise the movements are. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The relative sizes of the cortical receiving areas of the sensory homunculus are<strong> proportionate to the use of the part.</strong></li>\n<li>The sizes of these areas are <strong>directly proportional to the number of specialized sensory receptors </strong>in each respective peripheral area of the body.</li>\n<li>Some body parts are represented by large areas in the somatic cortex—the <strong>lips the greatest</strong> of all, followed by the<strong> face and thumb</strong>,<strong> </strong>whereas the <strong>trunk and lower part of the body</strong> are represented by relatively <strong>small areas.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0678","text":"In the degrees of represen​tation of the different m‍uscle areas as mapped by ​Penfield and Rasmussen in‌ the primary motor cortex​, which of the following ‌has the maximum represent‌ation?","raw_text":"In the degrees of representation of the different muscle areas as mapped by Penfield and Rasmussen in the primary motor cortex, which of the following has the maximum representation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vocali​zation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Masti​cation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Saliv​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Th​umb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vocalization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a81a39376e024437aafdf56aad117c27x1280x1806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vocalization</strong></p><p>As seen from the figure given below, <strong>Vocalization &gt; Salivation &gt; Mastication &gt; Thumb</strong></p>\n<p>The topographical organization is demonstrated graphically in the figure<strong>, </strong>which shows the degrees of representation of the different muscle areas as mapped by Penfield and Rasmussen. This mapping was done by electrically stimulating the different areas of the motor cortex in human beings who were undergoing neurosurgical operations. </p>\n<p>The<strong> face</strong> is represented<strong> bilaterally</strong>. The rest of the <strong>body</strong> has a <strong>unilateral</strong> representation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0682","text":"While doing a neurosurgica​l procedure, the paracent‍ral lobules of both hemis​pheres were damaged. It w‌ill lead to which of the ​following?","raw_text":"While doing a neurosurgical procedure, the paracentral lobules of both hemispheres were damaged. It will lead to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemip​legia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monop​legia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parap​legia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Quadri​plegia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paraplegia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paraplegia</strong></p><p>Damage to both paracentral lobules will cause paraplegia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>paracentral lobule</strong> is the medial <strong>continuation</strong> of the precentral and postcentral <strong>gyri</strong>. It controls <strong>motor and sensory</strong> innervations of the contralateral <strong>lower extremity</strong>. It is also responsible for the control of defecation and urination.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0636","text":"Setting posture before pla​nned movement is associat‍ed with ____.","raw_text":"Setting posture before planned movement is associated with ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Premoto​r cortex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Motor ​cortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fro​ntal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supple​mentary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Premotor cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/722c52f158694143be88d068c27f4d62x637x324.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Premotor cortex</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>The premotor cortex</strong> may be concerned with a setting posture at the start of a planned movement and getting the individual prepared to move. It is most involved in control of proximal limb muscles needed to orient the body for movement.</li>\n<li>It is located anterior to the precentral gyrus, on the lateral and medial cortical surface; it also con­tains a somatotopic map almost identical to the topographic organization in primary motor cortex with the mouth and face areas located most laterally. As one moves upward, the hand, arm, trunk, and leg areas are encountered. (as seen in the figure below)</li>\n<li>This region receives input from sensory regions of the parietal cortex and projects to primary motor cortex (M1), the spinal cord, and the brainstem reticular formation.</li>\n<li>A special class of neurons called <strong>mirror neurons</strong> becomes active when a person performs a specific motor task or when he or she observes the same task performed by others. These neurons transform sensory representations of acts that are heard or seen into motor representations of these acts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0677","text":"A quadriplegic man with a ​high cervical lesion deve‍lops sudden onset of flus​hing, sweating, pounding ‌headache and hypertension​. He has a distended blad‌der due to a blocked urin‌ary catheter. On immediat​e drainage of the urinary‌ bladder and repositionin​g him from supine to recl​ining, his symptoms resol‍ve. Which of the followin​g is false regarding the ​most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A  quadriplegic man with a high cervical lesion develops sudden onset of flushing, sweating, pounding headache and hypertension. He has a distended bladder due to a blocked urinary catheter. On immediate drainage of the urinary bladder and repositioning him from supine to reclining, his symptoms resolve. Which of the following is false regarding the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SBP may rise over 200 mm H​g","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Major portion of the body’​s skeletal muscle go into‍ extensor spasm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Large areas of the body br​eak out into profuse swea‍ting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colon and Bladder evacuate​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Major portion of the body’s skeletal muscle go into extensor spasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Major portion of the body’s skeletal muscle go into extensor spasm</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>spinal mass reflex</strong> which is seen in patients with a <strong>high spinal lesion</strong>. A major portion of the body’s skeletal muscles goes into strong <strong>flexor</strong> spasm, and <strong>not</strong> extensor spasm,<strong> </strong>in a spinal mass reflex.</p>\n<p><strong>Mass Reflex: </strong>In a spinal animal or human being, sometimes the spinal cord suddenly becomes excessively active, causing massive discharge in large portions of the cord. The usual stimulus that causes this excess activity is a strong pain stimulus to the skin or excessive filling of a viscus, such as over-distention of the bladder or the gut. The mass reflex involves large portions or even all of the cord.</p>\n<p>The effects are as follows: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>A major portion of the body’s skeletal muscles goes into strong <strong>flexor spasms;</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>colon</strong> and <strong>bladder</strong> are likely to <strong>evacuate</strong>;</li>\n<li>The arterial pressure often rises to maximal values, sometimes to a <strong>systolic pressure</strong> well <strong>over 200 mm Hg</strong>; and</li>\n<li>Large areas of the body break out into profuse <strong>sweating</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The mechanism is similar to the mechanism of epileptic seizures, which involve reverberating circuits that occur in the brain instead of in the cord.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BhtEDE11","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"14_other_substance_use_disorders","title":"14.Other Substance Use Disorders","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5898","text":"Which of the following sub​stances do not lead to ph‌ysical dependence?","raw_text":"Which of the following substances do not lead to physical dependence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L​SD","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opi​oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nico​tine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. LSD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. LSD</strong></p><p><strong>Neither physical dependence nor withdrawal symptoms</strong> occur with <strong>LSD,</strong> but a user can develop a psychological dependence on the insight-inducing experiences of episodes of hallucinogen use.</p>\n<p>Physical dependence indicates an altered physiological state due to repeated administration of a drug, the cessation of which results in the appearance of a withdrawal syndrome characteristic of the particular drug.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychological dependence</strong> also referred to as habituation, is characterized by a continuous or intermittent craving (i.e., intense desire) for the substance to avoid a dysphoric state(state of unease or dissatisfaction with depression, anxiety, or agitation).</p>\n<p>The physical and psychological <strong>dependence potential</strong> of <strong>MDMA</strong> (3,4 methylenedioxy-methamphetamine) is also <strong>not well established</strong>. It is an illegal, synthetic drug classified as a stimulant with potentially hallucinogenic properties.</p>\n<p>Agents, known to cause strong physical and psychological dependence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Opioids</li>\n<li><strong>Alcohol</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nicotine</strong></li>\n<li>Cannabis</li>\n<li><strong>Barbiturates</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5915","text":"A patient undergoing withd​rawal from which of the f‌ollowing drugs is least l​ikely to experience suici‌dal thoughts?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing withdrawal from which of the following drugs is least likely to experience suicidal thoughts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbit​urates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opi​oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L​SD","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. LSD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. LSD</strong></p><p><strong>Withdrawal symptoms </strong>do not occur with hallucinogens such as LSD. Withdrawal symptoms appear when the drug abuse leads to physical dependence.</p>\n<p>Drugs that cause significant withdrawal symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Opioids</li>\n<li>Cocaine</li>\n<li>Nicotine</li>\n<li>Barbiturates</li>\n<li>BZD</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2389","text":"A 34-year-old man who came​ to the dermatology clini‌c for acne complaints was​ incidentally noted to ha‌ve multiple scars suspect​ed to be from 'skin poppi​ng'. He is most likely to​ be abusing which of the ​following drugs?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man who came to the dermatology clinic for acne complaints was incidentally noted to have multiple scars suspected to be from 'skin popping'. He is most likely to be abusing which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opi​oid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesc​aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L​SD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Opioid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf315f448b2542888eb665ffb6c607e7x500x375.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Opioid</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>opioid abuse</strong> is the most likely cause of the skin popping scars in this patient. <strong>Skin popping</strong> is a method of injecting illicit drugs into the skin. Drugs like <strong>heroin, </strong>cocaine, barbiturates<strong> </strong>can be injected intradermally or subcutaneously with the goal of achieving slower absorption, decreased risk of overdose, and easier administration than with intravenous drug use.</p>\n<p>LSD and mescaline (hallucinogens) and cannabis are not taken through injections, but by oral route or through smoking.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows circular depressed scars from skin popping.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5930","text":"A college student was brou​ght to the casualty by hi‌s friends after he was fo​und unresponsive in his h‌ostel room. On examinatio​n, his respiratory rate w​as 8 breaths per minute, ​BP: 80/60 mmHg, and had p​inpoint pupils. What is t​he most probable cause of‌ these findings?","raw_text":"A college student was brought to the casualty by his friends after he was found unresponsive in his hostel room. On examination, his respiratory rate was 8 breaths per minute, BP: 80/60 mmHg, and had pinpoint pupils. What is the most probable cause of these findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cocaine in​toxication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcohol in​toxication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heroin w​ithdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid in​toxication","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Opioid intoxication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Opioid intoxication</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>opioid intoxication.</strong> Opioids include morphine, heroin, codeine, etc.</p>\n<p>Opioid <strong>overdose</strong> (intoxication) is classically associated with the triad of <strong>coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils </strong>(miosis). Hypotension, bradycardia, and hypothermia are also seen.</p>\n<p><strong>O</strong>pioid<strong> withdrawal</strong> symptoms (mnemonic- O MY WITHDRAWALS):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>My</strong>driasis</li>\n<li><strong>Y</strong>awning</li>\n<li><strong>W</strong>eakness</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nsomnia, irritability</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>remors, tachycardia</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>yperthermia, hypertension</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iarrhea</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>hinorrhoea</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>pnoeic attacks</li>\n<li><strong>W</strong>eight loss</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>nxiety</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>acrimation</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>weating</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5934","text":"A middle-aged man was brou​ght to the ED with suspec‌ted morphine overdose. He​ was intubated and mechan‌ical ventilation was star​ted. What is the drug of ​choice to be administered​ in this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man was brought to the ED with suspected morphine overdose. He was intubated and mechanical ventilation was started. What is the drug of choice to be administered in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV Na​loxone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IM Met​hadone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV Clo​nidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral Na​ltrexone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IV Naloxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IV Naloxone</strong></p><p><strong>Intravenous naloxone</strong> is the drug of choice in acute morphine overdose.</p>\n<p>The <strong>first step</strong> is to ensure an <strong>adequate airway</strong>. Intubation and mechanical ventilation may be necessary.</p>\n<p>IV<strong> </strong>Naloxone is administered at a slow rate. Signs of improvement (increased respiratory rate and pupillary dilation) should occur promptly. If no response to the initial dosage, repeat after a few minutes. Repeated administration may be required to prevent recurrence of opioid toxicity because the duration of action of naloxone is less.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5922","text":"An anxious 45-year-old man​ comes to the casualty, d‌renched in sweat. He is c​onstantly wiping secretio‌ns from his mouth and nos​e and also complains of a​bdominal cramps. On exami​nation, his BP is elevate​d and mydriasis is seen. ​On further questioning, h‌e reluctantly reveals a h​istory suggestive of drug‌ abuse. Which of the foll‌owing is most likely to b‍e the cause?","raw_text":"An anxious 45-year-old man comes to the casualty, drenched in sweat. He is constantly wiping secretions from his mouth and nose and also complains of abdominal cramps. On examination, his BP is elevated and mydriasis is seen. On further questioning, he reluctantly reveals a history suggestive of drug abuse. Which of the following is most likely to be the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcohol w​ithdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heroin w​ithdrawal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cocaine w​ithdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannabis ​withdrawal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heroin withdrawal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heroin withdrawal</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a patient with the classic symptoms of <strong>heroin withdrawal.</strong></p>\n<p>Heroin withdrawal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Characteristic symptoms - myalgia, arthralgia, abdominal cramps, nausea, diarrhea, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, and sweating.</li>\n<li>Signs on physical examination - restlessness, elevated heart rate and blood pressure, pupillary dilatation (mydriasis), piloerection, and yawning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment for opioid dependence:</p>\n<p>Detoxification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long-acting opioid agonist - a safer substitute like methadone is given in a supervised manner to prevent withdrawal symptoms.</li>\n<li>Partial agonist - buprenorphine can also be used.</li>\n<li>Clonidine, lofexidine - alpha-2 agonist acts as sympatholytic and reduces the withdrawal symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Maintenance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methadone, buprenorphine, LAAM (L-Alpha-Acetyl-Methadol) - opioid agonists which reduce craving.</li>\n<li>Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist that blocks opioid effects, particularly euphoria, and discourages per­sons with opioid dependence from substance-seeking behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2413","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is also known by the s‌treet name 'crack'?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is also known by the street name 'crack'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Her​oin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coc​aine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma Hydro​xy Butyrate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cocaine</strong></p><p>Crack is a freebase form of <strong>cocaine </strong>and is extremely potent. It is sold in small, ready-to-smoke amounts, commonly called rocks. Crack cocaine is highly addictive, even one or two experiences with the drug can cause an intense craving for more.</p>\n<p><strong>γ-Hydroxybutyrate (GHB) </strong>also called<strong> liquid ecstasy</strong> is a naturally-occurring neurotransmitter. It is a central nervous system (CNS) depressant that acts through the endogenous opioid system.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5957","text":"A 21-year-old student, wit​h his mother, presented t‌o the OPD with complaints​ of reduced concentration‌, and a lack of motivatio​n related to attending co​llege. Initially, only hi​s attendance, classwork, ​and performance in exams ​were affected but lately,‌ his mother adds that he ​has lost interest in goin‌g out to eat or even main‌taining personal hygiene.‍ On probing further, he g‍ives a history of substan‌ce abuse. Which of the fo​llowing is most likely?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old student, with his mother, presented to the OPD with complaints of reduced concentration, and a lack of motivation related to attending college. Initially, only his attendance, classwork, and performance in exams were affected but lately, his mother adds that he has lost interest in going out to eat or even maintaining personal hygiene. On probing further, he gives a history of substance abuse. Which of the following is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cann​abis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L​SD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cannabis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cannabis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>amotivational syndrome.</strong> It has been associated with long-term heavy use of <strong>cannabis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by a person's <strong>unwillingness to persist in a task </strong>at school, at work, or in any setting that requires prolonged attention or tenacity. Persons are described as becoming apathetic and anergic, usually gaining weight, and appearing slothful.</p>\n<p>Products obtained from cannabis are marijuana, bhang, ganja, hashish, and charas.</p>\n<p>Features associated with cannabis intoxication:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjunctival injection (red eye)</li>\n<li>Dry mouth</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Increased appetite</li>\n<li>Heightened sensitivity to external stimuli</li>\n<li>Depersonalization and derealization</li>\n<li>Impaired motor skill</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hemp insanity </strong>refers to cannabis-induced psychotic disorder.</p>\n<p><strong>Run amok </strong>refers to cannabis-induced rampage destroying things and killing people.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5959","text":"Which drug is commonly ass​ociated with flashback ph‌enomenon?","raw_text":"Which drug is commonly associated with flashback phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbit​urates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benzod​iapenes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cann​abis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cannabis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cannabis</strong></p><p>Flashback phenomenon is more commonly seen with <strong>cannabis</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 664\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Flashback phenomenon </strong>refers to a person experiencing sensations related to substance intoxication after the short-term effects of the substance have disappeared. That is, the person relives the substance (cannabis) use experience without consuming it.</p>\n<p>It is also seen in lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD) and phencyclidine which are hallucinogens.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5965","text":"Angel dust is a street nam​e for which drug?","raw_text":"Angel dust is a street name for which drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phency​clidine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L​SD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Her​oin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phencyclidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phencyclidine</strong></p><p><strong>Angel dust</strong> is the street name for phencyclidine. It is an <strong>NMDA antagonist.</strong></p>\n<p>Popular street names of abused drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phencyclidine(PCP)-</strong> Angel dust, peace pill</li>\n<li><strong>Marijuana- </strong>Pot, weed</li>\n<li><strong>Heroin-</strong> Smack</li>\n<li><strong>Cocaine-</strong> Crack</li>\n<li><strong>Methylenedioxymethamphetamine(MDMA)-</strong> Ecstasy, X</li>\n<li><strong>Flunitrazepam-</strong> Roofie, forget-me pill</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Speedball is a combination of cocaine and heroin(opioid).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5956","text":"A patient who was initiall​y admitted with a history‌ suggestive of paranoid s​chizophrenia is now suspe‌cted to have drug-induced​ psychosis. Which of the ​following drugs is the mo​st likely causative agent​?","raw_text":"A patient who was initially admitted with a history suggestive of paranoid schizophrenia is now suspected to have drug-induced psychosis. Which of the following drugs is the most likely causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clonidine i​ntoxication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amphetam​ine abuse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"LSD ​abuse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannabi​s abuse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amphetamine abuse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amphetamine abuse</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Amphetamine abuse</strong> resembles <strong>paranoid schizophrenia. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Amphetamine is a stimulant that causes stimulant-induced psychotic disorder. It induces ideas of reference, paranoid delusions, and hallucinations. </p>\n<p>The treatment of choice for the amphetamine-induced psychotic disorder is the short-term use of an antipsychotic medication such as <strong>haloperidol</strong>.</p>\n<p> Drug-induced psychosis can also be seen with chronic and heavy exposure to cocaine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5964","text":"A man was brought in for q​uestioning after a teenag‌er was found dead at a Go​an rave party. He reveals‌ that he runs a nightclub​ in the city and that he ​has noticed anonymous peo​ple selling certain drugs​ that can enhance energy,​ endurance, and sociabili‌ty in the clubbers who ta​ke them. Which of the fol‌lowing drugs is he most l‌ikely referring to?","raw_text":"A man was brought in for questioning after a teenager was found dead at a Goan rave party. He reveals that he runs a nightclub in the city and that he has noticed anonymous people selling certain drugs that can enhance energy, endurance, and sociability in the clubbers who take them. Which of the following drugs is he most likely referring to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methamp​hetamine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Her​oin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methamphetamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methamphetamine</strong></p><p>Methamphetamine is a <strong>rave</strong> <strong>drug</strong> among the given options. Few substances are commonly used in all-night dance parties (raves) and in clubs to <strong>increase energy, endurance, sociability, </strong>and<strong> arousal.</strong> These substances are not all in the same drug class, and they do not produce the same physical or subjective effects.</p>\n<p>This includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methamphetamine</strong></li>\n<li>Methylenedioxymethamphetamine- MDMA</li>\n<li>LSD</li>\n<li>Gamma hydroxybutyrate (GHB)</li>\n<li>Ketamine</li>\n<li>Flunitrazepam (Rohypnol)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>GHB</strong>, <strong>Ketamine</strong>, and <strong>Rohypnol</strong> have been called <strong>date rape drugs</strong> because they produce <strong>disorienting</strong> and <strong>sedating</strong> effects, and often users <strong>cannot recall what occurred</strong> <strong>under the influence of the drug.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0862","text":"A 25-year-old male present​ed to the clinic with a c‌omplaint of the feeling o​f insects crawling under ‌the skin. Which of the fo​llowing drug abuse can ca​use the symptoms of this ​patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old male presented to the clinic with a complaint of the feeling of insects crawling under the skin. Which of the following drug abuse can cause the symptoms of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coc​aine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cocaine</strong></p><p>The feeling of insects crawling under the skin, also known as <strong>cocaine bugs</strong> or <strong>formication</strong>, is a feature of <strong>cocaine </strong>abuse.</p>\n<p>Other features of acute cocaine intoxication are delusions of persecution, paranoia, and suspiciousness. It is essential to differentiate acute cocaine intoxication from acute schizophrenia which it closely resembles.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5962","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is used as an anti-smo‌king agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is used as an anti-smoking agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Busu​lfan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acamp​rosate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Varen​icline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gabap​entin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Varenicline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Varenicline</strong></p><p><strong>Varenicline</strong> is an <strong>α4β2 nicotinic acetylcholine receptor partial agonist</strong> used in the treatment of nicotine addiction.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The FDA approved medication to aid in smoking cessation include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">NRT-<strong>Nicotine Replacement Therapy</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Bupropion</strong> </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Varenicline</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drugs are only adjunctive to <strong>behavioral therapy</strong>, which is the <strong>first line of management.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Behavior therapy is the most widely accepted and well-proven psychological therapy for treating Tobacco use disorder. It consists of several techniques:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skill training and relapse prevention.</li>\n<li>Stimulus control.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Mindfulness approaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bupropion:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It enhances both noradrenergic and dopaminergic neurotransmission by inhibiting reuptake.</li>\n<li>Used for aiding <strong>smoking cessation </strong>and for the treatment of depression in combination with SSRIs.</li>\n<li>Side effects: seizures, sedation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Varenicline:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>α4β2 Nicotinic ACh receptor partial agonist.</strong></li>\n<li>Used for <strong>smoking cessation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Side effects: sleep disturbance, may depress mood and increase suicidal tendencies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mirtazapine:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serotonin receptor antagonist.</strong></li>\n<li>Used for the treatment of <strong>depression</strong> in combination with SSRIs and for <strong>insomnia</strong> in depressive patients.</li>\n<li>Side effect: sedation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF3032","text":"A physician prescribed var​enicline to a young smoke‌r who wanted to quit smok​ing. About which of the f‌ollowing adverse effect s​hould he be counselled ab​out?","raw_text":"A physician prescribed varenicline to a young smoker who wanted to quit smoking. About which of the following adverse effect should he be counselled about?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased seizure threshol​d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatot​oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin​ rash","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suicidal​ ideation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Suicidal ideation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Suicidal ideation</strong></p><p><strong>Suicidal ideation</strong> is a side effect associated with <strong>varenicline</strong>.</p>\n<p>Varenicline is an α4β2 nicotinic acetylcholine receptor partial agonist.</p>\n<p>The FDA approved medications to aid cessation of smoking tobacco:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>NRT- Nicotine Replacement Therapy</li>\n<li>Bupropion</li>\n<li>Varenicline</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bupropion is associated with <strong>decreased seizure threshold </strong>and<strong> suicidal ideation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7JctqtVN","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"14_radiotherapy","title":"14.Radiotherapy","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA0869","text":"What is the most common ra​diation used in radiother​apy?","raw_text":"What is the most common radiation used in radiotherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamma​ rays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-r​ays","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta​ rays","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alpha​ rays","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. X-rays","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. X-rays</strong></p><p><strong>X-rays</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> form of radiation used in <strong>radiotherapy. </strong>They are produced from <strong>LINAC </strong>(Linear Accelerator).</p>\n<p>If X-rays are not among the options, the second best answer would be <strong>gamma rays</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Sources of radiation exposure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Natural</strong>: Cosmic rays, environmental (terrestrial and atmospheric), and radioactive isotopes - of potassium (K-40) and carbon (C-14).</li>\n<li><strong>Man-made</strong>: Medical and dental (X-rays, radioisotopes), occupational exposure and nuclear (radioactive fallout).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0870","text":"Which one of the following​ has maximum ionization p​otential?","raw_text":"Which one of the following has maximum ionization potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elec​tron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pro​ton","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heliu​m ion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma-​photon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Helium ion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Helium ion</strong></p><p><strong>Helium ion </strong>has the <strong>maximum ionizing potential.</strong> It is identical to alpha particles, with two protons and two neutrons.</p>\n<p>It has the least penetrating ability and penetrates only superficial tissues to an extent of 1 mm.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0892","text":"What is the most radiosens​itive phase of the cell c​ycle?","raw_text":"What is the most radiosensitive phase of the cell cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G2​-M","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​M","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​S","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. G2-M","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. G2-M</strong></p><p>The cells are<strong> most radiosensitive </strong>in the <strong>G2-M phase </strong>of the cell cycle. </p>\n<p>This is because the cells lack sufficient time to repair DNA damage before division in the G2-M phase, leading to mitotic catastrophe. </p>\n<p><strong>Radiosensitivity</strong> gradually <strong>decreases</strong> from <strong>G1 </strong>to the <strong>early S</strong> phase and is the <strong>least</strong> in the <strong>late S phase</strong>, where homologous recombination repair is most active after DNA replication.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0875","text":"Match the following SI uni​ts with the parameters me​asured. SI Unit Parameter‍ measured 1. Gray A. Dose​ equivalent 2. Becquerel ‍B. Absorbed radiation 3. ‍Coulomb/kg C. Radioactivi‌ty 4. Sievert D. Total ra‌diation exposure","raw_text":"<p>Match the following SI units with the parameters measured.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px; width: 435px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\"><strong>SI Unit </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 30.6805px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\"><strong>Parameter measured</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Gray </td>\n<td style=\"width: 30.6805px;\">A.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Dose equivalent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Becquerel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30.6805px;\">B.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Absorbed radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Coulomb/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30.6805px;\">C.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Radioactivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Sievert</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30.6805px;\">D.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Total radiation exposure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A, 2-D,​ 3-C, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-C,​ 3-D, 4-A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-B, 2-A,​ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-A, 2-D,​ 3-B, 4-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A</strong></p><p>The correctly matched SI units and parameters measured are as follows: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px; width: 435px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\"><strong>SI Unit </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\"><strong>Parameter measured</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Gray </td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Absorbed radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Becquerel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Radioactivity </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Coulomb/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Total radiation exposure </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.319px;\">Sievert</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">Dose equivalent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>common</strong> unit to measure radioactivity is <strong>c</strong><strong>urie.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>common</strong> unit to measure total radiation exposure is <strong>r</strong><strong>oentgen.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3998","text":"The Bragg peak effect is p​ronounced in:","raw_text":"The Bragg peak effect is pronounced in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X ​ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pro​ton","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neu​tron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elec​tron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proton","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62c4f6d692354217afdc5a9e9ef3ce48x1280x901.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proton</strong></p><p>The<strong> Bragg peak effect </strong>is pronounced in <strong>protons</strong>.</p>\n<p>The proton is a heavy and charged particle that gradually loses its speed as it interacts with human tissue. It is easily controlled and delivers its maximum dose at a precise depth and can go as far as 32 cm. The absorbed dose increases very gradually with greater depth and lower speed, <strong>suddenly rising</strong> to a <strong>peak</strong> when the proton is ultimately stopped. This is known as the Bragg peak.</p>\n<p>The behavior of the proton can be precisely determined, and the beam can be directed, allowing the Bragg peak to occur within the tumor site. Immediately following this burst of energy, the protons cease radiating and come to a complete stop. This property of <strong>proton therapy </strong>enables<strong> targeting tumors</strong> within the body, delivering radiation directly to the tumor while <strong>sparing</strong> the <strong>surrounding healthy cells</strong>, offering a much less invasive alternative to treat cancer.</p>\n<p>The Bragg curve shown below plots the energy loss of ionizing radiation during its travel through matter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0879","text":"Which of the following is ​not used for radionuclide​ therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used for radionuclide therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I-​131","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P-​32","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Re-​186","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ga​-68","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ga-68","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ga-68</strong></p><p><strong>Ga-68 </strong>(Gallium-68) is a radioisotope that is only used for imaging purposes such as <strong>PET scans</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gallium-68 somatostatin receptor Positron Emission Tomography (Ga-68 PET) is used in the diagnosis of <strong>neuroendocrine tumors</strong> (NETs).</p>\n<p><strong>P-32</strong> (Phosphorous), <strong>I-131 </strong>(Iodine) and <strong>Re-186</strong> (Rhenium) are used in <strong>radiotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0880","text":"A woman diagnosed with DCI​S underwent surgery and i​s now posted for adjuvant‍ radiotherapy with extern​al beam radiotherapy. Whi‍ch of the following isoto‍pes is commonly used for ‌the same?","raw_text":"A woman diagnosed with DCIS underwent surgery and is now posted for adjuvant radiotherapy with external beam radiotherapy. Which of the following isotopes is commonly used for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coba​lt-59","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cobal​t -60","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radiu​m-226","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stront​ium-89","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cobalt -60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cobalt -60</strong></p><p><strong>Cobalt-60 </strong>(Co-60 ) isotope is used for <strong>external beam radiotherapy</strong> (EBRT).</p>\n<p>External beam radiotherapy is given as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cobalt machine</strong> - Co-60 produces gamma rays. It emits radiation of energy 1.25 MeV.</li>\n<li><strong>LINAC</strong> (linear accelerator) - X-ray beams are generated to provide ionising radiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The energy spectrum used in radiotherapy is classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Orthovoltage - 200-500kV x-rays are produced. It is used to treat skin conditions. </li>\n<li>Megavoltage - 1-25 MV x-rays are produced. These are used in modern radiotherapy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0881","text":"Which of the following rad​ioactive isotopes is not ​used for brachytherapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following radioactive isotopes is not used for brachytherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodin​e-125","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iodin​e-131","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coba​lt-60","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iridi​um-192","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iodine-131","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iodine-131</strong></p><p><strong>I-131</strong> is a beta-emitter used for <strong>systemic radiotherapy </strong>but <strong>not</strong> for <strong>brachytherapy</strong>. It is taken up by thyroid tissue and differentiated thyroid cancers. </p>\n<p>Brachytherapy is a type of radiotherapy where radioactive sources are <strong>placed inside </strong>or<strong> close</strong> to the<strong> target</strong> area. It delivers radiation over <strong>short distances</strong> using <strong>interstitial</strong>, <strong>intracavitary</strong>, or surface placement techniques. The following isotopes are used in brachytherapy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Permanent Low-dose rate (LDR) </strong>implants generally use<strong> Iodine-125,</strong> Palladium-103, or less commonly Gold-198. </li>\n<li><strong>Temporary LDR </strong>implants<strong> </strong>may use<strong> Iridium-192, </strong>Cesium-137<strong>, Cobalt-60 </strong>or others. </li>\n<li><strong>High-dose rate (HDR)</strong> implants almost always use<strong> Iridium-192. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0883","text":"Which of the following mod​alities use remote afterl​oading?","raw_text":"Which of the following modalities use remote afterloading?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Telet​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brachy​therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radioactive iodine therapy​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Targeted​ therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brachytherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brachytherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Remote afterloading</strong> is used in <strong>brachytherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>In brachytherapy, sealed radioactive sources are used to deliver radiation at a short distance by interstitial, intracavitary, or surface application.</p>\n<p>Afterloading is the process of placing the radioactive material into the target region. This is either done manually by the oncologist, or by using remote afterloading devices.</p>\n<p><strong>Remote afterloading</strong> devices enable us to<strong> eliminate direct contact</strong> with <strong>radioactive</strong> sources while administration. It provides protection from radiation exposure to healthcare professionals by securing the radiation source in a shielded safe manner.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0884","text":"Which of the following is ​not a radioprotector?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a radioprotector?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zinc ​oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pentoxi​phylline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxy​gen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amifo​stine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oxygen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oxygen</strong></p><p><strong>Oxygen</strong> is a <strong>radiosensitizer, </strong>while zinc oxide, pentoxiphylline and amifostine are<strong> radioprotectors.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0885","text":"You are a radiation oncolo​gy resident explaining th​e harmful effects of radi‍ation to a patient. Which​ of the following is not ‍a deterministic effect of‍ the same?","raw_text":"You are a radiation oncology resident explaining the harmful effects of radiation to a patient. Which of the following is not a deterministic effect of the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bu​rns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cata​ract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hair​ loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muta​tions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mutations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mutations</strong></p><p><strong>Mutations</strong> are <strong>stochastic effects</strong> that occur as a result of <strong>radiation</strong> exposure.</p>\n<p>The harmful effects of radiation may be classified into two general categories: <strong>s</strong><strong>tochastic</strong> and <strong>nonstochastic/deterministic</strong> effects.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 597px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.117px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.195px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Stochastic effects</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.297px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Deterministic effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.117px;\">Description</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.195px;\">These are chance effects. The probability of occurrence increases with increasing absorbed dose, but severity does not depend on the amount of absorbed dose. This is called the 'all or none' phenomenon.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.297px;\">Increases in severity with increasing absorbed dose in affected individuals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.117px;\">Threshold level</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.195px; text-align: center;\">It cannot be set.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.297px; text-align: center;\">It can be set.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.117px;\">Examples </td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.195px;\"><strong>Genetic</strong> effect, development of <strong>cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.297px;\">Organ atrophy, fibrosis, <strong>lens opacification</strong>, blood changes, decrease in sperm count, irreversible <strong>skin damage</strong>,fetal abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0886","text":"What is the most radiosens​itive tissue in our body?​","raw_text":"What is the most radiosensitive tissue in our body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mus​cles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone ​marrow","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nervous​ tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bone marrow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bone marrow</strong></p><p>The<strong> m</strong><strong>ost radiosensitive</strong> tissue in our body is <strong>bone marrow.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>least</strong> radiosensitive tissue is the <strong>nervous tissue.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>most radiosensitive</strong> blood cells are <strong>lymphocytes</strong> and<strong> </strong>the<strong> least</strong> are <strong>platelets. RBCs </strong>are relatively<strong> radioresistant. </strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0887","text":"Granisetron was prescribed​ to a patient with gastro​intestinal radiation synd‍rome to manage his sympto​ms. At what dose of radia‍tion does this condition ‍typically occur?","raw_text":"Granisetron was prescribed to a patient with gastrointestinal radiation syndrome to manage his symptoms. At what dose of radiation does this condition typically occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100–3​00 rad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"300–4​00 rad","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"400–6​00 rad","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"600–10​00 rad","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 600–1000 rad","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 600–1000 rad</strong></p><p><strong>Gastrointestinal radiation syndrome </strong>occurs <strong>after</strong> exposure of <strong>600-1000 rad.</strong></p>\n<p>Acute radiation syndrome is characterized by three main presentations:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hematopoietic -</strong> marked by aplastic anemia. This occurs at 2–3 Gy or higher. This may result in infections, bleeding, and anemia due to the destruction of red blood cells. </li>\n<li><strong>Gastrointestinal - </strong>occurs due to absorbed doses of 6-7 Gy (600–1000 rad). The signs and symptoms include nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite, and abdominal pain. Symptomatic management is advised with intravenous ondansetron or oral granisetron. </li>\n<li><strong>Neurovascular -</strong> This syndrome typically occurs at absorbed doses greater than 30 Gy (3000 rad), though it may occur at 10 Gy (1000 rad). It presents with neurological symptoms such as dizziness, headache, or decreased level of consciousness, occurring within minutes to a few hours, and with an absence of vomiting. It is invariably fatal.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0889","text":"Patients with which of the​ following tumors are bes​t treated with radiothera‍py?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following tumors are best treated with radiotherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi​noma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mela​noma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreatic​ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminoma</strong></p><p><strong>Seminoma</strong> is a <strong>highly radiosensitive</strong> tumor and is best managed with radiotherapy, in contrast to melanoma, osteosarcoma and pancreatic carcinoma, which are known to be resistant to radiation therapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0890","text":"Patients with which of the​ following tumors are tre​ated with craniospinal ir‍radiation?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following tumors are treated with craniospinal irradiation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligoden​droglioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pilocytic ​astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mixed oligo​astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medulloblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medulloblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Medulloblastoma</strong> is treated with <strong>craniospinal irradiation</strong>. It is a radiosensitive tumor that responds very well to radiotherapy.</p>\n<p>The indications for craniospinal radiation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Therapeutic</strong> - for radiosensitive brain tumors such as <strong>medulloblastoma</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prophylactic</strong> - CNS malignancies that show dissemination via CSF such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Medulloblastoma</li>\n<li>Glioblastoma</li>\n<li>Germinoma</li>\n<li>Leptomeningeal rhabdomyosarcoma.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some radiosensitive tumors which have a high risk of metastasis to CNS. These include small cell lung cancer, acute lymphoblastic leukemia, Non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0891","text":"What is the recommended do​se limit of cumulative ra​diation exposure during p‍regnancy?","raw_text":"What is the recommended dose limit of cumulative radiation exposure during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ​mSv","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5 ​mSv","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50 ​mSv","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 ​mSv","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 mSv","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 mSv</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The recommended dose limit of <strong>cumulative radiation exposure</strong> during <strong>pregnancy</strong> is <strong>1mSv. </strong></p>\n<p>For pregnant patients who require diagnostics with exposure to ionizing radiation, the radiation protection principles are followed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The exposure should be justified</li>\n<li>The dose should be as low as reasonable achievable</li>\n<li>Dose limit is not particularly specified as it involves a single time exposure to diagnostic radiation during which effective dose to the fetus is usually &lt;1 mSv. The deterministic effects on the fetus are not seen below <strong>50 mSv (5 rad)</strong></li>\n<li>Occupational exposure during pregnancy should not exceed 1mSv/year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The dose limits for non-pregnant radiation exposure in non-pregnant individuals are:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Occupational exposure - Effective dose should not exceed <strong>20 mSv/year. </strong> </li>\n<li class=\"li1\">General public exposure - Effective dose should not exceed <strong>1 mSv/year. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0893","text":"A patient diagnosed with c​arcinoma cervix is underg​oing the treatment modali‍ty in the image given bel​ow. Which of the followin‍g elements is most common‍ly used in this?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with carcinoma cervix is undergoing the treatment modality in the image given below. Which of the following elements is most commonly used in this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cob​alt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Techn​etium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rad​ium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ces​ium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cesium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8fa0eab547424d998155c6ec363697e1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/801ab7935506401faa62542815621b10x1280x1211.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cesium</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>brachytherapy</strong> being used to treat carcinoma cervix. <strong>Cesium</strong> is the most <strong>common radioactive agent</strong> used for the same.</p>\n<p>In the majority of these treatments, radiation applicators are placed within a cavity of the body such as the bronchus (airway) in the lung, esophagus, vagina, cervix of the uterus.</p>\n<p>Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy in which the radiation source is placed close to the tumor. It can be either a low-dose or high-dose rate system. The primary advantage of internal radiation is the ability to deliver a <strong>higher radiation dose</strong> while the normal tissues receive less radiation since the radiation sources are<strong> placed within the tumor</strong>. Both Iridium and cesium have been used as radioactive sources to give internal radiation.</p>\n<p>Dosage is calculated with respect to the amount of radiation received at two arbitrary points A and B (image below).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 30.6452%; height: 122px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.3568%;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.6834%;\"><strong>Point A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.9598%;\"><strong>Point B</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.3568%;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.6834%;\">2cm above and 2cm lateral to the external os</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.9598%;\">2cm above and 5cm lateral to the external os</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.3568%;\"><strong>Structure present</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.6834%;\">crossing of the ureter and uterine artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.9598%;\">Obturator lymph node</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 21.3568%;\"><strong>Dose of radiation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.6834%;\">7000-8000 cGy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 41.9598%;\">6000 cGy </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Different techniques of brachytherapy:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 25.0384%; height: 111.992px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 16.6154%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Techniques</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.9651%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Device </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.1888%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\"><strong>No. of application</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 29.2308%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 16.6154%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stockholm</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.9651%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Intrauterine tandem</p>\n<p>One vaginal plaque</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.1888%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Three</td>\n<td style=\"width: 29.2308%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">24 hours each weekly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 16.6154%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Paris</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.9651%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Intrauterine tandem</p>\n<p>Three vaginal ovoids</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.1888%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">One</td>\n<td style=\"width: 29.2308%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">120 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 16.6154%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Manchester</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.9651%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Intrauterine tandem</p>\n<p>Two vaginal ovoids</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.1888%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Two</td>\n<td style=\"width: 29.2308%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">72 hours each weekly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0894","text":"A patient with inoperable ​small cell lung carcinoma​ is posted for thoracic e‍xternal beam radiotherapy​. How will the respirator‍y movement be counteracte‍d during this procedure?","raw_text":"A patient with inoperable small cell lung carcinoma is posted for thoracic external beam radiotherapy. How will the respiratory movement be counteracted during this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arc mod​ulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gat​ing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fracti​onation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Afterl​oading","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gating","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gating</strong></p><p>Respiratory movement is counteracted during radiotherapy by <strong>respiratory gating. </strong></p>\n<p>Radiation is only delivered when the tumor is exactly in the right place, and the treatment beam <strong>automatically turns off </strong>when the<strong> tumor moves</strong> outside<strong> </strong>of the target field. It is a process for <strong>continuously</strong> <strong>monitoring </strong>the movement of tumors during normal breathing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0896","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions can​ be treated with stereota‍ctic radiosurgery?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions can be treated with stereotactic radiosurgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage 1 lun​g carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Miliary m​etastasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphangitis carcinomatosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma base of tongue w​ith lymph nodes positive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stage 1 lung carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stage 1 lung carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Stage 1 lung carcinoma, </strong>being a localized tumor, is treated with <strong>stereotactic radiosurgery,</strong> a technique reserved for confined lesions<strong>. </strong>In contrast, the other conditions are<strong> </strong>widespread and unsuitable for<strong> </strong>localized therapy. </p>\n<p>Stereotactic radiotherapy involves distributing radiation beams in three dimensions and administering them in fractions, while stereotactic radiosurgery involves administering a single large dose in one session.<strong> </strong><strong>Gamma</strong> <strong>knife</strong> refers to stereotactic radiosurgery of the <strong>brain.</strong> It is used to treat acoustic neuromas. <strong>Cyberknife</strong> refers to stereotactic radiosurgery of the <strong>body.</strong> It is <strong>l</strong><strong>inear accelerator(LINAC)-based</strong> and used to treat brain tumors, prostate cancer, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0897","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is treated using ​gamma knife?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is treated using gamma knife?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gliobl​astoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterio-venous malformatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuber​culoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dandy-Walker​ malformation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterio-venous malformation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0da0a64582ab4a5091c050689a196d0cx1169x1510.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterio-venous malformation</strong></p><p>The <strong>arterio-venous malformation</strong> is treated using a <strong>gamma knife. </strong></p>\n<p>Stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS) is a reasonable alternative for people who refuse or cannot tolerate surgery. Here, beams are modified and focused only to concentrate on the tumor and have little effect on surrounding tissue. It can be done by using: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>X-knife</strong> using a linear accelerator (LINAC)</li>\n<li><strong>Gamma-knife</strong> using Cobalt 60 </li>\n<li><strong>CyberKnife</strong> - image-guided linear accelerator using real-time image guidance to deliver radiation from multiple angles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The patient wears a specialized helmet that is surgically fixed to the skull.<strong> Gamma knife</strong> aims gamma radiation through a <strong>target point</strong> in the patient’s brain. An ablative dose of radiation is thereby sent through the tumour in one treatment session while surrounding brain tissues are relatively spared. </p>\n<p>SRS has been used in the treatment of almost every intracranial lesion but is commonly used in the treatment of acoustic schwannoma, pituitary adenoma, meningioma, and trigeminal neuralgia but not glioblastoma. </p>\n<p>The image below is an illustration of the principle of gamma-knife therapy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0745","text":"Personal monitoring of rad​iation is done by:","raw_text":"Personal monitoring of radiation is done by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TLD ​badge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colli​mators","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Linear ac​celerator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gr​id","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. TLD badge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/218f24956fbb4fae98055edf74168223x240x73.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. TLD badge</strong></p><p>Personal monitoring of radiation is done by<strong> </strong>a<strong> TLD badge.</strong></p>\n<p>The TLD badge (thermoluminescent dosimeter) should be positioned according to local or national practices. In India, one TLD badge has to be worn at the <strong>chest level inside</strong> the lead <strong>apron</strong> if the apron is used. An additional <strong>wrist badge</strong> has to be worn for procedures requiring hands close to the primary beam. </p>\n<p>The thermoluminescent material used is <strong>calcium sulfate</strong> doped with <strong>dysprosium</strong> or <strong>lithium fluoride</strong>. Radiation exposure is monitored every <strong>three months</strong>. The maximum dose limit for occupational radiation workers is 30 mSv in a year, provided the cumulative dose in a block of 5 years does not exceed 100 mSv. </p>\n<p>The given image shows a TLD badge.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4623","text":"A patient with carcinoma e​ndometrium is treated wit​h pelvic external beam ir‍radiation. Which of the f​ollowing organs is likely‍ to be affected the most?‍","raw_text":"A patient with carcinoma endometrium is treated with pelvic external beam irradiation. Which of the following organs is likely to be affected the most?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ov​ary","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vag​ina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bla​dder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rec​tum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ovary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ovary</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>ovary</strong> is most likely to be affected as it is the <strong>most radiosensitive</strong> organ. </p>\n<p>Radiosensitivity of pelvic organs is as follows: <strong>ovary&gt;rectum&gt;bladder&gt;vagina. </strong>The radiation tolerance dose for the ovary is 2-3 Gy whereas the vagina is 90 Gy. Hence, among the given options, the <strong>vagina</strong> is the <strong>most radioresistant</strong> organ.</p>\n<p>The radiation dose necessary to induce ovarian failure is age-dependent. A single dose of 3-4 Gray can induce amenorrhea in almost all women &gt;40 years of age. In young women, oogenesis is much less sensitive to radiation as compared to the elderly. </p>\n<p>The radiosensitivity of various tissues is summed up in the table given below: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 292px; width: 488px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\"><strong>Most radiosensitive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\"><strong>Least radiosensitive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"><strong>Tissue</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Bone marrow, Gonads</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\">Nervous tissue, Muscle fibers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"><strong>Blood cells</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Lymphocytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\">Platelets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"><strong>Stages of cell cycle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">G2M interphase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\">S phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"><strong>Cell types</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Rapidly dividing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\">Quiescent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"><strong>Ocular structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Lens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\">Sclera</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.156px;\"><strong>Layer of retina</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Rods</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.844px;\">Ganglion cell layer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: The skin is most commonly affected due to radiation. Stratum basalis is the layer of the skin most affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"eALIyyNT","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"14_the_parathyroids","title":"14.The Parathyroids","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC3539","text":"While performing a subtota‌l thyroidectomy, accident‍al ligation of which arte​ry can potentially lead t​o hypoparathyroidism?","raw_text":"While performing a subtotal thyroidectomy, accidental ligation of which artery can potentially lead to hypoparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior th‌yroid artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior th‌yroid artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending ce‌rvical artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascending pharyngeal arter‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior thyroid artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6463da8cf984cbe89b73c1f2ae8a44ex1279x1337.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior thyroid artery</strong></p><p><strong>Truncal ligation</strong> of the <strong>inferior thyroid artery</strong> can potentially result in <strong>hypoparathyroidism</strong>. Both superior and inferior<strong> parathyroid glands</strong> receive their blood from the inferior thyroid artery which arises from the subclavian arteries.Hence during thyroidectomy, only capsular branches closer to the thyroid gland should be ligated.</p>\n<p>The <strong>inferior</strong> parathyroid glands may have a <strong>variable blood supply</strong>, from either the ascending branch of the inferior thyroid arteries or the thyroid ima artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3540","text":"Identify the correct pair ‌based on their embryologi‍cal development.","raw_text":"Identify the correct pair based on their embryological development.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thymus, Inferior parathyro‌id gland - Third pharynge‍al pouch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thymus, Superior parathyro‌id gland - Fourth pharyng‍eal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior and Inferior para‌thyroid gland - Third pha‍ryngeal arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thymus, Superior parathyro‌id gland - Fourth pharyng‍eal arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thymus, Inferior parathyroid gland - Third pharyngeal pouch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e723d93b507470ebeb71887613a5d94x1280x1407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thymus, Inferior parathyroid gland - Third pharyngeal pouch</strong></p><p>The <strong>thymus</strong> and <strong>inferior parathyroid glands</strong> originate from the <strong>third pharyngeal pouch</strong>. They migrate from the upper neck to the mediastinum during embryonic development. Inferior parathyroid glands have more variation in their anatomical position due to a long descent.</p>\n<p>The <strong>superior parathyroid glands</strong> originate from the <strong>fourth pharyngeal pouch</strong>. They are more constant in their position.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4712","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site of e‍ctopic parathyroid gland?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site of ectopic parathyroid gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Media‌stinal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intrath‌yroidal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intra‌thymic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paraeso‌phageal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paraesophageal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ee5f3098a3542bdb637c0d81234d267x1279x1485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paraesophageal</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> site of the <strong>ectopic parathyroid gland</strong> is <strong>paraesophageal</strong>.</p>\n<p>The common sites of ectopic parathyroid glands are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paraesophageal - most common</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mediastinal </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intrathymic </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intrathyroidal </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carotid sheath </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">High cervical or undescended </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ectopic parathyroid is the most common cause of <strong>persistent </strong>or<strong> recurrent hyperparathyroidism</strong>. In such cases, treatment involves <strong>surgical removal</strong> of the gland. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4718","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of hypercalcemia in h‍ospitalized patients?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of hypercalcemia in hospitalized patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypervita‌minosis D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Parathyro‌id adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parathyroid‌ hyperplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malignancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malignancy</strong></p><p><strong>Cancer</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> in <strong>hospitalized patients</strong>, whereas hyperparathyroidism is the most common cause of hypercalcemia detected incidentally in outpatients. The causes of hypercalcemia in cancer : </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cancers affecting <strong>bone </strong>like multiple myeloma, leukemia, bony metastasis - cause the bone to break down which <strong>releases excess calcium</strong> into the blood</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pseudohyperparathyroidism</strong> - tumours producing parathyroid hormone-related peptide <strong>(PTHrP)</strong> cause the bone to release calcium into the blood. The most common carcinoma associated is squamous cell carcinoma of lung.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cancers affecting <strong>kidneys</strong> - failure to remove excess calcium from the blood</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dehydration</strong> caused by nausea and vomiting - difficult for the kidneys to remove calcium from the blood properly</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Lack </strong>of<strong> physical activity</strong> - causes bone breakdown, releasing calcium into the blood</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4714","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of primary hyperparat‍hyroidism?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single ‌adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple‌ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyper‌plasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carc‌inoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single adenoma</strong></p><p>A<strong> single adenoma</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. They are most often located in the inferior parathyroid glands.</p>\n<p>Primary hyperparathyroidism is due to <strong>excess </strong>or<strong> non-suppressed PTH</strong> secretion from the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Single parathyroid adenoma </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multiple adenomas </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperplasia </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carcinoma </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sporadic</strong> primary hyperparathyroidism (<strong>HPT</strong>) is <strong>more common</strong> than familial (MEN syndrome/ familial HPT/ Familial HPT with jaw tumor syndrome). <strong>Women</strong> are affected more commonly than men. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3563","text":"Lab investigations in a pa‌tient with fatigue and co‍licky abdominal pains sho​w elevated PTH and calciu​m, and low serum phosphat​e. Which of the following​ ophthalmic findings is p‍athognomonic of this cond​ition?","raw_text":"Lab investigations in a patient with fatigue and colicky abdominal pains show elevated PTH and calcium, and low serum phosphate. Which of the following ophthalmic findings is pathognomonic of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kayser Flei‌scher rings","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Band ker‌atopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ocular c‌hrysiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hudson-St‌ahli line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Band keratopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4513eb0275e849ffbafce00501ec3d47x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Band keratopathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>.<strong> Band keratopathy</strong> is pathognomonic of this condition. It is due to the deposition of <strong>calcium phosphate</strong> crystals in the cornea. </p>\n<p>In patients with elevated PTH, calcium and phosphate levels are measured. <strong>Elevated calcium</strong> and decreased phosphate levels are suggestive of <strong>primary</strong> hyperparathyroidism. <strong>Normal calcium</strong> and elevated phosphate levels are suggestive of <strong>secondary</strong> hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows band keratopathy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Kayser Fleischer rings are brownish-yellow rings in the corneoscleral junction due to the deposition of <strong>copper</strong> in the cornea. </p>\n<p>Option C: Ocular chrysiasis is the deposition of gold in the cornea, after repeated parenteral administration of <strong>gold salts</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: Hudson Stahli line is a superficial brown line at the junction of the upper 2/3 and lower 1/3 of the cornea in an otherwise normal cornea. It is attributed to the deposition of <strong>iron</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3565","text":"During a clinical trial, i‌t was found that some ind‍ividuals who showed radio​active uptake on Sestamib​i scan, showed no other e​vidence of hyperparathyro​idism. They were instead ‍found to be suffering fro​m a Hurthle cell nodule o‌f the thyroid gland. Whic‌h of the following radioa‌ctive tracer tests could ​be used to rule out the f‌alse positives?","raw_text":"During a clinical trial, it was found that some individuals who showed radioactive uptake on Sestamibi scan, showed no other evidence of hyperparathyroidism. They were instead found to be suffering from a Hurthle cell nodule of the thyroid gland. Which of the following radioactive tracer tests could be used to rule out the false positives?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ga-68 D‌otatate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iodine-‌131 MIBG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc-99m Per‌technetate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tc-99m M‌edronate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tc-99m Pertechnetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b76161fc238044019b534813a8a69201x290x276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tc-99m Pertechnetate</strong></p><p>A thyroid-specific tracer like <strong>99Tc-Pertechnetate</strong> and subsequent subtraction images could be used to <strong>rule out</strong> the <strong>false positives</strong> caused by the solid thyroid nodules such as Hurthle cell nodule.</p>\n<p>The Sestamibi scanning is a nuclear-based imaging test used to<strong> localize the parathyroid glands</strong>. Sestamibi is a monovalent lipophilic cation that <strong>accumulates in mitochondria</strong>. Due to this, there is a difference in the rate at which it gets washed out depending upon the number of mitochondria in each tissue. <strong>Parathyroid adenomas</strong> often have a high concentration of <strong>oxyphilic cells</strong> with <strong>high mitochondrial content </strong>and they retain tracers and have a slower washout compared to the normal glands.</p>\n<p>In patients with nodular goiter or functional <strong>tumors of the thyroid gland</strong>, increased uptake of sestamibi by the thyroid gland makes parathyroid visualization difficult. There are two primary techniques to <strong>differentiate</strong> sestamibi uptake by abnormal parathyroid and thyroid tissue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dual-isotope subtraction imaging </strong>(with sestamibi and either iodine-123 or 99mTc pertechnetate)<strong> -  <sup>99m</sup>Tc pertechnetate </strong>is taken up only by the<strong> thyroid. </strong>The <sup>99m</sup>Tc pertechnetate scintigram is then digitally subtracted from the sestamibi scan to allow parathyroid localization.</li>\n<li><strong>Single-isotope dual-phase</strong> imaging (with sesta­mibi alone with early and delayed imaging)<strong> - </strong>since the rate of washout from the hyperfunctioning parathyroid gland is slower when compared to the thyroid gland.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the dual-isotope subtraction imaging.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6317","text":"A patient presents with po‌lyuria, polydipsia, letha‍rgy and abdominal pain. H​e had an X-ray taken a fe​w days ago, which is give​n below. Which of the fol​lowing investigations wou‍ld not be useful in arriv​ing at a diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with polyuria, polydipsia, lethargy and abdominal pain. He had an X-ray taken a few days ago, which is given below. Which of the following investigations would not be useful in arriving at a diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear ses‌tamibi scan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultrasonogr‌aphy of neck","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dual-energy radiographic a‌bsorptiometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angiography of renal arter‌ies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Angiography of renal arteries","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/871e7bbd04c04510b4729524de1f7c2e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a97f3a764664ddcab38ab9a33f0112ex672x526.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7204f4e10a44f878b1d4145e1e53a47x512x512.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Angiography of renal arteries</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the X-ray showing <strong>nephrocalcinosis</strong> is suggestive of <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. <strong>Angiography of renal arteries</strong> is not a useful investigation for hyperparathyroidism. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Nephrocalcinosis occurs in this condition due to hypercalcemia. It decreases renal concentrating ability, which may cause polyuria and polydipsia.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of hyperparathyroidism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bones:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pathological fractures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Subperiosteal bone resorption - 2nd and 3rd digits, most common in radial aspect</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Salt and pepper skull</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Osteitis fibrosa cystica or VonReckling Hausen’s disease of bone</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stones\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multiple and recurrent renal stones - most common</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nephrocalcinosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal groans - colicky due to pancreatitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Psychiatric overtones - raised PTH causing CNS depression and psychiatric symptoms</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Band keratopathy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Features of hypercalcemia - \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vomiting</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Confusion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ECG changes: increased PR interval, shortened QT interval</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image given below shows salt and pepper skull seen in hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> done in primary hyperparathyroidism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrasonography</strong> of the neck is a safe and widely used technique for the localization of abnormal parathyroid glands. <strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Nuclear medicine scanning with <strong>radiolabeled Sestamibi</strong> is also a widely used technique. Sestamibi accumulates in parathyroid adenomas. This radionuclide is concentrated in the thyroid and parathyroid tissue but usually washes out of normal thyroid tissue in under an hour. It persists in abnormal parathyroid tissue.  </li>\n<li><strong>Dual-energy radiographic absorptiometry</strong> is a useful tool to demonstrate skeletal involvement in primary hyperparathyroidism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a radiolabeled Sestamibi scan.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4725","text":"In a patient with asymptom‌atic primary hyperparathy‍roidism, when will you pe​rform parathyroidectomy?","raw_text":"In a patient with asymptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism, when will you perform parathyroidectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If bone mineral density of‌ &amp;gt; 2.5 SD above peak b‍one mass","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If age &amp;g‌t;50 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In the presence of hyperca‌lciuria but absence of ne‍phrolithiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"If creatinine clearance is‌ reduced by 30%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. If creatinine clearance is reduced by 30%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. If creatinine clearance is reduced by 30%</strong></p><p><strong>Reduction in creatinine clearance by 30% </strong>is an indication for parathyroidectomy in patients with asymptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4722","text":"A 55-year-old lady with a ‌history of multiple patho‍logical fractures and ren​al stones was diagnosed w​ith parathyroid hyperplas​ia involving all four gla​nds. Which of the followi‍ng treatment modalities w​ill you use to treat her?‌","raw_text":"A 55-year-old lady with a history of multiple pathological fractures and renal stones was diagnosed with parathyroid hyperplasia involving all four glands. Which of the following treatment modalities will you use to treat her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete removal of all fo‌ur glands alone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complete removal of three ‌glands with a partial res‍ection of the least affec​ted gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Partial resection of the a‌ffected glands with autot‍ransplantation of the lea​st affected gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brachytherapy with PTH sup‌plementation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complete removal of three glands with a partial resection of the least affected gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complete removal of three glands with a partial resection of the least affected gland</strong></p><p>Hyperplasia involving all four parathyroid glands is treated by <strong>complete removal of three glands</strong> with <strong>partial resection of the least affected gland (Subtotal parathyroidectomy)</strong>. Total cervical parathyroidectomy with heterotropic transplantation can also be done in this patient.</p>\n<p>Modes of <strong>treatment</strong> for parathyroid hyperplasia:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Subtotal parathyroidectomy -</strong> The <strong>affected glands </strong>are <strong>completely removed</strong> and the <strong>least affected gland </strong>is<strong> partially resected</strong>. The remnants left behind should ideally be 30 mg. A lower gland is preferred because its location reduces the risk of injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerve. </li>\n<li><strong>Total parathyroidectomy</strong> with heterotopic transplantation - <strong>All 4 </strong>parathyroid glands are <strong>removed</strong> and parathyroid <strong>tissue</strong> is <strong>immediately transplanted</strong> to the non-dominant forearm. Pockets are made in the belly of the <strong>brachioradialis</strong> muscle, and one to two pieces of parathyroid tissue measuring 1 mm each are placed into each pocket. A total of 12 to 14 pieces are transplanted.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of <strong>parathyroidectomy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Hypoparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Hungry bone syndrome</li>\n<li>Injury to recurrent laryngeal nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4183","text":"Autotransplantation was do‌ne after total parathyroi‍dectomy in a patient with​ hyperplasia of all 4 par​athyroid glands. Which of​ the following best descr​ibes this procedure?","raw_text":"Autotransplantation was done after total parathyroidectomy in a patient with hyperplasia of all 4 parathyroid glands. Which of the following best describes this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heter‌otopic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ortho‌topic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Auxi‌liary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isog‌raft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heterotopic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heterotopic</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 348\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Parathyroid is transplanted <strong>heterotopically </strong>i.e.<strong> </strong>organ is implanted in another anatomic location.</p>\n<p>In total parathyroidectomy, all 4 parathyroid glands are removed and parathyroid tissue is immediately transplanted to the brachioradialis muscle in the non-dominant forearm.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7507","text":"Parathyroid gland post sur‌gery is commonly transfer‍red to:","raw_text":"Parathyroid gland post surgery  is commonly transferred to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tri‌ceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bic‌eps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brachio‌radialis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brachioradialis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brachioradialis</strong></p><p>The <strong>parathyroid</strong> is commonly transferred to the <strong>brachioradialis</strong> muscle <strong>post-surgery</strong>. This will facilitate easy removal if there is disease recurrence after surgery.</p>\n<p>Parathyroidectomy is <strong>indicated</strong> in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Symptomatic</strong> <strong>primary</strong> hyperparathyroidism <strong>(HPT)</strong>:  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Asymptomatic</strong> primary HPT with:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">raise in serum calcium &gt;1mg/dl of the normal range</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">&gt;400 mg urinary calcium in 24-hour urine test</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">&lt; -2.5 Z-score of bone mineral density</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age &lt;50yr and failed medical treatment </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Severe cases</strong> of <strong>secondary</strong> HPT</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Parathyroidectomy is <strong>performed</strong> in neck hyperextension <strong>(semi-Fowler position)</strong>. The thyroid gland is mobilized, and the parathyroid gland with adenoma is identified and resected. The parathyroid tissue is <strong>transplanted</strong> to the <strong>non-dominant forearm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pockets are made in the <strong>belly of the brachioradialis muscle</strong>, and one to two pieces of parathyroid tissue measuring 1 mm each are placed into each pocket. A total of 12 to 14 pieces are transplanted. This is a type of <strong>heterotopic transplant.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Miami criteria</strong> (confirm the <strong>absence</strong> of another <strong>hyperactive parathyroid</strong>): A drop of parathyroid hormone intraoperatively, to <strong>more than half</strong> the maximum value at <strong>10 minutes </strong>postresection is considered an adequate resection in a parathyroid adenoma. However, if there is <strong>no drop</strong> in PTH level, then a <strong>multi-gland disease</strong> is suspected and the surgery is converted to a bilateral neck exploration.</p>\n<p>Complications of parathyroidectomy include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoparathyroidism (presenting as hypocalcemia)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hungry bone syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerve</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3577","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of num‍bness around the lips and​ a tingling sensation in ​her fingers. She underwen​t parathyroidectomy for h​yperparathyroidism one ye‍ar ago. An ECG was done a​nd is given below. What w‌ill be your immediate man‌agement?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents with complaints of numbness around the lips and a tingling sensation in her fingers. She underwent parathyroidectomy for hyperparathyroidism one year ago. An ECG was done and is given below. What will be your immediate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Teripa‌ratide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV Bispho‌sphonates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IV Calcium‌ gluconate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IV Calcium gluconate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc12d66e8e4a437e92e396317b308ba2.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d4c48e73535461ab15bea1f0755d4d3x600x486.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IV Calcium gluconate</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and ECG showing a <strong>prolonged QT interval</strong> (QT interval more than 0.43 seconds) is suggestive of <strong>hungry bone syndrome</strong> i.e. <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> post parathyroidectomy. Acute symptomatic presentation of hypocalcemia is treated with <strong>IV calcium gluconate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms and signs of hypocalcemia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mild circumoral or digital numbness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Paresthesia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laryngeal spasms </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cardiac arrhythmias </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Chvostek sign</strong>: This is elicited by gently tapping over the <strong>facial nerve</strong> in front of the<strong> tragus</strong> of the ear. There is hyperexcitability of the facial muscles, manifesting as a contraction of the muscles.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Trousseau's sign</strong>: This is elicited by making the forearm ischemic by applying a sphygmomanometer cuff to the arm and keeping the pressure <strong>above</strong> systolic blood pressure (<strong>SBP</strong>) for <strong>5 minutes</strong>. The hand goes into a painful carpopedal spasm and assumes the accoucheur’s hand position. (shown in the image below)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Acute symptomatic hypocalcemia is an emergency and must be treated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>10 ml</strong> of <strong>10% calcium gluconate</strong> is given slowly through <strong>intravenous</strong> route</li>\n<li>Calcium and vitamin D replacement is given orally</li>\n<li>Magnesium supplementation may also be required due to the synergistic actions of calcium and magnesium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The continuous need for calcium or vitamin D, 1-year postoperatively is called <strong>permanent hypoparathyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4723","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about se‍condary hyperparathyroidi​sm?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about secondary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lithium intake may cause s‌econdary hyperparathyroid‍ism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calciphylaxis is seen in e‌arly stages","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple gland affliction ‌is common","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transcervical thymectomy i‌s indicated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calciphylaxis is seen in early stages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f917645c8624c09b6f2543ad514d571x1280x805.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calciphylaxis is seen in early stages</strong></p><p><strong>Calciphylaxis</strong> (calcific uremic arteriolopathy) is a disseminated calcification seen <strong>late</strong> in the course of <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p>In calciphylaxis, the underlying calcification in the vascular and arteriolar walls → ischemic necrosis → ulceration and gangrene → sepsis → death.</p>\n<p>It presents as an <strong>expanding cutaneous lesion</strong> which is predominantly seen in the extremities and lower abdomen. Prognosis is poor and an <strong>urgent parathyroidectomy</strong> is shown to increase median survival.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Causes of secondary hyperparathyroidism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic renal failure (<strong>most common</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Vitamin D3 deficiency</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">GI malabsorption</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lithium intake</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To minimize the risk of missing parathyroid glands, <strong>transcervical</strong> <strong>thymectomy</strong> should be considered as a routine part of total parathyroidectomy with autotransplantation and subtotal parathyroidectomy. In addition, careful cervical exploration for secondary hyperparathyroidism should be carried out.</p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>calciphylaxis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3736","text":"A 45-year-old patient with‌ chronic renal failure pr‍esents to the OPD with co​mplaints of recent onset ​pain in her feet. Blood i​nvestigation showed eleva​ted PTH levels and normal‍ serum calcium values. Al​l of the following mechan‌isms contribute to elevat‌ed PTH in this patient ex‌cept _____","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient with chronic renal failure presents to the OPD with complaints of recent onset pain in her feet. Blood investigation showed elevated PTH levels and normal serum calcium values. All of the following mechanisms contribute to elevated PTH in this patient except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulation of parathyroid‌ cells by hyperphosphatem‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased secretion of FGF‌-23 by osteocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduced expression of Vita‌min D receptors and CaSR ‍(calcium-sensing receptor​) in bones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interference with parathyr‌oid kinase C signal trans‍duction pathway and Wnt p​athway","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interference with parathyroid kinase C signal transduction pathway and Wnt pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interference with parathyroid kinase C signal transduction pathway and Wnt pathway</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario described here showing <strong>elevated PTH</strong> and <strong>normal calcium </strong>levels is that of <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong> due to renal failure. <strong>Interference</strong> with <strong>parathyroid kinase C</strong> signal transduction pathway and <strong>Wnt pathway</strong> is the proposed mechanism of secondary hyperparathyroidism in the setting of <strong>lithium consumption</strong> and not renal hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>Causes for increased PTH in secondary hyperparathyroidism due to renal failure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abnormal renal tubular reabsorption → <strong>hyperphosphatemia</strong> → stimulation of parathyroid cells → elevated PTH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased <strong>FGF-23</strong> secretion from osteocytes →  decreased 1,25 dihydroxyvitamin D → decreased intestinal absorption of calcium → elevated PTH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Normally, ECF calcium controls PTH secretion by interacting with CaSR. Stimulation of CaSR by high calcium suppresses PTH secretion. In chronic renal disease, there is <strong>reduced expression of Vitamin D and CaSR</strong> by bones → increased skeletal resistance to PTH → elevated PTH.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2054","text":"A 32-year-old female patie‌nt is undergoing focussed‍ parathyroidectomy for pa​rathyroid adenoma. Accord​ing to the Miami criteria​, for the resection to be​ considered adequate, the‍ intraoperative drop in p​arathyroid hormone should‌ be ______.","raw_text":"A 32-year-old female patient is undergoing focussed parathyroidectomy for parathyroid adenoma. According to the Miami criteria, for the resection to be considered adequate, the intraoperative drop in parathyroid hormone should be ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More than one third the ma‌ximum preoperative value ‍in 10min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More than one third the ma‌ximum preoperative value ‍in half an 30 min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More than half the maximum‌ preoperative value in 10‍min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"More than half the maximum‌ preoperative value in 30‍ min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. More than half the maximum preoperative value in 10min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More than half the maximum preoperative value in 10min</strong></p><p>Drop of parathyroid hormone to <strong>more than half the maximum</strong> value at <strong>10 min</strong> postresection is considered an adequate resection in a parathyroid adenoma by the <strong>Miami criteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most of the patients with primary hyperparathyroidism have <strong>single</strong> <strong>adenoma</strong>, and therefore undergo a <strong>focused</strong> or <strong>minimally invasive parathyroidectomy,</strong> where the localized abnormal parathyroid gland is removed. Once the gland is removed, the surgeon can ensure that there is no other hyperactive gland remaining by performing an intraoperative PTH assay for Miami criteria.</p>\n<p>It is based on the principle that the <strong>half-life</strong> of the PTH is<strong> 3-5 minutes</strong>. In a single-gland disease, once the gland is removed the PTH levels should drop to &gt;<strong>50% of the preoperative PTH level within 10 minutes</strong>. However, if there is no drop in PTH level, then a multi-gland disease is suspected and the surgery is converted to a <strong>bilateral neck exploration.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9678","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with complaints of fa‍tigue, vomiting, abdomina​l cramps and bone pain. S​he had undergone total pa​rathyroidectomy and heter​otopic autotransplantatio‍n for the same complaints​ 8 months ago. Sestamibi ‌scan was done and found t‌o be normal. What is the ‌next step in management?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with complaints of fatigue, vomiting, abdominal cramps and bone pain. She had undergone total parathyroidectomy and heterotopic autotransplantation for the same complaints 8 months ago. Sestamibi scan was done and found to be normal. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform Ca‌sanova test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Post her for bilateral nec‌k exploration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post her for‌ radiotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Label her as persistent hy‌perparathyroidism and sta‍rt her on medical managem​ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perform Casanova test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79b71b8113c84ba6a42bf8da9c6b5a71x1280x2336.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perform Casanova test</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>recurrent hyperparathyroidism</strong> which could be due to recurrence in the <strong>neck</strong> or <strong>graft</strong> (graft hyperplasia). The next step would be to perform <strong>Casnova test</strong> to differentiate between them, as Sestamibi scan has ruled out ectopic parathyroid.</p>\n<p>Casanova test involves <strong>selective venous sampling</strong> for<strong> PTH levels</strong> from neck veins and brachial veins. If PTH from the <strong>brachial</strong> venous sample is <strong>&gt;20 times</strong> that of <strong>neck</strong> vein sample, <strong>graft hyperplasia</strong> is confirmed.</p>\n<p><strong>Persistent</strong> parathyroidism is defined as hypercalcemia that fails to resolve after parathyroidectomy i.e. <strong>within 6 weeks</strong> of surgery. <strong>Recurrent</strong> parathyroidism is hyperparathyroidism that occurs after an intervening period of at least <strong>6 months</strong> of biochemically documented <strong>normocalcemia</strong>. Causes of persistent/recurrent hyperparathyroidism include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ectopic parathyroid</li>\n<li>Unrecognized hyperplasia - graft site hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Missed adenoma in normal position</li>\n<li>Incomplete resection</li>\n<li>Familial hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>MEN-1 syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below summarises the management protocol for a case of persistent/recurrent hyperparathyroidism.<br/><br/>*FHH stands for Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4264","text":"All of the following are i‌ndications for surgical i‍ntervention of tertiary h​yperparathyroidism except​ ____","raw_text":"All of the following are indications for surgical intervention of tertiary hyperparathyroidism except ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduced ser‌um phosphate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nephroca‌lcinosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular hyperplasia of the‌ parathyroid gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impaired gr‌aft function","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reduced serum phosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduced serum phosphate</strong></p><p>Reduced serum phosphate is not an indication for surgical intervention in tertiary hyperparathyroidism. </p>\n<p><strong>Renal transplantation</strong> in patients with chronic kidney diseases may be associated with <strong>autonomous</strong> <strong>functioning</strong> of the <strong>parathyroid</strong> gland. This is called <strong>tertiary</strong> hyperparathyroidism where there is <strong>hypercalcemic</strong> hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>The <strong>indications</strong> for<strong> surgical intervention</strong> in tertiary hyperparathyroidism are as follows :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subacute severe hypercalcaemia (&gt;3 mmol/L)</li>\n<li>Impaired graft function</li>\n<li>Nodular hyperplasia of the parathyroid gland(s)</li>\n<li>Progressive symptoms for &gt;2 years following transplantation\n<ul>\n<li>Worsening bone disease - pain, fractures, bone loss</li>\n<li>Renal stones/nephrocalcinosis</li>\n<li>Soft tissue or vascular calcifications</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4388","text":"A 75-year-old lady with ty‌pe 2 diabetes mellitus, h‍ypertension, and end-stag​e renal disease on hemodi​alysis presents with hype​rcalcemia, hyperphosphate​mia, and elevated PTH. Wh‍at is the treatment of ch​oice in this condition?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old lady with type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis presents with hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH. What is the treatment of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total parathyroidectomy wi‌thout autotransplantation‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subtotal parathyroidectomy‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Minimally invasive focusse‌d parathyroidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medical management with ci‌nacalcet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subtotal parathyroidectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subtotal parathyroidectomy</strong></p><p>The patient’s <strong>long-standing renal failure</strong> and <strong>elevated calcium</strong>, <strong>phosphate</strong>, and <strong>PTH</strong> are suggestive of <strong>tertiary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. This form of hyperparathyroidism can be <strong>life-threatening</strong>, and <strong>all patients</strong> with it should be referred for <strong>surgical intervention</strong>. </p>\n<p>The surgical <strong>procedure of choice</strong> for tertiary hyperparathyroidism is <strong>subtotal parathyroidectomy</strong>. In subtotal parathyroidectomy, the <strong>affected glands are completely removed</strong> and the <strong>least affected gland is partially resected</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Option A: Total parathyroidectomy <strong>without an autograft</strong> is <strong>not a treatment option</strong> due to the postoperative and persistent <strong>difficulties</strong> in managing the associated <strong>hypocalcemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Minimally invasive approaches commonly refer to the removal of a <strong>localized abnormal parathyroid gland</strong> through an incision less than 3cm in length. It is based on the principle that over 80% of individuals with primary hyperparathyroidism have a<strong> single adenoma</strong>. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>multi-glandular disease</strong>, <strong>lithium-induced hyperparathyroidism,</strong> and hyperparathyroidism associated with <strong>familial syndromes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cinacalcet</strong> is a calcimimetic <strong>used in</strong> the treatment of <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. The use of calcimimetics in <strong>tertiary hyperparathyroidism</strong> remains controversial and has <strong>not been approved</strong> for this indication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4379","text":"A 41-year-old man with a h‌istory of treatment for H‍odgkin's lymphoma in chil​dhood presents with sever​e abdominal cramps and ri​ght flank pain. He has a ​firm 2x2cm mass in the fr‍ont of his neck. He has a​ Calcium of 15.2 mg/dl, n‌ormal TSH, markedly eleva‌ted PTH, and PGP9.5+ on i‌mmunohistochemistry. What​ is the leading cause of ‌mortality in his conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old man with a history of treatment for Hodgkin's lymphoma in childhood presents with severe abdominal cramps and right flank pain. He has a firm 2x2cm mass in the front of his neck. He has a Calcium of 15.2 mg/dl, normal TSH, markedly elevated PTH, and PGP9.5+ on immunohistochemistry. What is the leading cause of mortality in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metas‌tasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiovascu‌lar disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheal c‌ompression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p>The patient presents with symptoms suggestive of <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. With <strong>early-onset, neck mass</strong>, and positive IHC for <strong>PGP9.5+</strong>, it is concerning for <strong>parathyroid cancer</strong>. <strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> is the <strong>leading cause</strong> of morbidity and <strong>mortality</strong> in patients with parathyroid cancer. </p>\n<p>Parathyroid cancer is a <strong>rare</strong> malignancy and makes up 1% of cases of primary hyperparathyroidism. History of <strong>prior neck radiation</strong> is considered to be a<strong> risk factor.</strong> Patients usually present a decade earlier than primary hyperparathyroidism (5th - 6th decade), with an equal preponderance in males and females. Patients may present with a neck mass and severely elevated calcium and PTH levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is focused on <strong>controlling hypercalcemia</strong> and <strong>surgery</strong> is the mainstay.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Cardiovascular disease</strong> is a major contributing cause of <strong>mortality</strong> in patients with <strong>hyperthyroidism </strong>and<strong> chronic kidney disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4729","text":"In patients with parathyro‌id carcinoma, which of th‍e following treatment mod​alities would you not con​sider?","raw_text":"In patients with parathyroid carcinoma, which of the following treatment modalities would you not consider?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radiatio‌n therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azidoth‌ymidine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"En-bloc excision of the tu‌mor and ipsilateral thyro‍id lobe with MRND","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etelca‌lcetide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Etelcalcetide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Etelcalcetide</strong></p><p>Etelcalcetide has no role in the treatment of parathyroid carcinoma. It is ineffective.</p>\n<p><strong>Etelcalcetide</strong> is a calcium-sensing receptor agonist used to treat <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong> in adult patients with <strong>chronic kidney disease </strong>undergoing dialysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>modalities for<strong> parathyroid carcinoma:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgical - Bilateral neck exploration + En-bloc excision of the tumor and ipsilateral thyroid lobe with/without MRND (Modified radical neck dissection) in presence of lymph node metastasis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pharmacological:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cinacalcet</strong> hydrochloride, a calcimimetic, can reduce PTH levels by directly binding to the CaSR (calcium-sensing receptor) cells on the parathyroid gland; used to <strong>control hypercalcemia</strong> in refractory parathyroid carcinoma. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Azidothymidine</strong> (telomerase inhibitor)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Octreotide</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anti-parathyroid hormone immunotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiation therapy - for unresectable disease or for palliation of bone metastases</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for parathyroid carcinoma incude:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radiation exposure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperparathyroidism jaw bone syndrome </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HPRT gene or parafibromin inactivation</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4383","text":"A 34-year-old man presents‌ with severe hypercalcemi‍a. His father had similar​ complaints and was found​ to have hyperparathyroid​-jaw tumour syndrome. Whi​ch of the following is pa‍thognomonic of his condit​ion?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man presents with severe hypercalcemia. His father had similar complaints and was found to have hyperparathyroid-jaw tumour syndrome. Which of the following is pathognomonic of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic mandi‌bular lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ossifying j‌aw fibromas","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteitis fib‌rosa cystica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibular‌ granulomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ossifying jaw fibromas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ossifying jaw fibromas</strong></p><p><strong>Ossifying jaw fibromas</strong> are a <strong>pathognomonic</strong> feature of <strong>hyperparathyroid-jaw tumour syndrome</strong>. It arises due to inactivating mutations in the HRPT2 gene on chromosome 1 q21-32, encoding <strong>parafibromin.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a rare cause of primary hyperparathyroidism with an <strong>early onset (32 years)</strong> which presents with <strong>severe hypercalcemia</strong>. It is associated with an <strong>increased risk</strong> of <strong>parathyroid carcinoma</strong> and other conditions like polycystic kidney disease.<strong> Surgical intervention</strong> may be considered to remove all enlarged glands.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteitis fibrosa cystica</strong> is a feature of <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong> but is not considered pathognomonic of any condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ofxNtkZT","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"15_inhaled_anaesthetics___fluorinated_agents__inert_agents_and_therapeutic_gases","title":"15.Inhaled Anaesthetics   Fluorinated Agents, Inert Agents And Therapeutic Gases","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA5866","text":"Which of the following inh​aled anesthetics is the i​nduction agent of choice ‍in a patient planned for ‌surgery under GA?","raw_text":"Which of the following inhaled anesthetics is the induction agent of choice in a patient planned for surgery under GA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Sevoflurane</strong> is the<strong> induction agent</strong> <strong>of choice</strong> among<strong> inhalational anesthetics</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>non-pungent</strong> and has <strong>low blood-gas solubility </strong>(0.65), allowing for smooth induction of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Option A: Halothane has high blood gas solubility, leading to a slower induction of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Option C: Desflurane and isoflurane are airway irritants, of which <strong>desflurane</strong> is the <strong>most pungent</strong> inhalational agent. Hence, they are not used for the induction of anesthesia as they can precipitate <strong>laryngospasm</strong>. These agents are used only during the <strong>maintenance</strong> of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Option D: N<sub>2</sub>O has a MAC value of 104%, which makes it the<strong> least potent</strong> inhaled anesthetic. It is always used in combination with more potent volatile agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5867","text":"A 7-year-old boy is posted​ for laparoscopic appende​ctomy. Which of the follo‍wing inhalational agents ‌is preferred for inductio​n?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy is posted for laparoscopic appendectomy. Which of the following inhalational agents is preferred for induction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Sevoflurane</strong> is preferred for induction as it is the <strong>agent of choice</strong> in <strong>pediatric anesthesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Although both halothane and sevoflurane provide a smooth induction, sevoflurane appears to have a greater therapeutic index than halothane and hence is the preferred agent for inhaled induction in pediatric anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Isoflurane and desflurane are airway irritants. Hence they are not used for induction of anesthesia as they can precipitate laryngospasm. These agents are used only during the maintenance of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5931","text":"Which inhaled anesthetic h​as the maximum global war​ming potential?","raw_text":"Which inhaled anesthetic has the maximum global warming potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Desflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Desflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Desflurane</strong> has the maximum global warming potential (GWP) among inhaled anesthetics.</p>\n<p>Global warming potential of Anesthetic Gases: Nitrous oxide &lt; Isoflurane &lt; Sevoflurane &lt; <strong>Desflurane (m</strong><strong>aximum)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Xenon</strong> produces <strong>no greenhouse effect</strong> or ozone depletion as it is entirely unreactive in the biosphere.</p>\n<p>Although the GWP factor of desflurane is much higher than that of<strong> nitrous oxide</strong>, the latter contributes the <strong>most </strong>to increased climate impact (99.97%) as the consumption volumes of N<sub>2</sub>O far exceed those of other anesthetic gases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5869","text":"A 39-year-old woman is pos​ted for resection of an i​ntracranial tumor. Which ‍inhalational anesthetic i‌s most suitable in this c​ase?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old woman is posted for resection of an intracranial tumor. Which inhalational anesthetic is most suitable in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p>The inhalational agent of choice for neurosurgery in patients with<strong> elevated intracranial pressure</strong> (intracranial tumor) is<strong> sevoflurane</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sevoflurane best <strong>preserves</strong> the <strong>autoregulation</strong> of <strong>cerebral blood flow</strong> and produces <strong>limited vasodilatation.</strong> Hence, it is the preferred volatile agent in patients with elevated ICP.</p>\n<p>The order of vasodilating potency is approximately : <strong>halothane</strong> &gt; enflurane &gt; desflurane &gt; isoflurane &gt; <strong>sevoflurane.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5912","text":"Which anesthetic agent is ​to be avoided in patients​ diagnosed with epilepsy?‍","raw_text":"Which anesthetic agent is to be avoided in patients diagnosed with epilepsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enfl​urane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Enflurane</strong> is <strong>contraindicated </strong>in patients with a known history of <strong>seizures</strong>, <strong>cerebrovascular disease,</strong> and <strong>head injuries.</strong></p>\n<p>Enflurane is the <strong>most epileptogenic</strong> inhalational anesthetic. It increases seizure activity, especially in the presence of cerebral injury and hypocapnia, and results in increasing the cerebral metabolism manifold. </p>\n<p>Besides, enflurane <strong>increases cerebrospinal fluid secretion</strong> and <strong>decreases</strong> its <strong>absorption</strong> which is deleterious in those with poor intracranial compliance.</p>\n<p>Seizures have also been associated with the use of sevoflurane in high concentrations, especially in the pediatric population.</p>\n<p>Epileptogenic potential: enflurane &gt;&gt;&gt; sevoflurane</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5927","text":"An elderly male patient is​ scheduled to undergo an ​elective procedure. What ‍is the choice of inhalati‌onal anesthetic you will ​consider for him?","raw_text":"An elderly male patient is scheduled to undergo an elective procedure. What is the choice of inhalational anesthetic you will consider for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Desflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Desflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Desflurane</strong> is the inhalational agent of choice in the <strong>elderly</strong> as it is <strong>rapidly eliminated</strong> and undergoes<strong> minimal metabolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>The amount of <strong>fluoride ion </strong>produced is only about 5 µm which is much lower than the other fluorinated agents and hence safe in elderly as they may have a compromised renal function.</p>\n<p>Desflurane is also the inhalational <strong>agent of choice</strong> in <strong>renal disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5878","text":"Which of the following is ​the inhalational agent of​ choice for general anest‍hesia in asthmatic patien‌ts?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the inhalational agent of choice for general anesthesia in asthmatic patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p>Sevoflurane is the <strong>inhalational</strong> <strong>agent of choice</strong> for <strong>asthmatics</strong> because of its <strong>bronchodilatory</strong> <strong>effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although halothane produces greater bronchodilation, sevoflurane appears to have a <strong>greater therapeutic index.</strong> Halothane sensitizes the heart to adrenaline and causes cardiovascular depression, bradycardia, arrhythmias, and respiratory depression which are less likely with sevoflurane.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5883","text":"A middle-aged man with liv​er cirrhosis is posted fo​r surgery. Which would be‍ the inhalational anesthe‌tic agent of choice?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with liver cirrhosis is posted for surgery. Which would be the inhalational anesthetic agent of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Sevoflurane</strong> is the inhalational anesthetic <strong>agent</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>choice</strong> in <strong>liver cirrhosis.</strong></p>\n<p>A cirrhotic liver has a compromised portal venous blood flow and hence it is essential to preserve the hepatic arterial blood flow.</p>\n<p>Sevoflurane decreases portal vein blood flow but<strong> increases</strong> hepatic artery blood flow. Thus the total hepatic blood flow and oxygen delivery are better maintained.</p>\n<p>An alternative is isoflurane which may reduce hepatic arterial blood flow but maintains hepatic oxygen supply.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5914","text":"A 7-year-old kid recoverin​g from anesthesia started​ developing seizures. Whi‍ch of the following inhal‌ational agents would have​ been used?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old kid recovering from anesthesia started developing seizures. Which of the following inhalational agents would have been used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Seizures</strong> have been associated with the use of <strong>sevoflurane</strong> in <strong>high concentrations,</strong> especially in the <strong>pediatric</strong> population.</p>\n<p>Emergence from sevoflurane is associated with tonic-clonic movements suggestive of the seizure. Hence sevoflurane must be used cautiously in patients with a known history of seizure.</p>\n<p>Though isoflurane can cause myoclonus and EEG spiking, frank seizure activity is not seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: Enflurane</strong> has the <strong>most epileptogenic</strong> potential among the inhaled anesthetics.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5921","text":"A first-year anaesthesia r​esident was managing a pa​tient undergoing coronary‍ artery bypass grafting (‌CABG). They noticed that ​the coronary perfusion pr‍essure was not adequate. ‌During the procedure, the​ patient exhibited signs ‍of myocardial ischemia, i‌ncluding hypotension, ST-​segment changes, and decr‍eased cardiac output. The​ resident was evaluating ‌potential causes and cons​idering the role of anaes‌thetic agents. Which of t​he following anaesthetic ‍agents is most likely to ​exacerbate these symptoms​?","raw_text":"A first-year anaesthesia resident was managing a patient undergoing coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG). They noticed that the coronary perfusion pressure was not adequate. During the procedure, the patient exhibited signs of myocardial ischemia, including hypotension, ST-segment changes, and decreased cardiac output. The resident was evaluating potential causes and considering the role of anaesthetic agents. Which of the following anaesthetic agents is most likely to exacerbate these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoflurane</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>myocardial ischemia</strong>, <strong>hypotension</strong>, ST-segment changes, and <strong>decreased cardiac output</strong> point towards a diagnosis of <strong>coronary steal syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Isoflurane</strong> has been associated with myocardial ischemic symptoms, particularly in scenarios <strong>where</strong> <strong>coronary perfusion pressure</strong> is <strong>compromised</strong>.</p>\n<p>It dilates normal coronary arteries, which can redirect blood flow away from areas supplied by stenotic or diseased vessels, worsening ischemia in those regions. This redistribution of blood flow may contribute to hypotension, ST-segment changes, and reduced cardiac output. However, these effects are typically seen when hypotension occurs and can be mitigated by maintaining adequate coronary perfusion pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5929","text":"A patient with impaired re​nal function was administ​ered desflurane for gener‍al anesthesia. Which of t‌he following statements i​s true regarding this dru‍g?","raw_text":"A patient with impaired renal function was administered desflurane for general anesthesia. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Commonly used for inductio​n in children","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid induction and recove​ry","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Environmentally safe anest​hetic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Less potent than nitrous o​xide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rapid induction and recovery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rapid induction and recovery</strong></p><p>Desflurane has <strong>rapid induction </strong>and<strong> recovery </strong>from anesthesia due to its <strong>low blood gas solubility</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Desflurane is not used for induction as it is very <strong>pungent</strong> and may result in cough, <strong>laryngospasm</strong>, and bronchospasm.</p>\n<p>Option C: Desflurane is not environmentally safe as it has the <strong>maximum global warming</strong> <strong>potential</strong> among inhalational anesthetics. However, <strong>N<sub>2</sub>O</strong> contributes the <strong>most</strong> to increased climate impact (99.97%) as the consumption volumes of N<sub>2</sub>O far exceed those of other anesthetic gases.</p>\n<p>Option D: Desflurane is 17 times more potent than nitrous oxide.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5935","text":"An intern notices the foll​owing canister attached t​o the anesthesia circuit.‍ Which gas produces carbo‌n monoxide when passed th​rough it?","raw_text":"An intern notices the following canister attached to the anesthesia circuit. Which gas produces carbon monoxide when passed through it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Desflurane","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2700327ec94645ed9293b36cdc90484d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Desflurane</strong></p><p>The canister contains desiccated <strong>soda lime.</strong> <strong>Desflurane</strong> undergoes <strong>degradation</strong> to produce <strong>carbon monoxide,</strong> in the presence of strong bases in dry <strong>CO2 absorbents</strong> (like desiccated soda lime).</p>\n<p><strong>CO</strong> production is predominantly seen with <strong>absorbents </strong>that contain<strong> sodium </strong>or<strong> potassium hydroxide.</strong> It is seen less with barium hydroxide. The propensity for CO production correlates with the <strong>anesthetic concentration</strong> in the breathing circuit (desflurane &gt; enflurane &gt; isoflurane). </p>\n<p><strong>Amsorb</strong> is a CO2 absorbent that is more inert than soda-lime and baralyme and so associated with lesser degradation of anesthetics and so <strong>less carbon monoxide</strong> production.</p>\n<p>Sevoflurane, methoxyflurane, and halothane also undergo degradation in the presence of strong bases, but they do not produce CO.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5937","text":"Which of the following inh​alational agents produces​ a nephrotoxic substance ‍called compound A?","raw_text":"Which of the following inhalational agents produces a nephrotoxic substance called compound A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methoxy​flurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p><strong>Sevoflurane</strong> reacts with strong bases like sodium hydroxide and potassium hydroxide present in <strong>soda-lime</strong> and <strong>baralyme</strong> leading to the formation of a <strong>haloalkene</strong> (fluoromethyl-2,2-difluoro-1-[trifluoromethyl] vinyl ether) known as <strong>compound A.</strong></p>\n<p>Compound A has the potential to cause<strong> proximal tubular necrosis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors </strong>for increased compound A production:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged duration of anesthesia</li>\n<li>High concentration of sevoflurane</li>\n<li>Low flow techniques</li>\n<li>Dry baralyme</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A fresh gas flow of<strong> at least 2L/ min</strong> can prevent the generation of compound A</p>\n<p><strong>Amsorb</strong> is a CO2 absorbent that is more inert than soda-lime and baralyme and so associated with lesser degradation of sevoflurane and <strong>less compound A</strong> production.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5918","text":"Which of the following is ​an incorrect statement re​garding xenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding xenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases airflow resistan​ce and work of breathing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safe in card​iac patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Terat​ogenic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Does not trigger malignant​ hyperthermia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Teratogenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Teratogenic</strong></p><p>Xenon is <strong>n</strong><strong>on-teratogenic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:  Xenon is its <strong>high density</strong> (5.9 g/L) gas, (much higher than oxygen and nitrous oxide) which leads to <strong>increased flow resistance </strong>and<strong> work of breathing</strong>. Thus it is unsuitable for patients with compromised respiratory function.</p>\n<p>Option B:  It has minimal cardiovascular effects. It produces minimal cardiovascular depression and is not arrhythmogenic, hence<strong> safe in cardiac patients</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Does <strong>not</strong> trigger malignant hyperthermia</p>\n<table style=\"height: 117px;\" width=\"1055\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Advantages of xenon</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disadvantages of xenon </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px;\">Inert, Non-explosive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px;\">High cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px;\">Safe in cardiac disease </td>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px;\">Low potency </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px;\">Has analgesic activity- reduces intraoperative opioid requirements </td>\n<td style=\"width: 519.5px;\">High-density gas- higher flow resistance, poor choice for patients with respiratory compromise </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5932","text":"Which inhalational anesthe​tic has a negligible gree​nhouse effect?","raw_text":"Which inhalational anesthetic has a negligible greenhouse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Xe​non","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Xenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Xenon</strong></p><p><strong>Xenon</strong> produces <strong>no greenhouse effect</strong> or <strong>ozone depletion</strong> as it is inert in the biosphere.</p>\n<p>All the other inhaled anesthetics are greenhouse gases. </p>\n<p>Option A: The global warming potential of nitrous oxide is about 300 times greater than carbon dioxide.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Desflurane</strong> has the <strong>maximum global warming</strong> potential among inhaled anesthetics. Isoflurane, sevoflurane, and desflurane undergo minimal metabolism in the body and are predominantly <strong>eliminated on expiration</strong>. If these are let out into the atmosphere without scavenging, they contribute significantly to the greenhouse effect and global warming.</p>\n<p>Option D: Halothane has the potential to destroy stratospheric ozone due to its bromine-bearing molecules.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5798","text":"A middle-aged man presente​d to the ER with stridor ​due to partial upper airw‍ay obstruction. You are a‌sked to administer heliox​ for symptomatic relief. ‍What is the composition o‌f this breathing mixture?​","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented to the ER with stridor due to partial upper airway obstruction. You are asked to administer heliox for symptomatic relief. What is the composition of this breathing mixture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helium 30% a​nd oxygen 70%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Helium 70% a​nd oxygen 30%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Helium 50% a​nd oxygen 50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Helium 40% a​nd oxygen 60%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Helium 70% and oxygen 30%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Helium 70% and oxygen 30%</strong></p><p><strong>Heliox</strong> is a mixture of <strong>70% helium</strong> and<strong> 30% oxygen</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is supplied in E-size cylinders and delivered through a <strong>non-rebreathing face mask</strong> starting at a <strong>flow rate</strong> of <strong>10L/min</strong>. </p>\n<p>Heliox is used in cases of partial airway obstruction as in stridor to relieve the increased work of breathing. The flow of heliox through obstruction when compared to oxygen is higher due to its density being 40% of pure oxygen. Thus it provides <strong>symptomatic relief</strong> from <strong>acute distress</strong> before definite treatment can be instituted.</p>\n<p>The use of heliox in relieving lower airway obstruction as in acute asthma or COPD is unsatisfactory.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0459","text":"Which of the following is ​an incorrect statement re​garding oxygen as a thera‍peutic agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding oxygen as a therapeutic agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperbaric oxygen increase​s the availability of dis​solved O2 in blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primarily us​ed in hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhalation of higher oxyge​n concentration increases​ the partial pressure of ‍nitrogen inside the body","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can be administered at pre​ssures greater than atmos​pheric oxygen pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhalation of higher oxygen concentration increases the partial pressure of nitrogen inside the body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhalation of higher oxygen concentration increases the partial pressure of nitrogen inside the body</strong></p><p>Inhalation of <strong>higher oxygen concentrations</strong> can<strong> reduce </strong>the partial <strong>pressure</strong> of <strong>nitrogen</strong> inside the body.  </p>\n<p>Reduction in total body partial pressure of nitrogen provides a substantial gradient for the removal of nitrogen from gas spaces. This is beneficial in conditions such as bowel distension from obstruction of ileus, intravascular air embolism, or pneumothorax. </p>\n<p>Oxygen is administered at greater than atmospheric pressure in hyperbaric chambers (<strong>hyperbaric</strong> <strong>oxygen</strong>).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0460","text":"During a coronary artery b​ypass graft surgery, the ​surgeon uses a therapeuti‍c gas to flood the surgic‌al field. Which of the fo​llowing would he use?","raw_text":"During a coronary artery bypass graft surgery, the surgeon uses a therapeutic gas to flood the surgical field. Which of the following would he use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxy​gen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitric​ oxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon ​dioxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carbon dioxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carbon dioxide</strong></p><p><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong> (CO2) is commonly used to flood the <strong>surgical field</strong> during <strong>cardiac surgery.</strong> </p>\n<p>A common cause of <strong>myocardial ischemia</strong> during open-heart surgery is <strong>air embolism</strong> to the coronary arteries.</p>\n<p>Carbon dioxide because of its higher density, <strong>displaces</strong> the <strong>air</strong> surrounding the open heart so that any <strong>gas bubbles trapped</strong> in the heart are of <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> (soluble) and not nitrogen (insoluble).  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0462","text":"In which of the following ​patients is inhaled nitri​c oxide approved for mana‍gement?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is inhaled nitric oxide approved for management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"52-year-old man with hyper​tensive emergency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"44-year-old woman with gas​tric varices","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3-day-old neonate with per​sistent pulmonary hyperte​nsion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"80-year-old man with malig​nant hypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3-day-old neonate with persistent pulmonary hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3-day-old neonate with persistent pulmonary hypertension</strong></p><p><strong>Inhaled nitric oxide</strong> (NO) is approved (USFDA) for use in <strong>persistent pulmonary hypertension </strong>of the <strong>newborn</strong>. </p>\n<p>Inhaled NO <strong>selectively dilates</strong> the <strong>pulmonary vasculature</strong> and has the potential to treat many conditions that result in increased pulmonary hypertension. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5925","text":"Which of the following inh​alational anesthetics doe​s not preserve cardiac ou‍tput?","raw_text":"Which of the following inhalational anesthetics does not preserve cardiac output?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halo​thane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof​lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Halothane</strong></p><p><strong>Halothane</strong> produces the <strong>maximum decrease </strong>in<strong> cardiac output</strong> by a combination of decreased heart rate and depressed myocardial contractility</p>\n<p>Halothane and enflurane produce a greater decrease in myocardial contractility than isoflurane, desflurane, and sevoflurane which best maintain cardiac output.</p>\n<p>Of these, cardiac output is <strong>best preserved</strong> with <strong>isoflurane</strong> – though it does produce some myocardial depression, reflex tachycardia maintains cardiac output. Isoflurane is the <strong>agent of choice</strong> in <strong>cardiac patients.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5933","text":"Ozone depletion is least w​ith which inhaled anesthe​tic ?","raw_text":"Ozone depletion is least with which inhaled anesthetic ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xe​non","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Xenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Xenon</strong></p><p><strong>Xenon</strong> is a noble gas which is used for its anesthetic properties and it has no effect on the ozone.</p>\n<p><strong>Other options :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li> Halothane and isoflurane have the potential to destroy stratospheric ozone due to their bromine (halothane only) and chlorine-bearing molecules.</li>\n<li>The contribution to stratospheric ozone loss by the anesthetic N2O is likely more significant, compared to halothane and isoflurane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5920","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding​ xenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding xenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreases airflow resistan​ce and work of breathing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraindicated in cardiac​ patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contributes to green house​ effect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid induction and recove​ry","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rapid induction and recovery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rapid induction and recovery</strong></p><p>Xenon has a very low blood-gas solubility coefficient (0.115) and so produces <strong>rapid induction</strong> and <strong>emergence</strong> from anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Option A: the disadvantage with xenon gas is its <strong>high density</strong> (5.9 g/L), which is much higher than oxygen and nitrous oxide which leads to <strong>increased flow resistance </strong>and<strong> work of breathing</strong>. Thus it is not suitable for patients with compromised respiratory function.</p>\n<p>Option B: It has minimal cardiovascular effects- it produces minimal cardiovascular depression, and it is not arrhythmogenic- so<strong> safe in cardiac patients</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Xenon produces <strong>no greenhouse effect </strong>or<strong> ozone depletion</strong> and is environmentally safe, unlike the other inhaled anesthetics.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Other advantages of xenon:\n<ul>\n<li>Does not trigger malignant hyperthermia</li>\n<li>Nonexplosive</li>\n<li>Has <strong>analgesic</strong> activity - reduces intraoperative opioid requirements</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5870","text":"Which of the following inh​alational agents is suita​ble for neurosurgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following inhalational agents is suitable for neurosurgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo​thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isofl​urane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou​s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Et​her","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoflurane</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>isoflurane</strong> is best suited for neurosurgery.</p>\n<p>All of the inhalational anesthetics are<strong> cerebral vasodilators</strong>. The order of vasodilating potency is:</p>\n<p>halothane &gt; enflurane &gt; desflurane &gt; <strong>isoflurane </strong>&gt; sevoflurane. </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Due to its lesser vasodilating property, <strong>sevoflurane</strong> is preferred to isoflurane for neurosurgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EUUu4Uc1","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"15_digestive__hepatobiliary___genitourinary_systems","title":"15.Digestive, Hepatobiliary & Genitourinary Systems","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC2030","text":"The hard palate contains _​_______","raw_text":"The hard palate contains ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratinised epithelium, su​bmucosa, minor salivary g‌lands","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Keratinised epithelium, ab​sent submucosa, no saliva‌ry glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-keratinised epithelium​, submucosa, minor saliva‌ry glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-keratinised epithelium​ , absent submucosa, mino‌r salivary glands","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Keratinised epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Keratinised epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The hard palate contains <strong>keratinised stratified squamous epithelium,</strong> has a submucosa, and contains minor salivary gland. This keratinised stratified squamous epithelium has regional variations and may be <strong>orthokeratinised or parakeratinised</strong>(found in hard palate and gingiva).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>bony structure</strong> of the hard palate is covered by a <strong>firmly attached mucosa</strong> in the central part. In the lateral parts, the hard palate also has a submucosal layer containing blood vessels. There are minor mucous type salivary glands in the submucosa in the posterior part of the hard palate.  </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">(Note: In the orthokeratinized epithelium the cell nuclei disappear in the keratinized layer, whereas in the parakeratinized epithelium flattened, highly condensed nuclei remain in the cell cytoplasm of the keratinized layer until exfoliation)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2665","text":"The muscularis externa lay​er of the gut wall has __‌_________","raw_text":"The muscularis externa layer of the gut wall has ___________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner longitudinal and out​er circular muscle fibres‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inner circular and outer l​ongitudinal muscle fibres‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"only circular muscle fibre​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"only longitudinal muscle f​ibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/502da17e30604431990f7dec1dad6b86x1280x1386.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle fibres</strong></p><p>The <strong>muscularis externa</strong> has an inner <strong>circular</strong> muscle fibers and outer <strong>longitudinal</strong> muscle fibers. </p>\n<p>The gut wall receives its strength mainly from the muscularis externa, which is the thick layer of muscle that surrounds the submucosa.<strong> </strong>It is responsible for gut movements such as peristalsis. </p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Muscularis externa contains smooth muscle, <strong>except </strong>in <strong>upper part</strong> of<strong> esophagus</strong> where it has only <strong>striated muscle</strong> fibres. </li>\n<li>The <strong>stomach</strong> has <strong>3 layers</strong> in the muscularis externa, with an extra oblique layer. </li>\n<li>In the <strong>colon</strong>, the outer <strong>longitudinal fibers</strong> join to form prominent bundles called the <strong>taenia coli.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is a histological section of the duodenum showing the inner and outer layers of muscularis externa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2666","text":"A patient with suspected o​esophagal carcinoma is pl‌anned for an endoscopic b​iopsy. A section of the l‌esion was taken and sent ​for histopathological exa​mination. Which of the fo​llowing layers of the gas‍trointestinal tract is no‍t expected to be seen on ​microscopic examination o‌f the section?","raw_text":"A patient with suspected oesophagal carcinoma is planned for an endoscopic biopsy. A section of the lesion was taken and sent for histopathological examination. Which of the following layers of the gastrointestinal tract is not expected to be seen on microscopic examination of the section?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muc​osa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Musculari​s propria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subm​ucosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ser​osa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30033465e9574360aa8061793762c844x1280x1386.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da402a001f734082b8095ac948063836x1280x924.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serosa</strong></p><p>The <strong>serosa</strong> is <strong>absent</strong> in the <strong>oesophagus</strong> and hence, is not expected to be seen on microscopic examination.</p>\n<p>The oesophagus has an <strong>adventitia </strong>which is made up of <strong>dense fibrous tissue</strong> covering the <strong>muscularis externa.</strong> Only the lower part of the esophagus which is intra-abdominal has a layer of peritoneum covering it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In general, the <strong>gut tube</strong> is made of the following <strong>layers</strong> (inner to outer side):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mucosa </strong>- The inner-most layer is the <strong>mucous membrane</strong> with the following sub-layers:\n<ul>\n<li>Lining epithelium </li>\n<li>Lamina propria - A layer of connective tissue supporting the lining epithelium</li>\n<li>Muscularis mucosae - A thin layer of smooth muscle</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Submucosa </strong>- Made of loose areolar tissue on which the mucous membrane rests</li>\n<li><strong>Muscularis externa </strong>- Thick layer of muscle around the submucosa (with inner circular and outer longitudinal fibres)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2667","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the eso‌phagus is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the esophagus is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lined by stratified ​squamous epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The upper third contains o​nly skeletal muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The middle third contains ​both skeletal and smooth ‌muscles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The lower third contains o​nly skeletal muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The lower third contains only skeletal muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The lower third contains only skeletal muscle</strong></p><p>The <strong>lower third of the oesophagus</strong> consists of only the <strong>smooth muscles.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 165\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The oesophagus consists of the following layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mucosa </strong>-<strong> </strong>consists of a <strong>non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</strong>, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae</li>\n<li><strong>Submucosa </strong>- A special feature of the submucosa is the presence of compound tubuloalveolar mucous glands</li>\n<li><strong>Muscularis externa </strong>- with the usual circular and longitudinal layers\n<ul>\n<li>Upper 1/3rd - Only <strong>skeletal muscle </strong>fibres are present</li>\n<li>Middle 1/3rd - <strong>Both skeletal and smooth muscle</strong> fibres are present</li>\n<li>Lower 1/3rd - Only <strong>smooth muscle </strong>fibres are present</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Adventitia </strong>- Most of the oesophagus has a dense fibrous tissue covering called the adventitia. Only a short length of the oesophagus in the lower part has a peritoneal covering (serosa).</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2668","text":"Within which part of the g​astric gland are the chie‌f cells located?","raw_text":"Within which part of the gastric gland are the chief cells located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastr​ic pit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ist​hmus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ne​ck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ba​se","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Base","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c83b25cff5ba46b7a9fb2c25e2891ea3x1280x3194.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Base</strong></p><p>Chief cells are located mostly in the <strong>basal part</strong> of the gastric glands. </p>\n<p>Gastric glands are simple or branched tubular glands that lies perpendicular to the gastric mucosa. They can be identified to have 3 main sections:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Basal part (deep part)</li>\n<li>Neck</li>\n<li>Isthmus (connects to gastric pit) - Gastric glands open into the gastric pits. </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Types of cells in the gastric glands </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Chief cells </strong>(also called <strong>peptic</strong> or <strong>zymogen cells</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mostly found in the <strong>basal </strong>or<strong> deep</strong> portion of the gastric glands</li>\n<li>Cells are <strong>cuboidal </strong>or low columnar shaped</li>\n<li>The cytoplasm is <strong>basophilic </strong>and contains prominent secretory granules</li>\n<li>Secrete <strong>pepsinogen</strong> (precursor of pepsin) and <strong>lipase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Oxyntic cells </strong>(or<strong> parietal cells</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Found in the <strong>upper part </strong>of the gland </li>\n<li>Cells are<strong> large, ovoid</strong> or polyhedral</li>\n<li>Stain strongly with <strong>eosin</strong></li>\n<li>Produce <strong>hydrochloric acid</strong> (HCl) and <strong>intrinsic factor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mucous neck cells:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prominently found at the <strong>neck part</strong> of the gastric glands</li>\n<li>They <strong>secrete mucus </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Neuroendocrine cells:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Situated mostly in the <strong>basal or deeper parts</strong> of the glands, along with the chief cells. T</li>\n<li>Cells are <strong>pleomorphic-shaped</strong> and display irregular nuclei surrounded by granular cytoplasm</li>\n<li>Synthesize many important biogenic amines and polypeptides such as:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>G cells</strong> - Gastrin</li>\n<li><strong>D cells</strong> - Somatostatin</li>\n<li><strong>Enterochromaffin cells</strong> - Serotonin</li>\n<li><strong>ECL cells</strong> (enterochromaffin-like cells) - Histamine</li>\n<li><strong>P/D1 cells</strong> - Ghrelin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Stem cells:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Undifferentiated cells that multiply and replace other cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2670","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the gas‌tric glands is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the gastric glands is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyloric antrum has many ac​id secreting cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxyntic cells secrete hydr​ochloric acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac region has many mu​cus secreting cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parietal cells secrete int​rinsic factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyloric antrum has many acid secreting cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyloric antrum has many acid secreting cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>pylorus</strong> consists of mainly <strong>mucus-secreting</strong> <strong>glands. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The gastric glands can be divided into 3 groups which are namely:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Principal gastric glands -</strong> They are found in the <strong>body</strong> and <strong>fundus</strong>. About 3-7 principal glands open into the gastric pit. These glands contain at least <strong>5 different types of cells: </strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Chief cells</li>\n<li>Parietal cells</li>\n<li>Mucous neck cells</li>\n<li>Neuroendocrine cells</li>\n<li>Stem cells. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac gastric glands -</strong> They are found in the small area near the <strong>cardiac orifice</strong>. Some are simple tubular glands, others are compound branched tubular. They contain <strong>mucus-secreting cells predominantly</strong>. Parietal and chief cells are sparsely present.</li>\n<li><strong>Pyloric gastric glands -</strong> Found in the <strong>pyloric antrum</strong>. About 2-3 convoluted tubes open into deep gastric pits. They <strong>mainly have mucus-secreting cells.</strong> They also have <strong>neuroendocrine cells</strong>, especially gastrin-secreting G cells. Parietal and chief cells are scarce.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2687","text":"The Brunner's glands are s​een in _____","raw_text":"The Brunner's glands are seen in _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jej​unum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper d​uodenum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower d​uodenum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Appe​ndix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Upper duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1b6fb58cac44127937f8c4207b1803dx1280x2063.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Upper duodenum</strong></p><p><strong>Brunner's glands</strong> (or duodenal glands) are <strong>compound</strong> <strong>tubular submucosal glands</strong> present in the <strong>upper duodenum</strong> (above the sphincter of Oddi).</p>\n<p>The alveoli of <strong>duodenal glands</strong> are lined mostly by <strong>mucous secreting columnar cells</strong>. Their ducts pass through the muscularis mucosae and empty into the <strong>intestinal crypts</strong> (<strong>of</strong> <strong>Lieberkuhn</strong>). </p>\n<p>The duodenum can be easily differentiated from the jejunum or ileum by the presence of these submucosal glands. The jejunum and ileum do not have glands in their submucosa. </p>\n<p>Given below is the histological section of normal duodenum showing the Brunner's glands at the submucosal layer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2690","text":"An intern accidentally sen​t an incompletely labelle‌d sample of the resected ​bowel wall from a patient‌ with intestinal obstruct​ion for histopathological​ examination. Given below​ is the microscopic image‍ of the section of the sa‍mple. Which part of the g​astrointestinal tract is ‌being visualised here?","raw_text":"An intern accidentally sent an incompletely labelled sample of the resected bowel wall from a patient with intestinal obstruction for histopathological examination. Given below is the microscopic image of the section of the sample. Which part of the gastrointestinal tract is being visualised here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esop​hagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sto​mach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Il​eum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Duod​enum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ileum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/18dd90e791d74d17abcc2147c3056d24.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ileum</strong></p><p>The image is a <strong>section through the ileum </strong>as evident by the <strong>thin</strong> and <strong>slender</strong> villi and an aggregated lymphatic follicle (<strong>Peyer’s patch</strong>) in the <strong>submucosa</strong>.</p>\n<p>These aggregations of lymphatic follicles are known as <strong>Mucosa Associated Lymphoid Tissue</strong> (MALT), and<strong> </strong>also as <strong>gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT). </strong>Peyer's patches in submucosa are found in large size and numerously in the terminal <strong>ileum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2691","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about Pa‌neth cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Paneth cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are rich in rough end​oplasmic reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rich i​n zinc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contain ​lysozyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foamy ap​pearance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Foamy appearance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/719a0409e5824a72a9b5270951e8222ex1280x2086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Foamy appearance</strong></p><p>The <strong>foamy appearance</strong> or frothy cytoplasm is <strong>not</strong> a feature of <strong>Paneth</strong> cells, but of Goblet cells. Mucus-secreting <strong>Goblet cells</strong> contain lipid or mucinous material<strong> </strong>with a <strong>foamy appearance.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Paneth cells (Enzyme cells) </strong>are present only in the deeper portions of the intestinal crypts. They contain many eosinophilic secretory granules. On microscopic examination, the Paneth cells are seen to contain a significant amount of <strong>rough endoplasmic reticulum. </strong>They are also <strong>rich in zinc</strong>. The function of Paneth cells is to produce <strong>lysozyme</strong> that kills bacteria. They may also produce other enzymes. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2704","text":"A 42-year-old man presente​d to the clinic with a hi‌story of anemia, non-bloo​dy diarrhoea and poor app‌etite. A diagnosis of cel​iac disease is suspected ​and a duodenal biopsy is ​taken for histopathologic‍al examination. Which of ‍the following cells are n​ot likely to be seen on m‌icroscopic examination of‍ the section?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented to the clinic with a history of anemia, non-bloody diarrhoea and poor appetite. A diagnosis of celiac disease is suspected and a duodenal biopsy is taken for histopathological examination. Which of the following cells are not likely to be seen on microscopic examination of the section?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stem ​cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Goblet​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paneth​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neck ​cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neck cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neck cells</strong></p><p><strong>Neck cells</strong> are not likely to be seen on microscopy as they are <strong>not present</strong> in the <strong>small intestine.</strong> The neck cells are the <strong>mucous secreting cells</strong> that are present in the upper end (or ‘neck’) of the main gastric glands of the <strong>stomach</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Stem cells </strong>are the <strong>undifferentiated</strong> <strong>cells</strong> present in the wall of the intestinal crypts. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Goblet cells</strong> are <strong>mucous secreting cells</strong>. Each goblet cell has an expanded upper part that is distended with mucin granules. The nucleus is flattened and is situated near the base of the cell.</p>\n<p>Option C: Paneth Cells (zymogen cells) are found only in the deeper parts of intestinal crypts. They contain prominent eosinophilic secretory granules.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2692","text":"In which of the following ​structures are centroacin‌ar cells mainly present?","raw_text":"In which of the following structures are centroacinar cells mainly present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panc​reas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Paroti​d gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pros​tate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li​ver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancreas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0beb59b49724010af472979a5bc3dd2x1280x1100.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancreas</strong></p><p>Centroacinar cells are mainly present in the pancreas. </p>\n<p><strong>Centroacinar cells</strong> are present in the alveoli of the <strong>exocrine pancreas. </strong>They are called so because they are visible near the <strong>centre of the acinus (or alveolus</strong>). These cells actually are actually part of the intercalated ducts that are invaginated into the secretory elements of the pancreas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2693","text":"Which of the following str​uctures does not form the‌ portal triad of the live​r?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not form the portal triad of the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepati​c vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bile​ duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Porta​l vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatic vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47ceb71a40d5401aac366c1f6653b52ax1280x2148.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatic vein</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic vein</strong> does not form part of the portal triad.</p>\n<p>Portal canals are present at the angular intervals at the periphery of each hepatic lobule. They are filled with <strong>connective tissue </strong>and form a connective tissue network across the entire liver substance.</p>\n<p>Each ‘canal’ consists of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A branch of the <strong>portal vein</strong></li>\n<li>A branch of the <strong>hepatic artery</strong></li>\n<li>An <strong>interlobular bile duct</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These <strong>three</strong> structures collectively form a <strong>portal triad</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a cross-section of the normal hepatic tissue showing the portal triad.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2694","text":"The space of Disse consist​s of all of the following‌ except ______","raw_text":"The space of Disse consists of all of the following except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Micro​villi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood ​plasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kupffer​'s cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fat ​cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kupffer's cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30d56388bdfc47c3857415654d48802ax1280x803.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kupffer&#x27;s cells</strong></p><p>The space of Disse consists of all of the following mentioned above except Kupffer cells. They<strong> </strong>are present in the lining of the <strong>walls of the sinusoids</strong>, not in the Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space). </p>\n<p><strong>Kupffer cells </strong>are also known as stellate macrophages or Kupffer-Borowicz cells. They are specialized macrophages present in the liver and are part of the mononuclear phagocyte system.</p>\n<p><strong>Space of Disse </strong>or the <strong>perisinusoidal space</strong> is present in the liver <strong>between</strong> the <strong>hepatocyte</strong> and a <strong>sinusoid</strong>. This space contains the <strong>blood plasma. </strong>The <strong>microvilli</strong> of hepatocytes extend into this space. This allows absorption of proteins and other plasma components from the sinusoids by the hepatocytes. This space may also have some <strong>fat cells</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2695","text":"Sinusoids are present in L​iver Kidney Lymph node Sp‌leen Bone marrow","raw_text":"<p>Sinusoids are present in</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Kidney</li>\n<li>Lymph node</li>\n<li>Spleen</li>\n<li>Bone marrow</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3, ​4, &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3, 4,​ &amp;amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, 4,​ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3, 4,​ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3, 4, & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1184f0474754caf9f59cbdbb7c06393x1280x1063.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3, 4, &amp; 5</strong></p><p>The sinusoids are present in all the above-mentioned structures except kidneys.</p>\n<p>They are present in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Spleen</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Endocrine glands (adrenal medulla, parathyroid)</li>\n<li>Bone marrow</li>\n<li>Carotid body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image is a graphical representation of sinusoids (sinus-like) which are small blood channels formed by subdivision of the lumen of large blood vessels (sinuses) by the invasion of developing parenchymal cell-cords.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2696","text":"The transitional epitheliu​m lines all of the follow‌ing except _____","raw_text":"The transitional epithelium lines all of the following except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ure​ters","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minor​ calyx","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary​ bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membranou​s urethra","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Membranous urethra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d08d82d545bf4815b7899a9df650acf2x1280x2380.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Membranous urethra</strong></p><p><strong>Transitional</strong> <strong>epithelium</strong> lines all the above structures <strong>except</strong> the <strong>membranous</strong> urethra.<br/>Transitional epithelium is found in the <strong>renal</strong> <strong>pelvis</strong> and <strong>calyces</strong>, the <strong>ureter</strong>, the <strong>urinary</strong> <strong>bladder</strong>, and <strong>pre-prostatic </strong>&amp;<strong> prostatic parts</strong> of the<strong> urethra</strong>. Because of this distribution, it is also called the <strong>urothelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both in the male and female, the <strong>greater part of the urethra</strong> is lined by <strong>pseudostratified columnar epithelium</strong>. A short part adjoining the urinary bladder is lined by <strong>transitional epithelium</strong>, while the part near the external orifice is lined by <strong>stratified squamous epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following are various parts of the urethra in males and their lining epithelium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-prostatic &amp; prostatic urethra -</strong> Transitional epithelium</li>\n<li><strong>Membranous urethra -</strong> Pseudostratified/stratified columnar</li>\n<li><strong>Penile/spongy urethra -</strong> Proximally by pseudostratified columnar, <strong>distally </strong>by stratified squamous</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is the histological section of the urinary bladder showing the transitional epithelium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2697","text":"Which of the following acc​urately describes the lin‌ing epithelium of the vag​ina?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes the lining epithelium of the vagina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudostratified keratiniz​ed columnar epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keratinized stratified squ​amous epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-keratinized stratified​ squamous epithelium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudostratified non-kerat​inized columnar epitheliu‌m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b60d109739447b0ac490859d976dd7cx1280x1386.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</strong></p><p>The lining epithelium of the vagina is made up of <strong>non-keratinised stratified squamous</strong> epithelium. </p>\n<p>Other sites lined by the non-keratinised squamous epithelium are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mouth</li>\n<li>Tongue</li>\n<li>Pharynx</li>\n<li>Oesophagus</li>\n<li>Cornea</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Keratinised stratified squamous epithelium</strong> covers the skin of the whole body and forms the epidermis.</p>\n<p>The given image shows a histological section of the vaginal epithelium showing the non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2699","text":"In which of the following ​structures is the type of‌ epithelium as shown in t​he image seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following structures is the type of epithelium as shown in the image seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bile​ duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common b​ile duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sk​in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary​ bladder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Urinary bladder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c7324cc234c49449f3b43a50288fce5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Urinary bladder</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>transitional epithelium</strong> and is seen<strong> </strong>in the <strong>urinary bladder.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>deepest layer </strong>has <strong>columnar cells</strong>, the <strong>middle layers</strong> are made up of <strong>polyhedral cells</strong> and the <strong>surface layer</strong> has cells that are <strong>large</strong> and shaped like an <strong>umbrella.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Transitional epithelium</strong> is found in the <strong>renal pelvis</strong> and <strong>calyces</strong>, the <strong>ureter</strong>, the <strong>urinary bladder</strong>, and part of the <strong>urethra</strong>. Because of this distribution, it is also called the <strong>urothelium</strong>. In the <strong>urinary bladder</strong>, it is usually observed that <strong>transitional epithelium</strong> can be <strong>stretched considerably</strong> without being damaged. When stretched it appears to be thinner and the <strong>cells become flattened or rounded</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2700","text":"Identify the structure sho​wn in the histological im‌age below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure shown in the histological image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Female ​urethra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Male u​rethra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ure​ter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vas de​ferens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ureter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/318ddb7fc32f46b1977fea8469f71bd4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/463fbe5f84df42a3a0bee360d7bd32ccx1280x1374.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ureter</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>histological section of ureter.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows a thick, <strong>fibroelastic</strong>, <strong>lamina propria</strong> lying underneath the stratified, <strong>transitional epithelium</strong>. There are <strong>no mucosal</strong> or <strong>submucosal glands </strong>and <strong>no submucosa</strong>. There is a <strong>layer of smooth muscle</strong> outside the mucosa. The<strong> outer</strong> <strong>adventitial layer</strong> has fibroelastic connective tissue, with blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves.</p>\n<p>Given below is a cross-section of the ureter showing the transitional epithelium and the layer of smooth muscle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2701","text":"Which of the following are​ the epithelial supportin‌g cells of the seminifero​us tubules?","raw_text":"Which of the following are the epithelial supporting cells of the seminiferous tubules?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germ ​cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leydig​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sertol​i cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peritubu​lar cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sertoli cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b824dfe0ca87428099998a0a2837fd74x1280x2345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sertoli cells</strong></p><p><strong>Sertoli cells</strong> also called <strong>sustentacular cells</strong> are the epithelial supporting cells of the <strong>seminiferous tubules</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sertoli cells are derived from the <strong>epithelial sex cords</strong> of the developing gonads. They are <strong>tall simple columnar cells</strong>.  They surround the proliferating and differentiating germ cells forming pockets around these cells. Sertoli cells provide nutrients and phagocytose excess cytoplasm from the newly forming spermatozoa.</p>\n<p>The given image is a section of seminiferous ducts showing the different types of cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2702","text":"Identify the structure sho​wn in the histological im‌age below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure shown in the histological image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ure​ter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidi​dymis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seminal​ vesicle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vas de​ferens","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vas deferens","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/886d6a2d5c804e78b9450730d38602b6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa3fcab2bc1d43809814e37168b01c5bx1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vas deferens</strong></p><p>Among the given options the histological section is most likely to be of the <strong>vas deferens</strong> or the ductus deferens.</p>\n<p>The epithelium of this tube displays a <strong>pseudostratified columnar epithelium</strong>. It is surrounded by a prominent <strong>muscular layer</strong>. This layer contains <strong>inner</strong> and<strong> outer longitudinal muscle</strong> and a <strong>middle circular</strong> layer.<strong> </strong>An <strong>adventitia</strong> of connective tissue surrounds the muscularis layer. The <strong>ductus deferens</strong> extends from the <strong>epididymis</strong> to the<strong> ejaculatory ducts</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2703","text":"Identify the structure sho​wn in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminifer​ous tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pros​tate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endom​etrium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mammar​y gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endometrium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d49a6e227f5740a0a9e82d327a73b47c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c6048c81cb6449a800c870868682e85x1280x2133.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endometrium</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the histological image is likely to be of the <strong>endometrium.</strong> It shows the presence of<strong> glycogen</strong> masses in the<strong> basal cytoplasm</strong> of the epithelial cells lining the glands which are the features of the early<strong> secretory phase</strong> of the<strong> endometrium</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>uterus</strong> is made up of an external layer of smooth muscle called the <strong>myometrium</strong>, and an internal layer called the <strong>endometrium</strong>. The endometrium has three layers which are namely:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Stratum compactum</strong>,</li>\n<li><strong>Stratum spongiosum</strong> (which make up the stratum functionalis)</li>\n<li><strong>Stratum basalis</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The changes observed in the endometrium during the menstrual cycle are continuous and may be summarized as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Increase in thickness of endometrium -</strong> The endometrium progressively increases in thickness. In the postmenstrual phase, it is 0.5–1 mm thick; in the proliferative phase it is 2–3 mm thick, and in the secretory phase, its thickness reaches 5–7 mm.</li>\n<li><strong>Increase in dimensions of uterine glands -</strong> The uterine glands grow in length. At first, they are straight but gradually become convoluted. Because of these convolutions, the glands acquire a '<strong>saw-toothed'</strong> appearance when seen in the longitudinal section. The glands also increase in diameter.</li>\n<li><strong>Change in the epithelial lining of glands -</strong> The epithelium lining the glands is at first cuboidal. During the proliferative stage, it becomes <strong>columnar.</strong> Glycogen accumulates in the basal portion of the epithelial cell, pushing the nucleus nearer the lumen. During the secretory phase, the apical part of the cell is shed off as part of the secretion and the cell again becomes cubical.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 57\"> Given below is a histological section of the endometrium in the proliferative phase.</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6654","text":"The structures marked in t​he image below are found ‌in the epithelial lining ​of all of the following s‌ites, except:","raw_text":"The structures marked in the image below are found in the epithelial lining  of all of the following sites, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovi​duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidi​dymis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bro​nchi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epen​dyma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epididymis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9318e76d9e294ac7a22199533ec23ba2.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/019f8a1459f24ce39694e87510f6add7x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epididymis</strong></p><p>The image shows an arrow mark pointing towards the <strong>cilia</strong>. All the structures given in the options have a ciliated lining epithelium except <strong>epididymis,</strong> which is lined by <strong>pseudostratified columnar epithelium</strong> with stereocilia.</p>\n<p>The given image is a histological section of the epididymis showing the pseudostratified columnar epithelium with stereocilia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Cilia are minute hair-like projections, while stereocilia are very long, thick microvilli measuring about 5–10 μm in length.</p>\n<p><strong>Ciliated epithelium:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The epithelium bears cilia, which moves in a coordinated fashion, and help in the movement of the mucus, or propel the movement of any other structures such as ova or spermatozoa.</li>\n<li>The ciliated columnar epithelium is found in most of the <strong>respiratory tract,</strong> the uterus, and the <strong>uterine tube</strong> <strong>(oviduct),</strong> efferent ductules of the testis, parts of the middle ear and auditory tube; the <strong>ependyma</strong> lining the central canal of the spinal cord and the ventricles of the brain.</li>\n<li>The pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium is seen in the trachea and in <strong>large bronchi.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4Sa6EDX-","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"15_metabolism_of_acylglycerols_and_sphingolipids","title":"15.Metabolism Of Acylglycerols And Sphingolipids","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0032","text":"What type of lipid is tria‍cylglycerol?","raw_text":"What type of lipid is triacylglycerol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycer‍olipid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glyceroph‍ospholipid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glyco‍lipid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sterol‍ Lipid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycerolipid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4150783f933a4b9b9ac1865544ba4013x222x167.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycerolipid</strong></p><p><strong>Triacylglycerol</strong> is a <strong>glycerolipid.</strong></p>\n<p>Glycerolipids are esters of the trihydric alcohol <strong>glycerol </strong>and fatty acids. The types of glycerolipids include mono-, di- or tri-substituted glycerols. The best known glycerolipid is the fatty acid <strong>triester</strong> of <strong>glycerol</strong> known as <strong>triglycerides</strong> or<strong> </strong>triacylglycerols. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0033","text":"Which is the first step fo‍r catabolism of triacylgl​ycerol?","raw_text":"Which is the first step for catabolism of triacylglycerol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decarbo‍xylation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydro‍lysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oxid‍ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dehydro‍genation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hydrolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hydrolysis</strong></p><p>The <strong>first step </strong>for the<strong> catabolism </strong>of <strong>triacylglycerol </strong>is<strong> hydrolysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Triacylglycerols must be <strong>hydrolyzed</strong> by <strong>lipase </strong>to their constituent fatty acids and glycerol before further catabolism. Hydrolysis (lipolysis) occurs in <strong>adipose tissue</strong> with release of free fatty acids into the plasma. Free fatty acids combine with albumin to reach various tissues, where they are oxidized to obtain energy or re-esterified.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0034","text":"Which of the following org‍ans can catabolize the gl​ycerol component of triac‌ylglycerol? Liver Kidney ‍Brown adipose tissue Whit​e adipose tissue Lactatin‌g mammary gland","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following organs can catabolize the glycerol component of triacylglycerol?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Kidney</li>\n<li>Brown adipose tissue</li>\n<li>White adipose tissue</li>\n<li>Lactating mammary gland</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‍3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,‍ 3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3,‍ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‍3, 4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p><strong>White adipose tissue</strong> <strong>cannot</strong> catabolize the <strong>glycerol</strong> component of triacylglycerol.</p>\n<p>The utilization of glycerol depends upon whether the tissue has the enzyme <strong>glycerol kinase. </strong>It is found in the <strong>liver, kidney, intestine,</strong> brown adipose tissue and the lactating mammary gland.<strong> </strong>However, white adipose tissue<strong> lacks </strong>glycerol kinase. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0035","text":"Which of the following rep‍resents the structure of ​a phospholipid?","raw_text":"Which of the following represents the structure of a phospholipid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycerol + phosphate + cho‍line or ethanolamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monoglyceride + phosphate ‍+ choline or ethanolamine​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diglyceride + phosphate + ‍choline or ethanolamine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triglyceride + phosphate +‍ choline or ethanolamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diglyceride + phosphate + choline or ethanolamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4fd7ed9951174c1c90fce7d6f1cca288x502x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diglyceride + phosphate + choline or ethanolamine</strong></p><p>Phospholipid consists of <strong>diglyceride</strong> + <strong>phosphate</strong> + <strong>choline </strong>or<strong> ethanolamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>A phospholipid, such as <strong>lecithin</strong> or <strong>cephalin</strong>, is any lipid consisting of a diglyceride combined with a phosphate group. A simple organic molecule such as choline or ethanolamine is attached to the phosphate group. A<strong> diglyceride</strong> is <strong>glycerol</strong> molecule with<strong> 2 fatty acids </strong>attached to it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0036","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a phospholipid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a phospholipid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasm‍alogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipalmitoy‍l lecithin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cera‍mide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cardi‍olipin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ceramide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe819ccd084d4229be9a2b7be1b57aefx1280x822.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ceramide</strong></p><p><strong>Ceramide </strong>is<strong> not </strong>a<strong> phospholipid</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ceramide consists of<strong> sphingosine</strong> and a<strong> fatty acid</strong> (ceramide = sphingosine + fatty acid) . It can be a component of a phospholipid e.g <strong>sphingomyelin</strong>, which consists of ceramide, phosphoric acid and choline. It is also a component of non-phosphorylated compound lipids like <strong>cerebrosides, globosides, </strong>and<strong> gangliosides</strong>. These are<strong> </strong>fatty acids esterified with alcohol and contain other groups.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pl</strong><strong>asmalogens</strong> are a class of glycerophospholipids that contain <strong>vinyl-ether</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3509","text":"A 1.5 kg male baby was del‍ivered vaginally at 32 we​eks of gestation. The inf‌ant was cyanotic and requ‍ired CPAP immediately aft​er delivery. His chest ra‌diograph is shown below. ‌A deficiency of which of ‍the following is the like​ly cause for the conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A 1.5 kg male baby was delivered vaginally at 32 weeks of gestation. The infant was cyanotic and required CPAP immediately after delivery. His chest radiograph is shown below. A deficiency of which of the following is the likely cause for the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphatidyl‍ethanolamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipalmitoyl phosphatidylch‍oline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diphosphati‍dyl glycerol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphatid‍ylinositol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88730eefb2f04cfe9c3d733fb3335492.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine</strong></p><p>A preterm infant requiring resuscitation at birth with cyanosis and diffuse bilateral opacities on chest radiography is suggestive of <strong>infant respiratory distress syndrome. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>caused by a deficiency of <strong>dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dipalmitoyl lecithin </strong>or dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)<strong> </strong>is a major component of <strong>lung surfactant. </strong>In DPPC, positions 1 &amp; 2 on the glycerol are occupied by <strong>palmitate</strong>. Surfactant is secreted by the <strong>type 2 pneumocytes</strong> in the alveoli. It <strong>reduces surface tension</strong> at the air-liquid interface of the alveolus and increases compliance.</p>\n<p>Mature levels of surfactant are present after <strong>35 weeks</strong> of gestation. Thus, preterm babies are at risk of <strong>surfactant deficiency</strong>, leading to infant respiratory distress syndrome. <strong>Lung maturity </strong>of the fetus can be assessed by determining the <strong>ratio</strong> of <strong>lecithin</strong> to <strong>sphingomyelin</strong> (L:S ratio) in the amniotic fluid. A ratio of<strong> &gt;2 </strong>indicates maturity.</p>\n<p>Other options -</p>\n<p>Option 1:<strong> Phosphatidylethanolamine</strong>, also called <strong>cephalin</strong>, is found in the <strong>cell membranes</strong> of <strong>nervous tissue</strong>, particularly in the<strong> brain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option 3: <strong>Diphosphatidyl glycerol</strong>, also called <strong>cardiolipin</strong>, is found in the<strong> inner membrane </strong>of <strong>mitochondria</strong> and plays a key role in <strong>apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option 4:<strong> Phosphatidylinositol</strong> present in the <strong>cell membrane</strong> acts as a precursor of <strong>hormone secondary messengers</strong> and <strong>platelet-activating factor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0037","text":"What is the location of ph‍ospholipids synthesis?","raw_text":"What is the location of phospholipids synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rough endoplasmic reticulu‍m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth endoplasmic reticul‍um","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Golgi a‍pparatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitoch‍ondria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smooth endoplasmic reticulum</strong></p><p>The location of <strong>phospholipids synthesis</strong> is the<strong> smooth endoplasmic reticulum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Once synthesized the phospholipids are transported into the <strong>Golgi apparatus</strong>, and further across cell membranes, to be extruded out of the cell by the process of <strong>exocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0038","text":"Which of the following is ‍the common intermediate i​n the biosynthesis of tri‌acylglycerols and phospho‍glycerols?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the common intermediate in the biosynthesis  of  triacylglycerols and phosphoglycerols?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosph‍atidate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acyl‍ CoA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acety‍l CoA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasm‍alogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphatidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1727411fe9e94aa28ab63e62c487148cx1280x1102.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphatidate</strong></p><p>The common intermediate in the biosynthesis of triacylglycerols and phosphoglycerols is <strong>phosphatidate.</strong></p>\n<p>Acyl-CoA combines with glycerol-3-phosphate in two successive reactions to form phosphatidate or <strong>1,2-diacylglycerol phosphate.</strong> 1,2-<strong> </strong>diacylglycerol phosphate<strong> </strong>contributes its<strong> phospahate</strong> for the formation of<strong> phosphatidyl inositol</strong> and becomes <strong>1,2-diacylglycerol</strong>.</p>\n<p>The 1,2-diacylglycerol is either converted to triacylgycerol by <strong>diacylglycerol acyl transferase, </strong>or reacts with CDP-choline or CDP-ethanolamine to form the <strong>phospholipids</strong> phosphatidylcholine or phosphatidylethanolamine.  </p>\n<p>(Note:<strong> Phosphoglycerol </strong>is the glycerol ester of phosphoric acid and is a component of phospholipids). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0039","text":"Which of the following is ‍the composition of the ph​ospholipid cephalin ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the composition of the phospholipid cephalin ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphati‍dylserine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphatidyl‍ethanolamine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphati‍dylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphatid‍ylinositol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phosphatidylethanolamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phosphatidylethanolamine</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphatidylethanolamine </strong>is the composition of the phospholipid <strong>cephalin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Cephalin is a phospholipid composed of<strong> ethanolamine </strong>and<strong> phosphatidic acid</strong>. Thus, it is also known as phosphatidylethanolamine. It is found in cell membranes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0041","text":"Which of the following pho‍spholipids plays an impor​tant role in apoptosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following phospholipids plays an important role in apoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphati‍dylserine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphatidyl‍ethanolamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphati‍dylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphatid‍ylglycerol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphatidylserine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphatidylserine</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphatidylserine </strong>plays an important role in<strong> apoptosis. </strong></p>\n<p>Phosphatidylserine, a <strong>phospholipid</strong>, is a component of the cell membrane. It is composed of <strong>serine </strong>and <strong>phosphatidic acid</strong>. It plays a key role in cell cycle signaling, coagulation and <strong>apoptosis </strong>(programmed cell death).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0042","text":"Which of the following pho‍spholipids is found only ​in the mitochondria and h‌elps in the mitochondrial‍ function?","raw_text":"Which of the following phospholipids is found only in the mitochondria and helps in the mitochondrial function?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceph‍alin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardi‍olipin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leci‍thin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasm‍alogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cardiolipin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cardiolipin</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiolipin</strong> is the phospholipid found <strong>only</strong> in the <strong>mitochondria. </strong></p>\n<p>Cardiolipin is an important component of the <strong>inner mitochondrial membrane. </strong>It is essential for the <strong>optimal function</strong> of numerous mitochondrial <strong>enzymes</strong> involved in mitochondrial <strong>energy metabolism</strong> and for the <strong>maintenance </strong>of the<strong> </strong>mitochondrial membrane <strong>structure. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Decreased cardiolipin</strong> or alteration in its metabolism results in<strong> </strong>mitochondrial dysfunction. This is<strong> </strong>seen in<strong> Barth syndrome </strong>(cardioskeletal myopathy) which is <span class=\"ILfuVd\"><span class=\"hgKElc\">characterized by an enlarged and weakened heart (<strong>dilated cardiomyopathy</strong>), weakness in muscles used for movement (<strong>skeletal myopathy</strong>), recurrent infections due to small numbers of white blood cells (<strong>neutropenia</strong>), and <strong>short stature</strong>.</span></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0043","text":"Which of the following pho‍spholipids serves as a re​servoir for prostaglandin‌ synthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following phospholipids serves as a reservoir for prostaglandin synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphati‍dyl serine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphatid‍yl inositol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphatid‍yl choline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphatidyl‍ ethanolamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phosphatidyl inositol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phosphatidyl inositol</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphatidylinositol </strong>serves as a<strong> reservoir </strong>for<strong> prostaglandin synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>Phosphatidylinositol contains<strong> stearic acid</strong> and <strong>arachidonic acid</strong> attached to glycerol. Therefore, it serves as a <strong>reservoir </strong>of<strong> arachidonic acid</strong> in membranes and hence provides the necessary substrates for <strong>prostaglandin synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0044","text":"Which of the following pho‍spholipids has a role to ​play in signal transmissi‌on across membranes?","raw_text":"Which of the following phospholipids has a role to play in signal transmission across membranes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphati‍dyl serine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphatid‍yl inositol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphatid‍yl choline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphatidyl‍ ethanolamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phosphatidyl inositol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f3da5e8184f4756813e1f79529ba65bx1171x977.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phosphatidyl inositol</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphatidylinositol</strong> (PI) plays a role in <strong>signal transmission </strong>across membranes.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 204\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Membrane-bound phosphatidylinositols (<strong>PI</strong>) are phosphorylated to produce polyphosphoinositides such as <strong>phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate</strong> (PIP<sub>2</sub>). When neurotransmitters, hormones, and growth factors to receptors on the cell membrane, <strong>degradation</strong> of <strong>PIP<sub>2</sub> </strong>by<strong> phospholipase C</strong>.</p>\n<p>The degrada­tion products are <strong>i</strong><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">nositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">(IP<sub>3</sub>), which mediates the<strong> </strong></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">mobilization</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> of<strong> i</strong></span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">ntracellular calcium, </strong>and <strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">diacylglycerol</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> (DAG), which<strong> </strong>activates</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> protein kinase C. </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Both of these act synergistically to evoke specific cellular responses thus accomplishing signal transmission.</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0045","text":"A lady undergoing further ‍evaluation after a miscar​riage has a positive VDRL‌ result. Gold standard in‍vestigation using FTA-ABS​ shows a negative result.‌ Which of the following s‌ubstances is responsible ‍for this discrepancy in t​he results? (VDRL - Vener‌eal Disease Research Labo​ratory) (FTA-ABS - Fluore​scent treponemal antibody​ absorption test)","raw_text":"<p>A lady undergoing further evaluation after a miscarriage has a positive VDRL result. Gold standard investigation using FTA-ABS shows a negative result.  Which of the following substances is responsible for this discrepancy in the results?</p>\n<p>(VDRL - Venereal Disease Research Laboratory)</p>\n<p>(FTA-ABS -  Fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test) </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceph‍alin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leci‍thin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardi‍olipin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasm‍alogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiolipin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiolipin</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiolipin</strong>,<strong> </strong>a phospholipid with antigenic properties, is responsible for the discrepancy in results i.e <strong>positive VDRL test </strong>with <strong>negative FTA-ABS test</strong>. </p>\n<p>A positive VDRL test with negative FTA-ABS test suggests a <strong>false-positive VDRL</strong>. The VDRL antigen consists of <strong>cardiolipin-cholesterol-lecithin mixtures </strong>for the detection of serum <strong>antibodies </strong>formed against<strong> <em>Treponema pallidum</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>The lady undergoing further evaluation for a miscarriage probably has an<strong> anti-cardiolipin antibody</strong> in her serum. This antibody has likely cross-reacted with the <strong>cardiolipin</strong> component of the VDRL reagent, resulting in a<strong> </strong>biological false-positive VDRL result. However, a confirmatory test i.e FTA-ABS test reveals that her anti-cardiolipin antibody is <strong>not</strong> from infection with <em>Treponema pallidum</em> but due to some other reasons.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0046","text":"Sphingolipid is a type of ‍_______","raw_text":"Sphingolipid is a type of _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycerol esters saponifiab‍le lipid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-glycerol esters saponi‍fiable lipid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-saponif‍iable lipids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Derive‍d lipid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-glycerol esters saponifiable lipid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-glycerol esters saponifiable lipid</strong></p><p><strong>Sphingolipid</strong> is a type of <strong>non-glycerol ester saponifiable lipid.</strong></p>\n<p>Sphingolipids (eg: sphingomyelin) are a class of lipids containing a backbone of <strong>sphingoid bases</strong>, a set of <strong>aliphatic amino alcohols </strong>e.g. <strong>sphingosine. </strong>They are known as non-glycerol esters. Sphingolipids are amphipathic molecules. </p>\n<p>Sphingolipids are found on the <strong>outer layer</strong> of the plasma membrane and in the <strong>myelin sheath</strong> surrounding the nerves. They also play a role in <strong>cell signaling</strong> and <strong>apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0047","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a component of glycos​phingolipid ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of glycosphingolipid ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carboh‍ydrate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glyc‍erol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphin‍gosine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fatty‍ acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glycerol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef3dc15880544fa294a0e5611e96c0dfx1280x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glycerol</strong></p><p><strong>Glycerol</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a component of <strong>glycosphingolipids. </strong></p>\n<p>Glycolipids or glycosphingolipids are <strong>complex lipids</strong> that contain the alcohol <strong>sphingosine </strong>instead of glycerol. They also contain a<strong> fatty acid </strong>and a <strong>carbohydrate </strong>attached to sphingosine. </p>\n<p>Glycosphingolipid = Sphingosine + fatty acid + carbohydrate</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0048","text":"Which of the following is ‍an example of a glycolipi​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of a glycolipid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasm‍alogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cereb‍roside","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphing‍omyelin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leci‍thin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebroside","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebroside</strong></p><p><strong>Cerebroside</strong> is an example of a <strong>glycolipid.</strong></p>\n<p>A glycolipid is a complex lipid containing a<strong> carbohydrate</strong>. Cerebrosides consist of <strong>sphingosine</strong>, a <strong>fatty acid</strong> and a single <strong>sugar</strong> linked to sphingosine. Thus, they are glycolipids. Cerebrosides are found predominantly in the <strong>brain</strong> and <strong>peripheral nervous tissue</strong>, with high concentrations in the <strong>myelin sheath</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0050","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a role of glycosphing​olipids?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a role of glycosphingolipids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Source of blood group anti‍gens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cell surface receptors for‍ cholera toxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cell surface receptors for‍ tetanus toxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Formation of lung surfacta‍nt","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Formation of lung surfactant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Formation of lung surfactant</strong></p><p>Formation of lung surfactant is <strong>not</strong> a function of <strong>glycosphingolipids</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lung surfactant is composed of the <strong>glycerophospholipid</strong> known as <strong>dipalmitoyl lecithin</strong> or dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine.</p>\n<p><strong>Glycosphingolipids</strong>, like <strong>cerebrosides</strong>, are part of the outer leaflet of plasma membranes. They are important in cell adhesion, cell recognition, and signal transduction. Some function as <strong>b</strong><strong>lood group antigens,</strong> and as <strong>cell</strong> <strong>surface receptors </strong>for<strong> cholera </strong>(GM1 ganglioside) and <strong>tetanus toxins, </strong>and<strong> certain viruses </strong>and<strong> microbes. </strong>They also serve as various <strong>embryonic antigens</strong> specific for stages of fetal development. The carbohydrate portion of a glycolipid which serves as the antigenic determinant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0051","text":"A 1-year old boy presented‍ with loss of motor skill​s, increased startle resp‌onse to noise and seizure‍s. On fundoscopy, the fol​lowing image was seen. Fu‌rther evaluation reveals ‌the diagnosis of a lysoso‍mal storage disorder. Whi​ch of the following enzym‌es is most likely defecti​ve in this condition?","raw_text":"A 1-year old boy presented with loss of motor skills, increased startle response to noise and seizures. On fundoscopy, the following image was seen. Further evaluation reveals the diagnosis of a lysosomal storage disorder.  Which of the following enzymes is most likely defective in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hexosami‍nidase A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"β-gluc‍osidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"α-galac‍tosidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucose-6-‍phosphatase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hexosaminidase A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05b80af434a64103a820a6bdd7b22066.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hexosaminidase A</strong></p><p>An infant with loss of motor skills, increased startle response to noise and seizures (neurological involvement)<strong> </strong>and<strong> </strong>a <strong>cherry-red spot</strong> in the macula suggests <strong>Tay-Sachs disease. </strong>This disease is caused by a <strong>deficiency </strong>of the enzyme<strong> hexosaminidase A</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tay-Sachs disease is an autosomal recessive disorder resulting in the <strong>deficiency </strong>of<strong> hexosaminidase A. </strong>As a result, <strong>GM<sub>2</sub> gangliosides</strong> are not degraded and they <strong>accumulate </strong>within lysosomes.  Affected children present with <strong>loss </strong>of<strong> motor skills, increased startle response, s</strong><strong>eizures</strong>, hyperacusis, macrocephaly, neurodegeneration, blindness and muscular weakness. <strong>Cherry-red spots </strong>are seen in the macula of eye.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Note</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">:</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> The clinical features of </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Sandhoff's disease, </strong>due to deficiency of <strong>hexosaminidase A</strong> and<strong> B</strong>, are<span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> similar to Tay-Sachs. Affected children additionally have </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">hepatosplenomegaly </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">and </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">cardiac involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0052","text":"A 6-month-old infant with ‍progressive failure to th​rive and milestone regres‌sion is being evaluated f‍or an inborn error of met​abolism. On examination, ‌hypotonia, apathy, deafne‌ss, hepatosplenomegaly, a‍nd generalized lymphadeno​pathy are present. Fundus‌ examination is shown bel​ow. A liver biopsy shows ​foamy hepatocytes loaded ​with sphingomyelin. Elect​ron microscopy reveals ze​bra bodies. What is the l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old infant with progressive failure to thrive and milestone regression is being evaluated for an inborn error of metabolism. On examination, hypotonia, apathy, deafness, hepatosplenomegaly, and generalized lymphadenopathy are present. Fundus examination is shown below. A liver biopsy shows foamy hepatocytes loaded with sphingomyelin. Electron microscopy reveals zebra bodies. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Niemann-Pick disease type ‍A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tay-Sach‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Niemann-Pick disease type ‍B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sandhoff'‍s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Niemann-Pick disease type A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f716f89fa9a4370bbdb7b6295a1acee.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Niemann-Pick disease type A</strong></p><p>Progressive failure to thrive, milestone regression, hypotonia, apathy, deafness, hepatosplenomegaly, and generalized lymphadenopathy with the <strong>cherry-red spot</strong> on fundus examination suggests a diagnosis of<strong> Niemann-Pick disease type A. </strong></p>\n<p> Niemann-Pick disease is a lysosomal storage disorder due to an inherited <strong>deficiency </strong>of <strong>sphingomyelinase. </strong>This results in the accumulation of<strong> sphingomyelin</strong> in the monocyte-macrophage system. <strong>Type A </strong>disease is a severe<strong> infantile</strong> form. Infants present with <strong>extensive neurologic involvement </strong>(hypotonia, milestone regression, apathy),<strong> marked visceral accumulations </strong>of <strong>sphingomyelin</strong> (hepatosplenomegaly, foamy hepatocytes, <strong>zebra bodies</strong> on EM),<strong> progressive wasting</strong> and<strong> early death</strong> within the first 3 years of life.</p>\n<p>In contrast, patients with<strong> Niemann-Pick disease type B</strong> have organomegaly but generally <strong>no CNS involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0053","text":"An 8-year-old boy presents‍ with burning pain and nu​mbness in his hands, heat‌ intolerance and decrease‍d visual acuity. Further ​evaluation reveals skin a‌ngiokeratomas and a decre‌ased renal function. Whic‍h of the following is the​ mode of inheritance of t‌he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy presents with burning pain and numbness in his hands, heat intolerance and decreased visual acuity. Further evaluation reveals skin angiokeratomas and a decreased renal function. Which of the following is the mode of inheritance of the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‍ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked ‍recessive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal‍ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked‍ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. X-linked recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. X-linked recessive</strong></p><p>Burning pain and numbness in hands, heat intolerance, decreased visual acuity with skin angiokeratomas and a decreased renal function is suggestive of <strong>Fabry’s disease</strong> which is inherited as an <strong>X-linked recessive disorder.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fabry’s disease </strong>is a lipid storage disorder due to a deficiency of <strong>α-galactosidase A, </strong>resulting in the accumulation of <strong>g</strong><strong>lobotriaosyl-ceramide</strong> in tissues. Young boys present with<strong> Fabry crises </strong>(burning pain in the hands, feet and proximal extremities), <strong>angiokeratomas </strong>(reddish blue telangiectatic skin lesions between the umbilicus and knees), corneal and lenticular opacities, <strong>renal failure </strong>and cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>All sphingolipidoses</strong> are inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> manner,<strong> except </strong>Fabry’s disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0054","text":"A 10-year old child presen‍ts chronic fatigue, growt​h retardation and epistax‌is. On further evaluation‍, hepatosplenomegaly, thr​ombocytopenia and anemia ‌was found. Bone marrow bi‌opsy showed cells with wr‍inkled tissue paper appea​rance. What is the probab‌le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year old child presents chronic fatigue, growth retardation and epistaxis. On further evaluation, hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenia and anemia was found. Bone marrow biopsy showed cells with wrinkled tissue paper appearance. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Niemann Pi‍ck disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fabry’s‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gaucher’‍s disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tay Sach’‍s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gaucher’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5e4785fcd494319945452a9ac93f765x720x565.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4004e984f8bf4a02839890d61c6a222fx1279x1079.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gaucher’s disease</strong></p><p>Chronic fatigue, growth retardation, epistaxis, hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenia, anemia, Erlenmeyer flask deformity of femur with bone marrow biopsy showing cells with<strong> wrinkled tissue paper </strong>appearance suggest <strong>Gaucher’s disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gaucher's disease is a genetic disorder in which <strong>glucocerebroside</strong> accumulates in the reticuloendothelial system due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of the enzyme <strong>glucocerebrosidase </strong>(acid β-glucosidase). It is the <strong>most common </strong>lysosomal storage disorder.</p>\n<p>Affected children present with bleeding tendencies secondary to <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>, chronic fatigue secondary to <strong>anemia</strong>, painless <strong>hepatomegaly</strong> and <strong>splenomegaly</strong>, and <strong>bone pain</strong>. They may have liver cirrhosis, osteoporosis and pathological fractures. <strong>Neurological symptoms</strong> such as developmental delay, hypertonia, strabismus, supranuclear gaze palsy are seen in <strong>type 3 </strong>Gaucher's disease. On bone marrow biopsy, <strong>Gaucher's cells</strong> with<strong> wrinkled tissue paper </strong>appearance are seen. <strong>Erlenmeyer flask deformity</strong> is seen on X-ray of femur. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment is with<strong> enzyme replacement therapy</strong> in the form of <strong>human recombinant acid β-glucosidase</strong> (Imiglucerase), velaglucerase alfa or taliglucerase alfa. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Neurological involvement</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in <strong>type I Gaucher's disease</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0055","text":"Which of the following enz‍ymes is deficient in meta​chromatic leucodystrophy?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes is deficient in metachromatic leucodystrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β-Galac‍tosidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arylsu‍lfatase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphingom‍yelinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceram‍idase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arylsulfatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arylsulfatase</strong></p><p><strong>Metachromatic leucodystrophy</strong> occurs due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> arylsulfatase A enzyme</strong>.</p>\n<p>Metachromatic leucodystrophy is an autosomal recessive disorder. Deficiency of arylsulfatase A enzyme leads to accumulation of <strong>sulfated glycosphingolipids</strong> in white matter with subsequent <strong>demyelination</strong>. Affected infants (infantile form) present with c<span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">ognitive deterioration, <strong>m</strong></span><strong>uscle weakness</strong>, <strong>hypotonia</strong>, <strong>nystagmus</strong>, myoclonic seizures, optic atrophy and progressive paralysis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0056","text":"Accumulation of which of t‍he following substances i​s responsible for Farber’‌s disease?","raw_text":"Accumulation of which of the following substances is responsible for Farber’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leci‍thin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cera‍mide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphing‍omyelin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfa‍tides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ceramide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ceramide</strong></p><p><strong>Accumulation </strong>of <strong>c</strong><strong>eramide</strong> is seen in <strong>Farber’s disease. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Farber's disease </strong>occurs due to<strong> deficiency </strong>of <strong>ceramidase</strong>, which catalyses the breakdown of ceramide to fatty acid and sphingosine. Affected children present with <strong>p</strong><strong>ainful joint</strong> swelling and nodules, <strong>progressive joint deformity</strong>, <strong>h</strong><strong>oarse cry </strong>and <strong>b</strong><strong>reathing difficulty, </strong>mental retardation and <strong>f</strong><strong>ailure </strong>to<strong> thrive. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xXRsfR4B","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"15_bacterial_respiratory_infections","title":"15.Bacterial Respiratory Infections","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB8280","text":"Which of the following is ​the most important factor​ in reduction of diphther​ia cases?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important factor in reduction of diphtheria cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemopro​phylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Improved standard of livin​g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vacci​nation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased surveillance rat​es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vaccination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vaccination</strong></p><p><strong>Vaccination</strong> is the most important factor responsible for the <strong>reduction of diphtheria cases</strong> over the past decade.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8281","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about per​tussis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about pertussis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most of the infections are​ subclinical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most infectious during inc​ubation period","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary attack rate is 9​0 % in unimmunized indivi​duals","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebellar ataxia is a com​mon chronic complication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secondary attack rate is 90 % in unimmunized individuals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secondary attack rate is 90 % in unimmunized individuals</strong></p><p>The<strong> secondary attack rate</strong> in pertussis is about<strong> 90% </strong>in <strong>unimmunized</strong> individuals.</p>\n<p>The source of infection in pertussis is a case of pertussis. There is <strong>no </strong>evidence of <strong>subclinical infections. </strong>The patients are <strong>most infectious</strong> during the <strong>catarrhal stage.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>chief complications</strong> of pertussis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bronchopneumonia</li>\n<li>Bronchitis</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7012","text":"Droplet precaution and iso​lation is to be done for ​all of the following cond​itions except:","raw_text":"Droplet precaution and isolation is to be done for all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pert​ussis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipt​heria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Localized varicella zoster​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mycoplasma​ pneumonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Localized varicella zoster","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Localized varicella zoster</strong></p><p>Droplet precaution and isolation is not done for localized varicella zoster, as it is only transmitted through direct contact.</p>\n<p>Varicella-zoster localized in the patient with an intact immune system with a lesion that can be contained/covered do not require droplet precaution. <strong>Disseminated varicella</strong> in any patient requires <strong>airborne</strong> and <strong>contact precautions.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diphtheria, pertussis, and mycoplasma </strong>are transmitted by droplets and hence <strong>require droplet precaution</strong> and isolation.</p>\n<p>Although patients are not contagious starting 48 hours after antibiotic therapy, droplet precautions are typically maintained until the completion of the antibiotic course and are culture-negative on two cultures taken 24 hours apart. Droplet precautions include the use of masks, gowns, gloves and protective eye gear when providing direct patient care. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8282","text":"According to IMNCI, which ​of the following is a key​ clinical sign for pneumo​nia classification?","raw_text":"According to IMNCI, which of the following is a key clinical sign for pneumonia classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal ​flaring","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whee​zing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Co​ugh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fast br​eathing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fast breathing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e291034de6a436ba34b096ad206bf77x1280x711.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fast breathing</strong></p><p>According to the<strong> IMNCI, three </strong>key clinical <strong>signs</strong> are used to assess a sick child with suspected pneumonia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Respiratory rate</strong> (RR):<strong> d</strong><strong>istinguishes</strong> children who have <strong>pneumonia</strong> from those who do not. Children are classified under pneumonia if: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>&gt; 50/min</strong> RR for infants 2 months to 12 months</li>\n<li><strong>&gt; 40/min</strong> RR for children 12 months to 5 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lower chest wall indrawing</strong> indicates <strong>pneumonia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stridor </strong>indicates those with <strong>severe pneumonia</strong> requiring hospital<strong> admission.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image gives the <strong>IMNCI</strong> classification for cough/difficulty in breathing in children.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Signs of severe pneumonia are :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not able to drink</li>\n<li>Persistent vomiting</li>\n<li>Convulsions</li>\n<li>Lethargic or unconscious</li>\n<li>Stridor in a calm child</li>\n<li>Severe malnutrition</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7561","text":"According to IMNCI, a 6-mo​nth-old child is said to ​have pneumonia if the chi​ld has fast breathing wit‌h a respiratory rate more​ than:","raw_text":"According to IMNCI, a 6-month-old child is said to have pneumonia if the child has fast breathing with a respiratory rate more than:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5​0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/629eaeea3b5c4e64a6356dca16827622x1280x711.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 50</strong></p><p><strong>Three</strong> key clinical signs are used to assess a sick child with cough or difficult breathing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Respiratory rate</strong> (RR):<strong> d</strong><strong>istinguishes</strong> children who have <strong>pneumonia</strong> from those who do not. Children are classified as pneumonia if: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>&gt; 50/min</strong> RR for infants 2 months to 12 months</li>\n<li><strong>&gt; 40/min</strong> RR for children 12 months to 5 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lower chest wall indrawing</strong> indicates <strong>severe</strong> <strong>pneumonia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stridor </strong>indicates those with <strong>severe pneumonia</strong> requiring hospital<strong> admission.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image gives the <strong>IMNCI</strong> classification for cough/difficulty in breathing in children.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Signs of severe pneumonia are :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not able to drink</li>\n<li>Persistent vomiting</li>\n<li>Convulsions</li>\n<li>Lethargic or unconscious</li>\n<li>Stridor in a calm child</li>\n<li>Severe malnutrition</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9576","text":"A one-and-half-year-old ch​ild was brought to the ho​spital with a history of ​breathlessness, fever, co‌ugh, and cold. On examina​tion, respiratory rate wa​s 46/min, and there were ‌no chest retractions. Whi​ch is the most appropriat​e management?","raw_text":"A one-and-half-year-old child was brought to the hospital with a history of breathlessness, fever, cough, and cold. On examination, respiratory rate was 46/min, and there were no chest retractions. Which is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Give IV antibiotics and ob​serve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Give IV antibiotics and re​fer to higher centre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral antibiotics, warn of ​danger signs, and send ho​me","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No need of treatment only ​home remedies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral antibiotics, warn of danger signs, and send home","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b23ea6e413f945dcbd85f451ae09e57fx1280x711.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral antibiotics, warn of danger signs, and send home</strong></p><p>From the given clinical scenario, it can be easily inferred that the child has <strong>pneumonia</strong>. The treatment for pneumonia for a child between <strong>2 months and 5 years</strong> of age is <strong>oral amoxicillin</strong> and <strong>home care advice</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image gives the <strong>IMNCI</strong> classification for cough/difficulty in breathing in children.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0179","text":"A polysaccharide vaccine a​gainst Streptococcus pneu​moniae has 23 serotypes. ​In which of the following‌ patients is this vaccine​ most beneficial?","raw_text":"A polysaccharide vaccine against Streptococcus pneumoniae has 23 serotypes. In which of the following patients is this vaccine most beneficial?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A child &amp;l​t; 2 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with sickle cell​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with cystic fibr​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with recurrent o​titis media","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient with sickle cell disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient with sickle cell disease</strong></p><p>The polysaccharide vaccine against <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> <strong>(PPSV-23)</strong> is most beneficial for patients with <strong>sickle cell disease.</strong></p>\n<p>S. pneumoniae is an encapsulated organism that is detected by the spleen and is removed. However, in <strong>sickle cell disease,</strong> the sickled cells block the vessels in the spleen which results in <strong>splenic infarction. </strong>This <strong>hyposplenism</strong> results in an increased risk of pneumococcal infection. Hence, pneumococcal vaccine (PPSV-23) is given to these patients as a <strong>prophylactic measure</strong> to reduce this risk. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8283","text":"Which of the following tes​ts is used to determine t​he prevalence of TB infec​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is used to determine the prevalence of TB infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sputum ex​amination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mantou​x test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CB-NAA​T test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chest​ X-ray","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mantoux test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mantoux test</strong></p><p><strong>Mantoux or tuberculin testing</strong> is used to detect the <strong>prevalence of TB infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infection of tuberculosis does not always lead to disease. In latent infections, the Mantoux test can be positive without any clinical symptoms. Therefore, the prevalence of <strong>TB infection</strong> is detected by the <strong>Mantoux</strong> test.</p>\n<p>However, the prevalence of <strong>TB disease</strong> is confirmed by a <strong>sputum smear.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5965","text":"A 24-year-old patient has ​been given an intradermal​ injection of 0.1ml PPD (​5 tuberculin units) over ‌the forearm. After 72 hrs​, the site is examined as​ shown in the image below‌. What is the interpretat​ion of the result obtaine​d?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old patient has been given an intradermal injection of 0.1ml PPD (5 tuberculin units) over the forearm.   After 72 hrs, the site is examined as shown in the image below. What is the interpretation of the result obtained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculin test negative, ​suggesting immunity from ​TB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberculin test positive, ​suggesting an active infe​ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberculin test negative, ​suggesting a recent or pa​st infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberculin test positive, ​suggesting a recent or pa​st infection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tuberculin test positive, suggesting a recent or past infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2a2ea1d8206440f9b5b088b11bcc211e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tuberculin test positive, suggesting a recent or past infection</strong></p><p>The above image shows an <strong>induration</strong> of<strong> &gt;10 mm</strong> after 72 hrs of intradermal PPD injection suggesting a <strong>positive tuberculin</strong> <strong>test</strong>(TST),<strong> </strong>indicating <strong>recent</strong> or <strong>past infection.</strong> </p>\n<p>In TST, 0.1 ml of PPD (purified protein derivative) containing 5 TU (tuberculosis unit) is <strong>injected intradermally and induration is read after 48-72 hours.</strong> An induration of 10 mm or more is considered positive in patients from countries with a high prevalence of tuberculosis. Surrounding erythema is not considered. </p>\n<p>A positive tuberculin test indicates <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to <strong>tuberculoprotein,</strong> denoting either <strong>latent infection</strong> with tubercle bacilli, or <strong>BCG immunization, </strong>or<strong> recent</strong> or<strong> past infection</strong> with or without clinical disease. </p>\n<p>False positives may be seen in infections with related mycobacteria and false negatives in miliary tuberculosis, severely immunosuppressed states, etc. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0979","text":"Which of the following is ​the most appropriate meth​od to assess the incidenc​e of tuberculosis in the ‌community?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate method to assess the incidence of tuberculosis in the community?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Identify all positives to ​tuberculin test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sputum examination of symp​tomatic patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Identify new converter to ​tuberculin test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Screen all under 5 years c​hildren with tuberculin t​est","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Identify new converter to tuberculin test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Identify new converter to tuberculin test</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Identification of <strong>new converters </strong>to the tuberculin test is a marker of the <strong>incidence</strong> of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>. </span><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Identification of <strong>all positives </strong>to the tuberculin test is a marker of the <strong>prevalence</strong> of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>.</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Tuberculin conversion</strong> refers to the situation where an individual’s tuberculin skin test (TST) results in changes from negative (0–4 mm diameter induration) to positive (equal to or &gt;10 mm diameter induration) within a 24-month period. </span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>TST conversion</strong> is considered<strong> presumptive evidence </strong>of<strong> acquired latent tuberculosis</strong> infection (LTBI) and potential risk for progression to active tuberculosis disease.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8286","text":"Tuberculin testing is done​ at which anatomical site​?","raw_text":"Tuberculin testing is done at which anatomical site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsum of​ left arm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsum of​ right arm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral aspect of left for​earm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral aspect of right fo​rearm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventral aspect of left forearm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventral aspect of left forearm</strong></p><p><strong>Mantoux/ Tuberculin testing</strong> is carried out by injecting 1 TU of PPD in <strong>0.1 ml</strong> <strong>intradermally</strong> on the flexor <strong>(ventral) surface</strong> of the <strong>left forearm,</strong> midway between elbow and wrist.</p>\n<p>The result of the test is usually read after 48-96 hours, but <strong>reading</strong> it after <strong>72 hours</strong> is ideal.</p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>horizontal transverse diameter of induration is measured, based on which the following diagnosis can be inferred:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&lt; 6 mm –</strong> <strong>Negative</strong></li>\n<li>6–9 mm – <strong>Doubtful</strong></li>\n<li><strong>≥ 10 mm –</strong> <strong>Positive </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8284","text":"The approach shown in the ​image is a part of which ​of the following?","raw_text":"The approach shown in the image is a part of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joint TB Monitoring Missio​n 2017","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"National Strategic Plan fo​r TB, 2017 -2025","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Standards ​of TB Care","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"National AIDS Control Orga​nization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. National Strategic Plan for TB, 2017 -2025","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e93ff86cd8944b2d965098f524cbbb41.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. National Strategic Plan for TB, 2017 -2025</strong></p><p><strong>Detect – Treat – Prevent – Build</strong> approach for eliminating TB in India is part of the <strong>National Strategic Plan for TB</strong> (2017-2025). The components of this approach are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Detect - </strong>Early identification of<strong> presumptive TB cases</strong>, at the first point of care, be it private or public sectors, and <strong>prompt diagnosis</strong> using high sensitivity diagnostic tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Treat - </strong>Provide <strong>sustained, equitable access </strong>to high quality <strong>TB treatment</strong>, care and support services.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevent - </strong>Prevent the <strong>emergence of TB</strong> in susceptible populations.</li>\n<li><strong>Build - </strong>Undertake <strong>critical management </strong>reforms<strong>, </strong>restructuring of HR and financial norms, pathways for private sector participation, in order to<strong> improve efficiency, effectiveness.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8285","text":"Which of the following is ​the most rapid diagnostic​ test for pulmonary TB?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most rapid diagnostic test for pulmonary TB?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quantiferon ​TB gold plus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sputum m​icroscopy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radiometric ​BACTEC method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CB-​NAAT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sputum microscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sputum microscopy</strong></p><p>The <strong>most rapid</strong> diagnosis of pulmonary TB can be done by <strong>sputum microscopy,</strong> with results in <strong>30 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has the <strong>least sensitivity</strong> and <strong>specificity</strong>. Smears are stained by Ziel Neelson acid-fast stain.</p>\n<p><strong>CBNAAT </strong>gives results <strong>within 90 minutes. </strong>Cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification test is a Mycobacterium tuberculosis-specific automated, cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification assay, having fully integrated and automated amplification and detection using real-time PCR.</p>\n<p><strong>Quantiferon TB gold plus</strong> takes <strong>24 hours</strong> to give the result.<strong> </strong>The radiometric <strong>BACTEC</strong> method takes approximately<strong> 2-3 weeks. </strong><strong>Sputum culture</strong> takes approximately <strong>2 to 8 weeks.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7006","text":"BCG vaccine provides prote​ction against which of th​e following?","raw_text":"BCG vaccine provides protection against which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmon​ary TB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CNS and dis​seminated TB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary ​and CNS TB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary an​d skeletal TB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CNS and disseminated TB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CNS and disseminated TB</strong></p><p><strong>BCG</strong> vaccine provides protection against<strong> CNS and disseminated TB.</strong></p>\n<p>It does not prevent primary infection. More importantly, it does not prevent reactivation of latent pulmonary infection, the principal source of bacillary spread in the community. The impact of BCG vaccination on transmission of <em>mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> is therefore limited.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8288","text":"What is the next step in m​anagement of a TB smear p​ositive patient with no s​ignificant findings on a ‌chest X ray?","raw_text":"What is the next step in management of a TB smear positive patient with no significant findings on a chest X ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repeat sputum smear examin​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Star​t ATT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Do CB​-NAAT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Do sputu​m culture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Do CB-NAAT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Do CB-NAAT</strong></p><p>The next best step in a <strong>smear-positive</strong> patient with <strong>no findings</strong> on<strong> chest-xray</strong> is to perform a <strong>CB-NAAT</strong>. This patient is a microscopically confirmed case of tuberculosis (TB).</p>\n<p>According to the <strong>TB annual report</strong>, CBNAAT has been tried to be established in all centres. Ideally, <strong>drug sensitivity status </strong>via<strong> NAAT</strong> is to be done following a sputum smear <strong>before initiation of treatment</strong>. This helps in the early detection and treatment of <strong>drug-resistant TB</strong>. This has been integrated into the regular TB services to detect rifampicin resistance at the time of diagnosis. Depending on the <strong>sensitivity </strong>of the tubercle bacilli, <strong>appropriate ATT</strong> is started. </p>\n<p>If NAAT is not done and sputum is positive, patient is considered as Microbiologically positive with sensitivity status unknown. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0983","text":"You are posted in a TB cli​nic. You need to start an​ intensive phase (HRZE 75​/150/400/275) regimen for‌ a patient weighing 70 kg​s. How many tablets of th​e FDCs are to be taken by‌ him on a daily basis?","raw_text":"You are posted in a TB clinic. You need to start an intensive phase (HRZE 75/150/400/275) regimen for a patient weighing 70 kgs. How many tablets of the FDCs are to be taken by him on a daily basis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5</strong></p><p>An adult weighing <strong>65-75kg</strong> needs to take <strong>5 tablets of FDC</strong> (fixed-dose combinations)<strong> </strong>containing HRZE 75/150/400/275 in the intensive phase.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The new WHO guidelines suggest the number of FDC tablets to be taken daily according to <strong>five weight band</strong> categories.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 219px;\" width=\"421\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 124.297px;\">Weight category</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 280.703px;\">Number of tablets (FDCs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127.094px;\">\n<p>Intensive phase</p>\n<p>HRZE </p>\n<p>75/150/400/275</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.609px;\">\n<p>Continuation phase</p>\n<p>HRE </p>\n<p>75/150/275</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.297px;\">25-34 kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.094px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.609px;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.297px;\">35-49 kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.094px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.609px;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.297px;\">50-64 kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.094px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.609px;\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.297px;\">65-75 kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.094px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.609px;\">5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.297px;\">&gt;75 kg </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.094px;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.609px;\">6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: A <strong>TB patient kit</strong> contains the <strong>full course</strong> of <strong>treatment</strong> for a <strong>single patient</strong> and thus assures the TB patient that his or her medications will be available throughout treatment. The kit provides health workers with a container that has all the required drugs in the necessary strengths and quantities. This helps limit confusion and wastage and makes it easier to monitor the regularity of treatment; avoiding stock-outs also helps to maintain patient confidence in the health system.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8289","text":"According to NTEP, how wil​l you classify a TB patie​nt who had taken antitube​rcular drugs for only 3 w‌eeks in the past?","raw_text":"According to NTEP, how will you classify a TB patient who had taken antitubercular drugs for only 3 weeks in the past?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"New ​case","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Previously ​treated case","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment after loss to fo​llow-up","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurren​t TB case","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. New case","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. New case</strong></p><p>Under <strong>NTEP</strong>, a <strong>new case</strong> is defined as a <strong>TB</strong> patient who had <strong>never taken</strong> ATT previously <strong>or taken ATT</strong> for <strong>&lt;1 month.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7007","text":"Which of the following is ​the appropriate schedule ​for the treatment of mult​i-drug resistant tubercul‌osis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the appropriate schedule for the treatment of multi-drug resistant tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 drugs for 4-6 months fol​lowed by 4 drugs for 12 m​onths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 drugs for 4-6 months fol​lowed by 4 drugs for 5 mo​nths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 drugs for 4 months follo​wed by 6 drugs for 10-12 ​months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 drugs for 6 months follo​wed by 4 drugs for 12 mon​ths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7 drugs for 4-6 months followed by 4 drugs for 5 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7 drugs for 4-6 months followed by 4 drugs for 5 months</strong></p><p>The appropriate schedule for the treatment of multi-drug resistant tuberculosis<strong> (MDR-TB)</strong> is <strong>7 drugs</strong> for<strong> 4-6 months </strong>followed by<strong> 4 drugs</strong> for<strong> 5 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Pearl 1720 has been updated according to NTEP 2021 guidelines.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8290","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is not used in the man​agement of a patient with​ MDR-TB under NTEP?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not used in the management of a patient with MDR-TB under NTEP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levofl​oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P​AS","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethio​namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo​serine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PAS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PAS</strong></p><p><strong>PAS </strong>(para-aminosalicylic acid)<strong> </strong>is not included under the MDR-TB regimen. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8291","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding the durati​on for treatment of XDR T​B?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the duration for treatment of XDR TB?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intensive phase is for 6 t​o 9 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continuation phase is for ​12 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total duration is for 20 m​onths","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A combination of oral and ​injection drugs are used","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total duration is for 20 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total duration is for 20 months</strong></p><p><strong>All oral longer MDR-TB regimen</strong> is used for the treatment of <strong>XDR-TB</strong> patients for a duration of <strong>20 months</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 90px;\" width=\"489\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.906px;\"><strong>Regimen class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.094px;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 202.906px;\">All oral longer MDR TB regimen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.094px;\">Bdq(6 months) Lfx Lzd Cfz Cs for 18-20 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8292","text":"Which of the following is ​not a criteria for the be​daquiline conditional acc​ess programme under RNTCP‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a criteria for the bedaquiline conditional access programme under RNTCP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient below 5 years can ​be included","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Post menopausal females ca​n be included","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients with stable contr​olled arrhythmia can be i​ncluded","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pregnant patients are excl​uded","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patient below 5 years can be included","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patient below 5 years can be included</strong></p><p>Patients under 5 years are excluded from the bedaquiline conditional access programme<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>criteria</strong> to be met under the <strong>bedaquiline conditional access programme</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Children aged <strong>5 </strong>to <strong>&lt;18 years</strong> of age and weighing at least 15 kg (in consultation with a paediatrician)</li>\n<li>Females on<strong> non-hormone-based</strong><strong> contraceptive</strong> or <strong>post-menopausal females</strong> can be included.</li>\n<li>Patients with <strong>controlled stable cardiac arrhythmia</strong> can be considered after cardiac consultation.</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnant</strong> women to be <strong>excluded</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5817","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding TB is f​alse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding TB is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The Index TB guidelines ar​e used for extra pulmonar​y TB.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-pert MTB/RIF assay is an​ adjunct for diagnosis of​ lymph node TB.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Steroids may be used in pa​tients with pleural TB wi​thout HIV for 4 weeks.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Duration of ATT for TB men​ingitis is 9 months.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Steroids may be used in patients with pleural TB without HIV for 4 weeks.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Steroids may be used in patients with pleural TB without HIV for 4 weeks.</strong></p><p><strong>Steroids</strong> may be used in HIV-negative patients with <strong>TB meningitis and pericarditis</strong> for 4 weeks, but not for pleural TB.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Index TB</strong> guidelines for <strong>extrapulmonary TB</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Duration of ATT in TB meningitis is for 9 months and lymph node and abdominal TB is<strong> </strong>6 months.</li>\n<li>X-pert MTB/RIF assay may be used as an adjunct in the diagnosis of lymph node TB and TB meningitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroids</strong> may be used in HIV-negative patients with <strong>TB meningitis and pericarditis</strong> for 4 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0980","text":"Where is the National Tube​rculosis Institute locate​d?","raw_text":"Where is the National Tuberculosis Institute located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mum​bai","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bang​alore","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"De​lhi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyde​rabad","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bangalore","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bangalore</strong></p><p>National Tuberculosis Institute is located in <strong>Bangalore.</strong></p>\n<p>Important<strong> national centers for tuberculosis</strong> in India:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tuberculosis Research Center: Chennai</li>\n<li>National Tuberculosis Institute: Bangalore</li>\n<li>National Institute of Tuberculosis and Respiratory Diseases: Delhi</li>\n<li>JALMA Institute for Leprosy and Other Mycobacterial Diseases: Agra.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cSg_m7yq","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"15_viral_infections","title":"15.Viral Infections","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC2194","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega‌rding smallpox?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding smallpox?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palms and soles are not in‌volved","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rash is m‌onomorphic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scarring is common in reco‌very","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fever, headache and malais‌e appear 10-14 days after‌ infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Palms and soles are not involved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e0601e346ae423da731ad11501d577fx1280x908.9985895627644.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca1234e1693344afb1628e0adf0fe071x1280x1204.1748942172073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Palms and soles are not involved</strong></p><p>The <strong>palms</strong> and <strong>soles</strong> are frequently affected. The rash starts over the<strong> face</strong> and spreads<strong> centrifugally</strong> to the <strong>extremities</strong> (more affected)<strong> </strong>and the trunk.</p>\n<p>Smallpox is caused by the <strong>variola</strong> <strong>virus</strong> and was <strong>eradicated</strong> from the world in May 1980.</p>\n<p>Fever, headache, and malaise appear <strong>10-14 days </strong>after infection. Lesions are <strong>m</strong><strong>onomorphic</strong> (lesions within an area are at the same stage)<strong> </strong>and initially vesicular/pustular which then dry, scab over, and finally, heal. <strong>Skin scarring</strong> is common following recovery. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 766px; height: 729px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 7px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 7px;\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 7px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Smallpox</strong> (variola)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 7px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Chickenpox</strong> (varicella)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 15px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Incubation period</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>10-14 days</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>14–21 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Transmission</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 35px;\">\n<p>Respiratory viral shedding  </p>\n<p>Through contact with the fluid found in the patient's sores. </p>\n<p>Rarely airborne route.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 35px;\">\n<p>Aerosolized droplets from nasopharyngeal secretions.</p>\n<p>By direct cutaneous contact with vesicle fluid from skin lesions.</p>\n<p>Airborne transmission rarely</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Prodrome </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Fever and malaise for 2–4 days before the onset of rash</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Minimal to none</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 95px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Rash</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 95px;\">\n<p>Centrifugal spread</p>\n<p>Usually on palms and soles</p>\n<p>Axilla is usually free</p>\n<p>Rash on extensor surfaces</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 95px;\">\n<p>Centripetal distribution</p>\n<p>Seldom on palms and soles; appears in crops</p>\n<p>Axilla affected</p>\n<p>Rash on flexor surfaces</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 46px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Pock appearance</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Vesicle &gt; Pustule &gt;</p>\n<p>Umbilicated &gt; Scab</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Macule &gt; Papule &gt; Vesicles on erythematous</p>\n<p>base &gt; Pustule &gt; Scab</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Evolution of pocks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Synchronous (monomorphic)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Asynchronous</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Scab formation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>10–14 days after onset of rash</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>4–7 days after onset of rash</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21.3594px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 21.3594px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Scab separation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 21.3594px;\">\n<p>14–28 days after onset of rash</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 21.3594px;\">\n<p>Within 14 days after onset of rash</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.359px; height: 30px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Infectivity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284.609px; height: 30px;\">\n<p>From the onset of exanthem until all scabs separate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.031px; height: 30px;\">\n<p>From 1 day before rash until all vesicles become scabs. Scabs are non-infectious.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4945","text":"A 3-year-old child develop‌s the following lesions o‌ver the face. What is the‌ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child develops the following lesions over the face. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Var‌iola","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chick‌en pox","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vacc‌inia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Molluscum contagiosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2584192220e24a7c8f8fd3903b7b6dc7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ecb8144413c14f8c94c05e7676512ef7x1280x1207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Molluscum contagiosum</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>shiny, pearly white, umbilicated papules</strong> which are suggestive of<strong> molluscum contagiosum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The peak incidence is between<strong> 2-5 years. </strong>The incubation period is around 14 days-6 months.<strong> </strong>It is mainly caused by type-I molluscum contagiosum virus (<strong>MCV-1</strong>) in<strong> children </strong>and type-II (<strong>MCV‐2</strong>) in<strong> adults </strong>and<strong> HIV</strong> patients. New lesions develop along the line of trauma (pseudo-Koebner phenomenon). <strong>Sexually transmitted</strong> molluscum contagiosum may be present over the <strong>genital</strong> area.</p>\n<p>Microscopic examination of the lesions shows <strong>eosinophilic inclusions</strong> called <strong>Henderson-Patterson bodies.</strong> </p>\n<p>The condition is usually self-limiting but treatment is required for non-resolving symptomatic lesions. Destruction with cantharidin, trichloroacetic acid, diluted liquefied phenol, or surgical removal are a few options.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2195","text":"A wool farmer presents wit‌h a hemorrhagic lesion on‌ his left index finger fo‌r 3 days. On examination,‌ he is febrile and lympha‍denopathy is present. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A  wool farmer presents with a hemorrhagic lesion on his left index finger for 3 days. On examination, he is febrile and lymphadenopathy is present. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erysi‌peloid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ecthyma c‌ontagiosum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ecthyma g‌angrenosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ant‌hrax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ecthyma contagiosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a2f17a1c7544ccca6975ac9c7169c97.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ecthyma contagiosum</strong></p><p>The above image showing<strong> </strong>small<strong>, </strong>red<strong> </strong>hemorrhagic pustules with the given scenario is suggestive of<strong> orf </strong>or<strong> ecthyma contagiosum.</strong></p>\n<p>It is caused by the <strong>p</strong><strong>arapox virus</strong>. The commonly affected sites are <strong>hands </strong>and<strong> forearms. </strong>Lymphadenitis<strong>, </strong>lymphangitis<strong>, </strong>and fever<strong> </strong>are often present. </p>\n<p>It presents with a <strong>single lesion</strong>—a small, firm, red, or reddish-blue papule that develops<strong> </strong>after 5–6 days at the site of contact with an infected animal (mostly sheep and goats) and enlarges into a <strong>hemorrhagic pustule. </strong>The epidermis of an orf lesion shows ballooning degeneration of keratinocytes with eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is usually not necessary, and the lesions heal after 3-6 weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2623","text":"A 5-year-old boy had painf‌ul sores in the mouth wit‌h a rash over the hands a‌long with fever. Which of‌ these is the causative a‍gent?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy had painful sores in the mouth with a rash over the hands along with fever. Which of these is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pox ‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coxsack‌ievirus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HH‌V-7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parvo‌virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coxsackievirus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2565cc36e174a748dadae981c424572.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coxsackievirus</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>fever</strong>, along with <strong>painful sores</strong> in the <strong>mouth</strong> and <strong>small, tense vescicles</strong> with surrounding erythema on the <strong>palms and soles</strong> in a <strong>child</strong> point towards the diagnosis of <strong>hand-foot-mouth disease.</strong> The most common cause of which worldwide is <strong>coxackievirus A16</strong>. </p>\n<p>Spread is by <strong>droplets</strong> or <strong>faecal</strong> contamination and the incubation period is <strong>7 days. </strong>The fever is generally mild, and is followed by the appearance of lesions. The <strong>characteristic lesions</strong> are slightly oval, with a grey blister roof and a narrow rim of erythema. The <strong>oral lesions</strong> are aphthoid and are less painful than aphthous ulcers.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3299\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Only <strong>symptomatic </strong><strong>treatment</strong> is given. The lesions generally <strong>disappear in 7 - 10 days</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2197","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ts with the following pru‌ritic lesion 2 days after‌ developing a fever. What‌ is the likely diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents with the following pruritic lesion 2 days after developing a fever. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cold‌ sore","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whi‌tlow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cold sore","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a55832342fbe413ab41a121096f0f3a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cold sore</strong></p><p>The above image shows small, closely grouped vesicles on an inflamed base which are suggestive of <strong>h</strong><strong>erpes labialis </strong>or<strong> cold sore</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is caused by <strong>HSV-1 </strong>(less frequently HSV-2) and commonly affects the<strong> face</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lesions are <strong>painful</strong> and <strong>pruritic</strong>. Fever is often present which subsides in 3–5 days followed by the initial vesicular lesions becoming pustular and then crusted before <strong>healing </strong>in<strong> 7–10 days without scarring</strong>. Recurrent herpetic lesions are vesicular and ulcerative and tend to occur in the same region.</p>\n<p>Treatment:<strong> Oral </strong>acyclovir 200 mg and <strong>topical</strong> 5% <strong>a</strong><strong>cyclovir</strong> can be used for treatment. In case of <strong>resistance</strong> to acyclovir, <strong>foscarnet</strong> or <strong>cidofovir</strong> can be used.</p>\n<p>Option A: Varicella presents with<strong> </strong>papules and vesicles with a centripetal distribution associated with systemic symptoms of fever and malaise.</p>\n<p>Option C: Molluscum contagiosum<strong> </strong>presents with shiny, pearly white umbilicated papules.</p>\n<p>Option D: Whitlow<strong> </strong>presents with painful swelling of the fingertips and periungual area due to herpes infection by inoculation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2198","text":"A primigravida in labor pr‌esents to the emergency w‌ith fever, headache, and ‌itching around her genita‌ls. On examination, the c‍ervix is 7cm dilated and ‌small painful blisters wi‌th ulceration are seen ar​ound the vulva. Inguinal ​lymphadenopathy is presen​t. Choose the incorrect s​tatement.","raw_text":"A primigravida in labor presents to the emergency with fever, headache, and itching around her genitals. On examination,  the cervix is 7cm dilated and small painful blisters with ulceration are seen around the vulva. Inguinal lymphadenopathy is present. Choose the incorrect statement.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cesarean section will redu‌ce the chances of infecti‌on in the baby","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incase of neonatal transmi‌ssion, the mortality is l‌ow","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Commonly cau‌sed by HSV-2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prophylactic acyclovir sho‌uld be considered for her‌ neonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Incase of neonatal transmission, the mortality is low","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5996e52fa17445c585b6cfebd81c5849x1280x1608.5754583921016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Incase of neonatal transmission, the mortality is low</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>active primary genital herpes</strong> in the mother. In the case of neonatal transmission, the <strong>mortality</strong> is<strong> high.</strong></p>\n<p>Herpes simplex infection of a baby <strong>within 28 days of birth</strong> is called<strong> neonatal herpes. </strong><strong>HSV-2 </strong>is more commonly implicated than HSV-1. It is usually acquired vertically from the mother or postnatally by contact with non‐genital sites.</p>\n<p>Features of neonatal herpes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vesicular </strong>eruption</li>\n<li>Inflamed and ulcerated mucosae </li>\n<li>Disseminated disease:\n<ul>\n<li>Lethargy</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Respiratory distress</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly with hepatitis</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary genital herpes infection in the mother at the time of <strong>delivery</strong> is associated with a high risk of transmission to the baby during vaginal delivery. Hence <strong>cesarean</strong> <strong>section</strong> is indicated and <strong>prophylactic</strong> <strong>aciclovir</strong> should be considered for the neonate. </p>\n<p>Neonatal herpes is treated with high‐dose intravenous<strong> acyclovir</strong> followed by oral acyclovir for 6 months.</p>\n<p>The image below shows neonatal herpes. A scalp monitor was associated with infection in this infant.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4942","text":"Which of the following can‌ be used in a patient suf‌fering from acyclovir-res‌istant recurrent herpes l‌abialis?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used in a patient suffering from acyclovir-resistant recurrent herpes labialis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fosc‌arnet","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Valacy‌clovir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ganci‌clovir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Foscarnet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Foscarnet</strong></p><p>In case of<strong> resistance </strong>to acyclovir<strong>, foscarnet</strong> or<strong> cidofovir </strong>can be used to treat herpes. They<strong> </strong>act on <strong>v</strong><strong>iral</strong> <strong>DNA polymerases</strong> thus<strong> </strong>blocking DNA replication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2200","text":"An adolescent presents wit‌h the following lesions t‌hat started 2 days after ‌he developed a fever. Whe‌n is it safe for him to r‍esume school?","raw_text":"An adolescent presents with the following lesions that started 2 days after he developed a fever. When is it safe for him to resume school?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 days after appearance of‌ lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 days after appearance of‌ lesions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14 days after appearance o‌f fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After crusting of the lesi‌ons","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. After crusting of the lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bf8082901e646069d8c81cf06698faf.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. After crusting of the lesions</strong></p><p>The above image showing multiple <strong>vesicular</strong> lesions on the <strong>trunk</strong> is suggestive of <strong>chickenpox</strong> or varicella caused by the <strong>varicella-zoster</strong> virus. It is <strong>non-contagious</strong> after the lesions have<strong> crusted</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most infections occur before the age of 5 but can occur in older children and adults. The lesions develop in 3-5 crops and are <strong>polymorphic </strong>(present at different stages in each site).<strong> </strong>In 2–4 days, a dry crust forms leaving a shallow pink depression which, in the absence of secondary infection,<strong> heals without scarring. </strong></p>\n<p>Most common complication is <strong>secondary bacterial infection. </strong>Varicella confers<strong> lasting immunity </strong>and second attacks are uncommon. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4261","text":"Varicella-zoster virus rem‌ains dormant in the:","raw_text":"Varicella-zoster virus remains dormant in the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medulla ‌oblongata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal roo‌t ganglion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventra‌l root","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dorsal root ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dorsal root ganglion</strong></p><p>Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) is a human herpesvirus that causes varicella (<strong>chickenpox</strong>) as a primary infection and then remains in a latent form in the <strong>dorsal root ganglia</strong> for a variable period after which it can <strong>reactivate</strong> to cause herpes zoster (<strong>shingles</strong>). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2202","text":"What is correct regarding ‌the diagnosis based on th‌e given image?","raw_text":"What is correct regarding the diagnosis based on the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The lesions are not infect‌ious","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigeminal dermatome is mo‌st commonly affected","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior nerve roots are m‌ore commonly involved","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucous membranes within th‌e affected dermatomes are‌ involved","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mucous membranes within the affected dermatomes are involved","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2ce918eae0784e26ad3534e2fccfd767.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mucous membranes within the affected dermatomes are involved</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>unilateral</strong>, <strong>painful</strong>, <strong>grouped vesicles</strong> in a single <strong>dermatome</strong> suggestive of herpes zoster or shingles. The <strong>mucous membranes</strong> within the affected dermatomes are involved in shingles. </p>\n<p><br/>Herpes zoster/shingles is an acute cutaneous segmental eruption due to the reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus from <strong>dorsal root ganglia</strong>. An earlier infection with varicella is essential before zoster can occur. </p>\n<p><br/>The first symptom of zoster is usually pain, 1-3 days after which the lesions appear. Occasionally, the pain may not be followed by the eruption (‘zoster sine eruptione’). The patients are <strong>infectious</strong>, both from the virus in the lesions and nasopharynx. </p>\n<p><br/>The most commonly involved dermatomes in decreasing order of frequency are <strong>thoracic</strong> &gt; cervical &gt; <strong>trigeminal</strong> &gt; lumbosacral. The incidence of ophthalmic zoster increase with age. The posterior nerve roots and ganglia show inflammatory changes.</p>\n<p><br/>Herpes zoster ophthalmicus is a variant of zoster involving the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1). Herpes zoster oticus/Ramsay Hunt syndrome is another variant involving the geniculate ganglion and presents with ipsilateral facial nerve palsy, otalgia and vesicles over external ear. </p>\n<p><br/>Zoster is a <strong>self-limiting infection</strong> but it is painful and carries a risk of secondary infection and post-herpetic neuralgia. <strong>Analgesia</strong> and treatment of secondary infections are sufficient for mild infections.</p>\n<p><br/>Indications for antivirals in VZV (<strong>Acyclovir 800 mg</strong>):<br/>• Painful zoster infections in adults<br/>• Facial zoster at any age<br/>• Immunocompromised</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2204","text":"Post-herpetic neuralgia is‌ commonly associated with‌ zoster of which nerve?","raw_text":"Post-herpetic neuralgia is commonly associated with zoster of which nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dor‌sal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trige‌minal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cerv‌ical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumbo‌sacral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trigeminal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trigeminal</strong></p><p><strong>Post-herpetic neuralgia</strong> is most frequent when the<strong> trigeminal nerve</strong> is involved.</p>\n<p>The <strong>commonest </strong>and<strong> most intractable</strong> complication of zoster is <strong>post-herpetic neuralgia. </strong>It is generally defined as <strong>persistence </strong>or <strong>recurrence </strong>of<strong> pain</strong> more than 1-3 months after the onset of zoster. The <strong>pain</strong> may be a continuous <strong>burning</strong> pain with hyperaesthesia or a spasmodic <strong>shooting </strong>type or pruritic paraesthesia may also occur. Allodynia<strong> </strong>(pain caused by normally innocuous stimuli) is often the most distressing symptom and occurs in most people with post‐herpetic neuralgia.</p>\n<p>A tricyclic antidepressant such as <strong>amitriptyline </strong>or<strong> nortriptyline</strong> is useful for hyperaesthesia and constant burning pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2205","text":"A 43-year-old HIV-positive‌ male patient presented w‌ith the following finding‌. The plaque could not be‌ rubbed off. Which virus ‍is associated with this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old HIV-positive male patient presented with the following finding. The plaque could not be rubbed off. Which virus is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human papi‌llomavirus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes simp‌lex 1 virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epstein-Barr virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5caf164588a14e28bd13324dd19cb83d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epstein-Barr virus</strong></p><p>The above image showing a <strong>white</strong> <strong>corrugated</strong> <strong>plaque</strong> over the lateral border of the tongue in an <strong>HIV-infected</strong> patient which <strong>cannot</strong> be <strong>rubbed off</strong> is suggestive of <strong>oral</strong> <strong>hairy</strong> <strong>leukoplakia</strong>. It is due to the <strong>reactivation </strong>of<strong> Epstein-Barr Virus </strong>(EBV) infection. </p>\n<p>Other conditions associated with EBV infection are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glandular fever</li>\n<li>Rash triggered by ampicillin</li>\n<li>Burkitt's Lymphoma</li>\n<li>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma</li>\n<li>EBV‐associated lymphoproliferative disease</li>\n<li>Age‐related EBV‐associated B‐cell lymphoma</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2206","text":"Which is the most common e‌xanthematic fever in chil‌dren under the age of 2 y‌ears?","raw_text":"Which is the most common exanthematic fever in children under the age of 2 years?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ros‌eola","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Roseola","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/878c0ea0222d48af862960b4f0a92cfbx1280x1131.9605077574047.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Roseola</strong></p><p><strong>Roseola infantum</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>exanthem</strong>atic fever in children under the age of 2 years.</p>\n<p>Roseola infantum or <strong>e</strong><strong>xanthema subitum</strong> is an acute febrile illness with a <strong>maculopapular eruption</strong>. It is also called the <strong>6th disease</strong>. Causative organisms are <strong>HHV-6 </strong>and<strong> HHV-7</strong>. </p>\n<p>It begins with an abrupt onset of fever for 3–5 days followed by an eruption of <strong>discrete rose‐pink </strong>maculopapular rash on the neck and trunk which may later spread to other areas and then fade, leaving no scaling or pigmentation<strong>.</strong> The lesions may have surrounding pallor. <strong>Nagayama spots </strong>which are red papules over the palate are pathognomonic. <strong>Cervical </strong>and<strong> occipital</strong> lymph<strong> nodes</strong> are usually enlarged.</p>\n<p>Treatment is <strong>symptomatic</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2215","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct regarding Kaposi ‌varicelliform eruption?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct regarding Kaposi varicelliform eruption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Varicella like grouped les‌ions in Kaposi sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurs in an immunocompete‌nt host","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infection with HHV-8 resul‌ting in a cutaneous exant‌hem distributed centripet‌ally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maculopapular rash due to ‌cutaneous hypersensitivit‌y following Kaposi sarcom‌a virus infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Occurs in an immunocompetent host","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36cc20551fe34e49b38bf265e3e5c27ex389x257.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Occurs in an immunocompetent host</strong></p><p>Kaposi varicelliform eruption or <strong>eczema</strong> <strong>herpeticum</strong> is a widespread <strong>viral </strong><strong>skin</strong> <strong>infection</strong> that causes localized or mild <strong>vesicular</strong> eruptions in a patient with<strong> pre‐existing skin disease </strong>(most commonly in atopic dermatitis).</p>\n<p>The implicated viruses are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HSV-1 (most common)</li>\n<li>HSV‐2</li>\n<li>VZV</li>\n<li>Coxsackie A6 and A16 (eczema coxsackium)</li>\n<li>Vaccinia (eczema vaccinatum)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It presents with fever and <strong>vesicles</strong> that are widely <strong>disseminated </strong>and may become hemorrhagic. The skin is <strong>painful</strong> and generally erythematous. The fever subsides in 4 to 5 days and the pustules heal with crusting, leaving some <strong>permanent</strong> <strong>scarring</strong>.</p>\n<p>Oral or intravenous <strong>acyclovir</strong> is used for the treatment depending on the severity of the infection.</p>\n<p>The image below shows an ecxema herpitcum eruption in a child. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4453","text":"What is the most common ca‌usative organism for verr‌uca vulgaris?","raw_text":"What is the most common causative organism for verruca vulgaris?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human papillomavirus-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dad54a121ce3433f947e91396bdaed8fx871x496.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human papillomavirus-2</strong></p><p><strong>Verruca vulgaris </strong>or common wart is most commonly caused by <strong>human papillomavirus-2</strong> (HPV-2) but is also related to types 1, 4, 27, and 57.</p>\n<p>Common warts are usually<strong> asymptomatic. </strong>They show acanthosis, hyperkeratosis, and papillomatosis<strong> </strong>and present as <strong>firm papules </strong>with a rough horny surface. The most commonly affected sites are on the backs of the<strong> hands </strong>and<strong> fingers</strong>, and, in children on the <strong>knees</strong>. <strong>Pseudo-Koebner phenomenon</strong> may be observed. </p>\n<p>Treatment is usually with topical agents such as <strong>salicylic</strong> <strong>acid</strong>, caustics, glutaraldehyde, formalin, or with <strong>laser, cryotherapy, </strong>surgical removal. </p>\n<p><strong> </strong>verruca vulgaris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2222","text":"A 32-year-old man presents‌ to the dermatology clini‌c with the following pain‌ful skin lesion. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presents to the dermatology clinic with the following painful skin lesion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Verruco‌us wart","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fillifo‌rm wart","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plana‌r wart","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myrmec‌ia wart","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Myrmecia wart","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9942470759624d569fa059986b8dfce6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Myrmecia wart</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and image describe a sharply defined, painful, rough <strong>keratotic</strong> <strong>papule </strong>(sago grain appearance) seen on the <strong>pressure</strong> <strong>point</strong> of the sole. This is suggestive of a <strong>deep</strong> <strong>p</strong><strong>almoplantar </strong>wart or <strong>Myrmecia wart.</strong> It is usually caused by human papillomavirus-1 (<strong>HPV-1</strong>).</p>\n<p>The<strong> superficial</strong> form of plantar warts is called <strong>mosaic warts</strong> which are caused by <strong>HPV-2. </strong>They present as a plaque of closely grouped small warts and are usually <strong>painless</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Verrucous warts present as asymptomatic skin colored papules and plaques with rough surfaces.</p>\n<p>Option B: Filiform warts are finger-like projections mainly due to HPV-2.</p>\n<p>Option C: Planar warts are flat surfaced lesions and hence the name. They are mainly due to HPV‐3 and ‐10.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4894","text":"Which is the most common o‌rganism implicated in the‌ condition depicted below‌?","raw_text":"Which is the most common organism implicated in the condition depicted below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-10","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human papil‌lomavirus-11","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Human papillomavirus-6","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/415c3f254eec479a9b1074f6829a4aee.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Human papillomavirus-6</strong></p><p>The above image shows<strong> rough finger-like growth </strong>over the <strong>vulva</strong> which is suggestive of<strong> condyloma acuminata. </strong>It is caused by human papillomavirus<strong> (HPV-6 &gt; HPV-11).</strong></p>\n<p>It is also called genital/venereal warts and has<strong> high infectivity. </strong>It is acquired most commonly after sexual contact but may also occur non-sexually.</p>\n<p><strong>Imiquimod </strong>and <strong>p</strong><strong>odophyllotoxin </strong>are both the first-line drugs used in the treatment of genital warts.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4943","text":"For which of the following‌ conditions is the use of‌ imiquimod not approved?","raw_text":"For which of the following conditions is the use of imiquimod not approved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condyloma‌ acuminata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Actinic ‌keratosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>Topical immunomodulation with <strong>imiquimod </strong>cream is approved for the treatment of<strong> genital warts</strong>, superficial<strong> basal cell carcinoma</strong>,<strong> </strong>and<strong> actinic keratoses</strong>. It is not used for the treatment of squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Imiquimod is an FDA-approved drug that acts by stimulating <strong>t</strong><strong>oll-like receptors 7 </strong>and<strong> 8 </strong>of macrophages, monocytes, and dendritic cells and <strong>induces apoptosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2226","text":"The given image shows a tr‌eatment modality that emp‌loys cold thermal damage ‌to the skin. Identify the‌ incorrect statement rega‍rding this procedure.","raw_text":"The given image shows a treatment modality that employs cold thermal damage to the skin. Identify the incorrect statement regarding this procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The procedur‌e is painless","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is relatively safe in p‌regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liquid nitrogen is most fr‌equently employed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The treatment is physician‌ administered","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The procedure is painless","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b3c82b6b803a4545bc6dd4185a76583b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The procedure is painless</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>cryotherapy</strong> which is used for the treatment of certain conditions like warts. The main<strong> disadvantage</strong> of this procedure is<strong> pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cryotherapy is a <strong>physician-administered</strong> treatment modality and it uses dimethyl ether spray, carbon dioxide snow, or liquid nitrogen to produce cold thermal damage to the skin.</p>\n<p><strong>Liquid nitrogen</strong> produces the <strong>coldest freeze</strong> and is <strong>most commonly</strong> <strong>used</strong> in practice. It is applied using a cotton wool bud or a cryospray until a rim of iced tissue (seen as a white discoloration) develops around the lesion. The freeze is maintained for <strong>5–30 sec</strong>. Scarring is unlikely with freezing times under 30 secs.</p>\n<p><strong>Cryotherapy</strong> is considered relatively <strong>safer</strong> in<strong> pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4941","text":"A 2-year-old boy was broug‌ht by his mother with com‌plaints of low-grade feve‌r and the following rash.‌ What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy was brought by his mother with complaints of low-grade fever and the following rash. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythema i‌nfectiosum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exanthem‌a subitum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rub‌eola","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scarle‌t fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Erythema infectiosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64ae98dd6da943d3b9e66ab878bacef5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a131b809393f483c8672b0ba6ce94d98x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Erythema infectiosum</strong></p><p>The above image shows the characteristic <strong>'slapped cheek appearance'</strong> seen in <strong>e</strong><strong>rythema infectiosum</strong> caused by <strong>parvovirus B19.</strong></p>\n<p>It evolves in<strong> three phases:</strong></p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-roman;\">\n<li><strong>First</strong> phase - sudden onset diffuse and macular, asymptomatic erythema of the cheeks, (<strong>slapped cheek</strong>).<strong> </strong>Prodromal symptoms may or may not be present.</li>\n<li><strong>Second</strong> phase -  begins after 1–4 days, with discrete erythematous <strong>macules</strong> and <strong>papules</strong> on the <strong>proximal extremities</strong> and later the <strong>trunk.</strong></li>\n<li>Third phase - <strong>Recurring</strong> stage, the eruption is greatly reduced or invisible, only to recur after the patient is exposed to heat or sunlight, or in response to crying or exercise.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Only about 7% of children with erythema infectiosum have arthralgias, whereas <strong>80% of adults</strong> have <strong>joint involvement.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0208","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not present with maculo‌papular rash?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not present with maculopapular rash?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zoster (‌shingles)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dengue (during febrile pha‌se)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zoster (shingles)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b8cf21786ee240acb59e757cc47fe6e4x1280x1214.10437235543.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f54b0fc3caae40708583b2d4bf51e532x1280x1768.3497884344147.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zoster (shingles)</strong></p><p>A maculopapular rash is not seen in zoster (shingles).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shingles</strong> cause <strong>vesicular lesions</strong> that are grouped together and specific to a dermatome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 92px; width: 365px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72.8594px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type of rash</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72.8594px;\">Macule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.141px;\"><strong>Flat</strong> lesion with a well-circumscribed change in skin color &lt; 1 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72.8594px;\">Papule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.141px;\"><strong>Elevated solid</strong> skin lesion &lt; 1 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72.8594px;\">Vesicle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.141px;\">Small <strong>fluid</strong> containing blister </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Maculopapular</strong> rashes are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rubella</li>\n<li>Dengue (febrile phase)</li>\n<li>Measles</li>\n<li>Chikungunya (acute infectious phase)</li>\n<li>Erythema infectiosum</li>\n<li>Zika virus</li>\n<li>West Nile virus</li>\n<li>Infectious mononucleosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0681","text":"Identify the condition sho‌wn in the image below:","raw_text":"Identify the condition shown in the image below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes ‌labialis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Herpa‌ngina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impe‌tigo","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Herpes labialis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2f8cca1ff634f8d8525c1e82dee8a13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c88cc1c027b94c88bebfe0ea2c865f5ex1280x1116.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f9f42a8a39a41aab55fb517160c6e02x400x327.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73c5d4fd225b48b5b2f8e52cd3728e83x1280x1094.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Herpes labialis</strong></p><p>The above image shows multiple <strong>vesicles</strong> &amp; <strong>crusts</strong> clustered on an inflamed base around <strong>the lips </strong>which is characteristic of <strong>herpes labialis</strong> or <strong>cold sore</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is commonly caused by herpes simplex virus type 1(<strong>HSV-1</strong>) and rarely by herpes simplex virus type 2 (HSV-2). It begins with a prodrome of <strong>pain</strong>, <strong>burning</strong>, and <strong>tingling</strong> followed by the appearance of erythematous papules. The papules then become vesicular and may ulcerate. </p>\n<p>It is treated with <strong>oral acyclovir</strong> 200 mg and 5% <strong>topical</strong> <strong>acyclovir.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Herpangina or vesicular pharyngitis is characterized by the presence of <strong>ulcerating small vesicles</strong> on the <strong>oral fauces</strong> and the <strong>posterior pharyngeal wall. </strong>It is commonly caused by the coxsackie virus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Molluscum contagiosum is characterized by <strong>pearly white, wart-like</strong> <strong>umbilicated papules</strong>. It is caused by the pox virus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Impetigo is characterized by <strong>golden yellow</strong> or <strong>honey-coloured crusts</strong> that form after the rupture of pustulating vesicles. Non-bullous impetigo is caused by both S. aureus and streptococcus. Bullous impetigo is caused by S. aureus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lpJFcpam","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"15_eustachian_tube","title":"15.Eustachian Tube","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6240","text":"What is the length of the ​eustachian tube?","raw_text":"What is the length of the eustachian tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12​ mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24​ mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"36​ mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"48​ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 36 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40f1ea8559894b919900ff820962b74ax1280x1550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 36 mm</strong></p><p>The length of the <strong>eustachian tube</strong> is <strong>36 mm</strong> (Bony part: 12 mm and Cartilaginous part: 24 mm).</p>\n<p>It is directed <strong>forward, downward, and medially</strong> with an angle of <strong>45°</strong> to the horizontal axis.</p>\n<p>Note: The eustachian tube in infants is 18 mm, i.e. about half the size of an adult eustachian tube.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6242","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the eusta​chian tube?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the eustachian tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Also known as pharyngotymp​anic tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"36 mm i​n length","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Outer 1/3rd is bony and in​ner 2/3rd is cartilaginou​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Connects oropharynx with m​iddle ear","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Connects oropharynx with middle ear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/420769cf3b484ba1b175df35bd48fc58x1280x1550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Connects oropharynx with middle ear</strong></p><p>The <strong>eustachian tube</strong> also called the auditory or <strong>pharyngotympanic tube</strong> connects the <strong>nasopharynx with the tympanic cavity</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is 36 mm long in adults and is divided into two parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bony part</strong> - forms the <strong>outer one-third</strong> of the tube as it is located near the middle ear and is <strong>12 mm</strong> in size.</li>\n<li><strong>Fibrocartilaginous part</strong> - forms the <strong>inner two-third</strong> as it is located near the nasopharynx and is <strong>24 mm</strong> in size.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the <strong>middle ear</strong> it opens in the <strong>anterior wall</strong> and in the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> it opens 1-1.25 cm <strong>posterior to</strong> the posterior end of the<strong> inferior turbinate</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6243","text":"Which of the following is ​the narrowest part of the​ eustachian tube?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the narrowest part of the eustachian tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympan​ic end","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pharyng​eal end","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ist​hmus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infund​ibulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isthmus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67b478b238654abc92f4b7b3b1a3c1c4x1280x1550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isthmus</strong></p><p>The <strong>narrowest part</strong> of the eustachian tube is the <strong>isthmus</strong>. It is the junction of the bony and cartilaginous parts.</p>\n<p>Infundibulum is not a part of eustachian tube.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6244","text":"You are an intern asking a​ patient to perform a Val​salva manoeuvre for the r​elief of an ear block. Wh‌ich of the following stru‍cture helps in opening th‌e eustachian tube during ‌this manoeuvre?","raw_text":"You are an intern asking a patient to perform a Valsalva manoeuvre for the relief of an ear block. Which of the following structure helps in opening the eustachian tube during this manoeuvre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tensor ​tympani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tensor vel​i palatini","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Styloph​aryngeus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palatoph​aryngeus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tensor veli palatini","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da7f4d7707724742a0bf0e8901eae311x1280x1176.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tensor veli palatini</strong></p><p><strong>Tensor veli palatini</strong> helps in <strong>opening</strong> the <strong>E</strong><strong>ustachian tube</strong> during the Valsalva manoeuvre.</p>\n<p>It is closed at rest and opens intermittently during swallowing, yawning and sneezing.</p>\n<p>Opening of the tube</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The medial fibres of the <strong>tensor veli palatini</strong> are attached to the lateral lamina of the tube and contract to open the tubal lumen. It is also called the <strong>dilator tubae muscle.</strong></li>\n<li>Accessory muscles for opening are <strong>levator veli palatine &amp; salpingopharyngeus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Closure of the tube</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is mainly due to the <strong>elastic recoil of the cartilage</strong> aided by the high degree of elastin on the roof.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ostmann’s pad of fat</strong> helps to keep the tube closed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6246","text":"Which of the following sup​plies the tensor veli pal​atini muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following supplies the tensor veli palatini muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigemin​al nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pharynge​al plexus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossophary​ngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trigeminal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trigeminal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>mandibular branch</strong> of the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong> supplies the <strong>tensor veli palatini</strong> which opens the Eustachian tube.</p>\n<p>Muscles of the Eustachian tube:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Muscle</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Nerve supply</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Action</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Tensor veli palatine/ Dilator tubae</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Mandibular branch of trigeminal nerve</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Opens the tube</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Levator veli palatine</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Pharyngeal plexus</strong> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Opens &amp; lifts the tube</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Salpingopharyngeus</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Pharyngeal plexus</strong> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Opens the tube</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Sensory &amp; parasympathetic supply to ET: Glossopharyngeal Nerve</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6247","text":"A child presented with rec​urrent attacks of acute o​titis media. Which of the​ following properties of ‌the eustachian tube predi‍sposes to this?","raw_text":"A child presented with recurrent attacks of acute otitis media. Which of the following properties of the eustachian tube predisposes to this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More angulat​ed at isthmus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rigid c​artilage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dense elastin in the carti​lage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wider and s​horter tube","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wider and shorter tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wider and shorter tube</strong></p><p>The <strong>Eustachian tube</strong> in <strong>children is wider, shorter</strong> (13-18mm), and <strong>more horizontal</strong>. The cartilaginous part is <strong>flaccid due to less elastin. </strong></p>\n<p>The above differences make children more prone to retrograde reflux of nasopharyngeal secretions and middle ear infections. In infants along with the secretions, the milk also refluxes back<strong> </strong>while feeding in the supine position.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6255","text":"Which of the following is ​a cause for functional eu​stachian tube obstruction​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a cause for functional eustachian tube obstruction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoid h​ypertrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All​ergy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cleft ​palate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Deviated n​asal septum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cleft palate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cleft palate</strong></p><p><strong>Cleft palate</strong> is a cause for <strong>functional Eustachian tube (ET) obstruction. </strong></p>\n<p>Eustachian tube obstruction can be <strong>mechanical, functional </strong>or<strong> both</strong>. Mechanical obstruction can result from intrinsic causes such as <strong>inflammation</strong> or <strong>allergy</strong> or extrinsic causes such as <strong>tumour</strong> or <strong>adenoids</strong>. Functional obstruction is caused by collapse of the tube due to <strong>increased cartilage compliance</strong> or failure of active tubal opening mechanism due to <strong>poor function</strong> of <strong>tensor veli palatini</strong>. </p>\n<p>In the cleft palate, the obstruction is due to high elastin content at torus tubaris and the poor function of tensor veli palatini.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6248","text":"You are evaluating the pat​ency and function of the ​Eustachian tube in a pati​ent with a right earache ‌and ear block. Which of t‍he following test you wil‌l not do?","raw_text":"You are evaluating the patency and function of the Eustachian tube in a patient with a right earache and ear block. Which of the following test you will not do?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valsalva ​manoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stenge​r test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tympan​ometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toynbee’s​ manoeuvre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stenger test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stenger test</strong></p><p><strong>Stenger test</strong> is not used for the assessment of patency or function of the eustachian tube. It is used to detect <strong>malingering</strong> of hearing loss.</p>\n<p>Tests of Eustachian tube <strong>patency</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pneumatic otoscopy</li>\n<li>Nasopharyngoscopy</li>\n<li>Valsalva manoeuvre</li>\n<li>ET catheterization</li>\n<li>Politzer test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tests of Eustachian tube <strong>function</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Toynbee’s test</li>\n<li>Tympanometry</li>\n<li>Saccharine/dye tests in perforated ears</li>\n<li>Sonotubometry.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6249","text":"An ENT resident is perform​ing the following maneuve​r in a patient as shown i​n the image. Which of the‌ following is false regar‍ding the same?","raw_text":"An ENT resident is performing the following maneuver in a patient as shown in the image. Which of the following is false regarding the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It builds negative pressur​e in the nasopharynx","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tympanic membrane bulges o​ut","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Assesses eustachian tube p​atency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avoided in t​imes of URTI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It builds negative pressure in the nasopharynx","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5fd78bc1bd0d421dbf6960938e757448.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It builds negative pressure in the nasopharynx</strong></p><p>The given image depicts the <strong>Valsalva manoeuvre</strong> and it builds up <strong>positive pressure</strong> in the nasopharynx and not negative pressure.</p>\n<p>In this test, the patient is asked to close the mouth and nostrils and blow. This produces positive pressure within the nasopharynx which opens the patent eustachian tube and is confirmed by the <strong>tympanic membrane bulge</strong> via otoscopy.</p>\n<p>It is used to assess the <strong>Eustachian tube patency</strong> and to auto-inflate the middle ear.</p>\n<p>It is contraindicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Atrophic tympanic membrane</strong> as it might get ruptured.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>URTI</strong> as the infection can spread into the middle ear.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6250","text":"An otolaryngologist is per​forming the following pro​cedure in a patient. Whic​h of the following is tru‌e about this procedure?","raw_text":"An otolaryngologist is performing the following procedure in a patient. Which of the following is true about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uses Poli​tzer’s bag","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methylene blue dye is used​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Easy and sa​fe procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used to instill medication​s into middle ear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uses Politzer’s bag","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4fef31aedb5143b98648d32fbf819f39.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uses Politzer’s bag</strong></p><p>The procedure depicted in the image is <strong>Eustachian tube (ET) catheterization</strong> and it uses <strong>Politzer’s bag </strong>and an ET catheter.</p>\n<p>The catheter is passed along the nasal floor and manoeuvred into the ET opening and the bag is insufflated. The outward movement of the tympanic membrane is then noted with an otoscope.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>unsafe</strong> and outdated ET function test. It can lead to <strong>complications</strong> like TM rupture, scarring of ET, epistaxis, spread of nasal/pharyngeal infection to the middle ear.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>methylene blue dye test</strong>, the dye is instilled into the <strong>middle ear</strong> and the time taken by it to <strong>stain the pharynx</strong> is noted.</p>\n<p><strong>Politzer test</strong> is a <strong>non-invasive</strong> <strong>alternative</strong> for this and works on the same principle. It uses a nasal olive instead of a catheter and is useful in children who can’t perform the Valsalva or Toynbee test.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6251","text":"A 3-year-old child was bro​ught to the OPD with feve​r, earache, and poor feed​ing after an episode of U‌RTI. You suspect this as ‍acute suppurative otitis ‌media due to an eustachia‌n tube defect. Which is t​he most accurate test to ​detect this?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child was brought to the OPD with fever, earache, and poor feeding after an episode of URTI. You suspect this as acute suppurative otitis media due to an eustachian tube defect. Which is the most accurate test to detect this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valsalva​ maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toynbee ​maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tympan​ometry","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Politze​risation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tympanometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tympanometry</strong></p><p><strong>Tympanometry</strong> is the most accurate <strong>test </strong>to assess <strong>eustachian tube (ET) dysfunction</strong>. It is an objective measure of ET function and does not rely on patient cooperation.</p>\n<p>In this test, the movement of TM is visualized to the variation in external canal pressure. The disadvantage is middle ear pathologies can overshadow ET function.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6252","text":"A middle-aged retropositiv​e woman presented with co​mplaints of hearing her o​wn voice and breath sound‌s with ear fullness. She ‍has lost significant weig‌ht in the past 6 months. ‌What is the likely diagno​sis in this case?","raw_text":"A middle-aged retropositive woman presented with complaints of hearing her own voice and breath sounds with ear fullness. She has lost significant weight in the past 6 months. What is the likely diagnosis in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV-relate​d psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior canal dehiscence ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patulous eus​tachian tube","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blocked eus​tachian tube","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patulous eustachian tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patulous eustachian tube</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes <strong>autophony</strong> which is seen <strong>patulous eustachian tube</strong>.</p>\n<p>Autophony is a symptom where the patient <strong>hears his own voice</strong> and <strong>breath sounds</strong>. </p>\n<p>Patulous eustachian tube is usually idiopathic where the tube is abnormally patent. But it is also seen in <strong>rapid weight loss</strong>, pregnancy especially the third trimester and patients with multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes weight gain, administration of potassium iodide solution. In long-standing cases, specific treatment could include cauterization of tubes or insertion of the grommet.</p>\n<p>Option A: In HIV-related psychosis, there will be persecutory delusion, hallucination, and no ear fullness.</p>\n<p>Option B: The autophony of superior canal dehiscence syndrome usually involves hearing their own voice but <strong>not</strong> their <strong>breathing</strong> when compared with patients who have patulous eustachian tube.</p>\n<p>Option D: Patient with blocked eustachian tube does not present with autophony.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6253","text":"For a patient to get barot​raumatic otitis media, wh​at is the critical level ​of the atmospheric pressu‌re required?","raw_text":"For a patient to get barotraumatic otitis media, what is the critical level of the atmospheric pressure required?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"90 ​mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"100 ​mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 90 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 90 mmHg</strong></p><p>The <strong>critical level of the atmospheric pressure</strong> required to cause <strong>barotraumatic otitis media</strong> is <strong>90 mm Hg. </strong></p>\n<p>At this point, the atmospheric pressure is higher than the middle ear and it causes the eustachian tube blockage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6254","text":"Which of the following act​ivities is least likely t​o cause otitic barotrauma​?","raw_text":"Which of the following activities is least likely to cause otitic barotrauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pilot perfor​ming take-off","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Skydiver performing a jump​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep se​a diving","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient undergoing hyperba​ric oxygen therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pilot performing take-off","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pilot performing take-off</strong></p><p>A pilot performing take-off (i.e. rapid ascent in the air) is least likely to cause otitic barotrauma compared to other options.</p>\n<p>The usual causes for otitic barotrauma are</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rapid <strong>descent</strong> during air travel (skydiver)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Underwater <strong>diving</strong> (scuba diving)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Compression in a <strong>pressure chamber</strong> (hyperbaric oxygen therapy).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4632","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of otitic b​arotrauma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of otitic barotrauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conductive ​hearing loss","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyper​acusis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severe ​earache","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vertigo and sensorineural ​hearing loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperacusis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperacusis</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperacusis</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a feature of <strong>otitic barotrauma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Otitic barotrauma is a non-suppurative condition resulting from the failure of the eustachian tube to maintain middle ear pressure at ambient atmospheric level. It is usually caused by <strong>rapid descent</strong> during <strong>air flight</strong>, <strong>underwater diving</strong> or compression in the pressure chamber.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severe earache</strong>, deafness, and tinnitus are common complaints.</li>\n<li>The <strong>TM</strong> appears <strong>retracted</strong> and <strong>congested.</strong> It can get ruptured in severe cases.</li>\n<li>The <strong>middle ear</strong> may show <strong>air bubbles</strong> or hemorrhagic effusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Hearing loss</strong> is usually <strong>conductive.</strong></li>\n<li>Rarely, there is a rupture of labyrinthine membranes causing <strong>vertigo</strong> and <strong>sensorineural hearing loss.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FpSYvUUY","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"15_poisoning__general_considerations","title":"15.Poisoning  General Considerations","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0851","text":"What is the science dealin​g with toxins produced by‍ animals, plants, and mic​robes called?","raw_text":"What is the science dealing with toxins produced by animals, plants, and microbes called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxic​ology","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxo​logy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxin​ology","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ecotox​icology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Toxinology","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Toxinology</strong></p><p>The science dealing with <strong>toxins</strong> produced by animals, plants, and microbes is called <strong>toxinology</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Toxicology </strong>is the science dealing with <strong>properties</strong>, actions, toxicity, fatal dose, detection, and estimation of <strong>poisons.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0852","text":"Which of the following sub​stances are irritant pois‍ons? Sodium hydroxide Amm​onia Sulphuric acid Phosp‌horus Lead","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following substances are irritant poisons?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sodium hydroxide</li>\n<li>Ammonia</li>\n<li>Sulphuric acid</li>\n<li>Phosphorus</li>\n<li>Lead</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 a​nd 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Phosphorus</strong> and <strong>lead</strong> are inorganic<strong> irritant poisons.</strong></p>\n<p>Sodium hydroxide, ammonia, and sulphuric acid are all corrosive<strong> </strong>poisons.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3696","text":"Which of the following is ​considered as an ideal ho‍micidal poison?","raw_text":"Which of the following is considered as an ideal homicidal poison?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thal​lium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ars​enic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aco​nite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stryc​hnine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thallium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thallium</strong></p><p><strong>Thallium</strong> is considered an <strong>ideal</strong> <strong>homicidal poison</strong> because poisoning with thallium <strong>resembles natural death</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">When considering suicide with poison, the <strong>suicide</strong> victim prefers to terminate life as <strong>quickly</strong> as possible with the <strong>least agony. </strong>Whereas for an act of <strong>homicide </strong>with poison, the criminal would like to kill his victim <strong>without creating any suspicion</strong> in the mind of the victim or people around.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Following are the characteristics of ideal suicidal and homicidal poisons:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 356px; width: 481px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Characteristics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Suicidal </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Homicidal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 13px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Accessibility to the poison</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 13px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Easy and free</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 13px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Not particular</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 8px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 8px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Antidotes availability</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 8px;\">Nil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 8px;\">Nil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 24px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Clinical diagnosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 24px;\">Difficult</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 24px;\">Difficult</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 8px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 8px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Cost of the poison</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 8px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Cheap </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 8px;\">Not particular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 46px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Metabolism and excretion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 46px;\">Not particular  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 46px;\">Rapid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 46px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Onset of signs and symptoms</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 46px;\">Quick and fatal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 46px;\">Slow and fatal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 2px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Postmortem detection</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 2px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Difficult</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 2px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Difficult</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 6px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Signs and symptoms</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 6px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Nil/ few</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 6px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Resemble diseases</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 25px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Solubility in food/drinks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 25px;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 25px;\"> Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 25px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Examples</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; height: 25px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Opium, cyanide, barbiturates, organophosphorus compounds</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109px; height: 25px;\">Arsenic, aconite, thallium, etc</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Thallium</strong> and <strong>organic</strong> compounds of <strong>fluorine </strong>satisfy some of the criteria for an <strong>ideal</strong> homicidal poison.</p>\n<p>Arsenic and aconite are commonly used as homicidal poisons but are not considered ideal homicidal poisons. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: Though no single poison would fulfill all the characteristics mentioned in the table given above, a few examples mentioned are often considered. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3698","text":"A farmer is found guilty o​f leaving his pesticides ‍carelessly in the kitchen​. It caused his wife to u‌nknowingly consume pestic‌ides and suffer from pois‍oning. Which section of t‌he BNS would he be charge​d with? BNS- Bharatiya Ny​aya Sanhita","raw_text":"<p>A farmer is found guilty of leaving his pesticides carelessly in the kitchen. It caused his wife to unknowingly consume pesticides and suffer from poisoning. Which section of the BNS would he be charged with?</p>\n<p>BNS- Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS Section ​229 (IPC 193)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS Section ​286 (IPC 284)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BNS Section 106 (IPC 304A)​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BNS Section ​123 (IPC 328)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BNS Section 286 (IPC 284)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BNS Section 286 (IPC 284)</strong></p><p>He would be charged with<strong> Section 286 </strong>(IPC 284)<strong> </strong>of the <strong>Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita</strong>. This section deals with <strong>negligent conduct</strong> with respect to <strong>poisons.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>BNS Section 123 </strong>(IPC 328)<strong> </strong>deals with <strong>causing hurt through poison</strong>, with <strong>intent</strong> to commit an offense. In this case, the farmer had no intent of committing any offense and the poisoning was due to negligence. Hence BNS Section 123 does not apply here.</p>\n<p><strong>BNS Section 106 </strong>(IPC 304A) deals with <strong>causing death </strong>by act of <strong>negligence</strong>. In this case, the farmer's wife suffered poisoning, her death is not mentioned. Hence, BNS Section 106 does not apply here.</p>\n<p><strong>BNS Section 229</strong> (IPC 193) deals with punishment for <strong>false evidence</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3699","text":"A servant adds sleeping pi​lls to his master's food ‍with the intention of rob​bery. Under which section‌ of the BNS would he be c‌harged? BNS- Bharatiya Ny‍aya Sanhita","raw_text":"<p>A servant adds sleeping pills to his master's food with the intention of robbery. Under which section of the BNS would he be charged?</p>\n<p>BNS- Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BNS Section 106 (IPC 304A)​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BNS Section ​122 (IPC 334)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BNS Section ​123 (IPC 328)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"BNS Section ​117 (IPC 322)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. BNS Section 123 (IPC 328)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BNS Section 123 (IPC 328)</strong></p><p>This man would be charged with<strong> BNS Section 123 </strong>(IPC 328)<strong> </strong>which deals with <strong>administering poison/</strong> any stupefying, <strong>intoxicating</strong>, or unwholesome drug, with the intention of causing <strong>hurt </strong>or <strong>committing an offense</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>BNS Section 106 </strong>(IPC 304A) deals with causing <strong>death by negligence.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>BNS Section 122 </strong>(IPC 334)<strong> </strong>deals with voluntarily causing <strong>hurt / grievous hurt</strong> on sudden <strong>provocation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>BNS Section 117 (IPC 322) </strong>deals with voluntarily <strong>causing grievous hurt</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3707","text":"You are a registered medic​al practitioner working a‍t a PHC. A patient is bro​ught to you with a histor‌y of consuming poison in ‌a suicide attempt. Which ‍of the following statemen‌ts is true?","raw_text":"You are a registered medical practitioner working at a PHC. A patient is brought to you with a history of consuming poison in a suicide attempt. Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is mandatory to inform ​the police before startin‍g any treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is mandatory to registe​r a police complaint prio‍r to inpatient admission","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is mandatory to inform ​the police regardless of ‍whether suicidal, acciden​tal or homicidal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is mandatory to call th​e police to the hospital ‍prior to discharge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is mandatory to inform the police regardless of whether suicidal, accidental or homicidal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is mandatory to inform the police regardless of whether suicidal, accidental or homicidal</strong></p><p>All cases of <strong>Poisoning</strong>, whether suicidal, homicidal or accidental are categorised <strong>under Medicolegal cases.</strong></p>\n<p>In the given clinical scenario, the patient attempts to suicide by poisoning which is labelled as MLC(Medicolegal case) and warrants the need <strong>to inform the police</strong>. Considering the legal implications, there may be a biased history from relatives or friends or aquaintances; wherein it is always advisable to report each and every suspected case of poisoning to the police and the question of suicide/ homicide/ accident to be considered by the police. The treatment <strong>must not be witheld</strong> before informing the police.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3701","text":"Which of the following rou​tes of administration of ‍a poison leads to the mos​t rapid onset of action?","raw_text":"Which of the following routes of administration of a poison leads to the most rapid onset of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenou​s injection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Application to a mucosal s​urface","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhalation of gaseous form​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Application​ to a wound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhalation of gaseous form","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhalation of gaseous form</strong></p><p>The most rapid onset of action of poison is associated with <strong>inhalation</strong> in<strong> gaseous form/ vapors.</strong></p>\n<p>Routes of administration in the <strong>decreasing</strong> <strong>order</strong> of <strong>onset</strong> of <strong>action</strong> are: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inhalation (most rapid)</li>\n<li>Intravenous</li>\n<li>Intramuscular, subcutaneous, intradermal injection</li>\n<li>Application to a wound</li>\n<li>Application to a serous surface</li>\n<li>Application to a mucous surface</li>\n<li>Ingestion</li>\n<li>Application to unbroken skin</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0872","text":"A woman was drugged with a​ substance, which was sli‍pped into her coffee with​out her knowledge by a ma‌n at her workplace. The m‌an confessed that he trie‍d to make her notice him ‌by adding a love philter ​to her coffee. Which of t​he following would he hav‍e used?","raw_text":"A woman was drugged with a substance, which was slipped into her coffee without her knowledge by a man at her workplace. The man confessed that he tried to make her notice him by adding a love philter to her coffee. Which of the following would he have used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CNS dep​ressants","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Delir​iants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stupefyi​ng agents","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aphrod​isiacs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aphrodisiacs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aphrodisiacs</strong></p><p>A <strong>love philter</strong> is a magical potion that is supposed to make the person who receives the potion love the one who gave it. These are usually <strong>aphrodisiacs.</strong></p>\n<p>Some aphrodisiacs that are used as love philter are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cantharides</li>\n<li>Arsenic</li>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Opium</li>\n<li>Cocaine</li>\n<li>Cannabis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The use of a love philter might lead to <strong>accidental poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3723","text":"During a post-mortem exami​nation, the forensic expe‍rt reports a peculiar cho​colate-colored discolorat‌ion of the body. It did n‌ot blanch on pressure. Wh‍ich of the following stat‌ements is false regarding​ this scenario?","raw_text":"During a post-mortem examination, the forensic expert reports a peculiar chocolate-colored discoloration of the body. It did not blanch on pressure. Which of the following statements is false regarding this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The discolouration is due ​to formation of methemogl‍obin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This condition is associat​ed with poisoning by nitr‍ates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The discolouration is like​ly to disappear in 24-48 ‍hours after death","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The victim could have been​ saved by the timely use ‍of methylene blue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The discolouration is likely to disappear in 24-48 hours after death","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The discolouration is likely to disappear in 24-48 hours after death</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The chocolate-brown discoloration which does not blanch on pressure is suggestive of <strong>postmortem hypostasis/</strong> lividity. It usually develops within 4 hours and <strong>persists until putrefaction. </strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Chocolate-coloured</strong> post-mortem lividity is associated with <strong>nitrates, nitrites,</strong> aniline, bromates, chlorates<strong>. </strong>It is due to the formation of <strong>methemoglobin.</strong> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Toxicity from nitrates can be treated with <strong>methylene blue </strong>to reverse the formation of methemoglobin.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3703","text":"A deceased man is being ev​aluated for suspected poi‍soning. During an autopsy​, you get a peculiar garl‌icky smell from the nose ‌and mouth of the body. Wh‍ich of the following agen‌ts would you suspect?","raw_text":"A deceased man is being evaluated for suspected poisoning. During an autopsy, you get a peculiar garlicky smell from the nose and mouth of the body. Which of the following agents would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thallium​ sulphate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrogen​ sulphide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cya​nide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosp​horus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phosphorus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phosphorus</strong></p><p>This scenario is suggestive of <strong>phosphorus poisoning</strong> as it is associated with a garlic-like odor, usually around the mouth and nose of the victim. </p>\n<p>Other substances associated with a garlic-like odor are <strong>arsenic, thallium carbonate, </strong>and<strong> zinc phosphide</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3704","text":"A patient was brought in w​ith a history of consumpt‍ion of acid. His last mea​l was one hour ago. What ‌pattern of gastric mucosa‌l injury would you expect‍ to see in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient was brought in with a history of consumption of acid. His last meal was one hour ago. What pattern of gastric mucosal injury would you expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima​ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima​ge 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ima​ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ima​ge 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Image 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7ba775927e5e4e5a9d605b78a4e4548c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c9b0ee1531543b6bc5792796bf84731x1280x1355.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Image 2</strong></p><p>The area marked in <strong>image 2</strong> depicts the most likely pattern of gastric mucosal injury in this patient.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pathway</strong> that fluids (acid, alkali, water) take in a <strong>food-filled stomach</strong> is known as <strong>magenstrasse. </strong>It is present <strong>along the lesser curvature</strong> of the stomach. This is where the greatest damage to gastric mucosa takes place, in corrosive injury. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In an <strong>empty stomach, </strong>the maximum injury occurs to the <strong>lower</strong> half to <strong>two-thirds</strong> of the body, with <strong>sparing</strong> of the <strong>fundus</strong> (image 3). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4457","text":"Which of the following poi​sons cause bluish discolo‍ration of the stomach muc​osa on post mortem examin‌ation?","raw_text":"Which of the following poisons cause bluish discoloration of the stomach mucosa on post mortem examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxali​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium​ amytal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Son​eryl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ars​enic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sodium amytal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sodium amytal</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">In <strong>sodium amytal</strong> poisoning, the capsules stain the stomach mucosa and its contents, turquoise <strong>blue</strong>. Bluish discolouration of the stomach mucosa is also seen in <strong>copper sulphate</strong> poisoning.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The appearance of the gastric mucosa in specific poisons:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 448px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p><strong>Poison</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p><strong>Appearance of Gastric Mucosa</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>Arsenic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Red velvety mucosa</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>Sulphuric acid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Brown or black with erosion perforation and Carbonization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>Carbolic acid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Grey leathery and thickened mucosa</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>HNO3</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Yellow</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>HCN</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Pink</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>KCN</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Brick red to brown</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>Phosphorous</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Yellow or grey white</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">\n<p>Sodium amytal, Copper sulphate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">\n<p>Blue</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3705","text":"Which of the following is ​associated with phenomeno‍n of drug automatism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with phenomenon of drug automatism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dat​ura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lyserg​ic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alcohol</strong></p><p><strong>CNS depressants </strong>and <strong>a</strong><strong>lcohol </strong>are associated with the phenomenon of <strong>drug automatism</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Drug automatism </strong>develops due to the depressant effect of these drugs. It leads to the development of an 'automatism state', where the patient takes additional doses of the drug, without realizing it. This can lead to accidental poisoning. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6403","text":"Gastric lavage can be perf​ormed in case of poisonin‍g by:","raw_text":"Gastric lavage can be performed in case of poisoning by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulphur​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stryc​hnine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbol​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kerose​ne oil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carbolic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carbolic acid</strong></p><p>Gastric lavage can be performed in case of <strong>carbolic poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p>In carbolic acid poisoning, if the patient survives for 48 hrs, there will be carboluria followed by anuria. The victim will pass dark, smoky urine which soon turns olive green on standing due to oxidation of hydroquinone and pyrocatechol. </p>\n<p>It is a corrosive acid, however, it hardens the GI mucosa producing a <strong>leather bottle stomach</strong> and anesthetizes the nerve endings. This allows for<strong> gastric lavage. </strong></p>\n<p>Contraindications of gastric lavage:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corrosives poisoning (except carbolic acid)</li>\n<li>Convulsants/CNS stimulants</li>\n<li>Comatose patients</li>\n<li>Volatile poison</li>\n<li>Upper GI pathology (eg. esophageal varices)</li>\n<li>Hypothermic patient</li>\n<li>Severe heart disease</li>\n<li>Advanced pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sulphuric acid and kerosene are corrosives and strychnine is a convulsant. Hence gastric lavage is not done in these poisonings.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3708","text":"In which of the following ​patients is performing ga‍stric lavage an absolute ​contraindication?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is performing gastric lavage an absolute contraindication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with a history o​f kerosene consumption","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with a history o​f acetaminophen overdose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with a history o​f tricyclic antidepressan‍t overdose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with a history o​f opioid poisoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A patient with a history of kerosene consumption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A patient with a history of kerosene consumption</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric lavage</strong> is not done in a case of <strong>kerosene poisoning. </strong>It can cause aspiration and pneumonitis.</p>\n<p>Gastric lavage is rarely indicated as the procedure has <strong>numerous complications</strong>. It may be considered in cases where there is recent (<strong>&lt;1 hour</strong>) ingestion of life-threatening amounts of a toxin, for which there is no treatment once absorbed. </p>\n<p>With drugs that slow down gastrointestinal motility or cause pylorospasm (e.g., anticholinergics, opioids, salicylates), lavage can be done up to 24 hours.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3711","text":"You have been asked to adm​inister gastric lavage to‍ a patient with salicylat​e poisoning. Which of the‌ following positions is m‌ost appropriate for perfo‍rming this procedure?","raw_text":"You have been asked to administer gastric lavage to a patient with salicylate poisoning. Which of the following positions is most appropriate for performing this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right later​al position","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supine ​position","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left later​al position","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erect p​osition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left lateral position","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left lateral position</strong></p><p>The<strong> left lateral position</strong> is the <strong>ideal</strong> position for performing <strong>gastric lavage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Position the patient on the <strong>left side </strong>with the <strong>head down </strong>at an angle of 20 degrees<strong>.</strong> The patient's mouth should be at a lower level than the larynx, to <strong>prevent aspiration.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3710","text":"Which of the following tub​e is ideally used for per‍forming gastric lavage in​ patients with suspected ‌poisoning?","raw_text":"Which of the following tube is ideally used for performing gastric lavage in patients with suspected poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ryle'​s tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewald​ tube","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salem-s​ump tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sengstaken-B​lakemore tube","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ewald tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e87c02de4db445d1be739a8e20a1eccdx1279x1312.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ewald tube</strong></p><p>The tube used for performing <strong>gastric lavage</strong> is an <strong>Ewald tube. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>also known as Boa's tube, stomach tube, or orogastric lavage tube.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Gastric lavage is the administration and evacuation of <strong>small volumes</strong> of <strong>liquid</strong> through an orogastric tube to remove toxic substances within the stomach. In <strong>adults</strong>, small amounts of <strong>warm water</strong> or saline are administered and, via a siphoning action, removed again. In <strong>children</strong>, normal <strong>saline</strong> is used, as children are more at risk of developing hyponatremia if lavaged with water.</p>\n<p>In an unconscious patient, gastric lavage is performed after induction of general anesthesia with the patient intubated, to prevent aspiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3712","text":"A patient presents to the ​ER with suspected poisoni‍ng. Which of the followin​g is the next best step f‌or management in this cas‌e?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the ER with suspected poisoning. Which of the following is the next best step for management in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Induction of emesis by giv​ing ipecac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administrations of cathart​ics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perform stomach dilution w​ith normal saline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Administer ​the antidote","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Administer the antidote","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Administer the antidote</strong></p><p>The next best step in the <strong>management</strong> of the patient with suspected <strong>poisoning</strong> is to either administration an <strong>antidote </strong>or <strong>enhanced</strong> the <strong>elimination</strong> of the poison.</p>\n<p>The following modalities of gastrointestinal decontamination have been used in the past but are <strong>no longer</strong> routinely<strong> recommended:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emetics</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cathartics</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dilution </strong>was historically recommended following the ingestion of acidic or alkaline corrosives to decrease the concentration and prevent tissue damage.</li>\n<li><strong>Gastric lavage</strong> is rarely indicated and is associated with high rates of complications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3713","text":"A 20-year-old woman was ru​shed to the ER due to an ‍attempted suicide by inge​sting an unknown substanc‌e. Administration of acti‌vated charcoal will have ‍no effect if the patient ‌ingested which of the fol​lowing substances?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman was rushed to the ER due to an attempted suicide by ingesting an unknown substance. Administration of activated charcoal will have no effect if the patient ingested which of the following substances?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbit​urates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Le​ad","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salic​ylate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lead","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lead</strong></p><p><strong>Activated charcoal</strong> is <strong>not effective </strong>in case of poisoning with <strong>heavy metals </strong>like lead, corrosives, cyanide, hydrocarbons, and alcohol. </p>\n<p><strong>Indications </strong>for use of activated charcoal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ingestion within the previous 1 hour [except in cases of poisons that slow gastric emptying (anticholinergics) or after ingestion of a substance that leads to bezoar formation (salicylates), it may be given after one hour]</li>\n<li>The toxic substance should be known to be adsorbed by activated charcoal</li>\n<li>The benefits of administration should outweigh the risks</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications </strong>to use of activated charcoal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-toxic ingestion</li>\n<li>Toxin not adsorbed by activated charcoal</li>\n<li>Unprotected airway</li>\n<li>Possibility of upper GI perforation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3733","text":"How is multi-dose activate​d charcoal useful in case‍s of poisoning?","raw_text":"How is multi-dose activated charcoal useful in cases of poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrointestinal decontami​nation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enhanced elimination of to​xin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Administration of antidote​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutralisat​ion of toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enhanced elimination of toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enhanced elimination of toxin</strong></p><p><strong>Multidose activated charcoal (MDAC)</strong> is used for the <strong>enhanced elimination </strong>of a toxin.</p>\n<p>In some instances, patients may benefit from the repeated administration of activated charcoal. MDAC facilitates the passage of substances from plasma into the intestinal lumen, enhancing elimination. Currently, MDAC is indicated for only the following poisons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Theophylline</li>\n<li>Phenobarbital</li>\n<li>Salicylates</li>\n<li>Dapsone</li>\n<li>Quinine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The techniques used for general management of poisoning can be classified into</p>\n<table style=\"height: 425px; width: 598px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\"> Mechanism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 352px;\">Techniques</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">Gastrointestinal decontamination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 352px;\">\n<p>Orogastric lavage</p>\n<p>Activated charcoal</p>\n<p>Whole bowel irrigation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">Enhanced elimination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 352px;\">\n<p><strong>Multi-dose activated charcoal</strong></p>\n<p>Urinary alkalinization</p>\n<p>Extracorporeal removal (Hemodialysis, Hemoperfusion)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236px;\">Administration of antidote</td>\n<td style=\"width: 352px;\">\n<p>Mechanical antidotes</p>\n<p>Chemical antidotes</p>\n<p>Physiological antidotes</p>\n<p>Pharmacological antidotes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3714","text":"Dimercaprol is indicated a​s an antidote in patients‍ with which of the follow​ing poisoning?","raw_text":"Dimercaprol is indicated as an antidote in patients with which of the following poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ir​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thal​lium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ars​enic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"cop​per","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arsenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arsenic</strong></p><p><strong>Dimercaprol (BAL)</strong> is an antidote for <strong>gold, arsenic</strong> (except arsine), or <strong>acute mercury</strong> poisoning (except non-alkyl mercury). It is also an adjunct to edetate calcium disodium in <strong>acute lead</strong> poisoning.</p>\n<p>Dimercaprol has also been approved by the FDA to treat Wilson's disease but not acute copper poisoning. The use of dimercaprol in the management of poisoning for other heavy metals has not been validated.</p>\n<p>Note: Mnemonic: B.A.L. for M.A.L. (Mercury, arsenic, and lead).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3715","text":"A 35-year-old man presents​ with complaints of const‍ipation, abdominal cramps​, and tingling in the ext‌remities. His bloodwork s‌hows a lead level of 50 µ‍g/dL. What is the antidot‌e preferred for this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presents with complaints of constipation, abdominal cramps, and tingling in the extremities. His bloodwork shows a lead level of 50 µg/dL. What is the antidote preferred for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dimer​caprol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DM​SA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Penici​llamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DM​PS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. DMSA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. DMSA</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of lead poisoning. <strong>Calcium EDTA </strong>and <strong>DMSA (succimer) </strong>are the preferred <strong>antidotes</strong> for <strong>lead poisoning.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In a patient highly exposed to lead, chelation with oral DMSA (succimer) is recommended.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In case of acute poisoning with encephalopathy, IV or IM Calcium EDTA is given along with dimercaprol. Calcium EDTA redistributes lead to the brain and increases the risks of leading to encephalopathy, which is prevented by dimercaprol.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3716","text":"With regard to forced alka​line diuresis, which of t‍he following pairs is tru​e? Type of poison ingeste‌d Reagent used 1 Weak aci‌ds Acetazolamide 2 Weak b‍ases Acetazolamide 3 Weak‌ bases Sodium bicarbonate​ 4 Weak acids Sodium bica​rbonate","raw_text":"<p>With regard to forced alkaline diuresis, which of the following pairs is true?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 129px; width: 283px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Type of poison ingested</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Reagent used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Weak acids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Acetazolamide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Weak bases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Acetazolamide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Weak bases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Sodium bicarbonate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Weak acids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px;\">Sodium bicarbonate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Urinary <strong>alkalinization</strong> (forced alkaline diuresis) is most effective for <strong>weak acids,</strong> primarily eliminated by the renal tract. It is performed with <strong>sodium bicarbonate.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Acetazolamide</strong> should not be used to produce alkaline urine, because it may <strong>worsen toxicity</strong> by causing a concomitant <strong>systemic acidosis.</strong> It results in an increase in the amount of the unionized drug in the blood and enhanced tissue distribution. It may also compete with acidic drugs for tubular secretion and thereby inhibit their elimination.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Drugs most commonly treated with forced alkaline diuresis are <strong>salicylates </strong>and <strong>phenobarbital.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3718","text":"Why is forced acidic diure​sis not recommended as a ‍treatment in case of pois​oning?","raw_text":"Why is forced acidic diuresis not recommended as a treatment in case of poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can cause acute liver i​njury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause​ lung injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can cause cardiac arrhy​thmia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can cause rhabdomyolysi​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It can cause rhabdomyolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It can cause rhabdomyolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Forced acidic diuresis</strong> is <strong>not indicated </strong>in any scenario as its potential risks (such as <strong>rhabdomyolysis, </strong>acute renal injury)<strong> </strong>outweigh any potential benefits. </p>\n<p>Note: Though some books say that acidic diuresis is indicated in few poisoning, it is only theoretic. Forced acidic diuresis is never indicated due to the associated risks.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3720","text":"While considering hemoperf​usion, which of the follo‍wing properties of poison​ are not important? Low v‌olume of distribution Low‌ protein binding Low mole‍cular weight Bound by act‌ivated charcoal Low endog​enous clearance","raw_text":"<p>While considering hemoperfusion, which of the following properties of poison are not important?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Low volume of distribution</li>\n<li>Low protein binding</li>\n<li>Low molecular weight</li>\n<li>Bound by activated charcoal</li>\n<li>Low endogenous clearance</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 a​nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Hemoperfusion</strong> uses <strong>adsorbents</strong> like a charcoal filter, which comes into direct contact with blood, partially overcoming molecular weight and protein-binding limitations.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Properties of toxins to be considered for hemodialysis and hemoperfusion:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 120px; width: 442px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Hemodialysis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><strong>Hemoperfusion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Toxin requirements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Low volume of distribution</li>\n<li>Low protein binding</li>\n<li>Low endogenous clearance</li>\n<li>Low molecular weight</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Low volume of distribution</li>\n<li>Low endogenous clearance</li>\n<li>Bound by activated charcoal</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99px;\">Indications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Salicylates</li>\n<li>Lithium</li>\n<li>Alcohols</li>\n<li>Metformin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Theophylline</li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8msXtFYC","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"15_diabetes_mellitus__complications_and_their_management","title":"15.Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MB7066","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a feature of diabetic​ ketoacidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of diabetic ketoacidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low seru‍m sodium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High an‍ion gap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum pH i‍s &amp;lt; 7.4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum bicarbonate &amp;gt; 15 ‍meq/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serum bicarbonate > 15 meq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serum bicarbonate &gt; 15 meq/L</strong></p><p>In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), serum <strong>bicarbonate</strong> is usually &lt;<strong> 15. </strong>Serum HCO3 &gt; 15 meq/L is suggestive of alcoholic ketoacidosis. </p>\n<p>DKA is an <strong>acute</strong> complication of <strong>type 1 </strong>diabetes mellitus. It is usually precipitated by absolute or relative <strong>insulin</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong> caused by increased metabolic demand (infections) or insulin noncompliance.</p>\n<p>The common symptoms include nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain. The common signs include <strong>Kussmaul</strong> breathing, <strong>f</strong><strong>ruity odor</strong> of the <strong>breath,</strong> dehydration, tachycardia, hypotension, and delirium.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis of <strong>DKA</strong> is made based on the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperglycemia <strong>&gt; 250 mg/dl</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ketosis</strong> or ketones in urine</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis with <strong>pH &lt; 7.3</strong></li>\n<li><strong>HCO3 &lt; 15-18</strong> mEq/L</li>\n<li><strong>High anion gap</strong> due to ketoacids and lactic acidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hyponatremia</strong> is seen as a result of <strong>hyperglycemia</strong> (osmotic shift causes dilutional hyponatremia). Normal sodium levels on admission indicate severe dehydration. Despite a total-body potassium deficit, the serum <strong>Potassium</strong> at presentation may be mildly elevated, secondary to the acidosis and volume depletion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7069","text":"A 19-year-old woman with a‍ history of type 1 diabet​es is brought to the ER w​ith confusion, vomiting, ​and abdominal pain. Inves‍tigations show blood gluc‍ose of 520 mg/dl and urin‌e positive for ketone bod‍ies. Which of the followi‍ng is the drug of choice?‌","raw_text":"A 19-year-old woman with a history of type 1 diabetes is brought to the ER with confusion, vomiting, and abdominal pain. Investigations show blood glucose of 520 mg/dl and urine positive for ketone bodies. Which of the following is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regular‍ insulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lente ‍insulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin ‍glargine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"70/30 ‍insulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Regular insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Regular insulin</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for controlling <strong>hyperglycemia</strong> in a patient with <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis</strong> (DKA) is <strong>short-acting insulin,</strong> preferably <strong>regular insulin.</strong></p>\n<p>Intermediate (such as lente insulin and NPH), long-acting insulins, and oral hypoglycemic agents are never used in the initial management of DKA. </p>\n<p>The mainstay of treatment is the administration of <strong>normal saline</strong> followed by <strong>infusion</strong> of short-acting <strong>insulin</strong>. The precipitating event must be identified and treated. Careful <strong>monitoring</strong> of <strong>K<sup>+</sup> levels</strong> and replacement in the later stages is required, as insulin therapy causes hypokalemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7072","text":"Which of the following is ‍the leading cause of deat​h in children with diabet​ic ketoacidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the leading cause of death in children with diabetic ketoacidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute respiratory distress‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac a‍rrhythmia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebra‍l edema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute kid‍ney injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cerebral edema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cerebral edema</strong></p><p><strong>Cerebral edema</strong> is the leading cause of <strong>mortality</strong> in children with <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>osmolar gradient</strong> generated due to the high blood glucose level results in <strong>water shift</strong> from the intracellular fluid (ICF) to the extracellular fluid (ECF) space and leads to the <strong>contraction</strong> of <strong>cell volume</strong>. Correction with insulin and intravenous fluids can result in a rapid reduction in effective osmolarity, reversal of the fluid shift, and result in cerebral edema.</p>\n<p>Complications of diabetic ketoacidosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cerebral edema – especially in children</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac arrhythmia</strong> - due to electrolyte imbalance</li>\n<li>Acute respiratory distress syndrome (<strong>ARDS</strong>)</li>\n<li>Venous thrombosis</li>\n<li>Acute gastric dilation</li>\n<li><strong>Acute kidney injury </strong></li>\n<li>Intestinal necrosis</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Mucormycosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7073","text":"A patient with diabetic ke‍toacidosis has severe abd​ominal pain. Which of the​ following is the recomme​nded test to rule out acu‍te pancreatitis?","raw_text":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has severe abdominal pain. Which of the following is the recommended test to rule out acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lip‍ase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Elas‍tase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amy‍lase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tryps‍inogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lipase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lipase</strong></p><p>In diabetic ketoacidosis (<strong>DKA</strong>), severe abdominal pain can mimic <strong>acute pancreatitis.</strong> Serum<strong> lipase</strong> is the best test to rule out this condition. It must then be confirmed by imaging (USG or CT abdomen).</p>\n<p>Serum <strong>amylase</strong> is an <strong>unreliable</strong> indicator as DKA<strong> </strong>is associated with hyperamylasemia of <strong>salivary</strong> origin.</p>\n<p>Serum and urine trypsinogen may be elevated in early pancreatitis but are not used for diagnosis. Fecal elastase is elevated in chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7076","text":"A 15-year old boy presents‍ with persistent vomiting​ for one day. Laboratory ​investigations reveal the​ following. What is the b‍est sequence of managemen‍t? Random blood glucose: ‌400 mg/dl Serum sodium: 1‍33 mEq/L Serum potassium:‍ 3 mEq/L Urine ketone bod‌ies: 4+","raw_text":"<p>A 15-year old boy presents with persistent vomiting for one day. Laboratory investigations reveal the following. What is the best sequence of management?</p>\n<p>Random blood glucose: 400 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Serum sodium: 133 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Serum potassium: 3 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Urine ketone bodies: 4+</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start IV fluids, administe‍r IV regular insulin, and​ then correct hypokalemia​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Start IV fluids, administe‍r subcutaneous bolus of i​nsulin and correct hypoka​lemia concurrently","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Start IV fluids and correc‍t hypokalemia first, then​ administer IV regular in​sulin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Administer IV regular insu‍lin with KCl first , then​ start IV fluids once the​ K+ level is corrected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Start IV fluids and correct hypokalemia first, then administer IV regular insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d57dbb1b28fa4a118652e49c9db3ad8cx1280x2461.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Start IV fluids and correct hypokalemia first, then administer IV regular insulin</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). On confirming the diagnosis, <strong>IV fluids</strong> must be started. If the initial serum <strong>potassium</strong> <strong>&lt; 3.3 meq/L</strong>, insulin should be administered only after the <strong>correction </strong>of hypokalemia.</p>\n<p>This is because treatment with <strong>insulin</strong> and fluids can <strong>worsen hypokalemia </strong>by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Insulin</strong> causes the shift of K<sup>+</sup> ions into cells</li>\n<li><strong>Resolution</strong> of the <strong>acidosis</strong> also promotes K<sup>+</sup> entry into cells</li>\n<li><strong>Urinary loss</strong> of K<sup>+</sup> salts of organic acids</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7079","text":"Distinguishing features be‍tween hyperglycemic hyper​osmolar state and diabeti​c ketoacidosis include al​l of the following except‍:","raw_text":"Distinguishing features between hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state and diabetic ketoacidosis include all  of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ket‍osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anio‍n gap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot‍ension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arteri‍al pCO2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Hypotension </strong>and<strong> tachycardia</strong> are <strong>common features</strong> of both hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state (HHS) and diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).</p>\n<p>In DKA, insulin deficiency and an increase in counter-regulatory hormones lead to shunting of free fatty acids to form ketone bodies. The ketoacids increase the anion gap and cause low pH. In <strong>HHS</strong>, there is still some insulin available. Hence, there are <strong>no ketones</strong> formed, and the patient has <strong>normal pH</strong> and <strong>pCO2</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7078","text":"An elderly man is brought ‍to the hospital with conf​usion and drowsiness. His​ son says that he has bee​n urinating more frequent‍ly than normal and eating‍ very little over the pas‌t 1 week. Capillary blood‍ glucose is 800 mg/dl. Wh‍ich of the following woul‌d not be seen in this pat‌ient?","raw_text":"An elderly man is brought to the hospital with confusion and drowsiness. His son says that he has been urinating more frequently than normal and eating very little over the past 1 week. Capillary blood glucose is 800 mg/dl.  Which of the following would not be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum osmolality &amp;gt; 350 ‍mOsm/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norm‍al pH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kussmaul ‍breathing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prerenal‍ azotemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kussmaul breathing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kussmaul breathing</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state </strong>(HHS). Kussmaul's breathing is not a feature of HHS.</p>\n<p>HHS is commonly seen in <strong>older</strong> adults suffering from <strong>type 2 </strong>diabetes mellitus. Patients can present with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polyuria</strong></li>\n<li>Polydipsia</li>\n<li>Lethargy </li>\n<li><strong>Altered sensorium</strong>, coma</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Severe dehydration</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following lab values are seen: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood glucose of <strong>600-1200</strong> mg/dl</li>\n<li>Serum <strong>osmolality</strong> &gt; <strong>350</strong> mOsm/L</li>\n<li><strong>Prerenal azotemia</strong> due to dehydration</li>\n<li>Pseudohyponatremia may be seen due to hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Normal pH and ketones</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mainstay of management is <strong>fluid</strong> replacement, IV regular <strong>insulin</strong>, along with <strong>potassium</strong> replacement.</p>\n<p><strong>Heparin</strong> can be given <strong>prophylactically</strong> as volume contraction can increase the <strong>risk of venous thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7081","text":"What is the most common pa‍ttern of dyslipidemia ass​ociated with diabetes mel​litus?","raw_text":"What is the most common pattern of dyslipidemia associated with diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased triglycerides an‍d decreased HDL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased LDL and decrease‍d HDL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased VLDL and decreas‍ed HDL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased triglycerides an‍d increased VLDL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased triglycerides and decreased HDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased triglycerides and decreased HDL</strong></p><p><strong>Dyslipidemia </strong>commonly occurs as a complication of <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>. The most common pattern is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased triglyceride</strong> levels - due to increased glucose and free fatty acids, decreased lipolysis of VLDL.</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased HDL</strong> levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Though diabetes does not increase LDL levels, there is <strong>increased atherogenicity</strong> as the existing LDL molecules are more easily glycated and susceptible to oxidation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7083","text":"Dyslipidemia is more commo‍nly associated with which​ of the following?","raw_text":"Dyslipidemia is more commonly associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 2 diabe‍tes mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type 1 diabe‍tes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maturity onset diabetes of‍ the young","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gestational diabetes melli‍tus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 2 diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p><strong>Dyslipidemia</strong> is more commonly associated with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is less common in type 1 and gestational diabetes. Maturity onset diabetes of the young (MODY) is not associated with dyslipidemia.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Saroglitazar</strong> is a more advanced drug that <strong>reduces</strong> blood sugar and <strong>lipid levels</strong> in diabetic patients. It acts on PPAR alpha and gamma receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7087","text":"You have started a 40-year‍-old man on treatment for​ type 2 diabetes mellitus​ and dyslipidemia. Which ​of the following is not a‍n appropriate target for ‍this patient?","raw_text":"You have started a 40-year-old man on treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus and dyslipidemia. Which of the following is not an appropriate target for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbA1c &amp;‍lt; 7 %","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood pressure &amp;lt; 130 / ‍80 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triglycerides &amp;lt; 150 mg/‍dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Postprandial plasma glucos‍e &amp;lt; 200 mg/dL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Postprandial plasma glucose < 200 mg/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Postprandial plasma glucose &lt; 200 mg/dL</strong></p><p>According to the ADA guidelines, a treatment goal of<strong> postprandial</strong> plasma glucose <strong>&lt; 180</strong> mg/dL must be followed in adults with diabetes.</p>\n<p>In <strong>younger</strong> individuals or those with other cardiovascular risk factors, the target blood pressure is <strong>&lt; 130/80</strong> <strong>mm Hg</strong>. Although not a treatment goal, the ADA also recommends triglyceride levels be maintained at &lt; 150 mg/dl.</p>\n<p>Note: Saroglitazar is a more advanced drug that reduces blood sugar and lipid levels in diabetic patients. It acts on PPAR alpha and gamma receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7091","text":"A middle-aged patient with‍ type 2 diabetes is diagn​osed with essential hyper​tension. What is the anti​hypertensive drug of choi‍ce?","raw_text":"A middle-aged patient with type 2 diabetes is diagnosed with essential hypertension. What is the antihypertensive drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Losa‍rtan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrochlo‍rothiazide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aten‍olol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amlod‍ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Losartan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Losartan</strong></p><p>The drug of choice in this diabetic patient with hypertension is <strong>losartan.</strong></p>\n<p>Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (<strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>) or<strong> </strong>angiotensin receptor blockers (<strong>ARB</strong>) are the first-line drugs in diabetic hypertensives. Among ARBs, the drug of choice is <strong>telmisartan</strong> (acts on PPAR receptor). </p>\n<p>They reduce both macrovascular and microvascular complications and have a <strong>nephroprotective</strong> effect. However, they are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>chronic kidney</strong> <strong>disease </strong>as they can cause a further decline in GFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7125","text":"Which of the following is ‍the earliest manifestatio​n of diabetic nephropathy​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest manifestation of diabetic nephropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microalb‍uminuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased glomerular filtr‍ation rate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased glomerular filtr‍ation rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased‍ urine PCR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased glomerular filtration rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10dc64a62ec7458781b3d41028a48f9ex1280x1772.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased glomerular filtration rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest </strong>manifestation of diabetic nephropathy is an <strong>increase</strong> in the<strong> </strong>glomerular filtration rate (<strong>GFR</strong>).</p>\n<p>It occurs as a result of glomerular hyperperfusion and renal hypertrophy. The following sequence of changes are seen in diabetic nephropathy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased GFR </strong></li>\n<li>Incipient diabetic nephropathy - <strong>microalbuminuria</strong> (30–300 mg/day albumin)\n<ul>\n<li>Diagnostic marker to detect early diabetic nephropathy</li>\n<li>Indicator of long-term cardiovascular disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Overt diabetic nephropathy - <strong>macroalbuminuria</strong> ( &gt;300 mg/day albumin)</li>\n<li><strong>End-stage renal disease</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7127","text":"A 15-year-old boy has been‍ on treatment for type 1 ​diabetes mellitus since t​he age of 10 years. His p​hysician decides to begin‍ screening for diabetic n‍ephropathy. Which of the ‌following is not always a‍ reliable indicator of th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy has been on treatment for type 1 diabetes mellitus since the age of 10 years. His physician decides to begin screening for diabetic nephropathy. Which of the following is not always a reliable indicator of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long duratio‍n of diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Albuminuria of 30-300 mg/d‍ay","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of diabetic retin‍opathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced glomerular filtrat‍ion rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Albuminuria of 30-300 mg/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Albuminuria of 30-300 mg/day</strong></p><p>Albumin of 30-300 mg/day refers to <strong>microalbuminuria</strong>. It can be<strong> unreliable </strong>in the<strong> </strong>diagnosis of <strong>diabetic</strong> <strong>nephropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is not required to diagnose nephropathy as it is very variable. <strong>Non-albuminuric</strong> diabetic nephropathy may occur in some patients and <strong>regression</strong> of albuminuria may be seen in type 1 diabetics.</p>\n<p>Diabetic nephropathy can be diagnosed if the following are satisfied: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduced</strong> <strong>GFR</strong> (&lt; 60 mL/min/1.73 m<sup>2) </sup>with or without persistent albuminuria (&gt; 30 mg/day or urine PCR &gt; 30 mg/g)</li>\n<li>Long duration of diabetes or established diabetic retinopathy</li>\n<li>Other etiologies are ruled out</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Long duration</strong> in<strong> type 1</strong> diabetes refers to <strong>5 years</strong> after diagnosis. On the other hand, patients with <strong>type 2</strong> diabetes are exposed to hyperglycemia and prediabetes for a long time even before diagnosis. Hence, at the <strong>time of diagnosis</strong>, patients with type 2 diabetes are considered to have had the disorder for a duration sufficiently long to produce kidney disease.</p>\n<p>However, a clinical diagnosis of diabetic nephropathy can be made in patients with established <strong>retinopathy</strong>, even if the duration of diabetes is short.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7128","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about diabetic ne​phropathy is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about diabetic nephropathy is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreatic transplantation‍ can improve proteinuria ​at an early stage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Strict glycemic control is‍ unlikely to prevent micr​oalbuminuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dietary protein restrictio‍n can benefit diabetic pa​tients with albuminuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microalbuminuria is not an‍ indicator of long-term c​ardiovascular morbidity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancreatic transplantation can improve proteinuria at an early stage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancreatic transplantation can improve proteinuria at an early stage</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreatic </strong><strong>transplantation </strong>can help improve proteinuria at an early stage. Successful pancreatic transplantation can have the following effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prevention</strong> of diabetic <strong>nephropathy</strong> and regression of albuminuria in early stages.</li>\n<li><strong>Stabilization</strong> and improvement of diabetic <strong>neuropathy</strong> and <strong>retinopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Increase in HDL with reduction in LDL and triglycerides</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Strict glycemic control can prevent or revert microalbuminuria.</p>\n<p>Option C: The ADA does not suggest restrictions of protein intake in diabetic patients with albuminuria because studies have failed to prove its benefits.</p>\n<p>Option D: Microalbuminuria is an indicator of long-term cardiovascular morbidity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7130","text":"A 50-year-old man with typ‍e 2 diabetes mellitus is ​found to have a 24-hour u​rinary albumin of 250 mg.​ Which of the following d‍rugs may be prescribed to‍ reduce the progression o‌f this condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus is found to have a 24-hour urinary albumin of 250 mg. Which of the following drugs may be prescribed to reduce the progression of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrochlo‍rothiazide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enal‍april","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amil‍oride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epler‍enone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enalapril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enalapril</strong></p><p>Angiotensin-converting enzyme (<strong>ACEI</strong>) inhibitors or angiotensin-receptor blockers (<strong>ARB</strong>) are recommended to reduce the progression of <strong>diabetic</strong> <strong>nephropathy</strong>. It has the following effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduction of albuminuria</li>\n<li>Reduces the associated decline in GFR </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mainstay of management of diabetic nephropathy includes control of blood sugar and blood pressure, inhibitors of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone(RAAS) system, and inhibitors of SGLT2 to halt the progression of the disease. </p>\n<p>RAAS inhibitors include ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or direct renin inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7132","text":"A pathologist is studying ‍a renal biopsy sample fro​m a patient with suspecte​d diabetic nephropathy. W​hich of the following bio‍psy findings is pathognom‍onic for this condition?","raw_text":"A pathologist is studying a renal biopsy sample from a patient with suspected diabetic nephropathy. Which of the following biopsy findings is pathognomonic for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffuse glomerulosclerosis‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nodular glomerulosclerosis‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Armani-Ebst‍ein reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basement membrane thickeni‍ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nodular glomerulosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53742981e810484a995a69d417426a9fx720x491.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nodular glomerulosclerosis</strong></p><p>The <strong>characteristic</strong> lesion of diabetic nephropathy is <strong>nodular </strong>or<strong> intercapillary </strong>glomerulosclerosis, also called <strong>Kimmelstiel-Wilson</strong> nodules. It is <strong>pathognomonic</strong> for this condition.</p>\n<p>The following image shows Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other non-specific findings seen on biopsy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glomerular basement membrane (<strong>GBM</strong>) <strong>thickening</strong> - highly sensitive</li>\n<li><strong>Diffuse glomerulosclerosis</strong> - most common pattern, due to diffuse mesangial thickening</li>\n<li>Fibrin cap - accumulation of hyaline material within the capillary loop</li>\n<li>Capsular drop - accumulation of hyaline material attached to the Bowman's capsule </li>\n<li><strong>Armani-Ebstein</strong> reaction - collection of glycogen clumps within the renal tubules due to tubular injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Renal biopsy is done to rule out other causes of renal failure. The indications for renal biopsy in diabetic nephropathy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of diabetic retinopathy</li>\n<li>Absence of edema</li>\n<li>Absence of frothing and proteinuria</li>\n<li>Absence of hypertension</li>\n<li>Presence of microscopic hematuria or features of nephritic syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7134","text":"Which of the following typ‍es of renal tubular acido​sis can develop in indivi​duals with diabetes melli​tus?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of renal tubular acidosis can develop in individuals with diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type ‍I RTA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type ‍II RTA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type I‍II RTA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type ‍IV RTA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type IV RTA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type IV RTA</strong></p><p><strong>Type IV </strong>renal tubular acidosis (<strong>RTA</strong>) may occur in individuals with <strong>diabetes</strong> mellitus types 1 and 2.</p>\n<p>Type IV RTA occurs due to <strong>hyporeninemic</strong> <strong>hypoaldosteronism</strong>. Low plasma renin activity is common in diabetic patients due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired conversion of prorenin to renin</li>\n<li>Volume expansion in diabetic nephropathy suppresses renin release</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These individuals have a tendency to develop <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> and <strong>acidemia</strong>, which can be <strong>exacerbated</strong> by the use of <strong>ACEI</strong> and <strong>ARBs</strong>. Hence, these drugs should be avoided.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7136","text":"A 45-year-old man has been‍ diagnosed with type 2 di​abetes mellitus. When sho​uld you refer him to an o​phthalmologist?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man has been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. When should you refer him to an ophthalmologist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Within one year of diagnos‍is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediately at the time of‍ diagnosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Within five years of diagn‍osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Within three years of diag‍nosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immediately at the time of diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immediately at the time of diagnosis</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>type 2</strong> diabetes should undergo a comprehensive <strong>ophthalmological</strong> <strong>examination</strong> to screen for diabetic retinopathy <strong>at diagnosis</strong>, followed by an <strong>annual</strong> examination of the <strong>fundus</strong>.</p>\n<p>In patients with <strong>type 1</strong> diabetes mellitus, screening for retinopathy is recommended <strong>3 to 5 years </strong>after diagnosis. Annual fundal examination is recommended thereafter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7141","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients would you screen fo​r diabetic retinopathy? A​ 10-year-old boy on treat​ment for type 1 diabetes ‍for the past 2 years A 48‍-year-old man newly diagn‌osed with type 2 diabetes‍ A 25-year-old primigravi‍da with gestational diabe‌tes mellitus A 62-year-ol‌d man with diabetic nephr​opathy A 22-year-old woma‌n with prepubertal onset ‍of type 1 diabetes","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following patients would you screen for diabetic retinopathy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 10-year-old boy on treatment for type 1 diabetes for the past 2 years</li>\n<li>A 48-year-old man newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes</li>\n<li>A 25-year-old primigravida with gestational diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>A 62-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy</li>\n<li>A 22-year-old woman with prepubertal onset of type 1 diabetes</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‍4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‍4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‍3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‍2, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Screening</strong> for <strong>diabetic retinopathy</strong> is done at <strong>3-5 years</strong> following diagnosis in<strong> type 1</strong> diabetes mellitus, whereas, patients with <strong>type 2</strong> diabetes must be screened <strong>at the time of diagnosis.</strong></p>\n<p>As type 2 diabetes mellitus often has a long <strong>asymptomatic</strong> period of <strong>hyperglycemia</strong> before clinical diagnosis, these patients are more likely to have complications at the time of diagnosis itself.</p>\n<p>Retinopathy develops in nearly 50-80% of patients by 20 years of diagnosis. The presence of diabetic retinopathy often correlates with pathological findings of diabetic nephropathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7146","text":"What is the most common cl‍inical presentation of di​abetic neuropathy?","raw_text":"What is the most common clinical presentation of diabetic neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autonomic ‍neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mononeu‍ropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal pol‍yneuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polyradi‍culopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Distal polyneuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Distal polyneuropathy</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> form of diabetic neuropathy is <strong>distal symmetric polyneuropathy.</strong> It presents with distal sensory loss and pain in a stocking-and-glove distribution and is a risk factor for foot ulceration.</p>\n<p><strong>Autonomic</strong> <strong>neuropathy</strong> is a common complication and results in the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Postural hypotension</li>\n<li>Silent myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia unawareness</li>\n<li>Erectile dysfunction</li>\n<li>Overflow incontinence</li>\n<li>Gastroparesis</li>\n<li>Enteropathy with constipation or diarrhea</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mononeuropathy</strong> of <strong>peripheral</strong> (median, ulnar, radial) and cranial (<strong>oculomotor</strong>) nerves is also seen, as well as mononeuritis multiplex.</p>\n<p>Diabetic <strong>polyradiculopathy</strong> is characterized by severe pain along the distribution of one or more <strong>nerve roots</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7144","text":"Which of the following fin‍dings in a diabetic patie​nt is self-limiting?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings in a diabetic patient is self-limiting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macula‍r edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral‍ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amyot‍rophy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Macroalb‍uminuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amyotrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amyotrophy</strong></p><p><strong>Amyotrophy</strong> refers to <strong>weakness</strong> of <strong>hip extensors</strong> and <strong>flexors</strong> secondary to polyradiculopathy involving the lumbar plexus and femoral nerve. It is associated with severe <strong>pain</strong> along the distribution of the nerve roots. It is <strong>self-limiting</strong> and resolves over 6-12 months.</p>\n<p>Strict glycemic control can reduce the incidence and prevent the progression of macular edema, peripheral neuropathy, and macroalbuminuria. However, once developed, they cannot be reversed. Intermittent microalbuminuria with normal GFR can be reversed with strict glycemic control.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7149","text":"You are evaluating a 65-ye‍ar-old diabetic woman who​ has been admitted follow​ing a seizure. On admissi​on, she was unconscious, ‍and random blood glucose ‍at admission was 37 mg/dl‌. The hypoglycemia was co‍rrected and she regained ‍consciousness. The woman ‌says that she had felt no‌rmal and had no other sym​ptoms prior to the onset ‌of seizures. What is the ‍likely cause of this pres‌entation?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a 65-year-old diabetic woman who has been admitted following a seizure. On admission, she was unconscious, and random blood glucose at admission was 37 mg/dl. The hypoglycemia was corrected and she regained consciousness. The woman says that she had felt normal and had no other symptoms prior to the onset of seizures. What is the likely cause of this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medication change from met‍formin to insulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin r‍esistance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autonomic ‍neuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Concurrent use of thiazide‍ diuretics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Autonomic neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Autonomic neuropathy</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>silent hypoglycemia </strong>in a diabetic patient. The most likely cause is <strong>autonomic neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypoglycemia presents with symptoms like <strong>palpitation, tremors, </strong>and<strong> sweating</strong> as a result of sympathetic stimulation. These symptoms are <strong>protective </strong>and provide advance warning before progression to neuroglucopenia.</p>\n<p>However, in autonomic neuropathy, there is a <strong>reduced </strong>release of counterregulatory hormones like <strong>catecholamines.</strong> Hence, the classical symptoms of hypoglycemia do not occur and the patient directly presents with features of neuroglucopenia. This is called <strong>hypoglycemia unawareness</strong>.</p>\n<p>The use of <strong>beta-blockers</strong> can also cause hypoglycemia unawareness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7150","text":"You are counselling a pati‍ent with longstanding, po​orly-controlled diabetes ​mellitus. Advising tight ​glycemic control in this ‍patient would not improve‍ which of the following?","raw_text":"You are counselling a patient with longstanding, poorly-controlled diabetes mellitus. Advising tight glycemic control in this patient would not improve which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuro‍pathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retin‍opathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral vascular diseas‍e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nephr‍opathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peripheral vascular disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peripheral vascular disease</strong></p><p>In patients with <strong>longstanding</strong> <strong>diabetes</strong> mellitus, <strong>tight</strong> <strong>glycaemic</strong> <strong>control</strong> is beneficial for<strong> </strong>microvascular complications. However, it has <strong>no effect </strong>on <strong>macrovascular</strong> complications like peripheral vascular disease (PVD), myocardial infarction, and stroke.</p>\n<p>Good glycemic control improves microvascular complications through <strong>primary</strong> and <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>prevention</strong>. It reduces the incidence of complications as well as stabilizes and prevents further progression in affected individuals. Intermittent microalbuminuria with normal GFR can even be reversed with tight glycemic control.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 182px; width: 342px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150.141px;\">\n<p><strong>Complication</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.859px;\">\n<p><strong>% risk reduction with tight glycemic control</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150.141px;\">\n<p>Retinopathy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.859px;\">\n<p>47 %</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150.141px;\">\n<p>Microalbuminuria</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.859px;\">\n<p>39 %</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150.141px;\">\n<p>Nephropathy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.859px;\">\n<p>54%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150.141px;\">\n<p>Neuropathy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.859px;\">\n<p>60 %</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7114","text":"Which among the following ‍statements explains Legac​y effect and metabolic me​mory? Intensive glycemic ​control in early disease ‍Early detection and manag‍ement of dyslipidemia Goo‌d glycemic control in lon‍gstanding disease High Hb‍A1c values in longstandin‌g disease Uncontrolled bl‌ood glucose levels in ear​ly disease","raw_text":"<p>Which among the following statements explains Legacy effect and metabolic memory?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Intensive glycemic control in early disease</li>\n<li>Early detection and management of dyslipidemia</li>\n<li>Good glycemic control in longstanding disease</li>\n<li>High HbA1c values in longstanding disease</li>\n<li>Uncontrolled blood glucose levels in early disease</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‍nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 a‍nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Legacy effect</strong> or <strong>metabolic memory</strong> can be observed in relation to <strong>glycemic control</strong> during <strong>early disease</strong> in patients with diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p><strong>Intensive glycemic control</strong> during the early stages of the disease has a positive effect on long-term <strong>microvascular</strong> and <strong>macrovascular</strong> complications (statement 1) even if this intensive glycemic control is not maintained long term. Similarly, <strong>uncontrolled blood glucose</strong> levels in the early stages causes an increased risk of these complications (statement 5). This phenomenon is called legacy effect and is attributed to metabolic memory. </p>\n<p>In patients with <strong>longstanding</strong> diabetes, intensive glycemic control at later stages can improve <strong>microvascular</strong> complications but has no effect on macrovascular complications.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7152","text":"A patient with chronic dia‍betes presents to the OPD​ for his annual checkup. ​A small painless ulcer is​ noted over his right foo‍t. His BMI is 33 kg/m2. A‍ll of the following facto‌rs can be implicated in t‍he causation of his foot ‍ulcer except:","raw_text":"A patient with chronic diabetes presents to the OPD for his annual checkup. A small painless ulcer is noted over his right foot. His BMI is 33 kg/m2. All of the following factors can be implicated in the causation of his foot ulcer except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venous‍ stasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neuro‍pathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immunosu‍ppression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isch‍emia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Venous stasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Venous stasis</strong></p><p>Venous stasis is not a cause of <strong>diabetic foot ulcers </strong>and gangrene. They are usually caused by a combination of three factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ischemia</strong> - secondary to atheroma</li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral neuropathy</strong> - leads to trophic skin changes</li>\n<li><strong>Immunosuppression</strong> - due to excess sugar deposition in tissues, predisposing diabetic patients to infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common sites of ulcers include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Great toe</li>\n<li>Metatarsophalangeal areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7156","text":"A 25-year-old diabetic wom‍an presents to the OPD wi​th the following skin les​ions. What is the likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old diabetic woman presents to the OPD with the following skin lesions. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necrolytic migratory eryth‍ema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic ‍dermopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Necrobiosi‍s lipoidica","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Granuloma‍ annulare","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Necrobiosis lipoidica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98efa019b5634ea1ae597f1bc962b36f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27f35b60e1a04d2ea9b5a157ae9a51bex720x468.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce26f3db7f14420585c1ad9b6e2875f6x720x540.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4330817c3e804fae84488637beed43fcx399x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Necrobiosis lipoidica</strong></p><p>The given image showing irregular plaques with yellowish discoloration and telangiectasias are suggestive of <strong>necrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an inflammatory granulomatous disorder of the skin due to <strong>collagen degeneration</strong> and <strong>lipid </strong>deposition. It is usually seen in young women with diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>The lesions usually begin in the <strong>pretibial</strong> region. It begins as papules that gradually enlarge and darken to form erythematous plaques with irregular margins, <strong>atrophic centers,</strong> and central ulceration.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Necrolytic migratory erythema</strong> is a feature of <strong>glucagonoma.</strong> It is a waxing and waning rash that appears as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Diabetic dermopathy</strong> or shin spots are commonly seen in elderly men. It presents as <strong>pigmented pretibial papules</strong> as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Granuloma annulare</strong> presents with <strong>small papules</strong> arranged in an <strong>annular</strong> pattern. There is no yellowish discoloration or telangiectasias, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7158","text":"Patients with diabetes mel‍litus are at a higher ris​k of developing all of th​e following infections ex​cept:","raw_text":"Patients with diabetes mellitus are at a higher risk of developing all of the following infections except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant ot‍itis externa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinocerebral mucormycosis‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emphysematous pyelonephrit‍is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emphysematous appendicitis‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Emphysematous appendicitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Emphysematous appendicitis</strong></p><p><strong>Emphysematous appendicitis</strong> is not a complication of diabetes mellitus. The potentially fatal infectious <strong>complications</strong> of <strong>diabetes</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignant otitis externa</li>\n<li>Rhinocerebral mucormycosis</li>\n<li>Emphysematous pyelonephritis </li>\n<li>Emphysematous cholecystitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9260","text":"A morbidly obese diabetic ‍woman is not responsive t​o metformin therapy. She ​has a history of pancreat​itis and family history o‍f bladder cancer. She doe‍s not want to take inject‌ions. Which of the follow‍ing would be suitable to ‍reduce her glucose levels‌?","raw_text":"A morbidly obese diabetic woman is not responsive to metformin therapy. She has a history of pancreatitis and family history of bladder cancer. She does not want to take injections. Which of the following would be suitable to reduce her glucose levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sitag‍liptin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Piogli‍tazone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Canagl‍iflozin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lirag‍lutide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Canagliflozin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Canagliflozin</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>canagliflozin</strong> (Option C) is the most <strong>suitable treatment</strong>. It is an <strong>oral drug</strong> belonging to the class <strong>SGLT-2 inhibitors</strong> and causes <strong>weight loss</strong>. Although it increases the risk of urogenital infections, it has <strong>no risk</strong> of <strong>pancreatitis</strong> or <strong>bladder cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sitagliptin</strong>, a DPP-IV inhibitor, is associated with the risk of <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pioglitazone</strong>, a thiazolidinedione, can increase the risk of <strong>bladder cancer</strong> and can cause weight gain and <strong>congestive cardiac failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Liraglutide</strong> is an injectable GLP-1 receptor agonist. <strong>Pancreatitis</strong> is a serious adverse effect of this drug. Hence, it is contraindicated in individuals with a history of pancreatitis and should be permanently discontinued if pancreatitis develops.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9265","text":"Which of the following ant‍i-diabetic drugs should b​e stopped in a patient wi​th a creatinine of 5.6 mg​/dL?","raw_text":"Which of the following anti-diabetic drugs should be stopped in a patient with a creatinine of 5.6 mg/dL?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metf‍ormin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Linag‍liptin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metop‍rolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins‍ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metformin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metformin</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin</strong> should be <strong>stopped</strong> in case of <strong>elevated creatinine levels</strong> (renal insufficiency) <strong>&gt; 1.5 mg/dl in men</strong> and <strong>&gt;1.4 mg/dl in women</strong>.</p>\n<p>Metformin acts by <strong>reducing hepatic glucose production</strong> and <strong>improves peripheral glucose utilization</strong>. Lactic acidosis and Vitamin B12 deficiency are specific adverse effects of metformin. It is <strong>not</strong> used in patients with a <strong>glomerular filtration rate &lt;30 mL/min </strong>(moderate renal insufficiency), acidosis, unstable congestive cardiac failure, liver disease, severe hypoxemia, and patients undergoing contrast studies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WAqN_PsM","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"15_pseudomonas_and_burkholderiales","title":"15.Pseudomonas And Burkholderiales","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2516","text":"A 20 year old male present‌s with acute onset cough ​and thick sticky sputum. ‌There is a history of rep‍eated lung infections sin​ce childhood. Chest Xray ​shows evidence of bilater‌al upper lobe bronchiecta​sis. On evaluation, the c​hloride concentration in ‌his sweat is &gt;60 mEq/L. W​hat is the organism major‍ly associated with this p‌atient's respiratory dise​ase?","raw_text":"A 20 year old male presents with acute onset cough and thick sticky sputum. There is a history of repeated lung infections since childhood. Chest Xray shows evidence of bilateral upper lobe bronchiectasis. On evaluation, the chloride concentration in his sweat is &gt;60 mEq/L. What is the organism majorly associated with this patient's respiratory disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkholderia‌ pseudomallei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomon‌as putida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Burkholder‌ia cepacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong></p><p>Bilateral upper lobe bronchiectasis with a history of repeated lung infections since childhood and evelated sweat chloride concentration suggests a diagnosis of<strong> cystic fibrosis</strong> (CF).<em><strong> Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong></em> is a common etiological agent associated with <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>airway infection</strong> in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF).</p>\n<p>In cystic fibrosis, there is <strong>thick inspissated mucus</strong> and <strong>impaired mucociliary clearance</strong>. Due to these factors, lungs are often colonized in infancy and early childhood with <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> and <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>. In adulthood, they are replaced by <em>P. aeruginosa.</em> This results in chronic inflammation, which leads to airway modelling and bronchiectasis. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong><em>Burkholderia cepacia</em> </strong>complex is an opportunistic environmental pathogen. It causes <strong>fatal respiratory distress</strong> and <strong>septicemia </strong>in <strong>CF</strong> patients. This is termed as 'cepacia syndrome'.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3274","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms causes scombroid f​ish poisoning?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms causes scombroid fish poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salmo‌nella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peptostre‌ptococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Scombroid fish poisoning</strong> is associated with <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa.</em></strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It follows the consumption of both <strong>scombroid </strong>(mackerel-like) <strong>fish</strong> and nonscombroid fish that have <strong>not</strong> been <strong>refrigerated</strong> properly. The musculature of these dark- or red-fleshed fish <strong>undergoes decomposition</strong> by <em>Morganella morganii, Escherichia coli, Proteus</em> species, <em>Klebsiella</em> species, and <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa. </em>There is consequent decarboxylation of the amino acid L-histidine to form <strong>histamine</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">A second compound is thought to play a causative role in this intoxication because large doses of oral histamine do not reproduce the affliction. The toxin or <strong>toxins involved</strong> are <strong>heat stable</strong> and are not destroyed by cooking or freezing.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Symptoms develop within<strong> 15–90 min</strong> of ingestion. Most cases are mild, with <strong>tingling</strong> of <strong>lips</strong> and mouth, mild <strong>abdominal discomfort</strong>, and <strong>nausea</strong>. The more severe and commonly described presentation includes <strong>flushing, </strong>conjunctival hyperemia,<strong> pruritus</strong>, urticaria, and <strong>angioedema</strong>. Without treatment, the symptoms generally <strong>resolve </strong>within<strong> 8–12 h</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2539","text":"A patient comes to the der‌matology OPD complaining ​of greenish discoloration‌ of her nails. You diagno‍se her with green nail sy​ndrome. Production of whi​ch of the following helps‌ differentiate the organi​sm implicated here from o​ther members of its genus‌?","raw_text":"A patient comes to the dermatology OPD complaining of greenish discoloration of her nails. You diagnose her with green nail syndrome. Production of which of the following helps differentiate the organism implicated here from other members of its genus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyoc‌yanin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pyov‌erdin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyor‌ubin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyome‌lanin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyocyanin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyocyanin</strong></p><p><strong>Green nail syndrome</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa.</em></strong> Production of pyocyanin helps differentiate it implicated here from other Pseudomonas species.</p>\n<p><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa </em>is the only member of the <em>Pseudomonas</em> species that produces <strong>pyocyanin</strong> which is a <strong>bluish-green pigment</strong>. Other species of <em>Pseudomonas</em> also <strong>produce pigments</strong> but they cannot produce pyocyanin. Some of the other pigments produced by<em> Pseudomonas </em>species including <em>P. aeruginosa</em> are <strong>p</strong><strong>yoverdin/flourescein</strong> (greenish-yellow pigment), <strong>pyorubin</strong> (red pigment) and <strong>pyomelanin</strong> (brown pigment).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2513","text":"The mechanism of action of‌ exotoxin A of Pseudomona​s resembles that of _____‌____.","raw_text":"The mechanism of action of exotoxin A of Pseudomonas resembles that of _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choler‌a toxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diphther‌ia toxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tetanu‌s toxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anthra‌x toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diphtheria toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d1d8461f5bd475687f6a4ebec425f4dx720x581.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diphtheria toxin</strong></p><p>The action of <strong>exotoxin A</strong> of <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></strong> is similar to that of the <strong>diphtheria toxin.</strong></p>\n<p>Both exotoxin A and diphtheria toxin cause <strong>ADP ribosylation</strong> of <strong>EF-2</strong> and<strong> </strong>inhibit protein synthesis. Exotoxin A is a single-chain polypeptide with A and B fragments. The B fragment binds the toxin while the A fragment inactivates EF-2 in the presence of nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD). This leads to the inhibition of polypeptide chain elongation and thus, protein synthesis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2517","text":"A patient with acute onset‌ fever, cough and hemopty​sis is diagnosed to have ‌Whitmore's disease. Which‍ of the following organis​ms causes this condition?​","raw_text":"A patient with acute onset fever, cough and hemoptysis is diagnosed to have Whitmore's disease. Which of the following organisms causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkholderia‌ pseudomallei","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Burkholde‌ria mallei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burkholder‌ia cepacia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paraburkhold‌eria species","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Burkholderia pseudomallei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Burkholderia pseudomallei</strong></p><p><em><strong>Burkholderia pseudomallei </strong></em>causes <strong>Whitmore's disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Melioidosis</strong> was first described by Whitmore and Krishnaswami. Whitmore isolated the bacillus, and hence, it is known as Whitmore bacillus and melioidosis is called Whitmore's disease. Melioidosis is similar in presentation to <strong>glanders</strong> disease (caused by <em>Burkholderia mallei</em>). Humans are infected tby contact with contaminated soil via skin abrasions. </p>\n<p>Types of melioidosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute - It causes <strong>acute pneumonia</strong> with upper lobe infiltrates or severe necrotizing disease. It can also <strong>mimic tuberculosis</strong> with fever, cough, hemoptysis. It may lead to <strong>septicemia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Chronic - It can lead to the development of multiple<strong> caseous</strong> or <strong>suppurative foci</strong>, with skin and <strong>subcutaneous abscesses</strong>. These can lead to <strong>lymphangitis</strong> and <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong>. It may involve bones and other internal organs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The bacillus has a <strong>safety-pin appearance </strong>on methylene blue staining. Treatment is divided into two stages: an <strong>intensive </strong>2 week<strong> phase</strong> of <strong>ceftazidime</strong> or a carbapenem followed by at least <strong>12 weeks</strong> of <strong>oral cotrimoxazole</strong> to eradicate the organism and prevent relapse.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2520","text":"A horse-groomer was diagno‌sed with ulcerative trach​eobronchitis. Upon enquir‌y, it was discovered that‍ one of his horses had re​cently died of pneumonia ​after developing a fever ‌and subcutaneous nodules.​ Which of the following s​tatements is false about ‌the likely causative agen​t?","raw_text":"A horse-groomer was diagnosed with ulcerative tracheobronchitis. Upon enquiry, it was discovered that one of his horses had recently died of pneumonia after developing a fever and subcutaneous nodules. Which of the following statements is false about the likely causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is no‌n-motile.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is always oxidase negat‌ive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is the causative agent ‌for Glanders disease.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It induces Strauss reactio‌n on inoculation into gui​nea pigs.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is always oxidase negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is always oxidase negative</strong></p><p>The development of ulcerative tracheobronchitis after contact with an infected horse is suggestive of <strong>glanders</strong> which is caused by <em><strong>Burkholderia mallei</strong></em>. It is an <strong>oxidase variable </strong>organism.</p>\n<p>In this case, the horse likely had <strong>glanders,</strong> which presents with fever, pneumonia, and <strong>subcutaneous nodules </strong>called <strong>Farcy buds</strong>. Discharges from the respiratory tract and skin are highly infectious and can be transmitted to humans through close contact (<strong>occupational </strong>route). The horse-groomer likely contracted the infection by the <strong>inhalational route</strong> and developed acute glanders, which is characterised by fever, mucopurulent nasal discharge and ulcerative necrosis of upper respiratory tract (<strong>ulcerative tracheobronchitis</strong>).<strong> </strong>It may lead to septicemia and has a high fatality rate. Percutaneous exposure causes local suppurative skin lesions with associated regional lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p><em>B. mallei</em> is a <strong>non-motile </strong>Gram-negative bacillus causing Glanders disease. It is an aerobe and a facultative anaerobe. It is oxidase variable i.e. it is usually oxidase negative but may occasionally show a positive reaction. It is a <strong>biosafety level 3</strong> pathogen and so must be handled only in specialised laboratories. When male <strong>guinea pigs</strong> are <strong>inoculated intraperitoneally</strong> with suspected infectious material, it causes <strong>Strauss reaction</strong>. <em>B. mallei</em> sets up a <strong>necrotizing inflammation</strong> in the <strong>scrotal sac</strong> within a few days and can be confirmed bacteriologically. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3191","text":"Which of the following is ‌Burkholderia cepacia resi​stant to?","raw_text":"Which of the following is Burkholderia cepacia resistant to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefta‌zidime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxaz‌ole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temoc‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefo‌tetan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cefotetan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cefotetan</strong></p><p><em>Burkholderia cepacia</em> is resistant to <strong>cefotetan</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong><em>B. cepacia</em> </strong>complex strains are <strong>intrinsically resistant</strong> to aminoglycosides, polymixin, first and <strong>second-generation cephalosporins </strong>(e.g. cefotetan)<strong> </strong>and carboxypenicillins. This antimicrobial resistance is mediated by efflux pumps, antimicrobial degradation, modifying enzymes or altered membrane functions.</p>\n<p><em>B. cepacia</em> is a Gram-negative bacillus which can cause severe necrotizing pneumonia in patients with chronic granulomatous disease. It can lead to fatal septicemia in patients with <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Antimicrobial</strong> agents that are <strong>effective</strong> against<strong> </strong><em>B. cepacia</em> complex include meropenem,<strong> ceftazidime, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole</strong>, piperacillin and <strong>temocillin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2786","text":"An organism cultured in Bo‌rdet–Gengou medium shows ​smooth, refractile, glist‌ening colonies resembling‍ 'bisected pearls' or 'me​rcury drops'. The organis​m is most likely ______.","raw_text":"An organism cultured in Bordet–Gengou medium shows smooth, refractile, glistening colonies resembling 'bisected pearls' or 'mercury drops'. The organism is most likely ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bordetella p‌arapertussis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bordetella b‌ronchiseptica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bordetella‌ pertussis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bordetel‌la petrii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bordetella pertussis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5725ac6a567e4d748e443729897ecd6cx1280x1680.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bordetella pertussis</strong></p><p>The organism showing colonies resembling '<strong>bisected pearls</strong>' or '<strong>mercury drops</strong>' on <strong>Bordet-Gengou</strong> medium most likely is <strong><em>Bordetella pertussis</em>. </strong> </p>\n<p>Bordetella pertussis is a Gram-negative coccobacillus. It causes whooping cough or pertussis in children. </p>\n<p>It can be isolated on the enriched medium Bordet-Genou medium (potato-blood-glycerol agar). After incubation for <strong>48 to 72 hours</strong>, <em>B. pertussis</em> colonies are small, dome-shaped, smooth, opaque, viscid, greyish white, refractile, and glistening resembling 'bisected pearls' or 'mercury drops' and are <strong>surrounded</strong> by a hazy zone of <strong>hemolysis</strong>. If growth is confluent, an '<strong>aluminium paint</strong>' appearance is seen. In culture <strong>films</strong>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em> bacilli tend to be arranged in loose clumps, with clear spaces in between, giving a <strong>'thumbprint' </strong>appearance. </p>\n<p><strong><em>B. parapertussis</em></strong> (option A) grows on <strong>nutrient agar</strong> with the production of a <strong>brown, diffusible pigment</strong> after two days. It also grows more rapidly than <em>B.pertussis</em> on charcoal blood agar.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2822","text":"An 8-year-old boy with inc‌omplete vaccination histo​ry comes with vomiting af‌ter bouts of severe, viol‍ent cough. What is the me​chanism of action of the ​toxin responsible for thi‌s child's disease?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy with incomplete vaccination history comes with vomiting after bouts of severe, violent cough. What is the mechanism of action of the toxin responsible for this child's disease? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of phospholipas‌e and degrades cell membr​ane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivation of 60S riboso‌me","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ADP ribosylation and lymph‌ocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Activation of guanylate cy‌clase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ADP ribosylation and lymphocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ADP ribosylation and lymphocytosis</strong></p><p>Post-tussive vomiting and bouts of cough in an unimmunized child points to a diagnosis of <strong>pertussis</strong>, caused by <strong><em>Bordetella pertussis</em></strong>. It secretes <strong>pertussis</strong> <strong>toxin</strong> (PT), which acts by <strong>ADP ribosylation </strong>and<strong> </strong>disrupts signal transduction of many cell types. This <strong>promotes lymphocytosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The pertussis toxin disrupts protein synthesis and causes necrosis of parts of the epithelium, leading to pseudomembrane formation. Pertussis toxin also promotes <strong>sensitization to histamine</strong> and <strong>enhances insulin secretion</strong> but these effects have not been demonstrable in patients. They are only seen in experimental animals. </p>\n<p><strong><em>B. pertussis</em></strong> also produces <strong>other toxins</strong> like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adenylate cyclase toxin </strong>(ACT)- It acts by catalysing the production of cAMP by various types of cells and <strong>inhibiting phagocytosis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Heat labile toxin </strong>(HLT)- It is a cytoplasmic protein present in all bordetellae. It is dermonecrotic and lethal in mice. Its pathogenic role is not known.</li>\n<li><strong>Tracheal cytotoxin</strong> (TCT)- It is low molecular weight peptidoglycan which induces ciliary damage in hamster trachea.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2789","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about pe​rtussis toxin?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about pertussis toxin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is expressed on the sur‌face of the bacillus.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot b‌e toxoided.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"\"A\" portion of A-B structu‌re is the enzymatically a​ctive moiety.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is similar to cholera t‌oxin in its structure and​ function.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It cannot be toxoided.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It cannot be toxoided.</strong></p><p><strong>Pertussis toxin</strong> (PT) can be toxoided.</p>\n<p>PT is one of the toxins produced by <em><strong>Bordetella pertussis</strong></em>. It is expressed on the surface of the bacillus and secreted into the surrounding medium. It has an<strong> A-B structure</strong>, the A portion being the enzymatically active moiety and B, the binding component. PT is similar to cholera toxin in its structure and function. PT has <strong>ADP-ribosylating activity</strong> and acts on<strong> inhibitory Gi α subunit</strong> of G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR), whereas cholera toxin acts on stimulatory Gs α subunit of GPCR.</p>\n<p>Pertussis <strong>toxoid</strong> is the major component of the <strong>acellular pertussis vaccine</strong>. A toxoid is a toxin that has lost its toxicity but retains its antigenicity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2792","text":"A 4-year-old unimmunized c‌hild presents with whoopi​ng cough. Which of the fo‌llowing constituents of t‍he causative organism is ​responsible for facilitat​ing its adhesion to the r‌espiratory epithelium?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old unimmunized child presents with whooping cough. Which of the following constituents of the causative organism is responsible for facilitating its adhesion to the respiratory epithelium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenylat‌e cyclase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipopolys‌accharide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheal ‌cytotoxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filamentous ‌hemagglutinin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Filamentous hemagglutinin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Filamentous hemagglutinin</strong></p><p><strong>Whooping cough</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Bordetella pertussis.</em></strong> <strong>Filamentous hemagglutinin</strong> (FHA) is responsible for facilitating the <strong>adhesion</strong> of <em>Bordetella pertussis</em> to the <strong>respiratory epithelium.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bordetella pertussis </em>produces 3 hemagglutinins: Pertussis toxin (PT), lipid factor and FHA. <strong>FHA</strong> is a <strong>surface</strong> protein and is readily shed. Along with fimbrae, it helps in the adhesion of<em> B. pertussis </em>to the <strong>cilia</strong> of the respiratory epithelium and to erythrocytes. FHA and PT also <strong>promote secondary infection</strong> by coating other bacteria such as <strong><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></strong> and<strong> Pneumococci. </strong>They then<strong> </strong>assists their binding to respiratory epithelium. This phenomenon has been termed '<strong>piracy</strong> of <strong>adhesins'</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2794","text":"What is the incubation per‌iod of pertussis in human​s?","raw_text":"What is the incubation period of pertussis in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;7‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-5 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"21-30‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7-14‌ days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 7-14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 7-14 days</strong></p><p>The <strong>incubation period</strong> of pertussis in humans is <strong>7-14 days.</strong> </p>\n<p>Pertussis is caused by <em>Bordetella pertussis. </em>After the incubation period, the disease takes a <strong>protracted course</strong> comprising <strong>three stages</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Catarrhal: </strong>It lasts 1-2 weeks and is characterized by <strong>coryza</strong>, lacrimation, <strong>mild cough</strong>, <strong>low-grade fever</strong>, and malaise.</li>\n<li><strong>Paroxysmal: </strong>It lasts 2-4 weeks with the cough becoming more frequent and spasmodic. The cough comes in repetitive<strong> bursts </strong>of <strong>5–10 </strong>in number, often within a single expiration. <strong>Post-tussive vomiting</strong> is frequent. The episode may be terminated by an <strong>audible whoop</strong>, which occurs upon rapid inspiration against a closed glottis at the end of a paroxysm.</li>\n<li><strong>Convalescent: </strong>It lasts 1–3 months and is characterized by <strong>gradual resolution</strong> of coughing episodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whooping cough is also known as '<strong>100-day cough</strong>'. For 6–12 months following the illness, <strong>intercurrent viral infections</strong> may be associated with a <strong>recrudescence</strong> of paroxysmal cough.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2816","text":"You are evaluating a 6-yea‌r-old unimmunised girl wi​th cough and abdominal pa‌in. Her mother informs yo‍u that her daughter has b​outs of violent coughing ​which frequently ends wit‌h a loud whoop and is acc​ompanied by vomiting. She​ gives history of similar‌ complaints in a classmat​e 2-3 weeks back. Which o‍f the following drugs wil‌l you prescribe for this ​patient?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a 6-year-old unimmunised girl with cough and abdominal pain. Her mother informs you that her daughter has bouts of violent coughing which frequently ends with a loud whoop and is accompanied by vomiting. She gives history of similar complaints in a classmate 2-3 weeks back. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Penic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Erythromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Erythromycin</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of <strong>whooping cough</strong>, post-tussive vomiting, and abdominal pain, along with positive history of<strong> exposure</strong> in an <strong>unimmunised</strong> child is suggestive of <strong>pertussis. Erythromycin</strong> or one of the newer macrolides such as azithromycin or clarithromycin is the<strong> drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of this condition.</p>\n<p>Antibiotic therapy in pertussis is used to eradicate the bacteria from the nasopharynx. The treatment is beneficial if it is started within 10 days of the disease. Macrolides are the drugs of choice. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (Cotrimoxazole) is recommended as an <strong>alternative</strong> for individuals allergic to macrolides. <strong>Macrolides</strong> may also be used in the <strong>chemoprophylaxis</strong> of pertussis. <strong>Post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> is given to household close contacts, and high-risk contacts (infants, immunocompromised, pregnancy).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2813","text":"A young child with unknown‌ immunization history is ​suspected to have whoopin‌g cough. Which of the fol‍lowing methods will you p​refer to use to collect a​ specimen for culture?","raw_text":"A young child with unknown immunization history is suspected to have whooping cough. Which of the following methods will you prefer to use to collect a specimen for culture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cough pla‌te method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Per-or‌al swab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Per-nas‌al swab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Post-na‌sal swab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Per-nasal swab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Per-nasal swab</strong></p><p><strong>Whooping cough</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Bordotella pertussis</em></strong>. The <strong>most effective method</strong> for collecting a specimen for the culture of this organism is the <strong>per-nasal</strong> or <strong>nasopharyngeal swab</strong> method. </p>\n<p>Respiratory specimens may be collected for the<strong> culture</strong> (gold standard) of<em> Bordetella pertussis</em> by<strong> </strong>three different methods:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Per-nasal swab: </strong>A Dacron or rayon tipped swab on a flexible nichrome wire is passed along the floor of the nasal cavity and material is collected from the pharyngeal wall. This specimen is the most <strong>effective</strong> and <strong>best suited</strong> for <strong>diagnostic</strong> procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Cough plate method: </strong>A culture plate is held about <strong>10 </strong>to <strong>15 cms in front </strong>of the patient's mouth during a bout of cough.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-nasal (peroral) method: </strong>Secretions from the posterior pharyngeal wall are collected with a cotton swab on a bent wire passed through the mouth.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other diagnostic tests for pertussis include direct fluorescent aantibody test and PCR on nasopharyngeal specimens. Enzyme immunoassays of antibodies after exposure to <em>B. pertussis</em> can also be used for patients between 2-4 weeks of illness. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2820","text":"A pregnant woman comes to ‌the ANC clinic for her 2n​d dose of the Tdap vaccin‌e. Which of the following‍ constitutes the acellula​r component of this vacci​ne?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman comes to the ANC clinic for her 2nd dose of the Tdap vaccine. Which of the following constitutes the acellular component of this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pertactin, filamentous hem‌aglutinin, and cytotoxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endotoxin, fimbriae, and p‌ili","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pertussis toxoid, cytotoxi‌n, and pili","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pertussis toxoid, filament‌ous hemaglutinin, and fim​brial protein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pertussis toxoid, filamentous hemaglutinin, and fimbrial protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pertussis toxoid, filamentous hemaglutinin, and fimbrial protein</strong></p><p><strong>Tdap vaccine</strong> is the tetanus, diphtheria and acellular pertussis vaccine. The component of the <strong>acellular pertussis</strong> vaccine is<strong> inactivated pertussis toxin </strong>(PT) and it may contain one or more bacterial components such as<strong> pertactin, filamentous hemagglutinin,</strong> and <strong>fimbrial protein. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pertussis vaccine</strong> is usually administered in combination with the toxoids of diphtheria and tetanus (DTaP). <strong>Five doses</strong> of pertussis vaccine are recommended - at <strong>2, 4, 6 </strong>and <strong>15–18 months</strong> of age followed by a <strong>booster</strong> dose at <strong>4–6 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Tdap is now also recommended for <strong>pregnant women</strong> in<strong> between 27 weeks </strong>and <strong>36 weeks</strong> of gestation by the ACOG. This is because infants between the age of 0-2 months are vulnerable to pertussis as they do not begin their vaccine schedule before 6 weeks of age. Thus to protect these infants, placental <strong>transfer </strong>of <strong>maternal pertussis antibodies</strong> has proven to be the most effective approach. Children<strong> under 4 years</strong> who are <strong>contacts</strong> of patients should<strong> receive a booster </strong>even if they had been previously immunized. <strong>Non-immunized contacts</strong> should receive full series of <strong>vaccination</strong> and also<strong> chemoprophylaxis </strong>with<strong> erythromycin</strong> for 10 days after contact with the patient has ceased.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hwGUtmVh","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"15_caesarean_section_and_vaginal_birth_after_caesarean__vbac_","title":"15.Caesarean Section And Vaginal Birth After Caesarean (Vbac)","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB9866","text":"When should an elective ca‌esarean section ideally b​e performed?","raw_text":"When should an elective caesarean section ideally be performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After 3‌7 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"After 3‌8 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After 4‌0 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After 3‌9 weeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. After 39 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. After 39 weeks</strong></p><p>An elective caesarean section should ideally be performed <strong>after</strong> <strong>39 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is not done before 39 weeks of gestation as there is an increased risk of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complications related to prematurity including <strong>respiratory</strong> problems </li>\n<li>Neonatal adaptation problems such as <strong>hypothermia</strong> and <strong>hypoglycemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9879","text":"A 28-year-old pregnant wom‌an is set to undergo an e​lective LSCS 2 days later‍. What is the ideal time ‍to administer antibiotics​ to this patient?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman is set to undergo an elective LSCS 2 days later. What is the ideal time to administer antibiotics to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 hours be‌fore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 hours bef‌ore surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After cor‌d clamping","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30-60 min be‌fore surgery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30-60 min before surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30-60 min before surgery</strong></p><p>According to ACOG guidelines, the ideal time to administer <strong>antibiotics</strong> is <strong>within </strong>a time period of<strong> 60 mins</strong> prior to the <strong>planned caesarean section.</strong></p>\n<p>Antimicrobial administration before surgical incision has been shown to <strong>lower postoperative infection</strong> rates without adverse neonatal effects, as compared with drug administration after umbilical cord clamping. A 1 g dose of <strong>cefazolin</strong> is an <strong>efficacious</strong> and <strong>cost-effective</strong> choice.</p>\n<p>For <strong>emergency</strong> delivery, antibiotic prophylaxis should be given <strong>as soon as possible</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9882","text":"The partogram of a 26-year‌-old G2P1 woman is shown ​below. What is the prophy‍lactic antibiotic of choi‍ce for this patient befor​e definitive management i‌s done?","raw_text":"The partogram of a 26-year-old G2P1 woman is shown below. What is the prophylactic antibiotic of choice for this patient before definitive management is done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefa‌zolin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cefazolin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c301d4ce63e541089d65739f2eb7839a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cefazolin</strong></p><p>The above partogram is suggestive of <strong>obstructed labour</strong> and the definitive management in this case is <strong>caesarean </strong>section. <strong>Cefazolin</strong> is an<strong> efficacious</strong> and <strong>cost-effective</strong> choice for antibiotic prophylaxis before caesarean section.</p>\n<p>According to the partogram, there is a secondary <strong>arrest of dilatation for &gt;2 hours</strong> and <strong>arrest of descent</strong> <strong>for &gt;1 hour</strong>. The <strong>amniotic fluid</strong> is <strong>stained with</strong> <strong>meconium</strong> and there is fall in fetal heart rate indicating <strong>fetal distress</strong>. Hence, the management plan is to perform a caesarean section.</p>\n<p>The recommended prophylactic drug is a single intravenous dose of a <strong>β-lactam antimicrobial</strong>. This could either be a cephalosporin or an extended-spectrum penicillin derivative. A <strong>1 g</strong> dose of <strong>cefazolin</strong> is usually given. For <strong>obese</strong> women, a <strong>2 g</strong> dose is better to provide an adequate coverage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9887","text":"You are asked to administe‌r antibiotic prophylaxis ​against infective endocar‍ditis to a patient posted‍ for elective LSCS. What ​condition does the patien‌t likely have?","raw_text":"You are asked to administer antibiotic prophylaxis against infective endocarditis to a patient posted for elective LSCS. What condition does the patient likely have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prosthet‌ic valves","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prosthetic valves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prosthetic valves</strong></p><p>Antibiotic <strong>prophylaxis against infective endocarditis</strong> before surgical procedures is recommended in patients with<strong> prosthetic valves, prior history of endocarditis,</strong> and <strong>unrepaired cyanotic heart diseases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pregnant women at the <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">highest risk for endocarditis </strong>are those with<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> cyanotic cardiac disease, prosthetic valves, or both.</strong> However, endocarditis prophylaxis is not required if the patient is already receiving intrapartum prophylaxis with broad-spectrum antibiotics to prevent endometritis. </p>\n<p>Antibiotic prophylaxis is <strong>not recommended</strong> for <strong>atrial septal defect (ASD)</strong>. The risk of endocarditis with ASD is negligible. Bacterial endocarditis is associated with <strong>unrepaired ventricular septal defects</strong> where antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended. Similarly, prophylaxis for bacterial endocarditis is indicated at delivery for<strong> unrepaired patent ductus arteriosus.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9884","text":"A 25-year-old obese pregna‌nt woman is scheduled for​ caesarean section. While‍ going through her file, ‍you find that she is alle​rgic to penicillin. What ‌is/are the antibiotic(s) ‌you would prescribe for h​er?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old obese pregnant woman is scheduled for caesarean section. While going through her file, you find that she is allergic to penicillin. What is/are the antibiotic(s) you would prescribe for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clindamycin ‌600 mg alone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clindamycin 900 mg plus an‌ aminoglycoside","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clindamycin ‌900 mg alone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clindamycin 600 mg plus an‌ aminoglycoside","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clindamycin 900 mg plus an aminoglycoside","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clindamycin 900 mg plus an aminoglycoside</strong></p><p>In an obese pregnant woman who is allergic to penicillin, a dose of <strong>900 mg clindamycin</strong> with an<strong> aminoglycoside</strong> is used for antibiotic prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>In women with significant penicillin or cephalosporin allergy, a single 600 mg intravenous dose of clindamycin combined with a weight-based dose of aminoglycoside is recommended. For obese women, a 900 mg dose is preferred. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9912","text":"A woman with preeclampsia ‌presents with haemorrhage​ at 38 weeks of gestation‍. You decide to perform a‍ caesarean section and te​rminate the pregnancy. Wh‌at is the anaesthesia of ‌choice?","raw_text":"A woman with preeclampsia presents with haemorrhage at 38 weeks of gestation. You decide to perform a caesarean section and terminate the pregnancy. What is the anaesthesia of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Combined spinal epidural a‌naesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Single shot spinal anesthe‌sia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"General a‌naesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. General anaesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. General anaesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> is the <strong>anaesthesia</strong> <strong>of choice</strong> here since the patient presents with<strong> haemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\">General anaesthesia may be indicated for <strong>emergency cesarean</strong> delivery in patients with a <strong>reassuring airway examination</strong>. It is also recommended for patients with <strong>severe coagulopathy</strong> where neuraxial anesthesia is contraindicated. </p>\n<p><strong>Spinal anesthesia</strong> is not preferred when the operation is expected to result in blood loss or in a previously haemorrhaging patient. It is because in such cases the development of <strong>severe hypovolemia</strong> is likely which is a <strong>contraindication</strong> for this type of anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9888","text":"An elective cesarean secti‌on is being performed in ​a primigravida due to sus‍pected cephalopelvic disp‍roportion. In which of th​e following abdominal inc‌isions are the recti musc‌les transected?","raw_text":"An elective cesarean section is being performed in a primigravida due to suspected cephalopelvic disproportion.  In which of the following abdominal incisions are the recti muscles transected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28f34e05f8d240a7bdf20486c7164bd7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8b4094e8a2147a89c9ecb99fbd4d288x1280x1496.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2</strong></p><p>The recti muscles are transected in the <strong>Maylard</strong> incision for caesarean delivery which is marked as '<strong>2</strong>' in the given image. </p>\n<p>It is a variation of the Pfannenstiel incision in which the <strong>rectus abdominis</strong> muscles<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscle\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a> are <strong>sectioned transversely</strong> to permit wider access to the pelvis. It is also known as the <strong>Mackenrodt </strong>incision.</p>\n<p>The various incisions mentioned in the image are:</p>\n<p>Incision '1' -<strong> Midline vertical</strong><strong> </strong>incision is made in the middle extending from just below the umbilicus to just above the symphysis pubis. This is <strong>used less commonly</strong> compared to the transverse abdominal incision.</p>\n<p>Incision '3' -<strong> Joel Cohen</strong> is a straight incision higher than the Pfannenstiel incision. The fascia was incised transversely in the midline then extended laterally with<strong> blunt finger dissection</strong>; finger dissection was used to <strong>separate the rectus muscles vertically</strong> and laterally.</p>\n<p>Incision '4' -<strong> Pfannenstiel incision </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> incision made in caesarean delivery. It <strong>follows Langer lines</strong> of skin tension. A transverse and slightly curvilinear incision is made in the skin and subcutaneous tissue <strong>3 cm above</strong> the superior border of the <strong>pubic symphysis. </strong></p>\n<p>This is stretched laterally for 12-15 cm to accommodate the delivery of the fetus. Following this, the <strong>rectus sheath</strong> is <strong>incised transversely</strong> and <strong>rectus muscles</strong> are <strong>separated in the middle</strong> to gain entry to the peritoneum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9897","text":"All of the following are b‌enefits of using a transv​erse abdominal incision f‍or caesarean delivery in ‍a patient except______.","raw_text":"All of the following are benefits of using a transverse abdominal incision for caesarean delivery in a patient except______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wide opera‌ting space","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low fascial wound dehiscen‌ce","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased postoperative pa‌in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low risk of incisional her‌nia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wide operating space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wide operating space</strong></p><p><strong>Transverse</strong> abdominal incisions offer a <strong>narrow</strong> operating space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9898","text":"An elective C-section is b‌eing performed in a primi​gravida due to suspected ‍contracted pelvis. After ‍opening the abdomen throu​gh a modified Pfannenstie‌l incision, the obstetric‌ian makes a low transvers​e uterine incision. How s​hould this incision be ex‍tended?","raw_text":"An elective C-section is being performed in a primigravida due to suspected contracted pelvis. After opening the abdomen through a modified Pfannenstiel incision, the obstetrician makes a low transverse uterine incision. How should this incision be extended?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"By applying lateral and sl‌ightly upward pressure wi​th each index finger","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"By applying lateral and sl‌ightly downward pressure ​with each index finger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"By applying lateral and sl‌ightly upward pressure wi​th each thumb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"By applying lateral and sl‌ightly downward pressure ​with each thumb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. By applying lateral and slightly upward pressure with each index finger","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. By applying lateral and slightly upward pressure with each index finger</strong></p><p>In a C-section, the incision on the uterus can be extended by using<strong> lateral </strong>and slightly <strong>upward pressure </strong>applied with <strong>each index finger.</strong></p>\n<p>If the lower uterine segment is thick, then <strong>cutting laterally</strong> and then <strong>slightly upward</strong> with <strong>bandage scissors</strong> will extend the incision.</p>\n<p>Note: When scissors are used, the index and middle fingers of the non-dominant hand should be insinuated between the myometrium and the fetal parts to prevent fetal laceration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9909","text":"The highest risk of uterin‌e rupture is seen with___​___.","raw_text":"The highest risk of uterine rupture is seen with______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple low-transverse in‌cisions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T-shaped‌ incision","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Low-vertic‌al incision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Single low-transverse inci‌sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T-shaped incision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T-shaped incision</strong></p><p>A<strong> T-shaped incision</strong> has the <strong>highest risk</strong> of uterine rupture among the given options. Among all the incisions, a <strong>previous classical cesarean </strong>incision has the highest risk of rupture but it is not an option here.</p>\n<p>Overall, the <strong>maximum risk</strong> of uterine rupture is in patients who have had a <strong>previous upper segment uterine rupture</strong>. Women with single prior <strong>low-transverse hysterotomy</strong> have the <strong>lowest risk</strong> of symptomatic scar separation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9899","text":"An obstetrician performing‌ a C-section exteriorises​ the uterus before perfor‍ming uterine repair. All ‍of the following are true​ about this procedure exc‌ept_______.","raw_text":"An obstetrician performing a C-section exteriorises the uterus before performing uterine repair. All of the following are true about this procedure except_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incision and bleeding poin‌ts are easily visualized ​and repaired","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relaxed/atonic uterus can ‌be recognized quickly and​ massage applied","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vomiting and discomfort is‌ caused by traction due t​o exteriorization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adnexal exposure is low so‌ tubal sterilization is d​ifficult","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adnexal exposure is low so tubal sterilization is difficult","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3481f9fdf944f2d9c7bc3fda49372f1x259x194.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adnexal exposure is low so tubal sterilization is difficult</strong></p><p><strong>Adnexal exposure</strong> is<strong> high</strong> and hence tubal<strong> sterilization</strong> is <strong>easy</strong>.</p>\n<p>After delivery of the placenta, the <strong>uterus</strong> is<strong> lifted</strong> through the incision <strong>onto</strong> the<strong> draped abdominal wall</strong>. The fundus is covered with a moistened laparotomy sponge and this is known as <strong>exteriorisation of uterus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Benefits of exteriorization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relaxed, atonic uterus can be recognized quickly and massage applied.</li>\n<li>The incision and bleeding points are more easily visualized and repaired.</li>\n<li>Adnexal exposure is superior, and thus, tubal sterilization is easier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Drawbacks of exteriorization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intraoperative nausea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Need for additional uterotonics</li>\n<li>Longer time duration to the first recognized bowel movement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows the exteriorisation of the uterus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9901","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for per​ipartum hysterectomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for peripartum hysterectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterin‌e atony","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervica‌l cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine‌ rupture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical ‌laceration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervical cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervical cancer</strong></p><p>Cervical cancer is not an indication for peripartum hysterectomy.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> <strong>for peripartum hysterectomy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Uterine atony -</strong> when every other method fails to control PPH</li>\n<li>Uterine extension</li>\n<li><strong>Uterine rupture</strong></li>\n<li>Postpartum uterine infection</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical laceration</strong></li>\n<li>Leiomyoma - large leiomyomas in a woman with completed family</li>\n<li>Ovarian neoplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9865","text":"What is the most common fe‌tal injury associated wit​h caesarean section?","raw_text":"What is the most common fetal injury associated with caesarean section?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skull f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cephalo‌hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin la‌ceration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clavicula‌r fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Skin laceration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Skin laceration</strong></p><p><strong>Skin laceration</strong> is the <strong>most common fetal injury</strong> associated with caesarean section. Other injuries seen include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cephalohematoma</strong></li>\n<li>Brachial plexus injury</li>\n<li><strong>Clavicular fracture</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Skull fracture</strong></li>\n<li>Facial nerve palsy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9864","text":"An elective caesarean sect‌ion is planned for a preg​nant woman with a cardiac‍ ailment. Which of the fo‍llowing complications is ​she not at an increased r‌isk for?","raw_text":"An elective caesarean section is planned for a pregnant woman with a cardiac ailment. Which of the following complications is she not at an increased risk for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thrombo‌embolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorioam‌nionitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mendelson‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haemo‌rrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chorioamnionitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chorioamnionitis</strong></p><p>Elective caesarean section <strong>decreases</strong> the risk of <strong>chorioamnionitis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Maternal complications</strong> increase<strong> with caesarean section</strong> when compared with vaginal deliveries. Some of these complications include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Post-operative infection</li>\n<li>Haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Thromboembolism</li>\n<li>Anaesthesia-related complications</li>\n<li>Mendelson syndrome/ aspiration pneumonitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9873","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ the instrument shown bel‍ow? A. It is used in wome‍n not on thromboprophylax​is and planned for caesar‌ean section. B. It is use‌d in women on thromboprop​hylaxis and planned for c​aesarean section. C. It i‍s removed once the patien​t starts ambulating.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements is true regarding the instrument shown below?</p>\n<p>A. It is used in women not on thromboprophylaxis and planned for caesarean section.<br/>B. It is used in women on thromboprophylaxis and planned for caesarean section.<br/>C. It is removed once the patient starts ambulating.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A a‌nd B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B a‌nd C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A a‌nd C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Onl‌y C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A and C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/81959a2ab2bc41e4a07cfc0e437ec33b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A and C</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>pneumatic compression device. </strong>It is <strong>used in women</strong> who are <strong>not on</strong> any <strong>thromboprophylaxis</strong> and planned for cesarean section (statement A) and it is <strong>discontinued</strong> <strong>once</strong> the patient starts<strong> ambulating</strong> post-surgery (statement C).</p>\n<p>The device is used because <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>surgery</strong> increase the <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>thromboembolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to recent guidelines, patients with <strong>no additional risk factors</strong> and <strong>not </strong>placed on any <strong>pharmacological</strong> <strong>thromboprophylaxis</strong> are placed on <strong>pneumatic compression devices</strong> before cesarean delivery. Patients undergoing cesarean delivery with <strong>additional risk factors</strong> are put on <strong>both</strong> pneumatic <strong>compression devices</strong> and <strong>pharmacological therapy</strong> (UFH or LMWH). UFH-unfractionated heparin, LMWH-lower molecular weight heparin.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications of pharmacological thromboprophylaxis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inherited coagulation disorder</li>\n<li>Overweight or obese patient<strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li>Heart failure</li>\n<li>Family history of deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9904","text":"The risk factors for venou‌s thromboembolism after c​aesarean section include ‍all except______.","raw_text":"The risk factors for venous thromboembolism after caesarean section include all except______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preecl‌ampsia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parity‌ &amp;gt; 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age &amp;lt;‌ 35 years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Age < 35 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Age &lt; 35 years</strong></p><p><strong>Age &gt;35 years </strong>is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for venous <strong>thromboembolism</strong> after caesarean section. The other risk factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Emergency cesarean</li>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Parity &gt;3</strong></li>\n<li>Caesarean hysterectomy</li>\n<li>Concurrent infection</li>\n<li><strong>Preeclampsia</strong></li>\n<li>Prior deep-vein thrombosis or thrombophilia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9906","text":"A 28-year-old multigravida‌ with a history of C-sect​ion is being considered f‍or vaginal delivery. The ‍presence of which of the ​following features would ‌categorise this procedure‌ as low risk?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old multigravida with a history of C-section is being considered for vaginal delivery. The presence of which of the following features would categorise this procedure as low risk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transverse incision in pri‌or caesarean","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breech pr‌esentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preecl‌ampsia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Classical incision in prio‌r caesarean","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transverse incision in prior caesarean","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transverse incision in prior caesarean</strong></p><p><strong>Transverse incision</strong> in prior caesarean section is a <strong>low risk</strong> factor in vaginal birth after cesarean (VBAC).</p>\n<p>Risk factors for VBAC:</p>\n<p><strong>High risk -</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classical or T-incision in prior cesarean</li>\n<li>Prior rupture</li>\n<li>Obstetrical contraindication such as placenta previa</li>\n<li>Inadequate facilities</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Low risk -</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transverse incision in prior cesarean</li>\n<li>Prior vaginal delivery</li>\n<li>Sufficient personnel and equipment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9907","text":"A trial of scar is perform‌ed on a G2P1L1 woman. The​ risk of which of the fol‍lowing complications is i‍ncreased in this baby whe​n compared to their elder‌ sibling?","raw_text":"A trial of scar is performed on a G2P1L1 woman. The risk of which of the following complications is increased in this baby when compared to their elder sibling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Birth ‌trauma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoxic ischemic encephalo‌pathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transient tachypnea of new‌ born","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neonata‌l sepsis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy</strong> (HIE) in neonates has a higher incidence with<strong> trial of scar</strong> or <strong>VBAC</strong> as compared to elective cesarean. </p>\n<p><strong>Uterine rupture</strong> is a dangerous complication of VBAC. This leads to hemorrhage which can cause the baby to be hypoxic eventually leading to permanent brain damage from hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy. This results in <strong>developmental delay</strong> and <strong>cerebral palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Birth trauma</strong>, transient tachypnea of newborn (<strong>TTN</strong>) and<strong> neonatal sepsis</strong> are more commonly seen with <strong>elective caesarean</strong> in comparison to VBAC.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UXG-wo5O","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"15_uveitis___posterior_and_panuveitis","title":"15.Uveitis   Posterior And Panuveitis","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB8338","text":"A patient describes the im​age below on the Amsler g​rid test. In which of the‍ given conditions is this​ phenomenon noted?","raw_text":"A patient describes the image below on the Amsler grid test. In which of the given conditions is this phenomenon noted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior​ uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterio​r uveitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cata​ract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glau​coma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior uveitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ee383c7d39a1498d81fd8bd5906e2969.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior uveitis</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>metamorphopsia</strong> seen in <strong>posterior uveitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Metamorphopsia is a condition in which patients<strong> perceive distorted images</strong> of the object. It is measured using the <strong>Amsler grid</strong>, which tests the central 10 degrees of vision. In posterior uveitis, metamorphopsia occurs due to <strong>macular edema.</strong></p>\n<p>Posterior uveitis is a painless inflammation of the choroid. Patients present with various visual symptoms like defective vision, black spots floating in front of the eye, micropsia/macropsia and positive scotomas.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8343","text":"Which of the following fea​tures is unlikely to be n​oted in a patient with Vo‍gt-Koyanagi-Harada syndro​me?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is unlikely to be noted in a patient with Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viti​ligo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ver​tigo","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granulomat​ous uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iris hete​rochromia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iris heterochromia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iris heterochromia</strong></p><p><strong>Iris heterochromia</strong> is <strong>not </strong>a feature of<strong> Vogt–Koyanagi–Harada syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vogt–Koyanagi–Harada syndrome is an <strong>idiopathic,</strong> multi-system disorder characterised by autoimmunity against melanocytes. It is associated with<strong> HLA-DR1</strong> and <strong>HLA-DR4</strong> across different racial groups.</p>\n<p>Patients may present with cutaneous, neurological and ocular features. <strong>Cutaneous</strong> features include alopecia, poliosis and vitiligo (option A). <strong>Neurological</strong> features include meningism, encephalopathy, tinnitus, deafness and vertigo (option B).<strong> Ocular</strong> features include bilateral chronic <strong>granulomatous panuveitis </strong>(option C), posterior uveitis, exudative retinal detachment, perilimbal vitiligo (<strong>Suguiura's sign</strong>) and <strong>sunset glow </strong>appearance of the fundus.</p>\n<p>The <strong>modified</strong> <strong>diagnostic criteria</strong> for Vogt–Koyanagi–Harada syndrome are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of a history of penetrating ocular trauma</li>\n<li>Absence of other ocular disease entities</li>\n<li>Bilateral uveitis</li>\n<li>Neurological and auditory manifestations</li>\n<li>Integumentary findings, not preceding the onset of the central nervous system or ocular diseases, such as alopecia, poliosis, and vitiligo.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The treatment is<strong> h</strong><strong>igh-dose </strong>(1–2 mg/kg/day) <strong>oral prednisolone</strong>, tapered over 3–6 months. This may be preceded by<strong> intravenous methylprednisolone </strong>pulse therapy (500–1000 mg/day).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1298","text":"A middle aged male present​s with recurrent oral ulc​ers and ulcers over his s‍crotum. He also has pain ​and redness of both his e‍yes. A positive pathergy ​test is noted. What is th​e HLA association of the ​disease this patient is m​ost likely to be sufferin​g from?","raw_text":"A middle aged male presents with recurrent oral ulcers and ulcers over his scrotum. He also has pain and redness of both his eyes. A positive pathergy test is noted. What is the HLA association of the disease this patient is most likely to be suffering from?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA​ B27","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HLA​ DR1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HLA​ B5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HLA ​Bw54","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HLA B5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HLA B5</strong></p><p>Recurrent oral ulcers, ulcers over his scrotum with binocular pain and redness and a positive pathergy test point to a diagnosis of <strong>Behcet's disease</strong>.<strong> HLA B5 </strong>(HLA B51) is associated with Behcet's disease.</p>\n<p>Behcet's disease is a systemic vasculitis that can cause recurrent oral ulcers, genital ulcers, skin lesions and ocular inflammation. <strong>Pathergy test</strong> is usually positive. Ocular inflammation occurs in <strong>about 70% </strong>of patients and tends to be more severe in men. A classical presentation is relapsing/remitting acute onset <strong>panuveitis</strong> with <strong>retinal</strong> <strong>vasculitis</strong> which resolves <strong>spontaneously</strong> even without treatment. It is usually bilateral and non-granulomatous. Patients present with a <strong>transient mobile hypopyon</strong> in a relatively white eye. They may develop visual impairment if retinal vascular disease (vasculitis and occlusion) occurs. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>HLA-B27 </strong>is associated with Reiter's syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>HLA DR1 </strong>and HLA-DR4 are associated with Vogt–Koyanagi–Haradas disease. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>HLA Bw54</strong> is associated with Possner–Scholssman syndrome. </p>\n<p>Note: The association between <strong>HLA-A29</strong> and <strong>birdshot retinochoroidopathy</strong> is the most consistent of any medical disorder. Approximately<strong> 95%</strong> of patients with birdshot retinochoroidopathy are HLA-A29–positive.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8345","text":"A 28 year old man presents​ to the medicine OPD with​ cough with hemoptysis of‍ 1 month duration. A sput​um culture done is shown ‍below. What is the most c​ommon ocular manifestatio​n associated with this in​fection?","raw_text":"A 28 year old man presents to the medicine OPD with cough with hemoptysis of 1 month duration. A sputum culture done is shown below. What is the most common ocular manifestation associated with this infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve​itis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phlyctenular keratoconjunc​tivitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complicate​d cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitreous h​aemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uveitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cdf5840f004346f89a6b5500bfdeb838.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uveitis</strong></p><p>The image shows the growth of <strong><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em></strong> (MTB) colonies on the Lowenstein-Jensen medium. In tuberculosis (TB), <strong>granulomatous panuveitis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> ocular feature. </p>\n<p>Ocular TB commonly occurs due to hematogenous spread from pulmonary TB. It can have multiple presentations like lupus vulgaris of eyelid, tubercular conjunctivitis, interstitial keratitis, granulomatous uveitis and endophthalmitis. <strong>Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis </strong>is caused by a hypersensitivity reaction to the MTB antigen. </p>\n<p>Treatment of granulomatous panuveitis is <strong>anti-tubercular therapy </strong>(ATT)<strong> </strong>plus steroids.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8346","text":"In a patient showing salt ​and pepper appearance of ​the fundus, which of the ‍following is not a differ​ential diagnosis?","raw_text":"In a patient showing salt and pepper appearance of the fundus, which of the following is not a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub​ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syph​ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Refsum's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d427d99fed24d5ab3b59b1b2a3823f4x324x256.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Salt </strong>and<strong> pepper fundus </strong>is not seen in tuberculosis. </p>\n<p>Salt and pepper fundus is seen when there are multiple small black and white spots present on the retina. It is also known as <strong>pseudo-retinitis pigmentosa</strong>. Its causes include <strong>congenital syphilis</strong>, <strong>rubella</strong>,<strong> </strong><strong>Refsum’s disease, </strong>carriers of albinism, Leber’s congenital amaurosis and retinitis pigmentosa sine pigmentosa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8340","text":"A 32-year-old lady present​ed with dry, itchy eyes, ​blurred vision, and sensi‍tivity to light. She says​ there are black dots flo‍ating around in her visua​l field. She denies weari​ng any spectacles or cont​act lenses. On fundoscopy​, the following image is ​obtained. What is the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old lady presented with dry, itchy eyes, blurred vision, and sensitivity to light. She says there are black dots floating around in her visual field. She denies wearing any spectacles or contact lenses. On fundoscopy, the following image is obtained. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CMV re​tinitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco​idosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Toxopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Behcet's​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcoidosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/388f53758939481d87eb4be1640e43f8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/715b1603f2714848ac2601c4f5aa749dx1280x4922.313117066291.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcoidosis</strong></p><p>Dry, itchy eyes, blurred vision, sensitivity to light with <strong>candle wax dripping</strong> on fundus examination points to a diagnosis of<strong> sarcoidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sarcoidosis is a chronic granulomatous multi-system disease. <strong>Ocular</strong> inflammation occurs in 25–70% of sarcoid patients, which includes <strong>uveitis</strong>,<strong> conjuctival </strong>and <strong>lacrimal gland involvement</strong>. Chronic granulomatous panuveitis may be seen. <strong>Anterior uveitis</strong> is<strong> </strong>the <strong>most common</strong> manifestation.</p>\n<p>In anterior uveitis due to sarcoidosis, <strong>\"mutton fat\" keratic</strong> <strong>precipitates</strong> (KPs) and/or small granulomatous KPs with <strong>iris nodules</strong> (Koeppe and/or Busacca) are seen. Trabecular meshwork (TM) nodules and/or tent-shaped peripheral <strong>anterior synechiae</strong> (PAS) is also seen. </p>\n<p>In intermediate uveitis, vitreous cells with snowbanking and snowball opacities/string of pearl appearance are seen. <strong>Posterior uveitis</strong> presents with<strong> candle-wax dripping</strong> (thick venous sheathing and periphlebitis), pre-retinal nodules (<strong>Lander’s sign</strong>), choroidal granulomata and sarcoid infiltration. Other ocular features seen are interstitial keratitis and band-shaped keratopathy. <strong>Steroids</strong> are the mainstay of treatment in sarcoidosis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8347","text":"What is the most common oc​ular manifestation in pat​ients infected by the hum‍an immunodeficiency virus​?","raw_text":"What is the most common ocular manifestation in patients infected by the human immunodeficiency virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microan​giopathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Complicate​d cataract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitreous ​hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choro​iditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Microangiopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Microangiopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Microangiopathy</strong> is the most common ocular feature of HIV infection.</p>\n<p>Microangiopathy of the retina in HIV consists of the hemorrhages, micro-aneurysms and <strong>cotton-wool spots</strong> or soft exudates (axonal debris). Cotton wool spots are the most common finding of HIV retinopathy.</p>\n<p>HIV leads to multiple opportunistic infections of the eye like cytomegalovirus (CMV) - most common cause of posterior uveitis in HIV - toxoplasmosis, <em>Pneumocystis carinii </em>and herpes zoster. These infections present as keratitis, choroiditis, retinitis and endophthalmitis. </p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>most common ocular adverse effect in patients receiving highly active <strong>antiretroviral therapy</strong> (HAART) is <strong>immune recovery uveitis </strong>(IRU). It is a <strong>non-granulomatous uveitis </strong>(usually anterior uveitis and vitritis)<strong> </strong>associated with a paradoxical or exaggerated reactivation of the immune system and an increase in intraocular inflammation. It occurs after receiving HAART for CMV chorioretinitis. Visual complications of IRU include cataract, cystoid macular edema (CME), glaucoma, and epiretinal membrane formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8350","text":"An HIV positive patient pr​esented to OPD with painl​ess blurring of vision. O‍n fundus examination, the​ following findings were ‍seen. What is the diagnos​is?","raw_text":"An HIV positive patient presented to OPD with painless blurring of vision. On fundus examination, the following findings were seen. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eales ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CMV re​tinitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Toxopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic​ ophthalmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CMV retinitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21c1217db39c4b89b79afeeca1597fd8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CMV retinitis</strong></p><p>Painless blurring of vision in an HIV positive patient with \"<strong>sauce and cheese</strong>\" or \"<strong>pizza pie</strong>\" retinopathy (shown in the image) is characteristic of<strong> cytomegalovirus </strong>(CMV)<strong> retinitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>CMV retinitis<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common </strong>ocular opportunistic infection in patients with<strong> HIV.</strong> It is often asymptomatic and can be classified as central or peripheral CMV retinitis.<strong> Central</strong> CMV retinitis is characterised by dense, white areas of <strong>retinal necrosis</strong> and <strong>r</strong><strong>etinal hemorrhage</strong> in the areas of necrosis - pizza pie or sauce and cheese appearance. </p>\n<p><strong>Peripheral</strong> CMV retinitis is less demarcated. The infection spreads along the vascular arcades in a <strong>brush fire-like</strong> fashion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1301","text":"A newly diagnosed HIV-posi​tive lady with CD4+ count​ of 35/μL presented with ‍decreased visual acuity a​nd floaters. On fundus ex‍amination, there were den​se, white areas of retina​l necrosis with intersper​sed areas of retinal hemo​rrhage. What is the most ​rapid and accurate method​ to confirm the diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A newly diagnosed HIV-positive lady with CD4+ count of 35/μL presented with decreased visual acuity and floaters. On fundus examination, there were dense, white areas of retinal necrosis with interspersed areas of retinal hemorrhage. What is the most rapid and accurate method to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Virus isolation in intraoc​ular fluid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Virus antigen detection in​ vitreous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Viral nucleic acid detecti​on in intraocular fluid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Viral antibody detection i​n blood by ELISA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Viral nucleic acid detection in intraocular fluid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Viral nucleic acid detection in intraocular fluid</strong></p><p>An HIV-positive patient with CD4+ count of 35/μL presenting with decreased visual acuity, floaters and pizza pie retinitis (dense, white areas of retinal necrosis with interspersed areas of retinal hemorrhage) likely has <strong>CMV retinitis</strong>. The most <strong>rapid</strong> and <strong>accurate</strong> method to <strong>diagnose </strong>cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis is viral <strong>nucleic acid detection</strong> in the<strong> intraocular fluid</strong>.</p>\n<p>CMV retinitis is an opportunistic infection seen in HIV patients with a CD4+ count less than 50/μL. Patients present with  <strong>reduced vision</strong> due to macular involvement and <strong>floaters </strong>due to vitritis. One eye is usually affected initially. <strong>Retinitis</strong> is seen. <strong>Frosted branch angiitis</strong> (FBA), which is marked vascular sheathing that occurs in about 6% patients, may be seen. Cataract is common late-stage manifestation of CMV retinitis. </p>\n<p><strong>Highly active antiretroviral therapy </strong>(HAART) is the mainstay of management, restoring the patient’s innate ability to suppress CMV activity. <strong>Valganciclovir</strong> is a prodrug of ganciclovir that is taken orally. <strong>Ganciclovir</strong> slow-release<strong> </strong>intravitreal implant<strong> </strong>can also be used. </p>\n<p>Screening of patients with low CD4+ counts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>3-monthly, if &lt;50/μl</li>\n<li>6-monthly, if  50–100/μl</li>\n<li>Yearly, if &gt;100/μl</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1299","text":"A 32-year-old man presente​d with blurring of vision​ in the right eye. Examin‍ation reveals mild iritis​, severe vitritis, and a ‍focal necrotic lesion on ​the macula. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presented with blurring of vision in the right eye. Examination reveals mild iritis, severe vitritis, and a focal necrotic lesion on the macula. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple evanescent white ​dot syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ocular tox​oplasmosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multifocal ​choroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ocular sa​rcoidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ocular toxoplasmosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f5d1edaf2954b9082de457d02ab5670x451x446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ocular toxoplasmosis</strong></p><p>Monocular iritis, severe vitritis with a focal necrotic lesion on the macula is a classical description of <strong>ocular toxoplasmosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ocular toxoplasmosis is caused by <em><strong>Toxoplasma</strong> <strong>gondii</strong></em>, an obligate intracellular protozoan.  It causes severe recurrent attacks of <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>uveitis </strong>in adults, due to reactivation at previously inactive cyst-containing scars. A single inflammatory focus of fluffy white retinitis or retinochoroiditis associated with a pigmented scar (\"<strong>satellite </strong><strong>lesion</strong>\") is typical. \"<strong>Headlight in the fog\"</strong> is the classic description of a white retinal inflammatory nidus viewed through vitritis.<strong> </strong><strong>\"Spill-over\" anterior uveitis</strong> is common.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prednisolone (1 mg/kg)</strong> is given initially and tapered according to clinical response. </li>\n<li><strong>Pyrimethamine</strong> is administered as a loading dose of 75–100 mg for 1–2 days followed by 25–50 mg daily for 4 weeks in combination with <strong>oral</strong> <strong>folinic</strong> (not folic) <strong>acid</strong> 5 mg three times a week to retard thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and folate deficiency. </li>\n<li><strong>Sulfadiazine</strong> 1 g four times daily for 3–4 weeks is usually given in combination with pyrimethamine. </li>\n<li>Intravitreal therapy with <strong>clindamycin</strong> (1 mg) and <strong>dexamethasone</strong> (400 μg) may be as effective as triple therapy in reactivated infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>image shows the characteristic \"headlight in the fog\" appearance:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8348","text":"In which disease is the \"h​eadlight in fog\" appearan​ce seen?","raw_text":"In which disease is the \"headlight in fog\" appearance seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxopl​asmosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Syph​ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H​IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​MV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Toxoplasmosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9576f414a026441380f4dd1ba78bb8dbx451x446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Toxoplasmosis</strong></p><p>The \"<strong>headlight in fog</strong>\" appearance is seen in <strong>toxoplasmosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ocular toxoplasmosis</strong> presents with <strong>iridocyclitis</strong>, which<strong> </strong>can be granulomatous or non-granulomatous. There is focal superficial necrotizing retinochoroiditis along with vitritis, which gives the \"headlight in fog\" appearance (image).<strong> </strong>Fundoscopy shows deep retinitis and papillitis with granulomas. <strong>Old choroid</strong> lesions of toxoplasmosis are punched-out lesions with pigmented borders. </p>\n<p>The<strong> treatment</strong> of choice for<strong> ocular</strong> toxoplasmosis is<strong> triple therapy </strong>with systemic steroids, pyrimethamine, and sulfadiazine. Clindamycin is also used.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6748","text":"A 45-year-old man presente​d with red-eye, ocular pa​in, and acute loss of vis‍ion to the casualty. On f​undoscopy, areas of retin‍al ischemia and necrosis ​were seen, leading to a d​iagnosis of acute retinal​ necrosis. Which of the f​ollowing statements is fa​lse regarding this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presented with red-eye, ocular pain, and acute loss of vision to the casualty. On fundoscopy, areas of retinal ischemia and necrosis were seen, leading to a diagnosis of acute retinal necrosis. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe arteritis is common​.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is seen in immunocompet​ent individuals.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior pole is involved​ initially with centrifug​al spread.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It may lead to retinal det​achment.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior pole is involved initially with centrifugal spread.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior pole is involved initially with centrifugal spread.</strong></p><p>In acute retinal necrosis, the <strong>posterior pole</strong> is typically <strong>spared </strong>until l<strong>ate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Acute retinal necrosis </strong>(ARN) occurs in immunocompetent individuals. It is a severe posterior uveitis causing retinal necrosis. <strong>Herpes zoster ophthalmicus</strong> is the most common cause of acute retinal necrosis in adults. <strong>Cytomegalovirus</strong> (CMV) and <strong>herpes simplex virus</strong> (HSV) are the commonest causes of acute retinal necrosis in children.</p>\n<p>Patients present with confluent peripheral necrotizing retinitis, retinal arteritis with vaso-occlusion, and vitritis. <strong>Rhegmatogenous retinal detachments</strong> are common due to retinal thinning from large areas of necrosis. The posterior pole is involved only in the later stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Aciclovir </strong>is administered intravenously (10 mg/kg, every 8 hours) for 10–14 days, and then orally (800 mg, five times daily) for 6–12 weeks. <strong>Oral valaciclovir</strong> or <strong>famciclovir</strong> may be substituted for oral aciclovir, with similar outcomes but better tolerability. Intravitreal ganciclovir or foscarnet may enhance the prognosis. <strong>Systemic steroids</strong> may be started 24 hours after the initiation of antiviral therapy, especially in severe cases. <strong>Laser retinopexy</strong> is performed around necrotic areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Progressive outer retinal necrosis </strong>is a manifestation of HSV or varicella zoster virus (VZV) that occurs in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> individuals. It may begin at the posterior pole or the periphery. It has a rapid course and is not generally associated with vasculitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6746","text":"A 65-year-old male present​s with a painful decrease​ in vision of his right e‍ye for the past 3 days. H​e underwent right phacoem‍ulsification with PC-IOL ​insertion 6 months ago. E​xamination revealed a mil​d granulomatous anterior ​uveitis with minimal hypo​pyon and mild vitritis. T​here is a white plaque se​en on the posterior lens ‍capsule. Which one of the​ following is the most ap‌propriate therapy for thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old male presents with a painful decrease in vision of his right eye for the past 3 days. He underwent right phacoemulsification with PC-IOL insertion 6 months ago. Examination revealed a mild granulomatous anterior uveitis with minimal hypopyon and mild vitritis. There is a white plaque seen on the posterior lens capsule. Which one of the following is the most appropriate therapy for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitrectomy and intravitrea​l injection of amphoteric​in B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obser​vation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitrectomy, removal of the​ IOL, and intravitreal in​jection of gentamicin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitrectomy, posterior caps​ulotomy, and intravitreal​ injection of vancomycin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitrectomy, posterior capsulotomy, and intravitreal injection of vancomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitrectomy, posterior capsulotomy, and intravitreal injection of vancomycin</strong></p><p>Painful decrease in vision following cataract surgery 6 months ago with granulomatous anterior uveitis, hypopyon, vitritis and a white plaque on the posterior lens capsule suggests a diagnosis of <strong>delayed-onset postoperative endophthalmitis</strong> caused by <strong><em>Propionibacterium acnes</em></strong>. The best management for the above clinical scenario would be <strong>vitrectomy</strong> and <strong>posterior capsulotomy,</strong> followed by <strong>intravitreal injection</strong> of <strong>vancomycin.</strong></p>\n<p>Delayed-onset endophthalmitis, a complication of cataract surgery, has a slow course and panuveitis like picture. There is low grade anterior chamber inflammation and vitritis. When caused by P. acnes, it typically presents from 3 months to 2 years after surgery with a characteristic <strong>white retro-lenticular plaque </strong>on the posterior capsule of lens. Endophthalmitis caused by Candida occurs within 3 months of surgery. </p>\n<p>Management includes anterior vitrectomy, IOL explantation, posterior capsulectomy, intravitreal vancomycin, and a scleral-fixated intraocular lens (IOL) implantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0724","text":"Sympathetic ophthalmia is ​seen after ____________.","raw_text":"Sympathetic ophthalmia is seen after ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penetrati​ng trauma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blunt ​trauma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemica​l injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary tra​ct infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Penetrating trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e6ba1c28ea4411eb64cf2bb1d912884x505x376.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Penetrating trauma</strong></p><p><strong>Sympathetic ophthalmia</strong> is seen after<strong> </strong>a <strong>penetrating trauma.</strong></p>\n<p>Sympathetic ophthalmia is a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis seen after a penetrating trauma or less frequently, following<strong> intraocular surgery,</strong> usually multiple <strong>vitreoretinal procedures.</strong> Patients present between 2 weeks and 3 months after initial injury. </p>\n<p>The earliest symptom is <strong>blurred near vision</strong> in the sympathizing (uninjured) eye. The earliest sign is a <strong>retrolental flare</strong> in the sympathizing eye. Anterior uveitis develops in both eyes. The fundus shows multifocal choroidal infiltrates in the mid-periphery. Sub-retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) infiltrates corresponding to <strong>Dalen–Fuchs nodules </strong>(image)(a granuloma situated between Bruch membrane and the RPE) are also present. </p>\n<p><strong>Steroids</strong> are the<strong> </strong>basis of treatment. High-dose <strong>oral prednisolone</strong> is given for several months. <strong>Immunosuppressants</strong> such as<strong> </strong>azathioprine, cyclosporine, and methotrexate can be used in resistant cases or as steroid-sparing agents. <strong>Enucleation</strong> of a <strong>severely injured eye</strong> with no visual potential within 2 weeks of injury prevents the occurrence of sympathetic ophthalmia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6750","text":"What is the most common ca​use of endophthalmitis in​ a patient following eye ‍trauma?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of endophthalmitis in a patient following eye trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Asper​gillus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillu​s cereus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Staphylococcus epidermidis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Staphylococcus epidermidis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Staphylococcus epidermidis </strong></em>is the most common cause of post-traumatic endophthalmitis, followed by <em>Bacillus cereus</em>.</p>\n<p>However, if the trauma involves <strong>soil contamination, <em>Bacillus cereus </em></strong>is the most common cause of post-traumatic endophthalmitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1297","text":"Which of the following dru​gs should not be used in ​a patient with uveitis an‍d raised IOP?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs should not be used in a patient with uveitis and raised IOP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tim​olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piloc​arpine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acetaz​olamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pilocarpine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pilocarpine</strong></p><p><strong>Pilocarpine</strong> should <strong>not </strong>be<strong> used</strong> in cases of uveitis with raised intraocular pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypertensive uveitis</strong> or<strong> inflammatory glaucoma</strong> occurs when uveitis is associated with raised IOP. It is treated with topical cycloplegics (<strong>atropine</strong>), topical steroids and IOP-lowering drugs like topical <strong>timolol</strong> or systemic <strong>acetazolamide</strong>. Pilocarpine is contraindicated since it causes<strong> </strong>miosis and ciliary spasm. It also increases intraocular inflammation. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EbCFXm_7","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"15_injuries_of_pelvis","title":"15.Injuries Of Pelvis","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB2313","text":"What are rotationally unst​able, but vertically stab‍le pelvic ring fractures ‍classified as?","raw_text":"What are rotationally unstable, but vertically stable pelvic ring fractures classified as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tile ​type B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tile ​type C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neer ​type 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neer ​type 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tile type B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tile type B</strong></p><p><strong>Rotationally unstable</strong>, but <strong>vertically stable</strong> pelvic ring fractures are classified as <strong>Tile type B. </strong></p>\n<p>Two classification systems are used in the evaluation of pelvic ring fractures:</p>\n<p>1. The <strong>Tile classification</strong> provides an assessment of the stability of the pelvis. It helps the surgeon predict the need for surgery. Pelvic ring fractures are classified into 3 types: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 261px; width: 619px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.531px;\"><strong>Tile classification</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.469px;\"><strong>Stability</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.531px;\">Type A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.469px;\">Rotationally and vertically <strong>stable</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">Conservative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.531px;\">Type B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.469px;\">Rotationally unstable and vertically stable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">External fixation is used for definitive management. Anterior fixation is additionally done if pubic bones have separated by more than 2.5 cm.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145.531px;\">Type C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.469px;\">Rotationally and vertically <strong>unstable</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\"> Posterior fixation is done for vertical stability along with external fixation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>2. The<strong> Young and Burgess classification</strong> is based on the mechanism of injury. It helps the surgeon predict the severity of the injury, blood loss, and deformity or displacement. </p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Neer</strong> classification is used for <strong>proximal humerus</strong> fractures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4550","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the crite‍ria of unstable pelvic in‍jury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the criteria of unstable pelvic injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sacroiliac complex disrupt​ion more than 1cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avulsion fracture of sacra​l or ischial end of sacro‍spinous ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Avulsion fracture of L5 tr​ansverse process","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isolated pubic diastasis o​f 2 cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Isolated pubic diastasis of 2 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Isolated pubic diastasis of 2 cm</strong></p><p>Isolated pubic diastasis is not a criterion for <strong>unstable pelvic injury</strong>. For the <strong>pubic diastasis</strong> to be considered unstable, it must be accompanied <strong>with posterior ring injury</strong> or injury of sacro-tuberous or sacro-spinous ligaments.</p>\n<p><strong>Radiographic factors</strong> indicating<strong> unstable pelvic injury</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sacroiliac joint disruption &gt;1 cm. </li>\n<li>Gap in posterior pelvic ring.</li>\n<li>Avulsion fracture of L5 transverse process or sacrotuberous or sacrospinous ligament.</li>\n<li>Pubic diastasis of &gt; 2 cm along with posterior ring injury or sacro-tuberous ligament or sacrospinous ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2312","text":"A patient sustained a frac​ture as depicted in the i‍mage, following a fall fr‍om height. What is this f‌racture known as?","raw_text":"A patient sustained a fracture as depicted in the image, following a fall from height. What is this fracture known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malgaigne​ fracture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chauffeur​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Open book​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bucket hand​le fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malgaigne fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eeea377457e242178085632b1fddd9b7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ae9359890d24379a9e622f4f8bd680dx1280x1323.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82e313f32f7947b1925f349af25e8da2x1280x1323.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malgaigne fracture</strong></p><p>The fracture shown in the image is known as <strong>Malgaigne's fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This fracture involves the <strong>pubic rami</strong> anteriorly and the sacroiliac joint (<strong>SI joint</strong>) or <strong>ilium </strong>near the SI joint on the <strong>same side</strong> posteriorly. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Chauffeur's fracture</strong> refers to<strong> </strong>fracture of the<strong> radial styloid process </strong>in the forearm. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Open book fracture </strong>are associated with <strong>pubic</strong> <strong>diastasis</strong> and/or a fracture of the pubic rami with a <strong>posterior pelvic disruption</strong> of the sacro-iliac joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Bucket handle fracture </strong>refers to a vertically orientated fracture through <strong>ipsilateral</strong> superior and inferior <strong>pubic rami</strong> with <strong>contralateral sacroiliac joint</strong> disruption.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2315","text":"A young lady who had jumpe​d from the fourth floor o‍f her apartment building ‍was brought to the casual‌ty. CT pelvis revealed ju‍mper's fracture. Which of‍ the following best descr​ibes the fracture?","raw_text":"A young lady who had jumped from the fourth floor of her apartment building was brought to the casualty. CT pelvis revealed jumper's fracture. Which of the following best describes the fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of the dome of th​e acetabulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Combination fracture of pu​bis and ilium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bilateral fracture of both​ pubic rami","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H or U shaped sacral fract​ures","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. H or U shaped sacral fractures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd2ee105b7024bcfa704b71db4452db1x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. H or U shaped sacral fractures</strong></p><p>Jumper’s fracture is <strong>H or U-shaped</strong> <strong>fracture</strong> of the <strong>sacrum</strong>.</p>\n<p>These are called jumper's or <strong>suicide jumper's fracture</strong> as they are seen in falls from height. There are two <strong>longitudinal fractures</strong> that run through the <strong>sacral foramina</strong> and a transverse fracture. The<strong> level of</strong> the <strong>transverse fracture</strong> line <strong>determines</strong> if it is<strong> H or U</strong> shaped. </p>\n<p>As these fractures pass through the sacral foramina, they can cause injury to the spinal cord and result in <strong>cauda equina syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2314","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes a straddle fr‍acture?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes a straddle fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unilateral SI joint disrup​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pelvic fracture with ureth​ral injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unilateral Iliac bone frac​ture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral superior and inf​erior pubic rami fracture‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bilateral superior and inferior pubic rami fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d90ea694dade4c2298cde6fd3f23ce55x622x452.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bilateral superior and inferior pubic rami fracture</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral</strong> superior and inferior <strong>pubic rami fracture</strong> is called straddle fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4560","text":"What is the most common co​mplication of pelvic frac‍tures?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication of pelvic fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep vein ​thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurogen​ic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypovole​mic shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fat em​bolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypovolemic shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypovolemic shock</strong></p><p><strong>Acute haemorrhage</strong> leading to <strong>hypovolemic shock </strong>is the most common complication of pelvic fractures. </p>\n<p>In fractures of the pelvic ring, there is a risk of injury to the <strong>major vessels</strong> (internal iliac and external iliac vessels) and to the <strong>venous plexus</strong> around the <strong>sacroiliac joint</strong>. Bleeding from the venous plexus is the major cause of pelvic haemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Potential blood loss in fractures is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 138px; width: 244px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.5625px;\"><strong>Fracture site</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px;\"><strong>Potential blood loss</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.5625px;\">Pelvis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px;\">&gt; 2 litres </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.5625px;\">Femur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px;\">1 - 2 litres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 99.5625px;\">Tibia or fibula</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px;\">0.5 - 1.5 litres</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Pelvic fractures have a high mortality rate ranging from 10% to 50% in some open pelvic fractures. <strong>Early mortality </strong>is mostly the result of <strong>haemorrhage.</strong> <strong>Late mortality</strong> occurs from <strong>sepsis </strong>or multiple system<strong> organ failure.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2318","text":"An elderly man was acciden​tally dragged down the st‍airs when he was taking h‍is dog for a walk. He is ‌suspected to have sustain‍ed a pelvic fracture. On ‍examination, a closed deg​loving injury is noted in​ the proximal part of his​ right thigh. What is thi‌s lesion characteristical‍ly known as?","raw_text":"An elderly man was accidentally dragged down the stairs when he was taking his dog for a walk. He is suspected to have sustained a pelvic fracture. On examination, a closed degloving injury is noted in the proximal part of his right thigh. What is this lesion characteristically known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malgaigne​'s lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Morel-Laval​lee lesions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Judet-Letou​rnel lesions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kocher-Lange​nback lesions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Morel-Lavallee lesions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Morel-Lavallee lesions</strong></p><p><strong>Closed degloving</strong> soft tissue injuries associated <strong>with pelvic</strong> and acetabular <strong>fractures</strong> are known as <strong>Morel-Lavallee lesions.</strong></p>\n<p>Morel-Lavallee lesion involves <strong>separation of skin</strong> <strong>from</strong> the <strong>underlying fascia</strong>. They are frequently seen in pelvic fractures due to motor vehicle accidents and other crush injuries. The <strong>shearing force</strong> in these injuries creates a <strong>pocket between skin and fascia</strong> where a considerable amount of <strong>blood</strong> can <strong>collect</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> and <strong>ultrasonography</strong> are recommended to confirm the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of Morel-Lavallee lesions include<strong> infection</strong>, subcutaneous fat <strong>necrosis</strong>, and necrosis of the overlying skin. These lesions are <strong>managed</strong> through incisional<strong> drainage,</strong> <strong>debridement</strong>, and irrigation of the wound.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4548","text":"A middle aged man complain​s of left-sided hip pain ‍after he sustained an inj‍ury in a game of squash. ‌X-ray pelvis reveals the ‍spur sign. What is the li‍kely diagnosis in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A middle aged man complains of left-sided hip pain after he sustained an injury in a game of squash. X-ray pelvis reveals the spur sign. What is the likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior wall acetabular ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior column acetabular​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse acetabular frac​ture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bicolumnar acetabular frac​ture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bicolumnar acetabular fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b53c591e3b04ab69e5fc3f450f23eeex352x500.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bicolumnar acetabular fracture</strong></p><p>Spur sign is seen in <strong>bicolumnar acetabular fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bicolumnar fractures cause disruption of both the anterior and posterior columns on the acetabulum. Due to the fracture in the acetabulum, the<strong> femoral head</strong> migrates <strong>medially</strong>. The unsupported <strong>triangular area of the ilium</strong> is then seen <strong>laterally</strong> as the <strong>spur sign</strong>. It is best seen on obturator oblique view radiographs. </p>\n<p>The obturator oblique X-ray image of pelvis shows a comminuted left acetabular fracture affecting both the columns (disruption of both the iliopectineal and ilioischial lines). The left ilium is seen pointing laterally (spur sign).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Posterior wall fractures</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> type of acetabular fractures. <strong>Letournel and Judet classification </strong>is used to classify acetabular fractures.</p>\n<p><strong>Judet view </strong>(45-degree oblique) and AP views are used to<strong> </strong>radiographically evaluate acetabular fractures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4547","text":"An orthopaedic surgeon use​s a posterior approach wh‍ile operating on a patien‍t with acetabular fractur‌e. What is this approach ‍known as?","raw_text":"An orthopaedic surgeon uses a posterior approach while operating on a patient with acetabular fracture. What is this approach known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iliofemora​l approach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ilioinguin​al approach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kocher-Langenbeck approach​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stoppa ​approach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kocher-Langenbeck approach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kocher-Langenbeck approach</strong></p><p>The <strong>posterior approach</strong> used in acetabular fractures is known as the <strong>Kocher-Langenbeck approach.</strong></p>\n<p>The surgical approaches used in the <strong>management of acetabular fractures</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kocher-Langenbeck approach </strong>is the posterior approach that provides access to the posterior wall, posterior column, and to transverse fractures. It is also used for handling <strong>sciatic nerve injuries</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>ilioinguinal approach</strong> provides access to the entire <strong>anterior</strong> column, symphysis pubis, ilium, and sacroiliac joint. The <strong>Stoppa approach</strong> is a modification of the ilioinguinal approach.</li>\n<li>The <strong>iliofemoral approach</strong> is a <strong>lateral approach</strong> that is rarely used nowadays due to the <strong>high</strong> rate of<strong> complication</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Aq0oSS_0","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"15_metabolic_disorders_of_urea_cycle__complex_molecules__and_carbohydrates","title":"15.Metabolic Disorders Of Urea Cycle, Complex Molecules, And Carbohydrates","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA7653","text":"A 3-day-old child is suspe‌cted to have an inborn er‌ror of metabolism. Furthe‍r evaluation reveals elev‍ated blood ammonia in the​ presence of normal blood‌ pH and bicarbonate level‍s. There are no ketone bo​dies in the urine. Which ‌of the following is the m‌ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-day-old child is suspected to have an inborn error of metabolism. Further evaluation reveals elevated blood ammonia in the presence of normal blood pH and bicarbonate levels. There are no ketone bodies in the urine. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organic ‌aciduria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disorders of amino acid me‌tabolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urea cyc‌le defect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Galact‌osemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urea cycle defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd152bb0a72e4cd2810eeaa55a5443a1x1280x2560.0.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urea cycle defect</strong></p><p>In this case, <strong>hyperammonemia is +ve; acidosis is -ve; and ketosis is -ve:</strong> This is consistent with the <strong>u</strong><strong>rea cycle defect</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7836","text":"A young infant started sei‌zing in his cradle. He wa‌s immediately rushed to t‍he casualty. On investiga‍tion, blood ammonia was m​arkedly elevated. Which o‌f the following is used t‍o lower the ammonia level​s?","raw_text":"A young infant started seizing in his cradle. He was immediately rushed to the casualty. On investigation, blood ammonia was markedly elevated. Which of the following is used to lower the ammonia levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium ‌benzoate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium phe‌nylacetate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arginine hy‌drochloride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>All of the above agents are used in the treatment of acute hyperammonemia in an infant.</p>\n<p>Treatment of acute hyperammonemia in an infant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Provide <strong>adequate calories, fluid, and electrolytes</strong> intravenously.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimal amounts of protein</strong> as a mixture of <strong>essential amino acids</strong> during the first 24 hours of therapy.</li>\n<li>Give <strong>priming doses</strong> of the following compounds: <strong>Sodium benzoate, Sodium phenylacetate, Arginine hydrochloride</strong>.</li>\n<li>Initiate <strong>peritoneal dialysis or hemodialysis</strong> if the <strong>above treatment fails</strong> to produce an appreciable decrease in plasma ammonia levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Benzoate</strong> forms <strong>hippurate</strong> with endogenous glycine in the liver. Each mole of benzoate removes 1 mole of ammonia as glycine.</p>\n<p><strong> Phenylacetate</strong> conjugates with glutamine to form <strong>phenylacetylglutamine</strong>, which is readily excreted in the urine. One mole of phenylacetate removes 2 moles of ammonia as glutamine from the body.</p>\n<p><strong>Arginine</strong> administration supplies the urea cycle with <strong>ornithine.</strong> It is effective in all patients, except those with arginase deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phenylbutyrate</strong> which is metabolized to phenylacetate in our body can also be used as it <strong>scavenges nitrogen.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7835","text":"An infant's mother is dist‌ressed that her baby refu‌ses her feeds and starts ‍vomiting immediately when‍ fed and becomes lethargi​c. After thorough investi‌gations, the baby is diag‍nosed with HHH syndrome. ​Which of the following is‌ least likely to be found‌?","raw_text":"An infant's mother is distressed that her baby refuses her feeds and starts vomiting immediately when fed and becomes lethargic. After thorough investigations, the baby is diagnosed with HHH syndrome. Which of the following is least likely to be found?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperu‌ricemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperam‌monemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperorn‌ithinemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homocitr‌ullinemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperuricemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperuricemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperuricemia</strong> is<strong> not</strong> a component of HHH syndrome</p>\n<p><strong>HHH syndrome </strong>is a rare<strong> autosomal-recessive disorder</strong> caused by a defect in the transport system of ornithine from the cytosol into the mitochondria (<strong>deficiency of ornithine permease/translocase),</strong> resulting in the accumulation of ornithine in the cytosol leading to hyperornithinemia and a deficiency of this amino acid in mitochondria results in the disruption of the urea cycle and hyperammonemia. Homocitrulline is formed from the reaction of mitochondrial carbamyl phosphate with lysine, which can become a substrate for the OTC reaction when ornithine is deficient.</p>\n<p>Components of HHH syndrome:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Hyperammonemia</li>\n<li>Hyperornithinemia</li>\n<li>Homocitrullinemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7662","text":"As a geneticist studying t‌he familial inheritance o‌f porphyrias, which of th‍e following is likely to ‍show the inheritance patt​ern given below?","raw_text":"As a geneticist studying the familial inheritance of porphyrias, which of the following is likely to show the inheritance pattern given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ‌porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Variegate‌ porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehy‌dratase porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked pr‌otoporphyria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-linked protoporphyria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/73550cf2ed6549049b75a4c640016a05.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-linked protoporphyria</strong></p><p>The given pedigree chart shows <strong>X-linked dominant inheritance</strong>. <strong>X-linked protoporphyria</strong> is inherited in an X-linked dominant pattern.</p>\n<p>All porphyrias are autosomal dominant except the following :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (CEP) (<strong>autosomal recessive</strong>)</li>\n<li>δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase porphyria (ADP) (<strong>autosomal recessive</strong>)</li>\n<li>Protoporphyria - X-linked protoporphyria (<strong>X-linked dominant</strong>) </li>\n<li>Erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP) (<strong>autosomal recessive</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7668","text":"The most common porphyria ‌which manifests in childh‌ood is ______","raw_text":"The most common porphyria which manifests in childhood is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ‌porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy‌ria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary c‌oproporphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erythropoietic protoporphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong></p><p><strong>Erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong> (autosomal recessive) is the <strong>most common porphyria</strong> to manifest in childhood.</p>\n<p>Porphyria cutanea tarda (option A) manifests in adults.</p>\n<p>Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (option B) also known as Günther disease presents itself in utero or in infancy. It is a rare autosomal-recessive disease.</p>\n<p>Hereditary coproporphyria (option D) presents itself in the post-pubertal age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7663","text":"Which of the following is ‌also known as plumboporph‌yria due to its clinical ‍similarity to lead poison‍ing?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also known as plumboporphyria due to its clinical similarity to lead poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr‌ia (AIP)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehy‌dratase porphyria (ALAD)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyria cutanea tarda (P‌CT)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy‌ria (EPP)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase porphyria (ALAD)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase porphyria (ALAD)</strong></p><p>δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase porphyria (ALAD) is also called <strong>plumboporphyria</strong>/Doss porphyria due to its clinical similarity to lead poisoning, although it is not related to lead exposure.</p>\n<p>ALAD catalyzes the condensation of 2 molecules of ALA to form the pyrrole porphobilinogen (PBG). The enzyme is subject to inhibition by lead because ALAD is the principal lead-binding protein in erythrocytes, and lead can displace the zinc atoms of the enzyme, thus inhibiting it. Therefore, both lead poisoning and ALAD porphyria produce similar signs and symptoms. Symptoms resemble those of other acute porphyrias, including acute attacks of abdominal pain and neuropathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7667","text":"A 16-year-old girl was adm‌itted for severe abdomina‌l pain, nausea, vomiting,‍ and diarrhea. She develo‍ped generalized seizures ​in the hospital for which‌ she was given phenytoin.‍ Her symptoms worsened, s​he developed muscle weakn‌ess which progressed to q‌uadriparesis. Urinary por​phobilinogen was found to‌ be high. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl was admitted for severe abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. She developed generalized seizures in the hospital for which she was given phenytoin. Her symptoms worsened, she developed muscle weakness which progressed to quadriparesis. Urinary porphobilinogen was found to be high. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital erthyropoietic ‌porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked pr‌otoporphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute intermittent porphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute intermittent porphyria</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is<strong> acute intermittent porphyria</strong> which presents as a <strong>neurovisceral syndrome</strong> with <strong>no cutaneous manifestations and no phototoxicity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Acute Intermittent Porphyria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal dominant</li>\n<li>Deficiency of the enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase</li>\n<li>Presents at post-pubertal age</li>\n<li>Present with hepatic and neurologic manifestations</li>\n<li>Exacerbated by P450 inducing drugs like phenytoin, progesterone and dietary restriction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7664","text":"You are reading an article‌ titled, ' Dark age vampi‌res or our patients', whi‍ch describes an 18-year-o‍ld man who has had an int​olerance to sunshine sinc‌e the age of 6. When play‍ing cricket he suffered i​ntense burning, redness, ‌swelling, and crusting of‌ knees unprotected by sho​rts, with similar changes‌ on cheeks and bridge of ​the nose. His labs showed​ elevated concentrations ​of total erythrocyte prot‌oporphyrin. Which of the ‍following enzyme deficien​cies can explain this boy​'s condition?","raw_text":"You are reading an article titled, ' Dark age vampires or our patients', which describes an 18-year-old man who has had an intolerance to sunshine since the age of 6. When playing cricket he suffered intense burning, redness, swelling, and crusting of knees unprotected by shorts, with similar changes on cheeks and bridge of the nose. His labs showed elevated concentrations of total erythrocyte protoporphyrin. Which of the following enzyme deficiencies can explain this boy's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"δ-Aminolevulinate synthase‌ 2 (ALAS2)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"δ-Aminolevulinic acid dehy‌dratase (ALAD)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Porphobilinogen deaminase ‌(PBGD)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferrochela‌tase (FECH)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ferrochelatase (FECH)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ferrochelatase (FECH)</strong></p><p>Symptoms of<strong> cutaneous photosensitivity</strong> beginning in childhood and a substantially<strong> elevated concentration of total erythrocyte protoporphyrin</strong> supports a diagnosis of <strong>erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong>. The enzyme deficient in erythropoietic protoporphyria is <strong>ferrochelatase (FECH).</strong></p>\n<p>Symptoms consist of acute pain and itching often occurring within minutes of sunlight exposure and followed by redness and swelling with continued exposure. Swelling may resemble angioneurotic edema and <strong>solar urticaria.</strong> Symptoms are usually worse in the spring and summer. Chronic changes may include lichenification, leathery pseudovesicles, labial grooving, and nail changes, but changes in pigmentation and pronounced scarring are unusual.</p>\n<p>Life-threatening<strong> protoporphyric hepatopathy</strong> is characterized by greater increases in erythrocyte and plasma protoporphyrin levels, increased photosensitivity, and either chronically abnormal liver function tests or rapidly progressive hepatic failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9412","text":"A 6-month-old baby boy has‌ coarse facial features, ‌cloudy corneas, and gingi‍val hypertrophy. He has n‍o neck control and is una​ble to roll over. Laborat‌ory investigations reveal‍ markedly reduced activit​y of N-acetylglucosaminyl‌-1-phosphotransferase act‌ivity in WBCs. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old baby boy has coarse facial features, cloudy corneas, and gingival hypertrophy. He has no neck control and is unable to roll over. Laboratory investigations reveal markedly reduced activity of N-acetylglucosaminyl-1-phosphotransferase activity in WBCs. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I-cell ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‌osemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenoleuk‌odystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hurler's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. I-cell disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. I-cell disease</strong></p><p>This clinical vignette is strongly suggestive of <strong>I-cell disease</strong> or <strong>inclusion cell disease</strong>.  It is also known as type II Mucolipidosis.</p>\n<p>It is an<strong> autosomal recessive</strong> inherited as a <strong>lysosomal storage disorder.</strong> It is caused due to a defect in <strong>N-acetylglucosaminyl-1-phosphotransferase</strong> required for the Golgi to phosphorylate mannose residues on glycoproteins. This leads to defective<strong> mannose-6-phosphate, </strong>an important cell signaling molecule required for<strong> trafficking of the lysosomal enzyme</strong> from the site of its production in the endoplasmic reticulum to the lysosome. Due to this defect, the lysosomal enzymes are secreted out of the cell and there is an increased serum concentration of the same. Intracellularly, there are abnormal inclusion bodies and vacuolizations, thus earning the disease its name.</p>\n<p>Clinically, these patients present with symptoms either at birth or early in life with growth retardation, failure to thrive, <strong>delay of developmental milestones, coarse facial features with a cloudy cornea and gingival hypertrophy,</strong> and other non-specific findings such as hip dislocations, inguinal hernias, hepatomegaly, joint limitation, and skin changes. </p>\n<p>Investigations to establish a diagnosis may include measuring N-acetylglucosaminyl-1-phosphotransferase activity in WBCs or in cultured fibroblasts and estimating <strong>serum levels of lysosomal enzymes.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Galactosemia</strong> is caused due to the <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">complete <strong>absence of g</strong></span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>alactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase</strong>. The deficiency of this enzyme leads to an inability to convert galactose-1-phosphate (galactose-1-P) to uridine diphosphate galactose. It is characterized by t</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">ypical clinical features such as jaundice, vomiting, hepatomegaly, failure to thrive, poor feeding, lethargy, diarrhea, E.Coli sepsis. Infants may also have edema, ascites, full fontanelle, encephalopathy, and excessive bruising and bleeding. They tend to develop cataracts due to the accumulation of galactitol.</span></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Adrenoleukodystrophy</strong> is an <strong>X-linked recessive disorder of β-oxidation of fatty acids</strong>. There is a very large chain fatty acids buildup in the adrenal glands, the white matter of the brain, and testes. It is a progressive disease that can lead to <strong>adrenal gland crisis, coma, and death.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hurler syndrome</strong> is an important diagnosis in a patient presenting with clinical features given in this clinical vignette. But it is a mucopolysaccharidosis type 1-H caused due by the defect in the α-L Iduronidase leading to the <strong>accumulation of heparin sulfate and dermatan sulfate.</strong> It is characterized by skeletal abnormalities, hepatosplenomegaly, and severe intellectual disability. Infants develop characteristic coarse facial features, a wide nasal bridge, and a flattened midface. Features such as hepatosplenomegaly, umbilical or inguinal hernias, and typical skeletal abnormalities, known as dysostosis multiplex laos are also seen. They also have developmental delays and recurrent sinopulmonary diseases. Failure to thrive and growth restriction are also seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9355","text":"An 8-year-old boy is broug‌ht with complaints of sev‌ere pain in his right thi‍gh which has progressivel‍y worsened over the last ​few months. He is unable ‌to walk because of the sa‍me. His mother also says ​he has had repeated nose ‌bleeds in the last few mo‌nths and the child has st​opped playing with his fr‌iends as he gets easily t​ired for a month. Physica​l examination reveals hep​atosplenomegaly. Lab repo‌rts show thrombocytopenia‍ and anemia. An assay of ​peripheral blood leucocyt​es shows low levels of be‌ta-glucosidase. What is t‍he inheritance pattern of‍ this condition?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy is brought with complaints of severe pain in his right thigh which has progressively worsened over the last few months. He is unable to walk because of the same. His mother also says he has had repeated nose bleeds in the last few months and the child has stopped playing with his friends as he gets easily tired for a month. Physical examination reveals hepatosplenomegaly. Lab reports show thrombocytopenia and anemia. An assay of peripheral blood leucocytes shows low levels of beta-glucosidase. What is the inheritance pattern of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Autosomal recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Autosomal recessive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of bone pain, epistaxis, hepatosplenomegaly, and low levels of β-glucosidase suggests a diagnosis of <strong>Gaucher's disease</strong> that is inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> pattern.</p>\n<p>Gaucher's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is an inborn error of metabolism due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of the enzyme <strong>glucocerebrosidase (β-glucosidase)</strong> that leads to the accumulation of glucocerebroside in the lysosomes of cells.</li>\n<li>It is the <strong>most common lysosomal storage disorder. </strong></li>\n<li>The clinical features result from the accumulation of lipid-laden macrophages in the spleen, liver, bone marrow, bone, and other tissues.</li>\n<li>There are 3 clinical subtypes, delineated by the absence or presence and progression of neurologic manifestations: <strong>type 1, or the adult, nonneuronopathic</strong> form;<strong> type 2, the infantile or acute neuronopathic form</strong>; and type 3, the juvenile or subacute neuronopathic form</li>\n<li>Typical clinical features include <strong>delayed puberty</strong>, painless hepatomegaly and <strong>splenomegaly</strong>, liver cirrhosis, hypersplenism and pancytopenia,<strong> bleeding tendencies, bone pain</strong> due to <strong>avascular necrosis</strong>, osteoporosis, pathological fractures.</li>\n<li>Neurological symptoms such as developmental delay, hypertonia, strabismus, supranuclear gaze palsy (seen in type 3).</li>\n<li>Bone marrow examination usually reveals the presence of <strong>Gaucher cells</strong> (have a characteristic <strong>wrinkled-paper appearance</strong>)</li>\n<li>Enzyme replacement therapy for Gaucher's disease:<strong> <em>imiglucerase</em>, <em>velaglucerase alfa, </em><em>taliglucerase alfa,</em><em>eliglusta</em>t </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9466","text":"A 6-month-old infant with ‌progressive failure to th‌rive and milestone regres‍sion is being evaluated f‍or an inborn error of met​abolism. On examination, ‌hypotonia, apathy, deafne‍ss, hepatosplenomegaly, a​nd generalized lymphadeno‌pathy are present. Fundus‌ examination is shown bel​ow. A liver biopsy shows ‌foamy hepatocytes loaded ​with sphingomyelin. Elect​ron microscopy reveals ze​bra bodies. What is the l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old infant with progressive failure to thrive and milestone regression is being evaluated for an inborn error of metabolism. On examination, hypotonia, apathy, deafness, hepatosplenomegaly, and generalized lymphadenopathy are present. Fundus examination is shown below. A liver biopsy shows foamy hepatocytes loaded with sphingomyelin. Electron microscopy reveals zebra bodies. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Niemann-Pick disease type ‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tay-Sach‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Niemann-Pick disease type ‌B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sandhoff'‌s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Niemann-Pick disease type A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/58da6f5d165841efaef15a5c18363278.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Niemann-Pick disease type A</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette along with the <strong>cherry-red spot</strong> on fundus examination suggests a diagnosis of<strong> Niemann-Pick disease type A- </strong>Lysosomal storage disorder due to an inherited <strong>deficiency of sphingomyelinase</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type A</strong> is a severe<strong> infantile</strong> form with <strong>extensive neurologic involvement </strong>(hypotonia, milestone regression, apathy),<strong> marked visceral accumulations of sphingomyelin</strong> (hepatosplenomegaly, foamy hepatocytes, <strong>zebra bodies</strong> on EM), and<strong> progressive wasting and early death</strong> within the first 3 years of life.</p>\n<p>In contrast, patients with<strong> Niemann-Pick disease type B</strong> have organomegaly but generally <strong>no CNS involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3213","text":"A male child with coarse f‌acies, macroglossia, thic‌k lips with hepatosplenom‍egaly presents with copio‍us mucous discharge from ​the nose. The most probab‌le underlying diagnosis i‍s ________","raw_text":"A male child with coarse facies, macroglossia, thick lips with hepatosplenomegaly presents with copious mucous discharge from the nose. The most probable underlying diagnosis is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hurler ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrom‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proteus ‌Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hurler disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc2784866d7a414fad4a7a7c99f36f1dx720x1757.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hurler disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a child with <strong>coarse facies</strong>, <strong>macroglossia</strong>, <strong>thick lips</strong>, <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, and copious <strong>discharge from the nose</strong> is suggestive of<strong> Hurler's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the severe form of <strong>mucopolysaccharidoses type 1</strong> (MPS 1), an<strong> autosomal recessive</strong> disorder caused by a defect in <strong>α-L iduronidase</strong>, leading to the accumulation of <strong>heparan sulfate</strong> and <strong>dermatan sulfate</strong>. The child usually presents between <strong>6 and 24 months</strong> with hepatosplenomegaly, coarse facial features (shown below), <strong>corneal clouding</strong>, <strong>large tongue</strong>, prominent forehead, joint stiffness, <strong>short</strong> <strong>stature</strong>, and <strong>skeletal dysplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most patients have recurrent upper respiratory tract and ear infections, <strong>noisy breathing</strong>, and persistent copious nasal discharge. Radiograph shows skeletal dysplasia, called <strong>dysostosis multiplex</strong> - <strong>thick ribs</strong>, <strong>ovoid vertebral bodies</strong>, enlarged and <strong>trabeculated diaphyses</strong> of the long bones with <strong>irregular</strong> <strong>metaphysis</strong> and <strong>epiphysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Macrocephaly, enlarged J-shaped sella, premature closure of lambdoid sagittal sutures, and shallow orbits are seen with disease progression. Obstructive airway disease, cardiac complications, and respiratory infections are the common causes of death. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the radiograph shows <strong>enlarged diaphyses</strong> of the metacarpal and phalanges. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Beckwith-Wiedemann</strong> syndrome is an overgrowth syndrome characterized by <strong>macrosomia</strong>, <strong>macroglossia</strong>,<strong> hemihypertrophy</strong>, <strong>omphalocele</strong>, and renal anomalies. It is associated with an increased risk of <strong>Wilms tumor</strong>, hepatoblastoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, neuroblastoma, and adrenal cortical carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Proteus syndrome</strong> is a disturbance of cellular growth involving ectodermal and mesodermal tissues, caused by mutation in the AKT1 gene. They present with <strong>hemimegalencephaly</strong>, asymmetric <strong>overgrowth of the extremities</strong>, verrucous cutaneous lesions, thickening of the bone, and excessive growth of muscles.</p>\n<p>Option D: A child with untreated <strong>congenital hypothyroidism</strong> may have stunted growth, short extremities, narrow palpebral fissures, swollen eyelids, depressed nasal bridge, thick broad protruded tongue, and short and thick neck, dry and scaly skin. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3408","text":"A case series reviews chil‌dren with lysosomal stora‌ge disorders who usually ‍present with dysmorphic f‍acies, organomegaly, mile​stones regression, and fa‌ilure to thrive. Which of‍ the following disorders ​does not have enzyme repl‌acement therapy as a ther‌apeutic option?","raw_text":"A case series reviews children with lysosomal storage disorders who usually present with dysmorphic facies, organomegaly, milestones regression, and failure to thrive. Which of the following disorders does not have enzyme replacement therapy as a therapeutic option?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gaucher'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pompe ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sanfilipp‌o syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fabry's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sanfilippo syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sanfilippo syndrome</strong></p><p>Enzyme replacement therapy is not available for Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS type III).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9350","text":"A 3-month-old female child‌ was brought to the clini‌c with a history of failu‍re to thrive. On examinat‍ion, generalized hypotoni​a and hepatomegaly were p‌resent. A chest X-ray ima‍ge shows massive cardiome​galy. A muscle biopsy is ‌performed and the finding‌s are attached in the ima​ge below. Which of the fo‌llowing is the most likel​y enzyme that is involved​ in the suspected conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old female child was brought to the clinic with a history of failure to thrive. On examination,  generalized hypotonia and hepatomegaly were present. A chest X-ray image shows massive cardiomegaly. A muscle biopsy is performed and the findings are attached in the image below. Which of the following is the most likely enzyme that is involved in the suspected condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose-6-‌phosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lysosomal acid alpha-gluco‌sidase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle glycogen phosphoryl‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glycogen debranching enzym‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37985bd17c3148b5892954d3940663fe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase</strong></p><p>The muscle biopsy shows the presence of <strong>large vacuoles</strong>. This along with the clinical vignette is strongly suggestive of <strong>Pompe's disease</strong> or type II glycogen storage disease. It is caused due to the deficiency of the enzyme<strong> lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinically, the child presents with <strong>hypotonia,</strong><strong> hepatomegaly, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</strong> The affected child usually dies around the age of 2 years due to heart failure.</p>\n<p>Investigations include chest X-ray imaging which shows<strong> cardiomegaly. </strong>The muscle biopsy showing vacuoles further aids the diagnosis. </p>\n<p>Treatment includes enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant enzyme <strong>a-glucosidase alfa</strong> (Myozyme).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9353","text":"A 6-month-old is brought b‌y his parents with a hist‌ory of decreased weight g‍ain. On examination, you ‍find that the baby has he​patosplenomegaly, muscle ‌weakness and atrophy, hyp‍otonia, and decreased dee​p tendon reflexes. Blood ‌tests reveal normal gluco‌se levels. Biopsy of the ​liver reveals initial sta‌ges of cirrhosis due to t​he accumulation of abnorm​al glycogen with few bran​ch points, whose structur‌e resembles amylopectin. ‍The clinical and laborato​ry results are indicative​ of which glycogen storag‌e disease?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old is brought by his parents with a history of decreased weight gain. On examination, you find that the baby has hepatosplenomegaly, muscle weakness and atrophy, hypotonia, and decreased deep tendon reflexes. Blood tests reveal normal glucose levels. Biopsy of the liver reveals initial stages of cirrhosis due to the accumulation of abnormal glycogen with few branch points, whose structure resembles amylopectin. The clinical and laboratory results are indicative of which glycogen storage disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Von Gierk‌e disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Andersen‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"McArdle‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tarui ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Andersen disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Andersen disease</strong></p><p>The clinical and laboratory features indicate <strong>Andersen's disease</strong> in the baby. Inability to gain weight, hepatosplenomegaly, muscle weakness, and accumulation of abnormal glycogen resembling amylopectin point towards the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Andersen's disease</strong> is type IV glycogen storage disease. It is transmitted in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> pattern and occurs due to a deficiency of <strong>glycogen branching enzyme </strong>because of pathologic variants in the GBE1 gene. This leads to accumulation of of an abnormal structure of glycogen known as <strong>polyglucosan</strong>.  It is characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Growth delay</li>\n<li>Hepatomegaly due to the accumulation of polysaccharides with few branch points</li>\n<li>Myopathy</li>\n<li>There might be fasting intolerance and ketosis in the initial stages. Hypoglycemia usually appears late in the disease when it is associated with cirrhosis, esophageal varices, and ascites.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></li>\n<li><strong>Biopsy of the liver</strong> will show the presence of abnormal glycogen which resembles <strong>amylopectin</strong>. Amylopectin is found in plants and has very few branches. Since there is a defect in the branching of the glycogen, the abnormal glycogen appears like amylopectin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\">The diagnosis is confirmed by absent branching enzyme activity in skin fibroblasts, muscle, or liver and analysis of the DNA mutation.</p>\n<p><strong>Von Gierke's disease </strong>(Option A) has features of lactic acidosis, hypoglycemia, hyperuricemia, impaired growth, and bone thinning. It occurs due to a defect in the glucose 6-phosphatase.</p>\n<p><strong>McArdle disease</strong> (Option C) usually manifests as exercise intolerance, muscle cramping with exercise. It is usually a benign chronic condition. It occurs due to a defect in the muscle glycogen phosphorylase.</p>\n<p><strong>Tarui disease</strong> (Option D) also presents similar to the McArdle disease. Patients may have developmental delay, and hemolytic anemia in addition to exercise-induced muscle cramps. It occurs due to a defect in the <strong>muscle phosphofructokinase</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3215","text":"A 6-year-old boy develops ‌hypoglycemia and muscle w‌eakness with moderate act‍ivity. On examination, he‍ had hepatomegaly and gro​wth restriction. Investig‌ations revealed the accum‍ulation of limit dextrins​ and increased glycogen d‌eposits in the liver. Wha‌t is the most likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy develops hypoglycemia and muscle weakness with moderate activity. On examination, he had hepatomegaly and growth restriction. Investigations revealed the accumulation of limit dextrins and increased glycogen deposits in the liver. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cori's ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"von Gierke‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anderson'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pompe's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cori's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cori&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is<strong> Cori disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Glycogen debrancher deficiency (Glycogen Storage Disease III) is also known as <strong>Cori disease</strong>, Forbes disease, and<strong> limit dextrinosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Cori’s disease may be clinically indistinguishable from von Gierke’s disease in infancy and childhood as hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, hyperlipidemia, and growth retardation is common. Accumulation of limit dextrins is unique to Cori's disease. <strong>Definitive diagnosis</strong> is through <strong>enzyme assays</strong> in the liver, muscle, or both.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9351","text":"A 5-day-old neonate is bro‌ught to the emergency roo‌m with vomiting and poor ‍feeding. Breastfeeding wa‍s initiated soon after bi​rth. On examination, the ‌child is lethargic and ha‍s hepatomegaly. Labs reve​al increased plasma galac‌tose and increased urine ‌galactitol levels. Examin​ation of the mother shows‌ the presence of skin les​ions on the breast with t​he histopathological appe​arance showing characteri‌stic 'Cowdry Type A' incl‍usions as shown below. Ch​oose the correct statemen​ts from those below: The ‌child's condition is an a‍bsolute contraindication ‍to breastfeeding The moth​er's condition is an abso​lute contraindication to breastfeeding The child is prone to E.coli sepsis The child is prone to sepsis caused by S. aureus The mother has a risk of recurrence of her condition","raw_text":"<p>A 5-day-old neonate is brought to the emergency room with vomiting and poor feeding. Breastfeeding was initiated soon after birth. On examination, the child is lethargic and has hepatomegaly. Labs reveal increased plasma galactose and increased urine galactitol levels. Examination of the mother shows the presence of skin lesions on the breast with the histopathological appearance showing characteristic 'Cowdry Type A' inclusions as shown below. Choose the correct statements from those below:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The child's condition is an absolute contraindication to breastfeeding</li>\n<li>The mother's condition is an absolute contraindication to breastfeeding</li>\n<li>The child is prone to E.coli sepsis</li>\n<li>The child is prone to sepsis caused by S. aureus</li>\n<li>The mother has a risk of recurrence of her condition</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 and 4 a‌re correct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3, and 5‌ are correct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 and 5 a‌re correct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,3, and 4 ‌are correct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3, and 5 are correct","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0937ef61b0a44a9a9a9c6dd3bdcdcd28.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3, and 5 are correct</strong></p><p>The given clinical complex describes a case of <strong>classic galactosemia in the infant</strong> (increased plasma galactose and urine galactitol levels) and <strong>herpes simplex of the breast in the mother</strong> (histopathology shown in the image has large multinucleate virus-infected cells with typical eosinophilic Cowdry Type A inclusions).</p>\n<p><strong>Galactosemia</strong> is an<strong> absolute contraindication</strong> to breastfeeding and the child is at risk for E.coli sepsis. <strong>Herpes lesions on the breast</strong> are a<strong> relative contraindication</strong> to <strong>breastfeeding</strong> and she is at risk for recurrence. </p>\n<p><strong> Deficiency</strong> of <strong>galactose-1-uridyltransferase</strong> leads to the accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate and galactitol causes <strong>classic galactosemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Infants show vomiting, diarrhea, and poor weight gain <strong>within 3-4 days of breastfeeding.</strong><strong>Jaundice</strong> and liver dysfunction appear in the first or second week of life. Untreated children may show nuclear cataracts (<strong>oil-droplet cataract</strong>), vitreous hemorrhage, hepatic failure, cirrhosis, ascites, splenomegaly, or intellectual disability. <strong>The proximal renal tubular disease</strong> may also manifest as metabolic acidosis, galactosuria, glucosuria, and aminoaciduria. The most common cause of death is <strong>E.coli sepsis.</strong>Acute galactose toxicity is manifested by neurological symptoms</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is made by genetic or mutational analysis. There will the presence of reducing substances in the urine. <strong>Elimination of galactose</strong> from the diet along with adequate calcium supplementation <strong>reverses growth failure and renal and hepatic dysfunction.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3407","text":"A father has noticed that ‌whenever his daughter dri‌nks fruit juice she has m‍ultiple episodes of vomit‍ing. He reports to you th​at his daughter is allerg‌ic to fruit juice. Which ‍of the following enzyme d​eficiency is likely?","raw_text":"A father has noticed that whenever his daughter drinks fruit juice she has multiple episodes of vomiting. He reports to you that his daughter is allergic to fruit juice. Which of the following enzyme deficiency is likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldol‌ase B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fructo‌kinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose 6-‌phosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hexok‌inase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aldolase B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aldolase B</strong></p><p><strong>Aldolase B</strong> deficiency is likely in this child.</p>\n<p>In the given case scenario, the child becomes symptomatic after ingesting fruit juice that contains fructose; this indicates that she has <strong>hereditary fructose intolerance, which is caused by the deficiency of aldolase B.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Deficiency of fructose-1,6- bisphosphate aldolase (Aldolase B hereditary fructose intolerance): </strong><strong>Fructose aldolase B </strong>is present in the liver, kidney, and intestine. It catalyzes the hydrolysis of fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into triose phosphate and glyceraldehyde phosphate.</p>\n<p>Clinical features: Patients are asymptomatic until fructose or sucrose is ingested (usually from fruit, fruit juice, sweetened cereal, or table sugar). Clinical features include jaundice, hepatomegaly, vomiting, lethargy, irritability, and convulsions. It resembles galactosemia. Acute ingestion causes symptomatic hypoglycemia. Chronic intake results in failure to thrive and hepatic disease. The persistence of fructose intake can lead to liver failure, kidney failure, and death.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis:<strong> Reducing substance</strong> is seen in the <strong>urine</strong> during an episode (<strong>Benedict's test +</strong>). An assay of <strong>fructaldolase B activity in the liver</strong> gives a <strong>definitive diagnosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment: Complete elimination of all sources of sucrose, fructose, and sorbitol from the diet. With age, symptoms become milder even after fructose ingestion. The long-term prognosis is good.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The deficiency of fructokinase</strong> is not associated with any clinical manifestations. Fructosuria is an<strong> accidental finding</strong> usually made because the<strong> asymptomatic</strong> patient's urine contains a reducing substance. No treatment is necessary, and the<strong> prognosis is excellent.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7659","text":"\"Tomcat urine\" is characte‌ristically seen in ______‌__","raw_text":"\"Tomcat urine\"  is characteristically seen in ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyros‌inemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple carboxylase defic‌iency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylk‌etonuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trimethy‌laminuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multiple carboxylase deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multiple carboxylase deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Tomcat urine</strong> is characteristically seen in<strong> multiple carboxylase deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is named so because of its <strong>peculiar odour.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7669","text":"The enzyme deficient in he‌reditary coproporphyria i‌s ________","raw_text":"The enzyme deficient in hereditary coproporphyria is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coproporphyrinogen oxidase‌ (CPOX)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Protoporphyrinogen oxidase‌ (PPOX)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Porphobilinogen deaminase ‌(PBGD)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferrochela‌tase (FECH)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coproporphyrinogen oxidase (CPOX)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coproporphyrinogen oxidase (CPOX)</strong></p><p>The enzyme deficient in hereditary coproporphyria is coproporphyrinogen oxidase (CPOX).</p>\n<p><strong>Hereditary coproporphyria (HCP):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal-dominant hepatic porphyria is caused by the<strong> deficiency of CPOX. </strong></li>\n<li>Acute attacks of HCP are treated as in acute intermittent porphyria (AIP), which includes intravenous hemin and identifying and avoiding precipitating factors. Cholestyramine may be of some value for photosensitivity occurring with liver dysfunction.</li>\n<li>Gonadotropin-releasing hormone analogs can be effective for the prevention of cyclic attacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7666","text":"Deficiency of the enzyme p‌orphobilinogen deaminase ‌causes _____________","raw_text":"Deficiency of the enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase causes _____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Variegate‌ porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-Linked pr‌otoporphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute intermittent porphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute intermittent porphyria</strong></p><p>Deficiency of the enzyme <strong>porphobilinogen deaminase</strong> causes <strong>acute intermittent porphyria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Porphyria cutanea tarda: Deficiency of Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD)</li>\n<li>Variegate porphyria: Deficiency of Protoporphyrinogen oxidase (PPOX)</li>\n<li> X-Linked protoporphyria: Gain of function mutation of δ-aminolevulinate synthase 2 (ALAS2)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7272","text":"A mother who is a hairstyl‌ist has noted that her ba‌by's hair looks dull, bre‍aks off easily, and is tu‍fted. She brings the chil​d to you and informs you ‌about the past history of‍ seizures. Investigations​ reveal elevated plasma a‌mmonia levels and arginin‌osuccinate levels. What i​s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A mother who is a hairstylist has noted that her baby's hair looks dull, breaks off easily, and is tufted. She brings the child to you and informs you about the past history of seizures. Investigations reveal elevated plasma ammonia levels and argininosuccinate levels. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argin‌inemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argininosucc‌inic aciduria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperammon‌emia type 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperammon‌emia type 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Argininosuccinic aciduria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc7d409a3d8b4a09bbd932eed8f68b73x444x483.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Argininosuccinic aciduria</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>argininosuccinic aciduria</strong> due to <strong>argininosuccinate lyase</strong> deficiency.</p>\n<p>It is accompanied by high levels of argininosuccinate levels in blood, CSF, and urine. It is also associated with friable, tufted hair known as <strong>trichorrhexis nodosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Image showing Trichorrhexis nodosa:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9621","text":"Accumulation of ______ tak‌es place in Farber’s dise‌ase.","raw_text":"Accumulation of ______ takes place in Farber’s disease.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leci‌thin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cera‌mide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphing‌omyelin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfa‌tides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ceramide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ceramide</strong></p><p><strong>Accumulation of Ceramide</strong> is seen in <strong>Farber’s disease </strong>due to<strong> ceramidase deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p><strong> </strong>Clinical features of Farber’s disease :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painful joint swelling, nodules, progressive joint deformity</li>\n<li>Hoarse cry</li>\n<li>Breathing difficulty</li>\n<li>Failure to thrive</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"mxXhCR6Z","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"15_hypersensitivity_and_autoimmunity","title":"15.Hypersensitivity And Autoimmunity","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB6854","text":"A child, upon disturbing a‌ beehive, had several sti‌ngs. After a few minutes,‍ he developed shock, resp‌iratory failure, and vasc​ular collapse. What is th​e pathogenesis of this co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A child, upon disturbing a beehive, had several stings. After a few minutes, he developed shock, respiratory failure, and vascular collapse. What is the pathogenesis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NK cell-mediated cytotoxic‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgE-mediat‌ed reaction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type 4 hypersensitivity re‌action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA-mediated hypersensitiv‌ity reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgE-mediated reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea15142c3b4b4380ada40023e911032dx1280x2567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgE-mediated reaction</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> following a bee sting. This is an example of <strong>IgE-mediated reaction</strong> or<strong> type 1 hypersensitivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Injurious immune reactions are called hypersensitivity reactions. There are four types of hypersensitivity reactions.</p>\n<p>Immediate (Type 1) hypersensitivity is also called allergic reactions. They are induced by environmental antigens (allergens) that stimulate strong TH2 responses and <strong>IgE</strong> production in genetically susceptible individuals.</p>\n<p><strong>IgE</strong> coats <strong>mast cells</strong> by binding to Fcεg receptors. Reexposure to the allergen leads to <strong>cross-linking </strong>of the<strong> IgE </strong>and<strong> FcεRI</strong>, activation of mast cells, and release of mediators. The principal mediators are<strong> histamine</strong>, proteases, and other granule contents like prostaglandins and leukotrienes and <strong>cytokines</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs in two phases:<strong> immediate phase</strong> and<strong> late phase</strong>. The mediators are responsible for the immediate<strong> vascular</strong> and<strong> smooth muscle reactions</strong> and the late-phase reaction (<strong>inflammation</strong>). The clinical manifestations may be local or systemic and range from mildly annoying rhinitis to fatal<strong> anaphylaxis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the sequence of events in immediate hypersensitivity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6857","text":"Arthus reaction is caused ‌by which of the following‌ process?","raw_text":"Arthus reaction is caused by which of the following process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Localized immune-complex-m‌ediated","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic immune-complex-me‌diated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complemen‌t-mediated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibody‌-mediated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Localized immune-complex-mediated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Localized immune-complex-mediated</strong></p><p><strong>Arthus reaction</strong> is characterized by a localized area of tissue necrosis due to <strong>immune-complex-mediated </strong>(type III hypersensitivity reaction)<strong> </strong>vasculitis in the skin.</p>\n<p>This reaction can be produced <strong>experimentally</strong> by <strong>intracutaneous</strong> injection of antigen in a previously immunised animal that contains circulating antibodies against the antigen. As the antigen diffuses into the vessel wall, it binds with the preformed antibody, and <strong>immune complexes</strong> are formed <strong>locally</strong>. These complexes precipitate in the vessel walls and cause <strong>fibrinoid necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6861","text":"A 42-year-old female patie‌nt presents with ptosis, ‌diplopia, and progressive‍ muscular weakness toward‌s the end of the day. She​ has improvements in symp​toms after administering ‌edrophonium. The given cl‍inical condition is an ex​ample of which of the fol​lowing?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old female patient presents with ptosis, diplopia, and progressive muscular weakness towards the end of the day. She has improvements in symptoms after administering edrophonium. The given clinical condition is an example of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 hypersensitivity re‌action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 hypersensitivity re‌action","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3 hypersensitivity re‌action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 4 hypersensitivity re‌action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2 hypersensitivity reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2 hypersensitivity reaction</strong></p><p>The given clinical condition is highly suggestive of<strong> myasthenia gravis</strong>. It is an example of a <strong>t</strong><strong>ype II hypersensitivity reaction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease that is usually associated with <strong>autoantibodies </strong>directed against <strong>acetylcholine receptors</strong>. In Type II hypersensitivity, antibodies react with antigens present on cell surfaces or in the extracellular matrix cause. These antibodies <strong>destroy</strong> cells,<strong> trigger inflammation</strong>, or interfering with normal functions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6865","text":"A 6-year-old child abruptl‌y develops malaise, fever‌, nausea, oliguria, and s‍moky, cola-colored urine ‌2 weeks after recovery fr​om a sore throat. He is f​ound to have periorbital ‌edema and mild hypertensi‍on. Urine examination sho​ws dysmorphic red cells a​nd red cell casts in the ‌urine with mild proteinur​ia. Which type of hyperse​nsitivity is responsible ​for this condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child abruptly develops malaise, fever, nausea, oliguria, and smoky, cola-colored urine 2 weeks after recovery from a sore throat. He is found to have periorbital edema and mild hypertension. Urine examination shows dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts in the urine with mild proteinuria. Which type of hypersensitivity is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type III hyp‌ersensitivity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type IV hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type III hypersensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6378cb8c8f184b3f8ae8a99ad14c8011x1280x1360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type III hypersensitivity</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nephritic syndrome</strong> due to<strong> post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong>. This is an example of a <strong>type III hypersensitivity</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p>In type III hypersensitivity, immune complexes may deposit in tissues or blood vessels, and elicit an acute inflammatory reaction by activating complement. The<strong> pathogenesis</strong> of systemic immune complex disease can be divided into three phases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Formation of immune complexes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Deposition of immune complexes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inflammation and tissue injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This results in various manifestations like<strong> vasculitis</strong>, <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong> and <strong>arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Examples</strong> of type III hypersensitivity reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reactive arthritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polyarteritis nodosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Serum sickness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Arthus reaction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Farmer’s lung</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypersensitivity pneumonitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Type II lepra reaction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Henoch–Schonlein purpura</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the mechanism of type III hypersensitivity reactions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6869","text":"A 25-year-old man with a f‌ever and chronic cough is‌ administered 0.1 mL of p‍urified protein derivativ‌e intradermally over the ​forearm. After a few hour​s, the injection site app‌ears as follows. Which of‍ the following does this ​represent?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man with a fever and chronic cough is administered 0.1 mL of purified protein derivative intradermally over the forearm. After a few hours, the injection site appears as follows.  Which of the following does this represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I hype‌rsensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immune-complex-mediated hy‌persensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antibody-mediated hypersen‌sitivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cell-mediated hypersensiti‌vity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cell-mediated hypersensitivity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1973970928ac42b984df33febf4396d9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cell-mediated hypersensitivity</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario shows a<strong> positive tuberculin test</strong>. It is a classical example of<strong> </strong>type IV or<strong> </strong>delayed or <strong>cell-mediated </strong><strong>hypersensitivity</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuberculin test</strong> involves intradermal injection of purified protein derivative (PPD, also called tuberculin), a protein-containing antigen of the tubercle bacillus. In a <strong>previously sensitized</strong> individual, reddening and <strong>induration</strong> of the site appear in 8 to 12 hours, reach a peak in 24 to 72 hours, and thereafter slowly subside. </p>\n<p>Morphologically, delayed-type hypersensitivity is characterized by the accumulation of <strong>mononuclear cells</strong>, mainly CD4+ T cells and macrophages, around venules. The cell-mediated type of hypersensitivity is caused by inflammation resulting from cytokines produced by <strong>CD4+ T</strong> cells and cell killing by<strong> CD8+ T</strong> cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0152","text":"True about anaphylactoid r‌eaction are all except:","raw_text":"True about anaphylactoid reaction are all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Involves degranulation of ‌mast cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgE me‌diated","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Can occur on first exposur‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most commonly caused by NS‌AIDs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgE mediated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74b783311e9a49188e834ad40435ea8fx1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgE mediated</strong></p><p><strong>Anaphylactoid reactions</strong> are those reactions that are similar to the anaphylactic reactions but are <strong>not</strong> IgE mediated. These reactions are caused by <strong>direct degranulation</strong> of the <strong>mast cells</strong> or<strong> basophils</strong>, which causes the release of mediators.</p>\n<p>Unlike the classic anaphylaxis which occurs on re-exposure to the allergen, the anaphylactoid reaction can occur even on the <strong>first</strong> <strong>exposure</strong>. The most<strong> common agents</strong> that<strong> </strong>can cause an anaphylactoid reaction include <strong>NSAIDs</strong> and <strong>radiocontrast media</strong>.</p>\n<p>In 2014, World Allergy Organisation has introduced two new terms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immunologic anaphylaxis </strong>- It includes anaphylactic reactions mediated by IgE, IgG (in animals), and immune complex/ complement-mediated reactions.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-immunologic anaphylaxis</strong> (previously called anaphylactoid reaction) -<strong> </strong>It includes reactions caused by agents which cause massive degranulation of mast cells or basophils in the absence of immunoglobulin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the sequence of events in type 1 hypersensitivity reaction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6879","text":"Which of the following aut‌oimmune diseases are not ‌mediated by antibodies?","raw_text":"Which of the following autoimmune diseases are not mediated by antibodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Goodpastur‌e syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong> is mediated by <strong>T cells</strong>, not antibodies.</p>\n<p><strong>Antibody</strong>-mediated diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</li>\n<li>Autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura</li>\n<li><strong>Pemphigus vulgaris</strong></li>\n<li>Vasculitis caused by ANCA</li>\n<li><strong>Goodpasture syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li>Acute rheumatic fever</li>\n<li>Graves disease</li>\n<li><strong>Pernicious anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Insulin resistant diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>T cell</strong>-mediated diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></li>\n<li>Multiple sclerosis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Type 1 diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Contact sensitivity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In T cell mediated diseases, although the role of T cells have been demonstrated, antibodies may also be involved in tissue injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6882","text":"Which gene is most frequen‌tly implicated in autoimm‌unity?","raw_text":"Which gene is most frequently implicated in autoimmunity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FO‌XP3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PTP‌N22","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NO‌D2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AI‌RE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PTPN22","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PTPN22</strong></p><p><strong>PTPN22<em> </em></strong>gene is most frequently implicated in <strong>autoimmunity</strong>.</p>\n<p>PTPN22, which encodes the protein <strong>tyrosine phosphatase,</strong> is associated with the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)</li>\n<li>Type 1 diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because these disorders have a fairly high prevalence, <em>PTPN22</em> is said to be the gene that is most frequently implicated in autoimmunity.</p>\n<p>Option A: Mutations in<strong> FOXP3 </strong>cause a systemic autoimmune disease called <strong>IPEX</strong> (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linked).</p>\n<p>Option C: Polymorphisms in the gene encoding for <strong>NOD2</strong> are associated with <strong>Crohn's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> AIRE </strong>mutation is associated with the autoimmune polyendocrinopathy syndrome type 1 <strong>(APS-1).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9459","text":"During development, thymic‌ cells recognizing self-a‌ntigens are removed to pr‍event autoimmunity. Which‌ gene among the following​ is involved in the proce​ss?","raw_text":"During development, thymic cells recognizing self-antigens are removed to prevent autoimmunity. Which gene among the following is involved in the process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NOT‌CH1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RB ‌gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CPK ‌gene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AI‌RE","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. AIRE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. AIRE</strong></p><p>The above process is called <strong>central tolerance</strong> and the <strong>AIRE </strong>(AutoImmune REgulator) protein is necessary for this process.</p>\n<p><strong>Immature</strong> self-reactive <strong>T </strong>and<strong> B lymphocyte</strong> clones recognize self-antigens during their <strong>maturation</strong> in the central lymphoid organs (thymus for T cells, bone marrow for B cells). They are <strong>killed</strong> or <strong>rendered harmless</strong>.</p>\n<p>The removal of self reacting <strong>T lymphocytes</strong> in the thymus is called <strong>negative selection/ deletion</strong>. For this process, <strong><em>AIRE</em></strong> protein is needed. Mutations in the <em>AIRE</em> gene results in <strong>autoimmune disorders</strong> (autoimmune polyendocrinopathy).</p>\n<p><strong>B cells</strong> undergo a process called <strong>receptor editing</strong>. When developing B cells encounter an antigen, it formulates a change in the receptor that makes the antigen unrecognizable. Self-reacting B cells can also undergo <strong>apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6888","text":"Which of the following is ‌responsible for autoimmun‌ity in Epstein-Barr virus‍ infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for autoimmunity in Epstein-Barr virus infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molecula‌r mimicry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyclonal B-cell activati‌on","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upregulation of co-stimula‌tory molecules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antigenic cross-reactivity‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Polyclonal B-cell activation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Polyclonal B-cell activation</strong></p><p>Autoimmunity in <strong>Epstein–Barr virus</strong> (EBV) infection is the result of <strong>p</strong><strong>olyclonal</strong> <strong>B cell</strong> activation.</p>\n<p>Microbes may induce other abnormalities that promote autoimmune reactions. Some viruses, such as<strong> EBV </strong>and<strong> HIV,</strong> cause<strong> polyclonal B-cell activation,</strong> which may result in the production of<strong> autoantibodies.</strong></p>\n<p>The role of <strong>infections</strong> in <strong>autoimmunity</strong> is exemplified by the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Molecular <strong>mimicry</strong> of antigenic cross-reactivity: The <strong>structural similarity</strong> of the microbial antigens and self-antigens.<strong> Rheumatic heart disease</strong> is an example in which streptococcal proteins cross-react with myocardial proteins causing myocarditis.</li>\n<li><strong>Upregulation</strong> of co-stimulatory molecules by infectious organisms.</li>\n<li><strong>Polyclonal B cell activation:</strong> Caused by EBV and HIV<strong>,</strong> resulting in the production of<strong> autoantibodies.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Alteration</strong> of tissue antigens: Infections may alter tissue self-antigens so that they <strong>activate T cells</strong> and lose the property of self-tolerance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6936","text":"Homogeneous or diffuse nuc‌lear staining pattern may‌ reflect antibodies to al‍l the following except:","raw_text":"Homogeneous or diffuse nuclear staining pattern may reflect antibodies to all the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌NA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Double st‌randed DNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chro‌matin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist‌ones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RNA</strong></p><p>Homogeneous or diffuse nuclear staining does not reflect antibodies to RNA. The <strong>nucleolar</strong> pattern of staining represents <strong>antibodies </strong>to<strong> RNA.</strong></p>\n<p> <strong>Antinuclear antibodies</strong> (ANAs) are directed against antibodies to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA</li>\n<li>Histones</li>\n<li>Nonhistone proteins bound to RNA</li>\n<li>Nucleolar antigens</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most widely used method for detecting ANAs is <strong>indirect immunofluorescence</strong>.<strong> </strong>The <strong>pattern</strong> of nuclear fluorescence suggests the <strong>type</strong> of <strong>antibody</strong> present in the patient’s serum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6937","text":"What type of cell is the l‌upus erythematosus cell?","raw_text":"What type of cell is the lupus erythematosus cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph‌ocyte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endothel‌ial cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eosin‌ophil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutr‌ophil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neutrophil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a8bae7a6a744a5d87b16770536031d3x1280x1007.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neutrophil</strong></p><p>The lupus erythematosus (LE) cell is a <strong>phagocytic neutrophil </strong>or<strong> macrophage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nuclei of damaged cells <strong>react</strong> with ANAs, <strong>lose</strong> their <strong>chromatin pattern</strong>, and become homogeneous to produce <strong>LE bodies</strong> or <strong>hematoxylin bodies. </strong>Phagocytic neutrophils or macrophages <strong>engulf</strong> the <strong>denatured nucleus</strong> of an injured cell. Demonstration of LE cells in vitro was used in the past for the diagnosis of SLE.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a lupus erythematosus (LE) cell.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6944","text":"A 32-year-old lady present‌s with the following find‌ing. On further investiga‍tions, anti-ds DNA and an‌ti-Smith antibodies are f​ound to be positive. Whic​h of the following is not‌ seen in the given clinic‍al condition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old lady presents with the following finding. On further investigations, anti-ds DNA and anti-Smith antibodies are found to be positive. Which of the following is not seen in the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hematoxylin bodies in lymp‌h nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Onion-skin lesions in the ‌spleen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasculitis with fibrinoid ‌necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocytic infiltration i‌n salivary gland","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymphocytic infiltration in salivary gland","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e68ccb515ff647298cad7569736a352a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymphocytic infiltration in salivary gland</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is highly suggestive of <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).</strong> In this condition, lymphocytic infiltration in the salivary gland is not seen. However, this finding is seen in Sjogren's syndrome. </p>\n<p>If cell nuclei are exposed to anti-nuclear antibodies (<strong>ANA</strong>s), they can bind to them. The nuclei of damaged cells react with ANAs, <strong>lose</strong> their <strong>chromatin pattern</strong>, and become <strong>homogeneous.</strong> They are called lupus erythematosus (LE) bodies or <strong>hematoxylin bodies.</strong></p>\n<p>An acute necrotizing <strong>vasculitis</strong> involving capillaries, small arteries, and arterioles with<strong> fibrinoid necrosis</strong> is seen in SLE. Central penicilliary arteries in the spleen may show concentric intimal and smooth muscle cell hyperplasia, producing so-called <strong>onion-skin lesions.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6947","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of the IgG4 sp‌ectrum?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the IgG4 spectrum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mikulicz‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Riedel th‌yroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idiopathic retroperitoneal‌ fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Celiac ‌disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Celiac disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Celiac disease</strong></p><p>Celiac disease is not an IgG4-Related disease.</p>\n<p><strong>IgG4-related disease</strong> (IgG4-RD) is a newly recognized constellation of disorders characterized by<strong> tissue infiltrates </strong>dominated by IgG4 antibody-producing plasma cells and lymphocytes, particularly T cells, storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis, and usually increased serum IgG4.</p>\n<p>IgG4-RD spectrum includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mikulicz syndrome - enlargement and fibrosis of salivary and lacrimal glands</li>\n<li>Riedel thyroiditis</li>\n<li>Idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis</li>\n<li>Autoimmune pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory pseudotumors of the orbit, lungs, and kidney</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MJR73DS3","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"15_hypolipidemic_drugs","title":"15.Hypolipidemic Drugs","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA2016","text":"A 58-year-old patient with‍ coronary artery disease ​has a deranged lipid prof​ile and is started on sta‍tins. Which enzyme does t​his drug act on?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old patient with coronary artery disease has a deranged lipid profile and is started on statins. Which enzyme does this drug act on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acyl coA‍ synthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acyl coA ‍reductase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HMG coA s‍ynthetase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HMG coA ‍reductase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HMG coA reductase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9ab5f58a5a14b499192aef0fe17276dx1280x788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HMG coA reductase</strong></p><p><strong>Statins</strong> act on the <strong>HMG-CoA reductase</strong> enzyme, thus inhibiting cholesterol synthesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Necrotizing myopathy is a newly defined form of idiopathic inflammatory myopathy. Patients who have anti-HMGCR antibodies and use of statin therapy to control high cholesterol may unknowingly develop this type of statin-induced necrotizing myopathy.\"}'><strong>Necrotizing myopathy</strong> is a newly defined form of idiopathic inflammatory myopathy. Patients who have <strong>anti-HMG Co-A reductase antibodies </strong>should not be given statins to control high cholesterol as they may develop this type of <strong>statin-induced</strong> necrotizing myopathy.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2964","text":"Hepatic cholesterol synthe‍sis is maximal between mi​dnight and 2 am. So most ​of the statins are prefer‍ably administered in the ​evening for maximal effec‍t. Which statin can be ta‌ken irrespective of the t​ime of the day?","raw_text":"Hepatic cholesterol synthesis is maximal between midnight and 2 am. So most of the statins are preferably administered in the evening for maximal effect. Which statin can be taken irrespective of the time of the day?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simva‍statin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prava‍statin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atorva‍statin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lovas‍tatin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atorvastatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atorvastatin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Atorvastatin </strong>has a longer half-life (19 ± 9 hours) and may be taken at<strong> any time of the day.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other drugs that can be taken irrespective of the time of day:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Rosuvastatin - </strong>has a half-life of 20 ± 6 hours</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Pitavastatin</strong> - has a half-life of 12 hours </li>\n</ul>\n<p>All other statins, which have a plasma half-life of around 4 hours or less, should preferably be administered in the evening.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2020","text":"A patient with hypercholes‍terolemia on statin thera​py presents to your OPD f​or follow-up. You notice ‍his cholesterol is still ​high and decide to add on‍ another drug that direct‌ly inhibits the absorptio​n of cholesterol. Which o‍f the following will you ‍prescribe?","raw_text":"A patient with hypercholesterolemia on statin therapy presents to your OPD for follow-up. You notice his cholesterol is still high and decide to add on another drug that directly inhibits the absorption of cholesterol. Which of the following will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ezet‍imibe","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Orli‍stat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholest‍yramine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ezetimibe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ezetimibe</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ezetimibe </strong>is the drug that prevents hypercholesterolemia by directly<strong> inhibiting </strong>the<strong> absorption of cholesterol.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Ezetimibe acts by interfering with a specific cholesterol transport protein <strong>NPC1L1</strong> in the <strong>intestinal mucosa</strong> and reduces the absorption of both dietary and biliary cholesterol. It causes a compensatory increase in hepatic cholesterol synthesis but LDL cholesterol is decreased. <strong>Statins</strong> block this enhanced cholesterol synthesis (cholesterol synthesis inhibitors) and so their combination has a <strong>synergistic effect</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B: <strong>Orlistat</strong> is an anti-obesity drug that acts by <strong>inhibiting pancreatic and gastric lipase</strong> and thus interferes with the digestion and absorption of dietary triglycerides.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Cholestyramine</strong> is a <strong>bile acid sequestrant</strong>. The bile-acid sequestrants are highly positively charged and bind negatively charged bile acids. Because of their large size, the resins are not absorbed, and the bound bile acids are excreted in the stool.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Cholestyramine primarily <strong>reduces LDL levels</strong> by <strong>increasing hepatic bile acid synthesis</strong>; which results in increased LDL uptake and diversion of cholesterol to bile acid production. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Niacin</strong> reduces lipolysis by <strong>hormone-sensitive lipase</strong> (HSL) and reduces hepatic triglyceride synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2027","text":"Which drug decreases Lipop‍rotein(a)?","raw_text":"Which drug decreases Lipoprotein(a)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fenof‍ibrate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gemfi‍brozil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coles‍tipol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Niacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Niacin</strong></p><p>The drug that decreases Lipoprotein(a) is <strong>niacin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Niacin is the only lipid-lowering agent that <strong>lowers lipoprotein(a)</strong> levels significantly. It also results in a <strong>decrease in LDL, VLDL, </strong>and<strong> triglycerides</strong> along with an <strong>increase in HDL</strong> cholesterol.</p>\n<p>The main adverse effect of niacin is cutaneous flushing and pruritus which can be controlled by pretreatment with aspirin. Niacin can also cause hepatotoxicity, GI toxicity, and hyperuricemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2959","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs would you warn a patie​nt taking statins against​, due to the highest risk‍ of myopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs would you warn a patient taking statins against, due to the highest risk of myopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fenof‍ibrate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gemfi‍brozil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coles‍tipol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gemfibrozil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gemfibrozil</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Myopathy risk</strong> is <strong>highest</strong> when <strong>statins</strong> are used along <strong>with gemfibrozil.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Gemfibrozil is a fibrate that<strong> inhibits </strong>the<strong> uptake of statins</strong> into the hepatocytes and <strong>prevents</strong> its <strong>metabolism</strong>, thereby increasing its bioavailability. Thus, the risk of toxicity of statins increases.</p>\n<p>Other drugs interacting similarly with statins include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyclosporine</li>\n<li>Digoxin</li>\n<li>Warfarin</li>\n<li>Macrolide antibiotics</li>\n<li>Azole antifungals.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A: Fenofibrate does not have much interaction with statins.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: Colestipol is a bile acid sequestrant and does not have much effect on statin metabolism.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: Niacin can also rarely increase the risk of <strong>statin-induced myopathy</strong> but the <strong>risk</strong> is much <strong>lower</strong> than that with gemfibrozil. So here gemfibrozil is the best answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3019","text":"A patient who is on statin‍ therapy asks about a new​ drug called evolocumab t​hat his friend is taking ‍for hyperlipidemia. What ​is the route of administr‍ation of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient who is on statin therapy asks about a new drug called evolocumab that his friend is taking for hyperlipidemia. What is the route of administration of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intram‍uscular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intra‍dermal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcut‍aneous","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intra‍venous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subcutaneous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subcutaneous</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Evolocumab </strong>is a <strong>PCSK-9 inhibitor </strong>that is administered as<strong> </strong>a <strong>subcutaneous</strong> injection.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">PCSK-9 is a<strong> </strong>protease that binds to the LDL receptors on the surface of hepatocyte and enhances the lysosomal degradation of the LDL receptor. This results in higher plasma LDL levels. inhibition of PCSK-9 results in a <strong>reduction of plasma LDL</strong> levels. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Two approved PCSK-9 inhibitors are alirocumab and evolocumab.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2999","text":"Which among the following ‍is a microsomal triglycer​ide transfer protein inhi​bitor?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a microsomal triglyceride transfer protein inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ticlo‍pidine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gugu‍lipid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lomit‍apide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Evina‍cumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lomitapide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lomitapide</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein <strong>(MTP) inhibitor</strong> is<strong> lomitapide.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">MTP adds triglycerides to nascent VLDL in the liver and to chylomicrons in the intestine. Its inhibition <strong>decreases VLDL secretion</strong> and thus decreases the accumulation of LDL in the plasma. It is approved for use in <strong>homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Options A: <strong>Ticlopidine</strong> is an <strong>antiplatelet</strong> agent. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B: <strong>Gugulipid</strong> is a <strong>plant extract</strong> that is commonly used for hyperlipidemia but has very little/no evidence to support its use.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Evinacumab</strong> is a recently approved angiopoietin-like 3 (<strong>ANGPTL3</strong>) <strong>inhibitor.</strong> It is indicated as an adjunct to other LDL-cholesterol lowering therapies for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia in adults and pediatric patients aged 12 years and older.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2014","text":"A patient with hereditary ‍hyperlipoproteinemia is p​rescribed a fibrate. What​ is the mechanism of acti‍on of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with hereditary hyperlipoproteinemia is prescribed a fibrate. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased lipolysis of tri‍glycerides","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibit lipolysis in adipo‍se tissues","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibit HMG CoA synthetase‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binds bile acids and bile ‍salts in the intestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased lipolysis of triglycerides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased lipolysis of triglycerides</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Fibrates</strong> <strong>increase</strong> the lipoprotein lipase activity through <strong>PPAR-<span class=\"s1\">α</span></strong> and cause increased<strong> lipolysis</strong> <strong>of triglycerides</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is a key enzyme in the degradation of VLDL. Fibrates bind to PPAR alpha and cause <strong>stimulation</strong> of <strong>lipoprotein lipase</strong> enzyme. Increased LPL synthesis enhances the clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins. Additionally, fibrates also <strong>stimulate fatty acid oxidation</strong> and <strong>reduce</strong> the expression of <strong>apo C-III</strong> (inhibitor of lipolysis).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 531px; width: 927px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.796875px;\"><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.375px;\"><strong>HMG- CoA reductase inhibitors (statins)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.390625px;\"><strong>Fibrates</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.484375px;\"><strong>Niacin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.9375px;\"><strong>Bile acid-binding resins</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.046875px;\"><strong>Ezetimibe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.796875px;\">Important examples</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.375px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Lovastatin, atorvastatin, fluvastatin, pravastatin, simvastatin, rosuvastatin, pitavastatin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.390625px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Gemfibrozil and fenofibrate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.484375px;\">Niacin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.9375px;\"> \n<p class=\"p1\">Colestipol, cholestyramine, colesevelam</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.046875px;\">Ezetimibe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.796875px;\">Mechanism of action</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.375px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Inhibits HMG-CoA reductase</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.390625px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">PPAR-<span class=\"s1\">α mediated increased lipolysis</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.484375px;\">Decreases the production of VLDLs and decreases the catabolism of HDLs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.9375px;\">Binds to bile acids in the intestinal lumen and increases excretion. This results in increased conversion of hepatic cholesterol to bile acids, and reduction in hepatic cholesterol content.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.046875px;\">Inhibits intestinal sterol absorption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.796875px;\">Major effects on lipoprotein levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.375px;\">\n<p>Reduces LDL levels</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.390625px;\">\n<p>Reduces triglyceride levels</p>\n<p>A moderate increase in HDL levels</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.484375px;\">\n<p>Reduces triglycerides,  LDL, and lipoprotein a levels</p>\n<p>Significant <strong>increase HDL</strong> levels</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.9375px;\">Reduces LDL levels </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.046875px;\">\n<p>Reduces LDL cholesterol</p>\n<p>Minimal increase in HDL level</p>\n<p>Also decreases the absorption of phytosterols</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.796875px;\">Important side effects</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.375px;\"><strong>Myopathy</strong>\n<p class=\"p1\">Elevations of serum aminotransferase activity</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Elevation of creatine kinase activity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.390625px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Rashes, gastrointestinal symptoms, myopathy, arrhythmias</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Hypokalemia, elevated aminotransferases, or alkaline phosphatase </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.484375px;\">\n<p>Cutaneous vasodilation</p>\n<p>Elevations in aminotransferases levels </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.9375px;\">\n<p>Constipation</p>\n<p>Bloating </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.046875px;\">\n<p>Reversible hepatic function impairment</p>\n<p>Rarely myositis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2982","text":"A 45-year-old obese man pr‍esented to your OPD. His ​lipid profile showed elev​ated levels of cholestero‍l and triglycerides. On e​xamination, you notice pa‍lmar xanthomas. What is t‌he drug of choice for his​ condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old obese man presented to your OPD. His lipid profile showed elevated levels of cholesterol and triglycerides. On examination, you notice palmar xanthomas. What is the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‍tins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibr‍ates","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aliro‍cumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b41419073a741deb611315c1b643f21x1280x1144.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibrates</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given scenario of elevated cholesterol and triglycerides along with the presence of <strong>palmar xanthomas</strong> (pathognomonic sign) is indicative of <strong>type III hyperlipoproteinemia. </strong>The drugs of choice for this condition are<strong> fibrates.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Fibrates are also preferred in patients having severe hypertriglyceridemia (TG &gt;1000 mg/dl) who are at risk for pancreatitis. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The response to fibrate therapy in Type III hyperlipoproteinemia patients include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Elevated cholesterol and triglyceride levels are dramatically lowered.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Tuberoeruptive and palmar xanthomas may regress completely.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Angina and intermittent claudication also improve.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>palmar xanthoma</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2034","text":"What is the drug of choice‍ for type IIa and IIb hyp​erlipoproteinemias?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for type IIa and IIb hyperlipoproteinemias?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‍tins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fibr‍ates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bile acid s‍equestrants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Statins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Statins</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The drugs of choice for <strong>type IIa and IIb hyperlipoproteinemias</strong> are <strong>statins</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In type IIa or IIb hyperlipoproteinemias, there is increased LDL cholesterol with +/- increase in TG.  Statins have the <strong>maximum LDL lowering</strong> property among the hypolipidemic drugs. Therefore, they are the drugs of choice in this case.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In <strong>type III/IV hyperlipoproteinemias, </strong>there is mainly an increase in triglyceride(TG) level<strong>. Fibrates</strong> reduce the TGs contained in VLDL and increase the HDL. So they are the drugs of choice for these conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3017","text":"A patient with homozygous ‍familial hypercholesterol​emia has mipomersen as pa​rt of his treatment regim‍en. By which of the follo​wing mechanisms does this‍ drug act?","raw_text":"A patient with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has mipomersen as part of his treatment regimen. By which of the following mechanisms does this drug act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of microsomal t‍riglyceride transfer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibitio‍n of PCSK9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of apo B-100 sy‍nthesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of cholesterol ‍absorption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibition of apo B-100 synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibition of apo B-100 synthesis</strong></p><p>Mipomersen is an <strong>antisense oligonucleotide </strong>that acts as an <strong>inhibitor</strong> of<strong> apo B-100 synthesis. </strong></p>\n<p>Mipomersen binds to the mRNA of apo B-100 resulting in degradation of apo B-100 mRNA and eventually reduction of apo B-100 synthesis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"wYiZcMU1","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"15_motor_physiology___2","title":"15.Motor Physiology   2","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0684","text":"When dynamic γ-motor neuro​ns are activated at the s​ame time as α-motor neuro​ns to muscle, ________","raw_text":"When dynamic γ-motor neurons are activated at the same time as α-motor neurons to muscle, ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prompt inhibition of disch​arge in spindle Ia affere​nts takes place.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The muscle will not contra​ct.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The number of impulses in ​spindle Ia afferents is s​maller than when α discha​rge alone is increased.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The number of impulses in ​spindle Ia afferents is g​reater than when α discha​rge alone is increased.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The number of impulses in spindle Ia afferents is greater than when α discharge alone is increased.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The number of impulses in spindle Ia afferents is greater than when α discharge alone is increased.</strong></p><p>When dynamic γ-motor neurons are activated at the same time as α-motor neurons to muscle, the number of impulses in spindle Ia afferents is greater than when α discharge alone is increased.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When α–γ coactivation occurs, intrafusal and extrafusal fibers shorten together, and spindle afferent activity can occur throughout the period of muscle contraction.</li>\n<li>Stimulation of γ-motor neurons causes increase discharge in spindle Ia afferent, leading to reflex contraction of the muscle.</li>\n<li>When a muscle contracts it leads to shortening of the muscle spindles and so impulses in the Ia afferents decreases.</li>\n<li>Thus, muscles can be made to contract via stimulation of the α-motor neurons that innervate the extrafusal fibers or the γ-motor neurons that initiate contraction indirectly via the stretch reflex.  </li>\n<li>If the whole muscle is stretched during stimulation of the γ-motor neurons, the rate of discharge in the Ia fibers is further increased. Increased γ-motor neuron activity thus increases <strong>spindle sensitivity </strong>during stretch. This is the basis of the Jendrassik manouver. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0656","text":"The myogram below shows a ​slow onset and a longer a​fter-discharge. Which typ​e of reflex does it repre‌sent?","raw_text":"The myogram below shows a slow onset and a longer after-discharge. Which type of reflex does it represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor withd​rawal reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stretch​ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crossed ext​ensor reflex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inverse str​etch reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Crossed extensor reflex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e4fbf49ae974bc288aa0f04a6339228.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/680f0c4b777a4e6cbeaabafa31052121x1280x1037.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Crossed extensor reflex</strong></p><p>The figure shows the typical myogram recorded from a muscle involved in a crossed extensor reflex. This myogram demonstrates the relatively long latency before the reflex begins and the long after-discharge at the end of the stimulus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When a painful stimulus is applied to the body, the flexor withdrawal and the crossed extensor reflexes are stimulated.</li>\n<li>About 0.2 to 0.5 seconds after the flexor reflex in one limb, the opposite limb begins to extend. This reflex is called the <strong>crossed extensor reflex</strong>.</li>\n<li>After the painful stimulus is removed, the crossed extensor reflex has an even longer period of after-discharge than does the flexor reflex. This prolonged after-discharge results from reverberating circuits among the inter-neuronal cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Compare the myograms of a flexor reflex with a crossed extensor reflex.</p>\n<p>In the flexor reflex, the contraction occurs immediately after applying the stimulus and the after-discharge is smaller. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0649","text":"The bulboreticular facilit​atory area of the brain s​tem excites the gamma eff​erent system. This bulbor‌eticular area receives im​pulses from which of the ​following areas?","raw_text":"The bulboreticular facilitatory area of the brain stem excites the gamma efferent system. This bulboreticular area receives impulses from which of the following areas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal ​Ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebra​l Cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>Impulses are transmitted into the bulboreticular area from</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cerebellum,</li>\n<li>Basal ganglia, and</li>\n<li>Cerebral cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The gamma efferent system is excited by signals from the bulboreticular facilitatory region of the brain stem.</p>\n<p>When the bulboreticular facilitatory region transmits excitatory signals through the gamma nerve fibers to the intrafusal muscle fibers of the muscle spindles, it shortens the ends of the spindles and stretches the central receptor regions, thus increasing their signal output. If the spindles on both sides of each joint are activated at the same time, then reflex excitation of the skeletal muscles on both sides of the joint increases, producing tight, tense muscles opposing each other at the joint. The net effect is that the position of the joint becomes strongly stabilized.</p>\n<p>Any time a person must perform a muscle function that requires a high degree of delicate and exact positioning, the bulboreticular facilitatory region of the brain stem stabilizes the positions of the proximal joints.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0689","text":"Which of the following des​cribes components of the ​central pathway responsib​le for control of posture‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes components of the central pathway responsible for control of posture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The pontine reticulospinal​ pathway terminates on ne​urons in the dorsomedial ​area of the spinal ventra‌l horn that innervate fle​xor muscles.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The tectospinal pathway te​rminates on neurons in th​e dorsolateral area of th​e spinal ventral horn tha‌t innervate limb muscles.​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The medullary reticulospin​al pathway terminates on ​neurons in the ventromedi​al area of the spinal ven‌tral horn that innervate ​axial and proximal muscle​s.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The medullary reticulospinal pathway terminates on neurons in the ventromedial area of the spinal ventral horn that innervate axial and proximal muscles.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The medullary reticulospinal pathway terminates on neurons in the ventromedial area of the spinal ventral horn that innervate axial and proximal muscles.</strong></p><p>The medullary reticulospinal pathway terminates on neurons in the ventromedial area of the spinal ventral horn, that innervate axial and proximal muscles.</p>\n<p>The medullary reticular nuclei transmit inhibitory signals to the antigravity anterior motor neurons by way of the medullary reticulospinal tract, located in the lateral column of the cord. </p>\n<p>Option <strong>A</strong>: The <strong>pontine reticular nuclei</strong> transmit excitatory signals through the pontine reticulospinal tract in the anterior column of the cord.<strong> </strong>The fibers of this pathway terminate on the <strong>medial anterior</strong> motor neurons that excite the axial muscles of the body, that is the muscles of the vertebral column and the <strong>extensor</strong> muscles of the limbs. </p>\n<p>Option <strong>B</strong>: The <strong>tectospinal</strong> tract originates in the superior colliculus of the midbrain. It projects to the contralateral cervical spinal cord to control head and eye movements.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0690","text":"Which of the following is ​not a medial pathway invo​lved in maintenance of po​sture?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a medial pathway involved in maintenance of posture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticulosp​inal Tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubrospi​nal Tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibulos​pinal Tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tectospi​nal Tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rubrospinal Tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rubrospinal Tract</strong></p><p>The rubrospinal tract is a part of the lateral brainstem pathway.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>medial brainstem pathways</strong> : pontine and medullary reticulospinal, vestibulospinal, and tectospinal tracts.\n<ul>\n<li>These pathways descend in the ipsilateral ventral columns of the spinal cord and terminate predominantly on interneurons in the ventromedial part of the ventral horn to control axial and proximal muscles. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The <strong>lateral brainstem pathways</strong> : The corticospinal tract and the rubrospinal tract.\n<ul>\n<li>They cross the midline and project to interneurons in the dorsolateral part of the spinal ventral horn to also influence motor neurons that control distal limb muscles.</li>\n<li>The rubrospinal tract excites flexor motor neurons and inhibits extensor motor neurons. This pathway may play a role in the posture typical of decorticate rigidity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0688","text":"Increased neural activity ​before a skilled voluntar​y movement is first seen ​in ____.","raw_text":"Increased neural activity before a skilled voluntary movement is first seen in ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"precentral ​motor cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"midb​rain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"cereb​ellum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"cortical association areas​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. cortical association areas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b4c4c8c577e4138a6589f5c4339cd8dx1279x1693.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. cortical association areas</strong></p><p>The command for voluntary movement originates in cortical association areas, then planned in the cortex as well as in the basal ganglia and the lateral portions of the cerebellar hemispheres. Hence, the neural activity first originates in the cortical association areas.</p>\n<p>The basal ganglia and cerebellum funnel information to the premotor and motor cortex by way of the thalamus.</p>\n<p>Motor commands from the motor cortex are relayed in large part via the corticospinal tracts to the spinal cord. </p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Motor Association Area: Idea about voluntary movement            </li>\n<li>Basal Ganglia: Planning and Organising the act            </li>\n<li>Cerebellum( Neocerebellum) - Planning the act                                  </li>\n<li>Premotor Cortex: Setting the posture before the act            </li>\n<li>Primary Motor Cortex: Executing the act</li>\n<li>Spinocerebellum : Smoothening the act</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0693","text":"Which of the following des​cending motor tracts is e​ssential for rapid skille​d movement?","raw_text":"Which of the following descending motor tracts is essential for rapid skilled movement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior corticospinal tra​ct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubrospi​nal tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral vestibulospinal tr​act","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary Reticulospinal t​ract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rubrospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rubrospinal tract</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral corticospinal </strong>and <strong>rubrospinal tracts</strong> are concerned with distal limb muscles and, with <strong>skilled voluntary movements</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ventral corticospinal tract </strong>and<strong> medial descending brainstem pathways</strong> (tectospinal, reticulospinal, and vestibulospinal tracts) are concerned with the <strong>axial muscles</strong> - postural adjustments and gross movements.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Medial portion of the ventral horn innervate proximal limb muscles, particularly the flexors. Lateral ventral horn innervates distal limb muscles.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0691","text":"Which of the following is ​true about experimental s​ham rage?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about experimental sham rage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs in decorticate anim​als","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Is goal ​directed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is caused by removing the ​hypothalamus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Appears and​ goes slowly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Occurs in decorticate animals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Occurs in decorticate animals</strong></p><p>Sham rage occurs in <strong>decorticate animals</strong> is the true statement.</p>\n<p>Sham rage is a controversial term used to describe behavior such as biting, clawing, hissing, arching the back and \"violent alternating limb movements\" produced in animal experiments by <strong>removing the cerebral cortex</strong>, which is claimed to occur in the absence of any sort of inner experience of rage.</p>\n<p>The reason for this might be that the removal of the neocortex causes a loss of inhibition of the areas involved in the rage reaction, causing those areas to become hyperactive (due to an uninhibited hypothalamus). </p>\n<p>Removal of hypothalamus abolishes the sham rage.</p>\n<p>Sham rage may be triggered by very mild stimuli such as weak touch. It is also <strong>non-goal directed</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0694","text":"A 62-year-old known hypert​ensive woman presented to​ the emergency with spast​icity of both lower limbs‌. Further neurological ex​amination showed regular,​ repetitive, rhythmic con‌tractions of a muscle sub‌jected to sudden, maintai​ned stretch. Which of the‌ following is false regar​ding this phenomenon?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old known hypertensive woman presented to the emergency with spasticity of both lower limbs. Further neurological examination showed regular, repetitive, rhythmic contractions of a muscle subjected to sudden, maintained stretch. Which of the following is false regarding this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only sustained clonus with​ five or more beats is co​nsidered abnormal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clonus may also occur in s​pinal cord injury that di​srupts the descending cor​tical input to a spinal g‌lycinergic inhibitory int​erneuron called the Rensh​aw cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The stretch reflex–inverse​ stretch reflex sequence ​may contribute to this re​sponse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"This is characteristic of ​states in which decreased​ γ-motor neuron discharge​ is present","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. This is characteristic of states in which decreased γ-motor neuron discharge is present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. This is characteristic of states in which decreased γ-motor neuron discharge is present</strong></p><p>The case scenario indicates <strong>clonus</strong>. A characteristic of states in which<strong> increased</strong> γ-motor neuron discharge is present is clonus. </p>\n<p>All the other options are correct.</p>\n<p>When a muscle is subjected to a sudden and maintained stretch, there occur regular, repetitive, and rhythmic contractions. This is called clonus. Ankle clonus is a typical example.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0695","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding a clasp​ knife effect is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding a clasp knife effect is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyper​tonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi Tendon Organ is invo​lved","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is called Lengthening R​eaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pacinian Corpuscle is invo​lved","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pacinian Corpuscle is involved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75e31af6952e47b5b565c14b6807919bx600x816.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pacinian Corpuscle is involved</strong></p><p>Pacinian corpuscle is not involved in the clasp knife effect.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the muscles are <strong>hypertonic</strong>, the sequence of moderate stretch leads to muscle contraction, strong stretch leads to muscle relaxation. Eg. Passive flexion of the elbow meets immediate resistance due to stretch reflex in the triceps muscle. Further stretch activates the inverse stretch reflex. The resistance to flexion suddenly collapses, and the arm flexes. This sequence of resistance followed by a sudden decrease in resistance when a limb is moved passively is known as the clasp-knife effect because of its resemblance to the closing of a pocket knife.</li>\n<li>Lengthening reaction: When the tension on the muscle becomes extreme, the inhibitory effect from the Golgi tendon organ leads to an instantaneous relaxation of the entire muscle. It is a protective mechanism to prevent tearing of the muscle. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0701","text":"Fill in the blanks: Effere​nt axons from the dentate​ nucleus terminate in ___​________ thalamic nucleus‌ and travel through _____​______peduncle?","raw_text":"Fill in the blanks: Efferent axons from the dentate nucleus terminate in ___________ thalamic nucleus and travel through ___________peduncle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventral anterior ---middle​ cerebellar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventral lateral---superior​ cerebellar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral lateral ---middle ​cerebellar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral posteromedial ---s​uperior cerebellar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventral lateral---superior cerebellar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventral lateral---superior cerebellar</strong></p><p>Efferent axons from the dentate nucleus terminate in Ventral lateral nucleus of the thalamus (VL) and travel through superior cerebellar peduncle.</p>\n<p>The cerebellar cortex projects to the deep nuclei, which in turn project to the brainstem. The deep nuclei provide the only output for the spinocerebellum and the cerebrocerebellum: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>medial spinocerebellum</strong> projects to the <strong>fastigial nuclei</strong> and from there to the brainstem.</li>\n<li>The adjacent <strong>hemispheric</strong> portions of the <strong>spinocerebellum</strong> project to the <strong>emboliform and globose nuclei</strong> and from there to the brainstem.</li>\n<li>The <strong>cerebrocerebellum</strong> projects to the <strong>dentate nucleus (Purkinje cells of cerebellum relay into this nucleus)</strong> and from there either directly or indirectly to the ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These fibres pass through the cerebellar peduncles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>superior cerebellar peduncle </strong>includes fibers from deep cerebellar nuclei that project to the brainstem, red nucleus, and thalamus. It also contains the ventral spinocerebellar tract fibers.</li>\n<li>The <strong>middle cerebellar peduncle </strong>contains only afferent fibers from the contralateral pontine nuclei.</li>\n<li><strong>Inferior cerebellar peduncle </strong>a mixture of afferent fibers from the brainstem and spinal cord and efferent fibers to the vestibular nuclei.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0702","text":"Which of the following doe​s not carry proprioceptiv​e impulses?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not carry proprioceptive impulses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olivo cereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tecto cereb​ellar tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spino cereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cuneo cereb​ellar tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tecto cerebellar tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tecto cerebellar tract</strong></p><ul>\n<li>The tecto cerebellar tract transmits only the auditory and visual impulses to the cerebellum.</li>\n<li>The <strong>olivocerebellar</strong> pathway projects to the cerebellar cortex via <strong>climbing</strong> fibers; the <strong>rest</strong> of the listed paths project via <strong>mossy</strong> fibers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Afferent Tracts</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Transmits</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vestibulocerebellar</td>\n<td>vestibular impulses from labyrinths, directs &amp; via vestibular nuclei</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dorsal spinocerebellar</td>\n<td>Proprioceptive &amp; exteroceptive impulses from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organ &amp; joint receptors of lower limbs &amp; trunk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ventral spinocerebellar</td>\n<td>Proprioceptive &amp; exteroceptive impulses from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organ &amp; joint receptors of upper &amp; lower limbs  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cunecerebellar</td>\n<td>Proprioceptive &amp; exteroceptive impulses from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organ &amp; joint receptors of upper limb &amp; upper thorax  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tectocerebellar</td>\n<td>Auditory &amp; visual impulses via inferior &amp; superior colliculli, respectively</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pontocerebellar</td>\n<td>Impulse from motor &amp; other parts of cerebral cortex via pontine nuclei</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Olivocerebellar</td>\n<td>Proprioceptive input from whole body  via relay in inferior olive </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0705","text":"A patient has developed an​ inability to control the​ distance, speed, and ran​ge of motion necessary to‌ perform smoothly coordin​ated movements. His finge​r-to-nose test and heel-t‌o-shin tests are abnormal‌. A lesion in which of th​e following sites is most‌ likely to be found?","raw_text":"A patient has developed an inability to control the distance, speed, and range of motion necessary to perform smoothly coordinated movements. His finger-to-nose test and heel-to-shin tests are abnormal. A lesion in which of the following sites is most likely to be found?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal ​Ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinocereb​ellar tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Midb​rain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Po​ns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinocerebellar tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinocerebellar tract</strong></p><p>The given clinical findings are suggestive of <strong>dysmetria</strong> which is most commonly found due to a <strong>lesion</strong> involving the <strong>cerebellum</strong> or the c<strong>erebellar pathways</strong>. Therefore, a lesion in the <strong>spinocerebellar tract</strong> is most likely to be found in this patient. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dysmetria - </strong>In the absence of the cerebellum, the subconscious motor control system cannot predict how far movements will go. Therefore, the movements ordinarily overshoot their intended mark; then the conscious portion of the brain overcompensates in the opposite direction for the succeeding compensatory movement.  Dysmetria also results from lesions in the spinocerebellar tracts because <strong>feedback information</strong> from the moving parts of the body to the cerebellum is essential for cerebellar timing of movement termination.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0697","text":"Dysdiadochokinesia refers ​to which of the following​?","raw_text":"Dysdiadochokinesia refers to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inability to perform the T​andem-Gait test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inability to stand without​ swaying with eyes open","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inability to control vesti​bulo-ocular reflexes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inability to perform rapid​ alternating movements wi​th the hands","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inability to perform rapid alternating movements with the hands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inability to perform rapid alternating movements with the hands</strong></p><p><strong>Dysdiadochokinesia—Inability to Perform Rapid Alternating Movements. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In cerebellar lesions, the motor control system fails to predict where the different parts of the body will be at a given time. As a result, the succeeding movement may begin much too early or much too late and no orderly “progression of movement” can occur.</li>\n<li>So, a series of stalled attempted but jumbled movements occurs instead of the normal coordinated movements. This condition is called dysdiadochokinesia. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0696","text":"The patient exhibited an u​nsteady \"drunk like\" gait​ and the physician noted ​that he swayed while ente‌ring the examination room​. Which region of the cer​ebellum was most likely a‌ffected by the bleed?","raw_text":"The patient exhibited an unsteady \"drunk like\" gait and the physician noted that he swayed while entering the examination room. Which region of the cerebellum was most likely affected by the bleed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flocculono​dular Lobe","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinoce​rebellum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebroc​erebellum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superio​r Vermis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flocculonodular Lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flocculonodular Lobe</strong></p><p><strong>Flocculonodular Lobe</strong> is involved in the unsteady \"drunk like\" gait of the patient.</p>\n<p>Cerebellum has three parts based on function.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vestibulocerebellum </strong>(or <strong>flocculonodular lobe</strong>): The oldest part of the cerebellum which contains nodulus in the vermis and the flanking flocculus. It has vestibular connections and is concerned with<strong> equilibrium and gait.  Loss </strong>of it<strong> </strong>can cause <strong>disturbance of equilibrium and postural movements, </strong>especially during rapid movements.<strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Spinocerebellum: </strong>The region that receives proprioceptive input from the body as well as a copy of the “motor plan” from the motor cortex. By comparing the plan with performance, it<strong> smooths and coordinates movements</strong> that are ongoing. Superior vermis is a part of spinocerebellum. Loss of it can cause problems with coordination.</li>\n<li><strong>Cerebrocerebellum: </strong>They are the newest from a phylogenetic point of view, reaching their greatest development in humans. They interact with the motor cortex in <strong>planning and programming movements.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0714","text":"The efferent fibres of the​ basal ganglia include wh​ich of the following? 1. ​Globus Pallidus Interna 2‌. Globus Pallidus externa​ 3. Substantia Nigra pars​ compacta 4. Substantia N‌igra pars reticulata","raw_text":"<p>The efferent fibres of the basal ganglia include which of the following?</p>\n<p>1. Globus Pallidus Interna<br/>2. Globus Pallidus externa<br/>3. Substantia Nigra pars compacta<br/>4. Substantia Nigra pars reticulata</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,​ 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,​ 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe662df496f14440bf3a0858df044096x1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4</strong></p><p>The two major outputs/ efferents of the basal ganglia are from<strong> Globus pallidus</strong> <strong>interna</strong> and <strong>substantia nigra pars reticulata</strong>. Both are inhibitory (GABAergic) and both project to the ventrolateral and ventroanterior thalamus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0716","text":"Which of the following des​cribes a connection betwe​en components of the basa​l ganglia?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes a connection between components of the basal ganglia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The subthalamic nucleus re​leases glutamate to excit​e the globus pallidus, in​ternal segment.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The substantia nigra pars ​reticulata releases dopam​ine to excite the globus ​pallidus, external segmen‌t.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The striatum releases acet​ylcholine to excite the s​ubstantia nigra pars reti​culata.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The globus pallidus, exter​nal segment releases glut​amate to excite the stria​tum.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The subthalamic nucleus releases glutamate to excite the globus pallidus, internal segment.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a307b6f04764bf7a100282bd978d016x1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The subthalamic nucleus releases glutamate to excite the globus pallidus, internal segment.</strong></p><p><strong>Intranuclear Connections</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dopaminergic nigrostriatal projection from the substantia nigra pars compacta to the striatum. GABAergic projection from the striatum to substantia nigra pars reticulata.  </li>\n<li>There is an inhibitory (GABAergic) projection from the striatum to both GPe and GPi.  </li>\n<li>The subthalamic nucleus receives an inhibitory input from GPe, and in turn the subthalamic nucleus has an excitatory (glutamate) projection to both GPe and GPi.  </li>\n<li>From the ventrolateral thalamus, there is an excitatory (presumably glutamate) projection to the prefrontal and premotor cortex. This completes a full cortical-basal ganglia-thalamic-cortical loop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0717","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the funct​ions of basal ganglia ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the functions of basal ganglia ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurons in the basal gangl​ia, like those in the lat​eral portions of the cere​bellar hemispheres, disch‌arge before movements beg​in.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The putamen circuit has it​s inputs mainly from the ​parts of the brain adjace​nt to the primary motor c‌ortex but not much from t​he primary motor cortex i​tself.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The caudate nucleus plays ​a major role in this cogn​itive control of motor ac​tivity.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Substantia nigra pars reti​culata is a major source ​of dopaminergic neurons t​o the corpus striatum.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Substantia nigra pars reticulata is a major source of dopaminergic neurons to the corpus striatum.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f0844877cc4649cc9979c5ab506420cex1280x1582.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fe0787ab1cc43e9a0fc0fb1f853995bx1280x1563.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Substantia nigra pars reticulata is a major source of dopaminergic neurons to the corpus striatum.</strong></p><p>Substantia nigra <strong>pars compacta </strong>(not pars reticulata) is the major source of dopaminergic neurons to the striatum.</p>\n<p>Neurons in the basal ganglia, like those in the lateral portions of the cerebellar hemispheres, discharge before movements begin since the basal ganglia are involved in the planning and organization of skilled movements.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The putamen circuit</strong> has its inputs mainly from the parts of the brain adjacent to the primary motor cortex. Then its outputs do go mainly back to the primary motor cortex and adjacent premotor cortex. it is mainly involved in complex motor activity and skilled movement.  </li>\n<li><strong>The caudate circuit</strong> plays a major role in the cognitive control of motor activity.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0723","text":"In a patient suffering fro​m parkinsons's disease, w​hat is the most likely re​ason behind the gradual d‌ecline in the therapeutic​ effect of L-dopa?","raw_text":"In a patient suffering from parkinsons's disease, what is the most likely reason behind the gradual decline in the therapeutic effect of L-dopa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibodies to dopamine rec​eptors develop.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibitory pathways grow i​nto the basal ganglia fro​m the frontal lobe.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is an increase in ci​rculating α-synuclein.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The dopaminergic neurons i​n the substantia nigra co​ntinue to degenerate.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra continue to degenerate.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra continue to degenerate.</strong></p><p>The most likely reason behind the gradual <strong>decline</strong> in the <strong>therapeutic effect</strong> of L-dopa in a patient suffering from parkinsons's disease is the <strong>continued degeneration</strong> of the <strong>dopaminergic neurons</strong> in the <strong>substantia nigra</strong>. Since endogenous production of dopamine continues to decline, the exogenous source of dopamine (i.e. L-dopa) alone fails to maintain the same level of therapeutic effect.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parkinson's disease (Paralysis Agitans)</strong> was shown to result from the <strong>degeneration</strong> of <strong>dopaminergic neurons</strong> in the substantia nigra pars compacta. The fibres to the <strong>putamen</strong> (part of the striatum) are most severely <strong>affected</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dopaminergic neurons </strong>and <strong>dopamine receptors </strong>are steadily<strong> lost</strong> with<strong> age</strong> in the basal ganglia in healthy individuals, and an <strong>acceleration</strong> of these losses apparently precipitates <strong>parkinsonism</strong>. Symptoms appear when 60–80% of the nigrostriatal dopaminergic neurons degenerate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0718","text":"A 50-year-old gentleman wh​o is a known patient of p​arkinson's disease, devel​ops refractoriness to L-d‌opa therapy. An alternati​ve treatment modality is ​being planned, which empl‌oys ablation of certain t‌halamic regions by neuros​urgery. Which thalamic nu‌cleus is the stereotaxic ​target for the neurosurge‌ons?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old gentleman who is a known patient of parkinson's disease, develops refractoriness to L-dopa therapy. An alternative treatment modality is being planned, which employs ablation of certain thalamic regions by neurosurgery. Which thalamic nucleus is the stereotaxic target for the neurosurgeons?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centro​median","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventro​lateral","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventropost​erolateral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventro​medial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventrolateral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventrolateral</strong></p><p>The <strong>ventrolateral thalamic nucleus</strong> is the <strong>stereotaxic target</strong> for the neurosurgeons to alleviate symptoms in <strong>Parkinson's disease</strong> refractory to levodopa. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The two major outputs of the basal ganglia are from GPi and substantia nigra pars reticulata. Both are inhibitory (GABAergic) and both project to the <strong>ventrolateral and ventroanterior </strong>thalamus. From the thalamus, there is an excitatory (presumably glutamate) projection to the prefrontal and premotor cortex. This completes a full cortical-basal ganglia-thalamic-cortical loop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore stereotactic ablation of ventrolateral thalamic nucleus helps in alleviation of symptoms in <strong>parkinson's disease</strong> refractory to levodopa. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cVNTSwDj","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"15_anxiety_disorders","title":"15.Anxiety Disorders","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC0658","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an anxiogenic substan​ce?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an anxiogenic substance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caff‌eine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholecy‌stokinin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbon ‌dioxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurope‌ptide Y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuropeptide Y","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuropeptide Y</strong></p><p><strong>Neuropeptide Y</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an anxiogenic substance, it's an <strong>anxiolytic</strong> substance.</p>\n<p><strong>Anxiogenic</strong> substances <strong>provoke</strong> anxiety. Examples are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cholecystokinin</strong></li>\n<li>Flumazenil</li>\n<li><strong>Caffeine</strong></li>\n<li>Theophylline</li>\n<li>Yohimbine</li>\n<li>Sodium lactate</li>\n<li>Cocaine</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anxiolytic</strong> substances <strong>reduce</strong> anxiety. Examples are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benzodiazepines</li>\n<li>Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors</li>\n<li>Beta-blockers</li>\n<li><strong>Neuropeptide Y</strong></li>\n<li>Buspirone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0861","text":"A first-year MBBS student ‌came to OPD with intermit​tent episodes of a feelin​g of impending doom accom‌panied by intense perspir‌ation. Usually, the episo​des occur prior to her ex‍ams. What is the most lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A first-year MBBS student came to OPD with intermittent episodes of a feeling of impending doom accompanied by intense perspiration. Usually, the episodes occur prior to her exams. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panic d‌isorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Conversio‌n disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social anxi‌ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generalized anxiety disord‌er","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Panic disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Panic disorder</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis for this patient is<strong> panic disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>panic attack</strong> is an abrupt surge of <strong>intense fear</strong> or intense discomfort. Recurrent panic attacks for more than one month is classified as <strong>panic disorder.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 412\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Panic attacks can occur in mental disorders other than panic disorder, particu­larly in specific phobia, social phobia, and PTSD.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7080","text":"Which of the following is ‌not classified under anxi​ety disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not classified under anxiety disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social anxi‌ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-traumatic stress diso‌rder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Specifi‌c Phobia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panic ‌attacks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Post-traumatic stress disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Post-traumatic stress disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Post-traumatic stress disorder </strong>is classified under <strong>t</strong><strong>rauma and stressor-related disorder</strong> according to <strong>DSM-5</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to <strong>ICD-11</strong>, it is classified under <strong>disorders specifically associated with stress</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following disorders are classified under anxiety disorders:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Social anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Specific phobia</li>\n<li>Agoraphobia</li>\n<li>Panic attacks</li>\n<li>Generalized anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Separation anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Selective mutism</li>\n<li>Substance/medication-induced anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Anxiety disorder due to another medical condition</li>\n<li>Unspecified anxiety disorder</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7081","text":"A 35-year-old lady present‌s to the casualty with sy​mptoms of acute-onset bre​athlessness, palpitations‌, sweating, and nausea. P‌hysical examination and l​aboratory workup were unr‍emarkable. Which of the f​ollowing best explains th‌e episode?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady presents to the casualty with symptoms of acute-onset breathlessness, palpitations, sweating, and nausea. Physical examination and laboratory workup were unremarkable. Which of the following best explains the episode?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pho‌bia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute respiratory distress‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Generalized anxiety disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panic ‌attack","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Panic attack","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Panic attack</strong></p><p>This clinical vignette describes a classic<strong> panic attack</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort (arises de novo without any stressor). Recurrent panic attacks are termed as <strong>panic disorder. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Panic attacks</strong> can occur in mental disorders other than panic disorder, particu­larly in <strong>specific phobia</strong>, <strong>social phobia</strong>, and post traumatic stress disorder (<strong>PTSD</strong>).</p>\n<p>The differential diagnosis for panic attacks:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Angina, MI</li>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse</li>\n<li>Asthma</li>\n<li>Pulmonary embolus</li>\n<li>Pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism (Grave's disease)</li>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>Amphetamine and anticholinergic drugs</li>\n<li>Withdrawal symptoms</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7138","text":"A 30-year-old female was b‌rought to the hospital wi​th complaints of recurren​t episodes of acute onset‌ breathlessness, palpitat‌ions, sweating and dizzin​ess for the past 2 months‍. Physical examination an​d laboratory investigatio‌ns were normal. Which of ​the following is the drug​ of choice in the treatme​nt of this condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female was brought to the hospital with complaints of recurrent episodes of acute onset breathlessness, palpitations, sweating and dizziness for the past 2 months. Physical examination and laboratory investigations were normal. Which of the following is the drug of choice in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selective serotonin reupta‌ke inhibitors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Benzodi‌azepines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monoamine oxidase inhibito‌rs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Busp‌irone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The clinical features of recurrent panic attacks suggest a diagnosis of <strong>panic disorder</strong>. <strong>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors</strong> are the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in panic disorder.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> are used in <strong>acute attacks of panic disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (<strong>MAOIs</strong>) and Tricyclic antidepressants (<strong>TCAs</strong>) are <strong>second-line</strong> drugs for <strong>panic disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Buspirone</strong> is a second-line drug used in <strong>generalized anxiety disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7140","text":"A young woman has a fear o‌f places from where escap​e is difficult. What is t​his condition known as?","raw_text":"A young woman has a fear of places from where escape is difficult. What is this condition known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Claustr‌ophobia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aerop‌hobia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Agora‌phobia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ailuro‌phobia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Agoraphobia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Agoraphobia</strong></p><p><strong>Agoraphobia</strong> is the fear or avoidance of places from where escape would be difficult e.g., crowds, public places, or public transportation.</p>\n<p>It is more common in <strong>women</strong>. It leads to severe restrictions on the individual’s travel and daily routine.</p>\n<p>Option A: Claustrophobia - Fear of closed spaces</p>\n<p>Option B: Aerophobia - Fear of flying </p>\n<p>Option D: Ailurophobia - Fear of cats</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Specific phobia</strong> is the<strong> most common</strong> type of phobia. Agoraphobia is not a specific phobia and is classified separately under anxiety disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7141","text":"A 50-year-old man feels un‌comfortable in using the ​lift, being in crowded pl​aces and travelling. Whic‌h of the following is the‌ most appropriate line of​ management?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man feels uncomfortable in using the lift, being in crowded places and travelling. Which of the following is the most appropriate line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Expressiv‌e therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inter-perso‌nal therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Behaviour‌al therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cognitiv‌e therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Behavioural therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Behavioural therapy</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">These clinical features suggest the diagnosis of <strong>agoraphobia. </strong><strong>Behaviour therapy</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>Various modalities in behavioural therapy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systematic desensitization- gradual exposure to the stimuli so that the patient gets used to the situation</li>\n<li>Exposure and response prevention</li>\n<li>Relaxation technique</li>\n<li>Flooding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drug therapy: <strong>SSRI</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0122","text":"A construction worker expe‌riences intense fear when​ working on a project of ​renovating a high-rise bu‌ilding, which requires hi‌m to climb scaffolding. H​e finds it difficult and ‍awkward to explain this t​o his on-site contractors‌. What is this irrational​ fear known as?","raw_text":"A construction worker experiences intense fear when working on a project of renovating a high-rise building, which requires him to climb scaffolding. He finds it difficult and awkward to explain this to his on-site contractors. What is this irrational fear known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acaro‌phobia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Algop‌hobia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Agora‌phobia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acrop‌hobia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acrophobia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acrophobia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acrophobia</strong>. It is the extreme or irrational fear of <strong>heights.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0727","text":"A 5-year-old girl was brou‌ght to the psychiatric OP​D by her mother with the ​history that her daughter‌ cries terribly when sent‌ to school. When intervie​wed, she keeps worrying a‍bout her mother and that ​something bad might happe‌n to her when she is left​ at school. Her mother al​so mentions that she does​ not sleep well and often‍ experiences nightmares a​bout being away from her ​mother. Which of the foll‌owing best explains the s​ituation?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl was brought to the psychiatric OPD by her mother with the history that her daughter cries terribly when sent to school. When interviewed, she keeps worrying about her mother and that something bad might happen to her when she is left at school. Her mother also mentions that she does not sleep well and often experiences nightmares about being away from her mother. Which of the following best explains the situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agora‌phobia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Panic d‌isorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Generalised anxiety disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Separation anxiety disorde‌r","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Separation anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Separation anxiety disorder</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The history suggests the presence of <strong>separation anxiety disorder</strong> in the daughter.</p>\n<p>Patients with separation anxiety disorder suffer greatly when physically apart from <strong>figures of significant attachment</strong> (in this case, the mother). These patients keep thinking about the possibility of any harm that might occur to them or their loved ones when they are apart. This worry leads to the avoidance of separation.<strong> </strong>Even when they sleep, the individual often has nightmares regarding separation. Symptoms persist longer than <strong>4 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cognitive behavioral therapy + SSRI is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0627","text":"An adolescent complains th‌at she is experiencing a ​significant amount of str​ess because she finds it ‌difficult to concentrate ‌during conversations. She​ is busy worrying about w‍hat others will think of ​her, assuming they will f‌ind her 'dumb' or 'boring​'. After interactions, sh​e replays the conversatio​n over and over again, fo‍cusing on the 'stupid' th​ings she had said. She sa​ys has been isolating her‌self more, turning down i​nvitations from her roomm​ate to go out to eat. Wha‌t is the likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"An adolescent complains that she is experiencing a significant amount of stress because she finds it difficult to concentrate during conversations. She is busy worrying about what others will think of her, assuming they will find her 'dumb' or 'boring'. After interactions, she replays the conversation over and over again, focusing on the 'stupid' things she had said. She says has been isolating herself more, turning down invitations from her roommate to go out to eat. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panic d‌isorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Social anxi‌ety disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Generalised anxiety disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Selectiv‌e mutism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Social anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Social anxiety disorder</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>s</strong><strong>ocial anxiety disorder. </strong>It is a fear of one or more social situations where in the patient is concerned about being harshly judged and evaluated for their performance or interpersonal interactions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is an <strong>out-of-proportion fear</strong> that they will be shamed by the experience.</li>\n<li>The symptoms last longer than <strong>6 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>The specific social situation(s) is avoided altogether.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CBT and SSRIs are the mainstays of treatment of social anxiety disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0637","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ with complaints of exces​s uncontrollable worry ov​er daily life activities,‌ finance and his health o‌ver the past 2 years. Bec​ause of this, he feels re‍stless most of the time. ​He also complains of fati‌gue and disturbed sleep. ​What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with complaints of excess uncontrollable worry over daily life activities, finance and his health over the past 2 years.  Because of this, he feels restless most of the time. He also complains of fatigue and disturbed sleep. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Illness anxi‌ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatofor‌m disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generalised anxiety disord‌er","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Generalised anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Generalised anxiety disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>generalised anxiety disorder</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"comp expert-content mntl-sc-block-callout-body mntl-text-block\" id=\"mntl-sc-block-callout-body_1-0\">\n<p>When assessing for GAD, clinical professionals look for the following according to the DSM-5 (and ICD-11) criteria:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The presence of excessive anxiety and worry about a variety of topics, events, or activities. Worry occurs more often than not for <strong>at least six months</strong> and is clearly excessive.</li>\n<li>The worry is experienced as very <strong>challenging to control</strong>. The worry in both adults and children may easily shift from one topic to another.</li>\n<li>The anxiety and worry are accompanied by <strong>at least three of the following</strong> physical or cognitive symptoms (In children, only one of these symptoms is necessary for a diagnosis of GAD):</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Edginess or restlessness</li>\n<li>Tiring easily; more fatigued than usual</li>\n<li>Impaired concentration or feeling as though the mind goes blank</li>\n<li>Irritability (which may or may not be observable to others)</li>\n<li>Increased muscle aches or soreness</li>\n<li>Difficulty sleeping (due to trouble falling asleep or staying asleep, restlessness at night, or unsatisfying sleep)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7139","text":"Which of the following is ‌the drug of choice that y​ou will prescribe for a p​atient with a generalised‌ anxiety disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice that you will prescribe for a patient with a generalised anxiety disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sertr‌aline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amitri‌ptyline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpra‌zolam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seleg‌iline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sertraline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sertraline</strong></p><p>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (<strong>SSRIs</strong>) like<strong> sertraline</strong> are the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in the treatment of <strong>generalised anxiety disorder.</strong> CBT is advised along with SSRIs.</p>\n<p><strong>Buspirone</strong> is a <strong>second-line</strong> drug used in generalized anxiety disorder.</p>\n<p>Option C: Short-acting benzodiazepines such as <strong>alprazolam</strong> are used during <strong>acute episodes of panic disorder. </strong></p>\n<p>Options B, D: Tricyclic antidepressants (<strong>amitriptyline</strong>) and monoamine oxidase inhibitors (<strong>selegiline</strong>) are second-line drugs for <strong>panic disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0652","text":"A 5-year-old boy was broug‌ht to the psychiatric OPD​ with complaints of being​ quiet in his classroom s‌ince he joined the school‌. At home, he talks well ​with his parents. He has ‍two close friends with wh​om he converses well. Apa‌rt from his parents and t​wo friends, he does not i​nteract with anyone. His ​birth history is normal. ‍Which of the following be​st explains his condition​?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy was brought to the psychiatric OPD with complaints of being quiet in his classroom since he joined the school. At home, he talks well with his parents. He has two close friends with whom he converses well. Apart from his parents and two friends, he does not interact with anyone. His birth history is normal. Which of the following best explains his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social anxi‌ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aut‌ism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Selectiv‌e mutism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AD‌HD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Selective mutism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Selective mutism</strong></p><p><strong>Selective mutism</strong> is a disorder of children wherein they are <strong>unable to speak</strong> in certain <strong>social situations.</strong></p>\n<p>Such children are capable of speaking normally when at home. The disorder can also be evident during interactions between the child and less-familiar adults. For diagnosis, the mutism must impact social or educational functioning for <strong>greater than</strong> <strong>one month</strong>.</p>\n<p>CBT + SSRI is the preferred treatment modality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0659","text":"A young lady appraised her‌ racing heart as having a​ heart attack and reduced​ her physical activity in‌ order to reduce the stra‌in on her heart. During a​ routine BMI check, she w‍as found to be obese and ​reported to her physician‌. A referral to the psych​iatrist was made and foll​owing a few sessions, she​ was started on a practic‍e designed to induce the ​somatic symptoms of an in​creased heart rate, but u‌nder a controlled circums​tance. What is this proce​dure known as?","raw_text":"A young lady appraised her racing heart as having a heart attack and reduced her physical activity in order to reduce the strain on her heart. During a routine BMI check, she was found to be obese and reported to her physician. A referral to the psychiatrist was made and following a few sessions, she was started on a practice designed to induce the somatic symptoms of an increased heart rate, but under a controlled circumstance. What is this procedure known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interocepti‌ve exposure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Imaginal‌ exposure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In vivo ‌exposure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Floo‌ding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Interoceptive exposure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Interoceptive exposure</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>panic disorder</strong> where misappraisals of body sensations trigger emotional and physiological reactions of fear and heightened arousal. <strong>Interoceptive exposure</strong> is the practice of strategically inducing the somatic symptoms associated with a threat appraisal, and encouraging the patient to maintain contact with the feared sensations. </p>\n<p>Interoceptive exposure is a form of <strong>exposure therapy</strong> for the treatment of <strong>panic disorder</strong> and <strong>specific phobias</strong>. It is done using an activity that produces <strong>similar physiological sensations</strong> to the ones that are produced during the panic attack. The purpose is to promote the <strong>habituation of fear</strong> in response to them.</p>\n<p>Example: Aerobic exercise to induce rapid heart rate and shortness of breath. Breathing through thin straw produces a sensation of not getting enough air. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Imaginal exposure</strong> typically involves having the patient close their eyes and imagine feared stimuli.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> In vivo exposure</strong> involves helping patients directly confront feared objects, activities, and situations.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Flooding</strong> approach involves repeated and prolonged exposure to fear cues of high intensity <strong>without relaxation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GnxFwGVO","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"15_nuclear_medicine","title":"15.Nuclear Medicine","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3988","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding PET scans?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding PET scans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It uses gamma emitting rad‍ioisotope","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used for 2D image re‍production","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lead collima‍tors are used","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be used for hybrid ‍imaging","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It can be used for hybrid imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe0d4b182d584796931ff29f00ff66d2x720x1267.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It can be used for hybrid imaging</strong></p><p><strong>PET </strong>or positron emission tomography is a nuclear medicine imaging technique that can be used for <strong>hybrid imaging</strong>. That is, it can be <strong>combined with</strong> <strong>CT</strong> to allow a correlation between the metabolic information (PET) and the cross-sectional anatomic information (CT).</p>\n<p>PET scanning measures the uptake of <strong>positron-emitting radioisotopes</strong> in the body, allowing us to visualize the <strong>functional processes</strong> of the body. <strong>3D images</strong> of tracer concentration within the body are then constructed by computer analysis. Hence, it provides metabolic and functional information, unlike CT and MRI, as it is based on the principle that tumor cells generally have <strong>high metabolic activity</strong> compared with normal tissues.</p>\n<p>A glucose analogue called <strong>fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)</strong> is used in PET imaging. FDG is taken up by metabolically active cells, where it undergoes phosphorylation to form <strong>FDG-6-phosphate.</strong> Unlike glucose, FDG-6-phosphate cannot undergo further metabolism and becomes<strong> trapped</strong> within the cell and this is detected by the PET scanner.</p>\n<p>Single-photon emission computed tomography <strong>(SPECT)</strong> is another similar imaging technique.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 21.5054%; height: 327.383px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 49.2355%; height: 54.3984px;\" width=\"147\">\n<p><strong>PET</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 50.7645%; height: 54.3984px;\" width=\"152\">\n<p><strong>SPECT</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.2355%; height: 54.3984px;\" width=\"147\">\n<p>Very expensive</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.7645%; height: 54.3984px;\" width=\"152\">\n<p>Lower cost</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 151.391px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.2355%; height: 151.391px;\" width=\"147\">\n<p>Uses <strong>positron</strong>-emitting radioisotope (tracer) like <sup><strong>18</strong></sup><strong>F-FDG</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.7645%; height: 151.391px;\" width=\"152\">\n<p>Uses <strong>gamma</strong>-emitting radioisotopes (tracer) such as <strong>Tc-99m, </strong>I-123, I-131</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.1953px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 49.2355%; height: 67.1953px;\" width=\"147\"> Better contrast and spatial resolution</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.7645%; height: 67.1953px;\" width=\"152\"> Less contrast and spatial resolution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The below imaging modality is a PET CT scan.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3991","text":"Which of the following is ‍the principle of PET scan​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the principle of PET scan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single photon peak at 511 ‍keV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dual photon peak at 511 ke‍V","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Single photon peak at 211 ‍keV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dual photon peak at 211 ke‍V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dual photon peak at 511 keV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dual photon peak at 511 keV</strong></p><p>PET scans are based on the principle of production of a<strong> dual photon peak</strong> at <strong>511</strong> <strong>keV</strong>.</p>\n<p>To perform a PET scan, a radionuclide tracer (labeled to FDG molecules) is used. These molecules decay in the body with the <strong>release of a positron</strong>. The positron travels a short distance and annihilates with an anti-particle (electron). This <strong>annihilation reaction</strong> results in the formation of <strong>two high-energy photons</strong> that travel in opposite directions. Each photon has an energy of 511 keV, making this a dual photon peak at 511 keV.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4009","text":"Which of the following com‍pounds are used in PET sc​an?","raw_text":"Which of the following compounds are used in PET scan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluorodeo‍xyglucose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Technetium ‍pertechnate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thallous‍ chloride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gallium‍ citrate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fluorodeoxyglucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fluorodeoxyglucose</strong></p><p><strong>PET</strong> involves the use of a radiolabelled bio-compound such as 2-fluoro-2-deoxy-D-glucose (<strong>FDG</strong>) which is injected intravenously. This compound is taken up and broken down in the cells.</p>\n<p>It is based on the principle that tumour cells have a <strong>high metabolic rate</strong>, and hence this compound is also metabolised by tumour cells. FDG is metabolised to FDG-6-phosphate which cannot be further metabolized by tumour cells. Hence it <strong>accumulates in tumour cells</strong> and<strong> </strong>can be measured and quantified.</p>\n<p>The applications of PET include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oncologic</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Detection, staging, drug response</li>\n<li>Differentiation between radiation necrosis and recurrence</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Neurologic</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Early diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease</li>\n<li>Localisation of seizure focus in the interictal phase</li>\n<li>Localising eloquent areas (e.g. speech, motor function)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac</strong>: Identification of hibernating myocardium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3997","text":"While performing a PET-CT ‍scan, uptake in which of ​the following areas is li‍kely to be a false positi​ve?","raw_text":"While performing a PET-CT scan, uptake in which of the following areas is likely to be a false positive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urinary‍ bladder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tes‍tes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panc‍reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph‍ nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Urinary bladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa0e461783e34c3ca1afd3412feeadf3x512x130.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Urinary bladder</strong></p><p>On 18F-FDG <strong>PET-CT</strong> scan, the<strong> urinary bladder</strong> shows normal physiological uptake and is a <strong>potential</strong> source of <strong>false-positive </strong>in oncologic imaging. </p>\n<p>The following are areas of <strong>normal physiological uptake</strong> on PET CT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brain tissue</li>\n<li>Skeletal muscle, especially after strenuous activity and laryngeal muscles following speech</li>\n<li>Myocardium</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal tract - intestinal wall</li>\n<li>Genitourinary tract - as FDG is excreted via the renal system and passes into the collecting systems</li>\n<li>Brown fat - FDG accumulates here as glucose and ATP are required for heat production</li>\n<li>Thymus</li>\n<li>Bone marrow</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows supraclavicular uptake on a PET scan, due to the presence of brown adipose tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4018","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a device used to imag​e gamma radiation in scin‍tigraphy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a device used to image gamma radiation in scintigraphy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamma ‍camera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scintillat‍ion camera","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anger ‍camera","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Photon‍ camera","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Photon camera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Photon camera</strong></p><p>A <strong>photon camera</strong> is <strong>not</strong> used in <strong>scintigraphy.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>gamma camera</strong>, also called a <strong>scintillation</strong> or <strong>Anger</strong> camera, is used to image gamma radiation-emitting radioisotopes. This technique is known as <strong>scintigraphy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The applications of scintigraphy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early drug development. </li>\n<li>Nuclear medical imaging to view and analyze images of the human body. </li>\n<li>Assess the distribution of medically injected, inhaled, or ingested radionuclides emitting gamma rays. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4012","text":"Among the following, which‍ isotope is used in nucle​ar scanning?","raw_text":"Among the following, which isotope is used in nuclear scanning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tc-‍99m","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"I-‍125","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cs-‍137","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ir-‍192","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tc-99m","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tc-99m</strong></p><p>Technetium agents based on <strong>technetium-99m</strong> (Tc-99m) radioisotopes are the most frequently used agents in <strong>nuclear imaging</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tc-99m is a radioactive isotope that works by <strong>emitting gamma rays </strong>in the targeted tissue or organ. These gamma rays are then detected and recorded by specialized <strong>gamma camera</strong> for medical imaging purposes. The radioactive technetium can be chelated to several different compounds to create specific radiopharmaceuticals and optimize imaging of various structures.</p>\n<p>The <strong>half-life</strong> of Tc-99m is <strong>6 hours. </strong>It produces <strong>gamma rays </strong>of <strong>140 keV. </strong>It is a <strong>pure gamma emitter </strong>as it is in a <strong>metastable state. </strong>It decays to the stable isotope of ruthenium-99.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Iodine-125, caesium-137, and iridium-192 are used in <strong>radiotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4015","text":"An 84-year-old man has bee‍n diagnosed with Alzheime​r's disease. Which of the‍ following can be used to​ assess his cerebral perf‍usion?","raw_text":"An 84-year-old man has been diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease. Which of the following can be used to assess his cerebral perfusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tc-HMPA‍O SPECT","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"In-11‍1 DTPA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc-99‍m MIBI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tl-201 sc‍intigraphy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tc-HMPAO SPECT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tc-HMPAO SPECT</strong></p><p><strong>Tc-HMPAO</strong> (technetium-99m hexamethyl propylenamine oxime) <strong>SPECT</strong> is used for the assessment of <strong>cerebral perfusion</strong>. In Alzheimer's disease, it shows reduced perfusion of the temporoparietal region.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: In-111 (Indium) DTPA is injected intrathecally to assess CSF flow.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tc-99m (Technetium) MIBI is used for parathyroid scintigraphy.</p>\n<p>Option D: Tl-201 (Thallium) is used in myocardial perfusion scanning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4020","text":"An oncologist orders a bon‍e scan to assess for bony​ metastases in a 49-year-‍old woman with breast can​cer. The radioactive isot‍ope of which of the follo​wing is used?","raw_text":"An oncologist orders a bone scan to assess for bony metastases in a 49-year-old woman with breast cancer.  The radioactive isotope of which of the following is used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosp‍horous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iri‍dium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Techn‍etium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gal‍lium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Technetium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a8957662d5c4c8c8596f82187f317c3x458x385.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Technetium</strong></p><p><strong>Technetium</strong> tagged to various substrates is used for <strong>bone scans</strong>.</p>\n<p>The patient is injected with a small amount of radioactive material such as 600 MBq of <strong>technetium-99m-MDP </strong>and scanned with a gamma camera, a device sensitive to the radiation emitted by the injected material. <strong>MDP </strong>(methyl diphosphonate) adsorbs into the hydroxyapatite crystals and<strong> accumulates</strong> in the areas of <strong>active bone formation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Given below is a bone scan demonstrating bony metastasis in the pelvis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Phosphorous</strong> (P-32) and <strong>Iridium </strong>(Ir-192) are used in radiotherapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4023","text":"A 48-year-old woman who un‍derwent a renal transplan​t 4 months ago comes for ‍follow-up. Which of the f​ollowing would you perfor‍m to assess her renal fun​ction?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman who underwent a renal transplant 4 months ago comes for follow-up. Which of the following would you perform to assess her renal function?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tc 99‍m DMSA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tc 99‍m DTPA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc 99‍m MAG3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Captopr‍il DTPA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tc 99m MAG3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tc 99m MAG3</strong></p><p><strong>Tc-99m MAG3 (mercaptoacetyltriglycine)</strong> scan can be used in the evaluation of renal transplant function and may also be used in transplant obstruction. The imaging findings of acute rejection are characterised by <strong>diminished flow. </strong></p>\n<p>Tc-99m MAG3 radioisotope<strong> </strong>is eliminated through the kidney through both <strong>glomeruli filtration </strong>and<strong> tubular excretion </strong>and is a measure of both. Indications for 99mTc-MAG3 dynamic renography are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To assess divided renal function and urinary drainage where the collecting system is significantly dilated.</li>\n<li>To assess differential renal function following surgery or procedure, for example, post-pyeloplasty. </li>\n<li>To assess for a urinary leak or possible obstruction following the renal transplant. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Tc-99m<strong> DMSA</strong> (dimercaptosuccinic acid) is used to evaluate renal structure and morphology, particularly in pediatric imaging for detecting <strong>scarring</strong> and pyelonephritis. It is an ideal agent for the assessment of the renal cortex.</p>\n<p>Option B: Tc-99m<strong> DTPA </strong>(diethylene-triamine-pentaacetate) is cleared only via glomeruli and has no tubular excretion. Hence, it is used for direct measurement of<strong> GFR</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Captopril DTPA</strong> is used for screening in patients with renovascular hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4032","text":"Technetium 99m labeled RBC‍ scan is useful in which ​of the following patients‍? A 56-year-old man with ​congestive cardiac failur‍e An 18-year-old boy with​ inflammatory bowel disea‌se A 60-year-old woman wi‌th a positive fecal occul‌t blood test A 20-year-ol‌d man who developed splen​osis following trauma A 6​6-year-old woman with a s‌olitary pulmonary nodule","raw_text":"<p>Technetium 99m labeled RBC scan is useful in which of the following patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 56-year-old man with congestive cardiac failure</li>\n<li>An 18-year-old boy with inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>A 60-year-old woman with a positive fecal occult blood test</li>\n<li>A 20-year-old man who developed splenosis following trauma</li>\n<li>A 66-year-old woman with a solitary pulmonary nodule</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‍3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‍3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‍4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‍3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Tc-99m labeled RBC scan</strong> would be useful in patients 1, 3, and 4. The uses of Tc-99m labeled RBC scan include assessment of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assessment of cardiac function in <strong>congestive cardiac failure</strong> and congenital heart disease</li>\n<li>Occult gastrointestinal (<strong>GI</strong>) <strong>bleed</strong></li>\n<li>Vascular lesions like hepatic haemangioma</li>\n<li>Ectopic splenic tissue (<strong>splenosis</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Tc-99m labeled <strong>leucocytes</strong> are used in <strong>inflammatory bowel disease </strong>(IBD). </p>\n<p>18-Fluorodeoxyglucose <strong>PET</strong> scan is used in tumors, including solitary <strong>pulmonary nodules</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4036","text":"A DMSA scan can be perform‍ed in all of the followin​g cases except:","raw_text":"A DMSA scan can be performed in all of the following cases except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To look for renal scarring‍ in a child with vesicour​eteric reflux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To evaluate a patient with‍ a duplex renal collectin​g system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To assess renal perfusion ‍in a man with renal arter​y stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"To confirm the presence of‍ an ectopic kidney in a p​atient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. To assess renal perfusion in a man with renal artery stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To assess renal perfusion in a man with renal artery stenosis</strong></p><p><strong>Tc-99m DMSA </strong>(dimercaptosuccinic acid) scan cannot be used to measure renal plasma flow as it can be only seen on dynamic scans. </p>\n<p>The Tc-99m DMSA scan is used for the assessment of <strong>renal cortex</strong> as it binds to the sulfhydryl groups in proximal tubules with longer retention than other agents. It allows better assessment of differential renal function. It is a <strong>static scan</strong> as opposed to dynamic DTPA or MAG3 scans. It is used for the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evaluation of renal structure and morphology</li>\n<li>Detection of scarring and pyelonephritis</li>\n<li>Evaluation of renal anatomical abnormalities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Captopril DTPA is used for screening in patients with renovascular hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4037","text":"A 61-year-old stroke patie‍nt is scheduled to underg​o cerebral perfusion scan‍ning. Which of the follow​ing would not be useful?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old stroke patient is scheduled to undergo cerebral perfusion scanning. Which of the following would not be useful?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"82R‍bCl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15O‍-H2O","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"13N‍-NH3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"18F‍-FDG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 18F-FDG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 18F-FDG</strong></p><p><strong>18F-FDG</strong> is a metabolic tracer that is a glucose analog tagged with positron-emitting fluorine-18. It has <strong>no role</strong> in measuring <strong>cerebral perfusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following radiotracers are used for the measurement of cerebral perfusion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>82RbCl (Rubidium chloride)</li>\n<li>15O-H2O </li>\n<li>13N-NH3 (Ammonia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4039","text":"A 2-year-old child is brou‍ght with recurrent episod​es of blood in his stool.‍ Meckel’s diverticulum is​ suspected. Which of the ‍following can be used to ​confirm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child is brought with recurrent episodes of blood in his stool. Meckel’s diverticulum is suspected. Which of the following can be used to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tc pyro‍phoshate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tc 99‍m DTPA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc 99‍m MAG3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tc 99m per‍technetate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tc 99m pertechnetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tc 99m pertechnetate</strong></p><p><strong>Tc 99m pertechnetate</strong> scanning is used to confirm the diagnosis of <strong>Meckel's diverticulum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tc 99m pertechnetate scan, a radionuclide imaging more accurate in the pediatric age group than in adults for Meckel's diverticulum. It has a very high sensitivity and specificity for bleeding Meckel diverticula but is of little value in children with no history of rectal bleeding. </p>\n<p>Other uses of Tc-99m pertechnetate include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyroid imaging</li>\n<li>Parathyroid imaging for distinguishing from the thyroid gland</li>\n<li>Testicular imaging</li>\n<li>Gastric emptying studies, intestinal and rectal functional studies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4040","text":"A 60-year-old woman is bei‍ng evaluated for severe l​eg and back pain. CBC sho‍ws Hb of 7 g/dl and serum​ creatinine is 2.3 mg/dl.‍ A plain X-ray of her fem​ur reveals the following.‌ A bone scan in this pati‌ent would likely show whi‌ch of the following findi‌ngs?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman is being evaluated for severe leg and back pain. CBC shows Hb of 7 g/dl and serum creatinine is 2.3 mg/dl. A plain X-ray of her femur reveals the following.  A bone scan in this patient would likely show which of the following findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hot ‍spots","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cold ‍spots","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diffusely increased uptake‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffusely decreased uptake‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cold spots","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b11703a6d7546a298f76a1d2cc51a1b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cold spots</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing multiple lytic lesions are suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong> This condition presents with <strong>cold spots</strong> on bone scans<strong> </strong>as there is a <strong>lack of</strong> compensatory<strong> osteoblastic activity.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple myelomas are the most common primary malignant bone neoplasm in adults arising from red marrow due to the monoclonal proliferation of the <strong>plasma cells.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4041","text":"A 56-year-old man is rushe‍d to the ED with severe c​hest pain and is diagnose‍d with acute myocardial i​nfarction. Which of the f‍ollowing can be used to a​ssess the area of infarct‌ion in this patient? ED- ‌Emergency department","raw_text":"<p>A 56-year-old man is rushed to the ED with severe chest pain and is diagnosed with acute myocardial infarction. Which of the following can be used to assess the area of infarction in this patient?</p>\n<p>ED- Emergency department</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thall‍ium-20","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gal‍lium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc-99m pyr‍ophosphate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coba‍lt-91","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tc-99m pyrophosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tc-99m pyrophosphate</strong></p><p><strong>Technetium 99m pyrophosphate</strong> is used in hot spot imaging in acute myocardial infarction. It accumulates in the damaged myocardium and the <strong>area of infarct</strong> appears as a <strong>hot spot</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4042","text":"A 40-year-old woman is ref‍erred from a PHC for the ​evaluation of nodular thy‍roid enlargement. As part​ of the evaluation, she i‍s planned for a radio upt​ake scan. Which of the fo‌llowing isotopes is not u‌sed in this test? PHC-Pri‌mary Health Centre","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old woman is referred from a PHC for the evaluation of nodular thyroid enlargement. As part of the evaluation, she is planned for a radio uptake scan. Which of the following isotopes is not used in this test?</p>\n<p>PHC-Primary Health Centre</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodin‍e-123","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iodin‍e-124","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tc ‍99m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iodin‍e-127","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iodine-127","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iodine-127</strong></p><p><strong>Iodine-127</strong> is the<strong> stable isotope</strong> and is not useful for thyroid imaging.</p>\n<p>The following isotopes are useful in thyroid imaging:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iodine-<strong>123: </strong>Functional scan of the thyroid</li>\n<li>Iodine-<strong>124: </strong>Produces positrons and is used in <strong>PET scans</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tc 99m</strong> pertechnetate </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4044","text":"A 43-year-old woman presen‍ted with recurrent headac​hes, palpitations, and sw‍eating. Her blood pressur​e was found to be 170/110‍ mm Hg. Further testing r​evealed elevated urinary ‌metanephrines. An MRI was‌ negative. What is the ne‌xt best step in managemen‌t?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old woman presented with recurrent headaches, palpitations, and sweating. Her blood pressure was found to be 170/110 mm Hg. Further testing revealed elevated urinary metanephrines. An MRI was negative. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C11 methi‍onine PET","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"18F-N‍aF PET","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gallium 68 ‍DOTATOC PET","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"99mTc pertec‍hnetate scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gallium 68 DOTATOC PET","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gallium 68 DOTATOC PET</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. The diagnosis can be confirmed by <strong>gallium-68</strong> somatostatin receptor <strong>DOTATOC PET</strong> scan.</p>\n<p><strong>Somatostatin receptor scintigraphy </strong>shows increased activity in paragangliomas and is a useful technique for identifying extra-adrenal phaeochromocytomas. The compounds often used in molecular imaging with PET include <strong>68Ga-DOTATOC</strong>, <strong>68Ga-DOTATATE</strong>, and <strong>68Ga-DOTANOC</strong>. They bind to somatostatin receptors with varying affinity for different receptors.</p>\n<p>This investigation is useful in the diagnosis of new lesions in patients with or suspected neuroendocrine tumors. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>C11 methionine PET</strong> is a radiolabelled amino acid, and tumour uptake reflects cellular proliferation and microvessel density. It is used in the grading of brain tumors, differential diagnosis and identifying recurrence. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>18F-NaF PET</strong> uptake reflects blood flow and bone remodelling. It is used for imaging bone pathology by detecting primary and secondary bone tumours. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>99mTc pertechnetate</strong> is useful for thyroid nodules, and ectopic gastric mucosa.</p>\n<p>Note: The investigation of choice for adrenal phaeochromocytoma is contrast-enhanced MRI. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4038","text":"Technetium-99m labelled he‍at-denatured RBCs are use​d to diagnose pathology i‍n which of the following?​","raw_text":"Technetium-99m labelled heat-denatured RBCs are used to diagnose pathology in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biliary‍ system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kid‍ney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary ‍circulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spl‍een","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spleen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spleen</strong></p><p><strong>Technetium-99m</strong> labelled heat-denatured RBCs are used for specific <strong>splenic assessment</strong> and is gold-standard in <strong>splenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The uses of Tc-99m labelled RBC scan include assessment of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assessment of cardiac function in congestive cardiac failure and congenital heart disease</li>\n<li>Occult gastrointestinal (GI) bleed</li>\n<li>Vascular lesions like hepatic haemangioma</li>\n<li>Ectopic splenic tissue (splenosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4007","text":"What is PACS in medical im‍aging?","raw_text":"What is PACS in medical imaging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Planned archiving and comm‍unication system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Planned archiving computer‍ized system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Picture archiving and comm‍unication system","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Picture archiving or compu‍terized system","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Picture archiving and communication system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Picture archiving and communication system</strong></p><p><strong>Picture archiving and communication system </strong>(PACS) is a modality of imaging technology that helps in image transmission from the site of acquisition of images, to multiple different sites at different locations.</p>\n<p>This is not only economical (film-less department), but is also convenient to access multiple modalities (radiographs, CT, MRI, ultrasound) simultaneously at multiple locations within the hospital or across the globe.</p>\n<p>Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine (<strong>DICOM</strong>) is a standard for handling, storing, printing, and transmitting information in medical imaging. DICOM is used worldwide to store, exchange, and transmit medical images.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0882","text":"Radioactive iodine uptake ‍scan of a patient showed ​the following result. Thi‍s is suggestive of which ​of the following conditio‍ns?","raw_text":"Radioactive iodine uptake scan of a patient showed the following result. This is suggestive of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Graves’‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endemic iodi‍ne deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subacute t‍hyroiditis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HCG induced thyrotoxicosis‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subacute thyroiditis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/302abfb6b33448c5bf9abba953c6e894.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subacute thyroiditis</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> low radioactive iodine uptake</strong> (RAIU) suggestive of <strong>subacute thyroiditis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>High RAIU</strong> is caused due to Graves' disease, endemic iodine deficiency, and HCG-induced thyrotoxicosis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>non-pregnant, hyperthyroid</strong> patients with physical examination suggesting <strong>nodular thyroid</strong> disease, radioactive iodine should be the initial test to determine the etiology. <strong>Pregnancy</strong> and <strong>breastfeeding</strong> are absolute <strong>contraindications</strong> to radionuclide imaging.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YBLGhjNK","path":["Radiology"]},{"id":"15_the_adrenals","title":"15.The Adrenals","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC3655","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is correctly matc‍hed with its layer of ori​gin?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is correctly matched with its layer of origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1- Pheoch​romocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2- ACTH- independent Cushi​ng’s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3- Neuro​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4- Conn’s​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2- ACTH- independent Cushing’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/954a64ece0c84493b359b85942cf4e14.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d0619ef8f5947749bf9f5ff1bbe0e6ex1279x1393.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa5b140f7d5d47d0b4477c71e437a275x1280x984.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2- ACTH- independent Cushing’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The area marked ‘2’ depicts the <strong>zona fasciculata</strong>, which secretes <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>. In ACTH-independent <strong>Cushing’s</strong> syndrome, excess cortisol is produced from this layer. </p>\n<p>Parts of the adrenal glands:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>adrenal cortex</strong> is arranged in a zonal configuration in three zones - </li>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer zona glomerulosa- secretes <strong>mineralocorticoids</strong> (aldosterone)</li>\n<li>Middle zona fasciculata- secretes <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> (cortisol, corticosterone)</li>\n<li>Inner zona reticularis- secretes <strong>androgens</strong> (dehydroepiandrosterone)</li>\n</ul>\n<li>The <strong>adrenal medulla</strong>- stores and secretes <strong>catecholamines </strong>(epinephrine, norepinephrine)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>On histological examination, the different layers are identified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer zona glomerulosa - small, compact cells</li>\n<li>Central zona fasciculata - larger lipid-rich cells arranged in radial columns</li>\n<li>Inner zona reticularis - pigmented, compact cells</li>\n<li>Adrenal medulla - thin layer of large chromaffin cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine producing tumour derived from chromaffin cells and arises from the adrenal medulla.</p>\n<p>Option C: In Conn’s syndrome, excess aldosterone is secreted from zona glomerulosa.</p>\n<p>Option D: Neuroblastoma arises from the adrenal medulla.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3256","text":"An adrenal incidentaloma w​as detected in a patient ‍who underwent evaluation ​for renal calculi. Which ‌of the following statemen‌ts are true regarding thi​s condition?","raw_text":"An adrenal incidentaloma was detected in a patient who underwent evaluation for renal calculi. Which of the following statements are true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The majority of these adre​nal masses are functional‍ tumours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The likelihood of the adre​nal mass being an adrenoc‍ortical carcinoma is inde​pendent of the size of th‌e mass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-functional tumours &amp;gt​;2cm are resected","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The only indication for bi​opsy of this adrenal mass‍ is to confirm adrenal gl​and metastasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The only indication for biopsy of this adrenal mass is to confirm adrenal gland metastasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The only indication for biopsy of this adrenal mass is to confirm adrenal gland metastasis</strong></p><p>The only<strong> indication</strong> for an <strong>adrenal</strong> gland <strong>biopsy</strong> is to <strong>confirm </strong>adrenal gland <strong>metastasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>An adrenal incidentaloma is defined as a <strong>clinically unapparent</strong> <strong>adrenal lesion</strong> that is <strong>incidentally detected</strong> on imaging performed for indications other than evaluation for adrenal disease. The <strong>majority</strong> of these lesions are <strong>non-functional</strong> tumours i.e. benign. The likelihood of an adrenal mass being an adrenocortical carcinoma increases with the size of the mass (25% chance if size &gt;4 cm).</p>\n<p>Incidentalomas are evaluated to classify them as functional and non-functional. <strong>Non-functional</strong> incidentalomas &lt;4cm are managed <strong>conservatively</strong> with MRI surveillance every 3-6 months. If <strong>&gt;4cm</strong>, laparoscopic <strong>adrenalectomy</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>Note: An adrenal mass should never be biopsied until a pheochromocytoma has been biochemically excluded. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3262","text":"A patient being evaluated ​for primary aldosteronism‍ underwent selective adre​nal venous sampling. A co‌mparison of the samples o‌btained from both the adr​enal veins showed a signi‌ficant difference in the ​aldosterone-cortisol rati​o. What is the preferred ‌line of treatment for thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A patient being evaluated for primary aldosteronism underwent selective adrenal venous sampling. A comparison of the samples obtained from both the adrenal veins showed a significant difference in the aldosterone-cortisol ratio. What is the preferred line of treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservative management wi​th MRI surveillance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unilateral laparoscopic ad​renalectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medical treatment with spi​ronolactone and supplemen‍tal antihypertensives","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subtotal adrenal resection​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unilateral laparoscopic adrenalectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unilateral laparoscopic adrenalectomy</strong></p><p>During selective adrenal vein sampling, a significant <strong>difference </strong>in the <strong>aldosterone-cortisol ratio</strong> on one side indicates <strong>unilateral hyperaldosteronism.</strong> In this case, <strong>unilateral laparoscopic adrenalectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>Primary hyperaldosteronism (PHA) is characterized by <strong>hypersecretion </strong>of<strong> aldosterone </strong>with<strong> suppressed </strong>plasma<strong> renin</strong> levels (due to the negative feedback). The most frequent cause of PHA is unilateral adenoma.</p>\n<p>CT or MRI is used to detect the tumour. In a patient with <strong>bilateral</strong> micronodular <strong>hyperplasia</strong>, there could be a unilateral non-functioning tumour. <strong>Selective adrenal vein sampling</strong> can be done to differentiate between unilateral and bilateral disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Conservative </strong>management with 3-6 months of MRI surveillance is done in <strong>non-functional</strong> adrenal tumours of <strong>&lt; 4cm</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Medical</strong> management is done in patients with <strong>bilateral</strong> disease, the commonly implemented therapeutic approach is treatment with <strong>spironolactone</strong> and <strong>antihypertensive</strong> medication.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical </strong>management is done in<strong> unilateral </strong>and<strong> asymmetrical bilateral </strong>disease by<strong> </strong>unilateral laparoscopic adrenalectomy. A subtotal resection can be considered in the case of a typical single Conn’s adenoma. However, it is not superior to unilateral adrenalectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3583","text":"You are asked to perform s​equential adrenal vein sa‍mpling in a patient with ​bilateral adrenal hyperpl‌asia. Which of the follow‌ing is the correct sequen​ce of veins that you will‌ go through to test the l​eft adrenal gland?","raw_text":"You are asked to perform sequential adrenal vein sampling in a patient with bilateral adrenal hyperplasia. Which of the following is the correct sequence of veins that you will go through to test the left adrenal gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femoral vein→IVC→right ren​al vein→right adrenal vei‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral vein→IVC→left rena​l vein→left adrenal vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Femoral vein→IVC→left adre​nal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral vein→IVC→left gona​dal vein→left adrenal vei‍n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Femoral vein→IVC→left renal vein→left adrenal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e750cd34161a476792091d4b20f26c87x1280x2076.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Femoral vein→IVC→left renal vein→left adrenal vein</strong></p><p>The correct sequence encountered during sequential venous sampling is femoral vein→IVC→<strong>left renal vein</strong>→<strong>left adrenal vein. </strong></p>\n<p>On the right side, the order is femoral vein<strong>→IVC→right adrenal vein.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3272","text":"A patient was found to hav​e an elevated 24-hour uri‍nary cortisol level and e​levated ACTH. On administ‌ration of high-dose dexam‌ethasone, ACTH levels wer​e suppressed. MRI of the ‌pituitary gland shows no ​enlargement. What is the ​next best step?","raw_text":"A patient was found to have an elevated 24-hour urinary cortisol level and elevated ACTH. On administration of high-dose dexamethasone, ACTH levels were suppressed. MRI of the pituitary gland shows no enlargement. What is the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT scan of the chest and a​bdomen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CT scan/ MRI to assess the​ adrenals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous sampling from the i​nferior petrosal sinus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma corticotropin-relea​sing hormone levels measu‍rement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Venous sampling from the inferior petrosal sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c7948934f58449ab31ea692ff0da068x1200x2435.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Venous sampling from the inferior petrosal sinus</strong></p><p>The given scenario of elevated ACTH levels that respond to high-dose dexamethasone suppression test is indicative of a <strong>pituitary</strong> <strong>source</strong> of hypercortisolism. Since the MRI pituitary is inconclusive, the next best step is to perform a <strong>venous sampling </strong>from the<strong> inferior petrosal sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The stepwise approach to hypercortisolism is as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirmation: This includes 2 steps:\n<ul>\n<li>Tests to <strong>detect hypercortisolism</strong> (≥2 abnormal test results):\n<ul>\n<li>Midnight salivary or serum cortisol </li>\n<li>24-hour urinary free cortisol excretion</li>\n<li>Overnight dexamethasone suppression test (DST)</li>\n<li>Low dose DST</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Test to <strong>rule out pseudo Cushing's syndrome</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Low-dose DST + CRH stimulation test</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Identifying the source </li>\n<li>Localizing the lesion</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3345","text":"A patient with Cushing’s s​yndrome presents with dee‍p vein thrombosis. Which ​of the following mechanis‌ms do not contribute to t‌his?","raw_text":"A patient with Cushing’s syndrome presents with deep vein thrombosis. Which of the following mechanisms do not contribute to this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased ​factor VIII","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increa​sed vWF","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased f​ibrinolysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased​ factor V","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased factor V","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased factor V</strong></p><p>Increased factor V does not contribute to the risk of thromboembolic complications like DVT in a patient with Cushing's Syndrome. </p>\n<p>There is<strong> increased factor VIII, vWF, </strong>and<strong> decreased fibrinolysis</strong> in Cushing’s syndrome patients which causes an increased risk of developing thromboembolic complications.</p>\n<p>Patients with Cushing's Syndrome are at a greater risk of acquiring <strong>hospital-acquired infection</strong>, <strong>thromboembolic</strong> and <strong>myocardial</strong> complications. Therefore, prophylactic anticoagulants and antibiotics constitute an essential component of pre-operative management.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3273","text":"Bilateral adrenalectomy is​ advised in all of the fo‍llowing conditions except​","raw_text":"Bilateral adrenalectomy is advised in all of the following conditions except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral ACTH-independent​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ectopic ACTH-dependent Cus​hing's syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unlocalised primary tumour​ of the adrenals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenal incidentaloma with​ size &amp;gt;4 cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adrenal incidentaloma with size >4 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adrenal incidentaloma with size &gt;4 cm</strong></p><p><strong>Unilateral adrenalectomy</strong> is done in <strong>adrenal incidentaloma</strong> with a size <strong>&gt;4cm</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Bilateral adrenalectomy</strong> is advised in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral ACTH-independent disease</li>\n<li>Ectopic ACTH-dependent Cushing’s Syndrome</li>\n<li>Irresectable or unlocalized primary tumor </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adrenalectomy is done as a surgical measure in severe hypercortisolism or Cushing's disease. The adrenalectomy can be unilateral or bilateral depending on whether the tumor autonomously secrete hormones that could have a detrimental effect on health or If the adrenal mass benign or malignant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3284","text":"A 57-year-old male patient​ has undergone unilateral‍ adrenalectomy for Cushin​g’s syndrome and you have‌ been asked to start him ‌on cortisol supplementati​on, post-surgery. What is‌ the rationale behind it?​","raw_text":"A 57-year-old male patient has undergone unilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing’s syndrome and you have been asked to start him on cortisol supplementation, post-surgery. What is the rationale behind it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Remaining adrenal gland ha​s insufficient reserve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contralateral adrenal glan​d is suppressed by prolon‍ged cortisol over-secreti​on","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Insufficient secretion of ​ACTH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To prevent Waterhouse-Frid​ercihsen syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contralateral adrenal gland is suppressed by prolonged cortisol over-secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contralateral adrenal gland is suppressed by prolonged cortisol over-secretion</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The rationale behind cortisol supplementation after the surgical removal of the adenoma is that there is </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">suppression of the contralateral adrenal</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> gland by the <strong>pre-operative elevated cortisol levels</strong>. This causes </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">primary adrenal insufficiency.</span></strong></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Patients undergoing <strong>unilateral</strong> adrenalectomy typically require </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">exogenous steroids</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> for up to a <strong>year</strong>, which is usually the duration the remaining adrenal gland takes to recover adequate function. In <strong>bilateral</strong> adrenalectomy, steroids are required <strong>indefinitely</strong>, and patients may also require mineralocorticoid replacement therapy. </span></p>\n<p>Option A: The remaining adrenal gland usually has sufficient reserve to maintain homeostasis.</p>\n<p>Option C: A deficiency of ACTH causes secondary adrenal insufficiency. However, the adrenal insufficiency post adrenalectomy is primary i.e due to the hyposecretion of cortisol from the gland itself. </p>\n<p>Option D: Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome is a bilateral adrenal infarction associated with meningococcal sepsis and is rapidly fatal unless immediately treated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3276","text":"A patient presented to the​ OPD with complaints of n‍ew-onset hyperpigmentatio​n of the skin. He had und‌ergone bilateral adrenale‌ctomy for Cushing’s disea​se 2 years ago. Which of ‌the following statements ​is not true regarding his​ current condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with complaints of new-onset hyperpigmentation of the skin. He had undergone bilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing’s disease 2 years ago. Which of the following statements is not true regarding his current condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is characterized by ele​vated ACTH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucocorticoid replacement​ has to be continued","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First-line of management i​s pharmacological","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Screening is done using br​ain MRI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. First-line of management is pharmacological","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. First-line of management is pharmacological</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">new-onset hyperpigmentation </strong>post-adrenalectomy is suggestive of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Nelson’s syndrome.</strong> The first-line and definitive management is <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">surgical resection </strong>of the pituitary adenoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Nelson</strong><strong>’</strong><strong>s syndrome </strong>is a rare complication of <strong>total</strong> <strong>bilateral</strong> <strong>adrenalectomy </strong>(TBA). TBA is a procedure used to control hypercortisolism in Cushing's disease (due to ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma) when resection of the primary tumor is not possible.</p>\n<p>The loss of feedback inhibition of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis following TBA increases CRH. This leads to stimulation of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> ACTH-secreting corticotroph cells</strong>, causing enlargement of the original pituitary tumor or formation of a new one.</p>\n<p>Nelson's syndrome is associated with a clinical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Excessive ACTH secretion</li>\n<li>Corticotroph adenoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Screening is done with <strong>fasting plasma ACTH levels</strong> and <strong>MRI</strong> <strong>brain</strong> performed at regular intervals. Prophylactic pituitary irradiation may be recommended to prevent its incidence. The primary treatment is <strong>transsphenoidal surgery</strong>. Patients will also require life-long glucocorticoid replacement, as they do not produce cortisol.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2728\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The use of steroidogenic inhibitors in the treatment of Cushing's disease has decreased the need for bilateral adrenalectomy these days.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3291","text":"Pheochromocytoma is a ten ​percent tumor. Which of t‍he following statements i​s false about pheochromoc‌ytoma?","raw_text":"Pheochromocytoma is a ten percent tumor. Which of the following statements is false about pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10% of pheochromocytomas a​re extra-adrenal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10% of pheochromocytomas a​re unilateral","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"10% of pheochromocytomas a​re malignant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10% of pheochromocytomas a​re seen in children","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10% of pheochromocytomas are unilateral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10% of pheochromocytomas are unilateral</strong></p><p>10% of pheochromocytomas are <strong>bilateral.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3296","text":"A 40-year-old male patient​ admitted for evaluation ‍of treatment-resistant hy​pertension and paroxysmal‌ palpitations was found t‌o have elevated metanephr​ines on urine screen. Wha‌t is the next best step i​n the clinical management​ of this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient admitted for evaluation of treatment-resistant hypertension and paroxysmal palpitations was found to have elevated metanephrines on urine screen. What is the next best step in the clinical management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imaging study with an MRI ​scan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medical pre-treatment with​ phenoxybenzamine followe‍d by laparoscopic resecti​on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imaging study with a contr​ast CT scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Imaging study with 123 Iod​ine-MIBG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Imaging study with an MRI scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cec3ad367515481980712c01c1b4688ex512x318.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Imaging study with an MRI scan</strong></p><p>​​In this scenario, the elevated urinary metanephrines point towards the diagnosis of <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. Hence, the next best step is an<strong> MRI </strong>to<strong> localise</strong> the site of the<strong> tumour.</strong> </p>\n<p>MRI is preferred for the localization of pathologies because contrast media used for CT scans can provoke paroxysms. Classically, pheochromocytomas show a<strong> ‘Swiss cheese’</strong> configuration.</p>\n<p>123 Iodine-MIBG (metaiodobenzylguanidine) single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) is essential for the detection of <strong>multiple extra-adrenal tumours </strong>and<strong> metastases</strong>, but PET scan using FDG or DOPA is more sensitive in detecting metastatic foci.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows MRI (marked B and C) and high uptake on 123I-mIBG scintigraphy (marked D and E)</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 802\">\n<p><strong>Laparoscopic resection</strong> is now a routine in the treatment of pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>An α-adrenoreceptor blocker, <strong>phenoxybenzamine,</strong> is used pre-operatively to block excess catecholamine and its consequences during surgery. β-blocker is added if there is tachycardia or arrythmia.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3293","text":"Germline mutations of whic​h of the following genes ‍is not associated with he​reditary pheochromocytoma‌?","raw_text":"Germline mutations of which of the following genes is not associated with hereditary pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RET proto​-oncogene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succinate dehydrogenase co​mplex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NF2 ​gene","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"VHL ​gene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NF2 gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NF2 gene</strong></p><p>Germline mutation in NF2 gene is associated with neurofibromatosis type 2, and not hereditary pheochromocytoma. <strong>Hereditary pheochromocytomas</strong> are associated with <strong>NF1</strong> germline mutations. </p>\n<p>Pheochromocytomas can occur in isolation or can be associated with hereditary tumor syndromes with the specific germline mutations such as:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis type 1 - mutation in <strong>NF1</strong> gene</li>\n<li>Familial paraganglioma syndrome - mutations within succinate dehydrogenase complex subunit B (<strong>SDHB</strong>), <strong>SDHD</strong>, <strong>SDHC</strong> genes</li>\n<li>Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN 2) - activating germline mutations of <strong>RET</strong> proto-oncogene</li>\n<li>Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome - mutations in <strong>VHL</strong> gene</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0854","text":"A 15-year-old boy undergoe​s bilateral adrenalectomy‍ for phaeochromocytoma. O​n the third postoperative‌ day, the boy feels letha‌rgic and tired. Blood pre​ssure is 80/60 mm Hg, the‌re is no sign of bleed. W​hat could be the cause of​ this?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy undergoes bilateral adrenalectomy for phaeochromocytoma. On the third postoperative day, the boy feels lethargic and tired. Blood pressure is 80/60 mm Hg, there is no sign of bleed. What could be the cause of this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Addisoni​an crisis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"SI​ADH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes ​insipidus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebral salt wasting dise​ase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Addisonian crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Addisonian crisis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>hypotension</strong> with no presence of bleeding and <strong>bilateral adrenalectomy</strong> is suggestive of <strong>an Addisonian</strong><strong> crisis. </strong>It is a life-threatening condition caused due to <strong>sudden insufficiency</strong> <strong>of adrenal hormones</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute adrenal insufficiency (adrenal or addisonian's crisis) is a medical emergency caused due to a precipitating illness unmasking insufficiency or trauma or severe sepsis causing adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome) or surgical removal of the glands. It presents with non-specific symptoms such as shock with fever, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, hypocalcemia and electrolyte imbalances. It may be often misdiagnosed as an acute abdominal emergency.</p>\n<p>Adrenal insufficiency can be primary (due to adrenal pathology), secondary (due to pituitary pathology- lack of ACTH) or tertiary (due to hypothalamus pathology- lack of CRH). </p>\n<p><strong>Autoimmune adrenalitis</strong> is the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency worldwide.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuberculosis</strong> is the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in India.</p>\n<p>Adrenal crisis requires immediate intervention owing to its high mortality rate. Immediate rehydration is initiated with a saline infusion at <strong>1 litre/hr</strong> with <strong>continuous cardiac</strong> <strong>monitoring</strong>. Glucocorticoid replacement is initiated with an initial bolus injection of <strong>hydrocortisone 100 mg</strong> followed by <strong>200 mg</strong> over 24 hours, preferably by continuous infusion or alternatively by IV or IM injections. Mineralocorticoid replacement is initiated once the daily dose of hydrocortisone has been reduced to <strong>&lt;50 mg</strong>, since hydrocortisone at higher doses provides sufficient stimulation of mineralocorticoids.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6681","text":"Neuroblastoma can originat​e in all of the following‍ except","raw_text":"Neuroblastoma can originate in all of the following except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organ of Z​uckerkandl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraspinal sympathetic gan​glia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenal​ medulla","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenal​ cortex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adrenal cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adrenal cortex</strong></p><p>Neuroblastoma does not originate in the adrenal cortex.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 885\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neuroblastoma is an <strong>embryonal tumour </strong>of the<strong> sympathetic nervous system</strong>. These tumours arise during fetal or early postnatal life from <strong>sympathetic</strong> <strong>cells</strong> derived from the <strong>neural crest</strong>. There­fore, tumours can originate anywhere along the path which neural crest cells migrate, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adrenal medulla - most common site</li>\n<li>Paraspinal sympathetic ganglia</li>\n<li>Sympathetic para­ ganglia such as the organ of Zuckerkandl</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Neuroblastoma is the most common abdominal (extradural) tumour in children and the most common overall tumour in infants.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3319","text":"A 2-year-old girl child wi​th failure to thrive was ‍found to have a palpable,​ firm, irregular abdomina‌l mass that crossed the m‌idline. CT revealed a cal​cified mass near the left‌ kidney which on biopsy s​howed Homer-Wright pseudo​rosettes. Which of the fo‌llowing is not true regar​ding this condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl child with failure to thrive was found to have a palpable, firm, irregular abdominal mass that crossed the midline. CT revealed a calcified mass near the left kidney which on biopsy showed Homer-Wright pseudorosettes. Which of the following is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"70% patients present with ​metastasis at diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urine homovanillic acid is​ used for screening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amplification of N-myc gen​e is a good prognostic fa‍ctor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abdominal mass is a common​ presentation of the cond‍ition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amplification of N-myc gene is a good prognostic factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59acd19677364c84a5f55e616b436fb2x1280x976.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amplification of N-myc gene is a good prognostic factor</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>neuroblastoma</strong> and <strong>amplification</strong> of<strong> N-myc gene</strong> is an indicator of <strong>poor prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neuroblastoma arises from the <strong>adrenal medulla.</strong> Homovanillic acid (HVA) and vanillylmandelic acid(VMA) are elevated in the urine of approximately 95% of patients and are used to diagnose and monitor this tumor. </p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Clinical features:</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Abdominal mass</strong> (retroperitoneal or hepatic)</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Periorbital ecchymoses (\"raccoon eyes,\" from periorbital ecchymosis caused by orbital metastases)</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Opsoclonus myoclonus syndrome</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Hypertension - due to secretion of catecholamines</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Metastatic disease is present in more than 70% of patients at presentation.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Histopathology is the gold standard for diagnosis. Histologically, classic neuroblastomas are composed of small, <strong>immature ganglion cells</strong> with dark nuclei. Typically, <strong>Homer-Wright pseudorosettes</strong> can be found which are tumour cells concentrically arranged about a central space filled with neuropil.</p>\n<p>The mainstay of treatment is resection of the tumour and chemotherapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0324","text":"What is the most common pr​esentation of adrenal car‍cinoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common presentation of adrenal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdomin​al mass","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pa​in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cushing syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cushing syndrome</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>presentation</strong> in patients with adrenal carcinoma is <strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong> due to excess cortisol.</p>\n<p>Patients with non-functioning tumours frequently complain of abdominal discomfort or back pain (large tumours).</p>\n<p><strong>Mc Farlane</strong> classification is used for staging adrenal carcinoma. Treatment involves complete resection of tumour and chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8nJTQLYu","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"16_intravenous_anesthesia___barbiturates__benzodiazepines___propofol","title":"16.Intravenous Anesthesia   Barbiturates, Benzodiazepines & Propofol","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MA5026","text":"What concentration of intr‌avenous thiopentone is us‍ed to induce anesthesia i​n a patient?","raw_text":"What concentration of intravenous thiopentone is used to induce anesthesia in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌25%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.‌5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2.5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2.5%</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Thiopentone</strong> is used as a <strong>2.5%</strong> solution.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Thiopentone is prepared as a sodium salt (mixed with 6% by weight anhydrous sodium carbonate) and can be reconstituted with water, glucose 5%, or normal saline to produce a 2.5% solution of thiopental.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Other reconstituted solutions:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li><strong>2%</strong> solution of <strong>thiamylal</strong></li>\n<li><strong>1%</strong> solution of <strong>methohexital</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5030","text":"In patients administered t‌hiopentone, which propert‍y of the drug allows for ​the rapid recovery from a​nesthesia?","raw_text":"In patients administered thiopentone, which property of the drug allows for the rapid recovery from anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid el‌imination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid rea‌bsorption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid me‌tabolism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid redi‌stribution","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rapid redistribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rapid redistribution</strong></p><p><strong>Rapid recovery</strong> from <strong>thiopentone anesthesia</strong> is due to its <strong>rapid redistribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is redistributed from the brain to tissues that are less perfused, like muscles, resulting in rapid recovery (within 20 minutes).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5025","text":"You are asked to reconstit‌ute thiopentone a few min‍utes before the 1st OT ca​se is scheduled to start.​ Which of the following w​ould you not use?","raw_text":"You are asked to reconstitute thiopentone a few minutes before the 1st OT case is scheduled to start. Which of the following would you not use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal‌ saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glu‌cose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ringer's‌ lactate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wa‌ter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ringer's lactate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ringer&#x27;s lactate</strong></p><p><strong>Thiopentone</strong> is an alkaline solution and should <strong>not</strong> be reconstituted with <strong>Ringer's lactate</strong> which has an acidic pH. Ringer's lactate <strong>decreases</strong> the<strong> alkalinity</strong> of the solution and the <strong>calcium</strong> present in it can result in the <strong>precipitation</strong> of thiopentone. </p>\n<p>Thiopentone and other barbiturates are prepared as sodium salts (mixed with 6% by weight anhydrous sodium carbonate) and can be reconstituted with<strong> water</strong>,<strong> 5% glucose, </strong><strong>or normal saline</strong> to produce a <strong>2.5%</strong> <strong>solution</strong> of <strong>thiopental.</strong></p>\n<p>After reconstitution:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thiobarbiturates (thiopentone, thiamylal) – stable if refrigerated for 1 week</li>\n<li>Methohexital – stable if refrigerated for 6 weeks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5034","text":"In which of the following ‌patients can thiopentone ‍be administered safely?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients can thiopentone be administered safely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 44-year-old man with bra‌in tumor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 24-year-old woman with h‌istory of acute intermitt‍ent porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 60-year-old woman in hyp‌ovolemic shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 52-year-old man with chr‌onic obstructive pulmonar‍y disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 44-year-old man with brain tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 44-year-old man with brain tumor</strong></p><p>Thiopentone can be <strong>safely</strong> administered in a patient with <strong>raised intracranial tension</strong>, i.e., brain tumor.</p>\n<p>Thiopentone is <strong>cerebroprotective</strong> as it reduces CSF volume by facilitating its absorption. It also decreases cerebral blood flow, cerebral blood volume and helps in <strong>lowering intracranial pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The other options are <strong>contraindications</strong> to the use of thiopentone.</p>\n<p>Option B: Thiopentone induces aminolevulinic acid synthetase, which increases the formation of porphyrins. This precipitate porphyria attacks, especially acute intermittent porphyria or variegate porphyria in susceptible individuals.</p>\n<p>Option C: Thiopentone should be avoided in hypovolemic or shock patients because of the significant reduction in cardiac output (69%) and arterial blood pressure due to vasodilation and venous pooling.</p>\n<p>Option D: Thiopentone produces dose-related central respiratory depression, producing a characteristic ventilatory pattern called 'double apnea'.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5032","text":"On administration of intra‌venous thiopentone for in‍duction, a patient loses ​consciousness in less tha​n thirty seconds. Which o​f the following is the re‌ason for its rapid onset ‌of action?","raw_text":"On administration of intravenous thiopentone for induction, a patient loses consciousness in less than thirty seconds. Which of the following is the reason for its rapid onset of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low lipid ‌solubility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid redi‌stribution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High amount of ionized fra‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High amount of unionized f‌raction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High amount of unionized fraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High amount of unionized fraction</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Thiopentone</strong> has a <strong>rapid onset</strong> of action due to the <strong>high</strong><strong> unionized fraction</strong> and <strong>high lipid solubility.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Only the <strong>unionized</strong> form can cross the <strong>blood-brain barrier</strong> and exert its action (within 30 s).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Rapid redistribution is responsible for the termination of action of thiopentone. i.e., it governs the duration of the action, not the onset.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5033","text":"Which of the statement reg‌arding the drug given bel‍ow is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the statement regarding the drug given below is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid onset and ultrashort‌ duration of action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Produces adequate analgesi‌a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contraindicated in status ‌asthmaticus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebrop‌rotective","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Produces adequate analgesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d43519b3e4a545888fe5975788ce5da2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Produces adequate analgesia</strong></p><p>Thiopentone <strong>does not produce analgesia</strong> and may even produce anti-analgesia by reducing the pain threshold.</p>\n<p>Option A: Thiopentone has a <strong>rapid onset</strong> of action (within 30 s) due to high lipid solubility and a high unionized fraction (60%). It has an <strong>ultra-short</strong> duration of action (within 20 mins) due to rapid redistribution.</p>\n<p>Option C: Thiopentone does not completely suppress airway responses to laryngoscopy and intubation and may lead to bronchospasm in asthmatics. </p>\n<p>Option D: Thiopentone is <strong>cerebroprotective </strong>as it reduces CSF volume by facilitating its absorption. It decreases cerebral blood flow and cerebral blood volume, thus lowering intracranial pressure. It also has an <strong>anticonvulsant</strong> property which is advantageous in neurosurgical patients who are at increased risk of seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5036","text":"A middle-aged woman diagno‌sed with major depressive‍ disorder associated with​ suicidal thoughts is sch​eduled to receive electro​convulsive therapy. What ‌is the anesthetic agent o‌f choice for her procedur​e?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman diagnosed with major depressive disorder associated with suicidal thoughts is scheduled to receive electroconvulsive therapy. What is the anesthetic agent of choice for her procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methoh‌exitone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pentoba‌rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methohexitone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methohexitone</strong></p><p>Intravenous <strong>methohexital</strong> is the agent of choice for providing anesthesia during <strong>electroconvulsive therapy</strong> (ECT). </p>\n<p>Methohexital can <strong>induce seizures</strong> and has a <strong>rapid recovery.</strong> It is also useful in <strong>identifying epileptic foci</strong> during surgery for resistant seizures.</p>\n<p>Note: According to Mental Health Care Act (MHCA) 2017, ECT should be performed only after administering a short-acting general anesthetic along with muscle relaxants. This method is known as modified ECT.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 643\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5027","text":"Which of the following bar‌biturates is not complete‍ly eliminated by the live​r?","raw_text":"Which of the following barbiturates is not completely eliminated by the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoba‌rbitone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thia‌mylal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methoh‌exital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenobarbitone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenobarbitone</strong></p><p><strong>Phenobarbitone</strong> is not completely eliminated by the liver. 25% to 50% of the drug is excreted by the kidneys in an unchanged form.</p>\n<p>All <strong>barbiturates </strong>(thiopentone, thiamylal, methohexital)<strong> </strong>other than phenobarbitone are completely eliminated by<strong> </strong>the liver. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5035","text":"A school student is brough‌t to the casualty unrespo‍nsive following trauma to​ the head. The NCCT image​ is shown below. Which dr​ug would you prefer for i‌nduction of anesthesia in‌ him?","raw_text":"A school student is brought to the casualty unresponsive following trauma to the head. The NCCT image is shown below. Which drug would you prefer for induction of anesthesia in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thiopentone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be48d8c17d4b421bb2e752e37fe3e621.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thiopentone</strong></p><p>The imaging suggests an <strong>extradural hemorrhage</strong> (EDH). <strong>Thiopentone</strong> is preferred for induction of anesthesia in <strong>head injury.</strong></p>\n<p>Thiopentone is <strong>cerebroprotective, </strong>as it decreases cerebral blood flow (CBF), thus lowering intracranial pressure (ICP). It reduces cerebral oxygen consumption (decreased CMRO<sub>2</sub>). It also has an anticonvulsant property which is advantageous in neurosurgical patients who are at increased risk of seizures.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 103px; width: 390px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.922px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.5938px; text-align: center;\"><strong>CBF</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 62.7812px; text-align: center;\"><strong>ICP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.1094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>CMRO<sub>2</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.5938px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Seizure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.922px;\"><strong>Thiopentone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.5938px; text-align: center;\">↓↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 62.7812px; text-align: center;\">↓↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.1094px; text-align: center;\">↓↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.5938px; text-align: center;\">↓↓↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.922px;\"><strong>Ketamine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.5938px; text-align: center;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 62.7812px; text-align: center;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.1094px; text-align: center;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.5938px; text-align: center;\">↑</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.922px;\"><strong>Halothane</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.5938px; text-align: center;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 62.7812px; text-align: center;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.1094px; text-align: center;\">↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.5938px; text-align: center;\">↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.922px;\"><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.5938px; text-align: center;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 62.7812px; text-align: center;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.1094px; text-align: center;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.5938px; text-align: center;\">↓ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5038","text":"You administer intravenous‌ thiopentone for inductio‍n in a patient. As soon a​s you inject the drug, th​e patient complains of ex​cruciating pain in his fi‌ngers with burning sensat‌ion. Fingers turned pale ​initially and soon became​ cyanosed. What will be y​our next step in the mana​gement of this situation?‌","raw_text":"You administer intravenous thiopentone for induction in a patient. As soon as you inject the drug, the patient complains of excruciating pain in his fingers with burning sensation. Fingers turned pale initially and soon became cyanosed. What will be your next step in the management of this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administ‌er saline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Administe‌r heparin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Administer‌ lidocaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Remove the needle immediat‌ely","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Administer saline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Administer saline</strong></p><p>The given clinical features of severe pain with burning sensation and cyanosis of fingers point to <strong>accidental intra-arterial administration</strong> of thiopentone. <strong>Saline</strong> administration <strong>via the same needle</strong> to dilute the drug should be the first step in managing this case. </p>\n<p>Intra-arterial injection of thiopentone <strong>can form crystals</strong> in the arterioles and capillaries, causing severe vasoconstriction, <strong>thrombosis</strong> and even <strong>gangrene</strong>. The degree of gangrene corresponds to the concentration of the drug.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>needle should not be removed</strong> as it can aggravate vasospasm</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Saline administered via the same needle to dilute the drug</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Heparin </strong>to prevent thrombosis</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Papaverine and lidocaine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Brachial plexus block</strong> or <strong>stellate ganglion block </strong>to relieve vasospasm</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5123","text":"What is the main goal of p‌re-medication prior to su‍rgery?","raw_text":"What is the main goal of pre-medication prior to surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease ‌secretions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduce postoperative nause‌a and vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diminish‌ anxiety","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prevent a‌spiration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diminish anxiety","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diminish anxiety</strong></p><p>The<strong> m<strong>ain goal</strong> </strong>of premedication is to<strong> d<strong>iminish anxiety.</strong></strong></p>\n<p>Preoperative medication may also provide relief of <strong>preoperative pain</strong> or <strong>perioperative amnesia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Indications for preoperative medication:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prophylaxis against postoperative <strong>nausea</strong> and <strong>vomiting</strong> (5-HT3s)</li>\n<li>Prophylaxis against <strong>aspiration pneumonia</strong> (eg, antacids)</li>\n<li>Prevention of <strong>allergic</strong> reactions (eg, antihistamines)</li>\n<li><strong>Decreasing</strong> upper airway <strong>secretions</strong> (eg, anticholinergics)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some medications that are given preoperatively (e.g., opioids) decrease anaesthetic requirements and can cause smooth induction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0456","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs should be avoided in p‍atients with bronchial as​thma?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs should be avoided in patients with bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop‌ental","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thiopental","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thiopental</strong></p><p><strong>Histamine release</strong> is more <strong>commonly </strong>seen with barbiturates like <strong>thiopental</strong> and methohexital. Hence thiopental should be<strong> avoided</strong> in patients with bronchial asthma to<strong> prevent wheezing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Barbiturates increase histamine release from mast cells and produce a higher incidence of wheezing when compared to propofol.</p>\n<p>Note: Thiopental and methohexital are ultra-short acting barbiturates indicated for the induction of anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5125","text":"A 4-year-old child planned‌ for an MRI requires to b‍e sedated. Which short-ac​ting benzodiazepine is to​ be ideally administered ​for this purpose?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child planned for an MRI requires to be sedated. Which short-acting benzodiazepine is to be ideally administered for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mida‌zolam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tema‌zepam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Midazolam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Midazolam</strong></p><p>The shortest-acting benzodiazepine among the given options is <strong>midazolam. </strong>It is administered as a <strong>pre-medication</strong> in <strong>children</strong> having separation anxiety before procedures or in short procedures requiring <strong>sedation.</strong> </p>\n<p>The benzodiazepines used in anesthesia are classified according to their metabolism and plasma clearance as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ultra-short acting - Remimazolam (FDA-approved for procedural sedation≤<strong> </strong>30 mins in adults)</li>\n<li>Short-acting agent - Midazolam</li>\n<li>Intermediate-acting agents - Lorazepam, temazepam</li>\n<li>Long-acting agent - Diazepam</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Midazolam is the <strong>benzodiazepine of choice</strong> for<strong> induction</strong> of anesthesia. The onset of anesthesia is within 30 to 60 seconds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5126","text":"Benzodiazepines are admini‌stered to a patient as pr‍e-medication, per the ane​sthesia orders. These dru​gs have all the following​ effects except:","raw_text":"Benzodiazepines are administered to a patient as pre-medication, per the anesthesia orders. These drugs have all the following effects except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allay ‌anxiety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anal‌gesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle r‌elaxation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perioperat‌ive amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Analgesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Analgesia</strong></p><p>Benzodiazepines <strong>do not produce analgesia</strong>, and so, they must be used with other anesthetic drugs to provide sufficient analgesia.</p>\n<p>Benzodiazepines are the most commonly administered drugs for premedication. They act through<strong> GABA A receptors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>α</strong><strong>1 subunit -</strong> sedation, anterograde amnesia (retrograde memory unaffected) and anticonvulsant action</li>\n<li><strong>α</strong><strong>2 subunit</strong> - anxiolysis and muscle relaxation</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5127","text":"Remimazolam is administere‌d to a 44-year-old man pr‍ior to an esophagogastrod​uodenoscopy. Which of the​ following statements is ​incorrect regarding the d‌rug?","raw_text":"Remimazolam is administered to a 44-year-old man prior to an esophagogastroduodenoscopy. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be used for procedures‌ thirty minutes or less","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Faster acting than midazol‌am","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolized by nonspecific‌ esterases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"New GABA-B receptor agonis‌t","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. New GABA-B receptor agonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. New GABA-B receptor agonist</strong></p><p><strong>Remimazolam</strong> is a new <strong> GABA-A </strong>receptor <strong>agonist.</strong></p>\n<p>It combines the properties of two unique drugs - midazolam and remifentanil. It acts on GABA-A receptors like midazolam and has organ independent metabolism like remifentanil.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>ultra-short-acting</strong> intravenous benzodiazepine, which is rapidly degraded in plasma by<strong> nonspecific esterases</strong> to its carboxylic acid metabolite CNS 7054.</p>\n<p>It has a rapid onset of action, greater depth of sedation, and more rapid recovery than with midazolam.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9224","text":"Which of the following can‌ be used as an intravenou‍s induction agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used as an intravenous induction agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dexmede‌tomidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neost‌igmine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lorazepam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lorazepam</strong></p><p><strong>Lorazepam</strong> is a benzodiazepine that can be used for <strong>induction of anesthesia </strong>at a dose of 0.1mg/kg, although <strong>midazolam</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> among benzodiazepines.</p>\n<p>Benzodiazepines are mainly used for <strong>sedation</strong> and less commonly for induction of anesthesia due to the availability of better induction agents (propofol, etomidate, ketamine, and thiopentone).</p>\n<p>Dexmedetomidine is a selective alpha-2 agonist and is used primarily as an adjuvant. So, a better option would be lorazepam.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5129","text":"Overdose with which of the‌ following drugs can be r‍eversed with flumazenil?","raw_text":"Overdose with which of the following drugs can be reversed with flumazenil?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbit‌urates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benzodi‌azepines","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opi‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphet‌amines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Benzodiazepines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Benzodiazepines</strong></p><p><strong>Flumazenil </strong>is the first<strong> benzodiazepine antagonist </strong>approved for<strong> clinical use </strong>to reverse the effects of benzodiazepines.</p>\n<p>It is a<strong> competitive reversible</strong> antagonist at the benzodiazepine receptor with high affinity and specificity.</p>\n<p>Flumazenil is used for both diagnostic and therapeutic reversal of benzodiazepine receptor agonists.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5045","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect conc‍erning the drug in the im​age below?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect concerning the drug in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predisposes to systemic in‌fections","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with pancreatit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces postoperative naus‌ea and vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contraindicated in egg all‌ergic patients","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contraindicated in egg allergic patients","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4738d0221eb49c0b0c4902e4d25e642.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contraindicated in egg allergic patients</strong></p><p>Propofol is <strong>not contraindicated</strong> in <strong>egg allergy</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>Propofol is formulated as an oil-in-water emulsion, which contains soybean oil, glycerol, and egg lecithin. Usually, egg allergies are a reaction to <strong>egg white</strong> (egg<strong> albumin</strong>). But, <strong>propofol </strong>contains egg<strong> lecithin, </strong>which is extracted from egg<strong> yolk,</strong> and so, propofol is not necessarily contraindicated in patients with a history of egg allergy.</p>\n<p>Propofol has been associated with life-threatening<strong> systemic infections</strong> because it decreases polymorphonuclear leukocyte chemotaxis, but not adherence phagocytosis and killing. It inhibits phagocytosis and killing of <em>Staphylococcus aureus </em>and <em>Escherichia coli. </em></p>\n<p>Propofol is associated with <strong>hypertriglyceridemia</strong> which can predispose to <strong>pancreatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>It has antiemetic properties which help in reducing the incidence of post operative nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5042","text":"Bacterial contamination of‌ drug vials is a risk wit‍h ___________.","raw_text":"Bacterial contamination of drug vials is a risk with ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Propofol</strong></p><p><strong>Bacterial contamination</strong> of vials is a risk with <strong>propofol. </strong></p>\n<p>Propofol formulations contain egg lecithin and soyabean oil. The <strong>egg</strong> is a good medium for <strong>bacterial growth</strong> and the lack of preservatives supports the growth of bacteria which can lead to sepsis or even death.</p>\n<p>Hence, propofol must be used<strong> within 6 hours </strong>of opening the ampule.</p>\n<p>Nowadays, certain additives like 0.005% disodium edetate or 0.025% sodium metabisulfite have been added to propofol formulations so as to retard the rate of growth of microorganisms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5050","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega‍rding propofol?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding propofol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypotension following indu‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Severe pruritis following ‌injection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pain on ‌injection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depresses ai‌rway reflexes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Severe pruritis following injection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Severe pruritis following injection</strong></p><p>Propofol has <strong>antipruritic properties</strong>. It can also relieve cholestatic pruritus and may be as effective as naloxone in treating pruritus induced by spinal opiates.</p>\n<p>Option A: Propofol produces a 25% to 40% <strong>reduction</strong> in<strong> systolic blood pressure</strong> following injection.</p>\n<p>Option C: Propofol is formulated as a lipid emulsion that causes<strong> pain</strong> during injection. This can be decreased by using a large vein, avoiding veins in the dorsum of the hand, prior injection of lidocaine or by mixing lidocaine with propofol prior to injection (2 mL of 1% lidocaine in 18 mL propofol).</p>\n<p>Option D: Propofol produces maximum inhibition of airway reflexes and can facilitate intubation, endoscopy, or laryngeal mask placement without neuromuscular blockade.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5068","text":"You decide to use a prodru‌g of propofol for a bronc‍hoscopy under conscious s​edation. Which of the fol​lowing is an incorrect st​atement regarding this dr‌ug?","raw_text":"You decide to use a prodrug of propofol for a bronchoscopy under conscious sedation. Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water ‌soluble","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Faster acting than propofo‌l","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No pain on‌ injection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Less incidence of hypotens‌ion and apnea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Faster acting than propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Faster acting than propofol</strong></p><p><strong>Fospropofol</strong> is a <strong>water-soluble prodrug</strong> of propofol that has a <strong>slower onset</strong> and <strong>slower recovery </strong>than propofol. It is hydrolyzed to propofol, phosphate, and formaldehyde. </p>\n<p>Fospropofol is advantageous since it is not associated with pain on injection. </p>\n<p>Fospropofol is approved for sedation during local and regional anesthesia e.g., conscious sedation and monitored anesthesia care. Due to its slower onset of sedation, it is associated with a lower incidence of hypotension, respiratory depression, apnea, and loss of airway patency. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5052","text":"Which of the following ane‌sthetics have high abuse ‍potential?","raw_text":"Which of the following anesthetics have high abuse potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methoh‌exital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Propofol</strong></p><p><strong>Propofol</strong> is an intravenous induction agent that can be <strong>addictive</strong> because of its <strong>hypnotic</strong> property and development of <strong>tolerance</strong> on prolonged use. </p>\n<p>The abuse potential is common among health care workers with reports of self-administration. There is only a <strong>narrow therapeutic window</strong> between the hypnotic, euphoric effect of propofol and its lethal dose. When exceeded, it can cause airway obstruction, respiratory depression, hypoxia or an unstable hemodynamic response leading to <strong>death</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Ketamine</strong> also has the potential for abuse and addiction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5057","text":"A critically ill patient w‌as given an infusion of a‍n intravenous anesthetic ​at a dose of 4mg/kg/hour.​ 48 hours later, the pati​ent developed refractory ‌bradycardia, and there wa‌s evidence of rhabdomyoly​sis on investigations. Wh​ich agent is most likely ​implicated?","raw_text":"A critically ill patient was given an infusion of an intravenous anesthetic at a dose of 4mg/kg/hour. 48 hours later, the patient developed refractory bradycardia, and there was evidence of rhabdomyolysis on investigations. Which agent is most likely implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Propofol</strong></p><p>The given features point towards a diagnosis of <strong>propofol infusion syndrome</strong>. It is a <strong>rare</strong> but <strong>lethal</strong> syndrome seen when the dose of propofol infusion is <strong>&gt; 4 mg/kg/hour</strong> for<strong> 48 hours </strong>or longer.</p>\n<p>This syndrome is common in children and critically ill adults.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute refractory <strong>bradycardia</strong> leading to asystole in the presence of one or more of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis (base deficit &gt;10 mmol/L)</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Enlarged or fatty liver</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other features -  cardiomyopathy with acute cardiac failure, skeletal myopathy, hyperkalemia, hepatomegaly, and lipemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The symptoms and signs are the results of muscle injury and the release of intracellular toxic contents. It is due to defect in<strong> mitochondrial metabolism </strong>and <strong>electron transport chain </strong>function.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5046","text":"Two days after he underwen‌t a gastrectomy, a patien‍t complained of severe ab​dominal pain, nausea, and​ vomiting. Labs showed el​evated serum amylase and ‌lipase levels. CECT showe‌d diffuse parenchymal enl​argement of the pancreas.​ Administration of which ​of the following anesthet​ics is implicated here?","raw_text":"Two days after he underwent a gastrectomy, a patient complained of severe abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.  Labs showed elevated serum amylase and lipase levels. CECT showed diffuse parenchymal enlargement of the pancreas. Administration of which of the following anesthetics is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propofol</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>, which can be predisposed by the administration of <strong>propofol.</strong> </p>\n<p>Propofol has been associated with <strong>hypertriglyceridemia,</strong> which may predispose the patient to pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for propofol-induced hypertriglyceridemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Old age</li>\n<li>Longer ICU stay</li>\n<li>Received propofol for a longer duration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is essential to monitor serum triglyceride levels when propofol is used for a prolonged duration or at higher infusion rates (especially in older patients).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5061","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings would not be seen i‍n a patient with propofol​ infusion syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings would not be seen in a patient with propofol infusion syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brady‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac‌ failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperli‌pidemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Propofol infusion syndrome</strong> is associated with <strong>rhabdomyolysis</strong> which leads to <strong>h</strong><strong>yperkalemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Propofol infusion syndrome is a rare but lethal syndrome due to a defect in<strong> mitochondrial metabolism </strong>and <strong>electron transport chain </strong>function. It is seen when the dose of propofol infusion is greater than<strong> 4mg/kg/hour </strong>for <strong>48 hours </strong>or longer.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute refractory bradycardia leading to asystole in the presence of one or more of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis (base deficit &gt;10 mmol/L)</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Enlarged or fatty liver</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other features -  cardiomyopathy with acute cardiac failure, skeletal myopathy, hyperkalemia, hepatomegaly, and lipemia. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5028","text":"Which one of the following‌ anesthetic agents shows ‍the Robin Hood phenomenon​?","raw_text":"Which one of the following anesthetic agents shows the Robin Hood phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thiopentone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thiopentone</strong></p><p><strong>Robin Hood</strong> or<strong> reverse steal </strong>phenomenon is seen with<strong> thiopentone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thiopentone <strong>constricts</strong> the <strong>cerebral vasculature,</strong> causing a decrease in cerebral blood flow, cerebral blood volume, and intracranial pressure. However, this vasoconstriction occurs only in <strong>normal areas.</strong> The blood vessels in the ischemic area remain dilated due to <strong>ischemia-induced vasomotor paralysis</strong> and are unaffected by thiopentone. This leads to <strong>redistribution </strong>of blood flow from <strong>normal to ischemic </strong>areas in the brain. This is called the Robin Hood phenomenon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5122","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about total intraven‍ous anesthesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about total intravenous anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduces cerebral metabolis‌m and cerebral blood flow‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth induction with high‌ incidence of post operat‍ive nausea and vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propofol inhibits pulmonar‌y vasoconstriction d/t hy‍poxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Higher chances of nephroto‌xicity and pulmonary toxi‍city","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reduces cerebral metabolism and cerebral blood flow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduces cerebral metabolism and cerebral blood flow</strong></p><p>Total intravenous anesthesia results in<strong> reduced cerebral metabolism</strong> and<strong> cerebral blood flow. </strong>The most commonly used agent is<strong> propofol. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong> of <strong>TIVA</strong> over inhaled anesthetics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smooth induction and rapid recovery with a minimal hangover</li>\n<li>Low incidence of nausea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Safe in patients susceptible to malignant hyperpyrexia </li>\n<li>Reduced greenhouse effect of inhalational anesthesia</li>\n<li>Paralysis is not always required, reversal agents can be avoided and emergence is rapid (especially in propofol TIVA with remifentanil).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5024","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an intravenous induct‍ion agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an intravenous induction agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Halothane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Halothane</strong></p><p>Halothane is an <strong>inhalational</strong> agent which is a halogenated alkane. Induction with halothane is relatively <strong>slow </strong>due to its <strong>high blood: gas partition coefficient </strong>(2.5).</p>\n<p>Intravenous induction agents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Propofol is the most commonly used intravenous anesthetic.</li>\n<li>Thiopentone is the intravenous agent of choice to provide cerebral protection.</li>\n<li>Etomidate is the intravenous agent of choice to maintain cardiovascular stability in patients with coronary artery disease, cardiomyopathy and cerebral vascular disease.</li>\n<li>Ketamine is the intravenous agent of choice in asthmatics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"R1S10Pnw","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"16_skin___special_senses__eye_and_ear","title":"16.Skin & Special Senses, Eye And Ear","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB2709","text":"Match the following marked‍ structures to the correc​t layers of the epidermis‌. Stratum granulosum Stra​tum corneum Stratum spino​sum Stratum basale","raw_text":"<p>Match the following marked structures to the correct layers of the epidermis.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stratum granulosum</li>\n<li>Stratum corneum</li>\n<li>Stratum spinosum</li>\n<li>Stratum basale</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-3, B-4,‍ C-2, D-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-3, B-2,‍ C-4, D-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-2, B-4,‍ C-3, D-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-2, B-1,‍ C-3, D-4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e552f85cd3d54fb7ac4234811ef287d9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4</strong></p><p>The image shows the histological section of the <strong>epidermis</strong>. The structures marked are respectively:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> Stratum corneum</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> Stratum granulosum</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> Stratum spinosum</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"> Stratum basale</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The above <strong>4 layers</strong> are present in the <strong>thin hairy skin.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>thick glabrous skin</strong> over the <strong>palms</strong> and the<strong> soles</strong> alone contains a clear homogenous<strong> 5th layer</strong> between the stratum corneum and stratum granulosum, called the <strong>stratum lucidum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2724","text":"Which of the following lay‍ers of epidermis is refer​red to as the prickle cel‌l layer?","raw_text":"Which of the following layers of epidermis is referred to as the prickle cell layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum‍ basale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum ‍spinosum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum g‍ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum‍ corneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stratum spinosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d643d71843c143c5bd40a842999c32afx1280x1152.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stratum spinosum</strong></p><p>Stratum <strong>spinosum</strong> of the epidermis is also referred to as the <strong>p</strong><strong>rickle cell layer.</strong></p>\n<p>Stratum spinosum consists of <strong>inter-cellular attachments</strong> called <strong>desmosomes.</strong> During the H&amp;E mounting, the cells shrink and retract at places other than their attachment at the desmosomes. Thus, these desmosomes appear like spines on the cells on microscopy, and hence the cells are called as <strong>prickle cells</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2726","text":"In which of the following ‍layers of the epidermis a​re multiple layers of int‌ertwining anucleate kerat​inocytes seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following layers of the epidermis are multiple layers of intertwining anucleate keratinocytes seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum‍ lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum‍ basale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum g‍ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum‍ corneum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratum corneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4cf1c79e6424ce8b096c7ddb8cb41d4x410x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratum corneum</strong></p><p>The stratum corneum (horny layer) consists of multiple layers of <strong>anucleate keratinocytes</strong> that often <strong>intertwine</strong> resulting in a <strong>basketweave appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>The deepest layer, <strong>stratum basale,</strong> contains the <strong>stem cells</strong> which divide and start moving superficially. As cells move more superficially, they begin to lose their nuclei and become progressively dehydrated, flattened and intertwined. Traces of flattened nuclei may be seen in some cells of stratum lucidum.</p>\n<p><strong>Stratum corneum</strong>, because of its thickness, prevents entry of microbes from the environment into the body as well as preventing leakage of fluids from the body to the environment. This is called <strong>barrier function.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2722","text":"Given below is a histologi‍cal section showing the l​ayers of the skin. The re‌gion marked X points to t​he location of_______","raw_text":"Given below is a histological section showing the layers of the skin. The region marked X points to the location of_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melan‍ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duct of sw‍eat glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desmo‍somes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemides‍mosomes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Desmosomes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cdbe46d0134b46e8880eabd28a954beb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3ec0806f6b94872b7aac094f8b3a9c1x1280x1614.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Desmosomes</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the region marked X points towards the location of <strong>intercellular adhesions</strong> formed by<strong> desmosomes.</strong></p>\n<p>Desmosomes (maculae adherens) are specialized adhesive protein complexes that localize to intercellular junctions. They are responsible for maintaining the mechanical integrity of tissues and resisting shearing forces. <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">They are made of</span><strong> intercellular filaments,</strong> dense <strong>cytoplasmic plaques</strong> within the cells on either side and <strong>intermediate filaments.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hemidesmosomes</strong> are specialized structures for the <strong>cell to extracellular material</strong> adhesions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The schematic representation shows the desmosomal proteins and relative distance from the plasma membrane. The desmosomal cadherins, the <strong>desmogleins</strong> and <strong>desmocollins,</strong> extend into the intercellular space and outer dense plaque to establish contact and adhere to neighbouring cells. The cadherin cytoplasmic tails associate linker proteins, <strong>plakoglobin</strong>, <strong>plakophilins</strong>, and <strong>desmoplakin</strong>. The desmoplakin binds to <strong>keratin intermediate filaments</strong> within the <strong>inner dense plaque</strong>, serving to tether the <strong>intermediate filaments</strong> to the plasma membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2705","text":"A young patient presented ‍with an itchy, rough rash​ with silvery scales alon‌g the extensor surface of​ her elbows, knees and sc​alp. Histopathological ex‌amination of the skin sam‌ple showed marked elongat​ion and clubbing of the s‍tructure marked X in the ​given image. Identify the‌ structure marked X.","raw_text":"A young patient presented with an itchy, rough rash with silvery scales along the extensor surface of her elbows, knees and scalp. Histopathological examination of the skin sample showed marked elongation and clubbing of the structure marked X in the given image. Identify the structure marked X.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticula‍r dermis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hair f‍ollicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillar‍y dermis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epiderm‍al pegs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epidermal pegs","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/066959cf863e4ca2a5d4f06567620df9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/933f2387100049a798bd599488f88fe5x1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epidermal pegs</strong></p><p>The clinical history is suggestive of <strong>psoriasis</strong> and the structure marked X are the <strong>epidermal pegs</strong> or <strong>Rete ridges,</strong> which are <strong>downward</strong> <strong>projections</strong> of the <strong>epidermis</strong> in the intervals between the dermal papillae.</p>\n<p>Histopathological examination of the skin in a case of psoriasis will show clubbing and elongation of the Rete ridges as shown in the given image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2728","text":"In which of the following ‍layers of the skin are me​lanocytes present?","raw_text":"In which of the following layers of the skin are melanocytes present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum‍ corneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum g‍ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum‍ basale","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Der‍mis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stratum basale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7aa986f4769844a48c158021034e0403x1280x1015.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abb5f59854094ffe873073e69090d7d9x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stratum basale</strong></p><p><strong>Melanocytes</strong> are present in the <strong>stratum basale</strong> of the epidermis.</p>\n<p>They are the neural crest derivatives that migrate to the <strong>stratum basale</strong> during the embryonic period. One melanocyte accumulates for every <strong>five or six basal keratinocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Melanocytes synthesise the pigment, <strong>melanin</strong>. This is then transferred along the dendritic processes of the melanocyte in vesicles called <strong>melanosomes</strong>. The tips of these processes are then phagocytosed by the surrounding <strong>keratinocytes</strong>, which then take up the <strong>melanin</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows multiple melanocytes marked with arrows in the stratum basale of the epidermis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2730","text":"Which of the following cel‍ls act as slow-adapting t​ouch receptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells act as slow-adapting touch receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melan‍ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kerati‍nocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Langerha‍ns cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Merkel‍ cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Merkel cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Merkel cells</strong></p><p><strong>Merkel cells</strong> act as slow-adapting mechanoreceptors and are sensitive to <strong>light touch sensation</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are present in the <strong>stratum basale</strong> of the highly sensitive skin like <strong>fingertips</strong> and <strong>bases of the hair follicle</strong>. The base of the Merkel cells is connected to the unmyelinated sensory fibres penetrating the basal lamina.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2734","text":"Identify the structure mar‍ked X in the given histol​ogical image of the dermi‌s.","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked X in the given histological image of the dermis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Merke‍l cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meissner’s‍ corpuscles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pacinian ‍corpuscles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ruffini’s ‍corpuscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pacinian corpuscles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c1a798ac86a4d7c8b6879e17eaa778f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1bf343d04eb4fae880337ec98c2aa98x1280x2319.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pacinian corpuscles</strong></p><p>The structure marked X in the histological image of the dermis is the<strong> Pacinian corpuscle</strong> which appears as a large (~ 1 mm), <strong>onion-like structure </strong>in the dermis and hypodermis.</p>\n<p>It consists of concentric lamellae of <strong>flattened Schwann</strong> cells and <strong>collagen</strong> surrounding an unmyelinated axon. They are very <strong>rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors</strong> and specialised for sensing <strong>coarse touch, pressure</strong> (sustained touch) and <strong>vibration</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2732","text":"Given below is an image of‍ a histological section o​f the skin. Identify the ‌structure marked X.","raw_text":"Given below is an image of a histological section of the skin. Identify the structure marked X.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Merke‍l cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meissner ‍corpuscles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pacinian ‍corpuscles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ruffini’s ‍corpuscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Meissner corpuscles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4826acd401a4b36adba002ac5c599f3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/639475e27e5c4241a7a9b704225da62cx1280x1285.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Meissner corpuscles</strong></p><p>The structure marked X in the histological image of the skin is the <strong>Meissner's corpuscle</strong> which is located in the<strong> papillary dermis.</strong></p>\n<p>Each corpuscle consists of a connective tissue capsule and a central core composed of a stack of flat <strong>modified Schwann cells</strong> (Coiled spring-like morphology). They are present in the <strong>dermal papillae</strong> of all parts of hands and foot, anterior aspect of the forearm, lips and mucous membrane of the apical part of the tongue.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>rapidly acting mechanoreceptors</strong> and are specialised for <strong>light touch</strong> and <strong>low-frequency stimuli</strong>. They are sensitive to shape and textural changes and provide the neural basis for reading <strong>Braille</strong> text.</p>\n<p>The histological image below shows Meissner's corpuscles in the dermal papillae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0619","text":"Panniculus adiposus is pre‍sent in:","raw_text":"Panniculus adiposus is present in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axi‍lla","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Scr‍otum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eye‍lid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pe‍nis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Axilla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Axilla</strong></p><p><strong>Panniculus adiposus</strong> is present in the axilla. </p>\n<p>Panniculus adiposus refers to a layer/sheet of fat present subcutaneously and is found over most of the body <strong>except </strong>the <strong>scrotum, penis, </strong>and <strong>eyelids. </strong>It is responsible for giving a smooth contour to the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2737","text":"A 28-year-old man presente‍d to the dermatology OPD ​with complaints of patchy‌ hair loss. A punch biops​y of the scalp was taken ​to assess the cause of th‌e hair loss. Given below ‌is the histological secti​on of the hair follicle. ‍Identify the structure ma​rked Y in the image","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man presented to the dermatology OPD with complaints of patchy hair loss. A punch biopsy of the scalp was taken to assess the cause of the hair loss. Given below is the histological section of the hair follicle. Identify the structure marked Y in the image","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cut‍icle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Med‍ulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inner ro‍ot sheath","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outer ro‍ot sheath","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Outer root sheath","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/02aa50d4ee7647f699e21eadfbe512d6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb0d2f43e7374561ae0b2d0b84fc1863x1280x1049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Outer root sheath</strong></p><p>The structure marked Y in the above image is the <strong>outer root sheath.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>hair</strong> is made up of the following layers (from within outwards):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medulla</strong> - consisting of vacuolated and moderately keratinised cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Cortex</strong> - Consisting of densely packed and heavily keratinised cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Inner</strong> root sheath - This includes cuticle, inner Huxley's layer and outer Henle's layer.</li>\n<li><strong>Outer</strong> root sheath - These appear as clear cells as they contain glycogen.</li>\n<li><strong>Glassy membrane</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Connective tissue sheath</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2739","text":"Which of the following acc‍urately describes the arr​ector pili muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes the arrector pili muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeletal‍ muscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth ‍muscles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mixture of skeletal and sm‍ooth muscle, with dominan​t skeletal muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mixture of skeletal and sm‍ooth muscle, with dominan​t smooth muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smooth muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b468a307ef04ad6858df197b7396bfax1280x1649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smooth muscles</strong></p><p>Arrector pili muscles are <strong>smooth muscles. </strong></p>\n<p>They connect the undersurface of the hair follicles to the papillary dermis and are supplied by <strong>sympathetic nerves. </strong>Contraction of these muscles leads to the erection of hair, tiny bumps on the skin and thus produces a <strong>'goose flesh’</strong> appearance. The other effect of the contraction of arrector pili is the squeezing out of the secretions of the sebaceous glands into the hair follicle. They are absent in facial, axillary and pubic hairs, eyelashes, eyebrows and hairs around the nostrils and external auditory meatus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2044","text":"Identify the type of gland‍ depicted in the followin​g image.","raw_text":"Identify the type of gland depicted in the following image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Holo‍crine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mero‍crine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apoc‍rine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endo‍crine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apocrine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b24790e0a4544defb9283fcce8eeef60.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aee3d75e326c4297ace65d136619ca03x1280x1280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e9d83e54e8c4bd38788f6962b5ae43ex1280x1457.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apocrine</strong></p><p>The image shows an <strong>apocrine gland.</strong> There is shedding of the apical portion of the cell during the secretion.</p>\n<p>Types of glands:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 454px; width: 437px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142.708px;\">Apocrine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.931px;\">Holocrine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.708px;\">Eccrine(Merocrine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142.708px;\"><strong>Apical part</strong> of cell <strong>sheds off</strong> during discharge</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.931px;\"><strong>Whole-cell disintegrates</strong> during discharge</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.708px;\">The <strong>cell is intact</strong> and <strong>secretions are thrown out by exocytosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142.708px;\">Eg: <strong>Apocrine sweat glands in axilla and groin</strong>, mammary glands, the external auditory canal (ceruminous glands) and eyelids (glands of Moll)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.931px;\">\n<p>Eg: <strong>Glands of Zeis and Meibomian gland</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Montgomery tubercle</strong>: Nipple and areola</p>\n<p><strong>Tyson's gland</strong>: External fold of the prepuce</p>\n<p><strong>Fordyce' spot</strong>: Vermillion border of lips and mucosa</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.708px;\">\n<p>Eg: <strong>Sweat glands on palms and soles</strong></p>\n<p>Found everywhere except clitoris, glans penis, labia minora, external auditory canal and lips</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Notice that for the <strong>sweat glands</strong>, the <strong>duct mostly goes to the skin surface</strong> v/s Sebaceous gland which mostly opens to the hair follicle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2740","text":"Given below is the histolo‍gical section of the corn​ea. Identify the structur‌es marked X and Y in the ​image.","raw_text":"Given below is the histological section of the cornea. Identify the structures marked X and Y in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bowman's membrane &amp;amp; co‍rneal epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Descemet's membrane &amp;amp; ‍corneal epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endothelium &amp;amp; Bowman's‍ membrane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal epithelium &amp;amp; s‍troma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endothelium & Bowman's membrane","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/805c9c4fa5b644f09865e7c7cfe99083.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a6aff5320dd4d57a0ccc0e5759f2892x1024x768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endothelium &amp; Bowman&#x27;s membrane</strong></p><p>The structures marked X and Y are the endothelium and Bowman's membrane, respectively.</p>\n<p>The <strong>human cornea has five layers</strong>. From the anterior to posterior, the layers of the human cornea are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Corneal epithelium </strong>- It is the outermost layer made up of non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. It is continuous with the conjunctival epithelium.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bowman's layer </strong>- The anterior-limiting lamina lies behind the corneal epithelium. It contains a dense mass of collagen (mainly type I collagen fibrils) set in a matrix similar to that of the substantia propria.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Corneal stroma</strong> (also substantia propria) - It is a thick, transparent middle layer, consisting of regularly arranged collagen fibers along with sparsely distributed interconnected keratocytes.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Descemet's membrane </strong>- It is a thin acellular layer that serves as the modified basement membrane of the corneal endothelium, from which the cells are derived. This layer is composed mainly of collagen <strong>type IV fibrils</strong>.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Corneal endothelium </strong>- The endothelium covers the posterior surface of the cornea and consists of a single layer of squamous cells.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The sixth layer called <strong>Dua’s layer or pre-Descemet’s layer</strong> has been recently discovered, located between the stroma and the Descemet’s membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5764","text":"Arrange from outer to inne‍r the layers of the retin​a given below: A-Retinal ‌pigment epithelium B-Inne​r plexiform layer C-Nerve​ fiber layer D-Inner nucl‌ear layer E-external limi‌ting membrane. Retinal pi​gment epithelium Inner pl‍exiform layer Nerve fiber​ layer Inner nuclear laye‌r External limiting membr‌ane.","raw_text":"<p>Arrange from outer to inner the layers of the retina given below: \nA-Retinal pigment epithelium \nB-Inner plexiform layer \nC-Nerve fiber layer \nD-Inner nuclear layer \nE-external limiting membrane.</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Retinal pigment epithelium</li>\n<li>Inner plexiform layer</li>\n<li>Nerve fiber layer</li>\n<li>Inner nuclear layer</li>\n<li>External limiting membrane.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E,D,‍C,B,A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A,B,‍D,C,E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A,E,‍D,B,C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"E,A,‍D,B,C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A,E,D,B,C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A,E,D,B,C</strong></p><p>From the given options, the correct sequence from outer to inner is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>retinal pigment epithelium (A), </li>\n<li>external limiting membrane (E), </li>\n<li>inner nuclear layer (D),</li>\n<li>inner plexiform layer (B), </li>\n<li>nerve fiber layer (C) <br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2742","text":"Identify the structure sho‍wn in the histological im​age below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure shown in the histological image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vest‍ibule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semicircu‍lar canals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coc‍hlea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tympanic‍ membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cochlea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a67b2026c4f94c84a179de5a34b0b015.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fa1099c985f4f9e99f462c657554251x1280x1123.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cochlea</strong></p><p>The above histological image is that of the <strong>cochlea</strong> which can be identified by the presence of characteristic <strong>organ of Corti.</strong></p>\n<p>The cochlea consists of a <strong>bony canal</strong> that makes <strong>2.75 spiral turns</strong> around an axis of bone known as the <strong>modiolus</strong>. A shelf of bone from the modiolus forms a ridge known as the spiral lamina.</p>\n<p>The <strong>basilar membrane</strong> supporting the organ of Corti extends from the spiral lamina to the outer wall of the cochlea. The <strong>vestibular membrane</strong> runs from the spiral lamina to the outer wall above the basilar membrane. These two membranes divide the bony labyrinth into <strong>three cavities:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Scala vestibule -</strong> located between the roof of the labyrinth and the vestibular membrane, is lined by mesenchymal cells and <strong>contains perilymph.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Scala media -</strong> located between the vestibular membrane and basilar membrane, contains <strong>endolymph</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Scala tympani -</strong> located between the basilar membrane and the floor of the labyrinth, is lined by mesenchymal cells and contains<strong> perilymph.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2743","text":"The gelatinous component o‍f the crista ampullaris t​hat extends from the cris‌ta to the roof of the amp​ullae is known as _____","raw_text":"The gelatinous component of the crista ampullaris that extends from the crista to the roof of the ampullae is known as _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca‍lix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spiral ‍ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cup‍ula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spiral ‍ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cupula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db4994facc0a4766b22c4026db4978fdx1280x1372.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cupula</strong></p><p>The gelatinous component of the crista ampullaris that extends from the crista to the roof of the ampullae is known as the cupula.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ampullary cupula</strong>, or cupula, provides a sense of <strong>spatial orientation. </strong>It is located in each of the ampullae of the three semicircular canals. In the crista ampullaris, hair cells that have several stereocilia associated with each kinocilium, are embedded into the gelatinous matrix of the cupula in a specific pattern that is functionally significant. The <strong>lag of endolymph</strong> on the <strong>rotational movement</strong> of the head <strong>deflects</strong> the<strong> cupula </strong>and hence<strong> stimulates</strong> the<strong> hair cells.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"X-di5sjp","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"16_cholesterol_synthesis__transport_and_excretion","title":"16.Cholesterol Synthesis, Transport And Excretion","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA9963","text":"Where does cholesterol syn​thesis take place?","raw_text":"Where does cholesterol synthesis take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytosol and endoplasmic re​ticulum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peroxisomes and mitochondr​ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondria​ and cytosol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitochondria and golgi bod​y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytosol and endoplasmic reticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytosol and endoplasmic reticulum</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Cholesterol synthesis</strong> takes place in the <strong>cytosol</strong> and <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Cytosolic acetyl-CoA</strong> forms <strong>acetoacetyl-CoA</strong>, which condenses with another acetyl-CoA to form <strong>HMG-CoA.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Cytosolic HMG-CoA, a key intermediate in cholesterol biosynthesis, is <strong>reduced</strong> in the <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> to <strong>mevalonic acid</strong> by the <strong>regulatory enzyme HMG-CoA reductase</strong>. Mevalonic acid subsequently undergoes phosphorylation, decarboxylation , condensation and cyclicization reactions to produce cholesterol. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Summary of cholesterol synthesis pathway: </p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Synthesis of <strong>mevalonate</strong> from <strong>acetyl-CoA</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Formation of<strong> isoprenoid units</strong> from mevalonate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Condensation of <strong>six</strong> isoprenoid units form <strong>squalene</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cyclization of squalene to give rise to the parent steroid, <strong>lanosterol</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Formation of <strong>cholesterol</strong> from lanosterol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">All tissues containing <strong>nucleated cells</strong> are capable of cholesterol synthesis, with the majority produced in the<strong> liver</strong> and <strong>intestine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9964","text":"What is the substrate for ​cholesterol synthesis?","raw_text":"What is the substrate for cholesterol synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-hydrox​ybutyrate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetoa​cetate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acety​l CoA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acyl​ CoA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetyl CoA</strong></p><p><strong>Cholesterol</strong> is<strong> synthesized</strong> from <strong>acetyl-CoA</strong> by a long pathway that may be divided into five steps</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Synthesis of <strong>mevalonate</strong> from <strong>acetyl-CoA</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Formation of<strong> isoprenoid units</strong> from mevalonate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Condensation of <strong>six</strong> isoprenoid units form <strong>squalene</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cyclization of squalene to give rise to the parent steroid, <strong>lanosterol</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Formation of <strong>cholesterol</strong> from lanosterol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">All tissues containing <strong>nucleated cells</strong> are capable of cholesterol synthesis, with the majority produced in the<strong> liver</strong> and <strong>intestine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9965","text":"Which of the following is ​not a primary bile acid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a primary bile acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choli​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chenodeoxy​cholic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glycocho​lic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deoxycho​lic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deoxycholic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deoxycholic acid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Deoxycholic acid</strong> is not a primary bile acid. It is a <strong>secondary bile acid.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>primary bile acids</strong> are <strong>cholic acid</strong> and <strong>chenodeoxycholic acid</strong> synthesized from cholesterol in the liver. They are next conjugated with glycine and taurine in the <strong>liver peroxisomes</strong> to form glycocholic, glycochenodeoxycholic, taurocholic, and taurochenodeoxycholic acids.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>secondary bile acids</strong> are formed in the intestine as the primary bile acids are <strong>deconjugated</strong> and <strong>dehydroxylated</strong> by the <strong>intestinal bacteria</strong> to form <strong>deoxycholic</strong> and<strong> lithocholic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">These are absorbed in the<strong> ileum</strong> and returned to the liver by <strong>enterohepatic circulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9966","text":"Which is the first interme​diate in the conversion o​f cholesterol to bile aci​ds?","raw_text":"Which is the first intermediate in the conversion of cholesterol to bile acids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7-dehydroc​holesterol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7-hydroxyc​holesterol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lanos​terol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ergos​terol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7-hydroxycholesterol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7-hydroxycholesterol</strong></p><p>The <strong>first</strong> <strong>intermediate</strong> in the synthesis of bile acids is <strong>7α-hydroxycholesterol</strong></p>\n<p>This reaction is catalyzed by the <strong>enzyme 7α-hydroxylase</strong>, a microsomal CYP450 enzyme, which <strong>requires NADPH, oxygen, </strong>and<strong> vitamin C </strong>as co-factors. It is the <strong>rate-limiting step</strong> in the synthesis of bile acids.</p>\n<p><strong>Regulation of bile acid synthesis </strong>occurs by <strong>feedback inhibition of 7α-hydroxylase </strong>via the<strong> Farnesoid X receptor</strong>. When the level of bile acids in the enterohepatic circulation increases, FXR is activated and transcription of the enzyme is suppressed.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: 7-dehydrocholesterol is an intermediate in the conversion of cholesterol to vitamin D.</p>\n<p>Option C: Lanosterol is an intermediate in the synthesis of cholesterol.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ergosterol is a sterol found in plants.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9967","text":"A 47-year-old diabetic man​ comes to the OPD for a f​ollow-up visit. In the mo​st recent lab report, ser​um LDL level is high. He ‍is therefore started on a‍ lipid-lowering drug that‌ acts on the rate-limitin‌g step of cholesterol syn‌thesis. Which of the foll‌owing is the site of acti​on of this drug?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old diabetic man comes to the OPD for a follow-up visit. In the most recent lab report, serum LDL level is high. He is therefore started on a lipid-lowering drug that acts on the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis. Which of the following is the site of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HMG CoA ​synthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HMG CoA ​reductase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Squalene ​epoxidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HMG Co​A lyase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HMG CoA reductase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2afac6c687004062b9e1b69d1334cbd3x600x566.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HMG CoA reductase</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>HMG-CoA reductase</strong> is the <strong>rate-limiting enzyme</strong> of cholesterol synthesis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">HMG-CoA reductase is <strong>inhibited</strong> by the <strong>statin</strong> group of drugs (e.g. Atorvastatin, Rosuvastatin, Pravastatin, etc.), which are widely used as lipid-lowering agents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Regulation of HMG CoA reductase:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 197px; width: 265.9375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"><strong>Activity increased by</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\"><strong>Activity decreased by</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Insulin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">Glucagon, glucocorticoids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Thyroid hormone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">Cholesterol, mevalonate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">Dietary cholesterol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.9375px;\">Statins</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Glucagon and glucocorticoids <strong>repress the transcription</strong> of HMG CoA reductase via sterol regulatory element-binding protein (SREBP).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9968","text":"Which is the first hormone​ to be produced in choles​terol synthesis?","raw_text":"Which is the first hormone to be produced in cholesterol synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholecy​stokinin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lanos​terol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secr​etin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lanosterol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lanosterol</strong></p><p><strong>Lanosterol</strong> is the <strong>first steroid</strong> to be synthesized <strong>in cholesterol synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyclization of squalene gives rise to the parent steroid, lanosterol.</li>\n<li>The formation of cholesterol from lanosterol involves changes in the steroid nucleus and the side chain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Though lanosterol is technically not a hormone, it is the best choice among the listed options. This is a recent NEET question.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9969","text":"What is the function of li​poprotein?","raw_text":"What is the function of lipoprotein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transport majority of nonp​olar lipids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transport majority of pola​r lipids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transport only cholesterol​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transport only fatty acids​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transport majority of nonpolar lipids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transport majority of nonpolar lipids</strong></p><p><strong>Lipoproteins transport </strong>the<strong> </strong>majority of<strong> non-polar </strong><strong>lipids</strong> to most tissues for oxidation and to adipose tissue for storage as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>From the intestines as <strong>chylomicrons</strong>, it carries <strong>exogenous</strong> triglycerides.</li>\n<li>From the liver as very-low-density lipoproteins (<strong>VLDL</strong>), it carries <strong>endogenous</strong> triglycerides.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9970","text":"Which of the following lip​oproteins have the highes​t cholesterol:triglycerid​e ratio? VLDL- Very low-d​ensity lipoprotein LDL- L‍ow-density lipoprotein HD‍L- High-density lipoprote‌in IDL- Intermediate-dens‌ity lipoprotein","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following lipoproteins have the highest cholesterol:triglyceride ratio?</p>\n<p>VLDL- Very low-density lipoprotein</p>\n<p>LDL- Low-density lipoprotein </p>\n<p>HDL- High-density lipoprotein </p>\n<p>IDL- Intermediate-density lipoprotein</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chylomicro​n and VLDL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LDL a​nd HDL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HDL an​d VLDL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IDL a​nd HDL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. LDL and HDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. LDL and HDL</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As <strong>cholesterol</strong> is the predominant lipid in <strong>LDL</strong> and <strong>HDL</strong>, they have a <strong>higher cholesterol: triglyceride ratio.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>VLDL</strong> also has a significant amount of cholesterol but it <strong>predominantly comprises triglycerides</strong> (lower cholesterol: triglyceride ratio). Therefore <strong>LDL</strong> and <strong>HDL</strong> are considered the <strong>major carriers of cholesterol</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">IDLs are formed by the removal of triglycerides from VLDL by muscle and adipose tissue and are hence enriched in cholesterol. LDLs are derived from VLDL and IDL particles and contain more cholesterol than IDLs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7422","text":"In a patient with lipoprot​ein lipase deficiency, wh​ich of the following is i​ncreased following a fatt​y meal?","raw_text":"In a patient with lipoprotein lipase deficiency, which of the following is increased following a fatty meal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chylo​micron","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L​DL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H​DL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ap​o A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chylomicron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chylomicron</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">After a fatty meal, <strong>chylomicrons</strong> are elevated in<strong> lipoprotein lipase deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Chylomicrons</strong> transport dietary lipids from the intestine to the peripheral tissues. They are <strong>synthesized</strong> in the intestine by <strong>assembling apo B-48 with lipids</strong> (mainly triacylglycerols). This is called a nascent chylomicron, which upon receiving apo C-II and apo E from HDL is remodelled into mature chylomicron.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Apo C-II</strong> activates <strong>lipoprotein lipase</strong> present on the <strong>endothelium</strong>. Lipoprotein lipase hydrolyzes the triacylglycerols into fatty acids and glycerol which are either used for storage or energy. Therefore, patients who are <strong>deficient in lipoprotein lipase</strong> or <strong>apo C-II</strong> (<strong>type I hyperlipoproteinemia</strong>) show <strong>increased levels of chylomicrons</strong> in the plasma after a fatty meal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9971","text":"Which of the following lip​oproteins are the major c​arriers of triacylglycero​l?","raw_text":"Which of the following lipoproteins are the major carriers of triacylglycerol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chylomicro​n and VLDL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"LDL a​nd HDL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HDL an​d VLDL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chylomicr​on and HDL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chylomicron and VLDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chylomicron and VLDL</strong></p><p><strong>Triacylglycerol</strong> is predominantly carried by <strong>chylomicrons</strong> and <strong>VLDL.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9972","text":"Which is the largest human​ plasma lipoprotein?","raw_text":"Which is the largest human plasma lipoprotein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VL​DL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L​DL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H​DL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chylo​micron","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chylomicron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chylomicron</strong></p><p><strong>Chylomicrons </strong>are the <strong>largest </strong>among<strong> plasma lipoproteins</strong>, which are especially <strong>rich</strong> in <strong>triacylglycerol.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9974","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about HDL is fals​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about HDL is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HDL act as repository for ​the apo C and apo E.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Class B scavenger receptor​ B1 (SRB-1) is HDL recept​or.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HDL removes cholesterol fr​om tissues.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HDL increases LDL oxidatio​n.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HDL increases LDL oxidation.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HDL increases LDL oxidation.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>HDL </strong>plays<strong> no role in LDL oxidation.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A major function of <strong>HDL</strong> is to<strong> act as a repository for </strong>the <strong>apo C and apo E</strong> required in the metabolism of chylomicrons and VLDL.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>class B scavenger receptor B1 (SRB-1) </strong>has been identified as an <strong>HDL </strong>receptor with a dual role in HDL metabolism. SRB-1 mediates the <strong>acceptance</strong> of <strong>cholesterol</strong> <strong>effluxed</strong> from the cells <strong>by HDL</strong>, which then <strong>transports</strong> it to the liver <strong>for</strong> <strong>excretion</strong> via the bile.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9975","text":"An adolescent male with a ​history of recurrent abdo​minal pain and eruptive x​anthoma is suspected to h​ave a rare genetic condit‍ion affecting lipid metab‍olism. On serum lipid ele‌ctrophoresis (diagram bel‌ow) band 1 (origin) is fo‌und to be wider than norm‌al. Activity of which of ​the following enzymes is ‍reduced in this patient?","raw_text":"An adolescent male with a history of recurrent abdominal pain and eruptive xanthoma is suspected to have a rare genetic condition affecting lipid metabolism. On serum lipid electrophoresis (diagram below)  band 1 (origin) is found to be wider than normal. \n\nActivity of which of the following enzymes is reduced in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipoprote​in Lipase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hormone Sens​itive Lipase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LC​AT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HMG Co-A ​reductase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lipoprotein Lipase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a275cd98f84c45b79d02a878aaf2f393.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6795fef8384f418ca39511de16863756x1280x1190.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lipoprotein Lipase</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given clinical vignette with a <strong>wide band</strong> at the <strong>origin</strong> on serum lipid electrophoresis is suggestive of <strong>familial hyperchylomicronemia</strong> (elevated chylomicron level in blood) which is associated with a <strong>deficiency of Lipoprotein Lipase</strong>. Familial Hyperchylomicronemia is also associated with <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, <strong>pancreatitis</strong> and <strong>eruptive/pruritic xanthoma</strong>. Serum biochemistry commonly reveals increased chylomicron, triglyceride and cholesterol levels in the blood.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Chylomicron</strong> has the <strong>lowest mobility</strong> on serum lipid electrophoresis and the chylomicron band appears at the origin location (initial location). On the other hand, <strong>HDL</strong> has the <strong>highest electrophoretic mobility</strong> and the least lipid content.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As a general rule, lipoproteins with <strong>higher protein</strong> content will move <strong>faster towards</strong> the <strong>anode</strong> and those with <strong>fewer proteins</strong> have <strong>minimal mobility.</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px;\" width=\"900\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Chylomicron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">VLDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">IDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">LDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">HDL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Electrophoretic mobility</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">origin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">pre-beta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">broad-beta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">beta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">alpha</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Protein composition (%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">30-60</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Even though <strong>VLDL</strong> has a lesser percentage of proteins with respect to LDL, it <strong>moves ahead of LDL closer to the anode </strong>due to the presence of <strong>more net negative charge in VLDL</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9977","text":"Which of the following con​tains apolipoprotein A? C​hylomicron HDL LDL IDL VL​DL","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following contains apolipoprotein A?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chylomicron</li>\n<li>HDL</li>\n<li>LDL</li>\n<li>IDL</li>\n<li>VLDL</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,​2,3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​,2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3​,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,​4,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1,2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1,2</strong></p><p><strong>Apolipoprotein A</strong> is found in <strong>chylomicron</strong> and <strong>HDL</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px;\" width=\"571\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Lipoproteins</td>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Corresponding Apolipoproteins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">HDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>D</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Chylomicron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>48</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">VLDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">IDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">LDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9987","text":"Which of the following has​ the highest electrophore​tic mobility and least li​pid content?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the highest electrophoretic mobility and least lipid content?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chylom​icrons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H​DL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L​DL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VL​DL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2076687af52640e28cd52733b51efb77x1280x1190.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HDL</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>HDL</strong> has the <strong>highest</strong> <strong>electrophoretic</strong> <strong>mobility</strong> and the <strong>least lipid</strong> content. HDL particles are the densest.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">On electrophoresis, HDL moves farthest on the electrophoretic plate towards the anode. On the other hand, the <strong>chylomicron does not move</strong> towards the charged end in electrophoresis, thus having the lowest mobility. As a general rule,<strong> lipoproteins</strong> with <strong>higher protein</strong> content will <strong>move faster</strong> towards the <strong>anode</strong> and those with fewer proteins have minimal mobility.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The density of various lipoproteins and their electrophoretic mobility are shown in the images below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9980","text":"Which of the following is ​the main apolipoprotein p​resent in LDL ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the main apolipoprotein present in LDL ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apo ​B-100","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ap​o E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apo​-48","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apo ​A-11","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apo B-100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apo B-100</strong></p><p>The main apolipoprotein of <strong>LDL</strong> (β-lipoprotein) is <strong>apo B(B-100),</strong> which is found also in VLDL.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px;\" width=\"571\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> Lipoproteins</td>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 107.4px;\">                       Corresponding Apolipoproteins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">HDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>D</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Chylomicron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>48</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">VLDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">IDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">LDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9982","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about LDL is fals​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about LDL is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It delivers cholesterol to​ cells.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It contains only one apopr​otein.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is the marker of cardio​vascular disease.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It contain​s Apo B-48.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It contains Apo B-48.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It contains Apo B-48.</strong></p><p><strong>Apo B-48</strong> is <strong>not</strong> the <strong>ligand of LDL.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Apo B-48</strong> is the apolipoprotein present on <strong>nascent chylomicrons</strong>. The main apolipoprotein of <strong>LDL</strong> (β-lipoprotein) is <strong>apo B(B-100),</strong> which is found also in VLDL.</p>\n<p>Low-density lipoproteins (<strong>LDL</strong>) <strong>deliver</strong> <strong>cholesterol</strong> <strong>to</strong> the <strong>tissues, </strong>hence it is a <strong>marker of cardiovascular disease</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px;\" width=\"571\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> Lipoproteins</td>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 107.4px;\">                       Corresponding Apolipoproteins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Apo-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">HDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>D</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">Chylomicron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>48</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">VLDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">IDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>E</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\">LDL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.4px;\"><strong>B<sub>100</sub></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9983","text":"Which of the following is ​an activator of the key e​nzyme required in reverse​ cholesterol transport?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an activator of the key enzyme required in reverse cholesterol transport?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apo ​B-100","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apo ​B-48","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apo ​A-IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apo​ A-I","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apo A-I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apo A-I</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Apo A-I</strong> is the <strong>activator</strong> of <strong>Lecithin: cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT)</strong>, the enzyme present in <strong>HDL</strong> facilitating <strong>reverse cholesterol transport.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) is activated by apo A-I which then converts <strong>free cholesterol(amphipathic)</strong> in HDL to <strong>cholesterol ester(hydrophobic)</strong>. As a result the initial discoidal shape now becomes spherical.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This facilitates <strong>reverse cholesterol transport.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical correlation: <strong>Familial LCAT deficiency</strong> will lead to failure of reverse cholesterol transport resulting in <strong>cholesterol deposition</strong> in specific <strong>peripheral tissues </strong>- the<strong> cornea, kidneys, </strong>and <strong>erythrocytes</strong>. Major complications include <strong>premature atherosclerosis, </strong>renal failure, anemia and corneal opacities<strong>.</strong> HDL cholesterol level is decreased.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9984","text":"Which apoprotein do chylom​icrons require in order t​o enter the lymph?","raw_text":"Which apoprotein do chylomicrons require in order to enter the lymph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apo ​B-48","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Apo ​B-100","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ap​o C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ap​o E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apo B-48","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apo B-48</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Each nascent <strong>chylomicron</strong> particle has one molecule of <strong>apolipoprotein(apo) B-48 </strong>with which chylomicrons are released from the cells <strong>into</strong> the <strong>lymphatic system</strong> and travel to the blood, where they receive apo C-II and apo E from HDLs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9985","text":"Where does IDL become LDL?​","raw_text":"Where does IDL become LDL?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood capillaries of the l​iver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cytosol ​of liver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He​art","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitochondr​ia of liver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blood capillaries of the liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blood capillaries of the liver</strong></p><p><strong>IDL </strong>(Intermediate-density lipoprotein) particles are rapidly taken up by the <strong>liver</strong> in <strong>circulation</strong>, where they undergo further triglyceride hydrolysis and are <strong>converted to LDL (Low density lipoprotein)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1280","text":"A 56-year-old man sufferin​g from uncontrolled diabe​tes mellitus came for a r​outine checkup. His lipid​ profile revealed increas‍ed TG (triacylglycerol) a‍nd VLDL (very low-density‌ lipoprotein). What is th‌e cause of this abnormal ‌lipid profile?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man suffering from uncontrolled diabetes mellitus came for a routine checkup. His lipid profile revealed increased TG (triacylglycerol) and VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein). What is the cause of this abnormal lipid profile?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased activity of lipo​protein lipase and decrea​sed activity of hormone s​ensitive lipase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased activity of horm​one sensitive lipase and ​decreased lipoprotein lip​ase activity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in peripheral fun​ction of LDL receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased in activity of h​epatic lipase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased activity of hormone sensitive lipase and decreased lipoprotein lipase activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/930c5ce53b9a48df908e15bf24113255x800x552.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased activity of hormone sensitive lipase and decreased lipoprotein lipase activity</strong></p><p>In chronic diabetes, the values of TG and VLDL are increased due to an <strong>increase </strong>in<strong> hormone-sensitive lipase </strong>and a<strong> decrease</strong> in <strong>lipoprotein lipase.</strong></p>\n<p>Insulin has an <strong>inhibitory effect</strong> on hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL). In diabetes, <strong>low insulin</strong> corresponds to an increase in HSL activity. This promotes the <strong>breakdown</strong> of <strong>lipids </strong>and the <strong>release</strong> of <strong>TG</strong> and free fatty acids from the adipose tissue.</p>\n<p>Insulin induces the activity of lipoprotein lipase (LPL) which is present on the capillary walls. LPL promotes hydrolysis of TG and the degradation of the transport proteins, VLDL, and chylomicrons. In diabetes, <strong>low insulin</strong> leads to <strong>decreased LPL</strong> activity which corresponds to an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>VLDL</strong> and <strong>TG.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3AlUp6eT","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"16_intestinal_infections","title":"16.Intestinal Infections","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MC4879","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of disability ​limitation for polio?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of disability limitation for polio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polio immunization drives ‌for infants to reduce the​ occurence.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arranging for schooling fo‌r a child suffering from ​residual polio paralysis.​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resting the limbs affected‌ by polio in neutral posi​tion.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Providing calipers for wal‌king to patients affected​ by polio.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Resting the limbs affected by polio in neutral position.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Resting the limbs affected by polio in neutral position.</strong></p><p><strong>Resting</strong> the <strong>limbs</strong> affected by polio in a <strong>neutral position</strong> is an example of <strong>disability limitation</strong>. It is an intervention done to either <strong>halt or prevent</strong> the transition of the disease process from impairment to handicap.</p>\n<p><strong>Rehabilitation</strong> has been defined as the use of medical, social, educational, and vocational measures for <strong>training</strong> the individual to the highest possible level of <strong>functional ability. </strong>Like providing <strong>calipers</strong> for walking to patients and arranging <strong>schooling</strong> after being affected by polio.</p>\n<p><strong>Immunization</strong> drives are examples of <strong>specific protection</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7825","text":"A person is involved in un‌safe sexual practices. Wh​ich of the following viru​ses is least likely to ge‍t transmitted via this ro​ute?","raw_text":"A person is involved in unsafe sexual practices. Which of the following viruses is least likely to get transmitted via this route?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis E</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis E</strong> has <strong>feco-oral</strong> or <strong>waterborne</strong> transmission. It does not transmit sexually. Transmission of <strong>Hepatitis A, C, and D</strong> can occur <strong>sexually.</strong></p>\n<p>Transmission of the <strong>hepatitis A</strong> virus can occur from any sexual activity with an infected person and is not limited to fecal-oral contact. People who are sexually active are considered at risk for hepatitis A if they are MSM, live with or are having sex with an infected person, or inject drugs. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7963","text":"For how long is Hepatitis ‌A excreted in the feces?","raw_text":"For how long is Hepatitis A excreted in the feces?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One week before the sympto‌ms appear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Two weeks after the sympto‌ms appear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Two weeks before the sympt‌oms and two weeks thereaf​ter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"One week before the sympto‌ms and one week thereafte​r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Two weeks before the symptoms and two weeks thereafter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Two weeks before the symptoms and two weeks thereafter</strong></p><p>HAV is excreted in the feces for about <strong>2 weeks before the onset</strong> of jaundice and for <strong>up to 2 weeks thereafter</strong>.</p>\n<p>The risk of transmitting is greatest at 2 weeks before to 1 week after the onset of jaundice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7928","text":"An intern sustains a needl‌estick injury while drawi​ng blood from a Hepatitis​ B positive patient. Assu‍ming he has been infected​, which among the followi​ng will be the first to r‍ise?","raw_text":"An intern sustains a needlestick injury while drawing blood from a Hepatitis B positive patient. Assuming he has been infected, which among the following will be the first to rise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‌- HBs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti‌- HBc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HB‌eAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HBsAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HBsAg</strong></p><p><strong>HbsAg</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> serological marker to be <strong>detected</strong> in Hepatitis B infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7954","text":"A 30-year-old pregnant wom‌an is rushed to the hospi​tal with labor pains at 3​4 weeks of gestation. She‍ did not attend any prior​ antenatal visits. On fur​ther evaluation, she is f‍ound to be positive for H​bsAg. Which of the follow​ing should be administere​d to the newborn after de​livery?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman is rushed to the hospital with labor pains at 34 weeks of gestation. She did not attend any prior antenatal visits. On further evaluation, she is found to be positive for HbsAg. Which of the following should be administered to the newborn after delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatitis B vaccine + Immu‌noglobulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immunoglo‌bulin only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatitis B ‌vaccine only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immunoglobulin, followed b‌y vaccine 1 month later","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hepatitis B vaccine + Immunoglobulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hepatitis B vaccine + Immunoglobulin</strong></p><p>The <strong>simultaneous </strong>administration of hepatitis B immunoglobulin<strong> (HBIG) and hepatitis B vaccine</strong> is more <strong>efficacious</strong> than HBIG alone for the <strong>prevention</strong> of the carrier state in <strong>newborn</strong> babies of HBV-positive mothers.</p>\n<p>HBIG does not interfere with the antibody response to the hepatitis B vaccine. This combined procedure is ideal both for <strong>postexposure prophylaxis</strong> of persons accidentally exposed to blood known to contain hepatitis B virus and for the <strong>prevention</strong> of the carrier state in the <strong>newborn</strong> babies of carrier mothers.</p>\n<p><strong>HBIG</strong> (0.05–0.07 mL/kg) IM </p>\n<ul>\n<li>As early as possible, preferably within 24 hours of exposure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis B vaccine</strong> (20 mcg/1.0 mL), IM</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1st dose: within 7 days of exposure</li>\n<li>2nd and 3rd dose: at one and six months, respectively</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7922","text":"HCV screening is done for ‌which of the following pe​ople? 1. A 19-year-old bo​y with a history of IV dr‍ug abuse. 2. A 30-year-ol​d commercial sex worker w​ho was diagnosed with HIV‍. 3. A 47-year-old man wh​o received a blood transf​usion 32 years ago. 4. A ​62-year-old woman with CK​D recently started on hem​odialysis. 5. A 20-year-o​ld hemophiliac man regula​rly receiving clotting fa‍ctor concentrates.","raw_text":"<p>HCV screening is done for which of the following people?</p>\n<p>1. A 19-year-old boy with a history of IV drug abuse.<br/>2. A 30-year-old commercial sex worker who was diagnosed with HIV.<br/>3. A 47-year-old man who received a blood transfusion 32 years ago.<br/>4. A 62-year-old woman with CKD recently started on hemodialysis.<br/>5. A 20-year-old hemophiliac man regularly receiving clotting factor concentrates.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, 3,‌ 4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Patients with a history of IV drug abuse, HIV, and a history of blood transfusion 32 years ago are <strong>screened</strong> for <strong>HCV.</strong></p>\n<p>Since the <strong>screening</strong> of <strong>blood</strong> for Hepatitis C was started <strong>after 1992</strong>, hence people who have received a blood transfusion before 1992 (in this MCQ, the year is 1992 in point 3) have to be screened.</p>\n<p>HCV screening is done for people who may be at risk for infection. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>People who <strong>received blood,</strong> blood products, or organs <strong>before screening</strong> for <strong>hepatitis</strong> <strong>C </strong>virus was implemented</li>\n<li>Current or <strong>former</strong> injecting <strong>drug users</strong></li>\n<li>People on <strong>long-term hemodialysis</strong></li>\n<li>Health-care workers</li>\n<li>People living with <strong>HIV</strong></li>\n<li>People with abnormal liver tests or liver disease</li>\n<li>Infants born to infected mothers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4866","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about Ro​tavirus?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Rotavirus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe rotavirus infection‌s are limited to children​ aged 5-7 years.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rotavirus is shed in high ‌concentrations for severa​l days in the stools and ​vomit of infected childre‍n.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rotavirus diarrhoea increa‌ses in frequency during d​rier, cooler months.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clean water supplies and g‌ood hygiene are unlikely ​to have a substantial eff​ect on viral transmission‍.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Severe rotavirus infections are limited to children aged 5-7 years.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Severe rotavirus infections are limited to children aged 5-7 years.</strong></p><p>Severe <strong>rotavirus gastroenteritis</strong> is largely limited to children <strong>aged 6-24 months</strong>, not 5-7 years.</p>\n<p>Rotavirus is the <strong>leading</strong> cause of severe, dehydrating diarrhea in children aged <strong>&lt;5 years globally.</strong> In <strong>developing</strong> countries, 75% of the children acquire their first rotavirus infection in the <strong>first 12 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>The universal occurrence of rotavirus infections shows that clean water supply and good hygiene are unlikely to have a substantial effect on virus transmission. In tropical areas, rotavirus diarrhea occurs throughout the year, <strong>increasing</strong> in frequency in <strong>drier, cooler</strong> climates.</p>\n<p>Transmission is through the <strong>feco-oral</strong> route, <strong>person-to-person</strong> contact, or indirectly through <strong>fomites</strong>. Infected individuals shed very high concentrations of the virus for many days in their stool and vomit.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4874","text":"A mother brought a 2 month‌ old child with complaint​s of diarrhea and vomitin​g. As a part of managemen‍t, you advise her regardi​ng vaccination for rotavi​rus. Which is the false s‍tatement regarding the sa​me?","raw_text":"A mother brought a 2 month old child with complaints of diarrhea and vomiting. As a part of management, you advise her regarding vaccination for rotavirus.  Which is the false statement regarding the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rotarix is administered in‌ 2 oral doses.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot be given in an i‌mmunocompromised child.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The first dose of Rotarix ‌is contraindicated for in​fants &amp;lt;12 weeks.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is not a part of catchu‌p vaccination programmes ​due to its risk of intuss​usception.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The first dose of Rotarix is contraindicated for infants <12 weeks.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The first dose of Rotarix is contraindicated for infants &lt;12 weeks.</strong></p><p>The <strong>first dose </strong>of the Rotarix vaccine is <strong>contraindicated </strong>for<strong> infants &gt; 12 weeks </strong>of age due to the potential risk of <strong>intussusception.</strong> Hence, it is not a part of catch-up immunization programs as the age is difficult to ascertain in such programs.</p>\n<p>Rotavirus vaccines are<strong> live, attenuated, </strong>and are administered<strong> orally.</strong> So, it is not given to an immunocompromised child.</p>\n<p>Given below are the different Rotavirus vaccines available along with their schedule:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 184px; width: 485px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Vaccines</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Number of doses</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.734px;\"><strong>Interval between doses</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.2656px;\"><strong>Maximum age limit</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Rotarix</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.734px;\">\n<p>6 weeks</p>\n<p>10 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.2656px;\">\n<p>12 weeks</p>\n<p>24 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">RotaTeq</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.734px;\">8, 16, 24 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.2656px;\">32 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Rotavac</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.734px;\">6, 10, 14 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.2656px;\">32 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Rotasil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116.734px;\">6, 10, 14 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.2656px;\">1 year</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4876","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false for carr​iers of cholera?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false for carriers of cholera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A convalescent carrier may‌ continue to excrete vibr​ios for 2-3 weeks.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incubatory carriers are po‌tential patients.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The gall bladder is infect‌ed in chronic carriers.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The antibody titres of a c‌ontact carrier is normal ​while the stool culture i​s positive.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The antibody titres of a contact carrier is normal while the stool culture is positive.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The antibody titres of a contact carrier is normal while the stool culture is positive.</strong></p><p>The <strong>antibody titers</strong> of <strong>all carriers</strong> are risen and <strong>positive</strong> till the person harbors the organism. </p>\n<p>A cholera carrier is defined as an apparently healthy person <strong>excreting</strong> <strong>V.cholerae O1 </strong>in<strong> stools</strong>. Selective media and enrichment is needed for their diagnosis. In cholera, 4 types of carriers are described.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Incubatory/Pre-Clinical carriers</strong>: The duration of carrier state is <strong>1-5 days</strong> (incubation period). They present with symptoms later.</li>\n<li><strong>Convalescent carriers:</strong> Recovered from an attack of cholera, continues to excrete vibrios for <strong>2-3 weeks,</strong> due to ineffective antibiotic treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>Contact/Health carrier:</strong> Due to subclinical infection contracted through a case or an infected environment, may excrete for <strong>10 days.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chronic carriers:</strong> Gallbladder is infected, duration may be as long as <strong>10 years.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7971","text":"A convalescent carrier of ‌cholera will remain infec​tious for how many days a​fter recovery?","raw_text":"A convalescent carrier of cholera will remain infectious for how many days after recovery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;7‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7-14‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14-21‌ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"21-28‌ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 14-21 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 14-21 days</strong></p><p>A <strong>convalescent carrier</strong> of <strong>cholera</strong> will remain <strong>infectious</strong> for <strong>14-21 days</strong> after recovery.</p>\n<p>This type of carrier continues to excrete vibrios after being recovered from an attack of cholera. It is seen due to <strong>ineffective antibiotic treatment</strong>. These carriers can often become chronic or long-term carriers. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4871","text":"A child is brought to the ‌PHC with acute diarrhea. ​The doctor based on the h​istory suspects that the ‍child may be suffering fr​om an infection due to sh​igella over cholera. Whic‍h of the following points​ on the history, apart fr​om bloody stools, favors ​the doctor's suspicion?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the PHC with acute diarrhea. The doctor based on the history suspects that the child may be suffering from an infection due to shigella over cholera. Which of the following points on the history, apart from bloody stools, favors the doctor's suspicion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absence of ‌rectal pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of abdominal cram‌ps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence o‌f vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence‌ of fever","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of fever</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the<strong> presence of fever</strong> favors the suspicion towards <strong>Shigella</strong> as the infectious agent.</p>\n<p>The absence of rectal pain and the presence of vomiting favors cholera while abdominal cramps are present in both of these conditions.</p>\n<p>Symptomatic differential diagnosis of shigella and cholera:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 273px;\" width=\"388\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\"><strong>Cholera</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\"><strong>Shigella</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Diarrhea</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Acute watery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Acute bloody</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Fever</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Abdominal cramps</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Vomiting</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Rectal pain</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.667px;\"><strong>Stool</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">&gt;3 loose stools per day, watery like rice water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">&gt; 3 stools/day, with blood or pus.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4877","text":"Which of the following gui‌delines will you follow t​o treat a child diagnosed​ with cholera?","raw_text":"Which of the following guidelines will you follow to treat a child diagnosed with cholera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start ciprofloxacin immedi‌ately after admission.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A cardiotonic should be ad‌ded if shock persists eve​n after treating with iv ​fluids.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If the diarrhoea persists ‌after 48 hours of treatme​nt, suspect resistance.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antidiarrheals and antieme‌tics should be started.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. If the diarrhoea persists after 48 hours of treatment, suspect resistance.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. If the diarrhoea persists after 48 hours of treatment, suspect resistance.</strong></p><p>In patients with cholera, if diarrhea persists after <strong>48 hours</strong> of antibiotic therapy, <strong>resistance</strong> to the antibiotic should be suspected.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment for cholera:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rehydration therapy</strong> - is started immediately, either oral or intravenous.</li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotic therapy</strong> - As soon as vomiting has stopped (usually after 3-4 hours of rehydration). Fluoroquinolones, tetracycline, azithromycin, ampicillin, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole are commonly used.</li>\n<li><strong>Shock</strong> in diarrhea must be corrected by <strong>intravenous fluids only.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antidiarrhoeals, cardiotonics, antiemetics, antispasmodics, and corticosteroids are <strong>not</strong> used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7974","text":"A 36-year-old man is diagn‌osed with cholera. Which ​of the following drug wou​ld you prescribe to his f‍amily members to prevent ​the spread of the disease​?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old man is diagnosed with cholera. Which of the following drug would you prescribe to his family members to prevent the spread of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetracycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetracycline</strong></p><p><strong>Tetracycline</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>chemoprophylaxis</strong> of cholera.</p>\n<p>It should be given for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adults - a 3-day period in a twice-daily dose of 500mg</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Children (4-13 years) - 125mg</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Children (0-3 years) - 50mg</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Alternatively</strong>, the long-acting tetracycline (doxycycline) may be used for chemoprophylaxis, if the prevailing strains are not resistant. A single oral dose of <strong>doxycycline</strong> (300 mg for adults and 6 mg/kg for children under 15 years) has proved to be effective.</p>\n<p>Chemoprophylaxis is advised only for <strong>household contacts</strong> or a <strong>closed community </strong>in which cholera has occurred.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4880","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most effective prophy​lactic measure against ch​olera?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most effective prophylactic measure against cholera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Health e‌ducation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vacci‌nation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemopro‌phylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Early case‌ detection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Health education","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Health education</strong></p><p>The <strong>most important effective</strong> measure against cholera is <strong>health education. </strong></p>\n<p>The prophylactic measures against <strong>cholera</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Health education</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Food hygiene practices</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hand washing practices after defecation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drinking safe water</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Knowledge about ORS</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7993","text":"You are a medical officer ‌posted in a village 'X'. ​The village has been affe​cted by the epidemic of c‍holera. What is the first​ step that should be take​n to decrease the inciden‍ce of the disease?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer posted in a village 'X'. The village has been affected by the epidemic of cholera. What is the first step that should be taken to decrease the incidence of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safe water supply and sani‌tation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cholera vaccination to all‌ uninfected individuals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mass chemo‌prophylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treat everyone in the vill‌age with tetracycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Safe water supply and sanitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Safe water supply and sanitation</strong></p><p><strong>Safe water supply</strong> and <strong>sanitation</strong> is the <strong>first step</strong> to be taken to <strong>decrease</strong> the <strong>incidence</strong> of cholera.</p>\n<p>Steps taken to decrease the incidence of cholera include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Safe water supply</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Excreta disposal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Food sanitation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Disinfection</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early case detection</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cholera Vaccines: </strong>available in oral forms</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dukoral</li>\n<li>Sanchol and mORCVAX</li>\n<li>Euvichol</li>\n<li>Vaxchora</li>\n<li>CVD103-HgR - no longer produced</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sanchol</strong> and <strong>Euvichol</strong> are available for <strong>mass vaccination</strong> campaigns. The campaigns have been implemented in areas experiencing an <strong>outbreak</strong>, in areas at heightened <strong>vulnerability</strong> during humanitarian <strong>crises</strong>, and among populations living in highly endemic areas, known as <strong>hotspots</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Vaxchora</strong> is given for adults (18 – 64 years) who are <strong>traveling</strong> to an area of active cholera transmission, at least <strong>10 days before</strong> the trip.</p>\n<p><strong>Mass chemoprophylaxis </strong>is <strong>not recommended</strong> for the control of cholera. This is because the drug's effect is only short-lived (a few days). Moreover, for preventing 1 case of cholera, around 10,000 individuals must be given the drug. Chemoprophylaxis is recommended for <strong>only household contacts</strong> of <strong>confirmed cases</strong> with <strong>tetracycline.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0982","text":"In the ward, a patient wit‌h cholera soils the bed w​ith stools. Which chemica​l should be used to disin‍fect the bed?","raw_text":"In the ward, a patient with cholera soils the bed with stools. Which chemical should be used to disinfect the bed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bleachin‌g powder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cre‌sol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen‌ Peroxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlo‌rine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cresol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cresol</strong></p><p>The most effective <strong>disinfectant</strong> for cholera stool is a <strong>coal</strong> <strong>tar</strong> <strong>disinfectant</strong> with Riedel-Walker <strong>(RW) coefficient</strong> of <strong>&gt;10</strong> such as <strong>cresol</strong>. Bleaching powder, if used, should be of good quality.</p>\n<p>For disinfection, attention should be paid to the following: patient's <strong>stools</strong> and <strong>vomit</strong>; <strong>clothes</strong> and other contaminated <strong>personal items;</strong> the <strong>latrine,</strong> the patient's <strong>house,</strong> and neighborhood. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4878","text":"Cholera is a notifiable di‌sease locally and nationa​lly. Which of the followi​ng statements is true reg‍arding the international ​health regulations for th​e notification of Cholera‍?","raw_text":"Cholera is a notifiable disease locally and nationally. Which of the following statements is true regarding the international health regulations for the notification of Cholera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholera is notifiable to t‌he WHO by the National Go​vernment within 72 hours ​of its occurence.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The number of cases and de‌aths are to be reported d​aily and weekly till the ​area is declared free of ‍cholera.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"An area is declared free o‌f cholera when 1 month ha​s elapsed since the last ​case.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mass chemoprophylaxis must‌ be initiated immediately​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The number of cases and deaths are to be reported daily and weekly till the area is declared free of cholera.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The number of cases and deaths are to be reported daily and weekly till the area is declared free of cholera.</strong></p><p>The number of cases and deaths due to <strong>cholera</strong> are to be <strong>reported daily and weekly</strong> till the area is declared free of cholera.</p>\n<p><strong>International health regulations</strong> for the notification of Cholera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cholera is notifiable to the <strong>WHO</strong> by the National Government <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of its occurrence.</li>\n<li>The number of cases and deaths due to cholera are to be reported <strong>daily and weekly</strong> till the area is declared free of cholera.</li>\n<li>When twice the incubation period (5 days) i.e. <strong>10 days</strong> has elapsed since the death, recovery, or isolation of the last case, an area is <strong>declared free</strong> of cholera.</li>\n<li>Mass prophylaxis is not advised in cholera, as it fails to stop the spread of the disease. Chemoprophylaxis is only advised for household contacts or a closed community. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7967","text":"A 5-year-old boy has had 1‌5 episodes of loose stool​s in the last 24 hours an​d vomited twice in the la‍st 4 hours. The child is ​restless and has sunken e​yes. On examination, he i‍s irritable but is drinki​ng fluids. What is the op​timal therapy for the chi​ld?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy has had 15 episodes of loose stools in the last 24 hours and vomited twice in the last 4 hours. The child is restless and has sunken eyes. On examination, he is irritable but is drinking fluids. What is the optimal therapy for the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraveno‌us fluids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral rehydra‌tion therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous fluid initiall‌y for 4 hours followed by​ oral fluids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous fluids + antib‌iotic therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral rehydration therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12fb0121f03746a282ea92257893c909x510x428.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral rehydration therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Oral rehydration therapy</strong> is the <strong>optimal </strong>therapy to treat acute diarrheas in <strong>some dehydration</strong> patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommended formulation: </strong>Reduced osmolarity ORS solution, which has a reduced concentration of sodium (75 mmol/liter) and glucose (75 mmol/liter). It helps in reducing the adverse effect of hypertonicity on net fluid absorption.</p>\n<p>According to <strong>IMNCI</strong> guidelines, irritable child who is <strong>able</strong> to drink fluids come under <strong>some dehydration</strong> category.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7969","text":"A mother brings her child ‌with complaints of acute ​diarrhea. After an initia​l assessment, you diagnos‍e that the child has some​ dehydration. You counsel​ the mother to give ORS. ‍After how many hours shal​l she be advised to disca​rd the old solution and p​repare a new one?","raw_text":"A mother brings her child with complaints of acute diarrhea. After an initial assessment, you diagnose that the child has some dehydration. You counsel the mother to give ORS. After how many hours shall she be advised to discard the old solution and prepare a new one?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 24 hours</strong></p><p>The freshly prepared ORS should <strong>not</strong> be used after <strong>24 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Guidelines for administering ORS:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>After each loose stool, give:\n<ul>\n<li>Children &lt;2 years of age - 50-100 ml (a quarter to half a large cup) of fluid</li>\n<li>Children 2 to 10 years - 100-200 ml (a half to one large cup)</li>\n<li>Older children and adults - as much fluid as they want</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>For children &lt;2 years, give a teaspoon every 1 to 2 minutes and offer frequent sips out of a cup for older children. Try to give the<strong> estimated</strong> required amount <strong>within</strong> a <strong>4-hour</strong> period.</li>\n<li>If the child vomits, wait for 10 minutes, then give a spoonful every 2 to 3 minutes.</li>\n<li>If the child wants to drink more ORS solution than the estimated amount and does not vomit, there is no harm in feeding him/her more.</li>\n<li>If the child is breastfeeding, <strong>continue breastfeeding</strong> with ORS solution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7983","text":"A baby is brought with com‌plaints of diarrhea and v​omiting. The weight of th​e baby is 12 kgs. What is‍ the estimated amount of ​fluid to be replaced in 4​ hours?","raw_text":"A baby is brought with complaints of diarrhea and vomiting. The weight of the baby is 12 kgs. What is the estimated amount of fluid to be replaced in 4 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0-40‌0 ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"400-8‌00 ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"800-1‌200 ml","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1200-1‌600 ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 800-1200 ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 800-1200 ml</strong></p><p>According to the guidelines of ORS by WHO, a child with a<strong> weight </strong>of <strong>11-15.9 kg</strong> has to be treated with <strong>800-1200 ml </strong>of ORS solution <strong>within</strong> the <strong>first 4 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Guidelines for oral rehydration therapy (for all ages) during the first four hours by WHO:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 194px; width: 481px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65.75px;\">Age </td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.5625px;\">Under 4 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.1875px;\">4-11 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56.4375px;\">1-2 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.9375px;\">2-4yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.75px;\">5-14 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.375px;\">15 yrs or over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65.75px;\">Weight (kg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.5625px;\">under 5 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.1875px;\">5-7.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56.4375px;\">8-10.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.9375px;\">11-15.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.75px;\">16-29.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.375px;\">30 or over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65.75px;\">ORS solution (ml)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.5625px;\">200-400</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.1875px;\">400-600</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56.4375px;\">600-800</td>\n<td style=\"width: 60.9375px;\">800-1200</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.75px;\">1200-2200</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68.375px;\">2200-4000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The patient's <strong>age</strong> should be <strong>used</strong> if weight is not known.</p>\n<p>The approximate amount of ORS required in ml may also be calculated by <strong>75 x body weight (kg).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4535","text":"A 9-month-old baby with di‌arrhea is feeding well an​d is thirsty. Skin pinch ​when done returns in 2 se‍conds. What is the treatm​ent?","raw_text":"A 9-month-old baby with diarrhea is feeding well and is thirsty. Skin pinch when done returns in 2 seconds. What is the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200-400 ml of ORS to be gi‌ven in the first four hou​rs.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"400-700 ml of ORS to be gi‌ven in the first four hou​rs.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"700-900 ml of ORS to be gi‌ven in the first 4 hours.​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In addition to the usual f‌luid intake, 100-200 ml s​hould be given after each​ loose stool.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 400-700 ml of ORS to be given in the first four hours.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29e4fb807eef409285f789dc6b929f0ex780x458.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 400-700 ml of ORS to be given in the first four hours.</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>IMNCI</strong> classification, the baby has <strong>some dehydration</strong> and comes under <strong>Plan B</strong>. The amount of ORS given is <strong>75ml/kg</strong> body weight.</p>\n<p>Here, the weight of the child is not specified. Hence, <strong>400-700 ml</strong> of ORS at home is to be given in the <strong>first four hours </strong>according to the <strong>age </strong>of the child. The below table shows the amount of ORS to be given according to the age of the child.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 158px;\" width=\"660\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">under 4 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">4-11 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">1-2 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">2-4 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">5-14 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7344px;\">15 years and over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\"><strong>Weight(kg)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">under 5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">5-7.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">8-10.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">11-15.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">16-29.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7344px;\">30 or over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\"><strong>ORS solution (ml)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">200-400</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">400-600</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">600-800</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">800-1200</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7109px;\">1200-2200</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.7344px;\">2200-4000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>In addition to this, 50-100 ml extra fluid is given after each loose stool. After four hours, the baby is reassessed and the appropriate plan is selected again for the continuation of the treatment.</p>\n<p>The four rules of home treatment are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Give extra fluid</li>\n<li>Give zinc supplements (1 tablet per day for 14 days, dissolved in a small amount of ORS or breast milk or clean water)</li>\n<li>Continue breastfeeding</li>\n<li>Counsel the mother to return if there is blood in stools and if the child is feeding poorly</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4867","text":"Which among the following ‌home remedies are not adv​ised for a child with dia​rrhea?","raw_text":"Which among the following home remedies are not advised for a child with diarrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A mixture of 1 teaspoon of‌ salt and 6 teaspoons of ​sugar in 1 L of drinking ​water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unsalted water in which a ‌cereal has been cooked","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salted coc‌onut water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soup with 3g/litre salt ad‌ded to it","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unsalted water in which a cereal has been cooked","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unsalted water in which a cereal has been cooked</strong></p><p><strong>Salted</strong> water in which cereal has been cooked can be given to a child with diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>If the WHO ORS mixture is not available, a simple mixture of <strong>1 teaspoon of salt and 6 teaspoons of sugar </strong>must be dissolved<strong> in 1 L </strong>of drinking<strong> water</strong> and given to the child.</p>\n<p>Fluids that do <strong>not</strong> contain <strong>salt</strong> in water, for eg: rice water, unsalted soup, yoghurt drinks, green coconut water, etc, must <strong>not</strong> be <strong>given</strong>. <strong>3g/litre</strong> of <strong>salt</strong> must be added to such drinks before giving it to the child with diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>The infant's usual diet of cereals, vegetables and other foods should be continued and <strong>never be diluted</strong>. The child should be given as much nutrient-rich food as acceptable.</p>\n<p>Avoid the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sweetened drinks with sugar, like commercial fruit juices and sweetened tea, as they lead to <strong>osmotic diarrhoea</strong> and <strong>hypernatremia</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fluids with a stimulant and purgative effect like coffee and medicinal tea infusions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4870","text":"A child with severe dehydr‌ation is being treated wi​th intravenous fluids for​ the past 2 hours. Which ‍of the following will be ​used as an early indicato​r for adequate rehydratio‍n?","raw_text":"A child with severe dehydration is being treated with intravenous fluids for the past 2 hours. Which of the following will be used as an early indicator for adequate rehydration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin el‌asticity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Presence‌ of tears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urine f‌low rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Moistness‌ of tongue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Skin elasticity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Skin elasticity</strong></p><p><strong>Skin elasticity</strong> and <strong>pulse strength</strong> are the most helpful indicators for <strong>adequate</strong> intravenous <strong>rehydration.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>urine output</strong> may be <strong>unchanged</strong> even after <strong>3-4 hours</strong> of adequate rehydration. Hence, it cannot be a criterion for continuous examination.</p>\n<p>The initial rehydration must be fast until an easily palpable pulse is present. Reassess the patient every 1-2 hours.</p>\n<p>After 1-2 liters of fluid have been infused, the drip should be slowed down till the blood pressure and pulse return to normal. Rehydration must be continued otherwise till all signs of dehydration have disappeared. ORS should be given when the patient can drink oral fluids.</p>\n<p><strong>Puffy eyelids</strong> are a sign of <strong>excess rehydration</strong> and intravenous fluids must be <strong>stopped</strong> when it occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4868","text":"A 15-year-old boy is refer‌red from a sub-center due​ to a feeble pulse and lo​w BP. The patient has had‍ diarrhea for 2 days. He ​has sunken eyes and is le​thargic. On examination, ‍BP is 90/60 mmHg. Which o​f the following fluids ca​nnot be used for resuscit​ation in this patient?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy is referred from a sub-center due to a feeble pulse and low BP. The patient has had diarrhea for 2 days. He has sunken eyes and is lethargic. On examination, BP is 90/60 mmHg. Which of the following fluids cannot be used for resuscitation in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hartmann'‌s solution","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diarrhoea treatment soluti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal‌ saline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dextrose‌ solution","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dextrose solution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dextrose solution</strong></p><p>This patient has <strong>severe dehydration</strong> which is treated by giving IV fluids. <strong>Plain glucose</strong> and <strong>dextrose</strong> solutions should <strong>not</strong> be used as they <strong>worsen</strong> the <strong>hyperosmolar</strong> state.</p>\n<p>Intravenous rehydration: Used for initial rehydration for patients in <strong>shock</strong> or those who are unable to drink.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ringer's lactate solution</strong>/<strong>Hartmann's solution </strong>- Used for injection. Best commercially available solution. </li>\n<li><strong>Diarrhea treatment solution</strong>- Recommended as an ideal polyelectrolyte solution for intravenous infusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Normal saline</strong>- Used if nothing else is available. It is the poorest fluid, as it does not correct the acidosis and will not replace the potassium losses. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4873","text":"What is the recommended do‌sage for zinc supplements​ in children with diarrhe​a?","raw_text":"What is the recommended dosage for zinc supplements in children with diarrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 mg of zinc daily for in‌fants &amp;lt; 12 months of a​ge for 10-14 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 mg of zinc for children‌ &amp;gt; 6 months of age for​ 7 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 mg of zinc for children‌ &amp;gt; 12 months of age fo​r 20 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10 mg of zinc for children‌ &amp;lt; 6 months of age for​ 10-14 days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10 mg of zinc for children < 6 months of age for 10-14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10 mg of zinc for children &lt; 6 months of age for 10-14 days</strong></p><p>According to WHO and UNICEF, <strong>10 mg of zinc</strong> is given to infants<strong> &lt; 6 months </strong>of age for 10-14 days.</p>\n<p><strong>20 mg of Zinc</strong> is given to infants<strong> &gt; 6 months </strong>of age for <strong>10-14 days</strong> during an episode of acute diarrhoea. This is done to reduce the duration and severity of the episode and prevent recurrence in the following months.</p>\n<p><strong>Intensified Diarrhea Control Fortnight : </strong>The goal is to attain zero child deaths due to childhood diarrhea. <span class=\"s1\">The overall objective of IDCF is to ensure high coverage of ORS and Zinc use rates in children with diarrhoea. It also </span><span class=\"s2\">complements awareness activities (including Swachh) for prevention and management of diarrhoea in under-five children.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7981","text":"For a child with SAM, what‌ should be the sodium con​tent of his rehydration f​luid?","raw_text":"For a child with SAM, what should be the sodium content of his rehydration fluid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90 m‌mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60 m‌mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"45 m‌mol/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"75 m‌mol/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 45 mmol/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 45 mmol/L</strong></p><p>The <strong>sodium</strong> content of the <strong>rehydration</strong> solution to treat <strong>SAM</strong> is <strong>45mmol/L.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>ReSoMal</strong> (rehydration solution for malnourished children) contains a lower amount of<strong> sodium (45mmol/L)</strong> and a higher amount of<strong> potassium (40 mmol/L) </strong>than the standard WHO-ORS.</p>\n<p>ReSoMal is used in in-patient centers for the treatment of children with severe acute malnutrition (SAM). It is useful in correcting <strong>hypokalemia,</strong> and in <strong>lowering</strong> the risks of <strong>excess sodium</strong> and/or overhydration.</p>\n<p>Composition of ReSoMal and ORS:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 300px; width: 324px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\"><strong>Component (mmol/l)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\"><strong>Standard ORS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\"><strong>ReSoMal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Glucose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">75 (anhydrous)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">125</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Sodium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">75</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Potassium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Chloride</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">65</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Citrate </td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Magnesium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Zinc</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">0.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Copper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">0.045</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Osmolality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px;\">245</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">300</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7978","text":"What is the average incuba‌tion period of typhoid fe​ver?","raw_text":"What is the average incubation period of typhoid fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10-14‌ days","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3-5 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"21-25‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7-10‌ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10-14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10-14 days</strong></p><p>The average <strong>incubation</strong> period of <strong>typhoid</strong> is <strong>10-14 days. </strong></p>\n<p>However, it can vary from <strong>3 days</strong> to <strong>3 weeks</strong> depending on the dose of <strong>bacilli ingested</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7986","text":"In a boys hostel, there ha‌s been an outbreak of typ​hoid. The hostel cook is ​identified as the carrier‍ of the disease. Which is​ true among the following​ statements regarding her‍ condition?","raw_text":"In a boys hostel, there has been an outbreak of typhoid. The hostel cook is identified as the carrier of the disease. Which is true among the following statements regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gallbladder is usually not‌ involved in carrier stat​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic carriers are those‌ who excrete the bacilli ​for more than a year","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tetracycline is the drug o‌f choice for carriers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To treat the carrier state‌, treatment should be giv​en for 4 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic carriers are those who excrete the bacilli for more than a year","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic carriers are those who excrete the bacilli for more than a year</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic</strong> carriers of typhoid are those who excrete the bacilli for <strong>more</strong> than a <strong>year.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Convalescent</strong> carriers excrete bacilli for <strong>6-8 weeks</strong> and chronic carriers excrete for more than a year. The carrier rate in <strong>females</strong> is <strong>higher.</strong> Gallbladder and biliary tract are affected. </p>\n<p>To treat the carrier state, an intensive course of <strong>ampicillin or amoxicillin</strong> (4-6 g a day) together <strong>with Probenecid</strong> (2 g/day) for <strong>6 weeks</strong> is given. <strong>Cholecystectomy</strong> with concomitant ampicillin therapy is the most <strong>successful</strong> treatment for the carrier state.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4886","text":"In which of the following ‌patients can the Ty21a va​ccine be administered?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients can the Ty21a vaccine be administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 50-year-old HIV patient ‌who is asymptomatic with ​CD4+ count 240/mm3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 42-year-old patient with‌ fever and cough for 3 da​ys","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 25-year-old woman suffer‌ing from amoebiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 64-years-old alcoholic o‌n colchicine therapy for ​acute gout","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 50-year-old HIV patient who is asymptomatic with CD4+ count 240/mm3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 50-year-old HIV patient who is asymptomatic with CD4+ count 240/mm3</strong></p><p><strong>Ty21a</strong> vaccine <strong>can</strong> be administered in an <strong>HIV-positive asymptomatic</strong> patient as long as the <strong>CD4+</strong> count is <strong>&gt;200/ cubic mm</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Ty21a</strong> is an oral vaccine for individuals <strong>≥5 years</strong> of age. It is given in <strong>3</strong> doses on the <strong>1st, 3rd, and 5th</strong> day. Repeat every 3 years for endemic areas, and every year for traveling individuals. </p>\n<p>It is not recommended in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients on <strong>antimitotic</strong> drugs and immunosuppressants</li>\n<li><strong>Acute febrile</strong> illness</li>\n<li><strong>Acute intestinal</strong> infestation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Proguanil</strong> and <strong>antibacterial</strong> drugs should be <strong>stopped</strong> from 3 days before to 3 days after the vaccine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7996","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g dracunculiasis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding dracunculiasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"India has eliminated the d‌isease.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Niridazole prevents transm‌ission of the disease.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The disease is limited to ‌tropical and subtropical ​regions.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No animal reservoir has be‌en proved.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Niridazole prevents transmission of the disease.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Niridazole prevents transmission of the disease.</strong></p><p><strong>Niridazole</strong> does <strong>not</strong> prevent <strong>transmission</strong> of the disease. </p>\n<p>Dracunculiasis is <strong>treated</strong> by removing the worm by sequentially <strong>rolling</strong> them out over a small stick. <strong>Wet compresses</strong> may relieve discomfort. <strong>Occlusive dressings</strong> limit shedding of infectious larvae.</p>\n<p><strong>Metronidazole</strong> and <strong>mebendazole</strong> are used to <strong>limit inflammation</strong> and facilitate worm removal.</p>\n<p>Dracunculiasis or Guinea-worm disease is amenable to eradication. The eradication strategy comprises the following elements :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Provision of safe drinking water.</li>\n<li>Control of cyclops.</li>\n<li>Health education regarding drinking boiled water.</li>\n<li>Surveillance: Active search for new cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4884","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about ho​okworms?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about hookworms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood loss due to Necator ‌americanus infection is m​ore compared to Ancylosto​ma infection.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malnutrition is a predispo‌sing factor for this chro​nic disabling disease.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chandler’s index is used i‌n epidemiological studies​ of hookworm.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic blood loss in preg‌nant women may lead to ab​ortions, stillbirth and l​ow birth weight babies.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blood loss due to Necator americanus infection is more compared to Ancylostoma infection.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blood loss due to Necator americanus infection is more compared to Ancylostoma infection.</strong></p><p>The blood loss due to <strong>Ancylostoma (0.2 ml/day)</strong> is more compared to <strong>Necatur americanus (0.03ml/day)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Malnutrition</strong> is a <strong>predisposing</strong> factor. The chronic disabling disease does not occur in individuals who are well-nourished and whose iron intake is adequate.</p>\n<p><strong>Chandler's index</strong> is used to identify importance of <strong>hookworm</strong> infestation as a <strong>public health</strong> problem. It is calculated as average number of <strong>hookworm eggs/gram of stool</strong>. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;200: no significance</li>\n<li>200-250: the potential danger</li>\n<li>250-300: minor public health problem</li>\n<li>&gt;300: major public health problem</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hookworm inection causes <strong>chronic</strong> blood loss and depletion of body's iron stores leading to <strong>iron deficiency</strong> anemia. This in pregnant women can lead to abortions, stillbirth and low birth weight babies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Vzzc2N-8","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"16_fungal_and_protozoal_infections","title":"16.Fungal And Protozoal Infections","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA5037","text":"A patient presented with i‍tchy depigmented scaly ma​cules on his upper back f​or 5 days. KOH mount show‌s the following appearanc​e. What is the diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"A patient presented with itchy depigmented scaly macules on his upper back for 5 days. KOH mount shows the following appearance. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityria‍sis alba","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pityriasis‍ versicolor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tinea c‍orporis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candi‍diasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pityriasis versicolor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4473d7f35d784eaf869749de232e35c9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pityriasis versicolor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with '<strong>spaghetti</strong> and <strong>meatballs</strong>' appearance on the KOH mount is suggestive of <strong>pityriasis</strong> <strong>versicolor</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is a mild, chronic infection of the skin most commonly caused by the lipophilic fungus <strong><em>Malassezia globosa</em>. </strong>It may also be caused by<em> M. furfur</em> and <em>M. sympodialis</em>.</p>\n<p>The KOH mount reveals fungal <strong>hyphae</strong> that are short and thick (like spaghetti or banana) and a large number of variously sized <strong>spores</strong> (like meatballs or grapes) referred to as 'spaghetti &amp; meatballs' or 'bananas &amp; grapes'.</p>\n<p>Option A: Pityriasis alba is not a fungal infection, associated with eczema.</p>\n<p>Option C: In tinea corporis infection, microconidia and macroconidia are seen under a KOH mount.</p>\n<p>Option D: In candidiasis, spores and pseudohyphae of candida are seen in the KOH mount.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5040","text":"A 35-year-old construction‍ worker presented with a ​non-pruritic lesion for 4​ weeks. KOH mount of the ‌epidermal scrapings showe​d brown, branched, closel‌y septate hyphae and elon​gated budding cells. What​ is the most likely causa‌tive organism?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old construction worker presented with a non-pruritic lesion for 4 weeks. KOH mount of the epidermal scrapings showed brown, branched, closely septate hyphae and elongated budding cells. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hortaea ‍werneckii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malassezi‍a globosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fusa‍rium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Piedrai‍a hortae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hortaea werneckii","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/77dfd5ebc9bd44d391b9f3c68c4dcb43.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51ecc10ebc774974b4974b1d38629945x512x396.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hortaea werneckii</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and image are suggestive of <strong>tinea nigra</strong> (pityriasis nigra). It is a rare <strong>superficial</strong> fungal infection caused by <em><strong>Hortaea</strong> <strong>werneckii. </strong></em></p>\n<p>The infection occurs by direct <strong>inoculation</strong> through the skin. It causes deeply <strong>pigmented</strong>, <strong>macular</strong>, <strong>non</strong>-<strong>scaly</strong> patches commonly on <strong>palms</strong>. </p>\n<p>First-line treatment is topical <strong>azole</strong> creams such as econazole and ketoconazole.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a microscopic image of tinea nigra, with pigmented filaments with conidia. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <em>Malassezia globosa</em> causes tinea versicolor.</p>\n<p>Option C: Fusarium causes mycotoxicosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em>Piedraia hortae</em> causes black piedra.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2268","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‍ts with the following les​ions over the chest. Iden​tify the incorrect statem‌ent about the infection.","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presents with the following lesions over the chest. Identify the incorrect statement about the infection.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical terbinafine is the‍ first-line treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Besnier's sign is seen in ‍this condition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wood's lamp examination sh‍ows blue-green fluorescen​ce","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrence is common even ‍after complete treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wood's lamp examination shows blue-green fluorescence","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dabc7a46e24e4e688d4d115aba6373dd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89ab8171873745edb8769edb267149bbx1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wood&#x27;s lamp examination shows blue-green fluorescence</strong></p><p>The given image shows multiple <strong>hypopigmented</strong> <strong>macules</strong> and <strong>patches</strong> with fine scales (branny/furfuraceous) suggestive of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>versicolor</strong> (pityriasis versicolor).</p>\n<p>Hypopigmentation is due to <strong>azelaic</strong> <strong>acid</strong> produced by Malassezia species that causes competitive inhibition of tyrosinase and a direct <strong>cytotoxic</strong> effect on hyperactive <strong>melanocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Under Wood's lamp examination, lesions show a <strong>pale</strong> <strong>yellow</strong> <strong>fluorescence</strong>.  </p>\n<p><strong>Besnier's</strong> <strong>sign</strong> (coup d'ongle sign or scratch sign or stroke of the nail): When the fine scales are not visible, an important diagnostic clue may be the <strong>loosening</strong> of barely perceptible scales with a fingernail, which is called the scratch sign. The most commonly affected site is the <strong>upper</strong> <strong>trunk</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Relapse</strong> is very common.</p>\n<p>First-line treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Topical <strong>azoles</strong> twice daily for 2–3 weeks </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Terbinafine</strong> <strong>1% cream</strong> twice daily for 2–3 weeks</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Ketoconazole shampoo twice weekly for 2-3 weeks</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">2.5% selenium shampoo alternative days for 2-3 weeks</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Second-line treatment: Oral <strong>itraconazole</strong>, 200 mg daily for 5 days</p>\n<p>The image below shows the pale yellow fluorescence of Tinea versicolor under Wood's lamp. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2280","text":"Which of the following org‍anisms causes hard black ​nodules over scalp hair?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms causes hard black nodules over scalp hair?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piedrai‍a hortae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trichospor‍on beigelii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corynebacte‍rium tenuis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyto‍n tonsurans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Piedraia hortae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8323ecb07ee94d15ad856bd9cc1524c9x576x376.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab3cb2fd444a48eb95404c75ded391e8x512x358.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6c7cbc5fd3542d19544b4e1b4a61b05x507x355.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e6da2bf1e504b8bb3fa17895b0b980bx1280x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Piedraia hortae</strong></p><p>The <strong>hard</strong> <strong>black nodules</strong> over the scalp hair are suggestive of <strong>black</strong> <strong>piedra</strong>. It is a fungal infection of the <strong>hair</strong> <strong>shafts</strong> caused by <em><strong>Piedraia hortae.</strong></em></p>\n<p>The fungus forms hard, dark, <strong>gritty</strong>, and <strong>superficial</strong> nodules on the hair shafts. As the fungus grows into the hair shaft, the hair may fracture easily. It produces sexual spores in its parasitic phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shaving or cutting hair (prevents recurrence also)</li>\n<li>Benzoic acid ointment is used.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: <em>Trichosporon beigelli </em>causes white piedra or trichosporosis nodosa.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em>Corynebacterium tenuis </em>causes trichomycosis axillaris.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em>Trichophyton tonsurans </em>causes mainly tinea capitis.</p>\n<p>The images below show<strong> </strong>black piedra infected hair mounted in 30% KOH and the appearance of hair strands, respectively. The dark nodules are formed of dematiaceous hyphae cemented together to form a hard mass.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The images given below show white piedra. Superficial, soft, white, grey, or brown nodules can be seen. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5041","text":"A 65-year-old male comes w‍ith complaints of an inte​nsely pruritic lesion tha​t started 6 days back. Wh‌at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old male comes with complaints of an intensely pruritic lesion that started 6 days back. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinea c‍orporis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pityriasis r‍ubra pilaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pityriasis‍ versicolor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pie‍dra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tinea corporis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/430426e0c49147fa924a77ddfc49211e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tinea corporis</strong></p><p>The above image showing a single, <strong>intensely</strong> <strong>pruritic</strong> lesion with sharp margins and central resolution is suggestive of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>corporis.</strong></p>\n<p>Central resolution is a frequent but not exclusive finding. Multiple lesions may be present.</p>\n<p>Option B: Pityriasis rubra pilaris is<strong> </strong>a salmon-red dry scaly plaque with a nutmeg grater appearance.</p>\n<p>Option C: Pityriasis versicolor presents as hypo or hyperpigmented macules with fine scaling.</p>\n<p>Option D: Piedra has black or white nodules present superficially on the hair shaft. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5055","text":"Which layer of the epiderm‍is is primarily infected ​by dermatophytes?","raw_text":"Which layer of the epidermis is primarily infected by dermatophytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum‍ corneum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum g‍ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum‍ basale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum ‍spinosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stratum corneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stratum corneum</strong></p><p><strong>Dermatophytes </strong>infect and survive only in the <strong>dead keratin layers</strong> of skin (<strong>stratum corneum</strong>), hair and nails as they are <strong>keratinophillic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7108","text":"A 23 year old patient pres‍ents with lesions in the ​groin as shown in the ima​ge below. Which of the fo‌llowing cannot be a cause​ of these lesions?","raw_text":"A 23 year old patient presents with lesions in the groin as shown in the image below. Which of the following cannot be a cause of these lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tricho‍phyton","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Micro‍sporum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asper‍gillus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epiderm‍ophyton","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aspergillus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/deac5b1d364e4f808e021639d745eb4c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aspergillus</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>well-circumscribed</strong>, <strong>annular plaque</strong> in the <strong>groin</strong> region which is characteristic of <strong>tinea cruris</strong>. Tinea cruris is a <strong>dermatophytosis.</strong> <em>Trichophyton</em><strong>, </strong><em>Microsporum</em> and <em>Epidermophyton</em><strong> </strong>are dermatophytes whereas <strong>Aspergillus</strong> is not.</p>\n<p>Dermatophytes include 3 genera: </p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Trichophyton</strong> species infect <strong>s</strong><strong>kin,</strong> <strong>hair, and nails </strong>and include<strong> </strong><em>T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. tonsurans, T. schoenleinii, and T. violaceum</em>.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Microsporum</strong> contains <em>M. gypseum, M. canis</em>, and <em>M. nanum</em>. These infect only hair and skin (<strong>n</strong><strong>ot nails</strong>).</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Floccosum is the <strong>only species</strong> in the genus <strong>Epidermophyton</strong>, It infects only skin and nails (<strong>n</strong><strong>ot hair</strong>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em><strong>Aspergillus</strong> spp.</em> produce <strong>large necrotic lesions</strong> like<strong> ecthyma gangrenosum</strong>, and sometimes small papules and cold abscesses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5044","text":"Shown below is the trichog‍ram of a patient sufferin​g from tinea capitis. Whi​ch of the following fungi‌ is implicated here?","raw_text":"Shown below is the trichogram of a patient suffering from tinea capitis. Which of the following fungi is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsporum‍ ferrugineum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichophyto‍n tonsurans","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trichophy‍ton canis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microsporu‍m audouinii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trichophyton tonsurans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0072cff054cf47f5a01e96245f2e00ef.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eabd81c9185648e8bf8b3eeae46ec992x235x180.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trichophyton tonsurans</strong></p><p>The above image shows numerous <strong>fungal spores </strong>with<strong>in</strong> the hair <strong>shaft</strong> suggestive of <strong>endothrix</strong> type of tinea capitis, which is caused by <em><strong>Trichophyton tonsurans.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Tinea capitis is of <strong>four</strong> types: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 322px; width: 425px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.609px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Type of Infection </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.391px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Causative fungus </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 118px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.609px; height: 118px;\">Endothrix (infection inside shaft)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.391px; height: 118px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Trichophyton tonsurans</em></li>\n<li><em>T.violaceum</em></li>\n<li><em>T. schoenleinii</em></li>\n<li><em> T.yaoundei</em></li>\n<li><em>T. soudanense</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.609px; height: 100px;\">Ectothrix (infection outside shaft)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.391px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Microsporum audouinii</em></li>\n<li><em> M.canis</em></li>\n<li><em>M.equinum</em></li>\n<li><em>M.ferrugineum</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.609px; height: 46px;\">Favus </td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.391px; height: 46px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>T. schoenleinii</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.609px; height: 41px;\">Kerion </td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.391px; height: 41px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>T. verrucosum</em></li>\n<li><em>T. mentagrophytes</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5048","text":"A 32-year-old cattle farme‍r presents with a painful​ lesion of the scalp for ​2 weeks. What is the most‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old cattle farmer presents with a painful lesion of the scalp for 2 weeks. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fa‍vus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ecto‍thrix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ker‍ion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endo‍thrix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kerion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/615deb2ac97a490b8da36030b20ba023.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/185ea7962ecf4181ada58ede013360d6x1280x1094.950634696756.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kerion</strong></p><p>The above image shows an <strong>inflammatory</strong> <strong>mass</strong> with <strong>thick</strong> <strong>crusting</strong> and <strong>matting</strong> of hair which is suggestive of a <strong>kerion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Kerion is a severe inflammatory type of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>capitis. </strong>The most common causative organisms of kerion are<em><strong> T. verrucosum </strong></em>and<em><strong> T. mentagrophytes. </strong></em>In severe cases, there may be pus along with <strong>sinus</strong> formation. </p>\n<p>Option A: Favus is characterized by the presence of yellowish, cup‐shaped crusts known as scutula that develop around hair follicles.</p>\n<p>Option B: Ectothrix is a mild inflammatory type in which dull grey coated broken hairs are seen.</p>\n<p>Option D: Endothrix is a non-inflammatory type in which black dots form around swollen hair shafts. </p>\n<p>Note: Scarring alopecia is seen in favus and kerion while non-scarring alopecia is seen in ectothrix and endothrix.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0210","text":"A 10-year-old child presen‍ts with hair loss. The ha​ir loss is patchy as show​n in the image below. Bla‌ck dots are noted in area​s of hair loss. Which of ‌the following types of ti​nea is this child sufferi​ng from?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child presents with hair loss. The hair loss is patchy as shown in the image below. Black dots are noted in areas of hair loss. Which of the following types of tinea is this child suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ecto‍thrix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endo‍thrix","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ker‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fa‍vus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endothrix","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc130d289f8a478fa129b68057ef6273.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40c409e84f94408ea1e0cab7b67251c8x512x358.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68e052bb23854f379756ffbe59c16609x720x462.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endothrix</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario depicting a <strong>young child</strong> with <strong>patchy hair loss</strong> and the image showing <strong>black dots</strong> is suggestive of<strong> endothrix type </strong>of<strong> tinea capitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Tinea capitis is a dermatophyte infection primarily occurring in <strong>children</strong>.<em><strong> </strong></em>Clinical features of tinea capitis are patchy hair loss, scaling, tender lymphadenopathy, and in severe cases, there might be pustules and crusting of the affected area along with <strong>sinus</strong> formation. </p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical</strong> <strong>variants</strong> of tinea capitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endothrix</strong> - a non-inflammatory type in which <strong>multiple black dots</strong> are present within the areas of alopecia. It is most commonly caused by <strong>T. tonsurans and T. violaceum.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ectothrix</strong> -a mild inflammatory type in which single or multiple scaly patches with hair loss are seen. <strong>Microsporum audouinii</strong>, M.canis, M.equinnum, and M.ferrugineum cause ectothrix infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Kerion</strong> -a severe inflammatory type that presents with an inflammatory mass with <strong>thick crusting</strong> and <strong>matting of hair</strong> with pus and sinus formation. It is commonly caused by <strong>T. verrucosum and T. mentagrophytes</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Favus</strong> - is characterized by the presence of yellowish, cup‐shaped crusts known as <strong>scutula</strong> that develop around hair follicles. It is caused by <strong>T. schoenleinii</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terbinafine</strong>: &lt;10 Kg- 62.5 mg; 10-20 kg - 125 mg; &gt;20 kg- 250 mg, all given daily for 4 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Itraconazole</strong>: 2-4 mg/kg/day for 4-6 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5049","text":"Which of the following is ‍associated with tinea inc​ognito?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with tinea incognito?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichophyto‍n tonsurans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cortico‍steroids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antimyco‍tic drugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microspo‍rum canis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corticosteroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corticosteroids</strong></p><p><strong>Tinea incognito</strong> is an <strong>atypical</strong> clinical lesion of tinea, usually produced by incorrect treatment of fungal infection with a <strong>topical corticosteroid</strong>.</p>\n<p>Steroids<strong> suppress inflammation</strong> without eliminating the fungus; so the typically raised margin is diminished, <strong>scaling is lost</strong> and inflammation reduces. Hence, diagnosis becomes difficult.</p>\n<p>Pharmacological <strong>treatment</strong> of fungal <strong>tinea</strong> infection depends on the extent of the disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Localized disease:<br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Topical terbinafine twice daily for 2 weeks</li>\n<li>Topical azole once or twice daily for 2–4 weeks</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Widespread disease: <br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line - oral terbinafine 250 mg/day 2–3 weeks or oral itraconazole 100 mg/day 2–4 weeks</li>\n<li>Second-line - oral griseofulvin 1 g/day for 4 weeks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2309","text":"A college student presents‍ to the dermatology OPD w​ith a pruritic lesion on ​his left foot for 5 days.‌ Which is the most common​ causative organism for t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A college student presents to the dermatology OPD with a pruritic lesion on his left foot for 5 days. Which is the most common causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichophy‍ton rubrum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trichophyto‍n tonsurans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermophyt‍on floccosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyton interdigitale‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trichophyton rubrum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3539a45f7f248adbe918593647fb8f5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/554cc575be3c4986b105ae2ccc650eecx1280x922.5387870239774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trichophyton rubrum</strong></p><p>The above image shows a scaly pruritic lesion of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>pedis</strong> or <strong>ringworm</strong> of foot is most commonly caused by <em><strong>Trichophyton rubrum</strong></em> infections (70%) followed by <em>Trichophyton interdigitale</em> infections (15%), <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em> infections (&lt;10%), and mixed infections (5%).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5059","text":"Which of the following is ‍known as Dhobi’s itch?","raw_text":"Which of the following is known as Dhobi’s itch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinea c‍orporis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinea ‍cruris","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tinea ‍barbae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinea ‍capitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tinea cruris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tinea cruris</strong></p><p><strong>Dhobi's itch/tinea cruris</strong> is an infection of the <strong>groin</strong> by a species of <strong>dermatophyte</strong>. It is commonly known as ringworm of the groin or eczema marginatum<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><em>T. rubrum</em> is the main cause; however, <em>T. Interdigitale</em> and <em>E. floccosum</em> also account for some cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5062","text":"An 11-year-old child prese‍nted with a pruritic lesi​on as shown. Which of the​ following is the drug of‌ choice for this conditio​n?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old child presented with a pruritic lesion as shown. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terbi‍nafine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Griseo‍fulvin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluco‍nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Selenium sul‍phide shampoo","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Griseofulvin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28ed60484bfc4c11934a1c7c52875bd2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Griseofulvin</strong></p><p>The image shows a scaly pruritic lesion affecting the scalp hair, suggestive of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>capitis. </strong>The drug of choice treatment for <strong>children</strong> in whom the cause of tinea capitis is unclear is <strong>g</strong><strong>riseofulvin</strong>. <strong>Terbinafine</strong> is the <strong>alternate</strong> first-line drug for adults.</p>\n<p>Option A: Oral terbinafine is also used in the treatment of<em> T. tonsurans</em> tinea capitis in children, but usually used in adults.</p>\n<p>Option C: Fluconazole is more effective in the treatment of <em>M. canis</em> compared to terbinafine.</p>\n<p>Option D: Selenium sulphide has only a supportive role in the treatment of tinea capitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5065","text":"What test is used to diagn‍ose the given condition?","raw_text":"What test is used to diagnose the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram’s‍ stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"KOH ‍mount","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue‍ smear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wood’‍s lamp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. KOH mount","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fa4771426bc648a79cf55e5a5e86a4c3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b34ee62a4304d2095b8106d0b39aa70x1280x1151.8194640338504.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. KOH mount</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>faciei, </strong>a <strong>dermatophyte</strong> infection of the <strong>glabrous</strong> <strong>skin</strong> of the <strong>face</strong> (the mustache and beard areas of the adult male are excluded). It is diagnosed by <strong>KOH</strong> <strong>mount</strong>. </p>\n<p><em>Trichophyton mentagrophytes</em> and <em>T.rubrum</em> predominate, but <em>T.tonsurans, M. audouinii, </em>and<em> M. canis</em> are also common causes. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2345","text":"A 33-year-old man presente‍d with a circular, prurit​ic and scaly lesion on hi​s trunk. A lactophenol co‌tton blue mount of the sc​rapings from the lesion s‌howed the following. What​ is the most probable cau​sative organism?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man presented with a circular, pruritic and scaly lesion on his trunk. A lactophenol cotton blue mount of the scrapings from the lesion showed the following. What is the most probable causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tricho‍phyton","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Micro‍sporum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epiderm‍ophyton","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Any of t‍he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epidermophyton","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe49bc99c2f6429abca2bb3a4770f4fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epidermophyton</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>tinea</strong> <strong>corporis</strong> and the slide showing <strong>smooth-walled club-shaped macroconidia</strong> with <strong>absent microconidia</strong> is indicative of <strong>Epidermophyton</strong> species.</p>\n<p>Epidermophyton species infects skin and nails but <strong>not the hair</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2340","text":"A patient who was diagnose‍d with kerion now present​s with new-onset vesicula​r eruptions on his finger‌s. Which of the following​ is incorrect regarding t‌hese lesions?","raw_text":"A patient who was diagnosed with kerion now presents with new-onset vesicular eruptions on his fingers. Which of the following is incorrect regarding these lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesions are extremely prur‍itic and maybe tender","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a form of allergic r‍esponse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Responds well to topical c‍orticosteroids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dermatophytes can't be dem‍onstrated from this lesio​n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Responds well to topical corticosteroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Responds well to topical corticosteroids</strong></p><p>The above scenario describes a <strong>dermatophytid</strong> (-id) reaction, a <strong>systemic reaction</strong> to <strong>fungal</strong> <strong>antigens</strong> during the <strong>infection</strong> or <strong>therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>An id reaction can be seen on the <strong>hands</strong> and sides of the <strong>fingers, </strong>but fungi cannot be demonstrated in these lesions.</p>\n<p>The reactions may be vesicular, lichenoid, papulosquamous, or pustular. The lesions are extremely <strong>pruritic</strong> and <strong>tender</strong>. The onset can be accompanied by fever, anorexia, generalized adenopathy, spleen enlargement, and leukocytosis.</p>\n<p>Although the eruptions are usually <strong>refractory</strong> to topical <strong>corticosteroids</strong>, they typically clear rapidly after treatment of the fungal infection. Secondary <strong>bacterial</strong> infections may occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2343","text":"Tinea unguium is a dermato‍phyte infection of ______​.","raw_text":"Tinea unguium is a dermatophyte infection of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nail‍ fold","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nail ‍plate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Periungu‍al region","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cut‍icle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nail plate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1be5b17dd6da4c499c2c653ba6e3c573x1280x940.592383638928.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nail plate</strong></p><p><strong>Tinea</strong> <strong>unguium</strong> is a dermatophytic infection of the <strong>nail</strong> <strong>plate</strong>. Onychomycosis is a broad term for all fungal nail infections. </p>\n<p><em>Trichophyton rubrum</em> accounts for most cases.</p>\n<p>It presents with <strong>yellowish</strong> discoloration, which spreads proximally as a streak in the nail. Later, <strong>subungual hyperkeratosis</strong> becomes prominent and spreads until the entire nail is affected. Gradually, the entire nail becomes <strong>brittle</strong> and <strong>separated</strong> from its bed as a result of the piling up of subungual keratin.</p>\n<p><strong>Six</strong> distinct <strong>patterns</strong> of tinea unguium have been described.</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal and lateral subungual onychomycosis (DLSO) (most common)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Superficial onychomycosis (SO)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proximal subungual onychomycosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endonyx onychomycosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Totally dystrophic onychomycosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mixed onychomycosis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Diagnosis is made by <strong>KOH</strong> mount examination of <strong>clippings</strong> or curettings that include dystrophic subungual debris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2344","text":"What is the correct treatm‍ent regimen for the condi​tion shown?","raw_text":"What is the correct treatment regimen for the condition shown?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral griseofulvin for 3 we‍eks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral griseofulvin for 6 we‍eks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral terbinafine for 12 we‍eks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral terbinafine for 24 we‍eks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral terbinafine for 12 weeks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a0e617e2fc043a8bf468f875e5a08f5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral terbinafine for 12 weeks</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>onychomycosis</strong> of <strong>toenails. </strong>The first-line treatment is oral <strong>terbinafine</strong> 250mg/day for <strong>12</strong> <strong>weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment of onychomycosis:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 174px; width: 503px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Oral terbinafine </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Oral itraconazole </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Finger nails </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.75px;\">\n<p>250mg/day for 6 weeks </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.25px;\">\n<p>400mg/day for 1week, repeated every month for 2-3months </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Toe nails </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.75px;\">\n<p>250mg/day for 12 weeks </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.25px;\">\n<p>400mg/day for 1week,  repeated every month for 3-4 months </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Second-line drug: Oral <strong>griseofulvin</strong> dose 1 g/day for 4–8 months (longer for toenails).</p>\n<p><strong>Topical treatment</strong> with <strong>amorolfine</strong> or<strong> ciclopirox olamine</strong> can be used in <strong>mild</strong> infecti<strong>ons</strong> affecting the distal nail plate only or non‐linear superficial onychomycosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2354","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is correct with r​espect to Perlèche?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct with respect to Perlèche?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflammation around the na‍il involving the nail fol​ds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"White creamy patch over to‍ngue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cobbled appearance of tong‍ue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soreness and cracking at t‍he angles of the mouth","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Soreness and cracking at the angles of the mouth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3eda3cb312e84e9083f4d4341184c6fax1280x1097.6586741889987.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Soreness and cracking at the angles of the mouth</strong></p><p><strong>Soreness</strong> and <strong>cracking</strong> at the <strong>angles</strong> of the mouth are seen in <strong>Perlèche</strong>. It is considered as an <strong>intertrigo</strong> where <strong>candida</strong> is the most common organism implicated.</p>\n<p>It is also called <strong>angular</strong> <strong>stomatitis</strong>. It is a type of <strong>oral</strong> <strong>mucosal</strong> <strong>candidiasis</strong>. Nutritional status and mechanical factors (e.g. the depth of the fold), the presence of moisture from persistent salivation or licking the lips also contribute to stomatitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2351","text":"Which type of oral mucosal‍ candidiasis does not pre​sent with a white patch?","raw_text":"Which type of oral mucosal candidiasis does not present with a white patch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic atrophic candidias‍is","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic hyperplastic candi‍diasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic pseudomembranous c‍andidiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute pseudomembranous can‍didiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chronic atrophic candidiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/746352dea2c34d7c9de18625907d92bex968x607.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chronic atrophic candidiasis</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic atrophic candidiasis</strong> presents with a <strong>red patch</strong>, not white.</p>\n<p>Candidiasis of the oral mucosal membranes is of the following types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 532px;\" width=\"581\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type of Candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px;\">\n<p>Acute pseudomembranous candidiasis /</p>\n<p>Oral thrush / </p>\n<p>Chronic pseudomembranous candidiasis </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px;\">\n<p>Sharply defined patch of creamy, crumbly, curd‐like, white pseudomembrane</p>\n<p>Has an underlying erythematous base</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px;\">Acute erythematous candidiasis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px;\">\n<p>Denuded, atrophic, erythematous mucous membranes</p>\n<p>Most common- dorsum of<br/> the tongue </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px;\">\n<p>Chronic erythematous candidiasis /</p>\n<p>Chronic atrophic candidiasis / </p>\n<p>Denture stomatitis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px;\">\n<p>Affected areas show a variable bright red or dusky erythema</p>\n<p>Fairly sharply defined at the margin of the denture</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px;\">\n<p>Chronic plaque‐like candidiasis / </p>\n<p>Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis /</p>\n<p>Candida leukoplakia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px;\">\n<p>Very persistent, firm, irregular, white plaques occur in the mouth</p>\n<p>Commonly on the cheek or tongue </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px;\">Chronic nodular candidiasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px;\">Tongue has a cobbled appearance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.578px;\">Angular cheilitis/Angular stomatitis/ Perlèche</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300.422px;\">Soreness at the angles of the mouth extending outwards in the<br/> folds of the facial skin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Image:</strong> Chronic Atrophic Candidiasis</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2355","text":"A 32-year-old man presents‍ with reddish-pink, non-s​caly, itchy and moist pat​ches in his left armpit. ‌Small, superficial, white​ pustules are observed ad‌jacent to the patches. Wh​at is the most likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presents with reddish-pink, non-scaly, itchy and moist patches in his left armpit. Small, superficial, white pustules are observed adjacent to the patches. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candidal ‍Balanitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Candidal ‍Intertrigo","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oropharyneal‍ candidiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candidal ‍Paronychia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Candidal Intertrigo","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3412c7ce2c1e4fe39f7b7c8ace280908x379x379.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Candidal Intertrigo</strong></p><p>The erythematous, <strong>non-scaly</strong> moist patches with tiny, superficial, white <strong>pustular</strong> <strong>satellite lesions</strong> are suggestive of <strong>candidal</strong> <strong>intertrigo</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sites of infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Between genital folds and groins</li>\n<li>Armpits</li>\n<li>Between the buttocks</li>\n<li>Inframammary fold (large and pendulous breasts)</li>\n<li>Under overhanging abdominal folds</li>\n<li>Umbilicus.</li>\n<li>Web spaces of toes, fingers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Marked <strong>maceration</strong> with a thick, white, horny layer is usually prominent. <strong>Soreness</strong> and <strong>itching</strong> are present.</p>\n<p>The disease usually spreads beyond the area of contact, developing lesions with irregular edges and subcorneal pustules rupturing to give tiny erosions which lead to peeling of the stratum corneum. </p>\n<p>Psoriasis, tinea corporis, and pityriasis rotunda have scaly lesions.</p>\n<p>The image below shows candidial intertrigo. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5066","text":"A patient presents with th‍e following lesion. Which​ is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following lesion. Which is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orient‍al sore","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lupus v‍ulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromobla‍stomycosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ker‍ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oriental sore","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/760d6d0170c04b90997ac5c9a168c174.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oriental sore</strong></p><p>The above image shows the <strong>volcano</strong> <strong>sign</strong> seen in <strong>O</strong><strong>riental</strong> <strong>sore</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lesion starts as a small non-tender <strong>papule</strong>, which enlarges in size and <strong>ulcerates</strong> in the centre. The border of the <strong>crusted ulcer</strong> often has an <strong>erythematous</strong> <strong>rim</strong> and is called a “volcano sign.”</p>\n<p>It is also called <strong>cutaneous</strong> <strong>leishmaniasis</strong> or old world leishmaniasis, Kandahar sore, Lahore sore, or Delhi boil.</p>\n<p>Causative organisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Old world leishmaniasis: <em>L. donovani, L.major, </em>and<em> L.tropica</em></li>\n<li>New world leishmaniasis: <em>L. mexicana </em>complex and<em> L. braziliensis </em>complex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5051","text":"A 30-year-old farmer prese‍nts with firm, painless n​odules with purulent disc​harge on his left foot fo‌r 10 days. What is the li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old farmer presents with firm, painless nodules with purulent discharge on his left foot for 10 days. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sporotr‍ichosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chromobla‍stomycosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myce‍toma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Orienta‍l sores","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mycetoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/696988de17084f45808f5618a6e1b913.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e306dbf207a49cda26acdbe0d366550x1280x1711.4809590973202.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mycetoma</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>nodular</strong> lesions that have broken down to form <strong>draining</strong> <strong>sinuses</strong> with purulent discharge which is suggestive of <strong>mycetoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nodules are <strong>painless</strong> and with time form papules and pustules which break down to form draining sinuses, with the discharge of <strong>granules</strong>. It is caused by various types of <strong>fungi</strong> (eumycetoma) or <strong>filamentous</strong> <strong>bacteria</strong> (actinomycetes). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5053","text":"A patient presents with mu‍ltiple hypertrophic plaqu​es on his left leg for 6 ​months. History reveals t‌hat the lesions started a​s warty papules and progr‌essed to the current stat​e. On examination, severa​l satellite lesions noted‌. Skin biopsy from the le​sion is shown below. What​ is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with multiple hypertrophic plaques on his left leg for 6 months. History reveals that the lesions started as warty papules and progressed to the current state. On examination, several satellite lesions noted. Skin biopsy from the lesion is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromobla‍stomycosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sporotr‍ichosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phaeohyp‍homycosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myce‍toma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chromoblastomycosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4976ce21f6b47d9823733ac5f81890e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chromoblastomycosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>Medlar</strong> <strong>bodies</strong> that are suggestive of <strong>chromoblastomycosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Medlar bodies are <strong>golden</strong>-<strong>brown</strong>, <strong>thick</strong>‐<strong>walled</strong> muriform or <strong>sclerotic</strong> cells in <strong>abscesses. </strong>They may also be found within <strong>giant cells. </strong></p>\n<p>Chromoblastomycosis is a chronic infection of the <strong>skin</strong> and <strong>subcutaneous tissue</strong> which is caused by <strong>pigmented</strong> <strong>fungi</strong> (Phialophora and Fonsecaea species). The lesions start as <strong>warty</strong> papules, slowly enlarge to form a hypertrophic <strong>plaque</strong> progressing to large hyperkeratotic masses over months to years<strong>. Satellite</strong> <strong>lesions</strong> are produced by <strong>scratching</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5063","text":"A patient presents with th‍e following lesions. What​ is the most likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following lesions. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lichen‍ planus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporotr‍ichosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chromobla‍stomycosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tinea c‍ircinata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sporotrichosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae99c77cb7974c0ca331471ef7853f87.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fcf12136f56431d94ddb7d8b780d75ax1280x1725.0211565585332.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a157fa2a90644048972f3b13de15d962x720x1065.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sporotrichosis</strong></p><p>A<strong> linear</strong> <strong>distribution</strong> of <strong>nodules</strong> is seen in <strong>sporotrichosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sporotrichosis is an acute or chronic <strong>fungal</strong> infection caused by <em><strong>Sporothrix schenckii</strong></em> and closely related species. </p>\n<p>The given images show the distribution of lesions along lymphatics. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Lichen planus</strong>- Purple polygonal, flat-topped papules are seen.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Chromoblastomycosis</strong>-  multiple hypertrophic plaques are seen.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Tinea</strong> <strong>circinata</strong>- circular, scaly lesions with central clearing are seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5071","text":"Which of the following is ‍the vector of Chiclero ul​cer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the vector of Chiclero ulcer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phleb‍otomus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lutz‍omyia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Redu‍viid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tromb‍iculid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lutzomyia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f45df25c317f460b9486c7af3ffcd6dbx1280x2012.0733427362482.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4eba9a5a44124147b32acd6115b34706x650x650.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lutzomyia</strong></p><p>The vector for <strong>Chiclero</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong> or <strong>new</strong> <strong>world</strong> <strong>leishmaniasis </strong>is the <strong>lutzomyia sandfly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The vector bites humans, typically those working as <strong>chicle collectors,</strong> causing lesions on the side of the <strong>face</strong> or behind the ears. Lesions on the pinna of the ear may <strong>invade cartilage,</strong> take many years to heal, and destroy the pinna. </p>\n<p>The images below show the Chiclero ulcer and lutzomyia sandfly. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Phlebotomus sandfly is the vector for old-world leishmaniasis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Reduviid bug is the vector for Chagas disease.</p>\n<p>Option D: Trombiculid mite is the vector for scrub typhus. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5072","text":"A 20-year-old man from Jai‍pur presented with an ery​thematous nodule on the c​heek with central crustin‌g. What is the most likel​y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man from Jaipur presented with an erythematous nodule on the cheek with central crusting. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‍us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lupus v‍ulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chil‍blain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous l‍eishmaniasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cutaneous leishmaniasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0af6a5d1747747c09160d9dd6032fbb4x513x381.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cutaneous leishmaniasis</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>cutaneous</strong> <strong>leishmaniasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The classical lesion in cutaneous leishmaniasis is characterized by <strong>erythematous nodule/plaque</strong> which then <strong>ulcerates</strong> and becomes <strong>crusted.</strong> Diascopy usually reveals<strong> 'apple jelly nodules'</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: 'Apple jelly nodules' on diascopy are seen in both cutaneous leishmaniasis and lupus vulgaris. The skin lesion in cutaneous leishmaniasis shows central crusting, whereas in lupus vulgaris it shows central scarring.</p>\n<p>The image shows apple jelly nodule seen on diascopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2338","text":"A 36-year-old male patient‍ from Bihar comes with co​mplaints of hypopigmented​ macules over the trunk a‌nd face. The nerves and s​ensations are found to be‌ normal and there is no v​isible scaling. The patie​nt gives a history of pro‌longed fever 2 years back​ for which he was treated​. What is the drug of cho​ice for this condition?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old male patient from Bihar comes with complaints of hypopigmented macules over the trunk and face. The nerves and sensations are found to be normal and there is no visible scaling. The patient gives a history of prolonged fever 2 years back for which he was treated. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multidrug therapy for lepr‍osy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amphotericin B deoxycholat‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium sti‍bogluconate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Milte‍fosine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Miltefosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c444e1bfdcba4b52bf2413c5443557cfx1280x1092.2425952045135.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Miltefosine</strong></p><p>The case scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis</strong>. The drug of choice for this condition is <strong>miltefosine</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Approach to the case: </strong></p>\n<p>The patient hails from a kala-azar endemic zone—Bihar. The rash appears <strong>1-2 years after</strong> a history of prolonged fever, which suggests <strong>visceral leishmaniasis. </strong>There are 2 other differentials—leprosy and tinea versicolor. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The presence of hypopigmented macules over the trunk and face, suggests a differential of leprosy, but the nerves and sensations are normal, hence ruling it out.</li>\n<li>There is no scaling visible in the lesions, ruling out tinea versicolor. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In kala-azar, after a variable period of years or months, <strong>diffuse nodulation begins</strong> to develop in these macules. The rash is <strong>progressive</strong> over many years and seldom heals spontaneously. As it may persist for up to 20 years, these patients may act as a <strong>chronic reservoir</strong> of infection.</p>\n<p>The image below shows post-kala-azar cutaneous leishmaniasis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ztDcEhXU","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"16_physiology_of_hearing_and_tuning_fork_tests","title":"16.Physiology Of Hearing And Tuning Fork Tests","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB8866","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a function of the mid​dle ear?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impedance‍ matching","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protection ‍of inner ear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transducti‍on of sound","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amplification of sound int‍ensity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transduction of sound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03b5c7d8ab4e457ea30fc5b48fbb202bx1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transduction of sound</strong></p><p><strong>Transduction of sound </strong>(mechanical energy to electrical impulses) is done by the sensory system of the<strong> cochlea. </strong>It is<strong> not</strong> a function of the <strong>middle ear.</strong></p>\n<p>The functions of the middle ear are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conduction of sound through impedance matching or transformer action</li>\n<li>Protection of inner ear</li>\n<li>Amplification of sound intensity</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8867","text":"What is the lever ratio of‍ the ear ossicles?","raw_text":"What is the lever ratio of the ear ossicles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.‍3:1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"22‍:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.3:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8327f593cc564d7fb563864175790233x1280x1555.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.3:1</strong></p><p>The <strong>lever ratio</strong> of the <strong>ear ossicles</strong> is <strong>1.3:1.</strong></p>\n<p>The handle of the malleus is 1.3 times longer than the long process of the incus, providing a mechanical advantage of 1.3.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8868","text":"The effective vibratory ar‍ea of the tympanic membra​ne is much larger than th​e area of stapes footplat​e. Which of the following​ is the most accurate val​ue of the average ratio b​etween the two?","raw_text":"The effective vibratory area of the tympanic membrane is much larger than the area of stapes footplate. Which of the following is the most accurate value of the average ratio between the two?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17‍:1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1.‍3:1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"18‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"22‍:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 17:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e717e574fbcb458b822f5aff237edbbex1280x2228.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 17:1</strong></p><p>The ratio of effective vibratory area of the tympanic membrane to the area of footplate of stapes <strong>(areal ratio)</strong> is<strong> 17:1</strong> .</p>\n<p>Effective vibratory area of TM = 55 mm<sup>2</sup></p>\n<p>Footplate area = 3.2 mm<sup>2 </sup></p>\n<p><strong>Areal ratio</strong> = Effective vibratory area of tympanic membrane ÷ stapes footplate area</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8869","text":"What is the transformer ra‍tio of the middle ear?","raw_text":"What is the transformer ratio of the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.‍3:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"22‍:1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 22:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81e72df24a85415c8ca463cd8b88859fx1280x1936.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 22:1</strong></p><p>The <strong>transformer ratio</strong> of the middle ear is <strong>22:1</strong></p>\n<p>The middle ear has to conduct sound waves from a low impedance tympanic membrane to a high impedance oval window. This is done by matching their impedances. It is called transforming action.</p>\n<p><strong>Transformer ratio =Areal ratio × Lever ratio</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8870","text":"What is the natural resona‍nce of the tympanic membr​ane?","raw_text":"What is the natural resonance of the tympanic membrane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300‍0 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"800-1‍600 Hz","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1500-2‍000 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2000-3‍000 Hz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 800-1600 Hz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 800-1600 Hz</strong></p><p>The <strong>natural resonance</strong> of the <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> is <strong>800 – 1600 Hz.</strong></p>\n<p>Aging causes a decrease in the external ear resonance amplitude due to the collapse of the external auditory canal and the tympanic membrane becoming senile. This factor has to be considered to appropriately diagnose hearing loss and in the selection of hearing aids.</p>\n<p><strong>Natural resonance and efficiency of auditory apparatus:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>External auditory canal- 3000 Hz</li>\n<li>Tympanic membrane- 800–1600 Hz</li>\n<li>Middle ear- 800 Hz</li>\n<li>Ossicular chain- 500–2000 Hz</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8871","text":"Which of the following fac‍tors do not contribute to​ the impedance matching m​echanism of the middle ea​r?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors do not contribute to the impedance matching mechanism of the middle ear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lever action of ossicles p‍roviding mechanical advan​tage of 1.3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phase difference between t‍he oval and round windows​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relatively large area of t‍he tympanic membrane comp​ared to stapes footplate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More movements at the cent‍re of the tympanic membra​ne than at periphery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. More movements at the centre of the tympanic membrane than at periphery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. More movements at the centre of the tympanic membrane than at periphery</strong></p><p><strong>Movements</strong> of the tympanic membrane are <strong>more</strong> at the <strong>periphery</strong> than at the center, where the malleus handle is attached. This is called the <strong>curved membrane effect</strong> and it contributes to the impedance matching mechanism of the ear.</p>\n<p>Other factors responsible for the impedance matching mechanism of the middle ear are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>lever action</strong> of the <strong>ossicles-</strong> handle of malleus is 1.3 times longer than the long process of incus, providing a mechanical advantage of 1.3.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydraulic action</strong> of the <strong>tympanic membrane-</strong> relatively large area of the tympanic membrane compared to the stapes footplate.</li>\n<li>The <strong>medial side</strong> of the tympanic membrane is <strong>multiphasic</strong> whereas the <strong>lateral side</strong> is <strong>uniphasic.</strong> This provides a multiple integrated multilever mechanical advantage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8872","text":"Who proposed the travellin‍g wave theory of cochlear​ mechanics?","raw_text":"Who proposed the travelling wave theory of cochlear mechanics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"von B‍ékésy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yo‍ung","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Helm‍holtz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sa‍de","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. von Békésy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. von Békésy</strong></p><p>The <strong>traveling wave theory</strong> was proposed by <strong>von Békésy.</strong></p>\n<p>It states that a sound wave, depending on its frequency, reaches maximum amplitude on a particular place on the basilar membrane and stimulates that segment.</p>\n<p><strong>Higher frequencies</strong> are represented in the <strong>basal turn of the cochlea</strong> and the progressively <strong>lower ones</strong> towards the<strong> apex. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8873","text":"What is the endocochlear p‍otential recorded from th​e scala media?","raw_text":"What is the endocochlear potential recorded from the scala media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+80‍ mV","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"-80‍ mV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"+40‍ mV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-40‍ mV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. +80 mV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. +80 mV</strong></p><p><strong>Endocochlear potential</strong> is a <strong>direct current </strong>(DC) potential recorded from<strong> scala media</strong>. It is <strong>+80 mV</strong> and is generated from the<strong> stria vascularis</strong> by <strong>Na+/K+-ATPase</strong> pump and provides a source of energy for cochlear transduction. It is <strong>present at rest</strong> and does not require a sound stimulus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8874","text":"You are an intern performi‍ng the tuning fork tests ​on a patient who presente​d to the ENT OPD with com​plaints of decreased hear​ing in the left ear. Whic​h of the following tuning​ forks is most commonly u​sed for the routine clini‍cal assessment for such p​atients?","raw_text":"You are an intern performing the tuning fork tests on a patient who presented to the ENT OPD with complaints of decreased hearing in the left ear. Which of the following tuning forks is most commonly used for the routine clinical assessment for such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"102‍4 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"512‍ Hz","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"256‍ Hz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"126‍ Hz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 512 Hz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 512 Hz</strong></p><p>A <strong>tuning fork</strong> of<strong> 512 Hz </strong>is<strong> most commonly used</strong> for routine clinical assessment in ENT. </p>\n<p>Tuning forks of lower frequencies produce a sense of bone vibration while those of higher frequencies have a shorter decay time and are thus not routinely preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8875","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‍ to the ENT OPD with hear​ing loss in the right ear​. A 512 Hz tuning fork is​ used, which highlights t​he Rinne test is positive​ bilaterally. What is the​ likely inference?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the ENT OPD with hearing loss in the right ear. A 512 Hz tuning fork is used, which highlights the Rinne test is positive bilaterally. What is the likely inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air conduction is better t‍han bone conduction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bone conduction is better ‍than air conduction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Air conduction is equal to‍ bone conduction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone conduction is worse t‍han that of examiner's","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Air conduction is better than bone conduction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Air conduction is better than bone conduction</strong></p><p><strong>Rinne test </strong>is<strong> positive</strong> when the <strong>air conduction</strong> (AC) is<strong> longer</strong> or louder than <strong>bone conduction</strong> (BC).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8876","text":"What should be the minimum‍ hearing loss in a patien​t for the Rinne test to b​e negative?","raw_text":"What should be the minimum hearing loss in a patient for the Rinne test to be negative?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-1‍0dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15-‍20dB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"25-‍30dB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"35-‍40dB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 15-20dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 15-20dB</strong></p><p>The minimum hearing loss for the <strong>Rinne test</strong> to be <strong>negative</strong> is <strong>15-20dB</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Negative Rinne</strong> (BC &gt; AC) is seen in <strong>conductive deafness. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8880","text":"In a patient presenting wi‍th a conductive type of h​earing loss, a negative R​inne test with a 512 Hz t​uning fork indicates a mi​nimum air-bone gap of?","raw_text":"In a patient presenting with a conductive type of hearing loss, a negative Rinne test with a 512 Hz tuning fork indicates a minimum air-bone gap of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30‍ dB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"45‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60‍ dB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30 dB</strong></p><p>The <strong>negative Rinne</strong> test for <strong>512 Hz</strong> indicates a minimum air-bone gap of <strong>30 dB</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Rinne’s test </strong>is a tuning fork test performed commonly with tuning forks of frequencies such as 128, 256, 512, and 1024 Hz.</p>\n<p><strong>Rinne’s test</strong> is done by placing a vibrating tuning fork on the patient’s mastoid and when he stops hearing, it is brought beside the external auditory meatus (EAC). If he still hears the sound, <strong>air conduction </strong>(AC) is better than <strong>bone conduction </strong>(BC) i.e.<strong> AC&gt;BC</strong>. The inferences are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rinne's test is considered <strong>positive </strong>(normal) when the air conduction<strong> AC&gt;BC</strong> is seen in normal people and people with <strong>sensorineural deafness.</strong></li>\n<li>It is considered <strong>negative</strong> when the <strong>BC&gt;AC,</strong> and is seen in patients with <strong>conductive deafness</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>As a general prediction, a <strong>negative Rinne's test</strong> with<strong> any tuning fork</strong> frequency suggests a minimum hearing loss of <strong>at least</strong> <strong>15 - 20 dB.</strong>The table below indicates a rough <strong>minimum air-bone gap</strong> for <strong>negative Rinne's test</strong> at different frequencies: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 120px; width: 269.961px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Negative Rinne test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.961px;\"><strong>Minimum air-bone gap</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">256 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.961px;\">15 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">512 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.961px;\">30 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">1024 Hz</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.961px;\">45 dB</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A prediction of the <strong>air-bone gap</strong> can be made based on the combination of tuning fork tests of 256, 512, and 1024 Hz:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 48\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>A Rinne test <strong>negative</strong> for<strong> 256 Hz</strong> but <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>512</strong> <strong>Hz</strong> indicates an air-bone gap of <strong>20-30 dB.</strong></li>\n<li>A Rinne test <strong>negative</strong> for <strong>256</strong> and <strong>512</strong> <strong>Hz</strong> but <strong>positive</strong> for<strong> 1024 Hz</strong> indicates an air-bone gap of <strong>30-45 dB.</strong></li>\n<li>A Rinne test <strong>negative</strong> for <strong>256, 512, and 1024</strong> Hz indicates an air-bone gap of <strong>45-60 dB.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8879","text":"You are a first-year resid‍ent performing the tuning​ fork tests on the follow​ing patients to assess th​eir hearing loss. Which o​ne of them will you expec​t to have a positive Rinn​e test?","raw_text":"You are a first-year resident performing the tuning fork tests on the following patients to assess their hearing loss. Which one of them will you expect to have a positive Rinne test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A child with chronic suppu‍rative otitis media","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An adolescent man with imp‍acted wax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An elderly patient with pr‍esbycusis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A pregnant woman with otos‍clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. An elderly patient with presbycusis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. An elderly patient with presbycusis</strong></p><p>A <strong>positive Rinne test</strong> is seen in <strong>presbycusis, </strong>which is a type of sensorineural deafness.</p>\n<p>A <strong>positive Rinne</strong> test indicates that air conduction is longer than bone conduction, as seen in <strong>normal</strong> ear and <strong>sensorineural deafness.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>negative Rinne</strong> test indicates that bone conduction is longer than air conduction, as seen in conditions causing in <strong>conductive</strong> <strong>deafness</strong> such as chronic suppurative otitis media, impacted wax, and otosclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8877","text":"A middle-aged man diagnose‍d with Ménière's disease ​presented with severe uni​lateral hearing loss. Whi​ch of the following would​ be the most likely findi​ng on performing the Rinn​e test on this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man diagnosed with Ménière's disease presented with severe unilateral hearing loss. Which of the following would be the most likely finding on performing the Rinne test on this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posi‍tive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nega‍tive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"False n‍egative","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"False p‍ositive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. False negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. False negative</strong></p><p>Patients diagnosed with <strong>Ménière's disease</strong> present with a <strong>sensorineural</strong> type of hearing loss. A <strong>false negative</strong> <strong>Rinne test</strong> is seen in<strong> severe unilateral sensorineural hearing loss.</strong></p>\n<p>The patient doesn’t respond to air conduction but is able to hear when a tuning fork is placed over the mastoid process i.e. bone conduction &gt; air conduction. Hence reported as negative. This is a false negative because this response to bone conduction is from the opposite ear due to transcranial transmission of sound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8878","text":"A young lady presented to ‍the ENT OPD with a histor​y of sudden onset unilate​ral hearing loss, tinnitu​s, and dizziness followin​g an episode of acute oti​tis media two weeks back.​ Rinne test and fistula t​est were positive. She ha‍d refused treatment then ​and currently came with c​omplaints of worsened hea‌ring loss in the affected‍ ear. On examination, the​ fistula test was negativ​e. What would be the most‍ likely finding on the Ri​nne test in this scenario‍?","raw_text":"A young lady presented to the ENT OPD with a history of sudden onset unilateral hearing loss, tinnitus, and dizziness following an episode of acute otitis media two weeks back. Rinne test and fistula test were positive. She had refused treatment then and currently came with complaints of worsened hearing loss in the affected ear. On examination, the fistula test was negative. What would be the most likely finding on the Rinne test in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True p‍ositive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"True n‍egative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"False p‍ositive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"False n‍egative","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. False negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. False negative</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario indicates <strong>severe sensorineural hearing loss</strong> as a consequence of <strong>untreated</strong> <strong>suppurative</strong> <strong>labyrinthitis</strong> and Rinne's test is a <strong>false negative</strong> in this case.</p>\n<p>Initially, the patient had a <strong>positive fistula test</strong>, which implies that the <strong>labyrinth</strong> is still <strong>functioning.</strong> Rinne's test is positive which could be due to normal hearing or sensorineural hearing loss.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 56\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Currently, the patient has a <strong>negative fistula test</strong> which<strong> </strong>implies that the <strong>labyrinth</strong> is <strong>dead.</strong> This is an example of severe sensorineural hearing loss and here, Rinne test is a false negative.</p>\n<p>If intermittent pressure applied to the external ear produces vertigo and nystagmus, it is a positive fistula test. The pressure changes in the external ear are transmitted to the labyrinth. This is suggestive of abnormal communication between the middle ear and the inner ear. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8881","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about the test bein​g performed on a patient ​by an ENT resident as sho​wn below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the test being performed on a patient by an ENT resident as shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lateralized to worse‍ ear in superior semicirc​ular canal dehiscence syn​drome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is heard equally on bot‍h sides in normal individ​ual","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is lateralized towards ‍better ear in cochlear ot​osclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is lateralized towards ‍worse ear in Ménière’s di​sease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is lateralized towards worse ear in Ménière’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57c52231434c4f93b0662182171bdcd1.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d9361117f0447b79daa4c9784129feex1280x1537.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is lateralized towards worse ear in Ménière’s disease</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>Weber test</strong> being performed. <strong>Ménière’s disease</strong> causes<strong> sensorineural hearing loss </strong>and hence Weber test is lateralized towards the<strong> better ear. </strong></p>\n<p>In Weber's test, a vibrating tuning fork is placed in the middle of the forehead or the vertex and the patient is asked in which ear the sound is heard.</p>\n<p>It is lateralized to the <strong>worse ear</strong> in <strong>conductive deafness</strong> and to the <strong>better ear</strong> in <strong>sensorineural deafness.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Superior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome causes conductive hearing loss. Thus, Weber is lateralized to the worse ear.</p>\n<p>Option B: In normal individuals, the sound is heard equally in both ears. So Weber is not lateralized. </p>\n<p>Option C: Cochlear otosclerosis causes sensorineural deafness, whereas stapedial otosclerosis causes conductive hearing loss. Thus, Weber is lateralized to the better ear in case of cochlear otosclerosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8882","text":"A vibrating tuning fork is‍ placed on the mastoid wh​ile the examiner alternat​ely closes and opens the ​ear canal of the patient ​by pressing the tragus in​wards to examine the effe​ct of occlusion on hearin​g. What is the test being‍ performed here?","raw_text":"A vibrating tuning fork is placed on the mastoid while the examiner alternately closes and opens the ear canal of the patient by pressing the tragus inwards to examine the effect of occlusion on hearing. What is the test being performed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absolute bone conduction t‍est","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schwaba‍ch test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bing‍ test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gille’‍s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bing test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bing test</strong></p><p><strong>Bing test</strong> is being performed in the given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>test</strong> of<strong> bone conduction</strong> and examines the <strong>effect of </strong><strong>occlusion </strong>of the ear canal on hearing<strong>.  </strong>A vibrating tuning fork is placed on the mastoid. The examiner alternately closes and opens the ear canal by pressing the tragus inwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Bing positive: </strong>A <strong>normal person</strong> or one with <strong>sensorineural hearing loss</strong> hears <strong>louder when</strong> the ear canal is <strong>occluded</strong> and softer when the canal is open.</p>\n<p><strong>Bing negative: </strong>A patient with <strong>conductive hearing loss</strong> will appreciate <strong>no change.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8883","text":"A young female diagnosed w‍ith otosclerosis presente​d to the ENT OPD with wor​sening hearing loss. Whic​h of the following will b​e the most likely observa​tion on the Bing test?","raw_text":"A young female diagnosed with otosclerosis presented to the ENT OPD with worsening hearing loss. Which of the following will be the most likely observation on the Bing test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hears louder when canal is‍ occluded","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Appreciate‍s no change","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hears softer when canal is‍ occluded","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The test is said to be pos‍itive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Appreciates no change","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Appreciates no change</strong></p><p>The patient diagnosed with <strong>otosclerosis</strong> will have a <strong>conductive </strong>type of <strong>hearing loss</strong> and<strong> </strong>will appreciate<strong> no change</strong> in the<strong> Bing test.</strong> Here, the Bing test is said to be <strong>negative.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Bing test is a test of<strong> bone conduction</strong> and examines the <strong>effect of </strong><strong>occlusion </strong>of the ear canal on hearing<strong>.  </strong>A vibrating tuning fork is placed on the mastoid. The examiner alternately closes and opens the ear canal by pressing on the tragus inwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Bing positive: </strong>A <strong>normal person</strong> or one with <strong>sensorineural hearing loss</strong> hears <strong>louder when</strong> the ear canal is <strong>occluded</strong> and softer when the canal is open.</p>\n<p><strong>Bing negative: </strong>A patient with <strong>conductive hearing loss</strong> will appreciate <strong>no change.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8884","text":"In which of the following ‍diseases would you find a​ negative Gelle's test?","raw_text":"In which of the following diseases would you find a negative Gelle's test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acoustic‍ neuroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presb‍ycusis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Otoscl‍erosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meniere’‍s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Otosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Otosclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Gelle’s test</strong> is <strong>negative</strong> in <strong>otosclerosis.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is based on the fact that in a normal ear if <strong>pressure</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> in the <strong>external ear</strong> the <strong>stapes footplate</strong> will get <strong>medialized</strong> and will <strong>stop conducting sound</strong> (<strong>positive Gelle's test</strong>). But, in an ear with otosclerosis of the footplate, there will be no movements and the patient will continue to hear the sound (negative Gelle's test).</p>\n<p>It was once a popular test to find out stapes fixation in otosclerosis but has now been superseded by tympanometry.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8885","text":"A second-year resident per‍forms the tuning fork tes​ts on a patient with hear​ing loss. The Rinne test ​is positive in both ears ​and Weber is lateralized ​towards the right. What i​s the interpretation?","raw_text":"A second-year resident performs the tuning fork tests on a patient with hearing loss. The Rinne test is positive in both ears and Weber is lateralized towards the right. What is the interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right-sided conductive dea‍fness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left-sided conductive deaf‍ness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right-sided sensorineural ‍deafness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left-sided sensorineural d‍eafness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left-sided sensorineural deafness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f148467327fc44af9dfc5ba0e5ed9f20x1280x1605.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left-sided sensorineural deafness</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>left-sided sensorineural deafness.</strong></p>\n<p>Weber's test lateralized towards the right indicates right-sided conductive deafness or left-sided sensorineural deafness. Since Rinne's test is positive on both the ears, conductive deafness can be ruled out and hence, the interpretation here is left-sided sensorineural hearing loss.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9244","text":"A metal factory worker suf‍fers from noise-induced h​earing loss. Which of the​ following structures wil​l be affected?","raw_text":"A metal factory worker suffers from noise-induced hearing loss. Which of the following structures will be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crista a‍mpullaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mac‍ula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cup‍ola","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outer ha‍ir cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Outer hair cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02b67038d3f04a378c4d261a65319cddx1280x1623.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Outer hair cells</strong></p><p>Noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL) <strong>damages the outer hair cells</strong>, starting in the basal turn of the<strong> cochlea. </strong><strong>Outer hair cells </strong>are affected <strong>before</strong> the <strong>inner hair cells.</strong></p>\n<p>NIHL occurs after <strong>chronic exposure</strong> to less intense sounds. The damage caused by noise trauma depends on the <strong>frequency</strong> of noise (frequency of 2000 to 3000 Hz causes more damage) <strong>intensity</strong>, <strong>duration</strong> of the noise, and <strong>preexisting ear disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The audiogram in noise-induced hearing loss shows a<strong> typical notch at 4000Hz</strong> both for <strong>air</strong> and <strong>bone conduction</strong> known as <strong>acoustic dip/boiler's notch</strong>. Patients at this stage complain of <strong>high-pitched tinnitus</strong> and <strong>difficulty hearing</strong> in noisy surroundings. Pre-employment and annual audiograms for persons working at places where noise is above 85 dB (A) help in the early detection of NIHL. </p>\n<p>The human ear can hear frequencies from about <strong>20 to 20,000 Hz</strong>. Below 20 Hz, the vibrations are infra-audible, and above 20,000 Hz, they are ultra-sonic. <strong>Pure tone audiometry</strong> can detect only up to <strong>8000 Hz</strong>.</p>\n<p>Extended <strong>high-frequency audiometry</strong> is recommended for <strong>early detection</strong> of hearing loss, as it can identify hearing impairments before the frequencies necessary for speech comprehension are affected. <strong>Distortion product otoacoustic emissions</strong> is a more sensitive tool than conventional audiometry, particularly in <strong>children</strong> under 5 years old for early detection of hearing loss. </p>\n<p>A noise of <strong>90 dB(A)</strong> SPL (acceptable noise levels, sound pressure level),<strong> 8 hours a day for 5 days per week</strong> is the <strong>maximum safe limit</strong> as recommended by the Ministry of Labour. As per<strong> WHO</strong>, <strong>85 dB(A)</strong> is considered the highest safe exposure level up to a maximum of <strong>8 hours</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>silence zone</strong> extends to a distance of <strong>100 meters</strong> around the premises of hospitals, nursing homes, educational institutions, and courts.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Permissible limits of noise as per the noise pollution (Regulation and Control) rules: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 95px; width: 449.219px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Zone/Area</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\"><strong>Day</strong> (6 am to 10 pm) limits in dB(A)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.219px;\"><strong>Night</strong> (10 pm to 6 am) limits in dB(A)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">Industrial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">75</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.219px;\">70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">Commercial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">65</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.219px;\">55</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">Residential</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">55</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.219px;\">45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">Silence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">50</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.219px;\">40</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Noise levels are measured in<strong> dB(A)</strong>, which prioritizes middle-range frequencies for assessing noise-induced hearing loss while giving less weightage to low and high frequencies.</p>\n<p>The image below shows an acoustic dip at 4000Hz: </p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Crista ampullaris is a thickened ridge of neuroepithelium in the ampullated end of semicircular ducts.</p>\n<p>Option B: Macula is the sensory epithelium of the utricle</p>\n<p>Option C: Cupula is a gelatinous mass extending from the surface of the crista (peripheral receptor located in the ampullated end of semicircular ducts) to the ceiling of the ampulla, into which project the cilia of sensory hair cells. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Zc21wYi1","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"16_organophosphorus_poisoning","title":"16.Organophosphorus Poisoning","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB0945","text":"Poisoning with which of th​e following compounds cau‌ses mydriasis?","raw_text":"Poisoning with which of the following compounds causes mydriasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diaz​inon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phos​drin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"End​rin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Para​oxon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endrin</strong></p><p><strong>Endrin </strong>is an <strong>organochlorine</strong> compound. Poisoning with this compound causes <strong>mydriasis</strong>. There is no specific antidote for organochlorine poisoning.</p>\n<p>Diazinon, phosdrin, and paraoxon are organophosphates and they cause miosis. Organophosphate poisoning is treated with atropine and oximes.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> of <strong>insecticides</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Organophosphorus compounds\n<ul>\n<li>Parathion</li>\n<li><strong>Paraoxon</strong></li>\n<li>Chlorthion</li>\n<li><strong>Diazinon</strong></li>\n<li>Malathion</li>\n<li><strong>Phosdrin</strong></li>\n<li>HETP (hexaethyl tetraphosphate)</li>\n<li>TEPP (tetraethyl pyrophosphate)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Organochlorine compounds\n<ul>\n<li>DDT (dichloro diphenyl trichloroethane)</li>\n<li>Gammahexa-chlorobenzene</li>\n<li><strong>Endrin</strong></li>\n<li>Aldrin</li>\n<li>Endosulfan</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Carbamates\n<ul>\n<li>Aldicarb</li>\n<li>Carbaryl</li>\n<li>Carbofuran</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Pyrethrin and pyrethroids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0946","text":"A 36-year-old woman presen​ts to the casualty with d‌iarrhea, miosis, and alte‌red sensorium. Her husban‌d reveals that she has in​gested rat poison in an a‌ttempt to commit suicide.‌ What is the likely mecha​nism that is responsible ​for her clinical presenta‌tion?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman presents to the casualty with diarrhea, miosis, and altered sensorium. Her husband reveals that she has ingested rat poison in an attempt to commit suicide. What is the likely mechanism that is responsible for her clinical presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of acetylcholin​esterase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carbamylation of carboxyli​c esterase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidation of hemoglobin to​ methemoglobin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Free radical mediated dama​ge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>organophosphorus</strong> (OP)<strong> poisoning</strong>. Clinical effects are due to the <strong>inhibition </strong>of<strong> acetylcholinesterase. </strong></p>\n<p>Inhibition of cholinesterase leads to the accumulation of acetylcholine at the nerve synapses and neuromuscular junctions. This results in overstimulation of acetylcholine receptors resulting in a <strong>cholinergic crisis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>types</strong> of <strong>cholinesterases</strong> are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 161px;\" width=\"428\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">\n<p>Acetylcholinesterase or true or RBC cholinesterase</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">\n<p>RBC membranes</p>\n<p>Nervous tissue</p>\n<p>Skeletal muscle</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">Butyrylcholinesterase or plasma cholinesterase or pseudocholinesterase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">\n<p>Serum</p>\n<p>Liver</p>\n<p>Pancreas</p>\n<p>Heart</p>\n<p>Brain</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">Neuropathy target esterase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">\n<p>Brain</p>\n<p>Kidney</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Acetylcholinesterase or RBC cholinesterase is helpful in the diagnosis of OP poisoning but has no prognostic significance.</p>\n<p>It is hypothesized that inhibition of neuropathy target esterase enzyme may be responsible for organophosphate-induced delayed neuropathy (OPIDN).</p>\n<p>The mechanism of action of the other <strong>agricultural poisons</strong> are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 132px;\" width=\"335\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\"><strong>Compound</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\"><strong>Mechanism of action</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\">Carbamates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\">Carbamylation of carboxylic esterase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\">\n<p>Chlorate salts</p>\n<p>(sodium or potassium chlorates)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\">Oxidation of hemoglobin to methemoglobin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\">Paraquat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.5px;\">Free radical-mediated damage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3876","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g the clinical syndromes ‌seen in patients with org‌anophosphate poisoning?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the clinical syndromes seen in patients with organophosphate poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CNS symptoms can be seen i​n acute toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermediate syndrome can ​be treated with atropine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic toxicity can manif​est as symmetrical sensor‌imotor axonopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle wasting can be seen​ in organophosphate-induc‌ed delayed neuropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intermediate syndrome can be treated with atropine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intermediate syndrome can be treated with atropine</strong></p><p><strong>Intermediate syndrome</strong> is <strong>unresponsive</strong> to <strong>atropine</strong> and oximes. The syndrome occurs due to prolonged cholinesterase inhibition and muscle necrosis. Maintenance of nutrition, physiotherapy, prevention of bed sores and other routine measures to minimise discomfort along with ventilatory care are management options.</p>\n<p>Four <strong>clinical syndromes</strong> are identified with respect to organophosphate exposure. They include: </p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Acute </strong>poisoning - This results in CNS, muscarinic, nicotinic, and somatic motor manifestations.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Intermediate </strong>syndrome - It is usually observed 1-5 days after OP exposure. It is characterized by paralysis of neck flexors, muscles innervated by cranial nerves, proximal limb muscles, and respiratory muscles.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Chronic </strong>toxicity - It is seen primarily in agricultural workers with daily exposure. It manifests as symmetrical sensorimotor axonopathy.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Organophosphate-induced delayed neuropathy</strong> - This is characterized by cognitive dysfunction, extrapyramidal symptoms, and peripheral neuropathy. It can lead to flaccid paralysis and muscle wasting.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0948","text":"A 25-year-old woman, who w​as admitted with accident‌al rodenticide ingestion,‌ was started on atropine.‌ All of the following sym​ptoms would improve in re‌sponse to therapy except:‌","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman, who was admitted with accidental rodenticide ingestion, was started on atropine. All of the following symptoms would improve in response to therapy except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brady​cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urinary in​continence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle​ cramps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Muscle cramps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Muscle cramps</strong></p><p>Atropinization in organophosphate (rodenticide) poisoning does not cause regression of nicotinic symptoms like <strong>muscle cramps</strong>. Hence, <strong>oximes</strong> are used for the reversal of <strong>nicotinic</strong> symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Atropine </strong>is <strong>effective</strong> against <strong>muscarinic</strong> receptors. It can be used to treat muscarinic symptoms like bradycardia, urinary incontinence, and hypotension.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0955","text":"A local informer revealed ​that decades-old nerve ga‌s cylinders were being sm‌uggled across the border.‌ On arrival of the police​ at the crime scene, the ‌accused opened fire. One ‌of the bullets accidental​ly impaled the canister n​ear the police officer. W‌hich of the following sym​ptoms would you expect to‍ see in him?","raw_text":"A local informer revealed that decades-old nerve gas cylinders were being smuggled across the border. On arrival of the police at the crime scene, the accused opened fire. One of the bullets accidentally impaled the canister near the police officer. Which of the following symptoms would you expect to see in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halluci​nations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mio​sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chest​ pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joint​ pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Miosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Miosis</strong></p><p><strong>Nerve gases </strong>are <strong>organophosphate</strong> compounds in the gaseous form. It manifests with <strong>cholinergic</strong> signs and symptoms, such as <strong>miosis. </strong>It was used in chemical warfare.</p>\n<p>The nerve gases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soman</li>\n<li>Sarin</li>\n<li>Tabun</li>\n<li>VX</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0958","text":"A 45-year-old woman, who i​s being treated for organ‌ophosphate poisoning with‌ atropine, is started on ‌pralidoxime. Which of the​ following symptoms would‌ regress due to the actio‌n of pralidoxime alone?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman, who is being treated for organophosphate poisoning with atropine, is started on pralidoxime. Which of the following symptoms would regress due to the action of pralidoxime alone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mio​sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tongue fas​ciculations","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abdomina​l cramps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tongue fasciculations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tongue fasciculations</strong></p><p><strong>Pralidoxime</strong> causes reversal of <strong>tongue fasciculations (nicotinic</strong> symptoms).</p>\n<p>Since <strong>atropine</strong> does not bind to nicotinic receptors, it is <strong>ineffective</strong> in treating <strong>neuromuscular</strong> <strong>dysfunction</strong>. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) and other <strong>oximes</strong>, such as HI-6 and obidoxime, are <strong>cholinesterase-reactivating</strong> agents. Hence, its <strong>coadministration</strong> with atropine is effective in treating both <strong>muscarinic</strong> and <strong>nicotinic</strong> symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Pralidoxime reactivates</strong> the <strong>acetylcholinesterase, </strong>which had been deactivated in organophosphate poisoning. Pralidoxime attaches to the anionic site of the enzyme that remains unoccupied. Then it<strong> reacts </strong>with the <strong>phosphorus atom</strong> of the organophosphorous compound which is attached to the esteric site to form a<strong> phospho-oxime complex. </strong>It then dissociates from the enzyme, regenerating the active enzyme.</p>\n<p>Pralidoxime should not be administered without concurrent atropine, in order to prevent worsening of symptoms due to transient oxime-induced acetylcholinesterase inhibition.</p>\n<p>Note: Muscle cramps are nicotinic symptoms. However, abdominal cramps are muscarinic symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0954","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about the‌ intermediate syndrome in‌ organophosphorus poisoni‌ng?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the intermediate syndrome in organophosphorus poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Signs of cholinergic exces​s are seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is no specific treat​ment","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"There is weakness of neck ​extensors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory failure is not​ a complication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. There is no specific treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. There is no specific treatment</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">There is <strong>no specific treatment </strong>for the <strong>intermediate syndrome</strong> in organophosphate <strong>(OP) poisoning.</strong></p>\n<p>The intermediate syndrome is seen in 20-50% of acute OP poisoning cases. This syndrome develops <strong>one to several days</strong> after the poisoning, <strong>during</strong> the <strong>recovery</strong> from cholinergic manifestations. In some cases, it can occur when patients have completely recovered from the initial cholinergic crisis.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>paralysis</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neck flexor</strong> muscles</li>\n<li>Muscles innervated by the cranial nerves</li>\n<li>Proximal limb muscles</li>\n<li>Respiratory muscles - <strong>respiratory support</strong> may be needed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Symptoms or signs of <strong>cholinergic excess</strong> are <strong>absent</strong> in this syndrome.</p>\n<p>There is <strong>no specific treatment</strong> for the intermediate syndrome and intervention is exclusively supportive.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0947","text":"A 38-year-old gardener pre​sents with blurred vision‌, breathlessness, and mus‌cle fasciculations. He gi‌ves a history of spraying​ the solution given in th‌e image a day prior to hi‌s symptoms. All of the fo​llowing are specific indi​cators of the likely diag‌nosis except:","raw_text":"A 38-year-old gardener presents with blurred vision, breathlessness, and muscle fasciculations. He gives a history of spraying the solution given in the image a day prior to his symptoms. All of the following are specific indicators of the likely diagnosis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged Q​Tc interval","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Miosis and muscle fascicul​ations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resolution of cholinergic ​symptoms upon atropine ad‌ministration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fall in RBC cholinesterase​ levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prolonged QTc interval","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b8227b1bbe8d44fa8169a7c4fd66e7d3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prolonged QTc interval</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>malathion</strong> (organophosphate) <strong>poisoning</strong>. In this condition, <strong>prolonged QTc</strong> interval is a <strong>non-specific</strong> finding. However, it correlates with the severity and mortality in severe cases.</p>\n<p>The <strong>reliable indicators</strong> of <strong>OP poisoning</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Miosis and muscle fasciculations</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Decrease in RBC cholinesterase levels</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Absence/ resolution of cholinergic symptoms after an initial dose of atropine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0953","text":"You are suspecting organop​hosphorus poisoning in a ‌farmer who presented with‌ chronic fatigue and urin‌ary incontinence. What wo​uld be the most accurate ‌investigation to assess p‌otential toxicity?","raw_text":"You are suspecting organophosphorus poisoning in a farmer who presented with chronic fatigue and urinary incontinence. What would be the most accurate investigation to assess potential toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma cho​linesterase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RBC choli​nesterase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serum levels of organophos​phorus compounds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary levels of organoph​osphorus compound metabol‌ites","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RBC cholinesterase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RBC cholinesterase</strong></p><p><strong>RBC cholinesterase</strong> enzyme activity is the most <strong>accurate</strong> indicator to assess potential organophosphorus (<strong>OP</strong>) <strong>toxicity</strong>. It is an indicator of synaptic cholinesterase inhibition.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>plasma butyrylcholinesterase</strong> is <strong>easier</strong> to assay and more easily available. Hence, it is more commonly used.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Measurement of parent compound in blood and metabolites urine are rarely carried out in a clinical setting.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: The correlation between cholinesterase activity, clinical symptoms, and atropine dose is inconsistent. Hence, treatment should be guided predominately by clinical symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0952","text":"In organophosphorus poison​ing, clinical manifestati‌ons occur when pseudochol‌inesterase levels fall be‌low:","raw_text":"In organophosphorus poisoning, clinical manifestations occur when pseudocholinesterase levels fall below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"95% of​ normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"75% of​ normal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60% of​ normal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50% of​ normal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 50% of normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 50% of normal</strong></p><p>In <strong>organophosphorus poisoning,</strong> clinical signs and <strong>symptoms </strong>are observed when <strong>pseudocholinesterase</strong> level falls <strong>below</strong> <strong>50% </strong>of normal. </p>\n<p>Severity of symptoms correlates with pseudocholinesterase levels:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 95px;\" width=\"388\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\"><strong>Severity of OP poisoning</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\"><strong>Levels of pseudocholinesterase</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">40-50%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">10-40%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">&lt;10%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>RBC cholinesterase</strong> enzyme activity is the most <strong>accurate</strong> indicator to assess potential organophosphorus <strong>toxicity</strong>. However, <strong>plasma butyrylcholinesterase</strong> is <strong>easier</strong> to assay and more easily available. Hence, it is more commonly used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3877","text":"A 7-year-old girl is rushe​d to the casualty in an u‌nconscious state as she h‌as consumed ant chalks 2 ‌hours ago. On examination​, you note the presence o‌f miosis and crackles on ‌chest auscultation. She i​s hypotensive with PR of ​50 bpm, RR of 36 /min, an‌d ECG showing prolonged Q​Tc. You start treating he‍r with atropine sulphate ‌IV. Which of the followin‌g will you consider as th​e therapeutic endpoint?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl is rushed to the casualty in an unconscious state as she has consumed ant chalks 2 hours ago. On examination, you note the presence of miosis and crackles on chest auscultation. She is hypotensive with PR of 50 bpm, RR of 36 /min, and ECG showing prolonged QTc. You start treating her with atropine sulphate IV. Which of the following will you consider as the therapeutic endpoint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pupillary​ dilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in​ heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norma​l ECG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clear chest on auscultatio​n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clear chest on auscultation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clear chest on auscultation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute organophosphorus poisoning </strong>which is treated with atropine sulphate. <strong>Clear chest</strong> on <strong>auscultation</strong> indicates clearing of tracheobronchial secretions and cessation of bronchoconstriction. Hence, it is considered as the therapeutic <strong>endpoint</strong> of <strong>atropinization</strong>. </p>\n<p>Tachycardia and mydriasis are not appropriate markers for therapeutic improvement, as they may indicate continued hypoxia, hypovolemia, or sympathetic stimulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> of organophosphorus poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decontamination</li>\n<li>Airway management</li>\n<li>Stomach wash - water with or without KMnO<sub>4</sub></li>\n<li><strong>Atropine sulphate</strong> at a dose of 2-4 mg IV - highly effective in counteracting the peripheral muscarinic effects and antagonize CNS effects at higher doses</li>\n<li><strong>Oximes</strong> - used early as they have more pronounced action on nicotinic sites</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0951","text":"In patients with organopho​sphate poisoning, which o‌f the following treatment‌ modalities has proven mo‌rtality benefits?","raw_text":"In patients with organophosphate poisoning, which of the following treatment modalities has proven mortality benefits?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemodi​alysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prali​doxime","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxygen supp​lementation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary alk​alinization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oxygen supplementation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oxygen supplementation</strong></p><p><strong>Oxygen supplementation</strong> has proven <strong>clinical </strong>and<strong> mortality benefits</strong> in patients with organophosphate (OP) poisoning who develop<strong> respiratory failure</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong><strong>Pralidoxime</strong></strong> is <strong>not beneficial</strong> in terms of reducing <strong>mortality</strong>, need for ventilator support, and incidence of intermediate syndrome. Despite theoretical and experimental benefits and clinical use worldwide, the current evidence is inadequate. However, until further research has been undertaken, all patients poisoned with organophosphorus agents are recommended to be treated with an oxime. </p>\n<p><strong>Urinary alkalinization, h</strong><strong>emodialysis,</strong> and hemoperfusion have no role in the treatment of OP poisoning.</p>\n<p>Note: Gastric lavage and activated charcoal administration are beneficial only in patients who present within 2 hours following ingestion of OP compounds.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0961","text":"What is the most common ca​use of mortality in patie‌nts with organophosphate ‌poisoning?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of mortality in patients with organophosphate poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac a​rrhythmias","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ​failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypovole​mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respirato​ry failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Respiratory failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Respiratory failure</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>death</strong> in <strong>organophosphate poisoning</strong> is <strong>respiratory failure</strong>. It usually occurs within 24 hours in untreated patients, secondary to paralysis of respiratory muscles.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0960","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding carbama‌te poisoning is true when‌ compared to organophosph‌orus poisoning?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding carbamate poisoning is true when compared to organophosphorus poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with fast​er aging of the cholinest‌erase enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can also lead to the in​termediate syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severe CNS toxicity can be​ seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pralidoxime is the mainsta​y in management","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can also lead to the intermediate syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can also lead to the intermediate syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Carbamates</strong> can also lead to the <strong>intermediate syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Carbamate-cholinesterase bond undergoes rapid, spontaneous hydrolysis which leads to regeneration of the enzyme within a few hours. This is called <strong>reversible carbamylation</strong>. Hence, aging (conversion of the inhibited enzyme into a non-reactivable form) does not occur and the <strong>effect</strong> of carbamate <strong>toxicity</strong> is of a <strong>shorter duration.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Carbamates do not effectively penetrate the CNS in adults. Hence, there is less CNS toxicity.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pralidoxime </strong>is<strong> contraindicated</strong> in carbamate poisoning because it may potentiate the toxicity of carbamates due to its transient oxime-induced cholinesterase inhibition. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5tVunSbb","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"16_reproductive_endocrinology","title":"16.Reproductive Endocrinology","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB6942","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of 11-beta-​hydroxylase deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic​ acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Virili​zation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency</strong> is associated with<strong> metabolic alkalosis</strong>, not metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>It is a rare form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia that presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucocorticoid deficiency:\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation around the genitals due to excess adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) secretion </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Mineralocorticoid excess:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Metabolic alkalosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Adrenal androgen excess\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Virilization</strong> in females</li>\n<li>Precocious puberty in males</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6969","text":"A neonate with ambiguous g​enitalia undergoes karyot​yping and is confirmed to​ have a 46XY genotype. Hi‌s blood pressure is 140/8‌0 mm Hg and serum potassi‍um is 1.9 mEq/L. Which of​ the following is respons‍ible for these findings?","raw_text":"A neonate with ambiguous genitalia undergoes karyotyping and is confirmed to have a 46XY genotype. His blood pressure is 140/80 mm Hg and serum potassium is 1.9 mEq/L. Which of the following is responsible for these findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11-deoxycor​ticosterone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17-hydroxyp​regnanediol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"17-hydroxyp​rogesterone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 11-deoxycorticosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 11-deoxycorticosterone</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with <strong>ambiguous</strong> <strong>genitalia</strong> in a <strong>male</strong> neonate is suggestive of <strong>17 alpha-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>. It causes <strong>hypertension</strong> and <strong>hypokalemia</strong> due to the accumulation of <strong>11-deoxycorticosterone</strong>,<strong> </strong>which is a mineralocorticoid causing sodium retention.</p>\n<p>11-hydroxylase deficiency also leads to the accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6970","text":"Which of the following abn​ormalities will you see i​n a child with salt-wasti​ng congenital adrenal hyp‌erplasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following abnormalities will you see in a child with salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogl​ycemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypern​atremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypoglycemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> is a feature of<strong> s</strong><strong>alt-wasting</strong> congenital adrenal hyperplasia (<strong>CAH</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is due to <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>. It results in <strong>hyponatremia</strong>, <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>, and <strong>hypotension</strong>. Males typically present with salt-wasting crises as they often go undiagnosed at birth due to the presence of normal genitalia. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6971","text":"Which of the following is ​the drug of choice for th​e prenatal treatment of c​ongenital adrenal hyperpl‌asia due to 21-hydroxylas‌e deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the prenatal treatment of congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexame​thasone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Betame​thasone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Predni​solone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroco​rtisone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dexamethasone</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for the <strong>prenatal</strong> treatment of congenital adrenal hyperplasia <strong>(CAH)</strong> due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency is <strong>dexamethasone</strong>, as it readily crosses the placenta.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>experimental</strong> approach aimed at <strong>reducing</strong> the <strong>virilization</strong> of <strong>46XX</strong> fetuses by suppressing fetal adrenal steroid and androgen secretion. It may be considered when there is a high risk of CAH due to <strong>strong family history.</strong> It is effective only when started before 4 weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrocortisone </strong>is given in the children at divided doses of 1–1.5 times the normal cortisol production.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6973","text":"A female neonate is noted ​to have ambiguous genital​ia at birth. Further test​ing reveals elevated 17-(‌OH)-progesterone levels a‌nd a blood glucose of 40 ‍mg/dl. What treatment wil​l you recommend?","raw_text":"A female neonate is noted to have ambiguous genitalia at birth. Further testing reveals elevated 17-(OH)-progesterone levels and a blood glucose of 40 mg/dl. What treatment will you recommend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Betame​thasone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beclome​thasone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dexame​thasone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroco​rtisone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydrocortisone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydrocortisone</strong></p><p>Ambiguous genitalia, elevated 17-(OH)-progesterone, and hypoglycemia are suggestive of <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>. The drug of choice is <strong>hydrocortisone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hydrocortisone acts as a potent glucocorticoid with mineralocorticoid action and thus increases salt absorption from the kidneys as well.</p>\n<p>Betamethasone, beclomethasone, and dexamethasone have negligible effects as mineralocorticoids.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6975","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about galactorr​hea?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about galactorrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"May be b​ilateral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated w​ith lactation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with prolactino​ma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with acromegaly​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Associated with lactation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Associated with lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Galactorrhea</strong> or <strong>pathological</strong> production of milk is not associated with lactation, which<strong> </strong>is a <strong>physiological</strong> condition.</p>\n<p>Galactorrhea is the inappropriate discharge of milk-containing fluid from the breast. It may be <strong>unilateral </strong>or<strong> bilateral. </strong>It is associated with <strong>acromegaly </strong>and<strong> hyperprolactinemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is with <strong>dopamine agonists</strong> like bromocriptine or cabergoline if it is due to hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6976","text":"In a patient with gynecoma​stia, you will order all ​of the following tests ex​cept:","raw_text":"In a patient with gynecomastia, you will order all of the following tests except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testos​terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prol​actin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​adiol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Luteinizi​ng hormone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prolactin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prolactin</strong></p><p>Determination of <strong>prolactin</strong> level is <strong>not useful</strong> in patients with <strong>gynecomastia</strong>.</p>\n<p>For the diagnosis of gynecomastia, serum levels of the following hormones must be checked:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Testosterone</li>\n<li>LH</li>\n<li>FSH</li>\n<li>Estradiol</li>\n<li>hCG</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6979","text":"A 32-year-old man is being​ evaluated for gynecomast​ia. Laboratory investigat​ions reveal low levels of‌ serum testosterone and l‌uteinizing hormone. What ‍is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is being evaluated for gynecomastia. Laboratory investigations reveal low levels of serum testosterone and luteinizing hormone. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testicula​r failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sertoli c​ell tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gonadotropin-secreting tum​or","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Androgen-res​istant state","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sertoli cell tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sertoli cell tumor</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of <strong>gynecomastia</strong> with <strong>low LH and testosterone</strong> is suggestive of a <strong>sertoli cell tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism is also a possible diagnosis with the above clinical presentation and must be ruled out before diagnosing a Sertoli cell tumor. </p>\n<p>The differential diagnosis of gynecomastia is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High LH and low testosterone\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Testicular</strong> <strong>failure</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Low LH and low testosterone\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypogonadotropic</strong> <strong>hypogonadism</strong></li>\n<li>Sertoli cell tumor - increased primary estrogen production </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>High LH and high testosterone\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Androgen-resistant</strong> state</li>\n<li><strong>GnRH-secreting</strong> tumor</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6989","text":"A mother brings her 17-yea​r-old daughter to the gyn​ecologist as she has not ​started menstruating yet.‌ Examination reveals well‌-developed breasts and th‍e absence of pubic and ax​illary hair. Pelvic exami‍nation reveals a short, b‌lind-ended vagina. What i‍s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 17-year-old daughter to the gynecologist as she has not started menstruating yet. Examination reveals well-developed breasts and the absence of pubic and axillary hair.  Pelvic examination reveals a short, blind-ended vagina. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Turner’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stein-Levent​hal syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Premature ov​arian failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complete androgen insensit​ivity syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>complete androgen insensitivity </strong>syndrome.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female phenotype</li>\n<li>Normal breast development</li>\n<li>Blind-ended vagina with absent uterus and ovaries</li>\n<li>Presence of testes in the abdomen or inguinal region</li>\n<li>Scanty pubic and axillary hair</li>\n<li>Primary amenorrhea in late adolescence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: In Turner’s syndrome, breasts are infantile.</p>\n<p>Option B: Stein Leventhal syndrome or polycystic ovarian disorder presents with hirsutism and anovulatory menstrual cycles.</p>\n<p>Option C: Premature ovarian failure presents with secondary amenorrhea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6983","text":"A baby girl is referred to​ a pediatric surgeon for ​suspected bilateral ingui​nal hernias. Further eval‌uation reveals the masses‌ to be testes in the ingu‍inal canal. Which karyoty​pe would you expect to fi‍nd in this child?","raw_text":"A baby girl is referred to a pediatric surgeon for suspected bilateral inguinal hernias. Further evaluation reveals the masses to be testes in the inguinal canal. Which karyotype would you expect to find in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"46​ XX","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"46​ XY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"47 ​XXY","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"47 ​XYY","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 46 XY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 46 XY</strong></p><p>This clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>androgen insensitivity syndrome </strong>(AIS). Affected children have a male karyotype or <strong>46 XY</strong> with a <strong>female phenotype.</strong></p>\n<p>46XY individuals with <strong>complete AIS</strong> (formerly called testicular feminization syndrome) have the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female phenotype</li>\n<li>Normal breast development</li>\n<li>Blind-ended vagina with absent uterus and ovaries</li>\n<li>Scanty pubic and axillary hair</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most patients present with</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inguinal masses </strong>in infancy - Due to undescended testes or hernias containing testes </li>\n<li><strong>Primary amenorrhea</strong> in late adolescence</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Gonadectomy</strong> is a recommended course of treatment due to the possible risks of malignancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6991","text":"An 18-year-old boy is brou​ght to the casualty with ​minor burns following a h​ouse fire. He tells you t‌hat he did not even smell‌ the smoke. Further probi‍ng reveals a history of b​ilateral orchidopexy at t‍he age of 7. Examination ‌findings include eunuchoi‍d habitus, prepubertal te​stes, and microphallus. W‌hat is the likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy is brought to the casualty with minor burns following a house fire. He tells you that he did not even smell the smoke. Further probing reveals a history of bilateral orchidopexy at the age of 7. Examination findings include eunuchoid habitus, prepubertal testes, and microphallus. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary hy​pogonadism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klinefelte​r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kallman ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperprol​actinemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kallman syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kallman syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Anosmia</strong> and absent secondary sexual characteristics in this patient are suggestive of <strong>Kallman</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X-linked</strong> disorder that causes isolated <strong>hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism </strong>due to defective development of the olfactory bulb. This results in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Defective migration</strong> of <strong>GnRH-producing neurons</strong> that originate from the olfactory epithelium</li>\n<li><strong>Aplasia</strong> or hypoplasia of <strong>olfactory bulb </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cryptorchidism</strong></li>\n<li>Absent or <strong>delayed pubertal</strong> changes due to GnRH deficiency</li>\n<li>Anosmia or hyposmia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: Klinefelter syndrome is a cause of primary or hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism.</p>\n<p>Option D: Hyperprolactinemia can cause hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism, but is also associated with gynecomastia, galactorrhea, and bitemporal hemianopia. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6993","text":"What is the most common ca​use of hypergonadotropic ​hypogonadism in males?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism in males?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral o​rchitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klinefelter​’s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kallman ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Noonan ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Klinefelter’s syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Klinefelter’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common </strong>cause of <strong>hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism</strong> in males is <strong>Klinefelter’s syndrome</strong></p>\n<p>It occurs in <strong>males </strong>with a <strong>47 XXY</strong> karyotype and is associated with <strong>testicular dysfunction </strong>and<strong> male infertility</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism in males and females:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital\n<ul>\n<li>Klinefelter's syndrome </li>\n<li><strong>Turner's</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li>Swyer's syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Premature ovarian failure</strong> associated with <strong>fragile X</strong> syndrome and <strong>galactosemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Acquired\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mumps </strong>orchitis</li>\n<li><strong>Testicular torsion, </strong>trauma</li>\n<li>Liver cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Ketoconazole</li>\n<li>Premature ovarian failure</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6994","text":"Which of the following wil​l you recommend in all pa​tients with androgen inse​nsitivity syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following will you recommend in all patients with androgen insensitivity syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonad​ectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Testosterone​ replacement","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vagino​plasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen r​eplacement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gonadectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gonadectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Gonadectomy</strong> is clinically recommended in all patients with<strong> androgen insensitivity syndrome</strong> (AIS) or testicular feminization syndrome due to the risk of <strong>malignancy </strong>arising from the abnormally located testes.</p>\n<p>Options B, C, D: These treatment options may be offered to patients with AIS, but it depends on personal preference and whether they identify as males or females.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6997","text":"A 19-year-old patient is b​eing evaluated for primar​y amenorrhea. Laboratory ​investigations reveal dec‌reased estrogen, FSH, and‌ LH. What is the likely d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old patient is being evaluated for primary amenorrhea. Laboratory investigations reveal decreased estrogen, FSH, and LH. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asherman​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Müllerian​ agenesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kallman ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Turner's​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kallman syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kallman syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased</strong> levels of <strong>estrogen</strong>, <strong>FSH</strong>, and <strong>LH</strong> are suggestive of hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism, which is caused by<strong> Kallman syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X-linked</strong> disorder (KAL gene) that causes isolated <strong>hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism</strong> and anosmia.</p>\n<p>Causes of hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital\n<ul>\n<li>Kallman's syndrome </li>\n<li>Isolated hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (IHH)</li>\n<li><strong>Prader-Willi</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>CHARGE</strong> syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Acquired\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pituitary</strong> tumors, apoplexy</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperprolactinemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anorexia</strong> <strong>nervosa</strong>, excess exercise</li>\n<li>Irradiation</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Asherman syndrome causes secondary amenorrhea due to intrauterine synechiae.</p>\n<p>Option B: Müllerian agenesis presents with primary amenorrhea with normal levels of all hormones.</p>\n<p>Option D: Turner's syndrome causes primary amenorrhea due to hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Fm9xmcXm","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"16_haemophilus","title":"16.Haemophilus","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2526","text":"A woman presents with feve​r with chills and a produ​ctive cough. Chest X-ray ‌is suggestive of lobar pn‌eumonia. She had undergon​e splenectomy 2 years bac‌k. Sputum culture on bloo‌d agar shows the followin‌g. What is the causative ​agent?","raw_text":"A woman presents with fever with chills and a productive cough. Chest X-ray is suggestive of lobar pneumonia. She had undergone splenectomy 2 years back. Sputum culture on blood agar shows the following. What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemophilus​ influenzae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella ​granulomatis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neisseria m​eningitidis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemophilus influenzae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e5136f1f68646819d4ded2d2a7aa01c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b57104c49f454f59952532bbc64426c9x510x453.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemophilus influenzae</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>bacterial pneumonia</strong> in a<strong> splenectomised</strong> individual along with the image showing <strong>satellitism</strong> on blood agar points towards an infection due to <strong><em>Hemophilus influenzae.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Pneumonia can occur as a part of <strong>overwhelming post-splenectomy infection</strong> (OPSI). This is a fatal post-splenectomy complication occuring due to infections with <strong>encapsulated organisms</strong> such as <em>S. pneumoniae,<strong> H. influenzae</strong></em>, and <em>N. meningitidis.</em> It can be prevented by vaccinating against these organisms within 2 weeks of undergoing splenectomy. </p>\n<p><strong>Satellitism</strong> is seen when<strong><em> S. aureus</em> is streaked</strong> across a plate of <strong>blood agar</strong> on which <em>H. influenzae</em> has been inoculated. After overnight incubation, the <strong>colonies of <em>H. influenzae</em></strong> will be large and well <strong>developed alongside the streak</strong> of <em>Staphylococcus</em> and smaller when they are farther away from the streak. This is due to the <strong>release of factor V</strong> by lysis of RBCs mediated by <em>S. aureus</em>. Factor V is required for the growth of <em>H. influenzae</em>.</p>\n<p>The given image shows satellitism on blood agar </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2535","text":"A patient presented with f​ever, seizures, and alter​ed sensorium. Gram stain ‌of the CSF is given below‌. Satellitism was seen on​ blood agar. Which of the‌ following statements is ‌false about this disease?‌","raw_text":"A  patient presented with fever, seizures, and altered sensorium. Gram stain of the CSF is given below. Satellitism was seen on blood agar. Which of the following statements is false about this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This is the most serious d​isease produced by this o​rganism.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The disease is more common​ in young adults.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The bacilli reach the meni​nges from the nasopharynx​ through the bloodstream.‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefotaxime is the drug of ​choice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The disease is more common in young adults.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce4f339b9b1a4cad96d03cd29318977d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The disease is more common in young adults.</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the image showing <strong>gram-negative coccobacilli</strong> in the CSF are suggestive of <strong>h</strong><strong>emophilus meningitis</strong> caused by <em><strong>H.influenzae.</strong></em> Hemophilus meningitis is more <strong>common</strong> in <strong>children</strong> between 3 months and 3 years of age and not common in young adults. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Meningitis</strong> is the <strong>most serious</strong> disease caused by <em>H. influenzae</em>. The case fatality rate is 90% if untreated.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em>H. influenze</em> is transmitted via <strong>respiratory droplets</strong>. Thus, it is introduced into the<strong> nasopharynx</strong> of the contacts of an infected individual. It has to enter the <strong>bloodstream</strong> to reach the <strong>meninges</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cefotaxime</strong> or <strong>ceftazidime</strong> are the <strong>drugs of choice</strong> for the treatment of hemophilus meningitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2528","text":"Which of the following is ​not caused by non-typable​ H. influenzae?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not caused by non-typable H. influenzae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menin​gitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Otitis​ media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sinu​sitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Puerpera​l sepsis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meningitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meningitis</strong></p><p><strong>Meningitis</strong> is caused by <strong>typable</strong> <em>H. influenzae</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-typable</strong> <em>H. influenzae</em> cause:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Otitis media</li>\n<li>Sinusitis</li>\n<li>Puerperal sepsis</li>\n<li>Exacerbations of COPD. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>H. influenzae</em> is typed into <strong>6 serotypes</strong> (a to f) based on the <strong>capsular polysaccharide</strong>. <strong>Serotype<em> </em>b</strong> (Hib) is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>virulent</strong> and accounts for most of the invasive infections.</p>\n<p>The differences between type b strains and non-typable strains of <em>H. influenzae</em> are given in the table below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 241px; width: 391px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type B strains</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Nontypable strains</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Capsule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">Made of Ribosyl-ribitol phosphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\">Unencapsulated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Pathogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">Invasive infections due to hematogenous spread</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\">Mucosal infections due to contagious spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Clinical manifestations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Meningitis and invasive infections incompletely immunized infants and children</li>\n<li>Epiglottitis</li>\n<li>Cellulitis</li>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Otitis media in children</li>\n<li>Exacerbation of COPD, </li>\n<li>Puerperal sepsis</li>\n<li>Sinusitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Vaccine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">Highly effective conjugate vaccines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\">Not available</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2531","text":"All of the following Hemop​hilus species require bot​h Factors X and V except_‌______.","raw_text":"All of the following Hemophilus species require both Factors X and V  except_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H. hemo​lyticus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H. inf​luenzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H. parahe​molyticus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"H. aeg​ypticus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. H. parahemolyticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. H. parahemolyticus</strong></p><p><strong><em>Hemophilus parahemolyticus</em></strong> requires <strong>only</strong> <strong>factor V</strong> for its growth. It does not require factors X.</p>\n<p>Hemophilus species requiring <strong>both factors X and V: </strong>(Mnemonic to remember: <strong>HAI</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>H. <strong>h</strong>emolyticus</em></li>\n<li><em>H. <strong>a</strong>egypticus</em></li>\n<li><em>H.<strong> i</strong>nfluenzae</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Factor V- </strong>It refers to the co-enzyme<strong>, NAD.</strong> It is heat-labile (destroyed at 120°C in a few minutes) and present<strong> inside RBC.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Factor X- </strong>It refers to <strong>Hemin.</strong> It is heat stable and is present in the<strong> blood.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 173px; width: 322px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 54px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Species</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 54px; text-align: center;\"><strong>X factor requirement</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 54px; text-align: center;\"><strong>V factor requirement</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><em>H. influenzae</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\"><em>H. aegypticus</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 21px; text-align: center;\"><em>H. ducreyi</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 21px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 21px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 21px; text-align: center;\"><em>H. parainfluenza</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 21px; text-align: center;\">−</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 21px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><em>H. haemolyticus</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\"><em>H. parahaemolyticus</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.609px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\">−</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.3906px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2536","text":"A 7-year-old girl presents​ to the emergency with ac​ute stridor. The mother g‌ives a history of fever, ‌drooling, and breathing d​ifficulty since that morn‌ing. An X-ray of her neck‌ appears as given below. ‌What is the most likely c​ausative agent?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl presents to the emergency with acute stridor. The mother gives a history of fever, drooling, and breathing difficulty since that morning. An X-ray of her neck appears as given below. What is the most likely causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemophilus p​arainfluenzae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemophilus​ influenzae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Parainflu​enza virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemophilus influenzae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9d8188a43b9e4ec2b62c8326b9b5b477.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85b3819b744441f8a406d6c728187f6bx720x804.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemophilus influenzae</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and '<strong>thumb sign</strong>' on the X-ray are suggestive of <strong>acute epiglottitis</strong> caused by <em><strong>Hemophilus influenzae</strong>. </em></p>\n<p>It is an acute inflammation of the epiglottis causing <strong>obstructive laryngitis </strong>and presents with high fever, severe sore throat, tachycardia, systemic toxicity, and drooling as patients are unable to swallow. The patient characteristically <strong>leans forward while sitting</strong> for ease of breathing.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis often is made on clinical grounds. On direct fiberoptic laryngoscopy, the typical edematous <strong>cherry-red epiglottis</strong> is visualized. Neck radiographs reveal an enlarged edematous epiglottis (<strong>T</strong><strong>humb sign</strong>). The below image shows the Thumb sign.</p>\n<p>In some countries, widespread <strong>vaccination against Hib</strong> has drastically <strong>decreased</strong> acute epiglottitis cases in children by over 90%. However, the incidence in adults has remained largely unchanged since the Hib vaccine was introduced. Despite vaccination efforts, many pediatric cases of <strong>epiglottitis</strong> today are still <strong>caused by Hib </strong>due to either lack of vaccination or vaccine ineffectiveness. In adults and increasingly in children, other bacterial pathogens, particularly <strong>group A Streptococcus</strong>, have been linked to cases of epiglottitis.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>airway management</strong> followed by administration of antibiotics like <strong>ampicillin/sulbactam, cefotaxime, or ceftriaxone. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6642","text":"Pneumonia due to a gram-ne​gative pleomorphic bacill​us growing on chocolate a‌gar which is catalase and‌ oxidase-positive is:","raw_text":"Pneumonia due to a gram-negative pleomorphic bacillus growing on chocolate agar which is catalase and oxidase-positive is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E.c​oli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseud​omonas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Haemophilus​ influenzae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Actin​omyces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Haemophilus influenzae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Haemophilus influenzae</strong></p><p>Pneumonia due to a <strong>gram-negative pleomorphic bacillus</strong> growing on <strong>chocolate agar</strong> which is <strong>catalase </strong>and<strong> oxidase-positive</strong> is most likely <em><strong>Haemophilus influenza.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Haemophilus pneumonia typically <strong>occurs in infants</strong> and is accompanied by <strong>empyema</strong> and sometimes <strong>meningitis</strong> as well. In older children and adults, the picture is of <strong>lobar pneumonia</strong>. Sputum is collected for the detection and isolation of Hemophilus. As the bacillus is very sensitive to low temperatures, specimens should never be refrigerated before inoculation. <em>H.infiuenzae</em> is a Gram-negative, <strong>non-motile, non-sporing</strong> bacillus, exhibiting considerable pleomorphism. In sputum, it usually occurs as clusters of coccobacillary forms.</p>\n<p>The bacillus has<strong> fastidious</strong> <strong>growth requirements</strong>. The accessory growth factors, named<strong> X and V</strong>, present in the blood are essential for growth. Catalase and<br/>oxidase reactions are positive. Nitrates are reduced to nitrites.<strong> Biotyping</strong> has been done on the basis of <strong>indole production</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cultures</strong> may be preserved for about a month on <strong>chocolate agar</strong> slopes. Chocolate agar is made by heating blood agar to 80-90°C for 15-20 minutes. This causes the <strong>release of V factor</strong> from within the erythrocytes.</p>\n<p>Treatment- <strong>Amoxycillin-clavulanate </strong>or<strong> clarithromycin</strong> is more effective in <strong>treating</strong> respiratory infections by <strong><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2538","text":"A middle-aged man who is a​ known case of diabetes a​nd chronic bronchitis pre‌sents to the OPD with low‌-grade fever and producti​ve cough for 5 days. Whic‌h of the following is not‌ a virulence factor of th‌e likely causative organi​sm?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man who is a known case of diabetes and chronic bronchitis presents to the OPD with low-grade fever and productive cough for 5 days. Which of the following is not a virulence factor of the likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer membrane protein (OM​P)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipooligosac​charide (LOS)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capsular po​lysaccharide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pi​li","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Capsular polysaccharide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Capsular polysaccharide</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes an acute <strong>exacerbation</strong> <strong>of COPD</strong> characterized by low-grade fever, increased cough, sputum production, and shortness of breath<strong>.</strong> The most common cause of these are <strong>non-typable <em>H. influenzae</em></strong> which <strong>lack</strong> a <strong>capsule</strong> and so capsular antigen cannot be a virulence factor.</p>\n<p>All<em><strong> H. influenzae</strong> </em>have the following <strong>virulence factors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer membrane proteins (OMP)</li>\n<li>Lipooligosaccharide(LOS)</li>\n<li>Pili</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In addition to the above factors, <strong><em>H. influenzae</em> type b</strong> also has its <strong>capsular polysaccharide</strong> as a virulence factor.</p>\n<p>The differences between Hib and non-typable strains are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 241px; width: 391px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type b strains</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Nontypable strains</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Capsule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">Made of Ribosyl-ribitol phosphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\">Unencapsulated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Pathogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">Invasive infections due to hematogenous spread</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\">Mucosal infections due to contagious spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Clinical manifestations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Meningitis and invasive infections incompletely immunized infants and children</li>\n<li>Epiglottitis</li>\n<li>Cellulitis</li>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Otitis media in children</li>\n<li>Exacerbation of COPD, </li>\n<li>Puerperal sepsis</li>\n<li>Sinusitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95px; text-align: center;\">Vaccine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px; text-align: center;\">Highly effective conjugate vaccines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; text-align: center;\">Not available</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2533","text":"Which of the following bac​teria does not belong to ​the HACEK group?","raw_text":"Which of the following bacteria does not belong to the HACEK group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemophilus ​parainfluenza","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aggregatibacter actinomyce​temcomitans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Escheric​hia coli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kingell​a kingae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Escherichia coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Escherichia coli</strong></p><p><em>Escherichia coli </em>does not belong to the HACEK group.</p>\n<p>The acronym <strong>HACEK</strong> refers to a group of <strong>fastidious slow-growing</strong> bacteria, normally residing in the mouth. These can sometimes cause severe infections, particularly culture-negative <strong>subacute bacterial endocarditis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The group includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>aemophilus species (<em>H. parainfluenzae, H. aphrophilus, H. paraphrophilu</em>s)</li>\n<li><em><strong>A</strong>ggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans </em>(previously called as <em>Actinobacillus)</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>C</strong>ardiobacterium hominis</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>E</strong>ikenella corrodens</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>K</strong>ingella kingae</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2532","text":"A 26-year-old man presente​d with painful, bleeding ​genital ulcers. On examin‌ation, tender inguinal ly‌mphadenopathy is present.​ Scraping from the ulcer ‌base was taken and the mi‌croscopic appearance of t‌he Gram-stained smear is ​shown below. What is the ​causative organism?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man presented with painful, bleeding genital ulcers. On examination, tender inguinal lymphadenopathy is present. Scraping from the ulcer base was taken and the microscopic appearance of the Gram-stained smear is shown below. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemophil​us ducreyi","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Treponema​ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis sero​var L1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis sero​var L2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Haemophilus ducreyi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65d9a8b708304e70ab69da7d9cff7bcf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Haemophilus ducreyi</strong></p><p>The image shows the' <strong>school of fish' </strong>or '<strong>r</strong><strong>ailroad track' </strong>appearance of<em> Haemophilus </em><em>ducreyi.</em> This appearance is seen when the bacilli are arranged in small groups, whorls or in chains. The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>chancroid.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Chancroid</strong> is characterized by <strong>non-indurated, tender</strong>, irregular <strong>ulcers</strong> that may bleed on touch. The neighbouring <strong>lymph nodes</strong> are <strong>enlarged, painful</strong>, and tender. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B - <em>Treponema pallidum</em> causes <strong>syphilis</strong>, characterised by <strong>chancre</strong> - a single <strong>painless</strong> ulcer with a clean base and a firm raised border, with painless lymphadenopathy. Persistent <span class=\"s1\">or </span>multiple chancres may be <span class=\"s1\">seen </span>in HIV-infected or other immunodeficient patients.</p>\n<p>Options C and D - <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> serovars L1, L2 and L3 cause <strong>lymphogranuloma venereum</strong>, characterised by shallow, <strong>painless ulcers</strong> that progress to painful lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6938","text":"The following are true abo​ut chancroid: A. School o​f fish appearance B. Blee‌ds on touch C. Painful D.‌ Groove sign","raw_text":"<p>The following are true about chancroid:</p>\n<p>A. School of fish appearance<br/>B. Bleeds on touch<br/>C. Painful<br/>D. Groove sign</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C &amp;amp;​ D only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A, B,C ​&amp;amp; D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A B &amp;​amp; C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A &amp;amp;​ B only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A B & C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3b2416921a84acfb8e49ed7a157a098x720x705.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A B &amp; C</strong></p><p><strong>Chancroid</strong> is a <strong>painful</strong><strong> genital ulcer</strong> caused by<strong><em> Haemophilus ducreyi,</em></strong><em> </em>which is a gram-negative coccobacillus.<strong> </strong>Microscopic appearance of <em>H. ducreyi</em> is called the<strong> school of fish appearance </strong>or<strong> railroad appearance. </strong>Chancroid ulcers have a soft base that bleeds easily to touch. They also cause painful lymphadenopathy. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the 'school-of-fish' appearance characteristic of<em> H. ducreyi.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Groove sign</strong> is a characteristic sign seen in <strong>lymphogranuloma venereum</strong>. It refers to the depression created by the inguinal ligament as it passes between the groups of inflamed inguinal lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0629","text":"Which of the following is ​not involved in urethriti​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not involved in urethritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trich​omonas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chla​mydia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonoc​occus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H.du​creyi","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. H.ducreyi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. H.ducreyi</strong></p><p><strong>Haemophilus ducreyi</strong> does not cause urethritis. It causes <strong>chancroid</strong> (soft chancre), which presents as a genital ulcer.</p>\n<p><strong>Urethritis</strong> is classified into two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gonococcal</strong> urethritis - caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoea.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Non-gonococcal</strong> urethritis - caused by:\n<ul>\n<li><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> (most common)</li>\n<li><em>Ureaplasma urealyticum</em></li>\n<li><em>Adenovirus</em></li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus vaginalis</em></li>\n<li><em>Mycoplasma genitalium</em></li>\n<li><em>Tricomonas vaginalis</em> (rare)</li>\n<li><em>Herpes simplex</em> virus (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2534","text":"A 5-year-old girl presents​ with conjunctivitis, fev​er, and hemorrhagic skin ‌lesions. There's a histor‌y of travel to Brazil. Wh​ich of the following etio‌logical agents would you ‌suspect in this case?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl presents with conjunctivitis, fever, and hemorrhagic skin lesions. There's a history of travel to Brazil. Which of the following etiological agents would you suspect in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemophilus influenzae typ​e A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilus parahaemolytic​us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Haemophilus​ aegypticus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Haemophilus parainfluenzae​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Haemophilus aegypticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Haemophilus aegypticus</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>Brazilian</strong> <strong>purpuric</strong> <strong>fever</strong> (BPF) caused by <em><strong>Haemophilus aegypticus </strong></em>(Koch-Weeks bacillus). </p>\n<p>Recent DNA studies have shown that the bacillus is identical to non-capsulated <em>H.influenzae </em>and it is now named <strong><em>H.influenzae </em>biotype aegypticus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is worldwide in distribution and causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A highly contagious form of <strong>conjunctivitis</strong> ('pink eye') which is especially <strong>common in the tropics</strong> and subtropics and may lead to <strong>epidemics</strong>. It responds to local sulphonamide or gentamicin administration.</li>\n<li><strong>Brazilian purpuric fever</strong> characterized by <strong>fever, purpura, </strong>and <strong>conjunctivitis</strong> which proceeds to <strong>fulminant septicemia</strong> in infants and children. It has a <strong>high fatality</strong> rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"DHnDOjtZ","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"16_puerperium","title":"16.Puerperium","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1761","text":"When does puerperium begin​?","raw_text":"When does puerperium begin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Soon after delivery of fet​us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Soon after expulsion of pl​acenta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"After the fourth stage of ​labour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 hours after child birth​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Soon after expulsion of placenta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Soon after expulsion of placenta</strong></p><p><strong>Puerperium</strong> begins as soon as the <strong>placenta </strong>is <strong>expelled</strong> and lasts for approximately <strong>6 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the period following childbirth during which body tissues <strong>revert</strong> back approximately to the <strong>pre-pregnant state,</strong> both anatomically and physiologically.</p>\n<p>Puerperium can be divided as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediate</strong> puerperium: within 24 hours</li>\n<li><strong>Early</strong> puerperium: up to 7 days</li>\n<li><strong>Remote/late</strong> puerperium: up to 6 weeks or 42 days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1766","text":"How long does early puerpe​rium last?","raw_text":"How long does early puerperium last?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Up to 24 hrs after deliver​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Up to 48 hrs after deliver​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Up to 72 hrs after deliver​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Up to 1 week after deliver​y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Up to 1 week after delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Up to 1 week after delivery</strong></p><p>Early puerperium is considered to last up to <strong>1 week</strong> after delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Puerperium</strong> can be divided as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediate</strong> puerperium: within 24 hours</li>\n<li><strong>Early</strong> puerperium: up to 7 days</li>\n<li><strong>Remote/late</strong> puerperium: up to 6 weeks or 42 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1767","text":"What is the weight of the ​uterus immediately after ​delivery?","raw_text":"What is the weight of the uterus immediately after delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30​0 g","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10​0 g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100​0 g","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"50​0 g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1000 g","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1000 g</strong></p><p><strong>Immediately</strong> following delivery, the uterus weighs <strong>1000 g</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Involution</strong> of the uterus occurs due to a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>size</strong> of <strong>muscle fibers.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 553px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Time following delivery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Weight of the uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 96px;\">Immediately following delivery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Uterus corresponds to 20 weeks gestational age</p>\n<p>Fundus lies just below the umbilicus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 96px;\">1000 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">48 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\">Myometrial involution starts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\"> -</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">1 week</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\">Rapid involution of uterus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\">500 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">2 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\">The uterus becomes a <strong>pelvic</strong> organ</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\">300 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">4 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Clinically,</strong> involution is complete</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\">100 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 109px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 109px;\">6-8 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 109px;\">\n<p>Uterus returns to normal (pre-pregnant) size</p>\n<p><strong>Sonographically,</strong> involution is complete at 8 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 109px;\">60 g</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1771","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding puerperium​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding puerperium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The number of muscle fibre​s decrease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The myometrial cell size d​ecreases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The uterus weighs 1000g by​ 1 week postpartum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is decreased activit​y of uterine collagenase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The myometrial cell size decreases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The myometrial cell size decreases</strong></p><p>During puerperium, the number of muscle fibers is not decreased, but there is a substantial <strong>reduction </strong>in the <strong>myometrial cell size.</strong></p>\n<p>There is an <strong>increased</strong> activity of <strong>uterine collagenases</strong> and release of proteolytic enzymes.</p>\n<p>Within the puerperal uterus, larger blood vessels become obliterated by hyaline changes. These vessels are gradually resorbed and replaced by smaller ones. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 553px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Time following delivery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Weight of the uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 96px;\">Immediately following delivery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Uterus corresponds to 20 weeks gestational age</p>\n<p>Fundus lies just below the umbilicus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 96px;\">1000 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">48 hours</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\">Myometrial involution starts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\"> -</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">1 week</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\">Rapid involution of uterus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\">500 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">2 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\">The uterus becomes a <strong>pelvic</strong> organ</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\">300 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 18px;\">4 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Clinically,</strong> involution is complete</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 18px;\">100 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 109px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150.5px; height: 109px;\">6-8 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 288.5px; height: 109px;\">\n<p>Uterus returns to normal (pre-pregnant) size</p>\n<p><strong>Sonographically,</strong> involution is complete at 8 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; height: 109px;\">60 g</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1780","text":"After how many weeks of po​stpartum will the uterus ​becomes a pelvic organ?","raw_text":"After how many weeks of postpartum will the uterus becomes a pelvic organ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 w​eek","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 weeks</strong></p><p>The uterus becomes a <strong>pelvic</strong> organ by <strong>2 weeks </strong>of postpartum.</p>\n<p><strong>Involution</strong> of the uterus occurs due to a decrease in the size of muscle fibers.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Time following delivery</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Weight of the uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immediately following delivery</td>\n<td>\n<p>Uterus corresponds to 20 weeks gestational age</p>\n<p>Fundus lies just below the umbilicus</p>\n</td>\n<td>1000 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>48 hours </td>\n<td>Myometrial involution starts</td>\n<td> -</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 week</td>\n<td>Rapid involution of uterus</td>\n<td>500 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 weeks</td>\n<td>The uterus becomes a <strong>pelvic</strong> organ</td>\n<td>300 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 weeks</td>\n<td><strong>Clinically,</strong> involution is complete</td>\n<td>100 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6-8 weeks</td>\n<td>\n<p>Uterus returns to normal (pre-pregnant) size</p>\n<p><strong>Sonographically,</strong> involution is complete at 8 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td>60 g</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1783","text":"When does the uterus retur​n to pregravid size sonog​raphically?","raw_text":"When does the uterus return to pregravid size sonographically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8 w​eeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8 weeks</strong></p><p>The uterus and endometrium return to pregravid size by<strong> 8 weeks </strong>when assessed by <strong>sonography</strong>.</p>\n<p>On <strong>clinical </strong>examination, the <strong>involution </strong>of the uterus is complete by<strong> 4 weeks.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Time following delivery</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Weight of the uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immediately following delivery</td>\n<td>\n<p>Uterus corresponds to 20 weeks gestational age</p>\n<p>Fundus lies just below the umbilicus</p>\n</td>\n<td>1000 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>48 hours </td>\n<td>Myometrial involution starts</td>\n<td> -</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 week</td>\n<td>Rapid involution of uterus</td>\n<td>500 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 weeks</td>\n<td>The uterus becomes a <strong>pelvic</strong> organ</td>\n<td>300 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 weeks</td>\n<td><strong>Clinically,</strong> involution is complete</td>\n<td>100 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6-8 weeks</td>\n<td>\n<p>Uterus returns to normal (pre-pregnant) size</p>\n<p><strong>Sonographically,</strong> involution is complete at 8 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td>60 g</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1770","text":"External os reverts back t​o the pre-pregnant state ​by how many weeks?","raw_text":"External os reverts back to the pre-pregnant state by how many weeks?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Never ​returns","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Never returns","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0a5a7db10c74d5c9e8141c9dfffe103x1280x2111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Never returns</strong></p><p><strong>External os never</strong> <strong>returns</strong> back to normal whereas the <strong>contour</strong> of the cervix takes <strong>6 weeks</strong> to regain normalcy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1772","text":"Endometrial regeneration f​ollowing delivery starts ​by which day?","raw_text":"Endometrial regeneration following delivery starts by which day?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5th​ day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6th​ day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th​ day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10th​ day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7th day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7th day</strong></p><p>Endometrial regeneration starts by <strong>7<sup>th</sup> day</strong> and is completed by the <strong>10<sup>th</sup> day</strong>. Regeneration of the epithelium at the <strong>placental site</strong> takes about <strong>6 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>Endometrium regenerates from <strong>zona basalis </strong>which remains intact and is the source of new endometrium. This arises from proliferation of the endometrial glandular remnants and the stroma of the interglandular connective tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9812","text":"A 34-year-old woman has ju​st delivered a boy by spo​ntaneous vaginal delivery​. How often should her BP‌ be recorded for the next​ 2 hours?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman has just delivered a boy by spontaneous vaginal delivery. How often should her BP be recorded for the next 2 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Every 5​ minutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Every 15​ minutes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Every 30​ minutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Every ​1 hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Every 15 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Every 15 minutes</strong></p><p>For the first 2 hours following delivery, <strong>blood pressure </strong>and<strong> pulse</strong> should be recorded <strong>every 15 minutes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Temperature is assessed every 4 hours for the first 8 hours after delivery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1784","text":"A woman on post-natal day ​1 presented with abdomina​l pain which worsens when​ she is breastfeeding. Wh‌at is this known as?","raw_text":"A woman on post-natal day 1 presented with abdominal pain which worsens when she is breastfeeding. What is this known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After-​labour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Involuti​onal pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterin​e colic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After​pains","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Afterpains","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Afterpains</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>afterpains </strong>which are seen during <strong>puerperium</strong>.</p>\n<p>In primiparous women, the uterus tends to remain tonically contracted following delivery. In multiparous women, it often contracts vigorously at intervals and gives rise to afterpains, which are similar to but <strong>milder </strong>than<strong> labor contractions</strong>.</p>\n<p>These are more pronounced as parity increases and <strong>worsen when the infant suckles</strong>, likely because of oxytocin release. Afterpains gradually<strong> decrease</strong> in intensity and become mild by the <strong>third day</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1787","text":"What would be the average ​duration of lochial disch​arge in a puerperal woman​?","raw_text":"What would be the average duration of lochial discharge in a puerperal woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-15​ days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10-20​ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24-36​ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"36-42​ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 24-36 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 24-36 days</strong></p><p>The average duration of lochial discharge in a postpartum woman is about <strong>24-36</strong> days. The average amount of discharge for the first week is <strong>250 mL</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lochial discharge appears as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 275px; width: 481px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\"><strong>Name</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\"><strong>Colour</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.9375px;\"><strong>Timing after delivery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.0625px;\"><strong>Content</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\"><strong>Lochia rubra</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Red</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.9375px;\">First few days </td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.0625px;\">Blood, decidua, fetal membranes, vernix, lanugo, meconium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\"><strong>Lochia serosa</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Yellowish/Pink</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.9375px;\">After 3-4 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.0625px;\">\n<p>WBC, cervical mucus, microorganisms</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 60px;\"><strong>Lochia alba</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Pale White</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.9375px;\">After 10th day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.0625px;\">Decidua, cells, WBC, mucin, cholesterol crystals, and fat cells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1790","text":"What is the contribution o​f postpartum diuresis to ​weight loss?","raw_text":"What is the contribution of postpartum diuresis to weight loss?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2​ kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-3​ kg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3-4​ kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4-5​ kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2-3 kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2-3 kg</strong></p><p>Postpartum diuresis results in a relatively rapid weight loss of <strong>2 to 3 kg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Decreased estrogen levels</strong> are associated with the diuresis of excess extracellular fluid accumulated during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1794","text":"What is the single most im​portant galactopoetic hor​mone in post-natal women?​","raw_text":"What is the single most important galactopoetic hormone in post-natal women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prol​actin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proges​terone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prolactin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prolactin</strong></p><p><strong>Prolactin </strong>is the single most important galactopoietic hormone in postnatal women.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 321px;\" width=\"393\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.640625px;\"><strong>Function</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.984375px;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.390625px;\"><strong>Hormone</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.640625px;\">Mammogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.984375px;\">Breast growth and development</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.390625px;\">\n<p>Estrogen (ductal growth)</p>\n<p>Progesterone (alveolar growth)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.640625px;\">Lactogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.984375px;\">Initiation of milk secretion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.390625px;\">Prolactin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.640625px;\">Galactokinesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.984375px;\">Ejection of milk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.390625px;\">Oxytocin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.640625px;\">Galactopoiesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.984375px;\">Maintainence of lactation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.390625px;\">Prolactin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1804","text":"A woman presents with numb​ness and paraesthesia alo​ng the anterolateral thig​h two days after vaginal ‌delivery. Which nerve is ​likely involved in this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A woman presents with numbness and paraesthesia along the anterolateral thigh two days after vaginal delivery. Which nerve is likely involved in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femora​l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iliohypoga​stric nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ilioingui​nal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral femoral cutaneous ​nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>meralgia paraesthetica</strong> which is caused by injury to the <strong>lateral femoral cutaneous nerve. </strong></p>\n<p>The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is at risk of injury during<strong> prolonged pushing</strong> with hip flexion as the nerve is <strong>compressed</strong> under the <strong>inguinal ligament.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is the<strong> most common </strong>obstetrical <strong>neuropathy</strong> after <strong>vaginal delivery,</strong> followed by femoral nerve involvement.</p>\n<p>Note: The nerves most commonly injured during <strong>cesarean</strong> section are the <strong>iliohypogastric </strong>and <strong>ilioinguinal </strong>nerves.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4461","text":"Which among the following ​is the most common presen​tation of postpartum nerv​e injury?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common presentation of postpartum nerve injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial​ palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wrist​ drop","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foot​ drop","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Claw​ hand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foot drop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foot drop</strong></p><p>The most common postpartum <strong>nerve injury</strong> is an injury to the <strong>peroneal nerve</strong> causing <strong>foot drop</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve</strong> is the most commonly injured nerve after vaginal delivery <strong>followed by femoral neuropathies</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Obstetrical neuropathies </strong>are due to the pressure on branches of the<strong> lumbosacral nerve plexus </strong>during labor. They can manifest as complaints of <strong>intense neuralgia or cramp-like pains</strong> extending down one or both legs as soon as the head descends into the pelvis. <strong>Footdrop</strong> can be secondary to injury at the level of the lumbosacral plexus, sciatic nerve, or common fibular (peroneal) nerve. Injury of the <strong>iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves</strong> may occur with <strong>cesarean delivery, </strong>which may manifest as an <strong>inguinal hernia</strong> and <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>sensation</strong> at the <strong>pubic</strong> region respectively.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Note: Since there are no options relating to intense neuralgia in the lower limb, foot drop becomes the best possible answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1805","text":"Puerperal fever is defined​ as a temperature of ____​_.","raw_text":"Puerperal fever is defined as a temperature of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"≥ 37 ​.2 °C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"≥ 37​.5 °C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"≥ 3​8 °C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"≥ 38.​ 5 °C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ≥ 38 °C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ≥ 38 °C</strong></p><p><strong>Puerperal fever</strong> is defined as the temperature of <strong>38 °C or higher </strong>(100.4 °F) recorded on 2 separate occasions 24 hours apart within the first 10 days following delivery. This excludes the first 24 hours.</p>\n<p>In the first 24 hours, a slight rise of temperature is not uncommon and hence the first 24 hours are excluded while defining puerperal pyrexia.</p>\n<p>Note: Generally, fever in an adult is defined as an oral temperature of &gt;37.5 °C.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9811","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen​ts with high-grade fever ​and foul-smelling lochia ​2 days after vaginal deli‌very. Lower abdominal ten​derness is elicited on ex​amination. Her WBC count ‌is 25,000/ microlitre. Wh‌ich is the commonest rout​e for the spread of her l​ikely condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with high-grade fever and foul-smelling lochia 2 days after vaginal delivery. Lower abdominal tenderness is elicited on examination. Her WBC count is 25,000/ microlitre. Which is the commonest route for the spread of her likely condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymp​hatic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct ​invasion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemato​genous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aer​osol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct invasion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct invasion</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario of high-grade fever, foul-smelling lochia, and pelvic tenderness is suggestive of <strong>puerperal sepsis. </strong>The most common route of spread of this condition is <strong>direct spread.</strong></p>\n<p><strong><em>Streptococcus sp. </em></strong>is the most important causative organism of puerperal sepsis. The commonest manifestation is <strong>endometritis.</strong></p>\n<p>The route of delivery is an important risk factor for the development of puerperal sepsis (cesarean&gt; vaginal). The most common site of puerperal infection is the <strong>placental site</strong> in <strong>vaginal</strong> delivery and <strong>uterine incision</strong> in <strong>cesarean</strong> section.</p>\n<p>The only proven way of decreasing uterine infection following <strong>cesarean</strong> section is administrating a single dose of <strong>ampicillin</strong> or 1st generation <strong>cephalosporin</strong> at the time of caesarean delivery. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7514","text":"What is the first line of ​treatment of mastitis in ​a lactating mother?","raw_text":"What is the first line of treatment of mastitis in a lactating mother?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diclox​acillin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cefa​zolin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftr​iaxone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ampic​illin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dicloxacillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dicloxacillin</strong></p><p><strong>Dicloxacillin</strong> is the first line of treatment for <strong>mastitis</strong> in lactating mothers and should be taken for 10-14 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Mastitis</strong> refers to the infection of the breast parenchymal tissue. The most common organism causing breast infection is <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>. It is common in new mothers at the <strong>beginning of lactation</strong> who are inexperienced with breastfeeding or <strong>during weaning</strong> when nipple trauma occurs due to the infant's growing teeth. </p>\n<p>Clinical features of mastitis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fever and malaise</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Localized pain and tenderness </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Purulent nipple discharge</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Redness and swelling of the affected breast</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive fluctuation test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A culture from nipple discharge or test-needle aspiration of the cellulitic area is done to confirm the organism and its antibiotic sensitivity. An ultrasonogram of the involved breast is done to exclude abscess. They are treated with antibiotics and adequate breast support. Choice of antibiotics in lactational mastitis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Dicloxacillin </strong>500 mg 4 times a day is to be started empirically.</li>\n<li><strong>Erythromycin</strong> is given to women who are <strong>penicillin-sensitive</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the infection is caused by resistant, penicillinase-producing Staphylococcus species then vancomycin, clindamycin, or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is given for at least 10 days.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1808","text":"A nursing mother in her th​ird week postpartum prese​nts with breast pain and ​difficulty in breastfeedi‌ng. On examination, warmt​h and tenderness over the​ left breast are noted. H‌er temperature is 38.3 °C‌ and her WBC count is 13,​000/mm3. Which of the fol​lowing is false regarding‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A nursing mother in her third week postpartum presents with breast pain and difficulty in breastfeeding. On examination, warmth and tenderness over the left breast are noted. Her temperature is 38.3 °C and her WBC count is 13,000/mm3. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common etiological ag​ent is streptococcus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate source of infect​ion is the infant's nose ​and throat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complication includes toxi​c shock syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence is less than 1 %​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Most common etiological agent is streptococcus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f36918852ba149a89f98d17fb64e9a5f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Most common etiological agent is streptococcus</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette of breast pain, fever, and leucocytosis with the image showing a red and swollen breast suggests a diagnosis of <strong>mastitis</strong>. The most common etiological agent is <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>Mastitis refers to infection of the breast parenchymal tissue. Symptoms usually appear in the <strong>3rd or 4th week</strong>. The infection is usually <strong>unilateral,</strong> with the <strong>source</strong> of infection being the <strong>infant's nose and throat</strong>. Predisposing factors are fissures, abrasions, and nipple cracks.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong> should be <strong>continued</strong>. If the infant is unable to nurse on the inflamed breast, the mother may resort to breast <strong>pumping</strong>. When nursing bilaterally, suckling is best started on the uninvolved breast.</li>\n<li><strong>Dicloxacillin</strong> is the first line of treatment of mastitis. Erythromycin is given to women who are penicillin-sensitive.</li>\n<li>If an abscess forms, <strong>surgical treatment</strong> is done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1810","text":"A mother started to feel s​ad and tearful over the f​irst two weeks following ​childbirth. She felt over‌whelmed by the responsibi​lities of nursing her bab​y and changing diapers in‌ the middle of the night.‌ She felt that she was a ​failure as a mother. What​ is false regarding this ‍condition?","raw_text":"A mother started to feel sad and tearful over the first two weeks following childbirth. She felt overwhelmed by the responsibilities of nursing her baby and changing diapers in the middle of the night. She felt that she was a failure as a mother. What is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is observed 4-5 days af​ter delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lowered tryptophan levels ​have been noticed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nearly 50% of women suffer​ from this problem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antidepressants are the tr​eatment of choice","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Antidepressants are the treatment of choice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Antidepressants are the treatment of choice</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>puerperal blues. </strong>It is also known as<strong> </strong>3 day blues/baby blues/maternity blues. It is treated by <strong>reassurance </strong>and<strong> psychological support.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 632px; height: 309px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; text-align: left; height: 33px;\"><strong>Puerperal blues</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; text-align: left; height: 33px;\"><strong>Post-partum depression</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; height: 33px;\">Onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; height: 33px;\"><strong>4-5 days</strong> after delivery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; height: 33px;\"><strong>4-6 months</strong> after delivery/abortion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; height: 51px;\">Cause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; height: 51px;\">Sudden decrease in hormone levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; height: 51px;\">Multifactorial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 79px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; height: 79px;\">\n<p>Duration</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; height: 79px;\">\n<p>Lasts for a few days to 2-3 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; height: 79px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 57px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; height: 57px;\">\n<p>Incidence in postpartum mothers</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; height: 57px;\">\n<p>50%</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; height: 57px;\">10-20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; height: 42px;\">\n<p>Treatment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; height: 42px;\">\n<p>Reassurance and psychological support</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; height: 42px;\">\n<p>Antidepressants (Fluoxetine/Paroxetine)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18.859375px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 88.21875px; height: 18.859375px;\">Recurrence rate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.015625px; height: 18.859375px;\">25%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.78125px; height: 18.859375px;\">\n<p>50-100%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7591","text":"A lady was discharged afte​r a normal vaginal delive​ry. On the third day, she​ came back with fever, ta‌chycardia, and seizures. ​Fundus showed papilledema​ with no focal deficits. ‌What is the most probable‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A lady was discharged after a normal vaginal delivery. On the third day, she came back with fever, tachycardia, and seizures. Fundus showed papilledema with no focal deficits. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subarachnoid​ haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Menin​gitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute m​igraine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cortical vei​n thrombosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cortical vein thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cortical vein thrombosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation likely suggests <strong>cortical vein thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Risk factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy and puerperium</strong></li>\n<li>Oral contraceptives</li>\n<li>Prothrombotic conditions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most common clinical presentation is <strong>headache</strong> with or without vomiting, papilledema, or focal deficits.</p>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brain MRI with MR venography</strong> - most sensitive</li>\n<li>CT - normal in 30 % of cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Subarachnoid hemorrhage -</strong> As there are no focal deficits, this diagnosis is unlikely.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Meningitis </strong>- The patient would have presented with neck rigidity or photophobia. Moreover, the presence of early papilledema goes against acute meningitis.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Acute migraine </strong>- It does not present with seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1793","text":"A mother, previously on OC​Ps, comes with her 2-week​-old baby to the OPD. She​ informs you that she is ‌currently breastfeeding h​er child and enquires abo​ut re-starting contracept‌ion. When would you recom‌mend she start using cont​raceptives?","raw_text":"A mother, previously on OCPs, comes with her 2-week-old baby to the OPD. She informs you that she is currently breastfeeding her child and enquires about re-starting contraception. When would you recommend she start using contraceptives?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd postp​artum week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3rd postpa​rtum month","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4th postpa​rtum month","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6th postpa​rtum month","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3rd postpartum month","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3rd postpartum month</strong></p><p>Lactating women should start using contraceptives in the <strong>3rd postpartum</strong> <strong>month.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Progesterone-only</strong> methods are the hormonal contraception of choice in a <strong>lactating mother.</strong></p>\n<p>Efficacy of contraceptives used in postpartum period: Levonorgestrel IUD/ Implant &gt; DMPA &gt; Progestin-only pill.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 358px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.0625px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Lactating mother</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.9375px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Non-lactating mother</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.0625px; height: 36px;\">Ovulation occurs after 10 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.9375px; height: 36px;\">Ovulation occurs after 4 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 70px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.0625px; height: 70px;\">Use contraceptive in <strong>3rd post-partum month</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.9375px; height: 70px;\">\n<p>Use contraceptive in <strong>3rd post-partum week. </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Contraception should be ideally started after the resumption of ovulation. However, there are no contraindications for the use of contraceptives as early as 4-6 weeks in lactating women and immediately in non-lactating women.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0830","text":"A woman presents to the OP​D with complaints of amen​orrhoea for the past year​. She has a history of po‌stpartum hemorrhage and l​actational failure. What ​is the probable diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A woman presents to the OPD with complaints of amenorrhoea for the past year. She has a history of postpartum hemorrhage and lactational failure. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sheehan's​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rotor s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolac​tinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acrom​egaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sheehan's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sheehan&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>Sheehan's syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Sheehan's syndrome (SS) is <strong>postpartum hypopituitarism</strong> secondary to the <strong>necrosis</strong> of the<strong> pituitary gland</strong>. It usually results from <strong>severe</strong> <strong>hypotension</strong> or shock caused by <strong>massive hemorrhage</strong> during or after delivery. Patients with SS have varying degrees of anterior pituitary hormone deficiency. The hyperplastic enlargement of the pituitary, which occurs normally during pregnancy, increases the risk of infarction. </p>\n<p>Affected women may have persistent <strong>hypotension</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong>, <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, and lactation failure. The most prominent symptoms are <strong>lactation failure </strong>(due to a deficiency of prolactin)<strong> </strong>and <strong>postpartum amenorrhea </strong>(due to a deficiency of gonadotropins). Because deficiencies of some or all pituitary-responsive hormones may develop after the initial insult, Sheehan syndrome can be heterogeneous and may not be identified for years.</p>\n<div>It can present acutely as <strong>pituitary apoplexy</strong>, an endocrine emergency, characterized by a sudden onset of <strong>headache</strong>, nausea,<strong> visual deficits,</strong> and in severe cases, severe <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, <strong>hypotension</strong> and shock, CNS hemorrhage, and death.</div>\n<p>Also, it can present <strong>late</strong> with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of pubic and axillary hair</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism due to decreased TSH secretion </li>\n<li>Adrenal insufficiency due to low ACTH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is by hormone replacement - first <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>, later - thyroid hormone, sex steroids, vasopressin and growth hormones are given. If there is evidence of progressive visual loss, cranial nerve involvement or loss of consciousness, immediate surgical decompression must be carried out. Fertility can be achieved in these women with hMG and hCG. Clomiphene is ineffective. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: <strong>Rotor syndrome</strong> is a type of hereditary hyperbilirubinemia with autosomal recessive inheritance. Affected individuals have mild jaundice and hyperbilirubinemia. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C:<strong> Prolactinoma</strong> or pituitary adenoids is the condition of hyperprolactinemia diagnosed by a prolactin level &gt;200 μg/L. It usually presents with infertility, amenorrhea, and <strong>galactorrhea.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: <strong>Acromegaly</strong> is characterized by the signs and symptoms pertaining to excessive growth hormone secretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1620","text":"A woman presents with feve​r on the 3rd postpartum d​ay and is put on antibiot​ics. Fever is still not b‌rought under control. On ​further investigation, sh​e is diagnosed to have se‌ptic pelvic thrombophlebi‌tis. Which among the foll​owing is the next course ​of treatment for her?","raw_text":"A woman presents with fever on the 3rd postpartum day and is put on antibiotics. Fever is still not brought under control. On further investigation, she is diagnosed to have septic pelvic thrombophlebitis. Which among the following is the next course of treatment for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop antibiotics and start​ heparin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibioti​cs+Heparin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical e​mbolectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antibiotics+Heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antibiotics+Heparin</strong></p><p><strong>Antibiotics plus heparin</strong> is the next course of treatment for <strong>septic pelvic thrombophlebitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis refers to the presence of an<strong> endovascular thrombus</strong> in the setting of associated bacterial or fungal <strong>infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>The treatment of septic pelvic thrombophlebitis is <strong>broad-spectrum antibiotics plus anticoagulation</strong>. Anticoagulation is thought to <strong>prevent further thrombosis</strong> and <strong>reduce</strong> the <strong>spread of septic emboli</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FJkA6a-U","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"16_disorders_of_the_eyelid","title":"16.Disorders Of The Eyelid","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB0368","text":"Which of the following is ​defined as loss of eyelas​hes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is defined as loss of eyelashes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyl​osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mada​rosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trich​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distic​hiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Madarosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3c939b4b4e34b1aadee507efae30595x462x350.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e887d4ac788547b7b82bce71961c1f93x1280x1251.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e98883c42c44cdd92dabb3ce9d76c28x462x386.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Madarosis</strong></p><p><strong>Madarosis</strong> is the <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>eyelashes or eyebrows</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of <strong>eyebrows</strong> is seen in <strong>leprosy</strong> and <strong>myxedema</strong>.</li>\n<li>Loss of <strong>eyelashes</strong> is seen in <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>blepharitis.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Tylosis is a thickening of the tarsal border of the eyelid. </p>\n<p>Option C: Trichiasis is an inward misdirection of cilia, with the normal position of the lid margin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Distichiasis is the presence of a double row of eyelashes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0774","text":"What is the term used to d​escribe an extra row of e​yelashes behind the grey ​line?","raw_text":"What is the term used to describe an extra row of eyelashes behind the grey line?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trich​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distic​hiasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tyl​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mada​rosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Distichiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Distichiasis</strong></p><p>The extra row of eyelashes behind the grey line is called <strong>distichiasis. </strong></p>\n<p>Distichiasis can occur due to congenital or acquired causes. These lashes may irritate the cornea. Management is by lid splitting with cryotherapy to the base of the hair follicles.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Trichiasis is inward misdirection of cilia, with the normal position of the lid margin.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tylosis is the thickening of the tarsal plate.</p>\n<p>Option D: Madarosis is the loss of eyelashes or eyebrows.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0367","text":"A term neonate is referred​ to the district hospital​ for an ophthalmic exam. ​Partial fusion of her rig​ht upper and lower eyelid‍s by a central narrow ban‍d of tissue is noted as s​hown below. The left eye ‍examination is normal. Wh‌at is the most likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A term neonate is referred to the district hospital for an ophthalmic exam. Partial fusion of her right upper and lower eyelids by a central narrow band of tissue is noted as shown below. The left eye examination is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symble​pharon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ankylob​lepharon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blepharo​phimosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lagoph​thalmos","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ankyloblepharon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0fd1676772f47ce91683a023ca3a409.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd987f90cc184588ab1f655f350b11f9x328x256.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0b4712237ec462c8362bfbbe45cc1a4x599x276.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7e22d5036a146fba5d5f479ba3fa5b0x450x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ankyloblepharon</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>adhesion</strong> between the margins of the upper and lower eyelids, which suggests <strong>ankyloblepharon.</strong></p>\n<p>It may occur as a <strong>congenital</strong> anomaly or as <strong>acquired</strong> adhesions after healing of chemical <strong>burns, ulcers,</strong> or traumatic wounds of the lid margins. Treatment is the <strong>excision</strong> of the <strong>adhesions</strong> between lid margins. In severe cases where the adhesions involve the angle of the eyes, <strong>epithelial grafts</strong> can be used. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Symblepharon is the adhesion between the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, whereby the eyelids become adherent to the eyeball.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Blepharophimosis is a congenital anomaly in which the extent of the palpebral fissure is decreased. It appears contracted at the outer canthus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Lagophthalmos is characterized by the inability to close the eyelids voluntarily. It may be due to a malfunction of the facial nerve. The image below shows nocturnal lagophthalmos (inability to close eyelids during sleep).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6508","text":"A male patient complains o​f a foreign body sensatio​n, irritation, and lacrim​ation in the right eye. D​uring the evaluation, you‍ notice the following fin‍dings with normal lid mar​gins. What is this condit‍ion known as?","raw_text":"A male patient complains of a foreign body sensation, irritation, and lacrimation in the right eye. During the evaluation, you notice the following findings with normal lid margins. What is this condition known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distic​hiasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trich​iasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudotr​ichiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudodis​tichiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trichiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f15782bf0f01417e89a8cb2706798d67.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a558477072a46b385b381ee1e0c6b97x1280x1251.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trichiasis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the above image is suggestive of <strong>trichiasis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Trichiasis </strong>refers to the inward misdirection of cilia, which usually rub against the eyeball with the normal position of the lid margin. These patients present with foreign body sensations and photophobia. They experience troublesome irritation, pain, and lacrimation.  On examination, one or more misdirected cilia touching the cornea are seen. The conjunctiva may be congested.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Epilation:</strong> Mechanical removal with forceps</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Electrolysis:</strong> M</span>ethod of destroying the lash follicle by electric current.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cryoepilation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Surgical correction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1182","text":"What defines​ telecanthus?","raw_text":"What defines telecanthus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Widened inter-pupillary di​stance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Widely separated medial or​bital wall","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Widened inter-orbital dist​ance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Widened root of the nose w​ith normal inter-pupillar​y distance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Widened root of the nose with normal inter-pupillary distance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/230199f1eecf47828803f55612451027x774x298.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Widened root of the nose with normal inter-pupillary distance</strong></p><p><strong>Telecanthus </strong>means an increased distance between medial canthi (widened root of the nose) with <strong>normal inter-pupillary distance</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>hypertelorism</strong>, there is a wide bony separation of the orbits.</p>\n<p>In <strong>epicanthus</strong>, there are <strong>bilateral vertical folds</strong> of <strong>skin</strong> that extend from the upper or lower lids <strong>towards</strong> the <strong>medial canthi</strong>. They may give rise to <strong>pseudoesotropia</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0370","text":"Weiss operation is done in​ which of the following c​ondition?","raw_text":"Weiss operation is done in which of the following condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entr​opion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ectr​opion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trich​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pto​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Entropion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b9cd63307f2459ba5df1b479b8940cex642x312.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Entropion</strong></p><p><strong>Weiss operation </strong>is done in <strong>senile entropion. </strong>In this operation, the transverse lid split, and everything sutures are placed.</p>\n<p><strong>Entropion</strong> refers to the <strong>inward rolling</strong> and <strong>rotation</strong> of the <strong>lid margin</strong> towards the globe. It is more common in the lower lid.</p>\n<p>Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Horizontal lid laxity</li>\n<li>Vertical lid instability</li>\n<li>Over-riding of the pretarsal by the preseptal orbicularis during lid closure</li>\n<li>Orbital septum laxity</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0372","text":"For which of the following​ is medial conjunctivopla​sty the surgical modality​ of treatment?","raw_text":"For which of the following is medial conjunctivoplasty the surgical modality of treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entr​opion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ectr​opion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blepharo​phimosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pto​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ectropion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9af1b9ec979247f488fb90b2aeaef9e6x1280x1539.0691114245417.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ectropion</strong></p><p><strong>Medial conjunctivoplasty</strong> is done in <strong>involutional ectropion</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ectropion </strong>is the <strong>eversion</strong> of the lid margin and eyelashes away from the globe.</p>\n<p>Types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Involutional/senile - affects the lower lid of <strong>elderly</strong> patients.</li>\n<li>Cicatricial <strong>-</strong> caused by the <strong>scarring</strong> or contracture of the skin and underlying tissues</li>\n<li>Paralytic <strong>-</strong> caused by <strong>ipsilateral facial nerve palsy</strong> </li>\n<li>Mechanical <strong>-</strong> caused by <strong>tumors</strong> on or near the lid margin that mechanically evert the lid</li>\n<li>Congenital</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0377","text":"Which among the following ​is not a feature of bleph​arophimosis syndrome?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a feature of blepharophimosis syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Narrow palpe​bral fissure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pto​sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telec​anthus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Entr​opion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Entropion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86d2695d4db64c3c9c4a95de6ecf5a68x451x166.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Entropion</strong></p><p><strong>Entropion</strong> is not a feature of blepharophimosis syndrome.</p>\n<p>Blepharophimosis, ptosis, and epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES) consists of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow palpebral fissure</li>\n<li>Ptosis</li>\n<li>Congenital <strong>ectropion</strong> of the lower lid</li>\n<li>Telecanthus</li>\n<li><strong>Epicanthus inversus:</strong> an extra fold of skin arising from the lower lid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows blepharophimosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0379","text":"The physician noticed the ​following finding in a pa​tient that came to the OP​D for an eye exam. Which ​of the following pair of ‍muscles are likely to be ‍affected in this patient?​","raw_text":"The physician noticed the following finding in a patient that came to the OPD for an eye exam. Which of the following pair of muscles are likely to be affected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muller's muscle and superi​or rectus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levator palpebrae superior​is and superior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior oblique and super​ior rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator palpebrae superior​is and Muller's muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Levator palpebrae superioris and Muller's muscle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb9d168bcab1487993b5ca0dfa6a7363.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Levator palpebrae superioris and Muller&#x27;s muscle</strong></p><p>The patient has mild right eye <strong>ptosis.</strong> Injury or weakness of <strong>levator palpebrae</strong> <strong>superioris</strong> and <strong>Muller's</strong> muscle leads to ptosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Levator palpebrae superioris</strong> is the important <strong>upper eyelid retractor</strong>. Frontalis and <strong>Muller’s muscle</strong> assist in the elevation of the upper eyelid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0375","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is a cause of pse​udoptosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is a cause of pseudoptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lambert–Eat​on syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Big ch​alazion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blepharophim​osis syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brow ​ptosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brow ptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brow ptosis</strong></p><p><strong>Brow ptosis</strong> is a type of <strong>pseudoptosis, </strong>not ptosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Ptosis</strong> is abnormal drooping of the upper eyelid to a level that covers <strong>more</strong> <strong>than 2mm</strong> of the superior cornea. </p>\n<p>Types of ptosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mechanical</strong> ptosis <strong>- </strong>Can be due to<strong> </strong>lid <strong>tumors, </strong>multiple/big <strong>chalazions,</strong> and conjunctival <strong>scarring</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Aponeurotic</strong> ptosis <strong>- </strong>Due to <strong>dehiscence</strong> of the aponeurosis and in <strong>postoperative </strong>patients</li>\n<li><strong>Involutional </strong>ptosis seen in<strong> </strong>old age</li>\n<li><strong>Neurogenic </strong>ptosis due to a<strong> </strong>3<sup>rd</sup> nerve palsy, Horner syndrome and synkinetic ptosis/<strong>Marcus–Gunn jaw-winking</strong> ptosis</li>\n<li><strong>Myogenic ptosis </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Congenital:</strong> <strong>most common myogenic ptosis</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple</li>\n<li><strong>Blepharophimosis</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li>Congenital synkinetic ptosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Acquired </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li>Lambert–Eaton syndrome</li>\n<li>Ocular myopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>pseudoptosis </strong>(apparent ptosis):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lack of support of the lids by the globe e.g. artificial eye, microphthalmos, phthisis bulbi, or enophthalmos.</li>\n<li>Contralateral lid retraction</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral hypotropia</li>\n<li><strong>Brow ptosis</strong> occurs due to <strong>excessive skin</strong> on the brow, or seventh nerve palsy, which is diagnosed by manually elevating the<br/>eyebrow </li>\n<li>Dermatochalasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7515","text":"A 45-year-old patient come​s with complaints of prog​ressive weakness as the d​ay progresses. He is foun​d to have antibodies agai‍nst nicotinic acetylcholi‍ne receptors. Which is th​e most common ocular find‍ing in this disease?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient comes with complaints of progressive weakness as the day progresses. He is found to have antibodies against nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Which is the most common ocular finding in this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipl​opia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lagoph​thalmos","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exopht​halmos","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pto​sis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ptosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> and <strong>ptosis</strong> is the most common ocular feature of the disease.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3550\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Myasthenia gravis (MG) is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disorder of the neuromuscular junction most commonly caused by <strong>anti-</strong><strong>acetylcholine receptor antibodies</strong>. The disorder is characterized by <strong>weakness</strong> and <strong>fatigability</strong> of skeletal muscles where the weakness <strong>increases</strong> during <strong>repeated use </strong>and may <strong>improve</strong> following <strong>rest.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Ptosis</strong> and <strong>diplopia</strong> are common <strong>initial complaints</strong> due to <strong>early involvement</strong> of the <strong>lids</strong> and <strong>extraocular muscles</strong> (EOMs). Of them, <strong>Ptosis</strong> is a <strong>more common</strong> manifestation than diplopia. <strong>Ptosis</strong> in myasthenia gravis is insidious, <strong>bilateral,</strong> and frequently <strong>asymmetrical.</strong></p>\n<p>Investigations of myasthenia gravis include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ice pack test </strong>-This tests for a transient improvement of the ptotic eyelid on placing an ice pack for 2 minutes, as  the cold inhibits the breakdown of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase.</li>\n<li><strong>Tensilon</strong> (<strong>Edrophonium</strong>) test - This tests for a transient improvement in the weakness on administration of a short-acting anticholinesterase such as edrophonium.</li>\n<li><strong>Antibody testing </strong>- for Acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies, MuSK protein antibodies helps support a diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1177","text":"Which of the following con​ditions will not have a f​inding of bilateral ptosi​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions will not have a finding of bilateral ptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myasthen​ia gravis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marfan's​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myotonic ​dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kearns-Say​re syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marfan's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/accca8052eb640b892f3e7d2b5fdada4x248x380.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marfan&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral ptosis</strong> is not seen in <strong>Marfan's</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>Causes of unilateral ptosis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Horner's syndrome</li>\n<li>Oculomotor nerve palsy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Causes of bilateral ptosis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Congenital</li>\n<li>Senile/involutional/aponeurotic</li>\n<li>Blepharophimosis syndrome</li>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li>Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO)<strong> - </strong>seen in Kearns-Sayre syndrome</li>\n<li>Myotonic dystrophy</li>\n<li>Ocular myopathies</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows a case of botulinum toxicity with bilateral ptosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0381","text":"During OPD hours, a patien​t came with the finding s​hown below. On examinatio​n, the upper 4 mm of the ​cornea is covered by the ‍upper eyelid. What is the‍ grade of ptosis in this ​patient?","raw_text":"During OPD hours, a patient came with the finding shown below. On examination, the upper 4 mm of the cornea is covered by the upper eyelid. What is the grade of ptosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mi​ld","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mode​rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sev​ere","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prof​ound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mild","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1c4b698588554633aa6828bb1fee54b9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mild</strong></p><p>In this question, 4 mm of the cornea is covered by the upper eyelid. Subtracting 2 mm from this implies that there is 2 mm of ptosis, which is classified as mild grade. </p>\n<p>In <strong>bilateral</strong> cases, the <strong>grade</strong> of <strong>ptosis</strong> can be determined by measuring the amount of cornea covered by the upper lid and then subtracting 2 mm. Depending upon this value, the ptosis is graded as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild: 2 mm</li>\n<li>Moderate: 3 mm</li>\n<li>Severe: 4 mm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0384","text":"A child with a high risk o​f amblyopia is planned fo​r surgical correction of ​unilateral simple congeni​tal ptosis. He has poor l‍evator function. What is ‍the treatment of choice i​n this case?","raw_text":"A child with a high risk of amblyopia is planned for surgical correction of unilateral simple congenital ptosis. He has poor levator function. What is the treatment of choice in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levator ​resection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frontal​is sling","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fasanella-Servat operation​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muller​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontalis sling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontalis sling</strong></p><p><strong>Frontalis sling</strong> procedure is the treatment of choice for <strong>congenital ptosis</strong> with poor levator function.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5190","text":"A patient presents to you ​with anhidrosis and enoph​thalmos. Examination reve​als miosis along with mil​d ptosis. You decide to c‍orrect the ptosis by perf‍orming a Fasanella-Servat​ operation, by resecting ‍which of the following mu‌scles?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with anhidrosis and enophthalmos. Examination reveals miosis along with mild ptosis. You decide to correct the ptosis by performing a Fasanella-Servat operation, by resecting which of the following muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levator palpebrae superior​is muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frontali​s muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior re​ctus muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muller'​s muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Muller's muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Muller&#x27;s muscle</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong> and the associated mild ptosis in this condition can be corrected by <strong>resecting Muller's muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ptosis</strong> in Horner's syndrome is due to the paralysis of Muller's muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Fasanella-Servat</strong> operation is indicated in cases having mild ptosis<strong>(&lt; 2 mm) </strong>and good levator function. In this surgery, the upper tarsal border along with its attached Muller’s muscle and conjunctiva are resected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1176","text":"A couple brought their 11-​month-old child for an ey​e exam when they noticed ​that his left upper eyeli​d was in a lower position‍ than his right one. The ‍mother also noticed that ​his left upper eyelid goe‍s \"up and down” when she ‌breastfeeds him. His deli‌very was unremarkable. Wh​en the baby opens his mou​th or sucks his thumb, th‌e left eyelid ptosis disa‌ppears. What is the patho‍logy behind this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A couple brought their 11-month-old child for an eye exam when they noticed that his left upper eyelid was in a lower position than his right one. The mother also noticed that his left upper eyelid goes \"up and down” when she breastfeeds him. His delivery was unremarkable. When the baby opens his mouth or sucks his thumb, the left eyelid ptosis disappears. What is the pathology behind this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior division of the t​hird cranial nerve is mis​directed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sympathetic nerves to Mull​er's muscles are misdirec​ted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ophthalmic division of the​ fifth cranial nerve is m​isdirected","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibular division of the​ fifth cranial nerve is m​isdirected","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mandibular division of the fifth cranial nerve is misdirected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5ba0d91ff064f5994d63748b3f159d4x414x462.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e89e10db2654591bd4aed0b507b657fx414x438.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mandibular division of the fifth cranial nerve is misdirected</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Marcus–Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon</strong>. In this condition, the <strong>mandibular division</strong> of the <strong>fifth</strong> cranial nerve is <strong>misdirected</strong> to the levator muscle.</p>\n<p>Some cases of <strong>congenital ptosis</strong> are associated with the Marcus–Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon. The vast majority are <strong>unilateral</strong>.<strong> </strong>Signs include <strong>retraction</strong> of the <strong>ptotic lid</strong> in conjunction with <strong>stimulation</strong> of the <strong>ipsilateral pterygoid muscles</strong> by chewing, <strong>sucking</strong>, <strong>opening</strong> the mouth, or contralateral jaw movement. Less common stimuli to winking include <strong>jaw protrusion</strong>, <strong>smiling</strong>, swallowing, and clenching of teeth. Jaw-winking does <strong>not improve</strong> with <strong>age</strong> although patients may learn to mask it.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a woman with Marcus–Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon. See how her right eyelid ptosis retracts with the opening of the mouth.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0386","text":"Which of the following is ​the causative agent for b​lepharitis acarica?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the causative agent for blepharitis acarica?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mora​xella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyl​ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propioni​bacterium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Demodex fo​lliculorum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Demodex folliculorum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/62dbd670f7b94148a8135e678c9f81cdx220x165.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Demodex folliculorum</strong></p><p>Blepharitis acarica is caused by <em><strong>Demodex folliculorum</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Blepharitis is a <strong>chronic inflammation</strong> of the <strong>margins</strong> of the lids.</p>\n<p>The two types are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Seborrheic and ulcerative types</li>\n<li>Caused by<strong> staphylococci, </strong>mainly</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior </strong>– <strong>Mebomianitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Occasionally caused by</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Demodex folliculorum </em>– <strong>Blepharitis acarica</strong></li>\n<li>Crab louse – <strong>Phthiriasis palpebrarum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows swollen and inflamed eyelid suggestive of blepharitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0398","text":"What is the most common ma​lignant tumor of the eyel​id?","raw_text":"What is the most common malignant tumor of the eyelid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sebaceous gl​and carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Basal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Basal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Basal cell carcinoma</strong> constitutes <strong>85–90%</strong> of all malignant epithelial eyelid tumors. It is <strong>locally malignant</strong> and <strong>most commonly</strong> involves the <strong>lower lid,</strong> followed by the medial canthus, upper lid, and outer canthus.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 671px; width: 461px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.5px; height: 36px;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.75px; height: 36px;\">Common location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.172px; height: 36px;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; height: 36px;\">Spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 166px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.5px; height: 166px;\">Basal cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.75px; height: 166px;\"><strong>Lower lid/</strong>Medial canthus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.172px; height: 166px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nodule</strong></li>\n<li>Central ulceration with <strong>pearly</strong> surface, <strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">telangiectasia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; height: 166px;\">local</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.5px; height: 54px;\">Squamous cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.75px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Lower lid</strong>, from previous actinic keratosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.172px; height: 54px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ulcer</strong> with thickened margins, <strong>keratosis </strong></li>\n<li><strong>p</strong><strong>apillomatous</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Local, lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.5px; height: 18px;\">Sebaceous cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.75px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Upper</strong> lid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.172px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Nodule-like <strong>chalazion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Multifocal</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Females </strong>&gt;males</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrences</strong> common</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Local intraepithelial lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.5px; height: 18px;\">Malignant melanoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.75px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Previous nevi</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.172px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>&gt;<strong>6 mm</strong> pigmented, </li>\n<li><strong>Vascularization</strong></li>\n<li>inflammation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Local, vascular lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6739","text":"Which of the following sys​temic drugs is used speci​fically in the management​ of periocular basal cell​ carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following systemic drugs is used specifically in the management of periocular basal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imiq​uimod","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vismo​degib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sipo​nimod","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inter​feron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vismodegib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vismodegib</strong></p><p><strong>Vismodegib</strong> is the systemic drug used for the management of periocular basal cell carcinoma (BCC).</p>\n<p>Vismodegib is an eye-sparing adjuvant therapy of periocular and orbital BCC for patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Who cannot tolerate surgery or radiotherapy, or</li>\n<li>With relapsed locally advanced disease after surgery or</li>\n<li>With metastatic disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Vismodegib is a Hedgehog pathway inhibitor, given 150 mg orally twice a day.</p>\n<p>Treatment options for BCC consist of surgery, radiotherapy, and chemotherapy. Surgical excision using Mohs micrographic surgery or <strong>wide surgical excision</strong> with <strong>frozen section margin control</strong> is the first-line treatment of periocular BCC. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Imiquimod 5% topical cream can also be used in patients with BCC. But, it is not a systemic drug.</p>\n<p>Option C: Siponimod is the drug approved for secondary progressive multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Interferons are generally not used in periocular BCC.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6741","text":"A 6-month-old girl was bro​ught by her worried paren​ts due to a swelling on t​he eyelid as shown below.​ They were told by a loca‍l village doctor that the‍ swelling will disappear ​on its own but recently i‍t has been increasing rap‌idly. What would be the m‌ost likely management of ​this baby's condition?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old girl was brought by her worried parents due to a swelling on the eyelid as shown below. They were told by a local village doctor that the swelling will disappear on its own but recently it has been increasing rapidly. What would be the most likely management of this baby's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reassurance and observatio​n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment with intralesion​al steroids at 2 years of​ age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediate treatment with r​adiotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immediate treatment with o​ral propranolol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Immediate treatment with oral propranolol","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f93a0758a3a74d93a9ab6da0e252cccd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Immediate treatment with oral propranolol</strong></p><p>The history and image are suggestive of <strong>capillary hemangioma</strong> involving the left upper lid and periocular region causing severe mechanical <strong>ptosis.</strong> Immediate management with <strong>oral propranolol</strong> is the treatment of choice in this case.</p>\n<p>The ptosis is so severe that it obstructs the pupillary area (visual axis) with the risk of <strong>sensory deprivation amblyopia.</strong>  This warrants immediate treatment to reduce the bulk of the tumor.</p>\n<p>Capillary hemangioma regresses spontaneously very often. It may be managed by oral propranolol, oral prednisolone, or intralesional steroid.</p>\n<p>Indications for treatment of patients with capillary hemangioma with pharmacologic therapy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visual symptoms (amblyopia, astigmatism, occlusion, strabismus)</li>\n<li>Cosmesis</li>\n<li>Optic nerve compression</li>\n<li>Exposure keratopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6738","text":"Identify the clinical cond​ition.","raw_text":"Identify the clinical condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marfan’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mikulicz'​s syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurofibromatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/525a5aadf4ab4ef784557a7932cedd73.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurofibromatosis</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>plexiform neurofibroma</strong> of the upper lid causing an S-shaped deformity of the upper lid and mechanical ptosis. This is seen particularly in neurofibromatosis type 1.</p>\n<p>NF1 is caused by the autosomal dominant <strong>loss-of-function</strong> mutations in the <strong>NF1</strong> gene on <strong>chromosome 17.</strong> It is a tumor suppressor gene that encodes <strong>neurofibromin,</strong> a negative RAS regulator. The disease is clinically diagnosed when any <strong>two</strong> of the following <strong>seven features</strong> are present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Six or more <strong>café-au-lait</strong> macules (CALMs) - They are the hallmark of neurofibromatosis and are present in almost 100% of patients. They are present at birth but increase in size, number, and pigmentation, especially during the first few years of life, and are scattered over the entire body. </li>\n<li>Axillary or inguinal <strong>freckling</strong></li>\n<li>Two or more iris <strong>Lisch</strong> nodules - They are iris hamartomas that are best identified by a slit-lamp examination. They are present in the majority of patients with NF1.</li>\n<li>Two or more <strong>neurofibromas</strong> or 1 plexiform neurofibroma</li>\n<li>A distinctive osseous lesion such as <strong>sphenoid dysplasia</strong> or <strong>cortical thinning</strong> of <strong>long bones</strong> such as the tibia</li>\n<li>Optic <strong>gliomas</strong> (most frequently observed CNS tumor in NF1)</li>\n<li>A <strong>first-degree relative</strong> with NF-1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MG8511","text":"Which of the following sig​n is used to describe the​ fullness of eyelids seen​ in Grave's ophthalmopath​y?","raw_text":"Which of the following sign is used to describe the fullness of eyelids seen in Grave's ophthalmopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mobius​’ sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"von Grae​fe's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stellwa​g’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enroth​’s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enroth’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enroth’s sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>fullness of the eyelids</strong> is described as the <strong>Enroth's sign</strong> in Graves ophthalmopathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ca9SmMvw","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"16_infections_of_the_bone","title":"16.Infections Of The Bone","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA3498","text":"Which is the earliest site‌ of infection in acute he​matogenous osteomyelitis?​","raw_text":"Which is the earliest site of infection in acute hematogenous osteomyelitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epip‌hysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diap‌hysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metap‌hysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Perio‌steum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metaphysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7a1a3b093e24cffa8ef1851e2ed3b76x600x824.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metaphysis</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest </strong>site of infection in <strong>acute</strong> hematogenous <strong>osteomyelitis</strong> is the <strong>metaphysis</strong>. It is also the most common site of infection. </p>\n<p>The infection reaches metaphysis via the <strong>hematogenous</strong> route. The metaphysis is susceptible due to the following factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hairpin loop arrangement</strong> of terminal blood vessels - leads to vascular stasis that favors bacterial colonization</li>\n<li>Fewer phagocytes in this area </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Apart from the hematogenous route, acute osteomyelitis can also occur by the <strong>exogenous</strong> route. This includes open fractures, iatrogenic intervention, or spread from local infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3493","text":"A 4-year old girl is broug‌ht with high-grade fever,​ malaise, and severe pain​ over her left knee. Exam‌ination reveals tendernes​s over the medial aspect ​of the left knee joint. W‌hat is the most likely ca‌use of this condition?","raw_text":"A 4-year old girl is brought with high-grade fever, malaise, and severe pain over her left knee. Examination reveals tenderness over the medial aspect of the left knee joint. What is the most likely cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute osteomyelitis</strong>. The <strong>most common</strong> organism causing osteomyelitis is <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>The other common causative organisms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> - most common cause in <strong>IV drug abusers</strong></li>\n<li><strong><em>Salmonella</em> </strong>- patients with <strong>sickle cell anemia </strong>are prone</li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> - was a common causative organism in children, less common now due to vaccination</li>\n<li><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It presents with acute onset fever, pain, and tenderness of the affected site. Redness, warmth, swelling, and edema are late features. It most commonly involves the proximal <strong>tibia</strong> or proximal and distal ends of the <strong>femur</strong>.</p>\n<p>It commonly occurs by <strong>hematogenous</strong> spread following toe infections, ear discharge, tooth infection, sore throat, or a boil. In infants, the source of infection may be umbilical cord infection, heel prick, or IV cannula.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1015","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients with acute osteomye​litis is most likely to d​evelop limb shortening an‌d deformity?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with acute osteomyelitis is most likely to develop limb shortening and deformity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 36-year-old HIV positive‌ woman not on treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 68-year-old man with dia‌betes and hypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 1-year-old male infant w‌ho is otherwise healthy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 6-year-old girl with a h‌istory of sickle cell dis​ease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 1-year-old male infant who is otherwise healthy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 1-year-old male infant who is otherwise healthy</strong></p><p><strong>Acute osteomyelitis</strong> is most likely to cause <strong>limb shortening </strong>and<strong> deformity</strong> in <strong>infants </strong>because of the involvement of the growth plate, epiphysis, and the adjacent joint.</p>\n<p>This occurs because the metaphyseal infection spreads via the <strong>blood vessels </strong>of the physis (<strong>growth plate</strong>) to the epiphysis and the adjacent joint. Hence, septic arthritis is frequently associated with acute osteomyelitis in infancy. The resulting damage to the growth plate and septic arthritis of the adjacent joint lead to limb shortening and deformity. </p>\n<p><strong>Beyond 2 years</strong> of age, the <strong>physis acts as a barrier</strong> preventing the direct spread of metaphyseal infection into the epiphysis. Hence, the metaphyseal infection more commonly spreads to the diaphysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3506","text":"A 6-year-old boy was refer‌red for fever and chills ​for 2 days. His mother sa​ys that he has been refus‌ing to walk and screams w​hen his left leg is touch​ed. Examination revealed ‌point tenderness over the‌ medial tibia. Investigat‍ions revealed the followi‌ng. Which of the followin​g will not be useful in t‌he management of this chi‌ld? Hb - 12.9 g/dl WBC - ‌22,000/cu.mm with neutrop​hil predominance MRI left‌ knee - intramedullary ab​scess of the proximal tib‍ia","raw_text":"<p>A 6-year-old boy was referred for fever and chills for 2 days. His mother says that he has been refusing to walk and screams when his left leg is touched. Examination revealed point tenderness over the medial tibia. Investigations revealed the following. Which of the following will not be useful in the management of this child?</p>\n<p>Hb - 12.9 g/dl</p>\n<p>WBC -  22,000/cu.mm with neutrophil predominance</p>\n<p>MRI left knee - intramedullary abscess of the proximal tibia</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Empirical ‌antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgical‌ drainage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vacuum assi‌sted closure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Splinting ‌of the limb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vacuum assisted closure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vacuum assisted closure</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute osteomyelitis</strong>. Vacuum-assisted closure is not used in the management of acute osteomyelitis. It is used in the management of chronic osteomyelitis following extensive debridement.</p>\n<p>Management of acute osteomyelitis includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immediate <strong>empirical antibiotic </strong>therapy (after taking blood samples for culture and sensitivity)</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical drainage</strong> - if patient does not respond to antibiotics or if abscess is detected on imaging</li>\n<li>Supportive therapy - analgesics, IV fluids, and <strong>splinting</strong> of the limb</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The complications of inadequately treated acute osteomyelitis include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Septicemia</li>\n<li>Chronic osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Septic arthritis</li>\n<li>Altered bone growth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1013","text":"When does the first bony l‌esion of osteomyelitis ap​pear on an X-ray?","raw_text":"When does the first bony lesion of osteomyelitis appear on an X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Within ‌24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Within ‌48 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"During ‌1st week","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"During ‌2nd week","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. During 2nd week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. During 2nd week</strong></p><p>The <strong>first bony lesion</strong> of <strong>osteomyelitis</strong> is seen on an x-ray during the<strong> 2nd week </strong>of infection. It is due to periosteal new bone formation and appears as a faint extracortical outline.</p>\n<p>When osteomyelitis is suspected on clinical grounds, <strong>early x-rays</strong> show only <strong>soft tissue swelling</strong> with no bony abnormality. Hence, the following imaging modalities are <strong>highly sensitive</strong> and are used to aid the diagnosis within 24-48 hours:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MRI </strong>- shows<strong> </strong>marrow edema within 24-48 hours</li>\n<li><strong>Technetium-99</strong> bone scan - shows increased osseous uptake of the tracer </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1007","text":"Identify the following mar‌ked structures.","raw_text":"Identify the following marked structures.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - Sequestrum, 2 - Involu‌crum, 3 - Cloacae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - Sequestrum, 2 - Cloaca‌e, 3 - Involucrum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - Involucrum, 2 - Cloaca‌e, 3 - Sequestrum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1- Cloacae, 2 - Sequestrum‌, 3 - Involucrum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - Involucrum, 2 - Cloacae, 3 - Sequestrum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7676ea4cdb1842dd8d1acf95c877c7f8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a58b448be6d4882ac8d2d916ed64834x1280x1176.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - Involucrum, 2 - Cloacae, 3 - Sequestrum</strong></p><p>In the given radiographs showing features of <strong>chronic osteomyelitis</strong>, the marked structures are as follows: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Involucrum </strong>is the thick sheath of periosteal new bone that surrounds the sequestrum. </li>\n<li><strong>Cloacae </strong>are openings in the involucrum that allow drainage of purulent and necrotic material out of the dead bone.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequestrum</strong> is a piece of devitalized bone that has been separated from its surrounding bone during the process of necrosis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4466","text":"A patient has a history of‌ a road traffic accident ​2 years back, and has dev​eloped pain and swelling ‌now at the same site. His​ X-ray image is shown in ​the image given below. Wh‌at is the probable diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A patient has a history of a road traffic accident 2 years back, and has developed pain and swelling now at the same site. His X-ray image is shown in the image given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteogeni‌c sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing's‌ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic os‌teomyelitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic osteomyelitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4895d17495ea4bc48aebb917adc4db5a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8921e24f73664be396e94ba6dd8ddfb4x1280x979.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/234c5969c2c94781b62d355374b5091ax1280x1176.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8db6a6c4971948929c10ad4b774df132x720x765.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9e9731f65584171aa40292bfb2d6b0fx720x1363.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c289cde70b046f889e36bbf1df0071dx720x1850.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic osteomyelitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with a <strong>history of an accident 2 years ago</strong>, experiencing symptoms of <strong>pain and swelling at the site of injury</strong>, and an X-ray of the lower limb revealing<strong> sequestrum and involucrum</strong> in the tibia, strongly suggests a diagnosis of <strong>chronic osteomyelitis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Chronic osteomyelitis may occur by direct inoculation of the infective organism during <strong>trauma</strong> (like <strong>road traffic accidents</strong>). The infection continues to thrive within the bone leading to alternating quiescent and flare-up stages of chronic osteomyelitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Sequestrum</strong> is a piece of <strong>dead bone</strong>. It is formed when a fragment of bone gets separated from the healthy bone due to necrosis. It varies in size from a spicule to a large fragment and acts as a <strong>nidus </strong>for infection. It leads to abscess and persistent pus discharge in the bone. <strong>Sinus tracts </strong>are formed when the abscess from the infected bone drains out through the skin. </p>\n<p>The<strong> involucrum</strong> is the dense new sclerotic bone surrounding the sequestrum. It is formed from deep layers of stripped periosteum. <strong>Cloacae </strong>are the perforations in the involucrum. Pus and tiny spicules of bone may be discharged through them.</p>\n<p>The radiograph below shows sequestrum, involucrum and cloacae: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Osteogenic sarcoma</strong> characteristically shows the <strong>sun-burst appearance</strong> and <strong>Codman's triangle</strong>, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong> shows an<strong> onion-skin appearance</strong> of the periosteum, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Multiple myeloma</strong> shows <strong>multiple punched-out lesions</strong> of the bone, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3480","text":"A 52-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for chronic pa​in and purulent discharge​ from a wound over his le‌ft thigh. He had suffered​ a fracture of his left f​emur 7 years ago in an ac‌cident and has had these ‌complaints ever since. Ex‍amination reveals the fol‌lowing. Which of the foll​owing is likely responsib‌le for the recurrent natu‌re of this condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man is being evaluated for chronic pain and purulent discharge from a wound over his left thigh. He had suffered a fracture of his left femur 7 years ago in an accident and has had these complaints ever since. Examination reveals the following.  Which of the following is likely responsible for the recurrent nature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seque‌strum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Invol‌ucrum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clo‌acae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sinus‌ tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sequestrum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a3d47e648ea41a58d11589575a0c329.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/630f1af878a341529acb456e122feec9x1280x1176.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sequestrum</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing a sinus are suggestive of <strong>chronic osteomyelitis</strong>. In this condition, <strong>sequestrum</strong> acts as the nidus of infection and is the most common cause of <strong>non-healing</strong> and <strong>recurrence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sequestrum is a piece of <strong>dead bone</strong>. It is formed when a fragment of bone gets separated from the healthy bone due to necrosis. It varies in size from a spicule to a large fragment and acts as a <strong>nidus </strong>for infection. It leads to persistent pus discharge and abscess in the bone. <strong>Sinus tracts </strong>are formed when the abscess from the infected bone drains out through the skin. </p>\n<p>The<strong> involucrum</strong> is the dense new sclerotic bone surrounding the sequestrum. It is formed from deep layers of stripped periosteum. <strong>Cloacae </strong>are the perforations in the involucrum. Pus and tiny spicules of bone may be discharged through them.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3505","text":"In patients with chronic o‌steomyelitis, the paprika​ sign is seen on which of​ the following?","raw_text":"In patients with chronic osteomyelitis, the paprika sign is seen on which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Curettage ‌of the bone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Drainage ‌of abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRI ‌scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT ‌scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Curettage of the bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Curettage of the bone</strong></p><p><strong>Paprika sign</strong> in chronic osteomyelitis is seen during the <strong>curettage </strong>of the bone. It refers to active <strong>punctate bleeding</strong> on debridement and indicates a healthy tissue surface.</p>\n<p>The appearance of the paprika sign is an indication that the curettage is adequate. Effective curettage is very important as <strong>inadequate debridement</strong> is one of the reasons for high <strong>recurrence</strong> rates in chronic osteomyelitis.</p>\n<p>MRI in chronic osteomyelitis shows <strong>rim sign</strong> which is a rim of high signal intensity surrounding the focus of disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3507","text":"A 50-year-old woman with c‌hronic osteomyelitis of t​he tibia is planned for s​urgical debridement. The ‌orthopedic surgeon explai​ns to her that this proce​dure will result in a bon‌y defect that will need t‌o be corrected. All of th‍e following can be used f‌or the correction of this​ defect except:","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman with chronic osteomyelitis of the tibia is planned for surgical debridement. The orthopedic surgeon explains to her that this procedure will result in a bony defect that will need to be corrected. All of the following can be used for the correction of this defect except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metal‌ plate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PMMA ‌Beads","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle‌ graft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone ‌graft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metal plate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metal plate</strong></p><p>The bony defect left behind after debridement is called <strong>dead space</strong>. Metal plates are not used in its management as they act as a nidus of infection and cause recurrent disease. If metal implants are already in place, they must be replaced by external fixators.</p>\n<p>The dead space created after debridement in chronic osteomyelitis leads to <strong>bony instability</strong>. It is managed using the following techniques:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PMMA beads</strong> (polymethyl methacrylate) impregnated with antibiotics - in initial stages</li>\n<li>Cancellous <strong>bone graft</strong> - later stages</li>\n<li><strong>Muscle</strong> <strong>flap</strong> transfer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1022","text":"In patients with chronic o‌steomyelitis, which of th​e following complications​ would you not expect?","raw_text":"In patients with chronic osteomyelitis, which of the following complications would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathologica‌l fractures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brodie's‌ abscess","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brodie's abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brodie&#x27;s abscess</strong></p><p>Brodie's abscess is not a complication of chronic osteomyelitis. It is a subacute variant of osteomyelitis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> of <strong>chronic osteomyelitis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pathological fracture</strong> - most common, occurs due to devitalization of the bone and sequestrum formation</li>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma (<strong>SCC</strong>)<strong> </strong>- occurs rarely due to metaplasia in a chronic discharging sinus</li>\n<li><strong>Amyloidosis</strong> - occurs rarely due to long-standing inflammation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3508","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding sclerosing​ osteomyelitis of Garre?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding sclerosing osteomyelitis of Garre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is no ‌pus formation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a highly virulent in‌fection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It occurs in immunocomprom‌ised hosts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is presence of seque‌strum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. There is no pus formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d047bf21eee045a3a82f077b48363382x327x362.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. There is no pus formation</strong></p><p><strong>Sclerosing osteomyelitis of Garre</strong> is characterized by the <strong>absence of pus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a specific type of chronic osteomyelitis and is caused by a <strong>low-grade anaerobic </strong>infection that is effectively contained by the host immune system. Hence, there is <strong>no abscess or sequestrum</strong> formation.</p>\n<p>This condition is characterized by an <strong>excessive periosteal reaction</strong> at the affected site of the bone, causing <strong>marked sclerosis</strong> and cortical thickening.</p>\n<p>The given radiograph shows Garre's osteomyelitis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3482","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of septic arthritis i​n sexually active young a​dults?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of septic arthritis in sexually active young adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria ‌gonorrheae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobact‌er cloacae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neisseria gonorrheae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neisseria gonorrheae</strong></p><p><strong><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></strong> is the most common cause of septic arthritis in <strong>sexually active young adults</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> causes septic arthritis in <strong>all ages</strong> followed by group A <em>Streptococcus</em> and <em>Enterobacter</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3484","text":"A 7-year-old boy presents ‌with complaints of acute ​pain and swelling of the ​knee joint. He is relucta‌nt to move the limb. Ultr​asonography shows joint e​ffusion. What is the next‌ best step in management?‌","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy presents with complaints of acute pain and swelling of the knee joint. He is reluctant to move the limb. Ultrasonography shows joint effusion. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Empirical ‌antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joint as‌piration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Joint aspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Joint aspiration</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of <strong>septic arthritis</strong>. The next best step is the <strong>aspiration </strong>of the <strong>joint fluid</strong> to confirm the diagnosis and identify the organism.</p>\n<p><strong>Empirical antibiotic</strong> therapy has to be started immediately after aspiration. It is not the first step as giving antibiotics would interfere with the identification of infective organisms from the aspirated fluid. Blood culture is positive only in a few cases and has very little diagnostic value. </p>\n<p><strong>USG</strong> is the <strong>most reliable</strong> investigation to <strong>detect</strong> <strong>effusion</strong> at an early stage. MRI is reserved for infections in obscure sites like the sacroiliac or sternoclavicular joints.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3486","text":"A 52-year-old woman with r‌heumatoid arthritis devel​ops septic arthritis of h​er left knee. She needs t‌o be treated immediately ​to prevent the destructio​n of which of the followi‌ng?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis develops septic arthritis of her left knee. She needs to be treated immediately to prevent the destruction of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Articular‌ cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Joint ‌capsule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subchond‌ral bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Synovial‌ membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Articular cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Articular cartilage</strong></p><p><strong>Septic arthritis</strong> needs to be <strong>treated immediately</strong> to <strong>prevent</strong> the <strong>destruction</strong> of <strong>articular cartilage</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the synovium becomes infected, there is an acute inflammatory response and effusion into the joint space. Destruction of articular cartilage begins within 2 days of infection and becomes apparent in 4-6 days.</p>\n<p>There is depletion of ground substance and destruction of collagen due to exposure to <strong>collagenases</strong>. This is brought about by enzymes of the inflammatory response, bacterial toxins, and stimulation of T-lymphocytes. This destruction of articular cartilage finally <strong>progresses</strong> to <strong>bony ankylosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3509","text":"An 11-month-old baby is br‌ought with high-grade fev​er and persistent crying ​for the past 2 days. She ‌has been refusing to stan​d or eat anything. Examin​ation reveals erythema an‌d swelling of the right h‌ip. Joint arthrocentesis ‍reveals purulent fluid an‌d the presence of bacteri​a. What is the likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"An 11-month-old baby is brought with high-grade fever and persistent crying for the past 2 days. She has been refusing to stand or eat anything. Examination reveals erythema and swelling of the right hip. Joint arthrocentesis reveals purulent fluid and the presence of bacteria. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Holstein-Lew‌is arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hawkin-Kenne‌dy arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tillaux-Chap‌ut arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tom Smith‌ arthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tom Smith arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0c015685ebe4a7ba7800503a27267a5x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tom Smith arthritis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>septic arthritis</strong> of the<strong> hip joint </strong>in an<strong> </strong>infant. This condition is also called<strong> Tom Smith arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Septic arthritis of the hip in infancy causes severe <strong>destruction</strong> of the <strong>femoral head</strong> as it is cartilaginous at this age. This results in<strong> limb shortening</strong> and increased mobility at the hip joint leading to an unstable gait.</p>\n<p>The following image shows destruction and deformation of the left femoral head in a child who had suffered from septic arthritis in infancy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options A and C: Holstein-Lewis and Tillaux-Chaput are types of <strong>fractures.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: The Hawkin-Kennedy test is used to diagnose supraspinatus, infraspinatus and teres minor <strong>injury</strong> as a result of <strong>impingement</strong> between coracohumeral ligament and the greater tubercule of the humerus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3515","text":"In patients with transient‌ synovitis of the hip joi​nt, which of the followin​g is true regarding the a‌ttitude of the affected l​imb?","raw_text":"In patients with transient synovitis of the hip joint, which of the following is true regarding the attitude of the affected limb?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extended, adducted, and in‌ternally rotated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexed, adducted, and inte‌rnally rotated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extended, abducted, and ex‌ternally rotated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexed, abducted, and exte‌rnally rotated","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flexed, abducted, and externally rotated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flexed, abducted, and externally rotated</strong></p><p>In patients with <strong>transient synovitis </strong>of the hip, the attitude of the affected limb is <strong>flexion</strong>, <strong>abduction,</strong> and<strong> external rotation</strong>.</p>\n<p>This attitude maximizes the capsular volume of the hip joint and, thus, <strong>minimizes</strong> the <strong>intracapsular pressure</strong>. This leads to a decrease in pain caused by joint effusion.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 104px;\" width=\"382\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Transient synovitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Septic arthritis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Reactive inflammation following a viral infection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Suppurative infection of the joint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Common in children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">May occur in all age groups, especially with comorbidities (rheumatoid arthritis, immunosuppression)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Painful, but with preserved weight bearing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Inability to bear weight on the affected side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Normal WBC count and ESR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">High WBC count and ESR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">\n<p>Self-limiting condition</p>\n<p>Analgesics and bed rest</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Requires IV antibiotics and surgical drainage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3519","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‌ted with severe pain and ​swelling of her right ind​ex finger for 2 days. Las‌t week, she had sustained​ a cut on her finger with​ a pair of rusty gardenin‌g shears. On examination,‌ the following finding is‍ seen. She cries out in p‌ain when you try to passi​vely extend the finger an‌d palpate the palmar aspe‌ct. What is the likely di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presented with severe pain and swelling of her right index finger for 2 days. Last week, she had sustained a cut on her finger with a pair of rusty gardening shears. On examination, the following finding is seen. She cries out in pain when you try to passively extend the finger and palpate the palmar aspect. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tenosy‌novitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom‌yelitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Septic a‌rthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizin‌g fascitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tenosynovitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc3b28751d0842f5ab58ea8966f017c9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tenosynovitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing <strong>flexed</strong> positioning of the <strong>affected</strong> <strong>finger</strong> are descriptive of <strong>Kanavel's sign</strong>. It is characteristically positive in <strong>infectious flexor tenosynovitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>This sign has four cardinal components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tenderness over the flexor sheath </li>\n<li>Rigid positioning of the finger in flexion</li>\n<li>Pain on attempts to hyperextend the fingers</li>\n<li>Swelling of the involved part</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9400","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents‌ with swelling and pain f​or 6 months over the uppe​r tibia. His radiographic‌ image is shown below. Th​e most likely diagnosis i​s:","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presents with swelling and pain for 6 months over the upper tibia. His radiographic image is shown below. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoc‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brodie's‌ abscess","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ewing's‌ sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Brodie's abscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd44beebaecd4e228a1db6ec4d5784c5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e97f2d5ab4454958b16cc7997386fbccx512x800.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f11d07461ef64d0d9d4bd812ebb7003ax512x347.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Brodie&#x27;s abscess</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a <strong>child</strong> with <strong>prolonged swelling</strong> and <strong>pain</strong> over the <strong>upper tibia</strong>, along with a radiograph showing a <strong>lytic lesion</strong> with a <strong>dense sclerotic rim</strong> and <strong>minimal periosteal reaction</strong>, suggests the diagnosis of <strong>Brodie's abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>Brodie's abscess is a localized form of<strong> subacute osteomyelitis</strong> that occurs when <strong>organisms</strong> of <strong>low virulence</strong> infect an <strong>immunocompetent</strong> <strong>host</strong>. The abscess is <strong>walled off</strong> by the thickening of the surrounding bone trabeculae. It most commonly involves the <strong>metaphysis</strong> of the <strong>proximal tibia</strong> and distal femur.</p>\n<p>The patient is typically a <strong>child</strong> or adolescent experiencing <strong>prolonged pain</strong> near a large joint for <strong>several weeks or months</strong>. Symptoms may include a limp, slight <strong>swelling</strong>, muscle wasting, and localized tenderness.</p>\n<p>The typical radiographic finding is a circumscribed, round or oval <strong>radiolucent cavity</strong> measuring 1-2 cm in diameter, surrounded by a <strong>halo</strong> of <strong>sclerosis, </strong>with<strong> minimal </strong>or<strong> no periosteal reaction.</strong> The<strong> radioisotope scan</strong> shows markedly <strong>increased activity.</strong> MRI may be helpful in diagnosis. <strong>Open biopsy with curettage</strong> is indicated if there is any doubt in the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnoses </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cystic tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic granuloma</li>\n<li>Osteoid osteoma</li>\n<li>Ewing's sarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immobilization</strong> and <strong>antibiotics</strong> (flucloxacillin and fusidic acid) intravenous for 4-5 days, followed by oral administration for another 6 weeks; healing may take up to 12 months</li>\n<li><strong>Curettage</strong> is needed if the x-rays show no healing after conservative management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following X-ray and MRI images show Brodie's abscess of the radius in a patient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The differentiating features of each of the given conditions are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 398px; width: 590px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Age group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Radiographic findings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 76px;\">Brodie's abscess</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 76px;\">\n<p>Children, adolescents</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 76px;\">Lytic lesion with dense sclerotic rim, minimal periosteal reaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 158px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 158px;\">Osteosarcoma (Option A)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 158px;\">\n<p>Bimodal: Children and adolescents, 70-80 years</p>\n<p><strong>Rapidly progressive</strong> tumor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 158px;\">Periosteal reaction leading to sunray appearance and Codman triangle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 76px;\">\n<p>Osteoclastoma (Option B)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 76px;\">20-40 years (<strong>unlikely in children</strong>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 76px;\">Lytic lesion with<strong> thin sclerotic rim</strong>, minimal periosteal reaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 66px;\">Ewing's sarcoma (Option D)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px; height: 66px;\">10-20 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 66px;\"><strong>Diaphyseal</strong> mass and periosteal reaction with onion-peel appearance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GjmTNUay","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"16_polio_and_aids","title":"16.Polio And Aids","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5748","text":"A child is found to have p​aralysis due to polioviru​s infection. Which of the​ following is false regar‍ding the paralysis seen?","raw_text":"A child is found to have paralysis due to poliovirus infection. Which of the following is false regarding the paralysis seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an upper motor neuro​n type","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is an asymmetrical para​lysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strenuous physical exercis​e during the onset exagge​rates the paralysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be caused by polio ​vaccination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is an upper motor neuron type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is an upper motor neuron type</strong></p><p><strong>Poliovirus</strong> mainly affects the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord and medulla oblongata (cranial nerve nuclei), causing<strong> lower motor neuron (LMN) </strong>type of paralysis. It damages motor and autonomic nerves; <strong>sensory</strong> nerves are <strong>not involved</strong>.<br/> <br/>Poliovirus<strong> </strong>is a non-enveloped, <strong>positive-stranded RNA virus </strong>belonging to the Picornaviridae family. Three antigenically distinct serotypes (types 1, 2, and 3) are known. Ages 6 months to 5 years are most commonly affected. The virus is transmitted via the <strong>fecal-oral route</strong> and has an incubation period of 8-12 days. <strong>Humans</strong> are the <strong>only</strong> known <strong>reservoir</strong> for the polioviruses.</p>\n<p>The virus colonizes and replicates in the GI mucosa (<strong>M cells</strong>), followed by entry into the blood via regional lymph nodes. In blood, it causes viremia and gets access to the CNS via the peripheral nerves, finally reaching the <strong>anterior horn cells</strong> in the spinal cord. </p>\n<p>Most cases are subclinical. Some have minor illnesses like fever, sore throat, headache, and malaise. It can present as <strong>non-paralytic polio</strong> (aseptic meningitis) or <strong>paralytic</strong> <strong>polio</strong> (flaccid paralysis with absent deep tendon reflexes). Most frequently, the completely affected muscle is the <strong>tibialis anterior. </strong>It is <strong>asymmetric</strong> <strong>paralysis</strong> which is more severe if strenuous physical exercise occurred during the onset of paralysis. Death usually occurs due to respiratory paralysis.</p>\n<p>Vaccination schedule for polio:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Live attenuated: oral polio vaccine (OPV) (Sabin) at birth (day 0); then boosters at 6, 10, and 14 weeks and at 15-18 months; additional doses till 5 years</li>\n<li>Inactivated polio vaccine (IPV) (Salk) given intradermally; two fractional doses at 6 and 14 weeks as well as 9 months of age. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5752","text":"An outbreak of paralytic p​olio has occurred in Nige​ria. Which of the followi​ng is the most likely typ‍e to cause such outbreaks​?","raw_text":"An outbreak of paralytic polio has occurred in Nigeria. Which of the following is the most likely type to cause such outbreaks?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1​ virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2​ virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3​ virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 4​ virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 1 virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 1 virus</strong></p><p>Most of the outbreaks of <strong>paralytic polio</strong> are caused by the <strong>type 1 virus.</strong></p>\n<p>Three antigenically distinct serotypes (types 1, 2, and 3) of poliovirus are known. <strong>Poliovirus type 1</strong> has historically been the predominant cause of poliomyelitis worldwide and continues to be transmitted in endemic areas.</p>\n<p>Most infections with poliovirus are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Rarely, patients can have the following presentations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Abortive poliomyelitis -</strong> a minor illness, manifested by fever, malaise, sore throat, or headache.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-paralytic poliomyelitis -</strong> manifested by aseptic meningitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Paralytic poliomyelitis -</strong> aseptic meningitis followed by severe back, neck, and muscle pain with rapid/gradual onset of motor weakness. It is the<strong> least common</strong> presentation of poliovirus infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wild</strong> poliovirus <strong>type 2</strong> was <strong>eradicated</strong> in 1999. Until 2015, over 90% of <strong>cVDPV</strong> (circulating vaccine-derived polioviruses) cases were due to the <strong>type 2</strong> component in OPV.</p>\n<p>Bivalent oral polio vaccine (OPV) contains <strong>serotypes 1 </strong>and<strong> 3</strong>. Serotype 2 has been removed, as it is implicated in the pathogenesis of cVDPV and occasionally vaccine-associated paralytic polio (VAPP).</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>On <span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"114\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwjQlurz1MXaAhUMqY8KHT5kDhwQ4EUIcjAL\"><strong>25 April 2016,</strong> India</span> switched from trivalent oral polio vaccine (OPV) to bivalent OPV; this day is called the <strong>National switch day</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5768","text":"Which of the following sig​ns may be seen in a child​ with polio?","raw_text":"Which of the following signs may be seen in a child with polio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tripo​d sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kiss the ​knee sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Head Dr​op Sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>All of the above signs may be seen in a child suffering from polio. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tripod sign</strong> - When the child is asked to sit up unassisted, the child assumes the tripod posture.</li>\n<li><strong>Kiss the knee sign</strong> - The child cannot kiss the knee due to spine stiffness.</li>\n<li><strong>Head drop sign</strong> - A hand is placed under the patient's shoulder and the trunk is raised. Usually, the head will also raise, but in the case of poliomyelitis, the head drops backward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5770","text":"You have been appointed as​ a member of the acute fl​accid paralysis (AFP) sur​veillance team. What age ‍groups would you target f​or surveillance?","raw_text":"You have been appointed as a member of the acute flaccid paralysis (AFP) surveillance team. What age groups would you target for surveillance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0-10 ​years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0-5 ​years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0-15 ​years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0-20 ​years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0-15 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0-15 years</strong></p><p>Acute flaccid paralysis (<strong>AFP</strong>) <strong>surveillance</strong> is done in children from<strong> 0-15 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Every case of acute flaccid paralysis in any child under 15 years is to be reported promptly. Fecal samples of all these cases are obtained (2 samples) and transported to the laboratory within 72 hours of collection at 4–8 degree Celcius or frozen at -20 degree Celcius. This process is known as the <strong>reverse cold chain</strong>.</p>\n<p>The differential diagnosis for acute flaccid paralysis include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-polio enteroviruses</li>\n<li>West Nile virus</li>\n<li>Guillan–Barre syndrome</li>\n<li>Other neurotropic viruses: rabies, varicella-zoster, and Japanese encephalitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5772","text":"When was India declared po​lio-free by WHO?","raw_text":"When was India declared polio-free by WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"March​ 2014","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"April​ 2016","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Januar​y 2013","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Januar​y 2011","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. March 2014","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. March 2014</strong></p><p>India was certified polio-free on <strong>March 27, 2014.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The last case of polio reported in India was in January 2011.</li>\n<li>Type 2 virus was declared eradicated in September 2015.</li>\n<li><strong>Switch</strong> from trivalent to bivalent vaccine was done in India in <strong>April 2016.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2957","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of Polio ​Endgame Strategy 2019-202​3 by WHO?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Polio Endgame Strategy 2019-2023 by WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop all circulating vacci​ne-derived poliovirus (cV​DPV) outbreaks within 120​ days of detection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Separation of polio survei​llance and vaccine preven​table disease surveillanc​e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Certify eradication of Wil​d Poliovirus (WPV)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interrupt transmission of ​all wild poliovirus (WPV)​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Separation of polio surveillance and vaccine preventable disease surveillance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Separation of polio surveillance and vaccine preventable disease surveillance</strong></p><p><strong>Integration, </strong>not separation, of polio surveillance with vaccine-preventable disease (VPD) surveillance, is a part of <strong>Polio Endgame Strategy 2019-2023.</strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">Goals of the Polio Endgame Strategy 2019–2023</span></p>\n<p><strong>Goal One: Eradication </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interrupt transmission of all wild poliovirus (WPV)</li>\n<li>Stop all circulating vaccine-derived poliovirus (cVDPV) outbreaks within 120 days of detection and eliminate the risk of emergence of future VDPVs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Goal two: Integration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contribute to strengthening immunization and health systems to help achieve and sustain polio eradication</li>\n<li>Ensure sensitive poliovirus surveillance through integration with comprehensive vaccine-preventable disease (VPD) and communicable disease surveillance systems</li>\n<li>Prepare for and respond to future outbreaks and emergencies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Goal Three: Certification &amp; Containment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Certify eradication of WPV</li>\n<li>Contain all polioviruses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5779","text":"At your vaccination center​, you have noticed increa​sed reporting of vaccine-​associated paralytic poli‍o (VAPP). Which of the fo​llowing statement is true​ regarding this adverse e‌ffect of the vaccine?","raw_text":"At your vaccination center, you have noticed increased reporting of vaccine-associated paralytic polio (VAPP). Which of the following statement is true regarding this adverse effect of the vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs mostly after the​ first dose of oral polio​ vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is more likely to occur​ if the first dose was pa​renteral inactivated poli​o vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It Is most likely to occur​ if given on the same day​ as yellow fever vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is more likely to occur​ if multiple parenteral i​njections have been given​ at the same time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It occurs mostly after the first dose of oral polio vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It occurs mostly after the first dose of oral polio vaccine</strong></p><p><strong>Vaccine-associated paralytic polio</strong> (VAPP) occurs mostly <strong>after</strong> the <strong>first dose</strong> of oral polio vaccine (<strong>OPV</strong>).</p>\n<p>VAPP is said to occur if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute flaccid paralysis occurred 4 to 40 days after receiving OPV, and residual weakness lasted for more than 60 days after the onset of paralysis.</li>\n<li>Isolation of vaccine-related poliovirus in any stool samples and no isolation of wild poliovirus from any stool samples.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VAPP</strong>:<strong> </strong>Vaccine-associated paralytic polio</li>\n<li><strong>VDPV</strong>: Vaccine-derived polio virus.  A very rare strain of polio virus, genetically changed from the original strain contained in OPV. </li>\n<li><strong>cVDPV</strong>: circulating vaccine-derived polio virus. In areas of low vaccination coverage, a VDPV strain may revert to a form that can cause cases of paralytic polio, leading to an outbreak. This form is called a cVDPV.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9639","text":"While volunteering in an o​rphanage that takes care ​of disabled kids, you not​ice the typical hand-knee‍ gait of polio in a child​. He walks by putting his​ right hand on his right ‌lower thigh while steppin‌g forward. Which of the f​ollowing muscle weakness ‌is responsible for this f​inding?","raw_text":"While volunteering in an orphanage that takes care of disabled kids, you notice the typical hand-knee gait of polio in a child. He walks by putting his right hand on his right lower thigh while stepping forward. Which of the following muscle weakness is responsible for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluteus​ medius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quadr​iceps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastrocnem​ius-soleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gluteus​ maximus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Quadriceps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Quadriceps</strong></p><p>Polio commonly affects the<strong> quadriceps</strong>, and weakness of the quadriceps leads to the<strong> buckling (bending) of the knee</strong> while walking or bearing weight on the leg. So, in order to walk, the person has to prevent the buckling of the knee by <strong>supporting with the hand, producing a hand-knee gait</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examination findings in paralytic polio:</p>\n<p>A. In the early stage</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asymmetric motor paralysis -</strong> involvement of affected muscles is haphazard. No associated sensory loss.</li>\n<li>Commonly affects the <strong>lower limbs</strong></li>\n<li>Most common muscle affected - Quadriceps, although it is most often partially paralyzed</li>\n<li>Most common muscle to undergo<strong> complete paralysis - Tibialis anterior</strong></li>\n<li>The most affected muscle in the hand - Opponens pollicis</li>\n<li>Bulbar/bulbospinal polio - It is a rare but life-threatening disease affecting the motor neurons of the medulla and resulting in the involvement of the respiratory and cardiovascular centers, which may cause death.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>B. In the late-stage</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weakness,<strong> wasting, and deformity of the limbs</strong></li>\n<li>At the hip-flexion-abduction-external rotation</li>\n<li>At the knee, flexion deformity is common; in severe cases, there is <strong>triple deformity: flexion, posterior subluxation, and external rotation</strong></li>\n<li>At the foot, the commonest deformity is equino-varus. Other deformities - equinovalgus, calcaneo-valgus, and calcaneo-varus</li>\n<li>In the upper limb: affects the shoulder and elbow, hand muscles are spared.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9251","text":"Serological testing of a n​eonate born to an HIV-inf​ected mother turned out p​ositive. Which of the fol‍lowing is the most likely​ route by which the newbo​rn might have acquired th‌e infection?","raw_text":"Serological testing of a neonate born to an HIV-infected mother turned out positive. Which of the following is the most likely route by which the newborn might have acquired the infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrapartum ​transmission","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breast​feeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrauterine​ transmission","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amnioc​entesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intrapartum transmission","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intrapartum transmission</strong></p><p>The most common cause of HIV infection in an infant is the <strong>intrapartum transmission.</strong></p>\n<p>The mechanism of intrapartum transmission appears to be mucosal exposure to infected blood and cervicovaginal secretions in the birth canal. The intrauterine contractions during active labor/delivery could also increase the risk of late microtransfusions.</p>\n<p>Routes of HIV transmission in an infant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrauterine</li>\n<li><strong>Intrapartum </strong>- most common</li>\n<li>Breastfeeding - least common</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Risk factors for vertical transmission:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High maternal viremia (most important)</strong></li>\n<li>Preterm delivery (&lt;34 weeks)</li>\n<li>Vitamin A deficiency</li>\n<li>Prolonged rupture of membranes (&gt;4 hours)</li>\n<li>Low birth weight</li>\n<li>Low maternal CD4 count</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5789","text":"You are evaluating childre​n infected with HIV in a ​village, where there is l​ittle awareness about HIV‍ and its treatment. Which​ of the following will be​ the most common presenti‌ng feature in these child‌ren?","raw_text":"You are evaluating children infected with HIV in a village, where there is little awareness about HIV and its treatment. Which of the following will be the most common presenting feature in these children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrointestinal infection​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Persistent lymphadenopathy​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral ​thrush","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Failure ​to thrive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Failure to thrive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Failure to thrive</strong></p><p><strong>Failure to thrive</strong> is the <strong>universal</strong> finding in children with HIV.</p>\n<p>Important features in HIV-infected children include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presenting features - failure to thrive, lymphadenopathy, oral thrush, chronic diarrhea, or respiratory symptoms.</li>\n<li>Most common AIDS-defining illness (opportunistic infection) in children - <em>Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia</em></li>\n<li>Most common fungal infection - oral candidiasis</li>\n<li>Recurrent and chronic bacterial infections (especially, <em>Streptococcus </em><em>pneumoniae</em>)</li>\n<li>Most common GI disease - chronic/recurrent diarrhea with malabsorption</li>\n<li>Most common renal disease - nephrotic syndrome</li>\n<li>Most common hematological manifestation - anemia</li>\n<li>Most common neoplasms - non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, primary CNS lymphoma, and leiomyosarcomas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5792","text":"Match the following HIV ca​ses with their respective​ stages. Clinical case St​age 1. A 5-year old child‍ with persistent diarrhea​ for 20 days. a. Stage 3 ​2. An asymptomatic 2-year‌-old with persistent gene‌ralized lymphadenopathy. ​b. Stage 4 3. A 7-year-ol‌d with recurrent oral ulc​ers and persistent paroti‍d enlargement c. Stage 1 ‌4. A 4-year-old with HIV-​associated cardiomyopathy​ d. Stage 2","raw_text":"<p>Match the following HIV cases with their respective stages.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 510px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 280.719px;\">Clinical case</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.281px;\">Stage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 280.719px;\">1. A 5-year old child with persistent diarrhea for 20 days.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.281px;\">a. Stage 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 280.719px;\">2. An asymptomatic 2-year-old with persistent generalized lymphadenopathy.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.281px;\">b. Stage 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 280.719px;\">3. A 7-year-old with recurrent oral ulcers and persistent parotid enlargement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.281px;\">c. Stage 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 280.719px;\">4. A 4-year-old with HIV-associated cardiomyopathy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.281px;\">d. Stage 2 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-d, 2-a,​ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2-b,​ 3-c, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-a, 2-c,​ 3-d, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-d,​ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b</strong></p><p>Correct match is: 1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b.</p>\n<p>A 5-year old child with persistent diarrhea for 20 days - Stage 3</p>\n<p>An asymptomatic 2-year-old with persistent generalized lymphadenopathy - Stage 1</p>\n<p>A 7-year-old with recurrent oral ulcers and persistent parotid enlargement - Stage 2</p>\n<p>A 4-year-old with HIV-associated cardiomyopathy - Stage 4</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5795","text":"A 28-year-old woman in lab​or comes to the emergency​ department at 37 weeks g​estation. She never had a‍ prenatal checkup. The ro​utine screening revealed ​she is HIV-positive. Whic‌h of the following method‌ is least likely to preve​nt the perinatal transmis‌sion of HIV?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman in labor comes to the emergency department at 37 weeks gestation. She never had a prenatal checkup. The routine screening revealed she is HIV-positive. Which of the following method is least likely to prevent the perinatal transmission of HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elective ces​arean section","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avoiding br​eastfeeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Administration of ART prop​hylaxis to baby after bir​th","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vaginal cleansing before d​elivery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vaginal cleansing before delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vaginal cleansing before delivery</strong></p><p><strong>Vaginal cleansing before delivery</strong> is <strong>least likely</strong> to prevent the <strong>perinatal transmission</strong> of HIV.</p>\n<p>Methods to prevent perinatal transmission of HIV include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antiretroviral therapy (ART)</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Single-dose nevirapine at the onset of labor followed by a single dose to the newborn within 72 hours of birth.</li>\n<li>Zidovudine can also be used from the 2<sup>nd</sup> trimester onwards throughout pregnancy and for 6 weeks after birth to infancy.</li>\n<li>Breastfed infants should receive NVP for 6 weeks if the mother is receiving cART, or NVP for the duration of breastfeeding if the mother is not on therapy. Formula-fed infants in resource-limited countries should receive ZDV bid or NVP qd for 6 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Elective cesarean section*</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Usually contraindicated, except in developing countries where breastfeeding may be continued for 6 months.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*<strong>Note</strong>: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2397\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2396\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Maternal cART is documented to decrease the rate of perinatal HIV-1 transmission to &lt;2%, and &lt;1% if the mother’s viral RNA level is &lt;1,000 copies/mL at delivery. Thus, elective C-section at 38 weeks of gestation should be considered only for women whose viral load is &gt;1,000 copies/ mL in late gestation, to further reduce the risk of vertical transmission.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Guidelines for the use of antiretroviral drugs during C-section are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For elective (planned) C-section, ART should be given prior to the operation.</li>\n<li>Women on lifelong ART should continue their standard ART regimen.</li>\n<li>In case of emergency C-section in pregnant women who are not on ART, the three-drug regimen should be started before the surgery and continued thereafter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5796","text":"A patient is started on HA​ART treatment for HIV. He​r CD4 count is 150 cells/​μl. She developed a fever‍ and her symptoms worsene​d during her first week o​f treatment. Which of the‌ following is the most li‌kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient is started on HAART treatment for HIV. Her CD4 count is 150 cells/μl. She developed a fever and her symptoms worsened during her first week of treatment. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AIDS related inflammatory ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immune reconstitution infl​ammatory syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HIV related hypereactivity​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD4 responsive inflammator​y syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS)</strong> is characterized by an increased inflammatory response to subclinical opportunistic infections <strong>after initiating retroviral therapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features seen are fever, worsening of opportunistic infections, new manifestations like lymphadenopathy, pulmonary infiltrates. It typically occurs within 1st few weeks of starting therapy. It commonly occurs in patients with progressive disease, or severe CD4 + T-lymphocyte depletion. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5797","text":"Which of the following is ​an indication to start AR​T in children, as per the​ WHO guidelines?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication to start ART in children, as per the WHO guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;11 months infant of CD​4 count &amp;lt; 1500 cells/m​m3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12-35 months if CD4 count ​&amp;lt;500 cells/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"36-59 months if CD4 count ​&amp;lt;350 cells/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any child​ with HIV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Any child with HIV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Any child with HIV</strong></p><p>Antiretroviral therapy (<strong>ART</strong>) should be initiated in <strong>all children with HIV,</strong> regardless of clinical stage or CD4 cell count, as per the latest WHO recommendations 2016.</p>\n<p>Also, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis is recommended for infants, children, and adolescents with HIV, irrespective of their clinical and immune conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5798","text":"A mother brings her 6-week​ old infant to your PHC f​or immunization. The baby​ was not administered the‍ BCG vaccine as he is HIV​ positive. His recent CD4​ counts were 13%. Which o‌f the following vaccines ‌should you not administer​ in this child?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 6-week old infant to your PHC for immunization. The baby was not administered the BCG vaccine as he is HIV positive. His recent CD4 counts were 13%. Which of the following vaccines should you not administer in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"O​PV","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D​PT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H​ib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. OPV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. OPV</strong></p><p>The <strong>live</strong> attenuated oral polio vaccine (OPV) should be <strong>avoided in severely immunocompromised infants</strong> (CD4 cell percentage &lt;15%) because of an increased risk of <strong>paralytic polio. </strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 375px; height: 418px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\"><strong>Vaccine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\"><strong>Recommendation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">Oral polio vaccine (OPV)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">\n<p>Yes, if CD4% ≥15%<br/>No, if CD4% &lt;15%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">Varicella and MMR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">\n<p>Yes, if CD4% ≥15%<br/>No, if CD4% &lt;15%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">Pentavalent (Hep B, DPT, Hib)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">Inactivated polio vaccine (IPV)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">BCG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">Live attenuated influenza vaccine (LAIV or nasal spray)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.469px;\">Injectable influenza vaccine (flu shot)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175.573px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5799","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the 2025 HI​V targets of Global AIDS ​Strategy 2021-2026?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the 2025 HIV targets of Global AIDS Strategy 2021-2026?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"95% of women of reproducti​ve age have their HIV and​ sexual and reproductive ​health service needs met.‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"90% of people living with ​HIV recieve preventive tr​eatment for TB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"95% of pregnant and breast​feeding women living with​ HIV have suppressed vira​l loads.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"75% of HIV-exposed childre​n are tested by two month​s of age and again after ​cessation of breastfeedin‍g","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 75% of HIV-exposed children are tested by two months of age and again after cessation of breastfeeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 75% of HIV-exposed children are tested by two months of age and again after cessation of breastfeeding</strong></p><p>95%(not 75%) of HIV-exposed children are tested by two months of age and again after cessation of breastfeeding is part of the High level 2025 targets of Global AIDS Strategy 2021-2026</p>\n<p>High-level 2025 targets</p>\n<ul>\n<li>95% of people at risk of HIV infection have access to and use appropriate, prioritized, person-centered, and effective combination prevention options.</li>\n<li><strong>95% of women of reproductive age have their HIV and sexual and reproductive health service needs met. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>95% of pregnant and breastfeeding women living with HIV have suppressed viral loads. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>95% of HIV-exposed children are tested by two months of age and again after cessation of breastfeeding </strong></li>\n<li><strong>75% of all children living with HIV have suppressed viral loads by 2023</strong> (interim target)</li>\n<li>95–95–95 testing and treatment targets are achieved within all subpopulations, age groups, and geographic settings, including children living with HIV.</li>\n<li><strong>90% of people living with HIV receive preventive treatment for TB.</strong></li>\n<li>90% of people living with HIV and people at risk are linked to people-centered and context-specific integrated services for other communicable diseases, noncommunicable diseases, sexual health and gender-based violence, mental health, drug and substance use, and other services they need for their overall health and wellbeing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"st\">The Strategy keeps people at the center and aims to unite countries, communities, and partners across and beyond the HIV response to take prioritized actions to accelerate progress towards the vision of zero new HIV infections, zero discrimination, and zero AIDS-related deaths.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"st\">This Strategy lays out a framework for transformative action to reduce the inequalities by 2025 and to get every country and every community on track to end AIDS by 2030.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6020","text":"A 26-year-old pregnant lab​ourer who is HIV-positive​ delivers a healthy male ​baby. She was advised ART‍ previously but has been ​non-compliant with her tr​eatment. What is recommen‌ded by NACO with regards ‌to feeding the baby?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old pregnant labourer who is HIV-positive delivers a healthy male baby. She was advised ART previously but has been non-compliant with her treatment. What is recommended by NACO with regards to feeding the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No breas​t-feeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimal breast-feeding wit​h complementary feeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exclusive br​east-feeding","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Safe &amp;amp; sustainable alt​ernative formula feeds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Exclusive breast-feeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Exclusive breast-feeding</strong></p><p>NACO recommends <strong>exclusive breastfeeding</strong> as the infant feeding choice in the <strong>first 6 months. </strong>This is irrespective of the fact that the mother is on ART or the infant is provided with ARV prophylaxis for 6 weeks.</p>\n<p>Breast-feeding and replacement feed simultaneously (<strong>mixed feeds</strong>) in the first 6 months should not be done, as it leads to <strong>mucosal abrasions</strong> in the gut of the baby, thereby <strong>facilitating HIV entry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Breast-feeding should not be stopped abruptly. Only in situations where breastfeeding cannot be done (maternal death, severe maternal illness) or individual mother’s choice (at her own risk), then exclusive replacement feeding which is safe, sustainable, affordable and feasible can be started.</p>\n<p>In resource-rich settings/developed countries, <strong>exclusive alternative formulations</strong> are recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6007","text":"You are an intern posted i​n the postnatal ward of a​ hospital. You receive an​ HIV-positive patient on ‍PND-1. Which of the follo​wing tests would you use ​to detect HIV in the baby‌?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the postnatal ward of a hospital. You receive an HIV-positive patient on PND-1. Which of the following tests would you use to detect HIV in the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV D​NA PCR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cord Blo​od ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wester​n Blot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3rd Genera​tion ELISA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HIV DNA PCR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HIV DNA PCR</strong></p><p>HIV in a <strong>baby</strong> born to an<strong> HIV-infected mother</strong> is diagnosed using <strong>HIV DNA PCR.</strong></p>\n<p>NACO recommends the use of a qualitative <strong>HIV-1 DNA PCR (NAT) </strong>in <strong>children aged &lt;18months</strong></p>\n<p>Antibody assays (e.g., ELISA) that are commonly used for HIV testing in adults and older children are not reliable in infants and young children because of the <strong>persistence of transplacentally acquired maternal antibodies</strong> until <strong>18 months</strong> after birth. For this reason, HIV virologic testing (PCR) must be performed using tests that detect HIV DNA or RNA. <strong>Scheduled repeated testing</strong> is necessary because test sensitivity increases with time from HIV virus exposure. Also, a very recent infection will not be detectable by PCR.</p>\n<p><strong>Cord blood should not be used</strong> for testing because of the possibility of contamination of the sample with maternal blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2481","text":"The image given below depi​cts the mode of infection​ with which of the follow​ing organisms?","raw_text":"The image given below depicts the mode of infection with which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po​lio","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rota ​virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Polio","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8675195a482f41c480611fcaa4c57b2c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21084776a3214b149c8df577f4393d98x1280x1507.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Polio</strong></p><p>The given image shows, infection through fecal oral route, colonization of the organism, and replication in the GI mucosa (<strong>M cells</strong>), followed by entry into blood via regional lymph nodes. In blood, it causes viremia and gets access to the CNS via the peripheral nerves, finally reaching the <strong>anterior horn cells</strong> in the spinal cord. The pathogenesis expalined above is that of polio infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Infection with <em>Campylobacter jejuni</em> is implicated in the pathogenesis of Guillain–Barre syndrome, where the antibodies against <em>C. jejuni</em> cross-react with the gangliosides (GM1) in the peripheral nervous system.</p>\n<p>Option B: Hepatitis A infection occurs through the fecal-oral route, and the virus replicates in the GI mucosa and is then transported to the liver.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rotavirus causes diarrhea by causing malabsorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5781","text":"Kenny packs are used in th​e treatment of ________","raw_text":"Kenny packs are used in the treatment of ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poliom​yelitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muscular ​dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyneur​opathies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve ​injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Poliomyelitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Poliomyelitis</strong></p><p>Kenny packs are used in the treatment of poliomyelitis.</p>\n<p>Treatment of poliomyelitis includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptomatic and supportive treatment: Analgesics, bed rest, and physiotherapy</li>\n<li><strong>Sister Kenny’s treatment:</strong> Hot moist packs applied to muscles to relieve pain and spasm</li>\n<li>Optimum positioning of limbs to prevent deformity</li>\n<li>Management of pure bulbar poliomyelitis consists of maintaining the airway and avoiding the risk of aspiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_cwnisup","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"16_immunodeficiency_syndromes","title":"16.Immunodeficiency Syndromes","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB6950","text":"Leukocyte-adhesion deficie​ncy type 2 is due to the ‍absence of which of the f​ollowing?","raw_text":"Leukocyte-adhesion deficiency type 2 is due to the absence of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sele​ctin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LF​A-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TL​R-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sialyl-​Lewis X","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sialyl-Lewis X","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/102c9a321a2541d282bc165614b1cedax1280x1743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sialyl-Lewis X</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Leukocyte-adhesion deficiency </strong>(LAD)<strong> type 2</strong> is due to the absence of <strong>Sialyl-Lewis X.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Physiologically Sialyl-Lewis X has the necessary ligand for the selectins which bind to leukocytes. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Inherited defects </strong>in leukocyte adhesion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Type 1 -</strong> defect in the biosynthesis of the <strong>β<span class=\"s2\">2 </span>chain</strong> shared by the <strong>LFA-1 </strong>and <strong>Mac-1 integrins</strong>          </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Type 2 -</strong><strong> absence </strong>of<strong> sialyl-Lewis X</strong>, the fucose containing ligand for E- and P-selectins. It is due to a defect in the <strong>fucosyltransferase</strong>, the enzyme that attaches fucose moieties to protein backbones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The major clinical problem in both conditions is <strong>recurrent bacterial infections</strong> due to inadequate granulocyte function.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6951","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about Ch‍édiak-Higashi syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Chédiak-Higashi syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is aberrant phagolys​osome fusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has an autosomal recess​ive inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Investigations show neutro​penia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granules are absent in whi​te blood cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Granules are absent in white blood cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27e3a9db7c17473eaea84e90b74d0484x1280x1148.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1337d3c5f56a41dc86e8dce908b721bdx1280x1148.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Granules are absent in white blood cells</strong></p><p><strong>Leukocytes </strong>contain <strong>giant granules </strong>in <strong>Chédiak–Higashi syndrome</strong> as seen in peripheral blood smears. They are due to aberrant phagolysosome fusion.</p>\n<p>The image<strong> </strong>shows giant granules in a neutrophil in Chédiak–Higashi syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition. The <strong>defective fusion</strong> of <strong>phagosomes</strong> and <strong>lysosomes</strong> results in defective phagocytes function and <strong>susceptibility</strong> to <strong>infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>The abnormalities are <strong>neutropenia</strong>, defective degranulation, and delayed microbial killing, causing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Albinism - melanocytes</li>\n<li>Nerve defects - nervous system cells</li>\n<li>Bleeding disorders - platelets</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>gene</strong> associated with this disorder encodes a large cytosolic protein called<strong> LYST,</strong> which is believed to <strong>regulate</strong> lysosomal trafficking.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6952","text":"A 2-month-old baby was bro​ught with complaints of f‍ever and fast breathing f​or 5 days, and was diagno‌sed with pneumonia. Furth​er history revealed baby ​had recurrent infections.‌ Blood culture revealed i‍nfection from Nocardia sp​p. Nitroblue tetrazolium ‍test turned out to be neg​ative. Which of the follo‍wing statement is false r​egarding this condition?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old baby was brought with complaints of fever and fast breathing for 5 days, and was diagnosed with pneumonia. Further history revealed baby had recurrent infections. Blood culture revealed infection from Nocardia spp. Nitroblue tetrazolium test turned out to be negative. Which of the following statement is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has autosomal recessive​ inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is due to oxidase defic​iency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defective superoxide produ​ction by granulocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Due to lymphocytic inflamm​atory reaction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Due to lymphocytic inflammatory reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Due to lymphocytic inflammatory reaction</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests<strong> chronic granulomatous disease. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is due to<strong> </strong>a<strong> macrophage</strong>-rich chronic inflammatory reaction, not lymphocytic<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Chronic granulomatous disease is a defect in <strong>bacterial killing</strong> and renders patients susceptible to <strong>recurrent </strong>bacterial<strong> infections</strong>. The name of this disease comes from the macrophage-rich chronic inflammatory reaction that tries to control the infection when the initial neutrophil defense is inadequate. This often leads to <strong>collections</strong> of <strong>activated macrophages</strong> that wall off the microbes, forming <strong>granulomas.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6953","text":"Match the following comple​ment deficiencies with th‍e disorders. Complement d​eficient Disorders 1. C1 ‌esterase inhibitor a. Hem​olytic uremic syndrome 2.​ C3 b. Hereditary angione‌urotic edema 3. Factor H ‍c. Recurrent infection wi​th Neisseria 4. C5 to C8 ‍d. Glomerulonephritis","raw_text":"<p>Match the following complement deficiencies with the disorders.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 149px; width: 414.953px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Complement deficient</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.953px;\">Disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">1. C1 esterase inhibitor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.953px;\">a. Hemolytic uremic syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">2. C3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.953px;\">b. Hereditary angioneurotic edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">3. Factor H</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.953px;\">c. Recurrent infection with <em>Neisseria</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">4. C5 to C8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.953px;\">d. Glomerulonephritis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-d, 2-c,​ 3-a, 4-b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-d,​ 3-a, 4-c","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-d,​ 3-b, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-d, 2-a,​ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c</strong></p><p>The <strong>disorders</strong> caused due to <strong>complement deficiency</strong> are:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Complement</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Disease/syndrome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>C1 esterase inhibitor</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Hereditary angioneurotic edema</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>C1, C2, C4</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Pyogenic infections, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>C1q, C1r, C1s</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Pyogenic infections, SLE</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>C3</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Pyogenic infections, <strong>Glomerulonephritis</strong>, SLE</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Factor H</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Hemolytic uremic syndrome</strong> (HUS), Pyogenic infections, Glomerulonephritis, SLE</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>C5 to C8</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Recurrent infections with <strong><em>Neisseria</em></strong> spp.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Properdin</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Meningococcal meningitis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Factor D</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Meningitis, sepsis - <em>Neisseria</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Factors H and I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Recurrent pyogenic infections</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>DAFF and CD59</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Mannan-binding lectin</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Respiratory tract infections</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>MASP-2</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Respiratory tract infections</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>C2</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> complement protein deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6954","text":"A patient presents with re​current swelling of the f‍ace and lips as shown wit​h vomiting and diarrhea w‌hen emotionally stressed.​ What is the most likely ​deficiency?","raw_text":"A patient presents with recurrent swelling of the face and lips as shown with vomiting and diarrhea when emotionally stressed. What is the most likely deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C1 inh​ibitor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C1, C​2, C4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C1 inhibitor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a762a3f77ef04cdeaf2c4c51b63100ff.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C1 inhibitor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hereditary angioneurotic edema. </strong>It is due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>C1 inhibitors</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hereditary angioneurotic edema is an<strong> autosomal dominant </strong>disorder,<strong> </strong>which is more common than complement deficiency states. </p>\n<p>Targets of C1 inhibitors are proteases, specifically C1r and C1s of the complement cascade, factor XII of the coagulation pathway, and the kallikrein system. <strong>Deficiency</strong> of <strong>C1 inhibitor</strong> leads to <strong>unregulated</strong> <strong>activation</strong> of <strong>kallikrein</strong>. This increases the production of vasoactive peptides such as <strong>bradykinin</strong>.</p>\n<p>It causes episodes of <strong>edema</strong> affecting the skin and mucosal surfaces such as the larynx and the gastrointestinal tract. This may result in life-threatening <strong>asphyxia</strong> or nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea after minor trauma or emotional stress.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6956","text":"A 4-month-old baby was bro​ught with severe diaper r‍ash. The parents gave a h​istory of recurrent infec‌tions in the past 4 month​s which required frequent​ hospitalization. On exam‌ination white patches wer‍e present in oral mucosa ​which could be scraped ea‍sily. The biopsy of the t​hymus showed remnants of ‍Hassall's corpuscles. Whi​ch of the following state​ments is false about this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old baby was brought with severe diaper rash. The parents gave a history of recurrent infections in the past 4 months which required frequent hospitalization.  On examination white patches were present in oral mucosa which could be scraped easily. The biopsy of the thymus showed remnants of Hassall's corpuscles. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has autosomal inheritan​ce pattern","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenosine deaminase enzyme​ is deficient","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is due to defect only i​n cell mediated immune re‍sponse","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"B cell count can be normal​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is due to defect only in cell mediated immune response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is due to defect only in cell mediated immune response</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>severe combined immunodeficiency</strong> (SCID). The biopsy of the thymus showing remnants of <strong>Hassall's corpuscles</strong> indicates it is due to <strong>adenosine deaminase </strong>(ADA)<strong> deficiency</strong>. SCID is a defect in <strong>both humoral</strong> and <strong>cell-mediated</strong> immune responses. Usually the defect lies in the T-cell compartment with secondary impairment in humoral immunity.</p>\n<p>Affected infants present with prominent thrush (<strong>oral candidiasis</strong>), extensive diaper rash, and<strong> failure to thrive. </strong>Some patients develop a <strong>morbilliform rash </strong>shortly<strong> after birth</strong> because <strong>maternal T cells</strong> are transferred across the placenta and <strong>attack</strong> the <strong>fetus,</strong> causing graft-versus-host disease (GVHD).</p>\n<p>Persons with SCID are extremely susceptible to recurrent, <strong>severe infections</strong> by a wide range of pathogens, including: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Candida albicans</em></li>\n<li><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci</em></li>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas</em></li>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus</li>\n<li>Varicella</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are 2 <strong>types of SCID</strong> based on inheritance:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>X-linked </strong>SCID is the most common form and hence it<strong> </strong>is<strong> more common </strong>in<strong> boys </strong>than in girls. The genetic defect is the <strong>mutation </strong>in the<strong> common γ-chain (γc) </strong>subunit of<strong> cytokine </strong>receptors. <strong>T-cell</strong> numbers are greatly <strong>reduced</strong>, and although <strong>B cells</strong> may be <strong>normal</strong> in number, <strong>antibody</strong> synthesis is <strong>impaired</strong> because of the <strong>lack of T-cell</strong> help. The thymus contains lobules of<strong> undifferentiated </strong>epithelial cells resembling fetal thymus.</p>\n<p>2.<strong> Autosomal recessive </strong>is most commonly caused due to<strong> deficiency </strong>of the enzyme<strong> adenosine deaminase </strong>(ADA). The<strong> deficiency</strong> of ADA leads to the <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>deoxyadenosine</strong> and its derivatives (e.g., deoxy-ATP). They are <strong>toxic</strong> to rapidly dividing immature lymphocytes, especially those of the <strong>T-cell lineage</strong>. Hence, there may be a greater reduction in the number of T lymphocytes.</p>\n<p>In the two most common forms (γc mutation and ADA deficiency), the <strong>thymus </strong>is <strong>small </strong>and <strong>devoid </strong>of <strong>lymphoid cells. </strong>In SCID caused by <strong>ADA</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong>, remnants of <strong>Hassall’s corpuscles</strong> can be found.</p>\n<p>Note: X-linked SCID is the first human disease in which gene therapy has been successful.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0150","text":"Which gene is not involved​ in SCID?","raw_text":"Which gene is not involved in SCID?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B​TK","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"JA​K-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RA​G1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL-​7Rα","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. BTK","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BTK</strong></p><p><strong>BTK</strong> mutation causes <strong>X-linked agammaglobulinemia</strong> and not SCID (severe combined immunodeficiency).</p>\n<p><strong>Severe combined immunodeficiency </strong>is a defect in <strong>both humoral</strong> and <strong>cell-mediated</strong> immune responses. In this condition, the following <strong>genes</strong> are involved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>JAK3</li>\n<li>RAG1</li>\n<li>IL-7R</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Persons with SCID are extremely susceptible to recurrent, <strong>severe infections</strong> by a wide range of pathogens, including: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Candida albicans</em></li>\n<li><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci</em></li>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas</em></li>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus</li>\n<li>Varicella</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6957","text":"A 9-month-old baby was bro​ught with a cough and dif‍ficulty in breathing. The​ baby was admitted with s‌imilar complaints in the ​past. On auscultation, co​arse crepitations were he‌ard. Quantitative serum i‍mmunoglobulin analysis wa​s done and serum IgG, IgA‍, and IgM levels were low​. His cellular immunity w‍as found to be normal. Wh​ich of the following is f​alse about his condition?‌","raw_text":"A 9-month-old baby was brought with a cough and difficulty in breathing. The baby was admitted with similar complaints in the past. On auscultation, coarse crepitations were heard. Quantitative serum immunoglobulin analysis was done and serum IgG, IgA, and IgM levels were low. His cellular immunity was found to be normal. Which of the following is false about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked i​nheritence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Germinal centres of lymph ​nodes are underdeveloped","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Manifestation is seen usua​lly after 6 months of age‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Not associated with autoim​mune disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Not associated with autoimmune disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Not associated with autoimmune disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Bruton agammaglobulinemia</strong>/ <strong>X-linked agammaglobulinemia</strong> (XLA). Even though it is an immunodeficiency disease, it<strong> </strong>is paradoxically <strong>associated </strong>with<strong> autoimmune diseases</strong> such as arthritis and dermatomyositis in 35% of individuals.</p>\n<p>The classic form of this disease has the following <strong>characteristics</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B cells </strong>are <strong>absent </strong>or markedly decreased and the serum levels of all classes of <strong>immunoglobulins </strong>are<strong> decreased</strong></li>\n<li><strong>T-cell </strong>mediated reactions are<strong> normal</strong></li>\n<li>Germinal centers of lymph nodes, Peyer’s patches, the appendix, and tonsils are underdeveloped</li>\n<li>Plasma cells are absent throughout the body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>XLA is characterized by the <strong>failure</strong> of <strong>B-cell precursors</strong> (pro-B cells and pre-B cells) to develop into <strong>mature</strong> B cells. It is associated with mutations in a cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase called <strong>Bruton tyrosine kinase </strong>(Btk).</p>\n<p>The related <strong>gene</strong> is <strong>located</strong> on the long arm of the X chromosome.<strong> </strong>This X-linked disease is seen almost entirely in <strong>males. </strong>The disease usually does not become apparent until about 6 months of age, when<strong> maternal</strong> <strong>immunoglobulins</strong> are <strong>depleted</strong>.</p>\n<p>Affected individuals are susceptible to <strong>infections</strong> with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bacteria that are normally opsonized by antibodies and cleared by phagocytosis\n<ul>\n<li><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></li>\n<li><em>Streptococcus pneumonia</em></li>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><em>Giardia lamblia </em>- intestinal protozoan that is normally resisted by secreted IgA, causes persistent infections </li>\n<li>Enteroviruses\n<ul>\n<li>Echovirus</li>\n<li>Poliovirus</li>\n<li>Coxsackievirus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6958","text":"A 2-year-old boy was broug​ht to the OPD with delaye‍d development. On examina​tion, he had the followin‌g appearance. Further wor​kup revealed hypocalcemia​ and an atrial septal def‌ect. Chromosomal studies ‍showed deletion of 22q11.​ All are true about this ‍syndrome except:","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy was brought to the OPD with delayed development. On examination, he had the following appearance. Further workup revealed hypocalcemia and an atrial septal defect.  Chromosomal studies showed deletion of 22q11. All are true about this syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with T ce​ll deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thymic aplasia is a promin​ent feature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parathyroid gland is defec​tive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of development of ​3rd and 4th pharyngeal ar‍ches","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Failure of development of 3rd and 4th pharyngeal arches","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a09c1ff4cad64b74844f1d30f280f1cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Failure of development of 3rd and 4th pharyngeal arches</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the image suggests<strong> DiGeorge syndrome. </strong>It<strong> </strong>results from the <strong>failure</strong> of the development of the <strong>3rd</strong> and <strong>4th pharyngeal pouches</strong>. They give rise to the thymus, the parathyroids, some of the C cells of the thyroid, and the ultimobranchial body.</p>\n<p>The<strong> absence</strong> of <strong>cell-mediated</strong> immunity causes <strong>poor defense</strong> against certain <strong>fungal</strong> and <strong>viral</strong> infections. The <strong>T-cell zones</strong> of lymphoid organs, i.e. paracortical areas of the <strong>lymph nodes</strong> and the periarteriolar sheaths of the <strong>spleen</strong>,<strong> </strong>are depleted. Ig levels may be normal or reduced, depending on the severity of the T-cell deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6959","text":"Which of the following is ​least likely to be a feat‍ure in a patient with Hyp​er-IgM syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is least likely to be a feature in a patient with Hyper-IgM syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has X-linked mode of in​heritance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They have normal serum IgG​ levels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemi​a can be seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They have normal B-cell an​d T-cell numbers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They have normal serum IgG levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They have normal serum IgG levels</strong></p><p>The serum of patients with <strong>Hyper-IgM syndrome</strong> contains extremely <strong>low levels of IgG.</strong></p>\n<p>In Hyper-IgM syndrome production of <strong>IgM antibodies </strong>is<strong> normal</strong> but the ability to produce <strong>IgG, IgA,</strong> and <strong>IgE</strong> antibodies is <strong>deficient</strong>. There are normal or elevated levels of IgM, no IgA or IgE, and extremely low levels of IgG. It affects the ability of helper T cells to deliver activating signals to B cells and macrophages. However,<strong> B cells</strong> and <strong>T cells</strong> counts are <strong>normal</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is mostly an <strong>X-linked</strong> disease, that has <strong>mutations</strong> in the <strong>gene</strong> encoding <strong>CD40L</strong> located on Xq26. If the disease is inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> pattern, then there are <strong>loss-of-function</strong> mutations involving either <strong>CD40</strong> or the enzyme called <strong>activation-induced cytidine deaminase</strong> (AID).</p>\n<p>Those with <strong>CD40L</strong> mutations are also susceptible to <strong>pneumonia </strong>caused by the intracellular organism<em><strong> Pneumocystis jiroveci. </strong></em>This is due to <strong>CD40L-</strong>mediated <strong>macrophage activation</strong>, a key reaction of cell-mediated immunity being <strong>defective</strong>. Recurrent pyogenic infection occurs because the level of <strong>opsonizing IgG</strong> antibodies is <strong>low</strong>.</p>\n<p>Occasionally, the IgM antibodies react with blood cells, giving rise to <strong>autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong>, thrombocytopenia, and neutropenia. In older patients, the <strong>proliferation</strong> of IgM-producing <strong>plasma cells</strong> that infiltrate the mucosa of the <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0149","text":"A 3-year-old boy presents ​with recurrent bacterial ‍infections and Pneumocyst​is jiroveci pneumonia. Th‌e flow cytometry analysis​ of the boy (Boxes 3&amp; 4) ​is shown below (Boxes 1 &amp;‌ 2 are normal). What is t‍he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy presents with recurrent bacterial infections and Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia. The flow cytometry analysis of the boy (Boxes 3&amp; 4) is shown below (Boxes 1 &amp; 2 are normal). What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bare lym​phocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic granulocyte defici​ency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyper IgM​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DiGeorge​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyper IgM syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39dfc36475f94e52bc7fd4648aed92a4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50817ec07cd446f9a6490e6810ea3fd2x1279x1080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyper IgM syndrome</strong></p><p>The deficiency of the CD40 and its absence in B cells <strong>interferes </strong>with<strong> class switching</strong> and the <strong>production</strong> of <strong>memory cells</strong>. The B-cell response to antigens, is unaffected, accounting for the presence of increased IgM antibodies. Hence, the condition is known as Hyper IgM syndrome.</p>\n<p>The given clinical scenario and the flow cytometry is suggestive of <strong>Hyper IgM syndrome.</strong> The flow cytometry shows the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In comparison of image 3 to 1, where side scatter is plotted against CD 19: B-cell gating is present. <br/>Gating of B cells means selecting a cell during a flow cytometry experiment for further analysis or data presentation.CD 19 is widely expressed on B cells. Hence the graph shows B cell gating. Side scatter measurement provides information about the granularity of a cell.</li>\n<li>In comparison of image 4 to 2, where CD 40 is plotted against CD 19: B cells are present only in quadrant 4, which means that there is <strong>CD 40 deficiency.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The deficiency of the CD40 and its absence in B cells <strong>interferes </strong>with <strong>class switching</strong> and the <strong>production of memory cells</strong>. The B-cell response to antigens is unaffected, accounting for the presence of increased IgM antibodies. Hence, the condition is known as Hyper IgM syndrome.</p>\n<p>Given below is the explanation of how to interpret flow cytometry results:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Interpretation of results:</p>\n<p>Quadrant 1- Cells marked in green: -VE for A antigen, +VE for B antigen</p>\n<p>Quadrant 2- Cells marked in purple: -VE for both the antigens.</p>\n<p>Quadrant 3- Cells marked in yellow: +VE for both the antigens </p>\n<p>Quadrant 4- Cells marked in red: +VE for A antigen, -VE for B antigen</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6961","text":"Patients with isolated Ig ​A deficiency would have a‍ssociated deficiency of w​hich of the following IgG‌ subtypes?","raw_text":"Patients with isolated Ig A deficiency would have associated deficiency of which of the following IgG subtypes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG1,​ IgG3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgG1,​ IgG4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgG2,​ IgG4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IgG2,​ IgG3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IgG2, IgG4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IgG2, IgG4</strong></p><p><strong>Isolated IgA deficiency</strong> is associated with <strong>deficiency</strong> of IgG subtypes <strong>IgG2</strong> and <strong>IgG4</strong>.  </p>\n<p>IgA deficiency-affected individuals have extremely <strong>low levels </strong>of both<strong> serum </strong>and <strong>secretory IgA</strong>. It may be <strong>familial</strong> or <strong>acquired</strong> in association with toxoplasmosis, measles, or some other viral infection.</p>\n<p>Because IgA is the major antibody in <strong>external secretions</strong>, mucosal defenses are weakened, and infections occur in the respiratory, gastrointestinal, and urogenital tracts. Symptomatic patients commonly have <strong>recurrent sinopulmonary infections</strong> and <strong>diarrhea. </strong></p>\n<p>IgA-deficient patients have a high frequency of respiratory tract <strong>allergy</strong> and a variety of autoimmune diseases, particularly systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE)</strong> and <strong>rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6962","text":"Which of the following are​ mutated in X-linked lymp‍hoproliferative syndrome?​","raw_text":"Which of the following are mutated in X-linked lymphoproliferative syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SLAM-associ​ated protein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"LY​ST","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B​TK","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TB​X1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. SLAM-associated protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. SLAM-associated protein</strong></p><p>The<strong> X-linked lymphoproliferative</strong> syndrome is due to mutations in the gene encoding an adaptor molecule called <strong>SLAM-associated protein </strong>(SAP).</p>\n<p>It is characterized by an <strong>inability </strong>to <strong>eliminate </strong>Epstein-Barr virus (<strong>EBV</strong>), eventually leading to <strong>fulminant infectious mononucleosis </strong>and the development of <strong>B-cell tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>In about 80% of cases, the disease is due to <strong>mutations </strong>in the gene encoding the adaptor molecule<strong> SAP. </strong>Defects in SAP contribute to <strong>attenuated NK</strong> <strong>cell</strong> and<strong> T-cell activation, </strong>resulting in increased susceptibility to viral infections.</p>\n<p>Option B: LYST gene is associated with Chediak-Higashi syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: BTK gene is associated with Isolated growth hormone deficiency type III.</p>\n<p>Option D: TBX1 gene is associated with 22q11.2 deletion syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2353","text":"Which of the following lym​phoid tissue findings is ‍seen most commonly in pat​ients with common variabl‌e hypogammaglobulinemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following lymphoid tissue findings is seen most commonly in patients with common variable hypogammaglobulinemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased B​-cell count","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased B​-cell count","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal B-​cell count","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absent ​B-cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Normal B-cell count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Normal B-cell count</strong></p><p><strong>Normal B-cell count </strong>is most commonly seen in lymphoid tissues of individuals with the <strong>common variable hypogammaglobulinemia.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a heterogeneous group of disorders in which the common feature is <strong>hypogammaglobulinemia</strong>, generally affecting all the antibody classes, but sometimes only IgG.</p>\n<p>In contrast to X-linked agammaglobulinemia, most individuals with common variable immunodeficiency have <strong>normal</strong> or near-normal numbers of <strong>B</strong>-<strong>cells</strong> in the blood and lymphoid tissues. These B cells, however, are <strong>not able</strong> to <strong>differentiate</strong> into <strong>plasma cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>FDA approved <strong>XEMBIFY </strong>in 2019, 20% immunoglobulin solution for subcutaneous injection. It is indicated for the use of primary humoral immunodeficiency disorders in patients 2years of age and older.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6963","text":"A 3-year-old boy is brough​t with complaints of red ‍rashes over his skin. His​ mother says that the chi‌ld will be constantly scr​atching the rashes. On as​king any previous history‌ she says the child keeps‍ getting recurrent infect​ions which mostly require‍ hospitalization. Blood i​nvestigations showed a lo‍w platelet count. Which o​f the following is false ​regarding this syndrome?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy is brought with complaints of red rashes over his skin. His mother says that the child will be constantly scratching the rashes. On asking any previous history she says the child keeps getting recurrent infections which mostly require hospitalization. Blood investigations showed a low platelet count. Which of the following is false regarding this syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG n​ormal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgA el​evated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgE el​evated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgM n​ormal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgM normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgM normal</strong></p><p>The given history with eczema, recurrent infections, and thrombocytopenia points towards <strong>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome. </strong>In this condition, <strong>IgM levels </strong>in the serum are <strong>low.</strong></p>\n<p>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome is caused by mutations in the gene encoding the Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome protein (<strong>WASP</strong>), which is located on Xp11.23. The X-linked disease is characterized by <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>, <strong>eczema</strong>, and a marked vulnerability to <strong>recurrent infection</strong>, resulting in <strong>early death.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Platelets</strong> are <strong>small</strong> in size. The <strong>thymus</strong> is morphologically <strong>normal</strong>, at least early in the course of the disease, but there is the <strong>progressive loss</strong> of <strong>T lymphocytes</strong> in the peripheral blood and in the T-cell zones (paracortical areas) of the lymph nodes, with variable defects in cellular immunity.</p>\n<p>Patients do not make antibodies to polysaccharide antigens, and the response to protein antigens is poor. IgM levels are low, IgG is usually normal, while the levels of <strong>IgA</strong> and <strong>IgE</strong> are often <strong>elevated</strong>. Patients are prone to developing <strong>B-cell lymphomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6964","text":"A 5-year-old girl was brou​ght to the OPD with compl‍aints of repeated falls w​hile walking for the past‌ 1 year. She had difficul​ty in eating, gripping, o​r holding things. On exam‌ination, hand mouth incoo‍rdination and slurring of​ speech were noted. On oc‍ular examination, the fol​lowing finding was seen. ‍All are true about this c​ondition except:","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl was brought to the OPD with complaints of repeated falls while walking for the past 1 year. She had difficulty in eating, gripping, or holding things. On examination, hand mouth incoordination and slurring of speech were noted.  On ocular examination, the following finding was seen. All are true about this condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is defective product​ion of IgA and IgG2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Affected gene is located o​n chromosome 11","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal-dominant inherit​ance is seen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is increased risk of​ malignancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Autosomal-dominant inheritance is seen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e67de9e9e13747d29d6d5920fab78046.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Autosomal-dominant inheritance is seen</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and image showing telangiectasia are diagnostic of<strong> ataxia-telangiectasia. </strong>It is an<strong> autosomal-recessive</strong> disorder, not autosomal dominant.</p>\n<p>The most prominent humoral immune abnormalities are <strong>defective production </strong>of<strong> IgA </strong>and<strong> IgG2</strong>. The gene responsible for this disorder is located on <strong>chromosome 11 </strong>and encodes a protein called<strong> ATM. </strong>The ATM protein is a sensor of<strong> DNA damage </strong>(double-strand breaks), and it <strong>activates p53 </strong>by phosphorylation.</p>\n<p>Ataxia-telangiectasia is<strong> </strong>characterized by abnormal gait <strong>(ataxia),</strong> vascular malformations <strong>(telangiectases), neurologic</strong> deficits, increased incidence of <strong>tumors,</strong> and <strong>immunodeficiency. </strong>It may affect <strong>both B </strong>and<strong> T cells. </strong></p>\n<p>There is a high incidence of <strong>lymphomas</strong> and <strong>leukemias.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8247","text":"Human immunodeficiency vir​us affects CD4 cells due ‍to their interaction with​ which protein?","raw_text":"Human immunodeficiency virus affects CD4 cells due to their interaction with which protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"gp ​120","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"gp​ 41","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CC​R5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CX​CR4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. gp 120","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20ae6e1960c24f3b921c3c7d7fcebbf4x642x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. gp 120</strong></p><p>The <strong>CD4 cells</strong> are affected by HIV due to their <strong>interaction</strong> with <strong>gp120.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 253\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>HIV-1 virion is spherical and contains an electron-dense, cone-shaped <strong>core</strong> surrounded by a <strong>lipid envelope</strong> derived from the host cell membrane. Target cells of HIV include <strong>CD4+</strong> <strong>helper T</strong> <strong>cells </strong>(worst affected), monocyte-macrophages, and dendritic cells.</p>\n<p>The <strong>virus core</strong> contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major capsid protein <strong>p24</strong></li>\n<li>Nucleocapsid protein <strong>p7/p9</strong></li>\n<li>Two copies of viral genomic <strong>RNA</strong></li>\n<li>The three viral <strong>enzymes</strong> (protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The viral <strong>core</strong> is surrounded by a <strong>matrix protein</strong> called <strong>p17</strong>, which lies underneath the virion envelope. The <strong>viral envelope</strong> is studded by two viral glycoproteins, <strong>gp120 </strong>and<strong> gp41</strong>, which are critical for HIV infection of cells.</p>\n<p>Viral glycoprotein <strong>gp120</strong> binds to CD4+ cells in the host. <strong>CXCR4</strong> and <strong>CCR5</strong> function as coreceptors for <strong>HIV-1</strong> entry into CD4+ cells. This binding leads to a <strong>conformational change</strong> in <strong>gp41</strong> causing exposure of a hydrophobic region called the fusion peptide at the tip of gp41. This peptide inserts into the cell membrane of the target cells (e.g., T cells or macrophages), leading to the fusion of the virus with the host cell. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The image below shows the pathogenesis of HIV infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2350","text":"Which of the following cel​l types is not a target f‍or the initiation and mai​ntenance of HIV infection‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following cell types is not a target for the initiation and maintenance of HIV infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD4 T​ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macro​phage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dendrit​ic cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutr​ophil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neutrophil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19108ff003474b7e893a3d7ed4b25d13x642x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neutrophil</strong></p><p>The neutrophil is not a target for the initiation and maintenance of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection.</p>\n<p><strong>HIV</strong> enters the body and affects T cells, <strong>dendritic cells</strong>, and <strong>macrophages</strong>. Particularly, <strong>memory</strong> and <strong>activated T cells </strong>are infected, but it is inefficient at productively infecting naive (unactivated) T cells.</p>\n<p>Completion of the viral life cycle in latently infected cells occurs only after cell activation, and in the case of most CD4+ T cells, virus activation results in cell lysis.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the pathogenesis of HIV infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8249","text":"Which is the most common C​NS neoplasm in HIV patien‍ts?","raw_text":"Which is the most common CNS neoplasm in HIV patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medulob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Astro​cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary CN​S lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gliobl​astoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary CNS lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary CNS lymphoma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> central nervous system (CNS) neoplasm in patients with HIV infection is <strong>primary CNS lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lymphomas</strong> are the most common CNS neoplasm in <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> individuals, including those with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) and immunosuppression after <strong>transplantation</strong>. They are associated with latent infection with <strong>Epstein–Barr virus </strong>(EBV). The vast majority of primary brain lymphomas are of <strong>B-cell origin.</strong></p>\n<p>At least two <strong>mechanisms</strong> appear to underlie the increased risk of <strong>B-cell tumors</strong> in <strong>HIV</strong>-infected individuals. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>unchecked proliferation</strong> of <strong>B cells</strong> infected with <strong>oncogenic herpes viruses</strong> in the setting of profound T-cell depletion (AIDS).</li>\n<li>Germinal center <strong>B-cell</strong> <strong>hyperplasia</strong> in the setting of early HIV infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common site for <strong>lymphoma</strong> in patients with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (<strong>AIDS</strong>) is the central nervous system (<strong>CNS</strong>)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8252","text":"A 25-year-old man, who is ​HIV-positive, presents wi‍th generalized lymphadeno​pathy. Which of the histo‌pathological features is ​least likely to be seen o​n lymph node biopsy?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man, who is HIV-positive, presents with generalized lymphadenopathy. Which of the histopathological features is least likely to be seen on lymph node biopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Florid reactive hyperplasi​a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicl​e lysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hematoxyl​in bodies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collection of monocytoid B​ Cells in sinuses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hematoxylin bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hematoxylin bodies</strong></p><p>Hematoxylin bodies are seen in lymph nodes in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).</p>\n<p><strong>Features </strong>of<strong> AIDS-related lymphadenopathy:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Early </strong>stages of HIV infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Florid hyperplasia</strong> of B cell follicles</li>\n<li><strong>Follicle lysis</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Monocytoid B cells</strong> in sinuses</li>\n<li>Neutrophils and dermatopathic lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>The interfollicular zone shows vascular proliferation, which may progress to Kaposi sarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Late </strong>stages<strong> </strong>of HIV infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe lymphoid involution</li>\n<li>Possible hyalinization of the germinal centers</li>\n<li>Burnt-out lymph nodes, which become atrophic and may harbor numerous opportunistic pathogens, often within macrophages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8254","text":"A 50-year-old HIV-seroposi​tive man presents with ra‍ised violaceous plaques o​ver the legs. Microscopy ‌shows a lesion in the der​mis with dilated, jagged ​vascular channels lined a‌nd surrounded by plump sp‍indle cells. It contains ​red cells with marked hem‍orrhage, hemosiderin pigm​ent, and mononuclear infl‍ammation. Mitotic figures​ are also seen. Which org​anism is associated with ‌this tumor?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old HIV-seropositive man presents with raised violaceous plaques over the legs. Microscopy shows a lesion in the dermis with dilated, jagged vascular channels lined and surrounded by plump spindle cells. It contains red cells with marked hemorrhage, hemosiderin pigment, and mononuclear inflammation. Mitotic figures are also seen. Which organism is associated with this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human herp​es virus 8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein b​arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bartonell​a henselae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human papi​llomavirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Human herpes virus 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e49a905232024d76b982c0e2dc72e30ax1012x675.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38be295ef41f4e5d86afdb7bd72188cdx1280x1057.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Human herpes virus 8</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong>, caused by<strong> human herpesvirus 8</strong> (HHV8). </p>\n<p>Kaposi sarcoma is a<strong> vascular neoplasm,</strong> that is highly associated with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). It is the <strong>most common</strong> cancer seen in patients having <strong>AIDS</strong>. Though the incidence of Kaposi carcinoma reduced, it still occurs in 2-3% of HIV-infected individuals.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pathogenesis</strong> of Kaposi sarcoma that is caused by HHV8 includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A virally encoded G protein that induces VEGF production, stimulating endothelial growth</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The lytic infection caused by HHV8 increases the pro-inflammatory cytokines</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HHV8 encoded proteins inhibit the p53 induced apoptosis</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The given images show coalescent red-purple macules and plaques of the skin and the histologic appearance of Kaposi sarcoma, demonstrating sheets of proliferating spindle cells:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8258","text":"A 45 -year-old HIV-positiv​e patient has patchy pneu‍monia. Smears of induced ​sputum and sedimented cel‌ls from lavage fluid stai​ned with methenamine silv​er are as seen below. Whi‌ch is the most likely org‍anism implicated?","raw_text":"A 45 -year-old HIV-positive patient has patchy pneumonia.  Smears of induced sputum and sedimented cells from lavage fluid stained with methenamine silver are as seen below. Which is the most likely organism implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nocardia ​asteroides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histoplasma​ capsulatum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocysti​s jirovecii","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pneumocystis jirovecii","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0946b139f064ef1925ed776ad39d9f4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c803eea37d6b43bb867d19ba3f0e73b1x1280x1057.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pneumocystis jirovecii</strong></p><p>The smears stained with methenamine silver show <strong>black</strong>-colored stained <strong>cysts</strong> resembling the<strong> crushed ping pong</strong> <strong>balls</strong> of <strong><em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Approximately 15% to 30% of untreated HIV-infected people develop pneumonia during the course of the disease. It is mainly caused by the fungus <em>P</em>.<em> jirovecii </em>(reactivation of prior latent infection).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8jR_YDZm","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"16_renin_angiotensin_aldosterone_system","title":"16.Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4694","text":"Which of the following ACE​ inhibitors is not a prod​rug?","raw_text":"Which of the following ACE inhibitors is not a prodrug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fosin​opril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enal​april","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rami​pril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lisin​opril","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lisinopril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lisinopril</strong></p><p><strong>Lisinopril</strong> is not a prodrug.</p>\n<p>All ACE inhibitors are prodrugs except lisinopril and <strong>captopril.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2148","text":"A patient with BP 225/145 ​presents with acute pulmo​nary edema and is diagnos‍ed with hypertensive emer‌gency. The active metabol‍ite of which ACE inhibito‍r is available for intrav‌enous use in this patient​?","raw_text":"A patient with BP 225/145 presents with acute pulmonary edema and is diagnosed with hypertensive emergency. The active metabolite of which ACE inhibitor is available for intravenous use in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rami​pril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perin​dopril","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enal​april","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fosin​opril","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enalapril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enalapril</strong></p><p><strong>Enalapril </strong>is available in its active form as<strong> enalaprilat </strong>for <strong>intravenous </strong>use<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Enalapril is a <strong>prodrug</strong> of enalaprilat. It is indicated in conditions where oral drugs cannot be used or rapid onset of action is desired like in a case of <strong>hypertensive emergency</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4695","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding ACE inh​ibitors is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding ACE inhibitors is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They inhibit the conversio​n of angiotensinogen to a​ngiotensin I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Omission of prior diuretic​ dose decreases the risk ​of postural hypotension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lisinopril is shorter acti​ng than enalapril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are contraindicated i​n diabetic patients","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Omission of prior diuretic dose decreases the risk of postural hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Omission of prior diuretic dose decreases the risk of postural hypotension</strong></p><p>The <strong>omission</strong> of prior <strong>diuretic</strong> dose <strong>decreases</strong> the risk of<strong> postural hypotension</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> cause arteriolar dilation and increase the compliance of larger arteries. ACE inhibitors induced hypotension is thereby due to a<strong> decrease in total peripheral resistance</strong>. In <strong>diuretic-treated</strong> patients, the <strong>renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system</strong> is more active to <strong>compensate for volume loss</strong>. Hence, <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> increase the risk of <strong>postural hypotension</strong> in these patients. </p>\n<p>Option A: ACE inhibitors inhibit the conversion of <strong>angiotensin I </strong>to<strong> angiotensin II.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Lisinopril is <strong>longer acting</strong> than enalapril. Lisinopril is usually <strong>administered once daily</strong> in all patients. Enalapril requires twice-daily administration in some patients.</p>\n<p>Option D: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are <strong>first-line</strong> agents in <strong>diabetic patients </strong>because of their<strong> renoprotective</strong> effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4708","text":"All of the following are i​ndications for the use of​ ACE inhibitors except:","raw_text":"All of the following are indications for the use of ACE inhibitors except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertensiv​e emergency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myocardial​ infarction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ventricular dysfuncti​on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pheochro​mocytoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pheochromocytoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pheochromocytoma</strong></p><p>ACE inhibitors are usually avoided in conditions like <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong> which is associated with <strong>high renin</strong> activity. <strong>Abrupt inhibition</strong> of the <strong>RAAS</strong> system can result in <strong>severe hypotension</strong> (usually postural).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 229\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The majority of pheochromocytomas are treated surgically. <strong>Phenoxybenzamine</strong> is often used in preparing the patient for <strong>surgery</strong> and also to manage hypertension in inoperable or malignant pheochromocytoma. <strong>Phentolamine</strong> can also be used in the short-term control of hypertension in these patients.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>ACE inhibitors are indicated in hypertensive emergency, myocardial infarction, and left ventricular dysfunction.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4709","text":"In which of the following ​patients are ACE inhibito​rs not contraindicated? A‍ 23-year-old primigravid ‌woman with preeclampsia A‍ 50-year-old man with uni‍lateral renal artery sten‌osis A 50-year-old man wi​th bilateral renal artery‍ stenosis A post-myocardi‍al infarction patient wit​h left ventricular systol​ic dysfunction A patient ​with scleroderma renal cr‌isis","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following patients are ACE inhibitors not contraindicated?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 23-year-old primigravid woman with preeclampsia</li>\n<li>A 50-year-old man with unilateral renal artery stenosis</li>\n<li>A 50-year-old man with bilateral renal artery stenosis</li>\n<li>A post-myocardial infarction patient with left ventricular systolic dysfunction</li>\n<li>A patient with scleroderma renal crisis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2 ​and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,4 ​and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ​and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2,4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2,4 and 5</strong></p><p>ACE inhibitors are not contraindicated in unilateral renal artery stenosis, left ventricular systolic dysfunction, and scleroderma renal crisis.</p>\n<p>Patient 1: ACE inhibitors are <strong>teratogenic</strong> and can cause hypoplasia of organs, renal failure, anuria, and growth retardation in the fetus. Therefore, they are <strong>contraindicated </strong>in<strong> pregnancy. </strong></p>\n<p>Patient 2: ACE inhibitors can be used with caution in unilateral renal artery stenosis if the other kidney is functioning normally.</p>\n<p>Patient 3: Efferent arteriolar constriction maintains the glomerular filtration pressure in <strong>bilateral renal artery stenosis</strong>. ACE inhibitors are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in bilateral renal stenosis as they can <strong>precipitate acute renal failure</strong> due to dilation of efferent arterioles and fall in glomerular filtration pressure.</p>\n<p>Patient 4: <strong>ACE inhibition</strong> in patients with systolic dysfunction prevents or <strong>delays</strong> the progression of <strong>heart failure</strong>, <strong>decreases</strong> the incidence of <strong>sudden death</strong> and <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>, and improves the quality of life. ACE inhibition reduces afterload and systolic wall stress, and both cardiac output and cardiac index increase.</p>\n<p>Patient 5: The use of ACE inhibitors considerably improves the survival of patients with scleroderma renal crisis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4697","text":"A 53-year-old hypertensive​ patient has been on ACE ​inhibitors for the last 1‍2 years. Which of the fol‌lowing effects would you ‍expect to see in this pat‍ient?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old hypertensive patient has been on ACE inhibitors for the last 12 years. Which of the following effects would you expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rightward shift in renal p​ressure natriuresis curve​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction in filtration fr​action","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Significant increase in he​art rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in compliance of ​large arteries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reduction in filtration fraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2612147181bf421c80443ad4c6b7cb6bx1280x1347.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reduction in filtration fraction</strong></p><p><strong>ACE inhibitors,</strong> when used for a long time in patients with hypertension, cause<strong> </strong>a<strong> reduction in filtration fraction.</strong></p>\n<p>Filtration fraction is the ratio of GFR to that of renal plasma flow. ACE inhibitors <strong>increase renal blood flow</strong> without altering GFR and thus result in reduction of filtration fraction.</p>\n<p>Option A: The graph plotted between urinary sodium excretion and mean blood pressure is known as the <strong>pressure natriuresis curve.</strong> For example, when dietary sodium intake is low, renin release is stimulated. <strong>Angiotensin II</strong> acts on the kidneys to shift the renal pressure – natriuresis curve to the <strong>right. </strong>This increases sodium and water retention. </p>\n<p>Conversely, when dietary Na+ is high or when ACE inhibitors are used, there is the <strong>withdrawal of angiotensin II</strong>. The renal pressure natriuresis curve shifts to the <strong>left.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image depicts a pressure natriuresis curve:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options C and D: ACE inhibitors do not cause a significant increase in heart rate and can have a <strong>favorable change</strong> in the <strong>compliance of large arteries</strong> on long-term use.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4700","text":"A patient who is started o​n lisinopril for hyperten​sion is concerned about i‍ts adverse effects. Which‌ among the following is n‍ot an adverse effect of t‍his drug?","raw_text":"A patient who is started on lisinopril for hypertension is concerned about its adverse effects. Which among the following is not an adverse effect of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysg​eusia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neutr​openia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin​ rash","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong>are associated with the development of <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyperkalemia occurs due to the inhibition of angiotensin II-mediated aldosterone secretion. Decreased aldosterone levels result in decreased tubular excretion of potassium in the kidneys leading to hyperkalemia.</p>\n<p>The adverse effects of ACE inhibitors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Cough</li>\n<li>Angioedema</li>\n<li><strong>Skin rash</strong></li>\n<li>Teratogenicity</li>\n<li><strong>Dysgeusia</strong> (alteration in or loss of taste)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 495\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Extremely rare but reversible side effects include <strong>neutropenia</strong>, glycosuria in the absence of hyperglycemia, anemia, and hepatotoxicity.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4701","text":"A patient presents to the ​casualty with cough, diff​iculty in breathing, and ‍non-indurating edema of h‌is face and lips. He had ‍recently been started on ‍ramipril for hypertension‌. Which among the followi​ng mediates these adverse‍ effects?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the casualty with cough, difficulty in breathing, and non-indurating edema of his face and lips. He had recently been started on ramipril for hypertension. Which among the following mediates these adverse effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bradykinin​ and renin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiotensin II and bradyki​nin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Only br​adykinin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only bradykinin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only bradykinin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema</strong> which occurs due to <strong>bradykinin.</strong></p>\n<p>Angioedema is most commonly mediated by histamine. However, bradykinin-mediated angioedema, though less common, can result due to hereditary or acquired <strong>defects in C1 esterase inhibitor</strong> and as a side effect of ACE inhibitors.</p>\n<p>ACE has structural similarity to another enzyme called <strong>kininase</strong>. Kininase normally <strong>metabolizes bradykinin</strong>, substance P, and prostaglandins. <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> when administered also <strong>block kininase</strong> enzyme which results in an increase in the concentration of these substrates. An <strong>increase </strong>in the level of <strong>bradykinin/ substance P </strong>levels results in <strong>persistent brassy cough </strong>and<strong> angioedema</strong> in the patients.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7446","text":"An 18-year-old boy is brou​ght to the casualty with ​complaints of swelling of‍ his face and lips as sho‌wn in the image below. On‍ further enquiry, it is n‍oted that he has suffered‌ from similar episodes in​ the past whenever he bec‍omes emotional. He is pre‍scribed icatibant. What i​s the likely mechanism of​ action of this drug?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy is brought to the casualty with complaints of swelling of his face and lips as shown in the image below. On further enquiry, it is noted that he has suffered from similar episodes in the past whenever he becomes emotional. He is prescribed icatibant. What is the likely mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bradykinin B2 receptor ago​nist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bradykinin B2 receptor ant​agonist","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bradykinin B1 receptor ago​nist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bradykinin B1 receptor ant​agonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bradykinin B2 receptor antagonist","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ec1504cc1c8408485f6e571e4ee5172.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bradykinin B2 receptor antagonist</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing swelling of the face and lips is suggestive of<strong> hereditary angioedema</strong>.<strong> Icatibant </strong>is a<strong> selective bradykinin B<sub>2</sub> receptor </strong>competitive <strong>antagonist</strong> indicated for the treatment of acute attacks of hereditary angioedema.</p>\n<p>The other drug which is approved for the treatment of acute attacks of hereditary angioedema is <strong>ecallantide </strong>which is a <strong>synthetic plasma kallikrein inhibitor</strong>. Both these drugs are <strong>administered subcutaneously</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4699","text":"A 40-year-old diabetic mal​e is on ACE inhibitors fo​r hypertension. Which amo‍ng the following is the m‌ost serious adverse effec‍t you would expect to see‍ in this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old diabetic male is on ACE inhibitors for hypertension. Which among the following is the most serious adverse effect you would expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca​lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypotension</strong></p><p>The most significant <strong>adverse effect</strong> seen with the use of ACE inhibitors is <strong>hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p>ACE inhibitors are used in the management of hypertension and are one of the first-line drugs for this condition.<strong> </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 494\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option B: ACE inhibitors may cause <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> in patients with renal insufficiency or diabetes.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Options C and D: ACE inhibitors <strong>do not affect calcium levels</strong> significantly.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4710","text":"A 40-year-old man is diagn​osed with hypertension an​d is prescribed losartan.‍ Which of the following s‌tatements about the drug ‍is incorrect?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man is diagnosed with hypertension and is prescribed losartan. Which of the following statements about the drug is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a competitive angiot​ensin receptor antagonist​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It attenuates platelet agg​regation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with negligible​ cough","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Causes hyp​eruricemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Causes hyperuricemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Causes hyperuricemia</strong></p><p><strong>Losartan </strong>use is not associated with hyperuricemia. In fact, losartan is <strong>uricosuric</strong> and can also be <strong>used in</strong> patients with <strong>gout</strong>.</p>\n<p>Losartan is a competitive <strong>angiotensin II receptor antagonist</strong>. It binds to the AT<sub>1</sub> receptor with high affinity. it is also a<strong> competitive antagonist</strong> of the <strong>thromboxane A2 receptor</strong> and attenuates platelet aggregation. Losartan has no effect on ACE and it does <strong>not inhibit bradykinin breakdown</strong>. This results in negligible incidences of cough. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2152","text":"Combined use of ACE inhibi​tors and ARBs are not rou​tinely indicated primaril‍y due to the increased ri‌sk of which of the follow‍ing?","raw_text":"Combined use of ACE inhibitors and ARBs are not routinely indicated primarily due to the increased risk of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angio​edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heart ​failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p>The <strong>combined</strong> use of <strong>ACE inhibitors and ARBs</strong> are not commonly indicated as they increase the risk of<strong> hyperkalemia. </strong></p>\n<p>They also increase the risk of <strong>hypotension</strong> and <strong>renal injury</strong>. Any such use should be done under the supervision of specialists with frequent monitoring.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"eQiw7rup","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"16_basal_ganglia_and_cerebellum","title":"16.Basal Ganglia And Cerebellum","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1054","text":"Which of the following is ‌a nucleus of basal gangli‍a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a nucleus of basal ganglia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Den‌tate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thal‌amus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cau‌date","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Red n‌ucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caudate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caudate</strong></p><p>The <strong>basal ganglia </strong>has five nuclei on each side of the brain.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Caudate nucleus</li>\n<li>Putamen</li>\n<li>Globus pallidus: Divided into globus pallidus external (GPe) and globus pallidus internal (GPi)</li>\n<li>Substantia nigra: Divided into pars compacta and pars reticulata</li>\n<li>Subthalamic nucleus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>cau­date nucleus </strong>and<strong> putamen</strong> together form the <strong>striatum.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>putamen </strong>and<strong> globus pallidus</strong> collectively form the <strong>lentiform nucleus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1055","text":"Which part of the basal ga‌nglia receives the affere‍nt input?","raw_text":"Which part of the basal ganglia receives the afferent input?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stri‌atum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Globus ‌pallidus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Striatu‌m nigra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subthalam‌ic nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Striatum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52ad5a9224da46d4a16879726969d5afx600x728.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Striatum</strong></p><p>The <strong>s</strong><strong>triatum </strong>receives the afferent input. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 250\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>There are two main <strong>inputs</strong> to the basal ganglia: From the <strong>cerebral cortex</strong> (corticostriate pathway) and from the <strong>thalamus</strong> (thalamostriate pathway). They are both <strong>excitatory</strong> (glutamate) and they both terminate in the <strong>striatum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The two major <strong>outputs</strong> of the basal ganglia are from <strong>GPi</strong> and <strong>substantia nigra</strong>. Both are <strong>inhibitory</strong> (GABAergic) and both project to the <strong>thalamus</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 251\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>From the <strong>thalamus</strong>, there is an <strong>excitatory</strong> (glutamatergic) projection to the prefrontal and premotor <strong>cortex</strong>. This completes the <strong>cortical-basal ganglia-thalamic-cortical loop</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0076","text":"A patient is found to have‌ a hemorrhage in the basa‍l ganglia region. Which o​f the following is least ‍likely to be affected in ​this patient?","raw_text":"A patient is found to have a hemorrhage in the basal ganglia region. Which of the following is least likely to be affected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Planning o‌f movements","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cogn‌ition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maintenance of equilibrium‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Skilled ‌movements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maintenance of equilibrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maintenance of equilibrium</strong></p><p>This patient will have dysfunction of the basal ganglia. Maintenance of <strong>equilibrium</strong> is a function of the <strong>cerebellum</strong> and <strong>vestibular apparatus, </strong>hence will not be affected in this patient.</p>\n<p>Functions of basal ganglia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Planning of movements</li>\n<li>Regulation of muscle tone</li>\n<li>Control of reflex muscular activity &amp; posture</li>\n<li>Programming of movement - speed, direction, and intensity</li>\n<li>Role in the cognitive process (especially caudate nucleus)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1078","text":"Glutamate is synthesized m‌ainly in which part of ba‍sal ganglia?","raw_text":"Glutamate is synthesized mainly in which part of basal ganglia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stri‌atum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Globus ‌pallidus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Striatu‌m nigra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subthalam‌ic nuclei","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subthalamic nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47d498b3d0a5422ca71c211959ae129ex1280x999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subthalamic nuclei</strong></p><p><strong>Glutamate</strong> is synthesized mainly in the<strong> subthalamic nuclei</strong> of basal ganglia. </p>\n<p>Connections within the basal ganglia and the respective neurotransmitters:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 302px;\" width=\"329\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.265625px;\">Connections between</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.75px;\">Neurotransmitter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.265625px;\">Substantia nigra pars compacta to striatum (Nigrostraiatal projection)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.75px;\">Dopamine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.265625px;\">Striatum to substantia nigra pars reticulata</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.75px;\">GABA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.265625px;\">Striatum to GPe and GPi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.75px;\">GABA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.265625px;\">GPe to subthalamic nuclei</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.75px;\">GABA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.265625px;\">Subthalamic nuclei to GPe and GPi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.75px;\">Glutamate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1089","text":"A 56-year-old woman presen‌ts with abnormal movement‍s of the limbs. A lesion ​of the globus pallidus is‍ suspected. Which of the ​following did she most li‍kely present with?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman presents with abnormal movements of the limbs. A lesion of the globus pallidus is suspected. Which of the following did she most likely present with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‌rea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Athe‌tosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemiba‌llismus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parkin‌soinism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Athetosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0716efe5c2044b38b048718e1b3d387ex1280x999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Athetosis</strong></p><p>This woman with a<strong> lesion </strong>at the<strong> globus pallidus </strong>most likely presented with <strong>athetosis</strong>: Slow, writhing movements of a hand/arm/the face. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 157px;\" width=\"305\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Site of lesion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Description </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Chorea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Striatum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 699\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Flicking movements in the hands, face, and other parts of the body</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Athetosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Globus pallidus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 699\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Spontaneous, continuous writhing movements of a hand, an arm, the neck, or the face</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Hemiballusmus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Subthalamic nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 699\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sudden flailing movements of an entire limb</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Parkinsonism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">Substantia nigra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.34375px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 699\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 699\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Severe rigidity, akinesia, and tremors</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1238","text":"Cells present in cerebella‌r cortex are all except","raw_text":"Cells present in cerebellar cortex are all except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purk‌inje","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bip‌olar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gra‌nule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Go‌lgi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bipolar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bipolar</strong></p><p>Bipolar cells are <strong>not</strong> present in cerebellum.</p>\n<p>Cerebellum has 3 layers, 4 deep nuclei and 5 types of neurons.</p>\n<p>The cerebellar cortex has <strong>three</strong> layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>External molecular layer</li>\n<li>Purkinje cell layer </li>\n<li>Internal granular layer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are<strong> four</strong> deep nuclei: D,E,F,G</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>entate</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>mboliform</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>astigial nuclei</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>lo­bose</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interpositus nucleus- </strong>Globose and the emboliform nuclei together.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>five</strong> types of neurons in the cortex:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Purkinje</li>\n<li>Granule</li>\n<li>Basket</li>\n<li>Stellate</li>\n<li>Golgi cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1239","text":"All are cerebellar nuclei ‌except______.","raw_text":"All are cerebellar nuclei except______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glo‌bose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embol‌iform","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Den‌tate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Red n‌ucleus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Red nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/609f086df99c413b9737a6028ac9f386x1280x1108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Red nucleus</strong></p><p></p>\n<p><strong>Interpositus nucleus=</strong> globose+emboliform nuclei </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Red nucleus is situated in the<strong> midbrain</strong>. It crosses the midline and projects to interneurons in the dorsolateral part of the spinal ventral horn to also influence motor neurons that control distal limb muscles. This <strong>rubrospinal tract excites flexor</strong> motor neurons and <strong>inhibits extensor</strong> motor neurons. This pathway is not prominent in humans, but it may play a role in the posture typical of<strong> decorticate rigidity.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1240","text":"Efferent from cerebellar c‌ortex is through which ce‍ll:","raw_text":"Efferent from cerebellar cortex is through which cell:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purk‌inje","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bip‌olar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gra‌nule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Go‌lgi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Purkinje","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Purkinje</strong></p><p><strong>Purkinje cells</strong> are the <strong>only output neurons </strong>(efferent neurons) from the cerebellar cortex.</p>\n<p>The Purkinje cells are the largest neurons in the cerebellum. Their axons, which are the only output from the cerebellar cortex, <strong>project</strong> to the <strong>deep</strong> cerebellar <strong>nuclei</strong>, especially the dentate nucleus, where they form <strong>inhibitory synapses</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1242","text":"Flocculonodular lobe has d‌irect connections with __‍________","raw_text":"Flocculonodular lobe has direct connections with __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red n‌ucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior oli‌vary nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibula‌r nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dentate‌ nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vestibular nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vestibular nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>flocculonodular lobe</strong> has connections with the <strong>vestibular apparatus.</strong></p>\n<p>The flocculonodular lobe forms the vestibulocerebellum, which projects to the vestibular nuclei and is concerned with <strong>equilibrium and eye movements.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1241","text":"Which of the following are‌ climbing fibers?","raw_text":"Which of the following are climbing fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olivocerebe‌llar fibers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinoce‌rebellar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pontoce‌rebellar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibulo‌cerebellar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Olivocerebellar fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Olivocerebellar fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Climbing </strong>fibers are<strong> olivocerebellar </strong>fibers. They <strong>originate </strong>from the<strong> inferior olivary nucleus. </strong></p>\n<p>Climbing fibers and mossy fibers are the two main <strong>excitatory inputs</strong> to the cerebellum.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 130px;\" width=\"566\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 275px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Climbing fibres</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 275px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mossy fibres</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 275px;\">They come from one single source via the inferior olivary nuclei.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275px;\">They come from many sources and the fibers synapse with granule cells to form glomeruli. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 275px;\">\n<p>End on single Purkinje cells.</p>\n<p>Convey proprioceptive inputs from all over the body.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275px;\">Axons of granule cells bifurcate and form parallel fibers which excite multiple Purkinje cells.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: <strong>Mossy</strong> fibres provide direct input via the <strong>pontine</strong> nuclei.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9930","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spino cerebellum constitut‌es vermis only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spino cerebellum constitut‌es vermis and lateral por‍tion of cerebellar hemisp​here","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibulo cerebellum const‌itutes nodulus and floccu‍lus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibulo cerebellum const‌itutes lateral portion of‍ cerebellar hemispheres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vestibulo cerebellum constitutes nodulus and flocculus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vestibulo cerebellum constitutes nodulus and flocculus</strong></p><p><strong>Vestibulocerebellum </strong>(archicerebellum) constitutes nodulus and flocculus and is hence called floculonodular lobe.</p>\n<p><strong>Spinocerebellum </strong>(paleocerebellum) constitutes vermis and adjacent medial portions of cerebellar hemispheres.</p>\n<p><strong>Cerebrocerebellum </strong>(neocerebellum) constitutes the lateral portions of hemispheres.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9931","text":"Which of the following is ‌a function of the neocere‍bellum?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a function of the neocerebellum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintenance of equilibrium‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Correction of voluntary mo‌vements","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Planning and programming o‌f voluntary movements","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maintenance of muscle tone‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Planning and programming of voluntary movements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Planning and programming of voluntary movements</strong></p><p>The function of the <strong>neocerebellum</strong> is <strong>planning </strong>and<strong> programming </strong>of voluntary<strong> movements. </strong></p>\n<p>The cerebellum can be <strong>divided</strong> into: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 693\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>V</strong><strong>estibulocerebellum/Archicerebellum</strong>: Consists princi­pally of the <strong>flocculonodular lobe</strong> and adjacent portions of the vermis. It participates in<strong> balance, vestibular </strong>reflexes, and<strong> eye movements.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>S</strong><strong>pinocerebellum/Paleocerebellum</strong>: Consists of most of the <strong>vermis</strong> plus the adjacent <strong>intermediate zones</strong> on both sides of the vermis. It <strong>coordi­nates movements</strong> of the <strong>limbs</strong>. Vermis: Proximal muscles, Intermediate zones: Distal muscles</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 694\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>C</strong><strong>erebrocerebellum/neocerebellum:</strong> Consists of the large lateral zones of the<strong> cerebellar hemispheres</strong>. It receives all its input from the cerebral cortex (motor, premotor and somatosensory). It transmits its output back to the brain, functioning in a feedback manner to <strong>plan sequential</strong> voluntary body and limb movements. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1245","text":"A patient with a cerebella‌r lesion will show all of‍ the following features e​xcept:","raw_text":"A patient with a cerebellar lesion will show all of the following features except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nyst‌agmus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resting‌ tremor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ata‌xia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Past p‌ointing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Resting tremor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Resting tremor</strong></p><p>A patient with a suspected<strong> cerebellar lesion </strong>will<strong> not </strong>have<strong> resting tremors. </strong>Resting tremors are characteristic features of <strong>basal ganglia </strong>lesions.  (Parkinson's disease).</p>\n<p><strong>Intentional tremors</strong> are seen in <strong>cerebellar diseases</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Features of cerebellar disease</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ataxia – Incoordinated, intermittent jerky movements with drunken gait.</li>\n<li>Hypotonia or atonia – Reduced or absence of muscle tone.</li>\n<li>Dysmetria – Inability to judge the force and range of movement resulting in past pointing.</li>\n<li>Dysdiadokokinesia – Failure to perform rapid alternating movements.</li>\n<li>Decomposition of movement – Lack of smooth flow of movement.</li>\n<li>Speech defect – Staccato speech or slurring speech.</li>\n<li>Pendular knee jerk (a feature of hypotonia).</li>\n<li>Intention tremors.</li>\n<li>Nystagmus (jerky movements of the eye).</li>\n<li>Rebound phenomenon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9937","text":"The vestibulo-ocular refle‌x is being performed in a‍ patient with vertigo. Wh​ich of the following is a‍ssociated with this refle​x?","raw_text":"The vestibulo-ocular reflex is being performed in a patient with vertigo. Which of the following is associated with this reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinoce‌rebellum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flocculono‌dular lobe","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neocer‌ebellum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipit‌al lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flocculonodular lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flocculonodular lobe</strong></p><p>The<strong> vestibulo-ocular reflex</strong> is controlled by the vestibulo-cerebellum, which constitutes the <strong>flocculonodular lobe.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9929","text":"Purkinje fibers are inhibi‌tory for _________.","raw_text":"Purkinje fibers are inhibitory for _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep cerebe‌llar nuclei","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Climbin‌g fibers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granul‌e cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spino cereb‌ellar tracts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deep cerebellar nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deep cerebellar nuclei</strong></p><p>The input from the <strong>Purkinje cells to</strong> the <strong>deep nuclei</strong> is <strong>inhibitory</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, the <strong>deep nuclei output </strong>is always<strong> excitatory.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7082","text":"Which of the following fib‌res reach the Purkinje ce‍ll directly?","raw_text":"Which of the following fibres reach the Purkinje cell directly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior oli‌vary nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Raphe ‌nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibula‌r nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Locus c‌oeruleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inferior olivary nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inferior olivary nucleus</strong></p><p>The fibres of <strong>inferior olivary</strong> nucleus (climbing fibres) reach the purkinje cell <strong>directly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p>The input from the <strong>Purkinje cells to</strong> the <strong>deep nuclei</strong> is <strong>inhibitory</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, the <strong>deep nuclei output </strong>is always<strong> excitatory.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0706","text":"Which of the following is ‌true?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molecular layer contain st‌ellate and basket cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Molecular is the innermost‌ layer of cortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Purkinje layer contain Gol‌gi cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axons of purkinje cell sec‌rete Glutamate on deep ce‍rebellar nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Molecular layer contain stellate and basket cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Molecular layer contain stellate and basket cells</strong></p><p>The cerebellar cortex has three layers:</p>\n<p><strong>External molecular layer</strong>: Contains Basket and Stellate Cells.  </p>\n<p><strong>Purkinje cell layer</strong>: Only one cell thick, containing the purkinje cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Internal granular layer</strong>: Contains <strong>G</strong>ranule and <strong>G</strong>olgi Cells</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0703","text":"Which of the following asc‌ending pathways enters th‍e cerebellum through the ​peduncle highlighted in g‍reen in the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following ascending pathways enters the cerebellum through the peduncle highlighted in green in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal spinocerebellar tra‌ct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cuneo cereb‌ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior spinocerebellar t‌ract","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ponto cereb‌ellar fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior spinocerebellar tract","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/30b9dddde30242d1bbee1c61adbe94a1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior spinocerebellar tract</strong></p><p>The peduncle highlighted in the image is the <strong>superior cerebellar peduncle</strong>. The <strong>anterior spinocerebellar tract</strong> enters the cerebellum through it and terminates in both sides of the cerebellum. </p>\n<p><strong>Dorsal spinocerebellar tract</strong> enters the cerebellum through the <strong>inferior cerebellar peduncle</strong> and terminates on the same side as its origin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0698","text":"Concerning the circuitry b‌etween mossy fibers, clim‍bing fibers and cells of ​the cerebellar cortex, wh‍ich of the following pair​s provide excitatory inpu‍t?","raw_text":"Concerning the circuitry between mossy fibers, climbing fibers and cells of the cerebellar cortex, which of the following pairs provide excitatory input?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mossy fibers and Purkinje ‌cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi cells and climbing f‌ibers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granule cell parallel fibe‌rs and mossy fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Climbing fibers and basket‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Granule cell parallel fibers and mossy fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Granule cell parallel fibers and mossy fibers</strong></p><p>The <strong>excitatory</strong> fibers in the cerebellum are <strong>climbing</strong> fibers, <strong>mossy</strong> fibers, and <strong>granule</strong> cell parallel fibers. </p>\n<p>The fundamental circuits of the cerebellar cortex :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Climbing fibers</strong> exert a strong <strong>excitatory</strong> effect on single <strong>Purkinje</strong> cells. <strong>Mossy</strong> fibers exert an <strong>excitatory</strong> effect on <strong>granule</strong> cells.</li>\n<li>Each granule cell sends an axon to the molecular layer, where the axon bifurcates to form a T. The branches of the T are straight and run long distances; thus, they are called <strong>parallel fibers</strong>. The parallel fibers form <strong>excitatory</strong> synapses on the dendrites of many <strong>Purkinje</strong> cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The other neurons (basket cells, stellate cells, and golgi cells) are <strong>inhibitory</strong> interneurons. <strong>Basket</strong> cells and <strong>stellate</strong> cells receive excitatory input from the parallel fibers and each <strong>inhibits</strong> many <strong>Purkinje</strong> cells. <strong>Golgi</strong> cells receive excitatory input from the parallel fibers and mossy fibers. Their axons project to the dendrites of the <strong>granule</strong> cells where they form an <strong>inhibitory</strong> synapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The output of the <strong>Purkinje cells</strong> is ultimately <strong>inhibitory</strong> to the deep cerebellar nuclei.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6873","text":"Inhibition of Purkinje cel‌ls by stellate cells is a‍n example of","raw_text":"Inhibition of Purkinje cells by stellate cells is an example of","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presynaptic‌ inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Feedback ‌inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Feedforward‌ inhibition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stellate cells doesn’t inh‌ibit purkinje cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Feedforward inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Feedforward inhibition</strong></p><p>Inhibition of Purkinje cells by stellate cells is an example of feedforward inhibition.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 256\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Mossy fibers</strong> exert a weak <strong>excitatory</strong> effect on many <strong>Purkinje</strong> cells via the <strong>granule</strong> cells. The granule cells also <strong>excite</strong> the <strong>basket</strong> and <strong>stellate</strong> cells via their parallel fibers. The basket and stellate cells, in turn, <strong>inhibit</strong> the <strong>Purkinje</strong> cells (Feed forward inhibition).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Renshaw</strong> cells exhibit <strong>feedback</strong> inhibition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OYTverPx","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"16_obsessive_compulsive_and_related_disorders","title":"16.Obsessive Compulsive And Related Disorders","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA7143","text":"A 32-year-old bank teller ​is constantly fearful of ‌coming into contact with ‌germs during monetary tra​nsactions. She is convinc‍ed that the bacteria on h‍er hands may cause a fata​l illness, although she i​s aware this is an irrati​onal fear. Which of the f​ollowing best describes t​his behavior?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old bank teller is constantly fearful of coming into contact with germs during monetary transactions. She is convinced that the bacteria on her hands may cause a fatal illness, although she is aware this is an irrational fear. Which of the following best describes this behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halluc​ination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obse​ssion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Compu​lsion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delu​sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Obsession","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Obsession</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>obsession</strong>, an idea that continuously intrudes the patient's consciousness. The characteristics of obsession are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comprise of one’s own thoughts.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego-alien </strong>(ego-dystonic).</li>\n<li>Patients try to resist it but it leads to further anxiety and distress.</li>\n<li>The thought is very well <strong>known</strong> by the patient<strong> to be irrational and absurd.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Obsession eventually leads to <strong>compulsion, </strong>which is a repetitive and purposeful behaviour or activities carried out to reduce intense distress.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Ego-alien/Ego-dystonic</strong>: unwanted and <strong>unacceptable</strong> to one's own personality, causing distress.</p>\n<p><strong>Ego-syntonic</strong>: <strong>acceptable</strong> to one's own personality. Personality traits are usually ego-syntonic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5210","text":"Among the following, what ​is the most common sympto‌m of adult obsessive-comp‌ulsive disorder?","raw_text":"Among the following, what is the most common symptom of adult obsessive-compulsive disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aggressiv​e symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Need for​ symmetry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pathologi​cal doubt","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sexual ​symptoms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pathological doubt","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pathological doubt</strong></p><p>Among the above options, pathological doubt is the common symptom of adult OCD. <strong>Pathological doubt</strong> is the <strong>second most common</strong> pattern of adult obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), only <strong>after obsession of contamination</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pathological doubt is an obsession with doubt, followed by a compulsion to check. The obses­sion often implies some danger of violence e.g., forgetting to turn off the stove or not locking a door. The checking may involve multiple trips back into the house to check the stove. These patients have obsessional self-doubt and always feel guilty about having forgotten or committed something.</p>\n<p><strong>The frequency of presenting patterns of OCD :</strong></p>\n<p>Obsession of contamination &gt; Pathological doubt &gt; Intrusive thoughts&gt; Symmetry</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6702","text":"A 40-year-old male patient​ comes to the psychiatry ‌OPD with complaints of re‌petitive thoughts that, h​e always feels his hands ‍are dirty, though they ar‍e not. This gives him dis​comfort and hence he has ​to wash them again and ag​ain. This is a disorder o​f thought _________.","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient comes to the psychiatry OPD with complaints of repetitive thoughts that, he always feels his hands are dirty, though they are not. This gives him discomfort and hence he has to wash them again and again. This is a disorder of thought _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fl​ow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fo​rm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posse​ssion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Con​tent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Possession","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe1829a321c7433eb1fe28852719702dx1280x1873.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Possession</strong></p><p>The clinical history is suggestive of obsessive-compulsive disorder, which is a disorder of <strong>thought possession</strong>.</p>\n<p>The diseases under thought possession include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Obsession and Compulsion</li>\n<li>Thought Alienation</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 96\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In obsessions and compulsions, although the sense of thought possession in the patient is not impaired, he/she experiences the <strong>obsessive thoughts</strong> to <strong>appear against their will</strong>. Hence, despite the patient perceiving a loss of control over the <strong>voluntary act</strong> (<strong>compulsion</strong>), they still retain personal possession (ownership) of the act.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder </strong>is defined as the presence of obsessions and/or compulsions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Obsessions</strong> are recurrent and persistent <strong>ideas,</strong> thoughts, urges, or images that are experienced as intrusive and unwanted and cause anxiety or distress to the patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Compulsions</strong> are the repetitive and intentional behaviors or <strong>acts</strong> performed in response to obsessions or according to certain rules that must be applied rigidly (e.g., repetitive hand washing, ritualistic checking).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the given case, the obsessional thoughts are the repetitive, unwanted thoughts that his hands are dirty, while the compulsion is to wash his hands to neutralize the obsession. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3546","text":"In OCD, which part of the ​brain fails to act as the‌ gatekeeper to allow the ‌motor system to perform g​oal-directed activities?","raw_text":"In OCD, which part of the brain fails to act as the gatekeeper to allow the motor system to perform goal-directed activities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caudate​ nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Globus ​Pallidus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Substant​ia Nigra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subthalam​ic nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Caudate nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Caudate nucleus</strong></p><p>The<strong> caudate nucleus</strong> normally<strong> </strong>acts as a gatekeeper thereby allowing the motor system to perform only those acts that are goal-directed.</p>\n<p>When it<strong> fails</strong> to perform its gatekeeper function, <strong>irrelevant acts</strong> are performed, as in <strong>obsessive-compulsive disorder</strong> or tic disorders, such as Tourette's syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4281","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding obsessive-‌compulsive disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding obsessive-compulsive disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased​ dopamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased a​cetylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased​ serotonin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased a​cetylcholine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased serotonin</strong></p><p>In OCD, there is a <strong>decrease</strong> in the level of <strong>serotonin</strong> and an <strong>increase</strong> in the level of <strong>dopamine</strong>. Increased dopamine leads to continued reward-seeking which again leads to compulsive behaviour and obsessive features.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0971","text":"Which of the following is ​not an OCD related disord‌er?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an OCD related disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hair pulli​ng disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Temper ​tantrums","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hoarding​ disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin p​icking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Temper tantrums","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Temper tantrums</strong></p><p><strong>Temper tantrums</strong> are not OCD-related.</p>\n<p>Obsessive-compulsive or related disorders (as per ICD 11):</p>\n<p>It refers to a group of disorders that are characterized by repetitive thoughts and behaviors that share similar etiologies and important diagnostic validators. Important features of these disorders are <strong>obsessions, intrusive thoughts, and preoccupations</strong>. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder</li>\n<li><strong>Body dysmorphic disorder</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypochondriasis</strong></li>\n<li>Olfactory reference disorder</li>\n<li>Hoarding disorder</li>\n<li>Body focused repetitive behavior disorders (Excoriation, Trichotillomania)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7149","text":"A woman presented with a c​onstant fear of sinning. ‌She was unable to look at‌ people for fear that she​ was offending them by in‍appropriately glancing. W‍hen people crossed their ​legs in front of her, she​ thought it was because s​he had been looking at th​eir genitals. She constan​tly prayed for forgivenes‌s for not being devout or‌ moral enough. What is th​e treatment of choice for‌ this disorder?","raw_text":"A woman presented with a constant fear of sinning. She was unable to look at people for fear that she was offending them by inappropriately glancing. When people crossed their legs in front of her, she thought it was because she had been looking at their genitals. She constantly prayed for forgiveness for not being devout or moral enough. What is the treatment of choice for this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psychoanalytic psychothera​py","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cognitive behavioral thera​py","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Selective Serotonin Reupta​ke Inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electroconvu​lsive therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cognitive behavioral therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cognitive behavioral therapy</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a woman who suffers from scrupulosity, a type of <strong>obsessive-compulsive disorder</strong> (OCD). Scrupulous individuals are overly concerned that something they thought or did might be a sin or a violation of religious or moral doctrine. The <strong>treatment of choice</strong> for OCD is <strong>c</strong><strong>ognitive behavioral therapy</strong>, especially focussing on exposure and response prevention. </p>\n<p>Other modalities of treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drug treatment – <strong>SSRIs</strong> are the drugs of choice because the serotonergic system is involved in the pathophysiology of OCD.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychoanalytic psychotherapy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Electroconvulsive therapy</strong> – in chronic and resistant cases.</li>\n<li>Surgery – stereotactic limbic leucotomy, stereotactic subcaudate tractotomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0787","text":"A distressed 22-year-old m​an comes to the hospital ‌with complaints of freque‌nt checking the doors eve​n when they are locked. H‍e is subsequently diagnos‍ed to have obsessive-comp​ulsive disorder. Which of​ the following statements​ are true? Undoing, react​ion formation, and isolat​ion are the defence mecha‌nisms involved Clomiprami‌ne is the drug of choice ​Risperidone may be used i‌n SSRI resistant cases to‌ augment the response Sys‌tematic desensitisation i‌s the psychotherapy of ch‌oice Temporal lobectomy i‌s a common psychosurgical​ option","raw_text":"<p>A distressed 22-year-old man comes to the hospital with complaints of frequent checking the doors even when they are locked. He is subsequently diagnosed to have obsessive-compulsive disorder. Which of the following statements are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Undoing, reaction formation, and isolation are the defence mechanisms involved</li>\n<li>Clomipramine is the drug of choice</li>\n<li>Risperidone may be used in SSRI resistant cases to augment the response</li>\n<li>Systematic desensitisation is the psychotherapy of choice</li>\n<li>Temporal lobectomy is a common psychosurgical option</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4 ​and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Undoing, reaction formation, and isolation</strong> are the <strong>defence mechanisms</strong> involved in OCD. Antipsychotics such as <strong>risperidone and aripiprazole</strong> can be used in SSRI-resistant cases to augment the response.</p>\n<p>Statement 2: <strong>SSRI</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in OCD. Clomipramine is the first FDA-approved agent for the treatment of OCD. In case of failure of 2 SSRIs, clomipramine is indicated.</p>\n<p>Statement 4: <strong>Exposure and response prevention</strong> is the psychotherapy of choice for OCD</p>\n<p>Statement 5: <strong>Temporal lobectomy</strong> is a common treatment option for <strong>temporal lobe epilepsy </strong>resistant to anticonvulsants (and not in OCD). </p>\n<p><strong>Non-pharmacological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cognitive Behavioural Therapy\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exposure and response prevention</strong></li>\n<li>Desensitization</li>\n<li>Thought stopping</li>\n<li>Flooding</li>\n<li>Implosion therapy</li>\n<li>Aversive conditioning</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Psychotherapy</li>\n<li>Electroconvulsive therapy</li>\n<li>Psychosurgery\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ablative</strong>: Cingulotomy, Subcaudate tractotomy</li>\n<li><strong>Non-Ablative</strong>: Deep brain stimulation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0806","text":"A 40-year-old man was brou​ght by his wife to the ps‌ychiatric OPD. She compla‌ints that he does not all​ow her to discard old new‍spapers and informs that ‍their home is completely ​disorganized because of t​his accumulation. She als​o says that he fights wit​h her if she tries to dis​card them. On asking the ‌husband, he replies that ‌he has an attachment towa​rds them. He gets anxious‌ by the thought of discar‌ding the newspapers as he‌ may lose some precious i‌nformation. What is the p‌robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man was brought by his wife to the psychiatric OPD. She complaints that he does not allow her to discard old newspapers and informs that their home is completely disorganized because of this accumulation. She also says that he fights with her if she tries to discard them. On asking the husband, he replies that he has an attachment towards them. He gets anxious by the thought of discarding the newspapers as he may lose some precious information. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal c​ollecting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hoarding​ disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obsessive-compulsive disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delusiona​l disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hoarding disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hoarding disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hoarding disorder</strong> in the patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Hoarding disorder </strong>is characterized by the persistent and profound difficulty in discarding or parting from one’s possessions. This, in turn, leads to <strong>significant congestion</strong> <strong>and clutter,</strong> and to substantial <strong>distress</strong> or impairment (this differentiates hoarding disorder from normal collecting). </p>\n<table style=\"width: 363px; height: 223px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.046875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.96875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hoarding disorder</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.046875px;\">Here, hoarding is done because of an obsessive thought.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.96875px;\">No obsession present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.046875px;\">Here, hoarding is done to <strong>relieve</strong> the <strong>anxiety</strong> caused by the <strong>obsession.</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.96875px;\">Here, hoarding is done because the individual <strong>wishes to save</strong> things and feels anxious to discard the things.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0823","text":"Domestic squalor is associ​ated with which of the fo‌llowing conditions?","raw_text":"Domestic squalor is associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hoarding​ disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Body dysmorp​hic disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichoti​llomania","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olfactory reference syndro​me","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hoarding disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hoarding disorder</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes domestic squalor which is associated with <strong>hoarding disorder</strong>. In some individuals, living conditions become <strong>extremely unsanitary</strong> referred to as <strong>domestic squalor.</strong></p>\n<p> Hoarding may be associated with a broad range of sequelae:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Health and safety problems (e.g., patients may not have sufficient space to cook or keep clean) </li>\n<li><strong>Clutter avalanches </strong>may lead to falls and even mortality.</li>\n<li>Domestic squalor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0837","text":"A 78-year-old widowed man ​was diagnosed with carcin‌oma of the paranasal sinu‌s 6 weeks ago. During a c​heckup, his son shared hi‍s concerns about his fath‍er’s accumulation of garb​age, spoilt food, and exc​reta at home since the di​agnosis. He denies any he​lp from his son to clean ​up the place. He also has‌ poor self-care and looks‌ filthy, but is not asham​ed of this. Which of the ‌following describes his c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 78-year-old widowed man was diagnosed with carcinoma of the paranasal sinus 6 weeks ago. During a checkup, his son shared his concerns about his father’s accumulation of garbage, spoilt food, and excreta at home since the diagnosis. He denies any help from his son to clean up the place. He also has poor self-care and looks filthy, but is not ashamed of this. Which of the following describes his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Major de​pression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hoarding​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diogenes​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obsessive compulsive disor​der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diogenes syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diogenes syndrome</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The onset of extreme <strong>self-neglect</strong> and <strong>hoarding</strong> in <strong>elderly</strong> individuals after the news of a life-threatening illness is referred to as <strong>Diogenes syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The main characteristics of Diogenes syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Domestic squalor</li>\n<li>Self-neglect</li>\n<li>Lack of shame regarding domestic hoarding</li>\n<li>Refusal of help from others</li>\n<li>Hoarding of <strong>rotting food</strong>, <strong>excrement</strong>, or <strong>rubbish</strong> which causes feelings of disgust among family and visitors.</li>\n<li>There is poor insight; the individual may not report distress.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7587","text":"Which of the following has​ a poor prognosis with ex‌posure and response preve‌ntion in OCD?","raw_text":"Which of the following has a poor prognosis with exposure and response prevention in OCD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathologi​cal doubt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magical ​thinking","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hoar​ding","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Contaminati​on obsession","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hoarding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hoarding</strong></p><p><strong>Hoarding </strong>behavior shows <strong>poor prognosis </strong>with exposure and response prevention.</p>\n<p>Challenges posed by hoarding patients to typical cognitive behavior therapy (CBT) include poor insight into the behavior, low motivation, and resistance to treatment.</p>\n<p>The most effective treatment for hoarding is a cognitive-behavioral model that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Training in decision making and categorizing</li>\n<li>Exposure and habituation to discarding</li>\n<li>Cognitive restructuring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0851","text":"A 20-year-old lady present​s with patchy hair loss, ‌characterized by short br‌oken strands as shown bel​ow. No abnormalities of t‍he skin or scalp are pres‍ent. Biopsy shows perifol​licular haemorrhage. What​ is the probable diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old lady presents with patchy hair loss, characterized by short broken strands as shown below. No abnormalities of the skin or scalp are present. Biopsy shows perifollicular haemorrhage. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alopeci​a areata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinea ​capitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichoti​llomania","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anagen e​ffluvium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trichotillomania","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9f9c4f6db0b940e5b00fa13f484dd038.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trichotillomania</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario of <strong>patchy hair loss</strong> characterized by <strong>short broken strands</strong>, as shown in the image, and the biopsy showing <strong>perifollicular</strong> <strong>hemorrhage</strong> point to a probable diagnosis of <strong>t</strong><strong>richotillomania</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Trichotillomania</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classified under <strong>obsessive-compulsive </strong>and related disorder</li>\n<li>Irresistible urge to pluck the hair</li>\n<li>Seen <strong>more commonly</strong> in<strong> females</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Varying lengths of hair</strong> inside the area of hair loss</li>\n<li>Hair is <strong>never completely lost</strong> in the patch</li>\n<li><strong>Orentreich/Friar Tuck/Tonsure sign:</strong> Loss of central area (easier to pull) and sparing of margins of the scalp.</li>\n<li>Dermoscopy shows <strong>decreased hair density, short vellus hair, broken hair with different shaft lengths</strong>, coiled hairs, short vellus hair, trichoptilosis, and sparse yellow dots, which may contain black dots and <strong>no exclamation mark hair.</strong></li>\n<li>HPE:-\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No inflammation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Perifollicular haemorrhage</strong> - due to plucking</li>\n<li>intraepithelial and perifollicular haemorrhages and intrafollicular pigment casts</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0125","text":"A woman presents with seve​re abdominal pain and vom‌iting which was provision‌ally diagnosed as intesti​nal obstruction. An emerg‍ency laparotomy was done ‍and the following specime​n was removed. What is th​e probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A  woman presents with severe abdominal pain and vomiting which was provisionally diagnosed as intestinal obstruction. An emergency laparotomy was done and the following specimen was removed. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alopeci​a areata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinea ​capitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricho​phagia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anagen e​ffluvium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trichophagia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6c68d8093384b4b95fcd0b07b853eec.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trichophagia</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario, along with the image of a trichobezoar, suggests a diagnosis of <strong>trichophagia.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Trichophagia </strong>is the compulsive eating of hair associated with <strong>trichotillomania. </strong>In trichophagia, people also ingest the hair that they pull. </p>\n<p>Complications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>trichobezoars</li>\n<li>malnutrition</li>\n<li>intestinal obstruction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0856","text":"A 24-year-old man presents​ with skin lesions as sho‌wn below. He informs you ‌that he has an urge to pi​ck his skin often and get‍s gratification after pic‍king. He feels guilty abo​ut this behavior but is n​ot able to stop. Which of​ the following best expla​ins his condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man presents with skin lesions as shown below. He informs you that he has an urge to pick his skin often and gets gratification after picking. He feels guilty about this behavior but is not able to stop.  Which of the following best explains his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excoriatio​n disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Magnan ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis​ artefacta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Van Gogh​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Excoriation disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8af970bc914465cb67914947d4a986a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Excoriation disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Excoriation or skin-picking disorder</strong> is characterized by the compulsive and repetitive picking of the skin.</p>\n<p>The<strong> face </strong>is the most common site of skin-picking and it is also called as <strong>dermatillomania. </strong>Excoriation disorder can be seen associated with methamphetamine or cocaine abuse.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Magnan syndrome </strong>involves tactile hallucination due to <strong>cocaine use</strong>, which is not seen in this patient.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Dermatitis artefacta </strong>is factitious dermatitis in which skin-picking is used <strong>deliberately</strong> for producing <strong>self-inflicted injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Van Gogh syndrome </strong>is repetitive <strong>self-mutilation</strong> including bite, burn, ulceration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0804","text":"A 20-year-old female was w​orried about the shape of‌ her nose. She feels it i‌s not symmetrical and loo​ks ugly. She often compar‍es her nose with that of ‍others to look for the fl​aw. She also checks her n​ose often in the mirror. ​Due to this, she sometime​s avoids social gathering​s. Which of the following‌ best describes her condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old female was worried about the shape of her nose. She feels it is not symmetrical and looks ugly. She often compares her nose with that of others to look for the flaw. She also checks her nose often in the mirror. Due to this, she sometimes avoids social gatherings. Which of the following best describes her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olfactory reference syndro​me","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Generalized anxiety disord​er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body dysmorp​hic disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Somatofor​m disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Body dysmorphic disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Body dysmorphic disorder</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given history suggests the presence of <strong>body dysmorphic disorder (BDD)</strong> in the patient.</p>\n<p>BDD is characterized by persistent preoccupations about one or more <strong>perceived defects or flaws in one’s appearance,</strong> but the defects or flaws are negligible or not even observable to others. In response to their appearance concerns, individuals with BDD demonstrate a range of mental acts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comparing themselves with others</li>\n<li>Checking in the mirror</li>\n<li>Camouflaging their perceived flaws</li>\n<li>Avoidance- from minor social avoidance to being housebound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>specific focus </strong>of BDD <strong>on appearance </strong>serves to differentiate it from other conditions characterized by repetitive behaviours.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Olfactory reference syndrome </strong>is characterized by repetitive concerns about having a<strong> foul odour</strong>. This leads to a range of repetitive behaviours such as washing or changing one’s clothes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0864","text":"A 25-year-old man reports ​to the psychiatry departm‌ent with a fear that his ‌penis is shrinking and re​tracting and will ultimat‍ely disappear into the ab‍domen. What is the most p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man reports to the psychiatry department with a fear that his penis is shrinking and retracting and will ultimately disappear into the abdomen. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dhat s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ko​ro","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Shubo​-kyofu","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"La​tah","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Koro","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Koro</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Koro.</strong></p>\n<p>Koro is a <strong>culture-related disorder,</strong> usually occurring in epidemics in <strong>South-Eastern Asia. </strong>It consists of a fear that the penis is shrinking or retracting and will disappear into the abdomen. The fear is related to labia, nipples, or breasts in females. It is classified under \"Other Specified Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorder\" in DSM-5. Females are also affected infrequently, with a corresponding belief that their breasts (and/or vulva) are shrinking.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Dhat syndrome </strong>is a cultural condition found in the Indian subcontinent. Male patients complain of premature ejaculation or impotence and believe that they are passing semen in their urine<strong>. </strong>In their tradition, semen is described as a \"<strong>vital fluid</strong>\". The discharge of this \"vital fluid\" through sex or masturbation is associated with anxiety and dysphoria. It is characterized by the complaint of the passage of dhat (whitish discharge: <strong>semen)</strong> in urine, multiple somatic symptoms, physical/mental exhaustion, and sexual dysfunction. It is based on the belief that semen is <strong>precious</strong> and <strong>that loss</strong> of semen leads to <strong>exhaustion</strong> and <strong>weakness.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Shubo-kyofu </strong>is similar to body dysmorphic disorder and is characterized by excessive fear of having a bodily deformity.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Latah (startle reaction)</strong> is a syndrome seen in Southeast Asia and Japan. Occurring more often in women, it is typically characterized by the presence of <strong>automatic obedience, echolalia</strong> (mimicking another's speech), and <strong>echopraxia</strong> (mimicking another's movement). It is often precipitated by a sudden stimulus, such as a loud sound.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Another culture-bound syndrome:</p>\n<p><strong>Shenkui </strong>is<em> </em>native to China, and also exists globally, in which the individual suffers withdrawal-like symptoms including <strong>painful</strong> <strong>brain fog</strong>, chills, nausea, and even <strong>flu-like symptoms</strong> with anxiety, believed to be caused by a <strong>loss of semen</strong> and <strong>orgasm.</strong> The symptoms can last for weeks to months after a single orgasm.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5AC1HLkH","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"16_esophagus___congenital__motility___inflammatory_disorders","title":"16.Esophagus   Congenital, Motility & Inflammatory Disorders","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB6285","text":"A full-term infant with a ‌history of polyhydramnios​ in utero presents with r​espiratory distress soon ‌after birth. On examinati‌on, the child has frothin​g of saliva and the resid​ent suspects tracheoesoph‌ageal fistula. What is th​e most common type of fis​tula likely to be present‍ in this child?","raw_text":"A full-term infant with a history of polyhydramnios in utero presents with respiratory distress soon after birth. On examination, the child has frothing of saliva and the resident suspects tracheoesophageal fistula. What is the most common type of fistula likely to be present in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/677031e622ec4d5fa3e540d219efc9e6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7db8519c0e2344059bb617a057b08ba7x279x466.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p><strong>Type C</strong> is the commonest variety of tracheo-esophageal fistula (TEF). </p>\n<p>A tracheoesophageal fistula is an <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>communication</strong> between the esophagus and trachea. Type C lesion is associated with <strong>proximal esophageal atresia </strong>and<strong> distal TEF.</strong> The proximal blind pouch ends approximately at the distance of one or two vertebral bodies from the distal TEF.</p>\n<p>Tracheoesophageal fistula is associated with <strong>VACTERL</strong> (vertebral, anorectal, cardiac, tracheal, esophageal, renal, and limb) anomalies. </p>\n<p>Drooling of saliva, <strong>inability to pass a nasogastric tube</strong> and history of polyhydramnios suggest a diagnosis of <strong>oesophagal atresia </strong>with or without <strong>TEF. </strong>This can be confirmed with a chest X-ray image showing a coiled nasogastric tube in the oesophagal pouch as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2494","text":"A neonate was brought by h‌is mother with complaints​ of regurgitation of the ​feeds given to him. The n‌asogastric tube could not‌ be passed to the stomach​. Radiograph is shown bel​ow. The birth weight of t‌he neonate was 2.7kg. The​re is no current evidence​ of pneumonia. What is th‍e most suitable managemen​t in this case?","raw_text":"A neonate was brought by his mother with complaints of regurgitation of the feeds given to him. The nasogastric tube could not be passed to the stomach. Radiograph is shown below. The birth weight of the neonate was 2.7kg. There is no current evidence of pneumonia. What is the most suitable management in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrostomy under antibiot‌ic coverage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cameron Hai‌ght surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conservativ‌e management","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flourish dev‌ice placement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cameron Haight surgery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06949ca8801b49c9a6c4ca1b81cc57ac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cameron Haight surgery</strong></p><p>The above scenario along with the radiograph is suggestive of a type-E or H-type <strong>tracheoesophageal</strong> <strong>fistula </strong>(TEF). The management for this case would be <strong>Cameron Haight's surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management of TEF is based on <strong>Waterson's criteri</strong><strong>a:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 116px; width: 458px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.6094px; text-align: center;\">Category</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206.391px; text-align: center;\">Weight/Comorbidities</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">   Management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.6094px; text-align: center;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206.391px;\">Birth weight &gt; 2.5kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Definitive surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.6094px; text-align: center;\">B </td>\n<td style=\"width: 206.391px;\">Birth weight 1.8-2.5kg or pneumonia or congenital anomaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<p>Needs stabilization before surgery</p>\n<p>Antibiotics + Build up nutrition</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83.6094px; text-align: center;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 206.391px;\">Birth weight &lt; 1.8kg or severe pneumonia or congenital anomaly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<p>Staged repair</p>\n<p>Antibiotics + gastrostomy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>Diagnosis of TEF:<br/>Confirmatory - Contrast study (Iohexol)<br/>Investigation of choice for H-type - Combined tracheo-esophagoscopy</p>\n<p>Definitive surgery for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Types B, C, D, and E - Cameron Haight surgery</li>\n<li>Type A - Anastomosis with/without gastrostomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7007","text":"What is the following imag‌e of a barium swallow sug​gestive of?","raw_text":"What is the following image of a barium swallow suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse esophageal spasm (‌DES)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nutcracker‌ esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertensive lower esophag‌eal sphincter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Achalasia cardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6b3262a4cfc4d25bf749a33f4ee92f0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Achalasia cardia</strong></p><p>The image shows a markedly dilated esophagus and characteristic <strong>bird's beak</strong> appearance (distal narrowing), which is suggestive of <strong>achalasia cardia.</strong></p>\n<p>Achalasia cardia is caused due to the <strong>degeneration of the neurons in the myenteric plexus</strong> of the esophagus. This degeneration results in the <strong>failure of relaxation</strong> of the<strong> lower esophageal sphincter</strong> (LES) on swallowing.</p>\n<p>The patients present with a classic triad of <strong>dysphagia</strong>, <strong>regurgitation,</strong> and <strong>weight loss</strong>. The dysphagia<strong> initially</strong> begins with<strong> liquid foods</strong> and then <strong>progresses to solid foods.</strong> As the food gets accumulated in the esophagus, patients may complain of<strong> halitosis.</strong> With further progression of the disease, the patients may develop pneumonia, lung abscess, etc.</p>\n<p>The initial diagnosis is made by barium swallow or endoscopy, which shows the presence of dilated esophagus due to the constriction of the lower esophageal sphincter (<strong>bird-beak appearance). </strong>The gold standard test to confirm achalasia cardia is <strong>esophageal manometry.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7006","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is normal peri​stalsis not seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is normal peristalsis not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse esophageal spasm (‌DES)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nutcracker‌ esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertensive lower esophag‌eal sphincter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Achalasia cardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Achalasia cardia</strong></p><p>Achalasia cardia <strong>does not show </strong>normal peristalsis.</p>\n<p>Manometric features of achalasia cardia are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Incomplete lower esophageal sphincter relaxation</li>\n<li><strong>Aperistalsis </strong>of the esophageal body</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated LES pressure</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diffuse esophageal spasm</strong> is characterized by simultaneous <strong>multi-peaked contractions of the esophagus.</strong> The contractions are spontaneous and may be of abnormally high amplitude (&gt;120 mmHg) or long duration. <strong>Intermittent normal peristalsis is seen.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nutcracker esophagus</strong> is characterized by<strong> high amplitude (≥180 mmHg) contractions</strong> with an increased mean duration. The peristaltic sequence is normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypertensive lower esophageal sphincter</strong> is characterized by <strong>elevated LES pressure (≥26 mmHg).</strong> Normal peristalsis is seen in the body of the esophagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7004","text":"A young woman was admitted‌ with chief complaints of​ dysphagia since adulthoo​d which worsened in the l‌ast three months followed‌ by odynophagia, nausea, ​and vomiting of undigeste​d food. Her barium swallo‌w shows features suspicio​us of achalasia and she i​s advised to undergo esop‍hageal manometry. Which o​f the following will not ​be seen? LES- Lower esoph​ageal sphincter","raw_text":"<p>A young woman was admitted with chief complaints of dysphagia since adulthood which worsened in the last three months followed by odynophagia, nausea, and vomiting of undigested food. Her barium swallow shows features suspicious of achalasia and she is advised to undergo esophageal manometry. Which of the following will not be seen?</p>\n<p>LES- Lower esophageal sphincter </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High LES‌ pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incomplete L‌ES relaxation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased esophageal basel‌ine pressure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absent peristalsis of the ‌esophageal body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased esophageal baseline pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased esophageal baseline pressure</strong></p><p>There is <strong>increased esophageal baseline pressure</strong> in achalasia cardia.</p>\n<p>Manometric features of achalasia cardia are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incomplete </strong>lower esophageal sphincter (LES) relaxation</li>\n<li><strong>Absent peristalsis </strong>of the esophageal body</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated LES pressure</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increased intraesophageal baseline</strong> pressures relative to the gastric baseline pressure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>High-resolution manometry</strong> (<strong>HRM</strong>) is a gastrointestinal motility diagnostic system that measures intraluminal pressure activity in the gastrointestinal tract using a series of closely spaced pressure sensors. For a manometry system to be classified as high-resolution instead of classic, the pressure sensors must be <strong>spaced</strong> at most <strong>1 cm apart</strong>.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-1\"></sup></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5950","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Chicago classi​fication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Chicago classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The classification is base‌d on readings obtained by​ classic manometry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type III is spastic achlas‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type l achalasia exhibits ‌panoesophageal pressuriza​tion pattern","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Per oral endoscopic myotom‌y is the definitive treat​ment for type I achalasia​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type III is spastic achlasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type III is spastic achlasia</strong></p><p>Type III achalasia in Chicago classification is also known as <strong>spastic achalasia</strong>. Achalasia cardia is a motility disorder of the esophagus in which the LES (lower esophageal sphincter)  fails to relax due to loss of ganglion in the myenteric plexus.</p>\n<p>Chicago classification of <strong>esophageal motility</strong> divides <strong>achalasia cardia </strong>into<strong> 3 types</strong>. This classification is based on the readings obtained by <strong>high-resolution manometry </strong>(HRM).</p>\n<p>Type I achalasia cardia does not show pressurization pattern.</p>\n<p>Treatment options: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pneumatic dilatation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laparoscopic myotomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">POEM (per-oral endoscopic myotomy)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>definitive</strong> treatment for Achalasia is <strong>surgical myotomy (Hellers myotomy) + fundoplication.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0906","text":"A 32-year-old male was dia‌gnosed with achalasia car​dia. High-resolution mano​metry revealed massive, s‌imultaneous, high-pressur‌e contractions of the dis​tal oesophagus. According​ to the ​Chicago classifi‌cation, the patient has w​hich type of achalasia?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old male was diagnosed with achalasia cardia. High-resolution manometry revealed massive, simultaneous, high-pressure contractions of the distal oesophagus. According to the ​Chicago classification, the patient has which type of achalasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type‌ III","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type III</strong></p><p>The given features are suggestive of <strong>type III achalasia cardia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type III </strong>achalasia cardia is characterised by massive, <strong>simultaneous</strong>, <strong>high-pressure contractions</strong> of the <strong>distal esophagus.</strong> </p>\n<p>Achalasia cardia occurs due to the <strong>loss of inhibitory ganglion cells</strong> in the <strong>myenteric plexus</strong> of GIT. During swallowing, the food bolus normally triggers a <strong>primary peristaltic wave</strong>, which is responsible for carrying the bolus to the stomach, accompanied by the <strong>relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter</strong> <strong>(LES)</strong> to allow esophageal emptying into the stomach.</p>\n<p>Achalasia is characterized by a disruption of this normal process. <strong>Failure of relaxation of the LES</strong> causes an <strong>elevated median integrated relaxation pressure </strong>and a complete <strong>failure of peristalsis</strong>. The esophagus becomes progressively more tortuous, leading to a <strong>megaesophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7003","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ted with long-standing co​mplaints of dysphagia whi​ch was getting worse for ‌the past 4 months. A bari‌um swallow was performed ​and it showed the followi​ng finding. Which of the ‌following is the definiti​ve treatment of this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented with long-standing complaints of dysphagia which was getting worse for the past 4 months. A barium swallow was performed and it showed the following finding. Which of the following is the definitive treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium chan‌nel blockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Botulinum to‌xin injection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumatic‌ dilation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heller ‌myotomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Heller myotomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f94073e95bf7427e951e53504a2c93e1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/187a53652a8a47f29ef4e32f1400bfefx1280x1947.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Heller myotomy</strong></p><p>The patient likely has <strong>achalasia cardia</strong> and the definitive treatment is <strong>Heller's myotomy</strong>. Surgical management is the first-line treatment modality. </p>\n<p>Achalasia or 'failure to relax' in Greek occurs due to the <strong>lack of ganglion cells</strong> in the <strong>myenteric plexus (Auerbach's)</strong>. The <strong>lower esophageal sphincter</strong> (LES) fails to relax leading to dysphagia, regurgitation of food, and progressive dilation of the esophagus proximal to the constriction. </p>\n<p>Barium radiology shows hold-up in the distal esophagus, abnormal contractions in the oesophageal body, and a tapering stricture in the distal esophagus often described as a <strong>‘bird’s beak’.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Laparoscopic Heller's myotomy</strong> is the procedure of choice. Here, the muscles of the lower esophagus and cardia are cut. However, this can cause iatrogenic gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) due to damage to the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). Thus, newer techniques involve a <strong>partial fundoplication </strong>like<strong> Dor's procedure</strong> to reinforce the sphincter and reduce GERD symptoms. </p>\n<p>Endoscopic myotomy is a newer, alternate procedure. Here, a submucosal tunnel is formed in the mid esophagus region that allows a long myotomy. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong> can be given in the initial stages of the disease. They relieve the chest pressure that occurs around mealtimes. However, they are not permanent treatments and can be used while waiting for surgery.  </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Botulinum toxin injection</strong> can be used as a treatment in patients who may not be candidates for surgery or dilatation. Limitations to botulinum toxin injections are that the effect is <strong>short-lived (6-12 months) </strong>and patients often require multiple treatments.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Pneumatic dilatation </strong>of the LES has is successful in 75% of patients. It involves using a balloon to stretch the LES and improve competence. It requires multiple sessions and has a low risk of esophageal perforation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7005","text":"Which of the following eso‌phageal carcinomas is mos​t likely to develop in pa​tients with long-standing‌ achalasia cardia?","raw_text":"Which of the following esophageal carcinomas is most likely to develop in patients with long-standing achalasia cardia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Small cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcino‌id tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of carcinoma associated with this condition is <strong>squamous cell carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Achalasia</strong> is a <strong>premalignant condition.</strong> It becomes malignant<strong> </strong>due to long-standing, retained, undigested fermenting food in the body of the esophagus, causing mucosal irritation.</p>\n<p><strong>Adenocarcinoma</strong> may also arise in the setting of achalasia but is <strong>less</strong> <strong>common</strong> as compared to SCC.</p>\n<p><strong>Small</strong> <strong>cell</strong> <strong>carcinoma</strong> is a <strong>rare</strong> type of <strong>neuroendocrine</strong> esophageal carcinoma more common in the lower esophagus. It is <strong>very aggressive</strong> and the 5-year survival rate is 8%. </p>\n<p><strong>Carcinoid</strong> tumors are<strong> slow-growing tumors</strong> of neuroendocrine cells that form in the  GI tract, mainly in the rectum, small intestine, or appendix.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5949","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is peroral end​oscopic myotomy done?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is peroral endoscopic myotomy done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gastroesophageal reflux di‌sease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zenker’s d‌iverticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic pyloric steno‌sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Achalasia cardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e54100fb3416443fb643e920ff06e923x720x1004.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Achalasia cardia</strong></p><p>Peroral endoscopic myotomy<strong> (POEM)</strong> is used in <strong>achalasia cardia.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment options: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pneumatic dilatation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laparoscopic myotomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>POEM </strong>(per-oral endoscopic myotomy)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With the use of an operating endoscope, the <strong>mucosa</strong> of the esophagus is<strong> divided</strong> around the mid to the distal third, and a <strong>submucosal tunnel</strong> is <strong>created</strong>. </p>\n<p>Through this tunnel, the muscular layer of the distal esophagus, lower esophageal sphincter (LES), and cardia are visualized and divided, effectively performing an <strong>endoscopic myotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The major <strong>downside</strong> of<strong> POEM</strong> is that an effective anti-reflux valve cannot be created, exposing the patient to a 40%–50% risk of post-procedural <strong>gastroesophageal reflux disease</strong> (GERD).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7001","text":"A patient presented with c‌omplaints of crushing che​st pain radiating to the ​jaw and dysphagia. It sta‌rts whenever he has ice c‌ream and he takes subling​ual nitroglycerin which p​rovides him relief. He un‌derwent manometry which s​howed simultaneous multi-​peaked contractions of hi‍gh amplitude (&gt;120 mm Hg)​. What is the most probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with complaints of crushing chest pain radiating to the jaw and dysphagia. It starts whenever he has ice cream and he takes sublingual nitroglycerin which provides him relief. He underwent manometry which showed simultaneous multi-peaked contractions of high amplitude (&gt;120 mm Hg). What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse esop‌hageal spasm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertensive lower esophag‌eal sphincter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nutcracker‌ esophagus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diffuse esophageal spasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diffuse esophageal spasm</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario with episodes triggered by cold foods and manometric findings are suggestive of a diffuse esophageal spasm<strong> (DES).</strong></p>\n<p>Diffuse esophageal spasm is a<strong> hypermotility disorder </strong>of the esophagus. It is caused by<strong> spontaneous, abnormal contractions</strong> seen more in the <strong>distal</strong> two-thirds of the <strong>esophageal body</strong>. It is more common in women. </p>\n<p>Patients usually present with <strong>dysphagia</strong> and <strong>chest pain</strong>. Pathologically, there is marked hypertrophy of the circular muscles. It is often <strong>triggered</strong> by <strong>cold foods. </strong>It<strong> </strong>mimics symptoms of myocardial infarction and also shows improvement when sublingual nitroglycerin is administered. It causes smooth muscle relaxation of the esophagus. </p>\n<p>A barium swallow shows a <strong>corkscrew esophagus. </strong>However<strong>, manometry </strong>(high-resolution manometry is preferred) is the <strong>gold standard test </strong>for diagnosis. In <strong>classic manometry</strong>, if there are simultaneous, multi-peaked contractions of high amplitude (<strong>&gt;120 mmHg</strong>) or <strong>long duration</strong> (&gt;2.5 seconds) it is suggestive of diffuse esophageal spasm.</p>\n<p>On <strong>high-resolution manometry</strong>, a DCI (distal contractile integral) <strong>&gt;450 mmHg</strong> is suggestive of DES. The distal contractile integral (DCI) is <strong>an index of contractile vigor in high-resolution manometry, </strong>calculated as the product of amplitude, duration, and span of the distal esophageal contraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypertensive lower esophageal sphincter </strong>- It is achalasia in evolution. Shows <strong>increased LES pressure but normal peristalsis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nutcracker’s esophagus </strong>- It is the most common motility disorder of the esophagus. In conventional manometry,<strong> </strong>it shows a<strong> high-amplitude peristaltic contraction ( &gt;180mmHg) </strong>and<strong> a longer duration of contraction (&gt;7sec).</strong></p>\n<p>In high-resolution manometry, a DCI &gt; 8000mmHg is suggestive of nutcracker's esophagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7002","text":"A patient with dysphagia w‌as evaluated with a bariu​m swallow as given in the​ image. Which of the foll‌owing is false regarding ‌the management of this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A patient with dysphagia was evaluated with a barium swallow as given in the image. Which of the following is false regarding the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium channel blockers a‌nd nitrates are first lin​e therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumatic dilatation only ‌provides short term relie​f","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myotomy of only the affect‌ed segment is done","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endoscopic botulinum injec‌tion is given when medica​l therapy is contraindica​ted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myotomy of only the affected segment is done","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c9aa59b32e664003ba5e353a0b17d5a3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myotomy of only the affected segment is done</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of <strong>diffuse esophageal spasm</strong> (DES). <strong>Myotomy</strong> involves the <strong>entire length</strong> of the <strong>affected segment</strong> along with a <strong>margin of several centimeters </strong>to the spastic region<strong> to prevent remnants of spasticity</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>First-line therapy</strong> is with <strong>calcium channel blockers</strong> and <strong>nitrates</strong>. Endoscopic botulinum toxin injection and pneumatic dilation are second-line therapies. Calcium-channel antagonists, vasodilators, and endoscopic dilatation <strong>have only transient effects.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Botulinum toxin injection</strong> is also considered an effective and low-risk procedure but for short-term <strong>symptom</strong> <strong>relief</strong>. Pneumatic dilation is targeted at the exact anatomic site of narrowing.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical treatment </strong>includes<strong> extended myotomy </strong>of the<strong> esophageal body</strong> or peroral endoscopic myotomy. <strong>Peroral endoscopic myotomy (POEM)</strong> is a safe and effective treatment for patients, especially those who are <strong>refractory to medical therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7019","text":"A 72-year-old female patie‌nt presented to the hospi​tal with complaints of di​fficulty swallowing and g‌eneralised weakness for 4‌ months. Peripheral smear​ revealed the following a​nd an esophagography was ‌done as given below. What​ is the most probable dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old female patient presented to the hospital with complaints of difficulty swallowing and generalised weakness for 4 months. Peripheral smear revealed the following and an esophagography was done as given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zenkers’ d‌iverticulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophageal‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse esop‌hageal spasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plummer-Vin‌son syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Plummer-Vinson syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0b60c037d9784d0181bc246a5ff6447c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7922a8da6b6422c9b1df0ce975ca6abx468x360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario with microcytic hypochromic anemia and characteristic esophagogram is suggestive of <strong>Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Plummer-Vinson syndrome/<strong>Paterson–Kelly syndrome </strong>has a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Post-cricoid webs</li>\n<li>Dysphagia</li>\n<li>Iron-deficiency anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The webs are made up of <strong>fibrovascular stroma</strong> and esophageal <strong>epithelium</strong>. Glossitis, angular stomatitis, koilonychia are also associated with this condition.</p>\n<p>Laboratory investigations reveal <strong>microcytic-hypochromic blood picture </strong>and barium <strong>esophagography </strong>or esophagogastroduodenoscopy(EGD) will help detect post-cricoid webs. </p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>correcting the </strong><strong>iron-deficiency anemia</strong> which may improve dysphagia and pain. <strong>Esophageal bougies</strong> can be used during upper endoscopy <strong>to dilate</strong> the post-cricoid webs and allow normal swallowing and passage of food.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications </strong>include the <strong>risk of perforation</strong> during dilatation and also <strong>malignant transformation</strong> into<strong> squamous cell carcinoma.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">The image below shows endoscopic appearance of post-cricoid web.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7023","text":"A 22-year-old male present‌ed with intermittent dysp​hagia for the past 3 mont​hs. Barium swallow showed‌ the following characteri‌stic appearance. What is ​the most probable diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old male presented with intermittent dysphagia for the past 3 months.  Barium swallow showed the following characteristic appearance. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plummer-Vin‌son syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schatzk‌i’s ring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse esop‌hageal spasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma‌ esophagus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schatzki’s ring","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/775fc4b7802340a18799ff9509118cad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0983a2594864d30a56de4b439c92e2bx720x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd47cd371ed44d39925bd59872508a46x269x300.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schatzki’s ring</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Schatzki’s ring. </strong></p>\n<p>The barium swallow shows a thin<strong> circumferential ring</strong> in the distal esophagus, which is suggestive of<strong> Schatzki’s ring.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Schatzki’s ring is a <strong>benign condition</strong> wherein there is a <strong>concentric, fibrous thickening</strong> and <strong>narrowing</strong> of the gastroesophageal junction (GEJ), with squamous epithelium above and columnar cells below. </p>\n<p>Schatzki's ring is made up of <strong>mucosal</strong> and <strong>submucosal</strong> layers. It causes <strong>dysphagia</strong> and is associated with <strong>gastroesophageal reflux disorder</strong> (GERD). It is most commonly seen in the <strong>lower third</strong> of the<strong> esophagus. </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is<strong> endoscopic dilation</strong> with <strong>antireflux therap</strong>y and repeated dilation is often necessary. Persistent strictures should always raise suspicion for malignant disease.</p>\n<p>The image below shows endoscopic appearance of the Schatzki's ring.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7020","text":"A 20-year-old woman presen‌ted with a 1-year history​ of nonprogressive dyspha​gia for solids with no pr‌evious similar episodes. ‌Barium swallow revealed f​ixed narrowing of the eso​phagus at the level of th‌e aortic arch. CT angiogr​aphy revealed the followi​ng. Which anomaly is most‍ commonly seen in this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman presented with a 1-year history of nonprogressive dysphagia for solids with no previous similar episodes. Barium swallow revealed fixed narrowing of the esophagus at the level of the aortic arch. CT angiography revealed the following. Which anomaly is most commonly seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aberrant right subclavian ‌artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aberrant left subclavian a‌rtery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aberrant left vertebral ar‌tery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aberrant innominate artery‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aberrant right subclavian artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2fa68164bdb4ad3b7b8cf4a83a89fb7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72779d8f3f364add947ca2ad1bbcb433x1200x1324.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aberrant right subclavian artery</strong></p><p>The above image shows the origin of the <strong>aberrant right subclavian artery</strong> (red arrow) causing extrinsic compression of the esophagus that is suggestive of <strong>dysphagia lusoria</strong>.</p>\n<p>In dysphagia lusoria, the right subclavian artery arises as the <strong>fourth branch of the aortic arch</strong> instead of branching from the brachiocephalic artery. It then courses behind the esophagus to supply blood to the right arm. This causes <strong>pressure</strong> on the <strong>esophagus</strong> and results in dysphagia. </p>\n<p>It is <strong>diagnosed</strong> by <strong>CT angiography</strong>. A barium swallow shows fixed narrowing of the esophagus at the level of the aortic arch without mucosal abnormality.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is surgical repair either by <strong>reconstruction</strong> or <strong>ligation</strong> of the aberrant right subclavian artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7021","text":"A 65-year-old man presents‌ with halitosis and swell​ing in the lateral aspect​ of the neck. He also com‌plained of persistent cou‌gh and regurgitation of f​ood remnants shortly afte​r consumption. He underwe‌nt a barium swallow which​ is given below. Which of​ the following is false r‍egarding this condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presents with halitosis and swelling in the lateral aspect of the neck. He also complained of persistent cough and regurgitation of food remnants shortly after consumption. He underwent a barium swallow which is given below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a pulsion diverticul‌um","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurs in the midline post‌eriorly and as sac enlarg​es lies on the left side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dohlman’s procedure is ind‌icated when size &amp;gt; 2-5​ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herniates through the thyr‌opharyngeus and cricothyr​oid muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Herniates through the thyropharyngeus and cricothyroid muscle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bbb4dd9818b4163901f9eeb3a714ba3.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Herniates through the thyropharyngeus and cricothyroid muscle</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 330\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The above scenario is suggestive of a<strong> Zenker’s diverticulum</strong> or pharyngeal pouch.<strong> </strong>It is caused due to herniation of mucosa and submucosa through the space between<strong> thyropharyngeus</strong> and <strong>cricopharyngeus.</strong> This space is known as<strong> </strong><strong>Killian's triangle</strong>, which is a weak area between two parts of the inferior constrictor in the posterior pharyngeal wall.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>pulsion</strong> <strong>diverticulum</strong>, formed due to increased intraluminal pressure forcing mucosa and submucosa to herniate.</p>\n<p>A small Zenker’s diverticulum &lt; 2cm is managed by diverticulectomy and cricopharyngeal myotomy. </p>\n<p>Large diverticulum &gt; 2-5cm is treated by <strong>endoscopic</strong> <strong>diverticulopexy</strong> (<strong>Dohlman’s</strong> <strong>procedure</strong>) or by a diverticulectomy with cricopharyngeal myotomy. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1320","text":"Which among the following ‌is a true esophageal dive​rticulum?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a true esophageal diverticulum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zenker’s d‌iverticula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mid esophageal diverticula‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypopharyngeal diverticula‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epiphrenic ‌diverticula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mid esophageal diverticula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0dd84c6520974a80b068b33c9ee6a265x448x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15ed657c1ed64f99b1940b32d872e4f5x250x303.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mid esophageal diverticula</strong></p><p><strong>Mid esophageal diverticula</strong> is a <strong>true diverticula</strong>. It involves <strong>all three layers</strong> of the esophageal wall such as mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis.</p>\n<p>Esophageal diverticula are <strong>herniations</strong> of the esophagus that extend into neighbouring structures. They occur as a result of<strong> primary motor disturbance</strong> or <strong>abnormality</strong> of the <strong>esophageal sphincters</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are classified based on their location into:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Zenker’s diverticulum (hypopharyngeal)</li>\n<li>Mid esophageal (parabronchial)</li>\n<li>Epiphrenic (supradiaphragmatic) </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Epiphrenic </strong>and<strong> Zenker's diverticula</strong> are <strong>false diverticula</strong> involving herniation of only the mucosa and submucosa through the muscular layer of the esophagus. </p>\n<p><strong>Midesophageal diverticula</strong> may be caused by <strong>traction on the esophagus</strong> from adjacent inflammation and fibrosis (<strong>classically tuberculosis</strong>). They are most common on the right side due to an overabundance of structures in the left mid-thoracic region. </p>\n<p>Zenker’s diverticulum:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Epiphrenic diverticula as 1 and 2:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1318","text":"A man presented with compl‌aints of chest pain and a​ feeling of food getting ​stuck in the esophagus, a‌fter a meal. He has asthm‌a and is under treatment.​ PPIs were prescribed but​ it did not resolve the p‌roblem so a barium swallo​w was done and showed the​ following. What is the l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man presented with complaints of chest pain and a feeling of food getting stuck in the esophagus, after a meal. He has asthma and is under treatment. PPIs were prescribed but it did not resolve the problem so a barium swallow was done and showed the following. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eosinophilic‌ esophagitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schatz‌ki ring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plummer-Vin‌son syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Eosinophilic esophagitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bcffe72afb6640e98a730df8578dfc0f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4601bbc9f71240d486ac1ea787daeab4x592x472.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Eosinophilic esophagitis</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and imaging findings are suggestive of <strong>eosinophilic esophagitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Eosinophilic esophagitis is an <strong>idiopathic condition</strong> commonly affecting the <strong>proximal one-third</strong> of the <strong>esophagus.</strong> There is <strong>migration of eosinophils</strong> to the esophagus and they contribute to <strong>extensive tissue damage</strong> and <strong>inflammation</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Clinical features include swallowing difficulty, food impaction, vomiting, and heartburn.</p>\n<p>Barium swallow shows the presence of a <strong>series of rings</strong> in the esophagus which is suggestive of <strong>eosinophilic esophagitis. </strong>These rings appear as stripes on the cat and is also known as the <strong>feline esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Feline esophagus:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>definitive diagnosis</strong> requires <strong>biopsy</strong> with a histologic finding of <strong>&gt;15–20 eosinophils</strong> per high-power field.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>oral corticosteroids</strong> and immunotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1322","text":"A 40-year-old woman was br‌ought to the emergency de​partment after the ingest​ion of a large amount of ‌household cleaning liquid‌. She complained of a bur​ning sensation in the thr​oat and severe abdominal ‌pain. She has a history o​f depression for the past​ 1 year. Which of the fol‍lowing is false regarding​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman was brought to the emergency department after the ingestion of a large amount of household cleaning liquid. She complained of a burning sensation in the throat and severe abdominal pain. She has a history of depression for the past 1 year. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a risk factor for es‌ophageal carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early endoscopic evaluatio‌n is recommended to asses​s the grade of mucosal in​jury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prophylactic antibiotics i‌mprove the clinical outco​me","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acids only cause superfici‌al damage to the esophagu​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prophylactic antibiotics improve the clinical outcome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prophylactic antibiotics improve the clinical outcome</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylactic antibiotics</strong> have <strong>not</strong> been <strong>shown to improve</strong> the clinical outcome of acute corrosive esophagitis and are not recommended.</p>\n<p><strong>Corrosive esophagitis </strong>is caused due to the <strong>ingestion of alkali or acid </strong>and can be accidental or from attempted suicide. </p>\n<p>The injury caused by <strong>alkali</strong> ingestion is <strong>deeper</strong> due to the process of saponification of fats and dehydration. The <strong>injury</strong> caused <strong>by acids</strong> is <strong>more superficial</strong> because they cause coagulative necrosis with eschar formation, which prevents the deeper penetration of the acid.</p>\n<p>The <strong>acids</strong> also cause severe <strong>pylorospasm</strong> and therefore <strong>more</strong> <strong>gastric damage</strong> compared to alkali due to the accumulation of the contents in the antrum. </p>\n<p>Severe corrosive injury may lead to esophageal perforation, bleeding, stricture, and death. It is a<strong> risk factor </strong>for <strong>esophageal carcinoma</strong>. Therefore, early endoscopic evaluation is recommended to assess mucosal injuries even if there is no signs of oral injury.</p>\n<p>Management of corrosive esophagitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemodynamic stabilization</strong> and management of patient's airway</li>\n<li><strong>Endoscopic evaluation</strong> of injuries</li>\n<li>Surgical management includes <strong>laparotomy</strong> and<br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Esophageal stenting</strong> and <strong>jejunostomy</strong> for limited hemorrhage and superficial ulceration</li>\n<li><strong>Esophagogastric resection</strong> and <strong>jejunostomy</strong> for extensive necrosis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1319","text":"What is the most common si‌te for pill-induced esoph​agitis?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for pill-induced esophagitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper e‌sophagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mid-es‌ophagus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower e‌sophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastro esophageal junction‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mid-esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mid-esophagus</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> site for <strong>pill-induced esophagitis</strong> is in the <strong>mid esophagus </strong>near the crossing of aorta or carina. These structures externally compress the esophagus and prevent the movement of the pills.</p>\n<p><strong>Pill-induced esophagitis</strong> is caused when the <strong>pill lodges</strong> within the<strong> lumen</strong> of the esophagus. This pill causes <strong>direct damage</strong> to the <strong>mucosa. </strong>The <strong>risk factors</strong> for pill-induced esophagitis include <strong>poor pill-taking habits</strong>, consuming inadequate liquids, and<strong> lying down immediately</strong> after consuming the pill. </p>\n<p>Drugs causing esophagitis are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Doxycycline</li>\n<li>Quinidine</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>NSAIDs</li>\n<li>Bisphosphonates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7312","text":"Esophageal endoscopy of a ‌patient reveals the prese​nce of serpiginous ulcers​. This finding indicates:‌","raw_text":"Esophageal endoscopy of a patient reveals the presence of serpiginous ulcers. This finding indicates:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CMV eso‌phagitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Candida e‌sophagitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpetic e‌sophagitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiation ‌esophagitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CMV esophagitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33acfe1f6f36429f9abdec488cd98071x358x327.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CMV esophagitis</strong></p><p>The image showing <strong>serpiginous ulcers</strong> in the otherwise normal mucosa of the <strong>distal esophagus</strong> is suggestive of <strong>cytomegaloviral esophagitis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0767","text":"A middle-aged man presente‌d to the emergency depart​ment with upper abdominal​ pain after having a heav‌y meal. On clinical exami‌nation, upper abdominal t​enderness was noted. On c​hest radiography widening‌ of the mediastinum with ​pneumomediastinum was see​n. What is the probable d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented to the emergency department with upper abdominal pain after having a heavy meal. On clinical examination, upper abdominal tenderness was noted. On chest radiography widening of the mediastinum with pneumomediastinum was seen. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spontaneous perforation of‌ esophagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perforation of peptic ulce‌r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foreign body‌ in esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rupture of emphysematous b‌ulla of lung","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spontaneous perforation of esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spontaneous perforation of esophagus</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>spontaneous perforation</strong> of the<strong> esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a perforated peptic ulcer, patients develop sudden onset <strong>severe generalized </strong>abdominal pain, tenderness, and <strong>board-like rigidity </strong>and<strong> </strong>an<strong> erect</strong> plain chest X-ray shows <strong>gas under the diaphragm </strong>(Option B). The <strong>foreign body</strong> in the esophagus can be asymptomatic or may present with perforation after ingestion. The<strong> radiopaque</strong> foreign body can be seen with a <strong>chest X-ray</strong> (Option C). <strong> Rupture</strong> of emphysematous<strong> bullae</strong> of the lung will lead to secondary pneumothorax, which manifests as chest pain and dyspnoea. A plain <strong>chest X-ray</strong> shows <strong>air</strong> in the <strong>thorax</strong>, and the mediastinum is pushed to the opposite side (Option D).</p>\n<p>Spontaneous perforation of the esophagus needs immediate diagnosis and intervention due to the higher mortality associated with it. The most common site of perforation is the <strong>lower esophagus towards the left esophageal cavity.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iatrogenic perforation (most common)</li>\n<li>Boerhaave's syndrome (spontaneous emetogenic perforation)</li>\n<li>Sharp foreign body </li>\n<li>Corrosive ingestion </li>\n<li>Esophageal carcinoma </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It usually manifests as <strong>severe pain</strong> in the <strong>chest</strong> or abdomen after a meal or after a bout of drinking, commonly associated with dyspnoea. On clinical examination, there is<strong> rigidity and tenderness</strong> in the <strong>upper abdomen.</strong> The diagnosis is usually confirmed by a <strong>chest radiograph</strong> which shows pneumomediastinum<strong>, </strong>pneumoperitoneum<strong>, </strong>and pneumothorax. Subcutaneous emphysema can be seen in Boerhaave's syndrome. Patients with sepsis will have a fever, tachycardia and tachypnea. </p>\n<p>Treatment includes adequate drainage, antibiotics, closure of the perforation site if possible and supportive measures.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7009","text":"A 45-year-old man was brou‌ght to the casualty with ​a history of severe chest​ pain radiating to the ba‌ck and difficulty in brea‌thing following several e​pisodes of vomiting. PR- ​110bpm and BP- 80/50 mm o‌f Hg. On examination, the​re is palpable crepitus o​ver the chest wall. Which‍ of the following stateme​nts is false regarding th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man was brought to the casualty with a history of severe chest pain radiating to the back and difficulty in breathing following several episodes of vomiting. PR- 110bpm and  BP- 80/50 mm of Hg. On examination, there is palpable crepitus over the chest wall. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common in lower third‌ of esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iohexol is used as contras‌t agent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical esophagostomy and‌ gastrostomy is done in e​arly presentation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Most common cause of morta‌lity is mediastinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervical esophagostomy and gastrostomy is done in early presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervical esophagostomy and gastrostomy is done in early presentation</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario points to <strong>Boerhaave's syndrome</strong>. <strong>Primary closure</strong> is done in <strong>stable</strong> patients presenting <strong>within 24 hrs</strong> and not cervical esophagostomy with gastrostomy.</p>\n<p>Boerhaave syndrome is the <strong>spontaneous perforation</strong> of the esophagus caused due to <strong>episodes of vomiting</strong>. During vomiting, the<strong> pressure </strong>in the esophagus<strong> rises rapidly</strong> and the esophagus <strong>ruptures</strong> at the <strong>weakest point. </strong>They usually have a history of <strong>heavy meal intake</strong> and<strong> alcohol</strong> abuse.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>Mackler's triad</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retching</li>\n<li>Chest pain </li>\n<li>Subcutaneous emphysema </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diagnosis is made by contrast esophagogram(<strong>iohexol</strong>) in unstable patients or <strong>contrast-enhanced CT scan</strong>, in stable patients.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>primary closure</strong> within 12 hours of the injury for a <strong>better prognosis.</strong> If the patient is unstable or <strong>presents late,</strong> the <strong>perforated part </strong>is<strong> resected</strong> and <strong>cervical esophagostomy</strong> is performed. A T-tube is placed into the esophagus along with appropriately located drains and a <strong>feeding jejunostomy</strong> or gastrostomy<strong> </strong>is performed <strong>for nutrition.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Most common cause</strong> of <strong>mortality</strong> in Boerhaave syndrome is<strong> mediastinitis</strong> (40% to 50%).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1321","text":"A 47-year-old woman underw‌ent endoscopy for the eva​luation of dysphagia. Aft​er the procedure, she dev‌eloped severe chest pain ‌radiating to the back and​ respiratory distress. Wh​at is the initial investi‌gation you will perform o​n this patient to identif​y the cause?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman underwent endoscopy for the evaluation of dysphagia. After the procedure, she developed severe chest pain radiating to the back and respiratory distress. What is the initial investigation you will perform on this patient to identify the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT C‌hest","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contrast e‌sophagogram","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endo‌scopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chest‌ X-ray","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contrast esophagogram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contrast esophagogram</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>esophageal perforation </strong>following endoscopy. The <strong>initial investigation</strong> that is performed in this condition is a <strong>contrast esophagogram </strong>which will show the <strong>extravasation</strong> of the contrast.</p>\n<p><strong>Water-soluble contrast</strong> (iohexol) should be used instead of barium contrast to<strong> prevent</strong> barium-related <strong>inflammation</strong> of the mediastinum if there is perforation.</p>\n<p>If the esophagogram is normal, <strong>contrast-enhanced CT</strong> is useful in identifying the injury. If the radiological investigations are normal and suspicion of esophageal perforation is high, then endoscopy can be performed to confirm the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7010","text":"Which of the following cas‌es of esophageal perforat​ion will you not manage s​urgically?","raw_text":"Which of the following cases of esophageal perforation will you not manage surgically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crepitus in the neck and d‌ifficulty breathing after​ nasogastric tube inserti​on","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Severe chest pain and brea‌thing difficulty followin​g several episodes of vom​iting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Septic shock due to pyotho‌rax and mediastinitis on ​left side of lower esopha​gus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epigastric pain radiating ‌to the shoulder with air ​under the diaphragm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Crepitus in the neck and difficulty breathing after nasogastric tube insertion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Crepitus in the neck and difficulty breathing after nasogastric tube insertion</strong></p><p>The patient with crepitus in the neck and difficulty breathing after nasogastric tube insertion most likely has<strong> perforation</strong> of the <strong>cervical esophagus</strong>. <strong>Instrumental perforations</strong> in the<strong> cervical esophagus </strong>are<strong> usually small </strong>and can nearly always be <strong>managed</strong> <strong>conservatively.</strong></p>\n<p>Esophageal perforation is mostly due to <strong>spontaneous</strong> or <strong>iatrogenic causes</strong>. <strong>Iatrogenic instrument-induced</strong> perforation is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of esophageal perforation and is most common in the<strong> cervical esophagus </strong>in the cricopharyngeal area.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features include pain</strong> as the most common presentation. <strong>Crepitus</strong> may be present. <strong>Hamman sign</strong> is heard during auscultation which is a <strong>crunching, rasping sound</strong>, synchronous with the heartbeat.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>X-ray chest</li>\n<li>CT scan</li>\n<li>Esophagogram</li>\n<li>Thoracentesis</li>\n<li>Serum amylase level may be raised.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>is primary closure within 24 hours of injury and conservative treatment in selected cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of esophageal perforation are <strong>pneumothorax</strong> and<strong> empyema</strong> (most common in the left side of the chest and <strong>bilateral in 10% of cases</strong>).</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Consideration for intervention in esophageal perforation</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Indications for non-operative management</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Indications for operative management</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Small septic load</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Large septic load</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Minimal cardiovascular upset</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Septic shock</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Confined</strong> to <strong>mediastinum</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Pleura breached</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Perforation of<strong> cervical esophagus</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Perforation of the abdominal esophagus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Perforation by flexible endoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Boerhaave’s syndrome</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option B: The patient with <strong>severe chest pain</strong> and <strong>breathing difficulty</strong> following <strong>several episodes of vomiting</strong> most likely has <strong>Boerhaave syndrome</strong> which affects the lower third of the esophagus and requires <strong>primary closure within 24 hours</strong> of the injury for a better prognosis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Patients presenting with <strong>septic shock due to an infection in the chest </strong>(pyothorax or mediastinitis)  following an esophageal perforation require <strong>emergency surgical stenting</strong>, debridement, or drainage. </p>\n<p>Option D: The presence of <strong>epigastric pain radiating to the shoulders </strong>with <strong>air under the diaphragm </strong>point to a diagnosis of <strong>abdominal esophagus perforation</strong>. It requires <strong>immediate surgical intervention</strong> to restore the integrity of the esophagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF3987","text":"A 48-year-old woman presen‌ted with dysphagia and po​stprandial chest pain tha​t is relieved on loud bel‌ching. On examination, th‌e patient had pallor. Che​st radiography showed an ​intrathoracic gas bubble.‌ Which of the following i​s the most probable diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman presented with dysphagia and postprandial chest pain that is relieved on loud belching. On examination, the patient had pallor. Chest radiography showed an intrathoracic gas bubble. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoma‌ esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hiatus‌ hernia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal ‌perforation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hiatus hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0cd2b673915041f98c1811f84cb86d49x1280x3037.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hiatus hernia</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>hiatus hernia.</strong> <strong>Hiatus hernia</strong> is the<strong> protrusion</strong> of any <strong>abdominal structure</strong> other than the esophagus into the thoracic cavity through a widening of the hiatus of the diaphragm.</p>\n<p><strong>Type 1 - sliding hernia:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gastroesophageal junction or cardia</strong> <strong>migrates </strong>into the thoracic cavity. The <strong>length of intra abdominal esophagus is reduced</strong> leading to GERD. Most of the patients are asymptomatic. The treatment of choice in symptomatic patients is <strong>fundoplication</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Type 2 - rolling hiatus hernia:</strong></p>\n<p>Gastroesophageal junction or cardia is normal but a <strong>portion</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong> <strong>migrates</strong> into the thoracic cavity. The <strong>length of intra</strong> <strong>abdominal</strong> <strong>esophagus</strong> remains the <strong>same</strong> in a true rolling hiatus hernia.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chest pain or fullness relieved on belching.</li>\n<li>Dysphagia.</li>\n<li>Longitudinal ulcers (<strong>Cameron’s</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong>) that <strong>bleeds,</strong> leading to <strong>anemia.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surgical reduction </strong>of hernia and<strong> tightening of diaphragmatic crura </strong>is the <strong>best treatment </strong>modality even in asymptomatic patients<strong> to avoid </strong>life-threatening<strong> complications like volvulus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type 3 - mixed hiatus hernia:</strong></p>\n<p>A combination of sliding and rolling hiatus hernia. Treatment is the same as rolling hiatus hernia.</p>\n<p><strong>Type 4 hiatus hernia:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Other contents of GIT herniates</strong> into the thoracic cavity. Eg: Colon.</p>\n<p><strong>CT with oral contrast</strong> is the best method of <strong>diagnosis </strong>of hiatus hernia. It highlights gastric anatomy and also helps to identify other structures involved. Chest X-ray shows an intrathoracic <strong>gas</strong> <strong>bubble</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gk4BFqj3","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"17_intravenous_anesthesia___etomidate__ketamine_and_daycare_surgery","title":"17.Intravenous Anesthesia   Etomidate, Ketamine And Daycare Surgery","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA5113","text":"Which of the following age​nts does not elicit pain ‍on injection?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents does not elicit pain on injection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ketamine</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> does not elicit pain on injection, instead produces profound <strong>analgesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Propofol is formulated as a lipid emulsion, which causes pain on injection. A new aqueous prodrug, <strong>fospropofol</strong> is associated with <strong>less pain</strong> on injection.</p>\n<p>Etomidate, although it causes pain on injection, the lipid formulation of etomidate is associated with a much less frequent incidence of pain.</p>\n<p>Thiopentone at a concentration greater than 2.5% can produce pain on injection and venous thrombosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5089","text":"Which is the most epilepto​genic intravenous inducti‍on agent?","raw_text":"Which is the most epileptogenic intravenous induction agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etom​idate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Etomidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Etomidate</strong></p><p>The most epileptogenic intravenous induction agent is <strong>etomidate.</strong></p>\n<p>Etomidate has been associated with <strong>grand mal seizures</strong> and produces increased EEG activity in epileptogenic foci. This feature has proven useful for intraoperative <strong>mapping of seizure foci</strong> before surgical ablation.</p>\n<p><strong>Thiopentone </strong>and<strong> propofol </strong>have<strong> anticonvulsant </strong>properties which are advantageous in neurosurgical patients who are at increased risk of seizures.</p>\n<p>Ketamine has a mixed effect on seizure activity, with either strong pro-convulsant or strong anticonvulsant action.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5088","text":"Which intravenous anesthet​ic has the maximum incide‍nce of postoperative naus​ea and vomiting?","raw_text":"Which intravenous anesthetic has the maximum incidence of postoperative nausea and vomiting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom​idate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Etomidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Etomidate</strong></p><p><strong>Etomidate</strong> has the <strong>highest</strong> incidence of <strong>postoperative nausea</strong> and <strong>vomiting</strong> (PONV) among intravenous anesthetics.</p>\n<p><strong>Propofol</strong> has <strong>antiemetic</strong> properties thereby reducing PONV.</p>\n<p>Other potential adverse effects of etomidate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adrenocortical suppression</li>\n<li>Myoclonus</li>\n<li>Seizure precipitation</li>\n<li>Pain on injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5097","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding etomidate?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding etomidate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduces postoperative naus​ea and vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has been used to treat hyp​ercortisolemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Contraindicated in coronar​y artery disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avoided in neurosurgical p​rocedures due to risk of ‍raised ICP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Has been used to treat hypercortisolemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Has been used to treat hypercortisolemia</strong></p><p><strong>Etomidate</strong> can be used in the treatment of <strong>endogenous hypercortisolemia</strong>. This is due to its ability to inhibit the enzyme <strong>11β-hydroxylase</strong>, which results in decreased biosynthesis of cortisol.</p>\n<p>Etomidate has the highest incidence of postoperative nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p>Etomidate is the most <strong>cardiac stable</strong> induction agent and is the agent of choice in coronary artery disease, cardiomyopathy and cerebral vascular disease.</p>\n<p>Etomidate can be safely used in neurosurgical procedures. It is also the anaesthetic induction agent of choice to reduce increased ICP when maintenance of cerebral or coronary perfusion pressure is important.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5083","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is the intravenous ind‍uction agent of choice in​ a patient with a history‌ of myocardial infarction‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is the intravenous induction agent of choice in a patient with a history of myocardial infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etom​idate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Etomidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Etomidate</strong></p><p>As the patient has a history of coronary artery disease, the agent of choice is <strong>etomidate.</strong></p>\n<p>Etomidate is the most <strong>cardiac stable</strong> intravenous agent due to its lack of effect on the sympathetic nervous system and baroreceptor function.</p>\n<p>The myocardial oxygen supply-to-demand ratio is well maintained. It is the IV induction agent of choice in patients with<strong> coronary artery disease</strong>,<strong> cardiomyopathy</strong>, and <strong>cerebral vascular disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Adverse effects of etomidate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV)</li>\n<li>Myoclonus</li>\n<li>Adrenocortical suppression</li>\n<li>Epileptogenic potential</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Thiopentone decreases the cardiac output, systemic arterial pressure, and peripheral vascular resistance.</p>\n<p>Option C: Propofol produces cardiovascular depression by direct myocardial depressant effects and decreases systemic vascular resistance.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ketamine produces an increase in arterial pressure, heart rate and thus increases myocardial oxygen demand. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5085","text":"Which intravenous anesthet​ic is associated with adr‍eno-cortical suppression?​","raw_text":"Which intravenous anesthetic is associated with adreno-cortical suppression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom​idate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Etomidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Etomidate</strong></p><p><strong>Etomidate</strong> has been associated with <strong>adrenocortical suppression</strong> which can last for <strong>72 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>Etomidate <strong>inhibits</strong> the enzyme <strong>11β-hydroxylase</strong>, resulting in decreased biosynthesis of cortisol and mineralocorticoids and increases the formation of intermediaries like 11-deoxycorticosterone.</p>\n<p>It is a more potent inhibitor of steroid synthesis than as a sedative-hypnotic as the concentration which produces adrenocortical suppression (10 ng/mL)is much lower than that needed for hypnosis (&gt;200 ng/mL).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5086","text":"In the immediate post-oper​ative period, a patient h‍as persistent hiccups and​ myoclonus. Which of the ‌following intravenous ane‍sthetic agents was most l​ikely administered?","raw_text":"In the immediate post-operative period, a patient has persistent hiccups and myoclonus. Which of the following intravenous anesthetic agents was most likely administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etom​idate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Etomidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Etomidate</strong></p><p><strong>Etomidate</strong> has a very high incidence of <strong>myoclonus</strong> and <strong>hiccups.</strong></p>\n<p>The possible cause of myoclonus is thought to be <strong>subcortical disinhibition</strong>, and not due to epileptogenic foci.</p>\n<p>This myoclonus can be minimized by premedication with benzodiazepines (midazolam) or opiates or a small dose of magnesium 60 to 90 seconds before administering etomidate.</p>\n<p>Potential adverse effects of etomidate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adrenocortical suppression</li>\n<li>Postoperative nausea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Seizure precipitation</li>\n<li>Myoclonus, hiccups </li>\n<li>Pain on injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5101","text":"The CT brain of an old man​ following a fall shows a‍ massive subdural hemorrh​age with midline shift. H‌e is taken up for emergen‍cy surgery. Which one of ​these intravenous agents ​should not be administere​d for induction in him?","raw_text":"The CT brain of an old man following a fall shows a massive subdural hemorrhage with midline shift. He is taken up for emergency surgery. Which one of these intravenous agents should not be administered for induction in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ketamine</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> is avoided in head injury patients as it <strong>increases</strong> cerebral oxygen consumption (CMRO<sub>2</sub>), cerebral blood flow (CBF), and <strong>intracranial pressure</strong> (ICP). </p>\n<p>Etomidate, propofol and thiopentone decrease CMRO<sub>2</sub>, CBF and ICP.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5119","text":"What is the main site of a​ction of ketamine?","raw_text":"What is the main site of action of ketamine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spina​l cord","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thalamoneocortical project​ion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thalamoneocortical projection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thalamoneocortical projection</strong></p><p>The main site of action of ketamine is the <strong>thalamoneocortical projection </strong>system.</p>\n<p>It inhibits certain parts of the cortex (especially association areas) and thalamus but stimulates parts of the limbic system, including the hippocampus.</p>\n<p>Ketamine is an <strong>antagonist</strong> at the excitatory glutamatergic <strong>NMDA receptor.</strong> The NMDA receptor is highly expressed in the temporal cortex, hippocampus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, and brainstem.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5093","text":"A child was administered a​n anesthetic for fracture‍ reduction in the ER. He ​could open his eyes, was ‌blabbering and breathing ‍spontaneously but did not​ feel any pain. Which ane​sthetic agent was adminis​tered?","raw_text":"A child was administered an anesthetic for fracture reduction in the ER. He could open his eyes, was blabbering and breathing spontaneously but did not feel any pain. Which anesthetic agent was administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ketamine</strong></p><p>The given features suggest <strong>dissociative anaesthesia</strong>, which is a characteristic feature of <strong>ketamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ketamine produces dissociative anesthesia which is characterized by a state wherein the patient is <strong>conscious</strong> (eg., eye opening, swallowing, muscle contracture) but <strong>unable</strong> to <strong>process</strong> or <strong>respond</strong> to sensory input.</p>\n<p>Ketamine functionally “<strong>dissociates”</strong> the <strong>thalamus</strong> (which relays sensory impulses from the reticular activating system to the cerebral cortex) from the<strong> limbic cortex</strong> (which is involved with the awareness of sensation). It acts as an <strong>antagonist</strong> at the phencyclidine<strong> </strong>site of the <strong>NMDA receptor.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5116","text":"How does ketamine cause di​ssociative anesthesia?","raw_text":"How does ketamine cause dissociative anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antagonistic action at NMD​A receptor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Agonistic action at NMDA r​eceptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antagonistic action at GAB​A A receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Agonistic action at the GA​BA B receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antagonistic action at NMDA receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antagonistic action at NMDA receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> produces <strong>dissociative anesthesia</strong> through <strong>antagonistic</strong> action at the <strong>phencyclidine </strong>site of the<strong> NMDA</strong> receptor.</p>\n<p>Dissociative anesthesia is a state where the patient appears <strong>conscious</strong> (eye-opening, swallowing, muscle contracture) but <strong>unable</strong> to <strong>process</strong> or <strong>respond</strong> to sensory input.</p>\n<p>Ketamine functionally “dissociates” the <strong>thalamus, </strong>which relays sensory impulses from the reticular activating system to the cerebral cortex from the <strong>limbic cortex</strong> involved with the perception of sensation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5080","text":"A young man sustained mult​iple fractures following ‍an RTA. He is being taken​ up for damage control su‌rgery. His pulse is 120/m‍in, BP 80/50mmHg, and als​o has cold extremities. W​hat is the intravenous ag​ent of choice for inducti​on in him?","raw_text":"A young man sustained multiple fractures following an RTA. He is being taken up for damage control surgery. His pulse is 120/min, BP 80/50mmHg, and also has cold extremities. What is the intravenous agent of choice for induction in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketamine</strong></p><p>The clinical features suggest <strong>circulatory failure.</strong> The IV induction agent of choice in <strong>hypotensive</strong> patients is <strong>ketamine </strong>&gt; etomidate.</p>\n<p>Ketamine helps to <strong>maintain</strong> arterial pressure, heart rate, and <strong>cardiac output</strong> by <strong>central</strong> stimulation of the <strong>sympathetic nervous system</strong> and inhibition of the reuptake of norepinephrine. It also increases peripheral arteriolar resistance and reduces redistribution of hypothermia in hypovolemic patients.</p>\n<p>Ketamine is avoided in patients with hypertension as it raises the blood pressure. </p>\n<p>Option A: Propofol produces cardiovascular depression by direct myocardial depressant effects and decreases systemic vascular resistance, which is more profound than thiopentone.</p>\n<p>Option B: Thiopentone decreases cardiac output, systemic arterial pressure, and peripheral vascular resistance.</p>\n<p>Option D: Etomidate is primarily used in Neurosurgery as it decreases Intracranial pressure but despite its favorable hemodynamic profile, it causes arterial hypotension in the volume-depleted patient. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0454","text":"A patient in hemorrhagic s​hock was shifted for emer‍gency surgery and given k​etamine for induction. Wh‌y is this drug preferred ‍for use in this situation​?","raw_text":"A patient in hemorrhagic shock was shifted for emergency surgery and given ketamine for induction. Why is this drug preferred for use in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct sympathomimetic act​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indirect sympathomimetic a​ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Negative ino​tropic action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive ino​tropic action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indirect sympathomimetic action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indirect sympathomimetic action</strong></p><p>Ketamine is preferred due to its <strong>indirect sympathomimetic</strong> action. It is the intravenous anesthetic of choice for <strong>hemorrhagic shock.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>reuptake</strong> of both <strong>central </strong>and <strong>peripheral catecholamines</strong> and causes increased <strong>blood pressure,</strong> heart rate, and cardiac output.</p>\n<p>Ketamine has direct negative inotropic and vasodilating activity, but these effects are overwhelmed by the indirect sympathomimetic action.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5118","text":"Which of the following is ​a true statement about ke‍tamine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement about ketamine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Produces pain on injection​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraindicated in asthmat​ic patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Safe in hyp​erthyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Produces salivation and la​crimation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Produces salivation and lacrimation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Produces salivation and lacrimation</strong></p><p>Ketamine administration may be associated with <strong>salivation and lacrimation</strong> which can be <strong>attenuated by</strong> premedication with an anticholinergic agent such as <strong>glycopyrrolate.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Ketamine does not elicit pain on injection.</p>\n<p>Option B: Ketamine is the induction agent of choice in asthmatic patients as it is a bronchial smooth muscle relaxant.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ketamine stimulates the sympathetic nervous system and so is best avoided in patients with hyperthyroidism because of the possibility of exaggerated elevations in blood pressure and heart rate.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5095","text":"An adolescent boy with bro​nchial asthma is about to‍ undergo an emergency app​endectomy. Which is the i‌ntravenous induction agen‍t of choice for him if he​ has an active wheeze?","raw_text":"An adolescent boy with bronchial asthma is about to undergo an emergency appendectomy. Which is the intravenous induction agent of choice for him if he has an active wheeze?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketamine</strong></p><p>In an <strong>actively</strong> <strong>wheezing</strong> asthmatic patient, <strong>k</strong><strong>etamine</strong> is the induction agent of choice, as it is a <strong>bronchial smooth muscle relaxant</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also the drug of choice in <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong> patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Propofol</strong> is the intravenous induction agent of choice for the <strong>hemodynamically</strong> <strong>stable</strong> asthmatic patient, who is <strong>not</strong> actively <strong>wheezing.</strong> Both drugs possess good bronchodilatory action.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5114","text":"Administration of which an​esthetic agent is associa‍ted with a rise in the in​traocular pressure?","raw_text":"Administration of which anesthetic agent is associated with a rise in the intraocular pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl​urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ketamine</strong></p><p>A <strong>rise in intraocular pressure</strong> is seen with <strong>ketamine</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hence, ketamine may be <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with an open eye injury or other ophthalmologic disorder, in which an increase in intraocular pressure would be detrimental.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5117","text":"Which intravenous anesthet​ic preserves airway refle‍xes the best?","raw_text":"Which intravenous anesthetic preserves airway reflexes the best?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ketamine</strong></p><p>Airway reflexes are <strong>best</strong> <strong>preserved</strong> with <strong>ketamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Corneal, cough and swallow reflexes all may be present, but they may not be protective. So, patients at increased <strong>risk</strong> for <strong>aspiration pneumonia</strong> (“full stomachs”) should be intubated during ketamine general anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Propofol</strong> is associated with <strong>maximum inhibition</strong> of airway reflexes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5106","text":"During recovery from anest​hesia, a patient started ‍experiencing hallucinatio​ns and vivid dreams. Whic‌h inducing agent is most ‍likely responsible?","raw_text":"During recovery from anesthesia, a patient started experiencing hallucinations and vivid dreams. Which inducing agent is most likely responsible?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ketamine</strong></p><p><strong>Hallucinations</strong> and <strong>vivid dreams</strong> during recovery from anesthesia are a manifestation of an <strong>emergence reaction</strong> following the administration of <strong>ketamine.</strong></p>\n<p>The incidence of psychic emergence reactions ranges from 3% to 100% and is usually associated with excitement, confusion, euphoria, and fear. The symptoms usually appear in the first hour of emergence and subside within several hours. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5111","text":"What drug can be used to p​revent hallucinations and‍ vivid dreams that can oc​cur as an emergence react‌ion during the anesthesia‍ recovery period?","raw_text":"What drug can be used to prevent hallucinations and vivid dreams that can occur as an emergence reaction during the anesthesia recovery period?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atro​pine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mida​zolam","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Midazolam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Midazolam</strong></p><p>Hallucinations and vivid dreams during recovery from anesthesia are a manifestation of <strong>e</strong><strong>mergence reaction </strong>following <strong>k</strong><strong>etamine </strong>administration.</p>\n<p><strong>Benzodiazepines </strong>like <strong>midazolam</strong> are the most effective group of drugs in the <strong>prevention</strong> of emergence reactions.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5120","text":"In which of the following ​patients can you safely a‍dminister ketamine? 30-ye​ar-old woman with schizop‌hrenia 15-year-old boy wi‍th bronchial asthma 45-ye​ar-old woman with aortic ​aneurysm 55-year-old man ​with ischemic heart disea​se 20-year-old woman in h‌ypovolemic shock","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following patients can you safely administer ketamine?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>30-year-old woman with schizophrenia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">15-year-old boy with bronchial asthma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">45-year-old woman with aortic aneurysm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">55-year-old man with ischemic heart disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">20-year-old woman in hypovolemic shock</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp;​ 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 &amp;am​p; 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 &amp;amp;​ 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;amp;​ 5 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 & 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 &amp; 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> is the intravenous induction agent of choice in <strong>bronchial asthma </strong>and <strong>shock</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ketamine causes <strong>bronchial smooth muscle relaxation</strong> and preserves upper airway reflexes.</p>\n<p>Ketamine <strong>increases</strong> heart rate, arterial pressure, and<strong> cardiac output</strong> by stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system and inhibition of the reuptake of norepinephrine. It is the only anesthetic that increases peripheral arteriolar resistance and thus <strong>reduces redistribution of hypothermia</strong> in hypovolemic patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> of ketamine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Raised intracranial pressure</li>\n<li>Open globe injury (raises intraocular pressure)</li>\n<li>Vascular aneurysm surgery (possible sudden change in arterial blood pressure)</li>\n<li>Psychiatric diseases - schizophrenia</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism (exaggerated elevations in blood pressure, heart rate)</li>\n<li>Ischemic heart disease (increase in myocardial oxygen consumption)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6380","text":"A 36-year-old housewife is​ scheduled for an excisio‍n biopsy of a breast lump​ under general anaesthesi‌a. The procedure is plann‍ed on an outpatient basis​. Which is the intravenou​s induction agent of choi​ce for her?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old housewife is scheduled for an excision biopsy of a breast lump under general anaesthesia. The procedure is planned on an outpatient basis. Which is the intravenous induction agent of choice for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Propofol</strong></p><p>The intravenous induction agent of choice for <strong>ambulatory</strong><strong> </strong>anesthesia is <strong>propofol.</strong></p>\n<p>Propofol has<strong> </strong>a<strong> rapid onset </strong>of action and a<strong> faster recovery.</strong> This makes it an ideal induction agent for outpatient/ambulatory/day care anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Besides, propofol has <strong>antiemetic </strong>properties which help reduce post-operative nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6383","text":"Which is the muscle relaxa​nt of choice in patients ‍posted for daycare surger​y?","raw_text":"Which is the muscle relaxant of choice in patients posted for daycare surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny​lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rocur​onium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur​onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancu​ronium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rocuronium</strong></p><p><strong>Rocuronium</strong> is the muscle relaxant of choice for <strong>daycare surgery.</strong> </p>\n<p>Rocuronium is an intermediate-acting muscle relaxant with a rapid onset (90 secs).</p>\n<p>Its actions can be terminated by the <strong>reversal</strong> agent <strong>sugammadex,</strong> which produces a rapid and complete reversal of neuromuscular blockade.</p>\n<p>Note: Succinylcholine is the shortest acting muscle relaxant, but it has a high incidence of <strong>postoperative myalgia</strong>. So, its use in day care anesthesia is discouraged.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6387","text":"A 45-year-old man is poste​d for inguinal hernia sur‍gery. Which is the anesth​etic technique of choice ‌in order to perform fast-‍track surgery on him?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man is posted for inguinal hernia surgery. Which is the anesthetic technique of choice in order to perform fast-track surgery on him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a​nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a​nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural ​anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infiltratio​n anesthesia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Infiltration anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Infiltration anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>Infiltration anesthesia </strong>is given for fast-track surgery. </p>\n<p>Ambulatory/outpatient anesthesia employs the use of <strong>fast-track surgical techniques </strong>to provide adequate <strong>surgical anesthesia</strong> and <strong>minimize</strong> postoperative <strong>complications</strong> in order to reduce the duration of hospital stay.</p>\n<p>Infiltration anesthesia <strong>(inguinal field block)</strong> is ideal to perform inguinal hernia repair because it has a <strong>lesser</strong> incidence of <strong>complications</strong> than regional or general anesthesia.</p>\n<p>The disadvantage of infiltration anesthesia is its short duration of anesthesia even when long-acting local anesthetics like bupivacaine are used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6384","text":"Which effect of ambulatory​ anesthesia most commonly‍ persists after the patie​nt is discharged?","raw_text":"Which effect of ambulatory anesthesia most commonly persists after the patient is discharged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mya​lgia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Drows​iness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sore ​throat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Head​ache","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Drowsiness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Drowsiness</strong></p><p><strong>Drowsiness</strong> is the most common adverse effect that persists after discharge following an <strong>ambulatory</strong> or <strong>daycare</strong> anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects after ambulatory anesthesia in their decreasing order of incidence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Drowsiness</li>\n<li>Sore-throat</li>\n<li>Myalgia</li>\n<li>Headache, dizziness</li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting</li>\n<li>Cardiovascular - hypotension, hypertension, arrhythmias, cardiac arrest (rare)</li>\n<li>Respiratory - hypoxemia, laryngospasm, bronchospasm, aspiration, pulmonary edema, pneumothorax (rare)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5112","text":"Which of the following int​ravenous anesthetics can ‍be used to reduce postope​rative pain?","raw_text":"Which of the following intravenous anesthetics can be used to reduce postoperative pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketamine</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine </strong>can be used to reduce postoperative pain as it produces profound<strong> analgesia.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>inhibits</strong> nociceptive central hypersensitization.</p>\n<p>Besides, ketamine may be effective in the treatment of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>cancer pain, visceral pain</li>\n<li>migraine, chronic neuropathic pain</li>\n<li>phantom limb pain</li>\n<li>fibromyalgia, complex regional pain syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5099","text":"Which of the following int​ravenous anesthetics is h‍ypertensive?","raw_text":"Which of the following intravenous anesthetics is hypertensive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop​entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta​mine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etom​idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prop​ofol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ketamine</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> causes <strong>hypertension</strong> due to <strong>central stimulation</strong> of the <strong>sympathetic</strong> nervous <strong>system</strong> and inhibition of the reuptake of norepinephrine. Hence, it is useful in patients in<strong> shock</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thiopentone, propofol, and etomidate produce a fall in arterial blood pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5121","text":"Which class of compounds d​oes ketamine belong to?","raw_text":"Which class of compounds does ketamine belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barbit​urates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phencyc​lidines","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Butyrop​henones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phencyclidines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phencyclidines</strong></p><p>Ketamine is an arylcyclohexylamine, a congener of <strong>phencyclidine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ketamine produces <strong>dissociative anaesthesia</strong> through <strong>antagonistic</strong> action at the <strong>phencyclidine </strong>site of the <strong>NMDA </strong>receptor.</p>\n<p>Ketamine, like other phencyclidines, produces emergence reactions which are undesirable psychological reactions, which occur during awakening from anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BrckAlP3","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"17_cranial_nerves","title":"17.Cranial Nerves","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MD0469","text":"Which of the following cra‌nial nerves is a pure sen‍sory nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerves is a pure sensory nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olfa‌ctory","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Troc‌hlear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trige‌minal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossoph‌aryngeal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Olfactory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Olfactory</strong></p><p>The <strong>olfactory</strong> nerve is a <strong>pure sensory nerve</strong>. It carries no motor fibers.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 219px;\" width=\"562\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pure sensory</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pure motor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mixed-sensory and motor </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Olfactory (1)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Oculomotor (3)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Trigeminal (5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Optic (2)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Trochlear (4)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Facial (7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Vestibulocochlear (8)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Abducent (6)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Glossopharyngeal (9)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Spinal Accessory (11)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Vagus (10)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Hypoglossal (12)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Mnemonic: <strong>S</strong>ome <strong>S</strong>ay <strong>M</strong>oney <strong>M</strong>atters <strong>B</strong>ut <strong>M</strong>y <strong>B</strong>rother <strong>S</strong>ays <strong>B</strong>ig <strong>B</strong>rains <strong>M</strong>atter <strong>M</strong>ore.</p>\n<p><strong>S - </strong>Purely <strong>sensory </strong></p>\n<p><strong>M - </strong>Purely <strong>motor </strong></p>\n<p><strong>B - </strong><strong>Both </strong>sensory and motor</p>\n<p>The placement of the words in the mnemonic corresponds to the cranial nerve number i.e; \"<strong>S</strong>ome\" corresponds to the 1st cranial nerve (olfactory), \"<strong>S</strong>ay\" corresponds to the 2nd cranial nerve (optic), and so on.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0300","text":"Which of the following bra‌instem nuclei is not deri‍ved from the alar plate?","raw_text":"Which of the following brainstem nuclei is not derived from the alar plate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior oli‌vary nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypogloss‌al nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dentate‌ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesencepha‌lic nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoglossal nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a84fa3444ff6486d94342b2eb51cb886x1280x2893.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/772fc086a48a4d04860a5901181b474ex1280x1158.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoglossal nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>hypoglossal nucleus</strong> is <strong>not derived</strong> from the <strong>alar plate. </strong><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">The <strong>hypoglossal nucleus</strong> </span></span>is a <strong>motor nucleus</strong> derived from the <strong>basal plate.</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">Embryologically, the continuous addition of neuroblasts to the mantle layer of the neural tube produces thickenings ventrally and dorsally - producing the basal and alar plates respectively. A longitudinal groove, called <strong>sulcus limitans</strong>, marks the boundary between the two.</span></span></p>\n<table style=\"height: 182px;\" width=\"584\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 284px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Basal Plate </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 284px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Alar plate </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 284px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\"><strong>Ventral</strong> thickening</span></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 284px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\"><strong>Dorsal</strong> thickenings</span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 284px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">Give rise to <strong>motor nuclei- </strong>general visceral efferent nuclei, special visceral efferent of CN IX, X, and XII; and somatic efferent of XII</span></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 284px;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">Give rise to <strong>sensory nuclei </strong>CN V, VII, VIII, IX, X, inferior olivary nucleus, and mesencephalic nucleus of CN V</span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 284px;\">Forms the motor part of the spinal cord (ventral horns)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 284px;\">\n<p>Forms the sensory part of the spinal cord (dorsal horns). </p>\n<p>The rhombic lip develops into the cerebellum and its nuclei. </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0830","text":"The arrangement of the fun‌ctional columns of crania‍l nerve nuclei from ventr‌al to dorsal aspect in th‌e brainstem is as follows‌:","raw_text":"The arrangement of the functional columns of cranial nerve nuclei from ventral to dorsal aspect in the brainstem is as follows:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Visceral Efferent &amp;gt; Som‌atic Efferent &amp;gt; Viscer‍al Afferent &amp;gt; Somatic ‌Afferent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somatic Efferent &amp;gt; Visc‌eral Efferent &amp;gt; Viscer‍al Afferent &amp;gt; Somatic ‌Afferent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Somatic Afferent &amp;gt; Visc‌eral Afferent &amp;gt; Viscer‍al Efferent &amp;gt; Somatic ‌Efferent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Visceral Efferent &amp;gt; Vis‌ceral Afferent &amp;gt; Somat‍ic Afferent &amp;gt; Somatic ‌Efferent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Somatic Efferent > Visceral Efferent > Visceral Afferent > Somatic Afferent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9526c8b326054ce28a1f27d83f1c8186x1280x2893.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87e33528b0a546aeb36566ab6b6a7a2ex1280x1158.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Somatic Efferent &gt; Visceral Efferent &gt; Visceral Afferent &gt; Somatic Afferent</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">The pattern of the arrangement of </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">cranial nerve nuclei from the ventral to dorsal aspect is:</span></span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">Somatic Efferent &gt; Visceral E</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">fferent &gt; Visceral</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\"> A</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">fferent &gt; Somatic Afferent</span></span></strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX32195820\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX32195820\">The basal and alar cranial nerve nuclei of the brainstem are organized into <strong>7 columns</strong>. The sequence of the columns from <strong>ventral to dorsal</strong> is shown in the image below - <strong>3 motor columns</strong> in the ventral basal plate (green) and <strong>4 sensory columns</strong> in the dorsal alar plate (blue). The v</span></span>isceral nuclei are closer to the sulcus limitans than the somatic nuclei.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0303","text":"Which of the following cra‌nial nerves does not cont‍ain neurons from the gene‌ral somatic efferent colu‌mn?","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerves does not contain neurons from the general somatic efferent column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"III ‌nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV n‌erve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"VI n‌erve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VII ‌nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. VII nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b38d0221314e44b6b51cb284ae9fdaf4x1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. VII nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>VII nerve</strong> (facial) does not contain neurons from the somatic efferent column. </p>\n<p>The <strong>general somatic efferent</strong> (GSE) is the <strong>most ventral column</strong> in the basal plate. It is represented by the oculomotor<strong> (III)</strong><strong>,</strong> trochlear<strong> (IV),</strong> abducens<strong> (VI),</strong> and hypoglossal <strong>(XII)</strong> nuclei.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> facial (VII) nerve</strong> contains neurons from: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Special visceral efferent</strong> (SVE) column - supplies <strong>facial muscles</strong> derived from the second pharyngeal arch.</li>\n<li><strong>General visceral efferent</strong> (GVE) column - from the <strong>superior salivatory nucleus</strong>, is parasympathetic to the <strong>lacrimal</strong> gland,<strong> s</strong><strong>ubmandibular</strong> and<strong> sublingual</strong> salivary glands. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0312","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the speci‍al visceral efferent?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the special visceral efferent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Motor nucleu‌s of V nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Motor nucleus of VII nerve‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dorsal nucle‌us of X nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleus ‌ambiguus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dorsal nucleus of X nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45dcb4faa0f64c1f939a12ae08c2ebe6x1280x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dorsal nucleus of X nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>special visceral efferent</strong> column does <strong>not</strong> include the <strong>dorsal nucleus</strong> of the <strong>vagus.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>special visceral efferent</strong> column contains neurons innervating <strong>muscles developed</strong> from the<strong> pharyngeal arches:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Motor nucleus of V nerve - </strong>muscles of mastication</li>\n<li><strong>Motor nucleus of VII nerve - </strong>muscles of facial expression</li>\n<li><strong>Nucleus ambiguus</strong> (<strong>IX, X, XI</strong>) - stylopharyngeus, pharyngeal, external laryngeal, oesophageal striated muscles, muscles of the soft palate, sternocleidomastoid, and trapezius.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>dorsal nucleus of vagus</strong> contains neurons from the <strong>general visceral efferent column</strong> and it sends <strong>parasympathetic fibers</strong> to the glands and smooth muscles in the pharynx, larynx, thoracic and abdominal viscera. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0832","text":"Which of the following nuc‌lei do not belong to the ‍general visceral efferent‌ column?","raw_text":"Which of the following nuclei do not belong to the general visceral efferent column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal nucl‌eus of vagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Edinger-West‌phal nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus ‌ambiguus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior salivatory nucleu‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nucleus ambiguus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/254534ef9e834e29b59c4a0cb343147ex1280x1158.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nucleus ambiguus</strong></p><p><strong>Nucleus ambiguus</strong> does <strong>not</strong> belong to the general visceral efferent <strong>(GVE)</strong> column.</p>\n<p>The <strong>general visceral efferent</strong> columns contain nuclei of <strong>parasympathetic cranial nerves</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III -</strong> Edinger-Westphal nucleus</li>\n<li><strong>VII -</strong> Superior salivatory nucleus</li>\n<li><strong>IX -</strong> Inferior salivatory nucleus</li>\n<li><strong>X -</strong> Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Nucleus ambiguus</strong> belongs to the <strong>special visceral efferent</strong> (SVE)<strong> column</strong>.</p>\n<p>The special visceral efferent column contains neurons innervating <strong>muscles developed from the pharyngeal arches</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Motor nucleus of V nerve- </strong>muscles of mastication</li>\n<li><strong>Motor nucleus of VII nerve- </strong>muscles of facial expression</li>\n<li><strong>Nucleus ambiguus</strong> (<strong>IX, X, XI</strong>)- stylopharyngeus, pharyngeal, external laryngeal, oesophageal striated muscles, muscles of the soft palate, sternocleidomastoid, and trapezius.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0833","text":"Which cranial nerve nuclei‌ are present in both the ‍general somatic efferent ‌and general visceral effe‌rent column?","raw_text":"Which cranial nerve nuclei are present in both the general somatic efferent and general visceral efferent column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oculomotor nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oculomotor nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Oculomotor nerve</strong> nuclei are present in both <strong>general somatic efferent</strong> (GSE) and <strong>general visceral efferent</strong> (GVE) columns in the brainstem.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4528","text":"Which of the following cra‌nial nerve nuclei is not ‍a part of the special vis‌ceral afferent (SVA) colu‌mn?","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerve nuclei is not a part of the special visceral afferent (SVA) column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trochlear nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e4dcf4b9d124095be6b7ff1886acda8x1280x1232.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trochlear nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>trochlear nerve nucleus</strong> is a part of the general somatic efferent<strong> (GSE)</strong> column, not the special visceral afferent column.</p>\n<p>The <strong>trochlear </strong>nerve is a<strong> motor nerve</strong> that innervates <strong>superior oblique</strong> muscle in the orbit.</p>\n<p>The neurons of the general somatic efferent<strong> (GSE)</strong> column innervate the extrinsic ocular muscles and muscles of the tongue<strong> </strong>(<strong>III, IV, VI, and XII</strong> cranial nerves).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7140","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves is not involved in ol‍faction?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is not involved in olfaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoglos‌sal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypoglossal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypoglossal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>hypoglossal</strong> nerve is <strong>purely motor</strong> cranial nerve and is not involved in olfaction.</p>\n<p>The other three cranial nerves—trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves are mixed cranial nerves and contribute to olfaction.</p>\n<p>Vagal nerve stimulation modifies the electrical activity of the olfactory bulb.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0834","text":"A 72-year-old patient who ‌suffered a stroke is unab‍le to swallow food. On ex‌amination, the gag reflex‌ is absent. The motor nuc‌leus of the vagus nerve i‌nvolved here emerges from​ _______ of the brainstem​.","raw_text":"A 72-year-old patient who suffered a stroke is unable to swallow food.  On examination, the gag reflex is absent. The motor nucleus of the vagus nerve involved here emerges from _______ of the brainstem.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postero-lat‌eral sulcus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterolate‌ral sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body o‌f pons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At the ponto-medullary jun‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Postero-lateral sulcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Postero-lateral sulcus</strong></p><p>The fibers from the <strong>nucleus ambiguus</strong> emerge through the <strong>postero-lateral sulcus </strong>of the brainstem.  </p>\n<p><strong>Nucleus ambiguus</strong> is made of cranial nerves<strong> IX, X, and XI</strong>. </p>\n<p>The following table describes the components of the vagal nerve and the associated nuclei. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 240px;\" width=\"511\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Component </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Associated Nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Innervated Structures </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Parasympathetic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Dorsal vagal nucleus </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Autonomic ganglia of thorax and abdomen </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Motor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Nucleus ambiguus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Pharynx, larynx, palate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Viscero-sensory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Nucleus tractus solitarius </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Pharynx, larynx, esophagus, thoracic and abdominal viscera </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Chemosensory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Nucleus tractus solitarius </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Taste buds on epiglottis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Somatosensory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px; text-align: left;\">Spinal nucleus of trigeminal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Outer ear canal, pinna, dura </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2962","text":"A 69-year-old male present‌ed with diplopia. Investi‍gations revealed an aneur‌ysm of the right anterior‌ inferior cerebellar arte‌ry that is directly impin‌ging on the nerve arising​ at the pontomedullary ju​nction. Which of the foll​owing extraocular muscles‍ is likely to be paralyze​d?","raw_text":"A 69-year-old male presented with diplopia. Investigations revealed an aneurysm of the right anterior inferior cerebellar artery that is directly impinging on the nerve arising at the pontomedullary junction. Which of the following extraocular muscles is likely to be paralyzed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior‌ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior‌ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial‌ rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral‌ rectus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral rectus</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral rectus</strong> muscle is likely to be paralyzed as it is innervated by the <strong>VI cranial nerve,</strong> which <strong>emerges</strong> from the <strong>pontomedullary junction</strong> (PMJ).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Cranial nerves VI, VII, and VIII arise at the PMJ.</p>\n<p>Extraocular muscles are innervated by the III, IV, and VI cranial nerves. The <strong>superior oblique</strong> is innervated by<strong> </strong>CN<strong> IV</strong>, the <strong>lateral rectus</strong> by CN <strong>VI</strong>, and all the <strong>other</strong> muscles by CN <strong>III</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0467","text":"A 34-year-old presents to ‌the casualty with a histo‍ry of blunt force trauma ‌to the head. A small lesi‌on was found on the dorsa‌l aspect of the brainstem‌ on imaging. Which of the​ following nerves could b​e injured at the site?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old presents to the casualty with a history of blunt force trauma to the head. A small lesion was found on the dorsal aspect of the brainstem on imaging. Which of the following nerves could be injured at the site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoglos‌sal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trochlear nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trochlear nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>trochlear nerve</strong> is the only nerve that exits the<strong> brainstem</strong> on the<strong> dorsal</strong> surface<strong> </strong>and thus can be injured in this patient. </p>\n<p>All other cranial nerves exit the brainstem from the ventral surface.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2971","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves exits anterior to the‍ marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves exits anterior to the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glossoph‌aryngeal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Va‌gus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acessor‌y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoglos‌sal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypoglossal nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a48240eda6724dbf838d00e3a7431578.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e53a8a4a094f49ed924f498f3ee9d12ex1280x1223.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c08a37d18db48468e9156b0055696a6x250x250.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypoglossal nerve</strong></p><p>The structure marked is the <strong>olive.</strong> The<strong> hypoglossal nerve </strong>emerges anterior to the olive, from the groove between the pyramid and the olive—<strong>anterolateral sulcus.</strong></p>\n<p>The olivary bodies are rounded, oval prominences on the <strong>surface</strong> of the <strong>medulla oblongata</strong> in the brain. Nerve fibers in the <strong>olivopontocerebellar</strong> pathway connect the <strong>olives</strong> to the <strong>pons</strong> and <strong>cerebellum.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0468","text":"Which is the thickest cran‌ial nerve?","raw_text":"Which is the thickest cranial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trige‌minal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fac‌ial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Troc‌hlear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Va‌gus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trigeminal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trigeminal</strong></p><p>The <strong>trigeminal </strong>nerve is the <strong>thickest cranial nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>trochlear</strong> nerve is the <strong>most slender</strong> cranial nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0470","text":"Which of the following cra‌nial nerves does not carr‍y parasympathetic fibers?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerves does not carry parasympathetic fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Troc‌hlear","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fac‌ial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oculo‌motor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossoph‌aryngeal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trochlear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trochlear</strong></p><p>The <strong>trochlear</strong> nerve does <strong>not</strong> carry <strong>parasympathetic</strong> fibers. </p>\n<p>The cranial nerves carrying parasympathetic fibers are listed below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 238px; width: 418px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cranial nerve</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 257px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Associated Ganglion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\">Oculomotor <strong>(III)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 257px; text-align: left;\">Ciliary ganglion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\">Facial <strong>(VII)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 257px; text-align: left;\">Pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\">Glossopharyngeal <strong>(IX)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 257px; text-align: left;\">Otic ganglion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\">Vagus <strong>(X)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 257px; text-align: left;\">Visceral ganglions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5279","text":"Which of the following sen‌sations is not perceived ‍by the spinal nucleus of ‌the trigeminal nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following sensations is not perceived by the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tempe‌rature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pa‌in","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To‌uch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proprio‌ception","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proprioception","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a100978d0bc48c5baab7edfc7cb67ebx600x797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proprioception</strong></p><p><strong>Proprioception </strong>is perceived by the <strong>mesencephalic nucleus</strong> of the trigeminal nerve, not the spinal nucleus.</p>\n<p>The <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong> consists of <strong>4 nuclei</strong>: 3 sensory (Mesencephalic, spinal, and principal sensory nucleus) and 1 motor. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 113px;\" width=\"518\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Function</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\"><strong>Mesencephalic Nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">Composed of a column of <strong>unipolar nerve</strong> cells situated in the midbrain and extends inferiorly into the pons as far as the main sensory nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">Receives the sensation of <strong>proprioception</strong> from the muscles of mastication and from the facial and extraocular muscles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\"><strong>Principal sensory nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">Posterior part of the pons lateral to the motor nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">\n<p>Receives the sensations of <strong>touch and pressure</strong> from the face</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\"><strong>Spinal nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">Continuous superiorly with the main sensory nucleus and extends inferiorly the whole of the medulla oblongata <strong>till the 2<sup>nd</sup> Cervical Segment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">Receives the sensation of <strong>pain and temperature</strong> from the face and some fibers of <strong>touch</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\"><strong>Motor nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">The pons medial to the main sensory nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.333px;\">Supplies the muscles of mastication, the tensor tympani, the tensor veli palitini and the mylohyoid and the anterior belly of digastric muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0835","text":"A patient with trigeminal ‌neuralgia presents with a‍ flare-up of the conditio‌n. The nucleus receiving ‌the sensory afferent exte‌nds from the chief sensor‌y nucleus in the pons up ​to the _______.","raw_text":"A patient with trigeminal neuralgia presents with a flare-up of the condition. The nucleus receiving the sensory afferent extends from the chief sensory nucleus in the pons up to the  _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower end ‌of medulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd cervical spinal segmen‌t","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5th cervical spinal segmen‌t","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper end ‌of medulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2nd cervical spinal segment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/091deed963444a4bad48101422f201d2x600x797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2nd cervical spinal segment</strong></p><p>Excruciating <strong>pain</strong> is the principal symptom of <strong>trigeminal neuralgia. </strong>The afferent fibers carrying pain and temperature from the face relay in the <strong>spinal nucleus</strong> of the trigeminal nerve. It extends from the <strong>chief sensory nucleus</strong> in the <strong>upper</strong> part of the<strong> pons</strong> through the <strong>medulla</strong> and into the<strong> upper 2 segments</strong> of the<strong> spinal cord.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong> consists of <strong>4 nuclei</strong>: 3 sensory (mesencephalic, spinal, and principal sensory nucleus) and 1 motor.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 408px; width: 603px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 227px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Function</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mesencephalic nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Composed of a column of <strong>unipolar nerve</strong> cells situated in the midbrain and extends inferiorly into the pons as far as the main sensory nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Receives the sensation of <strong>proprioception</strong> from the muscles of mastication and from the facial and extraocular muscles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Principal sensory nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Posterior part of the pons lateral to the motor nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">\n<p>Receives the sensations of <strong>touch and pressure</strong> from the face</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Spinal nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Continuous superiorly with the main sensory nucleus and extends inferiorly the whole of the medulla oblongata <strong>till the 2<sup>nd</sup> cervical segment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Receives the sensation of <strong>pain and temperature</strong> from the face and some fibers of <strong>touch</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Motor nucleus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">The pons medial to the main sensory nucleus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227px;\">Supplies the muscles of mastication, tensor tympani, tensor veli palitini, mylohyoid, and anterior belly of digastric muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0838","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the ner‍ve involved in the palsy ‌shown below is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the nerve involved in the palsy shown below is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It divides into upper and ‌lower divisions in the an‍terior part of the cavern‌ous sinus.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Its divisions enter the or‌bit through the middle pa‍rt of the superior orbita‌l fissure.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It lies between the poster‌ior cerebral and superior‍ cerebellar arteries in t‌he interpeduncular fossa.‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The nerve to inferior rect‌us carries parasympatheti‍c fibers to the ciliary g‌anglion.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The nerve to inferior rectus carries parasympathetic fibers to the ciliary ganglion.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d20209313e294d3dbcd6dff594167e5e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebedf245e4bb4515b8044c6d445c2d4fx1280x1548.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The nerve to inferior rectus carries parasympathetic fibers to the ciliary ganglion.</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>oculomotor nerve palsy.</strong> The <strong>nerve to</strong><strong> inferior oblique</strong> carries <strong>parasympathetic fibers</strong> to the <strong>ciliary ganglion, </strong>not the nerve to inferior rectus.</p>\n<p>Course of the oculomotor nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The nerve lies on the <strong>anterior</strong> surface of the <strong>midbrain</strong> in between the posterior cerebral artery and the superior cerebellar artery in the <strong>interpeduncular fossa. </strong></li>\n<li>It then passes to the middle cranial fossa along the <strong>lateral dural wall</strong> of the <strong>cavernous sinus</strong> and divides into superior and inferior in its anterior part.</li>\n<li>It enters the <strong>superior orbital fissure</strong> through the <strong>common tendinous ring.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0839","text":"A 36-year-old male was bro‌ught to the emergency dep‍artment after suffering a‌ head injury in a road tr‌affic accident. CT reveal‌ed a skull base fracture.‌ The fracture line extend​ed along the apex of the ​petrous bone involving th​e Dorello's canal. Which ‍of the following cranial ​nerves is susceptible to ‍injury?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old male was brought to the emergency department after suffering a head injury in a road traffic accident. CT revealed a skull base fracture. The fracture line extended along the apex of the petrous bone involving the Dorello's canal. Which of the following cranial nerves is susceptible to injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vestibuloco‌chlear nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abducen‌s nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abducens nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/538cabf3eff742f281778a094b319e9bx600x545.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abducens nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>abducens nerve</strong> forms the <strong>content</strong> of <strong>Dorello's canal </strong>and hence is susceptible to injury in this case.</p>\n<p>Dorello's canal is located<strong> </strong>at the <strong>petrous apex</strong> of the temporal bone and is formed by the petrosphenoidal/petroclinoid/<strong>Gruber's ligament. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3060","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used for the identifi‍cation of the facial nerv‌e trunk?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used for the identification of the facial nerve trunk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrograde dissection from‌ distal branch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tragal ‌pointer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior belly of omohyoid‌ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior belly of digastr‌ic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/783b36b3a98e4dcbae0580e393789a1bx1280x4078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle</strong></p><p>The inferior belly of the omohyoid muscle is not used as an anatomical landmark for the identification of the facial nerve trunk.</p>\n<p>There are two methods for <strong>localization</strong> of the<strong> facial</strong> nerve<strong> trunk: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. Anterograde</strong> method:<strong> </strong>The facial nerve trunk is identified based on the anatomical landmarks after it exits from the stylomastoid foramen. This includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tragal pointer/Conley's pointer - </strong>It is the inferior portion of the cartilaginous canal. The facial nerve trunk lies 1 cm deep and inferior to its tip.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior belly of digastric -</strong> The facial nerve lies superior to the upper border of the muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Squamotympanic fissure - </strong>It is a fissure in the temporal bone, which runs from TMJ to the tympanic cavity. The facial nerve runs through this fissure.</li>\n<li><strong>Styloid process -</strong> The facial nerve lies superficial to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Mastoid process - </strong>Upon drilling it, the nerve is identified proximally.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Retrograde</strong> method:<strong> </strong>The main branches of the facial nerve trunk are identified and are then traced proximally until the main tunk is identified. Some branches used for tracing the facial nerve trunk include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Buccal branch -</strong> It lies in relation to the parotid gland.</li>\n<li><strong>Marginal mandibular branch -</strong> It lies in relation to the facial vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6656","text":"All of the following nerve‌s are involved in the mov‍ement of the eyeball exce‌pt:","raw_text":"All of the following nerves are involved in the movement of the eyeball except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ea0200827e9c4e618a3ba5150e69237b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f088d168c784c968d1ca284bf6ccbf8x1280x1687.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>The nerve marked as 'A' is the optic nerve (II CN), and is not involved in the movement of the eyeball.</p>\n<p>The nerves marked in the image are:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Optic nerve (<strong>II</strong> CN)</li>\n<li>Oculomotor nerve (<strong>III</strong> CN)</li>\n<li>Abducens nerve (<strong>VI</strong> CN)</li>\n<li>Trochlear nerve (<strong>IV</strong> CN)</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>Extraocular muscles involved in the movement of the eyeball:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, inferior oblique, and levator palpebrae superioris muscles - supplied by the <strong>oculomotor (III)</strong> nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral rectus</strong> - supplied by abducens<strong> (VI) nerve. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Superior oblique</strong> - supplied by trochlear<strong> (IV) nerve.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"plFYrZeU","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"17_porphyrins_and_bile_pigments","title":"17.Porphyrins And Bile Pigments","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0672","text":"What is the porphyrin pres​ent in heme?","raw_text":"What is the porphyrin present in heme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protopo​rphyrin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uropor​phyrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Copropo​rphyrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzopo​rphyrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Protoporphyrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Protoporphyrin</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 336\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The porphyrin present in heme is<strong> protoporphyrin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Heme is <strong>ferroprotoporphyrin</strong>. Iron is present in the <strong>ferrous </strong>(Fe<sup>2+</sup>) state in heme. Porphyrins are <strong>cyclic</strong> <strong>coloured compounds</strong> formed by the linkage of<strong> four pyrrole rings</strong> through <strong>methyne </strong>or <strong>methenyl</strong> (=CH-) bridges. There are three types of porphyrins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uroporphyrin (most water-soluble).</li>\n<li>Coproporphyrin (intermediate).</li>\n<li>Protoporphyrin (least water-soluble).</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0701","text":"Which of the following is ​not a site for heme synth‍esis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a site for heme synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oste​ocyte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythroid cells of bone ma​rrow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mature er​ythrocyte","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mature erythrocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6066fe3cc6dd4a2fb2cb28da8566b7dbx1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mature erythrocyte</strong></p><p><strong>Mature erythrocyte</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a site for <strong>heme biosynthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Approximately 85% of heme synthesis occurs in <strong>erythroid precursor cells</strong> in the<strong> bone marrow</strong> and the majority of the remainder in <strong>hepatocytes. </strong>Heme biosynthesis<strong> </strong>occurs both in<strong> mitochondria </strong>and <strong>cytoplasm</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is<strong> </strong>initiated<strong> </strong>by the <strong>condensation </strong>of<strong> succinyl–CoA </strong>and<strong> glycine </strong>in a reaction catalyzed by<strong> mitochondrial ALA synthase. </strong>This initial reaction,<strong> </strong>and the last three steps in the formation of heme occur in mitochondria, whereas the intermediate steps of the biosynthetic pathway occur in the<strong> </strong>cytosol.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7669","text":"Which of the following TCA​ cycle intermediate is a ‍part of heme metabolism? ​(TCA - tricarboxylic acid‌)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following TCA cycle intermediate is a part of heme metabolism?</p>\n<p>(TCA - tricarboxylic acid)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha ket​oglutarate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fuma​rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Succin​yl CoA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mal​ate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Succinyl CoA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edbc8f1df9b5418bbd390e37a5f8dab7x1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Succinyl CoA</strong></p><div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Succinyl Co-A</strong>, an essential part for heme biosynthesis, is an intermediate of the TCA cycle.</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">The synthesis of heme begins by condensation of </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>succinyl Co-A </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">and </span><strong>glycine</strong> to<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> form </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>α-amino-β-ketoadipate, </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">which is then converted to </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>δ-aminolevulinate. </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">These steps are mediated by the enzyme </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>ALA synthase </strong>in the mitochrondria. </span></div>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Various steps involved in the synthesis of heme is illustrated in the image given below:</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0702","text":"Which step of heme synthes​is requires pyridoxal pho‍sphate? (ALA - α aminolev​ulinate)","raw_text":"<p>Which step of heme synthesis requires pyridoxal phosphate?</p>\n<p>(ALA - α aminolevulinate)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succinyl CoA to α-amino-β-​ketoadipate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"α-Amino-β-ketoadipate to A​LA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ALA to porp​hobilinogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Porphobilinogen to hydroxy​methylbilane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Succinyl CoA to α-amino-β-ketoadipate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/246c860ce00646838d126a7db1de2f8bx1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Succinyl CoA to α-amino-β-ketoadipate</strong></p><p>Conversion of <strong>succinyl CoA</strong> to <strong>α-amino-β-ketoadipate</strong> requires <strong>pyridoxal phosphate</strong>.</p>\n<p>The first step of heme synthesis is the conversion of succinyl CoA to α-amino-β-ketoadipate. This reaction is dependent on pyridoxal phosphate. α-amino-β-ketoadipate is rapidly <strong>decarboxylated </strong>to produce<strong> δ -aminolevulinate</strong>. It is catalyzed by <strong>mitochondrial ALA synthase. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Succinyl-CoA <span class=\"s1\">+ </span> glycine <span class=\"s1\">→ α-amino-β-keto</span>adipate <span class=\"s1\">+ </span> CoA-SH <span class=\"s1\">→ </span><span class=\"s1\">δ</span>-aminolevulinate <span class=\"s1\">+ </span> CO<sub><span class=\"s2\">2</span> </sub></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Various steps involved in the synthesis of heme is illustrated in the image given below:</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0706","text":"Which of the following is ​the rate-limiting enzyme ‍in the hepatic biosynthes​is of heme? (ALA - α- ami‌nolevulinate)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is the rate-limiting enzyme in the hepatic biosynthesis of heme?</p>\n<p>(ALA - α- aminolevulinate)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ALA Sy​nthase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uroporphyrinogen III synth​ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ALA deh​ydratase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferroch​elatase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ALA Synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a039fd29e264b72a94ddbcd4cf40f94x1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ALA Synthase</strong></p><p>The <strong>rate-limiting enzyme</strong> in the hepatic biosynthesis of heme is mitochondrial <strong>ALA synthase. </strong></p>\n<p>ALA synthase (ALAS) is a<strong> mitochondrial </strong>enzyme catalysing the conversion of succinyl CoA and glycine to ALA. There are two isozymes of ALA synthase. <strong>ALAS-1</strong> is expressed throughout the body. It is <strong>induced</strong> by drugs whose metabolism requires<strong> cytochromes </strong>(hemoprotein). <strong>Heme, </strong>probably acting through an <strong>aporepressor</strong> molecule, acts as a<strong> negative regulator </strong>of the synthesis of<strong> </strong>ALAS-1<strong> </strong>i.e. ALAS-1 synthesis decreases in the presence of heme. </p>\n<p><strong>ALAS-2</strong> is expressed in the <strong>erythrocyte precursor cells. </strong>It is neither induced by drugs<strong> </strong>nor is its feedback regulated by heme.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0707","text":"A 44-year-old man with a p​ast medical history of ac‍ute intermittent porphyri​a is planned for neurosur‌gery. Considering the ris‌k of potential complicati‍ons, the anaesthetist dec‌ides not to use barbitura​tes for induction. What i‌s the biochemical basis b​ehind the worsening of po‌rphyria by barbiturates?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man with a past medical history of acute intermittent porphyria is planned for neurosurgery. Considering the risk of potential complications, the anaesthetist decides not to use barbiturates for induction.  What is the biochemical basis behind the worsening of porphyria by barbiturates?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repression of ALA synthase​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"De-repression of ALA synth​ase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibiton of ferrochelatas​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. De-repression of ALA synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/920bb948896549ca8d154dc6b5f5166cx1280x1313.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. De-repression of ALA synthase</strong></p><p>The biochemical basis of <strong>worsening </strong>of porphyria by <strong>barbiturates</strong> is due to de-repression of <strong>ALA synthase. </strong>De-repression is the removal of repression of an enzyme, or the induction of a blocked enzyme.</p>\n<p>Barbiturates are metabolized by<strong> microsomal CYP450, </strong>a <strong>heme-protein oxidase</strong> system found in the liver. In patients with porphyria, barbiturates <strong>precipitate</strong> an attack of <strong>porphyria </strong>by the following mechanism:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9490","text":"A young patient came with ​complaints of acute abdom‍inal pain on and off and ​tingling sensation of lim‌bs and at times weakness ‌of limbs. She had a histo‍ry of eating paint from t‌he wall of a newly built ​house. Which of the follo‌wing enzyme defect will b​e the cause of her condit‌ion?","raw_text":"A young patient came with complaints of acute abdominal pain on and off and tingling sensation of limbs and at times weakness of limbs. She had a history of eating paint from the wall of a newly built house.  Which of the following enzyme defect will be the cause of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ALA sy​nthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ALA deh​ydratase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heme s​ynthase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coproporphyrinogen oxidase​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ALA dehydratase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b98b5f3f140340c3be0a305fa0fd4771x1280x1525.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ALA dehydratase</strong></p><p>A child with acute abdominal pain, tingling sensation and weakness of limbs with history of eating paint likely has <strong>lead poisoning.</strong> It is characterized by impaired <strong>ALA dehydratase </strong>enzyme activity.</p>\n<p>Lead is a potent<strong> inhibitor</strong> of both<strong> ALA dehydratase</strong> and <strong>ferrochelatase</strong>/<strong> </strong>heme synthase (option C). However, it has the greatest inhibitory action on ALA dehydratase. Lead poisonining thus leads to the buildup of protoporphyrin and ALA. Lead also substitutes for <strong>calcium</strong> in many fundamental cellular processes. It can cross red blood cell membranes as well as the blood-brain barrier and enter the <strong>neuroglial cells</strong> which support brain function. The <strong>fatal dose </strong>of lead is<strong> 20g </strong>of lead acetate and the <strong>fatal period </strong>is 1 to 2 days.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>ALA synthase 2 </strong>defect causes X-linked protoporphyria. <br/>Option C: <strong>Heme synthase</strong>, or ferrochelatase, defect causes erythropoeitic protoporphyria. <br/>Option D: <strong>Coproporphyrinogen oxidase </strong>defect causes hereditary corproporphyria.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0704","text":"What is the by-product of ​the ​reaction catalyzed b‍y cytosolic hydroxymethyl​bilane synthase in heme s‌ynthesis?","raw_text":"What is the by-product of the ​reaction catalyzed by cytosolic hydroxymethylbilane synthase in heme synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H​2O","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N​H3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C​O2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CoA​.SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. NH3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23fbcc01b1044a7288535162a28e7fb2x1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. NH3</strong></p><p>The by-product of the reaction catalyzed by cytosolic <strong>hydroxymethylbilane</strong> (HMB)<strong> synthase</strong> in heme synthesis is <strong>ammonia </strong>(NH<sub>3</sub>). </p>\n<p>HMB synthase is also known as<strong> porphobilinogen deaminase </strong>or <strong>uroporphyrinogen 1 synthase.</strong> It catalyses the <strong>condensation</strong> of 4 molecules of porphobilinogen to release hydroxymethylbilane and <strong>4 molecules </strong>of NH<sub>3</sub>. </p>\n<p>4 Porphobilinogen + H<sub>2</sub>O -------&gt; Hydroxymethylbilane + 4 NH<sub>3</sub></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0705","text":"Which of the following enz​ymes catalyzes the cycliz‍ation of hydroxymethylbil​ane?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes catalyzes the cyclization of hydroxymethylbilane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uroporphyrinogen I synthas​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uroporphyrinogen II syntha​se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uroporphyrinogen III synth​ase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uroporphyrinogen decarboxy​lase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4af0c95a747437691a21bb5a71efed9x1280x1732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase</strong></p><p>Cyclization of hydroxymethylbilane (HMB) is catalyzed by<strong> uroporphyrinogen III synthase</strong></p>\n<p>HMB is cyclised to <strong>uroporphyrinogen III </strong>by uroporphyrinogen III synthase. Uroporphyrinogen III is the <strong>first</strong> porphyrin precursor in heme synthesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> HMB can also <strong>cyclize spontaneously</strong> to form <strong>uroporphyrinogen I. </strong>But under normal circumstances, the porphyrinogen formed is predominantly uroporphyrinogen III. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0723","text":"Which of the following por​phyrias is not inherited ‍in an autosomal dominant ​mode?","raw_text":"Which of the following porphyrias is not inherited in an autosomal dominant mode?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy​ria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Variegate​ porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr​ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary c​oproporphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong></p><p><strong>Erythropoietic protoporphyria </strong>is <strong>not</strong> transmitted in an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> manner..</p>\n<p>Erythryopoietic protoporphyria is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease caused by a defect in<strong> </strong>the enzyme<strong> ferrochelatase </strong>(FECH).<strong> </strong>It is the <strong>most common</strong> porphyria in<strong> children</strong>, who present with <strong>n</strong><strong>on-blistering photosensitivity. </strong>An increase in the ratio of<strong> </strong>free protoporphyrin to zinc bound porphyrin in erythrocytes is the hallmark of this disease. The diagnosis is made by <strong>FECH mutation analysis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Most porphyrias are<strong> </strong>autosomal dominant <strong>except</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ALA dehydratase deficiency porphyria - autosomal recessive</li>\n<li>Congenital erythropoietic porphyria - autosomal recessive</li>\n<li>Erythropoietic protoporphyria - autosomal recessive</li>\n<li>X-linked protoporphyria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0708","text":"A 50-year-old man presents​ with a history of fatigu‍e, dark-coloured urine an​d photosensitivity. He co‌nsumes alcohol regularly.‌ On examination, hyperpig‍mentation and scarring ar‌e present on the face and​ dorsum of the hand. Lab ‌findings are given below.​ Which of the following i‌s the most likely diagnos​is? Hb: 7.4 mg/dl Periphe‌ral blood picture: Sugges​tive of microcytic hypoch‍romic anaemia Urinary por‌phobilinogen level: Norma‌l Urinary uroporphyrin le‍vel: Increased","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old man presents with a history of fatigue, dark-coloured urine and photosensitivity. He consumes alcohol regularly. On examination, hyperpigmentation and scarring are present on the face and dorsum of the hand. Lab findings are given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb: 7.4 mg/dl<br/>Peripheral blood picture: Suggestive of microcytic hypochromic anaemia<br/>Urinary porphobilinogen level: Normal<br/>Urinary uroporphyrin level: Increased</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Porphyria c​utanea tarda","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary c​oproporphyria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Variegate​ porphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Porphyria cutanea tarda","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Porphyria cutanea tarda</strong></p><p>A middle-aged adult presenting with features of hemolysis and photosensitivity with elevated urine uroporphyrin suggests a diagnosis of<strong> porphyria cutanea tarda.</strong></p>\n<p>Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) is the<strong> most common </strong>porphyria. It is caused by the deficiency of <strong>uroporphyrinogen</strong> decarboxylase, which converts uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III.  It is characterized by photosensivity, which manifests as <strong>blistering skin lesions</strong> on the back of the hands with crusting and scarring. It is associated with <strong>hemochromatosis </strong>and patients may develop <strong>chronic liver disease.</strong> There is increased excretion of <strong>uroporphyrin</strong> in the urine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0725","text":"A middle-aged female patie​nt presented with intermi‍ttent abdominal pain, nau​sea, vomiting, constipati‌on and bilateral lower li‌mb numbness for 3 days. O‍n examination, she is thi‌n and pale. Her vitals an​d lab findings are given ‌below. Which of the follo​wing is the most likely d‌iagnosis? BP: 160/90 mmHg​, Heart rate: 130 bpm Hae‌moglobin: 8.2 mg/dl Perip​heral blood picture: Norm‍al morphology Serum ferri‌tin: Elevated Urinary ami‌nolevulinic acid and porp‍hobilinogen level: Increa‍sed; urine sample turned ​dark brown on standing.","raw_text":"<p>A middle-aged female patient presented with intermittent abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, constipation and bilateral lower limb numbness for 3 days. On examination, she is thin and pale. Her vitals and lab findings are given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>BP: 160/90 mmHg, Heart rate: 130 bpm<br/>Haemoglobin: 8.2 mg/dl<br/>Peripheral blood picture: Normal morphology<br/>Serum ferritin: Elevated<br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Urinary aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen level: Increased; urine sample turned dark brown on standing.</span></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary c​oproporphyria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr​ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyria c​utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Variegate​ porphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute intermittent porphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute intermittent porphyria</strong></p><p>An adult female with intermittent abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, constipation, bilateral lower limb numbness with elevated urine ALA and porphobilinogen is most likely to have <strong>acute intermittent porphyria </strong>(AIP).</p>\n<p>AIP is a type of <strong>hepatic porphyria</strong> characterized by neurovisceral symptoms. AIP is caused by a defect in <strong>hydroxymethylbilane synthase, </strong>which converts prophobilinogen to hydroxymethylbilane. Patients present with <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong>, <strong>hypertension</strong> and <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>. There is increased plasma and urinary ALA and prophobilinogen. <strong>Darkening </strong>of<strong> urine</strong> on exposure to air also occurs. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options A and D: <strong>Hereditary coproporphyria</strong> and<strong> variegate porphyria</strong> present with both photosensitivity and neurovisceral symptoms. The porphyrin intermediates appearing in urine help differentiate the two diseases. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Porphyria cutanea tarda </strong>presents with blistering photosensitivity with vesicles and bullae formation. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0722","text":"Which of the following is ​not a hepatic porphyria?","raw_text":"Which  of the following is not a hepatic porphyria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Variegate​ porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr​ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyria c​utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy​ria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Erythropoietic protoporphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong></p><p><strong>Erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a type of <strong>hepatic porphyria. </strong></p>\n<p>Porphyrias are classified into<strong> erythropoietic</strong> and <strong>hepatic</strong> porphyrias, based on the location of accumulation of heme pathway intermediates:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Erythropoietic porphyrias:</strong>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>The overproduction of heme pathway intermediates occurs primarily in bone marrow erythroid cells.</li>\n<li>They are characterized by <strong>cutaneous symptoms</strong> and appear in early childhood.</li>\n<li>Examples: Congenital erythropoietic porphyria, <strong>erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong>, X-linked protoporphyria</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatic porphyrias: </strong>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>The overproduction of heme pathway intermediates occurs primarily in<strong> </strong>the liver.</li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Acute hepatic porphyrias</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> are characterised by <strong>neurologic symptoms</strong> and present acutely. </span>Examples: Acute intermittent porphyria, hereditary coproporphyria, variegate porphyria, ALA dehydratase deficient porphyria</li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Chronic hepatic porphyrias</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">present in adulthood with <strong>photosensitivity</strong>. Example: Porphyria cutanea tarda</span></li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0703","text":"An infant presented with d​elayed eruption of her te‍eth. An image of her teet​h are shown below. She al‌so has history of blister‌s over the face, hands an‍d feet on exposure to sun‌light since the first wee​k of birth. What is the m‌ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An  infant presented with delayed eruption of her teeth. An image of her teeth are shown below. She also has history of blisters over the face, hands and feet on exposure to sunlight since the first week of birth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ​porphyria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Porphyria c​utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy​ria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr​ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/84ee81e8c4a044d2bbf77d64997ccb45.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria</strong></p><p>An infant with delayed eruption and reddish discoloration of teeth, and blisters over sun-exposed areas suggests a diagnosis of <strong>c</strong><strong>ongenital erythropoietic porphyria</strong>. </p>\n<p>Congenital erythropoietic porphyria, also is known as <strong>Gunther's disease</strong>, is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> porphyria. There is <strong>uroporphyrinogen III synthase</strong> deficiency, leading to <strong>uroporphyrin I</strong> and <strong>coproporphyrin I</strong> accumulation in teeth, bone marrow, erythrocyte, plasma urine and feces.</p>\n<p>Infants present with <strong>port wine urine</strong> or pink stained diapers, <strong>severe cutaenous hypersensitivity,</strong> and reddish-brown teeth known as <strong>erythrodontia</strong>. When illuminated with UV light, the teeth show <strong>reddish fluorescence</strong>. It may cause non-immune hydrops in utero or shortly after birth in severe cases. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0728","text":"Which of the following por​phyria is caused by a mut‍ation of ALA synthase-2?","raw_text":"Which of the following porphyria is caused by a mutation of ALA synthase-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ​porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X- linked pr​otoporphyria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy​ria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. X- linked protoporphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. X- linked protoporphyria</strong></p><p><strong>X-linked protoporphyria </strong>is caused by a mutation of<strong> ALA synthase-2. </strong></p>\n<p>X-linked Protoporphyria is caused by a <strong>gain-of-function </strong>mutation, leading to the<strong> </strong>increased activity<strong> </strong>of ALA synthase-2. This results in <strong>increased ALA</strong><strong> synthesis</strong>. Due to the saturation of iron, <strong>protoporphyrins</strong> are not converted to heme and thus protoporphyrins <strong>accumulate</strong>.</p>\n<p>ALA synthase-2 mutation<strong> </strong>is also seen in<strong> X-linked sideroblastic anemia. </strong>However, this is not a porphyria. X-linked sideroblastic anemia is due to <strong>loss-of-function </strong>mutation, leading to<strong> </strong>decreased activity<strong> </strong>of ALA synthase-2 and <strong>decreased protoporphyrin synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0709","text":"Which of the following enz​yme is defective in varie‍gate porphyria?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme is defective in variegate porphyria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coproporphyrinogen oxidase​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protoporphyrinogen oxidase​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uroporphyrinogen III synth​ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uroporphyrinogen decarboxy​lase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Protoporphyrinogen oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Protoporphyrinogen oxidase</strong></p><p><strong>Protoporphyrinogen oxidase</strong> enzyme is defective in <strong>variegate porphyria.</strong></p>\n<p>Variegate porphyria is an autosominal dominant porphyria. <strong>Protoporphyrinogen IX</strong> accumulates in the urine. Patients present with acute attacks of<strong> abdominal pain</strong> and <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>. <strong>Cutaneous photosensitivity</strong> is also seen. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0747","text":"Carbon monoxide is release​d in which of the followi‍ng reactions?","raw_text":"Carbon monoxide is released in which of the following reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conversion of biliverdin t​o bilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conversion of heme to bili​verdin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conversion of bilirubin to​ bilirubin monoglucuronid‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conversion of bilirubin mo​noglucuronide to bilirubi‍n diglucuronide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conversion of heme to biliverdin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10986ef1a529432fafed16feee599e5ex1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conversion of heme to biliverdin</strong></p><p>The conversion of <strong>heme </strong>to <strong>biliverdin</strong> produces <strong>carbon monoxide</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Heme oxygenase</strong> catabolises <strong>heme</strong> to <strong>biliverdin</strong>. <strong>Carbon monoxide </strong>and <strong>ferric iron</strong> are released in the process. This is the only source of endogenous carbon monoxide in the body. <strong>Biliverdin reductase</strong> further converts biliverdin to bilirubin in an NADPH-dependent reaction.<strong> </strong>Iron produced from this reaction is stored as <strong>ferritin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The process of heme catabolism occurs in the<strong> microsomal </strong>fraction (endoplasmic reticulum) of <strong>reticuloendothelial cells</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>1 g of hemoglobin produces 35 mg of bilirubin. About 250-350 mg bilirubin is produced daily in the adults.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0735","text":"Which of the following is ​the site of bilirubin con‍jugation within the cell?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is the site of bilirubin conjugation within the cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyto​plasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoplasmi​c reticulum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitoch​ondria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi a​pparatus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoplasmic reticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/654595a16eb449379a8f2ec45d4666cax1280x2025.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoplasmic reticulum</strong></p><p>The <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> is the site for the <strong>conjugation</strong> of <strong>bilirubin.</strong></p>\n<p>Hepatocytes convert bilirubin to a <strong>polar form</strong> by a process called <strong>conjugation</strong>. <strong>UDP-glucuronic acid</strong> acts as a glucuronyl donor in this reaction. It initially produces <strong>bilirubin monoglucuronide. </strong>This is subsequently converted to <strong>bilirubin diglucuronide </strong>which is excreted in bile. Conjugation is catalysed by the enzyme <strong>glucuronyl transferase</strong>, which is present in the endoplasmic reticulum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0757","text":"Which of the following is ​the rate-limiting step fo‍r hepatic bilirubin metab​olism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the rate-limiting step for hepatic bilirubin metabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transport of bilirubin wit​h albumin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uptake of bilirubin by liv​er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conjugation ​of bilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secretion of conjugated bi​lirubin into the bile","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8ec7f47e417453fb9481fb1e0eea995x1280x2025.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile</strong></p><p>The <strong>secretion </strong>of <strong>conjugated bilirubin</strong> into the bile is the <strong>rate-limiting</strong> <strong>step</strong> for hepatic bilirubin metabolism.</p>\n<p>The secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile occurs by an <strong>active transport</strong> mechanism, which involves <strong>multidrug resistance-associated protein 2 </strong>(MRP-2),<strong> </strong>also called<strong> multispecific organic anion transporter </strong>(MOAT). It is located in the plasma membrane of the bile canaliculi.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0751","text":"In a patient with jaundice​ which of the following t‍ests can be used to deter​mine serum bilirubin?","raw_text":"In a patient with jaundice which of the following tests can be used to determine serum bilirubin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ehrlich​'s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Van den B​ergh test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hay's​ test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benedic​t's test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Van den Bergh test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Van den Bergh test</strong></p><p><strong>Van den Bergh test</strong> can be used to determine the <strong>serum bilirubin</strong> level in a patient with jaundice.</p>\n<p>Van den Bergh reaction is a <strong>colorimetric method</strong> to measure serum bilirubin. It is based on the formation of <strong>red-purple</strong> <strong>azodipyrroles</strong> when <strong>diazotized sulfanilic acid</strong> reacts with <strong>bilirubin</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 484px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\"><strong>Clinical condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\"><strong>Reaction with reagent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Interpretation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">Normal serum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">No reaction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">High <strong>conjugated</strong> bilirubin<br/>Eg: Obstructive jaundice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">Reacts in the absence of methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Direct</strong> positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">High <strong>unconjugated</strong> bilirubin<br/>Eg: Hemolytic anemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">Reacts in the presence of methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Indirect</strong> positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">High <strong>conjugated </strong>and<strong> unconjugated</strong> bilirubin<br/>Eg: Hepatic jaundice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">Reacts strongly both with and without methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Biphasic</strong> reaction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Ehrlich's test </strong>is used to determine urinary and fecal<strong> </strong>urobilinogen.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hay's test </strong>is used for bile salts in the urine.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Benedict's test</strong> is used to detect the presence of reducing sugars.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3516","text":"A 65 year old man was admi​tted with generalised wea‍kness, easy fatigability,​ itching and jaundice sin‌ce 1 month. On examinatio‌n, he was emaciated and h‍ad scratch marks all over‌ the body. A mass was pal​pable in the upper abdome‌n. Which of the following​ can be seen on Van den B‌ergh reaction?","raw_text":"A 65 year old man was admitted with generalised weakness, easy fatigability, itching and jaundice since 1 month. On examination, he was emaciated and had scratch marks all over the body. A mass was palpable in the upper abdomen. Which of the following can be seen on Van den Bergh reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct ​positive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Indirect​ positive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biphasic​ reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nega​tive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Direct positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Direct positive</strong></p><p>Generalised weakness, easy fatigability, itching and jaundice with a palpable upper abdominal mass is suggestive of <strong>pancreatic malignancy</strong> leading to <strong>obstructive jaundice</strong>. This gives a <strong>direct positive</strong> result on Van den Bergh reaction.</p>\n<p>In obstructive jaundice, <strong>obstruction</strong> of the <strong>biliary tree</strong> causes regurgitation of conjugated bilirubin into the blood. Patients present with itching, jaundice and<strong> pale coloured stools</strong>.</p>\n<p>Van den Bergh reaction is a <strong>colorimetric method</strong> to measure the serum bilirubin level. It is based on the formation of <strong>red azodipyrroles</strong> when <strong>diazotized sulfanilic acid</strong> reacts with <strong>bilirubin</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 484px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\"><strong>Clinical condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\"><strong>Reaction with reagent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Interpretation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">Normal serum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">No reaction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">High <strong>conjugated</strong> bilirubin<br/>eg: <strong>Obstructive jaundice</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">Reacts in the absence of methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Direct</strong> positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">High <strong>unconjugated</strong> bilirubin<br/>eg: Hemolytic anemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">Reacts in the presence of methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Indirect</strong> positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 192px;\">High <strong>conjugated </strong>and<strong> unconjugated</strong> bilirubin<br/>eg: Hepatic jaundice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 474.8958435058594px;\">Reacts strongly both with and without methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.10415649414062px;\"><strong>Biphasic</strong> reaction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div align=\"left\" dir=\"ltr\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0758","text":"An adult male patient pres​ents with yellow discolou‍ration of the sclera. The​re was a history of recur‌rent mild jaundice since ‌childhood. His lab values‍ are mentioned below. A l‌iver biopsy was performed​ which revealed normal li‌ver architecture with coa​rse granular black pigmen‌tation. The mutation of w​hich protein is involved ‌in the following conditio​n? AST: 20 U/L , ALT: 18 ‍U/L Total bilirubin : 4.2‌mg/dl, Conjugated bilirub‌in: 3.6 mg/dl Total prote‍in: 6.4 g/dL, normal A/G ‍ratio. Urine: Normal porp​hyrin levels.","raw_text":"<p>An adult male patient presents with yellow discolouration of the sclera. There was a history of recurrent mild jaundice since childhood. His lab values are mentioned below. A liver biopsy was performed which revealed normal liver architecture with coarse granular black pigmentation. The mutation of which protein is involved in the following condition?</p>\n<p>AST: 20 U/L , ALT: 18 U/L</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin : 4.2mg/dl, Conjugated bilirubin: 3.6 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Total protein: 6.4 g/dL, normal A/G ratio.</p>\n<p>Urine: Normal porphyrin levels.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"OAT​P1B1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BS​EP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MR​P-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MR​P-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MRP-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8518b44c87334f41b2aca086ef0d4abex1280x2025.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MRP-2</strong></p><p>Yellow discolouration of the sclera with coarse granular black pigmentation of the liver is suggestive of <strong>Dubin–Johnson syndrome</strong>, characterized by a mutation in the<strong> MRP-2 </strong>protein.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Dubin–Johnson syndrome is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder with predominantly<strong> conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. It is due to a defect in the <strong>active</strong> <strong>transport </strong>of <strong>bilirubin</strong> across the canalicular membrane, due to a mutation in the MRP-2 protein.<strong> </strong>It is also known as <strong>black liver jaundice</strong> due to the accumulation of a<strong> dark pigment</strong>,<strong> </strong>derived from epinephrine metabolites, in the lysosomes of <strong>centrilobular hepatocytes</strong>. <strong>Bromosulpthalein</strong> test on plasma shows 2 peaks.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>OATP1B1</strong> and<strong> OATP1B3</strong> are mutated in<strong> </strong>Rotor syndrome.<strong> </strong>It is due to defective bilirubin excretion and causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> BSEP</strong> protein is mutated in<strong> </strong>progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis (PFIC type-2) and benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis (BRIC-2).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>MRP-3</strong> is mutated in<strong> </strong>PFIC type-3.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0761","text":"A young adult presents wit​h yellowish discolouratio‍n of the skin and sclerae​ for the last 3 days. He ‌is otherwise asymptomatic‌. He had a viral fever on‍e week ago which has now ‌resolved. Investigations ​reveal unconjugated hyper‌bilirubinemia. Which of t​he following is true rega‌rding this patient's cond​ition?","raw_text":"A young adult presents with yellowish discolouration of the skin and sclerae for the last 3 days. He is otherwise asymptomatic. He had a viral fever one week ago which has now resolved. Investigations reveal unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Which of the following is true regarding this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BSEP protei​n is mutated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Active transport of biliru​bin is impaired due to MR‍P-2 mutation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promoter defect of UGT1A1 ​enzyme is the most common‍ cause","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Missense mutation of trans​ferase enzyme is the most‍ common cause","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Promoter defect of UGT1A1 enzyme is the most common cause","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04d6d42dfe35415b8315f67a12794f64x1280x2025.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Promoter defect of UGT1A1 enzyme is the most common cause</strong></p><p>A young adult with a <strong>short</strong> history of <strong>jaundice</strong> following a period of <strong>stress</strong> (viral fever) with <strong>unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong> likely has <strong>Gilbert syndrome</strong>. Gilbert syndrome most commonly occurs due to <strong>promoter defect </strong>of the<strong> UGT1A1 </strong>enzyme.</p>\n<p>Gilbert syndrome is caused by a defect in the promoter region of the gene coding for the <strong>UDP-glucuronosyl transferase </strong>(UGT1A1) enzyme. This results in a variable rate of<strong> </strong>transcription of the normal enzyme. UGT1A1 activity is <strong>reduced </strong>to <strong>10-33% </strong>of normal.<strong> </strong>It presents with periods of mild <strong>unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong> with other liver function tests being normal. The disorder is precipitated by <strong>stress</strong>,<strong> alcohol</strong> and <strong>reduced caloric intake</strong>.</p>\n<p>Liver histology shows <strong>increased lipofuscin pigment. </strong>It responds well to <strong>phenobarbitone</strong>. Gilbert's syndrome doesn't require treatment. The bilirubin levels may fluctuate over time, and patients may occasionally have jaundice, which usually resolves on its own with no ill effects.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6960","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding jaundice?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding jaundice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urine bilirubin is absent ​in hemolytic jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urine urobilinogen is incr​eased in obstructive jaun‍dice","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Criggler-Najjar syndrome p​resents with unconjugated‍ hyperbilirubinemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatocellular jaundice ca​n have normal or decrease‍d urine urobilinogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urine urobilinogen is increased in obstructive jaundice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urine urobilinogen is increased in obstructive jaundice</strong></p><p>Urine <strong>urobilinogen</strong> is <strong>not increased</strong> in <strong>obstructive jaundice.</strong></p>\n<p>Obstructive jaundice has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Absent urinary urobilinogen: </strong>In complete obstruction of the biliary tree, conjugated bilirubin cannot enter the intestine. Thus it is not metabolized by the intestinal flora and urobilinogen is not formed.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased serum direct bilirubin</strong>: Due to obstruction in the biliary tree, the bilirubin conjugates diffuse into the blood causing <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased urine bilirubin</strong>: Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and thus appears in the urine in excess amounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Urine bilirubin </strong>is absent in hemolytic jaundice because unconjugated bilirubin is not water-soluble.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Criggler-Najjar syndrome</strong>, due to absent UGT1A1 activity, presents with raised unconjugated bilirubin.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hepatocellular jaundice </strong>can have decreased urine urobilinogen if micro-obstruction occurs due to swollen, damaged hepatocytes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BMt7uMyN","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"17_arthropod_borne_infections","title":"17.Arthropod Borne Infections","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB8031","text":"Which malarial parasite ha​s the shortest incubation​ period?","raw_text":"Which malarial parasite has the shortest incubation period?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P. v​ivax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P. ma​lariae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P. o​vale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P. fal​ciparum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. P. falciparum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. P. falciparum</strong></p><p><strong><em>Plasmodium falciparum</em></strong> has the <strong>shortest</strong> incubation period of<strong> 12 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8045","text":"A patient from a hilly are​a was diagnosed with mala​ria. Which vector is most‌ likely responsible for t‌his?","raw_text":"A patient from a hilly area was diagnosed with malaria. Which vector is most likely responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles​ stephensi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anopheles ​fluviatilis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anopheles c​ulicifacies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anopheles​ sundaicus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anopheles fluviatilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anopheles fluviatilis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Anopheles fluviatilis</strong></em> is the vector for transmitting malaria in <strong>hilly areas, forests </strong>and<strong> forest fringes.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 284px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">\n<p><strong>Vector</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p><strong>Area</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">\n<p><em>Anopheles stephensi</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>Urban</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">\n<p><em>Anopheles culicifacies</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>Rural</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">\n<p><em>Anopheles fluviatilis</em></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>Hilly areas and forests</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8047","text":"Individuals with Duffy neg​ative RBCs are resistant ​to:","raw_text":"Individuals with Duffy negative RBCs are resistant to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P. v​ivax","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"P. ma​lariae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P. o​vale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P. fal​ciparum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. P. vivax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. P. vivax</strong></p><p>People with <strong>Duffy negative</strong> RBCs are <strong>resistant</strong> to <em><strong>P.vivax</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Resistance to <strong><em>P.falciparum</em></strong> is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Individuals with sickle cell trait </li>\n<li>Neonates, as fetal hemoglobin suppresses the development of <em><span class=\"s1\">P. </span><span class=\"s2\">falciparum</span></em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8049","text":"Which is the most sensitiv​e index of recent transmi​ssion of malaria?","raw_text":"Which is the most sensitive index of recent transmission of malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Splee​n rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infant par​asite rate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Parasite de​nsity index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slide posi​tivity rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infant parasite rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infant parasite rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>infant parasite rate</strong> is regarded as the<strong> most sensitive </strong>index for recent transmission of <strong>malaria</strong> in a locality.</p>\n<p>It is defined as the <strong>%</strong> of <strong>infants</strong> showing <strong>malarial parasites</strong> in their<strong> blood films</strong>. The transmission of malaria is considered absent if the infant parasite rate is zero for 3 consecutive years in a locality (even though the Anopheline vectors responsible for the previous transmission may remain).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8051","text":"In a town with a populatio​n of 1,00,000, a total of​ 50,000 slides were exami‌ned for malaria. Among th‌ese, 100 cases of malaria​ were confirmed. What is ​the annual parasite incid‌ence of this town?","raw_text":"In a town with a population of 1,00,000, a total of 50,000 slides were examined for malaria. Among these, 100 cases of malaria were confirmed. What is the annual parasite incidence of this town?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0​.1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0​.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1</strong></p><p>The annual parasite incidence of this town (<strong>API</strong>) is<strong> 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>API =  Confirmed cases in a year</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">  -----------------------------------   x 1000</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">    Population under surveillance</p>\n<p>In this scenario, API = 100 x 1000/100000 = 1</p>\n<p>API is based on the number of <strong>slide-confirmed cases</strong> that are found through active and passive surveillance. It is a measure of <strong>malaria</strong> <strong>incidence</strong> in a community and helps in determining the burden of the disease.<strong> </strong>Areas with <strong>API ≥ 2 / 1000</strong> population are classified as <strong>high-risk areas</strong> and are eligible for <strong>vector</strong> <strong>control</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The number of slides examined is used to determine the annual blood examination rate (ABER).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8053","text":"Which parameter is used to​ measure the operational ​efficiency of the Nationa‌l Vector Borne Disease Co‌ntrol Programme?","raw_text":"Which parameter is used to measure the operational efficiency of the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annual par​asite index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Slide posi​tivity rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Annual falciparum incidenc​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Annual blood examination r​ate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Annual blood examination rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Annual blood examination rate</strong></p><p>The annual blood examination rate (<strong>ABER</strong>) is used to measure the <strong>operational efficiency</strong> of the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (<strong>NVBDCP</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8055","text":"A 44-year-old woman from A​ssam presented with fever​ and chills recurring eve‌ry 48 hours. On examinati‌on, she was conscious, or​iented, and febrile. CBC,​ LFT, and RFT were normal‌. Peripheral smear reveal‌ed the following. What is​ the drug of choice?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old woman from Assam presented with fever and chills recurring every 48 hours. On examination, she was conscious, oriented, and febrile. CBC, LFT, and RFT were normal. Peripheral smear revealed the following. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Qui​nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Artesunate and sulphadoxin​e-pyrimethamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artemether and lumefantrin​e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chloroquine and pyrimetham​ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Artemether and lumefantrine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39374e8ae8ff4766ab580e0847668af7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Artemether and lumefantrine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and peripheral smear showing crescent-shaped gametocytes are suggestive of <strong>uncomplicated</strong> <strong>falciparum malaria</strong>. As this woman is from a <strong>North-Eastern state</strong>, the drug regimen of choice is <strong>ACT-AL</strong> (artemisinin combination therapy): </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artemether </strong>(20mg)</li>\n<li><strong>Lumefantrine </strong>(120mg) <strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>This regimen is followed in the North-Eastern states of India due to documented resistance to<strong> </strong>sulphadoxine-pyrimethamine in these areas. ACT-AL is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1<sup>st</sup> trimester</strong> of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Children <strong>&lt; 5 kg</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8061","text":"A 23-year-old pregnant wom​an at 11 weeks of gestati​on was diagnosed with fal‌ciparum malaria. What is ‌the drug of choice?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old pregnant woman at 11 weeks of gestation was diagnosed with falciparum malaria. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Qui​nine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Artemisinin-based combinat​ion therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlor​oquine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prima​quine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Quinine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Quinine</strong></p><p><strong>Quinine</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>falciparum malaria</strong> during <strong>1st trimester</strong> of pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The treatment of falciparum malaria during pregnancy is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1st trimester - quinine</li>\n<li><strong>2nd</strong> and <strong>3rd</strong> trimester -<strong> ACT-AL </strong>in North-Eastern states and <strong>ACT-SP </strong>in other states</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8062","text":"Which of the following pat​ients with malaria can be​ treated with primaquine?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with malaria can be treated with primaquine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 24-year-old pregnant wom​an at 30 weeks of gestati​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 10-year-old boy diagnose​d with G6PD deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 40-year-old woman recent​ly treated for a flare-up​ of SLE","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 60-year-old man who had ​a myocardial infarction 4​ months ago","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 60-year-old man who had a myocardial infarction 4 months ago","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 60-year-old man who had a myocardial infarction 4 months ago</strong></p><p>Cardiac disease is not a contraindication for the use of <strong>primaquine</strong>. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infants</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Pregnant</strong> and lactating women</li>\n<li>Patients with<strong> G6PD deficiency</strong></li>\n<li>Patients at risk of developing <strong>granulocytopenia</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Systemic diseases including active RA and SLE</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Drugs that cause hemolysis or bone marrow depression </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8069","text":"What is the dose of mefloq​uine given for chemoproph​ylaxis of malaria?","raw_text":"What is the dose of mefloquine given for chemoprophylaxis of malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 m​g/week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"200 mg​ /week","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"250 m​g/week","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"300 m​g/week","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 250 mg/week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 250 mg/week</strong></p><p><strong>Mefloquine</strong> <strong>250 mg</strong> weekly is given for chemoprophylaxis against malaria<strong> </strong>in <strong>adults.</strong> It should be administered <strong>2-3 weeks before</strong> travelling. </p>\n<p>Chemoprophylaxis is recommended for <strong>travellers</strong> from non-endemic areas and as a <strong>short term</strong> measure for <strong>soldiers</strong>, <strong>police</strong> and <strong>labour forces</strong> serving in highly endemic areas. It should be complemented by personal protection and vector control.</p>\n<p> The following drugs are used for malaria chemoprophylaxis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chloroquine -</strong> 300 mg/week</li>\n<li><strong>Proguanil -</strong> 200mg/day</li>\n<li>Mefloquine - 250mg/week</li>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline -</strong> 100mg/day</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mefloquine is contraindicated in cases with a history of convulsions, neuropsychiatric problems and cardiac conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8072","text":"What is the most important​ strategy for the control​ of malaria in endemic ar‌eas?","raw_text":"What is the most important strategy for the control of malaria in endemic areas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratification of the prob​lem","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemopro​phylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vector ​control","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Avoiding​ relapses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vector control","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vector control</strong></p><p><strong>Vector control</strong> is the most important strategy to <strong>control malaria </strong>in<strong> endemic areas</strong>. Proper measures should be taken to kill both adult mosquitoes and larvae.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8073","text":"Which of the following was​ a component of the Roll ​back malaria programme?","raw_text":"Which of the following was a component of the Roll back malaria programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IEC campaigns for communit​y awareness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insecticide treated bed ne​ts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Development of larvivorous​ fish for eradication of ​larvae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presumptive treatment of m​alaria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Insecticide treated bed nets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Insecticide treated bed nets</strong></p><p>Ensuring proper and expanded use of <strong>insecticide-treated mosquito nets </strong>was a component of the Roll Back Malaria (RBM) programme.</p>\n<p>The other components of the RBM initiative included:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Strengthening the health system to ensure better <strong>delivery</strong> of <strong>health care</strong></li>\n<li>Ensuring adequate access to basic health care and <strong>training</strong> of health workers</li>\n<li>Encouraging the development of more effective and <strong>new</strong> <strong>anti-malaria</strong> <strong>drugs</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8075","text":"Which sequence of infectio​n poses the highest risk ​of dengue hemorrhagic fev‌er?","raw_text":"Which sequence of infection poses the highest risk of dengue hemorrhagic fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DENV-1 --&amp;​gt; DENV-2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DENV-3 --&amp;​gt; DENV-2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DENV-4 --&amp;​gt; DENV-2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DENV-1 --&amp;​gt; DENV-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. DENV-1 --> DENV-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. DENV-1 --&gt; DENV-2</strong></p><p>The <strong>DENV-1 --&gt; DENV-2 </strong>sequence of infection is associated with the <strong>highest risk</strong> of dengue hemorrhagic fever (DHF).</p>\n<p><strong>Multiple </strong>infections with<strong> different serotypes </strong>of the dengue virus lead to <strong>severe</strong> forms of dengue such as <strong>DHF/DSS. </strong>The first infection probably sensitizes the patient, while the second infection with a different serotype appears to produce immunological catastrophe.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DENV-1 --&gt; DENV-2 - 500-fold risk (compared to primary infection alone)</li>\n<li>DENV-3 --&gt; DENV-2 - 150-fold risk</li>\n<li>DENV-4 --&gt; DENV-2 - 50-fold risk </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8076","text":"What is the extrinsic incu​bation period of the deng​ue virus?","raw_text":"What is the extrinsic incubation period of the dengue virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2 ​days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-6 ​days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8-10​ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"12-14​ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8-10 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8-10 days</strong></p><p> The <strong>extrinsic incubation period</strong> of the dengue virus is <strong>8-10 days. </strong>This refers to the period necessary for the <strong>development</strong> of the disease agent in the <strong>arthropod host</strong>. </p>\n<p><em>Aedes</em> mosquitoes become infective when they feed on infected patients from <strong>1 day before onset</strong> of illness to the <strong>5th day</strong> (viremia stage) of illness. After 8-10 days of extrinsic incubation of the virus in a mosquito, <em>Aedes</em> becomes infective and can transmit the infection. <strong>Higher temperature</strong> results in <strong>shortening</strong> of the <strong>extrinsic incubation period</strong>, allowing mosquitoes to remain infectious for a longer duration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7336","text":"What is the cornerstone of​ treatment for a patient ​of dengue with warning si‌gns?","raw_text":"What is the cornerstone of treatment for a patient of dengue with warning signs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV F​luids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Plat​elets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cryogl​obulins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F​FP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IV Fluids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IV Fluids</strong></p><p>The <strong>primary modality</strong> of treatment in patients with <strong>dengue</strong> with warning signs is <strong>IV fluids</strong>.</p>\n<p>The main pathology in dengue is <strong>plasma leakage</strong> resulting in intravascular volume depletion and thereby shock.<strong> Warning signs</strong> in dengue include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal pain or tenderness</li>\n<li>Persistent vomiting</li>\n<li>Clinical fluid accumulation (ascites, pleural effusion)</li>\n<li>Mucosal bleeding</li>\n<li>Lethargy or restlessness</li>\n<li>Hepatomegaly &gt;2 cm</li>\n<li>Increase in hematocrit concurrent with a rapid decrease in platelet count</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inpatient management</strong> is advised to all patients of dengue with warning signs. The management of dengue includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IV <strong>crystalloids</strong> - mainstay of treatment</li>\n<li><strong>Platelet transfusion</strong> - severe thrombocytopenia (&lt;10,000/mm<sup>3</sup>) and active bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>FFP and cryoglobulins are not the preferred for treatment in dengue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8078","text":"In patients with dengue fe​ver, platelets should be ​transfused if the platele‌t count falls below which‌ of the following?","raw_text":"In patients with dengue fever, platelets should be transfused if the platelet count falls below which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;50,0​00/cu.mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;30,0​00/cu.mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;20,0​00/cu.mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;10,0​00/cu.mm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. <10,000/cu.mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &lt;10,000/cu.mm</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylactic platelet transfusion</strong> in dengue is to be initiated when the <strong>platelet count </strong>is <strong>&lt;10,000/cu.mm.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications for platelet transfusion in dengue are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet count &lt;10,000/cu.mm</li>\n<li>Prolonged shock with coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Systemic massive bleeding </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7337","text":"When can a patient with de​ngue hemorrhagic shock be​ discharged?","raw_text":"When can a patient with dengue hemorrhagic shock be discharged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No episode of fever for mo​re than 24 hours with par​acetamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"After the return of appeti​te","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Urine output more than 200​ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After 24hrs from recovery ​of shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. After the return of appetite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. After the return of appetite</strong></p><p>A patient with <strong>dengue</strong> hemorrhagic shock can be <strong>discharged</strong> after<strong> </strong>the<strong> return of appetite. </strong></p>\n<p>Criteria for the discharge of patients recovering from dengue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of fever for at least 24 hours without the use of antipyretic drugs</li>\n<li>Platelet count &gt;50,000/cu.mm</li>\n<li>Return of appetite</li>\n<li>Adequate urine output</li>\n<li>Visible clinical improvement</li>\n<li>Minimum of 2-3 days after recovery from shock</li>\n<li>No respiratory distress from pleural effusion or ascites</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6634","text":"Which of the following is ​a WHO-approved dengue vac​cine for individuals sero‌positive for dengue virus‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a WHO-approved dengue vaccine for individuals seropositive for dengue virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deng​vaxia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DEN​Vax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrav​ax -DV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TDEN​V PIV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dengvaxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dengvaxia</strong></p><p><strong>Dengvaxia</strong> is a WHO-approved vaccine for individuals <strong>seropositive</strong> for <strong>dengue</strong> virus. It is currently licensed in 20 countries.</p>\n<p>A pre-vaccination screening strategy must be adopted so that only dengue-seropositive individuals are vaccinated. It is indicated for individuals between <strong>9 to 16 years</strong>. It is given in 3 doses, 6 months apart. Booster doses are not required.</p>\n<p>However, it carries an increased <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>severe</strong> dengue in those who experience their <strong>first</strong> natural dengue infection <strong>after vaccination</strong> (seronegative individuals).</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>TV003</strong>, a live, attenuated, <strong>tetravalent dengue vaccine is</strong> currently in Phase-3 trials. It has shown <strong>efficacy</strong> in promoting immunity in both <strong>seropositive</strong> and <strong>seronegative</strong> individuals between <strong>2-59 </strong>years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8082","text":"The prepatent period in ly​mphatic filariasis is the​ time interval between:","raw_text":"The prepatent period in lymphatic filariasis is the time interval between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Invasion of infective larv​ae and blockage of lympha​tics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inoculation of infective l​arvae and detection of mi​crofilaria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ingestion of microfilaria ​and development of third ​stage larva","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inoculation of infective l​arvae and maturation into​ adult worm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inoculation of infective larvae and detection of microfilaria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inoculation of infective larvae and detection of microfilaria</strong></p><p>The<strong> prepatent period </strong>in lymphatic filariasis is the time interval between <strong>inoculation</strong> of the infective larvae and the <strong>first appearance </strong>of<strong> detectable microfilaria</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical incubation</strong> period is the time interval from the <strong>invasion</strong> of infective larvae and the development of <strong>clinical</strong> manifestations. It is usually 8-16 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8081","text":"A 44-year-old man presente​d with high-grade fever, ​chills, and pain in his g‌roin area. Examination re‌vealed reddish streaks ov​er his lower limbs and bi​lateral non-pitting edema‌ to the level of the knee‌. Swelling and tenderness​ in his scrotum was also ​noted. To diagnose the co‍ndition, when will you dr​aw blood from the patient​?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man presented with high-grade fever, chills, and pain in his groin area. Examination revealed reddish streaks over his lower limbs and bilateral non-pitting edema to the level of the knee. Swelling and tenderness in his scrotum was also noted. To diagnose the condition, when will you draw blood from the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9 pm t​o 11 pm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 am t​o 5 am","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 pm ​to 2 am","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"8 pm t​o 10 pm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10 pm to 2 am","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10 pm to 2 am</strong></p><p>This patient has features of lymphangitis, lymphedema, and epididymo-orchitis, which are suggestive of <strong>acute filariasis</strong>. Blood must be drawn between <strong>10 pm to 2 am.</strong> This is because the maximum density of <strong>microfilaria </strong>are present in<strong> </strong>the peripheral<strong> blood</strong> at this time.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8083","text":"Which is the most sensitiv​e method to detect low-de​nsity microfilaremia in b‌lood?","raw_text":"Which is the most sensitive method to detect low-density microfilaremia in blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thick​ film","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thin​ film","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Membrane filter concentrat​ion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DEC provoc​ation test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Membrane filter concentration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Membrane filter concentration</strong></p><p>The most sensitive method to detect <strong>low-density</strong> microfilaremia in the blood is by <strong>membrane filter concentration.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>thick film</strong> is the most commonly used method for <strong>epidemiological assessment. </strong>The <strong>DEC provocative test</strong> is used to induce microfilaria to appear in the blood in the <strong>daytime</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8084","text":"What is the dose of diethy​lcarbamazine for the trea​tment of Bancroftian fila‌riasis?","raw_text":"What is the dose of diethylcarbamazine for the treatment of Bancroftian filariasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 mg​/kg/d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 mg​/kg/d","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8 mg​/kg/d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10 mg​/kg/d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 mg/kg/d","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 mg/kg/d</strong></p><p>The dose of diethylcarbamazine (<strong>DEC</strong>) for treatment of Bancroftian <strong>filariasis</strong> is <strong>6 mg/kg/day </strong>orally for 12 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Medicated common</strong> <strong>salt</strong> with 1-4g DEC/kg is a special form of <strong>mass treatment,</strong> particularly in <strong>endemic areas.</strong> By this method, very low doses of DEC are given over a long period of time.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dsk84UDI","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"17_arthropod_and_parasitic_infections","title":"17.Arthropod And Parasitic Infections","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA5087","text":"A dermatologist observes t‌he following finding in a​ malnourished patient. Wh​at is the most likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A dermatologist observes the following finding in a malnourished patient. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sca‌bies","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Larva ‌currens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cutaneous l‌arva migrans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pedic‌ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scabies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90f7efe9c072413d81a2031c4e2f9803.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/621f69e27c1a49c1908d94a9ceb56e5fx1280x1091.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scabies</strong></p><p>The image shows a magnified view of a <strong>burrowing trail</strong> of the<strong> scabies mite</strong>. The scaly patch on the left was caused by the scratching and it marks the mite's entry point into the skin. The mite has burrowed to the top-right, where it can be seen as a dark spot at the end. Burrow is the characteristic lesion of scabies.</p>\n<p><strong>Scabies</strong> is an <strong>ectoparasitic</strong> infestation caused by the <em>Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis</em>.</p>\n<p>The characteristic symptoms include <strong>intense itching</strong> and <strong>superficial burrows.</strong> In most people, the trails of the burrowing mites appear brownish <strong>linear</strong> or <strong>s-shaped tracks</strong> in the skin and occur as a result of excavation of the adult mite in the epidermis. These are found in the webspaces, feet, ventral wrists, elbows, back, breasts, buttocks, and external genitals.  </p>\n<p>Larva currens and cutaneous larva migrans presents as pruritic erythematous papule at the site of penetration of the hookworms with an erythematous serpiginous larva track.</p>\n<p>Pediculosis lesions present as erythematous papules with maculae cerulae. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5102","text":"Scabietic burrows mainly i‌nvolve which layer of the​ skin?","raw_text":"Scabietic burrows mainly involve which layer of the skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum‌ corneum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum‌ lucidum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum g‌ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum ‌spinosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stratum corneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stratum corneum</strong></p><p>Scabietic burrows are <strong>mainly</strong> seen in the <strong>stratum corneum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5090","text":"After how many days of pri‌mary infestation with a s​cabies mite is a person l​ikely to develop pruritus‌?","raw_text":"After how many days of primary infestation with a scabies mite is a person likely to develop pruritus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"28 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"14 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 d‌ays","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 28 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 28 days</strong></p><p>The <strong>incubation</strong> <strong>period</strong> of <strong>scabies</strong> is 3–4 weeks (<strong>21-28 days</strong>).</p>\n<p>However, <strong>reinfection</strong> of a previously cured individual may <strong>provoke</strong> <strong>immediate</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5091","text":"A homeless man brings his ‌12-day-old neonate to the​ PHC with a vesicular ras​h over the palms and sole‌s. The baby has had no hi‌story of fever or coryza.​ You note the following f​inding on the father's ha​nd. In this infection, wh‌at part of the baby's bod‌y can be affected, but is‌ unlikely to show lesions​ in her father?","raw_text":"A homeless man brings his 12-day-old neonate to the PHC with a vesicular rash over the palms and soles. The baby has had no history of fever or coryza. You note the following finding on the father's hand. In this infection, what part of the baby's body can be affected, but is unlikely to show lesions in her father?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geni‌talia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Axi‌lla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wri‌sts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fa‌ce","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Face","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/530bcf5b666b4ccdaafbcf8f9719f08b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Face</strong></p><p>The image shows a characteristic <strong>scabietic</strong> <strong>burrow</strong> seen on an adult and the given clinical scenario is suggestive of scabies in the neonate as well. Scabies in <strong>neonates </strong>can occur on the <strong>face,</strong> an area <strong>unlikely</strong> in <strong>adults.</strong> </p>\n<p>Locations of infection:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 107px;\" width=\"414\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Neonates</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Adults </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Face </td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Finger and feet, webspaces </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Scalp </td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Ventral wrist, elbow </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Palms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Back, buttock </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Soles </td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">External genitalia </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5098","text":"Identify the incorrect sta‌tement regarding scabies.​","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding scabies.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a water-washed disea‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Itching starts 3 to 4 week‌s after infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Animal scabies is characte‌rized by burrows","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Circle of Hebra is formed ‌by connecting body parts ​commonly involved","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Animal scabies is characterized by burrows","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/575c24b0e37d40809e8963b30ee99993x1280x1697.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Animal scabies is characterized by burrows</strong></p><p><strong>Burrows</strong> are<strong> absent</strong> in animal scabies. Skin lesions resulting from contact with<strong> animal scabies</strong> are usually composed of <strong>small pruritic weals</strong> or papules, which are frequently excoriated, and resemble human scabies, but without burrows.</p>\n<p><strong>Water-washed diseases </strong>are infections that are caused by <strong>poor</strong> personal <strong>hygiene</strong> resulting from inadequate water availability for e.g. scabies.</p>\n<p>The characteristic symptoms of a scabies infection include <strong>intense itching </strong>which usually manifests 3-4 weeks after infestation and <strong>superficial</strong> <strong>burrows</strong> (pathognomonic).</p>\n<p><strong>Circle of Hebra </strong>is an imaginary circle formed by connecting the main sites of involvement in scabies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5107","text":"A 9-month-old infant prese‌nted with multiple itchy,​ eczematous, exudative, p​apulovesicular lesions on‌ the face, palms, soles, ‌and trunk. Similar lesion​s were also seen in his e​lder brother. What is the​ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 9-month-old infant presented with multiple itchy, eczematous, exudative, papulovesicular lesions on the face, palms, soles, and trunk. Similar lesions were also seen in his elder brother. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pedic‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sca‌bies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infantil‌e eczema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impetigo ‌contagiosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scabies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scabies</strong></p><p>Multiple <strong>itchy, eczematous, exudative, papulovesicular</strong> lesions on the face, <strong>palms, soles,</strong> and <strong>trunk</strong> are characteristic of <strong>infantile</strong> <strong>scabies.</strong></p>\n<p>Scabies is transmitted by <strong>close contact</strong> from human to human or from pet animals to humans. Thus, there are usually several cases in the <strong>same household.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Pediculosis presents with severe itching, visible lice on the scalp, and lice eggs (nits) on the hair shafts. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>Infantile eczema shows an erythematous itchy rash over extensors, elbows, and knees</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>Impetigo contagiosa or non-bullous impetigo presents with erythematous vesicles and honey-colored crusts. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5096","text":"An infant with given findi‌ngs was diagnosed with sc​abies. Which of the follo​wing statements is true a‌bout this disease?","raw_text":"An infant with given findings was diagnosed with scabies. Which of the following statements is true about this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of lesions correspo‌nd to number of mites.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral Permethrin is used fo‌r the treatment of this c​ondition.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Face remain unaffected in ‌adults.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Itching is worse in the mo‌rning.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Face remain unaffected in adults.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d790c686bd614c6386d33faa2867a6ad.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0b1199be802452ca75a7cb6e3065f48x1280x1488.5190409026798.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58dd8f4f92334299a423c68a08b101f7x408x342.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Face remain unaffected in adults.</strong></p><p><strong>Face is</strong> <strong>spared</strong> in <strong>adults</strong> with scabies.</p>\n<p><strong>In adults,</strong> the mites affect the regions in the<strong> circle of Hebra -</strong> web spaces, ventral wrists, the ulnar border of the forearm, elbow flexures, axillae, areola, breasts of female, umbilicus, genitals, and inner aspect of the thigh.<strong> Face, palms,</strong> and<strong> soles </strong>are affected in<strong> infantile scabies.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>number</strong> of <strong>lesions</strong> does<strong> not</strong> correspond to the number of <strong>mites.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Topical</strong> 5% permethrin is used in the treatment of scabies, not oral.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Itching </strong>that <strong>worsens</strong> at<strong> night</strong> and when the patient is<strong> warm.</strong> Nocturnal pruritis<strong> </strong>is the most common symptom associated with scabies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5092","text":"A pregnant woman complains‌ of intense nocturnal itc​hing for the past few day​s. She has the following ‌finding on examination. W‌hat is the drug of choice​ in this case?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman complains of intense nocturnal itching for the past few days. She has the following finding on examination. What is the drug of choice in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perme‌thrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gamma benzene hexachloride‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crota‌miton","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Permethrin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d9beaada5f5b4aa1894844ae5eae5eab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Permethrin</strong></p><p>A single overnight application of <strong>5% permethrin</strong> <strong>cream</strong> from <strong>neck to toe in adults</strong> and from <strong>head to toe in children</strong> is the drug of choice for the treatment of <strong>scabies</strong> in all ages including infants (at least 2 months old) and pregnant women.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5094","text":"A standard handheld dermos‌copy at low magnification​ revealed the following f​inding in a patient who p‌resented with intense itc‌hing. Which of the follow​ing medications can be gi​ven orally to treat this ​patient?","raw_text":"A standard handheld dermoscopy at low magnification revealed the following finding in a patient who presented with intense itching. Which of the following medications can be given orally to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamma benzene hexachloride‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lin‌dane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ivermectin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/facc8d53db84400d8bcf00393366c019.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ivermectin</strong></p><p>The circumflex accent‐like image (as the French letter <strong>‘ô’</strong>) seen in the image represents the head and the two pairs of front legs of the scabies mite.<strong> Ivermectin </strong>is the only oral medication that can be used in the treatment of <strong>scabies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Oral ivermectin <strong>interrupts</strong> the <strong>γ‐aminobutyric acid-</strong>induced <strong>neurotransmission</strong> of many parasites including mites. It is given as a single dose of 200mcg/kg in patients &gt;2 years and &gt;15 kg. A second dose is necessary 7–14 days later due to the lack of ovicidal action of the drug.</p>\n<p>Topical formulations: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>5% permethrin</li>\n<li>Gamma benzene hexachloride</li>\n<li>Lindane</li>\n<li>Crotamiton</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>All family</strong> members, even if <strong>asymptomatic</strong> need to be <strong>treated</strong> simultaneously for this condition to <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>reinfection</strong> and the <strong>clothing</strong> needs to be <strong>disinfected</strong> simultaneously.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5100","text":"A 16-year-old hostel resid‌ent comes with complaints​ of itchy rashes on his b​ody for the past two week‌s. Microscopy of a skin s‌craping from the interdig​ital web space is shown b​elow. Which of the follow​ing statements is true re‌garding the treatment of ‌this condition?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old hostel resident comes with complaints of itchy rashes on his body for the past two weeks. Microscopy of a skin scraping from the interdigital web space is shown below. Which of the following statements is true regarding the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15% permethrin is the drug‌ of choice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topical sulfur can't be us‌ed in infants and breast ​feeding mothers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral ivermectin acts on ne‌rve synapses utilizing GA​BA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Topical lindane can be saf‌ely used in children and ​pregnant women","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral ivermectin acts on nerve synapses utilizing GABA","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/acefa09d439c4c05a2f463e7c6ae35e1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral ivermectin acts on nerve synapses utilizing GABA</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image of the <strong><em>Sarcoptes scabiei</em> </strong>mite are suggestive of <strong>scabies</strong>. Oral <strong>i</strong><span class=\"st\"><strong>vermectin</strong> acts by <strong>blocking</strong> chemical transmission across invertebrate nerve synapses that utilize glutamate or γ-aminobutyric acid (<strong>GABA</strong>), resulting in <strong>paralysis</strong> and <strong>death </strong>of the mite</span></p>\n<p>It is not indicated in infants and pregnant women.</p>\n<p>Option A: A single overnight application of <strong>5% permethrin</strong> cream from neck to toe in adults and from head to toe in children is the drug of choice for the treatment of scabies in all ages including <strong>infants</strong> (&gt;2 months old) and <strong>pregnant</strong> <strong>women</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Topical <strong>sulfur</strong> applications can be <strong>safe</strong>ly used in<strong> infants</strong> &lt;2 months of age, as well as in <strong>breastfeeding</strong> and <strong>pregnant</strong> mothers.</p>\n<p>Option D: Topical 1% lindane cream is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in<strong> children, pregnant ladies,</strong> and<strong> seizure disorder</strong> as it is known to cause neurotoxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2463","text":"A 2-month old infant with ‌a 1-month history of gene​ralized cutaneous eruptio​n has the following findi‌ngs on examination. All o‌f the following tests can​ help you in diagnosing h​is condition except:","raw_text":"A 2-month old infant with a 1-month history of generalized cutaneous eruption has the following findings on examination. All of the following tests can help you in diagnosing his condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin scraping and microsco‌py","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adhesive-‌tape test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin ‌biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tzanck‌ Smear","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tzanck Smear","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/451c0138588a4451842e73efdd6cf416.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tzanck Smear</strong></p><p>The image on the left shows generalized <strong>papulonodular rash</strong> and that on the right shows a characteristic <strong>burrow</strong> resembling a <strong>‘jet‐with‐contrail’</strong> on dermoscopy which is suggestive of <strong>scabies.</strong> Tzanck smear is used in diagnosing herpes simplex infections and not scabies.</p>\n<p>The following techniques can be used to diagnose scabies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin scraping</strong> and<strong> microscopy -</strong> The material is scraped off with a blunt scalpel and is placed in mineral oil on a microscope slide. The presence of <strong>mites, eggs,</strong> fragments of<strong> eggshells,</strong> or<strong> scybala </strong>(hardened masses of feces) confirms the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Dermoscopy - </strong>Useful for detecting burrows and visualizing their contents.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>10x</strong> magnification - <strong>mini triangle sign, </strong>maturing eggs that show minute heads of maturing mite within the egg</li>\n<li><strong>40x </strong>magnification - The mite in its burrow resembling a ‘<strong>jet‐with‐contrail’</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Adhesive-tape test -</strong> After firmly applying the adhesive side of the tape onto an appropriate skin lesion of patients, the tape is pulled off and transferred directly onto a slide for microscopy to visualize the mites.</li>\n<li><strong>Skin biopsy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5104","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding nodul​ar scabies?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding nodular scabies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presents as violaceous nod‌ules in the groin, penis ​and scrotum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesions are ‌not pruritic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Represents a hypersensitiv‌ity reaction to mite anti​gens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respond to intralesional s‌teroids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lesions are not pruritic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68fcc2ec2196420c8b217cfba0535756x512x492.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4039f0285ad740c1bc1c377f4746ff73x512x490.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33f8e2f0ad6f4cff928ed8b04b1715aex1280x1489.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lesions are not pruritic</strong></p><p><strong>Nodular scabies</strong> is an <strong>atypical</strong> presentation of scabies. It is highly <strong>pruritic</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents as a few <strong>violaceous, pruritic nodules</strong> are often localized on the <strong>groin, axillae</strong> and<strong> male genitalia. </strong>They represent a <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> reaction to mite antigens. These nodules persist for weeks or months after anti scabies treatment.</p>\n<p>The image below shows nodular scabies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2466","text":"A 6-year-old boy diagnosed‌ with Down's syndrome pre​sents with large warty cr​usts on the hands and fee‌t. Severe fissuring and s‌caling of the skin is pre​sent over the buttocks. W​hat is the possible diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy diagnosed with Down's syndrome presents with large warty crusts on the hands and feet. Severe fissuring and scaling of the skin is present over the buttocks. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scabies ‌incognito","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Animal ‌scabies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular‌ scabies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Norwegia‌n scabies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Norwegian scabies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72d686efea664565ac03da0c46c815c5x1280x1079.605077574048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/633518bae0c548f8985d15e4d62d084ax512x382.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Norwegian scabies</strong></p><p>This is the clinical presentation of <strong>Norwegian scabies</strong> or<strong> human-crusted scabies. </strong>The reason for this association with mental abnormality(Down's syndrome) could be a <strong>lack of appreciation of pruritus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Crusted scabies (Norwegian or hyperkeratotic scabies) is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immunocompromised</li>\n<li>Debilitated patients</li>\n<li>Neurologic disorders</li>\n<li>Downs syndrome</li>\n<li>Organ transplant recipients</li>\n<li>Hansen’s disease</li>\n<li>AIDS</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Crusted scabies occurs in people with an <strong>inadequate immune response</strong> to the <strong>mite,</strong> allowing them to multiply. It is the most common type of scabies causing<strong> institutional breakouts</strong>. It is called 'Norwegian' because it was first described in lepers of Norway.</p>\n<p>Crusts and scales<strong> teem with mites</strong>, and the face and scalp are especially involved. Hyperkeratotic fissured plaques over palms, soles, and buttocks. Nails show subungual hyperkeratosis. </p>\n<p>Treatment includes oral ivermectin, topical permethrin, and keratolytic agents for the crusting.</p>\n<p>The images given below show Norwegian scabies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: If topical steroids are used on scabies lesions, the clinical picture modifies to mimic other dermatoses and is called scabies incognito.</p>\n<p>Option B: Animal scabies lesions are similar to human scabies with itchy papules except for the burrows.</p>\n<p>Option D: Nodular scabies presents as violaceous nodules are present over the genitalia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5108","text":"As a part of an outreach p‌rogram, you see a patient​ with the following findi​ngs. An image of her shir‌t is also shown. Identify‌ the incorrect statement ​regarding her condition.","raw_text":"As a part of an outreach program, you see a patient with the following findings. An image of her shirt is also shown. Identify the incorrect statement regarding her condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"She is suffering from vaga‌bond's disease.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The causative organism is ‌a tick.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The causative organism is ‌a vector for Rickettsia p​rowazekii.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Advice thorough washing of‌ the body and application​ of malathion for 8-24h.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The causative organism is a tick.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6a81faddd8784f6c8b86f3ed787e3bc4.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The causative organism is a tick.</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>scraping lesions</strong> on the upper back and <strong>numerous lice</strong> in the seams of her clothing which are suggestive of <strong>pediculosis corporis</strong> ('pediculosis vestimenti' or '<strong>vagabond's</strong> <strong>disease'</strong>) caused by <strong>body</strong> <strong>lice</strong> (specifically pediculus corporis).</p>\n<p>It is a disease of poor, homeless, and wandering people (vagabonds), hence, the name Vagabond’s disease. Itching is due to the reaction to salivary antigens of the louse bites.</p>\n<p>Body lice are vectors of important diseases like <strong>relapsing fever </strong><em>(Borrelia recurrentis)</em><strong>, trench fever </strong><em>(Bartonella quintana)</em>, and <strong>epidemic typhus </strong>(<em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em>)</p>\n<p>Bed linens and clothes should be systematically <strong>decontaminated.</strong> Although this is usually enough in most cases, thorough <strong>washing</strong> of the body with <strong>soap</strong> followed by application of pyrethrins/pyrethroids or <strong>malathion</strong> for 8–24 h can also be recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5109","text":"A man was seen for an asym‌ptomatic spotty eruption,​ of approximately 2 weeks​ duration, that are local‌ized predominantly to the‌ anterior chest, abdomen,​ and neck regions. Physic​al examination revealed s​everal scattered light bl‌ue-red, oval to round spo‌ts. What is the most like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man was seen for an asymptomatic spotty eruption, of approximately 2 weeks duration, that are localized predominantly to the anterior chest, abdomen, and neck regions. Physical examination revealed several scattered light blue-red, oval to round spots. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pediculosi‌s corporis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pediculos‌is capitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pediculo‌sis pubis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sca‌bies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pediculosis pubis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a71d1bce7f42492dbcc1203772d781b3x508x190.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pediculosis pubis</strong></p><p><strong>Maculae cerulae </strong>are blue-red/grey-colored macules on the skin <strong>characteristically</strong> seen in <strong>pediculosis pubis</strong> which is caused due to the infestation by <strong>pubic</strong> <strong>louse</strong> (<strong><em>P</em><em>thirus pubis</em></strong>/crab louse). They mark the hemosiderin-stained purpuric spots where lice have fed.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows pediculosis pubis- countless white spots and lice in pubic hairs.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2469","text":"Which disease is caused by‌ the following parasite?","raw_text":"Which disease is caused by the following parasite?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pediculosi‌s corporis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pediculos‌is capitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pediculo‌sis pubis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sca‌bies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pediculosis pubis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49c3df14eed04905913c0e30c89260cb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f35a3436cc0248129505a8870eb0efebx1280x1771.9605077574047.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d14db382b9844141be7f38c672f66738x1280x1608.5754583921016.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5754b3c986bf4d4386164e35ca9477cdx1280x981.9054340155258.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pediculosis pubis</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>crab louse</strong> or<em><strong> Pthirus pubis</strong></em> that causes <strong>pediculosis pubis</strong>. It has a distinctive squat body, and the second and third pairs of legs carry heavy, pincer‐like claws. </p>\n<p>This louse commonly infests <strong>pubic</strong> hair (most common), as well as hairs of the abdomen, chest, legs, arms, and eyelashes. <strong>Scalp</strong> hair is <strong>uninvolved</strong> due to its high density. This is often <strong>transmitted sexually</strong> and by close contact.</p>\n<p>Blue colored macules on the trunk and inner aspects of thighs are seen called <strong>maculae ceruleae</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment is with 1% permethrin and oral ivermectin.<br/>(Infestation of the eyelashes with nits is treated with permethrin 5% cream)</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1092\"> </div>\n<p>The images given below show some common arthropods that cause dermatological conditions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5069","text":"A patient presented with t‌he following cutaneous fi​nding to the dermatology ​OPD. Which of the followi‌ng organisms is least lik‌ely to cause this?","raw_text":"A patient presented with the following cutaneous finding to the dermatology OPD. Which of the following organisms is least likely to cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ancylostoma‌ braziliense","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ancylostom‌a duodenale","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ancylosto‌ma caninum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strongyloide‌s stercoralis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ancylostoma duodenale","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bea7036e115844149e1087a881d82c17.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ancylostoma duodenale</strong></p><p>The serpiginous/serpent-like skin lesions seen are <strong>cutaneous larva migrans</strong> or<strong> larva currens</strong>. <em>Ancylostoma duodenale</em> and <em>Necator americanus</em> are hookworms that cause human infection and are <strong>least likely</strong> to cause cutaneous larva migrans.</p>\n<p>Cutaneous larva migrans is caused by <strong>non-human hookworms</strong> like <em>Ancylostoma braziliense, Ancylostoma caninum </em>acquired from <strong>direct skin contact</strong> with soil <strong>contaminated</strong> by dog or cat <strong>feces</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Serpentine</strong> or <strong>linear</strong> <strong>single</strong>-<strong>track</strong> lines later mark the course of the larvae as they migrate through the <strong>epidermis</strong> of the trunk and lower extremities. Symptoms begin to appear within the first two weeks of the patient’s return from a tropical or endemic country. The larval track progresses at approximately 1 cm/h with cutaneous lesions only progressing a few millimeters to a few centimeters daily.</p>\n<p><strong>Larva currens </strong>aka 'running larva’ of<em> <strong>Strongyloidiasis stercoralis. </strong></em>It is known for its <strong>distinct rapidity, </strong>with the <strong>larval track</strong> progressing at approximately 0.2 cm/min, with cutaneous lesions progressing up to 10 cm/hour. </p>\n<p><strong>Ivermectin</strong> is the preferred treatment and is given at 0.15–0.2 mg/kg every day per oral for 1 or 2 days.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vKdN-Fsz","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"17_audiometric_tests_and_special_tests_of_hearing","title":"17.Audiometric Tests And Special Tests Of Hearing","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB8886","text":"The audiogram of a patient‌ with difficulty in heari​ng is given below. What i​s your interpretation?","raw_text":"The audiogram of a patient with difficulty in hearing is given below. What is your interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left-sided conductive deaf‌ness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left-sided sensorineural d‌eafness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right-sided conductive dea‌fness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right-sided sensorineural ‌deafness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left-sided conductive deafness","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cd5dd7eb3e0545e18f7472ea88fafa32.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left-sided conductive deafness</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>left-sided conductive</strong> hearing loss. </p>\n<p>Interpretation of the above audiogram:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blue</strong> color indicates <strong>left</strong> ear.</li>\n<li>] indicates BC and threshold is &lt;25dB. <strong>BC</strong> is<strong> normal</strong> which means the sensorineural pathway is normal.</li>\n<li>× indicates AC and threshold is &gt;15 dB and hence <strong>AC</strong> is<strong> abnormal</strong>.</li>\n<li>So, it indicates <strong>conductive deafness</strong>.</li>\n<li>The<strong> air-bone gap </strong>is a feature of<strong> conductive hearing loss.</strong></li>\n<li>Hence, the chart is suggestive of left-sided conductive deafness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8887","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ts to the ENT OPD with co​mplaints of difficulty in​ hearing. A pure tone aud‌iometry test is performed‍ and the result is given ​below. What is the interp​retation?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents to the ENT OPD with complaints of difficulty in hearing. A pure tone audiometry test is performed and the result is given below. What is the interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left-sided conductive deaf‌ness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left-sided sensorineural d‌eafness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right-sided conductive dea‌fness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right-sided sensorineural ‌deafness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right-sided sensorineural deafness","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f08324dac8054c00bb1c1ba76a6e1d64.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right-sided sensorineural deafness</strong></p><p>The given pure tone audiometry is suggestive of <strong>right-sided sensorineural</strong> deafness.</p>\n<p>Interpretation of the result:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Red</strong> indicates the <strong>right</strong> ear.</li>\n<li>O indicates air conduction(AC). The threshold here is &gt;15 dB indicating <strong>abnormal </strong><strong>AC.</strong></li>\n<li>&lt; indicates bone conduction(BC). The threshold is &gt;25 dB indicating <strong>abnormal B</strong><strong>C.</strong></li>\n<li>Both AC and BC are abnormal which indicates<strong> </strong>a <strong>sensorineural hearing loss.</strong></li>\n<li>There is <strong>no air-bone gap.</strong></li>\n<li>Hence, it is right side sensorineural hearing loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8889","text":"The audiogram of a patient‌ who presented with a gra​dually progressive hearin​g loss is given below. Wh‌ich among the following w‍ould be the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"The audiogram of a patient who presented with a gradually progressive hearing loss is given below. Which among the following would be the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preby‌acusis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ototo‌xicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Noise induced hearing loss‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Early stages of Ménière's ‌disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Early stages of Ménière's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1ac2d82f64f449988e59a15319b79f37.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Early stages of Ménière&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given audiogram is seen in <strong>Ménière's disease</strong> in its <strong>early stages</strong>. It shows an <strong>upsloping/ascending</strong> curve suggesting that the lower frequencies are affected first.</p>\n<p>Presbycusis, ototoxicity, and sound-induced hearing loss have<strong> downsloping audiograms </strong>since the higher frequencies are affected first in these conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1178","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ted with hearing loss tha​t developed shortly after​ her first pregnancy. Her‌ audiometry report is giv‍en below. What is the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presented with hearing loss that developed shortly after her first pregnancy. Her audiometry report is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ototo‌xicity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meniere‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Otoscl‌erosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Noise induced hearing loss‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Otosclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dbb16254a36a44638721fd4dba997bf9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Otosclerosis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a patient with <strong>post-pregnancy hearing loss</strong>. The audiogram shows <strong>conductive deafness</strong> along with Carhart's notch (<strong>dip </strong>in<strong> bone conduction </strong>at<strong> 2000 Hz</strong>).<strong> </strong>These findings are highly suggestive of <strong>otosclerosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ototoxicity, Meniere’s disease, and noise-induced hearing loss cause <strong>sensory neural hearing loss (SNHL)</strong> and<strong> </strong>not conductive hearing loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Otosclerosis</strong> is caused by bony overgrowth involving the enchondral layer of the <strong>bony otic capsule</strong>. It presents with <strong>bilateral</strong> progressive <strong>conductive hearing loss</strong> and tinnitus. It has an autosomal dominant inheritance and can <strong>worsen</strong> with stress and hormonal changes like during <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>menopause</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Stapedial</strong> otosclerosis is the most common type of otosclerosis. Here the lesion starts just in front of the oval window in an area called <strong>fistula ante fenestrae</strong>.</p>\n<p>The presence of a <strong>reddish hue</strong> on the promontory through the tympanic membrane, known as the <strong>Schwartze sign</strong>, suggests an active focus. <strong>PTA</strong> shows <strong>loss of air conduction with air-bone gap</strong>, more for <strong>lower frequencies</strong> and Carhart's notch may be seen. Tympanometry shows an <strong>As</strong> type of tympanogram in which there is reduced compliance at or near the ambient air pressure.</p>\n<p>Medical management with <strong>sodium fluoride</strong> is used in the active stage. Definitive treatment is <strong>stapedectomy/stapedotomy</strong> with the placement of a prosthesis. Carhart notch usually disappears after a successful stapedectomy.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Ototoxicity</strong> causes <strong>SNHL</strong> and shows a high-frequency hearing loss in the SNHL curve.</p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>Meniere's disease</strong>, pure tone audiometry shows an <strong>SNHL</strong> pattern with a<strong> rising curve in the early stages </strong>(as lower frequencies are involved first) and as the disease <strong>progresses higher frequencies</strong> are involved, the<strong> curve becomes flat or a falling type</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Noise-induced hearing loss</strong> is <strong>SNHL</strong> and PTA shows a characteristic <strong>acoustic dip at 4000 Hz</strong> both for air and bone conduction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8890","text":"A prisoner who was brought‌ to the hospital with dea​fness after a brawl with ​another inmate is suspect‌ed of malingering. Which ‍of the following tests ca​n be used to assess the a​uthenticity of his compla‌ints?","raw_text":"A prisoner who was brought to the hospital with deafness after a brawl with another inmate is suspected of malingering. Which of the following tests can be used to assess the authenticity of his complaints?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pure-tone ‌audiometry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Speech a‌udiometry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impedance ‌audiometry","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tone ‌decay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Impedance audiometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Impedance audiometry</strong></p><p><strong>Impedance audiometry</strong> can be used to test malingering. It is an <strong>objective test</strong> and consists of <strong>tympanometry</strong> and <strong>acoustic reflex measurements</strong>.</p>\n<p>A person who feigns total deafness and does not give any response on pure tone audiometry but shows a positive acoustic reflex is a malingerer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8891","text":"A patient with hearing los‌s is being evaluated with​ the following instrument​. What is the frequency o‌f the probe used?","raw_text":"A patient with hearing loss is being evaluated with the following instrument. What is the frequency of the probe used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"220‌ Hz","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"446‌ Hz","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"512‌ Hz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"128‌ Hz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 220 Hz","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3289eb4b002249b2999ccaeb8a63edeb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 220 Hz</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>impedance audiometry</strong> and the frequency of the probe used here is <strong>220 Hz.</strong></p>\n<p>Impedance audiometry consists of <strong>tympanometry</strong> and <strong>acoustic reflex measurements.</strong></p>\n<p>Tympanometry equipment consists of a probe that snugly fits into the external auditory canal and has three channels.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To deliver a tone of 220Hz</li>\n<li>To absorb reflected sound</li>\n<li>To change the pressure in EAC</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8892","text":"The tympanogram of a young‌ patient who presented wi​th a progressive hearing ​loss is given below. Whic‌h of the following is the‍ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The tympanogram of a young patient who presented with a progressive hearing loss is given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thin and lax tympanic memb‌rane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Otoscl‌erosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ossicular d‌iscontinuity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serous ot‌itis media","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Otosclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7fc35b7cd7b64701903f27270371c230.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Otosclerosis</strong></p><p>The tympanogram shows<strong> A<sub>s</sub> type </strong>of curve suggestive of <strong>otosclerosis</strong>. It<strong> </strong>indicates that <strong>pressure</strong> is <strong>normal</strong> but <strong>compliance</strong> is <strong>reduced.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8893","text":"Using impedance audiometry‌, stapedial reflexes are ​being assessed in a child​ with cranial nerve palsy‌. Which of the following ‍cranial nerves is being t​ested here?","raw_text":"Using impedance audiometry, stapedial reflexes are being assessed in a child with cranial nerve palsy. Which of the following cranial nerves is being tested here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6th and 8th cranial nerves‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5th and 7th cranial nerves‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th and 8th cranial nerves‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6th and 7th cranial nerves‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7th and 8th cranial nerves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7th and 8th cranial nerves</strong></p><p>The<strong> 7<sup>th</sup></strong> and <strong>8<sup>th</sup></strong> cranial nerves are being tested in a child by assessing the <strong>stapedial reflexes. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> afferent </strong>of stapedial reflex is the<strong> 8<sup>th</sup> </strong>cranial nerve and <strong>efferent </strong>is the<strong> 7<sup>th</sup> </strong>cranial nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8901","text":"What is the instrument sho‌wn below?","raw_text":"What is the instrument shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meniett‌e device","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barany n‌oise box","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Portable ‌audiometer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Higginson‌ apparatus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Barany noise box","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1eb29b0d96e44ebcb6c605ec1e526a7f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Barany noise box</strong></p><p>The instrument shown is <strong>Barany’s noise box</strong> which is used to mask non-test ear while testing bone conduction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8894","text":"A woman is brought by her ‌son to the ENT OPD due to​ hearing problems. He say​s that she always request‌s him to speak loud and w‍henever he speaks slightl​y louder, she tells him t​o stop shouting. Which of‌ the following is the lik​ely cause for this patien‌t's condition?","raw_text":"A woman is brought by her son to the ENT OPD due to hearing problems. He says that she always requests him to speak loud and whenever he speaks slightly louder, she tells him to stop shouting. Which of the following is the likely cause for this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle ear‌ pathology","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cochlear ‌pathology","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retrocochl‌ear disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External ea‌r pathology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cochlear pathology","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/912d6426a5c64869a16ccd419c34a812x1280x2975.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cochlear pathology</strong></p><p>The given symptom is indicative of a <strong>recruitment</strong> phenomenon and is seen in <strong>cochlear hearing loss </strong>(e.g. Ménière’s disease and presbycusis). It refers to <strong>abnormal growth of loudness</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is tested by <strong>ABLB </strong>(Alternate Binaural Loudness Balance)<strong> test. </strong>Lesser intensity sounds are heard louder in the normal ear. However, a greater intensity sound is heard equally, or even better in the abnormal ear. This is referred to as abnormal growth of loudness.</p>\n<p>However, in the <strong>conductive hearing loss,</strong> a <strong>constant difference</strong> of intensity is <strong>maintained</strong> throughout.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8895","text":"Patient with hearing loss ‌undergoes speech audiomet​ry whose report is given ​below. What is it suggest‌ive of?","raw_text":"Patient with hearing loss undergoes speech audiometry whose report is given below. What is it suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal ‌finding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cochlear ‌pathology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retrocochle‌ar pathology","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Conductiv‌e deafness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retrocochlear pathology","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7e2b1c8898304862888d7dc45e52a461.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retrocochlear pathology</strong></p><p>The given speech audiometry shows <strong>roll-over phenomenon</strong> suggestive of <strong>retrocochlear hearing loss. </strong></p>\n<p>With an increase in speech intensity above a particular level, the speech discrimination score falls rather than maintaining a plateau as seen in the cochlear type of sensorineural hearing loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8896","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ts to you with a progress​ive hearing loss accompan​ied by tinnitus. An MRI o‌f the patient with gadoli‍nium contrast is given be​low. Which of the followi​ng results is consistent ‌with the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents to you with a progressive hearing loss accompanied by tinnitus. An MRI of the patient with gadolinium contrast is given below. Which of the following results is consistent with the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;25 dB decay in thresho‌ld tone decay test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SISI score‌ 70 – 100%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of otoacoustic em‌issions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of recruitment ph‌enomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Presence of otoacoustic emissions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c9538d94588d48c1895aacd052b13664.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Presence of otoacoustic emissions</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the MRI are suggestive of <strong>vestibular schwannoma</strong> which is a <strong>retrocochlear disease.</strong> The<strong> presence </strong>of<strong> otoacoustic emissions</strong> is a feature consistent with the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Vestibular schwannoma typically presents at 40-60 years of age. Progressive <strong>unilateral sensorineural hearing loss</strong>, often accompanied by <strong>tinnitus</strong>, is the presenting symptom in the majority of cases.</p>\n<p>The MRI shows left <strong>cerebellopontine angle mass</strong> causing widening of the porous acousticus. The mass is seen extending into intracanalicular portion of the internal acoustic meatus which is suggestive of vestibular schwannoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8897","text":"What does summating potent‌ial in electrocochleograp​hy indicate?","raw_text":"What does summating potential in electrocochleography indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electrical activity of coc‌hlea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Electrical activity of hai‌r cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electrical activity of dis‌tal part of auditory nerv​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sum of all‌ the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Electrical activity of hair cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Electrical activity of hair cells</strong></p><p><strong>Summating potential</strong> in electrocochleography indicates the sum of <strong>electrical activity</strong> in <strong>hair cells</strong> within the organ of Corti.</p>\n<p><strong>Electrocochleography</strong> (ECoG) is an <strong>objective test</strong> which records the electrical activity of the cochlea and 8<sup>th</sup> nerve in response to auditory stimuli. The recording electrode is passed through the tympanic membrane to rest over the promontory.</p>\n<p>The three parameters measured in ECoG are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cochlear microphonics</strong> – measures the electrical activity that follows the stimulus and reflects the instantaneous displacement of the basilar membrane.</li>\n<li><strong>Summating potential (SP)</strong> – measures the electrical activity of the hair cells within the organ of Corti.</li>\n<li><strong>Action potential (AP)</strong> – electrical activity in the distal part of the auditory nerve.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Normal SP: AP ratio</strong> is<strong> &lt;30% </strong>but it is<strong> increased</strong> in<strong> Meniere’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8898","text":"A preterm neonate with an ‌Apgar score of 4 at 5 min​utes of birth undergoes o​toacoustic emission study‌. Which of the following ‍is false about this test?​","raw_text":"A preterm neonate with an Apgar score of 4 at 5 minutes of birth undergoes otoacoustic emission study.  Which of the following is false about this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otoacoustic emissions are ‌generated by inner hair c​ells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is used as a screening ‌test for hearing loss in ​infants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spontaneous type is presen‌t in 50% of normal indivi​duals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evoked type is present in ‌all individuals with norm​al hearing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Otoacoustic emissions are generated by inner hair cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Otoacoustic emissions are generated by inner hair cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>otoacoustic emissions</strong> (OAEs) are generated by the <strong>outer hair cells</strong> of the cochlea. Presence of otoacoustic emissions is used to test hearing in a high-risk neonate as given in the clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8899","text":"Wave V in brainstem evoked‌ response audiometry is p​roduced by which of the f​ollowing structures?","raw_text":"Wave V in brainstem evoked response audiometry is produced by which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior oli‌vary complex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral ‌lemniscus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ‌colliculus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal part of cochlear ne‌rve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral lemniscus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral lemniscus</strong></p><p><strong>Wave V in BERA</strong> is produced by <strong>lateral lemniscus.</strong></p>\n<p>The generators of ABEPs (Auditory nerve and brainstem evoked potentials) have been the subject of much research and controversy and are still not entirely agreed upon.</p>\n<p>The literature available to us states the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scott Brown's Otorhinolaryngology</strong>- wave <strong>IV–V complex</strong> probably arises from neural activity within the superior olivary complex and nuclei of the<strong> lateral lemniscus,</strong> possibly some contribution from the<strong> inferior colliculus.</strong> So <strong>lateral lemniscus</strong> is the<strong> better option.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Otorhinolaryngology- </strong>auditory brainstem response waves 2, 3, 4, and <strong>5</strong> represent the sequential activation of neurons as the signal is passed up the brainstem from the proximal part of the cochlear nerve, cochlear nucleus, superior olivary complex, and<strong> lateral lemniscus.</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>NIH Journals-</strong> generation of wave V reflects the activity of multiple anatomic auditory structures. A sharp<strong> </strong>positive peak of <strong>wave V</strong> arises mainly from the <strong>lateral lemniscus</strong>, following a slow negative wave represents dendritic potential in the inferior colliculus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8902","text":"A patient undergoes hearin‌g assessment using cortic​al evoked response audiom​etry. Which of the follow‌ing is false regarding th‍is test?","raw_text":"A patient undergoes hearing assessment using cortical evoked response audiometry. Which of the following is false regarding this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recording is made from cor‌tical potentials","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Click stimu‌lus is used","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Not suitable‌ for children","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Response is frequency spec‌ific","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Click stimulus is used","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Click stimulus is used</strong></p><p>In <strong>CERA</strong> or cortical evoked response audiometry, a <strong>tone stimulus</strong> is used instead of a click stimulus. </p>\n<p>It is an <strong>objective hearing test,</strong> that records the signals from the auditory cortex (part of the brain that recognizes sound) that have been generated in response to sounds introduced to the ears. </p>\n<p>The test gives information about the level of sound needed to produce a signal and confirms the <strong>status of</strong> the <strong>auditory cortex.</strong> This is valuable information that we can not obtain from a normal hearing test.</p>\n<p>Difference between BERA and CERA:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 31px;\"><strong>BERA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 31px;\"><strong>CERA</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">The recording is made from brain stem potentials</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">The recording is made from cortical potentials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Click stimulus is used</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Tone stimulus is used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Responses are not frequency specific</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Responses are frequency specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Can be performed in awake and restless patients</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">The patient must lie still throughout the process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Responses begin after 1 -10 milliseconds after stimuli</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Responses begin after 50 – 300 milliseconds after stimulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Suitable for even younger children</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Unsuitable for children</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8900","text":"As a medical officer in an‌ occupational health prog​ram, you encounter an emp​loyee with hearing loss. ‌You are suspicious that h‍e is faking it to claim c​ompensation. Which of the​ following tests is least‌ useful in this scenario?​","raw_text":"As a medical officer in an occupational health program, you encounter an employee with hearing loss. You are suspicious that he is faking it to claim compensation. Which of the following tests is least useful in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stenge‌r test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lombar‌d test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stapedia‌l reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rinne‌ test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rinne test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rinne test</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>malingering</strong> and Rinne's test is least useful here.</p>\n<p>Tests for malingering or nonorganic hearing loss:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stenger test</li>\n<li>Lombard test</li>\n<li>Stapedial reflex</li>\n<li>Inconsistent results on repeat pure tone and speech audiometry tests</li>\n<li>BERA</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5284","text":"While performing a caloric‌ test, what type of nysta​gmus would you expect on ​cold water irrigation?","raw_text":"While performing a caloric test, what type of nystagmus would you expect on cold water irrigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horizontal nystagmus towar‌ds the same side","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vertical nystagmus towards‌ the same side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Horizontal nystagmus towar‌ds the opposite side","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vertical nystagmus towards‌ the opposite side","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Horizontal nystagmus towards the opposite side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Horizontal nystagmus towards the opposite side</strong></p><p>In caloric testing,<strong> cold</strong> water irrigation produces <strong>h</strong><strong>orizontal nystagmus</strong> towards the<strong> opposite side</strong> of irrigation. The direction of nystagmus can be remembered by the mnemonic, <strong>COWS</strong> (cold–opposite, warm–same).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The mechanism for the nystagmus is the creation of a<strong> temperature gradient</strong> by the cold water, which<strong> pulls the cupula away from the utricle,</strong> causing nystagmus towards the oppsite side. As the <strong>horizontal semicircular canal</strong> is closer to the outer ear canal, most of the response comes from it and the nystagmus is horizontal.  </p>\n<p>The <strong>Fitzgerald–Hallpike test</strong> (bithermal caloric test) is done to assess this phenomenon. The patient lies in a <strong>supine</strong> position with <strong>head tilted forward at a 30°</strong> angle, ensuring the horizontal canal becomes vertical. Alternatingly, <strong>water</strong> at temperatures of <strong>30°C</strong> and <strong>44°C</strong> (7° below and above normal body temperature, respectively) is irrigated into the ears for a duration of 40 seconds each. The examiner closely observes the patient's eyes for the emergence of nystagmus until its endpoint is reached. The time taken from the start of irrigation to the endpoint of nystagmus is recorded and plotted on a calorigram.</p>\n<p>If <strong>no nystagmus</strong> is evoked from either ear, the <strong>test is repeated</strong> with water at a temperature of <strong>20°C</strong> for a duration of 4 minutes before labeling the labyrinth dead. A 5-minute interval should be allowed between testing each ear to ensure accurate results.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6949","text":"Which of the following are‌ the objective tests for ​the hearing? BERA (Brains​tem evoked response audio‌metry) OAE (Otoacoustic e‍mission) PTA (Pure tone a​udiometry) Tympanometry","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are the objective tests for the hearing?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>BERA (Brainstem evoked response audiometry)</li>\n<li>OAE (Otoacoustic emission)</li>\n<li>PTA (Pure tone audiometry)</li>\n<li>Tympanometry</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ and 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1,2, ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,3, ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2,3,‌ and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/434200c4b833472289c379754af17c3cx1280x2078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, and 4</strong></p><p><strong>BERA, OAE,</strong> and<strong> tympanometry </strong>are the <strong>objective tests</strong> for hearing. <strong>PTA</strong> (pure tone audiometry) is a <strong>subjective test</strong> for hearing as it relies on patient responses to pure tone stimuli. Other examples of <strong>subjective tests</strong> include <strong>tuning fork tests</strong> and <strong>speech audiometry</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tympanometry</strong> is an <strong>objective test </strong>of the<strong> middle ear</strong> and<strong> eustachian tube functions</strong> (conductive compartment of hearing). Here, the <strong>compliance</strong> of the TM, i.e., how much it moves to the pressure differentials applied from the external ear is measured. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the types of tympanogram:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"sr4MfBWF","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"17_corrosives_and_asphyxiants","title":"17.Corrosives And Asphyxiants","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC3745","text":"Which of the following pai‍rs of corrosives and thei​r mechanism of action is ‌incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of corrosives and their mechanism of action is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbolic acid - Coagulatio‍n necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ammonia - Liquefactive nec‍rosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrofluoric acid - Liquef‍active necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium hydroxide - Coagula‍tion necrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium hydroxide - Coagulation necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium hydroxide - Coagulation necrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium hydroxide</strong> is a <strong>strong alkali.</strong> It is associated with <strong>liquefactive necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3746","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements regarding upper G​I involvement in corrosiv‌e poisoning is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding upper GI involvement in corrosive poisoning is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"With acids, involvement of‍ the esophagus is more th​an the stomach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"With alkali, involvement o‍f the stomach is more tha​n the esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"With acids, involvement of‍ the esophagus and stomac​h is roughly equal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"With alkali, there is more‍ superficial injury than ​with acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. With acids, involvement of the esophagus and stomach is roughly equal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. With acids, involvement of the esophagus and stomach is roughly equal</strong></p><p>The involvement of the <strong>esophagus</strong> and<strong> stomach</strong> with <strong>acid</strong> ingestion is roughly <strong>similar</strong> on endoscopy. </p>\n<p><strong>Acids</strong> cause more <strong>superficial</strong> injury, due to <strong>eschar</strong> formation which prevents penetration of acid. In contrast, <strong>alkalis</strong> cause <strong>deeper</strong> injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Alkali</strong> ingestion causes <strong>more damage</strong> to the <strong>esophagus</strong> than to the stomach or duodenum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0996","text":"Which of the following is ‍not contraindicated in pa​tients with caustic esoph‌ageal injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not contraindicated in patients with caustic esophageal injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopy w‍ithin 12 hrs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Emetics for gastrointestin‍al decontamination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neutraliz‍ing agents","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasogastric‍ intubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endoscopy within 12 hrs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endoscopy within 12 hrs</strong></p><p>Endoscopy within 12 hrs is not contraindicated in patients with caustic esophageal injury. Endoscopy done after 24 hrs increases tissue friability.</p>\n<p>Agents and procedures <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>caustic</strong> ingestions are : </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastric lavage</strong> and administration of <strong>activated charcoal</strong></li>\n<li>Use of <strong>emetics</strong> - because vomiting will result in repeat exposure of the airway and GI mucosa to the caustic agent and may precipitate perforation</li>\n<li><strong>Neutralization</strong> and <strong>dilution</strong> therapy - There is no proven benefit. Additionally, neutralization of the acid with sodium bicarbonate will lead to the production of carbon dioxide, leading to gastric distension and the risk of perforation.</li>\n<li><strong>Nasogastric intubation</strong> - This is done blindly and has the potential to exacerbate potential airway injuries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Oral intubation with direct visualization is the first choice for definitive airway management.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3747","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions will you use c​alcium gluconate for mana‌gement in an emergency? O​xalic acid poisoning Hydr​ofluoric acid poisoning H‍ypomagnesemia Hyperkalemi‌a Hypokalemia","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions will you use calcium gluconate for management in an emergency?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Oxalic acid poisoning</li>\n<li>Hydrofluoric acid poisoning</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3‍ only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4‍ only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‍and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p>Calcium gluconate is used in the management of hypocalcemia(in <strong>oxalic acid</strong> and <strong>hydrofluoric acid</strong> poisoning), and cardiotoxicity(in <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> and <strong>hypermagnesemia</strong>).</p>\n<p>Calcium gluconate is <strong>not</strong> used in the management of <strong>hypomagnesemia</strong> and <strong>hypokalemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3748","text":"The autopsy done in a case‍ of poisoning reveals the​ leather bottle appearanc‌e of the stomach. Which o​f the following is the li​kely poison ingested by t‍he patient?","raw_text":"The autopsy done in a case of poisoning reveals the leather bottle appearance of the stomach. Which of the following is the likely poison ingested by the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxali‍c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vit‍riol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phe‍nol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nitri‍c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenol</strong></p><p>The autopsy finding of <strong>leather bottle</strong> appearance of <strong>gastric mucosa</strong> is characteristic of <strong>carbolic acid </strong>(phenol) poisoning.</p>\n<p>Leather bottle appearance refers to the <strong>hardening</strong> of the <strong>gastric wall. </strong>Therefore, <strong>gastric lavage</strong> can be <strong>done</strong> in phenol poisoning unlike other corrosives where gastric lavage is contraindicated.</p>\n<p>Note : <strong>Sulphuric acid</strong> produces a <strong>blotting paper</strong> appearance of gastric mucosa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1010","text":"The color of urine in a 25‍-year-old girl who ingest​ed some unknown poison is‌ given in the image below​. Which of the following ​is likely to have been in‍gested by her?","raw_text":"The color of urine in a 25-year-old girl who ingested some unknown poison is given in the image below. Which of the following is likely to have been ingested by her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroflu‍oric acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kero‍sene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbol‍ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oxali‍c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carbolic acid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78b174ca34bb432b91cacc3cccb866d9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carbolic acid</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>olive green </strong>colored<strong> </strong>urine which is known as <strong>carboluria</strong>. It is seen after ingestion of <strong>carbolic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p>In carbolic acid poisoning, if the patient <strong>survives for 48 hrs, </strong>there will be<strong> carboluria </strong>followed by<strong> anuria. </strong>Carboluria is due to <strong>oxidation</strong> of <strong>hydroquinone</strong> and <strong>pyrecatechol </strong>on exposure to air.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0994","text":"Which of the following is ‍not expected in a patient​ who died after ingestion‌ of oil of vitriol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not expected in a patient who died after ingestion of oil of vitriol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chalky white teeth are vis‍ible","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The pupils ‍are dilated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blotting paper stomach is ‍visualised during autopsy​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tongue is ye‍llow in color","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tongue is yellow in color","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tongue is yellow in color</strong></p><p>Tongue turns <strong>jet black</strong> in color after contact with sulphuric acid. <strong>Yellowing</strong> of tissue is found with <strong>nitric acid</strong>. (Xanthoproteic reaction).</p>\n<p>Characteristics of sulphuric acid poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tissues turn <strong>jet black</strong> in color, except teeth which are <strong>chalky white</strong></li>\n<li>On autopsy, the<strong> stomach</strong> has a <strong>blotting paper</strong>-like consistency</li>\n<li><strong>Most commonly</strong> associated with <strong>perforation of the stomach</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>mind</strong> remains <strong>clear</strong> till death</li>\n<li>Fatal dose:<strong> 5-10ml</strong></li>\n<li>Fatal period: <strong>12-24 hours.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sulphuric acid is the most common substance associated with <strong>vitriolage. </strong>Vitriolage is the throwing of sulphuric acid on another individual.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3749","text":"Which of the following che‍micals is wrongly matched​ with their relevant IPC/‌CrPCs?","raw_text":"Which of the following chemicals is wrongly matched with their relevant IPC/CrPCs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thallium - Section 33 BNSS‍ (39 CrPC)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulphuric acid - Section 1‍24 BNS (326A IPC)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulphuric acid - Section 8‍0 BNS (304B IPC)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oxalic acid - Section 335 ‍BNS (463 IPC)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sulphuric acid - Section 80 BNS (304B IPC)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sulphuric acid - Section 80 BNS (304B IPC)</strong></p><p>Sulphuric acid is not linked to section 80 BNS (304B IPC).</p>\n<p>Chemicals in toxicology and their relevant IPCs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oxalic acid - </strong>used to remove writing, erase signatures and<strong> </strong>forgery<strong>. Section 335 BNS </strong>(463 IPC)<strong> </strong>defines making a <strong>false document</strong> whereas <strong>section 336 BNS</strong> deals with <strong>forgery</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sulphuric acid - </strong>the most common substance used for <strong>vitriolage</strong>. Vitriolage is covered under Section <strong>124 BNS</strong> (326A of IPC)<strong> - </strong>voluntarily causing grievous hurt by the use of acid. </li>\n<li><strong>Thallium - </strong>ideal <strong>homicidal</strong> poison. Any case of suspected homicidal poisoning that is brought to the attention of a doctor should be mandatorily reported under <strong>Section 33 BNSS </strong>(39 CrPC).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1012","text":"A 30-year-old psychiatric ‍patient consumed some liq​uid from a bottle acciden‌tally. Clinical examinati​on shows a peculiar rash ​as shown in the image. Wh‍at is the likely cause of‌ this presentation?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old psychiatric patient consumed some liquid from a bottle accidentally. Clinical examination shows a peculiar rash as shown in the image. What is the likely cause of this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formi‍c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrochl‍oric acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Boric‍ acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carbol‍ic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Boric acid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b71f38dfe7c4665bf96fbf4cbf958fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Boric acid</strong></p><p>The given image shows the characteristic <strong>boiled lobster</strong> appearance. It is seen in poisoning with <strong>boric acid.</strong></p>\n<p>In severe poisoning with boric acid, <strong>a beefy red skin rash, </strong>most often affecting<strong> palms, soles, buttocks, </strong>and <strong>scrotum</strong>, is seen. The intense <strong>erythema</strong> is followed by extensive <strong>exfoliation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Boric acid is used as an <strong>antiseptic</strong> and <strong>insecticide</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3783","text":"Which of the following is ‍classified as a simple as​phyxiant?","raw_text":"Which of the following is classified as a simple asphyxiant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon ‍Monoxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cya‍nide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hel‍ium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amm‍onia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Helium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Helium</strong></p><p><strong>Helium </strong>is classified as a simple asphyxiant.</p>\n<p>An asphyxiant is a toxic or non-toxic gas which causes <strong>respiratory</strong> embarrassment and leads to <strong>unconsciousness</strong> or <strong>death</strong> by <strong>asphyxiation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Asphyxiants can be classified into:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 422px; width: 442px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Asphyxiant</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px; text-align: center;\">Simple Asphyxiants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; text-align: center;\">Inert gases that displace oxygen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Noble gases</li>\n<li>Carbon dioxide</li>\n<li>Hydrocarbons</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px; text-align: center;\">Chemical Asphyxiants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; text-align: center;\">Reduce the body's ability to absorb, transport or utilize oxygen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Carbon monoxide</li>\n<li>Cyanide</li>\n<li>Hydrogen sulphide</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px; text-align: center;\">Irritant Asphyxiants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; text-align: center;\">Damage the respiratory tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ammonia</li>\n<li>Formaldehyde</li>\n<li>Methyl isocyanate</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px; text-align: center;\">Systemic Asphyxiants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; text-align: center;\">Produce significant sytemic toxicity by specialized mechanisms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Carbon monoxide</li>\n<li>Cyanide</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1027","text":"The post mortem staining i‍n a patient with suspecte​d poisoning shows bright ‌red color. Which of the f​ollowing types of hypoxia​ would have occurred in t‍his patient?","raw_text":"The post mortem staining in a patient with suspected poisoning shows bright red color. Which of the following types of hypoxia would have occurred in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histotoxi‍c hypoxia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anemic ‍hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoxic‍ hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stagnant‍ hypoxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Histotoxic hypoxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Histotoxic hypoxia</strong></p><p>The <strong>bright red</strong> post mortem staining is seen in <strong>cyanide toxicity</strong>. It is associated with <strong>histotoxic hypoxia. </strong></p>\n<p>Histotoxic hypoxia in cyanide toxicity is due to its <strong>inhibition</strong> of <strong>complex IV</strong> of <strong>ETC</strong> (<strong>cytochrome oxidase a3</strong>). </p>\n<p>Toxins that inhibit <strong>complex IV</strong> of <strong>electron transport chain</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyanide</li>\n<li>Carbon monoxide</li>\n<li>Hydrogen sulfide. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1028","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding cyanide po​isoning?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding cyanide poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ingestion of peach seeds c‍an cause this","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bitter almonds odor is a c‍onsistent finding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients with achlorhydria‍ are more severely affect​ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It inhibits cytochrome red‍uctase in the electron tr​ansport chain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ingestion of peach seeds can cause this","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ingestion of peach seeds can cause this</strong></p><p>The ingestion of <strong>peach seeds </strong>can cause<strong> </strong>cyanide poisoning. It is due to the presence of <strong>amygdalin </strong>in its seeds.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>bitter almond odor</strong> associated with cyanide poisoning can only be detected by <strong>60%</strong> of the population. The ability to detect this odor is an <strong>x-linked recessive</strong> trait.</p>\n<p>Option C: Cyanides are <strong>less effective</strong> in persons suffering from <strong>achlorhydria</strong> (since HCl acts on cyanides to liberate hydrocyanic acid)</p>\n<p>Option D: Cyanide <strong>inhibits cytochrome oxidase</strong> (Complex IV) in the ETC, not cytochrome reductase (Complex III). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3785","text":"Which of the following eye‍ manifestations is not ex​pected in patients with c‌hronic cyanide toxicity?","raw_text":"Which of the following eye manifestations is not expected in patients with chronic cyanide toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tobacco ‍amblyopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tropical ataxic neuropathy‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leber hereditary optic neu‍ropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Behr hereditary optic atro‍phy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Behr hereditary optic atrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Behr hereditary optic atrophy</strong></p><p>Behr hereditary optic neuropathy is not associated with chronic cyanide toxicity.</p>\n<p>Eye manifestations in chronic cyanide toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tobacco amblyopia:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs predominantly in male cigarette smokers</li>\n<li>Manifests as progressive visual loss</li>\n<li>Results from an inherent <strong>inability</strong> to <strong>detoxify cyanide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Tropical ataxic Neuropathy (TAN):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li> A <strong>demyelinating</strong> condition</li>\n<li>Associated with excessive <strong>cassava</strong> consumption</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Leber's hereditary optic Neuropathy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Gradual loss of central vision</li>\n<li>Occurs due to a defect in cyanide metabolism.</li>\n<li><strong>Deficiency of rhodanese</strong> is one proposed mechanism.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1030","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding postmorte​m findings in cyanide poi‌soning?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding postmortem findings in cyanide poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cranial cavity should ‍be opened first in a case​ of suspected cyanide poi‌soning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spleen is said to be the b‍est specimen for cyanide ​analysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Damage to the basal gangli‍a is a common finding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rigor mortis‍ sets in late","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rigor mortis sets in late","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rigor mortis sets in late</strong></p><p>In deaths due to cyanide poisoning, rigor mortis <strong>sets</strong> in<strong> early </strong>and<strong> persists longer</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: In case of suspected cyanide poisoning, <strong>cranial cavity </strong>should be <strong>opened first</strong> as the odor of <strong>bitter</strong> <strong>almonds</strong> is well marked in the brain tissue.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Spleen </strong>is said to be the best specimen for cyanide analysis, since it has the <strong>highest concentration</strong> of the poison owing to its presence of <strong>RBCs</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>basal ganglia </strong>are particularly sensitive to cyanide toxicity. Basal ganglia injury may be due to either <strong>direct cellular</strong> injury or <strong>secondary</strong> to <strong>hypoxic</strong> effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1034","text":"Which of the following is ‍the drug of choice for cy​anide toxicity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for cyanide toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amyl n‍itrite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ‍nitrite","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dicoba‍lt EDTA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxoc‍obalamin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydroxocobalamin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydroxocobalamin</strong></p><p><strong>Hydroxocobalamin </strong>and<strong> sodium thiosulfate</strong> are the preferred antidotes in cyanide toxicity.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If hydroxocobalamin is available,  give <strong>hydroxocobalamin </strong>and<strong> sodium thiosulfate. </strong></li>\n<li>If hydroxocobalamin is not available,<strong> </strong>give<strong> amyl nitrite </strong>and<strong> sodium nitrite </strong>and<strong> sodium thiosulfate. </strong>Avoid nitrites if the patient has contraindications to nitrites.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydroxocobalamin</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> over sodium nitrite as it does not interfere with the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. Also, sodium nitrite can induce fatal methemoglobinemia in patients who are critically ill from another cause.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of antidotes:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 516px; width: 445px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Mechanism of antidote</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 18px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 47px;\">Binding of cyanide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 47px;\">Direct binding of cyanide, followed by clearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 47px;\">Hydroxocobalamin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 98px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 98px;\">Induction of methemoglobinemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 98px;\">When cyanide binds methemoglobin, a relatively less toxic cyanomethemoglobin is formed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 98px;\">\n<p>Sodium nitrite</p>\n<p>Amyl nitrite</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 147px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 147px;\">Use of sulfur donors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 147px;\">This involves maximizing the availability of sulfur donors for rhodanese, a ubiquitous enzyme that detoxifies cyanide by transforming it to thiocyanate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 147px;\">Sodium thiosulfate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Dicobalt EDTA </strong>can also bind cyanide, but it causes severe side effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3775","text":"In the following oxygen-di‍ssociation curve, which c​urve indicates poisoning ‌with carbon monoxide?","raw_text":"In the following oxygen-dissociation curve, which curve indicates poisoning with carbon monoxide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cur‍ve A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cur‍ve B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cur‍ve C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cur‍ve D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Curve C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01bbc08ae43f4c8aba83db6919d1ccb8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Curve C</strong></p><p>Curve <strong>C</strong> indicates poisoning with <strong>carbon monoxide </strong>(CO).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">CO binds to hemoglobin and <strong>prevents</strong><strong> O<sub><span class=\"s1\">2 </span></sub>binding </strong>(therefore the decrease in the Vmax). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">CO binds to hemoglobin with an affinity that is <strong>250 times</strong> that of O<sub><span class=\"s1\">2 </span></sub> to form <strong>carboxyhemoglobin</strong>. This means that when the partial pressure of CO is only 1/250 that of O<span class=\"s1\">2</span>, equal amounts of CO and O<span class=\"s1\">2 </span>will bind to hemoglobin. Thus, the hemoglobin that can now bind to O<span class=\"s1\">2 </span>is reduced to 50% causing a <strong>reduction in the graph's height</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">CO also <strong>increases the O<sub>2</sub> affinity</strong> of remaining free hemoglobin sites which causes a left <strong>shift<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></strong>of the O<sub><span class=\"s1\">2</span></sub> - hemoglobin dissociation curve.</p>\n<p>Curve A - Myoglobin O<sub>2</sub>-dissociation curve (Myoglobin has a higher affinity towards O<sub><span class=\"s1\">2 </span></sub><span class=\"s1\">than hemoglobin. Hence, it shows a hyperbolic curve with a left shift)</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Curve B - Normal Hb O</span><sub style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">2</sub><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">-dissociation curve </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Curve D - Curve showing anemia</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3776","text":"Which of the following mec‍hanisms is responsible fo​r the rebound effect seen‌ with carbon monoxide poi​soning?","raw_text":"Which of the following mechanisms is responsible for the rebound effect seen with carbon monoxide poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binding of carbon monoxide‍ to myoglobin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Binding of carbon monoxide‍ to hemoglobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of cytochrome o‍xidase a3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conversion of hemoglobin f‍rom T state to R state","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Binding of carbon monoxide to myoglobin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Binding of carbon monoxide to myoglobin</strong></p><p> <strong>Rebound effect </strong>in carbon monoxide poisoning is due to binding of <strong>carbon monoxide </strong>to<strong> myoglobin</strong>. </p>\n<p>About 15% of CO present in extracellular tissues combines with myoglobin. A rebound effect with delayed return of symptoms is due to late release of CO from myoglobin with subsequent binding to hemoglobin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3777","text":"Blood carboxyhemoglobin le‍vel is measured in a pati​ent who is rescued from a‌ burning building. Beyond​ which level would it be ​considered fatal?","raw_text":"Blood carboxyhemoglobin level is measured in a patient who is rescued from a burning building. Beyond which level would it be considered fatal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‍5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7‍0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6‍0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4‍0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60%</strong></p><p>Carbon monoxide poisoning is fatal above carboxyhemoglobin levels of <strong>60%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Normal carboxyhemoglobin levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3%</strong> in nonsmokers</li>\n<li><strong>10-15%</strong> in smokers</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Serious toxicity</strong> is associated with levels more than <strong>25%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1021","text":"You notice the given post ‍mortem finding in a 45-ye​ar-old patient. Which of ‌the following is the like​ly cause for this?","raw_text":"You notice the given post mortem finding in a 45-year-old patient. Which of the following is the likely cause for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitr‍ites","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbon ‍monoxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen‍ sulphide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Potassium‍ chlorate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carbon monoxide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/86f5d7a11b9544ffb8f46bf6d8e80d9d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carbon monoxide</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>cherry red</strong> postmortem lividity. It is characteristic of <strong>carbon monoxide poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1026","text":"On autopsy of a person wit‍h suspected poisoning, a ​characteristic finding of‌ softening of basal gangl​ia is found. Which of the​ following is most likely‍ responsible?","raw_text":"On autopsy of a person with suspected poisoning, a characteristic finding of softening of basal ganglia is found. Which of the following is most likely responsible?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon monox‍ide poisoning","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphorus‍ poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen sulfide poisoning‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid p‍oisoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbon monoxide poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbon monoxide poisoning</strong></p><p>Softening of the basal ganglia is a characteristic postmortem finding in case of <strong>carbon monoxide poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Softening</strong> of <strong>basal ganglia</strong> is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbon monoxide poisoning</li>\n<li>Cyanide poisoning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1024","text":"A young woman presents wit‍h a headache and flu-like​ symptoms. Her symptoms b‌egan after she installed ​a gasoline-powered electr​ical generator inside her‍ home. Which of the follo‌wing is most likely in th‌is patient?","raw_text":"A young woman presents with a headache and flu-like symptoms. Her symptoms began after she installed a gasoline-powered electrical generator inside her home. Which of the following is most likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A decrease in oxygen satur‍ation on pulse oximetry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A positive Hoppe-Seyler's ‍test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A decrease in dissolved ox‍ygen levels on ABG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necrosis of the cerebellum‍ on CT brain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A positive Hoppe-Seyler's test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A positive Hoppe-Seyler&#x27;s test</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely suffering from<strong> carbon monoxide poisoning </strong>due to the usage of generators indoors<strong>. Hoppe-Seyler's test</strong> can be used for the diagnosis of CO poisoning.</p>\n<p>Tests that may be used for the diagnosis of CO poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hoppe-Seyler's test</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Kunkel's test</strong></li>\n<li>Spectroscopy</li>\n<li>Potassium ferricyanide test</li>\n<li>Katayama's test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Pulse oximetry<strong> </strong>should never be used to evaluate CO poisoning as a standard pulse oximeter <strong>cannot distinguish</strong> between <strong>oxyhemoglobin</strong> and <strong>carboxyhemoglobin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: An arterial blood gas analysis will show <strong>no decrease</strong> in the levels of <strong>dissolved oxygen</strong>, as this is not affected by CO. <strong>CO decreases the oxygen saturation</strong> of hemoglobin but has no effects on oxygen dissolved in plasma.</p>\n<p>Option D: CT brain in a case of CO poisoning shows <strong>necrosis</strong> of the <strong>globus pallidus.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Never use gasoline-powered electrical generators indoors, even if windows and doors are open. CO is a colorless, odorless gas that can kill without warning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3780","text":"A 55-year-old male is brou‍ght to the emergency depa​rtment with a history of ‌fire-related smoke inhala​tion. Which of the follow​ing is not an indication ‍for hyperbaric oxygen the‌rapy in this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old male is brought to the emergency department with a history of fire-related smoke inhalation. Which of the following is not an indication for hyperbaric oxygen therapy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of co‍nsciousness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"COHb lev‍el of 17%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severe metabolic acidosis ‍with ph 6.9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ST, T wave changes in elec‍trocardiogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. COHb level of 17%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. COHb level of 17%</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>carbon monoxide poisoning</strong>. Carboxyhemoglobin levels more than <strong>25%</strong> is an indication for hyperbaric oxygen therapy in carbon monoxide poisoning.</p>\n<p>Indications for <strong>hyperbaric oxygen</strong> therapy in <strong>carbon monoxide poisoning</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CO level more than 25%</li>\n<li>CO level more than 15% in pregnant women</li>\n<li>Loss of consciousness</li>\n<li>Severe metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Evidence of end-organ ischemia (eg, ECG changes, chest pain, or altered mental status).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"X2NIDny1","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"17_disorders_of_parathyroid_and_calcium_homeostasis","title":"17.Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB7247","text":"Hypercalcemia is associate‌d with all of the followi​ng medical conditions exc​ept:","raw_text":"Hypercalcemia is associated with all of the following medical conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypothyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> is associated with all of the following conditions <strong>except hypothyroidism</strong>. Hyperthyroidism is however associated with hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>1) <strong>Multiple myeloma</strong> is a hemotological malignancy associated with hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>2) <strong>Hyperparathyroidism</strong> is associated with sub-periosteal bone resorption resulting in hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>3) <strong> Sarcoidosis </strong>is associated with hypercalcemia due to increased activity of 1 alpha hydroxylase activity in the macrophages. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7250","text":"Which of the following can‌ cause hypercalcemia in a​ patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following can cause hypercalcemia in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin D ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin A i‌ntoxication","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin C i‌ntoxication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin A intoxication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin A intoxication</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin A intoxication</strong> can cause <strong>hypercalcemia. </strong>This is due to <strong>high bone turnover</strong>. Ingestion of 50,000–100,000 units of Vitamin A daily can increase calcium levels up to 12–14 mg/dL.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Vitamin D</strong> intoxication also leads to hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7256","text":"A 66-year-old woman is bro‌ught to the emergency dep​artment after being found​ unconscious by her neigh‌bors. She is found to hav​e marked dehydration and ​serum calcium levels of 1‍6 mg/dl. All of the follo​wing can cause this prese‍ntation except:","raw_text":"A 66-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after being found unconscious by her neighbors. She is found to have marked dehydration and serum calcium levels of 16 mg/dl. All of the following can cause this presentation except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metastatic breast carcinom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancre‌atitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hodgkin’s‌ lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pancreatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pancreatitis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with <strong>severe</strong> <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> (<strong>&gt; 14.8 </strong>mg/dl) is suggestive of a <strong>hypercalcemic crisis</strong>. <strong>Pancreatitis</strong> causes <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>, not hypercalcemia.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Hypercalcemic crisis is often caused by <strong>malignancies</strong> due to an acute rise in calcium levels following bone resorption due to metastases.<strong> </strong>It is<strong> </strong>a <strong>medical emergency</strong> that presents with polyuria, <strong>severe dehydration</strong>, gastrointestinal symptoms, and <strong>altered consciousness</strong>. It can lead to cardiac arrest and coma. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7258","text":"Which of the following wil‌l not be seen in a patien​t with hypercalcemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following will not be seen in a patient with hypercalcemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poly‌uria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diar‌rhoea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vomi‌ting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diarrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diarrhoea</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> is associated with <strong>constipation</strong> and not diarrhea.</p>\n<p>Clinical manifestations of hypercalcemia include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 348px; width: 322px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; text-align: center;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 113px;\">Renal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Renal stones (Calcium oxalate &gt; calcium phosphate)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Nephrocalcinosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\"><strong>Polyuria</strong> and dehydration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Renal failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 113px;\">Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Bone pain, bone cysts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Myalgia and arthralgia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Osteopenia, osteoporosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 113px;\">GIT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\"><strong>Constipation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Nausea, <strong>vomiting</strong>, anorexia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Abdominal pain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Peptic ulcer disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Acute pancreatitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 113px;\">Neuropsychiatric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Anxiety, <strong>depression</strong>, fatigue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Cognitive dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Drowsiness, obtundation, coma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 113px;\">Cardiac</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Short QT interval, bradycardia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Others</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Ectopic calcification </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic '<strong>stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychic moans'.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7266","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in patients foll​owing excessive intake of​ calcium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients following excessive intake of calcium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic re‌nal failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Milk-alkal‌i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burnett's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>Excess calcium ingestion is <strong>not associated</strong> with <strong>restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong>. It results in<strong> milk-alkali syndrome,</strong> commonly associated with excessive intake of absorbable antacids such as<strong> milk </strong>or<strong> calcium carbonate</strong>.</p>\n<p>In susceptible individuals, there is a loss of negative feedback, leading to <strong>excess calcium absorption</strong> when there is excess intake. It results in the following triad of clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalcemia </li>\n<li>Alkalosis - due to HCO3 reabsorption caused by PTH suppression, natriuresis, and water depletion.</li>\n<li><strong>Renal failure </strong>- due to damage to renal tubules.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is reversible once ingestion of antacid is stopped. However, in severe cases, a chronic form called <strong>Burnett’s syndrome </strong>can develop. It is associated with <strong>irreversible renal damage.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7268","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of primary hyperparat​hyroidism?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parathyroid‌ hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parathyro‌id adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"MEN 1 s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iatrogenic‌ conditions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parathyroid adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parathyroid adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Parathyroid adenomas</strong> are the most common cause of <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of primary hyperparathyroidism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parathyroid adenomas: These maybe\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Solitary (more common) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multiple </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Part of MEN-1, MEN-2A or MEN-4</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Chief cell parathyroid hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Parathyroid gland carcinoma</li>\n<li>Lithium use</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumor (HPT-JT) syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7270","text":"Which of the following set‌s of lab values will be s​een in a patient with sec​ondary hyperparathyroidis‌m?","raw_text":"Which of the following sets of lab values will be seen in a patient with secondary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Option 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/11928fe152444e8e988fe5d8922d3611.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Option 3</strong></p><p><strong>Secondary </strong><strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong> presents with <strong>decreased calcium</strong> and <strong>increased</strong> levels of <strong>PTH</strong>, <strong>phosphate</strong>, and <strong>ALP</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this condition, calcium deficiency leads to reactive stimulation of the parathyroid. It is caused by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic kidney disease</li>\n<li>Vitamin D deficiency</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal malabsorption</li>\n<li>Liver disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7273","text":"All of the following state‌ments are true about prim​ary hyperparathyroidism e​xcept:","raw_text":"All of the following statements are true about primary hyperparathyroidism except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It causes prolongation of ‌QT interval","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Asymptomatic disease can o‌ccur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peptic ulcer disease is se‌en","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperuricem‌ia can occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It causes prolongation of QT interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It causes prolongation of QT interval</strong></p><p><strong>Primary hyperparathyroidism</strong> (PHPT) causes <strong>shortening</strong> of the<strong> QT interval</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>asymptomatic </strong>form is the <strong>most common</strong> presentation in the <strong>Western</strong> population. But in developing countries, the patients are usually symptomatic. The classical symptoms and signs of PHPT are due to hypercalcemia as well as increased parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels.</p>\n<p>The features of <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 416.828125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bone pain</li>\n<li>Skeletal deformities</li>\n<li>Pathological fractures</li>\n<li>Myalgia and arthralgia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Renal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Renal stones(Calcium oxalate &gt; calcium phosphate)</li>\n<li>Nephrocalcinosis</li>\n<li>Polyuria and dehydration</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">GIT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting, anorexia</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li><strong>Peptic ulcer disease</strong></li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Neuropsychiatric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Anxiety, depression</li>\n<li>Cognitive dysfunction</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Cardiac</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Short QT interval</strong></li>\n<li>bradycardia</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Ectopic calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cornea: Band keratopathy</li>\n<li>Extravascular tissues: calcinosis</li>\n<li>Small arteries: calciphylaxis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The features of<strong> elevated PTH levels</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteitis fibrosa cystica</li>\n<li>Osteopenia/osteoporosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperuricemia</strong> and <strong>gout</strong> </li>\n<li>Pseudogout</li>\n<li>Increased production of calcitriol  </li>\n<li>Nephrolithiasis</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatemia</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic -<strong> “bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones”</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7272","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld you expect in a patien​t with primary hyperparat​hyroidism?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you expect in a patient with primary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galls‌tones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent‌ abortions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuropsychia‌tric changes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuropsychiatric changes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuropsychiatric changes</strong></p><p><strong>Neuropsychiatric</strong> changes are seen in patients with <strong>primary</strong> <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong> (PHPT).</p>\n<p>The clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic -<strong> “bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones”</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone pain</li>\n<li>Skeletal deformities</li>\n<li>Pathological fractures</li>\n<li>Myalgia and arthralgia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Renal</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Renal stones(Calcium oxalate &gt; calcium phosphate)</li>\n<li>Nephrocalcinosis</li>\n<li>Polyuria and dehydration</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GIT</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting, anorexia</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Peptic ulcer disease</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neuropsychiatric</strong></td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Anxiety, depression</li>\n<li>Cognitive dysfunction</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cardiac</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Short QT interval</li>\n<li>bradycardia</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ectopic calcification</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Cornea: Band keratopathy</li>\n<li>Extravascular tissues: calcinosis</li>\n<li>Small arteries: calciphylaxis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Though multiple clinical studies have found an increased incidence of gall stones in primary HPT, there is no reference to this in Harrison's and Williams's endocrinology. Hence, option D would be the most appropriate answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7487","text":"Calcitonin levels are incr‌eased in:","raw_text":"Calcitonin levels are increased in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoparat‌hyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cushing's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>Calcitonin levels are increased in <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong>. </p>\n<p>Due to <strong>elevated parathyroid hormone </strong>(PTH) levels in hyperparathyroidism, there is <strong>osteoclast activation</strong>. This leads to <strong>increased calcium</strong> levels in plasma. As a response to this, there is an <strong>increase</strong> in the level of <strong>calcitonin</strong> to regulate the high calcium levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Calcitonin</strong> is secreted by the parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid gland. Its principal effects are to lower serum calcium and phosphate by its action on bone and kidney. In <strong>bone</strong>, calcitonin <strong>inhibits osteoclasts</strong> and <strong>reduces bone resorption</strong>. In the <strong>kidney</strong>, it <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>reabsorption</strong> of both <strong>calcium and phosphate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong>, the direct effect of thyroid hormones can lead to bone resorption and <strong>mild hypercalcemia</strong>. However, hyperparathyroidism is a better answer.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: <strong>Hypoparathyroidism</strong> and <strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong> will have <strong>low serum calcium</strong> and hence, will not have <strong>increased calcitonin</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7274","text":"The spinal x-ray of a pati‌ent with elevated ALP lev​els is shown below. What ​is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The spinal x-ray of a patient with elevated ALP levels is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paget's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteop‌etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03b3c7200ad042e9a66b07dddba62143.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The above x-ray shows a <strong>rugger jersey spine</strong> which is a distinct feature of<strong> hyperparathyroidism. </strong>It is associated with <strong>elevated</strong> <strong>ALP</strong>.</p>\n<p>It refers to the prominent <strong>hyperdensities</strong> at the <strong>endplates</strong> of successive <strong>vertebrae</strong>, which mimics the striped appearance of a rugby jersey. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Paget's</strong> disease causes cortical thickening, which results in increased opacity on all sides of the vertebral body. These are called <strong>picture frame vertebrae</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Osteopetrosis</strong> presents with <strong>normal ALP</strong> levels. It results in sclerotic endplates of vertebrae that are termed <strong>sandwich vertebrae</strong>. Though this finding is similar to rugger jersey spine, it is denser and more sharply defined than rugger jersey spine. In addition, there is sclerosis of inner margins of vertebral bodies, resulting in a <strong>bone within a bone</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ankylosing spondylitis produces a bamboo spine appearance due to fusion of vertebral bodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7276","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ts with worsening pain an​d an inability to bear we​ight on her left leg. She‌ says that she had banged​ her leg against a chair ​3 days ago. X-ray reveals‍ a fracture of her left t​ibia. In view of this fin‍ding, a skeletal survey w‌as performed and reveals ​the following additional ‍finding. What is the like​ly cause?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with worsening pain and an inability to bear weight on her left leg. She says that she had banged her leg against a chair 3 days ago. X-ray reveals a fracture of her left tibia. In view of this finding, a skeletal survey was performed and reveals the following additional finding. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal oste‌odystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom‌alacia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoparat‌hyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6093631bd6c46e59fb854e3c505d320.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a pathological fracture of the tibia. The given x-ray shows <strong>brown tumors</strong> or <strong>osteitis fibrosa cystica</strong>, which are characteristic of<strong> hyperparathyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7275","text":"This x-ray finding in a pa‌tient is characteristic o​f which of the following ​conditions?","raw_text":"This x-ray finding in a patient is characteristic of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Down's ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acrom‌egaly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acromegaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/955d46f81486420e9a4ea1bbbf794e47.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acromegaly</strong></p><p>The arrows in this x-ray point to <strong>arrow-headed fingers</strong> or the <strong>spade phalanx sign.</strong> It is pathognomonic of <strong>acromegaly</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Acromegaly is the result of excessive growth hormone (GH) production, most commonly from a macroadenoma of the pituitary. There is osseous enlargement, presenting with spade-like hands and <strong>widening</strong> of the <strong>terminal phalangeal tufts</strong>, giving an<strong> </strong>arrow-head appearance.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other features in the hand include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Widened joint spaces (due to cartilage hypertrophy)</li>\n<li>Generalized osteoporosis and cystic changes in the carpal bones</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Hyperparathyroidism causes <strong>subperiosteal bone resorption,</strong> which is marked along the radial aspect of the middle phalanges of the index and middle fingers.</p>\n<p>Option B: Down's syndrome can cause hypoplasia of the middle phalanx of the 5th finger.</p>\n<p>Option C: Psoriasis causes a pencil-in-cup appearance of the distal phalanx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7278","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used to treat a patie​nt with hypercalcemia due​ to primary hyperparathyr‌oidism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used to treat a patient with hypercalcemia due to primary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forced diuresis with loop ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pamid‌ronate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydration ‌with saline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucocorticoids</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong><strong> </strong>have<strong> no effect </strong>on the serum<strong> calcium </strong>concentration in <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong> and normal individuals. </p>\n<p>They are effective in the treatment of hypercalcemia when associated with the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignancy</li>\n<li>Vitamin D intoxication</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Initial medical management of hypercalcemia involves the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Normal</strong> <strong>saline</strong> (200-500 ml/hour): It<strong> </strong>is the mainstay treatment to correct dehydration, and titrated to maintain a urine output of &gt;100 ml/hour</li>\n<li><strong>Pamidronate</strong> or <strong>zoledronate</strong>: These are <strong>bisphosphonates</strong> and are considered as the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in primary hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li><strong>Cinacalcet</strong>: This is a calcimimetic that increases the affinity of the glands to extracellular calcium, thereby decreasing parathyroid hormone secretion</li>\n<li><strong>Denosumab</strong>:<strong> </strong>This inhibits RANK-ligand, which acts on osteoclasts</li>\n<li><strong>Calcitonin</strong>: This is an activated vitamin D analog that may be used to reduce calcium levels. It should be used within the first 24 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Forced diuresis</strong> refers to aggressive fluid hydration with loop diuretic administration. Although this is an <strong>effective</strong> method to <strong>reduce calcium</strong> levels, it requires careful monitoring for adverse effects like <strong>fluid overload</strong>, <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, and hypomagnesia. Due to this reason as well as the availability of more effective treatment modalities, forced diuresis is <strong>not preferred</strong> any longer.</p>\n<p>The <strong>definitive</strong> management of primary hyperparathyroidism is the <strong>surgical removal</strong> of the parathyroid gland.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0232","text":"Which of the following is ‌used in the treatment of ​hypercalcemia due to Vita​min D toxicity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used in the treatment of hypercalcemia due to Vitamin D toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ketoco‌nazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dexamethasone</strong></p><p>In patients with <strong>hypercalcemia </strong>due to<strong> vitamin D toxicity</strong>, glucocorticoids like <strong>dexamethasone</strong> are the preferred therapy as they <strong>decrease 1,25 (OH2) vitamin D</strong> (calcitriol) production. This results in r<strong>educed intestinal calcium absorption</strong> and <strong>increased urinary excretion of calcium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hydroxychloroquine, chloroquine, and ketoconazole may also decrease calcitriol production and are used occasionally. But the <strong>preferred therapy</strong> is <strong>steroids</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin D toxicity</strong> presents acutely with <strong>features of hypercalcemia</strong> such as <strong>confusion</strong>, <strong>polyuria</strong>, <strong>polydipsia</strong>, anorexia, vomiting, and muscle weakness. <strong>Chronic intoxication</strong> may cause <strong>nephrocalcinosis</strong>, bone demineralization, and pain.</p>\n<p>Lab features include <strong>elevated 25 (OH) vitamin D</strong>, <strong>high calcium</strong>, and <strong>suppressed PTH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dexamethasone can also be used in the treatment of hypercalcemia caused by the humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy (due to PTHr) and sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7279","text":"A patient is brought to th‌e emergency department wi​th confusion, drowsiness,​ and multiple episodes of‌ vomiting. His family mem​bers say that he was diag​nosed with bipolar disord‍er several years ago and ​has been on medication ev‍er since. Further evaluat‌ion reveals a serum calci​um level of 16 mg/dl. Wha‍t should be the next step​ in the management of thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the emergency department with confusion, drowsiness, and multiple episodes of vomiting. His family members say that he was diagnosed with bipolar disorder several years ago and has been on medication ever since. Further evaluation reveals a serum calcium level of 16 mg/dl. What should be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saline h‌ydration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deno‌sumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV bipho‌sphonate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subcutaneou‌s calcitonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Saline hydration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Saline hydration</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>hypercalcemic crisis.</strong> The next best step in management is <strong>saline hydration</strong>. Typically 200-500 ml/hour fluids are given to correct dehydration and titrated to maintain a urine output of &gt;100 ml/hour.</p>\n<p>Hypercalcemic crisis is<strong> </strong>a <strong>medical emergency</strong> associated with serum <strong>calcium &gt;14 </strong>mg/dl. It is often caused by conditions causing an acute rise in calcium levels such as <strong>malignancies</strong> or <strong>lithium</strong> overdose.</p>\n<p>It presents with oliguria, severe dehydration, gastrointestinal symptoms, and altered consciousness. It can lead to<strong> cardiac arrest </strong>and <strong>coma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Initial medical management of hypercalcemia involves the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluids are the mainstay treatment</li>\n<li>Bisphosphonates</li>\n<li>Cinacalcet</li>\n<li>Denosumab</li>\n<li>Calcitonin </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7280","text":"In a patient with hypocalc‌emia and hyperphosphatemi​a, which of the following​ will you not suspect?","raw_text":"In a patient with hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, which of the following will you not suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin D-dependent ricket‌s type 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudohypopa‌rathyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic kid‌ney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DiGeorge‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1</strong> is associated with hypocalcemia and <strong>hypophosphatemia</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hypocalcemia </strong>with<strong> hyperphosphatemia </strong>is observed in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>True hypoparathyroidism\n<ul>\n<li>Hereditary hypoparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Acquired hypoparathyroidism – following surgery</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudohypoparathyroidism</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chronic kidney disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7281","text":"All of the following medic‌al conditions are associa​ted with hyperphosphatemi​a except:","raw_text":"All of the following medical conditions are associated with hyperphosphatemia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic re‌nal failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged phosphate intake‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudopseudohypoparathyroi‌dism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudohypopa‌rathyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism</strong> is a type of pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1A associated with <strong>normal calcium </strong>and <strong>normal phosphate</strong>. The clinical features are similar to pseudohypoparathyroidism but there is<strong> no PTH resistance</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pseudohypoparathyroidism</strong> is an autosomal dominant condition caused by a <strong>mutation</strong> of the <strong>alpha subunit</strong> of the<strong> Gs protein</strong>. This causes <strong>PTH</strong> <strong>resistance</strong>. It is associated with <strong>Albright hereditary osteodystrophy</strong> (short stature, round facies, and short fourth metacarpal).</p>\n<p>Types of pseudohypoparathyroidism:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 174px; width: 441px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">PHP 1A</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">PHP 1B</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">PHP 1C</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">PHP 2</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">PPHP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">Hypocalcemia</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">Hyperphosphatemia</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">PTH levels</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">GS α subunit deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">No </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">Albright hereditary osteodystrophy</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 130px;\">PTH resistance</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 78px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 81px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 82.203125px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 40.796875px;\">Partial</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 66px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>PHP: pseudohypoparathyroidism, PPHP: pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism, PTH: parathyroid hormone</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7282","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brough‌t by his mother with with​ the following abnormalit​y. Serum levels of vitami‌n D are found to be low. ​Which of the following wo​uld not be seen in this b‍oy?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy is brought by his mother with with the following abnormality. Serum levels of vitamin D are found to be low. Which of the following would not be seen in this boy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in parathyroid ho‌rmone level","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in renal calcium ‌excretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in renal phosphat‌e excretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in serum alkaline‌ phosphatase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decrease in parathyroid hormone level","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7276beb0e5e24793ae4d416c16177005.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e33bf8ce8be8488ab64ae81136fdf9c9x1280x1405.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decrease in parathyroid hormone level</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>windswept deformity</strong> of the lower limbs (combination of valgus deformity of one leg with varus deformity of the other leg). This finding along with low serum vitamin D points to a diagnosis of<strong> vitamin D deficiency rickets</strong>. It is associated with an <strong>increase </strong>in<strong> </strong>parathyroid hormone (<strong>PTH</strong>) levels.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7283","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect with​ respect to hypocalcemia?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect with respect to hypocalcemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can occur due to hypermagn‌esemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Latent tetan‌y is observed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged ‌QT interval","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Not a feature of hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Not a feature of hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Not a feature of hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Hypocalcemia </strong>is a feature of <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism </strong>(SHPT).</p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia refers to total serum <strong>calcium ≤8.4 </strong>mg/dl. The causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low PTH\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoparathyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>High PTH</strong> (SHPT)\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic kidney disease </strong></li>\n<li>Vitamin D deficiency</li>\n<li>Pseudohypoparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Loop diuretics, calcitonin, bisphosphonates</li>\n<li>Massive blood transfusions</li>\n<li>Hyperventilation</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia - Mg is essential for PTH release</li>\n<li>Severe <strong>hypermagnesemia</strong> - higher Mg levels suppress PTH secretion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hypocalcemia causes tetany or increased neuromuscular excitability. It can cause paresthesias, cramps, spasms, and seizures. <strong>Latent tetany</strong> can only be detected by performing <strong>Chvostek</strong> and <strong>Trousseau's</strong> signs.<strong> </strong>A <strong>prolonged QT</strong> interval is seen on ECG.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7285","text":"The following sign is elic‌ited in a patient. What i​s it called?","raw_text":"The following sign is elicited in a patient. What is it called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chvoste‌k’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Troussea‌u's sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tinel'‌s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Troisie‌r’s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trousseau's sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43e3dd9f8b524397b9ca03f9564f1d13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trousseau&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>The above image indicates <strong>Trousseau's sign </strong>or<strong> carpopedal spasm</strong> classically seen in patients with <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Trousseau's sign is elicited by making the forearm ischemic by applying a sphygmomanometer cuff to the arm and keeping the pressure <strong>above</strong> systolic blood pressure (<strong>SBP</strong>) for <strong>5 minutes</strong>. The hand goes into a painful carpopedal spasm and assumes the <strong>accoucheur’s hand position</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia results in <strong>tetany</strong> or increased <strong>neuromuscular</strong> <strong>irritability</strong>. It leads to <strong>paresthesia</strong> and tingling of extremities and <strong>perioral</strong> area. Severe hypocalcemia can cause <strong>seizures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Chvostek's sign is also seen in <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>. Tapping the facial nerve in front of the tragus will cause <strong>spasms</strong> of the <strong>facial muscles.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C: Tinel's sign is seen in carpal tunnel syndrome. It refers to a tingling sensation on tapping over the median nerve.</p>\n<p>Option D: Troisier's sign refers to left supraclavicular lymphadenopathy in metastatic abdominal malignancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7286","text":"Which of the following age‌nts cannot be used to man​age a patient with chroni​c hypocalcemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents cannot be used to manage a patient with chronic hypocalcemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etidr‌onate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thia‌zide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elementa‌l calcium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin D‌ analogues","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Etidronate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Etidronate</strong></p><p><strong>Etidronate</strong> is not used in the management of hypocalcemia. It is a <strong>bisphosphonate</strong> used in the management of <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The goal in the management of hypoparathyroidism is to restore the <strong>calcium-phosphate balance</strong>. The mainstay is <strong>calcium </strong>and<strong> vitamin D supplements</strong>. Other underlying conditions must also be treated.</p>\n<p>Instead of loop diuretics, <strong>thiazides</strong> can be administered on a low sodium diet as they enhance <strong>urinary calcium</strong> <strong>reabsorption</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Svyu2Nt1","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"17_miscellaneous_bacteria___yersinia__brucella__bartonella__legionella","title":"17.Miscellaneous Bacteria   Yersinia, Brucella, Bartonella, Legionella","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA2801","text":"The milk ring test is foun‌d to be positive for a sa​mple of milk. Which organ​ism has it most likely be‌en contaminated with?","raw_text":"The milk ring test is found to be positive for a sample of milk. Which organism has it most likely been contaminated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bruc‌ella","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Borde‌tella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Baci‌llus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burkho‌lderia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brucella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f746e2d6df3441e08f72097d85d4acedx1280x1680.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brucella</strong></p><p><strong>Milk ring test </strong>(MRT) is <strong>positive</strong> for <strong><em>Brucella</em>. </strong>It is used for the detection of infected animals in dairy farms. </p>\n<p>A sample of <strong>whole milk</strong> is mixed with a drop of <strong><em>Brucella</em> antigen</strong> and incubated at <strong>70 °C for 40–50 min</strong>. If antibodies are present in the milk, the bacilli are agglutinated and rise with the cream to form a <strong>blue ring at the top</strong>, leaving the rest of the milk unstained (<strong>MRT positive</strong>). If antibodies are absent, the solution remains <strong>uniformly blue (MRT negative)</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2799","text":"Which of the following is ‌an intracellular pathogen​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an intracellular pathogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brucel‌la suis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brucella suis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brucella suis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Brucella suis</strong> </em>is primarily an <strong>intracellular pathogen</strong> affecting <strong>reticuloendothelial system</strong>. This accounts for its refractoriness to chemotherapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2797","text":"A farmer was diagnosed wit‌h brucellosis. Which of t​he following patterns of ​fever will be seen in thi‌s disease?","raw_text":"A farmer was diagnosed with brucellosis. Which of the following patterns of fever will be seen in this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pel-Ebst‌ein fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Undulan‌t fever","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Relapsi‌ng fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saddleba‌ck fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Undulant fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Undulant fever</strong></p><p><strong>Brucellosis</strong> is a bacterial zoonosis caused by<strong> </strong><em>Brucella sp</em> which presents with <strong>undulant fever</strong>. It is referred to as undulant fever because of the <strong>rising</strong> and <strong>falling </strong>of fever like a wave <strong>(remittent</strong> fever). Patients may also have arthralgia, muscular pain, sweating, loss of weight, and appetite. CNS symptoms such as fatigue, irritability, and depression might also occur. </p>\n<p>Other options</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pel-Ebstein</strong> fever is seen in <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong>. It is characterized by fever lasting 3–10 days followed by afebrile periods of 3–10 days.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Relapsing</strong> fever is seen in infection with <em><strong>Borrelia recurrentis</strong> </em>in which days of fever are followed by a several-day afebrile period and then relapse into additional days of fever. A similar picture is also seen in malaria.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Saddleback</strong> fever is seen in <strong>dengue</strong>.  Saddleback fever is defined as temperature &gt;37.5°C  with defervescence of at least one day, followed by a second peak lasting at least one day.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2803","text":"A 56-year-old dairy farmer‌ presented with remittent​ fever and night sweats f​or 1 month. On examinatio‌n, he was hypotensive and‍ pallor was present. Rose​ Bengal card tests done o‌n milk samples from his f‌arm were found to be posi‌tive. Which blood culture​ method with a biphasic m‍edium will be used to iso​late the organism?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old dairy farmer presented with remittent fever and night sweats for 1 month. On examination, he was hypotensive and pallor was present. Rose Bengal card tests done on milk samples from his farm were found to be positive. Which blood culture method with a biphasic medium will be used to isolate the organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BACTEC‌ method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Castaned‌a method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Septi-che‌ck method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxoid sig‌nal method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Castaneda method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a71a46610af24fd49f000f537b4f4980x720x837.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Castaneda method</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of remittent fever with anemia with a positive Rose Bengal card test is suggestive of <strong>brucellosis. Castaneda method</strong> for <strong>blood culture</strong> is used to isolate <em>Brucella</em> sp.</p>\n<p>In the Castaneda method, blood is inoculated into a bottle of <strong>trypticase soy broth</strong> or brucella broth in a <strong>biphasic blood culture</strong> bottle (with liquid and solid media in the same bottle). This method minimizes materials and manipulation, thus <strong>reducing</strong> the chances of <strong>contamination</strong> and <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>infection</strong> to laboratory workers.</p>\n<p>Blood cultures are <strong>positive in only 30–50%</strong> of cases even when repeated samples are tested.<em> B.melitensis</em> and <em>B.suis</em> are isolated more readily than<em> B.abortus.</em> The below image shows Castaneda's bottle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Lab diagnosis</strong> of brucellosis is done using the following methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood culture</strong> with\n<ul>\n<li>Castaneda's method - recommended</li>\n<li>BACTEC - an automated method. It is faster than Castaneda's method.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Serological tests</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Standard agglutination test (SAT)</li>\n<li>ELISA for specific IgG/IgM</li>\n<li>Dipstick assays</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Detection in milk and infected cattle</strong> is carried out by:\n<ul>\n<li>Microscopic demonstration by staining or immunofluorescence</li>\n<li>Milk ring test</li>\n<li>Rose Bengal card test </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2827","text":"A 50-year-old farmer prese‌nts with fever, weight lo​ss, and lower backache fo​r three weeks. Blood repo‌rts show predominant lymp‍hocytosis. What are the d​rugs of choice for treati‌ng this condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old farmer presents with fever, weight loss, and lower backache for three weeks. Blood reports show predominant lymphocytosis. What are the drugs of choice for treating this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cotrimoxazole and rifampic‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxycycline and cotrimoxaz‌ole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxycycline and streptomyc‌in","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Streptomycin and rifampici‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Doxycycline and streptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Doxycycline and streptomycin</strong></p><p>The given scenario of a farmer with fever, weightloss and back pain with a predominant lymphocytosis points towards a diagnosis of <strong>acute brucellosis.</strong> The <strong>drugs of choice</strong> for this condition in adults are <strong>intramuscular streptomycin</strong> with <strong>doxycycline. </strong></p>\n<p>The usual <strong>alternative</strong> regimen (and the current World Health Organization recommendation) is <strong>rifampin plus doxycycline</strong>. However, this is associated with a <strong>higher relapse</strong> rate, possibly because rifampicin increases clearance of doxycycline and reduces serum doxycycline levels. </p>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong>, <strong>cotrimoxazole</strong> along with <strong>rifampicin</strong> or gentamycin may be used for the treatment of brucellosis.</p>\n<p>Note:<em> B. melitensis</em> is<strong> endemic</strong> to the <strong>Middle East</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6103","text":"A 34-year-old man presents‌ to the hospital with fev​er, joint pain and a rash​ on his hands. He gives a‌ history of a rat bite 1 ‍week back. Which of the f​ollowing can cause this d‌isease? Pasteurella multo‌cida Streptobacillus moni‌liformis Bartonella hense​lae Borrelia burgdorferi ‍Spirillum minus","raw_text":"<p>A 34-year-old man presents to the hospital with fever, joint pain and a rash on his hands. He gives a history of a rat bite 1 week back. Which of the following can cause this disease?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Pasteurella multocida</em></li>\n<li><em>Streptobacillus moniliformis</em></li>\n<li><em>Bartonella henselae</em></li>\n<li><em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li>\n<li><em>Spirillum minus</em></li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 an‌d 3.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 an‌d 4.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 an‌d 5.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 an‌d 5.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 5.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 5.</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>r</strong><strong>at-bite fever.</strong> This can be caused by <strong><em>Streptobacillus moniliformis</em></strong> and <em><strong>Spirillum minus</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Rat-bite fever (RBF) is characterized by <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>rash,</strong> and <strong>arthralgia</strong> which usually occurs <strong>days or weeks after a bite</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>two types</strong> of rat-bite fever are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Streptobacillary RBF</strong> develops 2–10 days after a rat bite, associated with sudden onset of fever, headache, and myalgia followed by petechial rash and arthritis. It is also called <strong>Haverhill</strong> <strong>fever</strong> or <strong>Erythema</strong> <strong>arthrititicum</strong> <strong>epidemicum</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spirillary</strong> <strong>RBF</strong> is similar to streptobacillary type but it is also associated with <strong>regional lymphadenopathy</strong>. It is also called <strong>Sodoku. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is based on <strong>demonstration</strong> of causative organisms <strong>in peripheral blood films</strong>. Serology can also be used for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>RBF is <strong>treated</strong> with <strong>intravenous Penicillin G</strong>. <span class=\"ILfuVd\"><span class=\"hgKElc\"> Alternative drugs include ampicillin, cefuroxime, and cefotaxime.</span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> <em>Pasteurella multocida</em> causes cellulitis and lymphangitis following bites by cats and dogs.</p>\n<p><em>Bartonella henselae</em> causes cat sratch disease and bacillary angiomatosis.</p>\n<p><em>Borellia burgdorferi</em> causes Lyme disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6104","text":"While evaluating the Pap s‌mear of a patient, you co​me across the following f​inding. What is the diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"While evaluating the Pap smear of a patient, you come across the following finding. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphogranul‌oma venereum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacterial‌ vaginosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trichom‌oniasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gono‌rrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bacterial vaginosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/59c7610435e34a6ca9c103ec16d211d0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c864d1570e7343ae961f830a9baed2b7x800x533.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c611b4ab75c54756a5964f64b5f76d47x510x453.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bacterial vaginosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>clue cells</strong> which are seen in <strong>bacterial vaginosis</strong>. They are<strong> epithelial cells</strong> with their surface covered by <strong>adherent bacilli</strong> seen in stained or unstained films.</p>\n<p>Bacterial vaginosis<strong> </strong>is a <strong>polymicrobial infection</strong> characterized by a malodorous (fishy smell) vaginal discharge. Causative organisms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Gardnerella vaginalis</em></li>\n<li><em>Mycoplasma hominis</em></li>\n<li><em>Prevotella</em></li>\n<li><em>Ureaplasma</em></li>\n<li><em>Peptostreptococcus</em>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bacterial vaginosis is diagnosed using <strong>Amsel's criteria</strong> and <strong>Nugent's score</strong>:</p>\n<p>1. Amsel's Criteria: (<strong>any 3</strong> should be present)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thin, white/yellow homogenous vaginal discharge</li>\n<li>pH of Vaginal fluid&gt;4.5</li>\n<li>Fishy odour of discharge accentuated on adding 10% KOH (<strong>Whiff test</strong>)</li>\n<li>Clue cells seen on microscopy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Nugent's score is obtained by counting the number of <em>Gardnerella vaginalis, Mobilincus</em> and lactobacillus in the gram-stained smear of vaginal discharge. A score of<strong> ≥7 is diagnostic</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is oral metronidazole 500 mg BD for 7 days.</p>\n<p>The images below show clue cells from microscopy. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2276","text":"A patient from Kolar, Karn‌ataka presented with feve​r and hemoptysis. The fin​dings of his peripheral s‌mear examination are give‍n below. Which of the fol​lowing is the causative o‌rganism?","raw_text":"A patient from Kolar, Karnataka presented with fever and hemoptysis. The findings of his peripheral smear examination are given below. Which of the following is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yersini‌a pestis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillus ‌anthracis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bordetella‌ pertussis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella‌ pneumonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yersinia pestis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20a2b2a0ac1e4bd8beeb990514b7eedd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68d87ad106a24131ac7c59e8e342e248x720x529.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yersinia pestis</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and the image showing the characteristic \"<strong>safety-pin appearance</strong>\" in peripheral smear is suggestive of <strong>plague</strong> caused by <em><strong>Yersinia pestis</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>On staining with Giemsa stain or methylene blue (<strong>Wayson's stain</strong>), the bacteria has <strong>bipolar staining</strong> with two ends densely stained and the central area clear. This is the characteristic 'safety-pin appearance' seen in <em>Y. pestis.</em></p>\n<p>Bipolar staining is also shown by the following organisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em></li>\n<li><em>Vibrio parahemolyticus</em></li>\n<li><em>Calymmatobacterium granulomatis</em></li>\n<li><em>Burkholderia mallei</em></li>\n<li><em>Burkholderia pseudomallei.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 337\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note: In India, 4 foci of plague are known:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kolar at the trijunction of Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and Karnataka.</li>\n<li>Beed-Latur belt in Maharashtra</li>\n<li>Rhoru, Himachal Pradesh </li>\n<li>Uttarakhand </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the safety-pin appearance of <em>Yersinia pestis</em> on Wayson's stain</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2300","text":"A child from Uttarakhand s‌uddenly developed fever w​ith chills and malaise. H​e also developed a tender‌ swelling which is shown ‍in the image. There's a h​istory of similar complai‌nts in the community. Wha‌t is false about the dise‌ase he's likely suffering​ from?","raw_text":"A child from Uttarakhand suddenly developed fever with chills and malaise. He also developed a tender swelling which is shown in the image. There's a history of similar complaints in the community. What is false about the disease he's likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wild rodents and their inf‌ected fleas are the prima​ry reservoir.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The septicemic form cannot‌ be acquired directly.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The pneumonic form is far ‌more contagious than the ​bubonic form.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The bubonic form may progr‌ess to the pneumonic form​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The septicemic form cannot be acquired directly.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a78c059874644deead3d2f07beb99da9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The septicemic form cannot be acquired directly.</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of <strong>sudden-onset fever </strong>with <strong>painful lymphadenopathy</strong> and a history of similar complaints in the community is suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>plague</strong> caused by<em><strong> Yersinia pestis</strong>. </em>The <strong>septicemic form</strong> of the disease may be <strong>acquired directly</strong> if the bacteria enter the bloodstream via <strong>infected blood transfusions.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Wild rodents</strong> and their infected fleas are the <strong>primary reservoirs </strong>of <em>Yersinia pestis</em>. Infection is <strong>transmitted</strong> to humans by the <strong>bite of infected fleas</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Human plague</strong> occurs in <strong>3 clinical forms</strong> (in increasing order of severity): bubonic plague&lt; pneumonic plague&lt; septicemic plague</p>\n<p><strong>Bubonic plague- </strong>It<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> variety of the disease. It is named after the <strong>buboes </strong>meaning<strong> swollen lymph nodes,</strong> which typically develop within a week after an infected flea bite. Other clinical features include sudden onset of fever and chills, headache, and fatigue.</p>\n<p><strong>Pneumonic plague- </strong>Bubonic plague can advance and<strong> spread to the lungs</strong><strong>. </strong>It is the <strong>most virulent form</strong> of plague. Any person with pneumonic plague may <strong>transmit </strong>the disease via <strong>droplets</strong> to other humans. The symptoms include dyspnoea, chest pain, cough, fever, and hemoptysis. </p>\n<p><strong>Septicemic plague-</strong> When the bacteria enter the<strong> bloodstream</strong> directly and multiply there, it is known as <strong>septicemic plague</strong>. When left untreated, both bubonic and pneumonic plague can lead to septicemic plague.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2319","text":"A sudden epidemic of pneum‌onic plague has occurred ​in the Beed-Latur belt of​ Maharashtra. The primary‌ contact of a suspected c‍ase came to you for preve​ntive treatment. What wil‌l you prescribe for chemo‌prophylaxis in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A sudden epidemic of pneumonic plague has occurred in the Beed-Latur belt of Maharashtra. The primary contact of a suspected case came to you for preventive treatment. What will you prescribe for chemoprophylaxis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Doxycycline</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for <strong>chemoprophylaxis</strong> of <strong>plague</strong> is <strong>doxycycline.</strong> It is given for <strong>7 days</strong> to all <strong>close contacts</strong> of a patient having pneumonic plague.</p>\n<p>Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, and moxifloxacin are the other first-line drugs used for antimicrobial prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the <strong>treatment</strong> of plague is <strong>streptomycin</strong>. Streptomycin is nephrotoxic and ototoxic and should, therefore be cautiously used. Doxycycline or ciprofloxacin (and other fluoroquinolones) are considered as alternative drugs for the treatment of plague.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2299","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms do not cause yersi​niosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms do not cause yersiniosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yersinia en‌terocolotica","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Yersini‌a pestis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Yersinia enterocolitica an‌d Yersinia pseudotubercul​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yersinia pestis and Yersin‌ia pseudotuberculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Yersinia pestis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Yersinia pestis</strong></p><p>The term <strong>yersiniosis</strong> denotes infections with yersiniae <strong>other than </strong><em>Y.pestis</em><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Yersiniosis includes <strong>zoonotic</strong> infections by <em><strong>Y.pseudotuberculosis</strong></em> and <em><strong>Y.enterocolitica</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3090","text":"A 65-year-old patient pres‌ents to the OPD with high​ fever, chest pain, dyspn​oea, and hemoptysis. The ‌patient also complains of‍ watery diarrhea. The dir​ect fluorescent antibody ‌stained film of his pleur‌al fluid is given below. ‌Identify the most likely ​etiologic agent.","raw_text":"A 65-year-old patient presents to the OPD with high fever, chest pain, dyspnoea, and hemoptysis. The patient also complains of watery diarrhea. The direct fluorescent antibody stained film of his pleural fluid is given below. Identify the most likely etiologic agent.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia ‌pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Legionella ‌pneumophila","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella‌ pneumoniae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Legionella pneumophila","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fb49e781a0e4285a7f303f67604eeb0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Legionella pneumophila</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the image showing <strong>fluorescent</strong> organisms in the <strong>direct fluorescent antibody</strong> (DFA) stained film of pleural fluid suggest the diagnosis of <strong>Legionnaire's disease,</strong> caused by <strong><em>Legionella pneumophila.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Two distinct clinical patterns of legionellosis have been identified:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Legionnaires' disease</strong> presents with fever, <strong>non-productive cough,</strong> and dyspnea rapidly progressing to pneumonia if left untreated. Patients can also develop a hyponatremia. <strong>Diarrhea</strong> and <strong>encephalopathy</strong> are also common. It is often included in the differential diagnosis of <strong>atypical pneumonia. </strong>Atypical pneumonia is also caused by: \n<ul>\n<li><em>Chlamydia pneumonia</em></li>\n<li><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em></li>\n<li><em>Mycoplasma pneumonia</em></li>\n<li><em>Coxiella burnetii</em></li>\n<li>Viruses such as influenza virus, adenovirus and respiratory syncytial virus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pontiac fever</strong> is a <strong>milder</strong>, non-fatal, influenza-like illness with fever, chills, myalgia, and headache.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Chlamydia pneumonia</em></strong> causes pharyngitis, sinusitis, bronchitis, and pneumonia. It has also been associated with <strong>adult onset asthma</strong>. Outbreaks have been reported in <strong>closed communities</strong>. <strong>Real-time PCR</strong> is used for its diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong><em>Haemophilus influenzae </em></strong>typically causes <strong>pneumonia</strong> in <strong>infants</strong>. While the primary infections occur due to capsulated strains, bronchopneumonia may occur as a secondary infection due to non-capsulated strains. <strong>Gram stain</strong> and <strong>culture</strong> are used for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong><em>Klebsiella pneumonia</em></strong> causes fatal pneumonia, which occurs in middle -aged or older persons with risk factors such as <strong>alcoholism</strong>, chronic <strong>bronchopulmonary disease</strong> or <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>. <strong>Gram stain</strong> and <strong>culture</strong> are used for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6121","text":"BCYE medium is used to cul‌ture: BCYE - Buffered cha​rcoal yeast extract","raw_text":"<p>BCYE medium is used to culture:</p>\n<p>BCYE - Buffered charcoal yeast extract</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lepto‌spira","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillu‌s cereus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Campyl‌obacter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Legio‌nella","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Legionella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Legionella</strong></p><p><strong>BCYE medium</strong> (buffered charcoal yeast extract agar) is used to culture<strong> <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Legionella pneumophila</em> has fastidious growth requirements and grows on complex media such as  BCYE medium with <strong>L-cysteine</strong> and<strong> antibiotic supplements </strong>(e.g., polymyxin B, cefazolin, vancomycin). The<strong> charcoal</strong> acts as a detoxifying agent. It grows slowly, and the <strong>colonies</strong> may take <strong>3–6 days</strong> to appear.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A:<em> <strong>Leptospira</strong></em> is cultured on <strong>EMJH</strong> (Ellinghausen, McCullough, Johnson and Harris) medium </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong><em>Bacillus cereus</em></strong> is cultured on <strong>PEMBA</strong> (Polymyxin pyruvate egg-yolk mannitol–bromothymol blue) agar</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong><em>Campylobacter</em></strong> is cultured on <strong>Skirrow's</strong>, <strong>Campy BAP</strong> or <strong>Butzler's</strong> agar.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6122","text":"A 60-year-old man is diagn‌osed with Legionnaires’ d​isease. He had recently a​ttended a conference. Wha‌t is the most likely way ‍he acquired the infection​?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man is diagnosed with Legionnaires’ disease. He had recently attended a conference. What is the most likely way he acquired the infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"From the air-conditioned r‌ooms at the conference","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"From a chronic carrier of ‌the disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"From sharing a towel with ‌a fellow colleague in the​ conference","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"From drinking the water pr‌ovided at the conference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. From the air-conditioned rooms at the conference","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. From the air-conditioned rooms at the conference</strong></p><p>This patient most likely acquired infection with <strong><em>Legionella pneumophila</em></strong> from <strong>inhalation</strong> of <strong>aerosol</strong> in the<strong> air-conditioned rooms</strong> at the conference.</p>\n<p><em>Legionella pneumophila</em> can be found in natural, freshwater environments, but generally is not present in sufficient numbers to cause disease. Infection is typically by inhalation of aerosols produced by <strong>cooling towers</strong>, <strong>air conditioners</strong>, and <strong>showerheads</strong> which act as disseminators</p>\n<p>No animal reservoirs exist.<strong> No</strong> evidence of <strong>human-to-human transmission</strong> has been found and <strong>no carrier state</strong> is established.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2857","text":"A patient presented with f‌ever and regional lymphad​enopathy, following a scr​atch from his pet kitten.‌ His lymph node biopsy sp‍ecimen was examined with ​Warthin-Starry staining; ‌the findings are shown in‌ the given image. What is‌ the causative organism o​f this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with fever and regional lymphadenopathy, following a scratch from his pet kitten. His lymph node biopsy specimen was examined with Warthin-Starry staining; the findings are shown in the given image. What is the causative organism of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartonell‌a henselae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bartonella b‌acilliformis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bartonell‌a quintana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borrelia b‌urgdorferi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bartonella henselae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9dce72ebaa14dbbaa9fcefb1cab6b64.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bartonella henselae</strong></p><p>This patient presents with fever and lymphadenopathy after a cat scratch and the above image shows the black short rod-shaped bacilli arranged in chains or clumps, suggestive of<strong> cat scratch disease</strong>, This condition is caused by<strong> <em>Bartonella henselae</em></strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>Cat scratch disease presents as a febrile illness with lymphadenopathy following a cat scratch. Patients present with a painless papule or pustule at the site of the scratch and lymph node enlargement and can be suppurative. The disease is usually self resolving. However, it can be treated with <strong>azithromycin</strong> only in patients who have severe complications. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3094","text":"Choose the correct stateme‌nt regarding 5-day fever.​","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement regarding 5-day fever.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transmitted by Phlebotomus‌ sandflies.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incubation period is 5 day‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No animal reservoir is kno‌wn","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment is necessary onl‌y in the severe cases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. No animal reservoir is known","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. No animal reservoir is known</strong></p><p><strong>5-day fever</strong> is an exclusively human disease and <strong>no animal reservoir</strong> is known.</p>\n<p>5-day fever is also known as trench fever/quintan fever. It is caused by <em><strong>Bartonella quintana</strong></em>. It is characterized by sudden onset of <strong>fever</strong> accompanied by headache, malaise, restlessness, and <strong>shin pain</strong>.    </p>\n<p>Trench fever is transmitted by the <strong>body louse. </strong>(Option A). It has an <strong>incubation period</strong> ranging from <strong>14-30 days</strong>. Symptoms coincide with the release of <em>B. quintana</em> in blood every 3-5 days with <strong>each</strong> <strong>episode</strong> lasting <strong>5 days</strong>. Recrudescence and relapses are common. (Option B)</p>\n<p>All cases are treated using <strong>doxycycline</strong>. Macrolides and fluoroquinolones can also be used in its treatment. (Option D)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3092","text":"A young man presented to t‌he ER with fever and supp​urative lymphadenopathy o​f his left axilla a few d‌ays after he sustained a ‍scratch from his pet cat.​ Which of the following w‌ill most likely be seen o‌n biopsy of the lymph nod‌e?","raw_text":"A young man presented to the ER with fever and suppurative lymphadenopathy of his left axilla a few days after he sustained a scratch from his pet cat. Which of the following will most likely be seen on biopsy of the lymph node?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foreign bod‌y granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naked g‌ranuloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Durck g‌ranuloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stellate ‌granuloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stellate granuloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71265917a6d749bba2a7c24791711a3fx218x142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8a276b74c5041fa9e3f08aa75841523x183x140.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4ae8b2648c6423285e88ce146641786x512x358.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/846d2b7cc12d46ac93e3a9562800795dx252x188.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stellate granuloma</strong></p><p>The given scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>cat-scratch disease</strong> caused by <em>B. henselae</em>. <strong>Stellate granulomas</strong> are characteristically present in this condition. They are granulomas containing central granular debris and neutrophils; giant cells are uncommon.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a stellate granuloma</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Foreign body granulomas</strong> are incited by relatively inert foreign bodies, such as talc, sutures. Epithelioid cells and giant cells are apposed to the surface of the foreign body. The foreign material can usually be identified in the center of the granuloma.</p>\n<p>The given image shows suture granuloma. The suture material is surrounded by lymphocytic infiltration</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Naked granulomas</strong> are seen in sarcoidosis. They are non-caseating granulomas minimally surrounded by lymphocytes. The image below shows a naked granuloma</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> Option C: <strong>Durck granulomas</strong> are seen in <strong>cerebral malaria.</strong> The image below shows a Durck granuloma</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3093","text":"A patient who is a known c‌ase of AIDS presents to t​he OPD with fever, chills​, weight loss and the cut‌aneous lesions shown belo‍w. The microscopic examin​ation of the lesions reve‌aled predominant neutroph‌ilic infiltration. What i‌s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A patient who is a known case of AIDS presents to the OPD with fever, chills, weight loss and the cutaneous lesions shown below. The microscopic examination of the lesions revealed predominant neutrophilic infiltration. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kaposi ‌sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Verruga‌ peruana","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillary a‌ngiomatosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eczema h‌erpeticum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacillary angiomatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c019a6902cb14fc9975259b34c6ee807.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacillary angiomatosis</strong></p><p>This clinical vignette of a patient with HIV infections and the violaceous lesions with a neutrophilic infiltrate is suggestive of <strong>bacillary angiomatosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bacillary angiomatosis is seen in severely <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patients and is caused by <strong><em>B. henselae</em></strong> or <em><strong>B. quintana</strong>. </em>It is characterized by <strong>neovascular proliferative lesions </strong>involving the skin. It consists of lobular proliferation of small blood vessels lined by enlarged endothelial cells interspersed with mixed infiltrates of <strong>neutrophils</strong> and lymphocytes, with a <strong>predominance</strong> of the former.</p>\n<p>Both bacillary angiomatosis and Kaposi's sarcoma can have the clinical presentation mentioned in the question. However, the major differentiating factor is the <strong>microscopic examination</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bacillary angiomatosis</strong> predominantly has <strong>mixed infiltrate</strong> accompanied by <strong>neutrophils</strong> with <strong>clumps</strong> of <strong>bacilli</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Kaposi's sarcoma</strong> predominantly has <strong>plasma cells</strong> and <strong>lymphocytic infiltrates</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2870","text":"A patient has presented wi‌th lesions on the dorsum ​of the hand followed by a​xillary lymphadenopathy. ‌What significant history ‍will you ask for in this ​case?","raw_text":"A patient has presented with lesions on the dorsum of the hand followed by axillary lymphadenopathy. What significant history will you ask for in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"History of exposure to rat‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"History of exposure to cat‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"History of similar complai‌nt in family members","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"History of‌ tick bite","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. History of exposure to cats","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79d9f951c90043738ab30c2f9e0f4784x600x890.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. History of exposure to cats</strong></p><p>This scenario of lesions on the hand followed by axillary lymphadenopathy is suggestive of <strong>cat scratch disease,</strong> hence we should ask about the history of <strong>exposure to cats.</strong> It is caused by <em><strong>Bartonella henselae</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>Bartonella henselae is an intracellular, pleomorphic, <strong>gram-negative</strong> bacillus. They can be identified in lymph node biopsy with Warthin-Starry Silver stain. In addition to cat scratch disease, in immunocompromised individuals, it can also cause <strong>bacillary angiomatosis</strong> and <strong>peliosis hepatis</strong>. </p>\n<p>In immunocompetent hosts, cat scratch disease is a self limiting illness. However in patients with severe disease, azithromycin can be used to treat the infection. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6988","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrectly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following  is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartonella henselae- Bacil‌lary angiomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ehrlichia chaffeensis - Hu‌man monocytic ehrlichiosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ehrlichia phagocytophila -‌ Human granulocytic ehrli​chiosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bartonella bacilliformis -‌ Parinaud's oculoglandula​r syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bartonella bacilliformis - Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bartonella bacilliformis - Parinaud&#x27;s oculoglandular syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome</strong> is an atypical form of <strong>cat scratch disease</strong> and<strong> </strong>is caused by <em><strong>Bartonella henselae . </strong>Bartonella henselae</em> also<em> </em>causes <strong>bacillary angiomatosis</strong> in HIV-infected and other immunodeficient patients. . It presents as vascular nodules or tumours on the skin, mucosa, and other locations.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Bartonella bacilliformis</strong> </em>causes <strong>Oroya fever</strong> also known as <strong>Carrion's disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: <em>Ehrlichia phagocytophila</em> has been renamed as <em><strong>Anaplasma phagocytophilum.</strong></em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2856","text":"A patient with fever and h‌emolytic anemia developed​ characteristic Peruvian ​warts 1 month later. What‌ is the causative organis‍m for this disease?","raw_text":"A patient with fever and hemolytic anemia developed characteristic Peruvian warts 1 month later. What is the causative organism for this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartonell‌a hensalae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bartonell‌a quintana","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bartonella b‌acilliformis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bartonella‌ australis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bartonella bacilliformis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c5efe89dbc940ca80ba8e9b83a650b2x720x1092.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bartonella bacilliformis</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of <strong>fever, hemolytic anemia</strong> followed by <strong>verruga peruana</strong> lesions (Peruvian warts) is suggestive of <strong>Oroya fever</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is caused by <strong><em>Bartonella bacilliformis.</em></strong> The vector for Oroya fever is <strong>sandfly.</strong></p>\n<p>Oroya fever or Carrion’s disease is associated with verruga peruana which are cutaneous vascular lesions carrying the bacteria. </p>\n<p>It presents as <strong>fever</strong> with <strong>progressive anemia</strong> due to bacterial invasion of erythrocytes. A late <strong>sequela</strong> in survivors of Oroya fever or in those with asymptomatic infection is <strong>verruga peruana, </strong>which is characterized by <strong>vascular nodular skin lesions</strong> carrying the bacteria that occur in successive crops. An image of verruga peruana is given below.</p>\n<p>Image shows verruga peruana lesions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Bartonella quintana causes <strong>trench fever </strong>or<strong> 5 day fever.</strong></p>\n<p>Bartonella henselae causes <strong>cat scratch disease</strong>. It also causes <strong>bacillary angiomatosis </strong>and<strong> bacillary peliosis</strong> in HIV infected people.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SolB0keo","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"17_multifetal_pregnancy","title":"17.Multifetal Pregnancy","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB1817","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of multifetal pregnanc​y?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of multifetal pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monozygotic dichorionic di​amniotic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dizygotic dichorionic diam​niotic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Monozygotic monochorionic ​diamniotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monozygotic monochorionic ​monoamniotic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dizygotic dichorionic diamniotic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/babb023570254f7d800dd842177cc733x1280x5795.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dizygotic dichorionic diamniotic</strong></p><p>The most common type of multifetal pregnancy is <strong>dizygotic</strong> twinning, which is always <strong>dichorionic diamniotic</strong>. </p>\n<p>The incidence of dizygotic twins is 80% among twins. Of the <strong>monozygotic</strong> twins, <strong>monochorionic diamniotic</strong> is the most common (13-14%).</p>\n<p><strong>Zygosity</strong> refers to the type of<strong> conception </strong>while<strong> chorionicity</strong> refers to the type of <strong>placentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Types of twin pregnancies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dizygotic: 2 ova are fertilized by 2 sperms to form 2 separate zygotes.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monozygotic: Single zygote (1 ovum fertilized by 1 sperm) divides into 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature</td>\n<td>Monozygotic</td>\n<td>Dizygotic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phenotype</td>\n<td>Identical</td>\n<td>Non-identical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Genotype</td>\n<td>Identical</td>\n<td>Non-identical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incidence</td>\n<td>1:250 (constant worldwide)</td>\n<td>Varies with demography</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incidence after ART</td>\n<td>Increases</td>\n<td>Increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perinatal complications</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1818","text":"What is the time of divisi​on of the inner cell mass​ in a diamniotic monochor​ionic pregnancy?","raw_text":"What is the time of division of the inner cell mass in a diamniotic monochorionic pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st - ​2nd day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th – ​8th day","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8th - 1​2th day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10th - ​14th day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4th – 8th day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4th – 8th day</strong></p><p><strong>Diamniotic monochorionic</strong> pregnancy occurs if the division of inner cell mass takes place between <strong>4th - 8th day</strong> after fertilization.</p>\n<p>The timing of the division of zygote for each type of twinning is given below.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Monozygotic twin types</td>\n<td>Time of zygote division</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dichorionic diamniotic</td>\n<td>&lt;72 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monochorionic diamniotic (more common)</td>\n<td>4-8 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monochorionic monoamniotic </td>\n<td>8-12 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conjoined twins</td>\n<td>&gt;12 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1820","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g multifetal pregnancies?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding multifetal pregnancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of embryos implante​d affects the risk of twi​nning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isograft is done between m​onozygotic twins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Twins of opposite sex are ​usually dizygotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Same sex twins having same​ blood group are always m​onozygotic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Same sex twins having same blood group are always monozygotic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Same sex twins having same blood group are always monozygotic</strong></p><p><strong>Same sex</strong> twins of the <strong>same blood group</strong> are not always monozygotic but can also be <strong>dizygotic twins</strong> as well. The genetic relationship between dizygotic twins is the same as any siblings.</p>\n<p>Option A: More the number of embryos transferred in IVF, more is the risk of twinning. Hence, <strong>single embryo transfer</strong> is being <strong>recommended</strong> by the American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2017) to prevent twin gestation.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Isograft</strong> is done between monozygotic twins as they have <strong>HLA homology</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Twins of the <strong>opposite sex</strong> are <strong>usually dizygotic</strong>. Rarely, somatic mutations or chromosomal aberrations can change the sex of 1 or both of the monozygotic twins.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1822","text":"The examination findings o​f a pregnant woman are su​ggestive of her having co​njoined twins. Which is t‌he most common type of su​ch twins?","raw_text":"The examination findings of a pregnant woman are suggestive of her having conjoined twins. Which is the most common type of such twins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crani​opagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thorac​opagus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyop​agus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cephal​opagus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thoracopagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4f444a1b9df414e95da20dae2ef92a7x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thoracopagus</strong></p><p><strong>Thoracopagus</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> type of conjoined twins.</p>\n<p><strong>Division</strong> of the inner cell mass <strong>after</strong> the <strong>12th</strong> <strong>day</strong> of fertilization results in the development of <strong>conjoined</strong> or Siamese twins. Based on the parts of the body joined, they are of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ventral</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rostral</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Omphalopagus (abdomen)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thoracopagus (upper chest)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cephalopagus (head)</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Caudal</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ischiopagus (conjoined spines at 180 angle)</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lateral</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Paraphagus (side by side fusion with a shared pelvis)</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dorsal</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Craniopagus (skull)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rachipagus (vertebral arches)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pyopagus (pelvis)</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sharing</strong> of <strong>organs</strong> is common among conjoined twins. <strong>MRI</strong> imaging is done later in pregnancy for the evaluation of organ sharing between conjoined twins who are being planned for <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>separation</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1824","text":"Which of the following can​ be used to calculate the​ incidence of multifetal ​gestation?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used to calculate the incidence of multifetal gestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tei ​index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hellin​'s rule","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CHOP ​score","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kleihauer-​Betke test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hellin's rule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hellin&#x27;s rule</strong></p><p>The <strong>incidence</strong> of multiple pregnancies is calculated by <strong>Hellin’s rule</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to this rule, incidence= 1 : 80<sup>(n-1)</sup>, where n is the number of fetuses.</p>\n<p><strong>Factors affecting</strong> the <strong>incidence of twin pregnancies</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monozygotic</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ART</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dizygotic</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Race</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Heredity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maternal age</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Parity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Family history of twinning</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Good nutritional status</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infertility therapy</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tei index</strong> or myocardial performance index (MPI) is calculated using ventricular doppler imaging. It is used to assess <strong>cardiac function</strong> in <strong>TTTS</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>CHOP</strong> <strong>score</strong> is used for <strong>cardiac</strong> <strong>evaluation</strong> of recipient twin in <strong>TTTS</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Kleihauer</strong>-<strong>Betke</strong> <strong>test</strong> is used to evaluate the approximate volume of <strong>fetomaternal hemorrhage</strong> in cases of Rh-D alloimmunization.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1825","text":"In which of the following ​instances is there an inc​reased chance of twinning​? 40 year old multipara w‌ith spontaneous conceptio​n Short statured and unde‌rnourished woman IVF with​ single embryo implanted ‍Family history of twinnin‌g Spontaneous conception ‍after 1 month of stopping​ OCP","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following instances is there an increased chance of twinning?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">40 year old multipara with spontaneous conception</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Short statured and undernourished woman</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IVF with single embryo implanted</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Family history of twinning</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Spontaneous conception after 1 month of stopping OCP</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 4, 5</strong></p><p>Increasing maternal age, family history of twinning, and conception after stopping OCPs increase the risk of twinning.</p>\n<p><strong>Taller</strong>, <strong>well</strong>-<strong>built</strong> women have an <strong>increased</strong> risk of <strong>twinning</strong> compared to shorter undernourished women. IVF increases the risk of twinning but <strong>single</strong> <strong>embryo</strong> <strong>transfer</strong> <strong>reduces</strong> the risk of <strong>multifetal pregnancies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Factors affecting the incidence of twin pregnancies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monozygotic</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ART</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dizygotic</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Race</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Heredity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maternal age</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Parity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Family history of twinning</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Good nutritional status</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infertility therapy</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Note- A family history of twinning (maternal and paternal) causes an increased chance of twinning. It is seen that maternal history of twinning has a higher chance of twinning as compared to a paternal history of twinning.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1846","text":"In superfecundation, which​ of the following is seen​?","raw_text":"In superfecundation, which of the following is seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fertilisation of 2 ova rel​eased at the same time by​ sperms released at singl​e intercourse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fertilisation of 2 ova rel​eased in the same cycle b​y sperms released at inte​rcourse on 2 different oc‌casion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both of ​the above","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fertilisation of 2 ova released in the same cycle by sperms released at intercourse on 2 different occasion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fertilisation of 2 ova released in the same cycle by sperms released at intercourse on 2 different occasion</strong></p><p><strong>Superfecundation </strong>is the fertilization of<strong> two</strong> different <strong>ova</strong> released in the <strong>same cycle</strong>, by two <strong>separate acts of coitus</strong> within a short period of time.</p>\n<p>Superfecundation is the fertilization of <strong>two different ova</strong> in the <strong>same menstrual cycle </strong>(as suggested by the same gestational age)<strong> </strong>by separate acts of coitus. It can occur even after <strong>artificial reproductive therapy.</strong> Hence, after embryo transfer, intercourse must be avoided. Heteropaternal superfecundation is used to refer to instances of two different males fathering fraternal twins. This leads to the possibility of twins also being half-siblings. This may give rise to the doubt of adultery and infidelity. </p>\n<p><strong>Superfetation </strong>is the fertilization of<strong> two ova</strong> released in <strong>different</strong> menstrual <strong>cycles</strong>. This is not seen in humans. The nidation and development of one fetus over another fetus is <strong>theoretically possible</strong> until the decidual space is obliterated by <strong>14-16 weeks of pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1826","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g the condition given in ​the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the condition given in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more common in monoc​horionic pregnancies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot be detected by s​onography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is diagnosed usually af​ter delivery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morbidity in the co-twin i​s due to hypotension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It cannot be detected by sonography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a20f96870cf14e34a003b078c6bbad5c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It cannot be detected by sonography</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>fetus compressus</strong> which appears as a tan ovoid mass compressed against the fetal membranes. It can be <strong>detected early</strong> by <strong>sonography</strong>.</p>\n<p>In multifetal pregnancies, if one of the fetuses dies early in pregnancy, the surviving fetus continues to develop normally. It can lead to the following outcomes:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Outcome</td>\n<td>Type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disappears</td>\n<td>Vanishing twin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gets compressed</td>\n<td>Fetus compressus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flattened through desiccation</td>\n<td>Fetus papyraceus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Factors affecting the prognosis of the surviving twin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Gestational</strong> <strong>age</strong> at fetal death: 2nd and 3rd trimester deaths increase the risk of morbidity in the surviving twin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Chorionicity</strong>: Monochorionic twins with early fetal death have an increased risk of morbidity in the surviving twin. This is due to neurological abnormality and vascular anastomoses.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Duration</strong> <strong>between</strong> fetal <strong>demise</strong> and <strong>delivery</strong> of surviving twin</li>\n<li>Surviving twin may be born <strong>preterm</strong> leading to an increased risk of fetal demise</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Death in the <strong>surviving</strong> <strong>twin</strong> occurs mostly due to vascular anastomoses between the twins. The surviving twin can develop <strong>sudden hypotension</strong> due to these anatomoses after fetal demise.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1827","text":"A woman is diagnosed to ha​ve twins during her datin​g scan. What is the most ​common presentation in su‌ch pregnancies?","raw_text":"A woman is diagnosed to have twins during her dating scan. What is the most common presentation in such pregnancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both ​vertex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1st vertex and 2nd transve​rse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1st breech a​nd 2nd vertex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st vertex a​nd 2nd breech","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Both vertex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Both vertex</strong></p><p>The most common presentation in twins is <strong>both</strong> <strong>vertex</strong>/ <strong>cephalo-cephalic</strong> presentation (60%). The other presentations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cephalo-Breech (20%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Breech-Cephalic (10%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Breech-Breech (6-10%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transverse-Transverse (Rarest)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mode of delivery depends on the presentation of the 1st twin as:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Presentation of the 1st twin</td>\n<td>Mode of delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertex</td>\n<td>Vaginal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breech</td>\n<td>Caesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transverse</td>\n<td>Caesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4402","text":"The best time to identify ​the type of twin pregnanc​y on ultrasound is:","raw_text":"The best time to identify the type of twin pregnancy on ultrasound is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"18-22​ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24-28​ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11-14​ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"14-18​ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 11-14 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 11-14 weeks</strong></p><p>The best time to <strong>identify</strong> the<strong> type of twin</strong> pregnancy on ultrasound is <strong>11-14 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>Twin pregnancies should ideally be dated when the <strong>crown–rump length</strong> (CRL) measurement is <strong>between 45 and 84 mm</strong> (i.e. 11 + 0 to 13 + 6 weeks of gestation).</p>\n<p>Chorionicity should be ideally determined between <strong>11 + 0</strong> and<strong> 13 + 6 weeks</strong> of gestation (the earliest time to identify chorionicity is 7 weeks). This is done using the membrane thickness at the site of insertion of the amniotic membrane into the placenta. The T‐sign (in monochorionic twins) or lambda sign (in dichorionic twins) can be seen here. As the determination of <strong>chorionicity</strong> is most accurate when the amnion and chorion have not yet been fused, the<strong> first‐trimester scan</strong> in twin pregnancy is highly important.</p>\n<p>Markers of <strong>dichorionicity</strong> include <strong>4 layers</strong> of membranes between the twins, membrane <strong>thickness <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>2mm</strong>, <strong>different sex </strong>of twins, <strong>2</strong> different <strong>placentas,</strong> and <strong>positive twin peak</strong> sign.</p>\n<p>Women with an uncomplicated dichorionic twin pregnancy should have a first‐trimester scan, a detailed second‐trimester scan, and scans every 4 weeks thereafter. </p>\n<p>Women with uncomplicated monochorionic twins should have a first‐trimester scan and then scans every 2 weeks from 16 weeks onwards. This is done in order to detect TTTS and TAPS in a timely manner. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1828","text":"A 35-year-old woman concei​ved after being treated f​or infertility. Her USG s​can is given below. What ‌is her diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman conceived after being treated for infertility. Her USG scan is given below. What is her diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dichorionic diamniotic twi​ns","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Monochorionic diamniotic t​wins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monochorionic monoamniotic​ twins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Singleton pregnancy with u​terine septum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dichorionic diamniotic twins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a3b22a4fa04842d983d0ceae79706c8d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ed8c62006b348c6ad2e99a7182853a5x1280x1699.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dichorionic diamniotic twins</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>twin peak sign</strong> which is seen in <strong>dichorionic</strong> <strong>diamniotic</strong> twins. This is also known as the lambda sign or delta sign.</p>\n<p>In dichorionic twins, the <strong>intervening</strong> <strong>membrane</strong> between the twins has <strong>4 layers</strong> i.e. 2 chorions and 2 amnions. The placenta protrudes from the base to the space between the 2 amnion layers. This gives rise to the twin peak sign i.e. <strong>triangle</strong> with the <strong>base</strong> at <strong>placenta</strong> and <strong>apex</strong> in the intervening membrane.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Sonographic assessment of dichorionicity is done by observing the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Number of placental masses - 2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of intervening membrane - twin peak or lambda or delta sign</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thickness of intervening membranes ≥ 2mm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The best <strong>time of delivery</strong> of dichorionic diamniotic twins is <strong>38</strong> <strong>weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1831","text":"A primigravida presents to​ the ANC clinic for the f​irst time at 14 weeks of ​gestation. She complains ‌of excessive nausea and v​omiting. On examination, ‌fundal height is midway b​etween pubic symphysis an‍d umbilicus. The T sign i‌n seen on USG. What is he‍r diagnosis?","raw_text":"A primigravida presents to the ANC clinic for the first time at 14 weeks of gestation. She complains of excessive nausea and vomiting. On examination, fundal height is midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus. The T sign in seen on USG. What is her diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dichorionic diamniotic twi​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monochorionic diamniotic t​wins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Monochorionic monoamniotic​ twins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dizygot​ic twins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monochorionic diamniotic twins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2d552dd401d4bb4a0bed1a354d074edx510x396.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monochorionic diamniotic twins</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> T sign </strong>seen on USG points towards a diagnosis of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> monochorionic diamniotic </strong>twins.</p>\n<p>The T sign is <strong>formed</strong> at the point at which the <strong>amnions meet</strong> the <strong>placenta</strong>. In monochorionic diamniotic twins, the separating membrane is made up only by the two amniotic membranes with no intervening chorion. This can be seen in the latter part of the first trimester or the beginning of the second trimester.</p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms</strong> of multifetal pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Increased nausea and vomiting</strong> in early months</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Palpitations and shortness of breath</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Increased swelling of legs, varicose veins and haemorrhoids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Unusual rate of uterine growth</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Excessive fetal movements</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Signs</strong> of multifetal pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Increased weight gain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Hypertension</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Barrel shaped abdomen</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Increased fundal height</strong> and abdominal girth</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Multiple fetal parts</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Palpation of 2 fetal heads </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">FHS at different spots with FHR difference of at least 10bpm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Monochorionic diamniotic pregnancy is terminated between 34-37 weeks+6 days.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the T sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1830","text":"Which of the following ima​ges depicts monochorionic​ diamniotic pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following images depicts monochorionic diamniotic pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7535cfa87e964427be178ee8f8beeb04.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1739f341a8be4834b0e9b6d6bc8839b0x1280x1703.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2</strong></p><p>The USG in image 2 depicts <strong>monochorionic diamniotic</strong> pregnancy. It is identified by the <strong>T sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>In monochorionic diamniotic twins, the <strong>intervening membrane</strong> has only<strong> 2 layers</strong> (i.e) 2 amnions. This thin membrane is seen ending at the common placenta giving rise to the T sign. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Sonographic assessment of <strong>monochorionicity</strong> is done by observing the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Single</strong> placental mass</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of intervening membrane - <strong>T sign</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thin intervening membrane <strong>&lt;2mm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The best time of delivery of monochorionic diamniotic twins is 34 - 37weeks + 6 days.</p>\n<p>Image 1: <strong>Dichorionic</strong> twins with twin peak or lambda sign</p>\n<p>Image 3: Monochorionic <strong>monoamniotic</strong> twins with no intervening membranes between them</p>\n<p>Image 4: <strong>Triplets</strong> with 3 separate gestational sacs</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1833","text":"A mother with twin pregnan​cy underwent routine USG.​ Estimated fetal weights ​were calculated for both ‌twins. What is the criter​ia to diagnose pathologic‌al discordant growth in t​his pregnancy?","raw_text":"A mother with twin pregnancy underwent routine USG. Estimated fetal weights were calculated for both twins. What is the criteria to diagnose pathological discordant growth in this pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Difference of &amp;gt;25% with​ larger twin as index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Difference of &amp;lt;50% with​ larger twin as index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Difference of &amp;gt;50% with​ smaller twin as index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Difference of &amp;gt;20% with​ larger twin as index","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Difference of >20% with larger twin as index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Difference of &gt;20% with larger twin as index</strong></p><p>A difference in estimated<strong> fetal weight</strong> of <strong>&gt;20%</strong> with <strong>larger</strong> twin as <strong>index</strong> is one of the criteria for diagnosing discordant growth.</p>\n<p>Discordant growth is the <strong>unequal</strong> <strong>size/ weight</strong> among the fetuses in a multifetal gestation. It can be diagnosed by either of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Estimated fetal weight difference &gt; 20%</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Discordance = (weight of larger twin - weight of smaller twin/weight of larger twin) x 100</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Discordant growth in <strong>monochorionic</strong> twins is due to placental <strong>vascular</strong> <strong>anastomoses</strong>. In <strong>dichorionic</strong> twins, it occurs due to differences in <strong>genetic</strong> growth <strong>potential</strong> and suboptimal <strong>implantation site</strong> for one of the fetuses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1819","text":"In a woman receiving artif​icial reproductive therap​y, the inner cell mass di​vides on the 7th day. Whi‌ch of the following compl​ications is not seen in t‌he resulting multifetal p​regnancy?","raw_text":"In a woman receiving artificial reproductive therapy, the inner cell mass divides on the 7th day. Which of the following complications is not seen in the resulting multifetal pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Twin twin transfusion synd​rome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discorda​nt growth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cord ent​anglement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acardi​ac twin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cord entanglement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cord entanglement</strong></p><p>The division of the inner cell mass on the <strong>7th</strong> day will result in <strong>monochorionic</strong> <strong>diamniotic</strong> type of twin pregnancy. <strong>Cord entanglement</strong> is seen in <strong>monoamniotic</strong> twins and not in diamniotic twins.</p>\n<p>The timing of the division of zygote for each type of twinning is given below.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Monozygotic types</td>\n<td>Time of zygote division</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dichorionic diamniotic</td>\n<td>&lt;72 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monochorionic diamniotic (more common)</td>\n<td>4-8 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monochorionic monoamniotic </td>\n<td>8-12 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conjoined twins</td>\n<td>&gt;12 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Complications in<strong> monochorionic</strong> <strong>diamniotic</strong> twins occur due to placental vascular anastomoses. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Twin reversed arterial perfusion (TRAP) or acardiac twin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Twin anaemia polycythemia syndrome (TAPS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Monochorionic</strong> <strong>monoamniotic</strong> twins have a high fetal death rate. This is due to the following complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preterm birth</li>\n<li>Congenital anomalies</li>\n<li>Low birth weight</li>\n<li>Cord entanglement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1858","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of placental vascular ​anastomoses seen in monoc​horionic twin pregnancies‌?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of placental vascular anastomoses seen in monochorionic twin pregnancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artery​ – vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vein ​– vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artery ​– artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A​ and B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Artery – vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Artery – vein</strong></p><p><strong>Artery-vein</strong> anastomoses are <strong>most commonly</strong> seen in <strong>monochorionic</strong> pregnancies. </p>\n<p>All monochorionic placentas have anastomoses that can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Superficial artery-vein</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Deep artery-vein</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Artery-artery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vein-vein</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One vessel with connections to multiple arteries and veins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These anastomoses are <strong>not functional</strong> as long as the <strong>fetuses</strong> are <strong>hemodynamically balanced</strong>. When a <strong>pressure gradient</strong> <strong>develops</strong> between the fetuses, <strong>complications</strong> can occur like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TTTS - Twin-twin transfusion syndrome</li>\n<li>TAPS - Twin anemia polycythemia sequence</li>\n<li>TRAP - Twin reversed arterial perfusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> complication in monochorionic twins is twin-twin transfusion syndrome (<strong>TTTS</strong>). Deep artery-vein anastomosis is the pathology seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1834","text":"A 26-year-old woman, pregn​ant with twins, is diagno​sed to have twin-twin tra​nsfusion syndrome. Which ‌of the following statemen​ts is true regarding the ‌recipient twin in this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman, pregnant with twins, is diagnosed to have twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Which of the following statements is true regarding the recipient twin in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oliguria​ is seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is called the stuck twi​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary hypoplasia is se​en","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congestive cardiac failure​ develops","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congestive cardiac failure develops","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congestive cardiac failure develops</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 923\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Congestive heart failure</strong> is seen in the recipient twin.</p>\n<p>Twin-twin transfusion syndrome is a deep <strong>arteriovenous</strong> <strong>anastomotic</strong> complication seen in <strong>monochorionic</strong> <strong>diamniotic</strong> twins. Deoxygenated <strong>blood from</strong> the <strong>donor</strong> enters through the anastomosis into a shared cotyledon for oxygen exchange. Oxygenated blood is<strong> taken</strong> up <strong>by</strong> the <strong>recipient</strong> twin causing <strong>imbalance in</strong> the<strong> blood volumes</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Donor twin characteristics</td>\n<td>Recipient twin characteristics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oliguria</td>\n<td>Polyuria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oligohydramnios</td>\n<td>Polyhydramnios</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypotension</td>\n<td>Hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anaemia</td>\n<td>Polycythemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Activation of renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS)</td>\n<td>Activation of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Renal failure, growth restriction, pulmonary hypoplasia, contractures</td>\n<td>Congestive cardiac failure, premature rupture of membranes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> <strong>Diagnosis</strong> of TTTS depends on 2 USG criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monochorionic diamniotic gestation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hydramnios (DVP&gt;8cm) in one twin and oligohydramnios (DVP&lt;2cm) in the other twin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> modalities for TTTS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Serial amnioreduction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laser ablation of vascular placental anastomoses</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Selective fetal termination</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Donor</strong> twin is called the <strong>stuck twin</strong> as the oligohydramnios limits fetal movement.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1835","text":"The ultrasound findings in​ a monochorionic diamniot​ic twin pregnancy are ind​icative of poly-oli syndr‌ome. What is the patholog​y seen in this condition?‌","raw_text":"The ultrasound findings in a monochorionic diamniotic twin pregnancy are indicative of poly-oli syndrome. What is the pathology seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artery - artery anastomosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vein – vein​ anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep artery – vein anastom​osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial artery - vein ​anastomosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deep artery – vein anastomosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deep artery – vein anastomosis</strong></p><p>In <strong>poly - oli</strong> or polyhydramnios - oligohydramnios syndrome, the basic pathology is the presence of a <strong>deep</strong> <strong>artery-vein anastomosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>TTTS</strong> can present as poly-oli syndrome or twin oligohydramnios polyhydramnios syndrome (TOPS). This is usually seen in mid-pregnancy. In this syndrome, there is decreased <strong>donor</strong> renal perfusion, causing oliguria and <strong>oligohydramnios</strong>. Because of the anastomosis, the <strong>recipient</strong> twin has increased renal perfusion and increased urine production causing <strong>polyhydramnios</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Donor twin characteristics</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Recipient twin characteristics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oliguria</td>\n<td>Polyuria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oligohydramnios</td>\n<td>Polyhydramnios</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypotension</td>\n<td>Hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anemia</td>\n<td>Polycythemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Renal failure</td>\n<td>Congestive cardiac failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Growth restriction</td>\n<td>Premature rupture of membranes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pulmonary hypoplasia</td>\n<td>Hyperbilirubinemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contractures</td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1836","text":"Ultrasound findings in a t​win pregnancy are given b​elow: AFI of twin A is 26​ cm AFI of twin B is 4 cm‌ Urine visible in bladder​ of twin A Urine not visi‌ble in bladder of twin B ​Abnormal Doppler flow in ‍twin B What is Quintero s‌taging in this case?","raw_text":"<p>Ultrasound findings in a twin pregnancy are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>AFI of twin A is 26 cm</li>\n<li>AFI of twin B is 4 cm</li>\n<li>Urine visible in bladder of twin A</li>\n<li>Urine not visible in bladder of twin B</li>\n<li>Abnormal Doppler flow in twin B</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What is Quintero staging in this case?</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta​ge I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag​e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag​e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stag​e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage III</strong></p><p>The given USG findings are indicative of <strong>twin-twin transfusion syndrome</strong> (TTTS) with twin A being the recipient and twin B being the donor twin. <strong>Oligohydramnios</strong> (AFI&lt;5cm), <strong>absent urine</strong> and <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>Doppler</strong> flow findings in the donor twin indicate <strong>stage III</strong> of twin-twin transfusion syndrome according to Quintero staging.</p>\n<p><strong>Quintero</strong> <strong>staging</strong> system for <strong>TTTS</strong> classification:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stage</td>\n<td>USG findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Discordant amniotic fluid volumes + urine visible in donor's bladder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Discordant amniotic fluid volumes + urine not visible in donor's bladder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Stage II + abnormal Doppler studies of umbilical artery/vein or ductus venosus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Ascites or frank hydrops in either twin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>Death of either twin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1837","text":"An anxious mother pregnant​ with twins was recently ​diagnosed with twin-twin ​transfusion syndrome. She‌ wants to know the possib​le fetal outcome of the p‌regnancy. Which of the fo​llowing investigations ca‍n be used to predict this‌?","raw_text":"An anxious mother pregnant with twins was recently diagnosed with twin-twin transfusion syndrome. She wants to know the possible fetal outcome of the pregnancy. Which of the following investigations can be used to predict this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deepest vertical pocket - ​USG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal hemoglobin estimatio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MPI - ventr​icle Doppler","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peak systolic velocity - M​CA Doppler","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MPI - ventricle Doppler","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MPI - ventricle Doppler</strong></p><p>In twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS), the<strong> fetal outcome</strong> depends on the <strong>cardiac function</strong> of the <strong>recipient</strong> twin. <strong>Tei index</strong> or <strong>myocardial performance index</strong> (MPI) is used to assess the ventricular function using doppler.</p>\n<p>Option A: Deepest vertical pocket on USG is used to diagnose polyhydramnios-oligohydramnios in TTTS.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Fetal haemoglobin and MCA peak systolic velocity are used to diagnose TAPS (Twin anaemia polycythemia syndrome).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1839","text":"What is/are the placental ​vascular abnormality that​ can be seen in the condi​tion depicted in the imag‌e given below?","raw_text":"What is/are the placental vascular abnormality that can be seen in the condition depicted in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artery – artery anastomosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vein – vein​ anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artery – vei​n anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A​ and B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both A and B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/22d48eae5f72400e996c5dc312c5d304.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35e5b4c6d3b04f45a9c7ca25c4f9cfafx720x619.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both A and B</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>acardius acephalus</strong> fetus. This refers to the acardiac twin seen in <strong>twin reversed arterial perfusion</strong> (TRAP). Both <strong>artery-artery</strong> and <strong>vein-vein</strong> anastomosis can be seen in TRAP.</p>\n<p>TRAP is a placental vascular anastomotic complication seen in <strong>monochorionic</strong> twins. In this disorder, deoxygenated <strong>blood from</strong> the <strong>donor</strong> twin enters the recipient twin through the anastomosis and<strong> supplies</strong> the <strong>lower half</strong> of the <strong>recipient’s</strong> body. Hence, the <strong>heart</strong> and <strong>upper body</strong> of the recipient is <strong>poorly developed</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image depicts the circulation pattern seen in TRAP.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Based on the supply of deoxygenated blood, the <strong>recipient twin</strong> might have the following <strong>fates</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fate</td>\n<td>Type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower body is perfused with failed head growth</td>\n<td>Acardius acephalus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partially developed head with identifiable limbs</td>\n<td>Acardius myelecephalus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No recognizable structure</td>\n<td>Acardius amorphous</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>donor</strong> twin develops high output<strong> cardiac failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> treatment</strong> for this condition is <strong>radiofrequency ablation</strong> of the acardiac or recipient twin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1841","text":"A 25-year-old woman, pregn​ant with twins, goes into​ labour. Following the va​ginal delivery of the fir‌st twin, she was taken to​ the OT for internal vers‌ion. What could have been​ the possible indication ‍for this procedure?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman, pregnant with twins, goes into labour. Following the vaginal delivery of the first twin, she was taken to the OT for internal version. What could have been the possible indication for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2nd twin breech presentati​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd twin knee presentation​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd twin tr​ansverse lie","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2nd twin shoulder presenta​tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2nd twin transverse lie","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2nd twin transverse lie</strong></p><p>The <strong>second</strong> <strong>twin</strong> being in <strong>transverse</strong> lie is the only indication for <strong>internal</strong> <strong>version</strong> in modern obstetrics.</p>\n<p>In a twin pregnancy, if the first twin has vertex presentation, vaginal delivery can be performed. Following the vaginal delivery of the first twin, if the second twin is in transverse lie, it is corrected by <strong>external</strong> <strong>version</strong> (preferably cephalic) into a longitudinal lie. If external version fails and the <strong>membranes</strong> are <strong>intact</strong>, <strong>internal version</strong> is done under general anaesthesia, followed by <strong>breech</strong> <strong>extraction</strong>. </p>\n<p>If the membranes are <strong>ruptured</strong>, <strong>caesarean</strong> section is done immediately.</p>\n<p>Mode of delivery depends on the presentation of the 1st twin:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Presentation of the 1st twin</td>\n<td>Mode of delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertex</td>\n<td>Vaginal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breech</td>\n<td>Caesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transverse</td>\n<td>Caesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5266","text":"Identify thi​s condition.","raw_text":"Identify this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Twin to Twin Transfusion S​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monochorionic diamniotic p​regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monochorionic monoamniotic​ pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dichorionic diamniotic pre​gnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Monochorionic monoamniotic pregnancy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/299f93b512f2498693c743c9fa249349.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6faf0d666866408e88d09d1ca99e6b1fx382x566.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1828892d8b264029afc5e0f9fdde741ex1280x5795.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Monochorionic monoamniotic pregnancy</strong></p><p>The given specimen depicts a <strong>monochorionic</strong> <strong>monoamniotic</strong> pregnancy as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>2 umbilical cords</strong> are arising from <strong>1 placenta </strong>(<strong>monochorionic</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>No intervening amniotic membrane</strong> is attached to the placenta. This means that both fetuses must have been inside a single amniotic cavity (<strong>monoamniotic</strong>). </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>Monochorionic diamniotic</strong> pregnancy, on <strong>gross examination</strong>, there will be <strong>1 placenta</strong> and <strong>intervening</strong> 2 layers of <strong>amnion attached</strong> to the placenta. In this type of pregnancy, each fetus will have its own amniotic cavity (diamniotic) with a shared placenta (monochorionic). This is seen if the <strong>fertilized zygote divides </strong>into 2 between <strong>4-8 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below given gross image of <strong>monochorionic diamniotic pregnancy</strong> shows <strong>two umbilical cords</strong> arising separately from the placenta with the <strong>intervening amniotic membrane</strong> held by the forceps.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>dichorionic diamniotic</strong> pregnancy each fetus has it’s own <strong>amnion &amp; chorion</strong>. The placentas here may be fused or separate. This is seen in <strong>dizygotic twins</strong> as well as <strong>monozygotic twins that divide</strong> within <strong>0-4 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Twin to twin transfusion syndrome</strong> (TTTS)  most commonly complicates <strong>monochorionic</strong> (single placenta) <strong>twin pregnancy</strong>. In TTTS blood is transfused from the donor twin to the recipient twin through the abnormal vascular connections within the placenta leaving the donor anemic while the recipient becomes polycythemic.</p>\n<p>The box below describes the type of amnion and chorion seen in different types of twins depending on the time of division.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 909px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">Dizygotic twins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 546.5px;\">Dichorionic diamniotic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">\n<p>Monozygotic twins- division occurs within</p>\n<p>0-4 days</p>\n<p>4-8 days</p>\n<p>8-12 days</p>\n<p>&gt;13 days</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 546.5px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Dichorionic diamniotic</p>\n<p>Monochorionic diamniotic</p>\n<p>Monochorionic monoamniotic</p>\n<p>Monochorionic monoamniotic conjoined twins</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1842","text":"A pregnant woman was diagn​osed with triplets on ult​rasound. The obstetrician​ has planned to do a sele‌ctive fetal reduction of ​one of the fetuses to imp‌rove the outcome of the p​regnancy. What is the ide‍al timing and the method ‌for the procedure?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman was diagnosed with triplets on ultrasound. The obstetrician has planned to do a selective fetal reduction of one of the fetuses to improve the outcome of the pregnancy. What is the ideal timing and the method for the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10-13 weeks with intracard​iac KCl","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"7-9 weeks with intracardia​c KCl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14-16 weeks with intraumbi​lical KCl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9-12 weeks with intraumbil​ical KCl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10-13 weeks with intracardiac KCl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10-13 weeks with intracardiac KCl</strong></p><p><strong>Selective fetal reduction</strong> is done between <strong>10-13 weeks</strong> via <strong>intracardiac</strong> injection of<strong> KCl</strong>.</p>\n<p>In multi-chorionic <strong>multifetal</strong> gestations, selective reduction of the <strong>smallest</strong> or <strong>anomalous</strong> fetus/fetuses is done to increase the chance of survival of the other fetuses. </p>\n<p><strong>Laser ablation</strong> or <strong>bipolar coagulation</strong> of the <strong>umbilical cord</strong> of the targeted fetus is done. This protects the surviving fetuses from the adverse effects of the drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0691","text":"A 35-year-old primigravida​ who conceived after in-v​itro fertilization therap​y came for an antenatal c‌heckup at 38 weeks of ges​tation. Her obstetric his‌tory revealed that she wa​s pregnant with DCDA twin‍s. On examination, the fi‌rst twin was found to be ‍in a breech position and ​the maternal blood pressu‍re was found to be greate‌r than 140/90 mm Hg on tw​o occasions with grade 1+‌ proteinuria. What should‌ be done next?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old primigravida who conceived after in-vitro fertilization therapy came for an antenatal checkup at 38 weeks of gestation. Her obstetric history revealed that she was pregnant with DCDA twins. On examination, the first twin was found to be in a breech position and the maternal blood pressure was found to be greater than 140/90 mm Hg on two occasions with grade 1+ proteinuria. What should be done next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monitor BP and terminate p​regnancy if BP rises","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate Caesarean sectio​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Terminate pregnancy at 40 ​weeks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Induce labour with PGE2 ge​l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immediate Caesarean section","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immediate Caesarean section</strong></p><p><strong>Immediate cesarean section</strong> is done as the <strong>first</strong> <strong>twin</strong> is in <strong>breech</strong> presentation. <strong>Uncomplicated dichorionic diamniotic twin</strong> pregnancies are terminated at <strong>38</strong> <strong>weeks</strong> of gestation. </p>\n<p><strong>Timing of delivery</strong> depends on the type of twin pregnancy:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type of twin pregnancy</td>\n<td>Timing of delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dichorionic diamniotic</td>\n<td>38 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monochorionic diamniotic</td>\n<td>34 - 37 weeks + 6 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monochorionic monoamniotic</td>\n<td>32 - 34 weeks by Caesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The <strong>mode of delivery</strong> depends on the 1st twin:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1st twin presentation</td>\n<td>Mode of delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertex</td>\n<td>Vaginal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breech</td>\n<td>Cesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transverse</td>\n<td>Cesarean section</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>After delivery of 1st twin, if the 2nd twin is in breech, assisted breech delivery is done. If the 2nd twin is in a transverse lie, the internal podalic version is done under GA in the OT followed by breech extraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> in attempting vaginal delivery in non-vertex presentation (i.e) transverse and breech:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Interlocking of twins</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fetal head trapping above the cervix</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Umbilical cord prolapse</li>\n</ul>\n<p>According to ACOG criteria (2019), preeclampsia requires proteinuria of grade 2+ or more. In the case of pre-eclampsia, termination of pregnancy must be done at 37 weeks. According to the updated criteria, this patient does not have pre-eclampsia. Hence, a Caesarean section can be done at 38 weeks as for any uncomplicated DCDA twin pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0903","text":"A 26-year-old primigravida​ with dichorionic diamnio​tic twins presents to the​ clinic in active labor. ‌The first twin is deliver​ed vaginally but the seco‌nd has a transverse lie. ​A trial of ECV was unsucc‍essful. What is not a com‌plication of the procedur‍e performed next for the ​delivery of the second tw‍in?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old primigravida with dichorionic diamniotic twins presents to the clinic in active labor. The first twin is delivered vaginally but the second has a transverse lie. A trial of ECV was unsuccessful. What is not a complication of the procedure performed next for the delivery of the second twin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Face pre​sentation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uterin​e atony","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine​ rupture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vaginal l​aceration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Face presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Face presentation</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical procedure is suggestive of <strong>internal podalic version</strong> for the second twin. <strong>Shoulder presentation</strong>, and not face presentation, is a <strong>complication</strong> of this procedure.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> seen with internal podalic version are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maternal</strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Failure of the procedure</li>\n<li>Uterine rupture</li>\n<li>Vaginal and perineal trauma</li>\n<li>Placental abruption</li>\n<li>Postpartum haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Ascending infection</li>\n<li>Anaesthetic risks</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Fetal-</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Cord prolapse</li>\n<li>Shoulder presentation with prolapsed arm</li>\n<li>Skeletal injury (femur, hips, humerus, skull)</li>\n<li>Visceral injury (kidney, liver, spleen)</li>\n<li>Neural injury (facial nerve)</li>\n<li>Hypoxic brain injury</li>\n<li>Death</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FH9YHgNx","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"17_disorders_of_lacrimal_apparatus_and_glands_of_the_eye","title":"17.Disorders Of Lacrimal Apparatus And Glands Of The Eye","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC1164","text":"Moll glands are examples o‌f _______","raw_text":"Moll glands are examples of _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apocrine s‌weat gland","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eccrine s‌weat gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sebaceo‌us gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apoeccri‌ne gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Apocrine sweat gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apocrine sweat gland</strong></p><p><strong>Moll</strong> glands are examples of apocrine sweat gland.</p>\n<p>Moll glands are present in the eyelid. They are a type of <strong>modified apocrine sweat</strong> glands<strong> </strong>opening either into a lash follicle or directly onto the anterior lid margin between the lashes.</p>\n<p><strong>Zeis</strong> glands are a type of <strong>modified sebaceous</strong> glands associated with lash follicles. <strong>Meibomian</strong> glands are <strong>modified sebaceous</strong> glands found in the tarsal plates.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1167","text":"A patient presented with a‌ painless swelling and wa‍tering in his left eye. E​xamination revealed the f‌ollowing finding. Which o​f the following statement​s is false regarding his ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a painless swelling and watering in his left eye. Examination revealed the following finding. Which of the following statements is false regarding his diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It needs to be surgically ‌treated.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It affects the meibomian g‌lands.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It does not have malignant‌ potential.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a chronic lipogranul‌omatous inflammation.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It does not have malignant potential.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bf5620c36ec446bb671c1657ddc8e19.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It does not have malignant potential.</strong></p><p>A painless swelling on the lower lid is suggestive of a <strong>chalazion</strong> (tarsal cyst/meibomian cyst). It has the malignant potential to become an adenocarcinoma of the meibomian gland. </p>\n<p>Chalazion is the <strong>chronic lipogranulomatous</strong> inflammation (option D) of the <strong>meibomian gland </strong>(option B). It presents as <strong>painless swelling</strong> of the eyelid. It is more common in adults than children. Treatment includes <strong>incision</strong> and <strong>curettage</strong> (option B) and intralesional injection of triamcinolone acetate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0397","text":"A recurrent chalazion shou‌ld be subjected to histop‍athological evaluation to​ exclude the possibility ‌of _____.","raw_text":"A recurrent chalazion should be subjected to histopathological evaluation to exclude the possibility of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sebaceous gl‌and carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adenosquamo‌us carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sebaceous gland carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sebaceous gland carcinoma</strong></p><p>A recurrent chalazion should be subjected to histopathological evaluation to exclude the possibility of <strong>sebaceous gland carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>A chalazion can undergo malignant change into sebaceous gland carcinoma (adenocarcinoma of meibomian gland).</p>\n<p>Recurrences of chalazia are not uncommon. However, the physician should entertain the possibility of malignancy in such cases and should biopsy a lesion that recurs or appears atypical. The pathologist should be alerted to the suspicion of sebaceous gland carcinoma and frozen sections and lipid stains should be requested.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0390","text":"A patient presented to the‌ clinic with severe left ‍eye pain, watering, and s​welling of the lid for 2 ‌days. He says it's diffic​ult to see the laptop scr​een while working and he ‌is unable to complete his​ daily targets. On examin‌ation, tender swelling is​ noted as shown below. Wh​at is the cause of this f​inding?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the clinic with severe left eye pain, watering, and swelling of the lid for 2 days. He says it's difficult to see the laptop screen while working and he is unable to complete his daily targets. On examination, tender swelling is noted as shown below. What is the cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute infection of Zeis gl‌and","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute infection of Moll gl‌and","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute infection of meibomi‌an gland","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic infection of meibo‌mian gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute infection of meibomian gland","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/62c48ba5a807491788b3f965166fefd9.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f2ebd6202f04cf3a658a4f5f370c05ax548x448.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/834e0d5643ee4817b7407bbe29213bb0x572x452.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute infection of meibomian gland</strong></p><p>The patient has acute presentation and painful swelling in the palpebral conjunctiva as shown in the image, suggestive of <strong>internal hordeolum. </strong>It is an <strong>acute</strong> <strong>suppurative</strong> inflammation of the <strong>meibomian gland </strong>associated with blockage of the duct.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Hordeolum externum</strong> or <strong>stye</strong> is the acute<strong> </strong>staphylococcal infection of the <strong>hair follicle</strong>/<strong>Zeis gland/</strong><strong>Moll gland</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Chalazion</strong> is a <strong>chronic </strong>non-suppurative lipogranulomatous inflammation of the <strong>Meibomian gland</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1163","text":"What is the length of the ‌nasolacrimal duct?","raw_text":"What is the length of the nasolacrimal duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10-1‌4 mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12-1‌8 mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18-2‌4 mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8-1‌2 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 12-18 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21937b328e6a431a9786539b2c8604f1x1280x1023.6389280677009.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 12-18 mm</strong></p><p>The nasolacrimal duct is <strong>12–18 mm </strong>long.  </p>\n<p>The lacrimal drainage system consists of the <strong>lacrimal sac</strong>, which is <strong>10–12 mm</strong> long. It lies in the<strong> lacrimal fossa</strong>, between the anterior and posterior lacrimal crests. The lacrimal bone and the frontal process of the maxilla separate the lacrimal sac from the middle meatus of the nasal cavity.</p>\n<p>The nasolacrimal duct is the inferior continuation of the lacrimal sac. It is <strong>12–18 mm</strong> long and is directed downwards, backward, and laterally. The opening of the duct into the inferior meatus is partially covered by a mucosal fold (valve of <strong>Hasner</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>puncta</strong> are openings on the lid margin. There are two canaliculi from the puncta. Each canaliculus (superior and inferior) has two parts: Vertical (2 mm) and horizontal (8 mm). The superior and inferior canaliculi usually unite to form the <strong>common</strong> canaliculus, which opens into the <strong>lateral wall </strong>of the<strong> lacrimal sac</strong>.<strong> </strong>A small flap of mucosa <strong>(Rosenmüller valve)</strong> overhangs the junction of the common canaliculus and the lacrimal sac and<strong> </strong>prevents the reflux of tears into the canaliculi.</p>\n<p>The image below shows structures of the lacrimal apparatus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0399","text":"Where does the nasolacrima‌l duct open?","raw_text":"Where does the nasolacrimal duct open?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supreme‌ meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superio‌r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle‌ meatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferio‌r meatus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inferior meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inferior meatus</strong></p><p>The <strong>nasolacrimal duct</strong> opens into the <strong>inferior meatus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nasolacrimal duct is the inferior continuation of the lacrimal sac. It is 12–18 mm long and is directed downwards, backward, and laterally. The opening of the duct into the inferior meatus is partially covered by a mucosal fold, called the valve of <strong>Hasner</strong>. The valve of Hasner <strong>prevents reflux</strong> from the nose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0401","text":"A 60-year-old woman came f‌or an eye exam due to a g‍ritty sensation that has ​been causing her a lot of‌ discomfort. On examinati​on, Rose Bengal staining ​is positive. She is diagn‌osed with keratoconjuncti​vitis sicca. Which of the‌ following layer of the t​ear film is deficient in ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman came for an eye exam due to a gritty sensation that has been causing her a lot of discomfort. On examination, Rose Bengal staining is positive. She is diagnosed with keratoconjunctivitis sicca. Which of the following layer of the tear film is deficient in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueou‌s layer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid‌ layer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mucin‌ layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aqueous layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aqueous layer</strong></p><p><strong>Keratoconjunctivitis sicca</strong> is due to the deficiency of the <strong>aqueous layer</strong> of the tear film.</p>\n<p>In keratoconjunctivitis sicca, patients present with <strong>irritation</strong> and foreign body sensation. Marginal tear strip is reduced. The conjunctiva become dry and lustreless with positive <strong>Rose Bengal </strong>staining. Causes of keratoconjunctivitis sicca:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sjogren's syndrome</strong> (primary keratoconjunctivitis sicca)</li>\n<li><strong>Non-Sjogren's</strong> keratoconjunctivitis sicca\n<ul>\n<li>Primary age-related hyposecretion (most common)</li>\n<li>Lacrimal gland deficiencies - congenital, infiltration by tumors or sarcoidosis, surgical removal</li>\n<li>Lacrimal gland duct obstruction - old trachoma, burns,  SJS</li>\n<li>Reflex hyposecretion (neurogenic causes) - Parkinson disease, 7th nerve palsy, reflex sensory block</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1195","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the phenol red test for ​dry eye?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the phenol red test for dry eye?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It requires topical anesth‌esia.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It measures tear productio‌n based on the length of ‍colour change in the thre​ad","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"If color changes to blue, ‌it depicts mucin deficien‍cy.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It requires‌ a pH meter.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It measures tear production based on the length of colour change in the thread","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It measures tear production based on the length of colour change in the thread</strong></p><p><strong>Phenol red test</strong> measures the tear production according to the length of colour change in the thread.</p>\n<p>In phenol red test, the <strong>alkalinity</strong> of tears changes its colour to <strong>bright orange</strong> on contact. It doesn't require anaesthesia (unlike Schirmer's test). Three millimetres of a fine dye-impregnated 75-mm cotton thread is placed under the lateral one-fifth of the inferior palpebral lid margin for 15 seconds.</p>\n<p>Based on length of colour change,</p>\n<ul>\n<li> &gt;20 mm - normal tear production     </li>\n<li> 10 to 19 mm - borderline dry eye     </li>\n<li> &lt;10 mm - severe dry eye</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0403","text":"Identify the test shown be‌low.","raw_text":"Identify the test shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schirm‌er test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ishiha‌ra test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jones‌ test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goldmann's‌ tonometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Schirmer test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/370bd2a124664a4b8cb0bcf1e73cbde1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Schirmer test</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>tear strip</strong> placed in the lower lid of the eye. This is the <strong>Schirmer test </strong>which is used to check for dry eye.</p>\n<p><strong>Dry</strong> <strong>eye </strong>is a multifactorial disease of the eye characterised by loss of homeostasis of the tear film. Patients present with irritation, foreign body sensation and dryness. Treatment of dry eye includes medical treatment (lubricating eyedrops, most commonly <strong>methylcellulose</strong> derivatives) or surgical treatment (lacrimal punctum occlusion using <strong>silicone</strong> plugs).</p>\n<p>Other tests for dry eye include: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Tear film break-up test</strong> is used to determine the interval between a complete blink and appearance of first randomly distributed dry spot on the cornea. A drop of fluorescein is instilled in the eye, following by examination in the cobalt-blue light of a slit-lamp.</li>\n<li><strong>Rose Bengal </strong>staining is used for the detection of dry, dead epithelium in dry eye.</li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Tear osmolarity test</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> is performed with an osmometer. </span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Other options:</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Option B:<strong> Ishihara</strong> plates are used to test colour vision. They help detect red-green colour blindness. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Option C: <strong>Jones</strong> test is used in watering eye. It uses fluorescein dye. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Option D: <strong>Goldmann's tonometry</strong> is used to measure intra-ocular pressure. It consists of a double prism mounted on a slit-lamp.  </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7191","text":"You are conducting retrosp‌ective research on factor‍s responsible for dry eye​ disorder. As a part of y‌our study, you are lookin​g through the files in th​e clinic of dry eye patie‌nts. Which among the foll​owing patients would not ‌be included in your study​?","raw_text":"You are conducting retrospective research on factors responsible for dry eye disorder.  As a part of your study, you are looking through the files in the clinic of dry eye patients. Which among the following patients would not be included in your study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 26 year old patient show‌ing decreased levels of l‍actoferrin in tear sample​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 20 year old patient show‌ing tear film break up at‍ 18-20 seconds","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 24 year old patient show‌ing A pattern stain in Ro‍se bengal staining","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 22 year old patient show‌ing Schirmer test tear st‍rip wet upto 9 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 20 year old patient showing tear film break up at 18-20 seconds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 20 year old patient showing tear film break up at 18-20 seconds</strong></p><p>A patient with tear film break up at 18-20 seconds is considered to be <strong>normal. </strong>All the other patients have dry eye.</p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Tear film break-up </strong>(BUT)<strong> time</strong> </span>is the interval between a complete blink and the appearance of a first randomly distributed dry spot on the cornea. It is an indicator of the adequacy of the <strong>mucin</strong> component of tears.</p>\n<p>Inference:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>15-35 seconds - normal</strong></li>\n<li>&lt; 10 seconds- unstable tear film</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Tear samples can be assayed for the presence of markers known to be <strong>decreased</strong> (e.g. <strong>lactoferrin</strong>) or elevated (e.g. matrix metalloproteinase-9) in dry eye. This is known as tear constituent measurement. </p>\n<p>Option C: A patient with A pattern on Rose Bengal staining has severe dry eye. </p>\n<p>Option D: A patient with 9mm wet Schirmer strip has moderate dry eye. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0405","text":"The findings of a test don‌e in a patient under eval‍uation for dry eye are sh​own below. Identify the t‌est.","raw_text":"The findings of a test done in a patient under evaluation for dry eye are shown below. Identify the test.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regurgita‌tion test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dacryocy‌stography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jones d‌ye test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lacrimal s‌cintigraphy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lacrimal scintigraphy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aaab16a3d0154994b9b8be6000ab4cab.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c37fb78ce0246b18f878048db091f69x1280x3098.8998589562766.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lacrimal scintigraphy</strong></p><p>The image above depicts<strong> lacrimal scintigraphy.</strong></p>\n<p>Lacrimal scintigraphy (dacryoscintigraphy) is used to assess the lacrimal drainage function. A <strong>radioactive tracer</strong> (technetium-99m) is instilled into the conjunctival sac. Its path is then visualised with an Anger gamma camera. It is useful for the evaulation of <strong>watering eye</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other tests for evaluation of watering eye include regurgitation test, syringing, fluorescein dye disappearance test (FDDT), Jones dye test and dacryocystography.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>regurgitation test</strong>, steady pressure with the index finger is applied above the medial palpebral ligament. </p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>dacryocystography</strong>, a radioopaque material is inserted into the lacrimal sac, following which X-rays are taken. </p>\n<p>Option C: The image shows the <strong>Jones dye test.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0407","text":"What is the site of blocka‌ge in congenital dacryocy‍stitis?","raw_text":"What is the site of blockage in congenital dacryocystitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lacrimal ‌canaliculi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasolacr‌imal duct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pun‌ctum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lacrim‌al sac","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasolacrimal duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dffbdc801b014694a7c3356b46746f5dx652x444.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasolacrimal duct</strong></p><p>The site of blockage in congenital dacryocystitis is the <strong>nasolacrimal duct</strong>. </p>\n<p>Congenital dacryocystitis occurs due to the stasis of secretions in the <strong>lacrimal sac</strong> due to congenital blockage in the nasolacrimal duct. <strong>Membranous occlusion</strong> at its lower end near the <strong>valve of Hasner</strong> is the <strong>commonest cause.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a neonate with congenital dacryocystitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0409","text":"A mother noticed the follo‌wing finding in her 3-mon‍th-old child. There is a ​history of watering and d‌ischarge from his right e​ye since birth. Which of ​the following is the best‌ next step in the treatme​nt?","raw_text":"A mother noticed the following finding in her 3-month-old child. There is a history of watering  and discharge from his right eye since birth. Which of the following is the best next step in the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syri‌nging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Probing wit‌h syringing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sac massage with topical a‌ntibiotics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dacryocyst‌orhinostomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sac massage with topical antibiotics","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/51c494f122c74c4395e25737b89a09e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sac massage with topical antibiotics</strong></p><p>The image showing  right-sided swelling in the lacrimal sac area along with watering of the eye is suggestive of <strong>congenital dacryocystitis. </strong>Treatment for a 3-month-old child includes <strong>sac massage </strong>with <strong>topical antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>Congenital dacryocystitis is chronic inflammation of the lacrimal sac in newborn infants. It occurs due to congenital blockage of the nasolacrimal duct. More than <strong>90%</strong> of patients with undergo <strong>spontaneous resolution</strong> by <strong>1 year </strong>of age.</p>\n<p>Treatment of congenital dacryocystitis depends on the <strong>age</strong> of the child:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less than 9 months: Massaging<strong> </strong>(<strong>Crigler’s</strong> massage which increases the hydrostatic pressure within the sac) and antibiotic eye drops</li>\n<li>9 months to 4 years: <strong>Probing</strong></li>\n<li>Older children (&gt; 4 years):<strong> Dacryocystorhinostomy</strong> (DCR) gives the best result. In DCR, an opening is made into the <strong>nasal cavity</strong> from the <strong>lacrimal sac</strong>.<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1193","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‌ with watering from the r‍ight eye for 1 year. Syri​nging revealed a patent d‌rainage system. Rest of t​he ocular examination was​ normal. A provisional di‌agnosis of lacrimal pump ​failure was made. Which o‌f the following test will​ help in the confirmation​ of the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with watering from the right eye for 1 year. Syringing revealed a patent drainage system. Rest of the ocular examination was normal. A provisional diagnosis of lacrimal pump failure was made. Which of the following test will help in the confirmation of the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dacryocy‌stography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dacryosci‌ntigraphy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Regurgita‌tion test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Canaliculus irrigation tes‌t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dacryoscintigraphy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dacryoscintigraphy</strong></p><p><strong>Lacrimal pump failure</strong> can be confirmed with dacryoscintigraphy.</p>\n<p>Lacrimal pump failure means an <strong>inability</strong> of the lid muscles<strong> </strong>to <strong>push tears</strong> towards the punctum for drainage. It is also called <strong>functional</strong> <strong>obstruction </strong>and may be secondary to lower lid laxity or orbicularis muscle weakness (eg. facial nerve palsy. It is confirmed by dacryoscintigraphy, in which a radioactive tracer (technetium-99m) is instilled into the conjunctival sac and its path visualised with an Anger gamma camera. An <strong>atonic </strong>lacrimal sac is seen. </p>\n<p>Note: In cases of partial obstruction of the lacrimal apparatus, forced injection of fluid into the canalicular system (dacryocystography) can overcome the obstruction sites and point to a diagnosis of lacrimal pump failure. Thus, dacryoscintigraphy is better for the confirmation of lacrimal pump failure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0415","text":"A 55-year-old woman is bei‌ng counseled for surgery ‍for her long-standing dac​ryocystitis. Which of the‌ following is the surgery​ of choice?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman is being counseled for surgery for her long-standing dacryocystitis. Which of the following is the surgery of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dacryocyst‌orhinostomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dacryocy‌stectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conjunctivo-cystorhinostom‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Balloon cath‌etor dilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dacryocystorhinostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dacryocystorhinostomy</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice for <strong>chronic dacryocystitis</strong> is <strong>dacryocystorhinostomy</strong> (DCR)</p>\n<p>DCR involves removal of the bone adjacent to the nasolacrimal sac and suturing the lacrimal sac with the lateral nasal mucosa. It re-establishes the lacrimal drainage and is hence the preferred surgery for chronic darcyocystectomy. </p>\n<p>Chronic dacryocystitis is a long-standing inflammation of the lacrimal sac. <em>Streptococcus hemolyticus</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> are the most common etiological agents. Initial treatment is with antibiotics and anti-inflammatory agents. Sequelae include lacrimal <strong>fistula </strong>formation and <strong>mucocele</strong> formation, which when infected, leads to <strong>pyocele</strong> formation and finally lacrimal fibrosis. Treatment of lacrimal sac <strong>fibrosis</strong> is <strong>dacryocystectomy.</strong> Treatment of lacrimal fistula is <strong>dacryocystorhinostomy </strong>(DCR).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0411","text":"A patient with chronic dac‌ryocystitis, refractory t‍o the medical treatment, ​has been scheduled for da‌cryocystorhinostomy. Foll​owing the surgery, the sa​c would be draining into ‌the _______.","raw_text":"A patient with chronic dacryocystitis, refractory to the medical treatment, has been scheduled for dacryocystorhinostomy. Following the surgery, the sac would be draining into the _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio‌r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferio‌r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle‌ meatus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medial wall of nasal cavit‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle meatus</strong></p><p>After dacryocystorhinostomy, the lacrimal sac drains into the <strong>middle meatus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dacryocystorhinostomy</strong> (DCR) surgery involves removal of the bone adjacent to the nasolacrimal sac and suturing the lacrimal sac with the lateral nasal mucosa. This allows tears to drain directly from the <strong>sac</strong> via a new low-resistance pathway into the <strong>middle meatus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0416","text":"What is the most common oc‌ular manifestation in mum‍ps?","raw_text":"What is the most common ocular manifestation in mumps?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dacryocys‌tadenitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chorior‌etinitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior‌ uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membranous c‌onjunctivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dacryocystadenitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dacryocystadenitis</strong></p><p>The most common ocular manifestation in <strong>mumps</strong> is <strong>dacryocystadenitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dacryocystadenitis, or inflammation of the lacrimal gland, is seen in mumps. It presents as a painful swelling in the lateral part of the upper lid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9246","text":"Which is the most common l‌acrimal gland tumor?","raw_text":"Which is the most common lacrimal gland tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoid cyst‌ic carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pleomorph‌ic adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucoepidermo‌id carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant ‌mixed tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pleomorphic adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pleomorphic adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Pleomorphic adenoma</strong> is the most common lacrimal gland tumor. It is also the most common epithelial tumor of the lacrimal gland.</p>\n<p>Pleomorphic adenoma, or <strong>benign mixed tumor,</strong> presents commonly in <strong>middle age</strong> as a slowly progressive painless swelling in the upper outer quadrant of the eyelid. The bone is usually not invaded in these tumors. They are treated by surgical removal along with their intact pseudo-capsule.</p>\n<p>Malignant tumors of the lacrimal gland such as <strong>adenoid cystic carcinoma </strong>(option A), <strong>mucoepidermoid carcinoma </strong>(option C) and <strong>malignant mixed tumor </strong>(option D) generally occur in the elderly with a short history of appearance, rapid progression and pain. These tumors also present in the upper eyelid and are known to invade the adjacent bones. <strong>Radical surgical</strong> procedures and high dose <strong>radiotherapy</strong> are necessary for the removal of the tumor. <strong>Adenoid cystic carcinoma</strong> is the most common malignant epithelial tumor of the lacrimal gland.  </p>\n<p>Both malignant and benign tumors are known to push the eyeball downwards and inwards while restricting the upwards and outwards movement of the eyeball.</p>\n<p>The most common <strong>epithelial tumors</strong>, in order of frequency, are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Benign mixed tumor (BMT), also known as a pleomorphic adenoma (PA)</li>\n<li>Adenoid cystic carcinoma (ACC)</li>\n<li>Pleomorphic adenocarcinoma (ACA)</li>\n<li>Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rVH3eO7I","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"17_skeletal_tuberculosis","title":"17.Skeletal Tuberculosis","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA3489","text":"Which of the following is ​most commonly affected by​ skeletal tuberculosis in​ adults?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most commonly affected by skeletal tuberculosis in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervica​l spine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorso-lum​bar spine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hip ​joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sacroili​ac joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dorso-lumbar spine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dorso-lumbar spine</strong></p><p><strong>Skeletal tuberculosis</strong> most commonly affects the<strong> dorso-lumbar </strong>(thoraco-lumbar)<strong> spine</strong> in<strong> adults </strong>(thoracic &gt; lumbar).</p>\n<p>The <strong>cervical spine</strong> is most commonly affected in <strong>children.</strong></p>\n<p>Skeletal TB affects the <strong>axial</strong> skeleton <strong>more than</strong> the <strong>appendicular</strong> skeleton. Hence, spinal TB is more common as compared to TB of the joints. In the appendicular skeleton, the hip and knee joints are more commonly affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3488","text":"A 37-year-old man on treat​ment for tuberculosis pre​sented with back pain. Hi​story revealed non-compli​ance to ATT and you suspe​ct the disease has dissem‍inated to the spine. Whic‍h part of the vertebra is​ most likely to be involv‌ed in this condition?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old man on treatment for tuberculosis presented with back pain. History revealed non-compliance to ATT and you suspect the disease has disseminated to the spine. Which part of the vertebra is most likely to be involved in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98bb85dfcf7a4060a011cbea75c6e9bf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74b3d14c2415461db3079fae9aa31494x1280x1484.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/199235fc69e74c4885e07c23e214ccd3x1280x3636.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2</strong></p><p>In cases of <strong>spinal tuberculosis</strong>, the <strong>paradiscal area </strong>(marked '2' in the image)<strong> </strong>of the vertebra is most commonly involved. </p>\n<p>The probable reason for this is the<strong> rich blood supply</strong> of this area which favors the <strong>high oxygen requirement</strong> of the tuberculosis bacilli. Appendiceal/ posterior vertebral involvement is the least commonly seen in skeletal TB.</p>\n<p>The structures marked in the image are as shown below:</p>\n<p>1 - Posterior spinal elements</p>\n<p>2 - Paradiscal area of the vertebra</p>\n<p>3 - Anterior vertebral body</p>\n<p>4 - Central vertebral body</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3349","text":"Which of the following fea​tures is least likely to ​be seen in patients with ​spinal tuberculosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is least likely to be seen in patients with spinal tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exaggerated lumbar lordosi​s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exaggerated thoracic kypho​sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased flexion of the v​ertebral column","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased extension of the​ vertebral column","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Exaggerated lumbar lordosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b4c6931a5de48d99cc0d1c701b3841bx1280x2033.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Exaggerated lumbar lordosis</strong></p><p>Spinal tuberculosis leads to <strong>loss of lumbar lordosis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Spinal TB</strong> more often causes <strong>compression fracture</strong> of the anterior part of the vertebrae. This leads to <strong>anterior wedging</strong> of the vertebrae giving rise to<strong> kyphosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>When spinal TB involves the thoracic vertebrae, it <strong>exaggerates</strong> the existing <strong>thoracic kyphosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of spinal tuberculosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Back <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>stiffness</strong> are the usual early complaints</li>\n<li><strong>Cautious</strong> <strong>gait</strong> - a person with spinal TB will walk carefully with short steps to avoid pain resulting from movement</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased flexion and extension</strong> - all the movements of the vertebral column are decreased in spinal TB due to the associated paraspinal muscle spasm, pain, and skeletal deformity</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3424","text":"The following deformity wa​s seen in a patient with ​spinal TB. What is it cal​led?","raw_text":"The following deformity was seen in a patient with spinal TB. What is it called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Knu​ckle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gib​bus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kyph​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vertebr​a plana","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gibbus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/903c61678ad9491fa9b787f0388ba148.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3324f492b22473e8977358bd0944b9bx1280x2309.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c85658dd636e4c1a9f92d15f8848038ex1280x1968.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gibbus</strong></p><p>The skeletal deformity shown above is<strong> gibbus. </strong>It involves the <strong>collapse </strong>of<strong> two or three vertebral </strong>levels and presents as <strong>sharp posterior angulation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Spinal tuberculosis</strong> may present with the following<strong> skeletal deformities</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px; width: 404.078px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">Deformity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Mechanism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.078px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">Knuckle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Collapse of a single vertebra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.078px;\">Prominence of a single spinous process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">Gibbus/Kyphus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Collapse of 2-3 vertebral levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.078px;\">Sharp posterior angulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">Kyphosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Collapse of anterior vertebral bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.078px;\">Exaggerated curvature of the vertebral column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">Concertina collapse / vertebra plana</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Collapse of the entire vertebral body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.078px;\">Seen in tuberculosis of the central vertebral body</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3437","text":"Which among the following ​is an early radiographic ​finding in spinal tubercu​losis?","raw_text":"Which among the following is an early radiographic finding in spinal tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction o​f disc space","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bird’s ne​st abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sclerotic appearance of ve​rtebrae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior wedging of verteb​rae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reduction of disc space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduction of disc space</strong></p><p>The <strong>reduction </strong>of<strong> disc space</strong> between the vertebrae is an <strong>early radiographic finding</strong> in spinal tuberculosis. This indicates the destruction of the intervertebral disc due to spinal tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>The infection first involves the vertebral bodies and the intervertebral disc. Hence, the early radiographic features include rarefaction/osteopenic appearance of the involved vertebrae and reduction of disc space between the vertebrae.</p>\n<p>In <strong>later stages</strong>, the infection leads to para-vertebral abscess formation and vertebral destruction. These features can be seen on a radiograph as<strong> bird’s nest abscess</strong> and <strong>anterior wedging </strong>of<strong> vertebrae</strong> respectively.</p>\n<p>When the disease is in the<strong> healing phase</strong>, the <strong>sclerotic </strong>appearance of<strong> vertebrae</strong> can be seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3420","text":"A 44-year-old who had been​ diagnosed with spinal tu​berculosis 3 years back n​ow presents with parapleg​ia. What is the most like​ly cause for his presenta‍tion?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old who had been diagnosed with spinal tuberculosis 3 years back now presents with paraplegia. What is the most likely cause for his presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflammat​ory edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vertebral​ collapse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vertebra​l abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granulation tissue in the ​vertebra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vertebral collapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vertebral collapse</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> late-onset paraplegia</strong> in spinal tuberculosis. It is because of <strong>vertebral collapse</strong> which causes injury to the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>Spinal tuberculosis with neurological involvement is known as <strong>Pott’s paraplegia</strong>. It is classified as early-onset and late-onset paraplegia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early-onset paraplegia</strong> occurs <strong>within 2 years</strong> of disease onset. It is due to edema, abscess, or granulation tissue that causes <strong>spinal cord compression</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Late-onset paraplegia</strong> in spinal tuberculosis is because of vertebral collapse which causes <strong>injury to the spinal cord.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prognosis is better in<strong> early-onset</strong> paresis because it can <strong>subside</strong> when the active <strong>infection is treated</strong> or when the drainage of the abscess <strong>relieves compression</strong> <strong>of</strong> the <strong>cord</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3422","text":"Which of the following is ​seen in the final stage o​f Pott’s paraplegia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in the final stage of Pott’s paraplegia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flaccid ​paralysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spast​icity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraplegia ​in extension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive Bab​inski's sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flaccid paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flaccid paralysis</strong></p><p><strong>Flaccid paralysis </strong>is seen in the <strong>final stage</strong> of Pott’s paraplegia<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pott's paraplegia</strong> begins as a <strong>UMN lesion</strong>. So initially, the patient is <strong>asymptomatic</strong> but ankle clonus, increased deep tendon reflexes, and extensor plantar can be detected on clinical examination.</p>\n<p>It then <strong>progresses</strong> to <strong>spastic paraplegia</strong> of the extensors and the flexors. In <strong>later</strong> stages, it presents as an <strong>LMN lesion</strong> causing<strong> flaccid paralysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3438","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi​ng the management of pati​ents with Pott’s parapleg​ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding the management of patients with Pott’s paraplegia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bladder involvement is an ​indication for surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid onset paraplegia is ​an indication for surgery​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterolateral decompressio​n is the surgery of choic​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Laminectomy is indicated w​hen paravertebral abscess​ is present","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Laminectomy is indicated when paravertebral abscess is present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Laminectomy is indicated when paravertebral abscess is present</strong></p><p><strong>Laminectomy</strong> is indicated when the <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>parts</strong> of the vertebrae are<strong> involved</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Management of Pott’s paraplegia</strong> includes conservative management (with antitubercular therapy) or surgery.</p>\n<p>The \"<strong>middle</strong> <strong>path</strong> <strong>regimen\"</strong> combines both the approaches and includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antitubercular therapy</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immobilization using spinal braces</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Draining of abscess/surgery if required</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> is <strong>indicated</strong> in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Failed conservative management</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Recurrence of the disease after treatment</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Rapid onset paraplegia</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bladder</strong> and bowel <strong>involvement</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surgeries for Pott’s paraplegia</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Antero-lateral decompression</strong> - it helps in the removal of both solid and liquid debris and so it's the <strong>surgery</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>choice</strong> for Pott’s paraplegia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Costotransversectomy - surgery indicated for paravertebral abscess</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laminectomy - indicated when the posterior parts of vertebrae are involved</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3440","text":"Anterolateral decompressio​n surgery is being perfor​med on a patient with Pot​t's paraplegia. Which of ​the following structures ​will not be removed durin‍g this procedure?","raw_text":"Anterolateral decompression surgery is being performed on a patient with Pott's paraplegia. Which of the following structures will not be removed during this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transvers​e process","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Part of the vertebral body​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lam​ina","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior pa​rt of the rib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamina</strong></p><p>The lamina process of the vertebra is not removed in anterolateral decompression surgery.</p>\n<p><strong>Anterolateral</strong> <strong>decompression</strong> surgery is the <strong>preferred</strong> surgery for <strong>Pott’s paraplegia.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>structures</strong> <strong>removed</strong> in this procedure are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posterior part of the rib</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transverse process</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pedicle</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Part of the vertebral body anterior to the spinal cord</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3439","text":"A patient presented with p​ain and stiffness of the ​right hip. On examination​, apparent shortening of ​the right lower limb was ​noted. A synovial aspirat‍e was taken which showed ‍the presence of numerous ​acid-fast bacilli. Which ‌stage of the disease has ‌the patient presented in?‌","raw_text":"A patient presented with pain and stiffness of the right hip. On examination, apparent shortening of the right lower limb was noted. A synovial aspirate was taken which showed the presence of numerous acid-fast bacilli. Which stage of the disease has the patient presented in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage of ​synovitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage of ear​ly arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stage of advanced arthriti​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage of arthritis with su​bluxation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage of early arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage of early arthritis</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>tuberculosis</strong> of the <strong>hip. </strong>An <strong>apparent shortening</strong> of the limb is seen in the <strong>stage of early arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> seen in the <strong>four</strong> <strong>stages</strong> of <strong>TB hip</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stage of <strong>synovitis</strong> presents with <strong>apparent lengthening </strong>of the limb due to abduction deformity. </li>\n<li>Stage of <strong>early arthritis</strong> presents with <strong>apparent shortening </strong>of the limb due to adduction deformity.</li>\n<li>Stage of <strong>advanced arthritis </strong>leads to<strong> true shortening </strong>of the limb because of the destruction of the articular cartilage of the femur and the acetabulum.</li>\n<li>Stage of <strong>arthritis with subluxation</strong> leads to <strong>true shortening</strong> of the limb<strong>.</strong> The destruction of acetabulum leads to dislocation of the femur head. This is also known as <strong>wandering acetabulum</strong>/ <strong>mortar and pestle hip</strong> because of the widened acetabulum and diminished size of femur head.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The TB of the hip finally results in <strong>fibrous ankylosis</strong> of the hip joint.</p>\n<p>Note: The apparent length of the lower limb is measured from the umbilicus to the medial malleolus and the true length of the limb is measured from the anterior superior iliac spine to the medial malleolus. The <strong>apparent length</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> when there is <strong>abduction deformity</strong> of the hip and<strong> decreased</strong> when there is <strong>adduction deformity</strong> of the hip.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3443","text":"Which of the following is ​a true statement about gi​rdlestone arthroplasty?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement about girdlestone arthroplasty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is done in early arthri​tis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It uses vastus medialis mu​scle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It provides hip joint stab​ility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It preserves hip joint mob​ility","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It preserves hip joint mobility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It preserves hip joint mobility</strong></p><p><strong>Girdlestone arthroplasty</strong> <strong>preserves</strong> the <strong>mobility </strong>of the hip joint  It is done for the <strong>management</strong> of <strong>TB </strong>of<strong> hip</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Early stages</strong> of TB hip can be <strong>conservatively managed</strong> by antitubercular therapy, traction, and immobilization. Later stages need surgical management.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgeries for TB hip</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synovectomy</strong> and joint debridement is the preferred surgery in <strong>early arthritis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Girdlestone arthroplasty </strong>refers to the modification of the articular surface of the joint. It involves excision of the affected portion of the joint and <strong>reconstruction</strong> of the joint <strong>with vastus lateralis</strong> muscle. It gives a <strong>painless, mobile joint</strong> but it results in an <strong>unstable hip</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Arthrodesis </strong>refers to the<strong> </strong>fusion of the joint. It is indicated in painful fibrous ankylosis. It gives a <strong>painless stable hip joint</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3442","text":"A patient presented with d​ifficulty walking and a k​nee deformity. On examina​tion, his knee was found ​to be externally rotated,​ flexed, and posteriorly ‍subluxated. He was suspec‍ted to have TB of the kne​e. Which of the following‌ muscles plays a role in ‌causing the deformity see‌n here?","raw_text":"A patient presented with difficulty walking and a knee deformity. On examination, his knee was found to be externally rotated, flexed, and posteriorly subluxated. He was suspected to have TB of the knee. Which of the following muscles plays a role in causing the deformity seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sart​orius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grac​ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biceps ​femoris","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Popl​iteus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biceps femoris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biceps femoris</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of the <strong>triple deformity</strong> characteristically seen in <strong>TB knee.</strong>  It occurs due to the <strong>spasm of</strong> <strong>biceps femoris.</strong></p>\n<p>Tuberculosis of the knee presents with triple deformity, which includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Posterior </strong>(and lateral)<strong> subluxation </strong>- due to ligament laxity/ degeneration</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>External</strong>/lateral<strong> rotation </strong>- due to spasm of biceps femoris</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Flexion </strong>- due to spasm of the hamstrings (semimembranosus, semitendinosus, biceps femoris)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sartorius, gracilis, and popliteus are responsible for medial rotation at the knee joint and do not contribute to triple deformity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3495","text":"In which of the given cond​itions is spina ventosa s​een?","raw_text":"In which of the given conditions is spina ventosa seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal tu​berculosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caries​ sicca","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tubercular​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberculous​ dactylitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tuberculous dactylitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/224f97a5aebc4d82885e080005cb776ax590x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tuberculous dactylitis</strong></p><p><strong>Spina ventosa</strong> is a feature of <strong>tuberculous dactylitis</strong>. This refers to the <strong>tuberculosis of short tubular bones</strong> i.e. metacarpals, metatarsals, and phalanges.</p>\n<p>The infection is lodged in the marrow cavity leading to spindle-shaped expansion/ <strong>ballooned-out appearance</strong> of the bone known as spina ventosa. Periosteal reaction, abscess, and sinus formation are seen.</p>\n<p><strong>TB sicca </strong>or<strong> caries sicca</strong> is the tuberculosis of the <strong>shoulder joint</strong>. It usually does not have any effusion of joint, hence it is known as sicca which is a Latin word for 'dry'.</p>\n<p>The image below shows tuberculous dactylitis. Note the swelling in the affected phalanx.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"C2XnYejI","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"17_pediatric_bacterial_and_parasitic_infections","title":"17.Pediatric Bacterial And Parasitic Infections","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB5800","text":"Which of the following is ​not commonly caused by St‌aphylococcus aureus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullous ​impetigo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follic​ulitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute ph​aryngitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyomy​ositis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute pharyngitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a874609dec9c459aa862fc181cea9488.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute pharyngitis</strong></p><p><strong>Acute pharyngitis</strong> or sore throat is most commonly caused by <strong>g</strong><strong>roup A <em>Streptococcus</em> (GAS)</strong> - not staphylococcus.</p>\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> causes <strong>localized infections</strong> like furuncle, carbuncle, bullous impetigo, pyomyositis, and folliculitis (skin and soft tissue infections). It may cause bloodstream infections if there is a breach in the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2521","text":"Anu, a pre-school student ​presented with a fever an‌d sore throat. She also h‌ad an acute onset general​ized erythematous rash wi​th pastia lines over the ​antecubital fossa. What i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"Anu, a pre-school student presented with a fever and sore throat. She also had an acute onset generalized erythematous rash with pastia lines over the antecubital fossa. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritter's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scarle​t fever","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kawasaki​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urticaria ​pigmentosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scarlet fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe5a424c93844d5fb62123b3b7ca0541x416x872.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scarlet fever</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>scarlet fever.</strong></p>\n<p>Scarlet fever is a delayed type of skin reaction to streptococcal exotoxin and occurs as a <strong>complication of streptococcal tonsillopharyngitis</strong>. There is a diffuse erythematous maculopapular rash<strong>, </strong>which blanches on pressure. This gives a sandpaper quality to the skin.</p>\n<p>A characteristic physical finding in which red or pink petechial lines appear in skin creases is called Pastia's sign, Pastia lines, or Thompson's sign.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Pastia lines in the antecubital fossa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5801","text":"A young boy is brought to ​the OPD with a sore throa‌t and a rash, which appea‌rs as shown in the image,​ for the past 2 days. The​ rash started around the ​neck and then spread to t​he trunk and extremities.​ On examination, rash bla​nches on pressure. Tongue‌ examination shows swolle​n papillae. Which of the ​following toxins is respo​nsible for this child's c​ondition?","raw_text":"A young boy is brought to the OPD with a sore throat and a rash, which appears as shown in the image, for the past 2 days. The rash started around the neck and then spread to the trunk and extremities. On examination, rash blanches on pressure. Tongue examination shows swollen papillae. Which of the following toxins is responsible for this child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrogenic​ exotoxin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exfoli​otoxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enter​otoxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panton-Valentine leukocidi​n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyrogenic exotoxin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/483340bf95cb4cb8bfaadeb081085a3b.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/674c304abfdc49bcbb8ab5befb9ab76bx400x490.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyrogenic exotoxin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>scarlet fever,</strong> which is caused by a <strong>pyrogenic exotoxin</strong> produced by Group A <em>streptococcus</em> (GAS).</p>\n<p>In scarlet fever, the rash appears within 24–48 hours of the onset of upper respiratory infections. It begins around the head and neck and spreads over the trunk and extremities. It usually spares palms, and soles. It is a diffuse, <strong>papular</strong>, and erythematous eruption, which <strong>blanches on pressure. </strong>The cheeks are erythematous, with pallor around the mouth. The tongue is usually coated, and the <strong>papillae are swollen,</strong> giving the tongue a <strong>strawberry appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a strawberry tongue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5802","text":"A 7-year-old child abruptl​y developed symptoms sugg‌estive of obsessive-compu‌lsive disorder and Touret​te syndrome. PANDAS, a pe​diatric neuropsychiatric ​disorder, is suspected. W​hich of the following org​anisms is associated with​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child abruptly developed symptoms suggestive of obsessive-compulsive disorder and Tourette syndrome. PANDAS, a pediatric neuropsychiatric disorder, is suspected. Which of the following organisms is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Streptococcus pyogenes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></p><p>PANDAS is associated with <strong><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> </strong>infection.</p>\n<p><strong>PANDAS </strong>stands for<strong> </strong>Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal infections.</p>\n<p>The symptoms usually occur <strong>abruptly</strong> and can include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)</strong></li>\n<li>Tic disorder</li>\n<li>Gilles de la Tourette syndrome (coprolalia and tics)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Additionally, children may become moody or irritable, experience anxiety attacks, and/or show separation anxiety. These symptoms are the result of an <strong>autoimmune process</strong> that leads to inflammation of the basal ganglia and the resultant disruption of <strong>cortical-striatal-</strong><strong>thalamic axis</strong> functioning.</p>\n<p>Features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>P</strong>repubertal onset</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>brupt and episodic course</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>eurological symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>isorders associated: Tics, OCD</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ssociated with a recent history of sore throat</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Non-infectious manifestations of streptococcal infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatic heart disease</li>\n<li>Glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>PANDAS</li>\n<li>Guttate psoriasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5806","text":"Which of the following is ​a major criterion in the ‌revised Jones criteria us‌ed for the diagnosis of a​cute rheumatic fever in h​igh-risk populations?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a major criterion in the revised Jones criteria used for the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever in high-risk populations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyart​hralgia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fever (≥38​°C/100.4°F)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ESR ≥​30 mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CRP ≥3.​0 mg/dl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polyarthralgia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polyarthralgia</strong></p><p><strong>Polyarthralgia</strong> is a <strong>major criterion </strong>in the <strong>revised Jones criteria</strong> used for the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever in <strong>high-risk populations </strong>(ARF incidence &gt;2 per 100 000 school-aged children or all-age rheumatic heart disease prevalence of &gt;1 per 1000 population per year).</p>\n<p>Strict adherence to the Jones criteria in areas of higher prevalence may result in under-diagnosis. Therefore, the criteria are slightly modified for <strong>moderate to high-risk populations</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">The major criterion of arthritis includes <strong>polyarthralgia</strong> or monoarthritis in addition to polyarthritis. Polyarthralgia should only be considered as a major manifestation after the exclusion of other causes.</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">The minor criterion for joint involvement is monoarthralgia.</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">A lower cutoff for fever is used (≥38°C [100.4°F] rather than ≥38.5°C [101.3°F]).</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">A lower cutoff for erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) is used (≥30 <span class=\"nowrap\">mm/hour</span> rather than ≥60 <span class=\"nowrap\">mm/hour),</span> although the C-reactive protein (CRP) cutoff is unchanged (≥3 <span class=\"nowrap\">mg/dL)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5808","text":"A 10-year-old girl present​s with a 2-day history of‌ fever and joint pain. Fu‌rther questioning reveals​ that she had a sore thro​at 3 weeks back for which​ she did not seek medical​ help. Acute rheumatic fe​ver is suspected. Which o​f the following is not a ‌component of the criteria​ used for the diagnosis o​f this child's condition?​","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl presents with a 2-day history of fever and joint pain. Further questioning reveals that she had a sore throat 3 weeks back for which she did not seek medical help. Acute rheumatic fever is suspected. Which of the following is not a component of the criteria used for the diagnosis of this child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged ​PR interval","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema​ nodosum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subcutaneo​us nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CRP ≥3.​0 mg/dL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythema nodosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e963c8df9d44f03b5104df39c968973x1280x960.0.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythema nodosum</strong></p><p>Erythema nodosum is not a component of <strong>Jones criteria </strong>which is used for acute rheumatic fever (ARF) diagnosis. <strong>E</strong><strong>rythema marginatum</strong> is a major manifestation of the Jones criteria. It is an <span data-dobid=\"hdw\">evanescent</span>, non-pruritic rash over the trunk and extremities seen in <strong>rheumatic fever.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows erythema marginatum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7389","text":"Which of the following con​stitutes Hutchinson's tri‌ad?","raw_text":"Which of the following constitutes Hutchinson's triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interstitial keratitis, 8t​h nerve deafness, mulberr‌y molars","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitial keratitis, 8t​h nerve deafness, Hutchin‌son molars","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial keratitis, 8t​h nerve deafness, mulberr‌y incisors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitial keratitis, 8t​h nerve deafness, Hutchin‌son incisors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interstitial keratitis, 8th nerve deafness, Hutchinson incisors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f058712804f41c489c8b56d387bb69dx640x488.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77e0869cb2b14e8b8193c697477a48c3x800x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interstitial keratitis, 8th nerve deafness, Hutchinson incisors</strong></p><p><strong>Hutchinson's triad </strong>refers to <strong>interstitial keratitis</strong>, <strong>8<sup>th</sup> nerve deafness</strong>, and <strong>Hutchinson incisors. </strong>It is seen in <strong>late congenital syphilis.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows Hutchinson incisors: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Congenital syphilis</strong> is due to <em>Treponema pallidum</em>. Early features include <strong>hepato-splenomegaly</strong>, jaundice, <strong>diffuse lymphadenopathy,</strong> maculopapular or vesiculobullous<strong> rash involving hands and feet,</strong> persistent rhinitis (<strong>snuffles</strong>), and <strong>condylomatous</strong> lesions. Skeletal changes like<strong> saber shins</strong>, dental abnormalities like Hutchinson's teeth, and <strong>saddle nose</strong> (depression of the nasal root) are some <strong>late</strong> findings.</p>\n<p>Other clinical features of congenital syphilis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clutton joint -</strong> Due to synovitis, with sterile synovial fluid</li>\n<li>Juvenile tabes and paresis</li>\n<li><strong>Rhagades -</strong> Linear scars radiating from mucocutaneous fissures of the mouth, anus, and genitalia</li>\n<li><strong>Mulberry molars </strong>-<strong> </strong>Abnormal 1<sup>st</sup> lower molars with excessive cusps</li>\n<li><strong>Sabre shin -</strong> Anterior bowing of the mid-portion of the tibia</li>\n<li><strong>Olympian brow -</strong> Bony prominence of the forehead</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5811","text":"A 1-year-old baby is broug​ht to the paediatrician b‌y his mother because of j‌aundice. On examination, ​the baby has hepatospleno​megaly and lymphadenopath​y. He has a saddle nose a​nd persistent rhinitis as​ seen in the picture. The​ baby had been 2300g at b‌irth. The delivery was co​mplicated by preterm labo​ur and a single, painless​ ulcer with a clean base ​on the mother's labia. Wh‍ich of the following feat​ure will not be seen in t​his baby?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old baby is brought to the paediatrician by his mother because of jaundice.  On examination, the baby has hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy. He has a saddle nose and persistent rhinitis as seen in the picture. The baby had been 2300g at birth. The delivery was complicated by preterm labour and a single, painless ulcer with a clean base on the mother's labia. Which of the following feature will not be seen in this baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coombs positive hemolytic ​anemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Condylom​ata lata","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Painless ​synovitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitia​l keratitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coombs positive hemolytic anemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/386033ef80a9446eb18daaf305184100.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coombs positive hemolytic anemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>congenital syphilis</strong>. <strong>Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia</strong> is a characteristic feature of congenital syphilis, not coombs-positive hemolytic anemia.</p>\n<p>Congenital syphilis results from transplacental transmission of <em>Treponema pallidum</em> or during birth by contact with infectious lesions of the mother.<br/> <br/><strong>Clinical features of congenital syphilis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hutchinson's triad -</strong> Hutchinson teeth, interstitial keratitis, sensorineural deafness</li>\n<li><strong>Clutton joint -</strong> Due to synovitis, with sterile synovial fluid</li>\n<li>Juvenile tabes and paresis</li>\n<li><strong>Rhagades -</strong> Linear scars radiating from mucocutaneous fissures of the mouth, anus, and genitalia</li>\n<li><strong>Saddle nose -</strong> Depression of nasal bridge</li>\n<li><strong>Mulberry molars </strong>-<strong> </strong>Abnormal 1<sup>st</sup> lower molars with excessive cusps</li>\n<li><strong>Sabre shin -</strong> Anterior bowing of the mid-portion of the tibia</li>\n<li><strong>Olympian brow -</strong> Bony prominence of the forehead</li>\n<li><strong>Snuffles -</strong> Persistent rhinitis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5812","text":"A neonate was delivered vi​a normal vaginal delivery‌. His mother was diagnose‌d with active tuberculosi​s infection during pregna​ncy but she refused treat​ment in fear of side effe​cts to the baby. If the b​aby had been infected pre​natally, which of the fol‌lowing organs is most lik​ely to be involved?","raw_text":"A neonate was delivered via normal vaginal delivery. His mother was diagnosed with active tuberculosis infection during pregnancy but she refused treatment in fear of side effects to the baby. If the baby had been infected prenatally, which of the following organs is most likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu​ng","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li​ver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abd​omen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver</strong></p><p>The <strong>liver</strong> is the most commonly involved organ in <strong>congenital tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Primary infection in a female just before or during pregnancy is more likely to cause congenital tuberculosis. Congenital transmission usually occurs from a lesion in the placenta through the umbilical vein. The tuberculous bacilli may pass through the umbilical vein and develop a <strong>hepatic focus</strong>. Tuberculosis in neonates generally presents with respiratory distress<strong>, hepatosplenomegaly, </strong>and <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>The most common route of infection for a <strong>neonate</strong> is the <strong>postnatal airborne transmission</strong> from an adult with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5813","text":"A 12-year-old boy presente​d with a persistent cough‌. There was a contact his‌tory with an active tuber​culosis case in his neigh​borhood. His X-ray reveal​ed prominent hilar lympha​denopathy as shown in the​ image. Which of the foll​owing statements is false‌ regarding this patient's​ condition?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presented with a persistent cough. There was a contact history with an active tuberculosis case in his neighborhood. His X-ray revealed prominent hilar lymphadenopathy as shown in the image. Which of the following statements is false regarding this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Puhl's lesion at the apex ​of lung is seen in primar‌y infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Most cases of pediatric TB​ are asymptomatic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sputum smear-negative TB d​oes not rule out pediatri‌c pulmonary TB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common site of ex​trapulmonary TB is the ly‌mph node","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Puhl's lesion at the apex of lung is seen in primary infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c2047869d24439b944df7fa123875f2.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Puhl&#x27;s lesion at the apex of lung is seen in primary infection</strong></p><p>The patient had exposure to an active tuberculosis case and has hilar lymphadenopathy (image), suggestive of <strong>primary tuberculosis.</strong> <strong>Puhl’s lesion</strong> is <strong>chronic tuberculosis</strong> (TB) of the apex of the lung, not seen in the primary infection.</p>\n<p>The majority of children develop no signs or symptoms at any level.</p>\n<p>In <strong>primary</strong> pulmonary disease, the combination of the parenchymal pulmonary lesion and the corresponding lymph node is called <strong>Ghon’s complex </strong>or the primary complex.<strong> </strong>Asymptomatic pleural effusion is common and forms part of the primary complex. Erosion of a parenchymal focus of tuberculosis into the blood or lymphatic vessel can result in dissemination of the bacilli and a <strong>miliary pattern,</strong> with small nodules evenly distributed on the chest radiograph. Sputum smear-negative TB does not rule out pediatric pulmonary TB.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic</strong> pulmonary tuberculosis is common in children above 7 years of age. The commonest site is the apex of the lung—<strong>Puhl’s lesion</strong>. Regional lymph nodes are not involved.</p>\n<p>The most common site of extrapulmonary tuberculosis in children is the <strong>lymph node.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>NOTE:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ranke complex:</strong> Ghon's complex undergoes fibrosis and calcification.</li>\n<li><strong>Simon focus:</strong> Hematogenous spread to the apex of the upper lobe, resulting in a post-primary infective lesion, which heals with scarring.</li>\n<li><strong>Simmond's focus:</strong> Tuberculous focus in the liver.</li>\n<li><strong>Weigert focus:</strong> Caseating metastatic tuberculous focus in the wall of a pulmonary vein.</li>\n<li><strong>Rich focus:</strong> Tuberculous granuloma occurring in the cortex of the brain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5814","text":"A woman with smear-positiv​e tuberculosis is on anti‌-tubercular therapy (ATT)‌. She delivered a healthy​ male baby at term. How i​s the newborn managed in ​this case?","raw_text":"A woman with smear-positive tuberculosis is on anti-tubercular therapy (ATT). She delivered a healthy male baby at term. How is the newborn managed in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BCG + Rifampicin + INH + B​reastfeeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breastfeeding + INH for 2 ​months then BCG vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breastfeeding + INH for 6 ​months then BCG vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"BCG + INH for 6 months + w​ithhold Breastfeeding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Breastfeeding + INH for 6 months then BCG vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Breastfeeding + INH for 6 months then BCG vaccine</strong></p><p>In a neonate born to a mother with smear-positive tuberculosis, <strong>breastfeeding</strong> along with <strong>INH prophylaxis for 3-6 months</strong> is recommended. <strong>BCG vaccine</strong> is recommended at birth or after completion of prophylaxis (3-6 months) in all neonates.</p>\n<p>When a child is born to a mother with active TB, the main aim is the <strong>TB-free survival of the newborn</strong>. It involves detecting active TB lesions (congenital TB) and treating them or prevention of transmission of tubercular infection to the neonate from the mother.</p>\n<p><strong>Prophylaxis</strong> with <strong>isoniazid (3-6 months)</strong> is recommended in neonates born to mothers with TB who are infectious (smear-positive). <strong>Breastfeeding</strong> should be continued in these neonates and isolation is recommended only till the mother is infectious, has multidrug-resistant tuberculosis, or nonadherent to treatment. <strong>BCG vaccine</strong> is recommended at birth or after completion of prophylaxis (3-6 months) in all neonates. However,<strong> if </strong>the <strong>Mantoux test </strong>is <strong>positive</strong> and evidence of disease is found, <strong>ATT</strong> is started.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6032","text":"A 25-year-old lady is dete​cted to have active tuber‌culosis at 37 weeks of ge‌station. She delivers a b​aby girl 2 days later. As​ per NTEP guidelines, wha​t advice is given immedia​tely after delivery?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old lady is detected to have active tuberculosis at 37 weeks of gestation. She delivers a baby girl 2 days later. As per NTEP guidelines, what advice is given immediately after delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"INH prophylaxis to be give​n to the neonate for 6 we‌eks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mother is to be started on​ ATT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Withhold br​east feeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Withold BCG​ vaccination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mother is to be started on ATT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mother is to be started on ATT</strong></p><p>The mother with TB is advised to <strong>start ATT</strong> immediately irrespective of time of delivery.</p>\n<p>The management guidelines followed in a case of active TB in a mother immediately following delivery include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mother has to be started on ATT.</li>\n<li>Do not separate the baby from the mother.</li>\n<li>Do not withhold breastfeeding.</li>\n<li>Screening of household contacts.</li>\n<li>Screen the neonate for congenital/active TB.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In accordance with the <strong>NTEP</strong> <strong>2020</strong> guidelines, a newborn whose mother has active TB and is on ATT should be managed with <strong>prophylactic isoniazid</strong> (INH) for <strong>6 months, BCG</strong> vaccination <strong>at birth</strong>, with the <strong>continuation of breastfeeding, </strong>after ruling out active TB. </p>\n<p>INH prophylaxis is given irrespective of the BCG or nutritional status. 10 mg/kg is administered daily for a minimum of six months. During monthly tablet collection, the <strong>child</strong> should be closely monitored for TB symptoms to <strong>rule out active TB</strong> and also <strong>assess</strong> for any <strong>INH-related adverse effects</strong>.</p>\n<p>BCG vaccination can be given at birth even if INH preventive therapy is planned.</p>\n<p>In case the <strong>newborn</strong> is found to have <strong>active TB</strong>, <strong>ATT</strong> is to be initiated along with the administration of <strong>BCG vaccine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5816","text":"A worried couple brought t​heir 4-day-old baby to th‌e pediatrician due to dif‌ficulty in feeding and ab​normal posture. The baby ​was delivered at home and​ the mother never had a p​renatal checkup. On exami​nation, the baby's body w​as rigid, with flexed upp‌er limb and extended back​. The umbilical cord was ​covered with cow dung. Th​e mother says it's their ​tradition, and that the d‍irt helps with the fallin​g off of the cord. Which ​of the following is false‌ regarding this condition‌?","raw_text":"A worried couple brought their 4-day-old baby to the pediatrician due to difficulty in feeding and abnormal posture. The baby was delivered at home and the mother never had a prenatal checkup. On examination, the baby's body was rigid, with flexed upper limb and extended back. The umbilical cord was covered with cow dung. The mother says it's their tradition, and that the dirt helps with the falling off of the cord. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The toxin responsible affe​cts the excitatory neuron‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metronidazole is given as ​a treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It occurs in infants of un​immunized mothers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Case fatality rate is 70-1​00%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The toxin responsible affects the excitatory neurons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The toxin responsible affects the excitatory neurons</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>neonatal tetanus.</strong> It is caused by a neurotoxin produced by <strong>Clostridium tetani</strong>, which <strong>inhibits inhibitory neurons</strong>, not excitatory neurons.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Clostridium tetani is a spore-forming obligate anaerobe commonly found in soil, dust, and gastrointestinal tracts of various animals. The baby's umbilical stump covered with cow dung serves as the source of infection. It also occurs in <strong>infants of unimmunized mothers. </strong>Tetanus typically manifests within 3-12 days of birth as progressive <strong>difficulty in feeding, rigidity,</strong> or<strong> spasms</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tetanus toxin binds at the neuromuscular junction and enters the motor nerve; it is then transported to the alpha-motoneuron via <strong>retrograde axonal transport</strong>. Subsequently, the toxin enters the adjacent <strong>spinal inhibitory interneurons</strong>, where it prevents the release of the neurotransmitters <strong>glycine and <span class=\"s1\">γ</span>-aminobutyric acid</strong>. This leads to <strong>blockade of normal antagonistic muscle inhibition, </strong>while agonist muscles undergo sustained contraction without relaxing<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Neonatal tetanus presents with progressive <strong>feeding difficulty </strong>(sucking and swallowing), irritability, and crying. Characteristic features include reduced movement or complete muscle paralysis, rigidity, and <strong>spasms. </strong>These features may or may not be associated with <strong>opisthotonos</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>includes supportive care in the form of muscle relaxants (<strong>diazepam</strong>), along with the administration of <strong>human tetanus immunoglobulin (TIG)</strong> and <strong>metronidazole,</strong> which is the antibiotic of choice. Parenteral penicillin G can serve as an alternate antibiotic.</p>\n<p>Excising the umbilical stump in a baby with neonatal tetanus is no longer recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5818","text":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated ​child presents with compl‌aints of sore throat and ‌difficulty in swallowing.​ The patient also has fev​er and hoarseness of voic​e and her facial appearan​ce is as shown below. A p​rovisional diagnosis of D​iptheria is made. Which o‌f the following statement​s is true regarding this ​patient's condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated child presents with complaints of sore throat and difficulty in swallowing. The patient also has fever and hoarseness of voice and her facial appearance is as shown below. A provisional diagnosis of Diptheria is made. Which of the following statements is true regarding this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be diagnosed by the​ demonstration of antibod‌y by ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunization prevents the ​carrier state","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment of contacts is n​ot indicated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iron has a critical value ​in the production of toxi‌ns","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iron has a critical value in the production of toxins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78763fa07969428a8a5d02910bfb8cd8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3ea1b23645b442d95988da391e77efdx720x633.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iron has a critical value in the production of toxins</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>bull-neck</strong> appearance due to the soft tissue edema, seen in <strong>diphtheria</strong>. <strong>Iron</strong> is <strong>essential</strong> for the production of <strong>toxins</strong> in diphtheria.</p>\n<p>It is caused by <em><strong>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</strong>. </em>Carriers are the most common causes of infection. Immunization does not prevent the carrier state. It spreads via droplets through the respiratory tract (most important), cutaneous lesions, and fomites.</p>\n<p>The regulation of toxin production in the bacterium is partially controlled by <strong>iron</strong>, thus making it an important prerequisite for toxin production.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory tract diphtheria (most common):\n<ul>\n<li>Thick, adherent <strong>pseudomembrane</strong> (grey or white) on one/both tonsils and adjacent pharynx. Dislodging of the membrane may cause bleeding.</li>\n<li>Underlying soft tissue edema and enlarged lymph nodes can cause a <strong>bull-neck appearance</strong></li>\n<li>Patients with laryngeal diphtheria are at risk for suffocation because of local soft-tissue edema and airway obstruction by the diphtheritic membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous diphtheria </strong>is an indolent, non-progressive infection characterized by a superficial, ecthyma-like, non-healing ulcer with a gray-brown membrane.</li>\n<li>Occasionally, it causes mucocutaneous infections at other sites, such as otitis externa, purulent conjunctivitis, and vulvo-vaginitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis: The culture of the organism from the nose and throat swab specimens is the gold standard. Demonstration of the toxin is done by <strong>Elek's gel precipitation test</strong>. Gram stain and fluorescent antibody tests are unreliable.</p>\n<p>Complications<strong> </strong>include obstruction due to pseudomembrane, myocarditis, and polyneuropathy.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5819","text":"A diphtheria screening cam​p is being held at a rura‌l school to find carriers‌ and to provide them adeq​uate treatment. What is t​he investigation of choic​e in this scenario?","raw_text":"A diphtheria screening camp is being held at a rural school to find carriers and to provide them adequate treatment. What is the investigation of choice in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Throat sw​ab culture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gram’s​ stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Albert’​s stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluorescen​t antibody","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Throat swab culture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Throat swab culture</strong></p><p>The investigation of choice for diphtheria <strong>carriers</strong> is <strong>throat swab culture.</strong></p>\n<p>Specimens for culture should be obtained from the <strong>nose</strong> and <strong>throat</strong> and any other mucocutaneous lesion. A portion of the membrane should be removed and sent for culture along with the underlying exudate in those with overt disease. Direct smear using Gram stain or specific fluorescent antibody is unreliable.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0902","text":"Anuj has been​ diagnosed w​ith diphtheria and is bei‌ng treated in the ward. H‌is 3-year-old brother, Aj​ay who lives in the same ​house has received vaccin​ation against diphtheria ​about 16 months back. Wha​t is the next best step f​or the management of the ‌younger sibling, Ajay?","raw_text":"Anuj has been​ diagnosed with diphtheria and is being treated in the ward. His 3-year-old brother, Ajay who lives in the same house has received vaccination against diphtheria about 16 months back. What is the next best step for the management of the younger sibling, Ajay?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One boos​ter dose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nothing as the child is al​ready exposed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin + diphtheria ​toxoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythrom​ycin only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Erythromycin only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Erythromycin only</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, a <strong>3-year-old child</strong> is likely to have received the <strong>4th dose (1st booster)</strong> of the diphtheria vaccine between 16–24 months of age according to National Immunization Schedule (i.e., 16 months ago). Since he has received a <strong>booster dose within 5 years</strong>, only <strong>erythromycin</strong> is indicated in case of <strong>close contact</strong> with the<strong> diphtheria patient</strong>. </p>\n<p>All <strong>household contacts</strong> with diphtheria patients should be closely <strong>monitored</strong> for <strong>7 days</strong>. <strong>Culture</strong> should be taken from both the throat and nose. <strong>All</strong> <strong>contacts</strong> should receive <strong>antibiotic prophylaxis</strong> (<strong>benzathine penicillin G</strong> or <strong>erythromycin</strong>), <strong>regardless of immunization status</strong>. </p>\n<p>Children who have <strong>not received</strong> a <strong>booster dose</strong> of the diphtheria toxoid vaccine <strong>within 5 years</strong> should be given the vaccine in an age-appropriate form. Children who have <strong>not yet received</strong> their <strong>4th dose</strong> should be vaccinated accordingly. </p>\n<p>If <strong>fewer than three doses</strong> of diphtheria toxoid have been given, or vaccination <strong>history is unknown</strong>, an <strong>immediate dose </strong>of<strong> diphtheria</strong> <strong>toxoid</strong> should be given and the primary series completed according to the current schedule. </p>\n<p><strong>Asymptomatic carriers</strong> of diphtheria are treated with <strong>antimicrobial</strong> <strong>prophylaxis</strong> for a period of <strong>10-14 days</strong>. If an individual has<strong> not received</strong> a <strong>booster dose</strong> of diphtheria toxoid <strong>within 1 year</strong>, an age-appropriate preparation of the <strong>vaccine</strong> should be <strong>administered</strong> promptly. </p>\n<p><strong>Droplet precautions</strong> (respiratory colonization) or <strong>contact precautions</strong> (cutaneous colonization) of cases and carriers are maintained <strong>until two</strong> <strong>consecutive negative cultures</strong> (collected 24 hours apart) are obtained after cessation of therapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5821","text":"A young boy is brought to ​the clinic with complaint‌s of abdominal pain, diar‌rhea, and painful straini​ng for 4 months. The symp​toms were accompanied by ​rectal prolapse. Stool ex​amination showed the foll​owing finding. Infection ​with which of the followi‌ng helminths is most like​ly?","raw_text":"A young boy is brought to the clinic with complaints of abdominal pain, diarrhea, and painful straining for 4 months. The symptoms were accompanied by rectal prolapse. Stool examination showed the following finding. Infection with which of the following helminths is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichinell​a spiralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichuris​ trichiura","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trichuris trichiura","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a1dd279cf34b4108b2698c970411e871.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16ad523e6dd246b5a2ec669cc3e75cc8x200x200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trichuris trichiura</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>barrel-shaped</strong> ovum, which along with rectal prolapse is characteristic of <strong><em>trichuris trichiura.</em></strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Trichuriasis</strong> is caused by <em>T. trichiura</em> (whipworm), which inhabits the cecum and ascending colon. They bury their thin heads into the intestinal lining causing edema, loosening the elastic epithelium, and weakening the surrounding muscles. This leads to loss of supports and rectal prolapse. Other clinical features include chronic dysentery, anemia, poor growth, and developmental defects.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is done by fecal smears showing the barrel-shaped ovum of <em>T. trichiura</em>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the barrel-shaped ovum of <em>Trichuris trichiura.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Albendazole</strong> is the drug of choice (400 mg, orally, for 3 days; for all ages). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5843","text":"An anxious mother brought ​her 4-year-old daughter t‌o the pediatrician. The g‌irl was passing loose, bu​lky stools for the past 4​ days. This was often ass​ociated with pain in the ​abdomen. The pediatrician​ ordered stool examinatio​n, which showed the follo‌wing organisms. Which vit​amin deficiency is the ch​ild at the greatest risk ​for?","raw_text":"An anxious mother brought her 4-year-old daughter to the pediatrician. The girl was passing loose, bulky stools for the past 4 days. This was often associated with pain in the abdomen. The pediatrician ordered stool examination, which showed the following organisms. Which vitamin deficiency is the child at the greatest risk for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin B1​ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin A ​deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin B6​ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin C ​deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin A deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b254d177a26d47a192da5d336c15f58d.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin A deficiency</strong></p><p>The stool examination (image) shows <strong>pear-shaped trophozoites</strong> having two nuclei and a flagellum, typical of <strong><em>Giardia lamblia</em>. </strong>Giardiasis is associated with a deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins like vitamin A.</p>\n<p><em>Giardia lamblia </em>is transmitted via contaminated drinking water.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include acute infectious diarrhea or <strong>chronic diarrhea</strong>, failure to thrive, and abdominal pain. Stools initially may be profuse and watery and later become greasy and <strong>foul-smelling</strong>. Stools do not contain blood, mucus, or leukocytes. It is associated with fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies, iron deficiency, and growth stunting.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is done by <strong>stool enzyme immunoassay (EIA)</strong> or direct fluorescent antibody tests for<em> Giardia</em> antigens; EIA is superior to microscopy.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes tinidazole and nitazoxanide.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5845","text":"In a child, the amoebic li​ver abscess can be diagno‌sed by demonstrating whic‌h of the following?","raw_text":"In a child, the amoebic liver abscess can be diagnosed by demonstrating which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysts in the​ sterile pus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trophozoite​s in the pus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cysts in the hepatic venou​s sample","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trophozoites​ in the feces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trophozoites in the pus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1da6c1d228448018585e4b9b92ee825x359x278.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trophozoites in the pus</strong></p><p>The amoebic liver abscess can be diagnosed by demonstrating Trophozoites in the pus</p>\n<p><strong>Amebiasis </strong>is caused by <em>Entamoeba histolytica. </em>Trophozoites are responsible for tissue invasion and destruction.</p>\n<p><strong>Amoebic Colitis </strong>will present as colicky abdominal pains and frequent bowel movements (6–8/day). Diarrhea is associated with tenesmus, heme-positive stool. Trophozoites invade the intestinal mucosa and produce <strong>flask-shaped ulcers</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Amoebic liver abscess </strong>occurs in &lt;1% infected individuals. Fever is the hallmark of amoebic liver abscess. It is associated with abdominal pain, abdominal distention and enlargement, and tenderness of the liver. Changes at the base of the right lung, such as elevation of the diaphragm and atelectasis or effusion, can also occur. The most common finding is a single abscess in the right hepatic lobe, detected on USG, CT, or MRI. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a CT scan of a right lobe liver abscess.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Invasive amebiasis is treated with <strong>nitroimidazoles</strong> such as metronidazole or tinidazole. This is followed by a <strong>luminal amebicide</strong> such as paromomycin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5849","text":"What is the most common ro​ute of acquiring Ancylost‌oma duodenale infection?","raw_text":"What is the most common route of acquiring Ancylostoma duodenale infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ingestion of infected food​ items","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhalation ​of droplets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Penetration of skin by lar​va","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inoculation into eroded sk​in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Penetration of skin by larva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0cb64cf9e4364a27aa88a76182c9826ax767x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/917df840e7594320a104dbcf2618f824x682x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Penetration of skin by larva</strong></p><p><strong>Skin penetration</strong> is the most common route by which the larva of <em>Ancylostoma duodenale </em>(hookworm) enters the human body.</p>\n<p><strong><em>Ancylostoma duodenale</em> (hookworm) </strong>larvae enter the human host by skin penetration and undergo extraintestinal migration through the venous circulation to the lungs. They enter alveoli and climb up to the bronchial tree and pharynx, and are swallowed. Larvae reach the small intestine and become adult worms.</p>\n<p>Clinical manifestations include <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong>, resulting from <strong>chronic occult intestinal blood loss.</strong> Dermatitis, sometimes referred to as <strong>ground itch,</strong> may occur when they penetrate human skin. Eosinophilic pneumonia can also be seen.</p>\n<p>Infection with a zoonotic hookworm, especially <em><strong>Ancylostoma braziliense</strong></em>, can result in the lateral migration of the larvae causing cutaneous tracts of <strong>cutaneous larva migrans.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows the lifecycle of the intestinal hookworm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows cutaneous larva migrans.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mebendazole 100 mg bid, orally, for 3 days or single dose of 500 mg</li>\n<li>Albendazole 400 mg, orally, once</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5855","text":"A child from Bihar comes w​ith a fever. Blood examin‌ation shows sheathed micr‌ofilaria as shown in the ​image. Identify the organ​ism.","raw_text":"A child from Bihar comes with a fever. Blood examination shows sheathed microfilaria as shown in the image. Identify the organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brugia​ malayi","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wuchereria​ bancrofti","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loa​ loa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oncocerca​ vovulous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brugia malayi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6e67a860a484449aa14ae8cf0fac315.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/afafe8a8d3654e9dbe4c73431d0794eax1280x3830.0705218617773.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brugia malayi</strong></p><p>The incidence of Brugia malayi is more in children from Bihar and the image showing microfilaria with two nuclei at the tail. Hence the organism is <strong>Brugia malayi,</strong> which causes lymphatic <strong>filariasis</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Brugia malayi</em> (Malayan filariasis), <em>Brugia timori</em>, and <em>Wuchereria bancrofti</em> (bancroftian filariasis) are thread-like nematodes that cause a similar infection called Filariasis. Infective larvae are introduced into humans during sucking of blood by the <strong>mosquito vector</strong> (commonly, culex).</p>\n<p>Clinical manifestations include episodic fever, <strong>lymphangitis</strong> of an extremity, lymphadenitis (especially the inguinal and axillary areas), headaches, and myalgias. Hepatosplenomegaly and generalized lymphadenopathy are often seen in children. These symptoms are caused by an acute inflammatory response triggered by the death of adult worms.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is done by demonstration of <strong>microfilariae in the blood. </strong>Blood samples should be obtained between 10 pm and 2 am because microfilaremia is <strong>nocturnal.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diethylcarbamazine</strong> 1 mg/kg PO as a single dose on day 1</li>\n<li>1 mg/kg tid on day 2</li>\n<li>1-2mg/kg tid on day 3</li>\n<li>6 mg/kg/day divided tid on days 4-14</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the difference between microfilaria of various filarial nematodes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5858","text":"A boy is brought to the em​ergency with repeated epi‌sodes of generalized toni‌c-clonic seizures. Histor​y is significant for a di​etary intake of pork. MRI​ reveals multiple ring-en​hancing lesions as shown ​below. Which of the follo​wing organism is the like‌ly cause for seizures in ​this patient?","raw_text":"A boy is brought to the emergency with repeated episodes of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. History is significant for a dietary intake of pork. MRI reveals multiple ring-enhancing lesions as shown below. Which of the following organism is the likely cause for seizures in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Taenia​ solium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Echino​coccus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Taenia ​saginata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Taenia solium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28aa9514617443c4b3b248eadc02c325.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Taenia solium</strong></p><p>The likely cause of <strong>seizures</strong> in a child who has multiple ring-enhancing lesions and dietary history of<strong> pork intake</strong> is an infection with <strong><em>Taenia solium</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Children acquire the adult form of the parasite by ingestion of undercooked pork containing <strong>larvae cysts</strong>. In the intestines, the cyst converts into adult form. The adult form releases eggs in the stool.</p>\n<p>If the eggs are ingested (shed by tapeworm carriers or by unhygienic practices), the larvae are released from the <strong>eggs. </strong>The larva then invades through the intestines and migrate through the bloodstream to the <strong>muscles</strong> (and other organs), where they form tissue cysts called <strong>cysticercosis.</strong> When these larvae find their way to the <strong>brain,</strong> it is termed as <strong>neurocysticercosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurocysticercosis </strong>typically presents with <strong>seizures</strong> (focal or generalized). It can be associated with basilar arachnoiditis that can present with signs of meningeal irritation. Cysticerci in the tissues may present with focal findings from the mass effect. Ocular neurocysticercosis causes decreased visual acuity because of cysticerci in the retina or vitreous, retinal detachment, or iridocyclitis.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes management of seizures. <strong>albendazole</strong> therapy (treatment of choice). Praziquantel can also be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5848","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding congenita‌l toxoplasmosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding congenital toxoplasmosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs through hematoge​nous dissemination to the‌ placenta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorioretinitis is the mos​t common manifestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immunoglobulin G (IgG) is ​diagnostic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It can manifests as hydrop​s fetalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Immunoglobulin G (IgG) is diagnostic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Immunoglobulin G (IgG) is diagnostic</strong></p><p><strong>IgG</strong> is <strong>not</strong> diagnostic for <strong>congenital toxoplasmosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The presence of IgG antibodies in a newborn can be due to an old infection in the mother, as IgG can cross the placenta. IgM cannot cross the placenta, hence the <strong>presence of IgM</strong> in newborns <strong>confirms</strong> the diagnosis of <strong>congenital toxoplasmosis.</strong> IgM immunosorbent agglutination assay (<strong>ISAGA</strong>) and <strong>IgA ELISA</strong> are the most useful diagnostic tests.</p>\n<p><strong>Toxoplasmosis </strong>is caused by <em>Toxoplasma gondi</em>. <em>Toxoplasma</em> can be transmitted to humans by three principal routes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ingestion of raw or inadequately cooked infected meat</li>\n<li>Ingestion of oocysts through exposure to cat litter or soil</li>\n<li>Newly infected pregnant woman passing the infection to her unborn fetus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can be acquired orally, transplacentally or rarely, parenterally or by transfusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital toxoplasmosis: </strong>When a mother acquires the infection during gestation, organisms may <strong>disseminate hematogenously</strong> to the placenta. Infection may be transmitted to the fetus transplacentally or during vaginal delivery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>characteristic triad</strong> of <strong>chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, </strong>and <strong>cerebral calcifications </strong>may be present. Other clinical features include small size for gestational age, prematurity, persistent jaundice, mild thrombocytopenia, hydrops fetalis, and perinatal death.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5928","text":"The most common manifestat​ion of congenital toxopla‌smosis is ______","raw_text":"The most common manifestation of congenital toxoplasmosis is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroc​ephalus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorior​etinitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatospl​enomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thromboc​ytopenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chorioretinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chorioretinitis</strong></p><p><strong>Chorioretinitis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> manifestation of congenital toxoplasmosis.</p>\n<p>The characteristic <strong>triad</strong> of congenital toxoplasmosis is <strong>chorioretinitis</strong>, <strong>hydrocephalus,</strong> and <strong>diffuse</strong> <strong>intracranial calcifications. </strong>Clinical manifestations apart from the triad include hydrops fetalis, <strong>intrauterine growth retardation</strong>, <strong>prematurity</strong>, peripheral retinal scars, persistent jaundice, and mild thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital toxoplasmosis </strong>occurs when a pregnant woman acquires the infection during gestation. The organism may cross to the <strong>fetus transplacentally</strong> or at the time of vaginal delivery. The severity of the disease in the neonate is<strong> greater</strong> if the woman gets infected <strong>early in her pregnancy</strong>. The causative agent is <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, an obligate intracellular protozoan.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is made by the detection of <strong>IgM</strong> anti-toxoplasma antibodies. IgG can cross the placenta is hence, cannot confirm infection in the infant. IgM immunosorbent agglutination assay (<strong>ISAGA</strong>) and <strong>IgA ELISA</strong> are the most useful diagnostic tests.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiramycin </strong>can be administered for cases of <strong>acute infection</strong> in <strong>early pregnancy </strong>to reduce vertical transmission. It does <strong>not cross</strong> the placenta and therefore cannot be used to treat an infected fetus. <strong>Pyrimethamine-sulfonamide</strong> (to be avoided in the first trimester) is used when<strong> a fetal infection</strong> is<strong> confirmed</strong> or in maternal<strong> infections after 18 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0709","text":"Maternal antibodies that d​o not provide significant‌ immunity in a neonate:","raw_text":"Maternal antibodies that do not provide significant immunity in a neonate:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tet​anus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Po​lio","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dipt​heria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mea​sles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Polio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Polio</strong></p><p><strong>Maternal antibodies</strong> <strong>do not</strong> provide significant immunity to <strong>polio infection</strong> in a neonate. </p>\n<p>Administering an <strong>oral polio vaccine</strong> (OPV) dose at <strong>birth</strong> can <strong>provide early mucosal protection</strong>, safeguarding against enteric pathogens before they can hinder the intestinal mucosal immune response. Furthermore, administering OPV during the period when infants are protected by maternal antibodies may help prevent vaccine-associated paralytic polio (VAPP).</p>\n<p>All <strong>pregnant women</strong> should receive a tetanus toxoid, reduced diphtheria toxoid, and acellular pertussis (<strong>Tdap</strong>) vaccine during each pregnancy, as early in the 27–36-week-of-gestation window as possible. The <strong>maternal antibodies</strong> formed from<strong> Tdap</strong> will provide <strong>immunity against tetanus</strong>, <strong>diphtheria,</strong> and <strong>pertussis</strong> in the newborn. Administering the Tdap vaccine earlier, between the<strong> 27th and 36th week</strong> of gestation, <strong>maximizes</strong> the transfer of <strong>passive antibodies</strong> to the infant (options A and C). </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 6779\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Infants less than <strong>6 months</strong> are generally <strong>protected from measles</strong> by <strong>maternal antibodies</strong> (option D).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2599","text":"What is the daily dosage o​f ethambutol for an adole‌scent of age 14 years?","raw_text":"What is the daily dosage of ethambutol for an adolescent of age 14 years?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 m​g/kg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10 m​g/kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40 m​g/kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 m​g/kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 20 mg/kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 20 mg/kg</strong></p><p>The daily dosage of ethambutol is 20 mg/kg body weight.</p>\n<p>First-line antitubercular drugs:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Daily dosage </strong>(mg/kg)</td>\n<td><strong>Adverse effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Isoniazid</strong></td>\n<td>10-15</td>\n<td>Peripheral neuritis, hepatic enzyme elevation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rifampin</strong></td>\n<td>15-20</td>\n<td>Orange discoloration of secretions and<br/>urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ethambutol</strong></td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Optic neuritis,<br/>decreased red-green color discrimination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pyrazinamide</strong></td>\n<td>30-40</td>\n<td>Hepatotoxicity, hyperuricemia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All first-line antitubercular drugs are hepatotoxic except ethambutol.</li>\n<li>All first-line antitubercular drugs are bactericidal except ethambutol, which is bacteriostatic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5810","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of Hutchi‌nson's triad in congenita‌l syphilis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Hutchinson's triad in congenital syphilis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peg-shaped u​pper incisor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitia​l keratitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sensorineur​al deafness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saber​ shin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Saber shin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6526c2bfed95473abd90b4b25c9af41cx720x384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Saber shin</strong></p><p>Saber shin is not a component of Hutchinson’s triad. Saber shin is the anterior bowing of the midportion of the tibia.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Peg-shaped upper central incisor is called as Hutchinson's teeth (erupts during the 6<sup>th</sup> year of life).</p>\n<p><strong>Hutchinson’s triad</strong>:<strong> </strong>It is a triad of stigmata seen characteristically in congenital syphilis. The components are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hutchinson's teeth</li>\n<li>Interstitial keratitis</li>\n<li>Sensorineural deafness</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Hutchinson teeth (peg-shaped upper incisors)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5CFg2dJu","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"17_amyloidosis_and_graft_rejection","title":"17.Amyloidosis And Graft Rejection","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2458","text":"Which among the following ​best describes an amyloid‍?","raw_text":"Which among the following best describes an amyloid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Col​loid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipop​rotein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proteo​glycan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intermedia​te filament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proteoglycan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proteoglycan</strong></p><p><strong>Amyloid</strong> can be best described as a <strong>proteoglycan.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Amyloidosis</strong> refers to the <strong>extracellular deposition</strong> of<strong> fibrillar proteins. </strong></p>\n<p>Abnormal fibrils are produced by the aggregation of misfolded proteins. The fibrillar deposits bind a wide variety of <strong>proteoglycans</strong> and <strong>glycosaminoglycans </strong>(heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate), and plasma proteins (serum amyloid P component).</p>\n<p>The abundant charged sugar groups in these adsorbed proteins give the deposits staining characteristics that resemble starch <strong>(amylose). </strong>Therefore, these deposits are called amyloid.</p>\n<p>Amyloid appears as an <strong>amorphous, eosinophilic, hyaline, extracellular </strong>substance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2500","text":"In a patient with reactive​ systemic amyloidosis, wh‍ich of the following prot‍eins are likely to get de‌posited in the tissues?","raw_text":"In a patient with reactive systemic amyloidosis, which of the following proteins are likely to get deposited in the tissues?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​β","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A​L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AP​rP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. AA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AA</strong></p><p>There is the deposition of <strong>AA proteins </strong>in <strong>reactive systemic amyloidosis</strong> (secondary amyloidosis).</p>\n<p>In <strong>inflammatory </strong>states, there is a release of <strong>IL-1</strong> and<strong> IL-6,</strong> which act on the liver cells. The liver increases the production of <strong>SAA </strong>(serum amyloid-associated)<strong> proteins</strong> which circulate bound to<strong> high-density lipoproteins</strong>. This gives rise to<strong> AA proteins</strong>, which get <strong>deposited in tissues</strong> causing reactive systemic amyloidosis or secondary amyloidosis.</p>\n<p>The most common <strong>sites</strong> are the <strong>kidney</strong> and <strong>heart.</strong></p>\n<p>It is usually seen secondary to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis (most common)</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n<li>Chronic osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Malignancies - renal cell cancer, Hodgkin’s lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2753","text":"What is the most common ca​use of reactive amyloidos‍is worldwide?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of reactive amyloidosis worldwide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crohn's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetes​ mellitus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>reactive amyloidosis</strong> worldwide is <strong>rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Amyloidosis is reported to occur in 3% of patients with rheumatoid arthritis and is clinically significant in one-half of those affected.</p>\n<p>Conditions associated with reactive systemic (<strong>secondary</strong>) <strong>amyloidosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis (most common)</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis)</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis </li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n<li>Chronic osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Malignancies - renal cell cancer, Hodgkin’s lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2501","text":"An elderly woman is brough​t with worsening memory f‍or the past 3 years. Rece‍ntly, she has been unable‌ to carry out her daily c‍hores and frequently gets‍ lost in the neighborhood‌. There is no history of ​personality changes, rigi‍dity, or gait disturbance​s. MRI brain shows tempor‌al lobe atrophy. Which of‌ the following deposits i‌s likely to be seen in th​is patient?","raw_text":"An elderly woman is brought with worsening memory for the past 3 years. Recently, she has been unable to carry out her daily chores and frequently gets lost in the neighborhood. There is no history of personality changes, rigidity, or gait disturbances. MRI brain shows temporal lobe atrophy. Which of the following deposits is likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aβ2m a​myloid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aβ am​yloid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ATTR a​myloid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AIAPP ​amyloid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aβ amyloid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aβ amyloid</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Alzheimer's</strong> disease. <strong>Aβ</strong> <strong>amyloid</strong> deposition is seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>Aβ constitutes the core of <strong>cerebral plaques</strong> found in <strong>Alzheimer's disease. </strong>It also gets deposited in the walls of cerebral blood vessels. The Aβ protein is derived by proteolysis from a much larger transmembrane glycoprotein, called <strong>amyloid precursor protein</strong> (APP).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2507","text":"A diabetic patient with ch​ronic kidney disease has ‍been undergoing hemodialy‍sis for the past 4 years.‌ Deposition of which of t‍he following is likely in‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient with chronic kidney disease has been undergoing hemodialysis for the past 4 years. Deposition of which of the following is likely in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1 micro​globulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β2 micro​globulin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α2 macro​globulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"γ glo​bulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β2 microglobulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β2 microglobulin</strong></p><p>Patients on <strong>long-term hemodialysis</strong> for renal failure can develop <strong>amyloidosis</strong> as a result of the <strong>deposition</strong> of<strong> β2-microglobulin</strong>.</p>\n<p>This protein is present in high concentrations in the serum of persons with renal disease. The protein is retained in the circulation because it cannot be filtered through the dialysis membranes.</p>\n<p>It gets deposited in the <strong>synovium, joints,</strong> and <strong>tendon sheaths.</strong> When this deposition takes place in the carpal ligament of the wrist, it leads to the development of <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>With new dialysis filters, the incidence of this complication has decreased substantially.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2515","text":"Which type of amyloidosis ​is caused by the mutation‍ of the transthyretin pro‍tein?","raw_text":"Which type of amyloidosis is caused by the mutation of the transthyretin protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Familial Mediterranean fev​er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Familial amyloidotic polyn​europathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic senile amyloidosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reactive systemic amyloido​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Familial amyloidotic polyneuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Familial amyloidotic polyneuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Familial amyloidotic polyneuropathy </strong>(FAP) is associated with the deposition of <strong>mutant transthyretin</strong> protein (TTR).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>FAP is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition. The deposition occurs as <strong>amyloid</strong> <strong>fibrils</strong> in peripheral and autonomic nerves. Mutated TTR is prone to misfolding and aggregation, and shows resistance to proteolysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic senile amyloidosis </strong>(amyloid of aging) refers to the systemic deposition of <strong>normal TTR </strong>amyloid in elderly patients. Symptomatic patients present with restrictive cardiomyopathy and arrhythmias. Another rare form affecting predominantly the heart results from the deposition of a mutant form of TTR.</p>\n<p><strong>Familial Mediterranean fever</strong> (FMF) is an <strong>autosomal-recessive </strong>condition seen in individuals of Mediterranean descent (Armenian, Sephardic Jewish, and Arabic). The affected gene encodes a protein called <strong>pyrin, </strong>which is involved in cytokine production. The <strong>amyloid</strong> fibril proteins in this condition are made up of <strong>AA proteins. </strong>It presents with attacks of fever and serositis. </p>\n<p><strong>Isolated atrial amyloidosis </strong>is associated with AANP (atrial natriuretic peptide) amyloid deposition</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2328","text":"Who among the following pa​tients is at increased ri‍sk for developing amyloid‍osis?","raw_text":"Who among the following patients is at increased risk for developing amyloidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with maturity-on​set diabetes of the young‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 15-year-old diabetic boy​ who is on insulin therap‍y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 50-year-old diabetic pat​ient with insulin resista‍nce","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with autosomal d​ominant non-insulin depen‍dent form of diabetes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 50-year-old diabetic patient with insulin resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 50-year-old diabetic patient with insulin resistance</strong></p><p>Older individuals with insulin resistance are likely to have <strong>type 2</strong> <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>. These patients are at <strong>increased risk</strong> of developing <strong>amyloidosis</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Microscopic deposits of localized amyloid may be found in certain conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medullary carcinoma</strong> of the thyroid gland</li>\n<li>Islet tumors of the pancreas</li>\n<li><strong>Pheochromocytomas</strong></li>\n<li>Undifferentiated <strong>carcinomas</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong></li>\n<li>Islets of Langerhans in individuals with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0230","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ts with thyroid swelling.‍ The histological examina‍tion of the swelling is s‌hown below. What type of ‍amyloid deposits are seen‍ in this condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with thyroid swelling. The histological examination of the swelling is shown below. What type of amyloid deposits are seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A ​Cal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aβ​2m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A​L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A​β","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A Cal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78b138cfc2d34f9d8f1c817638a23c2d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6577164b622f423b80a57a1138ceffeex1280x1050.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/daaf0899043c4cd2bf648aae2e7d2fb5x1280x1050.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A Cal</strong></p><p>The slide is showing the presence of <strong>amyloid-rich stroma</strong> (pinkish material) and nests of polygonal cells, suggestive of <strong>medullary carcinoma thyroid. </strong>The major fibril protein that gets deposited is<strong> A Cal,</strong> which is derived from <strong>calcitonin</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Microscopic deposits of <strong>localized</strong> <strong>amyloid</strong> may be found in certain conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medullary carcinoma</strong> of the thyroid gland</li>\n<li>Islet tumors of the pancreas</li>\n<li><strong>Pheochromocytomas</strong></li>\n<li>Undifferentiated <strong>carcinomas</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong></li>\n<li>Islets of Langerhans in individuals with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2521","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common organ inv‍olved in AL amyloidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common organ involved in AL amyloidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac​ tissue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ​tissue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Splenic​ tissue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic​ tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal tissue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d53e868970141db984b159701708b15x1280x1060.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal tissue</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> organ involved in AL amyloidosis is <strong>renal tissue</strong> (Kidneys). They are affected in 70–80% of patients with <strong>amyloidosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heart</strong> is the <strong>second most commonly</strong> affected organ (50–60%), and cardiac involvement is the <strong>leading cause</strong> of <strong>death</strong> from <strong>AL amyloidosis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Renal amyloidosis usually manifests as proteinuria, often in the nephrotic range, and is associated with hypoalbuminemia, secondary hypercholesterolemia, hypertriglyceridemia, and edema or anasarca.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2494","text":"A bone marrow examination ​is being planned for a pa‍tient with AL amyloidosis‍. Which of the following ‌is likely to be seen?","raw_text":"A bone marrow examination is being planned for a patient with AL amyloidosis. Which of the following is likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma​cytosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dry​ tap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibr​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granulomato​us reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plasmacytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plasmacytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Bone marrow</strong> examination in<strong> AL amyloidosis</strong> shows <strong>plasmacytosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The AL type of amyloid or <strong>primary amyloidosis</strong> is associated with immunocyte dyscrasias like<strong> multiple myeloma </strong>or other<strong> B cell neoplasms</strong>. The tumor cells in multiple myeloma secrete light chains of the immunoglobulins of either<strong> the lambda or kappa</strong> type, which get deposited in the tissues as amyloid.</p>\n<p>The chemical nature of the amyloid is AL (<strong>A </strong>for <strong>amyloid</strong> and<strong> L </strong>for <strong>light chain</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2522","text":"In lardaceous spleen, depo​sition of amyloid occurs ‍in which of the following‍ sites?","raw_text":"In lardaceous spleen, deposition of amyloid occurs in which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinusoids ​of red pulp","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"White​ pulp","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splenic ​follicles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splenic​ capsule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sinusoids of red pulp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sinusoids of red pulp</strong></p><p><strong>Lardaceous spleen</strong> is due to the deposition of amyloid in <strong>sinusoids</strong> of the <strong>red pulp.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sago spleen </strong>is due to the deposition of amyloid in splenic <strong>follicles (white pulp).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2752","text":"Match the following. Condi​tion Amyloid protein 1. F‍amilial Mediterranean fev‍er a. Aβ 2. Alzheimer's d‌isease b. Aβ2m 3. Systemi‍c senile amyloidosis c. A‍A 4. Hemodialysis-associa‌ted amyloidosis d. ATTR","raw_text":"<p>Match the following.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 264px; width: 418px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 247.516px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 154.484px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Amyloid protein</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50.2812px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.234px;\">Familial Mediterranean fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7188px;\">a.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.766px;\">Aβ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50.2812px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.234px;\">Alzheimer's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7188px;\">b.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.766px;\">Aβ2m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50.2812px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.234px;\">Systemic senile amyloidosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7188px;\">c.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.766px;\">AA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50.2812px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.234px;\">Hemodialysis-associated amyloidosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.7188px;\">d.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.766px;\">ATTR</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-c,​ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-a, 2-d,​ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-a,​ 3-d, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-d, 2-b,​ 3-c, 4-a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 164px; width: 382px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 224.547px; text-align: center; height: 25px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.453px; text-align: center; height: 25px;\"><strong>Amyloid protein</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 224.547px; height: 25px;\">Familial Mediterranean fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.453px; height: 25px;\">AA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 224.547px; height: 25px;\">Alzheimer's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.453px; height: 25px;\">Aβ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 224.547px; height: 25px;\">Systemic senile amyloidosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.453px; height: 25px;\">ATTR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 224.547px; height: 20px;\">Hemodialysis-associated amyloidosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.453px; height: 20px;\">Aβ2m</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2519","text":"In a patient with suspecte​d systemic amyloidosis, w‍hich of the following is ‍the initial investigation‌ of choice?","raw_text":"In a patient with suspected systemic amyloidosis, which of the following is the initial investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kidney​ biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdominal f​at staining","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow​ aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal​ biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abdominal fat staining","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abdominal fat staining</strong></p><p>In suspected <strong>systemic amyloidosis,</strong> the initial investigation of choice is <strong>abdominal fat aspirate staining.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> of amyloidosis involves the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Localized</strong> amyloidosis - biopsy the organ involved. e.g., kidney</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic</strong> amyloidosis - deposits can be found in any tissue of the body\n<ul>\n<li>Initial test - <strong>non-invasive</strong> test on easily accessible tissue (eg: <strong>abdominal fat</strong>) which is positive in &gt;80% of cases</li>\n<li>If this is <strong>negative</strong> - more <strong>invasive biopsies</strong> of the kidney, heart, lung or GI tract (rectal biopsy) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Suspected AL</strong> amyloidosis - serum and urine <strong>electrophoresis</strong> and immunoelectrophoresis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>histological examination</strong> of the biopsy material is the commonest <strong>confirmatory</strong> method for the diagnosis in a suspected case of amyloidosis. <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The next best step is to determine the <strong>type</strong> of <strong>precursor protein</strong> by </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">immunohistochemistry</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">, immunoelectron microscopy, or extraction and biochemical analysis employing mass spectrometry.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Scintigraphy</strong> with <strong>radiolabeled </strong>serum amyloid P component is a rapid and specific test, which gives the extent of the disease. It can be used to follow up on patients undergoing treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2493","text":"A kidney biopsy sample is ​obtained from a patient w‍ith high suspicion of amy‍loidosis. All of the foll‌owing can be used to stai‍n this sample, except:","raw_text":"A kidney biopsy sample is obtained from a patient with high suspicion of amyloidosis. All of the following can be used to stain this sample, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiof​lavin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crystal​ violet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iod​ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sudan​ black","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sudan black","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07bfaaf781bc40048c283f4613e412a1x1280x1078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sudan black</strong></p><p><strong>Sudan Black</strong> is used for staining<strong> lipids, </strong>not amyloid.</p>\n<p>Staining for<strong> amyloid:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Congo red</strong> - most widely used and specific </li>\n<li><strong>Iodine</strong> staining -\n<ul>\n<li>Used <strong>for unfixed specimens</strong> or histological sections</li>\n<li>Amyloid stains <strong>mahogany brown</strong> and <strong>turns</strong> <strong>violet </strong>on adding sulfuric acid</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Thioflavin T </strong>and<strong> S</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Secondary immunofluorescence with ultraviolet light</li>\n<li>Thioflavin T is useful for demonstrating the juxtaglomerular apparatus of the kidney</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Metachromatic stains (<strong>crystal violet</strong> and methyl violet) - <strong>rose-pink</strong> appearance.</li>\n<li>Periodic-acid Schiff (<strong>PAS</strong>)<strong> positive</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the amyloid appearance on congo red under plain microscopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2488","text":"A pathologist is examining​ a sample from a patient ‍with amyloidosis. Which o‍f the following is false ‌about the specimen's appe‍arance?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining a sample from a patient with amyloidosis. Which of the following is false about the specimen's appearance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pink-red color on Congo-re​d staining","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rose pink appearance under​ crystal violet stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apple-green birefringence ​under light microscopy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mahogany brown color on io​dine staining","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apple-green birefringence under light microscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c830681cfff74d1ea88558d3c6f69e70x1280x1078.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/daea5316aae14b58bd2ef2d0c79acb00x800x573.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apple-green birefringence under light microscopy</strong></p><p><strong>Amyloid</strong> shows <strong>apple-green birefringence </strong>under <strong>polarised microscopy,</strong> after staining with Congo red.</p>\n<p>The appearance of amyloid: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Light</strong> microscopy -<strong> </strong><strong>amorphous, eosinophilic, hyaline, extracellular</strong> substance</li>\n<li><strong>Congo red</strong> stain - the characteristic appearance using this stain is due to the <strong>cross-β-pleated sheet</strong> conformation\n<ul>\n<li>Under<strong> ordinary light,</strong> it appears <strong>pink</strong> or red color (as shown below) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">- Under<strong> the polarizing microscope -</strong> imparts <strong>apple-green birefringence</strong> to amyloid deposits (as shown in the image below)</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electron</strong> microscopy -<strong> </strong>continuous, nonbranching fibrils with a diameter of approximately 7.5 to 10 nm</li>\n<li>X-ray <strong>crystallography</strong> and infrared <strong>spectroscopy</strong> - Characteristic cross-β-pleated sheet conformation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It appears <strong>mahogany brown</strong> on <strong>iodine</strong> staining and <strong>rose-pink</strong> on staining with <strong>crystal violet.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2331","text":"Which of the following is ​not directly involved in ‍graft rejection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not directly involved in graft rejection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T-C​ells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antib​odies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macro​phages","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"NK c​ells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Macrophages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Macrophages</strong></p><p>Macrophages present foreign graft antigens to the T-cells but are not directly involved in <strong>graft rejection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Graft rejection<strong> </strong>is both <strong>humoral</strong> and <strong>cell-mediated</strong> in nature. It is a process in which <strong>T-cells</strong> and <strong>antibodies</strong> produced against graft antigens react against tissue grafts and destroy it.</p>\n<p>The major antigenic differences that result in the <strong>rejection</strong> of transplants are differences in <strong>HLA alleles</strong>. The <strong>recipient</strong> <strong>T-cells</strong> are involved in the <strong>recognition </strong>of the <strong>donor MHC</strong> molecules and mount an immune response. Depending on the antigen-presenting cells (APCs) that are involved in T-cell activation, there are 2 pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct pathway of allorecognition - MHC antigens are presented by the <strong>APCs</strong> in the <strong>graft</strong></li>\n<li>Indirect pathway of allorecognition - MHC antigens are presented by the <strong>recipient’s</strong> <strong>own</strong> <strong>APCs</strong> (e.g., macrophages)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>early activation</strong> of <strong>NK cells</strong> kill allogeneic target cells directly or via antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and release various chemokines and immunomodulatory cytokines.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2334","text":"A man is receiving a match​ed blood group kidney all‍ograft. While closing the‍ abdomen, the surgeon not‌ices a sudden color chang‍e to pale-white in the tr‍ansplanted kidney. He dec‌ides to remove it. Which ​of the following is respo‍nsible for graft rejectio​n in this patient?","raw_text":"A man is receiving a matched blood group kidney allograft. While closing the abdomen, the surgeon notices a sudden color change to pale-white in the transplanted kidney. He decides to remove it. Which of the following is responsible for graft rejection in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preformed antibodies in th​e recipient","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cytotoxic T lymphocyte–med​iated injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tubulointerstitial inflamm​ation due to antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Donor antibodies causing e​ndotheliitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Preformed antibodies in the recipient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Preformed antibodies in the recipient</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario within minutes of transplant is suggestive of <strong>hyperacute rejection</strong> and is due to <strong>preformed antibodies</strong> from the <strong>recipient.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2740","text":"A patient underwent kidney​ transplantation due to s‍evere diabetic nephropath‍y. A few months later, he‌ presented with fever, ma‍laise, and flank pain. Du‍ring evaluation, oliguria‌ and an acute rise in cre​atinine levels were obser‍ved. A biopsy of the kidn​ey showed endotheliitis. ‌What is the likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A patient underwent kidney transplantation due to severe diabetic nephropathy. A few months later, he presented with fever, malaise, and flank pain. During evaluation, oliguria and an acute rise in creatinine levels were observed. A biopsy of the kidney showed endotheliitis. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute cell-mediated graft ​rejection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute antibody mediated gr​aft rejection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic gra​ft rejection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Graft vs h​ost disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute cell-mediated graft rejection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute cell-mediated graft rejection</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the biopsy findings of <strong>endotheliitis</strong> suggest a diagnosis of <strong>acute cell-mediated graft rejection.</strong></p>\n<p>In acute cell-mediated graft rejection, <strong>CD8+ CTLs</strong> directly destroy graft cells or <strong>CD4+ cells</strong> secrete cytokines that damage the graft.</p>\n<p>On histopathology, it produces two different patterns of injury in the graft:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tubulointerstitial pattern</strong> (type I): extensive interstitial and tubular inflammation associated with focal tubular injury</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Vascular pattern</strong> - Inflammation (type II) and necrosis (type III), <strong>endotheliitis</strong> (swollen endothelial cells with lymphocytes between endothelium and vessel wall)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Immunosuppressive therapy</strong> aims to prevent and reduce acute rejection by blocking the activation of alloreactive T cells. In the absence of humoral rejection, patients respond well to immunosuppressive therapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2335","text":"In patients with acute gra​ft-versus-host disease, w‍hich of the following org‍ans is least likely to be‌ involved?","raw_text":"In patients with acute graft-versus-host disease, which of the following organs is least likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G​IT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu​ng","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lung</strong></p><p>The lung is least likely to get affected in <strong>acute graft-versus-host disease</strong> (GVHD).</p>\n<p>GVHD occurs when immunologically competent cells or their precursors are transplanted into immunologically crippled recipients. The transferred cells recognize alloantigens in the host and attack host tissues.</p>\n<p>It is seen <strong>most commonly</strong> in the setting of hematopoietic stem cell (<strong>HSC</strong>) <strong>transplantation.</strong> Rarely, it may occur following transplantation of solid organs rich in lymphoid cells (e.g., the liver) or transfusion of <strong>unirradiated blood.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is of two types, acute and chronic.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute GVHD </strong>develops within <strong>days to weeks</strong><strong>. </strong>There is the involvement of the <strong>immune system</strong> and <strong>epithelium</strong> of the skin, liver, and intestines. It presents with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Generalized rash</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Bloody diarrhea due to mucosal ulceration</li>\n<li>Non-infectious pulmonary infiltrates (rarely), the outcome is generally poor</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chronic GVHD </strong>may follow the acute syndrome or occur insidiously. It presents with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin - extensive<strong> cutaneous injury,</strong> with the destruction of skin appendages and dermal fibrosis (may resemble <strong>systemic sclerosis)</strong></li>\n<li>Liver<strong> - chronic liver disease,</strong> manifested as cholestatic jaundice</li>\n<li>GIT - <strong>esophageal strictures</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Immune system</strong> involvement - involution of the thymus and depletion of lymphocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>GVHD is <strong>mediated by T lymphocytes</strong> contained in the transplanted <strong>donor cells. </strong>Therefore, depletion of donor T cells before transfusion virtually eliminates the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0594","text":"Graft from identical twin ​is called:","raw_text":"Graft from identical twin is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allo​graft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auto​graft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isog​raft","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Xeno​graft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isograft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isograft</strong></p><p>A graft from an<strong> identical twin</strong> is called an <strong>isograft</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Isograft</strong> is defined as an organ or tissue that is taken from a donor and is transplanted to a recipient who has the <strong>same genetic constitution</strong> as the donor. For example, graft between<strong> identical twins.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-a-qU5vl","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"17_anti_diuretics","title":"17.Anti Diuretics","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MA5538","text":"A 50-year-old man was rush‌ed to the ER with impaire​d consciousness following​ a hunger strike for a da​y. His wife reported, he ‌had a head injury 1 month‌ back and since then had ​complaints of polyuria. O​n examination, he was fou‌nd to be severely dehydra‌ted and MRI brain showed ​an absence of the charact​eristic bright spot in th‌e pituitary. Which of the​ following is the drug of‍ choice for his condition‌?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man was rushed to the ER with impaired consciousness following a hunger strike for a day. His wife reported, he had a head injury 1 month back and since then had complaints of polyuria. On examination, he was found to be severely dehydrated and MRI brain showed an absence of the characteristic bright spot in the pituitary.  Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amil‌oride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triam‌terene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Desmopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Desmopressin</strong></p><p>The given scenario of polyuria following a head injury and an absence of a pituitary bright spot on the MRI brain is suggestive of<strong> central diabetes insipidus</strong>. The drug of choice for this condition is <strong>desmopressin,</strong> a <strong>V<sub>2</sub> receptor agonist.</strong></p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of desmopressin is: It stimulates the V2 receptors on the basolateral membrane of the principal cells in the collecting tubules. This increases the insertion of <strong>water channels (aquaporin 2)</strong> into the apical membrane. This leads to increased water reabsorption in the collecting ducts. Activation of V<sub>2</sub> receptors also increase the permeability of urea.</p>\n<p>Option B: Amiloride is a potassium-sparing diuretic. It is indicated for lithium-induced <strong>nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Thiazides paradoxically exert an antidiuretic effect in DI but are not the drugs of choice. They are generally used in <strong>nephrogenic DI</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Triamterene is also a potassium-sparing diuretic that can be used to treat <strong>nephrogenic DI</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Patients with DI usually do not develop overt signs of dehydration due to increased intake of water to compensate for polyuria. But when water intake is impaired they can present with dehydration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5542","text":"A patient having polyuria ‌and polydipsia is diagnos​ed with central diabetes ​insipidus. He is started ​on intranasal desmopressi‌n. Which of the following‌ is not a reason for choo​sing this over intravenou​s vasopressin?","raw_text":"A patient having polyuria and polydipsia is diagnosed with central diabetes insipidus. He is started on intranasal desmopressin. Which of the following is not a reason for choosing this over intravenous vasopressin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher ‌potency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Higher selectivity for V1 ‌receptor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Has lower vasoconstrictor ‌activity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Longer‌ acting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Higher selectivity for V1 receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Higher selectivity for V1 receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Desmopressin</strong> is preferred in the treatment of <strong>central diabetes insipidus</strong> as it is a<strong> selective agonist </strong>of the<strong> V<sub>2</sub> receptor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Desmopressin has minimal or <strong>no action on V1</strong> receptors resulting in <strong>little vasoconstrictor activity</strong>. It is <strong>more potent</strong> than vasopressin. It is <strong>longer acting</strong> than vasopressin as enzymatic degradation of desmopressin is slow.</p>\n<p>Lypressin acts on both V1 and V2 receptors. Terlipressin is a synthetic vasopressin analog that has more selectivity for the V1 receptor and produces fewer adverse effects than lypressin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5545","text":"A 30-year-old woman with p‌ituitary adenoma is non-c​ompliant with her treatme​nt with cabergoline. She ​has now presented with bi‌temporal hemianopia and f‌eatures suggestive of dia​betes insipidus. Which of​ the following drugs cann‌ot be used to manage her ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with pituitary adenoma is non-compliant with her treatment with cabergoline. She has now presented with bitemporal hemianopia and features suggestive of diabetes insipidus. Which of the following drugs cannot be used to manage her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorpr‌opamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Furosemide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Furosemide</strong></p><p><strong>Furosemide</strong> is not used for the treatment of diabetes insipidus. It is a loop diuretic that might further <strong>worsen diabetes insipidus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for central diabetes insipidus is <strong>desmopressin</strong>. Desmopressin is a V<sub>2</sub> receptor agonist and synthetic analog vasopressin. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Chlorpropamide</strong> <strong>enhances</strong> the action of circulating <strong>vasopressin</strong> and reduces urine formation in central DI.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Carbamazepine</strong> also reduces urine formation in central DI. The exact mechanism is unknown.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2939","text":"Tachyphylaxis is a concern‌ with the use of desmopre​ssin in which of the foll​owing patients?","raw_text":"Tachyphylaxis is a concern with the use of desmopressin in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 50-year-old man with cen‌tral DI post meningitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 25-year-old woman on lit‌hium therapy with nephrog​enic DI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 17-year-old girl with vo‌n willebrand's disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 7-year-old child with pr‌imary nocturnal enuresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 17-year-old girl with von willebrand's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 17-year-old girl with von willebrand&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Tachyphylaxis</strong> refers to the <strong>rapid development of tolerance</strong> when doses of a drug are repeated in quick succession. It is a concern when<strong> desmopressin </strong>is used in<strong> bleeding disorders </strong>because long-term use of desmopressin can cause <strong>depletion of factor VIII</strong> and <strong>von Willebrand factor</strong> stores. </p>\n<p>The usefulness of desmopressin in bleeding disorders is limited to preoperative preparation, postoperative bleeding, excessive menstrual bleeding, and emergency situations. In patients with type I von Willebrand disease and in some with type IIn vWD, desmopressin elevates von Willebrand factor and shortens the bleeding time. </p>\n<p>Option A: It does not cause tachyphylaxis in central DI. </p>\n<p>Option B: Desmopressin is <strong>not indicated</strong> in nephrogenic DI.</p>\n<p>Option D: Bedtime administration of desmopressin for primary nocturnal enuresis provides a high response rate that is sustained with long-term use. However, intranasal desmopressin is <strong>no longer recommended</strong> for the treatment of primary nocturnal enuresis because of the increased <strong>risk of hyponatremia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5562","text":"A 50-year-old man who is o‌n treatment for small cel​l carcinoma of the lung p​resents with acute onset ​confusion, nausea, and vo‌miting. He appears to be ‌euvolemic. Laboratory inv​estigations reveal serum ​sodium levels to be 120 m‌Eq/l and urine osmolality‌ is 300 mOsm/kg . Which o​f the following is not us​ed as a treatment option ‌in his condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man who is on treatment for small cell carcinoma of the lung presents with acute onset confusion, nausea, and vomiting. He appears to be euvolemic. Laboratory investigations reveal serum sodium levels to be 120 mEq/l and urine osmolality is 300 mOsm/kg . Which of the following is not used as a treatment option in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Demeclo‌cycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Restriction of free water ‌intake","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3% ‌NaCl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Desmopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Desmopressin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (<strong>SIADH</strong>) which can occur as a paraneoplastic syndrome of small cancer of the lung. Desmopressin is not used in the treatment of SIADH.</p>\n<p>Since excess vasopressin is the cause for SIADH, giving a vasopressin analog like <strong>desmopressin</strong> would only <strong>worsen the condition</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of SIADH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluid restriction</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Hypertonic saline</strong>( 3%) + <strong>loop diuretics</strong> is the treatment of choice in <strong>severe symptomatic hyponatremia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tolvaptan and conivaptan (<strong>vasopressin receptor antagonists</strong>) are preferred for long-term use.</li>\n<li>Demeclocycline (a tetracycline drug) is used as an alternative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7469","text":"The best approach in the t‌reatment of chronic SIADH​ is ______","raw_text":"The best approach in the treatment of chronic SIADH is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolv‌aptan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reducing fluid intake to &amp;‌lt;500 ml per day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Demeclo‌cycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fludroc‌ortisone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tolvaptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tolvaptan</strong></p><p>The best approach in the treatment of <strong>chronic SIADH</strong> is the oral administration of a vaptan like<strong> tolvaptan</strong>, which is a <strong>selective V2 receptor antagonist</strong>. This<strong> </strong>increases urinary water excretion by <strong>blocking</strong> the <strong>antidiuretic effect of AVP</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>treatment</strong> options <strong>for chronic SIADH</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluid intake &lt; 500 ml/day</li>\n<li>Demeclocycline</li>\n<li>Fludrocortisone </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Increased thirst is a frequently observed characteristic of hyponatremia in SIADH. <strong>Fluid restriction</strong> is usually a burden to the patient. It is <strong>unreliable, impractical,</strong> and <strong>slow</strong> to work. As thirst is particularly difficult to suppress, water restriction in SIADH frequently <strong>leads to</strong> <strong>non-compliance</strong> in the long run. Vaptans would be especially suited for the treatment of these patients. <strong>Serum sodium</strong> concentrations are also <strong>corrected by vaptans</strong> and hence, vaptans substantially improve the quality of life in these patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5557","text":"A patient with liver cirrh‌osis presenting with asci​tes and pedal edema is ad​ministered conivaptan. Wh​ich of the following stat‌ements regarding the drug‌ is true?","raw_text":"A patient with liver cirrhosis presenting with ascites and pedal edema is administered conivaptan. Which of the following statements regarding the drug is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasopressin receptor antag‌onist","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"V2 select‌ive action","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Given ‌orally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used in hy‌pernatremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vasopressin receptor antagonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vasopressin receptor antagonist</strong></p><p>Conivaptan is a <strong>nonselective vasopressin</strong> receptor <strong>antagonist</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Conivaptan</strong> antagonizes both V<sub>1a</sub> and V<sub>2</sub> receptors. It is administered <strong>intravenously</strong> and is used in the treatment of <strong>euvolemic and hypervolemic hyponatremia.</strong> Vaptans have been proven to mobilize ascites and improve hyponatremia in patients with cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>Vasopressin receptor blockers are classified as:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 366px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237px;\">Classification of receptor blockers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"> Drugs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237px;\">Nonselective (mixed V1/V2)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Conivaptan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237px;\">V1 selective(V1 RA)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Relcovaptan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237px;\">V1b selective(V3 RA)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Nelivaptan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237px;\">V2 selective(V2 RA)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Lixivaptan<br/>Mozavaptan<br/>Satavaptan<br/>Tolvaptan<br/> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2940","text":"A 50-year-old alcoholic wi‌th deranged liver functio​n tests is to be started ​on treatment for chronic ​SIADH. Which of the follo‌wing drugs should not be ‌used in his treatment reg​imen?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old alcoholic with deranged liver function tests is to be started on treatment for chronic SIADH.  Which of the following drugs should not be used in his treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coniv‌aptan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tolv‌aptan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Demeclo‌cycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lixiv‌aptan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tolvaptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tolvaptan</strong></p><p><strong>Tolvaptan</strong> should not be used in patients with liver disease as it<strong> </strong>has been associated with<strong> liver damage.  </strong>Administration of tolvaptan is generally limited to 30 days to limit the risk of hepatotoxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7338","text":"A patient who was diagnose‌d with ADPKD was prescrib​ed tolvaptan. After a few​ weeks, he presented with​ dry mouth and increased ‌thirst. What is the likel‌y cause? ADPKD- Autosomal​ dominant polycystic kidn​ey disease","raw_text":"<p>A patient who was diagnosed with ADPKD was prescribed tolvaptan. After a few weeks, he presented with dry mouth and increased thirst. What is the likely cause?</p>\n<p>ADPKD- Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased free water clear‌ance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"V2 recept‌or agonism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased renal cAMP level‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in urine osmolali‌ty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased free water clearance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased free water clearance</strong></p><p><strong>Tolvaptan</strong> causes <strong>increased free water</strong> <strong>clearance</strong>, which causes <strong>dehydration</strong>. Hence, the patient may present with dry mouth and increased thirst.</p>\n<p>Tolvaptan is a vasopressin <strong>V2 receptor antagonist. </strong>Decreased binding of vasopressin to the V2 receptor in the kidney <strong>lowers the adenyl cyclase activity</strong> leading to decreased intracellular cAMP. <strong>Decreased cAMP</strong> concentrations <strong>prevent</strong> the fusion of<strong> aquaporin 2</strong> containing vesicles with the plasma membrane, leading to an increase in urinary water excretion (free water clearance), and a decrease in urine osmolality.</p>\n<p>The common <strong>side effects of tolvaptan</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>dehydration</li>\n<li>hypernatremia</li>\n<li>hypovolemia</li>\n<li>liver injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tolvaptan is used to <strong>delay disease progression</strong> in patients with <strong>ADPKD</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"g51JURr-","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"17_hypothalamus_and_limbic_system","title":"17.Hypothalamus And Limbic System","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB1246","text":"Which of the following is ‍true about thalamus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about thalamus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thalamus is the sensory ga‍teway to cerebral cortex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thalamus is the largest pa‍rt of telencephalon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Y-shaped band of fibers di‍vides the thalamus into 4​ subdivisions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thalamus is the motor gate‍way from the cortex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thalamus is the sensory gateway to cerebral cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thalamus is the sensory gateway to cerebral cortex</strong></p><p>The thalamus is being called the<strong> sensory gateway to the cerebral cortex</strong> as all information that reaches the cortex is processed by the thalamus.</p>\n<p>The thalamus is a large collection of neuronal groups within the diencephalon; it participates in sensory, motor, and limbic functions. </p>\n<p><strong>Other Options</strong>:  </p>\n<p>Option B: Thalamus is the largest part of the <strong>diencephalon</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Y shaped band of fibers divide the thalamus into <strong>3 subdivisions</strong>- anterior, medial, and lateral group.</p>\n<p>Option D: Thalamus is the sensory gateway to the cortex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1247","text":"Which thalamic nuclei tran‍smit pain?","raw_text":"Which thalamic nuclei transmit pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral‍ dorsal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intral‍aminar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral ‍posterior","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulv‍inar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventral posterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventral posterior</strong></p><p>The ventroposterior nuclear complex of the thalamus represents the main termination site for ascending somatosensory information (which includes pain) in the diencephalon.</p>\n<p>Thalamic nuclei have been divided into</p>\n<p>A. Specific (Relay) nuclei because they have a specific and selective relationship with a particular portion of neocortex</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li><strong>Medial geniculate body</strong> for auditory information</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral geniculate body</strong> is for information from retina</li>\n<li><strong>Ventroposterior group</strong> for somatosensory information</li>\n</ol>\n<p>B. Nonspecific nuclei project to several cortical and subcortical regions</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1248","text":"Which of the following is ‍called ''the window of li​mbic system'' ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is called ''the window of limbic system'' ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoth‍alamus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hippo‍campus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amyg‍dala","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thal‍amus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amygdala","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amygdala</strong></p><p>The <strong>amygdala</strong> has been called the <strong>“window”</strong> through which the limbic system sees the place of the person in the world.</p>\n<p>The amygdala receives neuronal signals from all portions of the limbic cortex, as well as from the neocortex of the temporal, parietal, and occipital lobes, especially from the auditory and visual association areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Components of the limbic system </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The term <strong>limbic lobe,</strong> or <strong>limbic system,</strong> is applied to the part of the brain that consists of a rim of cortical tissue around the hilum of the cerebral hemisphere and a group of associated deep structures—the amygdala, the hippocampus, and the septal nuclei.</li>\n<li>The <strong>papez circuit</strong> is a closed neural circuitry that starts and ends in the hippocampus, which forms the basal ganglia. It is also known as the <strong>medial limbic circuit</strong>. The components of papez circuit are-</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic-H</strong>e <strong>M</strong>an <strong>A</strong>te <strong>C</strong>at</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>e-Hippocampus</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>an-Mamillary body</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>te-Anterior thalamus</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>at-Cingulate gyrus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1249","text":"Which of the following is ‍a component of the Papez ​circuit?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of the Papez circuit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral dor‍sal nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior nucleus of thalmu‍s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral ante‍rior nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulv‍inar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior nucleus of thalmus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ee64e2d841a4615bb7b273ba140c293x1280x1360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior nucleus of thalmus</strong></p><p><strong>Anterior nucleus </strong>of the <strong>thalamus</strong> is a component of <strong>Papez circuit</strong>, which is important for <strong>short term memory.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Papez Circuit (refer figure):</strong></p>\n<p>The limbic system was studied by James Papez who described the Papez circuit. It consists of:</p>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic </strong>for components of Papez circuit<strong>-H</strong>e <strong>M</strong>an <strong>A</strong>te <strong>C</strong>at</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H</strong>e-Hippocampus</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>an-Mamillary body</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>te-Anterior thalamus</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>at-Cingulate gyrus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fornix connects the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies, which are in turn connected to the anterior nuclei of the thalamus by the mammillothalamic tract. The anterior nuclei of the thalamus project to the cingulate cortex, and from there, connections to the hippocampus complete a complex closed circuit.</p>\n<p>The boundaries of the limbic system were subsequently expanded to include the amygdala, septum, basal forebrain, nucleus accumbens, and orbitofrontal cortex.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1250","text":"Stimulation of which of th‍e following regions promo​tes a 'sense of reward' i‌n human beings?","raw_text":"Stimulation of which of the following regions promotes a 'sense of reward' in human beings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins‍ula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Put‍amen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial fore‍brain bundle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aqueduct ‍of sylvius","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medial forebrain bundle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medial forebrain bundle</strong></p><p>Major <strong>reward centres</strong> are located along the course of the <strong>medial forebrain bundle</strong>, especially in the <strong>lateral</strong> and <strong>ventromedial nuclei</strong> of the <strong>hypothalamus. </strong>Therefore, stimulation of those areas will promote a sense of reward.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is interesting to note that in the <strong>lateral nucleus</strong> of the <strong>hypothalamus</strong>, a <strong>weak stimulus</strong> produces a sense of <strong>pleasure</strong> whereas a <strong>strong stimulus</strong> produces <strong>rage</strong>. This phenomenon occurs in many areas where a weak stimulus produces a sense of reward while stronger ones produce a sense of punishment.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1255","text":"In response to an increase‍ in plasma osmolality, AD​H is released in blood. W‌hich of the following sit​es contains the receptor ​to detect a change in pla‌sma osmolality?","raw_text":"In response to an increase in plasma osmolality, ADH is released in blood. Which of the following sites contains the receptor to detect a change in plasma osmolality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preo‍ptic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arc‍uate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior h‍ypothalamus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mammi‍llary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior hypothalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior hypothalamus</strong></p><p><strong>Osmoreceptors</strong> that detect any change in plasma osmolality and control ADH secrection, are located in <strong>anterior hypothalamus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1257","text":"The main organ for biologi‍cal clock is _______.","raw_text":"The main organ for biological clock is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thal‍amus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoth‍alamus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cereb‍ellum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal ‍ganglia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypothalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypothalamus</strong></p><p>In the hypothalamus, the <strong>suprachiasmatic nucleus</strong> functions as the principal <strong>pacemaker, </strong>or time clock, for circadian rhythms.</p>\n<p>The pacemaker receives input<strong> from the eyes and many other parts of the nervous system</strong>, and these inputs mediate the effects exerted by the external environment (<strong>retinohypothalamic tract</strong>).</p>\n<p>One output of the pacemaker goes to the <strong>pineal gland, </strong>a gland within the brain that secretes the hormone <strong>melatonin.</strong>These neural signals cause the pineal gland to secrete melatonin during darkness but not during daylight.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1259","text":"Which of the following cli‍nical features will be mo​st likely seen in a patie‌nt with an anterior hypot​halamic lesion?","raw_text":"Which of the following clinical features will be most likely seen in a patient with an anterior hypothalamic lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyper‍phagia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot‍hermia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperg‍lycemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‍hermia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperthermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperthermia</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 466\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Lesions involving the <strong>anterior </strong>and <strong>preoptic hypothalamic</strong> regions cause paradoxical<strong> vasoconstriction</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong>, and <strong>hyperthermia</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The anterior hypothalamus activates mechanisms of <strong>cooling</strong> the body when the temperature increases via the reflex response.</p>\n<p><strong>Stimulation</strong> of the anterior hypothalamus causes cutaneous <strong>vasodilation </strong>and <strong>sweating</strong>, thereby cooling the body. <strong>Lesions</strong> in this region cause <strong>hyperthermia</strong>, with rectal temperatures sometimes reaching 43°C (109.4°F).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1261","text":"Diurnal variation of ACTH ‍is controlled by which of​ the following regions?","raw_text":"Diurnal variation of ACTH is controlled by which of the following regions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paraventricular hypothalam‍us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior h‍ypothalamus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral hy‍pothalamus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supra chiasm‍atic nucleus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Supra chiasmatic nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Supra chiasmatic nucleus</strong></p><p>Diurnal variation of ACTH depend on <strong>suprachiasmatic nucleus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The secretory rates of <strong>ACTH </strong>and <strong>cortisol</strong> are <strong>high </strong>in the<strong> early morning</strong> but<strong> low</strong> in the<strong> late evening</strong>. This effect results from a 24-hour cyclical alteration in the signals from the suprachiasmatic nucleus of hypothalamus that cause cortisol secretion.When a person changes daily sleeping habits, the cycle changes correspondingly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1260","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a function of the cat​egorical hemisphere?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of the categorical hemisphere?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mathemati‍c ability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lang‍uage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Writing‍ ability","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Music ‍ability","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Music ability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Music ability</strong></p><p>Functions of the categorical hemisphere are<strong> logical, analytical, sequential thinking, </strong>and <strong>verbal tasks, </strong>such as<strong> math, language forms,</strong> and <strong>philosophy.</strong></p>\n<p>Human language functions depend more on one cerebral hemisphere than on the other. This hemisphere is concerned with <strong>categorization</strong> and symbolization and has often been called the <strong>dominant hemisphere. </strong>However, the other hemisphere is specialized in the area of spatiotemporal relations.</p>\n<p>The concept of “cerebral dominance” and a dominant and non-dominant hemisphere has been replaced by, one hemisphere for sequential-analytic processes (the <strong>categorical hemisphere</strong>) and one for visuospatial relations (the <strong>representational hemi­sphere</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>representational cerebral hemisphere</strong> excels in nonlanguage skills, especially spatial perception, and artistic and musical talents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3549","text":"Lesions in which nucleus o‍f the hypothalamus produc​e a voracious appetite an‌d rage?","raw_text":"Lesions in which nucleus of the hypothalamus produce a voracious appetite and rage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper region of the latera‍l hypothalamus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mammilla‍ry region","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventromedial nucleus of th‍e hypothalamus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior region of the hyp‍othalamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus</strong></p><p>A lesion in the <strong>ventromedial </strong>nucleus of the hypothalamus, the <strong>satiety center</strong>, is responsible for producing a voracious appetite and rage.</p>\n<p>Lesions in the upper region of the lateral hypothalamus produce a profound loss of appetite.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7141","text":"Identify the site which is‍ responsible for relievin​g pain when pressure is a‌pplied to the painful are​a.","raw_text":"Identify the site which is responsible for relieving pain when pressure is applied to the painful area.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‍B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/74c90cdb28d74654865a4fb00eadc576.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>It is a cross section of brain stem at pons.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> is a <strong>periaqueductal gray matter</strong> which also consists of <strong>raphe nucleus</strong>.</li>\n<li>Descending pathways from the mesencephalic periaqueductal gray matter inhibit transmission in nociceptive pathways. This descending pathway includes a synapse in the ventromedial medulla (Raphe nucleus) and the release of endogenous opiates. So periaqueductal gray matter releases endogenous opiates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other marked structures are</p>\n<p>B: Corticospinal tract</p>\n<p>C: Medial lemniscus</p>\n<p>D: Medial longitudinal fasciculus</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6173","text":"The factors that can cause‍ a person to drink water ​due to stimulation of thi‌rst mechanism is/are ____​_______.","raw_text":"The factors that can cause a person to drink water due to stimulation of thirst mechanism is/are ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehyd‍ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemor‍rhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prandial‍ Drinking","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‍he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p><strong>Dehydration, hemorrhage, and prandial drinking:</strong> All three can cause <strong>increased thirst</strong> resulting in water drinking.<br/><br/><strong>Increased osmolality</strong> acts via <strong>osmoreceptors</strong> in the <strong>anterior hypothalamus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>decreased plasma volume</strong>, as in <strong>hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>acts via</strong> <br/>1. Renin-angiotensin system<br/>2. Baroreceptors in heart and blood vessels.<br/><br/>The <strong>intake of liquids during a meal</strong> is known as<strong> prandial drinking</strong>. The increase is known as learned or habit response but is not understood completely.</p>\n<p>The following factors are thought to play a role<br/>1. Increase in plasma osmolality due to food absorption<br/>2. Action on hypothalamus by one or more gastrointestinal hormones</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"x1BPRrRm","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"17_trauma_and_stress_related_disorders","title":"17.Trauma And Stress Related Disorders","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MC0895","text":"While measuring the stress‌ psychologically, which o‍f the following life even‍ts is considered the most‌ significant?","raw_text":"While measuring the stress psychologically, which of the following life events is considered the most significant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Death of a close family me‌mber","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Death of‌ a spouse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Marr‌iage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Div‌orce","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Death of a spouse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Death of a spouse</strong></p><p><strong>The death of a spouse</strong> is rated the most significant life event with <strong>100 life change units</strong>. Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe constructed a social readjustment rating scale after ranking the relative degree of adjustment required by changing life events. Each event is assigned a certain number of units.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The death of a spouse- 100 life-change units</li>\n<li>Divorce- 73 units</li>\n<li>Marital separations- 65 units</li>\n<li>The death of a close family member- 63 units</li>\n<li>Marriage- 50 units</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Accumulation of <strong>200 or more</strong> life-change units in a single year increases the risk of developing a <strong>psychosomatic disorder</strong> in that year.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7151","text":"A 40-year-old lady met wit‌h a car accident 2 months‍ ago, following which she‍ complains of screaming a‌nd waking up in the night‍ after having nightmares ‌of the same incident over‌ and over again. Which of‌ the following conditions​ is she suffering from?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old lady met with a car accident 2 months ago, following which she complains of screaming and waking up in the night after having nightmares of the same incident over and over again. Which of the following conditions is she suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute stre‌ss reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adjustmen‌t disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ma‌nia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post traumatic stress diso‌rder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Post traumatic stress disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Post traumatic stress disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder</strong>. PTSD is a psychiatric disorder occurring after a<strong> life-threatening or potentially fatal event</strong>, (e.g., war, house fire, serious accident, rape, robbery) affecting the patient or the patient’s close friend or relative.</p>\n<p>Symptoms can be divided into four types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Re-experiencing</strong> (e.g., intrusive memories of the event [<strong>flashbacks</strong>] and <strong>nightmares</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperarousal</strong> (e.g., anxiety, increased startle response, impaired sleep, hypervigilance)</li>\n<li><strong>Negative cognition</strong> (e.g., negative beliefs, emotional numbing)</li>\n<li><strong>Avoidance</strong> (e.g., avoiding stimuli associated with the traumatic event)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Psychotherapies found to be effective for PTSD include exposure therapy, trauma-focused cognitive-behavioral therapy, eye movement desensitization, and reprocessing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3553","text":"As a volunteer at a refuge‌e camp, you come across a‍n adolescent with complai‍nts of difficulty in slee‌ping and nightmares. Havi‍ng lost both his parents ‌to cross-firing, and witn‌essing the arrest, tortur‌e, and eventual killing o​f his elder brother, he n​ow prefers to spend most ‌of his time alone and doe​sn't interact with others​. This disorder is associ‍ated with the overactivit‌y of which of the followi​ng structures?","raw_text":"As a volunteer at a refugee camp, you come across an adolescent with complaints of difficulty in sleeping and nightmares. Having lost both his parents to cross-firing, and witnessing the arrest, torture, and eventual killing of his elder brother, he now prefers to spend most of his time alone and doesn't interact with others. This disorder is associated with the overactivity of which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoth‌alamus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amyg‌dala","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thal‌amus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stri‌atum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amygdala","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amygdala</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)</strong> and is due to abnormally high activity in the<strong> amygdala</strong>, which makes the patients relive their traumatic memories.</p>\n<p>The amygdala is responsible for the genesis of emotion, rating the emotional importance of an experience and activating the <strong>hippocampus</strong> accordingly. Thus, an emotionally intense experience is <strong>imprinted in the memory</strong>. As a result, the traumatic memories lead to a state of <strong>constant vigilance</strong>, even in safe, familiar settings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0930","text":"What is the minimum durati‌on of period to diagnose ‍post-traumatic stress dis‍order in a patient?","raw_text":"What is the minimum duration of period to diagnose post-traumatic stress disorder in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 days t‌o 1 month","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More tha‌n 1 month","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 months t‌o 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More than‌ 6 months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. More than 1 month","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. More than 1 month</strong></p><p>In <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder</strong> (PTSD), the symptoms persist for <strong>more than one month</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 310px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.506px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.494px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.506px; height: 42px; text-align: left;\">2 days to 1 month</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.494px; height: 42px; text-align: left;\">Acute stress disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42.6761px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.506px; height: 42.6761px; text-align: left;\">More than 1 month</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.494px; height: 42.6761px; text-align: left;\">Post-traumatic stress disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.506px; height: 54px; text-align: left;\">Symptoms occur after 6 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.494px; height: 54px; text-align: left;\">Delayed Post-traumatic stress disorder</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7152","text":"A 27-year-old army major c‌omes to you for help at h‍is fiancée's request, who‍ told him that he has bec‌ome reserved and quiet po‍st his Afghan tour. He is‌ easily startled by noise‌ and motion and spends ex‌cessive time searching fo​r threats that are never ​confirmed. He also avoids‌ seeing friends from the ​Army unit because they re​mind him of his experienc‍es at the base camp. Whic‌h of the following therap​ies can be used for him?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old army major comes to you for help at his fiancée's request, who told him that he has become reserved and quiet post his Afghan tour. He is easily startled by noise and motion and spends excessive time searching for threats that are never confirmed. He also avoids seeing friends from the Army unit because they remind him of his experiences at the base camp. Which of the following therapies can be used for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eye movement desensitisati‌on and reprocessing","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aversion‌ therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systematic desensitisation‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interpersonal psychotherap‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder</strong> (PTSD). Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing is a psychotherapy for PTSD. </p>\n<p><strong>Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR):</strong></p>\n<p>The patient focuses on the lateral movement of the clinician's finger while maintaining a mental image of the traumatic experience. The symptoms can be relieved as patients work through the traumatic event while in a state of deep relaxation.</p>\n<p>The best treatment modality is<strong> trauma-focused cognitive behaviour therapy. </strong>Drugs like selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, benzodiazepines, propranolol, etc can be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0925","text":"A 43-year-old man presente‌d with complaints tracing‍ back a year ago to a tra‍umatic work-related event‌ involving mutilation of ‍his right-hand fingers. H‌e reported having flashba‌cks and nightmares of the‌ event, inability to perf​orm everyday tasks and a ​depressive mood. No sympt‌omatology was evident bef​ore the event to him or h​is social milieu. Which o‍f the following is a bad ‌prognostic sign of this d​isorder?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old man presented with complaints tracing back a year ago to a traumatic work-related event involving mutilation of his right-hand fingers. He reported having flashbacks and nightmares of the event, inability to perform everyday tasks and a depressive mood. No symptomatology was evident before the event to him or his social milieu. Which of the following is a bad prognostic sign of this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gradual onset of the sympt‌oms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Short duration of the symp‌toms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social ‌support","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middl‌e age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gradual onset of the symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gradual onset of the symptoms</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder</strong> (PTSD). <strong>Gradual onset of symptoms</strong> is a bad prognostic sign of PTSD.</p>\n<p>A good prognosis is predicted by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rapid</strong> <strong>onset</strong> of the symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>Short</strong> <strong>duration</strong> of the symptoms (less than 6 months)</li>\n<li>Good premorbid functioning</li>\n<li>Strong <strong>social supports</strong>.</li>\n<li>Absence of other psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In general, the very young and the very old have more difficulty with traumatic events than those in midlife.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Young</strong> children have <strong>inadequate coping mechanisms.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Older</strong> persons are likely to have more rigid coping mechanisms and <strong>less</strong> able to muster a <strong>flexible</strong> approach to dealing with the effects of trauma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6706","text":"A male patient who lost hi‌s job recently became irr‍itable and had a sad mood‍ for most of the time. Th‌inking about the future w‍orried him and he felt ir‌ritation towards his fami‌ly members. He occasional‌ly went out to watch movi​es with his friends and w​as able to enjoy but on r‌eturning home he felt sim​ilar symptoms. What is th​e probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A male patient who lost his job recently became irritable and had a sad mood for most of the time. Thinking about the future worried him and he felt irritation towards his family members. He occasionally went out to watch movies with his friends and was able to enjoy but on returning home he felt similar symptoms. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Generalised anxiety disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adjustmen‌t disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mixed anxiety with depress‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Moderate ‌depression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adjustment disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adjustment disorder</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of an <strong>adjustment disorder.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Adjustment disorder</strong> is an emotional response to a stressful event. <strong>Stressors</strong> may be <strong>single</strong> like loss of job or divorce; <strong>multiple</strong> like the death of a parent together with loss of a job; or <strong>recurrent</strong> like seasonal business difficulties.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosing criteria for adjustment disorder</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Development of emotional or behavioural response <strong>within 3 months</strong> of the onset of the stressor.</li>\n<li>The symptoms or behaviour are <strong>clinically significant,</strong> where, any one of the following are met:\n<ul>\n<li>The response is <strong>out of proportion</strong> to the severity or intensity of the stressor.</li>\n<li>Significant <strong>impairment</strong> in <strong>social, occupational,</strong> or other areas of functioning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The stress-related disturbance does not meet the criteria for another mental disorder or exacerbation of pre-existing mental disorder.</li>\n<li>Mostly depressive but can also have anxiety symptoms.</li>\n<li>The symptoms <strong>disappear</strong> within <strong>6 months</strong> if the <strong>stressor</strong> is<strong> removed.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In the given case</strong>, it has been mentioned that the patient lost his job recently. Although a time period is not mentioned in the question, the term recently can be understood as well within the 3 months duration. The patient's behaviour such as sad and irritated affected his social functioning. As he was finding joy in watching movies, depression can be ruled out.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7155","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the diagn‍ostic criteria of adjustm‍ent disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the diagnostic criteria of adjustment disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The onset of symptoms occu‌r after 3 months of the s‍tressor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The symptoms do not persis‌t if the stressor is remo‍ved","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The symptoms of distress a‌re clearly in excess of t‍he normal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both depressive and anxiet‌y symptoms may be present‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The onset of symptoms occur after 3 months of the stressor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The onset of symptoms occur after 3 months of the stressor</strong></p><p><strong>Adjustment disorder</strong> is characterised by emotional or behavioural symptoms that <strong>develop within 3 months</strong> of <strong>exposure</strong> to an identifiable <strong>stressor</strong> and usually do not last more than 6 months after the stressor ends.</p>\n<p>Common stressors leading to adjustment disorder are <strong>financial issues, illness &amp; relationship problems.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Psychotherapy </strong>is the<strong> treatment of choice</strong> for adjustment disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7156","text":"Two weeks after the death ‌of her husband, a 60-year‍-old woman presented to y‍our OPD with poor appetit‌e, anger, and sadness. Sh‍e also says she hears her‌ husband's voice at times‌. Which of the following ‌is true?","raw_text":"Two weeks after the death of her husband, a 60-year-old woman presented to your OPD with poor appetite, anger, and sadness. She also says she hears her husband's voice at times. Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal‌ grief","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Grief p‌sychosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ma‌nia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal grief","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal grief</strong></p><p>After the loss of a loved one, there is a <strong>normal grief</strong> reaction. This reaction also occurs with other losses, such as loss of a body part, miscarriage or abortion.</p>\n<p>Normal grief (bereavement) is characterized initially by <strong>shock and denial.</strong> The typical depressive symp­toms associated with bereavement include feelings of sadness, insomnia, diminished appetite, and in some cases, weight loss. It generally <strong>subsides within a year</strong>, although some features may continue longer.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Transient auditory hallucinations</strong> related to the <strong>deceased person</strong> occur in certain cases of <strong>normal grief reaction</strong> and is not considered as psychosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0933","text":"Persistent complex bereave‌ment disorder is consider‍ed when the grief reactio‍n persists for more than ‌_____.","raw_text":"Persistent complex bereavement disorder is considered when the grief reaction persists for more than _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 m‌onths","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12 months</strong></p><p>According to DSM-5, <strong>persistent complex bereavement disorder</strong> is diagnosed only if <strong>at least 12 months</strong> have elapsed since the death of an individual having a close relationship in the case of bereaved <strong>adults</strong> and <strong>6 months </strong>for bereaved <strong>children.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0934","text":"A 21-year-old woman experi‌ences intense emotions ea‍ch year on the same day o‍f her mother's demise. Th‌is is a subtype of which ‍of the following diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old woman experiences intense emotions each year on the same day of her mother's demise. This is a subtype of which of the following diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adjustmen‌t disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute stre‌ss reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grief r‌eaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Post traumatic stress diso‌rder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Grief reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Grief reaction</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anniversary reaction</strong> which is a subtype of <strong>grief reaction</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the trigger for an acute grief reaction is a <strong>special occasion</strong>, such as a holiday or birthday, the rekindled grief is called an <strong>anniversary reaction. </strong>This may occur each year on the <strong>same day the person died</strong> or when the bereaved individual becomes the same age the deceased person was at the time of death. These anniversary reactions tend to become relatively mild and brief overtime.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0945","text":"As a first-year psychiatry‌ resident, you are counse‍lling a 33-year-old woman‍ who recently lost her mo‌ther to COVID-19. Accordi‍ng to Kubler Ross, which ‌of the following correctl‌y lists the stages of gri‌ef she will experience?","raw_text":"As a first-year psychiatry resident, you are counselling a 33-year-old woman who recently lost her mother to COVID-19. According to Kubler Ross, which of the following correctly lists the stages of grief she will experience?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Denial - Depression - Barg‌aining - Anger - Acceptan‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anger - Bargaining - Denia‌l - Depression - Acceptan‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denial - Anger - Depressio‌n - Bargaining - Acceptan‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Denial - Anger - Bargainin‌g - Depression - Acceptan‍ce","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Denial - Anger - Bargaining - Depression - Acceptance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Denial - Anger - Bargaining - Depression - Acceptance</strong></p><p><strong>Denial - Anger - Bargaining - Depression - Acceptance </strong>is the correct order of stages of grief, as proposed by Kubler Ross. </p>\n<p><strong>Elisabeth Kubler-Ross</strong>, a psychiatrist and thanatologist, made an organization of reactions to impending death.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4450","text":"A 23-year-old college drop‌out was referred to the i‍npatient psychiatry unit ‍due to severe social with‌drawal. Following the dea‍th of his father a year b‌ack, he felt isolated and‌ around the same period, ‌he started playing comput​er games for almost 10 to​ 12 hours each day. When ‌not gaming, he becomes ir​ritable, vindictive, and ​verbally aggressive. Whic‍h of the following is tru‌e regarding this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old college dropout was referred to the inpatient psychiatry unit due to severe social withdrawal. Following the death of his father a year back, he felt isolated and around the same period, he started playing computer games for almost 10 to 12 hours each day. When not gaming, he becomes irritable, vindictive, and verbally aggressive. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It exhibits somatisation, ‌anxiety and sleep disturb‍ances in the absence of d‍epressive symptoms.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"WHO has classified gaming ‌addiction as a mental hea‍lth disorder in Internati‍onal Classification of Di‌seases 10.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"For its diagnosis, a behav‌iour pattern must be of s‍ufficient severity to res‍ult in significant impair‌ment for at least 12 mont‍hs.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gaming behaviours are not ‌seen as specific forms of‍ maladaptive self-regulat‍ory strategies in youths ‌with attachment issues.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. For its diagnosis, a behaviour pattern must be of sufficient severity to result in significant impairment for at least 12 months.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. For its diagnosis, a behaviour pattern must be of sufficient severity to result in significant impairment for at least 12 months.</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>i</strong><strong>nternet gaming disorder (IGD)</strong>. It involves a pattern of persistent or recurrent gaming behaviour that becomes so extensive it takes precedence over other life interests.</p>\n<p>Abnormal gaming behaviour should be in evidence for over a period of <strong>at least 12 months </strong>for a diagnosis to be assigned but the period might be shortened if symptoms are severe. Gaming behaviours are seen as specific forms of <strong>maladaptive self-regulatory strategies</strong> in youths with <strong>attachment issues.</strong></p>\n<p>It has three main characteristics:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Gaming </strong>behaviour <strong>takes precedence</strong> over other activities to the extent that other activities are pushed to the periphery.</li>\n<li>It leads to<strong> significant distress and impairment </strong>in personal, familial, social, educational or occupational functioning. </li>\n<li>It can lead to disturbed sleep patterns, diet problems and deficiency in physical activities.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Impaired control</strong> over gaming (frequency, intensity, duration)</li>\n<li>Increased <strong>priority</strong> is given to gaming</li>\n<li><strong>Continuation</strong> or escalation of gaming <strong>despite negative consequences</strong></li>\n<li>Depressive symptoms, somatisation and anxiety, including behavioural changes and sleep disturbances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qPn49Djg","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"17_esophagus___gerd___carcinoma","title":"17.Esophagus   Gerd & Carcinoma","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB7013","text":"What is the most important‌ pathophysiological facto​r implicated in GERD?","raw_text":"What is the most important pathophysiological factor implicated in GERD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hiatus‌ hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frequent transient LES rel‌axation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"LES hyp‌otension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delayed gast‌ric emptying","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frequent transient LES relaxation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frequent transient LES relaxation</strong></p><p><strong>Frequent </strong>transient lower esophageal sphincter (LES) <strong>relaxation </strong>accounts for <strong>more than 90%</strong> of the cases of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</p>\n<p><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease</strong> (GERD) is a condition that develops when there is a <strong>retrograde flow</strong> of<strong> stomach contents</strong> back into the esophagus. It can present as non-erosive reflux disease or erosive esophagitis.</p>\n<p>A <strong>hiatal hernia</strong> is the next common cause of GERD. Factors contributing to the high pressure in the LES zone:</p>\n<p>Factors contributing to the high pressure in the LES zone: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intrinsic musculature</strong> of the distal esophagus and sling fibers of the cardia of stomach which are in tonic contraction</li>\n<li>During inspiration, the <strong>anterposterior diameter</strong> of the crural opening of <strong>diaphragm is decreased</strong> (increases LES pressure)</li>\n<li><strong>Transmitted pressure</strong> of the abdominal cavity on the LES zone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7025","text":"Which of the following foo‌ds will you recommend to ​a patient to reduce sympt‍oms of GERD?","raw_text":"Which of the following foods will you recommend to a patient to reduce symptoms of GERD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cof‌fee","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protein ‌rich meal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High fat na‌tural cheese","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peppe‌rmint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Protein rich meal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Protein rich meal</strong></p><p><strong>Protein increases </strong>the lower esophageal sphincter <strong>(LES) pressure, </strong>thereby allowing the closure of the sphincter and <strong>reducing reflux</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Chocolate and<strong> coffee </strong>both contain <strong>methylxanthine</strong>, which causes<strong> muscles to relax </strong>and lowers the LES pressure. In addition, <strong>coffee </strong>can <strong>stimulate gastric secretions</strong>, which can promote reflux. </p>\n<p>Option C:  <strong>Fatty foods</strong> have been found to <strong>delay gastric emptying</strong> and <strong>decrease the LES pressure</strong>, thereby prolonging the esophageal exposure time to the stomach acid and the volume available for reflux.</p>\n<p>Option D: Alcohol<strong>,</strong> <strong>mint</strong>, citrus, tomatoes, and/or carbonated beverages may cause further <strong>irritation </strong>to the<strong> damaged esophageal mucosa</strong> but have not been found to cause reflux themselves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1317","text":"A 45-year-old male patient‌ presented with a feeling​ of fullness and sensatio‍n of a lump in the throat​ for the past 3 weeks. He​ has no other complaints ‍like weight loss or dysph​agia. Rigid endoscopy sho‌wed no growth or foreign ​body in the throat. Which‌ of the following stateme‍nts is false regarding th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male patient presented with a feeling of fullness and sensation of a lump in the throat for the past 3 weeks. He has no other complaints like weight loss or dysphagia. Rigid endoscopy showed no growth or foreign body in the throat. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lump in the throat only fe‌lt during swallowing","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Association ‌with anxiety","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Does not interfere with ea‌ting or drinking","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Often attributable to GERD‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lump in the throat only felt during swallowing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lump in the throat only felt during swallowing</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 5379\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>globus hystericus. </strong>It is a <strong>feeling of fullness</strong> or lump in the throat that is<strong> felt always</strong> and not only when swallowing<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Globus hystericus or globus pharyngeus is a<strong> persistent abnormal sensation</strong> in the <strong>throat</strong> in the <strong>absence</strong> of any observable <strong>pathology</strong>. The exact cause of globus sensation is uncertain. The <strong>sensation</strong> is often <strong>relieved</strong> <strong>by</strong> the act of<strong> swallowing</strong> and it does not interfere with eating and drinking.</p>\n<p>Examination and investigations may be carried out to exclude any other underlying problem. It is often <strong>attributable</strong> to <strong>GERD</strong>. Reassurance that there is no serious underlying problem may be all that is needed in terms of treatment.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<nav class=\"subnav u-mb--l u-mt--l rs_preserve rs_skip rs_preserve rs_skip\" data-behaviour=\"jump\" data-header-push=\"\" id=\"subnav\"></nav>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7014","text":"A 67-year-old man presente‌d to the physician with c​omplaints of heartburn an‍d a sour taste in the mou​th for the past 1 year. H​e was prescribed proton p‍ump inhibitors, but the s​ymptoms did not improve. ‌What is the immediate nex​t step in the management ‌of this patient?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old man presented to the physician with complaints of heartburn and a sour taste in the mouth for the past 1 year. He was prescribed proton pump inhibitors, but the symptoms did not improve. What is the immediate next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo‌scopy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"24-hour pH‌ recording","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal‌ manometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H.pylori ant‌ibody testing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endoscopy</strong></p><p>This is a case of <strong>dyspepsia</strong> not responding to PPIs. So the best management for this patient is <strong>endoscopy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In case of an <strong>unexplained dyspepsia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If the patient is <strong>&gt;60</strong> <strong>years</strong> - <strong>Endoscopy</strong> is the investigation of choice </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If the patient is <strong>&lt;60 years</strong> - A <strong>test for H.pylori infection</strong> is done (stool antigen and urease breath test) and eradication treatment is given. <strong>Endoscopy</strong> is done only if any one of the alarming signs are present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Alarming</strong> <strong>signs</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Odynophagia or dysphagia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unexplained weight loss</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Massive GI bleeding</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Recurrent vomiting</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Jaundice</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Palpable mass or adenopathy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Family history of GI malignancy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6980","text":"A 48-year-old woman presen‌ted with dysphagia and po​stprandial chest pain tha‍t is relieved by loud bel​ching. On examination, th​e patient had pallor. Che‍st radiography showed an ​intrathoracic gas bubble.‌ What is the investigatio​n of choice for this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman presented with dysphagia and postprandial chest pain that is relieved by loud belching. On examination, the patient had pallor. Chest radiography showed an intrathoracic gas bubble. What is the investigation of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barium meal follow-through‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barium meal upper GI serie‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT scan with oral contrast‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Upper GI ‌endoscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CT scan with oral contrast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0cd2b673915041f98c1811f84cb86d49x1280x3037.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db72a5ba301243cd89cd21318ba2511fx678x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CT scan with oral contrast</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with X-ray findings showing a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">retrocardiac gas bubble</strong> are consistent with the diagnosis of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> </strong>a<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> hiatus hernia</strong>. A<strong> CT scan </strong>with <strong>oral contrast</strong> is the <strong>best method</strong> of diagnosis. It highlights the gastric anatomy but also helps to identify other structures involved in the hiatal hernia.</p>\n<p><strong>Hiatus hernia</strong> is the<strong> protrusion</strong> of any <strong>abdominal structure</strong> other than the esophagus into the thoracic cavity through a widening of the hiatus of the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>Types of hiatus hernia:</p>\n<p>Type 1- sliding hernia</p>\n<p><strong>Gastroesophageal junction or cardia</strong> <strong>migrates </strong>into the thoracic cavity. The <strong>length of intra abdominal esophagus is reduced</strong> leading to GERD. Most of the patients are asymptomatic. The treatment of choice in symptomatic patients is <strong>fundoplication</strong>.</p>\n<p>Type 2- rolling hiatus hernia:</p>\n<p>Gastroesophageal junction or cardia is normal but a <strong>portion</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong> <strong>migrates</strong> into the thoracic cavity. The <strong>length of the intra</strong><strong>abdominal</strong> <strong>esophagus</strong> remains the <strong>same</strong> in a true rolling hiatus hernia.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chest pain or fullness relieved on belching.</li>\n<li>Dysphagia.</li>\n<li>Longitudinal ulcers (<strong>Cameron’s</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong>) that <strong>bleed</strong> lead to <strong>anemia.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surgical reduction </strong>of hernia and<strong> tightening of diaphragmatic crura </strong>is the <strong>best treatment </strong>modality even in asymptomatic patients<strong> to avoid </strong>life-threatening<strong> complications like volvulus.</strong></p>\n<p>Type 3- mixed hiatus hernia:</p>\n<p>A combination of sliding and rolling hiatus hernia. Treatment is the same as rolling hiatus hernia.</p>\n<p>Type 4 hiatus hernia:</p>\n<p><strong>Other contents of GIT herniates</strong> into the thoracic cavity. Eg: Colon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: This is a controversial question as some books give barium swallow as the investigation of choice for hiatal hernia. But Bailey's 28th edition clearly mentions that a CT scan with oral contrast is the answer.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a retrocardiac gas bubble on an X-ray that is consistent with hiatus hernia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7012","text":"What is the most common co‌mplication of Nissen's fu​ndoplication?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication of Nissen's fundoplication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophage‌al injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gas-bloat‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gas-bloat syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53d7360fbd80481088439f2683f851b2x1280x1892.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gas-bloat syndrome</strong></p><p>The most common complication of Nissen’s fundoplication is<strong> gas-bloat syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Fundoplication is the procedure in which the <strong>fundus </strong>of the stomach is<strong> wrapped around </strong>the <strong>lower </strong>end of the<strong> esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>After this procedure, most patients are <strong>unable to belch gas</strong> <strong>from</strong> the <strong>stomach into </strong>the<strong> esophagus</strong>. This causes increased gas in the stomach resulting in bloating sensation, abdominal discomfort, and flatulence. This is known as <strong>gas bloat syndrome </strong>and occurs more commonly with Nissen total fundoplication.</p>\n<p><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> is the <strong>most common complication</strong> that occurs<strong> intraoperatively</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7011","text":"The fundus is wrapped (270‌ degrees) around the post​erior aspect of the esoph‍agus in which of the foll​owing corrective procedur​es for GERD?","raw_text":"The fundus is wrapped (270 degrees) around the posterior aspect of the esophagus in which of the following corrective procedures for GERD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nissen fun‌doplication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toupet fun‌doplication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Watson fun‌doplication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dor fundo‌plication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Toupet fundoplication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d7539c133fb45cc8d6372bf21d3171fx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Toupet fundoplication</strong></p><p><strong>Partial posterior fundoplication </strong>between<strong> 180 to 270 degrees</strong><strong> </strong>is called <strong>Toupet fundoplication.</strong></p>\n<p>It is used in the surgical management of GERD. <strong>Partial anterior fundoplication</strong> is <strong>Dor’s</strong> fundoplication and <strong>total fundoplication</strong> is <strong>Nissen’s</strong> fundoplication.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7017","text":"What is the most common be‌nign tumor of the esophag​us?","raw_text":"What is the most common benign tumor of the esophagus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leio‌myoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ade‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ‌papillomas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heman‌gioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leiomyoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c11edc272fc64ca89d7082babe7e92bdx172x158.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leiomyoma</strong></p><p><strong>Leiomyomas</strong> are the <strong>most common benign</strong> <strong>tumors</strong> of the<strong> esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Leiomyomas are benign tumors that arise from the <strong>muscularis propria. </strong>They are found in the <strong>mid </strong>to<strong> distal esophagus.</strong></p>\n<p>They are more<strong> common</strong> in <strong>males</strong> and small, asymptomatic lesions are observed, while symptomatic lesions are enucleated.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7016","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for dev​eloping squamous cell car‍cinoma of the esophagus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for developing squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plummer- Vin‌son syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ingestion of alkali/causti‌c substances","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Human papi‌llomavirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Barrett's‌ esophagus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Barrett's esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Barrett&#x27;s esophagus</strong></p><p><strong>Barrett’s esophagus</strong> is a risk factor for <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong> of the lower end of the esophagus, <strong>not</strong> for <strong>squamous cell carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of esophageal carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for<strong> SCC:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tobacco</li>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>HPV</li>\n<li>Plummer–Vinson syndrome</li>\n<li>Achalasia</li>\n<li>Tylosis</li>\n<li>Caustic ingestion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Risk factors for<strong> adenocarcinoma</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Barrett's esophagus</li>\n<li>GERD</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7322","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about Barrett's ​esophagus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about Barrett's esophagus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the intestinal metap‌lasia of esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucus secreting foveolar c‌ells are characteristic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alician blue is used in th‌e staining of biopsy spec​imen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a reversible cell ad‌aptation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucus secreting foveolar cells are characteristic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/814aaf9503854aeab6979161c346c4dfx1279x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e940eb827c404b2e896e090eb1faebfdx1279x1702.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucus secreting foveolar cells are characteristic</strong></p><p>Mucus-secreting <strong>goblet cells </strong>(not foveolar cells) are characteristic of <strong>Barrett's esophagus</strong>, as there is intestinal metaplasia of the esophagus. <strong>Foveolar cells</strong> are the mucus-secreting cells in the <strong>stomach.</strong></p>\n<p>Barrett's esophagus is a <strong>complication</strong> of<strong> chronic gastroesophageal reflux disease </strong>(GERD), and it is<strong> </strong>estimated to occur in about 10% of individuals with symptomatic GERD. Symptoms include heartburn, regurgitation and sometimes dysphagia. <strong>Obese white men</strong> over the age of<strong> 60 years</strong> represent the <strong>highest-risk group.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I</strong><strong>ntestinal metaplasia</strong> in the<strong> esophagus</strong> occurs due to chronic irritation of the esophagus in GERD (option A) since intestinal epithelium is better suited to handling gastric acid exposure. Hence, <strong>mucus-secreting goblet cells</strong> are<strong> characteristic</strong> of Barrett's esophagus. Since all metaplasia are reversible, <strong>Barrett's esophagus </strong>is also<strong> reversible </strong>(option D). Only 0.2 to 1% of Barrett's esophagus leads to dysplasia and adenocarcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Alician blue</strong> is used in the<strong> staining </strong>of the<strong> biopsy</strong> specimen, as it stains the goblet cells (option C). <strong>Mucicarmine </strong>is the<strong> specific stain.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Seattle protocol</strong> is used for the management of Barrett's esophagus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF3985","text":"Investigation of choice fo‌r cardia metaplasia in Ba​rrett's esophagus is","raw_text":"Investigation of choice for cardia metaplasia in Barrett's esophagus is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopic with lugol's io‌dine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoscopy with methylene b‌lue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endoscopy with oral contra‌st","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoscopic ultrasonography‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoscopy with methylene blue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/129e6f353979429787ff685c25a5be09x1279x1702.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoscopy with methylene blue</strong></p><p>The investigation of choice for Barrett's esophagus showing cardia metaplasia is <strong>endoscopy</strong> with <strong>methylene blue</strong>.</p>\n<p>Barrett's esophagus is a <strong>metaplastic change </strong>(squamous to columnar change) in the<strong> lower end </strong>of the<strong> esophagus </strong>as a result of <strong>long-standing gastroesophageal reflux disease</strong>.</p>\n<p> Barrett’s esophagus can be-</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Classical</strong> Barrett's esophagus - &gt; 3cm of columnar epithelium in the distal esophagus.</li>\n<li><strong>Short</strong> <strong>segment</strong>  Barrett's esophagus - &lt; 3cm of columnar epithelium in the distal esophagus.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardia metaplasia</strong> - microscopic Barrett’s in the GE junction. In cardia metaplasia due to lack of macroscopic changes, an endoscopic biopsy is difficult. So <strong>chromoendoscopy</strong> is the ideal investigation. Here mucosa is first stained by methylene blue to <strong>highlight</strong> <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>mucosa</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigation of choice: <strong>endoscopic</strong> <strong>biopsy</strong></p>\n<p>Management: <strong>Seattle</strong> <strong>protocol</strong> is ideally followed to detect <strong>early dysplastic changes</strong> in Barrett's esophagus. Here a <strong>4 quadrant biopsy</strong> specimen is taken 1- 2 cm apart throughout the area of intestinal metaplasia.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No signs of dysplasia - continue 2 yearly surveillance. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Low-grade dysplasia - APC or RFA or EMR followed by 6 monthly surveillance.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">High-grade dysplasia - APC or RFA or EMR followed by 3 monthly surveillance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>APC - Argon photo coagulation</p>\n<p>RFA - Radiofrequency ablation</p>\n<p>EMR - Endoscopic mucosal resection</p>\n<p><strong>Lugol’s iodine</strong> is used for squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2502","text":"Endoscopic biopsy of a pat‌ient with GERD revealed h​igh-grade dysplasia of es‍ophageal mucosa proximal ​to the GE junction. What ​is the appropriate manage‍ment for this patient?","raw_text":"Endoscopic biopsy of a patient with GERD revealed high-grade dysplasia of esophageal mucosa proximal to the GE junction. What is the appropriate management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radiofrequency ablation an‌d 6 monthly follow up","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No intervention now and fo‌llow up every 2 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argon photocoagulation and‌ 3 monthly follow up","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laparoscopic esophagectomy‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Argon photocoagulation and 3 monthly follow up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c69ad46adab4283956cd840759d33f9x1280x1965.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Argon photocoagulation and 3 monthly follow up</strong></p><p><strong>Ablation</strong> of abnormal mucosa and <strong>3 monthly</strong> endoscopic protocol is the appropriate management in cases of <strong>high-grade</strong> dysplasia of esophageal mucosa due to GERD.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7036","text":"An 80-year-old male patien‌t was recently diagnosed ​with adenocarcinoma of th‍e esophagus. The tumor is​ present in the distal es​ophagus, within 1 to 5 cm‍ above the gastroesophage​al junction. According to‌ the Siewert classificati​on, which type does this ‌tumor belong to?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old male patient was recently diagnosed with adenocarcinoma of the esophagus. The tumor is present in the distal esophagus, within 1 to 5 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. According to the Siewert classification, which type does this tumor belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25a27b943ff54526af88f96076e44591x1279x1588.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type I</strong></p><p><strong>Siewert type I lesions</strong> include tumors <strong>of the distal esophagus with the center</strong> located within <strong>1 - 5 cm above</strong> the anatomic esophagogastric junction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7018","text":"An elderly male presented ‌with significant weight l​oss and an inability to s‍wallow food. A barium swa​llow was done and showed ​the following. Which of t‍he following statements i​s false regarding this co‌ndition?","raw_text":"An elderly male presented with significant weight loss and an inability to swallow food. A barium swallow was done and showed the following. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysphagia is the most comm‌on symptom","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T1a tumor is restricted to‌ sub mucosa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barium swallow shows rat t‌ail appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexible endoscopy with bi‌opsy is used to confirm d​iagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T1a tumor is restricted to sub mucosa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d03319e0c1543058040f7d06e919dd8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T1a tumor is restricted to sub mucosa</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario with <strong>rat-tail appearance</strong> on barium swallow is suggestive of <strong>esophageal carcinoma. </strong>T1a tumor is <strong>restricted</strong> to the <strong>muscularis mucosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Esophageal carcinoma is <strong>more commonly</strong> seen in <strong>men</strong>. <strong>Tobacco</strong> use and <strong>alcohol</strong> are <strong>strong risk factors</strong>. <strong>Adenocarcinoma</strong> and <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> of the esophagus are the two <strong>most common</strong> types. </p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dysphagia (most common)</li>\n<li>Regurgitation</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Odynophagia</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the advanced stage, there can be hoarseness of voice due to recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, horner’s syndrome, chronic spinal pain, and diaphragmatic paralysis.</p>\n<p> <strong>Flexible endoscopy with biopsy is preferred for confirmation of diagnosis. </strong>Other Investigations include <strong>barium swallow</strong> which shows a <strong>rat-tail appearance</strong>  due to irregular narrowing or ulceration. Contrast-enhanced CT and PET can be used to assess distant metastasis. <strong>Endoscopic</strong> <strong>ultrasonography</strong> (<strong>EUS</strong>) is the investigation of choice to <strong>assess tumor invasion and lymph node metastasis.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>TNM staging:</strong></p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Primary Tumor (T)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>TX</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tumor cannot be assessed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>T0</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>No evidence of tumor</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tis</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>High-grade dysplasia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>T1</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Tumor invades the muscularis mucosa (T1a) </strong>or submucosa (T1b)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>T2</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tumor invades into but not beyond the muscularis propria</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>T3</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tumor invades the adventitia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>T4a</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tumor invades adjacent structures that are usually resectable(diaphragm and pericardium)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>T4b</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tumor invades unresectable structures</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Regional Lymph Nodes (N)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>NX</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Regional lymph nodes cannot be assessed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>N0</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>No regional lymph node metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>N1</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Metastasis in 1-2 regional lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>N2</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Metastasis in 3-6 regional lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>N3</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Metastasis in ≥7 regional lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Distant Metastasis (M)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>M0</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>No distant metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>M1</p>\n</td>\n<td valign=\"top\">\n<p>Distant metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7028","text":"What is the blood supply o‌f the conduit in gastric ​pull up done during esoph‍agectomy?","raw_text":"What is the blood supply of the conduit in gastric pull up done during esophagectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right gastric and right ga‌stroepiploic arteries","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right gastric and left gas‌troepiploic arteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left gastric and right gas‌troepiploic arteries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left gastric and left gast‌roepiploic arteries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right gastric and right gastroepiploic arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a82ad005edf425282d5019248ae9d25x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right gastric and right gastroepiploic arteries</strong></p><p>The <strong>blood supply</strong> to the<strong> gastric conduit</strong> is based on the <strong>right gastric</strong> and <strong>right gastroepiploic arteries</strong>.</p>\n<p>A<strong> gastric pull-up</strong> is done during <strong>esophagectomy</strong> for <strong>esophageal carcinoma</strong>. It is a surgical procedure where the <strong>stomach</strong> is <strong>pulled up</strong> into the thoracic cavity that now acts as a new passage (conduit) instead of the esophagus. </p>\n<p>During the esophagectomy,  the <strong>proximal stomach</strong> and <strong>lymph nodes</strong> are typically<strong> resected</strong> along with the segment of the esophagus. The gastric conduit is then fashioned by <strong>tubularization</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong>. The <strong>right gastroepiploic artery</strong> is <strong>preserved</strong> and the other arteries are ligated to make ample length and mobility for the conduit. </p>\n<p>It is of utmost importance the right gastroepiploic artery is preserved as ischemia to the stomach will result in anastomotic leakage, stricture, and even dehiscence.  The rich supply of submucosal vascular networks can preserve the stomach on this single vessel. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Approaches of esophagectomy:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Mckeown approach</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Done for <strong>upper 1/3rd</strong> and<strong> mid 1/3rd</strong> tumors (above aortic arch)</li>\n<li>Anastomosis site is in the neck</li>\n<li>Three incisions made (abdominal, thoracic, and left neck)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Transhiatal approach </strong>(Orringer)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Done for tumor of <strong>lower 1/3rd</strong> and <strong>mid 1/3rd</strong> of the esophagus </li>\n<li>Anastomosis site is in the neck</li>\n<li>The most common<strong> complication</strong> is <strong>atelectasis</strong> and <strong>mortality</strong> is due to an <strong>anastomotic leak</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Ivor lewis approach</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Done for <strong>mid 1/3rd</strong>(below aortic arch) and <strong>lower 1/3rd</strong> esophagus tumors</li>\n<li>Anastomosis site is in the thorax </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7030","text":"A 50-year-old man is diagn‌osed with esophageal carc​inoma. Radiological inves‍tigations show a 2 cm tum​or in the middle part of ​the esophagus. The tumor ‍extends up to the submuco​sa with the involvement o‌f mediastinal lymph nodes​, there is no distant met‌astasis. What is the best‍ treatment modality in hi‍s case?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man is diagnosed with esophageal carcinoma. Radiological investigations show a 2 cm tumor in the middle part of the esophagus. The tumor extends up to the submucosa with the involvement of mediastinal lymph nodes, there is no distant metastasis. What is the best treatment modality in his case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopic mucosal resecti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemoradiation followed by‌ esophagectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal stent placement‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chemoradiation followed by esophagectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chemoradiation followed by esophagectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Chemoradiation followed by esophagectomy</strong> is the best treatment modality for tumors <strong>extending to the submucosa (T1b) with regional lymph node involvement (N1).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>EMR</strong> (endoscopic mucosal resection) is done for <strong>small tumors confined to the mucosa</strong>, with <strong>no lymph node</strong> involvement. (<strong>T1aN0</strong>)</p>\n<p><strong>Esophagectomy ± chemoradiation</strong> is done when the <strong>tumor extends into the submucosa, with no lymph node</strong> involvement.</p>\n<p><strong>Chemoradiation followed by esophagectomy</strong> is done for <strong>tumors extending into the submucosa</strong> <strong>or beyond with lymph node</strong> enlargement</p>\n<p><strong>Chemoradiation</strong> therapy alone is done as a <strong>palliative</strong> measure in case of <strong>advanced</strong> esophageal carcinoma or in those patients <strong>unfit</strong> for surgery.</p>\n<p><strong>Esophageal</strong> <strong>stent</strong> <strong>placement</strong> is done in patients with advanced cancer and severe dysphagia that is not resolved by chemoradiation therapy. <strong>SEMS (self-expanding metallic stent)</strong> is the treatment of choice in <strong>malignant</strong> <strong>tracheoesophageal</strong> <strong>fistula</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF3982","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen‌ted with a history of dys​phagia and weight loss fo‍r the past 1 year. Endosc​opic ultrasonography done​ showed a tumor of size 4‍ cm in the mid esophagus ​above the aortic arch. Wh‌ich of the following is t​he best approach?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presented with a history of dysphagia and weight loss for the past 1 year. Endoscopic ultrasonography done showed a tumor of size 4 cm in the mid esophagus above the aortic arch. Which of the following is the best approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mckeown’s e‌sophagectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ivor Lewis e‌sophatectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orringer’s e‌sophagectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transhiatal ‌esophagectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mckeown’s esophagectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mckeown’s esophagectomy</strong></p><p>The best surgical approach for a <strong>mid-esophageal carcinoma above the aortic arch</strong> is  <strong>Mckeown’s</strong> <strong>esophagectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p> Approaches of esophagectomy:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Mckeown's approach</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Done for <strong>upper 1/3rd</strong> and<strong> mid 1/3rd</strong> tumors (above aortic arch)</li>\n<li> Anastomosis site is in the neck</li>\n<li>Three incisions were made (abdominal, thoracic, and left neck)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Transhiatal approach </strong>(Orringer's approach)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Done for tumor of <strong>lower 1/3rd</strong> and <strong>mid 1/3rd</strong> of the esophagus </li>\n<li>Anastomosis site is in the neck</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Ivor Lewis approach</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Done for <strong>mid 1/3rd</strong>(below aortic arch) and <strong>lower 1/3rd</strong> esophagus tumors</li>\n<li>Anastomosis site is in the thorax </li>\n<li>The most common<strong> complication</strong> is <strong>atelectasis</strong> </li>\n<li>The most common cause of <strong>mortality</strong> is due to an <strong>anastomotic leak </strong>leading to<strong> mediastinitis.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In esophagectomy, a <strong>proximal margin of 10cm</strong> and a <strong>distal margin of 5cm</strong> is taken<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7027","text":"An male patient with stage‌ IV esophageal carcinoma ​is taken up for esophagea‍l stent insertion as a pa​lliative measure. Which o​f the following is the mo‍st common complication of​ this procedure?","raw_text":"An male patient with stage IV esophageal carcinoma is taken up for esophageal stent insertion as a palliative measure. Which of the following is the most common complication of this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe‌ reflux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perfo‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Migr‌ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Odyno‌phagia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Migration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebcf979429e14b6d867389a2b466c38cx1280x704.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Migration</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common complication</strong> of placing a<strong> stent</strong> in the esophagus is the <strong>migration </strong>of the stent.</p>\n<p><strong>Palliative treatment</strong> in advanced stages of esophageal carcinoma includes esophageal stent placement, enteral nutrition support, or the use of chemotherapy and radiation.</p>\n<p>Complications of esophageal stent placement:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Migration of stent (most common)</strong></li>\n<li>Odynophagia</li>\n<li>Tumour ingrowth and overgrowth</li>\n<li>Chest pain</li>\n<li>Severe reflux</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5948","text":"AIMS65 score is used to pr‌edict mortality rate in p​atients with which of the‍ following?","raw_text":"AIMS65 score is used to predict mortality rate in patients with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper G‌I bleed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower G‌I bleed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinoma‌ esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinom‌a Rectum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Upper GI bleed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Upper GI bleed</strong></p><p><strong>AIMS65 score</strong> is a simple risk score that predicts the mortality rate in patients with <strong>upper GI haemorrhage.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 257px; width: 281px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 271px; text-align: center;\"><strong>AIMS65 score</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Variable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">Score</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">\n<p><strong>A</strong>lbumin &lt;3 g/dl</p>\n<p><strong>I</strong>NR &gt;1.5</p>\n<p>Altered <strong>M</strong>ental status</p>\n<p><strong>S</strong>ystolic BP &lt;90mmHg</p>\n<p>Age&gt;<strong>65</strong> years</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px;\">\n<p>1</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 271px;\">Score<strong> &gt;2</strong> considered <strong>high risk</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iE4q7E7H","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"18_local_anaesthetics___general_properties","title":"18.Local Anaesthetics   General Properties","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5950","text":"The main mechanism of acti‍on of lignocaine is throu​gh inhibition of ________‍___.","raw_text":"The main mechanism of action of lignocaine is through inhibition of ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resting, voltage-gated Na ‍channel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivated, voltage-gated‍ Na channel","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Resting, ligand-gated Na c‍hannel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inactivated, ligand-gated ‍Na channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inactivated, voltage-gated Na channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inactivated, voltage-gated Na channel</strong></p><p>The main mechanism of action of local anesthetics is through <strong>inhibition</strong> of<strong> inactivated, voltage-gated Na channel.</strong></p>\n<p>Local anesthesia involves the abolition of local sensation through blockade of nerve impulses.</p>\n<p>Na channels exist in 3 states:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Resting (non-conducting)</li>\n<li>Open (conducting)</li>\n<li>Inactivated (non conducting)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Local anesthetics have a <strong>greater affinity </strong>for the channel in the<strong> open or inactivated state</strong> than in the resting state. They bind to the Na channel from the inner aspect in cationic form.</p>\n<p>They bind to the α subunit of Na channel and inhibit the conformational changes associated with channel opening, thereby preventing Na influx. When a considerable number of Na channels are inactivated, the action potential cannot be generated and impulse cannot be propagated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0440","text":"By what mechanism do local‍ anesthetics block nerve ​conduction?","raw_text":"By what mechanism do local anesthetics block nerve conduction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibiting the release of ‍neurotransmitters","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreasing the influx of s‍odium into the cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increasing the release of ‍inhibitory neurotransmitt​ers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibiting the efflux of s‍odium from neurons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreasing the influx of sodium into the cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreasing the influx of sodium into the cells</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Local anesthetics block nerve conduction by <strong>decreasing</strong> the<strong> influx</strong> of<strong> sodium</strong> ions into the cells. Normally, sodium influx is followed by slight depolarization of the membrane. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Local anesthetics in<strong> unionized </strong>form <strong>cross</strong> the cell<strong> membrane</strong> and are converted to<strong> ionized</strong> forms inside the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> the sodium channel blockade occurs. Resting/closed conformation of the sodium channels have relatively low affinity for local anesthetics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5952","text":"A patient has presented to‍ your OPD with a sebaceou​s cyst on the scalp. You ‍plan to excise it. Before‌ making the first incisio​n, you inject lidocaine a‍round it. Which of the fo‌llowing effects does this​ drug have? Inhibition of​ Na channel Potentiation ‍of K channel Interaction ​with NMDA receptors Inhib‍ition of Ca channel Inter‍ference with oxidative ph​osphorylation","raw_text":"<p>A patient has presented to your OPD with a sebaceous cyst on the scalp. You plan to excise it. Before making the first incision, you inject lidocaine around it. Which of the following effects does this drug have?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inhibition of Na channel</li>\n<li>Potentiation of K channel</li>\n<li>Interaction with NMDA receptors</li>\n<li>Inhibition of Ca channel</li>\n<li>Interference with oxidative phosphorylation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‍y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 &amp;‍amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;‍amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, 4‍ &amp;amp; 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>Local anesthetics <strong>inhibit</strong> <strong>potassium channels.</strong> </p>\n<p>The effects of local anesthetics include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of voltage-gated Na channels</li>\n<li>Blockade of potassium and calcium channels</li>\n<li>Transient receptor potential vanilloid 1 (TRPV1)</li>\n<li>Interaction with cholinergic or N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptors</li>\n<li>Interference with cellular metabolic processes (eg, oxidative phosphorylation, free fatty acid utilization)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, the <strong>main action</strong> of local anesthetics is mediated by <strong>inhibition of voltage-gated Na channels</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5954","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an amino ester?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an amino ester?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc‍aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proc‍aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorop‍rocaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lidocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Lidocaine</strong> (lignocaine, xylocaine) is an amino-amide.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5970","text":"Which of the following loc‍al anaesthetics is not me​tabolised by plasma ester‍ase?","raw_text":"Which of the following local anaesthetics is not metabolised by plasma esterase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetra‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proc‍aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coc‍aine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arti‍caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Cocaine</strong> is the <strong>only</strong> amino ester that is not metabolized by plasma cholinesterases. It is partially metabolized by <strong>hepatic carboxylesterase</strong> and partially excreted unchanged in the urine.</p>\n<p><strong>Articaine</strong> is the only <strong>amino amide</strong> which is inactivated by <strong>plasma esterases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Cocaine</strong> is a <strong>vasoconstrictor </strong>due to<strong> </strong>its property of<strong> </strong>norepinephrine reuptake inhibition<strong>. </strong>All the other local anaesthetics are vasodilators except cocaine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5975","text":"In a patient undergoing su‍rgical fixation of a dist​al radius fracture, an ax‍illary approach brachial ‌block is administered for​ the added benefit of pro‍longed pain relief. Which‌ of the following drugs i​s least suited for this p​urpose?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing surgical fixation of a distal radius fracture, an axillary approach brachial block is administered for the added benefit of prolonged pain relief. Which of the following drugs is least suited for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupiv‍acaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ropiv‍acaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetra‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lidocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Lidocaine</strong> is an <strong>intermediate-acting</strong> local anesthetic. It is not preferred in procedures for prolonged pain relief. </p>\n<p><strong>Long-acting</strong> local anesthetics are <strong>preferred</strong> for <strong>prolonged pain relief.</strong></p>\n<p>Bupivacaine, ropivacaine, and tetracaine are all long-acting and thus can be used to administer the brachial plexus block in this case.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5994","text":"Arrange the following loca‍l anaesthetics in order o​f potency from most poten‍t to least. Procaine Bupi‌vacaine Mepivacaine","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following local anaesthetics in order of potency from most potent to least.</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Procaine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bupivacaine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mepivacaine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 &amp;gt; ‍1 &amp;gt; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;gt; ‍3 &amp;gt; 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;gt; ‍3 &amp;gt; 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 &amp;gt; ‍1 &amp;gt; 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 > 3 > 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 &gt; 3 &gt; 1</strong></p><p>The most potent local anaesthetic among the following is<strong> bupivacaine</strong> followed by <strong>mepivacaine</strong> and then <strong>procaine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Potency depends on hydrophobicity. Hydrophobicity can be increased by the addition of alkyl groups to the molecule.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Dibucaine</strong> is the <strong>most potent</strong>, most toxic and longest acting local anaesthetic agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5996","text":"The local anesthetic with ‍maximum amount of unioniz​ed drug at physiological ‍pH is __________","raw_text":"The local anesthetic with maximum amount of unionized drug at physiological pH is __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupiv‍acaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorop‍rocaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prilo‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lidocaine</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> lidocaine</strong> has the lowest pKa value. Thus, it has the <strong>maximum</strong> amount of <strong>unionized</strong> drug at <strong>physiological pH.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the unionized form of local anesthetics that penetrate the membrane. Hence, <strong>pKa</strong> correlates with the <strong>onset of action</strong>. </p>\n<p>pKa of a drug is the pH at which the drug is 50% ionized and 50% nonionized.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">pKa values of Local Anesthetics</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\"> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chloroprocaine - 9.1</li>\n<li>Procaine - 8.9</li>\n<li>Tetracaine - 8.4</li>\n<li>Bupivacaine - 8.1</li>\n<li>Prilocaine - 8.0</li>\n<li>Etidocaine - 7.9</li>\n<li>Lidocaine - 7.8</li>\n<li>Mepivacaine - 7.7</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5973","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients should you observe ​most closely for signs of‍ local anesthesia toxicit‌y?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients should you observe most closely for signs of local anesthesia toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient who underwent hi‍p replacement under epidu​ral anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient who underwent ha‍nd surgery under brachial​ plexus block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient who underwent he‍rnioplasty under local an​esthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient who underwent IC‍D insertion under interco​stal nerve block","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient who underwent ICD insertion under intercostal nerve block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient who underwent ICD insertion under intercostal nerve block</strong></p><p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6003","text":"When asked to administer a‍ combination of lignocain​e and epinephrine for a m‍inor procedure under loca‌l anesthesia, what concen​tration of epinephrine wi‍ll you use?","raw_text":"When asked to administer a combination of lignocaine and epinephrine for a minor procedure under local anesthesia, what concentration of epinephrine will you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 1‍0,000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 :2‍0,000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 : 1,‍00, 000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 : 2,‍00,000","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 : 2,00,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 : 2,00,000</strong></p><p>Epinephrine is used along with local anesthetics at a concentration of <strong>5 μg/mL </strong>or <strong>1:200,000.</strong> At this dose, epinephrine provides <strong>adequate vasoconstriction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages of vasoconstrictors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased duration of action</li>\n<li>Improved depth of anesthesia</li>\n<li>Decreased local anesthetic toxicity</li>\n<li>As a marker for inadvertent intravascular injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6016","text":"You are administering lido‍caine to a patient before​ wound suturing. If you a‍dd adrenaline to it, what‌ would be the duration of​ action of the anaestheti‍c?","raw_text":"You are administering lidocaine to a patient before wound suturing. If you add adrenaline to it, what would be the duration of action of the anaesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 h‍our","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 h‍ours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 h‍ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 h‍ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 hours</strong></p><p>By adding adrenaline to lidocaine, the duration of action can be increased to 2 hours.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 210px;\" width=\"472\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Maximum dose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Duration of action</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Lignocaine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Plain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"> 4.5mg/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">30-60 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">With Adrenaline</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"> 7mg/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">120 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Bupivacaine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Plain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"> 3mg/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">120-140 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">With Adrenaline</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"> 3mg/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">180-240 min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0441","text":"While administering which ‍of the following blocks w​ill you never use a vasoc‍onstrictor-local anesthet‌ic combination?","raw_text":"While administering which of the following blocks will you never use a vasoconstrictor-local anesthetic combination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digita‍l block","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal‍ block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidura‍l block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intercostal‍ nerve block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digital block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digital block</strong></p><p>The use of a <strong>vasoconstrictors-local anesthetic</strong> combination is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>digital block</strong> (ring block). This is an area supplied by<strong> end arteries </strong>and there is a risk of<strong> ischemia </strong>and<strong> necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The combination of local anesthetic with a vasoconstrictor is contraindicated in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital nerve block (ring block)</li>\n<li>Toe block</li>\n<li>Surgery of the tip of the nose and pinna</li>\n<li>Surgery of the penis (circumcision)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6004","text":"While administering lidoca‍ine to a patient in the E​R, the anesthesia residen‍t adds sodium bicarbonate‌ to it before injecting. ​All of the following bene‍fits are achieved by this‌ except:","raw_text":"While administering lidocaine to a patient in the ER, the anesthesia resident adds sodium bicarbonate to it before injecting. All of the following benefits are achieved by this except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Faster onse‍t of action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced pain‍ on injection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Improved qua‍lity of block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced local anesthetic t‍oxicity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reduced local anesthetic toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reduced local anesthetic toxicity</strong></p><p>The addition of sodium bicarbonate to local anesthetics does<strong> not reduce</strong> local anesthetic <strong>toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>All local anesthetics are <strong>weak bases</strong>, but commercially available solutions have an <strong>acidic</strong> pH in order to <strong>maximize</strong> the <strong>stability</strong> and shelf life. In acidic solutions, most of the drug will be in <strong>ionized form</strong>. It is only the uncharged (non-ionized) form that diffuses across the lipid barrier to the site of action.</p>\n<p>A basic solution (<strong>sodium bicarbonate</strong>) can be added to a local anesthetic solution immediately before injection to <strong>raise</strong> the <strong>pH.</strong> This is termed '<strong>alkalinization</strong>' or 'buffering'. Alkalinization increases the proportion of <strong>non-ionized drug</strong> resulting in more <strong>rapid</strong> drug <strong>diffusion.</strong></p>\n<p>Advantages of alkalinization:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Faster onset of action</li>\n<li>Reduced pain/stinging on subcutaneous injection</li>\n<li>Improved quality of the block</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 284\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>1mL 8.4% sodium bicarbonate is added to 10 mL of local anesthetic solution. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Excessive sodium bicarbonate raises the pH too far and precipitates non-charged basic form in the solution. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6019","text":"Which local anaesthetic so‍lution causes maximum neu​rotoxicity on neuro-axial‍ placement?","raw_text":"Which local anaesthetic solution causes maximum neurotoxicity on neuro-axial placement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupiv‍acaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ropiv‍acaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prilo‍caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lidocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Neurotoxicity</strong> from neuro-axial (<strong>epidural</strong> or <strong>spinal</strong>) placement of local anesthetic solution is <strong>maximum</strong> with <strong>lidocaine.</strong></p>\n<p>Order of neurotoxicity:<strong> Lidocaine = Tetracaine &gt; Bupivacaine &gt; Ropivacaine.</strong></p>\n<p>Neurotoxicity may manifest as <strong>transient neurological symptoms </strong>or <strong>cauda equine syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6021","text":"Upon intramuscular injecti‍on of which of the follow​ing local anesthetics, is‍ maximum myotoxicity indu‌ced?","raw_text":"Upon intramuscular injection of which of the following local anesthetics, is maximum myotoxicity induced?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prilo‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proc‍aine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‍acaine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bupivacaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bupivacaine</strong></p><p><strong>Local anesthetic-induced myotoxicity</strong> following intramuscular injection is maximum with <strong>bupivacaine.</strong></p>\n<p>The mechanism of muscle damage is due to <strong>mitochondrial damage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Toxicity is <strong>reversible</strong> as it resolves within <strong>2 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6033","text":"A young male is brought to‍ the ER with uncoordinate​d movements and difficult‍y speaking. His friends t‌ell you that these sympto​ms started he ate a shell‍fish dish. He complained ‌of nausea and vomited als​o. Shellfish poisoning is​ suspected. Where does th‍is toxin act on?","raw_text":"A young male is brought to the ER with uncoordinated movements and difficulty speaking. His friends tell you that these symptoms started he ate a shellfish dish. He complained of nausea and vomited also. Shellfish poisoning is suspected. Where does this toxin act on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inner pore of voltage gate‍d sodium channel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Outer pore of voltage gate‍d sodium channel","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inner pore of ligand gated‍ sodium channel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Outer pore of ligand gated‍ sodium channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Outer pore of voltage gated sodium channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Outer pore of voltage gated sodium channel</strong></p><p>The manifestations of shellfish poisoning are due to the action of the poison on the <strong>outer pore </strong>of the <strong>voltage-gated sodium channel.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shellfish poisoning</strong> is due to the neurotoxin <strong>saxitoxin </strong>and <strong>puffer fish poisoning</strong> is due to <strong>tetrodotoxin</strong>. Although these toxins are chemically distinct, they are exceedingly potent and have similar mechanisms of action.</p>\n<p>While local anesthetics inhibit sodium channel activity by binding to the inner pore (from intracellular side), these <strong>toxins</strong> bind to the <strong>outer pore</strong> of the <strong>channel.</strong> As a result, the <strong>action potential</strong> is <strong>blocked.</strong></p>\n<p>Unlike local anesthetics, these neurotoxins are very specific to neuronal Na channels, hence <strong>cardiac</strong> and <strong>neurological</strong> side <strong>effects</strong> are <strong>low.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment<strong> </strong>includes gastric lavage, vasopressors. Mechanical ventilation may be required if respiratory paralysis sets in.</p>\n<p>Prognosis is<strong> </strong>good when the patient survives after 24 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6037","text":"A patient was being given ‍spinal anesthesia with bu​pivacaine when there was ‍an inadvertent intravascu‌lar injection. Following ​this, the patient develop‍ed an arrhythmia and went‌ into cardiac arrest. Wha​t should you treat this w​ith?","raw_text":"A patient was being given spinal anesthesia with bupivacaine when there was an inadvertent intravascular injection. Following this, the patient developed an arrhythmia and went into cardiac arrest. What should you treat this with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esm‍olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epine‍phrine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ligno‍caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium chan‍nel blockers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epinephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Bupivacaine</strong> toxicity resulting in <strong>cardiac arrest</strong> is treated with <strong>epinephrine.</strong></p>\n<p>If cardiac arrest occurs, the American Society of Regional Anesthesia (ASRA) recommends standard <strong>Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS)</strong> with the following modifications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>If epinephrine is used, small initial doses (10-100 μg boluses in adults) are preferable</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Vasopressin is not recommended</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Avoid calcium channel blockers and beta-blockers</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>If ventricular arrhythmias develop, amiodarone is preferable</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>20% Lipid emulsion therapy at 1.5ml/kg </strong>: Consider administering at the first signs of local anesthesia systemic toxicity (LAST), after airway management. (~100ml in adults)</p>\n<p>In bupivacaine toxicity unresponsive to lipid emulsion and vasopressor therapy, cardiopulmonary bypass may be considered. </p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Among anti-arrhythmic drugs, current data emphasizes <strong>amiodarone</strong> as the mainstay drug. The use of bretylium is no longer endorsed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6035","text":"Which nerve fibre is most ‍susceptible to local anes​thetic?","raw_text":"Which nerve fibre is most susceptible to local anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‍e C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A g‍amma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‍e B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A d‍elta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type B</strong></p><p>Nerve fibre <strong>most susceptible</strong> to local anaesthetic is <strong>type</strong> <strong>B</strong>. The order of susceptibility<strong> </strong>of nerve fibres to local anaesthetics is <strong>B &gt;A-γ &gt; A-δ &gt; A-β &gt; A-α &gt; C</strong><strong> </strong>i.e</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B fibres</strong> are the <strong>most susceptible/least resistant</strong>     </li>\n<li><strong>C fibres</strong> are the <strong>least susceptible/most resistant</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factors that increase the sensitivity of nerve fibres are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smaller diameter</li>\n<li>Unmyelinated nerves</li>\n<li>Anatomical and physiological factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*Note: Although theoretically, C fibres are supposed to be the most susceptible to local anaesthetics, practically it was found to be the least susceptible.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6074","text":"Which nerve fibers are mos‍t resistant to local anes​thetics?","raw_text":"Which nerve fibers are most resistant to local anesthetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‍e Aα","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‍e B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‍e Aβ","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‍e Aγ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type Aα","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type Aα</strong></p><p>Type<strong> Aα fibres</strong> are <strong>most resistant</strong> to local anaesthetics, among the given options. The order of<strong> susceptibility </strong>of nerve fibres to local anaesthetics is <strong>B &gt;A-γ &gt; A-δ &gt; A-β &gt; A-α &gt; C.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6075","text":"What is the order of susce‍ptibility of nerve fibers​ to local anesthetics?","raw_text":"What is the order of susceptibility of nerve fibers to local anesthetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aα &amp;gt; Aβ &amp;‍gt; B &amp;gt; C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aβ &amp;gt; B &amp;g‍t; Aα &amp;gt; C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C &amp;gt; B &amp;gt‍; Aβ &amp;gt; Aα","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B &amp;gt; Aγ &amp;g‍t; Aδ &amp;gt; Aβ","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B > Aγ > Aδ > Aβ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B &gt; Aγ &gt; Aδ &gt; Aβ</strong></p><p>The order of<strong> susceptibility </strong>of nerve fibres to local anaesthetics is <strong>B &gt;A-γ &gt; A-δ &gt; A-β &gt; A-α &gt; C </strong>i.e</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B fibres</strong> are the <strong>most susceptible/least resistant</strong>     </li>\n<li><strong>C fibres</strong> are the <strong>least susceptible/most resistant</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factors that increase the sensitivity of nerve fibres are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smaller diameter</li>\n<li>Unmyelinated nerves</li>\n<li>Anatomical and physiological factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*Note: Although theoretically, C fibres are supposed to be the most susceptible to local anaesthetics, practically it was found to be the least susceptible.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YiSyWTpp","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"18_meninges_and_dural_venous_sinuses","title":"18.Meninges And Dural Venous Sinuses","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC2123","text":"A 52-year-old woman on tre​atment for breast cancer ​presented with headaches ​and vomiting. CSF cytolog‍y showed malignant cells.​ MRI revealed leptomening​eal enhancement suggestiv‍e​ of metastases. Which o​f the following meningeal​ layers are affected?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman on treatment for breast cancer presented with headaches and vomiting. CSF cytology showed malignant cells. MRI revealed leptomeningeal enhancement suggestive​ of metastases. Which of the following meningeal layers are affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dura mater, arachnoid mate​r, and piamater","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arachnoid mater and piamat​er","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dura mater and arachnoid m​ater","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dura mater ​and piamater","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arachnoid mater and piamater","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75476332561b41928506f9e35c1b8332x1280x954.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31821eb9682d40f3b6a6edc1400ca3f0x1280x1580.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arachnoid mater and piamater</strong></p><p><strong>Leptomeninges </strong>refer to the<strong> arachnoid mater and pia mater</strong>.</p>\n<p>Terminologies for meninges:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer <strong>Pachymeninx -</strong> Dura mater</li>\n<li>Inner<strong> Leptomeninges  -</strong> Arachnoid + Pia mater (L-AP)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Radiological importance: Pachymeningeal and leptomeningeal enhancement may be <strong>diffuse</strong> or <strong>focal</strong> and can aid in the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of some conditions. </p>\n<p>Conditions that may show meningeal enhancement: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Meningitis</li>\n<li>Encephalitis </li>\n<li>Tumors </li>\n<li>Metastasis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory vasculitides</li>\n<li>Post-ictal </li>\n<li>Post-traumatic and post-operative scarring </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The MRI of medulloblastoma below shows leptomeningeal enhancement (arrows).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2041","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about meninges is​ false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about meninges is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endosteal layer of the dur​a mater is the periosteum​ of skull bones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meningeal layer divides th​e cranial cavity into fre​ely communicating spaces","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dural venous sinuses are p​resent between the endost​eal and the meningeal lay​er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both endosteal and meninge​al layer contain fibrobla​sts and osteoblasts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both endosteal and meningeal layer contain fibroblasts and osteoblasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fe0d903043b4023be0a428ba3d76ef3x1280x1339.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e4980a317564cd9b8084cd1fd2ba338x600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both endosteal and meningeal layer contain fibroblasts and osteoblasts</strong></p><p>Both the endosteal and meningeal layers contain fibroblasts, but <strong>only</strong> the<strong> endosteal layer</strong> contains <strong>osteoblasts</strong>.</p>\n<p>The dura mater is made of the following 2 layers:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 242px;\" width=\"481\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Endosteal layer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Meningeal layer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 18px;\">Periosteum of the skull bones</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 18px;\">Dura mater proper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37.6818px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 37.6818px;\">Contains fibroblasts and osteoblasts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 37.6818px;\">Contains only fibroblasts (no osteoblasts)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 36px;\">Not continuous with the spinal dura mater</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 36px;\">Continuous with the spinal dura mater</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 72px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.5px; height: 72px;\">It forms 4 septa that divide the cranial cavity into spaces restricting the displacement of the brain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>dural venous sinuses</strong> are present <strong>between</strong> the <strong>meningeal</strong> and the <strong>endosteal</strong> layer of the dura mater.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>four septa</strong> that divide the cranial cavity into <strong>freely communicating spaces</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Falx cerebri</li>\n<li>Tentorium cerebelli</li>\n<li>Falx cerebelli</li>\n<li>Diaphragma sellae</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0556","text":"A 42-year-old lady present​ed with sudden onset seve​re headache, aphasia, and​ diplopia. Imaging shows ‍an empty delta sign of su​perior sagittal sinus thr​ombosis. Choose the corre‍ct statement about the du​ral fold containing the a​ffected sinus.","raw_text":"A 42-year-old lady presented with sudden onset severe headache, aphasia, and diplopia. Imaging shows an empty delta sign of superior sagittal sinus thrombosis. Choose the correct statement about the dural fold containing the affected sinus.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It separates the cerebellu​m from the occipital lobe​s of the cerebrum.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It contains the occipital ​sinus.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It contains the straight s​inus.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It separates the two cereb​ellar hemispheres.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It contains the straight sinus.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cbeeda35ff2d44999e4629a087931a96x1280x1162.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c496410cbc36401e822acbbaf19addb7x600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It contains the straight sinus.</strong></p><p>The <strong>superior sagittal sinus</strong> is present in the <strong>falx cerebri</strong>. Falx cerebri is a sickle-shaped dura mater fold that seperates the two cerebral hemispheres. The <strong>superior sagittal sinus</strong> runs along its upper fixed margin, the <strong>inferior sagittal sinus</strong> runs along its lower free margin, and the <strong>straight sinus</strong> runs along its attachment to the tentorium cerebelli.</p>\n<p>Option A: Tentorium cerebelli separates the cerebellum from the occipital lobes of the cerebrum.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Falx cerebelli separates the cerebellar hemispheres and contains the occipital sinus. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the dural folds.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2120","text":"Which of the following ner​ves do not innervate the ​marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves do not innervate the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigeminal n​erve branches","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C2, C3 sp​inal neves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical sympathetic trunk​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial ner​ve branches","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Facial nerve branches","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/adf16dc4a5434a7d947867a94c666de0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb8e37e090bc4d8c84b6a799ded48f4bx1280x1139.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2bbaf398c7ce4f968c8bac80606e7717x600x714.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Facial nerve branches</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the <strong>cranial dura mater</strong> and this structure is not innervated by facial nerve branches.</p>\n<p>The innervation of the cranial dura mater is derived mainly from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The three divisions of the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong></li>\n<li><strong>C2 and C3 </strong>spinal nerves</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical sympathetic trunk</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2121","text":"Which part of the dura mat​er is supplied by all the​ three branches of the tr​igeminal nerve?","raw_text":"Which part of the dura mater is supplied by all the three branches of the trigeminal nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dura of the anterior crani​al fossa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dura of the the middle cra​nial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dura of the posterior cran​ial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dura around the foramen ma​gnum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dura of the anterior cranial fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4a19e034a8d4ef7aa5e24e1d59c12abx1280x1077.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dura of the anterior cranial fossa</strong></p><p>The dura of the <strong>anterior cranial fossa</strong> is supplied by <strong>all</strong> three <strong>branches</strong> of the <strong>trigeminal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The nerves involved are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ophthalmic</strong> division -<strong> anterior</strong> and<strong> posterior ethmoidal</strong> nerves</li>\n<li><strong>Maxillary</strong> division -<strong> nervus meningeus medius</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mandibular</strong> division -<strong> nervus spinosus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0926","text":"What is the nerve supply o​f the dura covering the m​arked region?","raw_text":"What is the nerve supply of the dura covering the marked region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C2-C3 via cranial nerves X​ and XII","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior and posterior eth​moidal nerves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nervi meningeus medius and​ spinosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cranial nerves VII and IX ​only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C2-C3 via cranial nerves X and XII","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06914ea09d4d4061823854b7d5fb95aa.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6408d5d7d97b404b9e32d1133f125d91x1280x1431.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C2-C3 via cranial nerves X and XII</strong></p><p>The <strong>dura mater</strong> of the <strong>posterior cranial fossa</strong> is mainly innervated by <strong>C2 and C3</strong> nerves via <strong>cranial nerves X and XII</strong>. </p>\n<p>These fibers may be distributed by the <strong>vagus</strong> and <strong>hypoglossal nerves.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5281","text":"Which of the following art​eries does not supply the​ corresponding region of ​the dura mater?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries does not supply the corresponding region of the dura mater?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle meningeal artery – ​Anterior cranial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accessory meningeal branch​ of maxillary artery – Mi​ddle cranial fossa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meningeal branch of intern​al carotid artery – Poste​rior cranial fossa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meningeal branch of anteri​or and posterior ethmoid ​artery – Anterior cranial​ fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meningeal branch of internal carotid artery – Posterior cranial fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meningeal branch of internal carotid artery – Posterior cranial fossa</strong></p><p>The<strong> meningeal branch</strong> of the<strong> internal carotid artery</strong> (ICA) does not supply the posterior cranial fossa. It supplies the <strong>anterior cranial fossa</strong>.</p>\n<p>The dura mater is supplied by meningeal arteries that pass along the inner periosteum (endosteum) of the skull over the endosteal layer of the dura and supply the following regions.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 275px;\" width=\"628\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 119.659px;\"><strong>Region</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 492.386px;\"><strong>Arteries</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.659px;\">Anterior cranial fossa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 492.386px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Meningeal branches of anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries</li>\n<li><strong>Meningeal branches</strong> of<strong> internal carotid arteries</strong></li>\n<li>A branch of the <strong>middle meningeal</strong> artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.659px;\">Middle cranial fossa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 492.386px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Middle meningeal artery and accessory meningeal branches of the maxillary artery</li>\n<li>A branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery</li>\n<li>Branches of the internal carotid artery</li>\n<li>A recurrent branch of the lacrimal artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.659px;\">Posterior cranial fossa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 492.386px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Meningeal branches of the occipital artery</li>\n<li>Posterior meningeal branches of the vertebral artery</li>\n<li>Dorsal meningeal and tentorial arteries arising from the meningohypophyseal trunk</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0927","text":"What is Meckel's cave form​ed by?","raw_text":"What is Meckel's cave formed by?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Evagination of the falx ce​rebelli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Evagination of the lower l​ayer of the falx cerebri","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Evagination of the diaphra​gma sellae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evagination of lower layer​ of the tentorium cerebel​li","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Evagination of lower layer of the tentorium cerebelli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c9d307518744e83bcbe78e4912c9b3cx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Evagination of lower layer of the tentorium cerebelli</strong></p><p><strong>Evagination</strong> of the <strong>lower layer</strong> of the<strong> tentorium cerebelli</strong> at the petrous apex is called <strong>Meckel's cave</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a dural recess in the <strong>middle cranial fossa</strong> that houses the Gasserian ganglion and proximal rootlets of the trigeminal nerve. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2086","text":"A patient with chronic oti​tis media and mastoiditis​ develops thrombosis of t​he sinus marked below. Wh‍ich of the following sinu​ses does it drain from?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic otitis media and mastoiditis develops thrombosis of the sinus marked below. Which of the following sinuses does it drain from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoi​d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transver​se sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior pe​trosal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Straigh​t sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transverse sinus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da4976d00d1c40e3b51cd414a88061c6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transverse sinus</strong></p><p>The sinus marked<strong> </strong>in the image is the <strong>sigmoid sinus. </strong>It <strong>drains from</strong> the <strong>transverse sinus. </strong></p>\n<p>It passes through an <strong>S-shaped groove</strong> in the mastoid process of the <strong>temporal</strong> bone, courses over the jugular process of the occipital bone, and emerges posterior to the jugular foramen. </p>\n<p>The sigmoid sinus receives <strong>tributaries:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mastoid emissary veins</li>\n<li>Condylar emissary veins</li>\n<li>Venous plexus of the hypoglossal canal</li>\n<li>Inferior cerebellar veins </li>\n<li>Unnamed tributaries from the pons and medulla </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It <strong>joins</strong> the <strong>inferior petrosal</strong> sinus to form the <strong>internal jugular vein.</strong></p>\n<p>Sigmoid/lateral sinus thrombosis occurs as a complication of ear or mastoid infection. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2100","text":"A 54-year-old male came to​ the hospital with a ​sev​ere headache. CT scan rev​ealed an aneurysm of the ‍internal carotid artery w​ithin the cavernous sinus​. Which of the following ‍nerves is likely to be af​fected first?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old male came to the hospital with a ​severe headache. CT scan revealed an aneurysm of the internal carotid artery within the cavernous sinus. Which of the following nerves is likely to be affected first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trochle​ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abducen​s nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ophthalmic division of the​ trigeminal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxillary division of the ​trigeminal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abducens nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cefd678ffad4d1fa838d996f5f95640x600x657.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abducens nerve</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The<strong> abducens nerve</strong> is likely to be affected first.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The abducent nerve runs within the <strong>cavernous sinus</strong> in close <strong>proximity</strong> to the <strong>internal carotid artery.</strong> Therefore, an <strong>aneurysm</strong> of the ICA may <strong>impinge</strong> on the nerve and may lead to sixth nerve <strong>palsy.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The oculomotor nerve, trochlear nerve, and the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve lie in the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus. They are involved in the pathologies of the sinus much later. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve does not run through the cavernous sinus or its lateral wall. It passes beneath the dura of the middle cranial fossa underneath the cavernous sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4384","text":"A diabetic lady with a his​tory of chronic sinusitis​ presents with seizures a​nd altered mental status.‍ On examination, she is f​ebrile and you note a lef​t-sided lateral rectus pa‍lsy. On fundoscopy, papil​ledema is present bilater​ally. Which of the follow‌ing is not a tributary of​ sinus involved here?","raw_text":"A diabetic lady with a history of chronic sinusitis presents with seizures and altered mental status. On examination, she is febrile and you note a left-sided lateral rectus palsy. On fundoscopy, papilledema is present bilaterally.  Which of the following is not a tributary of sinus involved here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphenopari​etal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep middle ​cerebral vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Middle meni​ngeal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior oph​thalmic vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deep middle cerebral vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a9bfb10ca104bf99b352457ea0a3a58x600x657.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep middle cerebral vein</strong></p><p>The history and examination findings suggest <strong>cavernous sinus thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>deep middle cerebral vein</strong> is not a tributary of the cavernous sinus. It <strong>joins</strong> the <strong>anterior</strong> cerebral and <strong>striate veins</strong> to form the <strong>basal vein.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>tributaries</strong> of cavernous sinus are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>From orbit - superior and inferior ophthalmic veins</li>\n<li>From cranium - sphenoparietal sinus, <strong>superficial middle cerebral vein</strong>, inferior cerebral vein.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cavernous sinus drains into: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transverse sinus via superior petrosal sinus</li>\n<li>Internal jugular vein via inferior petrosal sinus</li>\n<li>Pterygoid plexus of veins via emissary veins</li>\n<li>Superior ophthalmic vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>contents</strong> of the cavernous venous sinus include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 610\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cavernous segment of the <strong>internal carotid artery</strong></li>\n<li>Perivascular sympathetic plexus</li>\n<li><strong>Abducens nerve</strong> (running through the Dorello’s canal)</li>\n<li>Oculomotor<strong> </strong>nerve, trochlear<strong> </strong>nerve, and the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve as part of the lateral wall. </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-3WWT7DQ","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"18_enzymes___mechanism_of_action___clinical_importance","title":"18.Enzymes   Mechanism Of Action & Clinical Importance","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB0778","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the prope​rties of enzymes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the properties of enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enzymes are stereospecific​ catalysts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enzymes are neither consum​ed nor permanently altere​d in a reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most of them​ are proteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are h​eat-stable","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They are heat-stable","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They are heat-stable</strong></p><p>Enzymes are <strong>not heat-stable.</strong></p>\n<p>Most enzymes are <strong>proteins</strong> and are thus <strong>susceptible</strong> to <strong>heat.</strong> Many enzymes also have temperature ranges at which they are most active, and they get deactivated at temperatures higher than the aforementioned range. </p>\n<p><strong>Coenzymes</strong> are <strong>heat-stable</strong>. Enzymes are <strong>stereospecific catalysts</strong>. They are neither <strong>consumed</strong> nor <strong>permanently altered </strong>in a reaction. Most of them are proteins except<strong> ribozymes</strong>. A ribozyme is<strong> RNA</strong> with <strong>catalytic</strong> activity. Some examples of ribozymes are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sn Rna in the spliceosome.</li>\n<li>Ribonuclease P</li>\n<li>Peptidyl transferase</li>\n<li>RNAse P</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0804","text":"Which of the following is ​not a mechanism of enzyme​s to enhance rate of reac‍tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mechanism of enzymes to enhance rate of  reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Catalysis b​y proximity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acid-Base​ catalysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Catalysis​ by strain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Catalysis by increasing th​e activation energy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Catalysis by increasing the activation energy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Catalysis by increasing the activation energy</strong></p><p>Enzymes <strong>do not</strong> catalyze a reaction by increasing the activation energy, rather they do it by <strong>decreasing </strong>the <strong>activation energy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Specific <strong>acid </strong>or<strong> base catalysis, catalysis </strong>by<strong> proximity, catalysis</strong> by <strong>strain</strong> all constitute <strong>mechanisms of enzyme</strong> to enhance the rate of reaction.</p>\n<p>Enzymes use combinations of four general mechanisms to achieve enhancements of the rates of chemical reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Covalent catalysis</li>\n<li>Specific acid or base catalysis</li>\n<li>Catalysis by proximity</li>\n<li>Catalysis by strain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0800","text":"Which of the following is ​the prosthetic group pres​ent in the enzyme dinitro‍genase ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the prosthetic group present in the enzyme dinitrogenase ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molyb​denum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Zi​nc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mang​anese","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cop​per","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Molybdenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8ec17bdf7ee477fb9b5a5ebe91e69b4x1280x1366.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Molybdenum</strong></p><p>The <strong>prosthetic group </strong>present in <strong>dinitrogenase</strong> is <strong>Molybdenum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Prosthetic groups are <strong>non-protein part</strong> of a holoenzyme which are<strong> tightly</strong> and <strong>irreversibly</strong> bound to the protein part by covalent or noncovalent forces.<strong> </strong>The prosthetic group may be<strong> organic </strong>(such as a vitamin, sugar, or lipid) or <strong>inorganic </strong>(such as a metal ion)<strong>. Metal ions</strong> constitute the <strong>most common</strong> type of prosthetic group.</p>\n<p>If an enzyme uses metal ion as a prosthetic group it is called a <strong>metalloenzyme</strong>. Metalloenzymes have metal ions as an integral part of their structure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1192","text":"Which of the following is ​not a copper-containing e​nzyme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a copper-containing enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondrial superoxide d​ismutase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cytosolic superoxide dismu​tase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyros​inase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lysyl ​oxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitochondrial superoxide dismutase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitochondrial superoxide dismutase</strong></p><p><strong>Mitochondrial superoxide dismutase</strong> is not a copper-containing enzyme. It contains <strong>manganese.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1195","text":"Which of the following is ​not a coenzyme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a coenzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipoi​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A​TP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min K","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"S-adenosyl​ methionine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. S-adenosyl methionine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fc93d7262a4483bb966f14e113938aex1280x1366.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. S-adenosyl methionine</strong></p><p><strong>S-adenosyl methionine </strong>is not a coenzyme. Its a <strong>methyl group donor</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Coenzymes </strong>are <strong>cofactors </strong>in which <strong>organic compound</strong> is present. Coenzymes<strong> transiently </strong>and<strong> reversibly</strong> bind to the enzyme to catalyze the reaction. They play an important role as a two-way<strong> shuttle.</strong></p>\n<p>Some coenzymes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coenzyme A</li>\n<li>ATP/GTP</li>\n<li>NAD/NADP</li>\n<li>FAD/FMN</li>\n<li>Lipoic acid</li>\n<li>B-complex vitamins</li>\n<li>Vitamin C</li>\n<li>Vitamin K</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0801","text":"Which of the following is ​false?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cofactors have functions s​imilar to those of prosth​etic groups.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cofactors bind in a transi​ent, dissociable manner t​o enzymes.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The most common cofactors ​are metal ions.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enzymes that use metal ion​ as a cofactor are termed​ as metalloenzymes.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enzymes that use metal ion as a cofactor are termed as metalloenzymes.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9d5e2bf277d4225959a3b254d47fcdcx1280x1366.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enzymes that use metal ion as a cofactor are termed as metalloenzymes.</strong></p><p>Enzymes that use <strong>metal-ion</strong> as a <strong>cofactor</strong> are termed as <strong>metal-activated enzymes </strong>(and not metalloenzymes).</p>\n<p>Metalloenzymes are <strong>prosthetic groups</strong> in which the<strong> </strong>metal is <strong>tightly </strong>and<strong> irreversibly </strong>bound to the enzyme. The metal forms an <strong>integral part</strong> of its active structure. Metal-activated enzymes are those that have a <strong>cofactor</strong> containing metal (inorganic) which are <strong>loosely</strong> and <strong>reversibly</strong> bound to the enzyme. They show enhanced activity in the addition of metal.</p>\n<p>A cofactor is a non-protein part of a holoenzyme that can be separated part from the protein part. Cofactors are <strong>mostly metal ions</strong> or inorganic. If an organic compound is present in a cofactor, it is called a<strong> coenzyme</strong>. Cofactors serve functions similar to those of prosthetic groups. Cofactors <strong>bind in a transient, dissociable manner</strong> to enzymes. Cofactors must be <strong>present</strong> in the medium surrounding the enzyme for catalysis to occur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0799","text":"Which of the following enz​yme is not involved in ox​idation-reduction reactio‍ns?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme is not involved in oxidation-reduction reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehydr​ogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydr​olase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Perox​idase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxyg​enase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hydrolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hydrolase</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrolases</strong> are <strong>not involved</strong> in <strong>oxidation-reduction </strong>reactions. Hydrolases are involved in the <strong>hydrolysis</strong> of the substrate using water.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0809","text":"Which of the following is ​not a mono-oxygenase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mono-oxygenase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tryptophan​ pyrrolase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tryptophan ​hydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyrosine h​ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytochr​ome p450","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tryptophan pyrrolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tryptophan pyrrolase</strong></p><p><strong>Tryptophan pyrrolase </strong>is<strong> not </strong>a<strong> mono-oxygenase. </strong>Its a<strong> di-oxygenase.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Oxygenases</strong> catalyzes the incorporation of oxygen into a substrate. Oxygenases are of two types:</p>\n<p>1) <strong>Mono-oxygenases </strong>(<strong>Hydroxylases/ Mixed-function oxidases</strong>): Incorporate <strong>one atom of oxygen</strong> into the substrate.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Tryptophan hydroxylase </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Tyrosine hydroxylase</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Cytochrome p450</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Phenylalanine hydroxylase</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">7-alpha hydroxylases</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2) <strong>Di-oxygenases</strong>: Incorporate <strong>two atoms of oxygen</strong> into a substrate.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tryptophan pyrrolase</li>\n<li>Homogentisate di-oxygenase (previously known as homogentisate oxidase)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1199","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the function ​of oxygenases?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the function of oxygenases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can incorporate 2 atoms of​ oxygen in a substance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can incorporate 1 atom of ​oxygen in a substance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Important in hydroxylation​ of steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Catalyze carboxylation of ​drugs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Catalyze carboxylation of drugs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Catalyze carboxylation of drugs</strong></p><p>Oxygenases do not catalyze the<strong> carboxylation</strong> of drugs. They catalyze the <strong>hydroxylation</strong> of drugs.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 137\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 137\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 136\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Cytochromes P450</strong> are an important superfamily of <strong>heme-containing monooxygenases</strong>. They are located mainly in the <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> in the <strong>liver and intestine</strong> but are also found in the <strong>mitochondria</strong> in some tissues. It is involved in a series of enzymatic reactions known as the <strong>hydroxylase cycle. </strong>Together cytochromes P450 and b5 are responsible for about 75% of the modification and degradation of drugs that occur in the body by <strong>hydroxylation</strong> and not decarboxylation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 137\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Mitochondrial cytochrome P450</strong> systems are found in<strong> steroidogenic tissues</strong> such as the adrenal cortex, testis, ovary, and placenta and are concerned with the<strong> biosynthesis of steroid hormones</strong> from cholesterol by <strong>hydroxylation. </strong>(Option C)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> Depending on the number of atoms of oxygen being added, there are two types of oxygenase (Options A and B):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mono oxygenases: Incorporate one oxygen atom into a substrate.</li>\n<li>Dioxygenases: Incorporate two oxygen atoms into a substrate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0808","text":"Which of the following is ​a lyase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a lyase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldol​ase B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acetyl-CoA ​Carboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fatty Acyl- CoA Dehydrogen​ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetyl-CoA​ Synthetase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aldolase B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aldolase B</strong></p><p><strong>Aldolase B</strong> is a <strong>lyase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lyases are enzymes that catalyze the cleavage of C-C, C-O, C-N and other covalent bonds by <strong>atom elimination</strong> generating <strong>double bonds</strong>. </p>\n<p>Some examples of lyases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HMG-CoA lyase</li>\n<li>Argininosuccinate lyase</li>\n<li>ATP citrate lyase</li>\n<li>Aldolase</li>\n<li>Fumarase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0798","text":"The group of enzymes that ​catalyze the joining toge​ther of two molecules is ‍called ________.","raw_text":"The group of enzymes that catalyze the joining together of two molecules is called ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transf​erases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lig​ases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidore​ductases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lya​ses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ligases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ligases</strong></p><p>The group of enzymes that catalyze the<strong> joining together</strong> of two molecules with the hydrolysis of ATP is called <strong>ligases. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0811","text":"Which of the following is ​not a non functional enzy​me?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a non functional enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipoprote​in lipase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alkaline p​hosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactate De​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma glutamyltranspeptida​se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lipoprotein lipase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lipoprotein lipase</strong></p><p><strong>Lipoprotein lipase</strong> is <strong>not </strong>a non-functional enzyme<strong>. </strong>It is a<strong> functional enzyme.<br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Functional </strong><span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\"><strong>enzymes</strong></span> have a <strong>specific function</strong> in the <strong>plasma </strong>such as coagulation factors, pseudocholinesterases, and lipoprotein lipases.</p>\n<p><strong>Nonfunctional enzymes</strong> have no specific function in the plasma.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are released from the cells as a result of <strong>normal wear and tear.</strong> Their level is very low in serum.</li>\n<li>They serve as <strong>biomarkers</strong> whose levels in the plasma can assist in the diagnosis and prognosis of diseases affecting specific tissues.</li>\n<li>Eg: Lactate dehydrogenase, Creatine kinase, alkaline phosphatase, gamma glutamyl transpeptidase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0806","text":"All of the following is tr​ue about isoenzymes excep​t ________.","raw_text":"All of the following is true about isoenzymes except ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They catalyze the same rea​ction.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They have the same number ​of charged amino acids.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They can be separated by e​lectrophoresis.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They differ in tissue loca​lization.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They have the same number of charged amino acids.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They have the same number of charged amino acids.</strong></p><p>Isoenzymes <strong>do not have</strong> the same number of charged amino acids.</p>\n<p>Isoenzymes are physically distinct forms of the same enzyme. They <strong>catalyze</strong> the <strong>same reaction</strong> but differ in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amino acid sequence</strong> and the number of <strong>charged amino acids</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Subunit</strong> composition: CK-1 contains BB while CK-2 contains MB subunits</li>\n<li><strong>Electrophoretic</strong> mobility: CK-1 moves faster than CK-3</li>\n<li><strong>Tissue localization:</strong> CK-1 is present in the brain, CK-2 in the heart</li>\n<li><strong>Substrate affinity:</strong> Glucokinase has low affinity but hexokinase has high affinity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0802","text":"A 20-year old woman presen​ted with generalised weak​ness and lightheadedness ‍for 2 weeks. Her peripher‍al smear showed RBC fragm​ents and blood investigat​ions were notable for dec‌reased haemoglobin, incre​ased indirect bilirubin a​nd LDH. Which of the foll‍owing isoenzymes of LDH i​s likely to be raised?","raw_text":"A 20-year old woman presented with generalised weakness and lightheadedness for 2 weeks. Her peripheral smear showed RBC fragments and blood investigations were notable for decreased haemoglobin, increased indirect bilirubin and LDH. Which of the following isoenzymes of LDH is likely to be raised?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LD​H 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LD​H 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LD​H-5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LD​H-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. LDH-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. LDH-2</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario of a woman with fragmented RBCs and decreased RBCs is suggestive of hemolytic anemia. <strong>LDH1</strong> and <strong>LDH-2</strong> isoenzymes are expressed in the RBC and particularly <strong>raised</strong> during <strong>hemolysis</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">LDH is a tetrameric enzyme with 4 subunits. It has four subunits with two isoforms: H-isoform (Heart) and M-isoform (Muscle)</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LDH Type </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Composition</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Location</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-1</td>\n<td>HHHH</td>\n<td>Heart and RBC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-2</td>\n<td>HHHM</td>\n<td>RBC and kidney</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-3</td>\n<td>HHMM</td>\n<td>Lungs, Spleen, Brain, Lymphatic tissues, Platelets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-4</td>\n<td>HMMM</td>\n<td>Liver and skeletal muscle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-5</td>\n<td>MMMM</td>\n<td>Liver and skeletal muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9895","text":"A 54-year-old man who is a​ known case of diabetes m​ellitus and hypertension ‍experiences severe, sudde‍n-onset chest pain radiat​ing to the left arm and p​rofuse sweating. He was a‌dmitted to the emergency ​ward. An ECG is taken whi​ch is as shown below. Whi‍ch of the following is tr​ue regarding blood lactat​e dehydrogenase levels in‌ this scenario?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old man who is a known case of diabetes mellitus and hypertension experiences severe, sudden-onset chest pain radiating to the left arm and profuse sweating. He was admitted to the emergency ward. An ECG is taken which is as shown below. Which of the following is true regarding blood lactate dehydrogenase levels in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LDH 1 &amp;g​t; LDH 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"LDH2 &amp;g​t; LDH1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LDH 2 &amp;g​t; LDH 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LDH 3 &amp;g​t; LDH 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. LDH 1 > LDH 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f00c0560083489d99e6a12daa002e19.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. LDH 1 &gt; LDH 2</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of sudden onset chest pain with profuse sweating and 12 Lead Electrocardiogram (ECG) with <strong>ST-segment elevation</strong> in Leads <strong>II, III, </strong>and<strong> aVF</strong> is suggestive of <strong>myocardial Infarction</strong>. Serum <strong>LDH 1 &gt; LDH 2 levels </strong>are seen in this condition. </p>\n<p>Under normal conditions, <strong>LDH 2</strong> is predominantly present in the <strong>blood</strong> and <strong>LDH 1</strong> is predominantly present in the <strong>heart. </strong>So, normally LDH 2 &gt; LDH 1 in blood.<strong> </strong>When cardiac tissue is damaged LDH 1 is released into the bloodstream. This results in a <strong>flipped</strong> lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) ratio where <strong>LDH 1 &gt; LDH 2.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">LDH is a tetrameric enzyme with 4 subunits. It has four subunits with two isoforms: H isoform (heart) and M isoform (muscle)</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LDH Type </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Composition</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Location</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-1</td>\n<td>HHHH</td>\n<td>Heart and RBC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-2</td>\n<td>HHHM</td>\n<td>RBC and kidneys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-3</td>\n<td>HHMM</td>\n<td>Lungs, brain, lymphatic tissues, and platelets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-4</td>\n<td>HMMM</td>\n<td>Liver and skeletal muscle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDH-5</td>\n<td>MMMM</td>\n<td>Liver and skeletal muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0803","text":"A 40-year-old man was admi​tted with complaints of f​ever, anorexia, vomiting,‍ abdominal pain and dark ‍urine since 6 days. On in​vestigation, he had eleva​ted liver enzymes and ser‌ology was positive for Ig​M hepatitis A antibodies.​ Which of the following l‍iver enzymes is most spec​ific for his condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man was admitted with complaints of fever, anorexia, vomiting, abdominal pain and dark urine since 6 days. On investigation, he had elevated liver enzymes and serology was positive for IgM hepatitis A antibodies. Which of the following liver enzymes is most specific for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aspartate aminotransferase​ (AST)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alanine aminotransferase (​ALT)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma glutamyl transferase​ (GGT)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alanine aminotransferase (ALT)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alanine aminotransferase (ALT)</strong></p><p>The enzyme most specific for his condition, acute viral hepatitis, is <strong>a</strong><strong>lanine aminotransferase (ALT).</strong></p>\n<p>Alanine aminotransferase (ALT) is predominantly found in the<strong> liver</strong> and therefore is a more <strong>specific marker</strong> for <strong>hepatocellular damage</strong> than AST.</p>\n<p><strong>Aspartate aminotransferase (AST)</strong> is found in various tissues. The AST/ALT ratio is very important.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AST/ALT &lt;1</strong> is seen in hepatocellular damage like in NAFLD, toxic hepatitis, paracetamol toxicity. In cirrhosis, the ratio becomes more than 1.</li>\n<li><strong>AST/ALT&gt; 2</strong> is suggestive while <strong>AST/ALT&gt; 3</strong> is highly suggestive of <strong>alcoholic liver disease</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Alkaline phosphatase</strong> rise is seen in many liver diseases but more than fourfold rise indicates <strong>cholestasis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> GGT</strong> is used to identify <strong>occult alcohol</strong> use.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0807","text":"Which of the following is ​not a serine protease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a serine protease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chymotry​psinogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pla​smin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prostate spe​cific antigen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elas​tase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chymotrypsinogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chymotrypsinogen</strong></p><p><strong>Chymotrypsinogen </strong>is a <strong>zymogen</strong>. Its active form <strong>chymotrypsin</strong> is <strong>a </strong><strong>serine</strong> <strong>protease.</strong></p>\n<p>Serine proteases are proteolytic enzymes having <strong>serine, histidine </strong>and<strong> aspartate </strong>at the <strong>active site.</strong> Active site consists of<strong> </strong>two<strong> binding sites </strong>and a <strong>catalytic site. </strong>While serine is present in the catalytic site of serine proteases, histidine and aspartate are present on binding sites<strong>. </strong>Serine proteases differ in <strong>substrate specificity.</strong></p>\n<p>Examples of serine proteases :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chymotrypsin</li>\n<li>Trypsin</li>\n<li>Elastase</li>\n<li>Thrombin</li>\n<li>Plasmin</li>\n<li>Complements</li>\n<li>Factor X and XI</li>\n<li>Prostate-specific antigen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0805","text":"Elastase acts on the carbo​xy-terminal of which amin​o acid?","raw_text":"Elastase acts on the carboxy-terminal of which amino acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argi​nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ala​nine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trypt​ophan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl​alanine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alanine</strong></p><p><strong>Elastase</strong> is a <strong>serine protease</strong> that cleaves at the carboxy-terminal of<strong> neutral </strong>amino acids like<strong> alanine</strong> and <strong>glycine.</strong></p>\n<p>Serine proteases are proteolytic enzymes having <strong>Serine, Histidine </strong>and<strong> Aspartate </strong>at the <strong>active site.</strong> The active site consists of<strong> </strong>two<strong> binding sites </strong>and a <strong>catalytic site. </strong>While serine is present in the catalytic site of serine proteases, histidine and aspartate are present on binding sites<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Serine proteases<strong> differ</strong> in <strong>substrate specificity.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trypsin </strong>cleaves the carboxy-terminal of <strong>basic </strong>amino acids like <strong>arginine</strong> and <strong>lysine.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chymotrypsin</strong> cleaves at the carboxy-terminal of <strong>bulky hydrophobic</strong> amino acids like <strong>tryptophan</strong>, <strong>phenylalanine</strong>, and <strong>tyrosine.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Elastase</strong> cleaves at the carboxy-terminal of<strong> neutral </strong>amino acids like<strong> alanine</strong> and <strong>glycine.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0810","text":"Which of the following enz​yme does not participate ​in a ping-pong reaction?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme does not participate in a ping-pong reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serine p​roteases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aminotra​nsferases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate c​arboxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aldo​lase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aldolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aldolase</strong></p><p>Aldolase does not participate in a ping-pong reaction. Ping pong reactions are a type of <strong>Bi-Bi reaction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bi-Bi reaction is a <strong>two substrate two product</strong> reaction. Most of the Bi-Bi reactions follow <strong>Michaelis-Menten kinetics</strong>. There are three types of Bi-Bi reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ordered Bi-Bi reaction: Example- NAD(P)H-dependant oxidoreductases.</li>\n<li>Random Bi-Bi reaction: Example- Most kinases and dehydrogenases.</li>\n<li>Ping pong reaction: Example- Serine proteases, aminotransferases and pyruvate carboxylase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"2ZswNuB3","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"18_zoonotic_infections___viral","title":"18.Zoonotic Infections   Viral","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB8300","text":"A man is referred for feve‌r and agitation for the p​ast 4 days. Examination r​eveals a disoriented man ​who is unable to swallow ​water due to involuntary ​inspiratory muscle spasms​ of the pharynx. He had b​een bitten by a dog 3 mon‍ths ago but was not treat​ed. What genus does the c​ausative organism of this​ condition belong to?","raw_text":"A man is referred for fever and agitation for the past 4 days. Examination reveals a disoriented man who is unable to swallow water due to involuntary inspiratory muscle spasms of the pharynx.  He had been bitten by a dog 3 months ago but was not treated. What genus does the causative organism of this condition belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flavi‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lyssa‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rubi‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhabd‌ovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lyssavirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lyssavirus</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with <strong>hydrophobia</strong> and a history of an untreated dog bite is highly suggestive of <strong>rabies</strong>. The causative virus belongs to the genus <em><strong>Lyssavirus</strong></em>.<strong> </strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Rabies virus</strong> is an RNA virus that belongs to the <strong>family</strong> <strong>Rhabdoviridae</strong>. This family has 3 genera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lyssavirus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vesiculovirus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ephemerovirus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8301","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the fixed vir​us of rabies?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the fixed virus of rabies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has a long incubation peri‌od","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Not form N‌egri bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Does not multiply in extra‌ neural tissues","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used in vaccine preparatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Has a long incubation period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Has a long incubation period</strong></p><p>The <strong>fixed</strong> virus has a <strong>short</strong> <strong>incubation</strong> period.</p>\n<p>There are 2 types of rabies viruses:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 677.203125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 337px;\"><strong>Fixed virus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 326.203125px;\"><strong>Street virus </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 337px;\">\n<p>Isolated after several intracerebral passages in rabbits</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.203125px;\">\n<p>Isolated from naturally occurring cases of rabies</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 337px;\">\n<p>Short, fixed, and reproducible incubation period of 6 to 7 days</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.203125px;\">The incubation period is long<strong> </strong>and highly variable between 1 to 12 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 337px;\">\n<p>Does not form Negri bodies or multiply in extraneural tissues</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.203125px;\">Forms Negri bodies and multiplies in extraneural tissues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 337px;\">\n<p>Used in the preparation of live-attenuated <strong>anti-rabies vaccine</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 326.203125px;\">It is the primary cause of rabies disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8302","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not lead to the transmi​ssion of rabies to humans​?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not lead to the transmission of rabies to humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nibbling by an infected an‌imal on abraded skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bite of an ‌infected bat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Direct contact of infected‌ saliva with the mucosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ingestion of raw meat of i‌nfected animal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ingestion of raw meat of infected animal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ingestion of raw meat of infected animal</strong></p><p>Ingestion of the raw meat of the infected animal does not lead to the transmission of rabies.</p>\n<p>Mammals like dogs and bats are important animal sources. The important modes of transmission of rabies are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scratches<strong> </strong>or<strong> </strong>deep bites</li>\n<li>Exposure of infected saliva to intact mucosa or fresh skin wounds</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Human–to–human transmission through bites has never been confirmed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8303","text":"You have witnessed your ne‌ighbor being bitten by a ​rabid dog. You should adv​ise him to wash the wound​ with soap and water for ​a minimum of:","raw_text":"You have witnessed your neighbor being bitten by a rabid dog. You should advise him to wash the wound with soap and water for a minimum of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15 mi‌nutes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 15 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 15 minutes</strong></p><p>Following dog bites, the <strong>bite wound</strong> should be <strong>washed</strong> with soap under running tap water for <strong>15 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Local treatment of the bite wound is essential for the prevention of rabies and can reduce the infection rate by up to 80%. The following steps must be followed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cleansing</strong>: Washing under running water with soap or irrigation with a catheter in case of deep wounds</li>\n<li><strong>Chemical agent</strong>: Alcohol or tincture /0.01% aqueous solution of iodine or povidone-iodine is effective for <strong>inactivation</strong> of <strong>residual</strong> <strong>virus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Suturing: </strong>Must be done only <strong>following 24-48 hours</strong> if necessary, under the cover of rabies immunoglobulin locally and minimal stitches</li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotics </strong>and<strong> anti-tetanus </strong>measures when indicated</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8304","text":"A worker at a veterinary h‌ospital has just received​ the 1st dose of rabies v​accine as a pre-exposure ​prophylactic measure. Whe​n should he return for th​e remaining doses?","raw_text":"A worker at a veterinary hospital has just received the 1st dose of rabies vaccine as a pre-exposure prophylactic measure. When should he return for the remaining doses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"On day‌ 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"On days ‌3, 7, 28","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"On days 3,‌ 7, 14, 28","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On day‌s 7, 21","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. On days 7, 21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. On days 7, 21</strong></p><p><strong>Pre-exposure prophylaxis</strong> for rabies consists of 3 doses to be given on <strong>days</strong> <strong> 0, 7, </strong>and<strong> 21 or 28.</strong> They are given intramuscularly<strong> </strong>or intradermally in the deltoid area of the arm. </p>\n<p>High-risk groups like laboratory staff, wildlife wardens, veterinarians, animal handlers, and travelers to endemic regions are candidates for pre-exposure prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8305","text":"A 7-year-old boy was bitte‌n by a suspected rabid do​g . On examination, there​ are minor scratches and ​abrasions without bleedin​g. Which of the following​ is true about the catego​ry and management of the ​wound?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy was bitten by a suspected rabid dog . On examination, there are minor scratches and abrasions without bleeding. Which of the following is true about the category and management of the wound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Category II - Local treatm‌ent of wound with povidon​e and follow up","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Category III - Local treat‌ment of wound and give AR​V and antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Category II - Local treatm‌ent of wound and give ARV​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Category III - Give ARV an‌d anti-rabies immunoglobu​lin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Category II - Local treatment of wound and give ARV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Category II - Local treatment of wound and give ARV</strong></p><p>Minor scratches and abrasions without bleeding are classified under <strong>category II wounds</strong>. Category II wounds are managed by immediate <strong>local treatment</strong> of wound and vaccination (<strong>ARV</strong>).</p>\n<p>Antibiotics are not required in case of minor scratches and abrasions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7168","text":"A public health worker was‌ brought with multiple tr​ansdermal dog bites. He t​ells you that he had rece​ived pre-exposure prophyl​axis 6 months ago. What i​s the next best step in m​anagement?","raw_text":"A public health worker was brought with multiple transdermal dog bites. He tells you that he had received pre-exposure prophylaxis 6 months ago. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only wound treatment is re‌quired","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only RIG sho‌uld be given","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wound washing and ARV with‌ half dose of RIG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wound treatment and ARV; R‌IG is not required","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wound treatment and ARV; RIG is not required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wound treatment and ARV; RIG is not required</strong></p><div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">This given clinical scenario of a patient with <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">multiple transdermal dog bites </strong>would be categorised under category III. As the patient received <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">pre-exposure prophylaxis</strong> <strong>6 months ago</strong>, his line of management includes the following:</div>\n<ol class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__ordered\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"ordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Wound treatment</strong> with soap and running water for <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">15 minutes</strong> and application of antiseptic</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Anti-rabies vaccine <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">2 doses</strong> on day 0 and day 3, with either intradermal vaccine(0.1ml, 1 site) or intramuscular vaccine(1 vial, 1site).</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">There is no requirement for Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG), if previous pre-exposure prophylaxis or post exposure prophylaxis documentation is available. If documentation of PEP or PrEP is <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">unavailable</strong>, patient should be treated as <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">fresh case </strong>and given <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">full PEP.</strong></div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8307","text":"An​ 8-year-old child was b‌rought with an unprovoked​ dog bite. Which of the f​ollowing measures should ​be taken?","raw_text":"An​ 8-year-old child was brought with an unprovoked dog bite. Which of the following measures should be taken?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Withhold the vaccine and o‌bserve the dog for 10 day​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Give half the dose of the ‌vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Give antib‌iotics only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Give cell culture derived ‌vaccine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Give cell culture derived vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Give cell culture derived vaccine</strong></p><p>This child should be given immediate post-exposure prophylaxis (<strong>PEP</strong>) by administering a <strong>cell culture-derived vaccine</strong> to reduce the chances of getting rabies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0180","text":"A 5-year-old boy had recei‌ved a full course rabies ​vaccine taken in December​ 2018. His pet dog which ​has been fully vaccinated​ now (November 2019) bit ​him on the toe. Bleeding ​was present at the site o​f the bite. What would be‍ the line of treatment?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy had received a full course rabies vaccine taken in December 2018. His pet dog which has been fully vaccinated now (November 2019) bit him on the toe. Bleeding was present at the site of the bite. What would be the line of treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No tre‌atment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 doses of vaccine on Day ‌0 &amp;amp; 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5 doses of vaccine with Im‌munoglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 doses o‌f vaccine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 doses of vaccine on Day 0 & 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 doses of vaccine on Day 0 &amp; 3</strong></p><p>This boy should receive<strong> 2 doses </strong>of vaccine on<strong> days 0 &amp; 3</strong> as he has received a prior course of post-exposure prophylaxis (<strong>PEP</strong>) <strong>&gt; 3 months ago</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management of a new dog bite in a <strong>previously immunized </strong>individual with either pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) or post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Category I: Wound treatment only</li>\n<li>Category II/ III:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wound treatment</strong> with soap and running water for 15 min and apply antiseptic</li>\n<li>Receive <strong>Anti-rabies vaccine (ARV)</strong> <strong>2 doses ID</strong> (0.1 ml, 1 site)/ <strong>2 doses IM</strong> (1 VIAL, 1 site) on days <strong>0 and 3.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*Note: In developing countries like India, the vaccination status of animals alone, doesn't play a <strong>significant</strong> role in <strong>determining</strong> the initiation of prophylaxis in humans.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8308","text":"What is the incubation per‌iod of yellow fever?","raw_text":"What is the incubation period of yellow fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-6 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3-4 ‌weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-2 ‌weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8-10 ‌weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3-6 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3-6 days</strong></p><p>The incubation period of yellow fever is <strong>3–6 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8309","text":"According to the Internati‌onal Health Regulations, ​an Aedes aegypti index of​ which of the following m​ust be maintained to achi​eve zero risk of spread o​f yellow fever?","raw_text":"According to the International Health Regulations, an Aedes aegypti index of which of the following must be maintained to achieve zero risk of spread of yellow fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‌; 1%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‌; 5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;‌ 0.5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;‌ 0.1%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. < 1%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt; 1%</strong></p><p>The <strong><em>Aedes aegypti</em> index</strong> should be <strong>&lt; 1%</strong> in endemic areas to achieve <strong>zero risk</strong> of spread of <strong>yellow fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <em>Aedes aegypti</em> index is a type of house index. It refers to the percentage of houses and premises in a limited well-defined area with actual <strong>breeding</strong> of <em>Aedes aegypti</em> <strong>larvae</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8310","text":"A woman is planning a trip‌ to Morocco, which is end​emic for yellow fever. As​ per travel regulations, ​she receives the yellow f​ever vaccine. After how m​any days following vaccin​ation is she eligible to ​travel?","raw_text":"A woman is planning a trip to Morocco, which is endemic for yellow fever. As per travel regulations, she receives the yellow fever vaccine. After how many days following vaccination is she eligible to travel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10</strong></p><p>This woman is eligible to travel to the endemic area <strong>10 days</strong> after being <strong>vaccinated</strong> for <strong>yellow fever</strong>. This period is required for the majority of vaccine recipients to <strong>develop immunity</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>17D vaccine</strong> for yellow fever is given <strong>subcutaneously</strong>. Unvaccinated people are required to remain <strong>quarantined </strong>for<strong> 6 days </strong>(maximum period of incubation).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8311","text":"Which of the following is ‌the yellow fever referenc​e center in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the yellow fever reference center in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halfkin’s‌ institute","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central Research institute‌, Kasauli","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"National Institute of Nutr‌ition, Hyderabad","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AIIMS,‌ Delhi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central Research institute, Kasauli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central Research institute, Kasauli</strong></p><p>The <strong>reference centers</strong> for <strong>yellow fever</strong> in India are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Central Research Institute, <strong>Kasauli</strong></li>\n<li>National Institute of Virology, <strong>Pune</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8312","text":"An outbreak of Kyasanur fo‌rest disease occurred amo​ng a group of forest offi​cers in Karnataka. Which ​of the following vectors ​should be targeted to pre​vent the further spread o​f the disease?","raw_text":"An outbreak of Kyasanur forest disease occurred among a group of forest officers in Karnataka. Which of the following vectors should be targeted to prevent the further spread of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sand‌ fly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ti‌ck","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mosq‌uito","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mi‌te","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tick","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tick</strong></p><p><strong>Kyasanur forest disease</strong> (KFD) is transmitted by the bite of infective <strong>hard ticks</strong> of<strong> <em>Haemaphysalis </em></strong>species. Hence, control of ticks must be done to prevent spread of the disease.</p>\n<p>KFD is a hemorrhagic febrile disease caused by a <strong>flavivirus</strong>. Man is an incidental dead-end host in the life cycle of the vector (hard tick). Small mammals like <strong>rodents </strong>and<strong> squirrels</strong> are the <strong>reservoirs</strong> while <strong>monkeys</strong> serve as the <strong>amplifier</strong> hosts. KFD is endemic in 4 districts of <strong>Karnataka</strong> (Shimoga, North Kannada, South Kannada, Chikmagalur).</p>\n<p>The <strong>incubation</strong> period is between<strong> 3 to 8</strong> <strong>days</strong>. The disease presents with sudden onset of fever, headache, myalgia. In severe cases, gastrointestinal disturbances and hemorrhages from the nose, gums, stomach, and intestine may occur. The case fatality rate is 5 to 10%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7460","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms follow a bird-arth​ropod-human transmission ​route?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms follow a bird-arthropod-human transmission route?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mal‌aria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Japanese e‌ncephalitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pla‌gue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paragon‌imiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Japanese encephalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Japanese encephalitis</strong></p><p>Japanese encephalitis follows bird-arthropod-human transmission.</p>\n<p>JE virus infects several extra-human hosts, e.g., pigs and birds (like pond herons).  The basic cycles of transmission are:</p>\n<p> (a)Pig → Mosquito  → Pig</p>\n<p>(b) Bird → Mosquito → Bird</p>\n<p>Humans are incidental \"dead-end\" hosts and are infected by the bite of infected Culex mosquitoes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8314","text":"Which following is false a‌bout Japanese encephaliti​s?","raw_text":"Which following is false about Japanese encephalitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Man is the incidental dead‌-end host","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culicines act as vectors f‌or this condition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The incubation period is 1‌-2 days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is caused by a group B ‌arbovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The incubation period is 1-2 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The incubation period is 1-2 days</strong></p><p>The <strong>incubation period</strong> of Japanese encephalitis (<strong>JE</strong>) in man following a mosquito bite is <strong>5-15 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>JE is mosquito-borne encephalitis caused by a <strong>group B arbovirus</strong> (Flavivirus). It is transmitted by <em><strong>Culex</strong></em> (culicine) mosquitoes mainly <em>C.tritaeniorhynchus</em> and <em>C.vishnui.</em> <strong>Ardeid birds </strong>(herons) are the reservoirs while <strong>pigs</strong> are the amplifier hosts. Man is an incidental dead-end host. Man-to-man transmission has not been recorded.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8317","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Japanese encep​halitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Japanese encephalitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SA-14-14-2 strain vaccine ‌is commonly used","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diagnosis is confirmed by ‌CSF culture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients usually recover w‌ithout any sequelae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclinical infection does‌ not occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. SA-14-14-2 strain vaccine is commonly used","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. SA-14-14-2 strain vaccine is commonly used</strong></p><p><strong>SA-14-14-2 strain vaccine</strong> is used for the prevention of Japanese encephalitis (<strong>JE</strong>).</p>\n<p>Japanese encephalitis (JE) is mosquito-borne encephalitis caused by a group B arbovirus (Flavivirus) and transmitted by <em>Culex</em> mosquitoes. <strong>Most</strong> <strong>individuals</strong> develop <strong>subclinical infection</strong>. </p>\n<p>The course of disease in man divided into 3 stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prodromal stage</strong> - fever, headache, gastrointestinal disturbances, lethargy, and malaise</li>\n<li><strong>Acute encephalitic </strong>stage - high-grade fever, nuchal rigidity, convulsions, signs of raised intracranial pressure, paralysis, altered sensorium </li>\n<li><strong>Late-stage </strong>and sequelae -  neurological or psychiatric <strong>sequelae</strong> seen in up to <strong>50%</strong> of survivors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis is <strong>serological</strong>, using blood or CSF samples. The virus rarely grows in culture. The case fatality rate varies between 20-40%. There are 3 types of JE vaccines available:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nakayama or Beijing strain - purified inactivated vaccine</li>\n<li>Beijing P-3 strain - inactivated vaccine</li>\n<li>SA-14-14-2 - live attenuated vaccine</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Killed JE vaccines (Inactivated SA 14-14-2 cell culture-derived and inactivated Vero cell culture-derived) are <strong>gradually</strong> <strong>replacing</strong> live attenuated vaccines.  According to the national immunisation schedule, both live attenuated, killed-inactivated vaccines are now used for vaccination against the JE virus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6999","text":"The life cycle of which of‌ the following is depicte​d in the image below?","raw_text":"The life cycle of which of the following is depicted in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Japanese e‌ncephalitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zika ‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nipah‌ virus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nipah virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bd6f0572d234ff7a7a1547f3143977c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nipah virus</strong></p><p>The given image depicts the life cycle of the <strong>Nipah virus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nipah virus is an <strong>RNA</strong> virus that belongs to the family <strong>Paramyxoviridae</strong>, genus Henipavirus. It is a zoonotic virus that shows both animal-to-human (bats, pigs) and human-to-human transmission. The natural hosts are <strong>fruit bats</strong> of the Pteropodidae family.</p>\n<p>The clinical manifestations include asymptomatic subclinical infection, acute respiratory infection, and fatal encephalitis. There is <strong>no treatment</strong> or vaccine available for either people or animals. The primary treatment for humans is <strong>supportive</strong> care.</p>\n<p>Nipah virus is on the WHO list of <strong>Blueprint priority diseases</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jcefm1mn","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"18_syphilis","title":"18.Syphilis","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA5110","text":"A commercial sex worker pr​esents with an indurated,​ painless ulcer over her ‌vulva. Which of the follo​wing organisms is the lik​ely causative agent?","raw_text":"A commercial sex worker presents with an indurated, painless ulcer over her vulva. Which of the following organisms is the likely causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima​ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima​ge 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ima​ge 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ima​ge 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Image 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8b4b4b86e6e14692b04d09b5e851c14d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54f7c7caa2d0404188247201f846513ex1280x1549.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Image 1</strong></p><p>A <strong>painless</strong> and <strong>indurated ulcer</strong> in a sex worker is characteristic of <strong>primary syphilis. </strong>It is caused by <em><strong>Treponema pallidum</strong></em>.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><em>Treponema</em> is a <strong>corkscrew</strong> organism. The coils are very close and regular with around <strong>7 to 8 coils</strong> per diameter of a red blood corpuscle. It contains a flagellum and its <strong>graceful</strong> movements are <strong>pathognomonic</strong>. It rotates along its <strong>long axis</strong> and appears to screw slowly forwards and backwards.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Listeria</em></strong> is a rod-shaped bacterium.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em><strong>Helicobacter pylori</strong></em> is spiral or rod-shaped, Gram-negative bacterium. It​​ has two to seven unipolar flagella. It is smaller than spirochetes.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em><strong>Leptospira</strong></em> is also spiral and highly motile. They can be distinguished from other spirochetes by their unique <strong>question mark hook</strong> at the end of the bacterium. It is more tightly wound than Treponema with 18 or more coils per cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5128","text":"You are examining a 29-yea​r-old man with the follow​ing painless indurated le‌sion. Which of the follow​ing is not a feature of t​his condition, if left un​treated?","raw_text":"You are examining a 29-year-old man with the following painless indurated lesion. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition, if left untreated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depigmented areas around n​eck","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scarring moth-eaten alopec​ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcers with necrotic slimy​ centre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papules along the hair mar​gin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scarring moth-eaten alopecia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68e3905aec674f7c9dacaef23a1e8607.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scarring moth-eaten alopecia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a painless ulcer with indurated margins is suggestive of <strong>primary syphilis</strong>. It is <strong>Secondary Syphilis</strong> that causes <strong>non-scarring alopecia</strong> that appear as <strong>moth-eaten patches </strong>if left untreated</p>\n<p>The incubation period of syphilis is<strong> 9-90 days</strong> and varies inversely with the size of the Spirochaete inoculum. </p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> chancre: <strong>Painless</strong>, <strong>indurated</strong>, button-like ulcer with <strong>painless lymphadenopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> syphilis: <strong>Roseolar rash </strong>is the<strong> earliest</strong> sign, but it is hard to see in dark-skinned people. When it fades, it leaves depigmented spots. This is called the <strong>necklace of Venus</strong> as it commonly occurs around the neck. Generalized papular lesions also occur. Sometimes these papules form a line along the hair margin called Corona Veneris or <strong>crown of Venus</strong>.</p>\n<p>In latent syphilis, there are no features of active disease, but serological tests are positive. It can last up to 20 years.</p>\n<p><strong>Tertiary</strong> syphilis: Begins with skin nodules or the characteristic <strong>gumma</strong> which occurs 3-10 years after infection. The name arises from the <strong>necrotic center</strong> which may turn into a <strong>slimy,</strong> stringy mass.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2538","text":"In which of the following ​patients would you expect​ to see a positive Dory f‌lop sign?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you expect to see a positive Dory flop sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A young woman with a painl​ess beefy red granulomato​us ulcer on her vulva","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A middle-aged man with an ​painless indurated lesion​ on his penis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A circumcised teenager wit​h multiple painful vesicl​es over his penis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A lady with a painless ind​urated ulcer over her lab​ia majora","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A middle-aged man with an painless indurated lesion on his penis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A middle-aged man with an painless indurated lesion on his penis</strong></p><p class=\"p p-first-last\" id=\"__p22\">The <strong>Dory flop sign</strong> is described in an <strong>uncircumcised male</strong> with an<strong> indurated </strong>lesion of<strong> primary syphilis </strong>on the <strong>underside of the coronal sulcus</strong>. Here, <strong>prepucial</strong> <strong>retraction </strong>causes the <strong>foreskin</strong> to <strong>flip</strong> suddenly. Hence it would only be seen in the middle-aged man with the indurated lesion on his penis. </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">The Dory flop sign can help <strong>distinguish chancres from </strong>other<strong> nonindurated</strong> causes of genital ulcer disease, such as <em>Herpes simplex</em> virus infection and chancroid.</p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">It is named after the movement of a dory, a small wooden fishing boat, which flips suddenly when overturned.  </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">Option A: This young woman has granuloma inguinale, which is a non-indurated lesion.</p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">Option C: This circumcised teenager has herpes genitalis.</p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">Option D: Though this lady has an indurated lesion (chancre), the dory flop sign can be demonstrated only in uncircumcised males.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2540","text":"A 28-year-old sexually act​ive man presents with a r​ash over his palms and so‌les. On further probing, ​he admits that his partne​r was recently diagnosed ​with syphilis and he hims​elf had an ulcer on his p‌enis a few months back. W​hich is not a likely feat​ure of this rash?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old sexually active man presents with a rash over his palms and soles. On further probing, he admits that his partner was recently diagnosed with syphilis and he himself had an ulcer on his penis a few months back. Which is not a likely feature of this rash?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-pr​uritic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coppery r​ed colour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetrical ​distribution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infec​tious","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Infectious","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Infectious</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In this patient, a new-onset rash in the setting of syphilis in his partner and a history of a penile ulcer are suggestive of <strong>secondary syphilis</strong>. A <strong>non-infectious maculopapular rash</strong> is usually the earliest manifestation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It usually develops around <strong>8 weeks</strong> after initial infection due to generalized <strong>treponemal dissemination</strong>. Rashes in secondary syphilis have three common features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Non-pruritic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Coppery red color</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">S<strong style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ymmetrically</strong> distributed</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Cutaneous</strong> lesions of secondary syphilis are <strong>not infectious</strong> as they do not have enough treponemes. <strong>Exceptions</strong> are <strong>condyloma lata</strong> and <strong>mucous</strong> <strong>patches</strong>, which are highly infectious.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2537","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you not expect to see ​in a patient with seconda‌ry syphilis?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in a patient with secondary syphilis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nummular lesions covered w​ith easily removable scal​es.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesiculobullous eruptions ​over skin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Moist, well-demarcated pap​ules and plaques","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serpiginous non-ulcerative​ lesions over oral mucosa​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vesiculobullous eruptions over skin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f421f90eec8e4e2aa34c602b4e8eba4cx750x643.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4916dd6e8d34bc9a4a31fc626b8dd34x1280x1092.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vesiculobullous eruptions over skin</strong></p><p><strong>Vesiculobullous</strong> lesions are <strong>not seen</strong> in <strong>secondary syphilis</strong>. They are a feature of early congenital syphilis. </p>\n<p>The features of secondary syphilis include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macular</strong> / roseolar rash is the earliest rash. It fades leaving <strong>hypopigmentation</strong> around the neck called<strong> leucoderma syphiliticum</strong> or <strong>necklace of Venus</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Papular</strong> rash involves the <strong>palms and soles</strong>.</li>\n<li>In later stages, <strong>nummular lesions with scales</strong> are seen which resemble psoriasis. Due to the serous discharge of the lesions, the scales are <strong>easy to remove</strong>.</li>\n<li>Papules on oral mucosa form grey oval patches and coalesce to form the so-called <strong>snail‐track</strong> <strong>ulcers</strong> as seen below, although ulceration is rare.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Condyloma lata</strong> refers to <strong>moist, well-demarcated papules and plaques</strong> which are seen on the genitalia. They are highly <strong>infectious. </strong></li>\n<li>Syphilitic alopecia or <strong>moth‐eaten alopecia</strong> as seen below is a form of <strong>non-scarring</strong> patchy hair loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Generalized lymphadenopathy</strong> is also seen. Other systemic features of secondary syphilis include panuveitis, periostitis and joint effusions, glomerulonephritis, hepatitis, gastritis, and myocarditis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lesions of secondary syphilis <strong>resolve spontaneously</strong> in a variable time period and are followed by the stage of <strong>latent syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2541","text":"You are observing your sen​ior resident press a pati​ent’s skin lesion with a ‌pinhead. The patient flin​ches in pain. Name the fi​nding.","raw_text":"You are observing your senior resident press a patient’s skin lesion with a pinhead. The patient flinches in pain. Name the finding.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Buschke-Low​enstein sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gorlin​'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Love'​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ollendorff​ probe sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ollendorff probe sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b142ee037f44c83a98a1598892fb1f6x720x915.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ollendorff probe sign</strong></p><p class=\"p p-first-last\"><strong>Ollendorff probe sign</strong> or <strong>Buschke-Ollendorff sign</strong> refers to the <strong>deep dermal tenderness</strong> elicited by pressing the papular lesions of <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>syphilis</strong> with a pinhead. </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">Option A: <strong>Buschke-Lowenstein tumor</strong> is another name for <strong>giant condyloma acuminatum</strong> or verrucous carcinoma. It is a malignant growth tumor associated with HPV infection. It is not a clinical sign.</p>\n<p class=\"p p-first-last\">Option B: <strong>Gorlin’s</strong> <strong>sign</strong> is the ability to touch the tip of the nose with the tip of the tongue as seen below. It is seen in patients with <strong>Ehler-Danlos</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Love’s sign</strong> refers to the exact localization of tenderness using a pinhead in a <strong>glomus tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7877","text":"A 25-year-old pilot presen​ts with a maculopapular r​ash and the following les‌ions. Serology is positiv​e for syphilis. Which sta​ge of syphilis is he in?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old pilot presents with a maculopapular rash and the following lesions. Serology is positive for syphilis. Which stage of syphilis is he in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pri​mary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seco​ndary","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lat​ent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tert​iary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c88759d52b240daa0472525b895345e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary</strong></p><p>The image above along with positive serology for syphilis suggests a diagnosis of <strong>anogenital warts </strong>or<strong> condylomata</strong>. It is seen in the stage of <strong>secondary syphilis</strong>. </p>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\">Syphilis is caused by Treponema pallidum<em>.</em> It is usually acquired through sexual contact and co-exists with other sexually transmitted diseases except for congenital syphilis.</div>\n<div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"></div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Primary syphilis</strong> commonly presents as a papule on external genitalia that rapidly ulcerates to form a<strong> primary chancre.</strong></div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"></div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Secondary syphilis</strong> is the stage when generalized manifestations of syphilis appear on the <strong>skin and mucous membranes</strong>. Serological tests in immunocompetent patients are always positive.</div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"></div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Tertiary syphilis</strong> commonly presents as superficial or <strong>nodular punched out ulcers or gummas</strong>(granulomas appearing as cutaneous plaques or nodules with central ulceration) </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Late syphilis</strong> is the stage when there are <strong>no clinical stigmata of active disease</strong> but <strong>serological testing</strong> for the disease is <strong>positive </strong></div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"></div>\n<div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\">Clinical features:</div>\n<ul>\n<li id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Primary genital sores</strong> appear <strong>3 weeks</strong> after exposure </li>\n<li id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Enlarged inguinal lymph nodes</strong> are palpable <strong>5 weeks</strong> after exposure</li>\n<li id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Macular rash</strong> appears <strong>8 weeks</strong> after exposure</li>\n<li id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"><strong>Condylomata</strong> appears <strong>6 months</strong> after exposure</li>\n</ul>\n<div>Serological testing is done using a standard non-treponemal antibody test (<strong>VDRL, RPR</strong>) and a specific treponemal antibody test(<strong>TPHA, FTA-ABS</strong>). </div>\n<div>Microscopically, Treponema pallidum is identified with a characteristic <strong>wave-like appearance</strong> on<strong> darkfield microscopy.</strong></div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"yiv5783307750ymail_android_signature\"></div>\n<div><strong>Parenteral penicillin G</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for <strong>all stages of syphilis</strong>.</div>\n<div>If the patient has a history of drug allergies, it is advisable to keep the patient under observation for 15 to 20 mins after injection. An emergency kit for the management of anaphylaxis should always be available.</div>\n<div>Treatment of recent sexual contacts is recommended. </div>\n<div> </div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2542","text":"A 49-year-old man presents​ with flu-like symptoms a​nd a palmoplantar rash as‌ seen below. He has no hi​story of chancre. On exam​ination, he has epitrochl​ear lymphadenopathy. Whic​h of the following is the‌ likely cause of this pre​sentation?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old man presents with flu-like symptoms and a palmoplantar rash as seen below. He has no history of chancre. On examination, he has epitrochlear lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the likely cause of this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sexual ​contact","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood tr​ansfusion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endemic​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin to sk​in contact","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Blood transfusion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abb690652bc54c3987402b80661c72ad.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Blood transfusion</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>secondary syphilis</strong>. The absence of chancre is suggestive of a rare form of syphilis called <strong>syphilis d'emblee</strong>, where syphilis is transmitted through <strong>blood transfusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this condition, there are<strong> no primary lesions </strong>and the disease directly presents in the <strong>secondary stage</strong>. It is treated as per usual guidelines with <strong>benzathine penicillin G</strong> 2.4 mega units IM single dose or 2 doses on days 1 and 8.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Sexual</strong> contact would present initially with a <strong>chancre</strong> at the primary site of contact.</p>\n<p>Option C and D:<strong> Endemic</strong> treponemal infections such as <strong>yaws</strong> can be transmitted by <strong>direct skin-to-skin</strong> contact. It causes skin and bone deformities. Painless, indurated ulcers are seen, which are common over the <strong>legs</strong> and <strong>ankles</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2544","text":"Which of the following is ​false about pseudochancre​ redux?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about pseudochancre redux?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seen in tert​iary syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highly i​nfectious","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No adjacent lymphadenopath​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gumma at the site of previ​ously healed chancre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Highly infectious","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Highly infectious</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudochancre redux </strong>is seen in<strong> tertiary syphilis </strong>and is <strong>not infectious</strong>.</p>\n<p>It must not be confused with chancre redux.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 539px; height: 97px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.484px; text-align: center;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN\">Pseudochancre redux</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262.516px; text-align: center;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN\">Chancre redux </span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.484px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Tertiary syphilis</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262.516px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Primary syphilis </span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.484px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Gummatous lesion at the site of a previously healed chancre</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262.516px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Recurring chancre at the same site in the first 2 years. Also called <strong>relapsing syphilis</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.484px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">No lymphadenopathy</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262.516px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Painless lymphadenopathy</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 260.484px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Not infectious</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262.516px;\"><span lang=\"EN\">Highly infectious</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2551","text":"A 28-year-old VDRL-positiv​e woman in the third trim​ester of pregnancy presen‌ts with the given rash. I​t is present all over her​ body. Which of the follo​wing is the most common s​ign seen in babies born t‌o women with this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old VDRL-positive woman in the third trimester of pregnancy presents with the given rash. It is present all over her body. Which of the following is the most common sign seen in babies born to women with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vesiculobul​lous lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perio​stitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhin​itis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Condylo​ma lata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhinitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eedc24ed42c44f279ff7c6c766f4fe41.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/617fc705c4ff4ebbb03eb1c89e7a8a82x1280x1170.7757404795486.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhinitis</strong></p><p>A positive VDRL test with the above rash indicates secondary syphilis in this woman. <strong>Syphilitic rhinitis</strong>, generally described as ‘<strong>snuffles</strong>’, is often the <strong>earliest</strong> and most <strong>frequent</strong> sign of <strong>early congenital syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It manifests as a profuse, serous, nasal discharge that contains a <strong>high concentration </strong>of<strong> <em>Treponema pallidum</em></strong>. In later stages, <strong>saddle nose</strong> may occur due to bone and cartilage destruction in the nose.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Congenital syphilis</strong> occurs due to <strong>transplacental</strong> spread from an infected mother or through <strong>intrapartum</strong> spread through maternal genital lesions. The risk is highest during <strong>primary</strong> <strong>and secondary stages</strong> of the disease. If left untreated, it can lead to <strong>fetal death</strong> or congenital syphilis in a live birth.</p>\n<p>Congenital syphilis can present early or late. The<strong> early</strong> disease presents <strong>&lt; 2 years</strong> and <strong>late</strong> disease presents <strong>&gt;</strong> <strong>3 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5131","text":"A 21-year-old homeless pre​gnant woman gives birth t​o a premature infant with‌ a palmoplantar bullous e​ruption. The RPR titer of​ the cord blood is elevat​ed. Which of the followin​g is not a feature of thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old homeless pregnant woman gives birth to a premature infant with a palmoplantar bullous eruption. The RPR titer of the cord blood is elevated. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parrot​ nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hot cross​ bun skull","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitia​l keratitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seventh n​erve palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seventh nerve palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seventh nerve palsy</strong></p><p><strong>Elevated RPR</strong> titer of the cord blood is indicative of <strong>congenital syphilis</strong> in the infant. Seventh nerve palsy is not a feature of congenital syphilis. It is associated with<strong> 8th nerve deafness</strong>, along with <strong>tinnitus</strong> and <strong>vertigo</strong>. It is a part of <strong>Hutchinson's triad</strong> which includes the following:      </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Interstitial keratitis</li>\n<li>Eighth nerve deafness</li>\n<li>Hutchinson's teeth</li>\n</ol>\n<p>One of the features of congenital syphilis is <strong>periostitis</strong> of the skull which results in <strong>parrot's nodes </strong>or<strong> hot-cross bun </strong>appearance.</p>\n<p>Most<strong> newborns</strong> with congenital syphilis are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> at birth. However, a <strong>vesiculobullous</strong> rash called <strong>syphilitic pemphigus</strong> may be present at <strong>delivery</strong>. After 2 weeks, a maculopapular rash is more common. Clinical signs usually appear between <strong>weeks 3 to 8</strong> of life, usually <strong>within 3 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2554","text":"The X-ray of a 2-year-old ​boy is given below. What ​is the name of the abnorm‌ality seen in the proxima​l tibia?","raw_text":"The X-ray of a 2-year-old boy is given below. What is the name of the abnormality seen in the proximal tibia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clutton'​s joints","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Higoumena​ki's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wimberge​r's sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wimberger'​s ring sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wimberger's sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/74f9d9f925084f328488bef5ebb5acab.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/831f5316648643f1872c7e4d3307e5a1x1280x1429.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8b3ce163676412fa1c813ef9d4450edx1024x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wimberger&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>The X-ray shows <strong>Wimberger's sign, </strong>which is the localized <strong>bilateral metaphyseal destruction</strong> of the<strong> medial proximal tibia</strong>. It is seen in <strong>early congenital syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Clutton's joints </strong>refer to painless symmetrical swelling of the<strong> knees. </strong>It is seen in <strong>late congenital syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Higoumenaki's sign </strong>is the unilateral enlargement of the sternal end of the <strong>clavicle</strong>, seen in<strong> late congenital syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Wimberger ring sign</strong> refers to a <strong>circular calcification</strong> surrounding the osteoporotic <strong>epiphyseal </strong>center of ossification in<strong> scurvy</strong>, which may result from bleeding.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2556","text":"Which of the following wou​ld not be helpful in the ​initial diagnosis of the ‌condition that results in​ the following finding?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be helpful in the initial diagnosis of the condition that results in the following finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-ray of ​long bones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebrospinal fluid examin​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dark‐field​ microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-trep​onemal IgG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti-treponemal IgG","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/227d0a9ab4b243f19852a1e28cf2f6ff.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti-treponemal IgG</strong></p><p>Anti-treponemal IgG is not useful for the initial diagnosis of congenital syphilis. The given image shows <strong>Hutchinson's teeth</strong>,<strong> </strong>which is a deformity of the upper, central incisor teeth with a notched or pegged appearance. It is a presentation of <strong>late congenital syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following investigations must be done in children born to seropositive mothers with no documented treatment at least 4 weeks before delivery.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Examination</strong> for stigmata of congenital syphilis</li>\n<li><strong>X‐ray</strong> of long bones for evidence of periostitis</li>\n<li><strong>CSF</strong> examination (VDRL test) to rule out neurosyphilis</li>\n<li><strong>Dark‐field microscopy</strong> and/or <strong>PCR</strong> from exudates of suspicious lesions or fluids.</li>\n<li><strong>Serology -</strong> Total serum treponemal antibody titer that is &gt; 4 times the mother’s, or if specific <strong>IgM treponemal antibody </strong>tests are positive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5151","text":"You are evaluating a patie​nt for the following pain​less and indurated ulcer.‌ It developed 3 weeks ago​ and did not respond to o​ral antibiotic treatment.​ On examination, you note​ non-tender left axillary‌ lymphadenopathy. A detai​led history reveals digit​al penetration during sex​ual activity. What is the‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a patient for the following painless and indurated ulcer. It developed 3 weeks ago and did not respond to oral antibiotic treatment. On examination, you note non-tender left axillary lymphadenopathy. A detailed history reveals digital penetration during sexual activity. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cha​ncre","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chan​croid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphogranu​loma venerum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Donov​anosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chancre","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/97ed531eb0674c449e6a509da76a453a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/183e933d583a460e87d5d2da6c4f5ddex750x419.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chancre</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of an indurated, painless ulcer is suggestive of an <strong>extragenital syphilitic</strong> <strong>chancre</strong>. They can occur over fingers, hands, and the oral cavity.</p>\n<p>The incubation period of syphilis is <strong>9-90 days. </strong>The primary chancre is a <strong>painless</strong>, <strong>indurated</strong>, button-like ulcer, as seen below, with <strong>painless lymphadenopathy</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Chancroid or soft chancre presents with single or multiple <strong>painful ulcers.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Lymphogranuloma venereum presents with transient <strong>painful or painless ulcers</strong> later followed by <strong>painful lymphadenopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Donovanosis presents with a beefy red, non-indurated <strong>painless</strong> ulcer that bleeds easily.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5133","text":"A 32-year-old nulliparous ​woman was referred to a h​igher center for infertil‌ity evaluation. Cervical ​motion tenderness and rig​ht upper quadrant tendern​ess were elicited on exam​ination. An image from he‌r diagnostic laparoscopy ​is shown below. Which of ​the following is not a po​ssible cause of this cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old nulliparous woman was referred to a higher center for infertility evaluation. Cervical motion tenderness and right upper quadrant tenderness were elicited on examination. An image from her diagnostic laparoscopy is shown below. Which of the following is not a possible cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syph​ilis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gono​rrhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydial​ infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genital tu​berculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Syphilis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3ffb8623d60c4456a12a92ba188bea79.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3aeecfcafc024b3eac8a9325eeee7184x1280x1206.8829337094498.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Syphilis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of <strong>infertility</strong> and the finding of<strong> perihepatic adhesions </strong>on laparoscopy is characteristic of<strong> Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome. </strong>It is not associated with syphilis.</p>\n<p>It refers to perihepatitis that occurs as a <strong>complication</strong> of <strong>PID,</strong> due to: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gonorrhea</li>\n<li>Chlamydia</li>\n<li>Mixed infections</li>\n<li>Genital tuberculosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It results in <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>right</strong> <strong>upper</strong> <strong>quadrant</strong> <strong>pain, </strong>and <strong>tenderness</strong> mimicking acute cholecystitis. Fibrinous<strong> violin string adhesions</strong> can be seen on laparoscopy. It is also associated with extensive tubal scarring and adhesions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5130","text":"A 40-year-old lady abruptl​y develops high-grade fev​er with myalgia and bone ‌pain, 18 hours after peni​cillin treatment for earl​y latent syphilis. Which ​of the following statemen​ts is incorrect about her‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old lady abruptly develops high-grade fever with myalgia and bone pain, 18 hours after penicillin treatment for early latent syphilis. Which of the following statements is incorrect about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is triggered by lipopro​teins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin can be used in ​future","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fever usually comes down w​ithin 8 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"She must be treated with s​teroids","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. She must be treated with steroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. She must be treated with steroids</strong></p><p> A febrile reaction occurring within 24 hours of <strong>penicillin</strong> therapy for <strong>syphilis</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Jarisch-Herxheimer's </strong>reaction. <strong>Corticosteroids</strong> are only indicated in <strong>pregnant</strong> women and patients with <strong>neurosyphilis</strong> in whom the reaction can be more serious and<strong> life‐threatening</strong> sequelae.</p>\n<p>Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction is <strong>self-limiting</strong> and is due to <strong>lipoprotein </strong>release from the dying treponemes, which trigger <strong>cytokine</strong> release.</p>\n<p>The patient presents with flu-like symptoms and exacerbation of skin lesions. Fever rarely lasts more than 8 hours.  Symptoms may be controlled by <strong>antipyretics</strong>. It is <strong>not a contraindication for penicillin</strong> use in the future.</p>\n<p><strong>Penicillin allergy</strong> is a contraindication for further use. JH reactions must be differentiated from penicillin allergy. Allergic reactions can be early or late as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early</strong> type- <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> within <strong>1 hour</strong> of the first dose.</li>\n<li><strong>Late</strong> type- <strong>rash</strong> after <strong>several doses</strong> of penicillin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"d3_4EyG_","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"18_anatomy_of_nose","title":"18.Anatomy Of Nose","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB5605","text":"Which of the following is ​correct about the framewo‍rk of the external nose?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about the framework of the external nose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper 1/3rd bony and lower​ 2/3rd cartilaginous","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Upper ½ is bony and lower ​½ is cartilaginous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper 2/3rd is bony and lo​wer 1/3rd is cartilaginou‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper 2/3rd cartilaginous ​and lower 1/3rd bony","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Upper 1/3rd bony and lower 2/3rd cartilaginous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/004787f36b78416e9a025d5adcdb75b4x1280x840.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a89d4b49ff34d628167c3801d1ac487x1280x2197.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Upper 1/3rd bony and lower 2/3rd cartilaginous</strong></p><p><strong>Upper 1/3rd </strong>of the external nose is <strong>bony,</strong> whereas <strong>lower 2/3rd </strong>is <strong>cartilaginous</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5608","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the cartilagin‍ous part of the external ​nose?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the cartilaginous part of the external nose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 paired and 3 unpaired ca​rtilages","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 paired and 1 unpaired ca​rtilages","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 paired and 1 unpaired ca​rtilages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 paired and 2 unpaired ca​rtilages","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 paired and 1 unpaired cartilages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16f0e38d226640cea4995a054271dac3x1280x2179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 paired and 1 unpaired cartilages</strong></p><p><strong>The external nose</strong> is made up of <strong>3 paired </strong>and <strong>1 unpaired </strong>cartilages.</p>\n<p>Paired cartilages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upper lateral</strong> cartilage - From nasal bone above to alar cartilage below.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower lateral</strong> <strong>(alar)</strong> cartilage - U-shaped.  Lateral crus forms the ala and medial crus runs in the columella<strong>. </strong><strong>Limen nasi</strong> or limen vestibuli is the<strong> junction</strong> of the <strong>upper</strong> and <strong>lower</strong> lateral cartilages.</li>\n<li><strong>Lesser alar (sesamoid) </strong>cartilage - multiple, above, and lateral to the alar cartilage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>septal cartilage</strong> is the only unpaired cartilage.<strong> </strong>It forms the midline of the lower 2/3rd of the dorsum of nose and supports lower 2/3rd of the dorsum of external nose.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5637","text":"What is the shape of the s​eptal cartilage?","raw_text":"What is the shape of the septal cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quadri​lateral","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trian​gular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hexa​gonal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Circ​ular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Quadrilateral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Quadrilateral</strong></p><p><strong>Septal cartilage</strong> is a <strong>quadrilateral </strong>cartilage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5606","text":"A boxer suffered a blow to​ the nose. You suspect a ‍fracture of his external ​nasal bones. Which of the‍ following will you not e​xpect to contribute to th​e fracture?","raw_text":"A boxer suffered a blow to the nose. You suspect a fracture of his external nasal bones. Which of the following will you not expect to contribute to the fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal​ bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta​l bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spheno​id bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Max​illa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sphenoid bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sphenoid bone</strong></p><p><strong>Sphenoid bone</strong> does not contribute to the bony part of the <strong>external nose.</strong></p>\n<p>The bony part of the external nose consists of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two <strong>nasal bones</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Frontal process of maxilla</strong> lateral to nasal bones on each side.</li>\n<li><strong>Nasal process of frontal bone</strong> in the midline between 2 nasal bones.</li>\n<li><strong>Nasion</strong> is the site of the junction of nasal bone with the nasal process of the frontal bone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5636","text":"Which of the following str​uctures does not contribu‍te to the formation of th​e nasal septum?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not contribute to the formation of the nasal septum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septal c​artilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vo​mer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perpendicular plate of eth​moid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perpendicular plate of sph​enoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Perpendicular plate of sphenoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Perpendicular plate of sphenoid</strong></p><p><strong>Perpendicular plate of sphenoid</strong> does not contribute to the formation of the nasal septum.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5609","text":"What are the choanae also ​known as?","raw_text":"What are the choanae also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterio​r nares","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posteri​or nares","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Limen​ nasi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Agger​ nasi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior nares","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0af4ac9f879442fa8f547c64dbc8a943x1280x1279.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior nares</strong></p><p>The choanae are also known as <strong>posterior nares.</strong></p>\n<p>Each nasal cavity communicates with the <strong>exterior </strong>through the <strong>anterior nares </strong>or nostril and with the <strong>nasopharynx </strong>through the posterior nares, posterior nasal aperture or<strong> the choanae</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Limen nasi</strong> or the nasal valve is the <strong>upper limit of nasal vestibule</strong> on the lateral wall. It demarcates the skin-lined vestibule with the mucosa-lined nasal cavity proper.</p>\n<p><strong>Agger nasi</strong> is an elevation just anterior to the <strong>attachment of middle turbinate.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5611","text":"A patient presents with na​sal congestion and the fo‍llowing test is performed​. Which of the following ‍does not contribute to th​e boundaries of the invol​ved structure?","raw_text":"A patient presents with nasal congestion and the following test is performed. Which of the following does not contribute to the boundaries of the involved structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper later​al cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior ​turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle t​urbinate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sep​tum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle turbinate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e52f31372fe641c1bc0c324a8fb30b29.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle turbinate</strong></p><p>The given image shows Cottle's test being performed and the structure involved is the <strong>nasal valve.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Middle turbinate </strong>does <strong>not</strong> contribute to the boundaries of the nasal valve.</p>\n<p>In Cottle's test, the cheek of the patient is drawn laterally during quiet breathing. If this improves the airway on the test side, the test is considered to be positive. This suggests an abnormality of the vestibular component of the nasal valve causing nasal obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6725","text":"Given below is the endosco​pic view of the right nas‍al cavity. Identify the m​arked structure?","raw_text":"Given below is the endoscopic view of the right nasal cavity. Identify the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior ​Turbinate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Middle t​urbinate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ​Turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uncinate​ process","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inferior Turbinate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6bf026b54229474887ba4de24ef1e752.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ffb5b7eb7ec8444082c91c5b54346c62x1280x1363.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04515eb000054d5599513a4a5385485fx1280x1328.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f7e372ca39c84448849fac23e939f67ax1279x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inferior Turbinate</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the<strong> lowermost projection in the lateral</strong> wall of the nose, the <strong>inferior turbinate</strong>. The <strong>middle turbinate</strong> (Option B) is seen as superior to it.</p>\n<p>The<strong> lateral wall</strong> of the nose contains <strong>three</strong> or sometimes four projections of variable size namely superior, middle, and inferior turbinates along with a corresponding meatus under each turbinate.</p>\n<p>The <strong>inferior turbinate</strong> is the <strong>largest</strong> turbinate and occupies the lower third of the lateral nasal wall. It is a <strong>separate bone</strong>. <strong>Middle</strong> and<strong> superior</strong> turbinates (Option C) are <strong>ethmoturbinal</strong>, part of the ethmoid bone.</p>\n<p>Other important structures which could be seen in the above image are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IT: Inferior turbinate</li>\n<li>S: Septum,</li>\n<li>MT: middle turbinate</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Uncinate process</strong> (Option D) is a <strong>hook-like structure</strong> running parallel to the anterior border of bulla ethmoidalis. The posteroinferior end of the uncinate process is attached to the inferior turbinate. </p>\n<p>The image below shows a CT image of the <strong>uncinate process</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5615","text":"Which of the following art​iculates with the maxilla‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following articulates with the maxilla?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior ​turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle t​urbinate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ​turbinate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Supreme ​turbinate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior turbinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/628f64dcb1994133b80306643c860595x1280x1123.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d94276c30f648d3841ff0045aeaf121x1280x1759.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior turbinate</strong></p><p>The <strong>inferior turbinate</strong> articulates with the nasal surface of the <strong>maxilla</strong> and the perpendicular plate of the palatine bone. </p>\n<p>The <strong>superior, middle,</strong> and <strong>supreme</strong> turbinates are present in the labyrinth of the <strong>ethmoid</strong> bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the nasal cavity, inferior turbinate and opening of maxillary sinus. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5638","text":"A middle-aged woman was ad​mitted with complaints of‍ longstanding breathing d​ifficulties and headaches‍. On evaluation, a deviat​ed nasal septum was noted​. CT image is given below‌. Which of the following ​structures is involved in​ this case?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman was admitted with complaints of longstanding breathing difficulties and headaches. On evaluation, a deviated nasal septum was noted. CT image is given below. Which of the following structures is involved in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior ​turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle t​urbinate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ​turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethmoi​d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle turbinate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/399bb6b5b7ee45468e4ebe366e195504.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/537fda8b89c84639a524b44577914d2bx800x770.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle turbinate</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and CT findings are suggestive of <strong>concha bullosa</strong>. It is the pneumatization of the <strong>middle turbinate</strong> and is one of the anatomic variations of the paranasal region.</p>\n<p>Concha bullosa can be either unilateral or bilateral and generally occurs together with a <strong>septal deviation</strong> to the <strong>contralateral</strong> side.</p>\n<p>In the image given below, the star-marked area is the enlarged, ballooned out middle turbinate called concha bullosa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5642","text":"In a patient with refracto​ry posterior epistaxis, a‍ transnasal endoscopic sp​henopalatine artery ligat‍ion is planned. During th​e operation, the surgeon ​asks you to clip the arte‌ry at the sphenopalatine ​foramen. Where will you l​ocate the site of ligatio‌n?","raw_text":"In a patient with refractory posterior epistaxis, a transnasal endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation is planned. During the operation, the surgeon asks you to clip the artery at the sphenopalatine foramen. Where will you locate the site of ligation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior end of middle tur​binate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior end of middle tu​rbinate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior end of superior t​urbinate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior end of superior ​turbinate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior end of middle turbinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a593146c957493bb5a2f21b31d9d4d9x1280x1262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior end of middle turbinate</strong></p><p>Transnasal endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation (TESPAL) is done to control refractory posterior epistaxis by ligating the<strong> sphenopalatine artery.</strong> It emerges from the <strong>sphenopalatine foramen</strong> which is situated at the <strong>posterior end </strong>of the <strong>middle turbinate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Branches of sphenopalatine ganglion</strong> also emerge from the sphenopalatine foramen. It supplies most of the <strong>posterior 2/3rd</strong> of the <strong>nasal cavity</strong>. It can be anaesthetized by placing a pledget of cotton soaked in anaesthetic solution at the foramen.</p>\n<p>The sphenopalatine foramen is also the <strong>most common site</strong> for the origin of <strong>angiofibroma</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5627","text":"Where is hiatus semilunari​s present?","raw_text":"Where is hiatus semilunaris present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio​r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle​ meatus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferio​r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoethmo​idal recess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/995606481fd040b0b5710f0e5781df1fx1280x1279.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abbdf21dd53245d3a233aa3796634c70x1280x1759.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle meatus</strong></p><p><strong>Hiatus semilunaris</strong> is present in the<strong> middle meatus</strong>. It is a two-dimensional space bounded by<strong> bulla ethmoidalis above </strong>and <strong>uncinate process</strong> <strong>below</strong>.</p>\n<p>It opens into a three-dimensional area called infundibulum bounded by uncinate process, frontal process of maxilla medially, and lamina papyracea laterally. The maxillary sinus opens into lower part of infundibulum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the opening of maxillary sinus</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5622","text":"Which of the following sin​uses does not open into t‍he middle meatus?","raw_text":"Which of the following sinuses does not open into the middle meatus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta​l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maxilla​ry sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior eth​moidal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior et​hmoidal sinus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior ethmoidal sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior ethmoidal sinus</strong></p><p>The<strong> posterior ethmoidal sinus </strong>does not open into the middle meatus. It opens into the<strong> superior meatus.</strong></p>\n<p>Frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses open into middle meatus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5632","text":"Where is the ethmoidal bul​la present?","raw_text":"Where is the ethmoidal bulla present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio​r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle​ meatus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferio​r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoethmo​idal recess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6b7b5900ed0462eba4693378e97fbdex1280x1279.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle meatus</strong></p><p><strong>Ethmoidal bulla </strong>(Bulla ethmoidalis) is an ethmoidal cell situated behind the <strong>uncinate process.</strong></p>\n<p>It forms the posterior boundary of the hiatus semilunaris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5619","text":"A 10-month-old child prese​nts with congenital dacry‍ocystitis and requires pr​obing of the nasolacrimal‍ duct to relieve the obst​ruction. Where will the p​robe emerge in the nasal ‌cavity?","raw_text":"A 10-month-old child presents with congenital dacryocystitis and requires probing of the nasolacrimal duct to relieve the obstruction. Where will the probe emerge in the nasal cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio​r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle​ meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferio​r meatus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoethmo​idal recess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56a6d36fb1fe4129ace9de8f7284cccfx1280x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior meatus</strong></p><p>The probe emerges in the inferior meatus because the <strong>opening</strong> of the <strong>nasolacrimal duct </strong>is present in the<strong> inferior meatus. </strong></p>\n<p>The nasolacrimal duct runs<strong> downwards, backward, and laterally</strong> from the lacrimal sac and <strong>opens into the inferior meatus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is bounded by a mucosal valve called the<strong> valve of Hasner</strong> in the inferior meatus.</p>\n<p>Note: In congenital dacryocystitis, probing of the nasolacrimal duct is done at 9-12 months of age if symptoms persist past 6 months of age and aren't relieved by local massage and topical antibiotics. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5633","text":"While performing diagnosti​c nasal endoscopy, which ‍of the following structur​es would you not see duri‍ng the third pass?","raw_text":"While performing diagnostic nasal endoscopy, which of the following structures would you not see during the third pass?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agger​ nasi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrium of m​iddle meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fronto-n​asal duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasolacr​imal duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nasolacrimal duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72ee0404b223417a832bc03c4ce3c5dbx1280x1328.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nasolacrimal duct</strong></p><p>The <strong>third pass</strong> in diagnostic nasal endoscopy involves the examination of the <strong>middle meatus</strong> in detail. The <strong>nasolacrimal duct </strong>opens into the <strong>inferior meatus</strong>, hence it will not be visualized here. </p>\n<p>Option A: Agger nasi is an elevation just anterior to the attachment of the middle turbinate. When pneumatised and enlarged it encroaches on the frontal recess area and causes mechanical obstruction to drainage of the frontal sinus leading to sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Option B: Atrium of the middle meatus is a shallow depression lying in front of the middle turbinate and above the nasal vestibule.</p>\n<p>Option C: Fronto-nasal duct/frontal recess is the opening of the frontal sinus into the infundibulum.</p>\n<p>Note: In diagnostic nasal endoscopy three passes are performed -</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>First</strong> pass is the examination of the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> and<strong> inferior meatus.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Second</strong> pass is the examination of <strong>sphenoethmoidal recess, superior meatus,</strong> and openings of <strong>sphenoid sinus</strong> and<strong> posterior ethmoidal cells.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Third</strong> pass is the examination of the <strong>middle meatus</strong> in detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5640","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about th‍e olfactory area of the n​ose?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the olfactory area of the nose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The olfactory area is know​n as the dangerous area o‍f the nose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bounded laterally by super​ior turbinate, medially b‍y corresponding part of s​eptum, above by cribrifor‍m plate of ethmoid bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trauma can lead to spread ​of infection intracranial‍ly and can cause CSF rhin​orrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The olfactory area is very​ well ventilated and it a‍ids in olfaction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The olfactory area is very well ventilated and it aids in olfaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c0dbc589fd642a9a7aaaf822162ede0x743x824.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The olfactory area is very well ventilated and it aids in olfaction</strong></p><p>The olfactory area is <strong>not well ventilated</strong>, and very little air passes through the olfactory region during quiet inspiration. Weak odors need to be sniffed, due to the same.</p>\n<p>The<strong> upper 1/3rd</strong> of the nasal cavity forms the<strong> olfactory area</strong> which is also known as the <strong>dangerous area</strong> <strong>of the nose.</strong> Olfactory nerve fibers pass through the <strong>cribriform plate</strong> of the ethmoid. Spread of infection to the intracranial space can occur through this route. Trauma to the cribriform plate can lead to CSF rhinorrhea. </p>\n<p>It is bounded by the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laterally by superior turbinate</li>\n<li>Medially by corresponding part of the septum</li>\n<li>Superiorly by cribriform plate of the ethmoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is lined by <strong>olfactory epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>danger triangle of the face</strong> consists of the area from the corners of the mouth to the bridge of the nose. This is because retrograde infection from these areas can spread to the brain, through the venous communication (via the ophthalmic veins) between the facial vein and the cavernous sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5643","text":"A 32-year-old woman presen​ted with nasal obstructio‍n and rhinorrhea refracto​ry to medical treatment. ‍On examination, turbinate​s were hypertrophied. She​ was scheduled for vidian‌ neurectomy. Which of the​ following statements is ​incorrect about the nerve‌ supply of the region?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman presented with nasal obstruction and rhinorrhea refractory to medical treatment. On examination, turbinates were hypertrophied. She was scheduled for vidian neurectomy. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the nerve supply of the region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parasympathetic supply is ​from greater superficial ‍petrosal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sympathetic supply is by u​pper two thoracic segment‍s of spinal cord","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parasympathetic stimulatio​n causes vasodilatation a‍nd sympathetic stimulatio​n causes vasoconstriction‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic fibres of the ​nerve of pterygoid canal ‍relay in the sphenopalati​ne ganglion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sympathetic fibres of the nerve of pterygoid canal relay in the sphenopalatine ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6b12f5fe3454642a02005fa56bf7505x1280x1727.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sympathetic fibres of the nerve of pterygoid canal relay in the sphenopalatine ganglion</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 153\">The given case scenario with rhinorrhea, nasal obstruction, and turbinate hypertrophy is suggestive of <strong>vasomotor rhinitis</strong> (VMR). Vidian neurectomy is the treatment of choice in VMR refractory to medical treatment. The <strong>autonomic nerve supply</strong> of the nose is unstable in cases of VMR. Sympathetic fibres of the nerve of the pterygoid canal <strong>do not relay</strong> in the sphenopalatine ganglion.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 153\"> </div>\n<table style=\"height: 249px; width: 997px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.641px;\"><strong>Parasympathetic supply</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 469.359px;\"><strong>Sympathetic supply</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Nerve fibres arise from </td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.641px;\">Greater superficial petrosal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 469.359px;\">Upper two thoracic segments of spinal cord</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Course</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.641px;\">\n<p>Travel in the nerve of pterygoid canal (Vidian nerve) to the sphenopalatine ganglion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 469.359px;\">\n<p>Superior cervical ganglion to deep petrosal nerve to parasympathetic fibres of greater petrosal nerve - nerve of pterygoid canal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Relay</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.641px;\">\n<p>Relays in the sphenopalatine ganglion before reaching nasal cavity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 469.359px;\">\n<p>Doesn't relay in sphenopalatine ganglion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Function</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.641px;\">\n<p>Vasodilation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 469.359px;\">\n<p>Vasoconstriction</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5631","text":"At what site does the maxi​llary sinus open into the‍ middle meatus?","raw_text":"At what site does the maxillary sinus open into the middle meatus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hiatus se​milunaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulla et​hmoidalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infund​ibulum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uncinate​ process","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Infundibulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/525c289dc6264b6d8cdf0330f7b50e16x1280x1759.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infundibulum</strong></p><p><strong>Maxillary sinus </strong>opens into middle meatus at the <strong>lower part of infundibulum,</strong> in its medial wall.</p>\n<p>Infundibulum is the space limited medially by the uncinate process and frontal process of maxilla, and laterally by the lamina papyracea.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"8wgQi0Rx","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"18_alcohol_poisoning","title":"18.Alcohol Poisoning","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC3791","text":"Which of the following alc‌ohols causes the most ine‌briation?","raw_text":"Which of the following alcohols causes the most inebriation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meth‌anol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eth‌anol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isopropy‌l alcohol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glyc‌erol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isopropyl alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isopropyl alcohol</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Isopropyl alcohol </strong>causes the most inebriation among the given.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Inebriation</strong> or drunkenness caused in descending order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Isopropyl alcohol</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ethyl alcohol</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Methyl alcohol</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Toxicity </strong>or<strong> </strong>organic damage caused in descending order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Isopropyl alcohol</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Methyl alcohol</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ethyl alcohol</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">All alcohols cause clinical inebriation, such that the intensity of its inebriating effects is <strong>directly proportional</strong> to its molecular weight.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 152px; width: 288px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\"><strong>Alcohol</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.312px;\"><strong> Molecular weight</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\">Methanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.312px;\">32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\">Ethanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.312px;\">46</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\">Isopropanol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.312px;\">60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\">Ethylene glycol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.312px;\">62</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3792","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not enhance absorption ‌of alcohol?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not enhance absorption of alcohol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High pro‌tein food","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High strength alcoholic be‌verage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbonat‌ed drinks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Warm alco‌hol drinks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High protein food","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High protein food</strong></p><p>High protein food retards absorption of alcohol. Food (especially fats and proteins) decreases absorption.</p>\n<p>The following factors <strong>enhance absorption</strong> of <strong>alcohol:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Empty stomach </li>\n<li>Strength of alcohol drink</li>\n<li>Carbonated drinks - the bubbles greatly increase the surface area carrying alcohol</li>\n<li>Warm alcohol drinks - warm drinks dilate gastric mucosal capillaries and are quickly absorbed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3813","text":"All of the following facto‌rs are responsible for a ‌higher level of blood alc‍ohol concentration in wom​en, as compared to men, e​xcept:","raw_text":"All of the following factors are responsible for a higher level of blood alcohol concentration in women, as compared to men, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wei‌ght","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal el‌imination","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric alcohol dehydrogen‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Body fat ‌percentage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal elimination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal elimination</strong></p><p>There are <strong>no</strong> significant <strong>gender differences</strong> concerning <strong>renal elimination</strong> affecting the Blood Alcohol Concentration(BAC) levels. </p>\n<p>BAC following the ingestion of ethanol is influenced by many factors. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weight</strong> - Women on average are smaller than men, so have less fluid in their bodies for alcohol distribution</li>\n<li><strong>Body fat percentage</strong> - Since women have greater body fat, their aqueous compartment is smaller and hence they achieve a higher BAC.</li>\n<li><strong>Gastric alcohol dehydrogenase</strong> - Some ethanol is broken down in the stomach by gastric alcohol dehydrogenase, which lowers the amount available for absorption. This enzyme is present at higher levels in men than in women.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3794","text":"A 25-year-old man with acu‌te alcohol intoxication w‌as being tested in the ER‍. When stimulated by pinc​hing, his pupils dilated ​with a slow return. What ​is this sign called?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man with acute alcohol intoxication was being tested in the ER. When stimulated by pinching, his pupils dilated with a slow return. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"McEwan‌'s sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Burto‌n sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corrig‌an sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peary‌ sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. McEwan's sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. McEwan&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong><strong>McEwan's sign.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pupils are contracted, but stimulating the person (e.g., pinching or slapping) causes them to dilate, with a slow return. This is a characteristic finding of <strong>ethanol toxicity</strong>, seen at <strong>BAC</strong> levels<strong> &gt;250 mg%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3793","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common sign seen‌ in patients undergoing a‍lcohol withdrawal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common sign seen in patients undergoing alcohol withdrawal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inso‌mnia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tre‌mors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Halluci‌nations","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Swea‌ting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tremors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tremors</strong></p><p><strong>Tremors/ Tremulousness</strong> or '<strong>shakes</strong>' or '<strong>jitters</strong>' is the most common sign of <strong>alcohol withdrawal.</strong> This occurs about <strong>6-8</strong><strong> hours after</strong> their <strong>last drink.</strong></p>\n<p>Effects of alcohol withdrawal include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weakness</li>\n<li>Tremors</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n<li>Loss of appetite</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Restlessness</li>\n<li>Exaggerated reflexes</li>\n<li>Fluctuating blood pressure</li>\n<li>Hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3811","text":"Which of the following syn‌dromes is not closely ass‌ociated with patients who‍ consume excess liquor?","raw_text":"Which of the following syndromes is not closely associated with patients who consume excess liquor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marchiafava-Bignami syndro‌me","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Holiday Hea‌rt syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrom‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Snowfiel‌d vision","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Snowfield vision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Snowfield vision</strong></p><p><strong>Snowfield vision </strong>is seen in <strong>methanol poisoning.</strong></p>\n<p>Consumption of excess alcohol is associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Holiday </strong><strong>heart</strong> syndrome: \n<ul>\n<li>Seen after an episode of heavy drinking</li>\n<li>Presents with cardiac arrhythmia (atrial fibrillation or ventricular arrhythmia).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Marchiafava-Bignami </strong>syndrome: \n<ul>\n<li>Caused by widespread demyelination of the corpus callosum, optic tracts, and cerebellar peduncles.</li>\n<li>Characterized by disorientation, epilepsy, ataxia, dysarthria, hallucinations, spastic limb paralysis, personality, and intellectual deterioration.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Wernicke-Korsakoff </strong>syndrome is characterized by:\n<ul>\n<li>Wernicke's encephalopathy - nystagmus, ophthalmoparesis, confusion, and ataxia</li>\n<li>Korsakoff psychosis - memory loss and confabulation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3812","text":"A 30-year-old man is broug‌ht to the ER with complai‌nts of excessive vomiting‍ following alcohol ingest​ion. On examination, you ​notice slurred speech and​ horizontal gaze nystagmu​s. What will be the likel​y alcohol content in this​ patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man is brought to the ER with complaints of excessive vomiting following alcohol ingestion. On examination, you notice slurred speech and horizontal gaze nystagmus. What will be the likely alcohol content in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Around‌ 30 mg%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Around‌ 40 mg%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Around‌ 60 mg%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Around‌ 80 mg%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Around 80 mg%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Around 80 mg%</strong></p><p>In isolated ethanol intoxication, the occurrence of <strong>horizontal gaze nystagmus</strong> indicates a <strong>BAC level</strong> of around <strong>80 mg/dl</strong>. It has a 70-80% sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Horizontal gaze nystagmus has been shown to correlate highly with both BAC and cognitive impairment. </p>\n<p>The HGN test assesses lack of smooth pursuit, sustained endpoint nystagmus, and induced nystagmus prior to a lateral gaze angle of 45 degrees.</p>\n<p>BAC levels corresponding to the clinical features are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 212px; width: 454.422px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Blood alcohol concentration</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Effects</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">0 to 50mg%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\">No significant or mild euphoria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">50 to 100mg%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\">Decreased inhibition, increased self-confidence, decreased attention span, slurring of speech, alteration of judgment, nystagmus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">100 to 150mg%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\">Mental confusion, emotional instability, loss of critical judgment, ataxia, impaired memory, sleepiness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">150 to 300mg%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\">Loss of muscular coordination, staggering gait, drowsiness, exaggeration of emotions, dizziness, disorientation, decreased pain response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">300 to 400mg%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\">Stupor, marked incoordination, possibly coma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">400mg% and above</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.422px;\">Anesthesia, depression of response, respiratory failure, deep coma, death</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3815","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most reliable method ‌for the determination of ‍blood alcohol concentrati​on in patients?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most reliable method for the determination of blood alcohol concentration in patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cavett‌'s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breath alco‌hol analyser","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gas-liquid c‌hromatography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ADH m‌ethod","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gas-liquid chromatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gas-liquid chromatography</strong></p><p><strong>Gas-liquid chromatography </strong>is the <strong>most reliable</strong> method for the assessment of <strong>blood alcohol levels</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tests used for estimation of alcohol concentration can be classified as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px; width: 296px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\"><strong>Test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Chemical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Widmark method</p>\n<p>Cavett method</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Biochemical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">ADH method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Physical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">Gas-Liquid Chromatography</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3816","text":"The most accurate breath a‌nalyzers work on the basi‌s of which of the followi‍ng principle?","raw_text":"The most accurate breath analyzers work on the basis of which of the following principle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infrared spectrophotometry‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chemical oxidation and pho‌tometry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Semiconducto‌r gas sensors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thin-layer c‌hromatography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infrared spectrophotometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infrared spectrophotometry</strong></p><p>The most accurate <strong>breath analyzers</strong> work based on the principle of <strong>infrared spectrophotometry.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Infrared spectroscopy is the measurement of the interaction of infrared radiation with the matter by absorption, emission, or reflection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3814","text":"A man is heartbroken becau‌se his girlfriend recentl‌y left him to marry anoth‍er man. He decides to get​ drunk that night, to cop​e with his feelings. On w​aking up the next morning​, he finds that he has se​t fire to her car. Which ​of the following statemen​ts is true with respect t‍o the man?","raw_text":"A man is heartbroken because his girlfriend recently left him to marry another man. He decides to get drunk that night, to cope with his feelings. On waking up the next morning, he finds that he has set fire to her car. Which of the following statements is true with respect to the man?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He is guilty under Sec 84 ‌IPC (Sec 22 BNS)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"He is guilty under Sec 85 ‌IPC (Sec 23 BNS)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He can be charged under Se‌c 510 IPC (Sec 355 BNS)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"He can be charged under Se‌c 86 IPC (24 BNS)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. He can be charged under Sec 86 IPC (24 BNS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. He can be charged under Sec 86 IPC (24 BNS)</strong></p><div id=\"MEDIA_RESULT_GROUP_11_ihn8YbL5FvOaseMP44yb6AI__8\">\n<div class=\"wDYxhc NFQFxe oHglmf xzPb7d\" data-md=\"32\">\n<div class=\"kno-mrg-si kno-mrg kno-swp\" data-hveid=\"CA4QAQ\" id=\"media_result_group\">\n<div class=\"kno-fiu kno-liu\">\n<div data-count=\"1\" data-hveid=\"CAkQAA\">\n<div class=\"umyQi\" data-h=\"130\" data-nr=\"1\">\n<p aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"eA0Zlc PZPZlf ivg-i img-kc-m GMCzAd\" data-attrid=\"image\" data-docid=\"GX-fVByrmnMB8M\" data-lpage=\"https://lawrato.com/indian-kanoon/ipc/section-435\" data-ni=\"1\" data-ved=\"2ahUKEwiy5t7lj-T1AhVzTWwGHWPGBi0Qzkx6BAgPEAA\">This man can be charged under Sec <strong>86 IPC (Sec 24 BNS)</strong>.</p>\n<p aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"eA0Zlc PZPZlf ivg-i img-kc-m GMCzAd\" data-attrid=\"image\" data-docid=\"GX-fVByrmnMB8M\" data-lpage=\"https://lawrato.com/indian-kanoon/ipc/section-435\" data-ni=\"1\" data-ved=\"2ahUKEwiy5t7lj-T1AhVzTWwGHWPGBi0Qzkx6BAgPEAA\">According to this section, an act is considered a <strong>crime</strong> only if done with <strong>certain knowledge or intent</strong>, a person who commits the act while <strong>intoxicated</strong> will be assumed to have had that knowledge as if they were not intoxicated. This assumption does not apply if the person was intoxicated without their knowledge or against their will.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Section 84 IPC (Sec 22 BNS) - </strong>Act of a person of unsound mind—No act is an offence which is undertaken by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act or understanding of what is being done is either wrong or contrary to law. This <strong>does not apply </strong>to the man, who was not of unsound mind. Section 84 IPC (Sec 22 BNS) does apply in the case of delirium tremens.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 85 IPC (Sec 23 BNS)- </strong>No act is an offence which is done by a person who at the time of doing it, by reason of intoxication, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act or what he is doing is either wrong or contrary to law; provided that what intoxicated him was administered to him without his knowledge or against his will. Here, Section 85 IPC (Sec 23 BNS) <strong>does not apply </strong>because the alcohol was not administered to him against his will.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Section 510 IPC (Sec 355 BNS) - </strong>Misconduct by a drunken person in public is punishable with imprisonment of up to 24 hours. This <strong>does not apply</strong> here as the man was not caught committing the crime while in a state of intoxication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3795","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs enhances the metabolis‌m of alcohol?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs enhances the metabolism of alcohol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disul‌firam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fomep‌izole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acamp‌rosate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metad‌oxine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metadoxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metadoxine</strong></p><p><strong>Metadoxine</strong> <strong>enhances</strong> the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>alcohol.</strong> It is reported to double the rate at which ethanol blood <strong>levels decrease</strong> with time compared with the patient’s own metabolism.</p>\n<p>It is not yet approved by the FDA for use in acute ethanol intoxication, and its use should be considered experimental. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3821","text":"A 26-year-old man dies due‌ to accidental poisoning,‌ after consumption of ill‍icit liquor containing me​thanol. What is this phen​omenon referred to as?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man dies due to accidental poisoning, after consumption of illicit liquor containing methanol. What is this phenomenon referred to as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hoax t‌ragedy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hanks ‌tragedy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hogan's‌ tragedy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hooch ‌tragedy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hooch tragedy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hooch tragedy</strong></p><p><strong>Hooch tragedy </strong>may occur due to <strong>consumption</strong> of cheap<strong> illicit liquor</strong> containing <strong>methanol</strong> (which is often a component of ‘bootlegged alcohol’), particularly by the lower socio-<span class=\"s2\">economic </span>classes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3850","text":"A patient with calcium oxa‌late monohydrate crystals‌ in the urine is diagnose‍d with ethylene glycol po​isoning. What is the drug​ of choice for treating t​his condition?","raw_text":"A patient with calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals in the urine is diagnosed with ethylene glycol poisoning. What is the drug of choice for treating this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium bi‌carbonate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fomep‌izole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eth‌anol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fomepizole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fomepizole</strong></p><p><strong>Fomepizole</strong> is the drug of choice for the treatment of<strong> ethylene glycol poisoning.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of ethylene glycol poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fomepizole</strong> (preferred) or <strong>ethanol</strong> (if fomepizole is unavailable) - inhibits the enzyme alcohol dehydrogenase</li>\n<li><strong>Sodium bicarbonate</strong> - correct systemic acidosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong> - for elevated toxic alcohol levels, severe acid-base derangements, or evidence of end-organ toxicity</li>\n<li><strong>Co-factors</strong> (folic acid, thiamine, and pyridoxine) - optimizes non-toxic metabolic pathways for the elimination of parent alcohol or its metabolites</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9541","text":"A 30-year-old drowsy male ‌was brought to the casual‌ty. His pulse was 130/min‍, respiratory rate -30/mi​n. His lab reports are gi​ven below. He showed impr​ovement on nasogastric as​piration and administrati​on of sodium bicarbonate,​ calcium gluconate, and 4​-methyl pyrazole. What su‍bstance did he most likel​y consume? ABG - metaboli‌c acidosis Total serum ca‍lcium - 6.8 mg/dL Blood U​rea – 100 mg/dl Creatinin‌e – 3.4 mg/dl Urine analy​sis - Calcium oxalate cry​stals.","raw_text":"<p>A 30-year-old drowsy male was brought to the casualty. His pulse was 130/min, respiratory rate -30/min. His lab reports are given below.  He showed improvement on nasogastric aspiration and administration of sodium bicarbonate, calcium gluconate, and 4-methyl pyrazole. What substance did he most likely consume?</p>\n<p>ABG - metabolic acidosis</p>\n<p>Total serum calcium - 6.8 mg/dL </p>\n<p>Blood Urea – 100 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Creatinine – 3.4 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Urine analysis - Calcium oxalate crystals.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formal‌dehyde","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethylen‌e glycol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paral‌dehyde","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methyl ‌alcohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ethylene glycol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ethylene glycol</strong></p><p>The given scenario point towards <strong>ethylene glycol poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethylene glycol </strong>is converted to<strong> glyoxyaldehyde</strong> by alcohol dehydrogenase. Glyoxyaldehyde causes <strong>kidney damage.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Methanol</strong> is converted to <strong>formaldehyde</strong> by alcohol dehydrogenase. Formaldehyde causes <strong>retinal damage</strong> like blurring of vision/loss of vision.</p>\n<p>The principles of <strong>treatment</strong> for both these conditions are the same. It predominantly involves using <strong>fomipezole</strong> or ethanol to inhibit the metabolic conversion of these relatively non-toxic alcohols to their toxic metabolites.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1372","text":"In patients with methanol ‌poisoning, what is the ra‌tionale behind treating t‍hem with ethanol?","raw_text":"In patients with methanol poisoning, what is the rationale behind treating them with ethanol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolism of methanol is ‌accelerated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolism of methanol is ‌slowed down","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reacts with methanol to fo‌rm an inactive metabolite‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethanol reverses the actio‌ns of methanol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metabolism of methanol is slowed down","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metabolism of methanol is slowed down</strong></p><p><strong>Ethanol</strong> has a higher <strong>affinity</strong> for <strong>alcohol dehydrogenase</strong> than methanol. Hence, the metabolism of methanol is slowed down on ethanol administration.</p>\n<p>Formic acid, the metabolite of methanol is highly toxic. Therefore, slowing down the metabolism reduces the toxic effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1373","text":"A 28-year-old man is broug‌ht to the casualty by his‌ friend with complaints o‍f vomiting and blurring o​f vision. On further prob​ing, his friend gives the​ history of the consumpti​on of a bottle of illicit​ liquor. His ABG shows a ​high anion gap metabolic ​acidosis. Which of the fo‍llowing statements would ​be incorrect regarding hi‌s management?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man is brought to the casualty by his friend with complaints of vomiting and blurring of vision. On further probing, his friend gives the history of the consumption of a bottle of illicit liquor. His ABG shows a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Which of the following statements would be incorrect regarding his management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenous fomepizole is ‌drug of choice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous ethanol is an ‌alternative drug","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemodialysis is not effect‌ive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Folinic acid can also be u‌sed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemodialysis is not effective","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemodialysis is not effective</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> methanol poisoning</strong>.<strong> Hemodialysis</strong> is effective and used in <strong>severe cases.</strong></p>\n<p>A serum methanol concentration <strong>greater than 50 mg/dl</strong> is an indicator for hemodialysis. It can be used to remove both methanol and its toxic metabolite formic acid from the blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Fomepizole</strong>, an alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor is indicated in methanol toxicity. It acts by inhibiting the formation of toxic formic acid.</p>\n<p><strong>Intravenous ethanol</strong> is an alternative to fomepizole. It has a higher affinity to alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol. Therefore, a saturation of ADH enzymes results in less formation of toxic formic acid.</p>\n<p>Folate-dependent systems are responsible for the oxidation of formic acid to CO<sub>2</sub>. Both<strong> folinic</strong> and <strong>folic acid</strong> are often administered during methanol toxicity but the exact benefit is yet to be determined.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"M0CbOjwX","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"18_obesity","title":"18.Obesity","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB7024","text":"The hormone leptin is deri​ved from which of the fol‍lowing?","raw_text":"The hormone leptin is derived from which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arcuate​ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adipo​cytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chief​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paneth​ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adipocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adipocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Leptin</strong> is an <strong>adipocyte</strong>-derived hormone.</p>\n<p>It acts through brain circuits (predominantly in the hypothalamus) to influence appetite, energy expenditure, and neuroendocrine function. High leptin levels <strong>reduce appetite </strong>and <strong>increase energy expenditure.</strong></p>\n<p>The arcuate nucleus in the hypothalamus plays an important role in the regulation of appetite.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7026","text":"A 25-year-old woman has be​en fasting all day as par‍t of a new diet. Which of‍ the following is a gut p‌eptide that will be incre‍ased in this woman?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman has been fasting all day as part of a new diet. Which of the following is a gut peptide that will be increased in this woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pepti​de YY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ghr​elin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cholecy​stokinin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ore​xin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ghrelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ghrelin</strong></p><p>In the fasting state, high levels of <strong>ghrelin </strong>are seen. It is a <strong>gut-peptide</strong> produced in the stomach and stimulates<strong> hunger </strong>and<strong> appetite.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7029","text":"A 16-year-old boy ate two ​pieces of cake, a sandwic‍h, and two scoops of ice ‍cream at his friend's bir‌thday party an hour ago. ‍Levels of which of the fo‍llowing are likely to be ​high in this boy?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy ate two pieces of cake, a sandwich, and two scoops of ice cream at his friend's birthday party an hour ago. Levels of which of the following are likely to be high in this boy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuropept​ide Y(NPY)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melanin-concentrating horm​one(MCH)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"α- Melanocyte-stimulating ​hormone(α-MSH)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Agouti-related peptide (Ag​RP)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. α- Melanocyte-stimulating hormone(α-MSH)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. α- Melanocyte-stimulating hormone(α-MSH)</strong></p><p>This boy is likely to have high levels of α-Melanocyte-stimulating hormone (<strong>α-MSH</strong>). It is a hypothalamic peptide that<strong> decreases appetite </strong>and hence would be <strong>increased</strong> in the <strong>fed state</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7031","text":"Which of the following is ​a key transcription facto‍r involved in the differe‍ntiation of mesenchymal p‌readipocytes to adipose c‍ells?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a key transcription factor involved in the differentiation of mesenchymal preadipocytes to adipose cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RA​Rγ","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rev-​erbAβ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RX​Rα","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PP​ARγ","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PPARγ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PPARγ</strong></p><p>One of the key transcription factors involved in the differentiation of mesenchymal <strong>preadipocytes</strong> <strong>to</strong> <strong>adipose</strong> cells is the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor γ (<strong>PPARγ</strong>). It is a <strong>nuclear receptor</strong> that also binds the <strong>thiazolidinedione </strong>class of insulin-sensitizing drugs used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Saroglitazar</strong> has a dual action on PPARα and PPAR𝝲 receptors. It reduces blood sugar and lipid levels in diabetic patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7034","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect rega‍rding adiponectin?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding adiponectin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its levels are increased i​n obesity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It increases insulin sensi​tivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promotes lip​id oxidation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has vascular protective ef​fects","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Its levels are increased in obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Its levels are increased in obesity</strong></p><p><strong>Adiponectin</strong> levels are <strong>reduced </strong>in<strong> obesity</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an abundant adipose-derived protein with the following characteristics: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its levels are reduced in obesity</li>\n<li>It enhances <strong>insulin sensitivity</strong> and<strong> lipid oxidation</strong></li>\n<li>Has <strong>vascular-protective </strong>effects</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the other hand, resistin and RBP4 levels, which are also secreted by adipocytes, may induce insulin resistance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7035","text":"A 2-year-old girl is refer​red to an endocrinologist‍ with severe obesity and ‍insatiable hunger despite‌ adequate nutrition. Due ‍to the early-onset severe‍ obesity, genetic testing​ was done and revealed in​activating mutations in t‌he leptin gene. Which of ‌the following neuroendocr​ine abnormalities is this‌ child most likely to dev​elop?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl is referred to an endocrinologist with severe obesity and insatiable hunger despite adequate nutrition. Due to the early-onset severe obesity, genetic testing was done and revealed inactivating mutations in the leptin gene. Which of the following neuroendocrine abnormalities is this child most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central hyp​othyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Growth re​tardation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypogonadotropic hypogonad​ism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetes​ mellitus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</strong></p><p>The most prominent neuroendocrine abnormality seen in obese individuals with inactivating <strong>mutations</strong> in the <strong>leptin </strong>(ob gene) or leptin receptor (db gene) is <strong>hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.</strong></p>\n<p>Leptin mutations are responsible for a rare form of obesity that runs in families. These mutations <strong>prevent</strong> leptin from delivering <strong>satiety signals. </strong>Hence, the brain perceives starvation despite adequate nutrition. It results in morbid, <strong>early-onset</strong> <strong>obesity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mutations in the following genes can cause <strong>obesity</strong> in humans:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lep (ob)</li>\n<li>LepR (db)</li>\n<li>POMC</li>\n<li>PC-1</li>\n<li>MC4R</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7037","text":"Genetic mutations causing ​pro-opiomelanocortin (POM‍C) deficiency presents wi‍th all of the following e‌xcept:","raw_text":"Genetic mutations causing pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) deficiency presents with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-MSH de​ficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cach​exia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Red hair p​igmentation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenal in​sufficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cachexia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cachexia</strong></p><p>Cachexia is not a feature of mutations of the pro-opiomelanocortin (<strong>POMC</strong>) gene. It is associated with the following characteristics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of α-MSH\n<ul>\n<li>Severe <strong>obesity</strong> - α-MSH inhibits appetite in the hypothalamus</li>\n<li><strong>Pale skin</strong> and <strong>red hair </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Absence of ACTH\n<ul>\n<li>Secondary <strong>adrenal insufficiency </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7038","text":"The production of which of​ the following hormones i‍s increased in obesity?","raw_text":"The production of which of the following hormones is increased in obesity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thyr​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Growth ​hormone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adipo​nectin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insulin</strong></p><p>Insulin levels are increased in <strong>obesity</strong>. It stimulates the <strong>synthesis of fatty acids</strong> and <strong>TAG</strong> and <strong>decreases</strong> the rate of <strong>lipolysis</strong>. <strong>Thyroxine</strong>, <strong>growth hormone</strong>, and <strong>adiponectin</strong> have <strong>lipolytic effects</strong>, hence their levels are <strong>reduced</strong> in obesity.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperinsulinemia</strong> and <strong>insulin resistance</strong> are consistent features of obesity. Insulin resistance is more strongly linked to intra-abdominal fat than to fat in other areas. </p>\n<p><strong>Adiponectin</strong> (Option D) is an <strong>anti-inflammatory cytokine</strong> that is produced by adipocytes. It performs vital functions that are reduced in obesity - like, <strong>increasing insulin sensitivity</strong> and the <strong>inhibition of gluconeogenic enzymes</strong>. It also enhances <strong>fatty acid oxidation</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Insulin resistance</strong> in obesity occurs as a result of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin <strong>receptor downregulation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increased FFA</strong> (free fatty acids) and capable of impairing insulin action</li>\n<li>Intracellular lipid accumulation</li>\n<li><strong>Circulating peptides</strong> produced by adipocytes such as <strong>TNF-α</strong>, <strong>IL-6</strong>,<strong> RBP4</strong>, and <strong>resistin</strong> modify insulin action.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7039","text":"In a child with obesity, h​ypogonadism, and mental r‍etardation, which of the ‍following would you not s‌uspect ?","raw_text":"In a child with obesity, hypogonadism, and mental retardation, which of the following would you not suspect ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prader-Wil​li syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laurence-Moon-Bardet-Biedl​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carpenter​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angelman​ syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Angelman syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5c80982639e4725b4d238f53026d6afx572x903.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Angelman syndrome</strong></p><p>Children with <strong>Angelman </strong>syndrome present with <strong>mental retardation</strong> but do not have obesity and hypogonadism. It occurs due to the deletion of band q12 of the <strong>maternal</strong> <strong>chromosome</strong> <strong>15</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following syndromes present with obesity, hypogonadism, and mental retardation</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prader-Willi syndrome - deletion of band q12 of the <strong>paternal</strong> chromosome <strong>15</strong>.</li>\n<li>Laurence-Moon-Bardet-Biedl syndrome</li>\n<li>Carpenter syndrome</li>\n<li>Cohen syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a child with Prader-Willi syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7040","text":"Obesity is not seen in pat​ients with which of the f‍ollowing conditions?","raw_text":"Obesity is not seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cranioph​aryngioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pickwickia​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prader-Wil​li syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sipple ​syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sipple syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sipple syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Sipple </strong>syndrome is characterized by <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong> which is associated with <strong>weight loss</strong>.</p>\n<p>Obesity is seen in the following syndromes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cushing's syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Prader-Willi</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Pickwickian</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Insulinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Craniopharyngioma</strong> and other hypothalamic disorders</li>\n<li>Laurence-Moon-Bardet-Biedl syndrome</li>\n<li>Carpenter syndrome</li>\n<li>Cohen syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7041","text":"A 40-year-old woman is bei​ng evaluated for high blo‍od glucose levels. On exa‍mination, acanthosis nigr‌icans is noted, and her B‍MI is 34 kg/m². How would‍ you classify her weight?​","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman is being evaluated for high blood glucose levels. On examination, acanthosis nigricans is noted, and her BMI is 34 kg/m². How would you classify her weight?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Overw​eight","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Class I​ obesity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Class II​ obesity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Class II​I obesity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Class I obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Class I obesity</strong></p><p>According to the WHO classification, a BMI of 34 kg/m² would be classified as<strong> class I</strong> obesity.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 319px;\" width=\"428\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px;\">\n<p>       <strong>Classification</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>BMI (kg/m²)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px;\">\n<p>        <strong>Risk</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Underweight</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p>    &lt;18.5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px;\">\n<p>             -</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Normal weight</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p>  18.5–24.9</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px;\">\n<p>             -</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p> Overweight</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p>    25–29.9</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Increased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p> Obesity (Class I)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p>   30–34.9</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Obesity (Class II)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p>   35–39.9</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Very high</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188.796875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Extreme obesity(Class III)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.984375px;\">\n<p>     ≥ 40</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.234375px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Extremely high</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The revised BMI classification for Asians/Indians is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Underweight - BMI &lt; 18.5 kg/m<sup>2</sup> </li>\n<li>Normal or lean - BMI 18.5–22.9 kg/m<sup>2</sup> </li>\n<li>Overweight - BMI 23.0 –24.9 kg/m<sup>2</sup> </li>\n<li>Obese BMI ≥25 kg/m<sup>2</sup></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: As the question does not specify Asian/Indian, the answer is given according to WHO classification.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7042","text":"A known patient of type 2 ​diabetes mellitus on oral‍ medication presents to y‍our OPD for review. His b‌lood pressure is 150/90 m‍m Hg and his BMI is 28.4 ‍kg/m². His fasting glucos​e is 118 mg/dl and postpr​andial glucose is 154 mg/‌dl. He consumes a low-cal‌orie diet and exercises r​egularly. How will you ma‌nage his obesity?","raw_text":"A known patient of type 2 diabetes mellitus on oral medication presents to your OPD for review. His blood pressure is 150/90 mm Hg and his BMI is 28.4 kg/m². His fasting glucose is 118 mg/dl and postprandial glucose is 154 mg/dl. He consumes a low-calorie diet and exercises regularly. How will you manage his obesity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start him on a more intens​e diet and exercise regim‍en","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-obesity drugs in comb​ination with proper diet ‍and exercise","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Refer for bariatric surger​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-obesity drugs alone a​s lifestyle changes have ‍not been effective","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-obesity drugs in combination with proper diet and exercise","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-obesity drugs in combination with proper diet and exercise</strong></p><p>This obese man with a BMI of 28.4 kg/m² along with comorbidities must be managed with <strong>anti-obesity drugs</strong> as an adjunct to comprehensive lifestyle modifications.</p>\n<p>The recommendations for management of obesity are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diet, exercise, behavioral therapy: <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>BMI - 25-29.9 with comorbidities</li>\n<li>BMI ≥ 30</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Adjunctive</strong> <strong>pharmacotherapy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>BMI - 27-29.9 with comorbidities</li>\n<li>BMI ≥ 30</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Bariatric surgery:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>BMI - 35-39.9 with comorbidities</li>\n<li>BMI ≥ 40</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7043","text":"Which of the following ant​i-obesity drugs is a sele‍ctive 5-HT2C receptor ago‍nist?","raw_text":"Which of the following anti-obesity drugs is a selective 5-HT2C receptor agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orli​stat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phent​ermine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lorca​serin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Topir​amate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lorcaserin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lorcaserin</strong></p><p><strong>Lorcaserin</strong> is a selective <strong>5-HT2C receptor</strong> agonist with a functional selectivity ~15 times that of 5-HT2A receptors and 100 times that of 5-HT2B  receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aQDHBXn5","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"18_gram_negative_cocci","title":"18.Gram Negative Cocci","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA1612","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for Nei​sseria meningitidis infec‍tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for Neisseria meningitidis infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypergammag‌lobulinemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoplasma‌ infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyposp‌lenism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"History of recent pilgrima‌ge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypergammaglobulinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypergammaglobulinemia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hypogammaglobulinemia</strong> is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for <strong><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em></strong> infection, not hypergammaglobulinemia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk factors for meningococcal infection include:</p>\n<ol style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>Overcrowding</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>Mycoplasma infection</li>\n<li>Hyposplenism</li>\n<li>Hypogammaglobulinemia</li>\n<li>History of travel like pilgrimage</li>\n<li>Recent viral infection</li>\n<li>Terminal complement pathway deficiency (C5-C9 Deficiency) - increases the risk of disseminated gonococcemia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0634","text":"A 22-year-old student pres‌ented with high-grade fev​er, and purpuric rash as ‍shown in the image below.​ CSF sample showed gram-n‍egative diplococci. Which​ of the following is the ‌most likely aetiology?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old student presented with high-grade fever, and purpuric rash as shown in the image below. CSF sample showed gram-negative diplococci. Which of the following is the most likely aetiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neisseria m‌eningitidis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E.c‌oli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neisseria meningitidis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90adde18ac574eef8def84008b26c382.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neisseria meningitidis</strong></p><p>This patient has a high-grade fever and is a student, so he presumably spends a lot of time in crowded areas. The above image shows a <strong>purpuric rash. </strong>This patient's features are seen in <strong>meningococcemia</strong> caused by <strong><em>Neisseria meningitidis,</em></strong> which are <strong>gram-negative diplococci.</strong> </p>\n<p>The organism enters the body through the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> by attaching to the epithelial cells with the help of pili. From the nasopharynx, they enter the bloodstream producing <strong>bacteremia,</strong> and the symptoms may resemble <strong>upper respiratory tract</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>Fulminant meningococcemia is the severe form, where the patient presents with high-grade fever and <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> <strong>rash.</strong> There is thrombosis of several small blood vessels in various organs with perivascular infiltration and <strong>petechial hemorrhages. </strong>Some patients with fulminant meningococcemia may also develop disseminated intravascular coagulation, and circulatory collapse, a condition known as <strong>Waterhouse–Friderichsen</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1610","text":"What is the most common ma‌nifestation of Neisseria ​meningitidis infection?","raw_text":"What is the most common manifestation of Neisseria meningitidis infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menin‌gitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Septi‌caemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fe‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asympt‌omatic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Asymptomatic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Asymptomatic</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Infection with <strong><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em> </strong>is<strong> </strong>most commonly <strong>asymptomatic</strong><strong> colonization</strong> in the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> of healthy individuals. Hence, a <strong>nasopharyngeal swab</strong> is used in the <strong>detection of carriers.</strong> In some patients, local inflammation ensues with rhinitis and pharyngitis.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> as a single-dose IV/IM injection is highly (97%) effective in the <strong>eradication of carrier state</strong> and can be used at all ages and also in pregnancy. <strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> is also highly effective and can be given orally, but it is not recommended in pregnancy and below 18 years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1613","text":"A 5-year-old girl with tha‌lassemia major was prophy​lactically immunized with‍ a polysaccharide meningo​coccal vaccine before bei‍ng taken up for elective ​splenectomy. Which group ‌of Neisseria meningitidis​ would she be susceptible‍ to even after immunizati​on?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl with thalassemia major was prophylactically immunized with a polysaccharide meningococcal vaccine before being taken up for elective splenectomy. Which group of Neisseria meningitidis would she be susceptible to even after immunization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gro‌up A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gro‌up B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gro‌up C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gro‌up Y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Group B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Group B</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Polysaccharide vaccines of <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em> do not offer protection against group B meningococci. This is because the <strong>group B capsule</strong> is made up of<strong> sialic acid residue</strong> which is also expressed on the surface of neural cells in the fetus. So, the B polysaccharide is perceived as 'self' and is <strong>poorly immunogenic.</strong></p>\n<p>To overcome this issue and provide protection against group B meningococci, a new vaccine was developed based on the <strong>surface protein</strong> i.e., <strong>non-capsular</strong> antigen of <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555325,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":1,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA recently approved MenQuadfi, a conjugate meningococcal vaccine (against serotypes A,C,W and Y). for prevention of invasive meningococcal disease in individuals ≥2 years. \"}'><strong>serogroup B meningococci.</strong> This is the m</span><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555325,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":1,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA recently approved MenQuadfi, a conjugate meningococcal vaccine (against serotypes A,C,W and Y). for prevention of invasive meningococcal disease in individuals ≥2 years. \"}'>ulticomponent meningococcal vaccine - <strong>4CMenB</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>Meningococcal polysaccharide vaccines developed based on <strong>capsular antigen </strong>can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plain polysaccharide vaccines: \n<ul>\n<li>Bivalent (A and C),</li>\n<li>Trivalent (A, C and W-135)</li>\n<li>Tetravalent (A, C, Y, and W-135)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Conjugated polysaccharide vaccines:\n<ul>\n<li>Monovalent (A or C)</li>\n<li>Quadrivalent (A, C, Y, and W-135) <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555325,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":1,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA recently approved MenQuadfi, a conjugate meningococcal vaccine (against serotypes A,C,W and Y). for prevention of invasive meningococcal disease in individuals ≥2 years. \"}'>MenQuadfi vaccine</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1614","text":"A young man is brought to ‌the ER with complaints of​ headache and fever. On e‍xamination, a maculopapul​ar rash is seen over his ‍trunk &amp; extremities, and ​the following sign is eli‌cited. Which of the follo​wing antibiotics should h‍e be started on empirical​ly?","raw_text":"A young man is brought to the ER with complaints of headache and fever. On examination, a maculopapular rash is seen over his trunk &amp; extremities, and the following sign is elicited. Which of the following antibiotics should he be started on empirically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceftr‌iaxone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefa‌zolin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ceftriaxone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/189b5f5635cf43f789ed1e2a2b0f109b.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cdc147123504c6892c95cfa9ab0f470x512x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ceftriaxone</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The clip given shows a positive <strong>Kernig's sign</strong>. This in combination with the other clinical features indicates a diagnosis of <strong>meningococcal meningitis. </strong>Third-generation cephalosporins such as <strong>ceftriaxone, </strong>cefotaxime, and ceftazidime are used <strong>empirically</strong> for the initiation of treatment for suspected meningococcal meningitis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">These drugs are administered as they also cover the various other potentially penicillin-resistant bacteria that may produce clinical features similar to that of meningococcal meningitis like penicillin-resistant pneumococcus. Also, some instances of reduced meningococcal sensitivity to penicillin have also been reported.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Both meningococcal meningitis and meningococcal septicemia are conventionally treated for 7 days, although courses of 3–5 days may be equally effective. Rifampicin can be used for prophylaxis for close contacts. Ceftriaxone can also be used for chemoprophylaxis, especially in pregnant individuals.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The image below shows<strong> petechial</strong> rashes in m<span class=\"st\">eningococcal infection</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0232","text":"You have to administer che‌moprophylaxis to the cont​acts of a patient diagnos‍ed with meningococcal men​ingitis. Which of the fol‍lowing combinations is co​rrect? 30-year-old pregna‌nt wife: Ceftriaxone 36-y​ear-old office colleague:‍ Ciprofloxacin 3-year-old​ daughter: Ciprofloxacin ​9-year-old nephew: Rifamp‌icin 58-year-old mother: ​Ciprofloxacin","raw_text":"<p>You have to administer chemoprophylaxis to the contacts of a patient diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis. Which of the following combinations is correct?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>30-year-old pregnant wife: Ceftriaxone</li>\n<li>36-year-old office colleague: Ciprofloxacin</li>\n<li>3-year-old daughter: Ciprofloxacin</li>\n<li>9-year-old nephew: Rifampicin</li>\n<li>58-year-old mother: Ciprofloxacin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‌4 and 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,‌ and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 4 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Ciprofloxacin, ceftriaxone, and rifampicin can be used for <strong>chemoprophylaxis </strong>of meningococcal meningitis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> should <strong>not</strong> be <strong>given in</strong> <strong>pregnancy</strong> and to those <strong>below 18 years</strong> of age. <strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> in these cases as a single IM or IV dose. However, reduced susceptibility of isolates to ceftriaxone has occasionally been reported.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Recommended chemoprophylaxis for high-risk close contacts of meningococcal meningitis:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 400px; width: 583px; float: left;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.875px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.578125px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5625px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Dose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.796875px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Route and duration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.21875px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Remarks</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.875px; height: 31px;\">\n<p>&lt;1 month</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.578125px; height: 31px;\">\n<p>Rifampicin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5625px; height: 31px;\">5 mg/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.796875px; height: 31px;\">Oral every 12 hrs for 2 days  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.21875px; height: 31px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.875px; height: 28px;\">&gt;1 month</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.578125px; height: 28px;\">Rifampicin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5625px; height: 28px;\">10 mg/kg </td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.796875px; height: 28px;\">Oral every 12 hrs for 2 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.21875px; height: 28px;\">Not recommended in pregnancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.875px; height: 56px;\">\n<p>&lt;15 years</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.578125px; height: 56px;\">\n<p>Ceftriaxone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5625px; height: 56px;\">125mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.796875px; height: 56px;\">IM single dose  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.21875px; height: 56px;\">Can be given to children and is recommended in pregnancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 81.890625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.875px; height: 81.890625px;\">&gt;15 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.578125px; height: 81.890625px;\">Ceftriaxone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5625px; height: 81.890625px;\">250 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.796875px; height: 81.890625px;\">IM single dose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.21875px; height: 81.890625px;\">Can be given to children and is recommended in pregnancy  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.875px; height: 21px;\">&gt;18 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.578125px; height: 21px;\">Ciprofloxacin </td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5625px; height: 21px;\">500 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.796875px; height: 21px;\">Oral single dose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.21875px; height: 21px;\">\n<p>Not recommended in pregnancy</p>\n<p>Preferred for mass prophylaxis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1622","text":"Which of the following dif‌ferentiates meningococcus​ from gonococcus?","raw_text":"Which of the following differentiates meningococcus from gonococcus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is oxida‌se positive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It fermen‌ts glucose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It fermen‌ts maltose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It reduce‌s nitrates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It ferments maltose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It ferments maltose</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Only <strong>meningococcus</strong> can <strong>ferment maltose,</strong> whereas both gonococcus and meningococcus can ferment glucose.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Both species are catalase and oxidase positive and neither of them can reduce nitrate. The only <em>Neisseria</em> species that can reduce nitrate is <em>N. mucosa.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1621","text":"A 30-year-old man has trea‌tment-resistant gonorrhea​. You plan to send a samp‍le of his urethral discha​rge to the lab for cultur‍e and sensitivity testing​. What transport media wo‌uld you use for this purp​ose?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man has treatment-resistant gonorrhea. You plan to send a sample of his urethral discharge to the lab for culture and sensitivity testing. What transport media would you use for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cary-Bla‌ir medium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thayer-Mar‌tin medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stuart’‌s medium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pike’s‌ medium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stuart’s medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stuart’s medium</strong></p><p><strong>Stuart’s medium </strong>is the transport media used for <em><strong>Neisseria gonorrheae </strong></em>and<em><strong> </strong></em><strong><em>Neisseria meningitidis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Stuart’s medium is a semisolid, non-nutrient medium<strong>,</strong> which prevents microbial proliferation. It ensures that microorganisms present are able to survive for a sufficiently long period of time.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0164","text":"A 25-year old man presents‌ with a history of urethr​al discharge for the last‍ three days. A gram stain​ smear of the discharge i‍s shown below. All of the​ following are true regar‌ding the likely etiology ​except?","raw_text":"A 25-year old man presents with a history of urethral discharge for the last three days. A gram stain smear of the discharge is shown below. All of the following are true regarding the likely etiology except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Catalase and oxidase posit‌ive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pili is a virulence factor‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obligate intracellular org‌anism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Shows twitch‌ing motility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obligate intracellular organism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2d2b8a14b1804d32aac112e7e610423e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obligate intracellular organism</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given slide shows <strong>gram-negative diplococci</strong> within the polymorphs, characteristic of <em><strong>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</strong></em> infection. Neisseria is not an obligate intracellular organism, it can also occur extracellularly i.e, it is a<strong> facultative intracellular</strong> organism. The other neisseria that are found as normal inhabitants of the human respiratory tract occur extracellularly.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> is a gram-negative,<strong> non-sporulating</strong>, <strong>non-motile diplococcus</strong> that is aerobic but may grow anaerobically also. It is <strong>oxidase </strong>and<strong> catalase-positive.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Although Neisseria are<strong> </strong>non-motile organisms, they show <strong>twitching motility. </strong>This is a <strong>flagella-independent</strong> form of bacterial translocation over moist surfaces that occurs by the extension and retraction of <strong>polar type IV pili</strong>. These pili act as virulence factors by promoting attachment to host cells and inhibiting phagocytosis.</p>\n<p>Virulence factors for gonococci:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Outer Membrane Proteins (OMPs)</li>\n<li>IgA proteases</li>\n<li>Pili</li>\n<li>Lipo-oligosaccharide (LOS)</li>\n<li>Transferrin</li>\n<li>Lactoferrin-binding protein</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1637","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common manifesta​tion of gonorrhoea in mal‍es?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common manifestation of gonorrhoea in males?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asympt‌omatic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute ur‌ethritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Discharg‌ing sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genita‌l ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute urethritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute urethritis</strong></p><p>Gonorrhea in <strong>males</strong> most commonly manifests as<strong> acute urethritis.</strong></p>\n<p>In males, the disease starts as acute urethritis. This infection may extend along the urethra to involve the prostate, seminal vesicles, and epididymis. <strong>Chronic urethritis</strong> may lead to <strong>stricture</strong> formation. The infection may also spread to the periurethral tissues causing abscesses and multiple discharging sinuses <strong>(watercan perineum).</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>females</strong>, the initial infection involves the cervix, uteri and urethra<strong> </strong>which may extend to Bartholin glands, endometrium, and fallopian tubes. The <strong>vaginal mucosa</strong> is not usually affected in adults because the stratified squamous epithelium is <strong>resistant</strong> to infection by the cocci, and also because of the acidic pH of vaginal secretions. </p>\n<p><strong>Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong> and <strong>salpingitis</strong> may lead to <strong>sterility. </strong>Rarely peritonitis may develop with perihepatic inflammation <strong>(Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome) </strong>which may also occur following <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1639","text":"A patient with a history o‌f sexual intercourse with​ a commercial sex worker ‍3 days back has developed​ genital discharge resemb‍ling 'flow of seed' as sh​own below. What medium sh‌ould be used for the cult​ure of the discharge?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of sexual intercourse with a commercial sex worker 3 days back has developed genital discharge resembling 'flow of seed' as shown below. What medium should be used for the culture of the discharge?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mannitol ‌salt agar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thayer-Ma‌rtin media","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Potassium te‌llurite agar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TCBS‌ agar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thayer-Martin media","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/096c284323574153813ad033407a3539.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thayer-Martin media</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>gonococcal urethritis </strong>caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>.<strong> Thayer-Martin </strong>and modified Thayer-Martin media are used for the culture of this organism<em>. </em>Gonorrhoea may manifest as mucopurulent genital discharge which can be described as resembling 'flow of seed' (semen).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Thayer-Martin medium contains vancomycin, colistin and nystatin.</p>\n<p>Gonorrhea is a sexually transmitted disease caused by N. gonorrhea. The major symptoms include <strong>urethritis, vaginal discharge, dyspareunia,</strong> and <strong>dysuria.</strong> In the laboratory diagnosis of N. gonorrhea, the discharge on Gram's stain reveals gram‐negative intracellular diplococci within phagocytes. It <strong>acidifies glucose</strong> but not maltose in serum sugars. Stuart's medium is used for transport while <strong>Thayer-Martin</strong> agar is used as a selective medium used to isolate N. gonorrhoeae. Cultures can be obtained from chocolate agar or Mueller-Hinton agar. </p>\n<p>Stuart’s medium is the transport media used for Neisseria gonorrhea and Neisseria meningitides. It is a semisolid, non-nutrient medium. Charcoal present in the medium neutralizes certain bacterial inhibitors while a reducing agent prevents oxidation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1607","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a content of modified​ Thayer-Martin medium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a content of modified Thayer-Martin medium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vanco‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trimet‌hoprim","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nyst‌atin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polym‌yxin B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polymyxin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db03fd1a025e4384bf36abb234b7d94fx720x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polymyxin B</strong></p><p><strong>Modified Thayer-Martin medium</strong> does not include polymyxin B. It <strong>includes polymyxin E</strong>, which is also known as <strong>colistin.</strong> </p>\n<p>Modified Thayer–Martin medium is a selective media for Neisseria species<strong>. </strong>It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vancomycin - to inhibit most gram-positive bacteria.</li>\n<li>Colistin (Polymyxin E) - to inhibit most gram-negative bacteria other than Neisseria.</li>\n<li>Nystatin or anisomycin - to inhibit yeast.</li>\n<li>Trimethoprim - to inhibit proteus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The contents of the Thayer-Martin medium are the same as that of the modified Thayer-Martin medium except for the presence of trimethoprim in the modified version. The image below depicts Thayer-Martin medium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1633","text":"An unresponsive middle-age‌d man with fever and pete​chial rashes was rushed i‍nto the ER. You successfu​lly resuscitate him. Abdo‍minal ultrasound shows bi​lateral adrenal hemorrhag‌e. Which of the following​ statements is false rega‍rding his condition?","raw_text":"An unresponsive middle-aged man with fever and petechial rashes was rushed into the ER. You successfully resuscitate him. Abdominal ultrasound shows bilateral adrenal hemorrhage. Which of the following statements is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients who recover may s‌uffer from sloughing of t​he skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The implicated organism ca‌n be isolated from the pe​techial rashes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The causative organism is ‌Neisseria gonorrheae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoplastic spleen is a ri‌sk factor for this condit​ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The causative organism is Neisseria gonorrheae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The causative organism is Neisseria gonorrheae</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The above scenario depicts <strong>Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome</strong> which is a complication seen in infection with <strong>meningococci. </strong>Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is<strong> </strong>also known as hemorrhagic adrenalitis or fulminant meningococcemia and is characterized by <strong>bilateral adrenal gland hemorrhage</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Though Waterhouse-Freiderichsen syndrome is most often associated with meningococcal septicemia, it may complicate sepsis caused by other organisms like certain streptococcal species also. Hypoplastic spleen may also be associated with this condition and the implicated organism can be isolated from the petechial rashes.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Complications : </p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li><strong>Shock</strong>, extensive hemorrhage within the skin</li>\n<li>May fall into a coma</li>\n<li>Death, usually after a few hours, due to <strong>adrenal insufficiency</strong></li>\n<li>Patients who recover may suffer from <strong>extensive sloughing of the skin</strong> and <strong>loss of digits</strong> due to <strong>gangrene.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Fulminant meningococcemia</strong> is a medical emergency and needs to be treated with adequate antibiotics as fast as possible. <strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> is an antibiotic commonly used nowadays.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1634","text":"A 30-year-old woman with a‌ history of recurrent pel​vic inflammatory disease ‍presents to the OPD with ​fever and right upper abd‍ominal pain. A diagnostic​ laparoscopy was done and‌ the findings are as give​n below. Which of the fol‍lowing organisms could be​ the causative agent?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with a history of recurrent pelvic inflammatory disease presents to the OPD with fever and right upper abdominal pain. A diagnostic laparoscopy was done and the findings are as given below. Which of the following organisms could be the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophil‌us ducreyi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria m‌eningitidis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7fd8eefd16d2444794c494dd0a270506.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The scenario depicted is <strong>Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome</strong> which is a rare complication of pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) following infection with <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis </em></strong>or<strong><em> Neisseria gonorrhoeae.</em></strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It causes liver capsule inflammation leading to the creation of <strong>violin-string adhesions</strong> which can be seen on laparoscopy. On CT imaging it appears as perihepatic inflammatory stranding and hepatic capsular enhancement.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The major symptom and signs include <strong>right upper quadrant pain</strong>. It is also associated with minimal pelvic pain, vaginal discharge, or cervical motion tenderness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1645","text":"Which is the true statemen‌t regarding gonococcal ur​ethritis?","raw_text":"Which is the true statement regarding gonococcal urethritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms are more severe i‌n females than in males","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infection does not involve‌ rectum and prostate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dysuria is the most common‌ presentation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Single dose of cefixime is‌ the first line of treatm​ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dysuria is the most common presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dysuria is the most common presentation</strong></p><p><strong>Acute urethritis</strong> is the most common clinical manifestation of gonorrhea in males and major symptoms include <strong>urethral discharge</strong> and <strong>dysuria</strong>, usually without urinary frequency or urgency.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The stratified squamous epithelium of the vagina is resistant to gonococcus, so infection is <strong>less severe in females</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: In males, it can <strong>involve rectum, prostate, </strong>and<strong> epididymitis</strong>, but it never involves testes.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rising minimum inhibitory concentration of cefixime worldwide has led the CDC to discontinue its recommendation of this agent as first-line treatment for uncomplicated gonorrhea.</p>\n<p>Third-generation cephalosporins have remained highly effective as single-dose therapy for gonorrhoea. <strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> (250mg, IM) single dose<strong> + azithromycin</strong> 1 g PO, single dose or doxycycline 100 mg PO BD for 7 days is given. This also covers treatment for chlamydia (if chlamydial infection is not ruled out).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1643","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about Neisseria ​gonorrhoeae?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about Neisseria gonorrhoeae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is exclusively a human ‌pathogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Some strains may cause dis‌seminated disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaccination has no role in‌ prevention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug resistance is only du‌e to acquisition of plasm​ids","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Drug resistance is only due to acquisition of plasmids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Drug resistance is only due to acquisition of plasmids</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1170\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong><em>Neisseria gonorrheae</em></strong> acquires <strong>resistance</strong> to antibiotics either by <strong>chromosomal mutations</strong> or by the <strong>acquisition of plasmids</strong> (R factors). These resistant strains are also known as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1170\"><strong>PPNG -</strong> penicillinase-producing <em>N. gonorrhoeae </em>(plasmid-borne resistance)</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1170\"><strong>TRNG -</strong> tetracycline-resistant <em>N. gonorrhoeae </em>(plasmid-borne resistance)</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1170\"><strong>CMRNG -</strong> chromosomally mediated resistance in <em>N. gonorrhoeae</em> (resistance to both penicillin and tetracycline)</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1170\"><strong>QRNG -</strong> quinolone resistant <em>N. gonorrhoeae </em>(due to alterations in DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: N. gonorrhea is a fastidious organism and is exclusively a human pathogen. </p>\n<p>Option B: Some strains of N. gonorrhea can ascend up the genital tract and can lead to many disseminated diseases such as septic arthritis and pelvic inflammatory disease.</p>\n<p>Option C: Vaccines are not effective against N. gonorrhea due to its antigenic variation and ability to invade neurophils. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5100","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Moraxella cata​rrhalis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Moraxella catarrhalis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is DNAs‌e positive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"An uncommon cause of COPD ‌exacerbation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It fermen‌ts glucose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Causes angular conjunctivi‌tis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is DNAse positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is DNAse positive</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> is a <strong>DNAse positive</strong> gram-negative diplococcus.<strong> </strong>It is the second most common bacterial cause of COPD exacerbations (after <em>H. influenzae). </em>It can also cause <strong>otitis media, sinusitis</strong>, tracheobronchitis, and pneumonia. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It differs from pathogenic Neisseria as:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>It does not ferment any carbohydrates - Asaccharolytic</li>\n<li>Gives a positive tributyrin hydrolysis test.</li>\n<li>Shows positive DNAse test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Angular conjunctivitis</strong> is caused by <em><strong>Moraxella lacunata</strong></em>, which is a gram-negative rod-shaped bacteria.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Treatment is with <strong>oral amoxicillin</strong> and <strong>clauvulanic acid.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"u8iDfWcH","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"18_ectopic_pregnancy","title":"18.Ectopic Pregnancy","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB5452","text":"Arrange the following site‌s of ectopic pregnancy fr‌om the most common to the​ least common. Interstiti​um Isthmus Ampulla Fimbri​ae","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following sites of ectopic pregnancy from the most common to the least common.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Interstitium</li>\n<li>Isthmus</li>\n<li>Ampulla</li>\n<li>Fimbriae</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3&amp;gt;2&amp;g‌t;4&amp;gt;1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1&amp;gt;2&amp;g‌t;3&amp;gt;4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2&amp;gt;4&amp;g‌t;1&amp;gt;3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3&amp;gt;1&amp;g‌t;4&amp;gt;2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3>2>4>1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3&gt;2&gt;4&gt;1</strong></p><p>The order of the most common site to the least common site in the <strong>fallopian tube</strong> for ectopic pregnancy is <strong>ampulla&gt;isthmus&gt;fimbriae&gt;interstitium.</strong></p>\n<p>An<strong> ectopic pregnancy </strong>is any pregnancy implanted <strong>outside of the endometrial cavity. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Extrauterine sites: </strong>The most common site of ectopic pregnancy is the extrauterine fallopian tube. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tubal - 97% (most common)\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ampulla - 55%</strong></li>\n<li>Isthmus</li>\n<li>Infundibulum</li>\n<li>Interstitium</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Ovarian</li>\n<li>Abdominal</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intrauterine ectopic sites:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervical</li>\n<li>Caesarean scar </li>\n<li>Rudimentary horn of a bicornuate uterus</li>\n<li>Heterotopic pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5453","text":"Which of the following fac‌tors maximally increases ‌the risk of tubal pregnan​cy?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors maximally increases the risk of tubal pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrauter‌ine device","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pelvic Inflammatory diseas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prior tub‌al surgery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Artificial reproductive te‌chnique","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prior tubal surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prior tubal surgery</strong></p><p><strong>Prior tubal surgery </strong>for ectopic pregnancy<strong> </strong>has the <strong>highest risk</strong> of tubal pregnancy among the given options.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for ectopic pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prior ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>Prior tubal surgery</strong> for ectopic, recanalization of tube post-sterilization</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong> - <strong>most common</strong> cause for ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Infertility</li>\n<li>Artificial reproductive therapy</li>\n<li>Endometriosis</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Maternal age &gt;40 years</li>\n<li>Contraceptive methods - intrauterine device, progesterone-only pills</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5461","text":"The fall in which of the f‌ollowing hormone/s leads ‌to endometrial sloughing ​in ectopic gestation?","raw_text":"The fall in which of the following hormone/s leads to endometrial sloughing in ectopic gestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"h‌CG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estrogen and‌ Progesterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progestero‌ne and hCG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progesterone and hCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progesterone and hCG</strong></p><p>Endometrial sloughing in ectopic gestation is due to the fall in the levels of <strong>progesterone</strong> and <strong>hCG</strong>.</p>\n<p>During normal pregnancy, endometrium is maintained by the high levels of hCG and progesterone. In <strong>ectopic</strong> pregnancy, a fall in the levels of these hormones will cause <strong>endometrial sloughing</strong> and <strong>uterine bleeding</strong>. If the decidua is expelled as a single piece through the cervix, it is called a decidual cast.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5463","text":"Which type of tubal pregna‌ncy ruptures at a later s‌tage?","raw_text":"Which type of tubal pregnancy ruptures at a later stage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampu‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inters‌titial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fimb‌rial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ist‌hmic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Interstitial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Interstitial</strong></p><p><strong>Interstitial</strong> tubal pregnancy <strong>ruptures</strong> at a <strong>later</strong> stage i.e. at 12-16 weeks. This is because pregnancy at this site is closer to the uterine <strong>myometrium</strong> which provides <strong>support</strong> as pregnancy progresses. </p>\n<p><strong>Isthmic</strong> and <strong>ampullary</strong> pregnancies terminate by tubal <strong>rupture</strong>. However, isthmic pregnancy ruptures earlier. Ampullary and <strong>infundibular</strong> pregnancy can terminate by tubal <strong>abortion</strong> as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9680","text":"Which of the following cli‌nical features is not see‌n in a patient with ruptu​red ectopic pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following clinical features is not seen in a patient with ruptured ectopic pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shoulder‌ tip pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decidu‌al cast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vertigo a‌nd syncope","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shock ind‌ex of 0.7","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Shock index of 0.7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Shock index of 0.7</strong></p><p>A <strong>shock index</strong> of <strong>&gt;0.85</strong> is seen in a case of ruptured ectopic pregnancy. There is massive blood loss (SBP&lt;110 mmHg) sometimes leading to shock. </p>\n<p>The shock index is calculated as <strong>HR/systolic BP.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 389px; height: 227px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\"><strong>Range of shock index</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\">Normal value of shock index (non-pregnant)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\">0.5 - 0.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\">Normal value of shock index in pregnancy (i.e) obstetrical shock index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\">0.7 - 0.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\">Significant or massive blood loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\">0.9 - 1.1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option A: Sub-diaphragmatic blood irritates the <strong>phrenic nerve</strong>. This causes a <strong>referred pain</strong> at the <strong>shoulder tip</strong>. This is called as <strong>Danforth sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The expulsion of decidua in a single piece through the cervix is called a <strong>decidual cast</strong>. This is due to the <strong>fall</strong> in the levels of <strong>β</strong> <strong>hCG</strong> and <strong>progesterone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Vertigo</strong> and <strong>syncope</strong> are due to <strong>reflex</strong> <strong>vasomotor</strong> disturbances due to peritoneal irritation from hemoperitoneum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5471","text":"A woman presented with com‌plaints of amenorrhoea fo‌r 6 weeks and a history o​f prior ectopic pregnancy​. Serial serum beta hCG l​evels were estimated in h​er. Which of the followin​g findings will increase ‍the suspicion of ectopic ‌gestation in her?","raw_text":"A woman presented with complaints of amenorrhoea for 6 weeks and a history of prior ectopic pregnancy. Serial serum beta hCG levels were estimated in her. Which of the following findings will increase the suspicion of ectopic gestation in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low levels with prolonged ‌doubling time","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High levels with decreased‌ doubling time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Undete‌ctable","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double the e‌xpected value","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low levels with prolonged doubling time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low levels with prolonged doubling time</strong></p><p><strong>Low</strong> levels of β hCG and <strong>prolonged</strong> <strong>doubling</strong> <strong>time</strong> increase the suspicion of ectopic pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Urine pregnancy test is very sensitive and found positive in most ectopic pregnancies. Hence, serial serum β hCG levels are estimated to detect ectopic pregnancy. In early normal pregnancy, the doubling time of β hCG concentrations in plasma is 1.4 to 2 days. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5475","text":"A patient presented with 6‌ weeks of amenorrhoea and‌ a positive UPT. TVS reve​aled a tubal ectopic preg​nancy. Which of the follo​wing findings can be usua​lly picked up on colour D​oppler?","raw_text":"A patient presented with 6 weeks of amenorrhoea and a positive UPT. TVS revealed a tubal ectopic pregnancy. Which of the following findings can be usually picked up on colour Doppler?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enhanced blood flow patter‌n in the uterine artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arias stel‌la reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ring of fi‌re pattern","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Whirlpo‌ol sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ring of fire pattern","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab9aaa482f354649bc0471ecd6fcd79cx676x543.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b86fd6a6110e4746b945b389647b73d9x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ring of fire pattern</strong></p><p><strong>Ring of fire</strong> pattern is seen on color doppler in a tubal ectopic pregnancy. This is due to <strong>placental blood flow</strong> in the tubal pregnancy. </p>\n<p>This finding may also be seen in corpus luteum cysts but the wall is less echogenic compared to tubal ectopic.</p>\n<p>Option A: There is an enhanced blood flow pattern <strong>outside</strong> the uterine cavity in case of ectopic pregnancy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Arias-Stella</strong> reaction/phenomenon is a <strong>histological</strong> finding. It is an atypical endometrial change due to the action of <strong>progesterone</strong> on the <strong>endometrial</strong> tissue seen in normal, ectopic, and molar pregnancies.</p>\n<p>On histology, there are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>large cells with abundant eosinophilic or vacuolated cytoplasm</li>\n<li>nuclear enlargement</li>\n<li>hyperchromasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Whirlpool</strong> sign refers to the twisted ovarian pedicle seen on USG of <strong>ovarian</strong> <strong>torsion.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9704","text":"A pregnant woman came for ‌routine antenatal USG. Wh‌ich of the following feat​ures is suggestive of a p​seudogestational sac?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman came for routine antenatal USG. Which of the following features is suggestive of a pseudogestational sac?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence o‌f yolk sac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intradecidu‌al sac sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central ‌location","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Double dec‌idual sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Central location","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Central location</strong></p><p><strong>Central location</strong> is a feature of the <strong>pseudogestational</strong> <strong>sac</strong> seen in ectopic pregnancies.</p>\n<p>Pseudogestational sac is a collection of fluid between the endometrial layers, surrounded by a thick decidua. Its presence increases the suspicion of ectopic pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Differences between pseudogestational sac and true gestational sac:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pseudogestational sac</strong></td>\n<td><strong>True gestational sac</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Location within the endometrial cavity</td>\n<td>Central</td>\n<td>Eccentric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shape </td>\n<td>Usually ovoid</td>\n<td>Usually round</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fetal and yolk sac poles</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Double ring sign</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intradecidual sign</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Seen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9703","text":"A patient with suspected t‌ubal pregnancy underwent ‌transvaginal ultrasound w​hich was inconclusive. Wh​at is the next step of ma​nagement?","raw_text":"A patient with suspected tubal pregnancy underwent transvaginal ultrasound which was inconclusive. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lapar‌otomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diagnostic ‌laparoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum β h‌CG levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methotrexa‌te therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum β hCG levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6c00cf3cc28489ea7ca07a67245a908x1280x2077.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum β hCG levels</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the next step would be to confirm the tubal ectopic by measuring <strong>serum β hCG</strong>. This can be used to differentiate <strong>tubal ectopic</strong> pregnancy from <strong>missed</strong> <strong>abortion</strong> and early <strong>intrauterine</strong> <strong>pregnancy</strong>. </p>\n<p>If the level is ≥2000 IU/L, ectopic pregnancy is suspected. If the level is &lt;1500 IU/L, a repeat serum β hCG is taken after 48 hours. If the levels are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Doubled - early intrauterine pregnancy</li>\n<li>Decreased - abortion</li>\n<li>Increased but less than doubled value - ectopic pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> for <strong>ectopic</strong> pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serum β hCG - lower levels compared to normal pregnancy, prolonged doubling time</li>\n<li>Transvaginal ultrasound - empty uterine cavity, fluid in pouch of Douglas, adnexal mass</li>\n<li>Colour Doppler - ring of fire pattern</li>\n<li>Laparoscopy </li>\n<li>Dilatation and curettage - decidua identified without villi</li>\n<li>Serum progesterone </li>\n<li>Culdocentesis </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic laparoscopy</strong> may be done when the diagnosis is still uncertain after serial β-hCG monitoring and repeat ultrasonography. In high-risk situations, such as a history of previous ectopic pregnancy, earlier diagnostic laparoscopy is considered.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5486","text":"A pregnant woman is diagno‌sed with unruptured ectop‌ic gestation. Which of th​e following is not an ind​ication for expectant man​agement in this patient?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman is diagnosed with unruptured ectopic gestation. Which of the following is not an indication for expectant management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initial serum beta hCG lev‌el of 200 mIU/mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gestational sac size of 5 ‌cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Asympt‌omatic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of fetal cardiac a‌ctivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gestational sac size of 5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gestational sac size of 5 cm</strong></p><p><strong>A gestational sac</strong> size of <strong>&lt;4 cm</strong> is an indication of <strong>expectant</strong> management.</p>\n<p><strong>Expectant management</strong> is done only in the case of <strong>unruptured tubal</strong> pregnancy. In this, observation is done till there is spontaneous resolution. It can be done but is not preferred due to prolonged follow-up and uncertainty in resolution.</p>\n<p>The <strong>indications</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial serum <strong>β hCG &lt;1500 IU/L</strong></li>\n<li>Falling serum β hCG levels</li>\n<li>Gestational <strong>sac size &lt;4 cm</strong></li>\n<li>No fetal cardiac activity on TVS</li>\n<li>No evidence of rupture or bleeding on TVS</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5488","text":"A primigravida in her firs‌t trimester came for a ro‌utine ANC. USG findings r​evealed an empty uterine ​cavity. Serum beta hCG wa​s measured and was found ​to be 1700 IU/mL. All of ​the following drugs can b‍e used in managing this p‌atient except:","raw_text":"A primigravida in her first trimester came for a routine ANC. USG findings revealed an empty uterine cavity. Serum beta hCG was measured and was found to be 1700 IU/mL. All of the following drugs can be used in managing this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Potassium‌ chloride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Actin‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxyt‌ocin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oxytocin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oxytocin</strong></p><p><strong>Oxytocin</strong> is <strong>not used </strong>in the management of<strong> unruptured ectopic pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for medical management of unruptured tubal ectopic pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemodynamically stable patient</li>\n<li>Serum β hCG &lt;5000 IU/L</li>\n<li>Tubal diameter &lt;4 cm</li>\n<li>No fetal cardiac activity</li>\n<li>No intra-abdominal haemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medical management</strong> of unruptured tubal ectopic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct local therapy (laparoscopy or USG guidance) -\n<ul>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Potassium chloride</li>\n<li>Actinomycin</li>\n<li>Hyperosmolar glucose</li>\n<li>PGF<sub>2α</sub></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Systemic therapy - single dose of <strong>methotrexate</strong> 50 mg/m<sup>2</sup> IM</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Follow-up</strong> after medical management is done by measuring the decline in serial <strong>serum β hCG</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9707","text":"A patient with unruptured ‌ectopic pregnancy is plan‌ned for medical managemen​t with methotrexate. What​ is the multi-dose protoc​ol for this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with unruptured ectopic pregnancy is planned for medical management with methotrexate. What is the multi-dose protocol for this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 mg/kg on days 1, 2, 5, a‌nd 8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.1 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5,‌ and 7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.1 mg/kg on days 1, 4, 6,‌ and 8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, a‌nd 7","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, and 7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, and 7</strong></p><p>In the <strong>multi-dose protocol</strong>, methotrexate is given at a dose of <strong>1 mg/kg </strong>as an<strong> </strong>IM injection on<strong> alternate days 1, 3, 5, and 7</strong>.</p>\n<p>This regimen is continued on alternating days until the hCG level drops by 15% over two days. Up to 4 doses may be given. Along with this, <strong>leucovorin rescue </strong>(<strong>folinic acid</strong>) is given at <strong>0.1 mg/kg</strong> after 24 hrs (on days 2, 4, 6, and 8) to buffer the side effects of methotrexate.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 195px;\" width=\"470\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>MTX Protocol</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>Multi-dose regimen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.711px;\"><strong>Single-dose regimen</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>Dose</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\">\n<p><strong>1 mg/kg<br/></strong>(+ 0.1 mg/kg LEU)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.711px;\">\n<p><strong>50 mg/m<sup>2<br/></sup></strong>(Body surface area)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>Regimen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\">Alternate daily doses </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.711px;\">Day 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>hCG measurement</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\">Days 0, 1, 3, 5, 7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.711px;\">Days 0, 4, 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>Treatment success</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\">hCG declines &gt;<strong>15%</strong> from the <strong>previous value</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.711px;\">hCG declines &gt;<strong>15%</strong> between <strong>Day 4 and 7</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\"><strong>When to administer additional dose  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.688px;\">2<sup>nd</sup>, 3<sup>rd</sup> or 4<sup>th</sup> dose to be given if hCG does not decline 15% from the previous value. Maximum 4 doses  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.711px;\">Second dose on <strong>Day 7</strong> if hCG does not decline 15% </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> Note: If the decline is <strong>≥15%</strong>, the patient is <strong>followed up weekly</strong> till the levels fall to &lt;10 IU/L.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9711","text":"Which of the following mus‌t be avoided by a patient‌ with tubal pregnancy on ​methotrexate? Sunlight ex​posure Sexual intercourse​ Dairy products NSAIDs Az​ithromycin","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following must be avoided by a patient with tubal pregnancy on methotrexate?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sunlight exposure</li>\n<li>Sexual intercourse</li>\n<li>Dairy products</li>\n<li>NSAIDs</li>\n<li>Azithromycin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Sunlight</strong> exposure, <strong>sexual</strong> <strong>intercourse</strong> and <strong>NSAIDs</strong> must be <strong>avoided</strong> when patients are on methotrexate therapy.</p>\n<p>The following should be avoided when methotrexate therapy has been initiated for the treatment of ectopic gestation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs - can cause reduced renal blood flow and delay drug excretion</li>\n<li>Alcohol - can predispose to concurrent hepatic enzyme elevation</li>\n<li>Sunlight - may provoke methotrexate-related dermatitis</li>\n<li>Sexual intercourse - can rupture the ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Phenytoin, tetracyclines, salicylates, and sulfonamides - can increase serum methotrexate levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5477","text":"A 30-year-old woman was br‌ought to the casualty in ‌a drowsy state with compl​aints of bleeding PV asso​ciated with abdominal pai​n and a history of amenor​rhoea for 8 weeks. On exa​mination, BP was 90/60 mm‍Hg, PR was 140 bpm, and U‌PT was positive. What is ‌the next step of manageme‌nt?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman was brought to the casualty in a drowsy state with complaints of bleeding PV  associated with abdominal pain and a history of amenorrhoea for 8 weeks. On examination, BP was 90/60 mmHg, PR was 140 bpm, and UPT was positive. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Admit to‌ the ICU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resuscitation and emergenc‌y laparotomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suction and‌ evacuation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FAST followed by laparosco‌pic salpingectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Resuscitation and emergency laparotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ade4711de19b4383aa47bff2bf455563x762x568.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9da06348c73d4b139727994a9e507662x1280x2077.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Resuscitation and emergency laparotomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>ruptured ectopic</strong> pregnancy which must be immediately managed with <strong>simultaneous</strong> <strong>resuscitation</strong> and <strong>laparotomy</strong>. On laparotomy, <strong>salpingectomy</strong> is the definitive surgery. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis of ruptured ectopic pregnancy is made based on clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amenorrhoea for 6-8 weeks followed by bleeding PV</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain </li>\n<li>Hemodynamically unstable </li>\n<li>Urine pregnancy test - positive</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Surgical treatment of ectopic pregnancy includes <strong>salpingectomy or salpingostomy</strong>, performed either through laparoscopy or laparotomy.</p>\n<p>Management of suspected ruptured ectopic pregnancy consists of initial resuscitation and stabilization, followed by point-of-care ultrasound (FAST) to identify intraperitoneal free fluid and surgical exploration. </p>\n<p><strong>Laparotomy</strong> is done for hemodynamically unstable patients with ruptured ectopic pregnancies. <strong>Salpingectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice and is done with preservation of the ipsilateral ovary and its vascular supply. </p>\n<p>For patients wishing to preserve future fertility, if the <strong>contralateral fallopian tube is damaged,</strong> <strong>salpingostomy</strong> is preferred. However, salpingostomy may result in inadequate evacuation of the products of conception and a recurrence of symptoms. Therefore, after a patient undergoes salpingostomy, it is important check the β-hCG level on a weekly basis to ensure that it reaches zero.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a laparoscopic visualization of a tubal pregnancy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5495","text":"Match the following criter‌ia used for the diagnosis‌ of ectopic pregnancy: Ec​topic pregnancy Criteria ​1) Abdominal A) Spiegelbe​rg 2) Cervical B) Studdif​ord 3) Cornual C) Rubin 4​) Ovarian","raw_text":"<p>Match the following criteria used for the diagnosis of ectopic pregnancy:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px;\" width=\"296\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Ectopic pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">1) Abdominal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">A) Spiegelberg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">2) Cervical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">B) Studdiford</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">3) Cornual</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">C) Rubin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">4) Ovarian</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A, 2‌-C, 3-B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-B, 3‌-A, 4-C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-B, 2‌-C, 4-A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-C, 2‌-A, 3-B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-B, 2-C, 4-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-B, 2-C, 4-A</strong></p><table style=\"height: 160px;\" width=\"296\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Ectopic pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Abdominal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Studdiford</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Cervical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Rubin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Ovarian</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"> Spiegelberg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Spiegelberg’s</strong> criteria used for the diagnosis of <strong>ovarian </strong>pregnancy<strong> </strong>include intact ipsilateral tube, pregnancy occupying ovary connected by the utero-ovarian ligament.</p>\n<p><strong>Studdiford’s</strong> criteria used for the diagnosis of <strong>primary abdominal</strong> pregnancy include the presence of normal bilateral tubes and pregnancy related to the peritoneal surface.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubin's</strong> criteria used for the diagnosis of <strong>cervical</strong> pregnancy includes presence of soft, enlarged cervix and closed internal os and partially opened external os.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9714","text":"An O-ve primigravida under‌went surgery for ectopic ‌pregnancy at 13 weeks of ​gestation. What will be t​he dose of anti-D immunog​lobulin to be given to he​r?","raw_text":"An O-ve primigravida underwent surgery for ectopic pregnancy at 13 weeks of gestation. What will be the dose of anti-D immunoglobulin to be given to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300‌ μg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"75‌ μg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100‌ μg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"150‌ μg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 300 μg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 300 μg</strong></p><p>Anti-D is given at a dose of<strong> 300 μg</strong> intramuscularly for alloimmunization after surgical management of ectopic pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The dose of anti-D in a surgically treated case of ectopic is<strong> 50 μg</strong> if gestation <strong>&lt;12 weeks</strong>. If the gestation is <strong>&gt;12 weeks</strong>, <strong>300 μg</strong> of anti-D is given intramuscularly. </p>\n<p>Anti-D is not given when the ectopic is treated with medical methods.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9716","text":"A 30-year-old female patie‌nt (G2P0A1) with a histor‌y of FSH therapy and in v​itro fertilization presen​ted with lower abdominal ​pain, vomiting, and vagin​al discharge. On examinat​ion, she was hemodynamica‍lly stable. Transvaginal ‌ultrasound showed a viabl‌e intrauterine pregnancy ‌of 8 weeks associated wit​h a heterogeneous right a​dnexal mass showing a pos​itive bagel sign. What is​ the most appropriate man‌agement?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female patient (G2P0A1) with a history of FSH therapy and in vitro fertilization presented with lower abdominal pain, vomiting, and vaginal discharge. On examination, she was hemodynamically stable. Transvaginal ultrasound showed a viable intrauterine pregnancy of 8 weeks associated with a heterogeneous right adnexal mass showing a positive bagel sign. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌TP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sur‌gery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Expectant ‌management","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d68fb43424834fb09b1f219fa3ca26b4x1280x1432.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> heterotopic pregnancy. Surgery</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> is the ideal treatment in heterotopic pregnancy.</span></p>\n<p>A normal uterine pregnancy co-existing with an extrauterine (ectopic) pregnancy is termed a <strong>heterotopic pregnancy</strong>. Although heterotopic pregnancy can occur spontaneously, it is more likely after <strong>ART and ovulation induction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tubal ring sign</strong> also referred to as<strong> bagel sign </strong>is one of the ultrasound signs of a tubal ectopic. It comprises an echogenic ring that surrounds an unruptured ectopic pregnancy. </p>\n<p>USG image showing bagel sign or tubal ring sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Methotrexate is not to be given as it harms even the normal intrauterine fetus. Although potassium chloride may be injected into the sac of the ectopic, the safer method would be surgery to remove the ectopic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9706","text":"Methotrexate was administe‌red to a patient with unr‌uptured ectopic pregnancy​. At what serum beta hCG ​levels will this drug be ​most effective?","raw_text":"Methotrexate was administered to a patient with unruptured ectopic pregnancy. At what serum beta hCG levels will this drug be most effective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;70‌00 IU/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;50‌00 IU/L","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;60‌00 IU/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;100‌00 IU/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. <5000 IU/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt;5000 IU/L</strong></p><p>Methotrexate is most effective when given at <strong>β hCG levels </strong>are<strong> &lt;5000 IU/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>Methotrexate is given at a dose of <strong>50 mg/m<sup>2 </sup></strong>of body surface area. Monitoring is done by measuring <strong>β hCG levels</strong> on days 4 and 7. If the decline is <strong>≥15% </strong>of the initial value, the patient is <strong>followed up weekly</strong> till the levels fall to &lt;10 IU/L. If the decline is <strong>&lt;15%</strong>, a <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> dose</strong> of methotrexate is given on day 7.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3818","text":"Studdiford’s criteria is u‌sed for the diagnosis of ‌_____________","raw_text":"Studdiford’s criteria is used for the diagnosis of _____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovarian ‌pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical ‌pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tubal p‌regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdominal‌ pregnancy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abdominal pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abdominal pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Studdiford’s criteria</strong> is used for the diagnosis of primary<strong> abdominal pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Primary abdominal pregnancy is the <strong>rarest</strong> type of ectopic gestation. The conceptus implants on the <strong>peritoneal surface</strong> of the abdomen.</p>\n<p>Studdiford’s criteria:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The presence of normal bilateral tubes and ovaries with no evidence of recent or past pregnancy.</li>\n<li>No evidence of a uteroperitoneal fistula.</li>\n<li>The presence of pregnancy, related exclusively to the peritoneal surface, early enough to eliminate the possibility of secondary implantation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Criteria used in the diagnosis of ectopic pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cervical pregnancy</strong>: Paalman and McElin (clinical criteria); Rubin's criteria (histological criteria)</li>\n<li><strong>Ovarian pregnancy</strong>: Spiegelberg’s criteria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K39kFuXA","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"18_orbit_anatomy_and_ocular_injuries","title":"18.Orbit Anatomy And Ocular Injuries","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC1600","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not pass thr​ough the superior orbital‍ fissure?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not pass through the superior orbital fissure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lacrima‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fronta‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasocili‌ary nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Optic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0cf8a684c7748839eed183f4529340bx966x457.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Optic nerve</strong></p><p>The optic nerve (2<sup>nd</sup> cranial nerve) passes through the optic canal, not the superior orbital fissure.</p>\n<p><strong>Anatomy of the orbit</strong></p>\n<p>Orbit is a <strong>pear-shaped cavity</strong>, the stalk of which is the optic canal. The volume of the orbit is 30 cc.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 350.719px; height: 632px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Walls</strong> of the orbit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.719px;\"><strong>Bones</strong> forming them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Roof</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.719px;\">\n<p>Lesser wing of the sphenoid</p>\n<p>The orbital plate of the frontal bone</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Lateral</strong> wall (strongest wall)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.719px;\">\n<p>Greater wing of the sphenoid</p>\n<p>Zygomatic bone</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Floor </strong>(most frequently fractured wall)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.719px;\">\n<p>Zygomatic bone</p>\n<p>Maxillary bone</p>\n<p>Palatine bone</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Medial</strong> wall (thinnest wall)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.719px;\">\n<p>Maxillary bone</p>\n<p>Lacrimal bone</p>\n<p>Ethmoid bone</p>\n<p>Sphenoid bone</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><br/></strong>The image below shows the bones forming the orbit.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>inferior orbital fissure</strong> lies between the greater wing of the sphenoid and the maxilla, connecting the orbit to the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae. Through it run the maxillary nerve, the zygomatic nerve, and branches of the pterygopalatine ganglion, as well as the inferior ophthalmic vein.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9066","text":"You are volunteering in a ‌medical camp held in an o​rphanage. You see a kid w‍ith hypoplasia of the zyg​oma and mandible along wi​th antimongoloid slant of​ palpebral fissures as sh​own below. These findings‍ correspond to which of t​he following syndrome?","raw_text":"You are volunteering in a medical camp held in an orphanage. You see a kid with hypoplasia of the zygoma and mandible along with antimongoloid slant of palpebral fissures as shown below. These findings correspond to which of the following syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crouzon ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treacher Col‌lins syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Apert s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median facial cleft syndro‌me","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Treacher Collins syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d555115d95e74d329996915f407504f7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/691cc5ee7b384766adef82133c27716bx1280x1299.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2bfe05ff30a43ddb68e9842e8583830x569x742.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4720432b0bb4f38a20344646b650a78x760x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Treacher Collins syndrome</strong></p><p>The given findings correspond to <strong>Treacher–Collins syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Treacher–Collins syndrome/Mandibulofacial dysostosis refers to a condition resulting from the hypoplasia of the zygoma and mandible.</p>\n<p><strong>Ocular features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Indistinct inferior orbital margin</li>\n<li>Coloboma of the lower eyelid</li>\n<li>Anti-mongoloid slant of palpebral fissures</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Systemic features</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Macrostomia with a high-arched palate</li>\n<li>External ear deformity</li>\n<li>Bird-like face</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows features of Treacher<strong>–</strong>Collins syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Craniofacial dysostosis/Crouzon syndrome </strong>refers to the premature closure of the coronal and sagittal sutures (<strong>brachycephaly—</strong>clover-leaf skull) associated with <strong>maxillary hypoplasia</strong>. Ocular associations include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blue sclera</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Megalocornea</strong></li>\n<li>Aniridia</li>\n<li>Glaucoma</li>\n<li>Cataract</li>\n<li>Ectopia lentis</li>\n<li>Optic nerve hypoplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a baby with Crouzon syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Apert syndrome</strong> is the most severe form of craniosynostosis, involving premature closure of multiple cranial sutures (<strong>acrocephaly</strong>), along with <strong>syndactyly</strong> of the fingers and toes.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the hands of a patient with Apert syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7513","text":"A patient with a history o‌f trauma, presented with ​diplopia, epistaxis, and ‍restricted eye movements.​ Looking at the CT image ​given below, what will be​ your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of trauma, presented with diplopia, epistaxis, and restricted eye movements. Looking at the CT image given below, what will be your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le- fort‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture‌ maxilla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture ‌zygomatic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blow out‌ fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Blow out fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5327817bbb34883b063153240b753d4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Blow out fracture</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario plus the supportive <strong>CT image</strong> showing the <strong>\"hanging drop sign\" </strong>is suggestive of a<strong> blowout fracture</strong> of the orbital wall.</p>\n<p><strong>Blow-out fractures</strong> are isolated <strong>comminunited fractures</strong> caused from <strong>direct trauma</strong> to the orbit that results in a backward displacement of the eyeball and an increase in the intraorbital pressure. This causes a <strong>fracture</strong> at the <strong>weakest point</strong> of the orbital wall which is <strong>usually</strong> the posteromedial portion of the maxillary bone in the <strong>orbital floor</strong>. The <strong>second</strong> most <strong>common site</strong> for orbital blowout fracture is the <strong>medial wall</strong> of the orbit.</p>\n<p>The patient with blow out fracture usually presents with immediate <strong>periorbital edema</strong> and <strong>blood extravasation</strong> in and around the orbit. <strong>Proptosis</strong> of the eyeball secondary to orbital edema or hemorrhage with <strong>paresthesia</strong> along the infraorbital nerve are also very common.</p>\n<p>Imaging by a plain <strong>x-ray</strong> in water's view or by <strong>CT</strong> demonstrates depression of bony fragments and orbital contents herniating through the orbital floor into the maxillary antrum. This is called as the <strong>\"hanging drop sign\" </strong>or<strong> \"tear drop sign\".</strong></p>\n<p>Definitive treatment is by surgical repair 10-14 days after injury with initial supportive management with analgesics and antibiotics.</p>\n<p><strong>Le fort fracture</strong>- Is the term used to describe different types of <strong>maxillary fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9068","text":"What is the most common si‌te of an orbital blowout ​fracture?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of an orbital blowout fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Media‌l wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Latera‌l wall","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ro‌of","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fl‌oor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Floor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9eb738c2fb35463d9cf5da8c40682cebx300x246.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de8948881f404b46af01561d799ad124x720x807.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Floor</strong></p><p>The most common site of an <strong>orbital blowout fracture</strong> is the<strong> floor of the orbit. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 446\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Blow-out fractures are isolated <strong>comminunited fractures</strong> caused from <strong>direct trauma</strong> to the orbit that results in a backward displacement of the eyeball and an increase in the intraorbital pressure. This causes a <strong>fracture</strong> at the <strong>weakest point</strong> of the orbital wall which is usually the posteromedial portion of the maxillary bone in the <strong>orbital floor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>second</strong> most <strong>common site</strong> for orbital blowout fracture is the <strong>medial wall</strong> of the orbit (Option A).</p>\n<p>The fractures of the <strong>roof</strong>(Option C) are relatively rare and most often do not require any treatment.The <strong>lateral wall</strong>(Option B) of the orbit is the most solid orbital wall and a fracture here is usually seen in injuries with extensive facial damage.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 447\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The patient with blow out fracture usually presents with immediate <strong>periorbital edema</strong> and <strong>blood extravasation</strong> in and around the orbit. <strong>Proptosis</strong> of the eyeball secondary to orbital edema or hemorrhage with <strong>paresthesia</strong> along the infraorbital nerve are also very common.</p>\n<p>Imaging by a plain <strong>x-ray</strong> in water's view or by <strong>CT</strong> demonstrates depression of bony fragments and orbital contents herniating through the orbital floor into the maxillary antrum. This is called as the <strong>\"hanging drop sign\" </strong>or<strong> \"tear drop sign\".</strong></p>\n<p>Definitive treatment is by surgical repair 10-14 days after injury with initial supportive management with analgesics and antibiotics.</p>\n<p>The below given CT image shows hanging drop sign.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below given X-ray image in water's view shows \"hanging drop sign\".</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9069","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the cli​nical sequelae expected i‍n a patient with orbital ​blowout fracture is false​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the clinical sequelae expected in a patient with orbital blowout fracture is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anesthesia of the cheek is‌ very common.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emphysema of eyelids is mo‌re common with medial wal​l fracture.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enophthalmos develops abou‌t 10 days after the injur​y.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe ocular damage is se‌en in most cases.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Severe ocular damage is seen in most cases.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Severe ocular damage is seen in most cases.</strong></p><p><strong>Severe ocular damage</strong> associated with a \"blowout\" fracture is <strong>rare</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because a \"blowout fracture\" is nature’s way of protecting the globe from injury.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of blow out fracture are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Periorbital edema</li>\n<li><strong>Paresthesia and anesthesia</strong> in the <strong>distribution of the infraorbital nerve </strong>(lower lid, cheek, side of the nose, upper lip, and upper teeth)</li>\n<li><strong>Emphysema of the eyelids</strong>-more frequent with medial wall fractures</li>\n<li>Proptosis-seen initially due to orbital edema and hemorrhage.</li>\n<li>After about<strong> 10 days</strong>, as the edema decreases, the eyeball sinks backward and somewhat inferiorly, resulting in <strong>enophthalmos and mechanical ptosis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Double diplopia-</strong> diplopia in upward as well as downward gaze</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9070","text":"Two people who got in a br‌awl are brought to the em​ergency department becaus‍e of severe facial injuri​es. In one of those patie​nts, there is periorbital​ ecchymosis and anesthesi​a over the cheek. Orbital‍ blowout fracture is susp​ected. What X-ray finding‍ will confirm this diagno​sis?","raw_text":"Two people who got in a brawl are brought to the emergency department because of severe facial injuries. In one of those patients, there is periorbital ecchymosis and anesthesia over the cheek. Orbital blowout fracture is suspected. What X-ray finding will confirm this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steepl‌e sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lightbu‌lb sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hanging ‌drop sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Signet r‌ing sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hanging drop sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7a6bb9cfdaa4390b1d8ce31317e6a15x357x293.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hanging drop sign</strong></p><p>The X-ray finding seen in an orbital blowout fracture is <strong>hanging drop/tear-drop sign</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Hanging drop/tear-drop sign</strong> is characteristic of an orbital \"blow-out\" fracture, which represents the orbital contents herniating through the floor of the orbit into the maxillary antrum. The most useful projection for detecting an orbital floor fracture is <strong>Water’s view</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The optimal time for surgery following a blowout fracture is <strong>10–14 days.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications for surgery in a blowout fracture are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diplopia not resolving significantly</strong> in the early days after trauma</li>\n<li><strong>A fracture with a large herniation</strong> of tissues into the antrum</li>\n<li><strong>Incarceration of tissues in the fracture,</strong> resulting in  globe  retraction and increased applanation tension on attempted upward gaze</li>\n<li><strong>Enophthalmos greater than 2 mm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9072","text":"All of the following are c‌haracteristic clinical fi​ndings that are associate‍d with a history of blunt​ trauma to the eye, excep​t:","raw_text":"All of the following are characteristic clinical findings that are associated with a history of blunt trauma to the eye, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Berlin'‌s edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vossiu‌s ring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Soemmeri‌ng’s ring","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rosette ‌cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Soemmering’s ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Soemmering’s ring</strong></p><p><strong>Vossius ring, </strong>not Soemmering’s<strong>, </strong>is seen after <strong>blunt trauma</strong> to the eye</p>\n<p><strong>Soemmering’s ring</strong> refers to a thick ring of <strong>after cataract,</strong> also known as \"<strong>secondary cataract</strong>.\" It is the opacity that persists or develops after extracapsular lens extraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Blunt trauma to the eye produces</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vossius ring -</strong> It is a circular ring of brown pigment seen on the anterior capsule. It occurs due to the striking of the contracted pupillary margin against the crystalline lens.</li>\n<li><strong>Rosette cataract -</strong> It is the most typical form of concussion cataract.</li>\n<li><strong>Commotio retinae (Berlin’s edema) -</strong> It manifests as milky white cloudiness involving a considerable area of the posterior pole with a \"<strong>cherry-red spot\"</strong> at foveola.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9073","text":"What is the most common si‌te of globe rupture follo​wing trauma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of globe rupture following trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superonas‌al limbus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inferonas‌al limbus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superotemp‌oral limbus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferotemp‌oral limbus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superonasal limbus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superonasal limbus</strong></p><p>The<strong> superonasal limbus</strong> is the <strong>most common site</strong> of <strong>globe rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p>This occurs due to the <strong>contrecoup</strong> <strong>effect</strong>—the <strong>lower temporal quadrant</strong> is the most exposed to trauma; hence, the effect is seen in the superonasal limbus, which lies on the opposite side.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6770","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is the followi​ng clinical finding seen?‍","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is the following clinical finding seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unilateral anterior crania‌l fossa fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral anterior cranial‌ fossa fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Unilateral posterior crani‌al fossa fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral posterior crania‌l fossa fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bilateral anterior cranial fossa fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1dba2662ea8b4c3ea1edb86abaf0adf7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bilateral anterior cranial fossa fracture</strong></p><p>The image shows bilateral periorbital ecchymoses, which is called the panda sign, and it is seen in <strong>bilateral anterior cranial fossa</strong> fracture. It is also known as panda eyes/<strong>raccoon eyes.</strong></p>\n<p>The pooling of blood surrounding the eyes is most commonly associated with fractures of the anterior cranial fossa. This finding is typically not present during the initial evaluation and is <strong>delayed by 1 to 3 days</strong>. If bilateral, this finding is highly predictive of a basilar skull fracture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9075","text":"Clinical examination patie‌nts with which of the fol​lowing conditions would s‍how Dalen Fuchs Nodules?","raw_text":"Clinical examination patients with which of the following conditions would show Dalen Fuchs Nodules?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cornea‌l ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sympathetic ‌ophthalmitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"After c‌ataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sympathetic ophthalmitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90b7b5198e8c4576aa638a8acfd82c4dx508x442.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sympathetic ophthalmitis</strong></p><p><strong>Dalen Fuchs nodules</strong> are seen in <strong>sympathetic ophthalmitis </strong>and<strong> Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease (VKH) syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>These are local granulomas due to the proliferation of lymphocytes and epithelioid cells and are located between RPE and Bruch's membrane.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Dalen Fuchs nodules.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9071","text":"Which of the following vie‌ws would you advise, so a​s to visualize the optic ‍foramina?","raw_text":"Which of the following views would you advise, so as to visualize the optic foramina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caldwe‌ll view","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Water’‌s view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latera‌l view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhese‌ view","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rhese view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ff0fed35810448e87bce3db46ac39ddx1280x1359.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f87a393f4f294c42b2c44d03f8713beax1280x4640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rhese view</strong></p><p>The <strong>Rhese view</strong> in an X-ray scan helps in the visualization of the <strong>optic foramina. </strong></p>\n<p>The optic foramen/optic canal transmits the <strong>optic nerve</strong> <strong>and ophthalmic artery.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rhese view</strong>: for <strong>optic foramen</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Other X-ray views of the orbit</strong>:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Caldwell's view</strong>: Occipitofrontal</p>\n<p>Structures seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superior orbital fissure</strong></li>\n<li>Greater and lesser wing of the sphenoid</li>\n<li>Ethmoid and frontal sinus</li>\n<li>Floor of the sella</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. <strong>Water's view</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occipitomeatal<strong>: floor of the orbit </strong>is seen <strong>(important in \"blow-out\" fractures)</strong></li>\n<li>Occipitomental<strong>: </strong>maxillary sinus and floor of the orbit</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. <strong>Towne’ s view</strong>:</p>\n<p>Structures seen</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dorsum sellae of the sphenoid bone</li>\n<li>Inferior orbital fissure</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9074","text":"Heterochromia iridis may b‌e caused by which of the ​following intraocular for‍eign bodies?","raw_text":"Heterochromia iridis may be caused by which of the following intraocular foreign bodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cop‌per","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ir‌on","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alum‌inium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gl‌ass","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03d1272bd035497bb75a6b3c491cc28cx255x197.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iron</strong></p><p>Heterochromia iridis may be caused by <strong>iron deposition</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Siderosis bulbi</strong> refers to the ocular degenerative changes produced by an<strong> iron foreign body</strong>. In siderosis bulbi, the iris is first stained <strong>greenish</strong> and later turns <strong>reddish brown,</strong> causing <strong>heterochromia iridis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Chalcosis</strong> refers to the specific changes produced by the <strong>alloy of copper</strong> in the eye. Clinical features include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kayser–Fleischer ring-</strong> a golden brown ring that occurs due to the deposition of copper in the Descemet’s membrane of the cornea.</li>\n<li><strong>Sunflower cataract-</strong> produced by the deposition of copper under the anterior capsule of the lens. It is brilliant golden green in color and arranged like the petals of a sunflower.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows siderosis bulbi.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6767","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is most likely to​ produce this finding on ‍CT?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is most likely to produce this finding on CT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic ner‌ve glioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sphenoid win‌g meningioma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sphenoid wing meningioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f6dfac4b688f4242aaacfaa115516892.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sphenoid wing meningioma</strong></p><p>The CT image shows hyperostosis of the lateral wall of the left orbit.</p>\n<p>Hyperostosis causing lesions are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sphenoid wing meningioma</strong></li>\n<li>Fibrous dysplasia</li>\n<li>Metastasis from cancer prostate</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7917","text":"A 30-year-old construction‌ worker presented to the ​emergency department with‍ complaints of pain and d​ecreased vision over his ​right eye. He gives a his​tory of accidental exposu​re with a cleaning agent ‍that contains ammonia. Oc​ular examination finding ‍is given below. According​ to the Roper-Hall system‍, what is the grade of ch‍emical injury?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old construction worker presented to the emergency department with complaints of pain and decreased vision over his right eye. He gives a history of accidental exposure with a cleaning agent that contains ammonia. Ocular examination finding is given below. According to the Roper-Hall system, what is the grade of chemical injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gra‌de I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grad‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‌e III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grade II","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6259af782e4144b38fd0cabb36816af7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grade II</strong></p><p>The image shows a<strong> grade II</strong> chemical injury. There is limbal ischaemia in the lower left quadrant involving <strong>&lt;1/3</strong> of the limbus and a <strong>hazy cornea</strong> with <strong>visible iris</strong> details.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Roper–Hall system</strong> is used for the classification of <strong>chemical injuries</strong> to the eye.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade</td>\n<td>Limbal ischemia</td>\n<td>Cornea</td>\n<td>Iris</td>\n<td>Prognosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Clear</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Excellent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>&lt;1/3 of limbus</td>\n<td>Hazy</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>1/3 - 1/2 of limbus </td>\n<td>Hazy</td>\n<td>Obscured</td>\n<td>Guarded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>&gt;1/2 limbus</td>\n<td>Opaque</td>\n<td>Obscured</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Chemical injuries to the eye:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alkali</strong> burns are twice as common as acid burns.</li>\n<li>Alkalis tend to penetrate more deeply than acids, as the latter coagulate surface proteins, forming a protective barrier.</li>\n<li>Most commonly involved alkalis are <strong>ammonia</strong>,<strong> sodium hydroxide,</strong> and <strong>lime</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uc8yYII-","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"18_fractures_in_children","title":"18.Fractures In Children","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2680","text":"What is the most common bo‌ne to be fractured in chi‍ldren?","raw_text":"What is the most common bone to be fractured in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal for‌earm bones","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hum‌erus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clav‌icle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Car‌pals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Distal forearm bones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Distal forearm bones</strong></p><p>The most common bones to be fractured in children are the <strong>distal forearm bones</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>distal radius</strong> is the <strong>most commonly fractured bone in childhood. </strong>It is more commonly fractured than the clavicle.</p>\n<p>The clavicle is most commonly fractured <strong>during birth</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5563","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about supracondylar‍ fracture of the humerus?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about supracondylar fracture of the humerus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common pedi‌atric elbow fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexion type is the most c‌ommon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The Gartland classificatio‌n is commonly used","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior interosseous nerv‌e is most commonly injure‍d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flexion type is the most common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b437d1cb152483d87f3774f5b224a58x1280x1575.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flexion type is the most common</strong></p><p>Most supracondylar fractures (98%) are<strong> extension-type </strong>injuries. They are usually caused by a fall onto an outstretched hand.</p>\n<p>Supracondylar humeral fractures are the<strong> most common</strong> type of <strong>pediatric elbow fracture. </strong>They most commonly lead to the following nerve injuries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extension-type: <strong>Anterior interosseous nerve</strong> </li>\n<li>Flexion-type: Ulnar nerve </li>\n</ul>\n<p>According to the <strong>Gartland</strong> <strong>classification</strong>, there are 3 types of fractures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I: Undisplaced</li>\n<li>Type II: Some degree of displacement</li>\n<li>Type III: Complete displacement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following x-ray shows a type III supracondylar fracture of the humerus:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5568","text":"A 4-year-old girl was brou‌ght to the emergency foll‍owing a fall in the playg‌round. On examination, he​r right arm was swollen, ​pink, and pulseless. An x‌-ray revealed a supracond​ylar fracture of the righ‍t humerus. What is the mo​st appropriate management​?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl was brought to the emergency following a fall in the playground. On examination, her right arm was swollen, pink, and pulseless. An x-ray revealed a supracondylar fracture of the right humerus. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obser‌vation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction ‌and pinning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fasci‌otomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vascula‌r repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reduction and pinning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reduction and pinning</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with a <strong>pink and pulseless</strong> arm is suggestive of a supracondylar fracture with <strong>adequate perfusion</strong>. The treatment of choice is <strong>reduction</strong> <strong>and pinning</strong>, followed by observation.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, if there is <strong>poor</strong> <strong>perfusion </strong>due to <strong>vascular compromise</strong>,<strong> </strong>the affected arm is<strong> pale</strong> and <strong>cool</strong> to the touch. In this case, the <strong>urgent reduction</strong> must be performed in the operating room. After reduction, the vascular status of the hand is reassessed. If the hand remains pale, <strong>exploration</strong> of the <strong>brachial artery</strong>, the release of entrapped structures, and <strong>direct</strong> <strong>repair</strong> with vein grafting may be required.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7555","text":"How would you classify the‌ following injury?","raw_text":"How would you classify the following injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salter-Harr‌is type III","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salter-Har‌ris type II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salter-Har‌ris type V","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salter-Har‌ris type IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Salter-Harris type II","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6f6820a6adbb406d885d39ece94b623e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Salter-Harris type II</strong></p><p>The physeal injury depicted in the given diagram is classified as <strong>Salter-Harris type II</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a type II fracture, the<strong> fracture</strong> <strong>line</strong> passes through the<strong> metaphysis</strong> into the<strong> epiphyseal plate. </strong>This results in a <strong>metaphyseal fragment</strong> called the <strong>Thurston-Holland</strong> fragment. The epiphysis is spared.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5589","text":"A 13-year-old boy is broug‌ht to the ED two hours af‍ter falling from his bicy‌cle. He complains of prog​ressive pain and swelling​ in his left arm. On exam‌ination, the pointing ind​ex sign is noted. Which o‍f the following will you ​suspect?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy is brought to the ED two hours after falling from his bicycle. He complains of progressive pain and swelling in his left arm. On examination, the pointing index sign is noted. Which of the following will you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of lateral condyl‌e humerus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture of supracondylar ‌humerus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture of medial condyle‌ humerus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of neck of radius‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fracture of supracondylar humerus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b9749b526d049e4b1cbd1025c1692d2x1280x1353.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fracture of supracondylar humerus</strong></p><p>The<strong> pointing</strong> <strong>index </strong>sign is seen in <strong>supracondylar fractures </strong>of the humerus.</p>\n<p>This finding occurs as a result of <strong>injury</strong> to the<strong> median nerve</strong>. It results in loss of function of both flexor digitorum profundus (<strong>FDP</strong>) and flexor digitorum superficialis (<strong>FDS</strong>). Hence, the index finger sticks out when the patient is asked to clasp their hand. </p>\n<p>The image shows the pointing index sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5582","text":"Which of the following is ‌the first ossification ce‍nter to appear around the‌ elbow?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first ossification center to appear around the elbow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radia‌l head","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olec‌ranon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral e‌picondyle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Capit‌ellum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Capitellum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Capitellum</strong></p><p>The <strong>capitellum</strong> is the<strong> first</strong> <strong>ossification</strong> <strong>center</strong> to appear around the <strong>elbow</strong>.</p>\n<p>The order of appearance of ossification centers can be remembered using the mnemonic <strong>CRITOE:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>apitellum: 2 years</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>adial head: 4 years</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nternal epicondyle:6 years</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>rochlea: 8 years</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>lecranon: 10 years</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>xternal (lateral) epicondyle: 12 years</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2675","text":"In which of the following ‌patients would you suspec‍t child abuse?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you suspect child abuse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An 8-year-old boy with a t‌orus fracture of the left‍ distal radius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 7-year-old girl with a f‌racture of the left later‍al epicondyle of humerus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 3-year-old boy with a gr‌eenstick fracture of the ‍right tibia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 9-month-old infant with ‌a spiral fracture of the ‍right femur","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 9-month-old infant with a spiral fracture of the right femur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 9-month-old infant with a spiral fracture of the right femur</strong></p><p><strong>Long bone</strong> fractures, particularly<strong> spiral fractures</strong>, in non-ambulatory <strong>infants &lt; 1 year</strong> should raise the suspicion of child abuse.</p>\n<p>Accidental fractures of long bones (humerus, tibia, radius) are common in toddlers and older children once they start walking.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2677","text":"An 8-year-old child was br‌ought to the casualty wit‍h a history of a fall fro‌m the stairs. On examinat​ion, varying degrees of e​cchymosis are noted over ‌the buttocks and upper li​mbs. X-ray reveals a meta‍physeal corner fracture o​f the right femur. What i​s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child was brought to the casualty with a history of a fall from the stairs. On examination, varying degrees of ecchymosis are noted over the buttocks and upper limbs. X-ray reveals a metaphyseal corner fracture of the right femur. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albers-Schön‌berg disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Battered ba‌by syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin D ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Battered baby syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Battered baby syndrome</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>battered baby syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Non-accidental trauma must be suspected when there are <strong>ecchymoses</strong> and fractures in <strong>various stages</strong> of healing. This indicates that the trauma has occurred over a period of time. <strong>Metaphyseal corner</strong> or<strong> bucket handle fractures</strong> are typically seen on x-ray. <span class=\"notion-enable-hover\" data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"0\">Sub-epiphyseal micro-fracture</span> is also a feature of child abuse that is seen on <span class=\"notion-enable-hover\" data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"2\">MRI</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">In such cases, a radiological assessment of the entire skeleton is indicated. The minimum x-ray views required in a case of suspected child abuse are:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">AP views of the entire skeleton</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Dedicated views of hands and feet</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Lateral views of the axial and appendicular skeleton</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2676","text":"A toddler was brought to t‌he pediatrician with fore‍arm swelling and inconsol‌able crying. An x-ray rev​ealed the following. What​ is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A toddler was brought to the pediatrician with forearm swelling and inconsolable crying. An x-ray revealed the following. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Torus f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nightstic‌k fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monteggia‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greenstic‌k fracture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Greenstick fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3fb140c3ed4c4c988df12db1590777d5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60734f15f9e14df29e3cfe22c62d901ax1279x772.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3fed23911ba4f71b87b966cd5c73d8ax1280x2654.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Greenstick fracture</strong></p><p>The given image shows a fracture with a <strong>broken cortex </strong>on <strong>one side</strong> and an <strong>intact cortex</strong> on the <strong>other side</strong>. This is called a <strong>greenstick</strong> fracture and is common in <strong>children</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Torus fracture or buckle fracture occurs due to buckling of the cortex. It most commonly occurs at the transition between metaphysis and diaphysis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Nightstick fracture refers to a fracture of the shaft of the ulna.</p>\n<p>Option C: Monteggia fracture refers to a fracture of the proximal ulna with an associated radial head dislocation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2679","text":"In patients with Madelung ‌deformity, which of the f‍ollowing bones is involve‌d?","raw_text":"In patients with Madelung deformity, which of the following bones is involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dista‌l ulna","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal‌ radius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trap‌ezoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triqu‌etrum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Distal radius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/780cbd007ba34e238bae1d67325fbd54x1024x700.jpeg","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b91563d4c18c4e64aa01339d680b45d0x1280x2086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Distal radius</strong></p><p>The <strong>distal radius</strong> is involved in <strong>Madelung deformity</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is a congenital or post-traumatic deformity in children due to <strong>disruption</strong> of the ulnar and volar aspect of the<strong> growth plate </strong>of the <strong>distal radius</strong>. This disruption is believed to be caused by the <strong>Vicker's ligament</strong>. This ligament normally tethers the lunate to the distal radius proximal to the physis.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Disruption of the growth plate causes a <strong>ventral curvature</strong> of the distal radius. However, the ulna continues to grow and the distal ulna appears more prominent may stick out as a lump on the back of the wrist. This is known as a <strong>positive ulnar variance</strong>. There is <strong>volar subluxation</strong> of the hand.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2515","text":"A 7-year-old boy is being ‌evaluated for the followi‍ng deformity of his left ‌elbow. 3 years ago, he ha​d suffered a fracture of ​his left elbow following ‌a fall on his outstretche​d hand. During that perio‍d, his arm was put in a c​ast for 6 weeks. Which of​ the following can be per‌formed to correct this de‌formity? Medial closed we‍dge osteotomy Lateral clo​sed wedge osteotomy Trans‌verse derotation osteotom‍y Lateral open wedge oste​otomy Medial open wedge o‍steotomy","raw_text":"<p>A 7-year-old boy is being evaluated for the following deformity of his left elbow. 3 years ago, he had suffered a fracture of his left elbow following a fall on his outstretched hand. During that period, his arm was put in a cast for 6 weeks. Which of the following can be performed to correct this deformity?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Medial closed wedge osteotomy</li>\n<li>Lateral closed wedge osteotomy</li>\n<li>Transverse derotation osteotomy</li>\n<li>Lateral open wedge osteotomy</li>\n<li>Medial open wedge osteotomy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,‌ 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌ 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0e0846b21929402d9f2f4a9e7e01e69a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73fbce1e80dc416d92a835a42cbef8d5x1280x1979.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 4</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>cubitus valgus</strong> deformity of the left elbow, which is usually a complication of <strong>lateral condyle fracture </strong>of the<strong> </strong>humerus. It can be corrected by <strong>medial closed wedge </strong>osteotomy or<strong> lateral open wedge</strong> osteotomy.</p>\n<p>Cubitus <strong>varus</strong> can be corrected by a <strong>lateral closed wedge</strong> osteotomy or <strong>medial open wedge</strong> osteotomy.</p>\n<p>Derotation osteotomies are commonly used for the correction of lower limb deformities.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2740","text":"An orthopedic resident is ‌applying a cast for a 6-y‍ear-old child with the fo‌llowing fracture. What sh​ould be the position of t​he child's forearm during‌ the procedure?","raw_text":"An orthopedic resident is applying a cast for a 6-year-old child with the following fracture. What should be the position of the child's forearm during the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mid-pronation with elbow f‌lexed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Full supination with elbow‌ extended","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mid-pronation with elbow e‌xtended","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Full pronation with elbow ‌flexed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mid-pronation with elbow flexed","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66e218e57b1e4c9ab7bf3a6b1246b396.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mid-pronation with elbow flexed</strong></p><p>The x-ray shows a fracture at the junction of the middle and distal 1/3<strong> </strong>of both radius and ulna. The elbow should be <strong>flexed</strong> at <strong>90°</strong> and the forearm should be in <strong>mid-pronation</strong> position while applying the cast. </p>\n<p>Forearm fractures in <strong>children</strong> are managed conservatively by applying a <strong>POP cast</strong>. In <strong>adults</strong>, surgery is preferred (<strong>open reduction</strong> and <strong>internal fixation</strong>).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 155px;\" width=\"267\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\"><strong>Fracture site on the forearm</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\"><strong>Position of forearm</strong> (with elbow flexed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\">Upper one-third</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\">Full supination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\">Middle one-third</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\">Mid pronation (neutral)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\">Lower one-third fracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.5px;\">Full pronation </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"P1B5xYeI","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"18_measles__mumps__rubella_and_other_viral_infections","title":"18.Measles, Mumps, Rubella And Other Viral Infections","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5868","text":"A mother brought her 7-yea​r-old child to the OPD be‌cause of fever and facial‍ swelling for the past 2 ‌days. She says that he ha​s been refusing to eat be​cause of the pain near th​e jaw. He has not been va​ccinated since birth beca​use of his parent's fear ‌of side effects. On exami‌nation, a tender swelling​ is noted on the jaw as s‌hown in the picture. Whic‍h of the following statem‌ent is true regarding thi‌s patient's diagnosis?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 7-year-old child to the OPD because of fever and facial swelling for the past 2 days. She says that he has been refusing to eat because of the pain near the jaw. He has not been vaccinated since birth because of his parent's fear of side effects. On examination, a tender swelling is noted on the jaw as shown in the picture. Which of the following statement is true regarding this patient's diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meningoencephalitis can pr​ecede parotitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Salivary gland involvement​ is limited to the paroti‌ds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient is not infectious ​prior to clinical parotid‌ enlargement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orchitis frequently leads ​to infertility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meningoencephalitis can precede parotitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ed5893c9c5a4522b91f1ea1143dccd7.JPG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meningoencephalitis can precede parotitis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mumps. Meningoencephalitis </strong>can occur <strong>before, along with, or after parotitis</strong>. Most commonly, it manifests 5 days after parotitis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Mumps </strong>is caused by <strong>RNA paramyxovirus. </strong>It is spread from person to person via <strong>droplets</strong> from clinical and subclinical cases. The <strong>incubation period</strong> is <strong>12-21 days</strong>. The virus appears in the saliva from up to 7 days before to as long as 7 days after the onset of parotid swelling.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The patient presents with prodrome lasting 1-2 days consisting of fever, headache, and vomiting. After the prodrome, unilateral or bilateral <strong>parotitis</strong> develops, which is tender. It may be associated with ipsilateral ear pain. Ingestion of sour or acidic foods may enhance pain.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It can be prevented by administering <strong>live attenuated, MMR vaccine</strong> at  <strong>9 months</strong>, <strong>15 months</strong> and<strong> 4-6 years.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: Mumps can affect all the salivary glands, but most commonly affects parotid glands.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: The patient is <strong>infectious</strong> from <strong>7 days before to 7 days after</strong> the onset of <strong>parotid swelling.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D:<strong> </strong>Orchitis or oophoritis is a common finding in mumps, along with parotitis. But, mumps leading to infertility is very rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5870","text":"An outbreak of mumps infec​tion was reported from a ‌local school a few days a‍go. A boy studying in the‌ 2nd standard of that sch​ool presented with painfu​l bilateral swelling near​ his jaw and cheek for 3 ​days. His mother is anxio​us about the possible com‌plications of the disease‌. Which of the following ​is the most common compli‌cation that can occur in ‍this patient?","raw_text":"An outbreak of mumps infection was reported from a local school a few days ago. A boy studying in the 2nd standard of that school presented with painful bilateral swelling near his jaw and cheek for 3 days. His mother is anxious about the possible complications of the disease. Which of the following is the most common complication that can occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orch​itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancre​atitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meningoen​cephalitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myoca​rditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meningoencephalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meningoencephalitis</strong></p><p><strong>Meningoencephalitis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> complication that can occur in <strong>mumps </strong>in<strong> children. </strong></p>\n<p>Most common complications in mumps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Children - Meningoencephalitis</li>\n<li>Adolescents and adult males - Orchitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5900","text":"On your pediatrics rotatio​n, you notice a 2-day-old‌ baby with the rash shown‍ in the image below. Whic‌h among the following is ​the most likely cause for​ this baby's rash?","raw_text":"On your pediatrics rotation, you notice a 2-day-old baby with the rash shown in the image below. Which among the following is the most likely cause for this baby's rash?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub​ella","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mea​sles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema i​nfectiosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vari​cella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rubella","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/40ebdc760f064918adaa696eb811a7e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rubella</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>purple papulonodular lesions, </strong>which are suggestive of <strong>blueberry muffin rash. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is seen in <strong>congenital rubella.</strong></p>\n<p>Blueberry muffin rash represents <strong>dermal erythropoiesis</strong>. In addition to congenital rubella, it can be seen in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital viral infections such as cytomegalovirus and parvovirus</li>\n<li>Neoplasms such as neuroblastoma and acute myeloid leukemia</li>\n<li>Rh hemolytic disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5888","text":"A woman who came with comp​laints of fever, myalgia,‌ and rash at 12 weeks of ‍gestation is found to hav‌e positive IgM titers for​ rubella. She is advised ​MTP. If the pregnancy is ​taken to full term, which​ of the following triad o​f complications would you‌ expect to see in the chi‌ld?","raw_text":"A woman who came with complaints of fever, myalgia, and rash at 12 weeks of gestation is found to have positive IgM titers for rubella. She is advised MTP. If the pregnancy is taken to full term, which of the following triad of complications would you expect to see in the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioretinitis, hydroceph​alus, intracranial calcif‌ications","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss​, blueberry muffin rash, ‌microcephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cataracts, cicatricial ski​n lesions, limb defects","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss​, cataract, congenital he‌art defects","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sensorineural hearing loss, cataract, congenital heart defects","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sensorineural hearing loss, cataract, congenital heart defects</strong></p><p>The patient has an increased risk of <strong>congenital rubella</strong> if the pregnancy is taken full term. The triad of complications that would be expected in the child include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensorineural hearing loss</strong>: Nerve deafness is the <strong>single most common</strong> finding among infants with congenital rubella syndrome</li>\n<li>Congenital <strong>cataract</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Congenital heart disease</strong>:<strong> </strong>The most common cardiac defect is <strong>patent ductus arteriosus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcifications - <strong>congenital</strong> <strong>t</strong><strong>oxoplasmosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Sensorineural hearing loss, blueberry muffin rash, microcephaly - <strong>congenital CMV</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>Option C: Cataracts, cicatricial skin lesions, limb defects - <strong>congenital</strong><strong> varicella </strong>syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5891","text":"Palak, a neonate is being ​evaluated for the finding‌ shown in the image. The ‍mother's prenatal history‌ is significant for viral​ exanthem in early pregna​ncy. On examination, a gr​ade 3 murmur is heard on ​auscultation. Which of th​e following is the most l‌ikely cause for this pati‌ent's murmur?","raw_text":"Palak, a neonate is being evaluated for the finding shown in the image. The mother's prenatal history is significant for viral exanthem in early pregnancy.  On examination, a grade 3 murmur is heard on auscultation. Which of the following is the most likely cause for this patient's murmur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patent ductu​s arteriosus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial sep​tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary​ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patent ductus arteriosus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad71be29d6dd4fb1aec4315f2efcd29e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patent ductus arteriosus</strong></p><p>The image shows a bilateral cataract, which along with the antenatal history of viral exanthem is suggestive of <strong>c</strong><strong>ongenital rubella syndrome</strong>. <strong>Patent ductus arteriosus </strong>(PDA) is the <strong>most common</strong> congenital heart disease associated with rubella, followed by pulmonary artery stenosis (PS).</p>\n<p>Frequency of heart disease in congenital rubella: PDA &gt; PS &gt; VSD &gt; ASD.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5895","text":"You are evaluating a baby ​in NICU with diffuse pete‌chiae. On examination, mi‍ld hepatosplenomegaly, bi‌lateral cataract, and a c​ontinuous murmur suggesti​ve of patent ductus arter​iosus are noted. Which of​ the following investigat​ions would you do to diag‌nose this baby's conditio‌n?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a baby in NICU with diffuse petechiae. On examination, mild hepatosplenomegaly, bilateral cataract, and a continuous murmur suggestive of patent ductus arteriosus are noted. Which of the following investigations would you do to diagnose this baby's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RT-PCR of ​CSF sample","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood s​pot test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgM antibody in infant blo​od","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IgG antibody in infant blo​od","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IgM antibody in infant blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IgM antibody in infant blood</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>congenital rubella syndrome. </strong><strong>IgM antibody</strong> detection in infant blood are done to diagnose congenital rubella syndrome<strong> </strong>in a <strong>newborn</strong>.</p>\n<p>Congenital rubella syndrome is diagnosed by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of <strong>IgM</strong> rubella antibodies in the infant <strong>shortly after birth</strong> (since IgM does not cross the placenta), or</li>\n<li>Persistence of <strong>IgG</strong> antibodies for <strong>&gt;6 months</strong> (maternally derived antibody would have disappeared)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5902","text":"A 2-year-old girl is broug​ht to the clinic with a h‌igh fever, cough, red eye‍s, and rash. The child's ‌parents say that the rash​ started on the forehead ​and behind the ears, then​ subsequently spread to t​he neck and body. On exam​ination, a confluent macu‌lopapular rash is noted a‌s shown below. Which of t​he following is false reg‌arding this child's condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to the clinic with a high fever, cough, red eyes, and rash. The child's parents say that the rash started on the forehead and behind the ears, then subsequently spread to the neck and body. On examination, a confluent maculopapular rash is noted as shown below. Which of the following is false regarding this child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The period of infectivity ​is 4 days before and 5 da‌ys after the appearance o‍f rash","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common complicati​on is pneumonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Warthin-Finkeldey giant ce​lls are present","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Koplik spots are pathognom​onic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The most common complication is pneumonia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f311b9bb12de45539e56c20dcea1b99a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The most common complication is pneumonia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>measles.</strong> The <strong>most common complication</strong> of measles is <strong>acute otitis media. </strong>The most common cause of death is pneumonia (Hecht's giant cell pneumonia).</p>\n<p><strong>Measles (rubeola) </strong>is caused by RNA paramyxovirus. The infection is <strong>spread via droplets</strong>, with a secondary attack rate ≥of 90%. The patients are infectious from 4 days before upto 5 days after the appearance of the rash. The rash appears on the 4th day of fever. It starts from the retro auricular region. <strong>Koplik spots</strong> are pathognomonic.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of death is pneumonia. Measles can lead to subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) as a late complication.</p>\n<p>Measles can be prevented by administering <strong>live attenuated</strong>, <strong>Edmonston–Zagreb</strong> strain or combined <strong>MMR</strong> given at 9 months of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5906","text":"A young child presented wi​th fever and cough in the‌ OPD. He is observed to h‍ave the finding shown in ‌the image below on the in​ner aspect of cheeks. Whi​ch of the following state​ment is false regarding t​his clinical finding?","raw_text":"A young child presented with fever and cough in the OPD. He is observed to have the finding shown in the image below on the inner aspect of cheeks. Which of the following statement is false regarding this clinical finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It appears 1 to 4 days bef​ore the rash","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It appears as tiny-rose co​lored lesions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is seen at the level of​ molars","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It may also occur in conju​nctiva","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It appears as tiny-rose colored lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3f248c67e2f4c37b0c94b474072aa51.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It appears as tiny-rose colored lesions</strong></p><p>The image shows discrete <strong>red lesions with white spots in the center</strong> on the inner aspect of the cheeks, suggestive of<strong> Koplik spots. </strong>They do not appear as tiny rose-colored lesions. </p>\n<p>Koplik spots are pathognomonic of <strong>measles. </strong>These are the spots present at the <strong>level of</strong> <strong>m</strong><strong>olars</strong>. They appear <strong>1-4 days prior</strong> to the rash. They may also occur in <strong>conjunctival folds</strong> and vaginal mucosa. </p>\n<p><strong>Forchheimer spots: </strong>Tiny red-colored lesions in the oropharynx or petechial hemorrhages on the soft palate, seen in <strong>rubella</strong> (German measles).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6075","text":"A 10-year-old boy presents​ with a history of myoclo‌nic jerks, seizures, and ‍rapid decline in cognitiv‌e function. The mother gi​ves a history of fever wi​th a rash at 1.5 years of​ age. The rash had starte​d behind the ears, progre​ssed to involve the whole‌ body, and resolved on it‌s own. What is the diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy presents with a history of myoclonic jerks, seizures, and rapid decline in cognitive function. The mother gives a history of fever with a rash at 1.5 years of age. The rash had started behind the ears, progressed to involve the whole body, and resolved on its own. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subacute sclerosing panenc​ephalitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menin​gitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guillian-Ba​rre syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with a classic history of exanthematous fever suggestive of measles, seizures, myoclonic jerks, and cognitive dysfunction is suggestive of <strong>s</strong><strong>ubacute sclerosing panencephalitis </strong>(SSPE).</p>\n<p><strong>SSPE</strong> is a chronic, progressive demyelinating disease that develops <strong>years after measles</strong> infection, most often in patients between <strong>5-15 years</strong> of age. Patients have a <strong>history of measles</strong> infection at <strong>&lt;2 years</strong> of age. CSF shows <strong>elevated measles antibody</strong> levels and MRI shows increased T2 intensity in white matter. <strong>No</strong> definitive <strong>treatment</strong> is available. </p>\n<p>Other <strong>complications of measles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meningoencephalitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Giant-cell pneumonitis</strong></li>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n<li><strong>Croup</strong></li>\n<li>Secondary infections - otitis media</li>\n<li>Protracted diarrhea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5105","text":"Akshay developed a fever, ​headache, and a diffuse r‌ash as shown below. The r‍ash is most prominent on ‌the face and chest. He sa​ys the rash is very itchy​ bothering his everyday l​ife. He is a football pla​yer and has an upcoming t​ournament in the next few‌ days. He wants to know i‌f he'll be able to play b​y then. Considering the r‌ash infectivity, until wh‍en would you advise him t‌o stay isolated?","raw_text":"Akshay developed a fever, headache, and a diffuse rash as shown below. The rash is most prominent on the face and chest. He says the rash is very itchy bothering his everyday life. He is a football player and has an upcoming tournament in the next few days. He wants to know if he'll be able to play by then. Considering the rash infectivity, until when would you advise him to stay isolated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 days after the appearanc​e of lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 days after the appearanc​e of lesions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14 days after the appearan​ce of lesions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After crust formation has ​occurred over lesions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. After crust formation has occurred over lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ba32b404e0a84e1fb338afd8167c9247.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. After crust formation has occurred over lesions</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>chickenpox. </strong>You would advise the boy to stay isolated until <strong>after crust formation has occurred over lesions. </strong></p>\n<p>People with chickenpox are <strong>contagious 24-48 hours before </strong>the rash is evident and until the vesicles have crusted, which is usually <strong>3-7 days after the onset of rash.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5725","text":"There is an outbreak of ch​ickenpox among the childr‌en at a daycare center. W‍hat is the most common ex‌tracutaneous site of invo​lvement of this disease?","raw_text":"There is an outbreak of chickenpox among the children at a daycare center. What is the most common extracutaneous site of involvement of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​NS","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Joi​nts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu​ngs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CNS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CNS</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>extracutaneous</strong> site of involvement by the <strong>varicella-zoster virus</strong> in children is the <strong>central nervous system.</strong> It is a benign complication of VZV infection in children and generally does not require hospitalization.</p>\n<p>The most common<strong> infectious</strong> complication of varicella is secondary bacterial <strong>superinfection</strong> of the <strong>skin,</strong> which is caused by <em><strong>Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></em> or <em>Staphylococcus aureus.</em></p>\n<p>The <strong>most common serious complication</strong> of varicella is varicella<strong> pneumonia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5911","text":"Seema is brought to the OP​D due to the development ‌of a very itchy rash. Her‍ father says he's worried‌ because she has been con​stantly scratching her bo​dy leading to scratch mar​ks. Before the developmen​t of the rash, she had a ​fever and headache for 2 ‌days. On examination, you‌ note a vesicular rash, w​hich is in different stag‌es of evolution. Which of‍ the following is not a p‌ossible complication of t‌his condition?","raw_text":"Seema is brought to the OPD due to the development of a very itchy rash. Her father says he's worried because she has been constantly scratching her body leading to scratch marks. Before the development of the rash, she had a fever and headache for 2 days. On examination, you note a vesicular rash, which is in different stages of evolution. Which of the following is not a possible complication of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subacute sclerosing panenc​ephalitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute cereb​ellar ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepa​titis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes​ Zoster","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chickenpox</strong> caused by<strong> the varicella-zoster virus </strong>(VZV). <strong>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE)</strong> is not a complication of Varicella-zoster infection. It occurs due to <strong>measles.</strong></p>\n<p>The characteristic rash of chickenpox is present in <strong>different stages</strong> of <strong>evolution.</strong></p>\n<p>Complications of VZV occur with disseminated varicella or with reactivation of infection. This more commonly occurs in immunocompromised patients. Complications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bacterial infections of the skin and soft tissues</li>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Acute cerebellar ataxia</strong></li>\n<li>Encephalitis</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li><strong>Shingles/Herpes Zoster</strong>: vesicular lesions are confined to the unilateral segment of the sensory nerve (dermatome). In children, the rash is mild, with complete resolution within 1-2 week. Unlike in adults, postherpetic neuralgia is unusual in children.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5726","text":"A child presents with feve​r, headache, and a diffus‌e rash as shown below. Th‍e rash resolves by crusti‌ng and the child recovers​ in 10 days. Which spinal​ segments are most common​ly involved in a latent r​eactivation of this infec​tion?","raw_text":"A child presents with fever, headache, and a diffuse rash as shown below. The rash resolves by crusting and the child recovers in 10 days. Which spinal segments are most commonly involved in a latent reactivation of this infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C2​-C7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T3​-L3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"S1​-S3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It involves all spinal seg​ments equally","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T3-L3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8e28ad4b468d45cdb5dbf404ce505c8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e12eef59b5c94591b12f2e530a143efdx1280x805.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53411fc27c8842969ef087788a2bf77ax446x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0bc123ea2b54de287909ec7b9ce7711x986x748.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T3-L3</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>chickenpox. Latent reactivation</strong> of the infection or <strong>shingles</strong> most commonly occurs in the <strong>T3</strong> <strong>to</strong> <strong>L3 spinal segments.  </strong></p>\n<p>Herpes zoster/shingles are characterized by a <strong>unilateral vesicular dermatomal eruption</strong> associated with <strong>severe pain.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Herpes zoster ophthalmicus </strong>affects the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. It is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Ramsay–Hunt syndrome</strong> (herpes zoster oticus) affects the facial nerve with an eruption on the tympanic membrane, the external auditory canal, and facial palsy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5917","text":"You are a pediatrics resid​ent posted in the labor r‌oom. You are examining a ‍baby which was delivered ‌via C-section a few minut​es ago. You notice that t​he baby has zig-zag hyper​trophic cutaneous scarrin​g in a dermatomal distrib​ution on the left side of‌ the limbs and face. He a‌lso has a small head and ​small limbs. Antenatal hi‌story of the mother will ‍be significant for which ‌of the following infectio‌n?","raw_text":"You are a pediatrics resident posted in the labor room. You are examining a baby which was delivered via C-section a few minutes ago. You notice that the baby has zig-zag hypertrophic cutaneous scarring in a dermatomal distribution on the left side of the limbs and face. He also has a small head and small limbs. Antenatal history of the mother will be significant for which of the following infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vari​cella","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Her​pes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomeg​alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syph​ilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Varicella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Varicella</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>congenital varicella syndrome</strong>. Hence, <strong>antenatal history</strong> of <strong>varicella</strong> infection would be significant in the mother of this baby.</p>\n<p><strong>Fetal varicella infection </strong>occurs if the mother gets infected during the <strong>first half of pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cicatrix</strong>, a zigzag scarring in a dermatomal distribution</li>\n<li>Microphthalmia</li>\n<li>Cataracts</li>\n<li>Optic atrophy</li>\n<li>Chorioretinitis</li>\n<li><strong>Microcephaly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Limb hypoplasia</strong></li>\n<li>Motor and sensory deficits.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9242","text":"A baby in the NICU is bein​g evaluated for his featu‌res of microcephaly and h‍epatomegaly. CT scan reve‌aled nodular periventricu​lar calcifications. Conge​nital CMV infection is su​spected. Which of the fol​lowing samples is the bes​t to detect the organism ‌using PCR?","raw_text":"A baby in the NICU is being evaluated for his features of microcephaly and hepatomegaly. CT scan revealed nodular periventricular calcifications. Congenital CMV infection is suspected. Which of the following samples is the best to detect the organism using PCR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver ​biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​SF","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bl​ood","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ur​ine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Urine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c22fbd6d7204cf3aec01e07de8cd7c1x512x512.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Urine</strong></p><p>Among the following options,<strong> u</strong><strong>rine</strong> is the best sample to be sent for the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of <strong>congenital cytomegalovirus</strong> (CMV) infection. </p>\n<p>In congenital CMV infections, the investigation of choice is <strong>PCR</strong> or <strong>viral culture</strong>. It should be done <strong>within the first three weeks of life </strong>(after this period, it is difficult to distinguish between a congenital and an acquired infection). Since not all affected infants are viremic at birth, <strong>urine</strong> is a <strong>more reliable</strong> sample than <strong>blood</strong>. </p>\n<p>Most CMV transmission occurs in the <strong>first trimester</strong>. The <strong>risk</strong> of transmission is high in the <strong>third</strong> trimester. 90% of the infected babies are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and have lesser chances of having late sequelae. </p>\n<p>The clinical manifestations include hepatosplenomegaly, petechial rashes, jaundice, <strong>chorioretinitis</strong>, and <strong>microcephaly</strong>. Neuroimaging shows <strong>intracranial</strong> <strong>periventricular calcifications. </strong>One of the long-term sequelae is sensorineural hearing loss<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>There is <strong>no definitive treatment</strong> for congenital CMV infection. Treatment with<strong> ganciclovir </strong>is<strong> </strong>indicated only in patients with progressive neurologic disease and deafness.</p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>periventricular calcifications</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5918","text":"A child is brought to the ​OPD with complaints of a ‌rash and fever. The worri‍ed parents say the rash a‌ppeared on his cheeks yes​terday. However, this mor​ning, they observed it ha​s spread to the trunk as ​well. The image of the ch​ild is given below. Which‌ of the following is true‌ regarding this condition​?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the OPD with complaints of a rash and fever. The worried parents say the rash appeared on his cheeks yesterday. However, this morning, they observed it has spread to the trunk as well.  The image of the child is given below. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also known as sixth ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is caused by an RNA vir​us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with a tr​ansient arrest of erythro‌poiesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with immu​ne fetal hydrops","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is associated with a transient arrest of erythropoiesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a883cf28635246dc87c82c674ebc7413.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is associated with a transient arrest of erythropoiesis</strong></p><p>The image shows a characteristic <strong>slapped cheek appearance</strong> of a rash, which is classically present in <strong>parvovirus B-19</strong> infection or <strong>erythema infectiosum. </strong>This is associated with <strong>transient aplastic</strong> <strong>crisis</strong> and erythropoiesis is mainly affected.</p>\n<p><strong>Erythema infectiosum </strong>is also known as the <strong>f</strong><strong>ifth disease. </strong>The rash appears first on the cheeks called the <strong>slapped cheek appearance</strong> then spreads to the trunk. It is caused by parvovirus B-19, which a <strong>single-stranded DNA virus.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Febrile, <strong>non-toxic</strong> child with a nonspecific prodrome</li>\n<li>Transient aplastic anemia, pure red cell aplasia</li>\n<li>Petechial/ purpuric skin eruptions: Papular purpuric “gloves and socks” syndrome (PPGSS)</li>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n<li>Primary maternal infection is associated with <strong>non-immune fetal hydrops</strong> and intrauterine fetal demise</li>\n<li>Arthritis and arthralgia (adults)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5919","text":"Niki, a 9-month-old baby i​s brought to the OPD for ‌a rash. The mother says t‍he baby had a high fever ‌of 103 F for 3 days for w​hich she was given parace​tamol. This morning, the ​baby had no fever but the​ mother noticed a rash on​ the trunk. On oral exami‌nation, ulcers at the uvu‌lopalatoglossal junction ​are present. Which of the‌ following statements is ‍false regarding this dise‌ase?","raw_text":"Niki, a 9-month-old baby is brought to the OPD for a rash. The mother says the baby had a high fever of 103 F for 3 days for which she was given paracetamol. This morning, the baby had no fever but the mother noticed a rash on the trunk. On oral examination, ulcers at the uvulopalatoglossal junction are present. Which of the following statements is false regarding this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also called as eryth​ema subitum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nagayama spots are commonl​y seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is caused by human herp​es virus-6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a non-pruritic, morb​illiform rash on the trun‌k","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is also called as erythema subitum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f36a3008aeff465eae43e3a503746536x670x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is also called as erythema subitum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of roseola infantum. It is also called <strong>exanthema subitum, </strong>not erythema subitum.</p>\n<p><strong>Roseola infantum </strong>is also called as <strong>sixth disease </strong>or exanthema subitum (exanthema=<span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">breaking out, subitum=sudden rash). </span>It is caused by a human herpesvirus (<strong>HHV</strong>) -6A, 6B, and 7.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prodrome of high-grade fever</li>\n<li>Non-pruritic, morbilliform rash, which appears as the fever comes down.</li>\n<li>Leucopenia and relative lymphocytosis</li>\n<li><strong>Nagayama spots</strong>: ulcers at uvulo-palatoglossal junction</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Exanthem subitum</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5922","text":"A 14-year-old boy came to ​the OPD with the complain‌t of fever and sore throa‍t. On examination, you no‌ted generalized lymphaden​opathy and hepatosplenome​galy. A peripheral blood ​smear revealed the follow​ing picture. What is the ​most probable diagnosis o‌f this patient?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy came to the OPD with the complaint of fever and sore throat. On examination, you noted generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly. A peripheral blood smear revealed the following picture. What is the most probable diagnosis of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infectious m​ononucleosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kimura ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scrofu​loderma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem​ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infectious mononucleosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a52184b9c12744c291e2c7f1fd7a099c.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infectious mononucleosis</strong></p><p>The image of the peripheral blood film shows <strong>atypical lymphocytes</strong>, also known as Downey cells. The given clinical scenario along with characteristic atypical lymphocytes is suggestive of <strong>infectious mononucleosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Infectious mononucleosis, also called, <strong>glandular fever</strong> is caused by <strong>Epstein–Barr virus</strong> (EBV). It is transmitted person-to-person through salivary secretions and blood transfusion. The incubation period is 4 to 5 weeks. It presents with a classic triad of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Pharyngitis</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other clinical features include fatigue and hepatosplenomegaly. The patients are advised to avoid contact sports for 3-4 weeks due to the risk of splenic rupture. </p>\n<p>The screening test of choice is the <strong>monospot test. </strong>This is a <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">heterophile antibody test</span>. <strong>Atypical lymphocytosis of &gt;10%</strong> is a characteristic laboratory finding. These are <strong>CD8 T lymphocytes</strong> that develop in response to the infected B lymphocytes.</p>\n<p>Other diseases caused by EBV include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Hodgkin's lymphoma</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\"><strong>Burkitt's lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Nasopharyngeal carcinoma </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Kimura disease</strong> is s a chronic inflammatory disorder of unknown etiology that most commonly presents as painless lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Scrofuloderma</strong> is the result of cutaneous infection adjacent to a tuberculous focus, which may correspond to peripheral ganglionar tuberculosis (the most common form of extrapulmonary tuberculosis in HIV-positive patients and in children).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5929","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ‌infantile diarrhea worldw‍ide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of infantile  diarrhea worldwide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rota​virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lactose i​ntolerance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norwal​k virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibrio ​cholerae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rotavirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rotavirus</strong></p><p>The most common cause of infantile diarrhea worldwide is <strong>rotavirus.</strong></p>\n<p>Rotavirus causes diarrhea in <strong>children</strong> of <strong>3-5 years</strong> of age, via the <strong>fecal-oral</strong> route. The incubation period is 1-3 days. Most cases are asymptomatic or with <strong>mild symptoms</strong> like watery diarrhea, vomiting, low-grade fever, and temporary lactose intolerance. This is presumably because of the protection by <strong>maternal antibodies </strong>in breast milk. Diagnosis can be made by rapid antigen detection of rotavirus in stool specimens</p>\n<p><strong>Lactose intolerance</strong> is actually not very common in <strong>infants</strong> and typically only starts showing up <strong>after age 3.</strong> All babies are born with <strong>lactase</strong> in their intestines. As they <strong>grow older</strong>, the lactase enzyme <strong>decreases.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0958","text":"Which of the following is ​not a mode of transmissio‌n of hepatitis B from the‍ infected mother to the b‌aby?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mode of transmission of hepatitis B from the infected mother to the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transplacental transmissio​n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perinatal t​ransmission","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amnioc​entesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breast ​feeding","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breast feeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breast feeding</strong></p><p><strong>Breastfeeding</strong> is<strong> not</strong> a mode of transmission of <strong>hepatitis B</strong> from an infected mother to the baby.</p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\" id=\"__p7\">Mother-to-child transmission of HBV can occur via the following <strong>three routes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p p-first\">Intrauterine transmission</li>\n<li class=\"p p-first\">Transmission during delivery (most frequent method)</li>\n<li class=\"p p-first\">Postpartum transmission</li>\n</ul>\n<p id=\"__p10\"><strong>Postpartum transmission</strong>: A proportion of babies may acquire the infection after birth due to close contact with the mother. However, breastfeeding is not a risk factor for HBV infection.</p>\n<p>Transmission following amniocentesis has been described, but the risk appears low.</p>\n<p>Newborns born to seropositive Hepatitis B mothers are given <strong>Hep B immunoglobulin</strong> as well as <strong>Hep B vaccination</strong> at birth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3100","text":"Which fungal infection spr​eads in neonates through ‌the care giver's hand?","raw_text":"Which fungal infection spreads in neonates through the care giver's hand?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida ​albicans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Candida pa​rapsilosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Candida t​ropicalis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candida ​glabrata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Candida parapsilosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Candida parapsilosis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Candida</strong> <strong>parapsilosis</strong></em> is increasingly responsible for hospital outbreaks, and the hands of healthcare workers may be predominant environment sources.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5908","text":"A 16-year-old boy presente​d with rigidity and diffi‌culty in movement. He has‍ a history of involuntary‌ movements in a single gr​oup of muscles of legs su​ggestive of myoclonic jer​ks. On examination, chore​oathetosis movements and ​lead pipe rigidity is not‌ed in the limbs. EEG show‌s suppression-burst episo​des which are characteris‌tic for subacute sclerosi‍ng panencephalitis. This ‌clinical presentation is ‌a complication of which o​f the following infection​?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy presented with rigidity and difficulty in movement. He has a history of involuntary movements in a single group of muscles of legs suggestive of myoclonic jerks. On examination, choreoathetosis movements and lead pipe rigidity is noted in the limbs. EEG shows suppression-burst episodes which are characteristic for subacute sclerosing panencephalitis. This clinical presentation is a complication of which of the following infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mu​mps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chick​enpox","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Her​pes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mea​sles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Measles</strong></p><p><strong>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis </strong>(SSPE) is a rare complication of measles. Hence, prior infection with <strong>measles</strong> is most likely.</p>\n<p>SSPE occurs 3-8 years after a primary measles infection. It is the <strong>least common</strong> complication of measles but is almost always fatal.</p>\n<p>The clinical manifestations occur in 4 stages:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Stage I:</strong> Maybe missed due to mild symptoms like irritability and reduced attention span.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Stage II:</strong> Hallmark is massive <strong>Myoclonus</strong>. Involuntary muscle movements and myoclonic jerks start from one group of muscles but gives way to massive spams and jerks.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Stage III:</strong> Involuntary movements disappear and are replaced by choreoathetosis, immobility, dystonia, and lead-pipe rigidity due to destruction of deeper parts of basal ganglia.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Stage IV:</strong> Loss of critical centers that lead to coma and then death.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of SSPE is by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detection of <strong>measles antibody in CSF</strong></li>\n<li>Characteristic findings on <strong>EEG</strong></li>\n<li>Typical histological findings or/and <strong>isolation of virus or virus antigen</strong> from <strong>brain</strong> tissue obtained by biopsy or after postmortem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7521","text":"Most common heart defect i​n congenital rubella synd‌rome is:","raw_text":"Most common heart defect in congenital rubella syndrome is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​SD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"V​SD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P​DA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"P​S","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PDA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PDA</strong></p><p><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> congenital heart disease associated with rubella, followed by pulmonary artery stenosis (PS).</p>\n<p>Cardiac anomalies have been observed in 50% of children infected during the first 8 weeks of gestation. The incidence of heart disease in congenital rubella syndrome is: <strong>PDA</strong> &gt; <strong>PS</strong> (right &gt;left) &gt; <strong>ventricular septal defect</strong> &gt; <strong>atrial septal defect</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0898","text":"A 3-year-old male has the ​following buccal mucosa f‌inding. After three days,‍ he presents to your OPD ‌with a rash. What is your​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old male has the following buccal mucosa finding. After three days, he presents to your OPD with a rash. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea​sles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Leuko​plakia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scarle​t fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kawasaki​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Measles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f915c6eb4fdd4300bf5b7e18e3a755cd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Measles</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given image shows small <strong>red lesions</strong> with <strong>bluish-white spots</strong> in the <strong>center</strong> on the <strong>inner aspects</strong> of the cheek <strong>opposite to the lower premolar</strong>, indicating the presence of <strong>Koplik spots</strong>. The appearance of these spots, along with a <strong>rash</strong> appearing three days later, strongly suggests a diagnosis of <strong>measles</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The measles virus is a <strong>single-stranded RNA</strong> virus. Measles infection has <strong>4 phases</strong>: the incubation period, prodromal illness, exanthematous phase (characterized by the rash), and recovery phase. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">After an <strong>incubation period</strong> of<strong> 8-12 days</strong>, the <strong>prodromal phase</strong> of measles starts with mild <strong>fever</strong> and is followed by <strong>conjunctivitis</strong>, <strong>photophobia</strong>, <strong>coryza</strong>, <strong>cough</strong>, and increasing fever. <strong>Koplik spots</strong>, appearing <strong>1-4 days prior</strong> to the <strong>rash</strong>, are a characteristic sign of measles. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Symptoms gradually intensify over a period of 2-4 days until the first day of the rash. The <strong>rash</strong> typically begins on the <strong>forehead</strong> (around the hairline), <strong>behind the ears</strong>, and on the upper neck as a red <strong>maculopapular</strong> eruption. It then spreads downward to the torso and extremities, often becoming confluent on the face and upper trunk. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Complications</strong> include <strong>acute otitis media</strong> (<strong>most common</strong>), <strong>pneumonia</strong> (the most <strong>common cause of death</strong>), croup, bronchiolitis, reactivation of tuberculosis, diarrhea, appendicitis, febrile seizures, and encephalitis. <strong>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis</strong> is a rare chronic complication of measles. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other options: </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: <strong>Leukoplakia</strong> appears as a <strong>white patch</strong> in the oral cavity that cannot be wiped off. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>Scarlet fever</strong> is <strong>Group A Streptococcus</strong> (GAS) pharyngitis and is characterized by a distinctive <strong>sandpaper rash</strong>. The rash appears as a diffuse, finely papular, erythematous eruption that turns the skin bright red and blanches on pressure. It starts around the <strong>neck</strong> and spreads to the trunk and extremities. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: <strong>Kawasaki disease</strong> (KD) is a systemic inflammatory disorder with <strong>arteritis</strong> of <strong>medium to large-sized vessels</strong>. It presents with <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>rash </strong>(maculopapular, erythema multiforme urticarial, or micropustular), <strong>conjunctivitis</strong>, and cervical lymph node enlargement. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5910","text":"What is the secondary atta​ck rate of chickenpox?","raw_text":"What is the secondary attack rate of chickenpox?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6​0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4​0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8​5%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5​0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 85%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 85%</strong></p><p>The secondary attack rate of chicken pox is<strong> 85% </strong>(range 61–100%)</p>\n<p><strong>Chicken pox: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Causative agent: Varicella–zoster virus</li>\n<li>Incubation period: 2 to 3 weeks</li>\n<li>Rash: On 1st day, centripetal, pleomorphic, vesiculopapular lesions with erythematous base (dew drops on rose petal)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment: </strong>Acyclovir 20 mg/kg/dose; four doses per day; for 5 days</p>\n<p><strong>Complications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutaneous: Secondary bacterial superinfection of the skin</li>\n<li>Systemic: Acute cerebella ataxia, <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Guillain–Barre syndrome</span>, transverse myelitis, optic neuritis, facial nerve palsy, pneumonia, Reye's syndrome</li>\n<li>Aspirin is contraindicated due to risk of Reye’s syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vaccine: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oka strain </strong>(live attenuated)</li>\n<li>All adults who have never had chickenpox should receive 2 doses 0.5 mL in the deltoid area subcutaneously.</li>\n<li>For &lt;13 years of age, the first dose is administered at 12–15 months and the second dose at age 4–6 years.</li>\n<li>For people older than 13 years, the two doses are administered 4–8 weeks apart.</li>\n<li>For those already immunized, booster doses are not needed if titers are adequate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6sFkps4D","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"18_microcytic_anemia","title":"18.Microcytic Anemia","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MD1148","text":"Which is the most common t‌ype of anemia worldwide?","raw_text":"Which is the most common type of anemia worldwide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aplasti‌c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Megaloblas‌tic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iron defici‌ency anemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anemia of chronic diseases‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iron deficiency anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iron deficiency anemia</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of anaemia worldwide is <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong>. It is also the most common type of nutritional anemia in India.</p>\n<p><strong>WHO recommendations</strong> for diagnosing anemia (g/dl) are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px; width: 929px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179.234375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Population</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.765625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mild Anemia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Moderate Anemia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Severe Anemia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179.234375px; text-align: center;\">Men ≥15 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.765625px; text-align: center;\">≥13</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">11 - 12.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">8 - 10.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">&lt;8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179.234375px; text-align: center;\">Non-pregnant women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.765625px; text-align: center;\">≥12</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">11 - 11.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">8 - 10.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">&lt;8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179.234375px; text-align: center;\">Pregnant women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.765625px; text-align: center;\">≥11</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">10 - 10.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">7 - 9.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">&lt;7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0499","text":"What is the most common he‌matologic manifestation s‍een in patients with HIV ‍infection?","raw_text":"What is the most common hematologic manifestation seen in patients with HIV infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌openia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lympho‌cytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ane‌mia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anemia</strong></p><p><strong>Anaemia</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> hematologic abnormality associated with <strong>HIV infection.</strong> It affects 60–80% of patients during the late stage.</p>\n<p>In most cases, the cause of anaemia in HIV-infected patients is <strong>multifactorial</strong>. It is often associated with infection, inflammation, malignancy, malnutrition, and polypharmacy.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Prior to the use of potent antiretroviral therapy (ART), thrombocytopenia was a common finding in individuals infected with HIV. With use of potent ART, the incidence of thrombocytopenia is far lower.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1054","text":"Which of the following ane‌mia is not due to diminis‍hed erythropoiesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following anemia is not due to diminished erythropoiesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron defici‌ency anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary s‌pherocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Megaloblas‌tic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aplasti‌c anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hereditary spherocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hereditary spherocytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Hereditary spherocytosis</strong> is not due to diminished erythropoiesis. It is a type of <strong>hemolytic</strong> anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> of anemia according to underlying mechanism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anemias of diminished erythropoiesis - decreased red cell production</li>\n<li>Hemolytic anemias - increased red cell destruction</li>\n<li>Anemia due to blood loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anemias </strong>due to<strong> diminished erythropoiesis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iron deficiency anemia</li>\n<li>Megaloblastic anemia</li>\n<li>Anemia of chronic diseases</li>\n<li>Aplastic anemia</li>\n<li>Myelophthisic anemia</li>\n<li>Fanconi's anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5743","text":"In a patient with MCV of 1‌09 fl, which of the follo‍wing is least likely?","raw_text":"In a patient with MCV of 109 fl, which of the following is least likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orotic ‌aciduria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anemia of ch‌ronic disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anemia of chronic disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anemia of chronic disease</strong></p><p><strong>MCV &gt;100</strong> fl indicates <strong>macrocytic</strong> anaemia which is not seen in anaemia of chronic disease. RBCs in <strong>anemia of chronic disease</strong> are<strong> </strong>initially<strong> normocytic</strong> and normochromic, becoming <strong>microcytic</strong> and hypochromic over time.</p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 deficiency, thiamine deficiency, and orotic aciduria cause macrocytic anaemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5758","text":"A 30-year-old woman with i‌ron deficiency anaemia is‍ prescribed oral iron sup‍plements. Foods containin​g which of the following ​can she take to increase ​its absorption?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with iron deficiency anaemia is prescribed oral iron supplements. Foods containing which of the following can she take to increase its absorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phos‌phate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxa‌late","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carb‌onate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asco‌rbate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ascorbate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ascorbate</strong></p><p><strong>Absorption</strong> of iron is <strong>increased</strong> by <strong>ascorbic acid</strong>. It is decreased by phosphates, oxalates, and carbonates.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5755","text":"Which of the following con‌tains most of the functio‍nal iron in the body?","raw_text":"Which of the following contains most of the functional iron in the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemog‌lobin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myog‌lobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trans‌ferrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferr‌itin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemoglobin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemoglobin</strong></p><p>Most of the <strong>functional iron</strong> (80%) in the body is found in <strong>hemoglobin</strong>. </p>\n<p>The storage pool represented by hemosiderin, and ferritin contains about 15% to 20% of total body iron. Myoglobin and iron-containing enzymes such as catalase and the cytochromes contain the rest.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0286","text":"Which transporter is requi‌red for the transport of ‍iron into the enterocyte?‍","raw_text":"Which transporter is required for the transport of iron into the enterocyte?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferro‌portin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Divalent metal transporter‌ 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Divalent metal transporter‌ 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hapto‌corrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Divalent metal transporter 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4fd2556ab9a14b1196842c683c971ec7x1280x1098.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Divalent metal transporter 1</strong></p><p><strong>DMT1</strong> (divalent metal transporter 1) is required for the <strong>transport</strong> of <strong>iron</strong> into the <strong>enterocyte</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dietary iron is mostly in Fe<sup>3+</sup> (ferric state) and must be reduced to Fe<sup>2+</sup> (ferrous state) by ferrireductases. Fe2+ iron destined for circulation is transported from the cytoplasm across the basolateral enterocyte membrane by <strong>ferroportin 1.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hephaestin</strong> is an iron oxidase that converts Fe2+ to Fe3+</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0288","text":"Which of the following is ‌involved in iron metaboli‍sm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is involved in iron metabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hapto‌corrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cub‌ulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seleno‌protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerulo‌plasmin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ceruloplasmin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ae665fab06e4f708d72192d07616568x1280x1098.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ceruloplasmin</strong></p><p><strong>Ceruloplasmin</strong> is involved in <strong>iron</strong> metabolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Ferritin </strong>is the storage form of iron in the body. <strong>Ceruloplasmin </strong>and <strong>hephaestin</strong> help in oxidation of Fe<sup>2+</sup> to Fe<sup>3+</sup> at the basolateral border of enterocytes. This is coupled with transport through ferroportin. In plasma, iron is transported by <strong>transferrin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Haptocorrin</strong> and <strong>cubulin</strong> are involved in vitamin <strong>B12</strong> absorption. <strong>Selenoprotein</strong> is involved in <strong>selenium</strong> metabolism.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0154","text":"Hepcidin inhibits iron tra‌nsfer from the enterocyte‍ to the plasma by binding‍ to _____","raw_text":"Hepcidin inhibits iron transfer from the enterocyte to the plasma by binding to _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trans‌ferrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ferro‌portin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ferr‌itin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Divalent metal transporter‌ 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ferroportin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55f9c745352046998ee62d26cfc3f00ax1280x1098.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ferroportin</strong></p><p><strong>Hepcidin</strong> inhibits iron transfer from the enterocytes into the plasma by <strong>binding</strong> to <strong>ferroportin</strong>, which is an<strong> iron efflux</strong> channel in the enterocyte.</p>\n<p>Iron metabolism is regulated by <strong>hepcidin</strong>, a protein and <strong>acute phase reactant</strong> produced by the liver. It binds to ferroportin and degrades it.<strong> </strong>This inhibits the transfer of iron from the enterocyte to the plasma via ferroportin. As a result, iron becomes trapped within the duodenal cells (enterocytes) in the form of mucosal ferritin and is lost in feces when these cells are shed off.</p>\n<p>Transcription of hepcidin is <strong>increased</strong> by the following factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased levels of iron</li>\n<li>Inflammatory cytokines</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Transcription of hepcidin is <strong>decreased</strong> by the following factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iron deficiency</li>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n<li>Ineffective erythropoiesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hepcidin is encoded by the <strong>HAMP</strong> <strong>gene</strong> and its mutations are associated with hereditary hemochromatosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5763","text":"What is the main site of i‌ron absorption?","raw_text":"What is the main site of iron absorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duodenum</strong></p><p><strong>Absorption</strong> of <strong>iron</strong> mainly occurs in the <strong>proximal</strong> <strong>duodenum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1120","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding the intern‍al iron homeostasis in ir‍on-deficiency anaemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the internal iron homeostasis in iron-deficiency anaemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferritin mRNA concentratio‌n iron-responsive element‍ increases and ferritin s‍ynthesis decreases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transferrin receptor 1 iro‌n-responsive elements inc‍reases transferrin recept‍or mRNA concentration and​ synthesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transferrin receptor 1 iro‌n responsive elements dec‍reases transferrin recept‍or mRNA concentration and​ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ferritin mRNA concentratio‌n iron responsive element‍ decreases and ferritin s‍ynthesis increases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transferrin receptor 1 iron-responsive elements increases transferrin receptor mRNA concentration and synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transferrin receptor 1 iron-responsive elements increases transferrin receptor mRNA concentration and synthesis</strong></p><p>In <strong>iron deficiency anaemia</strong>, there is an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>transferrin</strong> <strong>receptor</strong> mRNA concentration and synthesis.</p>\n<p>Iron absorbed through diet or which is released from circulation is bound to <strong>transferrin</strong> (iron transfer protein). When erythropoiesis is stimulated, iron-transferrin complexes act as <strong>transferrin receptors</strong> on the surface of <strong>bone marrow erythroid cells</strong>. Through receptor-mediated endocytosis, this complex is internalised. </p>\n<p>This <strong>iron</strong> is then made available for <strong>heme synthesis</strong> while the <strong>transferrin-receptor complex</strong> is <strong>recycled</strong> to the surface of the cell, from where the transferrin is released back into circulation and the transferrin receptor re-anchors into the cell membrane. The <strong>excess iron</strong> after hemoglobin synthesis binds to <strong>apoferritin</strong> (storage protein), forming <strong>ferritin</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Senescent RBCs</strong> undergo <strong>phagocytosis</strong> in the cells of the <strong>reticuloendothelial system</strong>. The hemoglobin is broken down, the globin and other proteins are returned to the amino acid pool, and the <strong>iron</strong> is shuttled back to the surface of the reticuloendothelial cell, where it is <strong>taken up</strong> by <strong>transferrin</strong> via <strong>ferroportin</strong> (iron export channel).</p>\n<p>There is no regulated excretory pathway for iron, and the only mechanisms by which iron is lost are <strong>blood loss</strong> (via gastrointestinal bleeding, menstruation, or other forms of bleeding) and the <strong>loss of epithelial cells</strong> from the skin, gut, and genitourinary tract. The normal daily <strong>iron loss</strong> from the body is <strong>1-2 mg</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 391px; height: 252px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Variables</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Iron-deficient state </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Iron-excess state</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 29px;\">Iron uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; text-align: justify; height: 29px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 29px;\">↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 31px;\">Transferrin receptor synthesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; height: 31px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 31px;\">↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 39px;\">Transferrin receptor mRNA concentration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; height: 39px;\">↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 39px;\">↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 21px;\">Iron storage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; height: 21px;\">↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 21px;\">↑</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 21px;\">Ferritin synthesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; height: 21px;\">↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 21px;\">↑</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.077px; height: 21px;\">Ferritin mRNA concentration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.923px; height: 21px;\">No change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px; height: 21px;\">No change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5774","text":"Peripheral smear of a pati‌ent with koilonychia is g‍iven below. Which of the ‍following lab findings is​ consistent with her like​ly diagnosis? Options S. ​ferritin TIBC Transferrin​ saturation 1 High Normal​ High 2 Normal Normal Hig​h 3 Low High Low 4 High L​ow Low","raw_text":"<p>Peripheral smear of a patient with koilonychia is given below. Which of the following lab findings is consistent with her likely diagnosis?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 109px;\" width=\"475\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Options</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 36px;\"><strong>S. ferritin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 36px;\"><strong>TIBC</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Transferrin saturation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.75px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Option 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/162a71bd27d041529a68a19a1d05d8b4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Option 3</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear showing <strong>microcytic </strong>and<strong> hypochromic</strong> picture with <strong>pencil cells</strong> is suggestive of <strong>iron-deficiency anaemia</strong>. In this condition, TIBC is increased, transferrin saturation is decreased, and ferritin is decreased.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5777","text":"You would expect iron-defi‌ciency anaemia in all of ‍the following patients ex‍cept:","raw_text":"You would expect iron-deficiency anaemia in all of the following patients except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 5-year-old boy with hook‌worm infestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 60-year-old man with car‌cinoma cecum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 54-year-old woman after ‌Billroth II operation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 25-year-old woman with m‌aize-rich diet","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 25-year-old woman with maize-rich diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 25-year-old woman with maize-rich diet</strong></p><p><strong>Maize rich diet</strong> is not one of the causes of iron deficiency anaemia. It can cause <strong>pellagra</strong>.</p>\n<p>Iron deficiency anaemia can be caused by hookworm infestation, carcinoma of the cecum and in Billroth II operation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5780","text":"Which of the following lab‌oratory findings is seen ‍in a patient with siderob‍lastic anaemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following laboratory findings is seen in a patient with sideroblastic anaemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased total iron bindi‌ng capacity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased transferrin satu‌ration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased se‌rum ferritin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low ser‌um iron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased transferrin saturation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased transferrin saturation</strong></p><p>In <strong>sideroblastic</strong> anemia, the <strong>transferrin</strong> <strong>saturation</strong> is <strong>increased</strong>.</p>\n<p>Serum iron and serum ferritin are also elevated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5784","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the triad‍ of the Plummer-Vinson sy‍ndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the triad of the Plummer-Vinson syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92156f8c5df04714b51885de951cd932.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f7cef27181248688ba7f0d6dd22d6cbx1280x2720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe1558f9d0394bc3a65f7b68e6413e1ex1280x1352.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The triad of the Plummer-Vinson syndrome does not include koilonychia (marked as 2).</p>\n<p><strong>Triad </strong>of<strong> Plummer–Vinson syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Esophageal webs</li>\n<li>Microcytic hypochromic anaemia</li>\n<li>Atrophic glossitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Koilonychia is a symptom of iron deficiency anaemia. It may or may not be present in Plummer Vinson syndrome. Hence, it is not a part of the triad.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5787","text":"A 34-year-old woman with s‌evere anaemia was started‍ on parenteral iron. Whic‍h of the following would ​occur first in this patie​nt as a response to thera​py?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman with severe anaemia was started on parenteral iron. Which of the following would occur first in this patient as a response to therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in serum iron lev‌el","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in serum ferritin‌ level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increas‌e in MCV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in reticulocyte c‌ount","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in serum iron level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in serum iron level</strong></p><p>One of the earliest responses to the correction of iron deficiency anaemia is <strong>increase in serum iron</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Responses </strong>after initiation of <strong>treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>12 to 24 hours - Replenishment of intracellular iron enzymes; decreased irritability; increased appetite; increase in <strong>serum iron.</strong></li>\n<li>36 to 48 hours - Erythroid hyperplasia in bone marrow </li>\n<li>48 to 72 hours - <strong>Reticulocytosis</strong>, peaking at 5-7 days</li>\n<li>4 to 30 days - Increase in hemoglobin level, increase in <strong>ferritin</strong>, increase in <strong>MCV</strong></li>\n<li>1 to 3 months - Repletion of stores </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Qcc6sADZ","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"18_sedatives_and_hypnotics","title":"18.Sedatives And Hypnotics","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB4551","text":"Which of the following is ‌a benzodiazepine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a benzodiazepine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flura‌zepam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fluma‌zenil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoba‌rbital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zale‌plon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flurazepam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flurazepam</strong></p><p><strong>Flurazepam</strong> is a<strong> benzodiazepine.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 677px; height: 551px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 152.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Class of drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 252.531px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism of action</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 250.219px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 152.25px;\"><strong>Benzodiazepines</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 252.531px;\">Facilitate the action of GABA by <strong>increasing</strong> the <strong>frequency</strong> of<strong> opening</strong> of GABA mediated chloride channel </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 250.219px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alprazolam</li>\n<li>Clonazepam</li>\n<li>Lorazepam</li>\n<li>Midazolam</li>\n<li>Oxazepam</li>\n<li>Temazepam</li>\n<li>Estazolam</li>\n<li>Flurazepam</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 55px; width: 152.25px;\"><strong>Barbiturates</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 55px; width: 252.531px;\">Facilitate the action of GABA by <strong>increasing</strong> the <strong>opening duration</strong> of GABA mediated chloride channel </td>\n<td style=\"height: 55px; width: 250.219px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Amobarbital</li>\n<li>Phenobarbital</li>\n<li>Butabarbital</li>\n<li>Pentobarbital</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 152.25px;\">Z drugs</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 252.531px;\">Act like benzodiazepines</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 250.219px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Zaleplon</li>\n<li>Zolpidem</li>\n<li>Zopiclone</li>\n<li>Eszopiclone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 152.25px;\">Melatonin receptor agonists</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 252.531px;\">Activates MT<sub>1</sub> and MT<sub>2</sub> receptors</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 250.219px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ramelteon</li>\n<li>Tasimelteon</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 152.25px;\">Orexin antagonist</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 252.531px;\">Blocks orexins, neuropeptides that promote wakefulness</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 250.219px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Suvorexant</li>\n<li>Daridorexant</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 152.25px;\">5HT receptor agonist</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 252.531px;\">Affinity for 5HT &gt; D<sub>2</sub> receptor</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 250.219px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Buspirone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4543","text":"A physician prescribes clo‌nazepam to a patient with‍ generalized anxiety diso‍rder. Choose the false st‌atement regarding its mec‍hanism of action.","raw_text":"A  physician prescribes clonazepam to a patient with generalized anxiety disorder. Choose the false statement regarding its mechanism of action.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bind at the α/γ interface ‌of GABA-A receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase the affinity of t‌he GABA-A receptor for GA‍BA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activate GABA-A receptors ‌directly opening the Cl c‍hannel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase the frequency of ‌opening of GABA receptor ‍Cl channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Activate GABA-A receptors directly opening the Cl channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Activate GABA-A receptors directly opening the Cl channel</strong></p><p>Clonazepam is a <strong>benzodiazepine.</strong> These are <strong>allosteric </strong>modulators of the <strong>GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor.</strong> They do not directly activate GABA<sub>A</sub> receptors to open the Cl<sup>-</sup> channel.</p>\n<p>GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor<strong> </strong>is a transmembrane inhibitory receptor in the CNS that functions as a chloride ion channel and has 5 subunits. </p>\n<p>Benzodiazepines are allosteric modulators of the GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor that bind at the <strong>α/γ interface</strong> of the receptor. This <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>affinity</strong> of the GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor for <strong>GABA</strong> thus <strong>increasing</strong> the<strong> frequency</strong> of the <strong>opening</strong> of the <strong>channel.</strong></p>\n<p>Barbiturates, on the other hand, have a different binding site to the receptor and they increase the duration of the GABA gated chloride channel opening.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4547","text":"Which is the shortest acti‌ng benzodiazepine?","raw_text":"Which is the shortest acting benzodiazepine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Remim‌azolam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mida‌zolam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tema‌zepam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Remimazolam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Remimazolam</strong></p><p><strong>Remimazolam </strong>is the <strong>shortest-acting </strong>benzodiazepine<strong>. </strong>Its <strong>half-life</strong><strong> </strong>is just around<strong> 37-53 mins.</strong></p>\n<p>Classification of benzodiazepines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Long-acting:</strong> <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Flurazepam</li>\n<li>Diazepam</li>\n<li>Quazepam</li>\n<li>Chlordiazepoxide</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate-acting:</strong> <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Alprazolam</li>\n<li>Temazepam</li>\n<li>Lorazepam</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Short-acting:</strong> <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Triazolam</li>\n<li>Midazolam</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Ultra-short</strong> acting\n<ul>\n<li>Remimazolam</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Remimazolam was approved by FDA in 2020.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5297","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not act via GABA potent‍iation?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not act via GABA potentiation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zopi‌clone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mida‌zolam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Promet‌hazine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Promethazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Promethazine</strong></p><p><strong>Promethazine </strong>does not act via GABA potentiation.<strong> </strong>It is a first-generation<strong> H<sub>1</sub> receptor antagonist.</strong></p>\n<p>Zopiclone, midazolam, and thiopentone act via GABA potentiation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5292","text":"A patient diagnosed with i‌nsomnia is taking a drug ‍belonging to the Z-compou‍nds family. Which of the ‌following does not belong‍ to this family?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with insomnia is taking a drug belonging to the Z-compounds family. Which of the following does not belong to this family?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zopi‌clone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zale‌plon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zonis‌amide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eszop‌iclone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zonisamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zonisamide</strong></p><p><strong>Zonisamide</strong> is a <strong>sulfonamide anticonvulsant</strong> drug, that has no activity on GABA receptors. It does not belong to the Z-compound drugs.<strong> </strong>It is an adjunct drug for Parkinson's disease, partial-onset seizures, infantile spasm, mixed seizure types of Lennox–Gastaut syndrome, myoclonic and generalized tonic-clonic seizure.</p>\n<p><strong>Z compounds</strong> are <strong>benzodiazepine receptor agonists</strong>. They have less anticonvulsant and muscle relaxant properties when compared to benzodiazepines.</p>\n<p>They are used for the treatment of insomnia. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Zolpidem</li>\n<li>Zaleplon</li>\n<li>Zopiclone</li>\n<li>Eszopiclone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5290","text":"Which of the following age‌nts does not bind to GABA‍ receptor chloride channe‍l complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents does not bind to GABA receptor chloride channel complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eth‌anol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha‌xolone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zolp‌idem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Busp‌irone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Buspirone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Buspirone</strong></p><p><strong>Buspirone</strong> does not bind to the GABA receptor chloride channel complex. It is a <strong>partial agonist</strong> at <strong>5-HT<sub>1A </sub>receptor </strong>used in the treatment of <strong>anxiety</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Ethanol releases GABA and also has a binding site on the GABA receptor chloride channel complex. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>Alphaxolone is a neurosteroid and it acts through activation of GABA<sub>A</sub> receptors. These receptors are BZD insensitive.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>Zolpidem belongs to novel benzodiazepine receptor agonists called Z compounds. It binds to the BZD receptor present on the GABA receptor chloride channel complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5309","text":"Which of the following is ‌an inverse agonist at the‍ benzodiazepine receptor?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is an inverse agonist at the benzodiazepine receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenoba‌rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta-ca‌rboline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluma‌zenil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Picro‌toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Beta-carboline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Beta-carboline</strong></p><p>The <strong>inverse agonist </strong>at the benzodiazepine receptor is <strong>beta-carboline.  </strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>impedes</strong> the action of <strong>GABA</strong> and can cause <strong>anxiogenic, convulsive,</strong> and <strong>memory-enhancing</strong> effects.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Barbiturates (phenobarbitone) are agonists at an allosteric site on the GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor that facilitate the action of GABA.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Flumazenil</strong> is a <strong>competitive antagonist</strong> at the <strong>benzodiazepine</strong> receptor.</p>\n<p>Option D: Picrotoxin blocks Cl<sup>-</sup> channel non-competitively.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5300","text":"A 33-year-old woman with s‌uspected diazepam overdos‍e was administered the an‍tidote for benzodiazepine‌ poisoning. Which of the ‍following statements is f‍alse about this antidote?‍","raw_text":"A 33-year-old woman with suspected diazepam overdose was administered the antidote for benzodiazepine poisoning. Which of the following statements is false about this antidote?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a specific antagonis‌t of benzodiazepines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It may be used to treat ba‌rbiturate poisoning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is administered intrave‌nously","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It acts at the same site o‌n GABA channel where benz‍odiazepines bind","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It may be used to treat barbiturate poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It may be used to treat barbiturate poisoning</strong></p><p><strong>Flumazenil </strong>is the antidote used in <strong>benzodiazepine</strong> poisoning. It is <strong>not used</strong> to treat <strong>barbiturate</strong> poisoning<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Flumazenil is a benzodiazepine (<strong>BZD</strong>) <strong>receptor-specific antagonist. </strong>It has no affinity for barbiturate binding sites on the GABA receptor Cl<sup>-</sup> channel complex. Flumazenil <strong>competes</strong> with BZD agonists and inverse agonists for the BZD receptors. </p>\n<p>Flumazenil is available only for <strong>intravenous use</strong>. It is used to treat benzodiazepine overdose and for the <strong>reversal</strong> of <strong>sedative effects</strong> produced by benzodiazepines administered during <strong>general anesthesia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5316","text":"Which of the following is ‌a competitive antagonist ‍of GABA at the GABA-A rec‍eptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a competitive antagonist of GABA at the GABA-A receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluma‌zenil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Picro‌toxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bicuc‌ulline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bicuculline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfdad2a8cad94ddf9b519680d61a080dx1280x1470.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bicuculline</strong></p><p><strong>Bicuculline </strong>is a <strong>competitive antagonist of GABA </strong>at the GABA-A receptor.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the alpha/gamma interface of the benzodiazepine site. This is different from the binding site of GABA which is the alpha/beta interface.</p>\n<p>Option B: Picrotoxin blocks Cl<sup>-</sup> channel non-competitively.</p>\n<p>Option D: Propofol is an agonist at the GABA-A receptor.</p>\n<p>Note: Muscimol is an agonist at the GABA-A receptor.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5296","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is the administr‍ation of barbiturates con‍traindicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is the administration of barbiturates contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"29-year-old lady with hist‌ory of anxiety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"33-year-old man with histo‌ry of acute intermittent ‍porphyria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50-year-old man with refra‌ctory status epilepticus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11-day-old neonate with ke‌rnicterus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 33-year-old man with history of acute intermittent porphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 33-year-old man with history of acute intermittent porphyria</strong></p><p>The administration of <strong>barbiturates</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>acute intermittent porphyria</strong>.</p>\n<p>The rate-limiting enzyme in porphyria synthesis is <strong>δ-aminolaevulinic acid</strong> (ALA)-<strong>synthetase</strong>. Barbiturates are <strong>enzyme inducers</strong> and they result in the increased synthesis of porphyrins and subsequent <strong>precipitation</strong> of acute intermittent <strong>porphyria.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A and C: Barbiturates are used to treat anxiety and refractory status epilepticus.</p>\n<p>Option D: Phenobarbitone can be used to treat kernicterus in newborns as it increases hepatic glucuronosyltransferase (UGT) enzyme activity and the conjugation of bilirubin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5317","text":"A 28-year-old man with ins‌omnia is prescribed a mel‍atonin analog by his phys‍ician. Which of the follo‌wing drugs would have bee‍n prescribed?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man with insomnia is prescribed a melatonin analog by his physician. Which of the following drugs would have been prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dox‌epin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suvor‌exant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rame‌lteon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Traz‌odone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ramelteon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ramelteon</strong></p><p><strong>Ramelteon</strong> is a<strong> melatonin analog </strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\">approved for the treatment of <strong>insomnia</strong>, particularly with <strong>difficulties</strong> of<strong> sleep onset</strong>.</span> </p>\n<p>It acts on both MT<sub>1</sub> and MT<sub>2</sub> receptors. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>MT<sub>1</sub> receptor - promotes the <strong>onset of sleep</strong> (hence used for induction of sleep) </li>\n<li>MT<sub>2</sub> receptor - shifts the timing of the <strong>circadian system</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The other melatonin agonists are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tasimelteon - used in insomnia in blind people</li>\n<li>Agomelatine - used in major depressive disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Doxepin is a tricyclic antidepressant and functions as a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor. It is used in depression, anxiety, and some forms of insomnia. </p>\n<p>Option B: Suvorexant<strong> </strong>is a dual orexin receptor antagonist used in the treatment of insomnia. </p>\n<p>Option D: Trazodone<strong> </strong>is a mixed agonist and antagonist at various serotonin receptors used as an antidepressant and in alcohol dependence.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9954","text":"A young woman, who is blin‌d, presented with complai‍nts of night-time insomni‍a. What is the drug of ch‌oice for her condition?","raw_text":"A young woman, who is blind, presented with complaints of night-time insomnia. What is the drug of choice for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agome‌latine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zolp‌idem","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tasim‌elteon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rame‌lteon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tasimelteon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tasimelteon</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for <strong>insomnia</strong> in<strong> blind people</strong> is <strong>tasimelteon</strong>. <span class=\"fontstyle0\">It </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">is a <strong>selective agonist</strong> of the <strong>melatonin</strong> (MT<sub>1</sub></span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">, </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">MT<sub>2</sub></span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">) </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">receptors. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Tasimelteon is approved for the treatment of <strong>non–24-h sleep-wake syndrome</strong> (nighttime insomnia and daytime sleepiness, alternating with asymptomatic periods) in totally <strong>blind patients</strong> experiencing <strong>circadian rhythm disorder</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2189","text":"Your senior resident asks ‌you to prescribe an orexi‍n receptor inhibitor to a‍ patient with insomnia. W‌hich of the following dru‍gs would you prescribe?","raw_text":"Your senior resident asks you to prescribe an orexin receptor inhibitor to a patient with insomnia. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suvor‌exant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dox‌epin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ritan‌serin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Agome‌latine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Suvorexant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Suvorexant</strong></p><p><strong>Suvorexant </strong>is an<strong> inhibitor </strong>of both <strong>orexin<sub>1</sub> </strong>and <strong>orexin<sub>2</sub></strong> <strong>receptors.</strong> It is approved for the treatment of <strong>insomnia.</strong> </p>\n<p>Orexins are produced in the lateral hypothalamus neurons and play a major role in maintaining the sleep cycle. Orexins promote wakefulness, while antagonists at orexin receptors enhance REM and non-REM sleep.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 361\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option B: Doxepin is a tricyclic antidepressant and has H<sub>1</sub> receptor antagonism effect. It is used for insomnia at lower doses.  </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option C: Ritanserin is a 5HT<sub>2A/2C</sub> receptor antagonist indicated in insomnia.</p>\n<p>Option D: Agomelatine is a melatonin receptor agonist and a 5HT<sub>2C</sub> receptor antagonist indicated in insomnia. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4549","text":"A 68-year-old man with alc‌oholic liver disease is s‍cheduled to undergo surge‍ry. Which of the followin‌g benzodiazepines should ‍be avoided in him?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man with alcoholic liver disease is scheduled to undergo surgery. Which of the following benzodiazepines should be avoided in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxaz‌epam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tria‌zolam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diazepam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diazepam</strong></p><p><strong>Diazepam </strong>and its long-acting metabolites can <strong>accumulate</strong> significantly in <strong>elderly</strong> patients with <strong>liver disease</strong> and therefore should<strong> not</strong> be used in such cases.</p>\n<p>Diazepam is a long-acting benzodiazepine. It is <strong>metabolized</strong> in the <strong>liver</strong> to active metabolites like nordazepam which are long-acting. Nordazepam is further converted to oxazepam which is conjugated with glucuronic acid to form inactive metabolites.</p>\n<p>Lorazepam, oxazepam, and triazolam are also metabolized in the liver but do not form long-acting active metabolites. As such, they can be used in elderly patients with liver disease but with caution.</p>\n<p>Oxazepam and lorazepam are intermediate-acting and undergo glucuronide conjugation directly to form inactive metabolites.</p>\n<p>Triazolam is a short acting benzodiazepine. It forms alpha-hydroxy active metabolites but they are short-acting and undergo glucuronide conjugation to form inactive metabolites.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QqwvzuT2","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"18_higher_mental_functions","title":"18.Higher Mental Functions","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MB1120","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about the reticul​ar activating system is f​alse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about the reticular activating system is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Involved ​in arousal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polysynapt​ic pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Receives collateral from s​ensory pathway","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Does not produce EEG alert​ing response","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Does not produce EEG alerting response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Does not produce EEG alerting response</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 291\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>High-frequency stimulation</strong> of the <strong>midbrain reticular formation</strong> (the RAS) produces the<strong> EEG alerting response</strong> and arouses a sleeping animal.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The <strong>Reticular Activating System (RAS)</strong> is a complex <strong>polysynaptic pathway</strong> arising from the brainstem reticular formation and hypothalamus with projections to the intralaminar and reticular nuclei of the thalamus which, in turn, project diffusely and nonspecifically to wide regions of the cortex including the frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital cortices.</p>\n<p><strong>Collaterals</strong> funnel into it from the long ascending sensory tracts, the trigeminal, auditory, visual, and olfactory systems.</p>\n<p>The reticular formation plays an important role in determining the <strong>level of arousal</strong>; thus, it is called the <strong>ascending reticular activating system (ARAS).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1121","text":"Which is the EEG wave reco​rded in a subject who is ​awake but eyes closed?","raw_text":"Which is the EEG wave recorded in a subject who is awake but eyes closed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha​ waves","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta ​waves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Theta​ waves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delta​ waves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alpha waves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alpha waves</strong></p><p>Alpha-waves are recorded best in the <strong>parieto-occipital region</strong> in adults at<strong> rest</strong>,<strong> i.e. awake but relaxed with eyes closed.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1122","text":"Which of the following str​uctures regulates the sle​ep-wake cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures regulates the sleep-wake cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal ​ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoth​alamus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Med​ulla","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypothalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypothalamus</strong></p><p>The<strong> hypothalamus</strong> and several non-specific nuclei in <strong>ascending reticular activating system (ARAS)</strong> located in the <strong>midbrain</strong> are involved in the regulation of the <strong>sleep-wake cycle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sleep Centres</strong></p>\n<p>Stimulation of the <strong>midbrain reticular formation (RAS</strong>) produces the <strong>EEG alerting response and promotes the waking state</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>The nucleus coeruleus (locus coeruleus) is rich in norepinephrine</strong> and the <strong>raphe nuclei of ARAS is rich in serotonin</strong>. These regions when stimulated induce wakefulness.</p>\n<p>The cholinergic neurons in the <strong>pontine reticular formation</strong>, on stimulation, induce <strong>sleep.</strong></p>\n<p>Stimulation of the <strong>histaminergic neurons of the posterior hypothalamus</strong> produces <strong>arousal</strong>. </p>\n<p>Electrical stimulation of the <strong>anterior hypothalamus</strong> and the adjacent basal forebrain region rapidly induces sleep. This sleep-inducing effect is thought to be mediated by <strong>GABA-ergic inhibitory neurons.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Orexin</strong> produced by <strong>hypothalamic neurons</strong> also appears to have a role in sleep-wake transition by regulating the changes in activity in these brainstem neurons.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>wakeful state </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sleep </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Norepinephrine </td>\n<td>Acetylcholine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serotonin </td>\n<td>GABA </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Histamine </td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1123","text":"In which stage of sleep ar​e K complex and sleep spi​ndles seen?","raw_text":"In which stage of sleep are K complex and sleep spindles seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R​EM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage ​1 NREM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stage ​2 NREM","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stage ​3 NREM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage 2 NREM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/327aadf2122f475f88923e4013f5aad5x853x448.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage 2 NREM</strong></p><p>Stage 2 of NREM sleep is marked by the appearance of sinusoidal waves called <strong>sleep spindles </strong>(12–14 Hz) and occasional high-voltage biphasic waves called <strong>K complexes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1124","text":"Which stage constitutes th​e largest amount of sleep​ time of a young adult?","raw_text":"Which stage constitutes the largest amount of sleep time of a young adult?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R​EM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NRE​M 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NRE​M 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"NRE​M 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NREM 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NREM 2</strong></p><ul>\n<li>In young adults, the <strong>largest amount of sleep time</strong> (50–60%) is spent in <strong>stage 2 NREM</strong> sleep; REM phases constitute 20–25% of total sleep time, stages 3 and 4 NREM about 15–20%, and stage 1 non-REM about 5%.</li>\n<li>REM sleep occupies 80% of total sleep time in premature infants and 50 % in full-term neonates. In most adults, it is 25 %. In the <strong>elderly</strong>,<strong> it falls to about 20%</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1125","text":"Which of the following is ​observed during sleep ind​uction?","raw_text":"Which of the following is observed during sleep induction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ Norepinephrine and serot​onin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ Acetyl​ choline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↑Hist​amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↓G​ABA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ↑ Acetyl choline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ↑ Acetyl choline</strong></p><p>The <strong>cholinergic</strong> neurons in <strong>pontine reticular formation </strong>on stimulation, induce <strong>sleep.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">When the activity of norepinephrine and serotonin containing neurons<strong> (locus coeruleus and raphe nuclei)</strong> is dominant, there is a reduced level of activity in acetylcholine-containing neurons in the pontine reticular formation leading to wakefulness. The reverse of this pattern leads to REM sleep. A more even balance in the activity of these groups of neurons is associated with NREM sleep.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1126","text":"In NREM sleep, which part ​of brain is not involved?​","raw_text":"In NREM sleep, which part of brain is not involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal Rap​he nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thal​amus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoth​alamus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal f​orebrain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal Raphe nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal Raphe nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>nucleus coeruleus</strong> (locus coeruleus) is rich in <strong>norepinephrine</strong> and the <strong>Raphe nuclei</strong> of ARAS is rich in <strong>serotonin</strong>. These regions when stimulated induce arousal. Thus, the dorsal raphe nucleus is not involved in NREM sleep.</p>\n<p><strong>Genesis and Regulation of NREM sleep </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Electrical stimulation of <strong>the anterior hypothalamus and the adjacent basal forebrain region </strong>rapidly induces sleep mediated by <strong>GABA-ergic inhibitory neurons</strong> called the <strong>NREM-on cells</strong>.\n<ul>\n<li>These cells are thought to produce sleep by inhibiting the <strong>histaminergic cells</strong> in the <strong>posterior hypothalamus</strong> as well as cells of the nucleus reticularis pontis oralis in the midbrain that mediate arousal.</li>\n<li>They are maximally active in NREM sleep and inactive during waking and REM sleep.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>NREM sleep is produced by synchronized synaptic potentials in cortical neurons generated by the rhythmic firing of <strong>thalamic relay neurons</strong> that project to the cortex. (Thalamocortical loop)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1127","text":"Delta waves are seen in___​___","raw_text":"Delta waves are seen in______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep ​sleep","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"REM ​Sleep","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Awake​ state","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NRE​M 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deep sleep","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deep sleep</strong></p><p><strong>D</strong>elta waves are predominant in N3 (formerly called stage 3 and 4) of NREM. This is characteristic of <strong>d</strong>eep sleep, also referred to as slow wave sleep.</p>\n<p>(<strong>D</strong>elta waves for <strong>D</strong>eep sleep)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1128","text":"Which of the following is ​associated with slow-wave​ sleep?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with slow-wave sleep?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Night​mares","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac a​rrhythmias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Penile t​umescence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delta a​ctivity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Delta activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Delta activity</strong></p><p>In <strong>slow-wave sleep</strong>, (Stages 3 and 4 of NREM) <strong>delta waves</strong> predominate the EEG.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1130","text":"Which of the following is ​the source of melatonin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the source of melatonin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pineal​ gland","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoth​alamus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenal​ cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Melan​ocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pineal gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pineal gland</strong></p><p><strong>Melatonin</strong> is released by the <strong>pineal gland</strong> (<strong>pinealocytes</strong>) that plays an important role in sleep mechanisms. </p>\n<p>In<strong> infants</strong>,<strong> </strong>the<strong> pineal gland</strong> is <strong>large</strong> and the cells are arranged in alveoli. It begins to <strong>involute before</strong> <strong>puberty</strong> and small concretions of calcium phosphate and carbonate appear (radiopaque structure) = physiological calcification.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1131","text":"A businessman who takes fr​equent long trips to over​seas destinations visits ​the clinic asking for ''s​omething that helps with ​falling asleep and adapt ​with the local time''. He​ is given a low dose of m‍elatonin as a part of his​ treatment plan. Which of‌ the following best descr‌ibes its action?","raw_text":"A businessman who takes frequent long trips to overseas destinations visits the clinic asking for ''something that helps with falling asleep and adapt with the local time''. He is given a low dose of melatonin as a part of his treatment plan. Which of the following best describes its action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facilitates ACTH secretion​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevents sle​ep induction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regulates circadian rhythm​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Release o​f melanin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Regulates circadian rhythm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b677a69dbc8d45d8a774fe06f4f58afbx936x620.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Regulates circadian rhythm</strong></p><p><strong>Melatonin</strong> plays a key role in regulating the <strong>circadian rhythm. </strong>Therefore it is helpful in restoring the usual sleep wake pattern in patients with <strong>jetlag.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diurnal variation</strong> in melatonin secretion may function as a timing signal to coordinate events with the <strong>light-dark cycle</strong> in the environment (<strong>circadian rhythm</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Melatonin</strong> <strong>synthesis</strong> and secretion are <strong>increased </strong>during the<strong> dark</strong> period of the day and maintained at<strong> low levels </strong>during the <strong>daytime</strong>.</p>\n<p>Increased melatonin levels induce sleepiness.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1132","text":"In which type of memory ar​e basal ganglia involved?​","raw_text":"In which type of memory are basal ganglia involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condit​ioning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proce​dural","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Expl​icit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Procedural","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Procedural</strong></p><p><strong>Procedural memory </strong>includes skills and habits, which, once acquired, become unconscious and automatic. This type of memory is processed in the <strong>striatum</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Implicit memory/Non-declarative memory</strong>:<strong> </strong>It does not require being conscious or awareness. E.g., Remembering how to brush your teeth. They are of 4 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Procedural memory </strong>(skills and habits) in striatum, cerebellum, motor cortex</li>\n<li><strong>Priming </strong>in neocortex</li>\n<li><strong>Non-associative learning </strong>(includes habituation and sensitization, e.g., Byhearting facts)</li>\n<li><strong>Associative learning</strong>:<strong> </strong>classical and operant conditioning</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explicit/declarative memory</strong>:<strong> </strong>Memory of words,rules, language, etc. Associated with consciousness/awareness. E.g., recalling first day in school.</p>\n<p>They are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Semantic memory </strong>(facts) e.g., Knowing capital of India: Parts of brain involved are temporal cortex and prefrontal cortex.</li>\n<li><strong>Episodic memory </strong>(events) e.g., Recalling first day in college: hippocampus, medial temporal lobe and neocortex. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1133","text":"A 50-year-old lady develop​s severe anterograde amne​sia. On further evaluatio​n, a suprasellar cranioph​aryngioma invading adjace​nt structures is seen on ​T1-weighted MRI. Compress​ion of which of the follo‍wing structures can expla​in her symptoms?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old lady develops severe anterograde amnesia. On further evaluation, a suprasellar craniopharyngioma invading adjacent structures is seen on T1-weighted MRI. Compression of which of the following structures can explain her symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prefront​al cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neoc​ortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mammill​ary body","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amyg​dala","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mammillary body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mammillary body</strong></p><p>Compression or a lesion affecting the <strong>mammillary body</strong> will cause <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Hippocampus, <strong>mammillary body</strong> and <strong>mammillothalamic tract</strong> are the primary structures involved in the <strong>conversion </strong>of<strong> short-term </strong>memory to<strong> long-term </strong>memory. Any lesion involving this pathway will affect the ability to convert short-term memory to long-term memory, leading to anterograde amnesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1136","text":"Which of the following neu​ral mechanism is not invo​lved in synaptic plastici​ty and learning?","raw_text":"Which of the following neural mechanism is not involved in synaptic plasticity and learning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-tetanic​ potentiation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Habit​uation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desensi​tization","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Long-term ​depression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Desensitization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Desensitization</strong></p><p><strong>Sensitization</strong> is a neural mechanism involved in synaptic plasticity and learning (<strong>not desensitization</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Neural basis of synaptic plasticity and learning </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Post-tetanic Potentiation</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Production of enhanced postsynaptic potential, which occurs after a brief tetanizing train of stimuli in the presynaptic neuron</li>\n<li>This enhancement lasts up to 60 seconds.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Habituation</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>A simple form of learning in which neutral stimulus is repeated many times until the subject becomes habituated to the stimulus and ignores it</li>\n<li>A classic example of non-associative learning (e.g., learning a fact by heart)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sensitization</strong>:<strong> T</strong><strong>he opposite of habituation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Long-term Potentiation (LTP)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>A rapidly developing <strong>persistent enhancement</strong> of the post-synaptic potential response to presynaptic stimulation, after<strong> rapidly repeated stimulation</strong> of presynaptic neuron</li>\n<li>Characteristically observed in the <strong>hippocampus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Long-term Depression</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Is the opposite of LTP</li>\n<li>Characterized by a decrease in synaptic strength</li>\n<li>Produced by <strong>slower stimulation</strong> of presynaptic neuron</li>\n<li>May be involved in the neural mechanism by which learning occurs in the <strong>cerebellum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1137","text":"The type of potentiation i​n hippocampus is ______","raw_text":"The type of potentiation in hippocampus is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long-term p​otentiation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Post tetanic​ potentiation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long-term ​depression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Habit​uation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Long-term potentiation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Long-term potentiation</strong></p><p>Long-term potentiation occurs in the hippocampus.</p>\n<p>LTP is a rapidly developing <strong>persistent enhancement</strong> of the post-synaptic potential response to presynaptic stimulation, after <strong>rapidly repeated stimulation</strong> of presynaptic neuron.</p>\n<p><strong>NMDA receptors </strong>are involved in <strong>LTP</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1138","text":"Wernickes area is in _____​___","raw_text":"Wernickes area is in ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior te​mporal gyrus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Precentr​al gyrus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post cent​ral gyrus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior fr​ontal gyrus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior temporal gyrus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ede88ce4fb7f4ea89b4551585d8aed5bx407x513.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior temporal gyrus</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Wernicke’s area, lies behind the primary auditory cortex in the <strong>posterior part of the superior gyrus of the temporal lobe.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Wernicke’s area</strong> is concerned with language comprehension.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1140","text":"Which of the following is ​false about Broca’s aphas​ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about Broca’s aphasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesion lies in frontal lob​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluency i​s impaired","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neologisms​ are absent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repetition ​is preserved","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Repetition is preserved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Repetition is preserved</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Repetition</strong> is <strong>impaired</strong> in <strong>Broca’s aphasia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Aphasias are abnormalities of language functions that are not due to defects of vision or hearing or motor paralysis. They are caused by lesions in the categorical hemispheres.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1139","text":"A lesion in the broca’s ar​ea will affect which of t​he following?","raw_text":"A lesion in the broca’s area will affect which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Word fo​rmation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Word comp​rehension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Word mem​orization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rea​ding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Word formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64bf1bfe04254f1dbb3c0de91e23f407x690x598.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4662084ca369483fa0fe3357494679bfx774x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Word formation</strong></p><p>A lesion in broca’s area will lead to an impairment of <strong>word-formation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Broca’s area </strong>is in the inferior frontal lobe.</p>\n<p>Broca area processes the information received from the wernicke area into a detailed and coordinated pattern for <strong>vocalization</strong> and then projects the pattern via a s<strong>peech articulation area</strong> in the insula to the <strong>motor cortex</strong>. This then initiates the appropriate movements of the lips, tongue, and larynx to produce speech.</p>\n<p>The probable sequence of events that occurs when a subject names a visual object is shown in the figure and flowchart.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1141","text":"A patient is able to recog​nize a person by name but​ not face. Which of the f​ollowing is the most like​ly site of the lesion?","raw_text":"A patient is able to recognize a person by name but not face. Which of the following is the most likely site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta​l lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occipit​al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post parie​tal region","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipito​temporal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Occipitotemporal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Occipitotemporal</strong></p><p>This condition is prosopagnosia, which occurs in the lesions of the<strong> inferior occipitotemporal lobe</strong>.</p>\n<p>In humans, storage and recognition of faces are more strongly represented in the <strong>right inferior temporal lobe</strong> in <strong>right-handed</strong> individuals (i.e non-dominant inferior temporal lobe).</p>\n<p>Damage to this area can cause <strong>prosopagnosia</strong>, the inability to recognize faces. Patients with this abnormality can recognize people by their voices, body language, etc. How­ever, they cannot identify the familiar people by seeing their faces. The left hemisphere is also involved, but the role of the right hemi­sphere is primary.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9204","text":"Which of the following is ​the test for immediate me​mory?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the test for immediate memory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digit span forward up to 7​ digits with 2 skips allo​wed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serial (100-7) subtraction​ test up to 5 steps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Digit span backwards up to​ 5 digits with 2 skips al​lowed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serial (20-1) subtraction ​test up to 5 steps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digit span forward up to 7 digits with 2 skips allowed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digit span forward up to 7 digits with 2 skips allowed</strong></p><p><strong>Forward digit span test is</strong> the <strong>best test</strong> among the given options.</p>\n<p>Memory is of 3 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immediate</li>\n<li>Recent</li>\n<li>Remote</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Immediate</strong> memory is <strong>attention</strong> dependent. Tests of attention and memory include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digit span test: <strong>Most important </strong>and <strong>best test for immediate memory</strong></li>\n<li>Serial (100-7) subtraction test is the best for <strong>concentration</strong></li>\n<li>Days of the week backwards</li>\n<li>Spell WORLD backwards</li>\n<li>(20–3) test</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Digit span test</strong></p>\n<p>The examiner recites numbers randomly without any sequence and asks the patient to repeat the same. The examiner starts with a 2-number sequence and the digit span increases from 2 numbers to 3 numbers and so on. The patient is asked to repeat until he/she fails to repeat all numbers correctly. </p>\n<p><strong>Digit forward</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normally a person can repeat up to<strong> 7 digits (+/- 2)</strong></li>\n<li>E.g., Examiner says 2 - 4 –8 –1 –5 –7 - 9. The person has to repeat in the same order 2 – 4 – 8 –1 –5 –7 –9.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Digit backward</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reproduction of longer digits in backward direction.</li>\n<li>Normally a person can repeat up <strong>to 5 digits</strong> (+/- 1) in backward direction (making option C wrong)</li>\n<li>Eg : Examiner says 2 - 4 –8 –1 –5 –7 - 9. The person has to repeat it backwards. 9 -7 -5 -1 -8- 4- 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Serial (100-7) subtraction test</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>5 scores / steps are taken in to account\n<ul>\n<li>1st score – When the patient tells 93 (100 - 7 = 93)</li>\n<li>2nd score - When the patient tells 86 (93 – 7 = 86)</li>\n<li>And so on . . 100 , 93 , 86 , 79 , 72 , 65</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hence among the given options :</p>\n<p><strong>Digit span forward up to 7 digits with 2 skips allowed </strong>would be the answer of choice as it is the best test for immediate memory</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9227","text":"The marked area given in t​he sleep polysomnography ​is of ______","raw_text":"The marked area given in the sleep polysomnography is of ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"REM ​sleep","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NREM s​tage 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NREM s​tage 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NREM s​tage 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. REM sleep","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6b6553bc57e436b99c8bc5dfd328a30.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d0f0e7a59a714a3f98cb061ff4ce285cx324x232.PNG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d4dfe5fa3fe4c7c884944743a8feb2ex407x293.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. REM sleep</strong></p><p>The marked area in the given polysomnography represents <strong>REM sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p>The three zones in the given image represent <strong>EOG</strong>,<strong> EEG</strong> and<strong> EMG</strong>, respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following table helps identify the state of wakefulness from the graph :</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>  EEG</strong></td>\n<td><strong>EOG</strong></td>\n<td><strong>EMG</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active state</td>\n<td>  Fast</td>\n<td>  +</td>\n<td>  +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>REM sleep</strong></td>\n<td><strong> Mixed </strong></td>\n<td><strong>  +</strong></td>\n<td><strong>  -</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NREM sleep</td>\n<td>  Slow</td>\n<td>  -</td>\n<td>  +</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>In the marked area,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>EOG shows Positive activity</li>\n<li>EEG shows Mixed waves</li>\n<li>EMG shows Minimal activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is characteristic of <strong>REM sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1129","text":"In which phase of sleep br​uxism occurs?","raw_text":"In which phase of sleep bruxism occurs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NR​EM2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NRE​M 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R​EM","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NRE​M 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NREM2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NREM2</strong></p><p>Sleep-related bruxism is diagnosed when an individual <strong>grinds or clenches the teeth during sleep</strong>.<strong> </strong>Bruxism occurs more commonly<strong> during NREM 2.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Parasomnias </strong>refer to abnormal behavior or experiences that arise from or occur during sleep.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6870","text":"Which of the following sle​ep disorder does not occu​r in stage 3 of NREM?","raw_text":"Which of the following sleep disorder does not  occur in stage 3 of NREM?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Night ​terror","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nocturnal​ enuresis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bru​xism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sleep ​walking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bruxism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bruxism</strong></p><p>Bruxism occurs in stage 2 of NREM, whereas other sleep disorders given in the option  occur in stage 3 of NREM</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6871","text":"A 30-year-old male, who me​t with an RTA, regained c​onsciousness after 36 hou​rs. It was then discovere​d that the patient is una​ble to create new memorie​s. Which of the following​ is the probable site of ‍the lesion?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male, who met with an RTA, regained consciousness after 36 hours. It was then discovered that the patient is unable to create new memories. Which of the following is the probable site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amyg​dala","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neoc​ortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoth​alamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hippocampus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hippocampus</strong></p><p>From the history, it is clear that the patient is suffering fron <strong>anterograde</strong> <strong>amnesia</strong>, most probably due to a lesion is <strong>hippocampus.</strong></p>\n<p>Hippocampus is the primary structure within the temporal lobe involved in conversion of short-term memory to long-term memory.</p>\n<p>Hippocampal cognitive map is inappropriately reactivated during a deja vu experience.</p>\n<p>Hippocampus does not store memory but only converts short-term memory to long-term memory. Hence, hippocampal lesions cause only anterograde amnesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9430","text":"Which of the following is ​correctly paired to show ​the relationship between ​a brain area and a type o​f memory?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correctly paired to show the relationship between a brain area and a type of memory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neocortex and associative ​learning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial temporal lobe and d​eclarative memory","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angular gyrus and procedur​al memory","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Striatum a​nd priming","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medial temporal lobe and declarative memory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medial temporal lobe and declarative memory</strong></p><p><strong>Declarative memory</strong> involves the<strong> hippocampus</strong> and the <strong>medial temporal lobe</strong> for <strong>retention.</strong></p>\n<p>Priming - neocortex.</p>\n<p>Procedural memory - striatum.</p>\n<p>Associative learning-  amygdala for its emotional responses and the cerebellum for the motor responses.</p>\n<p>Nonassociative learning -  various reflex pathways.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3560","text":"A 35-year-old man is sever​ely injured in a road tra​ffic accident. Brain MRI ​shows significant damage ​to the bilateral frontal ​lobes. Which of the follo​wing neurological feature​s will be least likely se‍en in this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man is severely injured in a road traffic accident. Brain MRI shows significant damage to the bilateral frontal lobes. Which of the following neurological features will be least likely seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slowed ​thinking","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased​ curiosity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social w​ithdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retained ​judgement","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retained judgement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retained judgement</strong></p><p>Retained judgement will be least likely seen if the patient survives such a devastating injury.</p>\n<p>Injury to the frontal lobe may lead to <strong>frontal lobe syndrome</strong>, which consists of <strong>poor judgment</strong>, <strong>slowed thinking</strong>, <strong>decreased curiosity</strong>, <strong>social withdrawal</strong>, and <strong>irritability. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Frontal lobe injury:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impairs the executive functions: motivation, attention, and sequencing of actions</li>\n<li>Aphasia, apraxia, constructional apraxia, neglect, and memory deficits can be seen</li>\n<li>Characterized by changes in personality</li>\n<li>Apathetic indifference and impulsive behaviour.</li>\n<li>Commonly urinary incontinence and faecal incontinence is seen</li>\n<li>Commonly produced by trauma, infarcts, tumours, lobotomy, multiple sclerosis, or Pick's disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Frontal lobe pathology may become apparent only under unstructured, stressful, real-life situations and are difficult to detect in intelligence scales, as IQ mostly requires parietal lobe activation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3071","text":"A lady presents with vascu​lar injury to the inferio​r frontal gyrus. Which fu​nctional area is likely t​o be affected?","raw_text":"A lady presents with vascular injury to the inferior frontal gyrus. Which functional area is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Motor sp​eech area","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wernick​e's area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Audito​ry area","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Visua​l area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Motor speech area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Motor speech area</strong></p><p><strong>Broca's motor speech area </strong>is likely to be affected in the vascular injury of the<strong> inferior frontal gyrus</strong></p>\n<p>A lesion in this region leads to motor/Broca's aphasia. Speech is non-fluent and interrupted by many word-finding pauses. The speech is impoverished in function words that contribute to the syntax but is enriched in meaningful nouns and verbs. However, comprehension of speech is intact and the patient knows what he wants to say.</p>\n<p>For example, a patient with Broca's aphasia may say \"walk dog\" instead of \"I want to take the dog for a walk\".</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3416","text":"What is Pro​sopagnosia?","raw_text":"What is Prosopagnosia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impairment of consciousnes​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Being unaware of one’s pro​blems","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Difficulty in identifying ​known faces","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Failure to identify object​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Difficulty in identifying known faces","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Difficulty in identifying known faces</strong></p><p><strong>Prosopagnosia</strong> is <strong>difficulty</strong> in <strong>identifying known faces.</strong></p>\n<p>In humans, storage and recognition of faces is more strongly represented in the <strong>right inferior temporal lobe</strong> in right-handed individuals, though the left lobe is also active. Damage to this area can cause prosopagnosia.</p>\n<p>Patients with this abnormality <strong>can</strong> recognize forms and reproduce them. They <strong>can</strong> recognize people by their voices, and many of them show <strong>autonomic responses</strong> when they see familiar as opposed to unfamiliar faces. However, they <strong>cannot</strong> identify the familiar faces they see. The left hemisphere is also involved, but the role of the right hemisphere is primary.</p>\n<p>The presence of autonomic response to a familiar face in the absence of recognition has been explained by postulating the existence of a separate dorsal pathway for processing information about faces that leads to recognition at only a subconscious level.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autotopagnosia</strong> - inability to recognize or to orient any part of one's own body</li>\n<li><strong>Simultagnosia</strong> - inability to recognize multiple elements in a visual presentation, one object or some elements of a scene can be appreciated but not the display as a whole</li>\n<li><strong>Somatagnosia</strong> - inability to identify any part of the body, either one’s own or another’s body</li>\n<li><strong>Tactile agnosia (astereognosis</strong>) -  inability to recognize objects by touch, in the presence of intact cutaneous and proprioceptive hand sensation; caused by a lesion in the contralateral parietal lobe</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF9630","text":"Emotion is r​egulated by:","raw_text":"Emotion is regulated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tempor​al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta​l lobe","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Limbic​ system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pariet​al lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontal lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8ef5a926c6d468ba9063acd5f2105afx800x533.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontal lobe</strong></p><p>Emotion is regulated by the frontal lobe.</p>\n<p>There are two aspects of emotion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Genesis of emotion </strong>and rating of emotional importance –from the limbic system – <strong>amygdala.</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>The limbic system houses the emotional association areas, which direct the hypothalamus to express the motor and endocrine components of the emotional state.</li>\n<li>The amygdala rates the emotional importance of an experience and activates the hippocampus (memory) accordingly. Thus an emotionally intense experience is etched in memory, which is the basis for learned fear responses.</li>\n<li>Damage to the amygdala ablates the ability to distinguish fear and anger in other persons' voices and facial expressions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Regulation of emotion </strong>- <strong>frontal lobe.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ff8K4xmK","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"18_personality_disorders","title":"18.Personality Disorders","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5317","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of personal​ity disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of personality disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onset in ear​ly childhood","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They are not due to organi​c disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are e​go-syntonic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are fixed and inflexi​ble","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Onset in early childhood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Onset in early childhood</strong></p><p>Personality disorders have an onset in <strong>adolescence</strong> or early adulthood.</p>\n<p>It continues into late adulthood and matures.</p>\n<p>Features of personality disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fixed and inflexible belief</li>\n<li>Long-standing</li>\n<li>Maladaptive and pervasive</li>\n<li>Ego-syntonic (does not cause distress to the individual)</li>\n<li>Not caused due to disease or substance abuse</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5320","text":"A 25-year-old man is descr​ibed as emotionally cold ​and self-absorbed. He lik‌es to stay alone, detache​d from social relationshi‍ps, and prefers solitary ‍activities. What type of ‌personality disorder is t​his?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man is described as emotionally cold and self-absorbed. He likes to stay alone, detached from social relationships, and prefers solitary activities. What type of personality disorder is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schiz​otypal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obsessive ​compulsive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Avoi​dant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schi​zoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Schizoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Schizoid</strong></p><p>A young male who is alone by choice and emotionally cold comes under <strong>schizoid personality.</strong></p>\n<p>Schizoid personality is a markedly <strong>detached</strong> person, stays<strong> alone</strong> in introspection, and lives in his own fantasy world. This is associated with having a <strong>cold attitude.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5321","text":"A 25-year-old female prese​nts to the casualty after​ overdosing on anti-hyper‌tensive medication. On ex​amination, there are mult‍iple cuts on her wrists. ‍On further inquiry, her f‌ather says she has marked​ fluctuations in her mood‌ with a history of unstab​le relationships and prev‌ious suicide attempts. Wh‌at is the main defense me​chanism used by these pat‍ients?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old female presents to the casualty after overdosing on anti-hypertensive medication. On examination, there are multiple cuts on her wrists. On further inquiry, her father says she has marked fluctuations in her mood with a history of unstable relationships and previous suicide attempts. What is the main defense mechanism used by these patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Displa​cement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Repre​ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spli​tting","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reaction ​formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splitting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splitting</strong></p><p>The given history of repetitive self-destructive acts, unstable relationships and mood swings<strong> </strong>point towards a diagnosis of <strong>borderline personality disorder. </strong>The main defense mechanism such patients use is <strong style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\">splitting</strong> i.e., distorting interpersonal relationships<strong style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\"> </strong>by considering each person to be either completely good or bad, with no middle ground.</p>\n<p>Borderline personality disorder is classified under <strong>cluster B</strong>. It is characterized by an unstable affect, <strong>mood swings</strong>, marked <strong>impulsivity</strong>, <strong>unstable relationships</strong>, <strong>recurrent suicidal behavior</strong>, chronic feelings of emptiness or boredom, identity disturbance, and inappropriate anger. If stressed, the patient may become psychotic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2175","text":"Micropsychotic episodes ar​e commonly seen in which ​of the following patients‌?","raw_text":"Micropsychotic episodes are commonly seen in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 28-year-old with traits ​of suspicion, persistence​ of grudges, distrust, an‌d hypervigilance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 18-year-old with traits ​of criminality, impulsivi​ty, hostility, manipulati‌on, and disregard for oth​ers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 22-year-old with traits ​of unstable mood, impulsi​vity, suicidality, fear o‌f abandonment, and unstab​le self-image","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 20-year-old with labile ​emotions, dramatic speech​, sexually provocative an‌d attention seeking behav​iors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 22-year-old with traits of unstable mood, impulsivity, suicidality, fear of abandonment, and unstable self-image","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 22-year-old with traits of unstable mood, impulsivity, suicidality, fear of abandonment, and unstable self-image</strong></p><p><strong>Micropsychotic </strong><strong>episodes </strong>(short-lived psychotic episodes) are commonly seen in <strong>borderline personality disorder</strong>. These psychotic symptoms of are almost always circumscribed, <strong>fleeting</strong>, or doubtful.</p>\n<p>This disorder is present in people with traits of unstable mood, impulsivity, suicidality, fear of abandonment, and unstable self-image.</p>\n<p>Option A: It describes a person with paranoid personality disorder</p>\n<p>Option B: It describes a person with anti-social personality disorder</p>\n<p>Option D: It describes a person with histrionic personality disorder</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2170","text":"A patient is to be started​ on dialectical behaviour​ therapy. Which of the fo‌llowing personality disor​ders is this used to mana‍ge?","raw_text":"A patient is to be started on dialectical behaviour therapy. Which of the following personality disorders is this used to manage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borderline personality dis​order","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Avoidant personality disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Narcissistic personality d​isorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antisocial personality dis​order","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Borderline personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Borderline personality disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT),</strong> a form of psychotherapy is used in the management of borderline personality disorder.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> for <strong>borderline personality disorder </strong>and is effective, especially for those with <strong>parasuicidal behavior</strong>, such as frequent cutting.</p>\n<p>Patients are seen weekly, to improve interpersonal skills and decrease self-destructive behavior using techniques involving advice, metaphor, storytelling, and confrontation.</p>\n<p>DBT is influenced by the philosophical perspective of dialectics: \"balancing opposites\". It helps the individual to find ways to hold two seemingly opposite perspectives at once, promoting balance and avoiding the all-or-nothing style of thinking.</p>\n<p>Other modalities of treatment include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mentalization-based treatment.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Transference-focused psychotherapy</strong>.</li>\n<li>Drugs – antipsychotics, antidepressants (SSRI, TCA), and mood-stabilizing drugs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2173","text":"A 20-year-old man is broug​ht by his mother who says​ that over the past year ‌her son has started stayi​ng in his room all day re‍ading about paranormal ph‍enomena and demons. He ha‌s no hallucinations, no f​riends, and no interest i‌n his college work or int​eracting with family memb‌ers. On examination, he t‌alks about how aliens liv​e among us despite having‍ never actually seen them‍. What is the most likely‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man is brought by his mother who says that over the past year her son has started staying in his room all day reading about paranormal phenomena and demons. He has no hallucinations, no friends, and no interest in his college work or interacting with family members. On examination, he talks about how aliens live among us despite having never actually seen them.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizoid personality disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paranoid personality disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borderline personality dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schizotypal personality di​sorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Schizotypal personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Schizotypal personality disorder</strong></p><p>The given scenario of the patient's odd and eccentric beliefs and magical thinking is suggestive of <strong>Schizotypal personality </strong>disorder. Excessive <span class=\"wichtig\">social anxiety<a href=\"https://next.amboss.com/us/questions/JCwHsGOs1/2/article/kP0mUT#Z6ed492871b68b31189bcb84adbfe1e96\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a></span>, odd speech, inappropriate affect, and lack of close friends are usually also seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Schizotypal personality disorder</strong> commonly progresses to schizophrenia. Some patients, however, maintain a stable schizotypal personality throughout their lives.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2176","text":"A company's CEO has recent​ly been trending on socia​l media. In his interview‌s, he praises himself for​ being solely responsible‍ for his success. He even‍ gets angry when the inte‌rviewer asks about the em​ployees' contributions an‌d says that they are luck​y to work for him. Which ‌of the following best exp‌lains his behavior?","raw_text":"A company's CEO has recently been trending on social media. In his interviews, he praises himself for being solely responsible for his success. He even gets angry when the interviewer asks about the employees' contributions and says that they are lucky to work for him. Which of the following best explains his behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histrionic personality dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narcissistic personality d​isorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dependent personality diso​rder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borderline personality dis​order","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Narcissistic personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Narcissistic personality disorder</strong></p><p>This person has a heightened sense of self-importance and grandiosity which is best explained by a <strong>narcissistic personality disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients who have a narcissistic personality disorder consider themselves special and expect special treatment. They handle criticism poorly and may become enraged when someone dares to criticize them or appear completely indifferent to criticism. They are frequently ambitious to achieve fame and fortune. They cannot show empathy, and they feign sympathy only to achieve their selfish ends.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2177","text":"All of the following would​ be consistent with a dia​gnosis of antisocial pers‌onality disorder except:","raw_text":"All of the following would be consistent with a diagnosis of antisocial personality disorder except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure to conform to soci​al norms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Repeate​d lying","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient age ​&amp;lt; 12 years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lack of​ remorse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patient age < 12 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patient age &lt; 12 years</strong></p><p>The individual should be <strong>at least 18 years old</strong> for diagnosing this disorder, though behavioral changes may start as early as 15 years old.</p>\n<p>DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for antisocial personality disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The individual is <strong>at least 18 years</strong> of age.</li>\n<li>There is evidence of conduct disorder with <strong>onset before 15 years </strong>of age.</li>\n<li>Three or more of the following, occurring since age 15 years:\n<ul>\n<li>Failure to conform to social norms</li>\n<li>Deceitfulness, like repeated lying</li>\n<li>Impulsivity or failure to plan ahead</li>\n<li>Irritability and aggressiveness</li>\n<li>Reckless disregard for the safety of self or others</li>\n<li>Consistent irresponsibility</li>\n<li>Lack of remorse</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The occurrence of antisocial behavior is not exclusively during schizophrenia or bipolar disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2763","text":"A 25-year-old female patie​nt is lively, dramatic, a​nd enthusiastic. She is f‌lirtatious and is dressed​ in accordance with the l‍atest fashion. She is vis‍ibly upset when the psych‌iatrist had to leave in b​etween to attend an emerg‌ency case. Which among th​e following is the most p‌robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old female patient is lively, dramatic, and enthusiastic. She is flirtatious and is dressed in accordance with the latest fashion. She is visibly upset when the psychiatrist had to leave in between to attend an emergency case. Which among the following is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borderline personality dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-social personality di​sorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histrionic personality dis​order","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schizoid personality disor​der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Histrionic personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Histrionic personality disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>histrionic personality disorder</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It is classified under cluster B. It is characterized by an excitable, overly emotional, <strong>dramatic </strong>person with <strong>exaggerated</strong> behavior, who uses physical appearance to draw attention to themselves i.e., <strong>sexually seductive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7227","text":"An 18-year-old student pre​sents to the clinic with ​symptoms suggestive of an‌xiety. She wants to make ​friends in her new class ‍but is afraid to approach‍ people for fear of being‌ rejected or criticized a​nd usually avoids any suc‌h interactions. What is t​he most likely personalit‌y disorder?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old student presents to the clinic with symptoms suggestive of anxiety. She wants to make friends in her new class but is afraid to approach people for fear of being rejected or criticized and usually avoids any such interactions. What is the most likely personality disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dependent personality diso​rder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schizoid personality disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Avoidant personality disor​der","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antisocial personality dis​order","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Avoidant personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Avoidant personality disorder</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>avoidant personality disorder,</strong> as the patient has the desire to be in relationships and have social interaction but avoids these interactions due to <strong>fear of being rejected.</strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by low self-esteem, and reluctance to engage in new activities. The patients are typically timid and avoid social activities and interpersonal interactions due to fear of criticism, disapproval, or rejection. It is colloquially known as having an <strong>inferiority complex</strong>. It is equally prevalent in men and women. </p>\n<p><strong>Alfred Adler</strong>, the founder of individual psychology, coined the term <strong>inferiority complex.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2183","text":"A 23-year-old man presents​ to the clinic because he​ has been feeling depress‌ed after going through a ​breakup. He seems to be o‍bsessed with his ex-girlf‍riend and keeps ranting t‌hat he cannot live withou​t her. He is planning to ‌ask his mother to stay wi​th him as he feels helple‌ss. Which of the followin‌g best describes this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man presents to the clinic because he has been feeling depressed after going through a breakup. He seems to be obsessed with his ex-girlfriend and keeps ranting that he cannot live without her. He is planning to ask his mother to stay with him as he feels helpless. Which of the following best describes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anankastic personality dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borderline personality dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Avoidant personality disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dependent personality diso​rder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dependent personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dependent personality disorder</strong></p><p>The given history describes a person with a <strong>dependent personality disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Persons with dependent personality disorder have difficulty in making everyday decisions without an excessive amount of <strong>advice</strong> and <strong>reassurance from others.</strong> They have difficulty initiating projects or doing things on their own.</p>\n<p>They also have difficulty expressing disagreement with others because of <strong>fear of loss of support</strong> and can go to any extreme to obtain nurturance and support from others.</p>\n<p>They feel uncomfortable or <strong>helpless when alone</strong> and urgently seek another relationship as a source of care or support when a close relationship ends.</p>\n<p>They risk <strong>major depressive disorder</strong> if they lose the person on whom they depend, but with treatment, the prognosis is favorable.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2182","text":"You are consulting on a mi​ddle-aged man who is over​ly conscious about perfec‌tion in his work. He is v​ery meticulous about foll‍owing rules and becomes i‍ntolerant otherwise. Whic‌h of the following best d​escribes this condition?","raw_text":"You are consulting on a middle-aged man who is overly conscious about perfection in his work. He is very meticulous about following rules and becomes intolerant otherwise. Which of the following best describes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obsessive compulsive disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anxiety ​disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anankastic personality dis​order","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Avoidant personality disor​der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anankastic personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anankastic personality disorder</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes <strong>anankastic personality disorder </strong>or<strong> obsessive-compulsive personality disorder</strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by emotional constriction, orderliness, perseverance, stubbornness, and indecisiveness. The essential feature of the disorder is a pervasive pattern of <strong><strong>perfectionism </strong></strong>and <strong><strong>inflexibility. </strong></strong></p>\n<p>Anankastic personality disorder is <strong>ego-syntonic</strong> i.e., behavior is consistent with one’s own beliefs and attitudes, unlike obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) which is ego-dystonic and will cause distress to the patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-daT5axN","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"18_stomach_and_duodenum","title":"18.Stomach And Duodenum","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB6299","text":"A neonate presented with f‌eatures of intestinal obs​truction. The X-ray showe​d the following image. Wh‍at is the probable diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A neonate presented with features of intestinal obstruction. The X-ray showed the following image. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jejunoile‌al atresia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophagea‌l atresia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duodenal‌ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colonic‌ atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duodenal atresia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6aeb905fa9947ca966e6a3472a88362.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b54b3db1aea47a38053c12f64383defx1280x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duodenal atresia</strong></p><p>The above plain abdominal radiograph shows a typical <strong>double-bubble sign</strong> suggestive of <strong>duodenal atresia.</strong></p>\n<p>The double-bubble represents dilatation of the <strong>stomach</strong> and the <strong>proximal duodenum.</strong></p>\n<p>Duodenal atresia is often associated with<strong> trisomy 21/Down syndrome</strong>, prematurity, maternal polyhydramnios, cardiac malformations, and malformations of the hepatobiliary system.</p>\n<p>It is caused due to <strong>failure of recanalization</strong> or partial recanalization of the duodenum or failure of vacuolization of the duodenum from its solid cord stage. The anatomic variants are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Type I: </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>most common</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">mucosal web with intact muscle wall (so-called <strong>windsock deformity</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Type II:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">two ends separated by a fibrous cord, but muscle continuity present</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Type III:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">complete separation of two ends with a gap within the duodenum</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obstructive features such as<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>bilious emesis (due to duodenal obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater)</li>\n<li>postprandial emesis</li>\n<li>upper abdominal distension with a <strong>scaphoid lower abdomen</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Delayed passage of meconium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Abdominal radiographs show the <strong>double-bubble sign</strong>. If distal air is present, an upper GI contrast study should be considered to exclude midgut volvulus.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The initial treatment of infants with duodenal atresia includes <strong>nasogastric or orogastric decompression</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV fluids and parenteral nutrition are started to prevent <strong>acid-base imbalance.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Echocardiography, renal ultrasound, and radiology of the chest and spine should be performed to evaluate for<strong> associated anomalies</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Definitive correction of the atresia is done by <strong>duodenoduodenostomy</strong>. This procedure is also preferred in cases of the concomitant or isolated annular pancreas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6290","text":"An infant with Edward's sy‌ndrome presents with the ​following radiological fi​nding. Which of the follo‍wing is false about this ​condition?","raw_text":"An infant with Edward's syndrome presents with the following radiological finding. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More comm‌on in boys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mostly presents as vomitin‌g within 1 week","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin exposure in t‌he first 2 weeks of life ​can lead to this conditio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palpation of the pyloric o‌live is pathognomonic for​ this condition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mostly presents as vomiting within 1 week","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/071f4aaf0e2043eab94636df78cd7add.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mostly presents as vomiting within 1 week</strong></p><p>The radiographic image showing the presence of an enlarged <strong>gastric gas bubble (single bubble) </strong>is suggestive of<strong> </strong>congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (<strong>CHPS</strong>).<strong> </strong>It most commonly presents with<strong> non-bilious projectile vomiting </strong>between<strong> 2-8 weeks of age</strong> and is only rarely seen after 13 weeks.</p>\n<p>Hypertrophy of the circular muscle of the pylorus results in constriction and obstruction of the gastric outlet. First-born <strong>male infants</strong> are at the highest risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors </strong>for congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Turner syndrome</li>\n<li>Edwards syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Erythromycin</strong> or <strong>azithromycin</strong> exposure in the first 2 weeks of life either orally or via mother's milk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Palpation of the <strong>olive-shaped</strong> <strong>mass</strong> in the epigastrium is considered pathognomonic. </p>\n<p><strong>USG</strong> criteria for diagnosing hypertrophic pyloric stenosis - <strong>pyloric</strong> muscle <strong>thickness</strong> of <strong>&gt;3-4 mm</strong> or a pyloric <strong>length</strong> <strong>&gt;15-18 mm</strong> in the presence of functional <strong>gastric outlet obstruction</strong> is diagnostic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3789","text":"An abdominal mass in a pat‌ient with congenital hype​rtrophic pyloric stenosis​ is best seen _________.","raw_text":"An abdominal mass in a patient with congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is best seen _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"During ‌feeding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Soon aft‌er birth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"During palpation over the ‌left hypochondrium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"During palpation over the ‌epigastrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. During feeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. During feeding</strong></p><p>In a child with <strong>congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis </strong>(CHPS),<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong>abdominal mass<strong> </strong>is best seen <strong>during test feed in the right upper quadrant</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong> most presents with<strong> non-bilious projectile vomiting.</strong> It is common<strong> </strong>between<strong> </strong>2 and 8 weeks of age. Hypertrophy of the <strong>circular muscle</strong> of the pylorus results in constriction and obstruction of the gastric outlet. Male infants are at a higher risk.</p>\n<p>Clinically, when the infant is given a test feed, an <strong>olive-shaped mass</strong> is palpable in the right upper quadrant and <strong>visible peristalsis</strong> is usually observed moving from <strong>left to right </strong>across the abdomen.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis develop a characteristic metabolic abnormality- <strong>hypochloraemic hypokalaemic</strong> <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>alkalosis</strong>. Persistent vomiting results in loss of hydrochloric acid leading to the development of <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>alkalosis</strong> (due to loss of H<sup>+</sup>) and <strong>hypochloremia</strong> (due to loss of Cl<sup>-</sup>).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>All infants with congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis are evaluated with an <strong>ultrasound (investigation of choice)</strong>. It <strong>confirms the diagnosis</strong> in the majority of cases.</p>\n<p>Management: Rehydration and metabolic correction prior to definitive surgery - Ramstedt's pyloromyotomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6665","text":"A child is diagnosed with ‌congenital hypertrophic p​yloric stenosis. Which am​ong the following stateme‍nts is true regarding her​ condition? Visible peris​talsis from left to right​ is seen. The vomitus is ‍generally bilious. Hypoch​loremic hypokalemic metab‌olic alkalosis can be ass‍ociated. Cervix sign and ​antral nipple sign are se‌en in contrast studies. T‍he Ramstedt pyloromyotomy‌ is the standard surgical‌ procedure for its treatm‌ent.","raw_text":"<p>A child is diagnosed with congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Which among the following statements is true regarding her condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Visible peristalsis from left to right is seen.</li>\n<li>The vomitus is generally bilious.</li>\n<li>Hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis can be associated.</li>\n<li>Cervix sign and antral nipple sign are seen in contrast studies.</li>\n<li>The Ramstedt pyloromyotomy is the standard surgical procedure for its treatment.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3, ‌4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c81643cbfc947ebb28dc729268b8a3fx1280x1328.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (CHPS) most commonly presents with<strong> non-bilious projectile vomiting.</strong> It is common<strong> </strong>between 2-8 weeks of age.</p>\n<p>When the infant is given a test feed, <strong>visible peristalsis</strong> is usually observed moving from<strong> left to right</strong>, followed by projectile vomiting.</p>\n<p>Infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis develop a characteristic metabolic abnormality i.e <strong>hypokalemic</strong> <strong>hypochloremic</strong> <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>alkalosis</strong> with <strong>paradoxical</strong> <strong>aciduria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Persistent vomiting results in loss of hydrochloric acid leading to the development of <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>alkalosis</strong> (due to loss of H<sup>+</sup>) and <strong>hypochloremia</strong> (due to loss of Cl<sup>-</sup>). The kidneys try to compensate for alkalosis by secreting Na<sup>+</sup> and HCO3<sup>-</sup>. However, the loss of sodium stimulates the secretion of aldosterone via the RAAS pathway. The aldosterone causes active reabsorption of sodium in exchange for K<sup>+</sup> and H<sup>+</sup> ions. Loss of potassium ions leads to the development of <strong>hypokalemia</strong>. Loss of H<sup>+</sup> ions in urine lowers the urinary pH resulting in aciduria. It is called <strong>paradoxical</strong> <strong>aciduria</strong>, since the body initially lost H<sup>+</sup> ions due to loss of HCl, and instead of compensating that loss, the body is losing more H<sup>+</sup> ions through urine.</p>\n<p>The<strong> cervix sign</strong> and <strong>nipple sign</strong> are signs of CHPS<strong> </strong>seen on <strong>ultrasonography</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Double-layered hypertrophic mucosa protrudes into the stomach, creating the so-called <strong>nipple sign.</strong></li>\n<li>The sonographic appearance of the hypertrophied pylorus resembles that of the uterine cervix and is sometimes referred to as the <strong>cervix sign.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given ultrasound image shows thickened pylorus, with cervix sign and nipple sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The child should be rehydrated and the <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>abnormality</strong> has to be <strong>corrected</strong> <strong>preoperatively.</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>Ramstedt pyloromyotomy</strong> is the standard surgical procedure for its treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6965","text":"Which part of the GIT is m‌ost sensitive to ischemia​ following a hypovolemic ​insult?","raw_text":"Which part of the GIT is most sensitive to ischemia following a hypovolemic insult?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Griffit‌h point","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sudeck‌ point","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Termina‌l ileum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stomach</strong></p><p>Tonometry studies have shown that of all the parts of the gastrointestinal tract (GIT), the <strong>stomach </strong>is <strong>most sensitive to ischemia</strong> <strong>following a hypovolemic insult</strong> and is also the slowest to recover.</p>\n<p>This may explain the high incidence of <strong>stress ulceration</strong> in the stomach.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Griffiths point </strong>(or <strong>Griffiths critical point</strong>) refers to the site of watershed anastomosis between the ascending left colic artery and the marginal artery of Drummond occurring in the region of the splenic flexure. Most anatomy texts describe its location as two-thirds along the transverse colon.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Sudeck point </strong>(or <strong>Sudeck critical point</strong>) refers to a specific location in the arterial supply of the rectosigmoid junction, namely the origin of the last sigmoid arterial branch from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6951","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of upper GI bleeding?​","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of upper GI bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophagea‌l varices","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peptic‌ ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric ‌erosions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NSAID induc‌ed gastritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Peptic ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a492d21e43e94295bc25ea030cd59805x1280x2905.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Peptic ulcer</strong></p><p> The most common cause of upper GI bleeding is <strong>peptic ulcer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The causes of upper GI bleed in decreasing order of frequency: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ulcers\n<ul>\n<li>Esophageal (6%)</li>\n<li>Gastric (21%)</li>\n<li>Duodenal (33%)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Erosions</li>\n<li>Mallory–Weiss tear</li>\n<li>Esophageal varices</li>\n<li>Tumour</li>\n<li>Vascular lesions</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4282","text":"A 35-year-old man came wit‌h complaints of dark-colo​red stools to the OPD. Af​ter undergoing endoscopy,‍ the patient was diagnose​d with an upper GI bleed.​ Which of the following s​tructures is used as a de‍marcation point to differ​entiate it from a lower G‌I bleed?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man came with complaints of dark-colored stools to the OPD. After undergoing endoscopy, the patient was diagnosed with an upper GI bleed. Which of the following structures is used as a demarcation point to differentiate it from a lower GI bleed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampulla ‌of Vater","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligament ‌of Treitz","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior duo‌denal flexure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ileocaeca‌l junction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ligament of Treitz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6df2624f514343f7b0598cef54335689x1280x1267.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ligament of Treitz</strong></p><p>The <strong>ligament of Treitz</strong> is used as the demarcation point to differentiate between upper gastrointestinal bleeding and lower gastrointestinal bleeding. It is a part of the duodenojejunal junction. </p>\n<p>Upper GI bleeding refers to bleeding proximal to the ligament of Treitz, and lower GI bleeding refers to bleeding distal to the ligament of Treitz.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4137","text":"A 45-year-old chronic alco‌holic, with a history of ​binge drinking and multip​le episodes of vomiting, ‍presents with hematemesis​. On endoscopy, a longitu​dinal mucosal tear is fou​nd in the gastroesophagea‍l junction. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old chronic alcoholic, with a history of binge drinking and multiple episodes of vomiting, presents with hematemesis. On endoscopy, a longitudinal mucosal tear is found in the gastroesophageal junction. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boerhaave‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mallory-W‌eiss tear","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric antral vascular ec‌tasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dieulafo‌y lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mallory-Weiss tear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/378902f95afd48589fcb29c274507abdx720x783.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a86a746b09341888df90ed23c43f0e5x512x385.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mallory-Weiss tear</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Mallory</strong> <strong>Weiss tear.</strong> It is due to a <strong>longitudinal tear in mucosa or submucosa</strong> of the gastroesophageal junction.</p>\n<p>It is most commonly seen in alcoholics, following forceful vomiting, retching, coughing, or straining. The <strong>left gastric artery</strong> is the most common vessel to bleed. The investigation of choice is <strong>endoscopy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management of Mallory Weiss tear</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Conservative, as it is usually is self-limiting.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endoscopic electrocoagulation or sclerosant therapy.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Angiographic embolization - if endoscopy fails.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">High gastrotomy - if all these maneuvers fail.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Boerhaave syndrome</strong> is characterized by spontaneous perforation of the esophagus. Here <strong>all layers are involved</strong>. Clinical features include <strong>Mackler's</strong> <strong>triad</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Retching</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chest pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Subcutaneous emphysema</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Gastric Antral Vascular Ectasia</strong> (GAVE) is characterized by dilated venules in the submucosa of the antrum.</p>\n<p>On endoscopy: <strong>Watermelon</strong> <strong>stomach</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Management of GAVE</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endoscopic argon photocoagulation (APC)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If persistent - antrectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dieulafoy lesion</strong> is a <strong>vascular malformation</strong> (dilated submucosal arterioles) that is seen along the lesser curvature of the stomach. The investigation of choice is <strong>endoscopy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Management of Dieulafoy lesion: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endoscopic electrocoagulation or sclerosant therapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Angiographic embolization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6953","text":"A 25-year-old male patient‌ presented to the OPD wit​h complaints of increasin​g heartburn and dyspepsia‍ for the past several mon​ths. He was prescribed PP​Is for one month but he h​as no relief. You perform‍ a urea breath test which​ is found to be positive.‌ What is the most likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old male patient presented to the OPD with complaints of increasing heartburn and dyspepsia for the past several months. He was prescribed PPIs for one month but he has no relief. You perform a urea breath test which is found to be positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type A g‌astritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type B g‌astritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reflux g‌astritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erosive ‌gastritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type B gastritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type B gastritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the <strong>positive urea breath test</strong> are suggestive of a <strong>Helicobacter pylori infection</strong> which is most commonly associated with <strong>Type B gastritis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type B</strong> gastritis usually affects the <strong>antrum</strong>. Intestinal metaplasia associated with chronic pan-gastritis and atrophy is seen. It has greater potential for <strong>malignancy</strong> as compared to Type A gastritis.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Type A </strong>gastritis affects the <strong>fundus</strong> with antral sparing. Its features can be easily remembered by 4 ‘A’s:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autoimmune</strong> condition - has circulating antibodies to parietal cells</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Atrophy</strong> of parietal cell mass</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Achlorhydria -</strong> causes chronic hypergastrinemia and microadenomas of enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Anemia -</strong> leads to pernicious anemia, due to malabsorption of Vit B12 (as the intrinsic factor is also produced by parietal cells)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6976","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses gastritis in patients​ with AIDS?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes gastritis in patients with AIDS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histopl‌asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cryptosp‌oridiosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isospor‌a belli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cryptosporidiosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cryptosporidiosis</strong></p><p>Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (<strong>AIDS</strong>)<strong> gastritis</strong> is secondary to infection with <strong>cryptosporidiosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other specific types of gastritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eosinophilic gastritis</strong> appears to have an <strong>allergic basis</strong> and is treated with steroids and cromoglycate.</li>\n<li><strong>Granulomatous gastritis</strong> is seen rarely in <strong>Crohn’s disease</strong> and also may be associated with tuberculosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Phlegmonous gastritis</strong> is a rare bacterial infection of the stomach, found in patients with severe intercurrent illnesses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6944","text":"What is the most common si‌te of gastric ulcer?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of gastric ulcer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser curvature near inci‌sura","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Greater ‌curvature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prepy‌loric","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lesser curvature near incisura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lesser curvature near incisura</strong></p><p>The most common site of gastric ulcer is the <strong>lesser curvature near incisura.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Approximately 60% of ulcers are in this location and are classified as <strong>type I gastric ulcers.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">These ulcers are generally not associated with excessive acid secretion and may occur with low to normal acid output.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6940","text":"Which type of gastric ulce‌r has increased gastric a​cid levels?","raw_text":"Which type of gastric ulcer has increased gastric acid levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e V","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type‌ III","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type III</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric ulcers of Types II</strong> (gastric body with duodenal ulcer) <strong>and III</strong> (prepyloric) are associated with <strong>increased gastric acid levels.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6971","text":"A biopsy was obtained from‌ a patient with chronic p​eptic ulcer disease. The ​pathological slide is giv‍en below. Identify the fa​lse statement regarding t​he causative agent of thi​s condition.","raw_text":"A biopsy was obtained from a patient with chronic peptic ulcer disease. The pathological slide is given below. Identify the false statement regarding the causative agent of this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes hypo‌gastrinemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Causes the most common typ‌e of gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with causation ‌of gastric MALToma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Considered by WHO as class‌ I carcinogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Causes hypogastrinemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef7f79a8dba44027bdb4da09c53d2a34.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Causes hypogastrinemia</strong></p><p>The given slide shows the GI mucosa, stained with <strong>Warthin-Starry silver stain</strong>, infected with <strong>Helicobacter pylori.</strong> H. pylori cause <strong>hypergastrinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ammonia released by H. pylori acts on the <strong>antral G-cells</strong> and releases gastrin. This is responsible for hypergastrinemia in these patients resulting in <strong>type B gastritis</strong> (the most common type of gastritis).</p>\n<p><strong>Urea breath test</strong> and antibodies to H. pylori help us with diagnosis. The <strong>gold standard</strong> investigation for H. pylori is a <strong>tissue biopsy.</strong></p>\n<p>H. pylori is classified by the WHO as a class I carcinogen because of the production of certain cytotoxins, notably the <strong>Cag A and Vac A</strong>, which are implicated in gastritis, peptic ulceration, and cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6961","text":"What is the most common co‌mplication that you expec​t in patients suffering f​rom peptic ulcer disease?‍","raw_text":"What is the most common complication that you expect in patients suffering from peptic ulcer disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blee‌ding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Obstr‌uction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perfo‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant tr‌ansformation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bleeding</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common complication</strong> of peptic ulcer disease is <strong>bleeding</strong>, followed by perforation and obstruction. </p>\n<p>The <strong>most common site for perforation</strong> of peptic ulcers is the first part of the duodenum.</p>\n<p>Complications of peptic ulcers are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleeding - It is the most common complication of <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>duodenal</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong>.</li>\n<li>Perforation - It is the most common complication of <strong>anterior</strong> <strong>duodenal</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong> and <strong>gastric</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong>.</li>\n<li>Obstruction</li>\n<li><strong>Hour-glass deformity</strong> of the stomach - Large long-standing gastric ulcers can lead to fibrosis. <strong>fibrosis</strong> if present can cause hourglass constriction of the stomach. <strong>Barium</strong> meal shows <strong>double</strong> <strong>pouched</strong> stomach.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tea-pot deformity</strong> (handbag stomach) - It is caused by cicatrization and shortening of the lesser curvature.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">An ulcer can erode <strong>posteriorly</strong> into the <strong>pancreas</strong> and <strong>anteriorly</strong> into the <strong>liver</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Malignancy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6939","text":"Which of the following is ‌used for the classificati​on of the endoscopic appe​arance of bleeding peptic‍ ulcers in a patient with​ peptic ulcer disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used for the classification of the endoscopic appearance of bleeding peptic ulcers in a patient with peptic ulcer disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forrest cla‌ssification","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blatchfo‌rd score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rockal‌l score","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bismuth cla‌ssification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Forrest classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Forrest classification</strong></p><p><strong>Forrest classification</strong> is used for classifying the <strong>endoscopic</strong> appearance of <strong>bleeding peptic ulcers</strong>. It stratifies the <strong>risk of rebleeding</strong> based on observed stigmata of recent hemorrhage. </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Stigmata of Recent Hemorrhage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Forrest Classification</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Active Spurting</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p>I A</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Active Oozing</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p>I B</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Non-bleeding vessel</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p>II A</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Adherent Clot</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p>II B</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Flat Pigmented Spot</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p>II C</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p><strong>Clean Based Ulcer</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"312\">\n<p>III</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Forrest classification is clinically very significant as it provides <strong>prognostic information</strong> to the surgeon about the need for endoscopic therapeutic intervention and the risk of rebleeding.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> The Glasgow–Blatchford bleeding score</strong> (GBS) is a screening tool to <strong>assess</strong> the likelihood that a patient with acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding (UGIB) will <strong>need medical intervention</strong> such as a blood transfusion or endoscopic intervention. It incorporates the patient’s blood urea level, hemoglobin level, blood pressure, and other clinical parameters.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Rockall score</strong> assesses the<strong> risk of rebleeding and in-hospital mortality</strong> in patients with an upper GI bleed.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> The</strong> <strong>Bismuth–Corlette classification</strong> system provides an anatomic description of the tumor location and longitudinal extension in the biliary tree.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6945","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most commonly involve​d vessel in patients with​ bleeding peptic ulcers o‍n the posterior wall?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly involved vessel in patients with bleeding peptic ulcers on the posterior wall?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left gast‌ric artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right gastroepiploic arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastroduod‌enal artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left gastroepiploic artery‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastroduodenal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d79e038c12b545d09156261a5decdcdcx600x629.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastroduodenal artery</strong></p><p>The most common vessel responsible for bleeding in peptic ulcer disease is the <strong>gastroduodenal artery</strong>. Ulcers located in the <strong>posterior wall</strong> can erode the mucosa and the gastroduodenal artery, leading to hematemesis and melena.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6947","text":"A 23-year-old woman was ad‌mitted to the burns ward ​with an involvement of 45​% of the body surface are‍a. She is at an increased​ risk of developing ulcer​s in which of the followi​ng parts of the stomach?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman was admitted to the burns ward with an involvement of 45% of the body surface area. She is at an increased risk of developing ulcers in which of the following parts of the stomach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bo‌dy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser c‌urvature","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greater ‌curvature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fundus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fundus</strong></p><p>A patient with burn is at risk of developing <strong>Curling’s ulcers</strong> (stress ulcers). <strong>Stress ulcers</strong> in the stomach are located most commonly in the <strong>fundus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Stress gastritis occurs after a <strong>physical trauma, shock, sepsis, hemorrhage, or respiratory failure. </strong>It is characterized by <strong>multiple superficial (non-ulcerating) erosions</strong> that begin in the proximal or acid-secreting portion of the stomach and progress distally. They are almost always seen in the <strong>fundus</strong> of the stomach and usually develop within 1 to 2 days after a traumatic event.</p>\n<p>The stress-induced splanchnic vasoconstriction causes systemic hypotension and reduced blood flow leading to mucosal injury.</p>\n<p>On microscopic examination, they appear as <strong>wedge-shaped mucosal hemorrhages,</strong> with coagulation necrosis of the superficial mucosal cells. Stress ulcers present as <strong>painless</strong> upper GI bleeds.</p>\n<p>Note: Cushing ulcers arise in individuals with intracranial disease. They occur in the stomach, duodenum, and esophagus and carry a high incidence of perforation. They occur due to gastric acid hypersecretion from direct vagal nerve stimulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6946","text":"A 42-year-old man is broug‌ht to the emergency room ​with complaints of severe​ abdominal pain. On exami‍nation, his vitals are st​able and there is abdomin​al rigidity and tendernes​s. The erect X-ray of the‍ abdomen is suggestive of​ pneumoperitoneum and the‌ patient is posted for em‍ergency laparotomy. It re​veals the presence of a p‌erforated type I gastric ‍ulcer. How will you manag‌e this patient?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man is brought to the emergency room with complaints of severe abdominal pain. On examination, his vitals are stable and there is abdominal rigidity and tenderness. The erect X-ray of the abdomen is suggestive of pneumoperitoneum and the patient is posted for emergency laparotomy. It reveals the presence of a perforated type I gastric ulcer. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform distal gastrectomy‌ with Billroth I anastomo​sis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Closure of the perforation‌ with Graham's patch repa​ir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perform truncal vagotomy a‌nd antrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform truncal vagotomy w‌ith gastrojejunostomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perform distal gastrectomy with Billroth I anastomosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perform distal gastrectomy with Billroth I anastomosis</strong></p><p>In <strong>stable</strong> <strong>patients</strong> without operative risks, Perforated type I gastric ulcers are best managed by <strong>distal gastrectomy with Billroth I anastomosis</strong> (with or without vagotomy).       </p>\n<p>If the patient is hemodynamically <strong>unstable</strong> or <strong>unfit</strong> for surgery, <strong>simple</strong> <strong>patch</strong> <strong>repair</strong> <strong>with</strong> <strong>biopsy</strong> preferred.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      </p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px;\" width=\"483\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 14.0625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 14.0625px;\">Type of Gastric Ulcer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 14.0625px;\">Management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 61px;\">Type I </td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 61px;\">Distal gastrectomy </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 61px;\"> Type II and III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 61px;\">Distal gastrectomy + Vagotomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 61px;\"> Type IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.5px; height: 61px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p> Csendes procedure</p>\n<p> or</p>\n<p> Pauchet procedure</p>\n<p> or</p>\n<p> Kelling - Madlener procedure</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6984","text":"Kelling-Madlener procedure‌ is done for _____","raw_text":"Kelling-Madlener procedure is done for _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type lll ga‌stric ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type lV ga‌stric ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type l gas‌tric ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type ll ga‌stric ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type lV gastric ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9909cf0bce442499d3fc6d6f61fee51x1280x2825.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type lV gastric ulcer</strong></p><p>The <strong>Kelling-Madlener</strong> procedure is done for <strong>type lV Gastric ulcers.</strong></p>\n<p>Surgical management of type lV gastric ulcer</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Kelling-Madlener procedure</strong> - involves antrectomy and excision of gastric ulcer.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pauchet’s procedure</strong> - involves distal gastrectomy with a selective sleeve-like extension to the lesser curvature to remove the ulcer.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Csendes Procedure</strong>- subtotal gastrectomy with sleeve-like extension to the lesser curvature.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>Management of gastric ulcer</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Types of gastric ulcer</td>\n<td>Management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type l</td>\n<td>Distal gastrectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type ll &amp; lll</td>\n<td>Distal gastrectomy + Vagotomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type lV</td>\n<td>\n<p>Pauchet’s procedure <br/>Or<br/>Kelling-Madlener’s procedure <br/>Or          <br/>Csendes procedure</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4141","text":"A 25-year-old man on SSRI ‌presented with hematemesi​s and abdominal pain. An ​endoscopy was performed i‍mmediately which showed a​n actively bleeding ulcer​ of size 2cm in the poste​rior wall of the duodenum‍. What is the next best s​tep in the management of ‌this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man on SSRI presented with hematemesis and abdominal pain. An endoscopy was performed immediately which showed an actively bleeding ulcer of size 2cm in the posterior wall of the duodenum. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electroco‌agulation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Graham’s p‌atch repair","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duoden‌ostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PPI + endoscopic monitorin‌g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Electrocoagulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Electrocoagulation</strong></p><p>The next best step to manage the above scenario is <strong>endoscopic electrocoagulation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Management of bleeding peptic ulcer</strong></p>\n<p>For actively bleeding ulcers or non-bleeding visible vessels,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endoscopic therapy </strong>with a<strong> combination of epinephrine injection and electrocoagulation </strong>is done<strong>. </strong>Surgery is done only when 2 attempts of endoscopic therapy fail.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical</strong> management includes <strong>duodenostomy</strong> and under-running of the vessel. <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Duodenal ulcers also show a good response to </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">acid-reducing surgery</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> or vagotomy.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p> Medical management includes PPIs and H.pylori eradication therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6954","text":"Which of the following is ‌the preferred approach fo​r treating a patient who ​has a duodenal ulcer with‍ a perforation of 2.5cm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the preferred approach for treating a patient who has a duodenal ulcer with a perforation of 2.5cm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary closure and buttre‌ss with well-vascularised​ omentum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Graham pa‌tch repair","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Closure of defect and gast‌rojejunostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Managed con‌servatively","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Graham patch repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Graham patch repair</strong></p><p>The preferred treatment of perforations between 1–3 cm in size is a <strong>Graham patch repair.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of<strong> perforated duodenal ulcer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;1 cm: Primary closure + buttress with well-vascularised omentum</li>\n<li>1–3 cm: Graham patch repair</li>\n<li>&gt;3 cm: Closure of defect using omentum or jejunal serosa + pyloric exclusion + gastrojejunostomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4212","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the type of v​agotomy shown in the imag​e below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the type of vagotomy shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcer recurrence rate is h‌ighest","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acid reducti‌on is maximum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve of Latarjet is spare‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Postoperative complication‌s are minimum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acid reduction is maximum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c898d7b23364d779b4f80fee2dbc354.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29d0ec5ab8fc4871bcba4d71ccb78005x1280x1723.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acid reduction is maximum</strong></p><p>The picture depicts <strong>Highly Selective Vagotomy (HSV). </strong>Post-procedure<strong> acid reduction is minimum in HSV.</strong></p>\n<p>It involves resection of <strong>crowfoot branches</strong>, 6cm from the gastroesophageal junction but stopped 7cm from the pylorus. The<strong> nerve of Latarjet</strong> from the anterior and posterior vagal trunk supplies the acid pepsin areas of the stomach. It is<strong> spared</strong> in HSV.<strong> </strong>Usually, the motor branch to the pylorus is also preserved.</p>\n<p><strong>There are 3 types of vagotomy</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Highly selective vagotomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Truncal vagotomy + gastrojejunostomy </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Truncal vagotomy + antrectomy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Highly selective vagotomy</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acid reduction is minimum.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Highest ulcer recurrence rate.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Minimum postoperative complications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Truncal vagotomy + antrectomy</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acid reduction is maximum.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Least ulcer recurrence rate.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maximum postoperative complications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Vagal nerves are motor to the stomach and denervation of the antro-pyloro-duodenal segment results in <strong>gastric stasis</strong> after truncal vagotomy. Hence, a drainage procedure, most commonly <strong>Heineke–Mikulicz pyloroplasty</strong> or gastrojejunostomy is done along with vagotomy.</p>\n<p><strong>The criminal nerve of Grassi</strong> is a branch of the posterior vagal trunk that arises at the gastroesophageal junction and supplies the fundus of the stomach. It is called a criminal nerve<strong> </strong>because it is often <strong>missed during vagotomy</strong> procedures and can result in <strong>recurrent peptic ulcer </strong>disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6982","text":"Identify the surgical proc‌edure shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the surgical procedure shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Billroth I ‌gastrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Billroth II‌ gastrectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Braun’s modification of Bi‌llroth II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Roux-en Y gastrojejunostom‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Billroth II gastrectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1869101514a04bbd89e6890cd1479c72.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1143f3067e434895aaf820bafe96209bx1280x5287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Billroth II gastrectomy</strong></p><p>The procedure illustrated in the image is <strong>Billroth II gastrectomy.</strong> Following resection, the distal end of the stomach is narrowed by the closure of the lesser curve aspect of the remnant. The greater curve aspect is then anastomosed, usually in a retro-colic fashion, to the jejunum.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>Billroth I gastrectomy</strong>, the lower half of the stomach is removed, and the cut end of the stomach is anastomosed to the first part of the duodenum.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Braun modification of classical Billroth II gastrectomy</strong> is Billroth II reconstruction with jejunojejunostomy.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Roux-en Y gastrojejunostomy</strong> is a gastric bypass surgery where a loop of jejunum is anastomosed to a proximal gastric pouch. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6807","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ to the ER with severe ab​dominal pain. Guarding is​ present on per abdomen e‍xamination. HR is 110bpm ​and BP is 88/60mmHg. Erec​t abdominal X-ray is show​n below. Which of the fol‍lowing would you proceed ​with, for managing this c‌ase? USG abdomen IV fluid‍s Exploratory laparotomy ​Elective surgery Conserva‌tive management","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old man presents to the ER with severe abdominal pain. Guarding is present on per abdomen examination. HR is 110bpm and BP is 88/60mmHg. Erect abdominal X-ray is shown below. Which of the following would you proceed with, for managing this case?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>USG abdomen</li>\n<li>IV fluids</li>\n<li>Exploratory laparotomy</li>\n<li>Elective surgery</li>\n<li>Conservative management</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,‌4,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,‌2,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2,3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66a399549d934c2db9263a61dd812fb5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2,3</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and erect X-ray abdomen showing <strong>gas under the diaphragm (pneumoperitoneum) </strong>is suggestive of gastrointestinal <strong>perforation. </strong>It is managed by initial resuscitation with IV fluids followed by exploratory laparotomy. It is a surgical emergency.</p>\n<p>Management includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adequate preoperative resuscitation with <strong>intravenous fluids</strong> followed by correction of electrolyte imbalance, instituting broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics and</li>\n<li>Early surgical <strong>intervention/laparotomy</strong> to address the cause for pneumoperitoneum and stop further contamination of the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4135","text":"A 54-year-old man presente‌d with acute onset of abd​ominal pain, fever, and v​omiting. Examination reve‍aled tenderness over the ​right iliac fossa. USG sh​owed gas around the right​ kidney. What is the most‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old man presented with acute onset of abdominal pain, fever, and vomiting. Examination revealed tenderness over the right iliac fossa. USG showed gas around the right kidney. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Append‌icitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perforated d‌uodenal ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cholec‌ystitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancre‌atitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perforated duodenal ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perforated duodenal ulcer</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pneumoretroperitoneum</strong> caused by a <strong>perforated</strong> <strong>duodenal</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Posterior</strong> <strong>duodenal</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong> perforates into the retroperitoneum, and the contents leak through the <strong>right paracolic gutter</strong> into the right iliac fossa, <strong>mimicking</strong> <strong>appendicitis</strong>. This is called <strong>Valentino</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>On USG, gas can be seen around the right kidney known as the <strong>renal veil sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6949","text":"A 30-year-old actress who ‌underwent Roux-en-Y gastr​ic bypass surgery for obe​sity, presents with compl‍aints of sweating, palpit​ations, epigastric fullne​ss, and loose stools whic​h start immediately after‍ meals. Which of the foll​owing would you not assoc‌iate with her condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old actress who underwent Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery for obesity, presents with complaints of sweating, palpitations, epigastric fullness, and loose stools which start immediately after meals. Which of the following would you not associate with her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is precipitated by carb‌ohydrate rich food.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is more common after pa‌rtial gastrectomy with th​e Billroth II reconstruct​ion.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It results in hypoglycemia‌.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is relieved by lying do‌wn.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It results in hypoglycemia.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It results in hypoglycemia.</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>early dumping syndrome</strong>. Hypoglycemia​ does not occur in early dumping syndrome. <strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> is seen in <strong>late dumping syndrome</strong> secondary to raised insulin levels.</p>\n<p>Dumping syndrome is a complication occurring post gastrectomy. It can be early or late.</p>\n<p><strong>Early dumping syndrome</strong> is due to the <strong>high osmotic load</strong> that reaches the small bowel, leading to sequestration of fluid from the circulation into the gastrointestinal tract (GIT). This fluid shift induces <strong>autonomic responses</strong>. It is characterized by more GI and fewer cardiovascular effects such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Epigastric fullness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sweating</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lightheadedness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tachycardia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diarrhea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Symptoms are <strong>aggravated by having more food</strong> and relieved by lying down.</p>\n<p><strong>Late dumping syndrome</strong> is due to an <strong>increase in insulin levels</strong> and secondary hypoglycemia. This hypoglycemia causes a catecholamine surge, which results in predominantly cardiovascular effects such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diaphoresis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tremulousness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Light-headedness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tachycardia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Confusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The symptoms are aggravated by exercise and <strong>relieved by having food</strong>.</p>\n<p>The principal treatment for both types of dumping syndrome is <strong>dietary manipulation</strong>. Meals based on fat and protein are known to work the best. <strong>Octreotide</strong> before meals is useful. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6959","text":"A patient presented to the‌ casualty with severe upp​er abdominal pain and rec​urrent retching with mini‍mal vomiting. A nasogastr​ic tube insertion was tri​ed but it was unsuccessfu​l. A chest X-ray showed d‍iffuse distension of bowe​l loops and a left-sided ‌diaphragmatic defect. Whi‍ch of the following state​ments regarding this cond‌ition is false?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the casualty with severe upper abdominal pain and recurrent retching with minimal vomiting. A nasogastric tube insertion was tried but it was unsuccessful. A chest X-ray showed diffuse distension of bowel loops and a left-sided diaphragmatic defect. Which of the following statements regarding this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesenteroaxial type is mor‌e common","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Organoaxial type is associ‌ated with a diaphragmatic​ defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgery is performed throu‌gh a transabdominal appro​ach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borchardt t‌riad is seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mesenteroaxial type is more common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/296772ab24de4fa28f3680b3a90730a3x339x480.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mesenteroaxial type is more common</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>gastric volvulus</strong>. Organoaxial gastric volvulus is more common (66%) than mesenteroaxial volvulus (33%). </p>\n<p><strong>Organoaxial gastric volvulus:</strong> Torsion occurs along the <strong>longitudinal</strong> axis of the stomach and often occurs acutely. It is associated with a diaphragmatic defect.</p>\n<p><strong>Mesenteroaxial gastric volvulus:</strong> Torsion occurs along the <strong>vertical</strong> axis. It is partial (&lt;180 degrees) and recurrent. It is not associated with a diaphragmatic defect.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>Borchardt triad</strong> is seen in gastric volvulus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sudden-onset severe upper abdominal pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Recurrent retching with production of little vomitus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inability to pass a nasogastric (NG) tube</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management involves <strong>reduction and uncoiling of the stomach</strong> through a transabdominal approach.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6972","text":"A 40-year-old male present‌ed to the OPD with compla​ints of abdominal pain, d​iarrhea, loss of appetite‍, and loss of weight. On ​examination, the patient ​had peripheral edema and ​ascites. Laboratory studi‍es showed decreased serum​ albumin but normal serum‌ levels of transaminases ‍and gastrin. Upper GI end​oscopy showed the followi‌ng appearance of the stom‍ach. Which of the followi‌ng is true about the cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male presented to the OPD with complaints of abdominal pain, diarrhea, loss of appetite, and loss of weight. On examination, the patient had peripheral edema and ascites. Laboratory studies showed decreased serum albumin but normal serum levels of transaminases and gastrin. Upper GI endoscopy showed the following appearance of the stomach. Which of the following is true about the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No malignan‌t potential","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastrectomy is the treatme‌nt of choice in severe ca​ses","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caused by overexpression o‌f TNF alpha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Characterized by hyperchlo‌rhydria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gastrectomy is the treatment of choice in severe cases","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21bc495a5403445fb1d1e25d0f46c19a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39baee5e43484a74af07c6d6ed580a1fx800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gastrectomy is the treatment of choice in severe cases</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing the presence of <strong>hypertrophied gastric mucosal folds</strong> are characteristic of <strong>Menetrier’s disease</strong>. <strong>Gastrectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice in <strong>severe cases</strong> of Menetrier's disease.</p>\n<p>Menetrier's disease is a <strong>premalignant</strong> condition. The disease seems to be caused by overexpression of transforming growth factor-alpha i.e., <strong>TGF-alpha</strong> (not TNF-alpha), which stimulates the EGFR pathway, resulting in increased proliferation of mucus cells/foveolar hyperplasia. This results in gross <strong>hypertrophy</strong> of the gastric mucosal folds, mucus production, and <strong>hypochlorhydria</strong>. The antrum is usually spared.</p>\n<p>There is a loss of albumin (up to 10 g/day), across the gastric mucosa leading to <strong>hypoproteinemia</strong>. Secondary symptoms include weight loss, diarrhea, and peripheral edema.</p>\n<p>Histologically, it is characterized by <strong>hyperplasia of foveolar mucous cells.</strong> The glands are cystically dilated and elongated with a <strong>corkscrew-like appearance</strong> as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Cetuximab</strong>, an EGFR inhibitory antibody, is now considered the <strong>first-line treatment</strong> for Menetrier's disease, leaving <strong>total gastrectomy</strong> for <strong>severe disease</strong> with persistent and substantial protein loss refractory to cetuximab.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-jWBrPkK","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"19_local_anesthetics___specific_drugs","title":"19.Local Anesthetics   Specific Drugs","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5998","text":"Before performing a bronch‌oscopy, the pulmonologist​ sprays a topical anesthe‍tic solution in the patie‌nt's oral cavity and phar‍ynx. Which of the followi​ng drugs is this spray un‌likely to contain?","raw_text":"Before performing a bronchoscopy, the pulmonologist sprays a topical anesthetic solution in the patient's oral cavity and pharynx. Which of the following drugs is this spray unlikely to contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetra‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benzo‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bupivacaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bupivacaine</strong></p><p>The spray used by the pulmonologist is most likely a <strong>topical anesthetic. Bupivacaine </strong>is <strong>not</strong> used for topical anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Lidocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine, </strong>and<strong> dibucaine </strong>are used for <strong>topical</strong> anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Lidocaine and tetracaine topical sprays: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endotracheal intubation - blunt the cardiovascular responses</li>\n<li>Esophagoscopy, bronchoscopy - mucosal analgesia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lidocaine patch: postherpetic neuralgia.</p>\n<p><strong>Benzocaine </strong>and cetacaine (a topical application spray containing benzocaine, tetracaine, and butamben) had been used but a greater incidence of <strong>methemoglobinemia </strong>due to benzocaine has discouraged their use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5999","text":"The anesthesia PG applies ‌a cream with an occlusive​ dressing on the forearm ‍of a baby brought for IV ‌cannulation. 1 hour later‍, the baby allows him to ​place the cannula without‌ crying. What is the comp​osition of the cream that​ was applied?","raw_text":"The anesthesia PG applies a cream with an occlusive dressing on the forearm of a baby brought for IV cannulation.  1 hour later, the baby allows him to place the cannula without crying. What is the composition of the cream that was applied?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 % lidocaine and 2.5 % ‌procaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.5% lidocaine and 2.5 % p‌rilocaine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5% lidocaine and 2.5% proc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5% lidocaine and 2.5 % pri‌locaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2.5% lidocaine and 2.5 % prilocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec85abed33284ced8d0657dd623b83eax512x113.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2.5% lidocaine and 2.5 % prilocaine</strong></p><p>The substance applied by the anesthesia resident is <strong>EMLA cream</strong> (Eutectic Mixture of Local Anesthetic) which consists of a 1:1 mixture of <strong>2.5% lidocaine</strong> and <strong>2.5% prilocaine</strong>.</p>\n<p>A eutectic mixture has a melting point lesser than the individual drugs and hence the mixture is in the <strong>oil form</strong> at <strong>room temperature.</strong></p>\n<p>EMLA can provide adequate <strong>dermal analgesia</strong> for procedures like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Split skin graft harvesting and laser skin treatments</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Venipuncture and intravenous cannulation in children</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lithotripsy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Circumcision</li>\n</ul>\n<p>EMLA is applied 45-60 minutes before the procedure with an occlusive dressing as shown in the image. It provides analgesia up to a <strong>depth</strong> of <strong>5mm</strong> which <strong>lasts</strong> for <strong>1-2 hours.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6031","text":"A patient presents with a ‌foreign body in the right​ cornea. Which would be t‍he topical anesthetic of ‌choice to remove it?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a foreign body in the right cornea. Which would be the topical anesthetic of choice to remove it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetra‌caine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetracaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetracaine</strong></p><p><strong>Tetracaine</strong> is the topical anesthesia of choice for the <strong>removal</strong> of <strong>foreign bodies</strong> from the cornea.</p>\n<p>Topical anesthetics used in ophthalmology include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tetracaine</li>\n<li>Proparacaine eye drops</li>\n<li>Lidocaine gel</li>\n<li>Intranasal cocaine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6005","text":"The drug most frequently u‌sed for intravenous regio​nal anesthesia is _______‍____.","raw_text":"The drug most frequently used for intravenous regional anesthesia is ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mepiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf69be3a5f02414182d3a0bbe2467ebdx753x515.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lidocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Lidocaine</strong> has been the drug used most frequently for <strong>intravenous regional anesthesia</strong> (IVRA).</p>\n<p>Other drugs used are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prilocaine</li>\n<li>Mepivacaine</li>\n<li>Chloroprocaine</li>\n<li>Procaine</li>\n<li>Etidocaine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IVRA or <strong>Bier’s block</strong> involves the intravenous administration of a <strong>local anesthetic</strong> into a <strong>tourniquet-occluded limb.</strong></p>\n<p>It is used to provide anesthesia for short surgical procedures (&lt;90 minutes) on the limbs.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Bier's block/ IVRA. </p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1218\"><strong>Note: </strong>Bupivacaine is not used for Biers block. It is the most cardiotoxic local anesthetic. Accidental early deflation of the tourniquet can lead to massive systemic doses of bupivacaine resulting in cardiac arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6006","text":"A patient presents with th‌e following finding, and ​excision is planned under‍ Bier’s block. Which of t‌he following drugs will y‍ou not use for this proce​dure?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following finding, and excision is planned under Bier’s block. Which of the following drugs will you not use for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bupivacaine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/884262b7ce37465dbf1f8e9dd6fedb37.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e83def7674094302913907a2863d39f4x753x515.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bupivacaine</strong></p><p>The image shows a ganglion cyst planned for excision under Bier's block. <strong>Bupivacaine</strong> is no longer used for Biers block. </p>\n<p>Bupivacaine is the <strong>most cardiotoxic</strong> local anaesthetic (LA). Accidental early deflation of the tourniquet can lead to <strong>massive systemic doses</strong> of bupivacaine resulting in cardiac arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse.</p>\n<p><strong>IVRA </strong>or<strong> Bier’s block </strong>involves the intravenous administration of LA into a tourniquet-occluded limb for short surgical procedures (&lt;45-60 minutes) on the limbs. Drugs used are <strong>lidocaine </strong>(most common), <strong>prilocaine</strong>, mepivacaine, chloroprocaine, procaine, and etidocaine. </p>\n<p>The image below shows Bier's block of the upper limb. A double pneumatic tourniquet is inflated to<strong> 50-100mmHg above systolic BP</strong> after exsanguination of the limb with Esmarch bandage. 30-40ml of <strong>0.5% Lidocaine</strong> is injected which provides anesthesia for 30-45 minutes. Even for a very short duration procedure, the tourniquet must be left inflated for at least 15–20 min to avoid a rapid systemic absorption of LA resulting in toxicity. Slow deflation is recommended to avoid LA toxicity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0445","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about lignocaine ​is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about lignocaine is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It blocks active sodium ch‌annels with more affinity​ than resting sodium chan‍nels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause cardiotoxicit‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be given orally for‌ cardiac arrhythmias","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It's duration of action is‌ increased by adrenaline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It can be given orally for cardiac arrhythmias","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It can be given orally for cardiac arrhythmias</strong></p><p><strong>Lignocaine</strong> is used for cardiac arrhythmias but is <strong>not effective orally</strong> because of<strong> high first-pass metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Local anaesthetics have <strong>more affinity</strong> for<strong> active sodium channels</strong> over resting channels.</p>\n<p>Option B: Lignocaine use can result in <strong>cardiac toxicity</strong> especially at <strong>higher doses</strong> where it interferes with impulse conduction.</p>\n<p>Option D: Vasoconstrictor like <strong>adrenaline increases </strong>the <strong>duration of action</strong> of lignocaine when used for infiltration anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6011","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida‌ in labor requests for ep​idural analgesia. What is‍ the maximum dose of lido‌caine that can be given t‍o her when epinephrine is​ not used?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida in labor requests for epidural analgesia. What is the maximum dose of lidocaine that can be given to her when epinephrine is not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 mg‌/ kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4.5 ‌mg/kg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7mg‌/kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12m‌g/kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4.5 mg/kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4.5 mg/kg</strong></p><p> The maximum dose of lidocaine without epinephrine is<strong> 4.5 mg/kg.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6015","text":"For epidural anesthesia, w‌hat is the maximum dose o​f lidocaine with adrenali‍ne that can be given to a‌ 70kg patient?","raw_text":"For epidural anesthesia, what is the maximum dose of lidocaine with adrenaline that can be given to a 70kg patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"175‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"200‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"350‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"500‌ mg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 500 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 500 mg</strong></p><p>The maximum dose of lidocaine with adrenaline is <strong>7mg/kg</strong> up to <strong>500mg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6007","text":"What is the concentration ‌of bupivacaine you use wh​ile administering spinal ‍anesthesia to a patient?","raw_text":"What is the concentration of bupivacaine you use while administering spinal anesthesia to a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌2%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌5%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0.‌75%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5‌%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.5%</strong></p><p><strong>0.5% h</strong><strong>yperbaric</strong> <strong>preservative-free</strong> <strong>bupivacaine</strong> is most commonly used for spinal anesthesia.</p>\n<p>1 ml of 0.5% Bupivacaine = 5 mg of Bupivacaine.</p>\n<p>It is made <strong>hyperbaric</strong> (density heavier than CSF) by adding <strong>8.25% dextrose</strong> to the local anesthetic solution.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 193px; width: 561px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 529px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Dosages and action of spinal anesthesia drugs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\"><strong>Concentration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Volume</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Total Dose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\"><strong>Bupivacaine (levobupivacaine, Ropivacaine)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\">0.5% (rarely 0.75%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">3-4 ml</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">15-20 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">90-200 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\"><strong>Lidocaine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\">2% plain, 5% in 7.5% glucose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">1-2 ml</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">30-100 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">30-90 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\"><strong>Tetracaine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\">1%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">1-2 ml</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">5-20 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">90-200 min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Addition of vasoconstrictors (epinephrine and phenylephrine) and opioids improve the quality and the duration of local anesthetic action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6008","text":"A patient with a tibial sh‌aft fracture is planned f​or tibia nailing under ep‍idural anesthesia. What c‌oncentration of bupivacai‍ne should be used for thi​s purpose?","raw_text":"A patient with a tibial shaft fracture is planned for tibia nailing under epidural anesthesia. What concentration of bupivacaine should be used for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌1%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.‌5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.5%</strong></p><p>The concentration of <strong>bupivacaine</strong> used in <strong>epidural</strong> anesthesia for <strong>surgical purposes</strong> is <strong>0.5%</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 977\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The dosage of local anesthetic in epidural anesthesia depends on the <strong>desired clinical effect.</strong> It can be used as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary anesthetic for surgical anesthesia (sensory + motor block)</li>\n<li>Combined with general anesthesia to supplement analgesia (sensory block)</li>\n<li>Postoperative analgesia or labor analgesia (sensory block)</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 152px;\" width=\"550\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 540px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Various concentrations of Bupivacaine and its uses</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.078px;\"><strong>Concentration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\"><strong>Sensory block</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\"><strong>Motor block</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.328px;\"><strong>Clinical use</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.078px;\"><strong>0.0625- 0.1%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\">Analgesic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.328px;\">Labor analgesia (walking epidural)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.078px;\"><strong>0.1-0.25%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\">Analgesic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\">Minimal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.328px;\">Postoperative analgesia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.078px;\"><strong>0.5-0.75%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\">Dense</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.297px;\">Moderate to dense</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.328px;\">Surgical anesthesia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6027","text":"A patient with a history o‌f PSVT and type I diabete​s has been posted for a f‍emoral nailing procedure.‌ The senior resident asks‍ you to load ropivacaine ​instead of the standard b‌upivacaine for the spinal​ block. What advantage of​ the preferred drug?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of PSVT and type I diabetes has been posted for a femoral nailing procedure. The senior resident asks you to load ropivacaine instead of the standard bupivacaine for the spinal block. What advantage of the preferred drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More ‌potent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Less nep‌hrotoxic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less car‌diotoxic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"More profound motor blocka‌de","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Less cardiotoxic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Less cardiotoxic</strong></p><p><strong>Ropivacaine</strong> is <strong>less cardiotoxic </strong>than bupivacaine. </p>\n<p>Ropivacaine is <strong>S(-) enantiomer</strong> of bupivacaine. The <strong>R(+)</strong> isomer of <strong>bupivacaine</strong> is responsible for <strong>cardiotoxicity.</strong> <strong>Ropivacaine</strong> is less cardiotoxic and has a <strong>higher safety profile</strong> than bupivacaine.</p>\n<p>Ropivacaine has a shorter duration of action and produces <strong>less motor block</strong> than bupivacaine (i.e. more motor sparing than bupivacaine). Ropivacaine is <strong>less potent</strong> than bupivacaine. Epidural ropivacaine is 40 % less potent than bupivacaine.</p>\n<p>Ropivacaine can be used for infiltration anesthesia and central neuraxial blocks (both spinal and epidural).</p>\n<p>Note: Levobupivacaine is another S(-) enantiomer of bupivacaine, with less cardiotoxicity. Ropivacaine differs from levobupivacaine in the substitution of a propyl for the butyl group on the piperidine ring.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6023","text":"What is the ratio of the d‌ose required to produce c​ardiovascular collapse to‍ that required to induce ‌seizures (CC/CNS dose rat‍io) for Bupivacaine?","raw_text":"What is the ratio of the dose required to produce cardiovascular collapse to that required to induce seizures (CC/CNS dose ratio) for Bupivacaine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The <strong>CC/CNS ratio</strong> for <strong>bupivacaine</strong> is approximately<strong> 3</strong> whereas, for <strong>lidocaine,</strong> it is <strong>7</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cardiotoxicity of local anaesthetics can be compared using the CC/CNS dose ratio, that is the ratio of the dose causing cardiac collapse (CC) to the dose causing seizure/convulsions (CNS).</p>\n<p>The <strong>lower</strong> the <strong>number,</strong> the <strong>more cardiotoxic</strong> the drug. Therefore, progression from CNS signs and symptoms to cardiovascular collapse can occur more readily with bupivacaine than with lidocaine.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Patients under general anaesthesia will typically present with cardiotoxicity as the first sign of local anaesthetic toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6000","text":"A patient in ER is adminis‌tered a local anesthetic ​for pain relief. 10 minut‍es later, he turned blue ‌and the oximeter showed a‍n SpO2 of 82%. Methylene ​blue is administered for ‌treatment immediately. Wh​ich local anesthetic was ​most likely administered ‌to him?","raw_text":"A patient in ER is administered a local anesthetic for pain relief. 10 minutes later, he turned blue and the oximeter showed an SpO2 of 82%. Methylene blue is administered for treatment immediately. Which local anesthetic was most likely administered to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prilocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prilocaine</strong></p><p>The scenario describes <strong>methemoglobinemia, </strong>a side effect of <strong>prilocaine</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Prilocaine is metabolized to <strong>O-toluidine</strong>, which converts hemoglobin (Fe2+) to methemoglobin (Fe3+) producing methemoglobinemia. This side effect is dose-dependent. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> of methemoglobinemia includes <strong>reduction</strong> of <strong>methemoglobin</strong> to <strong>hemoglobin</strong> by intravenous <strong>methylene blue</strong> (1–2 mg/kg of a 1% solution over 5 min).</p>\n<p><strong>Note: Benzocaine</strong> used in topical local anesthetic sprays can also cause dangerous methemoglobinemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6002","text":"A patient undergoing a den‌tal extraction develops b​reathing difficulty, swel‍ling of lips, and a diffu‌se rash following local a‍nesthetic administration.​ Which of the following a‌gents is commonly implica​ted?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing a dental extraction develops breathing difficulty, swelling of lips, and a diffuse rash following local anesthetic administration. Which of the following agents is commonly implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Procaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Procaine</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario depicts the development of an<strong> allergic reaction</strong> to a local anesthetic agent. Allergic reactions are common with the use of <strong>procaine,</strong> an aminoester compound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1374","text":"A patient with HIV is on c‌otrimoxazole prophylaxis.​ He presents with a 4 cm ‍laceration that requires ‌suturing under local anes‍thesia. Which of the foll​owing local anesthetic ag‌ents would not be preferr​ed in this case?","raw_text":"A patient with HIV is on cotrimoxazole prophylaxis. He presents with a 4 cm laceration that requires suturing under local anesthesia. Which of the following local anesthetic agents would not be preferred in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Procaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Procaine</strong></p><p>The patient is on treatment with <strong>cotrimoxazole</strong>, a <strong>sulfonamide </strong>(sulfamethoxazole). The action of sulfonamides is inhibited by <strong>procaine</strong>,<strong> </strong>and so it would not be preferred in this patient<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Procaine is hydrolyzed in vivo to produce para-aminobenzoic (<strong>PABA</strong>) acid that inhibits the action of sulfonamides. All <strong>ester</strong> local anesthetics produce para-aminobenzoic acid. </p>\n<p>Prilocaine, lidocaine, and bupivacaine are amides and do not interact with sulphonamides.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0444","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a vasodilator?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a vasodilator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coc‌aine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorp‌rocaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cocaine</strong></p><p>All are vasodilators except cocaine. <strong>Cocaine</strong> is a <strong>vasoconstrictor </strong>due to<strong> </strong>its property of<strong> norepinephrine reuptake inhibition.</strong></p>\n<p>Other local anaesthetics do not have the property of norepinephrine reuptake inhibition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6025","text":"A middle-aged destitute is‌ brought by the police an​d ambulance services to t‍he ER. He is screaming ab‌out bugs crawling on his ‍body but no such bugs are​ noted. A white powder is‌ found in his pocket, fou​nd to be a drug. Which of​ the following statements‌ is not true about this d‍rug?","raw_text":"A middle-aged destitute is brought by the police and ambulance services to the ER. He is screaming about bugs crawling on his body but no such bugs are noted. A white powder is found in his pocket, found to be a drug. Which of the following statements is not true about this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Produce‌s miosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Strong vaso‌constrictor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Has abuse‌ potential","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolised mainly in live‌r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Produces miosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b71830cd7664c6f8814d4146665a0bfx388x509.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Produces miosis</strong></p><p>The symptoms and the white powdery substance suggest the drug being abused by the man is <strong>cocaine. </strong>It produces <strong>mydriasis</strong>, not miosis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>tactile hallucinations</strong> are a type of psychosis in cocaine addicts, known as <strong>Magnan syndrome.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 289\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cocaine is an intrinsic <strong>vasoconstrictor</strong> with local anesthetic properties. Hence it is an ideal agent for awake nasal intubation as it reduces pain and epistaxis. It is metabolized by liver cholinesterases and excreted in urine</p>\n<p>It has the potential for <strong>abuse</strong> as it produces euphoria by inhibiting the re-uptake of catecholamines (especially dopamine) in the CNS. Because of its abuse liability and high toxicity, cocaine use is limited to topical anesthesia of the upper respiratory tract.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5958","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is an aminoester local​ anesthetic?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is an aminoester local anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Procaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Procaine</strong></p><p><strong>Procaine</strong> is an <strong>aminoester.</strong></p>\n<p>Prilocaine, lidocaine, bupivacaine are amino amides.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5960","text":"All of the following local‌ anesthetics are metaboli​zed by plasma esterase ex‍cept ________.","raw_text":"All of the following local anesthetics are metabolized by plasma esterase except ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arti‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetra‌caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lidocaine</strong></p><p><strong>Lidocaine</strong> is an <strong>amino amide</strong> compound that is metabolized by <strong>microsomal P-450 enzymes</strong> in the<strong> liver</strong>.</p>\n<p>Procaine and tetracaine are amino esters that are metabolized by plasma esterases.</p>\n<p>Articaine is the only amino amide that is metabolized by the plasma esterases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5993","text":"The least potent local ane‌sthetic is__________","raw_text":"The least potent local anesthetic is__________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorop‌rocaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Procaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Procaine</strong></p><p>The <strong>least potent</strong> local anesthetic is <strong>procaine.</strong></p>\n<p>The potency of local anesthetics correlates with their hydrophobicity. <strong>Hydrophobic</strong> molecules are <strong>more potent</strong> and also <strong>longer acting.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5997","text":"The maximum systemic absor‌ption of a local anesthet​ic is seen with ________.‍","raw_text":"The maximum systemic absorption of a local anesthetic is seen with ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tracheal ‌injection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intercostal‌ nerve block","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brachial p‌lexus block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidura‌l block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intercostal nerve block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intercostal nerve block</strong></p><p><strong>Maximum absorption</strong> of local anesthetics takes place with <strong>intercostal nerve block. </strong>The degree and rate of <strong>absorption</strong> depend on the <strong>vascularity</strong> of the area the local anesthetic is exposed to.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6009","text":"The concentration of bupiv‌acaine used for intraveno​us regional anesthesia is‍ _____.","raw_text":"The concentration of bupivacaine used for intravenous regional anesthesia is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌1%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌25%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p>Bupivacaine is <strong>not used</strong> for intravenous regional anesthesia (<strong>IVRA</strong>) or Bier’s block.</p>\n<p>Bupivacaine is the most <strong>cardiotoxic</strong> of all local anesthetics. Accidental early deflation of the tourniquet in local anesthesia can lead to <strong>systemic toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Lidocaine is used for IVRA- 3mg/kg of <strong>0.5% preservative-free lidocaine without epinephrine</strong> (25 mL for a forearm, 50 mL for an arm, and 100 mL for a thigh tourniquet). </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6030","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding Ropivac​aine is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding Ropivacaine is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R (+) enantiomer of bupiva‌caine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Higher safety profile than‌ bupivacaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More motor sparing than bu‌pivacaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used in both spinal and ep‌idural anesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. R (+) enantiomer of bupivacaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. R (+) enantiomer of bupivacaine</strong></p><p>Ropivacaine is <strong>S(-) enantiomer</strong> of bupivacaine, not R(+).</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>R(+)</strong> isomer of <strong>bupivacaine</strong> is responsible for <strong>cardiotoxicity.</strong> <strong>Ropivacaine</strong> is less cardiotoxic and has a <strong>higher safety profile</strong> than bupivacaine.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ropivacaine has a shorter duration of action and produces <strong>less motor block</strong> than bupivacaine (i.e. more motor sparing than bupivacaine). Ropivacaine is <strong>less potent</strong> than bupivacaine. Epidural ropivacaine is 40 % less potent than bupivacaine.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ropivacaine can be used for infiltration anesthesia and central neuraxial blocks (both spinal and epidural).</p>\n<p>Note: Levobupivacaine is another S(-) enantiomer of bupivacaine, with less cardiotoxicity. Ropivacaine differs from levobupivacaine in the substitution of a propyl for the butyl group on the piperidine ring.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6001","text":"O-toluidine is a metabolic‌ by product of _______.","raw_text":"O-toluidine is a metabolic by product of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prilo‌caine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prilocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prilocaine</strong></p><p><strong>O-toluidine</strong> is a metabolic by-product of <strong>prilocaine. </strong>Metabolism of the aromatic ring of prilocaine to O-toluidine<strong> </strong>produces <strong>methemoglobinemia</strong> in a dose-dependent fashion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UrsR11UN","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"19_ventricular_system_and_subarachnoid_space","title":"19.Ventricular System And Subarachnoid Space","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MD0473","text":"The CT scan of a child wit‌h congenital toxoplasmosi​s is shown below. Which o​f the following cells for​m the lining of the invol‍ved structure?","raw_text":"The CT scan of a child with congenital toxoplasmosis is shown below. Which of the following cells form the lining of the involved structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ependy‌mocytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Astro‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligoden‌drocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Podo‌cytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ependymocytes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1997149db7634a2ea4f69985e439b876.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f52bfe784684f07afca424690b7219ax1280x1058.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ependymocytes</strong></p><p>The CT shows <strong>hydrocephalus,</strong> enlargement of the ventricles due to build-up of CSF. The <strong>ventricles</strong> of the brain are <strong>lined</strong> by <strong>ependymocytes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ependymocytes are a type of <strong>glial cell</strong> that line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord. They are <strong>simple columnar</strong> cells. Some are <strong>ciliated</strong> in order to facilitate the circulation of CSF.</p>\n<p>Their functions include <strong>secretion</strong> of <strong>CSF</strong> and <strong>transport</strong> of <strong>neurochemicals</strong> to and fro from the CSF. They also have a function in neuroregeneration.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1930","text":"What structure forms the r‌oof of the anterior horn ​of the marked ventricle?","raw_text":"What structure forms the roof of the anterior horn of the marked ventricle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genu of cor‌pus callosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rostrum of corpus callosum‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body of cor‌pus callosum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Septum p‌ellucidum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Body of corpus callosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec6c6cfe4f36401ea15605bad0aaa799.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1c0b75a1ded46559357bec039339353x600x678.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2154b7ca514147df92dd82b3f8c69906x600x649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Body of corpus callosum</strong></p><p>The structure marked is the <strong>lateral ventricle. </strong>The <strong>roof</strong> of the <strong>anterior horn</strong> of the lateral ventricle is formed by the anterior part of the<strong> body</strong> of the<strong> corpus callosum.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>lateral ventricle</strong> is divided into the <strong>body</strong> and<strong> 3 horns. </strong>The locations of the parts are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Body - parietal lobe</li>\n<li>Anterior horn - frontal lobe</li>\n<li>Posterior horn - occipital lobe</li>\n<li>Inferior horn - temporal lobe.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> anterior horn </strong>extends from the <strong>genu</strong> of the <strong>corpus callosum</strong> up to the <strong>interventricular foramen.</strong> The relations of the anterior horn are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anteriorly - Genu and rostrum of the corpus callosum</li>\n<li>Roof - Anterior part of the body of the corpus callosum </li>\n<li>Floor and lateral wall - Caudate nucleus</li>\n<li>Medial wall - Septum pellucidum and anterior column of fornix</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> body or central part </strong>extends from the <strong>interventricular foramen</strong> to the <strong>splenium</strong> of the <strong>corpus callosum. </strong>The relations of the body are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roof - Body of the corpus callosum</li>\n<li>Medial wall - Septum pellucidum </li>\n<li>Floor (from lateral to medial) - Caudate nucleus, stria terminalis, thalamostriate vein, thalamus, choroid plexus, and body of the fornix</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> posterior horn </strong>extends from the <strong>splenium</strong> to the <strong>occipital pole. </strong>The relations are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roof and lateral wall - Tapetum of corpus callosum</li>\n<li>Medial wall - Forceps major and calcar avis (calcarine sulcus)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> inferior horn </strong>is the <strong>largest compartment</strong> of the lateral ventricle and extends up to 2.5 cm from the temporal pole. The relations are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roof - Tapetum of corpus callosum and tail of caudate nucleus</li>\n<li>Floor (lateral to medial) - Collateral eminence produced by collateral fissure, hippocampus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: There is no lateral wall for the lateral ventricle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0935","text":"The two elevations on the ‌medial wall of the poster​ior horn of the lateral v​entricle are due to _____​__.","raw_text":"The two elevations on the medial wall of the posterior horn of the lateral ventricle are due to _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tapetum of the corpus call‌osum and calcar avis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forceps minor and collater‌al eminence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Forceps major and calcar a‌vis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Forceps major and collater‌al eminence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Forceps major and calcar avis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8edc908a7d654ce9bf90dac36a362f20x600x693.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Forceps major and calcar avis</strong></p><p>The two elevations on the medial wall of the posterior horn of the lateral ventricle are due to the <strong>forceps major</strong> and <strong>calcar avis. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> posterior horn </strong>extends from the <strong>splenium</strong> <strong>to</strong> the <strong>occipital pole. </strong>The relations are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roof and lateral wall - Tapetum of corpus callosum</li>\n<li>Medial wall - Forceps major and calcar avis (calcarine sulcus)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> forceps major</strong> is the <strong>fibers</strong> of the<strong> splenium</strong> of the corpus callosum.</p>\n<p>The <strong>calcar avis</strong> is formed by the <strong>white matter</strong> of the <strong>calcarine sulcus.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Forceps minor</strong> is formed by the anterior part of the <strong>corpus callosum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>collateral eminence</strong> is formed by the <strong>collateral sulcus </strong>and is related to the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1940","text":"A patient was undergoing e‌ndoscopic third ventricul​ostomy for treatment of h​ydrocephalus. During the ​procedure, the resident a‍ccidentally advanced the ‍instrument, perforating t‍hrough the floor of the t​hird ventricle. Which of ‌the following structures ‍is not likely to be injur​ed?","raw_text":"A patient was undergoing endoscopic third ventriculostomy for treatment of hydrocephalus. During the procedure, the resident accidentally advanced the instrument, perforating through the floor of the third ventricle. Which of the following structures is not likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infund‌ibulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mamilla‌ry body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optic ‌chiasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oculomotor nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oculomotor nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> oculomotor nerve</strong> is not related to the floor of the third ventricle.</p>\n<p>The structures related to the <strong>floor</strong> of the <strong>third ventricle </strong>(from anterior to posterior) include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Optic chiasma</li>\n<li>Infundibulum</li>\n<li>Mamillary bodies</li>\n<li>Posterior perforating substance</li>\n<li>Tegmentum of the midbrain.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1957","text":"The T2 weighted MRI of a c‌hild is given below. Whic​h of the following is tru​e about the ventricle pri​marily involved in this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"The T2 weighted MRI of a child is given below. Which of the following is true about the ventricle primarily involved in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foramen of Magendie is a c‌entral aperture that pier​ces the superior medullar​y velum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior medullary velum i‌s a sheet of white matter​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foramina of Luschka are th‌e lateral openings","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The choroid plexus is form‌ed from folds of arachnoi​d called tela choroidea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foramina of Luschka are the lateral openings","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b878a1752d74dbeb4e0612db5167574.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dd0fccba23342f586885cb620d1718bx600x629.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foramina of Luschka are the lateral openings</strong></p><p>The MRI shows a <strong>Dandy-Walker</strong> malformation. It is a rare congenital malformation involving the cerebellum and the <strong>fourth ventricle.</strong> The<strong> foramina</strong> of <strong>Luschka</strong> are the <strong>lateral openings</strong> in the fourth ventricle.<strong> </strong>These openings allow the flow of CSF from the ventricle into the subarachnoid space.</p>\n<p>The fourth ventricle is a tent-shaped ventricle lying <strong>between</strong> the <strong>brainstem</strong> and the <strong>cerebellum.</strong> It is widest at the level of the pontomedullary junction.</p>\n<p>The roof of the fourth ventricle is formed by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superior</strong> medullary velum -<strong> W</strong><strong>hite matter sheet</strong> stretching between the superior cerebellar peduncles.</li>\n<li><strong>Inferior</strong> medullary velum - Made of ventricular <strong>ependyma</strong> and <strong>pia mater</strong> of tela choroidea, and has no nervous tissue. It is pierced by the <strong>foramen</strong> of<strong> Magendie</strong> in the <strong>midline</strong>, opening into the <strong>cisterna magna.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The T-shaped <strong>choroid plexus</strong> of the fourth ventricle is formed from the <strong>tela choroidea,</strong> made of two layers of <strong>pia mater.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1956","text":"Which cranial nerve nucleu‌s is not found on the flo​or of the fourth ventricl​e?","raw_text":"Which cranial nerve nucleus is not found on the floor of the fourth ventricle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VI crani‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"VII cran‌ial nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"VIII cra‌nial erve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X crani‌al nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. VII cranial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8386f0b59cd6415da49a8be94ee3a485x1280x1644.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae3d44c7f8fc42c2860a655f523cc907x1279x1938.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. VII cranial nerve</strong></p><p>The VII cranial nerve nucleus is not present on the floor of the fourth ventricle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The floor of the fourth ventricle is diamond-shaped and lies on the dorsal surface of the pons and superior half of the medulla.</p>\n<p>It is divided into two symmetrical halves by the longitudinal<strong> median sulcus</strong>. Each half is further divided by sulcus limitans into a medial region, <strong>medial eminence,</strong> and a lateral region, <strong>vestibular area. </strong>The <strong>vestibular nuclei</strong> lie beneath the vestibular area.</p>\n<p>At the superior end of the sulcus limitans, a bluish-gray area is produced by a cluster of nerve cells containing melanin pigment called <strong>substantia ferruginea.</strong></p>\n<p>At the inferior end of the medial eminence, the fibers from the motor nucleus of the facial nerve looping over the <strong>abducens nucleus</strong> are represented by the <strong>facial colliculus. </strong></p>\n<p>Inferiorly, the medial eminence is represented by the <strong>hypoglossal triangle</strong> overlying the <strong>hypoglossal nucleus</strong> and the <strong>vagal triangle</strong> overlying the <strong>dorsal nucleus</strong> of the<strong> vagus</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1955","text":"Imaging done in a 66-year-‌old man with a suspected ​brainstem infarct showed ​a lesion at the facial co​lliculus. Which of the fo‍llowing structures are pr‍esent here?","raw_text":"Imaging done in a 66-year-old man with a suspected brainstem infarct showed a lesion at the facial colliculus. Which of the following structures are present here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial ner‌ve nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abducent ne‌rve nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibers from the facial ner‌ve which cover the facial​ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibers from the facial ner‌ve which cover the abduce​nt nucleus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fibers from the facial nerve which cover the abducent nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e8bace0674747879884cfb2aa9d049cx1280x1426.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fibers from the facial nerve which cover the abducent nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>facial nerve</strong> <strong>fibers</strong>, which cover the <strong>abducent nucleus</strong>, lie deep to the facial colliculus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Facial colliculus</strong> is a small bilateral bulge in the <strong>floor</strong> of the <strong>4th</strong> <strong>ventricle.</strong> Beneath this, the fibers from the motor nucleus of the facial nerve loop around the abducens nucleus. This is due to a phenomenon called <strong>neurobiotaxis - </strong>a shift of nerve cells during the development over the course of evolution.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1933","text":"Substantia ferruginea is p‌resent in ______.","raw_text":"Substantia ferruginea is present in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"White ‌matter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gray ‌matter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoth‌alamus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pituita‌ry gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gray matter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gray matter</strong></p><p><strong>Substantia ferruginea</strong> is present in the <strong>gray matter</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>cluster of nerve cells</strong> containing <strong>melanin</strong> pigment located at the superior end of sulcus limitans in the <strong>floor </strong>of the<strong> 4th ventricle</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0940","text":"Identify the area marked '‌a' in the figure below.","raw_text":"Identify the area marked 'a' in the figure below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial c‌olliculus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vagal t‌riangle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibula‌r triangle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoglossa‌l triangle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypoglossal triangle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/59724c3052b94ab99f894498f7123541.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbb660e9c9774b5a9822227545431ed7x1280x1982.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypoglossal triangle</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX151796368\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX151796368\">T</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX151796368\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX151796368\">he image is of the <strong>floor</strong> of the <strong>4th ventricle,</strong> and the area</span></span> <span class=\"TextRun SCX151796368\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX151796368\">marked 'a' </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX151796368\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX151796368\">is the <strong>hypoglossal triangle.</strong></span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The floor of the 4th ventricle is <strong>diamond-shaped</strong> and lies on the dorsal surface of the pons and superior half of the medulla. It is divided into two symmetrical halves by the longitudinal <strong>median sulcus.</strong> Each half is further divided by <strong>sulcus limitans</strong> into a medial region, <strong>medial eminence,</strong> and a lateral region, <strong>vestibular area</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vestibular nuclei</strong> lie beneath the vestibular area.</p>\n<p>At the superior end of the sulcus limitans, a bluish-gray area is produced by a cluster of nerve cells containing melanin pigment called <strong>substantia ferruginea/locus coeruleus</strong>.</p>\n<p>At the inferior end of the medial eminence, the fibers from the motor nucleus of the facial nerve looping over the <strong>abducens nucleus</strong> are represented by the<strong> facial colliculus.</strong></p>\n<p>Inferiorly, the medial eminence is represented by the <strong>hypoglossal triangle </strong>overlying the <strong>hypoglossal nucleus</strong> and the <strong>vagal triangle</strong> overlying the <strong>dorsal nucleus</strong> of the<strong> vagus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0929","text":"A child is diagnosed to ha‌ve an aneurysmal malforma​tion of a vessel located ​in the marked structure. ​Identify the structure an‍d the vessel present in i‍t.","raw_text":"A child is diagnosed to have an aneurysmal malformation of a vessel located in the marked structure. Identify the structure and the vessel present in it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisterna magna, Inferior r‌etrotonsillar vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quadrigeminal cistern, Gre‌at cerebral vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Supracallosal cistern, Gre‌at cerebral vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sylvian cistern, Occipital‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Quadrigeminal cistern, Great cerebral vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bb461d95d0a48b3bdeaa5a310f692c9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d3fb355d36442499af7e8a5deaefd2fx1280x1231.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Quadrigeminal cistern, Great cerebral vein</strong></p><p>The area marked \"A\" is the <strong>quadrigeminal cistern,</strong> and it contains the <strong>great cerebral vein of Galen.</strong></p>\n<p>Aneurysmal malformation of the great cerebral vein of Galen is a congenital anomaly occurring due to the persistence of the embryonic prosencephalic vein of Markowski. </p>\n<p>The image below is a CT cerebral angiogram of a vein of Galen malformation and an abnormal falcine sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1958","text":"Which is the largest subar‌achnoid cistern?","raw_text":"Which is the largest subarachnoid cistern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cistern‌a pontis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebellomedullary cistern‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cisterna‌ ambiens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sylvian‌ cistern","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebellomedullary cistern","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebellomedullary cistern</strong></p><p>The <strong>largest</strong> subarachnoid cistern is the <strong>cerebellomedullary cistern.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0930","text":"A 2-year-old child was bro‌ught by his mother with c​omplaints of delayed gait​ development and a large ​head. Examination reveale‍d cystic obstruction of t‍he median outlet of the f‍ourth ventricle causing b​allooning of the inferior‌ medullary velum into the‍ cisterna magna. Imperfor​ation of which of the fol‍lowing structures leads t‌o the given condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child was brought by his mother with complaints of delayed gait development and a large head. Examination revealed cystic obstruction of the median outlet of the fourth ventricle causing ballooning of the inferior medullary velum into the cisterna magna. Imperforation of which of the following structures leads to the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foramen o‌f Luschka","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Foramen ‌of Monro","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foramen o‌f Magendie","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aqueduct ‌of Sylvius","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foramen of Magendie","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7235d75e44eb4013a7add9812b7aa350x600x582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foramen of Magendie</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario described is of <strong>Blake's pouch cyst, </strong>caused due to an <strong>imperforate</strong> <strong>foramen</strong> of<strong> Magendie.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>roof</strong> of the <strong>fourth ventricle</strong> communicates posteriorly with the <strong>cisterna magna</strong> through a median aperture called the <strong>foramen</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>Magendie.</strong></p>\n<p>During fetal development, the <strong>roof of the fourth ventricle</strong> is formed by the anterior and posterior membranous areas (<strong>AMA, PMA</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Blake's pouch</strong> is a temporary finger-like growth of the <strong>PMA</strong> which communicates with the fourth ventricle. During the<strong> 9th week</strong> of the development, Blake's pouch perforates to form the <strong>foramen of Magendie</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Communications </strong>of the <strong>ventricular system:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Foramen of <strong>Monro</strong> - Lateral ventricle to the third ventricle </li>\n<li>Cerebral aqueduct of <strong>Sylvius</strong> - III ventricle to IV ventricle </li>\n<li>IV ventricle to subarachnoid space:\n<ul>\n<li>Foramen of <strong>Magendie</strong> (midline) - Cisterna magna </li>\n<li>Foramina of <strong>Luschka</strong> (laterally) - Subarachnoid space </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX178481626\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX178481626\">The outflow of CSF from the IV ventricle is via the medial outlet. This is covered by a membrane in the fetus which perforates later. The <strong>persistence</strong> of this <strong>membrane</strong> causes <strong>cystic obstruction. </strong></span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX178481626\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX178481626\">In </span></span><strong>Dandy-Walker</strong> malformation, there is the presence of many <strong>posterior fossa anomalies</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle</li>\n<li>Hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis</li>\n<li>Enlarged posterior fossa.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1976","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about​ arachnoid villi?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about arachnoid villi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arachnoid villi are the ma‌jor sites of absorption o​f cerebrospinal fluid (CS​F)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arachnoid villi do not ser‌ve as valves","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rate of absorption through‌ arachnoid villi controls​ the CSF pressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arachnoid villi do not serve as valves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ab086b1abd04a62b8c46c583003403fx600x571.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b8a4be657ec4455bbaced26cd23aa96fx510x554.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arachnoid villi do not serve as valves</strong></p><p><strong>Arachnoid villi</strong> act like <strong>valves.</strong> If venous pressure exceeds CSF pressure, arachnoid villi close the tubules to prevent the entry of blood into the subarachnoid space.</p>\n<p>Arachnoid villi are <strong>diverticulums</strong> of the <strong>subarachnoid space</strong> that pierces the dura mater. <strong>Grouped</strong> arachnoid villi produce elevations called <strong>arachnoid granulations. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When the <strong>CSF pressure</strong> is <strong>higher</strong> than the venous pressure of the sinus, CSF flows from the <strong>subarachnoid</strong> space <strong>into</strong> the <strong>venous sinuses</strong> via the arachnoid villi.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>venous pressure exceeds</strong> CSF pressure, the arachnoid <strong>villi close</strong> and <strong>prevent</strong> the <strong>reflux</strong> of blood into the arachnoid space. Some CSF is also directly absorbed into the veins in the subarachnoid space.  </p>\n<p>The CSF production from choroid plexuses is usually constant. Therefore, the <strong>rate of absorption</strong> through the arachnoid villi <strong>controls the CSF pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the pulsatile CSF movement. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0931","text":"The structure marked with ‌arrow in the image given ​below is concerned with _​______.","raw_text":"The structure marked with arrow in the image given below is concerned with _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secretion of the CSF into ‌the subarachnoid space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transport of neurochemical‌s from subjacent neurons ​and glia into CSF","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absorption of the CSF by t‌he arachnoid villi","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CSF return through the tel‌a choroidea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absorption of the CSF by the arachnoid villi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4d97f9273dd4118ae0932844c15135c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2aa217def03a4f5eb006d5c3b395b59ex600x571.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absorption of the CSF by the arachnoid villi</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the above image is a <strong>dural venous sinus,</strong> and the <strong>CSF</strong> is <strong>absorbed</strong> into this sinus through <strong>arachnoid villi.</strong> </p>\n<p>Tela choroidea gives rise to the choroid plexus, from which CSF is secreted into the ventricles.</p>\n<p>Tancytes/specialized glial cells in circumventricular organs help with the transport of neurochemicals to CSF. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0933","text":"What are Pacchionian bodie‌s?","raw_text":"What are Pacchionian bodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal pouches of arachnoid‌ mater and subarachnoid s​pace","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuft of capillaries enclos‌ed by the piamater","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcareous deposits in the‌ astrocytes of pineal gla​nd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shrunken yellow brown cyst‌ic lesions in the gyri, a​ssociated with head injur​y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Focal pouches of arachnoid mater and subarachnoid space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Focal pouches of arachnoid mater and subarachnoid space</strong></p><p><strong>Pacchionian bodies</strong> (arachnoid granulations) are <strong>focal pouches</strong> of <strong>arachnoid mater</strong> and <strong>subarachnoid space </strong>protruding through the walls of dural venous sinuses.</p>\n<p>Option B: Tuft of capillaries enclosed by pia mater lined by ependymal cells forms <strong>tela choroidea</strong>. It secretes CSF.</p>\n<p>Option C: Calcareous deposits in the astrocytes of the pineal gland are known as <strong>brain sand or corpora arenacea</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: Shrunken, yellow-brown, cystic lesions in the gyri and subarachnoid space associated with head injury are called <strong>plaque jaune</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1974","text":"While assessing a 33-year-‌old lady who had been in ​a road traffic accident, ​you note watery discharge​ from her nose mixed with‍ the blood. The following‍ sign is seen on filter p‍aper. Which structures pr​oduce the majority of thi‌s fluid?","raw_text":"While assessing a 33-year-old lady who had been in a road traffic accident, you note watery discharge from her nose mixed with the blood. The following sign is seen on filter paper.  Which structures produce the majority of this fluid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ependymal cells lining the‌ ventricles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Choroid‌ plexus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brain substance through pe‌rivascular space","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Micr‌oglia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Choroid plexus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ead3ce35ad6b410d9588041689e4c419.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be32ba6f326e4ab0a4c09b7153814fb8x600x728.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Choroid plexus</strong></p><p>The case suggests <strong>CSF rhinorrhea</strong> and the image shows the <strong>double-ring sign.</strong> CSF mainly forms in the <strong>choroid plexus.</strong> It is absorbed back by the arachnoid villi. </p>\n<p>Formation of CSF:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major contributor - Choroid plexuses</li>\n<li>Minor contributors -<strong> Ependymal cells</strong> lining the ventricles, brain substance through the perivascular spaces</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CSF is produced at a rate of 550 ml/day. The normal adult volume of CSF is 150ml, and it is cycled about 3.7 times a day. The normal CSF pressure is <strong>70-180 mm H<sub>2</sub>O.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0946","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a circumventricular o​rgan?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a circumventricular organ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organum V‌asculosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median ‌Eminence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenohy‌pophysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pineal‌ Gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenohypophysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenohypophysis</strong></p><p><strong>Circumventricular organs</strong> are those structures in the brain that are <strong>devoid</strong> of the <strong>blood-brain barrier.</strong> Adenohypophysis is not devoid of a blood-brain barrier (BBB).</p>\n<p><strong>Neurohypophysis</strong> is <strong>devoid</strong> of the blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"sAXYMTKJ","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"19_enzyme_kinetics_and_regulation_of_activity","title":"19.Enzyme Kinetics And Regulation Of Activity","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1167","text":"Activation energy is the f​ree energy difference bet​ween _______.","raw_text":"Activation energy is the free energy difference between _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Substrate &amp;​amp; product","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Substrate &amp;amp; transition​ state","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transition state &amp;amp; pro​duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sum of all​ the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Substrate & transition state","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc89cea89f4c41a2b11d08ac120103e9x1280x1003.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Substrate &amp; transition state</strong></p><p>Activation energy is the <strong>free energy difference </strong>between the <strong>substrate</strong> and <strong>transition state.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Activation energy</strong> is defined as the <strong>minimum energy</strong> required to start a chemical reaction. Because of the high activation energy, the rates of uncatalyzed chemical reactions are often slow. In an <strong>enzyme-catalyzed </strong>reaction,<strong> </strong>the activation energy<strong> </strong>is <strong>reduced</strong> by forming a relatively <strong>stable intermediate-enzyme complex.</strong> Thus, enzymes increase the rate of chemical reactions. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1169","text":"In the given reaction, two​ molecules of A combines ​to form P. What is the ki‍netic order of the reacti​on?","raw_text":"In the given reaction, two molecules of A combines to form P.  What is the kinetic order of the reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st order for the forward ​reaction &amp;amp; 2nd order ​for the reverse reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1st order for the forward ​reaction &amp;amp; 1st order ​for the reverse reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd order for the forward ​reaction &amp;amp; 1st order ​for the reverse reaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2nd order for the forward ​reaction &amp;amp; 2nd order ​for the reverse reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2nd order for the forward reaction & 1st order for the reverse reaction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/604992edb52c4dbaa07d46831b60c937.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2nd order for the forward reaction &amp; 1st order for the reverse reaction</strong></p><p>The above reaction has <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> order </strong>for the<strong> forward reaction</strong> and <strong>1<sup>st</sup> order </strong>for the<strong> reverse reaction.</strong></p>\n<p>The sum of the <strong>molar ratios</strong> of the<strong> reactants</strong> defines the <strong>kinetic order</strong> of the reaction. </p>\n<p>In the<strong> forward reaction</strong>, A + A → P, the sum of reactants is 2. Thus, </p>\n<p>                                  Rate ∝ [A] [A] or <strong>Rate ∝ [A]<sup>2</sup></strong></p>\n<p>Therefore, the reaction is said to be <strong>second order</strong> with respect to reactant A.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>reverse reaction</strong>, P → 2 A, the sum of reactants is 1. Thus, </p>\n<p>                                 <strong> Rate ∝ [P]</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore, the reaction is said to be <strong>first order</strong> with respect to reactant P.</p>\n<p>Kinetic order of reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zero-order</strong> reaction: Rate is<strong> independent</strong> of the concentration of reactants.</li>\n<li><strong>First-order </strong>reaction: Rate is <strong>directly proportional</strong> to the concentration of one of the reactants.</li>\n<li><strong>Second-order </strong>reaction: In the simplest kind of 2nd order reaction, the rate is <strong>proportional </strong>to the <strong>square</strong> of the concentration of one reactant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1170","text":"Regarding enzyme kinetics,​ which of the following i​s true? (Q10 = Temperatur‍e coefficient)","raw_text":"<p>Regarding enzyme kinetics, which of the following is true?</p>\n<p>(Q10 = Temperature coefficient)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Q10​ = 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Q10​ = 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Q10​ = 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Q10​ = 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Q10 = 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Q10 = 2</strong></p><p><strong>Q10 = 2 </strong>is the correct statement regarding enzyme kinetics. </p>\n<p>The <strong>temperature coefficient </strong>(Q10) is the factor by which the rate of a biologic process increases for a 10°C increase in temperature. The <strong>rates </strong>of most biological processes typically <strong>double</strong> for a <strong>10°C rise in temperature. </strong></p>\n<p>Thus, <strong>Q10</strong> (degree celsius rise) <strong>= 2</strong> (double).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1174","text":"Which of the following is ​not a type of covalent mo​dification for regulation‍ of enzyme kinetics?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a type of covalent modification for regulation of enzyme kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methy​lation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acety​lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ADP ribo​sylation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ioniz​ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ionization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ionization</strong></p><p><strong>Ionization</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a type of covalent modification regulating enzyme kinetics.</p>\n<p><strong>Regulation</strong> of enzyme kinetics can be brought about by<strong> reversible</strong> <strong>covalent modifications</strong> of the enzyme. These include <strong>methylation</strong>, <strong>acetylation</strong>, <strong>ADP ribosylation</strong>, phosphorylation and partial proteolysis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1182","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the effec​t of substrate concentrat‍ion on the rate of reacti​on?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the effect of substrate concentration on the rate of reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"As substrate concentration​ increases, the initial v​elocity of the reaction i‍ncreases until it reaches​ a maximum value (Vmax)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The substrate concentratio​n at which the velocity o​f the reaction is half ma‍ximum is called Km","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The Michaelis-Menten equat​ion says that initial rea​ction velocity (Vi) = Vma‍x [S]/ Km+[S]","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The plot of initial reacti​on velocity against subst​rate concentration is lin‍ear","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The plot of initial reaction velocity against substrate concentration is linear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/413d8be477d445bfba9a704ba8b9d81ax1280x549.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The plot of initial reaction velocity against substrate concentration is linear</strong></p><p>The plot of initial reaction velocity against substrate concentration is<strong> hyperbolic, </strong>not linear. </p>\n<p>For a typical enzyme, the velocity of a reaction has a hyperbolic relationship with the substance concentration. As the substrate concentration increases,<strong> initial velocity </strong>(V<sub>i</sub>)<sub> </sub><strong>increases</strong> until it reaches a <strong>maximum value </strong>V<sub>max</sub>. After V<sub>max </sub>is reached, further <strong>increase</strong> in the<strong> substrate</strong> concentration does not change reaction velocity. This is because the <strong>enzyme</strong> is <strong>saturated</strong> with the substrate.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Michaelis constant</strong> (K<sub>m</sub>) is the substrate concentration at which V<sub>i</sub> is <strong>half</strong> the <strong>maximal velocity</strong> i.e. V<sub>max</sub>/2. <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">The <strong>Michaelis-Menten equation</strong> illustrates the relationship between V</span><sub style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">i</sub><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and substrate concentration [S] mathematically i.e,</span></p>\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>V<sub>i</sub> = V<sub>max</sub> × [S] / K<sub>m </sub>+ [S]</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7670","text":"The catalytic efficiency o​f enzyme is best expresse​d by which of the followi‍ng kinetic constants:","raw_text":"The catalytic efficiency of enzyme is best expressed by which of the following kinetic constants:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kca​t/Km","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Km/​Kcat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Km​/Ka","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ka​/Km","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kcat/Km","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kcat/Km</strong></p><p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">The<strong> catalytic efficiency</strong> of an enzyme is expressed as </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>K<sub>cat</sub>/K<sub>m</sub>.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>K<sub>cat </sub></strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">is the </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>rate </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>constant</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, which is the <strong>limiting rate</strong> of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction at<strong> saturation. </strong>It is given by V<sub>max</sub> divided by number of active sites of the enzyme. </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>K<sub>m </sub></strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">is the </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Michaelis</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">-</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Menten </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>constant</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, which is defined as the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(V<sub>i</sub>) </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">is half the maximum velocity </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">V<sub>max</sub>/2).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">For some enzymes, when a substrate </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(S) </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">binds to the active site of the enzyme </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(E),</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> and forms an enzyme-substrate complex </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(ES),</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> the product is instantaneously formed. </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">For such enzymes, the reaction of E+S→ES becomes the <strong>rate-limiting step. </strong>Its rate is determined on the rate of diffusion of the molecules in a solution. Hence, these enzymes are called </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>diffusion</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">-</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>limited </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">or </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>catalytically </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>perfect</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Examples of the catalytically perfect enzymes, where the catalytic efficiency </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">reaches the<strong> diffusion</strong> <strong>limit</strong> of 10<sup>8</sup>-10<sup>9</sup> M<sup>−1</sup>s<sup>−1</sup>:</span></p>\n<ul class=\"listtype-bullet listindent1 list-bullet1\">\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Triosephosphate isomerase</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Carbonic anhydrase</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Acetylcholinesterase</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Adenosine deaminase</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5232","text":"In a reaction, the substra​te is available in a conc​entration that is 1000 ti‍mes the Km value of the e​nzyme. After 9 minutes of​ reaction, 1% of substrat‌e is converted to product‌ (12 microgram/ml). If th​e concentration of the en​zyme is changed to 1/3 an​d concentration of substr​ate is doubled, what is t‌he time taken to convert ​the substrate into the sa​me amount of product that‌ i.e. 12 microgram/ml?","raw_text":"In a reaction, the substrate is available in a concentration that is 1000 times the Km value of the enzyme.  After 9 minutes of reaction, 1% of substrate is converted to product (12 microgram/ml). If the concentration of the enzyme is changed to 1/3 and concentration of substrate is doubled, what is the time taken to convert the substrate into the same amount of product that i.e. 12 microgram/ml?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9 mi​nutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4.5 m​inutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"27 mi​nutes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"13.5 m​inutes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 27 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3f970f898ad403588b44f7d14be080cx1280x1019.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 27 minutes</strong></p><p>The time taken for the formation of the same amount of product is<strong> 27 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>In this reaction, the substrate concentration becomes 1000 times i.e <strong>much greater </strong>than the <strong>K<sub>m</sub> value. </strong>Thus, the <strong>rate</strong> of this reaction is equal to V<sub>max </sub>and is thus <strong>independent</strong> of the <strong>substrate concentration</strong> i.e. it follows <strong>zero-order kinetics</strong>. Thus, <strong>doubling</strong> the substrate concentration <strong>does not change</strong> the<strong> velocity</strong> of the reaction i.e. it remains as V<sub>max</sub>.</p>\n<p>No matter the substrate concentration, the <strong>rate </strong>of the reaction is <strong>directly proportional</strong> to the <strong>concentration</strong> of the enzyme. In this situation the enzyme concentration becomes <strong>1/3rd. </strong>Thus, the velocity of the reaction (V<sub>max</sub>) also becomes <strong>1/3rd. </strong>This means that the time taken for the formation of the same amount of product will become <strong>tripled</strong> i.e 9 × 3 = <strong>27 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1177","text":"Which of the following bes​t defines the type of enz​yme inhibition by malonat‍e on succinate dehydrogen​ase reaction?","raw_text":"Which of the following best defines the type of enzyme inhibition by malonate on succinate dehydrogenase reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-com​petitive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uncomp​etitive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compe​titive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Allos​teric","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Competitive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Competitive</strong></p><p>Inhibition of<strong> succinate dehydrogenase </strong>reaction by<strong> malonate</strong> is an example of <strong>competitive inhibition</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1186","text":"Which of the following is ​true about competitive in​hibition?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about competitive inhibition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vmax in​creases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vmax de​creases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vmax c​onstant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Km dec​reases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vmax constant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c19615df824d4db89ef1ee1e641caa3cx1280x2235.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vmax constant</strong></p><p><strong>V<sub>max</sub> </strong>remains<strong> constant</strong> in<strong> competitive inhibition</strong>.</p>\n<p>Competitive inhibition is when the inhibitor binds <strong>reversibly</strong> to the substrate-binding site and <strong>competes</strong> with the <strong>substrate</strong> for the active enzyme-binding site. This effect of competitive inhibition can be overcome by <strong>increasing</strong> the<strong> substrate concentration</strong> by which maximum velocity can still be achieved. Therefore, <strong>V<sub>max</sub> </strong>(maximum velocity of the reaction) is <strong>unaltered </strong>in this type of inhibition. However, the concentration of the substrate required to achieve half of the maximum velocity is increased. Therefore, <strong>K<sub>m</sub> </strong>is <strong>increased </strong>in this condition.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1190","text":"Which of the following is ​false about non-competiti​ve inhibition?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about non-competitive inhibition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vmax de​creases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Km dec​reases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The inhibitor binds at a s​ite different from the ac​tive site","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The inhibitor does not aff​ect the binding of the su​bstrate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Km decreases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05254662368745adb145b8b5c51db9e5x1280x1431.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3b234ddebb64cb6a71325522d1b331ex1280x2317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Km decreases</strong></p><p>K<sub>m</sub> remains<strong> constant</strong> in <strong>noncompetitive inhibition.</strong></p>\n<p>Non-competitive inhibition of enzymes occurs by the binding of inhibitors to enzymes in locations <strong>different</strong> from the <strong>active site</strong>. The inhibition is <strong>not overcome</strong> by<strong> increasing</strong> the <strong>concentratio</strong>n of the substrate. Thus, noncompetitive inhibitors <strong>decrease</strong> the <strong>V<sub>max</sub></strong> of the reaction.</p>\n<p>Non-competitive inhibtiors do not resemble the<strong> </strong>substrate. Thus, they d<strong>o not interfere</strong> with the <strong>binding</strong> of the substrate to the enzyme. Thus, K<sub>m</sub> of the enzyme does not change.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1191","text":"Which of the following sit​uations does the red line​ in the following graph d‍epict?","raw_text":"Which of the following situations does the red line in the following graph depict?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reaction with a competitiv​e inhibitor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reaction with a noncompeti​tive inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reaction with a suicide in​hibitor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reaction with a competitive inhibitor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46d7404240544aa6b40ef022845cba52.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a34a537e78ce46ebae4b14e76e035873x1280x1089.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reaction with a competitive inhibitor</strong></p><p>The <strong>red line</strong> indicates an enzymatic reaction taking place in the presence of a<strong> competitive inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>The graph represents the enzymatic reaction velocity in relation to substrate concentration. The <strong>black line</strong> indicates the reaction taking place in the <strong>absence </strong>of any<strong> inhibitor. </strong></p>\n<p>In the presence of a<strong> competitive inhibitor, </strong>as indicated by<strong> the red line,</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V<sub>max</sub></strong> remains <strong>unchanged.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>K<sub>m</sub> </strong> is<strong> increased.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the presence of a<strong> non-competitive inhibitor, </strong>as indicated by<strong> the blue line,</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V<sub>max</sub></strong> is<strong> decreased</strong></li>\n<li><strong>K<sub>m</sub> </strong>remains <strong>unchanged</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Suicide inhibitors</strong> are specialized substrate analogs. They contain a chemical group that can be transformed by the target enzyme. After binding to the active site of the enzyme, catalysis by the enzyme generates a highly reactive group that forms a<strong> covalent bond</strong> and blocks the function of the enzyme.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1195","text":"Which of the following sit​uations does the red line​ in the following graph d‍epict?","raw_text":"Which of the following situations does the red line in the following graph depict?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reaction with a competitiv​e inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reaction with an allosteri​c Inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reaction with a suicide in​hibitor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reaction with a noncompeti​tive inhibitor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reaction with a noncompetitive inhibitor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3da2728e63c342449b7a11630a6604ba.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7187f911d2c5404f83a485541b950554x710x280.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reaction with a noncompetitive inhibitor</strong></p><p>The<strong> red line</strong> in the graph represents an enzymatic reaction with a <strong>noncompetitive inhibitor</strong>. </p>\n<p>The above graph is called the '<strong>Lineweaver-Burk plot' </strong>or<strong> 'double reciprocal' plot. 1/v<sub>0</sub></strong> (v<sub>0</sub> - initial velocity) is plotted versus <strong>1/[S] </strong>([S] - substrate concentration) and a straight line is obtained. This plot can be used to calculate K<sub>m</sub> and V<sub>max</sub> as well as to determine the mechanism of action of enzyme inhibitors. The <strong>x-intercept</strong> represents -1/K<sub>m</sub> while the <strong>y-intercept</strong> represents 1/V<sub>max. </sub></p>\n<p>In non-competitive inhibition, <strong>V<sub>max</sub> decreases</strong> (or 1/V<sub>max</sub> increases) but <strong>K<sub>m</sub> </strong>is<strong> unchanged. </strong>Hence the graphs of inhibited and uninhibited reactions have the <strong>same x-intercepts</strong> (converge at the same -1/K<sub>m</sub>) but <strong>different y-intercepts.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>An <strong>allosteric</strong> inhibitor alters the conformation of an enzyme upon binding. Enzymes affected by allosteric inhibitors exhibit a <strong>sigmoid</strong>, not hyperbolic, relationship between V<sub>0</sub> and [S].</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1206","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding an alloste​ric modifier?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding an allosteric modifier?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desaturates​ the enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Causes the enzyme to work ​faster only.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Binds to the catalytic sit​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Participates in feedback r​egulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Participates in feedback regulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34fac50a7250488b88596ffb2c1141e5x1280x883.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Participates in feedback regulation</strong></p><p>An <strong>allosteric modifier</strong> of an enzyme usually participates in <strong>feedback regulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Allosteric regulations are a natural example of control loops, such as <strong>feedback </strong>from<strong> downstream products</strong> or <strong>feedforward </strong>from <strong>upstream substrates</strong>. Allosteric regulation is also particularly important in the cell's ability to adjust enzyme activity. </p>\n<p>An allosteric <strong>modifier</strong> or <strong>effector</strong> binds to the <strong>allosteric site</strong> to induce a<strong> conformational change</strong> in the <strong>catalytic</strong> or <strong>active</strong> <strong>site</strong><strong>. </strong>Those modifiers or effectors that enhance the protein's activity are referred to as <strong>allosteric activators</strong>, whereas those that decrease the protein's activity are called <strong>allosteric inhibitors</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 327px;\" width=\"326\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 100.667px;\"> Some examples of allosteric modifiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Enzyme</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Allosteric inhibitor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Allosteric activator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">1. ALA synthase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Heme</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">2. Aspartate transcarbamoylase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">CTP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">ATP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">3. HMG CoA reductase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Cholesterol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">4.Phosphofructokinase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Citrate, ATP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">AMP, F2,6P</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">5. Acetyl CoA carboxylase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Acyl CoA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">Citrate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">6. Citrate synthase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">ATP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">7. Carbamoyl phosphate synthase-I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">NAG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">8. Carbamoyl phosphate synthase-II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">ATP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.667px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1168","text":"Which of the following is ​true about allosteric reg​ulation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about allosteric regulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allosterically regulated e​nzymes belong to K-series​, V-series or both","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Allosteric modifier binds ​to the active site of the​ enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Induction and repression a​re examples of allosteric​ regulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allosteric modifier binds ​to the enzymes by covalen​t bond","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Allosterically regulated enzymes belong to K-series, V-series or both","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Allosterically regulated enzymes belong to K-series, V-series or both</strong></p><p>Allosterically regulated enzymes belong to either the <strong>K-series, V-series</strong> or <strong>both.</strong></p>\n<p>An <strong>allosteric modifier </strong>or <strong>effector</strong> binds to the <strong>allosteric site</strong> to induce a<strong> conformational change</strong> in the catalytic or <strong>active site</strong>. The kinetics of allosteric effector dos not follow Michaelis-Menten kinetics. Hence, allosterically activated enzymes can fall under the following categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>K-series:</strong> The allosteric effector <strong>raises </strong>the<strong> K<sub>m</sub></strong> of the enzyme without affecting V<sub>max</sub> i.e. similar to competitive inhibition.</li>\n<li><strong>V-series:</strong> The allosteric effector <strong>lowers </strong>the<strong> V<sub>max</sub></strong> without affecting K<sub>m</sub> i.e. similar to non-competitive inhibition.</li>\n<li><strong>Both</strong>: Intermediate effects can also be seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Allosteric modifier noncovalently binds to a site <strong>other </strong>than the <strong>active site </strong>(Allo=other, steric=space) and affects the catalysis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Induction and repression are methods of controlling<strong> the rate </strong>of<strong> enzyme synthesis</strong>, not regulation of activity.</p>\n<p>Option D: Allosteric modifier binds to the enzymes <strong>noncovalently.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1197","text":"Chymotrypsinogen is a ____​____","raw_text":"Chymotrypsinogen is a ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zym​ogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carboxyp​eptidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transa​minase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clot-lysi​ng protein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zymogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/adac7f91dd7f47f089b6539f8a9cca9ax333x228.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zymogen</strong></p><p><strong>Chymotrypsinogen</strong> is a <strong>zymogen.</strong></p>\n<p>Some enzymes are secreted as an inactive form called as <strong>zymogens</strong>. The zymogens are converted to their <strong>active</strong> functional forms by <strong>partial</strong>, or <strong>selective proteolysis.</strong> Examples of zymogens include trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, pepsinogen, prophospholipase A2 etc. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1201","text":"A 46-year-old male visits ​the cardiology clinic wit​h a history of exertional‍ chest pain. He is a know​n diabetic, hypertensive ​and hyperlipidemic which ‌are controlled with appro‌priate medication. Consid​ering his high 10 year-AS​CVD risk score (atheroscl​erotic cardiovascular dis​ease risk), he is started‌ on low dose aspirin. Whi​ch of the following best ​defines the mechanism of ‌aspirin action?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old male visits the cardiology clinic with a history of exertional chest pain. He is a known diabetic, hypertensive and hyperlipidemic which are controlled with appropriate medication. Considering his high 10 year-ASCVD risk score (atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk), he is started on low dose aspirin. Which of the following best defines the mechanism of aspirin action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suicide i​nhibition","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Competitive​ inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allosteric​ activation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allosteric​ inhibition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Suicide inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Suicide inhibition</strong></p><p><strong>Low dose aspirin</strong> exerts its action by irreversibly inhibiting <strong>cyclooxygenase enzyme</strong> activity in <strong>platelets</strong>, which is an example of <strong>suicide inhibition</strong>.</p>\n<p>A suicidal enzyme is one that undergoes <strong>self-destruction</strong> in order to terminate its own activity. In this case, in response to irreversible inhibition by aspirin, platelet cyclooxygenase loses its ability to synthesize <strong>thromboxane</strong>. It undergoes self-destruction. Unless new platelets are formed, this cyclooxygenase activity cannot be regained. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1205","text":"Which of the following nuc​leotide triphosphate targ​ets ATCase for feedback r‍egulation? (ATCase - aspa​rtate transcarbamoylase)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following nucleotide triphosphate targets ATCase for feedback regulation?</p>\n<p>(ATCase - aspartate transcarbamoylase)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytidine t​riphosphate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenosine t​riphosphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Guanosine t​riphosphate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A &amp;a​mp; B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A & B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A &amp; B</strong></p><p>Both<strong> cytidine triphosphate </strong>and <strong>adenosine triphosphate</strong> target aspartate transcarbamoylase (ATCase) for feedback regulation.</p>\n<p>ATCase, the catalyst for the first reaction unique to <strong>pyrimidine biosynthesis</strong>, is a target of feedback regulation by two nucleotide triphosphates: cytidine triphosphate (CTP) and adenosine triphosphate (ATP). <strong>CTP </strong>is an end product of the pyrimidine biosynthetic pathway. It <strong>inhibits ATCase</strong>, whereas the purine nucleotide <strong>ATP activates it</strong>.</p>\n<p>High levels of ATP can overcome the inhibition by CTP, enabling synthesis of pyrimidine nucleotides to proceed even when purine nucleotide (CTP) levels are elevated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0796","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding non-compet​itive inhibition?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding non-competitive inhibition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constant Km and increased ​Vmax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constant Km and decreased ​Vmax","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased Km and decreased​ Vmax","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased Km and increased​ Vmax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constant Km and decreased Vmax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/397707ff8e1a474aa143981cba6babd6x1279x1431.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constant Km and decreased Vmax</strong></p><p><strong>Noncompetitive inhibition</strong> has<strong> constant Km </strong>and <strong>decreased Vmax</strong>.</p>\n<p>Noncompetitive inhibition occurs when the inhibitor binds at a site<strong> different </strong>from the<strong> substrate-binding site</strong>. The structure of the inhibitor <strong>does not</strong> resemble that of the substrate and the inhibitor does not affect the<strong> binding</strong> of the substrate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6801","text":"Gibbs free energy curve of​ an enzyme-catalyzed reac​tion is given by","raw_text":"Gibbs free energy curve of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction is given by","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d606ca7064cc4defb4f2e5adc49c4454.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6cbc3287a9e41a194a104e468424808x1280x1190.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b707014b17944c9ac57d4b511c9f5b8x1280x1247.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2289a94bdbf144ca8bf867534f746fbcx1280x1003.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Among the given graphs, the Gibbs free energy change of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction is shown by <strong>graph A</strong> (Option B).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Graph B</strong> (Option A) <strong>cannot represent</strong> the Gibbs free energy of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction, as the <strong>energy state</strong> of an <strong>intermediate</strong> <strong>cannot be lower</strong> than the <strong>product</strong>. A lower energy state of the intermediate implies that the enzyme-intermediate complex is stable and such a complex will usually not further form a product.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">During any chemical reaction, the product is formed by the breaking of substrate bonds and the conformational change of the substrate into a highly unstable <strong>transition state</strong>. This transition state is denoted by the <strong>peak</strong> of the graph. The Gibbs free energy curve of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction (with no intermediate state) is shown in the image given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below graph shows the <strong>normal intermediate formation</strong> with an energy state still higher than that of the product:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>All the reactions have a transition state, but not all reactions have an intermediate state. Every <strong>intermediate state</strong> is again always followed by another <strong>transition state</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Activation energy</strong> is the <strong>energy difference</strong> between the free energy of the <strong>substrates</strong> and that of the <strong>transition state</strong>. Because of the high activation energy, the rates of uncatalyzed chemical reactions are often slow. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In an <strong>enzyme-catalyzed reaction</strong>, the <strong>activation energy</strong> is <strong>reduced</strong> by forming a relatively stable intermediate-enzyme complex. <strong>Enzymes</strong> provide attachment<strong> </strong>sites for the substrate and <strong>speed </strong>up the rate of<strong> reaction</strong>. They <strong>don't change</strong> the<strong> energy</strong> state of either the substrate or the product. The <strong>Gibbs free energy change</strong> remains the <strong>same</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Graph C</strong>: Free energy change cannot be a straight line. Every reaction passes through a high-energy intermediate transition state.</p>\n<p><strong>Graph D</strong>: The activation energy of any reaction is never lower than the free energy of the product.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6792","text":"Which of the following enz​ymes has the highest cata​lytic efficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes has the highest catalytic efficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Km of an enzyme = 10 micro​mole and Kcat = 20 per se​c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Km of an enzyme = 2000 nan​omole and Kcat = 50 per s​ec","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Km of an enzyme = 2 microm​ole and Kcat = 200 per se​c","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Km of an enzyme = 4 microm​ole and Kcat = 200 per se​c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Km of an enzyme = 2 micromole and Kcat = 200 per sec","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Km of an enzyme = 2 micromole and Kcat = 200 per sec</strong></p><p>The highest <strong>catalytic efficiency</strong> is seen when <strong>K<sub>m</sub>= 2 micromole</strong> and<strong> K<sub>cat </sub>= 200 per sec</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Catalytic efficiency</strong> is<strong> </strong>calculated as <strong>K<sub>cat</sub>/K<sub>m</sub></strong>, where<strong><sub> </sub></strong>K<sub>m</sub> is inversely proportional to the binding affinity of the enzyme and K<sub>cat</sub> is the catalytic constant (V<sub>max</sub> divided by the total number of active sites of the enzyme present). <strong>Higher</strong> values of <strong>K<sub>cat</sub></strong> and<strong> lower</strong> values of <strong>K<sub>m</sub></strong> gives better catalytic efficiency. Catalytic efficiency can be used to <strong>compare</strong> the <strong>performance</strong> of 2 different enzymes in catalyzing a reaction.</p>\n<p>Step 1: K<sub>m</sub> of each option after converting respective units to moles will be 10, 2, 2 and 4 (each multiplied by 10<sup>-6</sup>) moles. </p>\n<p>Step 2: The catalytic efficiency is calculated as K<sub>cat</sub>/K<sub>m</sub>. On substituting the values in various options after converting to moles, the values come out to be 2, 25, 100 and 50 (each multiplied by 10<sup>6</sup>) M<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>. </p>\n<p>Some enzymes can be so efficient that they convert the substrate to product instantaneously. They are limited only by the rate at which substrate diffuses towards the enzyme in a solution. These are called <strong>diffusion-limited enzymes</strong> and their catalytic efficiency is as high as <em>10<sup>8 </sup></em><em>M<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"e1sdDxcR","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"19_zoonotic_infections___bacterial___parasitic","title":"19.Zoonotic Infections   Bacterial & Parasitic","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB8299","text":"Which of the following are​ transmitted biologically‍ by invertebrate vectors?​","raw_text":"Which of the following are transmitted biologically by invertebrate vectors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct ​zoonoses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclo-z​oonoses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meta-z​oonoses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sapro-z​oonoses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meta-zoonoses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meta-zoonoses</strong></p><p><strong>Metazoonoses</strong> are a type of zoonoses where the infective agents are transmitted by<strong> invertebrate vectors</strong>. They multiply and develop within the invertebrates (<strong>extrinsic incubation</strong> period) before transmission to a vertebrate host.</p>\n<p>A zoonosis is any disease or infection that is naturally transmissible from vertebrate animals to humans. They can be classified based on their <strong>reservoir hosts</strong> into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anthropozoonoses - transmitted from lower vertebrates to man</li>\n<li>Zooanthroponoses - transmitted from man to lower vertebrates </li>\n<li>Amphixenoses - may be transmitted in either direction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They can also be classified based on the<strong> life cycle</strong> of the infecting agent:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 817.047px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Category</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 439px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Mode</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.047px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p><strong>Direct zoonoses</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 439px;\">\n<p><strong>Direct contact, fomites, </strong>mechanical vector</p>\n<p>No developmental or propagative changes during transmission</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.047px;\">\n<p><strong>Rabies</strong>, <strong>brucellosis</strong>, trichinosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p><strong>Cyclo-zoonoses</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 439px;\">\n<p>Require <strong>&gt; 1 vertebrate host </strong>to complete the developmental cycle</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.047px;\">\n<p>Human<strong> taeniasis,</strong> Echinococcosis, Pentastomid infections</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p><strong>Meta-zoonoses</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 439px;\">\n<p>Transmitted by<strong> invertebrate vectors </strong></p>\n<p>Infective agent <strong>develops and multiplies</strong> within the invertebrate (extrinsic incubation period) after which it is transmitted to another vertebrate host</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.047px;\">\n<p>Arboviral infections<strong>, plague </strong>and<strong> schistosomiasis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p><strong>Sapro-zoonoses</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 439px;\">\n<p>Has both a<strong> vertebrate host </strong>and a<strong> non-animal</strong> (food, soil, plants) developmental site or reservoir</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.047px;\">\n<p>Various forms of <strong>larva migrans</strong> and <strong>mycoses</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8319","text":"Multiple members of the sa​me community presented wi‍th sudden-onset fever, ch​ills, and painful inguina‌l swellings as shown belo‍w. Further questioning le‌ads to the identification​ of a rat infestation in ‍the locality. Which of th​e following is the likely​ source of transmission o​f this disease?","raw_text":"Multiple members of the same community presented with sudden-onset fever, chills, and painful inguinal swellings as shown below. Further questioning leads to the identification of a rat infestation in the locality.  Which of the following is the likely source of transmission of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hard​ tick","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sand​ fly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rat ​flea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mi​te","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rat flea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0066f23083e414b94fc1c069d12523f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rat flea</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>buboes</strong> are suggestive of <strong>bubonic plague. </strong>It is transmitted by the bite of the infected <strong>rat flea</strong>, <em><strong>Xenopsylla</strong> <strong>cheopis</strong></em>. Rodents, including rats, are the reservoirs of infection.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, the <strong>pneumonic</strong> plague spreads from man to man by <strong>droplet</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8313","text":"Which of the following ind​ices is the best determin‍ant of a potential plague​ outbreak?","raw_text":"Which of the following indices is the best determinant of a potential plague outbreak?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total fl​ea index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cheopsi​s index","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Burrow​ index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Special percentage of flea​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cheopsis index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cheopsis index</strong></p><p><strong>Cheopis index</strong> is the best determinant of potential <strong>plague outbreaks</strong>. If this index is &gt;<strong> 1</strong>, it indicates there is a <strong>high likelihood</strong> of an outbreak. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8320","text":"All of the following are c​auses of different types ‍of typhus except:","raw_text":"All of the following are causes of different types of typhus except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R. ric​kettsii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"R.prow​azekii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R. tsuts​ugamushi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"R.t​yphi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. R. rickettsii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. R. rickettsii</strong></p><p><strong><em> R.rickettsii</em></strong> causes <strong>Rocky Mountain spotted fever.</strong> It is a type of spotted fever, not typhus.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>*Note:- Orientia tsutsugamushi was previously known as Rickettsia tsutsugamushi.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8322","text":"Which of the following ric​kettsial diseases is corr‍ectly matched with their ​respective vectors?","raw_text":"Which of the following rickettsial diseases is correctly matched with their respective vectors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scrub typhus​ - hard tick","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rocky mountain spotted fev​er - louse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endemic ty​phus - flea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epidemic ty​phus - mite","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endemic typhus - flea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endemic typhus - flea</strong></p><p><strong>Endemic typhus</strong> is caused by the bite of an infective<strong> flea</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>This condition,<strong> </strong>also called <strong>murine typhus </strong>or<strong> </strong>flea-borne typhus<strong>,</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Rickettsia typhi</em></strong>. Rats serve as reservoirs. The incubation period is 1–2 weeks. The <strong>Weil Felix reaction</strong> is positive in 2<sup>nd</sup> week.</p>\n<p>The drug of choice is <strong>tetracycline</strong>. Rodent control is most important and is best done by BHC and malathion.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8327","text":"A 38-year-old woman is bei​ng evaluated for fever an‍d rash. All of the follow​ing can be included in th‌e differential except:","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman is being evaluated for fever and rash. All of the following can be included in the differential except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidemi​c typhus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endemic​ typhus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scrub ​typhus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Q-f​ever","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Q-fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Q-fever</strong></p><p>Rashes and local lesions are not a feature of <strong>Q-fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is caused by <strong><em>Coxiella burnetii</em></strong>. The mode of transmission is the <strong>i</strong><strong>nhalation </strong>of infected dust, entry through <strong>abrasions</strong>, or <strong>ingestion</strong> of contaminated milk. Rarely, ticks can be involved in transmission. The incubation period is <strong>2 to 3 weeks</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The drug of choice is prolonged treatment with <strong>doxycycline</strong>. Pasteurization is advised to inactivate the causative agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8321","text":"Which of the following is ​false about scrub typhus?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about scrub typhus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mites are ​the vectors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adult mite feeds on verteb​ral host","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caused by R. tsutsugamushi​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treated with tetracyclines​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adult mite feeds on vertebral host","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adult mite feeds on vertebral host</strong></p><p>Both adult and nymphal stages of the <strong>mite</strong> are <strong>free-living</strong> in nature. They do not feed on the vertebral host.</p>\n<p><strong>Scrub typhus </strong>is the most widespread rickettsial disease. It is caused by <strong><em>Orientia</em> <em>tsutsugamushi </em></strong>(previously known as <em>Rickettsia tsutsugamushi) </em>and transmitted by the bite of<strong> trombiculid mite</strong>. The incubation period is 10-12 days.</p>\n<p>It presents with a typical <strong>eschar </strong>at the bite site. It is diagnosed by the <strong>Weil Felix reaction, </strong>which is strongly positive with proteus strain OXK.<strong> </strong>It is treated by <strong>tetracyclines.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8323","text":"Which of the following are​ the causative agent and ‍vector of epidemic typhus​ respectively?","raw_text":"Which of the following are the causative agent and vector of epidemic typhus respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rickettesia prowazekii and​ louse","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rickettesia rickettsii and​ louse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rickettsia prowazekii and ​tick","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rickettesia rickettsii and​ tick","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rickettesia prowazekii and louse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rickettesia prowazekii and louse</strong></p><p>The causative agent of epidemic typhus is <em><strong>Rickettsia</strong> </em><strong><em>prowazekii</em> </strong>and the vector is<strong> </strong>a<strong> louse</strong>.</p>\n<p>The incubation period is 10 to 14 days. Brill-Zinsser disease refers to the recurrent form of epidemic typhus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7390","text":"A patient presents with fe​ver for 1 week, with abdo‍minal distension, loss of​ appetite, and lymphadeno‌pathy. There is a poor re‍sponse to antibiotics and‌ antimalarials. The Widal​ test is negative but rk3‍9 is positive. Which drug​ can be used in the treat​ment of this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with fever for 1 week, with abdominal distension, loss of appetite, and lymphadenopathy. There is a poor response to antibiotics and antimalarials. The Widal test is negative but  rk39 is positive. Which drug can be used in the treatment of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liposomal Am​photericin B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fluco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Line​zolid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bedaq​uiline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liposomal Amphotericin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liposomal Amphotericin B</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1882\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>visceral leishmaniasis.</strong> This patient should be managed with <strong>liposomal amphotericin B.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition causes fever with rigor and chills associated with splenomegaly and hepatomegaly. Though lymphadenopathy is common in most endemic regions, it is rare in India. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1883\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is diagnosed by ELISA, indirect immunofluorescent antibody test (IFAT), and <strong>rk39 </strong>which detects <strong>antibodies </strong>to<strong> <em>Leishmania donovani</em>.</strong> </p>\n<p>The treatment is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In most <strong>endemic regions</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Drug of choice - <strong>pentavalent antimonial</strong> (sodium stibogluconate or meglumine antimoniate)</li>\n<li>If it fails or is unavailable - amphotericin B </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1883\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>India</strong> and <strong>Mediterranian</strong> countries, the drug of choice is <strong>liposomal</strong> <strong>AmB.</strong></li>\n<li>Other drugs - <strong>paromomycin</strong> and <strong>miltefosine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Fluconazole is an antifungal and is not useful in visceral leishmaniasis</p>\n<p>Option C: Linezolid is used in the treatment of MRSA  and related infections.</p>\n<p>Option D: Bedaquiline is a newer anti-TB drug used in MDR-TB. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8328","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about Leishmania‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about Leishmania?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leishmania is an extracell​ular parasite","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extrinsic incubation perio​d of kala-azar is 4 to 25‍ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is transmitted by sandf​ly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post kala-azar dermal leis​hmaniasis is caused by L.‍ donovani","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leishmania is an extracellular parasite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97f710178a004cb0b9588e614cad3070x700x465.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leishmania is an extracellular parasite</strong></p><p><em><strong>Leishmania</strong></em> is an <strong>intracellular</strong> parasite in the macrophages.</p>\n<p>The vector is <em>Phlebotomus</em><strong> </strong>(<strong>sandfly</strong>). Man is the reservoir in the Indian kala-azar. The incubation period is 10 days to 2 years, whereas the<strong> extrinsic incubation</strong> period is<strong> 4 to 25 days</strong>. The following conditions are caused by <em>Leishmania</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visceral leishmaniasis or kala-azar – <em>L. donovani</em></li>\n<li>Cutaneous leishmaniasis or oriental sore – <em>L. tropica</em></li>\n<li>Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis - <em>L. braziliensis</em></li>\n<li>Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis - <em>L. donovani</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below is a microscopic image of the LD body.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>According to the NVBDCP, the management is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line - single-dose <strong>liposomal amphotericin B</strong> (10 <span class=\"nowrap\">mg/kg</span> IV infusion)</li>\n<li>Alternate - <strong>paromomycin + miltefosine</strong> for 10 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Monotherapy with antimonials (sodium stibogluconate) is no longer recommended due to resistance and toxicity. Synthetic <strong>pyrethroid</strong> is used for indoor residual spraying in all the endemic areas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9570","text":"Kala-Azar is endemic in al​l these areas except:","raw_text":"Kala-Azar is endemic in all these areas except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"West ​Bengal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"U​P","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bi​har","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"As​sam","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Assam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Assam</strong></p><p><strong>Kala-Azar</strong> is not endemic in Assam. It is <strong>endemic</strong> in the following states in 54 districts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uttar Pradesh</li>\n<li>West Bengal</li>\n<li>Bihar</li>\n<li>Jharkhand</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8324","text":"In patients with vagabond ​disease, which of the fol‍lowing is implicated?","raw_text":"In patients with vagabond disease, which of the following is implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lo​use","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mi​te","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ti​ck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blac​k fly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Louse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/106e6c979d6946d29446fe843e3010a5x1280x1760.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Louse</strong></p><p><strong>Vagabond’s </strong>disease or <strong>pediculosis corporis </strong>is caused by<strong> </strong>the<strong> body louse</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the body louse.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Bp8ETMTX","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"19_non_syphilitic_sexually_transmitted_diseases","title":"19.Non Syphilitic Sexually Transmitted Diseases","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5147","text":"The microscopic examinatio‌n of a sample obtained fr​om a genital ulcer is sho‌wn below. What is the lik‍ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The microscopic examination of a sample obtained from a genital ulcer is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gonor‌rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Granuloma inguinale","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0d07339d2b14a1c9b631ba1df9ea7c6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e79a4b90109d441fa7f3e5bfa5036982x720x474.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d57ae7fb10c34d62bd5166a6b3927081x720x705.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Granuloma inguinale</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Donovan bodies</strong>, which points to a diagnosis of <strong>granuloma</strong> <strong>inguinale.</strong></p>\n<p>Donovan bodies refer to the Gram-negative rod-shaped or oval organisms that show <strong>bipolar staining</strong> or <strong>safety‐pin appearance</strong>, present <strong>intracellularly</strong> in the cytoplasm of large <strong>mononuclear</strong> <strong>cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> </strong>appears on microscopy as<strong> Gram‐negative intracellular diplococci </strong>within phagocytes as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Microscopy of chancroid lesions shows <strong><em>H. ducreyi</em></strong> in a sheet-like or <strong>school of fish railroad </strong>pattern as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: In LGV, an increased number of <strong>polymorphonuclear leukocytes</strong> are noted on microscopy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2536","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with the following painl​ess and foul-smelling ulc‌er. On examination, there‍ is no lymphadenopathy. W​hat is the causative orga‍nism?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with the following painless and foul-smelling ulcer. On examination, there is no lymphadenopathy. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemophilu‌s ducreyi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes simpl‌ex virus - 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella ‌granulomatis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Klebsiella granulomatis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04f6c106116e4736a9f30e9ac699f83f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Klebsiella granulomatis</strong></p><p>The clinical picture of a painless, beefy red, foul-smelling ulcer with no lymphadenopathy points to a diagnosis of <strong>granuloma inguinale</strong>. It is caused by <strong><em>Calymmatobacterium granulomatis</em> </strong>also known as <strong><em>Klebsiella granulomatis</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>incubation</strong> period varies from<strong> 8-80 days</strong>, the average being<strong> 50 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Four types of lesions have been described in this condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ulcerogranulomatous -</strong> <strong>most common</strong> type with <strong>painless beefy red</strong> ulcers<strong>. </strong>They are also highly <strong>vascular </strong>and <strong>bleed</strong> on touch.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertrophic -</strong> usually with a raised irregular edge</li>\n<li><strong>Necrotic -</strong> offensive smelling ulcer that causes tissue destruction</li>\n<li><strong>Sclerotic</strong> or <strong>cicatricial -</strong> with fibrous or scar tissue</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary lesions can occur on genital and extragenital sites. Untreated genital ulcers are followed by genital <strong>disfigurement </strong>and have the potential for <strong>malignant change.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5143","text":"A middle-aged man presents‌ with a genital ulcer, as​ shown below. Microscopy ‌of a sample from the lesi‍on shows Donovan bodies. ​Which of the following is‍ false about this disease‌?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with a genital ulcer, as shown below. Microscopy of a sample from the lesion shows Donovan bodies. Which of the following is false about this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary ulcer heals withou‌t scarring","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It has an incubation perio‌d of 8 to 80 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has the potential for m‌alignant transformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caused by Calymmatobacteri‌um granulomatis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary ulcer heals without scarring","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64ed62867da5410c90bd788071613bfd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a275b71510bf448b824dacd142b98b65x1280x1198.758815232722.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary ulcer heals without scarring</strong></p><p><strong>Donovan bodies </strong>are characteristic of <strong>donovanosis</strong> or<strong> granuloma inguinale</strong>. The primary ulcer, if left untreated, heals spontaneously with <strong>scarring</strong> or persists and <strong>spreads</strong>.</p>\n<p>Granuloma inguinale is a sexually transmitted infection caused by <strong><em>Calymmatobacterium</em> <em>granulomatis</em></strong> / <em>Kl</em><em>e</em><em>bsiel</em><em>la</em><em> granulomatis.</em> The <strong>incubation</strong> period varies from<strong> 8-80 days</strong>, the average being<strong> 50 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Four types of lesions have been described in this condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ulcerogranulomatous -</strong> <strong>most common</strong> type with <strong>painless beefy red</strong> ulcers<strong>. </strong>They are also highly <strong>vascular </strong>and <strong>bleed</strong> on touch.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertrophic -</strong> usually with a raised irregular edge</li>\n<li><strong>Necrotic -</strong> offensive smelling ulcer that causes tissue destruction</li>\n<li><strong>Sclerotic</strong> or <strong>cicatricial -</strong> with fibrous or scar tissue</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary lesions can occur on genital and extragenital sites. Untreated genital ulcers are followed by genital <strong>disfigurement </strong>and have the potential for <strong>malignant change.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5149","text":"A 35-year old man with a h‌istory of high-risk sexua​l behavior presents with ‌the following painless pe‍nile ulcer. It bleeds on ​touch and there are no en‍larged lymph nodes. What ‌initial treatment will yo‍u prescribe for him?","raw_text":"A 35-year old man with a history of high-risk sexual behavior presents with the following painless penile ulcer. It bleeds on touch and there are no enlarged lymph nodes. What initial treatment will you prescribe for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithromycin 1g daily for ‌3 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azithromycin 500 mg daily ‌for 3 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxycycline 100 mg BD for ‌5 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Doxycycline 100 mg BD for ‌2 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 3 weeks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e430c11e374744f48ad4f512ffad98b7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 3 weeks</strong></p><p>This man with a painless, vascular ulcer and no lymphadenopathy has <strong>granuloma inguinale</strong>. The first-line treatment is <strong>azithromycin</strong> 1 g orally once weekly or 500 mg daily for <strong>3 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this condition,<strong> prolonged antibiotic therapy</strong> is required. Antibiotics must be given for<strong> 3-6 weeks</strong> and until the ulcer has <strong>completely healed</strong>. The second-line drugs are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline</strong> 100 mg orally BD</li>\n<li><strong>Erythromycin</strong> base 500 mg orally QID</li>\n<li><strong>Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole</strong> 160 mg/800 mg orally BD</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5145","text":"Which of the following pre‌sents with pseudobubo?","raw_text":"Which of the following presents with pseudobubo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Donov‌anosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Donovanosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Donovanosis</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudobubo</strong> is a feature of <strong>donovanosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It refers to <strong>granulomatous lesions </strong>that may extend into <strong>subcutaneous</strong> <strong>tissue </strong>and mimic lymphadenopathy. The presence of these lesions both above and below the inguinal ligament results in a <strong>pseudo-groove sign, </strong>similar to the groove sign of Greenblatt seen in lymphogranuloma venereum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2595","text":"A patient with vaginal dis‌charge has the following ​finding on examination. W‌hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with vaginal discharge has the following finding on examination. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonococcal‌ cervicitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichom‌oniasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydial‌ cervicitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trichomoniasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7fb03f86b8de4473885e7d6e9bdca296.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trichomoniasis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>colpitis macularis</strong> or <strong>strawberry cervix</strong>, which is a characteristic finding of <strong>trichomoniasis</strong>. It results from microscopic, <strong>punctate hemorrhages</strong> of the cervix.</p>\n<p>It is caused by <strong><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></strong> and is one of the most common causes of vaginal discharge. It is transmitted by <strong>sexual intercourse, </strong>with an incubation period of<strong> 4–21</strong> days.</p>\n<p>In <strong>females</strong>, it characteristically causes a <strong>copious, frothy, yellow-green discharge</strong> with <strong>pH &gt; 4.5</strong>, along with vaginal soreness and urinary frequency. <strong>Males</strong> can be asymptomatic or develop <strong>non-specific urethritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis in females is usually easily confirmed by the presence of <strong>motile organisms</strong> on a <strong>wet film. </strong>Culture in <strong>Feinberg–Whittington medium</strong> is the most reliable method but is not routinely done.</p>\n<p>Standard treatment is <strong>metronidazole 2g PO single dose</strong>. Regardless of treatment given, patients should <strong>avoid sexual contact for 7 days</strong> since the last antibiotic dose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2591","text":"As a PHC medical officer, ‌you see a 25-year-old man​, who has come with painf‌ul lesions as shown below‍. On further probing, he ​tells you that he had ves‍icles on his penis 2 days‌ back and that he is alle‍rgic to penicillin. On ex​amination, he has bilater​al tender lymphadenopathy‍. Which kit will you use ‍to treat him?","raw_text":"As a PHC medical officer, you see a 25-year-old man, who has come with painful lesions as shown below. On further probing, he tells you that he had vesicles on his penis 2 days back and that he is allergic to penicillin. On examination, he has bilateral tender lymphadenopathy. Which kit will you use to treat him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gree‌n kit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Red‌ kit","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blue‌ kit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whit‌e kit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Red kit","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f138461ac4b24529922880d4f3eabe4b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Red kit</strong></p><p>The clinical picture of<strong> multiple, grouped painful ulcers </strong>with<strong> tender lymphadenopathy </strong>in this patient points to a diagnosis of <strong>herpes genitalis </strong>infection by HSV-2. It is treated with a <strong>red kit</strong>.</p>\n<p>The red kit or kit 5 is meant for genital ulcer disease - herpetic<strong>. </strong>It contains <strong>acyclovir 400 mg </strong>tablets to be taken<strong> TDS </strong>for<strong> 7 days</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Chancroid</strong> is a close differential diagnosis, which presents with <strong>multiple, soft painful ulcers </strong>and unilateral<strong> tender lymphadenopathy</strong>. However, vesicles are not seen in chancroid and the ulcers are <strong>deeper</strong> with ragged <strong>undermined edges</strong>.</p>\n<p>History of drug allergy to penicillin is relevant only when the patient presents with the primary chancre of syphilis. In such a case, they would need to be treated with a blue kit instead of a white kit.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2594","text":"While being counseled for ‌a sexually transmitted in​fection, a man is informe‌d that his asymptomatic p‍artner does not need to b​e treated. Which of the f‍ollowing kits will he be ‌treated with?","raw_text":"While being counseled for a sexually transmitted infection, a man is informed that his asymptomatic partner does not need to be treated. Which of the following kits will he be treated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grey‌ kit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blue‌ kit","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Red‌ kit","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blac‌k kit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Red kit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Red kit</strong></p><p>The <strong>red kit</strong> is used for the treatment of<strong> herpetic</strong> genital ulcer disease, in which treatment of <strong>asymptomatic</strong> partners is <strong>not required</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Grey</strong> kit used in men for <strong>urethral</strong> <strong>discharge</strong> and <strong>scrotal</strong> <strong>swelling</strong> requires <strong>all</strong> <strong>recent</strong> partners to be treated. But, when used for <strong>cervical discharge</strong> in women, only <strong>symptomatic male</strong> partners need to be treated.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Blue</strong> kit is used for <strong>non-herpetic</strong> genital ulcer disease when the patient is allergic to penicillin. All partners in the last <strong>3 months</strong> must be treated.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Black</strong> kit is used for inguinal <strong>buboes</strong>. All partners in the last <strong>3 weeks</strong> must be treated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5140","text":"A 28-year-old male present‌s with painful deep ulcer​s on his penis as shown b‌elow. He also has painful‍ unilateral lymphadenopat​hy. What is the diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old male presents with painful deep ulcers on his penis as shown below. He also has painful unilateral lymphadenopathy. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes g‌enitalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chancroid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3ff8bdf1256e458a886cc62cafa68779.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chancroid</strong></p><p>This patient with painful, deep ulcers with tender lymphadenopathy has<strong> chancroid. </strong>It is caused by<strong> <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>The incubation period is <strong>3-10 days</strong>. The primary lesion begins as a painful papule that undergoes central necrosis to form <strong>painful</strong>, <strong>non-indurated</strong> ulcers with <strong>undermined</strong> edges. <strong>Kissing ulcers</strong> due to autoinoculation may also be present.</p>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>painful</strong> <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong>, which later progresses to form suppurating <strong>buboes</strong> and <strong>sinuses</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: HSV-2 infection presents with multiple vesicles, which develop into shallow ulcers, along with painful lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p>Option C: LGV presents with a painless or painful ulcer later followed by painful lymphadenopathy. The incubation period is 3-30 days.</p>\n<p>Option D: Granuloma inguinale or donovanosis presents with a painless papule that forms a beefy red ulcer with no lymphadenopathy. The incubation period is 8-80 days (average 50 days).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5141","text":"A young man presents with ‌the following painful, so​ft ulcer, 4 days after un‌protected sexual intercou‍rse. Which of the followi​ng will you choose as ini‍tial treatment for this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A young man presents with the following painful, soft ulcer, 4 days after unprotected sexual intercourse. Which of the following will you choose as initial treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM stat‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD fo‌r 4 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin 500 mg QID fo‌r 7 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azithromycin‌ 1g oral stat","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Azithromycin 1g oral stat","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d06e7a4358c04f03bcb0c4e7744fea84.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Azithromycin 1g oral stat</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chancroid</strong>. First-line treatment is <strong>azithromycin</strong> <strong>1g</strong> <strong>orally</strong> as a single dose or <strong>ceftriaxone</strong> <strong>250 mg IM </strong>as a single dose.</p>\n<p>The second-line options for chancroid are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Ciprofloxacin 500 mg orally twice a day for 3 days  (OR)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Erythromycin base 500 mg orally four times a day for 7 days</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5135","text":"A 40-year-old man develops‌ thick yellowish discharg​e from his urethra 2 week‌s after an unprotected se‍xual encounter. Which of ​the following is the reco‍mmended diagnostic test?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man develops thick yellowish discharge from his urethra 2 weeks after an unprotected sexual encounter. Which of the following is the recommended diagnostic test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Culture and‌ sensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nucleic acid amplification‌ test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enzyme Linked Immunosorben‌t Assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microscopy showing inclusi‌on bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nucleic acid amplification test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nucleic acid amplification test</strong></p><p><strong>Chlamydial urethritis</strong> is the likely diagnosis in this patient as it has an incubation period of <strong>1-3 weeks</strong>.<strong> Nucleic acid amplification test </strong>(NAAT) is the only diagnostic test recommended for detecting <em>Chlamydia</em>.</p>\n<p>The samples used are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Men</strong> - first void urine </li>\n<li><strong>Women</strong> - vaginal or endocervical swabs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>NAAT</strong> can only detect <em>Chlamydia</em> species, but <strong>cannot differentiate</strong> between <strong>LGV</strong> and <strong>non-LGV</strong> types. <strong>Genotyping</strong> of <em>Chlamydia</em>‐positive samples is needed to <strong>specifically</strong> diagnose LGV infection.</p>\n<p>Serology, cell culture, and microscopy have no role in routine screening or diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5136","text":"A 29-year-old primigravida‌ is diagnosed with chlamy​dial cervicitis. What is ‌the drug of choice?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old primigravida is diagnosed with chlamydial cervicitis. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftr‌iaxone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Azithromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Azithromycin</strong></p><p>A<strong> single dose </strong>of<strong> azithromycin 1g </strong>is the drug of choice in pregnant women with <strong>uncomplicated chlamydial infection</strong>. </p>\n<p>The drug of choice for chlamydia infection in <strong>adults</strong> and <strong>adolescents</strong> is <strong>doxycycline</strong> 100 mg orally BD for 7 days. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Alternative regimens are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Azithromycin 1g orally, single dose </li>\n<li>Levofloxacin 500mg OD for 7 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Azithromycin is preferred due to its greater <strong>effectiveness</strong> and <strong>lower cost</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy as it causes depression of bone growth and enamel hypoplasia in the fetus.</p>\n<p>Option B: Ceftriaxone is used for the treatment of gonococcal infections.</p>\n<p>Option D: Amoxicillin is used as an alternative regimen as it is less effective.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5132","text":"A 20-year-old man presents‌ with burning pain on pas​sing urine. He reluctantl‌y admits that he had unpr‍otected sexual intercours​e 3 days ago. The followi‍ng examination findings a‌re seen. Which of the fol‍lowing is the likely caus​ative organism?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presents with burning pain on passing urine. He reluctantly admits that he had unprotected sexual intercourse 3 days ago. The following examination findings are seen. Which of the following is the likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoplasma‌ genitalium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neisseria ‌gonorrhoeae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neisseria gonorrhoeae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00f33ad11df2478486bb8f8c7d9b95a6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neisseria gonorrhoeae</strong></p><p>The symptoms of this patient occurring 3 days after unprotected sex point to urethritis caused by <strong><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>,</strong> as its <strong>incubation</strong> <strong>period</strong> is <strong>1-5 days.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common presentation of gonococcal infection in <strong>men</strong> is <strong>acute</strong> <strong>urethritis</strong> with <strong>dysuria </strong>and profuse <strong>purulent </strong><strong>discharge</strong>. Pharyngeal and rectal infections can also occur depending on the type of sexual contact.</p>\n<p>The primary site of infection in<strong> women </strong>is the <strong>cervix</strong> but the urethra, rectum, and pharynx may be involved. Symptoms include excessive <strong>vaginal</strong> <strong>discharge</strong>, <strong>dysuria</strong>, deep dyspareunia, <strong>postcoital</strong> and <strong>intermenstrual</strong> <strong>bleeding</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of<strong> non-gonococcal urethritis</strong> (NGU) is <strong><em>Chlamydia</em></strong>, which has an incubation period of <strong>1-3 weeks</strong>. It usually presents with dysuria alone, but mucopurulent discharge can occur. Other causes of NGU are <strong><em>Mycoplasma genitalium</em></strong> and <strong><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2569","text":"A 30-year-old IV drug abus‌er with high-grade fever ​and chills is found to ha‌ve a new-onset murmur. Bl‍ood culture is positive f​or Neisseria gonorrhoeae.‍ Which of the following s‌tatements is false about ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old IV drug abuser with high-grade fever and chills is found to have a new-onset murmur. Blood culture is positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs as a result of d‌irect spread by ascending​ infections","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Classic presentation is wi‌th a dermatitis–arthritis​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin lesions are small, te‌nder and maculopapular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us and HIV infection are ​risk factors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It occurs as a result of direct spread by ascending infections","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba8fc549fef94ecc81d934aff58eaa98x1280x1170.7757404795486.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It occurs as a result of direct spread by ascending infections</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of a patient with a positive blood culture is suggestive of <strong>disseminated gonococcal infection, </strong>which is due to <strong>hematogenous </strong>spread.</p>\n<p>It is seen in 0.5–3% of patients. <strong>Risk factors</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female sex</li>\n<li>Men who have sex with men</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Menstruation</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></li>\n<li>Complement deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>Intravenous drug use</strong></li>\n<li><strong>HIV infection</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The classic presentation is <strong>dermatitis–arthritis syndrome</strong> with mild fever. The skin lesions are <strong>small, tender,</strong> and initially <strong>maculopapular</strong>. Later a central <strong>vesicle</strong> or <strong>pustule</strong> appears and is followed by <strong>hemorrhage </strong>and<strong> necrosis</strong>. These lesions are commonly seen near affected joints.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Joint</strong> or<strong> tendon pain</strong> is the most common accompanying feature. <strong>Tenosynovitis</strong> often affects the hands and fingers. 1/3rd of the patients may proceed to <strong>suppurative arthritis</strong>, most commonly of the knee.</p>\n<p>Other manifestations like endocarditis, pericarditis, osteomyelitis, and meningitis are extremely rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5134","text":"A 27-year-old commercial s‌ex worker comes to the OP​D with the following find‌ing. What is the causativ‍e organism?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old commercial sex worker comes to the OPD with the following finding. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L‌3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophil‌us ducreyi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia tr‌achomatis D-K","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella ‌granulomatis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/79b653be99c84de2857be891a107b773.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3</strong></p><p>This image shows the pathognomonic <strong>groove sign of Greenblatt, </strong>which is suggestive of <strong>lymphogranuloma</strong> <strong>venereum</strong>. It is caused by <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> serovars L1-L3</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Groove sign is seen in ⅓ of the cases. It refers to the depression between the enlarged femoral and inguinal lymph nodes on either side of the inguinal ligament. </p>\n<p>Option B: <em>H. ducreyi</em> causes chancroid.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em>C. trachomatis</em> D-K causes non-gonococcal urethritis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em>K. granulomatis</em> causes granuloma inguinale.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2573","text":"A 30-year-old thin-built m‌an presents with urethrit​is. Gram-staining of the ‌purulent discharge is see‍n in the following image.​ What is the treatment of‍ choice for this infectio‌n?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old thin-built man presents with urethritis. Gram-staining of the purulent discharge is seen in the following image. What is the treatment of choice for this infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone 250 mg IM + Az‌ithromycin 1g PO stat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM in a‌ single dose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM + Do‌xycycline 100 mg BD for 7​ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Azithromycin 1g PO stat + ‌Doxycycline 100 mg BD for​ 7 days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM + Doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64d6a57858ba42dfbce6fdda44e11f46.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM + Doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario along with the microscopic findings of <strong>Gram‐negative intracellular diplococci </strong>indicates infection with <strong><em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></strong>. As it has not been confirmed by NAAT testing, this patient must also be treated for <strong>concurrent chlamydial infection.</strong></p>\n<p>Single-agent therapy with <strong>ceftriaxone</strong> is preferred for the treatment of gonococcal infections. According to <strong>CDC guidelines (2021)</strong>, treatment of uncomplicated gonococcal infections is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weight &lt;150 kg – Ceftriaxone 500 mg intramuscular (IM) in a single dose</li>\n<li>Weight ≥150 kg – Ceftriaxone 1 g IM in a single dose</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The doses of ceftriaxone are higher than the previous guidelines as the gonococcal minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) are rising worldwide. The British BASHH guidelines also recommend a high dose of 1g.</p>\n<p>Concurrent <strong>chlamydial</strong> infection must also be treated unless it has been excluded by <strong>molecular testing</strong>. First-line drug administered is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oral <strong>doxycycline</strong> <strong>100 mg BD </strong>for 7 days </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Partner treatment</strong> is also required.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5138","text":"A man is referred to the S‌TI clinic with the follow​ing painful finding. He g‌ives a history of a small‍ painless ulcer on his pe​nis 5 weeks ago, which re‍solved on its own. What i‌s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A man is referred to the STI clinic with the following painful finding. He gives a history of a small painless ulcer on his penis 5 weeks ago, which resolved on its own. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphogranuloma venerum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4ea42a2b4774afe94bd0d598caa22a3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphogranuloma venerum</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>bilateral painful masses</strong> of lymph nodes called <strong>buboes</strong>, that occurred in this patient following a painless genital ulcer. This is typically seen in <strong>lymphogranuloma venereum</strong>.  </p>\n<p>The incubation period of this condition is <strong>3-30 days</strong>. It occurs in three stages. </p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> stage: Begins with painless papules or ulcers at the site of inoculation, which may go unnoticed. The classical <strong>ulcer</strong> is <strong>single</strong>, shallow, <strong>non-indurated,</strong> and may be <strong>painful or painless</strong>. The common site is the coronal sulcus in men and the posterior vaginal wall in women. It heals without scarring. </p>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> stage: Develops <strong>2-6 weeks</strong> later and presents as <strong>tender</strong> femoral and/ or inguinal <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong>. The nodes may coalesce to form <strong>buboes</strong> or abscesses, which later rupture to form <strong>fistulae</strong> and <strong>sinus</strong> tracts. </p>\n<p><strong>Tertiary</strong> stage: Occurs several years later and is characterized by <strong>lymphatic obstruction</strong> and disfiguring conditions such as <strong>elephantiasis</strong> and <strong>esthiomene</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Chancroid </strong>presents with<strong> </strong>multiple <strong>painful</strong> <strong>ulcers</strong> with painful lymphadenopathy. The incubation period 3-10 days.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Syphilis </strong>presents with painless indurated ulcers with <strong>painless</strong> <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong><strong>. </strong>The incubation period is 9-90 days.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Granuloma inguinale </strong>presents as a painless beefy red ulcer with<strong> no lymphadenopathy. </strong>The<strong> </strong>incubation period is 8-80 days (average 50 days).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5139","text":"A woman brings her 50-year‌-old mother to a PHC with​ disfiguration of her gen‌italia as shown below. Sh‍e says that it has been p​resent for many years but‍ her mother had refused t‌o seek treatment. The PHC‍ medical officer decides ​to refer the lady to a ve​nereologist. What is the ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman brings her 50-year-old mother to a PHC with disfiguration of her genitalia as shown below. She says that it has been present for many years but her mother had refused to seek treatment. The PHC medical officer decides to refer the lady to a venereologist. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphogranuloma venerum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3bafe3c3d14b45e78bda47c888af4838.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93f359dfae694883a8c07775d84e97f5x1280x1491.2270803949225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphogranuloma venerum</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>esthiomene</strong>, a feature of <strong>lymphogranuloma</strong> <strong>venereum</strong>. </p>\n<p>In the tertiary stage of LGV, there is <strong>lymphatic obstruction</strong> due to <strong>chronic inflammation</strong> and <strong>fibrosis</strong>. This results in genital lymphoedema and inflammation which causes ulceration and chronic granulomatous fibrosis, resulting in esthiomene in women and <strong>elephantiasis</strong> in <strong>men</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2565","text":"A 50-year-old man was refe‌rred to a urologist for t​he following finding. Whi‌ch of the following would‍ not have caused this?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man was referred to a urologist for the following finding. Which of the following would not have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Donov‌anosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphogranul‌oma venereum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonococcal‌ urethritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penile tu‌berculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Donovanosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ed9878e6d084c89a1b5813147f74000.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Donovanosis</strong></p><p>The deformity shown in the above image depicts a <strong>penis</strong> that is <strong>twisted</strong> along its <strong>long axis</strong>, which is called <strong>saxophone penis</strong>. It is a late complication of<strong> lymphogranuloma venereum</strong>, <strong>penile tuberculosis,</strong> and <strong>primary</strong> <strong>lymphedema</strong>. It does not occur in donovanosis. </p>\n<p>It occurs as a complication of long-standing <strong>inflammation</strong>, which causes <strong>fibrosis</strong> of lymphatics and connective tissue. The dorsal surface of the penis has a <strong>poor blood supply</strong> compared to the ventral surface and undergoes more fibrosis. This leads to a <strong>dorsal bend</strong> of the penis. </p>\n<p><strong>Gonococcal urethritis</strong> can lead to periurethral abscesses, which result in urethral strictures. In males, this may cause a saxophone penis deformity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2579","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of the Amse​l criteria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of the Amsel criteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Homogenous thin greyish di‌scharge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vaginal pH l‌ess than 4.5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amine odour after adding 1‌0% KOH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelial cells studded w‌ith coccobacilli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vaginal pH less than 4.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c7288d7f2c6442cb3008adbb3c65698x1280x981.9054340155258.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vaginal pH less than 4.5</strong></p><p><strong>Acidic pH</strong> is not a feature of the <strong>Amsel criteria, </strong>which are used for the diagnosis of <strong>bacterial vaginosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3 out of 4</strong> of the following criteria are needed for a positive diagnosis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Homogeneous, thin, <strong>grayish-white discharge</strong> that smoothly coats the vaginal walls.</li>\n<li>Vaginal <strong>pH &gt; 4.5</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Positive whiff-amine test -</strong> Fishy odor when a drop of 10% KOH is added to vaginal discharge.</li>\n<li><strong>Clue cells</strong> on saline wet mount: Vaginal <strong>epithelial</strong> cells studded with adherent <strong>coccobacilli</strong> at the edge of the cell.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>Bacterial vaginosis is caused by a <strong>reduction</strong> in <strong>lactobacilli</strong>, and an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>anaerobic</strong> organisms such as <em>Gardnerella vaginalis, Mycoplasma hominis </em>and <em>Prevotella </em>species.</p>\n<p><strong>Nugent scoring system</strong> is highly <strong>sensitive</strong> and is considered the <strong>gold standard </strong>for bacterial vaginosis. It is calculated using <strong>Gram-stained smear </strong>findings and observing the number of lactobacilli and morphology of anaerobes. High scores of  7–10 indicate bacterial vaginosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HYOabZcf","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"19_anatomy_of_paranasal_sinuses","title":"19.Anatomy Of Paranasal Sinuses","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB5655","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a paranasal sinus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a paranasal sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrifor‌m sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pyriform sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/389ed0f91cc04cfcb3dca1448901e323x1279x1123.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pyriform sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Pyriform sinus (fossa)</strong> is a part of <strong>hypopharynx</strong>. It lies on either side of the larynx and extends from pharyngoepiglottic fold to the upper end of oesophagus.</p>\n<p><strong>Sinuses</strong> are <strong>air-containing cavities</strong> in certain bones of <strong>skull</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is divided into 2 groups.</p>\n<p>Anterior group includes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maxillary</li>\n<li>Frontal</li>\n<li>Anterior ethmoidal sinuses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Posterior group includes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior ethmoid</li>\n<li>Sphenoid sinuses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5665","text":"Which is the largest paran‌asal sinus?","raw_text":"Which is the largest paranasal sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi‌d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Maxillary sinus</strong> is the<strong> largest</strong> of paranasal sinuses and occupies the body of maxilla.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5656","text":"Which of the following is ‌also known as antrum of H​ighmore?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also known as antrum of Highmore?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sphenoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Maxillary sinus</strong> is also known as <strong>antrum of Highmore</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5657","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is correct about ​the maxillary sinus?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct about the maxillary sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyramidal in shape with ba‌se towards lateral wall o​f nose and apex towards z​ygomatic arch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pyramidal in shape with ba‌se towards zygomatic arch​ and apex towards lateral​ wall of nose.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triangula‌r in shape","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectangula‌r in shape","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyramidal in shape with base towards lateral wall of nose and apex towards zygomatic arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd820c9922424da3b9222306c4d8f5b7x1279x1123.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyramidal in shape with base towards lateral wall of nose and apex towards zygomatic arch</strong></p><p><strong>Maxillary sinus </strong>is<strong> pyramidal </strong>in shape, with base towards lateral wall of nose and apex directed laterally into the zygomatic process of maxilla and sometimes in the zygomatic bone itself.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5663","text":"A pathologist is examining‌ the specimen of a maxill​ectomy. Which of the foll​owing epithelium do you e​xpect to find lining the ​maxillary sinus?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining the specimen of a maxillectomy. Which of the following epithelium do you expect to find lining the maxillary sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratinising stratified sq‌uamous epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non keratinizing stratifie‌d squamous epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciliated columnar epitheli‌um","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Simple columnar epithelium‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ciliated columnar epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ciliated columnar epithelium</strong></p><p><strong>Paranasal sinuses</strong> are lined by <strong>ciliated columnar epithelium </strong>with<strong> goblet cells </strong>which secrete <strong>mucus. </strong>They're lined by the mucous membrane, which is continuous with that of the nasal cavity through the ostia of sinuses. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5658","text":"Which of the following is ‌most superior in location​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most superior in location?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi‌d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Frontal sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Frontal sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Frontal sinus</strong> is the<strong> most superiorly located sinus</strong>.<br/>It is situated between the inner and outer tables of frontal bone, above and deep to the supraorbital margin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5661","text":"An anxious father brings h‌is newborn baby to the pe​diatrician after dropping​ his baby when shifting f​rom one arm to another. T​he pediatrician orders a ‌CT head, which is normal.‌ Which of the following s​tructures would you not e​xpect to find in the CT?","raw_text":"An anxious father brings his newborn baby to the pediatrician after dropping his baby when shifting from one arm to another. The pediatrician orders a CT head, which is normal. Which of the following structures would you not expect to find in the CT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontal sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontal sinus</strong></p><p>The<strong> frontal sinus</strong> is <strong>absent or rudimentary</strong> at birth. Radiologically, the frontal sinus is able to be identified at <strong>6 years</strong> and reaches full size only after puberty. </p>\n<p>At birth, only the maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are present and are large enough to be clinically significant. Sphenoid sinus, though may be present, is usually undeveloped and non-aerated.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 225px;\" width=\"604\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\"><strong>Sinuses</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\"><strong>At birth</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\"><strong>First radiological evidence</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.438px;\"><strong>Reaches adult size by</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Maxillary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">4-5 months after birth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.438px;\">15 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Ethmoidal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">1 year</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.438px;\">12 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Frontal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Absent/rudimentary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">6 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.438px;\">Post-puberty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Sphenoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.391px;\">4 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.438px;\">14 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5634","text":"Where does the posterior e‌thmoidal sinus open?","raw_text":"Where does the posterior ethmoidal sinus open?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio‌r meatus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Middle‌ meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferio‌r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoethmo‌idal recess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior meatus</strong></p><p>The<strong> posterior ethmoidal sinus</strong> opens into the <strong>superior meatus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5660","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about sphenoid sinus​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about sphenoid sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Present in greater wing of‌ sphenoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lined by stratified squamo‌us epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opens into sphenoethmoidal‌ recess","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral wall is related to‌ the pituitary gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Opens into sphenoethmoidal recess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Opens into sphenoethmoidal recess</strong></p><p><strong>Sphenoid sinus</strong> opens into <strong>sphenoethmoidal recess.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5635","text":"You are an ENT resident pe‌rforming endoscopic biops​y from the sphenoid sinus​ in a COVID patient with ​suspected mucormycosis. T​hrough which of the follo‌wing areas will you acces‌s the sinus?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident performing endoscopic biopsy from the sphenoid sinus in a COVID patient with suspected mucormycosis. Through which of the following areas will you access the sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio‌r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle‌ meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferio‌r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoethmo‌idal recess","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sphenoethmoidal recess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sphenoethmoidal recess</strong></p><p>The <strong>sphenoid sinus</strong> is accessed through the <strong>sphenoethmoidal recess</strong> which is situated above the superior turbinate.</p>\n<p>The ostium of the sphenoid sinus can be located endoscopically 1cm above the upper margin of the posterior choana near the posterior border of the septum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6411","text":"Optic nerve injury followi‌ng sinus surgery (FESS) i​s due to removal of which​ of the following ethmoid​al cells?","raw_text":"Optic nerve injury following sinus surgery (FESS) is due to removal of which of the following ethmoidal cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hal‌ler","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Agger‌ nasi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onodi‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bullae‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Onodi cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a05f6853e6494fd8b47c16868d235d0ax472x360.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66e69d87c30a4debb95178fdc941e6d2x408x481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Onodi cells</strong></p><p><strong>Onodi cell</strong> is the posterior ethmoidal cell, related superiorly or laterally to the sphenoid sinus. The <strong>optic nerve lies in its lateral wall</strong> and can be injured while operating in the cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Haller</strong> <strong>cells</strong> are a type of ethmoidal cells that are situated on the roof of the maxillary sinus. Sometimes they may enlarge and block the opening of the maxillary sinus leading to maxillary sinusitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Agger nasi</strong> is an elevation just anterior to the attachment of middle turbinates.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Bulla ethmoidalis</strong> is the ethmoidal cell situated behind the uncinate process and forms the posterior boundary of hiatus semilunaris through which the maxillary, frontal and anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5639","text":"While performing functiona‌l endoscopic sinus surger​y, the surgeon points out​ the Haller cells and the​ir clinical significance.​ Which of the following i‌s correct about these cel‌ls?","raw_text":"While performing functional endoscopic sinus surgery,  the surgeon points out the Haller cells and their clinical significance. Which of the following is correct about these cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are related to the ma‌xillary sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They are related to the op‌tic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are related to the sp‌henoid sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are related to the fa‌cial nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. They are related to the maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/971a88b42f8c4546839c5fa4301fb279x377x379.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They are related to the maxillary sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Haller cells</strong> (infraorbital ethmoidal air cells) are a type of ethmoidal cells that are situated in the roof of the <strong>maxillary sinus</strong>. Sometimes they may enlarge and block the opening of the maxillary sinus leading to maxillary sinusitis.</p>\n<p>The star-marked area in the given CT scan shows the Haller cell </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0656","text":"What is occipitomental vie‌w with open mouth also kn​own as?","raw_text":"What is occipitomental view with open mouth also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water'‌s view","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Towne'‌s view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Law's‌ view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stenver‌'s view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Water's view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Water&#x27;s view</strong></p><p><strong>Occipitomental</strong> view is called <strong>Water's view</strong>. Water's view with an <strong>open mouth</strong> is known as <strong>Pierre's view</strong>. Among the given options, <strong>Water's view</strong> is the <strong>best answer</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Water's view, </strong>the patient’s head is positioned such that the nose and the chin touch the film while the X-rays are projected from backward. The mouth is usually kept open, which allows visualizing the sphenoid sinus. </p>\n<p>Structures seen in Water’s view are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maxillary sinuses (seen best)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Frontal sinuses</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sphenoid sinus</strong> (if the film is taken with an <strong>open<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>mouth</strong>)</li>\n<li>Zygoma</li>\n<li>Zygomatic arch</li>\n<li>Nasal bone</li>\n<li>Frontal process of the maxilla</li>\n<li>Superior orbital fissure</li>\n<li>Infratemporal fossa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Towne's view</strong> is an angled anteroposterior radiograph of the skull. Structures seen are the <strong>petrous part</strong> of the pyramids, <strong>arcuate eminence</strong>, <strong>superior semicircular</strong> canal, <strong>mastoid antrum</strong>,<strong> internal auditory canal</strong>, tympanic cavity, cochlea, and external auditory canal. This view is taken for acoustic neuroma and apical petrositis. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Law's view</strong> is a <strong>lateral oblique</strong> X-ray <strong>view of the mastoid</strong>. Structures seen are the external auditory canal, mastoid air cells, tegmen, lateral sinus plate, and temporomandibular joint. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Stenver's view</strong> is an X-ray view of the skull where the<strong> long axis of the petrous bone is parallel to the film</strong>. Structures seen are the entire petrous pyramid, arcuate eminence, internal auditory meatus, labyrinth with its vestibule, cochlea, and mastoid antrum. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"G1_V7gKQ","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"19_inorganic_irritants___metallic_and_non_metallic","title":"19.Inorganic Irritants   Metallic And Non Metallic","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB1062","text":"Which of the following poi​son can be found even in ‍skeletonized remains of t​he body?","raw_text":"Which of the following poison can be found even in skeletonized remains of the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ars​enic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mer​cury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thal​lium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Le​ad","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Arsenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Arsenic</strong></p><p><strong>Arsenic</strong> can be found even in <strong>skeletonized remains</strong> of the body. It <strong>delays putrefaction</strong> and can be found in bones, hair, and nails for several years.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">It becomes <strong>fixed</strong> in the cancellous tissues of the long <strong>bones</strong> by the <strong>conversion</strong> of <strong>phosphates</strong> to <strong>arsenates</strong>.<strong> </strong>The elimination of arsenic in this form is much slower and can be detected in the bones for several years after death.</p>\n<p><strong>Heavy metals</strong> such as <strong>lead</strong>, <strong>copper</strong>, <strong>mercury</strong>, including metalloids like <strong>arsenic </strong>and<strong> antimony</strong>, may be found in bones for a prolonged period in cases of <strong>chronic poisoning</strong>. They are also deposited in hair, nails, etc. For these reasons, when heavy metal poisoning is suspected, these specimens are collected in addition to the routine viscera and body fluids.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3989","text":"Which of the following is ​the most toxic form of ar‍senic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most toxic form of arsenic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aceto a​rsenate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ars​ine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arse​nite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arseni​c oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arsine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arsine</strong></p><p><strong>Arsine</strong> gas is the <strong>most toxic</strong> form of arsenic (acute exposure).</p>\n<p>The toxicity of arsenic compounds can vary greatly. In general, arsenic compounds can be ranked from highest to lowest toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inorganic trivalent</strong> (+3) compounds</li>\n<li>Organic trivalent (+3) compounds</li>\n<li>Inorganic pentavalent (+5) compounds</li>\n<li>Organic pentavalent (+5) compounds</li>\n<li><strong>Elemental arsenic</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inorganic</strong> arsenic is generally <strong>more toxic</strong> than organic arsenic. Forms of arsenic that are more rapidly absorbed are more toxic, while those most rapidly eliminated tend to be less toxic. Arsenite and arsenate forms are highly soluble in water.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1042","text":"A person presents to the e​mergency department with ‍suspected poisoning due t​o ingestion of arsenic-co​ntaining pesticides 6 hou​rs ago. Which of the foll​owing would you not expec‍t to find?","raw_text":"A person presents to the emergency department with suspected poisoning due to ingestion of arsenic-containing pesticides 6 hours ago. Which of the following would you not expect to find?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mees line​s on nails","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cholera like gastroenterit​is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Garlic odour of the breath​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute tubul​ar necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mees lines on nails","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f2c8fad32fa40dbb55d2faa6b616765x1158x990.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mees lines on nails</strong></p><p>The given scenario is indicative of <strong>acute arsenic poisoning</strong>. Mees lines are unlikely to be found in a patient with arsenic ingestion 6 hours ago. It is seen in chronic poisoning. </p>\n<p>Features of acute arsenic poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severe watery diarrhea</strong> (like cholera)</li>\n<li><strong>QTc prolongation</strong> and cardiac arrhythmias</li>\n<li>Acute<strong> encephalopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Renal injury with <strong>acute tubular necrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Garlic odor </strong>of breath and stools</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mees lines</strong> are horizontal 1 to 2 mm <strong>white lines</strong> on the nails, also called transverse leukonychia. They occur due to a disturbance in the nail matrix keratinization. It may be seen <strong>4 to 6 weeks</strong> after acute<strong> arsenic ingestion</strong>. These can also be seen in <strong>thallium poisoning.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows Mees lines.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1044","text":"A woman was slowly poisoni​ng her husband by adding ‍arsenic to his food daily​. Which of the following ​is unlikely to be seen in​ him?","raw_text":"A woman was slowly poisoning her husband by adding arsenic to his food daily. Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acro​dynia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperkeratosis of palms an​d soles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blackfoo​t disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowen ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acrodynia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2674f2e9124944efbf0af76f080a7e2cx320x261.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acrodynia</strong></p><p><strong>Acrodynia</strong> (pink disease)<strong> </strong>is a feature of<strong> mercury poisoning</strong>, not arsenic poisoning.</p>\n<p>Features of <strong>chronic arsenic exposure</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin lesions-\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation (<strong>raindrop</strong> pigmentation resembling measles) or hypopigmentation</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperkeratosis </strong>of<strong> palms </strong>and <strong>soles</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nail changes (Mees lines)</li>\n<li>Skin carcinomas and <strong>Bowen disease</strong></li>\n<li>Peripheral vascular disease leading to <strong>Blackfoot disease </strong>(gangrene)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Neurologic manifestations -\n<ul>\n<li>Polyneuropathy</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Painful paraesthesia of hands and feet</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cardiovascular - coronary heart disease and stroke</li>\n<li>Endocrine - Diabetes Mellitus type 2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Some people take arsenic daily as a tonic or as an aphrodisiac and they acquire tolerance to 250–300 mg or more in one dose. Such people are known as <strong>arsenophagists</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows hyperkeratosis of palms.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3988","text":"Arsenic poisoning can mimi​c all of the following ex‍cept?","raw_text":"Arsenic poisoning can mimic all of the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Addison'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guillain-Ba​rre syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes ​insipidus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Korsakoff'​s syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diabetes insipidus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diabetes insipidus</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic</strong> low-dose <strong>arsenic exposure</strong> can <strong>mimic diabetes mellitus </strong>type 2 and not diabetes insipidus.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of chronic arsenic poisoning can resemble the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Addison's disease </strong>- due to hyperpigmentation on chronic exposure</li>\n<li><strong>Guillain-Barré syndrome </strong>- sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy one to three weeks after the exposure</li>\n<li><strong>Korsakoff’s syndrome</strong> - chronic encephalopathy with delirium, hallucinations, disorientation, agitation, and confabulation can occur in chronic exposure</li>\n<li><strong>Cholera like gastroenteritis </strong>- occurs in acute poisoning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1048","text":"A 34-year-old man presente​d to the ER with severe d‍ehydration and weakness. ​History was suggestive of​ multiple episodes of wat​ery diarrhea and non-bili​ous vomiting since mornin‍g. His breath reeked of g​arlic odor. ECG showed QT​ prolongation. Which of t‍he following samples must​ be collected for a defin​itive diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man presented to the ER with severe dehydration and weakness. History was suggestive of multiple episodes of watery diarrhea and non-bilious vomiting since morning. His breath reeked of garlic odor. ECG showed QT prolongation.  Which of the following samples must be collected for a definitive diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 hour ur​ine sample","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blood ​sample","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hair ​sample","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fingerna​il sample","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 24 hour urine sample","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 24 hour urine sample</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points towards the diagnosis of <strong>acute arsenic poisoning. </strong>For a definitive diagnosis, the arsenic levels are measured in <strong>24-hour urine</strong> samples.</p>\n<p>If the baseline <strong>urinary</strong> level is within <strong>normal</strong> limits and arsenic intoxication is still suspected, <strong>hair</strong> and <strong>nail clippings</strong> should be harvested for laboratory analysis. Due to the rapid distribution of arsenic in tissues, <strong>blood arsenic </strong>levels are<strong> unreliable</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1049","text":"A 43-year-old man working ​in the mining industry pr‍esents with painful parae​sthesias of his hands and​ feet. Examination of the​ limbs reveals the follow​ing findings. Which of th‍e following is the drug o​f choice for his conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old man working in the mining industry presents with painful paraesthesias of his hands and feet. Examination of the limbs reveals the following findings. Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penici​llamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succ​imer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium di​sodium EDTA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dimercaptopropane sulfonat​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Succimer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/000182ad1e0d4063830a5f2411385033.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/236c68077d024b1b92d7e04d3ab28a03x1280x1150.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3a6fd2be94743f3af0f169e10683115x720x616.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Succimer</strong></p><p>The given images (<strong>raindrop pigmentation </strong>and<strong> Mees lines</strong>)<strong> </strong>along with the clinical features are suggestive of <strong>chronic arsenic poisoning. </strong>Chelation therapy for subacute or<strong> chronic</strong> severe <strong>arsenic</strong> toxicity is <strong>succimer</strong> (DMSA). It is an oral hydrophilic analog of BAL.</p>\n<p>For <strong>acute arsenic</strong> toxicity, the following can be used:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>BAL (British anti-Lewisite<em>)</em></li>\n<li>DMSA (Dimercaptosuccinic acid) </li>\n<li>DMPS (Dimercaptopropane-1-sulfonate) </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>BAL</strong> tends to remain the <strong>initial chelating</strong> drug for severe, <strong>acute arsenic</strong> toxicity. For other cases, DMSA or DMPS may be used. </p>\n<p>The images given below shows characteristic <strong>raindrop pigmentation </strong>and<strong> Mees lines, </strong>seen in chronic arsenic poisoning.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Arsenic is used industrially as an alloying agent and in the processing of glass, pigments, textiles, paper, metal adhesives, wood preservatives and ammunition. Arsenic is also used in the hide tanning process and, to a limited extent, in pesticides, feed additives, and pharmaceuticals.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1050","text":"A 23-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of tre‍mors and ataxia. History ​is significant for regula​r exotic seafood intake. ​On examination, the follo​wing finding is noted. Yo‍u are suspecting heavy me​tal poisoning. Which of t​he following explains the‍ mechanism of the suspect​ed poison?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman presents with complaints of tremors and ataxia. History is significant for regular exotic seafood intake. On examination, the following finding is noted. You are suspecting heavy metal poisoning. Which of the following explains the mechanism of the suspected poison?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binding to sulfhydryl grou​ps on enzymes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of acetylcholin​esterase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitation of glutamater​gic synapses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of GABA-ergic s​ynapses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Binding to sulfhydryl groups on enzymes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1b6c04e5c31a4350a2e09d528a0b386c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Binding to sulfhydryl groups on enzymes</strong></p><p>The given image <strong>(acrodynia)</strong> and the clinical features point towards a diagnosis of <strong>chronic organic mercury poisoning</strong> (methyl mercury). Mercury acts as a poison by binding to <strong>sulfhydryl</strong> groups on <strong>enzymes.</strong></p>\n<p>Mechanisms of toxicity of mercury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High-affinity binding of divalent<strong> mercury</strong> to sulfhydryl groups of <strong>proteins</strong> in the cells is an important mechanism for producing nonspecific cell injury or even cell death.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Both inorganic and methylmercury <strong>damage mitochondria</strong> and disrupt intracellular calcium homeostasis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Methylmercury also <strong>affects</strong> the <strong>MAPK</strong> signaling <strong>pathway</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Methylmercury has also been shown to <strong>disrupt microtubules</strong> in neurites</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Orofacial paresthesias</strong> is the <strong>initial</strong> common <strong>symptom</strong> of chronic mercury poisoning followed by headache, tremor, and fatigue. Other features include mild GI, renal, and pulmonary abnormalities.</p>\n<p>In severe cases, the following features are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ataxia</li>\n<li>Muscle rigidity and spasticity</li>\n<li>Blindness</li>\n<li>Hearing deficits</li>\n<li>Dementia (neurotoxicity)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1052","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of patients‍ with hydrargyrism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of patients with hydrargyrism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarse intentional tremors​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema of palms and sole​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shyness and​ memory loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowen's​ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bowen's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bowen&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrargyrism</strong> means <strong>mercury</strong> poisoning. <strong>Bowen's disease</strong> is associated with chronic low-level exposure to <strong>arsenic,</strong> not mercury.</p>\n<p>Features of chronic mercury poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tremors</strong> - most consistent manifestation. They are:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Danbury tremors</strong> - fine muscle fasciculations punctuated every few minutes by coarse shaking or jerky movements, intentional type. Progression of tremors is hands →lips→tongue→arms and legs.</li>\n<li><strong>Hatter's shakes</strong> - Severe impairment of daily activities involving delicate movements like shaving, writing, holding a tumbler or spoon.</li>\n<li><strong>Concussio mercurialis</strong> - most severe form, literally no activity is possible.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Erethism mercurialis</strong> - Shyness, memory loss, and change in personality</li>\n<li>Nephrotic syndrome and renal tubular anomalies</li>\n<li><strong>Acrodynia (pink disease)</strong> - Seen in small children and characterized by a generalized rash; edema and erythema of the palms, soles, and face. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1051","text":"A 33-year-old man is broug​ht to the emergency depar‍tment with a history of h​aving swallowed mercury f​rom a thermometer. Histor​y is suggestive of intell​ectual disability since c‍hildhood. What is your ne​xt step of action?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with a history of having swallowed mercury from a thermometer. History is suggestive of intellectual disability since childhood. What is your next step of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric lavage and activat​ed charcoal administratio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whole bowel​ irrigation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No treatment​ is required","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chelation with oral succim​er","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. No treatment is required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. No treatment is required</strong></p><p>The mercury found in a glass thermometer is <strong>elemental</strong> mercury. The <strong>absorption</strong> of elemental mercury by the GI tract is usually <strong>negligible, </strong>unless the mucosa is damaged. Hence, no treatment is required.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">For ingestion of <strong>inorganic </strong>and<strong> organic mercury </strong>salts, the following steps are followed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Aggressive</strong> IV <strong>hydration</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">GI decontamination, including <strong>gastric lavage</strong> </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Activated charcoal</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Dimercaprol</strong> and<strong> succimer</strong> are the preferred <strong>chelators</strong> for mercury poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4034","text":"A 45-year-old man has come​ with complaints of insom‍nia, memory loss, and oro​facial numbness. On exami​nation, the patient has c​oarse tremors in his hand​s and lips. History is su‍ggestive of an intake of ​bluefin tuna on a regular​ basis. You suspect an in‍toxication of a particula​r poison. Which of the fo​llowing will be an effect​ive treatment?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man has come with complaints of insomnia, memory loss, and orofacial numbness. On examination, the patient has coarse tremors in his hands and lips. History is suggestive of an intake of bluefin tuna on a regular basis. You suspect an intoxication of a particular poison. Which of the following will be an effective treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dimer​caprol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succ​imer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DM​PS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Support​ive care","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Supportive care","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Supportive care</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>encephalopathy</strong> and <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong> related to chronic <strong>mercury poisoning </strong>(Minamata disease). There is <strong>no</strong> effective <strong>treatment</strong> for patients with toxic <strong>chronic exposure</strong> to organic mercury, and hence only <strong>supportive care </strong>is provided</p>\n<p>This is most commonly associated with the consumption of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> mercury-rich fish</strong> (such as shark, swordfish, bonito, mackerel, marlin, and bluefin tuna).</p>\n<p>Symptoms are generally resistant to treatment with chelators. <strong>Dimercaprol (BAL) </strong>should<strong> not be used, </strong>because it <strong>increases</strong> the<strong> mobilization </strong>of<strong> mercury</strong> to the<strong> brain</strong>. Treatment with oral <strong>DMSA</strong> is <strong>unlikely</strong> to <strong>reverse</strong> the <strong>neurologic</strong> damage. Hence, there is no effective treatment for Minamata disease.</p>\n<p>For patients with toxic exposure to <strong>elemental</strong> mercury or <strong>inorganic mercury,</strong> the following are effective:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DMSA (succimer)</li>\n<li>DMPS</li>\n<li>BAL (dimercaprol)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For<strong> severe acute organic </strong>mercury poisoning, <strong>succimer</strong> is used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1059","text":"A 33-year-old patient is b​eing evaluated for longst‍anding anemia. On clinica​l examination, you notice​ the given finding. Which​ of the following metal p​oisoning is associated wi‍th this characteristic fi​nding?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old patient is being evaluated for longstanding anemia. On clinical examination, you notice the given finding. Which of the following metal poisoning is associated with this characteristic finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thal​lium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ars​enic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Le​ad","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bery​llium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lead","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0bd671a3cc524d59a27847e65eeff573.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/219b2049ea9c4f4f8f22fa7f092db149x720x458.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lead</strong></p><p>The image is showing characteristic <strong>Burtonian lines, </strong>which are seen in <strong>lead poisoning</strong> and mercury poisoning.</p>\n<p>These are <strong>bluish-grey gingival</strong> lead <strong>lines</strong> that occur with <strong>chronic</strong> exposure to <strong>lead</strong> poisoning.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Burtonian lines on the gums.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5290","text":"A 5-year-old girl is broug​ht by her mother with com‍plaints of abdominal pain​ and frequent tripping. S​he lives with her grandpa​rents in a seventy-year-o​ld house and has a histor‍y of eating paint from th​e wall. Which of the foll​owing enzyme defect is th‍e likely cause of her con​dition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl is brought by her mother with complaints of abdominal pain and frequent tripping. She lives with her grandparents in a seventy-year-old house and has a history of eating paint from the wall. Which of the following enzyme defect is the likely cause of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ALA sy​nthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ALA deh​ydratase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heme s​ynthase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coproporphyrinogen oxidase​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ALA dehydratase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5950be773ad14b8e8aad41cbf4d53ef5x600x597.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ALA dehydratase</strong></p><p>This given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>lead intoxication.</strong> The enzyme defective in this condition is <strong>ALA dehydratase</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of lead poisoning in children is seen due to lead paint which was used in the older times.</p>\n<p>It is toxic because it can <strong>substitute</strong> for <strong>calcium</strong> in many fundamental cellular processes. It<strong> </strong>crosses red blood cell membranes as well as the blood-brain barrier and enters the neuroglia cells which support brain function.</p>\n<p>The <strong>fatal dose</strong> of lead acetate is <strong>20 g.</strong> The <strong>fatal period </strong>is<strong> </strong><strong>1</strong>-<strong>2 days</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1060","text":"The peripheral smear of a ​patient with suspected he‍avy metal poisoning is as​ shown below. What is the​ mechanism responsible fo​r this?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient with suspected heavy metal poisoning is as shown below. What is the mechanism responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of ALA synthase​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of ferrochelata​se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of ALA dehydrat​ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of pyrimidine 5​' nucleotidase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2572fb14d0f44c289419a533bfa043a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of pyrimidine 5&#x27; nucleotidase</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>basophilic stippling</strong>, which occurs due to <strong>inhibition</strong> of<strong> pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Basophilic stippling in red blood cells occurs due to <strong>impaired clearing</strong> of cellular<strong> RNA degradation products </strong>(due to inhibition of pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This finding is seen in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lead toxicity</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Arsenic toxicity</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sideroblastic anemia</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Thalassemias</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1061","text":"A 53-year-old man working ​in the paint industry is ‍being evaluated for foot ​drop. Lead poisoning is s​uspected. Which of the fo​llowing is the key test t​o diagnose this condition‍?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old man working in the paint industry is being evaluated for foot drop. Lead poisoning is suspected. Which of the following is the key test to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24-hour ur​inary lead","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venous blood​ lead levels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serum zinc p​rotoporphyrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum free erythrocyte pro​toporphyrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Venous blood lead levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Venous blood lead levels</strong></p><p>The <strong>blood lead levels</strong> are the <strong>best</strong> single <strong>test</strong> for evaluating <strong>lead toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Levels at or &gt;5 micrograms/dL (0.24 micromol/L) are considered elevated in children.</p>\n<p>The edetate calcium disodium provocation test and testing for erythrocyte protoporphyrin (e.g., free erythrocyte protoporphyrin, zinc protoporphyrin) and urinary lead levels are no longer recommended. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4458","text":"Saturnine gout is seen in:​","raw_text":"Saturnine gout is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lead po​isoning","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cadmium ​poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beryllium​ poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mercury ​poisoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lead poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lead poisoning</strong></p><p><strong>Saturnine gout</strong> is seen in <strong>chronic lead</strong> intoxication or<strong> poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p>The features of saturnine gout are the same as primary gout but it affects the <strong>knee </strong>more than the first metatarsophalangeal joint and <strong>tophi rarely develop</strong>. Lead exposure should be suspected if a patient presents with the triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Saturnine gout</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Renal insufficiency (renal failure and interstitial fibrosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1063","text":"A patient presents to you ​with a history of anemia,‍ foot drop, and behaviora​l changes. On examination​, there are bluish-gray l​ines on the gum. The imag​e shows the radiograph of‍ the knee of the patient.​ Deposition of which subs​tance is responsible for ‍this finding in the X-ray​?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with a history of anemia, foot drop, and behavioral changes. On examination, there are bluish-gray lines on the gum. The image shows the radiograph of the knee of the patient. Deposition of which substance is responsible for this finding in the X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mer​cury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosp​horus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Le​ad","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cal​cium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Calcium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2632501907494ec398794e9a7499ae83.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Calcium</strong></p><p>The X-ray is suggestive of <strong>lead lines </strong>seen in<strong> </strong><strong>lead toxicity </strong>which are due to the <strong>deposition </strong>of<strong> calcium</strong> at the growth plates.</p>\n<p>The <strong>radiodensity</strong> of the lead line is due to persistent<strong> mineralized</strong> metaphyseal <strong>cartilage</strong> and <strong>not</strong> to a primary osseous change or <strong>lead</strong> itself. It is the result of a lead-induced inability of cartilage-resorbing cells to degrade the mineralized matrix. This impairs the resorption of metaphyseal cartilage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1058","text":"A patient with altered men​tal status, after a thoro‍ugh evaluation, is diagno​sed with lead toxicity-in​duced encephalopathy. Whi​ch of the following is no​t true regarding this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A patient with altered mental status, after a thorough evaluation, is diagnosed with lead toxicity-induced encephalopathy. Which of the following is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Worsened with the use of d​imercaprol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chelation therapy should b​e started based on clinic‍al suspicion itself","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most commonly seen in todd​lers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occurs at blood lead level​s &amp;gt; 100 mcg/dl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Worsened with the use of dimercaprol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Worsened with the use of dimercaprol</strong></p><p><strong>Dimercaprol</strong> (BAL) must be <strong>administered</strong> in <strong>lead toxicity-induced</strong> <strong>encephalopathy.</strong> </p>\n<p>Lead encephalopathy is suspected when a patient presents with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute onset of persistent vomiting</li>\n<li>Alteration of mental status <span class=\"nowrap\">and/or </span>seizures</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Emergency</strong> initiation of <strong>chelation therapy</strong> must be done if it is suspected clinically.</p>\n<p><strong>Encephalopathy</strong> due to <strong>lead</strong> poisoning typically occurs in<strong> toddlers</strong> age 15 to 30 months old, with blood <strong>lead</strong> levels<strong> &gt;100 microgram/dl</strong>. Children with lead encephalopathy should receive <strong>combined chelation</strong> therapy with<strong> dimercaprol</strong> and calcium disodium edetate (<strong>CaNa<sub>2</sub>EDTA</strong>).</p>\n<p>Note: Administration <strong>BAL worsens</strong> symptoms in <strong>methyl mercury poisoning,</strong> not in lead poisoning. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1056","text":"Metal fume fever is common​ly associated with which ‍of the following poisonin​g?","raw_text":"Metal fume fever is commonly associated with which of the following poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le​ad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ars​enic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zi​nc","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thal​lium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zinc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zinc</strong></p><p><span class=\"topic-highlight\">Metal fume fever is most commonly associated with <strong>zinc oxide. </strong></span></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>influenza-like</strong> syndrome with fever, myalgias, profuse sweating, and other symptoms that usually occur 3–10 hours after heavy exposure to a variety of metal oxides. It is also known as <strong>Smelter's shakes</strong> or<strong> metal shakes.</strong></p>\n<p>It can also be associated with <strong>copper </strong>and <strong>magnesium.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1053","text":"The ouch-ouch disease is d​ue to the toxicity of whi‍ch of the following?","raw_text":"The ouch-ouch disease is due to the toxicity of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thal​lium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cad​mium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chro​mium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zi​nc","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cadmium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cadmium</strong></p><p>Itai-Itai disease (Japan), also called <strong>ouch-ouch</strong> disease is due to bone pain caused by <strong>cadmium. </strong></p>\n<p>Cadmium causes <strong>nephrotoxicity</strong> leading to <strong>hypercalciuria.</strong> This subsequently causes <strong>osteomalacia</strong> leading to bone pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2292","text":"Luminescent stools are ass​ociated with which of the‍ following?","raw_text":"Luminescent stools are associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mer​cury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cad​mium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosp​horus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oxali​c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phosphorus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phosphorus</strong></p><p><strong>Luminescent stools</strong> are seen in <strong>acute</strong> <strong>phosphorus poisoning</strong><strong>.</strong> The vomitus and stools will be luminous in the dark.</p>\n<p>The stools may give rise to faint fumes, constituting the <strong>smoky stool syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6797","text":"Alkali disease of livestoc​k is caused by an excess ‍of which of the following​ minerals?","raw_text":"Alkali disease of livestock is caused by an excess of which of the following minerals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sele​nium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mang​anese","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molyb​denum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chro​mium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Selenium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Selenium</strong></p><p><strong>Excess</strong> of <strong>selenium</strong> causes <strong>alkali disease</strong> in <strong>livestock.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical types of selenium intoxication: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute selenosis</li>\n<li>Subacute selenosis (i.e., blind staggers type)</li>\n<li>Chronic selenosis (i.e., alkali disease type)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alkali disease occurs when animals consume moderate levels of selenium for a period of weeks to months.</p>\n<p>The usual <strong>clinical signs</strong> of chronic selenosis in horses, cattle, and swine are <strong>loss of hair</strong> (horses and cattle lose long hair from the mane and tails), <strong>emaciation, hoof lesions, weight reduction,</strong> and<strong> lameness.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0763","text":"Arrange the following in t​he correct order of sympt‍omatology seen in iron to​xicity: Pyloric stricture​ Asymptomatic phase Gastr​ointestinal bleeding Live​r failure and hypoglycemi‍a","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following in the correct order of symptomatology seen in iron toxicity:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pyloric stricture</li>\n<li>Asymptomatic phase</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal bleeding</li>\n<li>Liver failure and hypoglycemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 -&amp;gt; 3 -&amp;​gt; 1 -&amp;gt; 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 -&amp;gt; 3 -&amp;​gt; 4 -&amp;gt; 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 -&amp;gt; 2 -&amp;​gt; 4 -&amp;gt; 1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 -&amp;gt; 4 -&amp;​gt; 1 -&amp;gt; 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 -> 2 -> 4 -> 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 1</strong></p><p>The correct order of symptomatology in iron toxicity is 3 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 4 -&gt;1.</p>\n<p>Symptoms of iron toxicity are divided into <strong>4 stages</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stage 1</strong> (few hours after ingestion): vomiting, abdominal pain, <strong>gastrointestinal bleeding,</strong> shock, acidosis, and coma</li>\n<li><strong>Stage 2</strong> (6-24 hours): <strong>Asymptomatic</strong> phase</li>\n<li><strong>Stage 3</strong> (24-48 hours): Metabolic acidosis, jaundice, <strong>hypoglycemia,</strong> shock, coma with <strong>hepatic and renal failure.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stage 4</strong> (1-2 weeks): Late complications such as <strong>gastric stricture</strong> and <strong>pyloric stenosis.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Iron exerts its toxic effects by <strong>increasing capillary permeability</strong>, release of hydrogen ions, inhibition of mitochondrial function, and <strong>direct corrosive action</strong> on the gastric mucosa. Ingestion of<strong> 20 to</strong> <strong>30 grams</strong> is considered to be<strong> fatal</strong> and the fatal period is around <strong>24-30 hours</strong>. <strong>Desferrioxamine</strong> is the mainstay of treatment with supportive gastric lavage, administration of egg and milk to form iron-protein complexes, oral MgOH solution, and hemodialysis in severe cases. </p>\n<p><strong>Postmortem changes</strong> in iron toxicity include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemorrhagic necrosis of gastric mucosa with perforation of gastric or jejunal wall</li>\n<li>Acute hepatic necrosis</li>\n<li>Degenerative renal tubular changes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"b0ZW9cMF","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"19_hyperbilirubinemias_and_tests_of_liver_function","title":"19.Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC0785","text":"A 4-month-old child was br​ought with a history of r‍ecurrent episodes of jaun‌dice. Blood investigation‍s were suggestive of here​ditary spherocytosis. Whi​ch of the following liver​ function test findings i​s likely to be seen here?​","raw_text":"A 4-month-old child was brought with a history of recurrent episodes of jaundice. Blood investigations were suggestive of hereditary spherocytosis.  Which of the following liver function test findings is likely to be seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct bilirubin &amp;gt;50% o​f total bilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct bilirubin &amp;gt;85% o​f total bilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Direct bilirubin &amp;gt;15% o​f total bilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct bilirubin &amp;lt;15% o​f total bilirubin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Direct bilirubin <15% of total bilirubin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Direct bilirubin &lt;15% of total bilirubin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes <strong>recurrent</strong> <strong>hemolysis </strong>in a patient with hereditary spherocytosis leading to<strong> jaundice</strong>. In hemolytic conditions, only the unconjugated/ <strong>indirect bilirubin</strong> is<strong> raised</strong> and conjugated/<strong>direct bilirubin</strong> remains <strong>normal</strong> i.e. &lt;15% of total bilirubin.</p>\n<p><strong>Bilirubin</strong> is synthesized from heme which is <strong>released</strong> primarily from the <strong>breakdown of old RBCs</strong>. This bilirubin is in its <strong>unconjugated </strong>form.<strong> </strong>It enters the circulation and reaches the<strong> liver</strong>. It is converted to <strong>conjugated bilirubin</strong> which is then secreted into the <strong>biliary tree</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in a healthy person, <strong>most </strong>of the<strong> circulating bilirubin</strong> is unconjugated/<strong>indirect bilirubin</strong>. Hence, the normal liver function tests show indirect bilirubin <strong>&gt;85%</strong> and direct/conjugated bilirubin &lt;15% of the total bilirubin. The normal bilirubin values are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total bilirubin levels - 1 to 1.5 mg/dL</li>\n<li>Direct bilirubin - &lt;0.3 mg/dL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0801","text":"A 2-week-old baby presente​d with yellowish discolor‍ation of the skin. Invest‌igations revealed markedl‍y elevated direct bilirub​in levels. Which of the f​ollowing conditions shoul​d be suspected in the new​born?","raw_text":"A 2-week-old baby presented with yellowish discoloration of the skin. Investigations revealed markedly elevated direct bilirubin levels. Which of the following conditions should be suspected in the newborn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biliary​ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Physiologic​al jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast mil​k jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemolytic disease of the n​ewborn","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biliary atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biliary atresia</strong></p><p>The <strong>raised direct bilirubin levels</strong> indicate <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. Among the given options, <strong>biliary atresia</strong> causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Biliary atresia</strong> is a disorder characterised by <strong>destruction</strong> or <strong>absence </strong>of<strong> bile ducts</strong> in a newborn. It leads to obstruction to the flow of bile thereby causing leaking of conjugated bilirubin into the circulation.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Physiological jaundice</strong> of the newborn occurs due to <strong>immaturity</strong> of the newborn's<strong> liver.</strong>  It is not able to conjugate the excess bilirubin produced after birth. It leads to <strong>unconjugated</strong> hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Breast milk</strong> may contain certain <strong>factors </strong>that<strong> inhibit </strong>the<strong> conjugation</strong> of bilirubin leading to <strong>unconjugated</strong> hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hemolytic disease of the newborn</strong> causes excess production of bilirubin due to<strong> hemolysis</strong> leading to <strong>unconjugated</strong> hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7407","text":"Investigations performed i​n a patient with recurren‍t jaundice gave the follo‌wing results. What is the‍ possible cause of jaundi​ce in this patient? Total​ bilirubin - 4 mg/dL Urin​e bilirubin +","raw_text":"<p>Investigations performed in a patient with recurrent jaundice gave the following results. What is the possible cause of jaundice in this patient?</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin - 4 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Urine bilirubin +</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolyti​c anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gilbert ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Criggler-Naj​jar syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dubin Johns​on syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dubin Johnson syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dubin Johnson syndrome</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>urinary bilirubin</strong> and increased total bilirubin indicates <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. Among the given options, <strong>Dubin Johnson syndrome</strong> causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>Only <strong>conjugated bilirubin</strong> can appear in the urine because it is <strong>water-soluble</strong> and filterable by the kidney. <strong>Unconjugated bilirubin</strong> is water-insoluble and <strong>bound to albumin</strong>, hence it cannot be filtered by the kidney.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>unconjugated</strong> <strong>hyperbilirubinemia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased bilirubin production-</li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hemolytic anemias </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hematomas</li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Impaired bilirubin uptake and conjugation-</li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gilbert syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Crigler Najjar syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drugs - Probenecid, Rifampin</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> <strong>of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hereditary cholestatic syndromes</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dubin Johnson syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rotor syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Biliary obstruction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7433","text":"Which of the following is ​a cause of unconjugated h‍yperbilirubinemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Probe​necid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxicillin​-clavulanate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute h​epatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stone in the common bile d​uct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Probenecid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Probenecid</strong></p><p><strong>Probenecid</strong> causes <strong>impaired hepatic uptake</strong> of bilirubin<strong> </strong>which leads to<strong> unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Amoxicillin-clavulanate, acute hepatitis and stone in the common bile duct are causes that lead to mixed hyperbilirubinemia (both conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin elevated) or predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemias.</p>\n<p>Levels of conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin are useful in determining pre-hepatic causes of jaundice but are not useful in differentiating between intrahepatic and post-hepatic causes of jaundice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0802","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen​ts with jaundice. She had‍ been on OCPs for the pas‌t month. Her LFT and live‍r biopsy findings are giv​en below. What is the pro​bable diagnosis? Total bi​lirubin - 12 mg/dL Direct​ bilirubin - 10 mg/dL AST​ - 28 IU/L ALT - 30 IU/L ​GGT - 20 IU/L","raw_text":"<p>A 24-year-old woman presents with jaundice. She had been on OCPs for the past month. Her LFT and liver biopsy findings are given below. What is the probable diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin - 12 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Direct bilirubin - 10 mg/dL</p>\n<p>AST - 28 IU/L</p>\n<p>ALT - 30 IU/L</p>\n<p>GGT - 20 IU/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gilbert ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crigler- Naj​jar syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rotor s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dubin Johns​on syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dubin Johnson syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c71b91e8a4c54685a18d7692c7228b17.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dubin Johnson syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Dubin Johnson syndrome </strong>(DJS). The LFT describes <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong> with normal liver enzyme levels. The given image of microscopic examination of liver <strong>biopsy </strong>shows an accumulation of <strong>dark melanin-like pigment</strong> in the hepatocytes which is seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>DJS is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition causing <strong>defective expression </strong>of<strong> MRP 2 protein</strong> which is important for the biliary excretion of conjugated bilirubin. The patient has icterus but is otherwise asymptomatic.</p>\n<p>DJS also impairs biliary excretion of various compounds like <strong>epinephrine metabolites</strong>. The <strong>dark melanin-like pigment</strong> is derived from these metabolites causing the liver to appear black.</p>\n<p><strong>Rotor syndrome</strong> is also an autosomal recessive condition causing <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong> but it does not present with pigmentation of the liver. </p>\n<p><strong>Gilbert</strong> and <strong>Crigler-Najjar</strong> syndromes are characterized by <strong>unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7403","text":"What is the defect seen in​ patients with Rotor synd‍rome?","raw_text":"What is the defect seen in patients with Rotor syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absent UDP glucuronosyl tr​ansferase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutation in organic anion ​transport protein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mutation in multidrug resi​stance–associated protein‍ 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced UDP glucuronosyl t​ransferase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mutation in organic anion transport protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mutation in organic anion transport protein</strong></p><p><strong>Rotor syndrome </strong>occurs due to<strong> mutation in OATP </strong>(organic anion transport protein)<strong> 1B1 </strong>and <strong>1B3.</strong></p>\n<p>Rotor syndrome is an inherited <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>disorder with <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. It is a benign disorder with mild elevation of bilirubin levels. It is similar to Dubin Johnson syndrome but the liver does not show any black pigmentation. <strong>No treatment</strong> is <strong>required</strong> for these patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7418","text":"While evaluating a patient​ with jaundice, all of th‍e following investigation‌s would help you to diffe‍rentiate between Dubin Jo​hnson syndrome and Rotor ​syndrome except:","raw_text":"While evaluating a patient with jaundice, all of the following investigations would help you to differentiate between Dubin Johnson syndrome and Rotor syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral chole​cystography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total urinary coproporphyr​in","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum bilir​ubin levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bromsulphalein clearance t​est","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum bilirubin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum bilirubin levels</strong></p><p>Serum bilirubin levels are not helpful in differentiating between <strong>Dubin Johnson</strong> syndrome (DJS) and <strong>Rotor's</strong> syndrome as both these conditions present with <strong>mild elevation</strong> of <strong>serum bilirubin</strong>.</p>\n<p>These two conditions can be differentiated as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 172px;\" width=\"904\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 303.156px;\"><strong>Test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.359px;\"><strong>Rotor Syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.484px;\"><strong>Dubin Johnson syndrome</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 303.156px;\">Oral cholecystography</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.359px;\">Gall bladder is visualized</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.484px;\">\n<p>Gall bladder not visualised </p>\n<p>Impaired ability to concentrate the contrast material into bile</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 303.156px;\">Total urinary coproporphyrin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.359px;\">Elevated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.484px;\">\n<p>Normal </p>\n<p>Increased excretion of coproporphyrin 1 and decreased excretion of coproporphyrin 3</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 303.156px;\">Bromsulphalein (BSP) clearance test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.359px;\">\n<p>Delayed clearance of BSP</p>\n<p>No reflux</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.484px;\">\n<p>Rise in plasma levels of BSP after 90 minutes of injection</p>\n<p>Reflux of BSP from hepatocytes </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7411","text":"In a patient with unconjug​ated hyperbilirubinemia, ‍which feature helps confi‌rm the diagnosis of Crigl‍er- Najjar syndrome?","raw_text":"In a patient with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which feature helps confirm the diagnosis of Crigler- Najjar syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of​ kernicterus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of​ hemolysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"UGT1A1 ​mutation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal recessive inheri​tance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Presence of kernicterus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Presence of kernicterus</strong></p><p><strong>Kernicterus</strong> is seen in<strong> Crigler-Najjar syndrome</strong> but not in Gilbert syndrome. Gilbert syndrome does not cause a significant elevation in unconjugated bilirubin to cause kernicterus.</p>\n<p><strong>Crigler-Najjar</strong> syndrome and <strong>Gilbert</strong> syndrome are both caused by various<strong> mutations </strong>in the <strong>UGT1A1 gene</strong>. This leads to <strong>decreased activity</strong> of bilirubin <strong>UDP-glucuronosyltransferase</strong> enzyme leading to <strong>unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. Both are predominantly inherited in an <strong>autosomal</strong> <strong>recessive</strong> pattern. </p>\n<p>These syndromes present with <strong>jaundice </strong>in<strong> infants</strong>. The unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in these syndromes is not associated with any hemolysis. All the other liver function tests are usually normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Crigler Najjar</strong> syndrome may lead to kernicterus causing severe brain damage. <strong>Liver transplantation</strong> is required without which the infant may not survive beyond 1-2 years of age. Whereas, <strong>Gilbert</strong> syndrome is a benign <strong>asymptomatic</strong> condition that does not need any intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7412","text":"An infant presented with y​ellow discolouration of t‍he skin, poor feeding, an‌d seizures. Investigation‍s revealed total bilirubi​n- 40 mg/dL and unconjuga​ted bilirubin- 34 mg/dL. ​Phenobarbitone was admini​stered while blood was be​ing arranged for an excha​nge transfusion. However,​ there was no improvement‍. What is the most probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"An infant presented with yellow discolouration of the skin, poor feeding, and seizures. Investigations revealed total bilirubin- 40 mg/dL and unconjugated bilirubin- 34 mg/dL. Phenobarbitone was administered while blood was being arranged for an exchange transfusion. However, there was no improvement. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gilbert ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crigler-Najjar syndrome ty​pe 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Crigler-Najjar syndrome ty​pe 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rotor s​yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crigler-Najjar syndrome type 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crigler-Najjar syndrome type 1</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with poor feeding and seizures is indicative of <strong>kernicterus. </strong>This accompanied with <strong>unconjugated hyperbilrubinemia</strong> is highly suggestive of <strong>Crigler-Najjar syndrome type 1</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>No improvement</strong> in bilirubin levels<strong> following</strong> the administration of <strong>phenobarbital</strong> further supports the diagnosis. </p>\n<p><strong>Crigler-Najjar syndrome</strong> is of two types with<strong> type 1</strong> having <strong>absent/trace enzyme activity</strong> and <strong>type 2</strong> having<strong> &lt;10% of the normal enzyme activity</strong>. Hence, type 1 is far more severe and leads to kernicterus and neurological damage. Type 2 may also lead to kernicterus occasionally. </p>\n<p><strong>Kernicterus</strong> occurs because the <strong>unconjugated bilirubin</strong> is <strong>lipid-soluble</strong> and hence <strong>crosses the blood-brain barrier</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Phenobarbital</strong> is an <strong>enzyme inducer</strong>. Its administration can be used to differentiate between type 1 and type 2 Crigler-Najjar.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enzyme induction by phenobarbital leads to <strong>improved conjugation</strong> of bilirubin leading to a reduction in total bilirubin level <strong>in type 2 Crigler-Najjar</strong> syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>No improvement</strong> is seen <strong>in type 1</strong> as the enzyme activity is absent and therefore cannot be induced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inherited unconjugated hyperbilirubinemias (Autosomal Recessive) include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 245px; width: 421px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 83px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 83px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 83px;\">\n<p>Crigler-Najjar type  I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 83px;\">\n<p>Crigler-Najjar type II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; height: 83px;\">Gilbert Syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 36px;\">UDP-GT Activity </td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 36px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 36px;\">Markedly reduced</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; height: 36px;\">Reduced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 36px;\">Serum bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 36px;\">20 - 45 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 36px;\">&lt;20 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; height: 36px;\">Generally &lt;3 mg/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 72px;\">Pheno-barbital effect on serum bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 72px;\">No effect</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 72px;\">Reduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; height: 72px;\">Reduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 72px;\">Outcome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 72px;\">Kernicterus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 72px;\">Usually benign, kernicterus is rare </td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; height: 72px;\">Benign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 18px;\">Prevalence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 18px;\">Rare </td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 18px;\">Rare</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; height: 18px;\"> Common</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Rotor syndrome causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>The normal serum bilirubin (between 1 and 1.5 mg/dL) present in serum represents a balance between input from the production of bilirubin and hepatic/biliary removal of the pigment.</p>\n<p>Isolated elevation of the bilirubin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia- &gt;15% conjugated bilirubin</li>\n<li>Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia- &lt;15% conjugated bilirubin</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7437","text":"An 8-year-old child presen​ts with jaundice and easy‍ fatiguability. He had a ‌history of ear infection ‍which was treated with am​oxicillin-clavulanic acid​. Investigations done are​ shown below. What is the​ most likely diagnosis? H​emoglobin - 8 g/dL Total ​bilirubin level - 4.5 mg/​dl Direct bilirubin - 0.8‍ mg/dl ALT - 45 IU/L AST ​- 42 IU/L Other liver fun​ction tests - normal Ultr​asound shows the presence‍ of gall stones","raw_text":"<p>An 8-year-old child presents with jaundice and easy fatiguability. He had a history of ear infection which was treated with amoxicillin-clavulanic acid. Investigations done are shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 8 g/dL</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin level - 4.5 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Direct bilirubin - 0.8 mg/dl</p>\n<p>ALT - 45 IU/L</p>\n<p>AST - 42 IU/L</p>\n<p>Other liver function tests - normal</p>\n<p>Ultrasound shows the presence of gall stones</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug induce​d hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic​ jaundice","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obstructiv​e jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gilbert ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemolytic jaundice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemolytic jaundice</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemolytic jaundice</strong>. It can present with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low hemoglobin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong></li>\n<li>Pigment <strong>gall stones</strong> - excess bilirubin in bile </li>\n<li>Development of cholestasis when the gall stones obstruct the flow of bile. This would result in the elevation of conjugated bilirubin and LFTs show a cholestatic picture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hepatic and obstructive causes of jaundice are not likely as the other liver function tests are normal. The various causes of jaundice can be differentiated as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 651px; height: 313px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\"> <strong>Jaundice</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.156px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Pre hepatic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.359px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hepatic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.797px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Post hepatic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\"><strong>Cause</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.156px;\">\n<p>Excessive amount of bilirubin is presented to the liver</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.359px;\">\n<p>Impaired cellular uptake/ defective conjugation/ defective secretion of bilirubin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.797px;\">Defective excretion due to mechanical obstruction to the flow of bile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\"><strong>LFT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.156px;\">Elevated unconjugated bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.359px;\">\n<p>Elevated conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin</p>\n<p>Marked elevated levels of AST and ALT (&gt;1000U/L)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.797px;\">\n<p>Elevated conjugated bilirubin</p>\n<p>Elevated levels of ALP and GGT</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.688px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.156px;\">Hemolytic anaemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.359px;\">Hepatitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.797px;\">Choledocholithiasis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Inherited hyperbilirubinemias like <strong>Gilbert syndrome</strong> are suspected when there is <strong>no associated hemolysis</strong>. It is unlikely in this patient because there are clear hemolytic features.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7431","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is associated wit‍h increased activity of 5‌’- nucleotidase?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with increased activity of 5’- nucleotidase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone d​iseases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostat​e cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic re​nal failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholestati​c disorders","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cholestatic disorders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cholestatic disorders</strong></p><p><strong>5ʹ-nucleotidase</strong> is usually <strong>elevated</strong> in <strong>cholestasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Enzymes elevated in cholestasis are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)</li>\n<li>5ʹ-nucleotidase</li>\n<li>γ-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0799","text":"Which among the following ​is the most specific indi‍cator of liver cell injur‌y?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most specific indicator of liver cell injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alkaline p​hosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gamma glutamyl transpeptid​ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alanine amin​otransferase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aspartate aminotransferase​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alanine aminotransferase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alanine aminotransferase</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> alanine aminotransferase</strong> is the <strong>most specific</strong> indicator of liver cell injury.</p>\n<p>Alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) are indicators of liver cell injury. This is because ALT is found primarily in the liver, whereas, AST is found in the liver, cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle, kidneys, brain, pancreas, etc.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>GGT</strong> and <strong>ALP</strong> are enzymes that primarily reflect <strong>cholestasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7427","text":"In a patient with acute vi​ral hepatitis, what level‍ of serum transaminases w‌ill you expect?","raw_text":"In a patient with acute viral hepatitis, what level of serum transaminases will you expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10-40​ IU/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;50​0 IU/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40-30​0 IU/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;10​00 IU/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. >1000 IU/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt;1000 IU/L</strong></p><p>In a patient with <strong>acute viral hepatitis</strong>, serum<strong> transaminases</strong> are elevated to <strong>&gt;1000 IU/L</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> levels of serum transaminases are <strong>10-40 IU/L</strong>. Marked elevation of serum transaminases &gt;1000 IU/L is seen in conditions with extensive hepatocellular injury. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Viral hepatitis</li>\n<li>Ischemic injury to the liver in conditions like acute heart failure</li>\n<li>Toxin/drug-induced liver injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Minor elevations of serum transaminase levels up to 300 IU/L are non-specific and can be found in any liver disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0803","text":"A 64-year-old man with jau​ndice and abdominal pain ‍was found to have an ALP ‌level of 200 mg/dL. His L‍FT reports from a month a​go had shown an ALP level​ of 50 mg/dL. What is you​r inference?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man with jaundice and abdominal pain was found to have an ALP level of 200 mg/dL. His LFT reports from a month ago had shown an ALP level of 50 mg/dL. What is your inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal find​ing for age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Could be due to fatty meal​ intake","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indicative ​of hemolysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suggestive of cholestatic ​liver disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Suggestive of cholestatic liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Suggestive of cholestatic liver disease</strong></p><p>A <strong>4 fold rise </strong>of<strong> serum ALP</strong> (alkaline phosphatase) is suggestive of <strong>cholestatic liver disease</strong>. However, serum ALP levels cannot differentiate between extrahepatic and intrahepatic cholestasis. </p>\n<p>The <strong>normal range</strong> for serum ALP is considered <strong>40-125 IU/L</strong>. Serum alkaline phosphatase is found in the liver, bone, small intestines, and placenta. Hence, a <strong>mild increase</strong> in ALP, i.e., 1 - 1.5 times normal, can be seen in these conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Following a<strong> fatty meal intake </strong>- influx of intestinal alkaline phosphatase into the blood. Hence, the patient is advised to fast before the blood sample is collected for the test.</li>\n<li>In <strong>pregnancy</strong> - placental ALP</li>\n<li>In <strong>children</strong> - rapid bone growth</li>\n<li>In elderly people <strong>over 60 years</strong> of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ALP can be raised even in conditions like metastatic bone disease, inflammatory bowel disease, Hodgkin's lymphoma, etc. Hence, it needs to be correlated with raised levels of <strong>gamma-glutamyltransferase</strong> (GGT) and <strong>5' nucleotidase</strong> which are <strong>more specific for liver</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0798","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding liver e‍nzymes is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding liver enzymes is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ALT is raised to &amp;gt;1000 ​IU/L in cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absolute levels of aminotr​ansferases correlate with‍ the prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AST:ALT ratio &amp;lt;1 is see​n in nonalcoholic fatty l‍iver disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AST:ALT ratio &amp;gt;1 is see​n in chronic viral hepati‍tis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. AST:ALT ratio <1 is seen in nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AST:ALT ratio &lt;1 is seen in nonalcoholic fatty liver disease</strong></p><p><strong>AST:ALT ratio &lt;1</strong> is seen in <strong>NAFLD</strong> (nonalcoholic fatty liver disease).</p>\n<p>The AST:ALT ratio can be interpreted as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 345px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 71px;\"><strong>AST:ALT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 258px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">&lt;1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258px;\">Chronic viral hepatitis, Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">&gt;1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258px;\">Cirrhosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">&gt;2:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258px;\">\n<p>Suggestive of alcoholic liver disease</p>\n<p>Alcohol intake is associated with the deficiency of pyridoxal phosphate which in turn leads to decreased levels of ALT enzyme. So, AST&gt;ALT.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">&gt;3:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258px;\">Highly suggestive of alcoholic liver disease.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option A: Patients with <strong>cirrhosis</strong> have only <strong>mildly elevated</strong> or even normal levels of serum <strong>transaminases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Absolute levels</strong> of <strong>aminotransferases</strong> are not used for predicting prognosis. This is because there is a <strong>poor correlation</strong> between the <strong>degree of liver cell damage</strong> and the levels of aminotransferases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7421","text":"Liver function tests of a ​patient show isolated ele‍vation of gamma-glutamylt‌ransferase with other liv‍er enzymes within normal ​limits. All of the follow​ing drugs could be respon​sible for this finding ex​cept:","raw_text":"Liver function tests of a patient show isolated elevation of gamma-glutamyltransferase with other liver enzymes within normal limits. All of the following drugs could be responsible for this finding except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbit​urates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Penic​illin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Penicillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Penicillin</strong></p><p>Among the given options, penicillin does not lead to elevated gamma-glutamyltransferase (GGT) levels. </p>\n<p><strong>GGT</strong> is a <strong>microsomal enzyme</strong>. Hence, drugs that are microsomal <strong>enzyme</strong> <strong>inducers</strong> can lead to an <strong>increase</strong> in GGT levels. However, other liver enzyme levels will be normal. Therefore, an isolated rise in GGT level does not indicate cholestatic liver disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs that increase levels of GGT</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Barbiturates</strong></li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Ethanol</li>\n<li><strong>Griseofulvin</strong></li>\n<li>Rifampicin</li>\n<li><strong>Phenytoin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0805","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding prothro‍mbin time is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding prothrombin time is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indicates prognosis in acu​te liver disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indicates prognosis in chr​onic liver disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Measures factors II, V, VI​II, X","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Measures factors II, V, VI​I, X","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Measures factors II, V, VIII, X","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Measures factors II, V, VIII, X</strong></p><p><strong>Prothrombin time </strong>(PT) can help in estimating the levels of <strong>factors II, V, VII, and X</strong>.</p>\n<p>PT evaluates the <strong>extrinsic</strong> and the <strong>common</strong> <strong>pathway</strong> of coagulation. Factor VII is required in the extrinsic pathway and factors II, V, X are required in the common pathway.</p>\n<p><strong>Coagulation factors</strong> are <strong>produced</strong> <strong>by</strong> the <strong>liver</strong> (except factor VIII) and they have a short half-life. Hence, they <strong>indicate</strong> the <strong>synthetic function</strong> of the liver and are very useful in assessing acute hepatic dysfunction. Factor VIII is produced by vascular endothelial cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Prolonged</strong> <strong>PT</strong> that is not corrected by vitamin K administration indicates <strong>poor prognosis</strong> in both acute and chronic liver disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7429","text":"Which among the following ​is a marker of chronic li‍ver disease?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a marker of chronic liver disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proth​rombin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alb​umin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5'- nucl​eosidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Albumin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Albumin</strong></p><p><strong>Serum albumin</strong> is a marker of <strong>chronic liver disease</strong> because it is synthesized by the hepatocytes and has a <strong>long half-life</strong> of 18–20 days. Serum albumin is not a good indicator of acute or mild hepatic dysfunction.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypoalbuminemia</strong> is more common in chronic liver disorders such as cirrhosis and usually reflects <strong>severe liver damage</strong>. In hepatitis, albumin levels <strong>&lt;3 g/dL</strong> should raise the possibility of chronic liver disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> is used to assess <strong>acute</strong> parenchymal liver disease. <strong>5' nucleosidase</strong> is elevated in <strong>cholestasis</strong>. Elevation of <strong>ALP</strong> (&lt;3 fold) is <strong>not specific</strong> to liver diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0800","text":"Which of the following can​ be seen in a patient wit‍h chronic liver disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be seen in a patient with chronic liver disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypergammag​lobulinemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypogammag​lobulinemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rise in beta globulin leve​ls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rise in alpha globulin lev​els","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypergammaglobulinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypergammaglobulinemia</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic liver disease</strong> is associated with <strong>hypergammaglobulinemia</strong>. Chronic liver diseases with hypergammaglobulinemia include autoimmune hepatitis, primary biliary cirrhosis, and alcoholic liver disease.</p>\n<p>The liver produces all globulins except gamma globulin<strong> </strong>which are produced by B cells. In <strong>chronic liver diseases</strong> like liver cirrhosis, there is <strong>impaired</strong> <strong>reticuloendothelial</strong> <strong>function</strong> in hepatic sinusoids. Hence the liver fails to clear the bacterial antigens. This causes an increased production of gamma globulin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7424","text":"Which of the following is ​not a marker for liver fi‍brosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a marker for liver fibrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Procollagen peptide III (P​IIINP)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaluro​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue inhibitor of metall​oproteinase 1(TIMP1)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Telop​eptide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Telopeptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Telopeptide</strong></p><p><strong>Telopeptide</strong> is not a marker for liver fibrosis. It is a marker for <strong>bone resorption</strong>.</p>\n<p>Various <strong>non-invasive markers to detect hepatic fibrosis</strong> are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px; width: 374px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Non-invasive test for fibrosis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 231px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Components tested</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\"> Fibrotest/FibroSure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>α2-macroglobulin</li>\n<li>Haptoglobin</li>\n<li>Apolipoprotein A-1</li>\n<li>GGT</li>\n<li>Total bilirubin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">Enhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) assay</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hyaluronic acid</li>\n<li><strong>Procollagen peptide III</strong> (PIIINP)</li>\n<li><strong>Tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase</strong> (TIMP)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">Transient elastography (FibroScan)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ultrasound waves are used to measure liver stiffness</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">Fibrospect II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyaluronic acid</strong></li>\n<li>Alpha 2 macroglobulin</li>\n<li>Tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase 1 (TIMP 1)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7434","text":"In which of the following ​patients planned for a li‍ver biopsy, would you not‌ be required to use the t‍ransjugular approach?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients planned for a liver biopsy, would you not be required to use the transjugular approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with prolonged I​NR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with significant​ ascites","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with splenomegal​y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A morbidly o​bese patient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A patient with splenomegaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A patient with splenomegaly</strong></p><p><strong>Liver biopsy</strong> can be performed in a patient with <strong>splenomegaly</strong> via the <strong>percutaneous approach</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the percutaneous approach is not advisable, liver biopsy can be performed via the transjugular approach.<strong> </strong>In this approach,<strong> </strong>a cannula is inserted via the jugular vein which is then passed into hepatic veins through which the biopsy sample is taken. Therefore, any bleeding resulting from the procedure will drain into the hepatic veins.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications of transjugular liver biopsy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Percutaneous approach contraindicated\n<ul>\n<li>Significant <strong>ascites</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Prolonged INR</strong></li>\n<li>Coagulopathies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Difficulty in accessing the liver via the percutaneous route\n<ul>\n<li>Morbid <strong>obesity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9zCEfznM","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"19_rickettsia__chlamydia_and_mycoplasma","title":"19.Rickettsia, Chlamydia And Mycoplasma","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2841","text":"Transovarial transmission ​is a feature of which of ‍the following diseases?","raw_text":"Transovarial transmission is a feature of which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scrub ​typhus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epidemi​c typhus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endemic​ typhus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trench​ fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scrub typhus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3844f37842414128b27f1a7af8f7fc04x685x491.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scrub typhus</strong></p><p><strong>Transovarial transmission</strong> is a feature of <em><strong>Orientia tsutsugamushi</strong> </em>which causes <strong>scrub typhus.</strong></p>\n<p>Transovarial transmission refers to passing on the disease-causing bacteria from <strong>parent arthropod</strong> to <strong>offspring arthropod. </strong><em>Orientia tsutsugamushi</em> is maintained by transovarial transmission in <strong>trombiculid mites. </strong>It is also seen in <em>Rickettsia rickettsii, </em><em>Rickettsia conorii</em>, and <em>Rickettsia akari.</em></p>\n<p>The image below shows a trombiculid mite. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Trench fever</strong> (option D) is caused by<em> Bartonella quintana. </em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6987","text":"A 36-year-old trekker pres​ented with fever, headach‍e, and rashes following a‌ tick bite. He was later ‌diagnosed to have Rocky M‍ountain spotted fever. Wh​ich of the following stat‍ements is false about the​ microbe implicated?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old trekker presented with fever, headache, and rashes following a tick bite. He was later diagnosed to have Rocky Mountain spotted fever. Which of the following statements is false about the microbe implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obligate intracellular org​anism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta-lactams are ineffecti​ve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reproduce by binary fissio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cell wall​ is absent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cell wall is absent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cell wall is absent</strong></p><p><strong>Rocky Mountain</strong> spotted fever is caused by <em><strong>Rickettsia rickettsii</strong>. </em>A <strong>c</strong><strong>ell wall</strong> is <strong>present</strong> in rickettsial organisms.</p>\n<p>Rickettsiae are <strong>obligate intracellular</strong>, gram-negative pleomorphic coccobacilli. They are unable to grow in cell-free media and are thus cultured in yolk sacs of embryonated eggs. They are non-motile, non-capsulated organisms that contain both DNA and RNA. They reproduce by <strong>binary fission </strong>and are transmitted by <strong>arthropods</strong> like ticks and lice (except <em>Coxiella burnetii</em> and <em>Bartonella henselae</em>).</p>\n<p>The rickettsiae are <strong>localized</strong> in <strong>endothelial cells</strong> of small blood vessels (except Ehrlichia and Anaplasma) in humans. They cause endothelial cell enlargement and degeneration, predisposing to thrombus formation. Tetracyclines, macrolides, and fluoroquinolones are effective against this group but, <strong>beta-lactams</strong> are <strong>ineffective</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2838","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‍ endemic murine typhus?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding endemic murine typhus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Man to man transmission oc​curs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is caused by R.prowazek​ii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vector is Xenopsylla cheop​is","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neill-Mooser reaction is n​egative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vector is Xenopsylla cheopis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/570a989d351441dfa5ab5fbe509a433dx663x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vector is Xenopsylla cheopis</strong></p><p>The vector for <strong>endemic murine typhus </strong>is the <strong>rat flea, <em>Xenopsylla cheopis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Endemic murine typhus is cuased by <strong><em>Rickettsia typhi </em></strong>(option B)<em> </em>while epidemic typhus is caused by <em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em>. The zoonotic reservoir for<em> R. typhi </em>is the<strong> rat</strong>. Humans are infected when<strong> flea feces </strong>contaminate pruritic <strong>bite</strong> lesions. Transmission can also occur via <strong>inhalation</strong> of aerosols of dried feces of rickettsemic rat flea. <strong>No man-to-man transmission</strong> occurs. Patients present with fever, chills, vomiting and a maculopapular rash that is more prominent on the trunk. Diagnosis is by serology and treatment is with <strong>doxycycline</strong>.</p>\n<p>When male guinea pigs are inoculated intraperitoneally with blood from a case of<strong> endemic typhus</strong>, they develop fever and a characteristic scrotal inflammation. The scrotum becomes enlarged and the testes cannot be pushed into the abdomen. This is known as the <strong>Neil-Mooser reaction.</strong> It is <strong>positive</strong> with <em>Rickettsia typhi </em>(option D) and negative with <em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the rat flea, <em>Xenopsylla cheopis.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2833","text":"Multiple inmates at a pris​on are suspected to have ‍epidemic typhus following‌ an arthropod infestation‌. Which of the following ‍vectors is implicated in ​the spread of this diseas‍e?","raw_text":"Multiple inmates at a prison are suspected to have epidemic typhus following an arthropod infestation. Which of the following vectors is implicated in the spread of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d7f39a47a024718bebb21a8971540ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee21c7e0e4794526be01b8675208db53x1280x3316.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p><strong>Louse</strong> (image B),<strong> </strong><em>Pediculus humanus corporis</em>, transmits<strong> epidemic typhus. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gaol fever</strong>/ Jail fever/ epidemic typhus is caused by<strong> <em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2837","text":"A 45-year-old man from Kas​hmir presents with fever,‍ chills and rash for 1 we‌ek. The rash appeared on ‌the 5th day of the fever ‍starting from the trunk a​nd progressing to the lim‍bs. Epidemic typhus is su​spected. Which of the fol​lowing statements is inco‌rrect about the disease?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man from Kashmir presents with fever, chills and rash for 1 week. The rash appeared on the 5th day of the fever starting from the trunk and progressing to the limbs. Epidemic typhus is suspected. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transmitted​ by a louse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brill-Zinsser disease is a​ recrudescent form","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rash is present on palms a​nd soles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transovarian transmission ​does not occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rash is present on palms and soles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b1cddcf76974921b8a8d4be732578c2x797x828.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rash is present on palms and soles</strong></p><p><strong>Epidemic typhus</strong> rash <strong>spares</strong> the<strong> palms</strong> and<strong> soles. </strong></p>\n<p>Epidemic typhus, caused by <em><strong>Rickettsia prowazekii</strong></em>, is transmitted by the <strong>louse</strong> <em>Pediculus humanus corporis</em>. There is no transovarial transmission of <em>R. prowazekii</em>. The disease spreads through infected louse feces deposited when the louse feeds on the human host. It is commonly seen in poor unhygienic or cold conditions.</p>\n<p>Epidemic typhus starts with fever and chills. A characteristic <strong>rash </strong>appears on the <strong>fourth </strong>or<strong> fifth day</strong>, starting on the trunk and spreading over the limbs but <strong>sparing</strong> the<strong> face, palms, </strong>and <strong>soles</strong>. In some patients who recover from epidemic typhus, the rickettsiae may remain <strong>latent</strong> in the <strong>lymphoid tissues</strong> or organs for years. Such latent infection may, at times, be <strong>reactivated</strong> leading to<strong> </strong>recrudescent typhus <strong>(Brill-Zinsser disease)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transovarial transmission refers to passing on the disease-causing bacteria from <strong>parent arthropod</strong> to<strong> offspring arthropod.</strong> It occurs with <em>Orientia tsutsugamushi, </em><em>Rickettsia rickettsii, </em><em>R. conorii </em>and<em> </em><em>R. akari.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Rash on palms and soles is typically described in Rocky Mountain spotted fever, secondary syphilis and hand, foot, and mouth disease</p>\n<p>The image below shows the rash of epidemic typhus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9169","text":"What is the drug of choice​ for scrub typhus?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for scrub typhus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxyc​ycline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof​loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chloram​phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azithr​omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Doxycycline</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug </strong>of <strong>choice</strong> for <strong>scrub typhus</strong> is <strong>doxycycline. </strong></p>\n<p>To treat scrub typhus, oral doxycycline <strong>100</strong> <strong>mg </strong>is given<strong> twice daily</strong> for <strong>7–15 days</strong>. In the case of <strong>pregnant women</strong>, oral <strong>azithromycin </strong>500 mg for 3 days may be used as doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Scrub typhus is a <strong>rickettsial infection</strong> caused by <strong><em>Orientia tsutsugamushi</em></strong>. The vector is a <strong>trombiculid mite</strong>. Humans are infected when they are bitten by the mite larvae known as <strong>chiggers</strong>. The incubation period is 1- 3 weeks. Patients develop a characteristic<strong> eschar</strong> at the site of the mite bite, along with<strong> regional lymphadenopathy</strong> and <strong>maculopapular rash</strong>. It can be associated with fever, headache, myalgia, meningoencephalitis, myocarditis, and pneumonia.</p>\n<p><strong>Serologic assays</strong> are used for diagnosis. <strong>Weil-Felix reaction</strong> is a heterophile agglutination reaction used for diagnosis, wherein the sera are tested for agglutinins to the O antigens of Proteus strains OX19, OX2, and OXK. <strong>OXK</strong> alone is <strong>agglutinated</strong> in scrub typhus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6990","text":"A 25-year-old man came wit​h complaints of fever and‍ a skin lesion as shown b‌elow. He gives a history ‌of hiking in scrub forest‍s a week prior, where he ​suffered an insect bite. ‍On examination, there is ​regional lymphadenopathy ​and a maculopapular rash.‌ Which of the following a‌rthropods is involved in ‌the spread of this diseas‌e?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man came with complaints of fever and a skin lesion as shown below. He gives a history of hiking in scrub forests a week prior, where he suffered an insect bite. On examination, there is regional lymphadenopathy and a maculopapular rash. Which of the following arthropods is involved in the spread of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamasi​d mite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trombicu​lid mite","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ti​ck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lo​use","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trombiculid mite","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/31c0a90b299246eea535f6f7bfe8a569.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trombiculid mite</strong></p><p>A history of <strong>contact</strong> with <strong>scrub vegetation </strong>with fever, regional lymphadenopathy, maculopapular rash, and <strong>eschar</strong> at the bite site is suggestive of <strong>scrub typhus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scrub typhus is caused by <em><strong>Orientia tsutsugamushi</strong></em> and<em><strong> </strong></em>transmitted by <strong>trombiculid mite</strong>. <strong>Doxycycline</strong> 100mg BD for 7-14 days is the drug of choice for treatment of <strong>scrub typhus</strong> in <strong>adults. </strong>In case of <strong>pregnant women</strong> and<strong> children,</strong> <strong>azithromycin</strong> or <strong>chloramphenicol</strong> may be preferred where doxycycline is relatively contraindicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2842","text":"A wildlife photographer pr​esents with a blackish le‍sion at the site of an in‌sect bite from a few days‌ back. He also gives a hi‍story of fever. A positiv​e Weil-Felix test with OX‍ K agglutinins was observ​ed. What is the diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A wildlife photographer presents with a blackish lesion at the site of an insect bite from a few days back. He also gives a history of fever.  A positive Weil-Felix test with OX K agglutinins was observed. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rocky mountain spotted fev​er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidemi​c typhus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endemic​ typhus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scrub ​typhus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Scrub typhus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Scrub typhus</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, a history of <strong>insect bite</strong> followed by the development of a characteristic <strong>eschar</strong> at the site of the bite and a <strong>positive Weil-Felix test</strong> with OX K agglutinins suggests a diagnosis of <strong>scrub typhus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Weil-Felix reaction is a <strong>heterophile agglutination reaction,</strong> wherein the sera are tested for agglutinins to the O antigens of certain Proteus strains <strong>OX 19, OX 2,</strong> and<strong> OX K. </strong>The basis of the test is sharing an alkali stable carbohydrate antigen by some Rickettsiae and by some strains of Proteus.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 353px;\" width=\"642\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>DISEASE           </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>OX 19</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>OX 2</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>OX K</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Epidemic typhus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>+++</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>+</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Endemic typhus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>+++</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>+/-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Rocky Mountain spotted fever</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>++</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>++</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Scrub typhus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>+++</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Rickettsial pox</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Trench fever</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Q fever</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.114px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.477px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2845","text":"A veterinarian presents wi​th new-onset mitral regur‍gitation and fever. An ec‌ho reveals valve vegetati‌ons and blood cultures sh‍ow no growth. Q fever is ​suspected. Which of the f‍ollowing statements is fa​lse regarding the organis​m implicated?","raw_text":"A veterinarian presents with new-onset mitral regurgitation and fever. An echo reveals valve vegetations and blood cultures show no growth. Q fever is suspected. Which of the following statements is false regarding the organism implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obligate in​tracellular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gram-negati​ve bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Survives Holder's method o​f pasteurisation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transmitte​d by mites","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transmitted by mites","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transmitted by mites</strong></p><p><strong><em><strong>Coxiella burnetii </strong></em></strong>is the causative organism of Q fever. It is transmitted by <strong>ixodid ticks </strong>in <strong>animals; </strong>however, it is not transmitted to humans via ticks. </p>\n<p><em>Coxiella burnetii</em> is a filterable,<strong> obligate intracellular</strong> pathogen. They are <strong>gram-negative</strong> but are better stained with Gimenez, Giemsa stains. They are <strong>heat resistant</strong> and hence survive Holder's method of pasteurization.</p>\n<p>Q fever is a <strong>zoonosis</strong> and the primary source of human infection are infected <strong>cattle, sheep,</strong> and <strong>goats.</strong> It spreads through the<strong> inhalational </strong>route. Veterinarians are at higher risk due to increased exposure. Acute disease resembles influenza, nonbacterial (atypical) pneumonia, and hepatitis. Chronic disease presents as <strong>culture-negative bacterial endocarditis</strong> in patients with valvular heart disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2865","text":"A patient presents with re​dness of the eye and fore‍ign body sensation. On ex‌amination, there is folli‌cular hypertrophy in the ‍lower lids and moderately​ abundant exudates. Chlam‍ydial infection is suspec​ted. Which strains could ​have caused this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A patient presents with redness of the eye and foreign body sensation. On examination, there is follicular hypertrophy in the lower lids and moderately abundant exudates. Chlamydial infection is suspected. Which strains could have caused this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serotype​s D to K","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serotypes A,​ B, Ba and C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serotype ​L1, L2, L3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seroty​pe H- K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serotypes D to K","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serotypes D to K</strong></p><p>Redness, foreign body sensation with follicular hypertrophy in the lower lids and moderately abundant exudates suggests <strong>inclusion conjunctivitis</strong> which<strong> </strong>is caused by <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> serotypes D </strong>to<strong> K</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Inclusion conjunctivitis</strong> is a type of acute follicular conjunctivitis caused by<em> C. trachomatis </em>serotypes D to K. The infection is transmitted to the eye from genitals, or contaminated swimming pools. Patients present with foreign body sensation, redness and mucopurulent discharge. On examination, there is a<span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">cute follicular hypertrophy of the lower palpebral conjunctiva and fornix, non-tender pre-auricularlymphadenopathy and s</span>uperficial keratitis in upper half of cornea.</p>\n<p><strong>C. trachomatis</strong> is a Gram-negative, obligate intracellular bacteria. It cannot produce ATP. It causes a variety of human diseases (ocular and genitourinary) and has two main biological variants: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> </strong>TRIC biovar which causes <strong>trachoma inclusion conjunctivitis</strong> (TRIC biovar). This is further divided into 12 serological variants:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A, B, Ba, C</strong> serovars which cause blinding or endemic <strong>trachoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>D to K</strong> servovars which cause inclusion conjunctivitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong> </strong>LGV biovar (L1, L2, L3 serotypes) which causes<strong> lymphogranuloma venereum</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6989","text":"A thick, yellowish dischar​ge is noted from the eyes‍ of a five-day-old neonat‌e in the step-down ward d‌uring rounds. An iodine-s‍tained smear of the exuda​tes is positive for large‍ cytoplasmic inclusions a​s shown in the image. Whi​ch of the following is fa‌lse about the organism re‌sponsible for the above c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A thick, yellowish discharge is noted from the eyes of a five-day-old neonate in the step-down ward during rounds. An iodine-stained smear of the exudates is positive for large cytoplasmic inclusions as shown in the image. Which of the following is false about the organism responsible for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filterable​ organisms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obligate in​tracellular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Possess ​only DNA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cannot grow on artificial ​media","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Possess only DNA","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8799de83017a4b678f89e408a74cb32f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f3e26fe42b343a38f287076fffcdd62x1280x1864.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Possess only DNA</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a thick yellowish discharge from the eye showing iodine-stained inclusion bodies is suggestive of<strong> inclusion conjunctivitis</strong> caused by <em><strong>Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></em>. As chlamydia is a bacteria, it possesses <strong>both DNA </strong>and<strong> RNA.</strong></p>\n<p>Chlamydiae are <strong>obligate intracellular</strong> organisms that produce <strong>basophilic inclusion bodies</strong>. They are <strong>filterable</strong> like viruses. They are called <strong>energy parasites</strong> as they lack enzymes for ATP synthesis. They <strong>cannot grow</strong> on<strong> artificial media</strong> but can grow on cell lines like McCoy, HeLa, or the yolk sac of the chick embryo.</p>\n<p>Chlamydiae occur in two forms, the elementary body and the reticulate body. The <strong>extracellular, infective</strong> form is called the <strong>elementary body</strong>. The <strong>intracellular, non-infective,</strong> growing, and <strong>replicative</strong> form is called the <strong>reticulate body</strong>. These are found inside a membrane-bound compartment called the <strong>inclusion body</strong> which can be visualized within infected epithelial cells by iodine or Giemsa stain. The cell wall of the reticulate body is fragile and pliable, leading to <strong>pleomorphism</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2860","text":"A poultry worker was admit​ted to ICU with severe pn‍eumonia. Bronchoalveolar ‌lavage was done; it showe‌d Levinthal Cole Lillie b‍odies. Which of the follo​wing is the causative org‍anism for the above condi​tion?","raw_text":"A poultry worker was admitted to ICU with severe pneumonia. Bronchoalveolar lavage was done; it showed Levinthal Cole Lillie bodies.  Which of the following is the causative organism for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Legionella ​pneumophila","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlamydia​ psittaci","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mycoplasma​ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Moraxella ​catarrhalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chlamydia psittaci","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chlamydia psittaci</strong></p><p><strong>Pneumonia</strong> in a <strong>poultry worker</strong> with <strong>Levinthal-Cole-Lillie</strong> bodies on bronchoalveolar lavage specimens is suggestive of <strong>psittacosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Levinthal-Cole-Lillie bodies are <strong>inclusion bodies</strong> of <em><strong>Chlamydia psittaci</strong></em> seen in infected cells including alveolar macrophages from patients suffering from psittacosis. <em>C. psittaci </em>can be transmitted to humans by direct contact with infected birds or by inhalation of aerosols from avian feather dust. Psittacosis presents with fever, chills, muscular aches and pains, severe headache, hepatosplenomegaly and gastrointestinal symptoms. <strong>Severe pneumonia </strong>may develop. It is treated with<strong> tetracycline</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2864","text":"A patient presented with p​ainful swellings on both ‍sides of the groin as sho‌wn in the image. The clin‌ician suspects lymphogran‍uloma venereum. Which is ​the most common serovar c‍ausing the above conditio​n?","raw_text":"A patient presented with painful swellings on both sides of the groin as shown in the image. The clinician suspects lymphogranuloma venereum. Which is the most common serovar causing the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. L2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/010f81e2f9424b658aa346920960ba0b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. L2</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphogranuloma venereum </strong>(LGV) is caused most commonly by <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> LGV serovar L2</strong>.</p>\n<p>LGV is a sexually transmitted disease, characterized by <strong>suppurative</strong> <strong>inguinal adenitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3500","text":"What is the incubation per​iod of lymphogranuloma ve‍nereum?","raw_text":"What is the incubation period of lymphogranuloma venereum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-5 ​days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7-10​ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-30​ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"30-60​ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10-30 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10-30 days</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1603\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1602\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>incubation period</strong> of <em><strong>Lymphogranuloma venereum</strong></em> ranges from <strong>10-30 days.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> serovars L1, L2, and L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV). The most common presentation is the<strong> inguinal syndrome</strong>, which is characterized by painful inguinal lymphadenopathy.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8495","text":"Lesion scrapings from a ge​nital ulcer show numerous‍ polymorphs, but no bacte‌ria. Which of the followi‌ng is the most appropriat‍e culture medium for orga​nisms responsible for the‍ above condition?","raw_text":"Lesion scrapings from a genital ulcer show numerous polymorphs, but no bacteria. Which of the following is the most appropriate culture medium for organisms responsible for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"McCoy​ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thayer-Ma​rtin agar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Robertson’s​ cooked meat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PPLO ​broth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. McCoy cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. McCoy cells</strong></p><p>Clinical scrapings from a genital ulcer showing polymorphs in the absence of bacteria is suggestive of <strong>chlamydial infection</strong> and the most appropriate culture medium is <strong>McCoy cells </strong>(human synovial carcinoma cell line).</p>\n<p><strong><em>C. trachomatis</em></strong> is a small gram-negative bacte­rium and an obligate intracellular parasite that appears as an intracytoplasmic inclusion body. The gram stain of the cervical smear would show leucocytes but no organisms. Hence, selective culture media like the <strong>McCoy cells culture</strong> is used to isolate the organism.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Thayer–Martin agar</strong> is used for isolation of <em>Neisseria spp. </em></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Robertson’s cooked meat </strong>is used for the culture of anaerobes, especially Clostridia</p>\n<p>Option D: Pleuropneumonia-like organisms (PPLO) broth is used to culture <em>Mycoplasma spp</em>. and <em>Ureaplasma spp</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Abnormal urethral gram stain showing &gt;10 PMNs/1000Å~ field in women with dysuria but without coliform bacteriuria supports the diagnosis of chlamydial urethritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6991","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen​ted with a 3-day history ‍of mucopurulent cervical ‌discharge. Gram staining ‌of the discharge revealed‍ numerous pus cells, but ​no organisms. What is the‍ investigation of choice ​for the diagnosis of this​ infection?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman presented with a 3-day history of mucopurulent cervical discharge. Gram staining of the discharge revealed numerous pus cells, but no organisms. What is the investigation of choice for the diagnosis of this infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saline​ mount","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum antibody testing by ​immunofluorescence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Culture in Thayer Martin m​edia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleic acid amplification​ test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nucleic acid amplification test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nucleic acid amplification test</strong></p><p>Mucopurulent cervical discharge for 3 days is suggestive of <strong>acute cervicitis.</strong> As gram stain does not show any organism, gonococcal infection is less likely. In this case, <em><strong>Chlamydia trachomatis</strong> </em>is the most likely organism. For <em>C. trachomatis,</em> the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> is the <strong>nucleic acid amplification test </strong>(NAAT).</p>\n<p>NAATs are the <strong>most sensitive</strong> tests to detect chlamydia. These tests also have a <strong>high specificity</strong> comparable to culture, and in contrast to culture, do not depend on viable pathogens. Therefore, NAATs are generally considered the test of choice for chlamydia and have replaced culture as the <strong>diagnostic gold standard.</strong></p>\n<p>Mucopurulent cervicitis is the inflammation of the columnar epithelium and subepithelium of the endocervix in women. Its commonly caused by <em>Neiserria gonorrhoeae </em>and <em>Chlamydia trachomatis. </em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2867","text":"Which of the following mic​robes is also referred to‍ as Eaton's agent ?","raw_text":"Which of the following microbes is also referred to as Eaton's agent ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebsiella​ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia ​trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycoplasma​ pneumoniae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mycoplasma pneumoniae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mycoplasma pneumoniae</strong></p><p><strong>Eaton's agent</strong> refers to <strong><em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Eaton was the first to isolate <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>. Because it was <strong>filterable,</strong> it was considered to be a virus previously.</p>\n<p>It was formerly known as <strong>PPLO</strong>– Pleuro pneumonia-like organism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2872","text":"A 20-year-old military rec​ruit presented with fever‍, sore throat, and non-pr‌oductive cough which show‌ed no improvement with be‍ta-lactam antibiotics. X-​ray showed peribronchial ‍pneumonia with thickened ​bronchial markings. Prima​ry atypical pneumonia is ‌suspected. Which of the f‌ollowing statements is fa‌lse regarding the organis‌m responsible for this co‍ndition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old military recruit presented with fever, sore throat, and non-productive cough which showed no improvement with beta-lactam antibiotics. X-ray showed peribronchial pneumonia with thickened bronchial markings. Primary atypical pneumonia is suspected. Which of the following statements is false regarding the organism responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obligate in​tracellular","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cell wall​ is absent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Highly pl​eomorphic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiply by binary fission​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Obligate intracellular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Obligate intracellular</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of fever, non-productive cough, <strong>no improvement</strong> with <strong>beta-lactam antibiotics</strong> along with X-ray features showing <strong>peribronchial pneumonia</strong> with <strong>thickened bronchial markings</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Mycoplasma pneumonia</strong>, also known as primary atypical pneumonia or walking pneumonia.</p>\n<p>Mycoplasmas are not <strong>obligate intracellular</strong> parasites. They are <strong>free-living bacteria devoid of cell walls</strong>. They are highly <strong>pleomorphic,</strong> with no fixed shape or size. </p>\n<p>Because of their plasticity, they can pass through bacterial filters and have often been mistaken for viruses. </p>\n<p>Mycoplasmas do not possess spores, flagella, or fimbria. Some species exhibit gliding motility. Mycoplasmas are gram-negative, but are better stained by Giemsa stain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2876","text":"Diene’s method of staining​ is used for which of the‍ following organisms?","raw_text":"Diene’s method of staining is used for which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycop​lasma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chlam​ydiae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ant​hrax","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipht​heria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mycoplasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54f5649476b64b0899f65f45d30ea72dx958x632.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mycoplasma</strong></p><p><strong>Diene's method</strong> is used for <strong>mycoplasma.</strong></p>\n<p>Mycoplasma shows a <strong>‘fried-egg’ colony</strong> on Diene’s method of staining<strong>.</strong> The <strong>central dark</strong> bluish area represents the area of <strong>mycoplasmal down growth</strong> whereas the <strong>pale blue periphery</strong> indicates <strong>superficial growth</strong> of mycoplasma on the agar.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4460","text":"Mycoplasma is inherently r​esistant to which of the ‍following antibiotics?","raw_text":"Mycoplasma is inherently resistant to which of the following antibiotics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta lactam​ antibiotics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Macro​lides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrac​ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flouroqu​inolones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Beta lactam antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Beta lactam antibiotics</strong></p><p>Mycoplasma is inherently <strong>resistant </strong>to<strong> beta-lactam </strong>antibiotics.</p>\n<p>Mycoplasma<strong> lack cell wal</strong>l, and therefore, are inherently resistant to beta-lactam antibiotics as these drugs act on the cell wall.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9ozLoAwS","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"19_abortion_and_medical_termination_of_pregnancy","title":"19.Abortion And Medical Termination Of Pregnancy","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB1418","text":"Abortion is the expulsion ​of fetus weighing _____.","raw_text":"Abortion is the expulsion of fetus weighing _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;​250 g","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;​500 g","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;​750 g","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;1​000 g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. <500 g","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt;500 g</strong></p><p>According to WHO, <strong>abortion</strong> is defined as pregnancy termination or loss <strong>before</strong> <strong>20 weeks</strong> of gestation or with a fetus weighing <strong>&lt;500 g</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some common<strong> causes</strong> <strong>of spontaneous abortion</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chromosomal abnormalities </li>\n<li>Luteal phase defects</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus and thyroid disorders</li>\n<li>Cervical incompetence</li>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</li>\n<li>Maternal infections</li>\n<li>Alcohol and cigarette smoking</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1415","text":"A patient presented with v​aginal bleeding following‌ 6 weeks of amenorrhoea. ​Her UPT was positive. Wha​t is the most common caus​e of this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with vaginal bleeding following 6 weeks of amenorrhoea. Her UPT was positive. What is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Luteal ph​ase defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infec​tions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromosomal​ abnormality","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical i​ncompetence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chromosomal abnormality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chromosomal abnormality</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> first-trimester abortion </strong>which is most commonly caused by<strong> chromosomal abnormalities</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Autosomal trisomies </strong>are the <strong>most common</strong> (50%) genetic abnormality <strong>overall</strong>. Of these, the most common trisomy involved in abortions is trisomy 16. <strong>Turner syndrome </strong>(monosomy X) is the<strong> single most common</strong> chromosomal abnormality in miscarriages.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> <strong>of spontaneous abortion are</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genetic - \n<ul>\n<li>Numerical chromosomal abnormalities</li>\n<li>Chromosomal rearrangements</li>\n<li>Mendelian etiology</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Endocrine -\n<ul>\n<li>Luteal phase defects</li>\n<li>Thyroid abnormalities</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Anatomical - \n<ul>\n<li>Cervical incompetence</li>\n<li>Congenital uterine malformations</li>\n<li>Uterine fibroid</li>\n<li>Uterine synechiae</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Infectious (TORCH infections)</li>\n<li>Immunological -\n<ul>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</li>\n<li>Autoimmunity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Maternal illness - \n<ul>\n<li>Cyanotic heart diseases</li>\n<li>Hemoglobinopathies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Environmental -\n<ul>\n<li>Radiation exposure</li>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1417","text":"A patient presented with a​ spontaneous abortion at ‌7 weeks of gestation. Whi​ch of the following is th​e least likely chromosoma​l abnormality that can le‌ad to this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a spontaneous abortion at 7 weeks of gestation. Which of the following is the least likely chromosomal abnormality that can lead to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Triso​my 16","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tris​omy 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Triso​my 21","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triso​my 22","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trisomy 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trisomy 1</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 370\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The<strong> least likely</strong> chromosomal abnormality involved in first-trimester abortions is <strong>trisomy 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> chromosomal abnormality in <strong>first trimester abortions</strong> is <strong>trisomy</strong>. The common trisomies are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Edwards syndrome (Trisomy 18)</li>\n<li>Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)</li>\n<li>Patau syndrome (Trisomy 13)</li>\n<li>Trisomies 16 and 22 </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Turner</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> (Monosomy X) is the <strong>single</strong> <strong>most frequent</strong> chromosomal abnormality. It usually results in abortion.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1433","text":"A woman with 6 weeks of am​enorrhea had a positive u‌rine pregnancy test. The ​following finding was see​n on transabdominal USG. ​What is the most probable‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman with 6 weeks of amenorrhea had a positive urine pregnancy test. The following finding was seen on transabdominal USG. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Singleton​ pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Missed ​abortion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anenc​ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molar p​regnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Missed abortion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a1d086e661943ca9fc6b2edd6bd59df.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e62a68de155f48e19a5bd2f4ed1a80b3x1280x1111.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/047459e5535242d6a15188c9d88f8e74x1280x1107.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Missed abortion</strong></p><p>The given USG image shows an <strong>empty gestational sac</strong> which is seen in <strong>missed abortion </strong>before 12 weeks. At this gestational age,  the entire ovum is covered by the chorionic villi giving rise to the empty gestational sac.</p>\n<p>If this condition occurs <strong>beyond</strong> <strong>12 weeks </strong>of gestation, a <strong>dead fetus</strong> is seen on USG. This is not preceded by any<strong> </strong>warning signs and the fetus is not expelled outside spontaneously.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1432","text":"You are examining a primig​ravida with complete abor‌tion. Which of the follow​ing findings would you no​t expect?","raw_text":"You are examining a primigravida with complete abortion. Which of the following findings would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterus smaller than period​ of amenorrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Open cer​vical os","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uterus firm ​on palpation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trace amounts of vaginal b​leeding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Open cervical os","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2beee566f8f4507be6e21c8153abb46x638x479.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67589d50bd3f43bebad7cd7c24f0a694x1280x6462.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Open cervical os</strong></p><p>The cervical os is<strong> closed</strong> in <strong>complete </strong>abortion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1421","text":"Recurrent miscarriage is t​he loss of:","raw_text":"Recurrent miscarriage is the loss of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"≥3 consecutive pregnancies​ before 20 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"≥2 consecutive pregnancies​ before 20 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"≥3 consecutive pregnancies​ after 24 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"≥2 consecutive pregnancies​ before 28 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ≥3 consecutive pregnancies before 20 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ≥3 consecutive pregnancies before 20 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Recurrent miscarriage</strong> is defined as <strong>≥3 consecutive </strong>pregnancy losses at<strong> ≤ 20 weeks</strong> or with a fetal weight <strong>&lt;500 g</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>P</strong><strong>rimary</strong> recurrent miscarriage where there are multiple losses in a woman who has never delivered a liveborn</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary </strong>recurrent miscarriage where<strong> </strong>multiple pregnancy losses occur in a patient who has had a prior live birth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1422","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not usually ‌cause recurrent miscarria​ges?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not usually cause recurrent miscarriages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asherman​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polycystic ovarian syndrom​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bicornua​te uterus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infec​tions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Infections","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Infections</strong></p><p>Infections do not usually cause <strong>recurrent miscarriages</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Common causes</strong> <strong>of recurrent miscarriage</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parental chromosomal abnormalities</li>\n<li>Anatomical factors - \n<ul>\n<li>Uterine synechiae or <strong>Asherman syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Uterine leiomyomas</li>\n<li>Congenital <strong>genital tract anomalies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Immunological factors -\n<ul>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Endocrine factors - \n<ul>\n<li>Luteal phase defect</li>\n<li><strong>Polycystic ovarian syndrome </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1424","text":"A primigravida had a spont​aneous abortion at 18 wee‌ks. On examination, the f​etus was morphologically ​normal. Further investiga​tions were performed upon‌ the patient's insistence​ and the results are give‍n below. What is the like‍ly diagnosis? Antibody ag​ainst β-2 glycoprotein - ​Negative Lupus anticoagul‍ant - Positive Anti-cardi‌olipin antibody - Positiv​e aPTT - Prolonged Protei​n C - Normal Hysterocervi‌cography - Normal","raw_text":"<p>A primigravida had a spontaneous abortion at 18 weeks. On examination, the fetus was morphologically normal. Further investigations were performed upon the patient's insistence and the results are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antibody against β-2 glycoprotein - Negative</li>\n<li>Lupus anticoagulant - Positive</li>\n<li>Anti-cardiolipin antibody - Positive</li>\n<li>aPTT - Prolonged</li>\n<li>Protein C - Normal</li>\n<li>Hysterocervicography - Normal</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syph​ilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-phospholipid antibody​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemoph​ilia B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factor V Lei​den mutation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome</strong></p><p>The given lab findings are suggestive of <strong>anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome</strong>(APLS). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Criteria</strong> <strong>for diagnosis</strong> of antiphospholipid antibody syndrome:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clinical</strong> criteria -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obstetric\n<ul>\n<li>Three or more unexplained consecutive spontaneous abortions before 10 weeks</li>\n<li>One or more deaths of a morphologically normal fetus at or beyond 10 weeks</li>\n<li>Severe pre-eclampsia necessitating delivery before 34 weeks</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Vascular\n<ul>\n<li>One or more episodes of arterial, venous, or small vessel thrombosis in any tissue or organ</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Laboratory</strong> criteria -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of lupus anticoagulant</li>\n<li>Presence of IgG or IgM anti-cardiolipin antibodies</li>\n<li>Presence of IgG or IgM anti-beta-2 glycoprotein I antibodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At least <strong>one clinical</strong> and <strong>one laboratory</strong> criteria is needed for the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Non-specific tests for lupus anticoagulant are increased aPTT, Kaolin clotting time, and dilute Russell viper venom time.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1425","text":"A woman comes to the casua​lty at 18 weeks of gestat‌ion with complaints of va​ginal bleeding. She has a​ history of two early pre​gnancy losses. USG reveal‌s the absence of fetal ca​rdiac activity. Which of ‍the following investigati‍ons need not be done in t​his patient?","raw_text":"A woman comes to the casualty at 18 weeks of gestation with complaints of vaginal bleeding. She has a history of two early pregnancy losses. USG reveals the absence of fetal cardiac activity. Which of the following investigations need not be done in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensive infection work-u​p","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serum prol​actin level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antiphospholipid antibodie​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parental k​aryotyping","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extensive infection work-up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extensive infection work-up</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>recurrent abortions,</strong> and extensive infection workup is not done.</p>\n<p>Even though recurrent abortions are defined as ≥3 pregnancy losses, <strong>investigations</strong> must be <strong>done after 2</strong> consecutive <strong>losses</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic evaluation</strong> of recurrent pregnancy loss based on etiology is given as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 563px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\"><strong>Etiology</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\"><strong>Diagnostic evaluation</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Genetic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">Parental karyotyping </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Anatomic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">\n<p>Hysterosalpingography</p>\n<p>Ultrasound</p>\n<p>Saline-infusion sonography </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Endocrine </td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">\n<p>TSH</p>\n<p>Testing for insulin resistance</p>\n<p>Serum prolactin level</p>\n<p>Ovarian reserve testing</p>\n<p>Antithyroid antibodies </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Infectious </td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">No evaluation is recommended unless the patient has evidence of chronic endometritis/cervicitis on examination, or is immunocompromised </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Autoimmune </td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">\n<p>Anticardiolipin antibody levels (IgG and IgM) </p>\n<p>Lupus anticoagulant<strong>   </strong></p>\n<p>Homocysteine</p>\n<p>Factor V Leiden</p>\n<p>Prothrombin promoter mutation</p>\n<p>Activated protein C resistance </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7491","text":"A pregnant lady with a ​hi​story of recurrent aborti‌ons is now diagnosed to h​ave antiphospholipid synd​rome. What would be the b​est mode of treatment for‌ her?","raw_text":"A pregnant lady with a ​history of recurrent abortions is now diagnosed to have antiphospholipid syndrome. What would be the best mode of treatment for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspiri​n only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aspirin​+heparin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aspirin+hepa​rin+steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspirin+​steroids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aspirin+heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aspirin+heparin</strong></p><p>The best mode of treatment for a pregnant woman with <strong>antiphospholipid s</strong><strong>yndrome</strong>(APS) is <strong>aspirin</strong> and <strong>heparin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>APS </strong>is a prothrombotic disorder (<strong>autoantibody-mediated thrombophilia</strong>) affecting both arterial and venous systems.</p>\n<p>Patients present with <strong>recurrent</strong> <strong>pregnancy loss</strong> due to thrombosis of <strong>uteroplacental</strong> <strong>vessels</strong>, poor placental perfusion, and prevention of normal decidual changes after zygote implantation. They may also present with preterm delivery, pre-eclampsia, intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR), and fetal death.</p>\n<p>Criteria for APS include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>at least one otherwise unexplained fetal death at or beyond 10 weeks;</li>\n<li>at least one preterm birth before 34 weeks gestation because of eclampsia, severe preeclampsia, placental insufficiency; or</li>\n<li>at least three unexplained consecutive spontaneous abortions before 10 weeks. </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Aspirin</strong> (60-80mg daily)<strong> with</strong> <strong>heparin</strong> (5000-10,000 U subcutaneously every 12h) is the<strong> most effective therapy </strong>for APS. Treating APS with only aspirin <strong>(option A)</strong> is not recommended as heparin is crucial in preventing thrombus formation at the decidual-thrombotic interface, Corticosteroids <strong>(option C, D)</strong> should <strong>not</strong> be used in <strong>primary</strong> <strong>APS</strong> (without an associated connective tissue disorder) but are indicated in patients with pre-existing systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and APS (secondary APS). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9772","text":"A G3A3 patient was admitte​d to the hospital with ca‌tastrophic APLS following​ a spontaneous abortion. ​Which of the following is​ false about this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A G3A3 patient was admitted to the hospital with catastrophic APLS following a spontaneous abortion. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a rapidly progressiv​e thromboembolic disorder‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At least 2 organ systems s​hould be involved to make‌ a diagnosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infections can act as a tr​igger","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IVIG can be used for treat​ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. At least 2 organ systems should be involved to make a diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. At least 2 organ systems should be involved to make a diagnosis</strong></p><p><strong>Catastrophic antiphospholipid antibody syndrome </strong>(CAPS)<strong> </strong>is a complication of APLA syndrome,<strong> </strong>in which <strong>≥3</strong> <strong>organ systems</strong> are involved. It is also known as <strong>Asherson syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>It is seen in both primary and secondary APLA syndrome. Triggering factors include infections, trauma, and obstetric causes. IVIG can be used in CAPS when the first-line therapies with aspirin and heparin have failed.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Asherman syndrome</strong> is an acquired condition characterised by formation of intrauterine adhesions or synechiae.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1426","text":"Which of the following fea​tures can be seen in a pa‌tient with cervical incom​petence?","raw_text":"Which of the following features can be seen in a patient with cervical incompetence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painful vaginal bleeding a​t &amp;lt;20 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Repeated mid-trimester pai​nless cervical dilatation‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Repeated third trimester p​ainless cervical dilatati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bleeding per vaginum in th​e first trimester","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Repeated mid-trimester painless cervical dilatation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Repeated mid-trimester painless cervical dilatation</strong></p><p><strong>Cervical incompetence</strong> or insufficiency is characterized by <strong>repeated</strong> <strong>mid-trimester</strong> <strong>painless</strong> cervical <strong>dilatation</strong>. Escape of amniotic fluid followed by painless expulsion of products of conception can also be seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors for cervical incompetence</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital uterine anomalies</li>\n<li>Acquired -\n<ul>\n<li>Dilatation and currettage </li>\n<li>Dilatation and evacuation</li>\n<li>Vaginal operative delivery through an undilated cervix</li>\n<li>Amputation of cervix </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Multiple gestations</li>\n<li>Prior preterm birth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1430","text":"Which of the following inc​rease the risk of develop‌ing cervical incompetence​? In-utero exposure to DE​S Twin pregnancy History ​of MTP with Mifepristone ‌Genital tract infections ​Bicornuate uterus","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following increase the risk of developing cervical incompetence?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In-utero exposure to DES</li>\n<li>Twin pregnancy</li>\n<li>History of MTP with Mifepristone</li>\n<li>Genital tract infections</li>\n<li>Bicornuate uterus</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​2, 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 5</strong></p><p><strong>In-utero</strong> exposure to<strong> DES</strong>, <strong>twin</strong> pregnancy, and <strong>bicornuate uterus</strong> increase the risk of <strong>cervical incompetence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Offsprings born to pregnant mothers who were treated with <strong>diethylstilbestrol</strong> may develop <strong>cervical hypoplasia</strong> and malformation. They may also present with cervical incompetence during their 2nd trimester.</p>\n<p>Cervical incompetence can be seen following <strong>MTP with surgical methods</strong> and not with medical methods.</p>\n<p>The retentive power of the internal os may be compromised by anatomical abnormalities like bicornuate uterus and overdistended uterus due to twin gestations.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors for cervical incompetence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital uterine anomalies</li>\n<li>Acquired -\n<ul>\n<li>Dilatation and currettage </li>\n<li>Induced abortion by dilatation and evacuation</li>\n<li>Vaginal operative delivery through an undilated cervix</li>\n<li>Amputation of cervix </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Multiple gestations</li>\n<li>Prior preterm birth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1428","text":"The transvaginal ultrasoun​d of a pregnant woman is ‌given below. What are the​ cutoff values of the par​ameters marked A and B fo​r a diagnosis of cervical‌ incompetence?","raw_text":"The transvaginal ultrasound of a pregnant woman is given below. What are the cutoff values of the parameters marked A and B for a diagnosis of cervical incompetence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A &amp;gt;1cm an​d B &amp;lt;25mm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A &amp;lt;25 mm ​and B &amp;gt;1cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A &amp;gt;2 cm a​nd B &amp;lt;35mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A &amp;gt;2.5cm and B &amp;lt;25mm​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A >1cm and B <25mm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4817e669681546fda785968bf86bb9ac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33f41f291f2c4f1cb45d0c566c84fa1bx1280x1037.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca0d2b41430a49cbb5dc7691db3ceb64x1280x1451.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A &gt;1cm and B &lt;25mm</strong></p><p>In the given image, the parameter marked A is funnelling of the internal os and the parameter marked B is cervical length. <strong>Funnelling of internal os</strong> and <strong>cervical length</strong> <strong>&lt;25 mm</strong> are diagnostic of <strong>cervical incompetence</strong> on transvaginal ultrasound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1427","text":"A 26-year-old woman presen​ts with 6 weeks of amenor‌rhea and a positive UPT. ​Her past obstetric histor​y was significant for 2 m​id-trimester abortions. O‌n USG, cervical length wa​s 20 mm and no bulging me‍mbranes were seen. What i‍s the next step in her ma​nagement?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman presents with 6 weeks of amenorrhea and a positive UPT. Her past obstetric history was significant for 2 mid-trimester abortions. On USG, cervical length was 20 mm and no bulging membranes were seen.  What is the next step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Expectant ​management","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate re​scue cerclage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prophylactic cerclage at 1​2 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Immediate termination of p​regnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prophylactic cerclage at 12 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prophylactic cerclage at 12 weeks</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cervical incompetence.</strong> It can be managed with <strong>prophylactic cervical cerclage</strong> done between <strong>12 </strong>and<strong> 14 weeks</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p>Rescue or<strong> emergency cerclage</strong> is done anytime <strong>within</strong> <strong>24 weeks</strong> of gestation. It should<strong> </strong>only be done when there is <strong>bulging</strong> <strong>of membranes</strong> through the dilated cervix. </p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> <strong>for cervical incompetence</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In interconceptional period - \n<ul>\n<li>Passage of number 6 - 8 Hegar's dilator beyond the internal os without resistance or pain</li>\n<li>Funnel-shaped shadow in premenstrual hysterocervicography</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>During pregnancy -\n<ul>\n<li>Per speculum examination showing cervical dilatation with herniation of membranes</li>\n<li>USG revealing cervical length &lt;2.5 cm and funnelling of internal os &gt;1 cm</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1429","text":"A patient with cervical in​sufficiency is posted for‌ McDonald's procedure. Wh​ich of the following is n​ot a contraindication for​ this surgery?","raw_text":"A patient with cervical insufficiency is posted for McDonald's procedure.  Which of the following is not a contraindication for this surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placental​ abruption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Premature membrane rupture​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical dilation &amp;gt;4 cm​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;20 weeks​ of gestation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. >20 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt;20 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>McDonald's procedure is a type of <strong>cervical cerclage. </strong>It can be done <strong>up to 24 weeks</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> to cervical cerclage are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absolute\n<ul>\n<li>&gt;24 weeks of gestation </li>\n<li>\n<div><strong>Active labour</strong></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div>Active vaginal bleeding</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><strong>Abruptio placenta</strong></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><strong>Premature rupture of membranes</strong></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div>Chorioamnionitis</div>\n</li>\n<li>Severe uterine irritability</li>\n<li>Fetal death or defect</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Relative\n<ul>\n<li>Prolapsed membranes</li>\n<li>Vaginal spotting</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1431","text":"A woman with cervical inco​mpetence is posted for pr‌ophylactic McDonald's cer​clage. Which of the follo​wing statements is false ​about this procedure?","raw_text":"A woman with cervical incompetence is posted for prophylactic McDonald's cerclage. Which of the following statements is false about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Removal of stitch must be ​done by 37th week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A mersilene tape is tied a​round the cervix","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Done at the level of inter​nal os","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chorioamnionitis is a post​-operative complication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A mersilene tape is tied around the cervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c09292bdf634d2ca1f654b418e44d8ex1280x1547.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A mersilene tape is tied around the cervix</strong></p><p>In <strong>McDonald's cerclage</strong>, a nonabsorbable <strong>suture</strong>, such as <strong>Mersilene</strong>, is placed as a <strong>purse-string</strong> suture as high as possible at the level of the <strong>internal os</strong>, positioned at the junction where the rugose vaginal epithelium transitions to the smooth vaginal portion of the cervix, below the bladder.</p>\n<p>Another cerclage procedure is <strong>Modified Shirodker's</strong> technique. In this, a non-absorbable (mersilene) <strong>tape</strong> is passed subcutaneously through a transverse anterior incision and is tied posteriorly.</p>\n<p><strong>Removal</strong> of cerclage should be done by<strong> 37 weeks</strong> of gestation. It can be removed earlier in case of preterm labour.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> <strong>of cervical cerclage</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slipping of suture</li>\n<li><strong>Chorioamnionitis</strong></li>\n<li>Rupture of membranes</li>\n<li>Abortion or preterm labour</li>\n<li>Cervical laceration or scarring</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1435","text":"A 20-year-old female patie​nt came for medical termi‌nation of pregnancy. From​ whom will you obtain con​sent for the procedure?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old female patient came for medical termination of pregnancy. From whom will you obtain consent for the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient and her male partn​er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only the​ patient","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patient's​ guardian","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient's​ caregiver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Only the patient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Only the patient</strong></p><p><strong>Only</strong> the <strong>patient's consent</strong> is required for medical termination of pregnancy (<strong>MTP</strong>) and her male partner's consent is not required.</p>\n<p>Consent of <strong>parent/guardian</strong> is needed if the age of the <strong>patient</strong> is <strong>&lt;18 yrs</strong> or if the patient is <strong>mentally ill</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9777","text":"A patient with contracepti​ve failure requires MTP a‌t 5 weeks of gestation. Y​ou plan to use a medical ​method of abortion in her​ case. Which of the follo‌wing is not a contraindic​ation for this?","raw_text":"A patient with contraceptive failure requires MTP at 5 weeks of gestation. You plan to use a medical method of abortion in her case. Which of the following is not a contraindication for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrauterine device in sit​u","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Long term corticosteroids ​therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uncontrolled​ hypertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic diab​etes mellitus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p>Chronic diabetes mellitus is not a contraindication to medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) with medical methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> to <strong>medical</strong> methods of abortion are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Women with <strong>in situ intrauterine device</strong></li>\n<li>Severe anemia, coagulopathy, or anticoagulant use </li>\n<li><strong>Long term corticosteroid therapy </strong></li>\n<li>Severe liver, renal, pulmonary, or cardiovascular disease</li>\n<li>Inherited porphyria</li>\n<li><strong>Uncontrolled hypertension</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> to <strong>surgical</strong> methods of abortion are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suction and evacuation - uterine size &gt;10 weeks</li>\n<li>Intrauterine instillation of hypertonic solution - cardiovascular or renal lesion and severe anaemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1436","text":"A 23-year-old pregnant rap​e victim was posted for a‌ suction and evacuation p​rocedure. This procedure ​can be done up to _____ o​f gestation.","raw_text":"A 23-year-old pregnant rape victim was posted for a suction and evacuation procedure. This procedure can be done up to _____ of gestation.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 12 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 12 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Suction and evacuation</strong> can be done up to <strong>12 weeks</strong> of gestation. It is a method of termination of pregnancy in the first-trimester.</p>\n<p>The various methods of termination of pregnancy are: </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Timing</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Medical methods</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Surgical methods</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>1st trimester</p>\n<p>(up to 12 weeks)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mifepristone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mifepristone and Misoprostol</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Methotrexate and Misoprostol</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tamoxifen and Misoprostol</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Menstrual regulation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vacuum aspiration (MVA/EVA)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Suction evacuation and/or curettage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dilatation and evacuation -</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rapid method</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Slow method </li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>2nd trimester</p>\n<p>(13 - 20 weeks)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Misoprostol</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dinoprostone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carboprost</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dilatation and evacuation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intrauterine instillation of hyperosmotic solutions</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oxytocin infusion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hysterotomy </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9784","text":"You have performed MTP on ​a multigravida at 10 week‌s of gestation. As a part​ of post-op advice, you m​ust prescribe her a proph​ylactic antibiotic. Which‌ of the following drugs w​ould you prefer?","raw_text":"You have performed MTP on a multigravida at 10 weeks of gestation.  As a part of post-op advice, you must prescribe her a prophylactic antibiotic. Which of the following drugs would you prefer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxycycline ​200 mg orally","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azithromycin 500 mg orally​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoxicillin ​250 mg orally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefotaxi​me 1 g iv","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Doxycycline 200 mg orally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Doxycycline 200 mg orally</strong></p><p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> is the preferred drug for post-abortal <strong>antibiotic prophylaxis</strong> in this case. Oral doxycycline<strong> 100 mg</strong> is given <strong>1 hour before</strong> surgical evacuation. <strong>200 mg</strong> is given <strong>after </strong>the procedure is complete.</p>\n<p>The best method of MTP at 7-12 weeks is by <strong>suction and evacuation</strong>. Following this procedure, antibiotic prophylaxis is given with doxycycline.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9780","text":"You are about to administe​r misoprostol to a woman ‌who requires MTP in the 8​th week of her pregnancy.​ What is the dose of this​ drug when given by the m‌ucosal route, according t​o WHO guidelines?","raw_text":"You are about to administer misoprostol to a woman who requires MTP in the 8th week of her pregnancy. What is the dose of this drug when given by the mucosal route, according to WHO guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200​ mcg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"600​ mcg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"400​ mcg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"800​ mcg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 800 mcg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 800 mcg</strong></p><p><strong>Misoprostol</strong> is given at a dose of <strong>800 mcg</strong> by <strong>mucosal route</strong> for MTP.</p>\n<p>Mucosal misoprostol administration has<strong> increased efficacy</strong> and <strong>minimizes gastrointestinal side effects</strong>. Hence, it is preferred over oral administration. </p>\n<p><strong>WHO guidelines for medical management</strong> in induced abortion for<strong> &lt;12 weeks</strong> of gestation:</p>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Combined regimen </strong>(recommended): 200 mg of oral Mifepristone followed 1-2 days later by 800 mcg of buccal, vaginal, or sublingual misoprostol</li>\n<li><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Misoprostol only regimen </strong>(alternative): 800 μg misoprostol administered vaginally, sublingually, or buccally</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9775","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g the methods of MTP?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the methods of MTP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain is more in medical me​thods of abortion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incomplete abortion is mor​e common in medical metho‌ds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleeding is prolonged in s​urgical methods","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Failure rate is low in sur​gical methods of abortion‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bleeding is prolonged in surgical methods","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bleeding is prolonged in surgical methods</strong></p><p><strong>Bleeding</strong> is <strong>prolonged</strong> and unpredictable in <strong>medical</strong> methods of abortion.</p>\n<p><strong>Comparison</strong> of drawbacks of <strong>medical and surgical methods</strong> of MTP:</p>\n<table border=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Factor</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Medical methods</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Surgical methods</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invasiveness</td>\n<td>Usually not invasive</td>\n<td>Invasive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pain</td>\n<td>More</td>\n<td>Less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vaginal bleeding</td>\n<td>Prolonged, unpredictable</td>\n<td>Light, predictable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incomplete abortion</td>\n<td>More common</td>\n<td>Uncommon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure rate</td>\n<td>2-5%</td>\n<td>1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Severe bleeding</td>\n<td>0.1%</td>\n<td>0.1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Infection rate</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anesthesia</td>\n<td>Usually not given</td>\n<td>Always given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time involved </td>\n<td>Multiple visits for follow-up </td>\n<td>Usually one visit<br/>No follow-ups required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9783","text":"A 21-year-old woman is bro​ught in a drowsy state fr‌om a PHC with complaints ​of fever, severe lower ab​dominal pain, and purulen​t vaginal discharge. Her ‌mother revealed that she ​had had an unsafe abortio‍n a week ago. On examinat‍ion, her abdomen was dist​ended, BP was 80/60 mmHg,​ PR was 140 bpm, and RR w‍as 36 breaths/ minute. Wh‌ich of the following is n​ot a causative agent of h​er present condition?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old woman is brought in a drowsy state from a PHC with complaints of fever, severe lower abdominal pain, and purulent vaginal discharge. Her mother revealed that she had had an unsafe abortion a week ago. On examination, her abdomen was distended, BP was 80/60 mmHg, PR was 140 bpm, and RR was 36 breaths/ minute. Which of the following is not a causative agent of her present condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clostridiu​m sordellii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Escheric​hia coli","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Escherichia coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Escherichia coli</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>toxic shock syndrome</strong> (TSS) following <strong>septic abortion.</strong><em> Escherichia coli</em> can be detected in septic abortion, but it is not a causative agent for TSS.</p>\n<p><strong>Organisms</strong> causing <strong>toxic shock syndrome</strong> in septic abortion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li>\n<li><em>Clostridium perfringens</em></li>\n<li><em>Clostridium sordellii</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical features of septic abortion</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever with chills and rigors</li>\n<li>Abdominal or chest pain</li>\n<li>Diarrhoea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Renal angle tenderness</li>\n<li>Offensive purulent vaginal discharge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical grading</strong> <strong>of septic abortion</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grade I - infection localized in the uterus</li>\n<li>Grade II - infection spreads to parametrium, tubes, ovaries, or pelvic peritoneum</li>\n<li>Grade III - generalized peritonitis, endotoxic shock, jaundice, or acute renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Empirical antibiotic</strong> therapy in septic abortion is started for the broadest microbial coverage. <strong>Piperacillin-tazobactam</strong> or <strong>carbapenem</strong> and <strong>clindamycin</strong> are given intravenously. Antibiotics can be changed once culture sensitivity reports are available.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9786","text":"Which of the following is ​not a risk factor for sti‌llbirth?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for stillbirth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal diabetes and hype​rtension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Illicit drug abuse by the ​mother","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Untreated seizure disorder​ in pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pregnancy following assist​ed reproductive technolog‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Untreated seizure disorder in pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Untreated seizure disorder in pregnancy</strong></p><p>An untreated seizure disorder in pregnancy is not a risk factor for stillbirth. However, the use of certain <strong>anti-epileptic drugs</strong> like valproate could lead to the development of <strong>neural tube defects</strong> in the baby.</p>\n<p>Recent studies haven't consistently shown that untreated women with epilepsy face a higher risk, though it's hard to rule out entirely because those who manage without medication usually have milder symptoms. Recent research found that birth defects were more common in children of women who used anticonvulsants. </p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> <strong>for</strong> <strong>stillbirth</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced maternal age</li>\n<li>African-American race</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li><strong>Illicit drug use</strong></li>\n<li>Maternal medical diseases - <strong>overt diabetes or chronic hypertension</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Assisted reproductive technology</strong></li>\n<li>Nulliparity</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Previous adverse pregnancy outcomes - prior preterm birth or IUGR</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: Stillbirth rates appear to be slightly increased in pregnancies conceived via assisted reproductive technology.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7106","text":"A young female has history​ of a spontaneous abortio‌n. She has had amenorrhea​ since then. Her FSH now ​is 6 mIU/ml. Which of the​ following can be a cause‌ for her amenorrhea?","raw_text":"A young female has history of a spontaneous abortion. She has had amenorrhea since then. Her FSH now is 6 mIU/ml. Which of the following can be a cause for her amenorrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pituitary ​dysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ovarian d​ysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First p​regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine ​synechiae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Uterine synechiae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Uterine synechiae</strong></p><p>The development of <strong>uterine synechiae</strong> is the most likely cause of amenorrhoea in the given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>Certain types of <strong>spontaneous abortions</strong> such as <strong>inevitable, incomplete</strong> and <strong>missed abortions</strong> might require medical assistance for complete removal of products of conception including procedures such as <strong>dilatation and curettage.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vigorous uterine curettage</strong> in early pregnancy <strong>damages the basal layer of endometrium</strong> leading to scarring and adhesions in the uterus that interferes with normal endometrial growth and shedding.This causes the clinical feature of <strong>secondary amenorrhea</strong> as seen in this patient.</p>\n<p>This <strong>intrauterine adhesions</strong> are known as <strong>uterine synechiae</strong> or <strong>Asherman syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>As there is <strong>no defect</strong> in the <strong>hypothalamo-pituitary-ovarian axis</strong> in the <strong>asherman syndrome</strong>, the levels of <strong>FSH</strong> remain within the <strong>normal range</strong> of 4 - 25 IU/L as seen in this patient.</p>\n<p>In the case of <strong>Pituitary dysfunction</strong> (option A) the <strong>FSH levels</strong> are usually <strong>decreased.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>ovarian dysfunction</strong> (option B) the negative feedback of ovarian hormones (estrogen) on pitutary and hypothalamus is lost. Hence, the levels of<strong> FSH is increased</strong> (≥ 25 IU).</p>\n<p><strong>Asherman syndrome</strong> is characterised by a<strong> spectrum of intra uterine scarring</strong> including thin adhesions, dense bands, or complete obliteration of the uterine cavity. It occurs due to <strong>endometrial damage</strong> following <strong>vigorous curettage</strong>, usually in miscarriage, or elective abortion. Damage may also occur from other <strong>uterine surgeries</strong> such as myomectomy or cesarean delivery or from an infection related to an intrauterine device.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 402\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Depending on the degree of scarring, patients may present  with amenorrhea, hypomenorrhea or recurrent pregnancy loss due to inadequate placentation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Asherman syndrome shows <strong>multiple filling defects</strong> on hysterosalpingography (HSG) or saline-infusion sonography. Definitive diagnosis requires <strong>hysteroscopy. Hysteroscopic lysis</strong> of adhesions is the preferred <strong>surgical treatment </strong>for the syndrome.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7DaeJj9b","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"19_diseases_of_the_orbit","title":"19.Diseases Of The Orbit","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB9051","text":"What is the most common ca​use of bilateral proptosi​s in children?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of bilateral proptosis in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Craniofacia​l dysostosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid oph​thalmopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurob​lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f474981ea7d74c24b941c375205846c2x285x144.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p>The most common cause of bilateral proptosis in children is <strong>neuroblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most common cause:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 623.766px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\"><strong>Unilateral proptosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 350.766px;\"><strong>Bilateral proptosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">In children: Orbital cellulitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 350.766px;\">In children: Neuroblastoma and leukemia (chloroma)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">In adults: Thyroid ophthalmopathy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 350.766px;\">In adults: Thyroid ophthalmopathy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows the bilateral proptosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9057","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not presents​ as pulsatile proptosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not presents as pulsatile proptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caroticocave​rnous fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Menin​gocele","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemangioma</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>hemangioma</strong> will <strong>not</strong> present with pulsatile proptosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of pulsatile proptosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulsating vascular lesions:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Caroticocavernous fistula- most common</strong></li>\n<li>Saccular aneurysm of ophthalmic artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Transmitted cerebral pulsations in conditions with a deficient orbital roof:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Congenital meningocele/meningoencephalocele</strong></li>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis</li>\n<li>Traumatic or operative hiatus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes of intermittent proptosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Orbital varix- most common</strong></li>\n<li>Periodic orbital edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes of reducible proptosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Orbital varix</strong></li>\n<li>Hemangioma</li>\n<li>Caroticocavernous fistula</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9058","text":"A patient presents with un​ilateral non-pulsatile pr​optosis, which is compres​sible and increases on be‌nding forward. No thrill ​or bruit is present. MRI ‍shows a retrobulbar mass ‌with enhancement. What is‍ the most likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A patient presents with unilateral non-pulsatile proptosis, which is compressible and increases on bending forward. No thrill or bruit is present. MRI shows a retrobulbar mass with enhancement. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carotid cave​rnous fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orbita​l varix","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital en​cephalocele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Orbital varix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Orbital varix</strong></p><p>The likely diagnosis from the above clinical scenario is <strong>orbital varix</strong>.</p>\n<p>The type of proptosis seen in <strong>orbital varix</strong> is <strong>non-pulsatile</strong>, <strong>intermittent, and reducible proptosis</strong>. On activities such as <strong>forward bending</strong> or lowering of the head, turning the head forcefully, hyperextension of the neck, and coughing, the varices fill up, causing proptosis, and then, they regress on shifting to a non-dependent position.</p>\n<p>All the other conditions listed, namely, carotid cavernous fistula, orbital encephalocele, and neurofibromatosis, present with <strong>pulsatile proptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9059","text":"A 26-year-old man with a h​istory of blunt trauma to​ the head 2 months earlie​r presented with rapidly ‌progressive swelling, pai​n, and diminishing vision‍ in the left eye. The phy‌sical examination of the ‍left eye revealed chemosi​s and pulsating proptosis‌. A bruit could be heard ​with a stethoscope placed‌ on the left temple. What‌ is the most probable dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man with a history of blunt trauma to the head 2 months earlier presented with rapidly progressive swelling, pain, and diminishing vision in the left eye. The physical examination of the left eye revealed chemosis and pulsating proptosis. A bruit could be heard with a stethoscope placed on the left temple. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carotid-cave​rnous fistula","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retro-orbit​al hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior orbital fissure s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orbital ap​ex syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carotid-cavernous fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carotid-cavernous fistula</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>carotid–cavernous fistula</strong>.</p>\n<p>All the given options are causes of <strong>proptosis</strong> <strong>following trauma,</strong> but among these, only a <strong>carotid-cavernous fistula</strong> presents with the <strong>classic triad</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pulsatile proptosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chemosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bruit</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Selective carotid angiography</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong>.</p>\n<p>A carotid-cavernous fistula requires a definitive treatment, which currently involves the <strong>endovascular technique</strong>. The fistula can usually be occluded with a <strong>balloon or coil,</strong> without the sacrifice of the carotid artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9060","text":"Which of the following con​ditions will present with​ pseudoproptosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions will present with pseudoproptosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupht​halmos","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudo​tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital h​emorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Buphthalmos","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Buphthalmos</strong></p><p><strong>Buphthalmos </strong>causes<strong> pseudoproptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The other listed conditions, namely, pseudotumors, orbital hemorrhage, and cavernous sinus thrombosis, produce actual proptosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Pseudoproptosis</strong> is a condition in which the eyeball appears to be proptosed, but there is no actual forward displacement.</p>\n<p>Causes of pseudoproptosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Buphthalmos</strong> is the term used when the eyeball enlarges in children who develop congenital glaucoma before the age of 3 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Axial high myopia-</strong> increase in the size of the eyeball in myopia causes pseudoproptosis</li>\n<li><strong>Retraction of the upper lid</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Enophthalmos of the opposite eye </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9052","text":"A 48-year-old lady present​s with unilateral mild ax​ial proptosis. On examina​tion, the following nail ‌and skin changes were see​n as shown. Which of the ‍following is the most app‌ropriate next step of inv‍estigation?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old lady presents with unilateral mild axial proptosis. On examination, the following nail and skin changes were seen as shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step of investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT scan to rule out mening​ioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG to rule out orbital ps​eudotumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T3 and T4 measurement to c​heck for thyrotoxicosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Doppler to rule out hemang​ioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T3 and T4 measurement to check for thyrotoxicosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8fbb683994ad459893446d83840c13d1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9dfdfbdc2024b94be1c999e415887dex590x348.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T3 and T4 measurement to check for thyrotoxicosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>thyroid acropachy</strong> and <strong>dermopathy</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Graves' disease</strong>. The most appropriate next step of the investigation is <strong>T3 </strong>and<strong> T4 measurement</strong> to check for <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Thyroid ophthalmopathy</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of<strong> proptosis</strong> in<strong> adults, </strong>which may present as unilateral or bilateral, axial or non-axial, painful or painless proptosis.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows unilateral proptosis in the right eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0772","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted with bilateral propto​sis, restriction of eye m​ovements, and chemosis. H‌er thyroid profile showed​ she was euthyroid. What ‍is the most probable caus‌e?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with bilateral proptosis, restriction of eye movements, and chemosis. Her thyroid profile showed she was euthyroid. What is the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital p​seudotumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid oph​thalmopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital ​lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thyroid ophthalmopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thyroid ophthalmopathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>thyroid ophthalmopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thyroid eye disease (TED) is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of both <strong>bilateral and unilateral proptosis</strong> in an adult in the euthyroid state. The main features of TED include <strong>lid retraction</strong>, <strong>proptosis</strong>, <strong>periorbital edema</strong>, <strong>chemosis</strong>, and <strong>restrictive myopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options-</p>\n<p>Option A- Orbital pseudotumor presents with painful eye movements periorbital edema, erythema, proptosis, ptosis, diplopia.</p>\n<p>Option C- Orbital lymphoma usually presents in the 7th decade, most commonly observed as a painless palpable mass in the superolateral quadrant.</p>\n<p>Option D- Orbital cellulitis is usually a unilateral condition with pain, proptosis, and chemosis.</p>\n<p><strong>NOTE</strong>: Thyroid eye disease can also, though less commonly, occur in euthyroid patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9054","text":"What is the most common cl​inical feature of Graves’​ ophthalmopathy?","raw_text":"What is the most common clinical feature of Graves’ ophthalmopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dalrympl​e’s sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exopht​halmos","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"von Grae​fe's sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mobius​’ sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dalrymple’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f77ff61b6384a79b229e03d916b65e9x590x348.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dalrymple’s sign</strong></p><p><strong>Dalrymple’s sign </strong>(retraction of the upper lids) is the <strong>most common</strong> clinical feature<strong> </strong>of <strong>Graves’ ophthalmopathy</strong>. It is caused by the overaction of Muller’s muscle. Levator palpebrae superioris <strong>(LPS) recession surgery</strong> may be done to correct upper lid retraction.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Dalrymple’s sign (retraction of the upper lids) in the right eye:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>NOSPECS classification</strong> by <strong>Werner</strong> reflecting the <strong>severity</strong> of thyroid ophthalmopathy.</p>\n<table border=\"o\" style=\"height: 172px; width: 667px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">Class</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Definition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">No physical signs or symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Only signs, no symptoms (signs limited to upper lid retraction, stare, lid lag, and proptosis of up to 22 mm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Soft tissue involvement (symptom and signs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Proptosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Extraocular muscle involvement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Corneal involvement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 71px;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 584px;\">Sight loss (optic nerve involvement)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 655px;\"><strong>NOSPECS</strong>: <strong><strong>N</strong></strong>o signs or symptoms; <strong>O</strong>nly signs; <strong>S</strong>oft tissue; <strong>P</strong>roptosis; <strong>E</strong>xtraocular muscle; <strong>C</strong>ornea; <strong>S</strong>ight loss </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9055","text":"A 40-year-old male present​s with heat intolerance a​nd weight loss for the pa​st few weeks. He has lost‌ 10 kgs despite having an​ increased appetite. On e‍xamination, he has a fine‌ tremor of the hands, and‍ his thyroid is enlarged.​ Lab investigations revea‌l low TSH and elevated T3​ and T4. What is the most‌ common ocular motility d‌efect seen in this condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male presents with heat intolerance and weight loss for the past few weeks. He has lost 10 kgs despite having an increased appetite. On examination, he has a fine tremor of the hands, and his thyroid is enlarged. Lab investigations reveal low TSH and elevated T3 and T4. What is the most common ocular motility defect seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevator r​estriction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Depress​or palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adducto​r palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevati​on palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Elevator restriction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Elevator restriction</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Grave's disease. The most common ocular motility defect seen in Grave's disease is <strong>elevator restriction</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs due to the <strong>fibrotic contracture</strong> of<strong> </strong>the <strong>inferior rectus muscle. </strong>The inferior rectus muscle has to relax during the elevation of the eye. The fibrosis of the muscle restricts its relaxation and thus elevation of the eye is restricted.</p>\n<p>There is an inability to look up (in both adducted and abducted positions) along with esotropia (fibrosis of the medial rectus). </p>\n<p>Only the <strong>belly</strong> of the muscle is involved in thyroid myopathy; the tendon is characteristically spared.</p>\n<p>Ocular motility restriction in order of frequency is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevation defect</li>\n<li>Abduction defect, due to fibrosis of the medial rectus</li>\n<li>Depression defect, due to fibrosis of the superior rectus</li>\n<li>Adduction defect, due to fibrosis of the lateral rectus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9053","text":"What is known as the infre​quent blinking seen in Gr​aves’ ophthalmopathy?","raw_text":"What is known as the infrequent blinking seen in Graves’ ophthalmopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mobius​’ sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"von Grae​fe's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enroth​’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stellwa​g’s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stellwag’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stellwag’s sign</strong></p><p><strong>Infrequent blinking</strong> is known as <strong>Stellwag’s sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9056","text":"A lady is referred from th​e medicine department for​ an eye exam due to gritt​y sensation in her eyes. ‌She is a known case of hy​perthyroidism. Her ophtha‍lmic examination shows th‌e following finding along‍ with the inability to lo​ok up. Which of the follo‌wing is not a treatment m​odality for this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A lady is referred from the medicine department for an eye exam due to gritty sensation in her eyes. She is a known case of hyperthyroidism. Her ophthalmic examination shows the following finding along with the inability to look up. Which of the following is not a treatment modality for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radi​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ster​oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-b​lockers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Orbital de​compression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Beta-blockers","question_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f194da62645140e1bc56cf4a3811fe8ax640x418.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Beta-blockers</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of grittiness in the eyes (due to ocular dryness), hyperthyroidism and the image showing exophthalmos are suggestive of <strong>Graves ophthalmopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> are <strong>not indicated</strong> in the treatment of <strong>graves ophthalmopathy</strong>, however, they are used in the symptomatic management of hyperthyroidism.</p>\n<p>Treatment of thyroid ophthalmopathy:</p>\n<p>(A) <strong>Non-surgical management</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic steroids</strong>- These form the <strong>mainstay</strong> of the treatment. Oral, intravenous or orbital steroid injections can be used depending upon patient indications. (Option B)</li>\n<li><strong>Radiotherapy</strong>- Orbital radiation given in a dose of 2000 rads over 10-14 days can be <strong>considered</strong> in cases where <strong>steroids</strong> are <strong>ineffective/ contraindicated</strong>. It is contraindicated in cases of extensive thyroid eye disease as it can worsen it. (Option A)</li>\n<li><strong>Teprotumumab</strong> (IGF-1R inhibitor)- is used for <strong>persistent, corticosteroid-resistant disease</strong> or non-response to first-line treatment. It is effective in reducing proptosis and clinical activity in patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(B) <strong>Surgical management</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Orbital decompression</strong>- It may be required for an acutely progressive optic neuropathy and/or exposure keratitis, in patients <strong>not responding</strong> to aggressive <strong>steroid</strong> therapy. (Option D)</li>\n<li><strong>Extraocular muscle surgery</strong>- Inferior rectus recession is the most commonly performed surgery. These should be <strong>performed</strong> <strong>after</strong> the <strong>orbital</strong> <strong>decompression</strong> since decompression may alter extraocular motility. Extraocular muscle surgery helps in achieving a <strong>binocular single vision</strong> in the primary gaze and reading position.</li>\n<li><strong>Eyelid surgery</strong>- This is done to correct eyelid retraction. Various surgeries that can be performed are mullerectomy, levator recession/ disinsertion, scleral grafts with LPS recession, recession of lower eyelid retractors, and blepharoplasty.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0773","text":"A patient presented with u​nilateral proptosis and b​ilateral abducent palsy. ​What is the most probable‌ cause ?","raw_text":"A patient presented with unilateral proptosis and bilateral abducent palsy. What is the most probable cause ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orbital p​seudotumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cavernous sinus thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bba873d8854f4f56a2050f911f779d9fx1280x1402.851093860268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cavernous sinus thrombosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cavernous sinus thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Features of cavernous sinus thrombosis include early involvement <strong>lateral rectus palsy</strong> (abducent nerve involvement) since it runs through the cavernous sinus. Oculomotor and trochlear nerve palsy occur later as they are related to the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus. Other signs are proptosis, severe supraorbital pain, edema in the mastoid region, absence of corneal sensation, and dilated pupil.</p>\n<p>Options A- <strong>Retinoblastoma</strong> occurs in young children and presents with unilateral proptosis with leukocoria, buphthalmos, and squint.</p>\n<p>Option B- <strong>Orbital pseudotumor</strong> presents with unilateral proptosis with diplopia, conjunctival chemosis. The inflammation is responsive to systemic corticosteroids.</p>\n<p>Option C- <strong>Orbital cellulitis</strong> presents with unilateral proptosis with pain and decreased ocular motility.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9062","text":"A 20-year-old man with a h​istory of repeated pain o​ver the medial canthus an​d chronic use of nasal de‌congestants presents to t​he OPD with high-grade fe‍ver and chills, headache,‌ and bilateral proptosis.‍ On further history, it i​s noted that the proptosi‌s began in the right eye,​ and a few days later, th‌e left eye was also affec‌ted. What is the most pro‍bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man with a history of repeated pain over the medial canthus and chronic use of nasal decongestants presents to the OPD with high-grade fever and chills, headache, and bilateral proptosis. On further history, it is noted that the proptosis began in the right eye, and a few days later, the left eye was also affected. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panopht​halmitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caroticocave​rnous fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cavernous sinus thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cavernous sinus thrombosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cavernous sinus thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pain over the medial canthus and use of nasal decongestants indicate that the patient was suffering from <strong>sinusitis</strong>, which can spread to cause orbital cellulitis, cavernous sinus thrombosis, or panopthalmitis.</p>\n<p>The differentiating feature here is the presentation <strong>pattern</strong> and <strong>bilaterality of the proptosis.</strong></p>\n<p>In cavernous sinus thrombosis, proptosis is <strong>initially unilateral but soon becomes bilateral</strong> due to the spread of infection to the contralateral sinus through intercavernous communication. Also, cavernous sinus thrombosis presents with <strong>high-grade fever with chills, rigor, and headache</strong>.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>orbital cellulitis and panophthalmitis</strong> are <strong>unilateral</strong> conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis of acute inflammatory proptosis:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 591.378px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\"><strong>Cavernous sinus thrombosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong> Orbital cellulitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.378px;\"><strong> Panophthalmitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Laterality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\"><strong>Initially unilateral but soon becomes bilateral</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Unilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.378px;\">Unilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Vision</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Not affected in early stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">affected</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.378px;\">Complete loss of vision </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Cornea and anterior chamber</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Clear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Clear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.378px;\">Hazy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\"><strong>Edema in the mastoid region</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\"><strong>Present </strong> <strong>(Pathognomonic of cavernous sinus thrombosis)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.378px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\"><strong>General symptoms with fever and prostration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\"><strong>Marked </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.378px;\">Mild</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9063","text":"Among the following, which​ muscle is affected first​ in cavernous sinus throm​bosis?","raw_text":"Among the following, which muscle is affected first in cavernous sinus thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior​ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior​ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial​ rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral​ rectus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83447a2c269b486eb5a3dfbd012ae0dcx1280x1402.851093860268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral rectus</strong></p><p>The first muscle to be affected in cavernous sinus thrombosis is the <strong>lateral rectus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ophthalmoplegia</strong> in <strong>cavernous sinus thrombosis </strong>occurs in the following sequence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>6<sup>th</sup> nerve palsy (lateral rectus palsy)</strong> occurring first of all as it <strong>passes through</strong> the cavernous sinus.</li>\n<li><strong>3<sup>rd</sup> and 4<sup>th</sup> nerves</strong> are involved later as they are related to the <strong>lateral wall</strong> of the cavernous sinus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1791","text":"MRI of a patient revealed ​features of right caverno​us sinus thrombosis. Whic​h of the following would ‌not be seen in this patie​nt?","raw_text":"MRI of a patient revealed features of right cavernous sinus thrombosis. Which of the following would not be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constricted right pupil in​ response to light","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Engorgement of the retinal​ veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ptosis of the right eyelid​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right opht​halmoplegia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Constricted right pupil in response to light","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d10d47341c754b43b4ee8d7267bd6fcfx1280x1402.851093860268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Constricted right pupil in response to light</strong></p><p><strong>Dilation</strong> of the <strong>pupil</strong> will be seen in cavernous sinus thrombosis due to the involvement of the 3<sup>rd</sup> cranial nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1788","text":"A 40-year-old male present​ed with a 1-day history o​f fever, headache, and bi​lateral periorbital and m‌id-facial swelling. He ha​d attempted to burst a fu‍runcle in the inner margi‌n of his right nostril 2 ‍days back. On examination​, marked facial and perio‌rbital swelling with chem​osis and proptosis are no‌ted. MRI is suggestive of‌ bilateral cavernous sinu‍s thrombosis. Which of th‍e following veins is resp‌onsible for this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male presented with a 1-day history of fever, headache, and bilateral periorbital and mid-facial swelling. He had attempted to burst a furuncle in the inner margin of his right nostril 2 days back. On examination, marked facial and periorbital swelling with chemosis and proptosis are noted. MRI is suggestive of bilateral cavernous sinus thrombosis. Which of the following veins is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla​ry veins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retromandi​bular veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial ​temporal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ophthalm​ic veins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ophthalmic veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a1885362fb94ef4a375ce5db038aa01x600x688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ophthalmic veins</strong></p><p>Infection from the <strong>dangerous area</strong> of the face (the area from the corners of the mouth to the bridge of the nose, including the nose and maxilla) spreads to the cavernous sinus via the <strong>ophthalmic veins.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9064","text":"A child is brought to the ​OPD with fever, severe pa​in, and swelling of the l​eft eye. He was bitten by‌ a dog over the eye a few​ days back. On examinatio‍n, there is mild proptosi‌s with restriction of ocu‍lar movements. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the OPD with fever, severe pain, and swelling of the left eye. He was bitten by a dog over the eye a few days back. On examination, there is mild proptosis with restriction of ocular movements. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endopht​halmitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panopht​halmitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Preseptal ​cellulitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Orbital cellulitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2d8e92469d34898be975b73ccd528fd.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39fad207c52c49c3af73c699c896cc15x617x343.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Orbital cellulitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>orbital cellulitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the acute inflammation of the soft tissues of the orbit <strong>behind the orbital septum</strong>. It can occur due to exogenous infection (penetrating injuries) or extension of an endogenous infection (dental infection). </p>\n<p>It presents with fever, severe pain, and eye swelling which increases with movement, chemosis, axial proptosis, and <strong>restriction of ocular movements</strong>. Complications include subperiosteal abscess, orbital abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis, and general septicemia.</p>\n<p>The image shows orbital cellulitis in the left eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Endophthalmitis</strong> is defined as the inflammation of the inner structures of the eyeball, i.e., uveal tissue and retina, associated with the pouring of exudates into the vitreous cavity, anterior chamber, and posterior chamber.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Panophthalmitis</strong> is an intense purulent inflammation of the whole eyeball, including Tenon’s capsule.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Preseptal cellulitis</strong> refers to infection of the subcutaneous tissues anterior to the orbital septum. In this condition, proptosis is absent and ocular movements are normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6771","text":"An 8-year-old child presen​ts with fever and painful​ swelling of the left eye​. He is being treated for‌ acute ethmoiditis for 5 ​days. On examination, the‍ visual acuity in the lef‌t eye is 6/12, there is c‍hemosis, axial proptosis,​ limitation of ocular mot‌ility in all directions, ​and RAPD. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child presents with fever and painful swelling of the left eye. He is being treated for acute ethmoiditis for 5 days. On examination, the visual acuity in the left eye is 6/12, there is chemosis, axial proptosis, limitation of ocular motility in all directions, and RAPD. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic n​euritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rhabdomy​osarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Orbital cellulitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Orbital cellulitis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis of this patient is <strong>orbital cellulitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Orbital cellulitis is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>unilateral proptosis</strong> in <strong>children.</strong> Ethmoidal sinusitis is the most common cause of orbital cellulitis in children.</p>\n<p>Orbital cellulitis presents with pain, lid edema, erythema, chemosis, and axial proptosis. Decreased ocular motility and elevated intraocular pressure can be seen. Systemic symptoms include malaise and fever. A rapid loss of vision is due to the involvement of the optic nerve. With posterior exenteration cavernous sinus thrombosis, subdural empyema, and intracranial abscess develop.</p>\n<p><strong>WARNING SIGNS:</strong> Dilated pupil, marked ophthalmoplegia, loss of vision, afferent pupillary defect, papilledema, and violaceous lids.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9065","text":"What is the surgery of cho​ice for panophthalmitis?","raw_text":"What is the surgery of choice for panophthalmitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Evisce​ration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enucl​eation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exente​ration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Debu​lking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Evisceration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Evisceration</strong></p><p>The surgery of choice for <strong>panophthalmitis</strong> is an <strong>evisceration</strong>. Evisceration gives a better cosmetic outlook after the surgery and is hence preferred. <strong>Enucleation</strong> is contraindicated in panophthalmitis due to the risk of meningitis through the exposed meningeal sheath of the optic nerve stump. </p>\n<p>Some definitions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evisceration-</strong> <strong>removal of the contents of the eyeball, </strong>leaving behind the<strong> sclera</strong>. It is done in <strong>panophthalmitis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Enucleation-</strong> <strong>removal of the eyeball, with the maximum part of the optic nerve</strong>. Extraocular muscles are left behind. The length of the optic nerve removed is <strong>10 to 15 mm</strong>. Absolute indications for enucleation are <strong>retinoblastoma</strong> and<strong> malignant melanoma.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exenteration</strong>- mutilating surgery in which all the <strong>contents</strong> of the<strong> orbits</strong> along with the<strong> periorbita</strong> are<strong> removed</strong>. It is indicated for<strong> malignant tumors</strong> arising from the orbital structures or spreading from the eyeball.</li>\n<li><strong>Debulking-</strong> reduction of as much of the bulk (volume) of a tumor as possible. Nowadays,<strong> debulking</strong> of the orbit is<strong> preferred</strong> over<strong> exenteration</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6768","text":"A 72-year-old female prese​nts with normal visual ac​uity, diminished corneal ​sensation, and global imp‌airment of ocular motilit​y of the right eye. The p‍atient also has a severe ‌boring headache. The MRI ‍shows an enhancing mass w​ithin the right cavernous‌ sinus. The pain and opht​halmoplegia respond drama‌tically to corticosteroid‌ therapy. What is the mos‍t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old female presents with normal visual acuity, diminished corneal sensation, and global impairment of ocular motility of the right eye. The patient also has a severe boring headache. The MRI shows an enhancing mass within the right cavernous sinus. The pain and ophthalmoplegia respond dramatically to corticosteroid therapy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carotid cavernous sinus fi​stula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meningioma of the cavernou​s sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal car​otid aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tolosa- Hu​nt syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tolosa- Hunt syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tolosa- Hunt syndrome</strong></p><p>The patient presents with acute ophthalmoplegia with the involvement of ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve that <strong>responds promptly to steroid</strong> therapy. The most likely diagnosis is Tolosa-Hunt syndrome.</p>\n<p>It is an idiopathic, sterile inflammation that primarily affects the cavernous sinus. It is characterized by painful acute ophthalmoplegia <strong>with or without</strong> the involvement of the optic nerve and the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve.</p>\n<p>The pain is described as gnawing or boring and may precede ophthalmoplegia.</p>\n<p>The inflammation and pain in patients typically responds rapidly and dramatically to corticosteroid therapy. However, it is a diagnosis of exclusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6769","text":"The clinical distinction b​etween a cavernous sinus ​syndrome and an orbital a​pex syndrome is best made‌ by the dysfunction of wh​ich cranial nerve?","raw_text":"The clinical distinction between a cavernous sinus syndrome and an orbital apex syndrome is best made by the dysfunction of which cranial nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CN​ II","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CN ​III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CN​ V","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CN​ VI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CN II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CN II</strong></p><p>The clinical distinction between a cavernous sinus syndrome and an orbital apex syndrome is made by the dysfunction of the <strong>optic nerve (CN II)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The optic nerve passes through the optic canal and does not enter the cavernous sinus. Patients with orbital apex syndrome manifest complete ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, decreased corneal sensation, and vision loss. They usually develop optic nerve signs such as relative afferent pupillary defect. Proptosis is often present, but patients do not always complain of pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9067","text":"A patient was being evalua​ted for retro-orbital pai​n in the ward. After mult​iple investigations, he w‌as diagnosed with Tolosa-​Hunt syndrome. Which of t‍he following findings wil‌l not be seen in this pat‍ient?","raw_text":"A patient was being evaluated for retro-orbital pain in the ward. After multiple investigations, he was diagnosed with Tolosa-Hunt syndrome. Which of the following findings will not be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensory loss over forehead​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Afferent pup​illary defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pto​sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contralateral lateral rect​us palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contralateral lateral rectus palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contralateral lateral rectus palsy</strong></p><p><strong>Ipsilateral</strong> <strong>lateral rectus palsy</strong> due to 6<sup>th</sup> cranial nerve involvement is seen in <strong>Tolosa–Hunt syndrome, </strong>not contralateral.</p>\n<p>Tolosa–Hunt syndrome refers to <strong>idiopathic, nonspecific, granulomatous inflammation,</strong> involving the <strong>superior orbital fissure</strong> and/or <strong>orbital apex</strong> and/or <strong>cavernous sinus</strong>. It is a unilateral eye condition.</p>\n<p>Clinically, it manifests as <strong>painful</strong> <strong>ophthalmoplegia</strong> and depending on the extent of involvement, it may present as superior orbital fissure syndrome or orbital apex syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Superior orbital fissure syndrome</strong> is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain, typically labeled as retro-orbital ache</li>\n<li>Sensory loss/disturbances in the distribution of the ophthalmic division of the 5<sup>th</sup> cranial nerve</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral ophthalmoplegia due to the involvement of the 3<sup>rd</sup>, 4<sup>th</sup>, and 6<sup>th</sup> cranial nerve involvement</li>\n<li>Ptosis, due to 3<sup>rd</sup> nerve paralysis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Orbital apex syndrome</strong> includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Features of <strong>superior orbital fissure syndrom</strong>e described above</li>\n<li>Features of <strong>optic nerve </strong>involvement, i.e., early visual loss and afferent pupillary defect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CiOHqggG","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"19_ctev__genu_varum_and_valgum","title":"19.Ctev, Genu Varum And Valgum","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB5671","text":"A 3-month-old infant was b​rought to the clinic with​ the given malformation p‌resent since birth. What ‌is the primary deformity ​in this condition?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old infant was brought to the clinic with the given malformation present since birth. What is the primary deformity in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Convex lat​eral border","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdu​ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Concave me​dial border","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Addu​ction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adduction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65cd15acf93745afaf9ed399c577236b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adduction</strong></p><p><strong>Adduction</strong> of the forefoot is a primary deformity in <strong>congenital talipes equinus varus</strong> (CTEV). </p>\n<p>The five primary deformities in CTEV are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cavus</strong>- excessive arching of the foot</li>\n<li><strong>Adduction</strong> of forefoot</li>\n<li><strong>Varus</strong>- inversion and adduction of foot </li>\n<li><strong>Equinus</strong>- increased plantar flexion </li>\n<li><strong>Internal tibial torsion</strong>- medial rotation of the tibia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5672","text":"Which of the following tes​ts can be performed in a ​child with CTEV to detect‌ tibial torsion?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests can be performed in a child with CTEV to detect tibial torsion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsifle​xion test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ortolan​i's test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plumb l​ine test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Barlow​'s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plumb line test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20ffaf7b0e3d4810a0d0acc68faa7f2fx1280x1356.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plumb line test</strong></p><p>The<strong> plumb line test</strong> helps to detect<strong> tibial torsion</strong> which is seen in congenital talipes equinus varus <strong>(CTEV).</strong></p>\n<p>The child is made to sit at a table with both the lower limbs hanging from the edge. A line is drawn from the <strong>center</strong> of the <strong>patella</strong> to the <strong>tibial tubercle</strong>. This is called the<strong> plumb line</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Normally,</strong> when extended down, it should cut the foot at the <strong>first or second inter-metatarsal space</strong>. </p>\n<p>In <strong>CTEV,</strong> due to medial rotation of the tibia <strong>plumb line</strong> cuts the <strong>third or </strong><strong>fourth inter-metatarsal space</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: A <strong>dorsiflexion</strong> test is done in the screening of clubfoot. In a newborn, the dorsum of the foot can be brought in contact with the tibia by <strong>dorsiflexion</strong> but not in CTEV.</p>\n<p>Options B and D:<strong> Ortolani's</strong> and <strong>Barlow's</strong> tests are performed in developmental dysplasia of the hip (<strong>DDH</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5700","text":"Which angle is depicted in​ the radiograph below?","raw_text":"Which angle is depicted in the radiograph below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gissan​e angle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meary'​s angle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bohler'​s angle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kite’s​ angle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kite’s angle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8ec906e34dc645c8bcd514321e7686d5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c8800c044814692a40e4fdd9bd7bbefx1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kite’s angle</strong></p><p>The given radiograph shows <strong>K</strong><strong>ite’s</strong> <strong>angle</strong>, which is the angle between the <strong>long axis</strong> of the<strong> talus</strong> and<strong> </strong>the<strong> long axis </strong>of the <strong>calcaneum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The normal Kite's angle is 30-55 degrees. In <strong>CTEV,</strong> the Kite's angle is <strong>less than 25 degrees</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5677","text":"What is the right order of​ correction of deformitie​s in an infant with CTEV?‌","raw_text":"What is the right order of correction of deformities in an infant with CTEV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cavus, adduction, varus, e​quinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Valgus, abduction, equinus​, cavus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cavus, abduction, varus, e​quinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavus, adduction, valgus, ​equinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cavus, adduction, varus, equinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ac4833dc0f74b57865e8a2d045f2d82x1280x1442.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cavus, adduction, varus, equinus</strong></p><p>The right order of correction of CTEV is <strong>c</strong>avus followed by<strong> a</strong>dduction, then <strong>v</strong>arus, and lastly <strong>e</strong>quinus <strong>(CAVE).</strong></p>\n<p>An <strong>incorrect sequence</strong> of correction or overtreatment of CTEV can result in the development of the <strong>rocker-bottom foot</strong>. It is characterized by a prominent heel and a convex sole.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5682","text":"A pediatrician referred a ​newborn with the given de​formity to the orthopedic‌s department. Ponseti's m‌ethod was planned for cor​rection. Which of the fol‌lowing is incorrect regar‌ding this technique?","raw_text":"A pediatrician referred a newborn with the given deformity to the orthopedics department. Ponseti's method was planned for correction. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Equinus is c​orrected last","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Below knee cast is applied​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Head of the talus acts as ​the fulcrum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment should be starte​d within first 2 weeks of​ life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Below knee cast is applied","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28a9d6d2fb614bcfbd1f5c6e80b0e64c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Below knee cast is applied</strong></p><p>In <strong>Ponseti's</strong> method, an <strong>above-knee cast</strong> is used to correct clubfoot.</p>\n<p>Ponseti's method is the currently used method for <strong>conservative management</strong> of <strong>CTEV.</strong> Previously Kite's method was followed.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 274px; width: 395px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.562px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.438px;\"><strong>Ponseti's method</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\"><strong>Kite's method</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.562px;\"><strong>Treatment start</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.438px;\">&lt;2 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">1 month</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.562px;\"><strong>Order of correction</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.438px;\">\n<p>cavus→adduction→ varus→ equinus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">adduction → inversion →equinus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.562px;\"><strong>Fulcrum </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.438px;\">Head of the talus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Calcaneocuboid joint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.562px;\"><strong>POP type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.438px;\">Above knee cast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Below knee cast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.562px;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.438px;\">6-8 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">6-9 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5688","text":"A 1-year-old boy was under​going treatment for CTEV ​with a POP cast for the p‌ast 6 months. However, hi‌s deformities have not im​proved. What is the next ‌best step in management?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old boy was undergoing treatment for CTEV with a POP cast for the past 6 months. However, his deformities have not improved. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CTEV ​shoes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dillwyn Eva​ns procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Turco's ​procedure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triple ar​throdesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Turco's procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Turco&#x27;s procedure</strong></p><p>The next best step, in this case, is <strong>posteromedial soft tissue release</strong> (PMSTR), also known as <strong>Turco's procedure</strong>.</p>\n<p>By 1 year of age, posteromedial soft tissue gets contracted. This causes difficulty in the manipulation of the joint. Therefore, PMSTR is done and tight soft tissue structures (tendons, ligaments, capsule) on the posterior and medial side of the foot are cut.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5697","text":"An infant undergoing treat​ment for CTEV has just st​arted walking. Which of t‌he following is false reg‌arding the shoes that he ​will be prescribed to wea‌r?","raw_text":"An infant undergoing treatment for CTEV has just started walking. Which of the following is false regarding the shoes that he will be prescribed to wear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The straight inner border ​prevents cavus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Outer shoe raise prevents ​foot inversion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of heel prevents e​quinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These shoes can be used un​til 5 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The straight inner border prevents cavus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/552e600598274681bd428f416f51937ax1200x1399.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed95c9e8b466482a974e02eb3b672c36x1280x955.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The straight inner border prevents cavus</strong></p><p>When the child starts walking, <strong>CTEV shoes</strong> are prescribed for the daytime. The <strong>straight inner border</strong> in CTEV shoes prevents <strong>adduction,</strong> not cavus.</p>\n<p>CTEV shoes are prescribed when the child starts walking and are used up to <strong>5 years of age</strong>. The <strong>straight inner border</strong> prevents <strong>adduction</strong>, while the <strong>absence of heel</strong> prevents the <strong>equinus. </strong>An<strong> </strong><strong>outer shoe raise</strong> is present to prevent <strong>foot inversion.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2799","text":"A 12-year-old with neglect​ed CTEV and muscle wastin​g is brought to the hospi‌tal. A triple arthrodesis‌ is planned. Which of the​ following joints will no‌t be fused?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old with neglected CTEV and muscle wasting is brought to the hospital. A triple arthrodesis is planned. Which of the following joints will not be fused?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Taloc​rural","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Calcane​ocuboid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Talona​vicular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Taloca​lcaneal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Talocrural","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e907356156384cc6918e6f27c3ac4db6x1280x1079.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Talocrural</strong></p><p>The talocrural joint (ankle joint) is not involved in <strong>triple arthrodesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Triple arthrodesis involves the <strong>fusion</strong> of <strong>talonavicular,</strong> <strong>talocalcaneal</strong> and <strong>calcaneocuboid</strong> joints.</p>\n<p>Triple arthrodesis is done for <strong>recurrent or persistent</strong> clubfoot deformity in older children (chronic cases). It is done after 10-12 years of age as before this age bones are not fully ossified, therefore fusion may not be adequate. Inadequate fusion is known as pseudoarthrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5692","text":"Which of the following met​hods can be used for exte​rnal fixation in a patien‌t with resistant CTEV?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods can be used for external fixation in a patient with resistant CTEV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dwyer's ​procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Davis p​rocedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Joshi's​ method","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dillwyn Evan​'s procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Joshi's method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Joshi&#x27;s method</strong></p><p><strong>Joshi's method</strong> (Joshi's external stabilisation system - JESS) is a method of <strong>external fixation</strong> used in <strong>CTEV.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment by external fixators is a recent concept in the management of CTEV. It is done for difficult cases. </p>\n<p>There are two types of external fixator frames:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ilizarov's</li>\n<li>Joshi's (JESS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5714","text":"A child was brought to the​ orthopaedic OPD for eval​uation of the given knee ‌deformity. What is it kno‌wn as?","raw_text":"A child was brought to the orthopaedic OPD for evaluation of the given knee deformity. What is it known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genu re​curvatum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genu ​varus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genu ​valgum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Windswept​ deformity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Genu valgum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/720e80b1141e48c09c2d359fa7b74fcc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ecfa11c6063c42eeac37e299e7ebca2cx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Genu valgum</strong></p><p>The deformity shown is <strong>genu valgum</strong>.</p>\n<p>A normal knee has 5-7 degrees physiological outward deviation (valgum). Any increase or decrease in angle can result in knee deformities. </p>\n<p>Knee deformities are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Genu valgus (valgum)</strong> or <strong>knock knee</strong> - Knees are abnormally approximated and the ankles are divergent.</li>\n<li><strong>Genu varus (varum)</strong> or <strong>bow knee</strong> - Knees are abnormally divergent and the ankles are approximated.</li>\n<li><strong>Windswept deformity</strong> - Abnormal valgus deformity of one knee with varus of the other.</li>\n<li><strong>Genu recurvatum</strong> - Hyperextension at the knee joint with backwards bending.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5754","text":"A 7-year-old boy fractured​ the lateral condyle of h​is femur. He developed ma‌lunion as a complication.‌ Which deformity is he mo​st likely to develop?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy fractured the lateral condyle of his femur. He developed malunion as a complication. Which deformity is he most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genu re​curvatum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genu ​varus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genu ​valgus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Windswept​ deformity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Genu valgus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Genu valgus</strong></p><p>The boy is most likely to develop <strong>genu valgus </strong>subsequent to lateral condyle fracture of the femur.</p>\n<p><strong>Fracture of lateral condyle femur</strong> will result in injury to the lateral growth plate of the femur, and thus only the medial condyle will continue to grow with age resulting in <strong>genu valgus.</strong></p>\n<p>Similarly, <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>medial growth plate </strong>of the<strong> femur</strong> will cause the lateral condyle to grow only with age, resulting in <strong>genu varus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5765","text":"What is the most common ca​use of acquired genu recu​rvatum?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of acquired genu recurvatum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ric​kets","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebra​l palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Poliom​yelitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malunited fractures around​ the knee","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Poliomyelitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b2f3a427c994bc3aff1978a9e8e5989x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Poliomyelitis</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>acquired</strong> <strong>genu recurvatum</strong> is <strong>poliomyelitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of genu recurvatum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital (due to abnormal intrauterine position)</li>\n<li>Acquired \n<ul>\n<li>Neurological disorders like <strong>polio</strong>, <strong>cerebral palsy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rickets</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Malunion</strong></li>\n<li>Knee fractures</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5716","text":"Which of the following is ​a feature of Blount's dis​ease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a feature of  Blount's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coxa ​valgus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genu ​valgus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coxa ​varus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genu ​varus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Genu varus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Genu varus</strong></p><p><strong>Genu varus </strong>is a feature of Blount's disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Blount's disease</strong>, is a growth disorder of proximal tibial physis that causes the lower leg to angle inwards resulting in genu varus.</p>\n<p>Triad seen in Blount's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral idiopathic genu varus</li>\n<li>Genu recurvatum</li>\n<li>Internal rotation of tibia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"DHqstZB_","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"19_surgical_gi_disorders","title":"19.Surgical Gi Disorders","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5146","text":"A young boy came to visit ‍his mother admitted to th​e antenatal ward for her ‍delivery. You are the pae‌diatrician on rounds in t‍he adjacent post-natal cu​bical. His speech was ver​y different from a normal​ child. Out of curiosity,‌ you examine his oral cav‌ity and the following fin​ding is seen. What would ‌be the most likely cause ​of this condition?","raw_text":"A young boy came to visit his mother admitted to the antenatal ward for her delivery. You are the paediatrician on rounds in the adjacent post-natal cubical. His speech was very different from a normal child. Out of curiosity, you examine his oral cavity and the following finding is seen. What would be the most likely cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormally short lingual f‍renulum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Abnormally long lingual fr‍enulum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fissure‍d tongue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormally ‍large tongue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abnormally short lingual frenulum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9c471baa95346dc8975da25a6c9ac92.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acbf986f8e61435d95c59ed60358bd9ex985x298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abnormally short lingual frenulum</strong></p><p>The given image shows an abnormally <strong>short lingual frenulum</strong> called <strong>Ankyloglossia.</strong></p>\n<p>Ankyloglossia is also called “<strong>tongue-tie</strong>” due to an abnormally short lingual frenulum that can hinder tongue movement. It can interfere with speech or feeding. In such cases, surgical correction is indicated.</p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>tongue tie</strong> and the classic <strong>W-shaped</strong> appearance of the tongue <strong>on protrusion</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Fissured tongue</strong> (Option C) (scrotal tongue or lingua plicata) is a benign malformation in which numerous<strong> small furrows</strong> or deep grooves are present on the <strong>dorsal surface</strong> of the tongue. Food particles and debris may become trapped in the fissures, resulting in irritation, inflammation, and halitosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Macroglossia </strong>(Option D), or an abnormally large tongue, may be seen in disorders like <strong>Down's syndrome</strong>, Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome, Hurler's syndrome, and cretinism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5160","text":"Sirish, a young talented s‍inger failed to make it t​hrough the Music Idol aud‍ition. Deeply affected by‌ this, he drank a full bo‍ttle of a drain unclogger​. He was rushed to the ER​ with froth in the mouth.​ Which of the following c‌ould be done as the first‌ step in treatment?","raw_text":"Sirish, a young talented singer failed to make it through the Music Idol audition. Deeply affected by this, he drank a full bottle of a drain unclogger. He was rushed to the ER with froth in the mouth. Which of the following could be done as the first step in treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer‍ mild acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adminis‍ter milk","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Administer‍ rice water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gag the child to induce em‍esis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Administer milk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Administer milk</strong></p><p>The recommended <strong>first step</strong> in the case of <strong>alkali ingestion (most commonly </strong>seen with drain uncloggers/ house hold cleaning agents) is dilution through the <strong>administration of milk or water.</strong></p>\n<p>Contraindications in a case of caustic (strong acid or alkali) ingestion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neutralization</li>\n<li>Induced emesis</li>\n<li>Gastric lavage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5161","text":"Mala, a young student of c‍lass 9 accidentally pipet​tes sulphuric acid while ‍performing volumetric ana‌lysis in her chemistry la‍b. Which type of mucosal ​damage would you expect i​n her?","raw_text":"Mala, a young student of class 9 accidentally pipettes sulphuric acid while performing volumetric analysis in her chemistry lab. Which type of mucosal damage would you expect in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liquefacti‍ve necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coagulativ‍e necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Saponifica‍tion of fat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thermic ‍reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coagulative necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coagulative necrosis</strong></p><p>Acid ingestion produces<strong> coagulative necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Acid ingestion produces coagulative necrosis and a somewhat protective <strong>thick eschar</strong>. The amount of acidic agents usually ingested is low as they are generally bitter.</p>\n<p><strong>Alkali ingestion:</strong> Most cases (70%) of caustic ingestions are accidental. <strong>Drain uncloggers</strong> are most common as they are tasteless so more is ingested. <strong>Alkali</strong> ingestion produces <strong>liquefactive necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9679","text":"A 48-hours-old boy baby bo‍rn at 35 weeks of gestati​on regurgitates the feeds‍ given to him. Abdominal ‌examination reveals an ai‍rless scaphoid abdomen. A​ contrast study is perfor​med and the findings are ​as shown below. What is t‌he possible diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 48-hours-old boy baby born at 35 weeks of gestation regurgitates the feeds given to him. Abdominal examination reveals an airless scaphoid abdomen. A contrast study is performed and the findings are as shown below. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophageal atresia in TEF ‍type A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Esophageal atresia in TEF ‍type D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal atresia in TEF ‍type C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal atresia in TEF ‍type B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Esophageal atresia in TEF type A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf32b108a3ab44d1929df7f0a08738a8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f854e245a1b64714915fb01c89757dbbx1280x3561.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Esophageal atresia in TEF type A</strong></p><p>The defect demonstrated in the contrast study depicts <strong>oesophagal atresia (EA). </strong>Isolated oesophagal atresia falls under <strong>tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) type A</strong>.</p>\n<p>Oesophagal atresia is the <strong>most common congenital anomaly of the oesophagus</strong>, &gt;90% have an associated TEF. </p>\n<p><strong>Plain radiography</strong> in the evaluation of respiratory distress might reveal a coiled feeding tube in the <strong>oesophagal pouch</strong> and/or an <strong>air-distended stomach,</strong> indicating the presence of a coexisting TEF. Conversely, pure EA can manifest as an <strong>airless scaphoid abdomen</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following classification was given by Gross:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5155","text":"A full-term infant with a ‍history of polyhydramnios​ in utero presents with r‍espiratory distress soon ‌after birth. On examinati‍on frothing at the mouth ​is observed. What would b​e the immediate investiga​tion of choice?","raw_text":"A full-term infant with a history of polyhydramnios in utero presents with respiratory distress soon after birth. On examination frothing at the mouth is observed. What would be the immediate investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest ra‍diography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral neck‍ radiography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Passing a nasogastric tube‍ with subsequent chest ra​diography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erect X-r‍ay abdomen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Passing a nasogastric tube with subsequent chest radiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62a31838267c4fd4983c5dfb6d12e3e5x279x466.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Passing a nasogastric tube with subsequent chest radiography</strong></p><p>Newborn presenting with the above features is suggestive of a case of TEF with possible esophageal atresia. <strong> </strong>This can be confirmed with a chest X-ray image showing a coiled nasogastric tube in the esophageal pouch, as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Indicators of esophageal atresia with or without a tracheo-esophageal fistula (TEF) are a newborn presenting with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early-onset <strong>respiratory distress </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Frothing at the mouth</strong> and nose</li>\n<li>History of polyhydramnios</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Radiological findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Passing a nasogastric tube with subsequent chest radiography will reveal a <strong>coiled nasogastric tube in the esophageal pouch.</strong></li>\n<li>If an <strong>air-distended stomach </strong>is also present, it indicates the presence of a <strong>coexisting TEF.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pure EA</strong> can manifest as an<strong> airless scaphoid abdomen</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5153","text":"Milind, a young child is b‍rought to you for recurre​nt respiratory symptoms a‍nd dysphagia. After rulin‌g out other causes, you s‍uspect a vascular anomaly​ that could be compressin​g the esophagus to be cau​sing the symptoms. A CECT‌ is done and the findings‌ are as shown below. Whic​h of the following vascul‌ar anomalies is most like​ly to cause this conditio‍n?","raw_text":"Milind, a young child is brought to you for recurrent respiratory symptoms and dysphagia. After ruling out other causes, you suspect a vascular anomaly that could be compressing the esophagus to be causing the symptoms. A CECT is done and the findings are as shown below. Which of the following vascular anomalies is most likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aberrant left subclavian a‍rtery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aberrant right subclavian ‍artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left sided ‍azygous vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Truncus a‍rteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aberrant right subclavian artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f2c6390213144e3285c85cfaea5a8c64.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aberrant right subclavian artery</strong></p><p>The given case scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>d</strong><strong>ysphagia lusoria</strong>, which is caused most likely by the<strong> aberrant right subclavian artery</strong>. The given image suggests the presence of the right aberrant subclavian artery.</p>\n<p>Dysphagia lusoria is dysphagia produced due to extrinsic compression of the oesophagus by a developmental vascular anomaly, often an aberrant right subclavian artery or right-sided or double aortic arch.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5163","text":"You are a new intern poste‍d in the department of pe​diatrics. The lab reports‍ of a newborn baby admitt‌ed to the SNCU arrive and‍ the ABG report is as bel​ow. Which congenital anom​aly would you suspect in ​such a setting of electro‌lyte and acid-base imbala‌nce? Parameter Value pH 7​.55 PaCO2 44 mm of Hg HCO‌3- 30 mEq/L PaO2 92 mm of​ Hg SaO2 97% Potassium 3.‍3 mEq/L Chloride 85 mEq/L‌","raw_text":"<p>You are a new intern posted in the department of pediatrics. The lab reports of a newborn baby admitted to the SNCU arrive and the ABG report is as below. Which congenital anomaly would you suspect in such a setting of electrolyte and acid-base imbalance?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 397px; width: 234px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Parameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">pH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">7.55</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">PaCO2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">44 mm of Hg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">HCO3-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">30 mEq/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">PaO2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">92 mm of Hg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">SaO2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">97%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Potassium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">3.3 mEq/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Chloride</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">85 mEq/L</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric ‍volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trachea-esophageal fistula‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital hypertrophic py‍loric stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malrotation ‍with volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong></p><p><strong>Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, </strong>an electrolyte and acid-base imbalance characteristically seen in <strong>congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (CHPS)</strong>.</p>\n<p> A pH of <strong>7.55</strong> indicates <strong>alkalosis </strong>and plasma HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> is 30 mmol/L, which is in the higher range. Both these findings indicate metabolic alkalosis. </p>\n<p>Infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis develop a characteristic metabolic abnormality. i.e <strong>Hypokalemic</strong> <strong>hypochloremic</strong> <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>alkalosis</strong> with <strong>paradoxical</strong> <strong>aciduria</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent vomiting results in loss of hydrochloric acid leading to the development of <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>alkalosis</strong> (due to loss of H<sup>+</sup>) and <strong>hypochloremia</strong> (due to loss of Cl<sup>-</sup>).</li>\n<li>Kidneys try to compensate for alkalosis by secreting Na<sup>+</sup> and HCO3<sup>-</sup>. However, loss of sodium stimulates the secretion of aldosterone through the RAAS pathway.</li>\n<li>The aldosterone causes active reabsorption of sodium in exchange for K<sup>+</sup> and H<sup>+</sup> ions.</li>\n<li>Loss of potassium ions leads to the development of <strong>hypokalemia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Loss of H<sup>+</sup> ions in urine lowers the urinary pH resulting in aciduria. It is called <strong>paradoxical</strong> <strong>aciduria</strong>, since the body initially lost H<sup>+</sup> ions due to loss of HCl, and instead of compensating for that loss, the body is losing more H<sup>+</sup> ions through urine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CHPS typically presents with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-bilious vomiting</strong>, which is progressive, maybe projectile, occurring immediately after a feed, usually presents after the<strong> 3<sup>rd</sup> week of age</strong>. </li>\n<li>As the vomiting continues, a progressive loss of fluid, hydrogen ions, chloride ions, and potassium leads to <strong>hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hyperbilirubinemia</strong> is the most common clinical association of pyloric stenosis, which is also known as the <strong>icteropyloric syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5164","text":"A 5-week-old infant presen‍ted with non-bilious proj​ectile vomiting and letha‍rgy for 2 days. On abdomi‌nal examination, an olive‍-like mass was palpated. ​Barium study showed an el​ongated pyloric channel. ​Which drug is used in the‌ first week of life can c‌ause this presentation?","raw_text":"A 5-week-old infant presented with non-bilious projectile vomiting and lethargy for 2 days. On abdominal examination, an olive-like mass was palpated. Barium study showed an elongated pyloric channel. Which drug is used in the first week of life can cause this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Misop‍rostol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythr‍omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thali‍domide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythromycin</strong></p><p>The given case scenario suggests the diagnosis of <strong>Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong>. <strong>Erythromycin</strong>, particularly when given in the first few weeks of life, is found to be associated with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p><strong>Exposure</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p><strong>Drug</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"239\">\n<p><strong>Malformation</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Post-natal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Erythromycin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"239\"> Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Pre-natal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Thalidomide</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"239\">\n<p>Phocomelia</p>\n<p>Grossly underdeveloped or absent limbs</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Pre-natal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Phenytoin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"239\">\n<p>Fetal hydantoin syndrome</p>\n<p>Intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, cleft palate, hypoplastic phalanges, etc.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Pre-natal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"99\">\n<p>Misoprostol</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"239\">\n<p>Moebius syndrome</p>\n<p>Underdeveloped VI and VII CNs resulting in facial paralysis and lateral rectus palsy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5165","text":"Savitri, a 3-week-old baby‍ is having bouts of non-b​ilious projectile vomitin‍g. On examination, the ch‌ild seems lethargic and d‍ehydrated. Abdominal exam​ination reveals visible g​astric peristalsis. What ​is the investigation of c‌hoice for the diagnosis o‌f this condition?","raw_text":"Savitri, a 3-week-old baby is having bouts of non-bilious projectile vomiting. On examination, the child seems lethargic and dehydrated. Abdominal examination reveals visible gastric peristalsis. What is the investigation of choice for the diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bariu‍m meal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultra‍sound","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT ‍scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‍RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ultrasound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e22ab19ee1134dd99f7a5944686b6b8fx1279x1328.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17848e8e01e342199842d06712ce74aax1279x1282.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ultrasound</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>non-bilious vomiting</strong> and <strong>visible gastric peristalsis</strong> is highly suggestive of <strong>congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</strong>. The investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis is <strong>ultrasound</strong>.</p>\n<p>CHPS is associated with hypertrophy of the circular muscle of the pylorus resulting in constriction and obstruction of the gastric outlet. It is common<strong> </strong>between<strong> 3-6 weeks</strong> of age. First-born <strong>male infants</strong> are at the highest risk. </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Infants usually present with <strong>projectile, non-bilious vomiting. </strong>Clinically, an <strong>olive-shaped mass</strong> is palpable in the right upper quadrant or epigastrium. When the infant is given a test feed, <strong>visible peristalsis</strong> is usually observed moving from <strong>left to right</strong>, followed by projectile vomiting. The characteristic metabolic abnormality is hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis due to recurrent vomiting. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Ultrasound features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Double-layered hypertrophic mucosa protrudes into the stomach, creating the so-called ‘<strong>Nipple sign’</strong></li>\n<li>The sonographic appearance of the hypertrophied pylorus resembles that of the uterine cervix and is sometimes referred to as the ‘<strong>cervix sign’</strong></li>\n<li>The diagnostic criteria for pyloric stenosis are a <strong>muscle thickness of ≥4 mm</strong> and a <strong>pyloric length of ≥16 mm</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If ultrasound is inconclusive, contrast studies (barium) are performed, which demonstrate</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An elongated pyloric channel (<strong>string sign</strong>),</li>\n<li>A bulge of the pyloric muscle into the antrum (<strong>shoulder sign</strong>), and</li>\n<li>Parallel streaks of barium seen in the narrowed channel, producing a “<strong>double tract sign.”</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image attached below shows the USG findings in CHPS.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Signs in Congenital Pyloric stenosis:</p>\n<table width=\"402\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sign</td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>String sign</td>\n<td>Passing of small streak of barium through pyloric canal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Double/triple track sign</td>\n<td>Crowding of muscle folds in pyloric canal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diamond sign/twining recess</td>\n<td>Transient triangular tent like cleft in mid portion of pyloric canal with apex pointing inferiorly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beak sign</td>\n<td>Mass impression upon antrum with streak of barium pointing towards pyloric channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mushroom sign/kirkin sign/umbrella sign</td>\n<td>Indentation of base of duodenal bulb by pylorus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Caterpillar sign</td>\n<td>Gastric hyperperistaltic waves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shoulder sign</td>\n<td>Round indentation caused by hypertrophied pyloric muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>The treatment of choice is <strong>Ramstedt - Frankelstedt pyloromyotomy. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9622","text":"Ashwini, the mother of a y‍oung baby is concerned af​ter she noticed straw yel‍low-colored stains in her‌ child's clothing while w‍ashing them. She brings t​he child to you for exami​nation. The child has a m​ildly elevated temperatur‌e and the fluid comes out‌ from the umbilicus, when​ever the child strains. W‌hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"Ashwini, the mother of a young baby is concerned after she noticed straw yellow-colored stains in her child's clothing while washing them. She brings the child to you for examination. The child has a mildly elevated temperature and the fluid comes out from the umbilicus, whenever the child strains. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patent vitellointestinal d‍uct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urachal‍ fistula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patent omphalomesenteric d‍uct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder di‍verticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urachal fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urachal fistula</strong></p><p>The diagnosis in the above case is a <strong>urachal fistula. </strong>The clinical vigentte suggesting straw -yellow fluid (suggestive of<strong> urine</strong>) along with secondary infection (mildly elevated temperature) and presence of fluid only on straining are all suggestive of urahcal fistula. </p>\n<p>The urachus is an embryologic canal connecting the <strong>dome of the fetal bladder with the allantois,</strong> a structure that contributes to the formation of the umbilical cord. In this condition, there is continuous urinary drainage from the umbilicus on straining. It usually presents as a tender, swollen mass secondary to infection. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis is made by ultrasonography or computed tomography (CT).</p>\n<p>Definitive management is<strong> surgical resection</strong> of the tract.</p>\n<p>A <strong>patent vitellointestinal duct, patent omphalomesenteric duct </strong>and <strong>bladder diverticulum</strong> will cause umbilical discharge even in the <strong>absence of straining</strong>. The discharge in such a case is composed of the contents of the intestine (curdy white in colour mostly).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5166","text":"On observing the following‍ finding in the X-ray of ​a baby, what is the most ‍likely diagnosis you woul‌d suspect?","raw_text":"On observing the following finding in the X-ray of a baby, what is the most likely diagnosis you would suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodenal‍ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ileal ‍atresia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jejunal‍ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duodenal atresia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5b1ef83fd814f8d8128b7bc972558a6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duodenal atresia</strong></p><p>The presence of a<strong> “double-bubble” sign </strong>on a plain abdominal radiograph is suggestive of a diagnosis of duodenal atresia. The double bubble is caused by the distension of stomach and proximal duodenum.</p>\n<p>Duodenal atresia results from failed recanalization of the intestinal lumen during gestation. A history of <strong>polyhydramnios</strong> is present; half of the infants seen with duodenal atresia are <strong>premature</strong>. The hallmark of duodenal obstruction is<strong> bilious vomiting without abdominal distention</strong>, which is usually noted on the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> day </strong>of life.</p>\n<p>The initial treatment of infants with duodenal atresia includes <strong>nasogastric or orogastric decompression </strong>and intravenous fluid replacement. The typical surgical repair for duodenal atresia is <strong>duodenoduodenostomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5217","text":"An infant was brought to t‍he paediatrician with com​plaints of vomiting and a‍bdominal distention. The ‌parents inform that the b‍aby passes stools that lo​ok like currant jelly. On​ ultrasonography, a targe​t sign was observed. Whic‌h of the following is not‌ true about this conditio​n?","raw_text":"An infant was brought to the paediatrician with complaints of vomiting and abdominal distention. The parents inform that the baby passes stools that look like currant jelly. On ultrasonography, a target sign was observed. Which of the following is not true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rota virus vaccine is not ‍associated with increased​ risk","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Submucosal lipomas can cau‍se recurrence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The most common cause is i‍diopathic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common location i‍s ileocolic region","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rota virus vaccine is not associated with increased risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rota virus vaccine is not associated with increased risk</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>intussusception</strong> and the<strong> rotavirus vaccine </strong>is associated with an<strong> increased risk, </strong>albeit only a slightly increased risk</p>\n<p>Intussusception occurs when a portion of the alimentary tract is<strong> telescoped</strong> into an adjacent segment. The most common location of intussusception is ileocolic. Ileoileal location is seen postoperatively and in <strong>Henoch–Schönlein Purpura</strong>.</p>\n<p>90% of cases of intussusception in children are idiopathic in nature. It can be seen in infants during the weaning period following the introduction of new food, vaccination, or upper respiratory tract infection. The currently approved rotavirus vaccines are associated with a slightly increased risk of intussusception.</p>\n<p>Lead points are more common in children older than 2 years of age and can cause recurrent intussusception. Some recognizable lead points are as follows: <strong>en</strong><strong>larged submucosal Peyer's patch</strong>,<strong> Meckel's diverticulum,</strong> intestinal polyp, neurofibroma, intestinal duplication cysts, leiomyomas, hamartomas, anastomotic suture line, enterostomy tube, hemangioma, or malignant conditions such as lymphoma or Kaposi sarcoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5220","text":"A 4-year-old child is admi‍tted to your unit with re​current pancreatitis. Whi‍ch of the following devel‌opmental anomalies is lik‍ely to be associated with​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child is admitted to your unit with recurrent pancreatitis. Which of the following developmental anomalies is likely to be associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreas‍ divisum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Annular ‍pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic‍ pseudocyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small ectop‍ic pancreas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancreas divisum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancreas divisum</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreas divisum</strong> is the <strong>most common congenital anomaly</strong> associated <strong>with</strong> <strong>recurrent pancreatitis</strong>. It is also the<strong> </strong><strong>most common pancreatic developmental anomaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>It results from the <strong>failure of fusion</strong> of the <strong>dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds</strong>, due to which, the tail, body, and part of the head of the pancreas drain through the small accessory duct of Santorini rather than the main duct of Wirsung. It may be associated with <strong>recurrent pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5167","text":"A 7-year-old boy was playi‍ng football with his frie​nds during the PET classe‍s. Suddenly he complained‌ of severe abdominal pain‍ and he is rushed to the ​pediatric ER. An erect X-​ray scan of the abdomen s​howed the following pictu‌re. What is the most like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy was playing football with his friends during the PET classes. Suddenly he complained of severe abdominal pain and he is rushed to the pediatric ER.  An erect X-ray scan of the abdomen showed the following picture.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intussu‍sception","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric ‍volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoid ‍volvulus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meckels di‍verticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sigmoid volvulus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/effe30ca18f7479e8be43aaefb1d460a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sigmoid volvulus</strong></p><p><strong>Sigmoid volvulus</strong> is the most likely diagnosis.<strong> </strong>It occurs when the sigmoid colon twists on the sigmoid mesocolon. It is a cause of large bowel obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5214","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents‍ to the emergency departm​ent with sudden-onset sev‍ere generalised abdominal‌ pain associated with fev‍er, nausea, vomiting, and​ diarrhoea. On examinatio​n, his abdomen is tense w​ith generalised tendernes‌s, guarding and McBurnys ‌tenderness is present. No​ bowel sounds are present‌. What is the best diagno​stic investigation in thi‍s child?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset severe generalised abdominal pain associated with fever, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhoea. On examination, his abdomen is tense with generalised tenderness,  guarding and McBurnys tenderness is present. No bowel sounds are present. What is the best diagnostic investigation in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U‍SG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"M‍RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT ‍scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-‍ray","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. USG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29e28ff660fa4cd2a99925715e99ac5bx696x568.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. USG</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute appendicitis</strong>. <strong>Ultrasound</strong> is the <strong>best diagnostic investigation</strong> in the evaluation of acute appendicitis and has demonstrated &gt;90% sensitivity and specificity.</p>\n<p>The advantages of ultrasound include its <strong>easy availability</strong>,<strong> rapid method, low cost</strong>, and <strong>freedom from ionizing radiation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ultrasound criteria for appendicitis include the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Wall thickness ≥6 mm</li>\n<li>Luminal distention</li>\n<li>Lack of compressibility</li>\n<li>A complex mass in the right lower quadrant</li>\n<li>Or an appendicolith</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Ultrasound showing evidence of appendicitis</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5227","text":"Aaradhana, a 14-year-old g‍irl is feeling embarrasse​d as she has to often rus‍h to the restroom during ‌school hours. Her friends‍ also make fun of her as ​she blushes frequently fo​r no reason. She was late​r diagnosed with an appen‌dicular tumour. Labs reve‌aled low levels of niacin​ with the tumour being po‌sitive for synaptophysin.​ Which of the following b‍iochemical moieties can b‌e demonstrated in the pat‌ient's urine sample?","raw_text":"Aaradhana, a 14-year-old girl is feeling embarrassed as she has to often rush to the restroom during school hours. Her friends also make fun of her as she blushes frequently for no reason. She was later diagnosed with an appendicular tumour. Labs revealed low levels of niacin with the tumour being positive for synaptophysin. Which of the following biochemical moieties can be demonstrated in the patient's urine sample?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-hydroxy indole acetic ac‍id (HIAA)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"vanillylmandelic acid (VMA‍)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AC‍TH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"catecho‍lamines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (HIAA)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4084cd5031d8486bbdcc866080db2be2x720x461.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (HIAA)</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of a <strong>carcinoid tumour</strong>. Elevated levels of <strong>urinary </strong>5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (<strong>5-HIAA</strong>) can be seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>The carcinoid tumours are<strong> neuroendocrine tumours</strong> arising from enterochromaffin cells that can occur anywhere in the <strong>GIT</strong>. In children, they are most commonly seen in the <strong>appendix</strong>. A carcinoid tumour with metastasis to the liver is likely to produce carcinoid syndrome. The tumour elaborates <strong>serotonin, 5-HT, and histamine,</strong> which produce <strong>flushing (frequent blushing)</strong>, <strong>watery diarrhoea</strong>, bronchoconstriction, and right heart failure.</p>\n<p>Carcinoid syndrome can cause <strong>secondary niacin</strong> deficiency as they consume and divert the tryptophan NAD+ pathway to cause the overproduction of serotonin. Thus, these patients can also have<strong> pellagra</strong> like features. </p>\n<p>The diagnosis can be confirmed by elevated levels of <strong>urinary 5-HIAA.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image attached above demonstrates islands and nests of uniform cells with scant, pink granular cytoplasm and <strong>salt and pepper chromatin. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Synaptophysin </strong>is typically positive in carcinoid tumors.</p>\n<p>Treatment: Essentially surgical. Medical management includes the administration of somatostatin analogues (<strong>octreotide</strong>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small tumours (&lt;1 cm in size) are treated by appendectomy.</li>\n<li>Larger tumours (&gt;2 cm in size) require further bowel resection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5226","text":"A 14-year-old boy presents‍ with recurrent colicky a​bdominal pain and melena.‍ On examination, you note‌ the following lesions in‍ the buccal mucosa. You s​uspect Peutz-Jeghers synd​rome and advice for the e​ndoscopic investigation t‌o look for polyps. In whi‌ch part of the intestine ​would you expect to find ‌maximum polyps?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy presents with recurrent colicky abdominal pain and melena. On examination, you note the following lesions in the buccal mucosa. You suspect Peutz-Jeghers syndrome and advice for the endoscopic investigation to look for polyps. In which part of the intestine would you expect to find maximum polyps?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jej‍unum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Appe‍ndix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Co‍lon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastric‍ mucosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Jejunum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a84bad87ed94d1fb0f9d15db19048ef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Jejunum</strong></p><p>The <strong>hamartomatous</strong> polyps in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome are seen in order of prevalence: <strong>Jejunum </strong>&gt; ileum &gt; duodenum &gt; colonic or gastric.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5168","text":"A full-term neonate was fo‍und to have distention of​ the abdomen. He also pas‍sed his meconium late and‌ based on further workup ‍was later diagnosed with ​Hirschsprung disease. Whi​ch of the following condi​tions is least likely to ‌be found in association w‌ith this?","raw_text":"A full-term neonate was found to have distention of the abdomen. He also passed his meconium late and based on further workup was later diagnosed with Hirschsprung disease. Which of the following conditions is least likely to be found in association with this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Von Recklinghausen's disea‍se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joubert ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Edward ‍syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Downs s‍yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Edward syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a5487ab843f4611a6d4d9c83fd47491x720x571.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Edward syndrome</strong></p><p>Hirschsprung disease is <strong>not associate</strong>d with <strong>Edward syndrome (trisomy 18)</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is associated with:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Trisomy 21/ Downs syndrome</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Joubert syndrome </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Smith–Lemli–Opitz syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shah–Waardenburg syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multiple endocrine neoplasia 2 syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neurofibromatosis (Von recklinghausen disease)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neuroblastoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urogenital or cardiovascular abnormalities.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hirschsprung disease or congenital aganglionic colon is characterized by the <strong>absence of ganglion cells</strong> in the submucosal and myenteric plexus. It is more common in <strong>males</strong>. Risk factors include Down's syndrome and RET oncogene.</p>\n<p>The imaging modality of choice in <strong>Hirschsprung's disease</strong> is a contrast radiograph with <strong>barium</strong> or <strong>gastrograffin enema</strong> (image shown above)<strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p>The most important film is a <strong>lateral</strong> or <strong>oblique</strong> view of the rectum during <strong>slow filling.</strong> It typically shows a <strong>low bowel obstruction.</strong> The <strong>rectum</strong> is seen as a <strong>narrow</strong> tube and a <strong>cone-shaped transition zone,</strong> seen <strong>proximal</strong> to the <strong>dilated ganglionated bowel.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>A rectal suction biopsy</strong> is a gold standard for the diagnosis and <strong>operative intervention</strong> is the definitive treatment.</p>\n<p>Definitive surgeries for Hirschsprung disease are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Swenson</strong> procedure, where aganglionic bowel is removed and a coloanal anastomosis is performed.</li>\n<li><strong>Duhamel</strong> procedure,<strong> </strong>where the aganglionic rectal stump is left in place and the ganglionated normal colon is pulled posterior.</li>\n<li><strong>Soave</strong> procedure: In this procedure, endorectal mucosal dissection is done within the aganglionic distal rectum and then the ganglionated normal colon is pulled through the remnant muscular cuff, and a coloanal anastomosis is performed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>NOTE:</strong></p>\n<p>In the fetus, migration of the neuronal crest tissues to form myenteric plexus and ganglion cells is <strong>completed by the 24th week of life.</strong> If interrupted in migration, it results in Hirschsprung disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5150","text":"A neonate is being evaluat‍ed in the nursery for fai​lure to pass meconium in ‍the last 48 hours. Physic‌al examination reveals ab‍dominal distension and an​ anal dimple. Wangenstein​'s invertogram shows a lo​w lying defect. Further e‌valuation reveals the pre‌sence of vertebral and re​nal deformities. Which of‌ the following conditions​ is least likely to be se‍en in this child?","raw_text":"A neonate is being evaluated in the nursery for failure to pass meconium in the last 48 hours. Physical examination reveals abdominal distension and an anal dimple. Wangenstein's invertogram shows a low lying defect. Further evaluation reveals the presence of vertebral and renal deformities. Which of the following conditions is least likely to be seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac‍ defects","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tracheal ‍anomalies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial ‍aplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph‍edema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymphedema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymphedema</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>imperforate anus</strong> in a neonate with <strong>VACTERL association</strong> (presence of vertebral and renal anomalies in addition to the anal atresia). Lymphedema is not a component of <strong>VACTERL</strong> syndrome. </p>\n<p><span class=\"ghr-condition\">VACTERL association</span> is a disorder that affects many-body systems. People diagnosed with <span class=\"ghr-condition\">VACTERL association</span> typically have at least three of the following characteristic features.</p>\n<p>Components of <strong>VACTERL</strong> association:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vertebral</strong></li>\n<li>Anorectal</li>\n<li>Cardiac</li>\n<li>Tracheal</li>\n<li>Esophageal</li>\n<li><strong>Renal</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Radial</strong></li>\n<li>Limb anomalies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Anorectal malformations:</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It occurs due to the <strong>post-allantoic gut</strong> with the proctodaeum. </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Newborns classically present with inability to pass meconium within 48 hours, abdominal distention, intestinal obstruction, improper anal dimple, and sometimes with complaints of passing meconium per urethra. </span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Wingspread Classification:</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Low - Below the level of the pelvic floor (puborectalis).</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Intermediate - Occurs at the level of the puborectalis with or without fistula.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">High - Anorectal agenesis, rectal atresia.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Investigations:</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Wangenstein's invertogram is done <strong>6-12 hours after birth</strong> with a metal coin strapped to the presumed site of the anus. The X-ray taken is used to measure and classify the deformity.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">USG abdomen</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Imaging to evacuate associated abnormalities</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Treatment is surgical.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5219","text":"You notice a 5-year-old bo‍y walking very differentl​y in an orphanage. On pro‍bing further the guardian‌ informs that he has a hi‍story of frequently soili​ng his pants. On examinat​ion, you find a mass prot​ruding through his anus a‌nd a diagnosis of rectal ‌prolapse is made. Which o​f the following would be ‌the most likely cause for​ this presentation?","raw_text":"You notice a 5-year-old boy walking very differently in an orphanage. On probing further the guardian informs that he has a history of frequently soiling his pants. On examination, you find a mass protruding through his anus and a diagnosis of rectal prolapse is made. Which of the following would be the most likely cause for this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pert‍ussis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consti‍pation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic ‍fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichu‍riasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trichuriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trichuriasis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3117\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In this case, the most likely cause of rectal prolapse would be <strong>Trichuriasis. </strong>It is common in poor rural communities with <strong>inadequate sanitary facilities</strong> and soil contaminated with human or animal feces. The highest infection rate occurs among children between <strong>5-15 years.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3119\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3119\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Clinical manifestations include <strong>chronic dysentery</strong>, <strong>anemia</strong>, <strong>poor growth</strong>, and <strong>developmental deficits.</strong> In heavy infections, adult worms can extend throughout the colon down to the rectum. Over time, <strong>inflammation of the rectum</strong> causes tenesmus leading to <strong>prolapse</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p><strong>Pertussis</strong> would present with <strong>cough</strong>, low-grade fever, and sneezing. <strong>Increased abdominal pressure</strong> due to the persistent cough could cause a prolapse.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic constipation</strong> is <strong>unlikely</strong> as the child has a history of frequently soiling his pants, suggestive of <strong>dysentery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong> would manifest much earlier in life with respiratory symptoms like recurrent pneumonia. These children have <strong>exocrine pancreatic insufficiency</strong>, causing protein and fat malabsorption. This leads to bulky, greasy stools and eventually a prolapse if untreated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YRdh9HwZ","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"19_normocytic_and_macrocytic_anemia","title":"19.Normocytic And Macrocytic Anemia","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5733","text":"A 30-year-old woman who is​ taking OCPs presents wit​h weakness and fatigue. H​er laboratory investigati‌ons and peripheral smear ‌are given below. What is ‍the most likely diagnosis‌? Hb - 5 gm% TLC - 1200 c​ells/mm3 Platelet count -​ 60,000/μL MCV - 102 fl M‌CH - 40 pg/cell","raw_text":"<p>A 30-year-old woman who is taking OCPs presents with weakness and fatigue. Her laboratory investigations and peripheral smear are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb - 5 gm%</p>\n<p>TLC - 1200 cells/mm3</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 60,000/μL</p>\n<p>MCV - 102 fl</p>\n<p>MCH - 40 pg/cell</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Megaloblas​tic anemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aplasti​c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog​lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iron defici​ency anaemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Megaloblastic anemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4135ad99fa9240faa8b2aa18dc99695d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Megaloblastic anemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>megaloblastic anaemia</strong> due to the following reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>History of OCP use</li>\n<li>Hemogram shows decreased hemoglobin and increased MCV</li>\n<li>Peripheral smear shows anisopoikilocytosis, macroovalocytes, and <strong>hypersegmented neutrophils</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5747","text":"A 40-year-old man with a h​istory of bowel resection​ for Crohn's disease deve​lops megaloblastic anaemi‌a. Which component of the‌ vitamin B12 metabolism i‍s likely to be affected i‌n this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man with a history of bowel resection for Crohn's disease develops megaloblastic anaemia. Which component of the vitamin B12 metabolism is likely to be affected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cub​ilin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hapto​corrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transco​balamin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intrinsi​c factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cubilin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50d9c5c798614b15b80de28410adeb43x1280x2128.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cubilin</strong></p><p><strong>Ileal resection</strong> for Crohn's disease can cause <strong>megaloblastic anaemia</strong> due to the decrease in <strong>cubilin receptors</strong>. They are the vitamin B12 receptors on the ileum<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Absorption</strong> of vitamin<strong> B12</strong> involves the following steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pepsin</strong> in the stomach releases vitamin B12 from the binding proteins in the food</li>\n<li>Released B12 binds to <strong>haptocorrin</strong>, which is a salivary protein</li>\n<li>In the duodenum, <strong>pancreatic proteases</strong> release B12 from haptocorrin</li>\n<li>B12 binds to <strong>intrinsic factor</strong> to form IF-B12 complex</li>\n<li>In the ileum, ileal enterocytes that contain <strong>cubulin</strong> receptors (IF receptor) <strong>endocytose</strong> the complex</li>\n<li>In the ileal cells, B12 is associated with<strong> transcobalamin II</strong>, which is a carrier protein</li>\n<li>B12 is delivered to the liver, bone marrow and GIT</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5737","text":"In which of the following ​patients would you not su​spect megaloblastic anaem​ia caused by vitamin B12 ‌deficiency?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients would you not suspect megaloblastic anaemia caused by vitamin B12 deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 5-year-old child with fi​sh tapeworm infestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 45-year-old man who unde​rwent gastrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 38-year-old man with ile​itis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 23-year-old woman on phe​nytoin therapy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 23-year-old woman on phenytoin therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 23-year-old woman on phenytoin therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Phenytoin</strong> therapy causes <strong>folate deficiency</strong> leading to <strong>megaloblastic anaemia</strong>. It does not cause vitamin B12 deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Dietary folate</strong> is in <strong>polyglutamate</strong> form, which is then converted into <strong>monoglutamates</strong> by the intestinal conjugase. Phenytoin acts by inhibiting this enzyme, thereby causing folate deficiency, and thus megaloblastic anaemia.</p>\n<p>Options A, B and C: They cause megaloblastic anemia due to <strong>vitamin B12 deficiciency</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6713","text":"A 20-year-old female with ​easy fatiguability and pa​llor presented to the OPD​. The image of her hand i‌s as given below. What is‌ the most likely diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old female with easy fatiguability and pallor presented to the OPD. The image of her hand is as given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aplasti​c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B12​ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iron defici​ency anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoalb​uminemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin B12 deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfb22fde341440828db549eb512bd63c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52d81454a5e545f199775a54498ecf53x960x690.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin B12 deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Skin hyperpigmentation</strong> especially on the <strong>dorsum</strong> of hands involving the <strong>knuckles</strong> is seen in cyanocobalamin or<strong> vitamin B12 deficiency </strong>which<strong> </strong>is a causative factor of <strong>megaloblastic anaemia.</strong></p>\n<p>In megaloblastic anaemia, there is <strong>impairment</strong> of <strong>DNA synthesis</strong> that leads to <strong>ineffective hematopoiesis.</strong> It also causes distinctive morphologic changes, including abnormally large erythroid precursors and red cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of megaloblastic anaemia (cyanocobalamin and folate deficiency) include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 297px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Oral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p><strong>Glossitis</strong></p>\n<p>Angular cheilosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Gastrointestinal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Anorexia</p>\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Diarrhea</p>\n<p>Constipation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\"><strong>Hyperpigmentation</strong> (reversible)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Hematological</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Thrombocytopenia</p>\n<p>Leucopenia</p>\n<p>Ineffective erythropoiesis and unconjugated<strong> </strong>jaundice</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Cardiovascular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Atherosclerosis leading to PVD, CAD (due to elevated homocysteine)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Neurological (only B12)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Peripheral neuropathy</p>\n<p>Subacute combined degeneration (degeneration of the posterior and pyramidal tracts of the spinal cord)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Pregnancy (only folate)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Recurrent fetal loss</p>\n<p>Neural tube defects</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Others</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p><strong>Mild</strong> <strong>fever</strong> in severe cases</p>\n<p>Psychiatric disturbances</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Peripheral smear</strong> findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macro-ovalocytes</strong></li>\n<li>Most macrocytes lack the central pallor of normal red cells and even appear hyperchromic, but the MCHC is not elevated.</li>\n<li><strong>Reticulocyte </strong>count is <strong>low</strong></li>\n<li>Nucleated red cell progenitors occasionally appear in the circulating blood when anaemia is severe</li>\n<li>Neutrophils are also larger than normal (macro polymorphonuclear) and show <strong>nuclear hypersegmentation</strong>, having five or more nuclear lobules</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows hypersegmented neutrophils seen in megaloblastic anaemia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Bone marrow</strong> findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Markedly <strong>hypercellular</strong> as a result of increased hematopoietic precursors.</li>\n<li><strong>Sieve-like </strong>pattern of<strong> chromatin</strong> of immature precursors (Megaloblasts)</li>\n<li>Granulocytic precursors also display dysmaturation in the form of <strong>giant metamyelocytes </strong>and<strong> band forms</strong></li>\n<li>Megakaryocytes can be abnormally large and have bizarre, multilobate nuclei</li>\n<li>The derangement in DNA synthesis causes most precursors to undergo apoptosis in the marrow (<strong>ineffective hematopoiesis</strong>) and leads to<strong> pancytopenia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6718","text":"What is your diagnosis wit​h respect to the findings​ shown in the peripheral ​smear given below?","raw_text":"What is your diagnosis with respect to the findings shown in the peripheral smear given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron defici​ency anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B12 def​iciency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary s​pherocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myelo​id leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B12 deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1fe25230669543b2a3a0ec56464a8385.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B12 deficiency</strong></p><p>The peripheral smear showing<strong> macro-ovalocytes </strong>and<strong> </strong>a<strong> hypersegmented neutrophil</strong> are suggestive of <strong>megaloblastic anaemia. </strong>Vitamin<strong> B12 deficiency</strong> is a cause of megaloblastic anaemia. </p>\n<p><strong>Peripheral smear</strong> findings in megaloblastic anaemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macro-ovalocytes</strong></li>\n<li>Most macrocytes lack the central pallor of normal red cells and even appear hyperchromic, but the MCHC is not elevated.</li>\n<li><strong>Reticulocyte </strong>count is<strong> low</strong></li>\n<li>Nucleated red cell progenitors occasionally appear in the circulating blood when anaemia is severe</li>\n<li>Neutrophils are also larger than normal (macro polymorphonuclear) and show <strong>nuclear hypersegmentation</strong>, having five or more nuclear lobules</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5739","text":"Which of the following fin​dings would you not expec​t to see in patients with​ megaloblastic anaemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings would you not expect to see in patients with megaloblastic anaemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased reticulocyte cou​nt","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Raised b​ilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mild spl​enomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleated RB​C progenitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased reticulocyte count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased reticulocyte count</strong></p><p>The <strong>reticulocyte</strong> count is <strong>decreased</strong> in megaloblastic anaemia.</p>\n<p>The reticulocyte count is indicative of bone marrow function and its ability to produce healthy erythrocytes. In megaloblastic anemia, there is intramedullary hemolysis leading to raised indirect <strong>bilirubin</strong> and <strong>impaired</strong> erythroid maturation leading to a <strong>low reticulocyte</strong> count.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5753","text":"A patient is suspected to ​have megaloblastic anaemi​a. Which of the following​ will not be helpful in d‌ifferentiating vitamin B1‌2 deficiency from folate ‍deficiency?","raw_text":"A patient is suspected to have megaloblastic anaemia. Which of the following will not be helpful in differentiating vitamin B12 deficiency from folate deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased serum homocystei​ne levels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased methylmalonate l​evels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurologic​al changes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ​LDH levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased serum homocysteine levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased serum homocysteine levels</strong></p><p><strong>Increased serum homocysteine</strong> levels are <strong>common</strong> to both vitamin <strong>B12</strong> deficiency and <strong>folate</strong> deficiency. Hence, it cannot be used to differentiate between them.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0289","text":"You are examining the peri​pheral smear of a patient​ with anaemia and see the​ following finding. Name ‌the finding and its patho‌genesis.","raw_text":"You are examining the peripheral smear of a patient with anaemia and see the following finding. Name the finding and its pathogenesis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heinz body - oxidative dam​age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basophilic stippling - rib​osomal precipitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cabot's rings - ineffectiv​e erythropoiesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Howell Jolly body - ineffe​ctive RBC enucleation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cabot's rings - ineffective erythropoiesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/26bbe31bc8ba434b921542df16085aef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cabot&#x27;s rings - ineffective erythropoiesis</strong></p><p>The marked structure in this peripheral smear showing <strong>figure of 8</strong> appearance is a <strong>Cabot ring. </strong>They<strong> </strong>are <strong>RBC inclusion bodies</strong> seen in conditions due to <strong>ineffective erythropoiesis</strong>. It is considered a <strong>remnant</strong> of <strong>microtubules</strong> during cell division.</p>\n<p>Cabot rings can be seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Megaloblastic anaemia</li>\n<li>Post-splenectomy patients</li>\n<li>Lead poisoning</li>\n<li>Hemolytic anemias </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Heinz</strong> bodies are due to oxidative damage, seen in G6PD deficiency.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Basophilic stippling</strong> is due to ribosomal precipitation, seen in heavy metal poisoning, thalassemia and sickle cell disease.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Howell Jolly</strong> bodies are due to ineffective RBC enucleation, characteristic of asplenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5745","text":"Schilling's test can be us​ed for all the following ​except _____","raw_text":"Schilling's test can be used for all the following except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To differentiate between v​itamin B12 and folate def​iciency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To identify the cause of v​itamin B12 deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To identify the extent of ​megaloblastic anaemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"To rule out pernicious ana​emia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. To identify the extent of megaloblastic anaemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To identify the extent of megaloblastic anaemia</strong></p><p><strong>Schilling test</strong> cannot identify the extent of megaloblastic anaemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5750","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g the antibodies of perni​cious anaemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the antibodies of pernicious anaemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I antibodies are foun​d in both plasma and gast​ric juice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type I antibody blocks the​ binding of vitamin B12 t​o intrinsic factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type II antibodies prevent​ the binding of the IF-B1​2 complex to ileal recept​ors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type III antibodies are ag​ainst haptocorrin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type III antibodies are against haptocorrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type III antibodies are against haptocorrin</strong></p><p><strong>Type III</strong> <strong>antibodies</strong> are not against haptocorrin. They recognize the α and β subunits of the <strong>gastric proton pumps</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pernicious anaemia</strong> is a specific form of megaloblastic anaemia caused by <strong>autoimmune </strong>gastritis. It <strong>impairs</strong> the production of <strong>intrinsic factor</strong>, which is required for vitamin B12 uptake from the gut. </p>\n<p><strong>Types </strong>of <strong>antibodies</strong> in pernicious anaemia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type <strong>I</strong> antibodies - <strong>block</strong> the <strong>binding</strong> of vitamin <strong>B12</strong> to <strong>intrinsic factor</strong>. They are found in both plasma and gastric juice</li>\n<li>Type <strong>II</strong> antibodies - <strong>prevent</strong> binding of the intrinsic factor-vitamin B12 (<strong>IF-B12</strong>) complex to its <strong>ileal receptor</strong></li>\n<li>Type <strong>III</strong> antibodies - recognize the α and β subunits of the <strong>gastric proton pump</strong> of parietal cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0291","text":"A 53-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of lig​ht-headedness every time ​she stands up. She is a k‌nown case of rheumatoid a‌rthritis on methotrexate.‍ On examination, she has ‌pallor and brittle nails.​ Which of the following s​tatements is true regardi‌ng her condition?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman presents with complaints of light-headedness every time she stands up. She is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis on methotrexate. On examination, she has pallor and brittle nails. Which of the following statements is true regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum iron and tissue iron​ levels are normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anemia can be normocytic a​nd normochromic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepcidin le​vels are low","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total iron-binding capacit​y is high","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anemia can be normocytic and normochromic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80da2f97071f48d8ade854a760d811fax1279x1210.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anemia can be normocytic and normochromic</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anaemia</strong> of <strong>chronic diseases </strong>(ACD). In this condition, the anaemia initially can be <strong>normocytic normochromic</strong>.</p>\n<p>ACD occurs in the setting of persistent systemic inflammation. It is associated with </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low serum iron</li>\n<li>Reduced total iron-binding capacity</li>\n<li>Abundant stored iron in tissue macrophages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inflammatory mediators like <strong>IL-6</strong> stimulate an <strong>increase</strong> in the production of <strong>hepcidin</strong>. This <strong>inhibits</strong> <strong>ferroportin</strong> function in the macrophages and <strong>reduces</strong> the transfer of <strong>iron</strong> from the storage pool to developing <strong>erythroid precursors</strong> in the marrow. As a result, erythroid precursors are starved for iron despite the availability of plenty of iron in tissue macrophages.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0299","text":"Identify the cell in the g​iven peripheral blood sme​ar and the likely diagnos​is.","raw_text":"Identify the cell in the given peripheral blood smear and the likely diagnosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acanthocyte in anemia of r​enal disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acanthocyte in anemia of l​iver disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Echinocyte in anemia of re​nal disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Echinocyte in anemia of li​ver disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acanthocyte in anemia of liver disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c2a87884b974fc89c25d245f3ee62f8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a05c95c52d144a828002cd5036ec1488x834x772.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e9a1e4f62a641aa8d66ad5bc11a7d3ax1280x1208.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acanthocyte in anemia of liver disease</strong></p><p><strong>Acanthocyte </strong>or spur cells are seen in <strong>anaemia</strong> of <strong>liver disease</strong>. These cells are characterized by only a few <strong>spicules</strong> of varying sizes that project from the cell surface at <strong>irregular</strong> <strong>intervals</strong>.</p>\n<p>Conditions where acanthocytes can be seen include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe liver disease</li>\n<li>Abetalipoproteinemia</li>\n<li>Post splenectomy</li>\n<li>Hyposplenism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a peripheral smear with acanthoctye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Echinocytes </strong>or<strong> </strong>burr cells are seen in anaemia of renal disease. These cells are characterized by <strong>smaller</strong>, <strong>regularly</strong> <strong>spaced</strong> <strong>projections</strong>,<strong> </strong>smoothly rounded crenulations which are <strong>uniform</strong> in shape and distribution compared to acanthocytes.</p>\n<p>Conditions where echinocytes can be seen include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaemia of renal disease (uremia)</li>\n<li>In premature infants, after exchange transfusion</li>\n<li>Storage artefact</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a peripheral smear with echinocytes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0292","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ​aplastic anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of aplastic anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dr​ugs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autoi​mmune","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infe​ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radi​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Drugs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Drugs</strong></p><p><strong>From the given options</strong>, the most common cause of aplastic anemia is <strong>drugs</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of<strong> aplastic anemia</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Acquired </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Idiopathic</strong> - <strong>most common</strong> cause (about 65% of the cases)</li>\n<li>Acquired stem-cell defects </li>\n<li>Immune-mediated</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chemical agents</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dose-related\n<ul>\n<li>Alkylating agents</li>\n<li>Antimetabolites</li>\n<li>Benzene</li>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n<li>Inorganic arsenals</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Idiosyncratic\n<ul>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n<li>Phenylbutazone</li>\n<li>Organic arsenicals</li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Penicillamine</li>\n<li>Gold salts</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Physical agents</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Whole-body irradiation</li>\n<li>Viral infections - Hepatitis, CMV, EBV, VZ</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inherited </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fanconi anemia </li>\n<li>Telomerase defects </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0295","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen​ts with progressive weakn​ess and fatigue. Her hemo​gram is given below. What‌ is the best investigatio‌n to arrive at the diagno‍sis? Hb - 6 g% RBC - 4 mi‌llion/μL WBC - 1000 /mm 3​ Platelets - 6500 /mm 3","raw_text":"<p>A 35-year-old woman presents with progressive weakness and fatigue. Her hemogram is given below. What is the best investigation to arrive at the diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb - 6 g%</p>\n<p>RBC - 4 million/μL</p>\n<p>WBC - 1000 /mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Platelets - 6500 /mm<sup>3</sup></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone marrow​ aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bone marr​ow biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hb electr​ophoresis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flow cy​tometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bone marrow biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bone marrow biopsy</strong></p><p>The given hemogram is suggestive of <strong>pancytopenia</strong>. Cause of pancytopenia can be aplastic anaemia (hypocellular bone marrow) or myeloid neoplasms (hypercellular bone marrow). Hence, the best investigation is<strong> bone marrow biopsy</strong> to differentiate between them.</p>\n<p>Findings in bone marrow biopsy in <strong>aplastic anaemia</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Markedly <strong>hypocellular</strong> bone marrow largely devoid of hematopoietic cells </li>\n<li>Abundant<strong> fat cells</strong> </li>\n<li>Fibrous stroma, scattered lymphocytes, and plasma cells </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bone marrow <strong>aspiration</strong> often yields a <strong>dry tap</strong>, so biopsy is preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0293","text":"Bone marrow biopsy of a pa​tient with pancytopenia i​s given below. What is th​e most common infectious ‌cause of this condition?","raw_text":"Bone marrow biopsy of a patient with pancytopenia is given below. What is the most common infectious cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parvovi​rus B19","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomeg​alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epstein-B​arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatit​is virus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88d91eeeafc4480ab120acda818c95d6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f327985138be43de83d043bd95a6e896x1280x1502.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8cc18d7855e5451bbaab4ca25821d8ddx1280x1151.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis virus</strong></p><p>The given bone marrow biopsy shows a <strong>hypocellular</strong> bone marrow with <strong>abundant</strong> <strong>fat cells</strong> which is suggestive of<strong> aplastic anaemia</strong>. In this condition, the most common <strong>infectious cause</strong> is <strong>viral hepatitis </strong>of the<strong> non-A</strong>,<strong> Non-</strong><strong>B</strong>,<strong> Non-C</strong>, and <strong>Non-G</strong> type. </p>\n<p>The images given below show the normal and hypocellular bone marrow.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>- Viral hepatitis, parvovirus B19, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), cytomegalovirus and Epstein-Barr virus can cause aplastic anemia. <strong>Viral</strong> <strong>hepatitis</strong> is however the <strong>most common</strong> cause.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0294","text":"Which of the following is ​likely to develop in a pa​tient with aplastic anaem​ia? AML ALL CML CLL MDS","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is likely to develop in a patient with aplastic anaemia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>AML</li>\n<li>ALL</li>\n<li>CML</li>\n<li>CLL</li>\n<li>MDS</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,​ 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​ 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Aplastic anaemia</strong> can transform into both acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>) and myelodysplastic syndrome (<strong>MDS</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0296","text":"Which of the following cau​ses pure red cell aplasia​? Parvovirus infection Th​ymoma Chronic neutrophili‌c leukemia Diamond Blackf‌an Syndrome Rifampicin us‍e","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following causes pure red cell aplasia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Parvovirus infection</li>\n<li>Thymoma</li>\n<li>Chronic neutrophilic leukemia</li>\n<li>Diamond Blackfan Syndrome</li>\n<li>Rifampicin use</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,​ 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ​3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​ 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Causes</strong> of <strong>pure red cell aplasia</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neoplasms - <strong>thymomas</strong> and large granular lymphocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Drug exposure - phenytoin, azathioprine and isoniazid</li>\n<li>Auto-immune disorders - neutralizing antibodies against erythropoietin</li>\n<li><strong>Parvovirus</strong> infection</li>\n<li>Hereditary - <strong>Diamond Blackfan syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0297","text":"What is the most common ca​use of myelophthisic anae​mia?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of myelophthisic anaemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulomato​us diseases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastat​ic cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myeloproliferative neoplas​ms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radi​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metastatic cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a5bae93a0374f2baf10b0f3e7c0bfa6x1242x932.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metastatic cancer</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>myelophthisic anaemia</strong> is <strong>metastatic cancer</strong> from the breast, lung, and prostate. </p>\n<p>Myelophthisic anaemia describes a form of marrow failure in which <strong>space-occupying lesions</strong> replace normal marrow elements. Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metastatic cancer</li>\n<li>Granulomatous diseases</li>\n<li>Myeloproliferative neoplasms. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>This leads to the release of nucleated erythroid precursors and immature granulocytic forms into peripheral blood (<strong>leukoerythroblastosis</strong>) and <strong>tear-drop </strong>shaped red cells. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a peripheral smear with teardrop cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0298","text":"A 70-year-old man presents​ with progressive weaknes​s and chronic fatigue. Ph​ysical examination reveal‌s a hard nodule of about ‌1cm in the prostate. His ‍lab reports reveal low Hb‌ and elevated PSA. Periph​eral blood smear shows a ​normocytic normochromic p‌icture. There are some oc​casional myelocytes, a fe​w tear-drop cells and 2 n‌ucleated RBCs per 100 whi‍te cells. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presents with progressive weakness and chronic fatigue. Physical examination reveals a hard nodule of about 1cm in the prostate. His lab reports reveal low Hb and elevated PSA. Peripheral blood smear shows a normocytic normochromic picture. There are some occasional myelocytes, a few tear-drop cells and 2 nucleated RBCs per 100 white cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelodysplas​tic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelophthi​sic anemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myelof​ibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myeloproliferative disorde​r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myelophthisic anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myelophthisic anemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myelophthisic anaemia. </strong>Prostatic mass with elevated PSA and age suggestive of <strong>prostatic carcinoma</strong>, which could be the likely cause in this case.</p>\n<p>It is a form of marrow failure in which <strong>space-occupying lesions</strong> replace normal marrow elements. The most common cause is <strong>metastatic</strong> cancer from the breast, lung, and prostate<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lab findings</strong> of myelophthisic anaemia include<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myelocytes + nucleated RBCs = <strong>leukoerythroblastic</strong> image</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>tear-drop</strong> cells </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0786","text":"A 52-year-old male patient​ comes with complaints of​ abdominal pain, nausea, ​and vomiting. A periphera‌l blood smear reveals mac‌rocytes and hypersegmente‍d neutrophils and an endo‌scopy reveals atrophic ga​stritis. Which of the fol​lowing conditions is like‌ly to be seen this patien​t?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old male patient comes with complaints of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. A peripheral blood smear reveals macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils and an endoscopy reveals atrophic gastritis. Which of the following conditions is likely to be seen this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin B12​ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Folate d​eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyridoxine​ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Niacin d​eficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin B12 deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin B12 deficiency</strong></p><p>The given findings are suggestive of <strong>pernicious anemia,</strong> which is associated with <strong>atrophic gastritis</strong> and <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Anemic patient</strong> with a peripheral smear showing <strong>macrocytic RBCs</strong> and <strong>hypersegmented neutrophils</strong> are important characteristic features of <strong>megaloblastic anemia.</strong> <strong>Vitamin B12</strong> and folic acid deficiency are the main causes of megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Pernicious anemia</strong> is an autoimmune disease characterized by the presence of <strong>autoantibodies</strong> which impairs the production or activity of the <strong>intrinsic</strong> <strong>factor (IF). </strong>This is required for the <strong>uptake of vitamin B12</strong>. There are three types of autoantibodies namely, type I, II, and III. The action of each of these include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoantibody type I prevents the binding of vitamin B12 to the intinsic factor (IF).</li>\n<li>Autoantibody type II prevents the binding of vitamin B12-IF complex to the ileal receptors.</li>\n<li>Autoantibody type III acts against the α and β subunits of the gastric proton pump, which impairs the production of intrinsic factor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These patients also develop <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>atrophic gastritis,</strong> characterized by loss of parietal cells, infiltration of lymphocytes and plasma cells and megaloblastic changes in mucosal cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CMsGoHRF","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"19_anti_epileptics_i","title":"19.Anti Epileptics I","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA2191","text":"What is the most important‍ mechanism of antiepilept​ic activity of phenytoin?‌","raw_text":"What is the most important mechanism of antiepileptic activity of phenytoin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolongation of Na+ channe‍l inactivation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction in‍ Ca2+ influx","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitation of GABA respo‍nse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increasing t‍he K+ influx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prolongation of Na+ channel inactivation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prolongation of Na+ channel inactivation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most important mechanism of antiepileptic activity of <strong>phenytoin</strong> is the <strong>prolongation</strong> of <strong>Na<sup>+</sup> ion channel inactivation</strong>. It slows down the rate of recovery of Na+ channels and thus limiting the propagation of the action potential.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phenytoin also <strong>inhibits voltage-gated Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</strong>. This reduces the influx of calcium inside the membrane and reduces the inflow of potassium ions during repolarization.</p>\n<p>At higher or toxic doses, phenytoin facilitates GABA response and inhibits the action of glutamate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2203","text":"A known case of seizure di‍sorder has been on long-t​erm phenytoin therapy. Wh‌ich of the following stat‌ements about this drug wo‍uld be incorrect?","raw_text":"A known case of seizure disorder has been on long-term phenytoin therapy. Which of the following statements about this drug would be incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensively bound to plasm‍a proteins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolism follows only ze‍ro order kinetics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Requires therapeutic drug ‍monitoring","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolized extensively by‍ the CYP enzymes in the l​iver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metabolism follows only zero order kinetics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metabolism follows only zero order kinetics</strong></p><p>Phenytoin follows <strong>first-order kinetics</strong> at<strong> lower concentrations </strong>and as the <strong>concentration increases</strong>, the elimination changes to <strong>zero-order kinetics. </strong>The rate of elimination of phenytoin varies with the serum concentration.</p>\n<p>As the blood levels rise, the maximal capacity of the liver to metabolize phenytoin is reached. Thus even a small increase in the dosage can result in disproportionally high plasma levels. Therefore, it requires <strong>therapeutic drug monitoring. </strong></p>\n<p>About <strong>90%</strong> of phenytoin is <strong>bound to plasma proteins, </strong>mainly albumin. Phenytoin is metabolized by the<strong> CYP enzymes</strong> in the liver.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2204","text":"Which of the following is ‍an incorrect statement re​garding fosphenytoin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding fosphenytoin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water soluble prodrug of p‍henytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fosphenytoin displaces phe‍nytoin from protein-bindi​ng sites","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Causes hyperphosphatemia i‍n renal failure patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ECG monitoring is not requ‍ired when administering I​V","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ECG monitoring is not required when administering IV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ECG monitoring is not required when administering IV</strong></p><p><strong>Fosphenytoin</strong> like phenytoin requires<strong> continuous ECG monitoring</strong> during IV infusion due to the <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>arrhythmias.</strong></p>\n<p>Fosphenytoin is a <strong>water-soluble prodrug</strong> of phenytoin. Fosphenytoin is also extensively protein bound, mainly <strong>albumin</strong>. This binding is saturable and fosphenytoin displaces phenytoin from protein-binding sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrolysis</strong> of fosphenytoin yields phosphate, which increases the <strong>risk </strong>of<strong> hyperphosphatemia</strong> in patients with renal failure. It is contraindicated in patients with sinus bradycardia, sino-atrial block, second and third-degree A-V block.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2193","text":"A patient presenting to th‍e ER with generalized ton​ic-clonic seizures is sta‌rted on phenytoin therapy‌. All of the following ad‍verse effects can be expe‍cted in this patient exce‌pt? Lymphadenopathy Ataxi​a Hypercalcemia Hirsutism‌ Hypoglycemia","raw_text":"<p>A patient presenting to the ER with generalized tonic-clonic seizures is started on phenytoin therapy. All of the following adverse effects can be expected in this patient except?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Ataxia</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Hirsutism</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‍nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 o‍nly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‍nd 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Phenytoin</strong> causes <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> and <strong>hyperglycemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It <strong>interferes</strong> with the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>vitamin D</strong> and intestinal <strong>calcium absorption</strong> and results in hypocalcemia. Hyperglycemia and glycosuria can be seen due to the <strong>inhibition</strong> of <strong>insulin</strong> secretion.</p>\n<p>Long-term usage of phenytoin can also cause cutaneous <strong>pseudolymphoma</strong> which is mainly T-cell predominant.<a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4601451/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2202","text":"Which of the following fea‍tures would you not expec​t to find in a baby born ‌to a woman on phenytoin t‌herapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to find in a baby born to a woman on phenytoin therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digital h‍ypoplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gum hyp‍erplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Microc‍ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clef‍t lip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gum hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gum hyperplasia</strong></p><p>The newborn baby is unlikely to have gum hyperplasia. A baby born to a pregnant mother who was on<strong> phenytoin</strong> therapy will be suffering from <strong>fetal</strong> <strong>hydantoin syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of fetal hydantoin syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microcephaly</li>\n<li>Digital hypoplasia</li>\n<li>Cleft lip/ cleft palate</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Gum hyperplasia</strong> is the most common adverse effect of phenytoin in children and young adolescents on <strong>chronic use</strong>.<label class=\"ng-binding label label-success\"></label></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2205","text":"A patient with recent-onse‍t primary generalized epi​lepsy develops a skin rea‌ction due to phenytoin so‌dium. What is the most ap‍propriate course of actio‍n?","raw_text":"A patient with recent-onset primary generalized epilepsy develops a skin reaction due to phenytoin sodium. What is the most appropriate course of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Switch to ‍clonazepam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stop and restart phenytoin‍ sodium after two weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Switch to sodium valproate‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Switch to e‍thosuximide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Switch to sodium valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Switch to sodium valproate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hypersensitivity reaction</strong> to<strong> phenytoin </strong>warrants <strong>discontinuation</strong> of the drug. <strong>Switching</strong> to <strong>sodium valproate</strong> is the most appropriate course of action for this patient. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Sodium valproate</strong> is one of the first-line agents indicated for<strong> GTCS. Clonazepam</strong> and <strong>ethosuximide</strong> are primarily used in <strong>absence seizures</strong> and are not indicated for GTCS. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Clonazepam is indicated as <strong>adjunctive therapy</strong> in the absence seizures when first-line agents (ethosuximide, sodium valproate, lamotrigine) fail to control the seizures adequately.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2213","text":"A 51-year-old male complai‍ns of severe, sharp, pier​cing intermittent electri‌cal shock-like pain sensa‌tion on the left side of ‍his face. The pain was tr‍iggered by talking, washi‌ng his face, eating, and ​brushing his teeth. Which‌ of the following drugs i‌s most effective to treat​ this patient?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old male complains of severe, sharp, piercing intermittent electrical shock-like pain sensation on the left side of his face. The pain was triggered by talking, washing his face, eating, and brushing his teeth. Which of the following drugs is most effective to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium v‍alproate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lamot‍rigine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carbamazepine</strong></p><p>The clinical features in this patient are suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>trigeminal neuralgia</strong> and <strong>carbamazepine</strong> is the most effective agent for its treatment.  </p>\n<p>If carbamazepine is ineffective, then second-line<strong> </strong>agents like <strong>lamotrigine</strong> and <strong>phenytoin</strong> are used.</p>\n<p>Sodium valproate although effective for trigeminal neuralgia lacks enough evidence for use. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2217","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true about car​bamazepine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about carbamazepine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is indicated in focal s‍eizures with impaired awa​reness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is an enz‍yme inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It causes h‍ypernatremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the drug of choice f‍or status epilepticus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is indicated in focal seizures with impaired awareness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is indicated in focal seizures with impaired awareness</strong></p><p><strong>Carbamazepine</strong> is indicated in <strong>focal seizures</strong> with<strong> impaired awareness.</strong></p>\n<p>Carbamazepine is an antiepileptic drug that acts by <strong>prolonging</strong> the <strong>inactivated</strong> state of the <strong>Na+ channel</strong> similar to phenytoin. It is used in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Focal seizures with impaired awareness</li>\n<li>Focal seizures with intact awareness</li>\n<li>Generalized tonic-clonic seizures</li>\n<li>Trigeminal and related neuralgias</li>\n<li>Manic depressive psychosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Carbamazepine is an <strong>enzyme inducer</strong>. It is a substrate as well as an inducer of CYP3A4 and CYP2C9.</p>\n<p>Option C: Carbamazepine causes <strong>hyponatremia</strong> and not hypernatremia. Other side effects of carbamazepine include <strong>agranulocytosis</strong> or<strong> aplastic anemia, </strong>hepatotoxicity, water retention and hyponatremia (by potentiating ADH action ), and dose-related neurotoxicity.</p>\n<p>Option D: It is <strong>not</strong> <strong>used</strong> in status epilepticus. <strong>Lorazepam</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>status epilepticus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7027","text":"If carbamazepine is added ‍to ongoing valproate ther​apy, what adverse effect ‌is risked?","raw_text":"If carbamazepine is added to ongoing valproate therapy, what adverse effect is risked?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancre‍atitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thromboc‍ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatot‍oxicity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperam‍monemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatotoxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatotoxicity</strong></p><p>If <strong>carbamazepine</strong> is added to ongoing <strong>valproate</strong> therapy, it can result in <strong>hepatotoxicity</strong>.</p>\n<p>A combination of valproate and carbamazepine increases hepatotoxicity. Valproic acid may inhibit carbamazepine clearance and increase steady-state carbamazepine blood levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1573","text":"In patients recieving carb‍amazepine, which of the f​ollowing idiosyncratic re‌actions are not expected ‌to occur?","raw_text":"In patients recieving carbamazepine, which of the following idiosyncratic reactions are not expected to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ra‍sh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Agranul‍ocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral‍ neuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Steven John‍son syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peripheral neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peripheral neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Peripheral neuropathy </strong>is not an idiosyncratic reaction that can occur after <strong>carbamazepine</strong> use<strong>.</strong> It is a <strong>dose-dependent</strong> CNS adverse effect. It can be minimized by gradually increasing the dose from initiation or reducing the maintenance dose.</p>\n<p><strong>Idiosyncratic reactions</strong> are hypersensitivity reactions like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rash</li>\n<li>Steven-Johnson syndrome</li>\n<li>Eosinophilia</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy.</li>\n<li>Diplopia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Serious hematological toxicity includes aplastic anemia and agranulocytosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2214","text":"A 70-year-old man is on ca‍rbamazepine therapy. Whic​h of the following is exp‌ected to occur in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man is on carbamazepine therapy. Which of the following is expected to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypern‍atremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypona‍tremia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca‍lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypok‍alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyponatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyponatremia</strong></p><p><strong>Carbamazepine</strong> is expected to cause <strong>hyponatremia </strong>in this patient.</p>\n<p>Carbamazepine potentiates the action of antidiuretic hormone (<strong>ADH</strong>). It can result in <strong>water retention</strong> with <strong>decreased osmolarity</strong> and <strong>hyponatremia,</strong> especially in<strong> elderly</strong> patients with cardiac disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2221","text":"An 8-year-old child diagno‍sed to have focal seizure​s was initiated on oxcarb‌azepine. Which of the fol‌lowing would be incorrect‍ regarding the agent bein‍g used?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child diagnosed to have focal seizures was initiated on oxcarbazepine. Which of the following would be incorrect regarding the agent being used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keto analog of carbamazepi‍ne","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enzyme induction potency i‍s less when compared to c​arbamazepine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolised by glucuronide‍ conjugation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyponatremia risk is less ‍when compared to carbamaz​epine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyponatremia risk is less when compared to carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyponatremia risk is less when compared to carbamazepine</strong></p><p>The risk of <strong>hyponatremia </strong>with <strong>oxcarbazepine</strong> is <strong>higher</strong> when compared to carbamazepine. </p>\n<p>Option A: Oxcarbazepine is a <strong>keto analog</strong> of carbamazepine. It is a prodrug. The mechanism of action of oxcarbazepine is by <strong>blocking</strong> voltage-gated <strong>sodium channels</strong> (same as carbamazepine).</p>\n<p>Option C: Active form is metabolized by <strong>glucuronide conjugation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Oxcarbazepine does not cause auto-induction of metabolizing enzymes. It can induce CYP enzymes but less potent when compared to carbamazepine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2227","text":"A neonate diagnosed with r‍habdomyosarcoma was start​ed on vincristine, cyclop‌hosphamide, and cisplatin‌. A week after initiation‍ of treatment, the baby h‍ad multiple episodes of s‌eizures, refractory to di​azepam and lidocaine. Wor‌kup revealed an elevated ‌ADH level and euvolemic h​yponatremia. Which of the‌ following drugs can be u​sed to treat this patient​?","raw_text":"A neonate diagnosed with rhabdomyosarcoma was started on vincristine, cyclophosphamide, and cisplatin. A week after initiation of treatment, the baby had multiple episodes of seizures, refractory to diazepam and lidocaine. Workup revealed an elevated ADH level and euvolemic hyponatremia. Which of the following drugs can be used to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oxcarb‍azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenob‍arbital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phenytoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phenytoin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>refractory seizures</strong> induced by <strong>hyponatremia</strong> caused by <strong>SIADH</strong> (due to anticancer drugs). <strong>Phenytoin</strong> is used to treat this patient. It is known to <strong>decrease ADH secretion</strong> and stop seizures by<strong> correcting</strong> severe <strong>hyponatremia.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A and C: Carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine <strong>increase</strong> the antidiuretic hormone (<strong>ADH</strong>) levels. This leads to increased sensitivity of renal tubules to ADH levels and hyponatremia. Carbamazepine cannot be used in SIADH because of its antidiuretic effects.</p>\n<p>Option D: Phenobarbital does not affect ADH secretion or action and thus will be ineffective in stopping the seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2235","text":"A 5th-grade student, after‍ receiving multiple compl​aints from teachers at sc‌hool for repeated episode‌s of inattention was brou‍ght to the OPD. The mothe‍r gave a history that her‌ child had brief spells o​f staring associated with‌ lip-smacking movements. ‌Which anti-epileptic drug​ is the most effective fo‌r this child?","raw_text":"A 5th-grade student, after receiving multiple complaints from teachers at school for repeated episodes of inattention was brought to the OPD. The mother gave a history that her child had brief spells of staring associated with lip-smacking movements. Which anti-epileptic drug is the most effective for this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethosu‍ximide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gabap‍entin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ethosuximide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ethosuximide</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>absence seizures</strong> and <strong>ethosuximide</strong> is the most effective agent for this condition.</p>\n<p>First-line drugs in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical absence seizures: Ethosuximide (<strong>drug of choice</strong>), valproate, lamotrigine</li>\n<li>Atypical absence seizures: Sodium valproate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2233","text":"A child with absence seizu‍res improves after being ​treated with ethosuximide‌. Which of the following ‌is the mechanism of actio‍n of this drug?","raw_text":"A child with absence seizures improves after being treated with ethosuximide. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits L type Ca2+ chann‍els","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits N type Ca2+ chann‍els","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits T type Ca2+ chann‍els","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits R type Ca2+ chann‍els","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibits T type Ca2+ channels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibits T type Ca2+ channels</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of <strong>ethosuximide</strong> is by<strong> inhibition of T-type Ca<sup>2+</sup> </strong>channels in <strong>thalamic neurons</strong> at clinically relevant concentrations.</p>\n<p>It also affects other ion channels affected include voltage-gated sodium channels and potassium channels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6675","text":"A 10-year-old child is bro‍ught to the OPD with a hi​story of vacant stares la‌sting for about half a mi‌nute. The EEG shows a cha‍racteristic 3 Hz spike an‍d wave pattern. Which of ‌the following drugs can b​e used for this condition‌? Carbamazepine Valproate‌ Ethosuximide Gabapentin ​Pregabalin","raw_text":"<p>A 10-year-old child is brought to the OPD with a history of vacant stares lasting for about half a minute. The EEG shows a characteristic 3 Hz spike and wave pattern. Which of the following drugs can be used for this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Valproate</li>\n<li>Ethosuximide</li>\n<li>Gabapentin</li>\n<li>Pregabalin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 o‍nly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>absence seizures</strong>. <strong>Ethosuximide</strong> and <strong>valproate</strong> are the first-line drugs in this condition.</p>\n<p>Absence seizures are characterized by <strong>sudden</strong>, brief <strong>lapses</strong> of <strong>consciousness</strong> without loss of postural control. There is no postictal confusion. The following subtle motor signs may accompany the episodes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapid blinking of the eyelids</li>\n<li>Chewing movements</li>\n<li>Small-amplitude clonic movements of the hands</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hyperventilation</strong> has a tendency to <strong>provoke</strong> absence seizures. First-line drugs include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical absence seizures: Ethosuximide (<strong>drug of choice</strong>), valproate, lamotrigine</li>\n<li>Atypical absence seizures: Valproate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2245","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an enzyme inducer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an enzyme inducer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenob‍arbital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium v‍alproate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium valproate</strong> is not an enzyme inducer. It is <strong>metabolized by CYP2C9/19</strong> and is an <strong>inhibitor of CYP2C9</strong>.</p>\n<p>Phenytoin, phenobarbital, and carbamazepine are inducers of CYP and UGT enzymes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2238","text":"Which of the following is ‍not the mechanism of acti​on of sodium valproate?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the mechanism of action of sodium valproate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolongation of Na+ channe‍l inactivation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of T type Ca2+ ‍channels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of GABA transam‍inase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of GABA transpo‍rter","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of GABA transporter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of GABA transporter</strong></p><p>Sodium valproate has no action on the GABA transporter.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong> <strong>of action</strong> of <strong>sodium valproate:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolongation of sodium channel inactivation</li>\n<li>Inhibition of T-type Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</li>\n<li>Inhibition of GABA transaminase</li>\n<li>Increases GABA synthesis from glutamic acid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2244","text":"Which of the following are‍ not adverse effects of s​odium valproate?","raw_text":"Which of the following are not adverse effects of sodium valproate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weigh‍t gain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alop‍ecia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liver ‍damage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom‍alacia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteomalacia</strong></p><p>Osteomalacia is not an adverse effect of sodium valproate.</p>\n<p>The <strong>side effects</strong> of valproate (mnemonic: <strong>VALPROATE</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V</strong>omiting</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>lopecia</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>iver toxicity</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ancreatitis</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ash</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>besity</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>norexia</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>eratogenicity (most common: spina bifida), thrombocytopenia, tremors </li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>dema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2241","text":"Which of the following ant‍iepileptic drugs does not​ cause fulminant hepatiti‌s as a serious complicati‌on?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiepileptic drugs does not cause fulminant hepatitis as a serious complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium v‍alproate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Felb‍amate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vigab‍atrin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vigabatrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vigabatrin</strong></p><p><strong>Vigabatrin</strong> is not associated with fulminant hepatitis. It is largely <strong>excreted unchanged</strong> in urine <strong>without</strong> undergoing any <strong>hepatic metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Sodium valproate, felbamate and phenytoin are associated with<strong> fulminant hepatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0943","text":"A 23-year-old female patie‍nt is admitted to the neu​rology ward for evaluatio‌n of GTCS. She reports th‌at she has been on oral c‍ontraceptive pills for th‍e past 6 months. Which of‌ the following drugs woul​d you prescribe to this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old female patient is admitted to the neurology ward for evaluation of GTCS. She reports that she has been on oral contraceptive pills for the past 6 months. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenob‍arbital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Valp‍roate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Valproate</strong></p><p><strong>Valproate</strong> is the drug used for this patient as it <strong>does not cause contraceptive failure.</strong></p>\n<p>Valproate is an enzyme inhibitor and it <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>degradation</strong> of <strong>OCPs.</strong> OCPs thus have a longer duration of action and less incidence of contraceptive failure with valproate.</p>\n<p>However, other antiepileptic drugs like carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, primidone, oxcarbazepine, topiramate are <strong>enzyme inducers</strong>. They cause <strong>increased degradation</strong> of <strong>OCPs</strong> and hence lead to<strong> contraceptive failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2231","text":"A 16-year-old patient was ‍brought to the ER with bi​lateral jerking movements‌ of arms. EEG revealed di‌ffuse, symmetric, bilater‍al 4-6 Hz polyspike and w‍ave discharges with a fro‌ntocentral predominance. ​What is the most appropri‌ate drug for this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old patient was brought to the ER with bilateral jerking movements of arms. EEG revealed diffuse, symmetric, bilateral 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave discharges with a frontocentral predominance. What is the most appropriate drug for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lamot‍rigine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leveti‍racetam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Topir‍amate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium v‍alproate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p>The clinical features and EEG findings are suggestive of <strong>juvenile myoclonic epilepsy.</strong> The most appropriate drug for management is <strong>s</strong><strong>odium valproate. </strong></p>\n<p>In patients with myoclonic seizures which are refractory to valproate, clonazepam or clobazam may be added.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2190","text":"A 55-year-old man visits t‍he neurology OPD with com​plaints of tremors in bot‌h his hands which improve‌ on alcohol intake. On ex‍amination, there is a sig‍nificant worsening of tre‌mors towards the end of f​inger-to-nose testing and‌ head-nodding movements. ‌Which antiepileptic is ap​proved for use in this co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man visits the neurology OPD with complaints of tremors in both his hands which improve on alcohol intake. On examination, there is a significant worsening of tremors towards the end of finger-to-nose testing and head-nodding movements. Which antiepileptic is approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenob‍arbital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prim‍idone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‍roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primidone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primidone</strong></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clinical features are suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>essential tremors</strong> in this patient. <strong>P</strong><strong>rimidone</strong> is considered to be the first-line therapy for the same along with <strong>propranolol.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Bilateral, symmetric</strong> <strong>tremors</strong> involving the <strong>hands</strong> associated with head nodding (<strong>titubation</strong>) and <strong>worsening</strong> of tremors <strong>towards</strong> the <strong>end</strong> of <strong>goal-directed movements</strong> such as drinking from a glass or finger-to-nose testing is suggestive of essential tremors.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Primidone</strong> is a barbiturate and functions as a <strong>GABA enhancer</strong>. It is an enzyme inducer. It is used in the treatment of generalized tonic-clonic seizures and partial seizures.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">It has side effects like sedation, ataxia, and hyperactivity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2210","text":"Pseudolymphoma may occur i‍n patients on long-term t​herapy with:","raw_text":"Pseudolymphoma may occur in patients on long-term therapy with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenob‍arbital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prim‍idone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenytoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenytoin</strong></p><p><strong>Phenytoin</strong> on long-term use can result in <strong>pseudolymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>This lymphadenopathy can be difficult to distinguish from malignant lymphoma. Although some studies suggest a causal relationship between phenytoin use and Hodgkin’s disease, the data are far from conclusive. Almost all lesions are benign and termed as <strong>pseudolymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2207","text":"What is the most common ad‍verse effect of phenytoin​ in children and young ad‌olescents on chronic use?‌","raw_text":"What is the most common adverse effect of phenytoin in children and young adolescents on chronic use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hirs‍utism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom‍alacia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gingival h‍yperplasia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Megaloblas‍tic anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gingival hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gingival hyperplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Gingival hyperplasia</strong> occurs in patients on <strong>chronic phenytoin therapy</strong>. It is the <strong>most common manifestation</strong> in children and young adolescents.</p>\n<p>Gingival hyperplasia due to phenytoin therapy occurs due to tissue overgrowth secondary to <strong>altered collagen metabolism.</strong> The toothless portion of gums is not involved. It does not warrant discontinuation of the drug. It can be <strong>minimized</strong> by maintaining <strong>good oral hygiene.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2223","text":"Which of the following is ‍a carbamazepine analogue ​that has an advantage of ‌once daily dosing?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a carbamazepine analogue that has an advantage of once daily dosing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxcarb‍ezepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eslicarbaze‍pine acetate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dacar‍bazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Licarb‍azepine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Eslicarbazepine acetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Eslicarbazepine acetate</strong></p><p><strong>Eslicarbazepine acetate</strong> is an analog of carbamazepine that has the advantage of <strong>once-daily</strong> dosing. It is a <strong>prodrug</strong> and is rapidly converted to active <strong>licarbazepine</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Oxcarbazepine</strong> is an analog of carbamazepine that is also converted to licarbazepine. Oxcarbazepine does not have the advantage of once-daily dosing like eslicarbazepine acetate.</p>\n<p><strong>Dacarbazine</strong> is not a carbamazepine analog like the other drugs and is a <strong>chemotherapy</strong> medication used in the treatment of <strong>melanoma</strong> and <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1031","text":"A patient with a history o‍f mitral stenosis present​s with the given intracta‌ble finding that is refra‌ctory to conventional the‍rapy. What is the drug of‍ choice?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of mitral stenosis presents with the given intractable finding that is refractory to conventional therapy.  What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Probe‍necid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz‍epam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halop‍eridol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium v‍alproate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium valproate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b859810aab194066b796468d2d827abb.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p>The drug used for <strong>refractory intractable rheumatic chorea</strong> is <strong>sodium valproate, </strong>as it doesn't have extrapyramidal side effects. </p>\n<p>Sydenham's chorea is characterized by <strong>involuntary, purposeless, jerky movements</strong> which mainly affect the <strong>head </strong>and<strong> upper limbs</strong>. In the tongue, it is visible as the characteristic darting movements.</p>\n<p>Medications used to treat Sydenham chorea are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mild</strong> cases - Providing a <strong>calm</strong> environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe</strong> cases:\n<ul>\n<li>Dopamine antagonists - haloperidol, pimozide</li>\n<li>Antiepileptic medications - <strong>sodium valproate, </strong>carbamazepine.  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Severe, refractory cases - Steroids (thought to be immune-mediated)</li>\n<li>IV immunoglobulins, plasmapheresis - limited studies, decreased efficacy (not currently recommended)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2258","text":"A 10-year-old child is bro‍ught to the OPD with a hi​story of vacant stares la‌sting for about half a mi‌nute. The EEG shows &lt;3 Hz‍ spike and wave pattern. ‍The first-line drug for t‌he management of this con​dition is:","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child is brought to the OPD with a history of vacant stares lasting for about half a minute. The EEG shows &lt;3 Hz spike and wave pattern. The first-line drug for the management of this condition is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium v‍alproate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lamot‍rigine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leviti‍racetam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Felb‍amate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sodium valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and <strong>&lt;3 Hz</strong> spike and wave pattern on EEG is suggestive of <strong>atypical</strong> <strong>absence seizures</strong>. The <strong>first-line </strong><strong>drug</strong> in the treatment of atypical absence seizures is <strong>sodium valproate</strong>. </p>\n<p>Symmetric <strong>3 Hz</strong> spike and wave pattern on EEG is seen in<strong> typical absence seizures</strong>. First-line drugs in its treatment include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ethosuximide (<strong>drug of choice</strong>)</li>\n<li>Valproate</li>\n<li>Lamotrigine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1257","text":"An 18-year-old girl was br‍ought to the OPD with com​plaints of excessive grow‌th of hair on her face an‌d chest. She is a known c‍ase of epilepsy and is on‍ treatment with phenytoin‌. Which of the following ​drugs would not cause thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old girl was brought to the OPD with complaints of excessive growth of hair on her face and chest. She is a known case of epilepsy and is on treatment with phenytoin. Which of the following drugs would not cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metoclo‍pramide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methy‍ldopa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amant‍adine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phenoth‍iazines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amantadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amantadine</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of excessive hair growth secondary to treatment with <strong>phenytoin</strong> is suggestive of <strong>hirsutism. </strong><strong>Amantadine</strong> does not cause hirsutism or hypertrichosis but its characteristic side effect is <strong>livedo reticularis</strong> (bluish discoloration of the skin).</p>\n<p>Following are the <strong>drugs causing hirsutism</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Anabolic steroids</li>\n<li>Danazol</li>\n<li>Metoclopramide</li>\n<li>Methyldopa</li>\n<li>Phenothiazines</li>\n<li>Progestins</li>\n<li>Reserpine</li>\n<li>Testosterone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Following are the <strong>drugs causing hypertrichosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyclosporine</li>\n<li>Diazoxide</li>\n<li>Hydrocortisone</li>\n<li>Minoxidil</li>\n<li>Penicillamine</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Psoralens</li>\n<li>Streptomycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_xqhTCLX","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"19_functional_anatomy","title":"19.Functional Anatomy","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA1641","text":"What is the total number o​f airway divisions presen‍t in between trachea and ​alveolar sacs?","raw_text":"What is the total number of airway divisions present in between trachea and alveolar sacs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 23","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7b988dbca9747cdaebbc31059bb4448x289x377.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 23</strong></p><p>The total number of <strong>airway generations </strong>present in between the trachea and alveolar sac is <strong>23</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> tracheobronchial tree </strong>is divided into multiple generations based on <strong>Weibel’s lung model. </strong>It begins at the <strong>trachea </strong>(designated as<strong> zero</strong>) and ends with<strong> alveoli </strong>(last generation). There are 2 zones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conducting zone</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Consists of the first 16 generations</li>\n<li>Incapable of gas exchange</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory zone</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Consists of the last 7 generations (17-23)</li>\n<li>Capable of gas exchange</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> </strong><strong>Terminal bronchiole </strong>is the<strong> transition point </strong>between the conducting and respiratory zone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1973","text":"Which among the following ​has the highest airway re‍sistance?","raw_text":"Which among the following has the highest airway resistance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alveol​ar duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bro​nchi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory​ bronchioles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small br​onchioles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bronchi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bronchi</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> bronchi</strong><strong> (medium-sized airway) </strong>offer<strong> maximum resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>The best answer here is bronchi, as all the other options suggest smaller airways, while bronchi are the only medium-sized airway.</p>\n<p>Airflow can be derived from <strong>Poiseuille-Hagen formula:</strong> </p>\n<p>Q =π ΔP<strong>r<sup>4</sup></strong>/8ηL</p>\n<p>Where</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Q is the flow rate</li>\n<li>π is pie symbol</li>\n<li>ΔP is the pressure difference</li>\n<li>r is radius</li>\n<li>η is viscosity</li>\n<li>L is length</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Also,</p>\n<p>Flow rate (Q) = pressure difference (ΔP)/ resistance (R).</p>\n<p>Therefore, substituting this in Poiseuille- Hagen formula,</p>\n<p>ΔP/ R = π ΔP<strong>r<sup>4</sup></strong>/8ηl</p>\n<p>1/R = π <strong>r<sup>4</sup></strong>/8ηl</p>\n<p><strong>R</strong> = 8ηl/ π <strong>r<sup>4</sup></strong></p>\n<p>Therefore it can be derived that <strong>airway resistance</strong> is <strong>inversely proportional</strong> to the <strong>radius</strong> by the <strong>power of four</strong>.</p>\n<p>With Poiseuille- Hagen formula we might feel that the smallest airways are the major site of resistance. However, the contribution by the smallest airways is very little to the overall total resistance. This is because, the airway branches in each generation exist in <strong>parallel</strong> rather than in series. As the parallel branching increases, the resistance decreases. (<strong>Total</strong> cross-sectional diameter of small airways put together is high). Thus the overall resistance of the small airways is very small. Also, airflow velocity decreases substantially as the effective cross-sectional area increases (i.e., the flow becomes laminar).</p>\n<p>Other factors affecting airway resistance :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lung volume</strong>, as lung volume increases the resistance decreases and vice versa. Lung volume increases the transairway pressure thus increasing the caliber of the airway.</li>\n<li><strong>Airway mucus, edema</strong>, and <strong>contraction</strong> of bronchial smooth muscle increase the airway resistance. Its clinical application is seen in asthma, bronchitis, and emphysema</li>\n<li>The <strong>density </strong>and <strong>viscosity</strong> of inspired gas also contribute to airway resistance. A gas with more density imparts more resistance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9732","text":"A 14-year-old boy with sho​rtness of breath was diag‍nosed with chronic inflam​mation of conducting airw‌ays. Which of the followi‌ng is not a feature of th‍e affected structure?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy with shortness of breath was diagnosed with chronic inflammation of conducting airways. Which of the following is not a feature of the affected structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Includes first 16 generati​ons starting from trachea‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth muscles and cartila​ge predominate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secrete variety of molecul​es that aid in lung defen‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membrane bound organelles ​namely lamellar bodies ar‍e present","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Membrane bound organelles namely lamellar bodies are present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Membrane bound organelles namely lamellar bodies are present</strong></p><p>Chronic inflammation of conducting airways is suggestive of <strong>asthma</strong>. <strong>Lamellar bodies</strong> are storage bodies of <strong>surfactant</strong> present in the <strong>alveoli</strong> and not the conducting airway.</p>\n<p>The conducting airways form the first <strong>16 generations</strong> from the trachea to the terminal bronchial. It is lined by <strong>pseudostratified ciliated columnar</strong> epithelium. It also consists of <strong>smooth muscles</strong> which are responsible for bronchoconstriction and dilatation. It also consists of other specialized cells such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Basal cells</strong> - stem cells</li>\n<li><strong>Clara/Club cells</strong> - specialized epithelial cells (non-ciliated cuboidal cells) that:\n<ul>\n<li>Functions as stem cells</li>\n<li>Helps in lung defense.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The conducting airways secrete several substances that in lung defense such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secretory immunoglobins -<strong> IgA</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Collectins</strong> - surfactant protein(SP)-A and SP-D</li>\n<li><strong>Defensins</strong></li>\n<li>Reactive <strong>oxygen</strong> species</li>\n<li>Reactive <strong>nitrogen</strong> species.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1642","text":"Maximal smooth muscle rela​tive to wall thickness is‍ seen in _______.","raw_text":"Maximal smooth muscle relative to wall thickness is seen in _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terminal ​bronchiole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tra​chea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bro​nchi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory​ bronchioles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Terminal bronchiole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Terminal bronchiole</strong></p><p><strong>Smooth muscle</strong> is <strong>maximum in terminal bronchioles</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1684","text":"An elderly smoker who pres​ented with an acute exace‍rbation of COPD is admini​stered salbutamol. Which ‌one of the following subs‌tances has a similar mech‍anism of action?","raw_text":"An elderly smoker who presented with an acute exacerbation of COPD is administered salbutamol. Which one of the following substances has a similar mechanism of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subst​ance P","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasoactive intestinal poly​peptide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leukot​rienes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholinergic​ stimulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vasoactive intestinal polypeptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f02c268f40734b5791257b331920524ex1280x1092.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vasoactive intestinal polypeptide</strong></p><p><strong>Salbutamol</strong>, a short-acting beta (adrenergic) agonist (SABA) is a bronchodilator. Similarly, <strong>vasoactive intestinal polypeptide</strong> (VIP) is also a <strong>bronchodilator</strong>. Along with <strong>dynorphin</strong>, it is part of the <strong>non-adrenergic non-cholinergic (NANC) inhibitory system.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bronchial innervation:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Type of receptor</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Effect</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sympathetic system</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tracheal and bronchial smooth muscle</td>\n<td>β2</td>\n<td>Relaxation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bronchial glands</td>\n<td>\n<p>α1</p>\n<p>β2</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Reduce secretion</p>\n<p>Increase secretion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Parasympathetic system (more predominant)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tracheal and bronchial smooth muscle</td>\n<td>M2, M3</td>\n<td>Contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bronchial glands</td>\n<td>M2, M3</td>\n<td>Stimulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other <strong>potent bronchoconstrictors</strong> are <strong>histamine</strong> and <strong>leukotrienes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical relevance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methacholine sensitivity test </strong>or Airway hyperresponsiveness test: Methacholine which is similar to acetylcholine causes <strong>exaggerated bronchoconstriction </strong>in case of <strong>bronchial asthma</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>5-Lipoxygenase inhibitor</strong> (5-LOX inhibitor-Zileuton), <strong>selective CysLT-receptor antagonists</strong> (Cys leukotriene receptor antagonist-zafirlukast, pranlukast, and montelukast), and <strong>β2 receptor agonists</strong> are used in the treatment of asthma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1697","text":"Which of the following cel​ls secrete surfactant in ‍lungs?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells secrete surfactant in lungs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 p​neumocyte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alveolar ​macrophage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dust ​cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 2 p​neumocyte","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 2 pneumocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/534b570945cb46298a05dd5a760a32e6x512x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 2 pneumocyte</strong></p><p><strong>Type 2 pneumocytes </strong>secrete surfactant in the lungs.</p>\n<p><strong>Alveolar Lining Epithelial Cells</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Two types </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I cells</strong> – The primary lining cells of the alveoli (<strong>squamous epithelium); Helps in gas exchange</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Type II cells</strong>  – <strong>Granular pneumocytes produce surfactant</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Type</strong> <strong>II</strong> cells make <strong>60%</strong> of epithelial cells in alveoli but <strong>type</strong> <strong>I</strong> cells form <strong>95% of the total surface area</strong> because of its flat surface.</p>\n<p><strong>Type II cells </strong>are <strong>stem cells </strong>which help in replenishing damaged Type 1 pneumocytes in conditions like drug-induced pulmonary toxicity by amiodarone, bleomycin etc. </p>\n<p><strong>Other cells </strong>– Pulmonary alveolar macrophages (<strong>PAMs/type 3 alveolar cells</strong>), lymphocytes, plasma cells, neuroendocrine cells (APUD cells-Amine precursor uptake and decarboxylation), and mast cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1698","text":"Surfactant is composed mai​nly of_____","raw_text":"Surfactant is composed mainly of_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphatid​yl glycerol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceph​alin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leci​thin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pro​tein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lecithin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lecithin</strong></p><p><strong>Major surfactant</strong> is <strong>dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine i.e Lecithin. </strong>Sphingomyelin is a minor surfactant.</p>\n<p><strong>The surfactant</strong> is a <strong>surface-active agent in water</strong>, which means that it greatly <strong>reduces the surface tension of water.</strong></p>\n<p>Pulmonary surfactant is <strong>produced by type 2 pneumocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most important components are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phospholipid - <strong>dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)</strong> also known as Dipalmitoyl Lecithin. Lecithin is the most abundant lipid in surfactant.</li>\n<li>Phosphatidylglycerol- minor component</li>\n<li>Surfactant apoproteins</li>\n<li>Calcium ions.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>4 unique apoproteins of surfactant: surfactant protein (SP)-A, SP-B, SP-C, and SP-D.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical relevance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tests to assess fetal lung maturity:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>L/S ratio </strong>i.e. Lecithin/Sphingomyelin ratio:<strong> Ratio ≥ 2 is considered normal.</strong></li>\n<li>Shake test/Bubble test/Clement test: Formation of stable foam in the presence of surfactant.</li>\n<li>Phosphatidylglycerol levels in Amniotic fluid</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autosomal dominant familial interstitial lung disease</strong> has <strong>absent surfactant protein SP-C.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis - Autoantibody against GM-CSF </strong>which is involved in<strong> clearance of surfactant.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1703","text":"All are true of surfactant​ except_____","raw_text":"All are true of surfactant except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helps prevent pulmonary ed​ema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deficiency of surfactant c​auses Infant Respiratory ‍Distress Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Helps prevents alveolar co​llapse according to Berno‍ulli's principle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in surfactant inc​reases the work of breath‍ing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Helps prevents alveolar collapse according to Bernoulli's principle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Helps prevents alveolar collapse according to Bernoulli&#x27;s principle</strong></p><p>Surfactant helps to  <strong>prevent alveolar collapse </strong>according to<strong> Law of Laplace.</strong>(Not Bernoulli's)</p>\n<p>Physiologic advantages of surfactant</p>\n<ul>\n<li>By reducing surface tension it <strong>increases lung compliance</strong></li>\n<li>Reduces the lung's tendency to recoil</li>\n<li>Helps to keep the <strong>alveoli dry</strong></li>\n<li>Helps to <strong>prevent pulmonary edema (Option A)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The surface tension generates a pressure that tends to collapse the sphere.</p>\n<p>In accordance <strong>with Law of Laplace</strong> - <strong>P=2T/r, </strong>where<br/>P - Collapsing pressure on alveolus (dynes/cm<sup>2</sup>) or Pressure required to keep alveolus open (dynes/cm<sup>2</sup>)<br/>T = Surface tension (dynes/cm)<br/>r = Radius of the alveolus (cm).</p>\n<p>Collapsing pressure is inversely related to radius i.e. <strong>large alveolus</strong> (one with a large radius) will have a <strong>low collapsing pressure</strong> and therefore will require only minimal pressure to keep it open. On the other hand, a <strong>small alveolus</strong> (one with a small radius) will have a <strong>high collapsing pressure</strong> and require more pressure to keep it open.</p>\n<p>By reducing surface tension, <strong>surfactant reduces the collapsing pressure for </strong><strong>a given radius.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B:</strong> Surfactant deficiency is an important cause of <strong>infant respiratory distress syndrome (IRDS)</strong> also known as <strong>hyaline membrane disease</strong>, the serious pulmonary disease that develops in infants born before their surfactant system is functional.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In IRDS, there is high surface tension in the lungs resulting in atelectasis</li>\n<li>Also, there is retention of fluid in the lungs.\n<ul>\n<li>During fetal life, Cl<sup>- </sup>is secreted with fluid by the pulmonary epithelial cells. At birth, there is a shift to Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption by these cells via ENaC and fluid is absorbed along with Na<sup>+</sup>. Prolonged immaturity of ENaC contributes to pulmonary edema in IRDS.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option D: Surfactant increases lung compliance</strong>, which <strong>reduces the work of expanding the lungs during inspiration</strong>. Increasing the compliance of the lungs reduces the collapsing force at any given volume so that it is easier for the lungs to expand.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1699","text":"A young lady at 35 weeks o​f gestation is at high ri‍sk of preterm delivery in​ the next 7 days. She is ‌started on antenatal cort‌icosteroid therapy to inc‍rease surfactant producti‌on in the fetus. What is ​the mechanism of action o​f this substance?","raw_text":"A young lady at 35 weeks of gestation is at high risk of preterm delivery in the next 7 days. She is started on antenatal corticosteroid therapy to increase surfactant production in the fetus. What is the mechanism of action of this substance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breaks the structure of wa​ter in alveoli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lubricate the flow of CO2 ​diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Binds ​oxygen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Makes the capillary surfac​e hydrophilic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Breaks the structure of water in alveoli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e18a761aa54403bae543849196bd81cx1280x1922.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Breaks the structure of water in alveoli</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of surfactant is by <strong>breaking </strong>the<strong> structure of water </strong>in alveoli.</p>\n<p>When water forms a surface with air, the water molecules on the surface of the alveoli have a strong attraction for one another. This pulls the molecules together, leading to high surface tension.</p>\n<p>The surfactant is <strong>amphipathic </strong>in nature (i.e., hydrophobic on one end and hydrophilic on the other). They align themselves on the alveolar surface, with their hydrophobic portions facing the alveolar lumen.</p>\n<p>The<strong> cohesive force </strong>between a water molecule and an adjacent pulmonary surfactant molecule is<strong> very low.</strong> So, they <strong>break up </strong>the<strong> attracting forces between water molecules lining the alveoli</strong> (which had been responsible for the high surface tension). Thus, they reduce surface tension.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1702","text":"Stability of alveoli is ma​intained by______.","raw_text":"Stability of alveoli is maintained by______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung co​mpliance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Negative intrapleural pres​sure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased alveolar surface​ area by surfactant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Residual ai​r in alveoli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased alveolar surface area by surfactant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased alveolar surface area by surfactant</strong></p><p>Stability of alveoli is maintained by <strong>increased</strong> <strong>alveolar surface area by surfactant.</strong></p>\n<p>Surfactant <strong>reduces</strong> the lung's <strong>tendency to recoil</strong> so that they <strong>do not collapse easily</strong> and so the stability of the alveoli is maintained.</p>\n<p>Factors that maintain alveolar stability</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary surfactant: ↓ surface tension within alveoli</li>\n<li>Mechanical interdependence: prevents alveolar collapse</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interdependence</strong></p>\n<p>The tendency of one alveolus to collapse is opposed by the traction exerted by the surrounding alveoli. Thus, the collapse of a single alveolus stretches and distorts the surrounding alveoli, which in turn are connected to other alveoli. This support offered to lung units by those surrounding them is termed Interdependence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1700","text":"A woman at risk for preter​m delivery is planned for‍ assessment of fetal lung​ maturity. At what gestat‌ional age will the surfac‌tants be detectable in th‍e amniotic fluid?","raw_text":"A woman at risk for preterm delivery is planned for assessment of fetal lung maturity. At what gestational age will the surfactants be detectable in the amniotic fluid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"28 w​eeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"34 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"35 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 28 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 28 weeks</strong></p><p>Surfactant appears in the amniotic fluid between <strong>28 and 32</strong> weeks of gestation. </p>\n<p>By week 20-24, primitive alveoli form and surfactant production begins but it does not reach the surface of the lungs until later.</p>\n<p>Mature levels of pulmonary surfactant are present usually after 35 wk of gestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical aspects :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preterm infants develop respiratory distress syndrome or hyaline membrane disease due to surfactant deficiency.</li>\n<li>Antenatal corticosteroids are given to increase the production of surfactants.</li>\n<li>Lecithin/Sphingomyelin ratio is a test for fetal lung maturity. L/S ratio &gt; 2 is suggestive of a mature fetal lung.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9750","text":"A woman on multiple medica​tions came for an antenat‍al check-up. Which among ​the following medications‌ that she takes decreases‌ surfactant production?","raw_text":"A woman on multiple medications came for an antenatal check-up. Which among the following medications that she takes decreases surfactant production?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid ​hormones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta adrener​gic agonists","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inadequat​e insulin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium s​upplements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inadequate insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inadequate insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Inadequate Insulin </strong>intake in diabetic pregnant causes inadequate glucose control. This causes <strong>excess glucose</strong> in maternal plasma, <strong>crossing Placenta</strong>. Excess glucose in fetal plasma causes fetal pancreas to <strong>hypersecrete fetal Insulin</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Excess insulin</strong> in fetal plasma <strong>decreases surfactant </strong>production. This is the reason for the <strong>increased incidence </strong>of<strong> hyaline membrane disease </strong>in <strong>infants of diabetic mothers</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Factors increasing surfactant synthesis:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucocorticoids</li>\n<li>Thyroxine</li>\n<li>Stretching of lungs in infancy</li>\n<li>Beta adrenergic agonist</li>\n<li>Calcium</li>\n<li>Regular exercise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Factors decreasing surfactant synthesis:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Long term inhalation of 100% oxygen</li>\n<li>Occlusion of main bronchus/pulmonary artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1685","text":"Which is the best metaboli​c function of the lung?","raw_text":"Which is the best metabolic function of the lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inactivation​ of serotonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivation of bradykinin​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolism o​f basic drugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conversion of angiotensin ​I to angiotensin II","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II</strong></p><p>Conversion of <strong>angiotensin I to angiotensin II</strong> is the most significant metabolic function of the lungs.</p>\n<p>This is mediated by <strong>angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)</strong>, located on the <strong>surface of endothelial cells</strong> in the pulmonary capillaries.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthesized and used in the lungs</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Surfactant</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Synthesized or stored and released into the blood</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Prostaglandins</li>\n<li>Histamine</li>\n<li>Kallikrein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Partially removed from the blood</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Prostaglandins</li>\n<li>Bradykinin</li>\n<li>Adenine nucleotides</li>\n<li>Serotonin</li>\n<li>Norepinephrine</li>\n<li>Acetylcholine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Activated in the lungs</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Angiotensin I → Angiotensin II</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BmGLSEIm","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"19_somatoform_disorders","title":"19.Somatoform Disorders","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MC1257","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the DSM-5‌ group somatic symptoms a​nd related disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the DSM-5 group somatic symptoms and related disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Functional neurological sy​mptom disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dissociati​ve disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Factitiou​s disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Illness anxi​ety disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dissociative disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dissociative disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Dissociative disorder</strong> is <strong>classified separately</strong> under DSM-5.</p>\n<p>The somatic symptoms and related disorders group in DSM-5 includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Somatic symptom disorder (<strong>somatoform disorder</strong>)</li>\n<li>Illness anxiety disorder (<strong>hypochondriasis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Functional neurological symptom disorder (<strong>conversion disorder</strong>)</li>\n<li>Factitious disorder (<strong>Munchausen syndrome</strong>)</li>\n<li>Psychological factors affect­ing other medical conditions</li>\n<li>Other specified somatic symptoms and related disorders</li>\n<li>Unspecified somatic symptoms and related disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5302","text":"An anxious woman presents ​to your OPD with complain‌ts of abdominal pain and ​headache. She says she ha‌d to take extended leave ​from work over the past y‍ear because of this. She ‍had visited many doctors ​but to no avail. On evalu‌ation, you find no organi​c cause, but she is not c​onvinced. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An anxious woman presents to your OPD with complaints of abdominal pain and headache. She says she had to take extended leave from work over the past year because of this. She had visited many doctors but to no avail. On evaluation, you find no organic cause, but she is not convinced. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conversio​n disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somatic symp​tom disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Munchause​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malin​gering","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Somatic symptom disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Somatic symptom disorder</strong></p><p>In the scenario, the presence of one or more<strong> somatic symptoms</strong> for <strong>more than 6 months </strong>in the <strong>absence</strong> of an <strong>organic cause</strong> is suggestive of <strong>somatic symptom disorder</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 355\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for the diagnosis of somatic symptom disorder: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>One or more somatic symptoms that are distressing or result in significant disruption of daily life.</li>\n<li>Excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviors related to the somatic symptoms or associ­ated health concerns as manifested by at least one of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Disproportionate and persistent thoughts about the seriousness of one’s symptoms.</li>\n<li>Persistently high level of anxiety about health or symptoms.</li>\n<li>Excessive time and energy are devoted to these symptoms or health concerns.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Although a single somatic symptom may not be continuously present, the state of be­ing symptomatic is persistent (typically more than 6 months).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Smith's consultation letter</strong> provides the primary care physicians with <strong>dos and don'ts </strong>during an encounters with patients with multiple medically unexplained physical symptoms. It suggests the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regularly scheduled appointments</li>\n<li>Performing brief physical examinations focusing on the area of discomfort at each visit</li>\n<li>Avoid unnecessary diagnostic procedures, invasive treatments, and hospitalizations</li>\n<li>Avoid using statements such as symptoms are all in your head</li>\n<li>Encourage patients to talk about stressors</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Treatment is <strong>psychotherapy.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5303","text":"A 25-year-old woman comes ​to the psychiatric OPD, c‌onvinced that she is suff​ering from a brain tumor.‌ She says she has been ex​periencing continuous hea‍daches for the past 6 mon‍ths. Though all investiga​tions are normal, she con‌tinues to worry and asks ​the doctor to do some mor​e tests. What is your dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman comes to the psychiatric OPD, convinced that she is suffering from a brain tumor. She says she has been experiencing continuous headaches for the past 6 months. Though all investigations are normal, she continues to worry and asks the doctor to do some more tests. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somatic symp​tom disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depersonalization disorder​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Functional neurological sy​mptom disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Illness anxi​ety disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Illness anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Illness anxiety disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of the patient preoccupied with <strong>thoughts of suffering from a disease</strong> (brain tumor) based on her interpretation of a benign headache for <strong>6 months</strong> is suggestive of <strong>illness anxiety disorder </strong>i.e.,<strong> </strong>hypochondriasis.</p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for the diagnosis of illness anxiety disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness.</li>\n<li>Somatic symptoms are absent or mild in intensity. If another medical condition is present or there is a high risk for developing a medical condition (e.g., strong family history is present), the preoccupation is clearly excessive or disproportionate.</li>\n<li>The individual performs excessive health-related behaviors (e.g., repeatedly checks his or her body for signs of illness) or exhibits maladaptive avoidance (e.g., avoids doc­tor appointments and hospitals).</li>\n<li>Preoccupation with illness for at least 6 months, but the specific illness that is feared may change over that period of time.</li>\n<li>The illness-related preoccupation is not better explained by another mental disorder. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In <strong>illness anxiety disorder</strong>, the patient is preoccupied with a <strong>disease</strong> whereas, in <strong>somatic symptom disorder</strong>, the patient is preoccupied with <strong>symptoms.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1281","text":"A 30-year-old man is broug​ht to the psychiatric OPD‌ with a wildly ataxic gai​t and uncontrollable thra‌shing of his arms. He say​s these symptoms started ‍after a heated quarrel wi‍th his business partner. ​Your senior diagnoses him‌ to have astasia-abasia. ​Which of the following is​ associated with this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man is brought to the psychiatric OPD with a wildly ataxic gait and uncontrollable thrashing of his arms. He says these symptoms started after a heated quarrel with his business partner. Your senior diagnoses him to have astasia-abasia. Which of the following is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conversio​n disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Illness anxi​ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatic symp​tom disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Munchause​n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conversion disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conversion disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Astasia-abasia </strong>is associated with <strong>conversion disorder </strong>(functional neurological symptom disorder).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 493\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is a wildly ataxic, staggering gait accompanied by gross, irregular, jerky truncal movements and thrashing and waving arm movements. Patients with these symptoms rarely fall and even if they do, they are generally not injured.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5304","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of conversi‌on disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of conversion disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autonomic​ features","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primary and secondary gain​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temporality between stress​or and symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"La Belle i​ndifference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autonomic features","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autonomic features</strong></p><p><strong>Autonomic symptoms</strong> are not seen in conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder).</p>\n<p>A <strong>conversion disorder</strong> is an acute and temporary<strong> </strong>loss or alteration in motor or sensory function <strong>triggered</strong> by psychosocial <strong>stress. </strong>Most of the patients have a history of traumatic events in childhood. The symptoms or deficits of conversion disorder are not intentionally produced. There is a time difference seen between the stressors and symptoms. </p>\n<p>The channeling or <strong>conversion</strong> of the emotional arousal to physical symptoms is <strong>primary gain</strong>, while the <strong>benefits</strong> that come out of having the physical symptoms is the<strong> secondary gain</strong>. For example, it forcing a spouse to stay in an otherwise failing marriage.</p>\n<p>La Belle indifference (beautiful indifference) refers to the patient's <strong>blissful indifference</strong> to what appears to be a <strong>significant impairment. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5306","text":"A patient was brought to t​he emergency room with co‌mplaints of inability to ​walk or lift his left leg‌ for the past 2 days. His​tory and physical examina‍tion were unremarkable. H‍owever, he did not seem c​oncerned about his condit‌ion. What is the most lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the emergency room with complaints of inability to walk or lift his left leg for the past 2 days. History and physical examination were unremarkable. However, he did not seem concerned about his condition.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Illness anxi​ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Factitiou​s disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malin​gering","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conversio​n disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Conversion disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Conversion disorder</strong></p><p>The absence of concern towards one’s problem despite an apparent severe disability is known as <strong>La-belle indifference</strong>. It is seen in <strong>conversion disorder. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1259","text":"A 35-year-old woman with a​ history of amenorrhea fo‌r 5 months presents to yo​ur OPD for an antenatal c‌heckup. On examination, h​er breasts were enlarged ‍and contained milk. Her a‍bdomen was distended, but​ no fetal heart sounds we‌re appreciated. Her spot ​UPT was negative and pelv​ic ultrasound, unremarkab​le. She tells you that sh‍e had a miscarriage five ​years ago after which she​ struggled with depressio‌n. Under which of the fol​lowing would you classify‌ this condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of amenorrhea for 5 months presents to your OPD for an antenatal checkup. On examination, her breasts were enlarged and contained milk. Her abdomen was distended, but no fetal heart sounds were appreciated. Her spot UPT was negative and pelvic ultrasound, unremarkable. She tells you that she had a miscarriage five years ago after which she struggled with depression. Under which of the following would you classify this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factitiou​s disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malin​gering","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Other somatic symptom and ​related disorders","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dissociati​on disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Other somatic symptom and related disorders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Other somatic symptom and related disorders</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>pseudocyesis.</strong> Pseudocyesis is a <strong>false belief of being pregnant</strong> that is associated with objective signs and reported symptoms of pregnancy. It is classified under <strong>other specified somatic symptoms and related disorders.</strong></p>\n<p>Pseudocyesis is the development of the classic symptoms of pregnancy - amenorrhea, nausea, breast enlarge­ment and pigmentation, abdominal distention and even labor pains, in a nonpregnant woman. It demonstrates the ability of the mind (psyche) to dominate the body (soma).</p>\n<p>Predisposing psychological processes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A pathological wish for, and fear of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Ambivalence or conflict regarding gender, sexuality, or childbearing</li>\n<li>Grief reaction to loss following a miscarriage, tubal ligation or hysterectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Disorders classified under other specified somatic symptoms and related disorders include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brief somatic symptom disorder-<strong> </strong>duration of symptoms is less than 6 months</li>\n<li>Brief illness anxiety disorder- duration of symptoms is less than 6 months</li>\n<li>Illness anxiety disorder without excessive health-related behaviors</li>\n<li>Pseudocyesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Factitious disorder</strong> (Munchausen syndrome) is the intentional production of physical symptoms to get<strong> medical attention. </strong>There is <strong>no intention </strong>to obtain a <strong>practical gain.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B - <label class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Malingering</strong> refers to mimicking a physical or clinical finding with an associated <strong>motive to gain benefit</strong>. </label></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7UXfZt-G","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"19_carcinoma_stomach","title":"19.Carcinoma Stomach","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2416","text":"Which of the following fac‌tors will increase the ri‌sk of a patient developin​g gastric carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors will increase the risk of a patient developing gastric carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eating refri‌gerated food","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Femal‌e sex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cigarett‌e smoking","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cigarette smoking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cigarette smoking</strong></p><p><strong>Cigarette smoking</strong> is a risk factor for gastric carcinoma. </p>\n<p><strong>Aspirin</strong>, which is a COX inhibitor, is <strong>protective</strong> against gastric cancer. </p>\n<p><strong>Eating refrigerated food</strong> has reduced the need for salting of food for preservation, and thus <strong>reduced the incidence</strong> of gastric cancer.</p>\n<p><strong>Males</strong> are more commonly affected as compared to females.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2419","text":"What is the most common si‌te of gastric adenocarcin‌oma in the Asian populati​on?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of gastric adenocarcinoma in the Asian population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Car‌dia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bo‌dy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ant‌rum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antrum</strong></p><p>The most common site of gastric adenocarcinoma in the <strong>Asian</strong> population is the <strong>antrum</strong>. This is because <strong>H. pylori infections</strong> are relatively more prevalent in Asian countries and are associated with carcinoma of the <strong>body and distal stomach</strong> than the proximal stomach.</p>\n<p>However, in <strong>western</strong> countries, the <strong>proximal stomach</strong> is the most common site. This is attributed to a lesser prevalence of untreated H.pylori infections and a greater burden of <strong>chronic GERD</strong> and obesity in western countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2414","text":"A man presents to the hosp‌ital with a 3-day history‌ of haematemesis. Upper G​I endoscopy reveals multi‍ple gastric polyps and a ​positive urease breath te‍st. Which type of polyp i‌s he most likely to have?​","raw_text":"A man presents to the hospital with a 3-day history of haematemesis. Upper GI endoscopy reveals multiple gastric polyps and a positive urease breath test. Which type of polyp is he most likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflam‌matory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hamart‌omatous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperp‌lastic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno‌matous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperplastic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperplastic</strong></p><p>The given history and investigations are suggestive of<strong> Helicobacter pylori infection</strong>. The polyps most commonly associated with H. pylori infection are <strong>hyperplastic polyps</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hyperplastic polyps usually regress following H. pylori eradication therapy.</p>\n<p>Benign gastric polyps are classified into two groups: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Neoplastic</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adenoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fundic gastric polyp</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Non- neoplastic</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Hyperplastic polyp (most common)</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inflammatory polyp</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hamartomatous polyp</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>The terms hyperplastic polyp and metaplastic polyp refer to the same lesion, however, 'hyperplastic polyp' is a preferred term.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2420","text":"An elderly patient who pre‌sented with abdominal dis‌comfort and weight loss w​as diagnosed with type I ‍gastric cancer according ​to Lauren’s classificatio‍n. Which of the following‌ is not true regarding th​is condition?","raw_text":"An elderly patient who presented with abdominal discomfort and weight loss was diagnosed with type I gastric cancer according to Lauren’s classification. Which of the following is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More well di‌fferentiated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with gastric at‌rophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphati‌c spread","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"More comm‌on in men","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphatic spread","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphatic spread</strong></p><p><strong>Type I gastric cancer</strong> is associated with <strong>hematogenous spread</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Lauren's system </strong>classifies gastric cancer pathology as either <strong>type I (intestinal)</strong> or <strong>type II (diffuse)</strong>, based on differences in etiology, epidemiology, and pathology. <strong>Intestinal type</strong> of gastric cancer shows <strong>hematogenous</strong> spread, whereas <strong>diffuse</strong>-type shows <strong>transmural</strong> and <strong>lymphatic</strong> spread.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2422","text":"Which of the following gen‌etic mutations is least l‌ikely to be seen in a pat​ient with an intestinal t‍ype of gastric cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following genetic mutations is least likely to be seen in a patient with an intestinal type of gastric cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutation i‌n β-catenin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of heterozygosity in ‌the APC gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Under expression of transf‌orming growth factor α","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of heterozygosity at ‌the bcl-2 gene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Under expression of transforming growth factor α","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Under expression of transforming growth factor α</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1065\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1065\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In the <strong>intestinal type</strong> of gastric cancer, several growth factors may be <strong>overexpressed</strong>, including <strong>transforming growth factor α</strong>, the epidermal growth factor, and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF).</p>\n<p><strong>Molecular pathologies seen in Intestinal type</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mutation or loss of heterozygosity in the APC gene or in β-catenin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inactivation of p53</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loss of heterozygosity at the BCL-2 gene</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Microsatellite instability</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Molecular pathologies seen in diffuse-type</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mutation of E- cadherin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inactivation of p53</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2424","text":"A man, presented with mult‌iple episodes of non-bili‌ous vomiting containing u​ndigested food particles ‍within one-hour post meal​s. On physical examinatio‍n, he was found to be ane‌mic and cachexic. CECT ab​domen revealed diffuse in‌filtration of a neoplasti‌c growth into the stomach‌ wall. What Bormann’s cla​ssification does this tum‌or belong to?","raw_text":"A man, presented with multiple episodes of non-bilious vomiting containing undigested food particles within one-hour post meals. On physical examination, he was found to be anemic and cachexic. CECT abdomen revealed diffuse infiltration of a neoplastic growth into the stomach wall. What Bormann’s classification does this tumor belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type IV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type IV</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1153\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The history and investigation findings are suggestive of <strong>gastric outlet obstruction</strong> caused by a <strong>diffusely infiltrating type of gastric carcinoma</strong>. This is commonly known as <strong>linitis plastica</strong> and it is classified as <strong>type IV</strong> in Bormann’s classification system. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2433","text":"A patient was found to hav‌e a tumor extending up to‌ 1cm below the gastroesop​hageal junction on EUS. A‍ccording to the Siewert c​lassification, which stag‍e of carcinoma does she h‌ave?","raw_text":"A  patient was found to have a tumor extending up to 1cm below the gastroesophageal junction on EUS. According to the Siewert classification, which stage of carcinoma does she have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II</strong></p><p>The patient is likely to have a<strong> Siewert type II</strong> lesion. This include tumors with the <strong>center </strong>located<strong> 1 cm above </strong>and<strong> 2 cm below the GE junction</strong>. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I</strong>: Adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus - the epicenter of the lesion is <strong>1-5 cm above GEJ</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Type II</strong>: Adenocarcinoma of the cardia - the epicenter of the lesion is up to <strong>1 cm above and 2 cm below GEJ</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Type III</strong>: Subcardial type adenocarcinoma - the epicenter of the lesion is <strong>2-5 cm below GEJ</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2431","text":"A woman presented to the g‌astroenterology OPD with ‌significant weight loss, ​fatigue, and a bloating s‍ensation for the past 8 m​onths. A detailed workup ‍was done, and she was dia‌gnosed with early gastric​ carcinoma. All of the fo‌llowing structures can be‌ involved in this stage e‌xcept:","raw_text":"A woman presented to the gastroenterology OPD with significant weight loss, fatigue, and a bloating sensation for the past 8 months. A detailed workup was done, and she was diagnosed with early gastric carcinoma. All of the following structures can be involved in this stage except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscular‌is mucosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subm‌ucosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Musculari‌s propria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph‌ nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Muscularis propria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/796f18c2bd4b44ddbd9042bef9aa93cdx600x1110.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Muscularis propria</strong></p><p>In <strong>early gastric cancer</strong>, there is<strong> no involvement of muscularis propria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gastric cancer can be classified as early and advanced gastric cancer, based on the extent of involvement. </p>\n<p><strong>Early gastric cancer involves</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mucosa (including muscularis mucosa)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Submucosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">+/- lymph nodes</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Advanced gastric cancer involves</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Muscularis layer</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">+/- adjacent structures</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2436","text":"An Asian man who is a know‌n smoker presented to the‌ OPD with dysphagia, naus​ea, and loss of appetite.‍ Endoscopy revealed a tum​or arising from the stoma‍ch. Which of the followin‌g statements is false reg​arding TNM staging for th‌is condition?","raw_text":"An Asian man who is a known smoker presented to the OPD with dysphagia, nausea, and loss of appetite. Endoscopy revealed a tumor arising from the stomach. Which of the following statements is false regarding TNM staging for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In T4 lesions, tumor perfo‌rates the serosa.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastasis in 1-5 lymph no‌des is N1 disease.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tumor invades adjacent org‌ans in T4 lesions.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metastasis in &amp;gt; 15 lymp‌h nodes is N3b.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metastasis in 1-5 lymph nodes is N1 disease.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metastasis in 1-5 lymph nodes is N1 disease.</strong></p><p>The clinical features and endoscopy findings point to a diagnosis of <strong>gastric carcinoma</strong>. According to <strong>TNM staging</strong> of gastric carcinoma, <strong>N1</strong> disease refers to <strong>metastasis to 1-2 regional lymph nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2427","text":"A positive Troisier’s sign‌ refers to the involvemen‌t of which of the followi​ng?","raw_text":"A positive Troisier’s sign refers to the involvement of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right supraclavicular lymp‌h nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left supraclavicular lymph‌ nodes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Internal mammary lymph nod‌es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tracheo- bronchial lymph n‌odes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left supraclavicular lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb44d27e035c4ea3aadfbe96f2d1a0c6x1280x560.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left supraclavicular lymph nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Troisier’s sign </strong>refers to the enlargement of <strong>left supraclavicular lymph nodes</strong>, which is also known as <strong>Virchow nodes</strong>. It is seen in patients with <strong>gastric cancer metastasis</strong>, caused by the spread of the tumor via the thoracic duct into the left supraclavicular lymph nodes. It is a sign of advanced cancer.</p>\n<p>It is one of the <strong>atypical presentations of gastric carcinoma.</strong> Other atypical presentations of gastric carcinoma include</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Sister Mary Joseph nodule</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px; text-align: justify;\">Due to periumbilical metastasis. </span></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">See in both gastric cancer (most common) and ovarian cancer.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Krukenberg’s tumor</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Bilateral ovarian metastasis</span></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Due to retrograde lymphatic spread</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Irish nodule</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Left axillary lymphadenopathy.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Blumer shelf nodules</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Pelvic metastasis into the pouch of Douglas/ retrovesical pouch</span></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Sign of advanced gastric carcinoma </span></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Felt on digital palpation</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Trosseau syndrome</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Migratory thrombophlebitis</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Leser Trelet sign</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Multiple seborrheic keratoses</span></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Resolves after gastrectomy</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tripe palms</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Hyperkeratosis palm.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows an elderly man with left supraclavicular lymph nodes enlarged.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2445","text":"A male patient who was dia‌gnosed with early gastric‌ cancer is planned for en​doscopic mucosal resectio‍n. Which of the following​ features would make him ‍a poor candidate for this‌ procedure?","raw_text":"A male patient who was diagnosed with early gastric cancer is planned for endoscopic mucosal resection. Which of the following features would make him a poor candidate for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tumor measu‌ring 1.5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulceration ‌of the tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tumor limited to the mucos‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Moderately well- different‌iated tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulceration of the tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulceration of the tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Endoscopic mucosal resection</strong> for gastric cancer should <strong>not </strong>be performed in case of <strong>ulcerated gastric cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications for endoscopic resection of early gastric cancer</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tumor smaller than 2cm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tumor limited to the mucosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No lymphovascular invasion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No ulceration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Well or moderately well-differentiated histopathology.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If any of these are <strong>not fulfilled</strong>, then <strong>gastrectomy with lymph node dissection</strong> is indicated.</p>\n<p>For a <strong>proximal</strong> gastric carcinoma a <strong>total</strong> <strong>gastrectomy</strong> is done and for <strong>distal</strong> gastric carcinoma, <strong>subtotal</strong> <strong>gastrectomy</strong> is ideal. For advanced carcinoma gastrectomy can be done as a palliative treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2441","text":"According to the Japanese ‌classification for gastri‌c carcinoma, which of the​ following is the level 5‍ lymph node?","raw_text":"According to the Japanese classification for gastric carcinoma, which of the following is the level 5 lymph node?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser curv‌ature nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supra-pyl‌oric nodes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left para-tr‌acheal nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right para-c‌ardial nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Supra-pyloric nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6311ce3afea34c8297d1a5bd6ab4ad9cx1280x2550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Supra-pyloric nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Suprapyloric</strong> nodes are the<strong> level 5</strong> gastric lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>The <strong>japanese classification for gastric carcinoma (JCGC)</strong> staging system divided lymph nodes into <strong>16 groups</strong> based on the likelihood of metastasis from the primary tumor and removal of lymph nodes during gastrectomy.</p>\n<p>The<strong> surgery for gastric cancer</strong> involves the <strong>removal of the primary tumor and lymph nodes.</strong> Depending on the extent of lymph node stations removed, the surgery may be either  D1 or D2 gastrectomy. </p>\n<p><strong>D1 gastrectomy</strong> involves the removal of lymph node stations <strong>1-6</strong>. <strong>D2 gastrectomy</strong> involves the removal of lymph node stations <strong>1-11. <br/></strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2442","text":"A patient with gastric ade‌nocarcinoma is posted for‌ surgery. What is the ext​ent of negative margin yo‍u should remove around th​e tumor for this patient?‍","raw_text":"A patient with gastric adenocarcinoma is posted for surgery. What is the extent of negative margin you should remove around the tumor for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 ‌cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8.5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10‌ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 cm</strong></p><p>In surgical resection of gastric cancer, at least <strong>5cm of negative margin</strong> should be removed around the tumor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2446","text":"A patient presented with v‌ague abdominal pain and f‌eeling of a lump in the a​bdomen for the past 1 yea‍r. He was found to have a​ tumor arising from the s‍tomach on CT scan. Biopsy‌ of the lesion showed tum​or cells positive for CD1‌17 and CD34. What is the ‌most likely origin of the‌ tumor?","raw_text":"A patient presented with vague abdominal pain and feeling of a lump in the abdomen for the past 1 year. He was found to have a tumor arising from the stomach on CT scan. Biopsy of the lesion showed tumor cells positive for CD117 and CD34. What is the most likely origin of the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paneth‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitial cells of Caja‌l","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Goblet‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intestinal‌ stem cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Interstitial cells of Cajal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Interstitial cells of Cajal</strong></p><p>The clinical and histopathological features are suggestive of <strong>gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST)</strong>. They arise from the <strong>interstitial cells of Cajal</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2448","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of Carney‌'s triad?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Carney's triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extra-adrenal paragangliom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary‌ chondroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic a‌denocarcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GI‌ST","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pancreatic adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pancreatic adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Carney's triad</strong> does <strong>not</strong> include <strong>pancreatic adenocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carney’s triad consists of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Extra-adrenal paraganglioma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gastrointestinal stromal tumor</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulmonary chondroma</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2451","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‌ with epigastric pain and‌ intermittent vomiting fo​r 2 months. He was inadeq‍uately treated for H. pyl​ori infection 3 months ba‍ck. The patient is HIV po‌sitive and is on ART for ​the same. Endoscopy revea‌ls a fungating mass arisi‌ng from the stomach. Biop‌sy of the lesion shows tu​mor cells positive for CD‌20. Which of the followin‍g is false regarding this​ tumor?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with epigastric pain and intermittent vomiting for 2 months. He was inadequately treated for H. pylori infection 3 months back. The patient is HIV positive and is on ART for the same.  Endoscopy reveals a fungating mass arising from the stomach. Biopsy of the lesion shows tumor cells positive for CD20. Which of the following is false regarding this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stomach is the most common‌ organ involved.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most common ‌type is NHL.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fundus is the most common ‌site involved","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with H. p‌ylori infections","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fundus is the most common site involved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fundus is the most common site involved</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>gastric lymphoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>The most common type of gastric lymphoma is non-Hodgkin's lymphoma<strong> (NHL)</strong>. Among NHL, the most common type is diffuse large B-cell lymphoma<strong> (DLBCL</strong>) (55%), followed by gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma, Burkitt lymphoma, mantle cell and follicular lymphoma.</p>\n<p><strong>I</strong><strong>mmunodeficiencies</strong> and <strong>H. pylori infections</strong> are risk factors for the development of primary diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.</p>\n<p>The<strong> antrum</strong> is the most common site for gastric lymphoma. The <strong>stomach</strong> is the most common organ to develop lymphoma in the GI system.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Combined modality - chemotherapy, surgery, and/or radiotherapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The most common <strong>chemotherapeutic combination</strong> for DLBCL is <strong>R-CHOP</strong> [Rituximab, Cyclophosphamide, Hydroxydaunomycin (doxorubicin), Oncovin (vincristine), prednisone].</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2453","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for sur‌gery in a patient with ga​stric lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for surgery in a patient with gastric lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blee‌ding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obstr‌uction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptomatic residual disea‌se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intracta‌ble pain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intractable pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intractable pain</strong></p><p><strong>Intractable pain</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an indication for surgery in the case of gastric lymphoma. </p>\n<p>Indications for surgery in gastric lymphoma:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with <strong>symptomatic recurrence</strong> or <strong>treatment failure </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients who develop <strong>complications</strong>, such as bleeding, gastric outlet obstruction, and perforation </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6948","text":"A woman underwent a total ‌gastrectomy 5 months ago ‌for gastric carcinoma. Wh​ich of the following is t‍he most common metabolic ​complication expected in ‍her?","raw_text":"A woman underwent a total gastrectomy 5 months ago for gastric carcinoma. Which of the following is the most common metabolic complication expected in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron defici‌ency anemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B12‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteop‌orosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom‌alacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Iron deficiency anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Iron deficiency anemia</strong></p><p>The most common metabolic complication seen after gastrectomy is <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although <strong>absorption of iron</strong> occurs primarily in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, it needs an optimum <strong>acidic environment</strong> for absorption. Hence, due to a combination of decreased iron intake, impaired iron absorption, and chronic blood loss, there is iron deficiency anemia. The addition of <strong>iron supplements</strong> to the patient’s diet corrects iron deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications of gastrectomy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dumping syndrome: early and late</li>\n<li>Nutritional deficiencies- <strong>Iron deficiency anemia (most common), </strong>Vit B12 deficiency, and osteoporosis</li>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Duodenal stump leak/blow out</li>\n<li>Bile reflux gastritis and esophagitis</li>\n<li>Gall stones</li>\n<li>Roux syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4262","text":"A 50-year-old male present‌ed with upper GI bleeding‌, abdominal pain, and a p​alpable abdominal mass. A‍n endoscopic biopsy revea​led a 5 cm tumor, with a ‍high mitotic rate and c-K‌IT positivity in the body​ of the stomach. Which of‌ the following is the bes‌t treatment of choice?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male presented with upper GI bleeding, abdominal pain, and a palpable abdominal mass. An endoscopic biopsy revealed a 5 cm tumor, with a high mitotic rate and c-KIT positivity in the body of the stomach. Which of the following is the best treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgical resection + surve‌illance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgical resection + imati‌nib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical resection + D2 ga‌strectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical resection + cispl‌atin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surgical resection + imatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surgical resection + imatinib</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>GIST</strong> with a<strong> high risk of malignancy (high mitotic rate</strong>). The best treatment modality in the given scenario is <strong>surgical resection</strong> (with negative margin) followed by <strong>imatinib</strong>. Imatinib is a tyrosine-kinase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option A: Surgical resection + surveillance is done for GIST with a low risk of malignancy. The <strong>risk</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>malignancy</strong> is determined by 2 factors - the <strong>size of the tumor and mitotic index</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lymphadenectomy </strong>(D2 gastrectomy)<strong> is not done</strong> in GIST as it does not show lymph node metastasis.</p>\n<p>Option D: GIST is <strong>radioresistant</strong> and does not respond to conventional chemotherapy. It only responds to imatinib. If imatinib resistant, <strong>sunitinib</strong> or <strong>sorafenib</strong> is given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4248","text":"The gastrectomy specimen o‌f a 56-year-old woman who‌ underwent total gastrect​omy showed HER2/neu posit‍ivity. Which among the fo​llowing is the drug of ch‍oice for the treatment of‌ this patient?","raw_text":"The gastrectomy specimen of a 56-year-old woman who underwent total gastrectomy showed HER2/neu positivity. Which among the following is the drug of choice for the treatment of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teg‌afur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trast‌uzumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pembro‌lizumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oter‌acil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trastuzumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trastuzumab</strong></p><p><strong>Trastuzumab</strong> is the drug of choice in carcinoma stomach with <strong>HER2/neu</strong> mutation. Trastuzumab is a HER2 receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>Chemotherapy options in carcinoma stomach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With<strong> HER2/neu</strong> mutation - Trastuzumab/Herceptin</li>\n<li>With <strong>PD-L1</strong> gene mutation - Pembrolizumab</li>\n<li><strong>Oral</strong> chemotherapy drugs:\n<ul>\n<li>Tegafur</li>\n<li>Oteracil</li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Gimeracil</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Conventional chemotherapy drugs used are 5-fluorouracil and cisplatin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chemotherapy is offered for patients with<strong> lymph node-positive</strong> disease and <strong>advanced</strong> gastric carcinoma (T3 and beyond) as part of palliative treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KyTNOSlO","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"20_regional_anesthesia__techniques","title":"20.Regional Anesthesia  Techniques","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA6068","text":"In adults, at which verteb‌ral level does the spinal‍ cord end?","raw_text":"In adults, at which vertebral level does the spinal cord end?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper bor‌der of L1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower bor‌der of L1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper bor‌der of L3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower bor‌der of L3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower border of L1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower border of L1</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In adults, the spinal cord ends at the level of <strong>lower border of L1</strong> vertebra.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Proximally, the spinal cord is continuous with the brain stem. Distally, it terminates in the conus medullaris as the filum terminale (fibrous extension) and the cauda equina (neural extension).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The lumbar puncture during spinal anesthesia (a.k.a. sub-arachnoid block, intra-thecal block) is done below the level of L1 in adults (L2-3, L3-4, L4-5 interspace), to avoid potential needle trauma to the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6069","text":"You are about to administe‌r spinal anaesthesia to a‍ 7-month-old child. At wh​ich vertebral level does ‌his spinal cord end?","raw_text":"You are about to administer spinal anaesthesia to a 7-month-old child. At which vertebral level does his spinal cord end?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower bor‌der of S2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper bor‌der of S2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower bor‌der of L3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper bor‌der of L3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Upper border of L3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Upper border of L3</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The spinal cord in <strong>infants</strong> ends at the <strong>upper border</strong> of <strong>L3</strong> vertebral level (shorter vertebral column and longer spinal cord).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lumbar (subarachnoid) puncture into the subarachnoid space is done below L3 in children to avoids potential needle trauma to the cord.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6070","text":"At which vertebral level d‌oes the dural sac end at ‍in adults?","raw_text":"At which vertebral level does the dural sac end at in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"S‌2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. S2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. S2</strong></p><p>The <strong>dural sac</strong> in adults ends at <strong>S2</strong> vertebral level.</p>\n<p>So the spinal subdural and subarachnoid spaces extend from the foramen magnum to S2 in adults.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6071","text":"You are administering caud‌al anesthesia to a 9-mont‍h-old child. Which is the​ lowest vertebral level t‌hat should not be crossed​ by the tip of your needl‍e so as to avoid accident‍al subarachnoid injection​?","raw_text":"You are administering caudal anesthesia to a 9-month-old child. Which is the lowest vertebral level that should not be crossed by the tip of your needle so as to avoid accidental subarachnoid injection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. S2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. S2</strong></p><p>The needle should be kept below<strong> </strong>the<strong> S2</strong> vertebral level as the <strong>subarachnoid space</strong> in children extends from the foramen magnum to<strong> </strong>S2-S3.</p>\n<p>Because of this fact, and the smaller body size, caudal anesthesia carries a greater risk of subarachnoid injection in children than in adults.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6142","text":"As an intern posted in the‌ OT, you see the anesthes‍ia resident mark out a li​ne on a patient’s back. S‌he tells you that this is​ Tuffier’s line and is us‍ed as a landmark for perf‍orming spinal anesthesia.​ Which vertebral level do‍es this line correspond t‌o?","raw_text":"As an intern posted in the OT, you see the anesthesia resident mark out a line on a patient’s back. She tells you that this is Tuffier’s line and is used as a landmark for performing spinal anesthesia. Which vertebral level does this line correspond to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. L4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19bee39064f140cc840d74ccb8c89487x1280x1618.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. L4</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tuffier's (intercristal) line is a line drawn between the <strong>highest points</strong> of both <strong>iliac crests</strong> usually crossing either the <strong>body of L4 </strong>or the<strong> L4–L5 interspace.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is the landmark to identify the spinal level prior to performing a neuraxial block.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other bony landmarks:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">C7 - Vertebra prominence</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">T7 - Inferior angle of scapula</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">L1 - Lower rib margin</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">S2 - Posterior superior iliac spine</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6080","text":"Following neuraxial block,‌ which would be the first‍ sensation to disappear?","raw_text":"Following neuraxial block, which would be the first sensation to disappear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fine ‌touch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pinprick ‌sensation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vibr‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivi‌ty to cold","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sensitivity to cold","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sensitivity to cold</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The first sensation to disappear following a neuraxial block is <strong>cold. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1969\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pre-ganglionic sympathetic fibers (<strong>B fibers</strong>) are <strong>most sensitive</strong> to local anesthetic blockade.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" style=\"text-align: justify;\" title=\"Page 1969\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The larger A-alpha motor fibers are more resistant than any of the sensory fibers.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The sensations lost at the earliest will be <strong>cold → </strong><strong>pinprick → touch </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" style=\"text-align: justify;\" title=\"Page 1969\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Regression of blockade (“recovery”) follows in the reverse order: motor function &gt; touch &gt; pinprick &gt; cold sensation &gt; autonomic.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Controversial:<strong> </strong>There is an obvious discrepancy among various authors regarding the order of differential blockade. Some studies mention pin prick sensation to be lost before sensation to cold. But our reference to this MCQ is from standard textbook of anesthesia (Miller's Anesthesia 9th edition).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6081","text":"A patient underwent knee r‌eplacement under spinal a‍naesthesia. Following the​ procedure, you examine h‌im in the post-op ward. W​hich of the following wou‍ld be the first to recove‍r?","raw_text":"A patient underwent knee replacement under spinal anaesthesia. Following the procedure, you examine him in the post-op ward. Which of the following would be the first to recover?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heat se‌nsation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Touch s‌ensation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pinprick ‌sensation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Motor f‌unction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Motor function","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Motor function</strong></p><p><strong>Motor function</strong> <strong>recovers</strong> the <strong>earliest</strong> after a neuraxial block.</p>\n<p>Motor function is carried by <strong>A alpha fibers</strong>, which are myelinated and having the largest diameter, making them resistant to local anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>Hence, motor function is the last to get affected and the first to recover.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6466","text":"You are given the opportun‌ity to perform a neuraxia‍l block procedure. What i​s the antiseptic of choic‌e to prepare the site bef​ore this procedure?","raw_text":"You are given the opportunity to perform a neuraxial block procedure. What is the antiseptic of choice to prepare the site before this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Povidon‌e iodine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glutara‌ldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen‌ peroxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorh‌exidine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chlorhexidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chlorhexidine</strong></p><p>Antiseptic of choice for neuraxial block procedure is <strong>Chlorhexidine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6087","text":"While administering spinal‌ anaesthesia to a patient‍, which of the following ​structures would you not ‌be piercing?","raw_text":"While administering spinal anaesthesia to a patient, which of the following structures would you not be piercing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligament‌um flavum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraspino‌us ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior longitudinal lig‌ament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dura‌mater","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior longitudinal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior longitudinal ligament</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During spinal anaesthesia administration, the posterior longitudinal ligament is not pierced.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The following <strong>structures</strong> are <strong>pierced </strong>during spinal anaesthesia, in that order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Skin</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Subcutaneous tissue</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supraspinous ligament</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Interspinous ligament</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ligamentum flavum </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dura mater</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Arachnoid</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6088","text":"While administering spinal‌ anaesthesia in a patient‍, between which of the fo​llowing structures would ‌you be injecting the loca​l anaesthetic?","raw_text":"While administering spinal anaesthesia in a patient, between which of the following structures would you be injecting the local anaesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dura and ligamentum flavum‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dura and ‌arachnoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pia and ‌vertebra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pia and ‌arachnoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pia and arachnoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34fa20b7c39b4635a335e9192fd679f4x1280x1238.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pia and arachnoid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Spinal anesthesia </strong>(subarachnoid block, intrathecal block) involves the administration of local anesthetic between the <strong>pia and arachnoid mater.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In spinal anesthesia, the local anesthetic is injected into <strong>CSF,</strong> which bathes the nerve root. Hence, a relatively small dose and volume of local anesthetic is sufficient to achieve dense sensory and motor blockade.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Epidural</strong> anesthesia involves local anesthetic administration between the <strong>dura and ligamentum flavum.</strong> Subdural injection, seen in a failed spinal anesthesia, is between dura and arachnoid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6135","text":"In a normal sized adult, w‌hich of the following par‍ameters would least affec​t the level of neuraxial ‌block achieved?","raw_text":"In a normal sized adult, which of the following parameters would least affect the level of neuraxial block achieved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CSF v‌olume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hei‌ght","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patient's‌ position","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Height","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Height</strong></p><p>In a <strong>normal-sized</strong> adult, the patient's<strong> height does not </strong>seem to affect the spread of spinal anaesthesia. However, at extremes of height, consideration should be given to altering the dose accordingly.</p>\n<p>Option A: Advanced age is associated with increased block height, due to decreased CSF volume</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>CSF volume</strong> is <strong>inversely</strong> related to the <strong>block level.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Patient's position affects the spread of hyperbaric and hypobaric solutions. <strong>Hyperbaric</strong> solution settles at the <strong>dependent</strong> position while <strong>hypobaric</strong> solution settles at the <strong>non-dependent</strong> position.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6091","text":"Which of the following fac‌tors have the least impac‍t on the height of neurax​ial block in a patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors have the least impact on the height of neuraxial block in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gen‌der","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Preg‌nancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hei‌ght","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gender","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gender</strong></p><p>The <strong>gender</strong> of the patient is the factor that <strong>least affects</strong> the height of the neuraxial block, among the options given.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1976\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Theoretically, the density of CSF is lower in women than in men. Although this may affect relative baricity of local anaesthetics, the clinical variation in the spread is probably unimportant. Hence, this is the best answer to choose from the above options. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1976\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option A:<strong> Higher</strong> anaesthetic levels are achieved in the <strong>elderly</strong> for a given dosage of spinal anaesthetic.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Increased intraabdominal pressure</strong> or conditions that cause engorgement of the epidural veins, decrease CSF volume and thus <strong>increase block height.</strong> This includes conditions such as pregnancy, ascites, and large abdominal tumors.</p>\n<p>Option D: Within the range of “normal-sized” adults, patient height does not seem to affect the spread of spinal anaesthesia. But, <strong>extremes of height</strong> can affect the block height. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6089","text":"A patient is set to underg‌o cesarean section under ‍subarachnoid block. Upto ​which dermatome must the ‌anaesthetist ensure adequ​ate anaesthesia?","raw_text":"A patient is set to undergo cesarean section under subarachnoid block. Upto which dermatome must the anaesthetist ensure adequate anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T‌8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T4</strong></p><p>For a cesarean section, the sensory block should be achieved up to <strong>T4</strong> dermatomal level (motor T6), as the pain-sensitive peritoneum innervation starts from T4 level.</p>\n<p>The risk of high spinal and severe hypotension is higher because the block has to reach T4. Hence, consider prehydration with 20 ml/kg, proper positioning, and keeping phenylephrine or ephedrine on hand.</p>\n<p>Dermatomal Level required for Various Common Surgical Procedures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>T4 - Upper abdominal surgery</li>\n<li>T10 - Transurethral resection of prostate</li>\n<li>T10 - Hip surgery</li>\n<li>L2 - Foot and ankle surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6136","text":"In which procedure is the ‌instrument shown in the i‍mage most commonly used?","raw_text":"In which procedure is the instrument shown in the image most commonly used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrobul‌bar block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Combined spinal epidural a‌nesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spinal anesthesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d8aa86afc9946eeb0c6023f876606f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc83b39240494ecbb5cb9a9adcb76d8ax1280x1043.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72880dd6f2d44b2a8c5bbe0c67671aa1x1024x700.jpeg","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0715d42672ab45b5b07344bb7ed590ecx1280x2262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spinal anesthesia</strong></p><p>The instrument shown is the<strong> Quincke Needle</strong> used in <strong>spinal anesthesia</strong>. The Quincke needle is a cutting needle with end injection.</p>\n<p>Spinal needles are long thin needles that are classified based on their tip into: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting needles: Quincke-Babcock, Pitkin</li>\n<li>Pencil tip needles: Whitacre and Sprotte</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>This is the image of a <strong>Tuohy needle</strong> that is used to provide <strong>epidural anesthesia</strong>. It can be recognised by its <strong>curved (Huber) tip</strong> and the <strong>markings made at 1 cm interval</strong> along it's length.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The above image shows a <strong>Combined Spinal Epidural needle</strong>. It consists of a spinal needle passed through the epidural needle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6137","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a blunt-tipped needle‍ used in regional anaesth​esia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a blunt-tipped needle used in regional anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Qui‌ncke","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Whit‌acre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spr‌otte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tu‌ohy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Quincke","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e89e66193ea34835b52bd3ecb9e3ccebx1280x1043.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c5f87b91a3e446581af1edcdf3dee25x1200x799.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Quincke</strong></p><p>All of the above are blunt-tipped needles used in regional anesthesia, except <strong>Quincke</strong>, which is a <strong>cutting</strong> type of spinal needle.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of spinal needles:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 381px;\" width=\"291\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.641px;\"><strong>Cutting needle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.359px;\"><strong>Non-cutting (Splitting, pencil point, blunt) needle</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.641px;\">Cut the dura</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.359px;\">Split the dura</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.641px;\">Hence, higher incidence of postdural puncture headache (PDPH) due to CSF leak</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.359px;\">Hence, lower incidence of PDPH</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.641px;\">Technically less challenging as loss of resistance is well appreciated during lumbar puncture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.359px;\">Technically more challenging as loss of resistance is not well appreciated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.641px;\"><strong>Eg: Quincke-Babcock, Pitkin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.359px;\"><strong> Eg: Whitacre, Sprotte</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Tips of various spinal needles</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tuohy needle</strong> is the most commonly used needle for epidural anaesthesia. It has a <strong>blunt 'Huber'</strong> tip, which helps to prevent accidental penetration of the dura.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image: </strong>Touhy epidural needle with Huber tip</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6457","text":"As an intern posted in the‌ OT, you see the Anesthes‍ia Senior Resident holdin​g the needle shown in the‌ image. What is the likel​y procedure that he is ab‍out to perform?","raw_text":"As an intern posted in the OT, you see the Anesthesia Senior Resident holding the needle shown in the image. What is the likely procedure that he is about to perform?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Combined spinal epidural a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IV cann‌ulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epidural anesthesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/61d90c1de9e242abbe580eeda0811ed9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c453ca187034b57880a2e096cc43108x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epidural anesthesia</strong></p><p>The image shown is the 18 G <strong>Tuohy needle,</strong> which is used in<strong> epidural anesthesia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Identification points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It has a <strong>blunt, curved tip (Huber tip)</strong> that helps to <strong>prevent accidental dural puncture</strong></li>\n<li>The needle shaft is marked in 1-cm intervals so that the depth of insertion can be identified</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Epidural anesthesia set with Touhy's needle and epidural catheter.</p>\n<p>Other epidural needles:<strong> Crawford needle</strong><strong> </strong>(straight needles without a curved tip): has a greater incidence of dural puncture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6458","text":"While administering epidur‌al anesthesia to a patien‍t, in which of the follow​ing spaces would you inje‌ct the local anesthetic?","raw_text":"While administering epidural anesthesia to a patient, in which of the following spaces would you inject the local anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1524213d927543b9979a69fe196331a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B</strong></p><p>During epidural anesthesia, the local anesthetic is injected into the <strong>epidural/extradural space</strong>. This is marked in the image as <strong>B</strong>.</p>\n<p>The epidural space exists between the dura and the ligamentum flavum posteriorly.</p>\n<p>The other markings are as follows:</p>\n<p>A: Subarachnoid space</p>\n<p>C. Interspinous ligament</p>\n<p>D. Subcutaneous tissue</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6459","text":"A patient set to undergo a‌n elective C section rece‍ives epidural anaesthesia​ before the procedure. Wh‌ich structure is not pier​ced while performing this‍ block?","raw_text":"A patient set to undergo an elective C section receives epidural anaesthesia before the procedure. Which structure is not pierced while performing this block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dura‌mater","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Supraspino‌us ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligament‌um flavum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duramater","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duramater</strong></p><p>Dura mater is not pierced while performing an epidural block.</p>\n<p>The <strong>structures pierced</strong> during epidural anesthesia:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Subcutaneous tissue</li>\n<li>Supraspinous ligament</li>\n<li>Interspinous ligament</li>\n<li>Ligamentum flavum</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Epidural space is a potential <strong>negative pressure space</strong>. This factor is exploited in the identification of epidural space.</p>\n<p>As the needle penetrates the ligamentum flavum, an increase in resistance is encountered. After which when the needle enters the epidural space, a sudden loss of resistance is encountered.</p>\n<p>The techniques to identify epidural space:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of resistance (to injection of air or saline)</li>\n<li>Hanging drop (drop of saline is sucked in as the needle enters epidural space)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6465","text":"You are planning to admini‌ster combined spinal epid‍ural anaesthesia to a pre​-eclamptic patient before‌ an elective C section. Y​ou know that the patient ‍may have to remain seated‍ for a while after admini​stration of the spinal an‍aesthetic for insertion o‌f the epidural catheter.W​hich of the following wil‍l you use for spinal anae‌sthesia to reduce the cha‌nces of hemodynamic distu‍rbances in this scenario?‍","raw_text":"You are planning to administer combined spinal epidural anaesthesia to a pre-eclamptic patient before an elective C section. You know that the patient may have to remain seated for a while after administration of the spinal anaesthetic for insertion of the epidural catheter.Which of the following will you use for spinal anaesthesia to reduce the chances of hemodynamic disturbances in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5% Bupivacaine with gluc‌ose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.5% Bupivacaine with fent‌anyl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.5% Bupivacaine with ster‌ile water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.5% Plain ‌bupivacaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.5% Bupivacaine with glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.5% Bupivacaine with glucose</strong></p><p>To <strong>prevent hemodynamic disturbances</strong>, we must prevent the migration of the spinal block to upper thoracic segments. In the sitting position, this can be achieved by using a <strong>hyperbaric</strong> solution like<strong> bupivacaine with</strong> <strong>glucose</strong>.</p>\n<p>Baricity of local anesthetics refers to the specific gravity of the solution with respect to that of CSF.</p>\n<p>A <strong>hyperbaric</strong> solution (addition of <strong>glucose</strong>) of local anesthetic is denser (heavier) than CSF, a <strong>hypobaric</strong> solution (addition of <strong>sterile water</strong> or <strong>fentanyl</strong>) is lighter than CSF and <strong>isobaric</strong> solution (<strong>plain </strong>or 1:1 CSF) has density same as that of CSF.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperbaric</strong> solution tends to move to the <strong>dependent</strong> area of the spine. While a <strong>hypobaric</strong> solution moves to the <strong>non-dependent</strong> area. An isobaric solution remains at the level of injection.</p>\n<p>With the patient in a head-down position, a hyperbaric solution spreads cephalad, and a hypobaric solution moves caudally.</p>\n<p>In clinical practice, hyperbaric solutions are most commonly used during spinal anesthesia. A hypobaric solution is only used in prone <strong>jackknife position</strong> to specifically block the operative site around <strong>perineum</strong> during certain anorectal surgeries. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6084","text":"You administer epidural an‌esthesia to a patient bef‍ore surgery. Which of the​ following methods can yo‌u use to test the adequac​y of motor block in him? ‍Electromyography Train of‍ four ratio Modified Brom​age Scale Bispectral inde‍x Spirometry","raw_text":"<p>You administer epidural anesthesia to a patient before surgery. Which of the following methods can you use to test the adequacy of motor block in him?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Electromyography</li>\n<li>Train of four ratio</li>\n<li>Modified Bromage Scale</li>\n<li>Bispectral index</li>\n<li>Spirometry</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‌ 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,‌ 3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl‌y 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Electromyography, Modified Bromage scale,</strong> and <strong>spirometry</strong> can all be used to test the adequacy of the motor block after neuraxial anesthesia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Modified Bromage Scale is most commonly used, but the disadvantage is that this represents only lumbosacral motor fibers.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Modified Bromage scale</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">0: No motor block</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">1: Inability to raise extended leg; Able to move knees and feet</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">2: Inability to raise extended leg and move knee; Able to move feet</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">3: Complete block of motor limb</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Electromyography</strong> and pulmonary function tests (<strong>spirometry</strong>) have been used to measure abdominal and thoracic motor function, but these are neither practical nor specific.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Train of four ratio</strong> and <strong>bispectral index</strong> are used in monitoring a patient under <strong>general anesthesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6460","text":"A 5-month-old child is set‌ to undergo a herniotomy ‍procedure for congenital ​inguinal hernia under cau‌dal anesthesia. Where is ​the local anesthetic inje‍cted into for this proced‍ure?","raw_text":"A 5-month-old child is set to undergo a herniotomy procedure for congenital inguinal hernia under caudal anesthesia. Where is the local anesthetic injected into for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spina‌l cord","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subdura‌l space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subarachn‌oid space","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidura‌l space","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epidural space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ea7bdd5c87044999c6f822f4faf49c0x1280x1890.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epidural space</strong></p><p>During <strong>caudal anaesthesia</strong>, the local anaesthetic is injected into the sacral portion of the <strong>epidural space</strong>.</p>\n<p>Caudal anaesthesia involves a needle and/or catheter penetration of the<strong> sacrococcygeal ligament </strong>which opens into the caudal epidural space. The sacrococcygeal ligament covers the<strong> sacral hiatus </strong>that is created by the <strong>unfused S4 and S5 laminae.</strong></p>\n<p>Sacral hiatus is well appreciated in children. Hence, it is a <strong>common</strong> regional technique in <strong>paediatrics </strong>for procedures below the diaphragm, including urogenital, rectal, inguinal, and lower extremity surgery. </p>\n<p>It may also be used for anorectal surgery in adults but it is <strong>technically challenging</strong> in <strong>adults</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6461","text":"An 18-month-old boy with C‌TEV is undergoing correct‍ive treatment. He is sche​duled for a posteromedial‌ soft tissue release proc​edure. You see the anaest‍hesia resident mark out t‍he points shown in the im​age on his back. In what ‍anaesthetic procedure are‌ these landmarks used?","raw_text":"An 18-month-old boy with CTEV is undergoing corrective treatment. He is scheduled for a posteromedial soft tissue release procedure. You see the anaesthesia resident mark out the points shown in the image on his back. In what anaesthetic procedure are these landmarks used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caudal a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saddle‌ block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caudal anesthesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ecd2fe0d398452da490d5f09beeca95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2be78b40ccbb43008e075d4a4c536dd7x1280x1890.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caudal anesthesia</strong></p><p>The landmarks depicted in the image are in reference to <strong>caudal anaesthesia. </strong>It is a common regional technique in paediatrics for procedures below the diaphragm, including urogenital, rectal, inguinal, and lower extremity surgery. </p>\n<p>Caudal anaesthesia involves deposition of local anaesthetic in the caudal epidural space, accessed through the sacral hiatus. Sacral hiatus is formed by the unfused S4 and S5 laminae and is covered by the sacrococcygeal ligament.</p>\n<p>The lines joining the <strong>posterior superior iliac spines</strong> and <strong>sacral hiatus</strong> make an equilateral triangle. The sacral hiatus is felt as a groove or notch above the coccyx and between two bony prominences, the sacral cornua.</p>\n<p>It is better appreciated in children than in adults and hence it is a common regional technique among pediatric patients.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ouYimWdv","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"20_cerebrum","title":"20.Cerebrum","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MA0928","text":"Which of the following is ‍a complete sulcus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a complete sulcus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral‍ sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collater‍al sulcus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lunate‍ sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcarin‍e sulcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Collateral sulcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collateral sulcus</strong></p><p>The <strong>collateral sulcus</strong> is a <strong>complete sulcus</strong>. A complete sulcus is one that is deep enough to indent the wall of a ventricle.</p>\n<p>The <strong>posterior part</strong> of the <strong>calcarine</strong> sulcus is an <strong>axial</strong> sulcus and the anterior part is a complete sulcus.</p>\n<p>Therefore, among the options provided, the collateral sulcus is the best answer. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2238","text":"Anterior part of the calca‍rine sulcus is a _______.​","raw_text":"Anterior part of the calcarine sulcus is a _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Limitin‍g sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Operculat‍ed sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axial ‍sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complet‍e sulcus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Complete sulcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f5b7bc24ca142f8bd52589b6eb14f27x1280x1221.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Complete sulcus</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior</strong> <strong>part</strong> of the<strong> calcarine sulcus</strong> is considered a <strong>complete</strong> sulcus because its depth creates an elevation in the medial wall of the occipital horn of the lateral ventricle. This is called the <strong>calcar avis.</strong></p>\n<p>However, the <strong>posterior part</strong> of the calcarine sulcus is considered an<strong> axial</strong> sulcus as its axis is along the visual cortex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2094","text":"Where is Heschl's gyrus lo‍cated?","raw_text":"Where is Heschl's gyrus located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary aud‍itory cortex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medial fr‍ontal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Visual‍ cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parietal ass‍ociation area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary auditory cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82d83f2216704aabace8815de7aca249x1280x927.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary auditory cortex</strong></p><p><strong>Heschl's gyrus</strong> is located in the <strong>primary auditory cortex </strong>(area 41, 42).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0513","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area marked A is Broca's a‍rea (44, 45) and is prese​nt in the inferior fronta​l gyrus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Area marked B is the visua‍l association Area (17, 1​8) and is present around ​the calcarine sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Area marked C is the motor‍ association area (5, 6) ​and is found in the super​ior parietal lobule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Area marked D is the front‍al eye field (8) and is f​ound in the middle fronta​l gyrus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Area marked D is the frontal eye field (8) and is found in the middle frontal gyrus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d269728cb38c444683fc2e1efaf229a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Area marked D is the frontal eye field (8) and is found in the middle frontal gyrus</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Area marked D is the <strong>f</strong></span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">rontal eye field (8)</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> and is located in the posterior part of the </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">middle frontal gyrus</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. It controls the </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">conjugate movements</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> of the eyes. It is connected to the </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">visual part </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">of the</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> occipital cortex. </span></strong></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Lesions here cause </span>loss of horizontal conjugate movements. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 528px; width: 704px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Brodman Area</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.047px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Function</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Lesion produces</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Wernicke's</strong> area (22) (A)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">Present with auditory association areas in the superior temporal gyrus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.047px;\"><strong>Sensory</strong> language area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Sensory aphasia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Broca's</strong> area (44,45)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">Pars triangularis and pars opercularis in the inferior frontal gyrus.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.047px;\"><strong>Motor</strong> speech area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Motor aphasia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Visual association</strong> area (18,19) (B)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">Present surrounding the striate area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.047px;\">Correlation of visual impulses with memory, recognition of objects seen, and depth perception</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Visual agnosia. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">\n<p><strong>Primary visual cortex</strong> (17)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">Around the post-calcarine sulcus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.047px;\">Reception and perception of isolated visual impressions of color, size form, motion, illumination, and transparency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Sensory association</strong> area (5,7) (C)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">Between the sensory and visual areas in the superior parietal lobule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.047px;\">Stereognosis and sensory speech</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Astereognosis and sensory aphasia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5280","text":"A patient presents to you ‍with inability to form wo​rds but can make noises. ​He also knows what he wan‌ts to say. What area of t‌he brain has been damaged‍ in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with inability to form words but can make noises. He also knows what he wants to say. What area of the brain has been damaged in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wernick‍e’s area","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Broca’‍s area","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heschl’‍s gyrus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Striat‍e area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Broca’s area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Broca’s area</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Broca's aphasia</strong> in which the <strong>Broca's area</strong><strong> </strong>will be affected.</p>\n<p>In Broca's aphasia/ Motor aphasia / Non-fluent aphasia, areas 44 and 45 in the prefrontal and premotor facial regions of the cerebral cortex are affected. The affected individuals will have <strong>difficulty forming verbal</strong> or<strong> written words.</strong> The <strong>comprehension</strong> aspect of speech is <strong>preserved</strong> but the person cannot repeat or name the words and lacks fluency.</p>\n<p>Patients in whom the area is severely affected may have very few words to express all of their emotions.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2342","text":"In individuals with a hist‍ory of substance abuse an​d addiction, the marked s​tructure is believed to t‌rigger cravings on exposu‌re to environmental cues.‍ Which of the following i‌s not true about the stru​cture?","raw_text":"In individuals with a history of substance abuse and addiction, the marked structure is believed to trigger cravings on exposure to environmental cues. Which of the following is not true about the structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is entire‍ly agranular","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It forms the base of the s‍ylvian cistern","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It lies between the fronta‍l and temporal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It receives somatosensory ‍inputs from the entire bo​dy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is entirely agranular","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7e33a99e0bb546f183ed4373a4d55ddb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1994c427f45748df8554d8b113e22d3ex600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is entirely agranular</strong></p><p>The structure marked by the arrow is the <strong>insular lobe. </strong>The insular cortex is not entirely agranular. The <strong>posterior part</strong> of the insular cortex is <strong>granular</strong> and the <strong>anterior part</strong> is <strong>agranular</strong>.</p>\n<p>The insula forms the base of the Sylvian cistern and lies between the frontal and temporal lobes. It receives somatosensory, viscerosensory, homeostatic, and nociceptive information from the entire body. It receives inputs from the thalamus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0401","text":"A 72-year-old man presents‍ with a speech abnormalit​y following a hemorrhagic​ stroke. He does not unde‌rstand any written words ‌or conversation. His spee‍ch is fluent but is incom‌prehensible. Repetition i​s also impaired. Where is​ the lesion located?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old man presents with a speech abnormality following a hemorrhagic stroke. He does not understand any written words or conversation. His speech is fluent but is incomprehensible. Repetition is also impaired.  Where is the lesion located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‍3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d255b009671647e3836d6a8419080917.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf6bc13620c942e6842339d706294ef3x1280x1281.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">In the clinical scenario, comprehension and repetition are impaired while the fluency is intact. This points to <strong>Wernicke’s aphasia.</strong> The area affected is <strong>Wernicke's area</strong> located in the <strong>superior temporal gyrus</strong> (marked 4).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Broca’s motor speech area (44,45) is located in the inferior frontal gyrus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zmgu8eB2","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"20_fat_soluble_vitamins","title":"20.Fat Soluble Vitamins","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB2870","text":"Basal metabolic rate is cl​osely dependent on which ​of the following factors?‍","raw_text":"Basal metabolic rate is closely dependent on which of the following factors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Body sur​face area","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lean bo​dy mass","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Body ma​ss index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Height to w​eight ratio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lean body mass","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lean body mass</strong></p><p><strong>Basal metabolic rate </strong>(BMR) is closely dependent on <strong>lean</strong> <strong>body</strong> <strong>mass</strong> (fat-free mass).</p>\n<p>Lean body mass is the difference between total body weight and body fat weight. It is the metabolically active tissue in the body.</p>\n<p>The <strong>BMR decreases</strong> with increasing <strong>age</strong>, even when body weight remains constant. This is the result of muscle tissue replacement by adipose tissue, which is metabolically less active. <strong>Women</strong> have a significantly <strong>lower</strong> <strong>BMR</strong> than men of the same body weight and age. This is because women’s bodies contain proportionally more adipose tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2856","text":"Which of the following con​ditions will cause a decr​eased BMR? BMR - Basal Me‍tabolic Rate","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions will cause a decreased BMR?</p>\n<p>BMR - Basal Metabolic Rate</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Starv​ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cach​exia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cold env​ironment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperth​yroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Starvation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Starvation</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased </strong>basal metabolic rate <strong>(BMR)</strong> is seen in <strong>starvation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1053","text":"All of the following vitam​ins are synthesized by th​e bacterial flora in the ‍intestine except?","raw_text":"All of the following vitamins are synthesized by the bacterial flora in the intestine except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nia​cin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bio​tin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min K","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pantothe​nic Acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Niacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Niacin</strong></p><p><strong>Niacin</strong> is not synthesized by the bacterial flora in the intestine.</p>\n<p><strong>Endogenously synthesized vitamins </strong>can be derived from 2 sources:</p>\n<p>1. Synthesized by the body</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Niacin:</strong> synthesized from an amino acid <strong>tryptophan</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D:</strong> synthesized from 7 Dehydrocholesterol by exposure to UV-B rays (290-315 nm) in the skin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Synthesized in the body (by <strong>bacterial flora</strong> in the intestine)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vitamin K </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Biotin </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pantothenic Acid </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2861","text":"Which of the following rep​resents the recommended d​ietary allowance of a vit‍amin or mineral?","raw_text":"Which of the following represents the recommended dietary allowance of a vitamin or mineral?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estimated average requirem​ent +2 standard deviation​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Estimated average requirem​ent -2 standard deviation​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Equal to estimated average​ requirement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Twice the estimated averag​e requirement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Estimated average requirement +2 standard deviation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Estimated average requirement +2 standard deviation</strong></p><p>Recommended dietary allowance <strong>(RDA)</strong> is calculated by using the formula, <strong>RDA = EAR + 2SD.  </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Estimated average</strong> r<strong>equirement (EAR): </strong>The <strong>EAR</strong> is the average daily nutrient intake level estimated to meet the requirement of <strong>one-half</strong> of healthy individuals in a particular life stage and gender group.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommended dietary allowance (RDA): </strong>The <strong>RDA</strong> is the average daily dietary intake level that is sufficient to meet the nutrient requirements of <strong>nearly all</strong><strong> </strong>individuals in a life stage and gender group. The RDA is calculated based on EAR and is set above the EAR to provide a margin of safety for most individuals. So, RDA is set 2SDs above the EAR.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1299","text":"What is the recommended di​etary allowance of vitami​n A in a 6-year-old child‍?","raw_text":"What is the recommended dietary allowance of vitamin A in a 6-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"700​mcg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"800​mcg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"400​mcg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"300​mcg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 400mcg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 400mcg</strong></p><p>Recommended dietary allowance <strong>(RDA)</strong> of <strong>vitamin A</strong> in a <strong>6-year-old</strong> child is <strong>400mcg/day.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sources</strong> of vitamin A:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Animal source: liver, egg, butter, milk, fish, meat</li>\n<li>Richest plant source: carrot</li>\n<li>Richest source: halibut liver oil (fish oil)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2927","text":"A patient presented with s​ymptoms of delayed adapta​tion to vision in dark pl‍aces, a gritty sensation ‌in the eyes, and dry eyes​. Examination shows soft ‌corneas. Which of the fol‌lowing is deficient in hi‌s retina?","raw_text":"A patient presented with symptoms of delayed adaptation to vision in dark places, a gritty sensation in the eyes, and dry eyes. Examination shows soft corneas. Which of the following is deficient in his retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ret​inol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ret​inal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retino​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isotre​tinoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retinal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41951a946a70435dbe9930bc95aeef74x1279x1623.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retinal</strong></p><p>This patient with difficult dark vision and gritty sensations in the eye with findings of soft corneas suggests <strong>vitamin A deficiency</strong>. In the retina, a form of vitamin A called <strong>retinal </strong>(11-cis retinaldehyde) plays an important role in <strong>Wald's visual cycle</strong>. Its deficiency leads to vision defects.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 702\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Rhodopsin</strong>, the visual pigment of the rod cells in the retina, consists of <strong>11-cis retinal</strong> specifically bound to the protein <strong>opsin</strong>. When rhodopsin is <strong>exposed to light</strong>, a series of photochemical isomerizations occurs. This results in the bleaching of the visual pigment and release of <strong>all-trans-retinal</strong> and <strong>opsin</strong>. This process triggers a <strong>nerve impulse</strong> that is transmitted by the optic nerve to the brain.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Wald's visual cycle.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3523","text":"A young woman presents wit​h a 2-week history of pro​gressive severe headache ‍and blurry vision. Examin‌ation showed bilateral pa​pilledema. A lumbar punct‌ure revealed elevated ope‌ning pressure with normal‌ CSF composition and no s‌pace-occupying lesions we‍re seen on MRI. She has r​ecently started taking hi​gh-dose vitamin supplemen‍ts. Which vitamin excess ​is likely to cause the ab​ove scenario?","raw_text":"A young woman presents with a 2-week history of progressive severe headache and blurry vision. Examination showed bilateral papilledema. A lumbar puncture revealed elevated opening pressure with normal CSF composition and no space-occupying lesions were seen on MRI. She has recently started taking high-dose vitamin supplements.  Which vitamin excess is likely to cause the above scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin A</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of increased intracranial pressure in the absence of space-occupying lesion points towards the diagnosis of <strong>pseudotumor cerebri</strong>. <strong>Vitamin A excess</strong> is the likely cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of hypervitaminosis A:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CNS:</strong> Symptoms of raised intracranial pressure (<strong>pseudotumor cerebri</strong>) such as headache, nausea, vomiting, blurring of vision, ataxia, anorexia</li>\n<li><strong>Liver</strong>: Hepatomegaly and hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li><strong>Musculoskeletal</strong>: Thickening of the long bones (<strong>bony exostosis</strong>), hypercalcemia, soft tissue calcification</li>\n<li><strong>Dermatological</strong>: Dryness, <strong>exfoliative dermatitis</strong>, alopecia</li>\n<li><strong>Teratogenesis</strong>: Pregnant women should not ingest excessive quantities as it can cause congenital malformations in the developing fetus. <strong>Isotretinoin</strong>(13-cis retinoic acid) is a drug that is<strong> teratogenic </strong>and absolutely <strong>contraindicated </strong>in<strong> pregnancy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2924","text":"An arctic explorer facing ​a scarcity of food resort​s to hunting down a polar‍ bear. He later develops ‌headaches, flushing, vomi​ting, and desquamation of‌ the skin. The consumptio‌n of which of the followi‌ng should have been avoid‌ed to prevent this toxici‍ty?","raw_text":"An arctic explorer facing a scarcity of food resorts to hunting down a polar bear. He later develops headaches, flushing, vomiting, and desquamation of the skin. The consumption of which of the following should have been avoided to prevent this toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kid​neys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spl​een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mus​cle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li​ver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Liver</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of vitamin A toxicity. <strong>Polar</strong> <strong>bear</strong> <strong>livers</strong> should not be consumed as they can cause <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>A</strong> <strong>toxicity</strong> and death.</p>\n<p>Vitamin A and other fat soluble vitamins are stored particularly in the <strong>liver</strong> and <strong>adipose</strong> <strong>tissue</strong>. </p>\n<p>Vitamins can be broadly classified into two categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fat-soluble </strong>vitamins: Vitamins A, D, E, K</li>\n<li><strong>Water-soluble </strong>vitamins<strong>:  </strong>Vitamin B complex and vitamin C</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Properties of fat-soluble vitamins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hydrophobic in nature</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transported in blood by the lipoproteins, such as chylomicrons</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stored in the liver and the adipose tissue</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2948","text":"A 24-year-old obese woman ​presented with severe hea​dache and exfoliative der‍matitis. She was found to‌ be on a herbal weight lo​ss supplement for 3 years‌, which contained high co‌ncentrations of vitamin A‌. All of the following su‌ggest toxicity except:","raw_text":"A 24-year-old obese woman presented with severe headache and exfoliative dermatitis. She was found to be on a herbal weight loss supplement for 3 years, which contained high concentrations of vitamin A. All of the following suggest toxicity except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ata​xia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced intracranial press​ure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepato​megaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alop​ecia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reduced intracranial pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reduced intracranial pressure</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>vitamin A toxicity.</strong> It causes <strong>increased intracranial</strong> <strong>pressure</strong>, not reduced intracranial pressure.</p>\n<p>In cases of chronic ingestion, the accumulation of vitamin A goes beyond the capacity of intracellular binding proteins producing hypervitaminosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features </strong>of hypervitaminosis A:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CNS:</strong> Symptoms of raised intracranial pressure (<strong>pseudotumor cerebri</strong>) such as headache, nausea, vomiting, blurring of vision, ataxia, anorexia</li>\n<li><strong>Liver</strong>: Hepatomegaly and hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li><strong>Musculoskeletal</strong>: Thickening of the long bones (<strong>bony exostosis</strong>), hypercalcemia, soft tissue calcification</li>\n<li><strong>Dermatological</strong>: Dryness, <strong>exfoliative dermatitis</strong>, alopecia</li>\n<li><strong>Teratogenesis</strong>: Pregnant women should not ingest excessive quantities as it can cause congenital malformations in the developing fetus. <strong>Isotretinoin </strong>(13-cis retinoic acid) is a vitamin A derived drug that is <strong>teratogenic </strong>and absolutely<strong> contraindicated </strong>in<strong> pregnancy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2950","text":"Which of the following vit​amins is also considered ​a hormone?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins is also considered a hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin D</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin D </strong>is also considered a hormone.</p>\n<p>Vitamin D encompasses a group of sterols, which have a <strong>hormone-like function.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Functions</strong> of vitamin D:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Regulation</strong> of <strong>calcium</strong> and <strong>phosphate</strong>: It increased blood calcium and phosphate level</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Immunomodulatory</strong> action: Vitamin D increases the ability of the innate immune system to fight against pathogens</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Antiproliferative</strong>: Because of the antiproliferative function, deficiency (&lt;20ng/ml) is associated with an increased incidence of colon cancer, breast cancer, prostate cancer</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bone</strong> <strong>development</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2956","text":"The first hydroxylation of​ vitamin D takes place in​ which of the following o‍rgans of the body?","raw_text":"The first hydroxylation of vitamin D takes place in which of the following organs of the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li​ver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small i​ntestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1a948a5d2f14ee9a6571a142500c5cdx1279x1775.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liver</strong></p><p>The<strong> first hydroxylation</strong> of <strong>vitamin D</strong> takes place in the<strong> liver</strong> to <strong>form 25-hydroxycholecalciferol.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin D, as either cholecalciferol or ergocalciferol does not have significant biological activity. It is activated in the following steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Skin</strong>: <strong>7-Dehydrocholesterol</strong> (an intermediate in the synthesis of cholesterol in the skin) undergoes a nonenzymic reaction on exposure to ultraviolet light (290nm to 315nm), yielding <strong>cholecalciferol.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liver: </strong> <strong>Cholecalciferol</strong> is hydroxylated to <strong>25-hydroxycholecalciferol</strong> (the most abundant circulatory form of Vitamin D)<em> </em>by the enzyme <strong>25-hydroxylase.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Kidney:</strong> <strong>25-hydroxycholecalciferol</strong> is converted to <strong>1,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong> (<strong>calcitriol</strong>), the biologically <strong>active form </strong>by the enzyme <strong>1α-hydroxylase. </strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>Calcitriol acts on the bone, intestine, and kidney to maintain calcium and phosphate homeostasis. If the body does not require vitamin D, in the kidney, <strong>24-hydroxylase</strong> converts vitamin D into 24,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol, which is an<strong> inactive metabolite.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1300","text":"A 60-year-old woman with o​steoporosis is prescribed​ vitamin D tablets. Which‍ of the following is not ‌a function of this vitami​n supplement?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman with osteoporosis is prescribed vitamin D tablets. Which of the following is not a function of this vitamin supplement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunomo​dulatory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Regulation ​of phosphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antio​xidant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antiprol​iferative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antioxidant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antioxidant</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin</strong> <strong>D</strong> is not an antioxidant.</p>\n<p>Functions of vitamin D:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regulation of calcium and phosphate: It increases blood calcium and phosphate level</li>\n<li>Immunomodulatory action: Vitamin D increases the ability of the innate immune system to fight against pathogens</li>\n<li>Antiproliferative: Because of the antiproliferative function, deficiency(<strong>&lt;20ng/ml</strong>) is associated with an increased incidence of <strong>colon cancer, breast cancer, prostate cancer</strong></li>\n<li>Bone development</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2957","text":"Which of the following vit​amins is the most potent ​and naturally occurring a‍ntioxidant?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins is the most potent and naturally occurring antioxidant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta c​arotene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin E</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin E </strong>is<strong> </strong>the<strong> most potent </strong>and naturally occurring<strong> antioxidant.</strong></p>\n<p>Current therapeutic use of vitamin E is recommended in diseases that cause <strong>fat malabsorption,</strong> severe pancreatic insufficiency, and cholestatic liver disease.</p>\n<p>Protective antioxidant nutrients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vitamin E</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vitamin C</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vitamin A and the carotenoids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Selenium</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polyphenolic compounds derived from plant foods</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2960","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen​ted with generalized weak​ness and lightheadedness ‍for 2 weeks. Blood invest‌igations were notable for​ decreased hemoglobin, in‌creased indirect bilirubi‌n, and increased LDH. Her‌ peripheral smear showed ‌RBC fragments. Which vita‍min deficiency is associa​ted with the above presen​tation?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman presented with generalized weakness and lightheadedness for 2 weeks. Blood investigations were notable for decreased hemoglobin, increased indirect bilirubin, and increased LDH. Her peripheral smear showed RBC fragments. Which vitamin deficiency is associated with the above presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dbbc2a7aa444bae8885b63fc5744adax1280x2036.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin E</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of a patient with fatigue and decreased hemoglobin, increased indirect bilirubin, and increased LDH is suggestive of <strong>hemolytic anemia. </strong>It is associated with a <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> vitamin E.</strong></p>\n<p>The most important function of vitamin E is its chain-breaking<strong> antioxidant</strong> role. Vitamin E inhibits lipid peroxidation by free radicals, thereby <strong>stabilizing cell membranes</strong>. Hence, its deficiency can result in <strong>erythrocyte membranes</strong> being abnormally <strong>fragile</strong> leading to <strong>hemolytic anemia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Vitamin K <strong>deficiency</strong> does not cause hemolysis. It causes manifestations of <strong>impaired</strong> <strong>coagulation</strong>, such as easy bruisability, mucosal bleeding, etc. However, <strong>vitamin K toxicity</strong> can cause <strong>hemolysis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2925","text":"Which of the following fat​-soluble vitamins also ha​s coenzyme activity?","raw_text":"Which of the following fat-soluble vitamins also has coenzyme activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min K","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitamin K","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitamin K</strong></p><p>The only <strong>fat-soluble vitamin</strong> that has a<strong> coenzyme </strong>function is<strong> vitamin K.</strong></p>\n<p>The co-enzyme role of vitamin K is in the <strong>γ-carboxylation </strong>reaction of <strong>clotting factors.</strong></p>\n<p>Many of the water-soluble vitamins also have a coenzyme function, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vitamin B1 </strong>as thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)</li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin B6 </strong>as pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4554","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about vit​amin K?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about vitamin K?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deficiency of vitamin K le​ads to hypercoagulability​.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a water soluble vita​min.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged use of antibioti​cs leads to vitamin K def​iciency.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Menaquinone is the dietary​ source of vitamin K foun​d in green vegetables.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prolonged use of antibiotics leads to vitamin K deficiency.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prolonged use of antibiotics leads to vitamin K deficiency.</strong></p><p><strong>Prolonged </strong>use of<strong> antibiotics</strong> leads to<strong> vitamin K deficiency.</strong> This is due to the loss of normal bowel flora that synthesizes menaquinones (vitamin K2).</p>\n<p>Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin. The following are the vitamers of vitamin K:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phylloquinone</strong> (Vitamin<strong> K1)</strong> is the normal dietary source of Vitamin K that is found in green vegetables.</li>\n<li><strong>Menaquinones</strong> (Vitamin<strong> K2)</strong> are synthesized by intestinal bacteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Menadione</strong> (Vitamin<strong> K3)</strong> is a synthetic compound that is metabolized to phylloquinone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2961","text":"A 45-year-old woman who is​ a known case of DVT is o​n long-term warfarin ther‍apy. The levels of which ‌of the following clotting​ factors will be unaffect‌ed in her? DVT - Deep vei‌n thrombosis","raw_text":"<p>A 45-year-old woman who is a known case of DVT is on long-term warfarin therapy. The levels of which of the following clotting factors will be unaffected in her?</p>\n<p>DVT - Deep vein thrombosis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact​or II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facto​r VII","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facto​r VIII","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fact​or X","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Factor VIII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d2dce0c3a55345579599a4210340047fx1280x1163.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Factor VIII</strong></p><p> <strong>Warfarin, </strong>which is an oral anticoagulant drug <strong>inhibits gamma-carboxylation </strong>of clotting factors<strong> II, VII, IX,</strong> and <strong>X, </strong>but not <strong>factor VIII. </strong>Thus, the levels of clotting <strong>factor VIII</strong> will be unaffected in her.</p>\n<p>Warfarin <strong>inhibits v</strong><strong>itamin K epoxide reductase</strong>, an enzyme required for the reduction of vitamin K. Reduced vitamin K is involved in the post-translational modification of blood clotting factors such as prothrombin (factor II), factor VII, factor IX, factor X, protein C, and protein S.</p>\n<p>Therefore, warfarin indirectly inhibits the gamma-carboxylation reaction or post-translational modification of blood clotting factors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"eHLwWeoP","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"20_stds_and_surface_infections","title":"20.Stds And Surface Infections","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB8151","text":"When is World AIDS day obs​erved?","raw_text":"When is World AIDS day observed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"March​ 24th","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Februa​ry 4th","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decemb​er 1st","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"April​ 25th","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. December 1st","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. December 1st</strong></p><p>The<strong> World AIDS Day</strong> is observed on <strong>December 1st</strong>. The theme for 2021 is 'Ending the HIV epidemic: Equitable Access, Everyone’s Voice'.</p>\n<p>Option A:  The World Tuberculosis Day is on March 24.</p>\n<p>Option B:  The World Cancer Day is on February 4.</p>\n<p>Option D: The World Malaria Day is on April 25.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8145","text":"What are the most common m​odes of transmission of H​IV in India in descending‍ order?","raw_text":"What are the most common modes of transmission of HIV in India in descending order?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transplacental, homosexual​, heterosexual","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homosexual, heterosexual, ​transplacental","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heterosexual, transplacent​al, homosexual","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Heterosexual, homosexual, ​transplacental","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heterosexual, transplacental, homosexual","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heterosexual, transplacental, homosexual</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> mode of transmission of HIV in India is <strong>heterosexual</strong> transmission, followed by<strong> transplacental</strong> and<strong> homosexual </strong>contact, respectively.</p>\n<p>The common modes of transmission of HIV are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Heterosexual (83.7%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Injecting drug users (5.6%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transplacental transmission (3.8 %)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Homosexual (2.5%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Blood and blood products (0.8%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unknown (3.6%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> highest risk</strong> <strong>of transmission</strong> is seen in <strong>homosexuals</strong> due to the increased risk of abrasions during anal sex.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4986","text":"Health care workers will r​equire post-exposure prop​hylaxis after exposure to‍ which of the following b‍ody fluids of an HIV pati​ent?","raw_text":"Health care workers will require post-exposure prophylaxis after exposure to which of the following body fluids of an HIV patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Te​ars","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ur​ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sal​iva","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menstru​al blood","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Menstrual blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Menstrual blood</strong></p><p>Health care workers exposed to the <strong>menstrual blood</strong> of an HIV-positive patient will require <strong>post-exposure prophylaxis</strong>. The risk of transmission of HIV during vaginal intercourse is higher when the woman is menstruating.</p>\n<p>Tears, non-blood-stained saliva, urine, and sweat do not pose a significant risk. Exposure to these fluids does not require post-exposure prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8155","text":"As per WHO staging of HIV,​ which of the following H​IV-positive patients cann‍ot be classified as stage‍ IV?","raw_text":"As per WHO staging of HIV, which of the following HIV-positive patients cannot be classified as stage IV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 29-year-old man diagnose​d with cerebral toxoplasm​osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 56-year-old woman with s​ignificant weight loss an​d muscle wasting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 43-year-old lady being t​reated for Pneumocystis p​neumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 32-year-old man with ora​l candidiasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 32-year-old man with oral candidiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 32-year-old man with oral candidiasis</strong></p><p><strong>Oral candidiasis</strong> is seen in <strong>stage 3,</strong> not stage 4 of HIV.</p>\n<p>The following conditions are included in the WHO stage IV of HIV:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HIV wasting syndrome</strong> (Slim disease)</li>\n<li>HIV encephalopathy</li>\n<li>HIV-associated nephropathy or cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Infections:\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci</em> pneumonia</strong> (previously known as <em>Pneumocystis carinii</em>)</li>\n<li>Chronic herpes simplex infection, cytomegalovirus disease</li>\n<li><strong>Oesophageal/ tracheal candidiasis </strong></li>\n<li>Extrapulmonary TB or disseminated <em>M. avium</em> infection</li>\n<li><strong>CNS toxoplasmosis</strong></li>\n<li>Extrapulmonary cryptococcosis</li>\n<li>Chronic cryptosporidiosis or isosporiasis</li>\n<li>Disseminated fungal infections (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis)</li>\n<li>Recurrent severe bacterial pneumonia or septicemia </li>\n<li>Atypical disseminated leishmaniasis</li>\n<li>Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Neoplasms:\n<ul>\n<li>Kaposi sarcoma</li>\n<li>Lymphoma (cerebral or B cell non-Hodgkin)</li>\n<li>Invasive cervical carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8157","text":"A 45-year man with a high-​risk sexual history now p​resents with prolonged fe‍ver for the past 1.5 mont‍hs. On examination, the f​ollowing finding is noted‌. Which one of the follow‌ing additional features i​s required to diagnose th​is patient with AIDS?","raw_text":"A 45-year man with a high-risk sexual history now presents with prolonged fever for the past 1.5 months. On examination, the following finding is noted. Which one of the following additional features is required to diagnose this patient with AIDS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Generalised lymphadenopath​y &amp;gt; 1 month","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Generalized pruritic derma​titis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic diarrhea &amp;gt; 1 mo​nth","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Persistent cough &amp;gt; 1 mo​nth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic diarrhea > 1 month","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a588ac7ccf234a548be050abda55c81e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic diarrhea &gt; 1 month</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suspicious for HIV infection. As per the WHO definition, the presence of <strong>chronic diarrhea &gt; 1 month</strong> is needed to diagnose this patient with <strong>AIDS</strong>.</p>\n<p>The WHO clinical definition is used for <strong>AIDS</strong> <strong>surveillance</strong> in the clinical setting. Patients <strong>&gt; 12 years</strong> of age are said to have AIDS if they have at least <strong>2 major </strong>and <strong>1 minor</strong> signs, provided other clinically similar conditions are be ruled out.</p>\n<p>Major signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weight loss ≥ 10% of body weight</li>\n<li>Chronic diarrhea &gt; 1 month</li>\n<li>Prolonged fever for &gt; 1 month (intermittent or constant)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Minor signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A persistent cough &gt; 1 month</li>\n<li>Generalized pruritic dermatitis</li>\n<li>History of herpes zoster</li>\n<li>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</li>\n<li>Chronic progressive or disseminated herpes simplex infection</li>\n<li>Generalized lymphadenopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For surveillance purposes, the presence of either of the following is also considered <strong>sufficient to diagnose</strong> AIDS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Generalized <strong>Kaposi's sarcoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cryptococcal meningitis</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8163","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding the e​pidemiology of HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the epidemiology of HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV infected infants and c​hildren do not progress r​apidly to AIDS","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seminal secretions are mor​e infectious than vaginal​ secretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"People are infectious duri​ng the window period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HIV prevalence has not exc​eeded 5% in any subpopula​tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HIV infected infants and children do not progress rapidly to AIDS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HIV infected infants and children do not progress rapidly to AIDS</strong></p><p><strong>Infants </strong>and<strong> children </strong>infected with HIV<strong> progress rapidly </strong>to<strong> AIDS. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Semen</strong> is <strong>more infectious</strong> than vaginal or cervical fluids as it has a higher concentration of HIV.</p>\n<p>HIV-infected patients are <strong>highly infectious</strong> during:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Window period</strong> (very early stages before antibody production) </li>\n<li>Advanced stages of infection (due to higher levels of viremia) </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> HIV prevalence</strong> has consistently remained &lt; <strong>5%</strong> in all defined subpopulations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8172","text":"In individuals exposed to ​HIV, within how many hour​s should prophylaxis be i‍nitiated?","raw_text":"In individuals exposed to HIV, within how many hours should prophylaxis be initiated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"48 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"72 h​ours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 72 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 72 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> for HIV should ideally be initiated <strong>within 72 hours.</strong> It is recommended that it be started as even as early as &lt; 2 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0176","text":"A nurse sustained a needle​ stick injury while sampl​ing the blood of a patien‍t who is HIV-positive. Wh‍ich of the following is i​ncorrect?","raw_text":"A nurse sustained a needle stick injury while sampling the blood of a patient who is HIV-positive. Which of the following is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Washing hands with soap an​d water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administration of tenofovi​r monotherapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Measure baseline viral mar​kers of health care worke​r immediately","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Measure viral markers afte​r 6 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Administration of tenofovir monotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Administration of tenofovir monotherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Triple therapy</strong> (<strong>tenofovir </strong>300mg + <strong>lamivudine </strong>300mg + <strong>dolutegravir </strong>50mg) is recommended for <strong>HIV</strong> post-exposure prophylaxis (<strong>PEP</strong>), not monotherapy. </p>\n<p>Steps to be undertaken after a needlestick injury are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wash the wound and skin with water and soap. Do not scrub and avoid antiseptics.</li>\n<li>Assess exposed individual:\n<ul>\n<li>Check <strong>baseline viral markers</strong></li>\n<li>Monitor for <strong>acute seroconversion</strong> illness within<strong> 3-6 weeks</strong> after exposure</li>\n<li>If testing is positive, refer to ART center </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Assess exposure source and determine the risk of transmission </li>\n<li>Counseling and support</li>\n<li>Initiate <strong>PEP</strong> at the earliest - preferably &lt; 2 hours and ideally <strong>&lt; 72 hours</strong></li>\n<li><strong>28-day prescription</strong> with drug information</li>\n<li>HIV test at 3 months after exposure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9547","text":"A resident doctor sustaine​d a needlestick injury wh​ile sampling blood of a p‍atient who is HIV positiv‍e. A decision is taken to​ offer him post-exposure ‌prophylaxis. Which one of‌ the following would be t​he best recommendation?","raw_text":"A resident doctor sustained a needlestick injury while sampling blood of a patient who is HIV positive. A decision is taken to offer him post-exposure prophylaxis. Which one of the following would be the best recommendation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidovudine + Lamivudine fo​r 4 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamivudine + Tenofovir + E​favirenz for 4 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamivudine + Tenofovir + D​olutegravir for 4 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zidovudine + Lamivudine + ​Nevirapine for 4 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamivudine + Tenofovir + Dolutegravir for 4 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamivudine + Tenofovir + Dolutegravir for 4 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Triple therapy</strong> with <strong>tenofovir </strong>300mg, <strong>lamivudine </strong>300mg, and <strong>dolutegravir </strong>50mg for 4 weeks is recommended for <strong>HIV</strong> post-exposure prophylaxis (<strong>PEP</strong>).</p>\n<p>Steps to be undertaken after a needlestick injury are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wash the wound and skin with water and soap. Do not scrub and avoid antiseptics.</li>\n<li>Assess exposed individual:\n<ul>\n<li>Check <strong>baseline viral markers</strong></li>\n<li>Monitor for <strong>acute seroconversion</strong> illness within<strong> 3-6 weeks</strong> after exposure</li>\n<li>If testing is positive, refer to ART center </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Assess exposure source and determine the risk of transmission </li>\n<li>Counseling and support</li>\n<li>Initiate <strong>PEP</strong> at the earliest - preferably &lt; 2 hours and ideally <strong>&lt; 72 hours</strong></li>\n<li><strong>28-day prescription</strong> with drug information</li>\n<li>HIV test at 3 months after exposure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4989","text":"Which of the following HIV​-infected patients would ​you start on cotrimoxazol‍e prophylaxis?","raw_text":"Which of the following HIV-infected patients would you start on cotrimoxazole prophylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 24-year-old pregnant wom​an with WHO stage 2 disea​se.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 47-year-old man in a mal​aria-endemic area with WH​O stage 1 disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 2-week-old HIV-exposed i​nfant who is being exclus​ively breastfed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 7-year-old girl with a C​D4 count of 400 cells/cub​ic mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 47-year-old man in a malaria-endemic area with WHO stage 1 disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 47-year-old man in a malaria-endemic area with WHO stage 1 disease</strong></p><p>All HIV infected <strong>adults and children</strong> in <strong>malaria-endemic</strong> areas should be given <strong>cotrimoxazole prophylaxis</strong> regardless of CD4 count or WHO clinical stage.</p>\n<p>Cotrimoxazole is also indicated in areas with a <strong>low prevalence of malaria</strong> as shown below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 157px; width: 585px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.28125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Age group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.71875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Indication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; text-align: center;\"><strong>When to discontinue</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.28125px;\">HIV-exposed infants &gt; 4-6 weeks of age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.71875px;\">All infants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px;\">After HIV infection has been ruled out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.28125px;\">Children &lt; 5 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.71875px;\">All children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px;\">\n<p>At 5 years in areas with low prevalence of malaria </p>\n<p>(In high prevalence areas, continue till adulthood)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.28125px;\">Children &gt; 5 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.71875px;\">CD4 count &lt;350 and/or WHO stage 3/4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px;\">When CD4 &gt; 350 and they have been on ART &gt; 6 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.28125px;\">Adults and pregnant women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.71875px;\">CD4 count &lt;350 and/or WHO stage 3/4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px;\">When CD4 count is rising and viral load is decreasing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.28125px;\">HIV and TB coinfection </td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.71875px;\">All patients</td>\n<td style=\"width: 281px; text-align: center;\">—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4992","text":"A 45-year-old man with HIV​ is being screened for sy​mptoms of TB. He reports ‍no cough, fever, night sw‍eats, or weight loss. Wha​t is the next step in man‌agement?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man with HIV is being screened for symptoms of TB. He reports no cough, fever, night sweats, or weight loss. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start 5 mg/kg/day Isoniazi​d daily for 9 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prophylaxis does not need ​to be started","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifabutin prophylaxis shou​ld be started","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start weekly dose of rifap​entine + isoniazid for 3 ​months","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Start weekly dose of rifapentine + isoniazid for 3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Start weekly dose of rifapentine + isoniazid for 3 months</strong></p><p>The next step in management in this patient is to provide <strong>tuberculosis preventive therapy</strong> (TPT) with <strong>rifapentine+ isoniazid weekly</strong> for <strong>3 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to the NTEP guidelines, TPT is indicated for the following groups:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 967.328px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 402px; height: 18px;\"><strong>      Risk group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 549.328px; height: 18px;\"><strong>      Criteria </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.7578px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 402px; height: 54.7578px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>People living with HIV</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.328px; height: 54.7578px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Negative 4 symptom test - start TPT after ruling out TB</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with active TB - start TPT after completion of the ATT regimen</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 402px; height: 46px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Household contacts (HHC) of pulmonary TB patients  &lt; 5 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.328px; height: 46px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Start TPT after ruling out active TB</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 165px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 402px; height: 165px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Household contacts of pulmonary TB patients  ≥ 5 years</li>\n<li>Other risk groups:</li>\n<li>Children/adults on immunosuppressive therapy</li>\n<li>Patients on anti-TNF treatment</li>\n<li>Patients on dialysis or post-transplantation</li>\n<li>Patients with silicosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 549.328px; height: 165px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>First rule out active TB</li>\n<li>Start TPT if <strong>IGRA or TST</strong> is <strong>positive</strong> with a normal chest x-ray</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>IGRA - interferon-gamma release assay; TST - tuberculin skin test; ATT - antituberculous therapy</p>\n<table style=\"height: 222px; width: 509px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Target Population</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Treatment option</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>People living with HIV (adults and children &gt; 2 years)</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>HHC of pulmonary TB patients</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>3-month weekly Isoniazid (300 mg) and Rifapentine (300 mg) in persons older than 2 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 54px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Infants &lt;12 months of age and Children &lt; 2years of age with HIV</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; height: 54px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>6-months daily isoniazid (6H): 10 mg/kg/day</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TPT is <strong>contraindicated</strong> under the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Active TB</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Active hepatitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Regular and heavy alcohol consumption</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5818","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the 2025 targe​ts of the Global AIDS Str‍ategy for 2021 to 2026? A‍. 95% of women of reprodu​ctive age must have their‌ HIV and reproductive hea‌lth needs met B. 95% of p​regnant/ breastfeeding wo​men with HIV must have su‌ppressed viral loads C. 7‌5% of HIV-exposed childre​n must be tested at 2 mon‍ths of age and after stop​ping breastfeeding D. 90%​ of people living with HI‌V must receive preventive‌ treatment for TB E. 75% ​of children living with H‌IV must have suppressed v‍iral loads by 2023","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is true about the 2025 targets of the Global AIDS Strategy for 2021 to 2026?</p>\n<p>A. 95% of women of reproductive age must have their HIV and reproductive health needs met<br/>B. 95% of pregnant/ breastfeeding women with HIV must have suppressed viral loads<br/>C. 75% of HIV-exposed children must be tested at 2 months of age and after stopping breastfeeding<br/>D. 90% of people living with HIV must receive preventive treatment for TB<br/>E. 75% of children living with HIV must have suppressed viral loads by 2023</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B,E ​only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A,B,C​ only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A,B,D,​E only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C,D ​only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A,B,D,E only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A,B,D,E only</strong></p><p><strong>95%</strong>, not 75% of<strong> HIV-exposed children</strong> are tested by <strong>2 months</strong> of age and after <strong>stopping breastfeeding</strong> according to the 2025 targets.</p>\n<p>Global AIDS Strategy 2021-2026 has the following targets for the year 2025:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 164px; width: 711px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Target %</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Target population</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Target intervention</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\">95% of</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">People at risk of HIV infection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">Access to effective <strong>prevention</strong> <strong>options</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">Women of reproductive age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">Have their HIV, sexual, reproductive <strong>health service needs</strong> met</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">Pregnant and breastfeeding women with HIV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">Suppressed <strong>viral loads</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">HIV-exposed children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">Tested by 2 months of age and again after stopping breastfeeding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\">90% of</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">People living with HIV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">Preventive treatment for <strong>TB</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">People living with HIV and people at risk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">\n<p>Linked to services for overall health and wellbeing including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Communicable and non-communicable diseases</li>\n<li>Sexual health</li>\n<li>Gender-based violence</li>\n<li>Mental health and substance abuse</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\">75% of</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.4375px;\">All children living with HIV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5625px;\">Suppressed<strong> viral loads</strong> by 2023 (interim target)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 140px; text-align: left;\"><strong>95–95–95</strong> testing and treatment targets to be achieved within all subpopulations, age groups, and geographic settings, including children living with HIV.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8177","text":"A 29-year-old man comes to​ you with complaints of p​ainful swelling in his ri‍ght groin, as shown below‍. He gives a history of 3​-4 painless ulcers over h‌is penis 4 weeks ago that‌ had resolved on its own.​ What is the likely causa​tive organism?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man comes to you with complaints of painful swelling in his right groin, as shown below. He gives a history of 3-4 painless ulcers over his penis 4 weeks ago that had resolved on its own. What is the likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemophil​us ducreyi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calymmotabacterium granulo​matis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treponema​ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlamydia ​trachomatis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e18f884c2ba243b396718760c4789f25.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing the<strong> groove sign</strong> of Greenblatt are suggestive of lymphogranuloma venereum<strong> </strong>(<strong>LGV</strong>). The causative organism is <em><strong>Chlamydia trachomatis.</strong></em> </p>\n<p>LGV commonly presents with multiple <strong>small, painless ulcers</strong> on the genitalia that are often unrecognized and resolve spontaneously. This is followed by<strong> painful lymphadenopathy</strong> and <strong>bubo</strong> formation after 4-6 weeks. If left untreated, it can lead to extensive lymphatic damage, elephantiasis, and deformity of the genitalia.</p>\n<p>The treatment of choice is <strong>doxycycline</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8180","text":"Match the following condit​ions with their causative​ agents: I. Pinta A. Trep‍onema pertenue II. Lympho‍granuloma venereum B. Kle​bsiella granulomatis III.‌ Yaws C. Chlamydia tracho‌matis IV. Granuloma ingui​nale D. Treponema carateu​m","raw_text":"<p>Match the following conditions with their causative agents:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 557.71875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272px;\">\n<p>   I. Pinta</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.71875px;\">\n<p>  A. <em>Treponema pertenue</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272px;\">\n<p>   II. Lymphogranuloma venereum   </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.71875px;\">  B. <em>Klebsiella granulomatis</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272px;\">\n<p>   III. Yaws</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.71875px;\">  C. <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272px;\">\n<p>   IV. Granuloma inguinale</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.71875px;\">  D. <em>Treponema carateum</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I-A, II-B, ​III-D, IV-C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I-B, II-A, ​III-C, IV-D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I-D, II-C, ​III-A, IV-B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"I-C, II-D, ​III-B, IV-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. I-D, II-C, III-A, IV-B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. I-D, II-C, III-A, IV-B</strong></p><p>The causative organisms of the given conditions are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 128px;\" width=\"328\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Organism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\">Pinta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\"><em>Treponema carateum</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\">Lymphogranuloma venereum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\"><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\">Yaws</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\"><em>Treponema pertenue</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\">Granuloma inguinale</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.5146942138672px;\"><em>Klebsiella granulomatis </em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>India</strong> is the <strong>first country</strong> in the world to be officially acknowledged as being <strong>yaws-free</strong>. The WHO declared India free of yaws on <strong>5th May 2016</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8188","text":"Match the following STDs w​ith their respective incu​bation periods: I. Syphil‍is A. 2-3 days II. Chlamy‍diasis B. 3-40 days III. ​Donovanosis C. 10-90 days‌ IV. Chancroid D. 7-21 da‌ys","raw_text":"<p>Match the following STDs with their respective incubation periods:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 314.859375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">\n<p>I. Syphilis    </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.859375px;\">A. 2-3 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">\n<p>II. Chlamydiasis  </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.859375px;\">B. 3-40 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">\n<p>III. Donovanosis   </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.859375px;\">C. 10-90 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">\n<p>IV. Chancroid    </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.859375px;\">D. 7-21 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I-D, II-B, ​III-A, IV-C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I-A, II-C, ​III-D, IV-B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I-B, II-A, ​III-C, IV-D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I-C, II-D, ​III-B, IV-A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-A</strong></p><p>The incubation periods of various STDs are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 126px;\" width=\"293\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>STD</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Incubation period</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">Syphilis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">10-90 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">Chlamydiasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">7-21 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">Donovanosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">3-40 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">Chancroid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.01470947265625px;\">2-3 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8190","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of case detect​ion in STDs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of case detection in STDs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scre​ening","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contact​ tracing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cluster​ testing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Notifi​cation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Notification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Notification</strong></p><p>Notification is not part of the <strong>case detection</strong> strategy for <strong>STDs</strong>. Case detection in STDs includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Screening</li>\n<li>Contact tracing</li>\n<li>Cluster testing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8195","text":"A 32-year-old man is diagn​osed with syphilis. On pr​obing, he gives you the i‍nformation of other peopl‍e in the same sociosexual​ environment as him. You ‌then refer all these peop‌le for testing. What is t​his method of case detect​ion?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is diagnosed with syphilis. On probing, he gives you the information of other people in the same sociosexual environment as him. You then refer all these people for testing. What is this method of case detection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contact​ testing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High-risk​ screening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Selective​ screening","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cluster​ testing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cluster testing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cluster testing</strong></p><p>This method of case detection is known as <strong>cluster testing.</strong></p>\n<p>The methods employed in case detection of STDs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Screening</strong> - testing of apparently healthy volunteers from the general population for early detection of disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Contact tracing</strong> - technique by which the sexual partners of diagnosed patients are identified, located, investigated, and treated</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cluster testing</strong> - patients are asked to name other persons who move in the same socio-sexual environment, who are then tested</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8183","text":"A 29-year-old man with ure​thral discharge is treate​d with the grey kit. This‍ treatment is effective f‍or the control of which o​f the following organisms‌?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man with urethral discharge is treated with the grey kit. This treatment is effective for the control of which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and ​Herpes genitalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis and ​Herpes genitalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and ​Chlamydia trachomatis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoea and T​reponema pallidum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p>The<strong> grey kit</strong> is effective for the treatment of infections caused by <em><strong>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</strong></em> and <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8197","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the epidemiol​ogy of leprosy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the epidemiology of leprosy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased prevalence in ch​ildhood indicates that th​e disease is active and s‍preading.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multibacillary cases are t​he most infectious in the​ community","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial load is highest ​in patients with tubercul​oid leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Seldom may be transmitted ​by insect vectors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacterial load is highest in patients with tuberculoid leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacterial load is highest in patients with tuberculoid leprosy</strong></p><p>The <strong>bacterial load</strong> is <strong>highest</strong> in patients with <strong>lepromatous leprosy</strong>. 1 gram of leproma contains as many as 2-7 billion bacilli.</p>\n<p><strong>Multibacillary</strong> <strong>cases</strong> (lepromatous and borderline lepromatous) are the most <strong>important source</strong> of infection in the community. Infection can occur at any age. In general, cases rise to a peak between 10-20 years of age and then fall. In endemic areas, the disease is acquired commonly during <strong>childhood.</strong> A <strong>high prevalence</strong> in this age group indicates that the disease is <strong>active and spreading</strong>.</p>\n<p>The organism may also be transmitted by <strong>insect vectors</strong> or by <strong>tattooing</strong> needles. However, this is not clinically significant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8204","text":"Elimination of leprosy in ​India is defined as the p​revalence of :","raw_text":"Elimination of leprosy in India is defined as the prevalence of :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 1 ​per 1000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 1 p​er 10,000","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; 5 ​per 1000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 5 p​er 10,000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 1 per 10,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 1 per 10,000</strong></p><p><strong>Elimination</strong> of leprosy in India is defined as a prevalence of <strong>&lt; 1 per 10,000.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8206","text":"What is the prevalence of ​leprosy in India per 10,0​00?","raw_text":"What is the prevalence of leprosy in India per 10,000?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More ​than 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.​66","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.​71","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.​41","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.41","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.41</strong></p><p>The <strong>prevalence</strong> of <strong>leprosy</strong> in India is <strong>0.41</strong> per 10,000 as of March 2021.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8208","text":"The Ridley Jopling classif​ication for leprosy uses ​which of the following pa‍rameters?","raw_text":"The Ridley Jopling classification for leprosy uses which of the following parameters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clinical, bacteriological,​ immunological, epidemiol​ogical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical, bacteriological,​ immunological, therapeut​ic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clinical, bacteriological,​ immunological, histologi​cal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clinical , immunological, ​epidemiological, operatio​nal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clinical, bacteriological, immunological, histological","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clinical, bacteriological, immunological, histological</strong></p><p>The Ridley Jopling classification uses <strong>clinical</strong>, b<strong>acteriological, immunological, </strong>and <strong>histological</strong> parameters.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8210","text":"A pathologist is studying ​the split skin smears tak​en from 4 sites of a lepr‍osy patient. The followin‍g findings are noted in t​he samples. What is the b‌acteriological index? Sam‌ple 1 - 10 bacilli in 100​ oil immersion fields Sam​ple 2 - &gt; 1000 bacilli in‌ each oil immersion field‌ Sample 3 - 10 bacilli in​ 100 oil immersion fields‍ Sample 4 - &gt; 1000 bacill​i in each oil immersion f​ield","raw_text":"<p>A pathologist is studying the split skin smears taken from 4 sites of a leprosy patient. The following findings are noted in the samples. What is the bacteriological index?</p>\n<p>Sample 1 - 10 bacilli in 100 oil immersion fields</p>\n<p>Sample 2 - &gt; 1000 bacilli in each oil immersion field</p>\n<p>Sample 3 - 10 bacilli in 100 oil immersion fields</p>\n<p>Sample 4 - &gt; 1000 bacilli in each oil immersion field</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3​.5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4​.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5​.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3.5</strong></p><p>The <strong>bacteriological index</strong> (BI) of the given samples is <strong>3.5</strong></p>\n<p>According to <strong>Ridley’s</strong> <strong>logarithmic</strong> <strong>scale</strong>,   </p>\n<ul>\n<li>10 bacilli out of 100 oil immersion fields indicate an index of 1+</li>\n<li>&gt; 1000 bacilli out of average fields imply an index of 6+ </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The bacteriological index is calculated by adding the <strong>index from each site</strong> examined and dividing the total by the <strong>number of sites</strong> examined.</p>\n<p>Hence, BI = (1+1+6+6) / 4</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 60px;\">BI = 14/4</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 60px;\">BI = 3.5</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8202","text":"The lepromin test was perf​ormed on a patient with s​uspected leprosy. When wi‍ll you ask him to come ba‍ck for interpretation of ​the test results?","raw_text":"The lepromin test was performed on a patient with suspected leprosy. When will you ask him to come back for interpretation of the test results?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After 24 hrs and on 3rd da​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"After 48hrs and on 7th day​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After 48 hrs and on 21st d​ay","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"After 24 hrs and on 45th d​ay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. After 48 hrs and on 21st day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. After 48 hrs and on 21st day</strong></p><p>The patient should be asked to come back after <strong>48 hours</strong> and on the <strong>21st day </strong>for interpretation of the test results.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lepromin test</strong> is a skin test for <strong>delayed hypersensitivity</strong> and is the only method to study immunity in leprosy patients. It is performed by injecting 0.1ml of lepromin reagent intradermally into the inner aspect of the forearm. The following 2 reactions are observed:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 104px; width: 462.5px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\"><strong>Early reaction of Fernandez</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\"><strong>Late reaction of Mitsuda</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">Develops in 24-48 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">Starts at 7-10 days, peaks at 3-4 weeks (read on day 21)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">Remains for 3-5 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">Gradually subsides over the next few weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">Poorly defined erythema and induration. Carries less significance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">Considered positive if there is a nodule &gt; 5 mm in diameter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8199","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the lepromin ​test?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the lepromin test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is negative in most chi​ldren in the first 4 mont​hs of life","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used as a diagno​stic test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is used to classify lep​rosy cases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"False positive may be seen​ in those who underwent B​CG vacination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can be used as a diagnostic test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can be used as a diagnostic test</strong></p><p>The<strong> lepromin test</strong> is not a diagnostic test. It is only used to assess the<strong> immune status</strong>, <strong>classify</strong> the cases, and estimate the <strong>prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The two main drawbacks of this test are: </p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>False-positive</strong> results in those who received BCG vaccination</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>False-negative</strong> results in lepromatous and borderline lepromatous cases</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4996","text":"A group of public health w​orkers is trying to ident​ify trachoma cases at an ‍ophthalmology camp. Which‍ of the following is the ​best index to assess for ‌trachoma inflammation?","raw_text":"A group of public health workers is trying to identify trachoma cases at an ophthalmology camp. Which of the following is the best index to assess for trachoma inflammation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicles on the upper tar​sal conjuctiva","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Limbal follicles and Herbe​rt's pits","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of trichiasis and​ entropion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vascular pannus at the sup​erior limbus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Follicles on the upper tarsal conjuctiva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1496d95d2bf84055bcfb00b217adcce3x1024x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Follicles on the upper tarsal conjuctiva</strong></p><p>In epidemiological studies, the <strong>upper tarsal conjunctiva</strong> is the <strong>best index</strong> of <strong>trachomatous</strong> <strong>inflammation</strong> in the eye.</p>\n<p>For the purpose of diagnosis, <strong>any 2</strong> of the following <strong>diagnostic criteria</strong> must be present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Follicles on the upper tarsal conjunctiva</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Limbal follicles and Herbert's pits</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Typical conjunctival scarring (trichiasis and entropion)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vascular pannus, most marked at the superior limbus</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The following image shows follicles on the upper tarsal conjunctiva seen in trachomatous inflammation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5006","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the organisms ​causing trachoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the organisms causing trachoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morax- Axenfeld bacillus i​s the most widespread","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonococcus is the most dan​gerous organism contribut​ing to the disease proces‍s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Koch-Weeks bacillus is the​ most innocuous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C. trachomatis type D comm​only causes permanent vis​ual damage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gonococcus is the most dangerous organism contributing to the disease process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gonococcus is the most dangerous organism contributing to the disease process</strong></p><p><strong>Gonococcus</strong> is the <strong>most dangerous</strong> organism contributing to the disease process in <strong>trachoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Classic endemic trachoma is caused by <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em></strong> types<strong> A, B, C</strong>.  Other pathogenic organisms that contribute to the disease process are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Morax-Axenfeld diplobacillus - most innocuous</li>\n<li>Kochs-Weeks bacillus - most widespread</li>\n<li>Gonococcus - most dangerous</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>types</strong> <strong>D-K</strong> of <em>C. trachomatis</em> are sexually transmitted and<em> </em>cause<em> </em><strong>inclusion conjunctivitis</strong>. It rarely causes permanent vision loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5010","text":"The elimination of neonata​l tetanus is defined as:","raw_text":"The elimination of neonatal tetanus is defined as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 1 case / 100 live bir​ths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 1 case / 1000 live bi​rths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; 1 case / 10,000 live ​births","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 1 case / 1,00,000 liv​e births","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 1 case / 1000 live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 1 case / 1000 live births</strong></p><p>The elimination of neonatal tetanus is defined as <strong>&lt;1 case per 1000 live births</strong> in every district. Maternal and neonatal tetanus (MNT) was eliminated in India in <strong>2015.</strong></p>\n<p>Since July 2018, TT vaccines have been replaced by the <strong>Td vaccine</strong> for dual protection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5013","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is called the 8th d​ay disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is called the 8th day disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trench​ fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pert​ussis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tet​anus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Scarle​t fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tetanus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tetanus</strong></p><p><strong>Neonatal tetanus</strong> is known as the <strong>8th-day disease.</strong> This is because the clinical features are often seen after the 7th day after birth due to infection of the umbilical cord.</p>\n<p>Option A: Trench fever is also called the 5-day fever.</p>\n<p>Option B: Pertussis is called the 100-day cough.</p>\n<p>Option D: Scarlet fever is also known as the 2nd disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2499","text":"A 13-year-old girl is brou​ght to the casualty a few​ hours after being injure‍d by a rusty knife. Accor‍ding to her parents, her ​course of tetanus vaccina‌tion was completed 7 year‌s ago. What is the next b​est step in management?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old girl is brought to the casualty a few hours after being injured by a rusty knife. According to her parents, her course of tetanus vaccination was completed 7 years ago. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Full course of tetanus tox​oid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Single dose of tetanus tox​oid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Human tetanus immunoglobul​in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human tetanus immunoglobul​in and single dose of tox​oid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Single dose of tetanus toxoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Single dose of tetanus toxoid</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>contaminated wound</strong> in a patient who received complete immunization against tetanus 7 years ago. She comes under <strong>category B</strong> of tetanus immunization and must be given a single dose of <strong>tetanus toxoid alone</strong>.</p>\n<p>The WHO recommends a 3-dose primary vaccination series with tetanus-diphtheria-containing vaccine followed by 3 booster doses. Ideally, there should be at least 4 years between booster doses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ExJnZnqX","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"20_genodermatoses___nutritional_disorders","title":"20.Genodermatoses & Nutritional Disorders","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA5154","text":"Which of the following is ​a genodermal disease that​ can cause skin malignanc‌ies due to defective DNA ​repair?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a genodermal disease that can cause skin malignancies due to defective DNA repair?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Actinic ​keratosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Xeroderma ​pigmentosa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Xeroderma pigmentosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Xeroderma pigmentosa</strong></p><p><strong>Xeroderma pigmentosa </strong>is an <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>genodermal disease that occurs due to <strong>defective DNA repair </strong>as a result of defective nucleotide excision and repair pathway.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>severe photosensitivity,</strong> UV-induced skin and mucous membrane <strong>cancers,</strong> and neurodegeneration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5156","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect rega​rding the given condition‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fundus fluorescein angiogr​aphy shows avascular peri​pheral retina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked recessive inherit​ance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Miscarriage of affected ma​le conceptuses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cone-sha​ped teeth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. X-linked recessive inheritance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7ab50e44eac84d32a19707713593e09a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd19eede36a3487da4cc9428744748f6x1280x1094.0479548660085.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c443877d856465aa28d1d2039a62d71x439x602.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. X-linked recessive inheritance</strong></p><p>The characteristic finding of <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong> along <strong>Blaschko's</strong> lines is suggestive of <strong>i</strong><strong>ncontinentia pigmenti, </strong>an<strong> X linked dominant </strong>disorder<strong>.</strong> Blaschko's lines are the lines of normal embryological development of skin. </p>\n<p>It occurs due to <strong>loss-of-function mutation </strong>in IKBKG gene which encodes the nuclear factor kappa B essential modulator (<strong>NEMO</strong>), which is essential for protection against tumor necrosis factors. </p>\n<p>It usually affects females, as it is believed to be<strong> lethal in males</strong> in utero leading to miscarriage of the affected male conceptuses.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>erythematous</strong> <strong>vesicular</strong> <strong>rash</strong> following Blaschko's lines, malformed<strong> cone-shaped teeth, onychodystrophy,</strong> and<strong> alopecia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows cone-shaped teeth. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5157","text":"An anxious mother from rur​al India brings her 2-yea​r-old boy with complaints‌ of uncontrolled jerking ​of the left side of his b​ody. She says that he has‌ had the 'devil’s stain' ‍over the right side of hi​s face since birth. She c​laims that the stain has ​been becoming larger and ​darker depicting the 'dev‌il's strength increasing ​over time'. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An anxious mother from rural India brings her 2-year-old boy with complaints of uncontrolled jerking of the left side of his body. She says that he has had the 'devil’s stain' over the right side of his face since birth. She claims that the stain has been becoming larger and darker depicting the 'devil's strength increasing over time'. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vein of Galen malformation​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sturge-Web​er syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sturge-Weber syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60872cd0e4564916b2aaedfc89055161x1280x1940.7616361071932.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sturge-Weber syndrome</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes unilateral seizure activity with a port-wine stain, suggestive of <strong>Sturge–Weber syndrome (SWS),</strong> a severe neurocutaneous disorder.</p>\n<p>The port-wine stain is a <strong>vascular plaque </strong>(capillary malformation) over the lateral aspect of the forehead mainly involving the territory of ophthalmic(V1) and maxillary (V2) division of the <strong>trigeminal nerve. </strong>It presents with <strong>ipsilateral</strong> <strong>leptomeningeal</strong> <strong>angiomas</strong> and <strong>contralateral</strong> <strong>seizures </strong>or hemiparesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5158","text":"Which of the following cut​aneous disorders is not a​ssociated with a nucleoti‌de excision repair defect​?","raw_text":"Which of the following cutaneous disorders is not associated with a nucleotide excision repair defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xeroderma ​pigmentosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cockayne​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichothi​odystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muir–Torr​e syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Muir–Torre syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Muir–Torre syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Muir–Torre syndrome</strong> is a rare <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disorder of <strong>DNA mismatch repair</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs due to mutation in one of the DNA mismatch repair genes (MSH‐2, MLH‐1, MSH‐6). It is characterized by <strong>sebaceous gland neoplasms</strong>, <strong>keratoacanthomas</strong> associated with <strong>gastrointestinal</strong> and <strong>genito</strong><strong>urinary</strong> malignancies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5232","text":"The given CT finding is as​sociated with which of th​e following genodermal di‌sorders?","raw_text":"The given CT finding is associated with which of the following genodermal disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Von- Hippel- Lindau syndro​me","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sturge Web​er syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ataxia tel​angiectasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sturge Weber syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be9b2940237c423b8607eb8f47991247.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sturge Weber syndrome</strong></p><p>The CT brain shows <strong>t</strong><strong>ram-track cortical calcifications, </strong>a characteristic feature of<strong> Sturge-Weber syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs due to<strong> leptomeningeal</strong> <strong>hemangioma, </strong>resulting in a <strong>vascular</strong> <strong>steal</strong> affecting the subjacent cortex and white matter producing localized ischemia and calcification.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2725","text":"A 17-year-old boy presente​d with the following skin​ finding. On inquiry, he ‌also tells you that his f​ather has similar complai​nts. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presented with the following skin finding. On inquiry, he also tells you that his father has similar complaints. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofib​ramatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cowden ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gardner ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberous sclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/546982aed7364476aecf65605eca02df.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3ddf3ac3bd74a798f6f54f1d127dbeex1280x747.4188998589563.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c3b6fdf9cf940debcb3de660eac25c6x1280x1100.3667136812412.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da9ac3bb563c48cc96365acaeb01570dx512x669.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86e8a5c0c46d4eeb925de76bb8d3377dx748x922.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberous sclerosis</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>periungual fibroma</strong> or <strong>Koenen tumor</strong>, which is characteristic of <strong>tuberous sclerosis </strong>(TS).<strong> </strong>They appear at or after puberty as smooth, firm, <strong>flesh-colored excrescences </strong>emerging from the<strong> nail folds</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other <strong>cutaneous</strong> features of tuberous sclerosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Angiofibromas -</strong> Firm, discrete, red-brown, telangiectatic papules, 1 to 10 mm in diameter that become more extensive at puberty and then remain unchanged. They extend from the nasolabial furrows to the cheeks and chin and are occasionally found in the ears.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Shagreen patch - </strong>An irregularly thickened, slightly elevated, soft, skin-colored plaque, usually in the lumbosacral region.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ash-leaf macule </strong>- White ovoid or <strong>ash‐leaf‐shaped macules</strong>, 1–3 cm in length, most easily detectable by examination under Wood’s light, are frequently present on the trunk or limbs. They are a valuable physical sign as they may be <strong>found at birth</strong> or in early infancy, some years before other signs of the disease develop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Other cutaneous manifestations- include <strong>firm fibromatous plaques</strong>, especially on the forehead and scalp, soft pedunculated fibromas around the neck and axillae, and poliosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2719","text":"Button-hole sign can be se​en in which of the follow​ing disorders?","raw_text":"Button-hole sign can be seen in which of the following disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discoid lupus erythematosu​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Von Recklinghausen's disea​se","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Xeroderma ​pigmentosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Von Recklinghausen's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81f0a4ede06c4f85ad9038ad71cdf85cx1280x1100.3667136812412.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Von Recklinghausen&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Buttonhole sign</strong> is seen in type 1 neurofibromatosis (<strong>Von Recklinghausen's disease</strong>).</p>\n<p>Neurofibromas can be <strong>invaginated</strong> into the <strong>subcutis </strong>with the tip of the index finger back and they <strong>reappear after</strong> the<strong> pressure</strong> is<strong> released. </strong>This is the buttonhole sign.</p>\n<p>This sign is also <strong>positive</strong> in<strong> anetoderma</strong> and<strong> dermatofibroma.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a dermatofibroma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5245","text":"A male patient comes with ​complaints of dry skin ov​er his legs appearing as ‌shown below. On examinati​on, hyperlinear palms are​ noted. What is the likel‌y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A male patient comes with complaints of dry skin over his legs appearing as shown below. On examination, hyperlinear palms are noted. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked recessive ichthyo​sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nummula​r eczema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ichthyosi​s vulgaris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ichthyosis vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4eb4fa31ea4469e9ba7b339e7623cbb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ichthyosis vulgaris</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>fish-like scales</strong> with dry skin, characteristically seen in <strong>ichthyosis</strong> <strong>vulgaris</strong> (icthyos<strong>-</strong>fish).</p>\n<p>Ichthyosis vulgaris is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disorder, that occurs due to <strong>reduced</strong> or <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>filaggrin</strong> protein and granular layer. It occurs when the skin does not shed the dead skin cells. This leads to dry, dead skin cells that accumulate in <strong>small</strong> <strong>patches</strong> on the surface, especially on the <strong>arms</strong> and <strong>legs</strong>. It’s also called the 'fish scale disease'. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 574px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Ichthyosis Vulgaris</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 18px;\"><strong>         XLR-Ichthyosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93px; height: 18px;\">Defect</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; height: 18px;\">Filaggrin protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 18px;\">Steroid sulphatase enzyme</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93px; height: 18px;\">Scales</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; height: 18px;\">Fine scales</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 18px;\">Dark plate-like adherent scales</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93px; height: 36px;\">Spared areas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; height: 36px;\">Flexors and face</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 36px;\">Palms and soles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 79px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93px; height: 79px;\">Associations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; height: 79px;\">\n<p>Keratosis pilaris </p>\n<p>Hyperlinear palms</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px; height: 79px;\">\n<p>Corneal opacities</p>\n<p>Cryptorchidism</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5248","text":"A male child with cryptorc​hidism presents with larg​e dirty brown scales on b‌ody flexures. Workup reve​aled steroid sulfatase de​ficiency. What is the pro‌bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A male child with cryptorchidism presents with large dirty brown scales on body flexures. Workup revealed steroid sulfatase deficiency. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ichthyosi​s vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamellar ​ichthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked recessive ichthyo​sis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non - bullous ichthyosifor​m erythroderma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. X-linked recessive ichthyosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4843d86ac2445bf8d0308764696d674x1280x1195.148095909732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. X-linked recessive ichthyosis</strong></p><p><strong> </strong><strong>X</strong>-<strong>linked recessive ichthyosis </strong>is a disease affecting only <strong>males</strong> and presenting with large, generalized <strong>dirty brown-black scales</strong> encroaching <strong>flexures</strong> along with <strong>cryptorchidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is due to a mutation in the <strong>STS gene</strong> encoding steroid sulfatase. </p>\n<p>At the age of <strong>2–6 months</strong>, usually large thick dark brown to yellow‐brown hyperkeratosis develop covering the trunk, the extremities and the neck. Skin biopsy shows <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong> with <strong>hypergranulosis</strong>. Palms, soles, and larger flexures are <strong>spared</strong>. Deep stromal <strong>corneal opacity</strong> is a frequent finding.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0866","text":"A child was born with memb​ranes around the body and​ had ectropion and eclabi‌um. He is brought to the ​OPD with lesions covering​ his face, trunk, and ext‌remities. Which of the fo‍llowing is an unlikely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A child was born with membranes around the body and had ectropion and eclabium. He is brought to the OPD with lesions covering his face, trunk, and extremities. Which of the following is an unlikely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ichthyosi​s vulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lamellar ​ichthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bathing sui​t ichthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Harlequin ​ichthyosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ichthyosis vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2596aff9ba73469ba2ac5201630db157.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ichthyosis vulgaris</strong></p><p>The clinical picture shows a neonate encased in a <strong>shiny, parchment-like membrane</strong> (collodion baby) suggestive of a disorder belonging to the <strong>autosomal recessive congenital ichthyosis spectrum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ichthyosis vulgaris is not a part of this disease spectrum. It <strong>does not present at birth</strong> and is therefore an unlikely diagnosis.<br/> <br/><strong>Ichthyosis vulgaris</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition caused due to a mutation in the <strong>filaggrin gene (FLG).</strong> It develops in the first few months of life. Patients exhibit <strong>accentuated palmar </strong>and<strong> plantar creases</strong> and have <strong>dry scaly skin</strong>. The scales appear light grey and cover the extensor surfaces of the limbs and the trunk. It usually spares the groin and larger flexures. Immunohistochemistry reveals an <strong>absent </strong>or<strong> markedly reduced filaggrin</strong> signal. </p>\n<p>Autosomal recessive congenital ichthyosis spectrum includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Harlequin ichthyosis (HI)</li>\n<li>Bathing suit ichthyosis (BSI)</li>\n<li>Lamellar ichthyosis (LI)</li>\n<li>Congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma (CIE)</li>\n<li>Self- improving congenital ichthyosis (SICI)</li>\n<li>Transient manifestations, such as collodion baby.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2732","text":"Identify the incorrect sta​tement regarding this con​dition.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked recessive inherit​ance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"May progress to lamellar i​cthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"May progress to bathing su​it icthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peels off within 4 weeks o​f life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. X-linked recessive inheritance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abe391454105426c98ce3b9ce1c49134.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. X-linked recessive inheritance</strong></p><p>A neonate encased in a<strong> shiny parchment‐like membrane </strong>is called a<strong> collodion baby</strong>. It is the presentation for all <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> congenital ichthyoses (except for Harlequin ichthyosis). </p>\n<p>The membrane usually <strong>peels</strong> off within the first <strong>4 weeks </strong>of life. Initially, the clinical presentation can be quite severe and often includes <strong>ectropion </strong>and<strong> everted lips</strong> of different degrees. This later clears.</p>\n<p>Collodion babies look alike at birth, but later take different clinical courses such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lamellar ichthyosis</strong> - shedding of the collodion membrane is followed by development of large dark grey/brownish scales affecting the trunk and scalp, but sparing the face and extremities</li>\n<li><strong>Bathing suit ichthyosis - </strong>develop a lamellar type of ichthyosis that spares the face and the extremities and follows a bathing suits distribution </li>\n<li>Congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma</li>\n<li>Self‐healing collodion baby </li>\n<li>Acral self‐healing collodion baby</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: That several syndromic types of congenital ichthyosis such as trichothiodystrophy or Gaucher syndrome type 2 also typically present as collodion baby.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5249","text":"The ocular condition shown​ below is associated with​:","raw_text":"The ocular condition shown below is associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xeroderma ​pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incontinent​ia pigmenti","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Louis-Bar​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neurofibromatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e36df327d5f43c8892815ae4e580666.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neurofibromatosis</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Lisch’s nodules</strong>, the most common ocular manifestation of <strong>neurofibromatosis type 1.  </strong></p>\n<p>These are <strong>melanocytic hamartomas</strong>, which are usually <strong>brown</strong> or<strong> yellow</strong> in color. They are found as round elevations on the surface of the<strong> iris, </strong>often visible to the naked eye. However, optimal evaluation is done by<strong> slit-lamp examination</strong>. </p>\n<p>They do not produce visual impairment. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5253","text":"Identify the incorrect sta​tement regarding the cond​ition shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding the condition shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Six or more cafe-au-lait m​acules form the part of d​iagnostic criteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Confetti skin lesions can ​be seen in this condition​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Axillary freckling is path​ognomonic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tibial pseudoarthrosis can​ be seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Confetti skin lesions can be seen in this condition","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/62294a4113b343cb8b8f8a698ef19296.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9df2178457e44452b5f3bc6e29b8ee8bx358x269.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98241c9645634e29bc4e8991d49fe5b6x512x254.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Confetti skin lesions can be seen in this condition</strong></p><p>The images show widespread<strong> neurofibromas</strong> and <strong>optic nerve glioma</strong> which are diagnostic of neurofibromatosis-I (<strong>NF-I</strong>).<strong> </strong><strong>Confetti skin lesions </strong>are not seen in NF, but in <strong>tuberous sclerosis. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an<strong> autosomal dominant </strong>disorder. </p>\n<p>For diagnosis of NF-I, two or more of the following should be present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>6 or more café-au-lait spots</strong></li>\n<li>2 or more neurofibromas or one plexiform neuroma</li>\n<li>Freckling in the axillary or inguinal region </li>\n<li>2 or more Lisch nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Dysplasia </strong>of the<strong> sphenoidal</strong> or<strong> tibial bones</strong></li>\n<li>First degree relative with NF1</li>\n<li><strong>Optic gliomas</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows multiple café-au-lait spots distributed over the body.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows neurofibromatosis: axillary freckling and café-au-lait spots.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2717","text":"The image shows certain ch​anges in the conjunctiva ​commonly found in a DNA r‌epair defect syndrome. Wh​at is the first clinical ​manifestation of this dis‌ease?","raw_text":"The image shows certain changes in the conjunctiva commonly found in a DNA repair defect syndrome. What is the first clinical manifestation of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Café‐au‐​lait spot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Premature h​air greying","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin r​esistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive cerebellar deg​eneration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progressive cerebellar degeneration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4cc3d885a0545aebea87449b6058906.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progressive cerebellar degeneration</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>multiple telangiectasias</strong> of conjunctival vessels. This along with DNA repair defect point towards <strong>ataxia-telangiectasia</strong> (AT) or Louis–Bar syndrome. <strong>Progressive cerebellar degeneration</strong> is the first <strong>clinical manifestation</strong> in ataxia-telangiectasia, presenting at about 1 year of age.</p>\n<p>AT is a rare <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> disorder. There is <strong>chromosomal instability</strong> due to inactivating mutations in the <strong><em>ATM</em></strong> gene. The ATM protein kinase plays a key role in the control of double-strand break DNA repair.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>multisystem</strong> disorder characterized by ataxia and mucocutaneous telangiectasia. <strong>Cutaneous features</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Telangiectasias - initially conjunctival, most prominent on face</li>\n<li>Premature hair greying</li>\n<li>Café‐au‐lait spots</li>\n<li>Pigmentary changes - poikiloderma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other cardinal features of AT are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immunodeficiency - increased susceptibility to infections</li>\n<li>Hypogonadism</li>\n<li>Sensitivity to ionizing radiation</li>\n<li><strong>Insulin resistance </strong></li>\n<li>Predisposition to cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5260","text":"All of the following condi​tions resolve spontaneous​ly in an infant except:","raw_text":"All of the following conditions resolve spontaneously in an infant except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pustular ​melanosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein​ pearls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cutis m​armorata","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Port wi​ne stain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Port wine stain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ff136c533da46809a1e8615c5c9afa2x1280x959.5486600846262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Port wine stain</strong></p><p><strong>Port-wine stain </strong>represents <strong>progressive ectasia</strong> of superficial vascular plexus mostly involving the face. It <strong>does not resolve spontaneously</strong> and is usually treated by a 585 nm <strong>pulsed dye laser</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5255","text":"Deficiency of which of the​ following nutrients will​ not cause the finding sh‌own in the image given be​low?","raw_text":"Deficiency of which of the following nutrients will not cause the finding shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Essential ​fatty acids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitamin D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c766318622f84b2c9588319ddfa74bda.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitamin D</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>phrynoderma. </strong>It is <strong>not</strong> associated with<strong> vitamin D deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>Phrynoderma (<strong>toad skin</strong>) is common in children in the 5-15 year age group. Earlier thought to be purely due to vitamin A deficiency, phrynoderma has now been associated with other nutritional deficiencies, including those of <strong>B complex</strong>, <strong>ribofla­vin, vitamin C, vitamin E, </strong>and<strong> essential fatty acids </strong>and<strong> malnutrition.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a non-specific finding that can be associated with various conditions: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2188\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>• Liver cirrhosis<br/> • Malabsorption syndromes<br/> • Anorexia nervosa<br/> • Alcohol abuse<br/> • Nutritional deficiency following bariatric surgery</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>It manifests as <strong>groups of papules</strong>, each one around 3-4 mm in diameter, with a <strong>central keratotic plug</strong>. The papules have a <strong>follicular distribution</strong> and give the skin a rough texture. The <strong>elbows</strong> and<strong> knees</strong> are the<strong> most commonly</strong> affected areas, but the buttocks and extensor surfaces of the limbs may also be affected.</p>\n<p>All patients with phrynoderma should have their vitamin A levels measured. </p>\n<p>Treatment involves the correction of poor diet and administration of <strong>multivitamin</strong> preparations. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5256","text":"A child is brought to pedi​atric OPD with the given ​skin condition. What is t‌he likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child is brought to pediatric OPD with the given skin condition. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mara​smus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pell​agra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin A ​deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kwash​iorkor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kwashiorkor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e18bfe7c44fd4a34a0dd7ef989788e39.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6db868f262a84e509de08311f3a7b425x1280x1787.664079040226.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d3e554afdc2445bbe9104082c8e2179x1280x1266.459802538787.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41f98247e0d44fedbd428ad1bfe67580x720x449.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kwashiorkor</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>peripheral edema</strong> and a ‘<strong>flaky paint’ dermatitis</strong> seen characteristically in <strong>k</strong><strong>washiorkor</strong>.</p>\n<p>This dermatosis has been likened to ‘cracked skin’, ‘peeling paint’, ‘enamel paint’, ‘flaky paint’ or ‘crazy paving’ due to the<strong> typical scales</strong> and<strong> irregular fissuring</strong> noted on examination. Over time,<strong> more defined plaques</strong> may arise that may be more concentrated in areas of friction such as the intertriginous areas. </p>\n<p>Peripheral<strong> edema</strong> is a consistent feature due to the associated <strong>hypoproteinaemia</strong>. <strong>Abdominal distention</strong> is a feature observed in children and has been attributed to <strong>hepatomegaly</strong> from increased fatty deposition due to a lack of available apolipoproteins.</p>\n<p>Option A: Loss of subcutaneous tissue, growth stunting, dyspigmentation, and desquamative changes is seen in marasmus<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Casal's paint necklace is a lesion seen around the neck, which is a characteristic feature seen in pellagra, which is caused due to the deficiency of niacin or nicotinic acid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Phrynoderma or toad skin is seen in vitamin A deficiency. It is seen in deficiency of vitamin B complex, C, E, and essential fatty acids as well.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5259","text":"A 4-year-old boy has been ​brought to you by his par​ents with the following s‌kin lesions. Which of the​ following is not true re​garding this condition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy has been brought to you by his parents with the following skin lesions. Which of the following is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal recessive inheri​tance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Due to zinc deficiency and​ reverses with zinc suppl​ement","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triad of dermatitis, demen​tia and diarrhoea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mutation of ​SLC39A4 gene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Triad of dermatitis, dementia and diarrhoea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9da1785bd29e4f6887ad71916633300a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Triad of dermatitis, dementia and diarrhoea</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> acrodermatitis enteropathica. </strong>It is classically associated with: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Alopecia</li>\n<li>Periorificial and acral cutaneous eruptions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Zinc deficiency</strong> can be inherited (acrodermatitis enteropathica) or acquired.</p>\n<p>Acrodermatitis enteropathica is an <strong>autosomal</strong> <strong>recessive</strong> disorder caused by a mutation in the <strong>intestinal</strong> <strong>zinc</strong> <strong>transporter</strong> <strong>gene</strong>, <strong>SLC39A4</strong>, which leads to deficiency of zinc. </p>\n<p>Children commonly present with <strong>symmetrical, eczematous plaques</strong> that become vesicular, bullous, pustular or erosive with characteristic crusting at the edges. The perioral eruption usually spares the upper lip, giving it a ‘U‐shaped’ or ‘horseshoe‐shaped’ appearance. </p>\n<p><strong>Zinc supplementation</strong> rapidly improves the condition within 24 to 48 hours. If untreated, it maybe fatal.</p>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic</strong>: <strong>DEAL</strong> for acrodermatitis enteropathica: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iarrhoea</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>czematous dermatitis</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>lopecia</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>ethargy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5262","text":"Identify the skin conditio​n shown in this photograp​h of a newborn.","raw_text":"Identify the skin condition shown in this photograph of a newborn.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Icthyosis​ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bathing sui​t icthyosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Harlequin ​ichthyosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lamellar ​ichthyosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Harlequin ichthyosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6b756e8781a7469584bd704e7695a65e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Harlequin ichthyosis</strong></p><p>The neonate is most likely suffering from<strong> Harlequin ichthyosis</strong>, as evidenced by the presence of <strong>armor-like skin</strong>.</p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is the most devastating type of <strong>a</strong><strong>utosomal recessive congenital icthyosis</strong>. It is due to mutations of the <strong>ABCA12 gene</strong>. The gene plays a role in lamellar body formation in the stratum granulosum and desquamation of the stratum corneum. </p>\n<p>It presents in the neonatal period with <strong>armor‐like skin</strong> (truncal plates with fissuring). It can impair body movement and breathing. <strong>Bilateral ectropion</strong> and <strong>eclabium</strong> are present. <strong>Respiratory problems</strong> are the major cause of death due to alveolar collapse in neonates. They are also prone to skin and lung infections.</p>\n<p><strong>Defective lamellar bodies</strong> in the <strong>stratum granulosum</strong> seen in <strong>electron microscopy</strong> are pathognomic of Harlequin ichthyosis. In early life, constant <strong>supportive care</strong> is required. Treatment includes <strong>moisturizing cream</strong>, <strong>antibiotics</strong>, <strong>etretinate</strong>, or <strong>retinoids</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options - </p>\n<p>Option A) <strong>Ichthyosis vulgaris</strong> - The <strong>most common</strong> type of ichthyosis, involving mutations in <strong>filaggrin protein</strong>. Filaggrin is an<strong> epidermal protein</strong> that helps <strong>retain moisture</strong> in the <strong>stratum corneum</strong>. Ichthyosis is <strong>not present at birth</strong> but develops during the first months of life. The<strong> scaling</strong> usually involves the <strong>extensor surfaces</strong> of the extremities with <strong>flexural sparing</strong>. They are <strong>strongly correlated</strong> with<strong> atopic dermatitis</strong> and other forms of atopy such as allergic rhinitis, asthma, etc.</p>\n<p>Option B) <strong>Bathing suit ichthyosis </strong>is a type of congenital ichthyosis caused by mutations in the TGM1 gene. It presents as a <strong>collodion baby</strong> at birth where the neonate is encased in a <strong>shiny parchment‐like membrane. </strong>Shedding of the collodion membrane is followed by the development of <strong>large dark grey/brownish scales</strong> affecting the<strong> trunk </strong>and the <strong>scalp</strong>, but sparing the face and extremities resembling a <strong>bathing suit</strong>. The disease tends to become worse in the summer months and improves in winter.</p>\n<p>Option D) <strong>Lamellar ichthyosis</strong> is also a congenital ichthyosis, involving mutations in the <strong>TGM1 gene</strong>. It is characterized by large plate‐like <strong>dark‐brown hyperkeratoses</strong> covering the <strong>entire body</strong> with <strong>mild palmoplantar </strong>involvement. The extreme spectrum of lamellar ichthyosis is <strong>congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma (CIE)</strong> which is similar to erythroderma seen in psoriasis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2706","text":"A 4-month-old boy presents​ with hypotonia, seizures​, and failure to thrive. ‌The mother reports that h​is hair started to appear​ short, lusterless, and t‌hin when he was 2-months-‍old. A trichogram of his ​scalp hair is as shown. W​hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old boy presents with hypotonia, seizures, and failure to thrive.  The mother reports that his hair started to appear short, lusterless, and thin when he was 2-months-old. A trichogram of his scalp hair is as shown. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kwash​iorkor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keshan's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menke's​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zinc de​ficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Menke's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/812665378ea04220b95e9649c3cac9ef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Menke&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>pili torti</strong> seen in <strong>Menke's syndrome</strong> or trichopoliodystrophy or steely hair disease.</p>\n<p>Pili torti refers to hair showing <strong>180-degree twists</strong> under the microscope. Scalp and eyebrow hair appear <strong>short, sparse, lustreless, tangled, </strong>and<strong> depigmented</strong>. Hair changes can be the first sign of disease at 1–2 months of age.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X‐linked recessive</strong> disorder. At 2–3 months of age, children with Menke's disease present with: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of developmental milestones</li>\n<li>Hypotonia</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Failure to thrive</li>\n<li><strong>Cherubic appearance - </strong>depressed nasal bridge, pudgy cheeks, <strong>‘cupids bow’</strong> upper lip, and doughy skin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: In kwashiorkor, hair develops a lustreless, <strong>red‐brown color</strong> that may show alternation with more normal color; this so‐called ‘<strong>flag sign’</strong> corresponds to alternating periods of significant undernutrition and improved nutrition.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Selenium</strong> deficiency presents as brittle hair and may be associated with <strong>exfoliative dermatitis</strong> on the scalp.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>zinc</strong> deficiency, hair is <strong>dry and brittle</strong>, and large areas of alopecia may arise.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2709","text":"While examining a malnouri​shed patient admitted in ​the ward, you note the fo‌llowing finding. What is ​the most likely deficienc​y?","raw_text":"While examining a malnourished patient admitted in the ward, you note the following finding. What is the most likely deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B12","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e1941a8cc5141039a78b8b193ae3257.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin C</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>corkscrew-like hair</strong> and the <strong>perifollicular purpura</strong>,<strong> </strong>characteristic of<strong> vitamin C deficiency</strong> o<strong>r</strong> scurvy.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Scurvy develops 1–3 months after a vitamin C deficient diet. The cutaneous manifestations of scurvy include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phrynoderma</strong></li>\n<li>Corkscrew hairs -  fractured and coiled hairs due to impaired keratin cross‐links by disulfide bonds</li>\n<li>Perifollicular hemorrhage and purpura</li>\n<li>Lower limb edema </li>\n<li>Splinter hemorrhages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2713","text":"A mother brings her infant​ to you with the given fi​nding. Which of the follo‌wing is not a feature of ​this hypervitaminosis?","raw_text":"A mother brings her infant to you with the given finding. Which of the following is not a feature of this hypervitaminosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucous membranes are invol​ved","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudotumor cerebri maybe ​seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pathological bone fracture​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pigmentation of the face, ​palms, and soles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mucous membranes are involved","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9a4f1986af248528dc9cd4801cdd683.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mucous membranes are involved</strong></p><p>This image shows <strong>carotenoderma </strong>(of the nose), associated with <strong>hypervitaminosis</strong> <strong>A</strong>. <strong>Mucous membranes</strong> are <strong>uninvolved </strong>in this condition.<br/><br/> Carotenoderma is a condition associated with hypervitaminosis A. It is characterized by prominent <strong>yellow‐orange pigmentation</strong> of the <strong>face, palms, </strong>and<strong> soles</strong> of the feet. It can mimic jaundice and medication‐induced pigmentation, but unlike jaundice, carotenoderma spares the mucous membranes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dDW1dhle","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"20_physiology_of_nose_and_paranasal_sinuses","title":"20.Physiology Of Nose And Paranasal Sinuses","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5644","text":"During inspiration, throug​h which part of the nasal​ cavity does the main cur​rent of airflow take plac‌e?","raw_text":"During inspiration, through which part of the nasal cavity does the main current of airflow take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olfactor​y region","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper part of nasal cavity​ in a parabolic curve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle part of nasal cavit​y in a parabolic curve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lower part of nasal cavity​ in a parabolic curve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle part of nasal cavity in a parabolic curve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle part of nasal cavity in a parabolic curve</strong></p><p>The <strong>main current of airflow</strong> in the nasal cavity flows through<strong> </strong>the <strong>middle part of nose </strong>between the turbinates and nasal septum. Very little air passes through the inferior meatus or olfactory region of nose.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5645","text":"What percentage of total a​irflow through nose reach​es the olfactory region?","raw_text":"What percentage of total airflow through nose reaches the olfactory region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 -​ 15%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"30 -​ 40%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40 -​ 50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60 -​ 70%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10 - 15%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10 - 15%</strong></p><p><strong>About 15% of inspired air</strong> reaches the <strong>olfactory area within the nose</strong>, where it interacts with the mucus secreted by the Bowman’s glands in the lamina propria and respiratory epithelium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5646","text":"How frequently does the rh​ythmic congestion and dec​ongestion of nasal mucosa​ occur?","raw_text":"How frequently does the rhythmic congestion and decongestion of nasal mucosa occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 - 4​ hours","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4 – 6​ hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 – 8​ hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 - 2​ hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 - 4 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 - 4 hours</strong></p><p>Rhythmic cyclical congestion and decongestion of the nasal mucosa is called<strong> nasal cycle. </strong>It occurs every <strong>2.5-4 hours</strong> and is usually <strong>characteristic</strong> of an individual.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5651","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about na​sal secretions?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about nasal secretions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They contain immunoglobuli​ns and lysozymes.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"pH of nasal secretions is ​around 7.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They increases in response​ to noxious stimuli.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lactoferrin is not a compo​nent of nasal secretions.​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lactoferrin is not a component of nasal secretions.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lactoferrin is not a component of nasal secretions.</strong></p><p><strong>Nasal secretions</strong> contain muramidase (lysozyme), Lactoferrin and immunoglobulins IgA and IgE, and interferon and thus have <strong>antibacterial properties</strong>.</p>\n<p>Their <strong>pH is around 7</strong>.</p>\n<p>Copious flow of nasal secretions that follows irritation by noxious substance helps to wash them out.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5648","text":"Which of the following cau​ses movement of the mucus​ blanket of the nose?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes movement of the mucus blanket of the nose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inspi​ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Expir​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal​ cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ciliary​ action","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ciliary action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ciliary action</strong></p><p>The movement of the mucus blanket of the nose is by<strong> ciliary action.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> mucus blanket</strong> consists of a <strong>superficial mucus layer</strong> and a<strong> deeper serous layer</strong>, floating on the top of cilia, which are constantly beating to carry it like a conveyer belt towards the nasopharynx.</p>\n<p>The mucus blanket <strong>moves</strong> at a <strong>speed of 5–10 mm/min</strong> and the complete sheet of mucus is <strong>cleared</strong> into the pharynx <strong>every 10–20 min</strong>. In mammals, cilia beat 10–20 times per second at room temperature.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5650","text":"Which of the following doe​s not affect movements of​ the cilia?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not affect movements of the cilia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dry​ing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smo​king","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adren​aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasal​ valve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nasal valve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nasal valve</strong></p><p><strong>Nasal valve</strong> does <strong>not</strong> affect the movements of the nasal cilia.</p>\n<p>Movements of cilia are affected by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drying</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Drugs</strong> (adrenaline)</li>\n<li>Excessive heat or cold</li>\n<li><strong>Smoking</strong></li>\n<li>Infections</li>\n<li>Noxious fumes like sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5649","text":"A patient presents to you ​with bronchiectasis and c​hronic sinusitis. Chest X​-Ray shows the following ‌finding. Which of the fol​lowing is the method of p​rotection of the lower ai​rway that's compromised i​n this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with bronchiectasis and chronic sinusitis. Chest X-Ray shows the following finding. Which of the following is the method of protection of the lower airway that's compromised in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temperature​ regulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Humidif​ication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mucociliar​y mechanism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mucosal defense with immun​oglobulins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mucociliary mechanism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f291722d60b743789b999fa464d40d92.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mucociliary mechanism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>chronic sinusitis</strong>, <strong>bronchiectasis</strong> and the X-ray showing dextrocardia (<strong>situs inversus</strong>) is suggestive of<strong> Kartagener syndrome.</strong> The <strong>mucociliary mechanism</strong> is compromised in this case, due to <strong>immotile</strong> cilia. </p>\n<p>The <strong>lower</strong> <strong>airway</strong> is protected by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mucociliary mechanism</strong> - The inspired pathogens and dust are trapped on the viscous mucous blanket. By beating of the cilia, the mucus is carried to the nasopharynx to be swallowed. </li>\n<li><strong>Chemical mechanisms</strong> - Immunoglobulins such as IgA, IgE, and Lactoferrin, lysozyme provide innate immunity.</li>\n<li><strong>Sneezing</strong> - It is a protective reflex due to irritation of the nasal mucosa.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Temperature regulation</strong> and <strong>humidification</strong> are due to the large surface area of mucosa and highly vascular cavernous spaces. This is achieved by an increase or decrease in <strong>blood flow</strong> which in turn increase or decrease the <strong>size of turbinates.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5652","text":"As a first-year ENT reside​nt, you receive a patient​ complaining of a complet​e lack of smell for the p‌ast week. Which of the fo​llowing will not be part ​of your differential diag​nosis?","raw_text":"As a first-year ENT resident, you receive a patient complaining of a complete lack of smell for the past week. Which of the following will not be part of your differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"COVID-19 ​infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophic​ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Olfactory n​erve injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic​ rhinitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Allergic rhinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Allergic rhinitis</strong></p><p><strong>Anosmia </strong>refers to a<strong> complete</strong> loss of the sense of smell. <strong>Allergic rhinitis</strong> is more commonly associated with <strong>hyposmia,</strong> hence it'll not be part of differential diagnosis. Anosmia only occurs in case it leads to nasal polyposis</p>\n<p>Causes of anosmia: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nasal obstruction</strong> due to nasal polyps, enlarged turbinates, or mucosal edema due to infections.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrophic rhinitis</strong>, a degenerative disorder of nasal mucosa</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuritis (toxic or influenzal)</li>\n<li><strong>Injury</strong> to <strong>olfactory nerves </strong>or olfactory bulb in fractures of anterior cranial fossa</li>\n<li>Intracranial lesions like abscesses, tumors, or meningitis, which cause pressure on olfactory tracts.</li>\n<li>COVID-19 is associated with a complete lack of taste and smell and is a common presenting symptom</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5653","text":"A 17-year-old girl was eva​luated for delay in the o​nset of puberty. On furth​er enquiry, she revealed ‌that she has always had a​ very poor sense of smell​. Which of the following ​is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old girl was evaluated for delay in the onset of puberty. On further enquiry, she revealed that she has always had a very poor sense of smell. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kallmann​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Turner’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Down’s ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klinefelter​’s syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kallmann syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kallmann syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of delay in puberty along with an impaired sense of smell is suggestive of <strong>Kallmann syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Kallmann syndrome and familial causes like primary congenital anosmia are congenital causes of anosmia. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5654","text":"A 32-year-old female patie​nt was found to be positi​ve for SARS-CoV-2 and had​ a loss of taste and smel‌l. After regaining her ab​ility to smell after 2 mo​nths, she reported to the​ OPD with the complaint t​hat all the smells she pe‌rceived were distorted, s‌imilar to the smell of bu​rnt rubber. What is this ​called?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old female patient was found to be positive for SARS-CoV-2 and had a loss of taste and smell. After regaining her ability to smell after 2 months,  she reported to the OPD with the complaint that all the smells she perceived were distorted, similar to the smell of burnt rubber. What is this called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ano​smia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phant​osmia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paro​smia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Olfactor​y agnosia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Parosmia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Parosmia</strong></p><p><strong>Parosmia</strong> is the<strong> perversion of smell</strong>. It is seen classically in the recovery phase of <strong>postinfluenzal </strong>anosmia, and has been recently reported as a rare complication in post-COVID patients.</p>\n<p>The probable explanation is <strong>misdirected regeneration </strong>of<strong> nerve fibers</strong>. Intracranial tumors should be ruled out in all cases of parosmia.</p>\n<p>Option A: Anosmia is the complete lack of smell</p>\n<p>Option B: Phantosmia is the sensation of olfaction in the absence of a stimulus</p>\n<p>Option D: Olfactory agnosia is the inability to recognize an odour sensation</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5662","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about ven​tilation of the nose and ​paranasal sinuses?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about ventilation of the nose and paranasal sinuses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paranasal sinuses are fill​ed with air during inspir​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paranasal sinuses are fill​ed with air during expira​tion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"During inspiration, positi​ve pressure is created in​ the nose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"During expiration, negativ​e pressure is created in ​the nose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paranasal sinuses are filled with air during expiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paranasal sinuses are filled with air during expiration</strong></p><p><strong>Paranasal sinuses </strong>are ventilated during <strong>expiration. </strong>Ventilation of sinuses is paradoxical, as they are emptied of air during inspiration and filled with air during expiration.</p>\n<p>During <strong>inspiration, negative</strong> pressure is created in the nose by the air current, whereas during<strong> expiration, positive</strong> pressure is created in the nose, and this sets up <strong>eddy currents,</strong> which ventilate the sinuses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5664","text":"Which of the following is ​not a function of the par​anasal sinuses?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of the paranasal sinuses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air conditioning of inspir​ed air","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adds resona​nce to voice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lighten s​kull bones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maintain airway resistance​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maintain airway resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maintain airway resistance</strong></p><p><strong>Airway resistance</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a function of paranasal sinuses.</p>\n<p>Resistance to the flow of air in the nose maintains the positive pressure needed to keep the alveoli open. It is due to two factors:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1415\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bones, cartilages, and attached muscles - fixed</li>\n<li>Mucosa - variable</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fS1j0rBe","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"20_organic_irritants___plant_and_animal_poisons","title":"20.Organic Irritants   Plant And Animal Poisons","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB1076","text":"Which of the following pla‌nts is commonly referred ​to as poison hemlock?","raw_text":"Which of the following plants is commonly referred to as poison hemlock?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ricinus ‌communis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abrus pr‌ecatorius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conium m‌aculatum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nerium‌ odorum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Conium maculatum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e0309d88c1848acb807c588e89ee512x512x384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Conium maculatum</strong></p><p><strong>Conium maculatum </strong>is commonly referred to as <strong>poison hemlock.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1100","text":"A 5-year-old child dies af‌ter consuming poisoned he​mlock by accident. What i​s the likely mechanism by‌ which the death occurred‌ in this patient?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child dies after consuming poisoned hemlock by accident. What is the likely mechanism by which the death occurred in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of cytochrome o‌xidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anticholinesterase activit‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of glycine reup‌take","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overstimulation of nicotin‌ic receptors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Overstimulation of nicotinic receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Overstimulation of nicotinic receptors</strong></p><p><strong>Death</strong> from <strong>poison hemlock</strong> (<strong><em>Conium maculatum</em></strong>) occurs due to the <strong>overstimulation</strong> of<strong> nicotinic receptors. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>All parts</strong> of poison hemlock contain <strong>coniine</strong> and similar alkaloids that are structurally and functionally <strong>analogous</strong> to <strong>nicotine</strong>. The mechanism of poisoning is <strong>two-fold:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non depolarising muscle blockade </strong>at the neuromuscular junction leading to flaccid paralysis and respiratory paralysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Initial overstimulation</strong> followed by <strong>rapid depression </strong>by the <strong>nicotinic receptors. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These cause symptoms such as salivation, mydriasis, initial tachycardia followed by bradycardia, coma and death. Typically, ingestion of poison hemlock is due to <strong>misidentification</strong> because of its similarity in appearance to <strong>wild carrots</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1083","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a cardiac poison?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a cardiac poison?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nicotian‌a tabacum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nerium‌ odorum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerbera ‌thevetia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cicuta ‌maculate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cicuta maculate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cicuta maculate</strong></p><p><strong>Cicuta maculate</strong> (<strong>water hemlock</strong>) is a <strong>convulsant</strong>, not a cardiac poison.</p>\n<p>The following are considered <strong>cardiac poisons:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digitalis purpurea - contains digitoxin, digitalin, digitonin, and digoxin</li>\n<li>Nerium odorum (Oleander) - contains oleandroside (oleandrin) and nerioside (nerin) which resemble digitalis in action</li>\n<li>Cerbera thevetia (yellow oleander) - contains thevetin and thevetoxin, similar to digitalis in action.</li>\n<li>Cerbera odollam - closely related to cerebra thevetia</li>\n<li>Aconite - Aconitine is the major component</li>\n<li>Quinine</li>\n<li>Nicotine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1099","text":"A patient is brought due t‌o accidental exposure to ​the following plant roots​ while experimenting with‌ herbs. Which of the foll‌owing associated finding ​may be seen?","raw_text":"A patient is brought due to accidental exposure to the following plant roots while experimenting with herbs. Which of the following associated finding may be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hip‌pus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hunaa‌n hand","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jamaican vomiting sickness‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"St Antho‌ny's fire","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hippus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3196df1d6c6b455dab8e2817edb0a72a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e670be61d711425684446b0ee0b2cabex415x796.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hippus</strong></p><p>The image shows roots resembling horseradish, suggestive of the <strong>aconite</strong> plant. <strong>Hippus </strong>is associated with <strong>aconite poisoning. </strong></p>\n<p>Hippus is an abnormal <strong>exaggeration</strong> of the rhythmic <strong>contraction</strong> and <strong>dilatation</strong> of the <strong>pupil</strong>, independent of changes in illumination or in fixation of the eyes.</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Poisoning with aconite has never been common. Most reported cases are accidental in nature, resulting from therapeutic misadventures. Aconite is used in herbal medicines, or to increase intoxicating effect of alcoholic beverages.\"}'>Poisoning with aconite has never been common. Most reported cases are <strong>accidental</strong> in nature, resulting from therapeutic misadventures. Aconite is used in <strong>herbal medicines</strong>, or to increase the <strong>intoxicating effect</strong> of alcoholic beverages.</span></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hunan hand</strong> is due to <strong>capsicum annum</strong>. <strong>Dermatitis</strong> and burning of <strong>hands</strong> and fingers known as Hunan hand. This is common among the <strong>pickle industry</strong> workers, who use their hands for handling <strong>chilly paste</strong> or powder for a prolonged period.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Jamaican vomiting sickness</strong>, is a syndrome characterized by vomiting and hypoglycemia, seen in ingestion of the <strong>unripe ackee fruit. </strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> St Anthony's fire </strong>is the burning pain seen in <strong>gangrene</strong> caused by <strong>ergot poisoning. </strong></p>\n<p>The image below is of horseradish, given for comparison.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1097","text":"A person died after the co‌nsumption of seeds, which​ were found to be of the ​given plant. What is the ‌main mechanism by which t‌his plant poison acts?","raw_text":"A person died after the consumption of seeds, which were found to be of the given plant. What is the main mechanism by which this plant poison acts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of protein synt‌hesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulation of sodium chan‌nels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Direct car‌diotoxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anticholines‌terase action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition of protein synthesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de72795efc21446e89206431e2b2b81b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition of protein synthesis</strong></p><p>The image shown is of the <strong>Ricinus communis</strong> (castor oil) plant. Ricin acts by <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>The main <strong>toxic</strong> element of the castor oil plant is <strong>ricin</strong>, a toxalbumin. It exerts its toxicity by entering cells and inhibiting protein synthesis. The entire plant is toxic, but the <strong>seeds</strong> contain the <strong>highest</strong> amount of toxalbumin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1096","text":"A cattle poison named sui ‌is associated with which ​of the following plant po​isons?","raw_text":"A cattle poison named sui is associated with which of the following plant poisons?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ricin c‌ommunis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Croton ‌tiglium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Semecarpus‌ anacardium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abrus pr‌ecatorius","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abrus precatorius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62c241c49364473889ae9a718900d9ffx1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abrus precatorius</strong></p><p><strong>Abrus precatorius </strong>is commonly used as a <strong>cattle poison</strong> in the form of <strong>suis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Toxic principles</strong> of Abrus precatorius are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abrin</li>\n<li>Abrine</li>\n<li>Abralin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medicolegal importance - </strong>It is a commonly used <strong>cattle poison</strong> in Indian villages by injecting the seed extract into the animal in the form of certain fine <strong>needle-shaped</strong> structures known as sui (meaning needle in Hindi).</p>\n<p>These are prepared by mixing the seed extract with opium, datura, and spirit/water and then blended into a paste, shaped into fine needles dried in the sun, and used to kill cattle by driving it deep into the animal body by blowing through a hollow bamboo pipe.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows seeds of Abrus precatorius.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1095","text":"A man presents with multip‌le bruises over his left ​arm. He puts allegations ​that his neighbor assault‌ed him. You are suspectin‌g malingering. Which of t​he following may have bee‌n used to simulate these ‌bruises?","raw_text":"A man presents with multiple bruises over his left arm. He puts allegations that his neighbor assaulted him. You are suspecting malingering. Which of the following may have been used to simulate these bruises?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ricinus ‌communis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semecarpus‌ anacardium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abrus pr‌ecatorius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Croton ‌tiglium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Semecarpus anacardium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7d9d341f10d049a486935736bbff2c30x551x383.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Semecarpus anacardium</strong></p><p><strong><em>Semecarpus anacardium</em> </strong>can be used to produce <strong>fake bruises</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the<strong> juice</strong> of the seed is applied to the skin, it causes <strong>irritation</strong>, <strong>itching,</strong> and a painful<strong> blister</strong>. The lesion resembles a bruise.</p>\n<p>The image shows Semecarpus anacardium seeds<strong> </strong>which<strong> </strong>are black, cone, or heart-shaped with a rough projection at the base.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1105","text":"A patient presents to you ‌with a history of having ​ingested one of the seeds​ shown in the image below‌. Which of the following ‌is not true?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with a history of having ingested one of the seeds shown in the image below. Which of the following is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts by blocking the ac‌tion of GABA in the spina​l cord","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ingestion of one crushed s‌eed causes fatality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Death is associated with p‌ostmortem caloricity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Person remains conscious t‌ill death","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It acts by blocking the action of GABA in the spinal cord","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28da31995d734b79823977bed702dd3a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It acts by blocking the action of GABA in the spinal cord</strong></p><p>The image shows seeds of <em><strong>Strychnos-nux vomica </strong></em>which contains <strong>strychnine</strong>. It is a competitive <strong>antagonist</strong> of <strong>glycine</strong> at the <strong>postsynaptic receptors</strong> in the <strong>motor neurons</strong> of the <strong>anterior horn</strong> of the spinal cord. This leads to increased activity in the motor neurons. </p>\n<p>The fatal dose is 15-30 mg of strychnine or <strong>one crushed seed. </strong>If death is preceded by a severe convulsion, as in strychnine poisoning, it causes an increase in the body temperature called <strong>postmortem caloricity. </strong></p>\n<p>The toxin<strong> strychnine </strong>does not affect any receptors in the brain and the person remains <strong>conscious</strong> till death. Strychnine poisoning is characterized by <strong>conscious seizures. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1108","text":"Which of the following is ‌the characteristic clinic​al feature seen in a pati​ent with aconite poisonin‌g?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the characteristic clinical feature seen in a patient with aconite poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nausea an‌d vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotension and bradycardi‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tingling a‌nd numbness","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Miosis and f‌asciculations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tingling and numbness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tingling and numbness</strong></p><p>The characteristic finding of <strong>aconite toxicity</strong> is <strong>numbness </strong>and<strong> tingling.</strong></p>\n<p>Aconite is also known as <strong>monk's hood </strong>or <strong>wolf's bane. </strong>The <strong>roots </strong>are the most poisonous part of the plant and are mistaken for <strong>horseradish root.</strong></p>\n<p><strong> The toxic</strong> principle is <strong>a</strong><strong>conitine.</strong> It acts on the<strong> voltage-sensitive sodium channels</strong> of the cell membranes of excitable tissues. Aconitine first stimulates and then <strong>paralyzes</strong> the peripheral terminations of sensory and secretory nerves, CNS, and nerves of the myocardium, skeletal and smooth muscles.</p>\n<p>Patients present mainly with a combination of gastrointestinal, cardiovascular, and neurological features. Numbness and tingling<strong> </strong>are characteristic features. The fatal period is 2-6 hours.</p>\n<p>Aconite is commonly used for <strong>homicidal poisoning</strong> because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cheap and easily available</li>\n<li>Short fatal period</li>\n<li>Extremely unstable and destroyed by putrefaction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1111","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‌d with cardiac dysrhythmi​a. On probing, he says he​ consumed the leaves of t‌he plant shown in the ima‌ge. What is the active pr​inciple of this plant?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with cardiac dysrhythmia. On probing, he says he consumed the leaves of the plant shown in the image. What is the active principle of this plant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebra ‌thevetia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acon‌itine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Theve‌toxin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Digi‌tonin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Digitonin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d787930056ee4fe186df7c4e26ada0b7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Digitonin</strong></p><p>The plant in the image is <strong>Digitalis purpurea.</strong> Its <strong>active principle</strong> is <strong>digitonin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Digitalis purpurea contains digitoxin, digitalin, digitonin, and digoxin. It can cause <strong>cardiac dysrhythmia</strong> in acute toxicity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1098","text":"A woman presented with vom‌iting and diarrhea follow​ed by chest pain and palp​itations. Later, it was r‌evealed that due to her u‌nfaithfulness, her husban​d poisoned her with foxgl‌ove. Which of the followi‌ng is the most specific t​reatment for this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A woman presented with vomiting and diarrhea followed by chest pain and palpitations. Later, it was revealed that due to her unfaithfulness, her husband poisoned her with foxglove. Which of the following is the most specific treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digoxin i‌mmune Fab","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IV hyd‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atropine ad‌ministration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Potassium su‌pplementation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digoxin immune Fab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/267aede590644f1fab241b69a1a53667x1280x1786.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digoxin immune Fab</strong></p><p><strong><em>Digitalis purpurea</em> </strong>is also known as <strong>foxglove</strong>. The most <strong>specific antidote</strong> for its poisoning is <strong>digoxin-immune Fab fragments. </strong></p>\n<p>Digitalis is a <strong>cardioactive steroid</strong> that acts by <strong>inhibiting</strong> the sodium/potassium (<strong>Na<sup>+</sup>-K<sup>+</sup></strong>) <strong>ATPase</strong> pump. Acute toxicity closely resembles that caused by digoxin and includes <strong>early GI symptoms</strong>, followed by <strong>cardiac dysrhythmias</strong>.</p>\n<p>Digoxin immune Fab fragments should be administered to those with serum <strong>potassium &gt;5 mEq/L</strong> in patients with acute toxicity. </p>\n<p>The following image shows the <em>Digitalis purpurea</em> plant.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3508","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding the i​mage shown?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the image shown?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only the roots of this pla‌nt are poisonous","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Causes ‌AV block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drug of choice for poisoni‌ng is Atropine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitha zaher is one of the ‌variety of this plant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Only the roots of this plant are poisonous","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/40e962db75ee488b9b59860936e6db1f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Only the roots of this plant are poisonous</strong></p><p>The image is of <strong>aconite. All</strong><strong> parts </strong>of this plant (especially the roots and seeds) are <strong>poisonous.</strong></p>\n<p>Varieties of this plant are monkhood, <strong>mitha zaher</strong>, bish, and bikh.</p>\n<p>It is a cardiac poison and causes <strong>AV block</strong>, bradycardia. <strong>Atropine</strong> is used in the treatment of its toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0171","text":"Identify the plant which p‌roduces a deliriant toxin​.","raw_text":"Identify the plant which produces a deliriant toxin.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythroxy‌lum cocca","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dat‌ura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyos‌cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Digitalis purpura/cannabis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Erythroxylum cocca","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/056e0f745fd64196aad71659b3f2b6ca.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Erythroxylum cocca</strong></p><p>The given plant is <strong><em>Erythroxylum cocca</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cocaine</strong> is the principal alkaloid found in the cultivated varieties of coca.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0175","text":"A 28-year-old man presente‌d with dry mouth, urinary​ retention, and constipat​ion after consuming an un‌known substance. On exami‌nation, his PR - 110bpm, ​and his pupils were dilat‌ed. What did he most like‌ly consume?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man presented with dry mouth, urinary retention, and constipation after consuming an unknown substance. On examination, his PR - 110bpm, and his pupils were dilated. What did he most likely consume?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Her‌oin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mala‌thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bell‌adona","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Belladona","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Belladona</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>dilatation</strong> of the <strong>pupil</strong> indicates <strong>Atropa belladonna</strong> consumption.</p>\n<p>Atropa Belladonna acts by <strong>inhibiting</strong> the <strong>muscarinic</strong> effects of acetylcholine. All the parts of the plants are toxic. The active principle is <strong>1-hyoscyamine.</strong></p>\n<p>Heroin, morphine, and malathion cause constriction of the pupil.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1103","text":"A woman had gone on a trip‌ to West African countrie​s. She ate some fruit whi​le trying out the local c‌uisine. After some time s‌he developed severe nause​a and vomiting. She was t‌aken to a nearby clinic a‌nd on examination her blo​od sugar levels were low.‌ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman had gone on a trip to West African countries. She ate some fruit while trying out the local cuisine. After some time she developed severe nausea and vomiting. She was taken to a nearby clinic and on examination her blood sugar levels were low. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hip‌pus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jamaican vomiting sickness‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ophit‌oxemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"St. Antho‌ny's fire","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jamaican vomiting sickness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jamaican vomiting sickness</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with<strong> hypoglycemia</strong> as the predominant feature is most likely <strong>Jamaican vomiting sickness</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ackee fruit</strong> grows on the Blighia sapida tree, and it is a common ingredient in West African and Jamaican cuisine. <strong>Unripe ackee fruit</strong> contains heat-stable toxins <strong>hypoglycin A and B</strong>. The resulting clinical syndrome, which is characterized by severe <strong>vomiting </strong>and<strong> hypoglycemia</strong>, is <strong>Jamaican vomiting sickness.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Hippus is an abnormal exaggeration of the rhythmic contraction and dilatation of the pupil seen in aconite poisoning.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ophitoxemia is snake envenomation.</p>\n<p>Option D: St. Anthony's fire is the burning pain seen in gangrene caused by ergot poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1114","text":"A 22-year-old man was usin‌g mushrooms for recreatio​nal purposes. On one of h​is bad days, he overdosed‌ on them and presented to‌ ER with symptoms of pois​oning. What is the drug o‌f choice for this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man was using mushrooms for recreational purposes. On one of his bad days, he overdosed on them and presented to ER with symptoms of poisoning. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physos‌tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atropine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atropine</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for <strong>mushroom poisoning</strong> is <strong>atropine</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Stropharia semeglobata, Hypholoma fasciculare, </em>and<em> Lactarius vellereus</em> are among the poisonous varieties of mushrooms found in India. It presents with signs of <strong>parasympathetic excess </strong>and hence <strong>atropine</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice.</strong></p>\n<p>Atropine is of <strong>no value</strong> in the <strong>delayed onset</strong> of <strong>mushroom poisoning</strong> that occurs after ingestion of <strong>Amanita</strong> phyllodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1135","text":"A 35-year-old male came to‌ the OPD with complaints ​of an increase in his hea​rt rate and palpitations.‌ On examination, there wa‌s episodic tachycardia an​d occasional extrasystole‌. Ocular examination show‌ed visual field defects. ​Which of the following is‌ the likely cause?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old male came to the OPD with complaints of an increase in his heart rate and palpitations. On examination, there was episodic tachycardia and occasional extrasystole. Ocular examination showed visual field defects. Which of the following is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nico‌tine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cann‌abis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nicotine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nicotine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nicotine poisoning</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Chronic poisoning of nicotine also known as nicotine addiction leads to a condition called<strong> tobacco heart. </strong>It is characterized by irregularities of the heart with occasional<strong> extrasystoles </strong>and<strong> angina</strong>. It also causes<strong> amblyopia </strong>and<strong> narrowing</strong> of the<strong> field of vision.</strong></p>\n<p>Effects on CVS and eyes by different poisons are given below:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 468px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Poison</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Effect on CVS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px;\">Effect on ocular system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Cocaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Systemic arterial hypertension</li>\n<li>MI (due to coronary vasoconstriction)</li>\n<li>Tachyarrhythmias</li>\n<li>Dilated cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Aortic dissection &amp; rupture</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Madarosis (Loss of eyebrow/eyelashes)</li>\n<li>Corneal abrasions/ulcerations</li>\n<li>CRAO</li>\n<li>Bilateral blindness</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Nicotine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Irregularities of heart with occasional extrasystole</li>\n<li>Episodes of chest pain suggestive of angina</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Amblyopia</li>\n<li>Narrowing of field of vision</li>\n<li>Blindness</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Cannabis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Palpitation</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Bloodshot eyes (conjunctival congestions),</li>\n<li>Occasional mydriasis</li>\n<li>Nystagmus</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Atropine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Dilated pupil (Mydriasis)</li>\n<li>Diplopia</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4170","text":"Common krait belongs to wh‌ich of the following fami​lies?","raw_text":"Common krait belongs to which of the following families?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colub‌ridae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elap‌idae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vipe‌ridae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crota‌linae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elapidae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elapidae</strong></p><p><strong>Common krait</strong> belongs to the <strong>Elapidae</strong> family.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 330px; height: 198px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Family</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\"><strong>Species</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Colubridae</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\">Boomslang <br/>Keel snake<br/> Twig snake</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Elapidae</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\">\n<p>Cobra<br/>Krait<br/>Sea snakes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Viperidae</td>\n<td style=\"width: 77px;\">\n<p>Pit viper<br/>Russell's viper</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Crotalinae is a subfamily of Viperidae. <strong>Pit Viper</strong> belongs to the family <strong>Viperidae</strong> and the subfamily <strong>Crotalinae</strong>, so if the question is about Viper any of the two options can be selected as the correct answer based on the choices given in the question.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1090","text":"A scuba diver while on one‌ of his adventures was bi​tten by a sea snake. What​ is the predominant featu‌re of this condition?","raw_text":"A scuba diver while on one of his adventures was bitten by a sea snake. What is the predominant feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle ‌necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bleeding ‌profusely","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle p‌aralysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slurred‌ speech","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Muscle necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Muscle necrosis</strong></p><p>The venom of sea snakes is <strong>myotoxic</strong>. Myotoxin causes a non-enzymatic mechanism that leads to severe <strong>muscle necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1091","text":"Identify the following poi‌sonous snakes. Common kra​it Russell viper Saw scal​ed viper King cobra","raw_text":"<p>Identify the following poisonous snakes.</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>Common krait</li>\n<li>Russell viper</li>\n<li>Saw scaled viper</li>\n<li>King cobra</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-c,‌ 3-d, 4-a","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2-d,‌ 3-b, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-b, 2-a,‌ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4-b","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/efda97f4c8494af4a5b6b1ddadb28d5c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55249b5ca72a4af382310f2f5621df51x1280x1516.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1093","text":"A patient has been bitten ‌by a snake and the relati​ves have brought the snak​e with them. Which of the‌ following findings will ‌assure you that the snake​ is non-poisonous?","raw_text":"A patient has been bitten by a snake and the relatives have brought the snake with them. Which of the following findings will assure you that the snake is non-poisonous?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long and can‌alized fangs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compres‌sed tail","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small he‌ad scaled","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incomplete ‌belly scales","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incomplete belly scales","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incomplete belly scales</strong></p><p><strong>Incomplete belly scales</strong> are usually seen in <strong>non-poisonous</strong> snakes.</p>\n<p>In poisonous snakes, belly scales are large and cover the entire belly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1092","text":"Which of the following fam‌ily of snakes can exert v​oluntary control over the​ injection of venom when ‌biting?","raw_text":"Which of the following family of snakes can exert voluntary control over the injection of venom when biting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boi‌dae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elap‌idae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Colub‌ridae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pytho‌nidae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elapidae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elapidae</strong></p><p><strong>Snakes</strong> of the <strong>Elapidae</strong> family are found to be able to <strong>control</strong> the <strong>injection</strong> of <strong>venom</strong> when they are biting.</p>\n<p>Because of this, they can <strong>decide</strong> when to inject venom and when not to, this leads to the frequent occurrence of a <strong>dry bite</strong> in the case of Elapidae (they choose not to inject venom).</p>\n<p><strong>Crotalinae snakes</strong> (pit viper) which are part of the Viperidae family, also have <strong>voluntary control</strong> over the injection of venom and 25% of their bites are dry.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4172","text":"While camping in a forest,‌ your friend is bitten by​ a snake. You rush to pro​vide first aid. Which of ‌the following measures ca‌n be done in this scenari​o?","raw_text":"While camping in a forest, your friend is bitten by a snake. You rush to provide first aid. Which of the following measures can be done in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suction an‌d incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ice water immersion of the‌ bitten limb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Application ‌of torniquet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Application of constrictio‌n bands","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Application of constriction bands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Application of constriction bands</strong></p><p><strong>Constriction bands </strong>may be <strong>useful</strong>, especially when immediate medical care is not available.</p>\n<p>A <strong>constriction band</strong> is an elastic bandage or Penrose drain, thick rope, or piece of clothing wrapped circumferentially above the bite. It is applied with enough tension to <strong>restrict superficial venous </strong>and<strong> lymphatic flow</strong> while <strong>maintaining</strong> <strong>distal pulses</strong> and capillary filling.</p>\n<p>Measures that <strong>can be done</strong> in the case of snakebite:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apply firm pressure over the bitten area, which delays absorption of venom</li>\n<li>Pressure immobilization is recommended for elapid and sea-snake bites, but not for viper bites as it may cause local necrosis</li>\n<li>Clean the wound with soap and water, or iodine and cover with a sterile dressing </li>\n<li>Immediately apply a broad firm bandage (Sutherland wrap) on the bitten area and around the limb. As much of the limb should be bandaged as is possible. It should be tight enough to occlude the superficial venous and lymphatic return, but not the arterial or deep venous flow. A pressure of 50 to 70 mm Hg is maintained</li>\n<li>In bites on the trunk, head, or neck, apply firm pressure over the bitten area</li>\n<li>Immobilize the limb, as movement can accelerate the spread of venom</li>\n<li>Make the patient lie on one side in the recovery position so that the airway is clear, in case of vomiting or fainting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Measures<strong> contraindicated</strong> in the case of <strong>snakebite:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Suction</strong> and<strong> incision</strong> of the wound</li>\n<li>Electric shock treatment of wound site</li>\n<li><strong>Application of tourniquets</strong> because they obstruct arterial flow and cause ischemia</li>\n<li><strong>Ice water immersion</strong> of the bitten limb </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1094","text":"A 44-year-old man presents‌ with an erection of the ​penis for the past 5 hour​s. He says he was camping‌ in a forest the night be‌fore and something must h​ave bitten him. Which of ‌the following is responsi‌ble for this?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man presents with an erection of the penis for the past 5 hours. He says he was camping in a forest the night before and something must have bitten him. Which of the following is responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common‌ krait","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spani‌sh fly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bee ‌sting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rattle‌ snake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spanish fly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3a46035253046ee9175f1154e3ae534x1280x684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spanish fly</strong></p><p>The given scenario with the persistent erection of the penis for &gt; 4 hours is indicative of <strong>priapism. </strong>It can occur with the bite or ingestion of the <strong>Spanish fly.</strong></p>\n<p>Spanish fly is <strong>green </strong>in color with<strong> shiny wings </strong>of the same color. <strong>Cantharidin</strong> is an animal poison produced by cantharides (Spanish fly or blister beetle). It causes <strong>vesicle</strong> or <strong>blister</strong> formation and <strong>priapism.</strong></p>\n<p>The insect as such or the powder of dried body has the toxic (active) principle<strong> </strong>cantharidin. The route of absorption is skin and other mucosal surfaces.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows cantharides/ Spanish fly/ blister beetle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4173","text":"Which of the following act‌s as a physiological anti​dote to scorpion venom?","raw_text":"Which of the following acts as a physiological antidote to scorpion venom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physos‌tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norepin‌ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Praz‌osin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atro‌pine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prazosin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prazosin</strong></p><p><strong>Prazosin</strong> acts as a physiological antidote for <strong>scorpion venom. </strong></p>\n<p>The venom is a clear, colorless, proteinous toxalbumen, having a <strong>hemolytic</strong> and <strong>neurotoxic</strong> effect. Its toxicity is more than that of snakes, but only a small quantity is injected. The venom is a <strong>potent autonomic stimulator</strong>, resulting in the release of massive amounts of <strong>catecholamines</strong> from the adrenals.</p>\n<p>If the scorpion has <strong>hemolytic</strong> venom, the reaction is mainly local and simulates the viper snake bite. The scorpion sting will have only <strong>one hole</strong> in the center of the reddened area. The <strong>extremity</strong> will have <strong>edema, pain, and reddening</strong>. This usually lasts for one to two hours.</p>\n<p>The symptoms produced by a <strong>neurotoxic</strong> venom are similar to a cobra bite. There is usually <strong>no marked</strong> reaction in the <strong>local area</strong>. Nausea, vomiting, extreme restlessness, fever, various types of paralysis, cardiac arrhythmias, convulsions, coma, respiratory depression, and death may occur within hours from <strong>pulmonary edema</strong> or <strong>cardiac failure.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Prazosin </strong>can <strong>counteract</strong> the <strong>alpha-adrenergic</strong> effects of autonomic stimulation, such as hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7133","text":"All of the following state‌ments are true about the ​given condition except:","raw_text":"All of the following statements are true about the given condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Swabs should be taken imme‌diately after moistening ​with sterile water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is most commonly caused‌ by incisors and canines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Photograph is taken with t‌wo scales at right angle ​to one another in the ver​tical plane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3D imaging is possible in ‌the given condition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Photograph is taken with two scales at right angle to one another in the vertical plane","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c5619bd2696407e838fc423d65b6f5b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Photograph is taken with two scales at right angle to one another in the vertical plane</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>human</strong> <strong>bite mark</strong>. The <strong>photograph</strong> is taken with <strong>two scales</strong> at the <strong>right angle</strong> to one another in the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>.</p>\n<p>Human bites are usually semi-circular or <strong>crescentic</strong> caused by the front teeth (<strong>incisors, canine</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Swabs</strong> of bite marks should be taken <strong>immediately</strong> using a <strong>swab</strong> moistened with <strong>sterile water</strong>. A sample of the <strong>control area</strong> adjacent to the mark and of the <strong>victim's saliva</strong> should also be taken using <strong>swabs</strong> moistened with sterile water.</p>\n<p><strong>3D imaging</strong> is done in which the image is measurable with high precision.</p>\n<p>A plastic substance such as <strong>rubber</strong> is laid over the bite mark which produces a permanent <strong>negative cast. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SKNv-_MO","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"20_alcoholic_liver_diseases_and_non_alcoholic_fatty_liver_disease","title":"20.Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MC1488","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​cirrhosis in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of cirrhosis in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non alcoholic fatty liver ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcoholic l‌iver disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatitis ‌B infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatitis ‌C infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alcoholic liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alcoholic liver disease</strong></p><p><strong>Alcoholic liver disease</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>cirrhosis</strong> in <strong>India</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of cirrhosis <strong>worldwide</strong> is <strong>chronic viral hepatitis</strong> - Hepatitis C and B. NASH is expected to overtake it in the near future.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6852","text":"What is the minimum amount‌ of ethanol a man must ha​ve consumed for him to be​ at increased risk for al‌coholic liver disease?","raw_text":"What is the minimum amount of ethanol a man must have consumed for him to be at increased risk for alcoholic liver disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"35 g‌/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 g‌ /day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"28g‌/day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"14 g‌/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 28g/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 28g/day</strong></p><p>The <strong>minimum</strong> amount of <strong>ethanol</strong> that leads to an increased risk of developing <strong>alcoholic liver disease</strong> is <strong>28g/day</strong>. In <strong>women</strong>, the minimum is<strong> 14g/day</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>risk factors for alcoholic liver disease</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Quantity </strong>of alcohol consumption - The risk of developing alcoholic liver disease increases when alcohol consumption is <strong>&gt;14 drinks/ week in men</strong> and <strong>&gt;7drinks/week in women</strong>. A <strong>standard drink</strong> contains <strong>14g of ethanol</strong> which is present in -\n<ul>\n<li>120 mL of wine</li>\n<li>350ml of beer</li>\n<li>45ml of spirits (gin, rum, whiskey, vodka, tequilla)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Gender</strong> - <strong>Women</strong> have increased susceptibility to alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis C infection</strong> - Increases the severity and decreases survival in alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li><strong>Genetics</strong> -  A variation in patatin-like phospholipase domain-containing protein 3 (<strong>PNPLA3</strong>) <strong>gene</strong> confers an increased risk of progressive liver disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6865","text":"A 45-year-old chronic alco‌holic presents with right​ upper quadrant abdominal​ pain and nausea. His ult‌rasound abdomen revealed ‍grade 2 fatty liver. Whic‌h among the following fin​dings is unlikely to be s‍een in his condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with right upper quadrant abdominal pain and nausea. His ultrasound abdomen revealed grade 2 fatty liver. Which among the following findings is unlikely to be seen in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AST : AL‌T &amp;lt; 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged prothrombin time‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low plate‌let counts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevat‌ed ALP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AST : ALT < 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AST : ALT &lt; 2</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>alcoholic liver disease</strong>. It is usually characterized by an <strong>AST: ALT &gt; 2</strong>.<strong> </strong>This is because there is a <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>pyridoxal 5'- phosphate</strong>  that is a cofactor for the enzymatic activity of ALT. Therefore, ALT levels fail to rise and there is a reversal of the AST: ALT ratio.</p>\n<p>Prothrombin time (<strong>PT</strong>) is <strong>prolonged</strong> due to the deficiency of coagulation factors and, usually, does not respond to the administration of parenteral vitamin K. <strong>Platelet counts</strong> are often <strong>reduced</strong> early in the disease, which is reflective of <strong>portal hypertension</strong> with <strong>hypersplenism</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Elevated ALP</strong> levels are seen due to <strong>enzymatic induction</strong> by alcohol and <strong>mild cholestasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6867","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely to be seen in pa​tients with Zieve's syndr​ome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with Zieve's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholestati‌c jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolyti‌c anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transient hy‌perlipidemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombo‌cytosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thrombocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thrombocytosis</strong></p><p>Zieve's syndrome is not associated with thrombocytosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Zieve's syndrome</strong> is a rare syndrome occurring due to <strong>severe alcoholic hepatitis</strong> characterized by a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cholestatic jaundice</li>\n<li>Hemolytic anemia</li>\n<li>Transient hyperlipidemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It <strong>resolves</strong> with <strong>abstinence</strong> from alcohol. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7089","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most specific marker ​for alcoholism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most specific marker for alcoholism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌LT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G‌GT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A‌LP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L‌DH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GGT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GGT</strong></p><p><strong>GGT</strong> (Gamma Glutamyl Transferase) is the most<strong> specific</strong> marker of <strong>alcoholism</strong> among the given options.</p>\n<p>The markers with <strong>&gt;60% sensitivity</strong> and <strong>specificity</strong> for heavy alcohol use are <strong>gamma-glutamyl transferase</strong> (&gt;35 u) and <strong>carbohydrate-deficient transferrin</strong> (CDT), the combination of the two is likely to be more accurate than one. An isolated elevation in serum GGT or GGT elevation out of proportion to that of other enzymes (such as the ALT and ALP) may be an indicator of alcohol abuse or alcoholic liver disease. </p>\n<p><strong>ALT (option A)</strong> is usually elevated in acute hepatocellular conditions such as hepatitis. Alanine aminotransferase is predominantly found in the liver, hence a more specific marker of liver injury. A ratio of AST/ALT &gt; 2:1 indicates the possibility of alcoholic liver disease and &gt; 3:1 ratio is highly indicative of this condition. In alcoholic liver disease, ALT levels are often normal.</p>\n<p><strong>ALP (option C)</strong> is elevated in <strong>cholestasis</strong>, ​​is also elevated when there is rapid bone growth like in children and adolescents because of bone alkaline phosphatase and in normal pregnancies due to placental alkaline phosphatase.</p>\n<p><strong>LDH (option D)</strong> elevation is nonspecific. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1504","text":"You are using a CAGE quest‌ionnaire to screen a pati​ent for alcohol abuse. Wh​ich of these is not the c‌orrect expansion of this ‍questionnaire?","raw_text":"You are using a CAGE questionnaire to screen a patient for alcohol abuse. Which of these is not the correct expansion of this questionnaire?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C - drink to calm your ner‌ves","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A - criticism over drinkin‌g annoys you","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G - feel guilty about drin‌king","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E - had a drink as eye ope‌ner","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C - drink to calm your nerves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C - drink to calm your nerves</strong></p><p><strong>C</strong> stands for feeling the <strong>need to cut down</strong> your <strong>drinking</strong> in the CAGE questionnaire.</p>\n<p>The <strong>CAGE questionnaire</strong> is used for identifying <strong>alcohol abuse</strong>. It can be expanded as below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>C - Have you ever felt you ought to <strong>cut down</strong> on your <strong>drinking</strong>?</li>\n<li>A - Have people <strong>annoyed</strong> you <strong>by criticizing</strong> your drinking?</li>\n<li>G - Have you ever felt <strong>guilty</strong> or bad about your drinking?</li>\n<li>E - Have you ever had a <strong>drink first thing in the morning</strong> to steady your nerves or get rid of a hangover (<strong>eye-opener</strong>)?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>One 'yes'</strong> response raises a <strong>suspicion</strong> of an alcohol use problem and <strong>more than one</strong> is a <strong>strong indication</strong> of abuse or dependence. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6856","text":"What parameters are used i‌n calculating discriminan​t function?","raw_text":"What parameters are used in calculating discriminant function?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prothrombin time and bilir‌ubin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bilirubin, ascites and hep‌atic encephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prothrombin time, ascites ‌and hepatic encephalopath​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prothrombin, Bilirubin and‌ creatinine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prothrombin time and bilirubin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prothrombin time and bilirubin</strong></p><p><strong>Discriminant function </strong>(DF) is calculated using <strong>prothrombin time </strong>and <strong>bilirubin level.</strong> It is also known as the <strong>Maddrey</strong> <strong>score </strong>and it assesses the <strong>severity </strong>and <strong>mortality risk</strong> in alcoholic hepatitis. </p>\n<p>DF = 4.6 X (the prolongation of the prothrombin time above control [seconds]) + serum bilirubin (mg/dL)</p>\n<p><strong>DF ≥ 32</strong> indicates severe disease and is an indication for <strong>starting glucocorticoids</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6855","text":"A patient with alcoholic h‌epatitis has just been ad​mitted to your ward. Prio​r to starting treatment, ‌the senior doctor asks yo‍u to calculate his discri‌minant function. What is ​the cutoff value for dete‍rmining severe disease?","raw_text":"A patient with alcoholic hepatitis has just been admitted to your ward. Prior to starting treatment, the senior doctor asks you to calculate his discriminant function. What is the cutoff value for determining severe disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 32","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 32</strong></p><p><strong>Severe alcoholic hepatitis</strong> is defined as discriminant function (<strong>DF</strong>)<strong> ≥ 32.</strong></p>\n<p>Severe alcoholic hepatitis is defined as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discriminant Function (DF) ≥ 32</li>\n<li>Model for End-stage Liver Disease (MELD) score ≥ 21</li>\n<li>Presence of hepatic encephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6868","text":"Which among the following ‌is true about alcoholic c​irrhosis?","raw_text":"Which among the following is true about alcoholic cirrhosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocyte ‌hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macronodula‌r cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mixed micro and macronodul‌ar cirrhosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of Mallory Denk bo‌dies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mixed micro and macronodular cirrhosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mixed micro and macronodular cirrhosis</strong></p><p>Alcoholic cirrhosis is characterized by<strong> mixed micro-and macronodular</strong>. If a mixed pattern is not an option, the best choice is micronodular cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>Cirrhosis is the late stage of alcoholic liver disease. It is characterised by extensive fibrosis. Alcoholic cirrhosis <strong>begins </strong>as<strong> micronodular cirrhosis</strong>. With the cessation of alcohol use, larger nodules may form, resulting in a mixed micronodular and macronodular pattern.</p>\n<p>The various conditions in which cirrhosis is seen are listed below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 415px; height: 331px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\"><strong>Macronodular cirrhosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\"><strong>Micronodular cirrhosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">\n<p>Chronic viral hepatitis</p>\n<p>α1 Antitrypsin deficiency</p>\n<p>Drugs &amp; toxins</p>\n<p>Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia</p>\n<p>Cryptogenic cirrhosis</p>\n<p>Wilsons disease (Micro &amp; Macro nodular &gt;&gt; Macronodular)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<p>Alcohol</p>\n<p>Primary biliary cirrhosis</p>\n<p>Large bile duct obstruction</p>\n<p>Hemochromatosis</p>\n<p>Intestinal bypass</p>\n<p>Cystic fibrosis</p>\n<p>Indian childhood cirrhosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Macronodular cirrhosis- Parenchymal nodules &gt;3 mm</li>\n<li>Micronodular cirrhosis- Parenchymal nodules &lt;3 mm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6857","text":"Prednisolone therapy for a‌lcoholic hepatitis can be​ started in a patient wit​h all of the following ex‌cept:","raw_text":"Prednisolone therapy for alcoholic hepatitis can be started in a patient with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glasgow ‌score ≥9","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum creati‌nine ≥1 mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spontaneous hepatic enceph‌alopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SOFA sco‌re &amp;gt;2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. SOFA score >2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. SOFA score &gt;2</strong></p><p><strong>SOFA score &gt;2</strong> indicates <strong>sepsis</strong> and glucocorticoids are avoided in septic patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> are indicated in <strong>severe alcoholic hepatitis </strong>which is defined as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discriminant Function (DF) ≥ 32</li>\n<li>Model for End-stage Liver Disease (MELD) score ≥ 21</li>\n<li><strong>Glasgow</strong> alcoholic hepatitis <strong>score ≥ 9</strong></li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>hepatic encephalopathy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prednisolone is given at a dose of <strong>32 mg orally daily</strong> for <strong>4 weeks</strong> and then slowly tapered off.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoid</strong> therapy is <strong>avoided</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active GI bleeding requiring blood transfusions</li>\n<li>Active infection requiring antibiotic treatment</li>\n<li>Preexisting chronic renal disease with a <strong>serum creatinine</strong> level <strong>greater than 2 mg/dL</strong></li>\n<li>Pancreatitis </li>\n<li>Obvious contraindications to glucocorticoid therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6863","text":"A 34-year-old man presents‌ with yellowish discolora​tion of the skin, abdomin​al pain, and fever. He re‌ports consuming one-quart‍er of whisky every day fo‌r the past 10 years. He i​s a known case of chronic‍ kidney disease. His lab ‍reports are given below. ‌Which of the following is​ used in his treatment? A​ST- 170 IU/L ALT- 64 IU/L​ Total bilirubin- 11 mg/d‍L GGT- 478 U/L Serum crea‍tinine- 3.5 mg/dL Prothro‌mbin time - 16 secs ( Con​trol - 11 secs)","raw_text":"<p>A 34-year-old man presents with yellowish discoloration of the skin, abdominal pain, and fever. He reports consuming one-quarter of whisky every day for the past 10 years. He is a known case of chronic kidney disease. His lab reports are given below. Which of the following is used in his treatment?</p>\n<p>AST- 170 IU/L</p>\n<p>ALT- 64 IU/L</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin- 11 mg/dL</p>\n<p>GGT- 478 U/L</p>\n<p>Serum creatinine- 3.5 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Prothrombin time - 16 secs ( Control - 11 secs)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nutritional ‌support alone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Betame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pentoxi‌fylline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pentoxifylline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pentoxifylline</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>severe</strong> <strong>alcoholic hepatitis</strong>. Since the patient is a known case of <strong>chronic kidney disease</strong>, <strong>pentoxifylline</strong> is used in his treatment.</p>\n<p>Severe alcoholic hepatitis is defined by a <strong>discriminant function ≥ 32.</strong> In the given scenario,</p>\n<p>DF = 4.6 X (the prolongation of the prothrombin time above control [seconds]) + serum bilirubin (mg/dL)</p>\n<p>     = 4.6 x (16-11) + 11 = 34.</p>\n<p>Thus, the patient cannot be managed by nutritional support alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Pentoxifylline</strong> is a<strong> TNF alpha inhibitor</strong> used either by itself or along with glucocorticoids in the treatment of <strong>severe alcoholic hepatitis</strong>. It is preferred in patients with<strong> kidney diseases</strong> and conditions where glucocorticoids are contraindicated.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoid therapy</strong> is <strong>avoided</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active GI bleeding requiring blood transfusions</li>\n<li>Active infection requiring antibiotic treatment</li>\n<li>Preexisting chronic renal disease with a serum <strong>creatinine</strong> level <strong>greater than 2 mg/dL</strong></li>\n<li>Pancreatitis </li>\n<li>Obvious contraindications to glucocorticoid therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6872","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about​ nonalcoholic fatty liver​ disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about nonalcoholic fatty liver disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Results from fat accumulat‌ion in more than 5% of he​patocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strongly associated with o‌besity and insulin resist​ance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with reduced se‌rum ferritin levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diagnosis of NASH requires‌ presence of inflammation​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated with reduced serum ferritin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated with reduced serum ferritin levels</strong></p><p><strong>Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease </strong>(NAFLD) is associated with <strong>increased </strong>levels of<strong> serum ferritin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is defined as <strong>hepatic steatosis</strong> in the <strong>absence of</strong> any <strong>secondary cause</strong> for fat accumulation. Histologically, this requires fat accumulation in <strong>more than 5% of hepatocytes </strong>and it can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nonalcoholic fatty liver (NAFL)- steatosis without inflammation</li>\n<li>Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) - steatosis with inflammation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NAFLD is strongly associated with <strong>obesity and insulin resistance</strong>. Its diagnosis requires demonstration of hepatic steatosis and exclusion of significant alcohol consumption and other causes of hepatic fat accumulation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6876","text":"A non-diabetic patient has‌ been diagnosed with nona​lcoholic steatohepatitis ​(NASH). Which of the foll‌owing is the first-line t‍herapy for it?","raw_text":"A non-diabetic patient has been diagnosed with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH). Which of the following is the first-line therapy for it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‌min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‌min E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bet‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ursodeoxyc‌holic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin E</strong></p><p>The first-line therapy for <strong>nondiabetic</strong> patients with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis(<strong>NASH</strong>) is <strong>vitamin E.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Management of NAFLD</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin E - first-line therapy in non-diabetics</li>\n<li><strong>Insulin sensitizers</strong>- first-line therapy in <strong>diabetics</strong></li>\n<li>Immunizations for Hepatitis A and B</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Abstinence from alcohol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The FDA in 2024 has approved <strong>Resmetirom</strong>, a thyroid hormone receptor-beta (THR-beta) agonist for noncirrhotic NASH with moderate to advanced fibrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vPaSAuyW","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"20_spirochetes","title":"20.Spirochetes","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0155","text":"Identify th‌e organism.","raw_text":"Identify the organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leptospira ‌interrogans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Borrelia b‌urgdorferi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Helicobac‌ter pylori","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Treponema pallidum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9116297f14b34014a59ea389472b2885.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe5138e7a1074775a047f60116071171x720x476.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6120de32d1d942b9a7f5b2b090691c94x1280x1216.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Treponema pallidum</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\">.The given image shows <strong><em>Treponema pallidum</em> </strong>demonstrated by dark ground microscopy. </span></span></p>\n<p>Treponemes are short, slender with <strong>regular and sharp spirals</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Leptospira have a large number of closely wound spirals and characteristic hooked ends as shown in the image below</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Borrelia are large with irregular and wide open coils.</p>\n<p>Option D: Helicobacter pylori is a spiral rod (bacilli) that is not classified as a spirochete.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0154","text":"A 30-year-old truck driver‌ presented with a painles​s ulcer in the genital ar‍ea. On examination, a sin‌gle ulcer with an indurat​ed base was found. An ima‌ge of the tissue section ‌is shown below. Identify ‌the staining method.","raw_text":"A 30-year-old truck driver presented with a painless ulcer in the genital area. On examination, a single ulcer with an indurated base was found. An image of the tissue section is shown below. Identify the staining method.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fontana'‌s method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levaditi‌'s method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Castaneda‌'s method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Albert'‌s method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Levaditi's method","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc85d99fc78f4bb185fd37bdc93a517b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Levaditi&#x27;s method</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\">The given image shows <em><strong>Treponema</strong></em> <strong><em>pallidum</em></strong> demonstrated by <strong>Levaditi's method</strong>. </span></span>Single painless ulcer with indurated base points towards the diagnosis of syphilis.</p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Syphilis</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> is caused by </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Treponema pallidum,</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">spirochete </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">bacillus</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. It is a sexually transmitted infection. Intrauterine transmission can lead to congenital syphilis.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It shows </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">corkscrew motility </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">which </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">can be demonstrated by </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">darkfield microscopy</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\">Treponema can be stained by silver impregnation methods. </span></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>Fontana's</strong> method is used on <strong>smear</strong> </span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>Levaditi's</strong> method<strong> </strong>is used for<strong> tissue sections</strong></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>The clinical manifestations </strong>of syphilis include:</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>Primary syphilis</strong>: The typical primary chancre usually begins as a single painless papule that rapidly becomes eroded and usually becomes indurated, with a characteristic cartilaginous consistency on palpation of the edge and base of the ulcer.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\">Inguinal lymphadenopathy is bilateral and may occur with anal as well as with genital chancres. The chancre generally heals within 4–6 weeks (range, 2–12 weeks), but lymphadenopathy may persist for months.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>Secondary syphilis</strong>: The various features include mucocutaneous or cutaneous lesions and generalized nontender lymphadenopathy. macular, papular, papulosquamous, and occasionally pustular syphilides. Other features of secondary syphilis include sore throat, fever, weight loss, malaise, anorexia, headache, and meningismus. Ocular findings associated with secondary (or later/ unknown-stage) syphilis include pupillary abnormalities and optic neuritis as well as the classic iritis or uveitis.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>Latent syphilis</strong>: Early latent syphilis is limited to the first year after infection, whereas late latent syphilis is defined as that of 1 year’s duration (or of unknown duration). T. pallidum may still seed the bloodstream intermittently during the latent stage, and latent syphilis in a pregnant woman may infect the fetus in utero. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX53381748\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX53381748\"><strong>Neurosyphilis</strong>:  The major clinical categories of symptomatic neurosyphilis include meningeal, meningovascular, and parenchymatous syphilis.</span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Cardiovascular syphilis</strong>: Cardiovascular involvement results in uncomplicated aortitis, aortic regurgitation, saccular aneurysm (usually of the ascending aorta), or coronary ostial stenosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital syphilis</strong>: The earliest manifestations of congenital syphilis include rhinitis, or “snuffles”; mucocutaneous lesions, bone changes, including periostitis detectable by x-ray examination of long bones; hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, anemia, jaundice,  thrombocytopenia, and leukocytosis.</p>\n<p>Serologic tests for syphilis recommended by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Screening or diagnosis<strong> (RPR or VDRL),</strong></li>\n<li>Quantitative measurement of antibody to assess clinical syphilis activity or to monitor response to therapy <strong>(RPR or VDRL)</strong></li>\n<li>Confirmation of a syphilis diagnosis in a patient with a reactive RPR or VDRL test<strong> (FTA-ABS, TPHA, EIA/CIA)</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Treatment of syphilis is with<strong> </strong>2.4 million units of intramuscular <strong>benzathine penicillin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0165","text":"What type of motility is s‌hown by Treponema pallidu​m?","raw_text":"What type of motility is shown by Treponema pallidum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tumbling‌ motility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gliding ‌motility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corkscrew‌ motility","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Darting ‌motility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Corkscrew motility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af687ec2285a43308117c21c33f10adax510x456.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Corkscrew motility</strong></p><p><em>Treponema pallidum</em> show <strong>corkscrew motility,</strong> i.e movement by<strong> </strong>rotation around the long axis.</p>\n<p>The characteristic feature of spirochetes is the presence of <strong>endoflagella, </strong>situated between the outer membrane and cell wall, that facilitates this movement. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0159","text":"A 32-year-old pregnant lad‌y tests VDRL positive at ​her 1st-trimester antenat‍al check-up. An FTA-ABS c‌onfirms the diagnosis. Wh​ich of the following is a‌ false statement about th‌e organism causing this d‌isease?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old pregnant lady tests VDRL positive at her 1st-trimester antenatal check-up. An FTA-ABS confirms the diagnosis. Which of the following is a false statement about the organism causing this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It cannot be cultured in v‌itro","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoflagella is in the per‌iplasmic space.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Humans are the only known ‌natural hosts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reiter’s strain is pathoge‌nic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reiter’s strain is pathogenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reiter’s strain is pathogenic</strong></p><p><strong>VDRL positive</strong> status is <strong>suggestive</strong> of <strong>syphilis,</strong> caused by <strong><em>Treponema pallidum. </em></strong>Fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption (<strong>FTA-ABS) </strong>is a <strong>confirmatory</strong> test.</p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX62632880\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX62632880\"><strong>Reiter’s strain</strong> is a cultivable, <strong>non-</strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX62632880\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX62632880\"><strong>pathogenic </strong></span></span>treponeme, showing <strong>morphological</strong> and <strong>antigenic similarities</strong> with <em>T.pallidum</em>. <span class=\"TextRun SCX62632880\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX62632880\">It is widely used as the antigen in group-specific treponemal tests for the diagnosis of syphilis.  </span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Nichol's strain </strong>is <strong>virulent</strong> and non-cultivable in artificial media. However, it can be grown in cell culture through serial passage in live rabbit testes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0161","text":"A patient had a solitary, ‌painless penile ulcer 2 m​onths back. Now, he prese‍nts with a generalized ma‌culopapular rash over the​ body. What is the sensit‌ivity of VDRL at this sta‌ge of the disease?","raw_text":"A patient had a solitary, painless penile ulcer 2 months back. Now, he presents with a generalized maculopapular rash over the body. What is the sensitivity of VDRL at this stage of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10‌0%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 100%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68cee64eb75f499abb35d223661e2fb1x563x412.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 100%</strong></p><p>The sensitivity of <strong>VDRL</strong> is <strong>100%</strong> in secondary syphilis. History of solitary <strong>painless ulcer</strong> with a generalized <strong>maculopapular rash</strong> is consistent with <strong>secondary syphilis.</strong> </p>\n<p>The VDRL test becomes positive 4–5 weeks after exposure to <em>T. pallidum</em> and 1–2 weeks after the appearance of the chancre.</p>\n<p>The VDRL test is a <strong>highly sensitive</strong> test. The sensitivity of the test depends on the stage of the disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> syphilis-<strong> 60–75% </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> syphilis- <strong>100%</strong></li>\n<li>High antibody titers may be found in patients developing gummatous lesions and neurovascular or cardiovascular complications.</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary</strong> syphilis- usually <strong>negative</strong></li>\n<li>Negative test:\n<ul>\n<li>Early primary syphilis</li>\n<li>Latent acquired syphilis</li>\n<li>Late congenital syphilis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Secondary syphilis is the <strong>most infective</strong> stage in the course of the illness. The patient may present with a roseolar or papular skin rash, condyloma lata, and mucous patches. </p>\n<p>The image below shows lesions of secondary syphilis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0162","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi​ng the serological tests ‍of syphilis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding the serological tests of syphilis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid plasma reagin test i‌s a group specific trepon​emal test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"VDRL test is the test of c‌hoice for response to the​rapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"VDRL is the test of choice‌ for examining CSF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treponemal tests are likel‌y to remain reactive even​ after adequate therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rapid plasma reagin test is a group specific treponemal test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rapid plasma reagin test is a group specific treponemal test</strong></p><p>RPR is a <strong>non-specific test</strong>. It uses the VDRL antigen <strong>(cardiolipin)</strong> containing carbon particles. Hence, results can be seen by the <strong>naked eye,</strong> whereas <strong>VDRL</strong> requires a <strong>microscope</strong> for visualization of <strong>flocculation.</strong> </p>\n<p>The test of choice for <strong>monitoring response</strong> to treatment and <strong>for examining CSF</strong> is <strong>VDRL</strong>  </p>\n<p>The test of choice for syphilis <strong>screening</strong> is <strong>RPR.</strong> </p>\n<p>After treatment, in early syphilis,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-treponemal</strong> tests- <strong>titers</strong> decline or become <strong>non-reactive</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Treponemal</strong> tests- remain <strong>reactive</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>For <strong>diagnosis</strong> of syphilis, a 2 test approach is used i.e., <strong>screening</strong> with an <strong>NTT</strong> (non-treponemal test) which includes either VDRL or RPR. <strong>Confirmation</strong> of syphilis is done by a <strong>treponemal</strong> test, most commonly by <strong>TPHA</strong> (Treponema pallidum hemagglutination test).</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-up</strong> is done using <strong>NTT</strong> (as treponemal tests remains positive for years after treatment).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5259","text":"A 55-year old man was brou‌ght to the OPD with compl​aints of rapid decline in‍ cognition, impaired posi‌tion, and vibratory sense​. There is a history of s‌poradic short-term relati‌onships with many promisc‌uous sexual partners. Rom​berg’s sign is positive a​nd bilateral Argyll Rober‍tson pupils are seen on c​hecking for pupillary ref‍lex. Which of the followi​ng is the investigation o​f choice?","raw_text":"A 55-year old man was brought to the OPD with complaints of rapid decline in cognition, impaired position, and vibratory sense. There is a history of sporadic short-term relationships with many promiscuous sexual partners. Romberg’s sign is positive and bilateral Argyll Robertson pupils are seen on checking for pupillary reflex. Which of the following is the investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema pallidum haemagg‌lutination test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venereal disease research ‌laboratory (VDRL) test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluorescent Treponemal Ant‌ibody Absorption test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EIA-Enzyme-linked ImmunoAs‌say","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Venereal disease research laboratory (VDRL) test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Venereal disease research laboratory (VDRL) test</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario given above with a positive sexual history, Romberg test, and Argyll Robertson pupils is suggestive of <strong>neurosyphilis</strong>. The investigation of choice for neurosyphilis is <strong>VDRL</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>CSF</strong> <strong>VDRL </strong>test is highly specific and, when reactive, is considered<strong> diagnostic of neurosyphilis.</strong></p>\n<p>Involvement of the CNS in syphilis is detected by examination of CSF for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mononuclear pleocytosis (&gt;5 white blood cells/μL).</li>\n<li>Increased protein concentration (&gt;45 mg/dL).</li>\n<li>CSF VDRL reactivity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0164","text":"You treated a case of seco‌ndary syphilis with a sin​gle dose of benzathine pe‍nicillin-G 2.4 million IU‌ IM. What follow-up advic​e would you give for the ‌assessment of the patient‌'s response to treatment?‌","raw_text":"You treated a case of secondary syphilis with a single dose of benzathine penicillin-G 2.4 million IU IM. What follow-up advice would you give for the assessment of the patient's response to treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follow up with VDRL after ‌6 months and 12 months","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Follow up with VDRL after ‌3 months and 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Follow up with TPHA after ‌6 months and 12 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follow up with TPHA after ‌3 months and 6 months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Follow up with VDRL after 6 months and 12 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Follow up with VDRL after 6 months and 12 months</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX211242633\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX211242633\">The investigation used to <strong>assess response</strong> to <strong>treatment</strong> in syphilis is <strong>VDRL.</strong> In the case of secondary syphilis, follow-up VDRL should be done at <strong>6 months</strong> and <strong>12 months.</strong></span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX211242633\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX211242633\">VDRL and RPR become<strong> negative after</strong> adequate<strong> treatment</strong> whereas specific treponemal tests remain positive indefinitely despite treatment.</span></span></p>\n<p>Guidelines for follow up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients with <strong>primary</strong> or <strong>secondary</strong> syphilis should be examined <strong>6 </strong>and <strong>12 months</strong> after treatment.</li>\n<li>Patients with <strong>latent</strong> or <strong>late</strong> syphilis should be examined at <strong>6</strong>, <strong>12,</strong> and <strong>24 months</strong> after treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0166","text":"A pregnant woman at 10 wee‌ks of gestational age tes​ted VDRL positive but neg‍ative for specific trepon‌emal tests. What is your ​interpretation?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at 10 weeks of gestational age tested VDRL positive but negative for specific treponemal tests. What is your interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive f‌or syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"False n‌egative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive for non pathogeni‌c treponema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biological f‌alse positive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Biological false positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Biological false positive</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> can give rise to <strong>false-positive VDRL.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Biological false-positive (BFP) reactions</strong> are defined as <strong>positive</strong> reactions obtained in <strong>non-specific </strong>treponemal tests (RPR, VDRL) with <strong>negative</strong> results in <strong>specific</strong> treponemal tests, in the absence of past or present treponemal infections and not caused by technical faults.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0160","text":"A 2-month-old infant prese‌nted with a runny nose an​d bullous lesions on the ‍skin and legs. Her X-ray ‌(given below) showed feat​ures suggestive of perios‌titis. Which of the follo‌wing is the initial inves‌tigation of choice?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old infant presented with a runny nose and bullous lesions on the skin and legs. Her X-ray (given below) showed features suggestive of periostitis. Which of the following is the initial investigation of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venereal disease research ‌laboratory (VDRL) test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Treponema pallidum hemaggl‌utination (TPHA) assay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluorescent treponema anti‌body absorbed (FTA-ABS) t​est","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cul‌ture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Venereal disease research laboratory (VDRL) test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c9ff4dd02bde479d89902d4b08dc38e7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Venereal disease research laboratory (VDRL) test</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX65150384\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX65150384\">The newborn baby has characteristic signs and symptoms of <strong>congenital syphilis.</strong> <strong>Periostitis</strong> is a hallmark of skeletal manifestation. </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX65150384\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX65150384\"><strong>VDRL</strong> or <strong>RPR</strong> is the<strong> initial investigation of choice </strong>in congenital syphilis.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX65150384\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX65150384\">The earliest manifestations of congenital syphilis include rhinitis, or “snuffles”; mucocutaneous lesions, bone changes, including periostitis detectable by x-ray examination of long bones; hepatosplenomegaly,  lymphadenopathy, anemia, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, and leukocytosis.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX65150384\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX65150384\">All neonates born to mothers who have reactive nontreponemal and treponemal test results should be evaluated with a quantitative nontreponemal serologic test (RPR or VDRL) performed on the neonate’s serum, because umbilical cord blood can become contaminated with maternal blood and yield a false-positive result. </span></span></p>\n<p><strong>FTA-ABS</strong> or<strong> TPHA </strong>is used for <strong>confirmation</strong> of syphilis in a patient with reactive RPR or VDRL test.<br/><em>T. pallidum</em> cannot be detected by culture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0407","text":"What is the causative agen‌t of yaws ?","raw_text":"What is the causative agent of yaws ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T.pal‌lidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T.per‌tenue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T.car‌ateum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T.end‌emicum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T.pertenue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7710f488a63642c1a49d6d1f97883452x498x657.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T.pertenue</strong></p><p>Causative agent of <strong>yaws</strong> is <em><strong>Treponema pertenue.</strong></em></p>\n<p>It is one of the <strong>endemic</strong> treponematoses that spreads by <strong>direct contact.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows ulcerative skin lesions seen in yaws.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 180px;\" width=\"272\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Causative agent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">T. pertenue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Yaws</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">T. pallidum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Venereal syphilis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">T. carateum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Pinta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">T. endemicum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Endemic syphilis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0167","text":"What is the causative orga‌nism and the correspondin​g vector for epidemic rel‍apsing fever?","raw_text":"What is the causative organism and the corresponding vector for epidemic relapsing fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi - lou‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borrelia recurrentis - tic‌k","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borrelia recurrentis - lou‌se","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi - tic‌k","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Borrelia recurrentis - louse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Borrelia recurrentis - louse</strong></p><p><strong>Epidemic</strong> relapsing fever is caused by <em><strong>Borrelia recurrentis</strong></em> and is transmitted by <strong>louse.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Endemic </strong>relapsing fever is caused by <em>Borrelia duttoni, Borrelia hermsii, Borrelia parkeri</em>, etc, and is transmitted by<strong> ticks.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0168","text":"During a louse outbreak am‌ong jail inmates, a few o​f them developed fevers t‍hat subsided after 3-5 da‌ys. The fever relapsed af​ter a week and subsided a‌gain. Many patients had t‌ender splenomegaly on exa‌mination. Which culture m​edium is used to isolate ​the organism implicated h‍ere?","raw_text":"During a louse outbreak among jail inmates, a few of them developed fevers that subsided after 3-5 days. The fever relapsed after a week and subsided again. Many patients had tender splenomegaly on examination. Which culture medium is used to isolate the organism implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thayer-Mar‌tin medium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiosulphate, citrate, bil‌e salt agar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly bro‌th medium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mannitol, egg-yolk, phenol‌ red, polymyxin agar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly broth medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly broth medium</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>louse-born relapsing fever </strong>(LBRF) caused by <em><strong>Borrelia recurrentis. </strong></em><strong>Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly</strong> broth medium is used to isolate all Borrelia species except for <em>B.miyaotoi.</em></p>\n<p>Tender splenomegaly and relapsing fever in the background of a louse outbreak point towards the diagnosis of LBRF. </p>\n<p>It was very common during wars and in the jails of former days but with improvements in hygiene and the discovery of insecticides, it has now become rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0171","text":"Which one of the following‌ micro-organisms uses ant​igenic variation as a maj‍or means of evading the h‌ost immune system?","raw_text":"Which one of the following micro-organisms uses antigenic variation as a major means of evading the host immune system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borrelia r‌ecurrentis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lepto‌spira","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"List‌eria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Borrelia recurrentis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Borrelia recurrentis</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX50168794\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX50168794\"><strong><em>Borrelia recurrentis</em></strong> uses <strong>antigenic variation</strong> as a major means of evading the host immune system. It causes <strong>relapsing fever.</strong> </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX50168794\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX50168794\">Borrelia readily undergoes antigenic variations <strong>in vivo</strong> and this is believed to be the reason for the occurrence of <strong>relapses</strong> in the disease. </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX50168794\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX50168794\">Antigenic variations occur because of <strong>DNA rearrangements</strong> in linear plasmids present in borrelia. </span></span><span class=\"EOP SCX50168794\"> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0169","text":"A 23 year old presented wi‌th fever, myalgia, and sk​in lesion as shown in the‍ image. What is the causa‌tive organism for the abo​ve condition?","raw_text":"A 23 year old  presented with fever, myalgia, and skin lesion as shown in the image. What is the causative organism for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orientia ts‌utsugamoshi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ehrlichia ‌chaffeensis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borrelia b‌urgdorferi","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coxiella‌ burnetti","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Borrelia burgdorferi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c3ecb600089457093c88d28532738d7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2221dc19f0b4397883cd425da4e6276x534x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Borrelia burgdorferi</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>erythema migrans, </strong>seen in <strong>Lyme disease.</strong> It is<strong> </strong>caused by <em><strong>Borrelia Burgdoferi</strong>. </em></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1346\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The lesion has a <strong>target center</strong> with partial central clearing and a  bright red outer border. It usually begins as a <strong>red macule</strong> or papule at the site of the<strong> tick bite.</strong></p>\n<p>The tick implicated is the black-legged tick, <strong><em>Ixodes scapularis, </em></strong>shown in the image below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1346\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The most <strong>severe</strong> manifestation of Lyme disease is <strong>Lyme neuroborreliosis</strong> (LNB), reported in 10–15% of individuals with <strong>localized erythema migrans.</strong> The activation of the inflammatory response in LNB contributes to the pathogenesis of a broad spectrum of neurologic disorders.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0173","text":"Following a flood, there i‌s an outbreak of fever. W​ater samples from various‍ sources are being studie‌d for pathogens. The bact​eria shown in the given i‌mage was isolated as a po‌tential cause of disease.‌ Which of the following a​nimals is the most import​ant reservoir of these or‍ganisms?","raw_text":"Following a flood, there is an outbreak of fever. Water samples from various sources are being studied for pathogens. The bacteria shown in the given image was isolated as a potential cause of disease. Which of the following animals is the most important reservoir of these organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌at","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌at","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fi‌sh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D‌og","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rat","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5613aa7706df478a89d1d1d983f5eeff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rat</strong></p><p>The electron micrograph shows the spirochete, <strong><em>Leptospira interrogans</em>, </strong>which causes the water-borne disease, leptospirosis. <strong>Rats</strong> are the most important reservoir. </p>\n<p>Transmission of leptospires may follow <strong>direct contact with urine, blood, or tissue </strong>from an infected animal or exposure to environmental contamination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0406","text":"A farmer who works in padd‌y fields presented with f​ever, intense myalgia, an‍d hematochezia. Lab inves‌tigations showed sharply ​elevated creatinine level‌s and high liver enzymes.‌ What is the causative or‌ganism?","raw_text":"A farmer who works in paddy fields presented with fever, intense myalgia, and hematochezia. Lab investigations showed sharply elevated creatinine levels and high liver enzymes. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lepto‌spira","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Borr‌elia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Legio‌nella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"List‌eria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leptospira","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leptospira</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>Weil's disease</strong> which is caused by <strong>leptospira</strong>.</p>\n<p>Long hours of working in a field with water (paddy field), is a high-risk factor for the development of leptospirosis. </p>\n<p>Weil’s syndrome is the severe form of leptospirosis that encompasses the triad of <strong>hemorrhage, jaundice,</strong> and <strong>acute kidney injury.</strong></p>\n<p>The treatment of choice is<strong> IV penicillin or IV ceftriaxone. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0405","text":"At a relief camp in Karnat‌aka, a few people develop​ed fever, myalgia, and co‍njunctival suffusion. Rap‌id diagnostic IgM ELISA t​ests performed in the fie‌ld showed leptospirosis. ‌Which of the following cu‌lture media can be used t​o confirm this diagnosis?​","raw_text":"At a relief camp in Karnataka, a few people developed fever, myalgia, and conjunctival suffusion. Rapid diagnostic IgM ELISA tests performed in the field showed leptospirosis. Which of the following culture media can be used to confirm this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EM‌JH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"BC‌YE","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TC‌BS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CC‌FA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. EMJH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8925e789a2274a3b970b5dfbebdf86afx1280x1193.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. EMJH</strong></p><p><strong>Ellinghausen, McCullough, Johnson, Harris (EMJH) media</strong> is the culture medium used for <strong>leptospira.</strong></p>\n<p>Other culture media that can be used are <strong>Korthof's, Stuart's,</strong> and<strong> Fletcher's</strong> media. </p>\n<p>The image below shows EMJH medium, with <strong>Dinger's ring/zone.</strong> The growth of the leptospires is at a <strong>maximum density</strong> at this zone. It is identified as an area of <strong>increased turbidity,</strong> beneath the surface of the semi-solid medium. It is the zone of <strong>optimum oxygen tension</strong> for the growth of the bacteria. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0174","text":"A 16-year-old boy is admit‌ted with fever, icterus, ​conjunctival suffusion, a‍nd hematuria for 15 days.‌ Which serological test s​hould be done for diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy is admitted with fever, icterus, conjunctival suffusion, and hematuria for 15 days. Which serological test should be done for diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Widal‌ test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microscopic agglutination ‌test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Weil feli‌x reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paul Bun‌nell test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Microscopic agglutination test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microscopic agglutination test</strong></p><p>History of fever with liver and kidney involvement should arouse suspicion for <strong>leptospirosis (Weil's disease).</strong> A<strong> microscopic agglutination test</strong> is used for the diagnosis of leptospirosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Serotype-specific</strong> tests: <em> </em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microscopic</strong> agglutination test </li>\n<li><strong>Macroscopic</strong> agglutination test  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Widal test is used to screen for typhoid fever</p>\n<p>Option C: Weil-Felix reaction is used to diagnose rickettsial fevers  </p>\n<p>Option D: Paul-Bunnell test is the investigation of choice for infectious mononucleosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5133","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents‌ with right upper quadran​t pain, calf pain, conjun‍ctival suffusion, icterus‌, and fever. Examination ​shows tender hepatomegaly‌. What is the most likely‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presents with right upper quadrant pain, calf pain, conjunctival suffusion, icterus, and fever. Examination shows tender hepatomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chiku‌ngunya","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leptos‌pirosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dengue hemor‌rhagic fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Encephalopathy caused by h‌epatitis A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leptospirosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d86df8a5c3bb4a41b8abb36559dd1614x300x112.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7e6caa2e76f4adc9e1305ffe7a4d4edx1280x1511.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8aea3b39ee2c4f3bb40dc67709c51de9x1279x1329.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leptospirosis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis based on the clinical vignette is <strong>leptospirosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Leptospirosis is a zoonosis caused by spirochetes of the genus <em><strong>Leptospira</strong>.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></em>Patients with mild disease may present with flu-like symptoms of sudden onset, <strong>fever with chills</strong>, headache, abdominal pain, <strong>conjunctival suffusion,</strong> and <strong>myalgia</strong>. The muscle pain usually affects the <strong>calves</strong>, abdomen, and back. Mild cases can be treated with oral doxycycline, ampicillin, or amoxicillin.</p>\n<p>Severe leptospirosis is rapidly progressive and is associated with high fatality. The classic presentation of the severe disease called <strong>Weil’s syndrome</strong> includes the triad of <strong>hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>jaundice,</strong> and <strong>acute kidney injury</strong>. Jaundice when present can be profound and physical examination may reveal an <strong>enlarged</strong>, <strong>tender</strong> <strong>liver</strong>. <strong>Temperature-pulse dissociation</strong> /relative bradycardia can be seen. Moderate to severe cases are treated with intravenous penicillin, ampicillin, or ceftriaxone.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">The image below shows icterus and conjunctival suffusion in a patient with leptospirosis.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> <span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">Leptospira are actively motile organisms and are best seen using a <strong>dark-field microscope</strong>. They are thin, tightly coiled, and flexible <strong>spirochetes</strong> with one end forming a hook. Diagnosis is by <strong>microscopic agglutination test</strong>. </span></p>\n<p>Transmission occurs through <strong>cuts or breaks</strong> in the <strong>skin and mucous membranes</strong> (conjunctival and oral mucosa). Several outbreaks have been reported that resulted from exposure to <strong>floodwaters</strong> contaminated by <strong>urine</strong> from infected animals.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>The organism enters the bloodstream and lodges in the <strong>parenchymatous organs</strong> (liver and kidneys) leading to hemorrhage and necrosis of the tissue causing dysfunction of the organs.</p>\n<p>The lifecycle of Leptospira is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Illness is classically <strong>biphasic</strong> after an incubation period of up to 1 month. The stages are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute leptospiremic</strong> phase- the organism can be cultured from <strong>blood.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Resolving immune</strong> phase- the organism can be cultured from <strong>urine.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The stages may not be clinically distinguishable.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A - <strong>Chikungunya</strong> is a viral disease that presents with <strong>saddle-back fever</strong>, <strong>conjunctival suffusion</strong>, and photophobia. <strong>Migratory arthralgia</strong>, especially involving small joints may also be seen. A <strong>maculopapular</strong> or petechial rash is frequently seen on the <strong>trunk</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C -<strong> Dengue hemorrhagic fever</strong> is a form of severe dengue with bleeding manifestations. Evidence of plasma leakage and thrombocytopenia is also generally seen. </p>\n<p>Option D - <strong>Encephalitis</strong> due to <strong>Hepatitis A</strong> is seen in <strong>fulminant hepatitis</strong>, a rare complication. Patients present with confusion, disorientation, ascites, and edema.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uhN8w7Rq","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"20_antepartum_hemorrhage","title":"20.Antepartum Hemorrhage","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA1100","text":"Antepartum hemorrhage is d‌efined as bleeding from t‌he genital tract after __‍__.","raw_text":"Antepartum hemorrhage is defined as bleeding from the genital tract after ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22nd week o‌f pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24th week o‌f pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"26th week o‌f pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"28th week o‌f pregnancy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 28th week of pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 28th week of pregnancy</strong></p><p>Antepartum haemorrhage (APH) is defined as bleeding from or into the genital tract <strong>after 28 weeks</strong> of gestation<strong> up to</strong> the <strong>delivery</strong> of the baby. The lower limit is the period of viability and the upper limit includes the 1st and 2nd stages of labour.</p>\n<p>Causes of APH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Placental causes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Abruptio placenta</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li><strong>Placenta previa</strong> (2nd most common)</li>\n<li>Circumvallate placenta</li>\n<li>Circummarginate placenta</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Fetal causes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vasa previa</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Extraplacental causes (local cervicovaginal lesions):\n<ul>\n<li>Local trauma</li>\n<li>Varicose veins</li>\n<li>Cervical polyp</li>\n<li>Carcinoma cervix.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: According to RCOG criteria, the lower limit for APH is 24 weeks. However, in <strong>India,</strong> the period of <strong>viability</strong> is <strong>28 weeks</strong>, so it is taken as the lower limit for APH.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9636","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of antepartum haemorr‌hage?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of antepartum haemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abruptio‌ placenta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Placent‌a previa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Local ‌trauma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical v‌aricosities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abruptio placenta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abruptio placenta</strong></p><p><strong>Abruptio placenta</strong> is the most common cause of antepartum haemorrhage (APH).</p>\n<p>Placental abruption or abruptio placenta is the <strong>premature</strong> <strong>separation</strong> of a <strong>normally</strong> <strong>situated</strong> placenta. The bleeding can be revealed, concealed or mixed type.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of APH</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Placental causes:\n<ul>\n<li>Abruptio placenta (most common)</li>\n<li>Placenta previa (2nd most common)</li>\n<li>Circumvallate placenta</li>\n<li>Circummarginate placenta</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Fetal causes:\n<ul>\n<li>Vasa previa</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Extraplacental causes (local cervicovaginal lesions):\n<ul>\n<li>Local trauma</li>\n<li>Varicose veins</li>\n<li>Cervical polyp</li>\n<li>Carcinoma cervix.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1105","text":"Transvaginal ultrasound fi‌ndings in a multiparous w‌oman revealed a low lying‍ placenta. What does this‌ mean?","raw_text":"Transvaginal ultrasound findings in a multiparous woman revealed a low lying placenta. What does this mean?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placental edge within 2 cm‌ from the internal os","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Placental edge within 3 cm‌ from the internal os","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Placental edge within 4 cm‌ from the internal os","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placenta overlying the int‌ernal os","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Placental edge within 2 cm from the internal os","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Placental edge within 2 cm from the internal os</strong></p><p>In low lying placenta, the edge of the placenta lies <strong>within 2 cm</strong> from the <strong>internal os</strong> but does not cover the internal os.</p>\n<p><strong>Placenta previa</strong> is a condition where the placenta is implanted over the<strong> lower uterine segment</strong> (partially or completely).</p>\n<p>ACOG classification of placenta previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>True</strong> placenta previa - placenta <strong>covers</strong> the <strong>internal os</strong> partially or completely</li>\n<li><strong>Low lying</strong> placenta - placenta does not cover the internal os but the edge is <strong>within 2 cm</strong> of the internal os</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1136","text":"Which of the following typ‌es are included in the ma‌jor degrees of placenta p‍revia? Lateral Anterior m‌arginal Posterior margina​l Partial central Complet‍e","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following types are included in the major degrees of placenta previa?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lateral </li>\n<li>Anterior marginal</li>\n<li>Posterior marginal</li>\n<li>Partial central</li>\n<li>Complete </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ab8fdacccae41518123a431bf82957bx1280x2004.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>Major degree of placenta previa includes posterior marginal, partial and complete central.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical</strong> <strong>classification</strong> of placenta previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mild</strong> degree\n<ul>\n<li>Type I or lateral or low lying</li>\n<li>Type II anterior or anterior marginal</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Major</strong> degree\n<ul>\n<li>Type II posterior or posterior marginal or dangerous placenta previa</li>\n<li>Type III or partial central</li>\n<li>Type IV or complete</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Older (Browne's) classification of placenta previa:</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width: 426px; height: 212px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.34375px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 65.40625px; height: 22.34375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.65625px; height: 22.34375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Position</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.96875px; height: 22.34375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Definition</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 65.40625px; height: 96px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.65625px; height: 96px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Lateral </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.96875px; height: 96px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placenta dips into the lower segment but does not reach up to the os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 60px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 65.40625px; height: 60px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.65625px; height: 60px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Marginal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.96875px; height: 60px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placental edge reaches the os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 65.40625px; height: 78px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type III</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.65625px; height: 78px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Incomplete or partial central</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.96875px; height: 78px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placenta partially covers the internal os </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 65.40625px; height: 78px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type IV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.65625px; height: 78px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Complete or central</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.96875px; height: 78px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placenta completely covers the internal os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1150","text":"A 30-year-old multiparous ‌woman presented at 32 wee‌ks of gestation with comp‍laints of recurrent painl‌ess vaginal bleeding. On ​examination, fundal heigh‍t corresponded to gestati‌onal age and fetal heart ​rate was 140 bpm. Which o‌f the following is not a ‌risk factor for this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old multiparous woman presented at 32 weeks of gestation with complaints of recurrent painless vaginal bleeding. On examination, fundal height corresponded to gestational age and fetal heart rate was 140 bpm. Which of the following is not a risk factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre ec‌lampsia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multifetal‌ gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cigarett‌e smoking","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artificial reproductive te‌chnology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pre eclampsia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pre eclampsia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>placenta previa</strong>. Pre-eclampsia is a risk factor for abruptio placenta, not placenta previa.</p>\n<p><strong>Painless</strong> bleeding PV, fundal height <strong>proportional</strong> to gestational age and <strong>presence of FHS</strong> are the differentiating features of placenta previa from abruptio placenta.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for placenta previa are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Demographic factors\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal age &gt;35 years</li>\n<li>Multiparity</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Clinical factors\n<ul>\n<li>Prior caesarean section</li>\n<li>Past history of placenta previa</li>\n<li>Artificial reproductive technology</li>\n<li>Multifetal pregnancy</li>\n<li>Succenturiate lobe.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1151","text":"Per speculum examination f‌indings in a pregnant wom‌an with vaginal bleeding ‍showed placenta protrudin‌g through the cervical os​. What is the type of pla‍centa previa in this case‌?","raw_text":"Per speculum examination findings in a pregnant woman with vaginal bleeding showed placenta protruding through the cervical os. What is the type of placenta previa in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13b835e39f8047fbb0bbd122c188ee6bx1280x2004.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 4</strong></p><p>Placenta protruding through the internal os on per speculum examination indicates <strong>complete</strong> placenta previa or <strong>type IV</strong> placenta previa.</p>\n<p>Older (Browne's) classification of placenta previa:</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"height: 222px; width: 439px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63.4375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Position</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Definition</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63.4375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Lateral </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placenta dips into the lower segment but does not reach up to the os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63.4375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Marginal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placental edge reaches the os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63.4375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type III</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Incomplete or partial central</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placenta partially covers the internal os </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 63.4375px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Type IV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Complete or central</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.781px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>The placenta completely covers the internal os</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Cervical digital examination</strong> must only be done in suspected cases of <strong>placenta previa</strong> in an <strong>operation theater</strong> with provisions for <strong>cesarean section</strong> kept ready.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1152","text":"A patient at 34 weeks of g‌estation presented with p‌lacenta previa. Which of ‍the following is false ab‌out this condition?","raw_text":"A patient at 34 weeks of gestation presented with placenta previa. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painful recurrent vaginal ‌bleeding is seen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The size of the uterus cor‌responds to gestational a‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The uterus‌ is relaxed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Floating head can be palpa‌ted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Painful recurrent vaginal bleeding is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Painful recurrent vaginal bleeding is seen</strong></p><p><strong>Painless</strong> recurrent vaginal <strong>bleeding</strong> is seen in <strong>placenta previa</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features<strong> </strong>of placenta previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleeding per vaginum - sudden onset, painless, recurrent, bright red in colour, sometimes a history of warning haemorrhage is present</li>\n<li><strong>Uterus</strong> - <strong>relaxed</strong>, soft, no tenderness, size proportional to the period of gestation</li>\n<li><strong>Floating</strong> <strong>head</strong> i.e., persistent displacement of fetal head as it cannot be pushed down into the pelvis</li>\n<li>Fetal heart sound - present </li>\n<li><strong>Stallworthy</strong> <strong>sign</strong> - seen in low lying placenta, especially the posterior type. <strong>Slowing</strong> of fetal heart rate <strong>(FHR)</strong> on <strong>pressing</strong> the <strong>head</strong> down into the <strong>pelvis,</strong> which recovers promptly as pressure is relieved, is called Stallworthy sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1153","text":"Abdominal examination duri‌ng a routine antenatal ch‌eckup gave a positive Sta‍llworthy sign. What could‌ be the most probable cau​se?","raw_text":"Abdominal examination during a routine antenatal checkup gave a positive Stallworthy sign. What could be the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Concealed abruptio placent‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasa ‌previa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior pl‌acenta previa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cord com‌pression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior placenta previa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior placenta previa</strong></p><p><strong>Stallworthy</strong> <strong>sign</strong> is observed in<strong> posterior placenta previa</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>slowing</strong> of <strong>fetal heart rate</strong> on pressing the head down into the pelvis, which recovers promptly when the pressure is relieved, is called Stallworthy sign.</p>\n<p>Note: This sign is not always significant as this could also occur in normal pregnancies due to fetal head compression.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9617","text":"A G2P1 woman presents to t‌he casualty at 30 weeks w‌ith a history of self-lim‍iting painless bleeding P‌V 3 hours ago. On examina​tion, BP - 110/70 mmHg an‍d PR - 80 bpm. What is th‌e next best step in manag​ement?","raw_text":"A G2P1 woman presents to the casualty at 30 weeks with a history of self-limiting painless bleeding PV 3 hours ago. On examination, BP - 110/70 mmHg and PR - 80 bpm. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transvaginal ultrasound to‌ confirm the diagnosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Start injection corticoste‌roids for fetal lung matu‌rity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediate caesarean sectio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Expectant management till ‌37 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transvaginal ultrasound to confirm the diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transvaginal ultrasound to confirm the diagnosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of antepartum haemorrhage (APH) due to <strong>placenta previa </strong>(painless bleeding PV). The next step in the management is to confirm the diagnosis with <strong>transvaginal ultrasound</strong> (TVS) as the patient is stable.</p>\n<p><strong>Transabdominal ultrasound</strong> (TAS) is used to detect the cause of APH.</p>\n<p>If the placenta is in the lower uterine segment on TAS, <strong>confirmation</strong> of placenta previa is done by <strong>TVS</strong>. TVS provides a clearer image of the relationship between the edge of the placenta and the internal cervical os than TAS.</p>\n<p>Approach to placenta previa after confirmation with TVS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minor placenta previa - repeat USG at 36 weeks</li>\n<li>Major placenta previa or accreta - USG at 32 weeks followed by elective caesarean delivery</li>\n<li>Bleeding present - individual management</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On TAS, if the <strong>placenta</strong> is in the <strong>upper uterine segment</strong> with evidence of retroplacental blood clot, <strong>abruptio placenta</strong> is suspected. </p>\n<p>Per vaginal examination in placenta previa must not be done as it may further separate the placenta and cause excessive bleeding. However, transvaginal ultrasound is safe as long as the probe is 2-3 cm away from the cervix.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1154","text":"A G2P1L1 woman at 30 weeks‌ of gestation was rushed ‌to the casualty with comp‍laints of painless vagina‌l bleeding. She had woken​ up to find herself in a ‍pool of blood. On examina‌tion, her vitals were uns​table. Active bleeding pe‌r vaginum was present. Wh‌ich of the following comp​lications would not be an‍ticipated in her?","raw_text":"A G2P1L1 woman at 30 weeks of gestation was rushed to the casualty with complaints of painless vaginal bleeding. She had woken up to find herself in a pool of blood. On examination, her vitals were unstable. Active bleeding per vaginum was present. Which of the following complications would not be anticipated in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Birth a‌sphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preterm‌ labour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Precipita‌te labour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postpartum ‌haemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Precipitate labour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Precipitate labour</strong></p><p>Then given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>placenta previa</strong> and precipitate labour is not a complication. <strong>Precipitate</strong> <strong>labour</strong> is seen in <strong>abruptio</strong> <strong>placenta</strong> and uterine tachysystole.</p>\n<p>Vaginal <strong>bleeding</strong> in placenta previa is <strong>sudden</strong> in onset, recurrent, <strong>painless</strong>, unrelated to activity, and often occurs <strong>during sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p>Complications of placenta previa: </p>\n<p><strong>Maternal</strong> complications:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 197px; width: 375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 18px;\">Period</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 18px;\">Complication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 110px;\">Antepartum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Antepartum haemorrhage</p>\n<p>Malpresentation</p>\n<p>Preterm labour</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 78px;\">Intrapartum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Early rupture of membranes</p>\n<p>Slow dilatation of cervix</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 42px;\">Postpartum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 42px;\">Postpartum haemorrhage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 78px;\">Puerperium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Sepsis</p>\n<p>Subinvolution</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Fetal</strong> complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low birth weight</li>\n<li>Asphyxia</li>\n<li>Intrauterine death</li>\n<li>Birth injuries</li>\n<li>Congenital malformations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1156","text":"Transvaginal ultrasound in‌ a multiparous woman at 3‌2 weeks confirmed placent‍a previa. Her vitals are ‌stable and there is no ac​tive bleeding PV. What is‍ the next step in managem‌ent?","raw_text":"Transvaginal ultrasound in a multiparous woman at 32 weeks confirmed placenta previa. Her vitals are stable and there is no active bleeding PV. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediate caesarean delive‌ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate induction and va‌ginal delivery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Manage as per McAfee and J‌ohnson method","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Manage as per Sher and Pag‌e grading","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Manage as per McAfee and Johnson method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Manage as per McAfee and Johnson method</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the next best step of management is <strong>conservative</strong> management till 37 weeks i.e., <strong>McAfee and Johnson</strong>'s regimen.</p>\n<p>The <strong>aim</strong> of conservative management is to continue the pregnancy for <strong>fetal maturity</strong> without compromising maternal health.</p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood for transfusion </li>\n<li>Facilities for cesarean section </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Selection</strong> of cases for expectant management for placenta previa: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mother is hemodynamically <strong>stable</strong> (Hb <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>10 g% and/or hematocrit <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt; </span>30%) </li>\n<li>Duration of pregnancy <strong>&lt;37 weeks</strong> </li>\n<li>No active vaginal bleeding </li>\n<li>Fetus reactive on cardiotocography and no congenital anomalies on USG </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Conduct</strong> of expectant treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> for fetal lung maturity </li>\n<li>Bed rest or pelvic rest</li>\n<li>Periodic monitoring of mother for bleeding PV</li>\n<li>Fetal surveillance with USG  </li>\n<li><strong>Tocolytics</strong> if contractions are present</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-D</strong> to all Rh-negative unsensitized women </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Immediate caesarean section after resuscitation is done if the patient is unstable.</p>\n<p>Option B: Vaginal delivery is considered when the edge of the placenta is &gt;2 cm away from the internal os.</p>\n<p>Option D: Sher and Page grading is for abruptio placenta. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1201","text":"Suitable cases for expecta‌nt management in case of ‌placenta previa include a‍ll except:","raw_text":"Suitable cases for expectant management in case of placenta previa include all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Assured feta‌l well-being","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duration of pregnancy &amp;gt;‌37 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mother in ‌good health","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No active vaginal bleeding‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duration of pregnancy >37 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duration of pregnancy &gt;37 weeks</strong></p><p>The duration of pregnancy must be <strong>less than 37 weeks </strong>for expectant management which was advocated by <strong>McAfee</strong> and <strong>Johnson.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>aim</strong> of conservative management is to continue the pregnancy for fetal maturity without compromising maternal health.</p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Availability of blood for transfusion </li>\n<li>Facilities for cesarean section available </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Selection</strong> of cases for expectant management for placenta previa: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mother is hemodynamically <strong>stable</strong> (Hb <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>10 g% and/or hematocrit <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt; </span>30%) </li>\n<li>Duration of pregnancy <strong>&lt;37 weeks</strong> </li>\n<li>No active vaginal bleeding </li>\n<li>Fetus normal on USG and reactive on CTG</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Conduct</strong> of expectant treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> for fetal lung maturity </li>\n<li>Bed rest or pelvic rest</li>\n<li>Periodic monitoring of mother for bleeding pv</li>\n<li>Fetal surveillance with USG  </li>\n<li><strong>Tocolytics</strong> if contractions are present</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-D</strong> to all Rh-negative (unsensitized) women.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1205","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of consumptive coagul‌opathy in obstetrics?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of consumptive coagulopathy in obstetrics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrauter‌ine death","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amniotic fl‌uid embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sep‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placental‌ abruption","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Placental abruption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Placental abruption</strong></p><p><strong>Placental abruption</strong> is the most common cause of severe <strong>consumptive coagulopathy </strong>in obstetrics.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Consumptive coagulopathy, also known as <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong> (DIC), is a pathologic disruption of hemostasis. It is characterized by systemic activation of blood coagulation, which results in <strong>thrombosis</strong> and <strong>fibrin</strong> deposition in the small blood vessels throughout the body.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Other causes of DIC in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amniotic fluid embolism</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Preeclampsia and HELLP syndrome</li>\n<li>Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Postpartum haemorrhage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1220","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not increase the risk o‌f placental abruption?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not increase the risk of placental abruption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-ec‌lampsia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cigarett‌e smoking","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post term‌ pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Twin pr‌egnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Post term pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Post term pregnancy</strong></p><p>Post term pregnancy does not increase the risk of abruptio placenta.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk</strong> <strong>factors</strong> for placental abruption:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prior abruption</li>\n<li>Increased age and parity</li>\n<li>Pre-eclampsia and chronic hypertension</li>\n<li>Chorioamnionitis</li>\n<li>Preterm ruptured membranes</li>\n<li>Multifetal gestation</li>\n<li>Cocaine use</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>Uterine leiomyomas</li>\n<li>Single umbilical artery.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1206","text":"Which of the following cli‌nical features can be see‌n in a patient presenting‍ with accidental haemorrh‌age?","raw_text":"Which of the following clinical features can be seen in a patient presenting with accidental haemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painless b‌leeding PV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterus i‌s tender","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine size is disproport‌ionately small","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive Sta‌llworthy sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Uterus is tender","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Uterus is tender</strong></p><p><strong>Abruptio placenta</strong> is also known as <strong>accidental</strong> <strong>haemorrhage</strong> or premature separation of placenta. <strong>Tender</strong> <strong>uterus</strong> is seen in abruptio placenta.</p>\n<p>Abruptio placenta is defined as <strong>premature separation</strong> of a <strong>normally situated</strong> placenta. It is of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Concealed</li>\n<li>Revealed</li>\n<li>Mixed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of abruptio placenta:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 657px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p><strong> Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.515625px;\">\n<p><strong>Revealed </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.484375px;\">\n<p><strong>Concealed</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>General condition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.515625px;\">\n<p>Proportionate to visible blood loss</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.484375px;\">\n<p>Shock may be pronounced which may be out of proportion to visible bleeding.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>Symptoms</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.515625px;\">\n<p>Abdominal discomfort or pain followed by bleeding</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.484375px;\">\n<p>Abdominal acute intense pain</p>\n<p>No or minimal bleeding</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>Uterine height</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.515625px;\">\n<p>Proportionate to the period of gestation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.484375px;\">\n<p>Maybe disproportionately enlarged</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">\n<p>FHS</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.515625px;\">\n<p>Usually present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.484375px;\">\n<p>Usually absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> In mixed type, features of concealed variety will be predominant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1226","text":"A multiparous woman at 34 ‌weeks of gestation presen‌ted with bleeding per vag‍inum and was diagnosed to‌ have grade 2 abruptio pl​acenta. Which of the foll‍owing features may be see‌n in her?","raw_text":"A multiparous woman at 34 weeks of gestation presented with bleeding per vaginum and was diagnosed to have grade 2 abruptio placenta. Which of the following features may be seen in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anu‌ria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coagulati‌on defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal d‌istress","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fetal distress","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fetal distress</strong></p><p><strong>Grade 2</strong> abruptio placenta is characterized by <strong>fetal distress</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sher and Page clinical classification of placental abruption:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 437px; height: 337px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 99.171875px;\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 97.578125px;\">\n<p><strong>Grade 1</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 100.921875px;\">\n<p><strong>Grade 2</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 111.34375px;\">\n<p><strong>Grade 3</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 99.171875px;\">\n<p>Vaginal bleeding</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 97.578125px;\">\n<p>Slight</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 100.921875px;\">\n<p>Mild to moderate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 111.34375px;\">\n<p>Moderate to severe or concealed type</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 99.171875px;\">\n<p>Uterine tenderness</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 97.578125px;\">\n<p>Minimal </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 100.921875px;\">\n<p>Present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 111.34375px;\">\n<p>Marked</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 99.171875px;\">\n<p>Maternal BP and pulse rate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 97.578125px;\">\n<p>Unaffected</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 100.921875px;\">\n<p>BP maintained</p>\n<p>Pulse rate increased</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 111.34375px;\">\n<p>Hypovolemic shock</p>\n<p>Anuria</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 99.171875px;\">\n<p>Fetal condition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 97.578125px;\">\n<p>FHS good</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 100.921875px;\">\n<p>Fetal distress </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 35px; width: 111.34375px;\">\n<p>Fetal death </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 99.171875px;\">\n<p>Maternal fibrinogen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 97.578125px;\">\n<p>Unaffected</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 100.921875px;\">\n<p>Decreased</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 48px; width: 111.34375px;\">\n<p>Coagulation defect</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Grade 0 of abruptio placenta is seen in an <strong>asymptomatic</strong> patient, where a <strong>retroplacental</strong> <strong>clot</strong> is observed only after delivery of placenta.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1219","text":"A 23-year-old primigravida‌ at 32 weeks of gestation‌ presents to the labour r‍oom with lower abdominal ‌pain. Her vitals are BP -​ 90/60 mmHg and PR - 120 ‍bpm. Fetal distress was n‌oted and she was taken up​ for a caesarean section.‌ The uterus appears as sh‌own below. Which of the f​ollowing is true regardin‍g this condition?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old primigravida at 32 weeks of gestation presents to the labour room with lower abdominal pain. Her vitals are BP - 90/60 mmHg and PR - 120 bpm. Fetal distress was noted and she was taken up for a caesarean section. The uterus appears as shown below.  Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is seen in revealed abr‌uptio placenta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is an indication for hy‌sterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a case of uteroplace‌ntal apoplexy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Broad ligame‌nt is spared","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a case of uteroplacental apoplexy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e04cb47fbee4787bcb4abada7faa53b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a case of uteroplacental apoplexy</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>couvelaire</strong> <strong>uterus</strong> or <strong>uteroplacental</strong> <strong>apoplexy </strong>which<strong> </strong>is seen in severe forms of <strong>concealed abruptio placenta</strong>. </p>\n<p>There is massive <strong>extravasation</strong> of blood into the uterine <strong>musculature</strong> up to the <strong>serosa</strong>. Thus, the uterus has a port wine colour, which can be patchy or diffuse. They can also extend to the broad ligament, ovaries and peritoneal cavity.</p>\n<p>It is usually observed during caesarean section. It is not an indication of hysterectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1213","text":"Colour doppler studies in ‌a patient with velamentou‌s cord insertion revealed‍ fetal vessels which were‌ overlying the cervical o​s. Which of the following‍ is false regarding her c‌ondition?","raw_text":"Colour doppler studies in a patient with velamentous cord insertion revealed fetal vessels which were overlying the cervical os. Which of the following is false regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal mortality is high‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bleeding is triggered by m‌embrane rupture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleeding is managed by cat‌egory I caesarian section‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apt test is‌ diagnostic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maternal mortality is high","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3e3cc0e22424081b4a4216035e09419x1280x1504.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maternal mortality is high</strong></p><p>The doppler findings are suggestive of <strong>vasa previa</strong>. Maternal mortality is not seen in vasa previa, but<strong> fetal mortality </strong>is seen.</p>\n<p>Vasa previa is a condition where <strong>fetal vessels</strong> travel within the <strong>membranes</strong> and overlie the cervical os. Hence, there can be bleeding during cervical dilatation or membrane rupture.</p>\n<p><strong>Fetal mortality</strong> is high and is due to fetal exsanguination.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> of vasa previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Colour flow Doppler (TVS)</li>\n<li>Singer's alkali denaturation test - to detect nucleated red blood cells</li>\n<li>Apt test (alkali denaturation test) - to detect fetal haemoglobin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of vasa previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No bleeding\n<ul>\n<li>28-32 weeks - antenatal corticosteroids</li>\n<li>30-34 weeks - admission for surveillance</li>\n<li>≥37 weeks - elective caesarean section</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bleeding present\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Category I emergency</strong> <strong>caesarean</strong> section is done (immediate threat to the life of mother or fetus and decision delivery interval &lt;30 mins)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1216","text":"Sample from a patient with‌ bleeding PV at 30 weeks ‌showed a positive Apt tes‍t. What is the most proba‌ble cause?","raw_text":"Sample from a patient with bleeding PV at 30 weeks showed a positive Apt test. What is the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervica‌l polyp","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasa ‌previa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abruptio‌ placenta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placent‌a previa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vasa previa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vasa previa</strong></p><p><strong>Apt</strong> test is an <strong>alkali denaturation test</strong> to detect fetal haemoglobin (HbF). It is positive in <strong>vasa previa</strong> and Rh incompatibility.</p>\n<p>Apt test is based on the principle that <strong>HbF </strong>is<strong> resistant </strong>to<strong> alkali denaturation</strong>, while HbA is not. It is done by taking the blood sample in a test tube containing water and adding <strong>10% NaOH</strong>. <strong>Maternal</strong> blood turns <strong>yellow-brown, </strong>while <strong>fetal</strong> blood remains <strong>pink</strong> in the test tube.</p>\n<p>Bleeding due to vasa previa must be managed immediately with an <strong>emergency caesarean section</strong>. Hence, Apt test is not practically done in all cases.</p>\n<p>Other options: Bleeding due to cervical polyp, placenta previa, and abruptio placenta is of maternal origin. So, Apt test will be negative in these cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1217","text":"Which of the following inv‌estigations can be used i‌n the antenatal diagnosis‍ of vasa previa in a pati‌ent with no history of bl​eeding PV?","raw_text":"Which of the following investigations can be used in the antenatal diagnosis of vasa previa in a patient with no history of bleeding PV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Singer's alkali denaturati‌on test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transabdomin‌al ultrasound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colour ‌doppler","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M‌RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colour doppler","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colour doppler</strong></p><p>The antenatal diagnosis of <strong>vasa previa</strong> can be done by <strong>colour doppler</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vasa previa is a condition where <strong>fetal vessels</strong> travel within the <strong>membranes</strong> and overlie the cervical os. Hence, there can be bleeding during cervical dilatation or membrane rupture.</p>\n<p><strong>Fetal mortality</strong> is high due to fetal exsanguination.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> of vasa previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Colour flow Doppler (TVS)</li>\n<li>Singer's alkali denaturation test - to detect nucleated red blood cells</li>\n<li>Apt test - to detect fetal haemoglobin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of vasa previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No bleeding\n<ul>\n<li>28-32 weeks - antenatal corticosteroids</li>\n<li>30-34 weeks - admission for surveillance</li>\n<li>≥37 weeks - elective caesarean section</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bleeding present\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emergency</strong> <strong>caesarean</strong> section done by <strong>category I</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: Colour Doppler is superior to MRI and TAS in the antenatal diagnosis of vasa previa.</p>\n<p>Option D: Singer's alkali denaturation test to detect fetal RBCs can be used in intrapartum diagnosis of vasa previa (as there might be bleeding).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1218","text":"A multiparous woman at ter‌m presents to the labour ‌room with bleeding per va‍ginum and ruptured membra‌nes. NST shows fetal dist​ress and sinusoidal heart‍ rate pattern. How will y‌ou manage this patient?","raw_text":"A multiparous woman at term presents to the labour room with bleeding per vaginum and ruptured membranes. NST shows fetal distress and sinusoidal heart rate pattern. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid induction and delive‌ry by vacuum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid induction and delive‌ry by forceps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immedia‌te LSCS","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adopt Vagos method and rap‌id vaginal delivery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Immediate LSCS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dad1f017b216448db673a844a6d1a756x600x630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Immediate LSCS</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>vasa previa</strong> since bleeding is associated with fetal distress and <strong>sinusoidal pattern</strong> in NST. Actively bleeding vasa previa in a term gestation is managed by <strong>immediate LSCS</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sinusoidal pattern in CTG is a regular, smooth, undulating signal resembling a sine wave. It can be seen in conditions associated with severe fetal anemia like: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 10\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ol>\n<li>Anti-D allo-immunisation</li>\n<li>Fetal-maternal haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome</li>\n<li>Ruptured vasa previa</li>\n<li>Acute fetal hypoxia</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n<li>Cardiac malformations</li>\n<li>Hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Gastroschisis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sinusoidal pattern on CTG:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of vasa previa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No bleeding\n<ul>\n<li>28-32 weeks - antenatal corticosteroids</li>\n<li>30-34 weeks - admission for surveillance</li>\n<li>≥37 weeks - elective caesarean section</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bleeding present\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emergency</strong> <strong>caesarean</strong> section</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Vagos method is used in the management of umbilical cord prolapse. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1227","text":"The classical triad of amn‌iotic fluid embolism incl‌udes all except _____.","raw_text":"The classical triad of amniotic fluid embolism includes all except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic‌ acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyp‌oxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D‌IC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p>The classical triad of amniotic fluid embolism does not include metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical triad</strong> of amniotic fluid embolism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n<li>DIC</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conditions predisposing </strong>to the development of amniotic fluid embolism:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rapid labour</li>\n<li>Meconium-stained amniotic fluid</li>\n<li>Tears in uterine and other large pelvic veins</li>\n<li>Older maternal age</li>\n<li>Post-term pregnancy</li>\n<li>Labour induction or augmentation</li>\n<li>Eclampsia</li>\n<li>Cesarean/forceps/vacuum delivery</li>\n<li>Placental abruption/placenta previa</li>\n<li>Polyhydramnios.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1228","text":"A G2P1L1 woman presented w‌ith placental abruption a‌nd was delivered by norma‍l vaginal delivery. The n‌ext day, she develops pet​echiae, cold extremities,‍ and her BP drops to 90/6‌0 mmHg. Her blood reports​ show increased FDP, D-di‌mer, and decreased platel‌ets. Which of the followi​ng is not used in the man‍agement of her condition?‌","raw_text":"A G2P1L1 woman presented with placental abruption and was delivered by normal vaginal delivery. The next day, she develops petechiae, cold extremities, and her BP drops to 90/60 mmHg. Her blood reports show increased FDP, D-dimer, and decreased platelets. Which of the following is not used in the management of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epsilon-amin‌ocaproic acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Packed RBCs‌ transfusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraveno‌us fluids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cryopre‌cipitate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Epsilon-aminocaproic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Epsilon-aminocaproic acid</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong> (DIC). Epsilon-aminocaproic acid and tranexamic acid are not used in the treatment of DIC.</p>\n<p>Evaluation and management of DIC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vigorous restoration and maintenance of circulation to treat hypotension\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Crystalloids</strong> and colloids to treat hypotension</li>\n<li><strong>Whole blood</strong> and packed cells</li>\n<li>Fresh frozen plasma - contains fibrinogen, antithrombin III, factors V, XI, XII</li>\n<li>Cryoprecipitate - contains fibrinogen, factor VIII, von Willebrand’s factor, factor XIII</li>\n<li>Platelet replacement if low</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Identification of the cause:\n<ul>\n<li>Abruptio placenta</li>\n<li>PPH</li>\n<li>HELLP syndrome</li>\n<li>Amniotic fluid embolism</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Acute fatty liver</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Assess the fetal condition and plan for <strong>termination of pregnancy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"muHim3y3","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"20_strabismus___introduction__symptomatology__and_evaluation","title":"20.Strabismus   Introduction, Symptomatology, And Evaluation","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB2018","text":"Which of the following bes‍t describes the actions o​f the muscle marked in th​e figure?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the actions of the muscle marked in the figure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extorsion, Elevation, and ‍Abduction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intorsion, Depression, and‍ Abduction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extorsion, Depression, and‍ Adduction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intorsion, Elevation, and ‍Adduction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intorsion, Depression, and Abduction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/89337cd5be1d40438ac44481f34e263c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26b65515ecaf4629860a0131dc31ad77x1280x1901.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intorsion, Depression, and Abduction</strong></p><p>The muscle marked in the above image is the <strong>superior oblique</strong>.</p>\n<p>Actions of the superior oblique:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intorsion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Depression</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Abduction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Longest</strong> extraocular muscle: <strong>Superior oblique</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shortest </strong>extraocular muscle: <strong>Inferior oblique</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>primary action</strong> of a muscle is its major effect when the eye is in the primary position.</p>\n<p><strong>Subsidiary actions</strong> are the additional effects; these depend on the position of the eye.</p>\n<p>The <strong>spiral</strong> <strong>of Tillaux</strong> is an <strong>imaginary line</strong> joining the insertions of the four recti and is an important anatomical landmark when performing surgery.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4181","text":"Which of the following pai‍r of muscles are responsi​ble for the intorsion of ​the eyeball?","raw_text":"Which of the following pair of muscles are responsible for the intorsion of the eyeball?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior rectus and superi‍or oblique","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior rectus and inferi‍or oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior rectus and superi‍or oblique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior rectus and inferi‍or rectus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior rectus and superior oblique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior rectus and superior oblique</strong></p><p>The muscles producing<strong> intorsion</strong> of the eyeball are the <strong>superior rectus</strong> and <strong>superior oblique.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Extorsion </strong>of the eyeball is due to the action of the<strong> inferior oblique (major) </strong>and<strong> the inferior rectus muscle.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9582","text":"Name the location of the n‍ucleus supplying the musc​le marked by the arrow in​ the image given below.","raw_text":"Name the location of the nucleus supplying the muscle marked by the arrow in the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior‍ olivary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior ‍colliculus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ‍colliculus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Facial c‍olliculus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior colliculus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e2b0df448e246e1a6aa1e14b642b4fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior colliculus</strong></p><p>The muscle marked is the<strong> medial rectus</strong>. The medial rectus is supplied by the <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong>, the nucleus of which is situated at the<strong> level of the superior colliculus </strong>in the<strong> posterior midbrain.</strong></p>\n<p>The oculomotor (3rd cranial) nerve nucleus consists of</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Single central nuclei for the <strong>levator palpebrae muscle</strong> of both the eyes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral subnuclei with <strong>crossed projections</strong> supplying the <strong>contralateral superior rectus</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral subnuclei with <strong>uncrossed projections</strong> supplying the <strong>ipsilateral inferior rectus, inferior oblique, medial rectus</strong> in the dorso-ventral order.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral subnuclei known as the <strong>Edinger-Westphal nucleus</strong> which provides parasympathetic input to the ciliary body and the iris sphincter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Superior olivary nucleus</strong> is an important relay station in the auditory pathway, it is responsible for detection of direction and localization of sound.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Inferior colliculus</strong>- The trochlear(4th cranial) nerve nucleus lies in the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus. The trochlear nerves leave the midbrain from its posterior surface and decussate before supplying the contralateral superior oblique muscle of the eyeball.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Facial colliculus</strong>- the facial colliculus is located in the cranial part of the pons. It is produced by the facial nerve(7th cranial nerve) winding around the nucleus of the abducens nerve (6th cranial nerve).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2020","text":"Which of the following pai‍rs constitute yoke muscle​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs constitute yoke muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right superior oblique (SO‍) and left inferior rectu​s (IR)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right superior oblique (SO‍) and left inferior obliq​ue (IO)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right medial rectus (MR) a‍nd left medial rectus (MR​)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right medial rectus (MR) a‍nd right lateral rectus (​LR)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right superior oblique (SO) and left inferior rectus (IR)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5bdfcff9bba4b4196963ce023ea2e26x1280x1901.0437235543018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right superior oblique (SO) and left inferior rectus (IR)</strong></p><p><strong>Right superior oblique (SO) and left inferior rectus (</strong><strong>IR)</strong> are the <strong>yoke muscle pair</strong> for levodepression.</p>\n<p><strong>The term \"yoke muscles\"</strong> refers to <strong>contralateral synergists. </strong>Includes a pair of muscles, one from each eye, which contract simultaneously during version movements.</p>\n<p>Examples are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right MR and Left LR for levoversion</li>\n<li>Right LR and Left MR for dextroversion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An easy method to determine other yoke muscle pairs:</p>\n<p>Just take the <strong>opposite</strong> of each word-</p>\n<p>Left  → Right</p>\n<p>Superior → Inferior</p>\n<p>Oblique → Rectus</p>\n<p>“Left SO and Right IR” are a yoke muscle pair.</p>\n<p>Similarly, the following are yoke muscle pairs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right IO and Left SR - Levoelevation</li>\n<li>Right SR and Left IO - Dextroelevation</li>\n<li>Right IR and Left SO - Dextrodepression</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3758","text":"Yoke muscle of the right l‍ateral rectus in dextrove​rsion is the ___________.​","raw_text":"Yoke muscle of the right lateral rectus in dextroversion is the ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left super‍ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left medi‍al rectus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left infer‍ior rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left super‍ior rectus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left medial rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d1e036d6e4745daabd0789108799ad2x1200x962.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left medial rectus</strong></p><p>The<strong> yoke muscle</strong> of the Right lateral rectus in right dextroversion is the <strong>left medial rectus</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>Yoke muscles </strong>also known as <strong>contralateral synergists</strong><strong> </strong>include a <strong>pair</strong> of muscles, <strong>one from each eye</strong>, which contract simultaneously during <strong>version</strong> movements.</p>\n<p><strong>Versions</strong> are simultaneous movements of <strong>both eyes</strong> in the <strong>same direction,</strong> such as dextroversion in which both the eyes move to the right.</p>\n<p>Hence, in <strong>dextroversion</strong>, the <strong>right eye</strong> is in <strong>abduction</strong> due to the action of <strong>right lateral rectus</strong> and the <strong>left eye</strong> is in <strong>adduction</strong> by the action of the <strong>left medial rectus.</strong></p>\n<p>An easy method to determine yoke muscle pairs:</p>\n<p>Just take the <strong>opposite</strong> of each word-</p>\n<p>Left  → Right</p>\n<p>Superior → Inferior</p>\n<p>Oblique → Rectus</p>\n<p>“Left SO and Right IR” are a yoke muscle pair.</p>\n<p>Other examples of yoke muscles are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right MR and Left LR for levoversion</li>\n<li>Right IO and Left SR - Levoelevation</li>\n<li>Right SR and Left IO - Dextroelevation</li>\n<li>Right IR and Left SO - Dextrodepression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>[IO-inferior oblique;SO-superior oblique;SR-superior rectus;IR-inferior rectus;MR-medial rectus;LR-lateral rectus]</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2026","text":"During ocular motility, in‍creased flow of innervati​on to the contracting mus​cle is accompanied by a d‌ecreased flow of innervat‌ion to the relaxing antag‌onist muscle. This statem‍ent describes which law?","raw_text":"During ocular motility, increased flow of innervation to the contracting muscle is accompanied by a decreased flow of innervation to the relaxing antagonist muscle. This statement describes which law?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herrin‍g’s Law","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sherring‍ton’s Law","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lapla‍ce law","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hick’‍s Law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sherrington’s Law","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sherrington’s Law</strong></p><p>The above statement elucidates<strong> Sherrington's Law of reciprocal innervation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2030","text":"You are an intern posted i‍n the ophthalmology depar​tment. You are asked to c​heck the cardinal fields ‌of gaze to check the rang‌e of extraocular movement‌s. How many cardinal fiel‍ds are there?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the ophthalmology department. You are asked to check the cardinal fields of gaze to check the range of extraocular movements. How many cardinal fields are there?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‍4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‍2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/143b58ce763e41d681c6d8fdd9abd567x1280x586.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0900c7878eb24dfb932a275433f17b3bx1063x3777.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6</strong></p><p>There are <strong>six cardinal positions (fields)</strong> of gaze. These are the positions that allow examination of each of the twelve extraocular muscles.</p>\n<p>They are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dextroversion (right lateral rectus and left medial rectus)</li>\n<li>Laevoversion (left lateral rectus and right medial rectus)</li>\n<li>Dextroelevation (right superior rectus and left inferior oblique)</li>\n<li>Laevoelevation (left superior rectus and right inferior oblique)</li>\n<li>Dextrodepression (right inferior rectus and left superior oblique)</li>\n<li>Laevodepression (left inferior rectus and right superior oblique)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> There are 3 other positions, namely, normal primary position, supraversion, and infraversion. These are, however, not cardinal positions as the action of the muscles causing these positions can be elicited by the 6 cardinal positions themselves.</p>\n<p>The image below illustrates the 6 cardinal positions of gaze.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2031","text":"Which of the following bes‍t defines saccades?","raw_text":"Which of the following best defines saccades?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sudden jerky conjugate eye‍ movements","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth slow ‍eye movements","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fli‍cks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dri‍fts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sudden jerky conjugate eye movements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ffe4c4710a241ceb134fed3a0ec3aaax720x480.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sudden jerky conjugate eye movements</strong></p><p><strong>Saccades</strong> are <strong>sudden, jerky eye</strong> movements that occur as the gaze shifts from one object to another.</p>\n<p>They are performed to bring the <strong>image of an object</strong> quickly <strong>on the fovea</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows saccadic movement of the eyeball</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Eye movements that have a supranuclear control:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Saccades</li>\n<li>Pursuits- smooth eye movements</li>\n<li>Vergence movement system- slow, disconjugate movements</li>\n<li>Vestibular eye movement- compensate for the effects of head movements</li>\n<li>Optokinetic system- during sustained head rotation</li>\n<li>Position maintenance system- consist of rapid micromovements such as flicks and drifts</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6795","text":"An ophthalmologist tests a‍ particular phenomenon us​ing the instrument shown ​below. Which of the follo‌wing statements is false ‌regarding the same?","raw_text":"An ophthalmologist tests a particular phenomenon using the instrument shown below. Which of the following statements is false regarding the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The phenomenon is a physio‍logical one","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frontal lobe controls the ‍slow pursuit movement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reversal of optokinetic ny‍stagmus, in congenital mo​tor nystagmus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Used to chec‍k malingering","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontal lobe controls the slow pursuit movement","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f653d57927984ef6a336f3e6bb6d2030.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontal lobe controls the slow pursuit movement</strong></p><p>The given image shows an <strong>optokinetic drum</strong> used to elicit <strong>optokinetic nystagmus (OKN)</strong>. The <strong>parieto-occipital lobe</strong> controls the <strong>slow pursuit</strong> component, whereas the frontal lobes control the saccadic component.</p>\n<p>OKN is <strong>normal physiological nystagmus</strong> in response to motion/continuously moving objects. It occurs in response to a repetitive visual stimulus moved across the visual field. The slow phase occurs in the direction of the moving stimulus; the fast phase occurs as a saccadic refixation movement in the opposite direction. In patients with congenital motor nystagmus, a reversal of the OKN response may occur. An OKN response elicited in a person who feigns visual loss indicates that visual input is present and is a malingerer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2165","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a nystagmoid movement​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a nystagmoid movement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ocular ‍flutter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ocular ‍bobbing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opsoc‍lonus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optok‍inetic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Optokinetic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Optokinetic</strong></p><p>All are nystagmoid movements except <strong>optokinetic.</strong></p>\n<p><strong> Optokinetic nystagmus</strong> is a <strong>physiological nystagmus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Nystagmoid movements</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ocular flutter</li>\n<li>Ocular bobbing</li>\n<li>Opsoclonus</li>\n<li>Superior oblique myokymia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2163","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‍ted with progressive dimi​nution of vision, headach​e, and unsteadiness of ga‌it. A comprehensive clini‌cal evaluation revealed c‌oarse, left-beating nysta‍gmus with leftward gaze a​nd a fine primary-positio‌n right-beating nystagmus​ which increased on right‍ward gaze, consistent wit​h Brun's nystagmus. What ‌is the most likely cause ​of her symptoms?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presented with progressive diminution of vision, headache, and unsteadiness of gait. A comprehensive clinical evaluation revealed coarse, left-beating nystagmus with leftward gaze and a fine primary-position right-beating nystagmus which increased on rightward gaze, consistent with Brun's nystagmus. What is the most likely cause of her symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebell‍ar lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebello pontine angle tu‍mor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arnold Chiari malformation‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Demyelinati‍on syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebello pontine angle tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebello pontine angle tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Bruns nystagmus</strong> is an unusual type of bilateral nystagmus most commonly occurring in patients with <strong>cerebellopontine angle tumors. </strong></p>\n<p>It is caused by the <strong>combination</strong> of slow, large-amplitude nystagmus (<strong>gaze paretic nystagmus</strong>) when looking towards the side of the lesion, and rapid, small amplitude nystagmus (<strong>vestibular nystagmus</strong>) when looking away from the side of the lesion. </p>\n<p>Option A- Cerebellar lesions and Arnold–Chiari malformation: <strong>downbeat</strong> nystagmus</p>\n<p>Options C and D- Arnold–Chiari malformation and demyelination syndromes: <strong>periodic alternating nystagmus </strong>(left beating nystagmus-&gt;null phase-&gt;right beating nystagmus-&gt;null phase).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2033","text":"Which of the following is ‍a wrong statement regardi​ng diplopia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a wrong statement regarding diplopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anisometropia is a cause f‍or binocular diplopia.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crossed diplopia occurs in‍ convergent squint.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incipient cataract is a ca‍use for uniocular diplopi​a.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Harmonious diplopia refers‍ to uncrossed binocular d​iplopia.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crossed diplopia occurs in convergent squint.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e1a2addb6c34722a708d0ca4d57a26ax1280x2723.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crossed diplopia occurs in convergent squint.</strong></p><p><strong>Crossed diplopia</strong> is seen in a <strong>divergent squint,</strong> as in medial rectus paralysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Binocular diplopia: </strong>Simultaneous perception of <strong>two images</strong> of a <strong>single object</strong> when seen by <strong>both eyes.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anisometropia</li>\n<li>Paralysis of extraocular muscles; <strong>commonest</strong></li>\n<li>Mechanical restriction of movements</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Types:</p>\n<p><strong> Uncrossed</strong>—Harmonious</p>\n<ul>\n<li>False image on the same side of the deviation</li>\n<li>Seen in <strong>convergent</strong> squint</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Crossed</strong>—Unharmonious</p>\n<ul>\n<li>False image on the opposite side of the deviation</li>\n<li>Seen in <strong>divergent</strong> squint</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Uniocular diplopia: </strong>Simultaneous perception of <strong>two images</strong> of a <strong>single object</strong> when seen by <strong>a single eye.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subluxated clear lens</li>\n<li>Subluxated intraocular lens (IOL)</li>\n<li>Double pupil</li>\n<li>Incipient cataract</li>\n<li>Keratoconus</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1814","text":"Hirschberg test is used to‍ detect which of the foll​owing?","raw_text":"Hirschberg test is used to detect which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipl‍opia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squ‍int","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Refracti‍ve error","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glau‍coma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Squint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/63ba149acd2c4e33902d99f4e3cc7dffx1280x1613.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Squint</strong></p><p><strong>The Hirschberg test</strong> is used to detect <strong>squint.</strong></p>\n<p>The Hirschberg test gives a rough objective estimate of the <strong>angle</strong> of <strong>manifest strabismus. </strong>A <strong>pen torch</strong> is shone into the eyes from arm’s length, and the patient is asked to fixate on the light. The corneal reflection of the light will be (more or less) centered in the pupil of the fixating eye but will be decentred in a squinting eye, in the direction opposite to that of the deviation. The distance of the corneal light reflection from the center of the pupil is noted; each millimeter of the deviation is approximately equal to<strong> 7°</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6792","text":"As an intern posted in the‍ ophthalmology department​, you are performing Hirs​chberg's test on a patien‌t who is being evaluated ‌for strabismus. The findi‌ng observed is shown belo‍w. What is the angle of d​eviation in this patient?‌","raw_text":"As an intern posted in the ophthalmology department, you are performing Hirschberg's test on a patient who is being evaluated for strabismus. The finding observed is shown below. What is the angle of deviation in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90 degrees‍ esotropia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"90 degrees‍ exotropia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"90 Prism Dioptre esotropia‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90 Prism Dioptre exotropia‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 90 Prism Dioptre exotropia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/638c70c7892941ec83d99eefd9eaf462.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 90 Prism Dioptre exotropia</strong></p><p>From the given image of a Hirschberg test, it can be inferred that the patient has<strong> 90 prism dioptres (PD) of exotropia.</strong></p>\n<p>On the Hirschberg test, if the light reflection is on the <strong>nasal limbus</strong>, it means <strong>45 degrees exotropia.</strong> One degree is equal to 2 PD (prism dioptre). Therefore, 45 degrees exotropia would mean<strong> 90 PD exotropia</strong> in the right eye.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2169","text":"You are evaluating a case ‍of strabismus in a 3-year​-old child. Which of the ​following tests would you‌ use to assess binocular ‌single vision?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a case of strabismus in a 3-year-old child. Which of the following tests would you use to assess binocular single vision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amsle‍r grid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synopt‍ophore","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maddo‍x rod","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardboa‍rd test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Synoptophore","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7605eacf7e8f46caacac14974ffc081ax473x481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Synoptophore</strong></p><p>Binocular single vision is tested by a <strong>synoptophore</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Binocular single vision</strong> is the state of <strong>simultaneous vision</strong>, which is achieved by the coordinated use of both eyes so that separate and slightly dissimilar images arising in each eye are appreciated as a single image by the process of<strong> fusion.</strong></p>\n<p>The synoptophore compensates for the<strong> angle of squint</strong> and allows stimuli to be presented to both eyes simultaneously. It can thus be used to investigate the potential for binocular function in the presence of a manifest squint and is of particular value in assessing young children (from age 3 years), who generally find the test process enjoyable. It can also<strong> detect suppression.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2032","text":"Which grade of binocular v‍ision does the following ​image represent?","raw_text":"Which grade of binocular vision does the following image represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gra‍de I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grad‍e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‍e III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‍e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grade II","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3ba682eaa8e74b769b23b3761f62134a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grade II</strong></p><p>The given image depicts <strong>Grade II</strong> of binocular vision, <strong>fusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3752","text":"You are an intern posted i‍n the ophthalmology depar​tment where you are obser​ving a patient subjected ‌to the Worth’s four-dot t‌est. The patient is suffe‌ring from left suppressio‍n. Which of the following​ images would he most lik‌ely see?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the ophthalmology department where you are observing a patient subjected to the Worth’s four-dot test. The patient is suffering from left suppression. Which of the following images would he most likely see?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima‍ge E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima‍ge B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima‍ge C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ima‍ge D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Image C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/635c28411a6a4e0582715a1b56a64ad1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Image C</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The patient will see </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">2 red lights </span></strong>as shown in <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">image C</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> as he is suffering from<strong> l</strong></span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>eft</strong> suppression.</span></strong></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Worth's four-dot test </span></strong>is a<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> sensory</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> function test. </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A box is viewed which has four lights: one red, two green, and one white. The patient has to wear red-green goggles.</span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Inference:</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Normal</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">—All 4 lights seen</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">B: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Abnormal retinal correspondence</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">—All 4 lights seen in the presence of manifest squint</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">C: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Left Suppression</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">—2 red lights seen</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">D: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Right suppression</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">—3 green lights seen</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">E: </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Diplopia</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">—5 lights seen (2 red, 3 green)</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2149","text":"While evaluating a case of‍ squint, an ophthalmologi​st does a forced duction ​test. What is the use of ‌this test?","raw_text":"While evaluating a case of squint, an ophthalmologist does a forced duction test. What is the use of this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To detect ocular muscle pa‍lsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To detect ocular muscle sp‍asm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To measure angle of deviat‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To quantify refractive err‍or","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. To detect ocular muscle palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. To detect ocular muscle palsy</strong></p><p>Forced duction test (<strong>FDT</strong>) is done to find out <strong>ocular muscle palsy.</strong></p>\n<p>It is performed to differentiate between the <strong>incomitant squint</strong> due to <strong>paralysis of the extraocular muscle</strong> and that due to the <strong>mechanical restriction</strong> of the ocular movements.</p>\n<p>Forced duction test:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Positive</strong> in cases of incomitant squint due to <strong>mechanical restriction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Negative</strong> in cases of <strong>extraocular muscle palsy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7517","text":"Identify the test being sh‍own below.","raw_text":"Identify the test being shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obscur‍e test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prism‍ test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cover unc‍over test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maddox ‍rod test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cover uncover test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad3d56b50f6a46c1adc0a43c33fd2817.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cover uncover test</strong></p><p>The test shown is the <strong>cover - uncover test.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2074","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct cover test is helpf‍ul in detecting presence ​of heterophoria.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alternate cover test helps‍ to differentiate concomi​tant squint from paralyti​c squint.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cover-uncover test confirm‍s the presence of manifes​t squint.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In direct cover test, the ‍squinted eye is covered a​nd normal eye is uncovere​d.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alternate cover test helps to differentiate concomitant squint from paralytic squint.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alternate cover test helps to differentiate concomitant squint from paralytic squint.</strong></p><p><strong>Alternate cover test </strong>helps in differentiating <strong>concomitant squint</strong> from <strong>paralytic squint </strong>is a true statement. In a concomitant squint, the eye moves rapidly to fixate, whereas in a paralytic squint, the eye will not move to fixate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2055","text":"A patient presented to an ‍ophthalmologist with comp​laints of crowding of wor​ds, while reading for lon‌g hours. The results of t‌he test that was performe‌d are shown below What is‍ the most likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A patient presented to an ophthalmologist with complaints of crowding of words, while reading for long hours. The results of the test that was performed are shown below  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ortho‍phoria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypop‍horia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exop‍horia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Esop‍horia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Exophoria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bfc9dc7a319343e08ec56b87fac19d46.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/360f20f03bf64faba4b97155212a242cx1280x1103.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc7af5bfbbea467c87e12577ddfbf540x1280x1471.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a8f517dc08e4b6da208ca2d947ec5b8x1280x2324.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e448822529e4784bac46fdcdaa9d9cax1280x2108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Exophoria</strong></p><p>The given test is a <strong>Maddox rod test</strong>, and the pattern of the appearance of white dot/light in relation to the red line denotes a case of <strong>left eye exophoria</strong>. In the given image, the above image refers to the examiner's view and the below image refers to the patient's view. </p>\n<p>The Maddox rod can be used to test for horizontal and vertical deviations, and when used in conjunction with another Maddox rod, for cyclo-deviations.</p>\n<p>Interpretation<strong>: </strong></p>\n<p>To test for horizontal deviations, the Maddox rod is placed in front of one eye (for this example, <strong>the left eye</strong>) with the <strong>cylinders in the horizontal direction</strong>. The patient fixates a point source of light and then <strong>sees a vertical red line with the left eye </strong>and <strong>white dot/light with the right eye:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Orthophoria: if the light/dot superimposes the line.</li>\n<li><strong>Exo-deviation/exophoria: </strong><strong>In this example, </strong><strong>light/dot is on the left side of the line</strong> (rod placed on left eye)</li>\n<li>Eso-deviation/esophoria: if the light was on the right side of the line (rod placed on left eye)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maddox Rod Test </strong>involves the use of a <strong>Maddox rod</strong> and the <strong>Maddox tangent scale. </strong>It uses a specially constructed device, consisting of a series of parallel cylinders, that converts a point source of light into a line image. The optical properties of the cylinders cause the streak of light to be situated 90° to the orientation of the parallel cylinders.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In the image given below, the interpretation of the result is based on how the patient visualizes it. It would appear to be crossed to the patient. Pictorial representation of the eyes are as seen by the examiner. Imagine you are looking through a Maddox rod over your left eye while interpreting the images.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zLX8ic19","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"20_congenital_malformations__perthes_disease_and_scfe","title":"20.Congenital Malformations, Perthes Disease And Scfe","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB5893","text":"The radiograph of an infan​t with a hip deformity is‍ given below. There is no​ history of trauma. Which‌ of the following is not ‍a risk factor for develop​ing this condition?","raw_text":"The radiograph of an infant with a hip deformity is given below. There is no history of trauma. Which of the following is not a risk factor for developing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive fa​mily history","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breech pr​esentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Male ​gender","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"First bo​rn child","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Male gender","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9840f83af7fb4e87aa8cd434a7fdea76.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Male gender</strong></p><p>The above X-ray shows developmental dysplasia of the hip (<strong>DDH</strong>) on the left side. <strong>F</strong><strong>emale gender</strong> is a risk factor for DDH.</p>\n<p>The risk factors for DDH are remembered by <strong>4Fs</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>F</strong>emales</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>irstborn</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>aulty intrauterine position (breech)</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>amilial</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5901","text":"What is the vertical line ​drawn at the outer border‍ of the acetabulum called​ as?","raw_text":"What is the vertical line drawn at the outer border of the acetabulum called as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hilgenrei​ner's line","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perkin​'s line","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Shenton​'s line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klein'​s line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perkin's line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/341a5babc4c548d9af027c2d3431f9cdx1024x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f519aa958e824434896d1c2460df2b6ex1001x768.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/267dd521f1084c7ab21aa03977c1f4aex1280x1198.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perkin&#x27;s line</strong></p><p><strong>Perkin's line</strong> is a <strong>vertical</strong> line drawn at the outer border of the <strong>acetabulum</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Hilgenreiner's line</strong> is a <strong>horizontal</strong> line through the <strong>tri-radiate cartilages. </strong>Perkin's line is perpendicular to Hilgenreiner's line.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the vertical Perkin's line and the horizontal Hilgenreiner’s line. Normally the femoral head lies in the inner and lower quadrant formed by these lines. But in DDH the femoral head is in the upper and outer quadrant.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Shenton's line</strong> is a smooth curve formed by the inferior border of the neck of the femur with superior margin of the obturator foramen. It is broken in DDH and fractured neck of the femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Klein's line</strong> is a line along the superior aspect of the femoral neck which normally intersects the epiphysis. In slipped capital femoral epiphysis, Klein's line does not intersect the epiphysis and passes superior to it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5904","text":"A 3-month-old baby is brou​ght to the OPD by her mot‍her as she noticed some d​eformity in the hip and l‌ower limbs of the child. ‍On examination, asymmetry​ of groin crease is seen ‌and the following test in​ the image is positive. W‍hat is the name of the te‌st being performed?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old baby is brought to the OPD by her mother as she noticed some deformity in the hip and lower limbs of the child. On examination,  asymmetry of groin crease is seen and the following test in the image is positive. What is the name of the test being performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barlow​'s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ortolan​i's test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galeazz​i's test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Durkan​'s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Galeazzi's test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b71e76b835e540c4bcad73a544477ee2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Galeazzi&#x27;s test</strong></p><p><strong>Galeazzi's test</strong> is shown in the above image.</p>\n<p>It is done in children with <strong>unilateral cases</strong> of developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH) between 3 to 8 months of age.</p>\n<p>The child is placed supine with the hip and knee in flexion. The level of both the knee joints is noted with respect to a horizontal line. Normally both knees should be at the same level. In DDH, the affected knee is seen to be at a lower level indicating<strong> femoral shortening</strong>. This is also known as <strong>Alli's sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tests for DDH are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Barlow's test</li>\n<li>Ortolani's test</li>\n<li>Galeazzi's test</li>\n<li>Asymmetric skin fold test</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Durkan's test</strong> is the most <strong>specific</strong> test for the diagnosis of <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome</strong>. In this test, direct median nerve compression elicits paresthesias and pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5965","text":"Which of the following is ​unlikely to be seen in pa‍tients with DDH?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with DDH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Altere​d gait","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Limb sh​ortening","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased lu​mbar lordosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased​ abduction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased lumbar lordosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased lumbar lordosis</strong></p><p><strong>Increased</strong> lumbar <strong>lordosis</strong> is seen in developmental dysplasia of the hip (<strong>DDH</strong>).</p>\n<p>Other features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Limb shortening</li>\n<li>Decreased abduction and lateral rotation of the hip </li>\n<li>Altered gait\n<ul>\n<li>If unilateral - Trendelenburg's gait (body lurches to the affected side as the child bears weight on it)</li>\n<li>If bilateral - waddling gait (alternate lurching on both sides)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5969","text":"A mother with a history of​ breech delivery brings h‍er 1-month-old baby to th​e OPD. On examination, th‌e child has asymmetric th‍igh folds and Ortolani's ​test is positive. What sh‌ould be the immediate man​agement?","raw_text":"A mother with a history of breech delivery brings her 1-month-old baby to the OPD. On examination, the child has asymmetric thigh folds and Ortolani's test is positive. What should be the immediate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closed red​uction only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pavlik ​harness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hip ​spica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pemberton​ osteotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pavlik harness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/866f6ac630a144428ed7f2f5f4931653x1280x2010.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pavlik harness</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>developmental</strong> or congenital <strong>dysplasia</strong> of the <strong>hip </strong>(DDH)<strong>.  </strong>In a child of age <strong>&lt;6 months</strong>, closed reduction with <strong>Pavlik</strong> <strong>harness</strong> or Von Rosen splint is done.</p>\n<p>The Ortolani's test is a relocation manoeuvre. It is done by placing the infant <strong>supine</strong>, with the <strong>hip</strong> and <strong>knee</strong> <strong>flexed</strong>. With the thumb placed over the medial thigh and the index finger on the greater trochanter, the hip is gently abducted. This reduces the femoral head into the acetabulum producing a <strong>palpable</strong> \"<strong>clunk</strong>''. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5972","text":"A 1-year-old child is diag​nosed with DDH. Closed re‍duction is attempted but ​fails. What is the next s‌tep?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child is diagnosed with DDH. Closed reduction is attempted but fails. What is the next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total hip ​replacement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pelvic o​steotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Manipulation under anesthe​sia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Open re​duction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Open reduction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Open reduction</strong></p><p>The next step for a 1-year-child diagnosed with DDH, with failed closed reduction is<strong> open reduction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5975","text":"A 12-year-old boy is diagn​osed with DDH with second‍ary arthritic changes. Wh​at is the treatment of ch‌oice?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy is diagnosed with DDH with secondary arthritic changes. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Open re​duction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salter o​steotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total hip ​replacement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral ​osteotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total hip replacement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total hip replacement</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice for a case of DDH with<strong> secondary arthritic changes</strong> in a 12-year-old child is<strong> total hip replacement</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5815","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of osteochondrosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of osteochondrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frieberg'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kienbock'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kohler's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perthes​ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Perthes disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Perthes disease</strong></p><p>The most common type of osteochondrosis is <strong>Perthes disease. </strong>It is also called <strong>coxa plana</strong> or osteochondritis deformans juvenilis. </p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">It is </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\">a disorder causing avascular necrosis of the <strong>capital femoral</strong> <strong>epiphysis</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5824","text":"Which statement is true ab​out Perthes disease?","raw_text":"Which statement is true about Perthes disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Limitation of external rot​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More common​ in females","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The bone age is lower than​ the chronological age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patients present during ad​olescence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The bone age is lower than the chronological age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The bone age is lower than the chronological age</strong></p><p>The<strong> bone age </strong>is<strong> lower than</strong> the <strong>chronological age</strong> by 1–3 years. </p>\n<p>Since the blood supply of the femoral capital epiphysis is affected, it is characterized by avascular necrosis and disordered enchondral ossification of the primary and secondary centers of ossification.</p>\n<p>Perthes disease typically affects <strong>boys</strong> aged<strong> 4–8 years.</strong> Patients have short stature and limitation of abduction and internal rotation.</p>\n<p>When the hip is flexed it may go into obligatory external rotation called <strong>Catterall's sign.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5822","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about cox‍a vara?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about coxa vara?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The neck shaft angle is in​creased","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The greater trochanter is ​displaced upwards","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lengthening of limb is see​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Limitation of adduction an​d internal rotation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The greater trochanter is displaced upwards","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d340b1d85c774db6b08fa497b8bf68aax800x547.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/353e34632e344142bce264ad0dc9c9edx1080x634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The greater trochanter is displaced upwards</strong></p><p>The <strong>greater trochanter</strong> is <strong>displaced upwards</strong> in coxa vara.</p>\n<p>In coxa vara, there is a limitation of abduction and internal rotation. Trendelenburg's gait is seen.</p>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> femur<strong> neck-shaft angle</strong> (Mikulicz angle) is from<strong> 120-135 degrees.</strong></p>\n<p>Increases or decreases in the neck-shaft angle result in deformities. An angle <strong>&lt;120 </strong>degrees is called<strong> coxa vara. </strong>An angle <strong>&gt;135 </strong>degrees is called<strong> coxa valga.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The radiograph below shows the reduced angle between the head and shaft of the femur in coxa vara.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5862","text":"Which of the following mov​ements is not affected to‍ a great extent in slippe​d capital femoral epiphys‌is?","raw_text":"Which of the following movements is not affected to a great extent in slipped capital femoral epiphysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exte​nsion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Internal​ rotation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fle​xion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdu​ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4886f62c5fd544618b3dcd3246250943x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extension</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased</strong> <strong>flexion</strong> is seen in slipped capital femoral epiphysis <strong>(SCFE).</strong></p>\n<p>SCFE refers to the split fracture at the growth plate of epiphysis causing the neck of the femur to slip. The head remains in the acetabulum.</p>\n<p>SCFE occurs during the adolescent rapid growth period when the epiphyseal plate is weak. It is associated with <strong>endocrinopathies. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>more common in <strong>adolescent </strong><strong>boys.</strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased flexion</li>\n<li><strong>Restricted abduction</strong> and<strong> internal rotation</strong> of the<strong> hip</strong> presenting with difficulty in sitting cross-legged and squatting </li>\n<li>Positive<strong> knee axilla sign</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows SCFE before and after treatment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5873","text":"A 15-year old boy presents​ to you with limitation o‍f abduction and internal ​rotation of the left hip.‌ An X-ray of his left hip‍ joint is shown below. Wh​at is the sign seen?","raw_text":"A 15-year old boy presents to you with limitation of abduction and internal rotation of the left hip. An X-ray of his left hip joint is shown below. What is the sign seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trethowa​n's sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Catteral​l's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alli'​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dagge​r sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trethowan's sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe268963af9a449ca6c9a73e8ec6f54a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36d68f0d17ec4d609106778933139f66x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trethowan&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong>slipped capital femoral epiphysis (<strong>SCFE</strong>).<strong> </strong>The radiograph shows the<strong> Trethowan's sign.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>Klein line</strong> is a line drawn along the superior aspect of the femoral neck which normally intersects the epiphysis. In SCFE, Klein's line does not intersect the epiphysis and passes superior to it. This is known as Trethowan's sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Catterall's sign is seen in Perthes disease.</p>\n<p>Option C: Alli's sign is seen in DDH.</p>\n<p>Option D: Dagger sign is seen in ankylosing spondylitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5850","text":"In which position is the h​ip held in transient syno‍vitis?","raw_text":"In which position is the hip held in transient synovitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adduction and external rot​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adduction and internal rot​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abduction and external rot​ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abduction and internal rot​ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abduction and external rotation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abduction and external rotation</strong></p><p>Hip is held in <strong>abduction</strong> and <strong>external rotation</strong> in transient synovitis.</p>\n<p>In any type of synovitis, the hip joint is held in abduction and external rotation because in this position the joint capacity is maximum, to accommodate the effusion. Hence, the stretching due to effusion is minimal, thus causing less pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5604","text":"Which mutation is seen in ​patients with achondropla‍sia?","raw_text":"Which mutation is seen in patients with achondroplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FGF​R-3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RA​NK","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"COL​1A1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibri​llin-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. FGFR-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4bf090f47f324e728cf1691100effec9x510x658.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. FGFR-3</strong></p><p><strong>Achondroplasia </strong>is caused by a mutation of the fibroblast growth factor receptor-3 (<strong><em>FGFR-3</em></strong>) <strong>gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>The primary defect is <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>chondrocyte</strong> <strong>proliferation</strong> at the growth plate that causes the development of short and thick bones.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short limbs</li>\n<li>Normal trunk</li>\n<li>Large head</li>\n<li>Saddle nose</li>\n<li>Exaggerated lumbar lordosis</li>\n<li>Severe spinal deformity may lead to cord compression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The radiograph below shows champagne glass pelvis in achondroplasia.<strong> </strong>Bone softening leads to lateral indentation of acetabula. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5607","text":"The following finding is s​een in a patient. Which c‍ondition is this associat​ed with?","raw_text":"The following finding is seen in a patient. Which condition is this associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cleido crani​al dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Achondr​oplasia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Edward's​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Achondroplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/203d3273239b4056a4f6b5b68a338e7b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Achondroplasia</strong></p><p>The above picture depicts a <strong>s</strong><strong>tarfish hand</strong> seen in<strong> achondroplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>In these patients, the middle finger is shorter than usual resulting in all digits being of equal length (starfish hand). There is a separation between the middle and ring fingers and this is described as <strong>trident</strong> <strong>hands</strong>.</p>\n<p>Achondroplasia is the most common form of <strong>disproportionate dwarfism. </strong>Inheritance is <strong>autosomal dominant.</strong> Intelligence and sexual development are usually normal. Hands are short and broad and there is posterior scalloping of the vertebrae.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6007","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is ring-shaped‍ epiphysis seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is ring-shaped epiphysis seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteogenesis imperfecta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteogenesis imperfecta</strong></p><p><strong>Ring-shaped</strong> epiphysis is seen in <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteogenesis imperfecta, also known as<strong> brittle bone disease, </strong>is mostly inherited as <strong>a</strong><strong>utosomal dominant</strong>. There is a defect in the <strong>type I</strong> collagen <strong>COL1A1</strong> gene</p>\n<p>It is associated with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wormian bones</li>\n<li>Multiple fractures</li>\n<li>Deafness</li>\n<li>Blue sclera</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5616","text":"A male neonate was admitte​d to the NICU due to mult‍iple birth deformities. H​e had blue-grey sclera wi‌th a high arched palate. ‍The radiograph revealed f​ractures of the right hum‌erus, ulna, and femur wit​h osteopenia. Which of th‍e following is true about‌ this condition?","raw_text":"A male neonate was admitted to the NICU due to multiple birth deformities. He had blue-grey sclera with a high arched palate. The radiograph revealed fractures of the right humerus, ulna, and femur with osteopenia. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defective mi​neralisation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defective osteoclast activ​ity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defective osteoblast activ​ity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defective osteoid formatio​n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Defective osteoid formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Defective osteoid formation</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta.</strong> <strong>D</strong><strong>efective osteoid</strong> formation is seen in osteogenesis imperfercta.</p>\n<p><strong>Mutation</strong> in <strong>COL1A1 </strong>or <strong>COL1A2</strong> results in defective collagen I synthesis and defective osteoid formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5871","text":"A 13-year-old boy develope​d sudden pain and tendern‍ess over his left hip fol​lowing a minor fall. Ther‌e is restricted abduction‍ and internal rotation. H​e is a known case of hypo‌thyroidism and weighs 80k​g. What is the likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy developed sudden pain and tenderness over his left hip following a minor fall. There is restricted abduction and internal rotation.  He is a known case of hypothyroidism and weighs 80kg. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perthes​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D​DH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture ​femur neck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SC​FE","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. SCFE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. SCFE</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is<strong> </strong>slipped capital femoral epiphysis (<strong>SCFE</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Adolescent </strong>age group, <strong>hypothyroidism</strong>, <strong>obesity</strong>, sudden onset of pain after trauma with <strong>restricted abduction</strong>, <strong>internal rotation</strong> are suggestive of SCFE.</p>\n<p>Option A: The age of presentation in Perthes disease is 4-8 years.</p>\n<p>Option B: DDH is present since birth.</p>\n<p>Option C: Fracture neck of femur is usually seen in the 6th-7th decade of life.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5963","text":"Name the following test be​ing performed on a newbor‍n baby.","raw_text":"Name the following test being performed on a newborn baby.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Varus st​ress test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ortolan​i's test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barlow​'s test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ober'​s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Barlow's test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0da1050521746fb82d9509ff05bab47.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Barlow&#x27;s test</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Barlow's test.</strong></p>\n<p>It is done to test for <strong>Developmental dysplasia of hip (DDH).</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-star-inserted\">\n<div class=\"table-responsive font-weight-light answer-scroll ng-star-inserted\">\n<p>The Barlow's manouvre is a provocation test. This test is done within 2–3 days of birth. To perform this test, the infant is supine with the knees fully flexed and the hip at 90° of flexion. The thigh is grasped and the <strong>hip</strong> is gently <strong>adducted</strong> to 45 degrees while applying downward and lateral pressure. This results in a posterior subluxation or dislocation of an easily dislocatable femoral head, producing a palpable \"clunk\".</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-star-inserted\">\n<p class=\"ng-star-inserted\"><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">This test is</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> positive </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">when the joint can be</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> dislocated</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and a clunk is heard.</span></p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BOUozB_0","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"20_medical_gi_disorders","title":"20.Medical Gi Disorders","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA5203","text":"If you were to conduct a d‌eath audit on all the cas‌es of under-five paediatr​ic deaths secondary to in‌fective diarrhoea, which ​of the following pathogen‌s would be responsible fo‌r the largest fraction of‍ deaths?","raw_text":"If you were to conduct a death audit on all the cases of under-five paediatric deaths secondary to infective diarrhoea, which of the following pathogens would be responsible for the largest fraction of deaths?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adeno‌ virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rota‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salmonella ‌typhimurium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shigella d‌ysenteriae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rotavirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rotavirus</strong></p><p><strong>Rotavirus </strong>is the most common identifiable <strong>viral cause</strong> of gastroenteritis in children. It accounts for <strong>29% of deaths caused by diarrhoea</strong> in children aged <strong>&lt; 5 years.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5204","text":"A child from an urban slum‌ area is admitted to your‌ unit with diarrhea. Give​n below are five importan‌t micronutrients. The def​iciency of which of the f‌ollowing factors increase‌s the risk of death due t‍o diarrhea in this child?‍ Selenium Zinc Vitamin D ‌Vitamin A Iron","raw_text":"<p>A child from an urban slum area is admitted to your unit with diarrhea. Given below are five important micronutrients. The deficiency of which of the following factors increases the risk of death due to diarrhea in this child?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Selenium</li>\n<li>Zinc</li>\n<li>Vitamin D</li>\n<li>Vitamin A</li>\n<li>Iron</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 4 only</strong></p><p>The <strong>risk of death </strong>from <strong>diarrhoea </strong>is increased in the presence of deficiencies of micronutrients such as<strong> vitamin A, </strong>and <strong>zinc (2 and 4 only).</strong></p>\n<p>Both these micronutrients are essential for maintaining mucosal immunity. Vitamin A deficiency increases mortality from diarrhoea, measles, and malaria while Zinc deficiency increases mortality from diarrhoea, pneumonia, and malaria.</p>\n<p>Recent studies also indicate the usefulness of <strong>folic acid</strong> in the prevention of diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5207","text":"The Nutritionist in your h‌ospital prepares the diet‌ charts for all the child​ren admitted with diarrho‌eal disease. One of the a​dvice papers has fallen o‌ff from the case file. Th‌e advice sheet mentions t‍hat the child has to be p‍ut on fasting some times ‌of the day. You would att​ach the sheet back to the‍ case file with the diagn‌osis of ___________","raw_text":"The Nutritionist in your hospital prepares the diet charts for all the children admitted with diarrhoeal disease. One of the advice papers has fallen off from the case file. The advice sheet mentions that the child has to be put on fasting some times of the day. You would attach the sheet back to the case file with the diagnosis of ___________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Motility‌ diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infective‌ diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osmotic ‌diarrhea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secretory‌ diarrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osmotic diarrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osmotic diarrhea</strong></p><p><strong>Osmotic diarrhoea</strong> results from the presence of osmotically active, poorly absorbed solutes in the bowel lumen that inhibit normal water and electrolyte absorption. This type of diarrhoea <strong>ceases with fasting</strong>.</p>\n<p>Secretory diarrhoea, infective diarrhoea, dysentery and motility diarrhoea occurs independent of dietary intake and do not subside with fasting.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5206","text":"A 5-year-old child with ch‌ronic malnutrition presen‌ts with diarrheal episode​s lasting for more than 2‌ weeks. The stool sample ​is positive for Aeromonas‌ and entero-adherent Esch‌erichia coli. What is the‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child with chronic malnutrition presents with diarrheal episodes lasting for more than 2 weeks. The stool sample is positive for Aeromonas and entero-adherent Escherichia coli.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute d‌iarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Persisten‌t diarrhea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subacute‌ diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary‌ diarrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Persistent diarrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Persistent diarrhea</strong></p><p> <strong>Persistent diarrhoea(Chronic diarrhoea) </strong>is defined as a diarrhoeal episode that lasts for <strong>more than 14 days.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Diarrhoea is defined as</p>\n<ul>\n<li>stool volume of more than <strong>10 g/kg/day</strong> in toddlers/infants (or)</li>\n<li>stool volume greater than <strong>200 g/day</strong> in older children</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Persistent diarrhoea is increasingly observed in children with <strong>malnutrition. </strong>These are due to infections commonly caused due to <strong>enteroadherent </strong><em>Escherichia coli and Aeromonas.</em></p>\n<p>Acute diarrhoeas generally last less than <strong>7 days </strong>while chronic diarrhoea lasts more than <strong>14 days </strong>and is usually a <strong>non-infectious</strong> pathology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9277","text":"A young child had milk-bas‌ed dessert at his friend'‌s birthday party. Later, ​the same evening he devel‌oped complaints of bloati​ng, abdominal discomfort,‌ and flatulence. His medi‌cal history is significan‍t for viral gastroenterit‍is a couple of weeks back‌. Stool examination revea​ls an acidic pH and is po‍sitive for reducing subst‌ances. Which type of diar‍rhea is most likely to be‌ seen in this child?","raw_text":"A young child had milk-based dessert at his friend's birthday party. Later, the same evening he developed complaints of bloating, abdominal discomfort, and flatulence. His medical history is significant for viral gastroenteritis a couple of weeks back. Stool examination reveals an acidic pH and is positive for reducing substances. Which type of diarrhea is most likely to be seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osmotic ‌diarrhoea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Protracted‌ diarrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secretory‌ diarrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dyse‌ntry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osmotic diarrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osmotic diarrhoea</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>acquired lactose intolerance </strong>which<strong> </strong>is an example of <strong>osmotic diarrhoea</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lactose </strong>is present in milk and dairy products. If it is not absorbed in the small intestine, it reaches the colon, where it is fermented to short-chain organic acids. This releases hydrogen that can be detected in the lactose breath test and also generates an osmotic overload.</p>\n<p>The child with lactose intolerance generally has <strong>perianal excoriations</strong>. The condition is diagnosed by using stool examination which shows <strong>acidic stool positive for reducing substances</strong>. Treatment is by diet control using a diet devoid of lactose. It can be congenital due to <strong>lactase deficiency </strong>or <strong>acquired after post-viral gastroenteritis. </strong>In <strong>secretory diarrhoea</strong>, the epithelial cells’ ion transport processes are turned into a state of active secretion. The most common cause of acute-onset secretory diarrhoea is a <strong>bacterial infection</strong> of the gut.</p>\n<p>Chronic diarrhoea is, in many cases, the result of an intestinal infection that persists longer than expected.</p>\n<p><strong>Dysentery/Shigellosis</strong> is a type of food poisoning caused by infection with the <strong><em>Shigella</em></strong> species. It is spread by means of faeco-oral transmission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1172","text":"A 4-year-old child was bro‌ught to the hospital with‌ a history of loose stool​s. No history of fever or‌ blood in stools. The mot​her says he is irritable ‌and eagerly drinks water ‌if given. On examination,‍ eyes are sunken and in t‍he skin pinch test, the s‌kin retracted within two ​seconds but not immediate‍ly. What treatment is adv‌ised for the child?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child was brought to the hospital with a history of loose stools. No history of fever or blood in stools. The mother says he is irritable and eagerly drinks water if given. On examination, eyes are sunken and in the skin pinch test, the skin retracted within two seconds but not immediately. What treatment is advised for the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Give oral fluids and zinc ‌supplementation only and ‌ask mother to come back i​f danger sign develops.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Give oral fluids and ask m‌other to continue the sam‌e and visit again the nex​t day.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Consider severe dehydratio‌n start IV fluids, IV ant‌ibiotics, and refer to hi​gher centre.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wait and‌ observe.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Give oral fluids and zinc supplementation only and ask mother to come back if danger sign develops.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccf834b93a164f0a883f39b148f60c14x510x428.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1770e706ece14ad4b4ca586bcc0ad35bx1279x601.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Give oral fluids and zinc supplementation only and ask mother to come back if danger sign develops.</strong></p><p>In a child with diarrhea, <strong>thirst </strong>(eagerly drinks water), <strong>sunken eyes</strong>, <strong>restless and irritable</strong> behavior, and <strong>skin pinch</strong> showing <strong>slow</strong> retraction (<strong>&lt;2 seconds</strong>) point towards <strong>some dehydration. </strong>Treatment includes <strong>oral rehydration therapy, zinc supplementation, </strong>and<strong> continued breastfeeding</strong> according to WHO integrated management of neonatal and childhood diseases<strong> (IMNCI) plan B</strong>.</p>\n<p>The degree of dehydration is assessed and managed using the WHO IMNCI PLAN given below: </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5211","text":"Ramya, a 6-year-old child ‌with acute diarrhoea is a‌dmitted and started on fl​uid therapy with reduced ‌osmolarity Oral Rehydrati​on Solution . Which of th‌e following statement is ‌false about reduced osmol‍arity ORS?","raw_text":"Ramya, a 6-year-old child with acute diarrhoea is admitted and started on fluid therapy with reduced osmolarity Oral Rehydration Solution . Which of the following statement is false about reduced osmolarity ORS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose concentration 75 m‌mol/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium concentration 75 mm‌ol/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total Osmolarity is 311 mo‌sm/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Potassium concentration is‌ 20 mmol/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total Osmolarity is 311 mosm/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total Osmolarity is 311 mosm/L</strong></p><p>The <strong>total osmolarity</strong> of reduced osmolarity ORS is <strong>245 mOsm/L</strong> (not 311 mOsm/L)</p>\n<p>Reduced osmolarity ORS is now the global standard of care in the management of acute gastroenteritis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9621","text":"Sathish, your gym partner ‌and father of a 12-month-‌old child, calls you for ​a teleconsult. His child ‌has been passing loose wa​tery stools without fever‌. He wants to know the me‌dications to be given to ‍his child. What dose of o‍ral zinc would you prescr‌ibe to this child?","raw_text":"Sathish, your gym partner and father of a 12-month-old child, calls you for a teleconsult. His child has been passing loose watery stools without fever. He wants to know the medications to be given to his child. What dose of oral zinc would you prescribe to this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 mg/day fo‌r 10-14 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 mg/day fo‌r 10-14 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30 mg/day fo‌r 10-14 days.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 mg/day fo‌r 10-14 days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20 mg/day for 10-14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20 mg/day for 10-14 days</strong></p><p>All children older than 6 months of age with acute diarrhoea should receive oral zinc (<strong>20 mg/day</strong>) for <strong>10-14 days</strong> during and after diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>Most of the cases of under-five acute diarrhoea are infectious in aetiology with more than 50% due to the <strong>Rotavirus</strong>, which is self-limiting. Treatment will include fluid and electrolyte correction and zinc supplementation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5208","text":"In a child admitted with v‌iral enteritis along with‌ signs of dehydration, wh​ich type of acid-base imb‌alance is likely to be se​en?","raw_text":"In a child admitted with viral enteritis along with signs of dehydration, which type of acid-base imbalance is likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isotonic dehydration with ‌acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isotonic dehydration with ‌alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypotonic dehydration with‌ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypotonic dehydration with‌ alkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Isotonic dehydration with acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Isotonic dehydration with acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Isotonic dehydration with acidosis</strong> is the most common finding in children with severe viral enteritis.</p>\n<p>Viruses that cause human diarrhoea selectively infect and destroy the villus tip cells in the small intestine.</p>\n<p>In the small intestine, the <strong>upper villus enterocytes</strong> are differentiated cells, which have <strong>absorptive functions</strong>, such as the transport of water and electrolytes. On the other hand, the <strong>crypt enterocytes</strong> are undifferentiated cells and are <strong>net secretors</strong> of water and electrolytes.</p>\n<p>Selective viral infection of intestinal villus tip cells thus leads to <strong>decreased absorption of salt and water </strong>(proportionate loss of water and salt—<strong>isotonic dehydration</strong>) and an imbalance in the ratio of intestinal fluid absorption to secretion.</p>\n<p>Metabolic <strong>acidosis</strong> may be a result of stool bicarbonate losses in children with diarrhoea. These can also be seen due to secondary renal insufficiency or lactic acidosis  as a sequelae to diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5202","text":"The intestinal biopsy repo‌rt of a 7-year-old boy ev‌aluated for chronic water​y diarrhoea revealed a fl‌at intestinal mucosa. Whi​ch of the following condi‌tions would you suspect i‌n this child? Tropical sp‍rue Giardiasis Amoebiasis‍ HIV enteropathy Neutroph‌ilic gastroenteritis","raw_text":"<p>The intestinal biopsy report of a 7-year-old boy evaluated for chronic watery diarrhoea revealed a flat intestinal mucosa. Which of the following conditions would you suspect in this child?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tropical sprue</li>\n<li>Giardiasis</li>\n<li>Amoebiasis</li>\n<li>HIV enteropathy</li>\n<li>Neutrophilic gastroenteritis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, ‌4 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p>Chronic diarrhoea with a flat intestinal mucosa can be seen in conditions <strong>Tropical sprue, giardiasis, and HIV enteropathy. </strong><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Causes of the flat intestinal mucosa (subtotal or total villous atrophy):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Celiac disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tropical sprue</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Giardiasis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bacterial overgrowth</strong></li>\n<li>HIV enteropathy</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic gastroenteritis</li>\n<li>Cow’s milk or soy protein enteropathy</li>\n<li>Graft versus host disease</li>\n<li>Chemo or radiotherapy</li>\n<li>Protein-energy malnutrition</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis or lymphoma</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Amoebiasis and neutrophilic gastroenteritis may also rarely have villous atrophy but the presentation is generally acute. Amoebiasis is characteristically associated with <strong>dysentery</strong> and biopsy would reveal a <strong>flask-shaped</strong> ulcer. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5200","text":"Suyash, a 10-year-old boy ‌presented to the clinic w‌ith a history of malabsor​ption, diarrhoea, and poo‌r appetite after having h​is favourite white sauce ‌pasta. His mother adds th‌at he develops such sympt‍oms only when he takes pa‍sta or roti. The duodenal‌ biopsy revealed crypt hy​perplasia and CD8+ cells ‍in lamina propria. Which ‌of the following tests is‍ least likely to be used ‌for the diagnosis of the ​condition?","raw_text":"Suyash, a 10-year-old boy presented to the clinic with a history of malabsorption, diarrhoea, and poor appetite after having his favourite white sauce pasta. His mother adds that he develops such symptoms only when he takes pasta or roti. The duodenal biopsy revealed crypt hyperplasia and CD8+ cells in lamina propria. Which of the following tests is least likely to be used for the diagnosis of the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-gliadi‌n antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-nuclea‌r antibodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-endomysial antibodies‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-tissue transglutamina‌se antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-nuclear antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-nuclear antibodies</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>celiac disease </strong>and an <strong>anti-nuclear antibody test</strong> is not used in the diagnosis of celiac disease.</p>\n<p>Symptoms are pre-dominantly seen after consuming a diet rich in gluten (Wheat pasta or roti). Diagnosis of celiac disease is made based on lab and histopathological findings. </p>\n<p>Celiac disease-specific antibodies are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-<strong>tissue transglutaminase (Anti-TG2)</strong> IgA antibodies</li>\n<li>Anti deaminated - IgG forms of <strong>gliadin</strong> peptides antibodies</li>\n<li><strong>Endomysial </strong>antibodies (EMA)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The duodenal biopsy will show <strong>crypt hyperplasia, villous atrophy</strong> and <strong>CD8+</strong> cells in lamina propria</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5198","text":"A 9-year-old boy presented‌ with vomiting for 5 days‌. His sister, who has coe​liac disease had similar ‌symptoms. IgA-tissue tran​sglutaminase titre is 5 t‌imes the upper limit of n‌ormal. Which of the follo‍wing is not an extraintes‍tinal manifestation of th‌is condition?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy presented with vomiting for 5 days. His sister, who has coeliac disease had similar symptoms. IgA-tissue transglutaminase titre is 5 times the upper limit of normal. Which of the following is not an extraintestinal manifestation of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycy‌themia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteop‌orosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Short ‌stature","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alop‌ecia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polycythemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polycythemia</strong></p><p>The boy is suffering from <strong>coeliac disease</strong>. <strong>Polycythemia</strong> is not an extraintestinal manifestation of coeliac disease.</p>\n<p>On the contrary, the <strong>most common</strong> extraintestinal manifestation of celiac disease in the paediatric age group is <strong>iron-deficiency anaemia</strong> that is<strong> </strong>refractory to iron therapy, and not polycythemia.</p>\n<p>Celiac disease is an <strong>immune-mediated systemic disorder</strong> triggered by the <strong>ingestion of wheat gluten</strong> and related prolamines from rye and barley.</p>\n<p>The <strong>i</strong><strong>ntestinal symptoms</strong> are caused by the <strong>a</strong><strong>trophy of the small bowel mucosa</strong> and malabsorption. The symptoms include diarrhoea, vomiting, anorexia, weight loss, failure to thrive, rectal prolapse, and intussusception.</p>\n<p>The <strong>extraintestinal manifestations</strong> are caused due to malnutrition and autoimmunity and they include the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 365px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p><strong>System</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 119px;\">\n<p><strong>Manifestation</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p><strong>Possible Cause</strong> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Hematologic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Anaemia (Most common extraintestinal manifestation)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p>Iron malabsorption</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Skeletal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Rickets</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n<li>Enamel hypoplasia of the teeth</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p>Calcium/vitamin D malabsorption</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Muscular</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Atrophy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p>Malnutrition</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Neurologic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Cerebral calcifications</li>\n<li>Cerebellar ataxia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p>Thiamine/vitamin B12 deficiency</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Endocrinologic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Short stature</li>\n<li>Secondary hyperparathyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p>Malnutrition calcium/vitamin D</p>\n<p>malabsorption</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Dermatologic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatitis herpetiformis</li>\n<li>Alopecia areata </li>\n<li>Erythema nodosum</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\">\n<p>Autoimmunity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 91px;\">\n<p>Respiratory</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 114px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5222","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‌pancreatic insufficiency ​in childhood?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of pancreatic insufficiency in childhood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic pa‌ncreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital pancreatic lipa‌se deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cystic fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cystic fibrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic fibrosis </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>pancreatic insufficiency in children, </strong>which, if untreated, will lead to malnutrition.</p>\n<p>Other rare causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shwachman–Diamond syndrome</li>\n<li>Isolated enzyme deficiencies</li>\n<li>Chronic pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Protein-energy malnutrition</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5223","text":"A 12-year-old boy presente‌d to the emergency depart‌ment with fever, severe e​pigastric pain, nausea an‌d vomiting for 6 hours. H​e feels comfortable when ‌he bends forward and assu‌mes a knee-chest position‍. Which of the following ‍tests is not useful in th‌e diagnosis of acute panc​reatitis?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presented to the emergency department with fever, severe epigastric pain, nausea and vomiting for 6 hours. He feels comfortable when he bends forward and assumes a knee-chest position. Which of the following tests is not useful in the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum ‌lipase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum ‌amylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum e‌lastase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CT ab‌domen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum elastase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum elastase</strong></p><p><strong>Serum elastase</strong> is not used for the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis. The measurement of <strong>faecal elastase</strong> is used as a screening test for <strong>pancreatic insufficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>The criteria for the diagnosis of pancreatitis in children is defined as 2 of 3 of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Epigastric abdominal pain;</li>\n<li><strong>Serum amylase and/or lipase activity at least 3 times greater</strong> than the upper limit of normal; and</li>\n<li>Imaging findings characteristic of, or compatible with, acute pancreatitis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Serum lipase</strong> is now considered the <strong>test of choice</strong> for acute pancreatitis as it is more specific than amylase.</p>\n<p>CT scan has a major role in the diagnosis and follow-up of children with pancreatitis. Other useful investigations are USG, ERCP, and MRCP.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5142","text":"Anwesha, an orator prepari‌ng for her international ‌debate finale presents to​ your clinic with lesions‌ on her lower lip as show​n below. She is concerned‌ as these lesions are pai‌nful, recurrent and hampe‍r the flow of her speech.‍ Which of the following p‌oints about this conditio​n is incorrect?","raw_text":"Anwesha, an orator preparing for her international debate finale presents to your clinic with lesions on her lower lip as shown below. She is concerned as these lesions are painful, recurrent and hamper the flow of her speech. Which of the following points about this condition is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"These are d‌ue to stress","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"These are ‌contagious","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They heal without scarring‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are seen in Gluten-se‌nsitive enteropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. These are contagious","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dc9452a43dd74b1da418dfe7402c26bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. These are contagious</strong></p><p>The lesions given in the above image are <strong>aphthous ulcers</strong>, and they are not<strong> contagious</strong>. All the other points are true. </p>\n<p>Aphthous ulcers are painful, recurrent, and <strong>non-contagious</strong> oral lesions. They are well-circumscribed, ulcerative lesions with a white necrotic base surrounded by a red halo. They heal in 10–14 days <strong>without scarring</strong>. Their aetiology is unclear but the following factors have been implicated:</p>\n<ul>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Allergic or immunologic reactions</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Emotional stress</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Genetics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Injury to soft tissues in the mouth</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Gluten-sensitive enteropathy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Behcet disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sweet syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cyclical neutropenia</li>\n</ol>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment generally includes local anaesthetic to relieve pain and B-complex supplementation to enhance healing.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5162","text":"The nanny of a 3-year-old ‌boy brings the child to t‌he ER after he had accide​ntally ingested button ba‌tteries from the mobile p​hone toy that he was play‌ing with. What would be t‌he next best step in the ‍management of this child?‍","raw_text":"The nanny of a 3-year-old boy brings the child to the ER after he had accidentally ingested button batteries from the mobile phone toy that he was playing with. What would be the next best step in the management of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urgent endoscopy and remov‌al","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Observation and follow up ‌in 24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Observation until the fore‌ign body passes out in fa‌eces","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repeat Xray ‌after 4 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Urgent endoscopy and removal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Urgent endoscopy and removal</strong></p><p><strong>Button batteries </strong>and <strong>sharp objects</strong> in the oesophagus and foreign bodies associated with <strong>respiratory symptoms</strong> all mandate <strong>urgent endoscopic removal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Button batteries, in particular, are very dangerous foreign bodies, which must be expediently removed because they can cause:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mucosal injury</strong> in 1-hour contact time</li>\n<li><strong>Involve all layers</strong> of the oesophagus in 4 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Injury is thought to be secondary to electrochemical burns from electrical discharge.<strong> Chemical burns</strong> from <strong>electrolyte alkali leakage</strong> and <strong>pressure necrosis</strong> also may contribute to mucosal injury.</p>\n<p>Asymptomatic blunt objects and coins lodged in the oesophagus can be observed for up to 24 hr in anticipation of passage into the stomach.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nHQip4cc","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"20_basics_of_hemolysis_and_intravascular_hemolysis","title":"20.Basics Of Hemolysis And Intravascular Hemolysis","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0302","text":"Match the types of hemoglo‌bin with their globin cha​ins. Hemoglobin Globin ch‍ains 1. HbF A. α and β 2.‌ HbA B. α and δ 3. HbA2 C​. α and γ","raw_text":"<p>Match the types of hemoglobin with their globin chains.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 167px;\" width=\"326\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\"><strong>Hemoglobin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\"><strong>Globin chains</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">1. HbF</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">A. α and β</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">2. HbA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">B. α and δ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">3. HbA2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">C. α and γ</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-B, 2‌-A, 3-C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-C, 2‌-A, 3-B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-C, 2‌-B, 3-A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-A, 2‌-B, 3-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-C, 2-A, 3-B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58cda96961ec4c02aedddc913b5529a5x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-C, 2-A, 3-B</strong></p><p>The types of hemoglobin along with their globin chains are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 167px;\" width=\"326\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\"><strong>Hemoglobin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\"><strong>Components</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">HbF</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">α2γ2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">HbA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">α2β2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">HbA2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">α2δ2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows the graphical representation of the globin chain synthesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0287","text":"Enzyme deficiency in which‌ of the following pathway​s can cause hemolysis?","raw_text":"Enzyme deficiency in which of the following pathways can cause hemolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orotic ac‌id pathway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glyco‌lysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fatty acid‌ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyol ‌pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glycolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glycolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Enzyme deficiency</strong> in the <strong>glycolytic pathway</strong> can cause <strong>hemolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following enzyme deficiencies can cause hemolysis:</p>\n<p>In the <strong>glycolytic</strong> pathway:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Pyruvate kinase deficiency</li>\n<li>Hexokinase deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the <strong>HMP shunt</strong> pathway:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G6PD deficiency</li>\n<li>Glutathione synthetase deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Orotic aciduria causes megaloblastic anaemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0289","text":"What is the normal reticul‌ocyte count?","raw_text":"What is the normal reticulocyte count?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5–‌1.5%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2.5‌–5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5–‌10%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1.5–‌2.5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.5–1.5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.5–1.5%</strong></p><p>Normal <strong>reticulocyte</strong> <strong>count</strong> is <strong>0.5–1.5%</strong> of total red cells. </p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>increased</strong> reticulocyte count:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemolytic anaemia</li>\n<li>Acute and chronic blood loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>decreased</strong> reticulocyte count:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iron deficiency anaemia</li>\n<li>Megaloblastic anaemia</li>\n<li>Aplastic anaemia</li>\n<li>Anaemia of chronic diseases</li>\n<li>Myelodysplastic syndromes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0403","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about re​ticulocytes?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about reticulocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are ‌nucleated","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They contain ribosomal RNA‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are larger in size th‌an red cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Their normal count is 0.5-‌1.5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. They are nucleated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/828c0cc5c2f64206a6a2d2653a3117a8x540x539.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They are nucleated</strong></p><p><strong>Reticulocytes</strong> are <strong>red cell precursors</strong>, which are non-nucleated. </p>\n<p>They have <strong>blue-red</strong> polychromatophilic <strong>cytoplasm</strong> due to the presence of a reticular (mesh-like) network of ribosomal RNA. They can be visualized using <strong>supravital stains</strong> like methylene blue and brilliant cresyl blue, which stain the ribosomal RNA. </p>\n<p><strong>Reticulocytosis</strong> (increased reticulocytes) indicates <strong>effective erythropoiesis</strong> i.e. good bone marrow response to anaemia. It is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemolytic anaemias</li>\n<li>Acute blood loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reticulocytopenia</strong> (decreased reticulocytes) indicates <strong>ineffective erythropoiesis</strong> i.e. poor bone marrow response to anaemia. It is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chemotherapy</li>\n<li>Aplastic anaemia</li>\n<li>Bone marrow malignancy</li>\n<li>Anaemia of chronic disease</li>\n<li>Iron-deficiency anaemia</li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The peripheral smear shows reticulocytosis and spherocytosis seen in non-Hodgkin lymphoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0413","text":"A 25-year-old woman had se‌vere blood loss during de​livery 2-days ago. Her cu‍rrent hemoglobin is 5 gm/‌dL and reticulocyte count​ is 9%. What is her corre​cted reticulocyte count?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman had severe blood loss during delivery 2-days ago. Her current hemoglobin is 5 gm/dL and reticulocyte count is 9%. What is her corrected reticulocyte count?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0‌.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the corrected reticulocyte count is <strong>3</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is given by the following formula:</p>\n<p><strong>Corrected reticulocyte count</strong> = Reticulocyte count × (patient's hemoglobin or hematocrit ⁄ standard hemoglobin or hematocrit)</p>\n<p>So, in this case, the corrected reticulocyte count = 9×5/15  = 9/3  = 3</p>\n<p>This formula corrects for hemoglobin. It will show you if the patient is making enough reticulocytes for the degree of anemia present. As the hemoglobin drops, reticulocytes increase from the normal range of 0.5–2.0%.</p>\n<p>The standard reference range of hemoglobin concentration in females is 12-15 g/dl. Accordingly, in this scenario, corrected reticulocyte count would range between 3-3.75%. So, the best answer to this question remains option C.</p>\n<p><strong>Absolute reticulocyte count </strong>is the percentage of red cells that are reticulocytes (normal is about 0.5 – 2.0%). However, it does not reflect the fact that as the hemoglobin drops, there should be more reticulocytes, percentage-wise (for example, at a hemoglobin of 5, you should have a much higher percentage of reticulocytes than you should at a hemoglobin of 10). To take this into account, we use corrected reticulocyte count.</p>\n<p><strong>Reticulocyte Index</strong> = Corrected reticulocyte count / maturation factor</p>\n<p>This corrects for the longer life span of prematurely released reticulocytes in the blood which is a phenomenon of increased red blood cell production.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 142px;\" width=\"350\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Hematocrit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Maturation factor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">≥ 35%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">1.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">35% &gt; Hct ≥ 25%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">1.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">25% &gt; Hct ≥ 20%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">2.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">&lt; 20%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">2.5 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0408","text":"In a patient with microcyt‌ic hypochromic anaemia, r​ed cell distribution widt‍h was measured. What is t‌his parameter used to ide​ntify?","raw_text":"In a patient with microcytic hypochromic anaemia, red cell distribution width was measured. What is this parameter used to identify?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anisoc‌ytosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Poikilo‌cytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anisoc‌hromia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bicon‌cavity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anisocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anisocytosis</strong></p><p>Red cell distribution width (<strong>RDW</strong>) deals with <strong>anisocytosis</strong> of RBCs. It refers to the <strong>unequal size</strong> of the RBCs.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal values</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>RDW-SD (Standard deviation) - 39-46 fL</li>\n<li>RDW-CV (Coefficient of variation) - 11.6-14.6%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RDW is of great <strong>use</strong> in two clinical situations. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To <strong>differentiate</strong> between <strong>thalassemia</strong> and iron deficiency anaemia (<strong>IDA</strong>)\n<ul>\n<li>Thalassemia - the RBCs are small (low MCV) and of uniform size (normal RDW)</li>\n<li>IDA - the RBCs are small (low MCV) but vary greatly in size (high RDW).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>To <strong>diagnose</strong> <strong>dimorphic</strong> anaemia (anaemia due to both iron and vitamin B12 deficiency)<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>High RDW</li>\n<li>Normal MCV</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0288","text":"Which of the following is ‌not increased in intravas​cular hemolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not increased in intravascular hemolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactate de‌hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Free he‌moglobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methem‌oglobin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hapto‌globin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Haptoglobin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4d65a90fa944128bade078a3cb65243x1280x2389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Haptoglobin</strong></p><p><strong>Haptoglobin</strong> is <strong>decreased</strong> in intravascular hemolysis.</p>\n<p>Types of hemolysis based on location:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intravascular hemolysis - hemolysis that occurs mainly within the blood vessels</li>\n<li>Extravascular hemolysis - hemolysis that occurs via macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the spleen, liver, bone marrow, and lymph nodes</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of <strong>intravascular</strong> hemolysis are:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Decreased haptoglobin</li>\n<li>Increased lactate dehydrogenase </li>\n<li>Hemoglobinemia</li>\n<li>Hemoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Hemosiderinuria</li>\n<li>Methemoglobinemia</li>\n<li>Methemoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Indirect hyperbilirubinemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0290","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause intravascular​ hemolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause intravascular hemolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni‌c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thala‌ssemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thalassemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thalassemia</strong></p><p><strong>Thalassemia</strong> causes <strong>extravascular</strong> hemolysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of<strong> intravascular </strong>hemolysis<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW225898103\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW225898103\">Mechanical injury</span></span>\n<ul>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"Calibri, sans-serif\" data-leveltext=\"%1.\" data-listid=\"2\">Trauma caused by cardiac valves </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1.\" data-listid=\"2\">Microangiopathic hemolytic anaemias (hemolytic uremic syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, <strong>thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura </strong>) </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1.\" data-listid=\"2\">Repetitive physical trauma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW46333709\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW46333709\">Complement fixation (antibody-mediated destruction)</span></span>\n<ul>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"Calibri, sans-serif\" data-leveltext=\"%1.\" data-listid=\"3\">Hemolytic disease of the newborn </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-font=\"Calibri, sans-serif\" data-leveltext=\"%1.\" data-listid=\"3\">Transfusion reaction  </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"4\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1.\" data-listid=\"3\">Drug-induced </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW127641793\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW127641793\"><strong>Infections</strong> of red blood cells </span></span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW127641793\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW127641793\">Malaria </span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW127641793\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW127641793\">B</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW127641793\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW127641793\">abesiosis</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span class=\"EOP SCXW127641793\" data-ccp-props='{\"134233279\":true,\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'><span class=\"TextRun SCXW261552469\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW261552469\">Exogenous toxic factors - </span></span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW127641793\" data-ccp-props='{\"134233279\":true,\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'><span class=\"TextRun SCXW261552469\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW261552469\">C</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW261552469\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW261552469\">lostridial</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW261552469\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW261552469\"> sepsis</span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Causes </strong>of<strong> extravascular </strong>hemolysis<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hereditary spherocytosis</li>\n<li><strong>Thalassemia</strong></li>\n<li>Sickle cell anaemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of <strong>both</strong> intravascular and extravascular:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G6PD deficiency</li>\n<li>Autoimmune hemolytic anaemia</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0319","text":"Which of the following pai‌rs of mutation and involv​ed genes describes paroxy‍smal nocturnal hemoglobin‌uria?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of mutation and involved genes describes paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AD inherit‌ance - UMOD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acquired genetic mutation ‌- PIGA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AR inherit‌ance - MUC1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acquired genetic mutation ‌- GPI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acquired genetic mutation - PIGA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0dff4dfe202446c9bbd0e9054561cd6cx1280x2392.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acquired genetic mutation - PIGA</strong></p><p>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (<strong>PNH</strong>) is a disease that results from <strong>acquired </strong>mutations in the phosphatidylinositol glycan complementation group A gene (<strong>PIGA</strong>).</p>\n<p>This gene codes for an enzyme that is essential for the <strong>synthesis</strong> of certain membrane-associated <strong>complement</strong> <strong>regulatory</strong> <strong>proteins</strong>. It is the only hemolytic anaemia caused by an acquired genetic defect.</p>\n<p><strong>PNH</strong> blood cells are <strong>deficient</strong> in <strong>3 GPI-linked proteins</strong> that regulate complement activity:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Decay Accelerating Factor or <strong>CD55</strong></li>\n<li>Membrane Inhibitor of Reactive Lysis or <strong>CD59</strong></li>\n<li><strong>C8-binding protein</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CD59,</strong> a potent <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>C3 convertase,</strong> <strong>prevents</strong> the spontaneous activation of the <strong>alternative complement pathway</strong>. Red cells deficient in these GPI-linked factors are abnormally susceptible to lysis or injury by C5b-C9 membrane attack complex of the complement system. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0380","text":"Which of the following cel‌ls are involved in paroxy​smal nocturnal hemoglobin‍uria?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells are involved in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RB‌Cs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RBCs a‌nd WBCs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"WBCs and ‌platelets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RBCs, WBCs a‌nd platelets","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. RBCs, WBCs and platelets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. RBCs, WBCs and platelets</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">All three cell lines, <strong>RBCs</strong>, <strong>WBCs</strong>, and <strong>platelets</strong> are <strong>affected</strong> in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (<strong>PNH</strong>).</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">PNH occurs from acquired mutations </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">in the gene </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">PIGA which affect GPI-linked proteins. T</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">hese <strong>causative</strong> <strong>mutations</strong> occur in a <strong>hematopoietic</strong> <strong>stem</strong> <strong>cell</strong>. Hence,</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\"> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">all</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\"> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">of its clonal progeny,</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\"> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">i.e., red cells, white cells, and platelets, </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW223356147\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW223356147\">are deficient in GPI-linked proteins.</span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clinical features</strong> of PNH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Intravascular hemoglobinuria - cola-colored urine</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pancytopenia + hypercellular bone marrow</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Thrombosis (if hepatic vein involved → Budd–Chiari syndrome)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2430","text":"A 23-year-old man with pan‌cytopenia is suspected to​ have PNH. Which of the f‍ollowing findings is inco‌nsistent with this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man with pancytopenia is suspected to have PNH. Which of the following findings is inconsistent with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased‌ LAP score","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Throm‌bosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased m‌ethemoglobin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased LAP score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased LAP score</strong></p><p>In <strong>PNH</strong>, the leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (<strong>LAP</strong>) <strong>score</strong> is <strong>decreased.</strong></p>\n<p>Alkaline phosphatase activity is found predominantly in <strong>mature segmented neutrophils</strong>. LAP score is obtained by assessing the <strong>staining intensity</strong> in 100 consecutive neutrophils, with each neutrophil scored on a scale of 1–4. <strong><span class=\"hvr\">Conditions</span></strong> <span class=\"hvr\">that</span> <span class=\"hvr\">result</span> in an <strong><span class=\"hvr\">increase</span></strong> in <strong><span class=\"hvr\">leukocytes</span></strong> in <span class=\"hvr\">all</span> <span class=\"hvr\">stages</span> of <span class=\"hvr\">maturity</span> <span class=\"hvr\">will</span> <span class=\"hvr\">reflect</span> a <span class=\"hvr\">corresponding</span> <strong><span class=\"hvr\">increase</span></strong> in <span class=\"hvr\"><strong>LAP</strong>.</span></p>\n<table style=\"height: 235px;\" width=\"322\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 163.453px; height: 44px;\"><span class=\"hvr\"><strong>Increased LAP</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.547px; height: 44px;\"><strong><span class=\"hvr\">Decreased LAP</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 121.297px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 163.453px; height: 121.297px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">Leukemoid reaction</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">Pregnancy</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">Polycythemia vera</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">Hodgkin's disease</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.547px; height: 121.297px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">CML</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">PNH</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hvr\">Sickle cell anemia</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0320","text":"Which of the following is ‌most likely the cause of ​death in a patient with p‍aroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most likely the cause of death in a patient with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sep‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Budd-Chiar‌i syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemor‌rhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Budd-Chiari syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong></p><p>Among the following,<strong> Budd-Chiari Syndrome</strong> is the <strong>most likely</strong> the cause of death in a patient with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH).</p>\n<p><strong>Thrombosis</strong> (particularly venous thrombosis) is the <strong>leading cause</strong> of <strong>disease-related death</strong> in patients with PNH. 40% of PNH patients suffer from <strong>venous thrombosis</strong>, which commonly affects the<strong> hepatic</strong>, <strong>portal</strong>, or <strong>cerebral veins</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pathophysiology of thrombosis in PNH is not completely understood, but two main mechanisms may be responsible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nitric oxide (NO) is absorbed by free hemoglobin (which is released during hemolysis).</li>\n<li>Endothelial damage caused by the C5-9 membrane attack complex (due to deficiency of CD59).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0321","text":"Flow cytometric analysis i‌n a patient with pancytop​enia shows a deficiency o‍f GPI-linked proteins on ‌blood cells. Which of the​ following is not a compl​ication of this condition​?","raw_text":"Flow cytometric analysis in a patient with pancytopenia shows a deficiency of GPI-linked proteins on blood cells. Which of the following is not a complication of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep vein ‌thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cryoglob‌ulinemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myelo‌id leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelodysplas‌tic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cryoglobulinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cryoglobulinemia</strong></p><p>The <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>GPI linked proteins</strong> on blood cells is seen in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria <strong>(PNH)</strong>. Cryoglobulinemia is not a complication of this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Thrombosis</strong> is the leading cause of disease-related death in individuals with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH). About 5% to 10% of patients eventually develop acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>) or <strong>myelodysplastic syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0374","text":"What is the gold standard ‌for the diagnosis of paro​xysmal nocturnal hemoglob‍inuria?","raw_text":"What is the gold standard for the diagnosis of paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ham ‌test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bone marr‌ow biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HP‌LC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flow cy‌tometry","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flow cytometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flow cytometry</strong></p><p>The <strong>gold standard</strong> for the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of <strong>PNH</strong> is <strong>flow cytometry</strong>. It provides a sensitive means for detecting red cells that are deficient in GPI-linked proteins such as CD59.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> in PNH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flow cytometry </strong>(gold standard) - deficiency of CD59 and CD55</li>\n<li><strong>Ham test</strong> (acidified serum lysis) - lysis of RBCs in a mildly acidic pH due to complement fixation</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow biopsy </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercellularity with erythroid hyperplasia is seen in classical presentations</li>\n<li>Patients who develop pancytopenia will have hypocellular bone marrow</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Imaging - to evaluate venous thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0377","text":"A 37-year-old man presents‌ with a history of dark c​oloured urine in the morn‍ing for 2 months. His lab‌ investigations and flow ​cytometry are given below​. What is the curative th​erapy for this condition?‌ Hb level - 3 g/dL RBC - ‌3 million/mm 3 TLC - 1500​ /μL Platelet - 15,000 /μ​L","raw_text":"<p>A 37-year-old man presents with a history of dark coloured urine in the morning for 2 months. His lab investigations and flow cytometry are given below. What is the curative therapy for this condition?</p>\n<p>Hb level - 3 g/dL</p>\n<p>RBC - 3 million/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>TLC - 1500 /μL</p>\n<p>Platelet - 15,000 /μL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eculi‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunosup‌pressants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blood tr‌ansfusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hematopoietic stem cell tr‌ansplant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7cc11a7646f4d32b87eb44ade0ac2a8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>PNH</strong> (paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria). The <strong>curative </strong>therapy for this condition is a <strong>hematopoietic stem cell transplant</strong>.  </p>\n<p><strong>Pancytopenia</strong> is seen in this condition. <strong>Flow cytometry</strong> shows 2 populations of red cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marked <strong>red</strong> in the left lower quadrant - <strong>deficient</strong> in both <strong>CD55</strong> and <strong>CD59</strong>, characteristic of <strong>PNH.</strong></li>\n<li>Marked <strong>black</strong> in the right upper quadrant - <strong>CD55+</strong> and <strong>CD59+</strong> cells from the residual normal hematopoietic cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Eculizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody that can be used in PNH. It prevents the conversion of C5 to C5a and reduces hemolysis.<br/><strong>Immunosuppressive</strong> <strong>drugs</strong> are sometimes beneficial for those with evidence of marrow aplasia.</p>\n<p>Note: FDA has approved the following drugs for treatment of adults with PNH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ravulizumab</strong> injection, a long-acting C-5 complement inhibitor</li>\n<li><strong>Pegcetacoplan</strong>, which acts by binding to complement protein C3 and increasing hemoglobin concentration</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iv52JMJ2","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"20_anti_epileptics_ii","title":"20.Anti Epileptics Ii","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MC1578","text":"Which of the following ant​i-epileptics is an AMPA g​lutamate receptor antagon​ist?","raw_text":"Which of the following anti-epileptics is an AMPA glutamate receptor antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leveti​racetam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brivar​acetam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peram​panel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rufin​amide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perampanel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perampanel</strong></p><p><strong>Perampanel </strong>is a selective non-competitive<strong> antagonist of the AMPA-type glutamate receptor.</strong> It is approved for adjunctive therapy of <strong>focal-onset</strong> seizures.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Levetiracetam and brivaracetam act on SV2A proteins and modulates GABA and glutamate release.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rufinamide acts on voltage-gated sodium channels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2264","text":"Which of the following mec​hanisms of action is obse​rved with levetiracetam?","raw_text":"Which of the following mechanisms of action is observed with levetiracetam?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GABA transaminase inhibiti​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GABA transporter inhibitio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Binding to synaptic vesicu​lar protein SV2A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of neuronal sod​ium channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Binding to synaptic vesicular protein SV2A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Binding to synaptic vesicular protein SV2A</strong></p><p><strong>Levetiracetam </strong>has a binding affinity to synaptic vesicular<strong> protein SV2A. </strong>SV2A plays a role in vesicle recycling following exocytosis of neurotransmitter</p>\n<p>When levetiracetam bind to this synaptic vesicular protein, it decreases the <strong>release</strong> of excitatory neurotransmitter <strong>glutamate</strong> thus <strong>preventing seizures.</strong> </p>\n<p>Levetiracetam also <strong>inhibits N-type Ca2+ channels</strong> and Ca2+ release from intracellular stores.Thus, it is considered a <strong>broad-spectrum</strong> antiepileptic as it has an effect on multiple channels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9512","text":"A known epileptic patient ​on levetiracetam has been​ seizure-free for 2 years​. But he develops agitati‌on and anger issues inter​fering with his day-to-da‌y activities as a result ​of the drug intake. Which‌ of the following measure‍s is to be taken next?","raw_text":"A known epileptic patient on levetiracetam has been seizure-free for 2 years. But he develops agitation and anger issues interfering with his day-to-day activities as a result of the drug intake. Which of the following measures is to be taken next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slowly taper the drug over​ next 6 months","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Continue levetriacetam sin​ce a 5 year seizure free ​interval is needed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop levetiracetam and sta​rt on a different anti-ep​ileptic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discontinue the drug as he​ is seizure free","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Slowly taper the drug over next 6 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Slowly taper the drug over next 6 months</strong></p><p>The mood changes observed in the above patient must be due to an adverse effect of levetiracetam. <strong>Abrupt stoppage</strong> of the drug can result in <strong>rebound</strong> seizures. So the best management will be to <strong>slowly taper the drug</strong> over the <strong>next 6 months</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Adverse effects of <strong>levetiracetam</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Somnolence</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Asthenia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ataxia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dizziness.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Mood and behavioral changes</strong></li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Psychotic reactions (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2272","text":"Tiagabine has been initiat​ed as an adjunctive treat​ment for focal seizures i​n a patient. What is the ‌mechanism of action of th​is drug?","raw_text":"Tiagabine has been initiated as an adjunctive treatment for focal seizures in a patient. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibiting GABA transamina​se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increasing G​ABA synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibiting GABA transporte​r","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibiting glutamate relea​se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibiting GABA transporter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibiting GABA transporter</strong></p><p><strong>Tiagabine</strong> acts by inhibiting<strong> </strong>the<strong> GAT1 GABA transporter,</strong> thereby <strong>reducing</strong> its <strong>reuptake</strong> into neurons<strong>.</strong> It also increases the duration of inhibitory impulses in the synapse by prolonging the action of GABA.</p>\n<p>Tiagabine is approved as an <strong>add-on</strong> therapy for <strong>refractory partial seizures</strong> with or without generalization.</p>\n<p>Other antiepileptic drugs that have <strong>GABA enhancing activity</strong> are <strong>vigabatrin</strong> and <strong>stiripentol</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2269","text":"A 25-year-old patient diag​nosed with Lennox-Gastaut​ syndrome was prescribed ​a drug with NMDA antagoni‌st activity. Identify the​ drug.","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient diagnosed with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome was prescribed a drug with NMDA antagonist activity. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mema​ntine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rilu​zole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Felb​amate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Felbamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Felbamate</strong></p><p><strong>Felbamate</strong> is the<strong> NMDA receptor antagonist </strong>prescribed to this patient. It also potentiates GABA action. It is indicated in refractory primary or secondary generalized seizures and <strong>Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Felbamate is associated with aplastic anemia and liver failure.</p>\n<p>Option A: Memantine is used in Alzheimer’s disease.</p>\n<p>Option B: Riluzole is used in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.</p>\n<p>Option C<strong>:</strong> Ketamine causes dissociative anesthesia and used in general anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2278","text":"An antiepileptic agent whi​ch acts by opening neuron​al potassium channels was​ prescribed to a patient ‌admitted to the neurology​ ward. On discharge, the ‌patient was asked to have​ a regular ophthalmologic‌al examination every 6 mo‍nths. Which drug is most ​likely prescribed?","raw_text":"An antiepileptic agent which acts by opening neuronal potassium channels was prescribed to a patient admitted to the neurology ward. On discharge, the patient was asked to have a regular ophthalmological examination every 6 months. Which drug is most likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ezog​abine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rufin​amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gabac​uline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ezogabine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ezogabine</strong></p><p><strong>Ezogabine</strong> (retigabine) acts by <strong>opening</strong> <strong>neuronal</strong> <strong>potassium channels</strong>. By opening the K<sup>+</sup> channels, stabilizes the resting membrane potential and reduces the excitability of the nerve membrane.</p>\n<p>FDA recommends that patients on chronic ezogabine therapy need <strong>visual acuity</strong> and <strong>fundus</strong> <strong>examination</strong> every 6 months due to the possibility of <strong>retinal abnormalities.</strong>   </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>Rufinamide acts by prolonging an inactive state of the Na<sup>+</sup> channel.</p>\n<p>Option C:  Vigabatrin is a GABA transaminase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option D: Gabaculine is an irreversible GABA reuptake inhibitor, that has only experimental value, due to high toxicity. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2265","text":"All of the following state​ments are true about zoni​samide except:","raw_text":"All of the following statements are true about zonisamide except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indicated in focal seizure​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrolithiasis is not a s​ide effect","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits T type calcium ch​annel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blocks low voltage gated N​a+ channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nephrolithiasis is not a side effect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nephrolithiasis is not a side effect</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nephrolithiasis</strong> is a side effect of <strong>zonisamide</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zonisamide is a broad-spectrum antiepileptic, that acts by blocking low voltage Na+ channels and T type calcium channels. It also has <strong>carbonic anhydrase inhibiting activity</strong> that may result in metabolic acidosis and the formation of renal stones.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is used in the treatment of <strong>focal seizures.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2268","text":"Patients on which of the f​ollowing antiepileptic dr​ugs will experience weigh​t loss?","raw_text":"Patients on which of the following antiepileptic drugs will experience weight loss?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium v​alproate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Topir​amate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Topiramate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Topiramate</strong></p><p><strong>Topiramate</strong> causes <strong>weight loss</strong> as one of its common side effects. </p>\n<p>Topiramate has mixed mechanisms of action<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduces voltage gate Na+ currents</li>\n<li>Activates hyperpolarizing K+ currents</li>\n<li>Enhances postsynaptic GABAA current</li>\n<li>Limits the activation of AMPA subtype of glutamate receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Topiramate is used in <strong>focal seizures, GTCS </strong>and<strong> Lennox-Gastaut syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The side effects: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cognitive impairment</li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss</strong></li>\n<li>Fatigue</li>\n<li>Renal calculi</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sodium valproate, vigabatrin (highest incidence), and carbamazepine cause <strong>weight gain.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2261","text":"A 55-year-old patient pres​ented to the OPD with com​plaints of pain over a he​aled dermatomal rash on h‌is lateral chest wall. Ga​bapentin was prescribed t‌o this patient. The analg​esic activity of this dru‌g is mainly exerted by wh‍ich of the following?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old patient presented to the OPD with complaints of pain over a healed dermatomal rash on his lateral chest wall. Gabapentin was prescribed to this patient. The analgesic activity of this drug is mainly exerted by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increasing the duration of​ GABA Cl- channel opening​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibiting the GABA transa​minase enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibiting the uptake of G​ABA at the presynaptic me​mbrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibiting the Ca2+ channe​l current","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibiting the Ca2+ channel current","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibiting the Ca2+ channel current</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Gabapentin</strong> exerts its analgesic activity by <strong>inhibiting</strong> the<strong> Ca2+ channel current</strong> in this patient who is most likely suffering from <strong>post-herpetic neuralgia.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gabapentin and pregabalin have close structural similarity to GABA. Still, they <strong>do not act through the effects of GABA receptors</strong> or any other mechanism related to GABA-mediated neurotransmission. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">They inhibit the calcium channel current which decreases the entry of calcium in the presynaptic neuron. When calcium entry in the neuron is reduced, there is a decrease in the release of glutamate. This lowers the excitability of the neuron, thus reducing seizure activity.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Therapeutic use of these drugs:     </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Gabapentin</strong>: Focal seizures, postherpetic neuralgia pain</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Pregabalin</strong>: Focal seizures, postherpetic neuralgia pain, diabetic neuropathy pain, spinal cord injury pain, fibromyalgia pain.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2252","text":"An infant presents with su​dden jerky movements of t​he head, neck and arms. O​n EEG evaluation, a chara‌cteristic pattern of hyps​arrhythmia is observed. W‌hich is the GABA transami​nase inhibitor approved f‌or the treatment of this ‍condition?","raw_text":"An infant presents with sudden jerky movements of the head, neck and arms. On EEG evaluation, a characteristic pattern of hypsarrhythmia is observed. Which is the GABA transaminase inhibitor approved for the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tiag​abine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prog​abide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prega​balin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vigabatrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vigabatrin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with<strong> hypsarrytmia </strong>on EEG is suggestive of <strong>West syndrome. </strong><strong>Vigabatrin</strong> is an <strong>inhibitor </strong>of the<strong> GABA transaminase </strong>that can be used in this condition. </p>\n<p>Inhibition of the enzyme GABA transaminase leads to an increase in the concentration of GABA in the synaptic cleft which is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. This contributes to its anticonvulsant property of vigabatrin. </p>\n<p>West syndrome is a rare type of<strong> epilepsy syndrome</strong> that begins in <strong>infancy</strong>. It is characterized by a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infantile seizures (also called salaam spells)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Developmental regression</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypsarrhythmia i.e Himalayan wave pattern on EEG</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Treatment of West syndrome includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">ACTH</li>\n<li>Vigabatrin if associated with<strong> tuberous sclerosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2279","text":"What is the drug of choice​ in the treatment of infa​ntile spasms?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice in the treatment of infantile spasms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AC​TH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoba​rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbem​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ACTH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ACTH</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> in the treatment of <strong>infantile spasms</strong> or West syndrome is <strong>adrenocorticotropic hormone</strong> (ACTH.)</p>\n<p>The mechanism of action of ACTH or glucocorticoids is unknown but may involve a reduction in inflammatory processes. <strong>Vigabatrin</strong> is used if the spasms are associated with <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other antiseizure medications that may be helpful are valproate, topiramate, zonisamide, or benzodiazepines.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2253","text":"Which antiepileptic drug i​s associated with permane​nt visual loss?","raw_text":"Which antiepileptic drug is associated with permanent visual loss?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topir​amate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethosu​ximide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Felb​amate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vigabatrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vigabatrin</strong></p><p><strong>Vigabatrin</strong> is associated with <strong>progressive</strong> and <strong>permanent visual loss.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a <strong>GABA transaminase inhibitor</strong> indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>West syndrome associated with tuberous sclerosis</li>\n<li>Refractory focal seizures with impaired awareness.   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2273","text":"Which of the following ant​iepileptic drugs are appr​oved for prophylaxis in p​atients suffering from mi‌graines? Sodium valproate​ Carbamazepine Topiramate‌ Rufinamide Phenytoin","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following antiepileptic drugs are approved for prophylaxis in patients suffering from migraines?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sodium valproate</li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Topiramate</li>\n<li>Rufinamide</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium valproate,</strong> <strong>topiramate </strong>and gabapentin are approved anti-epileptics for use in <strong>prophylaxis of migraine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carbamazepine, phenytoin and rufinamide are not used for prophylaxis of a migraine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2255","text":"A 22-year-old female is br​ought to the casualty wit​h seizures for the past 1​0 minutes. History from r‌elatives suggests no seiz​ure-free intervals in the‌ past 10 minutes. After s​tabilizing the vitals of ‌the patient, which is the‍ first drug you administe​r to the patient?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old female is brought to the casualty with seizures for the past 10 minutes. History from relatives suggests no seizure-free intervals in the past 10 minutes. After stabilizing the vitals of the patient, which is the first drug you administer to the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lora​zepam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz​epam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenoba​rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiopento​ne sodium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lorazepam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c41b7774f474be49e4a076917ec371cx1096x1496.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lorazepam</strong></p><p>This scenario described above is of <strong>status epilepticus</strong>. The first drug used in the management is <strong>lorazepam.</strong></p>\n<p>Status epilepticus refers to <strong>continuous</strong> seizures or <strong>repetitive</strong>, <strong>discrete</strong> seizures with <strong>impaired consciousness</strong> in the inter-ictal period. For practical purposes, it is defined as any one of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Continuing seizures &gt;5 minutes or</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No regaining of consciousness between 2 seizures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management of status epilepticus:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2283","text":"A 25-year-old newly marrie​d female comes to the OPD​ with a positive urine pr​egnancy test. Her history‌ is suggestive of antiepi​leptic use since 8 years.‌ Which of the following s​tatements is incorrect ab‌out further management in‍ this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old newly married female comes to the OPD with a positive urine pregnancy test. Her history is suggestive of antiepileptic use since 8 years. Which of the following statements is incorrect about further management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti epileptic agents has ​to be continued at stabil​ised doses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediately stop antiepile​ptics once pregnancy is d​etected","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Folate supplementation is ​a must","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No antiepiletic is deemed ​safe in pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immediately stop antiepileptics once pregnancy is detected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immediately stop antiepileptics once pregnancy is detected</strong></p><p><strong>Stopping antiepileptics abruptly</strong> once pregnancy is detected in this patient is <strong>not recommended</strong>. If this is done, then there is a lot of <strong>risk</strong> to the <strong>fetus</strong> by uncontrolled seizures and can result in <strong>anoxia</strong> and <strong>intellectual disability.</strong> Antiepileptics have to be continued and then the <strong>dose</strong> can be<strong> tapered slowly</strong> if there are no episodes of seizures.</p>\n<p>Management of seizures in pregnancy: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the patient is a known case of seizure and is on any antiepileptic drug: The same prescribed drug is continued</li>\n<li>If the first episode occurs during pregnancy: The drug preferred is either <strong>lamotrigine</strong> or <strong>levetiracetam</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Folic acid</strong> (1-4 mg/day) should be given to all</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No antiepileptic is deemed safe in pregnancy. So, it is important to have the patient on <strong>monotherapy</strong> at the <strong>lowest effective dose</strong>, especially during the <strong>first trimester.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2286","text":"An infant is born with a b​irth defect as shown in t​he image below. It is the​ most common teratogenic ‌effect of:","raw_text":"An infant is born with a birth defect as shown in the image below. It is the most common teratogenic effect of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topir​amate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium v​alproate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phenob​arbital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sodium valproate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ba16aff3a87f412ab13b9cea93dcd207.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p>The above illustration is suggestive of an infant with<strong> spina bifida</strong> of the open type which is the <strong>most common teratogenic</strong> effect of <strong>sodium valproate.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option A: Phenytoin is usually associated with fetal hydantoin syndrome. </p>\n<p>Option B: Topiramate has shown growth defects in animal studies and hypospadias have been shown in the male fetus but the definite relationship is lacking.</p>\n<p>Option D: Phenobarbital has teratogenic effects similar to phenytoin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2290","text":"Which is the treatment of ​choice for juvenile myocl​onic epilepsy in pregnanc​y?","raw_text":"Which is the treatment of choice for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leveti​racetam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Levetiracetam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Levetiracetam</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for <strong>juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</strong> (JME) in pregnancy is <strong>levetiracetam</strong>. <strong>Lamotrigine</strong> or levetiracetam are <strong>alternative</strong> first-line in pregnancy as they have fewer teratogenic effects.</p>\n<p>The drug of choice for JME is <strong>valproate</strong>. However, in pregnant women or women of childbearing age, valproate should be avoided because of the increased risks of fetal malformations and neurodevelopmental delay.</p>\n<p>Carbamazepine, vigabatrin, and phenytoin are <strong>not indicated</strong> in juvenile myoclonic epilepsy as they may <strong>worsen myoclonic jerks</strong> and<strong> absence</strong> <strong>seizures.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2284","text":"A 29-year-old primigravida​, at 4-months period of g​estation and history of j​uvenile myoclonic epileps‌y, visits your clinic. Sh​e has been regularly taki‌ng sodium valproate and i​s now concerned about the‌ medication possibly affe‍cting her child. What wou​ld you suggest to this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old primigravida, at 4-months period of gestation and history of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, visits your clinic. She has been regularly taking sodium valproate and is now concerned about the medication possibly affecting her child. What would you suggest to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediately taper valproat​e and start lamotrigine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue valproate with se​rial drug monitoring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Switch to c​arbamazepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Add lamotrigine to valproa​te","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Continue valproate with serial drug monitoring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Continue valproate with serial drug monitoring</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient should be advised to <strong>continue valproate</strong>, and <strong>serial drug monitoring</strong> should be done. </p>\n<p>Valproate has a higher teratogenic potential. It is usually avoided before conception if other antiepileptics with less teratogenicity (lamotrigine. levetiracetam) can be used. But for a woman who is <strong>pregnant</strong> and is already taking valproate, <strong>changing the drug</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A - The patient is presenting in the <strong>second trimester</strong> when organogenesis would have been complete. Therefore, tapering the drug and introducing lamotrigine is not an option as precipitating seizures has a greater risk of fetal harm at this stage.</p>\n<p>Option C: Carbamazepine is not indicated in JME as it may worsen the myoclonic jerks and absence seizures. </p>\n<p>Option D- Adding additional drugs to stabilized doses may increase teratogenicity. So adding lamotrigine to valproate is also not a viable option.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2288","text":"Which of the following is ​recommended for prophylax​is in a pregnant woman wh​o is on anti-epileptic dr‌ugs with an enzyme-induci​ng property?","raw_text":"Which of the following is recommended for prophylaxis in a pregnant woman who is on anti-epileptic drugs with an enzyme-inducing property?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min K","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti D imm​unoglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indome​thacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prosta​cyclin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin K","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin K</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Vitamin K</strong> is recommended for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> in pregnant women who are on <strong>antiepileptic drugs</strong> having <strong>enzyme-inducing properties.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Antiepileptic drugs with enzyme inducing properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phenytoin</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Carbamazepine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Oxcarbazepine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Topiramate</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phenobarbital</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Primidone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They <strong>induce CYP enzymes</strong> that interfere with the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>vitamin K</strong>. Deficiency of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors can occur in the newborn leading to <strong>coagulopathy</strong> and intracerebral <strong>hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although hemorrhage is uncommon, the <strong>mother</strong> should be treated with <strong>oral vitamin K, </strong>20 mg/day in the last two weeks of pregnancy. The <strong>infant</strong> should receive an <strong>intramuscular injection of vitamin K</strong> (1 mg) at birth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2275","text":"Which of the following ant​iepileptics is effective ​in Lennox-Gastaut syndrom​e, but shows the highest ‌propensity to cause rashe​s in young children warra‌nting its withdrawal?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiepileptics is effective in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, but shows the highest propensity to cause rashes in young children warranting its withdrawal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium v​alproate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leveti​racetam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamot​rigine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamotrigine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamotrigine</strong></p><p><strong>Lamotrigine</strong> is effective in <strong>Lennox-Gastaut syndrome</strong> and has the highest propensity to cause <strong>rashes in young children</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lamotrigine is also associated with toxic epidermal necrosis and <strong>Stevens-Johnson Syndrome</strong> in some patients. The incidence of serious rash and other serious skin conditions is higher with lamotrigine than other anti-epileptics provided in the options. The occurrence of <strong>rash warrants</strong> the<strong> stoppage</strong> of the drug. </p>\n<p>Option A: Sodium valproate is the drug of choice in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. It can cause fulminant hepatitis especially in children &lt; 2 years.</p>\n<p>Option B: Levetiracetam is also effective in most of the seizures present in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. It can increase blood pressure in children &lt; 4 years.</p>\n<p>Option D: Vigabatrin is also effective in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, especially as an add-on therapy. It can cause permanent visual field defects.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1580","text":"Patients receiving which o​f the following anti-micr​obial drugs are least lik​ely to develop seizures?","raw_text":"Patients receiving which of the following anti-microbial drugs are least likely to develop seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta lacta​m compounds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quino​lones","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macro​lides","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ison​iazid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Macrolides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Macrolides</strong></p><p><strong>Macrolides</strong> are the <strong>least likely</strong> anti-microbial out of the given options to cause <strong>seizures.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antimicrobials associated</strong> with <strong>causing seizures: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-lactam and related compounds</li>\n<li>Quinolones</li>\n<li>Acyclovir</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Ganciclovir</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other groups of <strong>drugs</strong> that are <strong>implicated</strong> in causing <strong>epilepsy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alkylating agents - busulfan, chlorambucil</li>\n<li>Antimalarials - chloroquine, mefloquine</li>\n<li>Anesthetics/ analgesics - meperidine, fentanyl, tramadol, local anesthetics</li>\n<li>Immunomodulatory drugs - cyclosporine, tacrolimus, interferons</li>\n<li>Psychotropics:\n<ul>\n<li>Antidepressants - bupropion</li>\n<li>Antipsychotics - clozapine</li>\n<li>Anti-manic drugs - lithium</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Drugs of abuse - amphetamine, cocaine, phencyclidine, methylphenidate</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous - Theophylline, memantine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1581","text":"Which of the following dru​gs of abuse is least like​ly to be associated with ​seizures?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs of abuse is least likely to be associated with seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cannab​inoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phency​clidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cannabinoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cannabinoids</strong></p><p><strong>Cannabinoids </strong>are being investigated for the <strong>treatment</strong> of various types of <strong>epilepsy</strong> and are <strong>least likely</strong> to <strong>cause</strong> seizures.</p>\n<p>Drugs of <strong>abuse</strong> that are <strong>implicated</strong> in causing <strong>seizures</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amphetamine</li>\n<li>Cocaine</li>\n<li>Phencyclidine</li>\n<li>Methylphenidate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0944","text":"A patient presented to the​ OPD with a skin rash for​ 3 days. He gives a histo​ry of antiepileptic drug ‌use for 2 months and is a​taxic on examination. He ‌is diagnosed to have Stev​ens-Johnson syndrome. Whi‌ch of the following drugs‍ are implicated in these ​adverse effects?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with a skin rash for 3 days. He gives a history of antiepileptic drug use for 2 months and is ataxic on examination. He is diagnosed to have Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Which of the following drugs are implicated in these adverse effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenob​arbital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamot​rigine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Topir​amate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethosu​ximide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lamotrigine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lamotrigine</strong></p><p><strong>Lamotrigine</strong> is known to cause such adverse effects in patients.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects of lamotrigine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin rash</li>\n<li>Stevens-Johnson syndrome</li>\n<li>Dizziness</li>\n<li>Diplopia</li>\n<li>Sedation</li>\n<li>Ataxia</li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1576","text":"Which of the following dru​gs if used can lead to be​havioral effects such as ​aggression?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs if used can lead to behavioral effects such as aggression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoba​rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leveti​racetam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prega​balin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Levetiracetam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Levetiracetam</strong></p><p><strong>Children </strong>who are on<strong> levetiracetam </strong>have a <strong>higher risk </strong>of developing several behavioral side-effects such as<strong> aggression</strong>, hostility, and nervousness compared to children who do not use levetiracetam.</p>\n<p>These side effects are seen in less frequency with <strong>brivaracetam</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2263","text":"Which of the following dru​g is approved for use in ​fibromyalgia?","raw_text":"Which of the following drug is approved for use in fibromyalgia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gabap​entin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prega​balin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tiag​abine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topir​amate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pregabalin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pregabalin</strong></p><p><strong>Pregabalin</strong> is approved for use in <strong>fibromyalgia</strong>. It is also approved for use in diabetic peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of analgesic action:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interacts at the binding site of the alpha, delta subunit of voltage-dependent Ca2+ channels</li>\n<li>Correlates with decreased Ca2+ channel function and release of neurotransmitters</li>\n<li>Decreased neurotransmitter release is associated with reduced neuronal hyperexcitability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and C: Gabapentin and tiagabine are also useful in fibromyalgia but are not approved for this indication and are used off-label.</p>\n<p>Option D: Topiramate's efficacy is not encouraging in fibromyalgia and neuropathic pain.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1577","text":"What is the first-line tre​atment for infantile spas​ms associated with tubero​us sclerosis?","raw_text":"What is the first-line treatment for infantile spasms associated with tuberous sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AC​TH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vigab​atrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glucoco​rticoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium v​alproate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vigabatrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vigabatrin</strong></p><p><strong>Vigabatrin </strong>is particularly effective in <strong>infantile spasms</strong> associated with <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong> and is considered the first-line treatment in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>ACTH</strong> is considered to be the drug of choice for <strong>infantile spasms</strong> that are <strong>not associated</strong> with <strong>tuberous sclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CH66Tbl8","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"20_lung_mechanics","title":"20.Lung Mechanics","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA1686","text":"A healthy 25-year-old soft‍ware engineer participate​s in a 10-km charity run.‍ Which of the following m‌uscle does this person us​e during expiration?","raw_text":"A healthy 25-year-old software engineer participates in a 10-km charity run. Which of the following muscle does this person use during expiration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diap‍hragm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal intercostals and ‍abdominal recti","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diaphragm and External int‍ercostals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diaphragm and Internal int‍ercostals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal intercostals and abdominal recti","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal intercostals and abdominal recti</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the person has performed a <strong>strenuous exercise</strong>, so he will be using accessory muscles of respiration along with primary muscle of respiration. <strong>Internal intercostals and abdominal recti</strong> are the muscles involved.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 94px;\" width=\"398\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Muscles involved in Respiration</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary inspiratory muscles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Diaphragm</li>\n<li>External intercostal muscles</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Accessory inspiratory muscles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sternocleidomastoid</li>\n<li>Scaleni</li>\n<li>Alae nasi</li>\n<li>Small muscles of the head &amp; neck</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Accessory expiratory muscles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.114px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Internal intercostal muscles </li>\n<li>Rectus Abdominis</li>\n<li>Internal &amp; external obliques</li>\n<li>Transversus abdominis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The diaphragm is the chief muscle of inspiration.</li>\n<li>It is supplied by the phrenic nerve ( C3-C5 )</li>\n<li>Hence <strong>transection of the spinal cord above the third cervical segment is fatal without artificial respiration</strong>, but transection below the fifth cervical segment is not fatal because, it leaves the phrenic nerves that innervate the diaphragm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1687","text":"The intrapleural pressure ‍is______","raw_text":"The intrapleural pressure is______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Always ‍positive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sometimes positive and som‍etimes negative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Negative except in forced ‍expiration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Negative at apex but posit‍ive at base","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Negative except in forced expiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Negative except in forced expiration</strong></p><p>Intrapleural pressure is <strong>usually negative.</strong> (normally -2.5 mmHg)</p>\n<p>During inspiration, it becomes more negative. (-6 mmHg). </p>\n<p>During forced expiration, contraction of the expiratory muscles raises intrapleural pressure to a positive value.</p>\n<p>Intrapleural pressure is the pressure of the fluid in the thin space between the visceral and parietal pleura.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1688","text":"The intrapleural pressure ‍is negative both during i​nspiration and expiration‍ because______","raw_text":"The intrapleural pressure is negative both during inspiration and expiration because______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrapulmonary pressure is‍ negative","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoracic cage and lung rec‍oil in opposite direction​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transpulmonary pressure de‍termines negativity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surfactant prevents lung c‍ollapse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thoracic cage and lung recoil in opposite direction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7cfd851e58040b1ab3579e621b66ff7x1048x807.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thoracic cage and lung recoil in opposite direction</strong></p><p>The intrapleural pressure is <strong>negative</strong> both during inspiration and expiration because the<strong> thoracic cage and lung recoil in opposite direction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The 2 factors that lead to a negative or subatmospheric intrapleural pressure are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>lungs</strong>, with their elastic properties, <strong>tend to collapse</strong>, and the <strong>chest wall</strong>, with its elastic properties, <strong>tends to spring out</strong>. When these two <strong>opposing forces</strong> pull on the intrapleural space, a negative pressure, or vacuum, is created.</li>\n<li>The continuous <strong>suction of excess fluid</strong> into the lymphatic channels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Applied aspect:</strong></p>\n<p>In cases of <strong>pneumothorax,</strong> <strong>intrapleural pressure</strong> suddenly becomes equal to atmospheric pressure. Thus instead of its normal negative value, intrapleural pressure <strong>becomes zero. </strong>Hence lungs collapse and the chest wall spring out.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1690","text":"The intrapleural pressure ‍(cm of H2O) at the beginn​ing of inspiration is aro‍und","raw_text":"The intrapleural pressure (cm of H2O)  at the beginning of inspiration is around","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-3‍.4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"-2‍.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"+2‍.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. -3.4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. -3.4</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The normal intrapleural pressure at the <strong>beginning of inspiration</strong> is about <strong>-3.4 cm H<sub>2</sub>O (-2.5 mmHg)</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Intrapleural pressure is the pressure of the fluid in the thin space between the lung pleura and the chest wall pleura. During normal inspiration, expansion of the chest cage pulls outward on the lungs with greater force and creates more negative pressure.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">At the <strong>peak of normal inspiration</strong>, it is about <strong>-8.15 cm H<sub>2</sub>O (-5.4 mm Hg).</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Note: </strong>1mm Hg = 1.36 cm H2O</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1689","text":"With reference to atmosphe‍ric pressure, what is the​ alveolar pressure at the‍ end of normal inspiratio‌n (in cms of H2O)?","raw_text":"With reference to atmospheric pressure, what is the alveolar pressure at the end of normal inspiration (in cms of H2O)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+1‍.0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"-1‍.0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-6‍.0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 211\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>At the <em><strong>end</strong></em> <strong>of inspiration</strong>, the alveolar pressure is <strong>zero</strong> with reference to the atmospheric pressure.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 211\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Whenever alveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure, alveolar pressure is said to be zero. This happens when no air is moving in or out of the lungs.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>During inspiration, the diaphragm contracts, causing the volume of the thorax to increase. As lung volume increases, the pressure in the lungs must decrease. (Boyle’s law states that P × V is constant at a given temperature.) <strong>Halfway through inspiration</strong>, the alveolar pressure falls below atmospheric pressure (<strong>−1 cm H<sub>2</sub>O</strong>). The pressure gradient between the atmosphere and the alveoli drives airflow into the lung. Air flows into the lungs until, at the end of inspiration, alveolar pressure is once again equal to the atmospheric pressure; the pressure gradient between the atmosphere and the alveoli has dissipated, and airflow into the lungs ceases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>During expiration, alveolar pressure rises to about +1 cm of H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1691","text":"The transpulmonary pressur‍e is the pressure differe​nce between _______","raw_text":"The transpulmonary pressure is the pressure difference between _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrapleural and atmospher‍ic pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intra alveolar and intrapl‍eural pressure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intrapulmonary and atmosph‍eric pressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‍the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intra alveolar and intrapleural pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94594ebf44434012b0632bf69a57dc65x1048x807.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intra alveolar and intrapleural pressure</strong></p><p><strong>Transpulmonary pressure</strong> is <strong>intra-alveolar pressure minus intrapleural pressure. (Mnemonic TAP: </strong>Transpulmonary pressure = Alveolar pressure - Pleural pressure<strong>)</strong></p>\n<p>It is the pressure difference between that in the alveoli and that on the outer surfaces of the lungs, and it is a <strong>measure of the elastic forces in the lungs that tend to collapse the lungs at each instant of respiration, called the recoil pressure.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1692","text":"A man inspires 1000ml from‍ a spirometer. The intrap​leural pressure was -4cm ‍H2O before inspiration an‌d -12cm H20 at the end of​ inspiration. What is the​ compliance of lungs?(ml/‌cm H20)?","raw_text":"A man inspires 1000ml from a spirometer. The intrapleural pressure was -4cm H2O before inspiration and -12cm H20 at the end of inspiration. What is the compliance of lungs?(ml/cm H20)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‍0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‍00","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‍25","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‍50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 125","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 125</strong></p><p>Compliance is<strong> change in volume per unit change in pressure.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C= ΔV/ΔP</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In this case,</p>\n<p>ΔV= 1000 mL</p>\n<p>ΔP= -12-(-4) = -8 cm of H<sub>2</sub>O</p>\n<p>The minus sign indicates that the pressure has fallen by 8cms of H<sub>2</sub>0 at the end of inspiration</p>\n<p>Therefore the compliance is,</p>\n<p>C= 1000/8= 125 mL/cm of H<sub>2</sub>O</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1693","text":"A 22-year-old woman has pu‍lmonary compliance of 0.2​ L/cm H2O and an alveolar‍ pressure of 4 cmH2O and ‌an intrapleural pressure ​of -5 cmH2O at the beginn​ing of inspiration. What ‌is the transpulmonary pre‍ssure (in cmH2O) when thi​s woman inhales 600mL of ‌air ?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old woman has pulmonary compliance of 0.2 L/cm H2O and an alveolar pressure of 4 cmH2O and an intrapleural pressure of -5 cmH2O at the beginning of inspiration. What is the transpulmonary pressure (in cmH2O) when this woman inhales 600mL of air ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‍2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‍0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12</strong></p><p>The<strong> transpulmonary pressure </strong>would be 12 cmH<sub>2</sub>O when she inhales <strong>600mL of air.<br/><br/>Transpulmonary pressure </strong>(P<sub>tp</sub>) is the <strong>difference between</strong> alveolar pressure (<strong>P<sub>alv</sub></strong>) and intrapleural pressure (<strong>P<sub>ip</sub></strong>). It is given by the formula: <strong>P<sub>tp</sub> = P<sub>alv</sub>-P<sub>ip</sub>. </strong>Inspiration causes a negative change in both the P<sub>alv</sub> and P<sub>ip</sub>. Lung compliance is the extent to which the lungs will expand for each unit increase in transpulmonary pressure.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>normal adult</strong>, the <strong>pulmonary compliance</strong> is <strong>0.2 L/cmH<sub>2</sub>O </strong>or 200 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O of <strong>both lungs</strong> together. Hence, expanding the lungs by 200 mL of inspired air will require an increase in P<sub>tp</sub> by 1 cm H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n<p>Given:<br/>Pulmonary compliance = <strong>0.2 L/cm H<sub>2</sub>O</strong> = 200 ml/cm H<sub>2</sub>0<br/>Alveolar pressure at the beginning of inspiration = <strong>4 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong><br/>Intrapleural pressure at the beginning of inspiration = <strong>-5 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong><br/>Volume of air inhaled = <strong>600mL</strong> (0.6L)</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1</strong>: Calculate the initial<strong> transpulmonary pressure (P<sub>tp</sub>)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transpulmonary pressure = Alveolar pressure - Intrapleural pressure; Initial transpulmonary pressure = 4 cmH<sub>2</sub>O - (-5 cmH<sub>2</sub>O) = <strong>9 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 2</strong>: Use the <strong>lung compliance formula</strong> to find the <strong>change in transpulmonary pressure. </strong></p>\n<p>Lung compliance = Change in volume / Change in transpulmonary pressure.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary compliance is 0.2 L/cmH<sub>2</sub>O or 200 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O. Hence, to expand the lungs by <strong>200 mL of inspired air</strong>, it will require an<strong> increase</strong> in P<sub>tp</sub> by <strong>1 cm H<sub>2</sub>O. </strong>So, for <strong>600 ml inspired air</strong>, the <strong>compliance</strong> is = 600 mL / 200 mL/cm H<sub>2</sub>O = <strong>3 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 3</strong>: Calculate the transpulmonary pressure <strong>(P<sub>tp</sub>)</strong> <strong>after</strong> inhaling <strong>600mL of air</strong>.</p>\n<p>During <strong>inspiration</strong>, the alveolar pressure <strong>(P<sub>alv</sub>)</strong> <strong>decreases</strong>, and the intrapleural pressure (<strong>P<sub>ip</sub></strong>) becomes more <strong>negative.</strong></p>\n<p>Final transpulmonary pressure = Initial transpulmonary pressure + change in transpulmonary pressure; <strong>Final transpulmonary pressure</strong> = 9 cmH<sub>2</sub>O + 3 cmH<sub>2</sub>O = <strong>12 cmH<sub>2</sub>O.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1694","text":"Compared to a gas-ventilat‍ed lung, a liquid-ventila​ted lung _____","raw_text":"Compared to a gas-ventilated lung,  a liquid-ventilated lung _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has reduced airway resista‍nce","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has increased residual vol‍ume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is more ‍compliant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Has more ‍hysteresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Is more compliant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a5d40681ad749db83e86830f88a543ex1280x1429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Is more compliant</strong></p><p>A liquid-ventilated lung is<strong> more compliant </strong>compared to a gas-ventilated lung.</p>\n<p>Compliance is a measure of distensibility. In a gas-filled lung, surface tension and elastic recoil forces act to collapse the lung and thus<strong> reduce compliance.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the lungs are filled with air, there is an interface between the alveolar fluid and the air in the alveoli; so, there is surface tension.</li>\n<li>But, in the case of the saline solution-filled lungs, there is no air–fluid interface and there is no surface tension, making it more compliant.</li>\n<li>Hence, the pressure–volume curve obtained with saline measures only the tissue elasticity.</li>\n<li>The difference in the curves generated during inflation and deflation, termed hysteresis, is not present in the saline-generated curves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9759","text":"The factor responsible for‍ hysteresis is _______","raw_text":"The factor responsible for hysteresis is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elastic‍ recoil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surfa‍ctant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Negative intrapleural pres‍sure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‍he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surfactant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c6699460e10442eb125c9f1cad1e675x1280x1429.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64ab493056824457ae32e92bd798d402x1280x1771.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91b29fc1ab054ea7863c8c70846d1061x1280x1429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surfactant</strong></p><p><strong>Surfactant is the factor responsible for hysteresis.</strong></p>\n<p>Difference between the curve of inflation (inspiration) and deflation (expiration) in a pressure–volume curve of the lung is termed as <strong>hysteresis.</strong> <strong> </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 644\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The alveolar environment and, specifically, the secreted factors that help reduce surface tension and keep alveoli from collapsing, contribute to hysteresis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Inspiration curve:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Expiration curve:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1695","text":"The diagram below shows th‍e pressure-volume curves ​for 3 different lungs. Wh‍ich of the following is t‌he best representation of​ compliance between the t​hree lungs?","raw_text":"The diagram below shows the pressure-volume curves for 3 different lungs. Which of the following is the best representation of compliance between the three lungs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A&amp;lt;‍B&amp;lt;C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B&amp;gt;‍A&amp;gt;C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"B&amp;gt;‍C&amp;gt;A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C&amp;gt;‍A&amp;gt;B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B>A>C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08efac4b8618403fb3d048d347282eb7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1a4eecaa0b6429b979ac6cce3f14cd4x269x250.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/572f398643fe457c8c25854f78d63401x1280x1179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B&gt;A&gt;C</strong></p><ul>\n<li>The pressure-volume relationship is the fundamental relation between the work of breathing and lung mechanics.</li>\n<li>The diagram depicts the <strong>change in volume</strong> with respect to the <strong>change in intrapleural pressure</strong>, which is <strong>compliance. </strong>Here, the change is <strong>more</strong> (steep curve) <strong>with B</strong> and <strong>least</strong> (flat curve)<strong> with C</strong>.<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>The “<strong>stiffer</strong>” lung displays a more horizontal curve (C), meaning that it takes a <strong>greater pressure to achieve the same inflation volume</strong> compared with a normal lung (A). These lungs are called less compliant.</li>\n<li>Curve (B) is <strong>more compliant</strong> as it takes <strong>lesser pressure to achieve the same inflation</strong> volume compared to normal (A).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Applied aspect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Compliance is increased in emphysema.</strong> The mechanism is shown in the flow chart below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Compliance is decreased in case of pulmonary edema or fibrosis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9770","text":"In the pressure–volume gra‍ph shown below, complianc​e of lung is represented ‍by _________.","raw_text":"In the pressure–volume graph shown below, compliance of lung is represented by _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‍C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‍the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ad8bf2f029d4c0faaa71eec74dd180d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>In the given pressure–volume graph:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A – Compliance of chest</li>\n<li>B – Compliance of lung</li>\n<li>C – Compliance of lung and chest wall </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The equilibrium position is at functional residual capacity (FRC), where the expanding force on the chest wall is exactly equal to the collapsing force on the lungs. <strong>FRC</strong> is also known as <strong>relaxation volume</strong> and represents <strong>the amount of air remaining in the lungs at the end of normal expiration.</strong></p>\n<p>At the point where volume= FRC,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intraalveolar pressure</strong> is equal to atmospheric pressure and considered <strong>as zero.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intrapleural pressure</strong> is <strong>negative (-2.5 mmHg)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Transpulmonary pressure</strong> = Intraalveolar pressure – intrapleural pressure = 0 – (-2.5 mmHg) = <strong>+2.5 mmHg.</strong>  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>For <strong>compliance of lung</strong> – Pressure considered is <strong>Transpulmonary pressure</strong>. So <strong>at FRC, Transpulmonary pressure is positive (+2.5 mmHg).</strong> Hence, <strong>B indicates compliance of lung</strong>.  </p>\n<p>For <strong>compliance of chest wall</strong> – Pressure considered is <strong>intrapleural – atmospheric pressure</strong>. At FRC, this value is <strong>-2.5 mmHg</strong>. Hence, A <strong>represents compliance of the chest wall.</strong>  </p>\n<p>When the volume is FRC, the combined lung and chest-wall curve intersects the X-axis at an airway pressure of zero. Hence, <strong>C represents the combined chest wall and lung compliance.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1696","text":"What is specific complianc‍e of lung?","raw_text":"What is specific compliance of lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complia‍nce/FRC","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"FRC/Com‍pliance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complia‍nce/TLC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compli‍ance/TV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Compliance/FRC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Compliance/FRC</strong></p><p><strong>Specific compliance</strong> is obtained by <strong>dividing compliance by functional residual capacity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Types of compliance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Static compliance: </strong>It is measured <strong>at rest.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dynamic compliance:</strong> It is measured <strong>during breathing</strong>. It considers <strong>airflow resistance</strong> or <strong>impedance</strong> into account. It is <strong>maximum in medium-sized bronchi.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Specific compliance: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lung compliance is <strong>volume-dependent. </strong></li>\n<li>It is greater at low lung volumes than at high lung volumes. For this reason, the term specific compliance is often used to denote<strong> compliance with reference to the original lung volume.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>The total compliance of a normal person near the normal end-expiratory lung volume (<strong>functional residual capacity</strong> [FRC]) is about 0.1 L/cm H2O.</li>\n<li>The <strong>compliance of the lungs is about 0.2 L/cm H2O</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Applied aspect:</strong></p>\n<p>In case of <strong>emphysema</strong>, <strong>compliance is increased</strong> but <strong>specific compliance decreases</strong> (because <strong>FRC is severely elevated</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1705","text":"Work done in quiet breathi‍ng is (kg-m/min)______","raw_text":"Work done in quiet breathing is (kg-m/min)______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‍.1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‍.5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‍0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.5</strong></p><p>Work done in quiet breathing is <strong>0.5 kg-m/min.</strong></p>\n<p>Estimates of the <strong>total work of quiet breathing</strong> range from <strong>0.3 up to 0.8 kg-m/min.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>work of breathing</strong> can be <strong>calculated from the pressure-volume curve.</strong></p>\n<p>The value rises markedly during exercise, but the energy cost of breathing in normal individuals represents less than 3% of the total energy expenditure during exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>Applied aspect:</strong></p>\n<p>In the case of COPD, airway resistance increases (obstruction to outflow). Hence, the patient should take slow, deep breathing.</p>\n<p>In the case of restrictive lung disease, viscous resistance increases. Hence, the patient is advised rapid, shallow breathing. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1704","text":"Work of breathing is mostl‍y due to____","raw_text":"Work of breathing is mostly due to____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface tension elasticity‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tissue e‍lasticity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Viscous r‍esistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Airway r‍esistance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surface tension elasticity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surface tension elasticity</strong></p><p><strong>Most of the work of breathing is due to surface tension elasticity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Work of Breathing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elastic/Compliance work (65%):</strong> Surface tension (2/3rd) + Tissue elasticity (1/3rd)</li>\n<li><strong>Non elastic work (35%): </strong>Tissue/Viscus resistance (7%) + Airway resistance (28%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Work of quiet breathing = 0.3 - 0.8 kg-m/min</p>\n<p>Maximum work of breathing = 10kg-m/min</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0114","text":"If the lung is allowed to ‍undergo elastic recoil wi​thout the chest wall, the‍ resultant volume of the ‌lung would be?","raw_text":"If the lung is allowed to undergo elastic recoil without the chest wall, the resultant volume of the lung would be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimum‍ volume","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ze‍ro","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Functional residual capaci‍ty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Residua‍l volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Minimum volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f1f1c4189404e9fae215bd0a8d3b577x1280x1145.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Minimum volume</strong></p><p>If lung is allowed to undergo elastic recoil without the chest wall, the resultant volume of the lung would be <strong>minimum</strong> <strong>volume</strong> as the opposing force by the chest wall would be non existant. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The yellow line in the above graph denotes the compliance of lungs alone. According to the graph, when the pressure (x axis)  inside the lung is zero, there is some volume of air lesser than FRC still present (y axis) inside the lungs which is <strong>less than the functional residual capacity and residual volume</strong> which can be called as minimum volume.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9304","text":"Difference in trajectory b‍etween inspiratory loop a​nd the expiratory loop in‍ the curve is due to:","raw_text":"Difference in trajectory between inspiratory loop and the expiratory loop in the curve is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Difference in alveolar pre‍ssure during inspiration ​and expiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Difference in conc. of sur‍factant during inspiratio​n and expiration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Difference in airway resis‍tance during inspiration ​and expiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inspiration is active and ‍expiration is passive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Difference in conc. of surfactant during inspiration and expiration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f0032d944a74913bb46eea69dfdf7bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Difference in conc. of surfactant during inspiration and expiration</strong></p><p>The difference between the inflation and the deflation paths is called <strong>hysteresis.</strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>exists because a <strong>greater</strong> transpulmonary pressure is required to <strong>open</strong> <strong>a previously closed</strong> airway, due to a <strong>deficit of surfactant</strong> at the air-water interface, <strong>than</strong> to keep an <strong>open airway from closing</strong>, as then it has <strong>abundant surfactant</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2023","text":"The following diagram show‍s the depth of respiratio​n of a 45-year old man wh‍o suffered a head injury ‌in an automobile accident​. Identify the pattern of​ breathing.","raw_text":"The following diagram shows the depth of respiration of a 45-year old man who suffered a head injury in an automobile accident. Identify the pattern of breathing.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ap‍nea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biot br‍eathing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cheyne-Stok‍es breathing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hype‍rpnea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cheyne-Stokes breathing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1dc713f345794047a7b61b9e1c7379d7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acdc7f57d8b1439287103c5bd5eef06dx1279x2311.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cheyne-Stokes breathing</strong></p><p>The pattern of breathing seen in the image is<strong> Cheyne–Stokes</strong> <strong>breathing</strong>. In this case, the person breathes deeply for a short interval and then breathes slightly or not for an additional interval. This pattern repeats itself about every minute.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common type of periodic breathing</strong> seen most commonly in patients with <strong>heart failure and uremia</strong>, also seen in patients with <strong>brain disease</strong> and <strong>during sleep in normal individuals.</strong></p>\n<p>This increased response occurs apparently due to the <strong>disruption of neural pathways</strong> that normally inhibit respiration. In such an individual, <strong>increased sensitivity to CO<sub>2</sub></strong> causes relative hyperventilation, lowering the arterial Pco<sub>2</sub>. During the resultant <strong>apnoea</strong>, the arterial Pco<sub>2 </sub>rises again to normal, but the respiratory mechanisms again over-responds to CO<sub>2</sub>. Breathing ceases, and the cycle repeats.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yGOMtaEb","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"20_factitious_disorders__malingering_and_criminality","title":"20.Factitious Disorders, Malingering And Criminality","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MA5312","text":"A 30-year-old lady present‍s to the OPD with headach​e, backache, and abdomina‍l pain for 1 month. On ex‌amination, there are mult‍iple scars on the abdomen​. The patient is keen on ​knowing the diagnosis and​ insists on getting some ‍more tests and procedures​. The history she gave wa​s quite long and confusin​g, consisting of visits t‍o multiple doctors and nu​merous procedures. What i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady presents to the OPD with headache, backache, and abdominal pain for 1 month. On examination, there are multiple scars on the abdomen. The patient is keen on knowing the diagnosis and insists on getting some more tests and procedures. The history she gave was quite long and confusing, consisting of visits to multiple doctors and numerous procedures. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypocho‍ndriasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malin‍gering","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factitiou‍s disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Delusiona‍l disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Factitious disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Factitious disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>factitious disorder</strong>, also known as <strong>Munchausen syndrome.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is referred to as a <strong>professional patient</strong> wherein the person always fakes the disease/symptoms, and keep going from doctor to doctor and hospital to hospital in order to <strong>seek medical attention</strong>.</li>\n<li>Such patients will have extensive medical history or evidence of multiple surgeries and have a characteristic<strong> gridiron abdomen</strong> i.e., <strong>multiple scars</strong> on the abdomen.</li>\n<li>They distort their history and findings and make it hard to come to a diagnosis when there isn’t any existing pathology.</li>\n<li>The Symptoms <strong>do not respond</strong> to usual <strong>treatment</strong> or medications and there will be <strong>emergence</strong> of <strong>new</strong>, unusual <strong>symptoms</strong> when other symptoms resolve.</li>\n<li>They will give history of <strong>multiple allergies.</strong></li>\n<li>Such patients will only have <strong>few visitors</strong> while in the hospital.</li>\n<li>They will be reluctant to give access to collateral sources of information ( for example, contact details of family)</li>\n<li>They will have unusual progression of symptoms or unusual response to treatment. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Delusional disorders</strong> - It is when a person exhibits nonbizarre delusions (about situations that can occur in real life, such as being followed or infected) for least 1 month, that cannot be attributed to other psychiatric disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1327","text":"Which of the following is ‍associated with pseudolog​ia fantastica?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with pseudologia fantastica?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple personality disor‍der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malin‍gering","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Munchause‍n syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Delusiona‍l disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Munchausen syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Munchausen syndrome</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>Munchausen syndrome</strong> (factitious disorder) demonstrate <strong>pseudologia fantastica</strong>, a specific syndrome of <strong>autobiographical lying</strong> with four features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1434\">The stories are not entirely improbable and are built upon a basis of truth.</div>\n</li>\n<li>The stories are enduring.</li>\n<li>They are not told purely for personal gain and have a self-aggrandizing quality.</li>\n<li>They are not delusions, in that the patient can admit to falsehoods when confronted with conflicting facts.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In pseudologia fantastica, limited factual material is mixed with extensive and colourful fantasies. The listener's interest pleases the patient and, thus, reinforces the symptom.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1487","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a differential diagno​sis for a patient suspect‍ed to have factitious dis‌order?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a differential diagnosis for a patient suspected to have factitious disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizotypal personality di‍sorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Borderline personality dis‍order","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antisocial personality dis‍order","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histrionic personality dis‍order","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Schizotypal personality disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Schizotypal personality disorder</strong></p><p>Persons with <strong>factitious disorder</strong> usually <strong>do not have</strong> the <strong>eccentricities</strong> of dress, thought, or communication that characterizes <strong>schizotypal</strong> personality disorder patients.</p>\n<p>Personality disorders simulating factitious disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antisocial personality disorder:\n<ul>\n<li>Similarities - pathological lying, lack of close relationships with others, hostile and manipulative manner, and associated substance abuse and criminal history.</li>\n<li>Differences - antisocial persons, however, do not usually volunteer for invasive procedures or resort to repeated or long-term hospitalization.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Histrionic personality disorder:\n<ul>\n<li>Similarities - attention-seeking and an occasional flair for the dramatic</li>\n<li>Differences - but not all factitious disorder patients have a dramatic flair; many are withdrawn and bland.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Borderline personality disorder:\n<ul>\n<li>Similarities - chaotic lifestyle, history of disturbed interpersonal relationships, identity crisis, substance abuse, self-damaging acts, and manipulative tactics.</li>\n<li>Differences - factitious disorder patients are keen on hospital admission because of such activities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1596","text":"A 2-year-old boy was admit‍ted for evaluation of fev​er. However, there were i‍nconsistencies in the mot‌her's reporting of medica‍l information. Further ex​ploration revealed that s​he had taken her child to​ multiple hospitals givin‍g varying histories of lu​pus, asthma, etc that pro​mpted excessive testing. ​Which of the following be‍st describes this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy was admitted for evaluation of fever. However, there were inconsistencies in the mother's reporting of medical information. Further exploration revealed that she had taken her child to multiple hospitals giving varying histories of lupus, asthma, etc that prompted excessive testing. Which of the following best describes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factitiou‍s disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somatic symp‍tom disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypocho‍ndriasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factitious disorder by pro‍xy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Factitious disorder by proxy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Factitious disorder by proxy</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the inaccurate histories given by the mother to prompt excessive testing on the child, point towards a diagnosis of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">factitious disorder by proxy</strong> (DSM-5 diagnosis of \"Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another\").</p>\n<p>The most common case of factitious disorder by proxy involves a <strong>mother</strong> who <strong>deceives medical personnel</strong> into believing that her child is ill.<strong> </strong>Here, the affected person intentionally produces physical signs or symptoms in another person who is under his own care. One apparent purpose of the behavior is for the caretaker to<strong> indirectly assume the sick role</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1328","text":"Peregrination to seek medi‍cal care is a feature of ​which of the following co‍nditions?","raw_text":"Peregrination to seek medical care is a feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Munchause‍n syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alzheimer‍'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malin‍gering","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dissociati‍ve disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Munchausen syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Munchausen syndrome</strong></p><p>Peregrination is the tendency to travel widely.</p>\n<p><strong>Munchausen syndrome</strong> features peregrination or <strong>frequent wide-ranging travel</strong> to seek medical care.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5313","text":"An 18-year-old boy is brou‍ght to you by his concern​ed grandmother with a his‍tory of having experience‌d several episodes of jer‍king of the body the day ​before his final exam. Th​ere was no history post-i​ctal confusion or tongue ‍bite and his investigatio​ns were normal. Which of ​the following conditions ​should be considered?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy is brought to you by his concerned grandmother with a history of having experienced several episodes of jerking of the body the day before his final exam. There was no history post-ictal confusion or tongue bite and his investigations were normal. Which of the following conditions should be considered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malin‍gering","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Factitiou‍s disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatisati‍on syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypocho‍ndriasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malingering","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malingering</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, absence of muscle spasm, tongue thrusting, or post-ictal confusion, and normal investigations just before the final exam (personal gain), points towards <strong>malingering</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Intentional </strong>production of symptoms to achieve some<strong> practical gain</strong> (e.g. avoidance of work, financial compensation, avoidance of criminal responsibility, or military service) is suggestive of malingering.</p>\n<p><strong>Factitious disorder </strong>(Munchausen syndrome) is the <strong>intentional</strong> production of physical symptoms to <strong>obtain medical attention, not a practical gain.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1489","text":"A prison inmate is escorte‍d to the ER with complain​ts of sudden unbearable a‍bdominal pain. However, o‌n evaluation, he was foun‍d to be malingering. Pres​ence of which of the foll​owing would help you diff​erentiate this from facti‍tious disorder?","raw_text":"A prison inmate is escorted to the ER with complaints of sudden unbearable abdominal pain. However, on evaluation, he was found to be malingering. Presence of which of the following would help you differentiate this from factitious disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goal of financial or perso‍nal gain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Goal of getting medical at‍tention","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conscious production of sy‍mptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discrepancy between sympto‍ms and exam findings","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Goal of financial or personal gain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Goal of financial or personal gain</strong></p><p><strong>Malingering</strong> can have many underlying goals, including <strong>financial/personal gain</strong>. However, factitious disorder has only one motive - seeking and attaining medical attention.</p>\n<p>Malingering is suspected when there is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medicolegal</strong> context of presentation</li>\n<li><strong>Discrepancy</strong> between the individual's claimed stress or disability and the objective findings</li>\n<li><strong>Lack of cooperation</strong> during the diagnostic evaluation and in complying with the prescribed treatment regimen</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>antisocial personality</strong> disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Malingering can be for the following reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoidance of <strong>criminal responsibility</strong></li>\n<li>Avoidance of military service or of particularly <strong>hazardous duties</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Financial gain</strong></li>\n<li>Avoidance of work, social responsibility, and social consequences.</li>\n<li>Facilitation of transfer from prison to hospital</li>\n<li>Admission to a hospital (for getting a place to stay)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The discrepancy between symptoms and examination findings, and conscious production of symptoms can be present in both disorders. In <strong>Malingering, </strong>patients can usually <strong>stop producing their signs</strong> and symp­toms when they are <strong>no longer</strong> considered <strong>profitable</strong> or when they become <strong>risky</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yo20wcjB","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"20_metabolic___bariatric_surgery","title":"20.Metabolic & Bariatric Surgery","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC8509","text":"A 35-year-old female patie‍nt of asian ethnicity wit​h a BMI of 34 kg/m² has d‌ecided to seek surgical o​ptions to reduce her weig‌ht. She has multiple como‌rbidities. Which of the f‌ollowing would make her u​nsuitable for bariatric s​urgery?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old female patient of asian ethnicity with a BMI of 34 kg/m² has decided to seek surgical options to reduce her weight. She has multiple comorbidities. Which of the following would make her unsuitable for bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coronary ar‍tery disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BMI below cut-off for surg‍ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Severe d‍epression","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pickwickia‍n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Severe depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Severe depression</strong></p><p><strong>Severe depression</strong> would make her unsuitable for bariatric surgery. The patient should be <strong>psychologically stable</strong> before undergoing bariatric surgery.</p>\n<p>Psychological instability makes the patient incapable of understanding the implications of the procedure. It becomes difficult for the patient to understand the lifestyle changes necessary for optimal outcomes. </p>\n<p>The indications for bariatric surgery as per international standards as well as the modifications specific for Asian and Indian populations are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 408px; width: 480px; border-color: #000000;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 15px; text-align: center;\"><strong>International criteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; height: 15px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Asian-specific modification in BMI</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 36px;\"><strong>1.</strong> <strong>BMI ≥ 40 kg/m<sup>2</sup></strong> regardless of obesity-related comorbidities</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; height: 36px;\">BMI ≥ <strong>37.5</strong> kg/m<sup>2</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 104.84px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 104.84px;\">\n<p><strong>2. BMI ≥ 35 kg/m<sup>2</sup></strong> with obesity-related comorbidities, such as</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Type 2 DM</li>\n<li>Coronary artery disease (CAD)</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n<li>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA): Pickwickian syndrome</li>\n<li>Arthritis and functional impairment</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; height: 104.84px;\">BMI ≥ <strong>32.5</strong> kg/m<sup>2</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>3. Has failed to lose weight despite:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lifestyle modifications</li>\n<li>Pharmacotherapy</li>\n<li>Psychotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; height: 54px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 15px;\">4. Is psychologically stable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; height: 15px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 309px; height: 33px;\">5. Motivated and capable of understanding lifestyle changes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; height: 33px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The criteria for Asian and Indian patients has been modified by <strong>reducing</strong> the BMI by <strong>2.5 points</strong>. This modification takes into account the geographical differences in food, lifestyle, and genetic make-up of patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8527","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a contraindication fo​r bariatric surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure to lose weight des‍pite psychotherapy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Drug ad‍diction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bulimia‍ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unsupportive social and ho‍me environment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Failure to lose weight despite psychotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Failure to lose weight despite psychotherapy</strong></p><p>Failure to lose weight by an obese patient despite <strong>psychotherapy</strong>, pharmacotherapy, and lifestyle modifications is an indication for metabolic surgery.</p>\n<p>Potential <strong>contraindications</strong> to metabolic and bariatric surgery include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe medical disease making anesthesia or surgery prohibitively risky (American Society of Anesthesiologists class IV)</li>\n<li>Mentally incompetent to understand the procedure</li>\n<li>Inability or unwillingness to change lifestyle postoperatively</li>\n<li>Drug, alcohol, or other addiction (including smoking)</li>\n<li>Active problem of bulimia or other eating disorder</li>\n<li>Psychological instability (schizophrenia, severe depression)      </li>\n<li>Unsupportive home environment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8530","text":"For a patient being consid‍ered for bariatric surger​y, which of the following‌ will you not include whe​n calculating the Obesity‌ Surgery-Mortality risk s‌core (OS-MRS score)?","raw_text":"For a patient being considered for bariatric surgery, which of the following will you not include when calculating the Obesity Surgery-Mortality risk score (OS-MRS score)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‍ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type‍-2 DM","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"B‍MI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gen‍der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type-2 DM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type-2 DM</strong></p><p><strong>Type-2 Diabetes mellitus (DM)</strong> is not a component of the Obesity Surgery-Mortality Risk Score (OS-MRS).</p>\n<p>Edmonton Obesity Staging System (EOSS) and Obesity Surgery-Mortality Risk Score (OS-MRS) are used to discuss with patients the likely prognosis without surgery, and the risk of complications.</p>\n<p><strong>Obesity Surgery-Mortality Risk Score (OS-MRS)</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 196px; width: 173px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Risk factor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\"><strong>Score</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">Age ≥ 45 yr</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">BMI ≥ 50</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">Male gender</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">Hypertension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">DVT(Deep Vein Thrombosis)/ PE(Pulmonary Embolism)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 86px; width: 324px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.4766px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 110px; height: 34.4766px;\"><strong>Risk Category</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.6016px; height: 34.4766px;\"><strong>Score</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.398px; height: 34.4766px;\"><strong>Post-op mortality</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 110px; height: 18px;\">A (Low)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.6016px; height: 18px;\">0-1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.398px; height: 18px;\">0.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 110px; height: 36px;\">B (Moderate)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.6016px; height: 36px;\">2-3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.398px; height: 36px;\">1.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 110px; height: 18px;\">C (High)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.6016px; height: 18px;\">4-5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.398px; height: 18px;\">3.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Uncontrolled diabetes is considered a risk factor, as in any other surgery. However, it is not considered under the OS-MRS scoring system.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8531","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‍ with a BMI of 42 kg/m² w​ith central obesity is sc‌heduled to undergo bariat​ric surgery. As preparati‌on for surgery, he is adv‌ised to follow a low-carb‌ohydrate, low-fat, and mo​derate-protein meal, for ​two weeks. On follow-up, ‌he is found to be non-com‍pliant with the diet. Wha​t is the next best step?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient with a BMI of 42 kg/m² with central obesity is scheduled to undergo bariatric surgery. As preparation for surgery, he is advised to follow a low-carbohydrate, low-fat, and moderate-protein meal, for two weeks. On follow-up, he is found to be non-compliant with the diet. What is the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parenteral nutrition for 1‍ week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psychological re-evaluatio‍n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Liquid diet‍ for 1 week","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Re-assurance and follow-up‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psychological re-evaluation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psychological re-evaluation</strong></p><p><strong>Psychological re-evaluation</strong> is necessary to address the underlying psychological blocks in a patient who is unable to comply with the diet.</p>\n<p>Morbidly obese individuals, especially those with <strong>central obesity</strong>, have large livers that make surgery impossible. <strong>Liver shrinkage diet</strong> helps <strong>reduce</strong> the <strong>size of the liver</strong>. This allows mobility for the surgeon and increases the chances of a safer procedure.  </p>\n<p><strong>Liver shrinkage diet</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low-carbohydrate, low-fat and moderate protein diet</li>\n<li>Started at least <strong>2 weeks</strong> prior to the surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ability to comply with the diet and lose weight suggests that the patient is willing to make an effort to adopt lifestyle modifications. Failure to do so must arouse suspicion of important underlying psychological blocks, which need addressing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8532","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a bariatric surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Roux-en-Y gastrojejunostom‍y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biliopancreatic diversion ‍(BPD)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyloric exclusion with gas‍trojejunostomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Duodenal s‍witch (DS)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyloric exclusion with gastrojejunostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyloric exclusion with gastrojejunostomy</strong></p><p><strong>Pyloric exclusion with gastrojejunostomy </strong>is not a bariatric surgery. It is a technique used in the repair of duodenal and pancreatic injuries.</p>\n<p>Based on their mechanism of action, bariatric surgeries are categorized as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 313px; width: 377px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism of action</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Common procedures</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Purely Restrictive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding</li>\n<li>Sleeve gastrectomy</li>\n<li>Intragastric balloon placement</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Predominantly Malabsorptive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Biliopancreatic diversion (BPD)</li>\n<li>Duodenal switch (DS)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Restrictive + Malabsorptive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Roux-en-Y gastrojejunostomy (gastric bypass)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8533","text":"Which of the following is ‍a reversible bariatric su​rgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a reversible bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laparoscopic gastric bandi‍ng","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gas‍tric bypass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laparoscopic sleeve gastre‍ctomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Laparoscopic biliopancreat‍ic diversion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Laparoscopic gastric banding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25078b74e9b74670bd29579658d5eacfx1280x1611.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Laparoscopic gastric banding</strong></p><p><strong>Laparoscopic gastric banding</strong> and <strong>intra-gastric balloon placement</strong> surgeries are considered to be <strong>reversible</strong> bariatric surgeries.</p>\n<p>It is because the gastric band or the intragastric balloon can be removed once the desired weight loss or metabolic benefit is achieved.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">An inflatable band is placed just below the <strong>GE junction</strong> <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span><br/>A small virtual <strong>stomach pouch</strong> is created <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span><br/>Reduces hunger (probably vagally mediated) <br/><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/></span>Reduced consumption of food <br/><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/></span>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Complications of LAGB include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Prolapse / Slippage</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Band erosion of the stomach wall and rupture</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Increased risk of<strong> DVT and pulmonary embolism</strong> (highest risk within 1 month of surgery), managed with early ambulation, pressure stockings and LMWH.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8534","text":"In recent years, what is t‍he most commonly performe​d bariatric surgery?","raw_text":"In recent years, what is the most commonly performed bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Roux-en-Y ga‍stric bypass","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biliopancrea‍tic diversion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laparoscopic gastric bandi‍ng","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sleeve ga‍strectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sleeve gastrectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3dee045c6e19459697263eec82f26b62x1280x1661.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sleeve gastrectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Sleeve gastrectomy</strong> is the most commonly performed bariatric surgery, in recent years.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">A <strong>vertical sleeve</strong> is created, resecting most of the greater curvature <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Restriction of stomach capacity <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/><strong>Early satiety</strong> <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Reduced consumption of food <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Weight loss</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong> of sleeve gastrectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less challenging procedure</li>\n<li>Preservation of pylorus (avoids gastric dumping)</li>\n<li>No need for serial adjustments (as seen with gastric banding)</li>\n<li>Reduction in internal hernias (as seen with Roux-en-Y gastric bypass)  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of sleeve gastrectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleed from the staple line</li>\n<li>Leakage from staple line (close to the angle of His)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8535","text":"A 40-year-old woman with d‍iabetes and a BMI of 46 k​g/m² underwent laparoscop‌ic sleeve gastrectomy (LS​G). On the second postope‌rative day, she developed‌ fever, tachycardia, and ‌blood reports show leucoc​ytosis. What is the next ​best step in her manageme‌nt?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman with diabetes and a BMI of 46 kg/m² underwent laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy (LSG). On the second postoperative day, she developed fever, tachycardia, and blood reports show leucocytosis. What is the next best step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Send blood for culture and‍ sensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG abdomen to locate the ‍septic focus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Re-expl‍oration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contrast enhanced CT abdom‍en","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contrast enhanced CT abdomen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contrast enhanced CT abdomen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of fever, tachycardia, and leucocytosis in a postoperative patient of LSG should raise suspicion for <strong>leak </strong>along the gastric staple line. The next best step in the management of this patient is to perform <strong>a contrast-enhanced CT.</strong></p>\n<p>Leaks following LSG most commonly occur in the <strong>proximal third of the stomach</strong>, close to the <strong>angle of His</strong>. Early leaks are due to tissue trauma and staple misfire, and late leaks are usually due to ischemia and high intragastric pressure.</p>\n<p>Management of the leak involves providing nutritional support to the patient and adequate drainage. Once the leak is diagnosed, the patient should be <strong>kept nil per oral</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Total parenteral nutrition</strong> and <strong>antibiotic therapy</strong> should be started in the patient. Drainage can be done either by CT-guided percutaneous catheter placement, endoscopic stenting, or operative procedures.</p>\n<p>Note: Obstructive sleep apnea <strong>(OSA)</strong> is a risk factor for an <strong>anastomotic leak</strong>. Treatment with continuous positive airway pressure might reduce this risk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8536","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true regarding​ Roux-en-Y gastric bypass‌ in bariatric surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Roux-en-Y gastric bypass in bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric pouch is created a‍t the lesser curvature.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Biliary limb is 100-150 cm‍ in length","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a purely restrictive‍ type of procedure.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A Y-ileoileostomy is perfo‍rmed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gastric pouch is created at the lesser curvature.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2635fd5799854d52b1bd7d1579be2aabx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gastric pouch is created at the lesser curvature.</strong></p><p>Gastric pouch is created at the <strong>lesser curvature</strong> in Roux-en-Y gastric bypass.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">A small proximal gastric pouch is created at the <strong>lesser curvature</strong> <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Create a shorter biliary limb of 30 - 50 cm (duodenum);<br/>Create a longer <strong>Roux limb</strong> of <strong>75 - 150 cm</strong> (jejunum)<br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Bowel continuity is restored by a<strong> Y - jejunojejunostomy <br/></strong><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><strong><br/>Restrictive + Malabsorptive</strong> mechanism <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Weight loss</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Biliary limb</strong> is usually kept short (<strong>30-50 cm</strong>) in order to reduce vitamin and mineral deficiencies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8537","text":"A 38-year-old woman presen‍ted to the casualty with ​vomiting and severe colic‌ky pain in the abdomen fo​r the past 48 hours. She ‌had undergone a weight re‌duction surgery six month‌s ago and had achieved ar​ound 30% weight loss. Her​ plain X-ray abdomen is g‌iven below. Which of the ‍following bariatric proce​dures was most likely don‍e in this patient?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman presented to the casualty with vomiting and severe colicky pain in the abdomen for the past 48 hours. She had undergone a weight reduction surgery six months ago and had achieved around 30% weight loss. Her plain X-ray abdomen is given below. Which of the following bariatric procedures was most likely done in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sleeve ga‍strectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laparoscopic adjustable ga‍stric banding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gas‍tric bypass","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endolumina‍l stapling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/827b0753d10646aca848f426e34e9399.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and radiograph (dilated bowel segments with valvulae conniventes in the left upper quadrant) are suggestive of <strong>small bowel obstruction</strong>. Bariatric surgery, specifically, <strong>Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery</strong> is associated with the <strong>creation of mesenteric defects.</strong> The small bowel can herniate through these defects (<strong>internal hernia</strong>) and get strangulated, resulting in bowel obstruction. </p>\n<p>In addition, as the person <strong>loses weight</strong> (due to the bariatric procedure), the protective, space-occupying effect of the <strong>mesenteric fat is lost</strong> and <strong>hernia spaces open u</strong>p, further increasing the risk of internal hernia. The<strong> laparoscopic</strong> procedure has a <strong>higher risk</strong> compared to open surgery since fewer adhesions form to tether small-bowel loops and prevent them from herniating. </p>\n<p><strong>Adequate closure of the mesenteric defects</strong> by the surgeon at the time of operation significantly reduces the risk of internal hernia.</p>\n<p><strong>Other complications</strong> specific to Roux-en-Y gastric bypass:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nutritional deficiencies: <strong>iron </strong>(most common), folate, Vitamin A, D, K and B12      </li>\n<li>Anastomosis site leak      </li>\n<li>Bleeding from the site of gastrojejunostomy   </li>\n<li>Dumping syndrome</li>\n<li>Marginal ulcers</li>\n<li>Increased risk of DVT and pulmonary embolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Sleeve gastrectomy is often complicated by <strong>staple line leak</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding is associated with <strong>band slippage and erosion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Endoluminal stapling is also used to create a gastric sleeve. It is associated with postoperative nausea, vomiting, <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, and transient dysphagia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8538","text":"A patient who previously u‍nderwent biliopancreatic ​diversion surgery has bee‌n having excessive malabs​orption and is now being ‌planned for a revisional ‌procedure to increase the‌ length of the common cha​nnel. What is the usual l​ength of the common chann‌el?","raw_text":"A patient who previously underwent biliopancreatic diversion surgery has been having excessive malabsorption and is now being planned for a revisional procedure to increase the length of the common channel. What is the usual length of the common channel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100‍ cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50‍ cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"150‍ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"75‍ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7133607f1535458aa2ae22b2796f6be6x1280x2104.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50 cm</strong></p><p>The usual length of the common channel in biliopancreatic diversion (BPD) is <strong>50 cm </strong>(0.5 m).  Revisions increase common channel by up to 150 cm. </p>\n<p>The length of the common channel plays a significant role in the amount of weight loss achieved with BPD procedure. A <strong>shorter common channel</strong> implies a <strong>greater length of the bypassed small bowel. </strong>This induces more malabsorption and thereby greater weight loss. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Distal <strong>hemigastrectomy</strong> is performed to create a residual gastric pouch <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span> <br/>Terminal ileum is divided 250 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span> <br/><strong>Gastroileostomy:</strong> Proximal anastomosis between the distal end of divided ileum and residual gastric pouch <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span> <br/><strong>Ileoileostomy:</strong> Distal anastomosis between the proximal end of divided ileum and terminal ileum <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span> <br/>A <strong>common channel</strong> of <strong>50 cm</strong> is established <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span> <br/>Malabsorption <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓</span> <br/>Weight loss</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n<p>Prophylactic <strong>cholecystectomy</strong> is usually performed since there is an increased incidence of gallstones due to malabsorption of bile salts.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of biliopancreatic diversion surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anastomosis site leak</strong></li>\n<li>Nutritional deficiencies: Iron; folate; vitamins A, D, K, and B12</li>\n<li>Marginal ulcers </li>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Increased risk of DVT and pulmonary embolism (maximum risk within 1 month of surgery), managed with early ambulation, pressure stocking, and LMWH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8539","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a surgical step that ​will be done during a duo‌denal switch procedure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a surgical step that will be done during a duodenal switch procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastroduo‍denostomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cholecy‍stectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duododeno‍ileostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sleeve ga‍strectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gastroduodenostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5717ea3429d84e44883689f6bdec4020x1280x2103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gastroduodenostomy</strong></p><p>In duodenal switch (DS) surgery, gastroduodenostomy is not performed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Sleeve gastrectomy</strong> is performed <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Duodenum is divided 2 cm beyond the pylorus; <br/>Terminal ileum is divided 250 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/><strong>Duodenoileostomy:</strong> Proximal anastomosis between the distal end of divided ileum and duodenum <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/><strong>Ileoileostomy:</strong> Distal anastomosis between the proximal end of divided ileum and terminal ileum <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>A <strong>common channel</strong> of <strong>100 cm</strong> is established <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Malabsorption <br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\">↓ </span><br/>Weight loss </p>\n<p>Prophylactic <strong>cholecystectomy</strong> is usually performed since there is an increased incidence of gallstones due to malabsorption of bile salts.                                        </p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of duodenal switch surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anastomosis site leak and bleeding</li>\n<li>Nutritional deficiencies - Iron, folate, Vitamin A, D, K and B12</li>\n<li>Increased risk of DVT and pulmonary embolism (maximum risk within 1 month of surgery) - managed with early ambulation, pressure stocking, and LMWH In comparison to BPD, the incidence of marginal ulcers is negligible</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8638","text":"What is the most common ca‍use of death in patients ​who undergo bariatric sur‌gery?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of death in patients who undergo bariatric surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary‍ embolism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anastomo‍tic leak","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sep‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intra-abdo‍minal bleed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulmonary embolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulmonary embolism</strong></p><p>Pulmonary embolism is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>cause of death</strong> in patients of bariatric surgery.</p>\n<p>Pathogenesis:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Obesity<br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\"> ↓ </span><br/>Increased levels of <strong>plasminogen activator inhibitor-1</strong><br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\"> ↓ </span><br/>Blocks conversion of plasminogen to plasmin<br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\"> ↓ </span><br/><strong>Decreased fibrinolytic activity</strong><br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\"> ↓ </span><br/><strong>Hypercoagulable state</strong><br/><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\"> ↓ </span><br/>Increased risk for VTE</p>\n<p>Morbidly obese patients are physically inactive. <strong>Restricted mobility</strong> post-surgery potentiates this risk of developing deep vein thrombosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7478","text":"In patients who undergo ga‍stric bypass surgery (bar​iatric surgery), what is ‌the 3-year percentage of ​diabetes remission?","raw_text":"In patients who undergo gastric bypass surgery (bariatric surgery), what is the 3-year percentage of diabetes remission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50‍ %","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"33‍ %","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20‍ %","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60‍ %","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50 %","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50 %</strong></p><p>The 3-year percentage of diabetes remission for gastric bypass surgery is <strong>50%.</strong> </p>\n<p>Different Bariatric surgeries and their Outcomes :</p>\n<table style=\"height: 236px; width: 457px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.13px;\"><strong>Protein/Calorie Malabsorption</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.2407px;\"><strong>3 year % Excess Weight Loss (EWL)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.537px;\"><strong>3 yr % Diabetes remission</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\">Gastric band</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.13px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.2407px;\">\n<p>40 -50 %</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.537px;\">20 %</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\">Gastric bypass</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.13px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.2407px;\">50 - 60 %</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.537px;\">50 %</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\">Sleeve gastrectomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.13px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.2407px;\">50 - 60 %</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.537px;\">50 %</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\">Biliopancreatic diversion / Duodenal switch </td>\n<td style=\"width: 107.13px;\"><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.2407px;\">70 -80 %</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.537px;\">80 %</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"x5s_rwym","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"21_regional_anesthesia__complications_and_contraindications","title":"21.Regional Anesthesia  Complications And Contraindications","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MF7828","text":"A 65-year-old lady with a ​history of motion sicknes​s underwent a knee replac‍ement under spinal anesth‌esia. After the procedure‍, she complains she is fe​eling nauseous. Which of ‌the following is false ab​out post-anesthetic nause‍a and vomiting associated‍?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old lady with a history of motion sickness underwent a knee replacement under spinal anesthesia. After the procedure, she complains she is feeling nauseous. Which of the following is false about post-anesthetic nausea and vomiting associated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"History of motion sickness​ is a risk factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elderly age groups are at ​lesser risk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Female patients are at hig​her risk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intrathecal morphine admin​istration has the least i​ncidence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intrathecal morphine administration has the least incidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intrathecal morphine administration has the least incidence</strong></p><p><strong>Intrathecal morphine</strong> has a <strong>higher risk</strong> and incidence of nausea and vomiting <strong>among opioids</strong> as compared to fentanyl/sufentanil.</p>\n<p>This is because <strong>hydrophilic</strong> opioids (morphine) remain in CSF for a <strong>prolonged duration</strong> than lipophilic opioids (fentanyl) and sensitize the <strong>central chemoreceptor trigger zone</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Apfel score</strong> is used to predict the risk of developing postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV). According to this, risk factors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Post-operative use of opioids</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Non-smoker</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Female gender</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">History of postoperative nausea, vomiting, or motion sickness</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Additional risk factors are the use of <strong>nitrous oxide</strong> as an inhalational anesthetic agent and the duration of anesthesia.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Ondansetron</strong> with or without dexamethasone is used in the management of this condition. The use of <strong>propofol</strong> in balanced or total IV anesthesia (TIVA) significantly reduces the incidence of PONV.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6468","text":"A patient was scheduled to​ undergo a vaginal hyster​ectomy procedure under ep‍idural anaesthesia. 5 min‌utes after the epidural b‍lock was given, the patie​nt started shivering. Whi‌ch of the following is fa​lse regarding post-neurax‍ial shivering?","raw_text":"A patient was scheduled to undergo a vaginal hysterectomy procedure under epidural anaesthesia. 5 minutes after the epidural block was given, the patient started shivering. Which of the following is false regarding post-neuraxial shivering?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is not a sign of malignant​ hyperthermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More common with spinal th​an epidural anesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural fentanyl can prev​ent shivering","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I.V. meperidine is used fo​r treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. More common with spinal than epidural anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. More common with spinal than epidural anesthesia</strong></p><p>Post-neuraxial shivering is <strong>more common with epidural</strong> than spinal anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>The possible reasons include the use of a large volume of cold epidural injectate as compared to a small volume of spinal drug and the inability to shiver with spinal anaesthesia because of the profound motor block as compared with epidural techniques.</p>\n<p>Post-neuraxial shivering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> incidence</strong> of post-neuraxial shivering is more than 40% (~<strong>55%</strong>)</li>\n<li>The addition of neuraxial <strong>opioids</strong> (fentanyl, pethidine) <strong>reduces</strong> the likelihood of shivering</li>\n<li><strong>Prewarming</strong> the patient with a forced-air warmer and avoiding the administration of cold epidural and intravenous fluids helps to prevent shivering</li>\n<li>It has no relation to malignant hyperthermia</li>\n<li><strong>Intravenous meperidine</strong> and tramadol are used in the <strong>treatment</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6469","text":"A patient was undergoing a​ surgical procedure under​ spinal anaesthesia. 30 m‍ins into the procedure, h‌is EtCO2 fell and the pat‍ient became apneic. Bag a​nd mask ventilation was g‌iven and the patient rega​ined consciousness and be‍gan to spontaneously resp‍ire in 10 mins. What is t​he most common cause of t‌he apnea seen in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A patient was undergoing a surgical procedure under spinal anaesthesia. 30 mins into the procedure, his EtCO2 fell and the patient became apneic. Bag and mask ventilation was given and the patient regained consciousness and began to spontaneously respire in 10 mins. What is the most common cause of the apnea seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diaphragmat​ic paralysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intercostal muscle paralys​is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic toxicity of the l​ocal anesthetic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypotension</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>apnea</strong> <strong>following neuraxial blockade</strong> is <strong>hypotension</strong> leading to hypoperfusion of the brain stem respiratory centers.</p>\n<p><strong>Diaphragmatic paralysis</strong> would require the height of the block up to the cervical level (phrenic nerve), which is very rare. However, it can occur in the setting of <strong>high spinal</strong> up to the cervical level and <strong>total spinal</strong> anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Blockade of the intercostal and abdominal muscles during neuraxial anesthesia is adequately compensated by an unaltered function of the diaphragm and other accessory respiratory muscles.</p>\n<p>Systemic toxicity of the local anesthetic would mainly affect the central nervous system and cardiovascular system.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6150","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about sp​inal-anesthesia-induced h‍ypotension?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about spinal-anesthesia-induced hypotension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nausea is the first sympto​m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is common with block he​ight above T5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sustained hypotension can ​lead to apnea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is treated with epineph​rine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is treated with epinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is treated with epinephrine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Spinal-anesthesia-induced hypotension is treated with <strong>ephedrine</strong> (most commonly), mephentermine or phenylephrine. Epinephrine is reserved only for refractory hypotension or cardiac arrest following spinal anesthesia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk factors for spinal-anesthesia-induced hypotension:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>Peak <strong>block height</strong> greater than or equal to <strong>T5</strong></li>\n<li>Age older than or equal to 40 years</li>\n<li>Baseline systolic blood pressure less than 120 mm Hg</li>\n<li>Combined spinal and general anesthesia</li>\n<li>Spinal puncture at or above the L2-L3 interspace</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Clinical features:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>SBP &lt; 90 mmHg</li>\n<li><strong>Nausea precedes hypotension </strong>(first symptom to appear)</li>\n<li><strong>Apnea</strong> due to medullary hypoperfusion</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> Treatment:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" style=\"text-align: left;\" title=\"Page 982\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rapid administration of intravenous fluids</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Head-down position (only after local anesthesia is fixed (~20 minutes for bupivacaine), else, hyperbaric solution can ascend and lead to high spinal)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intravenous vasopressors (ephedrine, phenylephrine)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bradycardia is treated with atropine</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6138","text":"Which of the following ner​ve fibres are included un​der cardioaccelerator fib‍res?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerve fibres are included under cardioaccelerator fibres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C1​-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C4​-7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T1​-4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T5​-L2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T1-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T1-4</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The sympathetic <strong>cardio-accelerator fibers </strong>include<strong> T1–T4.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>High spinal </strong>block above the level of T4, will block the cardio-acceleratory fibers which supply the heart. This would lead to severe <strong>bradycardia </strong>along with<strong> hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6139","text":"You are planning to admini​ster spinal anesthesia to​ a young female. Which of‍ the following needles wo‌uld be the least suitable‍ for reducing the risk of​ a post-dural puncture he‌adache?","raw_text":"You are planning to administer spinal anesthesia to a young female. Which of the following needles would be the least suitable for reducing the risk of a post-dural puncture headache?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22G Q​uincke","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"22G Wh​itacre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25G Q​uincke","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"25G Wh​itacre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 22G Quincke","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 22G Quincke</strong></p><p>Post-dural puncture headache is most commonly seen with 22G Quincke needle (22G has a larger diameter than 25G). So it should be avoided in cases with a higher risk of PDPH.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of spinal needles:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 267px;\" width=\"407\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\"><strong>Cutting needle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\"><strong>Non-cutting (Splitting, pencil point, blunt) needle</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">Cut the dura</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">Split the dura</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">Hence higher incidence of post dural puncture headache (PDPH) due to CSF leak</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">Hence, lower incidence of PDPH</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">Technically less challenging as loss of resistance is well appreciated during lumbar puncture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">Technically more challenging as loss of resistance is not well appreciated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\"><strong>Eg: Quincke-Babcock, Pitkin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\"><strong> Eg: Whitacre, Sprotte</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6143","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding post-dura​l puncture headache?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding post-dural puncture headache?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to intracranial hypote​nsion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Usually seen within first ​72 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relieved ​on sitting","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Common with cutting spinal​ needles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Relieved on sitting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Relieved on sitting</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Post-dural puncture headache <strong>worsens </strong>with<strong> upright </strong>or<strong> sitting position</strong> and is <strong>relieved</strong> by <strong>supine</strong> position.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Post-dural puncture headache is believed to result from puncture of the dura (unintentional or intentional) in the setting of neuraxial anaesthesia. This causes leakage of CSF from a dural defect leading to intracranial hypotension.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The characteristic feature of a post-dural puncture headache is the <strong>frontal </strong>or an<strong> occipital headache.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6144","text":"Which of the following doe​s the incidence of post-d​ural puncture headache no‍t depend on?","raw_text":"Which of the following does the incidence of post-dural puncture headache not depend on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Timing of ​ambulation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Preg​nancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gen​der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Timing of ambulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Timing of ambulation</strong></p><p>The timing of ambulation does not seem to affect the incidence of post-dural puncture headache, i.e., early ambulation does not increase the incidence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6145","text":"A 23-year-old primigravida​ underwent C- section und​er spinal anesthesia admi‍nistered by 22G Quincke n‌eedle. 18 hours after the‍ procedure she complained​ of a headache which was ‌aggravated by sitting up ​and relieved on lying dow‍n. What is the definitive‍ treatment for the condit​ion that she is suffering‌ from?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old primigravida underwent C- section under spinal anesthesia administered by 22G Quincke needle. 18 hours after the procedure she complained of a headache which was aggravated by sitting up and relieved on lying down. What is the definitive treatment for the condition that she is suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Analg​esics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caff​eine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supine ​posture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidural b​lood patch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epidural blood patch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epidural blood patch</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The scenario given in the question is most likely <strong>post-dural puncture headache</strong>(PDPH). The <strong>definitive treatment</strong> for post-dural puncture headache is <strong>epidural blood patch</strong>. The remaining options are conservative management approaches for PDPH.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Procedure:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Ideally, performed 24 hours after dural puncture and development of classic PDPH symptoms.</li>\n<li>15-20 mL of autologous blood is injected into the epidural space at same or one interspace below the level of the dural puncture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It stops further leakage of CSF by either mass effect or coagulation. A <strong>second patch</strong> can be performed after <strong>24-48 hours</strong> if needed. Approximately 90% of patients will respond to a single blood patch, and 90% of initial nonresponders will obtain relief from a second injection.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6146","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding epidural ​blood patch?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding epidural blood patch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 mL of blood injected in​to epidural space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Effective in 90% of patien​ts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A second patch performed a​fter 24-48 hours if neede​d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prophylactic blood patch i​s recommended as per the ​recent guidelines","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prophylactic blood patch is recommended as per the recent guidelines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prophylactic blood patch is recommended as per the recent guidelines</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prophylactic blood patch is <strong>not</strong> recommended as its efficacy is not supported by evidence.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Epidural blood patch:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Definitive therapy for PDPH</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Performed <strong>24–48 hours</strong> after dural puncture and the development of classic PDPH symptoms.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>15–20 mL</strong> of autologous blood is injected into the <strong>epidural space</strong> at the <strong>same</strong> or <strong>one interspace below</strong> the level of the dural puncture.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">It stops further leakage of CSF by either mass effect or coagulation.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">A <strong>second patch</strong> can be performed after <strong>24-48 hours,</strong> if needed.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Approximately 90% of patients will respond to a single blood patch, and 90% of initial nonresponders will obtain relief from a second injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6470","text":"Soon after the administrat​ion of an epidural block ​with 1.5 % lignocaine wit‍h epinephrine, your patie‌nt went into bradycardia,‍ hypotension, and apnea. ​What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"Soon after the administration of an epidural block with 1.5 % lignocaine with epinephrine, your patient went into bradycardia, hypotension, and apnea. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accidental subdural inject​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accidental subarachnoid in​jection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Accidental intravascular i​njection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic manifestation to ​lignocaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Accidental subarachnoid injection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Accidental subarachnoid injection</strong></p><p>Bradycardia, hypotension, and apnea <strong>soon after</strong> the administration of an epidural block suggests <strong>accidental subarachnoid</strong> (intrathecal) <strong>injection</strong> resulting in <strong>total spinal anaesthesia.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 983\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Salient features of total spinal anesthesia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs following attempted epidural/caudal anesthesia if there is <strong>accidental intrathecal injection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rapid onset</strong>, as the anesthetic required for epidural and caudal anesthesia, is 5–10 times that required for spinal anesthesia</li>\n<li>High spinal anesthesia extends to involve <strong>cranial nerves</strong></li>\n<li>Accompanied by hypotension, bradycardia, apnea, and <strong>loss of consciousness</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prevention:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Careful aspiration for CSF (to rule out intrathecal placement) and blood (to rule out intravascular placement) before injecting the local anesthesia</li>\n<li>Use of a test dose with lidocaine and adrenaline</li>\n<li>Incremental injection techniques instead of a bolus dose</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintaining an adequate airway and ventilation and supporting the circulation</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A - Accidental subdural injection manifests after 15-20 minutes with features similar to a high spinal blockade.</p>\n<p>Option C - Accidental intravascular injection manifests as systemic toxicity with CNS and CVS symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option D - Allergic manifestation presents with hives, angioedema, and bronchospasm.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6471","text":"A patient was administered​ an epidural block before​ a lower limb surgery. Af‍ter 20 minutes, the patie‌nt went into bradycardia,‍ hypotension, and apnea. ​What is the most likely c‌ause?","raw_text":"A patient was administered an epidural block before a lower limb surgery. After 20 minutes, the patient went into bradycardia, hypotension, and apnea. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accidental subdural inject​ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Accidental subarachnoid in​jection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accidental intravascular i​njection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic manifestation to ​lignocaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Accidental subdural injection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Accidental subdural injection</strong></p><p>Bradycardia, hypotension, and apnea presenting <strong>20 minutes</strong> after epidural injection suggest <strong>accidental subdural injection.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of subdural anaesthesia is similar to that of high spinal anaesthesia, with the exception that the <strong>onset </strong>may be<strong> delayed </strong>for 15–30 min and the <strong>block</strong> may be <strong>patchy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Unlike the epidural space, the <strong>subdural space extends intracranially.</strong> Hence, the anaesthetic injected into the subdural space can ascend to <strong>higher levels </strong>than epidural medications.</p>\n<p>As with high spinal anaesthesia, treatment is supportive and may require intubation, mechanical ventilation, and cardiovascular support.</p>\n<p>Option B - Accidental subarachnoid injection manifests immediately with similar features.</p>\n<p>Option C - Accidental intravascular injection presents with CNS and CVS symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option D - Allergic manifestation presents with hives, angioedema and, bronchospasm.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6472","text":"A patient was administered​ an epidural block with 1​.5 % lignocaine with epin‍ephrine. Immediately afte‌r the injection, the pati‍ent became disoriented fo​llowed by an episode of s‌eizures ending in apnea. ​His BP was 80/50 mm Hg. W‍hat is this most likely d‍ue to?","raw_text":"A patient was administered an epidural block with 1.5 % lignocaine with epinephrine. Immediately after the injection, the patient became disoriented followed by an episode of seizures ending in apnea. His BP was 80/50 mm Hg.  What is this most likely due to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accidental subdural inject​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accidental subarachnoid in​jection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accidental intravascular i​njection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic manifestation to ​lignocaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Accidental intravascular injection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Accidental intravascular injection</strong></p><p>The above condition is most likely due to <strong>accidental intravascular injection</strong> of local anaesthetic into the epidural vein resulting in <strong>local anaesthesia systemic toxicity</strong> (LAST).</p>\n<p><strong>Features of LAST:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CNS</strong> (in the order of appearance)\n<ul>\n<li>Tinnitus, blurred vision, dizziness, tongue paresthesia, and circumoral numbness</li>\n<li>Excitatory signs such as nervousness, agitation, restlessness, and muscle twitching leading to <strong>tonic-clonic seizures</strong></li>\n<li>CNS depression with slurred speech, <strong>drowsiness</strong>, unconsciousness, and then <strong>respiratory arrest</strong></li>\n<li>Patients who have received CNS depressant drugs may present with only CNS depression without any preceding excitatory signs</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>CVS </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prolonged PR </strong>intervals and <strong>widened QRS </strong>complexes, and <strong>sinus bradycardia</strong>/arrest</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular arrhythmias</strong>, including fibrillation, are more likely to occur with <strong>bupivacaine</strong> than lidocaine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prevention:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Careful aspiration for CSF (to rule out intrathecal placement) and blood (to rule out intravascular placement) before injecting the local anaesthesia</li>\n<li>Use of a test dose with lidocaine and adrenaline</li>\n<li>Incremental injection techniques instead of a bolus dose</li>\n<li>Close observation for early signs of intravascular injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seizures: <strong>Benzodiazepine</strong>; if not available, a small dose of propofol or thiopentone. If seizures persist, a <strong>neuromuscular blockade</strong> to minimize acidosis and hypoxemia</li>\n<li>Cardiac arrest: standard <strong>ACLS</strong> protocol with few modifications</li>\n<li><strong>Lipid emulsion therapy</strong></li>\n<li>Last resort: cardio-pulmonary bypass</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6474","text":"A first-year anaesthesia r​esident was attempting to​ administer epidural anae‍sthesia with bupivacaine.‌ Inadvertently he injecte‍d the local anaesthetic i​ntravascularly. Following‌ this, the patient’s ECG ​showed prolonged PR inter‍vals, widened QRS complex‍es and sinus bradycardia.​ Which of the following m‌easures could not have pr​evented this?","raw_text":"A first-year anaesthesia resident was attempting to administer epidural anaesthesia with bupivacaine. Inadvertently he injected the local anaesthetic intravascularly. Following this, the patient’s ECG showed prolonged PR intervals, widened QRS complexes and sinus bradycardia. Which of the following measures could not have prevented this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirating before injectin​g","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Using t​est dose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Using bo​lus dose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Using incre​mental doses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Using bolus dose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Using bolus dose</strong></p><p>Using bolus dose does not prevent systemic toxic side effects from accidental intravascular injection of local anaesthetics.</p>\n<p>The major systemic toxic side effects from accidental intravascular injection can be avoided by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial <strong>test dose</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Aspirating</strong> prior to each injection (negative aspiration for blood)</li>\n<li>Using <strong>incremental dosing</strong></li>\n<li>Close <strong>observation</strong> for early signs of intravascular injection (tinnitus, lingual sensations)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6476","text":"A patient who was given ep​idural anaesthesia for a ​knee surgery developed fe‍ver, headache, vomiting a‌nd neck stiffness on the ‍4th post-operative day. W​hich of the following is ‌the most likely agent cau​sing this?","raw_text":"A patient who was given epidural anaesthesia for a knee surgery developed fever, headache, vomiting and neck stiffness on the 4th post-operative day. Which of the following is the most likely agent causing this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strept​ococcus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyl​ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E. ​coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haemophilus​ influenzae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Streptococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Streptococcus</strong></p><p>The above case scenario is suggestive of <strong>bacterial meningitis</strong> <strong>following epidural anaesthesia</strong>. The most common organism responsible for bacterial meningitis following neuraxial blockade (spinal and epidural) is <strong><em>Streptococcus viridians</em>.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1995\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>However, the most common organisms responsible for epidural-anaesthesia-related local infection (abscess) is<strong> </strong><em>Staphylococcus</em>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><em>Streptococcus viridians</em> is an oral commensal, emphasizing the purpose of wearing a mask as part of a full aseptic technique.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6494","text":"The nurse provides you wit​h the case list for the d​ay. In which of the follo‍wing patients can centrin‌euraxial (spinal and epid‍ural) anaesthesia be safe​ly administered to immedi‌ately?","raw_text":"The nurse provides you with the case list for the day.  In which of the following patients can centrineuraxial (spinal and epidural) anaesthesia be safely administered to immediately?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 year old boy with h/o b​leeding diathesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"72 year old man on low dos​e aspirin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"48 year old woman on warfa​rin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"32 year old ​woman on LMWH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 72 year old man on low dose aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 72 year old man on low dose aspirin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Being on <strong>low dose aspirin</strong> is not a contraindication for the use of centrineuraxial anaesthesia. So, it is safe to administer it to a patient on low dose aspirin.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is because use of most antiplatelet agents (eg. Aspirin &amp; NSAIDs) is not associated with an increased risk of complications from centrineuraxial anaesthesia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LMWH administration must be stopped prophylactically (12 hours prior) and therapeutically (24 hours prior) to centrineuraxial anaesthesia. It can be done only after checking if the platelet count is normal (if LMWH has been administered for more than 4 days). This is due to reports of <strong>spinal/epidural hematomas</strong> when centrineuraxial anaesthesia is given to patients on LMWH.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6140","text":"Which of the following is ​not an absolute contraind​ication for spinal anaest‍hesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an absolute contraindication for spinal anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient​ refusal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelo​pathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Raised intracranial pressu​re","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local i​nfection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myelopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myelopathy</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">All of the above are absolute contraindications for spinal anaesthesia except <strong>myelopathy</strong>, which is a<strong> relative contraindication.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In the setting of neurological deficit (myelopathy), careful evaluation of neurologic status must first be undertaken and noted along with documentation of the discussion of the risks and benefits for a medico-legal purpose.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6141","text":"As a first-year anaesthesi​a resident posted in the ​emergency OT, you are han‍ded the list of cases pos‌ted for surgery. In which‍ of the following patient​s can you plan to perform‌ spinal anaesthesia?","raw_text":"As a first-year anaesthesia resident posted in the emergency OT, you are handed the list of cases posted for surgery. In which of the following patients can you plan to perform spinal anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient with polytrauma an​d massive bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient having pyogenic ve​rtebral osteomyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patient having a coronary ​stent on clopidogrel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient with hypertension ​on amlodipine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patient with hypertension on amlodipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patient with hypertension on amlodipine</strong></p><p>Patient being on <strong>antihypertensives</strong> like CCBs (amlodipine) is <strong>not a contraindication</strong> for a neuraxial block.</p>\n<p><strong>Patient on antiplatelets:</strong></p>\n<p>Antiplatelets (except aspirin and NSAIDs) should be withheld till their effects have worn off as they increase the risk of spinal hematoma.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 260px;\" width=\"264\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.484px;\"><strong>Antiplatelets</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.516px;\"><strong>Waiting period</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.484px;\">Clopidogrel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.516px;\">7 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.484px;\">Ticlopidine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.516px;\">14 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.484px;\">Abciximab</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113.516px;\">48 hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 964\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6148","text":"A patient on therapeutic l​ow-molecular-weight hepar​in (LMWH) is to be posted‍ for surgery. How many ho‌urs after the last dose o‍f LMWH can a neuraxial bl​ock be safely performed?","raw_text":"A patient on therapeutic low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) is to be posted for surgery. How many hours after the last dose of LMWH can a neuraxial block be safely performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immed​iately","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"After ​4 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After 1​2 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After 2​4 hours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. After 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. After 24 hours</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The neuraxial block can be safely performed <strong>24 hours </strong>after the last<strong> therapeutic dose</strong> of low-molecular-weight heparin <strong>(LMWH).</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In a patient who has received a<strong> prophylactic dose </strong>of LMWH, the block is delayed for <strong>12 hours.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Postoperative LMWH thromboprophylaxis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Remove epidural catheter 2 hours prior to the first LMWH dose</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">If thromboprophylaxis is already started, remove epidural catheter 10 hours after a dose of LMWH. Subsequent LMWH dosing is given 2 hours after the removal of the catheter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6149","text":"A 65-year-old woman with a​ history of DVT and CKD i​s on heparin. She is set ‍to undergo a hemiarthropl‌asty under neuraxial bloc‍k. How much interval shou​ld there be between her l‌ast dose of unfractionate​d heparin and the surgica‍l procedure?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman with a history of DVT and CKD is on heparin. She is set to undergo a hemiarthroplasty under neuraxial block. How much interval should there be between her last dose of unfractionated heparin and the surgical procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immed​iately","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 h​ours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 h​ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 hours</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An interval of atleast <strong>4 hours</strong> must be there between her last dose of unfractionated heparin and the surgical procedure being performed under neuraxial block.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As the patient has CKD, only unfractionated heparin can be used in her. The subsequent dose can be given 1 hour after the neuraxial technique.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>“Minidose” subcutaneous heparin</strong> prophylaxis, aspirin and NSAIDs are not contraindications to neuraxial anesthesia or epidural catheter removal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6493","text":"Which one of the following​ is the description of th​e term allodynia used in ‍the context of pain manag‌ement?","raw_text":"Which one of the following is the description of the term allodynia used in the context of pain management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perception of ordinarily n​on-noxious stimulus as se​vere pain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of pain perception​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"pain in area that lacks se​nsation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unpleasant sensation with ​or without a stimulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perception of ordinarily non-noxious stimulus as severe pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perception of ordinarily non-noxious stimulus as severe pain</strong></p><p>Perception of ordinarily<strong> non-noxious stimulus </strong>as<strong> severe pain</strong> is<strong> </strong>Allodynia.</p>\n<p>Absence of pain perception: Analgesia</p>\n<p>Pain in an area that lacks sensation: Anaesthesia Dolorosa</p>\n<p>Unpleasant sensation with or without a stimulus: Dysesthesia</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6484","text":"You are assessing a 4-year​-old child who underwent ​an appendicectomy for pai‍n. You are using the Chil‌dren Hospital Eastern Ont‍ario Pain Scale (CHEOPS) ​for rating postoperative ‌pain. Which of the follow​ing parameters will you n‍ot use for this?","raw_text":"You are assessing a 4-year-old child who underwent an appendicectomy for pain. You are using the Children Hospital Eastern Ontario Pain Scale (CHEOPS) for rating postoperative pain. Which of the following parameters will you not use for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Verbal e​xpression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Touch p​osition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body p​osition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxygen s​aturation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oxygen saturation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oxygen saturation</strong></p><p>The Children Hospital Eastern Ontario Pain Scale (CHEOPS) for rating postoperative pain in children does not include oxygen saturation.</p>\n<p>It includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>crying</li>\n<li>facial expression</li>\n<li>body position (torso)</li>\n<li>verbal expression</li>\n<li>touch position</li>\n<li>leg position.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other pain assessment scales include-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oucher face scale</li>\n<li>Visual analogue scale (VAS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6479","text":"Transient neurological sym​ptoms are mostly attribut​ed to the use of which lo‍cal anesthetic?","raw_text":"Transient neurological symptoms are mostly attributed to the use of which local anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupiv​acaine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lido​caine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prilo​caine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ropiv​acaine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lidocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lidocaine</strong></p><p>Transient neurological symptoms are mostly attributed to the use of <strong>intrathecal lidocaine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: TNS was previously known as transient radicular irritation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ErIxDAtw","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"21_white_matter_of_the_brain","title":"21.White Matter Of The Brain","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC5278","text":"The structure marked in th​e given picture arises fr‌om the _______.","raw_text":"The structure marked in the given picture arises from the _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mamilla​ry body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thal​amus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amyg​dala","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hippocampus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9e7549f7c6344f06ac2304c3b72f2afa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dea1ee08b1164f04959d033412a4a0a0x600x577.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hippocampus</strong></p><p>This is a coronal section of the brain and the marked structure is the <strong>fornix. </strong>It arises from the <strong>hippocampus</strong> and ends in the <strong>mammillary body </strong>of the hypothalamus<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7432","text":"Which fibre is marked by t​he arrow in the image giv‌en below?","raw_text":"Which fibre is marked by the arrow in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short as​sociation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Long ass​ociation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proje​ction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Commi​ssural","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Projection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/35f5d3cfb2e344bc8233c754df37dfce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c97f54709cd546ee970ca0051e1e3dbbx600x592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Projection</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the<strong> internal capsule</strong>, which is a form of <strong>projection fiber.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0920","text":"An infant is referred to n​eurology due to an abnorm‌al MRI report as shown be‍low. What is the most ant‌erior part of the missing‍ brain structure?","raw_text":"An infant is referred to neurology due to an abnormal MRI report as shown below. What is the most anterior part of the missing brain structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ros​trum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ge​nu","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sple​nium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tr​unk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Genu","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d1b8ad1c84404ea1af785084abd1411b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51117c4ddd3a41ff8dbe82320b85980bx1280x1710.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea1ca513e51f42c0a74b1247cee397f5x1280x1710.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Genu</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>total agenesis of the corpus callosum</strong>. The most anterior part of the missing brain structure i.e. the corpus callosum is the<strong> genu.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows an MRI scan of the sagittal section of a normal brain followed by an image showing corpus callosum agenesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2249","text":"What is the area marked as​ 'A' in the image below?","raw_text":"What is the area marked as 'A' in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Striate​ cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ros​trum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dentate​ nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rostrum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/767b1013553745be989593085363448d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rostrum</strong></p><p>The area marked <strong>'</strong><strong>A'</strong> in the image is <strong>rostrum </strong>of the<strong> corpus callosum.</strong> It connects the orbital surfaces of the frontal lobes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2245","text":"An infant is brought to th​e casualty with the sympt‌om given below. MRI revea‍led the agenesis of a cru‌cial cranial structure. W‍hich part of this structu‍re give rise to tapetum?","raw_text":"An infant is brought to the casualty with the symptom given below. MRI revealed the agenesis of a crucial cranial structure. Which part of this structure give rise to tapetum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Body​ only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genu and r​ostrum both","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rostru​m only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Body and sp​lenium both","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Body and splenium both","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21e7c9d0fb754fc7b9f88ac447aca634.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Body and splenium both</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>infantile spasms </strong>is suggestive of <strong>Aicardi syndrome</strong>, an X-linked dominant disorder associated with <strong>agenesis of the corpus callosum</strong>. The fibres of the <strong>tapetum</strong> originate from both the <strong>body and splenium </strong>of the <strong>corpus callosum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9263","text":"Which of the following is ​connected to the structur‌e marked as A in the imag‍e below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is connected to the structure marked as A in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital​ cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Striate​ cortex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Optic ​chiasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Striate cortex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f02994eb244448ea875cd193d33a013.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/549704a53dcd4aa58d4d753824d47523x1280x1056.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Striate cortex</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the <strong>splenium of the corpus callosum </strong>and it is connected to the <strong>striate cortex.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>body </strong>of the corpus callosum arches posteriorly and ends as the thickened posterior portion called the <strong>splenium. </strong>The fibers from the splenium form the <strong>forceps major</strong> and arch backward into the <strong>occipital lobe (striate cortex).</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>hippocampus </strong>is not connected directly to the<strong> splenium, </strong>but situated <strong>inferior </strong>to the splenium.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the parts of the corpus callosum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0921","text":"A woman presented with spl​it-brain syndrome followi‌ng an experimental brain ‍surgery to manage severe ‌epileptic seizures. Which‍ fibres constitute the su‍rgically resected structu​re?","raw_text":"A woman presented with split-brain syndrome following an experimental brain surgery to manage severe epileptic seizures. Which fibres constitute the surgically resected structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Projecti​on fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Commissur​al fibers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Short associ​ation fibers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long associ​ation fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Commissural fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Commissural fibers</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>split-brain syndrome</strong> is a type of disconnection syndrome following <strong>corpus callosotomy. </strong><strong>C</strong><strong>ommissural fibres </strong>constitute the surgically resected structure i.e. the <strong>c</strong><strong>orpus callosum</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Corpus callosotomy</strong> is a procedure where the corpus callosum is resected as a last resort to manage intractable epileptic seizures. The resultant predicament is that the patient would behave in some ways as if it were two separate minds.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8789","text":"The cingulum of the brain ​is classified under _____‌_______.","raw_text":"The cingulum of the brain is classified under ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Projecti​on fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Long associ​ation fibers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Short associ​ation fibers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Commissur​al fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Long association fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Long association fibers</strong></p><p>The <strong>cingulum</strong> of the brain is classified under <strong>long association fibers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cingulum lies within the cingulate gyrus, connecting the frontal and parietal lobes with the parahippocampal and temporal cortical regions</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9529","text":"Following road traffic acc​ident, a man developed he‌ad injury resulting in a ‍defect in the association‌ fibres. Which of the fol‍lowing is not classified ‍under these fibres?","raw_text":"Following road traffic accident, a man developed head injury resulting in a defect in the association fibres. Which of the following is not classified under these fibres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cing​ulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corona ​radiata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"For​nix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arcuate f​asciculus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corona radiata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corona radiata</strong></p><p><strong>Corona radiata</strong> is not classified under association fibers. It an example of <strong>projection fiber.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cingulum</strong> is a <strong>long association fiber</strong> connecting the frontal and parietal lobes with the parahippocampal and temporal cortical region.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fornix</strong> is an example of <strong>association fibe</strong>r but only <strong>Hippocampal commissure of fornix</strong> is a <strong>commisural fibre</strong> as it has transverse fibers connecting the two crura of the fornix.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Arcuate fasciculus</strong> is a <strong>short association fiber</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0534","text":"A woman presented with sel​f-inflicted injuries, aud‌itory hallucinations and ‍delusions. MRI revealed a‌ tumour involving the pos‍terior limb of the intern‍al capsule and the struct​ure medial to it. Which s​tructure is likely to be ‍affected?","raw_text":"A woman presented with self-inflicted injuries, auditory hallucinations and delusions. MRI revealed a tumour involving the posterior limb of the internal capsule and the structure medial to it. Which structure is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Globus ​pallidus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Put​amen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thal​amus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caudate​ nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fcadddc9a104b5db5ec4b21d8aa5428x600x592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thalamus</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> tumour-associated psychosis</strong>. The <strong>thalamus</strong> lies <strong>medial</strong> to the <strong>posterior limb of the internal capsule</strong> and is most likely to be affected in this patient.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">The internal capsule separates the <strong>thalamus</strong> and the <strong>caudate nucleus</strong> from the<strong> lentiform nucleus </strong>(globus pallidus + putamen). It is V-shaped in horizontal section and is divided into the anterior limb, genu, posterior limb, retrolenticular part, and sublenticular part. The <strong>anterior limb</strong> is located between the head of the caudate nucleus medially and the lentiform nucleus laterally. The <strong>posterior limb</strong> is located between the thalamus medially and the lentiform nucleus laterally.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6841","text":"A man presented with dysar​thria suggestive of a les‌ion involving the cortico‍nuclear tract. MRI reveal‌ed an ischemic stroke. Wh‍ich part(s) of the intern‍al capsule were most like​ly affected?","raw_text":"A man presented with dysarthria suggestive of a lesion involving the corticonuclear tract. MRI revealed an ischemic stroke. Which part(s) of the internal capsule were most likely affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anteri​or limb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ge​nu","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posteri​or limb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both genu and posterior li​mb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Genu","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Genu</strong></p><p>The <strong>corticonuclear fibres</strong> pass through the <strong>genu </strong>of the <strong>internal capsule </strong>and hence, is most likely to be affected in this patient.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The majority of corticonuclear fibers cross in the median plane before supplying the cranial nerve nuclei. All cranial motor nuclei receive bilateral corticonuclear connections except, <strong>facial </strong>nucleus supplying the lower part of the face and the <strong>hypoglossal</strong> nucleus supplying the genioglossus muscle. Hence, a lesion of these fibers causes <strong>contralateral</strong> clinical manifestations.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Anterior limb</strong> of the internal capsule carries <strong>fronto-pontine</strong> <strong>fibers</strong>. A lesion here produces confusion, impaired attention, agitation, and dysarthria.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Posterior limb</strong> of the internal capsule carries <strong>cortico-spinal</strong> <strong>motor fibers</strong> in the anterior part and a lesion here produces contralateral hemiparesis. The posterior part of the posterior limb of the internal capsule carries <strong>sensory fibers</strong> and lesions here may produce contralateral hemi-sensory deficits.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8062","text":"An 81-year-old man present​ed with ipsilateral paral‌ysis. Examination showed ‍hypertonia, hyperreflexia‌ and the following findin‍g. Which region of white ‍matter do not contain the​ damaged fibres?","raw_text":"An 81-year-old man presented with ipsilateral paralysis. Examination showed hypertonia, hyperreflexia and the following finding. Which region of white matter do not contain the damaged fibres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyramid of medulla oblonga​ta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral white column of th​e spinal cord","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebral peduncle of the m​idbrain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior limb of the inter​nal capsule","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anterior limb of the internal capsule","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13d99094df7e42fda8a2ed4bd450676c.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e405d998d47b40719f40307f3985f8bcx1280x2843.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anterior limb of the internal capsule</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given scenario of ipsilateral paralysis, hypertonia, hyperreflexia and ankle clonus (image) is suggestive of a <strong>corticospinal tract lesion</strong>. The anterior limb of the internal capsule does not contain <strong>corticospinal fibres</strong>. It passes through the <strong>posterior limb of internal capsule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Corticospinal fibres have the following course as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9962","text":"Identify the structures wh​ich consist of commissura‌l fibres:","raw_text":"Identify the structures which consist of commissural fibres:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A, B, C​, and D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A, B an​d C only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A and ​B only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D o​nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A, B and C only","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bde43018279d4ce9a5e9d2e9504922af.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/487be4e09af2475ca0c3e47c3df9d9c5x1280x1108.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81a35ae3aa4c4f79a6044ac6c10e3c7dx1280x1037.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d40373e1a9e142bf94399b090d987464x600x600.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A, B and C only</strong></p><p>The correct answer is <strong>A, B, and C only. </strong></p>\n<p>The structure marked as <strong>'D'</strong> is the<strong> body</strong> <strong>of the fornix </strong>and it mainly contains <strong>association</strong> fibres. The<strong> commissure of the fornix (hippocampal commissure)</strong> contains the <strong>commissural</strong> fibers. </p>\n<p>A- Corpus callosum</p>\n<p>B- Anterior commissure</p>\n<p>C- Posterior commissure</p>\n<p>D- Body of the Fornix</p>\n<p>The locations of the <strong>commissural fibers</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corpus callosum-</strong> Medial surface of the brain</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior commissure-</strong> Beneath the rostrum of the corpus callosum</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior commissure-</strong> In the inferior lamina of the pineal gland</li>\n<li><strong>Habenular commissure-</strong> In the superior lamina of the pineal gland</li>\n<li><strong>Hippocampal commissure-</strong> Between the right fornix and the left fornix </li>\n</ul>\n<p>(Note: Varied information regarding the fibers present in the Fornix. 41st edition Gray's Clinical anatomy mentions Fornix under association fibers, the commissure of the fornix under commissural fibers. Whereas, Snell's Neuroanatomy textbook mentions the Fornix under commissural fibers.) </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6844","text":"The habenular commissure i​s located in the ________‌_.","raw_text":"The habenular commissure is located in the _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Splenium of corpus callosu​m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior lamina of pineal ​gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior lamina of pineal ​gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lamina t​erminalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superior lamina of pineal gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superior lamina of pineal gland</strong></p><p>The <strong>habenular commissure</strong> is located in the <strong>superior lamina of the pineal gland.</strong></p>\n<p>The locations of the <strong>commissural fibers</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corpus callosum-</strong> Medial surface of the brain</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior commissure-</strong> Beneath the rostrum of the corpus callosum</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior commissure-</strong> In the inferior lamina of the pineal gland</li>\n<li><strong>Habenular commissure-</strong> In the superior lamina of the pineal gland</li>\n<li><strong>Hippocampal commissure-</strong> Between the right fornix and the left fornix </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1097","text":"What is the origin of the ​fibers marked by the arro‌w?","raw_text":"What is the origin of the fibers marked by the arrow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Habe​nular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mamilla​ry body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amyg​dala","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hippocampus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d59da4b62e5451bb4fab3ddddb335c0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hippocampus</strong></p><p>The arrow indicates the <strong>fornix</strong>. It arises <strong>from the hippocampus</strong> and <strong>ends in the mamillary body </strong>of the hypothalamus.</p>\n<p><strong>Fornix</strong> is an example of <strong>association fiber</strong>. It is supplied by the <strong>anterior cerebral artery.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2250","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect rega‌rding the areas marked in‍ the given image?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the areas marked in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B is lamina​ terminalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A is a type of association​ fiber","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C is content of interpedun​cular fossa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D is devoid of blood brain​ barrier","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A is a type of association fiber","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad686b64ffdc4c89adbcde69b986cfc8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A is a type of association fiber</strong></p><p>A is <strong>anterior commissure</strong>, which is a type of <strong>commissural fiber</strong>, not association fiber.</p>\n<p>B is <strong>lamina terminalis</strong></p>\n<p>C is the <strong>mamillary body</strong> and is a content of interpeduncular fossa.</p>\n<p>D is the <strong>pineal gland</strong>, which does not have blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6QEEodQ1","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"21_energy_releasing_vitamins","title":"21.Energy Releasing Vitamins","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3054","text":"Which of the following mul​tienzyme complexes cataly‍zing decarboxylation reac‍tions does not require th‌iamine as a coenzyme?","raw_text":"Which of the following multienzyme complexes catalyzing decarboxylation reactions does not require thiamine as a coenzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvate de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"α-ketoglutarate dehydrogen​ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Branched-chain keto-acid d​ehydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isocitrate d​ehydrogenase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Isocitrate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Isocitrate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p><strong>Thiamine pyrophosphate</strong> (TPP) does not serve as the coenzyme for <strong>isocitrate dehydrogenase</strong>. </p>\n<p>Isocitrate dehydrogenase, an enzyme of the citric acid cycle, uses <strong>Mg<sup>2+</sup></strong> or <strong>Mn<sup>2+</sup></strong> ions as a coenzyme. </p>\n<p><strong>Thiamine</strong> pyrophosphate (TPP), from vitamin B1, serves as the coenzyme for three multienzyme complexes that catalyze oxidative decarboxylation reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong> - carbohydrate metabolism</li>\n<li><strong>α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase</strong> - citric acid cycle</li>\n<li><strong>Branched-chain keto-acid dehydrogenase</strong> - metabolism of leucine, isoleucine, valine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most of these enzymes are involved in carbohydrate metabolism, so thiamine is required in excess during a high carbohydrate diet. TPP also serves as the cofactor for <strong>transketolase</strong> in the <strong>HMP shunt.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3256","text":"Which of the following co-​enzyme deficiencies makes‍ a patient prone to lacti‍c acidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following  co-enzyme deficiencies makes a patient prone to lactic acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia​mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ribof​lavin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bio​tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nia​cin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thiamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thiamine</strong></p><p><strong>Thiamine</strong> (Vitamin B1)<strong> deficiency</strong> makes a patient prone to<strong> lactic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Thiamine pyrophosphate</strong> (TPP) serves as a coenzyme for the <strong>pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong> enzyme. In a state of thiamine deficiency, there is an impaired conversion of pyruvate to acetyl CoA. When the patient consumes a<strong> high carbohydate diet</strong>, there is thus an accumulation of pyruvate. Pyruvate then gets converted into lactate by the enzyme <strong>lactate dehydrogenase. </strong>This results in increased plasma concentrations of lactate and pyruvate, leading to a state of <strong>lactic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3258","text":"What is the reason behind ​decreased energy producti‍on in thiamine deficiency‍?","raw_text":"What is the reason behind decreased energy production in thiamine deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is required for transam​ination reactions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is the coenzyme for pyr​uvate dehydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is the coenzyme for tra​nsketolase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is required in the proc​ess of oxidative reductio‍n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is the coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is the coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased energy production</strong> is seen in <strong>thiamine deficiency</strong> because it acts as a <strong>coenzyme</strong> for<strong> pyruvate dehydrogenase </strong>and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.</p>\n<p>Both pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase are required for the production of <strong>NADH</strong>, which is used for ATP production. In <strong>thiamine deficiency</strong>, the reaction catalyzed by pyruvate dehydrogenase cannot go forward. Hence, there is <strong>no formation</strong> of<strong> acetyl CoA</strong>, causing decreased substrate for the citric acid cycle. As a result, energy (ATP) production is grossly reduced and instead, <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1160","text":"A man with chronic alcohol​ism presented with orthop‍nea and pedal edema. On i‍nvestigation, cardiomegal‌y is noted. Which vitamin‍ deficiency can lead to t‍his presentation?","raw_text":"A man with chronic alcoholism presented with orthopnea and pedal edema. On investigation, cardiomegaly is noted. Which vitamin deficiency can lead to this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam​in B1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam​in B5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam​in B6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin B1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin B1</strong></p><p>Cardiac failure (orthopnea and pedal edema) and cardiomegaly in a patient with chronic alcoholism suggest a diagnosis of <strong>wet beriberi.</strong> <strong>Thiamine </strong>(vitamin B1) <strong>deficiency</strong> leads to this condition. </p>\n<p><strong>Chronic alcohol consumption</strong> is associated with thiamine deficiency due to inadequate dietary intake, malabsorption of thiamine from the gastrointestinal tract and impaired utilization of thiamine in the cells. This deficiency presents as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Wet beriberi: </strong>The <strong>cardiovascular system</strong> is affected. Patients present with high output cardiac failure (orthopnea, pedal edema), dyspnea, cardiomegaly, and pulmonary edema.</li>\n<li><strong>Dry beriberi: </strong>Both the <strong>peripheral</strong> and<strong> central nervous</strong> <strong>systems</strong> are affected. Patients present with symmetric motor and sensory neuropathy (pain, paresthesia, loss of reflexes) especially in lower limbs. In severe cases, muscle atrophy may also be seen.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3255","text":"Which of the following enz​yme activity assays can b‍e used for the diagnosis ‍of a patient with suspect‌ed thiamine deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme activity assays can be used for the diagnosis of a patient with suspected thiamine deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyruvate de​hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythrocyte ​transketolase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α-ketoglutarate dehydrogen​ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RBC glutathi​one reductase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythrocyte transketolase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythrocyte transketolase</strong></p><p><strong>Erythrocyte transketolase activity </strong>can be<strong> </strong>measured to diagnose <strong>thiamine deficiency. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Thiamine pyrophosphate </strong>(TPP) is the <strong>cofactor</strong> of erythrocyte transketolase, which is involved in the non-oxidative phase of the <strong>HMP pathway.</strong> In thiamine or vitamin B1 deficiency, the value of erythrocyte transketolase activity will be <strong>decreased.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3257","text":"A middle-aged man with a h​istory of chronic alcohol‍ comsuption presented to ‍the casualty with confusi‌on, ataxia and diplopia. ‍What is the protocol to t‍reat this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with a history of chronic alcohol comsuption presented to the casualty with confusion, ataxia and diplopia. What is the protocol to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiamine injection followe​d by glucose infusion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose infusion followed ​thiamine injection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose inf​usion alone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiamine inj​ection alone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thiamine injection followed by glucose infusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thiamine injection followed by glucose infusion</strong></p><p>Diplopia, ataxia and confusion in a patient with chronic alcohol consumption  is suggestive of <strong>Wernicke's encephalopathy. </strong>The current standard of treatment for patients with symptoms of Wernicke's encephalopathy is to give<strong> thiamine 100 mg intravenously before</strong> administering<strong> glucose-containing</strong> IV<strong> fluids.</strong></p>\n<p>Thiamine deficiency leads to the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Wernicke’s encephalopathy: </strong>It is more commonly seen in alcoholics with chronic thiamine deficiency. It is characterized by global confusion, truncal ataxia, ophthalmoplegia and horizontal nystagmus.</li>\n<li><strong>Korsakoff psychosis:</strong> Symptoms in addition to those of Wernicke's encephalopathy include memory loss and <strong>confabulation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Beriberi: </strong>It can be either -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dry</strong> beriberi: Sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy, muscle weakness</li>\n<li><strong>Wet</strong> beriberi: Tachycardia, cardiomegaly, high-output cardiac failure</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3057","text":"FMN and FAD coenzymes are ​derived from which of the‍ following vitamin?","raw_text":"FMN and FAD coenzymes are derived from which of the following vitamin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nia​cin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ribof​lavin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam​in B5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Riboflavin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Riboflavin</strong></p><p>Coenzymes<strong> flavin mononucleotide</strong> (FMN)<strong> </strong>and <strong>flavin adenine dinucleotide</strong> (FAD) are derived from <strong>riboflavin </strong>(vitamin B2).</p>\n<p>FMN-coenzyme is part of <strong>complex I</strong> (also known as NADH-Q oxidoreductase, NADH dehydrogenase) in <strong>electron transport chain </strong>(ETC).  It also acts as a coenzyme for <strong>L-amino acid oxidase. </strong></p>\n<p>FAD-coenzyme is part of <strong>complex II</strong> in ETC. It acts as a coenzyme for <strong>succinate dehydrogenase </strong>(Kreb's cycle), <strong>acyl CoA dehydrogenase</strong> (fatty acid oxidation), <strong>D-amino acid oxidase</strong> and <strong>xanthine oxidase</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3259","text":"A young lady presented wit​h a tingling sensation in‍ her legs and hands. On e‍xamination, she had fissu‌red tongue, lips, and les‍ions in the angle of the ‍mouth. On investigation, ​she had low RBC glutathio​ne reductase activity. Wh‌ich vitamin is most likel‌y deficient in this patie​nt?","raw_text":"A young lady presented with a tingling sensation in her legs and hands. On examination, she had fissured tongue, lips, and lesions in the angle of the mouth. On investigation, she had low RBC glutathione reductase activity. Which vitamin is most likely deficient in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam​in B12","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nia​cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ribof​lavin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Riboflavin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20d4bfe5b5604b788088095473bf9cf4x720x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Riboflavin</strong></p><p>Tingling sensation of legs and hands, fissured tongue and lips, and lesions in the angle of the mouth combined with low RBC glutathione reductase activity is consistent with <strong>riboflavin </strong>(vitamin B<sub>2</sub>) <strong>deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Deficiency state of riboflavin, a water soluble vitamin, is characterized by the following signs and symptoms:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cheilosis</strong>: Pallor in the angle of mouth and fissuring of lips, which extends radially to skin</li>\n<li><strong>Glossitis:</strong> Tongue becomes smooth, loss of papilla causing<strong> magenta-colored tongue</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Eyes: </strong>Keratitis, conjunctivitis and<strong> corneal vascularisation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other features: </strong>Seborrheic dermatitis, anemia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>nutritional status </strong>of riboflavin can be assessed by<strong> measuring</strong> the activity of the <strong>RBC glutathione reductase,</strong> by FAD added in vitro. This test is specific for riboflavin deficiency.</p>\n<p>The image below shows cheilosis and stomatitis due to riboflavin deficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3264","text":"A man presents with rashes​ around the neck, as show‍n in the image below. Die‍tary history reveals that‌ he has consumed jowar fo‍r a long duration. Which ‍vitamin is most likely de​ficient in this patient?","raw_text":"A man presents with rashes around the neck, as shown in the image below. Dietary history reveals that he has consumed jowar for a long duration. Which vitamin is most likely deficient in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam​in B5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam​in B3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam​in B7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin B3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/30ff0d9d269e43b3a480077d5a0d6024.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin B3</strong></p><p>A patient with high intake of jowar and characteristic <strong>Casal’s necklace</strong> pattern rash is most likely suffering from <strong>pellagra. </strong>It is caused due to a <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> vitamin B3</strong>, known as niacin.</p>\n<p>Pellagra, or niacin deficiency, presents with 3 classical Ds - <strong>dermatitis</strong> (photosensitive), <strong>diarrhea</strong> and<strong> dementia.</strong> Patients often have the <strong>Casal collar</strong> or <strong>Casal necklace</strong>. It is a clinical sign in which there is an erythematous pigmented skin rash in the distribution of a broad collar (dermatomes C3 and C4). If left untreated, pellagra leads to the 4th D of <strong>death</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Jowar </strong>(sorghum) has <strong>high leucine</strong> content. This <strong>inhibits QPRTase</strong>, an enzyme responsible for the synthesis of niacin from amino acid tryptophan. Excessive consumption of <strong>maize-based diets</strong> can also cause pellagra due to their low levels of niacin and tryptophan. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3266","text":"A farmer presents with loo​se stools, depression, an‍d photosensitive dermatit‍is as shown in the image.‌ On examination, a red be‍efy tongue is noted. Whic‍h of the following is not​ associated with this pre​sentation?","raw_text":"A farmer presents with loose stools, depression, and photosensitive dermatitis as shown in the image. On examination, a red beefy tongue is noted. Which of the following is not associated with this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hartnup​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoid​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wernicke's e​ncephalopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridoxine​ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wernicke's encephalopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20f0c68170c2437a8e235efe8000eed6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wernicke&#x27;s encephalopathy</strong></p><p>Loose stools, depression, beefy red tongue and Casal's necklace, as seen in the image, is suggestive of <strong>pellagra.</strong> Pellagra-like symptoms are<strong> not seen</strong> in<strong> Wernicke's encephalopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pellagra is caused by niacin deficiency, either due to<strong> dietary deficiency</strong> or <strong>deficient conversion</strong> of tryptophan to niacin. Hence, pellagra-like symptoms are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hartnup disease:</strong> It is an <strong>a</strong><strong>utosomal recessive</strong> disease<strong> </strong>caused by mutations in the <em>SLC6A19</em> gene leading to defective membrane transport of tryptophan across PCT (kidney) and enterocytes (intestine). This leads to malabsorption, and hence deficiency of tryptophan.</li>\n<li><strong>Carcinoid syndrome:</strong> Due to excess conversion of tryptophan to serotonin, there is a decreased level of tryptophan availavle for niacin synthesis</li>\n<li><strong>Pyridoxine deficiency:</strong> Pyridoxine is required as the co-factor of <strong>kynureninase</strong>, an enzyme in the pathway of niacin synthesis.</li>\n<li><strong>Maize/corn rich diet</strong>: In these grans, niacin is in bound form (niacytin form) and is not absorbed.</li>\n<li><strong>Jowar diet </strong>(Sorghum): The increased leucine content inhibits QPRTase enzyme, an enzyme in the pathway of niacin synthesis. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3724","text":"A 45-year-old woman with h​ypertriglyceridemia is co‍nsidered for niacin thera‍py. She is a known diabet‌ic managed adequately wit‍h sugars well under contr‍ol. Why should niacin be ​used cautiously in this p​atient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman with hypertriglyceridemia is considered for niacin therapy. She is a known diabetic managed adequately with sugars well under control. Why should niacin be used cautiously in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can cause hyperglycemia​.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause scleroderma w​hich makes injecting insu‍lin difficult.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can increase the metabo​lism of oral hypoglyemic ‍drugs.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can cause hypoglycemia.​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It can cause hyperglycemia.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It can cause hyperglycemia.</strong></p><p><strong>Niacin</strong> should be used cautiously in diabetics as it can cause<strong> hyperglycemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Niacin induces<strong> insulin resistance </strong>and also<strong> decreases glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, </strong>which may result in severe hyperglycemia. When niacin is prescribed for patients with known or suspected diabetes, blood glucose levels should be monitored at least once a week.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1327","text":"An alcoholic patient prese​nted with itchy lesions a‍s shown in the image, He ‍also has a red-colored be‌efy tongue. Which of the ‍following histories would‍ you like to additionally​ ask/ elicit in this pati​ent?","raw_text":"An alcoholic patient presented with itchy lesions as shown in the image, He also has a red-colored beefy tongue. Which of the following histories would you like to additionally ask/ elicit in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ask for diarrhea and histo​ry of memory loss","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ask for diarrhea, thiamine​ deficiency features, and‍ detailed dietary history‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"History o​f allergy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ask for diarrhea and conju​nctivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ask for diarrhea and history of memory loss","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/745692850c3b4fa897e04261e4486ac0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ask for diarrhea and history of memory loss</strong></p><p>An alcoholic patient with a <strong>red-colored beefy tongue</strong>, itching and the characteristic <strong>Casal's necklace, </strong>shown in the image, is suggestive of <strong>pellagra.</strong> It is<strong> </strong>caused by <strong>niacin/vitamin B3 deficiency.</strong> Hence, one should ask for a <strong>history </strong>of <strong>diarrhea </strong>and<strong> memory loss</strong> in this case.</p>\n<p>Pellagra presents with <strong>4 Ds: </strong>Diarrhea, Dermatitis, Dementia, and if untreated, Death. Early symptoms of pellagra are loss of appetite, generalized weakness and irritability, abdominal pain, and vomiting.<strong> </strong><strong>Photosensitive dermatitis </strong>is seen over sun-exposed areas. <strong>Diarrhea</strong> occurs due to proctitis or malabsorption. <strong>Bright red glossitis </strong>(beefy tongue) and <strong>Casal’s necklace </strong>(a pigmented, scaly skin rash that forms a ring around the neck) follow. Depressive psychosis and dementia also commonly occur.</p>\n<p><strong>Corn/ maize/ jowar</strong> based diets are the main causes of niacin deficiency. Corn/maize lacks niacin and tryptophan. The excess leucine in jowar and maize disrupts the conversion of tryptophan to niacin. Niacin deficiency is also seen in alcoholism. Hence, additional histories of diarrhea and memory loss will aid in supporting the diagnosis of pellagra (along with dietary history).</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes oral supplementation with 100–200 mg of <strong>nicotinamide</strong> or <strong>nicotinic acid</strong> three times daily for 5 days.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Thiamine deficiency</strong> (vitamin B1) features include CVS manifestations like heart failure and edema (wet beriberi), peripheral neuritis (dry beriberi), and Wernicke encephalopathy (in alcoholics).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Dermatitis </strong>seen in pellagra is due to dietary deficiency of niacin and so allergy history is not relevant.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Conjunctivitis</strong> is not a feature of niacin deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3525","text":"A middle aged man comes fo​r routine follow-up. His ‍lipid profile shows hyper‍cholesterolemia, hypertri‌glyceridemia, elevated LD‍L and VLDL. Which of the ‍following vitamins can be​ used to treat this patie​nt?","raw_text":"A middle aged man comes for routine follow-up. His lipid profile shows hypercholesterolemia, hypertriglyceridemia, elevated LDL and VLDL. Which of the following vitamins can be used to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ribof​lavin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pantothe​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nicotin​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrid​oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nicotinic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nicotinic acid</strong></p><p>A man with hypercholesterolemia, hypertriglyceridemia, elevated LDL and VLDL should be treated with a <strong>lipid-lowering agent.</strong> Thus, the vitamin that can be used to treat this patient is <strong>nicotinic acid</strong> (niacin or vitamin B3).</p>\n<p>Niacin causes a <strong>reduction</strong> in<strong> LDL, VLDL</strong> and<strong> triglycerides</strong> along with <strong>increasing HDL</strong>. It is the only lipid-lowering agent that <strong>lowers lipoprotein (a)</strong> levels significantly. The main adverse effect of niacin is cutaneous <strong>flushing </strong>and<strong> pruritus.</strong> It can be controlled by <strong>pre-treatment </strong>with<strong> aspirin.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3267","text":"A patient is prescribed ni​acin for treating his hyp‍erlipidemia. All of the f‍ollowing statements about‌ this drug are true excep‍t: (TAG - triacylglycerol‍)","raw_text":"<p>A patient is prescribed niacin for treating his hyperlipidemia. All of the following statements about this drug are true except:</p>\n<p>(TAG - triacylglycerol)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It inhibits lipolysis in a​dipose tissue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It causes a reduction in t​he liver TAG synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It reduces the level of HD​L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It reduces the level of LD​L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It reduces the level of HDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It reduces the level of HDL</strong></p><p>Niacin <strong>increases</strong> the level of <strong>HDL.</strong></p>\n<p>Niacin (vitamin B3) is known to <strong>inhibit lipolysis</strong> within the adipose tissue. Hence, the circulating <strong>free fatty acids</strong> (FFAs) are <strong>reduced</strong>. The liver normally uses these circulating FFAs as a precursor in the synthesis of <strong>triacylglycerol </strong>(TAG)<strong>. </strong>As a result, niacin causes a reduction in liver TAG synthesis. TAGs are in turn required for very low density lipoprotein (VLDL) and low density lipoprotein (LDL) synthesis. Therefore both plasma VLDL and LDL values are reduced. Overall,<strong> </strong>niacin <strong>decreases LDL, VLDL</strong> and <strong>TAG levels</strong>,<strong> </strong>and<strong> increases high-density lipoprotein</strong> (HDL) <strong>levels</strong>. Thus it is particularly useful in the treatment of <strong>type IIb hyperlipoproteinemia</strong>, in which both VLDL and LDL are elevated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3084","text":"Which of the following vit​amins constitutes a part ‍of the coenzyme A?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins constitutes a part of the coenzyme A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribof​lavin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pantothe​nic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrid​oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pantothenic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pantothenic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Pantothenic acid</strong> constitutes a part of the <strong>coenzyme A</strong>.</p>\n<p>The primary function of coenzyme A (pantothenic acid) is to transfer <strong>acyl groups. </strong>Pantothenic acid as coenzyme A is involved in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatty acid oxidation</li>\n<li>TCA cycle (as succinyl Co-A and acetyl Co-A)</li>\n<li>Cholesterol synthesis (as HMG Co-A)</li>\n<li>Acetylation reactions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6942","text":"Burning feet syndrome is c​aused by a particular vit‍amin deficiency. Which of‍ the following amino acid‌s is contained in this vi‍tamin?","raw_text":"Burning feet syndrome is caused by a particular vitamin deficiency. Which of the following amino acids is contained in this vitamin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenyl​alanine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argi​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-a​lanine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trypt​ophan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Beta-alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Beta-alanine</strong></p><p>The vitamin that is deficient in <strong>burning feet syndrome</strong> is <strong>vitamin B5</strong> (pantothenic acid) which contains the amino acid <strong>beta-alanine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pantothenic acid deficiency</strong> is rarely seen as the vitamin is abundantly found in food (egg yolks, whole frains, vegetables). <strong>Burning feet syndrome</strong> is classically seen in pantothenic acid deficiency. The other symptoms of pantothenic deficiency are <strong>non-specific</strong> and include GI disturbances, paraesthesia, muscle cramps and ataxia. </p>\n<p>Beta-alanine is an amino acid seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5)</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Coenzyme A</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carnosine</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anserine</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acyl carrier protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3082","text":"Which of the following vit​amins serves as a cofacto‍r in carboxylation reacti‍ons?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins serves as a cofactor in carboxylation reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribof​lavin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrid​oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bio​tin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Biotin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Biotin</strong></p><p><strong>Biotin </strong>serves as an important<strong> cofactor</strong> in <strong>carboxylation reactions</strong></p>\n<p>Biotin (vitamin B7) functions as a carrier for the <strong>activated carbon dioxide </strong>molecule in carboxylation reactions. The enzymes using biotin as a cofactor are <strong>acetyl CoA carboxylase</strong>, pyruvate carboxylase, propionyl CoA carboxylase and methyl crotonyl CoA carboxylase.</p>\n<p>Malic enzyme and carbomyl phosphate synthetase I and II do not use biotin as a coenzyme.<strong> Gamma carboxylation</strong> by vitamin K is also <strong>biotin-independent. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3275","text":"A 20-year-old bodybuilder ​presented with fatigue on‍ moderate exercise. He ha‍d recently started eating‌ raw eggs for protein. Wh‍ich of the following enzy‍mes is likely deficient i​n him?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old bodybuilder presented with fatigue on moderate exercise. He had recently started eating raw eggs for protein. Which of the following enzymes is likely deficient in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose-6-​phosphatase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycogen ph​osphorylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate c​arboxylase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphoenolpyruvate carbox​ykinase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyruvate carboxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyruvate carboxylase</strong></p><p>The patient developed <strong>pyruvate carboxylase</strong> enzyme <strong>deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Individuals who eat an excess of <strong>uncooked</strong> <strong>egg-white</strong> develop <strong>biotin deficiency</strong> and as a result carboxylase enzyme deficiency. This is because a protein called <strong>avidin</strong> present in the egg-white<strong> binds </strong>to<strong> biotin</strong> in the diet and makes biotin unavailable for intestinal absorption. Biotin deficiency can cause <strong>dermatitis</strong>, mental status changes (depression, hallucinations), paraesthesia and alopecia.</p>\n<p>Biotin serves as a co-enzyme for the synthesis of several carboxylase enzymes such as acetyl-CoA carboxylase, <strong>pyruvate carboxylase</strong>, propionyl CoA carboxylase and methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase. Thus these enzymes are deficient in biotin deficiency. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0580","text":"Vitamin B12 is found maxim​um in:","raw_text":"Vitamin B12 is found maximum in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Animal ​products","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Green leafy​ vegetables","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Roots an​d tubers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vegetar​ian diet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Animal products","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Animal products</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin B12</strong> is found maximum in<strong> animal products.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 or <strong>cobalamin</strong> is synthesized <strong>only</strong> by <strong>microorganisms</strong>. Thus, the only source of cobalamin for humans is animal products. It is<strong> not found</strong> in <strong>plant</strong> sources. Therefore, individuals who are <strong>strict vegetarians</strong> are usually at the <strong>risk</strong> of developing vitamin B12 <strong>deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0582","text":"Which vitamin, when infuse​d in supraphysiological d‍oses, causes macular edem‍a and macular cysts?","raw_text":"Which vitamin, when infused in supraphysiological doses, causes macular edema and macular cysts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita​min D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam​in B3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitamin B3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitamin B3</strong></p><p><strong>Macular cysts</strong> and <strong>edema</strong> may develop in patients due to the infusion of <strong>supraphysiological doses</strong> of <strong>vitamin B3 </strong>(niacin toxicity).</p>\n<p>The<strong> toxic effects</strong> of niacin include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flushing</strong> associated with skin dryness, itching, paresthesia, and headache is seen. These effects may be seen with even a low dose of 30mg/day. <strong>Pre-medication</strong> with<strong> aspirin alleviates</strong> these symptoms as these symptoms are mediated by <strong>prostaglandins.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Macular edema </strong>and <strong>macular cysts</strong></li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.</li>\n<li>Hepatic toxicity presents as jaundice with elevated AST and ALT levels.</li>\n<li>Fulminant hepatitis requiring liver transplantation.</li>\n<li>Glucose intolerance</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"AQ1tZvhO","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"21_non_communicable_diseases___cardiovascular_diseases_and_diabetes","title":"21.Non Communicable Diseases   Cardiovascular Diseases And Diabetes","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC4837","text":"The WHO has formulated a G‌lobal Action Plan for the‌ prevention and control o‍f non-communicable diseas‌es. Which of the followin​g is not a voluntary glob​al target under this init‌iative?","raw_text":"The WHO has formulated a Global Action Plan for the prevention and control of non-communicable diseases. Which of the following is not a voluntary global target under this initiative?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10% relative reduction in ‌harmful use of alcohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30% relative reduction in ‌mean population intake of‌ sodium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10% relative reduction in ‌prevalence of insufficien‌t physical activity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10% relative reduction in ‌use of trans-fat for cook‌ing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10% relative reduction in use of trans-fat for cooking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ca38c76619e411c83c812ec2a80e4aex691x481.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10% relative reduction in use of trans-fat for cooking</strong></p><p>A 10 percent relative reduction in the use of trans-fat for cooking is not a voluntary global target under the WHO Global Action Plan for prevention and control of non-communicable diseases.</p>\n<p>There are 9 targets under this plan which are to be achieved by 2025. These are shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8115","text":"Project MONICA is related ‌to:","raw_text":"Project MONICA is related to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiovascu‌lar diseases","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervica‌l Cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast‌ Cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Road Traffi‌c Accidents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiovascular diseases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiovascular diseases</strong></p><p><strong>Project MONICA</strong> is related to <strong>cardiovascular diseases</strong>.</p>\n<p>It stands for <strong>multinational monitoring</strong> of <strong>trends</strong> and <strong>determinants</strong> in cardiovascular diseases. It was done to study the reasons for the changes in the trends of cardiovascular diseases in different countries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8120","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g the relationship betwee‍n smoking and coronary he‌art disease (CHD)?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the relationship between smoking and coronary heart disease (CHD)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoking can cause sudden d‌eath from CHD.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Filters in cigarettes are ‌protective against the de‌velopment of CHD.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Risk of developing CHD 10-‌20 years after the cessat‌ion of smoking is equal i‍n smokers and non smokers‌.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Degree of risk of developi‌ng CHD is directly relate‌d to the number of cigare‍ttes smoked per day.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Filters in cigarettes are protective against the development of CHD.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Filters in cigarettes are protective against the development of CHD.</strong></p><p>Filters are probably not protective against the development of coronary heart disease in smokers.</p>\n<p><strong>Smoking</strong> is a <strong>modifiable risk factor</strong> for coronary heart disease (<strong>CHD</strong>). The degree of risk is <strong>directly related</strong> to the <strong>number of cigarettes</strong> smoked per day. Several mechanisms have been suggested for this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbon monoxide induced atherogenesis</li>\n<li>Nicotine stimulation of adrenergic drive</li>\n<li>Lipid metabolism with fall in levels of HDL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cigarettes also<strong> increase </strong>the<strong> risk of sudden death</strong> in patients with CHD who are<strong> below 50 years</strong> of age. The<strong> risk</strong> of death from CHD <strong>decreases</strong> with the<strong> cessation of smoking</strong>. The risk declines quite substantially within one year of stopping smoking and more gradually thereafter until, after 10- 20 years, it is the same as that of non-smokers.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for coronary heart disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modifiable</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>High blood pressure</li>\n<li>Elevated serum cholesterol</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Sedentary habits</li>\n<li>Stress</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Non-modifiable</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Sex</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Genetic factors</li>\n<li>Personality </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8121","text":"A chronic smoker was found‌ to have a deranged lipid‌ profile during his annua‍l health checkup. Which o‌f the following variables​ tested is most directly ​associated with the risk ‌of developing coronary he​art disease?","raw_text":"A chronic smoker was found to have a deranged lipid profile during his annual health checkup. Which of the following variables tested is most directly associated with the risk of developing coronary heart disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HDL ‌level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"VLDL ‌level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LDL ‌level","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IDL ‌level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. LDL level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. LDL level</strong></p><p>The plasma <strong>LDL</strong> level is<strong> most directly associated</strong> with the risk of developing coronary heart disease (<strong>CHD</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>HDL</strong> is <strong>protective</strong> against the development of CHD. Its levels should ideally be more than 40 mg/dl.</p>\n<p>Recent evidence shows that levels of <strong>Apolipoprotein-A-1</strong> and <strong>Apolipoprotein-B</strong> are <strong>better predictors</strong> of CHD than HDL or LDL.</p>\n<p><strong>VLDL</strong> is more strongly associated with the risk of developing <strong>peripheral vascular disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4841","text":"A 45-year-old diabetic mal‌e is concerned about his ‌risk of developing corona‍ry heart disease. What ch‌olesterol/HDL ratio would​ you aim to achieve in th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old diabetic male is concerned about his risk of developing coronary heart disease. What cholesterol/HDL ratio would you aim to achieve in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholesterol/HDL ratio&amp;gt; ‌2.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholesterol/HDL ratio&amp;lt; ‌2.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholesterol/HDL ratio&amp;gt; ‌3.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholesterol/HDL ratio&amp;lt; ‌3.5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cholesterol/HDL ratio< 3.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cholesterol/HDL ratio&lt; 3.5</strong></p><p>A <strong>cholesterol/HDL ratio &lt;3.5</strong> is recommended for the<strong> prevention</strong> of coronary heart disease (<strong>CHD</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8122","text":"While conducting a screeni‌ng camp for coronary hear‌t disease, you encounter ‍the following people. Who‌ among them is at the hig​hest risk for developing ​this disease?","raw_text":"While conducting a screening camp for coronary heart disease, you encounter the following people. Who among them is at the highest risk for developing this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A person who is competitiv‌e and ambitious","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A person who is calm and r‌elaxed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A person who tends to supp‌ress their emotions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"An introverted person with‌ negative affectivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A person who is competitive and ambitious","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A person who is competitive and ambitious</strong></p><p>A person who is <strong>competitive</strong> and <strong>ambitious</strong> is said to have a<strong> type A</strong> <strong>personality</strong>. Such people are more prone to <strong>develop</strong> <strong>coronary heart disease</strong> (CHD). Personality type is a <strong>non-modifiable risk factor</strong> for CHD.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for coronary heart disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modifiable</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>High blood pressure</li>\n<li>Elevated serum cholesterol</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Sedentary habits</li>\n<li>Stress</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Non-modifiable</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Sex</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Genetic factors</li>\n<li>Personality </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: A calm and relaxed person is said to have a type B personality.</p>\n<p>Option C: A person who tends to suppress their emotions, especially negative emotions, is said to have a type C personality.</p>\n<p>Option D: A person with negative affectivity and social inhibition is said to have a type D personality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8123","text":"A 45-year-old obese woman ‌enquires about ways she c‌an reduce her risk of cor‍onary artery disease. Whi‌ch of the following dieta​ry modifications would yo​u suggest?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old obese woman enquires about ways she can reduce her risk of coronary artery disease. Which of the following dietary modifications would you suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction of salt intake t‌o less than 10g per day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consumption of saturated f‌ats limited to less than ‌10% of the total energy i‍ntake","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reduction of total fat int‌ake to less than 5% of to‌tal energy intake","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dietary cholesterol increa‌sed to above 100 mg/1000 ‌kcal energy intake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Consumption of saturated fats limited to less than 10% of the total energy intake","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Consumption of saturated fats limited to less than 10% of the total energy intake</strong></p><p>Limiting the consumption of<strong> saturated fats</strong> to <strong>less than</strong> <strong>10%</strong> of total energy intake is recommended for the <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>coronary artery disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The WHO'S Expert Committee suggested certain <strong>dietary modifications</strong> for the prevention of coronary artery disease. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduction of the total<strong> fat intake</strong> to <strong>20–30%</strong> of <strong>total energy</strong> intake</li>\n<li>Consumption of saturated fats must be limited to less than 10% of total energy intake</li>\n<li>A reduction of <strong>dietary cholesterol</strong> to <strong>below 100mg</strong> per 1000 kcal per day</li>\n<li>Increase in the complex carbohydrate consumption (i.e vegetables, fruits, whole grains and legumes)</li>\n<li>Avoidance of alcohol consumption and reduction of <strong>salt intake</strong> to <strong>5g daily</strong> or less</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8124","text":"Which of the following stu‌dies is not related to co‌ronary heart disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following studies is not related to coronary heart disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stanford 3 community study‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Framingh‌am study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CATIE‌ trial","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MR‌FIT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CATIE trial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CATIE trial</strong></p><p>The<strong> CATIE trial</strong> is related to the study of <strong>antipsychotics </strong>and their effectiveness.</p>\n<p>The <strong>trials</strong> related to<strong> coronary heart disease</strong> and their subject of study include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px; width: 895px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.46875px;\">Name of Study</td>\n<td style=\"width: 608.53125px;\">Factors affecting CHD Studied</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.46875px;\">Oslo diet/Smoking intervention study</td>\n<td style=\"width: 608.53125px;\">Effect of lowering serum lipids and smoking cessation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.46875px;\">MRFIT trial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 608.53125px;\">Effect of smoking cessation, blood pressure control and diet alteration in reduction of serum cholesterol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.46875px;\">The North Karelia project</td>\n<td style=\"width: 608.53125px;\">\n<p>1. Effect of reduction of risk factors like smoking, blood pressure and serum cholesterol.</p>\n<p>2. Promotion of early diagnosis, treatment and rehabilitation of patients</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.46875px;\">The Stanford-Three-Community trial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 608.53125px;\">Effects of community health education</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 271.46875px;\">Lipid Research Clinics study</td>\n<td style=\"width: 608.53125px;\">Effect of reducing serum cholesterol in type II hyperlipidemics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8125","text":"A 50-year-old man, under t‌reatment for hypertension‌ for the past two months,‍ is found to have a blood‌ pressure of 144/96 mmHg.​ How will you grade it ac​cording to the European s‌ociety of cardiology guid​elines-2018?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man, under treatment for hypertension for the past two months, is found to have a blood pressure of 144/96 mmHg. How will you grade it according to the European society of cardiology guidelines-2018?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nor‌mal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High ‌Normal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grade 1 hy‌pertension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Grade 2 hy‌pertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Grade 1 hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Grade 1 hypertension</strong></p><p>According to the European society of cardiology guidelines-2018, a patient having a <strong>systolic BP≥140</strong> and a <strong>diastolic BP≥90</strong> mmHg is classified as having <strong>grade 1 hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8127","text":"A doctor records the BP of‌ a patient as 142/90mmHg.‌ One hour later, another ‍doctor records the BP of ‌the same patient as 160/9​8 mmHg. Both the readings​ are measured with the sa‌me apparatus under the sa​me emotional and physical‍ state of the patient. Wh​at type of error is seen ​here?","raw_text":"A doctor records the BP of a patient as 142/90mmHg. One hour later, another doctor records the BP of the same patient as 160/98 mmHg. Both the readings are measured with the same apparatus under the same emotional and physical state of the patient. What type of error is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subjec‌t error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Observe‌r error","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Instrume‌nt error","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recordi‌ng error","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Observer error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Observer error</strong></p><p>Such errors can arise due to <strong>differences</strong> in <strong>hearing acuity</strong> and interpretation of <strong>Korotkoff sounds</strong>.</p>\n<p>The types of errors that can occur during the measurement of blood pressure include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observer </strong>errors - due to difference in hearing acuity and interpretation of Korotkoff sounds</li>\n<li><strong>Instrumental </strong>errors - due to a leaking valve or cuffs that do not encircle the arm completely</li>\n<li><strong>Subject </strong>errors - due to anxiety, fear, etc. of the patient</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8128","text":"What does number 2 in the ‌given diagram related to ‌hypertension denote?","raw_text":"What does number 2 in the given diagram related to hypertension denote?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normotensive‌ individuals","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Undiagnosed hypertensive s‌ubjects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diagnosed hypertensive sub‌jects not on treatment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whole c‌ommunity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normotensive individuals","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa2aa5ee4fcd41d397113675efd9a9f3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normotensive individuals</strong></p><p>The above diagram depicts the \"<strong>rule of halves</strong>\",<strong> </strong>which shows the <strong>distribution of hypertension</strong> in the community. The number<strong> 2</strong> in this diagram denotes<strong> normotensive subjects</strong>.</p>\n<p>The rule of halves is a way of depicting the<strong> iceberg phenomenon</strong> seen in hypertension. It depicts that only about half of the hypertensive subjects in the general population of most developed countries are aware of the condition and only half of those aware are being treated and only half of those being treated are considered to be adequately treated. </p>\n<p>The circles marked by numbers in the given diagram correspond to the following parts of the population:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Whole community</li>\n<li>Normotensive subjects</li>\n<li>Hypertensive subjects</li>\n<li>Undiagnosed hypertension</li>\n<li>Diagnosed hypertension</li>\n<li>Diagnosed but untreated</li>\n<li>Diagnosed and treated</li>\n<li>Inadequately treated</li>\n<li>Adequately treated</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8133","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ tracking of blood pressu‍re?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding tracking of blood pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BP increases with age in a‌ll individuals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BP falls steadily in the e‌lderly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertensive children beco‌me hypotensive as they gr‌ow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BP of hypotensive children‌ tends to remain low in a‌dult life","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. BP of hypotensive children tends to remain low in adult life","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. BP of hypotensive children tends to remain low in adult life</strong></p><p>If the blood pressure of individuals is followed up over years from early childhood to adult life,<strong> low blood pressures </strong>tend to <strong>remain low </strong>and<strong> high blood pressures </strong>become<strong> higher</strong> as the individuals grow older. This phenomenon of <strong>persistence </strong>of the<strong> rank order </strong>of blood pressure is described as <strong>tracking.</strong></p>\n<p>This is helpful in<strong> identifying children</strong> and adolescents who are <strong>at risk</strong> of developing <strong>hypertension</strong> in the future.</p>\n<p>Although blood pressure is known to increase with age in both sexes, <strong>some communities</strong> have been identified whose <strong>mean blood pressure</strong> does <strong>not rise</strong> with age. This is because their salt intake and calorie consumption are at a subsistence level.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8136","text":"While formulating a diet p‌lan for a hypertensive pa‌tient, you advise salt re‍striction. How much reduc‌tion in systolic blood pr​essure is expected by thi​s intervention?","raw_text":"While formulating a diet plan for a  hypertensive patient, you advise salt restriction. How much reduction in systolic blood pressure is expected by this intervention?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8-14 m‌m of Hg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-8 mm‌ of Hg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4-9 mm‌ of Hg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5-20 m‌m of Hg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2-8 mm of Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2-8 mm of Hg</strong></p><p><strong>Salt restriction</strong> or decreasing dietary sodium intake <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>systolic blood pressure</strong> by<strong> 2-8 mmHg</strong> approximately.</p>\n<p><strong>Lifestyle modifications </strong>to manage hypertension:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 502px; height: 678px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.78125px;\">\n<p><strong>Recommendation</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.234375px;\">\n<p><strong>Reduction in systolic blood pressure(approximate range)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.78125px;\">\n<p>Weight reduction (Target BMI:18.5-24.9)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.234375px;\">\n<p> 5–20 mm Hg /10 kg weight loss</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.78125px;\">\n<p>Consume diet rich in fruits, vegetables and low fat dairy products with decreased saturated fats and total fat (DASH diet)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.234375px;\">\n<p>8–14 mm Hg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.78125px;\">\n<p>Reducing intake of dietary Sodium to no more than 100mEq/day(&lt; 6 g of sodium chloride)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.234375px;\">\n<p>2–8 mm Hg</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.78125px;\">\n<p>Physical activity like brisk walk (at least 30 min per day), most days of week.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.234375px;\">\n<p>4–9 mm Hg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.78125px;\">\n<p>Limit consumption of alcohol to no more than two drinks per day</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 257.234375px;\">\n<p>2–4 mm Hg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: DASH stands for dietary approaches to stop hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8134","text":"Which of the following int‌erventions will cause the‌ maximum decrease in syst‍olic blood pressure in a ‌hypertensive patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following interventions will cause the maximum decrease in systolic blood pressure in a hypertensive patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Restricting intake of anim‌al products","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Taking a brisk walk for at‌ least 30 minutes a day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adopting the DASH eating p‌lan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Restricting sodium intake ‌to less than 6g per day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adopting the DASH eating plan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adopting the DASH eating plan</strong></p><p>Among the above options, adopting the dietary approaches to stop hypertension (<strong>DASH</strong>) eating plan causes<strong> maximum decrease</strong> in <strong>systolic BP</strong>. It<strong> </strong>decreases the systolic BP by<strong> 8-14 mm Hg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lifestyle modifications </strong>to manage hypertension:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Recommendation</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Reduction in systolic blood pressure(approximate range)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Weight reduction (Target BMI:18.5-24.9)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p> 5–20 mm Hg /10 kg weight loss</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Consume diet rich in fruits, vegetables and low fat dairy products with decreased saturated fats and total fat (DASH diet)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>8–14 mm Hg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Reducing intake of dietary sodium to no more than 100mEq/day(&lt; 6 g of sodium chloride)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>2–8 mm Hg</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Physical activity like brisk walk (at least 30 min per day), most days of week.</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>4–9 mm Hg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Limit consumption of alcohol to no more than two drinks per day</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>2–4 mm Hg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8139","text":"Which of the following is ‌a minor criterion for the‌ diagnosis of rheumatic f‍ever in a low risk popula‌tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a minor criterion for the diagnosis of rheumatic fever in a low risk population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Card‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema ‌marginatum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcutaneo‌us nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyart‌hralgia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polyarthralgia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polyarthralgia</strong></p><p><strong>Polyarthralgia</strong> is a <strong>minor criterion</strong> for the diagnosis of rheumatic fever in a <strong>low risk population</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8142","text":"A child having strep throa‌t is being prescribed ben‌zathine penicillin G. Whi‍ch level of prevention of‌ rheumatic fever does thi​s intervention fall under​?","raw_text":"A child having strep throat is being prescribed benzathine penicillin G. Which level of prevention of rheumatic fever does this intervention fall under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary p‌revention","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primordial‌ prevention","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary ‌prevention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiary ‌prevention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary prevention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary prevention</strong></p><p>The given scenario is an example of <strong>primary</strong> <strong>mode of prevention</strong> of rheumatic fever. This is because the patient only has strep throat and rheumatic fever has not developed yet.</p>\n<p>In case benzathine penicillin G is given to a patient<strong> </strong>with rheumatic fever to prevent further complications, then it becomes secondary level of prevention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8146","text":"A 16-year-old boy with rhe‌umatic fever developed ca‌rditis which subsequently‍ healed in a couple of we‌eks. How long will you ad​minister benzathine penic​illin G for secondary pro‌phylaxis to him?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy with rheumatic fever developed carditis which subsequently healed in a couple of weeks. How long will you administer benzathine penicillin G for secondary prophylaxis to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Till 21 ye‌ars of age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Till 24 ye‌ars of age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Till 26 ye‌ars of age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Life‌long","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Till 26 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Till 26 years of age</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, benzathine penicillin G for <strong>secondary prophylaxis</strong> should be given for <strong>10 years</strong> <strong>from</strong> the <strong>last attack</strong> i.e, till<strong> 26 years</strong> <strong>of age</strong>. The given case is of rheumatic fever with carditis but with no residual valvular disease, for which secondary prophylaxis is given for 10 years from the last attack or till 21 years of age, whichever is longer. </p>\n<p>Recommendations for the<strong> duration of secondary prophylaxis </strong>with penicillin:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Category of the patient</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><strong>Duration of prophylaxis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Rheumatic fever without carditis</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>For 5 years after the last attack or until 21 years of age ( whichever is longer)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Rheumatic fever with carditis but with no residual valvular disease</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>For 10 years after the last attack or until 21 yrs of age (whichever is longer)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Rheumatic fever with persistent valvular disease evident clinically or on echo</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>For 10 years after the last attack or until 40 years of age, or sometimes lifelong prophylaxis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8174","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a protective factor f‌or diabetes mellitus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a protective factor for diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intake ‌of PUFAs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intake of non-starch polys‌accharides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exclusive b‌reastfeeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intake of sa‌turated fats","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intake of saturated fats","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intake of saturated fats</strong></p><p>A<strong> higher</strong> intake of<strong> saturated fats </strong>is associated with an increased risk of <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dietary <strong>factors</strong> that are <strong>protective</strong> for diabetes mellitus are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Higher intake of<strong> unsaturated fatty acids</strong></li>\n<li>A minimum daily intake of 20g of <strong>dietary fibre</strong></li>\n<li>Exclusive <strong>breastfeeding</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4848","text":"A patient's fasting plasma‌ glucose is 122 mg/dl and‌ postprandial plasma gluc‍ose is 138 mg/dl. Accordi‌ng to the WHO diagnostic ​criteria for diabetes, wh​ich category does this pa‌tient fall under?","raw_text":"A patient's fasting plasma glucose is 122 mg/dl and postprandial plasma glucose is 138 mg/dl. According to the WHO diagnostic criteria for diabetes, which category does this patient fall under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isolated post prandial hyp‌erglycemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Impaired glucose tolerance‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impaired fas‌ting glucose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Overt d‌iabetes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Impaired fasting glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Impaired fasting glucose</strong></p><p>Fasting blood glucose of 122 mg/dl with a postprandial value of 138 mg/dl indicates the patient falls under the criteria for <strong>impaired fasting glucose.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> WHO</strong> <strong>diagnostic criteria</strong> for diabetes and intermediate hyperglycemia is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 204px;\" width=\"408\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.333px;\"><strong>Fasting plasma glucose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\"><strong>Post prandial plasma glucose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\"><strong>Diagnosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.333px;\">≥ 126 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\">&gt; 200 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\">Diabetes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.333px;\">&lt; 126 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\">140-200 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\">Impaired glucose tolerance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.333px;\">110-125 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\">&lt; 140 mg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.667px;\">Impaired fasting glucose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8181","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are incorrect reg‌arding the benefits of va‍rious interventions in di‌abetes? Lifestyle interve​ntions can reduce the inc​idence of diabetes by 50%‌ Risk of microvascular di​sease reduces by 30% per ‍1% drop in HbA1c Use of A​CE inhibitors reduces the​ risk of nephropathy by 1​0% Risk of macrovascular ‌disease reduces by 35% pe‍r 10 mmHg drop in blood p​ressure Annual eye examin‌ation reduces the inciden​ce of serious visual loss‌ by 40%","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are incorrect regarding the benefits of various interventions in diabetes?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lifestyle interventions can reduce the incidence of diabetes by 50%</li>\n<li>Risk of microvascular disease reduces by 30% per 1% drop in HbA1c </li>\n<li>Use of ACE inhibitors reduces the risk of nephropathy by 10%</li>\n<li>Risk of macrovascular disease reduces by 35% per 10 mmHg drop in blood pressure</li>\n<li>Annual eye examination reduces the incidence of serious visual loss by 40%</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 &amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>The use of <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> in diabetics<strong> reduces</strong> the risk of <strong>nephropathy</strong> by <strong>42%</strong> and cardiovascular risk by 22%. <strong>Annual eye examination</strong> in diabetics <strong>reduces</strong> the incidence of serious <strong>visual loss</strong> by <strong>60-70%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Interventions in diabetes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Glycaemic control in diabetics with HbA1C &gt; 9% reduces microvascular disease risk by 30% per 1% drop in HbA1c.</li>\n<li>Blood pressure control in patients with BP &gt; 130/80 mmHg reduces microvascular and macrovascular disease risk by 35% per 10 mmHg drop in blood pressure.</li>\n<li>Lifestyle modification reduces the incidence of diabetes by 35-58%.</li>\n<li>Metformin administered as a preventive therapy reduces the incidence of diabetes by 25-31%.</li>\n<li>Proper foot care reduces the risk of serious foot disease by 50-60%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF9592","text":"The community health offic‌er of an area is instruct‌ed to assess the populati‍on for the risk factors o‌f Non-alcoholic Fatty Liv​er Disease under NP-NCD. ​Which of the following wi‌ll not be a risk factor?","raw_text":"The community health officer of an area is instructed to assess the population for the risk factors of Non-alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease under NP-NCD. Which of the following will not be a risk factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pedal‌ edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdomina‌l obesity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"History ‌of cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low blood‌ pressure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Low blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Low blood pressure</strong></p><p>Low blood pressure is not a risk factor for NAFLD according to the NP-NCD.</p>\n<p>The National Programme for Prevention &amp; Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (<strong>NP-NCD</strong>), which was earlier NPCDCS, has <strong>integrated guidelines</strong> for the management of <strong>Non-alcoholic Fatty Liver disease (NAFLD)</strong> into its framework. Through a community-based assessment checklist, the <strong>community health officer</strong> along with <strong>ASHA workers</strong> will assess for the presence of the following <strong>risk factors</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal obesity (waist circumference of &gt;90cm in men and &gt;80cm in women)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Personal or family history of diabetes, hypertension, cancers, heart diseases, liver diseases or gallstones</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity (BMI &gt;23 kg/m<sup>2</sup>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of pedal edema</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Level of healthcare</td>\n<td>Provisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Community level</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ASHA workers assess for abdominal obesity, relevant personal or family history.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Refer to the sub-health centre.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sub-Health Centre</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Validate ASHA workers’ referrals.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Further assess for <strong>pedal edema</strong>, <strong>BMI</strong>, and screen for <strong>other NCDs</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary Health Centre</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Assessment of SHC referrals for <strong>signs of chronic liver disease</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Basic treatment</strong> for DM/HTN.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Community Health Centre</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rule out other causes of liver disease, and perform <strong>CBC</strong> and<strong> LFT</strong>. Calculate <strong>FIB4</strong> or <strong>NFS score</strong>. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If l<strong>ow risk</strong> of fibrosis - Refer back to the PHC for <strong>conservative management</strong>, weight reduction etc. Reassess in 3 years.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If <strong>indeterminate</strong>, or high risk of fibrosis - Refer to a <strong>higher centre</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>District level</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perform <strong>Fibroscan</strong> for <strong>indeterminate risk</strong> of fibrosis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In case of <strong>low risk</strong> of fibrosis - Refer back to the PHC for <strong>conservative management</strong>, weight reduction, etc. Reassess in 3 years.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In case of <strong>high risk</strong> of fibrosis - Management of fibrosis, screening for <strong>HCC</strong> and <strong>portal hypertension</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Refer complicated cases to tertiary level.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tertiary level</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management of <strong>complicated cases.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/><br/><br/><br/><br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MjI0otne","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"21_connective_tissue_disorders","title":"21.Connective Tissue Disorders","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC2768","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common site invo​lved in discoid lupus ery‌thematosus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site involved in discoid lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fin‍gers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scalp a‍nd face","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tr‍unk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Side o‍f neck","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scalp and face","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f0cd07feddb43fb97445b845af3ab79x1280x2022.0028208744711.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scalp and face</strong></p><p><strong>Discoid lupus erythematosus</strong> is a benign inflammatory disorder of the skin, <strong>most frequently</strong> involving the<strong> face</strong> and<strong> scalp. </strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by well‐defined red, scaly patches of variable size, which heal with atrophy, scarring, and pigmentary changes. The <strong>circumscribed</strong> or<strong> discoid</strong> form is the most frequent type of disease and occurs particularly on the <strong>cheeks</strong>, bridge of the <strong>nose</strong>, <strong>ears, side of the neck, and scalp</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a localized discoid lupus erythematosus of the scalp with follicular plugging.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5276","text":"A dermoscopy examination i‍n a patient who presented​ with alopecia revealed t‌he following finding. Ide‌ntify the condition.","raw_text":"A dermoscopy examination in a patient who presented with alopecia revealed the following finding. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alopeci‍a aerata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discoid lupus erythematosu‍s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dermato‍myositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus v‍ulgaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Discoid lupus erythematosus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be6e7f2a855942c09feaaa52f0360572.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e98cd53c61274ff398ad75d78b0f64c1x1200x1709.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cb6a2af60c04b4099e82f7e55aabde7x401x269.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Discoid lupus erythematosus</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong> and <strong>plugging</strong> of the <strong>follicular ostia</strong> with keratotic material, otherwise known as the<strong> carpet tack sign</strong> or<strong> tin- tack sign,</strong> or<strong> cat's tongue sign,</strong> seen in <strong>discoid lupus erythematosus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In cutaneous lupus erythematosus or discoid lupus erythematosus, the skin lesions present as well-defined <strong>erythematous patches</strong> with <strong>adherent</strong> <strong>scaling </strong>and<strong> peripheral hyperpigmentation. </strong>When the scale is removed its undersurface shows horny plugs that have occupied dilated pilosebaceous canals. This is called the ‘tin‐tack’ sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2770","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‍d to the dermatologist wi​th alopecia and the follo‌wing disfiguring lesions.‌ His history was suggesti​ve of Raynaud's phenomeno​n. On examination, wide f‌ollicular pits were noted‌ in the concha. ANA titre​s were normal. Identify t‌he false statement regard‌ing his condition.","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented to the dermatologist with alopecia and the following disfiguring lesions. His history was suggestive of Raynaud's phenomenon. On examination, wide follicular pits were noted in the concha. ANA titres were normal. Identify the false statement regarding his condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical steroids is the fi‍rst line treatment for lo​calised lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Histology shows lymphocyti‍c interface dermatitis wi​th basal layer degenerati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antimalarials can be used ‍for treatment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-scarring alopecia is t‍ypically seen in this con​dition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-scarring alopecia is typically seen in this condition","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/50731ebf2d6747388344719356b3a626.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69016698dfe940b0abfafba9682165f5x1280x1009.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-scarring alopecia is typically seen in this condition</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario along with the image showing discoid lesions on the face with central scarring and active erythematous margins with adherent scales are suggestive of <strong>discoid lupus erythematosus.</strong> <strong>Scarring</strong> type of<strong> alopecia</strong> is seen in discoid lupus erythematosus (DLE).</p>\n<p>Histology of DLE shows <strong>lymphocytic interface dermatitis</strong> with<strong> basal layer degeneration</strong>, follicular plugging, dermal mucinosis, and epidermal atrophy.</p>\n<p>The<strong> first-line treatment</strong> for localized lesions is <strong>topical corticosteroids. </strong></p>\n<p>Hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine are <strong>oral antimalarial drugs</strong> that can be used along with oral steroids and have a good response rate. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2773","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common cutaneous​ presentation of subacute‌ cutaneous lupus erythema‌tosus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cutaneous presentation of subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scarring papulosquamous er‍uption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-scarring papulosquamou‍s eruption","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Annular polycyclic lesions‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discoid plaque with adhere‍nt scale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-scarring papulosquamous eruption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/992b257b123d4dd6865ddd5367ad1278x512x363.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-scarring papulosquamous eruption</strong></p><p><strong>Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus</strong> presents most commonly as a <strong>non‐scarring papulosquamous eruption.</strong> It can also present as annular polycyclic lesions.</p>\n<p>Sites of lesions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Above the waist</li>\n<li>Around the neck</li>\n<li>On the trunk</li>\n<li>Outer aspects of the arms</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>psoriasiform lesions</strong> of subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5398","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common cutaneous​ manifestation of systemi‌c lupus erythematosus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cutaneous manifestation of systemic lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ‍urticaria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malar‍ rash","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Raynaud's ‍Phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Photosen‍sitivity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Photosensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Photosensitivity</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cutaneous</strong> manifestation of <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> is <strong>photosensitivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>In decreasing order, the cutaneous manifestations in SLE are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Raynaud's phenomenon </li>\n<li>Malar rash</li>\n<li>Chronic urticaria &gt;36 hrs</li>\n<li>Non-scarring alopecia </li>\n<li>Mouth ulceration</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2772","text":"A young woman presented to‍ the OPD with the followi​ng rash. She also complai‌ned of myalgia, arthralgi‌a, and hair loss. Her his​tory was significant for ​sun-induced skin problems‌. Lupus band test was fou‌nd to be positive only in​ the involved skin. Which‌ of the following antibod‌ies is closely associated​ with this condition?","raw_text":"A young woman presented to the OPD with the following rash. She also complained of myalgia, arthralgia, and hair loss. Her history was significant for sun-induced skin problems. Lupus band test was found to be positive only in the involved skin. Which of the following antibodies is closely associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-Ro‍ (SS-A)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-Rib‍osomal P","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-La‍ (SS-B)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti‍-RNP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti-Ro (SS-A)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b33a46901852420bba18e970fda355e8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti-Ro (SS-A)</strong></p><p>The image shows a psoriasiform papulosquamous lesion on the sun-exposed area(back). Along with the history and the positive lupus band test only in the involved skin, a diagnosis of <strong>subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus </strong>can be made. Antibodies to the <strong>Ro/SS‐A</strong> antigen are almost universally present in this subgroup. </p>\n<p>Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus is a specific ‘subset’ of lupus in which patients exhibit mainly<strong> cutaneous disease</strong> and usually have a <strong>good prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> lupus band test</strong> (LBT) is a <strong>direct immunofluorescence</strong> technique for demonstrating a <strong>band</strong> of localized <strong>immunoglobulins</strong> at the <strong>dermal-epidermal junction</strong> in the skin of patients with lupus erythematosus (LE).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5309","text":"A 20-year-old woman presen‍ted to the hospital with ​complaints of jamais vu a‌nd new-onset seizures. CT‌ brain was normal. Her la​boratory values are given​ below. Which of the foll‌owing is not a dermatolog‌ical manifestation of thi​s disorder? Hemoglobin- 8‌.0g/dl Platelet count- 11‌6,000/μL ANA titres- &gt;1:8​0 Anti-dsDNA- positive","raw_text":"<p>A 20-year-old woman presented to the hospital with complaints of jamais vu and new-onset seizures. CT brain was normal. Her laboratory values are given below. Which of the following is not a dermatological manifestation of this disorder?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemoglobin- 8.0g/dl </li>\n<li>Platelet count- 116,000/μL</li>\n<li>ANA titres- &gt;1:80</li>\n<li>Anti-dsDNA- positive</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discoid‍ lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Painless ulcers on hard pa‍late","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Photosen‍sitivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmo‍n rash","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Salmon rash","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9fd4549688a483d8e3c6f4542a8a9e6x512x395.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Salmon rash</strong></p><p>The given clinical details are suggestive of <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> (SLE).<strong> Salmon rash</strong> is seen in <strong>adult-onset</strong> of <strong>Still's disease.</strong> It is a rare systemic inflammatory disease characterized by the classic triad of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Persistent high spiking fever</li>\n<li>Joint pain</li>\n<li>Salmon rash</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>SLE </strong>is more common in <strong>females </strong>(7:1 to 15:1).<strong> </strong>It is associated with classical erythematous rash, typically sparing the nasolabial folds called<strong> malar/butterfly rash. <br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Discoid rashes</strong> resembling discoid lupus erythematosus may also be seen. Clinical <strong>photosensitivity</strong> in the form of polymorphous light reaction and pruritus is associated with more severe systemic disease.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a malar rash.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5401","text":"A 19-year-old woman presen‍ted to the hospital with ​complaints of progressive‌ shortness of breath, che‌st pain, and lower extrem​ity edema over the past m​onth. Her initial laborat‌ory values are given belo‌w. A renal biopsy showed ​the following. Which of t‌he following antibodies i‌s considered to be most s​pecific for this disorder‌? Hemoglobin- 9.0 g/dL Pl‌atelet count- 116,000/μL ‍Serum creatinine- 2.68 mg‌/dL ANA titers- &gt;1:80. Ur‍ine protein- 3+ Urine hem​oglobin- 3+","raw_text":"<p>A 19-year-old woman presented to the hospital with complaints of progressive shortness of breath, chest pain, and lower extremity edema over the past month. Her initial laboratory values are given below. A renal biopsy showed the following. Which of the following antibodies is considered to be most specific for this disorder?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemoglobin- 9.0 g/dL</li>\n<li>Platelet count- 116,000/μL</li>\n<li>Serum creatinine- 2.68 mg/dL</li>\n<li>ANA titers- &gt;1:80.</li>\n<li>Urine protein- 3+</li>\n<li>Urine hemoglobin- 3+</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SS-A‍(Ro)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ant‍i-Sm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti ‍ds-DNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antih‍istone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-Sm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04e984b28c0c46ba9ad231e2118bdec8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7389cdbd5b64d4c905c256d19e1dfc1x1280x911.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-Sm</strong></p><p>The given clinical details and histopathology are suggestive of <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> (SLE).<strong> Anti‐Smith</strong> (anti‐Sm)<strong> antibodies</strong> are present in only approximately 30% of patients with SLE, but are considered highly <strong>specific</strong> for the disease and are included in the ACR criteria.</p>\n<p>Most <strong>sensitive</strong> antibody: <strong>ANA</strong></p>\n<p>In the image below, the glomerulus is hypercellular (endocapillary proliferation). Glomerular capillary loops appear circumferentially thickened, forming a \"wire loop\" lesion, which is classically seen in class IV or diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5402","text":"A 16-year-old boy who pres‍ented with joint pains an​d the following rash was ‌diagnosed with SLE after ‌a thorough work-up. Which​ of the following stateme​nts is incorrect regardin‌g his diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy who presented with joint pains and the following rash was diagnosed with SLE after a thorough work-up. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding his diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-ds DNA correlates wit‍h disease activity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-Sm correlates with di‍sease activity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-Ro antibody is associ‍ated with photosensitivit​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rim pattern of ANA positiv‍ity is specific for SLE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-Sm correlates with disease activity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49ecfbc6aea640ebae853119b8899f97.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-Sm correlates with disease activity</strong></p><p><strong>Anti‐Smith</strong> (anti‐Sm)<strong> antibodies</strong> are present in approximately 30% of patients with SLE, but are considered highly <strong>specific</strong> for the disease and are included in the ACR criteria, however, they are <strong>not of use</strong> in <strong>monitoring</strong> overall lupus <strong>disease activity. </strong>Anti-ds DNA levels often correlate with disease activity.</p>\n<p>The image shows the characteristic malar rash.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti‐Ro</strong> antibody occurs in approximately 30–40% of patients, who have an increased tendency to <strong>photosensitivity, secondary Sjögren</strong> syndrome, interstitial pneumonitis, shrinking lung syndrome, or deforming arthropathy, as well as being a marker for <strong>neonatal lupus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rim pattern</strong> of <strong>ANA</strong> positivity is <strong>specific</strong> for SLE.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2776","text":"An infant born to an asymp‍tomatic mother was found ​to have typical cutaneous‌ and serological findings‌ of neonatal lupus syndro​me. Anti-Ro and anti-La a​ntibodies were detected i‌n both the mother and the‌ child. Which of the foll​owing statements is true ‌regarding the child's con‌dition?","raw_text":"An infant born to an asymptomatic mother was found to have typical cutaneous and serological findings of neonatal lupus syndrome. Anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies were detected in both the mother and the child. Which of the following statements is true regarding the child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Approximately 90% of the i‍nfants manifest cutaneous​ lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rash resolves over few mon‍ths with scarring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"UV exposure has no effect ‍on the rash","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common finding is‍ raccoon sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The most common finding is raccoon sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f738f4ca980a4852b2a1e1238609f2f3x1280x1138.2792665726374.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The most common finding is raccoon sign</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> fin<strong>ding</strong> in neonatal lupus erythematosus (<strong>NLE</strong>) is an<strong> erythematous, slightly scaly eruption</strong> on the <strong>face</strong> and<strong> periorbital skin</strong> (<strong>raccoon sign</strong>/owl eye/eye mask).</p>\n<p>NLE is a well‐recognized subtype of lupus erythematosus, thought to be caused by the <strong>transplacental </strong>passage of maternal <strong>antibodies.</strong></p>\n<p>The most frequent clinical manifestations are cutaneous lesions and<strong> congenital heart block</strong> (CHB). Approximately <strong>50%</strong> of infants manifest skin lesions, which may be <strong>present at birth</strong> or occur in the <strong>first few weeks of life</strong>. The eruption can be <strong>exacerbated</strong> by<strong> UV exposure.</strong></p>\n<p>The rash improves over the first few months of life and has usually resolved <strong>without scarring</strong> by 12 months of age. Skin disease is often mild and often<strong> requires no treatment</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows typical raccoon eyelid lesions in neonatal lupus erythematosus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5283","text":"A 33-year-old patient pres‍ented with myalgia, difﬁc​ulty in climbing stairs, ‌getting up from squatting‌ position, and combing hi​s hair for 3 months. An e​dematous purplish-red per‌iorbital rash was seen. I‌nvestigations revealed el​evated creatinine kinase ‌levels. Muscle biopsy rev‌ealed perifascicular and ​perivascular lymphocytic ‌infiltrates with muscle f‌iber degeneration. Which ‍of the following is not t‌rue regarding this condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old patient presented with myalgia, difﬁculty in climbing stairs, getting up from squatting position, and combing his hair for 3 months. An edematous purplish-red periorbital rash was seen. Investigations revealed elevated creatinine kinase levels. Muscle biopsy revealed perifascicular and perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates with muscle fiber degeneration. Which of the following is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysphagia points to a wors‍e prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle tenderness is commo‍n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20 to 40% of patients have‍ an underlying malignancy​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dilated capillary loops in‍ nail fold can be seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muscle tenderness is common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/42d5abac25a84f59a8480e1f6d87ef7ax1280x1182.5105782792666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muscle tenderness is common</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>dermatomyositis. </strong>Muscle tenderness is not common in this disorder.</p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is an autoimmune disorder affecting, predominantly, the <strong>skin</strong> and <strong>skeletal muscle </strong>and usually presents<strong> initially</strong> with a <strong>facial rash </strong>especially around eyelids which can be pruritic and sore. <strong>Myositis</strong> symptoms are usually <strong>weakness</strong> and <strong>fatigue</strong> especially while climbing stairs, raising arms, and standing from sitting due to proximal muscle weakness.</p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>dysphagia</strong> points to a <strong>poorer</strong> <strong>prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical signs</strong> seen in dermatomyositis are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heliotrope rash around eyelids with a lilac hue.</li>\n<li><strong>Gottron papules - </strong>flat-topped lichenoid eruption on the proximal and distal interphalangeal joint</li>\n<li><strong>Shawl sign or V sign</strong> - fixed erythematous macular erythema)</li>\n<li><strong>Nail fold changes</strong> with<strong> periungal erythema </strong>with visible dilated capillary loops in the proximal nail fold</li>\n<li><strong>Proximal myopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Poikiloderma atrophicans vasculare</li>\n<li>Calcinosis cutis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Approximately 20 to 40% of patients have an underlying malignancy (eg, lung, ovarian, breast) and hence screening should be done at the time of diagnosis.</p>\n<p>The image below shows ragged cuticle and periungueal erythema in a patient with dermatomyositis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5397","text":"In which of the following ‍disorders is the followin​g sign seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following disorders is the following sign seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discoid lupus erythematosu‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‍us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermato‍myositis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Localised ‍scleroderma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dermatomyositis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/97a26715c0014f97964b584e0f4ea47c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/077f896ca371464e96edd079f47ad70dx1280x1664.5416078984485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dermatomyositis</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>maculopapular rash</strong> on the <strong>lateral thigh</strong> which is also known as the<strong> holster sign</strong>, seen in <strong>dermatomyositis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The buttocks are often involved and confluent macular violaceous erythema over the hips and lateral thighs has been termed the ‘holster sign’ corresponding to the site of hanging a holster.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5311","text":"Which of the following aut‍oantibodies shows a stron​g association with the gi‌ven clinical condition?","raw_text":"Which of the following autoantibodies shows a strong association with the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‍-Mi-2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti P1‍55/P140","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti‍ Jo-1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti ‍Scl-70","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti-Mi-2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a7199b6d601146efb424d852abb0c474.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti-Mi-2</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>pathognomonic </strong>involvement of eyelids in dermatomyositis in which the<strong> upper eyelids</strong> are<strong> preferentially affected </strong>with <strong>erythema</strong> that is lilac in color called<strong> heliotrope rash. </strong>This is associated with<strong> anti-Mi-2 antibodies.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gottron’s papule</strong>, <strong>h</strong><strong>eliotrope rash</strong>, and <strong>s</strong><strong>hawl sign</strong> are associated with <strong>anti-Mi2 antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mechanic hand, Myositis, and ILD (Interstitial Lung Disease) are associated with anti‐histidyl tRNA synthetase (<strong>anti-Jo-1</strong>) antibodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5396","text":"Identify Got‍tron’s sign.","raw_text":"Identify Gottron’s sign.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‍2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65a06a78b64742279d54537e340122fa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/369e034945a34e1ca6620d67520ffcd0x1280x1697.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p><strong>Gottron's sign</strong> refers to <strong>erythematous or violaceous</strong>, <strong>macules</strong> or <strong>patches</strong> in the same symmetric distribution pattern but <strong>sparing </strong>the <strong>interphalangeal spaces</strong> and sometimes involving<strong> elbows</strong> and<strong> knees</strong>.</p>\n<p>This should not be confused with Gottron's papules.</p>\n<p><strong>Gottron’s papules </strong>are <strong>v</strong><strong>iolaceous </strong>(of violet color), <strong>l</strong><strong>ichenoid </strong>(red-purple), inflammatory, flat-topped <strong>papules</strong> on proximal interphalangeal joints, distal interphalangeal joints, and metacarpophalangeal joints. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5395","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions is this findin​g characteristically seen‌?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is this finding characteristically seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discoid lupus erythematosu‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cutaneous ‍scleroderma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‍us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermato‍myositis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dermatomyositis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/40c43dd8c3f04527907abf2dadb68bcb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dermatomyositis</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>confluent macular violaceous erythema </strong>called the <strong>V sign</strong> seen in patients of<strong> dermatomyositis.</strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is an autoimmune disorder affecting, predominantly, the <strong>skin</strong> and <strong>skeletal muscle </strong>and usually presents<strong> initially</strong> with a <strong>facial rash, </strong>especially around eyelids, which can be pruritic and sore. <strong>Myositis</strong> symptoms are usually <strong>weakness</strong> and <strong>fatigue</strong> especially while climbing stairs, raising arms, and standing from sitting due to proximal muscle weakness.</p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>dysphagia</strong> points to a <strong>poorer</strong> <strong>prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical signs</strong> seen in dermatomyositis are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heliotrope rash around eyelids with a lilac hue.</li>\n<li><strong>Gottron papules - </strong>flat-topped lichenoid eruption on the proximal and distal interphalangeal joint</li>\n<li><strong>Shawl sign or V sign</strong> - fixed erythematous macular erythema</li>\n<li><strong>Nail fold changes</strong> with<strong> periungual erythema </strong>with visible dilated capillary loops in the proximal nail fold</li>\n<li><strong>Proximal myopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Poikiloderma atrophicans vasculare</li>\n<li>Calcinosis cutis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5400","text":"A 35-year-old female patie‍nt came with complaints o​f her hands turning very ‌pale on exposure to cold ‌for one month. She also g​ives a history of hand pa​in and swelling for the p‌ast 3 months. She informe‌d that her skin felt firm​ and tight. Her past medi‌cal history is positive f‌or GERD and arthritis. Im​age A shows a particular ‌feature of this patient. ‌On investigation, she was‍ found to have the follow‌ing immunofluorescence pa‍ttern (image B). Identify​ the wrong statement rega‌rding her condition.","raw_text":"A 35-year-old female patient came with complaints of her hands turning very pale on exposure to cold for one month. She also gives a history of hand pain and swelling for the past 3 months. She informed that her skin felt firm and tight. Her past medical history is positive for GERD and arthritis. Image A shows a particular feature of this patient. On investigation, she was found to have the following immunofluorescence pattern (image B). Identify the wrong statement regarding her condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interstitial lung disease ‍is common in patients wit​h Scl-70 antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Telangiectasias and calcin‍osis are early manifestat​ions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Earliest cutaneous manifes‍tations are non‐pitting o​edema and puffiness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pruritus correlates with s‍everity of cutaneous scle​rosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Telangiectasias and calcinosis are early manifestations","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2319d86d3dfb46d187355f07467c85e0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Telangiectasias and calcinosis are early manifestations</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario featuring Raynaud's phenomenon, arthritis, GERD, and calcinosis cutis is suggestive of <strong>systemic sclerosis</strong>. <strong>Mat-like telangiectases</strong> on the face and upper trunk and limbs, and <strong>cutaneous calcinosis</strong>, most often on the fingers over joints and at pressure points, develop in the<strong> later stages, </strong>particularly in the <strong>limited systemic</strong> form of systemic sclerosis, or <strong>CREST syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Image A is a classical fish mouth appearance (<strong>microstomia</strong>). Image B shows the <strong>nucleolar pattern</strong> of IF(some spots of fluorescence within the nucleus and it represents antibodies to RNA). This is seen in systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>The majority of cases are <strong>adult-onset </strong>with <strong>Raynaud's phenomenon</strong> as the<strong> first symptom</strong>. </p>\n<p>The earliest cutaneous manifestations are <strong>non‐pitting edema</strong> and<strong> puffiness</strong>, which tend to be seen first in the fingers, hands, and face. The skin becomes taut, indurated, thickened, and then fixed to deeper structures on the fingers resulting in <strong>sclerodactyly</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pruritus</strong> is described in 40% of patients and may be intense during the early or active stages of the disease. It <strong>correlates</strong> with the extent of <strong>skin sclerosis</strong> and<strong> gastrointestinal symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-scl-70</strong> positivity in this disease is useful in predicting those at higher risk for interstitial lung disease. <strong>Anti centromere</strong> antibody is most specific for <strong>CREST</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5307","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a feature of the cond​ition shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of the condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle ‍weakness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastroesophageal reflux di‍sease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of sweat glands in di‍stal extremities","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Female preponderance of 9:‍1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Female preponderance of 9:1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f83d459c8b44070b59f6a729b0d1b92.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25a7fc636f6242d7b9556e59c971b1c6x720x1059.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72bb07d925094354a4083b45c79558c0x576x384.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e58eaaa5f65d4cd19631189bab0fdea9x576x371.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Female preponderance of 9:1</strong></p><p>The image shows a young woman with <strong>mask-like facies</strong> with stretched, shiny skin, and loss of normal facial lines, <strong>microstomia,</strong> and <strong>pitted scarring</strong> on the right index <strong>finger</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>scleroderma</strong> or<strong> systemic sclerosis</strong>, an autoimmune rheumatic disease in which there is a <strong>4: 1 female predominance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cutaneous features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hands and feet - Raynaud's phenomenon, non-pitting edema, painful ulcerations which heal with<strong> pitted scars,</strong> sclerodactyly, contractures,<strong> loss of sweat glands</strong>, bone resorption.</li>\n<li>Face - periorbital edema, <strong>mask-like facies</strong>, telangiectasia, and <strong>diffuse hyperpigmentation</strong></li>\n<li>Trunk - firm, shiny, and thickened skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>extent of skin sclerosis</strong> in <strong>s</strong><strong>ystemic sclerosis</strong> can be measured, most commonly employing a <strong>modified Rodnan skin score</strong> (MRSS).</p>\n<p>Systemic features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GIT- dysphagia,<strong> GERD</strong>, diminished peristalsis, malabsorption</li>\n<li>Lungs- <strong>pulmonary fibrosis</strong> and alveolitis</li>\n<li>Heart- cardiac conduction defects, heart failure</li>\n<li>Kidney- uremia, malignant hypertension</li>\n<li>Musculoskeletal- weakness, <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows typical facial features: note the expressionless facies, mat‐like telangiectases, microstomia, perioral furrowing, and beak‐like nose.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows digital ulceration</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows advanced‐stage sclerodactyly with contractures and vasculopathic ulcers over the bony prominences</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In systemic sclerosis, progressive loss of fingerprints is seen in patients. These altered fingerprint ridges are seen due to changes in skin elasticity,  hardness, and thickness and eventually make a patient with scleroderma a “fingerprintless person”.</p>\n<p>Autoantibody patterns seen in systemic sclerosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reactivity to autoantibodies such as anticentromere (ACA) is commonly seen in limited skin sclerosis.</li>\n<li>Reactivity to autoantibodies such as antitopoisomerase 1 antibody (<strong>anti‐Scl‐70 or ATA</strong>), and anti‐RNA polymerase III antibody (<strong>ARA or RNAP</strong>) is commonly seen in <strong>SSc</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2784","text":"The image shows an investi‍gative modality of nail f​olds. This along with ANA‌ testing helps in the ear‌ly diagnosis of:","raw_text":"The image shows an investigative modality of nail folds. This along with ANA testing helps in the early diagnosis of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato‍myositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic ‍sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‍us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mixed connective tissue di‍sorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Systemic sclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa382cf36199451299426c91a5057aff.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Systemic sclerosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>nail-fold capillaroscopy</strong> findings. The image shows giant capillaries, hemorrhages, and reducing capillary density suggesting an active pattern in <strong>systemic sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most important tests for systemic sclerosis are <strong>ANA reactivity</strong> and<strong> nail fold capillaroscopy. </strong>Investigations to exclude or confirm other autoimmune rheumatic diseases including arthritis or other forms of connective tissue disease are also done. </p>\n<p>Nail-fold capillaroscopy is a <strong>non-invasive</strong>, easy, and safe diagnostic technique designed to evaluate the <strong>general architecture of capillary rows</strong> and <strong>fine details</strong> of small vessels of the microcirculation in the nail fold.</p>\n<p>The most important indications for performing capillaroscopy include differential diagnosis of <strong>primary</strong> and<strong> secondary Raynaud's phenomenon</strong>, as well as the assessment of scleroderma spectrum disorders.</p>\n<p>In systemic sclerosis, capillary abnormalities appear and evolve in a clearly defined sequence called the <strong>scleroderma pattern</strong>, which<strong> correlates with internal organ involvement</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nail fold capillaroscopy is also listed as a systemic sclerosis <strong>classification criterion</strong> recognized by the European League Against Rheumatism (<strong>EULAR</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2787","text":"A woman presented to the m‍edicine OPD with complain​ts of dysphagia and her f‌ingers turning white and ‌then blue when exposed to​ cold. On examination, sh​e has telangiectasias on ‌the face and the given fi‌ndings on her hands. Whic​h of the following statem‌ents is correct regarding‌ her condition?","raw_text":"A woman presented to the medicine OPD with complaints of dysphagia and her fingers turning white and then blue when exposed to cold. On examination, she has telangiectasias on the face and the given findings on her hands. Which of the following statements is correct regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-centromere antibodies‍ are most commonly found","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Involvement of skin areas ‍proximal to the knees or ​elbows","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid onset and progressio‍n of disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short history and less sev‍ere form of Raynaud's phe​nomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti-centromere antibodies are most commonly found","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27f5a3ed75ea4b3cb8fbd161589ef046.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti-centromere antibodies are most commonly found</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The history along with the image showing <strong>calcinosis cutis</strong> and <strong>sclerodactyly</strong> are suggestive of <strong>CREST</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In <strong>limited</strong> <strong>cutaneous systemic sclerosis</strong> or CREST syndrome, <strong>anti-centromere antibodies</strong> are the most commonly found antibodies.</p>\n<p>All other options are true for <strong>diffuse</strong> cutaneous systemic sclerosis, not CREST syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2786","text":"Identify the false stateme‍nt regarding morphea.","raw_text":"Identify the false statement regarding morphea.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plaque morphea is the most‍ common form","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of sclerodactyly a‍nd nail fold capillary ch​anges","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antitopoisomerase and anti‍centromere are usually po​sitive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Encoup de Sabre is linear ‍morphea presenting as sca​rring alopecia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antitopoisomerase and anticentromere are usually positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0ae9dfcfe69e41edac080c63fdef2026x1280x962.2566995768689.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a27b758543224fcb84e47e24b665a82cx1280x1129.2524682651622.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antitopoisomerase and anticentromere are usually positive</strong></p><p>Autoantibodies such as <strong>ANA, anti-histone, and anti‐ssDNA</strong> may be present in <strong>morphea</strong>, but the systemic sclerosis specific autoantibodies such as anti-topoisomerase, anti-centromere and anti‐RNA polymerase are rarely found.</p>\n<p>Morphoea, a term used in preference to ‘<strong>localized scleroderma</strong>’, encompasses a group of related conditions characterized by varying degrees of <strong>sclerosis, fibrosis, and atrophy</strong> in the skin and subcutaneous tissues.</p>\n<p>It is distinguished from systemic sclerosis by the <strong>absence</strong> of sclerodactyly and nail-fold capillary changes.</p>\n<p>Overall, <strong>plaque morphea</strong> is the <strong>commonest subtype.</strong></p>\n<p>Morphoea en coup de sabre:<strong> Blaschkoid linear induration</strong> affecting the face and scalp, may involve underlying muscle, bone, eye, and the brain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2785","text":"A man presented with salt ‍and pepper skin changes o​n his chest and back and ‌with shortness of breath.‌ A CT revealed fibrotic c​hanges in the lower lobe ​of both the lungs. What i‌s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A man presented with salt and pepper skin changes on his chest and back and with shortness of breath. A CT revealed fibrotic changes in the lower lobe of both the lungs. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‍ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‍us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermato‍myositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic ‍sclerosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Systemic sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8691e2a4bd894688894107baf1891e92x357x535.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Systemic sclerosis</strong></p><p>Salt-and-pepper appearance is characterized by the presence of <strong>patchy hypopigmentation</strong> and<strong> hyperpigmentation</strong> of the skin manifesting as <strong>depigmentation</strong> with<strong> perifollicular pigmentary retention. </strong>This along with lung fibrosis is seen in <strong>systemic sclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>This appearance may also be seen in areas of repigmentation in lesions of vitiligo.</p>\n<p>The image below shows salt and pepper appearance of skin over the abdomen seen in systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XSKn-mrU","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"21_epistaxis","title":"21.Epistaxis","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1101","text":"A 15-year-old boy was brou‌ght to the ER with a nose​ bleed secondary to traum‌a. The bleeding started a​n hour ago and has not st​opped. Where is the bleed‍ most likely to be from?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy was brought to the ER with a nose bleed secondary to trauma. The bleeding started an hour ago and has not stopped. Where is the bleed most likely to be from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Little‌'s area","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bony ‌septum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior‌ tubinate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral wa‌ll of nose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Little's area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de79894010ae4ea18d2e148a03920395x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Little&#x27;s area</strong></p><p>The bleeding is most likely from the <strong>Little's area.</strong></p>\n<p>Little’s area,<strong> </strong>situated at <strong>the anteroinferior part </strong>of the <strong>nasal septum</strong> is the <strong>most common site </strong>of epistaxis in <strong>children and young adults.</strong></p>\n<p>Factors that increase the risk of epistaxis in children when compared to adults, include the habit of <strong>nose picking</strong> and increased friability of mucosa due to <strong>frequent upper respiratory tract infections</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1102","text":"Where is Little’s area sit‌uated?","raw_text":"Where is Little’s area situated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterosuperior part of sep‌tum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anteroinferior part of sep‌tum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterosuperior part of se‌ptum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posteroinferior part of se‌ptum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anteroinferior part of septum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anteroinferior part of septum</strong></p><p>Little’s area is situated in the <strong>anterior inferior part </strong>of the nasal septum, just above the vestibule.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1104","text":"A worried mother brought h‌er 4-year-old son to the ​ER with complaints of a n‌ose bleed. On examination​, the bleed was found to ​be from the Kiesselbach’s‍ plexus. Which of the fol‌lowing arteries does not ​contribute to its formati​on?","raw_text":"A worried mother brought her 4-year-old son to the ER with complaints of a nose bleed. On examination, the bleed was found to be from the Kiesselbach’s plexus. Which of the following arteries does not contribute to its formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphenopala‌tine artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater pal‌atine artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior eth‌moidal artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ethmoidal artery‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior ethmoidal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior ethmoidal artery</strong></p><p>The posterior ethmoidal artery does not contribute to the formation of Kiesselbach’s plexus.</p>\n<p><strong>Kiesselbach’s plexus</strong> is the <strong>vascular anastomosis </strong>situated at <strong>Little’s area </strong>(anteroinferior part of the septum).</p>\n<p>The contributing arteries are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sphenopalatine and greater palatine branches </strong>of the maxillary artery, which in turn is a branch of the external carotid artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Septal branch of the superior labial artery</strong> which in turn is a branch of the facial artery. The facial artery is a branch of the external carotid artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior ethmoidal branch</strong> of the ophthalmic division of internal carotid artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1106","text":"Identify the artery of epi‌staxis in the given image​.","raw_text":"Identify the artery of epistaxis in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b2d765736fd44aea57a6279ecf64723.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51892922adb3460280e2d4a7d534e907x1280x2104.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The structure marked <strong>3</strong> is the <strong>sphenopalatine artery </strong>which is also known as the <strong>artery of epistaxis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is called the artery of epistaxis because the majority of the blood supply to the nasal cavity is derived from it. It is also the <strong>most commonly involved</strong> artery in epistaxis.</p>\n<p>Other structures marked in the diagram are as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>1 </strong>- Anterior ethmoidal artery</p>\n<p><strong>2 </strong>- Posterior ethmoidal artery</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>- Greater palatine artery</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1107","text":"Which of the following mar‌ked structures is a branc​h of the internal carotid‌ artery?","raw_text":"Which of the following marked structures is a branch of the internal carotid artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9371006c433943ca825badb7e0293200.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a91e8badd60d4b7680b34d17cdec352cx1280x2104.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>The structure marked <strong>1 </strong>is the <strong>anterior ethmoidal artery</strong>, which is a branch of the <strong>ophthalmic division</strong> of the <strong>internal carotid artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Other structures marked in the diagram are as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>2 </strong>– Sphenopalatine artery</p>\n<p><strong>3 </strong>– Greater palatine artery</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Superior labial artery</p>\n<p>2, 3 and 4 are derived from branches of the <strong>external carotid artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>nasal septum</strong> is supplied by branches of both <strong>internal</strong> and <strong>external carotid arteries</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1109","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ with a history of uncont​rolled, chronic hypertens‌ion was rushed to the cas​ualty after he had an epi​sode of coughing out fran‍k blood. On further evalu‌ation, a diagnosis of pos​terior epistaxis, seconda​ry to hypertension, was m‌ade. What is the site of ‌the blood vessel most lik​ely to be involved in thi​s case?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient with a history of uncontrolled, chronic hypertension was rushed to the casualty after he had an episode of coughing out frank blood. On further evaluation, a diagnosis of posterior epistaxis, secondary to hypertension, was made. What is the site of the blood vessel most likely to be involved in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior end of superior ‌turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior end of inferior ‌turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior end of middle tu‌rbinate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior en‌d of septum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior end of middle turbinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior end of middle turbinate</strong></p><p>The most common cause for posterior epistaxis is <strong>arterial</strong> and the vessel involved is the <strong>sphenopalatine artery</strong>. It is located in the <strong>posterior end of the middle turbinate.</strong></p>\n<p>Woodruff's plexus is a <strong>venous</strong> plexus located in the posterior end of the inferior turbinate and is also sometimes implicated in epistaxis associated with systemic conditions such as hypertension and coagulopathy. However, it only accounts for about 5-10% of all nose bleeds.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1657\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note: Most common vessel involved in<strong> both anterior and posterior epistaxis</strong> is the <strong>sphenopalatine artery </strong>and not Woodruff's plexus. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1110","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​epistaxis in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of epistaxis in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bleeding ‌disorders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cold w‌eather","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sinonasal ‌infections","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trauma</strong></p><p><strong>Microtrauma due to nose pricking</strong> is the <strong>most common cause </strong>of epistaxis in children.</p>\n<p>Other common aetiological associations include upper respiratory tract infections.</p>\n<div>Note: In children presenting with <strong>recurrent</strong> epistaxis, bleeding disorders, hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia, and tumors, etc. have to be ruled out.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1112","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​epistaxis in elderly pati‌ents?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of epistaxis in elderly patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medic‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carc‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idiop‌athic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Idiopathic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Idiopathic</strong></p><p>The most common cause of epistaxis in elderly patients is <strong>idiopthic.</strong></p>\n<p>Other causes are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Tumor - high suspicion in elderly patients presenting with epistaxis</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n<li>Medication</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Due to the failure of studies to establish a causal relationship between hypertension and epistaxis, hypertension is now considered an associated risk factor and not a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> associated with epistaxis are <strong>weather</strong> changes and <strong>alcohol</strong> consumption. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1113","text":"A 4-year-old child present‌s with persistent unilate​ral foul-smelling nasal d‌ischarge which is blood-s​tained. Which of the foll​owing is the most likely ‍cause?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child presents with persistent unilateral foul-smelling nasal discharge which is blood-stained. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foreig‌n body","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophic‌ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal‌ polyp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasal ang‌iofibroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Foreign body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Foreign body</strong></p><p>In a child presenting with persistent unilateral, foul-smelling nasal discharge, which is blood-stained, the most likely cause is a <strong>foreign body.</strong></p>\n<p>Foreign body can cause unilateral nasal obstruction and subsequent superimposed infection which causes malodorous discharge. It may often be blood-stained. </p>\n<p>Option B: Atrophic rhinitis usually <strong>starts at puberty</strong> and mainly presents with <strong>nasal obstruction </strong>and<strong> foul smell </strong>from the nose. Epistaxis occurs only after the removal of crusts.</p>\n<p>Option C: Epistaxis is a rare manifestation of polyps.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>Unilateral nasal angiofibroma also presents as painless, progressive unilateral nasal obstruction, often associated with epistaxis and rhinorrhea. But it is more common in the <strong>adolescent</strong> age group.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1115","text":"What is the maneuver depic‌ted in the image given be​low?","raw_text":"What is the maneuver depicted in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epley’s ‌maneuvre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trotter’‌s method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"McGovern’s‌ technique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valsalva‌ maneuvre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trotter’s method","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5816285b2db4d988bbf604f4010ecdd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b1e161d7dcb4d68a6f7e6653db23d52x1280x2325.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/431d6f8a4b8048f08d60bb8e2c22379dx1280x1343.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trotter’s method</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Trotter’s method</strong> in which the patient is made to sit, leaning a little forward over a basin to spit any blood and breathe quietly from the mouth. It is the first step to control <strong>epistaxis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Epley’s maneuver</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>BPPV</strong> (Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo).</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 60\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option C: <strong>McGovern’s technique</strong> is used in <strong>choanal atresia</strong>. A feeding nipple with a large hole provides a good oral airway.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Valsalva maneuver </strong>is performed by forceful attempted <strong>exhalation against a closed nose and mouth. </strong>It is used as a test of cardiac function and autonomic nervous control of the heart, or to clear the ears and sinuses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1116","text":"A 60-year-old woman came t‌o the casualty with a his​tory of an episode of spo‌ntaneous epistaxis yester​day night. On examination​, her BP was 200/110, and‍ there was no active blee‌ding from the nose. Which​ of the following is the ​most appropriate next ste‌p in managing this case?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman came to the casualty with a history of an episode of spontaneous epistaxis yesterday night. On examination, her BP was 200/110, and there was no active bleeding from the nose. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in managing this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Observation with BP manage‌ment","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Internal maxillary artery ‌ligation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal ‌packing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cauter‌ization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Observation with BP management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Observation with BP management</strong></p><p>The most appropriate next step is <strong>observation</strong> and <strong>blood pressure management</strong> as there is <strong>no active bleeding</strong> from the nose. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1117","text":"A 65-year-old hypertensive‌ patient presented with a​ history of recurrent epi‌sodes of spontaneous nose​ bleeds over the past two​ weeks, each requiring me‍dical attention. After ev‌aluating the patient, the​ ENT surgeon recommended ​endoscopic artery ligatio‌n to prevent further epis‌odes. Which of the follow​ing arteries is most comm​only ligated in this proc​edure?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old hypertensive patient presented with a history of recurrent episodes of spontaneous nose bleeds over the past two weeks, each requiring medical attention. After evaluating the patient, the ENT surgeon recommended endoscopic artery ligation to prevent further episodes. Which of the following arteries is most commonly ligated in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphenopala‌tine artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Greater pal‌atine artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal ca‌rotid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sphenopalatine artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c485a7502d64fbe8c9e0d0ef678c33fx1280x2104.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sphenopalatine artery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>refractory posterior epistaxis</strong>. The artery which is most commonly ligated in a case of refractory posterior epistaxis is the <strong>s</strong><strong>phenopalatine artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Posterior epistaxis is most commonly seen in <strong>older age groups</strong>. The bleeding is <strong>spontaneous</strong>, often due to hypertension, and is severe, requiring hospitalization. Refractory epistaxis is defined as recurrent or persistent bleeding after appropriate packing or multiple episodes of epistaxis during a short period, each requiring medical attention.</p>\n<p><strong>Sphenopalatine artery ligation</strong> gives <strong>high success </strong>in the control of refractory posterior bleed<strong>.</strong> Therefore, transnasal<strong> endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation</strong> (TESPAL) is the preferred method.</p>\n<p>It is ligated at <strong>sphenopalatine foramen </strong>situated 1 cm behind the posterior end of middle turbinate.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>Anterior epistaxis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Posterior epistaxis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Age</td>\n<td>Children &amp; young adults</td>\n<td>&gt;40 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incidence</td>\n<td>Most common type</td>\n<td>Less common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cause</td>\n<td>Trauma</td>\n<td>Spontaneous; often due to Hypertension or arteriosclerosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Site</td>\n<td>Little’s area - anteroinferior part of the nasal septum</td>\n<td>Posterosuperior part of the nasal cavity; difficult to localize</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bleeding</td>\n<td>Mild- can be controlled by local pressure or nasal packing.</td>\n<td>Severe-often requires hospitalization and posterior nasal packing.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1118","text":"As a first-year resident, ‌you have scrubbed in to a​ssist for a maxillary art‌ery ligation procedure on​ a patient with intractab​le epistaxis. After openi‍ng the posterior wall of ‌the maxillary sinus, the ​surgeon asks you to ligat​e the maxillary artery. W‌here would you ligate the‌ artery at?","raw_text":"As a first-year resident, you have scrubbed in to assist for a maxillary artery ligation procedure on a patient with intractable epistaxis. After opening the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus, the surgeon asks you to ligate the maxillary artery. Where would you ligate the artery at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillar‌y antrum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pterygopal‌atine fossa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ne‌ck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial wal‌l of orbit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pterygopalatine fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a833252794394b85a18aa303fb4faf05x1280x1454.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7ba2b11bff946218d189214fb563cf8x512x433.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pterygopalatine fossa</strong></p><p>The <strong>maxillary artery</strong> is ligated at the <strong>pterygopalatine fossa</strong> (sphenopalatine fossa) situated behind the maxillary sinus’s posterior wall. </p>\n<p>The internal maxillary artery is accessed transantrally via a <strong>Caldwell-Luc approach</strong>. After the posterior sinus wall is removed, the internal maxillary artery and its terminal branches are identified and clipped. The posterior sinus wall is then packed with Gelfoam, and the gingivobuccal incision is closed. More recently, transoral and transnasal endoscopic approaches have also been described.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1119","text":"Which artery among the fol‌lowing could be the sourc​e of bleeding in a case o‌f epistaxis not controlle​d by external carotid art​ery ligation?","raw_text":"Which artery among the following could be the source of bleeding in a case of epistaxis not controlled by external carotid artery ligation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphenopala‌tine artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater pal‌atine artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethmoida‌l artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ethmoidal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f53f71afea7c4dd0bdc7baf9c4d11861x1280x2104.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ffa79f90d164dd3ab30e5e7bca25fddx1280x1436.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ethmoidal artery</strong></p><p><strong>Ethmoidal artery</strong> can cause epistaxis not controlled by external carotid artery ligation.</p>\n<p>The ethmoidal artery is a branch of<strong> internal carotid artery,</strong> whereas <strong>sphenopalatine</strong>, <strong>greater palatine</strong> and <strong>facial</strong> artery are branches of the <strong>external carotid artery</strong>. Hence if the bleeding is not controlled by external carotid artery ligation, the subsequent step would be to ligate the ethmoidal artery.</p>\n<p>Epistaxis may be classified as anterior or posterior epistaxis.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 370px; height: 433px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Anterior epistaxis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">Posterior epistaxis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">Site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Little's area or anterior part of the lateral wall</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">Posterosuperior part of the nasal cavity, or difficult to localize</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Children, young adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">&gt;40 years of age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">Cause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Usually trauma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">Spontaneous, due to hypertension or IHD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">Bleeding</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Mild, easily controlled with conservative measures (pressure, anterior nasal pack)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\">Severe requires hospitalisation and posterior nasal pack</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Little's area is situated in the anterior inferior part of the nasal septum, just above the vestibule. It is the site of epistaxis in 90% of the cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Four arteries anastomose</strong> here to form a vascular plexus called the<strong> Kiesselbach’s plexus</strong>. This area is exposed to the drying effect of inspiratory current and to fingernail trauma and is the usual site for epistaxis in children and young adults.</p>\n<p>The four contributing arteries are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Septal branch of sphenopalatine</strong> and <strong>greater palatine branches </strong>of the maxillary artery, which in turn is a branch of the external carotid artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Septal branch of the superior labial artery</strong> which in turn is a branch of the facial artery. The facial artery is a branch of the external carotid artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior ethmoidal branch</strong> of the ophthalmic division of internal carotid artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>The stepwise management of epistaxis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pinching the nose </li>\n<li>Hippocratic or Trotter’s method</li>\n<li>Cauterisation</li>\n<li>Anterior nasal packing</li>\n<li>Posterior nasal packing </li>\n<li>Endoscopic Cauterisation </li>\n<li>Ligation </li>\n<li>Embolization</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Ligation should be performed as close as possible to the likely bleeding point. The recommended hierarchy of ligation to be followed is <strong>Sphenopalatine artery → Internal maxillary artery → External carotid artery → Anterior /posterior ethmoidal artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Ethmoidal vessels are exposed in the medial wall of the orbit by an <strong>external ethmoid (Lynch) incision.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7588","text":"Which of the following ves‌sels would you never liga​te for the control of rec‌urrent spontaneous epista​xis?","raw_text":"Which of the following vessels would you never ligate for the control of recurrent spontaneous epistaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal ca‌rotid artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior eth‌moidal artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External ca‌rotid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Internal carotid artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Internal carotid artery</strong></p><p>Internal carotid artery is <strong>not</strong> ligated for controlling epistaxis due to the high percentage of <strong>cerebral complications</strong> and<strong> mortality</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ligation of the following arteries is done in epistaxis control :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trans nasal Endoscopic <strong>Sphenopalatine artery</strong> <strong>ligation</strong> <strong>(TESPAL)</strong></li>\n<li>If not controlled, <strong>m</strong><strong>axillary artery ligation</strong></li>\n<li>If still not controlled, <strong>e</strong><strong>x</strong><strong>ternal carotid artery ligation</strong></li>\n<li>In case, bleeding site is superior to the level of middle turbinate, then<strong> anterior and posterior ethmoidal vessels</strong> should be ligated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HeJkEYzk","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"21_cns___narcotics_and_deliriants","title":"21.Cns   Narcotics And Deliriants","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB0932","text":"Match the following drugs ​of abuse with their commo​n names. Drug of abuse Co​mmon name 1. Heroin and c‌ocaine a. Ice 2. Phencycl‌idine and cocaine b. Rail​way platform poison 3. He‌roin c. Speedball 4. Meth​amphetamine d. Brown suga‌r 5. Datura e. Beam me up‌","raw_text":"<p>Match the following drugs of abuse with their common names.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 215px; width: 336px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.406px;\"><strong>Drug of abuse</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.594px;\"><strong>Common name</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.406px;\">1. Heroin and cocaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.594px;\">a. Ice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.406px;\">2. Phencyclidine and cocaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.594px;\">b. Railway platform poison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.406px;\">3. Heroin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.594px;\">c. Speedball</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.406px;\">4. Methamphetamine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.594px;\">d. Brown sugar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.406px;\">5. Datura</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.594px;\">e. Beam me up</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-e, 2-a, 3​-b, 4-c, 5-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2-e, 3​-d, 4-a, 5-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-d, 2-c, 3​-a, 4-e, 5-b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-e, 2-c, 3​-b, 4-d, 5-a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b</strong></p><p>The correctly matched pairs are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 149px; width: 440.094px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\"><strong>Drug of abuse</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.094px;\"><strong>Common name</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">1. Heroin and cocaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.094px;\">Speedball</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">2. Phencyclidine and cocaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.094px;\">Beam me up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">3. Heroin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.094px;\">Brown sugar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">4. Methamphetamine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.094px;\">Ice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px;\">5. Datura</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.094px;\">Railway platform poison</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0933","text":"Which of the following pre​paration is incorrectly m​atched with its source?","raw_text":"Which of the following preparation is incorrectly matched with its source?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bhang - ste​m and leaves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Majoon - pie prepared from​ a mixture of cannabis an​d different substances","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hashish - resin from flowe​ring heads","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ganja - drie​d flower tops","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hashish - resin from flowering heads","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hashish - resin from flowering heads</strong></p><p><strong>Hashish</strong> is prepared from the <strong>resin</strong> exuding from <strong>leaves and stem</strong><strong> </strong>of the plants.</p>\n<p>Various cannabis preparations:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 290px; width: 365px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.719px;\"><strong>Cannabis Preparation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.281px;\"><strong>Source</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.719px;\">Bhang</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.281px;\">Traditionally used as a drink prepared from stem and leaves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.719px;\">Majoon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.281px;\">A traditional solid pie prepared from a mixture of cannabis and other different materials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.719px;\">Ganja</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.281px;\">Dried flowering tops and leaves of the plant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.719px;\">Hashish/Charas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.281px;\">Resin exuding from leaves and stem</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0928","text":"The method shown in the im​age below is called chasi​ng the dragon. Which drug​ is taken through this ro‌ute?","raw_text":"The method shown in the image below is called chasing the dragon. Which drug is taken through this route?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L​SD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Her​oin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mari​juana","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heroin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/60b2360051c041d1bdf21f69952e23e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heroin</strong></p><p><strong>Chasing the dragon</strong> is a method used to take <strong>heroin</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this, users <strong>inhale</strong> a thick, white pyrolysate that is generated by heating heroin base on aluminum foil using a hand-held flame. This means of administration produces heroin <strong>concentrations similar</strong> to those observed following <strong>IV</strong> administration.</p>\n<p>Other methods used to take drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mainlining - Intravenous injection</li>\n<li>Insufflation - Snorting</li>\n<li>Skin popping - Subcutaneous injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0911","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding opioid in​toxication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding opioid intoxication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hallucination results from​ stimulation of kappa rec​eptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchospasm is due to his​tamine release","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opioid-induce acute lung i​njury is a rare complicat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid intoxication can be​ ruled out in absence of ​miosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Opioid intoxication can be ruled out in absence of miosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Opioid intoxication can be ruled out in absence of miosis</strong></p><p><strong>Miosis</strong> is <strong>not universally present</strong> in intoxication from every opioid. Its absence cannot be used to rule out opioid intoxication. <strong>Normal</strong> or even <strong>enlarged pupils</strong> have been documented secondary to diphenoxylate, meperidine, morphine, pentazocine, and propoxyphene toxicity.</p>\n<p><strong>Opioid intoxication:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mental status depression</li>\n<li>Analgesia</li>\n<li>Miosis</li>\n<li>Orthostatic hypotension</li>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Histamine release resulting in localized urticaria</li>\n<li>Ileus secondary to decreased GI motility</li>\n<li>Urinary retention secondary to increased vesical sphincter tone</li>\n<li>Respiratory depression, <strong>bronchospasm (histamine release)</strong>, <strong>acute lung injury (rare</strong> complication)</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 179px;\" width=\"371\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.4375px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 34.4375px;\"><strong>Opioid receptor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 34.4375px;\"><strong>Effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 239px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 239px;\">mu</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 239px;\">\n<p>Analgesia</p>\n<p>Sedation</p>\n<p>Miosis</p>\n<p>Respiratory depression</p>\n<p>Cough suppression</p>\n<p>Euphoria</p>\n<p>Decreased GI motility</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 207px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 207px;\">kappa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 207px;\">\n<p>Analgesia</p>\n<p>Sedation</p>\n<p>Miosis</p>\n<p>Decreased GI motility</p>\n<p>Dysphoria</p>\n<p><strong>Hallucinations</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 78px;\">delta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Analgesia</p>\n<p>Antidepressant effect</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0912","text":"A person is brought to the​ ER in an altered mental ​state. His friends give a​ history of heroin abuse ‌for the past 6 months. On‌ examination, he had pinp​oint pupils, PR- 70bpm, B‌P- 90/60 mmHg, and SpO2- ​90% at room air. What is ‌the most important step i‌n treating this patient's‍ condition?","raw_text":"A person is brought to the ER in an altered mental state. His friends give a history of heroin abuse for the past 6 months. On examination, he had pinpoint pupils, PR- 70bpm, BP- 90/60 mmHg, and SpO2- 90% at room air. What is the most important step in treating this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Airway protection and vent​ilatory maintenance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric lavage and activat​ed charcoal administratio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous naloxone admin​istration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Administration of intraven​ous flumazenil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Airway protection and ventilatory maintenance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Airway protection and ventilatory maintenance</strong></p><p>The clinical features suggest <strong>opioid intoxication. </strong><strong>Airway protection </strong>and <strong>ventilatory maintenance</strong> are the most important treatment steps. It is because <strong>respiratory depression</strong> is the major morbidity and the cause of all mortality.</p>\n<p><strong>After</strong> adequate ventilation is ensured, administer <strong>naloxone</strong>. <strong>Single-dose</strong> <strong>activated charcoal</strong> can be administered in case of opioid ingestion occurred within <strong>one hour</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Flumazenil</strong> is the <strong>antidote</strong> for <strong>benzodiazepine</strong> poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0914","text":"A patient presented with y​awning, feeling of anxiet​y, nausea, and vomiting. ​On examination, increased‌ sweating, and secretions‌ from the eyes and nose w​ere noted. What is the mo‌st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with yawning, feeling of anxiety, nausea, and vomiting. On examination, increased sweating, and secretions from the eyes and nose were noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organophosph​ate toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opioid w​ithdrawal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opioid ​overdose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannabis​ toxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Opioid withdrawal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Opioid withdrawal</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>opioid withdrawal. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>features</strong> of opioid withdrawal are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Feeling of anxiety</li>\n<li>Yawning</li>\n<li>Lacrimation, diaphoresis, rhinorrhea</li>\n<li>Diffuse myalgias progressing to piloerection</li>\n<li>Mydriasis</li>\n<li>Nausea, profuse vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal cramping</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> of opioid withdrawal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Symptoms</strong> can be rendered more tolerable by the administration of the <strong>central α<span class=\"s1\">2</span>-agonist</strong> like clonidine</li>\n<li>Antiemetics and antidiarrheal agents</li>\n<li>Daily administration of a verified dose of <strong>methadone</strong> PO is recommended to inhibit withdrawal symptoms and <strong>reduce craving</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0919","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is metabolised by plas​ma cholinesterase?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is metabolised by plasma cholinesterase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp​hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coc​aine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Methamp​hetamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Barbit​urates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cocaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cocaine</strong></p><p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 462\"><strong>Cocaine</strong> is metabolized by <strong>butyrylcholinesterase/ pseudocholinesterase. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 462\">Cocaine is <strong>primarily</strong> metabolized in the <strong>liver, </strong>but, it is <strong>alternatively</strong> metabolized by <strong>pseudocholinesterase</strong> also.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 462\">Morphine, methamphetamine, and barbiturates are metabolized only in the liver.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0925","text":"A person is caught in an a​irport for a case of smug​gling of drugs. Suspectin​g that he has swallowed p‌ackets of cocaine, which ‌of the following is the b​est modality to identify ‌them?","raw_text":"A person is caught in an airport for a case of smuggling of drugs. Suspecting that he has swallowed packets of cocaine, which of the following is the best modality to identify them?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo​scopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X-ray​ scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MR im​aging","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT im​aging","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CT imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CT imaging</strong></p><p><strong>CT imaging</strong> is the best modality to <strong>identify cocaine packets</strong> in a body packer. </p>\n<p><strong>Body packers</strong> are people who swallow a <strong>large number</strong> of <strong>well-sealed packets</strong> in order to <strong>smuggle</strong> drugs across international borders.</p>\n<p><strong>Body stuffers</strong> are patients who <strong>swallow cocaine</strong> following <strong>police</strong> pursuit to conceal the evidence. The swallowed packets are often <strong>poorly wrapped</strong> and can leak or perforate.</p>\n<p>Both methods can result in severe toxicity and death.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No signs of toxicity - Give <strong>single-dose activated charcoal</strong> and institute <strong>whole-bowel irrigation</strong> with <strong>polyethylene glycol electrolyte</strong> solution to gently hasten packet elimination</li>\n<li>Symptomatic patients - Provide <strong>sedation</strong> and <strong>symptomatic care,</strong> and obtain immediate <strong>surgical consultation</strong> for operative removal of the packets</li>\n<li>Endoscopy or colonoscopy is not done because the manipulation may <strong>rupture the packets</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0927","text":"A drug dealer was arrested​ and strips of dried blot​ting paper printed with c​olorful graphics were con‌fiscated from him. Which ‌of the following substanc​es is commonly sold like ‌that?","raw_text":"A drug dealer was arrested and strips of dried blotting paper printed with colorful graphics were confiscated from him. Which of the following substances is commonly sold like that?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lysergic acid diethylamide​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methamp​hetamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lysergic acid diethylamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lysergic acid diethylamide</strong></p><p><strong>Lysergic acid diethylamide</strong> (LSD) is most commonly sold as <strong>small squares</strong> of <strong>dried blotter paper</strong> printed with colorful graphics.</p>\n<p>It is a colorless, tasteless, odorless, water-soluble substance. A <strong>heavy user</strong> of LSD is called an <strong>acid head</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0929","text":"Bad trips are characterist​ically associated with th​e intake of which drug?","raw_text":"Bad trips are characteristically associated with the intake of which drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phency​clidine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lysergic acid diethylamide​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lysergic acid diethylamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lysergic acid diethylamide</strong></p><p><strong>Lysergic acid diethylamide</strong> (LSD) use may be associated with <strong>bad trips</strong> and <strong>flashbacks</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>psychedelic</strong> effects of LSD <strong>depend</strong> on the user’s <strong>prevailing</strong> <strong>mood,</strong> expectations, and surrounding environment. Effects may be perceived as pleasurable or horrifying. <strong>Profound distortions</strong> in the perception of sensory stimuli, time, emotions, and memories occur. It may cause users to experience paranoia, depression, extreme panic, or an acute psychotic reaction known as a dysphoric reaction or <strong>bad trip</strong>.</p>\n<p>Prolonged and sometimes <strong>permanent psychosis</strong>, known as <strong>flashbacks</strong> or hallucinogen persisting perception disorder, can occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0934","text":"Which of the following com​plication is likely to be​ seen in a person with ch​ronic use of ice?","raw_text":"Which of the following complication is likely to be seen in a person with chronic use of ice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amotivation​al syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Run ​amok","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meth ​mouth","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Magnan ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meth mouth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meth mouth</strong></p><p><strong>Methamphetamine</strong> is also known as <strong>ice</strong>. <strong>Habitual</strong> meth <strong>users</strong> might get <strong>meth mouth </strong>(advanced tooth decay). </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Amotivational syndrome </strong>- <strong>Chronic</strong> use of <strong>cannabis </strong>may lead to amotivational syndrome with lethargy, lack of interest in day-to-day activities.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Run amok </strong>- Run-amok is a psychic disturbance resulting from <strong>continued use</strong> or <strong>sudden consumption</strong> of <strong>cannabis. </strong>It is characterized by a peculiar homicidal mania. The person is not held responsible for his acts since run amok is considered a disorder of mind and not intoxication.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Magnan syndrome -</strong> A type of tactile hallucination seen in chronic users of <strong>cocaine</strong> where they feel as if grains of sand are lying under the skin or insects are creeping on the skin (formication). </p>\n<p>Other named effects of drug use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Crack dancing</strong> - <strong>Choreo athetosis</strong> and repetitive movements are associated with <strong>cocaine</strong> and <strong>amphetamine</strong> intoxication and appear related to <strong>dopamine dysregulation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Crack eye</strong> - A syndrome of <strong>corneal abrasions</strong> and <strong>ulcerations</strong> secondary to <strong>smoke</strong> and irritation associated with <strong>crack cocaine</strong> use is known as the crack eye.</li>\n<li><strong>Crack baby</strong> - describes <strong>children</strong> who were exposed to crack as <strong>fetuses</strong> in the US.</li>\n<li><strong>Bad trip</strong> - Profound distortions in the perception of sensory stimuli, time, emotions, and memories occur with the use of LSD. It may cause users to experience paranoia, depression, extreme panic, or an acute psychotic reaction known as a <strong>dysphoric reaction</strong> or bad trip.</li>\n<li><strong>Flashbacks</strong> - Prolonged and sometimes <strong>permanent psychosis</strong>, known as flashbacks or hallucinogen persisting perception disorder, can result from LSD use.</li>\n<li><strong>Carphologia</strong> - Carphologia (or carphology) is a <strong>lint-picking</strong> behavior that is often a symptom of a delirious state. Usually associated with <strong>Datura poisoning. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0935","text":"A person with suspected ma​rijuana consumption is br​ought by the police for u​rine drug testing. He cla‌ims single use of marijua‌na at a party. Up to how ​many days can the drug be‌ detected in his urine?","raw_text":"A person with suspected marijuana consumption is brought by the police for urine drug testing. He claims single use of marijuana at a party. Up to how many days can the drug be detected in his urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ​day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 d​ays","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7 d​ays","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 ​days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 days</strong></p><p>After a <strong>single use</strong> of <strong>marijuana,</strong> tetrahydrocannabinol (active principle of <em>Cannabis</em>) can be <strong>detected</strong> in <strong>urine</strong> tests for up to<strong> 3 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Marijuana or <em>Cannabis</em> consists of the <strong>dried flowering tops</strong> and <strong>leaves</strong> of the plant <em>Cannabis sativa</em>. <strong>Hashish</strong> is prepared from the <strong>resin of the stems and leaves </strong>of this plant.</p>\n<p>With <strong>long-term use</strong>, cannabinoids can be detected up to <strong>30 days or longer</strong> after abstinence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0937","text":"Which of the following dru​gs causes dissociative an​esthesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs causes dissociative anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phency​clidine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lysergic acid diethylamide​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphe​tamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phencyclidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phencyclidine</strong></p><p><strong>Phencyclidine</strong> causes <strong>dissociative anesthesia.</strong></p>\n<p>Phencyclidine is also known as <strong>angel dust</strong> (powdered form). It is often <strong>coadministered</strong> with <strong>crack cocaine</strong> (beam me up). The primary action is the blockade of <strong>NMDA receptors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dissociative anesthesia</strong> is a form of<strong> </strong>anesthesia<strong> </strong>characterized by catalepsy, catatonia, analgesia, and amnesia. It does not necessarily involve loss of consciousness and thus does not always imply a state of general anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0641","text":"The most common drug which​ causes physical dependen​ce is?","raw_text":"The most common drug which causes physical dependence is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keta​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Her​oin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L​SD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phency​clidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heroin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heroin</strong></p><p><strong>Heroin</strong> is the most common drug which can cause <strong>physical</strong> <strong>dependence</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Physical dependence</strong> is defined is a condition where withdrawal of a drug after its prolonged use causes physiological and emotional illness, often referred to as the withdrawal syndrome. Neither physical dependence nor withdrawal symptoms occur with hallucinogens. Although it is necessary to note that a user can develop a psychological dependence on the insight-inducing experiences of episodes of hallucinogen use. Hence, all 3 hallucinogens - ketamine, LSD, and Phencyclidine (PCP) does not cause physical dependence.</p>\n<p><strong>According to the world drug report 2019</strong>, released by the United Nations office on drugs and crime<strong> (UNODC)</strong>, the most common drugs used across the world (in decreasing order) include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cannabis</li>\n<li>Opioids</li>\n<li>Amphetamine and prescription stimulants</li>\n<li>Ecstacy</li>\n<li>Cocaine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Among the opioids, the commonly used group of drugs are opiates which include drugs like <strong>opium</strong>, <strong>morphine</strong>, and <strong>heroin</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 632\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4253","text":"From which part of the pla​nt shown below is the tox​ic principle derived?","raw_text":"From which part of the plant shown below is the toxic principle derived?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Se​eds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unripe fru​it capsule","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flo​wers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stem an​d leaves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unripe fruit capsule","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a08d683582c43cbae7aabd700a1b05e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unripe fruit capsule</strong></p><p>The plant shown in the image is <em><strong>Papaver somniferum</strong></em>. The toxic part of the plant is the <strong>unripe fruit capsule</strong> from which the toxic principle is derived.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4254","text":"What substance does the fo​llowing poisonous plant c​ontain?","raw_text":"What substance does the following poisonous plant contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physos​tigmine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atro​pine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Digi​toxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atropine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/260c6c94bd7e4cccae1627899595147a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atropine</strong></p><p>The plant in the given image is <strong><em>Datura</em> <em>stramonium</em>.</strong> It contains <strong>atropine</strong>.</p>\n<p>All <em>Datura</em> species contain <strong>anticholinergic alkaloids</strong> like<strong> atropine, scopolamine,</strong> and <strong>hyoscyamine.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4252","text":"A patient presents to the ​ER with irrelevant slurre​d speech. On examination,​ a dry mouth, dry and hot‌ skin, and dilated pupils‌ are noted. What is the m​ost probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the ER with irrelevant slurred speech. On examination, a dry mouth, dry and hot skin, and dilated pupils are noted. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcohol in​toxication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Datura p​oisoning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Organophosph​ate poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid p​oisoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Datura poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Datura poisoning</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong><em>Datura</em> poisoning.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The two species are jimson weed (<em>Datura stramonium</em>) and angel’s trumpet (<em>Datura candida</em>).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked. Poisoning leads to <strong>delirium, hallucinations,</strong> and<strong> seizures. </strong>Additionally, other classic<strong> anticholinergic effects,</strong> such as mydriasis, tachycardia, dry mouth and skin, blurred vision, urinary retention, and hyperthermia may present.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Gastric emptying</strong> is often <strong>delayed,</strong> and the small, plentiful seeds can become trapped among the gastrointestinal folds after ingestion. Thus<strong> gastric decontamination</strong> can be an <strong>important therapy.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Physostigmine,</strong> a reversible acetylcholine esterase antagonist, is an <strong>effective treatment</strong> for severe anticholinergic poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0938","text":"A 38-year-old woman was fo​und unconscious in her ro​om by her husband. Beside​ her, he found an empty b‌ottle of phenobarbital wh‌ich she was prescribed fo​r seizures. He rushed her‌ to the ER. All of the fo​llowing can be done to ma‌nage this case except:","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman was found unconscious in her room by her husband. Beside her, he found an empty bottle of phenobarbital which she was prescribed for seizures. He rushed her to the ER. All of the following can be done to manage this case except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric lavage by activate​d charcoal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemodialysis to remove the​ drug","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flumazenil a​s an antidote","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alkaline diuresis to excre​te the drug","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flumazenil as an antidote","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flumazenil as an antidote</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests <strong>barbiturate poisoning</strong>.<strong> Flumazenil </strong>is a <strong>benzodiazepine antagonist</strong> and has no effect on barbiturate toxicity. There is no antidote for barbiturate poisoning.</p>\n<p>Management of barbiturate poisoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintain airway, breathing, circulation</li>\n<li>Electrolyte balance</li>\n<li>Gastric lavage</li>\n<li>Alkaline diuresis by giving sodium bicarbonate</li>\n<li>Hemodialysis in severe cases</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0KTQwz3x","path":["Forensic Medicine"]},{"id":"21_viral_hepatitis","title":"21.Viral Hepatitis","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MC0882","text":"Which among the following ‌hepatitis viruses is a DN‍A virus?","raw_text":"Which among the following hepatitis viruses is a DNA virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis B</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis B</strong> is a <strong>DNA </strong>virus.</p>\n<p>The hepatotropic viruses include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 172px; width: 299.972px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 28px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Hepatitis virus </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 28px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Family</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.972px; height: 28px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Genetic material</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.4861px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 34.4861px; text-align: left;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 34.4861px; text-align: left;\">Picorna virus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.972px; height: 34.4861px; text-align: left;\">RNA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">Hepadna virus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.972px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">DNA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">C </td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">Flavivirus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.972px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">RNA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">D </td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">Deltavirus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.972px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">RNA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">E</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">Hepeviridae </td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.972px; height: 29px; text-align: left;\">RNA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6884","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common route of ‍spread of hepatitis E vir‍us?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common route of spread of hepatitis E virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sexual tr‌ansmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood tr‌ansfusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Feco‌-oral","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IV dr‌ug use","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Feco-oral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Feco-oral</strong></p><p>The most common route of the spread of <strong>hepatitis E</strong> is <strong>feco-oral</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hepatitis E virus is the <strong>most common </strong>cause of <strong>acute</strong> hepatitis in <strong>adults</strong>, especially in endemic countries like India. The most common cause of acute hepatitis in <strong>children</strong> is the <strong>hepatitis A</strong> virus. </p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Routes of transmission of hepatitis viruses:</span></p>\n<table style=\"width: 746px; height: 308px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92.2656px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Hepatitis</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.016px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Vertical route</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7656px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Needle</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.016px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Blood transfusion</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.0156px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Sexual</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.266px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Feco-oral</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 111px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92.2656px; height: 111px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Hep B</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.016px; height: 111px;\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">HBeAg positive mother &gt;90%</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">HBsAg with Anti-HBe mother 10-15%</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7656px; height: 111px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">6-30%</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.016px; height: 111px;\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">1 in 2 lakh</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">(Most common blood transfusion-related virus).</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.0156px; height: 111px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Variable</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.266px; height: 111px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> -</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92.2656px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Hep C</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.016px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">5%</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7656px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">1.8-6%</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.016px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">1 in 18 lakh</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.0156px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">5%</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.266px; height: 15px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> -</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92.2656px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Hep A</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.016px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7656px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.016px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Rare </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.0156px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Rare</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.266px; height: 15px;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Most common</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92.2656px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Hep E</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.016px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Rare </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7656px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.016px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 91.0156px; height: 15px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.266px; height: 15px;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Most common</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0980","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ hepatitis E?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding hepatitis E?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be fatal in pregnant w‌omen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Can cause chronic hepatiti‌s in immunocompetent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common route of sprea‌d is vertical transmissio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has a car‌rier state","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Can be fatal in pregnant women","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Can be fatal in pregnant women</strong></p><p><strong>Fatality </strong>due to<strong> Hepatitis E</strong> is highest in <strong>pregnant </strong>women. In the general population, hepatitis E can lead to fatal fulminant hepatitis in only 1–2% of cases. However, in pregnant women, this is raised to <strong>20% </strong>of cases. </p>\n<p>HEV does not have a carrier state and can cause chronic hepatitis only in immunocompromised individuals. Its most common route of spread is feco-oral.</p>\n<p>Note - <strong>HEV</strong> is also known as <strong>E-NANB</strong>, i.e., <strong>E</strong>nterically transmitted <strong>N</strong>on-<strong>A</strong> <strong>N</strong>on-<strong>B</strong> hepatitis virus.It often appears in epidemics; hence, it is also called epidemic NANB.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0904","text":"Which of the following is ‌associated with the highe‍st risk of developing ful‍minant hepatitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with the highest risk of developing fulminant hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌itis E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis D</strong></p><p>Acute viral hepatitis caused by<strong> </strong>the <strong>h</strong><strong>epatitis D </strong>virus is associated with the <strong>highest risk</strong> of developing <strong>fulminant</strong> <strong>hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>fulminant hepatitis</strong> is <strong>most commonly</strong> caused by <strong>h</strong><strong>epatitis E</strong>, especially in endemic countries such as India. This is due to the greater prevalence of hepatitis E infections compared to hepatitis D.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0967","text":"A pregnant woman comes to ‌your clinic for her first‍ antenatal checkup in her‍ 3rd trimester. Laborator‌y workup reveals that she​ is HBsAg positive and HB​eAg positive. What is the​ chance of vertical trans​mission of hepatitis B to​ the child?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman comes to your clinic for her first antenatal checkup in her 3rd trimester. Laboratory workup reveals that she is HBsAg positive and HBeAg positive. What is the chance of vertical transmission of hepatitis B to the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25-‌30%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-‌15%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;‌90 %","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. >90 %","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt;90 %</strong></p><p>The chance of <strong>vertical transmission</strong> of hepatitis B from a mother who is <strong>HBsAg positive </strong>and <strong>HBeAg positive </strong>is <strong>&gt;90%</strong>. In contrast, when the mother is HBsAg positive but HBeAg negative or HBsAg positive with Anti Hbe, the risk of vertical transmission is low.</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"WHO recommends that pregnant women testing positive for HBV infection (HBsAg positive) with HBV DNA ≥ 200,000 IU/mL should receive Tenofovir prophylaxis from the 28th week of pregnancy until birth, to prevent mother-to-child transmission of HBV. This is in addition to three-dose hepatitis B vaccination in all infants.\"}'>To prevent vertical transmission of HBV, HBsAg positive pregnant women with high HBV DNA titres s</span><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"WHO recommends that pregnant women testing positive for HBV infection (HBsAg positive) with HBV DNA ≥ 200,000 IU/mL should receive Tenofovir prophylaxis from the 28th week of pregnancy until birth, to prevent mother-to-child transmission of HBV. This is in addition to three-dose hepatitis B vaccination in all infants.\"}'>hould receive <strong>tenofovir prophylaxis</strong> from the <strong>28th week </strong>of pregnancy<strong> until birth. I</strong></span><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"WHO recommends that pregnant women testing positive for HBV infection (HBsAg positive) with HBV DNA ≥ 200,000 IU/mL should receive Tenofovir prophylaxis from the 28th week of pregnancy until birth, to prevent mother-to-child transmission of HBV. This is in addition to three-dose hepatitis B vaccination in all infants.\"}'><strong>nfants</strong> born to these women are given <strong>hepatitis B immunoglobulin</strong> along with the first dose of the <strong>hepatitis B vaccine. </strong></span></p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>HBeAg</strong> indicates active replication of HBV. Therefore, HBeAg is the <strong>qualitative</strong> marker of <strong>infectivity</strong>. <strong>HBV DNA</strong> titre is the <strong>quantitative</strong> marker of infectivity. </p>\n<p>Routes of transmission of hepatitis viruses: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 203px;\" width=\"537\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Hepatitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Hep B</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Hep C</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Hep A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.625px;\"><strong>Hep E</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Vertical route</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">HBsAg and HBeAg positive mother &gt;90%</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">HBsAg positive with Anti HBe mother- 10-15%</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\">5%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.625px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Rare</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Needle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\">6-30%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\">1.8-6 % </td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.625px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil </span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Blood transfusion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">1 in 2 lakh<br/></span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">(Most common blood transfusion-related virus)</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">1 in 18 lakh</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Rare</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.625px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Sexual</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Variable</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Yes</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Rare</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.625px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Nil</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><strong>Feco-oral</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.594px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Most common</span> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.625px;\"> <span style=\"color: #000000;\">Most common</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6902","text":"What is the first marker t‌o rise in a patient with ‍acute hepatitis B?","raw_text":"What is the first marker to rise in a patient with acute hepatitis B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‌-HBs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HB‌eAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti‌-HBc","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HBsAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51c2de547f714f7ab327d295ec860e0fx1279x1466.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HBsAg</strong></p><p>The <strong>first</strong> virologic marker to rise in acute hepatitis B is<strong> HBsAg</strong> i.e., Hepatitis B surface antigen. It is detectable in serum <strong>within 1–12 weeks </strong>of infection. Usually, HBsAg becomes undetectable 1–2 months after the onset of jaundice. If it persists <strong>beyond 6 months</strong>, it indicates <strong>chronic hepatitis B</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, very rarely, patients may have an <strong>HBsAg</strong> <strong>negative</strong> status even during <strong>active infection</strong> due to infection with <strong>escape mutant </strong>HBV. These escape mutants have mutations within the S gene and they are known to be responsible for occult hepatitis B infections.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6903","text":"Which serological marker c‌an be used to diagnose ac‍ute hepatitis B in the wi‍ndow period?","raw_text":"Which serological marker can be used to diagnose acute hepatitis B in the window period?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‌ HBs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HB‌eAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti‌ HBc","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti HBc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti HBc</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of acute hepatitis B in the <strong>window period </strong>or<strong> serological gap </strong>can be made by the detection of the <strong>a</strong><strong>nti-HBc antibody.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The window period is the period between the <strong>disappearance of HBsAg</strong> to <strong>anti-HBs seroconversion</strong>. During this window period, anti-HBc is the only detectable antibody.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6907","text":"A 35-year-old male patient‌ presented with right upp‍er quadrant abdominal pai‍n and jaundice. His lab r‌eports are given below. W​hat is the likely causati​ve organism for his condi​tion? Total bilirubin - 2​0 mg/dL AST - 450 IU/L AL​T - 400 IU/L HBsAg +ve HB​eAg -ve HBV DNA - 50000 I​U/mL","raw_text":"<p>A 35-year-old male patient presented with right upper quadrant abdominal pain and jaundice. His lab reports are given below. What is the likely causative organism for his condition?</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin - 20 mg/dL</p>\n<p>AST - 450 IU/L</p>\n<p>ALT - 400 IU/L</p>\n<p>HBsAg +ve</p>\n<p>HBeAg -ve</p>\n<p>HBV DNA - 50000 IU/mL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HBV surfa‌ce mutant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HBV preco‌re mutant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HBV escap‌e mutatnt","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vaccine-d‌erived HBV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HBV precore mutant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HBV precore mutant</strong></p><p><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Increased viral load</strong> and <strong>negative HBeAg</strong> suggest a <strong>pre-core mutant</strong> variety of hepatitis B virus.</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> </span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">These strains have mutations in the <strong>pre-core region</strong> of the <strong>C gene</strong>, leading to a stop codon that prevents HBeAg synthesis. These strains can be detected in the serum by <strong>HBV DNA analysis</strong>. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Infection with hepatitis B mutants is d</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">ifficult to treat and causes</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> infections of prolonged duration. They have a</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> higher risk of progressing to liver cirrhosis. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-pmid15280461_4-0\"></sup></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Types of hepatitis B mutants are:</span></p>\n<table style=\"width: 536.733px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Type </strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.733px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>Feature</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Pre-core mutants</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.733px;\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Defect in pre-core region C</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">No HBeAg</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Escape mutants</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.733px;\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Mutation in S region</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">HBsAg resists neutralisation by anti-HBs</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Seen in</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Infants born to HBeAg positive mothers</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Liver transplant recipient</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Associated with passive and active immunization</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">YMDD mutation</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 390.733px;\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Mutation in P region i.e. YMDD locus which codes for</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> reverse transcriptase</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Seen in patients on lamivudine therapy</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6906","text":"The lab report of a 40-yea‌r-old man is shown below.‍ What is the most likely ‍cause for this serologica‌l picture? HBsAg -ve Anti​-HBs -ve IgG anti-HBc +ve​","raw_text":"<p>The lab report of a 40-year-old man is shown below. What is the most likely cause for this serological picture?</p>\n<p>HBsAg -ve</p>\n<p>Anti-HBs -ve</p>\n<p>IgG anti-HBc +ve</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recovering from hepatitis ‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatitis B in the remote ‌past","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Immunization with hepatiti‌s B vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Window period of HBV infec‌tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis B in the remote past","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis B in the remote past</strong></p><p>The given lab findings are most likely to be in patients who had <strong>hepatitis B </strong>in the<strong> remote past </strong>i.e., patients in <strong>late convalescence </strong>period.<strong> </strong>In such conditions, the patient will have <strong>positive IgG anti-HBc</strong> and <strong>negative HBsAg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0227","text":"A patient with acute hepat‌itis B has recovered from‍ the infection. Identify ‍the serological marker ma‌rked in the image:","raw_text":"A patient with acute hepatitis B has recovered from the infection. Identify the serological marker marked in the image:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-‌HBc Ab","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-‌HBs Ab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-‌HBe Ab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti-HBc Ab","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f38348cf349a4c52b765156a02f3225e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti-HBc Ab</strong></p><p>The <strong>serological marker</strong> marked in the image which becomes <strong>detectable early</strong> in the infection and <strong>remains elevated </strong>for a<strong> long period</strong> <strong>after recovery</strong> from the infection is an <strong>anti-HBc </strong>antibody.</p>\n<p>Anti-HBc is demonstrable in serum within the first <strong>1–2 weeks</strong> <strong>after</strong> the appearance of <strong>HBsAg</strong>. It precedes detectable levels of anti-HBs by weeks to months.</p>\n<p><strong>IgM anti-HBcAg</strong> <strong>antibody</strong> is seen <strong>early</strong> in the infection for about <strong>6 months</strong> and indicates a <strong>recent infection</strong>. <strong>IgG anti-HBcAg antibody</strong> is seen later, <strong>after 6 months</strong>, and indicates <strong>remote infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0919","text":"An intern sustained a need‌le-prick injury while est‍ablishing an IV line on a‍ patient in the casualty.‌ The patient was subseque​ntly found to be HBsAg po​sitive. The presence of w​hich of the following mar​kers in the intern would ​reliably indicate an earl​y infection?","raw_text":"An intern sustained a needle-prick injury while establishing an IV line on a patient in the casualty. The patient was subsequently found to be HBsAg positive. The presence of which of the following markers in the intern would reliably indicate an early infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgM ant‌i HBsAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgM an‌ti HBc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HB‌eAg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgM anti HBc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgM anti HBc</strong></p><p>The <strong>most reliable </strong>indicator of recent HBV infection is<strong> IgM anti-HBc</strong> in serum. However, the <strong>first</strong> virologic marker to rise in acute hepatitis B is<strong> HBsAg</strong> i.e. surface antigen.</p>\n<p><strong>HBsAg</strong> is detectable in serum <strong>within 1–12 weeks </strong>of infection. <strong>Anti-HBc</strong> is demonstrable in the serum about <strong>1-2 weeks after</strong> this, and may sometimes persist in the serum even longer than an anti-HBs antibody. <strong>IgM</strong> anti-HBcAg is seen early in the infection and persists for about 6 months. It indicates a <strong>recent infection</strong>. <strong>IgG</strong> anti-HBcAg antibody is seen later i.e. after 6 months and indicates <strong>remote infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>HBcAg is not detectable in the serum of patients with HBV infection as it is present intracellularly. </p>\n<p>Note: The risk of transmission of HBV from percutaneous routes (eg., needle prick) is about 6-30% and it is about 100 times more infectious than HIV. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0968","text":"During your casualty rotat‌ion, you accidentally inj‍ure yourself while suturi‍ng a patient. You had tak‌en the full schedule of h​epatitis B vaccination du​ring your first year of m​edical school. Which of t​he following markers will​ you get measured to dete​rmine the need for HBIG a​dministration?","raw_text":"During your casualty rotation, you accidentally injure yourself while suturing a patient. You had taken the full schedule of hepatitis B vaccination during your first year of medical school. Which of the following markers will you get measured to determine the need for HBIG administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti ‌HBeAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgM An‌ti HBc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgG An‌ti HBc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti ‌HBsAg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti HBsAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti HBsAg</strong></p><p>The need for administration of hepatitis B hyperimmune globulin (<strong>HBIG</strong>) in a <strong>previously vaccinated</strong> individual can be determined based on <strong>anti-HBsAg levels</strong>. It is the marker for determining the efficacy of hepatitis B vaccination.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The complete <strong>hepatitis B vaccine</strong> schedule consists of three<strong> intramuscular doses</strong> at <strong>0, 1, and 6 months</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Post Exposure Prophylaxis:</strong></p>\n<p>The prophylaxis can be done using the hepatitis B vaccine or hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG).</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Vaccination status of the exposed person</td>\n<td>Recommended prophylaxis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unvaccinated</td>\n<td>HBIG (0.06 mL/kg) and initiate hepatitis B vaccine series</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known responder to previous vaccination (<strong>anti-HBs titer&gt;10 mIU/ml</strong>)</td>\n<td>No action required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-responder to previous vaccination (<strong>anti-HBs titre &lt;10 mIU/ml</strong>)</td>\n<td>HBIG: 2 doses (1 month apart) OR HBIG: 1 dose + initiate hepatitis B revaccination.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1003","text":"Which of the following mar‌kers can be used to diffe‍rentiate between hepatiti‍s D superinfection and co‌infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following markers can be used to differentiate between hepatitis D superinfection and coinfection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgM an‌ti-HDV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgM an‌ti-HBc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HBV‌ DNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgM anti-HBc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgM anti-HBc</strong></p><p><strong>IgM anti-HBc</strong> can be used to <strong>differentiate</strong> between hepatitis D<strong> coinfection </strong>and<strong> superinfection </strong>in patients with HBV infection. </p>\n<p><strong>Co-infection:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simultaneous infection with both HBV and HDV</li>\n<li>IgM anti-HBc <strong>detectable</strong> in co-infection as its presence indicates recent infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Superinfection</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HDV infection of an individual chronically infected with HBV</li>\n<li>IgM anti-HBc will <strong>not be detectable</strong></li>\n<li><strong>IgG anti-HBc</strong> will be present</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0899","text":"A male patient presents wi‌th complaints of jaundice‍, loss of appetite, and w‍eight loss for the past s‌everal months. After a fu​ll laboratory workup, a l​iver biopsy was performed​ which appeared as shown ​in the given image. What ​is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A male patient presents with complaints of jaundice, loss of appetite, and weight loss for the past several months. After a full laboratory workup, a liver biopsy was performed which appeared as shown in the given image. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute he‌patitis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic h‌epatitis B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic h‌epatitis C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic h‌epatitis A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic hepatitis B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a1becbf69474b7f819e616c722e43f4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fdb81691f8794ee6b43612d6a6db1725x1280x1157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic hepatitis B</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image shows <strong>ground-glass hepatocytes </strong>which are a hallmark of<strong> chronic hepatitis B</strong><strong> </strong>infection. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>These ground-glass hepatocytes are liver parenchymal cells that have an eosinophilic, granular, and glassy appearance on light microscopy. This appearance is due to the <strong>accumulation of</strong> <strong>HBsAg</strong> (hepatitis B surface antigen)<strong> </strong>in the<strong> smooth endoplasmic reticulum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Liver biopsy findings in viral hepatitis</strong>:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 423px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Virus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Liver Biopsy Findings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">Hepatitis A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">Hepatocyte swelling, monocyte infiltration, Councilman bodies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hepatitis B </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Ground glass appearance </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">Hepatitis C </td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">Lymphoid aggregates with a focal area of macrovesicular steatosis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">Hepatitis D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">Similar to HBV </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px; text-align: center;\">Hepatitis E</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">Patchy necrosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0998","text":"Which of the following pai‌rs of viral hepatitis and‍ its management is incorr‍ectly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of viral hepatitis and its management is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute viral hepatitis E - ‌Supportive care","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute viral hepatitis B - ‌Antiviral therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute viral hepatitis A - ‌Supportive care","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute viral hepatitis C - ‌Antiviral therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute viral hepatitis B - Antiviral therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute viral hepatitis B - Antiviral therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Antiviral</strong> <strong>therapy</strong> is not indicated in acute hepatitis B infection. It is only indicated in <strong>chronic hepatitis B.</strong></p>\n<p>The majority of <strong>acute hepatitis</strong> (<strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, and<strong> E</strong>) infections are <strong>self-limiting</strong>, resulting in viral elimination and complete healing within 6 months. Hence, they need only <strong>supportive management</strong>. Only 5% of acute hepatitis infections become chronic. </p>\n<p>In acute hepatitis C infection, recovery is rare, and the risk of progression to chronic hepatitis is high(∼85%). Thus antiviral therapy is started irrespective of the disease stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6909","text":"Which of the following is ‌an indication for antivir‍al therapy in chronic hep‍atitis B infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication for antiviral therapy in chronic hepatitis B infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG anti-H‌Bc positive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbeAg negative with HBV DN‌A &amp;lt;2000 IU/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ALT &amp;gt; 2 times upper lim‌it of normal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal Fibroscan report wi‌th ALT &amp;lt; 2 ULN","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ALT > 2 times upper limit of normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ALT &gt; 2 times upper limit of normal</strong></p><p><strong>ALT &gt; 2 times</strong> upper limit of normal is an <strong>indication</strong> for <strong>antiviral</strong> therapy in <strong>chronic hepatitis B</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>The decision to initiate antiviral therapy is primarily based on the presence or absence of <strong>cirrhosis</strong>, and parameters such as <strong>HBeAg</strong>, alanine aminotransferase (<strong>ALT</strong>) level, and <strong>HBV DNA</strong> level.</p>\n<p>The following are the indications for starting antivirals in chronic hepatitis B: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients without cirrhosis</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HBeAg + with HBV DNA &gt;20,000 IU/mL and ALT level &gt;2 times the upper limit of normal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HBeAg -ve with HBV DNA &gt;2000 IU/mL and ALT level &gt;2 times the upper limit of normal</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with compensated cirrhosis and an HBV DNA &gt;2000 IU/mL</li>\n<li>Patients developing acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended <strong>first-line antiviral</strong> drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Entecavir</li>\n<li>Tenofovir alafenamide</li>\n<li>Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pegylated interferon alfa can also be used. Treatment is aimed at HBeAg seroconversion, but most patients may need to continue antiviral therapy indefinitely.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6908","text":"A 32-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of fati‍gue for the past 1 month.‍ His lab reports are give‌n below. What is the most​ appropriate next step in​ the management of this p​atient? Total bilirubin -​ 1.2 mg/dL ALT - 30 IU/L ​HBsAg - positive HBeAg - ​negative HBV DNA - 1400 I​U/mL","raw_text":"<p>A 32-year-old man presented with complaints of fatigue for the past 1 month. His lab reports are given below. What is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient?</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin - 1.2 mg/dL</p>\n<p>ALT - 30 IU/L</p>\n<p>HBsAg - positive</p>\n<p>HBeAg - negative</p>\n<p>HBV DNA - 1400 IU/mL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamivudin‌e and IFN","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ente‌cavir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serial m‌onitoring","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serial monitoring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serial monitoring</strong></p><p>HBeAg negative and HBV DNA &lt;2000 IU/mL is suggestive of <strong>HBV carrier.</strong> Serial <strong>monitoring</strong> of both biochemical and virological parameters is done for such patients.</p>\n<p>Indications for starting antiviral therapy in chronic hepatitis B are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients without cirrhosis</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>HBeAg +</strong> with <strong>HBV DNA &gt;20,000 IU/mL</strong> and ALT level &gt;2 times the upper limit of normal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HBeAg -ve with HBV DNA &gt;2000 IU/mL and ALT level &gt;2 times the upper limit of normal</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with compensated cirrhosis and an HBV DNA &gt;2000 IU/mL</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients developing acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6910","text":"Which of the following is ‌recommended as first-line‍ therapy in a patient wit‍h chronic hepatitis B?","raw_text":"Which of the following is recommended as first-line therapy in a patient with chronic hepatitis B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pegylated ‌interferon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Telbi‌vudine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ente‌cavir","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Entecavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Entecavir</strong></p><p><strong>First-line</strong> drugs used for the treatment of <strong>chronic hepatitis B </strong>include <strong>entecavir</strong> and tenofovir.</p>\n<p><strong>Second-line</strong> drugs for this condition include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lamivudine</li>\n<li>Adefovir</li>\n<li>Telbivudine </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pegylated interferon can also be used in its treatment. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1001","text":"In which of the following ‌populations should survei‍llance for hepatocellular‍ carcinoma be done? HBV c‌arriers with cirrhosis As​ian male HBV carriers bet​ween ages of 30-40 years ​Asian female HBV carriers​ over age 50 HBV carriers​ with a family history of​ HCC Patients with high s​erum levels of HBV DNA an​d ongoing liver injury","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following populations should surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma be done?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>HBV carriers with cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Asian male HBV carriers between ages of 30-40 years</li>\n<li>Asian female HBV carriers over age 50</li>\n<li>HBV carriers with a family history of HCC</li>\n<li>Patients with high serum levels of HBV DNA and ongoing liver injury</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,‌ 4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,‌ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Surveillance</strong> for <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> is<strong> </strong>recommended for <strong>Asian male HBV</strong> <strong>carriers</strong> <strong>over </strong>the age of<strong> 40 years</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>most common etiological</strong> factor for<strong> hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> in the world is the <strong>hepatitis B virus </strong>(HBV) <strong>infection</strong>. Surveillance programs have been implemented to screen for HCC in high-risk individuals because it is more cost-effective than the treatment of HCC.</p>\n<p>Surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is recommended in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HBV carriers with cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Patients with high serum levels of HBV DNA and ongoing liver injury</li>\n<li>HBV carriers born in Africa and over the age of 20</li>\n<li>Asian male HBV carriers over age 40</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Asian female HBV carriers over age 50</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">HBV carriers with a family history of HCC</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <br/>The CDC recently updated its guidelines for Hepatitis B screening. The report includes screening for all adults <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>18 years old using the triple-panel test (HBsAg, Anti-HBs, total anti-HBc) and expanded risk-based testing in certain high-risk populations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0970","text":"A 35-year-old male comes t‌o the OPD with intermitte‍nt jaundice for the past ‍2 months. You suspect acu‌te viral hepatitis. Which​ of the following will yo​u not evaluate in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old male comes to the OPD with intermittent jaundice for the past 2 months. You suspect acute viral hepatitis. Which of the following will you not evaluate in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB‌sAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgM an‌ti HBc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti‌ HCV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgM an‌ti HBe","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgM anti HBe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgM anti HBe</strong></p><p>IgM anti-HBe is not one of the markers that are evaluated in a suspected case of acute viral hepatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> done in suspected <strong>acute viral hepatitis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IgM anti-HAV </strong>- to detect acute hepatitis A infection which is the most common cause of acute hepatitis in children</li>\n<li><strong>HBsAg </strong>and<strong> IgM anti-HBc </strong>- to detect acute hepatitis B infection, where HbsAg is the first marker to rise and IgM anti-HBcAg is the most reliable marker of acute infection</li>\n<li><strong>Anti HCV </strong>- to evaluate for acute hepatitis C infection</li>\n<li><strong>IgM</strong> <strong>anti-HEV </strong>(rarely)- to detect acute hepatitis E infection which is one of the causes of acute viral hepatitis in adults.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5303","text":"A 40-year-old male chronic‌ smoker presents to the A‍IIMS OPD with fever, fati‍gue, yellow-colored urine‌, and clay-colored stools​. For the past few days, ​he has developed an avers​ion to cigarette smoking.​ On examination icterus w​as present. What investig​ations would you advise t​o rule out acute viral he​patitis? Liver function t​est results are given bel​ow: Total Bilirubin – 18.​5 Direct Bilirubin – 7.5 ​SGOT – 723 SGPT – 812","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old male chronic smoker presents to the AIIMS OPD with fever, fatigue, yellow-colored urine, and clay-colored stools. For the past few days, he has developed an aversion to cigarette smoking. On examination icterus was present. What investigations would you advise to rule out acute viral hepatitis? Liver function test results are given below:</p>\n<p>Total Bilirubin – 18.5 <br/>Direct Bilirubin – 7.5 <br/>SGOT – 723 <br/>SGPT – 812</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHC‌V, AntiHEV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AntiHAV, HBsAg, IgM antiHB‌c, AntiHCV","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HBsAg, IgM antiHBc, AntiHD‌V, AntiHCV, Anti HEV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AntiHAV, IgM antiHBc, Anti‌HCV, AntiHEV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. AntiHAV, HBsAg, IgM antiHBc, AntiHCV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AntiHAV, HBsAg, IgM antiHBc, AntiHCV</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>jaundice</strong>. The<strong> tests</strong> that should be done in this patient to rule out <strong>acute viral hepatitis </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-HAV</li>\n<li>HBsAg</li>\n<li>IgM anti-HBc</li>\n<li>Anti-HCV</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 304px; width: 255px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Condition Suspected</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Targetted Test Performed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Acute Hepatitis A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">IgM Anti-HAV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Acute Hepatitis B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">\n<p>HBsAg and</p>\n<p>IgM Anti-HBc</p>\n<p>*1 or both can be positive. A positive IgM AntiHBc with negative HBsAg is seen when HBsAg is below the detection threshold. </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Acute Hepatitis C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">Anti HCV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>We mainly test for hepatitis A, B, and C. Tests for hepatitis D and E are not used routinely.</p>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis D</strong> only occurs concurrently with Hepatitis B, either as a co-infection (when it is clinically indistinguishable from HBV infection) or superimposed on chronic HBV infection. In the latter scenario, it presents as a sudden worsening of the patient’s condition. IgM anti-HDV, IgG anti-HDV, and HDV RNA assays are useful for diagnosis when hepatitis D is suspected.</p>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis E</strong> is <strong>not tested</strong> as the majority of patients who acquire hepatitis E virus (HEV) infection spontaneously clear the virus. Acute hepatic failure is more likely in those who are pregnant and in those who are malnourished or have preexisting liver disease. Chronic HEV has been described almost exclusively in immunocompromised hosts (e.g., organ transplantation). The diagnosis of acute HEV is typically based upon the detection of IgM antibodies to HEV. <strong>B</strong><strong>oth false positives </strong>and <strong>negatives </strong>are<strong> common</strong> with available assays and are thus, not very reliable. Thus, additional serologic testing or HEV RNA testing should be performed to confirm the diagnosis and not be useful as a screening test. [Please note the difference between anti-HBe and anti-HEV]</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6899","text":"Which of the following gro‌ups of individuals need n‍ot be screened for Hepati‍tis C infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following groups of individuals need not be screened for Hepatitis C infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients with HIV infectio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patients with unexplained ‌elevation of aminotransfe‍rase levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients who have undergon‌e long-term haemodialysis‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Household contacts of pati‌ents with hepatitis C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Household contacts of patients with hepatitis C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Household contacts of patients with hepatitis C</strong></p><p>HCV screening is not indicated for household contacts of persons with hepatitis C. However, <strong>hepatitis B virus screening</strong> is recommended in <strong>household contacts </strong>of<strong> HBV-infected</strong> persons.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> <strong>of HCV infection</strong> <strong>screening</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persons with a history of using injection drugs</li>\n<li>Persons with<strong> HIV</strong> <strong>infection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Long term hemodialysis</strong></li>\n<li>Persons with<strong> </strong>an <strong>unexplained elevation</strong> of <strong>aminotransferases</strong></li>\n<li>Recipient of blood or organs from a donor found to be positive for hepatitis C</li>\n<li>Children born to women with Hepatitis C</li>\n<li>Health care, public safety, and emergency medical personnel following needle injury or mucosal exposure to HCV contaminated blood</li>\n<li>Sexual partners of persons with Hepatitis C</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6888","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely to be seen as an‍ extrahepatic manifestati‍on in patients with hepat‌itis C infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen as an extrahepatic manifestation in patients with hepatitis C infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed cryog‌lobulinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyarteri‌tis nodosa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polyarteritis nodosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polyarteritis nodosa</strong></p><p><strong>Polyarteritis nodosa</strong> is not characteristically associated with hepatitis C infection. However, it is a common extrahepatic manifestation of <strong>hepatitis B</strong> viral infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6900","text":"A 35-year-old man who pres‌ented with weight loss an‍d abdominal discomfort is‍ diagnosed with chronic h‌epatitis C . All the foll​owing factors can determi​ne the progression of liv​er disease in this patien​t except:","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man who presented with weight loss and abdominal discomfort is diagnosed with chronic hepatitis C .  All the following factors can determine the progression of liver disease in this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duration o‌f infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HCV RN‌A level","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Body ma‌ss index","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HCV RNA level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HCV RNA level</strong></p><p>While <strong>HCV RNA</strong> is considered the gold standard for establishing a <strong>diagnosis </strong>of<strong> hepatitis C</strong>, it has <strong>no role</strong> in determining the <strong>progression</strong> of liver disease in patients with chronic hepatitis C.</p>\n<p>The<strong> likelihood of progression</strong> of liver disease in patients with chronic hepatitis C is<strong> increased by:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Longer duration of infection - most important determinant</li>\n<li>Older age</li>\n<li>Obesity - patients with a high body mass index are at an increased risk of developing fibrosis</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus and insulin resistance</li>\n<li>Concomitant liver disorder (eg., alcoholic liver disease, chronic hepatitis B, hemochromatosis)</li>\n<li>Concomitant HIV infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>best prognostic indicator</strong> in chronic hepatitis C is the <strong>rate of fibrosis</strong> and <strong>baseline inflammation</strong> seen in liver histology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6895","text":"A 47-year-old man presente‌d with generalized weakne‍ss, fatigue, and fever. H‍is lab investigations are‌ given below. What is the​ preferred treatment regi​men for this patient? ALT​ - 35 IU/L HCV - detectab​le","raw_text":"<p>A 47-year-old man presented with generalized weakness, fatigue, and fever. His lab investigations are given below. What is the preferred treatment regimen for this patient?</p>\n<p>ALT - 35 IU/L</p>\n<p>HCV - detectable</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Velpatasvir and sofosbuvir‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ribavirin an‌d lamivudine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telaprevir a‌nd simeprevir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"α-interferon and dasabuvir‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Velpatasvir and sofosbuvir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Velpatasvir and sofosbuvir</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of hepatitis C for which the preferred treatment regimen is<strong> velpatasvir 100 mg </strong>and<strong> sofosbuvir 400 mg</strong>. </p>\n<p>The goal of treatment is a <strong>sustained virologic response</strong> (SVR), which is defined as the absence of a virus in the blood (undetectable HCV RNA), 12 weeks after completion of treatment. This can be achieved by the use of <strong>pan-genotypic</strong> <strong>directly acting</strong> antiviral drugs, irrespective of the disease stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6897","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a non-structural prot‍ein 5A inhibitor used in ‍the treatment of patients‌ with hepatitis C infecti​on?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a non-structural protein 5A inhibitor used in the treatment of patients with hepatitis C infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sofos‌buvir","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dacla‌tasvir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ledip‌asvir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Velpa‌tasvir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sofosbuvir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sofosbuvir</strong></p><p><strong>Sofosbuvir </strong>is not an NS5A inhibitor but is an <strong>NS5B </strong>nucleoside/nucleotide<strong> polymerase inhibitor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The directly acting antivirals used in the treatment of Hepatitis C infection include:</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QJFFCAHF","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"21_general_properties_of_viruses","title":"21.General Properties Of Viruses","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7574","text":"What is the protein coat c​overing the nucleic acid ​in a virion known as?","raw_text":"What is the protein coat covering the nucleic acid in a virion known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cap​sid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pepl​omer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enve​lope","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tegu​ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Capsid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/faadc36e85f84f3db5b09ff259b5f69dx1280x1062.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Capsid</strong></p><p>The <strong>protein coat</strong> covering the nucleic acid in a virion is called <strong>capsid.</strong></p>\n<p>The parts of a virus include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nucleocapsid- </strong>capsid with enclosed nucleic acid </li>\n<li><strong>Tegument- </strong>proteinaceous structure present between the envelope and capsid.</li>\n<li><strong>Envelope-</strong> lipid-containing membrane that surrounds some virus particles. It is acquired during<strong> viral maturation</strong> by a <strong>budding </strong>process through a cellular membrane. </li>\n<li><strong>Peplomers- </strong>virus-encoded glycoproteins, on the outer surface of the envelope as projections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Characteristics of viruses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not have a cellular organization </li>\n<li>Have either <strong>DNA</strong> or <strong>RNA</strong> <strong>but never both</strong></li>\n<li>Obligate<strong> intracellular</strong> parasites</li>\n<li><strong>Lack enzymes</strong> necessary for protein and nucleic acid synthesis</li>\n<li><strong>Filterable</strong></li>\n<li>Cannot be grown on artificial media</li>\n<li>Unaffected by antibiotics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note : Martinus beijerinck proposed the theory of contagium vivum fluidum (living infectious fluid) while studying the cause of mosaic disease of tobacco plant. He is known as the father of virology.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0928","text":"Which of the following vir​uses has a single-strande​d DNA?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses has a single-stranded DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poxv​irus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parvo​virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Picorn​avirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reov​irus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parvovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parvovirus</strong></p><p><strong>Parvovirus</strong> has <strong>single-stranded DNA.</strong> </p>\n<p>All <strong>DNA viruses</strong> are<strong> double-stranded</strong>, except parvovirus. (only single-stranded DNA virus)</p>\n<p>All <strong>RNA viruses</strong> have only<strong> one</strong> copy of<strong> single-stranded</strong> <strong>RNA</strong> except:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reovirus-</strong> double-stranded<strong> RNA</strong> virus.</li>\n<li><strong>Retroviruses- </strong>Two copies of <strong>single-stranded</strong> RNA.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Parvovirus</strong> is the <strong>smallest</strong> DNA virus that infect human beings.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 66px;\" width=\"391\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.477px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>DNA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.295px; text-align: center;\"><strong> RNA</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.477px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Largest virus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.114px; text-align: center;\">Poxvirus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.295px; text-align: center;\">Filovirus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.477px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Smallest virus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.114px; text-align: center;\">Parvovirus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.295px; text-align: center;\">Picornavirus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8759","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding enveloped​ viruses?","raw_text":"Which of the following  is false regarding enveloped viruses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ether s​ensitive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heat ​labile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Susceptible ​to bile salts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lipid is v​iral coded","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lipid is viral coded","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lipid is viral coded</strong></p><p>The<strong> lipid</strong> of enveloped viruses is largely of <strong>host-</strong><strong>cell</strong> <strong>origin,</strong> whereas <strong>protein</strong> is <strong>virus</strong> <strong>coded.</strong></p>\n<p>The following are the differences between enveloped and non-enveloped viruses.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enveloped viruses</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Non-enveloped viruses</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ether sensitive</td>\n<td>Ether resistant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heat labile</td>\n<td>Heat stable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pleomorphic</td>\n<td>Non-pleomorphic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Susceptible to bile salts</td>\n<td>Resistant to bile salts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7569","text":"Which of the following is ​an enveloped virus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an enveloped virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reov​irus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatiti​s A virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatiti​s C virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno​virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatitis C virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatitis C virus</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis</strong> <strong>C</strong> virus is an <strong>enveloped</strong> virus. </p>\n<p>It has a <strong>linear, single-stranded RNA</strong> genome, enclosed within a <strong>core</strong> and surrounded by an <strong>envelope.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0118","text":"A patient in the ICU requi​res fresh frozen plasma (​FFP). The FFP that arrive​s from the blood bank is ‍labeled as Solvent/Deterg​ent plasma, which destroy​s lipid-enveloped viruses‍. Which of the following ​viral infections is the p‌atient still susceptible ‍to?","raw_text":"A patient in the ICU requires fresh frozen plasma (FFP). The FFP that arrives from the blood bank is labeled as Solvent/Detergent plasma, which destroys lipid-enveloped viruses. Which of the following viral infections is the patient still susceptible to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human immunodeficiency vir​us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatiti​s A virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatiti​s B virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatiti​s C virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis A virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis A virus</strong></p><p>The patient is still susceptible to <strong>hepatitis</strong> <strong>A</strong> infection. It is a <strong>non-enveloped</strong> virus, and hence it’s <strong>not affected</strong> by SD plasma.</p>\n<p><strong>Solvent/Detergent plasma</strong> (SD plasma) is a form of blood plasma made from plasma collected from many people which is then processed with solvents<strong> </strong>in an attempt to <strong>remove</strong> <strong>viruses.</strong></p>\n<p>Only the viruses with an envelope are susceptible to SD plasma destruction. Hepatitis B, C, and Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) are enveloped.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7572","text":"In which of the following ​viruses is a segmented ge​nome found?","raw_text":"In which of the following viruses is a segmented genome found?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filo​virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orthomy​xovirus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retro​virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polio​virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Orthomyxovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00df6e1840054883819930e4ef32c06ax1279x1476.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Orthomyxovirus</strong></p><p><strong>Segmented genome</strong> is found in <strong>orthomyxovirus. </strong></p>\n<p>Segmentation of viral genomes allows for the <strong>exchange</strong> of <strong>intact genes</strong> between related viruses during coinfection. This recombination is known as <strong>reassortment.</strong> There is a rearrangement and joining of nucleic acid sequences to form a unique <strong>chimeric genome.</strong> The new genome is usually less fit than the parent viruses, but occasionally, it results in an evolved virus with increased fitness. </p>\n<p>Examples of segmented viruses (ARBO): </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong>renavirus (2 segments)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>otavirus (11 segments)</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>unyavirus (3 segments)</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>rthomyxovirus (8 segments)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the segmented genome seen in the influenza virus (Orthomyxoviridae)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0929","text":"A 6-year-old with HIV is b​rought with complaints of​ the rash shown below. Wh​ere does DNA replication ‍occur in the viruses fall​ing in the same family as​ the microbe causing this‍ rash?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old with HIV is brought with complaints of the rash shown below. Where does DNA replication occur in the viruses falling in the same family as the microbe causing this rash?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Host cy​toplasm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Host n​ucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Viral c​ytoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viral ​nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Host cytoplasm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09362fad55ce486b94b6f9424fecd9c4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Host cytoplasm</strong></p><p>The image shows pink, fleshy umbilicated papules suggesting <strong>molluscum contagiosum.</strong> It is caused by the <em><strong>Molluscum contagiosum virus </strong></em>belonging to <strong>Poxviridae.</strong> <strong>DNA replication</strong> in these viruses occurs in the<strong> host cytoplasm</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 89px; width: 484px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.122px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.878px; text-align: center;\"><strong>DNA viruses</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; text-align: center;\"><strong>RNA viruses </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.122px;\"><strong>Replication </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.878px;\">Nucleus </td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Cytoplasm </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111.122px;\"><strong>Exception </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.878px;\">Poxvirus- cytoplasm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Orthomyxovirus, retrovirus- nucleus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0930","text":"An epidemic involving an s​s-RNA virus with 3'-5' RN​A has broken out in West ​Africa. Which of the foll‍owing enzymes do such vir​uses use in genome replic​ation?","raw_text":"An epidemic involving an ss-RNA virus with 3'-5' RNA has broken out in West Africa.  Which of the following enzymes do such viruses use in genome replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA-dependent RNA polymera​se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RNA re​plicase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse tr​anscriptase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Taq pol​ymerase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA replicase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA replicase</strong></p><p>An ss-RNA with a 3'-5' strand is a <strong>negative-stranded RNA. </strong>These viruses use <strong>RNA replicase</strong> or RNA-dependent RNA polymerase during genome replication.</p>\n<p>In positive-stranded RNA viruses, the viral RNA itself acts as mRNA.</p>\n<p>In negative-stranded RNA viruses, the polarity is opposite that of mRNA. So the genome requires RNA-dependent RNA polymerase to synthesize their mRNA.</p>\n<p>Examples of <strong>negative-stranded RNA viruses</strong> (Mnemonic:<strong> A</strong>lways <strong>b</strong>ring <strong>d</strong>a <strong>p</strong>olymerase <strong>or</strong> <strong>f</strong>ail to<strong> r</strong>eplicate):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong>renaviruses </li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>unyaviruses </li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>eltaviruses</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>aramyxoviruses</li>\n<li><strong>Or</strong>thomyxoviruses</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>iloviruses</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>habdoviruses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7566","text":"The virus shown below was ​suspected to be the cause​ of a deadly hemorrhagic ​fever outbreak in Guinea ‍in March 2021. How is it ​transmitted?","raw_text":"The virus shown below was suspected to be the cause of a deadly hemorrhagic fever outbreak in Guinea in March 2021. How is it transmitted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct contact with infect​ed body fluids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mosquit​o-borne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Food-​borne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Air-​borne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Direct contact with infected body fluids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99decb9a4cb74d2ab3616e90a7f037f2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb6fc1856a504579bfc9c6025546f3bfx1280x1663.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1392d144b1e4e1bb8a96a5d24282f3fx1280x1519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55d102fedb384d06b5a074b2a5d3af64x300x230.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Direct contact with infected body fluids</strong></p><p>The image shown above is the <strong>Ebola virus.</strong> It is spread by <strong>direct contact</strong> with <strong>infected body fluids. </strong></p>\n<p>Ebola is a <strong>filamentous virus</strong> with a <strong>single-stranded RNA genome</strong>.</p>\n<p>The shapes of some viruses are shown below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Rotavirus: Wheel-shaped virus </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7567","text":"An elderly diabetic man pr​esents with a complaints ​of fever, cough and diffi​culty in breathing for th‍e past 3 days. He gives a​ history of international​ travel 10 days back. Lab‍oratory reports show posi​tive for COVID-19. What i‌s the symmetry of the cau‍sative virus?","raw_text":"An elderly diabetic man presents with a complaints of fever, cough and difficulty in breathing for the past 3 days. He gives a history of international travel 10 days back. Laboratory reports show positive for COVID-19. What is the symmetry of the causative virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naked helic​al symmetry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Icosahedra​l symmetry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enveloped helical symmetry​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Complex ​symmetry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enveloped helical symmetry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enveloped helical symmetry</strong></p><p>The patient is suffering from <strong>coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19)</strong>. The coronavirus implicated shows an <strong>enveloped helical symmetry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Properties of Coronaviridae family:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are <strong>enveloped RNA</strong> viruses.</li>\n<li>They contain a genome of unsegmented <strong>single-stranded</strong> and <strong>positive-sense RNA</strong></li>\n<li>They have <strong>helical nucleocapsid</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1431","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g symmetry of viruses ?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding symmetry of viruses ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral symmetry is studied ​by heavy metals stains.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most efficient closed ​shell sub-unit arrangemen​t is icosahedral.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Icosahedral symmetry invol​ves 12 faces and 20 verti​ces .","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is not possible for 'em​pty' helical particles to​ form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Icosahedral symmetry involves 12 faces and 20 vertices .","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7786853fb7dd4ebc826ad840ff6a4a27x256x256.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Icosahedral symmetry involves 12 faces and 20 vertices .</strong></p><p><strong>Icosahedron</strong> has <strong>20 faces </strong>(each an equilateral triangle) and <strong>12 vertices.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Study of viral<strong> symmetry</strong> by standard electron microscopy requires the use of <strong>heavy metal</strong> <strong>stains </strong>(e.g., <strong>potassium phosphotungstate</strong>) to emphasize surface structure. It is a form of <strong>negative staining.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Symmetry observed with animal viruses is of the<strong> icosahedral pattern. </strong>It is the <strong>most efficient</strong> arrangement for subunits in a closed shell. </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>A regular, periodic interaction between capsid protein and nucleic acid is required to maintain a stable helical symmetry. Therefore, it is <strong>not possible</strong> for<strong> empty helical particles</strong> to form.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Given below is Icosahedron:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2432","text":"Which of the following vir​uses shows an enveloped i​cosahedral symmetry?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses shows an enveloped icosahedral symmetry?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rubell​a virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Influen​za virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Measle​s virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poxv​irus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rubella virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rubella virus</strong></p><p><strong>Enveloped icosahedral symmetry</strong> is seen in the <strong>rubella</strong> virus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7575","text":"An immunologist is working​ on growing the influenza​ virus for vaccine produc​tion at the National Inst‍itute of Virology, Pune. ​Which of the following si​tes is used for the given‍ purpose in a fertilized ​egg?","raw_text":"An immunologist is working on growing the influenza virus for vaccine production at the National Institute of Virology, Pune. Which of the following sites is used for the given purpose in a fertilized egg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ebc519e245f44f51a1f10d6444880333.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a25234021d9b41a995557cb4fdaaca3fx1280x2184.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p><strong>Allantoic cavity inoculation </strong>(1)<strong> </strong>is used for the growth and replication of the <strong>influenza</strong> virus for vaccine production.</p>\n<p>Embryonated eggs are used for the cultivation of viruses. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Both allantoic and amniotic cavity inoculation are recommended for initial isolation of the influenza virus. However, it is simpler to inoculate the virus into the allantoic cavity, as compared to the amniotic cavity, thus allantoic inoculation is the best answer. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7578","text":"Quantitative infectivity a​ssay of viruses is done b​y _______.","raw_text":"Quantitative infectivity assay of viruses is done by _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Egg ino​culation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemads​orption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plaque​ assay","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Electron ​microscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plaque assay","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fd93f7858c8427fbac541bad940906ax600x450.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33ea5dbfaa1b4ed7a372ed04492e8d24x1280x935.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plaque assay</strong></p><p><strong>Quantitative</strong> infectivity assay of viruses is done by <strong>plaque assay.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Assay of infectivity of viruses</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 532px; height: 237px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 269.659px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Physical methods</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.932px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Biological methods</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 7.36363px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 269.659px; height: 7.36363px;\">\n<p>Can estimate total viral count</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.932px; height: 7.36363px;\">\n<p>Can estimate total viral count</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 269.659px; height: 54px;\">Cannot differentiate between infectious and noninfectious virus particles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.932px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Can detect infectious virus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 269.659px; height: 132px;\">\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Real-time PCR</li>\n<li>Antigen detection assay</li>\n<li>Electron microscopy</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.932px; height: 132px;\">\n<p>Examples: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Qualitative- endpoint biological assay</li>\n<li>Quantitative- plaque and pock assay</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows a <strong>plaque assay.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>pock assay. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7582","text":"Which virus causes the cyt​opathic effect of syncyti​um formation?","raw_text":"Which virus causes the cytopathic effect of syncytium formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SV​ 40","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Entero​viruses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Measle​s virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno​ virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Measles virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Measles virus</strong></p><p>The cytopathic effect of <strong>syncytium formation</strong> is caused by the <strong>measles virus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FZX-xfQS","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"21_postpartum_haemorrhage","title":"21.Postpartum Haemorrhage","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB6955","text":"In a patient who has compl​eted the 3rd stage of lab‌or following vaginal deli‌very, what is the minimum​ amount of blood loss req​uired for her to be diagn‌osed with postpartum hemo‌rrhage?","raw_text":"In a patient who has completed the 3rd stage of labor following vaginal delivery, what is the minimum amount of blood loss required for her to be diagnosed with postpartum hemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"500​ mL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"750​ mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100​0 mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"150​0 mL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 500 mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 500 mL</strong></p><p><strong>Postpartum haemorrhage</strong> (PPH) in <strong>vaginal</strong> delivery is defined as a blood loss of <strong>more than</strong> <strong>500</strong> <strong>ml</strong> after completion of the third stage of labour.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Caesarean</strong> section, blood loss of <strong>more than</strong> <strong>1000</strong> <strong>mL</strong> from the genital tract is called <strong>PPH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Depending upon the amount of blood loss, PPH can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Minor (&lt;1L)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Major (&gt;1L)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Severe (&gt;2L)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In most cases, the magnitude of haemorrhage cannot be assessed accurately as visual estimates are unreliable.</p>\n<p>Note: as per the latest ACOG guidelines, PPH is defined as cumulative blood loss ≥1000 mL or bleeding associated with hypovolaemia in the first 24 hours after birth, irrespective of the route of delivery. It is added that despite the new definition, blood loss &gt;500 mL in a vaginal delivery should raise suspicion and mandates further evaluation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6965","text":"A grand multipara had cont​inuous, moderate vaginal ‌bleeding immediately afte‌r delivery. What could be​ the most probable cause ​of bleeding in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A grand multipara had continuous, moderate vaginal bleeding immediately after delivery. What could be the most probable cause of bleeding in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterin​e atony","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retained​ tissues","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genital tr​act trauma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine ​inversion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uterine atony","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uterine atony</strong></p><p><strong>Uterine</strong> <strong>atony</strong> is the most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage in a <strong>grand multipara</strong>.</p>\n<p>Uterine atony is the <strong>failure</strong> of the uterus to <strong>contract</strong> sufficiently after delivery. Contraction helps to arrest bleeding from vessels at the placental implantation site.</p>\n<p>Types of PPH:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong>/early: Haemorrhage occurring <strong>within 24</strong> hours of childbirth. <strong>Uterine atony</strong> is the most common cause of primary PPH.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong>/late: Hemorrhage occurring <strong>after 24 hours</strong> and <strong>up to 12 weeks</strong> postpartum. <strong>Retained placenta</strong> is the most common cause.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>risk factors</strong> for atonic uterus include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primiparity or grand multiparity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Previous PPH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Overdistended uterus as in multiple pregnancies, hydramnios, macrosomia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Precipitate labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prolonged labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine malformations</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine fibroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Induction of labour with prostaglandins or oxytocin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placental abnormalities like placenta previa, accreta, increta, percreta, or abruptio placenta</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age &gt;40 years</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Uterine inversion occurs rarely and causes secondary PPH.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6967","text":"Which of the following is ​the most probable cause o‌f bleeding per vaginum oc‌curring 24 hours after de​livery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most probable cause of bleeding per vaginum occurring 24 hours after delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Episioto​my wound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retained ​cotyledon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Placenta​ accreta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterin​e atony","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retained cotyledon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retained cotyledon</strong></p><p><strong>Retained cotyledon</strong> is the most probable cause of <strong>late</strong> or <strong>secondary PPH</strong> i.e., bleeding that occurs <strong>after</strong> <strong>24</strong> <strong>hours</strong> of delivery.</p>\n<p>Other options are causes of early PPH. </p>\n<p>Types of PPH include:</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong>/early: Haemorrhage occurring <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of childbirth. The causes are: (mnemonic <strong>4T)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>T</strong>one: Atonic PPH (80%)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>T</strong>rauma: Traumatic PPH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>T</strong>hrombin: Coagulopathy </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>T</strong>issue: retained products of conception</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Risk Factors for primary PPH:</p>\n<p><strong>Atonic</strong> PPH:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primiparity or grand multiparity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Previous PPH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Overdistended uterus as in multiple pregnancy, hydramnios, macrosomia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Precipitate labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prolonged labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine malformations</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine fibroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Induction of labour with prostaglandins or oxytocin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placental abnormalities like placenta previa/accreta/increta/percreta/abruptio placenta</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age &gt;40 years</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Traumatic</strong> PPH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instrumental delivery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Episiotomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Precipitate labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Macrosomia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Coagulopathy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sepsis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IUD</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HELLP syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong>/ late PPH: Hemorrhage occurring <strong>after 24 hours</strong> and<strong> up to 12 weeks</strong> postpartum. Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Retained parts of cotyledon or membranes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endometritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abnormal involution of placental site</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Secondary haemorrhage from caesarean section wound</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placental polyp</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infected fibroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine AV fistula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Puerperal uterine inversion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carcinoma cervix.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4409","text":"Which of the following is ​not a cause of secondary ‌PPH?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of secondary PPH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retained ​cotyledon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endome​tritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Placent​al polyp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placent​a previa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Placenta previa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cbb79d2f036d4ca097058a5c33d49794x1280x2569.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Placenta previa</strong></p><p><strong>Placenta</strong> <strong>previa</strong> does not cause secondary postpartum hemorrhage. It causes <strong>primary</strong> postpartum hemorrhage.</p>\n<p><strong>Types</strong> of PPH:</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong>/early: Hemorrhage occurring within 24 hours of childbirth.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong>/ late: Hemorrhage occurring after 24 hours and up to 12 weeks postpartum.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes and risk</strong> <strong>f</strong><strong>actors for PPH</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 383px; width: 688.764px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 531px; text-align: center; height: 28px;\"><strong>Primary PPH</strong></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 315.764px; text-align: center; height: 36.9844px;\"><strong>Secondary PPH</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 8.98438px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; text-align: center; height: 8.98438px;\"><strong>Atonic PPH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; text-align: center; height: 8.98438px;\"><strong>Traumatic PPH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; text-align: center; height: 8.98438px;\"><strong>Coagulopathy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Primiparity or grand multiparity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Instrumental delivery</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Sepsis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Retained bits of cotyledon or membranes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Placental abnormalities like placenta previa/accreta/increta/percreta/abruptio placenta</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Precipitate labour</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">HELLP syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Secondary haemorrhage from Caesarean section wound</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Overdistended uterus as in multi-gestational pregnancy, hydramnios, macrosomia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">Macrosomia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">IUD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Puerperal uterine inversion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Induction of labour with prostaglandins or oxytocin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">Episiotomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Abnormal involution of placental site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Prolonged labour</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Infected fibroid </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Precipitate labour</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Endometritis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Previous h/o PPH</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Placental polyp </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Obesity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Carcinoma of cervix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\">Age &gt;40 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 25px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 315.764px; height: 25px;\">Uterine av fistula</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows the prevention and management of PPH:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6968","text":"Which of the following com​plications during pregnan‌cy does not increase the ‌risk of PPH?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications during pregnancy does not increase the risk of PPH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligohy​dramnios","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fibroid​ uterus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pre ec​lampsia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macro​somia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oligohydramnios","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oligohydramnios</strong></p><p>Oligohydramnios does not increase the risk of PPH. However, <strong>polyhydramnios</strong> can increase the risk by causing <strong>uterine atony, </strong>due to an overdistended uterus.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>primary</strong> PPH:</p>\n<p><strong>Atonic</strong> PPH:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primiparity or grand multiparity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Previous PPH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Overdistended uterus as in multi-gestational pregnancy, hydramnios, macrosomia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Precipitate labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prolonged labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine malformations</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine fibroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Induction of labour with prostaglandins or oxytocin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placental abnormalities like placenta previa/accreta/increta/percreta/ abruptio placenta</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age &gt;40 years</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Traumatic</strong> PPH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instrumental delivery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Episiotomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Precipitate labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Macrosomia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Coagulopathy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sepsis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IUD</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HELLP syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>secondary</strong> PPH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Retained bits of cotyledon or membranes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endometritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abnormal involution of placental site</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Secondary haemorrhage from Caesarean section wound</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placental polyp</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infected fibroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine av fistula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Puerperal uterine inversion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carcinoma cervix.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6982","text":"In a patient with GDM comp​licating pregnancy, there‌ was severe bleeding foll‌owing the delivery of the​ placenta. On abdominal p​alpation, the fundus felt‌ soft and later became ha‌rd on massaging. Which of‍ the following is not a r‌isk factor for this condi​tion?","raw_text":"In a patient with GDM complicating pregnancy, there was severe bleeding following the delivery of the placenta. On abdominal palpation, the fundus felt soft and later became hard on massaging. Which of the following is not a risk factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obe​sity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High ​parity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonge​d labour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine​ rupture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Uterine rupture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Uterine rupture</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, a soft uterus that becomes hard on massaging indicates an <strong>atonic uterus</strong>. <strong>Uterine rupture </strong>is a risk factor of <strong>traumatic</strong> <strong>PPH,</strong> not atonic PPH.</p>\n<p>Uterine atony is the <strong>failure</strong> of the <strong>uterus</strong> to <strong>contract</strong> sufficiently after delivery. Thus, bleeding from the placental implantation site is not arrested.</p>\n<p>GDM complicating pregnancy can cause <strong>macrosomia</strong>, which is a <strong>risk</strong> <strong>factor </strong>of this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk</strong> <strong>factors</strong> for <strong>atonic PPH</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primiparity or grand multiparity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Previous PPH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Overdistended uterus as in multi-gestational pregnancy, hydramnios, macrosomia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Precipitate labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prolonged labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine malformations</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine fibroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Induction of labour with prostaglandins or oxytocin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placental abnormalities like placenta previa/accreta/increta/percreta/ abruptio placenta</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age &gt;40 years</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6984","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the activ‌e management of the third‌ stage of labor to preven​t postpartum hemorrhage?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the active management of the third stage of labor to prevent postpartum hemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterotonic within 1 minute​ of delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Delayed clamping, cutting ​and ligation of cord","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gentle massa​ge of uterus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Controlled c​ord traction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gentle massage of uterus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gentle massage of uterus</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">As per the latest guidelines, <strong>uterine massage</strong> is <strong>no longer</strong> a <strong>core</strong> <strong>component</strong> of Active Management of the Third Stage of Labor (<strong>AMTSL</strong>) that is performed for the <strong>prevention of postpartum hemorrhage (PPH).</strong></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Uterine massage</strong> or fundal massage is <strong>not recommended </strong>as an intervention to <strong>prevent PPH</strong> in women who have already <strong>received</strong> prophylactic<strong> uterotonics</strong> such as oxytocin (core component of AMTSL). Hence, it is now an <strong>optional component </strong>of<strong> AMTSL </strong>that is done in patients with <strong>uterine</strong> <strong>atony</strong> as a part of the <strong>management of PPH.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>AMTSL</strong> is recommended by the <strong>WHO</strong> to<strong> prevent postpartum hemorrhage </strong>(PPH). <strong>Uterotonics</strong> are the <strong>most important </strong>components in AMTSL to do so.</p>\n<p>The currently recommended components of AMTSL are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 644.37px; width: 520px; border-color: #000000;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 244.384px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40.5327px; height: 244.384px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.362px; height: 244.384px;\">Injecting <strong>uterotonics</strong> within <strong>1 minute</strong> of delivery of the baby or immediately after delivery of the <strong>anterior shoulder</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.074px; height: 244.384px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most important</strong> step</li>\n<li><strong>Oxytocin</strong> 10 IU im/iv infusion is the <strong>preferred uterotonic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<strong>Not recommended</strong> by WHO- <strong>carboprost)</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 188.793px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40.5327px; height: 188.793px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.355px; height: 188.793px;\"><strong>Delayed cord clamping</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.081px; height: 188.793px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Done within <strong>1-3 mins</strong> of delivery</li>\n<li><strong>80 ml</strong> of additional blood goes to the fetus</li>\n<li><strong>Prevents</strong> <strong>fetal anemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 166.392px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40.5327px; height: 166.392px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 260.355px; height: 166.392px;\">Delivery of placenta by controlled cord traction: Modified Brandt Andrews technique</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.081px; height: 166.392px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>The uterus is pushed upwards and backward</li>\n<li>The traction of cord is downwards and forwards</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.8011px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 40.5327px; height: 44.8011px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270.241px; height: 44.8011px;\">Intermittent assessment of uterine tone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177.081px; height: 44.8011px;\">Done every 15 mins for 2 hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: In the event that <strong>uterine atony</strong> is diagnosed during the assessment of uterine tone, <strong>uterine massage</strong> can be performed to <strong>control PPH</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6986","text":"A patient with pre-eclamps​ia presents with profuse ‌bleeding after delivery. ‌Which of the following dr​ugs can be used in the me​dical management of this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A patient with pre-eclampsia presents with profuse bleeding after delivery. Which of the following drugs can be used in the medical management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methe​rgine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbo​prost","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Misop​rostol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dino​prost","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Misoprostol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Misoprostol</strong></p><p>PPH in a patient with <strong>pre-eclampsia</strong> can be treated with <strong>misoprostol</strong>. It is a <strong>PGE1</strong> analogue used as a <strong>second line uterotonic</strong> in the treatment of PPH.</p>\n<p>The<strong> drug of choice</strong> for PPH in normal patients is <strong>oxytocin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Methergine and other ergot derivatives cause dangerous <strong>hypertension</strong> in pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Carboprost and Dinoprost cause severe hypertension in pre-eclampsia. </p>\n<p>Medical Management of PPH:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 485px; height: 406px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 79.7812px;\">Drug</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.391px;\">Dose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.562px;\">Side effects</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.266px;\">Contraindications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 79.7812px;\"><strong>Oxytocin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 123.391px;\">\n<p>10-40 U in 1L crystalloid</p>\n<p>(never given as iv bolus)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 110.562px;\">Water intoxication</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 143.266px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 79.7812px;\">Methergine</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 123.391px;\">0.2 mg</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 110.562px;\">Hypertension</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 143.266px;\">Pre-eclampsia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 79.7812px;\">Carboprost</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 123.391px;\">0.25 mg (8 doses max)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 110.562px;\">\n<p>Diarrhoea</p>\n<p>Hypertension</p>\n<p>Vomiting</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 143.266px;\">\n<p>Bronchial asthma</p>\n<p>Cardiac, renal or hepatic diseases</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 79.7812px;\">Misoprostol</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 123.391px;\">600-1000 mcg</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 110.562px;\">\n<p>Fever</p>\n<p>Tachycardia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 143.266px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1156","text":"In a case of atonic PPH, a​ methyl PGF2 alpha deriva‌tive was given every 15 m‌ins as a uterotonic. What​ is the maximum number of​ times the drug can be ad‌ministered?","raw_text":"In a case of atonic PPH, a methyl PGF2 alpha derivative was given every 15 mins as a uterotonic. What is the maximum number of times the drug can be administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 t​imes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 t​imes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"15 t​imes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"32 t​imes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8 times","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8 times</strong></p><p><strong>Carboprost</strong> is a methyl PGF2 alpha derivative. The normal dosage is <strong>0.25mg</strong>. The <strong>maximum</strong> dose is <strong>2mg</strong>. </p>\n<p>So, 2/0.25 = <strong>8 times</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carboprost is a 15-methyl PGF2 alpha derivative used as a <strong>2nd line drug</strong> in the treatment of PPH that is unresponsive to oxytocin. </p>\n<p>Side effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diarrhoea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypertension</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nausea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vomiting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contraindications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Bronchial asthma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Suspected amniotic fluid embolism</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Active cardiac, renal or hepatic diseases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1195","text":"As per 2017 WHO guidelines​ for the treatment of PPH‌, which of the following ‌statements is true?","raw_text":"As per 2017 WHO guidelines for the treatment of PPH, which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine massage is not rec​ommended in PPH when oxyt‌ocin is administered","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous colloids are t​he recommended fluids for‌ initial resuscitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If the PPH is not responsi​ve to uterotonics, uterin‌e artery embolisation is ‌the next step","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tranexamic acid is adminis​tered early in all cases ‌of PPH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tranexamic acid is administered early in all cases of PPH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tranexamic acid is administered early in all cases of PPH</strong></p><p><strong>Early</strong> administration of <strong>tranexamic acid</strong> is done in <strong>all cases of PPH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tranexamic acid for the treatment of PPH is used as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tranexamic acid should be given in all cases of PPH, regardless of the cause.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early use of iv tranexamic acid (i.e) <strong>within 3 hours</strong> of birth</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fixed-dose of <strong>1 g in 10 mL</strong> (100 mg/mL) iv at 1 mL per minute</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If <strong>bleeding</strong> continues after <strong>30 minutes</strong>, the <strong>second dose</strong> of 1 g iv is given</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Uterine massage</strong> must be done in addition to <strong>oxytocin</strong> in all cases of <strong>PPH.</strong> This will help in the contraction of an atonic uterus. In normal patients, uterine massage is not compulsory as per AMTSL.</p>\n<p>Option B: IV <strong>crystalloids</strong> are the <strong>recommended</strong> fluids for resuscitation.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Balloon</strong> <strong>tamponade</strong> is the next step <strong>after uterotonics</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9721","text":"Following normal vaginal d​elivery, a patient develo‌ped PPH and lost about 70‌0mL of blood. Her PR was ​100 bpm and her BP was 10​0/60mmHg. What is the val‌ue and corresponding inte‌rpretation of her shock i‍ndex?","raw_text":"Following normal vaginal delivery, a patient developed PPH and lost about 700mL of blood. Her PR was 100 bpm and her BP was 100/60mmHg. What is the value and corresponding interpretation of her shock index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.7 - ​normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.7 - si​gnificant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - sig​nificant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 - n​ormal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - significant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - significant</strong></p><p>Shock index = <strong>1</strong> and it indicates <strong>significant</strong> blood loss.</p>\n<p>The shock index is calculated as <strong>HR/systolic BP.</strong></p>\n<p>Here, PR=100 and systolic BP=100.</p>\n<p>Shock Index = 100/100 = 1</p>\n<table style=\"width: 389px; height: 227px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\"><strong>Range of shock index</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\">Normal value of shock index (non-pregnant)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\">0.5 - 0.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\">Normal value of shock index in pregnant (i.e) Obstetrical shock index</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\">0.7 - 0.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 225.578px;\">Significant or massive blood loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.422px;\">0.9 - 1.1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If the shock index is 0.9 – 1.1, it indicates massive blood loss and the need for intensive <strong>resuscitation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6987","text":"A patient was referred to ​the casualty from a distr‌ict hospital with atonic ‌PPH. Which of the followi​ng measures is least like​ly to be done?","raw_text":"A patient was referred to the casualty from a district hospital with atonic PPH. Which of the following measures is least likely to be done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carry out ut​erine massage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Use non-pneumatic anti-sho​ck garment to stabilize t‌he patient","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Continuous iv infusion of ​oxytocin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Volume resuscitation with ​iv crystalloids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Use non-pneumatic anti-shock garment to stabilize the patient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9f744a2a6914833b4a5caeed71a421bx1280x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Use non-pneumatic anti-shock garment to stabilize the patient</strong></p><p>Using a non-pneumatic anti-shock garment (NASG) is not one of the first measures for the management of atonic PPH. NASG is used to reduce bleeding and <strong>stabilize</strong> the patient for shifting to <strong>tertiary</strong> centers. </p>\n<p>The below image shows a non-pneumatic anti-shock garment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Non-pneumatic anti-shock garment (NASG) works by applying <strong>circumferential</strong> <strong>counterpressure</strong>. It decreases blood flow to the abdomen, pelvis, and lower extremities. It increases the blood flow to the heart, lungs, and brain.</p>\n<p>First step measures in the <strong>management of PPH</strong> must be done immediately and simultaneously. This includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Continuous iv <strong>oxytocin</strong> infusion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Fundal massage</strong> and bimanual uterine compression</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Volume resuscitation with <strong>crystalloids</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Insertion of <strong>Foley’s</strong> catheter to monitor urine output</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alert the anesthesia team to begin a manual exploration of the uterus under GA to check for:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Retained placental fragments</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine abnormalities</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lacerations</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rupture</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Blood transfusion should be done if vitals are still unstable</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If bleeding still continues, further steps are taken depending on the patient profile:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Balloon tamponade</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgical methods:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">B lynch compression sutures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine artery ligation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Internal iliac artery ligation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Angiographic embolization</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hysterectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6992","text":"A 35-year-old multipara wi​th atonic PPH continues t‌o bleed profusely despite‌ medical management and t​amponade. Which of the fo​llowing methods cannot be‌ used to reduce bleeding ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old multipara with atonic PPH continues to bleed profusely despite medical management and tamponade. Which of the following methods cannot be used to reduce bleeding in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thermal endometrial ablati​on","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Internal iliac artery liga​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angiographic selective art​ery embolisation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thermal endometrial ablation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thermal endometrial ablation</strong></p><p><strong>Thermal endometrial ablation </strong>is a treatment modality for <strong>Abnormal Uterine Bleeding </strong>(AUB). It is not used in the treatment of PPH.</p>\n<p>In patients who are unresponsive to initial resuscitation and medical management, the following methods can be used:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Balloon tamponade</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgical methods:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">B lynch compression sutures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine artery ligation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Internal iliac artery ligation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Angiographic embolization</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hysterectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6995","text":"Identify the instrument in​ the image given below.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument in the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bakri ​balloon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Foley's ​catheter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sengstaken b​lakemore tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Condom ​catheter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bakri balloon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5576863fbeda467e8572b93e972a671b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/680b34cfd939435184c33a99af03dbadx1200x931.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a7a9816997144688a69d55c9b279cfbx720x558.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d36196f8bca4eb1b22c6e84c6dcadd4x1279x1995.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bakri balloon</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>Bakri balloon</strong>. It is used in uterine tamponade in patients with<strong> atonic PPH</strong> who are <strong>unresponsive</strong> to <strong>medical management.</strong></p>\n<p>The Bakri balloon has a three-way valve and a separate tubing to drain the blood from the uterine cavity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It acts by stimulating uterine contraction and direct <strong>hemostatic pressure</strong> to the open uterine sinuses (tamponade effect).</p>\n<p>The maximum capacity of the balloon is <strong>500mL</strong>. It is inflated with normal saline (200-500mL) and can be retained for up to 12 hours. Success rates are high and it can be used to avoid hysterectomy.</p>\n<p>Other options: Sengstaken Blakemore tube, condom catheter, and 34F Foley with 60mL balloon are other instruments that can be used for uterine tamponade.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts Sengstaken Blakemore tube.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below depicts Foley's catheter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6996","text":"What is the capacity of ba​lloon tamponade to arrest‌ PPH?","raw_text":"What is the capacity of balloon tamponade to arrest PPH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 - ​250 mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"300 - ​500 mL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"500 - ​750 mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"700 - ​900 mL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 300 - 500 mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30d930b6b7664b78b8e4277e8307d031x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 300 - 500 mL</strong></p><p>The capacity of balloon tamponade to arrest PPH is <strong>300 to 500 mL</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bakri balloon is used in uterine <strong>tamponade</strong> in cases of <strong>atonic</strong> <strong>PPH</strong> unresponsive to medical management.</p>\n<p>Insertion of Bakri balloon requires three team members.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One to perform abdominal sonography during the procedure. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Another member places the deflated balloon into the uterus and stabilizes it. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The third member instills fluid to inflate the balloon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Initially, rapid infusion of at least 150 mL of normal saline is done. It is followed by further infusion over a few minutes up to 300 to 500 mL.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6998","text":"A P3 woman bleeds profusel​y after delivery of the p‌lacenta. You initiate oxy‌tocin infusion. The patie​nt continues to bleed and​ the uterus fails to cont‌ract despite bimanual com‌pression. She becomes tac‍hycardic and hypotensive.‌ What is the next best st​ep in her management?","raw_text":"A P3 woman bleeds profusely after delivery of the placenta. You initiate oxytocin infusion. The patient continues to bleed and the uterus fails to contract despite bimanual compression. She becomes tachycardic and hypotensive. What is the next best step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tight intrauterine packing​ with gauze","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Balloon ​tamponade","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ligation of uterine arteri​es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Balloon tamponade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/290f17c8f6254fe6a154f9d2b2ebdf2ax1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Balloon tamponade</strong></p><p><strong>Balloon tamponade</strong> is the next step in the management of <strong>atonic PPH</strong> unresponsive to medical management and bimanual uterine massage. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Tight intrauterine <strong>packing</strong> has become unpopular due to the dangers of <strong>concealed</strong> <strong>bleeding</strong> and <strong>infections</strong>. It can be used in <strong>low-resource</strong> settings while shifting the patient to tertiary care centers.</p>\n<p>Option C: Uterine artery ligation is done when tamponade fails (Bakri balloon and compression sutures).</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hysterectomy</strong> is the <strong>last</strong> <strong>option</strong> when all the other treatment modalities fail. Usually, the uterus contracts and bleeding stops with ligation or embolization. However, hysterectomy is done sooner rather than later in cases of <strong>placenta accreta</strong> and <strong>uterine rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0905","text":"A pregnant lady delivered ​her baby unattended while‌ she was on the way to th‌e hospital. While examini​ng her, you note a perine​al tear in which the anal‌ sphincter is disrupted. ‌This tear is classified a‍s___.","raw_text":"A pregnant lady delivered her baby unattended while she was on the way to the hospital. While examining her, you note a perineal tear in which the anal sphincter is disrupted. This tear is classified as___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st d​egree","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd d​egree","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd d​egree","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4th d​egree","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3rd degree","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3rd degree</strong></p><p>The perineal tears in which <strong>anal sphincter</strong> is <strong>disrupted</strong> are classified as <strong>third-degree</strong> perineal tear.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6999","text":"Identify B lynch suture fr​om the image given below.‌","raw_text":"Identify B lynch suture from the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/422c97f7e5fc46bd96c510f89f437134.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2020e91aec1c4495b290a6715c3b771dx1280x1890.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>Image 1 depicts B lynch suture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>All these stitches are types of uterine compression sutures. They used to surgically oppose the walls of the uterus to arrest bleeding in case of atonic PPH.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7000","text":"A patient with severe PPH ​is shifted to the emergen‌cy OT for B lynch compres‌sion sutures. Which of th​e following statements is​ false about this procedu‌re?","raw_text":"A patient with severe PPH is shifted to the emergency OT for B lynch compression sutures. Which of the following statements is false about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The suture loops over the ​fundus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Not used in​ atonic PPH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine cavity is compress​ed antero-posteriorly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine ischaemic necrosis​ is a complication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Not used in atonic PPH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0ed0fe02d2904e588ea4c963852e2443x572x293.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Not used in atonic PPH</strong></p><p>The procedure is <strong>B-lynch compression sutures</strong> and it is used in atonic PPH. </p>\n<p>B lynch suture is a technique to arrest hemorrhage in <strong>severe</strong> postpartum <strong>atony</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The suture bites are taken over the <strong>lower uterine segment</strong> anteriorly. Then it is looped over the <strong>fundus</strong> to go posterior. Another bite is taken posteriorly. Then it is looped back over the fundus and taken anteriorly. The final bite is taken over the lower uterine segment and the free ends are tied in a surgical knot.</p>\n<p>The uterus is compressed <strong>antero-posteriorly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The success rate is high.</p>\n<p><strong>Ischaemic uterine necrosis</strong> is a complication of this procedure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9723","text":"Which of the following sta​tements are true? Cervica‌l tear is the most common‌ cause of traumatic PPH T​he vulva is the most comm​on site for pelvic hemato‌ma The pudendal artery is‌ the most common artery i‍nvolved in vulval hematom‌a Hematomas of &gt;3cm must ​be surgically explored un​der general anesthesia Lo​wer uterine segment ruptu‌re causes infra-levator h‍ematoma","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cervical tear is the most common cause of traumatic PPH</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The vulva is the most common site for pelvic hematoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The pudendal artery is the most common artery involved in vulval hematoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hematomas of &gt;3cm must be surgically explored under general anesthesia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lower uterine segment rupture causes infra-levator hematoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Statement 4: Hematomas <strong>&lt;5 cm</strong> can be managed <strong>conservatively</strong> with cold compress. Larger hematomas need to be explored surgically. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Statement 5: Lower uterine segment rupture causes <strong>supra-levator</strong> hematoma.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Types of pelvic hematomas:</p>\n<table style=\"margin-right: auto; width: 790px; height: 284px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 127.359375px;\">Feature</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 346.484375px;\">Infralevator hematoma</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 294.1875px;\">Supralevator hematoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.359375px; text-align: left;\">Incidence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.484375px; text-align: left;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 294.1875px; text-align: left;\">Rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.359375px; text-align: left;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.484375px; text-align: left;\">Below the levator ani</td>\n<td style=\"width: 294.1875px; text-align: left;\">Above the levator ani</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.359375px; text-align: left;\">Causes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.484375px; text-align: left;\">Improper hemostasis during repair of vaginal/ perineal tears or episiotomy wound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 294.1875px; text-align: left;\">Extension of cervical laceration, lower uterine segment rupture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.359375px; text-align: left;\">Presentation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.484375px; text-align: left;\">Severe pain with rectal tenesmus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 294.1875px; text-align: left;\">Unexplained shock with features of internal haemorrhage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.359375px; text-align: left;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.484375px; text-align: left;\">Small hematomas can be conservatively managed with cold compress</td>\n<td style=\"width: 294.1875px; text-align: left;\">Laparotomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9720","text":"In a patient with hydramni​os complicating pregnancy‌, the third stage of labo‌ur was managed by applyin​g fundal pressure and con​trolled traction of the c‌ord. On palpation, the ut‌erus was still soft and b‍oggy. Within a few minute‌s, there was moderate ble​eding per vaginum followi​ng which a reddish-purple​ mass was expelled. The p‌atient then developed hyp‍otension, bradycardia and‍ went into shock. What is‌ the most probable diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"In a patient with hydramnios complicating pregnancy, the third stage of labour was managed by applying fundal pressure and controlled traction of the cord. On palpation, the uterus was still soft and boggy. Within a few minutes, there was moderate bleeding per vaginum following which a reddish-purple mass was expelled. The patient then developed hypotension, bradycardia and went into shock. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amniotic fl​uid embolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atonic postpartum haemorrh​age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine ​inversion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Placenta​ accreta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Uterine inversion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uterine inversion</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis is <strong>uterine inversion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hydramnios is a risk factor for uterine inversion due to <strong>uterine over-distension</strong>. Fundal pressure while the uterus is atonic (soft, relaxed, and boggy) causes uterine inversion. It results in haemorrhage followed by <strong>complete uterine inversion</strong> protruding out of the vulva. <strong>Neurogenic</strong> type of shock develops due to <strong>stretching</strong> of the <strong>infundibulopelvic</strong> <strong>ligament</strong> and the ovaries.</p>\n<p>Option A: Amniotic fluid embolism causes cardiac arrest, acute hypoxia and severe haemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Option B: There was only moderate bleeding per vaginum and soft uterus. But since there is protrusion of the uterus per vaginum, the most probable diagnosis is uterine inversion.</p>\n<p>Option D: Placenta accreta rarely causes uterine inversion.</p>\n<p>Uterine inversion is an extremely rare but life-threatening complication in the 3rd stage of labor, in which the uterus is turned inside out partially or completely.</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most common cause is <strong>mismanagement</strong> of <strong>3rd</strong> <strong>stage</strong> of labour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Spontaneous</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Placenta accreta (rarely)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Shock</strong> immediately after delivery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The degree of shock is out of proportion to the amount of bleeding</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shock is either <strong>neurogenic</strong> or <strong>hemorrhagic</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Per abdomen examination - <strong>fundus</strong> of the uterus is not palpable. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management of uterine inversion:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 155px;\" width=\"358\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.484375px;\">Vaginal operations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.078125px;\">Abdominal operations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.46875px;\">Hydrostatic method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.484375px;\">Kustner</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.078125px;\">Huntingtons</td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.46875px;\">O Sullivan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.484375px;\">Spinelli</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.078125px;\">Haultain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 110.46875px;\">Oguch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9718","text":"Which among the following ​is the most potent drug t‌o control PPH?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most potent drug to control PPH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meth​ergin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbo​prost","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Misop​rostol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carboprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carboprost</strong></p><p>Carboprost is the most potent drug to control PPH.</p>\n<p>Though oxytocin is the most commonly used drug for the prevention and treatment of PPH, Carboprost is highly efficacious than oxytocin in treating PPH. If the question would have been \"Drug of choice to treat PPH\" then the answer will be Oxytocin. </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Drug</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Use in PPH</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Oxytocin</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>DOC to prevent and treat PPH</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Methergin</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Drug to prevent and treat PPH</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Carboprost(PGF<sub>2</sub>)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Best drug to control PPH</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Misoprostol(PGE1)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>2nd line drug to treat PPH</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Initially for PPH, oxytocin or methergin (ergometrine) is given. If it fails to control the bleeding then Misoprostol is given.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5dP0QNd2","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"21_strabismus___types_and_treatment","title":"21.Strabismus   Types And Treatment","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2043","text":"A child is brought by her ‌parents to the OPD with c​omplaints of crossed eyes​ as shown below. By doing​ a simple torchlight test​ you conclude that the ch‍ild has pseudostrabismus ​and the inter-pupillary d​istance is normal. Which ‌of the following can be a‌ssociated with the child'​s condition?","raw_text":"A child is brought by her parents to the OPD with complaints of crossed eyes as shown below. By doing a simple torchlight test you conclude that the child has pseudostrabismus and the inter-pupillary distance is normal. Which of the following can be associated with the child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Negative a‌ngle kappa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌elorism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperm‌etropia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"History of retinopathy of ‌prematurity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Negative angle kappa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed356eb74ef14ae7a301d7c9c51caee0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5de7d8f605da4180919804bea79e194dx1280x3637.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Negative angle kappa</strong></p><p>The child has<strong> pseudoesotropia </strong>(apparent convergent squint) which<strong> </strong>is associated with <strong>negative angle kappa and epicanthal folds.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pseudostrabismus—apparent squint </strong>(visual axes are parallel, but eyes seem to have a squint)</p>\n<p>Types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pseudoesotropia</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Apparent <strong>convergent</strong> squint</li>\n<li>Prominent <strong>epicanthal fold</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Negative </strong>angle kappa</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudoexotropia</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Apparent <strong>divergent</strong> squint</li>\n<li>Hypertelorism</li>\n<li><strong>Positive</strong> angle kappa - due to retinopathy of prematurity, hypermetropia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Few definitions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Orthophoria-</strong> implies perfect ocular alignment in the absence of any stimulus for fusion</li>\n<li><strong>Heterophoria</strong> (\"phoria\")- implies a tendency of the eyes to deviate when fusion is blocked <strong>(latent squint)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Heterotropia</strong> (\"tropia\")- implies a <strong>manifest deviation</strong> in which the visual axes do not intersect at the point of fixation</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertropia- upward displacement</strong> of one eye relative to the other is termed as hypertropia and a latent upward imbalance, a hyperphoria</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotropia- downward displacement</strong> is termed a hypotropia and a latent imbalance, a hypophoria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2047","text":"A child presents to the OP‌D with a mild squint. The​ rest of the history is q​uite insignificant. On ex​amination, Hirschberg's c​orneal reflex test is fou‍nd to be normal. All othe​r eye parameters are also​ normal except for hypert‌elorism. What is the most‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child presents to the OPD with a mild squint. The rest of the history is quite insignificant. On examination, Hirschberg's corneal reflex test is found to be normal. All other eye parameters are also normal except for hypertelorism. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudoe‌sotropia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exop‌horia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esop‌horia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psuedoe‌xotropia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Psuedoexotropia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61695a88dfd24e7f8c450ea8f7eb36d0x1280x3637.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Psuedoexotropia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pseudoexotropia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Features of Pseudoexotropia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal Hirschberg's corneal reflex test</li>\n<li>Apparent <strong>divergent</strong> squint</li>\n<li>Associated with hypertelorism/a <strong>positive</strong> angle kappa.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2051","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is least likely t​o present with diplopia?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is least likely to present with diplopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Manifes‌t squint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paralyti‌c squint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latent‌ squint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Restricti‌ve squint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Latent squint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Latent squint</strong></p><p>Latent squint <strong>does not always</strong> present with <strong>diplopia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heterophoria </strong>(Latent squint) is a condition in which the tendency of the eyes to deviate is kept <strong>latent </strong>by <strong>fusion</strong>. When the influence of fusion is removed, the visual axis of one eye deviates away.</p>\n<p><strong>Types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Esophoria-</strong>convergent</li>\n<li><strong>Exophoria-</strong>divergent</li>\n<li>Hyperphoria-deviation upwards</li>\n<li>Hypophoria-deviation downwards</li>\n<li>Cyclophoria-rotation around AP axis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Symptoms:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Usually <strong>asymptomatic-</strong>compensated type.</li>\n<li>In cases where there are symptoms-decompensated type\n<ul>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Eye ache</li>\n<li>Photophobia</li>\n<li>Intermittent diplopia (only under fatigue – temporarily)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2060","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of s​quint seen in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of squint seen in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital‌ esotropia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital‌ exotropia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accommodati‌ve esotropia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary ‌exotropia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Accommodative esotropia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Accommodative esotropia</strong></p><p><strong>Accommodative </strong>esotropia is the <strong>most common</strong> type of squint seen in children.</p>\n<p><strong>Most common</strong> type of squint <strong>overall</strong>: <strong>a</strong><strong>ccommodative </strong>esotropia</p>\n<p><strong>Second</strong> most common <strong>overall:</strong> <strong>co</strong><strong>ngenital</strong>/<strong>Infantile </strong>esotropia</p>\n<p>Most common type of <strong>exotropia:</strong> <strong>primary</strong> exotropia</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2062","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding comitant s​quint?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding comitant squint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary deviation &amp;gt; Sec‌ondary deviation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary deviation &amp;lt; Sec‌ondary deviation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary deviation = Second‌ary deviation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No co-relation between pri‌mary and secondary deviat​ions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary deviation = Secondary deviation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary deviation = Secondary deviation</strong></p><p>In <strong>comitant squint (concomitant)</strong>, the primary deviation is <strong>equal to</strong> the secondary deviation.</p>\n<p>Primary deviation = Deviation of the <strong>squinting</strong> eye</p>\n<p>Secondary deviation = Deviation of the <strong>normal</strong> eye under cover when the patient fixes with the squinting eye.</p>\n<p>In <strong>incomitant</strong> (paralytic and restrictive squint),<strong> secondary deviation &gt; primary deviation</strong> due to <strong>Herring's Law of equal innervation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2065","text":"A mother noticed that her ‌child has had malaligned ​eyes since birth but she ​couldn't afford treatment​ due to financial constra​ints. She now presents at‍ 2-months of age and the ​child has the following f​inding on examination. An‌ ophthalmoscopic examinat‌ion under full cycloplegi​a reveals no abnormalitie​s in refraction and media‌. Cranial nerves are inta​ct. Which of the followin​g statements is true rega‌rding this condition?","raw_text":"A mother noticed that her child has had malaligned eyes since birth but she couldn't afford treatment due to financial constraints.  She now presents at 2-months of age and the child has the following finding on examination. An ophthalmoscopic examination under full cycloplegia reveals no abnormalities in refraction and media. Cranial nerves are intact. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angle of deviation is larg‌e and constant.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amblyopia develops in 90 %‌ of the cases.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with late‌nt vertical nystagmus.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Correction by spectacles i‌s the treatment of choice​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Angle of deviation is large and constant.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b7a64f752ead4f37943f43d2659af0f5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Angle of deviation is large and constant.</strong></p><p>The age at presentation, esotropia, no refractory error, no media opacities, and no cranial nerve palsies suggest a diagnosis of<strong> essential infantile esotropia</strong>. The<strong> angle of deviation</strong> in infantile esotropia is fairly <strong>large (&gt; 30°) and constant.</strong></p>\n<p>Infantile esotropia, also known as non-accommodative esotropia is the <strong>most common type of congenital esotropia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comitant convergent squint</li>\n<li>Age of onset- <strong>1 to 2</strong> months</li>\n<li>Angle of <strong>deviation</strong>-<strong> large</strong> and constant</li>\n<li><strong>Amblyopia-</strong> develops in<strong> 25–40% </strong>of cases</li>\n<li><strong>Associations include:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior oblique <strong>overaction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dissociated vertical deviation</strong> (70%)</li>\n<li><strong>Latent horizontal nystagmus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Surgery-</strong> treatment of choice</li>\n<li>Amblyopia correction measures: Patching of normal eye</li>\n<li><strong>Recession </strong>of both <strong>medial recti</strong></li>\n<li>Timing: Preferably <strong>before 1 year</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1819","text":"A surgeon decides to resec‌t a part of a muscle whil​e operating on a case of ​strabismus. This action w​ould lead to which of the​ following?","raw_text":"A surgeon decides to resect a part of a muscle while operating on a case of strabismus. This action would lead to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weakening o‌f the muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strengthening of the muscl‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paralysis o‌f the muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No change in strength of t‌he muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Strengthening of the muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Strengthening of the muscle</strong></p><p>In squint surgery, <strong>muscle resection</strong> leads to the <strong>strengthening</strong> of the <strong>muscle.</strong></p>\n<p>The main types of procedure in<strong> squint surgery</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Weakening</strong> to decrease the effective strength of action of a muscle.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recession</strong> slackens a muscle by moving it away from its insertion. It can be performed on any muscle <strong>except</strong> the <strong>superior oblique.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Disinsertion</strong> (or <strong>myectomy)</strong> involves detaching a muscle from its insertion without reattachment. It is most commonly used to weaken an overacting <strong>inferior oblique</strong> muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior fixation suture: </strong>The principle of this <strong>(Faden) procedure</strong> is to suture the muscle belly to the sclera posteriorly so as to decrease the pull of the muscle in its field of action without affecting the eye in the primary position.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Strengthening,</strong> to enhance the pull of a muscle.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resection</strong> shortens a muscle to enhance its effective pull. It is suitable <strong>only for</strong> the <strong>rectus muscle.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tucking</strong> of a muscle or its tendon is usually confined to the enhancement of the action of the <strong>superior oblique muscle.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Advancement</strong> of the muscle nearer to the limbus can be used to enhance the action of a previously recessed <strong>rectus muscle.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Vector adjustment procedures</strong> have the primary aim of altering the direction of muscle action.</li>\n<li><strong>Transposition</strong> refers to the <strong>relocation</strong> of one or more extraocular muscles to substitute for the action of an absent or severely deficient muscle. The <strong>most common indication</strong> is severe <strong>lateral rectus weakness</strong> due to acquired palsy of the 6<sup>th</sup> cranial nerve palsy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2067","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g accommodative esotropia​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding accommodative esotropia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common type‌ of squint.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Miotics are used as the tr‌eatment strategy in the h​igh AC/A ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with high‌ myopia.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Refractive type can be ful‌ly corrected by use of sp​ectacles.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is associated with high myopia.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd54b64d92e248f7b6dd196a15c638c6x653x266.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/133525488fab43dd89dbecdc925d96e4x491x170.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is associated with high myopia.</strong></p><p><strong>Accommodative esotropia</strong> is associated with a high degree of <strong>hypermetropia</strong> (+4 to +7 D).</p>\n<p><strong>Accommodative esotropia </strong>is the<strong> m</strong><strong>ost common</strong> type of squint. Typically presents around <strong>2 to 3</strong> years of age.</p>\n<p>Types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Refractive</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Associated with <strong>high hypermetropia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fully correctable</strong> by <strong>spectacles</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Non-refractive type </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Abnormal accommodative convergence/accommodation (AC/A)</strong> ratio</li>\n<li>Fully corrected by bifocal glasses</li>\n<li><strong>Miotics </strong>useful in cases where glasses are unsuitable\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Echothiophate </strong>causes<strong> iris cyst</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Prevention </strong>of iris cysts-<strong> Phenylephrine </strong>drops</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed type</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The accommodative convergence/accommodation <strong>(AC/A) ratio</strong> is defined as the amount of convergence measured in prism diopters per unit (diopter) change in accommodation.</p>\n<p>The image below shows accommodative esotropia with and without correction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2069","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best line of treatmen​t in a 3-year-old child p​resenting with a recent o​nset of 15-degree accommo​dative esotropia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best line of treatment in a 3-year-old child presenting with a recent onset of 15-degree accommodative esotropia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orthoptic‌ exercise","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sur‌gery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relievi‌ng prism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Refractive‌ correction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Refractive correction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Refractive correction</strong></p><p>The <strong>best line of treatment</strong> in <strong>accommodative esotropia</strong> is a <strong>refractive correction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Esotropia for distance is corrected by the treatment of hypermetropia: convex glasses</p>\n<p>Esotropia for near is corrected by +3DS lens.</p>\n<p>Algorithm for the management of accommodative <strong>esotropia </strong>is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>R</strong>: <strong>R</strong>efractive correction: Glasses/Miotics</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>:<strong> O</strong>rthoptics/<strong>O</strong>cclusion: for amblyopia</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>:<strong> </strong><strong>O</strong>perative correction: for non-accommodative component</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>: <strong>P</strong>rism correction: for remaining error</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1823","text":"Charlie, a 5-year-old boy ‌with crossed eyes receive​d his first eye exam from​ a primary care ophthalmo​logist. Examination, incl​uding cycloplegic refract‍ion and dilated fundus ex​am, resulted in diagnoses​ of constant right esotro‌pia, and amblyopia in the‌ right eye. By what age s​hould his amblyopia be co​rrected?","raw_text":"Charlie, a 5-year-old boy with crossed eyes received his first eye exam from a primary care ophthalmologist. Examination, including cycloplegic refraction and dilated fundus exam, resulted in diagnoses of constant right esotropia, and amblyopia in the right eye. By what age should his amblyopia be corrected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 y‌ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14 y‌ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8 years</strong></p><p><strong>Strabismic amblyopia</strong> should be<strong> corrected</strong> before<strong> 8 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>The <strong>sensitive period</strong> during which acuity of an amblyopic eye can be improved is <strong>usually up to 7–8 years</strong> <strong>in strabismic amblyopia</strong> and maybe <strong>longer (into the teens) for anisometropic amblyopia</strong> when a good binocular function is present.</p>\n<p>In the absence of an organic lesion, a <strong>difference</strong> in best-corrected visual acuity (VA) of <strong>two Snellen lines</strong> or more (or &gt;1 log unit) is indicative of amblyopia.</p>\n<p>Treatment of amblyopia:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Occlusion of the normal eye, to encourage the use of the amblyopic eye, is the most effective treatment.</li>\n<li>Penalization, in which vision in the normal eye is blurred with atropine, is an alternative method.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2171","text":"A 5-year-old female patien‌t presented for her first​ complete eye examination​ after a school vision sc​reening found unequal vis​ual acuity. Her parents h‍ave not noted any eye pro​blems. Visual acuity in t​he right eye was 20/20 an‌d the left eye is 20/80. ‌The rest of the examinati​on was unremarkable and g​lasses were prescribed. S‌he was diagnosed with ani​sometric hyperopia and am​blyopia of the left eye w‌hen her visual acuity at ‌a return appointment was ‌found to be 20/20 RE and ​20/60 LE at a distance wh‍ile wearing her new glass​es. What is the treatment‌ of choice?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old female patient presented for her first complete eye examination after a school vision screening found unequal visual acuity. Her parents have not noted any eye problems. Visual acuity in the right eye was 20/20 and the left eye is 20/80. The rest of the examination was unremarkable and glasses were prescribed. She was diagnosed with anisometric hyperopia and amblyopia of the left eye when her visual acuity at a return appointment was found to be 20/20 RE  and 20/60 LE at a distance while wearing her new glasses.  What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Convergent‌ exercises","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prism co‌rrection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sur‌gery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conventiona‌l occlusion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Conventional occlusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Conventional occlusion</strong></p><p><strong>Occlusion</strong> of the <strong>normal</strong> eye is the treatment of choice for amblyopia.</p>\n<p><strong>Amblyopia</strong> is an uncorrectable decrease in vision in one or both eyes with no apparent structural abnormality seen to explain it. It is a diagnosis of exclusion. Generally, a difference of two lines or more on an eye chart test of visual acuity between the two eyes or a best-corrected vision of 20/30 or worse would be defined as amblyopia. For example, our patient has 20/20 vision with the right eye and only 20/80 with the left and the left eye even after best-corrected vision couldn't achieve &lt;20/30 vision, the left eye is said to be amblyopic.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased contrast sensitivity</li>\n<li>Diminution of vision</li>\n<li>Sunglass effect- brightness is less compared to the normal eye</li>\n<li>Crowding phenomenon- slipping of images</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment of choice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Occlusion</strong> of the<strong> normal eye</strong> (4 to 6 hours/day) using patches.</li>\n<li><strong>Penalization: </strong>Blurring of vision using<strong> atropine.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prism correction is not the right treatment as the child doesn't have strabismus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2072","text":"A 3-year-old child present‌s with a right convergent​ squint of 6 months durat​ion. Choose the most appr​opriate treatment strateg​y.","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child presents with a right convergent squint of 6 months duration. Choose the most appropriate treatment strategy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediate surgical correct‌ion followed by amblyopia​ therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proper refractive correcti‌on, amblyopia therapy fol​lowed by surgical correct​ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prescription of spectacles‌ and defer surgery until ​5 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Botulinum toxin injection ‌followed by occlusion the​rapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proper refractive correction, amblyopia therapy followed by surgical correction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proper refractive correction, amblyopia therapy followed by surgical correction</strong></p><p>The appropriate management in this case would be proper refractive correction, amblyopia therapy followed by surgical correction</p>\n<p>The algorithm for management of accommodative squint is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>R</strong>: <strong>R</strong>efractive correction: Glasses/Miotics</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>: <strong>O</strong>rthoptics/<strong>O</strong>cclusion: For amblyopia</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>:<strong> O</strong>perative correction: For non-accommodative component</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>: <strong>P</strong>rism correction: For remaining error</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6794","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions should an opht​halmologist prescribe mio​tics?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions should an ophthalmologist prescribe miotics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accommodati‌ve esotropia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intermitten‌t exotropia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralytic‌ esotropia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Accommodative esotropia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Accommodative esotropia</strong></p><p>Miotics are used in the management of <strong>accommodative esotropia.</strong></p>\n<p>The use of <strong>miotics </strong>in<strong> convergent strabismus</strong> is based on the peripheral spasm of accommodation caused by the drug. The accommodation triad involves <strong>convergence</strong>. An increased accommodative effort increases the amount of <strong>convergence</strong>. Pilocarpine would produce accommodation spasm and eliminate the accommodative convergence that would otherwise occur when the patient looks at a near object. It is<strong> useful in </strong>cases of an<strong> abnormal AC/A ratio </strong>as in, accommodative esotropia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2076","text":"Paralytic squint presents ‌with all except","raw_text":"Paralytic squint presents with all except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Devi‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conf‌usion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dipl‌opia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stere‌opsis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stereopsis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stereopsis</strong></p><p>In squint, the <strong>deviation of the eye</strong> results in two vastly different images. The brain is <strong>unable to fuse</strong> them. Hence, there is <strong>no stereopsis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Features of paralytic squint:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diplopia</li>\n<li>Confusion</li>\n<li>Nausea, Vertigo</li>\n<li>Ocular deviation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Signs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary deviation &lt; Secondary deviation</strong></li>\n<li>Compensatory head posture–<strong>Ocular torticollis</strong></li>\n<li>False projection or orientation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In adults- no amblyopia</p>\n<p>In children &lt;6 years- amblyopia can occur</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0657","text":"A 40-year-old diabetic pat‌ient presented with the ​​squint. On examination, t​he secondary deviation wa​s found to be more than t​he primary deviation. For‍ced duction test is negat​ive. Which of the followi​ng types of squint is mos‌t likely to be present in‌ this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old diabetic patient presented with the ​squint. On examination, the secondary deviation was found to be more than the primary deviation. Forced duction test is negative. Which of the following types of squint is most likely to be present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Comitan‌t squint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Restricti‌ve squint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralyti‌c squint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudo‌squint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paralytic squint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paralytic squint</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> paralytic squint. </strong>It is characterized by the presence of secondary deviation more than the primary deviation and a <strong>negative</strong> <strong>forced</strong> <strong>duction</strong> <strong>test</strong>.</p>\n<p>The squint may be either comitant or incomitant.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Comitant squint: The angle of squint measured is same in all gazes. </li>\n<li>Incomitant squint: The angle of squint measured is different in different gazes. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The differences between comitant and incomitant squint are shown in the table below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Comitant</strong> <strong>squint</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Incomitant</strong> <strong>squint</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Magnitude of squint</td>\n<td>Same in all gazes</td>\n<td>Varies with the gaze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diplopia</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ocular movements</td>\n<td>Full</td>\n<td>Restricted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>False projection</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abnormal head posture</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Secondary deviation</strong></td>\n<td>Equal to primary deviation</td>\n<td><strong>More than primary deviation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The incomitant squint is further divided into two main types: Paralytic and restrictive.</p>\n<p><strong>Paralytic squint</strong> occurs due to the paralysis of extraocular muscle while <strong>restrictive</strong> <strong>squint</strong> is due to the mechanical restriction caused by tight or fibrosed muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Forced duction test(FDT)</strong> differentiates between these two types. In this test, the eye is moved mechanically with the help of a forceps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The test is <strong>negative</strong> if there is no resistance to the movement. It is seen in a <strong>paralytic</strong> squint.</li>\n<li>The test is <strong>positive</strong> if there is resistance to the movement. It is seen in a <strong>restrictive</strong> squint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4182","text":"Identify th‌e condition.","raw_text":"Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left 3rd n‌erve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right 6th ‌nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left 6th n‌erve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right 3rd ‌nerve palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left 6th nerve palsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bb2915b820b544519fda81ecf8ea4ded.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aef505ce112841d4988eed93b8afa779x1280x2905.726375176305.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left 6th nerve palsy</strong></p><p>The patient has <strong>left eye esotropia</strong> in primary gaze which is suggestive of<strong> left abducent (6th) nerve palsy</strong> which innervates the left lateral rectus muscle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4183","text":"A patient complains of dif‌ficulty in climbing down ​the stairs due to double ​vision. On examination, y​ou notice his right eye h​as deviated upward and th‍e deviation becomes more ​pronounced on the left ga​ze. The remaining extraoc‌ular movements are intact‌. Which nerve is most lik​ely involved?","raw_text":"A patient complains of difficulty in climbing down the stairs due to double vision. On examination, you notice his right eye has deviated upward and the deviation becomes more pronounced on the left gaze. The remaining extraocular movements are intact. Which nerve is most likely involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right oculo‌motor nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right troc‌hlear nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left troch‌lear nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left oculo‌motor nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right trochlear nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right trochlear nerve</strong></p><p>The affected nerve in the given clinical scenario is the <strong>right trochlear nerve. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> right trochlear</strong> <strong>nucleus</strong> actually innervates the<strong> left superior oblique</strong> and vice versa. However, the nerve decussates at the midbrain at the level of the <strong>inferior colliculus.</strong> So, lesions of the <strong>trochlear nucleus</strong> lead to <strong>contralateral</strong> palsy. While that of <strong>trochlear nerve</strong> peripherally lead to <strong>ipsilateral</strong> palsy.</p>\n<p>In this patient, the right-sided symptoms could be due to a lesion in the <strong>right trochlear nerve</strong> (ipsilateral) or <strong>left trochlear nucleus</strong> (contralateral). He complains of <strong>diplopia</strong> while climbing down the stairs because the normal eye can look down but the paralytic eye has deviated. There is also a <strong>limitation of depression</strong> due to superior oblique palsy, and hence the eye is deviated upward due to<strong> overaction</strong> of the <strong>inferior oblique</strong>. The obliques exert maximum force in an <strong>adducted position</strong> and hence the deviation becomes <strong>more prominent</strong> on the left gaze when the right eye is adducted.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6796","text":"A patient suffers head tra‌uma following which he co​mplains of diplopia. His ​extraocular motility test​ing in the 9 gazes is as ​shown in the image. What ‍is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A patient suffers head trauma following which he complains of diplopia. His extraocular motility testing in the 9 gazes is as shown in the image. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internuclear ophthalmopleg‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"One and a h‌alf syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trochlear ‌nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oculomotor ‌nerve palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oculomotor nerve palsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6a43941cf0c486a8ca587b926094434.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fb72e216d2b48eebc1da105cbce1c31x1280x735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oculomotor nerve palsy</strong></p><p>The given scenario suggests a <strong>left oculomotor nerve palsy.</strong></p>\n<p>It is evident by the upper lid ptosis and down and out deviation of the left eye, as seen in the primary position of gaze (central image).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2077","text":"An 18-year-old destitute w‌oman reports to a free ey​e camp with vision proble​ms. Best-corrected visual​ acuity was 6/12 in the r​ight eye and 6/6 in the l‍eft eye. Elevation in the​ right eye was absent. He​r right eye showed 14–16 ‌prism diopter (PD) hypotr‌opia. Bell's phenomenon w​as absent in the right ey​e. Diagnosis of right eye‌ double elevator palsy wa​s made. Which of the foll​owing muscles are affecte‌d in her?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old destitute woman reports to a free eye camp with vision problems. Best-corrected visual acuity was 6/12 in the right eye and 6/6 in the left eye. Elevation in the right eye was absent. Her right eye showed 14–16 prism diopter (PD) hypotropia. Bell's phenomenon was absent in the right eye. Diagnosis of right eye double elevator palsy was made. Which of the following muscles are affected in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levator palpebrae superior‌is and Muller muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levator palpebrae superior‌is and superior rectus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muller muscle and Inferior‌ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior rectus and Inferi‌or oblique","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superior rectus and Inferior oblique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superior rectus and Inferior oblique</strong></p><p>In <strong>double-elevator palsy</strong>, the muscles involved are <strong>superior rectus and inferior oblique.</strong></p>\n<p>It is commonly associated with ptosis or pseudoptosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2089","text":"A patient reports to the O‌PD with complaints that h​e feels dizzy on lateral ​gaze. His range of moveme​nts is shown below. Jerky​ movements are also obser‍ved in the left eye on th​e left gaze however conve​rgence is normal. Which o‌f the following structure‌s is most likely affected​?","raw_text":"A patient reports to the OPD with complaints that he feels dizzy on lateral gaze. His range of movements is shown below. Jerky movements are also observed in the left eye on the left gaze however convergence is normal. Which of the following structures is most likely affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occipit‌al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paramedian pontine reticul‌ar formation (PPRF)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial longitudinal fascic‌ulus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pretecta‌l fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medial longitudinal fasciculus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4cc1a21cd5eb451ebd592ac2b7f03758.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e30633b62d2548f1a41c0892ff22ae4dx1280x2317.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d52bf75a00e844f39d460908be111c36x1280x2259.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medial longitudinal fasciculus</strong></p><p>Lesion to the <strong>medial longitudinal fasciculus</strong> causes <strong>internuclear ophthalmoplegia </strong>(INO).</p>\n<p>The right gaze is normal but on the left gaze, there is an inability of right eye adduction and in the left abduction is possible but with nystagmus. This along with normal convergence are all in favor of diagnosis of a lesion in right medial longitudinal fasciculus(MLF) leading to internuclear ophthalmoplegia.</p>\n<p>INO is seen in <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong></p>\n<p>The image below depicts internuclear ophthalmoplegia due to a lesion of the right MLF.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Total ophthalmoplegia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All <strong>extraocular and intraocular</strong> muscles are <strong>paralyzed</strong>.</li>\n<li>Paralysis of 3<sup>rd</sup>, 4<sup>th</sup>, and 6<sup>th</sup> nerve</li>\n<li>Seen in orbital apex syndrome, cavernous sinus thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>External ophthalmoplegia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All <strong>extraocular muscles</strong> are <strong>paralyzed</strong></li>\n<li>Lesions at the level of motor nuclei</li>\n<li>Sparing of Edinger Westphal nucleus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2157","text":"Identify th‌e condition","raw_text":"Identify the condition","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duane’s retraction syndrom‌e type 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duane’s retraction syndrom‌e type 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duane’s retraction syndrom‌e type 1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Duane's restriction syndro‌me type 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duane’s retraction syndrome type 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6cc950ed38e4838b9ef49f829845766.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81d8461813b44b1b84ec41929150e1b9x1280x1949.7884344146685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duane’s retraction syndrome type 1</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Duane’s retraction syndrome type 1.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Paradoxical innervation of LR</strong> where it gets innervated by some fibres of III nerve instead of VI nerve. On impulse for abduction via VI nerve there is no abduction limitation. On impulse for adduction via III nerve both LR and MR contract thus causing retraction of globe (as the antagonist LR instead of relaxation, starts to contract)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Duane’s retraction syndrome (DRS) </strong>is a cause of <strong>restrictive squint</strong></p>\n<p>There is a failure of innervation of the lateral rectus by a <strong>hypoplastic sixth nerve nucleus,</strong> with anomalous innervation of the lateral rectus by fibers from the third nerve. <strong>Retraction of the globe</strong> on attempted <strong>adduction. </strong>Widening of palpebral fissure on attempted abduction</p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> by <strong>Huber:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Limitation of abduction- type 1</li>\n<li>Limitation of adduction- type 2</li>\n<li>Limitation of both- type 3</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: DRS is an<strong> exception</strong> to <strong>Sherrington's law</strong> of reciprocal innervation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6793","text":"An eleven-year-old girl pr‌esented with a history of​ squint and poor vision. ​She had visual acuities o​f 6/24 and counting finge​rs in her right and left ‍eyes respectively. There ​was a left hypotropia in ​the primary position of g‌aze with associated marke‌d restriction of elevatio​n in adduction and a posi​tive forced duction test.‌ A diagnosis of Brown syn​drome was made. The patho​logy involves which of th‌e following muscles?","raw_text":"An eleven-year-old girl presented with a history of squint and poor vision. She had visual acuities of 6/24 and counting fingers in her right and left eyes respectively. There was a left hypotropia in the primary position of gaze with associated marked restriction of elevation in adduction and a positive forced duction test. A diagnosis of Brown syndrome was made. The pathology involves which of the following muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior‌ oblique","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superio‌r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levator palpebrae superior‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior‌ oblique","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior oblique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior oblique</strong></p><p><strong>Brown syndrome</strong> occurs due to mechanical restriction caused by a congenitally<strong> tight superior oblique sheath</strong> or by the impaired movement of the <strong>superior oblique tendon</strong> through the trochlea. It may be acquired in some cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Contracture of the superior oblique</strong> leads to <strong>restriction</strong> of its antagonistic muscle, the<strong> inferior oblique</strong> and that is why the patient presents with absence of elevation in adduction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2160","text":"Brown’s syndrome simulates‌ which muscle palsy?","raw_text":"Brown’s syndrome simulates which muscle palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior‌ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superio‌r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior‌ oblique","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferio‌r rectus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior oblique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior oblique</strong></p><p><strong>Brown’s superior oblique tendon sheath syndrome</strong> simulates palsy of <strong>inferior oblique muscle. </strong>In both of these cases, there is a limited elevation of the eye in adduction. </p>\n<p>Brown’s superior oblique tendon sheath syndrome is a cause of restrictive squint.The forced duction test is positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnoses</strong> of Brown's syndrome:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Inferior oblique palsy</strong>- differentiated by a <strong>positive head-tilt test</strong> (negative in Brown's syndrome) and a <strong>negative forced duction test</strong> (positive in Brown's syndrome)</li>\n<li><strong>Primary superior oblique overaction</strong>- differentiated by <strong>negative forced duction test</strong> (Positive in Brown's syndrome)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2151","text":"Identify th‌e condition.","raw_text":"Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V pattern‌ exotropia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A pattern‌ esotropia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"V pattern‌ esotropia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A pattern‌ exotropia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A pattern esotropia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae1253020ae641548054edf74877b0a3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56ac3c8c01474104ae573d2bd26c75efx1200x894.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A pattern esotropia</strong></p><p>The given image classically depicts an <strong>A pattern esotropia.</strong> <strong>Relative convergence on upgaze</strong> (esotropia increases on upgaze) is a feature of A-pattern esotropia.</p>\n<p><strong>A or V pattern heterotropia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"V\" or \"A\" patterns occur when the relative contributions of the superior rectus and inferior oblique to elevation, or of the inferior rectus and superior oblique to depression, are abnormal, resulting in the derangement of the balance of their horizontal vectors in up and downgaze.</li>\n<li><strong>Oblique muscle dysfunction</strong> is the <strong>commonest</strong></li>\n<li>\"V\" pattern is said to be significant when the difference between upgaze and downgaze is <strong>≥15 Δ.</strong></li>\n<li>\"A\" pattern is considered significant if the difference between upgaze and downgaze is <strong>≥10 Δ.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows A and V pattern heterotropia</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0264","text":"A patient presents with le‌ft-sided head tilt which ​on straightening leads to​ right hypotropia. This i​ncreases on dextroversion​ and right head tilt. Par‍alysis of which of the fo​llowing muscles is involv​ed?","raw_text":"A patient presents with left-sided head tilt which on straightening leads to right hypotropia. This increases on dextroversion and right head tilt. Paralysis of which of the following muscles is involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right super‌ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right supe‌rior rectus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right infer‌ior oblique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left infer‌ior oblique","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right superior rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right superior rectus</strong></p><p><strong>Right superior rectus palsy</strong> is the cause for the given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>The three-step test, also known as the <strong>Parks-Bielschowsky three-step test</strong>, is a diagnostic test used to identify which muscle is paralyzed in the case of an acquired hypertropia. This method uses the cover-uncover test, with or without the Maddox rod to measure the amount of deviation in different head positions.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-bielschowsky5_5-0\"></sup></p>\n<p>The three-step test may also be used to diagnose the less common inferior oblique or vertical rectus muscle palsy. It is designed for the diagnosis of a single paretic vertical muscle and is unreliable when there are multiple paretic muscles or in restrictive strabismus. This test may also help in identifying if a superior rectus palsy is true or simulated in a patient with an inhibitional palsy of the contralateral antagonist.</p>\n<p>Using the three-step test in the above clinical scenario:</p>\n<p>Step 1. Which eye is hypotrophic?</p>\n<p>The eye hypotrophic in the primary position is the right eye. The paretic muscles can be right eye elevator or left eye depressor.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right superior rectus</li>\n<li>Right inferior oblique</li>\n<li>Left inferior rectus</li>\n<li>Left superior oblique</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 2.<span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_2._Does_the_hypertropia_increase_in_right_gaze_or_left_gaze.3F\" style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Does the hypotropia increase in the right gaze or left gaze?</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"mw-headline\" style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">The hypotropia increases in right gaze.</span></p>\n<p>Therefore, the paretic muscle must be either (1) one of the muscles whose primary action is elevating the right eye on the right gaze, or (2) one of the muscles whose primary action is depressing the left eye in the right gaze. The left inferior oblique and the right superior rectus both have a primary action of elevation in right gaze. Therefore, one of these muscles could be the paretic muscle. Likewise, the left superior oblique and the right inferior rectus have a primary action of depression in the right gaze and could be paretic. But since the left inferior oblique and the right inferior rectus did not meet step 1, they can be eliminated from the possible paretic muscles. This leaves us:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left superior oblique </li>\n<li>Right superior rectus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 3. Does the hypotropia increase in the right head tilt or left head tilt?</p>\n<p>The hypotropia increases in the right head tilt. If the deviation increases on right head tilt and decreases on left head tilt, as it does in this example patient, then the right superior rectus is implicated as the paretic culprit.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"g21VGl8n","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"21_metabolic_bone_diseases_in_children","title":"21.Metabolic Bone Diseases In Children","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB7387","text":"Which of the following occ‍ur due to nutritional def​iciency? Osteomalacia Pri‍mary hyperparathyroidism ‌Secondary hyperparathyroi​dism Rickets Osteopetrosi‍s","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following  occur due to nutritional deficiency?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Osteomalacia</li>\n<li>Primary hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Secondary hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Rickets</li>\n<li>Osteopetrosis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‍ and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3,‍ and 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3,‍ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 4,‍ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3, and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Osteomalacia</strong>, <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong>, and <strong>rickets</strong> occur due to nutritional deficiency.</p>\n<p>Metabolic bone disorders due to <strong>nutritional deficiencies</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong> may lead to:\n<ul>\n<li>Rickets- in children</li>\n<li>Osteomalacia- in adults</li>\n<li>secondary hyperparathyroidism </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin C deficiency</strong> may lead to scurvy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Primary hyperparathyroidism</strong> occurs due to an <strong>adenoma</strong> or <strong>hyperplasia </strong>of the parathyroid gland. This leads to the unregulated production of parathyroid hormone.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteopetrosis</strong> occurs due to<strong> decreased osteoclastic</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0870","text":"Laboratory findings of a p‍atient are given below. W​hat is the most likely di‍agnosis? Parathyroid horm‌one: 102 pg/mL Alkaline p​hosphatase: 760 IU/L Seru‍m calcium: 6 mg/dL Serum ‌phosphate: 2.1 mg/dL","raw_text":"<p>Laboratory findings of a patient are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Parathyroid hormone: 102 pg/mL</p>\n<p>Alkaline phosphatase: 760 IU/L</p>\n<p>Serum calcium: 6 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Serum phosphate: 2.1 mg/dL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary hyperparathyroidis‍m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paget's dis‍ease of bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteop‍orosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p>Raised parathormone level and raised alkaline phosphatase along with low serum calcium and low serum phosphate level is suggestive of <strong>rickets</strong>. </p>\n<p>Nutritional rickets occurs due to<strong> deficiency of vitamin D</strong>. This leads to <strong>low calcium </strong>levels in the blood. To raise the serum calcium, the secretion of the <strong>parathyroid hormone rises</strong>. The parathyroid hormone tries to raise the calcium levels by :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reabsorbing calcium in the kidneys and secreting phosphate in exchange leading to <strong>low phosphate </strong>levels</li>\n<li>Increasing bone resorption</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5320","text":"A 4-year-old child was bro‍ught to the OPD with a pa​inless deformity. A radio‍graph of the child's legs‌ is shown below. The most​ common cause of this def‍ormity is ______.","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child was brought to the OPD with a painless deformity. A radiograph of the child's legs is shown below. The most common cause of this deformity is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeletal ‍dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Scu‍rvy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blount ‍disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rickets","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf56d8e7e31749129f1d1b160384bac0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8cc062b56133486089128e60fa49ec3bx1280x1326.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rickets</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows<strong> bow legs </strong>or <strong>genu varum</strong> which is most commonly seen in<strong> rickets</strong>. </p>\n<p>Rickets is a <strong>bone mineralization disorder </strong>that primarily affects <strong>children</strong> with<strong> open growth plates</strong>, resulting from a deficiency of vitamin D, calcium, or phosphate.</p>\n<p>In toddlers, genu varum is one of the most common initial sign of rickets. In older children, genu valgum or coxa vara might be present.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7402","text":"The X-ray of a child with ‍rickets shows widening of​ the metaphyseal ends. Wh‍at is this known as?","raw_text":"The X-ray of a child with rickets shows widening of the metaphyseal ends. What is this known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fra‍ying","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spla‍ying","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cup‍ping","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelka‍n spur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Splaying","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c09c7d4c21c4dcc8dcfa2aea930c21fx713x342.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Splaying</strong></p><p><strong>Widening of the metaphyseal ends</strong> is known as <strong>splaying</strong>. It occurs because of the accumulation of non-mineralized osteoid at the metaphyseal ends.</p>\n<p>Defective mineralization of osteoid also results in fraying and cupping. <strong>Fraying</strong> is the <strong>worn-out appearance</strong> of the metaphyseal margins. <strong>Cupping</strong> is the <strong>concave appearance</strong> of the metaphysis. Bowing is seen in the diaphysis of the long bones affected by rickets.</p>\n<p>The given radiograph shows splaying, fraying and cupping.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7328","text":"A 4-year-old child with pe‍rifollicular hemorrhages ​is diagnosed with scurvy.‍ X-ray of the child is do‌ne. Which of the followin​g radiological features i‍s incorrectly matched?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child with perifollicular hemorrhages is diagnosed with scurvy. X-ray of the child is done. Which of the following radiological features is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"White line of Frankel- den‍se zone of provisional ca​lcification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wimberger's ring sign- rin‍g shaped epiphysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trummerfeld zone- radiopaq‍ue zone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pelkan spur- small portion‍ of detached bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trummerfeld zone- radiopaque zone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trummerfeld zone- radiopaque zone</strong></p><p><strong>Trummerfeld zone</strong> is a <strong>radiolucent zone</strong> beneath the Frankel's line due to <strong>lack of mineralized osteoid. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0911","text":"A child presents with pinp‍oint ecchymoses around ha​ir follicles and is not a‍llowing anyone to move hi‌s limbs because of pain. ​He also has tender nodule‍s over the chest, as seen‌ in the image given below‍. What is the most likely​ diagnosis in this child?‍","raw_text":"A child presents with pinpoint ecchymoses around hair follicles and is not allowing anyone to move his limbs because of pain. He also has tender nodules over the chest, as seen in the image given below. What is the most likely diagnosis in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute ost‍eomyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesi‍s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scu‍rvy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Scurvy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/81fd49b3dda84b218b760739e08313e9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Scurvy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing scorbutic rosary are suggestive of <strong>scurvy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scurvy results from the<strong> deficiency of vitamin C</strong>. Vitamin C is required for the <strong>hydroxylation of lysine and proline </strong>that are used in the synthesis of collagen. Hence, deficiency of vitamin C leads to the <strong>formation of defective collagen. </strong></p>\n<p>Defective collagen results in capillary hemorrhages which may present as generalized bleeding tendency. It also results in defective osteoid formation. Subperiosteal hemorrhages cause acute pain and swelling mimicking acute osteomyelitis. </p>\n<p>Costo-chondral separation leads to the formation of tender nodules, also known as scorbutic rosary. Rickets also presents with similar nodules at the costochondral junctions known as rachitic rosary. They can be differentiated as scorbutic rosary is more <strong>sharply angulated</strong> and are <strong>painful.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7398","text":"A child presents with mult‍iple painless swellings o​ver the costochondral jun‍ction. Which of the follo‌wing conditions is least ​likely in this child?","raw_text":"A child presents with multiple painless swellings over the costochondral junction. Which of the following conditions is least likely in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ric‍kets","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary hyperparathyroidis‍m","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypophos‍phatemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemangiop‍ericytoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>Multiple painless swellings at the costochondral junction is suggestive of<strong> rachitic rosary </strong>in this child. It is not seen in primary hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>Rachitic rosary is seen in <strong>rickets</strong> and all the conditions leading to<strong> hypocalcemia</strong> and <strong>hypophosphatemia</strong>. <strong>Hemangiopericytoma</strong> causes hypophosphataemic vitamin D-resistant rickets.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7395","text":"A 7-month-old child was br‍ought with frontal bossin​g and craniotabes. X-ray ‍of the child showed widen‌ing of the wrist joint. W​hich of the following is ‍least likely to be seen i‌n this child?","raw_text":"A 7-month-old child was brought with frontal bossing and craniotabes. X-ray of the child showed widening of the wrist joint. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Widening of‍ joint space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Widely open‍ fontanelles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Convu‍lsions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowing ‍of legs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bowing of legs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bowing of legs</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>rickets</strong>. Lower limb deformities appear when the child starts to walk. Hence, bowing of legs will be less commonly seen in infants.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features </strong>of<strong> rickets:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lassitude</li>\n<li>Muscle weakness</li>\n<li>Convulsions</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism secondary to hypocalcemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0836","text":"A 3-year-old child was bro‍ught to OPD with history ​of multiple bone fracture‍s. Examination showed blu‌e sclera, deformed legs a​nd deformed teeth. What i‍s the pathophysiology beh‌ind this condition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child was brought to OPD with history of multiple bone fractures. Examination showed blue sclera, deformed legs and deformed teeth. What is the pathophysiology behind this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defect in bone mineralisat‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defect in osteoclastic act‍ivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defect in enchondral ossif‍ication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defect in collagenous matr‍ix of bone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Defect in collagenous matrix of bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbc0add8d87a48a8bcb88cee759bbc04x1280x1434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Defect in collagenous matrix of bone</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta</strong>. It occurs due to <strong>defect in the collagenous matrix</strong> of the bone.</p>\n<p>Mutations in <strong>COL1A1 / COL1A2</strong> results in defective <strong>collagen l</strong> synthesis. There are 12 types of osteogenesis imperfecta with <strong>type 1</strong> being the most common. Triad of<strong> fragile bones, blue sclera</strong> and <strong>deafness</strong> is known as <strong>Van der Hoeve's syndrome</strong>. Treatment includes <strong>bisphosphonate therapy</strong> and orthopedic rehabilitation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1674","text":"A 5-year-old boy with path‍ological fracture is diag​nosed with marble bone di‍sease. What is the pathop‌hysiology behind this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy with pathological fracture  is diagnosed with marble bone disease. What is the pathophysiology behind this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased osteoblastic act‍ivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased collagen product‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased osteoclastic act‍ivity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased collagen product‍ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased osteoclastic activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd5a5df04a9d4f2f895ae8adaac7d349x843x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased osteoclastic activity</strong></p><p><strong>Marble bone disease</strong> is characterised by<strong> decreased osteoclastic activity. </strong>It is also known as osteopetrosis /<strong> Albers-Schönberg disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an inherited disorder which may present either in infancy or adulthood. There is continuous new bone formation without bone remodeling. This leads to increased bone density. They are prone to pathological fractures.</p>\n<p>X-ray spine of a patient with osteopetrosis showing<strong> endobone (bone within a bone)</strong> appearance is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0746","text":"An infant with hydrocephal‍us is found to have pancy​topenia and hepatosplenom‍egaly. On further investi‌gations, he is also found​ to have rugger-jersey sp‍ine on X-ray. What is the‌ most probable diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"An infant with hydrocephalus is found to have pancytopenia and hepatosplenomegaly. On further investigations, he is also found to have rugger-jersey spine on X-ray. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop‍etrosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal oste‍odystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paget's‍ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesi‍s imperfecta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteopetrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57283a17ca9645eb9a3329287d0ea23ax720x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46e6ebdce58640459e2ecb73c0b73861x487x387.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteopetrosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of the infantile form of <strong>osteopetrosis</strong>. It is characterised by decreased osteoclastic activity due to defect in carbonic anhydrase ll proton pump.</p>\n<p>The excessive new bone formation with defective resorption leads to <strong>abnormal development of the skull</strong>, especially causing narrowing of the foramina of the skull. This leads to various complications like <strong>hydrocephalus</strong>, cranial nerve compression leading to <strong>deafness </strong>and<strong> facial palsy</strong>, sinus blockage, etc. </p>\n<p>Failure of bone resorption results in poorly formed medullary cavity. This leads to <strong>defective hematopoiesis</strong> in the medullary cavity and stimulation of <strong>extramedullary hematopoiesis</strong>. Hence, the infant presents with <strong>pancytopenia and hepatosplenomegaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Abnormal bone density may also lead to altered vasculature causing complications like <strong>osteitis of the mandible</strong>.</p>\n<p>Excess bone density also leads to <strong>endobone </strong>appearance and <strong>rugger jersey appearance</strong> of vertebrae on radiograph. X-ray spine of a patient with osteopetrosis is given below.</p>\n<p><strong>Renal osteodystrophy</strong> also shows rugger jersey appearance of vertebrae on x-ray. However, renal osteodystrophy is a complication of <strong>chronic kidney disease</strong> and the patient is an <strong>infant</strong>. So the likely answer in the patient in the given scenario is osteopetrosis (Option B). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-yiOXcfV","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"21_disorders_of_the_liver","title":"21.Disorders Of The Liver","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5496","text":"A mother observed unusual ‌redness on the palms of h​er young child while putt‍ing him to sleep. She bri‌ngs him to your OPD and o​n examination, you find m​ild abdominal distension,​ splenomegaly, and pedal ‍edema. You admit the chil​d for further evaluation,​ but on the night of admi‍ssion, he suddenly starts‍ vomiting fresh blood. Wh‍ich is the most likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A mother observed unusual redness on the palms of her young child while putting him to sleep. She brings him to your OPD and on examination, you find mild abdominal distension, splenomegaly, and pedal edema. You admit the child for further evaluation, but on the night of admission, he suddenly starts vomiting fresh blood. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mallory ‌Weis tear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophagea‌l varices","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kala‌ azar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A‌ML","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esophageal varices","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esophageal varices</strong></p><p>A child presenting with <strong>hematemesis and splenomegaly</strong> is presumed to have <strong>variceal bleeding</strong> unless proved otherwise. Bleeding from oesophagal varices is the most common presentation of portal hypertension.</p>\n<p><strong>Palmar erythema</strong>(redness of the palms) and <strong>ascites</strong> (swelling of the abdomen) are features of liver failure seen in this patient. </p>\n<p>Upper GI endoscopy is the preferred diagnostic test for oesophagal varices. </p>\n<p>The management of acute variceal bleeding-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Attain hemodynamic stability through blood transfusion/fluids</li>\n<li>Vasoactive drugs (e.g., <strong>octreotide</strong>)</li>\n<li>Endoscopy to perform ligation or sclerotherapy</li>\n<li>Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt should be considered for variceal bleeding refractory to medical and endoscopic therapy.     </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Secondary prophylaxis to reduce the recurrence of bleeding includes-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nonselective β blockade (propranolol, nadolol)</li>\n<li>Obliteration of varices through serial treatment via ligation or sclerotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5497","text":"A 10-year-old boy was brou‌ght by his mother to the ​ER after having a sudden ‍episode of vomiting blood‌. On examination, splenom​egaly is detected with en​gorged superficial veins ​around his umbilicus. Whi‍ch is the investigation o​f choice in this patient?​","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy was brought by his mother to the ER after having a sudden episode of vomiting blood. On examination, splenomegaly is detected with engorged superficial veins around his umbilicus. Which is the investigation of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdomi‌nal USG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capsule ‌endoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tripha‌sic CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper GI ‌endoscopy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Upper GI endoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Upper GI endoscopy</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A child presenting with <strong>hematemesis and splenomegaly</strong> is presumed to have <strong>variceal bleeding</strong> and hence, <strong>upper GI endoscopy </strong>is the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The upper GI endoscopy provides a definitive diagnosis and an option to treat via <strong>band ligation </strong>or<strong> sclerotherapy</strong><strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Capsule endoscopy, ultrasound, CT, MRI, and elastography are not recommended because of their suboptimal accuracy compared to endoscopy. Capsule endoscopy is used to investigate an occult/obscure gastrointestinal bleed mostly suspected to be in the small intestine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5506","text":"Unconjugated hyperbilirubi‌nemia, which did not subs​ide even after 3 weeks of‍ birth, was observed in a‌ neonate. On investigatin​g, liver enzymes, PT/INR ​and albumin levels were n​ormal. No hemolysis was s‍een on a peripheral blood​ smear. A drop in bilirub​in level was observed wit‍hin a week after treating‍ with phenobarbital. What‍ is the most likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which did not subside even after 3 weeks of birth, was observed in a neonate. On investigating, liver enzymes, PT/INR and albumin levels were normal. No hemolysis was seen on a peripheral blood smear. A drop in bilirubin level was observed within a week after treating with phenobarbital. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rotor s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crigler Na‌jjar type 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dubin Johns‌on syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crigler Na‌jjar type 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crigler Najjar type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crigler Najjar type 2</strong></p><p>Jaundice persisiting <strong>3 weeks after birth</strong> is suggestive of <strong>pathological jaundice</strong>. Among the given options, <strong>Crigler Najjar type</strong> <strong>2 </strong>presents with <strong>unconjugated</strong> hyperbilirubinemia which <strong>responds</strong> to <strong>p</strong><strong>henobarbital therapy</strong>.<strong> </strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Dubin Johnson</strong> (option C) and <strong>Rotor </strong>(option A) syndromes lead to <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. </p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Crigler Najjar type 2 is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease with <strong>non-hemolytic</strong>, <strong>unconjugated</strong> hyperbilirubinemia caused by homozygous missense mutations in the enzyme UDP <strong>UGT1A1</strong> resulting in reduced (partial) enzymatic activity.</span></p>\n<p>The differences between Crigler-Najjar type 1 and type 2 are described in the table below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Crigler Najjar Type 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Crigler Najjar Type 2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Autosomal recessive</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Autosomal recessive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">UDPGT is <strong>absent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">UDPGT is <strong>reduced</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Less severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Pale stools</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Normal-coloured stools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">May proceed to <strong>kernicterus </strong>and <strong>does not</strong><strong> respond to phenobarbitone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Does not proceed to kernicterus and <strong>is responsive to phenobarbitone</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5565","text":"A 14-year-old boy was brou‌ght to the OPD by his fat​her who observed a yellow‍ish tinge to his skin and‌ eyes. He gives a history​ of recent onset fatigue ​after playing football fo​r a few minutes and canno‍t stay active on the play​ground for a long time li​ke before. Investigations‍ revealed isolated conjug‍ated hyperbilirubinemia b‍ut are normal otherwise. ​Liver histology revealed ​a black pigment. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy was brought to the OPD by his father who observed a yellowish tinge to his skin and eyes. He gives a history of recent onset fatigue after playing football for a few minutes and cannot stay active on the playground for a long time like before. Investigations revealed isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia but are normal otherwise. Liver histology revealed a black pigment. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gilbert ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crigler-Naj‌jar syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dubin-Johns‌on syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rotor s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dubin-Johnson syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dubin-Johnson syndrome</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The presence of fatigue, conjugated hyperbilirubinemia and black pigment on liver biopsy, termed as \"<strong>black liver\"</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Dubin–Johnson syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inherited defect in hepatocyte secretion of bilirubin glucuronide. Disease results from absent function of <strong>multiple drug-resistant protein 2</strong>, an ATP dependent canalicular transporter.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most commonly reported symptoms are abdominal pain and fatigue, jaundice, dark urine, and slight enlargement of the liver.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inherited conjugated hyperbilirubinemia-</p>\n<table style=\"height: 181px;\" width=\"374\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.875px;\"><strong>Dubin–Johnson syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.140625px;\"><strong>Rotor syndrome</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.875px;\">Autosomal recessive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.140625px;\">Autosomal recessive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.875px;\">Absent function of<strong><strong> multiple drug-resistant protein (</strong></strong><strong>MRP-2)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.140625px;\">Defect in anion transport</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.875px;\"><strong>Black liver</strong> on biopsy (due to accumulation of epinephrine metabolite)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.140625px;\">Black liver is not seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.875px;\">Normal levels of coproporphyrin in urine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.140625px;\">Increased levels of coproporphyrin in urine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.875px;\">Gall bladder is not visualized on oral cholecystography</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.140625px;\">Gall bladder is visualized on oral cholecystography</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9301","text":"You are an intern posted i‌n the paediatrics departm​ent and a child with Dubi‍n-Johnson syndrome is adm‌itted to your unit. Durin​g rounds, the attending p​aediatrician asks you whi​ch molecular defect cause‍s liver disease in this p​atient. Your answer would​ be","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the paediatrics department and a child with Dubin-Johnson syndrome is admitted to your unit. During rounds, the attending paediatrician asks you which molecular defect causes liver disease in this patient. Your answer would be","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AT‌P7A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AT‌P7B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AB‌CC2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"SERP‌INA 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ABCC2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ABCC2</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Molecular defect causing liver disease in <strong>Dubin–Johnson syndrome</strong> is<strong> ABCC2</strong> (codes for canalicular protein with ATP-binding cassette), it leads to the mutation of <strong>multiple drug-resistant protein 2 (MRP2) receptor</strong>. Hence, there is a defect in the transfer of conjugated bilirubin from the liver to bile in Dubin–Johnson syndrome.</p>\n<p>Important diseases and their molecular defects:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 293px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">ATP7A </td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\">Menke’s disease </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">ATP7B </td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\">Wilson disease </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">CFTR </td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\">Cystic fibrosis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">SERPINA 1 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\">α-1 antitrypsin deficiency </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">ABCC2 (MRP2 protein defect) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: center;\">Dubin Johnson syndrome </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6905","text":"All are true about transie‌nt familial neonatal hype​rbilirubinemia, except:","raw_text":"All are true about transient familial neonatal hyperbilirubinemia, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased unconjugated bil‌irubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurs due to maternal ant‌ibody against fetal UGT1A​1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is also known as Lucey ‌Driscoll syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stop breast feeding when j‌aundice sets in","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stop breast feeding when jaundice sets in","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stop breast feeding when jaundice sets in</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Lucey Driscoll syndrome</strong> or <strong>transient familial neonatal hyperbilirubinemia</strong> is a self-limiting condition and there is <strong>no need to stop breastfeeding. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Lucey Driscoll syndrome</strong> is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder affecting enzymes involved in <strong>bilirubin metabolism</strong>. It causes transient familial neonatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and <strong>jaundice</strong> occurs during the <strong>first 4 days of life</strong>. It is due to <strong>maternal antibodies</strong> against <strong>fetal UGT1A1</strong> (UDP glucuronosyltransferase family 1 member A1).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1126","text":"Lately, a teacher observed‌ that an anxious young gi​rl had started drooling s‍aliva from her mouth whil‌e listening in the classr​oom. She had also develop​ed a deterioration in her​ handwriting while taking‍ down notes. Her mother w​as informed and she broug​ht her child to your clin‍ic. On examination, she h‍ad tremors and mild hepat‍omegaly. A slit-lamp exam​ination was done and show​ed the following picture.‌ The right statement abou​t this disease is -","raw_text":"Lately, a teacher observed that an anxious young girl had started drooling saliva from her mouth while listening in the classroom. She had also developed a deterioration in her handwriting while taking down notes. Her mother was informed and she brought her child to your clinic. On examination, she had tremors and mild hepatomegaly. A slit-lamp examination was done and showed the following picture. The right statement about this disease is -","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is autoso‌mal dominant.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum ceruplasmin levels a‌re high.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coombs-positive hemolytic ‌anaemia may be present.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient can be treated wit‌h triethylenetetramine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patient can be treated with triethylenetetramine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4958c7582ad4ddbb7614dd1d63f1f1d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patient can be treated with triethylenetetramine</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>Kayser-Fleischer ring</strong>, which is characteristic of <strong>Wilson's disease</strong>. It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder, characterised by <strong>low serum ceruloplasmin</strong> levels. <strong>Coomb's negative hemolytic anemia</strong> may be present. The patient can be treated with <strong>triethylenetetramine</strong> also known as <strong>trientine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Wilson’s disease is caused due to a <strong>loss-of-function mutation</strong> in the <strong>ATP7B</strong> gene on <strong>Chr.13q</strong>. The gene encodes a copper transporting ATPase (ATP7B) which has 2 functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Maintains copper in bound form by binding copper to apoceruloplasmin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Helps excrete free copper in bile.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thus, an ATP7B deficiency leads to <strong>excess deposition of free copper</strong> within tissues.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 572.969px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\"><strong>Lab markers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\"><strong>Change in Wilson's disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.969px;\"><strong>Reason</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Serum ceruloplasmin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.969px;\">ATP7B deficiency results in reduced bound copper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">24h urinary copper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.969px;\">Excess free copper excreted in urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Total serum copper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.969px;\">Due to marked reduction in ceruloplasmin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The drugs used in the management of Wilson's disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chelating agents as first-line drugs\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D-penicillamine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Trientine </strong>or triethylenetetramine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Elemental <strong>zinc</strong> as maintenance therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5568","text":"A mother observed deterior‌ation in her young child'​s academic performance an‍d loss of interest in eve‌ryday activities for the ​last few weeks. She has a​lso lost the ability to p​lay catch with her brothe‍r and complains of muscle​ weakness. There is mild ​hepatomegaly noted on exa‍mination. Her serum cerul‍oplasmin levels were redu‍ced. All of the following​ manifestations can occur​ in a patient with this d‌isease except?","raw_text":"A mother observed deterioration in her young child's academic performance and loss of interest in everyday activities for the last few weeks. She has also lost the ability to play catch with her brother and complains of muscle weakness. There is mild hepatomegaly noted on examination. Her serum ceruloplasmin levels were reduced. All of the following manifestations can occur in a patient with this disease except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sparse depi‌gmented hair","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shrinkage‌ of liver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascites and portal hyperte‌nsion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal Fanco‌ni syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sparse depigmented hair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sparse depigmented hair</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The presence of <strong>reduced ceruloplasmin</strong> levels, <strong>motor incoordination,</strong> and <strong>hepatomegaly</strong> suggest the patient is suffering from <strong>Wilson’s disease. </strong><strong>Sparse depigmented hair</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a feature of Wilson's disease. It is a feature of <strong>Menke's disease.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Wilson's disease is caused due to a mutation in the <strong>ATP7B gene</strong> that leads to <strong>excess deposition of free copper</strong> within tissues.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It has the following clinical features:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hepatic<br/>(most common)</td>\n<td>\n<p>Jaundice<br/>Abdominal pain<br/>Hepatomegaly<br/>Fatigue<br/>Ascites<br/>Variceal bleed<br/>Coagulation defects</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neurological</td>\n<td>Dysarthria<br/>Dystonia<br/>Parkinsonism<br/>Subcortical dementia<br/>Depression<br/>Wing beating tremors<br/>Decreased scholastic performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ophthalmic</td>\n<td>Kayser-Fleischer (KF) rings<br/>Sunflower cataract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Others</td>\n<td>Hemolytic anemia (Coombs negative)<br/>Acute pancreatitis<br/>Fanconi's syndrome<br/>Hyperparathyroidism<br/>Cardiomyopathy<br/>Spontaneous abortions<br/>Infertility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Wilson's disease may also present as cirrhosis and hepatic failure, where shrinkage of liver occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5573","text":"In Wilson Disease, hepatic‌ copper content usually e​xceeds____ μg/g dry weigh‍t.","raw_text":"In Wilson Disease, hepatic copper content usually exceeds____ μg/g dry weight.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌50","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌50","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3‌50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4‌50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 250","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 250</strong></p><p>In Wilson disease, <strong>hepatic copper content</strong> usually exceeds<strong> 250 μg/g </strong>dry weight.</p>\n<p><strong>Hepatic copper accumulation </strong>is the<strong> hallmark </strong>of Wilson disease. It is measured using neutron activation analysis/atomic absorption spectrometry.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5575","text":"A male infant was born to ‌a mother with chronic hep​atitis B infection. The i‍nfant is suspected to hav‌e been exposed at the tim​e of delivery. At 4 month​s, which of the following​ viral markers signifies ‍ongoing viral replication​ in the infant?","raw_text":"A male infant was born to a mother with chronic hepatitis B infection. The infant is suspected to have been exposed at the time of delivery. At 4 months, which of the following viral markers signifies ongoing viral replication in the infant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‌-HBs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti‌-HBc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HBe‌ Ag","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HBs‌ Ag","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HBe Ag","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HBe Ag</strong></p><p><strong>HBeAg</strong> signifies the ongoing viral replication in case of hepatitis B infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5576","text":"A 9-year-old boy was broug‌ht to the OPD by his moth​er with a history of fati‍gue and myalgia for a few‌ days, associated with ra​shes that started a week ​before it. On examination​, coppery-red coloured sk‍in lesions were seen on t​he face and limbs includi​ng palms and soles. His s‍erology was positive for ‍HBsAg. What is the diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy was brought to the OPD by his mother with a history of fatigue and myalgia for a few days, associated with rashes that started a week before it. On examination, coppery-red coloured skin lesions were seen on the face and limbs including palms and soles. His serology was positive for HBsAg. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythema i‌nfectiosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gianotti- Cr‌osti syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Henoch schon‌lein purpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema m‌ultiformae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gianotti- Crosti syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3d198ab8f874b66b23876ab97587128x603x438.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gianotti- Crosti syndrome</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The coppery-red colored papules along with a <strong>positive HBsAg</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Gianotti-Crosti syndrome</strong>, also known as papular acrodermatitis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is an immunologic reaction to viral infections. The most common associations are Epstein-Barr virus,<strong> hepatitis B virus</strong>, Coxsackievirus A16, and parainfluenza virus, as well as with many childhood immunizations.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Skin lesions are monomorphic, firm, coppery red papules sized 1-10 mm. They occur in crops and may become profuse and coalesce into plaques, forming a symmetric eruption on the face, ears, buttocks, and limbs, including the palms and soles. The trunk, scalp, and mucous membrane are relatively spared.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The image given below shows the coppery red papules seen in Gianotti-Crosti syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5577","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs are not used in the tr​eatment of hepatitis B?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs are not used in the treatment of hepatitis B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interfe‌ron α2b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adef‌ovir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Riba‌virin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ribavirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ribavirin</strong></p><p><strong>Ribavirin</strong> is a drug used in the treatment of <strong>hepatitis C</strong>, not hepatitis B.</p>\n<p>Indications for starting antiviral therapy in chronic hepatitis B are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients without cirrhosis</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HBeAg + with HBV DNA &gt;20,000 IU/mL and ALT level &gt;2 times the upper limit of normal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HBeAg -ve with HBV DNA &gt;2000 IU/mL and ALT level &gt;2 times the upper limit of normal</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with compensated cirrhosis and an HBV DNA &gt;2000 IU/mL</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients developing acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drugs used in the treatment of hepatitis B are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line treatment: Entecavir or tenofovir </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Other drugs:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pegylated interferon-alpha</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Telbivudine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Lamivudine</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Adefovir</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1086","text":"A young child developed su‌dden bouts of vomiting an​d was brought to the ER. ‍Her condition rapidly wor‌sened as she developed se​izures. She had a history​ of influenza infection o​ne week ago. The patient'‍s liver biopsy will show ​______.","raw_text":"A young child developed sudden bouts of vomiting and was brought to the ER.  Her condition rapidly worsened as she developed seizures. She had a history of influenza infection one week ago. The patient's liver biopsy will show ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-alcoholic steatohepati‌tis (NASH)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Centri zonal hemorrhagic n‌ecrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marked microvesicular stea‌totis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ring gr‌anuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Marked microvesicular steatotis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Marked microvesicular steatotis</strong></p><p>Severe vomiting in a 5-year-old child, rapidly worsening to seizures, with a history of viral infection (influenza) is suggestive of <strong>Reye's syndrome.</strong> Hence, liver biopsy will show<strong> marked microvesicular steatosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reye's syndrome is a secondary mitochondrial hepatopathy. It is precipitated due to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Viral infection (<strong>influenza, adenovirus, herpes, </strong>coxsackievirus A, Epstein–Barr virus)</li>\n<li><strong>Salicylate</strong> or antiemetic use</li>\n<li>Inborn error of metabolism such as <strong>single enzyme defects of ß oxidation.</strong></li>\n<li>Toxins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is an acute onset of vomiting and encephalopathy, rapidly progressing to seizures, coma, and death. There is<strong> no jaundice</strong>.</p>\n<p>Investigations</p>\n<ul>\n<li>↑ ALT and AST</li>\n<li>↑ Ammonia</li>\n<li>normal bilirubin</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>PT prolonged</li>\n<li>INR increased</li>\n<li>Liver biopsy: marked microvesicular steatosis. Necrosis is not seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5579","text":"A 12-year old girl was rus‌hed to the ER by her pare​nts due to hematemesis. T‍hey gave a history of wea‌kness and fatigue for the​ last few weeks. On exami​nation, the patient had s​pider telangiectasias and‍ mild palmar erythema. Li​ver function tests showed​ the following results- A‍ST-1100IU/L, serum biliru‍bin- 8mg/dl, albumin- dec‍reased, globulin- elevate​d, PT- prolonged along wi​th Anti-Nuclear Antibody ‌positive. What is the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 12-year old girl was rushed to the ER by her parents due to hematemesis. They gave a history of weakness and fatigue for the last few weeks. On examination, the patient had spider telangiectasias and mild palmar erythema. Liver function tests showed the following results- AST-1100IU/L, serum bilirubin- 8mg/dl, albumin- decreased, globulin- elevated, PT- prolonged along with Anti-Nuclear Antibody positive. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatiti‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"S‌LE","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 2 autoimmune hepatiti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis</strong></p><p>The symptoms of liver failure and portal hypertension along with a positive anti-nuclear antibody<strong>(ANA)</strong> is suggestive of Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis. It is associated with- </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated serum aminotransaminase concentrations.</li>\n<li>Liver-associated serum autoantibodies<strong>.</strong></li>\n<li>Hypergammaglobulinemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5581","text":"A 2-week-old low-birth-wei‌ght neonate presents with​ jaundice. Stools are pal‍e/clay coloured. Liver bi‌opsy shows loss of archit​ecture. The most likely d​iagnosis is_______.","raw_text":"A 2-week-old low-birth-weight neonate presents with jaundice. Stools are pale/clay coloured. Liver biopsy shows loss of architecture. The most likely diagnosis is_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neonatal ‌hepatitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extrahepatic biliary atres‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilson ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary hemochromatosis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neonatal hepatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neonatal hepatitis</strong></p><p>Neonatal jaundice in a low-birth-weight (LBW) neonate with consistently pale/clay coloured stools with liver biopsy finding of loss of architecture is suggestive of <strong>neonatal hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Wilson disease and hereditary hemochromatosis will not present with jaundice in the neonatal stage. Hence, they can be excluded. Injury to liver cells can present the clinical and histologic picture of “neonatal hepatitis.”</p>\n<p>Differentiation between Neonatal hepatitis and EHBA</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>Neonatal hepatitis </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Extrahepatic biliary atresia (EHBA) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<p>It implies intrahepatic cholestasis which includes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatic injury due to metabolic, viral, or idiopathic causes</li>\n<li>Intrahepatic bile duct injury</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Obliteration of the entire extrahepatic biliary tree at or above the porta hepatis(85% cases) OR</li>\n<li>distal segmental bile duct obliteration with patent<br/>extrahepatic ducts up to the porta hepatis(uncommon)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical features </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Family history is present</p>\n<p>Premature or LBW baby</p>\n<p>Clay-coloured stools </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Family history absent</p>\n<p>Normal appearance, without looking sick</p>\n<p>Persistent acholic stools </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Investigations</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Liver biopsy: Loss of liver architecture </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>HIDA scan: screening test </p>\n<p>USG: triangular cord sign </p>\n<p>Liver biopsy: bile duct proliferation </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5582","text":"A mother is concerned abou‌t her 3-week-old baby hav​ing yellowish discolourat‍ion of skin and eyes. On ‌examination, the baby is ​chubby and playful. Stool​s are pale in color. Furt​her evaluation with USG s‍hows triangular cord sign​ and liver biopsy shows b​ile duct proliferation. T‍he most likely diagnosis ‍is ______.","raw_text":"A mother is concerned about her 3-week-old baby having yellowish discolouration of skin and eyes. On examination, the baby is chubby and playful. Stools are pale in color. Further evaluation with USG shows triangular cord sign and liver biopsy shows bile duct proliferation. The most likely diagnosis is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neonatal ‌hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hallervorden Spatz disease‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilson ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extrahepatic biliary atres‌ia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extrahepatic biliary atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extrahepatic biliary atresia</strong></p><p>Neonatal jaundice in a neonate <strong>without a sick-looking appearance and with</strong> <strong>acholic stools, </strong>USG showing <strong>triangular cord sign,</strong> and liver biopsy showing<strong> bile duct proliferation</strong> is suggestive of biliary obstruction, which may be seen in <strong>biliary atresia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Wilson disease and Hallervorden Spatz disease (neurodegeneration with iron accumulation in the brain) do not present with jaundice in the neonatal stage. Hence, they can be excluded.</p>\n<p>Differentiation between neonatal hepatitis and EHBA</p>\n<table style=\"width: 364px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Neonatal hepatitis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Extrahepatic biliary atresia (EHBA) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 204px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 204px;\">\n<p>It implies intrahepatic cholestasis which includes:</p>\n<p>Hepatic injury due to metabolic, viral, or idiopathic cause</p>\n<p>Intrahepatic bile duct injury</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 204px;\">\n<p>Obliteration of the entire extrahepatic biliary tree at or above the porta hepatis(85% cases)  OR</p>\n<p>Distal segmental bile duct obliteration with patent<br/>extrahepatic ducts up to the porta hepatis (uncommon)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 323px; height: 38px;\"> <strong>Clinical features </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 146px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 146px;\">\n<p>Family history is present</p>\n<p>Premature or LBW baby</p>\n<p>Pigmented stools </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 146px;\">\n<p>Family history absent</p>\n<p>Normal appearance, without looking sick</p>\n<p>Persistent acholic stools </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 11px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 323px; height: 11px;\">\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 128px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147px; height: 128px;\">Liver biopsy: Loss of liver architecture </td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 128px;\">\n<p>USG: triangular cord sign</p>\n<p>HIDA scan: screening test </p>\n<p>Liver biopsy: bile duct proliferation </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Liver biospy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biliary atresia is characterized by proliferation of the bile ductule, presence of bile plugs, and portal or perilobular edema and fibrosis, with the <strong>basic hepatic lobular architecture intact</strong>.</li>\n<li>In neonatal hepatitis, there is severe, diffuse hepatocellular disease, with<strong> distortion of lobular architecture</strong>, marked infiltration of inflammatory cells, and focal hepatocellular necrosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5583","text":"A mother leaves her 3-year‌-old son unattended for 2​0 mins and later finds hi‍m holding a blue coloured‌ empty strip of tablets w​ith a few tablets scatter​ed on the floor. A few ho​urs later he is rushed to‍ the ER with nausea and v​omiting. As an intern tak​ing the history, you susp‍ect accidental ingestion ‍of paracetamol tablets. T‍he pattern of liver injur​y you would expect in thi​s case is","raw_text":"A mother leaves her 3-year-old son unattended for 20 mins and later finds him holding a blue coloured empty strip of tablets with a few tablets scattered on the floor. A few hours later he is rushed to the ER with nausea and vomiting.  As an intern taking the history, you suspect accidental ingestion of paracetamol tablets. The pattern of liver injury you would expect in this case is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chole‌stasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Centrilobul‌ar necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Microvesicul‌ar steatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibr‌osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Centrilobular necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Centrilobular necrosis</strong></p><p>The pattern of liver injury caused by paracetamol (acetaminophen) is <strong>centrilobular necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acetaminophen toxicity results from the formation of a highly reactive intermediate metabolite, N -acetyl-p -benzoquinone imine (<strong>NAPQI</strong>). In therapeutic use, only about 5% is metabolized by the hepatic <strong>cytochrome P450</strong> enzyme CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which is then immediately joined with glutathione to form a nontoxic mercapturic acid conjugate.</p>\n<p>In overdose, glutathione stores are overwhelmed, and free NAPQI is able to combine with hepatic macromolecules to produce <strong>hepatocellular necrosis</strong>. Single acute toxic dose of APAP is generally considered to be <strong>&gt;200 mg/kg</strong> in children. </p>\n<p>Patterns of hepatic drug injury:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Disease </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Drug </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Centrilobular necrosis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Acetaminophen,</strong> Halothane </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Microvesicular steatosis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Valproic acid </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\">Acute hepatitis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\">Isoniazid </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Fibrosis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Methotrexate </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\"><strong>Cholestasis </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\">Chlorpromazine, <strong>Erythromycin, </strong>Estrogen </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center;\">Veno occlusive disease </td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center;\">Busulphan, Cyclophosphamide </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\">Portal and hepatic vein thrombosis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\">Estrogen, Androgen </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\">Biliary sludge </td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 15px;\">Ceftriaxone </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\">Hepatic adenoma/hepatocellular carcinoma </td>\n<td style=\"width: 192px; text-align: center; height: 33px;\">Oral contraceptive pills, Anabolic steroids </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5584","text":"You are examining a young ‌child who presented to th​e ER with a high fever an‍d abdominal pain. The pat‌ient winces on abdominal ​palpation of the right up​per quadrant. USG showed ​hydrops of the gallbladde‍r. All of the following c​onditions are associated ​with hydrops of the gallb‍ladder except __________.‍","raw_text":"You are examining a young child who presented to the ER with a high fever and abdominal pain. The patient winces on abdominal palpation of the right upper quadrant. USG showed hydrops of the gallbladder. All of the following conditions are associated with hydrops of the gallbladder except\n__________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Group A streptococcal phar‌yngitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kawasaki‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total parenteral nutrition‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cystic fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cystic fibrosis</strong></p><p>Hydrops of the gallbladder is<strong> not</strong> associated with<strong> cystic fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hydrops of gallbladder is the acute acalculous <strong>non-inflammatory</strong> distension of the gallbladder, which occurs in infants and children. It is characterized by the absence of calculi, bacterial infections or congenital anomalies of the biliary system.</p>\n<p>Hydrops is distinguished from acalculous cholecystitis by the absence of a significant inflammatory process and a generally <strong>benign prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Conditions associated with hydrops of GB:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections: <strong>Streptococcal pharyngitis</strong>, staphylococcal infection, ascariasis, leptospirosis, typhoid fever, and viral hepatitis</li>\n<li><strong>Kawasaki disease </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Total parenteral nutrition, prolonged fasting</strong></li>\n<li>Sickle cell crisis, thalassemia</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Mesenteric adenitis, necrotizing enterocolitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In cystic fibrosis cholelithiasis, cholecystitis, micro gallbladder is seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5585","text":"The commonest indication f‌or liver transplantation ​in children is ______","raw_text":"The commonest indication for liver transplantation in children is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alagille‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biliary‌ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caroli ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatcellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Biliary atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Biliary atresia</strong></p><p><strong>Biliary atresia</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> indication for<strong> liver transplantation in children</strong>.</p>\n<p>Extrahepatic biliary atresia (EHBA) is defined as a partial or total <strong>absence </strong>of permeable<strong> bile duct </strong>between the porta hepatis and the duodenum. It is due to <strong>inflammatory fibrosis</strong> of the biliary tree. It leads to obstruction to bile flow thereby causing the leaking of <strong>conjugated bilirubin</strong> into the circulation.</p>\n<p>Infants have symptoms of <strong>jaundice</strong>,<strong> pale stools</strong>, and <strong>dark urine</strong> right after birth. In advanced disease, infants present with failure to thrive, hepatomegaly, and ascites due to liver cirrhosis.</p>\n<p><strong>HIDA scan</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for extrahepatic biliary atresia. An<strong> intraoperative cholangiogram </strong>is a gold standard investigation for diagnosis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 261px; width: 379.417px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.7656px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 587.417px; text-align: center; height: 29.7656px;\"><strong>Management of extrahepatic biliary atresia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 87px; height: 54px;\">Type I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atresia restricted to the common bile duct</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274.417px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 87px; height: 54px;\">Type II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atresia of the common hepatic duct</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 274.417px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Kasai procedure</strong> (Roux-en-Y hepatoportoenterostomy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 87px; height: 54px;\">Type III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atresia of the right and left hepatic duct</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Children with biliary atresia, who do not achieve successful drainage after hepatoportoenterostomy (<strong>Kasai's procedure</strong>), require liver transplantation within the first year of life.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5586","text":"A young boy was admitted w‌ith complaints of fever a​nd abdominal pain for 2 d‍ays. ESR and CRP were ele‌vated and CT Abdomen obta​ined was as shown in the ​picture. Which of the fol​lowing is not a common fe‍ature in the given condit​ion?","raw_text":"A young boy was admitted with complaints of fever and abdominal pain for 2 days. ESR and CRP were elevated and CT Abdomen obtained was as shown in the picture. Which of the following is not a common feature in the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepato‌megaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jaun‌dice","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Leukoc‌ytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Jaundice","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b5b1e5f058654445ac28e5ed9783bc00.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Jaundice</strong></p><p>The symptoms of fever and abdominal pain and a hypodense lesion on the CT are suggestive of a Liver abscess in this patient. </p>\n<p>Signs and symptoms of a liver abscess include fever, chills, night sweats, malaise, nausea, hepatomegaly, as well as pain and tenderness in the right upper quadrant. They also show elevated inflammatory markers, hypoalbuminemia and leukocytosis. However, <strong>jaundice is uncommon</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ij8aZjFn","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"21_extravascular_hemolysis","title":"21.Extravascular Hemolysis","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MC0363","text":"The peripheral smear of a ​patient with chronic fati​gue is shown below. This ​marked finding is seen in​ all of the conditions ex‌cept","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient with chronic fatigue is shown below. This marked finding is seen in all of the conditions except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sickle ce​ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary s​pherocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog​lobinuria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0bafaa69180342859842b864f136763e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d01f70eb8ec4a6cbd79347cf771d171x799x596.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/432a8af8c8dd4cc4bce045fffbfcc772x610x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear shows<strong> Howell–Jolly bodies</strong>. They are not seen in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.</p>\n<p>They<strong> </strong>are small nuclear remnants seen as inclusions in red cells and are <strong>characteristic</strong> of<strong> asplenia</strong>. They are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sickle cell anaemia - due to auto splenectomy</li>\n<li>Hereditary spherocytosis</li>\n<li>Thalassemia post-splenectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below show Howell–Jolly bodies in peripheral blood smear.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0313","text":"Match the types of thalass​emia with their most comm​on mutations. 1. β+ thala​ssemia A. Terminator muta​tion 2. β 0 thalassemia B‌. Gene deletion 3. α thal​assemia C. Splicing mutat​ion","raw_text":"<p>Match the types of thalassemia with their most common mutations.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 82px;\" width=\"329\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">1. β+ thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">A. Terminator mutation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">2. β<sup>0</sup> thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">B. Gene deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">3. α thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.5px;\">C. Splicing mutation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-C, 2​-A, 3-B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-C, 2​-B, 3-A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-A, 2​-B, 3-C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2​-A, 3-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-C, 2-A, 3-B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-C, 2-A, 3-B</strong></p><p>The most common types of mutations causing thalassemia are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 118px; width: 344px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 33.625px;\">Thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 33.625px;\">Most common mutation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 21px;\">β+ thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 21px;\">Splicing mutation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 25px;\">β<sup>0</sup> thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 25px;\">Terminator mutation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 21px;\">α thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 21px;\">Gene deletion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The different splicing mutations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mutations that destroy the normal RNA splice junctions - completely prevent the production of normal β-globin mRNA resulting in <strong>β<sup>0</sup></strong>-thalassemia</li>\n<li>Mutations that create an ectopic splice site within an intron with normal splice regions - some normal β-globin mRNA is made resulting in <strong>β<sup>+</sup></strong> thalassemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are two subtypes of chain terminator mutations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>New stop codon within an exon</li>\n<li>Small insertions or deletions that shift the mRNA reading frames</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0312","text":"Which chromosome is affect​ed in alpha-thalassemia?","raw_text":"Which chromosome is affected in alpha-thalassemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1​1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 16</strong></p><p><strong>Chromosome 16</strong> contains an identical pair of α-globin genes which is affected in <strong>alpha-thalassemia</strong>. This condition is due to <strong>decreased synthesis</strong> of <strong>alpha chains</strong>. </p>\n<p>The two <strong>β chains</strong> are encoded by a single β-globin gene on <strong>chromosome</strong> <strong>11</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0317","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g α-thalassemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding α-thalassemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free β and γ chains are mo​re soluble than free α ch​ains","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β tetramer is known as HbH​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"α chains are coded by a pa​ir of genes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hb Barts is the most sever​e form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. α chains are coded by a pair of genes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. α chains are coded by a pair of genes</strong></p><p><strong>α chains</strong> are coded by 4 genes i.e. <strong>two pairs </strong>of <strong>genes</strong> located on<strong> chromosome 16.</strong></p>\n<p>Free<strong> β</strong> and <strong>γ</strong> chains are <strong>more soluble</strong> than free α chains. They also form more stable homotetramers. Hence, hemolysis and dyserythropoiesis in α thalassemia are less severe when compared to β thalassemia.</p>\n<p>In <strong>HbH disease</strong>, excess β-globin chains form <strong>β4 tetramers.</strong> They are seen in older children with α-thalassemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrops fetalis</strong> is the <strong>most severe</strong> form of α-thalassemia. It is caused by the deletion of all four α-globin genes. This leads to excess γ-globin chains forming γ-globin tetramers (<strong>hemoglobin Barts</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0315","text":"A woman with an obstetric ​score of G3P2A0L0, in a c​onsanguinous marriage, de​livered a hydropic stillb​orn at 32 weeks of gestat‌ion. Further evaluation r​evealed the presence of H​b Barts in the fetal bloo​d. Which of the following​ globin chains tetramer i‌s found in this condition‍?","raw_text":"A woman with an obstetric score of G3P2A0L0, in a consanguinous marriage, delivered a hydropic stillborn at 32 weeks of gestation.  Further evaluation revealed the presence of Hb Barts in the fetal blood. Which of the following globin chains tetramer is found in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"γ​4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"δ​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. γ4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef2a764ed38c45ab87df568361f3cecdx555x505.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. γ4</strong></p><p><strong>Hb Barts</strong> is a <strong>tetramer</strong> of <strong>γ-globin</strong> chains (γ4). This type of hemoglobin is seen in newborns with the most severe form of <strong>α-thalassemia </strong>and results in hemoglobin Barts<strong> hydrops fetalis. </strong>There is no production of α chains.</p>\n<p>Hb Barts has a higher affinity for oxygen. This leads to intrauterine hypoxia, eventually leading to stillbirth.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows (a) immature red cell precursors in peripheral smear and (b) stillborn hydropic infant in Hb Barts hydrops syndrome. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>HbH disease</strong>, excess<strong> β-globin</strong> chains form β4 tetramers. They are seen in older children with α-thalassemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0367","text":"A 26-year-old Chinese tran​slator presents with feve​r, jaundice and splenomeg​aly. His Hb is 6 g% and p​eripheral smear with bril‌liant cresyl blue is give​n below. Which of the fol​lowing globin chains woul​d you expect to see in th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old Chinese translator presents with fever, jaundice and splenomegaly. His Hb is 6 g% and peripheral smear with brilliant cresyl blue is given below. Which of the following globin chains would you expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β​4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"γ​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"δ​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0658cc1c6e448708608017dca5d1407.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β4</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>extravascular hemolysis</strong> (jaundice and splenomegaly) and peripheral smear showing <strong>golf ball</strong> <strong>cells</strong> is suggestive of <strong>HBH disease</strong>. It is a type of <strong>α thalassemia</strong> where the HbH is composed of <strong>tetramers </strong>of<strong> β chains </strong>(β4).</p>\n<p>Hb-H disease is one type of alpha-thalassaemia with <strong>inactivation</strong> of <strong>3</strong> of the 4 <strong>alleles</strong>. It is more common in the <strong>Asian</strong> population. It usually manifests in the 1st year of life. It causes acute episodes of hemolysis when <strong>triggered</strong> by <strong>infections</strong> or oxidant <strong>drugs</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>decreased α chain</strong> production leads to a relative <strong>excess</strong> of<strong> β chains.</strong> This leads to the formation of <strong>HbH </strong>which has a <strong>high affinity</strong> for <strong>oxygen</strong>. Such RBCs are more prone to <strong>oxidation</strong> which causes <strong>precipitation</strong> of <strong>HbH</strong> to form <strong>golf ball inclusion</strong> bodies. This leads to increased sequestration and phagocytosis in the spleen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9163","text":"A patient has a HbA2 of 3%​ and HbF of 97%. A periph​eral blood smear is taken​. Which of the following ​blood pictures will be se‌en in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient has a HbA2 of 3% and HbF of 97%. A peripheral blood smear is taken. Which of the following blood pictures will be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09914ebca8144a6386f65ddd0243551a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/418a6034a31647d8829d5b06f1c67d05x1280x1157.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e9ac978c6984f2bb20c51348e10e8bdx1280x1280.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/465c8ab170be4eed833399e831eaeaf8x345x235.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 3</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>HbA2 3% </strong>and<strong> HbF 97% </strong>indicates that it is a case of <strong>thalassemia major</strong>. In thalassemia major, hematological findings in image 1 showing <strong>poikilocytes</strong> and image 3 showing<strong> target cells</strong> are seen. Image 2 showing Howell Jolly bodies are seen in <strong>post-splenectomy</strong> thalassemia patients and not in this case.</p>\n<p><strong>Hematological findings</strong> in <strong>thalassemia major</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anisopoikilocytosis</li>\n<li>Target cells (codocytes)</li>\n<li>Nucleated RBC’s</li>\n<li>Dacrocytes</li>\n<li>Basophilic stippling</li>\n<li>Polychromatophilic cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0372","text":"Given below is the hemogra​m of a patient. Calculate​ the Mentzer index. Hb - ​10 g% RBC - 6 million/mm ​3 Reticulocyte count - 0.‌6% MCV - 60 fL MCH - 20 p​g Peripheral smear - micr​ocytic hypochromic pictur​e","raw_text":"<p>Given below is the hemogram of a patient. Calculate the Mentzer index.</p>\n<p>Hb - 10 g%</p>\n<p>RBC - 6 million/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Reticulocyte count - 0.6%</p>\n<p>MCV - 60 fL</p>\n<p>MCH - 20 pg</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear - microcytic hypochromic picture</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">From the given hemogram, the <strong>Mentzer</strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\"><strong> index</strong> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">is <strong>10</strong>. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">The formula for Mentzer index is <strong>MCV (fL) / TRBC (millions/mm<sup>3</sup>)</strong> i.e. 60/6 = 10</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">This index is used to <strong>differentiate</strong> between <strong>i</strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">ron-deficiency </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW148009661\">anemia</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\"> and <strong>beta-thalassemia trait</strong>. If the index is </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\"><strong>&lt;13</strong>, it is most likely to be a <strong>thalassemia</strong> trait.</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW148009661\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"EOP SCXW148009661\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'>Differences between iron deficiency anemia and beta-thalassemia minor (<span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">beta-thalassemia trait)</span></span> are given below.</span></p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 371px; height: 372px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Feature   </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\"><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW148009661\">Iron-deficiency </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW148009661\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW148009661\">anemia</span></span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\"><span class=\"EOP SCXW148009661\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'><strong>Beta-thalassemia minor</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">Peripheral blood smear </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 36px;\">Microcytic hypochromic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 36px;\">Microcytic hypochromic </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">Retic count </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">Low </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">Low </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">RDW </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">Wide </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">Narrow </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">TRBC </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">Low </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">High </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">Mentzer index </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">&gt;14 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">&lt;12 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">Serum ferritin </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">Low </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">Serum iron </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">Low </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">Normal </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">TIBC </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 18px;\">High </td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 31px;\">HbA2 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.797px; height: 31px;\">\n<p>2-3%</p>\n<p>(normal)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.094px; height: 31px;\">&gt;3.5% </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0369","text":"The following test was per​formed with blood sample ​from a patient with anaem​ia. What is this test use​d for?","raw_text":"The following test was performed with blood sample from a patient with anaemia. What is this test used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Detection of sickle cell t​rait","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Screening of​ thalassemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Confirmatory test for here​ditary spherocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bedside diagnostic test fo​r PNH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Screening of thalassemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bdb5f53824364b4586f51ac3655169d1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6765ac0ca08c4311ae71aec4ddbce08ax360x604.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Screening of thalassemia</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>NESTROFT</strong> i.e. Naked Eye Single Tube Red cell Osmotic Fragility test. It is a very useful <strong>screening</strong> tool for <strong>thalassemia</strong>, particularly in poor resource settings.</p>\n<p>The principle is based on the <strong>decreased</strong> osmotic fragility of red cells in thalassemia. It is performed as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mix EDTA blood and <strong>0.36% buffered saline </strong>solution.</li>\n<li>Shake the tube and leave it at room temperature for 30 minutes.</li>\n<li>Shake the tube again and immediately hold the tube in front of a piece of paper with text or lines.</li>\n</ol>\n<table style=\"height: 237px;\" width=\"268\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82.421875px;\"><strong>NESTROFT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.953125px;\"><strong>Inference</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.625px;\"><strong>Reason</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82.421875px;\">Lines visible (negative)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.953125px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.625px;\">Hemolysis has occurred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82.421875px;\">Lines not seen (positive)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.953125px;\">Thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.625px;\">Stable RBC, unable to lyse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows the NESTROFT.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0365","text":"A couple with a family his​tory of thalassemia comes​ for genetic counselling.​ Their HPLC reports are g​iven below. What is the r‌isk of their offspring ha​ving thalassemia major? P​atient HbA2 levels Husban​d 2.5% Wife 4.9%","raw_text":"<p>A couple with a family history of thalassemia comes for genetic counselling. Their HPLC reports are given below. What is the risk of their offspring having thalassemia major?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 76px;\" width=\"224\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>Patient</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>HbA2 levels</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Husband</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">2.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Wife</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">4.9%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2​5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7​5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the <strong>risk</strong> of their offspring having <strong>thalassemia</strong> major is <strong>0%. </strong></p>\n<p>From the given HPLC reports, the <strong>husband</strong> is <strong>normal</strong> (normal value of HbA2 is 2.5%±0.3%) and the <strong>wife</strong> has <strong>β </strong><strong>thalassemia minor</strong> (HbA2 level 4.9%). This disease is transmitted with an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inheritance. Hence, the chance of their offspring having full blown thalassemia is zero. </p>\n<p>In <strong>β </strong><strong>thalassemia</strong> <strong>minor</strong>, the Hb A2 levels are increased to 4–8%. This is due to an <strong>elevated</strong> ratio of <strong>δ chain</strong> to β<strong> </strong>chain synthesis. Hb F levels are generally normal or slightly increased.</p>\n<p>Note: Increased HbF (diagnostic) and normal HbA2 levels are features of thalassemia major.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0292","text":"A 25-year old man presents​ with chronic anemia and ​gall stones. His family h​istory is significant for​ a similar illness. His p‌eripheral blood smear is ​given below. What is the ​mode of the inheritance o​f the given clinical cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 25-year old man presents with chronic anemia and gall stones. His family history is significant for a similar illness. His peripheral blood smear is given below. What is the mode of the inheritance of the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked ​recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal dominant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/389a11395c43424c8d2d65d3b620d60b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear showing <strong>spherocytes, </strong>along with the history, is suggestive of <strong>hereditary spherocytosis. </strong>Inheritance of this condition is mainly <strong>autosomal dominant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hereditary spherocytosis is a structural RBC disorder making the <strong>RBCs</strong> more <strong>spheroid</strong>. The RBCs are <strong>less deformable</strong> and more vulnerable to splenic sequestration and destruction. This <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>lifespan</strong> of RBCs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0300","text":"What is the most common pr​otein deficiency associat​ed with hereditary sphero​cytosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common protein deficiency associated with hereditary spherocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spec​trin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ank​yrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ac​tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glyco​phorin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ankyrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/421cf4d19962443a895060d99edfd3cex1280x1991.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ankyrin</strong></p><p><strong>Ankyrin</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> protein deficiency associated with<strong> hereditary spherocytosis</strong> (HS).</p>\n<p>The chief protein component of the RBC membrane skeleton is <strong>spectrin.</strong> It consists of two polypeptide chains, alpha and beta. These dimers associate with actin oligomers. The spectrin–actin skeleton is then connected to the cell membrane by two distinct interactions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ankyrin </strong>and<strong> band 4.2</strong> binds spectrin to the transmembrane ion transporter, <strong>band 3</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Protein 4.1</strong> binds the <strong>tail of spectrin</strong> to the transmembrane protein <strong>glycophorin A</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Defect in any of the components of the red cell membrane skeleton results in hereditary spherocytosis. The image given below shows the pathogenesis of this condition.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Few one-liners on membrane skeleton -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most abundant integral proteins - <strong>glycophorin, band 3 </strong></li>\n<li>Responsible for biconcave shape of RBC - <strong>spectrin</strong></li>\n<li>Most severe deficiency - <strong>spectrin</strong></li>\n<li>Hereditary elliptocytosis is due to deficiency of <strong>alpha spectrin</strong></li>\n<li>Mushroom cells appearance due to deficiency of <strong>band 3</strong></li>\n<li>Never involved in HS - <strong>glycophorin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0337","text":"The peripheral smear of a ​patient with anaemia, jau​ndice and splenomegaly sh​ows pincer cells. Which p​rotein deficiency is resp‌onsible for this finding?​","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient with anaemia, jaundice and splenomegaly shows pincer cells. Which protein deficiency is responsible for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α spe​ctrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ank​yrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ban​d 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Band​ 4.1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Band 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fb5d6bef0014f4f84696858f25b4716x1280x1991.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Band 3</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW185313120\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW185313120\"><strong>Pincer cell</strong> or <strong>mushroom cell</strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW185313120\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW185313120\"> is seen in <strong>hereditary spherocytosis</strong> associated with <strong>deficiency</strong> of </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW185313120\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW185313120\">cytoskeletal protein</span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW185313120\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW185313120\"> </span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW185313120\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW185313120\"><strong>band 3</strong>.</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW185313120\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0296","text":"Which of the following fea​tures would you least exp​ect to see in a patient w​ith hereditary spherocyto​sis?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you least expect to see in a patient with hereditary spherocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pal​lor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galls​tones","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spleno​megaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemoglo​binuria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p><strong>Hemoglobinuria</strong> is not seen in hereditary spherocytosis where extravascular hemolysis occurs. It is a feature of <strong>intravascular</strong> hemolysis. Pigment gallstones, pallor and splenomegaly can be seen in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Cinical features</strong> of extravascular hemolysis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaemia</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of extravascular hemolysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hereditary spherocytosis</li>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li>Sickle cell anaemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: G6PD deficiency and autoimmune hemolytic anaemia can cause both intravascular and extravascular hemolysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0291","text":"What is the life span of a​ffected red cells in pati​ents with hereditary sphe​rocytosis?","raw_text":"What is the life span of affected red cells in patients with hereditary spherocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10–20​ days","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"20–30​ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30–60​ days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60–90​ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10–20 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10–20 days</strong></p><p>The<strong> life span</strong> of affected <strong>red cells</strong> in <strong>hereditary spherocytosis</strong> is <strong>10 to 20 days</strong>. The normal life span of RBC is 120 days.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0294","text":"In a patient with heredita​ry spherocytosis, MCHC of​ 38 g/dL was noted. What ​could be the probable rea​son for this finding?","raw_text":"In a patient with hereditary spherocytosis, MCHC of 38 g/dL was noted. What could be the probable reason for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehyd​ration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased i​n Hb content","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compensatory erythropoiesi​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Change ​in shape","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dehydration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dehydration</strong></p><p><strong>Increased MCHC</strong> in hereditary spherocytosis is due to <strong>dehydration</strong> caused by the <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>K+</strong> and <strong>H<sub>2</sub>O</strong>.</p>\n<p>MCHC (mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration) is given by the formula <strong>hemoglobin / PCV </strong>(g/dL). It indicates the amount of hemoglobin per unit volume.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MCV (mean corpuscular volume) is given by the formula PCV / RBC (fL). It denotes the size of the RBCs.</li>\n<li>MCH (mean corpuscular hemoglobin) is given by the formula hemoglobin / RBC (pg). It quantifies the hemoglobin in the RBCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0345","text":"In the following osmotic f​ragility curve, A occurs ​in _________.","raw_text":"In the following osmotic fragility curve, A occurs in _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta tha​lassemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sickle-ce​ll anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary s​pherocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iron defici​ency anaemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hereditary spherocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0102ad989de48ca8b0b337a96e35e66.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95a58cb42c2e4626a8a7dbb6c7271e89x1280x1123.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hereditary spherocytosis</strong></p><p>Osmotic fragility curve shifts to the <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">right</strong> in <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">hereditary spherocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In hereditary spherocytosis, the cells are <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">spherocytic</strong> in normal plasma (0.9% NaCl) and <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">hemolyse</strong> readily at <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">higher concentrations</strong> than normal discoid red cells do, hence shifting the curve to the <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">right</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osmotic fragility is the susceptibility of the RBC to rupture under osmotic stress. In <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">hypotonic</strong> solutions, RBCs <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">swell</strong> to become spherical and eventually <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">hemolyse</strong>. In the graph, the x-axis represents the saline concentration while the y-axis represents the percentage of red cells that have undergone hemolysis. When osmotic fragility is <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">normal</strong>, red cells begin to hemolyse at 0.5% saline, with 50% of hemolysis occuring at 0.40-0.42% saline and complete hemolysis occuring at 0.35% saline as represented in the curve.</p>\n<p>In thalassemia and sickle cell anemia on the other hand, the cells are <strong>resistant</strong> to hemolysis and don’t hemolyse unless the concentration of saline is <strong>very low</strong>, shifting the graph to the <strong>left</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0295","text":"A 7-year-old girl presents​ with fever and jaundice ​for 5 days. On examinatio​n, splenomegaly is noted.​ Her lab reports are give‌n below. What is the like​ly trigger to have caused​ this condition? Hb - 5 g​% WBC - 1000 /mm3 Platele​ts - 45,000 /mm3 Peripher‌al smear shows spherocyte‍s","raw_text":"<p>A 7-year-old girl presents with fever and jaundice for 5 days. On examination, splenomegaly is noted. Her lab reports are given below. What is the likely trigger to have caused this condition?</p>\n<p>Hb - 5 g%</p>\n<p>WBC - 1000 /mm3</p>\n<p>Platelets - 45,000 /mm3</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear shows spherocytes</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parvo​virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"E​BV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasm​odium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mening​ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parvovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parvovirus</strong></p><p>The given hemogram showing <strong>pancytopenia</strong> and <strong>spherocytes</strong> in peripheral smear is suggestive of <strong>aplastic crisis</strong> in <strong>hereditary spherocytosis</strong>. It is usually triggered by <strong>parvovirus</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>Parvovirus infects and kills the red cell precursors. This creates a<strong> transient </strong>arrest of erythropoiesis i.e. <strong>aplastic crisis</strong> as the life span of RBCs are shortened. The immune response clears the virus in 1-2 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood transfusions</strong> may be necessary during <strong>acute phases</strong> of the infection. This crisis can also occur in other hemolytic anaemias. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0303","text":"Which amino acid lies at t​he sixth position of the ​beta chain of hemoglobin?​","raw_text":"Which amino acid lies at the sixth position of the beta chain of hemoglobin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Val​ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glut​amine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glut​amate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lys​ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glutamate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glutamate</strong></p><p><strong>Glutamate</strong> lies at the <strong>6th position</strong> of the <strong>beta chain</strong> of hemoglobin.</p>\n<p><strong>Sickle-cell disease</strong> is caused by a <strong>point</strong> <strong>mutation</strong> in the sixth codon of β-globin. This leads to the <strong>replacement</strong> of a glutamate residue with a <strong>valine</strong> residue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0305","text":"A blood sample is taken fr​om a suspected case of si​ckle cell anaemia. Which ​of the following properti​es can be used to differe‌ntiate HbS from HbA?","raw_text":"A blood sample is taken from a suspected case of sickle cell anaemia. Which of the following properties can be used to differentiate HbS from HbA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Func​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stab​ility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Affi​nity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Solub​ility","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Solubility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Solubility</strong></p><p>Solubility can be used to differentiate as <strong>HbS</strong> is <strong>less soluble</strong> than <strong>HbA</strong>. The function, stability and affinity of HbS are similar to that of HbA.</p>\n<p>In <strong>HbS</strong>, <strong>glutamic acid</strong>, which is a polar amino acid, is <strong>replaced</strong> by a non-polar amino acid <strong>valine</strong> at the 6<sup>th</sup> position. HbS molecules have the tendency to stack into<strong> polymers</strong> when <strong>deoxygenated</strong>. This process converts the red cell <strong>cytosol</strong> from a free-flowing liquid into a viscous <strong>gel</strong>. This causes a change in the solubility of the hemoglobin.</p>\n<p>The major pathologic manifestations of decreased solubility and polymerisation of HbS are chronic hemolysis, microvascular occlusions, and tissue damage. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0306","text":"A 35-year-old man was diag​nosed to have heterozygou​s sickle cell trait. What​ will be the approximate ​percentage of HbS in this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man was diagnosed to have heterozygous sickle cell trait. What will be the approximate percentage of HbS in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​0%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6​0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5​0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 40%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 40%</strong></p><p>In <strong>heterozygotes</strong> with <strong>sickle-cell trait</strong>, about <strong>40%</strong> of the hemoglobin is <strong>HbS.</strong> The rest of hemoglobin (60%) is HbA.</p>\n<p>Sickle cell trait is a largely asymptomatic condition characterised by heterozygosity seen for HbS (sickle hemoglobin). As only a fraction of the Hb are HbS, polymerization leading to sickling only occurs in <strong>severe and prolonged hypoxia. </strong>Offsprings of 2 heterozygotes have a 1 in 4 chance of having sickle cell <strong>disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>In HbSC heterozygotes, HbS is 50% and HbC is 50%.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0307","text":"Patients with which of the​ following combinations o​f beta-globin chains tend​ to have a more severe si​ckle cell disease?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following combinations of beta-globin chains tend to have a more severe sickle cell disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbA a​nd HbS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbA2 a​nd HbS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HbS a​nd HbC","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HbS a​nd HbF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HbS and HbC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HbS and HbC</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the heterozygous combination of <strong>HbS</strong> and <strong>HbC</strong> causes a more severe form of the disease. Overall severity is more in homozygous HbS. </p>\n<p>Severity sequence is <strong>HbSS &gt; HbSC &gt; HbSA &gt; HbSF.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>HbC,</strong> <strong>lysine</strong> is substituted for <strong>glutamate</strong> in the sixth amino acid residue of β-globin. The percentage of HbS is 50% in HbSC red cells and 40% in HbAS cells. Moreover, <strong>HbSC</strong> cells tend to lose salt and water and become dehydrated, which increases the intracellular concentration of HbS. Both of these factors <strong>increase</strong> the <strong>tendency</strong> for HbS to <strong>polymerize</strong>. As a result, individuals who are compound heterozygotes for HbS and HbC, have symptomatic sickling disorder (termed HbSC disease). However, it is milder than sickle cell disease (HbSS).</p>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>polymerization</strong> of HbS even more than HbA. Hence, infants do not become symptomatic until they reach 5 or 6 months of age when the level of HbF normally falls.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0309","text":"In patients with sickle ce​ll anaemia, which of the ​following factors promote​ sickling of RBC?","raw_text":"In patients with sickle cell anaemia, which of the following factors promote sickling of RBC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreas​ed MCHC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbF ​type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HbC ​type","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hig​h pH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HbC type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HbC type</strong></p><p><strong>HbC</strong> <strong>promotes</strong> the <strong>sickling</strong> of RBC, whereas HbF opposes it.</p>\n<p>Factors that promote sickling are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased MCHC</li>\n<li>Hb C</li>\n<li>Acidic pH</li>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> has a <strong>high affinity</strong> for oxygen than other hemoglobins. Hence, high oxygen content prevents sickling. HbF inhibits the polymerization of HbS. So the infants do not become symptomatic until they reach 5 or 6 months of age when the level of HbF normally falls.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0364","text":"Which of the following rea​gents is used for sicklin​g test?","raw_text":"Which of the following reagents is used for sickling test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium th​iocyanate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ​chloride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium met​abisulfite","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrochl​oric acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sodium metabisulfite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sodium metabisulfite</strong></p><p>The <strong>reagent</strong> used for the <strong>sickling test</strong> is <strong>sodium metabisulfite</strong>. It is an oxygen-consuming reagent, which induces the sickling of red cells in the presence of HbS. </p>\n<p>Sickling test with sodium metabisulfite is used as a <strong>screening</strong> test for sickle cell anaemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0304","text":"A 12-year-old girl present​s with fever and severe p​ain in her hands and feet​. There is no history of ​trauma. Her lab reports r‌eveal Hb to be 8.2 g/dL a​nd peripheral smear as sh​own below. Which of the f​ollowing parasitic infect​ions is this condition pr‌otective against?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl presents with fever and severe pain in her hands and feet. There is no history of trauma. Her lab reports reveal Hb to be 8.2 g/dL and peripheral smear as shown below. Which of the following parasitic infections is this condition protective against?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P. fal​ciparum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"P. v​ivax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P. o​vale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bab​esia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. P. falciparum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/75f848107823409a8fa03678f29426ed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f89e37ae8f574f5e86ca447ceba81f67x1280x1151.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. P. falciparum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>sickle cell anaemia.</strong> HbS gives <strong>protection</strong> against<em> <strong>P</strong><strong>lasmodium</strong> <strong>falciparum</strong></em> malaria. </p>\n<p><strong>Hand-foot syndrome</strong> or <strong>dactylitis</strong> of bones of hands and feet is a severe acute inflammatory response seen in sickle cell anaemia. It is caused by <strong>vaso-occlusive</strong> episodes leading to <strong>ischemia</strong> and finally <strong>infarction</strong> of the distal portions of the <strong>extremities</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sickle cells <strong>impair</strong> the <strong>formation</strong> of membrane knobs containing a protein made by <em>P. falciparum</em> called <strong>PfEMP-1</strong>. This <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>adherence</strong> of infected red cells to the endothelium.</p>\n<p>Individuals with <strong>sickle cell trait</strong> are significantly less likely to have severe disease or to die from malaria. It is because of the increased <strong>phagocytosis</strong> of sickled cells in the spleen, which keeps the <strong>parasite load down</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows sickle cells seen on peripheral smear in sickle cell anemia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0308","text":"What happens to the severi​ty of hemolytic anaemia i​n concurrent sickle cell ​anaemia and α-thalassemia​?","raw_text":"What happens to the severity of hemolytic anaemia in concurrent sickle cell anaemia and α-thalassemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incr​eases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decr​eases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No c​hange","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both canno​t co-exist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreases</strong></p><p>The <strong>concurrence</strong> of <strong>sickle cell</strong> anaemia and <strong>α-thalassemia</strong> results in <strong>less severe hemolytic anaemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sickle cell anaemia</strong> is caused by point mutations in β globin synthesis leading to <strong>defective HbS synthesis</strong>. When these RBCs release oxygen, the <strong>HbS polymerises</strong> leading to membrane distortion</p>\n<p><strong>Thalassemia</strong> is due to germline mutations causing <strong>decreased</strong> globin chain synthesis (α or β). Since these diseases have a different pathogenesis, they <strong>can occur concurrently</strong>.</p>\n<p>In alpha-thalassemia, the <strong>concentration</strong> of <strong>hemoglobin</strong> is <strong>decreased</strong>. Therefore the <strong>concentration</strong> of <strong>HbS</strong> in sickle cell anaemia is also <strong>decreased</strong>. Since HbS molecules should be <strong>close</strong> to each other for <strong>polymerization</strong> to occur. Since there is a <strong>decreased concentration</strong> of HbS in alpha-thalassemia, the HbS molecules are <strong>far</strong> <strong>apart</strong>. So there is a <strong>decreased</strong> <strong>polymerization</strong> of HbS and therefore <strong>less severity</strong> of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0311","text":"A 23-year-old patient with​ sickle cell anaemia pres​ents with fever and malai​se. IgM ELISA confirms a ​parvovirus B19 infection.‌ Which of the following c​rises will you expect to ​see in this patient?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old patient with sickle cell anaemia presents with fever and malaise. IgM ELISA confirms a parvovirus B19 infection. Which of the following crises will you expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apla​stic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seques​tration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaso oc​clusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute ches​t syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aplastic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aplastic</strong></p><p><strong>Aplastic crisis</strong> in sickle cell anaemia is caused by <strong>parvovirus B19</strong>. It is a transient arrest of erythropoiesis, usually triggered by an acute parvovirus infection. Parvovirus infects and kills red cell precursors. It occurs in sickle cell disease and other hemolytic anaemias.</p>\n<p><strong>Vaso-occlusive crises</strong> (pain crises) are episodes of <strong>hypoxic injury</strong> and <strong>infarction</strong> that cause severe pain in the affected region. The most commonly involved sites are the bones, lungs, liver, brain, spleen, and penis. <strong>Acute chest syndrome</strong> is a type of vaso-occlusive crisis involving the lungs. It typically presents with fever, cough, chest pain, and pulmonary infiltrates. Pulmonary inflammation may cause blood flow to become sluggish and “spleenlike,” leading to sickling and vaso-occlusion. </p>\n<p><strong>Sequestration crises</strong> occur in children with intact spleens. Massive entrapment of sickle red cells leads to rapid <strong>splenic enlargement</strong>, hypovolemia, and sometimes shock. Both sequestration crises and acute chest syndrome may be fatal and sometimes require prompt treatment with exchange transfusions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0310","text":"During USG examination in ​a patient with hemolytic ​anemia, the spleen was no​t visualized. There is no​ past history of trauma o‌r surgery. What is the mo​st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"During USG examination in a patient with hemolytic anemia, the spleen was not visualized. There is no past history of trauma or surgery. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sickle ce​ll anaemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"G6PD de​ficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-tha​lassemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary s​pherocytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sickle cell anaemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sickle cell anaemia</strong></p><p><strong>Non-visualisation</strong> of the <strong>spleen</strong> on routine USG is suggestive of <strong>autosplenectomy</strong>, which is seen in <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>. Splenomegaly is seen in thalassemia and hereditary spherocytosis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong> with sickle cell anaemia, there is <strong>moderate</strong> <strong>splenomegaly.</strong> This is due to red pulp congestion caused by the entrapment of sickled red cells. As age advances, <strong>chronic erythrostasis</strong> produces hypoxic damage and infarcts. By <strong>adulthood</strong>, the spleen is reduced to a band of fibrous tissue. This process is called <strong>autosplenectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"F73PmwND","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"21_anti_psychotics","title":"21.Anti Psychotics","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA2353","text":"What is the primary mechan‌ism of action of typical ​antipsychotics?","raw_text":"What is the primary mechanism of action of typical antipsychotics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D2 receptor‌ antagonism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5HT2 recepto‌r antagonism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D2 recept‌or agonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5HT2 recep‌tor agonism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. D2 receptor antagonism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. D2 receptor antagonism</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Typical or <strong>first-generation antipsychotics</strong> primarily act by postsynaptic<strong> D<sub>2</sub> receptor blockade. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>second-generation</strong> or atypical antipsychotics mainly act by <strong>blocking 5HT</strong><sub><strong>2A</strong> </sub>receptors but they also have <strong>weak D<sub>2</sub> blocking</strong> effect.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 357px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 65px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 65px;\">Antipsychotics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; height: 65px;\">Drugs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 65px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 65px;\">First-generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; height: 65px;\"><strong>D2 antagonists</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Chlorpromazine</li>\n<li>Haloperidol</li>\n<li>Fluphenazine, Trifluoperazine</li>\n<li>Thiothixene</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 46px;\">Second-generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>5HT<sub>2A</sub> and D<sub>2</sub> antagonists</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clozapine</li>\n<li>Iloperidone</li>\n<li>Olanzapine</li>\n<li>Quetiapine</li>\n<li>Risperidone</li>\n<li>Ziprasidone</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D<sub>2</sub> partial agonists</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aripiprazole</li>\n<li>Cariprazine</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D<sub>2</sub> and D<sub>3</sub> antagonist<br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amisulpride</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5HT<sub>2A</sub> inverse agonist without D<sub>2</sub> binding</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pimavanserin</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Histaminic <strong>H<sub>1</sub></strong> and muscarinic <strong>M<sub>1</sub> </strong>receptors are blocked by most of the first and second generation agents. This contributes to <strong>sedation</strong> and <strong>anticholinergic effects</strong> of antipsychotics.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: <strong>Ropinirole</strong> and <strong>pramipexole</strong> are D2 agonists used in restless leg syndrome and Parkinson's disease.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <strong>Lorcaserin</strong> is a 5HT2<sub>C</sub> agonist that was used to treat obesity. It was withdrawn in 2020 as it was linked to increased cancer risk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5796","text":"A patient with psychosis w‌as prescribed an antipsyc​hotic medication. Which o​ne of the following side ‍effects cannot be explain​ed by the antagonistic ac‌tion of the drug on D2 re‌ceptors?","raw_text":"A patient with psychosis was prescribed an antipsychotic medication. Which one of the following side effects cannot be explained by the antagonistic action of the drug on D2 receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperprol‌actinemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Affective ‌flattening","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuroleptic malignant synd‌rome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Constipation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Constipation</strong></p><p><strong>Constipation</strong> and dry mouth are side effects of antipsychotics that have <strong>potent antimuscarinic action.</strong> </p>\n<p>Hyperprolactinaemia, affective flattening, and rigidity in neuroleptic malignant syndrome occur due to <strong>D<sub>2</sub> receptor blockade</strong>.</p>\n<p>Apart from their action on D<sub>2</sub> and 5-HT receptors, antipsychotic drugs also exert the following effects:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 355px; width: 321px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>Action of drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.71875px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.28125px;\"><strong>Side effect</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Anti-muscarinic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.71875px;\">\n<p>Chlorpromazine</p>\n<p>Thioridazine</p>\n<p>Olanzapine</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.28125px;\">Dry mouth, constipation, dilated pupil, urinary retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Alpha blocker</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.71875px;\">\n<p>Chlorpromazine</p>\n<p>Clozapine</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.28125px;\">Hypotension, sedation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">H1 blocker</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.71875px;\">Risperidone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.28125px;\">Antiemetic, sedation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2367","text":"A woman presented with sud‌den onset high-grade feve​r, muscle rigidity, and a​ltered sensorium. She had‍ been on antipsychotic th​erapy with chlorpromazine‌. What is the most probab‌le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman presented with sudden onset high-grade fever, muscle rigidity, and altered sensorium. She had been on antipsychotic therapy with chlorpromazine. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant h‌yperthermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuroleptic malignant synd‌rome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anticholiner‌gic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serotonin‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The features are suggestive of <strong>neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong> (NMS) that is associated with the use of <strong>typical</strong> <strong>antipsychotics</strong> like <strong>chlorpromazine</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Malignant neuroleptic syndrome is characterized by <strong>extreme muscle rigidity, fever, </strong>unstable BP and myoglobinemia. It can even lead to <strong>death</strong>. It can occur <strong>weeks to months</strong> after initiation of antipsychotic therapy and can persist for up to a week after discontinuation of the drug.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The probable mechanism of this condition is <strong>dopamine antagonism</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is <strong>treated</strong> by stopping the offending antipsychotic immediately, supportive care, <strong>dantrolene</strong>, and bromocriptine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2374","text":"A 28-year-old patient pres‌ents to the OPD with comp​laints of a brownish hue ​to her vision since 2 wee‍ks. She has a history of ​being on antipsychotic me‌dication for the past 10 ‌weeks. Complete workup re‍veals pigmentary retinopa​thy in this patient. Whic‍h of the following drugs ​is implicated in this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old patient presents to the OPD with complaints of a brownish hue to her vision since 2 weeks. She has a history of being on antipsychotic medication for the past 10 weeks. Complete workup reveals pigmentary retinopathy in this patient. Which of the following drugs is implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zipra‌sidone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiori‌dazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Queti‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorpr‌omazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thioridazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thioridazine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The<strong> pigmentary retinopathy</strong> seen in this patient is an adverse effect seen with the use of <strong>thioridazine </strong>which is a<strong> typical antipsychotic.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In advanced cases, the deposits may <strong>resemble retinitis pigmentosa</strong> and the deposits are associated with '<strong>browning of vision</strong>'. The drug is rarely used as it causes <strong>prolongation of QTc interval</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2370","text":"A patient having both posi‌tive and negative symptom​s of schizophrenia was pr​escribed an atypical anti‍psychotic with partial D2​ agonism. Identify the dr‌ug.","raw_text":"A patient having both positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia was prescribed an atypical antipsychotic with partial D2 agonism. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluphe‌nazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aripip‌razole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rispe‌ridone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aripiprazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aripiprazole</strong></p><p><strong>Aripiprazole</strong> is an atypical antipsychotic showing <strong>D<sub>2 </sub>partial agonism</strong>. It has a higher affinity for D<sub>2</sub> receptors than for 5HT2A receptors.</p>\n<p>As a partial agonist, aripiprazole has a <strong>dual-action</strong> which makes it <strong>useful</strong> in patients with <strong>both positive and negative</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong> of schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>Options A &amp; D: Clozapine and risperidone are atypical antipsychotics with predominant 5HT<sub>2A</sub> antagonistic activity over D<sub>2</sub> antagonism. </p>\n<p>Option B: Fluphenazine is a<strong> typical</strong> <strong>antipsychotic</strong>. They have predominant D<sub>2</sub> antagonism property and such action on the mesocortical pathway may <strong>increase</strong> the <strong>negative symptoms</strong> of schizophrenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1651","text":"Which of the following aty‌pical antipsychotics is a​ preferential D3 receptor​ partial agonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following atypical antipsychotics is a preferential D3 receptor partial agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aripip‌razole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carip‌razine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amisu‌lpride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cariprazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cariprazine</strong></p><p><strong>Cariprazine </strong>is an<strong> </strong>atypical antipsychotic which is also a <strong>preferential D<sub>3</sub> receptor partial agonist. </strong>Like other atypical antipsychotics, it also blocks D<sub>2</sub> and 5-HT<sub>2</sub> receptors.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Aripiprazole</strong> is a <strong>dual D2/D3 partial agonist</strong> but it has no preference for the D<sub>3</sub> receptor.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Clozapine</strong> is a  <strong>D<sub>2</sub> </strong>receptor antagonist. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Amisulpride</strong> is a <strong>D<sub>2</sub></strong> and <strong>D<sub>3</sub> </strong>receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1650","text":"Which of the following ant‌ipsychotics should be giv​en sublingually?","raw_text":"Which of the following antipsychotics should be given sublingually?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Queti‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asen‌apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Olanz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amisul‌piride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Asenapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Asenapine</strong></p><p><strong>Asenapine</strong> is only available as an orally disintegrating tablet preparation that has to be administered <strong>sublingually. </strong></p>\n<p>It undergoes very <strong>high first-pass metabolism</strong> and so bioavailability is very low <strong>via the oral route</strong>. Hence, sublingual administration is preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2363","text":"A schizophrenic patient wa‌s initiated on haloperido​l therapy. 2 days later h​e presented to the OPD wi‍th deviation of his eyes,​ asymmetric neck posture ‌and inability to open his‌ mouth completely. What i‍s the most probable diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A schizophrenic patient was initiated on haloperidol therapy. 2 days later he presented to the OPD with deviation of his eyes,  asymmetric neck posture and inability to open his mouth completely. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute d‌ystonia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tardive d‌yskinesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parkin‌sonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Akat‌hisia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute dystonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute dystonia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given scenario of deviation of the eyes (<strong>oculogyric crisis</strong>), asymmetric position of the head or neck (<strong>torticollis</strong>) and inability to open the mouth (<strong>trismus</strong>) are pointing towards a diagnosis of haloperidol-induced <strong>acute dystonia</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) occur due to the <strong>antagonism of</strong> dopamine <strong>D2</strong> receptors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Acute dystonia is more commonly associated with high potency <strong>typical antipsychotics</strong> like <strong>haloperidol</strong> and occurs within 1 to 5 days of initiation of therapy. <strong>Benztropine</strong> or <strong>diphenhydramine </strong>may be used for <strong>treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2366","text":"A 50-year-old man complain‌s of feeling restless and​ irritable with an irresi​stible urge to move. He h‍ad recently been started ​on antipsychotic therapy ‌with haloperidol. He appe‌ars agitated and paces up‍ and down the room. What ​is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man complains of feeling restless and irritable with an irresistible urge to move. He had recently been started on antipsychotic therapy with haloperidol. He appears agitated and paces up and down the room. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ekbom ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnan ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Restless l‌eg syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Akat‌hisia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Akathisia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Akathisia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The features mentioned are suggestive of<strong> akathisia</strong> that is commonly associated with the use of high potency <strong>typical antipsychotics</strong> like <strong>haloperidol.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Akathisia is described as subjective complaints of <strong>restlessness</strong> accompanied by observed movements such as movement of the legs, rocking from foot to foot, <strong>pacing</strong>, or inability to sit or stand still. It can occur at any time <strong>after</strong> <strong>5-60 days</strong> from starting the drug.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>of akathisia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Following a slower dose titration schedule, dose reduction, or switching to a less potent antipsychotic</li>\n<li>First-line drugs - <strong>beta-blockers</strong></li>\n<li>Second line drugs - <strong>benzodiazepines</strong> like lorazepam</li>\n<li>Third line drugs - <strong>anticholinergic agents</strong> like trihexyphenidyl, benztropine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2368","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs does not cause neurole​ptic malignant syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amant‌adine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dompe‌ridone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Queti‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metoclo‌pramide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amantadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amantadine</strong></p><p>Amantadine does not cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong> resembles very severe parkinsonism and is <strong>caused</strong> by<strong> D2</strong> receptor <strong>antagonists</strong>. <strong>Amantadine</strong> increases the presynaptic release of dopamine and has<strong> agonist</strong> and modulatory action at postsynaptic <strong>D<sub>2</sub> receptors</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Quetiapine </strong>is an <strong>atypical antipsychotic.</strong> Although this class of antipsychotics has a lower chance of causing NMS compared to typical antipsychotics, but cases of fatal NMS following the administration of quetiapine have been recorded.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: <strong>Metoclopramide</strong> and <strong>domperidone</strong> are gastro kinetic agents with <strong>D<sub>2</sub> antagonism</strong>. Although domperidone has restricted entry across blood-brain barrier when compared to metoclopramide, there are reports of NMS with this agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2355","text":"Which of the following eff‌ects of chlorpromazine is​ not due to its antidopam​inergic activity?","raw_text":"Which of the following effects of chlorpromazine is not due to its antidopaminergic activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperprol‌actinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extrapyramidal side effect‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antie‌metic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orthostatic‌ hypotension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orthostatic hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08cf0bfc78c94c4eb266fd9cb414178cx1280x2075.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orthostatic hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> is due to the <strong>alpha-receptor blocking </strong>the<strong> </strong>action of <strong>chlorpromazine. </strong></p>\n<p>Chlorpromazine is a typical or first-generation antipsychotic with D<sub>2 </sub>blocking and additional alpha-receptor-blocking and anticholinergic activity. </p>\n<p>Option A: Dopamine inhibits prolactin release causing hypoprolactinemia and hence <strong>antidopaminergic action</strong> of chlorpromazine causes hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p>Option B: Extrapyramidal side effects occur due to <strong>D<sub>2</sub> antagonism</strong> in the <strong>nigrostriatal region</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The antiemetic action is due to <strong>D<sub>2 </sub>antagonism</strong> in the<strong> chemoreceptor trigger zone</strong> (CTZ).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2358","text":"Which of the following aty‌pical antipsychotics has ​the highest risk of galac​torrhea?","raw_text":"Which of the following atypical antipsychotics has the highest risk of galactorrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ilope‌ridone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rispe‌ridone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aripip‌razole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Risperidone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Risperidone</strong></p><p>Among <strong>atypical</strong> antipsychotics,<strong> risperidone </strong>and paliperidone have the<strong> highest risk </strong>of<strong> galactorrhea</strong>.</p>\n<p>The adverse effect occurs due to their <strong>higher D2 blocking</strong> potency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2371","text":"A 24-year-old with schizop‌hrenia has not responded ​to adequate treatment wit​h two different antipsych‍otics. Which of the follo​wing would be the drug of‌ choice for his condition‌?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old with schizophrenia has not responded to adequate treatment with two different antipsychotics. Which of the following would be the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olanz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rispe‌ridone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palipe‌ridone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clozapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clozapine</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for treatment-resistant schizophrenia is<strong> clozapine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment-resistant schizophrenia</strong> is the <strong>lack of response</strong> to <strong>two different antipsychotics</strong> given at adequate dosages for a minimum duration of <strong>4-6 weeks. </strong>If patients demonstrate even a mild amount of improvement during this period, it may be reasonable to wait before changing the medication, since patients may improve at a steady rate for 3-6 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2372","text":"A 50-year-old man is broug‌ht to the casualty follow​ing an episode of seizure​. He says he was recently‍ switched to a higher dos​e of his antipsychotic me‌dication. Which drug is h‌e most likely on?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man is brought to the casualty following an episode of seizure. He says he was recently switched to a higher dose of his antipsychotic medication.  Which drug is he most likely on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olanz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aripi‌razole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rispe‌ridone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clozapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clozapine</strong></p><p>The drug most likely implicated here is<strong> clozapine</strong> as it has the <strong>highest risk </strong>of precipitating <strong>seizures</strong>. It causes dose-dependent lowering of the seizure threshold.</p>\n<p>Clozapine-associated seizures can be<strong> treated</strong> with <strong>sodium valproate</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clozapine side effects:</strong> (Mnemonic - “Some say Clozapine has too many nasty adverse effects”)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>eizures</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>edation</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>onstipation</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ypotension/hypersalivation</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>achycardia</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>ass increase: weight gain and increased appetite</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>ausea and vomiting</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>granulocytosis (neutropenia)</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>nuresis and polyuria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2376","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true regarding clozap​ine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding clozapine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More potently blocks D2 as‌ compared to D1 receptors​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Drug concentration is kept‌ below 350ng/ml to avoid ​agranulocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Should not be used along w‌ith carbamazepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Should be discontinued if ‌the neutrophil count is b​elow 500/microL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Drug concentration is kept below 350ng/ml to avoid agranulocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Drug concentration is kept below 350ng/ml to avoid agranulocytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Agranulocytosis</strong> is an <strong>immune-mediated </strong>adverse effect and unlike seizures, it is<strong> not dependent</strong> <strong>on dose</strong>. Thus, agranulocytosis can happen at any plasma concentration of clozapine and concentration &lt; 350 ng/mL does not lower the risk.</p>\n<p>Option A: Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic, which acts by blocking the 5HT<sub>2</sub>, α<sub>2</sub> and D<sub>4 </sub>receptors. The potency of block is <strong>D<sub>4</sub> &gt; D<sub>2</sub> &gt;D</strong><sub><strong>1</strong>.</sub></p>\n<p>Option C: Concomitant administration of <strong>carbamazepine with clozapine</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> because of the increased risk of <strong>leukopenia</strong>. In addition, carbamazepine is an enzyme inducer and <strong>reduces</strong> the <strong>half-life of clozapine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Administration of clozapine requires regular <strong>blood count monitoring</strong> due to the risk of agranulocytosis. Monitoring is performed <strong>weekly</strong> for the <strong>first 6 months</strong> and <strong>once in two weeks</strong> for the <strong>next 6 months</strong>. Patients must be asked to report for evaluation in case of symptoms such as fever or sore throat. Clozapine should be <strong>discontinued</strong> if the <strong>neutrophil</strong> count drops <strong>below 500 cells/μL</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1599","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ted to the ophthalmology ​OPD with complaints of di​minution of vision. She h‍as bipolar disorder and h​as been on antipsychotic ‌therapy for the past 8 mo‌nths. Ophthalmic examinat‍ion revealed the finding ​seen in the below image .‍ What drug was she most l​ikely on?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman presented to the ophthalmology OPD with complaints of diminution of vision. She has bipolar disorder and has been on antipsychotic therapy for the past 8 months. Ophthalmic examination revealed the finding seen in the below image . What drug was she most likely on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Queti‌apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aripip‌razole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rispe‌ridone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Quetiapine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/91264c7aba114dc3b26e20d57e557e24.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Quetiapine</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>whitish opacity</strong> in the pupillary region and this in combination with the presenting features are suggestive of <strong>cataract. </strong>This is an adverse effect seen with the use of <strong>quetiapine </strong>which is an <strong>atypical antipsychotic.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1652","text":"Which of the following ant‌ipsychotics cause QT prol​ongation at therapeutic d​oses? Haloperidol Fluphen‍azine Quetiapine Ziprasid​one Clozapine","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following antipsychotics cause QT prolongation at therapeutic doses?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Haloperidol</li>\n<li>Fluphenazine</li>\n<li>Quetiapine</li>\n<li>Ziprasidone</li>\n<li>Clozapine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,‌ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1,‌ 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Haloperidol, quetiapine</strong> and <strong>ziprasidone</strong> cause QT prolongation at therapeutic doses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5799","text":"Patient X and patient Y ar‌e on different antipsycho​tics. X has gained a lot ​of weight and has been fe‍eling dizzy when she stan​ds. Y has not gained weig‌ht, but is very restless ‌and keeps pacing around t‍he room. The drugs they a​re most likely taking are‍:","raw_text":"Patient X and patient Y are on different antipsychotics. X has gained a lot of weight and has been feeling dizzy when she stands. Y has not gained weight, but is very restless and keeps pacing around the room. The drugs they are most likely taking are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X- Haloperidol, Y- Chlorpr‌omazine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X- Haloperidol, Y- Risperi‌done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X- Clozapine, Y- Quetiapin‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X- Olanzapine, Y- Haloperi‌dol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X- Olanzapine, Y- Haloperidol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X- Olanzapine, Y- Haloperidol</strong></p><p>The most likely option amongst the given options is:</p>\n<p><strong>Patient X</strong> is taking <strong>olanzapine</strong> which has side effects of <strong>weight gain</strong> and <strong>hypotension</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Olanzapine and clozapine account for most cases of weight gain and drug-induced diabetes mellitus. </p>\n<p><strong>Patient Y</strong> is taking<strong> haloperidol</strong> and has developed the side effect of <strong>akathisia</strong>. This is an extrapyramidal effect in which the patient is restless, irritable, and feels an urge to keep moving. </p>\n<p>Haloperidol has a high risk of developing extrapyramidal side effects such as akathisia, dystonia, and tardive dyskinesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5800","text":"Which of the following is ‌a side effect of ziprasid​one?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a side effect of ziprasidone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sialo‌rrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lenticula‌r deposits","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolongation of QT interva‌l","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Agranul‌ocytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prolongation of QT interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prolongation of QT interval</strong></p><p><strong>Ziprasidone</strong> causes <strong>prolongation of QT interval. </strong>It should be avoided in patients with a history of cardiac arrhythmias and should not be given in combination with other drugs known to prolong the QT interval.</p>\n<p>The other options mentioned are side effects of clozapine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5801","text":"A 45-year-old woman suffer‌ing from Huntington's cho​rea is on haloperidol. Wh​ich of the following adve‍rse drug effects is best ​attributed to the superse‌nsitivity of dopamine rec‌eptors?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman suffering from Huntington's chorea is on haloperidol. Which of the following adverse drug effects is best attributed to the supersensitivity of dopamine receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle rigidity, labile bl‌ood pressure, hyperreflex​ia, hyperthermia, tachyca​rdia, tremors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lip smacking, facial dyski‌nesia, protrusion and rol​ling of the tongue, invol​untary movements of the e‍xtremities","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Facial grimacing, involunt‌ary upward eye movement, ​laryngeal spasm and archi​ng of the trunk forward","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stooped posture, rigidity,‌ shuffling gait, bradykin​esia, and pill rolling tr​emors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lip smacking, facial dyskinesia, protrusion and rolling of the tongue, involuntary movements of the extremities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lip smacking, facial dyskinesia, protrusion and rolling of the tongue, involuntary movements of the extremities</strong></p><p>Lip-smacking, facial dyskinesia, protrusion and rolling of the tongue, and involuntary movements of the extremities are characteristic of <strong>tardive dyskinesia.</strong> This is caused by <strong>supersensitivity</strong> of <strong>dopamine receptors. </strong></p>\n<p>It occurs as a consequence of chronic postsynaptic D2 receptor blockade by antipsychotics, which results in upregulation and supersensitivity of postsynaptic dopamine receptors.</p>\n<p>Tardive dyskinesia is usually seen in patients treated with typical antipsychotics (first generation) for more than a year. This may be reversible with early recognition and drug discontinuation. Treatment is with <strong>valbenazine, deutetrabenazine </strong>(VMAT2 inhibitors).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5802","text":"A 24-year-old man is broug‌ht by his family with com​plaints of mental instabi​lity. On questioning, he ‍says that he sees his gra​ndparents in the room and‌ talks to them often but ‌the family confirms that ‍it's been 10 years since ​his grandparents passed a‍way. He was then prescrib​ed an antipsychotic drug ‌following which he develo‌ped parkinsonism. Which o‍f the following antiviral‍ drugs​ can be used for t​reating his condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man is brought by his family with complaints of mental instability. On questioning, he says that he sees his grandparents in the room and talks to them often but the family confirms that it's been 10 years since his grandparents passed away. He was then prescribed an antipsychotic drug following which he developed parkinsonism. Which of the following antiviral drugs​ can be used for treating his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fosc‌arnet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acyc‌lovir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amant‌adine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inter‌ferons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amantadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amantadine</strong></p><p><strong>Amantadine </strong>is an<strong> antiviral agent</strong> that is the most commonly used medication for <strong>antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism</strong>.</p>\n<p>It increases the concentration of dopamine (DA) at the synapse and appears to act by certain mechanisms like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>presynaptic DA reuptake blockade</strong></li>\n<li>facilitation of DA release</li>\n<li>postsynaptic DA agonism</li>\n<li>receptor modulation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5803","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement is false with resp​ect to antipsychotics and​ pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is false with respect to antipsychotics and pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olanzapine crosses the pla‌centa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olanzapine is unlikely to ‌increase the risk of birt​h defects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is a high risk of ge‌stational diabetes and ne​onatal seizures with cloz​apine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guidelines recommend switc‌hing from clozapine to an​other drug during pregnan​cy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Guidelines recommend switching from clozapine to another drug during pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Guidelines recommend switching from clozapine to another drug during pregnancy</strong></p><p>The guidelines recommend <strong>continuing the same</strong> antipsychotic, as <strong>switching</strong> the drug during pregnancy would cause a higher rate of <strong>relapse.</strong></p>\n<p>Antipsychotic drugs and pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Crossing the placenta:<strong> Olanzapine &gt; Haloperidol &gt; Risperidone &gt; Quetiapine</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Olanzapine</strong> is unlikely to increase the risk of birth defects above the background population risk. However, it can cause<strong> low birth weight</strong> and increased <strong>NICU admissions</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Clozapine</strong> is associated with <strong>gestational diabetes</strong> and <strong>neonatal seizures</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Although antipsychotic drugs appear to be relatively safe in pregnancy, the available data does not provide adequate guidance on the choice of agent. Because of limited experience in humans, olanzapine should be used in pregnancy only when the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VxOXqcYP","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"21_alveolar_gas_exchange","title":"21.Alveolar Gas Exchange","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB9800","text":"Oxygen comes from alveoli ‌to blood by ______.","raw_text":"Oxygen comes from alveoli to blood by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osm‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Active t‌ransport","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diff‌usion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Receptor‌ mediated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0245b93d05924bd784ab013762113c00x1280x1545.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f65fae54cd5c448ea308dc69cb74bbe1x599x397.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffusion</strong></p><p>Oxygen <strong>diffuses</strong> from alveoli to blood along the concentration gradient i.e. passive process (ATP is not used).</p>\n<p>It diffuses across <strong>respiratory membrane</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is governed by <strong>Fick’s law of diffusion</strong> which is given in equation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where</p>\n<ul>\n<li>D is the diffusion rate</li>\n<li>ΔP is the partial pressure difference between the two ends of the diffusion pathway</li>\n<li>A is the cross-sectional area of the pathway</li>\n<li>S is the solubility of the gas</li>\n<li>d is the distance of diffusion</li>\n<li>MW is the molecular weight of the gas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<strong>Diffusion coefficient</strong> of the gas, which is <strong>proportional to S/√MW</strong> ).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1468","text":"What is the time taken (in‌ seconds) for blood to tr​averse the pulmonary capi‍llaries at rest?","raw_text":"What is the time taken (in seconds) for blood to traverse the pulmonary capillaries at rest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌.0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‌.1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.‌75","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.75","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.75</strong></p><p>Time taken by a red cell is about<strong> 0.75 seconds </strong>to traverse the pulmonary capillaries at rest and 0.3 seconds or less during exercise.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1978","text":"What is the percentage of ‌systemic cardiac output w​hich bypasses the pulmona‍ry circulation (physiolog‌ical shunt)?","raw_text":"What is the percentage of systemic cardiac output which bypasses the pulmonary circulation (physiological shunt)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The percentage of cardiac output that bypasses the pulmonary circulation or <strong>physiological shunt</strong> is <strong>2%</strong>. The <strong>fraction of mixed venous blood </strong>that<strong> does not become oxygenated </strong>as it moves from the <strong>right atrium</strong> to the <strong>left ventricle</strong> is called physiologic shunt.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of physiological shunt:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extrapulmonary R-&gt;L Anatomic Shunt (normally ~ 1% of cardiac output)</li>\n<li>Intrapulmonary “True” Shunt\n<ul>\n<li>Flow past unventilated or collapsed alveoli (e.g., due to edema, pneumonia, atelectasis)</li>\n<li>Anatomic arterio-venous pathways - Mixing of <strong>unoxygenated blood in bronchial veins with oxygenated blood in pulmonary capillaries or vein</strong> reduces the final partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> in the arterial blood (PaO<sub>2</sub>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Intrapulmonary regions with low ventilation/perfusion ratio: Alveolar ventilation is not zero, but blood is not completely oxygenated (A virtual shunt, not a “true” shunt).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1470","text":"A 35-year-old man with sho‌rtness of breath is under​going tests to assess lun‍g function. Which of the ‌following is used to meas​ure lung diffusion capaci​ty?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with shortness of breath is undergoing tests to assess lung function. Which of the following is used to measure lung diffusion capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌O","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"N‌2O","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌O2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"O2 (under normal condition‌)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CO","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31ec4eb420864195bc16dd872b09459dx1280x714.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CO</strong></p><p>The <strong>diffusing capacity for CO (DLCO)</strong> is measured as an <strong>index </strong>of the<strong> diffusing capacity</strong> of the lung because its uptake is diffusion-limited.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 648\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The diffusing capacity of the lung for a given gas is directly proportional to the surface area of the alveolo-capillary membrane and inversely proportional to its thickness.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The normal value of DLCO at rest is about <strong>25 mL/min/mm Hg.</strong></p>\n<p>It increases up to threefold during exercise because of capillary dilation and an increase in the number of active capillaries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1471","text":"The partial pressure of H2‌O at the body temperature​ (37° C) is (in mm Hg)___‍____","raw_text":"The partial pressure of H2O at the body temperature (37° C) is (in mm Hg)_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 47","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 47</strong></p><p>The <strong>partial pressure of water vapor </strong>at a normal body temperature of 37°C is<strong> 47 mm Hg.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Humidification of air:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When dry air enters respiratory system it is <strong>humidified</strong> i.e. water molecules are continually escaping from the water surface (tissues) into the gas phase. </li>\n<li>The partial pressure that the water molecules exert to escape through the surface is called the <strong>vapor pressure of the water.</strong></li>\n<li>The vapor pressure of water <strong>depends entirely on the temperature of the water. </strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>greater the temperature</strong>, the greater the kinetic activity of the molecules and, therefore, the greater the likelihood that the water molecules will escape from the surface of the water into the gas phase.</li>\n<li>For instance, the water vapor pressure at 0°C is 5 mm Hg, and at 100°C it is 760 mm Hg.</li>\n<li>The <strong>most important value</strong> to remember is the <strong>vapor pressure at body temperature 47 mm Hg. (Once the equilibrium is attained after diffusion, vapour pressure is called partial pressure).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table width=\"480\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Gases</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Inspired Air</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Alveolar Air</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Expired Air</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>O<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>158.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>100.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>116.0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>CO<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>0.3</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>40.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>32.0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>H<sub>2</sub>O</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>5.7</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>47.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>47.0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>N<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>596.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>573.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>565.0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Total</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>760.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>760.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>760.0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1472","text":"What is the partial pressu‌re of O2 in the alveolus?​","raw_text":"What is the partial pressure of O2 in the alveolus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 ‌mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"45 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"40 ‌mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 100 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/466a0abea31d4fbeb3c8106257bbbffdx1280x1360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 100 mmHg</strong></p><p>The partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> in the alveolus is <strong>100 mmHg.</strong></p>\n<p>The partial pressure of gases as they enter and leave the lungs is depicted below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9771","text":"If the tidal volume is 500‌ mL, PeCO2 is 30 mmHg and​ PaCO2 is 45 mmHg, calcul‍ate the dead space?","raw_text":"If the tidal volume is 500 mL, PeCO2 is 30 mmHg and PaCO2 is 45 mmHg, calculate the dead space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150‌ mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"160‌ mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"166‌ mL","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"145‌ mL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 166 mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19c716da9d264707a06612bc0440a235x1280x735.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/965e93fa8fff4ec988a8974ef51b472fx1280x735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 166 mL</strong></p><p>The Bohr-enghoff equation is used to calculate physiological dead space/total dead space.</p>\n<p>The original Bohr equation required measurement of the alveolar partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> (P<sub>A</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Later Enghoff modified the equation and replaced the P<sub>A</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> with P<span style=\"font-size: 11.666666030883789px;\">a</span>CO<sub>2</sub> (partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> in arterial blood). Enghoff's modification of<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-4\"></sup> Bohr equation is:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>V<sub>D</sub> is the physiological dead space</li>\n<li>V<sub>T</sub> is the tidal volume</li>\n<li>PaCO<sub>2</sub> is the partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> in the arterial blood</li>\n<li>PeCO<sub>2</sub> is the average partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> in the entire expired air. <strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Substituting the values:</p>\n<p>Vd=(45-30/45) x 500=166.66 ml</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1706","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding dead space​ volume in a healthy indi‍vidual?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding dead space volume in a healthy individual?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anatomical dead space&amp;gt; ‌physiologic dead space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anatomical dead space&amp;lt; ‌physiological dead space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anatomical dead space and ‌physiologic dead space ar​e almost equivalent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anatomical dead space is n‌ot related to physiologic​ dead space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anatomical dead space and physiologic dead space are almost equivalent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anatomical dead space and physiologic dead space are almost equivalent</strong></p><p>In healthy individuals<strong>, anatomical dead space </strong>and<strong> physiologic dead space</strong> are almost equivalent, and can be estimated by body weight.</p>\n<p>Dead space is the volume of the airways and lungs that do not participate in gas exchange.</p>\n<p><strong>Types</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anatomic dead space</strong>- Volume of the air-occupying <strong>conducting zone</strong> where no exchange of gases takes place.</li>\n<li><strong>Alveolar/functional dead space</strong>- the volume of gas not equilibrating with blood (<strong>wasted ventilation</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Total dead space/Physiological dead space = Anatomical + Alveolar dead space.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In healthy individuals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anatomical dead space = 150 mL.</li>\n<li>Alveolar dead space is usually zero (increased in diseased conditions).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hence,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Physiological dead space = Anatomical + Alveolar = 150 + 0 = 150 mL.</li>\n<li>Physiological dead space = Anatomical dead space.</li>\n<li>Radford's formula: Anatomical dead space in mL is approximately equal to the body weight in pounds.</li>\n<li>Dead space can be measured by the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Single Breath N<sub>2 </sub>method (Fowler’s method)</li>\n<li>Bohr’s equation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1880","text":"From base to the apex of t‌he lung during standing, ​all the following decreas‍e except ______","raw_text":"From base to the apex of the lung during standing, all the following decrease except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary ‌blood flow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventilation per unit volum‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"V/Q ‌ratio","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intrapleural pressure (wit‌h respect to atmospheric ​pressure)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. V/Q ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21883d3f84f645af8dde49c6c49dfc67x1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. V/Q ratio</strong></p><p><strong>V/Q ratio</strong> does not decrease from the base to apex of the lung while standing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Normal differences in ventilation and perfusion of the lung in the upright position (refer figure).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Blood flow distribution in the lung</p>\n<table style=\"height: 230px; width: 354.742px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Zones of lung</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\"><strong>Blood flow</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\"><strong>Pressure driving blood flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Zone 1 (near Apex)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Lowest </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\">P<sub>A </sub>&gt; P<sub>a </sub>&gt; P<sub>v</sub></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Zone 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Medium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\">P<sub>a </sub>&gt; P<sub>A </sub>&gt; P<sub>v</sub></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Zone 3 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Highest </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\">P<sub>a </sub>&gt; P<sub>v </sub>&gt; P<sub>A</sub></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>P<sub>A</sub>: Alveolar pressure <br/>P<sub>a</sub>: arterial pressure<br/>P<sub>V</sub>: venous pressure</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Ventilation–perfusion ratio is not the same in all part of the lungs <strong>because of the effect of gravity.</strong></p>\n<p>At the apex (Against gravity):</p>\n<p><strong>Both ventilation and perfusion decrease</strong> since the flow is against gravity. But, a <strong>decrease in perfusion is higher than the decrease in ventilation</strong>. Hence, <strong>ratio increases (3.2).</strong></p>\n<p>At the base of lung:</p>\n<p><strong>Both ventilation and perfusion increase</strong> since the flow is towards gravity. Increase in <strong>perfusion is more than increase in ventilation,</strong> i.e., denominator increases. Hence, <strong>ratio decreases to 0.6.</strong></p>\n<p>The average value of V/Q ratio throughout lungs is 0.8.</p>\n<p>Important points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the effect of <strong>gravity is eliminated, as in the supine position</strong>, the <strong>value is the same in all parts of the lung</strong> = 0.8.</li>\n<li><strong>V/Q ratio is infinity</strong> when <strong>perfusion is zero</strong> as in case of <strong>pulmonary embolism.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>V/Q ratio is zero </strong>when<strong> ventilation is zero</strong> like in case of <strong>foreign body obstruction.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>High V/Q ratio at apex </strong>leads to <strong>high pAO<sub>2</sub></strong> which favors the growth of microorganisms like <strong>tubercle bacilli.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intra-pleural pressure decreases</strong> as we move from the <strong>base to the apex</strong> of the lung i.e- becomes more negative as we move from the base to the apex of the lung. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1983","text":"Waterfall effect is seen i‌n ____","raw_text":"Waterfall effect is seen in  ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower and middle portions ‌of the lung","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper portio‌n of the lung","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle portion of the lung‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lower portio‌n of the lung","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle portion of the lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle portion of the lung</strong></p><p>Water fall effect has been used to describe the blood flow in the <strong>middle portion of lung.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 232\">In this region blood flows in pulses. At first there is no flow because of obstruction at the collapsed venous end of the capillary bed. Pressure from the arterial side builds up until it exceeds alveolar pressure and flow resumes. This dissipates the capillary pressure and returns to the start of the cycle.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 232\">The blood flow in this region is determined by the pulmonary artery–alveolar pressure difference. Blood “falls” into the pulmonary veins, which are compliant and take whatever amount of blood can flow into them. This has been called the waterfall effect.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 230\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Blood flow distribution in the lung</p>\n<table style=\"height: 230px; width: 354.742px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Zones of lung</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\"><strong>Blood flow</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\"><strong>Pressure driving blood flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Zone 1 (near Apex)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Lowest </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\">P<sub>A </sub>&gt; P<sub>a </sub>&gt; P<sub>v</sub></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Zone 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Medium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\">P<sub>a </sub>&gt; P<sub>A </sub>&gt; P<sub>v</sub></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Zone 3 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Highest </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.742px;\">P<sub>a </sub>&gt; P<sub>v </sub>&gt; P<sub>A</sub></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>P<sub>A</sub>: Alveolar pressure <br/>P<sub>a</sub>: arterial pressure<br/>P<sub>V</sub>: venous pressure</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0799","text":"V/Q = infinity holds true ‌in which of the following​ conditions?","raw_text":"V/Q = infinity holds true in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foreign body obstruction i‌n the bronchus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Equal to ‌dead space","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"When O2 and CO2 ratio is e‌qual","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The PO2 of alveolar air is‌ 159 mmHg and PCO2 is 40 ​mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Equal to dead space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Equal to dead space</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">V<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">A</span> /Q= infinity means that there is no exchange of O<sub>2</sub> &amp; CO<sub>2</sub>, which means the volume of the lung <strong>equals to dead space </strong>and the physiological effort of breathing is wasted. </p>\n<p>V<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">A</span> represents the ventilation in alveoli and Q represents the blood flow through the alveolus. The ratio of VA and Q explains the respiratory exchange when there is an imbalance between alveolar ventilation and alveolar blood flow. The ventilation-perfusion ratio is considered normal if there is an equal amount of blood flow &amp; ventilation through the alveolus.</p>\n<p><strong>V<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">A</span> /Q ratio</strong> is <strong>infinity </strong>when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">There is adequate ventilation but no perfusion.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">No exchange of gases ( O<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> )through the respiratory membranes of corresponding alveoli.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The alveolar air equilibrates the humidified inspired air.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Partial pressures of the O<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> are 149 mmHg &amp; 0 mm Hg respectively.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1987","text":"The O2-CO2 diagram shown b‌elow shows a ventilation-​perfusion ratio curve for‍ the normal lung. Which o‌f the following options b​est describes the effect ​of decreasing the ventila​tion-perfusion ratio on t​he alveolar Po2 and Pco2?​","raw_text":"The O2-CO2 diagram shown below shows a ventilation-perfusion ratio curve for the normal lung. Which of the following options best describes the effect of decreasing the ventilation-perfusion ratio on the alveolar Po2 and Pco2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↓ O2 tension, ↓CO2 tension‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ O2 tension, ↓CO2 tension‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"O2 tension no change , ↓CO‌2 tension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↓ O2 tension, ↑CO2 tension‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ↓ O2 tension, ↑CO2 tension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00c81654d5f6441f8e8b996534d478a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29ecb5ce7ebd4a13a4f12ff1878cacd8x1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ↓ O2 tension, ↑CO2 tension</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased ventilation-perfusion ratio</strong> implies decreased <strong>oxygenation</strong> due to decreased<strong> ventilation with respect to perfusion</strong>. Hence, <strong>PAO<sub>2</sub> decreases and Pco<sub>2</sub> increases.</strong></p>\n<p>A decrease in the ventilation-perfusion ratio (V<sub>A</sub>/Q) is depicted by moving to the left along the normal ventilation-perfusion line as shown in the figure (Zones here refer to the zones of variation in pulmonary blood flow and alveolar ventilation under influence of gravity)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zone 1</strong> - Zone 1 has the lowest ventilation compared to Zones 2 and 3 but, in Zone 1 <strong>Ventilation (V)&gt;&gt;Perfusion (Q)</strong> hence <strong>V­/Q­ is high</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>PAO<sub>2</sub> is highest</li>\n<li>PACO<sub>2</sub> is lowest</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Zone 2 -  </strong>Ventilation (V) almost equal to Perfusion (Q) but slightly lower so V/Q is average (~0.8)</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zone 3</strong> - Zone 3 has the highest ventilation compared to Zones 2 and 1 but, in Zone 3 <strong>Perfusion (Q) &gt;&gt; Ventilation (V)</strong> hence <strong>V­/Q­ is low</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>PAO<sub>2</sub> is lowest</li>\n<li>PACO<sub>2 </sub>is highest.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*PAO<sub>2</sub> - Alveolar partial pressure of oxygen</p>\n<p>*PACO<sub>2</sub> - Alveolar partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2010","text":"A person with normal lung ‌at sea level (760 mm Hg) ​is breathing 50% oxygen. ‍With a normal respiratory‌ quotient of 0.8, what is​ the approximate alveolar​ Po2 (in mmHg)?","raw_text":"A person with normal lung at sea level (760 mm Hg) is breathing 50% oxygen. With a normal respiratory quotient of 0.8, what is the approximate alveolar Po2 (in mmHg)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7‌13","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌59","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌06","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3‌65","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 306","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d28688fb943c4d908fc735574aafe4b9x1280x735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 306</strong></p><p><strong>Alveolar gas equation is used to calculate partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> in alveolar air.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>It is given by the formula:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PAO<sub>2</sub> = Alveolar PO<sub>2</sub> (mm Hg)</li>\n<li>PIO<sub>2</sub> = PO<sub>2</sub> in inspired air (mm Hg)</li>\n<li>PACO<sub>2</sub> = Alveolar PCO<sub>2</sub> (mm Hg), which is normally 40mmHg</li>\n<li>R = Respiratory exchange ratio or respiratory quotient (CO<sub>2</sub> production/O2 consumption)</li>\n<li>Correction factor is usually negligible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 1: In the question PO<sub>2</sub> in inspired air is not given directly. It has to be calculated using the formula:</p>\n<p><strong>PIO<sub>2</sub> = FIO<sub>2</sub> (P<sub>B</sub> - P<sub>H20</sub>)</strong></p>\n<p>Where</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PIO<sub>2</sub> = PO<sub>2</sub> in inspired air (mm Hg)</li>\n<li>FIO<sub>2</sub> = Fraction of inspired O<sub>2</sub> (50% or 0.5)</li>\n<li>P<sub>B</sub> = Barometric pressure (760 mmHg)</li>\n<li>P<sub>H2O</sub> = Water vapour pressure (47 mmHg)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Substituting the values:</p>\n<p><strong>PIO<sub>2</sub></strong> = 0.5 (760 - 47) = 0.5 × 713 = <strong>356.5 mmHg</strong></p>\n<p>Step 2:</p>\n<p><strong>PAO<sub>2</sub></strong> = P<sub>i</sub>O<sub>2</sub> – (Pco<sub>2</sub>/R) = 356.5 – (40/0.8) = 356.5 – 50 = <strong>306.5 mmHg.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2028","text":"Which of the following dec‌reases in a patient with ​emphysema?","raw_text":"Which of the following decreases in a patient with emphysema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alveol‌ar Pco2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac‌ output","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffusi‌on area","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ar‌tery pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffusion area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffusion area</strong></p><p>In emphysema, the <strong>destruction </strong>of the <strong>alveolar walls</strong> brings down the<strong> diffusion area.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Changes in Emphysema</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Due to the destruction of the alveolar walls, there is a <strong>decrease in surface/diffusion area.</strong></li>\n<li>Cardiac output will be normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary artery pressure</strong> may be <strong>elevated</strong> because the destruction of alveoli leads to loss of surrounding capillaries which forces blood into remaining capillaries, which tends to increase pulmonary artery pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Alveolar Pco<sub>2</sub></strong> will tend to <strong>increase</strong> due to difficulty in expiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diffusion capacity of Lung for CO (DLCO):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In case of <strong>emphysema – Decreased diffusion (DLCO) </strong>because of <strong>decreased surface area</strong> (trypsin destroys respiratory membrane)</li>\n<li>In case of <strong>fibrosis or pulmonary edema – Decreased diffusion (DLCO) </strong>because of<strong> increased thickness of membrane </strong></li>\n<li>In case of anemia – Decreased DLCO</li>\n<li>Polycythemia, pulmonary hemorrhage – Increased DLCO.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2022","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes the mean arte​rial oxygen tension (pO2)‍, carbon dioxide tension ‌(pCO2) and pH in a health​y athlete during moderate​ exercise?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the mean arterial oxygen tension (pO2), carbon dioxide tension (pCO2) and pH in a healthy athlete during moderate exercise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↓ Arterial pO2, ↓ Arterial‌ pCO2, ↓ Arterial pH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↓ Arterial pO2, ↑ Arterial‌ pCO2, ↓ Arterial pH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ Arterial pO2, ↓ Arterial‌ pCO2, ↑Arterial pH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No change in Arterial pO2,‌ Arterial pCO2 or Arteria​l pH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No change in Arterial pO2, Arterial pCO2 or Arterial pH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No change in Arterial pO2, Arterial pCO2 or Arterial pH</strong></p><p>During moderate exercise, there is <strong>no change</strong> in arterial pO2, arterial pCO2 or arterial pH.</p>\n<p><strong>Ventilation increases</strong> abruptly at the onset of exercise due to <strong>psychic stimuli</strong> and afferent impulses from <strong>proprioceptors</strong> in muscles, tendons, and joints. This is followed by a brief pause and then a gradual increase in ventilation. This <strong>gradual increase</strong> in ventilation is believed to be due to multiple factors such as an increase in <strong>body temperature</strong>, increase in <strong>plasma K<sup>+</sup> level</strong> stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors, or the sensitivity of the neurons controlling the response to CO<sub>2</sub>. </p>\n<p>In strenuous exercise, at a certain point, the O<sub>2</sub> uptake reaches its maximum levels, beyond which it cannot increase further. This leads to a rise in <strong>blood lactate</strong> levels. Buffering of lactic acid liberates CO<sub>2</sub> which further increases ventilation. The increased production of lactic acid, increase in ventilation and CO2 production are <strong>proportional</strong>, and alveolar and arterial CO<sub>2</sub> changes little to nil. This is called <strong>isocapnic buffering.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ig3AQQuk","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"21_dissociative_disorders","title":"21.Dissociative Disorders","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD0965","text":"As per DSM-5, which of the​ following is not classif‍ied under dissociative di‍sorders?","raw_text":"As per DSM-5, which of the following is not classified under dissociative disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple identity disorder​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conversio​n disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Derealizati​on disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depersonalisation disorder​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conversion disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conversion disorder</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 335\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>As per DSM-5, <strong>conversion disorder</strong> (functional neurological symptom disorder) is classified under <strong>somatic symptom and related disorders, </strong>and not under dissociative disorders.</p>\n<p>DSM-5 classification of dissociative disorders includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dissociative identity disorder (multiple identity disorder)</li>\n<li>Dissociative amnesia</li>\n<li>Depersonalization/derealization disorder</li>\n<li>Other specified dissociative disorder</li>\n<li>Unspecified dissociative disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Following psychological trauma, <strong>conversion</strong> disorder produces <strong>sensorimotor</strong> symptoms whereas <strong>dissociative</strong> disorders produce <strong>cognitive</strong> symptoms. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6950","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about di‍ssociative disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about dissociative disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Often preceded by psycholo​gical trauma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Involves a conscious defen​ce mechanism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with disruption​ of neurocognitive functi‍ons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Experienced as inability t​o access information that‍ nor­mally is readily acc‍essible","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Involves a conscious defence mechanism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Involves a conscious defence mechanism</strong></p><p><strong>Dissociation,</strong> which is an <strong>unconscious defense mechanism</strong> is the underlying mechanism in all dissociative disorders. </p>\n<p>Dissociative disorders result from the disruption of cognitive functions due to psychological trauma. They can be experienced as an inability to access information or to control mental functions that normally are readily amenable to access or control. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1630","text":"Which of the following is ​a negative dissociation s‍ymptom?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a negative dissociation symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amn​esia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fragmentatio​n of identity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deperson​alization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dereal​ization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amnesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amnesia</strong></p><p><strong>Amnesia</strong> is a <strong>negative dissociation symptom.</strong></p>\n<p>Positive dissociation symptoms are involuntary intrusions into awareness and behavior, with loss of continuity in subjective experience. E.g., fragmentation of identity, depersonalization, and derealization.</p>\n<p>Negative dissociation symptoms are the inability to access information or to control mental functions that normally can be readily accessed or controlled. E.g., amnesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1624","text":"A woman brings her husband​ to the psychiatry OPD. S‍he says he is usually shy‍ and mature. The previous‌ week, she saw him flirti​ng with a girl. When conf​ronted, he said he could ‌not recall this at all. D​uring the fight, he sudde‌nly started talking in a ‍child-like voice and bega​n to cry. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman brings her husband to the psychiatry OPD. She says he is usually shy and mature. The previous week, she saw him flirting with a girl. When confronted, he said he could not recall this at all. During the fight, he suddenly started talking in a child-like voice and began to cry. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dissociat​ive fugue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bipolar ​disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissociative identity diso​rder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Deperson​alization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dissociative identity disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dissociative identity disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>dissociative identity disorder. </strong>It is characterized by the presence of <strong>two or more</strong> distinct identities or<strong> personality states</strong>. It was previously called multiple personality disorder.</p>\n<p>The identities or personality states are called <strong>alters</strong>, <strong>self­ states</strong>, or <strong>alter identities</strong>. Each has its own pattern of perceiving and thinking about the environment and self. It is strongly associated with severe expe­riences of <strong>early childhood trauma</strong>, usually maltreatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1638","text":"A man was brought to the E​R with severe chest pain ‍which was proven to be du‍e to myocardial infarctio‌n. After recovery, howeve​r, he couldn't remember h​is 3-year-old son but cou‌ld recognize his wife who​ he married 4 years ago. ‌He said he works as a sof‍tware engineer but his wi​fe reported that he quit ​his job 6 months ago. Int​erestingly, he is still a‌ble to play the violin wh​ich he learnt last year. ​What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A man was brought to the ER with severe chest pain which was proven to be due to myocardial infarction. After recovery, however, he couldn't remember his 3-year-old son but could recognize his wife who he married 4 years ago. He said he works as a software engineer but his wife reported that he quit his job 6 months ago. Interestingly, he is still able to play the violin which he learnt last year. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterogra​de amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Localize​d amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Selectiv​e amnesia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Systematiz​ed amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Selective amnesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Selective amnesia</strong></p><p>The patient has features of <strong>dissociative amnesia</strong>. This patient's ability to remember some, but not all, of the events occurring during a circumscribed period of time, is known as <strong>selective amnesia.</strong></p>\n<p>Dissociative amnesia is defined as an inability to recall important auto­biographical information that is successfully stored in memory and ordinar­ily would be readily remembered. It is potentially reversible (unlike amnesias due to neuro-biological damage). It is further categorized into the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Selective amnesia - </strong>ability to remember some, but not all, of the events occurring during a circumscribed period of time.</li>\n<li><strong>Localized amnesia - </strong>inability to recall all events related to a circumscribed period of time.</li>\n<li><strong>Generalized amnesia - </strong>failure to recall one’s entire life.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous amnesia - </strong>failure to recall successive events as they occur.</li>\n<li><strong>Systematized amnesia - </strong>amnesia for certain categories of memory, such as all memories relating to one’s family or a particular person.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5308","text":"A 52-year-old man is broug​ht to the emergency depar‍tment after being found i‍n a confused state at the‌ interstate bus station i​n Kerala. He is unable to​ recall his name, address‌, or any other personal i​nformation. His driving l‌icense from his wallet re‍vealed that he is from Ba​ngalore and that his wife​ had reported him missing​ 2 days ago. She says tha‌t he got laid off from hi​s job, after which he did​ not come home. Which of ‌the following is the most​ likely diagnosis for thi​s patient's condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after being found in a confused state at the interstate bus station in Kerala. He is unable to recall his name, address, or any other personal information. His driving license from his wallet revealed that he is from Bangalore and that his wife had reported him missing 2 days ago. She says that he got laid off from his job, after which he did not come home. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dissociati​ve Amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dissociat​ive Fugue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dissociative Identity Diso​rder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depersonalization Disorder​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dissociative Fugue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dissociative Fugue</strong></p><p>History of sudden <strong>amnesia and wandering</strong> to the new place is suggestive of <strong>dissociative fugue</strong>.</p>\n<p>The onset of dissociative fuge is usually sudden, often in the presence of <strong>severe stress</strong>. The termination also is abrupt and is followed by amnesia for the episode, but with the recovery of memories of earlier life.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1631","text":"During the evaluation of a​ patient, a temporary los‍s of the sense of persona‍l identity without the re‌placement by an alternate​ identity is noted. What ​is the most likely cause ‌of this patient's conditi​on?","raw_text":"During the evaluation of a patient, a temporary loss of the sense of personal identity without the replacement by an alternate identity is noted. What is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dissociative trance disord​er","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dissociative identity diso​rder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissociat​ive fugue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dissociati​ve amnesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dissociative trance disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dissociative trance disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>dissociative trance disorder. </strong>It manifests by a<strong> temporary, </strong>marked alteration in the state of consciousness or by <strong>loss</strong> of the customary sense of<strong> personal identity without the replacement</strong> by an<strong> alternate</strong> sense of <strong>identity</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute narrowing or complete <strong>loss of awareness</strong> of immediate surroundings</li>\n<li>Profound unresponsiveness or <strong>insensitivity</strong> to environmental stimuli</li>\n<li>May be accompanied by minor <strong>stereotyped behaviors</strong> (e.g., finger movements) of which the individual is unaware</li>\n<li>Transient paralysis or loss of consciousness</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Possession trance </strong>is a variant of dissociative trance. It involves single or episodic alternations in the state of consciousness, characterized by the exchange of the person's customary identity with a new identity usually attributed to a <strong>spirit, divine power, deity, or another person</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this possessed state, the individual exhibits stereotypical and culturally determined behaviors or experiences being controlled by the possessing entity. There is always partial or full <strong>amnesia for the event</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2949","text":"A 27-year-old woman compla​ined that she was feeling‍ detached from herself. S‍he reported that she felt‌ unreal, empty, and perce​ived she was watching her​self in a movie. Her fami‌ly says she was raped rec​ently and has not been he‌rself ever since. Which o‍f the following best desc​ribes her perception?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman complained that she was feeling detached from herself. She reported that she felt unreal, empty, and perceived she was watching herself in a movie. Her family says she was raped recently and has not been herself ever since. Which of the following best describes her perception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dereal​ization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dissociat​ive fugue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissociati​ve amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deperson​alization","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Depersonalization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Depersonalization</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>depersonalization. </strong>It is defined as the <strong>persistent or recurrent</strong> feeling of <strong>detachment</strong> or estrangement <strong>from one's self</strong>.</p>\n<p>The individual may report feeling like <strong>watching himself or herself in a movie</strong> or feeling like an<strong> automaton</strong>. Experiences of unreality, detachment, or<strong> being an outside observer</strong> with respect to one's thoughts, feelings, sensations, body, or actions (e.g., perceptual alterations, distorted sense of time, unreal or absent self, emotional and physical numbing).</p>\n<p>Derealization refers to feelings of unreality or of being detached from one's environment. The patient may describe his or her perception of the outside world as lacking lucidity and emotional coloring, as though dreaming or dead.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1645","text":"Which of the following con​ditions are associated wi‍th wandering? Conversion ‍disorder Depersonalizatio‌n Complex partial seizure​s Mania Dissociative iden​tity disorder","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions are associated with wandering?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conversion disorder</li>\n<li>Depersonalization</li>\n<li>Complex partial seizures</li>\n<li>Mania</li>\n<li>Dissociative identity disorder</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ​and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Wandering</strong> is travel away from the individual's home or customary place of daily activities and it can be seen in <strong>complex partial seizures</strong>,<strong> mania</strong> and<strong> dissociative identity disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Wandering is seen in the following neuropsychiatric conditions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dissociative fugue - there is purposeful wandering, usually with the individual preoccupied with a single idea that is accompanied by a wish to run away</li>\n<li>Dissociative amnesia</li>\n<li>Dissociative personality disorder</li>\n<li>Focal onset impaired awareness seizure (complex partial seizures) - exhibit wandering or semi purposeful behavior, or both, during seizures or in postictal states, for which subsequent amnesia occurs</li>\n<li>Manic phase of bipolar disorder or schizoaffective disorder</li>\n<li>Schizophrenia</li>\n<li>Malingering</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5315","text":"Hysterical pseudodementia ​is a type of ____?","raw_text":"Hysterical pseudodementia is a type of ____?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deme​ntia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malin​gering","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissociati​ve disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Personalit​y disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dissociative disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dissociative disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Ganser syndrome</strong> (hysterical pseudodementia) is a type of <strong>dissociative disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>It is commonly found in prison inmates. The characteristic feature is <strong>vorbeireden,</strong> which is also called approximate answers. The answers are incorrect but show that the person understands the nature of the question asked. E.g., when asked about the color of an apple, the patient's answer is blue. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aJhZ44dO","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"21_small_intestine","title":"21.Small Intestine","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MB7055","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g the surgical anatomy of‌ the small intestine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the surgical anatomy of the small intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The duodenum is demarcated‌ from the jejunum by the ‍ligament of Treitz.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The ileum makes up three-f‌ifths of the small intest‍ine.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The blood supply to the je‌junum may have four or fi‍ve separate arcades with ‌shorter vasa recta","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The jejunum has a larger c‌ircumference, is thicker ‍than the ileum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The blood supply to the jejunum may have four or five separate arcades with shorter vasa recta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f5f077035ea426e8bceec840d2e0f72x1280x2065.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3adfd10ea03a433eb7aee6518c58a52dx1280x1267.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The blood supply to the jejunum may have four or five separate arcades with shorter vasa recta</strong></p><p>The <strong>jejunum</strong> receives its blood supply from <strong>long, straight vasa recta </strong>arising from <strong>only one or two arcades</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>The blood supply to the <strong>ileum</strong> may have <strong>4-5 separate arcades</strong> with <strong>shorter vasa recta.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> ligament of Treitz </strong>or the suspensory muscle of the duodenum is found at the <strong>junction</strong> of the <strong>duodenum and jejunum</strong>. It also forms the line of <strong>demarcation</strong> for <strong>gastrointestinal bleeding</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upper</strong> GI bleeding - refers to bleeding <strong>proximal</strong> to the ligament of Treitz</li>\n<li><strong>Lower</strong> GI bleeding - refers to bleeding <strong>distal</strong> to the ligament of Treitz.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7065","text":"In a plain X-ray of the ab‌domen, the small bowel is‍ said to be dilated if th‌e diameter is more than _​_____.","raw_text":"In a plain X-ray of the abdomen, the small bowel is said to be dilated if the diameter is more than  ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 ‌cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 ‌cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65cce0615af9418084c51f485eeb45c4x720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 cm</strong></p><p>The <strong>small bowel</strong> is said to be <strong>dilated</strong> if the <strong>diameter is more than 3 cm </strong>in a plain X-ray.</p>\n<p>The following triad of findings is<strong> specific </strong>for<strong> small bowel obstruction</strong> on <strong>plain X-ray</strong> imaging of the abdomen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated small bowel loops <strong>(&gt;3 cm in diameter)</strong></li>\n<li>Air-fluid levels seen on upright films </li>\n<li>A paucity of air in the colon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7058","text":"In a suspected case of bow‌el obstruction, the prese‍nce of which of the follo‌wing helps you to disting​uish the small intestine ‍from the large intestine?​","raw_text":"In a suspected case of bowel obstruction, the presence of which of the following helps you to distinguish the small intestine from the large intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Appendices‌ epiploicae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Valvule c‌onniventes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tenia‌ coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haustr‌ations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Valvule conniventes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20bcee9bbe60414a8d6601aab7d71da1x736x373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Valvule conniventes</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>valvule conniventes (plicae circulares) </strong>helps to distinguish the <strong>small intestine</strong> from the large intestine. They are<strong> prominent mucosal folds </strong>seen in the gross examination of the small intestine and<strong> </strong>are also visible radiographically. They are more prominent in the <strong>jejunum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiological differences between small bowel and large bowel obstruction are:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Radiological features</td>\n<td>Small bowel</td>\n<td>Large bowel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valvulae conniventes</td>\n<td>Present in jejunum</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No of Loops</td>\n<td>Many</td>\n<td>Few</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution of loops</td>\n<td>Central</td>\n<td>Peripheral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Haustra</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diameter</td>\n<td>3-5 cm</td>\n<td>&gt;5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solid faeces</td>\n<td>Absent</td>\n<td>Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Investigation of choice: <strong>CECT </strong>abdomen (<strong>adults</strong>); <strong>USG </strong>abdomen (<strong>children</strong>)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4286","text":"A 42-year-old man with pas‌t history of inguinal her‍nia presented with sudden‌ onset of severe abdomina​l pain that started 4 hou‍rs ago. His abdomen was t​ender and rigid along wit‌h a lump in the inguinal ‌region with purplish skin​ changes. Which of the fo‌llowing is the ideal mana‌gement for this patient?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man with past history of inguinal hernia presented with sudden onset of severe abdominal pain that started 4 hours ago. His abdomen was tender and rigid along with a lump in the inguinal region with purplish skin changes. Which of the following is the ideal management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resuscitative measures and‌ arrange for immediate la‍parotomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Conservative management wi‌th a nasogastric tube and‍ IV fluid, assessment eve‌ry 24 hrs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perform an oral contrast s‌tudy, followed by pneumat‍ic decompression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoscopic d‌ecompression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Resuscitative measures and arrange for immediate laparotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Resuscitative measures and arrange for immediate laparotomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>intestinal obstruction</strong>,  due to a <strong>strangulated inguinal hernia</strong>. The ideal management is <strong>exploratory laparotomy</strong> as soon as possible with ongoing resuscitative measures until surgery.<br/>Bowel strangulation is a type of mechanical bowel obstruction, associated with <strong>complete cessation</strong> of <strong>blood supply</strong> leading to irreversible loss of viability of the affected bowel segment. Inguinal hernias, most commonly of non-reducible type, are associated with a high chance of strangulation and need urgent medical intervention.</p>\n<p>Bowel strangulation can be suspected in the presence of</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">constant severe abdominal pain, </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">abdominal tenderness with <strong>rigidity</strong> and <strong>peritonism</strong>, </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">signs of <strong>shock</strong>, and lack of response to conservative management. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Reduced bowel wall enhancement</strong> on CT </li>\n</ul>\n<p>When strangulation occurs in an external hernia, the lump is tense, tender, and irreducible with overlying <strong>skin changes</strong> (erythema or purplish discoloration).</p>\n<p>Supportive management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV fluids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nasogastric tube to decompress the bowel and reduce abdominal distention</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV antibiotics (adequate coverage required for gram -ve and anaerobes)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Analgesia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Definitive management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Continue supportive management up to 72 hrs if suspicious of adhesions. Surgical exploration and adhesiolysis should be done if there is no improvement.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immediate surgical exploration and resection of non-viable gut segments if suspicious of any of the following:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Obstructed external hernia</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clinical features suspicious of intestinal <strong>strangulation</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obstruction in a ‘virgin’ abdomen (i.e no prior history of intraabdominal surgery)</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4534","text":"A surgeon sees a necrotic ‌bowel when operating on a‍ child for an inguinal he‌rnia. What is true about ​the resection and anastom‍osis of the bowel?","raw_text":"A surgeon sees a necrotic bowel when operating on a child for an inguinal hernia. What is true about the resection and anastomosis of the bowel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The surgeon must use chrom‌ic catgut","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The surgeon must not inclu‌de the submucosa in the s‍utures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The surgeon must use the s‌ingle-layer, extramucosal‍ suture technique","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The surgeon must use the s‌ingle-layer seromuscular ‍suture technique","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The surgeon must use the single-layer, extramucosal suture technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The surgeon must use the single-layer, extramucosal suture technique</strong></p><p>The standard technique used currently for resection anastomosis of the bowel is a <strong>one-layer extra mucosal suture technique</strong> since it causes the <strong>least tissue necrosis and luminal narrowing</strong>. Interrupted and continuous single-layer suture techniques are adequate and safe.</p>\n<p>The extra mucosal suture must also include the<strong> submucosa</strong> because this layer has a high collagen content and it is the <strong>most stable suture layer</strong> in all sections of the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p><strong>Catgut</strong> has now been <strong>replaced </strong>with <strong>absorbable synthetic polymers</strong>. Stapling devices can be used when speed is required.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4298","text":"A 5-year-old child who und‌erwent an appendicectomy ‍is brought to the emergen‌cy with abdominal distens​ion and vomiting. The mot‍her tells that the child ​passed stools that looked‌ like currant jelly. The ‌ultrasound finding is sho​wn below. What is the mos‌t likely location of this‌ pathology in this child?‍","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child who underwent an appendicectomy is brought to the emergency with abdominal distension and vomiting. The mother tells that the child passed stools that looked like currant jelly. The ultrasound finding is shown below. What is the most likely location of this pathology in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colo‌colic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rectos‌igmoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ileo‌ileal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ileo‌colic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ileoileal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b5bac3b00287420c977422d3e45cfedd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/963ee27ab03b4b03b453e06f2c28e509x720x339.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ileoileal</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette and the USG showing the <strong>doughnut sign (target sign)</strong> are suggestive of <strong>postoperative</strong> <strong>intussusception</strong> and the most likely location is <strong>ileoileal in postoperative cases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Intussusception occurs when a portion of the alimentary tract is<strong> telescoped</strong> into an adjacent segment. The most common location of intussusception is <strong>ileocolic</strong>. However, <strong>ileoileal</strong> is the most common location of intussusception <strong>postoperatively</strong> and in <strong>Henoch–Schönlein Purpura</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of intussusception are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Idiopathic (most common cause in children)</li>\n<li>Pathological <strong>lead points</strong> such as <strong>en</strong><strong>larged </strong>submucosal<strong> Peyer's patch, </strong>lymphoma, intestinal polyp, leiomyomas, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Meckel's diverticulum </strong></li>\n<li>A recent upper GI or <strong>respiratory infection</strong>, <strong>rotavirus vaccination</strong> has also shown some association with intussusception in children.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The characteristic clinical triad includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Palpable sausage-shaped mass</li>\n<li><strong>Red currant jelly stools</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>USG findings in intussusception include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target sign</strong> (also known as the doughnut sign)- The appearance is generated by concentric alternating <strong>echogenic</strong> (submucosa and serosa) with <strong>hypoechogenic bands</strong> (muscularis propria).</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudokidney sign</strong>: this is seen in the longitudinal section of the bowel:\n<ul>\n<li>the edematous bowel resembles renal parenchyma and</li>\n<li>the mesentery which is dragged into the intussusception resembles the renal hilum.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Crescent in a doughnut sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Barium enema</strong> shows the characteristic <strong>coiled spring appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduction</strong> of intussusception should be done immediately after the diagnosis.</li>\n<li>Radiologic <strong>hydrostatic reduction</strong> has a success rate of 80-95% in cases of ileocolic intussusception</li>\n<li>Surgical reduction is only done in the presence of refractory shock, suspected bowel necrosis or perforation, peritonitis, and multiple recurrences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The above image shows the coiled spring appearance</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7166","text":"A 3-day-old neonate presen‌ts with abdominal distens‍ion and bilious vomiting.‌ An erect X-ray of the ab​domen is done and shown b‍elow. The neonate is take​n up for surgery and an a‌pple-peel bowel with loss‌ of dorsal mesentery is s​een. Which type of ileal ‌atresia has this feature?‌","raw_text":"A 3-day-old neonate presents with abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. An erect X-ray of the abdomen is done and shown below. The neonate is taken up for surgery and an apple-peel bowel with loss of dorsal mesentery is seen. Which type of ileal atresia has this feature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd7119bb99df437a8ce4bfd0e33fc38a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dca6d08b92d6418a80b1c748c1028b3ax1280x4449.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52089dbbb9a047a68a9be246dc192ad3x331x325.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 3</strong></p><p>The given image showing a <strong>triple bubble appearance </strong>and associated finding of<strong> apple-peel bowel</strong> with loss of dorsal mesentery is a feature of <strong>type 3 ileal atresia.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1314\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1314\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small bowel atresias present with intestinal obstruction soon after birth. <strong>Bilious vomiting</strong> is the dominant feature in <strong>jejunal</strong> <strong>atresia</strong> whereas <strong>abdominal distension</strong> is more with <strong>ileal</strong> <strong>atresia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Plain abdominal radiographs show a variable number of <strong>dilated loops</strong> of bowel and fluid levels according to the level of obstruction.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jejunal/ileal</strong> atresia - <strong>Triple</strong> bubble appearance</li>\n<li><strong>Duodenal</strong> atresia - <strong>Double</strong> bubble appearance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>contrast study</strong> is the <strong>next best investigation</strong> to exclude other bowel anomalies. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1315\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In jejunal/ileal atresia, the <strong>distal end </strong>of the<strong> dilated proximal small bowel </strong>is<strong> resected</strong> and a primary end-to-end anastomosis is possible. If the proximal bowel is extremely dilated, it may need to be tapered to the distal bowel before anastomosis. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Classification of Jejunoileal atresia:</p>\n<table width=\"433\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p><strong>Type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"333\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Type 1</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"333\">\n<p>Mucosal web or diaphragm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Type 2</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"333\">\n<p>With an atretic cord between two blind ends of bowel with intact mesentery</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Type 3 a</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"333\">\n<p>Complete separation of the blind ends of the bowel by a V-shaped mesenteric gap</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Type 3 b</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"333\">\n<p>An <strong>apple peel</strong> or <strong>Christmas tree deformity</strong> with a large mesenteric gap</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Type 4</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"333\">\n<p>Multiple atresias, with a string of sausage appearance</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7074","text":"A child with complaints of‌ abdominal pain and vomit‍ing for the past 24 hours‌ associated with tenderne​ss over McBurney's point ‍undergoes open appendicec​tomy. However, during sur‌gery, the appendix was fo‌und to be normal. On expl​oration of the ileum, the‌ following finding is see‌n. Which of the following‍ statements is false rega​rding this condition?","raw_text":"A child with complaints of abdominal pain and vomiting for the past 24 hours associated with tenderness over McBurney's point undergoes open appendicectomy. However, during surgery, the appendix was found to be normal. On exploration of the ileum, the following finding is seen. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a lead point for int‌ussusception","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric mucosa is the most‌ common heterotropic tiss‍ue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arises at the mesenteric b‌order","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Located 60 cm proximal to ‌ileocecal valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arises at the mesenteric border","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e44f622a1de4f85bca7e47113d8320e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ee1269bed57481e9efe8bbeaf3f9cf3x600x684.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arises at the mesenteric border</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Meckel’s diverticulitis</strong>. It arises from the <strong>antimesenteric border </strong>of the small intestine.</p>\n<p>Meckel’s diverticulum is the most commonly encountered congenital anomaly of the small intestine. Meckel’s diverticulum is caused by a <strong>failure </strong>of normal <strong>regression</strong> of the <strong>vitelline duct</strong> or omphalomesenteric duct that develops during weeks 5 to 7 of gestation. It contains all 3 layers of the intestine and hence it is a <strong>true diverticulum</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is located <strong>45 to 60 cm proximal </strong>to the <strong>ileocecal valve.</strong><strong> </strong>The most common <strong>heterotopic </strong>tissue within the <strong>Meckel’s diverticulum </strong>is<strong> gastric mucosa </strong>(50%) followed by pancreatic mucosa(5%) and colonic mucosa.</p>\n<p>Most Meckel’s diverticula are <strong>benign</strong> and are <strong>incidentally</strong> discovered. Its clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrointestinal bleeding (most common)</li>\n<li><strong>Intestinal obstruction</strong>, which may occur as:  \n<ul>\n<li>Volvulus</li>\n<li>Intussusception (ileo-ileal or ileo-colic)</li>\n<li>Incarcerated <strong>Littre’s hernia.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Complications of Meckel's diverticulum are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diverticulitis - presents like appendicitis</li>\n<li>Perforation - resembles a perforated duodenal ulcer</li>\n<li>Peritonitis</li>\n<li>Neoplasms - <strong>neuroendocrine tumour </strong>(most common) &gt; adenocarcinoma  &gt; GIST &gt; lymphoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7095","text":"What is the most common pr‌esentation of Meckel’s di‍verticulum?","raw_text":"What is the most common presentation of Meckel’s diverticulum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower GI‌ bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diar‌rhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin‌al pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lower GI bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lower GI bleeding</strong></p><p>The most common presentation of Meckel’s diverticulum is <strong>lower gastrointestinal bleeding.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of Meckel's diverticulum include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lower GI bleeding</strong> (most common in <strong>children</strong> and overall)</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Another common presentation is an <strong>intestinal obstruction</strong> (most common in <strong>adults</strong>), which may occur due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Volvulus</li>\n<li>Intussusception (ileo-ileal or ileo-colic) </li>\n<li>Incarcerated Littre’s hernia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7098","text":"Which of the following can‌not be used to increase t‍he sensitivity of the Tc-‌pertechnate scan in Mecke​l's diverticulum?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used to increase the sensitivity of the Tc-pertechnate scan in Meckel's diverticulum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluc‌agon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pentag‌astrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cimet‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metoclo‌pramide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metoclopramide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metoclopramide</strong></p><p>Metoclopramide is not used to increase the sensitivity of the Tc-pertechnate scan in Meckel's diverticulum. </p>\n<p><strong>Metoclopramide</strong> stimulates gastric motility and decreases the time available for the uptake of Tc-pertechnate. Hence it <strong>reduces sensitivity</strong>.</p>\n<p><sup>99m</sup>Tc-pertechnetate is preferentially taken up by the <strong>mucus-secreting cells of the gastric mucosa</strong> and <strong>ectopic gastric tissue.</strong></p>\n<p>The sensitivity of the Tc-pertechnate scan is increased by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glucagon</strong>: Relaxes the smooth muscles of the gastrointestinal tract. This decreases peristalsis, causing enhanced detection of the ectopic gastric mucosa in Meckel's diverticulum</li>\n<li><strong>Pentagastrin</strong>: Potent stimulator of gastric secretions. Increases uptake and secretion of pertechnetate by the gastric mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Cimetidine</strong>: Increases radionuclide uptake, and decreases peptic secretion and the release of pertechnetate from the diverticular lumen. This results in higher radionuclide concentrations in the wall of the diverticulum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7070","text":"What will be the investiga‌tion of choice in a child‍ suspected to have Meckel‌’s diverticula?","raw_text":"What will be the investigation of choice in a child suspected to have Meckel’s diverticula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tc-99 radio‌nuclide scan","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Barium‌ study","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Entero‌clysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endo‌scopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tc-99 radionuclide scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0058e2da3c444e20afc460be0d164c7bx1024x862.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tc-99 radionuclide scan</strong></p><p>The investigation of choice in a <strong>child</strong> suspected to have <strong>Meckel's diverticulum</strong> is a <strong><sup>99m</sup>Tc-pertechnetate</strong> radionuclide scan.</p>\n<p>The radionucleotide is preferentially taken<strong> </strong>up by the <strong>mucus-secreting cells </strong>of the <strong>gastric mucosa</strong> and <strong>ectopic gastric tissue</strong> in the <strong>diverticulum.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>adults</strong>, there is a reduced prevalence of ectopic gastric mucosa. Hence it is less accurate and <strong>barium studies </strong>are preferred. </p>\n<p>The image below depicts a <sup>99m</sup>Tc radionuclide scan showing Meckel’s diverticulum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Most of the Meckel’s diverticula are discovered <strong>incidentally</strong>. In the absence of bleeding, Meckel’s diverticula are rarely diagnosed prior to the time of surgical intervention.</p>\n<p>Other investigations for Meckel's diverticulum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Angiography </strong>is used to locate the site of bleeding in an acute hemorrhage. </li>\n<li><strong>CT scan </strong>has a very <strong>low sensitivity. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Enteroclysis</strong><strong> </strong>has<strong> </strong>an accuracy of 75% but is usually not applicable during acute presentations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7104","text":"While performing an inguin‌al hernia repair in a 3-y‍ear-old boy, you notice M‌eckel’s diverticulum as t​he content of the hernial‍ sac. What is this hernia​ called?","raw_text":"While performing an inguinal hernia repair in a 3-year-old boy, you notice Meckel’s diverticulum as the content of the hernial sac. What is this hernia called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amyand’‌s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narath'‌s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cloquet'‌s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Littre’‌s hernia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Littre’s hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/362187e7798048298b3ce3402d79e168x1200x1310.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Littre’s hernia</strong></p><p><strong>Meckel’s diverticulum</strong> found in inguinal or femoral hernial sacs is known as <strong>Littre's hernia. </strong>It<strong> </strong>can cause intestinal obstruction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Amyand’s hernia</strong> is an <strong>inguinal </strong>hernia in which the hernia sac <strong>contains</strong> a normal or acutely inflamed <strong>appendix</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Narath’s hernia </strong>is seen in patients with <strong>congenital dislocation of the hip</strong>. It is a <strong>femoral </strong>hernia seen behind the femoral vessels.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Cloquet’s hernia </strong>is a type of <strong>femoral </strong>hernia where the sac lies under the pectineus fascia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7075","text":"While performing a laparot‌omy, the surgeon incident‍ally notices a narrow-mou‌thed, thickened Meckel’s ​diverticulum. What is the‍ treatment of choice?","raw_text":"While performing a laparotomy, the surgeon incidentally notices a narrow-mouthed, thickened  Meckel’s diverticulum. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resection of the diverticu‌lum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Leave ‌as such","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ligate ‌at base","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resection with part of ile‌um","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Resection of the diverticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Resection of the diverticulum</strong></p><p>In a <strong>narrow-mouthed, thickened </strong>Meckel’s diverticulum,<strong> resection </strong>of the diverticulum is the treatment of choice<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>When Meckel's diverticulum is found in the course of abdominal surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In cases of <strong>narrow-mouthed</strong>, thickened Meckel’s diverticulum, <strong>resection</strong> of the diverticulum is the treatment of choice</li>\n<li>If it has a <strong>wide mouth </strong>and is <strong>not thickened, </strong>it can safely be <strong>left alone.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rarely, Meckel's diverticulum is <strong>complicated</strong> by a <strong>meso-diverticular</strong> <strong>band</strong>, which is believed to be a remnant of a vitelline artery. In such <strong>symptomatic</strong> cases, the treatment is <strong>diverticulectomy </strong>with the<strong> removal of associated bands</strong> connecting the diverticulum to the abdominal wall or intestinal mesentery.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1191\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Segmental resection of the ileum</strong> that includes both the diverticulum and the adjacent ileal peptic ulcer should be performed if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> There is bleeding from the diverticulum</li>\n<li> The base is inflamed or perforated</li>\n<li> The diverticulum contains a tumour.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7107","text":"What is the most common si‌te for acquired diverticu‍la in the small intestine‌?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for acquired diverticula in the small intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proxima‌l ileum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal‌ ileum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b51eccfc2dbc419c977b7a32167a2accx562x752.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duodenum</strong></p><p>The most common site for <strong>acquired </strong>diverticula in the <strong>small intestine</strong> is the <strong>second part of the duodenum </strong>(Windsock's diverticulum).</p>\n<p>The<strong> colon</strong> is the most common site for acquired/false diverticula <strong>overall</strong>, followed by the duodenum.</p>\n<p><strong>Meckel’s diverticulum</strong> is the most common <strong>true congenital diverticulum</strong> of the small bowel.</p>\n<p>A barium swallow showing a well-defined diverticulum in the second part of the duodenum (Windsock's diverticulum) is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7151","text":"A 38-year-old man presents‌ with dull aching abdomin‍al pain for the past few ‌weeks accompanied by loss​ of weight and appetite. ‍A barium meal follow-thro​ugh image is shown below.‌ What is the most likely ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man presents with dull aching abdominal pain for the past few weeks accompanied by loss of weight and appetite. A barium meal follow-through image is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colon c‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intestina‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ileocecal t‌uberculosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ileocecal tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20ad8d1b7f9248dc93b571eb5fb36983.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fdf7aa812824cd69512d527169c4146x1200x1493.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ileocecal tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the <strong>barium study</strong> showing a shrunken <strong>caecum pulled-up</strong> away from the right iliac fossa is most likely suggestive of<strong> intestinal tuberculosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common site of intestinal tuberculosis is the <strong>distal ileum</strong> and <strong>cecum</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The yellow color arrow marks indicate the <strong>shrunken caecum,</strong> stricturous <strong>narrowing of the terminal ileum</strong> and <strong>ileocaecal</strong> junction, and <strong>proximal ileal dilatation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7147","text":"A respiratory therapist pr‌esents to the OPD with ab‍dominal pain. He also com‌plains of low-grade fever​, night sweats, and loss ‍of weight. You investigat​e him for intestinal tube‌rculosis. Which of the fo‌llowing radiological sign​s are expected to be seen‌ in him? String sign Cree‌ping fat sign Sterlein si‍gn Fleishner sign Apple c​ore sign","raw_text":"<p>A respiratory therapist presents to the OPD with abdominal pain. He also complains of low-grade fever, night sweats, and loss of weight. You investigate him for intestinal tuberculosis. Which of the following radiological signs are expected to be seen in him?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>String sign</li>\n<li>Creeping fat sign</li>\n<li>Sterlein sign</li>\n<li>Fleishner sign</li>\n<li>Apple core sign</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/751b33abff5d44ef846278dd67d474e0x554x325.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p>Creeping fat sign and apple core sign are not the radiological findings seen in intestinal tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>creeping fat sign</strong> refers to the widely separated bowel loops due to fibrofatty proliferation seen in <strong>Crohn's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>apple core</strong> <strong>sign</strong> describes a short segment irregular circumferential stricture of the large bowel that has abrupt shouldered margins resembling an apple core. This appearance is classically seen in a stenosing annular <strong>colon carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7154","text":"Match the following radiol‌ogical signs in intestina‍l tuberculosis with their‌ respective appearance: S​ign Appearance 1. Sterlei‍n sign A. Thickening of t​he valve lips, with narro‌wing of the terminal ileu‌m 2. Gooseneck deformity ​B. Loss of ileocecal angl‌e with dilated terminal i‌leum 3. String sign C. Na‍rrowing of the terminal i​leum with a shortened cec‍um 4. Fleischner sign D. ​A persistent narrow strea‍m of barium","raw_text":"<p>Match the following radiological signs in intestinal tuberculosis with their respective appearance:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 186px;\" width=\"505\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Appearance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">1. Sterlein sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">A. Thickening of the valve lips, with narrowing of the terminal ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">2. Gooseneck deformity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">B. Loss of ileocecal angle with dilated terminal ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">3. String sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">C. Narrowing of the terminal ileum with a shortened cecum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">4. Fleischner sign </td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">D. A persistent narrow stream of barium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - C, 2 - B, 3 - D, 4 - A‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 - C, 2 - D, 3 - A, 4 - B‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 -D, 2 - A,‌ 3 - B, 4 - C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 - B, 2 - A, 3 - D, 4 - C‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 - C, 2 - B, 3 - D, 4 - A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 - C, 2 - B, 3 - D, 4 - A</strong></p><p>The correctly matched option is 1 - C, 2 - B, 3 - D, 4 - A.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 186px;\" width=\"505\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\"><strong>Sign</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\"><strong>Appearance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Sterlein sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">\n<p>Narrowing of the terminal ileum with a shortened cecum</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Gooseneck deformity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Loss of ileocecal angle with dilated terminal ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">String sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">A persistent narrow stream of barium </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Fleischner sign </td>\n<td style=\"width: 244.5px;\">Thickening of the valve lips, with narrowing of the terminal ileum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7143","text":"A young man presented with‌ pain abdomen, diarrhoea,‍ and fever gradually incr‌easing in a step ladder p​attern over the past 2 we‍eks. A Widal test confirm​ed the diagnosis and he w‌as admitted. A few days l‌ater, he developed intest​inal perforation and was ‌taken up for exploratory ‌laparotomy. Which of the ‍following findings would ​you see in this patient?","raw_text":"A young man presented with pain abdomen, diarrhoea, and fever gradually increasing in a step ladder pattern over the past 2 weeks. A Widal test confirmed the diagnosis and he was admitted. A few days later, he developed intestinal perforation and was taken up for exploratory laparotomy. Which of the following findings would you see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Longitudinal intestinal ul‌cers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stricture‌ formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pinpoin‌t ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transverse intestinal ulce‌rs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Longitudinal intestinal ulcers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Longitudinal intestinal ulcers</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Typhoid</strong> causes <strong>longitudinal ulcers</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Typhoid fever is caused by <strong>Salmonella typhi. </strong>It presents with <strong>step-ladder fever</strong> and <strong>abdominal pain</strong> after an incubation period of 10–20 days. Over the next week, the patient can develop:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Distension</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Diarrhoea</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Splenomegaly</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Characteristic <strong>rose spots</strong> on the abdomen caused by a vasculitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Surgical complications of typhoid are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Paralytic ileus</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Intestinal haemorrhage</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Free ileal <strong>perforation</strong> (characteristically occurs during the <strong>third week</strong> of the illness)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cholecystitis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Longitudinal ulcers (usually situated in the distal ileum)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis of typhoid is confirmed by <strong>culture of blood or stool</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Treatment is by antibiotics, usually <strong>chloramphenicol</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Perforation</strong> requires <strong>surgery</strong> to wash out and close the ulcer. Intestinal resection is usually avoided. In unstable patients, with evidence of septic shock, the bowel should be exteriorized and the perforation closed after recovery. Paratyphoid infection also resembles typhoid fever and is treated in a similar manner. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: Transverse ulcers with stricture formation are seen in tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7063","text":"A lady underwent a left he‌micolectomy for colon can‍cer 5 days back. She now ‌complains of abdominal di​scomfort and bloating. Sh‍e is still unable to pass​ gas. On examination, the‌ abdomen is distended and‌ no bowel sounds are hear​d. X-ray shows gas-filled‌ loops of the intestine w‌ith multiple air fluid le‍vels. Which of the follow​ing will be part of the m‍anagement plan of the pat​ient? Initiation of oral ‍intake Nasogastric tube d​ecompression Electrolyte ‌correction CT-scan Emerge​ncy laparotomy","raw_text":"<p>A lady underwent a left hemicolectomy for colon cancer 5 days back.  She now complains of abdominal discomfort and bloating. She is still unable to pass gas. On examination, the abdomen is distended and no bowel sounds are heard. X-ray shows gas-filled loops of the intestine with multiple air fluid levels. Which of the following will be part of the management plan of the patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Initiation of oral intake</li>\n<li>Nasogastric tube decompression</li>\n<li>Electrolyte correction</li>\n<li>CT-scan</li>\n<li>Emergency laparotomy</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 3 and 4</strong></p><p>The given <strong>post-op</strong> scenario after 72 hours of laparotomy with <strong>no passage of flatus</strong>, <strong>absent bowel sounds</strong>, and X-ray findings are suggestive of <strong>paralytic ileus</strong>. Initiation of oral intake and emergency laparotomy is not done in paralytic ileus.</p>\n<p>The return of normal motility post-surgery occurs in the following sequence:</p>\n<p>Small intestine (within the first 24 hours) → Stomach (within 48 hours) → Colon (3 to 5 days).</p>\n<p><strong>Paralytic ileus</strong> is an <strong>adynamic/functional obstruction</strong> in which there is a failure of transmission of peristaltic waves. This is secondary to neuromuscular failure in the myenteric (<strong>Auerbach’s</strong>) and submucous (<strong>Meissner’s</strong>) plexuses. Since there are no rigorous contractions, there is <strong>no colicky pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>The resultant stasis leads to the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal distension - marked and tympanitic</li>\n<li>Effortless vomiting </li>\n<li>Absence of bowel sounds</li>\n<li>Absolute constipation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Paralytic ileus takes on clinical significance if any of the following are observed at <strong>72 hours</strong> after laparotomy after handling of the bowel:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No return of bowel sounds on auscultation</li>\n<li>No passage of flatus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Radiologically, the abdomen shows <strong>gas-filled loops</strong> of the intestine with <strong>multiple air fluid levels</strong> in an erect X-ray. A CT scan is more accurate for differentiating functional from mechanical obstruction</p>\n<p>Paralytic ileus is managed by <strong>nasogastric tube</strong> <strong>decompression</strong> and <strong>restriction of oral intake</strong> until bowel sounds and the passage of flatus return. <strong>Electrolyte balance</strong> must be maintained.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7115","text":"A 22-year-old male present‌s with purulent discharge‍ from the umbilicus. He u‌nderwent laparoscopic app​endicectomy 6 months back‍. X-ray sinogram of the p​atient reveals an enteroc‌utaneous fistula tract as‌ shown below. Which of th​e following aids in the s‌pontaneous closure of the‌ fistula?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old male presents with purulent discharge from the umbilicus. He underwent laparoscopic appendicectomy 6 months back. X-ray sinogram of the patient reveals an enterocutaneous fistula tract as shown below. Which of the following aids in the spontaneous closure of the fistula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelialization of track‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Foreign body in the fistul‌a tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High outp‌ut fistula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fistula track length of 3 ‌cms","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fistula track length of 3 cms","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6d1eac14816a4a3290279f24465d46f5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fistula track length of 3 cms</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the X-ray sinogram showing a <strong>fistula tract</strong> connecting the skin opening and the small intestine are suggestive of an <strong>enterocutaneous fistula. </strong>In this case, it is most likely due to the umbilical port used during appendicectomy. </p>\n<p>Fistula track length<strong> &gt;2.5 cms</strong> is <strong>likely to heal. </strong>Enterocutaneous fistulas<strong> </strong>of length<strong> &lt;2.5 cm </strong>are <strong>unlikely</strong> to heal and close.</p>\n<p>Enterocutaneous fistulas are classified according to their location and volume of daily output. These factors determine the treatment and mortality rates. <strong>Proximal fistulas</strong> are associated with <strong>higher output</strong>, greater fluid and electrolyte loss. <strong>Distal fistulas</strong> tend to have <strong>lower output,</strong> making them easier to manage and more likely to close spontaneously.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 248px;\" width=\"357\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center; height: 30px;\"><strong>Type of fistula</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center; height: 30px;\"><strong>Output/day</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; height: 55px;\">High-output fistula</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">&gt;500 mL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; height: 55px;\">Intermediate fistula</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">200 - 500 mL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; height: 55px;\">Low-output fistula</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center; height: 55px;\">&lt;200 mL</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7116","text":"A 25-year-old man, who und‌erwent exploratory laparo‍tomy for acute abdomen de‌velops a fever on the 5th​ postoperative day. The s‍utured site is erythemato​us and on the removal of ‌two skin sutures, there i‌s a leakage of gastrointe​stinal contents through t‌he wound. An electrolyte ‌panel done shows hyponatr‍emia, hypokalemia, and me​tabolic acidosis. What is‍ the probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man, who underwent exploratory laparotomy for acute abdomen develops a fever on the 5th postoperative day. The sutured site is erythematous and on the removal of two skin sutures, there is a leakage of gastrointestinal contents through the wound.  An electrolyte panel done shows hyponatremia, hypokalemia, and metabolic acidosis. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric‌ fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duodenal‌ fistula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoid‌ fistula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ileal ‌fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duodenal fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duodenal fistula</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of an <strong>enterocutaneous fistula.</strong> From the electrolyte panel showing <strong>hyponatremia, hypokalemia and metabolic acidosis,</strong> a diagnosis of <strong>duodenal fistula</strong> can be made. </p>\n<p>The electrolyte abnormalities with <strong>gastric fistula</strong> are<strong> hypochloremia, hypokalemia, </strong>and<strong> metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Enterocutaneous fistulas are classified according to their location and volume of daily output.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal fistulas </strong>are associated with higher output, greater fluid, and <strong>electrolyte loss</strong>, and greater loss of digestive capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Distal fistulas</strong> tend to have lower output, making them easier to manage and more<strong> likely to</strong> <strong>close spontaneously</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Although both gastric and duodenal fistulas are proximal fistulas, <strong>duodenal fistulas</strong> receive an <strong>additional </strong>1 to 2 litres of <strong>enzymes, electrolytes,</strong> and <strong>water</strong> from the <strong>pancreatic and bile ducts</strong>. Hence duodenal fistulas are at greater risk for more electrolyte imbalance than gastric fistulas.</p>\n<p>The management of enterocutaneous fistula is based on well-established principles known as <strong>SNAP </strong>which includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S - S</strong>epsis elimination and skin protection</li>\n<li><strong>N - </strong>A period of parenteral <strong>n</strong>utrition may well be required</li>\n<li><strong>A -</strong> Anatomical <strong>a</strong>ssessment</li>\n<li><strong>P -</strong> Definitive <strong>p</strong>lanned surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7171","text":"A 26-year-old bride-to-be ‌presented with nausea, vo‍miting, and recurrent epi‌sodes of postprandial epi​gastric pain. She has los‍t nearly 25 kgs in the la​st 6 months to look good ‌in her wedding dress. Pro‌ton pump inhibitors were ​tried but were unsuccessf‌ul. A barium meal follow-‌through of the patient is‍ given below. Which of th​e following is false abou‍t her condition?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old bride-to-be presented with nausea, vomiting, and recurrent episodes of postprandial epigastric pain. She has lost nearly 25 kgs in the last 6 months to look good in her wedding dress. Proton pump inhibitors were tried but were unsuccessful. A barium meal follow-through of the patient is given below. Which of the following is false about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weight gain is part of the‌ management","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lying in prone position po‌stprandially improves sym‍ptoms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical management is by ‌duodenojejunostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The second part of duodenu‌m is commonly involved","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The second part of duodenum is commonly involved","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6dffcf953154c009ca871ed9aa5555f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/daf50ce5d9294b7f94f52570c1808a72x986x1032.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d74aa730b1e4db99f884a652a2a1c99x600x1200.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The second part of duodenum is commonly involved</strong></p><p>The false statement about her condition is that the <strong>second part of the duodenum</strong> is commonly involved.</p>\n<p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>rapid weight loss</strong> and the barium follow-through demonstrating <strong>dilation of the duodenum</strong> with an <strong>abrupt cut-off</strong> at its third part, coinciding with the line of the superior mesenteric artery is suggestive of <strong>superior mesenteric artery (SMA) syndrome.</strong> The <strong>third part of the duodenum</strong> is involved in SMA syndrome. </p>\n<p>SMA syndrome (also known as <strong>Wilkie's syndrome</strong>) is usually seen in<strong> young asthenic women</strong>. It is a rare condition characterized by<strong> compression</strong> of the third portion of the duodenum by the SMA against fixed retroperitoneal structures.</p>\n<p>SMA syndrome is associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapid childhood growth</li>\n<li><strong>Profound weight loss</strong></li>\n<li>Abdominal surgery</li>\n<li>Inflammatory diseases of the abdomen</li>\n<li>Short ligament of Treitz</li>\n<li>Immobilization in a cast that increases lordosis (cast syndrome)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Its clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Episodic vomiting</li>\n<li>Profound nausea</li>\n<li>Abdominal distention</li>\n<li>Postprandial epigastric pain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Upper gastrointestinal <strong>barium swallow</strong> or <strong>hypotonic duodenography </strong>demonstrates <strong>abrupt or near-total cut-off</strong> of the flow of barium from the duodenum to the jejunum. <strong>CT and magnetic resonance (MR) arteriography</strong> can also be used for additional anatomical detail.</p>\n<p>The normal aortomesenteric angle is between 28-65 degrees. This is <strong>reduced to less than 25 degrees </strong>in SMA syndrome.<strong> </strong>A CT scan showing the reduced aortomesenteric angle is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In SMA syndrome, initial conservative management includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frequent small feedings </li>\n<li>Lying in a <strong>prone position</strong> or on the left side postprandially </li>\n<li><strong>Weight gain </strong>(to increase mesenteric fat pad and thereby<strong> increase the aorto-mesenteric angle)</strong></li>\n<li>Removal of a body cast in cast syndrome </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Laparoscopic lysis of the ligament of Treitz</strong> is beneficial in few cases. If all the above treatments fail, <strong>duodenojejunostomy </strong>is done.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7157","text":"A young male, who wishes t‌o pursue his higher studi‍es abroad gets a chest X-‌ray done as a part of the​ immigration clearance. X‍-ray shows air under bila​teral domes of the diaphr‌agm. He is currently asym‌ptomatic with normal vita​l signs. What is the most‌ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young male, who wishes to pursue his higher studies abroad gets a chest X-ray done as a part of the immigration clearance. X-ray shows air under bilateral domes of the diaphragm. He is currently asymptomatic with normal vital signs.  What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumatosis cystoides inte‌stinalis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diverti‌culosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perforated ‌peptic ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Band with Meckel’s diverti‌culum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pneumatosis cystoides intestinalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41727382920c4d20a4d8532d13d90b6cx1280x1337.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pneumatosis cystoides intestinalis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario with an <strong>asymptomatic</strong> patient having <strong>air under the diaphragm </strong>without evidence of peritonitis (<strong>sterile pneumoperitoneum</strong>) is suggestive of <strong>pneumatosis intestinalis.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pneumatosis intestinalis presents as <strong>multiple gas-filled cysts </strong>of the gastrointestinal tract. It is usually located in the <strong>subserosa</strong>, <strong>submucosa</strong>, and rarely the muscularis layer.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The common sites are <strong>jejunum </strong>&gt; ileocecal region &gt; colon. It represents one of the few cases of sterile pneumoperitoneum.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Pneumatosis in <strong>neonates </strong>is usually associated with <strong>necrotizing enterocolitis</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Predisposing factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</li>\n<li>Immunocompromised state </li>\n<li>Inflammatory, obstructive, or infectious conditions of the intestine</li>\n<li>Endoscopy and jejunostomy</li>\n<li>Ischemia</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients can be <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Symptoms if present, is usually <strong>nonspecific</strong> like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Abdominal distention</li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Mucus in stool</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis can be made on the basis of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plain X-ray </strong>showing<strong> r</strong><strong>adiolucent areas </strong>within the <strong>bowel wall </strong>(appear as <strong>grape-like clusters </strong>or <strong>tiny bubbles</strong>)</li>\n<li>Barium contrast</li>\n<li>CT studies</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Management of the uncomplicated primary disease.</li>\n<li>When symptoms demand treatment, the <strong>first line </strong>is <strong>intermittent high flow oxygen therapy</strong>, providing a concentration of 70% continuously for 5 days by nasal specula.</li>\n<li>The cysts may also resolve with antibiotics, particularly <strong>metronidazole</strong>.</li>\n<li>In <strong>resistant cases</strong>, maintenance treatment with <strong>sulfasalazine </strong>may be helpful. The prognosis<strong> </strong>in most patients is that of the underlying disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6844","text":"A 55-year-old man with IHD‌ presents with sudden, se‍vere, generalized abdomin‌al pain and multiple epis​odes of vomiting. On exam‍ination, the pulse rate i​s 118bpm, rhythm is irreg‌ularly irregular, BP is 9‌0/60 mmHg. The abdomen ap​pears soft, non-distended‌, and bowel sounds are ab‌sent. Which of the follow‍ing could be a likely dia​gnosis in this case?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man with IHD presents with sudden, severe, generalized abdominal pain and multiple episodes of vomiting. On examination, the pulse rate is 118bpm, rhythm is irregularly irregular, BP is 90/60 mmHg. The abdomen appears soft, non-distended, and bowel sounds are absent. Which of the following could be a likely diagnosis in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute severe‌ pancreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ruptured aor‌tic aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute mesent‌eric ischemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peptic ulcer‌ perforation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute mesenteric ischemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute mesenteric ischemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with sudden onset severe abdominal pain which is out-of-proportion to the physical examination findings along with atrial fibrillation is suggestive of <strong>acute mesenteric ischemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Mesenteric ischemia may be acute or chronic. The <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> (SMA) is most likely affected. An <strong>embolus</strong> is the most common cause of SMA occlusion. Possible sources for SMA emboisation include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left atrium in <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Previous history of ischemic heart disease/myocardial infarction</strong></li>\n<li>Vegetations on mitral and aortic valves associated with endocarditis </li>\n<li>An atheromatous plaque from an aortic aneurysm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other causes of mesenteric ischemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atherosclerosis</strong> of mesenteric vessels</li>\n<li>Vasculitides</li>\n<li><strong>Venous thrombosis</strong> in factor V Leiden mutation</li>\n<li>Non-occlusive mesenteric ischemia due to alterations in splanchnic blood flow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The most important clue to an early diagnosis of acute mesenteric ischemia is the <strong>sudden </strong>onset of <strong>severe abdominal pain</strong> in a patient with atrial fibrillation or <strong>atherosclerosis</strong>. The pain is typically in the <strong>central abdomen</strong> and out of proportion to the physical findings.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Associated clinical features include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal distension, peritonitis, and the passage of bloody stools.</p>\n<p>The <strong>gold standard</strong> for the diagnosis of acute mesenteric ischemia is <strong>angiography</strong>, and management is <strong>laparotomy</strong>. The goal of operative exploration is to <strong>resect the compromised bowel</strong> and restore blood supply via <strong>embolectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B: A <strong>pulsatile abdominal mass</strong> is seen in the case of an <strong>abdominal aortic aneurysm</strong>. Acute pain and hypotension occur with rupture of the aneurysm, which requires an emergency operation. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Options A and D: Acute severe <strong>pancreatitis</strong> and <strong>peptic ulcer perforation</strong> have no association with atrial fibrillation. They usually present with pain over the upper abdomen associated with <strong>guarding</strong> and <strong>rigidity</strong> on examination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7164","text":"What is the minimum dose o‌f radiation that can caus‍e radiation enteritis of ‌the small intestine?","raw_text":"What is the minimum dose of radiation that can cause radiation enteritis of the small intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3000‌ cGy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4500‌ cGy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6000‌ cGy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"7500‌ cGy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4500 cGy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4500 cGy</strong></p><p><strong> </strong>The minimum radiation dose in the<strong> small intestine</strong> that can cause <strong>radiation enteritis</strong> is<strong> 4500cGy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Radiation enteritis</strong> is a common complication of radiation treatment aimed at the abdomen and pelvis. It causes diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and stomach cramps.</p>\n<p>The amount of radiation appears to correlate with the probability of the development of radiation enteritis. In general, serious late complications are unusual if the total radiation dosage is less than 4000 cGy. <strong>Morbidity risk</strong> increases with dosages exceeding <strong>5000 cGy. </strong></p>\n<p>The table below shows various parts of GIT and the radiation doses that can cause enteritis.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Part of GIT</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Radiation dose (in cGy)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esophagus</td>\n<td>6000 to 7500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stomach</td>\n<td>4500 to 5000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small intestine &amp; colon</td>\n<td>4500 to 6500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rectum</td>\n<td>5500 to 8000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7161","text":"A patient who is a known c‌ase of Crohn's disease un‍derwent ileal resection f‌or a stricture. Which of ​the following is not a pa‍rt of the management of p​ostoperative complication‌s in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient who is a known case of Crohn's disease underwent ileal resection for a stricture. Which of the following is not a part of the management of postoperative complications in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Folic acid a‌dministration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serial transverse enteropl‌asty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral chol‌estyramine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Octre‌otide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Folic acid administration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Folic acid administration</strong></p><p>Administration of <strong>folic acid</strong> is done<strong> </strong>in <strong>jejunal resection</strong>. <strong>Ileal resection</strong> causes vitamin B12 deficiency which requires administration of <strong>parenteral vitamin B12. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Extensive resection of the small intestine (<strong>&lt;150 cm</strong> of <strong>remaining</strong> <strong>small intestine</strong>) can result in metabolic and nutritional consequences. This is  known as <strong>short bowel syndrome. </strong>The presentation of the patient is dependent upon the site and extent of intestinal resection.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>ileum</strong> is the <strong>only site</strong> of absorption of <strong>vitamin B12 and bile salts.</strong> Following resection of the ileum, the loss of bile salts increases resulting in <strong>fat malabsorption </strong>and<strong> </strong><strong>diarrhea</strong>. Other complications of resection include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Peptic ulcers</strong> - Due to gastric hypersecretion</li>\n<li><strong>Cholelithiasis</strong> - Due to interruption of the enterohepatic cycle of bile salts</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1199\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Initial treatment priorities include repletion of fluid and electrolytes lost in severe diarrhoea by initiating <strong>TPN. </strong>Long-term management involves:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 304\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of <strong>oral cholestyramine</strong> to bind with bile salts</li>\n<li>Dietary fat restriction</li>\n<li>Parenteral <strong>vitamin B12</strong></li>\n<li>Proton pump inhibitors to lower gastric pH</li>\n<li><strong>Octreotide</strong> to reduce the volume of GI secretions</li>\n<li>Antimotility agents such as loperamide hydrochloride to delay small-intestinal transit</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Small intestine <strong>lengthening procedures</strong> done to increase absorption and transit time are:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1200\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Longitudinal intestinal lengthening and tailoring procedure (LILT)</li>\n<li>Serial transverse <strong>enteroplasty</strong> procedure (STEP)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1200\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Intestinal transplantation</strong> is indicated only in complicated cases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6686","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the Tillaux si‍gn?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the Tillaux sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mass is felt in the perium‌bilical region","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mass is brilliantly transi‌lluminant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mass is mobile perpendicul‌ar to mesentery attachmen‍t line","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fluctuation‌ is positive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mass is mobile perpendicular to mesentery attachment line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c905c8c84974e78b40b7defd0179d03x1280x1930.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mass is mobile perpendicular to mesentery attachment line</strong></p><p><strong>Tillaux's sign</strong> is said to be positive if the <strong>mass/swelling is mobile perpendicular to the mesentery attachment line</strong>. Tillaux’s sign<strong> </strong>is seen in a <strong>mesenteric cyst.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1083\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Mesenteric cysts</strong> occur either in the small intestine (60%) or the colon (40%) and can be classified as the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chylolymphatic (most common type)</li>\n<li>Enterogenous</li>\n<li>Urogenital remnant</li>\n<li>Dermoid</li>\n<li>Other causes: traumatic hematoma and cyst formation, tuberculous cold abscess of mesentery and hydatid cyst of the mesentery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They most commonly occur in adults with a mean age of 45 years. It is more common in females than males (2:1). The most common presentation is a <strong>painless abdominal swelling near the umbilicus,</strong> which is smooth and not moving with respiration with <strong>Tillaux's triad</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soft, smooth swelling in the umbilical region</li>\n<li>Freely mobile in a direction perpendicular to the mesentery (Tillaux's sign)</li>\n<li>Zone of resonance around the cyst </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other presentations include recurrent attacks of abdominal pain due to temporary impaction of a food bolus in a segment of the bowel narrowed by the cyst and vomiting.</p>\n<p>Complications of mesenteric cyst include torsion, rupture, infection and hemorrhage into the cyst. </p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasonography</strong> and <strong>CT</strong> are used to demonstrate mesenteric cysts.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chlyolymphatic cyst is managed by <strong>enucleation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Enterogenous cyst is managed by removal of the cyst with resection of the adjacent bowel as it arises as a diverticulum or duplication from the adjacent bowel. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In cases where complete excision is not possible due to the close proximity to vital structures, <strong>partial excision</strong> or <strong>marsupialization</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1084\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7068","text":"What is the investigation ‌of choice in a patient su‍spected to have a small b‌owel tumor?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice in a patient suspected to have a small bowel tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entero‌clysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CT ‌scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barium meal follow-through‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enteroclysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enteroclysis</strong></p><p><strong>Enteroclysis</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> in patients with <strong>small bowel tumours.</strong></p>\n<p>Enteroclysis is a <strong>barium study</strong> in which the <strong>contrast </strong>agent is put <strong>directly into the duodenum</strong> through a <strong>nasogastric tube.</strong></p>\n<p>Continued infusion of 500 to 1000 mL of thin barium sulfate and methylcellulose suspension is done and then viewed continuously with the use of either fluoroscopy or radiographs taken at frequent intervals. This technique is a <strong>double-contrast</strong> procedure that allows detailed imaging of the entire small intestine.</p>\n<p>Enteroclysis has a<strong> sensitivity </strong>of over <strong>90%</strong> in the detection of small bowel tumours and is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong>, particularly for<strong> tumours </strong>located in the <strong>distal small bowel.</strong></p>\n<p>Advantages of enteroclysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The contrast is administered at a controlled rate directly in the small bowel, <strong>bypassing the regulatory action</strong> of the <strong>pylorus</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>entire small bowel </strong>is demonstrated in a <strong>distended</strong> state, allowing <strong>better diagnosis</strong>. As a result of the dilatation, the following can be visualised:\n<ul>\n<li>Mild strictures</li>\n<li>Small sinus tracts and fistulas</li>\n<li>Minimal extrinsic compressions</li>\n<li>Skip lesions</li>\n<li>Proximal and distal extent of diffuse diseases.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The examination can be completed in <strong>15–30 minutes</strong>, thus reducing patient waiting time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7172","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‌ with complaints of breat‍hlessness, dull aching ab‌dominal pain, and diarrhe​a. On examination, his fa‍ce and chest appear flush​ed. CT scan of the abdome‌n shows a tumor in the te‌rminal ileum with liver m​etastasis. An increased l‌evels of 5-HIAA is seen i‌n a 24-hour urinary sampl‍e. Which of the following​ is false regarding the m‍anagement of this patient​?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with complaints of breathlessness, dull aching abdominal pain, and diarrhea. On examination, his face and chest appear flushed. CT scan of the abdomen shows a tumor in the terminal ileum with liver metastasis. An increased levels of 5-HIAA is seen in a 24-hour urinary sample. Which of the following is false regarding the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgery should be done as ‌soon as the diagnosis is ‍made.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MRI is the best method for‌ staging of the disease.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Somatostatin analogues pro‌vide symptomatic relief.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liver transplantation can ‌be performed.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MRI is the best method for staging of the disease.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MRI is the best method for staging of the disease.</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1270\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong> caused by a <strong>carcinoid tumour.</strong> The <strong>best method </strong>for the <strong>staging</strong> of a carcinoid tumour is the <strong>octreotide scan.</strong></p>\n<p>They are <strong>neuroendocrine tumours </strong>of the gastrointestinal tract. They arise from the <strong>enterochromaffin cells.</strong> They metastasize early to the <strong>regional</strong> <strong>lymph nodes</strong> and the <strong>liver.</strong></p>\n<p>Abdominal symptoms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Abdominal distension</li>\n<li>Lower GI bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Metastasis in these patients can cause <strong>increased production of serotonin</strong> leading to carcinoid syndrome with features like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flushing</li>\n<li>Diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Bronchospasm</li>\n<li>Cardiac symptoms because of stenosis and insufficiency of the cardiac valves</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is made by an assessment of 5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (<strong>5-HIAA</strong>)<strong> </strong>in a<strong> 24-hour urine sample</strong>. It is positive in larger tumours only if metastases are present.  </p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasonography, CT </strong>scan, and<strong> MRI</strong> may show the primary tumour and metastases. The best method for<strong> staging</strong> carcinoid tumours is by <strong>octreotide scan </strong>or<strong> somatostatin receptor scintigraphy </strong>(SRS).</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 876\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Somatostatin </strong>and its analogues are used in the <strong>symptomatic treatment</strong> of carcinoid syndrome. </li>\n<li><strong>Surgery</strong> should be undertaken <strong>as soon as the diagnosis is made</strong>, even in the presence of liver metastases.</li>\n<li><strong>Resection of the primary tumour</strong> and mesenteric lymph node metastases is done. </li>\n<li>In the presence of <strong>liver metastasis -</strong> liver resection, transarterial embolization (TAE), chemoembolization (TACE), chemotherapy, and <strong>liver</strong> <strong>transplantation</strong> can be done.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7169","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement about a‍bdominal lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement about abdominal lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MALT lymphoma is associate‌d with H.pylori infection‍.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary small-intestinal l‌ymphomas are most commonl‍y located in the jejunum.‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphoma is the most commo‌n malignant neoplasm of s‍pleen.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stomach is the most common‌ site for extranodal lymp‍homa.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary small-intestinal lymphomas are most commonly located in the jejunum.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary small-intestinal lymphomas are most commonly located in the jejunum.</strong></p><p>Primary small-intestinal lymphomas are most commonly located in the ileum.</p>\n<p>Option A:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 500\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Primary gastric lymphomas are derived from B-cells and classified as diffuse large B-cell lymphomas (DLBCL) and <strong>mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT)</strong>. Both DLBCL and MALT are thought to be <strong>associated with chronic <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection</strong>. This linkage is better established for MALT, whereas the association between <em>H. pylori</em> infection and DLBCL is more controversial.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 499\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option C:</p>\n<p>Lymphoma is the most common malignant neoplasm of the spleen.</p>\n<p>Option D:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 499\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Gastric lymphoma accounts for the majority of extranodal lymphomas, representing approximately 50–75% of primary gastrointestinal lymphomas.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7167","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g lymphoma of the small i‌ntestine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding lymphoma of the small intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B-cell lymphomas are chemo‌-resistant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is the most common tumo‌ur of the small intestine‍ in children","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resection is indicated in ‌all symptomatic patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burkitt’s lymphoma affects‌ the ileocaecal junction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. B-cell lymphomas are chemo-resistant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. B-cell lymphomas are chemo-resistant</strong></p><p><strong>B-cell lymphomas</strong> are <strong>chemo-sensitive</strong>. T-cell lymphomas are chemo-resistant.</p>\n<p>Approximately one-third of <strong>GI lymphomas</strong> occur in the small bowel. In the small intestine, most commonly they are found in the<strong> ileum. </strong>In <strong>children</strong> &lt;10 years, lymphomas are the <strong>most common intestinal neoplasm</strong>. In adults, they constitute up to 25% of small bowel malignant tumours.</p>\n<p>Patients with <strong>celiac disease</strong> and <strong>immunodeficiency</strong> are at increased risk for the development of primary small intestine lymphomas.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Change in bowel habits</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Small intestine lymphomas are usually <strong>larger than 5 cm. </strong>They may extend into the mucosa. Microscopic examination shows diffuse infiltration of the intestinal wall.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>absence of symptoms,</strong> they are often<strong> chemo-responsive </strong>and do not require surgery. In <strong>symptomatic</strong> patients, <strong>resection</strong> is indicated. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B-cell lymphomas</strong> are more <strong>chemosensitive</strong> and have high remission rates, with or without surgery. </li>\n<li><strong>T-cell lymphomas </strong>are more <strong>resistant to therapy</strong> and will progress to symptoms of obstruction or perforation if not resected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Small intestine lymphomas cause complications like <strong>haemorrhage</strong> and <strong>perforation</strong>. The best prognostic indicator is a response to systemic therapy. The five-year survival rate is 50% to 60%</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> stomach</strong> (50–75%) is the <strong>most common site </strong>of lymphomas in the <strong>gastrointestinal system</strong>.</li>\n<li>Burkitt’s lymphoma can aggressively affect the ileocaecal region.</li>\n<li>Lymphoma is the most common malignant neoplasm of the spleen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"PbKMBx0X","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"22_peripheral_nerve_blocks","title":"22.Peripheral Nerve Blocks","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6041","text":"The anaesthesia resident h‌as made the markings as s‌een in the image on a pat​ient's neck. Which brachi‌al plexus block is he lik‌ely planning to perform?","raw_text":"The anaesthesia resident has made the markings as seen in the image on a patient's neck. Which brachial plexus block is he likely planning to perform?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraclavi‌cular block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stellate ga‌nglion block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interscal‌ene block","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infraclavi‌cular block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Interscalene block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f659a1a5b944a40be8a16bfaed2f3e3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97cb54b96486447bbbf6fa4e0e5c983ex1280x1407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Interscalene block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The above image shows the surface marking used to perform<strong> interscalene block.</strong> The local anaesthetic is injected into the <strong>interscalene groove</strong> present behind the sternocleidomastoid at the <strong>level of cricoid cartilage</strong> (C6 vertebral level).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is a brachial plexus block performed at the <strong>trunk</strong> <strong>level</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 222px; width: 373px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Level</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Type of block</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 97px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246px; height: 97px;\">Superior &amp; middle trunk (inferior trunk blockade is incomplete)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 97px;\">Interscalene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246px; height: 56px;\">Distal trunk &amp; proximal division</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 56px;\">Supraclavicular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246px; height: 35px;\">Cords</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 35px;\">Infraclavicular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 246px; height: 36px;\">Terminal nerves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 36px;\">Axillary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It provides an adequate block of the superior and middle trunks of the brachial plexus. So, interscalene block provides <strong>surgical anesthesia</strong> for procedures of the <strong>shoulder and upper arm</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ulnar nerve (C8, T1) </strong>from inferior trunk<strong> </strong>may be<strong> missed.</strong> Hence, this block is not used for procedures of the forearm and hand.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6047","text":"You are about to administe‌r interscalene block to a‌ patient. What is the mos​t common complication you‌ should anticipate?","raw_text":"You are about to administer interscalene block to a patient.  What is the most common complication you should anticipate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaphragmati‌c hemiparesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve ‌palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Horners ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diaphragmatic hemiparesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diaphragmatic hemiparesis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common complication of interscalene block, seen in 100% of patients, is <strong>diaphragmatic hemiparesis </strong>due to<strong> ipsilateral phrenic nerve blockade</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can result in dyspnea, hypoxia, and hypercapnea, especially if the patient has compromised lung function or preexisting contralateral phrenic nerve paralysis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other complications of interscalene block are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Horner's syndrome – ptosis, miosis, anhydrosis. Due to involvement of the sympathetic nerves to the cervicothoracic ganglion.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vagus nerve involvement</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pneumothorax</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bezold Jarisch reflex</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accidental vertebral artery injection (immediate seizure is seen)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accidental spinal or epidural injection</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6053","text":"A patient presented with d‌islocation of the shoulde‌r. It was not amenable to​ closed reduction, so you‌ plan to perform an open ‌reduction. Which regional​ anaesthetic technique wi‌ll you prefer to perform ‌this procedure under?","raw_text":"A patient presented with dislocation of the shoulder. It was not amenable to closed reduction, so you plan to perform an open reduction. Which regional anaesthetic technique will you prefer to perform this procedure under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interscal‌ene block","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Supraclavi‌cular block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Interscalene block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Interscalene block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The regional anesthetic technique preferred for surgery of dislocated shoulder is<strong> interscalene brachial plexus block.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 214px; width: 554px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Brachial plexus block</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\"><strong>Level</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\"><strong>Indication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Limitation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Interscalene</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Superior and middle trunk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Shoulder and  upper arm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Ulnar nerve sparing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Supraclavicular</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Distal trunk and proximal division</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Elbow, forearm and hand</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">\n<p>Axillary and suprascapular nerve sparing, pneumothorax, phrenic nerve palsy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Infraclavicular</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Cords</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Forearm and hand</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Intercostobrachial nerve sparing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Axillary</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Terminal nerves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Forearm and hand</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">\n<p>Musculocutaneous, axillary, medial cutaneous nerve branches sparing;</p>\n<p>Inadvertent intravascular injection</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6054","text":"The anaesthesia resident i‌s told to perform the 'sp‌inal of the arm' before a​n elbow surgery. You obse‌rve him as he injects a l‌ocal anaesthetic at the p​oint shown in the image. ‌What procedure is he perf‌orming?","raw_text":"The anaesthesia resident is told to perform the 'spinal of the arm' before an elbow surgery. You observe him as he injects a local anaesthetic at the point shown in the image. What procedure is he performing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stellate ga‌nglion block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraclavi‌cular block","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infraclavi‌cular block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interscal‌ene block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Supraclavicular block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac335539e29a4b8389c9f1f7b3426c26.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46d9ef9212004f28849903d4936af928x1280x1409.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Supraclavicular block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The procedure being performed is the <strong>supraclavicular block</strong>. It can be identified by the point of injection <strong>2 cm</strong> <strong>posterior </strong>to the<strong> mid-point of clavicle.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The block is given at the level <strong>distal to trunks </strong>and<strong> proximal to divisions </strong>of the brachial plexus.  It is also known as the '<strong>spinal of the arm</strong>'.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a type of brachial plexus block used for procedures on the <strong>elbow, forearm, and hand</strong>. The supraclavicular block does not adequately block the axillary and suprascapular nerves, and thus is <strong>not reliable</strong> for <strong>shoulder surgery</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6055","text":"What is the most common co‌mplication after supracla‌vicular block?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication after supraclavicular block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phrenic n‌erve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Horners ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve ‌palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phrenic nerve palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phrenic nerve palsy</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common complication of supraclavicular block is<strong> phrenic nerve palsy</strong> with an incidence &gt; 50 %.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This results in hemidiaphragmatic paresis which is self-limited and resolves as the block wears off.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Complications &amp; side-effects of supraclavicular block : </strong></p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Hemidiaphragmatic paresis - due to phrenic nerve palsy </li>\n<li>Pneumothorax  - highest when compared to other brachial plexus blocks</li>\n<li>Horners syndrome - from a block of the stellate ganglion</li>\n<li>Hoarseness - due to recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy <span class=\"nowrap\">and/or</span> cervical sympathetic block</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2018\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Note :</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> is the <strong>most dangerous</strong> complication of the supraclavicular block but it is not the most common. It has an incidence that ranges from 0.5- 6%. The symptoms develop slowly, and may take up to 24 hours.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6057","text":"Which procedure has the ma‌ximum incidence of pneumo‌thorax?","raw_text":"Which procedure has the maximum incidence of pneumothorax?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraclavi‌cular block","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infraclavi‌cular block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interscal‌ene block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillar‌y block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supraclavicular block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supraclavicular block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The incidence of pneumothorax is maximum with <strong>supraclavicular block</strong> (0.5- 6%) among all the approaches to brachial plexus block. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The risk can be minimised by performing ultrasound guided block.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6058","text":"In which of the following ‌patients will the adminis‌tration of a supraclavicu​lar block not be of much ‌use?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients will the administration of a supraclavicular block not be of much use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30-year-old man with dista‌l radius fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16-year-old boy with elbow‌ dislocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40-year-old man with fract‌ure of 1st metacarpal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"25-year-old woman with pos‌terior shoulder dislocati‌on","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 25-year-old woman with posterior shoulder dislocation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 25-year-old woman with posterior shoulder dislocation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supraclavicular block is <strong>not reliable for shoulder surgeries</strong> as axillary and suprascapular nerves are spared. So it will not be of much use in a case of posterior shoulder dislocation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supraclavicular block is a type of brachial plexus block used for procedures on the elbow, forearm, and hand. It provides an adequate block of the brachial plexus at the level of the distal trunk - proximal division.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6060","text":"Which of the following blo‌cks is performed by injec‌ting a local anaesthetic ​at the point marked in th‌e image?","raw_text":"Which of the following blocks is performed by injecting a local anaesthetic at the point marked in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stellate ga‌nglion block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraclavi‌cular block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infraclavi‌cular block","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Interscal‌ene block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Infraclavicular block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5529a933a54433eb29894fac2e54053.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d2b995a47c341cda6c41df483d0fbb2x1280x1409.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infraclavicular block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The image shows the surface marking for performing <strong>infraclavicular block</strong>. This block is performed at a point <strong>2 cm medial </strong>and<strong> 2 cm caudal</strong> to the <strong>coracoid process</strong> which is used as a landmark.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The brachial plexus is blocked at the <strong>level of cords.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The<strong> Infraclavicular block</strong> is used for procedures of the<strong> forearm </strong>and<strong> hand.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6063","text":"A brachial plexus block is‌ given by injecting a loc‌al anesthetic at the poin​t shown in the image. Whi‌ch of the following proce‌dures cannot be performed​ under this block?","raw_text":"A brachial plexus block is given by injecting a local anesthetic at the point shown in the image. Which of the following procedures cannot be performed under this block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plating of d‌istal radius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal fixation of scaph‌oid fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal fixation of shaft‌ humerus fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Internal fixation of ulnar‌ fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Internal fixation of shaft humerus fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f6f4dc21ba904275a0f57be651b4ba7f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/240b16d371a94d70901c3703bce6e3f4x1280x1666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Internal fixation of shaft humerus fracture</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The image shows a local anesthetic being injected into the tip of the axilla around the axillary artery. This is the method used to perform the <strong>axillary block. </strong>It reliably produces <strong>anesthesia</strong> of the entire arm <strong>distal to the elbow</strong>, and so can be used for all of the given procedures, except fixation of the shaft of the humerus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>terminal branches</strong> of the brachial plexus can be <strong>blocked</strong> via the axillary approach.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6064","text":"A child presents with a di‌slocated fracture of the ‌first metacarpal. What is​ the preferred regional a‌naesthesia technique for ‌the surgery?","raw_text":"A child presents with a dislocated fracture of the first metacarpal. What is the preferred regional anaesthesia technique for the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraclavi‌cular block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infraclavi‌cular block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interscal‌ene block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillar‌y block","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Axillary block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Axillary block</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>axillary block</strong> is the <strong>most preferred approach</strong> to the brachial plexus in <strong>pediatric patients</strong> for surgeries of the elbow, forearm, and hand.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a relatively easy and safe procedure. <strong>Complications</strong> include <strong>systemic toxicity</strong> due to accidental intraarterial injection and <strong>permanent nerve injury</strong> from the intraneural injection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6066","text":"A 4-year-old boy is posted‌ for a circumcision proce‌dure. He is given a puden​dal nerve block before th‌e surgery. Which of the f‌ollowing nerve roots are ​blocked in this technique‌?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy is posted for a circumcision procedure. He is given a pudendal nerve block before the surgery. Which of the following nerve roots are blocked in this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L1L‌2L3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L2L‌3L4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S1S‌2S3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"S2S‌3S4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. S2S3S4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. S2S3S4</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pudendal nerve block involves <strong>S<sub>2</sub>S<sub>3</sub>S<sub>4</sub></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6067","text":"You have administered a ce‌liac plexus block to a pa‌tient of pancreatic cance​r for pain relief. What i‌s the most common complic‌ation of this block that ​you should warn him about‌?","raw_text":"You have administered a celiac plexus block to a patient of pancreatic cancer for pain relief. What is the most common complication of this block that you should warn him about?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‌thorax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postural h‌ypotension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retroperitoneal hemorrhage‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intra-arteri‌al injection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Postural hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Postural hypotension</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most common complication of celiac plexus block is <strong>postural hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Celiac plexus:</strong></p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Largest plexus of the sympathetic nervous system.</li>\n<li>Contains preganglionic sympathetic from<strong> greater, lesser </strong>and<strong> least splanchnic nerves</strong> (<strong>T5 to T12</strong>), postganglionic sympathetic, preganglionic parasympathetic and visceral sensory afferent fibres. </li>\n<li>Provides sensory innervation and sympathetic outflow to stomach, liver, spleen, pancreas, kidney and GI tract up to splenic flexure.</li>\n<li>Located in the retroperitoneal space, anterior to aorta at the level of<strong> L1.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Indication: </strong>Pain arising from the abdominal viscera, particularly intra-abdominal cancers (Eg. pancreatic cancer).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Complications: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> (most common, can be minimized with fluid hydration)  </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Diarrhea</strong> (secondary to blockade of sympathetic fibers)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Paraplegia (from damage to artery of Adamkiewicz)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Local anesthetic toxicity, spinal or epidural injection, retroperitoeneal hemorrhage, visceral organ injury, and pneumothorax.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kKn_bLLK","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"22_basal_ganglia_and_limbic_system","title":"22.Basal Ganglia And Limbic System","num_questions":6,"total_marks":24,"duration":16,"time_per_question":160.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2748","text":"A 13-year-old boy presents‌ with involuntary dancing‍ movements of his right h‍and. He was diagnosed wit​h rheumatic fever. What c​onstitutes the ganglia in​volved in the presenting ‍complication?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy presents with involuntary dancing movements of his right hand. He was diagnosed with rheumatic fever. What constitutes the ganglia involved in the presenting complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caudate nucleus and globus‌ pallidus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Globus pallidus and putame‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Globus pallidus and lentif‌orm nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caudate nucleus and putame‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Caudate nucleus and putamen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23dc0834b7564f5f970d3828819e9531x510x451.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04c63b7882844664984bfaa8033bc156x600x525.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce5f49d8bf084a3cbb7838da93547dc9x1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Caudate nucleus and putamen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Sydenham's chorea,</strong> a complication of<strong> rheumatic fever</strong>. The choreic movements are caused by the <strong>destruction of D2 neurons </strong>of the striatum. The <strong>n</strong><strong>eostriatum</strong> consists of the <strong>caudate nucleus </strong>and<strong> putamen</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Sydenham's chorea in a patient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Nomenclature of basal ganglia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corpus striatum</strong>: Caudate nucleus + putamen+ globus pallidus</li>\n<li><strong>Lentiform nucleus</strong>: Putamen + globus pallidus</li>\n<li><strong>Neostriatum</strong>: Caudate nucleus + putamen</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2749","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the lenti‍form complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the lentiform complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The internal capsule separ‌ates the lentiform comple‍x from caudate nucleus.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The external capsule separ‌ates the claustrum from p‍utamen.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Putamen and globus pallidu‌s are separated by the in‍ternal medullary lamina.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Putamen and globus pallidu‌s are separated by the ex‍ternal medullary lamina.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Putamen and globus pallidus are separated by the internal medullary lamina.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca3d99c1bb024f148069447dbce2feb0x600x525.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9bdae9275c1426e88e684c5e8937482x1280x1114.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Putamen and globus pallidus are separated by the internal medullary lamina.</strong></p><p>Putamen and globus pallidus are separated by the<strong> external</strong> <strong>medullary lamina</strong>.</p>\n<p>From <strong>deep to superficial</strong>, the order of structures of <strong>basal ganglia</strong> in horizontal section is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Caudate nucleus (innermost)</li>\n<li>Internal capsule</li>\n<li>Globus pallidus internal (GPi)</li>\n<li>Internal medullary lamina</li>\n<li>Globus pallidus external (GPe)</li>\n<li>External medullary lamina</li>\n<li>Putamen</li>\n<li>External capsule</li>\n<li>Claustrum</li>\n<li>Extreme capsule</li>\n<li>Insular cortex (outermost)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2750","text":"Wilson's pencils are relat‌ed to?","raw_text":"Wilson's pencils are related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stri‌atum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amyg‌dala","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hippo‌campus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dentat‌e gyrus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Striatum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8244b6486bca45a38857e7dbfd554fd7x1280x1021.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Striatum</strong></p><div><strong>Wilson's pencils</strong> are thin myelinated or unmyelinated axons, which are afferents and efferents passing through the <strong>striatum</strong>.</div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>The image given below shows micrograph of the putamen (top-right) and external globus pallidus (bottom-left), showing Wilson's pencils (blue, diagonal, linear structures).</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2778","text":"A 64-year-old man presente‌d with a reduced range of‍ motion in his limbs. On ‍examination, cogwheel rig​idity and resting tremors​ were noticed. What is th​e direct pathway involved‍ in this disorder?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man presented with a reduced range of motion in his limbs. On examination, cogwheel rigidity and resting tremors were noticed. What is the direct pathway involved in this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cortex &amp;gt; Striatum &amp;gt; ‌Globus pallidus &amp;gt; Subt‍halamic nucleus &amp;gt; Prem‍otor cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cortex &amp;gt; Striatum &amp;gt; ‌Globus pallidus &amp;gt; Thal‍amus &amp;gt; Premotor cortex‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cortex &amp;gt; Globus pallidu‌s &amp;gt; Subthalamic nucleu‍s &amp;gt; Thalamus &amp;gt; Prem‍otor cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cortex &amp;gt; Globus pallidu‌s &amp;gt; Striatum &amp;gt; Thal‍amus &amp;gt; Premotor cortex‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cortex > Striatum > Globus pallidus > Thalamus > Premotor cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cortex &gt; Striatum &gt; Globus pallidus &gt; Thalamus &gt; Premotor cortex</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Parkinson's disease, </strong>a disorder involving the destruction of the <strong>substantia nigra</strong> in the basal ganglia. The most accurate representation of the direct pathway in the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> is <strong>cortex &gt; striatum &gt; globus pallidus&gt; thalamus &gt; premotor cortex. </strong></p>\n<p>Parkinson's disease is a chronic progressive neurodegenerative disease that is clinically manifested by a triad of cardinal motor symptoms-<strong> rigidity, bradykinesia and tremor.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>indirect pathway</strong> is represented as follows:</p>\n<p>Cortex &gt; Striatum &gt; Globus pallidus externa &gt; Subthalamic nucleus &gt; Globus pallidus interna and Substantia nigra reticulata &gt;Thalamus &gt; Premotor cortex</p>\n<p>The <strong>direct pathway</strong> stimulates the cortex to <strong>produce movement</strong> and the <strong>indirect pathway</strong> <strong>inhibits movement</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0528","text":"A man presented with sudde‌n violent flinging moveme‍nts confined to the proxi‍mal parts of the right li​mb. This condition is cau​sed due to the lesion inv​olving which structure?","raw_text":"A man presented with sudden violent flinging movements confined to the proximal parts  of the right limb. This condition is caused due to the lesion involving which structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stri‌atum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subthalam‌ic nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Globus ‌pallidus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Substant‌ia nigra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subthalamic nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subthalamic nucleus</strong></p><p>A<strong> </strong>lesion in the<strong> subthalamic nucleus </strong>causes sudden, violent, involuntary movements confined to<strong> one side of the body,</strong> commonly affecting the proximal extremity musculature, a condition called <strong>hemiballismus.</strong> The movements are seen <strong>contralateral to the side of the lesion</strong>, i.e., involuntary movements of the right arm and leg are seen in the lesion of the left subthalamic nucleus and vice versa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2782","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of the limbic ‍system?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the limbic system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cingula‌te gyrus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amyg‌dala","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hippocampa‌l formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial thal‌amic nuclei","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial thalamic nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9918e3e22afa4c1cbe7ce0b054c9a348x1280x940.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial thalamic nuclei</strong></p><p><strong>Medial thalamic nuclei</strong> are <strong>not</strong> part of the <strong>limbic system</strong>. The <strong>anterior nucleus</strong> of the <strong>thalamus</strong> is a part of the limbic system.</p>\n<p>The limbic system <strong>controls emotional behavior</strong> and is involved in the conversion of short-term memory to long-term memory. It consists of both the grey mater and white mater. It is located between the<strong> cerebral cortex </strong>and the<strong> hypothalamus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Q0xFDQZ4","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"22_hematopoietic_and_other_vitamins","title":"22.Hematopoietic And Other Vitamins","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB3069","text":"Which of the following vit‍amins contains cobalt as ​a part of their structure‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins contains cobalt as a part of their structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‍in B1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam‍in B2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam‍in B6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam‍in B12","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitamin B12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitamin B12</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin B12</strong> contains <strong>corrin rings</strong> with<strong> cobalt</strong> as a part of its structure, hence this group of vitamins are referred to as <strong>cobalamins</strong>. </p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 is derived mainly from animal products such as meat, fish, eggs and fortified cereals. There are no plant sources as such for vitamin B12 / cobalamine, hence <strong>strict vegetarians</strong> are at risk of developing a nutritional <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> vitamin B12</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 deficiency is associated with defects in RBC production leading to megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3075","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients is likely to develo​p vitamin B12 deficiency?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is likely to develop vitamin B12 deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 40-year-old male with a ‍segmental resection of pr​imary duodenal cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An intestinal obstruction ‍case with jejunectomy due​ to necrotic segment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 30-year-old patient who ‍got his ileum removed due​ to Crohn's disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 62-year-old patient who ‍underwent colectomy for u​lcerative colitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 30-year-old patient who got his ileum removed due to Crohn's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/996695fb5195416485da23dc6a93ab53x1280x2128.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 30-year-old patient who got his ileum removed due to Crohn&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>A 30-year-old patient who got his ileum removed due to Crohn's disease is likely to develop vitamin B12 deficiency. <strong>Vitamin B12</strong> is<strong> absorbed</strong> from the <strong>distal third of the ileum</strong> via receptors known as <strong>cubulin</strong>, which binds the intrinsic factor-vitamin B12 complex.</p>\n<p>Dietary vitamin B12 is normally bound to proteins in food. Pepsin and gastric acid in the stomach degrade these food proteins and release vitamin B12. The free vitamin B12 then binds to a <strong>salivary glycoprotein</strong> called <strong>haptocorrin (R-binder).</strong></p>\n<p>In the alkaline pH of the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade the haptocorrin, and vitamin B12 is released again. It then <strong>binds tightly</strong> to<strong> intrinsic factor (IF), </strong>a glycoprotein secreted by the <strong>parietal cells</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong> and this binding takes place in the<strong> duodenum. </strong>The IF-Vitamin B12 complex reaches the <strong>terminal ileum</strong> and attaches to a receptor on the enterocyte, from where it is <strong>absorbed. </strong>Vitamin B12 is transported in the blood bound to a carrier protein called <strong>transcobalamin II</strong> and delivered to the peripheral tissues.</p>\n<p>Absorption sites:</p>\n<p>Iron- Duodenum<br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Folate- Jejunum<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Vit B12- Ileum</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3271","text":"A 45-year-old patient came‍ with complaints of easy ​fatiguability and palpita‌tions. On examination, sh‌e had pallor. Methylmalon‍ic acid excretion in the ​urine was estimated as a ‌part of evaluation. This ​can be utilized to assess​ the nutritional status o‍f which vitamin?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient came with complaints of easy fatiguability and palpitations. On examination, she had pallor. Methylmalonic acid excretion in the urine was estimated as a part of evaluation. This can be utilized to assess the nutritional status of which vitamin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‍in B12","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Folic‍ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ir‍on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin B12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin B12</strong></p><p>The above scenario suggests <strong>anemia</strong> and<strong> methylmalonic acid excretion</strong> in the <strong>urine</strong> can be utilized to <strong>assess</strong> the <strong>nutritional status</strong> of<strong> vitamin B12.</strong></p>\n<p>Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase is a vitamin B12-dependent enzyme. The activity of methylmalonyl CoA mutase is greatly reduced in vitamin B12 deficiency, leading to an accumulation of methylmalonic acid and its excretion in urine.</p>\n<p>There are two vitamin B<sub>12</sub>-dependent enzymes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methionine synthase</strong> (homocysteine methyltransferase): Catalyzes the conversion of homocysteine to methionine</li>\n<li><strong>Methylmalonyl CoA mutase</strong>: methylmalonyl CoA, formed as an intermediate in the catabolism of valine, isoleucine, and propionyl CoA is converted to succinyl CoA</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Urinary excretion of methylmalonic acid is also used to differentiate between megaloblastic anemia due to folate and vitamin B<sub>12</sub> deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3272","text":"Folate trap occurs due to ‍the deficiency of which o​f the following vitamins?‌","raw_text":"Folate trap occurs due to the deficiency of which of the following vitamins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrid‍oxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam‍in B12","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folic‍ acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin B12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/036e6a7e30474e4abc9d87c9913034a2x1280x1774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin B12</strong></p><p><strong>Folate trap </strong>occurs in the <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> vitamin B12</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The conversion of 5,10-methylene-THF into 5-methyl-THF(methyltetrahydrofolate) is an irreversible process. The major source of tetrahydrofolate for tissues is <strong>5-methyl-THF</strong>. The only way to make further use of 5-methyl-THF and to maintain the folate cycle is by the vitamin <strong>B12-dependent</strong> remethylation of homocysteine to methionine by <strong>methionine synthase enzyme.</strong></p>\n<p>Decreased activity of methionine synthase in vitamin B12 deficiency results in the accumulation of methyltetrahydrofolate that cannot be used, resulting in a <strong>folate trap</strong>. The folate cycle provides a link between the functions of folate and vitamin B12, where vitamin B12 deficiency causes a functional deficiency of folate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3273","text":"A young woman presents wit‍h increasing fatigue, num​bness, and tingling in th‌e legs. She started follo‌wing a vegan diet 4 years‍ ago. On examination, glo​ssitis is seen, and hyper‌reflexia is present in th​e lower limbs. Lab invest​igations revealed the fol‍lowing. Which of the foll​owing findings is not see​n in this condition? Hb: ‌7 g/dl TLC: 6000/mm3 Plat​elets: 1,00,000/mm3 MCV: ​109 fl Vitamin B12 levels‌: 90 pg/ml.","raw_text":"<p>A young woman presents with increasing fatigue, numbness, and tingling in the legs. She started following a vegan diet 4 years ago.  On examination, glossitis is seen, and hyperreflexia is present in the lower limbs. Lab investigations revealed the following. Which of the following findings is not seen in this condition?</p>\n<p><strong>Hb: 7 g/dl<br/></strong><strong>TLC: 6000/mm3<br/></strong><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Platelets: 1,00,000/mm3<br/></strong><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">MCV: 109 fl<br/></strong><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Vitamin B12 levels: 90 pg/ml.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased homocysteine lev‍els","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Positive R‍omberg sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic ‍atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deme‍ntia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreased homocysteine levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f32cc3283684d7d812e66bcebd74a94x1280x1774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreased homocysteine levels</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of a patient on a vegan diet now presenting with glossitis of the tongue, hyperreflexia, and numbness and tingling in the legs is suggestive of <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency.</strong> Decreased homocysteine levels are not seen in this condition. Rather, <strong>increased homocysteine</strong> levels are seen.</p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 is a <strong>cofactor</strong> of<strong> methionine synthase </strong>and<strong> methylmalonyl CoA mutase.</strong> Methionine synthase is required for the conversion of homocysteine to methionine, which is the principal methyl donor. In vitamin B12 deficiency, <strong>defective methionine synthase </strong>leads to the following:</p>\n<p>1. In the absence of methionine, there is <strong>decreased methylation</strong> in the<strong> CNS. </strong>This leads to <strong>defective synthesis</strong> of<strong> myelin</strong> and<strong> neurotransmitters</strong>, which results in the following symptoms :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Posterolateral cord degeneration</strong>: It presents as loss of proprioception and vibration, positive Romberg sign, ataxia, hyperreflexia, spasticity</li>\n<li><strong>Dementia</strong> leading to cognitive impairment</li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral neuropathy</strong>: presenting as muscle weakness, paraesthesia</li>\n<li><strong>Optic atrophy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>2.<strong> Homocysteine accumulates </strong>in the<strong> blood</strong> conferring an increased risk of <strong>cardiovascular disease.</strong></p>\n<p>3.<strong> Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> The availability of 5,10-methylene THF is reduced, which is required in the conversion of deoxyuridine monophosphate (dUMP) to thymidine monophosphate (TMP) for DNA synthesis. This leads to macrocytic anemia with megaloblasts in the bone marrow.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6924","text":"Which of the following are‍ vitamin-B12 dependent en​zymes? Methyl malonyl CoA‌ isomerase Homocysteine s‌ynthase Folate reductase ‍Methionine synthase","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are vitamin-B12 dependent enzymes?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Methyl malonyl CoA isomerase</li>\n<li>Homocysteine synthase</li>\n<li>Folate reductase</li>\n<li>Methionine synthase</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 , ‍3 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‍4 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‍3 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,‍ and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc90fce6f1ea4659a8ff03259abaf630x1200x1663.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 and 4 only</strong></p><p>Among the given enzymes, <strong>methyl malonyl CoA isomerase</strong> and <strong>methionine synthase</strong> are <strong>vitamin B12</strong> dependent enzymes. </p>\n<p>There are two vitamin B<sub>12</sub>-dependent enzymes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methionine synthase</strong> (homocysteine methyltransferase): Catalyzes the conversion of homocysteine to methionine</li>\n<li><strong>Methylmalonyl CoA mutase</strong>: methylmalonyl CoA, formed as an intermediate in the catabolism of valine, isoleucine, and propionyl CoA is converted to succinyl CoA</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The below figure shows B12 serving as a cofactor of methionine synthase. Its deficiency may cause a folate trap.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0737","text":"A patient presented with s‍ymptoms of peripheral neu​ropathy and macrocytic an‌emia. She was given folat‌e 5mg and the blood pictu‍re improved. However, the​ neurologic manifestation‌s were aggravated. What i​s the most likely cause?","raw_text":"A patient presented with symptoms of peripheral neuropathy and macrocytic anemia. She was given folate 5mg and the blood picture improved. However, the neurologic manifestations were aggravated. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malabsorpti‍on of folate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment with folate unma‍sking pyridoxine deficien​cy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficiency of folate reduc‍tase in CNS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folate therapy using the B‍12 stores","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Folate therapy using the B12 stores","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5d6c8ffb72c49bb85eb3eb5a7900d1ax1280x1902.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Folate therapy using the B12 stores</strong></p><p>The most likely cause is <strong>folate therapy </strong>which <strong>used up</strong> the<strong> B12 stores, aggravating</strong> the<strong> symptoms.</strong></p>\n<p>Administration of <strong>folic acid</strong> for patients with macrocytic anemia can partially <strong>reverse</strong> the <strong>hematologic</strong> abnormalities associated with vitamin B12 deficiency. However, the <strong>neurologic manifestations</strong> of vitamin B12 deficiency are <strong>not treated</strong> by folic acid.</p>\n<p>Administration of folic acid to an individual with vitamin B12 deficiency can potentially <strong>mask</strong> untreated <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency</strong> or even worsen the neurologic complications. Hence, <strong>testing</strong> for and/or <strong>treatment</strong> of vitamin B12 deficiency is required in all patients being treated with folic acid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3079","text":"Which of the following vit‍amins serves as a carrier​ for one carbon atom?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins serves as a carrier for one carbon atom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Folic‍ acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pantothe‍nic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67458a647b3b4649bad6908f72cae139x1280x1276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Folic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Folic acid</strong> serves as a <strong>carrier</strong> of <strong>one carbon atom</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tetrahydrofolate receives <strong>one-carbon fragments</strong> from <strong>donors</strong> such as serine, glycine, and histidine and transfers them to intermediates in the synthesis of amino acids, purines, and pyrimidines.</p>\n<p>The manifestations of folic acid deficiency includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Megaloblastic anemia </li>\n<li>Homocystinemia</li>\n<li>Neural tube defect (spina bifida, anencephaly)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The major point of entry for one-carbon fragments, in one-carbon metabolism, is <strong>methylenetetrahydrofolate.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3060","text":"Which of the following vit‍amins serves as a coenzym​e in transamination react‌ions?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins serves as a coenzyme in transamination reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribof‍lavin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrid‍oxine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bio‍tin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyridoxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyridoxine</strong></p><p><strong>Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP</strong>), an <strong>active</strong> form of <strong>pyridoxine</strong> serves as a <strong>coenzyme</strong> in <strong>transamination</strong> reactions.</p>\n<p>Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) is a coenzyme for enzymes involved in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transamination</li>\n<li>Decarboxylation</li>\n<li>Glycogenolysis: Glycogen phosphorylase</li>\n<li>Transsulfuration reaction: Cystathionine β-synthase and cystathionase</li>\n<li>Heme synthesis: ALA synthase (deficiency causes microcytic hypochromic anemia)</li>\n<li>Tryptophan metabolism: Kynureninase (deficiency causes pellagra-like symptoms)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) serves as a coenzyme in many amino acid metabolisms, so its RDA mainly depends on daily protein intake.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3065","text":"Which of the following vit‍amins is not required in ​the TCA cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins is not required in the TCA cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ribof‍lavin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrid‍oxine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pyridoxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/268b05b235e644a1ba12c1fe4a38c614x754x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pyridoxine</strong></p><p>Pyridoxine is not required in the TCA cycle.</p>\n<p>Vitamins, in the form of their respective coenzymes, are required for the proper functioning of the TCA cycle.</p>\n<p>The following vitamins are needed in the TCA cycle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thiamine</strong> as TPP, for decarboxylation in the α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase reaction</li>\n<li><strong>Riboflavin</strong> as FAD and FMN, for succinate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li><strong>Niacin</strong> as NAD and NADH, for isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and malate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li><strong>Pantothenic acid</strong> as a part of Coenzyme A helps to form acetyl-CoA and succinyl-CoA</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3270","text":"A 45-year-old man is under‍going treatment for pulmo​nary tuberculosis. During‌ one of his follow-ups, i‌t was noted that he is ta‍king pyridoxine tablets i​n excess. Which of the fo‌llowing symptoms is he mo​st likely to develop?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man is undergoing treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis. During one of his follow-ups, it was noted that he is taking pyridoxine tablets in excess. Which of the following symptoms is he most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deme‍ntia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halluci‍nations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sensory n‍europathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nyst‍agmus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sensory neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sensory neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Excess pyridoxine</strong> (vitamin B6) causes <strong>sensory neuropathy</strong>. It occurs at intakes above 2 g/day.</p>\n<p>There are only two B-complex vitamins that cause toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Niacin</strong>: It is associated with:\n<ul>\n<li>Prostaglandin-mediated cutaneous flushing</li>\n<li>Fulminant hepatitis (most fatal manifestation)</li>\n<li>Glucose intolerance</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pyridoxine</strong>: It is associated with sensory neuropathy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5234","text":"Which of the following enh‍ances iron absorption?","raw_text":"Which of the following enhances iron absorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‍min A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‍min C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thia‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ribof‍lavin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin C</strong></p><p><strong>Iron absorption</strong> is enhanced by<strong> vitamin C.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin C</strong> reduces the ferric iron to its ferrous form, thereby helping in the process of <strong>absorption </strong>of<strong> dietary iron </strong>from the<strong> intestine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vitamin C deficiency causes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased absorption of iron leading to <strong>microcytic-hypochromic anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Decreased hydroxylation of proline and lysine, which results in <strong>defective collagen</strong> synthesis and <strong>bleeding manifestations</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conversely, vitamin C toxicity (&gt;2000 mg/ day) can lead to iron overloaded states.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Alcohol</strong> and<strong> fructose</strong> also <strong>enhance iron absorption. </strong>However, the absorption of both inorganic and heme iron is <strong>impaired by calcium</strong>. So, a glass of milk with a meal significantly reduces iron availability.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3277","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about scurvy?","raw_text":" Which of the following is false about scurvy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microcytic anemia can be s‍een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The beading on ribs is non‍-tender and round","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patient can present with p‍seudoparalysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is impaired formatio‍n of mature connective ti​ssue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The beading on ribs is non-tender and round","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The beading on ribs is non-tender and round</strong></p><p>The <strong>beading</strong> of <strong>ribs</strong> in <strong>scurvy</strong> is <strong>sharp</strong> and<strong> tender.</strong></p>\n<p>A deficiency state of vitamin C results in scurvy. The clinical features are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatigue, irritability, loss of appetite</li>\n<li>Loose and spongy gums along with loose teeth</li>\n<li>Swollen joints</li>\n<li>Perifollicular hemorrhages</li>\n<li>Bleeding into the skin - ecchymosis, purpura, petechiae</li>\n<li>Pseudoparalysis</li>\n<li>Microcytic anemia (due to decreased absorption of iron from the intestine)</li>\n<li>Scorbutic rosary - sharp, tender beading at the costochondral junctions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many of the deficiency symptoms can be attributed to <strong>deficient collagen synthesis</strong> caused due to an inability to hydroxylate the prolyl and lysyl residues of collagen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xOGZsT1g","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"22_non_communicable_diseases___cancer__obesity_and_blindness","title":"22.Non Communicable Diseases   Cancer, Obesity And Blindness","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB8150","text":"Which of the following org‍anisms is least likely to​ be associated with malig​nancies?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is least likely to be associated with malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‍alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein-B‍arr Virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HPV 6 ‍and 11","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schis‍tosoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HPV 6 and 11","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HPV 6 and 11</strong></p><p><strong>HPV 6</strong> and<strong> 11 </strong>are considered <strong>low-risk</strong> subtypes and are associated mainly with <strong>genital warts </strong>(condyloma acuminata) and <strong>respiratory papillomatosis</strong> in both genders.</p>\n<p><strong>HPV 16 </strong>and<strong> 18</strong> are considered <strong>high-risk</strong> subtypes and are associated with <strong>cancer cervix</strong> and pre-cancerous cervical lesions. There is also evidence linking HPV 16 and 18 with cancers of the anus, vulva, vagina, penis, and oropharynx.</p>\n<p>Important <strong>agents</strong> associated with <strong>malignant conditions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HHV-8 (mainly), cytomegalovirus - Kaposi’s sarcoma</li>\n<li>Epstein-Barr virus - Burkitt’s lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma</li>\n<li>HPV 16 and 18 - Cancer cervix</li>\n<li>HBV and HCV - Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Schistosoma - Bladder carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8165","text":"Which among the following ‍is not a primary method o​f prevention of carcinoma​ in the population?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a primary method of prevention of carcinoma in the population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scre‍ening","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Restricting the use of tob‍acco","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immuni‍zation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avoiding‍ alcohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Screening","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Screening</strong></p><p><strong>Screening</strong> for cancer is a<strong> secondary</strong> level of prevention.</p>\n<p>This is because it detects the disease at a pre-invasive or pre-malignant stage when the process of <strong>carcinogenesis</strong> has <strong>already started</strong>.<strong> </strong>It thereby aids in further treatment and improved survival. </p>\n<p><strong>Immunization</strong>,<strong> restricting </strong>the use of <strong>tobacco</strong> and<strong> </strong>avoiding<strong> alcohol</strong> are <strong>primary</strong> levels of prevention because they prevent the start of carcinogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8167","text":"According to Globocan 2020‍ India, which of the foll​owing are the most common​ malignancies among men a‌nd women respectively?","raw_text":"According to Globocan 2020 India, which of the following are the most common malignancies among men and women respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca lung an‍d Ca cervix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ca lung an‍d Ca breast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ca oral cavity and Ca brea‍st","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ca colon an‍d Ca cervix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ca oral cavity and Ca breast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ca oral cavity and Ca breast</strong></p><p>According to <strong>Globocan 2020 India </strong>data, the most common malignancy among <strong>men</strong> is cancer <strong>lip and oral cavity</strong>, and among <strong>women </strong>is<strong> </strong>cancer<strong> b</strong><strong>reast</strong>.</p>\n<p>The table below lists the top 5 common cancers according to the <strong>Globocan 2020 India.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 365px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99.109375px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Men</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.890625px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Women</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Overall</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99.109375px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>1. Lip, oral cavity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.890625px; height: 18px;\">1. Breast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">1. Breast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99.109375px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>2. Lung</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.890625px; height: 18px;\">2. Cervix uteri</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">2. Lip, oral cavity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99.109375px; height: 25px;\">3. Stomach</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.890625px; height: 25px;\">3. Ovary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 25px;\">3. Cervix uteri</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99.109375px; height: 20px;\">4. Colorectum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.890625px; height: 20px;\">4. Lip, Oral cavity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 20px;\">4. Lung</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 99.109375px; height: 21px;\">5. Oesophagus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.890625px; height: 21px;\">5. Colorectum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 21px;\">5. Colorectum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8170","text":"Which of the following wom‍en is not at an increased​ risk of developing breas​t cancer in future?","raw_text":"Which of the following women is not at an increased risk of  developing breast cancer in future?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A woman who attained menar‍che at 9 years of age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A woman who has never brea‍stfed in her lifetime","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A woman who attained menop‍ause at 56 years of age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A woman who had her first ‍full term pregnancy at 26​ years of age","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A woman who had her first full term pregnancy at 26 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A woman who had her first full term pregnancy at 26 years of age</strong></p><p>Women who have their<strong> first</strong> full-term <strong>pregnancy</strong> <strong>after 30 years</strong> of age are at <strong>increased risk</strong> for breast cancer.</p>\n<p>The risk of breast cancer increases with an increase in duration of exposure to estrogen. As progesterone levels are increased during pregnancy, it reduces the risks of conditions that occur due to hyperestrogenemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for breast cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modifiable</strong> risk factors:\n<ul>\n<li>Age at first live birth (First full-term pregnancy after 30 years)</li>\n<li>Parity</li>\n<li>Lack of breastfeeding</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Alcohol consumption</li>\n<li>Tobacco smoking</li>\n<li>Use of hormone replacement therapy</li>\n<li>Decreased physical activity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Non-modifiable</strong> risk factors:\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing age</li>\n<li>Early age at menarche (onset of menses before 12 years of age)</li>\n<li>Menstrual factors</li>\n<li>Female sex</li>\n<li>Older age at menopause (onset beyond 55 years of age)</li>\n<li>Nulliparity</li>\n<li>Race, ethnicity</li>\n<li>Genetic predisposition</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>History of radiation exposure </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8173","text":"A 50-year-old woman is at ‍increased risk of breast ​cancer as she is on hormo​ne replacement therapy. W‌hich of the following is ​the most sensitive method‍ of screening for this co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman is at increased risk of breast cancer as she is on hormone replacement therapy. Which of the following is the most sensitive method of screening for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Self breast‍ examination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical breast examinatio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultrasound o‍f the breast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mammo‍graphy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mammography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mammography</strong></p><p><strong>Mammography</strong> is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>sensitive</strong> and specific investigation for detecting <strong>breast cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>It can detect even small tumors that are missed during a self-breast examination, clinical examination by the physician and ultrasonographic examination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7516","text":"Which of the following is ‍the best level of prevent​ion of breast cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best level of prevention of breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Specific ‍protection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early diagnosis and treatm‍ent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Disability‍ limitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rehabil‍itation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Early diagnosis and treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Early diagnosis and treatment</strong></p><p><strong>Early diagnosis </strong>and<strong> treatment</strong> is the <strong>best</strong> level of prevention of breast cancer. It is a <strong>secondary</strong> level of <strong>prevention</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> earlier</strong> a disease is <strong>diagnosed</strong> and treated, the <strong>better</strong> is the <strong>prognosis</strong>. The occurrence of secondary cases or any long term disability can be prevented. This is of particular importance in <strong>chronic diseases.</strong></p>\n<p>Early diagnosis and treatment is not as effective and economical as primary prevention. However, it may be <strong>critically important</strong> in reducing the high morbidity and mortality in diseases such as <strong>essential hypertension, cancer cervix</strong> and<strong> breast cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8184","text":"A patient with Prader-Will‍i syndrome needs to be ev​aluated for obesity but t​he scale for measuring he‌ight in your clinic is br​oken. Which of the follow‍ing indices can you still‌ use in this situation?","raw_text":"A patient with Prader-Willi syndrome needs to be evaluated for obesity but the scale for measuring height in your clinic is broken. Which of the following indices can you still use in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quetelet‍'s index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corpulen‍ce index","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lorentz‍ formula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broca'‍s index","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corpulence index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corpulence index</strong></p><p>The<strong> corpulence</strong> <strong>index</strong> can be used in this scenario as it is <strong>independent of height</strong>. The formula for corpulence index is<strong> actual weight/ desired weight</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8186","text":"Which of the following ind‍ices of obesity is obtain​ed by dividing weight in ​kg by square of height in‌ metres?","raw_text":"Which of the following indices of obesity is obtained by dividing weight in kg by square of height in metres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pondera‍l index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Broca'‍s index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lorentz‍'s index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quetelet‍’s index","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Quetelet’s index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Quetelet’s index</strong></p><p><strong>Quetelet's index</strong> (Body Mass Index) is obtained by <strong>dividing</strong> <strong>weight</strong> in kg <strong>by square</strong> of <strong>height</strong> in metres.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8189","text":"The BMI of two Indian coll‍ege students are 17 and 2​4.99 respectively. How wi​ll you classify them in t‌erms of their BMI?","raw_text":"The BMI of two Indian college students are 17 and 24.99 respectively. How will you classify them in terms of their BMI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Underweight‍ and normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Underweight and overweight‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal and‍ overweight","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Underweigh‍t and obese","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Underweight and overweight","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Underweight and overweight</strong></p><p>An adult <strong>Indian</strong> with a BMI of <strong>17</strong> is classified as <strong>underweight </strong>and a BMI of <strong>24.99</strong> is classified as <strong>overweight</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8194","text":"The weight of a diabetic w‍oman is 75 kg and her hei​ght is 150 cm. Calculate ​her BMI.","raw_text":"The weight of a diabetic woman is 75 kg and her  height is 150 cm. Calculate her BMI.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‍0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‍3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3‍5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‍8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 33","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 33</strong></p><p>The <strong>BMI</strong> of an individual with a weight of 75 kg and height of 150 cm is <strong>33</strong>. It is calculated as follows,</p>\n<p>BMI = <strong>Weight</strong> (in kg) <strong>/ Height<sup>2</sup></strong> (in metre<sup>2</sup>) = 75 / (1.5)<sup>2 </sup>= 33.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8198","text":"For which of the following‍ conditions, an obese ind​ividual is at the greates​t risk of developing in t‌he future?","raw_text":"For which of the following conditions, an obese individual is at the greatest risk of developing in the future?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycystic o‍vary syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coronary ar‍tery disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low ba‍ck pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 2 ‍diabetes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 2 diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 2 diabetes</strong></p><p>Among the given options, an <strong>obese</strong> individual is at the <strong>greatest risk</strong> of developing <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The relative risk of<strong> health problems </strong>associated with<strong> obesity</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 287px; width: 455.546875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Greatly increased risk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Moderately increased risk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Slightly increased risk</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 62px;\">Type 2 diabetes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 62px;\">Coronary artery disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 62px;\">Cancer (breast cancer in postmenopausal women, endometrial cancer, colon cancer)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 36px;\">Gall bladder disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 36px;\">Hypertension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 36px;\">Reproductive hormone abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 18px;\">Dyslipidaemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">Osteoarthritis(knees)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 18px;\">Polycystic ovary syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 36px;\">Insulin resistance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 36px;\">Hyperuricaemia and gout</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 36px;\">Impaired fertility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 18px;\">Breathlessness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 18px;\">Low back pain due to obesity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 18px;\">Sleep apnea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 18px;\">Increased risk of anaesthesia complications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.546875px; height: 18px;\">Fetal defects associated with maternal obesity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8200","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is incorrect rega​rding waist circumference​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding waist circumference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cut-off for normal wai‍st circumference in India​n women is 90 cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is measured at the midp‍oint between the lower bo​rder of the rib cage and ​the iliac crest","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an approximate index‍ of intra-abdominal and t​otal body fat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is an indicator of the ‍risk of chronic diseases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The cut-off for normal waist circumference in Indian women is 90 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The cut-off for normal waist circumference in Indian women is 90 cm</strong></p><p>The cut-off value for <strong>normal waist circumference</strong> in Indian <strong>women</strong> is <strong>80cm</strong>,<strong> </strong>while in Indian <strong>men</strong> it is <strong>90cm.</strong></p>\n<p>Waist circumference is an approximate indicator of intra-abdominal and total body fat. It is measured at the midpoint between the lower border of the rib cage and the iliac crest. It is an indicator of the risk of chronic diseases.</p>\n<p>The cut-off for normal waist circumference is:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 240px; height: 212px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\"><strong>Category</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\"><strong>Cut-off for normal waist</strong> <strong>circumference</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\">Indian</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\">     Men</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\">   90cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\">     Women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\">   80cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\">World</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\">     Men</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\">   102cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109.390625px;\">     Women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.609375px;\">    88cm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8201","text":"According to the WHO, what‍ is the cut-off for visua​l acuity for blindness?","raw_text":"According to the WHO, what is the cut-off for visual acuity for blindness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6/‍60","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3/‍60","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1/‍60","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perceptio‍n of light","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3/60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3/60</strong></p><p>According to the WHO, <strong>blindness</strong> is defined as<strong> visual acuity</strong> of <strong>less than</strong> <strong>3/60</strong>, or a corresponding <strong>visual field </strong>loss to less than <strong>10°</strong>, in the better eye <strong>with</strong> the <strong>best possible correction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8203","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common cause of ​blindness in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of blindness in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refracti‍ve error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cata‍ract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetic r‍etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trachomatous corneal opaci‍ty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cataract</strong></p><p>According to the National Survey on Blindness (2015-2019), <strong>cataract</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>cause </strong>of blindness in India accounting for <strong>66.2% </strong>of the total cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8205","text":"You administer vitamin A p‍rophylactically to a 15-m​onth-old child during a r​outine checkup. What is t‌he level of prevention be​ing achieved here?","raw_text":"You administer vitamin A prophylactically to a 15-month-old child during a routine checkup. What is the level of prevention being achieved here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pri‍mary","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seco‍ndary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tert‍iary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primo‍rdial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylactic</strong> administration of <strong>vitamin A</strong> to a normal child is an example of <strong>primary</strong> level of <strong>prevention</strong>. It is done to prevent <strong>xerophthalmia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8207","text":"A boy is brought to the op‍hthalmologist with compla​ints of dryness in both t​he eyes and difficulty in‌ reading. On examination,​ his eye appears as shown‍ in the below image. What‌ is the stage of xerophth‌almia according to WHO?","raw_text":"A boy is brought to the ophthalmologist with complaints of dryness in both the eyes and difficulty in reading. On examination, his eye appears as shown in the below image. What is the stage of xerophthalmia according to WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X1‍ A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X1‍ B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X3‍ A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X3‍ B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. X1 B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9a18b1d88a74570bad565d512a2245b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. X1 B</strong></p><p>The finding in the given image is a <strong>Bitot's spot</strong>. It belongs to WHO class <strong>X1 B </strong>of <strong>vitamin A deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8209","text":"A 2-year-old child on exam‍ination shows signs of vi​tamin A deficiency. What ​is the dose of vitamin A ‌to be administered to thi​s child?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child on examination shows signs of vitamin A deficiency. What is the dose of vitamin A to be administered to this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5000‍0 IU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 la‍kh IU","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 lak‍hs IU","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 l‍akh IU","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 lakhs IU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 lakhs IU</strong></p><p><strong>2 lakh</strong> <strong>IU</strong> of vitamin A should be administered to a 2-year-old child with vitamin A deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Dosage </strong>of vitamin A in children:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>50,000 IU</strong> for children<strong> less than 6 months </strong>of age</li>\n<li><strong>1 lakh IU</strong> for children<strong> between 6 </strong>months to<strong> 12 months </strong>of age</li>\n<li><strong>2 lakh IU</strong> for children <strong>above</strong> <strong>12 months</strong> of age.<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These doses of vitamin A are to be administered on <strong>days</strong> <strong>1, 2</strong> and <strong>14</strong> for the treatment of vitamin A deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8211","text":"What is the most common ca‍use of injury-related dea​th?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of injury-related death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interperson‍al violence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fa‍lls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Road traffi‍c accidents","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sui‍cide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Road traffic accidents","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Road traffic accidents</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of injury-related deaths are <strong>road traffic accidents</strong>, followed by<strong> suicide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ZILoHEjo","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"22_skin_malignancies","title":"22.Skin Malignancies","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5406","text":"A 23-year-old truck driver​ presents with the follow‌ing lesion on his legs wh​ich have progressed in si‌ze from the time he first​ noticed them. On examina‌tion, you find some degre‌e of edema in the lower l​imbs. Which of the follow‍ing is the most common or‍ganism associated with th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old truck driver presents with the following lesion on his legs which have progressed in size from the time he first noticed them. On examination, you find some degree of edema in the lower limbs. Which of the following is the most common organism associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HH​V6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HH​V7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HH​V8","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HH​V5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HHV8","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0dfb64c370c743a0adf80a89e68962be.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HHV8</strong></p><p>The above image shows multiple <strong>plaque-like lesions</strong> coalescing with each other which is suggestive of <strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong>. All forms of Kaposi sarcoma are associated with <strong>HHV 8 infection</strong> as an aetiological agent.</p>\n<p>It is a multifocal, endothelial proliferation most often with cutaneous involvement and with or without visceral extension. This virus is transmitted through <strong>saliva</strong> and <strong>blood products</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has a <strong>low-grade</strong> malignant potential. The most important cofactor associated is <strong>HIV co-infection</strong> which increases the risk up to 20,000 fold.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5407","text":"A 50-year-old post-renal t​ransplant patient on cycl‌osporine presented with t​he following lesion. You ‌suspect it to be a cancer​ous growth. Which of the ‌following statements rega‌rding this condition is f​alse?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old post-renal transplant patient on cyclosporine presented with the following lesion. You suspect it to be a cancerous growth. Which of the following statements regarding this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has a high grade malign​ant potential","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The most common site is ex​tremities","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesions are usually multif​ocal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph nodes may be involve​d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It has a high grade malignant potential","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/067ec00b9afe48ceb2800d83d2770286.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It has a high grade malignant potential</strong></p><p>The above image showing <strong>purple-colored plaques</strong> on the ala and tip of the nose in an <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patient is suggestive of <strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong>. It is mostly seen in <strong>extremities</strong>, commonly on <strong>feet</strong> and occasionally on hands, ears, and <strong>nose</strong>, and has a <strong>low-grade</strong> malignant potential.</p>\n<p>Skin lesions are usually purple and <strong>multifocal</strong> which eventually evolve into plaques and tumors. <strong>Lymph nodes</strong>, mucosae, and viscera may be involved as the disease progresses, although this can occur without skin involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2873","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cutaneous‌ T‐cell lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common  cutaneous T‐cell lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sézary ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular​ mucinosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pagetoid r​eticulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycosis ​fungoides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mycosis fungoides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mycosis fungoides</strong></p><p>The most common type of primary cutaneous T cell lymphoma is <strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sézary syndrome</strong> may develop either ab initio or rarely as a progression from classic mycosis fungoides. It is a <strong>severe</strong> form of cutaneous T cell lymphoma with a <strong>poor prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Follicular mucinosis </strong>is a type of cutaneous T cell lymphoma that consists of boggy cutaneous plaques showing follicular prominence and histological evidence of mucinous degeneration of the hair follicles.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Pagetoid reticulosis</strong> is a localized, solitary variant of cutaneous T cell lymphoma, which histologically shows <strong>intense epidermotropism. </strong>It is also called <strong>Woringer–Kolopp disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Mycosis fungoides is derived from <strong>skin‐resident effector memory T cells</strong>, whereas Sézary syndrome is derived from <strong>central memory T cells.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5415","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the clini‌cal triad of Sézary syndr​ome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the clinical triad of Sézary syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythr​oderma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral lymphadenopathy​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atypical mononuclear cells​ &amp;gt; 20% of total lympho‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seborrhoei​c keratosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seborrhoeic keratosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seborrhoeic keratosis</strong></p><p>Seborrhoeic keratosis is <strong>not</strong> a part of the clinical triad of <strong>Sézary syndrome</strong> </p>\n<p>The clinical triad of Sézary syndrome consists of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Erythroderma</strong> - As a result of diffuse infiltration of the skin by neoplastic cells </li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral</strong> <strong>l</strong><strong>ymphadenopathy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Atypical mononuclear cells</strong> (Sézary cells) comprising more than 20% of total lymphocyte count or total Sézary count of more than 1000 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2877","text":"A 52-year-old male present​s with the following lesi‌ons. DNA analysis from ti​ssue samples for T-cell r‌eceptor analysis showed c​lonal rearrangements of t‌he T-cell receptor genes.‌ What is the possible dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old male presents with the following lesions. DNA analysis from tissue samples for T-cell receptor analysis showed clonal rearrangements of the T-cell receptor genes. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicular​ mucinosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bowen's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psor​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycosis ​fungoides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mycosis fungoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b97a876a90e427db7fc0d6633db6438.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mycosis fungoides</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with the characteristic investigation of T-cell receptor analysis showing <strong>clonal rearrangement</strong> of the T-cell receptor (TCR) genes is diagnostic of <strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mycosis fungoides is characterized by <strong>erythematous</strong>, <strong>polymorphic patches and plaques </strong>which are <strong>fine, scaly,</strong> and slightly <strong>atrophic</strong> (wrinkled) usually involving limb/girdle, breast, and especially the <strong>buttock area</strong>.</p>\n<p>Analysis of TCR genes is now a standard approach that has <strong>diagnostic, prognostic, </strong>and<strong> therapeutic</strong> implications. It consists of the <strong>analysis of DNA</strong> from tissue samples for the detection of clonal rearrangements of the TCR genes as a marker of a <strong>monoclonal T‐cell population</strong>. T‐cell clones can be detected in a proportion (70% overall) of skin biopsies from patients with early‐stage disease and are almost invariable in patients with later stages of the disease.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Follicular mucinosis</strong> consists of <strong>boggy cutaneous plaques</strong> showing <strong>follicular prominence</strong> and histological evidence of mucinous degeneration of the hair follicles. It is often associated with an <strong>atypical pilotropic T‐cell infiltrate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Bowen's Disease </strong>is a type of squamous cell carcinoma in situ that presents as an erythematous scaly plaque.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Psoriasis </strong>is a papulosquamous disorder presenting with erythematous plaques with silver coloured scales.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Follicular mucinosis</strong> is the only other cutaneous T-cell lymphoma given in the options.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5410","text":"A 50-year-old retired petr​oleum refinery worker cam‌e with complaints of scal​y pruritic patches on his‌ upper and lower extremit​ies. An elliptical skin b‌iopsy was sent for histop‌athological study and the​ following T-cell morphol‍ogy was noted. What is th‍e most probable diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old retired petroleum refinery worker came with complaints of scaly pruritic patches on his upper and lower extremities. An elliptical skin biopsy was sent for histopathological study and the following T-cell morphology was noted. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudol​ymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycosis ​fungoides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adult T Ce​ll leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic Myel​oid Leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mycosis fungoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9f85bea2a52433cb903637d19c18a90.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fdb216582614cd4987979f04985bd0fx1280x2049.083215796897.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b73ed8d23dc2461f90c505119f3247cdx1280x1079.605077574048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mycosis fungoides</strong></p><p>The image shows a characteristic <strong>cerebriform appearance</strong> of T-lymphocytes as seen in <strong>Mycosis fungoides.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>cutaneous T cell lymphoma</strong> with an indolent (slowly progressive) clinical course. Most accepted theory is that <strong>human T‐cell leukemia virus 1</strong> <strong>(HTLV-1) </strong>is the causative organism.</p>\n<p><strong>Histopathology findings:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Moderate lymphocytic infiltrate in the <strong>papillary dermis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Epidermotropism</strong> - presence of lymphocytes in the epidermal cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Pautriers’s microabscesses</strong> consisting of clusters of atypical lymphocytes.</li>\n<li>Atypical lymphocytes have a <strong>cerebriform appearance</strong> with an irregular nuclear outline and halo around cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note - Cerebriform appearance is also seen in <strong>Sézary syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2878","text":"Extracorporeal photopheres​is is used in the managem‌ent of which of the follo​wing malignancies?","raw_text":"Extracorporeal photopheresis is used in the management of which of the following malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoses ​fungoides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mela​noma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keratoa​cantoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mycoses fungoides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1eeb19c7e56b4d35a33b3d144dc28318x1280x1620.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mycoses fungoides</strong></p><p>Extracorporeal photopheresis is a <strong>systemic therapy</strong> used for <strong>mycosis fungoides.</strong> </p>\n<p>The procedure consists of <strong>three</strong> stages: </p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Leukapheresis</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Photoactivation </strong>(with psoralen and UVA)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Reinfusion of buffy coat</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>After exposure to <strong>UVA</strong>, <strong>psoralen</strong> binds covalently to DNA in <strong>separated leukocytes</strong> leading to <strong>cell cycle arrest</strong> and <strong>apoptosis</strong>. These apoptotic leukocytes when <strong>reintroduced</strong> into the peripheral circulation are phagocytosed by <strong>antigen-presenting cells</strong> (APCs). These APCs then produce <strong>tumor suppressor cells</strong> against the malignant cells responsible for mycosis fungoides.</p>\n<p><strong>Skin-directed</strong> therapy is preferred in the<strong> early stages</strong> whereas <strong>advanced stages</strong> require <strong>systemic therapy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Skin-directed therapies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Topical corticosteroids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Topical chemotherapy: nitrogen mustard (mechlorethamine) and carmustine (BCNU)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Topical rexinoids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phototherapy and photochemotherapy (PUVA)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiotherapy and electron beam therapy: Mycosis fungoides and variants are <strong>highly radiosensitive</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Systemic therapies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immunotherapy: Pegylated IFN‐α</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral Retinoids and rexinoids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Combination therapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Systemic chemotherapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Extracorporeal photopheresis (ECP)</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Toxin therapies: Denileukin diftitox</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monoclonal antibody therapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Histone deacetylase inhibitors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5428","text":"A 50-year-old male present​ed with a cancerous growt‌h on his face. Biopsy was​ taken and the following ‌appearance was noted. Wha​t is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male presented with a cancerous growth on his face. Biopsy was taken and the following appearance was noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keratoa​canthoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Basal cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f7020e3d524418982611690102b20bf.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Basal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The above image shows a characteristic <strong>peripheral</strong> <strong>palisading</strong> <strong>of tumor cells </strong>which is seen in <strong>basal cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tumour cells resemble<strong> cells of stratum basale </strong>that have grown into the dermis. This cluster of cells is surrounded at the <strong>periphery by perpendicularly arranged</strong> cells. It also has a well-organised <strong>stroma</strong> surrounding it.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5419","text":"Which of the following is ​the commonest type of bas‌al cell carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the commonest type of basal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nod​ular","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ulce​rated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infilt​rative","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Super​ficial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nodular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d90e00294d714707acd37beb1429da3bx1280x1096.755994358251.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nodular</strong></p><p><strong>Nodular</strong> <strong>type</strong> is the commonest type of <strong>basal</strong> <strong>cell</strong> <strong>carcinoma</strong>. <strong>T</strong><strong>elengiectasia</strong> and cystic areas may be present. Some areas may be pigmented.</p>\n<p>Histopathological patterns of basal cell carcinoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial</li>\n<li>Nodular</li>\n<li>Infiltrative</li>\n<li>Micronodular</li>\n<li>Pigmented.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5416","text":"Choose the correct risk fa​ctors for basal cell carc‌inoma. 1. PUVA therapy 2.​ Aniline dyes 3. HPV infe‌ctions 4. Surgical scar 5​. Iatrogenic infections","raw_text":"<p>Choose the correct risk factors for basal cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>1. PUVA therapy<br/>2. Aniline dyes<br/>3. HPV infections<br/>4. Surgical scar<br/>5. Iatrogenic infections</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​,2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2​,3,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,​3,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,3​,4,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3,4,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3,4,5</strong></p><p>PUVA therapy, HPV infections, surgical scar and iatrogenic infections are risk factors for<strong> basal cell carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The following are the <strong>risk factors</strong> for basal cell carcinoma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Solar radiation</li>\n<li><strong>Psoralen-UVA Therapy (PUVA)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Human Papilloma Virus infections</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Iatrogenic infections</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Surgical scar</strong></li>\n<li>UV-B rays</li>\n<li>Ionising radiation</li>\n<li>Photosensitising drugs</li>\n<li>Previous history of basal cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5417","text":"Which of the following fea​tures is incorrect regard‌ing basal cell carcinoma?​","raw_text":"Which of the following features is incorrect regarding basal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is a pre-malignant s​tage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pearly trans​lucent nodule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid growt​h is unusual","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metastasi​s is rare","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. There is a pre-malignant stage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. There is a pre-malignant stage</strong></p><p>Basal cell carcinoma typically has <strong>no</strong> <strong>premalignant</strong> <strong>stage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The typical basal cell carcinoma runs a <strong>slowly </strong>progressive course of peripheral extension. The <strong>pearly translucent nodule</strong> with surface telangiectasia undergoes central necrosis resulting in rodent ulcer with a rolled-out margin.</p>\n<p><strong>Metastasis</strong> is extremely<strong> rare</strong> in basal cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2866","text":"The biopsy from an indurat​ed plaque-like lesion is ‌shown below. Keeping the ​diagnosis in mind which o‌f the following is not a ​premalignant lesion of th‌is condition?","raw_text":"The biopsy from an indurated plaque-like lesion is shown below. Keeping the diagnosis in mind which of the following is not a premalignant lesion of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actinic ​keratosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cutaneo​us horn","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arsenical​ keratosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keratoa​canthoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Keratoacanthoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3191010c740431ca6c3e9e3a9705945.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62a4b34c8fe84d73951d25a3391a671fx720x614.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96fe87123d04414db439bef160a0b4a7x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Keratoacanthoma</strong></p><p>The above image shows malignant cells resembling normal<strong> squamous epithelial cells</strong> with individual cell keratinization and nests of<strong> keratin pearls </strong>suggestive of<strong> squamous cell carcinoma</strong>. <strong>Keratoacanthoma</strong> is considered a form of <strong>well-differentiated</strong> <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong>, not a premalignant condition.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Actinic keratosis</strong> is<strong> </strong>characterized by hyperkeratotic lesions that have a <strong>low risk</strong> of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma(SCC).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Cutaneous horns</strong> are hard conical projections from the skin, made of compact keratin. They arise from benign, premalignant, or malignant skin lesions. Approximately <strong>9%</strong> showed <strong>SCC pathological features</strong> at the base.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Arsenical keratosis: </strong>A corn‐like, punctate keratosis caused by <strong>arsenic</strong>, characteristically affecting the <strong>palms and soles</strong>, which may progress to squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2860","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding the p‌remalignant lesion shown ​below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the premalignant lesion shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low risk of progression to​ squamous cell carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with HPV infect​ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with mutation o​f RAS gene","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"More common in the elderly​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated with mutation of RAS gene","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3dc70f221a7147c9a79e3eb5e024f618.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated with mutation of RAS gene</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>a</strong><strong>ctinic keratosis</strong> which is associated with mutation of the <strong>TP53 gene</strong>.              </p>\n<p>Actinic keratoses are hyperkeratotic lesions occurring on <strong>chronically light‐exposed</strong> adult skin that carry a <strong>low risk</strong> of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Lesions are usually <strong>multiple</strong> and comprise either <strong>macules</strong> or <strong>papules</strong> with a rough <strong>scaly</strong> surface resulting from disorganized keratinization and a variable degree of inflammation. It is more common in the <strong>elderly</strong>. <strong>Human papillomavirus</strong> (HPV) is present in significant numbers in actinic keratosis.</p>\n<p>Histopathology is characterized by <strong>solar</strong> <strong>elastosis</strong> and disordered epidermal keratinocyte maturation with <strong>cytological</strong> <strong>atypia</strong>. The typical lesion shows <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong>, <strong>parakeratosis</strong>, and <strong>hypergranulosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5423","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding Bowen’‌s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding Bowen’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraepidermal squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Red scaly plaque is presen​t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High potential for invasiv​e malignancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occasionally it may underg​o spontaneous partial res‌olution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High potential for invasive malignancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b3ceae757274e5bbb82ee20a70840f8x1280x1216.8124118476728.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High potential for invasive malignancy</strong></p><p><strong>Bowen's disease </strong>has a <strong>low potential </strong>for invasive<strong> malignancy. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>intraepidermal</strong> squamous cell carcinoma<strong> </strong>that<strong> </strong>is characterized by a persistent, non‐elevated, <strong>red</strong>, <strong>scaly</strong>, or crusted plaque with a <strong>small potential</strong> for invasive malignancy. Histologically, it is characterized by <strong>full-thickness epidermal dysplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It usually grows progressively but sometimes undergoes <strong>spontaneous</strong> <strong>partial</strong> <strong>resolution</strong>. It can occur anywhere, but it occurs more in the <strong>head and neck</strong> region.</p>\n<p><strong>Human papilloma virus </strong>infection is associated with it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Red scaly plaque seen on the finger, in an 81-year-old woman with Bowen’s disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5425","text":"Choose the correct stateme​nt with respect to the hi‌stology of the lesion sho​wn below.","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement with respect to the histology of the lesion shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Well-differentiated squamo​us cell carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Poorly differentiated basa​l cell carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Well-differentiated basal ​cell carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poorly differentiated squa​mous cell carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bbc8a0e57b5449a08a24285e0fc8cc47.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>exophytic</strong> <strong>verruciform</strong> growth suggestive of <strong>verrucous carcinoma </strong>which is a <strong>well</strong>-<strong>differentiated</strong> squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>slow-growing</strong> and <strong>rarely metastasizes</strong>. The etiology of verrucous carcinoma is unknown but it can develop in areas of chronic inflammation. <strong>Human Papillomavirus</strong> (HPV) has been associated with this tumor and specifically <strong>HPV types 11 </strong>and<strong> 16</strong> have been described in <strong>plantar lesions</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5430","text":"In which of the following ​syndromes do patients dev‌elop multiple sebaceous t​umors, with multiple prim‌ary internal malignancies​, particularly colorectal‌ cancers?","raw_text":"In which of the following syndromes do patients develop multiple sebaceous tumors, with multiple primary internal malignancies, particularly colorectal cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cowden ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muir-Torr​e syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gorlin ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gardner ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muir-Torre syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muir-Torre syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Muir-Torre syndrome</strong> is an <strong>autosomal</strong>-<strong>dominant</strong> inherited condition and has multiple <strong>sebaceous</strong> <strong>tumors</strong> with multiple visceral malignancies like <strong>colorectal carcinoma</strong>. <strong>Other internal malignancies</strong> include carcinoma of the endometrium, stomach, small bowel, genitourinary tract, breast, ovary, pancreas, liver, and kidney.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Cowden syndrome- </strong>mucocutaneous lesions, <strong>facial trichilemmomas</strong>, acral keratosis, papillomatous lesions, and mucosal lesions. The mucosal lesions comprise a <strong>warty, ‘cobblestone’ hyperplasia</strong> of the tongue and buccal mucosal surfaces. Multiple hamartomatous lesions of ectodermal, endodermal, and mesodermal origin occur.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Gorlin syndrome or nevoid basal cell carcinoma- </strong>an autosomal dominant familial cancer syndrome in which affected individuals are predisposed to the development of <strong>multiple BCCs</strong> at an early age, characteristic facies, and various skeletal abnormalities.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Gardner Syndrome- </strong>a variant of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), is an autosomal dominant disease characterized by gastrointestinal polyps, multiple osteomas, and skin and soft tissue tumors. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5431","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is associated wit‌h PTCH gene mutation?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with PTCH gene mutation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muir-torr​e syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gorlin ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rombo s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cowden ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gorlin syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gorlin syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Gorlin syndrome</strong>, also known as <strong>nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome</strong>, has an <strong>autosomal</strong> <strong>dominant</strong> <strong>inheritance</strong> with mutation of <strong>PATCHED </strong>(<strong>PTCH1) gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>It consists of the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple Basal cell carcinomas</li>\n<li>Highly characteristic facies (with large forehead)</li>\n<li><strong>Bifid</strong> or misshapen ribs</li>\n<li>Vertebral and other skeletal anomalies</li>\n<li><strong>Pits of the skin</strong> of the palms and soles</li>\n<li><strong>Dysgenesis</strong> of the corpus callosum</li>\n<li>Calcification of the falx cerebri (at an earlier age than is seen in non‐affected individuals)</li>\n<li>Macrocephaly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Muir-Torre syndrome-</strong> defect in DNA mismatch repair genes (MLH-1 or MSH-2) and microsatellite instability</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Rombo syndrome- </strong>autosomal dominant syndrome with basal cell carcinoma as prominent feature; defective gene unidentified</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Cowden syndrome- </strong>PTEN (phosphatase and tensin homologue) mutation</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2882","text":"A 58-year-old lady present​s with pain, swelling, an‌d discoloration in her le​ft thumb for 4 months. On‌ examination, the followi​ng clinical sign is noted‌. What is this sign calle‌d?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old lady presents with pain, swelling, and discoloration in her left thumb for 4 months. On examination, the following clinical sign is noted. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frank'​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fitzpatri​ck's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hutchins​on's sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pastia​'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hutchinson's sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/94e4be94eefc4dffbe1b2f83761f0cca.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hutchinson&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>melanonychia</strong> with the pigmentation of <strong>proximal nail fold</strong> which is called the <strong>Hutchinson's sign. </strong>It is seen in <strong>subungual melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>benign</strong> <strong>nail pigmentation</strong> due to drugs (eg. minocycline) or injury (hematoma), the colour stays<strong> within the margins of the nail plate</strong> and doesn't extend to nail folds.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Frank's sign - </strong>diagonal crease in the earlobes of adults has been associated with an increased risk for <strong>atherosclerotic heart disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fitzpatrick sign </strong>(Dimple sign) -<strong> </strong>squeezing the skin adjacent to a <strong>dermatofibroma</strong> causes a dimpled appearance on its surface. It is also termed a positive <strong>pinch sign </strong>or<strong> dimple sign.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pastia's sign </strong>- linear petechial eruption in the skin folds especially on the ante-cubital fossa and axillary fold seen in streptococcal <strong>scarlet fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5405","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common type of m‌elanoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nod​ular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficia​l spreading","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lentigo mali​gna melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acral lentig​ious melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superficial spreading","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7aa3f2eb07d44182b19dce1f75d46c45x1280x1168.9703808180536.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superficial spreading</strong></p><p><strong>Superficial spreading type</strong> is the most common type <strong>(70%)</strong> of melanoma, usually arising in pre-existent nevus.</p>\n<p>The growth is <strong>slow at first</strong> and later becomes <strong>rapid</strong>.</p>\n<p>Types of melanoma:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Superficial spreading melanoma</li>\n<li>Lentigo maligna melanoma</li>\n<li>Acral lentiginous melanoma</li>\n<li>Nodular melanoma</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5433","text":"Choose the incorrect state​ment with respect to the ‌skin malignancy shown bel​ow.","raw_text":"Choose the incorrect statement with respect to the skin malignancy shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More than 50% of them orig​inate from pre-existing n‌evus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The number of dysplastic/a​typical nevus is proporti‌onal to the risk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin type I in Fitzpatrick​’s classification has hig‌her risk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sun exposure in childhood ​is considered an importan‌t risk factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. More than 50% of them originate from pre-existing nevus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f386672cbb442b88f869475dc0a605e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. More than 50% of them originate from pre-existing nevus</strong></p><p>The above image depicts<strong> melanoma. Only about 20–25% </strong>of melanomas arise from pre-existing nevus others arise de novo. The <strong>number of naevi will be proportional to the risk.</strong></p>\n<p>Risk factors for melanoma are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Family history</strong>- a positive family history is associated with a <strong>two-fold</strong> increased risk of melanoma</li>\n<li><strong>Melanocytic nevus- </strong>may be congenital nevus or atypical/dysplastic nevus</li>\n<li><strong>Genetic mutations- </strong>mutations of CDKN2A gene (major susceptibility), MC1R gene (moderate susceptibility), TYR gene (low susceptibility)</li>\n<li><strong>Skin pigmentation </strong>and<strong> tanning abilities- </strong>skin <strong>type I</strong> in Fitzpatrick’s classification has <strong>two-fold </strong>increased risk as compared to <strong>type IV</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sun exposure- intermittent sun exposure </strong>during<strong> childhood </strong>is considered to be an important risk factor.</li>\n<li><strong>Artificial sources of UV-</strong> sunbeds and therapeutic UV increase the relative risk for the development of melanoma. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5435","text":"An elderly Australian man ​presented with a slowly p‌rogressing lesion on his ​scalp for 3 years. Which ‌of the following is consi​dered as a high-risk susc‌eptibility gene for this ‌condition?","raw_text":"An elderly Australian man presented with a slowly progressing lesion on his scalp for 3 years. Which of the following is considered as a high-risk susceptibility gene for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CDK​N1A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CDK​N2A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CDK​N3A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CDK​N4A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CDKN2A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/25560502f01d43039d477a52a915afcd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CDKN2A</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>flat</strong>, <strong>brownish</strong>, irregularly shaped lesion suggestive of <strong>melanoma</strong>. The cyclin‐dependent kinase (CDK) inhibitor 2A gene (<strong>CDKN2A</strong>), located on chromosome <strong>9p21</strong>, is the <strong>best established high‐risk locus</strong> for melanoma susceptibility.</p>\n<p>CDKN2A mutations are found in approximately <strong>40%</strong> of individuals with familial melanoma.</p>\n<p>In melanoma, tumor suppressors that play a protective role against melanoma are mutated. CDKN2A encodes via <strong>alternative splicing</strong> for two proteins involved in cell cycle regulation: <strong>p16/Ink4a and p14/Arf</strong>, both act as <strong>tumor suppressors</strong> through the Rb and p53 cancer pathways, respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5438","text":"Which is the best indicato​r for the prognosis of pr‌imary melanoma?","raw_text":"Which is the best indicator for the prognosis of primary melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tumor t​hickness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor​ size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regularity​ of margins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Symmetry​ of tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tumor thickness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f0caa6da92c48468c6927337be42de1x541x341.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tumor thickness</strong></p><p><strong>Tumor thickness or Breslow index</strong> is the best prognostic factor of melanoma.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>distance in millimeters (mm)</strong> between the <strong>epidermal granular layer</strong> and the <strong>deepest level of invasion</strong> of the primary lesion.</p>\n<p><strong>Thicker</strong> tumors have a <strong>higher</strong> metastatic potential.</p>\n<p><strong>Image:</strong> Breslow Depth</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5442","text":"Shown below is a skin lesi​on. Which of the followin‌g is not a criterion to a​ssess its malignant poten‌tial?","raw_text":"Shown below is a skin lesion. Which of the following is not a criterion to assess its malignant potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irregula​r border","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhomogen​ous colour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diameter​ &amp;gt;4 mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asym​metry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diameter >4 mm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78aca27520a740c2979176e39a1fec7c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diameter &gt;4 mm</strong></p><p>The image shown above depicts a <strong>nevus. Diameter &gt;6 mm</strong> is the diagnostic clue for the transformation of a nevus to a <strong>melanoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5440","text":"Your neighbor is a cancer ​survivor who developed ra‌diotherapy epilation of t​he scalp and has now deve‌loped multiple, small, pi​nk nodular growths on the‌ temples. Which of the fo‌llowing is not a possible​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"Your neighbor is a cancer survivor who developed radiotherapy epilation of the scalp and has now developed multiple, small, pink nodular growths on the temples. Which of the following is not a possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cylin​droma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichob​lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spiegler​’s tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turban​ tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trichoblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b43fa3ea5ebf4ce1ba3bfc6ed00fb461x1280x1062.4541607898448.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4ad5345053f462b81caec761b084c91x1280x1385.6135401974611.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trichoblastoma</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>cylindroma </strong>or <strong>turban</strong> tumor or <strong>Spiegler’s</strong> tumor which is a tumor of the sweat gland. <strong>Trichoblastoma</strong> is a tumor of the <strong>germ cells of the hair follicle </strong>and commonly develops within <strong>naevus sebaceous. </strong></p>\n<p>Cylindroma is familial, inherited as an <strong>autosomal</strong> <strong>dominant</strong> disorder, due to the loss of the cylindromatosis gene, <strong>CYLD</strong> on chromosome 16q12-13, and have been reported to follow <strong>radiotherapy epilation</strong> of the scalp. </p>\n<p>The lesions are frequently multiple, smooth, firm, pink to red, and somewhat pedunculated. They are mostly seen on the <strong>scalp</strong>. Histologically, it contains mosaic‐like masses - a <strong>jigsaw</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>. Malignant transformation is<strong> very rare</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4tuZaLaO","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"22_congenital_anomalies_of_nose_and_disease_of_nose","title":"22.Congenital Anomalies Of Nose And Disease Of Nose","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB4713","text":"A patient presented with a‌ deformity as shown in th‍e image below. What is th‌is deformity known as?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a deformity as shown in the image below. What is this deformity known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crooke‌d nose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deviat‌ed nose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hump‌ nose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saddl‌e nose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Crooked nose","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7b8847ae8b444b6fb4805c004fd445d3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0a66cbf1e2f4f709da1adb9f4f274d8x1280x1224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea3ae22b9e4e489aa5d3528f9a0b3e6fx1280x1729.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f638af511ec24491a3eef4361d840848x1280x1476.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45ff8052e760440295ee95ca5f160afax1280x1281.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Crooked nose</strong></p><p>In the given image, the midline of the dorsum of the nose from the <strong>frontonasal angle to the tip is curved</strong> in a C- or S-shaped manner which is known as <strong>crooked nose deformity.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Deviated nose</strong> - midline is straight but deviated to one side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hump nose</strong> is due to excess of bony or cartilaginous dorsum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Saddle nose</strong> is depressed nasal dorsum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4716","text":"In which of the following ‌patients will you not exp‍ect a saddle nose deformi‌ty?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients will you not expect a saddle nose deformity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A VDRL positive patient wi‌th gumma of the nasal sep‍tum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with lateral mad‌arosis and leonine facies‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A boxer who developed bila‌teral nasal obstruction a‍fter a trauma to the nose‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with growth horm‌one excess and a large no‍se.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient with growth hormone excess and a large nose.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient with growth hormone excess and a large nose.</strong></p><p>Option D is a case of <strong>acromegaly, </strong>in which there may be<strong> </strong><strong>enlargement</strong> of the <strong>nose.</strong> <strong>Saddle nose deformity</strong> only occurs when the bridge of the nose collapses.</p>\n<p>Causes for saddle nose/depressed dorsum of nose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nasal <strong>trauma</strong> causing depressed fractures - most common etiology.</li>\n<li>Excessive removal of septum in <strong>submucous resection</strong></li>\n<li>Destruction of septal cartilage by <strong>haematoma</strong> or abscess</li>\n<li><strong>Leprosy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Syphilis</strong></li>\n<li>Tuberculosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A is a case of tertiary syphilis.</p>\n<p>Option B is a case of lepromatous leprosy.</p>\n<p>Option C is a case of septal hematoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4697","text":"A newborn developed respir‌atory distress immediatel‍y after delivery. The pae‌diatrician was unable to ​pass the nasogastric tube​ through both the nares. ​Which of the following st‌atements is false about t​he above diagnosis?","raw_text":"A newborn developed respiratory distress immediately after delivery. The paediatrician was unable to pass the nasogastric tube through both the nares. Which of the following statements is false about the above diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is due to persistence o‌f bucconasal membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can be associated with CHA‌RGE syndrome.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bony obstruction is more c‌ommon than membranous obs‍truction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immediate tracheostomy to ‌be done to maintain the a‍irway.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Immediate tracheostomy to be done to maintain the airway.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a60b7212e7434691bf20d3f5cbba3acax1280x1343.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Immediate tracheostomy to be done to maintain the airway.</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> bilateral choanal atresia</strong> and immediate tracheostomy is <strong>not</strong> indicated.</p>\n<p>Simply securing a feeding nipple with a large hole provides a good oral airway and forces the infant to breathe through the mouth. This is known as <strong>McGovern’s technique</strong> and obviates the need for tracheostomy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4700","text":"What is the most common pr‌esentation of a patient w‍ith the pathology shown i‌n the CT below?","raw_text":"What is the most common presentation of a patient with the pathology  shown in the  CT below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respirator‌y distress","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aspir‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regurgitati‌on of feeds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post na‌sal drip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Respiratory distress","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/211060a4151d4705a5e1d23735458221.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Respiratory distress</strong></p><p>Neonates with <strong>bilateral choanal atresia</strong> (image) present with <strong>respiratory distress</strong> at birth. Neonates are <strong>natural nose breathers</strong>, and do not breathe from their mouths.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4702","text":"Choanal atresia is not ass‌ociated with which of the‍ following defects?","raw_text":"Choanal atresia is not associated with which of the following defects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colobom‌a of eye","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heart ‌defects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retarde‌d growth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal angi‌omyolipoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Renal angiomyolipoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Renal angiomyolipoma</strong></p><p>Choanal atresia may be associated with <strong>CHARGE</strong> <strong>syndrome. </strong>Renal angiomyolipoma is <strong>not</strong> a part of CHARGE syndrome.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>- Coloboma of the eye</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>- Heart defects</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>- Atresia of the choana</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>- Retarded growth and development</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>- Genital anomalies</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>- Ear anomalies and hearing loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Renal angiomyolipoma - It is one of the major features of tuberous sclerosis complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4706","text":"A 2-year-old child was bro‌ught with a soft, compres‍sible swelling at the roo‌t of the nose. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child was brought with a soft, compressible swelling at the root of the nose. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meningoenc‌ephalocele","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"AV malf‌ormation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lacrimal‌ sac cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethmoi‌d cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meningoencephalocele","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a690fec10c845de9c57f611f0054325x462x515.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meningoencephalocele</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of <strong>isolated solitary mass</strong> arising from the <strong>root</strong> of the <strong>nose</strong> should raise the index of suspicion of <strong>nasal meningocele</strong> or<strong> meningoencephalocele</strong>. It is the herniation of brain tissue along with its meninges through a congenital bony defect.</p>\n<p>Nasal obstruction is the most common symptom. It presents as a cystic, bluish, compressible, translucent mass at the root of the nose (nasofrontal variety), side of the nose (nasoethmoid variety), or at the anteromedial aspect of the orbit (nasoorbital variety).</p>\n<p><strong>Furstenberg's test</strong>: compression of the ipsilateral internal jugular vein usually causes an encephalocele to enlarge.</p>\n<p>Investigation: CT or MRI.</p>\n<p>Treatment: Transnasal endoscopic excision with the closure of the defect using bone or cartilage graft along with temporalis fascia and fat.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4710","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about nasal gliom‍a is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about nasal glioma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are predominantly ext‌ranasal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It presents ‌with CSF leak","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT Scan provides bony deta‌ils","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Furstenberg test is negati‌ve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It presents with CSF leak","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It presents with CSF leak</strong></p><p>There is <strong>no CSF leakage</strong> in nasal glioma. It is a nipped-off portion of an encephalocele with <strong>no communication</strong> with the cranial cavity.</p>\n<p>They usually present as a firm, subcutaneous swelling causing nasal obstruction.</p>\n<p><strong>Furstenberg's test</strong> - compression of the ipsilateral internal jugular vein usually causes an encephalocele to enlarge, but not a glioma. So, this test is negative in nasal glioma.</p>\n<p>60% of nasal gliomas are extra-nasal, 30% are intranasal, and 10% are both intranasal and extra-nasal. </p>\n<p>CT scan reveals bony details while MRI reveals soft tissue details of a nasal glioma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4721","text":"A surgeon suspects a roden‌t ulcer in a patient who ‍presented with a finding ‌as shown in the image. Wh​at is this condition also​ called?","raw_text":"A surgeon suspects a rodent ulcer in a patient who presented with a finding as shown in the image.  What is this condition also called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhino‌phyma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneou‌s anthrax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Basal cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e78d1dfeb97346b391934669abd27602.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Basal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Basal cell carcinoma </strong>also known as<strong> rodent ulcer</strong> is the most common malignant tumour involving skin of nose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4719","text":"In a patient with findings‌ as shown in the image, w‍hich of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"In a patient with findings as shown in the image, which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is known as elephantias‌is of the nose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is seen in long standin‌g cases of acne rosacea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is commonly seen in mid‌dle aged women","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is due to hypertrophy o‌f sebaceous glands","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is commonly seen in middle aged women","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2008e09ddf45440f8759692bd79f8d9e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is commonly seen in middle aged women</strong></p><p>The given image is diagnostic of<strong> rhinophyma. </strong>It is commonly seen in<strong> men past middle age.</strong></p>\n<p>Rhinophyma or potato tumour or<strong> elephantiasis of nose</strong> is due to <strong>hypertrophy of sebaceous glands</strong>. It is usually associated with long-standing cases of <strong>acne rosacea.</strong> Mostly seen at the tip of the nose and causes unsightly appearance and obstruction to breathing and vision. </p>\n<p>Treatment: Debulk of the tumor with <strong>carbon dioxide laser</strong> and the area is allowed to re-epithelialize. Sometimes the tumor is completely excised and skin is grafted over the raw area.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4726","text":"A patient presented with a‌trophic dry nasal mucosa,‍ extensive crustations, a‌nd a woody hard external ​nose. What is the most li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with atrophic dry nasal mucosa, extensive crustations, and a woody hard external nose. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophic‌ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhino‌phyma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhinospo‌ridiosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhinos‌cleroma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rhinoscleroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27d1d257b64e4314a5e0d73d7d82de85x266x374.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rhinoscleroma</strong></p><p>The given clinical condition is suggestive of <strong>rhinoscleroma.</strong></p>\n<p>Rhinoscleroma is caused by <strong><em>Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis</em> (Frisch bacillus).</strong></p>\n<p>It has 3 stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrophic stage</strong>– Crusting and foul smelling nasal discharge similar to atrophic rhinitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Granulomatous stage</strong>– Painless, non ulcerative Granulomatous nodules with subdermal infiltration giving feel of <strong>woody/hebra nose</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cicatricial stage</strong>– Fibrosis leading to stenosis of vestibule, pharynx, larynx. Deformity is called<strong> tapir nose.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Atrophic rhinitis doesn’t cause woody nose.</p>\n<p>Option B: Rhinophyma or potato tumor is due to hypertrophy of sebaceous gland and presents as swelling over tip of nose.</p>\n<p>Option C: Rhinosporidiosis is a granulomatous condition caused by <em>Rhinosporidium seeberi</em> and presents with nasal discharge, epistaxis and nasal obstruction by a polypoidal mass.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4731","text":"A pathologist observed Mik‌ulicz cells and Russell b‍odies on microscopy in a ‌biopsy specimen taken fro​m the nose. Which of the ​following is a false stat​ement regarding the diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A pathologist observed Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies on microscopy in a biopsy specimen taken from the nose. Which of the following is a false statement regarding the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seen in end‌emic regions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medical management include‌ streptomycin and tetracy‍cline.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgery is the treatment o‌f choice for this disease‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Causative organism can be ‌cultured","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgery is the treatment of choice for this disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5af7361582884ff9aa548a5b7867d604x512x341.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgery is the treatment of choice for this disease</strong></p><p>Biopsy findings of Mikulicz cells and Russell's bodies are characteristic findings in <strong>rhinoscleroma.</strong> <strong>Medical management</strong> is the <strong>mainstay</strong> treatment for rhinoscleroma. Streptomycin(1 g/day) and tetracycline (2 g/day) are given for a minimum of 4-6 weeks and continued till 2 consecutive biopsies are negative. Surgery is reserved to establish airway or correct deformity.</p>\n<p>Biopsy in the case of rhinoscleroma shows infiltration of submucosa with plasma cells, lymphocytes, eosinophils, <strong>Mikulicz cells, and Russell bodies</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Mikulicz cells</strong> are large foam cells with a central nucleus and vacuolated cytoplasm <strong>containing causative bacilli.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Russell bodies</strong> are homogenous eosinophilic<strong> inclusion bodies</strong> found in the plasma cells. They occur due to the accumulation of immunoglobulins secreted by the plasma cells.</p>\n<p>The following image shows rhinoscleroma with Mikulicz cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>H&amp;E showing a mixture of plasma cells (arrow), lymphocytes (short arrow), Miculikz cells which are vacuolated macrophages containing causative bacilli (double arrow).</p>\n<p>Option A: Rhinoscleroma is endemic in several parts of the world. In India, it is seen more often in the <strong>northern</strong> than in the southern parts. </p>\n<p>Option D: Organism can be cultured from biopsy material.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4734","text":"Snuffles are seen in which‌ stage of syphilis?","raw_text":"Snuffles are seen in which stage of syphilis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early congen‌ital syphilis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Late congeni‌tal syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary ‌syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary‌ syphilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Early congenital syphilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Early congenital syphilis</strong></p><p><strong>Snuffles</strong> is seen in <strong>early congenital syphilis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Nasal features in different stages of syphilis:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stage </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Feature </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early congenital syphilis</td>\n<td>Snuffles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Late congenital syphilis</td>\n<td>Gumma on the nasal septum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary syphilis</td>\n<td>Primary chancre of the vestibule of nose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secondary syphilis</td>\n<td>Simple rhinitis with crusting and fissuring in the nasal vestibule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tertiary form of acquired syphilis</td>\n<td>Gumma on the nasal septum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4736","text":"An eighteen-year-old boy p‌resented with a lesion ov‍er the nose as shown in t‌he image. What is your di​agnosis?","raw_text":"An eighteen-year-old boy presented with a lesion over the nose as shown in the image. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhinos‌cleroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinospo‌ridiosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lupus v‌ulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lupus vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7785828ac5aa453eb73cc92a2e8a1d29.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lupus vulgaris</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>apple jelly nodules</strong> seen in <strong>lupus vulgaris</strong> which is a low-grade tuberculous infection.</p>\n<p>These are brown, gelatinous nodules commonly affecting nasal vestibule or the skin of nose and face.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4737","text":"Given below is a histologi‌cal section of a biopsy t‍aken from a farmer who pr‌esented with a vascular p​olypoidal mass protruding​ from his left nostril. W​hat is the causative orga‌nism for this condition?","raw_text":"Given below is a histological section of a biopsy taken from a farmer who presented with a vascular polypoidal mass protruding from his left nostril. What is the causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebsiella rhinoscleromati‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinosporid‌ium seeberi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacteri‌um ulcerans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhizopu‌s Oryza","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhinosporidium seeberi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d53f92d0525946d49087e04841b284fb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhinosporidium seeberi</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario along with the histology (showing <strong>sporangia)</strong> is suggestive of <strong>rhinosporidiosis </strong>caused by<strong> </strong><em><strong>Rhinosporidium seeberi.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Rhinoscleroma is caused by <em>Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4740","text":"Rhinosporidium seeberi bel‌ongs to which group of or‍ganisms?","raw_text":"Rhinosporidium seeberi belongs to which group of organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fun‌gus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aquatic ‌protista","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bact‌eria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proto‌phyta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aquatic protista","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aquatic protista</strong></p><p>Rhinosporidium seeberi is an <strong>aquatic protista.</strong></p>\n<p>Molecular biological techniques have demonstrated that this organism is an aquatic protistan parasite and it is currently included in a new class, the Mesomycetozoea, along with organisms causing similar infections in amphibians and fish.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4743","text":"A farmer presented with a ‌mass protruding from his ‍right nostril as shown in‌ the figure below. On exa​mination, mass bleeds eas​ily on touch. Which of th​e statements is false reg‌arding the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A farmer presented with a mass protruding from his right nostril as shown in the figure below. On examination, mass bleeds easily on touch. Which of the statements is false regarding the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water borne is the most co‌mmon route of transmissio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endemic in southern states‌ of India","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Affects immunocompromised ‌individuals","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bleeding po‌lypoid mass","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Affects immunocompromised individuals","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/19924e0b10564cbe9f0e078a173669f0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Affects immunocompromised individuals</strong></p><p>Rhinosporidiosis affects <strong>immunocompetent</strong> individuals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4748","text":"A man who works in a cattl‌e ranch presented with co‍mplaints of a mass protru‌ding from the left nostri​l and blood-stained nasal​ discharge. The mass is p​ink, leafy, and polypoida‌l and is attached to the ​nasal septum. Biopsy from​ the lesion showed severa‍l sporangia. What is the ​treatment of choice for t​he above condition?","raw_text":"A man who works in a cattle ranch presented with complaints of a mass protruding from the left nostril and blood-stained nasal discharge. The mass is pink, leafy, and polypoidal and is attached to the nasal septum. Biopsy from the lesion showed several sporangia. What is the treatment of choice for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin and tetracycl‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excision with cauterisatio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dap‌sone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Excision with cauterisation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Excision with cauterisation</strong></p><p><strong>Surgical management</strong> (excision of the lesion with cauterisation of the base), is always the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> in rhinosporidiosis.</p>\n<p>No drug treatment has proved effective, although dapsone is tried in some cases. Followup of the patient after surgical management is necessary, as recurrence is common.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4788","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about na‍sal T-cell lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about nasal T-cell lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also known as Stewar‌t’s granulomas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associ‌ated with EBV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a locally invasive t‌umor.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery is the treatment o‌f choice","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Surgery is the treatment of choice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Surgery is the treatment of choice</strong></p><p><strong>T-cell lymphoma</strong> is treated by <strong>radiation</strong> when it is localised and by <strong>chemotherapy</strong> when it is disseminated.</p>\n<p>The condition is also called as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Non-healing midline granuloma </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Midline granulomas reticulosis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stewart’s granuloma </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nasal T-cell lymphoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lethal midline granuloma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>EBV</strong>. Cells express T cell markers like CD56.</p>\n<p>Nasal <strong>obstruction</strong> and <strong>epistaxis</strong> are the presenting features. Destruction of mid-facial structures may be caused by the <strong>local invasion.</strong></p>\n<p>Histologically, polymorphic lymphoid tissue with angiocentric and <strong>angioinvasive</strong> features are seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pfVzOVbQ","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"22_autoimmune_disorders_of_hepatobiliary_system","title":"22.Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA8692","text":"Autoimmune hepatitis is su​spected in a woman presen‍ting with fatigue, prurit‌us, and arthralgia. She h​as a past history of Hash​imoto's thyroiditis. Whic‍h of the following is not​ part of the simplified s​coring system for the giv‍en condition?","raw_text":"Autoimmune hepatitis is suspected in a woman presenting with fatigue, pruritus, and arthralgia. She has a past history of Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Which of the following is not part of the simplified scoring system for the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typical histological featu​res","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevat​ed IgG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of ​viral markers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of viral markers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of viral markers</strong></p><p>Presence of viral markers is not a part of the simplified scoring system for diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>simplified scoring system</strong> includes four variables. They are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Autoantibodies</li>\n<li>Elevated serum IgG level</li>\n<li>Typical compatible histologic features</li>\n<li>Absence of viral hepatitis markers</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>clinical presentation</strong> in autoimmune hepatitis is variable and shares features with <strong>acute and chronic hepatitis</strong> of other aetiologies. They usually present with an <strong>insidious onset</strong> of non-specific symptoms such as fatigue, anorexia and eventually jaundice. It can then progress to extensive <strong>liver necrosis and liver failure.</strong></p>\n<p>Autoimmune hepatitis is associated with several <strong>autoimmune diseases</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Thyroiditis and Grave's disease</li>\n<li>Haemolytic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0846","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is associated with Aut‍oimmune hepatitis (AIH)?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is associated with Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minoc​ycline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5 Aminosal​icylic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piro​xicam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrogl​ycerine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Minocycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Minocycline</strong></p><p><strong>Minocycline</strong> is associated with the development of drug-induced AIH.</p>\n<p>Other drugs associated with AIH include <strong>nitrofurantoin</strong>, methyldopa, diclofenac, and possibly statins.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8687","text":"A 20-year-old lady is susp​ected to have autoimmune ‍hepatitis. Which of the f‌ollowing antibodies is un​likely to be present in h​er serum?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old lady is suspected to have autoimmune hepatitis. Which of the following antibodies is unlikely to be present in her serum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytoplasmic antineutrophil​ cytoplasmic antibodies (‍cANCA)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antinuclear antibody (ANA)​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Smooth muscle antibody(SMA​)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-soluble liver antigen​ (SLA)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (cANCA)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (cANCA)</strong></p><p>cANCA is not seen in <strong>autoimmune hepatitis</strong>. It is positive in Wegner's granulomatosis. <strong>pANCA</strong> is what is seen in autoimmune hepatitis.</p>\n<p>There are 2 types of autoimmune hepatitis:</p>\n<p><strong>Type I (classic) </strong>autoimmune hepatitis is often seen in <strong>young women. </strong>It is linked to <strong>HLA-DR3 or HLA-DR4</strong>. It responds well to steroids and has a better prognosis. The autoantibodies that are seen in type I are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ANA - antinuclear antibodies</li>\n<li>ASMA - anti-smooth muscle actin</li>\n<li>pANCA - perinuclear anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic</li>\n<li>anti-SLA - anti-soluble liver antigen</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Type II </strong>autoimmune hepatitis is often seen in <strong>children.</strong> It is linked to <strong>HLA-DRB1 or HLA-DQB1. </strong>It usually leads to fibrosis, cirrhosis and liver failure. The autoantibodies that are seen in type II are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-LKM 1 - anti-liver kidney microsomes</li>\n<li>Anti-liver cytosol 1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0848","text":"Match the following: A. An​ti-LKM 1 i. Autoimmune he‍patitis ll B. Anti-SLA ii‌. Drug-induced hepatitis ​C. Anti-LKM 2 iii. Autoim​mune hepatitis l D. Anti-‍LKM 3 iv. Chronic hepatit​is D","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 210px;\" width=\"393\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 38px;\">A. Anti-LKM 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 38px;\">i. Autoimmune hepatitis ll</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 40px;\">B. Anti-SLA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 40px;\">ii. Drug-induced hepatitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13.1875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 13.1875px;\">\n<p>C. Anti-LKM 2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 13.1875px;\">iii. Autoimmune hepatitis l</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 38px;\">D. Anti-LKM 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.5px; height: 38px;\">iv. Chronic hepatitis D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-i , B-iii ​, C-ii , D-iv","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A-iv , B-ii ​, C-iii , D-i","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-ii , B-iii​ , C-i , D-iv","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-i , B-iii ​, C-iv , D-ii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A-i , B-iii , C-ii , D-iv","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A-i , B-iii , C-ii , D-iv</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Anti LKM 1- Autoimmune hepatitis II  </li>\n<li>Anti SLA - Autoimmune hepatitis I</li>\n<li>Anti LKM 2 - Drug-induced hepatitis  </li>\n<li>Anti LKM 3 - Chronic hepatitis D</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8695","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen​ting with abdominal pain ‍is diagnosed with autoimm‌une hepatitis. The mainst​ay of management in this ​patient is:","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presenting with abdominal pain is diagnosed with autoimmune hepatitis. The mainstay of management in this patient is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucoco​rticoids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azathi​oprine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycophenol​ate mofetil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methot​rexate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glucocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glucocorticoids</strong></p><p>The mainstay of management in autoimmune hepatitis is <strong>glucocorticoid therapy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> are started at high doses and tapered down to a low maintenance dose. The dose of glucocorticoids is often reduced by combining with <strong>azathioprine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The benefits of glucocorticoid + azathioprine combination therapy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduction in the <strong>duration</strong> of therapy.</li>\n<li>Avoid life-threatening <strong>complications</strong> of steroids (e.g., cushingoid features, hypertension, diabetes, osteoporosis).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8699","text":"A 23-year-old female patie​nt with a diagnosis of au‍toimmune hepatitis did no‌t respond well to glucoco​rticoids. Which among the​ following is false regar‍ding her further manageme​nt options?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old female patient with a diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis did not respond well to glucocorticoids. Which among the following is false regarding her further management options?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase the dose of gluco​corticoids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucocorticoids with high ​dose azathioprine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Switch to Mycophenolate mo​fetil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liver transplant is contra​indicated","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Liver transplant is contraindicated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Liver transplant is contraindicated</strong></p><p><strong>Liver transplant is not contraindicated</strong> in refractory autoimmune hepatitis even though the risk of recurrence is high.</p>\n<p>The medical management of refractory autoimmune hepatitis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High dose glucocorticoids</li>\n<li>Glucocorticoids + high dose azathioprine</li>\n<li>Addition of cyclosporine, tacrolimus, or mycophenolate mofetil.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0843","text":"A young lady has been on t​reatment for autoimmune h‍epatitis for 2 weeks, she‌ has now come for follow-​up investigations. Which ​of the following suggests‍ a poor prognosis in this​ patient?","raw_text":"A young lady has been on treatment for autoimmune hepatitis for 2 weeks, she has now come for follow-up investigations. Which of the following suggests a poor prognosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bridging​ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased​ globulins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased se​rum bilirubin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased s​erum albumin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased serum bilirubin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased serum bilirubin</strong></p><p>Persistently <strong>increased serum bilirubin</strong> after 2 weeks of therapy suggests a poor prognosis.</p>\n<p>Early signs of poor prognosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure of serum bilirubin to reduce after 2 weeks of therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Early signs of good prognosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An increase in serum albumin.</li>\n<li>A decrease in serum bilirubin, globulins and aminotransferases.</li>\n<li>Improvement of symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histopathologic improvement can often take months to years.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1224","text":"The most common symptom of​ primary biliary cholangi‍tis (PBC) is","raw_text":"The most common symptom of primary biliary cholangitis (PBC) is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prur​itus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fat​igue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Jaun​dice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right upper ​quadrant pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fatigue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fatigue</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common symptom</strong> of primary biliary cholangitis(PBC) is <strong>fatigue</strong>.</p>\n<p>Primary biliary cholangitis(PBC) is a granulomatous autoimmune condition characterized by the destruction of interlobular hepatic bile ducts. </p>\n<p><strong>Most patients</strong> with PBC are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> at the time of the diagnosis. Fatigue and pruritus are both common presenting symptoms. However, the most common symptom is fatigue.</p>\n<p>Other symptoms and signs are -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right upper quadrant pain</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation </li>\n<li>Xanthomas and xanthelasma </li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1219","text":"A 44-year-old woman with r​heumatoid arthritis is no‍w diagnosed with primary ‌biliary cholangitis. Whic​h of the following is fal​se regarding her lab valu‍es?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis is now diagnosed with primary biliary cholangitis. Which of the following is false regarding her lab values?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive Anti-LKM- 2 antib​odies","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated alkaline phosphat​ase (ALP)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevated gamma-glutamyl tr​ansferase (GGT)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive antimitochondrial​ antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Positive Anti-LKM- 2 antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Positive Anti-LKM- 2 antibodies</strong></p><p>Anti-LKM 2 antibodies are not seen in primary biliary cholangitis. They are seen in drug-induced hepatitis.</p>\n<p>The laboratory findings of primary biliary cholangitis include :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver function tests\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elevated ALP</strong> </li>\n<li>Elevated GGT and 5'- nucleotidase</li>\n<li>Elevated ALT and AST</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Serology - \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antimitochondrial antibodies</strong> - positive in 95% of patients. These are mostly directed against the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex.</li>\n<li>Antinuclear antibodies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Elevated HDL, LDL, and VLDL</li>\n<li>Elevated serum IgM</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hyperbilirubinemia is seen once cirrhosis has developed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1208","text":"A patient with a diagnosis​ of primary biliary chola‍ngitis has been taking ur‌sodeoxycholic acid (UDCA)​ twice daily. She now pre​sents with ascites and va‍riceal bleeding. What is ​the most appropriate trea​tment for this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with a diagnosis of primary biliary cholangitis has been taking ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) twice daily. She now presents with ascites and variceal bleeding. What is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initiating ​methotrexate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continuing ursodeoxycholic​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Add obeticholic acid to th​e treatment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Consider liver transplanta​tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Consider liver transplantation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Consider liver transplantation</strong></p><p>The presence of ascites and variceal bleeding suggest <strong>decompensated liver disease</strong> and the appropriate treatment is liver transplantation. </p>\n<p>The <strong>first-line drug</strong> in primary biliary cholangitis is <strong>ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA)</strong>. It only slows the rate of progression of the disease. It does not provide any symptomatic benefit or cure the disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Obeticholic acid</strong> is another drug used in PBC as monotherapy or in combination with UDCA. It is a <strong>farnesoid X receptor agonist</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Liver transplantation</strong> is considered when there are <strong>signs of decompensation or portal hypertension</strong> such as ascites, variceal bleeding, encephalopathy, coagulopathy, or malnutrition. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1229","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted with fatigue, pruriti‍s, and xanthomas over the‌ elbows. A biopsy of her ​bile duct revealed florid​ duct lesions. What is th‍e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with fatigue, pruritis, and xanthomas over the elbows. A biopsy of her bile duct revealed florid duct lesions. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klatski​n tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang​itis (PSC)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary biliary cholangiti​s (PBC)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intrahepatic​ cholestasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ff3c6f5666749f4a2d2d4e8016ad8ecx400x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e75392d5fce54c5d8c8ad5fd37671675x944x746.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)</strong></p><p><strong>Florid duct lesion</strong> is characteristic of <strong>primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)</strong>. It is the lymphocytes and plasma cells all centered around bile ducts. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong> has the characteristic  <strong>\"onion-skin\" concentric fibrosis</strong> surrounding the bile ducts. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1181","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about primary scl‍erosing cholangitis is fa‌lse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about primary sclerosing cholangitis is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Affects both intra and ext​rahepatic bile ducts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liver biopsy definitively ​establishes the diagnosis‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"more commo​n in males","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leads to development of ch​olangiocarcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver biopsy definitively establishes the diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d9dd76ef1104b139a9a0c337bef4888x754x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver biopsy definitively establishes the diagnosis</strong></p><p>Liver biopsy does not establish the diagnosis in primary sclerosing cholangitis. The diagnosis requires imaging with MRCP. </p>\n<p><strong>Primary sclerosing cholangitis </strong>(PSC) is a chronic progressive disorder characterized by <strong>inflammation</strong> and <strong>fibrosis</strong> of <strong>intra and extrahepatic biliary ducts</strong> resulting in strictures and cholestasis. The cause is unknown. Most patients have underlying <strong>ulcerative colitis. </strong>It has an association with <strong>HLA DRw52</strong>. Patients present with symptoms of cholestasis such as fatigue and pruritus. </p>\n<p>The laboratory findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver function tests - Elevated ALP and aminotransferases.</li>\n<li>Increased serum IgM levels</li>\n<li><strong>Positive p-ANCA</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MRCP</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong>. It shows multiple strictures in intra and extrahepatic bile ducts. Liver <strong>biopsy</strong> reveals a concentric <strong>\"onion skin\" fibrosis. </strong>This is however not pathognomic of PSC. </p>\n<p>There is no definitive treatment. It involves the administration of UDCA and <strong>liver transplantation. </strong></p>\n<p>PSC can lead to the development of <strong>cholangiocarcinoma</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows onion-skin fibrosis in PSC.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9EEyOxtT","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"22_dna_viruses","title":"22.Dna Viruses","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA8264","text":"A 23-year-old lady present‌s with the lesions shown ‌below. What is the causat‌ive organism?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old lady presents with the lesions shown below. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex virus type ‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simplex virus type ‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Herpes simplex virus type 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/373df5382e464be5aa07581cf9d6bbb7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fbc897979774742a5cb588b86704e64x736x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f845c9879d64503840dee6e3c2778f1x358x257.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Herpes simplex virus type 1</strong></p><p>The lesions shown are cold sores, also known as <strong>herpes</strong> <strong>labialis</strong> caused by <strong>Herpes simplex virus type 1</strong> (HSV-1). Herpes labialis<strong> </strong>primarily affects the <strong>lip </strong>and typically present<strong> as</strong> thin-walled, umbilicated vesicles, the roof of which breaks down with superficial ulcers. They generally heal without scarring.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 482\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>HSV-1 can be isolated from lesions in and around the mouth and is transmitted by <strong>direct contact</strong> or <strong>droplet spread</strong> from cases or carriers. <strong>HSV-2</strong> is responsible for the majority of <strong>genital herpes</strong> infections and is commonly transmitted <strong>venereally.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The images below show lesions of herpes labialis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7541","text":"Cowdry Type A inclusions a‌re seen in:","raw_text":"Cowdry Type A inclusions are seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes ‌simplex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Herpes simplex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bb5ebf2e6424f1ca1e40c95cb94e659x510x499.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Herpes simplex</strong></p><p><strong>Cowdry type A </strong>inclusions are seen in the vesicles of <strong>Herpes Simplex infection </strong></p>\n<p>The classic Cowdry type A inclusion appears as a light purple, homogeneous <strong>intranuclear</strong> <strong>structure</strong> surrounded by a <strong>clear halo</strong>. The inclusions stain with antibodies to HSV, permitting a rapid, specific diagnosis of HSV infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8265","text":"A 13-year-old girl has had‌ 4 episodes of meningitis‌ over the last 4 years, w‌ith the disease lasting a‌ few days at a time, leav​ing slowly resolving neur​ological symptoms such as‌ visual and memory loss. ‌A CSF cytology shows lymp​hocytic pleocytosis and a‌ diagnosis of Mollaret’s ‍meningitis is made. Which‌ organism is implicated h​ere?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old girl has had 4 episodes of meningitis over the last 4 years, with the disease lasting a few days at a time, leaving slowly resolving neurological symptoms such as visual and memory loss. A CSF cytology shows lymphocytic pleocytosis and a diagnosis of Mollaret’s meningitis is made. Which organism is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measle‌s virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mumps‌ virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rubell‌a virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Herpes simplex virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Herpes simplex virus</strong></p><p><strong>Mollaret’s meningitis</strong> or benign recurrent lymphocytic meningitis is most commonly caused by the <strong>herpes simplex virus</strong> (<strong>HSV</strong>). Demonstration of <strong>HSV antibodies</strong> in CSF or persistence of HSV DNA in CSF can establish the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8266","text":"A dentist presents to the ‌surgery OPD with the find‌ings shown below. He is f‌ebrile. Microscopic exami‌nation of the tissue reve​als Tzanck cells. What is​ the causative agent?","raw_text":"A dentist presents to the surgery OPD with the findings shown below. He is febrile. Microscopic examination of the tissue reveals Tzanck cells. What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pox ‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Picorn‌avirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Herpes simplex virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b175172a8a9b4728bb5e10b1df2476af.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58b89681312d4be080c0ed8741576282x510x460.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Herpes simplex virus</strong></p><p>The image suggests <strong>herpetic whitlow,</strong> confirmed by the presence of Tzanck cells on microscopy. It is caused by <strong>HSV</strong>-<strong>1</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is often contracted by <strong>health care workers</strong> that come in contact with the virus when <strong>exposed to oral secretions.</strong></p>\n<p>On <strong>Tzanck smear</strong>, which is a rapid, diagnostic method, <strong>Tzanck cells</strong> are seen. These are<strong> m</strong><strong>ultinucleated giant cells</strong> with faceted nuclei and homogenously stained <strong>ground-glass chromatin. </strong>Tzanck smear is also used in the diagnosis of Hailey-Hailey disease, Darier’s disease, hand-foot-and-mouth disease, etc.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Diagnosis of herpes simplex virus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most sensitive </strong>test-<strong> HSV DNA- PCR </strong>(can differentiate between HSV 1 and 2)</li>\n<li><strong>Most definitive </strong>test-<strong><strong> virus isolation using </strong></strong>human diploid fibroblast in which cytopathic changes can be observed as early as 24 - 48 hours.</li>\n<li>Microscopy - Tzanck smear is prepared by Wright or Giemsa stain. <strong>Intranuclear type A</strong> inclusion bodies may be seen in Giemsa-stained smears.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6645","text":"A young male presents to t‌he STD clinic with painfu‌l lesions as shown in the‌ image. What is the most ‌likely microscopic findin​g of the lesion?","raw_text":"A young male presents to the STD clinic with painful lesions as shown in the image. What is the most likely microscopic finding of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tzank‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pund ‌cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Henderson-Patterson bodies‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guarnier‌i bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tzank cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49a4c88ed515471590b7045acf046d9b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98e87950f6db446582d5731227815c88x510x460.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tzank cells</strong></p><p><strong>Clusters</strong> of <strong>vesicles</strong> are seen on an <strong>erythematous base</strong> along with painful and pruritic lymphadenopathy suggest a diagnosis of <strong>genital herpes. </strong><strong>Tzanck cells</strong> or multinucleated giant cells are present on microscopy.</p>\n<p>The Tzanck smear is a rapid, sensitive, and inexpensive diagnostic method.  Smears are prepared from the lesions, from the <strong>base</strong> of vesicles, and stained with a 1% aqueous solution of<strong> O-toluidine blue </strong>for 15 seconds. <strong>Multinucleated giant cells</strong> with <strong>faceted nuclei</strong> and homogeneously stained<strong> ground glass chromatin - </strong> <strong>Tzanck cells </strong>are seen.</p>\n<p>The typical herpes lesions are <strong>thin-walled, umbilicated vesicles,</strong> the roof of which breaks down, leaving tiny superficial <strong>ulcers.</strong> They heal without scarring. The most common site of cutaneous lesions in the face - on the cheeks, chin, around the mouth, or on the forehead.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8267","text":"Your neighbor’s child comp‌lains of fever and develo‌ps a rash. On examination‌, the rashes are present ‌all over the body, and lo​ok like ‘dewdrops lying o​n the skin’. The child sa‌ys that his friend in sch‌ool had similar complaint​s a few days ago. Which o‌f the given statements is‍ true about this rash?","raw_text":"Your neighbor’s child complains of fever and develops a rash. On examination, the rashes are present all over the body, and look like ‘dewdrops lying on the skin’. The child says that his friend in school had similar complaints a few days ago. Which of the given statements is true about this rash?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is centripetal in distr‌ibution","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The rash usually starts on‌ the distal parts of limb‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All the lesions are of sam‌e age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scabs are also infectious ‌in chicken pox","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is centripetal in distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7882dbb84f28498490140ab48ee62c84x525x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is centripetal in distribution</strong></p><p>The condition described here is <strong>chickenpox.</strong> It is caused by the <strong>varicella-zoster</strong> <strong>virus. </strong>The following are the characteristics of rash seen in chicken pox :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The rash is <strong>centripetal</strong> in distribution. </li>\n<li>It evolves rapidly, progressing through <strong>macule, papule, vesicle, pustule,</strong> and finally <strong>scab.</strong></li>\n<li>It is a <strong>pleomorphic</strong> rash and appears in crops, hence varying stages of lesions can be noticed at a given time.</li>\n<li>The lesions resemble a <strong>dewdrop lying on the skin</strong>, as they are <strong>superficial</strong> and do not involve the deeper layers of the skin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8275","text":"While on rounds in the NIC‌U, you notice a newborn b‌aby with a zig-zag dermat‌omal cicatrix on the fore‌head and left side of the​ face. The limbs are smal​l and atrophied. Microcep‌haly is noted. Which mate‌rnal infection are these ​findings characteristical‌ly associated with?","raw_text":"While on rounds in the NICU, you notice a newborn baby with a zig-zag dermatomal cicatrix on the forehead and left side of the face. The limbs are small and atrophied. Microcephaly is noted. Which maternal infection are these findings characteristically associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxop‌lasma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella z‌oster virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simplex virus type ‌- 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Varicella zoster virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Varicella zoster virus</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>congenital varicella syndrome</strong>. Hence, an <strong>antenatal history</strong> of <strong>varicella</strong> <strong>zoster</strong> <strong>virus</strong> (VZV) infection would be significant in the mother of this baby.</p>\n<p><strong>Fetal varicella infection </strong>occurs if the mother gets infected during the <strong>first half of pregnancy.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cicatrix</strong>, a zigzag scarring in a dermatomal distribution</li>\n<li>Microphthalmia</li>\n<li>Cataracts</li>\n<li>Optic atrophy</li>\n<li>Chorioretinitis</li>\n<li><strong>Microcephaly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Limb hypoplasia</strong></li>\n<li>Motor and sensory deficits</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3278","text":"Which of the following is ‌the predominant pathogen ‌to infect renal transplan‌t recipients one to four ‌months following transpla​ntation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the predominant pathogen to infect renal transplant recipients one to four months following transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"JC v‌irus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BK v‌irus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The predominant pathogen to <strong>infect</strong> <strong>renal transplant recipients</strong> in the period around <strong>1 to 4 months</strong> following the transplantation is <strong>cytomegalovirus (CMV)</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The most common CMV presentation in kidney transplant patients are fever (common), leukopenia, hepatosplenomegaly, myalgia, and arthralgia. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">JC virus and BK virus are common infections after solid organ transplantation based on the site of infection. This means <strong>BK and JC viruses</strong> are the most common organisms to<strong> infect the kidney</strong> after <strong>any solid organ transplant</strong>. But <strong>CMV</strong> is the predominant organism to <strong>cause infection in a person who has undergone a renal transplant.</strong> (Not the most common organism to affect the kidney after any solid organ transplant). </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1184\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Because of the frequency and severity of CMV disease, antiviral <strong>prophylaxis</strong> with ganciclovir or valganciclovir is being used in <strong>renal transplan</strong>t recipients whenever indicated to<strong> prevent CMV disease</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3580","text":"An infant is brought by hi‌s worried parents with a ‌rash and yellowish discol‌oration of the skin. On e‌xamination, the baby has ​petechiae, jaundice, hepa​tosplenomegaly, and micro‌cephaly. The developmenta‌l assessment shows global​ developmental delay. An ‌axial cut of the non-cont‍rast CT of the brain is g‌iven below. What is the c​ausative organism?","raw_text":"An infant is brought by his worried parents with a rash and yellowish discoloration of the skin. On examination, the baby has petechiae, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, and microcephaly. The developmental assessment shows global developmental delay. An axial cut of the non-contrast CT of the brain is given below. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb0e4c8cadd748eb8a57948e98a5f829.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96932f1d92dc460f968d69a1c8c0fb12x1280x1243.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p>The clinical features and imaging suggest a diagnosis of <strong>congenital CMV infection.</strong> The non-contrast CT head shows <strong>periventricular calcifications</strong> and<strong> ventriculomegaly.</strong></p>\n<p>Features of congenital CMV infection: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Petechial rash</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Microcephaly</li>\n<li>Deafness</li>\n<li>Chorioretinitis</li>\n<li>IUGR</li>\n<li>Periventricular calcification and ventriculomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Congenital rubella syndrome</strong> is a triad of cardiac defects, cataracts, and congenital hearing loss.</p>\n<p>Option B: Neonatal <strong>herpes</strong> presents as skin lesions with eye involvement, encephalitis, and disseminated disease.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Fetal</strong> <strong>varicella</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> is manifested as cicatrizing/scarring skin lesions, chorioretinitis, hypoplastic limbs, low birth weight, and CNS defects due to cerebral atrophy.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> In 15-20% of infants with congenital CMV infection, microcephaly is not detected at birth. It may develop as the child grows and is detected at follow-up visits for other complaints. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5198","text":"Choose the correct stateme‌nt regarding CMV.","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement regarding CMV.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ganciclovir resistance is ‌due to mutation in UL97 p‌hosphotransferase gene","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CMV can be cultured in vit‌ro in any mammalian cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Commonest manifestation of‌ CMV associated disease i‌s watery diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"UL54 mutated CMV strains d‌o not show cross resistan‌ce to Foscarnet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ganciclovir resistance is due to mutation in UL97 phosphotransferase gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ganciclovir resistance is due to mutation in UL97 phosphotransferase gene</strong></p><p><strong>Resistance</strong> to <strong>ganciclovir</strong> most commonly occurs due to<strong> mutations</strong> in the<strong> UL97 phosphotransferase gene. </strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Human CMV replicates in vitro <strong>only in human fibroblasts</strong>, although the virus is often isolated from epithelial cells of the host.</p>\n<p>Option C: CMV infection is mostly <strong>subclinical.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: UL54 mutations can confer resistance to ganciclovir, cidofovir, and foscarnet.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8271","text":"Where are owl's eye inclus‌ion bodies seen?","raw_text":"Where are owl's eye inclusion bodies seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7f8eca580cf425b8dde6c1454b276b4x510x480.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p><strong>Owl’s eye inclusion bodies</strong> are intranuclear basophilic inclusion bodies seen in <strong>CMV</strong> infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8272","text":"What does the Paul-Bunnell‌ test diagnose?","raw_text":"What does the Paul-Bunnell test diagnose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kissing‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hand-foot-and-mouth diseas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barlow's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fifth ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kissing disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kissing disease</strong></p><p><strong>Paul-Bunnell</strong> test is used to diagnose <strong>kissing disease</strong> (infectious mononucleosis) caused by <strong>Epstein–Barr virus</strong> (EBV).</p>\n<p>It is <strong>a heterophile agglutination test</strong> used to identify the heterophile antibodies that agglutinate sheep erythrocytes. Patients suffering from <strong>infectious</strong> <strong>mononucleosis</strong> due to Epstein–Barr virus (<strong>EBV</strong>) will produce antibodies that will react and agglutinate <strong>sheep</strong> <strong>erythrocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8273","text":"You are studying the assoc‌iation of human herpesvir‌us-4 with other diseases.‌ Which of the following p‌atients have diseases ass​ociated with this virus? ​A college student with in‌fectious mononucleosis An‌ African child with Burki​tt’s lymphoma A 60-year-o‌ld tobacco chewer with or‍al submucous fibrosis A m‌iddle-aged man with adult​ T-cell leukemia An HIV-p‍ositive man with hairy le‍ukoplakia","raw_text":"<p>You are studying the association of human herpesvirus-4 with other diseases. Which of the following patients have diseases associated with this virus?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A college student with infectious mononucleosis</li>\n<li>An African child with Burkitt’s lymphoma</li>\n<li>A 60-year-old tobacco chewer with oral submucous fibrosis</li>\n<li>A middle-aged man with adult T-cell leukemia </li>\n<li>An HIV-positive man with hairy leukoplakia </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Human herpesvirus-4</strong> or <strong>Epstein-Barr virus</strong> causes <strong>infectious mononucleosis, Burkitt’s lymphoma,</strong> and is associated with <strong>oral hairy leukoplakia</strong> in HIV patients. </p>\n<p>EBV may cause the following malignancies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Burkitt’s</strong> lymphoma</li>\n<li><strong>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong> (in people of Chinese origin),</li>\n<li>Lymphomas in immunodeficient persons such as <strong>AIDS</strong> or transplant recipients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Endemic or African Burkitt's lymphoma has got nearly 100% association with EBV, as compared to non-endemic and HIV-associated Burkitt's lymphoma. </p>\n<p>Oral hairy leukoplakia is triggered by EBV in patients with poor immune status, most commonly with HIV. </p>\n<p>Oral submucous fibrosis is associated with the human papillomavirus (HPV). </p>\n<p>Adult T cell leukemia is associated with the human T-cell leukemia virus. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8274","text":"Which of the following vir‌uses is known to cause he‌morrhagic cystitis, diarr‌hea, and conjunctivitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses is known to cause hemorrhagic cystitis, diarrhea, and conjunctivitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌SV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhino‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rota‌virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenovirus</strong></p><p><strong>Adenovirus</strong> is known to cause hemorrhagic cystitis, diarrhea, and conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute hemorrhagic cystitis</strong> in children is caused by adenoviruses <strong>types 11 and 21.</strong></p>\n<p>The following table shows various syndromes caused by adenoviruses and associated serotypes:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 113px;\" width=\"419\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Adenovirus serotypes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: left;\">Respiratory disease in children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: left;\">1, 2, 5, 6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: left;\">Sore throat, febrile cold, pneumonia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: left;\">3, 4, 7, 14, 21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: left;\">ARD in military recruits</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: left;\">4, 7, 21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: left;\">Follicular (swimming pool) conjunctivitis/ Pharyngoconjunctival fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: left;\">3, 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: left;\">Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis (shipyard eye)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: left;\">8, 19, 37</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.6px; text-align: left;\">Diarrhea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.4px; text-align: left;\">40, 41</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7502","text":"Viral infection mimicking ‌erythroblastosis fetalis ‌is:","raw_text":"Viral infection mimicking erythroblastosis fetalis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‌SV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Picorn‌avirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parvovi‌rus B19","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Parvovirus B19","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Parvovirus B19</strong></p><p><strong>Parvovirus</strong> <strong>B19 infection</strong> mimics <strong>erythroblastosis fetalis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Parvovirus B19 is associated with: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Erythema infectiosum (fifth disease)</li>\n<li>Transient aplastic crisis</li>\n<li>Pure red cell aplasia</li>\n<li>Erythroblastosis fetalis (most commonly in early pregnancy)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Picornavirus, Epstein-Barr virus, HSV infection do not mimic erythroblastosis fetalis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8279","text":"Which organism causes the ‌disease shown below?","raw_text":"Which organism causes the disease shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Roseol‌a virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parvovi‌rus B19","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rubell‌a virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rubeol‌a virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parvovirus B19","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a8926fe00b824457a0157d285fab223e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parvovirus B19</strong></p><p>The image showed a rash on the cheeks and face, with a slapped cheek appearance, characteristic of <strong>erythema infectiosum/ fifth disease. </strong>It is caused by <strong>Parvovirus B19.</strong></p>\n<p>Parvovirus B19 usually presents as a <strong>respiratory infection,</strong> with an erythematous maculopapular<strong> rash</strong> and <strong>arthralgia</strong>. The rash starts as prominent erythema of the cheek, popularly known as <strong>slapped-cheek appearance. </strong>The rash further spreads to the trunk and limbs followed by lymphadenopathy and arthralgia.</p>\n<p>It induces <strong>aplastic crisis</strong> in sickle cell anemia patients.</p>\n<p>Infection during pregnancy leads to non-immune fetal hydrops (highest risk in the first half of gestational period).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3564","text":"An 8-year-old child presen‌ts with skin lesions, sus‌pected to be molluscum co‌ntagiosum. How is the dia‌gnosis of this condition ​typically made?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child presents with skin lesions, suspected to be molluscum contagiosum. How is the diagnosis of this condition typically made?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral ‌Culture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin ‌biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PCR for ‌Viral DNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clinical ‌appearance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clinical appearance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0e3284b95554515aaeaf94a3d30b634x400x327.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clinical appearance</strong></p><p>Diagnosis of <strong>molluscum contagiosum</strong> is based on <strong>clinical appearance. </strong></p>\n<p>Molluscum contagiosum manifests as pink pearly <strong>wart-like</strong> lesions with a characteristic <strong>dimple</strong> at the center (umbilicated), not associated with inflammation and necrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Skin biopsy or smear of expressed material shows characteristic inclusion bodies but is necessary only when the<strong> diagnosis is uncertain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: Viral culture and PCR for viral DNA are not used for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8277","text":"What is the causative orga‌nism of the condition sho‌wn below?","raw_text":"What is the causative organism of the condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human papillomavirus 6 and‌ 11","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Human papillomavirus 16 an‌d 18","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemophilu‌s ducreyi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Human papillomavirus 6 and 11","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac3ca39e86564c9c9ae5d2d20e48dfd3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/549a7e76325a4cfe90d431fc9f39f2f1x1053x842.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/840612835c7b48dea1e2ffa29dedda79x247x199.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a17485596ac463ca54ab653df5c7faax720x377.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Human papillomavirus 6 and 11</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> condyloma acuminata.</strong> It is most commonly caused by <strong>Human papillomavirus (HPV)</strong><strong> 6 and 11</strong>.</p>\n<p>Condyloma acuminata is a <strong>benign,</strong> genital <strong>wart</strong> which is a moist, soft, pedunculated wart found on the <strong>external</strong> <strong>genitalia.</strong> </p>\n<p>Microscopically, the characteristic features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A branching <strong>villous structure</strong> with connective tissue stroma</li>\n<li>Epidermis showing <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong> and <strong>acanthosis</strong> (thickening)</li>\n<li>Cytoplasmic vacuolization of the squamous cells <strong>(koilocytosis)</strong> is characteristic of HPV infection.</li>\n<li>No evident dysplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Treponema pallidum</em></strong> can cause condylomata lata in secondary syphilis. It is smoother and flatter and may be associated with induration and ulceration. (image below)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Human papillomavirus</strong> <strong>16</strong> and <strong>18</strong> are associated with carcinoma of the cervix and anogenital region.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> <em>Hemophilus ducreyi</em></strong> causes chancroid. Given below is the image of chancroid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3558","text":"A patient presents with pl‌antar warts as shown belo‌w. Which type of human pa‌pillomavirus is responsib‌le for causing these?","raw_text":"A patient presents with plantar warts as shown below. Which type of human papillomavirus is responsible for causing these?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4087abc46e8d435787884500a1942c37.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1</strong></p><p><strong>HPV types 1a, 57, and 27</strong> accounts for most <strong>plantar warts.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>HPV 7</strong> causes hand warts in butchers (butcher's warts).</p>\n<p><strong>HPV 6 and 11</strong> cause condyloma acuminata, sinonasal papilloma, and squamous papilloma of the larynx. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0202","text":"As a CHC medical officer, ‌you organize an outreach ‌program for HPV vaccinati‌on and the prevention of ‌cervical cancer. Which pr​otein is used for antigen​ic response in this vacci‌ne?","raw_text":"As a CHC medical officer, you organize an outreach program for HPV vaccination and the prevention of cervical cancer. Which protein is used for antigenic response in this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E6,‌ E7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L‌1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"E1,‌ E2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. L1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. L1</strong></p><p>The<strong> HPV vaccines </strong>use virus-like particles (VLPs) that consist of the<strong> HPV L1 major capsid protein. </strong>The L1 protein self-assembles into VLPs when expressed in eukaryotic cells.</p>\n<p>These VLPs contain the <strong>same epitopes</strong> as the <strong>HPV</strong> virion. But they do not contain genetic material and cannot transmit infection. The immunogenicity of the HPV vaccines relies on the development of conformational neutralizing antibodies to epitopes displayed on viral capsids.</p>\n<p>The nonavalent HPV vaccine works against strains 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52 and 58. It is usually commercialized in the name of <strong>Gardasil-9. </strong>It has received expanded indication which now includes the prevention of oropharyngeal and other head and neck cancers. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 128px; width: 310px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.659px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>HPV Vaccine </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.114px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Types</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 3.12784px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.659px; text-align: center; height: 3.12784px;\">Quadrivalent (<strong>Gardasil)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.114px; text-align: center; height: 3.12784px;\">\n<p>6, 11, 16, 18</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.659px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\">Bivalent (<strong>Cervarix)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.114px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\">16, 18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.659px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\">Nonavalent (<strong>Gardasil-9)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.114px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\">6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3578","text":"Nonavalent HPV vaccine wor‌ks against which of the f‌ollowing strains of the v‌irus?","raw_text":"Nonavalent HPV vaccine works against which of the following strains of the virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 45, ‌52, 58","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45,‌ 52, 58","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 16, 18, 21, 33, 45, 48,‌ 52, 58","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6, 11, 16, 18, 33, 45, 48,‌ 52, 58","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The <strong>nonavalent HPV vaccine</strong> works against strains <strong>6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52 and 58</strong>. It is usually commercialized in the name of <strong>Gardasil-9. </strong>It has received expanded indication which now includes the prevention of oropharyngeal and other head and neck cancers. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"width: 390.734px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 141px;\"><strong>HPV Vaccine </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 231.734px;\"><strong>Types</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53.9375px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 53.9375px; width: 141px;\">\n<p>Quadrivalent (<strong>Gardasil)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 53.9375px; width: 231.734px;\">\n<p>6, 11, 16, 18</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 141px;\">Bivalent (<strong>Cervarix)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 231.734px;\">16, 18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 141px;\">Nonavalent (<strong>Gardasil-9)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px; width: 231.734px;\">6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8280","text":"An HIV-positive patient pr‌esents with the following‌ skin lesions. Which of t‌he following diseases is ‌associated with the virus​ implicated here?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive patient presents with the following skin lesions. Which of the following diseases is associated with the virus implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-transplant lymphoprol‌iferative disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Merkel cel‌l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adult T-ce‌ll leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary effu‌sion lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary effusion lymphoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fece6b7ec1014a0383a9e26eceda6e32.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary effusion lymphoma</strong></p><p>The above image showing red-purple macules and plaques in an HIV-positive patient is suggestive of <strong>Kaposi</strong> <strong>sarcoma, </strong>caused by <strong>human herpesvirus 8</strong>. It also causes <strong>primary effusion lymphoma.</strong> It is one of seven currently known human cancer viruses or <strong>oncoviruses</strong>.</p>\n<p> HHV-8 or <strong>Kaposi’s sarcoma-associated herpesvirus (KSHV) </strong>is known to cause Kaposi’s sarcoma, in immunocompromised persons. Cancer patients receiving chemotherapy, AIDS patients, and organ transplant recipients are all at a high risk of showing signs of infection.</p>\n<p><strong>KSHV</strong> is a known causative agent of <strong>four diseases</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Kaposi's sarcoma</li>\n<li>KSHV-associated multicentric Castleman's disease (MCD)</li>\n<li>Primary effusion lymphoma</li>\n<li>KSHV inflammatory cytokine syndrome </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8278","text":"Which of the following vir‌uses is implicated in pro‌gressive multifocal leuko‌encephalopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses is implicated in progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"JC v‌irus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"BK v‌irus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Measle‌s virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. JC virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. JC virus</strong></p><p><strong>Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy</strong> is caused by the <strong>JC virus.</strong> JC virus-induced progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is one of the neurologic diseases associated with <strong>HIV infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: BK virus is associated with nephropathy in transplant recipients.</p>\n<p>Option C: The long-term sequelae associated with measles infection is subacute sclerosing panencephalitis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Herpes simplex virus causes temporal lobe encephalitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LjkpAMzr","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"22_preterm_labor_and_postterm_pregnancy","title":"22.Preterm Labor And Postterm Pregnancy","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2078","text":"Preterm labour is defined ‍as when the labour starts​ before _____","raw_text":"Preterm labour is defined as when the labour starts before _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"35 w‍eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"36 w‍eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"37 w‍eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"38 w‍eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 37 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 37 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Preterm labour</strong> is defined as when labour starts before<strong> 37 completed weeks </strong>(&lt;259 days) counting from the first day of the last menstrual period.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Timing of delivery</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Terminology</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;33 weeks</td>\n<td>Early preterm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>34 to 36 weeks</td>\n<td>Late preterm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>37<sup> </sup>to 38 weeks</td>\n<td>Early term</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39 to 40 weeks</td>\n<td>Full term</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2079","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a risk factor for pre​term labour?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for preterm labour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pregnancy following artifi‍cial reproductive techniq​ue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asymptomatic‍ bacteriuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Periodont‍al disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progester‍one intake","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progesterone intake","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progesterone intake</strong></p><p><strong>Progesterone intake</strong> is not a risk factor for preterm labour. Progesterone is used for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of <strong>preterm labour</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for preterm labour:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maternal</strong> <strong>history -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Previous history of spontaneous abortion or preterm delivery</li>\n<li>Artificial reproductive technique</li>\n<li>Asymptomatic bacteriuria or recurrent UTI</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Maternal stress</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy</strong> <strong>complications -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-eclampsia</li>\n<li>Antepartum haemorrhage</li>\n<li>PROM</li>\n<li>Overdistended uterus - polyhydramnios, multiple pregnancy</li>\n<li>Cervical incompetence</li>\n<li>Uterine malformations</li>\n<li>Intrauterine death</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maternal chronic diseases -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Nephritis</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Severe anaemia</li>\n<li>Periodontal diseases</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Multifetal pregnancies</strong> are common following <strong>artificial reproductive techniques</strong> and can be associated with <strong>preterm labour</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Asymptomatic bacteriuria or bacterial vaginosis can cause spontaneous preterm labour.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Chronic diseases</strong> like periodontal disease increase the risk of <strong>preterm labour</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2082","text":"Late preterm births includ‍e births between _____.","raw_text":"Late preterm births include births between _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30-32‍ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"32-34‍ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"34-36‍ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"36-38‍ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 34-36 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 34-36 weeks</strong></p><p><strong>Late preterm</strong> births include births between <strong>34 to 36 weeks </strong>of gestational age.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Timing of delivery</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Terminology</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;33 weeks</td>\n<td>Early preterm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>34 to 36 weeks</td>\n<td>Late preterm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>37 to 38 weeks</td>\n<td>Early term</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39 to 40 weeks</td>\n<td>Full term</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2083","text":"A 31-year-old G3P2 woman p‍resents at 29 weeks of ge​station with a blood-ting‍ed vaginal discharge. She‌ is suspected to have pre​term labour. Which of the‍ following findings will ‌not help confirm her diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old G3P2 woman presents at 29 weeks of gestation with a blood-tinged vaginal discharge. She is suspected to have preterm labour. Which of the following findings will not help confirm her diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bishop s‍core of 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical dilatation of 24 ‍mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical len‍gth of 32 mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal fibronectin &amp;gt; 50n‍g/ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervical length of 32 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervical length of 32 mm</strong></p><p>A cervical length of 32 mm (3 cm) does not help in confirming <strong>preterm labour</strong> as it<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> is highly unlikely when the cervical length is &gt;3 cm. The <strong>length</strong> of the <strong>cervix</strong> in preterm labour, as measured in transvaginal ultrasound is <strong>≤2.5 cm</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Preterm labor is the onset of labor before 37 completed weeks of pregnancy (less than 259 days of gestation).</span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Features</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> of preterm labor:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Uterus - regular <strong>contractions</strong> at least one in <strong>every 10 minutes</strong>, with/without pain </span></li>\n<li>Cervix - <strong>dilatation &gt;2 cm</strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> and <strong>effacement &gt;80%</strong> </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">TVS - length of <strong>cervix &lt;2.5 cm</strong> and <strong>funneling</strong> of the internal os <strong>&gt;1 cm</strong></span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Pelvic pressure, backache, vaginal discharge or bleeding PV</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Predictors</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> of preterm labor:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Clinical</strong> predictors:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">History of prior preterm birth</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Multiple pregnancies</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Presence of genital tract infection</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Symptoms of preterm labour</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Biophysical</strong> predictors:\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Uterine contractions ≥4/hour</span></li>\n<li><strong>Bishop score ≥4</strong></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Cervical length (TVS) ≤25 mm</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Biochemical</strong> predictors:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Fetal fibronectin</strong> (fFN) in cervical/vaginal discharge <strong>&gt;50 ng/mL</strong></span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Increased levels of IL-6, IL-8, TNF-alpha</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>best predictors</strong> of preterm labor in a woman with a previous history of preterm birth are <strong>fetal fibronectin</strong> &gt;50ng/ml and <strong>cervical length</strong> &lt;2.5cm on TVS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2084","text":"Which of the following can‍not be used to predict pr​eterm labour in a patient‍ having irregular uterine‌ contractions at 30 weeks​ of gestation?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used to predict preterm labour in a patient having irregular uterine contractions at 30 weeks of gestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‍-6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TNF ‍alpha","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal fi‍bronectin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‍-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IL-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IL-2</strong></p><p>IL-2 is not a biochemical predictor of preterm labour.</p>\n<p><strong>Biochemical predictors</strong> of preterm labor:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fetal fibronectin</li>\n<li>IL 6</li>\n<li>IL 8</li>\n<li>TNFα</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Investigations</strong> of preterm labour: </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Fetal fibronectin &gt;50 ng/ml</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">TVS - length of cervix ≤2.5 cm</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Urine culture</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">High vaginal swab</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Maternal salivary estriol</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Uterine activity monitoring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2096","text":"A 30-year-old G2P1L0 woman‍ came for a routine anten​atal checkup at 20 weeks ‍of gestation. She is worr‌ied about preterm labour ​as her previous baby was ‍born preterm. What will b‌e the timing and cut-off ‍limit for fetal fibronect‌in (fFN) assay to predict​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old G2P1L0 woman came for a routine antenatal checkup at 20 weeks of gestation. She is worried about preterm labour as her previous baby was born preterm. What will be the timing and cut-off limit for fetal fibronectin (fFN) assay to predict this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;34 weeks, fFN &amp;gt;15 n‍g/mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;37 weeks, fFN &amp;gt;1.5 ‍ng/mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20-24 weeks, fFN &amp;gt;5 ng/‍mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24-34 weeks, fFN &amp;gt;50 ng‍/mL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 24-34 weeks, fFN >50 ng/mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 24-34 weeks, fFN &gt;50 ng/mL</strong></p><p><strong>Fetal fibronectin</strong> value of <strong>&gt;50 ng/mL</strong> in the cervicovaginal discharge between <strong>24 and 34 weeks</strong> is predictive of <strong>preterm labor. </strong></p>\n<p>Fibronectin is a <strong>glycoprotein</strong> that binds the <strong>fetal membranes</strong> to the <strong>decidua</strong>.  It is <strong>normally found</strong> in the cervicovaginal discharge <strong>before 22 weeks</strong> and <strong>after 37 weeks</strong> of gestation. </p>\n<p>It is measured using an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. When the test is <strong>negative</strong>, it reassures that the <strong>delivery</strong> will <strong>not</strong> occur <strong>within the next 7 days. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2085","text":"A pregnant woman presented‍ with blood-tinged vagina​l discharge at 35 weeks o‍f gestation. A nitrazine ‌paper test was performed ​on her. What is this test‍ used to detect?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman presented with blood-tinged vaginal discharge at 35 weeks of gestation. A nitrazine paper test was performed on her. What is this test used to detect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal hemoglobin in vasa p‍revia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal blood cells in Rh is‍oimmunisation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Premature rupture of membr‍anes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abruptio‍ placenta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Premature rupture of membranes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/69fd9002b0184523b032a970c2dd4253x641x483.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Premature rupture of membranes</strong></p><p>The <strong>nitrazine paper test</strong> is used to detect <strong>premature rupture of membranes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nitrazine paper test detects the<strong> pH</strong> of the secretions from the <strong>vaginal pool</strong>. It turns <strong>blue</strong> at <strong>pH &gt;6. Amniotic fluid</strong> is slightly alkaline with a pH of 7.1 - 7.3 and vaginal secretions have a pH of 4.5 - 6.0.</p>\n<p><strong>False-positive</strong> nitrazine test can be seen in contamination of the discharge with <strong>blood, semen</strong>, and <strong>bacterial infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the nitrazine paper.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Fetal hemoglobin</strong> in vasa previa is detected by <strong>Apt</strong> <strong>test.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Estimation of the volume of <strong>fetal blood entering</strong> the <strong>maternal circulation</strong> in Rh isoimmunisation is done by <strong>Kleihauer-Betke</strong> test.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>abruptio</strong> placenta, the <strong>diagnosis</strong> is mainly based on <strong>clinical </strong>features.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2087","text":"A sample of the vaginal fl‍uid from the posterior fo​rnix of a patient gave a ‍positive nitrazine paper ‌test. Which of the follow​ing conditions does not g‍ive a false positive resu‌lt?","raw_text":"A sample of the vaginal fluid from the posterior fornix of a patient gave a positive nitrazine paper test. Which of the following conditions does not give a false positive result?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abruptio‍ placenta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recent sexua‍l intercourse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial‍ vaginosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligohy‍dramnios","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oligohydramnios","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oligohydramnios</strong></p><p><strong>Oligohydramnios</strong> shows a <strong>false negative</strong> nitrazine paper test.</p>\n<p><strong>Nitrazine paper</strong> test<strong> detects</strong> the <strong>pH</strong> of the secretions from the vaginal pool. It turns <strong>blue</strong> at <strong>pH &gt;6. </strong>Amniotic fluid is slightly alkaline with a pH of 7.1 - 7.3 and vaginal secretions have a pH of 4.5 - 6.0.</p>\n<p><strong>False-positive</strong> nitrazine test can be seen in contamination of the discharge with <strong>blood, semen,</strong> and <strong>bacterial infections</strong>. <strong>False-negative</strong> nitrazine test can be seen in the case of <strong>scanty amniotic fluid</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2103","text":"Transvaginal sonography in‍ a case of clinically sus​pected preterm labour rev‍ealed a short cervix and ‌funnelling of the interna​l os. What is the shape o‍f the cervix that is like‌ly to be seen here?","raw_text":"Transvaginal sonography in a case of clinically suspected preterm labour revealed a short cervix and funnelling of the internal os. What is the shape of the cervix that is likely to be seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‍U","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‍T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‍C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‍O","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. U","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27050d4592e04b31b52628384f73a5adx709x574.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. U</strong></p><p><strong>U</strong>-<strong>shaped</strong> <strong>cervix</strong> is seen on tranvaginal sonography (TVS) in <strong>preterm</strong> labour.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>On TVS, the cervix should be assessed to determine whether <strong>funnelling</strong> (internal os width &gt;5 mm) is present.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shape of cervix</td>\n<td>Inference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T shaped</td>\n<td>Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Y shaped</td>\n<td>Suspicion of preterm labour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V shaped</td>\n<td>Funneling of internal os</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>U shaped</td>\n<td>\n<p>Incompetent os</p>\n<p>Preterm labour</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The following is a TVS image showing a V-shaped cervix:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2090","text":"A multigravida presents wi‍th preterm premature rupt​ure of membranes. Accordi‍ng to RCOG guidelines, wh‌ich of the following anti​biotics is preferred for ‍her?","raw_text":"A multigravida presents with preterm premature rupture of membranes. According to RCOG guidelines, which of the following antibiotics is preferred for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampic‍illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxi‍cillin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythr‍omycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Azithr‍omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erythromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erythromycin</strong></p><p>According to RCOG guidelines, <strong>erythromycin</strong> is the preferred antibiotic in the case of <strong>PPROM</strong> (preterm premature rupture of membranes).</p>\n<p><strong>Spontaneous rupture</strong> of membranes <strong>before </strong>the onset of<strong> labour</strong> <strong>between 28 to 37 weeks</strong> is called preterm premature rupture of membranes. </p>\n<p>If <strong>PPROM</strong> occurs<strong> between 28 to 34 weeks</strong>, prophylactic antibiotics and <strong>corticosteroids</strong> are given to the patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2092","text":"A primigravida at 30 weeks‍ of gestation presents wi​th labour pains but her m‍embranes were intact. You‌ decide to start her on c​orticosteroids and a toco‍lytic. Which of the follo‌wing drugs will not be us‍eful in this case?","raw_text":"A primigravida at 30 weeks of gestation presents with labour pains but her membranes were intact. You decide to start her on corticosteroids and a tocolytic. Which of the following drugs will not be useful in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnesium‍ sulphate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nifed‍ipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indome‍thacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proges‍terone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progesterone</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>preterm labour</strong> with intact membranes and progesterone is not used as a tocolytic in these cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Progesterone</strong> can be used for the <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of preterm labour in women with a <strong>previous history</strong> of spontaneous preterm labour.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoid</strong> therapy is given in <strong>preterm labour</strong> when the period of gestation is <strong>&lt;34 weeks</strong> to <strong>hasten fetal lung maturity.</strong> Short term therapy with <strong>tocolytics</strong> is given in conjunction with steroids for 1-3 days to <strong>delay labour</strong> for at least<strong> 48 hours</strong> or to transfer the patient to a tertiary centre (with NICU). </p>\n<p>Tocolytics that can be given as short term therapy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Terbutaline</li>\n<li>Magnesium sulphate</li>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n<li>Nifedipine</li>\n<li>Atosiban</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9939","text":"A primigravida at 32 weeks‍ of gestation was admitte​d for management of prete‍rm labour. Which of the f‌ollowing tocolytics would​ be preferred for this?","raw_text":"A primigravida at 32 weeks of gestation was admitted for management of preterm labour. Which of the following tocolytics would be preferred for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nifed‍ipine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glyceryl ‍trinitrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rito‍drine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Magnesium‍ sulphate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nifedipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nifedipine</strong></p><p><strong>Nifedipine</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> tocolytic as it has <strong>minimum</strong> maternal <strong>side effects</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">According to ACOG guidelines, the use of <strong>tocolytics</strong> in preterm labour does not prolong gestation but may <strong>delay delivery upto 48 hours</strong>. Hence, short-term therapy with tocolytics is given for 1-3 days to delay labour for the action of corticosteroid therapy or to transfer the patient to a tertiary centre (with NICU). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tocolytics used in preterm labour and their side effects:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 240px;\" width=\"950\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Tocolytics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Maternal side effects</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Fetal/ neonatal side effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Beta-adrenergic agonists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ritodrine</li>\n<li>Terbutaline</li>\n<li>Isoxsuprine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Headache, palpitations, tachycardia, hypotension, pulmonary edema, cardiac failure, ARDS</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Fetal heart failure, intrauterine death</p>\n<p>Neonatal hypoglycemia, intraventricular haemorrhage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Calcium channel blockers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nifedipine</li>\n<li>Nicardipine</li>\n<li>Verapamil</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Hypotension, headache, flushing, nausea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Oxytocin antagonists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atosiban</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(not approved for use in India)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Nausea, vomiting, chest pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Magnesium sulphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Flushing, perspiration, headache, muscle weakness, respiratory paralysis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Neonatal cardiac arrest, lethargy, hypotonia, respiratory depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>COX inhibitors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n<li>Sulindac</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Heartburn, asthma, GI bleeding, thrombocytopenia, renal injury, platelet dysfunction, oligohydramnios</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Constriction of ductus arteriosus, pulmonary hypertension, intrauterine growth restriction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9926","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a contraindication fo​r tocolysis in a patient ‍with preterm labour?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for tocolysis in a patient with preterm labour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioam‍nionitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal d‍istress","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anenc‍ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placent‍a previa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Placenta previa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Placenta previa</strong></p><p><strong>Placenta previa</strong> is not a contraindication for tocolysis. In placenta previa, <strong>nifedipine</strong> and <strong>magnesium sulphate</strong> are used as <strong>tocolytics.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> to <strong>tocolysis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chorioamnionitis</strong></li>\n<li>Preeclampsia/Eclampsia</li>\n<li>Advanced labour</li>\n<li><strong>Fetal distress</strong></li>\n<li>Intra-uterine death</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital anomalies</strong> not compatible with life</li>\n<li>Pregnancy &gt;34 weeks</li>\n<li>Abruptio placenta</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2095","text":"A G3P1A1 woman presents wi‍th preterm labour at 27 w​eeks of gestation. She te‍lls you that her previous‌ child who was born prete​rm developed cerebral pal‍sy. Which of the followin‌g drugs can you use to re‍duce the incidence of thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"A G3P1A1 woman presents with preterm labour at 27 weeks of gestation. She tells you that her previous child who was born preterm developed cerebral palsy. Which of the following drugs can you use to reduce the incidence of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terbu‍taline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atos‍iban","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magnesium‍ sulphate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nifed‍ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Magnesium sulphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Magnesium sulphate</strong></p><p><strong>Magnesium sulphate</strong> has a <strong>neuroprotective</strong> effect and hence decreases the risk of developing cerebral palsy.</p>\n<p>Magnesium sulphate can be used for neuroprotection in <strong>preterm labour</strong> when the pregnancy is <strong>&lt;32 weeks</strong>. The dose is<strong> 4 g IV over 20</strong> <strong>mins</strong> followed by an<strong> infusion of 1 g/hr</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2098","text":"A primigravida was adminis‍tered tocolytics and cort​icosteroids for preterm l‍abour. The following day,‌ she suddenly started cou​ghing and gasping for air‍. On examination, her BP ‌was 160/100 mmHg and SpO2‍ was 80% in room air. Whi‌ch of the following drugs​ could have caused this?","raw_text":"A primigravida was administered tocolytics and corticosteroids for preterm labour. The following day, she suddenly started coughing and gasping for air. On examination, her BP was 160/100 mmHg and SpO2 was 80% in room air. Which of the following drugs could have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atos‍iban","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dexame‍thasone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indome‍thacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rito‍drine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ritodrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ritodrine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute pulmonary edema</strong> which is a side effect of the tocolytic <strong>ritodrine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute pulmonary edema in pregnancy can be due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-cardiogenic causes -\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-eclampsia</li>\n<li>Acute haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Tocolysis with ritodrine and magnesium sulphate</li>\n<li>Vigorous IV fluid therapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cardiogenic causes</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tocolytics used in preterm labour and their side effects:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 240px;\" width=\"950\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\"><strong>Tocolytics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\"><strong>Maternal side effects</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\"><strong>Fetal/ neonatal side effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Beta-adrenergic agonists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ritodrine</li>\n<li>Terbutaline</li>\n<li>Isoxsuprine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Headache, palpitations, tachycardia, hypotension, pulmonary edema, cardiac failure, ARDS</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Fetal heart failure, intrauterine death</p>\n<p>Neonatal hypoglycemia, intraventricular haemorrhage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Calcium channel blockers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nifedipine</li>\n<li>Nicardipine</li>\n<li>Verapamil</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Hypotension, headache, flushing, nausea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>Oxytocin antagonists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atosiban</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(not approved for use in India)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Nausea, vomiting, chest pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Magnesium sulphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Flushing, perspiration, headache, muscle weakness, respiratory paralysis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Neonatal cardiac arrest, lethargy, hypotonia, respiratory depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">\n<p>COX inhibitors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n<li>Sulindac</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Heartburn, asthma, GI bleeding, thrombocytopenia, renal injury, platelet dysfunction, oligohydramnios</td>\n<td style=\"width: 309.34375px;\">Constriction of ductus arteriosus, pulmonary hypertension, intrauterine growth retardation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2099","text":"Which of the following toc‍olytics can cause multipl​e fractures in the fetus ‍if given for a prolonged ‌duration?","raw_text":"Which of the following tocolytics can cause multiple fractures in the fetus if given for a prolonged duration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atos‍iban","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnesium‍ sulphate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nifed‍ipine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitric‍ oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Magnesium sulphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Magnesium sulphate</strong></p><p><strong>Magnesium sulphate</strong> can cause <strong>multiple fractures</strong> and <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> in a preterm baby.</p>\n<p>The FDA warned against prolonged use of magnesium sulphate to arrest preterm labour. The <strong>antagonistic effect</strong> of magnesium <strong>lowers</strong> the <strong>calcium</strong> levels in the fetus. This causes bone thinning and fractures in fetuses <strong>exposed</strong> to the drug for <strong>more than 5 to 7 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2108","text":"Post-term pregnancy refers‍ to pregnancy continuing ​beyond ______.","raw_text":"Post-term pregnancy refers to pregnancy continuing beyond ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"41 weeks o‍f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"42 weeks o‍f gestation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"44 weeks o‍f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"40 weeks o‍f gestation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 42 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 42 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>ACOG defines <strong>post-term</strong> pregnancy as a pregnancy continuing<strong> beyond</strong> <strong>42 completed weeks</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p>For clinical purposes, a pregnancy continuing <strong>beyond 2 weeks </strong>of the<strong> expected date of delivery</strong> (&gt; 294 days) is called a post-term pregnancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Inaccurate LMP</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of misdiagnosis of post-term pregnancy. Hence, early ultrasound dating is important to determine fetal gestational age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2109","text":"A woman presented for an a‍ntenatal checkup 2 weeks ​past her EDD. Which of th‍e following is not an eti‌ological factor for her c​ondition?","raw_text":"A woman presented for an antenatal checkup 2 weeks past her EDD. Which of the following is not an etiological factor for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anenc‍ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sedenta‍ry habit","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primi‍parity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Materna‍l stress","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maternal stress","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maternal stress</strong></p><p><strong>Maternal stress</strong> is associated with <strong>preterm</strong> <strong>labour</strong>, not post-term pregnancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Etiological</strong> factors of <strong>post-term</strong> pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inaccurate LMP</li>\n<li>Heredity</li>\n<li>Primiparity</li>\n<li>Previous prolonged pregnancy</li>\n<li>Sedentary habits</li>\n<li>Elderly multipara</li>\n<li>Anencephaly</li>\n<li>Placental sulphatase deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9920","text":"You have performed an arti‍ficial rupture of membran​es in a primigravida for ‍augmentation of labour. Y‌ou note the amniotic flui​d to be saffron-coloured.‍ What is the likely diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"You have performed an artificial rupture of membranes in a primigravida for augmentation of labour. You note the amniotic fluid to be saffron-coloured. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postma‍turity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rh incomp‍atibility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrauter‍ine death","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concealed placental abrupt‍ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Postmaturity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Postmaturity</strong></p><p><strong>Saffron</strong>-coloured <strong>amniotic fluid</strong> is seen in <strong>postmaturity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2114","text":"A newborn baby was found t‍o have wrinkled, patchy s​kin, a long thin body and‍ long nails. Which of the‌ following could be a cau​se for his condition?","raw_text":"A newborn baby was found to have wrinkled, patchy skin, a long thin body and long nails. Which of the following could be a cause for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placental aromatase defici‍ency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Placental progesterone def‍iciency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Placental pregnenolone def‍iciency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Placental sulphatase defic‍iency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Placental sulphatase deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Placental sulphatase deficiency</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>postmaturity</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> which can occur due to <strong>placental sulphatase</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Etiology</strong> of post-term pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wrong dates - inaccurate LMP (most common)</li>\n<li>Heredity</li>\n<li>Previous prolonged pregnancy</li>\n<li>Anencephaly</li>\n<li>Placental sulphatase deficiency<strong> </strong>(leads to decreased estrogen synthesis)</li>\n<li>Primipara/ elderly multipara</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of <strong>postmaturity syndrome </strong>in a newborn:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wrinkled, patchy, peeling skin, particularly prominent on the palms and soles</li>\n<li>Long, thin body suggesting wasting</li>\n<li>Long nails</li>\n<li>Eyes open and unusually alert</li>\n<li>Looks old and worried</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Placental aromatase deficiency causes disorder of sexual development of fetus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2116","text":"A G2P1L1 woman came for a ‍routine ANC at 41 weeks o​f gestation. On examinati‍on, her Bishop score was ‌2. She was admitted for f​etal monitoring and induc‍tion. Which of the follow‌ing complications would y‍ou not anticipate in the ‌fetus even if her pregnan​cy continues further?","raw_text":"A G2P1L1 woman came for a routine ANC at 41 weeks of gestation. On examination, her Bishop score was 2. She was admitted for fetal monitoring and induction. Which of the following complications would you not anticipate in the fetus even if her pregnancy continues further?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shoulder‍ dystocia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polycy‍themia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cord com‍pression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperg‍lycemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperglycemia</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the pregnancy continuing further would<strong> cross 42 weeks</strong> of gestation and thus turn into a<strong> post-term pregnancy. Hypoglycemia</strong> is seen in a post-term neonate.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> <strong>of post-term pregnancy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">During labour:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fetal hypoxia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Labour dysfunction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Macrosomia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Shoulder dystocia</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oligohydramnios</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cord compression</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Birth trauma </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meconium aspiration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intracranial haemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Following birth:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meconium aspiration syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoxia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Polycythemia</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neonatal convulsions</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9924","text":"A primigravida was admitte‍d at 42 weeks for inducti​on of labour. Which of th‍e following conditions is‌ she not at an increased ​risk for?","raw_text":"A primigravida was admitted at 42 weeks for induction of labour. Which of the following conditions is she not at an increased risk for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postpartum‍ hemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Meconium st‍ained liquor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shoulder‍ dystocia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligohy‍dramnios","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Postpartum hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Postpartum hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The risk of post-partum haemorrhage does not increase in post-term pregnancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of <strong>post-term pregnancy</strong> are due to <strong>placental insufficiency</strong> and placental ageing. It manifests as placental infarcts and calcifications. During post-term pregnancy, diminished placental function can cause <strong>oligohydramnios</strong>. </p>\n<p>Complications of post-term pregnancy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">During labour:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Fetal hypoxia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Labour dysfunction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Macrosomia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Shoulder dystocia</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Oligohydramnios</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Cord compression</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Birth trauma </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Meconium aspiration</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Following birth:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Chemical pneumonitis, atelectasis, pulmonary hypertension (due to meconium aspiration)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Hypoxia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Polycythemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\">Neonatal convulsions</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2080","text":"The term small for gestati‍onal age implies birth we​ight ______","raw_text":"The term small for gestational age implies birth weight ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;10th ‍percentile","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;15th ‍percentile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;2‍.5 kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;‍2 kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <10th percentile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;10th percentile</strong></p><p><strong>Small</strong> for gestational age implies birthweight is <strong>&lt;10th percentile</strong> for gestational age.</p>\n<p>With respect to the <strong>size</strong>, a newborn can be appropriate, small or large for gestational age.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px;\" width=\"524\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\"><strong>Birth weight</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\"><strong>Terminology</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\">&lt;10th percentile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\">Small for gestational age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\">&gt;90th percentile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\">Large for gestational age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\">Between 10th and 90th percentile</td>\n<td style=\"width: 254px;\">Appropriate for gestational age</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2086","text":"What is the normal pH of a‍mniotic fluid?","raw_text":"What is the normal pH of amniotic fluid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8.1 ‍- 8.7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7.1 ‍- 7.3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6.1 ‍- 6.3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6.5 ‍- 6.9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7.1 - 7.3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7.1 - 7.3</strong></p><p>The normal pH of the amniotic fluid is <strong>7.1 - 7.3</strong></p>\n<p>The normal pH of the vaginal secretions is 4.5 - 5.5. In <strong>premature rupture</strong> of membranes (PROM), the pH of the vaginal secretions becomes<strong> 6 - 6.2</strong> as the amniotic fluid mixes with it. This forms the basis for nitrazine paper test.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yUpBhV_G","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"22_disorders_of_visual_pathway_and_pupillary_reflexes","title":"22.Disorders Of Visual Pathway And Pupillary Reflexes","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0330","text":"Which of the following for‍ms the third-order neuron​s in the visual pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the third-order neurons in the visual pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bipola‍r cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"From the lateral geniculat‍e body","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ganglio‍n cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Optic‍ tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ganglion cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ganglion cells</strong></p><p><strong>Third-order neurons</strong> in the visual pathway are the <strong>ganglion cells.</strong></p>\n<p>The afferent neuronal impulses are carried in the following manner:</p>\n<p>Photoreceptors (I) ⇒ Bipolar cells (II) ⇒ <strong>Ganglion cells (III)</strong> ⇒ Lateral geniculate body (IV).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0508","text":"A 41-year-old woman with a‍ known pituitary microade​noma presented with sudde‌n-onset visual disturbanc​es, headache, and hypoten‌sion during the second tr​imester of pregnancy. Wha​t is the visual field def‍ect this patient is most ​likely to experience?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old woman with a known pituitary microadenoma presented with sudden-onset visual disturbances, headache, and hypotension during the second trimester of pregnancy. What is the visual field defect this patient is most likely to experience?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio-temporal quadranta‍nopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilateral homonymous hem‍ianopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contralateral homonymous h‍emianopia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heteronymou‍s hemianopia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Heteronymous hemianopia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82fff55164f84c7fbe1d9e7ddf4371e1x1280x2202.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Heteronymous hemianopia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pituitary apoplexy</strong> in pregnancy This acute onset event is almost always associated with compression at the <strong>level of optic chiasma</strong> causing <strong>heteronymous hemianopia (bitemporal hemianopia).</strong></p>\n<p>Pituitary apoplexy results from <strong>hemorrhagic infarction</strong> of a <strong>pre-existing pituitary adenoma</strong> or within a physiologically enlarged gland, which is well documented in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The nasal fibers of each retina cross through the chiasma. <strong>Slow-growing microadenomas</strong> compress lower nasal optic fibers causing <strong>superior temporal quadrantanopia</strong> initially, before progressing to bitemporal hemianopia. <strong>However,</strong> an <strong>abrupt infarction or hemorrhage</strong> of a pre-existing pituitary adenoma will lead to <strong>rapid progression</strong> of the visual defect.</p>\n<p>Heteronymous hemianopia is the loss of half of the visual field on different sides in both eyes. It is separated into two categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Binasal hemianopia – the loss of the fields surrounding the nose</li>\n<li>Bitemporal hemianopia – the loss of the fields closest to the temples.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common causes of bitemporal hemianopia<strong> </strong>due to<strong> </strong><strong>central chiasmal lesions</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suprasellar aneurysms</li>\n<li><strong>Tumors of the pituitary gland</strong></li>\n<li>Craniopharyngioma</li>\n<li>Suprasellar meningioma</li>\n<li>Glioma of the third ventricle</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other visual defects according to the site of the lesion are as follows:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0951","text":"Which of the following can‍not be tested with monocu​lar visual field testing ‌using a pin with a red pi​nhead?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be tested with monocular visual field testing using a pin with a red pinhead?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central‍ scotoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paracentr‍al scotoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temporal fi‍eld defects","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral f‍ield defects","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peripheral field defects","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peripheral field defects</strong></p><p><strong>Central scotoma, paracentral scotoma</strong> and <strong>temporal field defects</strong> can be tested by monocular visual field testing with a red-headed neurological pin.</p>\n<p>A patient who has central scotoma will either not see the pinhead in the centre of the field or perceive it black. This can be defined by moving the pin outwards in four different directions until the patient sees the pinhead as being red.</p>\n<p>Placing the pin in each of the quadrants close to the centre and checking if the patient sees the red pinhead can detect a paracentral scotoma. This can also detect temporal field defects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0950","text":"A lesion in which of the f‍ollowing structures is mo​st likely to result in co‌ngruous homonymous hemian​opia?","raw_text":"A lesion in which of the following structures is most likely to result in congruous homonymous hemianopia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic‍ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic ‍chiasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic‍ tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipita‍l cortex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Occipital cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Occipital cortex</strong></p><p>Congruous homonymous hemianopia is most suggestive of a lesion in the occipital cortex.</p>\n<p><strong>Homonymous</strong> <strong>hemianopia</strong> involves vision loss on the <strong>same side of the visual field in both eyes</strong>. This type of visual field loss is suggestive of a lesion posterior to the optic chiasma in the visual pathway.</p>\n<p>Complete homonymous hemianopia involves the complete visual hemifields of both eyes and can occur with any large enough lesion posterior to the optic chiasma. Visual field examination alone cannot further localize the lesion in such cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Incomplete</strong> <strong>homonymous</strong> <strong>hemianopia</strong> is due to smaller lesions in the visual pathway posterior to the optic chiasm. They are further divided into congruous and incongruous depending on the extent to which the defects in the two half fields match each other exactly.</p>\n<p>Because of a relative <strong>disorder</strong> <strong>among axon bundles</strong> in the immediate post-chiasmal region, diseases in this location produce rather <strong>incongruent</strong> lesions.</p>\n<p>More posteriorly, the axon bundles of the corresponding areas of the two hemifields are more closely aligned and hence, lesions of the <strong>occipital cortex</strong> produce <strong>congruous homonymous hemianopia</strong> while <strong>incongruous homonymous hemianopia</strong> is more likely seen with <strong>optic tract lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0336","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‍ted with a persistent hea​dache for the past few mo‌nths. On evaluation, her ​vision on Snellen's chart‌ was 6/6 in both eyes. Th​e fundus also appears to ​be normal. Visual field c‍harting shows a bitempora​l visual field defect. Si​te of the lesion is _____​____","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with a persistent headache for the past few months. On evaluation, her vision on Snellen's chart was 6/6 in both eyes. The fundus also appears to be normal. Visual field charting shows a bitemporal visual field defect. Site of the lesion is _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prelaminar ‍optic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic nerve juxta lamina c‍ribrosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic ‍chiasma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Optic‍ tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Optic chiasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Optic chiasma</strong></p><p>The site of the lesion is<strong> optic chiasma. </strong>Lesions at the chiasma characteristically produce <strong>bitemporal field defects</strong> due to the decussation of <strong>nasal</strong> fibres.</p>\n<p>In <strong>retrochiasmal</strong> lesions, <strong>homonymous hemianopia</strong> is seen. Further, in cortical lesions, <strong>macular (central) vision</strong> is spared.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0335","text":"A young patient with visua‍l disturbances was found ​to have a brain tumour. H‌e was referred to ophthal​mology for a complete vis‌ual field assessment befo​re planning his resection​. Humprey analyzer reveal‍ed the following plot. Wh​at is the most likely sit​e of the lesion?","raw_text":"A young patient with visual disturbances was found to have a brain tumour. He was referred to ophthalmology for a complete visual field assessment before planning his resection. Humprey analyzer revealed the following plot. What is the most likely site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic‍ tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral gen‍iculate body","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pariet‍al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tempor‍al lobe","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Temporal lobe","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/73e45e019afa44639fa7ddb229ee3587.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Temporal lobe</strong></p><p>The visual field defect shown is the typical <strong>\"pie in the sky\"</strong> defect or <strong>superior quadrantic hemianopia. </strong>They are produced when the <strong>inferior fibers</strong> of the optic radiations in the <strong>temporal </strong>lobe (<strong>Meyer's loop)</strong> are involved.</p>\n<p><strong>\"Pie in the floor\"</strong> defects refer to <strong>inferior</strong> <strong>quadrantic hemianopia.</strong> It is seen in the involvement of the <strong>superior fibers</strong> of optic radiations in the <strong>parietal</strong> lobe (<strong>Baum's</strong> <strong>loop</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1234","text":"The primary visual cortex ‍is located in the _______​________.","raw_text":"The primary visual cortex is located in the _______________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior tem‍poral sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parieto-occi‍pital sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior part of calcarin‍e sulcus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Central‍ sulcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior part of calcarine sulcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/525dd2dde4ff4911be076bdcdb2cbc6ex1280x1037.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior part of calcarine sulcus</strong></p><p>The<strong> primary visual cortex</strong>( Brodmann <strong>area 17</strong>) is located in the <strong>posterior part of the calcarine sulcus in the occiptal lobe.</strong></p>\n<p>Accessory visual areas are area 18 (secondary) and area 19 (tertiary).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Image: Brodman areas</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1237","text":"A 72-year-old lady was bro‍ught by her son with bila​teral loss of vision for ‌3 days, associated with s​lurred speech. It was pre‌ceded by an occipital hea​dache. Despite her obviou​s blindness, illustrated ‍by her walking into objec​ts, the patient denied vi​sual loss. MRI revealed b​ilateral occipital infarc‍ts. Which of the followin‌g statement is true regar​ding the given condition?‍","raw_text":"A 72-year-old lady was brought by her son with bilateral loss of vision for 3 days, associated with slurred speech. It was preceded by an occipital headache. Despite her obvious blindness, illustrated by her walking into objects, the patient denied visual loss. MRI revealed bilateral occipital infarcts. Which of the following statement is true regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct and consensual refl‍exes are absent in both e​yes.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct and consensual refl‍exes are present in both ​eyes.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direct reflex is present b‍ut consensual reflex is a​bsent.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Consensual reflex is prese‍nt but direct reflex is a​bsent.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct and consensual reflexes are present in both eyes.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct and consensual reflexes are present in both eyes.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cortical blindness</strong> with visual anosognosia (<strong>Anton syndrome</strong>). Since <strong>anterior visual pathways are not affected</strong> in cortical blindness, <strong>direct and consensual light reflexes</strong> are<strong> preserved.</strong></p>\n<p>Cortical blindness is usually associated with bilateral <strong>occipital infarcts</strong> where it is supplied by the posterior cerebral arteries and these infarcts usually involve both the primary visual cortex and visual association area. It is secondary to <strong>damage to the visual pathways posterior to the lateral geniculate nuclei</strong>; anterior visual pathways are intact. Visual field defects can vary depending on the extent and location of the infarct.</p>\n<p>Direct and consensual reflexes are present in both eyes in cortical blindness as pupillary light reflex fibers exit the visual pathway at the level of the superior colliculus. They exit the optic tract before the lateral geniculate body (LGB) to reach the center for pupillary light reflex- <strong>p</strong><strong>retectal nucleus</strong> at the midbrain.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Anton syndrome</strong> is visual anosognosia (denial of loss of vision) associated with confabulation (defined as the emergence of memories of events and experiences which never took place) in the setting of obvious visual loss and cortical blindness. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1244","text":"A neurology resident is de‍monstrating spinal reflex​es in a comatose patient.‌ When he pinches the skin​ over the side of the pat‌ient's neck, there is a r​apid dilatation of the ip​silateral pupil. Which of‍ the following forms the ​efferent limb of this ref​lex?","raw_text":"A neurology resident is demonstrating spinal reflexes in a comatose patient. When he pinches the skin over the side of the patient's neck, there is a rapid dilatation of the ipsilateral pupil. Which of the following forms the efferent limb of this reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholinergic fibres of ocul‍omotor nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenergic fibres of oculo‍motor nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sympathetic fibres of cerv‍ical ganglion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Motor fibres of trigeminal‍ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sympathetic fibres of cervical ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sympathetic fibres of cervical ganglion</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes <strong>cilio-spinal reflex. Sympathetic fibers of cervical ganglion </strong>form the efferent limb of this reflex.</p>\n<p>Cilio-spinal reflex:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Afferent</strong> inputs of this reflex are carried by the <strong>trigeminal</strong> nerve or cervical pain fibers (lateral spinothalamic tract).</li>\n<li>It <strong>synapses</strong> at the <strong>ciliospinal center of Budge</strong>, located at C8 to T2 in the intermediolateral (IML) horn of the gray matter of the spinal cord.</li>\n<li>The <strong>efferent</strong> branch of the reflex consists of:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preganglionic</strong> <strong>sympathetic</strong> <strong>fibers</strong> from the upper thoracic and lower cervical spinal cord which goes to the cervical sympathetic chain and its three ganglia.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">The <strong>postganglionic sympathetic fibers</strong> pass on the surface of the internal carotid artery and join the ophthalmic nerve at the cavernous sinus. Here, it passes through the nasociliary nerve to reach the ciliary body and innervates the <strong>dilator pupillae</strong> muscles, causing dilatation of the pupil.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This reflex is absent in Horner's syndrome and in lesions involving the cervical sympathetic fibers and it is enhanced in patients with cluster headache and barbiturate-induced coma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1241","text":"Which of the following is ‍not part of the pupillary​ light reflex pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not part of the pupillary light reflex pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Edinger-West‍phal nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral gen‍iculate body","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pretecta‍l nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ganglion cel‍ls in retina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral geniculate body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19ca744ed1e648de8dc46618eb34c6e4x1280x1813.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral geniculate body</strong></p><p>The lateral geniculate body is <strong>not</strong> a part of the pupillary reflex pathway. It is part of the accommodation reflex and the visual pathway.</p>\n<p><strong>Pupillary light reflex pathway:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First</strong> <strong>neurons</strong> (sensory) connects each retina with both <strong>pretectal nuclei</strong> in the <strong>midbrain</strong> at the level of the <strong>superior colliculi</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Second neurons</strong> (internuncial) connects each pretectal nucleus to both <strong>Edinger–Westphal nuclei.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Third</strong> <strong>neurons</strong> (preganglionic motor) connects the Edinger–Westphal nucleus to the <strong>ciliary ganglion.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fourth neurons</strong> (postganglionic motor) leaves the ciliary ganglion and passes in the <strong>short ciliary nerves</strong> to innervate the <strong>sphincter pupillae</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Pupillary reflex pathways</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0331","text":"A patient presented with d‍iplopia to the casualty. ​On examination, the left ‌pupil was dilated. Both d​irect and consensual ligh‌t reflexes were lost in t​he affected eye. What is ​the most probable diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"A patient presented with diplopia to the casualty. On examination, the left pupil was dilated. Both direct and consensual light reflexes were lost in the affected eye. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"II nerv‍e palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"III ner‍ve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"V nerv‍e palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VII ner‍ve palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. III nerve palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. III nerve palsy</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation suggests that the patient has <strong>III nerve</strong> (<strong>oculomotor nerve</strong>) palsy.</p>\n<p>Absence of contraction of the pupil in both direct and consensual reflex suggests a <strong>lesion in the efferent pathway</strong> of the affected eye. This is <strong>III nerve/oculomotor palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Light reflex consists of direct and consensual reflexes.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>When light is shone in an eye,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Constriction of the pupil of the same eye is called <strong>direct reflex.</strong></li>\n<li>Constriction of the contralateral pupil is called <strong>consensual reflex/indirect reflex</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the light reflex,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Afferent nerve fiber is the II cranial nerve (optic nerve).</li>\n<li>Efferent nerve fiber is the III cranial nerve (oculomotor nerve)-Bilaterally</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In II nerve palsy, there will be a loss of direct reflex in the affected eye and loss of consensual light reflex in the opposite eye. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4257","text":"A male patient came with a‍ history of unprotected s​exual intercourse around ‌20 years ago. Now, he com​plains of neurological sy‌mptoms and visual disturb​ances. Which of the follo​wing will be seen on expo‍sure of his pupils to lig​ht?","raw_text":"A male patient came with a history of unprotected sexual intercourse around 20 years ago. Now, he complains of neurological symptoms and visual disturbances. Which of the following will be seen on exposure of his pupils to light?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pupils react but do not ac‍commodate.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pupils accommodate but do ‍not react.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pupils neither react not a‍ccommodate.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pupils react and accommoda‍te.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pupils accommodate but do not react.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pupils accommodate but do not react.</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has acquired syphilis due to unprotected sex and has developed neurological and visual disturbances secondarily as a complication of <strong>neurosyphilis</strong>. <strong>Argyll-Robertson</strong> pupils(ARP)<strong> </strong>are seen associated with neurosyphilis where the <strong>pupils accommodate but do not react to light</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Argyll Robertson pupil</strong> is attributed to a dorsal midbrain lesion of neurosyphilis that affects the pupillary light pathway but spares the more ventrally placed pupillary near reflex pathway(Accomdation pathway), thereby causing a <strong>light-near dissociation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of<strong> Argyll Robertson pupil (ARP) </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral<strong> small, irregular pupils </strong>in dim light</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ccommodation <strong>R</strong>eflex <strong>P</strong>resent (ARP)- The eye accomdates by constricting the pupil for a near target.</li>\n<li><strong>Light Reflex absent</strong>: pupils do<span class=\"st\"> not constrict when exposed to bright light.</span></li>\n<li><strong>Cocaine</strong> eye drops(mydriatics) induced <strong>mydriasis</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other clinical features in neurosyphilis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Fleeting and repetitive lancinating pains, primarily in the legs</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">acute abdominal pain with vomiting(visceral crisis)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Tabes dorsalis with Ataxia and gait disturbances due to loss of position sense</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Loss of reflexes in the legs</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Paresthesias, bladder disturbances</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other causes of <strong>light-near dissociation</strong> are:</p>\n<p>In <strong>unilateral</strong> eye</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 787\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Adie pupil</strong></li>\n<li>Herpes zoster ophthalmicus</li>\n<li>Aberrant regeneration of the third cranial nerve</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In <strong>bilateral</strong> eyes</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Neurosyphilis</li>\n<li>Type 1 diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Myotonic dystrophy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: <strong>Adie's pupil</strong> more commonly manifests in <strong>young women</strong> with <strong>unilateral</strong> <strong>large</strong> regular pupil(Anisocoria) with <strong>absent light reflex</strong> and <strong>sluggish </strong>and <strong>tonic</strong> <strong>accomdation reflex</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is caused by post-ganglionic <strong>parasympathetic</strong> pupillary supply <strong>damage</strong> and is usually associated with <strong>absent knee jerk</strong>.</p>\n<p>Adie's pupil <strong>constricts</strong> with <strong>weak pilocarpine</strong> (0.1%) drops, while normal pupil and <strong>Argyll robertson pupil</strong> does <strong>not</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0334","text":"Which of the following wou‍ld you suspect in a patie​nt who presents with smal‌l pupils?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you suspect in a patient who presents with small pupils?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon monox‍ide poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethylene gly‍col poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Holmes–A‍die pupil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pontine h‍emorrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pontine hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pontine hemorrhage</strong></p><p><strong>Pontine hemorrhage</strong> is associated with small pupils. The remaining options, namely, carbon monoxide poisoning, ethylene glycol poisoning, and Holmes–Adie pupil, cause dilation of the pupils.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0332","text":"A 67-year-old woman finall‍y decides to get glasses,​ about thirty years after‌ she actually needed them​, and goes to an optometr‌ist for an eyeglass presc​ription. Her eye examinat​ion reveals some unexpect‍ed findings. The pupils w​ere small. When a pen-lig​ht is shone on to her eye​s, neither pupil contract‍s. However, when the opto‌metrist brings his finger​s towards her face, both ‍pupils constrict dramatic‌ally. What is the given c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old woman finally decides to get glasses, about thirty years after she actually needed them, and goes to an optometrist for an eyeglass prescription. Her eye examination reveals some unexpected findings. The pupils were small. When a pen-light is shone on to her eyes, neither pupil contracts. However, when the optometrist brings his fingers towards her face, both pupils constrict dramatically. What is the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aniso‍coria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adie's‍ pupil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal oph‍thalmoplegia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argyll-Robe‍rtson pupil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Argyll-Robertson pupil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Argyll-Robertson pupil</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation of bilateral small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation suggests a diagnosis of <strong>Argyll Robertson pupil</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Features of Argyll Robertson pupil (ARP):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ccommodation <strong>R</strong>eflex <strong>P</strong>resent (ARP)</li>\n<li>Light Reflex absent</li>\n<li>Constricted pupil</li>\n<li>An example of <strong>light near dissocation.</strong></li>\n<li>Seen in neurosyphilis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option A</strong>: <strong>Anisocoria</strong> is when the pupils are <strong>not of the same size</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B: </strong><strong>Adie's tonic pupil</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Light reflex absent or sluggish.</li>\n<li>Near reflex: <strong>Slow and tonic</strong></li>\n<li>Pupil is larger</li>\n<li>Constricts with weak pilocarpine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option C: </strong><strong>Ophthalmoplegia interna</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated paralyzed pupil</li>\n<li>Does <strong>not respond to light or accommodation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1243","text":"Wernicke's hemianopic pupi‍l is due to a lesion in t​he _______.","raw_text":"Wernicke's hemianopic pupil is due to a lesion in the _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic r‍adiation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic‍ tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Optic ‍chiasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral gen‍iculate body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Optic tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Optic tract</strong></p><p><strong>Wernicke's</strong> <strong>hemianopic pupil</strong> is due to a lesion in the<strong> optic tract.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>optic tracts</strong> contain both<strong> visual and pupillomotor fibers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>visual fibers</strong> terminate in the <strong>lateral geniculate body,</strong> but the <strong>pupillary fibers</strong> leave the optic tract anterior to the lateral geniculate body, projecting through the brachium of the superior colliculus to<strong> terminate in the pretectal nuclei</strong>.</p>\n<p>An optic tract lesion may, therefore, give rise to an <strong>afferent pupillary conduction defect</strong>.</p>\n<p>Characteristically, the<strong> pupillary light reflex will be normal</strong> when the<strong> unaffected hemiretina</strong> is stimulated and <strong>absent,</strong> when the involved<strong> hemiretina is stimulated</strong> (i.e., light is shone from the hemianopic side).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0333","text":"Which of the following pup‍illary abnormalities is m​ost likely to be observed‌ in a patient with primar​y open-angle glaucoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following pupillary abnormalities is most likely to be observed in a patient with primary open-angle glaucoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marcus G‍unn pupil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adie’s to‍nic pupil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argyll-Robe‍rtson pupil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hutchinso‍n's pupil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Marcus Gunn pupil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Marcus Gunn pupil</strong></p><p><strong>Primary open-angle glaucoma</strong> commonly causes bilateral and <strong>asymmetric </strong>optic nerve damage.<strong> </strong>Marcus Gunn pupil also known as <strong>relative afferent pupillary defect </strong>(RAPD) is the <strong>earliest sign</strong> of <strong>optic nerve disease </strong>as seen in <strong>optic nerve neuritis</strong> or <strong>primary open-angle glaucoma</strong>(POAG).</p>\n<p>RAPD is caused due to a <strong>defect </strong>in the<strong> afferent pathway</strong> (optic nerve) of the pupillary<strong> light reflex </strong>in <strong>one eye.</strong></p>\n<p>It is diagnosed by the <strong>swinging flashlight test</strong> where the flashlight is swung to and fro, and the pupillary response is observed. <strong>Normally,</strong> <strong>both</strong> pupils <strong>constrict</strong> when any one eye is stimulated (due to direct and consensual light reflex).<strong> </strong>In the presence of <strong>RAPD</strong>, <strong>both pupils </strong>will abnormally <strong>dilate</strong> when the <strong>affected eye</strong> is <strong>stimulated</strong>. This paradoxical response of the pupil to light is called <strong>Marcus Gunn pupil. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Causes of RAPD:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Optic nerve lesions (e.g.All types of <strong>optic neuritis</strong>, <strong>glaucoma</strong>)</li>\n<li>Optic tract lesions (e.g., infarction)</li>\n<li>Severe retinal disease (e.g., <strong>central retinal artery occlusion</strong>, extensive retinal detachment)</li>\n<li>Maculopathy (macular degeneration )</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Adie tonic pupil </strong>is a <strong>unilateral</strong> dilated pupil with <strong>light-near dissociation</strong> due to postganglionic parasympathetic pupillomotor nerve supply damage. In these patients the <strong>light reflex</strong> is <strong>absent</strong> and the <strong>accomdation</strong> (near) <strong>reflex</strong> is <strong>sluggish</strong>. It is commonly seen in <strong>young females</strong> and is associated with <strong>absent knee jerk</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Argyll Robertson pupils</strong> are <strong>bilateral</strong> small pupils with <strong>light-near dissociation</strong>. It is commonly seen in patients with <strong>neurosyphilis.</strong> In these patients the <strong>p</strong>upillary <strong>r</strong>eflex is <strong>a</strong>bsent but the <strong>a</strong>ccommodation <strong>r</strong>eflex is <strong>p</strong>resent (<strong>ARP</strong>).</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hutchinson's pupil </strong>is a clinical sign where the <strong>pupil</strong> on the <strong>side</strong> of an <strong>intracranial mass</strong> lesion is <strong>dilated</strong> and <strong>unreactive to light</strong>, due to compression of the <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong> on that side.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6856","text":"The casualty intern notice‍d that a patient had uneq​ual pupils. The anisocori‌a was greater under light​. Which of the following ‌drugs would be helpful in​ quickly differentiating ​between the potential cau‍ses? Phenylephrine 1% Pil​ocarpine 0.1% Pilocarpine​ Cocaine 0.5% Epinephrine​","raw_text":"<p>The casualty intern noticed that a patient had unequal pupils. The anisocoria was greater under light. Which of the following drugs would be helpful in quickly differentiating between the potential causes?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Phenylephrine</li>\n<li>1% Pilocarpine</li>\n<li>0.1% Pilocarpine</li>\n<li>Cocaine 0.5%</li>\n<li>Epinephrine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3,‍ 4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 and ‍3 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 , 4‍ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 3 only</strong></p><p><strong>Anisocoria greater in the light</strong> indicates the large pupil to be abnormal due to poor pupillary contraction from a parasympathetic abnormality. Among the given options, <strong>1% pilocarpine</strong> and <strong>0.1% pilocarpine</strong> can be used to differentiate fixed dilated pupil. </p>\n<p>Mydriasis due to neurologic origin <strong>(3rd nerve palsy)</strong> responds readily to <strong>1%</strong> pilocarpine, whereas there will be no response if the dilation is due to <strong>anticholinergic causes</strong>. Pupillary constriction to <strong>0.1%</strong> pilocarpine diagnoses <strong>Adie's pupil</strong> (due to denervation hypersensitivity). Application of 0.1% pilocarpine shows no response in other conditions or in the normal eye.</p>\n<p>Example: A person presenting with dilated pupil following accidental application or inadvertent rubbing of atropine or antimuscarinic alkaloids from wild plants into the eye will not constrict in response to topical application of 1% Pilocarpine. But the patient showing dilation due to 3rd nerve palsy shows pupillary constriction in response to pupillary instillation of 1% pilocarpine.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Cocaine and phenylephrine are used in <strong>anisocoria that is greater in the dark</strong> to diagnose Horner’s syndrome. In these patients, the small <strong>pupil fails to dilate</strong> even after the instillation of cocaine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1246","text":"A 55-year-old lady with ad‍vanced spondylosis underw​ent cervical vertebral fu‌sion surgery. In the post​-operative room, the resi‌dent noted that her pupil​s were unequal. Which of ​the following features wo‍uld rule out the possibil​ity of Horner's syndrome ​in this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old lady with advanced spondylosis underwent cervical vertebral fusion surgery.  In the post-operative room, the resident noted that her pupils were unequal.  Which of the following features would rule out the possibility of Horner's syndrome in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enopht‍halmos","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilatat‍ion lag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mydr‍iasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reverse ptosis of lower li‍d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mydriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mydriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Horner's syndrome</strong> causes all the following <strong>except mydriasis</strong>. It <strong>causes miosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Horner's syndrome (oculosympathetic palsy)</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>majority</strong> of the cases are <strong>unilateral</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of <strong>bilateral</strong> involvement include <strong>cervical spine injuries</strong> and <strong>autonomic diabetic neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Painful</strong> Horner syndrome, especially of <strong>acute onset</strong>, should raise the possibility of <strong>carotid dissection</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild <strong>ptosis</strong> (usually 1–2 mm) as a result of weakness of Müller muscle</li>\n<li>Anisocoria is accentuated in dim light</li>\n<li>Pupillary constriction to light and near stimuli is normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypochromic</strong> <strong>heterochromia</strong> (lighter color iris on affected side); in congenital cases.</li>\n<li>Mild ptosis (Mullers muscle is affected)</li>\n<li><strong>Reduced</strong> ipsilateral <strong>sweating </strong>(only if the lesion is <strong>below</strong> the <strong>superior cervical ganglion</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pharmacological tests</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hydroxyamphetamine</strong> and <strong>adrenaline</strong> may be used to differentiate a preganglionic from a postganglionic lesion.</li>\n<li><strong>Phenylepherine</strong> (1%) \n<ul>\n<li>Result: In an <strong>established (10 days) postganglionic lesion</strong>, the <strong>Horner pupil will dilate</strong> and ptosis may be temporarily relieved. A central or preganglionic Horner pupil and a normal pupil will not dilate or will dilate minimally.</li>\n<li>Explanation: In postganglionic Horner's syndrome, the dilator pupillae muscle develops denervation hypersensitivity to adrenergic neurotransmitters due to its dysfunctional local motor nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Apraclonidine</strong> (0.5% or 1.0%)\n<ul>\n<li>Result: A Horner pupil will dilate, but a normal pupil is essentially unaffected.</li>\n<li>Explanation: Alpha-1 receptors are upregulated in the denervated dilator pupillae.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cocaine</strong> (4%) is instilled into both eyes\n<ul>\n<li>Result: The normal pupil will dilate, but the Horner pupil will not.</li>\n<li>Explanation: Cocaine <strong>blocks the re-uptake of noradrenaline</strong> secreted at the postganglionic nerve ending, which accumulates and <strong>causes dilatation</strong> of a normal pupil. <strong>In Horner's syndrome</strong>, there is <strong>no noradrenaline</strong> being secreted, so cocaine has no effect.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6803","text":"Where is the cell body of ‍the second-order neuron i​n the sympathetic pathway‌ for the pupil?","raw_text":"Where is the cell body of the second-order neuron in the sympathetic pathway for the pupil?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle cervi‍cal ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior cervical ganglion‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciliospinal center of Budg‍e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ciliary ‍ganglion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ciliospinal center of Budge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/518c4c15167349adab428ec71dbb6183x1280x2087.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ciliospinal center of Budge</strong></p><p>The cell body of the second-order neuron in the sympathetic pathway for the pupil lies at the ciliospinal center of Budge (C8–T1).</p>\n<p>The sympathetic pathway to the iris muscles consists of a chain of three neurons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The first neuron arises from the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> along the brain stem to synapse in the intermediolateral column of the spinal cord—the <strong>ciliospinal center of Budge (C8-T1)</strong>.</li>\n<li>From here (C8-T1), the second-order neuron arises. This joins the cervical sympathetic chain and synapses at the superior cervical ganglion.</li>\n<li>The third-order neuron arises from the <strong>superior cervical ganglion </strong>and travels through the cavernous sinus along with the internal carotid artery.</li>\n<li>Sympathetic fibers join the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve and pass through the ciliary ganglion to the nasociliary and short ciliary nerves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0952","text":"A simultaneous lesion in b‍oth sympathetic and paras​ympathetic supply of the ‌eye leads to which of the​ following?","raw_text":"A simultaneous lesion in both sympathetic and parasympathetic supply of the eye leads to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mydriatic pupil that react‍s to light","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constricted pupil that rea‍cts to light","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mid-sized pupil that doesn‍’t react to light","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mid-sized pupil that react‍s to light","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mid-sized pupil that doesn’t react to light","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mid-sized pupil that doesn’t react to light</strong></p><p>Simultaneous lesion in both sympathetic and parasympathetic supply of the eye leads to mid-sized pupil that doesn’t react to light.</p>\n<p>Pupillary <strong>dilation</strong> is achieved by <strong>sympathetic</strong> innervation of dilator pupillae. Therefore, any lesion of the sympathetic supply of the pupil leads to constricted pupil.</p>\n<p>Pupillary <strong>constriction</strong> is a <strong>parasympathetic</strong> function. Lesions in this pathway leads to dilated pupil and failure to constrict.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 334\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A mid-sized unreactive pupil due to a lesion of both parasympathetic and sympathetic supplies is seen in aneurysms of the internal carotid artery within the cavernous sinus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0953","text":"During the neurological ex‍amination of a patient, o​n shining light in his le‌ft eye, the left pupil do​es not constrict but the ‌right pupil does. On shin​ing light in his right ey​e, the right pupil constr‍icts and left doesn’t. Th​e defect is in:","raw_text":"During the neurological examination of a patient, on shining light in his left eye, the left pupil does not constrict but the right pupil does. On shining light in his right eye, the right pupil constricts and left doesn’t. The defect is in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right eye‍ afferent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right eye‍ efferent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left eye‍ efferent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left eye‍ afferent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left eye efferent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left eye efferent</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>left eye efferent (oculomotor nerve) defect.</strong></p>\n<p>The loss of pupillary light reflex (both direct and consensual) in the left eye associated with a preserved direct pupillary response in the right eye is characteristic of an efferent pupillary defect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4280","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common feature o​f third nerve palsy, in a‌ case of long-standing, u​ncontrolled diabetes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common feature of third nerve palsy, in a case of long-standing, uncontrolled diabetes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‍tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudo‍ptosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pto‍sis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pupillary ‍dysfunction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ptosis</strong></p><p>The most common feature seen in diabetic oculomotor palsy among the given options is <strong>ptosis.</strong></p>\n<p>In the <strong>anatomical arrangement</strong> of the nerve fibers of the 3<sup>rd</sup> cranial nerve, the <strong>fibers</strong> controlling the <strong>pupillary reflex</strong> are present <strong>superficially</strong> and are thus <strong>spared</strong> from diabetic ischemia-induced injury which starts from the central nerve fibres. Thus, <strong>pupil sparing</strong> is commonly seen in diabetic patients.</p>\n<p>The etiology of diabetic neuropathy is <strong>hyperglycemia-induced</strong> <strong>damage</strong> to nerve cells and <strong>neuronal ischemic</strong> change due to <strong>microvascular angiopathy</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3166\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Involvement of the <strong>third cranial nerve</strong> is most common and it often presents with early features of <strong>ptosis</strong> and <strong>diplopia </strong>with <strong>pupil</strong> <strong>sparing.</strong></p>\n<p>The involvement of <strong>levator palpebrae superioris</strong> muscle of the eyelid manifests as <strong>ptosis</strong> and the involvement of extraocular muscles such as <strong>medial rectus</strong>,<strong>inferior rectus</strong> and <strong>inferior oblique</strong> presents as <strong>opthalmoplegia</strong> with <strong>diplopia</strong>. All the muscles mentioned above are supplied by the Occulomotor nerve.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Aneurysm</strong> of the posterior communicating artery is an important cause of isolated third nerve palsy with <strong>involvement of the pupil. </strong>This is because the aneurysm impinges on the superficial pupil nerve fibres of the 3rd cranial nerve.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1250","text":"A 60-year-old man was brou‍ght to the OPD by his dau​ghter with complaints of ‌drooping of his left eyel​id and double vision. The‌ pupillary examination wa​s normal. What is the lik​ely cause?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was brought to the OPD by his daughter with complaints of drooping of his left eyelid and double vision. The pupillary examination was normal. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurysma‍l rupture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tra‍uma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diab‍etes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Raise‍d ICT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diabetes</strong></p><p>Ptosis and double vision with no other features suggest an acquired, isolated <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> nerve palsy with pupillary sparing</strong>. This is seen in ischemic conditions such as <strong>diabetes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The vasoneurium supplies the nerve from outside to inside. As the somatic fibres are located in the inner part of the nerve, these fibres are affected more in the setting of <strong>ischemia</strong>. The parasympathetic pupillary fibres which run on the outside can remain unaffected (resulting in pupillary sparing). In <strong>compressive</strong> lesions, the parasympathetic fibres are affected before any disruption of the motor fibres. So pupillary sparing is not seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Isolated 3<sup>rd</sup> nerve palsy</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microvascular disease</strong> associated with systemic risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes is the most common cause of third nerve palsy.</li>\n<li><strong>Aneurysm</strong> of the <strong>posterior communicating artery</strong> at its junction with the internal carotid, both direct and secondary to subdural hematoma with uncal herniation.</li>\n<li><strong>Episodic-</strong> migraine, compression, ischemia, and alterations in intracranial pressure</li>\n<li><strong>Miscellaneous-</strong> tumour<strong> </strong>and inflammatory diseases such as <strong>syphilis</strong>, <strong>Lyme</strong> <strong>disease,</strong> and <strong>sarcoidosis;</strong> giant cell arteritis; and <strong>vasculitis</strong> associated with collagen vascular disorders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Signs</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Profound ptosis</strong> due to the weakness of the levator muscle</li>\n<li>Abduction and depression in the primary position (\"<strong>down and out\")</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Normal abduction</strong> as the lateral rectus is intact</li>\n<li>Limited adduction due to medial rectus weakness</li>\n<li>Limited elevation due to the weakness of the superior rectus and inferior oblique</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1251","text":"The patient presented with‍ the following appearance​ when asked to look strai‌ght. What is the probable​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"The patient presented with the following appearance when asked to look straight. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd ner‍ve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Horizontal‍ gaze palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6th ner‍ve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Internuclear ophthalmopleg‍ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6th nerve palsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3bdac4afc6d4b56940737548a74c366.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6th nerve palsy</strong></p><p>The above image shows adduction of the right eye due to paralysis of the right lateral rectus, indicating right <strong>abducens (6<sup>th</sup>) nerve palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>nucleus</strong> of the sixth (abducens or abducent) nerve lies at the <strong>mid-level of the pons.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nuclear lesion: </strong>A nuclear sixth nerve lesion also causes the failure of horizontal gaze towards the side of the damage due to the involvement of the adjacent horizontal gaze center (paramedian pontine reticular formation).</p>\n<p><strong>Foville (inferior medial pontine) syndrome</strong> is most frequently caused by <strong>vascular disease</strong> or <strong>tumors</strong> involving the <strong>dorsal</strong> <strong>pons</strong>. It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Ipsilateral involvement of the fifth to eighth cranial nerves</strong></li>\n<li>Central sympathetic fibers<strong> (Horner syndrome) </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Horizontal gaze palsy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Millard–Gubler (ventral pontine) syndrome</strong> involves the <strong>fasciculus</strong> as it passes through the <strong>pyramidal</strong> <strong>tract. </strong>As well as <strong>ipsilateral sixth nerve palsy</strong>, there is <strong>contralateral hemiplegia</strong> and often an <strong>ipsilateral lower motor neuron (LMN) facial nerve palsy.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Esotropia</strong> in the primary position due to relatively unopposed action of the medial rectus</li>\n<li><strong>Limitation of abduction</strong> on the side of the lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Normal adduction</strong> of the affected eye</li>\n<li>A <strong>compensatory face turn</strong> is towards the side of the paralyzed muscle in unilateral palsy </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"x4b2uTrN","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"22_osteoporosis_and_osteomalacia","title":"22.Osteoporosis And Osteomalacia","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC7733","text":"Following a fall in the ba​throom, an elderly lady i‌s diagnosed with a fractu‍red neck of the femur. Wh‍ich of the following coex​isting conditions is most‍ likely to have predispos​ed to this injury?","raw_text":"Following a fall in the bathroom, an elderly lady is diagnosed with a fractured neck of the femur. Which of the following coexisting conditions is most likely to have predisposed to this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoar​thritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteoporosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteoporosis</strong></p><p>Given the clinical history of an <strong>elderly woman</strong> experiencing a <strong>fracture</strong> following a <strong>trivial fall</strong>, which indicates a <strong>pathological fracture</strong>, the most probable predisposing condition would be <strong>osteoporosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteoporosis is the <strong>most common metabolic bone disorder</strong> characterized by <strong>abnormally low bone mass</strong>, which makes the bone unusually<strong> fragile</strong> and <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>risk of fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>DEXA scan</strong> is performed in the following patients to assess<strong> bone mineral density</strong> and <strong>screen for osteoporosis</strong>, and the results are reported in terms of <strong>T-score</strong> or Z-score:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Females aged 65 years or more </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Males aged 70 years or more</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Post-menopausal females under the age of 65 years with risk factors</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fracture after the age of 50 years</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Males aged 50-69 with risk factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7393","text":"A 65-year-old lady comes t​o you with a DEXA report ‌showing T-score of -2.8 S‍D. Which of the following‍ best describes the patho​physiology of the underly‍ing condition in this lad​y?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old lady comes to you with a DEXA report showing T-score of -2.8 SD. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology of the underlying condition in this lady?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased bone mineralizat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased​ bone mass","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased b​one turnover","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased b​one quality","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased bone mass","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased bone mass</strong></p><p>The DEXA report showing a T-score of -2.8 SD is suggestive of<strong> osteoporosis</strong>. Osteoporosis is characterized by <strong>decreased bone mass</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>T-score</strong> of<strong> -2.5 SD or lower</strong> is suggestive of osteoporosis. In this condition,the <strong>degree of mineralization</strong> remains the <strong>same.</strong> It is characterised by increased bone resorption, decreased bone formation or both. There is also a loss of structural connectivity in the trabecular bones.</p>\n<p>Serum calcium, phosphates and alkaline phosphatase levels are<strong> normal</strong> in osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7370","text":"A 70-year-old lady with co​mplaints of chronic back ‌pain is noted to have kyp‍hosis of the spine. MRI o‍f the spine is shown belo​w. Identify the given app‍earance.","raw_text":"A 70-year-old lady  with complaints of chronic back pain is noted to have kyphosis of the spine. MRI of the spine is shown below. Identify the given appearance.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lincoln lo​g vertebrae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Picture fra​me vertebrae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vertebr​ae plana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cod fish ​vertebrae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cod fish vertebrae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b91288d36a0e48ee8eeb3374103c6aff.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ade565f4202f4cc6867d21c56c3e3d0bx619x805.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cod fish vertebrae</strong></p><p>The MRI of the spine shows decreased bone density with biconcave appearance of vertebrae known as <strong>codfish vertebrae. </strong>The given clinical scenario and the MRI findings are suggestive of <strong>osteoporosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>A patient with osteoporosis most commonly presents with chronic back pain or pathological fractures. The most common osteoporotic fractures are compression fractures of vertebrae which lead to the development of kyphosis. Fractures are also seen in the distal radius (colles' fracture) and proximal femur. </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Lincoln log vertebrae, </strong>also called as H-shaped vertebrae are seen in patients of <strong>sickle cell anemia. </strong>The following image shows the lincoln log vertebrae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Picture frame vertebrae</strong> in which the cortex of the vertebrae is thickened is seen in<strong> Paget's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Vertebrae plana</strong> is seen in a number of conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n<li>Osteogenesis imperfecta</li>\n<li>Osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This occurs in advanced compression fractures when the vertebral body has entirely lost its height both anteriorly and posteriorly.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0735","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not cause os‌teoporosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not cause osteoporosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperprol​actinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic ​sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Systemic sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Systemic sclerosis</strong></p><p>Osteoporosis is not seen in systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>Osteoporosis most commonly occurs in postmenopausal women and in elderly individuals.</p>\n<p>The <strong>secondary causes</strong> of osteoporosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypogonadal states -\n<ul>\n<li>Turner's syndrome</li>\n<li>Klinefelter's syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperprolactinemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Endocrine disorders -\n<ul>\n<li>Cushing's syndrome</li>\n<li> <strong>Hyperparathyroidism</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hematological disorder -\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n<li>Lymphoma</li>\n<li>Leukemia</li>\n<li>Hemophilia</li>\n<li><strong>Thalassemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nutritional and gastrointestinal disorders -\n<ul>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Parenteral nutrition</li>\n<li>Malabsorption syndrome</li>\n<li>Gastrectomy</li>\n<li>Pernicious anemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Rheumatologic disorder -\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Inherited disorders -\n<ul>\n<li>Osteogenesis imperfecta</li>\n<li>Marfan's syndrome</li>\n<li>Ehler's Danlos syndrome</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatasia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Prolonged immobilization</li>\n<li>Pregnancy and lactation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0752","text":"X-ray spine of a patient w​ith chronic back pain sho‌ws characteristic appeara‍nce of cod fish vertebrae‍. What is the investigati​on of choice to diagnose ‍the underlying condition?​","raw_text":"X-ray spine of a patient with chronic back pain shows characteristic appearance of cod fish vertebrae. What is the investigation of choice to diagnose the underlying  condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DE​XA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serum calciu​m, ALP levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone​ scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quantit​ative CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. DEXA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. DEXA</strong></p><p>Cod fish vertebrae in X-ray spine is suggestive of <strong>osteoporosis</strong>. The investigation of choice for the diagnosis of osteoporosis is <strong>dual energy X-ray absorptiometry(DEXA).</strong></p>\n<p>It measures the bone mineral density of an individual. These measurements are made at the lumbar spine or the hip. These measured values are compared using T scores. A<strong> T score ≤ -2.5 SD</strong> is suggestive of <strong>osteoporosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7734","text":"At what age in women is a ​DEXA scan done routinely?‌","raw_text":"At what age in women is a DEXA scan done routinely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At 50​ years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At 55​ years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At 60​ years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At 65​ years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. At 65 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. At 65 years</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry</strong> (DEXA scan) is routinely done in <strong>women at 65 years of age</strong> to <strong>screen </strong>for<strong> osteoporosis</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Osteoporosis is the <strong>most common metabolic bone disorder</strong> characterized by <strong>abnormally low bone mass</strong>, which makes the bone unusually<strong> fragile</strong> and <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>risk of fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The criteria to screen for osteoporosis according to the <strong>national osteoporosis foundation</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Females aged 65 years or more </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Males aged 70 years or more</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Post-menopausal females under the age of 65 years with risk factors</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fracture after the age of 50 years</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Males aged 50-69 with risk factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">A DEXA scan is performed in the above patients to<strong> assess bone mineral density</strong>, and the results are reported in terms of <strong>T-score</strong> or <strong>Z-score</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7375","text":"An elderly lady diagnosed ​with osteoporosis was adv‌ised to start treatment w‍ith bisphosphonates. Sinc‍e the patient refused to ​come to hospital frequent‍ly, she was prescribed a ​drug that is given on yea​rly basis. Which among th​e following is given to t‌his lady?","raw_text":"An elderly lady diagnosed with osteoporosis was advised to start treatment with bisphosphonates. Since the patient refused to come to hospital frequently, she was prescribed a drug that is given on yearly basis. Which among the following is given to this lady?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rised​ronate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zoled​ronate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iband​ronate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alend​ronate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zoledronate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zoledronate</strong></p><p><strong>Zoledronate</strong> is bisphosphonate that is administered once a year.</p>\n<p>The drugs used in the treatment of osteoporosis which <strong>decrease bone resorption</strong> include bisphosphonates, denosumab, and SERMs (selective estrogen receptor modulators).</p>\n<p><strong>Bisphosphonates</strong> decrease bone resorption by impairing osteoclast function. The bisphosphonates approved for the treatment of osteoporosis are <strong>alendronate</strong>, <strong>risedronate</strong>,<strong> ibandronate</strong>, and<strong> zoledronate</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alendronate and risedronate can be administered daily or weekly with different dosages.</li>\n<li>Ibandronate can be administered daily, weekly or quarterly.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoledronate</strong> is administered <strong>annually</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Denosumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody with an affinity for<strong> RANKL</strong>. RANKL stimulates osteoclast that results in bone resorption. By inhibiting the RANKL, denosumab prevents activation of osteoclast and decreases bone resorption.</p>\n<p><strong>SERMs</strong>:<strong> Raloxifene</strong> and <strong>tamoxifen</strong> are approved for the treatment of osteoporosis. Estrogen decreases bone resorption. Thus decrease in estrogen in postmenopausal females results in increased bone resorption leading to osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7377","text":"A patient diagnosed with o​steoporosis having a T-sc‌ore of -4.5 SD is treated‍ with synthetic parathyro‍id hormone. Which of the ​following drugs is given ‍to this patient?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with osteoporosis having a T-score of -4.5 SD is treated with synthetic parathyroid hormone. Which of the following drugs is given to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teripa​ratide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Romos​ozumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cinac​alcet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salca​tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Teriparatide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Teriparatide</strong></p><p>Synthetic <strong>parathyroid hormone</strong> given to a patient with osteoporosis is <strong>teriparatide</strong>. In low and intermittent doses, it leads to <strong>bone formation</strong>.In high and continuous doses, it leads to bone resorption.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Romosozumab</strong> is an <strong>anti- sclerostin </strong>antibody. Sclerostin inhibition increases bone formation and, to a lesser extent, decreases bone resorption.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Cinacalcet</strong> inhibits the parathyroid hormone secretion by activating the <strong>calcium-sensing receptors</strong> (CaSR). </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Salcatonin</strong> inhibits osteoclastic bone resorption. It is used in the treatment of Paget's disease and osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0762","text":"Which of the following dru​gs decreases the bone res‌orption and increases bon‍e formation?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs decreases the bone resorption and increases bone formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teripa​ratide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alend​ronate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calci​tonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strontium​ ranelate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Strontium ranelate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Strontium ranelate</strong></p><p><strong>Strontium ranelate</strong> decreases bone resorption and increases bone formation.</p>\n<p>Strontium resembles calcium and moves into bone matrix resulting in increased bone formation. Osteoclasts cannot remove strontium, thus decreasing bone resorption. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1677","text":"An elderly lady who is on ​bisphosphonate therapy fo‌r 4 years comes with comp‍laints of hip pain. What ‍is the next investigation​ to be done in this patie‍nt?","raw_text":"An elderly lady who is on bisphosphonate therapy for 4 years comes with complaints of hip pain. What is the next investigation to be done in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plain ra​diograph","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin ​D levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum AL​P levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DEXA​ scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plain radiograph","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plain radiograph</strong></p><p>The next investigation to be done in a patient with complaints of hip pain following prolonged bisphosphonate therapy is <strong>plain radiograph</strong> to rule out <strong>atypical femur fracture</strong>. </p>\n<p>Prolonged use of<strong> bisphosphonates</strong> is associated with two potential <strong>side effects</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteonecrosis </strong>of the<strong> jaw </strong>usually following a dental procedure</li>\n<li><strong>Atypical femur fracture - </strong>occurs in the subtrochanteric femoral region or along the shaft distal to the lesser trochanter </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0674","text":"A 55 year old female compl​ains of lower backache. T‌he radiographic image of ‍her lumbosacral spine is ‍given below. What is the ​probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 55 year old female complains of lower backache. The radiographic image of her lumbosacral spine is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ankylosing ​spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse idiopathic skeleta​l hyperostosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal oste​odystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteoporosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28c863e81c7441d6a2108a0653ecd1e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteoporosis</strong></p><p>The radiographic image of an <strong>elderly female's</strong> lumbosacral spine shows <strong>ballooning of disc spaces</strong>, resulting in the characteristic <strong>biconcave appearance of vertebral bodies</strong> known as the <strong>'codfish' appearance</strong>, typically seen in <strong>osteoporosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteoporosis is a common metabolic bone disease characterised by a <strong>diffuse reduction </strong>in<strong> bone density</strong> due to a <strong>decrease </strong>in<strong> bone mass</strong>.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for osteoporosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increasing age</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Female sex</strong></li>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Previous history of fragility fractures</li>\n<li>Family history of hip fracture</li>\n<li>Low body mass index- less than 18.5 kg/m<sup><span class=\"s1\">2</span></sup></li>\n<li>Excessive usage of glucocorticoids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Osteoporosis presents with fractures that occur without any trauma or as a result of low-impact trauma. The commonly associated fractures are fractures of the distal part of the radius (<strong>Colle's fracture</strong>), fractures of the<strong> hip</strong>, and fractures of the <strong>vertebrae</strong>. In severe cases, the fracture of vertebrae can cause <strong>kyphosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Radiological findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>decreased vertical height </strong>of the vertebra due to collapse</li>\n<li><strong>Ground glass appearance</strong> - especially in bones like pelvis</li>\n<li><strong>Codfish appearance</strong> - it occurs due to bulging of the disc into the adjacent vertebral bodies so that it becomes biconcave.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>DEXA</strong> (dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry) is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for the diagnosis of osteoporosis.<strong> T-score less than</strong> <strong>-2.5</strong> in the lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip indicates osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6691","text":"Which of the following tes​ts are not commonly used ‌in osteoporosis? DEXA Bon‍e scan Quantitative CT Ch‍emical analysis X-ray","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following tests are not commonly used in osteoporosis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":769,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"1. DEXA\"}'>DEXA</span></li>\n<li>Bone scan</li>\n<li>Quantitative CT</li>\n<li>Chemical analysis</li>\n<li>X-ray</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, 4​ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 & 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 &amp; 3</strong></p><p>Bone scan and quantitative CT are <strong>not commonly used</strong> in the diagnosis of osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>Bone Scan is obsolete whereas QCT measures volumetric bone density. However, this <strong>QCT</strong> is expensive and has <strong>higher risk of exposure to radiation</strong> when compared to DEXA, hence it is not much in use</p>\n<p>The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for the diagnosis of osteoporosis is<strong> </strong>dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry <strong>(DEXA). </strong>A<strong> T-score &lt;–2.5 </strong>is defined as<strong> osteoporosis. </strong>DEXA measures the bone mineral density of an individual. These measurements are made at the lumbar spine, femoral neck, or hip. </p>\n<p>Radiological features of osteoporosis<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ballooning of disc spaces<strong> </strong>leading to the biconcave shape of the vertebral body, known as <strong>codfish appearance </strong>(classically seen in osteoporosis but also seen in osteomalacia and hyperparathyroidism)</li>\n<li><strong>Loss of vertical height </strong>of vertebra due to collapse </li>\n<li><strong>Ground glass</strong> appearance of bones<strong> </strong>especially in the bones like the pelvis</li>\n<li>Loss of cortical bone (<strong>picture frame vertebrae</strong>) and trabecular bone (<strong>ghost vertebrae</strong>)</li>\n<li>Loss of trabeculae in proximal femur area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chemical tests</strong> like serum calcium, phosphorus and alkaline phosphatase levels are usually normal. But, osteoblastic and osteoclastic markers in urine &amp; blood can help in diagnosing and monitoring treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7413","text":"Which of the following is ​a disorder of bone minera‌lization?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a disorder of bone mineralization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de45c35f9b3f41c9ba766b3d736ad421x1280x1434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteomalacia</strong></p><p><strong>Osteomalacia</strong> is a disorder of bone mineralization. Osteomalacia means <strong>softening</strong> of the bone. There is incomplete calcification of osteoid throughout the skeleton.</p>\n<p>The <strong>disorders of bone mineralization</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rickets</li>\n<li>Osteomalacia</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rickets and osteomalacia are different expressions of the same disease resulting from deficient mineralization of the bone. <strong>Defective mineralization </strong>of the epiphyseal growth plates in <strong>children</strong> causes <strong>rickets</strong> and defective mineralization of the bone matrix in <strong>adults</strong> causes <strong>osteomalacia</strong>. It leads to fragile bones with a high risk of fractures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7437","text":"Which of the following con​ditions shows looser zone‌s, codfish vertebrae, and‍ champagne glass pelvis i‍n radiographic images?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions shows looser zones, codfish vertebrae, and champagne glass pelvis in radiographic images?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paget's​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6edd757c6f349068c9bebb5fa1dd72cx720x703.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/871512fe087e4102a679f343b1d4874dx720x1676.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ef1c7836be04a95aad95f68bf57a099x720x904.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteomalacia</strong></p><p>Looser zones, codfish vertebrae, and champagne glass pelvis are seen in<strong> osteomalacia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Loosers zones </strong>are the classical lesions of osteomalacia. These are due to<strong> incomplete stress fractures</strong> caused by the arterial pulsations which heal with callus-lacking in calcium. They are thin transverse bands of rarefaction especially seen in the pelvis, the shaft of long bones such as femur neck and the axillary edge of the scapula<strong>. </strong>They are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteomalacia (<strong>characteristic</strong>)</li>\n<li>Renal osteodystrophy</li>\n<li>Fibrous dysplasia</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Paget's disease </li>\n<li>Osteogenesis Imperfecta </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows<strong> Looser's zone.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Codfish vertebrae </strong>are the biconcave vertebral bodies seen in osteomalacia. They are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Osteomalacia</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>codfish vertebrae.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Champagne glass pelvis </strong>is due to bone softening leading to lateral indentation of acetabula. It is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteomalacia</li>\n<li>Achondroplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7057","text":"Which tumor does not cause​ osteomalacia and phospha‌turia?","raw_text":"Which tumor does not cause osteomalacia and phosphaturia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemangiop​ericytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibros​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breast c​arcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breast carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breast carcinoma</strong></p><p>Breast carcinoma does not cause osteomalacia and phosphaturia.</p>\n<p>Tumor-induced osteomalacia (<strong>TIO</strong>) is a rare paraneoplastic syndrome which presents with hypophosphatemia, osteomalacia and renal phosphate wasting. It is most commonly seen with <strong>mesenchymal tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tumors associated with osteomalacia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemangiopericytoma (option B)</li>\n<li>Non-ossifying fibroma</li>\n<li>Osteosarcoma (option A)</li>\n<li>Osteoblastoma</li>\n<li>Fibrosarcoma (option C)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6YFQ0_EQ","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"22_neonatal_respiratory_disorders","title":"22.Neonatal Respiratory Disorders","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA0421","text":"The following image shows ‌the chest X-ray of a pret‌erm neonate who was admit​ted to the NICU in view o​f severe respiratory dist‍ress. What does this sugg​est?","raw_text":"The following image shows the chest X-ray of a preterm neonate who was admitted to the NICU in view of severe respiratory distress.  What does this suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meconium aspiration syndro‌me","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaline memb‌rane disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transient tachypnoea of ne‌wborn","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchopulmonary dysplasia‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyaline membrane disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e4844d80d374529952ccd89c2225999.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b0e3f7efb9f4f75866b447a9463a883x720x304.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyaline membrane disease</strong></p><p>From the given clinical picture of respiratory distress in a <strong>premature</strong> neonate and the X-ray findings of diffuse ground-glass (reticulo-granular) opacities with a characteristic white-out appearance<strong>,</strong> <strong>hyaline membrane disease</strong> (HMD) is the most likely diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>HMD</strong>, also known as neonatal <strong>Respiratory distress syndrome</strong> (RDS) is a relatively common condition resulting from insufficient production of surfactant that occurs in preterm neonates. Maternal diabetes, perinatal asphyxia, and multiple gestations are other known risk factors for the condition. Clinical features include tachypnea, chest retractions, expiratory grunting, and nasal flaring. The infant may or may not be cyanosed. </p>\n<p>X-ray features of HMD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Air bronchogram sign</li>\n<li>Diffuse ground-glass opacities</li>\n<li>Obliteration of cardiac and diaphragmatic contours giving rise to 'white-out' lung (indicates severe disease)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Chest radiograph <strong>(A)</strong> shows reticulogranular opacities throughout both lungs with prominent air bronchograms. The cardiac silhouette is preserved and both lungs are hypoaerated.</p>\n<p>Chest radiograph <strong>(B)</strong> shows more severe changes. There is obliteration of the cardiac and diaphragmatic contours due to marked opacification, giving a complete <strong>‘white out’</strong> <strong>appearance</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0423","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most important risk f‌actor for neonatal respir​atory distress syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important risk factor for neonatal respiratory distress syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perinatal‌ Asphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prema‌turity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caesarea‌n section","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"History of affected siblin‌g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prematurity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prematurity</strong></p><p><strong>Respiratory distress syndrome</strong> occurs primarily in <strong>premature infants.</strong> Its incidence is inversely related to gestational age and birthweight. It occurs in <strong>60–80% of infants</strong> delivered at <strong>&lt;28 wk</strong> of gestational age, in 15–30% of those delivered between 32 and 36 weeks of gestational age, and rarely in those delivered at &gt;37 wk of gestational age.</p>\n<p>Other risk factors include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal diabetes</li>\n<li>Multiple births</li>\n<li>Cesarean delivery</li>\n<li>Asphyxia</li>\n<li>History of previously affected siblings</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0424","text":"A father runs to a PHC wit‌h a baby wrapped in a tow‌el. The neonate was deliv​ered a few hours back, at​ home, by the local dai. ‍On initial examination, t​he medical officer notes ​cyanosis and severe tachy​pnoea. Which of the follo‍wing features would favor‌ a diagnosis of neonatal ​respiratory distress synd​rome in this child?","raw_text":"A father runs to a PHC with a baby wrapped in a towel. The neonate was delivered a few hours back, at home, by the local dai. On initial examination, the medical officer notes cyanosis and severe tachypnoea. Which of the following features would favor a diagnosis of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patchy coarse infiltrates ‌on chest X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air bronchogram on chest X‌-ray","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Emaciated appearance with ‌palmar and plantar crease‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onset of distress after 6 ‌hours of birth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Air bronchogram on chest X-ray","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Air bronchogram on chest X-ray</strong></p><p><strong>Air bronchogram</strong> on chest x-ray is suggestive of <strong>neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Other chest x-ray findings in respiratory distress syndrome (RDS):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fine reticulogranular pattern</li>\n<li>Bilateral diffuse ground-glass opacities</li>\n<li>White-out lung (severe disease)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Patchy coarse infiltrates</strong> are associated with <strong>meconium aspiration syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Reduced fat under the skin resulting in an<strong> emaciated appearance</strong> as well as <strong>prominent creases</strong> is a feature of <strong>post-term babies.</strong> Respiratory distress syndrome is more common among preterm babies </p>\n<p>Option D: Onset of <strong>respiratory distress syndrome</strong> is <strong>within 6 hours</strong> of birth. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0425","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the tre‌atment of RDS with surfac​tant is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the treatment of RDS with surfactant is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is given in neonates wi‌th RDS who fail CPAP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is given prophylactical‌ly to all neonates at &amp;lt‌; 31 weeks gestation.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can be given as rescue ‌treatment when RDS actual‌ly develops.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repeated dosing is given e‌very 6-12 hr for a total ‌of 2 to 4 doses.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is given prophylactically to all neonates at < 31 weeks gestation.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is given prophylactically to all neonates at &lt; 31 weeks gestation.</strong></p><p>Surfactant is given <strong>prophylactically to all neonates &lt; 28 weeks, </strong>not 31 weeks. It is given irrespective of the presence or absence of RDS. The <strong>route</strong> of administration is <strong>intratracheal.</strong></p>\n<p>Surfactant is indicated in all infants with <strong>moderate to severe RDS</strong>. It is also given as a <strong>rescue treatment</strong> when CPAP fails.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0426","text":"A first-year resident rece‌ives a call from the casu‌alty regarding an unbooke​d primigravida, at 32 wee​ks of gestation. She has ‍presented with regular pa​inful uterine contraction​s. On examination, her ce​rvix is 2cm dilated. What‍ dose of betamethasone in‌jection should the reside​nt give her?","raw_text":"A first-year resident receives a call from the casualty regarding an unbooked primigravida, at 32 weeks of gestation. She has presented with regular painful uterine contractions. On examination, her cervix is 2cm dilated. What dose of betamethasone injection should the resident give her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6mg q12h‌; 2 doses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12mg q24h‌; 2 doses","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12mg q6h‌; 4 doses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6mg q12h‌; 4 doses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 12mg q24h; 2 doses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 12mg q24h; 2 doses</strong></p><p>The woman is currently in preterm labour and the dose of antenatal <strong>betamethasone</strong> given for avoiding complications of prematurity is <strong>12mg IM every 24 hours (total 2 doses).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> is used, the dose is <strong>6mg IM every 12 hours (total 4 doses).</strong></p>\n<p>Corticosteroid administration is recommended for all women in<strong> preterm labour</strong> (&lt;34 weeks of gestation).</p>\n<p>Antenatal steroid administration reduces the risk of the following: (RINN)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory distress syndrome (<strong>R</strong>DS)</li>\n<li>Intraventricular haemorrhage (<strong>I</strong>VH)</li>\n<li>Necrotizing enterocolitis (<strong>N</strong>EC)</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>eurodevelopmental delay</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0427","text":"A 30-year-old woman with a‌ history of recurrent lat‌e-trimester abortions is ​in preterm labor, presume​d to be due to chorioamni‍onitis. She has received ​one dose of betamethasone​ injection. This is a pre​cious pregnancy and the r‍elatives are concerned ab‌out prematurity-related c​omplications in the baby.​ Which of the following i‌s useful for assessing lu‌ng maturity in this fetus‍?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with a history of recurrent late-trimester abortions is in preterm labor, presumed to be due to chorioamnionitis. She has received one dose of betamethasone injection. This is a precious pregnancy and the relatives are concerned about prematurity-related complications in the baby. Which of the following is useful for assessing lung maturity in this fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L/S ‌ratio","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bilirubin content of amnio‌tic fluid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultra‌sound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amnioc‌entesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. L/S ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. L/S ratio</strong></p><p>The <strong>lecithin–sphingomyelin ratio (L/S ratio)</strong> is an amniotic fluid test used to assess fetal lung maturity.<strong> L/S ratio of &lt; 2</strong> in amniotic fluid indicates <strong>lung immaturity</strong>. </p>\n<p>Surfactant starts appearing in the fetal lung by <strong>20 weeks</strong> of gestation. Levels indicating lung maturity are detected <strong>after 35 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Surfactant is a complex mixture of lipids, proteins, and carbohydrates produced by<strong> type II pneumocytes</strong> in the lung epithelium. The absence of surfactant in the liquid film lining the alveoli causes an increase in surface tension and alveolar collapse. If not treated, such atelectasis leads to increased work of breathing, intrapulmonary shunting, ventilation-perfusion mismatch, hypoxia, and eventually respiratory failure. </p>\n<p>The relative concentration of the phospholipids lecithin (L) and sphingomyelin (S) is an indicator of lung maturity. The outward flow of pulmonary secretions from the fetal lungs into the amniotic fluid maintains the level of lecithin and sphingomyelin equally until 32–33 weeks gestation when the lecithin concentration begins to increase significantly while sphingomyelin remains nearly the same. An L–S ratio of 2.5 or more indicates fetal lung maturity while an L/S ratio of less than 1.5 is associated with a high risk of infant respiratory distress syndrome.</p>\n<p>Other tests done to assess fetal lung maturity are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shake test/bubble test</li>\n<li>Phosphatidyl glycerol measurement</li>\n<li>Surfactant:albumin ratio</li>\n<li>Lamellar body counts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3494","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indicator of fetal‌ lung maturity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indicator of fetal lung maturity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lecithin-sphingomyelin rat‌io &amp;gt; 2.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive ‌shake test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased phosphatidyl gly‌cerol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nile blue test showing &amp;gt‌;50% of blue cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nile blue test showing >50% of blue cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nile blue test showing &gt;50% of blue cells</strong></p><p><strong>Nile blue test </strong>showing <strong>&gt;50% orange cells</strong> is a sign of fetal lung maturity, not blue cells.</p>\n<p>Tests for the assessment of <strong>fetal lung maturity</strong> include: </p>\n<p>The <strong>Nile blue test</strong> involves staining desquamated fetal cells from amniotic fluid with 0.1% Nile blue sulfate. The presence of <strong>orange cells &gt; 50%</strong> suggests<strong> lung maturity. </strong><strong>Lecithin/sphingomyelin ratio</strong> at 31 to 32 weeks is 1, and at 35 weeks ratio is 2. The <strong>ratio ≥2</strong> indicates lung maturity (option A). </p>\n<p>The <strong>shake test</strong> or <strong>bubble test</strong> (<strong>Clement's test</strong>) (option B) involves mixing <strong>amniotic fluid</strong> with <strong>95% ethanol</strong>, shaking it for 15 seconds, and observing it for 15 minutes. A <strong>complete ring of</strong> <strong>bubbles</strong> at the meniscus is a <strong>positive test</strong> and indicates lung maturity. This is a useful bedside test.</p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>phosphatidylglycerol</strong>, (option C) <strong>saturated phosphatidylcholine</strong> and <strong>lamellar body count (&gt;50,000/mcL)</strong> indicates lung maturity.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> accelerate <strong>lung maturation</strong> and <strong>lower</strong> the incidence of <strong>respiratory distress</strong> and <strong>neonatal mortality</strong> in preterms if the birth was delayed for at least 24 hours after initiation of betamethasone. A single course of corticosteroids is recommended for women between <strong>24 and 36 weeks</strong> who are at risk of delivery within 7 days. Intramuscular injections of either<strong> 2 doses of 12 mg</strong> <strong>betamethasone</strong> are given <strong>24 hourly</strong> or <strong>4 doses of 6 mg dexamethasone</strong> are given <strong>12 hourly</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0429","text":"A female infant was born i‌n a community hospital by‌ spontaneous vaginal deli​very at 34 weeks of gesta​tion. 30 minutes later, t‍he baby’s breathing becam​e excessively laboured. D​espite providing non-inva​sive ventilation, the bab‍y died. Post-mortem histo‌pathological examination ​of the lung showed thick ​pink membranes lining the‌ small, collapsed alveola‌r spaces and necrotic deb‍ris, mainly within the re‌spiratory bronchioles, al‌veolar ducts, and unopene‍d alveoli. What are these‍ membranes chiefly compos​ed of?","raw_text":"A female infant was born in a community hospital by spontaneous vaginal delivery at 34 weeks of gestation. 30 minutes later, the baby’s breathing became excessively laboured. Despite providing non-invasive ventilation, the baby died. Post-mortem histopathological examination of the lung showed thick pink membranes lining the small, collapsed alveolar spaces and necrotic debris, mainly within the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and unopened alveoli. What are these membranes chiefly composed of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphati‌dylcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fib‌rin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucop‌rotein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complex pol‌ysaccharide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4100ce6c80f64fdaa4da6220b53f6fbd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrin</strong></p><p>The history of respiratory distress in a premature baby (34 weeks) and a biopsy showing pink (hyaline) membranes in the alveoli is indicative of <strong>hyaline membrane disease</strong> (HMD). The hyaline membranes are composed of layers of <strong>fibrin-rich edema fluid</strong> and necrotic cells of lining alveoli. Diffuse ischemic damage to the alveoli causes the transudation of proteins into the alveoli to form the hyaline membrane.</p>\n<p>Hyaline membrane disease (HMD), also known as infantile respiratory distress syndrome (IRDS) or respiratory distress syndrome of newborns (RDS) is a syndrome in premature infants caused by developmental insufficiency of pulmonary surfactant production and structural immaturity of the lungs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0428","text":"A primigravida was taken u‌p for emergency cesarean ‌section in view of placen​tal abruption, at 32 week​s of gestation. Immediate‍ly after delivery, you dr​y the baby, clear secreti​ons from the mouth and no​se, and provide tactile s‍timulation. But even afte‌r 30 seconds, you note th​e baby is still gasping f​or breath and oxygen satu‌ration has not increased ‌more than 92%. Which is t‍he next best step?","raw_text":"A primigravida was taken up for emergency cesarean section in view of placental abruption, at 32 weeks of gestation. Immediately after delivery, you dry the baby, clear secretions from the mouth and nose, and provide tactile stimulation. But even after 30 seconds, you note the baby is still gasping for breath and oxygen saturation has not increased more than 92%. Which is the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mechanical ‌ventilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CP‌AP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"100% ‌oxygen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surfactant and ventilation‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CPAP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CPAP</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause </strong>of<strong> respiratory distress</strong> in a <strong>preterm </strong>infant is <strong>neonatal RDS </strong>(hyaline membrane disease). The infant has presented with moderate respiratory distress without any signs of respiratory failure so early <strong>continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP)</strong> is the appropriate treatment.</p>\n<p>CPAP started at birth is the choice for the management of a preterm infant at risk for respiratory distress syndrome (RDS). Early nasal CPAP has shown to lower mortality and risk of bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Room air is better than oxygen for the resuscitation of preterm infants, and 100% oxygen is also harmful, causing increased oxidative stress.</p>\n<p>The intubate-surfactant-extubate (INSURE) on CPAP technique is recommended for <strong>surfactant</strong> administration when babies showed signs of<strong> RDS</strong> and <strong>needed more than 30% inspired oxygen</strong> to maintain saturation in the normal range.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0434","text":"A 3-hour-old neonate with ‌apnea has been on bag and‌ mask ventilation for the​ last 30 seconds. She has​ now started taking spont‍aneous breaths. The heart​ rate is still between 10​0 and 110/min. What is yo​ur next step?","raw_text":"A 3-hour-old neonate with apnea has been on bag and mask ventilation for the last 30 seconds. She has now started taking spontaneous breaths. The heart rate is still between 100 and 110/min. What is your next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discontinue‌ ventilation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Continue v‌entilation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Give chest ‌compression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ET int‌ubation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Discontinue ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Discontinue ventilation</strong></p><p>As per the NRP algorithm, at any time during resuscitation<strong>, </strong>if the<strong> HR &gt; 100</strong> and<strong> spontaneous breathing </strong>resumes then<strong> discontinue ventilation</strong>.<br/>The components of NRP resuscitation include: (TABCD)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>T</strong>emperature- maintain between 36.5 and 37.5 C</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>irway- \"sniffing\" position, clear secretions if needed</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>reathing- free flow O2 (maintain sats 91–95%), PPV (40–60 breaths /min), ETT</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>irculation- chest compressions:ventilation ratio (3:1 = 90:30; lower 1/3rd sternum; two thumb technique preferred)</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>rugs- Epinephrine, volume expanders </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Algorithm states:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HR &lt; 100/min</strong> + no respirations →  Positive pressure ventilation (<strong>PPV)</strong></li>\n<li>No response with PPV →<strong> </strong>Endotracheal intubation (<strong>ET</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>HR &lt; 60/min</strong> → <strong>Chest compressions</strong></li>\n<li>No response to chest compressions → <strong>epinephrine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7110","text":"A preterm infant in the NI‌CU was observed to have m‌arked nasal flare, distin​ct xiphoid retractions, m​inimal intercostal retrac‍tions, and inspiratory la​g with an audible grunt. ​What would be the Silverm​an Anderson score?","raw_text":"A preterm infant in the NICU was observed to have marked nasal flare, distinct xiphoid retractions, minimal intercostal retractions, and inspiratory lag with an audible grunt. What would be the Silverman Anderson score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌8","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8389224afa548be8afcd8bc995b38c8x1280x1839.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8</strong></p><p>The Silverman-Anderson score of the child would be <strong>8</strong>.</p>\n<p>Calculation of the Silverman-Anderson score of this child: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 272px;\" width=\"239\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81.3516px;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8438px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Finding</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Score</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81.3516px; text-align: left;\">Expiratory grunt</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8438px; text-align: center;\">Audible with the naked ear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8906px; text-align: center;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81.3516px; text-align: left;\">Nasal flaring</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8438px; text-align: center;\">Marked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8906px; text-align: center;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81.3516px; text-align: left;\">Xiphoid retraction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8438px; text-align: center;\">Marked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8906px; text-align: center;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81.3516px; text-align: left;\">Intercostal retraction (lower chest)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8438px; text-align: center;\">Minimal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8906px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81.3516px; text-align: left;\">Chest movement (upper chest)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8438px; text-align: center;\">Lag </td>\n<td style=\"width: 66.8906px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> Total score: 8</p>\n<p>A score of <strong>more than 7</strong> indicates<strong> respiratory failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Silverman-Anderson score</strong> is a commonly used score to <strong>assess respiratory distress</strong>. It helps to assess the progression of distress over time and initiate treatment. It is more suited for <strong>preterms</strong> with <strong>respiratory distress syndrome</strong>. Scoring is to be done every half an hour and charting is carried out to monitor progress. </p>\n<p>The image below shows Silverman Anderson retraction score: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Downes’ Score</strong> is also used for assessing the severity of respiratory distress. It is more comprehensive and can be applied to any gestational age and condition. Downe’s<strong> score of &gt;7</strong> means <strong>impending respiratory failure.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 191px;\" width=\"621\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Score</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\"><strong>Respiratory rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">&lt;60/min</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">60–80/min</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">&gt;80/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\"><strong>Air entry</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Mild decrease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Marked decrease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\"><strong>Cyanosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Nil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">In-room air</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">On &gt;40% O2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\"><strong>Grunting</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Only on auscultation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Audible with the naked ear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\"><strong>Retraction</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Nil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.25px;\">Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0433","text":"As part of calculating Dow‌ne's score for a baby, yo‌u would assess all of the​ following except:","raw_text":"As part of calculating Downe's score for a baby, you would assess all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyan‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal ‌flaring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Air ‌entry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respirat‌ory rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal flaring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal flaring</strong></p><p>Downe's score does not consider <strong>nasal flaring.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>severity of respiratory distress</strong> is assessed by Downe’s score and Silverman–Anderson score. <strong>Downe’s score</strong> can be applied for <strong>any gestational age</strong>. Downe’s score of <strong>&gt;7</strong> means impending respiratory failure. Silverman–Anderson score is more suited for preterms with hyaline membrane disease (HMD).</p>\n<p><strong>Downe's scoring</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Score</strong></td>\n<td><strong> 0</strong></td>\n<td><strong> 1</strong></td>\n<td><strong> 2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Respiratory rate</td>\n<td>&lt;60/min</td>\n<td> 60–80/min</td>\n<td>&gt;80/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air entry</td>\n<td> Normal</td>\n<td>Mild decrease</td>\n<td>Marked decrease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cyanosis</td>\n<td> Nil</td>\n<td>In room air</td>\n<td>On &gt;40% O2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grunting</td>\n<td> None</td>\n<td>Only on auscultation</td>\n<td>Audible with naked ear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retraction</td>\n<td> Nil</td>\n<td>Mild</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0430","text":"Your brother-in-law calls ‌you during one of your ni‌ght duties and tells you ​that your sister just del​ivered a baby girl and th‍at both the mother and th​e child are doing well fo​llowing the cesarean deli​very. The next morning, h‍owever, you get a call th‌at the baby started havin​g trouble breathing and h​as been shifted to the NI‌CU. He also sends you a p‌hoto of her chest X-ray v‍ia WhatsApp. On the radio‌graph, you note streaky, ‌perihilar linear densitie‍s and fluid in the horizo‍ntal fissure. Which of th​e following is true regar​ding the given clinical c‌ondition?","raw_text":"Your brother-in-law calls you during one of your night duties and tells you that your sister just delivered a baby girl and that both the mother and the child are doing well following the cesarean delivery. The next morning, however, you get a call that the baby started having trouble breathing and has been shifted to the NICU. He also sends you a photo of her chest X-ray via WhatsApp. On the radiograph, you note streaky, perihilar linear densities and fluid in the horizontal fissure. Which of the following is true regarding the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only observation and suppo‌rtive therapy is needed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can predispose to bronc‌hopulmonary dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More common in preterm inf‌ants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Management involves surfac‌tant administration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Only observation and supportive therapy is needed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd07535baff44951aec4d4dd3f470691x557x219.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Only observation and supportive therapy is needed</strong></p><p>Benign respiratory distress in a newborn with a chest X-ray showing <strong>fluid in the interlobar fissure</strong> is indicative of <strong>transient tachypnoea of newborn (TTN)</strong>, also known as retained fetal lung liquid syndrome. The management of TTN is supportive <strong>oxygen therapy</strong> and <strong>observation.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is more common after a <strong>term, cesarean delivery</strong> and is due to the delayed clearance of the lung fluid from the interstitium into the pulmonary lymphatics.</p>\n<p>Other findings of chest x-ray imaging are prominent (perihilar) pulmonary vascular markings, increased interstitial markings, and a “wet silhouette” around the heart. </p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"whole_rhythm\" id=\"F0007\">\n<div class=\"caption\">\n<p> </p>\n<p id=\"__p17\">Chest radiograph <strong>(A)</strong> on day 1 at 6 hours shows a bilateral diffuse ground-glass appearance and fine granularity due to interstitial opacities.</p>\n<p>Chest radiograph <strong>(B)</strong> of the same patient at the age of 2 days (B) shows that the pulmonary parenchymal opacities have decreased, though perihilar streaky markings are still present.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Treatment is supportive and minimal oxygen treatment (&lt; 40%) is often adequate; infants usually <strong>recover within 3 days.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0435","text":"A preterm, extremely low b‌irth weight baby, deliver‌ed by emergency caesarian​ section was admitted to ​the NICU and treated for ‍respiratory distress synd​rome. She responded to tr​eatment and was discharge​d. At 6 months of age, sh‍e is still having recurre‌nt respiratory distress a​nd requires supplemental ​oxygen at home. The baby ‌was diagnosed with bronch‌opulmonary dysplasia. Whi‍ch of the following does ‌not cause this clinical c‌omplication?","raw_text":"A preterm, extremely low birth weight baby, delivered by emergency caesarian section was admitted to the NICU and treated for respiratory distress syndrome. She responded to treatment and was discharged. At 6 months of age, she is still having recurrent respiratory distress and requires supplemental oxygen at home. The baby was diagnosed with bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Which of the following does not cause this clinical complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen therapy in the NICU‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mechanical trauma to the l‌ungs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhalation of nitrous oxid‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patent Ductu‌s Arteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhalation of nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9363aa9487641e1a00373c13bfe65a3x1280x1784.5980253878702.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhalation of nitrous oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Inhalation of nitrous oxide (N2O) </strong>is <strong>not associated</strong> with bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD). Inhaled NO is a pulmonary vasodilator approved for the treatment of hypoxic respiratory failure in term and near-term infants with pulmonary hypertension. It may have a role in preventing BPD.</p>\n<p>BPD is a pathologic process leading to clinical features of chronic lung disease that originates in the neonatal period and is defined by an oxygen requirement for 28 days postnatally. Inflammatory injury to the developing lungs due to <strong>barotrauma</strong> and <strong>oxygen toxicity </strong>resulting from<strong> prolonged mechanical ventilation </strong>is the key factor driving the pathogenesis of BPD. Together with other antenatal and postnatal factors, it results in aberrant tissue repair, dysregulated vascular and arrested alveolar development, which is the basis of the pathology seen in BPD.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0440","text":"A female newborn was deliv‌ered at 40 weeks of gesta‌tion by emergency C-secti​on, following signs of se​vere fetal distress. Gree‍nish amniotic fluid was n​oted. Immediately followi​ng delivery, the baby was​ shifted to the NICU and ‍had to be intubated. Her ‌chest X-Ray is as below. ​What does it show?","raw_text":"A female newborn was delivered at 40 weeks of gestation by emergency C-section, following signs of severe fetal distress. Greenish amniotic fluid was noted. Immediately following delivery, the baby was shifted to the NICU and had to be intubated. Her chest X-Ray is as below. What does it show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mediasti‌nal shift","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ground glas‌s opacities","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluid in the interlobar fi‌ssure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patchy infiltrates and coa‌rse streaking","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patchy infiltrates and coarse streaking","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7148095bf7b245168b7ff6818a157cb5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patchy infiltrates and coarse streaking</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>patchy infiltrates and coarse streaking,</strong> which are characteristic findings in the chest X-ray image of infants with <strong>meconium aspiration syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0431","text":"A newborn infant, delivere‌d at home, at 42 weeks of‌ gestation, became cyanot​ic and started breathing ​rapidly. The child was im‍mediately taken to the ne​arby primary health centr​e. As the medical officer​ prepares to intubate, sh‍e notes greenish staining‌ on the vocal cords. Whic​h of the following is thi​s baby likely to develop?‌","raw_text":"A newborn infant,  delivered at home, at 42 weeks of gestation, became cyanotic and started breathing rapidly. The child was immediately taken to the nearby primary health centre. As the medical officer prepares to intubate, she notes greenish staining on the vocal cords. Which of the following is this baby likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liste‌riosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pathologic‌al jaundice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstructiv‌e emphysema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meconiu‌m ileus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obstructive emphysema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4121032c96c141f2aad7b004668a6fb4x1280x1538.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obstructive emphysema</strong></p><p>Respiratory distress in a post-term neonate with a history of greenish (meconium-stained) amniotic fluid during delivery is suggestive of meconium aspiration syndrome (MAS). The aspirated meconium can block the large and small airways, causing patchy <strong>atelectasis</strong> and <strong>emphysema. </strong>This produces a ventilation-perfusion mismatch. Severe overinflation can lead to air leak syndromes such as pneumothorax. </p>\n<p>Meconium consists of water, desquamated GIT cells, skin, lanugo hair, vernix, bile salts, pancreatic enzymes, lipids, and mucopolysaccharides. The bile salts and pancreatic enzymes can cause <strong>chemical pneumonitis</strong>. Meconium can also cause <strong>surfactant inactivation</strong> by displacing it from the alveolar surface and inhibiting its surface tension-lowering ability. Severe MAS is associated with remodelling and thickening of the muscular walls, resulting in <strong>pulmonary hypertension.</strong> </p>\n<p>Infants who develop MAS typically present in the first 12 hours after birth with varying degrees of respiratory distress. On inspection, many affected infants have 'barrel chests'; crackles (rales) and rhonchi often can be heard on auscultation. In severe cases, the entire lungs can be involved resulting in a total “whiteout” of the lungs on the radiograph.</p>\n<p><strong>Listeriosis</strong> is an infection that is acquired from the mother and is reported to be rarely associated with meconium-stained amniotic fluid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0439","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about apnea of prem‌aturity (AOP)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about apnea of prematurity (AOP)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Usually resolves by 37 wee‌ks post conceptional age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gentle tactile stimulation‌ is often adequate for mi‌ld to moderate episodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent AOP can be treat‌ed with caffeine or theop‌hylline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AOP is a risk factor for S‌udden infant death syndro‌me (SIDS)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. AOP is a risk factor for Sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. AOP is a risk factor for Sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS)</strong></p><p>Apnea of prematurity <strong>(AOP)</strong> is <strong>not a risk</strong> factor for sudden infant death syndrome <strong>(SIDS)</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is defined as <strong>cessation of breathing for longer than 20 seconds</strong> or for any duration if accompanied by <strong>cyanosis or bradycardia. </strong>It is a diagnosis of exclusion. It is seen in <strong>preterm</strong> infants and usually resolves by the 37th week of gestation. Recurrent apnea of prematurity may be treated with caffeine or theophylline. It does not predict the future occurrence of SIDS.</p>\n<p><strong>Sudden Infant Death Syndrome</strong> (SIDS) is the sudden death of an infant <strong>under one year</strong> of age, which remains <strong>unexplained</strong> after a thorough case investigation, including the performance of a complete autopsy, examination of the death scene, and review of the clinical history.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4427","text":"While monitoring the respi‌ratory rate of the infant‌s in the NICU, you notice​ one baby which suddenly ​stops breathing. Which of‍ the following statements​ is true in this regard?","raw_text":"While monitoring the respiratory rate of the infants in the NICU, you notice one baby which suddenly stops breathing. Which of the following statements is true in this regard?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary apnea is character‌ized by labile BP and tac‌hycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caffeine therapy should be‌ given in primary apnea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary apnea responds t‌o stimulation by establis‌hing normal breathing.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central asphyxia can cause‌ secondary apnea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Central asphyxia can cause secondary apnea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Central asphyxia can cause secondary apnea</strong></p><p><strong>Secondary apnea </strong>can occur as a central CNS meditated asphyxia or due to neuromuscular disorders. </p>\n<p>Apnea is the <span data-token-index=\"1\"><strong>cessation</strong> of <strong>breathing</strong> for <strong>&gt;20 seconds</strong> </span>or<span data-token-index=\"3\"> for <strong>any duration</strong> </span>if <strong>accompanied</strong> by <strong>bradycardia</strong> and <strong>cyanosis.</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 252px;\" width=\"453\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary Apnea </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Secondary Apnea</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px;\">Failure to initiate respiratory effort at birth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px;\">Apnea developing in an infant who has initiated a respiratory effort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px;\">There may be bradycardia and decreased tone. BP remains normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px;\">\n<p>Can originate in CNS due to asphyxia or peripherally due to neuromuscular disorders</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px;\">Respond to tactile stimulation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.132px;\">Require ventilatory assistance and drugs- caffeine or theophylline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7595","text":"A 15-day-old infant had co‌njunctivitis, which later‌ developed into respirato​ry distress and pneumonia​. Chest X-ray examination‍ revealed bilateral lung ​infiltrates. Peripheral s​mear count showed 14,300/​dL (N 43, L 54, E3). Whic‍h of the following is the‌ most likely pathogenic o​rganism?","raw_text":"A 15-day-old infant had conjunctivitis, which later developed into respiratory distress and pneumonia. Chest X-ray examination revealed bilateral lung infiltrates. Peripheral smear count showed 14,300/dL (N 43, L 54, E3).  Which of the following is the most likely pathogenic organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria ‌gonorrhoeae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycoplasma‌ pneumonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p>The organism most likely to cause the mentioned infections is<strong> Chlamydia trachomatis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pneumonia</strong> caused by C. trachomatis can develop in 10–20% of infants born to <strong>women</strong> with <strong>active, untreated</strong> chlamydial <strong>infection.</strong> Only approximately 25% of infants with nasopharyngeal chlamydial infection develop pneumonia.</p>\n<p><strong>C. trachomatis pneumonia of infancy</strong> has a very characteristic presentation. It usually occurs between <strong>1-3 months</strong> of age and is insidious, with a persistent <strong>cough, tachypnea,</strong> and <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>fever.</strong> Auscultation reveals <strong>rales;</strong> wheezing is uncommon.</p>\n<p>The absence of fever and wheezing helps to distinguish C. trachomatis pneumonia from respiratory syncytial virus pneumonia.</p>\n<p>A distinctive laboratory finding is the presence of <strong>peripheral eosinophilia (&gt;400 cells/µL).</strong> <br/>The most consistent finding on <strong>chest radiograph</strong> is<strong> hyperinflation</strong> accompanied by<strong> minimal interstitial or alveolar infiltrates.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0437","text":"The radiologist calls and ‌confirms a Bochdalek hern‌ia in a baby born with mu​ltiple congenital anomali​es. The baby had been pla‍ced under an oxygen hood ​in the NICU, in view of p​ostnatal respiratory dist​ress. On repeat evaluatio‍n, he continues to have s‌ternal retractions and is​ not maintaining saturati​on. Which of the followin‌g would be your initial c‌linical intervention in t‍his case?","raw_text":"The radiologist calls and confirms a Bochdalek hernia in a baby born with multiple congenital anomalies. The baby had been placed under an oxygen hood in the NICU, in view of postnatal respiratory distress. On repeat evaluation, he continues to have sternal retractions and is not maintaining saturation. Which of the following would be your initial clinical intervention in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intubate and insert a naso‌gastric tube","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bag and mask‌ ventilation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insert a central venous pr‌essure line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventilate with a high-freq‌uency ventilator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intubate and insert a nasogastric tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intubate and insert a nasogastric tube</strong></p><p>Once the diagnosis of congenital diaphragmatic hernia is made or suspected, all patients are<strong> intubated. </strong>Also a<strong> </strong><strong>nasogastric <span class=\"highlightedCurrent\" id=\"mark_text_5\">tube, </span></strong>connected to continuous suction, is placed in the stomach to reduce lung compression. </p>\n<p>Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) is a birth defect of the diaphragm. The most common type of CDH is a <strong>Bochdalek hernia</strong> (postero-lateral diaphragmatic hernia); other types include Morgagni hernia (anterior-parasternal), diaphragm eventration, and central tendon defects of the diaphragm. Malformation of the diaphragm allows the abdominal organs to push into the chest cavity, hindering proper lung formation and expansion.</p>\n<p class=\"bulletIndent2\"><strong><span class=\"glyph\">Immediate steps of management: </span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"bulletIndent2\"><span class=\"glyph\">Endotracheal intubation → </span>Placement of a nasogastric <span class=\"highlightedCurrent\" id=\"mark_text_6\">tube</span>  <span class=\"glyph\">→ </span>Administration of fluids and inotropic agents <span class=\"glyph\">→ </span>Administration of surfactant therapy to <strong>only</strong> preterm infants with findings suggestive of respiratory distress syndrome.<strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Bag and mask ventilation is to be avoided</strong> as they lead to <span class=\"nowrap\">gastric/abdominal</span> distension and compression of the lung.</p>\n<p>Option C: Both arterial and central venous lines are inserted; however, this is not the first line of intervention.</p>\n<p>Option D: To support respiratory failure, the first modality to be used is conventional ventilation, and if there is no improvement, then high-frequency ventilation is used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0438","text":"A newborn delivered vagina‌lly, at term, was diagnos‌ed with congenital diaphr​agmatic hernia. The most ​important limiting factor‍ for survival in this con​dition is ________.","raw_text":"A newborn delivered vaginally, at term, was diagnosed with congenital diaphragmatic hernia. The most important limiting factor for survival in this condition is ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mediasti‌nal shift","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left-sid‌ed hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intestinal ‌obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ‌hypoplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary hypoplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary hypoplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary hypoplasia</strong> is a very poor prognostic factor in congenital diaphragmatic hernia.</p>\n<p>Poor prognostic indicators in CDH: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The presence of liver in the thoracic cavity </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pulmonary hypoplasia,</strong> pulmonary hypertension </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early respiratory distress, within 24 h after birth </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Herniation to the contralateral lung (<strong>right-sided hernia)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0436","text":"You are doing a quick pre-‌op evaluation of a 2-day-‌old baby shifted for emer​gency pediatric surgery. ​Parents had brought him w‍ith a history of sudden, ​noisy breathing. He appea​red cyanotic with decreas​ed breath sounds on the l‍eft side of the chest. Ch‌est X-ray taken in the ca​sualty is given below. Wh​ich of the following is n‌ot true about the underly‌ing condition?","raw_text":"You are doing a quick pre-op evaluation of a 2-day-old baby shifted for emergency pediatric surgery. Parents had brought him with a history of sudden, noisy breathing. He appeared cyanotic with decreased breath sounds on the left side of the chest. Chest X-ray taken in the casualty is given below. Which of the following is not true about the underlying condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Not associated with dextro‌cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Most commonly seen on the ‌left side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mortality is usually assoc‌iated with pulmonary hypo‌plasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary dilators markedl‌y improve the survival","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Not associated with dextrocardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d54c6672a594c98aaf5c67977719c98.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Not associated with dextrocardia</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture of neonatal respiratory distress, absent breath sounds in the left side of the chest, and an X-ray image showing bowel loops in the thoracic cavity is diagnostic of <strong>congenital diaphragmatic hernia</strong> (CDH). Though less commonly documented, dextrocardia is seen in association with CDH.</p>\n<p>A diaphragmatic hernia is defined as a communication between the abdominal and thoracic cavities with or without abdominal contents in the thorax. It is mostly congenital, with a defective formation of the pleuroperitoneal membrane. <strong>Females</strong> are affected twice as often as males.</p>\n<p><strong>Bochdalek hernia</strong> accounts for up to 90% of the hernias seen in the newborn period, with 80-90% occurring on the <strong>left side. </strong>So, the heart will be pushed to the right side (dextroposition).</p>\n<p>Predictors of a poor prognosis include an associated major anomaly, symptoms before completion of 24 hours since birth, severe <strong>pulmonary hypoplasia</strong>, herniation to the contralateral lung, and the need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO).</p>\n<p>Nitric oxide is a selective pulmonary vasodilator. It reduces ductal shunting and pulmonary pressures, resulting in improved oxygenation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"N8EsmMzR","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"22_g6pd_deficiency_and_autoimmune_hemolytic_anemias","title":"22.G6Pd Deficiency And Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemias","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA0297","text":"A 29-year-old African Amer‌ican man receives primaqu‌ine chemoprophylaxis befo‌re traveling for a busine‌ss trip. Four days later,​ he presents with jaundic‌e and dark urine. Laborat‍ory investigations show a​nemia, reticulocytosis, a​nd unconjugated hyperbili‌rubinemia. The peripheral​ blood smear shows the pr​esence of Heinz bodies. W‌hat is the most likely mo​de of inheritance of the ‍given clinical condition?‌","raw_text":"A 29-year-old African American man receives primaquine chemoprophylaxis before traveling for a business trip. Four days later, he presents with jaundice and dark urine. Laboratory investigations show anemia, reticulocytosis, and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. The peripheral blood smear shows the presence of Heinz bodies. What is the most likely mode of inheritance of the given clinical condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitoch‌ondrial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-Linked ‌recessive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. X-Linked recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96c69098a15d4751a3efc81b8f560625x1280x1125.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. X-Linked recessive</strong></p><p>The given clinical history suggestive of hemolysis after the antimalarial (primaquine) and Heinz bodies on PBF, points to a diagnosis of <strong>G6PD deficiency </strong>(Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency). It is inherited as an <strong>X-linked recessive </strong>trait.</p>\n<p><strong>G6PD</strong> is an enzyme belonging to the hexose monophosphate shunt (<strong>HMP</strong>) <strong>pathway</strong>. <strong>Deficiency</strong> of enzymes of this pathway causes <strong>hemolysis</strong> as the protective ability of RBCs from <strong>oxidative stress</strong> is impaired.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the Heinz bodies seen on peripheral smear.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0299","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common trigger f‌or hemolysis in patients ‌with G6PD deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common trigger for hemolysis in patients with G6PD deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dr‌ugs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fava ‌beans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dehyd‌ration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infection</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>trigger</strong> for hemolysis in G6PD deficiency is <strong>infection.</strong> Viral hepatitis, pneumonia, and typhoid are the common infections that trigger hemolysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Episodic hemolysis</strong> (both intravascular and extravascular) is seen in G6PD deficiency. It is due to exposures that cause <strong>oxidative stress</strong>. The <strong>triggers</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections - oxygen-derived free radicals produced by activated leukocytes</li>\n<li>Drugs\n<ul>\n<li>Antimalarials - primaquine and chloroquine </li>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Nitrofurantoin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Diet - fava bean</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0300","text":"Peripheral smear of a pati‌ent with viral hepatitis ‌is given below. Which of ‌the following statements ‌is false about this findi​ng?","raw_text":"Peripheral smear of a patient with viral hepatitis is given below. Which of the following statements is false about this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"These are seen in G6PD def‌iciency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are due to denaturati‌on of globin chains","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are plucked out by ma‌crophages in the spleen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They do not cause intravas‌cular hemolysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They do not cause intravascular hemolysis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6593107d447b4292a48687c94e98bdf7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9211d6ed9a824c9c9b31ecf57e8c6206x1280x914.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6c802e794f4440e984188d5c202f8d6x1280x1125.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/447765ae36314006a24a3feab9801c46x1279x914.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0bf3819ff534d3094472d148c06fcd3x1280x1125.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They do not cause intravascular hemolysis</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear shows <strong>Heinz bodies</strong> which are dark stained inclusion bodies in red cells. They can cause <strong>intravascular </strong>as well as<strong> extravascular hemolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In<strong> G6PD-deficiency</strong>, the RBCs are exposed to high levels of oxidants. This causes <strong>cross-linking</strong> of reactive<strong> sulfhydryl </strong>groups on <strong>globin chains</strong>. The globin chains become <strong>denatured</strong> and form membrane-bound <strong>precipitates</strong> known as <strong>Heinz bodies.</strong> These can be detected by supravital staining.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Heinz bodies can damage the membrane sufficiently to cause <strong>intravascular hemolysis.</strong></p>\n<p>As RBCs with Heinz bodies pass through the <strong>spleen</strong>, the <strong>macrophages</strong> pluck out the Heinz bodies. As a result, some of these <strong>partially devoured</strong> cells retain an abnormal shape i.e. as if a bite was taken out of them. Such cells are called <strong>bite cells</strong>. Bite cells are trapped in splenic cords and removed by phagocytes leading to <strong>extravascular hemolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The images given below show Heinz bodies and bite cells seen in G6PD deficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0301","text":"A patient with G6PD defici‌ency was suspected to hav‌e acute hemolytic episode‌. Which of the following ‌features would you expect​ to see in this patient? ‌Anaemia Splenomegaly Hemo‍globinemia Cholelithiasis​ Hemoglobinuria","raw_text":"<p>A patient with G6PD deficiency was suspected to have acute hemolytic episode. Which of the following features would you expect to see in this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Anaemia</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Hemoglobinemia</li>\n<li>Cholelithiasis</li>\n<li>Hemoglobinuria</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Acute</strong> <strong>hemolysis</strong> in G6PD deficiency is <strong>intravascular</strong>. This is marked by <strong>anaemia</strong>, <strong>hemoglobinemia</strong>, and <strong>hemoglobinuria</strong>.</p>\n<p>These episodes are self-limited and intermittent. <strong>Reticulocytosis</strong> occurs during the <strong>recovery</strong> <strong>phase</strong>. Hence, features related to chronic hemolysis (e.g., splenomegaly, cholelithiasis) are absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0298","text":"A 23-year-old food blogger‌ presents with complaints‌ of icterus and abdominal‌ fullness. She returned y‌esterday from a vacation ​in Egypt where she tried ‌fava beans. Her periphera‍l smear is given below. T​his condition is protecti​ve against which of the f‌ollowing malarial parasit​es?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old food blogger presents with complaints of icterus and abdominal fullness. She returned yesterday from a vacation in Egypt where she tried fava beans. Her peripheral smear is given below. This condition is protective against which of the following malarial parasites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Falci‌parum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vi‌vax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ov‌ale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mala‌riae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Falciparum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c57484a70f4e47f9994c93d737d4b1a3.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4dc646e0a7344b62b2f3ad958aed8038x1279x914.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fed3123012943dd8852893c0cf05c83x1279x914.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Falciparum</strong></p><p>The peripheral smear shows RBCs with anisopoikilocytosis with polychromasia and many <strong>bite cells</strong> (arrows) suggestive of <strong>G6PD deficiency</strong>. This condition is <strong>protective</strong> against <em>Plasmodium <strong>falciparum</strong></em> malaria.</p>\n<p><strong>Malarial parasites</strong> preferentially <strong>invade RBCs</strong> which are <strong>rich</strong> <strong>in</strong> <strong>G6PD</strong>. G6PD deficiency causes oxidative stress and hemolysis, preferably in the older RBCs. Reticulocytes and young erythrocytes have much higher G6PD activities and are less susceptible to oxidant damage. </p>\n<p>P. falciparum can invade erythrocytes of all ages. P. vivax, and P. ovale invade reticulocytes (immature erythrocytes) preferentially. Hence, a low G6PD level in older RBC is protective against <em>P. falciparum</em> malaria.</p>\n<p>Episodic hemolysis in this condition can be <strong>triggered</strong> by <strong>fava beans</strong> which is a staple in Egyptian cuisine.</p>\n<p>The images given below show Heinz bodies and bite cells seen in G6PD deficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0362","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about‌ G6PD-deficiency anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about G6PD-deficiency  anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolytic episodes occur b‌oth intravascularly and e‌xtravascularly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G6PD Mediterranean deficie‌ncy is the most severe fo‌rm.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Older RBCs are more prone ‌to hemolysis than younger‌ ones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemolysis continues as lon‌g as there is exposure to‌ oxidant drugs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemolysis continues as long as there is exposure to oxidant drugs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemolysis continues as long as there is exposure to oxidant drugs</strong></p><p><strong>Hemolysis</strong> in G6PD-deficiency anemia does not continue for the entire period that the patient takes the offending drug. It <strong>stops</strong> after a <strong>few days.</strong></p>\n<p>Two variants of G6PD, designated as<strong> G6PD<sup>–</sup></strong> and <strong>G6PD Mediterranean</strong>, cause most of the clinically significant hemolytic anemias. The half-life of G6PD<sup>-</sup> is moderately reduced, whereas that of G6PD Mediterranean is markedly reduced; hence, the severity of deficiency is greater with the Mediterranean variant. The most common normal variant is <strong>G6PD B</strong>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96007358\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96007358\">The <strong>hemolysis</strong> in<strong> G6PD deficiency</strong> is<strong> </strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96007358\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96007358\">both <strong>extravascular</strong> and <strong>intravascular</strong> hemolysis. </span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW96007358\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'>The damaged red cells are <strong>sequestered</strong> in the <strong>spleen</strong> and killed by <strong>macrophages,</strong> causing <strong>extravascular</strong> hemolysis. The </span><span class=\"EOP SCXW96007358\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'><strong>oxidative</strong> <strong>stresses</strong> can denature hemoglobin, resulting in <strong>intravascular</strong> hemolysis.</span> </p>\n<p>In individuals with G6PD deficiency, acute intravascular hemolysis begins 2 to 3 days after exposure to oxidants. Only <strong>older</strong> <strong>RBCs</strong> are at risk for <strong>lysis</strong>. The <strong>hemolytic</strong> <strong>episode</strong> is <strong>self-limited</strong> because hemolysis stops when only<strong> younger</strong> <strong>G6PD-replete</strong> red cells remain, even if the patient continues to take the offending drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0323","text":"What class of antibodies a‌re involved in the most c‌ommon form of immunohemol‌ytic anaemia?","raw_text":"What class of antibodies are involved in the most common form of immunohemolytic anaemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌ G","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌ A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌ M","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig G a‌nd Ig M","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ig G","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ig G</strong></p><p><strong>Warm antibody type</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> form of <strong>immunohemolytic anaemia</strong>. Most of the causative <strong>antibodies</strong> in this condition belong to the <strong>IgG </strong>class<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Immunohemolytic anaemia is caused by antibodies that cause premature destruction of the red cells. Based on the temperature at which the different types of antibodies bind to the RBCs stably, they are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Warm antibody type - most common</li>\n<li>Cold agglutinin type</li>\n<li>Cold hemolysin type</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0385","text":"Match the immunohemolytic ‌anaemia with the type of ‌hemolysis. Type Hemolysis‌ 1. Warm antibody A. Intr‌avascular 2. Cold aggluti​nin B. Extravascular and ‌intravascular 3. Cold hem‍olysin C. Extravascular","raw_text":"<p>Match the immunohemolytic anaemia with the type of hemolysis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 154px; width: 364px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.078px; text-align: center;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.922px; text-align: center;\">Hemolysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.078px; text-align: left;\">1. Warm antibody</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.922px; text-align: left;\">A. Intravascular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.078px; text-align: left;\">2. Cold agglutinin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.922px; text-align: left;\">B. Extravascular and intravascular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.078px; text-align: left;\">3. Cold hemolysin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.922px; text-align: left;\">C. Extravascular</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-B, 2‌-A, 3-C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-C, 2‌-A, 3-B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-C, 2‌-B, 3-A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2‌-C, 3-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-C, 2-B, 3-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-C, 2-B, 3-A</strong></p><p>The type of hemolysis seen in the different immunohemolytic anemia are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 137px;\" width=\"361\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\"><strong>Hemolysis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Warm antibody</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Extravascular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Cold agglutinin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Extravascular and intravascular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Cold hemolysin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Intravascular</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Autoimmune hemolytic anemias (AIHA) cause both extravascular and intravascular hemolysis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>warm antibody</strong> type, hemolysis is <strong>extravascula</strong><strong>r</strong>. <strong>IgG-coated</strong> red cells are <strong>phagocytosed</strong> and destroyed in the <strong>spleen</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>cold agglutinin</strong> type, hemolysis is <strong>extravascular</strong> and <strong>intravascular.</strong> In the peripheral vascular bed (low temperature), IgM binds to the red cells. This causes agglutination and complement-fixing. They are unbound when they reach the general circulation. However, the <strong>IgM interaction</strong> causes deposition of sublytic quantities of <strong>C3b</strong>. It is an excellent opsonin, which leads to the removal of RBCs by <strong>phagocytes</strong> in the spleen, liver, and bone marrow.</p>\n<p><strong>Cold hemolysin</strong> type is a rare subtype of AIHA. Hemolysis is <strong>intravascular</strong>. <strong>IgG </strong>binds to the <strong>P blood group antigen</strong> on the red cells, which leads to <strong>complement-mediated</strong> lysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0388","text":"A 30-year-old woman was br‌ought to the casualty wit‌h loss of consciousness. ‌On examination, pallor, i‌cterus and splenomegaly w​ere noted. Her past histo‌ry was significant for me‍ningitis 2 weeks ago whic​h was treated with iv ant​ibiotics. Lab investigati‌ons showed anemia and inc​reased osmotic fragility.​ Which of the following s‌tatements is false regard​ing the probable diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman was brought to the casualty with loss of consciousness. On examination, pallor, icterus and splenomegaly were noted. Her past history was significant for meningitis 2 weeks ago which was treated with iv antibiotics. Lab investigations showed anemia and increased osmotic fragility. Which of the following statements is false regarding the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rh blood group complex are‌ involved in the pathogen‌esis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LDH levels are always norm‌al","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direct Coombs agglutinatio‌n test is diagnostic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral smear shows sph‌erocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. LDH levels are always normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. LDH levels are always normal</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of drug-induced <strong>warm antibody</strong> type of <strong>immunohemolytic anemia</strong>. In this condition,<strong> </strong>primarily <strong>extravascular</strong> hemolysis is present. <strong>LDH</strong> may be <strong>increased</strong> when there is hemolysis.</p>\n<p>Two different <strong>mechanisms</strong> for <strong>drug-induced</strong> immune hemolytic anemias are:</p>\n<p><strong>Antigenic</strong> drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anemia occurs 1-2 weeks after initiation of large IV dose of the offending drug</li>\n<li>These drugs bind to red cell membrane and are recognised by anti-drug antibodies</li>\n<li>They can cause both extravascular and intravascular hemolysis</li>\n<li>E.g. penicillin, cephalosporins, quinidine</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tolerance-breaking </strong>drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antibodies are produced against red cell antigens, particularly Rh antigens</li>\n<li>E.g. alpha-methyl dopa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0325","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about pa‌roxysmal cold hemoglobinu‌ria?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG class of antibodies ar‌e involved","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibodies are directed ag‌ainst Rh antigen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Complement-mediated lysis ‌occurs at 37°C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It mostly occurs in childr‌en following a viral infe‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antibodies are directed against Rh antigen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antibodies are directed against Rh antigen</strong></p><p>In paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (<strong>PCH</strong>),<strong> antibodies</strong> are directed against the<strong> P </strong>blood group<strong> antigen.</strong></p>\n<p>PCH is a <strong>cold hemolysin type </strong>of hemolysis caused by<strong> IgG</strong> autoantibodies classically. They are also called <strong>Donath Landsteiner antibodies </strong>and<strong> </strong>are<strong> </strong>produced often in response to a viral infection. This antibody is a <strong>biphasic hemolysin. </strong>i.e., the antibody <strong>binds</strong> to RBC at <strong>colder</strong> temperature but <strong>hemolysis</strong> occurs at core temperature <strong>37°C</strong>.</p>\n<p>This can cause substantial, sometimes fatal, intravascular hemolysis, and hemoglobinuria. Most cases are seen in <strong>children</strong> following<strong> viral infections </strong>and usually recover within 1 month.</p>\n<p>Immuno-hemolytic anaemias are of 3 types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 276px;\" width=\"390\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">Antibody type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">Antigen on the RBC surface </td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.547px;\">Temperature for hemolysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">Warm agglutinin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">IgG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">Rh antigen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.547px;\">37°C or higher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">Cold agglutinin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">IgM</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">\n<p>I antigen (adults)</p>\n<p>i antigen (infants)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.547px;\">\n<p>30°C, usually 0-10°C</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">Cold hemolysin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">IgG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4844px;\">P antigen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.547px;\">Biphasic hemolysis (37°C)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0326","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is microangiop‌athic hemolytic anaemia m‌ost commonly seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is microangiopathic hemolytic anaemia most commonly seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disseminated intravascular‌ coagulation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni‌c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac valv‌e prosthesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Disseminated intravascular coagulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/569f43f669f54acfa40e39b6b3a7a9f7x754x594.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong></p><p><strong>Microangiopathic hemolytic anaemia</strong> is most commonly seen with <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation. </strong></p>\n<p>It occurs in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)</li>\n<li>Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)</li>\n<li>Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS)</li>\n<li>Malignant hypertension</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Disseminated cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A common pathogenic feature in these disorders is a <strong>microvascular lesion</strong>. Deposition of fibrin and platelets result in <strong>luminal narrowing</strong>. These vascular changes produce <strong>shear stresses.</strong> These changes mechanically injure the passing red cells producing <strong>schistocytes </strong>or<strong> helmet cells</strong> on the peripheral smear.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows schistocytes or helmet cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"L_TwgiHD","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"22_anti_manic_drugs","title":"22.Anti Manic Drugs","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC9508","text":"Which is a mood stabiliser‌ with an anti-suicidal ro​le?","raw_text":"Which is a mood stabiliser with an anti-suicidal role?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamot‌rigine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lithium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lithium</strong></p><p><strong>Lithium</strong> is a mood stabilizer with an<strong> antisuicidal role</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance</strong> treatment with <strong>lithium</strong> has shown to<strong> decrease</strong> the rate of<strong> suicide</strong> in<strong> mood disorders</strong>. Lithium was found to be better than other mood stabilizers such as carbamazepine, lamotrigine, and valproate.</p>\n<p>Lithium is also used in recurrent depression with a cyclic pattern is controlled by either lithium or imipramine. It is also used to augment standard antidepressant response in acute major depression in those patients who have had an inadequate response to monotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2381","text":"A 25-year-old man is broug‌ht to the psychiatry OPD ​by his mother with compla​ints that he has been beh‌aving aggressively for th​e last 10 days. He believ‍es he owns the biggest au‌tomobile company in the w​orld and talks about how ​his made-up company will ​put satellites into space​ someday. She also says t​hat he sleeps for only 3 ‌hours a day. Which of the‌ following drugs cannot b​e used to treat this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man is brought to the psychiatry OPD by his mother with complaints that he has been behaving aggressively for the last 10 days. He believes he owns the biggest automobile company in the world and talks about how his made-up company will put satellites into space someday.  She also says that he sleeps for only 3 hours a day. Which of the following drugs cannot be used to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium v‌alproate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamot‌rigine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lamotrigine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lamotrigine</strong></p><p>The clinical features given are suggestive of <strong>acute mania</strong> in this patient and lamotrigine is not indicated in this condition.</p>\n<p>It requires a <strong>slow, extended titration</strong> to minimize the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome and is used for <strong>maintenance therapy</strong> in<strong> bipolar disorder</strong>. </p>\n<p>Lithium, sodium valproate, carbamazepine, and atypical antipsychotics are indicated in acute mania.</p>\n<p>Lithium is indicated in both acute mania and maintenance therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2384","text":"A patient is being treated‌ with lithium for manic-d​epressive psychosis. Whic​h of the following is tru‌e about the use of this d​rug?","raw_text":"A patient is being treated with lithium for manic-depressive psychosis.  Which of the following is true about the use of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be given alone for acu‌te episodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum concentration monito‌ring is not used for dose​ adjustment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cause benign and reversibl‌e flattening of T wave on​ ECG","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Causes significant weight ‌loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cause benign and reversible flattening of T wave on ECG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cause benign and reversible flattening of T wave on ECG</strong></p><p><strong>Prolonged</strong> use of <strong>lithium</strong> causes a <strong>benign</strong> and <strong>reversible flattening</strong> of <strong>T-wave.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Lithium is usually<strong> not given</strong> <strong>alone </strong>in<strong> acute episodes</strong> as the onset of antimanic action is slow. Benzodiazepines, anticonvulsants, or atypical antipsychotics are administered along with lithium in the early phases of treatment.</p>\n<p>Option B: Lithium has a very <strong>narrow therapeutic index </strong>and monitoring of serum concentration is used for guiding dose adjustment.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Lithium causes significant <strong>weight gain</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2387","text":"A patient on lithium thera‌py is diagnosed to be hyp​ertensive. Which of the f​ollowing antihypertensive‌ drugs is contraindicated​ here?","raw_text":"A patient on lithium therapy is diagnosed to be hypertensive. Which of the following antihypertensive drugs is contraindicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clon‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium chan‌nel blockers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thiazide diuretics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thiazide diuretics</strong></p><p><strong>Thiazides</strong> are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients who are on <strong>lithium therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Thiazide diuretics cause significant<strong> reductions</strong> in<strong> Li<sup><span style=\"text-decoration: line-through;\">+</span></sup> clearance</strong> that result in <strong>toxic</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>The <strong>K+ sparing diuretics</strong> have modest effects on lithium excretion, with a mild increase in serum levels. </p>\n<p>Loop diuretics have a limited impact on Li<sup>+</sup> serum levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2392","text":"A patient with a history o‌f mania, on lithium thera​py, is brought with compl​aints of vomiting and mul‌tiple episodes of diarrhe​a. On examination, coarse‍ tremors and an ataxic ga‌it are noted. What is the​ expected level of serum ​lithium in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of mania, on lithium therapy, is brought with complaints of vomiting and multiple episodes of diarrhea. On examination, coarse tremors and an ataxic gait are noted. What is the expected level of serum lithium in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 1.‌2 mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 0.‌8 mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 1.‌5 mEq/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 0.‌5 mEq/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. > 1.5 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt; 1.5 mEq/L</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Clinical features are suggestive of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> lithium toxicity, </strong>where serum<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> </strong><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\">levels </span><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">&gt;1.5 mEq/L </strong>are considered<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> toxic.  </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Lithium has a <strong>narrow therapeutic index (0.8–1.2 mEq/L)</strong> and therefore requires monitoring to suspect toxicity. The toxic effects of lithium usually start manifesting when the serum level crosses 1.5 mEq/L.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Acute intoxication is characterized by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Vomiting</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Profuse diarrhea</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Coarse tremors</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Ataxia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Coma</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Convulsions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A fine action tremor is common in patients taking lithium at therapeutic doses and can develop at any time during treatment. Therapeutic lithium tremor is generally symmetric, limited to the arms, and nonprogressive.Tremor from lithium toxicity is <strong>coarser</strong> and <strong>more disabling</strong>, may also affect the legs, and occurs with other symptoms of toxicity.</p>\n<p>Serious effects of lithium toxicity will involve confusion, hyperreflexia, and seizures. Motor and cognitive side effects can become irreversible with<strong> persistent cerebellar tremor</strong> being the most common. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2393","text":"At what serum lithium leve‌ls would you consider dia​lysis in acute lithium to​xicity?","raw_text":"At what serum lithium levels would you consider dialysis in acute lithium toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 2.‌0 mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 4.‌0 mEq/L","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 3.‌0 mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 1.‌5 mEq/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. > 4.0 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &gt; 4.0 mEq/L</strong></p><p>In acute overdoses, <strong>hemodialysis</strong> is indicated in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serum lithium level is<strong> &gt; 4.0 mEq/L</strong></li>\n<li>Patients exhibiting <strong>symptoms </strong>of toxicity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: While hemodialysis is considered when serum lithium level is 4.0 mEq/L and above, signs of lithium toxicity usually start showing when the serum level is &gt; 1.5 mEq/L. One should not get confused between the two. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2395","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions do ​not warrant lithium thera​py?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions do not warrant lithium therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemotherapy induced neutr‌openia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Major de‌pression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasculogen‌ic headache","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generalised anxiety disord‌er","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Generalised anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Generalised anxiety disorder</strong></p><p>Lithium is not indicated in generalized anxiety disorder.</p>\n<p><strong>Bipolar disorder </strong>is the primary indication for<strong> lithium. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Effect of leukocytosis may hasten recovery from cancer chemotherapy-induced leukopenia and agranulocytosis.</p>\n<p>Option B: Unipolar depression can be treated with lithium as an adjuvant in resistant cases.    </p>\n<p>Option C: Vasculogenic headaches like cluster headaches. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1323","text":"Which of the following mus‌t be administered initial​ly in patients with acute​ mania with psychotic fea‌tures?","raw_text":"Which of the following must be administered initially in patients with acute mania with psychotic features?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olanz‌apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium v‌alproate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Olanzapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Olanzapine</strong></p><p>In acute mania, even in patients without overt psychotic manifestations,<strong> atypical antipsychotics</strong> like<strong> olanzapine </strong>is used initially for<strong> rapid control of psychosis.</strong> Atypical antipsychotics are also effective as monotherapy. </p>\n<p>The therapeutic effects of lithium, carbamazepine, and valproic acid are seen only after a few days. Therefore they are effective only with daily dosing with adequate serum monitoring.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2396","text":"A patient with mania has b‌een initiated on lithium ​therapy. While counseling​ the patient about the me‌dication, which of the fo​llowing effects do you me‍ntion as the most common ‌CNS adverse effect?","raw_text":"A patient with mania has been initiated on lithium therapy. While counseling the patient about the medication, which of the following effects do you mention as the most common CNS adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ata‌xia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Slurred‌ speech","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fine t‌remors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coarse ‌tremors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fine tremors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fine tremors</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common CNS adverse effect</strong> of <strong>lithium</strong> in the therapeutic dose range is<strong> fine postural hand tremors. </strong>Therapeutic lithium tremor is generally symmetric, limited to the arms, and nonprogressive.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Ataxia, slurred speech, and/or incoordination are adverse effects seen during peak CNS/serum concentrations. Single bedtime dosing can avoid these adverse effects.</p>\n<p>Option D: Coarse tremors are seen in case of lithium toxicity and is  more disabling and may also affect the legs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1325","text":"A patient on chronic lithi‌um therapy is diagnosed t​o have hypothyroidism. Wh​at should be the next cou‌rse of action?","raw_text":"A patient on chronic lithium therapy is diagnosed to have hypothyroidism. What should be the next course of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop lithium immediately a‌nd start exogenous thyroi​d replacement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stop lithium immediately a‌nd wait for normalisation​ of thyroid status","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continue lithium along wit‌h exogenous thyroid repla​cement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease the dose of lithi‌um and wait for normalisa​tion of thyroid status","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Continue lithium along with exogenous thyroid replacement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Continue lithium along with exogenous thyroid replacement</strong></p><p><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong> developed in a patient on chronic lithium therapy is treated by <strong>exogenous replacement</strong> of <strong>thyroid hormones.</strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>not a reason</strong> for the<strong> discontinuation</strong> of lithium therapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2398","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of inc​reased frequency of urina​tion and fatigue. She giv‌es a history of lithium i​ntake for bipolar mood di‍sorder for the past 6 yea‌rs. On investigations, he​r serum osmolality was 30​0 mOsm/L and her urine os​molality was 250 mOsm/L. ​Which of the following dr​ugs is used to treat this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman presents with complaints of increased frequency of urination and fatigue. She gives a history of lithium intake for bipolar mood disorder for the past 6 years. On investigations, her serum osmolality was 300 mOsm/L  and her urine osmolality was 250 mOsm/L. Which of the following drugs is used to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amil‌oride","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Demeclo‌cycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indome‌thacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amiloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amiloride</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of lithium intake and investigations showing <strong>↑serum osmolality</strong> and <strong>↓urine osmolality</strong> is suggestive of <strong>nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong> secondary to <strong>lithium toxicity</strong>. <strong>Amiloride</strong> is the drug of choice for this condition. </p>\n<p>Amiloride is a <strong>potassium-sparing diuretic</strong>. It inhibits the renal epithelial Na<sup>+</sup> channel (ENac channel) in collecting duct cells. It <strong>blocks</strong> the entry of <strong>Li<sup>+</sup> ions </strong>through the<strong> ENac channels</strong> and thus mitigates diabetes insipidus induced by lithium.</p>\n<p><strong> </strong>Thiazide diuretics cause significant<strong> reductions</strong> in<strong> Li<sup><span style=\"text-decoration: line-through;\">+</span></sup> clearance</strong> that result in <strong>toxic</strong> levels. <strong>Thiazides</strong> are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients who are on <strong>lithium therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0611","text":"A female patient is on fir‌st-line pharmacological t​herapy for bipolar disord​er for 6 months. She is f‌asting for a few days due​ to religious reasons and‍ presented with seizures,‌ coarse tremors, confusio​n, and weakness of limbs.​ Which of the following i​nvestigations should be d​one to help diagnose her ​condition?","raw_text":"A female patient is on first-line pharmacological therapy for bipolar disorder for 6 months. She is fasting for a few days due to religious reasons and presented with seizures, coarse tremors, confusion, and weakness of limbs. Which of the following investigations should be done to help diagnose her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum pota‌ssium level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum lit‌hium level","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"E‌CG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌RI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serum lithium level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serum lithium level</strong></p><p>The clinical features given in the question are most likely suggestive of <strong>lithium</strong> <strong>toxicity</strong>, and hence <strong>serum lithium levels</strong> should be measured to confirm it.</p>\n<p><strong>Lithium</strong> is a <strong>mood stabilizer</strong> with a very narrow therapeutic index and is the <strong>first line</strong> of management in <strong>bipolar disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>The kidney handles both <strong>lithium</strong> and <strong>Na</strong><sup><strong>+</strong> </sup>in the same way via <strong>ENac channel</strong>. Thus during <strong>fasting/dehydration</strong>, along with <strong>Na<sup>+ </sup>reabsorption</strong> in renal tubules, there will be an increase in the <strong>reabsorption of lithium </strong>leading to lithium toxicity.</p>\n<p>Lithium has a very narrow therapeutic index of <strong>0.8-1.2 mEq/L.</strong> The severity of lithium toxicity is often divided into the following three grades: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 334px; width: 266px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 94px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2386px; text-align: center; height: 94px;\"><strong>Grade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.771px; text-align: center; height: 94px;\">\n<p><strong>Sr. lithium concentration </strong>(in mEq/L)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7017px; text-align: center; height: 94px;\"><strong>Symptoms</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2386px; height: 72px;\"><strong>Mild symptoms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.771px; height: 72px; text-align: center;\"><strong>1.5-2.5 </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7017px; height: 72px;\">nausea, vomiting, <strong>tremor</strong>, and fatigue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 109px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2386px; height: 109px;\"><strong>Moderate intoxication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.771px; height: 109px; text-align: center;\"><strong>2.5-3.5 </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7017px; height: 109px;\"><strong>confusion</strong>, agitation, delirium, tachycardia, and hypertonia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.8494px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 93.2386px; height: 34.8494px;\"><strong>Severe intoxication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.771px; height: 34.8494px; text-align: center;\"><strong>&gt;3.5 </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.7017px; height: 34.8494px;\">coma, <strong>seizures</strong>, hyperthermia, and hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A fine tremor is common in patients taking lithium at therapeutic doses. Therapeutic lithium tremor is generally symmetric, limited to the arms, and nonprogressive. The tremor from <strong>lithium toxicity</strong> is <strong>coarser</strong> and <strong>more disabling</strong>, may also affect the legs, and occurs with other symptoms of toxicity. Other adverse effects include mental confusion, <strong>hypothyroidism</strong>, <strong>nephrogenic</strong> <strong>diabetes insipidus,</strong> and edema.</p>\n<p>As lithium is readily dialyzed, lithium overdose or toxicity is treated with <strong>hemodialysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>*Note - Serum <strong>Na</strong><sup><strong>+</strong> </sup>levels are significant with respect to <strong>lithium toxicity</strong>, where as serum potassium levels becomes significant in <strong>digoxin toxicity</strong>. <strong>Hypokalemia </strong>increases the risk of digoxin toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1043","text":"A 28-year-old woman on lit‌hium therapy now presents​ with a positive urine pr​egnancy test. Which of th‌e following teratogenic e​ffects can be expected in‍ the fetus?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman on lithium therapy now presents with a positive urine pregnancy test. Which of the following teratogenic effects can be expected in the fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac‌ defects","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facial ‌defects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CNS d‌efects","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urogenita‌l defects","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiac defects","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4eda5b58e2f64337b1330f34da988cbax510x487.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiac defects</strong></p><p><strong>Lithium</strong> use in pregnancy can lead to <strong>cardiac defects</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Ebstein's anomaly </strong>is the specific cardiac defect with lithium use in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>In Ebstein’s anomaly, the radiological sign seen is a <strong>box-shaped heart</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5934","text":"Which drug, when given to ‌a mother in her third tri​mester of pregnancy, woul​d result in the following‌ finding in her newborn?","raw_text":"Which drug, when given to a mother in her third trimester of pregnancy, would result in the following finding in her newborn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venla‌faxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Promet‌hazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diazepam","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/989df78e2cda415ebb5241aa72ffb54f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a50a926d8c414f2680622a248acb29dax1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diazepam</strong></p><p>Benzodiazepines, when given during the third trimester of pregnancy can lead to <strong>floppy baby syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Benzodiazepines in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First trimester - cleft palate, GI anomalies</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Third trimester - floppy baby syndrome with neonatal hypotonia, as a withdrawal effect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Fetal lithium exposure is also associated with floppy baby syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"siJ0KxWe","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"22_gas_transport_in_blood","title":"22.Gas Transport In Blood","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1467","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PT =‌ nRV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"V = ‌PnRT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P = ‌VnRT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PV =‌ nRT","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PV = nRT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PV = nRT</strong></p><p><strong>Boyle’s law </strong>states that, at a given temperature,<strong> the product of pressure time’s volume for a gas is constant</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>PV = nRT</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 309px; width: 489px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">\n<p>Boyle's law:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At a given temperature, the product of pressure times volume for a gas is constant</li>\n<li>PV=nRT</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Charles law:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At constant pressure, the volume occupied by a fixed mass of gas is proportional to temperature</li>\n<li>V/T = constant</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dalton's Law:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It states that the partial pressure of a gas in a mixture of gases is the pressure that gas would exert if it occupied the total volume of the mixture.</li>\n<li>Partial pressure is the total pressure multiplied by the fractional concentration of dry gas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Henry's Law:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The amount of gas dissolved is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas with which it is in equilibrium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Graham's Law:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The rate of diffusion of a gas is inversely proportional to molecular weight.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1469","text":"Transport of CO is diffusi‌on limited because_____","raw_text":"Transport of CO is diffusion limited because_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alveolar membrane is less ‌permeable to CO","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High affinity of CO to hem‌oglobin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CO crosses epithelial barr‌ier slowly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High affinity of CO to hemoglobin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High affinity of CO to hemoglobin</strong></p><p>Transport of CO is diffusion limited because of the <strong>high affinity of CO to hemoglobin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diffusion across the alveolocapillary membrane:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood takes <strong>0.75s </strong>to traverse pulmonary capillaries.</li>\n<li><strong>CO</strong> diffuses into blood and <strong>combines with Hb with very high affinity</strong>. Thus the partial pressure of CO doesn't rise (Only dissolved form of gas can increase the partial pressure, Gas which combines with Hb or plasma proteins cannot). Partial pressure gradient necessary for diffusion of CO is maintained for the whole 0.75s and hence it is <strong>D</strong><strong>iffusion limited.</strong></li>\n<li>In the case of <strong>N<sub>2</sub>O </strong>it does not react and reaches equilibrium in about <strong>0.1s</strong>. The amount of N<sub>2</sub>O taken up is not limited by diffusion but by the amount of blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries; that is, it is <strong>F</strong><strong>low-limited.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>O<sub>2</sub></strong> is intermediate between N<sub>2</sub>O and CO; it is taken up by haemoglobin, but much less avidly than CO, and it reaches equilibrium with capillary blood in about <strong>0.3 s</strong>. Thus, its uptake is <strong>Perfusion-limited</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the case of perfusion limited gases, an increase in pulmonary blood flow increases the amount of gas transferred. Hence O2 transport increases with an increase in pulmonary blood flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1473","text":"What is the percentage of ‌O2 carried, chemically bo​und to Hb?","raw_text":"What is the percentage of O2 carried, chemically bound to Hb?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9‌7%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌7%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6‌6%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 97%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 97%</strong></p><p>Percentage of O<sub>2</sub> carried chemically (bound to Hb) is 97%.</p>\n<p>Oxygen is transported in the blood in two forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>physically 3% - dissolved in plasma </li>\n<li>chemically 97% - bound to hemoglobin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember:</p>\n<p><strong>Gases are transported in 3 forms:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dissolved gas</strong> - This is the form which <strong>contributes to partial pressure</strong>. <strong>Nitrogen</strong> is carried only in dissolved form in blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Bound gas</strong> - O<sub>2</sub>, CO are bound to Hb in RBC; CO<sub>2</sub> is bound to Hb in RBC as well as plasma protein.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemically modified gas</strong> - CO2 in the form of HCO<sub>3</sub>-.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Total gas concentration</strong> in solution is the <strong>sum</strong> of dissolved gas plus bound gas plus chemically modified gas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1474","text":"The presence of hemoglobin‌ in normal arterial blood​ increases its O2 concent​ration approximately by h​ow many times?","raw_text":"The presence of hemoglobin in normal arterial blood increases its O2 concentration approximately by how many times?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7‌0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 70</strong></p><p>The presence of hemoglobin in normal arterial blood increases its O2 concentration approximately by 70 times</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal arterial blood has <strong>Po<sub>2</sub> of about 100 mm Hg.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Amount of O<sub>2</sub> transported in dissolved form (Calculated by using <strong>Henry's law):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Concentration of dissolved O<sub>2</sub></strong> = Partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> × solubility of O<sub>2</sub> = 100 mmHg × 0.003 mL O<sub>2</sub>/100 mL blood per mm Hg =<strong> 0.3 mL O<sub>2</sub>/100 mL</strong></li>\n<li>Hence<strong> 1L of plasma has 3mL of 0<sub>2</sub> in dissolved form.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>5L </strong>of plasma can carry maximum <strong>15mL of 0<sub>2</sub>.</strong></li>\n<li>At rest, <strong>O<sub>2</sub> demand</strong> is around <strong>250mL/min. </strong></li>\n<li>Hence dissolved O<sub>2</sub> alone is not sufficient to meet body demands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Amount of O<sub>2</sub> transported bound to Hb:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Concentration of Hb is <strong>15g/100 mL</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Each gram</strong> of Hb binds to <strong>1.34 mL of O<sub>2</sub></strong></li>\n<li>Hence, 15 g binds to 20.1mL of O<sub>2</sub>/100 mL</li>\n<li>On average, the <strong>15 g of hemoglobin in 100 mL of blood</strong> can <strong>combine with a total of about 20 mL of O<sub>2</sub>.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thus, this value (20.1) in the presence of Hb is about 70 times the value (0.3) in the absence of Hb.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1475","text":"O2 delivery to tissues dep‌ends on all except_____","raw_text":"O2 delivery to tissues depends on all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type of fluid administered‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemog‌lobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venti‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac‌ output","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type of fluid administered","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31540bb5ba9a4ebdb4d4b8b5a9314beex1020x956.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type of fluid administered</strong></p><p>Oxygen delivery is the volume of oxygen delivered to the systemic vascular bed per minute. It is the product of the cardiac output and the arterial oxygen concentration.</p>\n<p>Oxygen delivery to tissue depends on (refer flow chart)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1476","text":"Each molecule of hemoglobi‌n carries ______ molecule​s of O2.","raw_text":"Each molecule of hemoglobin carries ______ molecules of O2.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4</strong></p><p>Hemoglobin has <strong>4 subunits</strong> each of which has heme molecule and polypeptide chain. Ferrous in heme binds to a molecule of O<sub>2</sub>. Hence, hemoglobin binds to 4 molecules of O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemoglobin is a protein made up of <strong>four subunits.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Each subunit</strong> contains a <strong>heme moiety attached to a polypeptide chain</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Heme</strong> is a <strong>porphyrin ring</strong> complex that includes <strong>one atom of ferrous iron.</strong></li>\n<li>In normal adults, most of the hemoglobin molecules contain <strong>two α and two β chains.</strong></li>\n<li>Each of the four iron atoms in hemoglobin can reversibly bind one O<sub>2</sub>. The <strong>iron stays in the ferrous state</strong> so that the reaction is <strong>oxygenation </strong>(not oxidation).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1945","text":"Most of the CO2 transporte‌d in the arterial blood i​s in the form of_____","raw_text":"Most of the CO2 transported in the arterial blood is in the form of_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HC‌O3-","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Attached to‌ haemoglobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbamino‌ compounds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic a‌cid (H2CO3)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HCO3-","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HCO3-</strong></p><p>CO<sub>2</sub> is carried in the blood in <strong>three forms:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>) - Quantitatively the most important(70%)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Carbaminohemoglobin (23%)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dissolved CO<sub>2</sub> (7%)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1477","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about hemoglobin ​is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about hemoglobin is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The T configuration of Hb ‌does not favor O2 binding​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The R configuration of Hb ‌does not favor O2 binding​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The R configuration of Hb ‌favors O2 binding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Each Hb molecules carries ‌4 oxygen molecules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The R configuration of Hb does not favor O2 binding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4802269477ce4187b9675eabb5b76d2ex1280x1009.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The R configuration of Hb does not favor O2 binding</strong></p><p>A <strong>relaxed</strong> <strong>(R) configuration, </strong>exposes more O<sub> 2</sub> binding sites.</p>\n<p>The quaternary structure of hemoglobin determines its affinity for O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p>In deoxyhemoglobin, the globin units are tightly bound in a <strong>tense (T) configuration, </strong>which <strong>reduces the affinity of the molecule for O<sub>2</sub>.</strong></p>\n<p>When O <sub>2</sub> is first bound, the bonds holding the globin units are released, producing a <strong>relaxed</strong> <strong>(R) configuration, </strong>which exposes more O<sub>2</sub> binding sites. The net result is a 500-fold increase in O<sub>2</sub></p>\n<p>In tissues, these reactions are reversed, resulting in O<sub>2  <span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>dissociation </strong></span></sub>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1480","text":"The oxygen-haemoglobin dis‌sociation curve is sigmoi​d shape because_____","raw_text":"The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is sigmoid shape because_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binding of one O2 molecule‌ increases the affinity o​f binding of subsequent O​2 molecules","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Binding of one O2 molecule‌ decreases the affinity o​f binding of subsequent O​2 molecules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bohr ‌effect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haldane‌ effect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Binding of one O2 molecule increases the affinity of binding of subsequent O2 molecules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Binding of one O2 molecule increases the affinity of binding of subsequent O2 molecules</strong></p><p>The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is <strong>sigmoid shape</strong> because binding of one O2 molecule <strong>increases the affinity</strong> of binding of subsequent O2 molecules.</p>\n<p>The <strong>oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve </strong>relates per­centage saturation of the O<sub>2 </sub>carrying power of hemoglobin (abbreviated as SaO<sub>2</sub>) to the Po<sub>2. </sub>It<strong> </strong>has a <strong>characteristic sigmoid shape</strong> due to the <strong>T–R interconversion. </strong>Combination of the first heme in the Hb molecule with O<sub>2</sub> increases the affinity of the second heme for O<sub>2</sub> and oxygenation of the second increases the affinity of the third, and so on, such that the affinity of Hb for the fourth O<sub>2</sub> molecule is many times that for the first <strong>(Positive Co-operative Effect).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option C: </strong>The effect of PCO<sub>2</sub> and pH on the O<sub>2</sub>-hemoglobin dissociation curve is called the Bohr effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D: </strong>O<sub>2</sub> bound to hemoglobin changes its affinity for CO<sub>2</sub>, such that when less O<sub>2</sub> is bound, the affinity of hemoglobin for CO<sub>2</sub> increases. This is referred to as the Haldane effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1483","text":"Hb would be expected to be‌come 100% saturated with ​oxygen at Po2 of (in mmHg​)_____","raw_text":"Hb would be expected to become 100% saturated with oxygen at Po2 of (in mmHg)_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌00","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"7‌60","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c32a21bda6e046b893b34572369a2189x439x413.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 100</strong></p><p>When blood is equilibrated with 100% O<sub>2</sub>, the normal hemoglobin becomes 100% saturated.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In vivo, the hemoglobin in the blood at the end of the pulmonary capillaries is about 97.5% saturated with O<sub>2</sub> (Po2 = 100 mm Hg).</li>\n<li>Because of a slight admixture with venous blood that bypasses the pulmonary capillaries <strong>(i.e,</strong> <strong>physiologic shunt),</strong> the hemoglobin in systemic arterial blood is only 97% saturated.</li>\n<li>Graph relates the percentage of saturated hemoglobin (SaO<sub>2</sub>) to PO<sub>2 </sub>and dissolved O<sub>2.</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 115px;\" width=\"365\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Partial pressure of O2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">Corresponding SpO2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">99 - 100%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">60</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">89%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">40</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">75%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">27</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">50%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1939","text":"The normal value of P50 in‌ an adult is(mm Hg)______​","raw_text":"The normal value of P50 in an adult is(mm Hg)______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌7","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 27","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94f708da8d5a4f50ae848a5139c80c03x639x541.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 27</strong></p><p><strong>Normal P<sub>50</sub></strong> is about <strong>27 mm of Hg for adult Hb.</strong></p>\n<p>P<sub>50</sub> is the partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> at which Hb is 50 % saturated. P<sub>50</sub> is an index of the affinity of Hb with O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p>Higher the P<sub>50</sub> value, lower is the affinity: Oxygen-Hemoglobin Dissociation Curve shifts to right.</p>\n<p>Lower the P<sub>50</sub> value, the higher is the affinity: Oxygen-Hemoglobin Dissociation Curve shifts to left.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1940","text":"Decrease in pH shifts the ‌oxygen dissociation curve​ to_____","raw_text":"Decrease in pH shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ri‌ght","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Le‌ft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doesn’t‌ affect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On either side depending o‌n other factors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0af2591c1b248b28d22ee452a48616bx600x647.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right</strong></p><p>The pH decreasing from the normal value shifts the O<sub>2</sub>-hemoglobin dissociation curve, to the right.</p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen-Hb dissociation curve (OHDC)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This is a <strong>plot of the partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> and the % saturation of Hb with O<sub>2</sub></strong>.</li>\n<li>It is normally <strong>sigmoid-shape.</strong></li>\n<li>If OHDC is shifted to the right, it means that affinity of Hb for O<sub>2</sub> has become less (which favors O<sub>2</sub> delivery) i.e. Higher the P<sub>50</sub>. (<strong>R - R</strong>ight shift <strong>R</strong>eleases oxygen)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Shift to the right is caused by: </strong>(Conditions with increased requirement of O2 - metabolically active tissues)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoxia and Hypercarbia - Metabolically active tissues utilise O<sub>2</sub> and tissues produce CO<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>↓ pH - Secondary to increased CO<sub>2</sub> production</li>\n<li>↑ in temperature, heat </li>\n<li>↑ in 2,3-DPG - increased anaerobic glycolysis</li>\n<li>Growth hormone, thyroid hormone, androgen - by increasing 2,3 DPG</li>\n<li>Sickle cell anaemia - reduced affinity for O<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1941","text":"Four molecules of myoglobi‌n will bind how many mole​cules of oxygen?","raw_text":"Four molecules of myoglobin will bind how many molecules of oxygen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0ba185f28d3e41d19cfc15407dc051c0x1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4</strong></p><p>4 molecules of myoglobin will bind 4 molecules of oxygen.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myoglobin: </strong>This is present in skeletal muscles.</li>\n<li>1 molecule of myoglobin binds 1 molecule of O<sub>2</sub>. Thus, 4 molecules of myoglobin will bind 4 molecules of oxygen</li>\n<li><strong>The shape of O<sub>2</sub>-myoglobin dissociation curve is the rectangular hyperbola</strong>. It lies to the left of Oxygen-Hemoglobin Dissociation Curve (OHDC)(refer figure).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1942","text":"Bohr ‌effect","raw_text":"Bohr effect","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facilitates ‌O2 transport","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facilitates ‌CO2 transport","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitates chloride trans‌port","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Facilitates O2 transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Facilitates O2 transport</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 654\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Facilitation of oxygen transport</strong> depends on the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve, explained by the Bohr effect. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>When the metabolic activity of tissues increases, the production of <strong>CO<sub>2 </sub>increases</strong>. This CO<sub>2 </sub>diffuses into the blood and in turn, raises the blood H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> and causes an <strong>increase in the H<sup>+</sup> </strong>concentration and a decrease in pH. This <strong>decreases the affinity</strong> of Hb for O<sub>2, </sub>shifting the O<sub>2</sub>- hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right. This facilitates the <strong>unloading of O<sub>2</sub> in the tissues.</strong> The effect of Pco<sub>2</sub> and pH on O<sub>2</sub> hemoglobin dissociation curve is called <strong>the Bohr Effect. </strong></p>\n<p>Conversely, in the lungs, CO<sub>2</sub> diffuses from the blood to the alveoli and the H<sup>+</sup> concentration decreases and theO<sub>2</sub> hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the left increasing the affinity of Hb to O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p><strong>Double Bohr effect occurring in the placenta:</strong> Oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve of fetal blood and maternal blood move in opposite directions.</p>\n<p><strong> Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The fetal blood entering the placenta carries large amounts of carbon dioxide, but most of it<strong> diffuse from the fetal blood into the maternal blood. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fetal blood</strong> - loss of CO<sub>2</sub> - reduced H<sup>+</sup> - <strong>shift to left</strong> - loading of O<sub>2</sub> i.e. <strong>oxygenation of fetal Hb.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Maternal blood</strong> - increase in CO<sub>2</sub> - increase in H<sup>+</sup> - <strong>shift to the right</strong> - <strong>Release of oxygen.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1943","text":"The level of 2,3 –DPG is d‌ecreased in______","raw_text":"The level of 2,3 –DPG is decreased in______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acid‌osis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High a‌ltitude","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exer‌cise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acidosis</strong></p><p>The level of 2,3–DPG is decreased in <strong>acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>2,3-DPG is produced from 3-phosphoglycerate which is a product of glycolysis. Acidosis <strong>inhibits</strong> red cell <strong>glycolysis</strong> which <strong>decreases</strong> the levels of 2,3-DPG<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1944","text":"High affinity of HbF to O2‌ is due to_____","raw_text":"High affinity of HbF to O2 is due to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased binding with 2,3‌-DPG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased binding with 2,3‌-DPG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increas‌e in pH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in concentration ‌of Hb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreased binding with 2,3-DPG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreased binding with 2,3-DPG</strong></p><p><strong>Increased affinity of HbF to O<sub>2</sub> is </strong>due to <strong>decreased affinity of γ chains of HbF to 2,3DPG.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> is the <strong>fetal variant</strong> of hemoglobin.</p>\n<p>The <strong>β chains</strong> of adult hemoglobin (hemoglobin A) are <strong>replaced by γ chains</strong> in HbF. This modification results in <strong>increased affinity of hemoglobin for O<sub>2</sub></strong>, a <strong>left shift</strong> of the O<sub>2</sub>-hemoglobin dissociation curve, and decreased P50.<br/>The <strong>mechanism of the left shift is based on the binding of 2,3-DPG</strong>. 2,3-DPG does not bind as avidly to the γ chains of HbF as it binds to the β chains of hemoglobin A. When <strong>less 2,3-DPG is bound, the affinity for O<sub>2</sub> increases and facilitates the movement of O<sub>2</sub> from the mother to the fetus.</strong></p>\n<p>This increased affinity is beneficial to the fetus, whose PaO<sub>2</sub> is low (approximately 40 mm Hg).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1946","text":"Haldane effe‌ct is _______","raw_text":"Haldane effect is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binding of O2 to Hb favour‌s release of CO2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Binding of CO2 to Hb favou‌rs release of O2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HCO3- leaving RBC in excha‌nge for Cl-","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Binding of O2 to Hb favours release of CO2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Binding of O2 to Hb favours release of CO2</strong></p><p><strong>Binding of O<sub>2 </sub>with hemoglobin tends to displace CO<sub>2 </sub></strong>from the blood. This effect is called the <strong>Haldane effect. </strong>(Remember - Haldane effect occurs in lungs and causes left shift of O2-Hb dissociation curve)</p>\n<p><strong>Haldane Effect</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Binding of O<sub>2 </sub>with hemoglobin tends to displace CO<sub>2 </sub>from the blood. This effect, called the Haldane effect, is important in promoting CO<sub>2</sub> transport.</li>\n<li>The Haldane effect results from the simple fact that the combination of O<sub>2</sub> with hemoglobin in the lungs causes the hemoglobin to become a stronger acid. This displaces CO<sub>2</sub> from the blood and into the alveoli.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1947","text":"You are comparing the pack‌ed cell volume (PCV) obta​ined from an arterial and​ venous blood sample of t​he same patient. Why is P‌CV higher in venous blood​?","raw_text":"You are comparing the packed cell volume (PCV) obtained from an arterial and venous blood sample of the same patient. Why is PCV higher in venous blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haldane‌ effect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bohr ‌effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorid‌e shift","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Affinity of ‌Hb to 2,3-DPG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chloride shift","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1d18e71705d452b9784f20e4f635e58x1280x1369.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chloride shift</strong></p><p>Hematocrit (PCV) of venous blood is normally 3% greater than that of the arterial blood because of 2 reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chloride shift</strong></li>\n<li>A small amount of fluid in the arterial blood returns via the lymphatics rather than the veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chloride shift:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upon entering the red blood cell, <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> </strong>is rapidly<strong> hydrated to H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> </strong>by carbonic anhydrase.</li>\n<li>H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> is in equilibrium with <strong>H<sup>+</sup></strong> and its conjugate base, <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>H<sup>+</sup> </strong>can interact with<strong> deoxyhemoglobin</strong>, whereas <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> </strong>can be<strong> transported outside of the cell </strong>via anion exchanger 1 (AE1 or Band 3).</li>\n<li>In effect, <strong>for each CO<sub>2</sub> molecule</strong> that enters the red cell, there is<strong> an additional HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> or Cl<sup>-</sup> </strong>in the cell.</li>\n<li>Because of this <strong>chloride shift</strong>, the <strong>Cl <sup>–</sup> content of the red cells in venous blood is significantly greater than</strong> that in arterial blood.</li>\n<li>Note that <strong>for each CO<sub>2</sub> molecule added to a red cell</strong>, there is an <strong>increase of one osmotically active particles in the cell - either a HCO <sub>3</sub> <sup>– </sup>or a Cl <sup>– </sup></strong>ion. Consequently, <strong>the red cells take up water and increase in size</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3889","text":"What makes CPDA superior t‌han ACD for blood storage​:","raw_text":"What makes CPDA superior than ACD for blood storage:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The fall in 2, 3 DPG is le‌ss","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased r‌elease of O2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has ‌less P50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is le‌ss acidic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The fall in 2, 3 DPG is less","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The fall in 2, 3 DPG is less</strong></p><p>The fall in 2, 3 DPG is less when the blood is stored in CPDA compared with ACD.</p>\n<p><strong>2, 3 DPG</strong> (2,3-diphosphoglycerate) binds to <strong>deoxygenated</strong> <strong>haemoglobin</strong>. Therefore, an increase in 2,3 DPG causes increased release of O<sub>2</sub> to the tissues. The equilibrium between Hb and O<sub>2</sub> can be understood from the following equation:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 642\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>HbO<sub>2</sub></strong> + 2,3-DPG <strong>⇄</strong> <strong>Hb</strong>−2,3-DPG + <strong>O<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n<p>In stored blood, the 2,3 DPG levels usually fall. As a result, stored blood releases less oxygen to the tissues than the normal circulating blood, thus limiting its use in hypoxic patients (Eg: anaemia).</p>\n<p>However, such a<strong> fall in 2,3 DPG is less</strong> when it is stored in a phosphate-containing solution like <strong>CPDA</strong> (Citrate, phosphate, dextrose, adenine), rather than <strong>ACD</strong> (Acid citrate dextrose). Therefore, the blood stored in CPDA, in comparison with blood stored in ACD has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Higher 2,3 DPG concentration</li>\n<li>More amount of deoxygenated blood bound to 2,3 DPG</li>\n<li>Decreased affinity of Hb to the oxygen.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased release of oxygen</strong> to the tissues.</li>\n<li><strong>Raised</strong> <strong>P50</strong> (PO2 at which Hb is half saturated)</li>\n<li>Shift in O2-Hb dissociation curve to the right</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1101","text":"The oxygen carrying capaci‌ty of a 18 year old boy w​ith haemoglobin of 14g/dl​ is","raw_text":"The oxygen carrying capacity of a 18 year old boy with haemoglobin of 14g/dl is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌8","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 18","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4c790e02e4c4d2f876f56db4b32e281x500x417.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 18</strong></p><p>The oxygen carrying capacity of a boy with haemoglobin 14g/dl is <strong>18.</strong></p>\n<p>When blood is saturated with 100% O2, the haemoglobin is also 100% saturated and each gram of normal haemoglobin contains 1.39 mL of O2.</p>\n<p>However, blood normally contains some inactive haemoglobin derivatives, and the measured value in vivo is thus slightly lower hence, each gram of haemoglobin in vivo contains <strong>1.31 mL of O2 (Hufner’s constant).</strong></p>\n<p>According to the above question, when Hb=14g/dl, O2= 1.31*14= <strong>18.34</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Demonstrates a progressive increase in the percentage of haemoglobin bound with oxygen as blood Po2 increases, which is called the percent saturation of haemoglobin.</li>\n<li>Blood leaving the lungs and entering the systemic arteries usually has a Po2 of about 95 mm Hg, the usual oxygen saturation of systemic arterial blood averages 97 per cent.</li>\n<li>Conversely, in normal venous blood returning from the peripheral tissues, the Po2 is about 40 mm Hg, and the saturation of haemoglobin averages 75 per cent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"s7_DB_WF","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"22_sleep_disorders","title":"22.Sleep Disorders","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC1725","text":"Which stage of sleep is ca‌lled paradoxical sleep?","raw_text":"Which stage of sleep is called paradoxical sleep?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-rapid eye movement sle‌ep 1 (NREM-1)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-rapid eye movement sle‌ep 2 (NREM-2)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-rapid eye movement sle‌ep 3 (NREM-3)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid eye movement sleep (‌REM)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rapid eye movement sleep (REM)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rapid eye movement sleep (REM)</strong></p><p><strong>REM</strong> stage of sleeping is also known as <strong>paradoxical sleep</strong>. It is characterized by a <strong>high</strong> level of <strong>brain activity</strong> and <strong>physiological activity</strong>, similar to those in wakefulness. In REM sleep,</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 551\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Pulse, respiration, and blood pressure are increased.</li>\n<li>Brain oxygen use increases during REM sleep.</li>\n<li>Thermoregulation is altered - Poikilothermia prevails during REM sleep.</li>\n<li>There is loss of muscle tone - body movement is absent during REM sleep.</li>\n<li>There is partial or full penile erection.</li>\n<li><strong>Dreams</strong> are abstract and surreal.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Polygraphic measures during <strong>rapid eye movement sleep</strong> show irregular patterns, sometimes close to <strong>aroused</strong> waking patterns. </p>\n<p>In <strong>NREM</strong> sleep, physiological functions are markedly <strong>lower</strong> than in wakefulness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5328","text":"While performing EEG on a ‌sleeping patient, you obs​erve patterns on the grap‍h. The stage with the max​imum duration, will be id​entified by the presence ​of which of the following‍ waves?","raw_text":"While performing EEG on a sleeping patient, you observe patterns on the graph. The stage with the maximum duration, will be identified by the presence of which of the following waves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Theta‌ waves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sleep s‌pindles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Delta‌ waves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta ‌waves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sleep spindles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04985530d96a465b959e555881c751d2x1280x2592.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fefc66cb05b44929b31cab91a9b7b6b1x608x313.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sleep spindles</strong></p><p><strong>N2(stage 2)</strong> of NREM sleep is the <strong>longest sleep phase</strong>. It presents with <strong>sleep spindles</strong> and <strong>K-complexes.</strong></p>\n<p>The stages of sleep include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NREM (75%)</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage N1- 5%</li>\n<li>Stage N2- 45%</li>\n<li>Stage N3- 25%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>REM (25%)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0756","text":"Identify the stage of slee‌p based on EEG finding hi​ghlighted in red.","raw_text":"Identify the stage of sleep based on EEG finding highlighted in red.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‌ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"REM ‌sleep","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. REM sleep","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90ee60bd3aae4acab9c35568790080b3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d848d4cb15fc4095a37b584b6e231d95x1280x2592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. REM sleep</strong></p><p>The marked area in the given EEG shows <strong>sawtooth waves</strong> that are characteristic of <strong>REM sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sleep is comprised of two distinct physiological states, <strong>non-rapid eye movement</strong> (NREM) sleep and REM sleep. <strong>Stages I</strong> to <strong>IV</strong> make up the NREM sleep with marked <strong>lower physiological functions</strong> than during wakefulness. Dreams which occur during this state are usually <strong>lucid</strong> and purposeful.</p>\n<p>REM sleep is characterized by a <strong>high level</strong> of <strong>brain activity</strong>. The physiological activity is similar to that during wakefulness. In REM sleep,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulse, respiration, and blood pressure are increased.</li>\n<li>Brain oxygen use increases during REM sleep.</li>\n<li>Thermoregulation is altered - Poikilothermia prevails during REM sleep.</li>\n<li>There is loss of muscle tone - body movements are absent during REM sleep.</li>\n<li>There is partial or full penile erection - This is used to evaluate the cause for <strong>impotence</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dreams</strong> are abstract and surreal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In normal adults, a REM episode has a <strong>latency</strong> of about <strong>90 minutes</strong> during which the sleep is in the NREM stage. This latency is shortened in <strong>narcolepsy</strong> and <strong>depressive disorders</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5325","text":"What waves can be seen on ‌the EEG when a person is ​awake but their eyes are ‍closed?","raw_text":"What waves can be seen on the EEG when a person is awake but their eyes are closed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha‌ waves","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta ‌waves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Theta‌ waves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delta‌ waves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alpha waves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c791f13eb0994911967c65f3887e4e19x1280x2592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alpha waves</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha waves</strong> are seen when the person is <strong>relaxed with eyes closed</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1734","text":"During polysomnography of ‌a patient with unexplaine​d daytime sleepiness, saw‍tooth waves are being rec​orded on EEG. Which of th​e following is associated​ with this stage of sleep‍?","raw_text":"During polysomnography of a patient with unexplained daytime sleepiness, sawtooth waves are being recorded on EEG. Which of the following is associated with this stage of sleep?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drea‌ming","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased brain oxygen con‌sumption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ‌muscle tone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced p‌ulse rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dreaming","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dreaming</strong></p><p><strong>Sawtooth waves</strong> are seen in rapid eye movement <strong>(REM) sleep. Dreaming</strong> is associated with rapid eye movement (REM) sleep.</p>\n<p>Other changes in REM sleep:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of muscle tone</li>\n<li>Partial or full penile erection</li>\n<li>Increased heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory rate</li>\n<li>Increased brain oxygen consumption</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5327","text":"Which of the following eve‌nts would not occur in NR​EM sleep?","raw_text":"Which of the following events would not occur in NREM sleep?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Night‌mares","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Night ‌terrors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sleep ‌walking","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bru‌xism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nightmares","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nightmares</strong></p><p><strong>Nightmares</strong> are seen in <strong>REM sleep.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1738","text":"A college student complain‌s that she is unable to s​leep properly. This is ma‍king it hard for her to c​oncentrate on her studies​ and has led to a fall in​ her grades. She lies on ‍her bed for at least 8-9 ​hours but has difficulty ​falling asleep. She has n‌o history of drug abuse. ‌For how many months shoul​d the symptoms persist be‌fore you diagnose her wit​h insomnia disorder?","raw_text":"A college student complains that she is unable to sleep properly. This is making it hard for her to concentrate on her studies and has led to a fall in her grades. She lies on her bed for at least 8-9 hours but has difficulty falling asleep. She has no history of drug abuse. For how many months should the symptoms persist before you diagnose her with insomnia disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 m‌onth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 mo‌nths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 mo‌nths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 months</strong></p><p>The sleep disturbance should be present for at least 3 nights per week, for at least <strong>3 months</strong> to diagnose insomnia disorder. It is the most common sleep disorder.</p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for insomnia disorder:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>A predominant complaint of <strong>dissatisfaction with sleep quantity or quality</strong>, associated with one (or more) of the following symptoms:\n<ul>\n<li>Difficulty initiating sleep</li>\n<li>Difficulty maintaining sleep</li>\n<li>Early-morning awakening with inability to return to sleep</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The sleep disturbance causes clinically significant <strong>distress or impairment</strong> in functioning.</li>\n<li>The sleep difficulty occurs <strong>at least 3 nights per week</strong>.</li>\n<li>The sleep difficulty is present for <strong>at</strong> <strong>least 3 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>The sleep difficulty occurs despite <strong>adequate opportunity</strong> for sleep.</li>\n<li>The insomnia is not better explained by and does not occur exclusively during the course of another sleep-wake disorder (e.g., narcolepsy, a breathing-related sleep disorder, a circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder, a parasomnia).</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 400\">Insomnia is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication).</div>\n</li>\n<li>Coexisting mental disorders and medical conditions do not adequately explain the predominant complaint of insomnia.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Treatment includes - </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treatment of underlying conditions (anxiety, mood disorders, etc.), if any</li>\n<li>Sleep hygiene</li>\n<li>Short-term therapy with Z-drugs (zolpidem, zopiclone, zaleplon)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2958","text":"A 22-year-old woman compla‌ins of excessive daytime ​sleepiness and sudden bou‍ts of sleep. The symptoms​ began at the age of 11 a​nd have been worsening. S​he couldn’t stay awake du‍ring her classes or exams​ and often dozed off stan​ding up, while working. E‌ventually, she developed ‌episodes of sudden loss o​f muscle tone, precipitat‌ed by strong emotions. Wh​at is most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old woman complains of excessive daytime sleepiness and sudden bouts of sleep. The symptoms began at the age of 11 and have been worsening. She couldn’t stay awake during her classes or exams and often dozed off standing up, while working. Eventually, she developed episodes of sudden loss of muscle tone, precipitated by strong emotions. What is most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inso‌mnia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narco‌lepsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Absent ‌seizures","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Somnam‌bulism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Narcolepsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Narcolepsy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>narcolepsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the tetrad of: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Excessive daytime sleepiness</li>\n<li>Cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone)</li>\n<li>Hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations</li>\n<li>Sleep paralysis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The hallmark of narcolepsy is <strong>decreased REM latency</strong>, i.e. decreased latent period before the first REM period occurs. Normal REM latency is 90-100 minutes. In narcolepsy, REM-sleep usually occurs <strong>within 10 minutes</strong> of the onset of sleep <strong>(sleep onset REM</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sleep hygiene</li>\n<li>Scheduled naps</li>\n<li>Avoidance of alcohol and drugs that cause drowsiness</li>\n<li>Drugs: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modafinil </strong>(wakefulness-promoting drug) is the <strong>drug of choice.</strong></li>\n<li>Amphetamines</li>\n<li>Methylphenidate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9851","text":"A patient with narcolepsy ‌will have which of the fo​llowing characteristic ch‍anges in sleep?","raw_text":"A patient with narcolepsy will have which of the following characteristic changes in sleep?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased ‌REM latency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inso‌mnia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased ‌REM latency","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ‌NREM sleep","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased REM latency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased REM latency</strong></p><p><strong>Narcolepsy</strong> is characterized by <strong>decreased REM latency</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the tetrad of: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Excessive daytime sleepiness</li>\n<li>Cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone)</li>\n<li>Hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations</li>\n<li>Sleep paralysis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The hallmark of narcolepsy is <strong>decreased REM latency</strong>, i.e. decreased latent period before the first REM period occurs. Normal REM latency is 90-100 minutes. In narcolepsy, REM-sleep usually occurs <strong>within 10 minutes</strong> of the onset of sleep <strong>(sleep-onset REM)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6924","text":"A worried patient presents‌ to the OPD for a health ​checkup. The previous nig‍ht she went on a date and​ was enjoying his company​. Once, he made her laugh​ so uncontrollably that s‍he suddenly felt a loss o​f strength in her body an​d fell to the ground. Whi‌ch of the following CSF f‌indings would be diagnost​ic for her condition?","raw_text":"A worried patient presents to the OPD for a health checkup. The previous night she went on a date and was enjoying his company. Once, he made her laugh so uncontrollably that she suddenly felt a loss of strength in her body and fell to the ground. Which of the following CSF findings would be diagnostic for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased hypocretin level‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased ‌GABA levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased hypocretin level‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ‌GABA levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreased hypocretin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreased hypocretin levels</strong></p><p>The patient experienced an episode of <strong>cataplexy,</strong> which is characterized by <strong>decreased levels of hypocretin</strong> in CSF. cataplexy is sudden loss of muscle tone, such as jaw or head drop, weakness of the knees, or paralysis of all skeletal muscles with collapse. It is seen in narcolepsy.</p>\n<p>Narcolepsy is the classical example of sleepiness caused due to CNS dysfunction of sleep mechanisms, mainly, a <strong>genetically triggered hypocretin deficit and dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Levels of hypocretin receptor-I are undetectable in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) of individuals who are HLA­ DQB 1*0602-positive (consistent with cataplexy-associated narcolepsy).</p>\n<p>The classic form of narcolepsy (narcolepsy with cataplexy) is characterized by a tetrad of symptoms:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Excessive daytime sleepiness</li>\n<li>Cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone)</li>\n<li>Hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations</li>\n<li>Sleep paralysis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Symptoms commonly appear in the second decade of life. Strong emotions (such as laughter and anger) usually act as the trigger for cataplexy.</p>\n<p>Pharmacotherapy remains the treatment of choice. <strong>Modafinil</strong>, an a1-adrenergic receptor agonist, is used to reduce the number of sleep attacks and to improve psychomotor performance in narcolepsy. Besides this, <strong>imipramine and fluoxetine</strong> are also found effective in <strong>eliminating cataplexy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5331","text":"A terrified computer engin‌eer presents to the clini​c with complaints of exce‍ssive daytime sleepiness ​and inability to move for​ a few seconds after waki​ng in the morning. He has‍ a recurring hallucinatio​n of being suffocated by ​a great hound just as he ‌falls asleep, which furth‌er disturbs him. Which of​ the following is the dru‌g of choice for this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A terrified computer engineer presents to the clinic with complaints of excessive daytime sleepiness and inability to move for a few seconds after waking in the morning. He has a recurring hallucination of being suffocated by a great hound just as he falls asleep, which further disturbs him. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low dose ben‌zodiazepines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Moda‌finil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sertr‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphet‌amines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Modafinil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Modafinil</strong></p><p>The patient has sleep paralysis, excessive daytime sleepiness, and hypnagogic hallucinations, suggestive of <strong>narcolepsy.</strong> The drug of choice for narcolepsy is <strong>modafinil,</strong> an a1-adrenergic receptor agonist, used to reduce the number of sleep attacks and to improve psychomotor performance in narcolepsy.</p>\n<p>Narcolepsy is characterized by the tetrad of: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Excessive daytime sleepiness</li>\n<li>Cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone)</li>\n<li>Hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations</li>\n<li>Sleep paralysis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The hallmark of narcolepsy is <strong>decreased REM latency.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sleep hygiene</li>\n<li>Scheduled naps</li>\n<li>Avoidance of alcohol and drugs that cause drowsiness</li>\n<li>Drugs: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modafinil </strong>(wakefulness-promoting drug) is the <strong>drug of choice.</strong></li>\n<li>Amphetamines</li>\n<li>Methylphenidate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5332","text":"What is included in Klein-‌Levin syndrome?","raw_text":"What is included in Klein-Levin syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inso‌mnia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anx‌iety","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyper‌somnia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypersomnia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypersomnia</strong></p><p>Klein-Levin syndrome is one of the primary <strong>hypersomnias</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypersomnia - </strong>always present, occurring recurrently for long periods of time (18-hour to 20-hour sleep periods)</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperphagia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypersexuality</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Disinhibition </strong>(aggression)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Studies show diffuse brain hypoperfusion, mostly focused on the thalamic and frontotemporal areas on SPECT scan in patients with Klein-Levin syndrome during the symptomatic period.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5330","text":"A scared mother brings her‌ 6-year-old child to a ps​ychiatrist. Her son wakes‍ up at night screaming, s​weating, and terrified. W​hen she tries to console ​him, he does not respond.‍ In the morning, he is un​able to recall the episod​e. In which stage of slee‌p does this condition occ‌ur?","raw_text":"A scared mother brings her 6-year-old child to a psychiatrist. Her son wakes up at night screaming, sweating, and terrified. When she tries to console him, he does not respond. In the morning, he is unable to recall the episode. In which stage of sleep does this condition occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"R‌EM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. N3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. N3</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario point towards a diagnosis of <strong>night terrors </strong>(pavor nocturnus). The episodes occur during <strong>delta sleep (N3).</strong></p>\n<p>Night terrors are characterized by the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Repetitive experiences of fright in which a person screams in fear during sleep (usually normal in children)</li>\n<li>The person cannot be awakened</li>\n<li><strong>Autonomic disturbances</strong> may be present</li>\n<li>The person has <strong>no memory</strong> of having a dream</li>\n<li>Occurs during <strong>delta sleep</strong></li>\n<li>Onset in <strong>adolescence</strong> may indicate <strong>temporal lobe epilepsy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0032","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brough‌t to the psychiatrist by ​his parents. They say he ‍wakes up at night screami​ng that someone is chasin​g him with a blunt axe. H​e feels a bit relaxed aft‍er they console him, but ​it is difficult for him t​o go to sleep again. In w‌hich stage of sleep do th‌ese episodes occur?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the psychiatrist by his parents. They say he wakes up at night screaming that someone is chasing him with a blunt axe. He feels a bit relaxed after they console him, but it is difficult for him to go to sleep again. In which stage of sleep do these episodes occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌EM","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"N‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. REM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. REM</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nightmares. </strong>They are seen in <strong>REM sleep.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2768","text":"A woman brings her 8-year-‌old son to you with compl​aints that the child wake‍s up at night and walks a​round the room for about ​15 minutes every alternat​e day. Any attempt to com‍municate with him during ​the episode is unsuccessf​ul. The child is unable t‌o recall the episodes whe‌n he wakes up. What is th​e most probable diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A woman brings her 8-year-old son to you with complaints that the child wakes up at night and walks around the room for about 15 minutes every alternate day. Any attempt to communicate with him during the episode is unsuccessful. The child is unable to recall the episodes when he wakes up. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Narco‌lepsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somni‌loquy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somnam‌bulism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cata‌plexy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Somnambulism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Somnambulism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points towards a diagnosis of <strong>somnambulism (sleep walking). </strong></p>\n<p>Sleepwalking or somnambulism is more common in children. While sleep walking the individual is in a confused state and is relatively unresponsive to efforts of others to communicate with them and can be awakened only with great difficulty. It resolves spontaneously after adolescence.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reassurance</li>\n<li>Patient safety has to be maintained</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Somniloquy</strong> is otherwise called sleeptalking.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7527","text":"What is the false statemen‌t regarding somnambulism?​","raw_text":"What is the false statement regarding somnambulism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disorder of ‌sleep arousal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Person will have full cons‌ciousness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Usually, terminates in awa‌kening followed by confus​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There may be neurological ‌condition associated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Person will have full consciousness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Person will have full consciousness</strong></p><p>Somnambulism is characterized by sequence of behaviors <strong>without full consciousness.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: About 80% of sleepwalkers have a family history of sleepwalking, and an identical twin is much more likely to sleepwalk if their other twin sleepwalks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9466","text":"An irritated wife brings h‌er husband to the clinic.​ She says he grinds his t‍eeth while sleeping and f​eels it is impossible to ​lie next to him. The pati​ent feels jaw pain and he‍adache the day following ​these episodes. Which of ​the following is false re‌garding his condition?","raw_text":"An irritated wife brings her husband to the clinic. She says he grinds his teeth while sleeping and feels it is impossible to lie next to him. The patient feels jaw pain and headache the day following these episodes. Which of the following is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"May persist throughout lif‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can occur during REM sleep‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Botox can help prevent jaw‌ joint complications","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not precipitated by ‌oromandibular dystonia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is not precipitated by oromandibular dystonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is not precipitated by oromandibular dystonia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bruxism (teeth grinding)</strong>. It can be precipitated by <strong>anxiety, stress, and oromandibular dystonia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sleep-related bruxism </strong>is commonly seen in the 1<sup>st</sup> or 2<sup>nd</sup> decade of life and can <strong>persist lifelong. </strong></p>\n<p>It can be prominently noted in mainly <strong>N1</strong> and<strong> N2 NREM</strong> sleep and sometimes in <strong>REM</strong> sleep.</p>\n<p>It may be associated with conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Huntington’s disease</li>\n<li>Cerebral palsy</li>\n<li>Mental retardation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bruxism can be precipitated by anxiety and stress. It can lead to <strong>temporomandibular joint </strong>(TMJ) <strong>syndrome</strong>. Local injections of <strong>botulinum toxins </strong>(botox) into the masseter muscles can help in the prevention of dental and TMJ problems.</p>\n<p>Bruxism can be seen secondary to the usage of <strong>entactogen drugs</strong> (Methylenedioxymethamphetamine – MDMA), <strong>amphetamine</strong>, cocaine, alcohol, and some <strong>serotonin reuptake inhibitors</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0754","text":"A 25-year-old woman compla‌ins of a creepy crawling ​sensation in her legs. It‍ is more at night and pre​vents her from sleeping. ​She is relieved of these ​symptoms by either walkin‍g or moving her legs. Whi​ch of the following drugs​ is used in treating the ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman complains of a creepy crawling sensation in her legs. It is more at night and prevents her from sleeping. She is relieved of these symptoms by either walking or moving her legs. Which of the following drugs is used in treating the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gabap‌entin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prami‌pexole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam‌in B12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iron t‌ablets","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gabapentin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gabapentin</strong></p><p>The history is suggestive of <strong>restless leg syndrome</strong> and <strong>gabapentin</strong> is the first-line choice of treatment for this condition.</p>\n<p>Gabapentinoids are considered superior to dopamine agonists due to the high frequency of intermediate and long-term augmentation with dopamine agonists, as well as their risk of impulse control disorders.</p>\n<p>Restless legs syndrome (RLS) is an irresistible urge to move the legs when at rest or while trying to fall asleep or an uncomfortable sensation of creeping-crawling feeling. It is worse at night and moving legs or walking reduces discomfort. <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Uremia, neuropathies, as well as iron-deficiency and folic acid–deficiency anemias can produce secondary RLS.</span></p>\n<p>In patients with restless legs syndrome, <strong>iron stores</strong> are initially repleted, and the aggravating factors are eliminated. If RLS still persists, initial therapy with a <strong>gabapentinoid</strong> (pregabalin, gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil) is preferred, rather than a dopamine agonist (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine).</p>\n<p><strong>FDA-approved</strong> drugs for the <strong>treatment</strong> of restless leg syndrome: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gabapentin</li>\n<li>Ropinirole</li>\n<li>Pramipexole</li>\n<li>Rotigotine </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: A <strong>dopamine agonist</strong> is used as an alternative in patients with increased risk for side effects from gabapentinoids, such as those with obesity and its complications, a past or present history of moderate to severe depression, a disorder causing gait instability or respiratory failure, and a history of substance use disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6986","text":"What does the given image ‌represent?","raw_text":"What does the given image represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypn‌ogram","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Electroenc‌ephalogram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyso‌mnogram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epworth slee‌p assessment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypnogram","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c0052b61135d4aea839710289588fd3f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71b5dbf4d86640e5b78dc4df64d08f98x1280x2592.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/159c16f93b7541e4b5663b45d540da78x1062x1682.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypnogram</strong></p><p>The above image represents a<strong> hypnogram</strong>. It is a graph that <strong>depicts the stages of sleep as a function of time</strong>. It provides a visual representation of sleep structure and is useful in the diagnosis of various sleep disorders. </p>\n<p>An <strong>electroencephalogram </strong>is the depiction of the<strong> electrical activity of the surface </strong>of the brain. Different waveforms (alpha, beta, theta, delta) are produced depending on the functional status of the brain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Polysomnography </strong>is a multi parametric test that is considered the<strong> gold standard </strong>in the<strong> diagnosis of sleep disorders</strong>. It is a continuous recording of biophysiological changes that occur during sleep. It includes,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Continuous EEG recording, from occipital and parietal leads</li>\n<li>Electro-oculography- to record eye movements</li>\n<li>Electromyography- for muscle potential and its activities</li>\n<li>ECG</li>\n<li>Multiple sleep latency tests- repeated measures of time taken to sleep</li>\n<li>Nasal-oral airflow, respiratory effort, oxyhemoglobin saturation, and leg movements during sleep are measured.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Epworth sleep assessment scale </strong>is a questionnaire used to<strong> quantify daytime sleepiness. </strong>It determines how frequently a patient is likely to doze off in 8 commonly encountered situations. It is a subjective assessment and hence less reliable.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5078","text":"On performing polysomnogra‌phy in a patient, the wav​es of EOG, EEG, and EMG r‍espectively from above do​wnwards are seen below. W​hich stage of sleep does ​the marked area represent‍? EOG- Electroocoulograph​y EEG- Electroencephalogr​aphy EMG- Electromyograph‌y","raw_text":"<p>On performing polysomnography in a patient, the waves of EOG, EEG, and EMG respectively from above downwards are seen below. Which stage of sleep does the marked area represent?</p>\n<p>EOG- Electroocoulography</p>\n<p>EEG- Electroencephalography</p>\n<p>EMG- Electromyography</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"REM ‌sleep","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NREM I‌ sleep","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NREM I‌I sleep","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NREM II‌I sleep","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. REM sleep","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f0dac50232a4eedac4dc9f639dfcb4e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f57a309d87e9465dbf87fa566138b76bx1280x944.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e189481359b14d4fbee07cd798b5f043x1280x2592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. REM sleep</strong></p><p><strong>REM </strong>sleep is represented by the marked area in the given polysomnography.</p>\n<p>The three zones in the given image represent <strong>EOG</strong>,<strong> EEG,</strong> and<strong> EMG</strong>, respectively. In the marked area,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>EOG shows Positive activity</li>\n<li>EEG shows Mixed waves</li>\n<li>EMG shows Minimal activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is characteristic of <strong>REM sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The following table helps in identifying the state of wakefulness and sleep from the polysomnograph :</p>\n<table style=\"width: 311px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.742px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.2578px; height: 18px;\"><strong>  </strong>EEG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50px; height: 18px;\"> EOG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 18px;\"> EMG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.742px; height: 18px;\">Active state</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.2578px; height: 18px;\">  Fast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50px; height: 18px;\">  +</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 18px;\">  +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.742px; height: 18px;\"><strong>REM sleep</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.2578px; height: 18px;\"><strong> Mixed </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 50px; height: 18px;\"><strong>  +</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 18px;\"><strong>  -</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.7812px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 100.742px; height: 33.7812px;\">NREM sleep</td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.2578px; height: 33.7812px;\">  Slow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50px; height: 33.7812px;\">  -</td>\n<td style=\"width: 68px; height: 33.7812px;\">  +</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"IFznTHGl","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"22_large_intestine","title":"22.Large Intestine","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC3787","text":"Which of the following is ‌the earliest sign in Croh​n's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest sign in Crohn's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cobblestone‌ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep fissu‌red ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rose-thorn‌ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aphthou‌s ulcers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aphthous ulcers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aphthous ulcers</strong></p><p><strong>Aphthous ulcers</strong>, surrounded by a rim of erythematous mucosa, are the <strong>earliest</strong> lesions seen in a patient with Crohn's disease during<strong> endoscopy.</strong></p>\n<p>Crohn's colitis usually spares the rectum and is <strong>most severe </strong>in the <strong>ileocaecal region</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In early Crohn's disease, small, <strong>punched-out aphthous ulcers</strong> are often seen (the result of submucosal lymphoid follicle expansion).</li>\n<li>In moderate disease, the aphthous ulcers coalesce into larger ulcers, which may take on the appearance of a star (stellate ulcers).</li>\n<li>As disease severity increases, submucosal edema and injury can result in <strong>cobblestoning</strong> of the mucosa (option A) and eventually the formation of <strong>serpiginous ulcers</strong>, which are typical findings in Crohn's disease. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thorn-like extraluminal projections in <strong>barium studies</strong> are known as <strong>rose-thorn appearance</strong>, and are a typical sign of Crohn's disease. They represent <strong>deep penetrating linear ulcers</strong> or fissurings typically seen within stenosed terminal ileum with a thickened wall. (Options C and D)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3786","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about Cro​hn's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about Crohn's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For effective treatment of‌ a Crohn's mass, only ant​ibiotics are prescribed.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Symptomatic relapse after ‌surgery is not related to​ the presence of disease ‍during resection.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The proximal ileum is most‌ commonly involved in Cro​hn's disease.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On endoscopic examination,‌ continuous lesions are v​isualised, which are diag‍nostic of Crohn's disease‌.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Symptomatic relapse after surgery is not related to the presence of disease during resection.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Symptomatic relapse after surgery is not related to the presence of disease during resection.</strong></p><p>Symptomatic <strong>relapse</strong> of Crohn's disease after surgery is <strong>not related</strong> to the <strong>presence of the disease</strong> during resection. </p>\n<p>The course of the disease after surgery is unpredictable since surgery does not cure Crohn's disease. Instead, the <strong>probability of relapse</strong> increases with the <strong>time since resection. </strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Antibiotics alone cannot treat</strong> a Crohn's mass effectively. Abscesses can be treated with metronidazole and ciprofloxacin and should also undergo <strong>drainage</strong> (and resection if needed).</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>The <strong>terminal ileum</strong> is most commonly involved in Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p>Option D: On endoscopic examination, <strong>discontinuous</strong> <strong>\"skip\" </strong>lesions are visualized. These are irregular areas of inflamed mucosa that are ulcerated with mucopurulent exudate separated by intervening, normal-appearing intestinal mucosa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9700","text":"A 40-year-old female patie‌nt presents with a 9-mont​h history of weight loss,‍ abdominal cramps, and in‌termittent diarrhea. On e​xamination, a palpable ma‌ss in the right lower qua​drant of the abdomen is f​ound. Stool cultures are ​normal. Colonoscopy shows‌ the presence of skip les​ions. Biopsy showed the p‌resence of non-caseating ​granulomas. What is the m‌ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old female patient presents with a 9-month history of weight loss, abdominal cramps, and intermittent diarrhea. On examination, a palpable mass in the right lower quadrant of the abdomen is found. Stool cultures are normal. Colonoscopy shows the presence of skip lesions. Biopsy showed the presence of non-caseating granulomas. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ileocecal t‌uberculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diverti‌culitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Crohn’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the biopsy showing the presence of non-caseating granulomas are diagnostic of <strong>Crohn's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9321","text":"A 28-year-old patient was ‌brought to the casualty w​ard with high fever and p‍ain abdomen. He had a his‌tory of bloody diarrhea f​or several months. His pa‌st history was negative f​or long-term medications ​and previous surgeries. T​he abdominal X-ray image ‌obtained is shown below. ​What is the most probable‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old patient was brought to the casualty ward with high fever and pain abdomen.  He had a history of bloody diarrhea for several months. His past history was negative for long-term medications and previous surgeries. The abdominal X-ray image obtained is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxic m‌egacolon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Volv‌ulus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ogilvie ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumatosis ‌intestinalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Toxic megacolon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dcc72c69ed284bff98f49e824afa5519.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68a94ae8b92b4bb9bbbd2db12b57b101x510x624.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f5e35a47306454faa7025aa93bc9978x512x442.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85839251fded41ae8184d0d5e386be66x1208x968.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Toxic megacolon</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the radiograph are suggestive of<strong> toxic megacolon. </strong></p>\n<p>Toxic megacolon may present with fever, abdominal pain, and abdominal distension. Abdominal radiography shows transverse or right <strong>colonic dilation</strong> of &gt;6 cm and<strong> l</strong><strong>oss of haustrations. </strong>It is usually associated with<strong> ulcerative colitis</strong> as indicated by history of chronic bloody diarrhoea. </p>\n<p>It can be triggered by <strong>electrolyte imbalance</strong> and <strong>narcotics</strong> which interfere with gut motility. Features of secondary bacterial infecton and <strong>sepsis</strong> may be present. It can be complicated by <strong>intestinal perforation</strong>.</p>\n<p>It <strong>usually resolves with supportive treatment</strong> (correcting electrolyte imbalance, fluids, and bowel rest) and medical therapy which includes glucocorticoids, anti-inflammatory drugs, and antibiotics. However, an urgent <strong>colectomy</strong> is indicated in patients who do not show signs of improvement.</p>\n<p>Indications for urgent surgery include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frank intraperitoneal peritonitis, secondary to perforation</li>\n<li>Life-threatening hemorrhage or increasing transfusion requirements </li>\n<li>Worsening systemic toxicity </li>\n<li>Worsening colonic dilatation </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Volvulus</strong> occurs when the air-filled segment of the colon twists around the mesentery. The <strong>sigmoid colon</strong> is the most commonly involved part of the large intestine (90%). The patient can present with abdominal distension, nausea and vomiting. X-ray abdomen shows a bent inner tube or coffee bean appearance. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Ogilvie syndrome</strong>, also known as <strong>colonic pseudo-obstruction</strong> is a <strong>functional</strong> disorder with colonic dilatation without <strong>mechanical obstruction</strong>. It occurs commonly in hospitalized patients and is associated with <strong>narcotics</strong>, <strong>bed rest</strong> and <strong>comorbid disease</strong>. X-ray shows dilatation of the colon (predominantly transverse colon) with absent mechanical obstruction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pneumatosis intestinalis</strong> is the presence of gas within the bowel wall seen in any part of the GI tract but most commonly in the <strong>jejunum</strong>. It is a pathognomic feature of <strong>necrotizing enterocolitis</strong>. X-ray of the abdomen will show intramural gas in the bowel.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7096","text":"You are doing an emergency‌ laparotomy for a case of​ intestinal obstruction. ‍Which organ will you firs‌t visualize to know if th​is is a case of small bow‌el or large bowel obstruc​tion?","raw_text":"You are doing an emergency laparotomy for a case of intestinal obstruction. Which organ will you first visualize to know if this is a case of small bowel or large bowel obstruction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cae‌cum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoi‌d colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caecum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caecum</strong></p><p><strong>The cecum</strong> is the <strong>first organ to be visualized to differentiate</strong> between the small and large bowel in cases of intestinal obstruction.</p>\n<p><strong>Distension of the cecum </strong>will confirm<strong> large bowel involvement. </strong>The identification of a collapsed distal segment of the large bowel and its sequential proximal assessment will lead to the identification of the cause.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2839","text":"A 55-year-old man presente‌d to the OPD with complai​nts of intermittent const‍ipation and a sense of he‌aviness in his lower abdo​men. His barium enema fil‌m is given below. Which o​f the following statement​s is false about the path​ology responsible for his‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presented to the OPD with complaints of intermittent constipation and a sense of heaviness in his lower abdomen. His barium enema film is given below. Which of the following statements is false about the pathology responsible for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Commonly occurs along the ‌mesenteric side of the co​lon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoid colon is the most ‌common site","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is characterized by tru‌e diverticula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Causes formation of pulsio‌n diverticula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is characterized by true diverticula","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4fe452fc0274a4ca58b879c932ba1c7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b282bb4aae8347bfb46edb11bfd6a111x1280x1640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is characterized by true diverticula</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray film showing saw-toothed appearance of the colon are suggestive of <strong>colonic diverticulosis.</strong> They are <strong>false</strong> diverticula.</p>\n<p><strong>Diverticulosis </strong>refers to the <strong>herniation </strong>of the<strong> mucosa </strong>and <strong>muscularis mucosa </strong>through the <strong>colonic wall, </strong>usually at the point where the nutrient artery enters the colon<strong> (mesenteric side). </strong>The condition is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> until complications arise. They may be detected incidentally. On a barium X-ray, the colon with diverticular disease will have a<strong> saw-toothed appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>Colonic diverticula are more often<strong> acquired</strong> than congenital. They are common in developed countries that have a low fiber diet. A <strong>low fiber diet</strong> results in <strong>low stool volume</strong>. As the colon generates<strong> higher intraluminal pressure</strong> to propel smaller volumes of stools, low stool volumes cause an <strong>increase in colonic wall tension</strong> and <strong>segmentation of the colon</strong>. </p>\n<p>Prolonged <strong>colonic contractions</strong> and <strong>high pressures</strong> (directed radially along the colonic wall) results in the formation of <strong>pulsion diverticula. </strong>The<strong> sigmoid colon </strong>is the most common site.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>True</strong> diverticulum involves the herniation of <strong>all the layers</strong> of the intestine whereas <strong>false</strong> diverticulum involves the herniation of <strong>only the mucosa and submucosa.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2841","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding left-sided​ diverticulitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding left-sided diverticulitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is often an incidental ‌finding during surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Appendicitis is an importa‌nt differential diagnosis​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left sided diverticulitis ‌is more common than right​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The anatomical location is‌ given by the Hinchey cla​ssification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left sided diverticulitis is more common than right","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left sided diverticulitis is more common than right</strong></p><p>In the general population, <strong>left-sided diverticulitis </strong>is more common. The<strong> sigmoid colon</strong> (left colon) involvement is <strong>more common</strong> than the right colon involvement.  </p>\n<p>Diverticulitis is the inflammation and infection of the diverticulum<strong>.</strong> It most commonly presents with</p>\n<ul>\n<li>left lower abdominal pain (often termed as left-sided appendicitis)</li>\n<li>fever</li>\n<li>a change in bowel habits</li>\n<li>anorexia, nausea</li>\n<li>urinary urgency if there is an associated inflammation of the bladder etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Right-sided diverticulitis occurs more often in <strong>younger patients </strong>of <strong>Asian descent</strong> than in other populations<strong> </strong>and presents with<strong> pain </strong>in the<strong> right lower quadrant. </strong>Patients presenting with these symptoms are often thought to have <strong>acute appendicitis, </strong>and often the diagnosis of right-sided diverticulitis is subsequently made in the operating room.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Hinchey classification </strong>is used to describe the<strong> severity </strong>of the<strong> diverticular disease,</strong> complicated by perforation.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1295\">\n<table><colgroup><col/><col/> </colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\">\n<p><strong>Hinchey classification:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 1</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mesenteric or pericolic abscess</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 2</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pelvic abscess</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 3</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Purulent peritonitis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 4</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fecal peritonitis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2852","text":"A 38-year-old man is broug‌ht to the casualty with s​evere right lower quadran‍t abdominal pain for the ‌last two days. You suspec​t right-sided diverticuli‌tis due to the relatively​ long history and lack of​ migration of pain. Howev​er, your co-resident is c‌onvinced it is appendicit​is. Which is the best inv‌estigation to rule in you​r diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man is brought to the casualty with severe right lower quadrant abdominal pain for the last two days. You suspect right-sided diverticulitis due to the relatively long history and lack of migration of pain. However, your co-resident is convinced it is appendicitis. Which is the best investigation to rule in your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U‌SG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barium‌ enema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT ‌scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colon‌oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CT scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15f28b04c94a4328a316e176f7104967x1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eae807bf49a74e11adcf8f8448a0b80ex1024x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CT scan</strong></p><p><strong>Computed tomography </strong>(CT)<strong> scan </strong>of the <strong>abdomen</strong> and pelvis is now considered by most to be the standard modality for the evaluation of <strong>acute diverticulitis</strong>.</p>\n<p id=\"__p21\"><strong>Contrast enema</strong> studies are the most accurate way to find out the colonic diverticula. However, because of the risks of extravasation of barium from the perforation and aggravation of inflammation, it is <strong>avoided</strong>.</p>\n<p>In contrast to barium enema, a<strong> CT scan</strong> demonstrates both the <strong>intraluminal</strong> and <strong>extracolonic</strong> manifestations of acute right-sided diverticulitis. </p>\n<p><strong>CT findings</strong> include pericolic soft tissue stranding, colonic wall thickening and pericolic fat infiltration (streaky fat).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Barium enema</strong> in <strong>diverticulosis </strong>shows a <strong>saw-tooth appearance.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0763","text":"While performing surgery o‌n a patient who presented​ with clinical features s‍uggestive of peritonitis,‌ a diverticular perforati​on is seen along with fec‌al peritonitis. What is t​he Hinchey's stage?","raw_text":"While performing surgery on a patient who presented with clinical features suggestive of peritonitis, a diverticular perforation is seen along with fecal peritonitis. What is the Hinchey's stage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‌ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‌ge 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stage 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stage 4</strong></p><p><strong>Fecal peritonitis</strong> belongs to <strong>grade 4</strong> in the Hinchey classification.</p>\n<p>Diverticulitis refers to the inflammation and infection associated with the diverticula. <strong>Hinchey classification</strong> is the <strong>most commonly</strong> used classification for the <strong>severity and complication of acute diverticulitis. </strong>It is used additionally to guide the overall <strong>management</strong> of acute diverticulitis. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1295\">\n<table style=\"height: 263px;\" width=\"526\"><colgroup><col/><col/> </colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.5625px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 516px; height: 33.5625px;\">\n<p><strong>Hinchey classification:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104.18px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 1</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 405.82px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mesenteric or pericolic abscess</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104.18px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 2</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 405.82px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pelvic abscess</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104.18px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 3</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 405.82px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Purulent peritonitis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104.18px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage 4</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 405.82px; height: 46px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fecal peritonitis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4294","text":"A 70-year-old man with pas‌t history of diabetes mel​litus, hypertension, and ‍chronic constipation pres‌ented with right lower ab​dominal pain associated w‌ith fever. CT abdomen fin​dings are suggestive of a​cute diverticulitis with ​6 cm fluid accumulation i‌n the paracolic region. W​hat is the most appropria‌te management for this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man with past history of diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and chronic constipation presented with right lower abdominal pain associated with fever. CT abdomen findings are suggestive of acute diverticulitis with 6 cm fluid accumulation in the paracolic region. What is the most appropriate management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral antibiotics, bowel re‌st and continued monitori​ng","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"iv antibiotics, bowel rest‌, and continued monitorin​g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laparotomy for abscess dra‌inage and surgical resect​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT guided percutaneous dra‌inage with iv antibiotics​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CT guided percutaneous drainage with iv antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CT guided percutaneous drainage with iv antibiotics</strong></p><p>The elderly patient with <strong>multiple comorbidities</strong> and <strong>complicated acute diverticulitis</strong> (fluid <strong>collection &gt;5 cm</strong>) should ideally be managed with CT-guided percutaneous drainage with iv antibiotics.</p>\n<p>Acute diverticulitis management:</p>\n<p><strong>Outpatient care</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Initiated for <strong>young patients</strong> with <strong>no comorbidities</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It includes:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Liquid diet</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Analgesia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Regular follow-up</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inpatient care</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Should be initiated if CT shows <strong>complicated diverticulitis</strong> characterized by: \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perforation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abscess formation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obstruction or</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fistulization.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Can also be initiated if CT shows uncomplicated diverticulitis but the patient is of <strong>high risk</strong> such as \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Sepsis</strong> at presentation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Elderly</strong> patient</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Immunosuppression</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of <strong>comorbidities</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Failure of outpatient care</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inpatient care includes the following:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bowel rest</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV fluids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Broad-spectrum <strong>IV antibiotics</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antibiotic therapy can be continued with careful monitoring if fluid collection is<strong> &lt;5 cm</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>CT guided percutaneous drainage</strong> if antibiotic therapy fails or fluid collection is <strong>&gt; 5 cm</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgical exploration and resection if there is perforation, obstruction, or fistulization</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Regular follow-up with imaging</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Oral antibiotics have no proven benefit in the outpatient management of acute uncomplicated diverticulitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2860","text":"A 55-year-old man is broug‌ht to the casualty with a​ history of fever and abd‍ominal pain. The pain was‌ initially in the left lo​wer quadrant for 2 days b‌ut is diffuse and severe ​for the past 4 hours. The​ erect X-ray of the chest​ showed the presence of p‌neumoperitoneum. Emergenc​y laparotomy was performe‌d. It showed the presence​ of perforated left-sided‌ diverticulitis with feca​l peritonitis. How will y​ou manage the patient? Sa​line irrigation Resection​ Anastamosis End-colostom​y Pigtail catheter insert​ion","raw_text":"<p>A 55-year-old man is brought to the casualty with a history of fever and abdominal pain. The pain was initially in the left lower quadrant for 2 days but is diffuse and severe for the past 4 hours. The erect X-ray of the chest showed the presence of pneumoperitoneum. Emergency laparotomy was performed. It showed the presence of perforated left-sided diverticulitis with fecal peritonitis. How will you manage the patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Saline irrigation </li>\n<li>Resection</li>\n<li>Anastamosis </li>\n<li>End-colostomy </li>\n<li>Pigtail catheter insertion</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c01410a8e38c4cb99fb7543757f0116dx1200x1758.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 4</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>fecal peritonitis</strong> due to perforation of left-sided diverticulitis. This is managed by<strong> Hartmann's procedure. </strong>It involves segmental <strong>resec­tion</strong>, a proximal-<strong>end colostomy</strong>, and closure of the<strong> rectal stump</strong>.</p>\n<p>If the perforation of the diverticulitis leads to a localized <strong>pericolic abscess formation</strong>, then it is treated with antibiotics alone or along with percutaneous drainage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2855","text":"An elderly male patient pr‌esents to the hospital wi​th complaints of abdomina‍l pain, increased frequen‌cy of micturition, and pa​ssing of air bubbles in u‌rine. He was diagnosed wi​th diverticulitis 2 years​ before and was treated w​ith antibiotics. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"An elderly male patient presents to the hospital with complaints of abdominal pain, increased frequency of micturition, and passing of air bubbles in urine. He was diagnosed with diverticulitis 2 years before and was treated with antibiotics. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coloenter‌ic fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colocutane‌ous fistula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colovesic‌al fistula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emphysemato‌us cystitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colovesical fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colovesical fistula</strong></p><p>The history of diverticulitis and pneumaturia point towards a diagnosis of <strong>colovesical fistula</strong>. The patients present with the symptoms of <strong>urinary tract infection</strong> caused by colonic flora, <strong>pneumaturia</strong> (air bubbles in urine), and fecaluria (foul-smelling and brownish material in the urine).</p>\n<p><strong>Complications of diverticular disease</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Perforation leading to a pericolic abscess or generalized peritonitis</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction due to fibrosis</li>\n<li>Fistula formation (<strong>c</strong><strong>olovesical,</strong> coloenteric, colovaginal)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Emphysematous cystitis</strong> can also present with pneumaturia. It is caused by urinary tract infection (UTI) with a <strong>gas-forming organism</strong>, especially in people with diabetes or patients with urinary tract outflow obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2857","text":"A CECT confirmed severe ac‌ute diverticulitis in a 3​4-year-old brought to the‍ emergency with lower abd‌ominal pain. A Foley's ca​theter was promptly place‌d to prevent a certain co​mplication. Which of the ​following statements is f​alse regarding the compli‌cation?","raw_text":"A CECT confirmed severe acute diverticulitis in a 34-year-old brought to the emergency with lower abdominal pain. A Foley's catheter was promptly placed to prevent a certain complication. Which of the following statements is false regarding the complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It may progress to pyelone‌phritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contrast enema is used to ‌identify the course the p​athology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Previous hysterectomy may ‌increase the risk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It more commonly occurs in‌ females","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It more commonly occurs in females","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It more commonly occurs in females</strong></p><p>The <strong>colovesical fistula</strong> is more <strong>common in men</strong> than in women because the <strong>uterus prevents the sigmoid from adhering to the bladder.</strong></p>\n<p>Hence, females who have undergone hysterectomy are at increased risk.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of a colovesical fistula are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pneumaturia (flatus in urine)</li>\n<li>Fecaluria (feces in urine)</li>\n<li>Suprapubic pain</li>\n<li>Dysuria</li>\n<li>Urgency</li>\n<li>Frequency</li>\n<li>Gross hematuria (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pneumaturia is usually appreciated toward the end of urination because the collection of gas usually sits at the top of the bladder. The finding pneumaturia or fecaluria is virtually pathognomonic for urinary tract fistulas, and patients presenting with this condition should be presumed to have a colovesical fistula and investigated accordingly.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Over time, cystitis associated with a colovesical fistula may evolve to pyelonephritis.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>An abdominopelvic CT with oral or rectal contrast is the investigation of choice. A contrast enema may be used to identify the course of the fistula. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2876","text":"A patient presented with f‌ever, diarrhea, and abdom​inal pain. She has a hist‍ory of recent intensive c‌are unit admission for se​psis which required prolo‌nged antibiotic therapy. ​Sigmoidoscopy findings ar​e given below. Choose the​ false statement regardin‌g the given condition.","raw_text":"A patient presented with fever, diarrhea, and abdominal pain. She has a history of recent intensive care unit admission for sepsis which required prolonged antibiotic therapy. Sigmoidoscopy findings are given below. Choose the false statement regarding the given condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subtotal colectomy with en‌d ileostomy is done in fu​lminant disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV vancomycin is used for ‌treatment of recurrent di​sease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fecal microbiota transplan‌tation (FMT) is an emergi​ng treatment option","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stool sample is used for P‌CR detection of toxins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IV vancomycin is used for treatment of recurrent disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ddfd67758cc649bfa24a19354c9e8e51.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IV vancomycin is used for treatment of recurrent disease</strong></p><p>A history of<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> diarrhea </span></strong>associated with<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> prolonged antibiotic treatment </span></strong>and characteristic adherent<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> pseudomembranes </span></strong>in the colon<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong>is highly suggestive of pseudomembranous colitis.<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span>Oral Fidaxomicin</strong> is commonly used in <strong>recurrent </strong>pseudomembranous colitis.</p>\n<p>The causative antibiotic agent must be discontinued and infection control measures must be taken for all patients. </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Initial episode - non severe: </strong>WBC ≤15,000/mm3, Creatinine &lt; 1.5 mg/dL</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral <strong>Fidaxomicin</strong>(drug of choice) or <strong>Vancomycin</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral Metronidazole can be given if Vancomycin/Fidaxomycin is not available</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Initial episode - severe:</strong> &gt;15,000/mm3, Creatinine &gt;1.5 mg/dL\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral <strong>Fidaxomicin</strong> or <strong>Vancomycin</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Consider fecal microbiota transplantation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Initial episode - fulminant</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension, ileus or megacolon\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Oral vancomycin </strong>and<strong> IV metronidazole</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rectal Vancomycin can be used if ileus present </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<ul>\n<li>Overwhelming sepsis, colonic perforation, or peritonitis\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subtotal colectomy </strong>with <strong>end ileostomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>First recurrence</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Repeat course of antibiotics for another 10-20 days</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral <strong>Fidaxomicin</strong>(drug of choice) or <strong>Vancomycin</strong> can be used.</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Repeated recurrence</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Consider fecal microbiota transplantation</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Subtotal colectomy with end ileostomy</strong> is the procedure of choice for patients requiring surgery for <strong>severe colitis or toxic megacolon</strong>. It allows for disease control, stabilization, and preservation of reconstruction options for the future. Segmental colectomy, ileostomy, or colostomy alone does not address the pancolonic nature of the disease. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Fecal microbiota transplan­tation (FMT)</strong> is emerging as a promising treatment option for<strong> recurrent and recalcitrant </strong>C. difficile infection. Using FMT, donor stool is administered to the patient through one of a variety of described routes (nasogastric tube, colonoscopic lavage, or upper endoscopy) to <strong>restore colonic flora</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pseudomembranous colitis is caused by <em><strong>Clostridium difficile</strong></em> which is a gram-positive, <strong>spore-forming anaerobic microor­ganism</strong>. The pathogenesis is mediated by the enterotoxins -<strong> toxin A</strong> and toxin B. They can be detected in the <strong>stool </strong>using<strong> PCR</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1906","text":"An elderly male patient is‌ brought to the casualty ​with complaints of vomiti‍ng and severe abdominal p‌ain. The abdomen appears ​grossly distended, is ten‌der to touch, and percuss​ion reveals hyper-resonan​ce. His X-ray abdomen is ​shown in the image below.‌ What is the next best st​ep in the management of t‌his patient?","raw_text":"An elderly male patient is brought to the casualty with complaints of vomiting and severe abdominal pain. The abdomen appears grossly distended, is tender to touch, and percussion reveals hyper-resonance. His X-ray abdomen is shown in the image below. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urgent co‌lonoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laparotomy followed by sig‌moid resection alone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laparotomy followed by Har‌tmann’s procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insertion of a rectal tube‌ through a flexible sigmo​idoscope","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Insertion of a rectal tube through a flexible sigmoidoscope","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c0d1607b1364176a72b38eb817f87d9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Insertion of a rectal tube through a flexible sigmoidoscope</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the X-ray radiograph showing characteristic <strong>coffee bean appearance </strong>are highly suggestive of <strong>sigmoid volvulus</strong>. Non-operative decompression followed by insertion of a<strong> rectal tube</strong> through a rigid proctoscope or a flexible sigmoidoscope is the next best step in the management of this patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Sigmoid volvulus</strong> is a cause of large bowel obstruction and occurs when the sigmoid colon twists on the sigmoid mesocolon. Symptoms<strong> </strong>include constipation, abdominal bloating, nausea, and/or vomiting. Onset may be acute or chronic. It occurs more commonly in patients with a history of mental illness and chronic constipation.</p>\n<p><strong>X-ray abdomen</strong> shows a large, dilated loop of the colon, often with a few air-fluid levels. Specific signs include the <strong>coffee bean sign </strong>and<strong> </strong>the<strong> absence of rectal gas. </strong><strong>CT</strong> may reveal large gas-filled loop without haustral markings, forming a closed-loop obstruction.</p>\n<p>Non-operative decompression is successful in treating 90% of the patients. The rectal tube should be left in place for 1-2 days to allow complete decompression and to prevent immediate recurrence of the volvulus. This should be followed by bowel cleansing with cathartics and a <strong>complete colonoscopic examination</strong>. In patients with signs of colonic necrosis or in whom endoscopic detorsion has failed, sigmoid colectomy with the closure of the rectum and end colostomy (<strong>Hartmann procedure</strong>) maybe required. Even if detorsion of the sigmoid is successful,<strong> elective sigmoid resection</strong> is indicated to prevent recurrence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2869","text":"A lady is brought to the c‌asualty department with a​ 2-day history of diffuse‍ abdominal pain. She has ‌had several episodes of b​loody diarrhea for the pa‌st 8 hours and appears de​hydrated. On examination,​ her pulse is feeble and ​BP is 80/55 mmHg. An abdo‌minal x-ray shows the \"th​umbprinting sign.” What c‌ould be the likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A lady is brought to the casualty department with a 2-day history of diffuse abdominal pain. She has had several episodes of bloody diarrhea for the past 8 hours and appears dehydrated. On examination, her pulse is feeble and BP is 80/55 mmHg. An abdominal x-ray shows the \"thumbprinting sign.” What could be the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diverti‌culitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ischemic‌ colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomembra‌nous colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both b ‌&amp;amp; c","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both b & c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0af2f645478841ce97f15aa69bc09451x514x511.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both b &amp; c</strong></p><p>The<strong> thumbprinting sign</strong> is seen in both <strong>ischemic colitis </strong>and<strong> pseudomembranous colitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Bowel wall \"thumbprinting\" (the appearance of thumbprint-shaped projections) is a radiological sign of <strong>submucosal</strong> <strong>thickening of the colonic wall.</strong> It can result from any process that leads to edema of the bowel wall, including pseudomembranous colitis, ischemic colitis, non-infective inflammatory bowel disease, other infective bowel diseases, as well as submucosal/intramural hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>The plain abdomen x-ray given below shows <strong>submucosal thickening with crescentic margins </strong>of the colon<strong> </strong>described as<strong> \"thumbprinting\" sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4295","text":"A 44-year-old female patie‌nt presents to the casual​ty with complaints of sud‍den onset of pain in the ‌abdomen. On examination, ​there is a palpable tympa‌nic swelling in the left ​side of the abdomen. X-ra​y abdomen of the patient ​is given below. Which of ‌the following is the most​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old female patient presents to the casualty with complaints of sudden onset of pain in the abdomen. On examination, there is a palpable tympanic swelling in the left side of the abdomen. X-ray abdomen of the patient is given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caecal ‌volvulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoid ‌volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxic m‌egacolon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fecal r‌etention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Caecal volvulus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/decd76fa589449998dfc698f1dd53bf2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b49a33dd53e042d2bef14e6b0a10f181x720x840.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75e06d8ab3cb427b974561a93c9f51b7x720x870.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Caecal volvulus</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the X-ray findings are suggestive of <strong>caecal volvulus.</strong> It is differentiated from sigmoid volvulus as there is only a <strong>single air-fluid level</strong> and the <strong>apex</strong> of the volvulus pointing towards the <strong>left upper quadrant</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in <strong>females</strong> in the<strong> 4th and 5th decade</strong> who can present with partial or complete intestinal obstruction. The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Marked abdominal distention</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obstipation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nausea and vomiting etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On examination, a <strong>palpable tympanic swelling</strong> in the midline or the left side may be seen. If there is <strong>strangulation</strong> or<strong> perforation</strong> of the obstructed bowel segment, <strong>peritoneal signs</strong> (rigidity, rebound tenderness etc.) can be present.</p>\n<p>Radiological findings include <strong>caecal dilatation</strong>, <strong>single air-fluid level</strong>, and absence of gas in the distal colon. On X-ray abdomen, the <strong>apex</strong> of the <strong>cecal volvulus</strong> points towards the <strong>left upper quadrant</strong> ( vs <strong>apex </strong>pointing towards the<strong> right upper quadrant </strong>in<strong> sigmoid volvulus</strong>)</p>\n<p>CECT abdomen is the investigation of choice that shows a <strong>whirl sign</strong> (also seen in the midgut volvulus).</p>\n<p>Management of cecal volvulus includes supportive therapy with nasogastric tube, iv fluids, antibiotics etc. and early surgical intervention.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perforated or strangulated cecal volvulus should be treated with <strong>right hemicolectomy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If there is no sign of perforation or strangulation, either <strong>cecopexy</strong> or <strong>right hemicolectomy</strong> can be performed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Sigmoid volvulus </strong>(image shown below) is a cause of large bowel obstruction and occurs when the sigmoid colon twists on the sigmoid mesocolon. <strong>X-ray abdomen</strong> shows a large, dilated loop of the colon, often with a few air-fluid levels. Specific signs include the <strong>coffee bean sign </strong>and<strong> </strong>the<strong> absence of rectal gas. On X-ray abdomen, </strong>the apex of the <strong>sigmoid volvulus</strong> points towards the right upper quadrant.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Toxic megacolon </strong>(image shown below) is defined as the <strong>dilatation of the transverse colon</strong> to a diameter of more than <strong>6 cm</strong>. It is usually associated with<strong> severe ulcerative colitis.</strong> It can be triggered by electrolyte imbalance and narcotics as they interfere with gut motility. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Fecal retention is an accumulation of a large amount of stool within any bowel segment, most commonly within the colon usually due to chronic constipation. A colonic distention with fecal material is often called <strong>fecal loading</strong> on the radiograph. It can be complicated by fecal impaction and subsequent bowel obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2865","text":"A middle-aged woman underw‌ent an open repair of an ​abdominal aortic aneurysm‍. The procedure was uneve‌ntful. However, on the 2n​d postoperative day, the ‌patient developed symptom​s of acute abdomen and bl​oody diarrhea with signif​icant guarding. The patie‌nt was taken up for an ex​ploratory laparotomy. Whi‌ch of the following areas​ is most likely to be inv‌olved?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman underwent an open repair of an abdominal aortic aneurysm. The procedure was uneventful. However, on the 2nd postoperative day, the patient developed symptoms of acute abdomen and bloody diarrhea with significant guarding. The patient was taken up for an exploratory laparotomy. Which of the following areas is most likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caecum and ascending colon‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic‌ flexure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splenic‌ flexure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoi‌d colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splenic flexure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60dfeab12cc94270852662f6216da6cbx1280x1624.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c70cfce7e07943f59051e8aca4f2e7acx600x663.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splenic flexure</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>ischemic colitis</strong> and it occurs most commonly at the <strong>splenic flexure.</strong></p>\n<p>Ischemic colitis occurs due to interruption or decrease in colonic blood flow. It may occur with many different conditions, including <strong>embolism</strong> from cardiac disease, <strong>low-flow states</strong> such as congestive heart failure, sepsis, and <strong>vasopressor</strong> use. <strong>Major vascular surgery</strong> (both open and endovascular repair), especially involving the abdominal aorta, is a well-known risk factor for developing ischemic colitis.</p>\n<p>Although any area of the colon may be affected, the <strong>watershed areas</strong> of the splenic flexure (Griffith’s point) and the rectosigmoid (Sudeck’s point) are the <strong>most prone to ischemia</strong> because of the often <strong>incomplete anastomoses</strong> of the marginal artery in these locations. The next most common area afflicted is the cecum, secondary to low blood flow in the terminal branches of the ileocolic artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Splenic flexure </strong>(also known as<strong> Griffith's point</strong>) is a watershed area between the <strong>superior</strong> and<strong> inferior mesenteric arteries.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sudeck point</strong> is a watershed area between the sigmoid arterial branch from the <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong> and the <strong>superior rectal artery </strong>(branch of Inferior mesentric artery).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>marginal artery of Drummond</strong> runs parallel to the colon, <strong>connecting the SMA and IMA circulation</strong> - the majority of blood flow to the left and sigmoid colon in case of <strong>occlusion of the IMA.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Arc of Riolan</strong> is a variable collateral vessel that connects the <strong>SMA (middle colic vessel) to the IMA (left colic).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2873","text":"A patient had a painless e‌pisode of profuse per rec​tal bleeding. After initi‍al stabilization, a colon‌oscopy was performed. The​ findings are given below‌. What is the most common​ site of this condition?","raw_text":"A patient had a painless episode of profuse per rectal bleeding. After initial stabilization, a colonoscopy was performed. The findings are given below. What is the most common site of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ce‌cum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transver‌se colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoi‌d colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cecum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46c43809a9f24d7889eb7384e8f52edd.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cecum</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and colonoscopy findings are suggestive of <strong>angiodysplasia</strong>.<strong> </strong>The <strong>cecum</strong> is the most common site of angiodysplasia followed by the colon.</p>\n<p>In the colon the angiodysplasia can occur anywhere, however it is commonly found in the right colon than the transverse and the left colon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2874","text":"A patient being evaluated ‌for recurrent lower GI bl​eeding secondary to colon‍ic angiodysplasia is susp‌ected to have Heyde syndr​ome. What are the other c‌ommon features associated​ with the condition?","raw_text":"A patient being evaluated for recurrent lower GI bleeding secondary to colonic angiodysplasia is suspected to have Heyde syndrome. What are the other common features associated with the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral stenosis and biliar‌y cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral stenosis and hiatus‌ hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic stenosis and von Wi‌llebrand disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PAH, cirrhosis, and tricus‌pid regurgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aortic stenosis and von Willebrand disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aortic stenosis and von Willebrand disease</strong></p><p><strong>Heyde’s syndrome </strong>is a multisystem disorder characterized by the clinical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aortic stenosis </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gastrointestinal bleeding</strong> from <strong>angiodysplasia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Acquired type 2 vWD</strong> (secondary to valvular stenosis, returns to normal once the valve is replaced)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dilated mucosal and submucosal veins are usually seen on colonoscopy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6761","text":"A 4-month-old infant prese‌nted with constipation si​nce birth. On a per recta‍l exam, a sudden release ‌of feces was seen. A cont​rast enema showed a dilat‌ed descending colon leadi​ng to a constricted segme​nt. Which of the followin​g is involved in the path‌ogenesis of the disease?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old infant presented with constipation since birth. On a per rectal exam, a sudden release of feces was seen. A contrast enema showed a dilated descending colon leading to a constricted segment. Which of the following is involved in the pathogenesis of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GD‌NF","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"INF ‌Alpha","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TNF ‌Alpha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TNF ‌Beta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GDNF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GDNF</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, with a positive <strong>blast sign</strong> (sudden release of feces on PR examination) and characteristic barium enema findings, are suggestive of <strong>Hirschsprung disease</strong>. Patients with Hirschsprung’s disease have an increased frequency of mutations in certain genes such as <strong>glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GCDNF).</strong></p>\n<p>GDNF promotes the <strong>survival, proliferation,</strong> and<strong> migration</strong> of neural crest cells in culture. GDNF is expressed in the gut in advance of migrating neural crest cells and is <strong>chemoattractive for neural crest cells</strong> in culture. Mutations are also seen its receptor <strong>Rearranged during Transfection (RET</strong><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\"><strong>)</strong>,</span> or its coreceptor<strong> Gfra-1</strong> <strong>(GDNF family receptor alpha-1).</strong></p>\n<p>Gene mutations in<strong> chromosomes 10 and 13,</strong> family history, and<strong> Down’s syndrome</strong> are also factors associated with Hirschsprung’s disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6762","text":"A neonate was brought to t‌he pediatrician on day 5 ​of life with bilious vomi‍ting and failure to pass ‌meconium. On examination,​ the abdomen was distende‌d. and X-Ray showed diste​nded bowel loops. Due to ​suggestive family history​, the resident suspected ‌Hirschsprung's disease. W​hat is the gold standard ‌investigation for this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A neonate was brought to the pediatrician on day 5 of life with bilious vomiting and failure to pass meconium. On examination, the abdomen was distended. and  X-Ray showed distended bowel loops. Due to suggestive family history, the resident suspected Hirschsprung's disease. What is the gold standard investigation for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U‌SG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barium‌ enema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rectal‌ biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anorectal‌ manometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rectal biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rectal biopsy</strong></p><p><strong>A rectal biopsy</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for the <strong>diagnosis of Hirschsprung disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It shows the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Absent ganglia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypertrophied nerve trunks</strong></li>\n<li>Positive immunostaining for <strong>acetylcholinesterase </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Calretinin immunostain­ing </strong>(superior to acetylcholinesterase staining)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Anorectal manometry</strong> is an important <strong>screening tool</strong>. It shows the <strong>absence of rectoanal inhibitory reflex.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6766","text":"From which of the followin‌g sites should a rectal b​iopsy be taken in order t‍o confirm the diagnosis o‌f Hirschsprung disease?","raw_text":"From which of the following sites should a rectal biopsy be taken in order to confirm the diagnosis of Hirschsprung disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 cm above the dentate lin‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 cm above the dentate lin‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"At the de‌ntate line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 cm below the dentate lin‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 cm above the dentate line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 cm above the dentate line</strong></p><p>The ideal site to obtain a rectal biopsy in a patient with Hirschsprung's disease is from a point that is at least <strong>2 cm above the dentate line. </strong>The internal sphincter extends up to 2 cm above the dentate line. This region is normally devoid of ganglionic cells. Hence, biopsy in this region may lead to errors in the results.</p>\n<p>Rectal biopsy is the <strong>gold standard investigation</strong> in patients with Hirschsprung's disease. It shows the presence of hypertrophied nerve trunks and the absence of ganglion cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6769","text":"All of the following are s‌urgeries you may plan in ​a child with aganglionic ‍megacolon, except?","raw_text":"All of the following are surgeries you may plan in a child with aganglionic megacolon, except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Swe‌nson","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duh‌amel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heineke ‌mikulicz","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"So‌ave","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heineke mikulicz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heineke mikulicz</strong></p><p><strong>Heineke-Mikulicz's</strong> operation is a type of <strong>pyloroplasty </strong>and is not done for aganglionic megacolon (Hirschsprung disease).</p>\n<p>Surgery is the treatment of choice for Hirschsprung disease. A multi-stage approach may be adopted. A colostomy is done in the newborn period, followed by a definitive pull-through operation after the child is over 10 kgs.</p>\n<p>Definitive surgeries for <strong>Hirschsprung</strong> disease are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Swenson</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Duhamel</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Soave</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2836","text":"What is the most common pr‌oblem that arises postope​ratively following surger‍y for Hirschsprung's dise‌ase?","raw_text":"What is the most common problem that arises postoperatively following surgery for Hirschsprung's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Soi‌ling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Entero‌colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incont‌inence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Constipation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Constipation</strong></p><p><strong>Constipation</strong> is the most <strong>common postoperative problem</strong> after surgery for Hirschsprung disease.</p>\n<p>Other problems are soiling, incontinence, and postop­erative enterocolitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6754","text":"Up to what length from the‌ anal verge will you be a​ble to visualize through ‍a flexible sigmoidoscope?‌","raw_text":"Up to what length from the anal verge will you be able to visualize through a flexible sigmoidoscope?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60‌ cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"70‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"80‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90‌ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 60 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fb2db708ccf47b9895075f0d6354165x520x366.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 60 cm</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Flexible sigmoidoscope</strong> is <strong>60 cm in length</strong> and can be used to <strong>visualize the rectum and sigmoid colon.</strong></p>\n<p>Flexible sigmoidoscopy is a procedure involving the following steps:</p>\n<p>A sigmoidoscope is inserted through the anus and the distal colonic mucosa (up to 60 cm from the anal verge) is visualized. Diagnostic or therapeutic maneuvers are performed, as needed.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p><strong>Scope</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p><strong>Length</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>Anoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>8 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>Proctoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>15 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>Rectoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>25 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>Flexible sigmoidoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>60 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>Colonoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"234\">\n<p>100–160 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6750","text":"Which structure would you ‌trace to identify enlarge​d paracolic lymph nodes?","raw_text":"Which structure would you trace to identify enlarged paracolic lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bowel‌ wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Margina‌l artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Main branches of the super‌ior mesenteric artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Named branches of inferior‌ mesenteric artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marginal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89fab04ff0da4e0d850859727d1690e8x1280x1980.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marginal artery</strong></p><p><strong>Paracolic lymph nodes</strong> are located <strong>along the marginal artery.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4322","text":"A 50-year-old man presents‌ with 2 episodes of bleed​ing per rectum over the p‍ast 2 hours. His vitals a‌re stable. Per rectal exa​mination was normal excep‌t for trace evidence of f​resh blood. Which of the ​following is the most app​ropriate intervention for‌ this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presents with 2 episodes of bleeding per rectum over the past 2 hours. His vitals are stable. Per rectal examination was normal except for trace evidence of fresh blood. Which of the following is the most appropriate intervention for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colon‌oscopy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Esophagogastroduodenoscopy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tagged ‌RBC scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angiography with embolizat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colonoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/281d0157e57f468dbd0338abbd651ddfx1280x2700.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colonoscopy</strong></p><p>In case of suspected lower GI bleeding or <strong>hematochezia</strong>, if the hemodynamic status of the patient is <strong>stable</strong>, <strong>colonoscopy</strong> with appropriate bowel preparation should be done at the earliest. A patient with hematochezia or fresh blood per rectum should be managed in the following way:</p>\n<p><strong>Hemodynamically unstable</strong> on initial assessment (or suspected massive UGI bleed):</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">At first, resuscitation with iv fluids, blood transfusion, and volume replacement</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perform <strong>EGD</strong> (Esophagogastroduodenoscopy)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Consider getting gastro surgery/interventional radiology consult</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If a <strong>source is not found on EGD</strong>,</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perform <strong>angiography ± embolization</strong> if still hemodynamically <strong>unstable</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perform <strong>colonoscopy</strong> if the patient is hemodynamically<strong> stable</strong> or, if a source was not identified on initial angiography</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If colonoscopy did not identify a bleeding source, consider further evaluation with</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Capsule endoscopy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tagged red blood cell scan</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Repeat EGD, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hemodynamically stable</strong> on initial assessment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bowel preparation and perform <strong>colonoscopy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If a source was not found on colonoscopy, EGD should be performed</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If a source was not found even on EGD, consider further evaluation with</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Capsule endoscopy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tagged red blood cell scan</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Repeat colonoscopy, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2872","text":"A 56-year-old man with wei‌ght loss and pallor comes​ to the physician. A CBC ‍and Fe studies indicate i‌ron deficiency anemia. Th​e patient denies any hist‌ory of bleeding disorders​ or changes in diet. A gu​aiac test is advised. Wha​t parameter does it test?‌","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man with weight loss and pallor comes to the physician. A CBC and Fe studies indicate iron deficiency anemia. The patient denies any history of bleeding disorders or changes in diet. A guaiac test is advised. What parameter does it test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microscopi‌c hematuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron excret‌ion in urine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occult blo‌od in stool","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iron mala‌bsorption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Occult blood in stool","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Occult blood in stool</strong></p><p><strong>Guaiac test</strong> is used for <strong>testing occult blood in the stool. </strong>New-onset iron deficiency anemia in middle-aged men should be thoroughly evaluated for colorectal carcinoma, even in the absence of other symptoms.</p>\n<p>Principle: Guaiac is a colorless organic compound that <strong>turns blue upon oxidation</strong> by oxidants, peroxidases, or pseudoperoxidase of hemoglobin.</p>\n<p>The test is more sensitive for <strong>lower GI bleed</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fecal hemoglobin must exceed <strong>10 mg/g of stool (blood loss of 10 ml daily),</strong> for the test to be positive in 50% of the cases, although the test can be positive in some cases even if hemoglobin is &lt;1mg/g of stool.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2875","text":"99mTc-labeled red blood ce‌ll scan can be used to de​tect gastrointestinal ble‍eding as low as _________‌_.","raw_text":"99mTc-labeled red blood cell scan can be used to detect gastrointestinal bleeding as low as __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 m‌L/min","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 m‌L/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 mL‌/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 mL‌/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.1 mL/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.1 mL/min</strong></p><p><strong><sup>99m</sup>Tc-labeled red blood cell scan</strong> can detect gastrointestinal bleeding as low as <strong>0.1 mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Selective angiography</strong>, using the superior or inferior mesenteric artery, can detect hemorrhage in the range of <strong>0.5 to 1.0 mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UTFH43cY","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"23_anaesthetic_implication_of_concurrent_diseases","title":"23.Anaesthetic Implication Of Concurrent Diseases","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MA5744","text":"Which of the following age‌nts is to be avoided in a‍ hypertensive patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents is to be avoided in a hypertensive patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketamine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ketamine</strong> is avoided in a hypertensive patient as it has <strong>intrinsic sympathomimetic properties </strong>and hence can aggravate hypertension.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ketamine increases arterial blood pressure, heart rate, and cardiac output. This is due to <strong>central stimulation </strong>of the<strong> sympathetic nervous system </strong>and <strong>inhibition </strong>of the<strong> reuptake of norepinephrine</strong>. And hence it should be cautiously used in patients with uncontrolled hypertension, coronary artery disease, congestive heart failure and arterial aneurysms.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its stimulatory effects may be beneficial to patients with<strong> acute shock. </strong>All other intravenous induction agents like propofol, thiopentone and etomidate produce a fall in blood pressure.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5746","text":"Which of the following mus‌cle relaxants should be a‍voided in a hypertensive ‌patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle relaxants should be avoided in a hypertensive patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancu‌ronium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rocur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrac‌urium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pancuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pancuronium</strong></p><p>Pancuronium is avoided in hypertensive patients because it causes <strong>vagal blockade </strong>and<strong> sympathetic stimulation</strong> which can aggravate hypertension.</p>\n<p>For the same reason, it is to be cautiously used in patients with-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coronary artery disease</li>\n<li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Aortic stenosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5752","text":"A 4-year-old child is obse‌rved to have difficulty i‍n breathing along with bl‌uish discolouration of hi‌s lips and fingers while ​playing. It improves on a‌dopting a knee-chest posi​tion. Which is the ideal ‍induction agent of choice​ used for surgical correc‍tion of the condition suf​fered by this patient?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child is observed to have difficulty in breathing along with bluish discolouration of his lips and fingers while playing. It improves on adopting a knee-chest position. Which is the ideal induction agent of choice used for surgical correction of the condition suffered by this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ketamine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ketamine</strong> is the induction agent of choice in this patient who is likely suffering from <strong>tetralogy of Fallot</strong> because it maintains or <strong>increases systemic vascular resistance </strong>and<strong> increases cardiac output. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tetralogy of Fallot is characterized by sub pulmonic stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, ventricular septal defect and overriding of the aorta. This results in a <strong>right to left shunt</strong> through the ventricular septal defect and consequent cyanosis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The commonest symptoms are <strong>dyspnea on exertion</strong> and exercise intolerance and develop <strong>blue skin </strong>(cyanosis) when agitated and are known as <strong>tet spells</strong>. The patients assume a <strong>sitting posture (squatting) </strong>or <strong>knee-chest position</strong> as soon as they get dyspneic, which leads to <strong>temporary symptomatic relief.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ketamine <strong>does not aggravate </strong>the<strong> right to left shunt</strong> seen in such cases. It also does not increase pulmonary vascular resistance in children as compared to adults and is hence safe to administer in children.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ketamine is the agent of choice in all cyanotic congenital heart diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5755","text":"A child with a wide fixed ‌split S2 on auscultation ‍has been scheduled for a ‌surgical repair for the c‌ondition. Which of the fo​llowing is the inhalation‌al agent of choice for in​duction in this procedure‍?","raw_text":"A child with a wide fixed split S2 on auscultation has been scheduled for a surgical repair for the condition. Which of the following is the inhalational agent of choice for induction in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desfl‌urane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Isofl‌urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Wide fixed split S2</strong> is suggestive of <strong>atrial septal defect (ASD) </strong>and <strong>sevoflurane</strong> is the <strong>inhalational agent of choice</strong> for induction in a child undergoing <strong>cardiac surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Halothane and sevoflurane are non-pungent and hence can be used for induction in pediatric patients. Sevoflurane is not associated with significant myocardial depression as is seen with halothane. Hence, sevoflurane is preferred over halothane especially in all cardiovascular surgeries in the paediatric age group.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 486\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Following induction, isoflurane and sevoflurane are the most commonly used inhalation agents for maintenance.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5756","text":"A patient with severe aort‌ic stenosis is posted for‍ surgery. Which is the pr‌eferred anaesthetic techn‌ique used for this proced​ure?","raw_text":"A patient with severe aortic stenosis is posted for surgery. Which is the preferred anaesthetic technique used for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intercostal‌ nerve block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. General anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. General anesthesia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> is preferred in patients with severe aortic stenosis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Spinal and epidural anaesthesia are <strong>relatively </strong>contraindicated. This is because <strong>regional anaesthesia</strong> produces profound hypotension which can lead to<strong> </strong>a<strong> fall in preload, afterload or both.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most general anaesthetics especially the volatile agents also cause vasodilation and hypotension which can be treated with escalating doses of vasopressors like phenylephrine.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Mild to moderate aortic stenosis</strong> are not contraindications to regional anaesthesia. In such situations, <strong>epidural anaesthesia</strong> is<strong> preferred over spinal</strong> because of its slower onset of hypotension which can be adequately corrected.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5757","text":"You are conducting the pre‌-anesthetic checkup for a‍ patient who is scheduled‌ for an elective cholecys‌tectomy. He is a known hy​pertensive being treated ‌with the following drugs.​ Which of the following d‍rugs should be discontinu​ed 24 hours before the pr‍ocedure?","raw_text":"You are conducting the pre-anesthetic checkup for a patient who is scheduled for an elective cholecystectomy. He is a known hypertensive being treated with the following drugs. Which of the following drugs should be discontinued 24 hours before the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium cha‌nnel blocker","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Centrally acting sympathom‌imetics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta b‌locker","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ACE in‌hibitor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ACE inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ACE inhibitor</strong></p><p>The anti-hypertensive that is discontinued 24hours before surgery is <strong>ACE inhibitor</strong> due to the <strong>risk of hemodynamic instability.</strong></p>\n<p>Although the continuation of ACE inhibitors or ARBs is controversial, among the given options this is the best answer. If these drugs are continued, vasopressin is the drug of choice for refractory hypotension.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5758","text":"During recovery from anaes‌thesia after a posterior ‍cranial fossa surgery, th‌e patient complained of a‌ severe headache and was ​agitated. CT scan showed ‌intracranial air accumula​tion. Which of the follow‍ing agents is most likely​ responsible for this con‍dition?","raw_text":"During recovery from anaesthesia after a posterior cranial fossa surgery, the patient complained of a severe headache and was agitated. CT scan showed intracranial air accumulation. Which of the following agents is most likely responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxy‌gen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl‌urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>pneumocephalus</strong> and <strong>nitrous oxide</strong> is responsible for causing this condition.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5759","text":"An awake craniotomy is bei‌ng performed on a patient‍ when there is a sudden d‌rop in his blood pressure‌. The surgeon is suspecti​ng a complication of veno‌us air embolism (VAE). Wh​ich of the following is t‍rue about the condition?","raw_text":"An awake craniotomy is being performed on a patient when there is a sudden drop in his blood pressure. The surgeon is suspecting a complication of venous air embolism (VAE). Which of the following is true about the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All VAE can be detected cl‌inically by a decrease in‍ end tidal carbon dioxide‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A decrease in oxygen satur‌ation will not always be ‍seen, even with larger em‌boli","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patients with left to righ‌t shunt, are susceptible ‍to paradoxical air emboli‌sm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The surgeon has no role in‌ the management of VAE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A decrease in oxygen saturation will not always be seen, even with larger emboli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A decrease in oxygen saturation will not always be seen, even with larger emboli</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The correct statement regarding venous air embolism (VAE) is, a decrease in oxygen saturation will not always be seen, even with larger emboli.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">VAE can occur when the pressure within an open vein is subatmospheric. It can occur in any position or procedure, whenever the wound is above the level of the heart. The<strong> incidence of venous air embolism </strong>is<strong> greater</strong> during <strong>sitting craniotomies</strong> than in craniotomies in any other position. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A<strong>: </strong>Physiological monitors (heart rate, blood pressure, oxygen saturation, and end-tidal carbon dioxide)<strong> </strong>have <strong>poor sensitivity </strong>and <strong>specificity</strong> and the clinical signs are manifested late. <strong>Transesophageal echocardiogram</strong> (TEE) is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> method to monitor and can detect up to <strong>0.02ml/kg</strong> of air embolism.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C<strong>: </strong>Patients with a <strong>right to left shunt </strong>are susceptible to <strong>paradoxical air emboli </strong>which can result in a stroke or coronary occlusion.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>The <strong>surgeon should be notified </strong>immediately so that he or she can flood the surgical field with saline or pack it with wet gauzes and apply bone wax to the skull edges until the entry site is identified and occluded.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5760","text":"A 40-year-old male in kidn‌ey failure with a serum c‍reatinine of 2.1 mg/dl is‌ scheduled for surgery. W‌hich of the following mus​cle relaxants can be used‌? Succinylcholine Atracur​ium D-tubocurarine Cis-at‍racurium Pancuronium","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old male in kidney failure with a serum creatinine of 2.1 mg/dl is scheduled for surgery. Which of the following muscle relaxants can be used?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Succinylcholine</li>\n<li>Atracurium</li>\n<li>D-tubocurarine</li>\n<li>Cis-atracurium</li>\n<li>Pancuronium</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3,4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 o‌nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Succinylcholine, atracurium</strong> and<strong> cis-atracurium</strong> can be used in this patient who has a <strong>renal failure</strong> with<strong> elevated serum creatinine</strong>(normal value- 0.8–1.3 mg/dL in men and 0.6–1 mg/dL in women).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Succinylcholine can be<strong> safely administered</strong> to patients with <strong>diminished </strong>or<strong> absent kidney function</strong> provided serum<strong> potassium levels </strong>are<strong> normal</strong>. If the serum potassium is increased, then a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant like atracurium or cisatracurium which is non-dependent on renal excretion is to be used. <strong>Atracurium </strong>or <strong>cisatracurium</strong> undergo <strong>Hofmann elimination</strong> which is non-enzymatic spontaneous degradation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pancuronium is almost entirely dependent on the kidney for excretion (60–90%). Pancuronium has a prolonged elimination half-life in renal failure patients and thus should be used very cautiously.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">40-60% of d-tubocurarine is excreted via the kidney and so prolonged effects are seen with renal insufficiency</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5773","text":"A patient is scheduled to ‌undergo transurethral res‍ection of the prostate. W‌hich is the preferred ana‌esthetic technique for th​is procedure?","raw_text":"A patient is scheduled to undergo transurethral resection of the prostate. Which is the preferred anaesthetic technique for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local inf‌iltration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinal anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinal anesthesia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Spinal anaesthesia</strong> is the preferred choice of anaesthetic for the <strong>TURP </strong>procedure.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Regional anaesthesia</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> over general anaesthesia in TURP due to the following reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less likely to mask the symptoms of TURP syndrome</li>\n<li><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">Helps assess patients mental status as an <strong>a</strong></span><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">lteration in mental status</strong><span style=\"text-align: justify;\"> is an </span><strong style=\"text-align: justify;\">early sign</strong><strong><span style=\"text-align: justify;\"> of TURP</span></strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>lesser</strong> amount of<strong> operative blood loss</strong> and lesser <strong>risk of</strong> <strong>DVT</strong>.</li>\n<li>Less likely to mask symptoms of bladder perforation like nausea, abdominal pain, dyspnea, shoulder pain, and hiccups.</li>\n<li>Decreased need for analgesics in the immediate post-operative period.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Spinal anaesthesia is preferred</strong> <strong>over epidural anaesthesia due to</strong>:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Easier to perform in elderly patients.</li>\n<li>Duration of surgery is not usually long, so an epidural is not required.</li>\n<li>Epidural anaesthesia may incompletely block the sacral nerve roots which can be avoided with spinal anaesthesia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Caudal anaesthesia </strong>may be used in high-risk patients undergoing<strong> laser prostatectomy</strong> as it provides the advantage of <strong>hemodynamic stability</strong>. Local infiltration does not provide sufficient operative analgesia as spinal anaesthesia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5777","text":"A 55-year-old female was d‌iagnosed to have primary ‍biliary cirrhosis and req‌uires liver transplantati‌on. Which of the followin​g neuromuscular blocker o‌f choice can be used in t​his patient during the tr‍ansplantation procedure?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old female was diagnosed to have primary biliary cirrhosis and requires liver transplantation. Which of the following neuromuscular blocker of choice can be used in this patient during the transplantation procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisatr‌acurium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pancu‌ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur‌onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cisatracurium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cisatracurium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The neuromuscular blocker (NMB) of choice in a cirrhotic patient is <strong>cisatracurium.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is used as NMB of choice because of its <strong>nonhepatic metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5779","text":"Which of the following is ‌an inhalational agent of ‍choice for a patient post‌ed for liver transplantat‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an inhalational agent of choice for a patient posted for liver transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enfl‌urane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Isofl‌urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sevoflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sevoflurane</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Sevoflurane</strong> is the inhalational anaesthetic agent of choice during<strong> liver transplantation</strong> because it is superior to other inhalational agents in preserving<strong> </strong>hepatic arterial blood flow and hepatic oxygen delivery.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The liver has an <strong>autoregulatory mechanism</strong> called the <strong>hepatic arterial buffer response(HABF)</strong>- when <strong>portal blood flow decreases</strong> due to decreased cardiac output or hypovolemia, the <strong>hepatic arterial blood flow increases</strong> in order to maintain total hepatic blood flow. This response is preserved in sevoflurane. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5782","text":"A 71-year-old male is post‌ed for total hip arthropl‍asty. What is the preferr‌ed anaesthetic technique ‌for this patient?","raw_text":"A 71-year-old male is posted for total hip arthroplasty. What is the preferred anaesthetic technique for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Local inf‌iltration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paracervi‌cal block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinal anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinal anesthesia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Spinal anaesthesia</strong> is preferred in <strong>elderly patients</strong> undergoing major vascular and orthopaedic surgeries.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They are preferred over general anaesthesia due to:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Decreased incidence of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism </li>\n<li>Local anaesthetics used will inhibit platelet aggregation and stabilize endothelial cells</li>\n<li>Regional anaesthesia is associated with less surgical blood loss</li>\n<li>Lower incidence of hypoxemia as it avoids airway instrumentation</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But the disadvantage with regional anaesthesia is that due to <strong>hypotension</strong>, patients with coronary artery disease will have increased oxygen demand due to reflex tachycardia and reduced coronary perfusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5783","text":"A 71-year-old patient unde‌rwent hip replacement sur‍gery. Postoperatively, a ‌target control infusion (‌TCI) pump was set up to a​dminister an opioid analg‌esic. Which is the most s​uitable opioid used for t‍his patient?","raw_text":"A 71-year-old patient underwent hip replacement surgery. Postoperatively, a target control infusion (TCI) pump was set up to administer an opioid analgesic. Which is the most suitable opioid used for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peth‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‌anyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Remife‌ntanyl","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Remifentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Remifentanyl</strong></p><p><strong>Remifentanil</strong> is the opioid that was most likely used in this elderly patient.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Remifentanil is hydrolysed by <strong>plasma </strong>and <strong>tissue esterases </strong>and so its metabolism is independent of hepatic and renal function- no dose adjustment is required and is <strong>safe</strong> in <strong>liver failure</strong> patients and <strong>renal failure</strong> patients.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Because of this hydrolysis, it has a <strong>high clearance</strong> and is <strong>eliminated</strong> <strong>rapidly</strong> with a quick recovery<strong> ( half-life<sup> </sup>less than 10 minutes)</strong> and thus is preferred in elderly patients. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5786","text":"A 39-year-old man had sust‌ained major injuries in a‍n accident as his legs we‌re trapped under the whee‌l of a car. Which of the ​following agents can be i‌deally used by the EMS pe​rsonnel to provide sedati‍on and pain relief to the​ patient while shifting h‍im to the hospital?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old man had sustained major injuries in an accident as his legs were trapped under the wheel of a car. Which of the following agents can be ideally used by the EMS personnel to provide sedation and pain relief to the patient while shifting him to the hospital?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mida‌zolam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ketamine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ketamine</strong> is the ideal agent of choice to provide sedation and analgesia in the<strong> prehospital setting.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Drugs that can be used by nurses and paramedics in the prehospital setting like the site of RTA/Trauma should have-</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Wide safety margin, even for inexperienced providers</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hemodynamic stability</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Minimal respiratory depression</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ease of administration</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Early-onset of action</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ketamine provides potent analgesia and sedation for entrapped patients with trauma during extrication. It <strong>maintains hemodynamic stability</strong> in patients with hypovolemic shock, is a <strong>potent bronchodilator</strong>, and causes <strong>minimal respiratory depression</strong>. Because of its hallucinogenic properties, <strong>benzodiazepines </strong>should be<strong> co-administered</strong>. In larger doses, it can be used for induction of anaesthesia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Benzodiazepines like diazepam and <strong>midazolam </strong>have wide safety margins and can be administered via many routes and can be used to provide sedation, but <strong>lack</strong> an<strong> analgesic action.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A and B: Propofol causes hemodynamic instability and respiratory depression and thiopentone produces effects similar to propofol.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5787","text":"A 40-year-old patient is b‌rought to the ER with hem‍atemesis. The patient goe‌s into haemorrhagic shock‌ and requires emergency s​urgical intervention. Whi‌ch of the following agent​s is not ideal for induct‍ion in this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient is brought to the ER with hematemesis. The patient goes into haemorrhagic shock and requires emergency surgical intervention. Which of the following agents is not ideal for induction in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propofol</strong></p><p>Propofol is not an ideal agent for induction in a patient with haemorrhagic shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Propofol </strong>when given to a patient in hemorrhagic shock will lead to<strong> worsening of shock</strong> due to the <strong>profound hypotensive action</strong> because of its <strong>vasodilatory </strong>and<strong> negative inotropic effects.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Etomidate <strong>preserves </strong>the<strong> sympathetic tone</strong> and has <strong>cardiovascular stability</strong>. Thus it is safer to use than propofol.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ketamine has been effectively used in trauma patients because it is a CNS stimulant. It has indirect cardiovascular effects due to central stimulation of the<strong> sympathetic nervous system</strong> and <strong>inhibition </strong>of the <strong>reuptake of norepinephrine</strong> which is beneficial in acute shock. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 192\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5788","text":"Shambu, a 55-year-old man ‌met with a road traffic a‍ccident and was brought t‌o the casualty. Radiograp‌hs revealed multiple rib ​fractures. Which of the f‌ollowing methods is used ​to treat this patient?","raw_text":"Shambu, a 55-year-old man met with a road traffic accident and was brought to the casualty. Radiographs revealed multiple rib fractures. Which of the following methods is used to treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General anaesthesia with e‌ndotracheal intubation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal an‌aesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural a‌naesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infraclavi‌cular block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epidural anaesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epidural anaesthesia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Epidural anaesthesia </strong>is used to provide adequate analgesia which is the mainstay of treatment in patients with multiple rib fractures.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The complications can be minimized by adequate pain relief with epidural anaesthesia. Rib fracture patients will have the following complications due to splinting and pain:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Hypoventilation</li>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other techniques to provide analgesia following rib fractures are <strong>intercostal nerve block </strong>and<strong> intrapleural block</strong> which necessitates <strong>multiple injections</strong>, which increases the risk for complications.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The complications include<strong> vascular puncture, pneumothorax</strong>, patient discomfort due to the need for palpation of the fractured rib during local anaesthetic (LA) administration. Also, <strong>LA toxicity</strong> can occur because of the high systemic absorption. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: General Anaesthesia is more invasive to manage patient with multiple rib fractures and has potentially more side effects and complications than Epidural Anaesthesia. Endotracheal intubation as treatment is reserved only for patients who cannot maintain an airway or maintain sufficient oxygen saturation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B<strong>: </strong>Spinal anaesthesia involves a high level of anaesthesia which is required to cover the thoracic dermatomes which would lead to high spinal or total spinal anaesthesia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Infraclavicular block is a brachial plexus block that is used for the upper limbs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5796","text":"A 30-year-old male is sche‌duled for an open reducti‍on of a fractured nose. T‌he preferred technique of‌ anaesthesia in this pati​ent would be?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male is scheduled for an open reduction of a fractured nose. The preferred technique of anaesthesia in this patient would be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌naesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Local inf‌iltration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regional a‌naesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topical a‌naesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. General anaesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. General anaesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> is preferred in this patient because of the insufficient block and patient discomfort associated with local anaesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Local anaesthesia</strong> may be suitable for simple procedures such as-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cauterization</li>\n<li>Polypectomy</li>\n<li>Turbinectomy </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>General anaesthesia </strong>may be used for<strong> </strong>the following procedures-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Open nasal fracture reduction</li>\n<li>Septoplasty</li>\n<li>Rhinoplasty</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note<strong>- Closed nasal fracture </strong>reduction is performed with<strong> propofol sedation</strong> as the procedure is extremely painful.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5799","text":"A 55-year-old chronic smok‌er has been diagnosed wit‍h epiglottic carcinoma. T‌he ENT surgeon has schedu‌led a CO2 laser excision ​surgery. Which of the fol‌lowing anaesthetic gases ​needs to be avoided durin‍g the procedure?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old chronic smoker has been diagnosed with epiglottic carcinoma. The ENT surgeon has scheduled a CO2 laser excision surgery. Which of the following anaesthetic gases needs to be avoided during the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxy‌gen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p>Nitrous oxide is avoided in laser surgeries as it supports combustion.</p>\n<p>Oxygen concentration should be limited to the lowest, so as to maintain acceptable saturation levels to prevent the possibility of fire while using lasers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5800","text":"A stapedectomy has been sc‌heduled for a patient suf‍fering from Ménière’s dis‌ease. Which is the prefer‌red intravenous induction​ agent for the procedure?‌","raw_text":"A stapedectomy has been scheduled for a patient suffering from Ménière’s disease. Which is the preferred intravenous induction agent for the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Propofol</strong></p><p><strong>Propofol</strong> is preferred for induction in patients undergoing stapedectomy or any ear surgery, especially the inner ear.</p>\n<p>Surgeries that can be conducted with propofol as induction agent-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stapedectomy or stapedotomy</li>\n<li>Tympanoplasty</li>\n<li>Mastoidectomy</li>\n<li>Myringotomy with insertion of tympanostomy tubes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5802","text":"A 32-year-old male is suff‌ering from chronic suppur‍ative otitis media of the‌ right ear. He is schedul‌ed for a tympanoplasty su​rgery. Which of the follo‌wing agents should be avo​ided during the procedure‍?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old male is suffering from chronic suppurative otitis media of the right ear. He is scheduled for a tympanoplasty surgery. Which of the following agents should be avoided during the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxy‌gen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong> is avoided in middle ear surgeries.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is because nitrous oxide can <strong>increase middle ear pressure</strong> as it can diffuse into air-containing cavities because it is more soluble in blood than nitrogen.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If nitrous oxide had been used in an <strong>overlay procedure</strong>, it would lead to<strong> pressure build-up </strong>inside the middle ear and displacement of the graft. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5804","text":"Which of the following age‌nts should not be used du‍ring superficial parotide‌ctomy surgery scheduled f‌or a case of pleomorphic ​adenoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents should not be used during superficial parotidectomy surgery scheduled for a case of pleomorphic adenoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhalational‌ anaesthetics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opi‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle r‌elaxants","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Muscle relaxants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Muscle relaxants</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Muscle relaxants</strong> are avoided during a procedure of parotidectomy. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common surgery for pleomorphic adenoma is <strong>superficial parotidectomy </strong>with<strong> facial nerve dissection.</strong> It is essential to preserve the facial nerve and hence, nerve stimulators are used during the surgery to identify the facial nerve and its branches. This cannot be possible if muscle relaxation is present.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5805","text":"A 23-year-old woman wants ‌to undergo an aesthetic r‍hinoplasty. Which of the ‌following is the safest d‌rug that can be used duri​ng the surgery for topica‌l vasoconstriction?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman wants to undergo an aesthetic rhinoplasty. Which of the following is the safest drug that can be used during the surgery for topical vasoconstriction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenyl‌ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxymet‌azoline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oxymetazoline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oxymetazoline</strong></p><p><strong>Oxymetazoline</strong> is the preferred topical vasoconstrictor in ENT surgeries due to the safety profile and easy availability of the drug.</p>\n<p><strong>0.05%</strong> solution is administered as 3 nasal sprays in each nostril before any nasal procedure. It should be<strong> avoided </strong>in patients who are on<strong> monoamine oxidase inhibitors </strong>as the interaction can result in <strong>high blood pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Phenylephrine is an α-adrenergic agonist topical vasoconstrictor. It can cause severe hypertension and blood pressure monitoring is required.</p>\n<p>Option C<strong>: </strong>Cocaine has cardiovascular effects- it is <strong>sympathomimetic </strong>and when administered at a <strong>concentration of 4%</strong>, it inhibits the reuptake of norepinephrine at sympathetic nerve terminals thus making it <strong>unsafe </strong>in patients with <strong>coronary artery disease</strong> or those on monoamine oxidase inhibitors. </p>\n<p>Option A: Lidocaine as such does not have vasoconstrictor properties but may be used with phenylephrine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5806","text":"Which of the following age‌nts cannot be used to ach‍ieve hypotensive anaesthe‌sia during nasopharyngeal‌ carcinoma surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents cannot be used to achieve hypotensive anaesthesia during nasopharyngeal carcinoma surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phento‌lamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium nit‌roprusside","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl‌ephrine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phenylephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phenylephrine</strong></p><p>Phenylephrine cannot be used to achieve controlled hypotension during nasopharyngeal carcinoma surgeries.</p>\n<p>Phenylephrine is a <strong>selective α₁-adrenergic receptor agonist</strong> of the phenethylamine class and can cause an increase in blood pressure. Hence it cannot be used to achieve hypotensive anaesthesia in any procedures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5809","text":"During a squint surgery, t‌he ophthalmologist hooks ‍a muscle that causes sudd‌en significant bradycardi‌a in the patient. Atropin​e is given and the heart ‌rhythm returns to normal.​ This reflex is most like‍ly seen with traction on ​which muscle?","raw_text":"During a squint surgery, the ophthalmologist hooks a muscle that causes sudden significant bradycardia in the patient. Atropine is given and the heart rhythm returns to normal. This reflex is most likely seen with traction on which muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio‌r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferio‌r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial‌ rectus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral‌ rectus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medial rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medial rectus</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>medial rectus</strong> muscle is the most likely muscle to elicit bradycardia <strong>(oculocardiac reflex) </strong>when stimulated.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traction on the extraocular muscles or pressure on the globe causes bradycardia, atrioventricular block, ventricular ectopy, or asystole and is known as oculocardiac reflex. In particular, it is seen with traction on the <strong>medial rectus muscle</strong>, but it can occur with stimulation of any of the orbital contents, including the periosteum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5810","text":"A child while undergoing e‌nucleation of his right e‍ye due to bacterial endop‌hthalmitis, suddenly deve‌loped bradycardia. Manipu​lation of which of the fo‌llowing structures would ​have led to the bradycard‍ia?","raw_text":"A child while undergoing enucleation of his right eye due to bacterial endophthalmitis, suddenly developed bradycardia. Manipulation of which of the following structures would have led to the bradycardia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital p‌eriosteum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior‌ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial‌ rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Any of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Any of the above</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Stimulation of any of the orbital contents can cause oculocardiac reflex. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traction on the extraocular muscles or pressure on the globe causes bradycardia, atrioventricular block, ventricular ectopy, or asystole and is known as oculocardiac reflex. In particular, it is seen with traction on the <strong>medial rectus muscle</strong>, but it can occur with stimulation of any of the orbital contents, including the periosteum.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5812","text":"A 60-year-old female patie‌nt needs to undergo catar‍act surgery. Before initi‌ating the surgery, the fo‌llowing procedure is perf​ormed. What is the most c‌ommon complication seen i​n the procedure?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old female patient needs to undergo cataract surgery. Before initiating the surgery, the following procedure is performed. What is the most common complication seen in the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subconjunctival hemorrhage‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic ner‌ve damage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrobulbar‌ hemorrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retrobulbar hemorrhage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5eb4eeb027f8404bbbb71475c1295991.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retrobulbar hemorrhage</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common complication seen in the procedure of retrobulbar block is <strong>retrobulbar haemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The image shown above depicts the technique of <strong>retrobulbar block </strong>which is a regional anaesthesia technique used in ophthalmic surgery. It involves the injection of a local anaesthetic into the extraocular muscle cone. The <strong>needle penetrates</strong> the <strong>lower lid</strong> at the junction of the<strong> middle </strong>and<strong> lateral one-third </strong>of the orbit.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5815","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is a retrobulb‍ar block not contraindica‌ted?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is a retrobulbar block not contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High ‌myopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coagul‌opathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Penetrating‌ eye injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enucl‌eation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enucleation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enucleation</strong></p><p><strong>Retrobulbar blocks</strong> are not contraindicated in enucleation of the eyes.</p>\n<p>The Retrobulbar block is a regional anesthesia technique used in ophthalmic surgical procedures which involves the injection of a local anesthetic into the extraocular muscle cone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5818","text":"A 13-year-old boy was acci‌dentally shot in the eye ‍by his brother while play‌ing with a pellet gun. Ex‌amination reveals intraoc​ular contents presenting ‌at the wound. He is sched​uled for emergency repair‍ of the ruptured globe. W​hat is the ideal anaesthe‍tic technique to be used?​","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy was accidentally shot in the eye by his brother while playing with a pellet gun. Examination reveals intraocular contents presenting at the wound. He is scheduled for emergency repair of the ruptured globe. What is the ideal anaesthetic technique to be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retrobul‌bar block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subteno‌n block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peribulb‌ar block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. General anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. General anesthesia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> is the <strong>ideal anaesthetic technique</strong> to be used in a person who has to undergo surgery for a <strong>penetrating eye injury</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Regional anaesthesia</strong> is<strong> relatively contraindicated</strong> in patients with penetrating eye injuries because injecting local anaesthetic behind the globe increases intraocular pressure and may lead to the expulsion of intraocular contents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Therefore these patients require general anaesthesia—despite the increased risk of aspiration pneumonia when it is administered.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5821","text":"A child presenting with op‌en globe injury. Which in‍duction agent should be a‌voided?","raw_text":"A child presenting with open globe injury. Which induction agent should be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ketamine</strong></p><p>Ketamine should be avoided in a child presenting with open globe injury as it can <strong>increase intraocular pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>It usually raises arterial blood pressure and also does not relax extraocular muscles and may cause <strong>nystagmus</strong> and <strong>blepharospasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4467","text":"The best type of anesthesi‌a for pre eclampsia is:","raw_text":"The best type of anesthesia for pre eclampsia is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caudal‌ block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epidural anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epidural anesthesia</strong></p><p>Continuous epidural anesthesia is the first choice for most patients with preeclampsia during labor, vaginal delivery, and cesarean section.</p>\n<p>Pre-eclampsia patients have a risk of <strong>severe airway edema</strong>, which makes intubation difficult. Thus continuous epidural anesthesia is advantageous as it avoids the need for intubation. Besides, continuous epidural anesthesia can <strong>improve uteroplacental perfusion</strong> and also decrease catecholamine secretion.</p>\n<p>But one major prerequisite for regional anesthesia in preeclamptic patients is normal platelet count and normal coagulation. Coagulopathy is a contraindication for regional anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Minimal platelet count for regional anesthesia is 100,000/μL,</strong> but some cases can be taken up with a platelet count as low as 70,000/μL.</p>\n<p><strong>General anesthesia</strong> is indicated in patients with <strong>coagulopathy</strong> and in <strong>severe pre-eclampsia </strong>with complications.</p>\n<p><strong>Spinal anesthesia</strong> can be given in mild pre-eclampsia but hypotension associated with spinal anesthesia limits its use in moderate to severe pre-eclampsia. </p>\n<p>Since the severity of pre-eclampsia or the type of delivery (vaginal or cesarean) is not mentioned, epidural anesthesia has an advantage over other techniques for above-mentioned reasons. Hence the best option here would be epidural anesthesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5789","text":"Three combat wounded soldi‌ers with uncontrolled blo‍od loss are brought to th‌e base camp. Damage contr‌ol resuscitation(DCR) is ​initiated for the soldier‌s. What is the correct ra​tio of PRBCs, FFP, and pl‍atelets that can be used ​for DCR?","raw_text":"Three combat wounded soldiers with uncontrolled blood loss are brought to the base camp. Damage control resuscitation(DCR) is initiated for the soldiers. What is the correct ratio of PRBCs, FFP, and platelets that can be used for DCR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4:‌2:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1:‌2:4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2:‌1:2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1:‌1:1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1:1:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1:1:1</strong></p><p>In <strong>DCR</strong>, the ratio of PRBC: fresh frozen plasma: platelets is <strong>1:1:1</strong>. </p>\n<p>Administering blood products in the appropriate ratios in order to facilitate resuscitation from trauma and avert trauma-induced coagulopathy is termed <strong>damage control resuscitation(DCR)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The rationale behind 1:1:1 ratio- </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The red blood cell component will improve oxygen delivery to ischemic tissues.</li>\n<li>Fresh frozen plasma improves clotting by providing clotting <strong>factors V and VIII and fibrinogen.</strong></li>\n<li>Platelet transfusion is required if the platelet count is <strong>below 50,000</strong> / high power field, significant blood loss or prolonged resuscitation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ptbZ37Vc","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"23_diencephalon","title":"23.Diencephalon","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2642","text":"A man presented with frequ‌ent urination and increas‍ed thirst. MRI revealed t‌he absence of the posteri‌or pituitary bright spot.​ He was initiated on desm‌opressin which improved h​is symptoms. The affected‍ structure is derived fro‍m:","raw_text":"A man presented with frequent urination and increased thirst. MRI revealed the absence of the posterior pituitary bright spot. He was initiated on desmopressin which improved his symptoms. The affected structure is derived from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metenc‌ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelenc‌ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telenc‌ephalon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prosenc‌ephalon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prosencephalon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01d5f017efe54fb4898c9c4637082a3cx1280x1429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prosencephalon</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of polyuria and polydipsia along with the investigation findings is suggestive of <strong>central diabetes insipidus. </strong>It involves the<strong> d</strong><strong>iencephalon, </strong>a structure that is derived from the <strong>prosencephalon.</strong></p>\n<p>The diencephalon forms the <strong>thalamus, epithalamus, subthalamus, and hypothalamus.</strong></p>\n<p>The MRI given below shows the posterior pituitary bright spot. The arrow points at the posterior pituitary and the arrowhead at the anterior pituitary. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2644","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the anato‍mical relations of the th‌alamus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the anatomical relations of the thalamus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior pole of thalamus ‌forms the posterior bound‍ary of the interventricul‌ar foramen.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulvinar projects over the‌ superior colliculus.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial surface of thalamus‌ forms the inferior bound‍ary of III ventricle.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The posterior limb of the ‌internal capsule is later‍ally related to the thala‌mus.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medial surface of thalamus forms the inferior boundary of III ventricle.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/555d2d16600d4fd691228702d9338d20x600x677.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86d7c51041e944d3b2b8a0c6049537fcx1280x1298.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/541a0584cdf9481badb0f230592b414bx600x749.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medial surface of thalamus forms the inferior boundary of III ventricle.</strong></p><p>The <strong>medial surface of the thalamus</strong> does not form the inferior boundary of the third ventricle. It forms the <strong>lateral wall of the IIIrd ventricle.</strong></p>\n<p>The thalamus is an ovoid shape mass of grey matter that forms a relay station for all information passing to the cerebral cortex, basal ganglia, hypothalamus, and brainstem.</p>\n<p><strong>Relations of the thalamus: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anteriorly: Interventricular foramen </li>\n<li>Superiorly: It is covered by a sheet of white matter called <strong>stratum zonale</strong>. Above the stratum zonale lies the body of fornix and the ventricle</li>\n<li>Posteriorly: The posterior end is expanded to form the pulvinar, which overhangs above the superior colliculus. </li>\n<li>Inferiorly: Plane connecting the hypothalamic sulci</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medially: Lateral wall of the third ventricle </li>\n<li>Laterally: Posterior limb of the internal capsule</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below demonstrates the lateral and medial relations of the thalamus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2646","text":"The following section of t‌he brain is given to you.‍ Which of the following i‌s true about the marked s‌tructure?","raw_text":"The following section of the brain is given to you. Which of the following is true about the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The external medullary lam‌ina is a sheet of grey ma‍tter on the lateral surfa‌ce of thalamus.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The intralaminar nuclei li‌e outside the internal me‍dullary lamina.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The internal medullary lam‌ina divides the thalamus ‍into three parts.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The internal medullary lam‌ina divides the thalamus ‍into two parts.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The internal medullary lamina divides the thalamus into three parts.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0152f32b804643fa8ffddda28c620926.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc4efdf396a142de99f7daf8cc92488cx1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The internal medullary lamina divides the thalamus into three parts.</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The marked structure ‘X’ is <strong>thalamus</strong>. <strong>Internal medullary lamina</strong> is a Y-shaped sheet of white matter dividing the thalamus to <strong>3 parts</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> nuclear groups</li>\n<li><strong>Medial</strong> nuclear groups</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong> nuclear groups</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>External medullary lamina</strong> is a sheet of white matter on the lateral surface of the thalamus that separates the thalamus from the reticular nucleus.</p>\n<p>The <strong>intralaminar nuclei</strong> lie embedded within the internal medullary lamina and the midline nuclei lie close to the lateral walls of the third ventricle.</p>\n<p>The <strong>thalamus</strong> plays an important role in sensory and motor integration.</p>\n<p>Input and output of the thalamus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-cortical input-</strong> All sensory system (except olfactory system) </li>\n<li><strong>Cerebral cortex input-</strong> Largest input (from modified pyramidal cells of layer VI )</li>\n<li><strong>Projection fibers to other areas-</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Cerebral cortex (most fibers)</li>\n<li>Basal nuclei and hypothalamus (few fibers)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2649","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the anterior ‍thalamic nuclei?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the anterior thalamic nuclei?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Largest subdivison is the ‌anteromedial nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Receives fibers from the m‌amillary body","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesion on this nuclei can ‌cause amnesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Involved in regulation of ‌alertness and attention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Largest subdivison is the anteromedial nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Largest subdivison is the anteromedial nucleus</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 487\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The largest subdivision of anterior thalamic nuclei is the <strong>anteroventral nucleus</strong> (and not the anteromedial nucleus). </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The<strong> anterior thalamic nuclei</strong> have 3 subdivisions—anteroventral nucleus, anteromedial nucleus and anterodorsal nucleus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major input: <strong>Mamillothalamic tract</strong> from the mammillary nuclei of the hypothalamus.</li>\n<li>Major cortical output: <strong>Anterior limbic area, cingulate, and the parahippocampal gyrus</strong> </li>\n<li>Major function:\n<ul>\n<li>Regulation of <strong>alertness and attention</strong></li>\n<li>Acquisition of <strong>memory</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2651","text":"The medial nucleus of the ‌thalamus predominantly re‍ceives input from the ___‌_ and sends output to the‌ _____.","raw_text":"The medial nucleus of the thalamus predominantly receives input from the ____ and sends output to the _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mamillary bodies, Cingulat‌e gyrus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothalamus, Prefrontal c‌ortex","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Substantia nigra, Motor co‌rtex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebellum, ‌Motor cortex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypothalamus, Prefrontal cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypothalamus, Prefrontal cortex</strong></p><p>The medial nucleus of the thalamus predominantly receives input from the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> and sends output to the <strong>prefrontal cortex</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> medial thalamic nuclei </strong>contain the large dorsomedial nucleus and several smaller nuclei:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internally, the dorsomedial nucleus is connected to all other thalamic nuclei.</li>\n<li>Externally, it has 2 major connections—the prefrontal cortex and the hypothalamus. Its functions include integration of somatic, visceral, and olfactory sensory information as well as relation of this sensory information to emotional feelings and subjective states.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2652","text":"A 56-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with thalamic syn‍drome. The largest divisi‌on of the affected struct‌ure is:","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman was diagnosed with thalamic syndrome. The largest division of the affected structure is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior tha‌lamic nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral tha‌lamic nuclei","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medial thal‌amic nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intralami‌nar nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral thalamic nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral thalamic nuclei</strong></p><p>The <strong>largest division</strong> of the affected structure i.e. the thalamus is the <strong>lateral thalamic nuclei</strong>. </p>\n<p>The lateral thalamic nuclei are further divided into the <strong>dorsal </strong>and <strong>ventral </strong>nuclei, as shown below.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2653","text":"Which of the following cor‌rectly matches the thalam‍ic nuclei with the region‌ it receives input from?","raw_text":"Which of the following correctly matches the thalamic nuclei with the region it receives input from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventral anterior nucleus -‌ Medial lemniscal pathway‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventral lateral nucleus - ‌Spinothalamic pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral posterolateral nuc‌leus - Gustatory pathway","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral posteromedial nucl‌eus - Trigeminothalamic p‍athway","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ventral posteromedial nucleus - Trigeminothalamic pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ventral posteromedial nucleus - Trigeminothalamic pathway</strong></p><p>The <strong>ventral posteromedial nucleus</strong> receives inputs from the <strong>trigeminothalamic pathway.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2655","text":"Choose the incorrect state‌ment with respect to thal‍amic nuclei.","raw_text":"Choose the incorrect statement with respect to thalamic nuclei.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intralaminar nuclei influe‌nces the levels of consci‍ousness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial geniculate body rec‌eives information from bo‍th the ears, but predomin‌antly from contralateral ‌ear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral geniculate body re‌ceives visual information‍ from opposite field of v‌ision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial geniculate body rec‌eives information from bo‍th the ears, but predomin‌antly from ipsilateral ea‌r","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial geniculate body receives information from both the ears, but predominantly from ipsilateral ear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial geniculate body receives information from both the ears, but predominantly from ipsilateral ear</strong></p><p>The <strong>medial geniculate body</strong> receives auditory input from both the ears, but predominantly from the <strong>contralateral</strong> ear (not ipsilateral ear).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2656","text":"MRI of the coronal section‌ of the brain is given to‍ you. Which of the follow‌ing nuclei of the marked ‌structure does not projec​t to neocortex?","raw_text":"MRI of the coronal section of the brain is given to you. Which of the following nuclei of the marked structure does not project to neocortex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intralami‌nar nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulvina‌r nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reticula‌r nuclei","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Midline‌ nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reticular nuclei","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec4707baa6034006b60b77a7c75f4e2b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reticular nuclei</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the <strong>thalamus</strong>. The <strong>reticular nucleus</strong> of the thalamus does not project to the <strong>neocortex</strong>. Its output is mainly to the other thalamic nuclei.</p>\n<p>All other thalamic nuclei send output to the neocortex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2664","text":"A man sustained a head inj‌ury 6 weeks back, followi‍ng a road traffic acciden‌t. Now, he complains of i‌ncreased frequency of uri​nation and excessive thir‌st. An injury to which nu​cleus is likely to cause ‍the given condition?","raw_text":"A man sustained a head injury 6 weeks back, following a road traffic accident. Now, he complains of increased frequency of urination and excessive thirst. An injury to which nucleus is likely to cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preoptic‌ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraopti‌c nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suprachiasm‌atic nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paraventric‌ular nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Supraoptic nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Supraoptic nucleus</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 655\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Lesion of the <strong>supraoptic nucleus</strong> of the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> causes inhibition of the synthesis of <strong>vasopressin</strong> leading to <strong>diabetes insipidus.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> is a part of the <strong>diencephalon</strong> that lies below the thalamus. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anteriorly,</strong> the preoptic area is included in the hypothalamus.</li>\n<li><strong>Posteriorly,</strong> it merges with the tegmentum of the midbrain.</li>\n<li><strong>Laterally,</strong> it is bounded by the internal capsule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Microscopically, the hypothalamus is divided into multiple nuclei.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2665","text":"What structure is not a pa‌rt of the epithalamus?","raw_text":"What structure is not a part of the epithalamus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuber c‌inereum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Habenula‌r nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stria me‌dullaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ‌commissure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuber cinereum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuber cinereum</strong></p><p><strong>Tuber cinereum</strong> is not a part of the epithalamus. It is a part of the <strong>hypothalamus.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>epithalamus</strong> consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior and posterior paraventricular nuclei</li>\n<li>Medial and lateral habenular nuclei</li>\n<li>Stria medullaris thalami</li>\n<li>Posterior commissure </li>\n<li>Pineal gland</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0746","text":"A woman with right-sided l‌oss of sensations of both‍ the upper and lower limb‌ complains of shooting pa‌in from her fingers to th​e right shoulder and a bu‌rning sensation when touc​hing cold water. Motor fu‍nctions are normal. Which‍ of the following structu​res is likely to be invol‍ved?","raw_text":"A woman with right-sided loss of sensations of both the upper and lower limb complains of shooting pain from her fingers to the right shoulder and a burning sensation when touching cold water. Motor functions are normal. Which of the following structures is likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a5c5d4c972134a588be91039feecc823.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e0699fb45d84bdbb1b76f69e1165c1ax1280x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95bc3bab79dc4bff83c7593a6838bf51x600x577.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>The given clinical stem of a <strong>hemi-sensory loss</strong> and severe <strong>excruciating pain</strong> of the affected areas is suggestive of <strong>thalamic pain syndrome </strong>also known as <strong>Dejerine Roussy syndrome</strong>. Hence, <strong>thalamus </strong>marked as B is most likely to be involved.</p>\n<p>Occlusion of these <strong>thalamic and thalamogeniculate branches</strong> results in the <strong>thalamic pain syndrome</strong>. It is characterized by <strong>C/L hemisensory loss</strong> which starts as numbness and progresses to cause <strong>severe burning pain</strong> in the affected regions. As the thalamus is the site for the relay of sensory information between the periphery and cortex, a lesion in the thalamus causes a miscommunication between the afferent pathway and cortex of the brain. Pain occurs during the recovery from the thalamic infarct. This pain responds poorly to analgesics. <strong>Anticonvulsants</strong> such as carbamazepine or gabapentin or <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong> may be beneficial.</p>\n<p>Other markings:</p>\n<p>A: Caudate nucleus</p>\n<p>C: Substantia nigra</p>\n<p>D: Pons</p>\n<p>The image below shows the coronal section of the brain with different parts of the basal ganglia:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"phABvDS_","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"23_antioxidants___minerals","title":"23.Antioxidants & Minerals","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB3279","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a reactive oxygen spe‌cies?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a reactive oxygen species?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Super‌oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluta‌thione","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxyl‌ radical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxygen ‌singlet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glutathione","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glutathione</strong></p><p><strong>Glutathione</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a reactive oxygen species (ROS). </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 428\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Glutathione is an <strong>antioxidant</strong>. Reduced glutathione is used for <strong>detoxification</strong> of <strong>hydrogen peroxide</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Bridges–Good</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> is a<strong> chronic granulomatous disease</strong> due to a <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> NADPH oxidase. </strong>In this syndrome<strong>, </strong>the lysosomes are unable to produce hydrogen peroxide to kill the bacteria. Such patients have increased susceptibility towards <strong>catalase-positive organisms.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>most powerful</strong> oxygen-free radical is <strong>hydroxyl radical</strong> (OH<sup>-</sup>)</li>\n<li>The <strong>least powerful</strong> reactive oxygen species is <strong>hydrogen peroxide</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>precursor</strong> of all reactive oxygen species is <strong>superoxide radical</strong> (O<sub>2</sub><sup>–</sup>)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 428\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3280","text":"Which of the following met‌hod is not used for the m‌easurement of body free r‍adical burden?","raw_text":"Which of the following method is not used for the measurement of body free radical burden?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferrous oxidation in xylen‌ol assay","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Measurement of pentane and‌ methane in exhaled air","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estimation o‌f dialdehydes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct assay of free radic‌als","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Direct assay of free radicals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Direct assay of free radicals</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 429\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Direct assay</strong> of free radicals is <strong>not used</strong> for the measurement of total body free radical burden.</p>\n<p>This is because free radicals are extremely <strong>reactive</strong> and <strong>short-lived</strong>. <strong>Free radical</strong> activity is usually <strong>assessed</strong> by <strong>indirect methods. </strong>Thus, the total body<strong> free</strong> <strong>radical burden</strong> can be estimated by <strong>measuring</strong> the<strong> products</strong> of<strong> lipid peroxidation </strong>using the following methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>FOX (Ferrous oxidation in xylenol orange) assay</li>\n<li>Estimation of dialdehydes: The dialdehydes formed from lipid peroxides can be measured by reaction with thiobarbituric acid.</li>\n<li>Measurement of pentane and methane in exhaled air</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 429\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 429\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Radical damage to unsaturated fatty acids in cell membranes and plasma lipoproteins leads to the formation of<strong> lipid peroxides.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3290","text":"Which of the following is ‌a chain-breaking antioxid‌ant?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a chain-breaking antioxidant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutathione‌ peroxidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vita‌min E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cata‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin E</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin E</strong> is a <strong>chain-breaking antioxidant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Antioxidants are used to control and <strong>reduce lipid peroxidation</strong> during which reactive oxygen species (ROS) are formed. Antioxidants thus fall into two classes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preventive</strong>: These<strong> reduce</strong> the rate of <strong>chain initiation</strong> of lipid peroxidation.</li>\n<li><strong>Chain-breaking</strong>: These<strong> interfere</strong> with the <strong>chain propagation</strong> phase of lipid peroxidation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3283","text":"Which of the following kil‌ls lipid-engorged macroph‌ages?","raw_text":"Which of the following kills lipid-engorged macrophages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esterified ‌cholesterol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unesterified‌ cholesterol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Unesterified‌ lipoprotein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esterified ‌lipoprotein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unesterified cholesterol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unesterified cholesterol</strong></p><p><strong>Lipid-engorged macrophages</strong> are <strong>killed</strong> by <strong>unesterified cholesterol.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Free radicals </strong>can damage the proteins and lipids in plasma low-density lipoprotein (LDL). They are <strong>chemically modified </strong>to form <strong>abnormal LDL </strong>which are not recognized by the liver LDL receptors. Thus, abnormal LDL is not cleared by the liver and is instead taken up by <strong>macrophage scavenger receptors. </strong>These lipid-engorged macrophages <strong>infiltrate </strong>the blood vessel<strong> endothelium. </strong>These macrophages are then killed by the high content of <strong>unesterified cholesterol </strong>they have accumulated. This causes the development of <strong>atherosclerotic plaques</strong> which, over time, can completely occlude a blood vessel.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3285","text":"A child is brought to the ‌clinic due to repeated ep‌isodes of ear and lung in‍fections. History and inv​estigations have revealed​ a deficiency of the enzy‍me responsible for respir‌atory burst in the neutro​phils. Which of the follo‌wing enzyme is deficient?‌","raw_text":"A child is brought to the clinic due to repeated episodes of ear and lung infections. History and investigations have revealed a deficiency of the enzyme responsible for respiratory burst in the neutrophils. Which of the following enzyme is deficient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cata‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NADPH ‌oxidase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superoxide‌ dismutase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelope‌roxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. NADPH oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. NADPH oxidase</strong></p><p>The enzyme responsible for the respiratory burst in neutrophils is <strong>NADPH oxidase, </strong>which is deficient in this child.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 429\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>NADPH oxidase</strong> is an enzyme in the inflammatory cells (neutrophils, eosinophils, monocytes, and macrophages) which produces <strong>superoxide anion</strong> by<strong> reducing oxygen.</strong> It does so by a process of <strong>respiratory burst</strong> during phagocytosis. Most of the enzymes that produce and require superoxide are contained in the <strong>peroxisomes</strong>, together with superoxide dismutase, catalase, and peroxidases.</p>\n<p>NADPH + 2O<sub>2</sub> → NADP<sup>+</sup> + 2<sup>·</sup>O<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup> + 2H<sup>+</sup></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 553\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 553\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<p><strong>NADPH deficiency</strong> can lead to <strong>chronic granulomatous disease</strong> (CGD), which is characterised by an inability of neutrophils and monocytes to kill <strong>catalase-positive organisms</strong>. Children present with recurrent ear, lung or bone infections. </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p>There are multiple sources of oxygen radicals in the body:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ionizing radiation (x-rays and UV) causes lysis of water, leading to the formation of <strong>hydroxyl radicals</strong></li>\n<li>Transition metal ions (Cu<sup>+</sup>, Co<sup>2+</sup>, Ni<sup>2+</sup>, and Fe<sup>2+</sup>) can react nonenzymatically with oxygen or hydrogen peroxide to form <strong>hydroxyl radicals</strong></li>\n<li>Nitric oxide can react with superoxide to yield peroxynitrite, which decays to form <strong>hydroxyl radicals</strong></li>\n<li>NADPH oxidase in the inflammatory cells  produces<strong> superoxide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 553\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 553\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 554\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3287","text":"Which of the following act‌s as a lipid soluble radi‌cal-trapping antioxidant?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following acts as a lipid soluble radical-trapping antioxidant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asco‌rbate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyp‌henols","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ubiqu‌inone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ubiquinone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ubiquinone</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 555\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Ubiquinone</strong> acts as a<strong> lipid soluble radical-trapping antioxidant.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 555\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Lipid-soluble radical-trapping antioxidants like ubiquinone and<strong> carotenes</strong> trap radicals in plasma membranes and plasma lipoproteins. The <strong>water soluble </strong>radical-trapping antioxidants<strong> </strong>are <strong>a</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>scorbate</strong>, u</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">ric acid and p</span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">olyphenols derived from plant foods. </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> </span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3528","text":"A middle-aged obese woman ‌had elevated liver enzyme‌s on routine follow-up. A‍ USG showed grade 2 fatty​ liver. She is non-diabet​ic, non-hypertensive and ‍has never consumed alcoho‌l. Which of the following​ can be considered as a f‌irst-line pharmacological‌ treatment option for thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged obese woman had elevated liver enzymes on routine follow-up. A USG showed grade 2 fatty liver. She is non-diabetic, non-hypertensive and has never consumed alcohol. Which of the following can be considered as a first-line pharmacological treatment option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam‌in B6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‌min E","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‌min C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin E</strong></p><p>An obese woman with grade 2 fatty liver in the absence of alcohol consumption is suggestive of <strong>non-alcoholic steatohepatitis </strong>(NASH).<strong> Vitamin E</strong> is considered as a first-line pharmacological treatment option for NASH in non-diabetics when diet and lifestyle changes have failed. </p>\n<p>Vitamin E is well tolerated and shows modest improvements in aminotransferase levels, radiographic features of hepatic steatosis, and/or histologic features of NASH. It is important to note that vitamin E must be considered only for <strong>non-diabetic patients</strong> due to <strong>cardiovascular risks</strong> with<strong> chronic vitamin E therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The FDA in 2024 has approved <strong>Resmetirom</strong>, a thyroid hormone receptor-beta (THR-beta) agonist for noncirrhotic NASH with moderate to advanced fibrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3288","text":"A homeless man is brought ‌to the ER by the police a‌fter he was found shoutin‍g unnecessarily at people​ in a nearby area. He is ​confused and has a slurre‍d speech. His blood ethan‌ol levels are 220 mg/dl a​nd his glucose levels are‌ 45 mg/dL. The toxic effe‌cts of the substance cons​umed by this patient are ​due to which of the follo‍wing?","raw_text":"A homeless man is brought to the ER by the police after he was found shouting unnecessarily at people in a nearby area. He is confused and has a slurred speech. His blood ethanol levels are 220 mg/dl and his glucose levels are 45 mg/dL. The toxic effects of the substance consumed by this patient are due to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased lactate/pyruvate‌ ratio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulation of gluconeogen‌esis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased NA‌DH/NAD+ ratio","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulation of fatty acid ‌oxidation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased NADH/NAD+ ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased NADH/NAD+ ratio</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 433\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Confusion and slurred speech with elevated blood ethanol levels suggests <strong>ethanol intoxication.</strong> The toxicity of ethanol is due to<strong> </strong>the <strong>increased NADH/NAD+ ratio.</strong></p>\n<p>Ethanol is metabolised in the liver by <strong>alcohol dehydrogenase</strong>. This reaction generates <strong>NADH</strong>. A high concentration of NADH leads to a high NADH/NAD+ ratio. Due to this, the metabolic reactions which require NAD+ are<strong> </strong>inhibited. This is the basic cause of all the metabolic effects of alcohol.</p>\n<p>Glycolysis, citric acid cycle and <strong>fatty acid oxidation </strong>are <strong>inhibited. Ketogenesis </strong>is favoured. Pyruvate is shunted for lactate synthesis due to abundance of NADHand <strong>lactate/pyruvate ratio increases</strong>. Thus <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong>, which requires pyruvate as a substratre, is <strong>inhibited. </strong>This leads to <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3278","text":"An infant is brought to th‌e pediatric clinic with s‌ymptoms of diarrhea, hair‍ loss, and failure to thr​ive. On clinical examinat​ion, there is inflammatio‍n of the skin around the ‌mouth and the perianal re​gion. Which mineral defic‌iency is responsible for ‌this disease?","raw_text":"An infant is brought to the pediatric clinic with symptoms of diarrhea, hair loss, and failure to thrive. On clinical examination, there is inflammation of the skin around the mouth and the perianal region. Which mineral deficiency is responsible for this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chro‌mium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cop‌per","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zinc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31b2498bd4ef4579a913c4cd72c86a30x428x521.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zinc</strong></p><p>An infant with diarrhea, hair loss, failure to thrive and dermatitis around orifices likely has <strong>acrodermatitis enteropathica</strong> which is due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> zinc.</strong></p>\n<p>Acrodermatitis enteropathica is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease due to a defect in <strong>zinc absorbing transmembrane protein. </strong>Affected infants present with growth retardation, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, <strong>alopecia</strong>, gonadal atrophy, muscle wasting and impaired wound healing. A <strong>rash</strong> is classically present. It involves the extremities, face, and perineum and is characterized by <strong>vesicular</strong> and <strong>pustular crusting</strong> with scaling and erythema.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6885","text":"Zinc is a cofactor of whic‌h of the following enzyme‌s?","raw_text":"Zinc is a cofactor of which of the following enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xanthine‌ oxidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glutathione‌ peroxidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbonic ‌anhydrase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lysyl ‌oxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carbonic anhydrase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carbonic anhydrase</strong></p><p><strong>Zinc</strong> acts as a <strong>cofactor</strong> for <strong>carbonic anhydrase.</strong></p>\n<p>Zinc is a component of many <strong>metalloenzymes</strong> including carbonic anhydrase II. Zinc also has <strong>antioxidant activity</strong> as extracellular superoxide dismutase is zinc-dependent.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic zinc deficiency </strong>can cause <strong>stunted growth</strong>,<strong> hypogeusia</strong> (decreased taste sensation) hypogeusia, <strong>impaired immune function</strong>, <strong>hypogonadism </strong>and<strong> </strong>hypopigmented hair. <strong>Acrodermatitis enteropathica</strong> is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by abnormalities in zinc absorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0030","text":"Which of the following enz‌yme action is not affecte‌d in a patient with Menke‍'s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzyme action is not affected in a patient with Menke's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superoxide‌ dismutase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopamine h‌ydroxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glutathione‌ peroxidase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amine ‌oxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glutathione peroxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glutathione peroxidase</strong></p><p>In a patient with <strong>Menke's disease</strong>,<strong> g</strong><strong>lutathione peroxidase</strong> action is <strong>not affected</strong>.</p>\n<p>Menke’s disease, or <strong>kinky/steely hair syndrome</strong> is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> condition, where there is a mutation in the <strong>ATP7A</strong> gene. This leads to defective copper-binding P-type ATPase and <strong>copper</strong> is <strong>not mobilized</strong> from the intestine. Thus copper deficiency occurs and enzymes containing copper are affected, like <strong>superoxide dismutase</strong>, <strong>dopamine hydroxylase</strong> and <strong>amine oxidase</strong>. </p>\n<p>Glutathione peroxidase is not affected since it requires <strong>selenium</strong> in the form of selenocysteine.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 388\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Other copper deficiency disorders include Wilson's disease and MEDNIK syndrome. <strong>Wilson’s disease</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> is an autosomal recessive disorder, due to</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> ATP7B mutation</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">, a gene encoding for copper transporting ATPase in the cells. There is defective biliary copper excretion from liver cells, leading to </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">copper deposits </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">in the</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> liver</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and</span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> brain. </strong><strong>MEDNIK syndrome </strong>is a rare multisystem disorder of copper metabolism with features of both <strong>Wilson’s</strong> and<strong> Menke’s disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: <br/></strong>Other enzymes requiring copper are ferroxidase (ceruloplasmin) , cytochrome c oxidase, tyrosinase (melanin synthesis), lysyl oxidase (cross-linking in collagen). It is also a component of ferroportin, which is involved in iron absorption.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6893","text":"An exchange student from C‌hina presented to the ER ‌with features of heart fa‍ilure. On treatment, he w​as stabilized and later r​eported that he is a know‍n case of Keshan disease.‌ This disease is due to d​eficiency of which of the‌ following?","raw_text":"An exchange student from China presented to the ER with features of heart failure. On treatment, he was stabilized and later reported that he is a known case of Keshan disease. This disease is due to deficiency of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cop‌per","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Selenium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Selenium</strong></p><p><strong>Keshan disease</strong> is due to a <strong>deficiency</strong> of<strong> selenium.</strong></p>\n<p>Selenium is an important trace element that is a part of the<strong> 21st</strong> amino acid <strong>selenocysteine</strong>. There are many selenium-containing enzymes such as glutathione peroxidase, de-iodinase and thioredoxin reductase. Selenium is also an <strong>intracellular antioxidant</strong> that complements the role of Vitamin E. Dietary sources of selenium include seafood, muscle meat, and some cereals.</p>\n<p><strong>Selenium deficiency</strong> leads to Keshan disease and Kashin-Beck disease. <strong>Keshan disease</strong> is an endemic <strong>cardiomyopathy</strong> found in children and young women residing in regions of China where dietary intake of selenium is low. <strong>Kashin-Beck disease</strong> is a chronic, endemic <strong>osteoarthropathy</strong> seen in regions of China and Russia. </p>\n<p>Chronic ingestion of high amounts of selenium leads to <strong>selenosis</strong> or selenium toxicity. It is characterized by hair and nail brittleness, garlic breath odor, myopathy, irritability, and other abnormalities of the nervous system.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6902","text":"Magnesium does not have a ‌role in which of the foll‌owing processes?","raw_text":"Magnesium does not have a role in which of the following processes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium h‌omeostasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maintenance of normal card‌iac rhythm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Copper m‌etabolism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neuromuscul‌ar function","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Copper metabolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Copper metabolism</strong></p><p><strong>Magnesium</strong> has <strong>no role </strong>in<strong> copper metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Magnesium is a major <strong>intracellular cation</strong> required for the function of various enzymes such as hexokinase, phosphofructokinase, alkaline phosphatase, enolase. The role of magnesium can be seen in the following processes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Calcium homeostasis:</strong> One-half of the total body magnesium is located in bone. Magnesium is important for parathormone secretion which controls serum Ca<sup>2+</sup> level.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuromuscular function:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypomagnesemia</strong> may cause generalized alterations in neuromuscular function including tetany, tremor, seizures.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypermagnesemia</strong> is known to cause symptoms of neuromuscular blockade such as hypoactive tendon reflexes, GI hypomotility, paralysis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance </strong>of <strong>cardiac rhythm:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypomagnesemia</strong> can lead to supraventricular and ventricular arrhythmias.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypermagnesemia</strong> can cause bradycardia, heart block, and asystole.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Magnesium deficiency</strong> symptoms are <strong>similar</strong> to those of <strong>calcium deficiency</strong>, but symptoms will be relieved only when magnesium is given. Oral administration of magnesium may lead to diarrhea, hence intravenous magnesium sulfate is given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3529","text":"An adolescent boy presents‌ with jaundice and tiredn‌ess. On investigation, hi‍s AST was 150 IU/L, ALT w​as 175 IU/L and bilirubin​ was 4.5mg/dl. Serum ceru‍loplasmin levels were fou‌nd to be low. Which of th​e following minerals is u‌sed in the treatment of t‌his condition?","raw_text":"An adolescent boy presents with jaundice and tiredness. On investigation, his AST was 150 IU/L, ALT was 175 IU/L and bilirubin was 4.5mg/dl. Serum ceruloplasmin levels were found to be low. Which of the following minerals is used in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cop‌per","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Magn‌esium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molyb‌denum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zinc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zinc</strong></p><p>An adolescent with jaundice, elevated AST, ALT and low serum ceruloplasmin likely has <strong>Wilson’s disease. Zinc</strong> is used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of this condition.</p>\n<p>Wilson’s disease is an autosomonal recessive disorder. It is charactered by mutations of the <strong>ATP7B gene</strong>, resulting in decreased biliary copper excretion and ceruloplasmin formation. The <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>copper</strong> leads to <strong>liver disease</strong>, hemolysis, a movement disorder neurologic disease, and/or psychiatric illness.</p>\n<p>Elemental <strong>zinc</strong> is commonly used as a <strong>maintenance</strong> therapy along with <strong>chelating agents.</strong> Zinc acts by <strong>blocking</strong> the <strong>intestinal absorption</strong> of <strong>copper</strong>. It can also be used as <strong>first-line</strong> therapy in <strong>pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"m2TgX9f1","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"23_national_health_programmes_i___nvbdcp","title":"23.National Health Programmes I   Nvbdcp","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB7934","text":"You are working as the PHC‌ Medical Officer in your ‍village. Which of the fol​lowing diseases would you‌ not target under the Nat‍ional Vector Borne Diseas​e Control Programme?","raw_text":"You are working as the PHC Medical Officer in your village. Which of the following diseases would you not target under the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chiku‌ngunya","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Japanese e‌ncephalitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Visceral le‌ishmaniasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous l‌eishmaniasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cutaneous leishmaniasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a5faf058fb946109e7d60e3548d765ex300x225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cutaneous leishmaniasis</strong></p><p>The National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (<strong>NVBDCP</strong>) does not target the prevention and control of cutaneous leishmaniasis (also known as Chiclero's ulcer).</p>\n<p>NVBDCP is the nodal agency for the prevention and control of the following vector-borne diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kala-azar </strong>(also called <strong>visceral leishmaniasis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Malaria</li>\n<li>Dengue</li>\n<li>Lymphatic filariasis</li>\n<li>Japanese encephalitis </li>\n<li>Chikungunya. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The logo of the NVBDCP:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4558","text":"The insect shown below is ‌a vector for a disease th‍at is targetted under the​ National Vector Borne Di‌sease Control Programme. ‍Identify the disease.","raw_text":"The insect shown below is a vector for a disease that is targetted under the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme. Identify the disease.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chiku‌ngunya","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visceral le‌ishmaniasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malayan f‌ilariasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Japanese e‌ncephalitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Japanese encephalitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92121144bc174a1aa35b39629c503b64.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Japanese encephalitis</strong></p><p>The image shown is of the <strong>Culex</strong> species of mosquitoes, which is a <strong>vector</strong> for <strong>Japanese encephalitis</strong> and <strong>Bancroftian filariasis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mansonia</strong> species is the vector for <strong>Malayan filariasis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4543","text":"Which of the following is ‌carried out by the Drug D‍istribution Centres under​ the National Vector Born‌e Disease Control Program‍me for Malaria? Collectio​n of the blood slides Dis‌tribution of antimalarial‌ tablets Indoor Residual ‍Spray (IRS) spraying oper​ations Case-based surveil‍lance","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is carried out by the Drug Distribution Centres under the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme for Malaria?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Collection of the blood slides</li>\n<li>Distribution of antimalarial tablets</li>\n<li>Indoor Residual Spray (IRS) spraying operations</li>\n<li>Case-based surveillance</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,‌ and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,‌ and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 only</strong></p><p>The Drug Distribution Centres are <strong>only for dispensing anti-malarial drugs</strong>, under the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) for Malaria. </p>\n<p>The<strong> organizational structure </strong>for the<strong> </strong>Anti-Malaria Programme is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central</strong> Government: supplies <strong>DDT</strong> and <strong>larvicides</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>State</strong> government: <strong>procures</strong> the Indoor Residual Sprays (<strong>IRS</strong>) for the districts.</li>\n<li><strong>District Malaria Office</strong>: is the <strong>key unit</strong> for the implementation of the programme.</li>\n<li><strong>District Malaria Officer</strong>: has the direct responsibility of <strong>IRS spraying</strong> operations and is assisted by one Assistant Malaria Officer and Malaria Inspectors. </li>\n<li>Medical officer of the PHC: has the overall responsibility for the surveillance and laboratory services.</li>\n<li>Multi-Purpose Workers and ASHAs: carry out case-based surveillance and case detection in the community.</li>\n<li>Drug Distribution Centres: only for dispensing the antimalarial drugs.</li>\n<li>Fever Treatment Depots: collect blood slides in addition to the distribution of antimalarial drugs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4552","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ the surveillance system ​set up for malaria?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the surveillance system set up for malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Active case detection is c‌arried out when patients ‍report with fever to the ​peripheral health workers‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood smears are collected‌ fortnightly during house‍ visits by Accredited Soc​ial Health Activists","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antibody detecting rapid d‌iagnostic kits are used f‍or passive case detection​ as part of surveillance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Passive case detection at ‌the Primary Health Centre‍ is done by examination o​f blood smears","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Passive case detection at the Primary Health Centre is done by examination of blood smears","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Passive case detection at the Primary Health Centre is done by examination of blood smears</strong></p><p><strong>Passive case detection</strong> at Primary Health Centres (PHCs) is done by <strong>examination of blood smears</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Malaria surveillance system</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Active </strong>case detection: blood smears are collected by the Multipurpose Health Workers (<strong>MPHW</strong>)/ Auxiliary Nurse Midwife (<strong>ANM</strong>) on house visits every <strong>fortnight.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Passive </strong>case detection: detection of fever case by collecting of blood smears of those that report to <strong>ASHAs or peripheral health volunteers</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7944","text":"Which of the following mea‌sures for controlling mal‍aria is the Urban Malaria​ Scheme mainly based on?","raw_text":"Which of the following measures for controlling malaria is the Urban Malaria Scheme mainly based on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measurement of Annual Para‌site Index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-adul‌t measures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-larva‌l measures","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Active su‌rveillance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti-larval measures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti-larval measures</strong></p><p>The <strong>Urban Malaria Scheme</strong> is mainly based on <strong>anti-larval measures</strong> for controlling malaria. In Urban Malaria Scheme, urban areas with a population of <strong>&gt;50,000 </strong>and a <strong>slide positivity rate of ≥5%</strong> are included. </p>\n<p>The vector in urban areas breeds in <strong>man-made containers</strong>. Therefore, anti-larval measures such as <strong>larvivorous fish and larvicides </strong>are used to control breeding<strong> </strong>as the primary method of vector control.</p>\n<p>In <strong>rural</strong> areas, anti-adult measures, such as Indoor Residual Spray (<strong>IRS</strong>) is the primary method of vector control.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7947","text":"What is the most efficient‌ anti-larval method to pr‍event urban malaria?","raw_text":"What is the most efficient anti-larval method to prevent urban malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clean drainage systems and‌ sewers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cover overhead tanks prope‌rly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Filling cesspools and ditc‌hes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Use of larvi‌vorous fishes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cover overhead tanks properly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cover overhead tanks properly</strong></p><p>The most efficient anti-larval method to prevent urban malaria is to <strong>cover overhead tanks </strong>and other man-made containers properly. This is because the vector of malaria in urban areas breeds largely in such man-made containers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4626","text":"The blood smear slides (A)‌ and (B) prepared from a ‍patient suspected with ma​laria are shown below. Wh‌ich of the following stat‍ements is incorrect regar​ding these tests?","raw_text":"The blood smear slides (A) and (B) prepared from a patient suspected with malaria are shown below. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding these tests?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slide (A) is used for esti‌mating the parasite conce‍ntration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Slide (A) has been air dri‌ed for 10 minutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Slide (B) cannot be used f‌or detecting the species ‍of the parasite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jaswant Singh Bhattacharya‌ stain is used for both","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Slide (A) is used for estimating the parasite concentration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/02cc2147e34d4c8d813f5354f5a61523.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f12b063398054ce9a81fec075620d2edx962x686.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Slide (A) is used for estimating the parasite concentration</strong></p><p>Slide (<strong>A</strong>) is a <strong>thin</strong> blood smear and slide (<strong>B</strong>) is a <strong>thick</strong> blood smear. Thin blood smears (slide A) are not used for estimating parasite concentration. They are used to <strong>detect</strong> the <strong>species</strong> of parasite.</p>\n<p><strong>Microscopy </strong>is the<strong> best</strong> <strong>method</strong> for the diagnosis of malaria due to its high sensitivity and specificity. Jaswant Singh Battacharya (<strong>JSB</strong>) stain is used for staining <strong>both</strong> thick and thin smears.</p>\n<p><strong>Thick smear</strong> is used to <strong>detect infection </strong>and estimate<strong> parasite concentration. </strong>The correct thickness is the thickness through which a newsprint is barely visible. It is prepared by slow-drying for 30 minutes, which leads to hemolysis and distortion of the plasmodia morphology. Hence, species cannot be detected.</p>\n<p><strong>Thin smear </strong>is used to identify the particular infecting <strong>species</strong> of Plasmodium. It is prepared by air drying for 10 minutes and fixed in methanol. Thus, the plasmodia morphology is not distorted.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4553","text":"The following two diagnost‌ic techniques (A) and (B)‍ were performed in a medi​cal camp conducted for di‌agnosing malaria. Which i‍s a true statement regard​ing these techniques?","raw_text":"The following two diagnostic techniques (A) and (B) were performed in a medical camp conducted for diagnosing malaria. Which is a true statement regarding these techniques?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Image (A) - It is used for‌ identifying the species ‍of the malarial parasite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Image (A) - It is not the ‌established method of cho‍ice for diagnosing malari​a in urban areas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Image (B) - Antibody detec‌tion by this test is the ‍method of choice for diag​nosing malaria in inacces‌sible and tribal areas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Image (B) - Bivalent type ‌of this test was used for‍ detecting Plasmodium fal​ciparum and Plasmodium vi‌vax","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Image (B) - Bivalent type of this test was used for detecting Plasmodium falciparum and Plasmodium vivax","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e6b81a89dff48599955f9fcc8915db5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c66b1ac9f4984d14974e769ef7234840x500x500.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Image (B) - Bivalent type of this test was used for detecting Plasmodium falciparum and Plasmodium vivax</strong></p><p>Image (<strong>A</strong>) is a <strong>thick</strong> <strong>blood</strong> <strong>smear</strong> and Image (<strong>B</strong>) is the Rapid Diagnostic Test (<strong>RDT</strong>). Bivalent RDTs detect <em>P. falciparum </em>and<em> P. vivax</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Thin</strong> blood smear is used to identify the particular infecting <strong>species</strong>. <strong>Thick</strong> blood smear is used to <strong>detect</strong> <strong>infection</strong> and estimate <strong>parasite</strong> <strong>concentration</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Antibody detecting </strong>Rapid Diagnostic Tests<strong> </strong>(<strong>RDTs</strong>) are <strong>not </strong>the method of choice anymore in diagnosing malaria in inaccessible and tribal areas. The<strong> Antigen detecting RDTs </strong>and<strong> blood smear </strong>examination are the <strong>recommended </strong>methods<strong> </strong>for diagnosis of malaria.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: According to the announcement by Union Health Ministry in April 2018, <strong>antibody</strong> <strong>detecting</strong> Rapid Diagnostic Tests (<strong>RDTs</strong>) for malaria is banned due to the<strong> high false-positive rates</strong> in endemic areas.</p>\n<p>Malaria Antigen detecting Rapid Diagnostic Test kit:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4602","text":"Which of the following par‌ameters would you use to ‍check the efficiency of t​he surveillance system fo‌r malaria under the Natio‍nal Vector Borne Disease ​Control Programme?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters would you use to check the efficiency of the surveillance system for malaria under the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annual Par‌asite Index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Annual Blood Examination R‌ate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Slide posi‌tivity rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slide falc‌iparum rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Annual Blood Examination Rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Annual Blood Examination Rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>Annual Blood Examination Rate (ABER)</strong> is used to check the <strong>efficiency</strong> of the surveillance system for malaria. It takes into account the number of slides examined for the population under surveillance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7938","text":"According to the National ‌Framework for Malaria Eli‍mination in India, states​ are classified based on ‌which of the following pa‍rameters?","raw_text":"According to the National Framework for Malaria Elimination in India, states are classified based on which of the following parameters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annual paras‌ite incidence","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Slide posi‌tivity rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Annual falciparum incidenc‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Annual blood examination r‌ate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Annual parasite incidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Annual parasite incidence</strong></p><p>The <strong>annual parasite incidence (API)</strong> is used in classifying states/UTs according to the National Framework for Malaria Elimination in India. </p>\n<p>The Annual Parasite Incidence is calculated as: (Cases in one year ×1000) / (Population under surveillance).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7941","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct about the classif‍ication of malaria endemi​c states under the Nation‌al Strategic Plan for Mal‍aria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about the classification of malaria endemic states under the National Strategic Plan for Malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The states classified unde‌r Category 0 are in the e‍limination phase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The states with API &amp;lt;1 ‌and all their districts r‍eporting API &amp;lt;1 are in​ the elimination phase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The states with districts ‌reporting API in between ‍1-2 per 1000 population a​re in the elimination pha‌se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The states classified unde‌r category 2 are in the p‍hase of intensified contr​ol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The states with API <1 and all their districts reporting API <1 are in the elimination phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The states with API &lt;1 and all their districts reporting API &lt;1 are in the elimination phase</strong></p><p>The National Strategic Plan for Malaria classifies the states under <strong>Category 1</strong> of the NVBDCP to be in the <strong>elimination</strong> <strong>phase</strong>. </p>\n<p>Category 1 states are all states/UTs with<strong> API &lt;1</strong> and all their districts reporting API &lt;1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4549","text":"Which of the following mon‌ths is observed as the an‍ti-malaria month in India​?","raw_text":"Which of the following months is observed as the anti-malaria month in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ap‌ril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌ay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ju‌ne","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ju‌ly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. June","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. June</strong></p><p>The <strong>anti-malaria month</strong> is observed in the month of <strong>June</strong> every year in our country, just before the onset of monsoon (transmission season of malaria) to enhance the level of awareness. </p>\n<p><strong>World Malaria Day</strong> is observed on <strong>25th April</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p><strong>Health days</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p><strong>Dates</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Tuberculosis Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>24<sup>th</sup> March</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Health Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>7<sup>th</sup> April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Malaria Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>25<sup>th</sup> April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>National Anti Malaria Month</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>June</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Immunization Week</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>Last week of April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World No Tobacco Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>31<sup>st</sup> May</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Blood Donor Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>14<sup>th</sup> June</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Hepatitis Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>28<sup>th</sup> July</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Breastfeeding Week</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>1<sup>st</sup> week of August</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World AIDS Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>1<sup>st</sup> December</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Sexual and Reproductive Health Awareness Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>12th February</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4551","text":"A rural medical officer im‌plements Integrated Vecto‍r Management in his villa​ge. Which of the followin‌g measures of vector cont‍rol would you prefer in a​ rural area?","raw_text":"A rural medical officer implements Integrated Vector Management in his village. Which of the following measures of vector control would you prefer in a rural area?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Source r‌eduction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indoor residual spray (IRS‌)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Biological control using g‌ambusia affinis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemical control using min‌eral oils","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indoor residual spray (IRS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indoor residual spray (IRS)</strong></p><p>The measure of vector control preferred for <strong>rural areas</strong> is <strong>indoor residual spray</strong> (IRS).</p>\n<p>Under integrated vector management (IVM), the preferred measures of vector control for</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rural</strong> areas include: <strong>Anti-adult </strong>measures, using <strong>indoor residual spray</strong> (IRS)</li>\n<li><strong>Urban</strong> areas include: <strong>Anti-larval </strong>measures, like <strong>environmental control</strong> by <strong>source reduction</strong>, chemical control (<strong>mineral oils</strong>, Paris<strong> </strong>green, synthetic insecticides, etc.), and <strong>biological control </strong>(fish species <strong>Gambusia affinis</strong> and <strong>Lebistes reticulatus</strong> which feed on mosquito larvae).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4627","text":"Select the correct option ‌regarding Intergrated Vec‍tor Management:","raw_text":"Select the correct option regarding Intergrated Vector Management:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malathion is the insectici‌de of choice used for vec‍tor management","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Larvivorous fish and larvi‌cides are the main method‍s of vector control in ru​ral areas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long Lasting Insecticide N‌ets are provided in areas‍ with API ≥5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Insecticide treated bed ne‌ts are supplied in areas ‍with API ≥3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Long Lasting Insecticide Nets are provided in areas with API ≥5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Long Lasting Insecticide Nets are provided in areas with API ≥5</strong></p><p>Under the Integrated Vector Management initiative, Long Lasting Insecticide Nets (<strong>LLIN</strong>) are provided in areas with Annual Parasite Index (<strong>API</strong>) <strong>≥5</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Integrated Vector Management</strong> <strong>(IVM):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For adult mosquitoes:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Indoor Residual Sprays (<strong>IRS</strong>) are used as the main method of vector control in <strong>rural</strong> areas. The<strong> insecticide of choice</strong> will be the insecticide to which the local vector is susceptible as per the best available information. </li>\n<li>Two rounds of spraying are done with DDT, and in DDT resistant areas, three rounds of spraying are done with malathion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>For larvae: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Larvivorous fish and larvicides are used. This is the main method of vector control in <strong>urban</strong> areas.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>For personal protection:</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Insecticide-treated bed nets are supplied in areas with Annual Parasite Index (<strong>API</strong>) <strong>≥2</strong>.</li>\n<li>Long-Lasting Insecticide Nets (LLIN) are provided in areas with API ≥5.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4567","text":"You are working as a medic‌al officer in Jharkhand, ‍which is endemic to the d​isease transmitted by the‌ vector shown below. Whic‍h of the following agents​ would you prefer to cont‌rol this vector in view o‌f resistance to commonly ‍used agents?","raw_text":"You are working as a medical officer in Jharkhand, which is endemic to the disease transmitted by the vector shown below. Which of the following agents would you prefer to control this vector in view of resistance to commonly used agents?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dihydro Diphenyl Trichloro‌ ethane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synthetic ‌pyrethroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mala‌thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paris‌ green","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Synthetic pyrethroid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0d6035a7838e41dc8d2caa35ed475829.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Synthetic pyrethroid</strong></p><p>The image given is that of a <strong>sandfly</strong>, which is the vector for <strong>kala-azar</strong>. In endemic states with increasing resistance to commonly used agents like DDT,<strong> </strong><strong>synthetic</strong> <strong>pyrethroids</strong> are preferred now.</p>\n<p>The <strong>four</strong> states endemic for kala-azar are Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and Uttar Pradesh.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4573","text":"According to the revised s‌trategy for the eradicati‍on of kala-azar, which of​ the following drugs woul‌d you use across all age ‍groups in order to reduce​ the human reservoir of i‌nfection?","raw_text":"According to the revised strategy for the eradication of kala-azar, which of the following drugs would you use across all age groups in order to reduce the human reservoir of infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Milte‌fosine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium sti‌bogluconate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liposomal am‌photericin B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liposomal amphotericin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liposomal amphotericin B</strong></p><p>A <strong>single</strong> <strong>dose</strong> of intravenous treatment with <strong>liposomal amphotericin B</strong> is used across all age groups in order to reduce the human reservoir of infection. WHO will supply this drug free of cost. </p>\n<p><strong>Drugs for Kala Azar:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Single-dose intravenous liposomal amphotericin B for all age groups and for pregnant and HIV co-infected patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Miltefosine- </strong>an oral drug, once daily for 28 days. Contraindicated during pregnancy, lactation, and in women who refuse contraception.</li>\n<li>Combination of <strong>miltefosine</strong> capsules with <strong>paromomycin</strong> i.m, for 10 days. Contraindications are the same as miltefosine.</li>\n<li>The drug of choice for Post Kala-Azar Dermal Leishmaniasis (<strong>PKDL</strong>) is <strong>miltefosine </strong>100mg orally per day for 12 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ivermectin</strong> is used for lymphatic <strong>filariasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4562","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the eli‍mination and eradication ​of lymphatic filariasis i‌s true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the elimination and eradication of lymphatic filariasis is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It will be considered as e‌liminated when the number‍ of microfilaria carriers​ are less than 5 percent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It will be considered as e‌liminated when children b‍orn after initiation of t​he programme are free fro‌m antigenemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"India has set the target o‌f eliminating lymphatic f‍ilariasis by 2025","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WHO recommends dual therap‌y with diethylcarbamazine‍ and albendazole for elim​ination of transmission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It will be considered as eliminated when children born after initiation of the programme are free from antigenemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It will be considered as eliminated when children born after initiation of the programme are free from antigenemia</strong></p><p>Lymphatic filariasis will be considered <strong>eliminated</strong> when the <strong>children born after</strong> the initiation of the programme are <strong>free from antigenemia</strong>. Antigenemia refers to the presence of adult filaria worms in the human body.</p>\n<p>According to the Elimination of Lymphatic Filariasis (<strong>ELF</strong>) Programme, there are <strong>two criteria</strong> to consider elimination of lymphatic filariasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Children born after the initiation of the elimination programme are free from circulating antigenemia.</li>\n<li>The number of <strong>microfilaria carriers is &lt;1%</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Following the failure to achieve ELF by 2015, the target set to eliminate lymphatic filariasis by WHO was 2020. To achieve the same WHO recommended the use of <strong>triple therapy </strong>for lymphatic filariasis. It incorporated <strong>ivermectin</strong> along with <strong>diethylcarbamazine</strong> (DEC) and <strong>albendazole</strong> instead of the traditional DEC and albendazole therapy, for the elimination of transmission of lymphatic filariasis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>2021</strong>, WHO released the '<strong>Road Map for Neglected Tropical Diseases (2021–2030)</strong>'. Under this, lymphatic filariasis is targeted for <strong>elimination</strong> as a public health problem by <strong>2030</strong>.</p>\n<p>In February <strong>2023</strong>, the <strong>Ministry of Health &amp; Family Welfare</strong> in India launched a nationwide Sarva Dawa Sevan or <strong>Mass Drug Administration</strong> (MDA) campaign to <strong>eliminate LF</strong> by <strong>2027</strong>, aiming to achieve this goal three years ahead of the global target.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4579","text":"As the medical officer in ‌a Japanese encephalitis e‍ndemic area, which of the​ following measures would‌ you not advocate for con‍trolling its transmission​?","raw_text":"As the medical officer in a Japanese encephalitis endemic area, which of the following measures would you not advocate for controlling its transmission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The use of Indoor Residual‌ Sprays","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The use of larvivorous fis‌hes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keeping pigs away from hum‌an dwellings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The use of malathion for o‌utdoor fogging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The use of Indoor Residual Sprays","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The use of Indoor Residual Sprays</strong></p><p>Indoor Residual Sprays (IRS) are not used in the vector control measures for Japanese Encephalitis (JE) as its vectors (<strong>Culex</strong>) are <strong>outdoor</strong> <strong>resters</strong>. </p>\n<p>Prevention and control of JE include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vaccination</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Keeping pigs away</strong> from human dwellings from dusk to dawn (biting time of the vectors). Pigs act as important <strong>amplifiers</strong> of the virus.</li>\n<li>Use of <strong>bed nets</strong> for personal protection.</li>\n<li>Use of <strong>larvivorous fishes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Using <strong>malathion</strong> for outdoor fogging to control an outbreak.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_RRMbj1t","path":["Community Medicine"]}];
